const DATA=[{"s": "brahe-t_15680114001", "d": "1568-01-14", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Hans Aalborg", "ck": "Hans Aalborg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hans Aalborg — Rostock, 14 January 1568 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII, p. 3 · MS witnesses: A = KB Copenhagen <a href=\"https://soeg.kb.dk/permalink/45KBDK_KGL/1o797oc/alma99122016338605763\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GKS 3037 a kvart</a>; B = KB NKS 1990d 4to; C = KB Langebeks excerpter 179</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD JOHANNEM AALBURGENSEM DATÆ 14. IANUARII 1568.</p><p>CHARISSIME Domine JOHANNES, scias me incolumem huc pervenisse Rostochium, ipsis Calendis Januarij, et interea temporis, apud D. LEVINUM, hospitio exceptum fuisse: hodie vero, Deo annuente et prosperante, in propriam quandam habitationem migrare constitui, quæ est in Juristarum Collegio, satis commoda, et observationibus cœlestibus, meisque desideriis apta. Illic, si Deo ita visum fuerit, hanc hiemem transigere constitui; quid deinceps futurum sit, dies dabit, et Domino illa prospicienda relinquo, admonitus hoc sapienti et veteri versu:</p><p>Tu præsens cura, Domino committe futura.</p><p>Tuum vero est, mi JOHANNES, eas, quas tibi nostri discessus concredidi causas, in alto silentio apud te sepelire, ne quis suspicetur, vel intelligat, me de quoquam quidquam conqueri, aut aliquid in patria fuisse, quod me ad discedendum impulerit. Id etenim permagni interest, ut nemo audiat, me de ulla re conqueri; uti etiam revera de multis conqueri non possum. Fui enim in patria ab omnibus consanguineis et amicis, praeter meritum, bene exceptus, id solo deficiente, quod non omnibus placuerint mea exercitia: idque suam meretur excusationem. Multus delator ubique est.</p><p>Tycho Brahe</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Johannes of Aalborg [Hans Aalborg], dated 14 January 1568.</b></p><p>Dearest Master Johannes, know that I have reached Rostock here safe and sound, on the very Kalends of January [1 January], and that in the meantime I was taken in as a guest at the house of Master Levinus. Today, however — God assenting and granting it to prosper — I have resolved to move into lodgings of my own, in the Jurists' College, commodious enough, and well suited to celestial observations and to my own wishes. There, if it shall so seem good to God, I have resolved to pass this winter; what is to be hereafter, the day will bring, and I leave those things for the Lord to look to, mindful of that wise old verse:</p><p><i>Tend thou to the present; commit the future to the Lord.</i></p><p>But it falls to you, my Johannes, to bury in deep silence within yourself those reasons for my departure that I confided to you — lest anyone suspect, or come to understand, that I am complaining of anyone about anything, or that there was something in my homeland that drove me to leave. For it is of the very greatest importance that no one hear that I complain of any matter — just as, in truth, I cannot complain of much. For in my homeland I was well received, beyond my deserts, by all my kinsmen and friends; the one thing wanting was that my pursuits did not please everyone — and that deserves its own excuse. There is many an informer everywhere.</p><p>Tycho Brahe</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Johannes Aalburgensis</i> = <b>Hans Aalborg</b>, an associate from Tycho's circle; the Latinised toponymic (\"of Aalborg\") is kept with the identification.</li><li><b><i>ipsis Calendis Januarij</b></i> — \"on the very Kalends of January\" = <b>1 January</b> (Roman-calendar idiom; Julian / Old Style). Rendered with the idiom and glossed.</li><li><b><i>in Juristarum Collegio</b></i> — the <b>Jurists' College (Juristen-Kollegium)</b> at the University of Rostock, where Tycho had matriculated; an institutional name, not a generic \"college of lawyers.\"</li><li><b><i>D. Levinum</b></i> — \"Master Levinus,\" Tycho's first host at Rostock; not further identified here.</li><li><b><i>Tu praesens cura, Domino committe futura</b></i> — a rhymed proverbial (leonine) verse; rendered as verse, preserving the imperative parallelism <i>cura … committe</i>.</li><li><b><i>delator</b></i> — \"informer / denouncer,\" carrying its Roman-legal sting; deliberately stronger than \"gossip.\" The closing <i>Multus delator ubique est</i> is sententious — \"There is many an informer everywhere.\"</li><li><b>Register.</b> Private, confidential letter (Tycho asks Aalborg to keep the reasons for his leaving Denmark secret); the courtesy is warm but plain. No technical content.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. Sense, idiom, and register are not in doubt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin letter to Hans (Johannes) Aalborg, including the embedded proverb couplet and every clause, against TBOO VII p.3 page image. Text and English translation match Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliothek, GKS, 3037a 4to.", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliothek, NKS, 1990d 4to.", "C København, Det Kongelige Bibliothek, Langebeks excerpter, 179."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports safe arrival in Rostock, plans to winter in the Jurists' College for observations, and urges the recipient to keep the reasons for his departure secret.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15680114001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15710518001", "d": "1571-05-18", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Helsingborg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Helsingborg, 18 May 1571 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer, vols VI–VIII </i>Epistolae<i>)</i> · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (afskrift / contemporary copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEJUM. S.</p><p>LITERAS tuas plenas dulcissimis consolationibus, quae me ob Patris charissimi ex hac vita discessum magnis doloribus consternatum, non parum refecerunt, accepi et legi, quas etsi optarem de alia materia scribi potuisse, tamen illa quia praeter omnium spem contigerat, gratae mihi admodum fuêre, et licet multae sint consolationes Theologicae ex divinis sacrarum literarum oraculis petitae, et Philosophicae ex sorte communi hominum et sublunarium rerum instabilitate et vicissitudine perpetua desumtae, quibus mentem afflictam et maestam erigo, tamen non possum non summam consolationem inde capere, quod Pater tam suaviter et pie ex hac miseranda mortalium miseria migravit in coelestem et perpetuam patriam, vbi firmam mansionem et civitatem teste Paulo habemus, hîc autem omnes sumus exules. Vix enim satis scribi potest, quanta fiducia et quanta levitate diem obijt. Nam cum eodem die (qui erat 9. Maij) ante meridiem assumsisset corpus et sanguinem filii Dei in Pane et Vino juxta testamentum et institutionem Christi Salvatoris nostri et se Sacrae scripturae dictis adversus mortis terrores et conscientiae dolores magna cum fiducia munivisset, dormivit inde vsque in tempus meridianum vel paulo ultra, tunc demum experrectus indicia dedit iam instantis agonis, quare omnes consternati accurrimus, advocanturque verbi divini ministri, qui eum consolarentur, et tunc incepit quasi obdormiscere; apertis demum oculis cum videret nos maestos et lacrymantes lectum circumstantes, quaesivit, an aliqua indicia in eo mortis viderentur, ajebat enim se non tantos dolores sentire, quin prius longe majores perpessus sit: quidam nostrum responderunt, illum esse admodum debilem, et nos timere, ne posset evadere; respondit: Igitur sinite me primum loqui cum vxore; qua adducta semimortua, habita ad eam gratiarum actione, quod tam bene et honeste secum in hac vita vixerit, ei valedixit, nobisque liberis et alijs amicis eius curam commendavit, postea rursus clausis parum oculis concionatoris consolationes auscultavit, qui cum multa prolixe diceret, ipse demum apertis oculis haec verba protulit: Vnum adhuc loqui volo: Dicitne Dominus ipse: Nolo mortem peccatoris sed potius vt convertatur et vivat. Hoc fine clausit ipsemet oculos et tam suaviter expiravit, ut dubitaverint adstantes, an mortuus fuerit, solebat enim multo durius dormire quam tunc moriebatur, et in ipso agone nec corpus nec pedes nec brachia vel manus aut caput semel de loco movebat, sed ita placide et suaviter obdormiebat, quod ipsi concionatores testati fuerint, se nunquam placidiorem exitum ullius hominis vidisse. Hic igitur felix et bonus Patris obitus me plurimum in doloribus erigit et propterea etiam copiosius de eo scripsi, quod ipsa recordatio me quasi reficiat, et non dubito te haud illubenter et sine taedio haec lecturum, iudico enim, nos hîc tum demum bene vixisse, cum bene morimur. Laboravit autem durissime, ut scis, morbo, quem illi Hyposarca existimaverunt esse, ego Hydropem ex pulmone, nam erat ei difficilis anhelitus, tussis gravissima, tumor varrusque pedis maximus usque ad illa in brachijs macilentus, et quasi in ijs absumebatur, ut vulgariter loquar. Postremo his omnibus malis supervenit febris tertiana acutissima, in cuius tertio paroxysmo extinguebatur die 9. huius mensis, ut dixi, inter primam et 2dam pomeridianam in ipso puncto plenilunij. Aeternus Deus eius animae sit (vt est) misericors et det nobis omnibus felicem et salutarem ex hac calamitosa vita discessum propter filium mediatorem, Amen. Charissime M. ANDREA, scribis, ut mittam tibi aquam quandam febribus curandis appropriatam, quam licet non habeam, nec scio te talem apud me vidisse, tamen non intermittam, quin vobis remedium non minus bonum quam certum et facile in tollendis febribus communicem, quod ab optimo amico Medico, qui hac ratione omnes febres excepta quartana sustulit, vix impetrare potui, licet ei quatuor secreta eius loco docueram. Id autem est tale: Accipe aequales partes ex his duabus herbis Centaurea et Carduobenedicta et decoque in aqua alterius harum stillatitia vel aqua Cichoreae aut etiam communi, demum huic decocto adde pulveris aliquid de inferiori mandibula Lupi piscis, et da ante paroxysinum, nec revela omnibus hanc medicinam, licet admodum levem et facilem, in effectu tamen certum et mirabile. Curantur etiam multi portantes in collo vivam araneam magnam testae nucis inclusam, donec moriatur, idque remedio magnetico, hoc etiam vti potes. Scio autem alia secreta et certa remedia in tollendis febribus cuiusque generis, sed ea potius praesens communicare volo, si opus erit, quam literis scribere. Bene vale. Cantilenas remitto, iam enim potius est tempus flendi quam canendi. Saluta M. JOH. THOMAe meo nomine.</p><p>Helsb. 18. Maij Anno 71.<br>Tycho Brahe</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel. Greeting.</b></p><p>I have received and read your letter, full of the sweetest consolations, which refreshed me not a little when I was stricken with great grief at my dearest father's departure out of this life; and although I could wish it had been possible for it to be written on some other matter, yet, because that [grief] had befallen me beyond all expectation, [your words] were exceedingly welcome to me. And although there are many consolations — theological, sought from the divine oracles of Holy Scripture, and philosophical, drawn from the common lot of mankind and from the instability of sublunary things and their perpetual vicissitude — with which I lift up my afflicted and grieving mind, yet I cannot but take the greatest consolation from this: that my father so gently and piously passed out of this pitiable wretchedness of mortals into the heavenly and everlasting homeland, where, on Paul's witness, we have a firm dwelling and a city, while here we are all exiles. For it can scarcely be written sufficiently with how great confidence and with how great lightness he met his day [of death]. For when on that same day (which was 9 May), before noon, he had received the body and blood of the Son of God in Bread and Wine, according to the testament and institution of Christ our Saviour, and had fortified himself with great confidence by the sayings of Holy Scripture against the terrors of death and the pains of conscience, he then slept until midday or a little beyond; then at last, waking, he gave signs of the death-struggle now at hand, whereupon we all ran up in dismay, and the ministers of the divine word were called to console him; and then he began as it were to fall asleep. At last, his eyes opened, when he saw us sad and weeping standing about the bed, he asked whether any signs of death were to be seen in him — for he said that he did not feel pains so great but that he had endured far greater before; some of us answered that he was very weak, and that we feared he might not recover. He replied: \"Then let me first speak with my wife.\" When she had been brought, half-dead [with grief], and he had given her thanks for having lived with him so well and honourably in this life, he bade her farewell, and commended her to the care of us children and of his other friends; afterward, his eyes again a little closed, he listened to the preacher's consolations, and when the man spoke many things at length, he himself at last, opening his eyes, uttered these words: \"One thing more I wish to say. Does not the Lord himself say: <i>I desire not the death of the sinner, but rather that he be converted and live?</i>\" With this ending he himself closed his eyes and so gently breathed his last that the bystanders doubted whether he had died — for he used to sleep much more heavily than he then was dying, and in the very death-agony he did not once move his body, nor feet, nor arms or hands or head from their place, but so peacefully and gently fell asleep that the preachers themselves bore witness that they had never seen a more peaceful end of any man. This happy and good death of my father, then, lifts me greatly in my griefs, and for that reason too I have written of it more copiously, because the very recollection as it were refreshes me; and I do not doubt that you will read these things not unwillingly and without weariness, for I judge that we have here lived well only then, when we die well. Now he laboured most grievously, as you know, under a disease which they reckoned to be anasarca [general dropsy], but I [reckoned to be] dropsy of the lung, for his breathing was difficult, his cough most severe, and there was a very great swelling and inflammation of the foot, while in those [other parts], in the arms, he was emaciated and was, so to speak, being consumed in them, to put it in common terms. Last of all, upon all these evils there supervened a most acute tertian fever, in whose third paroxysm he was extinguished on the 9th of this month, as I said, between one and two o'clock in the afternoon, at the very point of the full moon. May the eternal God be merciful to his soul (as he is), and grant to us all a happy and salutary departure out of this calamitous life, for the sake of the Son, the mediator. Amen. Dearest Master Anders, you write that I should send you a certain water suited for curing fevers; although I have none, nor do I know that you saw such a thing in my keeping, yet I shall not fail to share with you a remedy no less good than sure and easy for the removal of fevers, which I could scarcely obtain from an excellent friend, a physician, who by this method has removed all fevers except the quartan, though I had taught him four secrets of mine in its stead. It is as follows: Take equal parts of these two herbs, centaury and blessed thistle, and boil [them] in the distilled water of one of these, or in water of chicory, or even in common water; then to this decoction add a little powder from the lower jaw of the wolf-fish [Lupus piscis], and give it before the paroxysm; and do not reveal this medicine to everyone, for though it is very slight and easy, it is nonetheless in its effect sure and wonderful. Many too are cured by carrying at the neck a large live spider enclosed in a nutshell, until it dies — and that by a magnetic [sympathetic] remedy; this also you can use. I know, moreover, other secret and sure remedies for removing fevers of every kind, but I would rather share these in person, if there be need, than write them in a letter. Fare you well. I send back the songs, for now it is rather a time for weeping than for singing. Greet Master Johan Thomesen in my name.</p><p>Helsingborg, 18 May, year [15]71.<br>Tycho Brahe</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe (1546–1601), writing at age 24. <i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the Danish historian, royal chaplain, and ballad-collector — Tycho's close friend; the address-form is the standard Latinization (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> The death of Tycho's father, <b>Otte Brahe</b> (Latin <i>Pater</i>) of Knudstrup, on 9 May 1571; the letter is at once a consolatory reply and a <i>deathbed narration</i> (the <i>ars moriendi</i> form), followed by a fever-remedy at Vedel's request.</li><li><b><i>teste Paulo … firmam mansionem et civitatem … exules</b></i> — \"on Paul's witness … a firm dwelling and a city … exiles\": an allusion to Hebrews 11:9–16 / 13:14 (the patriarchs as strangers and pilgrims seeking \"a city\"); the Pauline attribution of Hebrews is Tycho's own (period-standard) and is preserved, not corrected.</li><li><b><i>Nolo mortem peccatoris sed potius ut convertatur et vivat</b></i> — Otte's dying words, quoting <b>Ezekiel 33:11</b> (Vulgate): \"I desire not the death of the sinner, but rather that he be converted and live.\" Kept as the quotation it is (italicised in translation).</li><li><b>Eucharistic / Lutheran register.</b> <i>corpus et sanguinem filii Dei in Pane et Vino juxta testamentum et institutionem Christi</i> — the explicitly Lutheran sacramental formula (real presence \"in Bread and Wine\"); rendered straight.</li><li><b>Medicine (protocol §5.4).</b> <i>Hyposarca</i> = <b>anasarca (general dropsy)</b> — the diagnosis \"they\" (the attending physicians) gave; <i>Hydrops ex pulmone</i> = \"dropsy of the lung\" — Tycho's own differing diagnosis (the hedge \"I [reckoned]\" preserved; he is offering a competing opinion, not asserting fact). <i>febris tertiana acutissima</i> = \"a most acute <b>tertian fever</b>\"; <i>tertio paroxysmo</i> = \"the third <b>paroxysm</b>.\" <i>quartana</i> = the <b>quartan fever</b>, which the remedy below does <i>not</i> cure.</li><li><b><i>tumor varrusque pedis</b></i> — \"swelling and inflammation of the foot.\" <i>varrus</i> here is read as <i>var(us)/varix</i>-type inflammation/eruption of the foot (a cutaneous/varicose swelling) rather than the orthopaedic <i>varus</i>; the precise pathological referent is uncertain — see crux below.</li><li><b>The fever recipe.</b> <i>Centaurea</i> = <b>centaury</b>; <i>Carduobenedicta</i> (= <i>Carduus benedictus</i>) = <b>blessed thistle</b>; <i>aqua stillatitia</i> = <b>distilled water</b>; <i>aqua Cichoreae</i> = water of chicory; <i>decoctum</i> = <b>decoction</b> (all per §5.4). <i>de inferiori mandibula Lupi piscis</i> = \"from the lower jaw of the <b>wolf-fish</b>\" — <i>Lupus piscis</i>, an early-modern materia-medica ingredient (the jawbone/tooth of a fish called <i>lupus</i>, variously the European seabass or the wolffish/<i>Anarhichas</i>); the species is not securely fixed by the text and is left as \"wolf-fish [<i>Lupus piscis</i>].\" To be given <i>ante paroxysmum</i> (\"before the paroxysm/fit\").</li><li><b><i>remedio magnetico</b></i> — the <b>magnetic (sympathetic) remedy</b>: the live spider worn at the neck in a nutshell until it dies, a period sympathetic-magic febrifuge. Rendered per §5.4 — <i>not</i> modernised to \"placebo\"; the worldview is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Cantilenas remitto … tempus flendi quam canendi</b></i> — \"I send back the songs … a time for weeping rather than for singing\": Vedel had evidently lent or sent songs/ballads (<i>cantilenae</i> — apt for the ballad-collector); Tycho's reply echoes Ecclesiastes 3:4 (\"a time to weep … a time to mourn\"). Kept as the allusion it is.</li><li><b><i>M. JOH. THOMAe</b></i> = <b>Master Johan Thomesen</b> (Johannes Thomae), to be greeted; a mutual acquaintance, otherwise unspecified in the letter. <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (\"Master,\" academic, §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Helsb. 18. Maij Anno 71</i> = \"<b>Helsingborg</b>, 18 May, [15]71\" (O.S. — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The internal date of the death, <i>9 Maij … in ipso puncto plenilunij</i> (\"9 May … at the very point of the full moon\"), is reproduced as written and <b>not</b> astronomically checked or \"corrected\" per protocol §3; Tycho's pairing of the civil time (between 1 and 2 p.m.) with the syzygy is itself of interest and is left to stand.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> The dataset records a single witness, a contemporary copy (<i>afskrift</i>) in Tycho Brahe's private archive, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen; the edition of record is Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i>.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound throughout; the two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed, are (i) the exact pathology of <i>tumor varrusque pedis</i> (<i>varrus</i>: inflammatory/varicose eruption vs. some other lesion) and (ii) the precise species behind <i>Lupus piscis</i> in the recipe (seabass vs. wolffish). Neither affects the sense of the narrative or the structure of the remedy.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images TBOO VII pp.3-5 (the letter to Andreas Severini Vellejus/Anders Sorensen Vedel begins mid-p.3, after an unrelated Aalborg letter of 14 Jan 1568 ends, and concludes mid-p.5, before a different reply-letter begins; p.6, also pulled in by the 4-page slice boundary, belongs to that later unrelated letter and was not needed). Collated the full deathbed narrative and the medical-remedy postscript against Dreyer: the date and time of death (9 May, between 1 and 2pm, at the full moon), the biblical quotation (Ezekiel 33:11, \"Nolo mortem peccatoris...\"), the diagnosis terms (Hyposarca vs Tycho's own Hydrops ex pulmone), the remedy (equal parts centaury and blessed thistle, powder from a wolf-fish's lower jaw, the four secrets withheld from the physician, the spider-in-a-nutshell charm), and the closing date (Helsingborg, 18 May 1571) and addressee (M. Joh. Thomae / Johan Thomesen). All match Dreyer and are rendered accurately. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. (Two words where the long-s glyph could be misread as 'f' at first glance - \"sinite\"/instabilitate-type spots - were checked against source_text and resolved by grammar/context, not counted as defects.)</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15790521001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges a letter of consolation on his father's death and reflects on theological and philosophical sources of comfort available to him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15710518001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15711113001", "d": "1571-11-13", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 13 November 1571 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae … 1568–1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>VERA NOBILITATE ET DIVINAe MATHESEOS COGNITIONE ORNATISSIMO D. TYCHONI BRAHEO, OTTONIDI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO COLENDO, S.</p><p>TERNAS ab Excellentia T. literas, εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών, mihi gratissimas et iucundissimas accepi, tum quod res patriae et tuas secundas nunciabant, tum quod singulare mei studium, quo plurimum delector, prae se ferebant. Etsi enim Patris obitum natura moleste, eundem tamen moderate ferre Philosophia iubet, eoque magis, quod iam tui iuris factus, Aula Cyclopica tanquam Scylla et Charybdi evitata, tibi et Musis, ac potius Christo vivendi summam facultatem habes: qua cordato quidem homini quid queat esse optatius? In deliberatione tua de peregrinatione triplici, meum non est prudentiae tuae quicquam praescribere, et alia fortasse iuvenibus, alia senibus consilia sunt aptiora. Sed tamen si tuo essem loco, qui et opibus et dignitate praestes, et aetate sis integra, de coniugio cogitarem, et re familiari tutanda et propaganda ad posteros, nisi si forte certis de causis hoc consilium tibi displicet. Sit tibi Philosophia et divina Mathesis uxoris loco, donec ijs hanc adiungere lubuerit. Tibi quidem domi tuae Hippocentauri isti negotijs multis a tuo animo alienis molestias facessunt, quas si sustinere non potes et semitam virtutis praecludere animadverteris, non inconsulte feceris, si loci mutatione tales enormitates devitaveris: Et si tunc omnino extra patriam evagari stat sententia, per Belgium et Gallias in Italiam transibis, eaque perlustrata, Augustam petes, nosque denuo invises rediturus in patriam. Rursus itaque tunc (nisi annus hic climactericus me sustulerit) consuetudine tua liberali et omnis eruditionis plena nostraque amicitia praesens denuo fruar, quibus plurimum dolui me destitutum post discessum hunc tuum, a quo gravia interea passus sum. 23. Novemb. superiore periculum magnum sustinui. Hoc autem anno 10. Aprilis nescio quo malo infortunio cecidi graviter de gradibus sive scalis, quibus ascenditur ad Musaeum meum, et deturbatus sum usque ad imum pavimentum in novis his aedibus, quas conduxi a D. AMBROSIO, quem nosti, eratque tantum praecipitium, ut cruento capite et dorso pene fracto aegre resurgens diu Chirurgi opera sim usus. Accesserunt et turbae domesticae perfidia quorundam, quos mihi amicos putavi, et sumtus reditibus majores, cum sordide potius quam splendide vivam, aes alienum inaudita apud nos annonae charitate, mihi attulerunt.</p><p>Valetudo autem, ut fert ingravescens aetas, subinde ruit in deterius, in primis vero me excruciat Lithiasis et Chiragra, quae vereor, ne mihi propediem scribendi facultatem sit ademtura. Nescio quae directio in mea Genesi mihi tanta mala circa haec tempora portendat. Forte Ascendens meum nunc venit ad corpus Martis in ♉ signo terrestri, prout tu eius locum correxisti, de quo mihi antea nescio quid mali ominatus es, postquam meam Genesin aliter verificasti, quam a CYPRIANO LEOVITIO factum est, et praeteritos casus convenientius adaptasti, ubi etiam de futuris nonnulla mihi rectius praedixisti, et cuperem, ut et nunc eandem per otium inspicere velles et de pluribus praemonere. Suades, ut labores et molestias minuam, quod et amice et prudenter suades: idque mihi facere necesse est, praesertim a Typographicis aerumnis liberato. Otium vero hoc iucunde collocare mihi non licet, amicis, quorum consuetudine fruerer, destituto. Nullum enim habeo hîc, postquam tu nos reliquisti, cum quo suaviter converser atque tempus eruditis colloquijs fallam, hoc est, non invenio hominem doctum, ingeniosum, consultum, gravem pariter ac facetum, humanum et candidum, qualis tu nobis fuisti, ceteri omnes aut honores affectant, aut quaestui aut uxoribus, quin et omnibus hisce simul, aliisque huiuscemodi dant operam et ijs ferme solis vivunt. Oportet itaque sortem meam patienter ferre, donec ab hoc ergastulo liberer. Deus Opt. Max. Excell. Tuae vitam diutinam et florentem conservet atque eandem pietate, sapientia, doctrina adaugeat, quae, uti scis, vera bona sunt, superantia ea, quae vulgus affectat. Indica nobis per otium, quid in Astronomicis nunc moliaris, et an haec sublimia studia continuare animus sit; neque tamen dubito, te in ijs, quae ad veram gloriam atque durabilem ducunt, constanter permansurum. Salutant te ambo fratres HAINZELIJ tui amantissimi plurimum et officiose.</p><p>Vale.<br>Idib. Novemb. An. 1571. Augustae Vindelicorum.<br>E. Tuae studiosiss.<br>Hieronymus Wolfius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, most richly adorned with true nobility and with the knowledge of the divine science of mathematics, his lord and friend to be honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>I have received three letters from Your Excellency, O most fortunate Tycho [εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών], most welcome and most delightful to me — both because they reported that affairs at home and your own were prospering, and because they bore witness to your singular regard for me, in which I take the greatest pleasure. For although nature [bids us] bear a father's death with grief, yet Philosophy bids us bear it with moderation — and the more so since now, become your own master, with the Cyclopean Court avoided as if it were Scylla and Charybdis, you have the fullest freedom to live for yourself and the Muses, or rather for Christ: and to a sensible man what could be more to be wished than that? In your deliberation about a threefold journey it is not for me to prescribe anything to your prudence, and perhaps some counsels are fitter for the young, others for the old. Yet were I in your place — you who excel in both wealth and rank, and are in the prime of life — I would think of marriage, and of safeguarding your estate and carrying it on to posterity, unless perhaps for particular reasons this advice displeases you. Let Philosophy and the divine science of mathematics stand to you in a wife's place, until it shall please you to add a wife to them. At home, to be sure, those Hippocentaurs of yours plague you with many affairs foreign to your disposition; and if you cannot endure them, and perceive that they bar your path of virtue, you will act not unwisely if by a change of place you escape such monstrous troubles. And if then your mind is set on roaming quite outside your homeland, you will pass through the Low Countries and France into Italy, and, having surveyed it, make for Augsburg, and visit us again on your way home. So then once more (unless this climacteric year carries me off) I shall enjoy in person your liberal company, full of all learning, and our friendship — of which I grieved bitterly to be bereft after this departure of yours, since which I have meanwhile suffered grave misfortunes. On 23 November last I came through a great danger. And this year, on 10 April, by I know not what evil mischance, I fell heavily down the steps or stairs by which one climbs to my study, and was hurled right to the very floor, in these new lodgings I have rented from Master Ambrosius, whom you know; and the fall was so great that, my head bloodied and my back all but broken, scarcely rising again, I had to use a surgeon's care for a long time. There were added, too, domestic upheavals through the treachery of certain men whom I had reckoned my friends; and expenses greater than my income — though I live meanly rather than splendidly — brought me into debt, amid an unheard-of dearth of provisions among us.</p><p>My health, moreover, as advancing age brings, keeps sliding into the worse; and above all the stone [lithiasis] and gout of the hand [chiragra] torment me, which I fear will shortly rob me of the power of writing. I do not know what direction in my geniture portends such great evils for me around this time. Perhaps my ascendant is now coming to the body of Mars in ♉ Taurus, an earthy sign, as you corrected its place — concerning which you earlier foreboded some evil to me, after you had verified my geniture otherwise than Cyprian Leowitz had done, and fitted my past circumstances more aptly, where too you predicted some things about the future more correctly; and I could wish that now too you would be willing to examine it at your leisure and forewarn me of more. You advise me to lessen my labours and troubles, which you advise both as a friend and wisely; and this I must do, especially now that I am freed from the drudgery of the press. Yet I am not permitted to spend this leisure pleasantly, robbed of the friends in whose company I might delight. For since you left us I have here no one with whom I may converse agreeably and beguile the time in learned discourse — that is, I find no man learned, ingenious, judicious, at once weighty and witty, humane and candid, such as you were to us; all the rest either court honours, or give themselves to gain or to wives — indeed to all these at once, and to others of the kind — and live for these almost alone. And so I must bear my lot patiently, until I am freed from this house of toil. May God, Best and Greatest, keep Your Excellency's life long and flourishing, and increase it in piety, wisdom, and learning — which, as you know, are the true goods, surpassing those the common crowd pursues. Let me know at your leisure what you are now undertaking in astronomy, and whether your mind is to continue these lofty studies; though I do not doubt that you will steadfastly persevere in those things that lead to true and lasting glory. Both the Hainzel brothers, who love you most dearly, send you their warmest and most dutiful greetings.</p><p>Farewell.<br>On the Ides of November [13 November], the year 1571, at Augsburg.<br>Most devoted to Your Excellency,<br>Hieronymus Wolf.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (the \"father of Byzantine studies\"), then directing the city's library and school; an early mentor-friend of Tycho's from his Augsburg years (1569–70).</li><li><b><i>Ottonides</b></i> — \"son of Otte,\" a Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father, who had died (the letter consoles Tycho on it).</li><li><b>Greek:</b> <i>εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών</i> — \"O most fortunate Tycho,\" a learned pun on Tycho's name (Greek <i>eu-tychḗs</i> \"fortunate,\" <i>týchē</i> \"fortune\"); transliterated and glossed, the wordplay flagged rather than flattened.</li><li><b><i>Aula Cyclopica … Scylla et Charybdi</b></i> — \"the Cyclopean Court … Scylla and Charybdis\": Wolf congratulates Tycho on escaping court service (the boorish \"Cyclopes\" of the court) for scholarship; the classical imagery is kept.</li><li><b><i>Hippocentauri isti</b></i> — \"those Hippocentaurs of yours,\" a contemptuous metaphor for the half-civilised people burdening Tycho at home with estate business; kept as metaphor.</li><li><b><i>peregrinatione triplici</b></i> — \"a threefold journey\" (the three-part travel plan Tycho had evidently described: the Low Countries → France → Italy, then back via Augsburg).</li><li><b>Astrology.</b> <i>directio in mea Genesi</i> = \"a <i>direction</i> in my <i>geniture</i>\" (a predictive technique applied to Wolf's natal figure); <i>Ascendens … ad corpus Martis in ♉ signo terrestri</i> = \"the ascendant coming to the body of Mars in Taurus, an earthy sign\"; <i>meam Genesin … verificasti … quam a Cypriano Leovitio</i> — Tycho had re-cast (corrected) Wolf's horoscope more accurately than <b>Cyprian Leowitz</b> (Cyprianus Leovitius, 1524–1574, the celebrated astrologer-astronomer) had. The glyph ♉ is kept in the source and named \"Taurus\" in the translation, per protocol §5.3.</li><li><b>Medicine.</b> <i>Lithiasis</i> = the stone; <i>Chiragra</i> = gout of the hand — both period terms, glossed (protocol §5.4).</li><li><b><i>annus climactericus</b></i> — a <b>climacteric year</b> (an astrologically critical year, at multiples of seven); Wolf (then 55) fears it may kill him.</li><li><b><i>Typographicis aerumnis</b></i> — \"the toils of the press\": Wolf's heavy editorial/printing labours (notably his Greek editions for the Augsburg presses).</li><li><b><i>Musaeum meum</b></i> — \"my study\" (the <i>Museum</i>, scholar's workroom), not a public museum.</li><li><b><i>ergastulo</b></i> — literally a slaves' workhouse/prison; here a metaphor for his wearisome life, rendered \"this house of toil.\"</li><li><b><i>fratres Hainzelii</b></i> — the <b>Hainzel brothers</b>, Johann Baptist and <b>Paul Hainzel</b> of Augsburg, patrician patrons of astronomy; with Paul Hainzel, Tycho had built a great 19-foot quadrant at Göppingen in 1569.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Idib. Novemb. … Augustae Vindelicorum</i> = \"the Ides of November (13 Nov.), … at Augsburg\" (O.S.).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — sound throughout; the only soft point is the precise force of <i>annus climactericus</i> for a man of 55 (a climacteric, not the grand climacteric of 63), flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only residue is the interpretive nuance of </i>annus climactericus<i>; no textual crux, sense secure, dateline clean.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VII p.5[bottom]-p.7[top], read as page images) sentence by sentence against source_text: matches Dreyer exactly throughout, including every proper name (Ambrosius, Cyprianus Leovitius, the Hainzel brothers), number/date (23 November, 10 April, the Ides of November 1571), and the closing formula. The English translation renders the Latin completely and accurately; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15750307001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges three letters from Tycho, congratulates him on newfound independence after his father's death, and offers cautious advice on a planned threefold journey.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15711113001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15720507001", "d": "1572-05-07", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Johannes Pratensis → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 7 May 1572 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>VIRO NOBILI TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDISSIMO.<br>VERNAS SALUTES, CHEIROS ET MELISSAS.</p><p>RECEPI literas tuas, amicissime TYCHO, quas magna cum voluptate legi. Obsecro, dum illas funderes, quis te Olympicus radius tinxit, ut me quoque eadem tinctura figeres? An te sacrum Veneris sulphur, an Bacchi aethereus Mercurius, an dium illud Apollinis sal ab antiquis celebratum afflavit? Non sint mihi Urania et Vulcanus propitij, si isto extemporaneo flumine meum delectaveris plurimum. Non semper praemeditata aut digesta diu felicius cadunt. Extemporanea et impetuosa esse illa necesse est, quae momento solidum penetrantia, penetrata in sui naturam convertunt: ut patres nos nostri et fulmina utriusque Meteorologiae docuerunt. Ubi Soli, quem colo, sacrificabo, supplex ipse precabor, saepius ut tali te calore percellat. Macte sis atque hac mentis sublimitate, paucis cognita, cresce, donec te Iovis armiger ales in solium magni referat Iovis, unde ruinas labantis possis informes cernere mundi. Quod si fieri debet, utique fugiendus Gheburah et malum Aquilo: Auster bonus quaerendus est et vitalis Oriens, ubi vitam, donum et talentum, quod nobis concreditum est, recepimus. Hic Auster Oriensque nec locorum astringitur miserijs, nec certam ad plagam mundi sensibilis declinat: Ubique sunt: intra, extra omnia: in antiqua Iliade, in thesauris Universi, in abyssis animorum: imo intrate; hoc multis quaesitum Latium et Saturnium regnum. Non igitur versus Austrum sensibilem quaerenda illa Olympicae civitatis civilitas: nisi quatenus rigida Septentrio, tantum non tuleris, uti etiam forte non semper feret splendorem, tantumque donum, vel te ipso (quod saepius inaudivi) vate atque interprete. Neque tamen hic, in quo spiramus, Aquilo solus formidatam Barbariem alit. Etenim in extremo Oriente Austroque animas Boreae penetrabile frigus etiam nonnunquam adurit, et latus hoc mundi, quod nebulae malusque Iupiter urget, amoena quoque temperie perfundi dubium non est: illis tamen solis cognita, qui supra temporis et locorum Horizontem lumen intuentur. Profanum itaque sit voluptatis hortum temporum locorumve carceribus includere. Rident hasce exuvias maculis purgatae Lunae et nitentes Berilli. Nosce te ipsum et omnia noveris. In hanc dioptram respicientem nunquam te turbida fallet imago. Hactenus silentia: nunc verbum unum. Venit hisce diebus ad nos Coloniâ imperterritus ille Danus Wiburgensis, cuius apud te saepenumero mentio facta est, Graecus, Arabs, Galenicus, Paracelsicus, Theologaster, Philosophus, Medicus, Syrupiaster, Chymista, et plus, si velles: interim tamen ex his omnibus nihil. Hic Wiburgiam invisurus diu non visit senem parentem, heri profectus est, rediturus ad nos brevi. Conditionem philosophicam captat, aptam Spagiricis pulveribus et sudore Chymico. Est mirabilis Apothecarius: tibi, modo frugi esse velit, non displicebit. Si animus tibi est talem aliquem servitio adiungere, procul dubio in patria et extra patriam utilis tibi esse poterit. Ubi hominem ipse videris, fronte tecum loquetur. Interea si ipse brevi huc non concesseris, de hoc ipso negotio rescribe verbum unum. Sed audi, Nymphas sylvestres violasse te Nympharum Amatores narrant: evulsis sacris quercubus et late minitantibus alnis, vitaque privatis arboribus: quoniam hae dolent, quod morte sua (quam illis te intulisse toties ajebas) alijs sepultis digestisque rebus, vitam calore non posse excitare per te prohibeantur. Certe ut in Austrum vel Occidentem ad nos perferantur ratibus, in medio Aquilone non deerunt Aquilones.</p><p>Vale; et si coronam vernam expetis, Nymphas placa.<br>Hafniae 7. Maij Anno 1572.<br>T.<br>Pratensis</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble Tycho Brahe, his most esteemed lord and friend.</b><br><b>Vernal greetings — of hands and of bees [CHEIROS ET MELISSAS].</b></p><p>I have received your letter, dearest Tycho, which I read with great delight. I beg you — while you were pouring it out, what Olympian ray steeped you, that you should fix me too with the same tincture? Was it the sacred sulphur of Venus, or the ethereal Mercury of Bacchus, or that divine salt of Apollo celebrated by the ancients, that breathed upon you? May Urania and Vulcan be no longer favourable to me, if you have not delighted mine exceedingly with that extemporaneous flood. Things long premeditated or digested do not always fall out more happily. Those things must of necessity be extemporaneous and impetuous which, penetrating a solid body in a moment, once penetrated convert it into their own nature — as our fathers, and the thunderbolts of both Meteorologies, have taught us. When I sacrifice to the Sun, whom I worship, I shall as a suppliant pray that he may smite you more often with such heat. Be blessed, and with this sublimity of mind — known to few — grow, until the winged armour-bearer of Jove bears you back into the throne of great Jove, whence you may discern the shapeless ruins of the tottering world. But if this must come to pass, then by all means Gheburah and the evil North Wind are to be shunned: the good South Wind is to be sought, and the life-giving East, where we received life, that gift and talent which has been entrusted to us. This South and East are neither bound by the miseries of places, nor do they decline toward any fixed quarter of the sensible world: they are everywhere — within, without, in all things: in the ancient Iliad, in the treasuries of the Universe, in the depths of souls; nay, enter in — this is the Latium and the realm of Saturn sought by many. Not, therefore, toward the sensible South is that civility of the Olympian city to be sought — except in so far as you cannot bear the rigid North, just as perhaps it too will not always bear the splendour and so great a gift, even with yourself (as I have often heard tell) as seer and interpreter. And yet not here alone, where we breathe, does the North Wind nourish the dreaded Barbarism. For even in the farthest East and South the penetrating cold of Boreas now and then scorches souls; and that this side of the world, which mists and an evil Jupiter beset, is also bathed in a pleasant temperateness, there is no doubt — yet known to those alone who gaze upon the light above the Horizon of time and place. Let it therefore be a profanation to shut up the garden of delight within the prisons of times or places. At these cast-off skins the Moon, cleansed of her spots, and the gleaming Beryls, smile. Know thyself, and thou shalt know all things. To one who looks back into this lens [dioptra] no turbid image shall ever deceive him. So much for silences: now one word. There came to us these past days from Cologne that undaunted Dane of Viborg, of whom mention has often been made in your presence — a Greek, an Arab, a Galenist, a Paracelsian, a sham theologian, a philosopher, a physician, a syrup-monger, a chymist, and more, if you would; yet meanwhile, out of all these, nothing. He, [meaning] to visit Viborg, has long not visited his aged parent; he set out yesterday, [meaning] to return to us shortly. He is angling for a philosophical post, suited to spagyric powders and chymical sweat. He is a marvellous apothecary: he will not displease you — provided he be willing to be honest. If you have a mind to attach some such man to your service, beyond doubt he could be useful to you both at home and abroad. When you see the man yourself, he will speak with you face to face [from his very brow]. Meanwhile, if you yourself do not come hither shortly, write back one word about this very matter. But hear this: the Lovers of the Nymphs relate that you have done violence to the woodland Nymphs — the sacred oaks torn up, and the alders that threaten far and wide, and the trees robbed of life; for these grieve that by their own death (which you so often used to say you had inflicted on them) they are hindered, by you, from being able to rouse life by heat, once other things have been buried and digested. Certainly, that they may be carried over to us by rafts into the South or the West, in the very midst of the North there will be no lack of North Winds.</p><p>Farewell; and if you long for a vernal crown, appease the Nymphs.<br>At Copenhagen, 7 May, the year 1572.<br>Thine,<br>Pratensis.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), Tycho's physician-friend and intimate, then rising at the University of Copenhagen (professor of medicine from 1571). The May 1572 exchange between the two introduced the subject of Tycho's <i>De nova stella</i> (1573): the new star of November 1572 was found later that year, and the published volume carried Latin poems by friends including Pratensis. This is the affectionate, ostentatiously learned reply to a lost letter of Tycho's that Pratensis judges blazing with inspiration.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>VERNAS SALUTES, CHEIROS ET MELISSAS</i> — \"Vernal greetings, <i>cheiros</i> and <i>melissas</i>.\" The two nouns are transliterated Greek: <b>χείρ / χεῖρας</b> (\"hand[s]\") and <b>μέλισσα / μελίσσας</b> (\"bee[s]\"). The phrase is a precious humanist conceit — \"greetings of hands and of bees,\" i.e. greetings as busy/sweet as bees in spring — kept in Greek-derived form and glossed rather than smoothed. <b>Flagged crux:</b> the exact construction (<i>salutes</i> governing two Greek accusatives) is deliberately mannered; the gloss preserves the playfulness without over-determining it.</li><li><b>Hermetic-alchemical imagery (sustained).</b> The letter is saturated with the language of the <i>prima materia</i> and the alchemical <i>tria prima</i>: <i>sulphur — Mercurius — sal</i> (\"sulphur, Mercury, salt,\" the three principles, here divinised as \"of Venus / of Bacchus / of Apollo\"); <i>tinxit … tinctura</i> (\"steeped … tincture,\" the alchemical <i>tinctura</i> that transmutes); <i>Vulcanus</i> (fire/the alchemist's furnace) paired with <i>Urania</i> (astronomy); <i>Spagiricis pulveribus et sudore Chymico</i> (\"spagyric powders and chymical sweat\" — <i>spagyric</i>, the Paracelsian art of separation-and-recombination, kept as period term). The conceit of the extemporaneous as that which \"penetrates a solid body in a moment and converts it into its own nature\" is alchemical-transmutational imagery applied to literary inspiration; rendered literally and flagged, not paraphrased away.</li><li><b><i>fulmina utriusque Meteorologiae</b></i> — \"the thunderbolts of both Meteorologies,\" i.e. the doctrine of lightning in both the natural-philosophical (Aristotelian <i>Meteorologica</i>) and a second (likely Scriptural/divine, or Paracelsian) sense; the precise pairing is left open and flagged.</li><li><b><i>Gheburah</b></i> — <b>Geburah</b> (Hebrew גְּבוּרָה, \"Severity / Might\"), the fifth Sephirah of the Kabbalistic Tree of Life, associated with judgment, Mars, and the harsh north. <b>Flagged crux:</b> Pratensis pairs <i>Gheburah</i> with <i>malum Aquilo</i> (\"the evil North Wind\") against <i>Auster bonus / vitalis Oriens</i> (\"the good South / life-giving East\") — a Christian-Kabbalistic cosmography in which the rigorous North is to be fled for the merciful South/East. The Hebrew term is kept untranslated in the body and identified here; its force is allusive and not fully recoverable, hence the [C] grade below.</li><li><b>Cardinal-direction allegory.</b> <i>Aquilo / Boreas / Septentrio</i> (North Wind / north) stand for harshness, \"Barbarism,\" and the unwelcoming Danish climate (literal and figurative); <i>Auster</i> (South Wind) and <i>Oriens</i> (East) for life, warmth, and grace. Pratensis insists these are <i>not</i> fixed geographical quarters but inward and everywhere (\"within, without, in all things … in the depths of souls\") — a Neoplatonic-Hermetic interiorising of the compass, kept literal.</li><li><b><i>Iovis armiger ales … in solium magni Iovis</b></i> — \"the winged armour-bearer of Jove [the eagle] … into the throne of great Jove\": the eagle of Jupiter bearing the soul aloft (Ganymede / apotheosis imagery), from which Tycho might \"discern the shapeless ruins of the tottering world\" — a contemplative ascent above the sublunary realm.</li><li><b><i>Latium et Saturnium regnum</b></i> — \"the Latium and realm of Saturn,\" i.e. the Golden Age (Saturn's reign in Latium, <i>Aeneid</i> VIII), here the inward paradise \"sought by many.\"</li><li><b><i>maculis purgatae Lunae et nitentes Berilli</b></i> — \"the Moon cleansed of her spots, and the gleaming Beryls\": the purified Moon and beryl (a scrying-stone) as emblems of the cleansed, all-reflecting soul. <i>Beryllus</i> as a speculum/scrying medium is a Hermetic commonplace; kept literal.</li><li><b><i>Nosce te ipsum</b></i> — \"Know thyself\" (the Delphic γνῶθι σεαυτόν, in Latin); <b><i>dioptram</b></i> = <i>dioptra</i> (a sighting-tube / lens), here a metaphorical optical glass of self-knowledge in which \"no turbid image shall deceive.\" Kept as <i>dioptra</i> and glossed \"lens\"; cf. the optical sense, not Tycho's later instruments.</li><li><b><i>imperterritus ille Danus Wiburgensis</b></i> — \"that undaunted Dane of Viborg\": an unnamed itinerant physician-alchemist from <b>Viborg</b> (Jutland), arrived via <b>Cologne</b> (<i>Colonia</i>), whom Pratensis recommends to Tycho with heavily ironic praise — <i>Graecus, Arabs, Galenicus, Paracelsicus, Theologaster, Philosophus, Medicus, Syrupiaster, Chymista</i> (\"a Greek, an Arab, a Galenist, a Paracelsian, a sham theologian, a philosopher, a physician, a syrup-monger, a chymist\") — \"yet out of all these, nothing.\" <i>Theologaster</i> and <i>Syrupiaster</i> are pejorative coinages (sham-theologian, syrup-quack), preserved. The man \"is angling for a philosophical post\" suited to alchemical work; Pratensis sounds Tycho on hiring him. <b>The figure is unidentified;</b> flagged.</li><li><b><i>fronte tecum loquetur</b></i> — \"he will speak with you from his [own] brow / face to face,\" i.e. you will read the man in his very countenance; idiom kept and glossed.</li><li><b>The Nymphs / felling-of-trees passage.</b> <i>Nymphas sylvestres violasse te … evulsis sacris quercubus et late minitantibus alnis</i> — \"you have done violence to the woodland Nymphs, the sacred oaks torn up and the wide-threatening alders [felled].\" A playful, semi-mythological reproach: Tycho is charged (by the \"Lovers of the Nymphs\") with clearing timber — <i>and</i> the conceit is alchemical, for the felled trees \"grieve\" that, killed by Tycho (<i>morte sua, quam illis te intulisse toties ajebas</i>), they cannot now \"rouse life by heat\" (i.e. serve as fuel/fire for his furnace) \"once other things have been buried and digested.\" <b>Flagged crux:</b> the passage fuses a literal estate-clearing (Tycho cutting wood, on Knudstrup land or for fuel) with an alchemical pun on death/putrefaction/heat (<i>sepultis digestisque rebus … vitam calore … excitare</i>) and an <i>Aquilones</i>-wordplay (<i>\"in the midst of the North there will be no lack of North Winds [Aquilones]\"</i>, the wind that drives the rafts). The double sense — sylvan complaint and chymical allegory — is rendered as literally as the syntax allows and not resolved into one reading; this is the principal driver of the overall grade.</li><li><b><i>coronam vernam … Nymphas placa</b></i> — \"if you long for a vernal crown, appease the Nymphs\": a closing flourish returning to the spring/Nymph conceit of the salutation.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Hafniae 7. Maij Anno 1572</i> = \"At <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), 7 May 1572\" (O.S.; Denmark was on the Julian calendar). The subscription <i>T.</i> abbreviates <i>Tuus</i> (\"Thine\"). No in-letter date discrepancy.</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Latin corrected 'Olympiae' to 'Olympicae' (Dreyer VII p.7) and removed the duplicated pronoun in 'profectus is est' to 'profectus est' (Dreyer VII p.8); English unaffected in both cases.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the epistolary frame, the alchemical <i>tria prima</i> conceit, the cardinal-direction allegory, and the Viborg-physician notice are rendered securely. The grade is set by three genuine cruxes that are flagged, not smoothed: (1) <b>Gheburah</b> and the Christian-Kabbalistic North/South cosmography, whose precise doctrinal load is not fully recoverable; (2) the Greek <i>CHEIROS ET MELISSAS</i> salutation conceit; and (3) the <b>Nymphs / felled-trees</b> passage, which deliberately superimposes a literal timber-clearing on an alchemical putrefaction-and-fire allegory. An honest [C] is preferred here to a falsely fluent [A].</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer (text matches the working transcription): the three cruxes are interpretive, not textual. <i>Geburah</i> = the 5th Sephirah, the 'Pillar of Severity' (left/north, Mars, restrictive judgment) per the standard Agrippan/Christian-Kabbalist correspondence; Pratensis's specific application remains an idiosyncratic literary fusion. [C] stands (interpretive).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.6-9 as page images; the audited letter (Johannes Pratensis to Tycho, 7 May 1572) runs pp.7(bottom)-8, between the Wolfius letter and Tycho's reply of 17 May. Collated the whole letter clause by clause — opening/closing lines, the Gheburah/Auster/Aquilo passage, the Danus Wiburgensis paragraph, and the Nymphs passage — against Dreyer; no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation of substance found, only the two low-severity single-word/single-word-insertion text errors above. The item was already published at grade C (self-assigned, per brief treated as unverified); this independent collation confirms C is warranted, though for the specific low-severity text errors identified here rather than any of the 'serious' defect types (invented day-numbers, fabricated clauses, flipped pronouns, dropped sentences) described in the brief — none of those were found in this letter.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints the adjective 'Olympicae' (Olympic/Olympian); source_text has 'Olympiae' (genitive of the place-name Olympia), a different word. The English translation ('Olympian city') actually matches Dreyer's true reading — the corruption sits only in the source_text field, not in the rendering. Dreyer prints: <i>illa Olympicae civitatis civilitas (TBOO VII p.7)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text inserts an extra pronoun 'is' not present in Dreyer's 'profectus est'. A minor duplication; the English translation is not affected by it. Dreyer prints: <i>heri profectus eft, rediturus ad nos brevi (TBOO VII p.8)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects against the Dreyer page images: p.7 prints 'illa Olympicae civitatis civilitas' and p.8 prints 'heri profectus est, rediturus ad nos brevi' (no 'is'). Both source_text corruptions confirmed and corrected; the English translation already matched Dreyer's true readings in both places.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15720517001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15720517001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Responds with playful alchemical and Neoplatonic imagery to praise the extemporaneous quality of Tycho's writing, comparing its heat to solar and divine inspiration.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15720507001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15720517001", "d": "1572-05-17", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Herrevad", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Pratensis — Herrevad, 17 May 1572 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (hendecasyllabic verse, written out as prose) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(ed. J. L. E. Dreyer, Hauniae 1913–29), vol. VII pp. 9–10 · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (fol. 40)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> The hendecasyllabic poem that the rubric announces — <b>absent from the working transcription</b> when this file was first made — has now been <b>recovered and supplied from Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 9–10</b> (= scan `brahe-t_opera_07.pdf` PDF pp. 23–24), de-spaced from the scan's OCR. Long-s misreadings are silently normalised (<i>suêre→fuêre</i>, <i>srugum→frugum</i>); a stray footnote-anchor caret after <i>Monstrabit</i> is dropped; one genuinely uncertain word is flagged in the Apparatus (the subject <i>is</i> in <i>\"quas nunc is … intulêre Musis\"</i>, where the plural verb wants a plural subject — <i>ii</i>/<i>hi</i>). The poem is a warm verse-letter answering Pratensis's of 7 May 1572: a humanist exhortation to scorn scholastic \"Barbarism\" and climb to the study of Earth and Heaven, then practical business — a prospective alchemical assistant (\"that undaunted Dane\"), the playfully mythologised delay in delivering felled timber (\"despoiling the Nymphs,\" forbidden by Ceres until the harvest rites are done), and a request for distilling <b>cucurbits</b> from the potter Andreas.</p>", "la": "<p>Sequitur mea responsio, quae carmine constat hendecasyllabico instar prosae consignato.</p><p>DOCTORI MEDICO JOHANNI PRATENSI, INPRIMISQUE SUO COLENDO AMICO. VULCANUM, URANIAM, JOVEM FAVENTES.</p><p>Servus noster, ut est domum reversus, asportavit epistolam remissam a te, care Pratensis, qua relecta, dici vix ego credo posse, quanta nobis hinc animi fuit voluptas. Namque haec abditiore visa vere est rerum cognitione plena, plusque antiquae sophiae tenere, quam nunc suetum est (proh dolor) in scholis doceri. Sed nemo tamen invidebit illis, spero, Barbariem merasque nugas, quas nunc dulcibus intulêre Musis. Namque hunc quis revocabit? quis reducet? Monstrabitne viam, suapte si quis mavult sponte via carere vera. Sed tu, docte Pratensis, nil moratus illos (ut facis) altiora scande, et Terrae simul et Poli supernos perlustrans aditus labore suavi, vulgo subtrahe te caduca temnens. Major gloria namque rebus altis debetur, nec habent pusilla laudem. Quid cessas? remoras habere nullas mens prognata polo potest, sed illa sublimis penetrat domos Deorum, libans Ambrosiam et suave nectar, quod dijs Jupiter ipse miscet, et dijs (si qui sunt) genitis simul ministrat etc. Sed cur digredior, mororve multis? Praestat nunc, ut epistolae tuae remittam responsum, brevibus quod his habeto.</p><p>Imperterritus ille Danus ad me quamprimum fuerit reversus illinc, mox se conferat huc iubeto, huncque praesentem cupio videre praesens, et cognoscere num sit aptus ille nostris moribus, et quid ipse sese facturum statuat, velitne furnis inservire meis fovens labores Vulcani et studijs vacare nostris, mecumque perque aliquod manere tempus. Tum demum statuam, quid inde fiet.</p><p>De Nymphis etiam scias, Pratensis, illas me spoliasse sat revera Alnis quercubus et suis capillis. Verum quod spolia illa non fuêre ad vos hactenus, ut decet, relata, in causa est Dea quae vocatur Alma, et frugum genetrix Ceres, quod illa cernens prae foribus suos labores instare, agricolas iuvare causam Nympharum vetuit, simul minata, se non propitiam futuram in illud tempus, quando solet venire messis, si Nymphas colerent diebus illis, quos sacros Cereri dedit vetustas. Coguntur miseri parere Divae, quae fructus tribuit Soli quotannis. Silvestres potius volunt vocare Nymphas, quam Cereris carere donis. Ast quando fuerint peracta sacra, quae nunc frugiferae Deae celebrant, Nymphas agricolae iuvare debent, vel si forte alicui interesse sacris (ut fit) displicet, otiosus ille Nympharum spolijs onustus ad vos mittetur, faciam ut ferat ferenda. Sed nunc scribere plura vix vacabit. Ergo care Pratensis, valeto. Et cum sis medicus, valere semper ut possis, facile est: saluta amicos. Fac me posse cucurbitas habere, quas vestrum figulum parare nobis (ut spero) impiger Andreas procurat. Dicas huic etiam precor salutem. Herrevadi, 17. Maij Anno 1572.</p><p>Tycho Brahe tibi dicatus.</p>", "en": "<p>There follows my reply, which consists of a hendecasyllabic poem set down in the manner of prose.</p><p><b>To the Doctor of Medicine Johannes Pratensis, and most especially to his cherished friend. May Vulcan, Urania, and Jove be favorable.</b></p><p>Our servant, as soon as he had got home, brought the letter sent back by you, dear Pratensis; and when I had read it over, I scarcely think it can be told how great a delight of mind it gave us. For it seemed truly full of a more hidden knowledge of things, and to hold more of the ancient wisdom than it is now the custom (alas!) to teach in the schools. Yet no one, I hope, will envy those [schools] the Barbarism and the mere trifles which [they] have now brought in upon the sweet Muses. For who will call it back? who will lead it back again? Does he [only] point out the road [to ruin] who, of his own will, prefers to do without the true way? But you, learned Pratensis — heeding them not at all (as is your wont) — climb to higher things, and, surveying with sweet toil the upper approaches alike of Earth and of Heaven, withdraw yourself from the crowd, scorning the things that perish. For greater glory is owed to lofty matters, and little things win no praise. Why do you hold back? A mind born of heaven can brook no hindrance, but, sublime, it pierces to the homes of the Gods, sipping Ambrosia and sweet nectar — which Jupiter himself mixes for the gods, and serves at the same time to the gods (if any there be) [that are] begotten — and so on. But why do I digress and delay with many words? Better now that I send back the answer to your letter, which take in these few [lines].</p><p>As soon as that undaunted Dane has come back to me from there, bid him betake himself hither at once; and I desire, myself present, to see him in person, and to learn whether he is suited to our ways, and what he resolves to do — whether he is willing to tend my furnaces, fostering the labors of Vulcan, and to give himself to our studies, and to stay with me for some space of time. Then at last I shall decide what is to come of it.</p><p>About the Nymphs, too, you should know, Pratensis, that I have indeed despoiled them well enough of their alders and oaks and their tresses [their leafage]. But that those spoils have not yet been delivered to you, as is fitting, the cause is the Goddess called Alma, and Ceres, mother of the crops: for she, seeing her own labors pressing at the door [the harvest near], forbade the farmers to assist the Nymphs' cause, threatening at the same time that she would not be favorable at that season when the harvest is wont to come, if they should court the Nymphs on the days antiquity made sacred to Ceres. The poor wretches are forced to obey the Goddess, who grants the soil its fruits each year; they would sooner summon the woodland Nymphs [later] than go without Ceres' gifts. But once the rites are accomplished that they now celebrate for the fruit-bearing Goddess, the farmers must aid the Nymphs; or if it happens to displease anyone to take part in the rites (as happens), that idle fellow, laden with the Nymphs' spoils, shall be sent to you — I will see that he carries what is to be carried. But now there will scarcely be leisure to write more. So, dear Pratensis, farewell. And since you are a physician, it is easy for you to be able to be always well: greet our friends. See that I can get the <b>cucurbits</b> [gourd-shaped distilling vessels] which your potter — the tireless Andreas, I hope — is seeing to make for us. Bid him too, I pray, good health. Herrevad, 17 May, in the year 1572.</p><p>Tycho Brahe, devoted to you.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Herrevad in May 1572, before <i>De nova stella</i> and his establishment on Hven; in this period resident at Herrevad Abbey with his maternal uncle Steen Bille, already deep in alchemy (the \"labors of Vulcan,\" the furnaces and cucurbits below).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Johannes Pratensis</i> = <b>Hans Philipsen / Johannes Pratensis</b> (1543–1576), Tycho's physician-friend at Copenhagen (later professor of medicine there), one of his closest correspondents in 1572 and, per <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i> (2002), \"the first to hear Tycho's ideas on the nova of 1572\" (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Herrevad</i> — Herrevad Abbey (Herrevadskloster) in Scania, where Tycho was then living and working with Steen Bille; kept as is per protocol §5.7. (Dreyer's scan prints the dateline place as the OCR-garbled <i>\"Herizuad\"</i> = Herrevad.)</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Tycho's reply to a letter of <b>Johannes Pratensis dated 7 May 1572</b> (Copenhagen, = idx brahe-t_15720507001). The two were actively exchanging Latin verse as letter-content in these weeks, months before the <i>Carmen ad fratrem</i> was printed.</li><li><b><i>carmen hendecasyllabicum … instar prosae consignato</b></i> — \"a hendecasyllabic poem set down in the manner of prose\": the reply is in eleven-syllable (Phalaecian) verse, but <b>written out continuously, not laid out line-by-line as verse</b>. This is a <b>distinct</b> poem from the <i>Carmen ad fratrem ejus gemellum</i> of 1572 (which is in dactylic distichs); the rubric is Dreyer's, from cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 fol. 40r. The translation follows the as-prose layout.</li><li><b>The superscription.</b> <i>VULCANUM, URANIAM, JOVEM FAVENTES</i> — \"(invoking) Vulcan, Urania, and Jove favorable\": the three patrons frame the letter's three strands — <b>Vulcan</b> (the alchemical furnace-work), <b>Urania</b> (astronomy), <b>Jove</b> (good fortune/friendship). A characteristic Tychonic sign-off flourish in embryo.</li><li><b>The humanist exhortation.</b> The opening movement praises Pratensis's letter as full of \"more hidden knowledge\" and \"ancient wisdom,\" and contrasts it with the \"Barbarism and strange trifles\" of the contemporary \"schools\" — Tycho urging his friend to \"climb to higher things\" and survey \"Earth and Heaven,\" scorning the perishable crowd. The Neoplatonic image of the heaven-born mind \"piercing to the homes of the Gods, sipping Ambrosia and nectar\" is the period commonplace, rendered without sharpening.</li><li><b>The \"undaunted Dane.\"</b> A prospective <b>alchemical assistant</b> — Tycho asks that the young man, once back, come to Herrevad so Tycho can judge whether he is \"willing to tend my furnaces, fostering the labors of Vulcan, and give himself to our studies.\" Concrete evidence of Tycho's 1572 alchemical establishment and his recruiting for it.</li><li><b>The Nymphs / Ceres allegory (the literary centrepiece).</b> A practical matter — the delivery of <b>felled timber</b> (alders and oaks, \"the Nymphs' tresses\") — is dressed as myth: the spoils cannot yet be sent because <b>Ceres</b> (the harvest-goddess, here also <i>Alma</i>) has forbidden the farmers to work for the woodland <b>Nymphs</b> during the days \"antiquity made sacred to Ceres,\" on pain of a poor harvest; once the harvest rites are done, the timber will come. A deliberate superimposition of literal timber-clearing on a mythological-religious conceit, rendered faithfully rather than flattened to its prose sense.</li><li><b><i>cucurbitas</b></i> — <b>cucurbits</b>: gourd-shaped earthenware <b>distilling vessels</b> (the lower vessel of an alembic), to be made by the potter <b>Andreas</b>. Another alchemical-apparatus detail, consistent with the furnace-work above. <i>figulum</i> = potter.</li><li><b>Textual note (poem now supplied).</b> The hendecasyllabic poem, absent from the working transcription, is <b>supplied from Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 9–10</b> (cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7, apograph; with corrections from 10686.66) and de-spaced from the scan OCR. One genuine reading-crux remains: in <i>\"Barbariem mirasque nugas, quas nunc <b>is</b> … intulêre Musis\"</i> the plural verb <i>intulêre</i> wants a plural subject, so the transmitted <i>is</i> is probably <i>ii</i>/<i>hi</i> (\"they,\" i.e. the schools/barbarians); flagged <i>[ii?]</i>, not silently emended. Long-s OCR misreadings (<i>suêre→fuêre</i>, <i>srugum→frugum</i>) are normalised and declared.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>17 May 1572</i>, Old Style (Julian); Denmark kept O.S. until 1700 (protocol §3). The dateline is internal to the poem (<i>Herrevad, 17 May 1572</i>) and matches the dataset label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Four Latin corruptions in the hendecasyllabic poem corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII pp.9-10: a corrupted \"mirasque\" plus a phantom bracketed \"is [ii?]\" restored to \"merasque\" (with the phantom word removed); \"Monstrabit\" restored to the interrogative \"Monstrabitne\"; \"siet\" restored to \"fiet\"; and \"(ut sit)\" restored to \"(ut fit)\". English corrected to match at three of the four places where sense was affected: \"strange\" to \"mere\" trifles, the flat statement recast as a rhetorical question, and \"(as may be)\" to \"(as happens)\"; the fiet/siet correction needed no English change since the existing rendering already carried the future sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the recovered hendecasyllabic poem is now translated in full, register-matched (warm humanist exhortation + practical alchemical/estate business + the mythological Nymphs/Ceres conceit), with the controlled glossary and the Vulcan/Urania/Jove framing preserved. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02):</b> the former crux — <i>\"source as held is incomplete,\" the poem untranslated</i> — is <b>resolved</b> by supplying the poem from Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 9–10. It is [B] rather than [A] for two minor residues, both flagged not smoothed: (1) the one uncertain word <i>is [ii?]</i> in the \"Barbarism\" sentence; (2) the as-prose layout of the verse (rendered as prose, per the rubric), so the metrical line-division is not represented.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.9-10 in full as page images, with particular attention to the hendecasyllabic poem per the brief's note that it was restored by an earlier pass. The restoration does NOT hold up clean: four word-level corruptions cluster inside the poem itself (merasque-&gt;mirasque plus a phantom 'is [ii?]' insertion; the dropped -ne on Monstrabitne; fiet-&gt;siet; ut fit-&gt;ut sit), while the prose second half of the letter (the Danish assistant, the Nymphs passage, the cucurbits, the closing 'Herrevadi/Herizuad. 17. Maij Anno 1572. TYCHO BRAHE tibi dicatus.') matches Dreyer exactly. Individually each defect is low/medium severity and none invents a fact wholesale, but their concentration in precisely the passage flagged for verification means the 'restored' poem cannot be certified clean -- hence SERIOUS/C rather than a pass.</p><p><b>4 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's poem reads 'merasque' (mere/sheer trifles) with no 'is'/'ii' present at all; our source_text corrupts the word to 'mirasque' (strange/wondrous) and inserts a phantom, editorially-flagged 'is [ii?]' that is simply not in Dreyer. The English follows the corrupted Latin ('strange' rather than 'mere'). Dreyer prints: <i>Barbariem merasque nugas, quas nunc dulcibus intulêre Musis</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's poem has the interrogative enclitic -ne (Will he show the way...?); our source_text drops it, turning a rhetorical question into a flat statement. Dreyer prints: <i>Monstrabitne viam</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints 'fiet' (it will become/happen, future indicative of fio); source_text has 'siet' (an archaic subjunctive of esse), a different word. Dreyer prints: <i>quid inde fiet</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints 'ut fit' (as happens/is the case); source_text has 'ut sit' (subjunctive of esse), changing the parenthetical's grammatical mood and licensing the looser English 'as may be' rather than 'as happens'. Dreyer prints: <i>(ut fit)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All four defects verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.9-10 page images (not the OCR text layer): merafque (l.14), Monftrabitne (l.16), fiet (l.30 p.9), and (ut fit) (p.10 l.3) all read exactly as Dreyer's claim states. No other part of the letter was touched; every changed word is accounted for in outcomes above.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15720507001", "next": "brahe-t_15730416001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15720507001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15730416001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Replies in hendecasyllabic verse cast as prose, expressing delight at Pratensis's letter and lamenting the displacement of true learning by scholastic barbarism.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15720517001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15730416001", "d": "1573-04-16", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Johannes Pratensis → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 16 April 1573 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(ed. J. L. E. Dreyer, Hauniae 1913–29), vol. VII pp. 10–13 · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> Published until 2026-07-11 as <i>lost</i>. That was an ingest defect: the tekstnet scrape stored the editorial note that stands <i>before</i> this letter (<i>Responsio ad has interijt…</i>, which refers to a different, genuinely lost reply) and dropped the letter itself. The letter survives complete — TBOO VII pp. 10–13 — and is given here in full for the first time.</p>", "la": "<p>VIRO VERE NOBILI ET GENEROSO TYCHONI BRAHE DE KNUDSTRUP, DOMINO OPTIMO ET AMICO SYNCERO. SALUTEM ET VER MELISSEUM.</p><p>RECEPI literas tuas, TYCHO optime, quibus negotia tua promovenda apud Artifices tuos mechanicos committis. Quod tardius ad te redeat hic tuus Tabellarius, in causa fuit, quod nundinæ Roschildenses me ultra propositum detinuerint: ubi mihi tantisper commorandum fuit, donec de vase meo et bonis Francofurto per Flensburgenses mercatores missis, fieri poteram certior.</p><p>Negotia tua sic se habent. Absolutæ sunt imagines omnes in Globo tuo. Divisus Meridianus et Horizon, paratum pedestale, sed hîc difficultas est non parva, de inauratione videlicet, de coloratione, seu pictura, ut scribis. Primum quidem Aurifaber non confidit se super argenteam totius Globi faciem, aureas stellas, cum debita imaginum repræsentatione, inducere posse. Etenim multis rationibus apud me nunc, tum apud te antea id explicavit. Quod ad Pictorem attinet, ille nunquam circulos illos et divisiones tam exacte, ut nunc insculptæ, exprimet, sed incrustabit quidem colorum crusta superficiem tersam et levem, quæ obtenebrabunt ea, quæ Aurifaber subtiliter delineavit. Ut interim sileam, quomodocunque hoc fuerit, non fore tandem diuturnum (etenim multis illa via incommodis obnoxia est) quam cito poterit vel læsione aliqua vel fortuito attactu pars crustæ coloratæ enucleari?</p><p>Sed audi meum consilium, quod ex Aurifabri quoque sententia tibi denuncio. Opus est nobile et regium imo aureum. Et quia Horizon et Meridianus et pedes deaurari debent, agedum deauremus prius totam superficiem Globi, hac tamen ratione, ut imagines ipsæ una cum stellis appareant minus tersæ: <i>das ist matt</i>, ut illi loquuntur. Partes autem intermediæ, seu superficies ipsa Globi polienda erit, et ad micationem et splendorem rutilantem deducenda, qui quoque splendor in ipsis stellis (si voluerimus) indui possit, ut iubar illud aureum, tanquam scintillans ex sublustri imaginis lumine, emicet.</p><p>Ita et forma illa stellarum insculpta exacte repræsentabit, quarum cuspidum subtilitatem coloribus æmulari, difficile est. Illi enim tenues illas cuspides et cavitates, quæ toti figuræ decus adijciant, oblinient, et replebunt venustate istorum angulorum deformata. Ita tandem aureus erit Globus, simplicitate cœli materiam et faciem æmulans, sic pedes capiti respondebunt, longe ablegatis versicoloribus et muliebribus illis oblectamentis oculorum: quæ quosdam delectant, ut monstra in picturis. Cedant variegationes instabiles, color unicus esto: color aureus esto. Sic non, si cervix equina humano capiti adiuncta fuerit, in piscem desinet atrum.</p><p>Sed mi TYCHO, hoc meum consilium est, tuo illæso et salvo iudicio. Ad pedem, Meridianum et Horizontem deaurandum significat se opus habere ad minimum 4 roseis nobel: si nostrum consilium amplexus fueris, addantur 3 vel 4 ad summum, et totum sese oculis nostris Silenus ille aperiet aureus. Tu imprimis sententiam tuam super hac re significa, et Aurifaber dicit se aurum hîc habere non posse: tuum erit ad pedes et Meridianum et Horizontem 4 nobiles rosatos, ut dixi, inprimis mittere. STEPHANUS hodie vel cras imponet rotulas, et experietur processum, urgebo quantum potero et promovebo. Instrumentum ferreum bene procedit, hac septimana, imo vesperi absolvetur, ut promisit Artifex.</p><p>Quod ad Typographum attinet, post prandium intelliges, tunc plura addam, iam enim animus est in patinis. Figuræ nunc excinduntur, instigo quantum possum. De papyro difficultas magna est: sed tamen corrademus. De epistola mea et carmine, quia nondum propter occupationes confecta sunt, ne sis solicitus, quoniam tota posterior pars operis primum absolvenda est, tuque interea nobis aderis. Sed ante 4 dies certum nuntium habebis, cum quo omnia mittam necessaria.</p><p>Titulum quoque una mitto, quem præfigemus operi; consulimus, ut ita relinquas. Etenim singula verba perlustravimus diligenter, sed si quid mutari voles, fiet id ubi veneris satis mature, quoniam titulus postremo cudetur. De reliquis iubeo te non esse sollicitum: curabimus ea diligenter. Verbum illud, quod titulo assuisti LVCVBRATIONES, nescio, an hîc locum habere possit. Nocturnæ quidem sunt, sed an lucubrationibus Atticis respondeant, etiamsi non dubitem: tamen ne magno aliquid hiatu promittamus et speciosis titulis et vastis fores exornemus, author sum. Non, quod etiam augustiore inscriptione dignum hoc opus tuum non iudicem: sed ut moderata illa submissiore in ipso limine adhibita, in ipso operis incalescentis progressu, potentius in animum lectorum, tantorum mysteriorum non præscium illabamur. Summa, titulum hunc immutare quoties libuerit, et Genialis tulerit impetus, dabitur occasio. Si pro contemplatione mathematica considerationem mathematicam poni voles, unum erit.</p><p>Lucubrationibus operam dant nigra lolligine ad lucernam chartas imbuentes, lucubrationibus pallescunt ad oleum legentes. Lucubrantur quoque qui ad mediam noctem vel Auroram ipsam madidi sunt. Et aurigis nautisque, si quibus alijs, suæ sunt lucubrationes. Nos exemti prostituta illa æquivocatione vocabuli, rerumque Domini facti, homonymias in eas terras relegabimus, in quibus vivos homines mortui incursant boves. Sed hæc post prandium. Nos sacra inebriet ambitio, non e vulgi fornicationibus petita, sed e castis Uraniæ et sororum Vestalium adytis etc.</p><p>Pro lignis mihi transmissis et dono datis gratias ago quam possum maximas, relaturus si potero. Ita hominum bonorum res comparata est, ut alij aliorum beneficis egeant. Vos tanto culmine mundi positi nobis inferioribus Dij esse potestis aut lupi, si exteriorem Anatomiam consideremus. Si abditam illam et interiorem, tu quoque et par et superior esse et potes, et, si cunabula vestra spectetis, debetis merito. Tu non exutus exteriori vinculo, etiam interiore nobiscum libertate triumphas, qua civitate donatus et eiusdem Reipublicæ civis et Augustus semper. Reliqua occupat fornicaria et infidum vulgus pellicibus satiatur suis. Illa pulchritudo te cæcum habet, oculis orbate sensilibus lucem ne respice.</p><p>Vale. Hafniæ raptissime 16. Aprilis Anni 1573.</p><p>Pratensis</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the truly noble and high-born Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, best of patrons and unfeigned friend. Greeting, and a spring of honey.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, excellent Tycho, in which you commit to me the furthering of your business with your mechanical craftsmen. That this courier of yours comes back to you later than expected was owing to the Roskilde fair, which held me beyond what I had intended: I had to wait there until I could get certain word of my cask and of the goods sent from Frankfurt by the Flensburg merchants.</p><p>Your affairs stand thus. All the figures on your globe are finished. The meridian and the horizon are divided, the pedestal is ready — but here the difficulty is not small: namely the gilding, and the colouring or painting, as you write. First, the goldsmith has no confidence that he can lay golden stars, with the figures duly represented, over the silvered face of the whole globe; he has explained this to me now at length, as he did to you before. As for the painter: he will never render those circles and divisions as exactly as they now stand engraved. He will merely crust over the clean, smooth surface with a crust of colours, and these will darken what the goldsmith has finely drawn. And let me say nothing, meanwhile, of the fact that however it turns out it will not last — for that method is exposed to many mishaps: how quickly might a piece of the coloured crust be picked away by some knock or a chance touch?</p><p>But hear my counsel, which I give you with the goldsmith's judgement behind it. The work is noble and royal — nay, golden. And since the horizon, the meridian and the feet must be gilded, come then: let us gild the whole surface of the globe first, but on this principle — that the figures themselves, together with the stars, should appear <i>less</i> burnished: <i>das ist matt</i>, as they say. The parts between, that is the surface of the globe itself, must be polished and brought to a glittering, ruddy brilliance; and that brilliance could be laid on the stars themselves too (if we wish), so that the golden radiance may flash out as if sparkling from the faint light of the figure.</p><p>In that way the engraved form of the stars will also represent them exactly — stars whose fineness of point it is hard to rival in colours. For the painters will smear over those slender points and hollows that give the whole figure its beauty, and will fill them in, the grace of those angles being spoilt. So at last the globe will be golden, rivalling in its simplicity the substance and the face of heaven; so the feet will answer to the head, once those particoloured, womanish delights of the eye are sent packing — the things that please certain people, as monsters do in paintings. Let unstable variegation give way; let there be one colour only: let the colour be gold. Then it will not happen that, a horse's neck having been joined to a human head, the thing tails off into a black fish.</p><p>But, my Tycho, this is my counsel — your own judgement untouched and intact. For gilding the foot, the meridian and the horizon he indicates that he needs at least 4 rose nobles; if you adopt our plan, let 3 or 4 more be added at the most, and that golden Silenus will open himself whole to our eyes. Signify your view on this above all — and the goldsmith says he cannot get gold here: it will be for you to send, first of all, the 4 rose nobles for the feet, the meridian and the horizon, as I have said. Stephanus will fit the little wheels today or tomorrow and will test the working of it; I shall press and further it as much as I can. The iron instrument is coming on well: it will be finished this week — indeed this evening, as the craftsman has promised.</p><p>As for the printer, you shall hear after lunch; then I shall add more, for at this moment my mind is on the dishes. The figures are being cut now; I urge it on as much as I can. About the paper there is great difficulty — but we shall scrape some together. As for my letter and my poem: do not be anxious that they are not yet finished, my occupations being what they are, since the whole latter part of the work has to be completed first, and you will be with us in the meantime. But within four days you will have a sure messenger, by whom I shall send everything needful.</p><p>I send the title as well, which we shall prefix to the work; we advise you to leave it as it stands, for we have gone over every single word with care. But if you should want anything altered, it can be done when you come, in good time, since the title is struck off last. About the rest I bid you not to worry: we shall see to it diligently. That word which you have stitched onto the title — LUCUBRATIONES — I do not know whether it can have a place here. Nocturnal these studies certainly are; but whether they answer to the <i>Attic</i> lucubrations — though I do not doubt it — still I am of the opinion that we should not promise something with a great gaping mouth, nor dress out the doorway with showy and cavernous titles. Not that I judge this work of yours unworthy even of a more august inscription; but that, a modest and more subdued one being used on the very threshold, we may steal the more powerfully into the minds of readers — who are not forewarned of such great mysteries — as the work warms to its progress. In sum, there will be occasion to change this title as often as you please, and as the genial impulse takes you. If you should want <i>mathematical consideration</i> put in place of <i>mathematical contemplation</i>, it will be all one.</p><p>They give their labour to \"lucubrations\" who steep their paper in black cuttlefish-ink by lamplight; readers grow pale over the oil at their \"lucubrations\". They \"lucubrate\" too who are soused till midnight or till dawn itself. And carters and sailors, if anyone, have lucubrations of their own. Let us, freed from that prostituted equivocation of the word, and made masters of the matter, banish such homonymies to those lands where dead oxen charge at living men. But of this after lunch. Let a sacred ambition intoxicate us — one sought not from the fornications of the crowd, but from the chaste sanctuaries of Urania and her Vestal sisters, etc.</p><p>For the timber sent me and given as a gift I give the greatest thanks I can, and shall repay it if I am able. Such is the condition of good men, that some have need of the kindnesses of others. You who are set at so great a height of the world can be to us below either gods or wolves, if we consider the outward anatomy; if that hidden and inward one, then you too can be both our equal and our superior — and, if you look to your cradle, you deservedly ought to be. You, not stripped of the outward bond, yet triumph with us in an inward liberty; endowed with that citizenship, a citizen of that same commonwealth, and always Augustus. The rest is taken up by the harlot, and the faithless crowd is sated with its concubines. That beauty holds you blind; you who are bereft of the eyes of sense, do not look back at the light.</p><p>Farewell. Copenhagen, in the greatest haste, 16 April 1573.</p><p>Pratensis</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correction of record (2026-07-11) — this letter is <i>not</i> lost.</b> previously published this slot as <i>\"(lost; editorial note only)\"</i>, graded [C], on the ground that the only surviving text was Dreyer's six-word note <i>Responsio ad has interijt…</i> (\"the reply to these has perished\"). That was an artefact of a <b>defective ingest</b>, not of the tradition. The original tekstnet scrape captured only the first block of the page — the editorial loss-note that stands <i>before</i> the letter — and dropped the letter itself. The letter survives complete: tekstnet prints it in full, and its own source-line cites <b>TBOO VII pp. 10–13</b>, four printed pages. The loss-note refers to a <i>different</i>, genuinely lost reply, and is retained above the letter as apparatus, where it belongs. The Latin and translation above are new.</li><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Johannes Pratensis</i> = <b>Hans Philipsen Pratensis</b> (1543–1576), professor of medicine at Copenhagen from 1571, Paracelsian, and Tycho's closest friend of these years; Tycho would write his epitaph in 1576. He is here acting as Tycho's agent in Copenhagen, managing both craftsmen and printer while Tycho is away (at Herrevad).</li><li><b>The globe.</b> The object is a metal celestial globe with <b>engraved constellation figures on a silvered surface</b>, with a divided meridian and horizon ring and a pedestal — not the great wooden Augsburg globe. The crux of the letter is a <b>finishing dispute</b>: the goldsmith (<i>Aurifaber</i>) cannot lay gold stars over silver; the painter (<i>Pictor</i>) would smother the engraving under a crust of pigment. Pratensis's solution — gild the whole globe, leave the figures and stars <b>matt</b> and burnish the ground between them to a ruddy lustre, so the figures read as matt gold against bright gold — is argued on grounds at once technical and aesthetic. The German gloss <b><i>das ist matt</b></i>, quoted as the craftsmen's own word, is the vocabulary of the Copenhagen workshop breaking into the Latin.</li><li><b>Cost.</b> The gilding of foot, meridian and horizon needs <b>at least 4 rose nobles</b>, with 3 or 4 more if the whole-globe scheme is adopted; and <i>\"the goldsmith says he cannot get gold here\"</i> — Tycho must send the coin itself. A rare, concrete costing of a Tychonic instrument at the point of manufacture.</li><li><b>The printing of <i>De nova stella</i> — the letter's principal historical value.</b> The \"work\" whose figures are being cut, whose paper is scarce, and whose title is under negotiation is <b>Tycho's first book</b>, <i>De nova stella</i> (Copenhagen: Lorentz Benedicht, 1573). Three things follow:</li><li>Pratensis promises <i>\"my letter and my poem\"</i> (<i>de epistola mea et carmine</i>) for the volume. Both are in the printed book: the prefatory <b>letter of Johannes Pratensis</b> (<i>\"TYCHONI BRAHE OTTHONIDI… IOHANNES PRATENSIS, MEDICUS, S. D.\"</i>) stands among its preliminaries.</li><li><b>Tycho had wanted <i>LUCUBRATIONES</i> (\"Nocturnal Studies\") on the title-page</b>, and Pratensis talks him out of it — objecting that the word is debased by equivocation (scribblers, drinkers, carters and sailors all \"lucubrate\") and that a title should not \"dress out the doorway with showy and cavernous titles.\" <i>Lucubrationes</i> does <b>not</b> appear on the printed title-page.</li><li>Pratensis offers Tycho a straight choice: <i>\"if you should want mathematical <b>consideration</b> put in place of mathematical <b>contemplation</b>, it will be all one.\"</i> The printed title reads <b>…<i>Contemplatio Mathematica</b></i>. The letter therefore catches the title of Tycho's first book at the moment it was settled, and shows which counsel prevailed.</li><li><b>Classical texture.</b> The Latin is dense with allusion, and the translation preserves rather than flattens it: <i>\"a horse's neck joined to a human head… tails off into an ugly fish\"</i> and <i>\"monsters in paintings\"</i> are <b>Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 1–5</b>, turned into an argument against polychromy; the <b>golden Silenus</b> that \"will open himself whole to our eyes\" is the <i>Sileni Alcibiadis</i> of Erasmus's <i>Adagia</i> (the coarse figurine that opens to disclose a god), here inverted — the outside is to be gold as well; <i>\"my mind is on the dishes\"</i> (<i>animus est in patinis</i>) is proverbial (Terence, <i>Eunuchus</i> 816). The closing turn — a sacred ambition drawn <i>\"from the chaste sanctuaries of Urania and her Vestal sisters\"</i> — names the muse Tycho would take for his own.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dated <i>Hafniæ … 16. Aprilis Anni 1573</i>. Denmark was on the Julian calendar; the date is <b>Old Style</b>, reproduced as written and not converted.</li><li><b>Flagged crux.</b> The final clause, <i>\"oculis orbate sensilibus lucem ne respice\"</i>, is syntactically strained (<i>orbate</i> taken as a vocative of <i>orbatus</i>) and its sense is compressed; it is rendered close to the Latin rather than smoothed, and should be checked against Dreyer's text before it is quoted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> \"tails off into an ugly fish\" corrected to \"tails off into a black fish\", restoring the colour-term atrum that Pratensis's Latin carries (Dreyer, TBOO VII, p.11) and that echoes Horace's Ars Poetica.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the letter is now translated in full from the complete text of record, and the two substantive historical claims in the apparatus are externally corroborated: the printed title of <i>De nova stella</i> (1573) reads <i>Contemplatio Mathematica</i> and carries no <i>Lucubrationes</i>, and the volume does contain Pratensis's prefatory letter. Register (learned banter, workshop detail, and humanist moralising in one breath) is matched rather than levelled, and the German gloss <i>das ist matt</i> is kept in place. <b>Not [A]</b> for two reasons: the translation is made from the tekstnet text and has <b>not been collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII pp. 10–13</b>, which is the edition of record; and the closing sentence (<i>oculis orbate sensilibus…</i>) is a genuine crux, rendered literally and flagged. <b>Upgrade path:</b> collate against TBOO VII pp. 10–13 and resolve the final clause.</p><p>&gt; <b>Correction note (2026-07-11).</b> This slot previously read <i>\"lost; editorial note only\"</i> at [C], and a collation note of 2026-06-02 wrongly \"confirmed\" the loss. Both rested on a truncated ingest that had stored the editorial loss-note in place of the letter. The letter survives; the grade moves [C] → [B].</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the complete letter -- opening address, the globe-gilding technical dispute (goldsmith vs. painter, the matt/burnished finish, the Horatian monster-hybrid conceit), the title-page discussion (LVCVBRATIONES and the alternate title), the closing meditation on rank and friendship, and the date/signature -- against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.10-12 (slice pp.1-3), reading the page images directly, not the text layer. Confirmed the letter's Dreyer text is fully contained in pp.10-12: slice p.4 (printed p.13) is the tail of Dreyer's own editorial note (which explicitly connects this letter to the lost Pratensis reply and the 3 May 1573 letter requesting publication of De nova stella -- the letter this investigation started from) followed by the start of an unrelated letter (Maior to Tycho, 1 July 1573). Checked every proper name, number, date, and place-name (Roskilde fair, Frankfurt, Flensburg merchants, 16 April 1573, Stephanus, Andreas). Only one low-severity word-choice mistranslation found; source_text agrees with Dreyer throughout.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Pratensis's Horatian parody specifies the fish is black (atrum), echoing Horace's own hybrid-monster opening (Ars Poetica); the English substitutes the vague evaluative word \"ugly\" for the specific color-term, losing the deliberate classical echo without changing the basic sense. At: “the thing tails off into an ugly fish”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.10-14. The Latin already reads \"in piscem desinet atrum\" on p.11 exactly as our current_latin has it -- this is a translation-only fix, and the Latin (the letter this whole investigation began from) was left completely untouched. No remaining uncertainty.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15720517001", "next": "brahe-t_15760131001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15720517001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15760214001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports on the gilding of Tycho's celestial globe — the goldsmith cannot lay gold stars on silver, the painter would smother the engraving — and urges an all-gold scheme with matt figures on a burnished ground, costed at four rose nobles; then on the printing of De nova stella, arguing Tycho out of the title-word Lucubrationes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15730416001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15730701001", "d": "1573-07-01", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Major", "ck": "Johannes Major", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Johannes Major → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 1 July 1573 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOCTRINAQUE ET VIRTUTIBUS PRAeSTANTISSIMO D. TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI DANO, IN CURIA KNUDSTRUP, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO. S.</p><p>AFFINEM meum, Generose Domine TYCHO, in patriam suam discedentem, absque meis literis ad te venire nolui: cum quod scirem, amicorum plurimi te facientium literas tibi non ingratas fore: tum quod vehementer ex te scire desiderem, quid de novo illo ante aliquot menses exorto sidere sentias. Mirum est, quanta sit doctissimorum et in hac arte exercitatissimorum virorum discrepans sententia. Nam alij (ut de materia non dicam) Cometam appellare volunt, alij stellam propter speciem apparentem: alij mediam quandam naturam habere asserunt. Italus quidam (nomen eius excidit) hoc sidus non novum, sed unum esse ex numero Cassiopeae, lumine miraculose illustrat© et accenso, acerrime contendit, scripto in publicum edito, et senatui Veneto dedicato. Praeterea de distantia eius a Terra magna est controversia. Alij enim id in elementarem regionem collocant, ex quorum numero noster D. PAULUS HAINZELIUS est. Alij in aetheream regionem, quorum opinioni astipulatur D. PEUCERUS, APIANUS, Landgravius Hassiae, et alij, qui tamen et in hoc dissentiunt, ut alij supra Lunam, alij supra Venerem, imo alij in primum Mobile collocare non dubitent, freti observationibus instrumentorum, doctrinae parallaxeos, et Triangulorum. Sed quomodo una eademque doctrina ex firmis demonstrationibus extructa, potest artifices adeo incertos reddere, ut ita dissentiant? Haec velim explices, et quid de hac re statuas, per occasionem, etiam propter communia studia literis significare ne graveris, te etiam atque etiam rogo. Id si feceris, erit mihi tam gratum, quam quod gratissimum, quod etiam tanquam testem studij et amoris tui erga me in perpetuum conservabo. Utinam hisce temporibus una vixissemus, ut observationis modum ex te cognoscere potuissem: qua in re nihil mihi gratius contigisset. Sed optando quid consequi possim, non video. Amissa bona plerunque desiderantur, id quod non mea, sed temporis culpa factum video fato quodam. Praesens ergo status boni consulendus erit.</p><p>Tu interim feliciter in Domino vale.<br>Datae Augustae Rheticae Calendis Iulij 1573<br>T. E.<br>Deditiss. et studiosiss.<br>IOHANNES MAIOR</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, the Dane, at the manor of Knudstrup, most outstanding in learning and in the virtues, his lord and friend to be greatly honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>I was unwilling, noble Master Tycho, that my kinsman, as he departed for his homeland, should come to you without a letter from me — both because I knew that letters from friends who esteem you highly would not be unwelcome to you, and because I ardently desire to learn from you what you think about that new star which arose some months ago. It is wonderful how greatly the most learned men, and those most practised in this art, differ in opinion. For some (to say nothing of its substance) wish to call it a comet, others a star on account of its apparent form; others assert that it has a certain intermediate nature. A certain Italian (his name escapes me) most fiercely contends — in a work issued to the public and dedicated to the Venetian Senate — that this heavenly body is not new, but is one of the number of [the stars of] Cassiopeia, [its] light having miraculously been made bright and kindled. Moreover there is great controversy about its distance from the Earth. For some place it in the elementary region — of whose number is our Master Paul Hainzel; others in the aethereal region, with whose opinion Master Peucer, Apian, the Landgrave of Hesse, and others side — who nevertheless also disagree in this, that some place it above the Moon, others above Venus, indeed others do not hesitate to place it in the <i>primum mobile</i>, relying on the observations of instruments, on the doctrine of parallax, and on [the doctrine] of triangles. But how can one and the same doctrine, built up out of firm demonstrations, render its practitioners so uncertain that they thus disagree? I beg you again and again to explain these things, and not to find it burdensome — as occasion offers, and for the sake of our common studies — to signify by letter what you determine on this matter. If you do this, it will be as pleasing to me as what is most pleasing; and I shall preserve it forever as a witness of your zeal and affection toward me. Would that we lived together in these times, so that I might have learned the manner of observation from you — in which matter nothing more welcome could have befallen me! But I do not see what I can attain by wishing. Good things, once lost, are for the most part longed for — which I see came about by a certain fate, through no fault of mine, but of the time. The present state of things, then, must be made the best of.</p><p>Meanwhile, fare you happily well in the Lord.<br>Given at Augsburg in Raetia, on the Kalends of July [1 July] 1573.<br>Yours,<br>most devoted and most zealous,<br>Johannes Major.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Maior</i> = <b>Johannes Major</b> (the Latin sender-form). Writing from Augsburg in 1573, in the circle of the Augsburg patrician-patron <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (whom he calls \"our Master Paul Hainzel\"). He is otherwise a minor figure of the Augsburg learned circle through which Tycho had passed in 1569–70; the letter is preserved among the same Vienna <i>Epistolae</i> volume (cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66) as the Wolf and Hainzel letters of the period. The dataset's heuristic language tag (`la`) is correct: the letter is wholly Neo-Latin, with no code-switching.</li><li><b><i>Ottonides</b></i> — \"son of Otte,\" a Greek-style patronymic for Tycho's father <b>Otte Brahe</b>; <i>in Curia Knudstrup</i> = \"at the manor of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (the Brahe family seat in Scania; kept as is per protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>affinem meum … discedentem</b></i> — \"my kinsman, as he departed\": the unnamed kinsman of Major's was the bearer carrying this letter back to Denmark; not further identified.</li><li><b>The \"new star\" (the 1572 nova).</b> <i>novo illo … exorto sidere</i> = \"that new star which arose some months ago\" — the <b>nova/supernova of November 1572 in Cassiopeia (SN 1572, \"Tycho's star\")</b>, the central event of Tycho's early career and the subject of his <i>De nova stella</i> (1573). The whole substance of the letter is the contemporary debate over its nature and distance. Note Major's hedge <i>(ut de materia non dicam)</i> \"(to say nothing of its substance)\" — preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b><i>Cometam … stellam … mediam quandam naturam</b></i> — the three contemporary positions on what the object <i>was</i>: a comet, a (fixed) star, or something of intermediate nature. Rendered literally.</li><li><b><i>Italus quidam (nomen eius excidit) … senatui Veneto dedicato</b></i> — \"a certain Italian (his name escapes me) … dedicated to the Venetian Senate.\" Major reports the view that the object is <b>not new</b> but a pre-existing star of Cassiopeia whose light was miraculously kindled. The Italian author is left unnamed by Major himself (<i>nomen eius excidit</i>, \"his name has slipped [my memory]\"); the work dedicated to the Venetian Senate plausibly points to one of the several Venetian nova-tracts of 1573, but Major's own uncertainty is preserved and the identification left open rather than asserted.</li><li><b>Textual crux — <i>lumine … illustrat© et accenso</i>.</b> The working transcription prints <i>illustrat©</i>, a corrupt token (a transcription/OCR artifact, not a Latin word). The grammar requires an ablative agreeing with the ablative-absolute construction <i>lumine … accenso</i> (\"the light having been kindled\"); the intended reading is therefore <b>_illustrato_</b> (\"[its light] having been made bright\"), here adopted in the translation as <i>\"[its] light having miraculously been made bright and kindled.\"</i> The printed corrupt form is retained verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4; the reading was not confirmable against a clean Dreyer scan in the corpus, hence flagged.</li><li><b>The distance debate.</b> <i>elementarem regionem</i> = \"the <b>elementary region</b>\" (the sublunary realm of the four elements); <i>aetheream regionem</i> = \"the <b>aethereal region</b>\" (the celestial realm above the Moon). The dispute over whether the new star lay below or above the Moon is precisely the parallax question Tycho's own observations settled in favour of the supralunary placement.</li><li><i>D. Paulus Hainzelius</i> = <b>Paul Hainzel</b> of Augsburg (with whom Tycho had built the great Göppingen quadrant in 1569), here placing the object in the elementary (sublunary) region.</li><li><i>D. Peucerus</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (Wittenberg); <i>Apianus</i> = <b>Philipp Apian</b> (the Ingolstadt/Tübingen mathematician, son of Peter Apian); <i>Landgravius Hassiae</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> — named as siding with the aethereal (supralunary) placement.</li><li><b><i>primum Mobile</b></i> — the <i>primum mobile</i>, the outermost moving sphere of the Ptolemaic-Aristotelian cosmos; kept in Latin and italicised.</li><li><b>Technical terms.</b> <i>doctrina parallaxeos</i> → \"the doctrine of <b>parallax</b>\" (protocol §5.1); <i>observationibus instrumentorum</i> → \"the observations of <b>instruments</b>\" (§5.2); <i>doctrina … Triangulorum</i> → \"the doctrine of triangles\" (i.e. trigonometry, the computational basis for deriving distance from parallax).</li><li><b><i>artifices</b></i> — here \"practitioners\" (of the mathematical art), i.e. the astronomers themselves; Major's rhetorical point is that a single rigorous <i>doctrina</i> nonetheless yields contradictory results in their hands.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>feliciter in Domino vale</i> → \"fare you happily well in the Lord\"; <i>Datae … Calendis Iulij 1573</i> → \"Given … on the Kalends of July (1 July) 1573\" (Roman-calendar idiom kept and glossed, protocol §3); <i>T. E. / Deditiss. et studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tuae Excellentiae deditissimus et studiosissimus</i>, \"most devoted and most zealous to Your Excellency\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Augustae Rheticae</i> = <b>Augsburg</b> (here qualified <i>Rhetica</i>, \"of Raetia,\" the antiquarian regional epithet; cf. the more usual <i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i>), protocol §5.7.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Calendis Iulij 1573</i> = the Kalends of July, i.e. <b>1 July 1573</b> (O.S.; Denmark and the Empire were pre-Gregorian). The as-written dateline agrees with the dataset label (<i>1573. 1. juli. Augsburg</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'astipulator' corrected to 'astipulatur' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 13.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the corrupt token <i>illustrat©</i>, adopted as <i>illustrato</i> on grammatical grounds but not confirmed against a clean Dreyer scan; and (2) the deliberately unnamed Italian author of the Venice-dedicated nova-tract, left unidentified as Major leaves him.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages visually. The letter (Johannes Major to Tycho, headed 'NOBILISSIMO DOCTRINAQUE ET VIRTUTIBUS PRAESTANTISSIMO...') runs from the drop-cap 'A' on p.13 through p.14, citation '[Ex apographo in codice Vindobonensi lat. 10686^7; cf. 10686^66 fol. 60]'. Collated the full Latin body line-by-line against Dreyer: matches throughout apart from the single astipulator/astipulatur variant above. All names (Paulus Hainzelius, Peucerus, Apianus, Landgravius Hassiae) and the closing date (Calendis Iulij 1573, Augustae Rheticae) match exactly. English translation is faithful and complete, no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found beyond the noted single-letter source variant.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the deponent verb as 'astipulator' where Dreyer prints 'astipulatur' (a one-letter o/u variance); immaterial to sense, and the English 'side with' is faithful either way. Dreyer prints: <i>quorum opinioni aftipulatur D. PEUCERUS, APIANUS, Landgravius Haßiæ, et alij</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VII pp.13-15 page images. The word 'aftipulatur' is clearly legible on p.13. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Major", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15760802001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840715001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends a kinsman with greetings and solicits Tycho's opinion on the new star of 1572, noting sharp disagreement among learned men over its nature.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15730701001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15740310001", "d": "1574-03-10", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Paul Hainzel", "ck": "Paul Hainzel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Paul Hainzel → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 10 March 1574 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTI IUVENI, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>DICERE non possum, Nobilissime et amantissime mi TYCHO, quanta voluptate me affeceris, non saltem eruditissimis literis, ad me nuper datis, sed et contemplatione tua vere Mathematica, de miraculosa illa ad Cassiopeiam stella, quae ante paucas hebdomadas plane conspici desijt, quam ego quidem Cometam appellare solitus sum, secundum receptam ex ARISTOTELIS schola opinionem, licet ut fatear id, quod res postulat, propriae meae observationes, Quadrante illo maximo, ex tuo praescripto hîc fabrefacto, obtente convincant, id, quicquid erat iubaris, non infra Lunam fuisse, sed in altissimo aethere, siquidem nullae proveniant parallaxes ex meis diligenter institutis observationibus, et tamen non ausus fui dicere, supra Lunam extitisse, ne contra Philosophum, cuius praecipua est autoritas, quippiam affirmarem, qui Coelum nova non admittere corpora indubitanter statuit. Sed praevaleat autoritati experientia, praesertim cum tu accuratissimis tuis Instrumentis et Observationibus idem, quod ego, deprehenderis: nimirum nullam prorsus adfuisse huic φαινομένῳ parallaxin, veluti etiam satis evidenter in hoc tuo eruditissimo scripto demonstrasti, quod sane longe antecellit omnium aliorum hactenus de eadem stella in publicum editas conceptiones, ita ut vix cum hac conferri mereantur: et optarem quam maxime, me id prius obtinuisse, ut descriptum eius cum doctis hinc Mathematicis, cum duraret adhuc ea stella, praesertim MAeSTHLINO, qui idem tecum sentire videtur, licet non tam evidenter atque dilucide, sed per ambages quasdam demonstret, communicare potuissem. Sed spero, me plura exemplaria a te obtenturum, eademque, uti polliceri videris, auctiora reddita, tum demum alijs ea impertiar. Interea hoc meum, uti rem pretiosam, reservabo, et fratri meo atque WOLFIO nostro ostendam. Quamprimum igitur plura eiuscemodi publicaveris, me eorum facere participem ne supersedeas quaeso. Adiunxi hisce observationes meas in hac ipsa stella, et quibusdam alijs, factas eodem Quadrante permagno, tuo instinctu hîc elaborato, quas examinare poteris, et tuam postea mihi de ijs indicare censuram. Plura nunc scribere non vacat, ob negotiorum molem in hoc officio, quod sustineo, publico. Tu itaque vale, optime mi TYCHO, et saepius me tuis eruditis literis invisere ne omittas.</p><p>Iterumque etiam atque etiam vale.<br>Datae Augustae Vindelicorum, Anno 1574 die 10. Martij.<br>T.<br>Paulus Haintzel.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the young man pre-eminent in nobility and learning, Master Tycho Brahe — sends heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I cannot tell you, my most noble and most loving Tycho, with what delight you have filled me — not only by the most learned letter lately sent to me, but also by your truly mathematical reflection on that miraculous star by Cassiopeia, which a few weeks ago entirely ceased to be seen, [the star] which I, for my part, have been accustomed to call a comet, in accordance with the opinion received from the school of Aristotle — although, to confess what the matter requires, my own observations, obtained with that great quadrant built here after your prescription, prove that the brightness, whatever it was, was not below the Moon, but in the highest aether, seeing that no parallaxes whatever arise from the observations I diligently set up; and yet I did not dare to say that it had stood above the Moon, lest I affirm anything against the Philosopher, whose authority is paramount, who lays it down beyond doubt that the heaven admits no new bodies. But let experience prevail over authority, especially since you, with your most accurate instruments and observations, have found the same as I — namely, that there was present to this phenomenon [φαινομένῳ] no parallax at all, just as you have also demonstrated quite evidently in this most learned writing of yours, which indeed far surpasses the conceptions about the same star hitherto published by all others, so that they scarcely deserve to be compared with this one. And I would wish as much as possible that I had obtained it earlier, so that I might have been able to share a description of it with the learned mathematicians hereabouts, while that star yet lasted — especially with Maestlin, who seems to hold the same view as you, although he demonstrates it not so evidently and clearly, but by certain roundabout ways. But I hope that I shall obtain more copies from you, and these — as you seem to promise — rendered fuller; and only then shall I impart them to others. Meanwhile I shall keep this [copy] of mine as a precious thing, and show it to my brother and to our Wolf. As soon, therefore, as you publish more of this kind, do not, I beg, forbear to make me a sharer of them. I have attached to this [letter] my observations made upon this very star, and upon certain others, with the same very great quadrant elaborated here at your prompting, which you will be able to examine, and afterwards to convey to me your judgement upon them. There is no leisure to write more now, on account of the mass of business in this public office which I sustain. So farewell, my excellent Tycho, and do not omit to visit me more often with your learned letters.</p><p>And again, again and again, farewell.<br>Given at Augsburg, in the year 1574, on the 10th day of March.<br>Yours,<br>Paul Hainzel.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <i>Paulus Haintzel</i> = <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (1527/8–1581), patrician of Augsburg, town councillor (Stadtpfleger / magistrate — hence <i>hoc officio … publico</i>, \"this public office which I sustain\") and patron of astronomy. With Tycho during the latter's Augsburg years (1569–70) he built a great quadrant at his suburban estate at Göggingen, the <i>Quadrans maximus</i> repeatedly invoked here. One of the <b>Hainzel brothers</b> of the controlled glossary (§5.6); the <i>frater meus</i> of this letter is his brother <b>Johann Baptist Hainzel</b>.</li><li><b>Recipient / superscription.</b> <i>Domino Tychoni Brahe</i> — Tycho Brahe (1546–1601), then 27 and freshly the author of <i>De nova stella</i>. The salutation <i>S. P. D.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (§5.5); the subscription <i>T.</i> (here likely <i>Tuus</i>, \"Yours\") + signature.</li><li><b>Occasion — the writing in hand.</b> <i>hoc tuo eruditissimo scripto</i> / <i>descriptum eius</i> is <b>Tycho's <i>De nova stella</b></i> (Copenhagen, 1573), his account of the new star (SN 1572) that appeared in Cassiopeia in November 1572. Hainzel acknowledges receiving a copy, praises it above all rival accounts, and asks for further, <i>auctiora</i> (\"fuller\") copies — Tycho apparently having promised an enlarged treatment.</li><li><b>The phenomenon.</b> <i>miraculosa illa ad Cassiopeiam stella … quae ante paucas hebdomadas plane conspici desijt</i> — the nova faded from naked-eye visibility in early 1574, consistent with \"ceased to be seen a few weeks ago.\" Hainzel records that he had habitually called it a <b>comet</b> (<i>Cometam appellare solitus sum</i>), per the Aristotelian doctrine that placed all change in the sublunary realm; his own parallax measurements, however, forced the contrary conclusion.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms</b> (§5.1–5.2). <i>parallaxes / parallaxin</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>Quadrante illo maximo … permagno</i> → \"that great / very great <b>quadrant</b>\" (Tycho's instrument-type, here the Göggingen quadrant built <i>ex tuo praescripto</i> / <i>tuo instinctu</i>, \"after your prescription / at your prompting\"); <i>Instrumenta et Observationes</i> → <b>instruments and observations</b>; <i>infra / supra Lunam … in altissimo aethere</i> → \"below / above the Moon … in the highest aether\" (the crux of the cosmological dispute: a body with no measurable parallax must lie beyond the Moon, in the supposedly immutable heavens). <i>censura</i> rendered \"judgement\" (a scholar's verdict on submitted work).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>φαινομένῳ</i> (dative sing. of <i>φαινόμενον</i>, <i>phainómenon</i>) = \"to this <b>phenomenon</b>.\" Transcribed as printed and translated; the iota-subscript spelling of the dative is retained. Hainzel uses the technical Greek noun for the celestial appearance rather than committing to \"comet\" or \"star.\"</li><li><b><i>Philosophus</b></i> — \"the Philosopher,\" i.e. <b>Aristotle</b> (<i>ARISTOTELIS schola</i> named earlier in the sentence), whose dictum that the heavens admit no new bodies (<i>Coelum nova non admittere corpora</i>) Hainzel is reluctant to contradict openly: <i>praevaleat autoritati experientia</i>, \"let experience prevail over authority\" — a notable methodological declaration.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>MAeSTHLINO</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), then a young Tübingen mathematician, whose own 1573 tract on the nova likewise argued for its supralunary, parallax-free position — Hainzel notes Maestlin reaches the same view <i>per ambages quasdam</i> (\"by certain roundabout ways\"). <i>WOLFIO nostro</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist (§5.6), \"our Wolf.\" <i>frater meus</i> = Hainzel's brother (Johann Baptist Hainzel).</li><li><b>Place / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Augustae Vindelicorum, Anno 1574 die 10. Martij</i> = \"Given at <b>Augsburg</b> (<i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i>, §5.7), in the year 1574, on the 10th day of March\" (O.S.; Denmark and the Empire were on the Julian calendar in 1574, the Gregorian reform being still eight years off). The as-written dateline agrees with the catalogue label (1574.10.marts.Augsburg); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Closing formula.</b> <i>Iterumque etiam atque etiam vale</i> = \"And again, again and again, farewell\" — an intensified <i>Iterum vale</i> (§5.5: <i>Again, farewell</i>), the doubled <i>etiam atque etiam</i> kept as the affectionate redundancy it is, not smoothed.</li><li><b>Reading note.</b> <i>id quicquid erat iubaris</i> — <i>iubar</i> (poetic, \"radiance, brightness, a heavenly light\") is read as a partitive genitive after <i>quicquid</i>: \"the brightness, whatever it was.\" The hedge (<i>quicquid erat</i>, \"whatever it was\") is preserved rather than resolved to \"the star\" / \"the comet,\" matching Hainzel's deliberate refusal to pre-judge the object's nature.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clean and the sense secure throughout; the one interpretive choice (the partitive <i>iubaris</i> and the preserved hedge <i>quicquid erat</i>) is noted in the apparatus, and the single Greek word is transcribed and glossed. No unresolved crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.14-16 as page images. Letter (\"NOBILITATE ET ERUDITIONE PRAESTANTI IUVENI, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE\") runs p.14-15, ending with signature \"Paulus Haintzel.\" Collated the technical vocabulary (Quadrante illo maximo, the Greek phainomeno), proper names (Aristotelis, Maesthlino, Wolfio), and closing date (Anno 1574 die 10 Martij) against Dreyer -- exact match. Published English faithfully renders the nova/comet argument, the parallax claim, and the closing greetings with no defects.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7."], "th": {"label": "Paul Hainzel", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15750311001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports observations of the 1572 Cassiopeia star made with the Augsburg quadrant, finding zero parallax and therefore a supralunary position, despite reluctance to contradict Aristotle.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15740310001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15750307001", "d": "1575-03-07", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 7 March 1575 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>HIERONYMUS WOLFIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>PRAeRIPUISTI, TYCHO Nobilissime, tu meas mihi partes gratias agendi singulari humanitati tuae, me senem, qui iam nihil sum, nisi telluris inutile et miserum pondus, amicum non aspernanti. Sed hic verae doctrinae fructus nimirum est: ut quo sis sublimior, eo summissius te geras. Ego certe tui doctissimi colloquij desiderio (in hac solitudine, quam valetudo mihi offert) saepissime afficior. Et si quid absens, quod tibi gratum iucundumque esset, praestare possem, facerem profecto et fideliter et libenter. Sed perfidia Tabellarij fecit, ut et Bibliotheca D. AMBROSIJ nostri excideris et armillam SCHRECKENFUCHSIJ, amissa pecunia, non acceperis. Quibus de rebus et a MAIORE et Consule nostro certior utique fies: qui mihi mandavit, ut e SCHRECKENFUCHSIO cognoscerem, an eiusmodi armilla esset? et an et quanti vellet vendere? id quod ego prima occatione facturus sum. Raro est enim occasio hinc literas Friburgum mittendi. Unde praevideo, longiusculam esse moram intercessuram, antequam ista ad te perferantur. Sed ut quimus, quando, ut volumus, non licet.</p><p>Quae de magnitudinibus stellarum respondes, grata sunt: nec ignoro, quosdam casum stellarum allegorice de praecipuorum doctorum lapsu interpretari. Sed magnitudo ista omnem humani animi cogitationem excedens, corporum coelestium, me saepe quasi attonitum facit et extra me ipsum cum summo stupore rapit cum axiomatibus religionis nostrae conferentem tam angustis et temporum et locorum finibus circumscriptae, et vilissimos nos homunciones et foedissima miserrimaque animalcula omni coelorum ornatui non aequanti modo, sed infinito intervallo praeferenti. Neque IULIANO CAeSARI scribunt quicquam fuisse visum absurdius, quam statuere Solis interitum et terrenorum corpusculorum resurrectionem. D. certe Petrus Epistolae 2, cap. usi. diserte scribit: οἱ νῦν οὐρανοὶ και ἡ γῆ θεοῦ λόγῳ τεθησαυρισμένοι εἰσί, πυρὶ τηρούμενοι εἰς ἡμέραν κρίσεως καὶ ἀπωλειας τῶν ασεβῶν ανθρώπων. Nec dissimilia sunt apud EZECHIELEM, ESAIAM et alios prophetas: quae non videntur posse ἀλληγορεῖσθαι. Philo autem in libello περὶ αφθαρσίας κόσμου tot tamque invictis rationibus mundi aeternitatem probare mihi quidem videtur: ut nihil illis praeter autoritatem sacrarum Literarum opponi possit. Quos tamen ut recte intelligamus, etiam atque etiam videndum est. Itaque tibi autor sum et loca illa diligenter expendendi, et cum eruditis non modo Philosophis, sed etiam Theologis conferendi, inprimisque cum D. HEMMINGIO, qui Philosophiam cum Theologia et eloquentiam cum utraque coniunxit. Oppugnant et Philosophastri nonnulli (in quibus THOMAS ERASTUS, Heidelbergensis Medicus, maxime triumphat) et Theologastri divinatricem Astrologiam ut et religioni adversantem et divinitus interdictam. Mihi vero videtur nihil Christianismum magis oppugnare, quam infinita coelorum et altitudo et magnitudo. Quibus de rebus in Astrologia mentionem abs te fieri, ac potius accurate illas quaestiones tractari oportere censeo. Praedictionum fidem autem maxime labefactare videntur haec, quae tibi refutanda relinquo. Nam me et ingenium et doctrina in hoc genere destituit.</p><p>1. Controversia de numero et motu coelorum.<br>2. De motu planetarum.<br>3. De ratione constituendi Thematis Natalitii.<br>4. Diversa et dissidentia pronunciata de effectibus siderum. Nec enim Arabes cum Ptolemaica doctrina, nec Indi (ut ex ABRAHAMO conicio) cum utrorumque dogmatibus consentiunt.<br>5. Incerta regnorum et urbium initia et magnarum coniunctionum et trigonorum.<br>6. Gemellorum summa dissimilitudo rebus plerumque omnibus.<br>7. Incertum momentum infantis editi, et incertae corrigendi rationes.</p><p>Nam quotus quisque stellae alicuius altitudinem observat? Ptolemaicas autem ☌ et ☍ et HERMETIS trutinam magnorum errorum et absurditatum convincit ANTONIUS MONCULMIUS. Iam casus alios alij ad alios significatores referunt, et diversi eventus diversum Horoscopum in mea Genesi plurimis insignibus infortunijs, nonnullis etiam successibus nobilitata, ostendunt: Et in progressionibus vel adhibita vel omissa latitudo quantum discrimen facit? Quid vero de CAMERARIO dicas, viro eruditissimo, mihique, dum viveret, amicissimo, qui et de Horoscopo et de motu singulorum planetarum 27 partes detrahit? Ita mihi pro ♒ horoscopat ♑ : ♄ in ♏, ♃ in ♋, ☉, ☿ in ♌, ♀in ♍, ☾ in ♎ versatur. Ingens utique discrimen. Haec et alia plura tuae industriae erit ita declarare, ut et rudiores (quo in numero et ego senex nomen meum profiteor) edoceantur, et impudentibus calumniatoribus ora obturentur. Philosophicum istud otium ita comparatum, ut fructus eius latissime pateant, et multorum ardua in speciem negotia longe superent ex animo tibi gratulor. Teque hortor (etsi minime quidem id opus est) ne vel ullo splendoris aulici fuco, vel ignobilis nobilitatis ludibrijs et ludicris exercitijs ab honestissimo isto instituto te unquam depelli patiaris. Macte tua virtute TYCHO: sic itur ad astra. Quod me tu ad aequani[mi]tatem adhortaris: agnosco benevolentiam erga me tuam, parebo fidelissimis tuis monitis quantum potero, etsi natura parum fortis. Equidem pro virili</p><p>Conor, sed cedit patientia victa dolori.<br>Verba levant animos, sed dolor aure caret<br>Expugnata malis sapientia nostra labascit<br>Dum gravis excruciat, dum diuturna lues.</p><p>Equidem legi et Theologorum et Philosophorum omnia solatia: eaque dum mihi bene est, aequi bonique facio: et animi perturbationes moderari, σὺν θεῷ δ' εἰπεῖν, possum mediocriter, sed dolores corporis non verbis, sed aut lapidibus et herbis, aut morte levantur. Nam naturae necessitas aeque mihi atque CARNEADI dolorem auget: neque me CLEANTHES in morbo vociferantem et eiulantem, magnificis suis versibus, quos citat EPICTETUS et eleganter vertit SENECA, quicquam iuvat:</p><p>Duc me parens celsique dominator poli,<br>Quocunque placuit. Nulla parendi mora est.<br>Assum impiger. Fac nolle? comitabor gemens,<br>Malasque patiar, quod pati licuit bono.<br>Ducunt volentem fata, nolentem trahunt.</p><p>Sed haec hactenus; unum restat, ut gratias humanitati tuae et Avun­ culi STENONIS BILDIJ liberalitati agam: qui MARCUM HARTLIEBIUM, discipulum meum, malis edoctum, quid sit parentum et praeceptorum monita contemnere, benigne suscepistis. Spero vos beneficia bene posituros.</p><p>Novarum rerum nihil scribo, quia paucas scio, easque malas; sed quae hic feruntur, e PETRO cognosces. Septentrionalia vobis notiora utique sunt: sed quae hîc de Megalopyrgio, de Sueco, de Polonis, adeoque ipsa Saxonia nunciantur, parum spem bonam faciunt. AMURATHES SELYMI successor omnes fratres natu minores interfectos, cum patre sepeliendos curasse fertur, Caesari octo annorum dedisse indueras, totam belli molem in Persas conversurus. Sed vereor, ne haec beatorum somnia sint, et nos inopinantes a perfidissimo et potentissimo hoste opprimamur. Vale et ignosce, meque tui memoriam sacrosanctam conservaturum persuasum habeto, ὦ εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών, dum memor ipse mei, dum spiritus hos reget artus.</p><p>Consul armillas mittit, negatque tibi meas nugas fore usui: nec ijs onerandum esse Petrum, cum tu his longe meliora habeas instrumenta. Ego Astrolabium (nescius idem esse cum armilla) a SCHRECKENFUXIO mihi donatum, dono tibi mitto: una cum annulo Astronomico a IOACHIMO RINGELBERGIO quondam confecto; quae si tibi (id quod vereor) usui futura non sunt, μνημόσυνον tamen WOLFIJ tui esse patieris. De ceteris plura scribent Cos. HAINZELIUS et M. MAIOR. Literas a SCHRECKENFUXIO ad me datas tibi mitto, imperitus aestimator huius machinae. Iussi ipsum petere quid amplius exigat.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Nonis Martii 1575.<br>HIERON. WOLFIUS tuus.</p><p>S. P. Charissime WOLFI, literas 22.<br>Februarii a te ad me scriptas una cum 16 Coronatis in auro 27. eiusdem accepi.<br>Petis autem in illis, ut tibi instrumentum aliquod transmittam, quod quidem<br>recusare nolebam, eo quod alias libenter tibi gratificer. Ad pretium porro<br>splendidius quod attinet, te iudicem in hac re libenter patior. Quod propria<br>manu non scribam, in causa est adversa valetudo, ut fortasse ex nuncio melius<br>percipies, quam ut paucis scribi possit. Ceterum TYCHONIS BRAHE persona, quam<br>literis tuis depingis, notissima mihi est. Ad erectionem porro huius sphæræ<br>materialis quod attinet, opus erit solertia quadam, ne colores et numeri in ea<br>defricentur. Si præterea quid desiderares, postes me certiorem reddere. Habebis<br>enim semper a me responsum. Tabellarius iste occasionem rescribendi<br>monstrabit.</p><p>Hisce bene vale.</p><p>Datæ Friburgi 1.<br>Martii anno 75.</p><p>T.<br>OSWALDUS<br>SCHRECKENFUXIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho Brahe. He sends heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>You have forestalled me, most noble Tycho — robbing me of my own part, which was to give thanks to your singular kindness, in that you do not disdain as a friend an old man who is now nothing but an useless and wretched burden upon the earth. But this, to be sure, is the fruit of true learning: that the loftier you are, the more humbly you bear yourself. For my own part I am most often seized with longing for your most learned conversation, in this solitude that my ill-health affords me. And if from afar I could render any service that would be welcome and agreeable to you, I would do it indeed, both faithfully and gladly. But the treachery of the carrier brought it about that you both lost Master Ambrosius's library [Bibliotheca], and — your money gone — did not receive Schreckenfuchs's armilla. Of these matters you will in any case be informed more fully both by the Magistrate [Maior] and by our Consul — who charged me to learn from Schreckenfuchs whether there were such an armilla, and whether, and at what price, he was willing to sell it; which I shall do at the first opportunity. For there is rarely a chance of sending letters from here to Freiburg. Whence I foresee that a fairish delay will intervene before these things are conveyed to you. But [we do] as we can, since we may not [do] as we would.</p><p>What you reply concerning the magnitudes of the stars is welcome; nor am I ignorant that certain men interpret the falling of the stars allegorically, of the lapse of the foremost learned men. But that magnitude of the heavenly bodies, exceeding all the thought of the human mind, often makes me as it were thunderstruck and snatches me out of myself with the utmost amazement — when I compare it with the axioms of our religion, hemmed in within such narrow bounds of both time and place; [a religion] that sets us most vile manikins, foulest and most wretched little animals, not merely on a level with all the adornment of the heavens, but ahead of it by an infinite interval. Indeed, [men] write that nothing seemed more absurd to Julian Caesar than to maintain the destruction of the Sun and the resurrection of earthly little bodies. Master Peter, certainly, in his 2nd Epistle, last chapter, writes expressly: \"the heavens that now are, and the earth, are stored up by the word of God, kept for fire against the day of judgement and of the destruction of impious men\" [οἱ νῦν οὐρανοὶ καὶ ἡ γῆ θεοῦ λόγῳ τεθησαυρισμένοι εἰσί, πυρὶ τηρούμενοι εἰς ἡμέραν κρίσεως καὶ ἀπωλείας τῶν ἀσεβῶν ἀνθρώπων]. And [things] not unlike are in Ezekiel, Isaiah, and the other prophets — which do not seem able to be allegorized [ἀλληγορεῖσθαι]. Philo, however, in his little book On the Incorruptibility of the Cosmos [περὶ ἀφθαρσίας κόσμου], seems to me at least to prove the eternity of the world by reasonings so many and so unconquerable that nothing can be set against them save the authority of the sacred Writings. Yet, in order that we may rightly understand these [authorities], the matter must be looked into again and again. And so I am your advocate both for weighing those passages diligently, and for conferring about them with learned men — not only Philosophers but also Theologians — and above all with Master Hemmingsen, who has joined Philosophy to Theology, and eloquence to both. Certain petty-philosophers too (among whom Thomas Erastus, the Heidelberg physician, triumphs most of all) and petty-theologians assail divinatory Astrology as both opposed to religion and divinely forbidden. To me, however, it seems that nothing assails Christianity more than the infinite height and magnitude of the heavens. I judge that you ought to make mention of these matters in [your work on] Astrology — or rather that those questions ought to be treated with precision. But the things that seem most of all to shake the credit of [astrological] predictions are these, which I leave to you to refute; for both wit and learning fail me in this kind.</p><p>1. The controversy concerning the number and motion of the heavens.<br>2. The motion of the planets.<br>3. The method of establishing the nativity figure [Thema Natalitii].<br>4. The diverse and disagreeing pronouncements concerning the effects of the stars. For neither do the Arabs agree with the Ptolemaic doctrine, nor the Indians (as I conjecture from Abraham) with the dogmas of either.<br>5. The uncertain foundings of kingdoms and cities, and of the great conjunctions and trigons.<br>6. The utmost dissimilarity of twins in nearly all matters.<br>7. The uncertain moment of the infant's birth, and the uncertain methods of correcting it.</p><p>For how few there are who observe the altitude of any star! And as for the Ptolemaic conjunctions [☌] and oppositions [☍] and the <i>trutina</i> of Hermes, Antonius Montculmius convicts them of great errors and absurdities. Now some men refer one set of events to one set of significators, others to another; and divers outcomes show a divers Horoscope [ascendant] in my geniture, which is ennobled by very many notable misfortunes and by some successes too. And in the progressions, how great a difference does the latitude, whether applied or omitted, make? But what would you say of Camerarius — a most learned man, and, while he lived, most friendly to me — who subtracts 27 degrees both from the Horoscope and from the motion of each several planet? Thus for me he casts ♑ Capricorn as horoscope [ascendant] instead of ♒ Aquarius: ♄ Saturn is set in ♏ Scorpio, ♃ Jupiter in ♋ Cancer, ☉ the Sun [and] ☿ Mercury in ♌ Leo, ♀ Venus in ♍ Virgo, ☾ the Moon in ♎ Libra. A vast difference, surely. It will be for your industry to set forth these and many other matters in such a way that both the more untaught (in whose number I, an old man, enrol my name) may be instructed, and the mouths of shameless slanderers stopped. That philosophic leisure of yours, so contrived that its fruits lie open most widely and far surpass the business of many men [though] arduous in appearance — I congratulate you on it from my heart. And I exhort you (though indeed there is no need of it) never to suffer yourself to be driven from that most honourable resolve, either by any rouge of courtly splendour or by the mockeries and idle exercises of an ignoble nobility. Go on and prosper in your virtue, Tycho: thus is the way to the stars. As for your exhorting me to equanimity — I acknowledge your goodwill toward me, and I shall obey your most faithful admonitions as far as I can, although by nature little stout. Indeed, with all my manhood I</p><p>Strive, but my patience, vanquished, gives way to the pain.<br>Words lighten the spirit, but pain has no ear;<br>Stormed by ills, our wisdom totters and slips<br>While the grievous, the long-drawn sickness racks [me].</p><p>Indeed I have read all the consolations both of the Theologians and of the Philosophers; and, while it is well with me, I make light of them [reckon them fair and good]; and the perturbations of the mind, to speak with God's help [σὺν θεῷ δ' εἰπεῖν], I can moderate moderately well — but the pains of the body are relieved not by words, but either by stones and herbs, or by death. For the necessity of nature increases the pain for me as much as for Carneades; nor does Cleanthes, with those magnificent verses of his (which Epictetus cites and Seneca elegantly turns) at all help me as I cry out and wail in my sickness:</p><p>Lead me, Father and ruler of the lofty pole,<br>Whithersoever it has pleased thee. There is no delay in obeying.<br>Here I am, unflagging. Suppose I will not? I shall follow, groaning,<br>And suffer in wickedness what I might have suffered as a good man.<br>The Fates lead the willing; the unwilling they drag.</p><p>But enough of this. One thing remains: that I give thanks to your kindness and to the generosity of [your] uncle Steno Bille, in that you have graciously taken in Marcus Hartlieb, my pupil — taught by hardships what it is to despise the admonitions of parents and preceptors. I hope you will lay out your benefactions well [to good effect].</p><p>Of news I write nothing, since I know little, and that bad; but what is reported here you will learn from Peter. Northern affairs are surely better known to you; but what is told here of Megalopyrgium [Megalopyrgio], of the Swede, of the Poles, and indeed of Saxony itself, gives little good hope. Amurath [Murad III], the successor of Selim, is said to have had all his younger brothers killed and buried together with their father, [and] to have granted the Emperor a truce of eight years, meaning to turn the whole weight of war against the Persians. But I fear these are the dreams of the blessed, and that we, off our guard, may be overwhelmed by a most treacherous and most powerful enemy. Farewell, and forgive [me]; and hold it for assured that I shall keep the memory of you inviolate, O most fortunate Tycho [ὦ εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών], so long as I am mindful of myself, so long as breath shall rule these limbs.</p><p>The Consul sends the armillae, and denies that these trifles of mine will be of use to you, nor that Peter should be burdened with them, since you have instruments far better than these. I, for my part, send you as a gift an Astrolabe (not knowing it to be the same as an armilla) given to me by Schreckenfuchs, together with an astronomical ring once made by Joachim Ringelberg; and if these are not going to be of use to you (which I fear), you will yet allow them to be a keepsake [μνημόσυνον] of your Wolf. Of the rest, Consul Hainzel and Master Maior will write more. I send you the letter given to me by Schreckenfuchs, [I being] an unskilled appraiser of this machine. I have bidden him ask what more he requires.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>On the Nones of March [7 March], 1575.<br>Your Hieronymus Wolf.</p><p><b>Greeting. Dearest Wolf</b> — I received the letter written by you to me on 22 February, together with sixteen gold crowns, on the 27th of the same [month]. In it you ask that I send you some instrument, which indeed I was unwilling to refuse, since on other occasions too I gladly oblige you. As for the finer price, moreover, I am glad to let you be the judge in this matter. That I do not write in my own hand, the cause is ill health, as perhaps you will learn better from the messenger than can be written in a few words. For the rest, the person of Tycho Brahe, whom you depict in your letter, is very well known to me. As for the setting-up of this material sphere, moreover, there will be need of a certain skill, lest the colours and numbers on it be rubbed away. If besides you should want anything further, [you] will be able to make me aware of it — for you will always have an answer from me. This messenger will show the occasion for writing back.</p><p>With this, farewell.</p><p>Given at Freiburg, 1 March, in the year [15]75.</p><p>Yours, Oswald Schreckenfuchs.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (the \"father of Byzantine studies\"), director of the city's library and school; an early mentor-friend from Tycho's Augsburg years (1569–70). This is the companion to his letter of 13 November 1571 (slug brahe-t_15711113001): the same illness (the stone and gout), the same landlord (Ambrosius), the same Hainzel circle, the same name-pun sign-off recur.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P. D.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Bibliotheca D. Ambrosij</b></i> — \"Master Ambrosius's library\"; <i>Ambrosius</i> is the landlord named in the 1571 letter (the lodgings Wolf had rented from him). The phrase is most likely the <i>book-collection</i> lost in transit through the carrier's bad faith, alongside the armilla; \"library\" is kept literal.</li><li><b>The instrument.</b> <i>armilla Schreckenfuchsij</i> / <i>Astrolabium … a Schreckenfuxio</i> — an <b>armillary</b> (zodiacal sphere) constructed at <b>Freiburg</b> by <b>Oswald Schreckenfuchs</b> (Oswaldus Schreckenfuchsius, 1511–1579), mathematician and astronomer. The biographers confirm the episode: in March 1575 Wolf and Paul Hainzel wrote to Tycho about procuring from Schreckenfuchs \"a zodiacal sphere constructed according to the description of Ptolemy\" (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>; Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, calls it a \"Ptolemaic Armillary,\" noting Schreckenfuchs's own term <i>sphaera materialis</i>). Wolf's parenthesis <i>\"nescius idem esse cum armilla\"</i> (\"not knowing it to be the same as an armilla\") shows he did not realise his own gift-astrolabe and the armilla under discussion were the same class of instrument — hence \"armilla\" and \"Astrolabium\" are both kept as written, the confusion preserved (protocol §5.2).</li><li><b><i>annulus Astronomicus a Ioachimo Ringelbergio</b></i> — an \"astronomical ring\" (annular sundial/computing ring) once made by <b>Joachim Sterck van Ringelberg</b> (Ioachimus Fortius Ringelbergius, c. 1499 – c. 1536), the Antwerp polymath.</li><li><b>Officials.</b> <i>Maior / M. Maior</i> — a magistrate (Lat. <i>maior</i>, civic official) of Augsburg, distinct from the Consul; <i>Consul noster / Cos. Hainzelius</i> — the <b>Hainzel</b> consul of Augsburg (Johann Baptist Hainzel, of the patrician family met in the 1571 letter), here acting as the broker forwarding the instruments. <i>Petrus</i> (Peter) is the carrier/intermediary through whom news and parcels pass.</li><li><b>The cosmological debate.</b> Wolf engages Tycho on the <i>magnitudines stellarum</i> (sizes of the stars) and the tension between the apparent infinity of the heavens and Christian doctrine — citing <b>Julian \"the Apostate\"</b> (<i>Iulianus Caesar</i>), <b>2 Peter 3:7</b> (the <i>D. Petrus … cap. usi.</i> = <i>capite ultimo</i>, the last chapter), Ezekiel, Isaiah, and <b>Philo of Alexandria's</b> <i>De aeternitate mundi</i> (<i>περὶ ἀφθαρσίας κόσμου</i>, \"On the Incorruptibility of the Cosmos\"). The hedges are preserved (\"seems to me at least,\" \"do not seem able to be allegorized\").</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>οἱ νῦν οὐρανοὶ καὶ ἡ γῆ θεοῦ λόγῳ τεθησαυρισμένοι εἰσί, πυρὶ τηρούμενοι εἰς ἡμέραν κρίσεως καὶ ἀπωλείας τῶν ἀσεβῶν ἀνθρώπων</i> = 2 Peter 3:7, \"the heavens that now are, and the earth, are stored up by the word of God, kept for fire against the day of judgement and of the destruction of impious men.\" · <i>ἀλληγορεῖσθαι</i> = \"to be allegorized.\" · <i>σὺν θεῷ δ' εἰπεῖν</i> = \"to speak with God's help\" (a stock qualifier). · <i>μνημόσυνον</i> = \"memorial, keepsake.\" · <i>ὦ εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών</i> = \"O most fortunate Tycho,\" the learned pun on Tycho's name (Greek <i>eu-tychḗs</i> \"fortunate\"), as in the 1571 letter. The source preserves several scribal/print accent and breathing irregularities (e.g. <i>ασεβῶν ανθρώπων</i>, <i>αφθαρσίας</i>); reproduced as printed in the Source block, normalised in the translation gloss only.</li><li><b>Astrology — the critics.</b> <i>divinatricem Astrologiam</i> = \"divinatory Astrology.\" <i>Thomas Erastus</i> (1524–1583), Heidelberg physician, a noted opponent of judicial astrology. <i>Philosophastri / Theologastri</i> — coinages of contempt, \"petty-philosophers / petty-theologians.\" <i>Antonius Montculmius</i> (also <i>Moncalmius</i>) — a 16th-c. critic who, Wolf says, refuted the Ptolemaic conjunctions/oppositions and the <i>trutina Hermetis</i>.</li><li><b>Astrology — technical terms.</b> <i>☌</i> / <i>☍</i> = <b>conjunction / opposition</b> (glyphs kept in source, named in translation, protocol §5.3). <i>trutina Hermetis</i> = the \"balance (scales) of Hermes,\" the <i>Trutina Hermetis</i>, the classic rule for rectifying a nativity from the Moon's place at conception (kept as a technical term). <i>significatores</i> = <b>significators</b>; <i>Horoscopum / horoscopat</i> = the <b>horoscope</b> in its original sense of the <b>ascendant</b>/ascending degree (translated \"horoscope [ascendant]\" to avoid the modern \"natal chart\" sense); <i>progressiones</i> = <b>progressions</b>; <i>latitudo</i> = (celestial) <b>latitude</b>. <i>partes</i> (geometric/zodiacal) = <b>degrees</b>.</li><li><b>The Camerarius rectification.</b> <i>Camerarius … qui … de Horoscopo et de motu singulorum planetarum 27 partes detrahit</i> — <b>Joachim Camerarius</b> (\"a most learned man, while he lived most friendly to me\"; the elder, 1500–1574, or his son Joachim the Younger, the physician-astrologer), who subtracts <b>27 degrees</b> from Wolf's ascendant and from each planet's place. The resulting placement, as printed: ascendant <b>♑ Capricorn</b> (in place of <b>♒ Aquarius</b>); <b>♄ Saturn in ♏ Scorpio</b>, <b>♃ Jupiter in ♋ Cancer</b>, <b>☉ Sun and ☿ Mercury in ♌ Leo</b>, <b>♀ Venus in ♍ Virgo</b>, <b>☾ Moon in ♎ Libra</b>. All glyphs kept in the Source block and named in English (protocol §5.3).</li><li><b><i>sic itur ad astra</b></i> — \"thus is the way to the stars,\" Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> 9.641; here a deliberate astronomical double-entendre (kept as the proverbial tag).</li><li><b>Verse 1</b> (<i>Conor, sed cedit patientia victa dolori …</i>) — an elegiac quatrain on the limits of patience under chronic pain; kept as verse. The first line adapts the topos of Ovid (cf. <i>patientia victa</i> / pain-overcomes-resolve), but the quatrain is given without a named source in the letter; the attribution is therefore not asserted.</li><li><b>Verse 2</b> (<i>Duc me parens celsique dominator poli …</i>) — <b>Seneca's</b> Latin version of the prayer of <b>Cleanthes</b> the Stoic, <i>Epistulae morales</i> 107.11 (the Greek original cited by <b>Epictetus</b>, <i>Enchiridion</i> 53). Wolf names both sources in the prose. Kept as verse; the last line is the famous <i>Ducunt volentem fata, nolentem trahunt</i> (\"The Fates lead the willing; the unwilling they drag\").</li><li><b><i>Carneades</b></i> — the Academic philosopher, here the type of the man whose physical pain no philosophy can argue away. <i>Cleanthes … in morbo vociferantem</i> — Wolf, ill, casts himself as a Cleanthes who cries out despite the Stoic hymn.</li><li><b>Persons (closing).</b> <i>Avunculus Steno Bildij</i> = <b>Steen (Steno) Bille</b>, Tycho's maternal uncle (at whose Scanian manor of Herrisvad Tycho observed the 1572 nova); <i>vos … suscepistis</i> is plural — Wolf thanks Tycho <i>and</i> Bille jointly for taking in his pupil. <i>Marcus Hartlieb</i> (Hartliebius), Wolf's wayward pupil, placed in their household. <i>D. Hemmingius</i> = <b>Niels Hemmingsen</b> (Nicolaus Hemmingius, 1513–1600), the eminent Copenhagen theologian, recommended as the man who joined philosophy, theology, and eloquence (Dreyer's note records that Hemmingsen, drawing on Calvin's <i>Admonitio adversus astrologiam</i>, was himself hostile to astrology — an irony Wolf does not flag).</li><li><b>News / political.</b> <i>Megalopyrgio</i> — literally \"great-tower\" (Gk. <i>megas</i> + <i>pyrgos</i>); a Graecized name whose referent is <b>not securely identifiable</b> — possibly a Latinized German toponym/personal name (e.g. a \"Großturm\"-type place) or a coded designation in Wolf's news-passage. <i>Sueco</i> = \"the Swede\" (the Swedish king, John III); <i>Poloni</i> = the Poles (the contested 1574–76 royal succession after Henry of Valois's flight); <i>Saxonia</i> = Saxony. <i>Amurathes Selymi successor</i> = <b>Murad III</b> (r. 1574–95), successor of <b>Selim II</b>, who on his accession (December 1574) had his five younger brothers strangled and buried with their father — the event Wolf reports. <i>Caesar</i> = the Holy Roman Emperor (Maximilian II). <b>Reading crux:</b> <i>\"Caesari octo annorum dedisse indueras\"</i> — <i>indueras</i> is corrupt; the sense plainly requires <i>indutias</i> (\"a truce/armistice of eight years\"), and is so translated, but the printed form is kept in the Source block and flagged here (protocol §4.2). The 1568 Adrianople truce between the Ottomans and the Habsburgs was indeed renewed under Murad.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Nonis Martii 1575</i> = \"on the <b>Nones of March</b> (7 March), 1575\" (Old Style; Denmark and the Empire were pre-Gregorian). The dataset's catalogue label \"1575. 7. marts. Augsburg?\" agrees with the dateline on the day; the place is supplied by the catalogue with a query (the dateline names no place), and is given as <b>Augsburg</b> on the strength of Wolf's residence and the companion 1571 letter (<i>Augustae Vindelicorum</i>), but the letter text itself does not name it — hence \"Augsburg\" in the header is editorial. <i>Iterum vale</i> = \"Again, farewell\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> The published tail of this letter (Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho, 7 March 1575) says Wolf is sending Tycho, as a gift, an astrolabe and an astronomical ring, and adds \"I send you the letter given to me by Schreckenfuchs\" (<i>Literas a Schreckenfuxio ad me datas tibi mitto</i>). The recovered segment is that very enclosure: a letter from Erasmus Oswald Schreckenfuchs to Wolf, dated four days earlier (1 March 1575) from Freiburg, which Wolf then forwarded on to Tycho.</li><li><b>Confirms Tycho's 1569 visit to Schreckenfuchs.</b> Schreckenfuchs writes that \"the person of Tycho Brahe, whom you depict in your letter, is very well known to me\" (<i>notissima mihi est</i>) — direct first-hand corroboration of the personal acquaintance recorded in Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>), where Tycho is said to have visited Erasmus Oswald Schreckenfuchs (1511–1579), professor at Freiburg, and admired his instrument collection during his 1569 travels.</li><li><b>Erasmus Oswald Schreckenfuchs</b> (1511–1579) was a mathematics and Hebrew professor successively at Tübingen, Freiburg and Basel, known for folio commentaries on Peurbach, Sacrobosco and the <i>Tabulae directionum</i> (Gingerich, <i>Annotated Census of Copernicus' De revolutionibus</i>).</li><li><b>Commercial/practical detail.</b> The letter records a payment of sixteen gold crowns received from Wolf on 27 February for an instrument (evidently the \"material sphere\" — an armillary instrument — that Wolf then re-gifted to Tycho \"not knowing it to be the same as an armilla,\" per the published tail), with Schreckenfuchs leaving the final price to Wolf's judgement and warning that assembling it will require care so its painted graduations (\"colours and numbers\") are not rubbed off in handling.</li><li><b>Crux: <i>postes</i> in \"Si praeterea quid desiderares, postes me certiorem reddere.\"</b> \"Postes\" (nominative/accusative plural of <i>postis</i>, \"doorpost\") does not construe grammatically in this clause. The translation above renders the evident intended sense — \"[you] will be able to make me aware of it\" — on the assumption that this is a corrupted or miscopied form of <i>poteris</i> (\"you will be able\"), which also fits the following clause (\"for you will always have an answer from me\"). The source reading has not been altered; this is flagged as an unresolved transcription crux, not silently corrected.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Wolf's numbered seven-item list of astrological controversies (Dreyer, TBOO VII p.16), missing in full from both the Latin source text and the translation, between 'Nam me et ingenium et doctrina in hoc genere destituit.' and 'Nam quotus quisque stellae alicuius altitudinem observat?'</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the body, the Greek, the astrology, and the verse are rendered securely (a sound [B] for those). Two flagged cruxes pull the overall grade to [C]: (1) <b><i>indueras</b></i> in the closing news-passage is a corrupt form translated by the evidently intended <i>indutias</i> (\"truce of eight years\"), the printed reading preserved and not silently emended; and (2) <b><i>Megalopyrgio</b></i> has no securely identifiable referent and is left transliterated. The place \"Augsburg\" in the header is editorial (the dateline gives only the date), as noted.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 17–18: <i>indueras</i> is printed verbatim and unemended (the printed-form-preserved handling is correct); <i>Megalopyrgio</i> is printed as a proper noun with no annotation and is unidentifiable from corpus or open web (a Graecized 'great-tower' name) — [C] stands on it.</p><p>Most of the letter is clear, workmanlike Latin and translates without difficulty; the identification of the sender, addressee and occasion is secure against the published tail, which explicitly announces this very enclosure. However there is a genuine, unresolved textual crux (<i>postes</i> for what is almost certainly <i>poteris</i>), and two phrases — \"ad pretium... splendidius\" and \"sphaerae materialis\" — are compressed instrument-trade shorthand whose precise sense (the specific instrument type, and what \"the finer price\" refers to) is inferred from context rather than certain. Grade C is warranted: sound throughout but with a real, flagged crux affecting one sentence.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp. 15-19, the full letter Wolf-to-Tycho 7 March 1575 plus the enclosed Schreckenfuchs postscript) in two chunks (1-4, 5). Collated opening (PRAeRIPUISTI, TYCHO Nobilissime...), closing (Nonis Martij 1575 / HIERON. WOLFIUS tuus, and the appended Schreckenfuchs note dated Friburgi 1. Martii anno 75), the Greek 2 Peter quotation, the Camerarius horoscope-degree table, the sums (16 gold crowns, 22/27 February dates), and named persons (Ambrosius, Schreckenfuchs, Consul, Maior, Hemmingsen, Erastus, Montculmius, Camerarius, Cleanthes/Epictetus/Seneca, Steno Bille, Marcus Hartlieb, Petrus, Amurath/Murad III) against the page images. One serious defect found: an entire Dreyer-printed numbered list (the seven astrological controversies Wolf poses) is missing from our source_text and consequently from the translation. Aside from that dropped list, the remaining text (including the verse quotations and the enclosed Schreckenfuchs letter) matches Dreyer exactly.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Dreyer (TBOO VII p.16) prints a full numbered seven-item list of the specific astrological controversies Wolf is asking Tycho to refute (heavens' motion, planetary motion, nativity-casting method, Arab/Indian doctrinal disagreement, uncertain civic foundings, twins' dissimilarity, uncertain birth-moment) between 'destituit.' and 'Nam quotus quisque'; our source_text drops the whole list, so the translation never had it to render. This is exactly the itemised agenda the rest of Wolf's paragraph (star altitudes, Ptolemaic aspects, horoscope discrepancies, Camerarius) is answering point-by-point, so its loss removes the structuring frame a scholar needs. Dreyer prints: <i>1. Controversia de numero et motu coelorum. 2. De motu planetarum. 3. De ratione constituendi Thematis Natalitii. 4. Diversa et dissidentia pronunciata de effectibus siderum. Nec enim Arabes cum Ptolemaica doctrina, nec Indi (ut ex Abrahamo conijcio) cum utrorumque dogmatibus consentiunt. 5. Incerta regnorum et urbium initia et magnarum conjunctionum et trigonorum. 6. Gemellorum summa dissimilitudo rebus plerumque omnibus. 7. Incertum momentum infantis editi, et incertae corrigendi rationes.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.15-18 of the slice, pp.15-19 printed; read as page images, not the OCR text layer): p.16 prints the numbered list 1-7 exactly as the audit cites, in the exact location claimed. Restored both the Latin (normalising long-s and ss-ligature to the current_latin house spelling already used throughout this document, e.g. 'ae' not the ligature, no lowercase 'j' in common words) and a matching English translation of the seven items. No other cruces surfaced on the pages read (pp.15-18); p.19, the Schreckenfuchs exchange, was outside the defect's location and was not needed.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15711113001", "next": "brahe-t_15780509001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that a carrier's treachery caused Tycho to miss acquiring Ambrosius's library and Schreckenfuchs's armilla, and offers to help procure the latter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15750307001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15750311001", "d": "1575-03-11", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Paul Hainzel", "ck": "Paul Hainzel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Paul Hainzel → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 11 March 1575 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer, vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTI IUVENI DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, AMICO SUO SINGULARI.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>SI vales, bene est, mi TYCHO, ego quidem Dei beneficio nunc recte valeo, et febre quartana tandem liberatus sum. Quae de Instrumentis tuis Astronomicis scripsisti, libenter legi, et illud Armillarum Ptolemaicarum, quod solum te nunc desiderare intellexi, WOLFIJ nostri auxilio atque literis (certo nuncio ad SCHRECKENFUCHSIUM Friburgum cum munere pro ijs Armillis misso) facile a SCHRECKENFUCHSIO impetravimus. Quod quidem PETRUS Aurifaber cum supellectile sua hinc ad vos transportabit. Et licet COPERNICUS plenam et ornatam de compositione huius Organi descriptionem in secundo suo de Revolutionibus libro cap. 14 referat, tamen etiam IOHANNIS REGIOMONTANI de constructione et usu eius enarrationem adiungere Instrumento volui: quam ex REGIOMONTANI scriptis (Norimbergae de quinque Instrumentis Astronomicis, Torqueto, Astrolabio Armillari, Regula magna Ptolemaica, Baculo Astronomico et Quadrato geometrico cum IOHANNIS SCHOeNERI Carolostadij additionibus, anno MDXLIII editis) transcribi curavi, si forte eo libro careres, de quo tamen sane dubito. Tabellarius iste nequam, qui nummis, quos illi credidisti, absumptis profugit, nondum redijt, is vel vagus et exui a patria errabit, vel quod abstulit, restituet. MARCUS HARTLIEB, uxoris meae ex sorore nepos, ante biennium Wittenbergam studiorum caussa a parentibus missus, annum forte ibi commoratus est. Priore semestri vel plus eo se, ut diligentem et liberum decet adolescentem, strenue gessit, et bonam in literis operam navare coepit. Posteriore semestri minus sui officij satagebat, quod quidem simul atque parentes eius licet serius resciscerent, statim eum Wittenbergâ ad fratrem IOHANNEM, I. V. Doctorem, Consiliarium Electoris, Heydelbergam ut proficisceretur, avocarunt. Is in itinere cum in quendam hominem, ne quid scribam vehementius, non bonum incidisset, et in eo itinere aliquot septimanis, non sine magno studiorum suorum et pecuniae dispendio, cunctaretur, tandem Heydelbergam ad fratrem cum comite venit, corporis se et valetudinis incommoda in via pertulisse fingens. Id quod socius eius apud fratrem etiam falso testatus: et MARCO fratri pecuniam in itinere erogasse affirmans, viginti Ioachimicos a D. Doctore IOHANNE MARCI fratre extorsit. MARCUS deinde cum a fratre de omnibus quae hactenus egisset, admonitus et obiurgatus esset, inscio et invito fratre, sibique ipsi male conscius Heydelbergâ discedit. Quo posthac profectus sit, aut ubi hactenus se detinuerit, non sat scimus. Verum PETRUS Aurifaber, qui tuas mihi reddidit, aiebat eum Hafniae apud te bonis conditionibus esse, et studijs incumbere. M. IOHANNES quoque MAIOR significabat, se literas a MARCO apud eundem PETRUM accepisse, quibus fere idem scribebat, et praeterea culpam deprecabatur, atque rogabat, ut apud patrem de reconciliatione ageret. Sed iratus pater aegre adhuc reconciliabitur. Si veram paenitentiam egerit atque resipuerit, gratum mihi feceris, si mea caussa eum, quod sine molestia tua fieri velim, iuveris: sin aliquid posthac praeter officium fecerit, et in priori sua negligentia perstiterit, quicquid in eum supplicii sumpseris, etiamsi eum egregie et male mulctaveris, et parentibus et nobis propinquis et cognatis suis rem gratam feceris. Mater eius, piae memoriae femina, praeterito mense, 3 nimirum Nonas Februarii, vesperi hora 8 pie in Christo obdormivit: cum ante annos 48 Nonis eiusdem mensis in hunc mundum nasceretur. Illud te admonere volui: In compositione magnarum PTOLEMAeI Armillarum (quae minutorum vel subtilioris distributionis capaces sunt) cum distantia Polorum (Aequinoctialis videlicet et Eclipticae) pro ratione temporis mutetur, Axonia, quae cum Delatore Zodiaci in Polis mundi convertuntur, ita in Delatore transijci posse, ut 24 minutorum spatio (quae sunt, ut nosti, differentiae maximae et minimae obliquitatis Signiferi) hinc inde pro mutatione Declinationis Solis maximae, quocunque volueris moveri, et nihilominus intra tale spatium ubivis et quandocunque certo loco secundum diametrum cum Polo mundi connecti convertique queant. Praeterea id quoque ad pleniorem huius Instrumenti perfectionem facere videtur (quo stellarum loca accuratius exquisitiusque et ad secunda, tertia, quarta etc. scrupula haberi atque capi possint) ut Geometrica illa et artificiosa plurimorum circulorum, intra se invicem ductorum, distributio in planis lateribus Armillarum atque earum maxillis (quod facile in tanta Armillarum latitudine fieri potest) debito modo notetur atque designetur. Sed quid ego noctuas Athenas, qui haec ad te? Illud pene significare tibi praetermiseram. Quadrantem nostrum Giggingensem Nonis Decembris proximis, increbrescente vehementiore vento, lapsum corruisse, eiusque usum nobis nunc penitus ademptum esse. Iterum atque iterum vale. Clavem ad capsulam Armillarum hic una mitto. Frater meus et ANDREAS WELSERUS plurimam tibi salutem reddunt, atque tui memoriam cum honorifica de te mentione saepenumero recordari solent.</p><p>Iterum vale, et me, quod facis, redamare ne desinas.<br>Dabantur Augustae Vindelicorum, die Aequinoctij verni, hoc est 5. Idus Martij, Anno salutis M. D. LXXV.<br>T. Paulus Haintzel.</p><p>Quae subiunguntur post scripta, erant manu fratris eius<br>Iohannis Baptistæ<br>Haintzelij, Septem viri Augustani, exarata.</p><p>Vir nobilissime, amice observande, intelligo ex scriptis et<br>lucubrationibus doctorum virorum regni vestri, Dominum PETRUM OXEUM summum esse Regni<br>vestri Consiliarium ac curiæ magistrum. Is, cum fuerit olim<br>Basileæ, sub SIMONE GRYNÆO, communi<br>præceptore nostro, et convictor et condiscipulus meus, habueritque<br>præceptorem D. D. CHRISTIERNUM MORSIANUM, virum doctum et bene de me<br>meritum, perquam libenter scire velim, an ambo recte habeant et<br>valeant. Quod, si amicitiæ nostræ, olim Basileæ inter nos contractæ,<br>adhuc sunt memores, velis ambos data occasione meo nomine amanter et<br>reverenter salutare, illisque mea officia et studia prompta et<br>parata deferre. Te etiam et tuam salutem propitio humani generis<br>Salvatori commendo, precans ex animo, ut quam diutissime salvus et<br>incolumis Ecclesiæ et Reipublicæ servetis et conservetis.</p><p>Tui observantiss.<br>Iohannes Baptista<br>Haintzelius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the young man pre-eminent in nobility and learning, Master Tycho Brahe, his singular friend — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>If you are well, it is good, my Tycho; I, for my part, by God's kindness am now in sound health, and have at last been freed from a quartan fever. What you wrote about your astronomical instruments I read gladly; and that matter of the Ptolemaic armillae — the only thing I gathered you now wanted — we have easily obtained from Schreckenfuchs, with the help and letters of our friend Wolf (a trusty messenger having been sent to Schreckenfuchs at Freiburg with a gift in return for those armillae). Peter the goldsmith will carry it from here to you along with his own baggage. And although Copernicus gives a full and elegant account of the construction of this instrument in the second book of his <i>De revolutionibus</i>, chapter 14, I nevertheless wished to add to the instrument Johann Regiomontanus's exposition too, on its construction and use — which I had transcribed from Regiomontanus's writings (published at Nuremberg, on the five astronomical instruments: the torquetum, the armillary astrolabe, the great Ptolemaic rule, the cross-staff [<i>baculus astronomicus</i>], and the geometric square, with the additions of Johannes Schöner of Karlstadt, in the year 1543) — in case you happened to lack that book, though of that I rather doubt. That worthless courier, who absconded after squandering the money you entrusted to him, has not yet returned; either he will wander as a vagrant and an exile from his homeland, or he will restore what he carried off. Marcus Hartlieb, my wife's nephew by her sister, sent two years ago by his parents to Wittenberg for the sake of his studies, stayed there a year, as it chanced. In the first half-year, or rather more, he conducted himself energetically, as becomes a diligent and free-born youth, and began to apply good effort to his letters. In the latter half-year he was less attentive to his duty; and as soon as his parents learned of this — albeit rather late — they at once recalled him from Wittenberg, that he should set out for Heidelberg to his brother Johannes, Doctor of Both Laws, councillor of the Elector. On the way, having fallen in (that I may write nothing too harsh) with a man who was not good, and having dawdled some weeks on that journey, not without great loss to his studies and his money, he came at last to his brother at Heidelberg with a companion, pretending that he had suffered bodily and ill-health troubles on the road. His companion likewise falsely testified to the same before the brother; and, affirming that he had laid out money for Marcus on the journey, he extorted twenty Joachimsthalers from Master Doctor Johannes, Marcus's brother. Then Marcus, when he had been admonished and rebuked by his brother for everything he had done up to that point, departed from Heidelberg without his brother's knowledge and against his will, ill at ease in his own conscience. Where he set out for afterward, or where he has detained himself up to now, we do not well know. But Peter the goldsmith, who delivered your letter to me, said that he was at Copenhagen with you on good terms, and applying himself to his studies. Master Johannes Maior also indicated that he had received a letter from Marcus, through the same Peter, in which he wrote much the same thing, and besides begged pardon for his fault and asked that [Maior] would treat with his father about a reconciliation. But the father, being angry, will still be hard to reconcile. If he shall have shown true repentance and come to his senses, you will do me a kindness if, for my sake, you help him — which I should wish to be done without trouble to yourself; but if hereafter he does anything contrary to his duty, and persists in his former negligence, then whatever punishment you exact upon him — even if you chastise him soundly and severely — you will do a welcome thing both to his parents and to us, his kinsmen and relations. His mother, a woman of pious memory, in the past month — namely on 3 February [the third before the Nones of February], at 8 in the evening — fell piously asleep in Christ, having been born into this world 48 years before on the Nones of the same month. This I wished to point out to you: in the construction of the great Ptolemaic armillae (which are capable of [a division into] minutes or of a still finer division), since the distance of the poles (namely of the equinoctial and of the ecliptic) changes with the lapse of time, the little axles, which turn with the zodiacal carrier-ring upon the poles of the world, can be so shifted within the carrier-ring that over a span of 24 minutes (which are, as you know, the differences of the greatest and least obliquity of the zodiac) they may be moved either way at will, in keeping with the change of the Sun's greatest declination, and may nonetheless, anywhere and at any time within such a span, be connected and turned together with the pole of the world along the diameter at a fixed place. Furthermore, this too seems to make for a fuller perfection of this instrument (whereby the places of the stars may be had and taken more accurately and exactly, even to seconds, thirds, fourths, etc. of scruples [of arc]): that that geometric and artful distribution of very many circles, drawn one within another, should be marked and traced out in due manner on the flat sides of the armillae and on their jaws [maxillae] (which can easily be done given so great a breadth of the armillae). But why do I [carry] owls to Athens, in [writing] these things to you? I had nearly omitted to mention this to you: that our quadrant at Göggingen, on the last Nones of December [5 December], a rather violent wind having risen, slipped and collapsed, and the use of it is now wholly taken from us. Again and again, farewell. The key to the case of the armillae I send here together [with this]. My brother and Andreas Welser return you many greetings, and are wont often to recall your memory with honourable mention of you.</p><p>Again, farewell; and do not cease — as you do — to return my love.<br>Given at Augsburg, on the day of the vernal equinox, that is, the 5th [day before] the Ides of March [11 March], in the year of salvation 1575.<br>Yours, Paul Hainzel.</p><p><b>What follows, appended after the writing, was penned in the hand of his brother Johann Baptist Hainzel, Septemvir of Augsburg.</b></p><p>Most noble sir, esteemed friend, I understand from the writings and learned labours of the learned men of your kingdom, that Master Peter Oxe is the chief Councillor of your kingdom and Master of the Court. Since he was once at Basel, under Simon Grynaeus, our common teacher, both my messmate and fellow-student, and had as his tutor Master Christiern Morsianus, a learned man and one well deserving of me, I should very gladly wish to know whether both are well and in good health. Wherefore, if they still remember our friendship, once contracted between us at Basel, would you, when occasion offers, greet both of them lovingly and respectfully in my name, and offer them my ready and prepared service and devotion. You too, and your welfare, I commend to the gracious Saviour of mankind, praying from my heart that you may, for as long as possible, remain safe and unharmed, and preserve yourselves for the Church and the Commonwealth.</p><p>Your most devoted, Johann Baptist Hainzel.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Paulus Haintzel</i> = <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (d. 1581), city councillor (<i>Ratsherr</i>) of Augsburg of the leading Hainzel patrician family; with his brother Johann Baptist Hainzel (burgomaster) a patron of astronomy and friend of Tycho from Tycho's Augsburg years (1569–70). It was Paul Hainzel who funded the celebrated great quadrant (radius ~14 cubits, c. 19 ft) built with Tycho in 1569 at his country estate at Göggingen, southwest of Augsburg.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> The dataset header repeats \"Tycho Brahe Tycho Brahe\"; a single addressee, Tycho, is intended (the salutation reads <i>Domino Tychoni Brahe</i>). At this date (March 1575) Tycho was at Copenhagen (<i>Hafniae</i>, as the letter itself states), before the 1576 grant of Hven.</li><li><b><i>Schreckenfuchs</b></i> = <b>Erasmus Oswald Schreckenfuchs</b> (1511–1579), mathematician and astronomer, then at <b>Freiburg im Breisgau</b> (<i>Friburgum</i>), commentator on Ptolemy, Peurbach and Sacrobosco; the source of the <i>Armillae Ptolemaicae</i>.</li><li><b><i>Petrus Aurifaber</b></i> — \"Peter the goldsmith\": <i>Aurifaber</i> is here read as the trade-name/surname of a craftsman-courier who carried both letter and instrument; rendered \"Peter the goldsmith.\" Not to be confused with the Wittenberg/Königsberg theologian-editor of the same Latinised name.</li><li><b><i>Schoeneri Carolostadij</b></i> = <b>Johannes Schöner</b> of <b>Karlstadt</b> (Carolostadius, 1477–1547), Nuremberg mathematician, who edited Regiomontanus's <i>Scripta</i> (the collection on the torquetum, armillary astrolabe, etc.). Hainzel dates the edition <i>anno MDXLIII</i> (<b>1543</b>); the standard Nuremberg Schöner edition of the Regiomontanus instrument tracts is usually dated <b>1544</b> — the as-written 1543 is preserved and the divergence flagged, not corrected.</li><li><b><i>Johannes / Johann Regiomontanus</b></i> = <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller of Königsberg, 1436–1476). The instrument list in the Schöner/Regiomontanus tract names five: <i>Torquetum</i>, <i>Astrolabium Armillare</i> (armillary astrolabe), <i>Regula magna Ptolemaica</i> (great Ptolemaic rule), <b><i>Baculus Astronomicus</b></i> (the astronomical staff / Jacob's-staff — the <i>baculus</i>, rendered with the English instrument-name \"cross-staff,\" with the source word <i>baculus astronomicus</i> bracketed), and <i>Quadratum geometricum</i> (geometric square). Note: the source word here is <i>baculus astronomicus</i>, not the glossary lemma <i>radius astronomicus</i> (§5.2); the English \"cross-staff\" denotes the same class of instrument, but the bracketed Latin preserves the source's own term.</li><li><b><i>Marcus Hartlieb</b></i> — Hainzel's wife's nephew (sister's son), a wayward student then said to be with Tycho at Copenhagen; <b>Johannes (Hartlieb)</b>, his elder brother, <i>I. V. Doctor</i> (<i>Iuris Utriusque Doctor</i>, Doctor of Both Laws — civil and canon) and councillor to the Elector [Palatine] at Heidelberg. <i>M. Iohannes Maior</i> = <b>Johannes Maior (Meier)</b>, gymnasium teacher at Augsburg. <i>Andreas Welserus</i> = <b>Andreas Welser</b>, of the Augsburg patrician/merchant Welser family.</li><li><b><i>viginti Ioachimicos</b></i> = \"twenty <b>Joachimsthalers</b>\" (silver coins of Joachimsthal, the <i>Joachimsthaler</i>/<i>thaler</i>); kept as the period coin-name.</li><li><b>Astronomy / instruments (the technical core).</b> <i>Armillae (Ptolemaicae)</i> = <b>Ptolemaic armillae / armillary</b> (the Ptolemaic <i>astrolabon</i>, the great observing armillary), per glossary §5.2. <i>Organum / Instrumentum</i> = <b>instrument</b> (§5.2). <i>Delator Zodiaci</i> — literally the \"carrier/bearer of the zodiac\": the ring that carries the ecliptic/zodiac, rendered \"zodiacal carrier-ring.\" <i>Axonia</i> (Gk.-derived, <i>ἀξόνια</i>, \"little axles/pivots\") — the small pivot-axles on which the carrier-ring turns at the poles; rendered \"little axles.\" <i>Poli mundi</i> = \"the poles of the world\" (celestial poles); <i>Polorum Aequinoctialis et Eclipticae</i> = the poles of the equinoctial (equator) and of the ecliptic. <i>obliquitas Signiferi</i> = \"obliquity of the zodiac\" (<i>Signifer</i> = the sign-bearer, the zodiac/ecliptic); the \"24 minutes [of arc]\" Hainzel cites as the range between greatest and least obliquity. <i>Declinatio Solis maxima</i> = \"the Sun's greatest declination\" (§5.1, <i>declinatio</i> → declination). <i>scrupula</i> (secunda, tertia, quarta) = \"scruples [of arc]\" — the sexagesimal subdivisions (seconds, thirds, fourths); <i>minuta</i> = \"minutes.\" <i>maxillae Armillarum</i> = \"the jaws of the armillae\" (the projecting edge-pieces/cheeks of the rings), rendered \"jaws [maxillae].\" The passage is Hainzel relaying a constructional refinement for the great armillary — allowing the ecliptic-carrying ring to be re-set within a 24′ range to follow the secular change in the obliquity of the ecliptic. The technical prose is dense but coherent; rendered faithfully without smoothing.</li><li><b><i>Copernicus … de Revolutionibus libro [II] cap. 14</b></i> — the reference is to Copernicus, <i>De revolutionibus</i> Book II (the construction of the <i>astrolabon</i>/armillary instrument is at II.14 in the standard text). The source as transcribed reads <i>in secundo suo … libro cap. 14</i>; the book-number \"second\" is preserved.</li><li><b><i>noctuas Athenas</b></i> — \"owls to Athens,\" the proverb (Erasmus, <i>Adagia</i>; cf. <i>γλαῦκ' Ἀθήναζε</i>) for needlessly supplying what one already has in abundance; Hainzel apologises for explaining instrument-craft to Tycho. Kept as the proverb (\"owls to Athens\"); the elided verb supplied in brackets \"[carry].\"</li><li><b><i>febris quartana</b></i> = <b>quartan fever</b> (§5.4).</li><li><b><i>Quadrantem … Giggingensem</b></i> = \"our <b>Göggingen</b> quadrant\" — the great quadrant at Hainzel's Göggingen estate (spelt <i>Giggingensem</i> in the source; the place is Göggingen/Göggingen near Augsburg, \"Goggingen\" in Dreyer, \"Göggingen\" in Norlind). Hainzel reports it blew down in a high wind on 5 December (<i>Nonis Decembris proximis</i>) and is no longer usable.</li><li><b>Dates within the letter.</b> <i>3 Nonas Februarii</i> — \"the third [day before] the Nones of February\" = <b>3 February</b> (Nones of February = 5 Feb.; counting inclusively, the 3rd-before = 3 Feb.), the death of Marcus's mother, said to have been born \"48 years before, on the Nones of the same month\" (i.e. born 5 February ~1527, died 3 February 1575). <i>Nonis Decembris</i> = the <b>Nones of December = 5 December</b> (collapse of the quadrant). Roman-calendar idiom retained and glossed per §3/§26; no silent conversion.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Dabantur Augustae Vindelicorum, die Aequinoctij verni, hoc est 5. Idus Martij, Anno salutis M. D. LXXV</i> = \"Given at <b>Augsburg</b>, on the day of the vernal equinox, that is, <b>the 5th [day before] the Ides of March = 11 March</b>, 1575.\" Ides of March = 15 March; <i>5. Idus Martij</i> counts inclusively (15, 14, 13, 12, 11) to <b>11 March</b>. The header date (11 March 1575, O.S.) agrees with the dataset <i>label</i> (\"1575. 11. marts. Augsburg\"). Hainzel's equation of 11 March with the vernal equinox reflects the pre-Gregorian Julian drift of the calendar against the true equinox; the dating is <b>O.S.</b> (the Gregorian reform of 1582 lay seven years ahead, and the Empire/Augsburg used the Julian calendar in 1575).</li><li><b>MS witnesses.</b> Two Vienna copies in the Tycho <i>Nachlass</i>: A = ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (shelfmarks as given in the dataset).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> The published tail of this letter (Paul Hainzel to Tycho, 11 March 1575) closes with news of a collapsed quadrant at Göggingen and greetings from \"my brother\" and Andreas Welser; the recovered segment is that brother's own postscript, explicitly headed as being in the hand of Johann Baptist Hainzel — a separate, self-contained note asking Tycho to relay greetings to two men at the Danish court.</li><li><b>Peter Oxe = Peder Oxe (1520–1575),</b> at this date Denmark's Rigshovmester (Lord High Steward) and its most powerful royal councillor, effectively running crown finance and administration for Frederick II; he died later the same year (24 October 1575), so this letter falls within his last months in office.</li><li><b>The Basel connection.</b> Johann Baptist Hainzel identifies himself as a former fellow-boarder and fellow-student of Peder Oxe at Basel, both taught by \"Simon Grynaeus\" — the corpus does not resolve whether this is Simon Grynaeus the elder (1493–1541, the celebrated Basel Hellenist and editor of Euclid/Proclus, a contemporary of Erasmus) or his nephew Simon Grynaeus the younger (1539–1582), also a Basel professor; Oxe's documented period abroad in the 1540s makes the elder Grynaeus the more plausible teacher, but this is not corroborated in our holdings and should be checked against a Peder Oxe biography before use.</li><li><b>Unidentified: \"D. D. Christiernum Morsianum.\"</b> Named as Peder Oxe's own tutor (\"praeceptorem\") and a man Johann Baptist is personally indebted to — this is a distinct individual from Elias Olsen Morsing (Tycho's later Hven assistant, also surnamed for the island of Mors), who was not yet active in the 1540s–50s timeframe implied here. our corpus does not otherwise identify a \"Christiern Morsing\" as Peder Oxe's tutor; this is a genuinely new name surfaced by the recovered text and worth flagging for further biographical research.</li><li><b>Possible conflict with Dreyer on which Hainzel brother held which office.</b> The recovered text's own heading calls Johann Baptist \"<i>Septem vir Augustanus</i>\" (a Septemvir — one of the Seven, an Augsburg patrician civic office). Dreyer's <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890), by contrast, states that of the two brothers \"the former [Johann Baptist] burgomaster, the latter [Paul] an alderman (septemvir).\" The letter's own contemporary self-description of Johann Baptist as Septemvir sits awkwardly with Dreyer's assignment of that office to Paul — either Dreyer reversed the brothers' offices or used the titles loosely; this is worth resolving against Augsburg civic records rather than taken as settled.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two one-word corruptions in the Latin apparatus restored from Dreyer: \"intellige\" to \"intelligo\" (TBOO VII, p.21) and \"Torquete\" to \"Torqueto\" (TBOO VII, p.19). Both English clauses were already correct and are unchanged.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the prose, courtesy, and the Hartlieb narrative are rendered securely; the residual soft points, all flagged rather than smoothed, are (i) the precise mechanical sense of the dense armillary-construction passage (<i>Axonia</i> / <i>Delator Zodiaci</i> / the 24′-obliquity re-setting), rendered faithfully but technical; and (ii) the source's edition-date <i>MDXLIII</i> (1543) for the Schöner/Regiomontanus instrument tracts, where the received bibliographic date is 1544 — preserved as written, not corrected. (The intercessor for Marcus's reconciliation — supplied as <i>[Maior]</i> — and <i>invito fratre</i> / \"against his will\" are interpretive but honestly bracketed/recoverable.)<br>&lt;/invoke&gt;</p><p>The Latin itself is plain, unremarkable humanist correspondence and its sense is not in doubt. The residual uncertainty is entirely biographical/identificatory rather than textual: which Simon Grynaeus is meant, who \"Christiern Morsianus\" was, and which brother actually held the Septemvir office are all open questions the translation flags but cannot resolve from the materials at hand. Grade B: the Latin translation is sound, but the apparatus rests on an uncollated cross-check against Dreyer and no corpus identification of Morsianus.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter -- Hainzel's body (the Ptolemaic armillae obtained via Schreckenfuchs/Wolf, the Regiomontani instrument list and its 1543 Nuremberg edition, the absconding courier, the Marcus Hartlieb family scandal with its dates and the 20-Joachimsthaler extortion, the mother's death date/age, the technical armillae-construction passage with its 24-minute figure, the Göggingen quadrant's collapse on the Nones of December, the closing date '5 Idus Martii 1575') plus Johann Baptist Haintzelius's appended postscript (Peter Oxe, Simon Grynaeus, Christiern Morsianus, Basel) -- against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.19-21 (slice pp.1-3), reading page images directly. Two source_text-vs-Dreyer divergences found, both narrow single-word corruptions in our Latin apparatus (a case ending, a verb mood); the English translation itself correctly follows Dreyer's actual printed reading in both places, so neither is a translation-fidelity defect -- but per protocol any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C. All proper names, numbers, dates, and sums checked and correct.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text has \"Torquete\", which is not a grammatical form here; Dreyer prints \"Torqueto\", correctly agreeing in case with the surrounding ablative list. The English correctly identifies the instrument as \"the torquetum\" regardless, so the translation itself is not at fault -- this is a corruption in our published Latin apparatus. Dreyer prints: <i>Torqueto (TBOO VII, p.19) -- the ablative case, agreeing with the rest of the ablative list of Regiomontanus's five instruments (Astrolabio Armillari, Regula magna Ptolemaica, Baculo Astronomico, Quadrato geometrico)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). source_text has the imperative \"intellige\" (\"understand!\"); Dreyer prints the indicative \"intelligo\" (\"I understand\"). This is a one-letter mood shift in our Latin apparatus. The English translation (\"I understand...\") in fact follows Dreyer's actual reading, not our own source_text -- further confirming the source_text is the corrupted party here, not the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>intelligo (TBOO VII, p.21, opening of Johann Baptist Haintzelius's postscript)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.19-22 (both disputed readings fall on pp.19 and 21, both examined). Both TEXT_ERRORs confirmed exactly as the audit described; both are pure Latin-apparatus corruptions that had not misled the (already correct) English translation. No remaining uncertainty.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Paul Hainzel", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15740310001", "next": "brahe-t_15790325001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that Schreckenfuchs's Ptolemaic armillae have been obtained and will be sent with Regiomontanus's treatise on the instrument's construction and use.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15750311001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15750406001", "d": "1575-04-06", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "George Buchanan", "ck": "George Buchanan", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Stirling", "t": "George Buchanan → Tycho Brahe — Stirling, 6 April 1575 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Georgii Buchanani … Opera omnia </i>(cited in the source as<i> G. Buchanani Opera omnia</i>; = the text reproduced in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>GENEROSISSIMO AC DOCTISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE OTTHONIDI.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>IAM agitur annus, TYCHO doctissime, ex quo GULIELMUS LUMMISDAL, cum isthinc domum rediret, librum tuum de Nova Stella reddidit, quod munus multis nominibus mihi fuit gratissimum, vel quod abs te esset, id est, ab homine claritate generis illustri, eximia ingenij felicitate, et literis perinde expolito, qui illam Philosophiae partem, quae est, ut cecinit vester MANILIUS,</p><p>Regales animos primum dignata movere,<br>Proxima tangent es rerum fastigia coelo,<br>iacentem erexisti, et e vulgi manibus in suam sedem, hoc est, regiam reduxisti. Cumulat etiam rari muneris gratiam tua humanitas, qui e tam excelso et dignitatis et doctrinae culmine nos humiles, penitus penitusque iacentes non solum favens spectas, sed prope manu attollis, et hortaris exemplo, ut et nos velle cupiamus nube vehi, validique humeris insistere Atlantis: illamque etsi falsam, plurimis nationibus tamen pro vera receptam opinionem tuis monumentis, labore et industria refutas, ingenia scilicet hominum sub hac languenti et frigida coeli plaga inertiae a natura damnata esse. Igitur cum omnes populi septentrionales publice, et nos privatim tibi debeamus plurimum, non existimabis, arrogantius a me factum, quod ad hunc usque diem non rescripserim. Procul enim a meae vitae instituto et moribus hoc crimen abesse, omnes norunt, qui me non ignorant. Neque etiam eos clam est, quam proximo biennio morbis assiduis gravissime afflictus fuerim, ac nullam pene horam ad scribendum vacuam habuerim, adeo ut cum alia multa leviora sim coactus intermittere, tamen quinque libros de Sphaera iam dimidiatos emittere, ac spem scribendi carminis penitus abijcere. Quamobrem tibi gratulor, qui istam generis claritatem a maioribus, ingenij felicitatem nactus, aetatem adhuc integram doctrina sic excolueris, ut familiae a natura splendidissimae tua virtute splendorem adieceris. Nos in hac aetatis effetae ignavia certe currenti libenter applaudemus, et si quid officij praeterea exigi a nobis poterit, tibi, quoad potero, gratificabor. Hunc autem GULIELMUM LUMMISDAL, qui has tibi tradet, rogavimus, ut de statu rerum et studiorum tuorum diligenter me certiorem faceret: quem etiam tibi commendamus, ut pro claritate tua ipsum in quacunque re iuves.</p><p>Vale.<br>Sterlini postridie Nonas Aprilis, Anno 1575.<br>GEORGIUS BUCHANANUS.</p><p>Interea autem, dum hæ Buchanani literæ forte sunt in itinere, ego cum quodam<br>nobili, eius Conterraneo, miseram illi libellum meum de Nova Stella, ea adhuc<br>durante, editum. Cum vero exemplum nullum earum literarum mihi supersit,<br>Epigramma solum modo apponam, eidem libro manu scriptum præfixeram .</p><p>AD CLARISSIMUM VIRUM ET POETAM EXCELLENTEM GEORGIUM BUCHANANUM<br>SCOTUM.</p><p>Hunc tibi non magnum mittit, Buchanane, libellum</p><p>    Ignotus Boreis Tycho remotus aquis:</p><p>Sit licet ignotus, tamen is te novit, amatque,</p><p>    Carminibus toto notus es orbe tuis.</p><p>Nec Boreas animos poterit disiungere nostros,</p><p>    Nam te etiam Boreæ frigidus Axis habet</p><p>Qualis enim cœlum convolvi Dania spectat,</p><p>    Talis et hoc ipsum Scotia vestra videt.</p><p>Exigua hæc igitur nostri monumenta laboris</p><p>    A Borea in Boream consule missa boni.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned gentleman Tycho Brahe, son of Otte. He sends heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>A year is now going by, most learned Tycho, since William Lumisden, on his way home from your parts, delivered your book <i>On the New Star</i> — a gift most welcome to me on many counts: both because it came from you, that is, from a man illustrious in the renown of his lineage, of outstanding felicity of intellect, and likewise polished in letters, who has raised up that part of Philosophy which (as your Manilius sang)</p><p>first deigned to stir the minds of kings,<br>[and which,] touching the topmost summits of things, lies next to heaven,<br>[—that part of Philosophy] which lay prostrate, and have led it back out of the hands of the common crowd into its own seat, that is, into a royal one. Your kindness, too, heaps grace upon a rare gift: you who, from so lofty a peak both of rank and of learning, not only look favourably upon us lowly men, lying utterly and altogether prostrate, but well-nigh raise [us] up with your hand, and exhort [us] by your example, so that we too may long to wish to be borne upon a cloud and to stand upon the shoulders of mighty Atlas; and you refute, by your works, by labour and industry, that opinion — false, yet received as true among very many nations — namely, that the intellects of men under this faint and cold tract of sky are by nature condemned to sluggishness. Therefore, since all the peoples of the north owe you most greatly in public, and we in private, you will not reckon it done too presumptuously by me that to this very day I have not written back. For all who are not unacquainted with me know that this fault is far from the rule and habit of my life. Nor is it hidden from them how grievously I have been afflicted, throughout the last two years, by relentless illnesses, and how I have had scarcely an hour free for writing — so much so that, while I have been compelled to set aside many other lighter [tasks], [I have been forced] nonetheless to let go of my five books <i>On the Sphere</i>, now [only] half-done, and to cast away utterly the hope of writing the poem. Wherefore I congratulate you, who, having got that renown of lineage from your ancestors, [and] felicity of intellect, have so cultivated with learning an age still in its prime that by your own merit you have added splendour to a family by nature most splendid. We, in the sluggishness of this worn-out age of ours, will gladly applaud one [who is] surely running [his course]; and if any further service can be required of us, I shall oblige you so far as I shall be able. This William Lumisden, moreover, who will hand this [letter] to you, we have asked to inform me diligently of the state of your affairs and of your studies; him too we commend to you, that, in keeping with your renown, you may help him in whatever matter [there may be].</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Stirling, the day after the Nones of April [6 April], the year 1575.<br>George Buchanan.</p><p>Meanwhile, however, while this letter of Buchanan's happened to be on its way [to me], I had, through a certain nobleman, his countryman, sent him my little book on the New Star, published while it [the star] was still lasting. But since no copy remains to me of that letter [which accompanied the gift], I shall set down only the epigram which I had prefixed, written in my own hand, to that same book.</p><p><b>To the Most Renowned Man and Excellent Poet, George Buchanan the Scot.</b></p><p>This not-great little book, Buchanan, Tycho sends to you —<br>    unknown, removed by northern waters:<br>Though he be unknown, yet he knows you, and loves you;<br>    by your songs you are known through the whole world.<br>Nor will the North Wind be able to part our two spirits,<br>    for the cold Axis of the North holds you too;<br>For such as Denmark sees the heavens wheeling,<br>    such likewise does your own Scotland see this very thing.<br>These slight memorials, then, of my labour,<br>    sent from one North to another North, take in good part.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent / sender.</b> <i>Georgius Buchananus</i> = <b>George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the Scottish humanist, Latin poet, and historian — at this date tutor to the young James VI and a senior figure at the Scottish court — writing from Stirling. His own <i>De Sphaera</i>, the five-book Latin didactic poem on the cosmos mentioned here, was never completed and was published posthumously (1586). The source cites his collected <i>Opera omnia</i> as edition of record.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe Otthonidi</i> — Tycho Brahe; <i>Otthonidi</i> \"son of Otte\" is the Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father (cf. <i>Ottonides</i> in the 1571 Wolf letter, protocol §5.6).</li><li><b><i>S. P. D.</b></i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Gulielmus Lummisdal</b></i> = <b>William Lumisden</b> (Lumsden), the Scot who carried Tycho's book to Buchanan a year earlier and now carries this reply back; spelled <i>Lummisdal</i> in the source on both occurrences. He is commended to Tycho in the closing sentence.</li><li><b><i>librum tuum de Nova Stella</b></i> — Tycho's <i>De nova stella</i> (Copenhagen, 1573), his account of the 1572 supernova in Cassiopeia. The phrase is descriptive (\"your book on the new star\"), not necessarily the exact title-page wording.</li><li><b>The Manilius quotation.</b> <i>ut cecinit vester Manilius</i> — \"as your Manilius sang,\" from the <i>Astronomica</i> (Book I, opening), the work Buchanan treats as Tycho's poetic patrimony (<i>vester</i>, \"your [people's],\" addressing the astronomer-poet). The two verses concern <i>Philosophia</i> / astronomy: the science that \"first deigned to stir the minds of kings\" and, \"touching the topmost summits of things, lies next to heaven.\" <b>Textual crux:</b> the source's second line reads <i>\"Proxima tangent es rerum fastigia coelo\"</i> — a garbled setting of the standard <i>proxima tangentis rerum fastigia caelo</i> (the participle <i>tangentis</i> split across a line-end into <i>tangent | es</i>). The printed form is kept verbatim in the Source block; the translation follows the intelligible reading <i>tangentis</i> (\"touching\"), construing it with the science that lies <i>proxima … caelo</i> (\"next to heaven\"). The grammatical hinge (whether <i>tangentis</i> qualifies the science or stands in another case) is the one genuinely soft point and is flagged here, not smoothed.</li><li><b><i>nube vehi, validique humeris insistere Atlantis</b></i> — \"to be borne upon a cloud and to stand upon the shoulders of mighty Atlas\": a poetic image of being lifted to the heavens (Atlas the sky-bearer); kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>The \"northern sluggishness\" topos.</b> <i>ingenia … hominum sub hac languenti et frigida coeli plaga inertiae a natura damnata esse</i> — the (classical, Ptolemaic-climatic) commonplace that minds bred under the cold northern sky are doomed to dullness; Buchanan's compliment is that Tycho's work <i>refutes</i> it. Rendered straight, with the hedge <i>etsi falsam … pro vera receptam</i> (\"though false, yet received as true\") preserved.</li><li><b><i>quinque libros de Sphaera … dimidiatos emittere</b></i> — Buchanan's own poem <i>De Sphaera</i> in five books, here described as <i>dimidiatos</i>, \"[only] half-done.\" The infinitive <i>emittere</i> is governed by <i>sim coactus</i> (\"I have been compelled\") and stands in parallel with <i>intermittere</i> (\"set aside\") and <i>abijcere</i> (\"cast away utterly\") — a list of works illness <i>forced him to give up</i>, not works he published. <i>emittere</i> is therefore rendered \"let go of,\" not \"put out/publish\": Buchanan is enumerating renunciations, and the <i>De Sphaera</i> was in fact never finished by him (it was completed and published posthumously, 1586). <i>spem scribendi carminis … abijcere</i> registers the same loss — his fear of never finishing it (illness, <i>morbis assiduis</i>). His epistemic framing — that illness <i>forced</i> (<i>coactus</i>) these renunciations — is kept; no claim is sharpened.</li><li><b><i>istam generis claritatem a maribus … nactus</b></i> — \"having got that renown of lineage from the men of your line.\" <i>a maribus</i> is specifically \"from the males / the male line\" (descent through the male line), not the neutral \"from your forebears\"; the specificity is preserved in the translation and noted here.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Sterlini postridie Nonas Aprilis, Anno 1575</i> = \"at <b>Stirling</b>, the day after the Nones of April, the year 1575.\" The Nones of April fall on the 5th; <i>postridie Nonas</i> is therefore <b>6 April</b> (O.S.; Scotland used the Julian calendar). This matches the dataset <i>label</i> (\"1575. 6. april. Stirling\"); no discrepancy. The Roman-calendar idiom is rendered with both idiom and civil date per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Source spellings <i>ingenij, abijcere, Otthonidi, coelo, vehi</i> and the capitalised proper names are humanist-Latin conventions, left as printed in the Source block.</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> This is not part of Buchanan's letter (the sender of record) at all, but Tycho's own explanatory note, in the first person, inserted to account for how the correspondence began: while Buchanan's letter of 6 April 1575 (the published tail) was crossing in transit, Tycho had already sent Buchanan a presentation copy of his <i>De Nova Stella</i> (1573) via an unnamed Scottish nobleman, \"his countryman.\" Since Tycho no longer had a copy of his own covering letter, he preserves only the Latin epigram he had inscribed by hand in the gift book itself.</li><li><b>The epigram is Tycho's own autograph dedicatory verse to George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the Scottish humanist, poet and historian — at this date tutor to the young James VI and one of the most celebrated Latin poets in Europe. The poem plays on the North/\"Boreas\" conceit throughout: Tycho, \"unknown\" in Scotland, sends greetings from one cold northern land (Denmark) to another (Scotland), arguing that the shared northern sky under which both men live unites them despite personal unacquaintance.</li><li><b>Confirms the gift-exchange context of the Buchanan correspondence.</b> The published tail of Buchanan's own reply names William Lumisden (\"Gulielmus Lummisdal\") as the bearer entrusted to bring news of Tycho's affairs back to Buchanan; the recovered note shows this was already the second leg of an exchange that began with Tycho's own unsolicited gift of <i>De Nova Stella</i>, carried by a different, unnamed Scottish nobleman — evidence of Tycho using his 1572 nova publication as an instrument of international humanist self-introduction within a year of its printing.</li><li><b>William Lumisden / \"Gulielmus Lummisdal\"</b> (the courier named in Buchanan's own letter) is not further identifiable in our corpus at this date; the only Lumisden the corpus surfaces is the unrelated eighteenth-century Jacobite antiquary Andrew Lumisden (1720–1801), too late to be relevant here.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from the corrupted 'a maribus' to 'a maioribus' ('from your ancestors'), restoring the word split across the Dreyer page break; English corrected to match, and the bracketed gloss explaining the corrupted reading removed. Per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII pp.21-22.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the prose is rendered securely and the dateline is unambiguous; the sole flagged crux is the corrupted second Manilius verse (<i>tangent es</i> for <i>tangentis</i>), where the source as printed is unconstruable and the translation follows the established reading of the <i>Astronomica</i>.</p><p>The prose explanatory note is straightforward and its sense secure. The elegiac epigram is likewise linguistically clear — no corrupt readings — but Latin verse compresses sense more tightly than prose, so the translation prioritises accurate rendering of content and word order over meter or rhyme; a specialist reading against the conventions of Neo-Latin epigram (e.g. the precise force of \"consule boni,\" rendered here as \"take in good part,\" a classical idiom for indulgent reception) would refine but is unlikely to change the sense materially. Not collated against a published critical edition. Grade B.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.21-23 as page images. The Buchanan letter runs from 'GENEROSISSIMO AC DOCTISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE OTTHONIDI' at the bottom of p.21 through the epigram at the bottom of p.22 (p.23 begins an unrelated Pratensis letter and was read only to confirm the boundary; a separate Haintzelius-to-Tycho covering note at the top of p.21 likewise precedes and is not part of this letter). Collated the Manilius quotation, the full prose body, the closing date (postridie Nonas Aprilis = 6 April 1575), Tycho's own editorial note, and the appended epigram word-for-word against the page images. One TEXT_ERROR found, as above -- a genuine hyphenation-splice corruption in our source Latin, not an OCR artefact, confirmed by re-reading the page-break at 'a ma-/ioribus'.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Dreyer's page break splits the word as 'ma-' (end of p.21) / 'ioribus' (top of p.22) = 'a maioribus', 'from [one's] ancestors' -- the ordinary sense of the clause ('renown of lineage from your ancestors'). Our source_text drops the 'io', rejoining the hyphenated halves as 'a maribus', 'from the males' -- a different and much odder claim. The translator rendered the corrupted reading literally and added a bracketed Latin gloss, but did not catch or flag the underlying corruption against Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>qui iftam generis claritatem a ma-ioribus, ingenij felicitatem nactus (TBOO VII pp.21-22, word hyphenated across the page break)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.21-22 (page images, not OCR text layer), where the word is unambiguously split 'ma-ioribus' across the page break. This was the one high-severity defect in the batch and is now resolved with both Latin and English corrected. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of the book *On the New Star* and praises Tycho for restoring astronomy from common neglect to its proper dignity.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15750406001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760131001", "d": "1576-01-31", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Johannes Pratensis → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 31 January 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, vols VI–VIII Epistolae) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOHANNES PRATENSIS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>SALUTEM ET OMNIA FAUSTA.</p><p>RECEPI literas tuas, TYCHO optime, et pyxidem obsignatam cum pecunia, quam postera statim die Rostochium misi; qui tulit, chirographum quitantiarium referet: cui quoque negotium dedi de vase tuo huc perferendo. Ubi venerit, diligenter curabo. Quam vellem te apud nos hîc esse: desiderio enim tui tangimur non parvo. Avet animus cognoscere ex te, quid rei in mundo agatur, quem nuper aliqua ex parte perlustrasti. Sumus hîc extra coeli Solisque vias, scenam mundanae fabulae vix audimus quidem, tantum abest ut spectatores simus. Sed fuerit hoc fatum Aquiloni debitum. Tu nuper reversus a foecundo illo Austro, postquam nobis coeli aperuisses penetralia, prius inaccessa ignotaque, moxque nobis inscijs et inopinantibus in Austrum dilapsum sub mediam brumam, totam deinde perlustrasti Teutoniam, atque Italiae vicinas oras, cumque viris doctis atque ingentibus ingentes et doctos miscuisti sermones: quae tua semper fuit voluptas. Sed vereor, ne hi una cum iucundo placidoque Austro te istuc alliciant, ut tacite eo mediteris iter. Sic enim Genius mihi in somnis dictavit, susurrans, te Basilaeam prae alijs Germaniae urbibus exoptare vicinam Italis atque Gallis, ubi magnus ille ERASMUS olim floruit, et multi etiamnum eximij viri clarent. At mallem te, modo amplus ille tuus sic restringi posset Genius, nobiscum in hac manere patria, et rigidos Boreae flatus una persentiscere, ac quantum in te est emollire, fi non prorsus avertere: aut saltem ut huius intemperiei socium patientem in te habeamus. Sed si omnino nos relinquere stat sententia, uti etiam forte meretur haec tuo Genio atque ingenio minus apta intemperataque aura, et importuna conversatio: da saltem adhuc insigne aliquod tuarum doctrinarum specimen: quale id fuit, cum te ultimo a nobis inexpectantibus subducens reliquisti. Fecisti autem hoc (quod laudo, bonique omnes praedicant) in Regis, qui sic expetebat, gratiam, quod nemo ex illustri nobilitate Danica ante te praestitit unquam, nec, uti existimo, quispiam in posterum facturus, adeo ut tute primus et postremus sis, qui hanc laudis adoream tibi vendicaris, quibus istae, quas tui ordinis homines affectant, gloriolae comparari nequeunt. Si itaque hoc meritum priori adjunxeris, ut antequam te carituri simus (vereor enim ne id fiat, licet tu propositum nos celes) nobis plura adhuc explanare non degravatus fueris, nos beatiores quam antea reddideris, tibique adhuc plus devinxeris. Et si id commodum tibi non fuerit, saltem de Hypothesibus PTOLEMAeI et COPERNICI nos summatim instrue, quaenam harum verior sit, et utrum ulli earum acquiescendum, an vero potius Ptolemaica assumptio sit castiganda, prout a te factum est, et Copernicea ad stabilitatem Terrae convertenda, uti etiam insinuasti tuoque sic nobis praeluxisti ingenio: aut si quid aliud in his secretioris cognitionis, quod nos fugiat, animo volvis, impertiaris quaeso. Quin et hoc ex te scire aveo, idque ego solus, utpote qui in praelectione publica II libri PLINIJ de mundo circa hunc nodum resolvendum non parum haeserim: quomodo videlicet intelligendum sit, quod dictus autor in fine XVI cap. ea, quae in Stationibus et Regressibus Planetarum videmus per inhibitionem et repercussionem radiorum Solarium fieri, ubi epicyclos omnes tollere videtur, ac si veteribus in usu non fuissent, mirabili sane invento, a se, uti tandem insinuare videtur, primum comperto, si modo res haec ipsi coelo congrua sit, et suis numeris constare possit, quod tibi per otium dispiciendum relinquo. Locus, quem indicavi, te de pluribus admonebit. Haberem et plura eius generis, de quibus avide tecum conferre cuperem, et nisi hyems et invalitudo ob continuas in pulmonem defluxiones mihi obstaret, ad vos in Scaniam transirem, atque ibi tecum, quae nostra est voluptas, aliquandiu suaviter philosopharer. Sed, o spes hominum fluxae et irritae. Cogimur saepe esse ubi nolumus, et rapimur eo, quo non ire deliberavimus. Verum optime mi TYCHO, amplius detinere te nolo. Tu fac nos hîc Haffniae brevi tua praesentia exhilares, atque ingeniorum nostrorum radios, iam lumen remittentes, et in crepusculum tendentes vespertinum, illustres ac vegetiores reddas, animisque nostris sacer, uti soles, illabaris.</p><p>Iamque vale, et fratrem tuum, aliosque qui tecum sunt, sanguine iunctos ex me officiose saluta.<br>Dabantur Haffniae ult. die Ianuarij Anno 1576.<br>T.<br>IOH. PRATENSIS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johannes Pratensis to Tycho Brahe. Greeting, and all good fortune.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, excellent Tycho, and the sealed box with the money, which I sent on to Rostock the very next day; the man who carried it will bring back a receipt for the quittance — to him I also gave the charge of conveying your vessel here. When it arrives, I shall see to it carefully. How I wish you were here with us: for we are touched by no small longing for you. My mind is eager to learn from you what is going on in the world [in mundo], which you have lately surveyed in some part. We are here outside the paths of the heaven and the Sun; we scarcely so much as hear the scene of the world's play — so far are we from being its spectators. But let this be the fate owed to the North. You, lately returned from that fruitful South, after you had opened to us the inner recesses of the heaven, before inaccessible and unknown — and then, while we were unaware and unsuspecting, having slipped away into the South in mid-winter — went on to traverse all Germany [Teutonia] and the neighbouring shores of Italy, and with men learned and great mingled great and learned discourse: which was ever your delight. But I fear lest these, together with the pleasant and mild South, lure you thither, so that you secretly plan a journey there. For thus a Genius dictated to me in my dreams, whispering that you long, before other cities of Germany, for Basel — close to the Italians and the Gauls — where that great Erasmus once flourished, and where many distinguished men are still illustrious today. Yet I would rather — if only that ample Genius of yours could be so reined in — that you remain with us in this homeland, and feel together with us the harsh blasts of Boreas, and, so far as in you lies, soften them, if not turn them away entirely; or at least that we may have in you a patient companion in this inclemency. But if it is your fixed resolve to leave us altogether — as perhaps this air, ill-suited and intemperate for your Genius and disposition, and this troublesome society, deserves — then give us at least some further notable specimen of your learning, such as that was when, withdrawing yourself from us last time when we did not expect it, you took your leave. This you did (a thing I praise, and which all good men proclaim) for the sake of the King, who so desired it — a thing no one of the illustrious Danish nobility ever rendered before you, nor, as I judge, will any do hereafter, so that you are at once the first and the last to claim this glory of praise for yourself, with which those little glories that men of your order pursue cannot be compared. If, therefore, you will add this service to the former — that, before we must do without you (for I fear that this will come to pass, though you conceal your intention from us), you will not think it a burden to expound still more to us — you will render us happier than before, and bind us yet more to yourself. And if that be not convenient to you, then at least instruct us in summary on the Hypotheses of Ptolemy and of Copernicus: which of these is the truer, and whether one ought to rest in either of them, or whether rather the Ptolemaic assumption is to be corrected — as has been done by you — and the Copernican turned back to the stability of the Earth, as you have also intimated and have by your genius thus lighted the way for us; or, if you turn over in your mind anything else of more secret knowledge in these matters that escapes us, share it, I beg. Moreover, this too I am eager to learn from you — and I alone — inasmuch as in my public lecture on the second Book of Pliny on the world I have stuck not a little over untying this knot: how, namely, it is to be understood that the said author, at the end of chapter XVI, [says] that those things which we see in the Stations and Retrogradations of the planets come about through the inhibition and repercussion of the Sun's rays — where he seems to do away with all epicycles, as though they had not been in use among the ancients — by a truly marvellous discovery first found out by himself, as he at length seems to intimate (if only this matter be congruous with the heaven itself, and can be made to agree with its own numbers): which I leave to you to examine at your leisure. The passage I have indicated will admonish you of further matters. I should have many more things of this kind too, about which I would eagerly desire to confer with you; and were not the winter and my ill-health — owing to continual catarrhs flowing down upon the lung — in my way, I would cross over to you into Scania, and there with you, which is our delight, philosophize agreeably for a while. But — O the fleeting and idle hopes of men! We are often compelled to be where we would not, and are dragged off whither we had resolved not to go. But, my excellent Tycho, I do not wish to detain you longer. Do you make us here at Copenhagen glad before long by your presence, and the rays of our wits — now sending back their light and tending toward the evening twilight — render bright and more vigorous, and glide, hallowed, into our spirits, as you are wont to do.</p><p>And now farewell, and dutifully greet from me your brother and the others who are with you, joined to you by blood.<br>Given at Copenhagen, the last day of January, the year 1576.<br>Yours,<br>Joh. Pratensis.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), professor of medicine at Copenhagen and one of Tycho's closest friends; a Paracelsian physician in the circle of Petrus Severinus. He would die suddenly later in 1576. The letter's anxiety that Tycho may leave Denmark is poignant against that backdrop.</li><li><b>Recipient/dating context.</b> Written 31 January 1576, shortly before Frederik II's grant of Hven (gift made 18 February 1576 N.S. / O.S. discussion aside) — Pratensis still fears Tycho will emigrate; the worry is real, not formulaic.</li><li><b><i>pyxidem obsignatam … vase tuo</b></i> — \"the sealed box … your vessel\": Pratensis is forwarding money to Rostock on Tycho's behalf and arranging carriage of a <i>vas</i> (an alchemical/chemical vessel, given Tycho's and Pratensis's shared Paracelsian chemistry). <i>chirographum quitantiarium</i> = a written quittance/receipt; kept literal.</li><li><b>Geography of the metaphor.</b> <i>Aquilo / Boreas</i> = the North (Denmark) and its cold winds; <i>Auster</i> = the South (Germany/Italy), figured as \"fruitful\" and \"mild.\" <i>Teutonia</i> = Germany; <i>Scania</i> = Skåne/Scania (where Tycho's family seat Knudstrup lay). The whole passage casts Denmark as offstage, \"outside the paths of the heaven and the Sun\" — the rhetorical conceit of provincial darkness.</li><li><b><i>Genius</b></i> — rendered \"Genius\" (the tutelary/inner spirit, twice: the dream-Genius that whispers, and Tycho's own \"ample Genius\"); kept as a period term, not flattened to \"genius\" in the modern sense.</li><li><b><i>Basilaeam … magnus ille ERASMUS</b></i> — <b>Basel</b> (where Erasmus of Rotterdam had lived and where his press flourished); Pratensis imagines Tycho drawn there for its nearness to Italy and France and its humanist lustre.</li><li><b>Astronomy — the Ptolemy/Copernicus request.</b> <i>de Hypothesibus PTOLEMAeI et COPERNICI … Ptolemaica assumptio sit castiganda … et Copernicea ad stabilitatem Terrae convertenda</i> — Pratensis asks Tycho to adjudicate the two <b>hypotheses</b> (geometric world-models): whether to <b>correct</b> (\"castigate\") the Ptolemaic <i>assumptio</i> and to <b>turn the Copernican system back to the stability of the Earth.</b> This is an early external witness — pre-dating the Hven instruments and the <i>De mundi</i> (1588) — that Tycho was already intimating a reformed, geo-stable model: \"as has been done by you … as you have also intimated.\" Read with care: Pratensis reports Tycho's <i>insinuation</i>, not a finished system; the hedges (<i>insinuasti</i>, <i>praeluxisti</i>) are preserved.</li><li><b>The Pliny crux.</b> <i>in praelectione publica II libri PLINIJ de mundo … in fine XVI cap. … Stationibus et Regressibus Planetarum … per inhibitionem et repercussionem radiorum Solarium … ubi epicyclos omnes tollere videtur</i> — Pratensis, lecturing publicly on <b>Pliny, <i>Natural History</i> Book II</b> (the cosmological book, \"de mundo\"), is stuck on a passage he places \"at the end of chapter XVI,\" where Pliny explains the planets' <b>stations and retrogradations</b> as caused by the <b>inhibition and repercussion of the Sun's rays</b>, seeming thereby to dispense with epicycles entirely and to claim the explanation as his own discovery. (The relevant Plinian matter is in the chapters on planetary motion, <i>NH</i> II §§69–77 in modern numbering; Pratensis's \"chapter XVI\" follows the chapter-division of his 16th-century edition and is <b>not</b> silently renumbered here — see calendar/citation note.) <i>nodum resolvendum</i> = \"untying this knot,\" the interpretive difficulty; <i>suis numeris constare</i> = \"agree with its own numbers\" (be quantitatively consistent). Rendered with Pliny's own causal language (\"inhibition and repercussion of the Sun's rays\") kept intact.</li><li><b><i>radios … in crepusculum tendentes vespertinum</b></i> — \"the rays [of our wits] tending toward the evening twilight\": the closing conceit that without Tycho the Copenhagen scholars' intellects are guttering toward dusk; <i>crepusculum</i> = <b>twilight</b> (glossary §5.1). <i>animis nostris sacer … illabaris</i> — \"glide, hallowed, into our spirits,\" a near-sacral compliment.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Humanist Latin of c. 1576: <i>coeli, foecundo, Ptolemaeī</i> with the <i>ae</i>-ligature; <i>ij</i> for <i>ii</i> (<i>alijs, eximij, inscijs, PLINIJ</i>); <i>fi non prorsus avertere</i> — the printed <i>fi</i> is a long-s misreading for <i>si</i> (\"if not\"), kept as printed in the Source block and read <i>si</i> in the translation (it parses only as <i>si</i>, parallel to the later <i>si … non fuerit</i>). No negation is added or dropped by this reading.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Dabantur Haffniae ult. die Ianuarij Anno 1576</i> = \"Given at Copenhagen, the last day of January, the year 1576\" — i.e. <b>31 January 1576</b> (O.S.; Denmark was on the Julian calendar). The catalogue label (\"1576. 31. januar. København\") agrees with the dateline. <i>Haffnia</i> = Copenhagen (glossary §5.7). The subscription <i>T.</i> before the name is the writer's flourish (cf. exemplar usage), left as \"Yours,\".</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the epistemic hedges in the astronomy request (<i>prout a te factum est … insinuasti</i>) are preserved without sharpening. Two flagged points: (1) the <b>Pliny chapter citation</b> — Pratensis's \"chapter XVI\" reflects his 16th-century edition's division and is deliberately left unconverted, not reconciled to modern <i>NH</i> II numbering; (2) the printed <i>fi</i> for <i>si</i> (\"if not… avert\"), an editorial long-s reading recorded in the apparatus and kept in the Source block.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Pliny ch.-XVI left unconverted (editorial) + a harmless fi→si long-s; no crux, sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 22-25 as page images (letter proper on 23-24). Collated opening and closing lines and every proper name (Rostochium, Basilaeam, Erasmus, Ptolemaei, Copernici, Plinij, Ioh. Pratensis) and the closing date (ult. die Ianuarij Anno 1576) against the printed page. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text divergence found; the English tracks the Latin sentence for sentence.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15730416001", "next": "brahe-t_15760214001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15720517001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15760214001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Confirms forwarding a sealed box and money to Rostock and expresses eagerness to hear Tycho's news from his recent travels through Germany.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760131001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760214001", "d": "1576-02-14", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Knutstorp", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Pratensis — Knudstrup, 14 February 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer, vol. VII)</i> · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOHANNEM PRATENSEM.</p><p>ACCEPI literas tuas, eruditissime et amantissime PRATENSIS, ex quibus intelligo te negotium nostrum Rostochiense probe curasse, quo nomine tibi gratias ago. Cupis a me scire quid rerum in mundo geratur, quidque ferat foecundus ille Auster, a quo nuper redij. Atque ego pleraque eorum quae istic cognoveram, tibi, quam primum reversus essem, Haffniae retuli, plura etiamnum relaturus, si quae ulterius in mentem venerint, ubi vos denuo invisero, quod intra paucos dies facere decrevi; est enim res magni momenti, quam nulli praeterquam Magnifico Domino Legato Gallico DANZAeO et tibi intimo meo aperire animus est, vestrumque de hac audire consilium. Quoniam vero de abitu meo denuo in Germaniam mens tibi nonnulla praesagit, licet ego idipsum supprimam, dicam tibi quippiam in aurem, nemini nisi soli Domino DANZAeO, quem scis taciturnum et fidum, aperiendum. Recte quidem sive vigilanti sive somnianti tibi tuus dictavit Genius vatidicus, me aliud iter versus Austrum iterum meditari. Quod forte inde subolfecisti, quia nullas ex ijs arcibus, quas Serenissimus Rex noster mihi, cum in reditu illum debitae observantiae causa Sorae submisse salutassem, clementissime et motu proprio obtulit inhabitandas, recipere voluerim, et quod videas me de ijs quae in hac patria geruntur non admodum esse solicitum. Quod insuper Basileam saepenumero in ore isthic apud vos habuerim, eius oportunitatem cupido philosophiae alumno et tranquillitatis Apollineae, Musarumque amatori depredicârim, in qua urbe et tu aliquando vixisti, eamque plurimi facere solitus es: Ex his ut opinor, Genius seu potius ingenium tuum perspicax et providum de meo abitu et istuc eundi intentione nonnulla coniecit et suspicionem tibi movit non prorsus frivolam. Nam ut te sensa animi mei occulta non prorsus celem, sic est ut coniectaris: Constitui enim, si Deo sic visum fuerit, hac instante aestate Germanos revisere, adeoque Basileam usque, quae in limitibus Italiae, Galliae et Germaniae iucunde et commode sita est, excurrere, ibique sedes firmas pro studijs meis Olympicis figere, atque Astronomiae instaurationem, quam diu multumque animo volvo, favente astrorum conditore, moliri. Quin et haec erat prioris meae peregrinationis non minima causa, ut locum inquirerem et rationes invenirem, quomodo meis studijs tutius et plenius vacare possem, ijsque rectius consulerem quam hîc in Dania: displicet enim, ut me saepe conquerentem audivisti, haec conversatio, hi mores, haec quotidiana fabula; ex quo vix aliquem hîc reperiam praeter te, cum quo de rebus arduis et vulgi captum exuperantibus, solidi quid conferam: at quam raro nobis simul esse contingat, tute scis. Cum mei ordinis hominibus, quoniam crebrius mihi agendum est, praesertim Herressuadij, Avunculum, quando apud ipsum dego, convenientibus, aut etiam me ipsum Knudstorpij invisentibus (alias enim quantum possum illorum consortia subterfugio) multum temporis mihi perit, multa alias ingrata audienda et facienda. Nosti enim quam sint nobilium horum mores a meis institutis alieni, quamque ab ipsa philosophia: excipio excipiendos, quos tamen plures esse mallem quam sunt.</p><p>Sed audi quid hisce diebus praeter omnem meam expectationem acciderit, idque solus audi, et nemini nisi uni DANZAeO nostro aperi, idque secreto. Cum ultimo die praecedentis septimanae, qui erat undecimus Februarij matutinis horis in lecto expergefactus, adhuc decumberem, et iter hoc de quo modo dixi, in Germaniam tacitus et solicite animo mecum agitarem, simulque qua ratione, consanguineis meis non attendentibus, elabi possem, cogitarem, idque in varias partes volverem atque revolverem; ecce ab improviso nunciatur Regium puerum huc Knudstorpium advenisse, qui tota nocte properasset, ut literas a Rege mihi inscriptas confestim traderet; erant autem adhuc tenebrae, instante saltem crepusculo matutino, et Sol intra duas horas vix oriebatur: iubeo itaque puerum illum ad lectum accersiri, qui erat genere Nobilis atque ex mea consanguinitate, is mihi statim Regis exhibuit literas, atque sibi ab ipso Rege iniunctum esse aiebat, ut absque omni mora me, dies noctesque iter faciendo, quaereret, ubicunque in Scania invenirer, atque illas mihi in manus proprias traderet, statimque reverteretur, quod et factum est. Erant autem scriptae in domo Regis venatoria Ibstrup, quae prope vestram Haffniam intra unum milliare, uti scis, in sylva proxima sita est, idque praecedente die decimo nimirum Februarii. Ego vero literas mox resignans comperio in ijs Regem iubere, ut se confestim accedam, ubicunque in Selandia versaretur, quod et citra omnem moram obsequiose praestiti, adeo ut eodem die, Regi in dicta domo venatoria ante occasum Solis adfuerim. Is tunc me per Nobilem suum cubicularium NICOLAUM PASSBERG in locum quendam solitarium acciri iussit, atque sic, nemine praesente, clementissime allocutus est: «Scis TYCHO, quae et qualia volens tibi obtulerim, cum ultimo Sorae apud me esses, iussique ut brevi ad me rediens, mihi ipsi significares, quidnam in his expeteres, aut ea de re Cancellarium meum NICOLAUM KAASIUM, vel Secretarium IOHANNEM SCHOUGARDIUM, per literas admoneres. At ex quo nihil eiuscemodi interea feceris, cum tamen alij talia avide affectent expectentque, nescio quid animo volvas, aut cur te non mature resolvas. Quidam ex Aulicis meis mihi insinuare visus est, se ab avunculo tuo STENONE BILLE tacite cognovisse te denuo in Germaniam cogitare: Quare te advocare volui, ut ex te ipso cognoscerem, qualis tua sit sententia in ijs quae obtuli, et cur talia non acceptes. Fieri potest, veluti conijcio, quod nulla eiuscemodi Arcium magnarum beneficia Regia obtinere cupias, ne studia tua, quibus delectaris, per causas forenses et alia negotia politica, quae requiruntur in tuendis et administrandis talibus, interturbentur, atque otium ijs vacandi tibi praecludatur. Quare cum nuper in Arce Helsingorensi essem, ubi nunc aedifico, prospiciens ex fenestra quadam Insulam illam parvam Huennam, quae isthic in freto, versus Landskroniam sita est, et a nullo nobili inhabitatur, conspexi, de qua etiam, si recte memini, dictus tuus avunculus STENO BILLE mihi semel dixit, idque antequam in Germaniam ultimo te contulisti, quod eius situs tibi arrideret. Incidit itaque mihi in mentem hanc esse tuis exercitijs tam Astronomicis quam destillatorijs apprime accommodam, siquidem alta sit et solitaria. Nullae quidem aedes ibi sunt, quas inhabitare posses, neque etiam reditus inde suppetunt necessarij. Verum ego tibi de utrisque sufficienter prospiciam. Si itaque hanc Insulam incolere volueris, lubens eam tibi soli concedam. Isthic degere potes quiete et eas exequi artes, quibus afficeris, nemine interpellante. Cumque arcem Helsingorensem splendidius instauravero, veluti nunc incepi, illic subinde commoratus, ad te nonnunquam in eam Insulam transfretabo, atque quid rerum agas in Astronomicis et destillationibus tuis inspiciam, conatusque tuos lubens iuvabo, non quod ego Astronomica intelligam aut sciam quorsum per omnia spectent, vel aliquid utilitatis inde pro mea persona sperem, sed saltem QVIA REX SVM, tuque in hoc meo Regno, ex ea familia, quae mihi semper chara fuit, oriundus, non postremam in his operam navasse diceris: Unde existimo mei officij esse, talia convenienter sustentare et promovere. Quorsum vero opus est, ut denuo in Germaniam concedas, teque isthic peregrinum reddas, cum hîc in patria eadem commode efficere possis quae isthic. Dabimus potius operam, ut eo res deducatur, quod Germani et aliarum Nationum homines, qui talia scire cupiunt, huc accedant, atque ea hîc videant et discant, quae alibi vix reperient. Delibera itaque tecum spacio aliquot dierum, et deinde quid in his tibi faciendum censeas, mihi significa, ubi inaudiveris me in arcem residentiae nostrae Friderigsburgensem reversum esse: Nolo enim ut coacte quidpiam hîc facias, sed liberum tibi relinquo, expensis ijs, quae dixi, statuere id, quod ex re tua iudicaveris. Atque ubi Friderigsburgi mihi adfueris, et quidpiam hac de re conclusum fuerit, tum demum de reditibus necessarijs atque aedificationum sumptibus ita ordinabo et tibi prospiciam, ut indemnis esse queas, atque illic commode peragere ea, quae regno huic et mihi Regi, tum quoque tibi ipsi et tuis esse poterint tam hîc quam alibi honorifica.« In hunc sane modum optimus ille et Patriae ac publici boni, honorisque studiosissimus Rex verba habuit, quibus peractis, ego qua potui et debui reverentia gratias humilime egi, et me, quamvis Insula clementer oblata, ob maris fluctus, quibus undique cingitur, accessus habeat difficiles et saepenumero admodum periculosos, submisse respondi ea de re deliberaturum, atque brevi meam resolutionem Friderigsburgi subiectissime indicaturum: sicque discessi et sequente die, huc domum reversus, ubi, uti et in itinere, de hoc Regio proposito multa tacite pensito; nam mirabile mihi videtur, imo alicuius peculiaris ominis, aut fati instar esse, quod Regius puer eo ipso tempore, quo de abitu in Germaniam solicite mecum deliberarem, matutinis horis tale nuncium adferret; quodque serenissimus et clementissimus Rex noster ex se ipso, me non petente, vel eiuscemodi quid cogitante, talia proponeret; et ut dicam aperte quod sentio, impense me moverunt haec Regis verba, eiusque laudabilis intentio, adeo ut animus meus vacillare et haesitare coeperit, et de priori proposito nonnihil remittere. Vereor enim, ne, si Rege quasi praevidente ea quae mihi novi abitus causas suggesserunt, atque his praeveniente, nihilominus discederem, et forte postea alibi in peregrinis oris mihi quidpiam adversi eveniret, nec omnia ex voto succederent (uti sunt res humanae fluxae et varijs casibus obnoxiae) tum eo magis inde affligerer atque anxius redderer et tanto plus poenitudinis inde caperem, quod nimirum has satis tolerabiles conditiones a laudatissimo Rege mihi singulari clementia benignissime cupienti, oblatas non acceptârim ac in Patria manserim. Nihil tamen certi adhuc constituere possum, nec etiam volo, antequam Domini DANZAeI et tuum audivero consilium. Matri et Avunculo aut etiam Fratri, haec, antequam penitus inter nos deliberatum et conclusum fuerit, in quas partes me dem, non aperiam. Neque enim volo ijs innotescere me denuo in Germaniam proficisci decrevisse. Haec mi PRATENSIS, eo plenius, prout peracta sunt, tibi refero, ut eo expeditius et melius cum DANZAeO nostro deliberare possis, quid mihi faciendum duxeritis, atque vestram sententiam, quamprimum isthuc venero (quod intra biduum vel triduum ad summum futurum spero) ingenue pro amicitia inter nos mutua et syncera aperiatis; neque enim diu cunctandum censeo, antequam Regi Friderigsburgi adsim, atque quomodo me resolverim exponam; quod facere D. V. constitui quamprimum unicum saltem diem Haffniae hac in re vobis tribuero, et quid suaseritis intellexero.</p><p>Quando hae literae a te lectae fuerint, et saepedicto Domino DANZAeO nostro ostensa, eas mox disrumpe aut in cineres redige, ne forte per incuriam alicubi repositae in aliorum manus perveniant; sicque secretae nostrae consultationes, ubi quam maxime nollemus, propalentur. Atque de his nunc satis; plura et particulariora, ubi praesens fuero, intelliges. Verum antequam epistolam claudam, unum adhuc restare video discutiendum, quod prioribus intentus, ferme praeterieram, de opinione illa Pliniana, ad quam quidem non tam absolute, prout rei moles exigit, nunc me resolvere datur, quae tamen obiter et tam cito in mentem venire possunt, dicam. PLINIJ ille locus, quem adducis, videtur obscurior, quam quod in continenti eum prorsus enodare concedatur, neque enim, quae voluit dicere, visus est. Imo mea quidem opinione, nisi plura hac de re intellexerit quam scripsit, sibi ipsi non satisfecit, nedum alijs. Non quidem dubium est planetas ad Solem evidentem obtinere relationem, atque respectu eius Stationes et Retrogradationes, atque nonnullas inaequalitates apparentes, tam quoad Longum quam Latum, differentias exhibere, at quod circa Trinum Solis quam proxime stent superiores tres Planetae, radiisque Solis inhibeantur, rectumque agant cursum, et ignea eius vi in sublime levantur (uti de his dictus ratiocinatur PLINIUS) tum quoque ea quae mox de Retrogradationibus subiungit, non video quomodo constare possint. Neque enim Solis radij quasi catenâ aut aliâ quadam combinatione sunt cum Planetis alligati, qua eos hac vel illac impellant, trahant, aut retrahant, nisi vim quandam quasi Magneticam illis hoc modo inesse imaginemur; et ne sic quidem motio erit coelestibus competens utpote circularis et regularis. Certum enim est Planetarum motiones ubique orbiculariter fieri, vel ex orbicularibus componi, ideoque ea quae PLINIUS insinuat, si ad Solem referenda sunt, quod etiam fieri posse non inficior, alia ratione applicanda veniunt, de qua nonnunquam cogitavi, sed nondum satis me resolvi, ut videlicet tollantur Epicycli (quos veteres etiam non assumpsisse ex PLINIO liquet) et nihilominus apparentiae constent. Sed existimo PLINIUM hanc suam speculationem undecunque tandem acceptam et quomodocunque intellectam in praxin et usum deducere nequivisse, ideoque difficultatem quandam hîc subt esse fassum. Simile quid apud VITRUVIUM, qui diu ante PLINIUM fuit, reperitur, sed locus nunc non succurrit. Is vero Architectonice magis quam Astronomice et Geometrice executus videtur, ex quo hanc sententiam PLINIUM hausisse verosimile est, licet is idipsum dissimulans eam sibi propriam et privatam asseruit. Verum de his ubi D. V. convenerimus, et commoditas dabitur talia disquirendi, ulterius philosophabimur. Tu vero, mi charissime PRATENSIS, quam felicissime vale, et Dominum DANZAeUM nostrum ex me officiose saluta; meque intra paucos, uti dixi, dies isthic ambo incolumes expectate.</p><p>Dabantur e sede nostra Knudsdorpiana die 14. Februarij A. 1576.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Johannes Pratensis.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, most learned and most loving Pratensis, from which I gather that you duly took care of our Rostock business, for which I thank you. You desire to know from me what is going on in the world, and what that fruitful South Wind brings — from which I have lately returned. And most of what I had learned there I reported to you, as soon as I had returned, at Copenhagen; and I shall report still more, should anything further come to mind, when I visit you again — which I have resolved to do within a few days; for it is a matter of great moment, which I am minded to disclose to no one save the Honourable Lord, the French Ambassador Dançay, and to you, my intimate, and to hear your counsel upon it. But since your mind has some presentiment of my departing into Germany again — even though I myself keep it suppressed — I will say something in your ear, to be disclosed to no one but Lord Dançay alone, whom you know to be discreet and trustworthy. Rightly indeed, whether waking or dreaming, did your prophetic Genius dictate to you that I am once more contemplating another journey toward the South. This you perhaps got wind of from the fact that I was unwilling to accept, for my habitation, any of those castles which our Most Serene King most graciously and of his own accord offered me when, on his return, I had humbly saluted him at Sorø out of the observance due to him; and because you see that I am not much concerned about the things that go on in this homeland. And further, because I had Basel often in my mouth there among you, and extolled its convenience for one who is an eager nursling of philosophy and a lover of Apolline tranquillity and of the Muses — a city in which you too once lived, and which you were wont to value highly: from these things, I suppose, your Genius — or rather your keen and provident wit — conjectured something about my departure and my intention of going thither, and roused in you a suspicion not altogether idle. For — not to conceal from you entirely the hidden feelings of my mind — it is as you conjecture: for I have determined, if it shall so seem good to God, to revisit the Germans this coming summer, and indeed to make an excursion as far as Basel — which is pleasantly and conveniently situated on the borders of Italy, France, and Germany — and there to fix a firm seat for my Olympic studies, and, with the favour of the founder of the stars, to set about that restoration of astronomy which I have long and much turned over in my mind. Indeed this too was no small cause of my earlier travels: to seek out a place and find means whereby I might more safely and more fully devote myself to my studies, and attend to them better than here in Denmark. For — as you have often heard me complain — this society, these manners, this daily round, displease me; and I can scarcely find anyone here besides yourself with whom I might confer anything solid about difficult matters that surpass the common grasp; yet how rarely it falls to us to be together, you yourself know. With men of my own order, since I have rather frequently to deal with them — especially at Herrevad, [meeting] my uncle when I am staying with him, or with those who come to visit me at Knudstrup (for otherwise I avoid their company as much as I can) — much of my time perishes, and many other unwelcome things must be heard and done. For you know how foreign the manners of these noblemen are to my own pursuits, and how far from philosophy itself: I make exception of those who deserve it, whom I could nonetheless wish to be more in number than they are.</p><p>But hear what has befallen in these days beyond all my expectation — and hear it alone, and disclose it to no one but our one Dançay, and that in secret. On the last day of the previous week, which was the eleventh of February, in the morning hours, having woken in my bed and still lying there, and turning over within myself, silently and anxiously, this journey into Germany of which I have just spoken — and at the same time considering by what means I might slip away without my kinsmen noticing, and rolling this this way and that — behold, all of a sudden it is announced that a King's page had arrived here at Knudstrup, having hastened all night long to deliver at once a letter addressed to me from the King; and it was still dark, with only the morning twilight coming on, and the Sun barely rising within two hours. So I order that page to be summoned to my bedside — he was a nobleman by birth and of my own kindred — and he straightway showed me the King's letter, and said that it had been enjoined upon him by the King himself to seek me out without any delay, travelling day and night, wherever in Scania I might be found, and to deliver it into my own hands, and then return at once — which was done. The letter had been written at the King's hunting-lodge of Ibstrup, which, as you know, lies near your Copenhagen within one mile, in the nearest wood — and that on the preceding day, the tenth of February, namely. I, then, breaking the seal at once, find in it that the King commands me to come to him immediately, wherever in Zealand he should be — which without any delay I obediently performed, so that on the same day I was present before the King at the said hunting-lodge before sunset. He then ordered me to be called, through his noble chamberlain Niels Parsberg, into a certain solitary place, and thus, with no one present, most graciously addressed me: «You know, Tycho, what things, and of what sort, I willingly offered you when you were last with me at Sorø; and I bade you, returning to me shortly, to signify to me yourself what you desired in these matters, or to advise my Chancellor Niels Kaas, or my Secretary Johan Schougaard, of it by letter. But since you have done nothing of the kind in the meantime — though others eagerly covet and look for such things — I do not know what you are turning over in your mind, or why you do not resolve yourself in good time. One of my courtiers seemed to hint to me that he had quietly learned from your uncle Steen Bille that you are thinking of going to Germany again. For which reason I wished to summon you, that I might learn from your own self what your view is on the things I offered, and why you do not accept them. It may be — as I conjecture — that you do not desire to obtain any royal grants of such great castles, lest your studies, in which you delight, be disturbed by lawsuits and other political affairs which are required in maintaining and administering such places, and the leisure for attending to them be cut off from you. Wherefore, when I was lately at Helsingør Castle, where I am now building, looking out from a certain window I caught sight of that small island Hven, which lies there in the sound, toward Landskrona, and is inhabited by no nobleman — concerning which also, if I rightly remember, your said uncle Steen Bille once told me, and that before you last betook yourself to Germany, that its situation pleased you. So it came into my mind that this island is exceedingly well suited to your exercises, both astronomical and distillatory, seeing that it is high-lying and solitary. There is indeed no dwelling there which you could inhabit, nor do the necessary revenues come from it. But I will provide you sufficiently with both. If, therefore, you should be willing to dwell on this island, I will gladly grant it to you alone. There you can live quietly and pursue those arts by which you are affected, with no one interrupting. And when I shall have more splendidly restored Helsingør Castle — as I have now begun — staying there from time to time, I will now and then cross over to you on that island, and inspect what you are doing in your astronomical matters and your distillations, and gladly assist your endeavours — not that I understand astronomy or know what it all aims at, or hope for any utility from it for my own person, but simply BECAUSE I AM KING, and you, born in this my kingdom, of that family which has always been dear to me, are said to have laboured not least in these things: whence I deem it part of my office to support and promote such things fittingly. But what need is there for you to withdraw into Germany again, and make yourself a stranger there, when here in your homeland you can conveniently accomplish the same things as there? Rather will we take pains that it be brought to this, that Germans and men of other nations who desire to know such things may come hither, and see and learn here what they will scarcely find elsewhere. Deliberate, therefore, with yourself for the space of some days, and then signify to me what you judge ought to be done in these matters, when you have heard that I have returned to the castle of our residence, Frederiksborg: for I do not wish you to do anything here under compulsion, but I leave it free to you, having weighed the things I have said, to determine that which you shall judge to be to your advantage. And when you have presented yourself to me at Frederiksborg, and something has been concluded in this matter, then at last I will so order things concerning the necessary revenues and the costs of building, and provide for you, that you may be without loss, and may conveniently carry out there those things which can be to the honour of this kingdom and of me the King, and also of you yourself and yours, both here and elsewhere.» In just this manner did that best of kings — most zealous for his fatherland and for the public good and honour — frame his words; and these ended, I, with all the reverence I could and ought, most humbly gave thanks, and submissively replied that — although the island was graciously offered — because of the sea's waves, by which it is girt on every side, it has approaches that are difficult and very often quite perilous, I would deliberate upon the matter, and would shortly, most submissively, declare my resolution at Frederiksborg. And so I departed, and on the following day returned home hither, where — as also on the road — I silently ponder much about this royal proposal; for it seems to me wondrous, indeed as though it were some peculiar omen or stroke of fate, that a King's page should at that very time, when I was anxiously deliberating with myself about departing into Germany, in the morning hours bring such a message; and that our Most Serene and Most Gracious King should, of his own accord, without my asking or even thinking of any such thing, propose such things. And — to say openly what I feel — these words of the King, and his praiseworthy intention, moved me exceedingly, so much so that my mind began to waver and hesitate, and to give up somewhat of its former resolve. For I fear lest, if — the King as it were foreseeing the things that suggested to me the causes of a new departure, and forestalling them — I should nonetheless depart, and afterwards perhaps something adverse should befall me elsewhere in foreign parts, and not everything should turn out as wished (as human affairs are fleeting and subject to various chances), then I should be the more afflicted by it and rendered anxious, and take from it so much the more regret — for having not accepted these tolerable enough conditions, offered me with singular clemency by a most praised King most kindly wishing me well, and for having stayed in my homeland. Yet I can determine nothing certain as yet, nor do I wish to, before I have heard the counsel of Lord Dançay and yourself. To my mother and my uncle, or even my brother, I will not disclose — before it has been thoroughly deliberated and concluded among us — which course I shall take. For I do not wish it to become known to them that I have resolved to set out for Germany again. These things, my Pratensis, I report to you the more fully, just as they took place, so that you may the more expeditiously and better deliberate with our Dançay as to what you judge I ought to do, and may frankly disclose your opinion to me — as soon as I shall have come thither (which I hope will be within two days, or three at most) — in keeping with the mutual and sincere friendship between us; for I think there must be no long delay before I present myself to the King at Frederiksborg and explain how I have resolved; which, [God willing,] I have determined to do as soon as I shall have devoted to you at least one single day at Copenhagen in this matter, and learned what you will have advised.</p><p>When this letter shall have been read by you, and shown to our oft-named Lord Dançay, tear it up at once or reduce it to ashes, lest perchance, laid by somewhere through carelessness, it come into others' hands, and so our secret consultations be made public where we should least of all wish it. And of these matters now enough; more, and in more particular detail, you will learn when I am present. But before I close the letter, I see there yet remains one thing to be discussed, which — intent on the foregoing — I had almost passed over: concerning that Plinian opinion, upon which, indeed, it is not now granted me to resolve so absolutely as the weight of the matter requires; yet I will say the things that can come to mind in passing and so quickly. That passage of Pliny which you adduce seems more obscure than that it may be unravelled altogether on the spot, for he does not appear [to have made clear] what he meant to say. Nay, in my opinion at least, unless he understood more about this matter than he wrote, he did not satisfy even himself, much less others. There is indeed no doubt that the planets bear an evident relation to the Sun, and with respect to it exhibit stations and retrogradations, and certain apparent inequalities — differences both as to longitude and latitude; but that the three superior planets stand as near as possible about the trine of the Sun, and are held back by the Sun's rays, and run a direct course, and are lifted on high by its fiery force (as the said Pliny reasons about these matters) — and then too the things he adds next about the retrogradations — I do not see how they can hold good. For the Sun's rays are not bound to the planets as if by a chain or some other linkage, by which they should drive, draw, or draw them back this way or that — unless we imagine some force, as it were magnetic, to be in them in this way; and not even so will the motion be one fitting for the heavenly bodies, namely circular and regular. For it is certain that the motions of the planets everywhere come about in a circular fashion, or are compounded of circular ones; and therefore the things Pliny insinuates, if they are to be referred to the Sun (which I do not deny can also be the case), come to be applied in another way — about which I have sometimes thought, but have not yet sufficiently resolved myself — namely, so that the epicycles be removed (which the ancients too did not assume, as is clear from Pliny) and the appearances nonetheless hold good. But I judge that Pliny was unable to bring this speculation of his — from wherever in the end it was received and however it was understood — into practice and use, and therefore confessed that there was some difficulty lurking here. Something similar is found in Vitruvius, who lived long before Pliny, but the passage does not now come to me. He, however, seems to have treated it architecturally rather than astronomically and geometrically; from whom it is likely that Pliny drew this view, although Pliny, dissembling that very thing, claimed it as his own and proper. But of these things, when, [God willing,] we have met and opportunity is given for examining such matters, we will philosophize further. But you, my dearest Pratensis, fare you most happily, and dutifully greet our Lord Dançay from me; and both of you, safe and sound, expect me there within a few days, as I said.</p><p>Given from our seat of Knudstrup, the 14th day of February, in the year 1576.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), physician and professor of medicine at Copenhagen, Tycho's closest intellectual friend in these years; he died only a few months after this letter. Per protocol §5.6.</li><li><b>Significance.</b> This is Tycho's own contemporaneous, confidential narrative of the audience at which <b>Frederik II</b> offered him the island of <b>Hven</b> (Hveen) — the foundational document for the founding of Uraniborg. Tycho reports the king's words in <i>oratio recta</i> (direct speech), set off here by guillemets as in the source.</li><li><b><i>negotium Rostochiense</b></i> — \"our Rostock business\": an errand Pratensis had transacted on Tycho's behalf at Rostock (unspecified here).</li><li><b><i>foecundus ille Auster</b></i> — \"that fruitful South Wind\": a figurative reference to Tycho's recent return from a journey to the south (Germany); kept as the author's metaphor.</li><li><b><i>Magnifico Domino Legato Gallico DANZAeO</b></i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (Danzaeus, c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French resident ambassador at the Danish court and a humanist confidant of Tycho's. Per protocol §5.6 register, <i>Magnificus</i> rendered \"Honourable.\"</li><li><b><i>Genius vatidicus / Genius seu potius ingenium</b></i> — \"prophetic Genius … your Genius, or rather your … wit\": Tycho plays on <i>Genius</i> (attendant/prophetic spirit) and corrects it to <i>ingenium</i> (native wit); the wordplay is preserved, not flattened.</li><li><b><i>studijs meis Olympicis</b></i> — \"my Olympic studies,\" i.e. studies of the heavens (the Olympian/celestial); kept as figure.</li><li><b><i>Astronomiae instaurationem</b></i> — \"restoration of astronomy\": Tycho's lifelong programme, the <i>instauratio astronomiae</i>; rendered \"restoration of astronomy\" per protocol §5.1.</li><li><b><i>favente astrorum conditore</b></i> — \"with the favour of the founder of the stars\" (God as creator of the heavens); a pious formula.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Haffnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Basilea</i> = <b>Basel</b>; <i>Sora</i> = <b>Sorø</b>; <i>Herressuadium</i> = <b>Herrevad</b> (the abbey held by Tycho's maternal uncle Steen Bille, where Tycho had his laboratory); <i>Knudstorpium / Knudsdorpiana sedes</i> = <b>Knudstrup</b> (the Brahe family seat in Scania, Tycho's birthplace); <i>Ibstrup</i> = the royal hunting-lodge of <b>Ibstrup</b> (later Jægersborg), N. of Copenhagen; <i>Arx Helsingorensis</i> = <b>Helsingør</b> (Kronborg) Castle, then being rebuilt by Frederik II; <i>Insula Huenna</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b> (Hveen), in the Sound toward <b>Landskrona</b> (<i>Landskronia</i>); <i>Friderigsburgum</i> = <b>Frederiksborg</b> Castle (Hillerød), the royal residence; <i>Scania / Selandia</i> = <b>Scania / Zealand</b>; <i>Dania</i> = <b>Denmark</b>. Per protocol §5.7.</li><li><b>Persons at court.</b> <i>Nicolaus Passberg</i> = <b>Niels Parsberg</b>, royal chamberlain (<i>cubicularius</i>); <i>Nicolaus Kaasius</i> = <b>Niels Kaas</b> (1535–1594), Chancellor of Denmark; <i>Iohannes Schougardius</i> = <b>Johan Schougaard</b> (Jacob Skougaard / the royal secretary named in chancery); <i>Steno Bille</i> = <b>Steen Bille</b> (Tycho's maternal uncle, abbot of Herrevad). \"My mother\" = <b>Beate Bille</b>; \"my brother\" and the unnamed <i>Avunculus</i> of the opening paragraph (the uncle at Herrevad) = Steen Bille.</li><li><b>King's self-justification.</b> <i>saltem QVIA REX SVM</i> — \"but simply BECAUSE I AM KING\": the small-capitals emphasis is in the source and is preserved in the translation as small caps/upper-case; it is the rhetorical hinge of the king's speech and is not editorially added.</li><li><b>Astronomy / the Pliny excursus.</b> The closing paragraph debates a passage in <b>Pliny the Elder</b> (<i>Naturalis Historia</i> II, on the planets being checked and accelerated by the Sun's rays near the trine aspect). Glossary §5.1 renderings used: <i>Stationes et Retrogradationes</i> → \"stations and retrogradations\"; <i>inaequalitates apparentes</i> → \"apparent inequalities\"; <i>Longum / Latum</i> → \"longitude / latitude\"; <i>Epicycli</i> → \"epicycles\"; <i>Trinum Solis</i> → \"the trine of the Sun\" (the 120° aspect). <i>vim quandam quasi Magneticam</i> — \"some force, as it were magnetic\": a notable early gesture by Tycho toward a magnetic celestial force; rendered literally, the hedge (\"as it were,\" <i>quasi</i>) preserved. <i>Vitruvius</i> = <b>Vitruvius</b> (<i>De architectura</i> IX), cited as Pliny's probable source.</li><li><b>Textual note.</b> In the Pliny paragraph the printed text reads <i>\"difficultatem quandam hîc subt esse fassum\"</i>; <i>subt</i> is an obvious misprint for <i>subesse</i> (so that the phrase = <i>difficultatem quandam hîc subesse fassum</i>, \"confessed that some difficulty lurks here\"). The printed form is retained in the Source block; the translation follows the corrected reading.</li><li><b><i>ACCCEPI</b></i> (opening word) — printed with three C's; a typographical error for <i>Accepi</i> (\"I have received\"). Retained as printed in the Source block.</li><li><b>Crux: the abbreviation <i>D. V.</b></i> — <i>D. V.</i> occurs twice: (i) <i>\"quod facere D. V. constitui quamprimum unicum saltem diem Haffniae hac in re vobis tribuero\"</i>; (ii) <i>\"de his ubi D. V. convenerimus\"</i>. The abbreviation is genuinely <b>ambiguous</b> and is <b>not resolved by the printed text</b>. The most economical reading is <i>Deo Volente</i> (\"God willing\") — the standard early-modern epistolary formula, and one Tycho uses in clear form earlier in this same letter (<i>si Deo sic visum fuerit</i>); on that reading <i>D. V.</i> is parenthetical and the surrounding words carry the sense (<i>Haffniae</i> = \"at Copenhagen\", <i>vobis</i> = \"to you [both]\", <i>convenerimus</i> = \"we shall have met\"). It could, however, conceivably stand for some other reference (e.g. a place/time formula). The editorial expansion <b>[God willing]</b> is therefore bracketed at both points rather than absorbed silently; <i>D. V.</i> is left unexpanded in the Source block. The earlier draft silently rendered both instances as \"at Copenhagen\" (the place-sense being supplied independently by <i>Haffniae</i>), which over-determined the abbreviation; that has been corrected.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux.</b> All dates are <b>Old Style (Julian)</b>; Denmark used O.S. until 1700. The dateline reads <i>\"die 14. Februarij A. 1576\"</i> (\"the 14th day of February, 1576\"), at Knudstrup — which agrees with the dataset label (<i>1576. 14. februar. Knutstorp</i>). The body, however, narrates an internal sequence of distinct dates, <b>reported, not reconciled</b>: (i) the king's summons was written at Ibstrup on <i>die decimo … Februarii</i> (<b>10 February</b>); (ii) Tycho received it and rode to the audience on <i>undecimus Februarij</i> (<b>11 February</b>), described as \"the last day of the previous week\"; (iii) he wrote this letter on <b>14 February</b>. These three dates are internally consistent (the audience three days before the letter) and are preserved exactly as written; none is silently converted or \"corrected.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two Latin transcription errors corrected: 'ACCCEPI' -&gt; 'ACCEPI' (tripled-C typo for the letter's opening verb); 'invisere' -&gt; 'invisero' (the 'ubi' clause requires the future-perfect, not the bare infinitive). No Dreyer slice (TBOO VII p.25) was available for this letter, so both corrections rest on internal Latin orthographic/grammatical evidence rather than direct collation against the page image; the English translation required no change in either case.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout, and two evident misprints in the printed base text (<i>subt</i> for <i>subesse</i>; <i>ACCCEPI</i> for <i>Accepi</i>) are flagged with the printed form retained in the Source block; the multi-date internal sequence is internally consistent and reproduced without conversion. The single flagged crux that keeps this from [A] is the <b>ambiguous abbreviation <i>D. V.</b></i> (twice), expanded only tentatively as <b>[God willing]</b> (<i>Deo Volente</i>) in square brackets and left unexpanded in the Source block; the reading is probable but not certain, and the text does not resolve it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. tboo was null in the packet (tekstnet serves only Danish for this letter, per the SPECIAL note, so no automatic TBOO page reference had been resolved and no slice_pdf existed). Located the letter myself: it runs TBOO Vol. VII (Epistolae Astronomicae) pp. 25-29, headed 'TYCHO AD IOH. PRATENSEM 14 FEB. 1576' throughout, found by reading the running headers/marginal apograph-notes on the neighbouring letters' page images (the Jan.31 1576 Pratensis-to-Tycho letter ends p.24; the Feb.15 1576 Pratensis reply begins p.30) and confirmed against the volume's calibrated printed-page/pdf-page offset (_tboo_volumes.json, VII offset +14). Built a page-image slice (150 DPI) of pdf pages 39-44 and read all 5 letter pages (25-29) visually via the Read tool in two 4-page chunks, per FIDELITY_BRIEF chunking rule. Collated the full Latin source_text word-for-word against the images (opening/closing lines and every proper name, date, and number: PRATENSIS, DANZAeO, Sorae, Basileam, Herressuadij, Knudstorpij, undecimus/decimo Februarii, Ibstrup, unum milliare, Selandia/Scania, NICOLAUM PASSBERG, NICOLAUM KAASIUM, IOHANNEM SCHOUGARDIUM, STENO BILLE, Arce Helsingorensi, Insulam Huennam, Landskroniam, Friderigsburgensem, PLINIUS, VITRUVIUM, the 14 Februarii 1576 dateline) and found it complete and correct except the two TEXT_ERRORs logged. Also collated the full published_english translation against the (Dreyer-verified) Latin clause by clause: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found anywhere in this long letter — it is a complete and accurate rendering, including the direct-speech block and the technical Pliny/Vitruvius passage.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin prints the opening verb with a tripled C (ACCCEPI); Dreyer prints ACCEPI. A corrupted word; sense unaffected and the translation renders it correctly regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>ACCEPI literas tuas, eruditissime et amantissime PRATENSIS (TBOO VII p.25, opening word)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin has the present infinitive invisere where Dreyer prints the future-perfect invisero, the form the ubi-clause and following quod-clause require; a verb-form corruption. The English rendering happens to be correct either way, since English does not distinguish the two forms in this construction. Dreyer prints: <i>ubi vos denuo invisero, quod intra paucos dies facere decrevi (TBOO VII p.25, l.~11)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice PDF was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so neither defect could be verified against the edition of record by direct inspection. Both were nonetheless resolved on internal evidence internal to the Latin itself: 'ACCCEPI' is not a possible Latin spelling (accipio's perfect is invariably 'accepi'), and 'invisere' cannot stand as the finite verb of a 'ubi' clause, which requires 'invisero' given the surrounding future sense. Both fixes are low severity and do not affect the English, which was already correct in each case. Because collation against Dreyer's actual page image was not possible, this letter is graded B rather than A even though both defects are resolved.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15760131001", "next": "brahe-t_15760215001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15760131001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15760215001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Confirms the Rostock business was handled and reveals a secret plan to depart for Germany, asking Pratensis to share it only with the French Ambassador Dançay.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760214001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760215001", "d": "1576-02-15", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck": "Johannes Pratensis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Johannes Pratensis → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 15 February 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a long appended commentary by Tycho, incorporating Lucretian verse) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOHANNES PRATENSIS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>GENEROSE Domine TYCHO, Amice observande, prae gaudio exsilij, quam primum legi in tuis, ea quae de Serenissimi Regis nostri Clementissima voluntate refers, quae etiam Magnifico Domino Legato Gallico, communi Amico nostro, qui ex asse tuus est, mox legenda exhibui; is pari mecum laetitia inde perfusus est, et utrique nostrum Genius pariter inspiravit, haec non sine bonis ominibus fieri; judicavimusque ambo, imo certo statuimus, haec singulari Numinis provisu Regi in mentem venisse, ut te tam benigne et liberaliter ad manendum in patria, adeoque sua sponte invitaret, idque non levibus adductus rationibus. Neque etiam dubium quin serio hoc agat, et forsan plura animo premit, quam in continenti promere voluit; scis Regum petita esse postulata. Et sane decet te, imo debes, mi TYCHO, debes, inquam, in gratiam Optimi Regis et Patriae communis, cui nati sumus, et quae multum iuris in nos sibi vendicat, propositum tuum mutare, atque Regiae voluntati obtemperare. Habes brevibus quo DANZAeI et mea vergat sententia, plura et validiora intelliges ubi adfueris. Quam haec tuorum necessariorum et aliorum nobilium, qui te mallent politicum per omnia sequi splendorem, atque eadem agere quae ipsi, animos percellent, ubi intellexerint Regem tibi et tuis studijs tantâ clementiâ favere, ut ea benigne promovere, atque in hoc regno liberaliter sustentare velit, idque non rogatus, motu proprio, benignissime offerat. Quid nunc dicturi sunt proximi quique tui, quid frater, quid alij? scis quid loquar et quare. Sed desino plura, siquidem intra diem unum vel alterum, uti polliceris, huc venturus sis. Et sane oportet te reditum ad Regem maturare, atque quam citissime ad postulata eius (neque enim solummodo, uti prius dixi, petita sunt) respondere: nosti enim quam expedite Rex omnia vult fieri, quamque cito absque lenta procrastinatione. Adsis itaque nobis, exoptatissime TYCHO, hîc Haffniae bonis avibus, vel potius Deo duce, qui haec consilia, quae nunc animo volvimus, gubernet ac felicitet, atque eodem ductore, mox Regem accede, eiusque votis tua obsequentissime coniunge: sic enim stat sententia, sic vult Apollo, sic suadet Urania, sic Mercurius suo caduceo imperat. Num adhuc dubitas? sed pluribus de his, uti dixi, coram, nihilque ambigo, quin ubi DANZAeUM et me audiveris, manibus pedibusque in nostram iturus sis sententiam. Vale, et haec pauca, pro tempore, boni consule: volui potius pauca quam nulla scribere, ut de nostro quod expetis consilio eo citius aliquid cognosceres, sicque promptius tuum conformare possis, nec decuit hunc famulum, qui mihi tuas reddidit, absque responso reverti, quem vel in via te obviam habiturum, vel etiam Knudsdorpij itineri accinctum, reperturum existimo. De tuis vero, non est quod sis solicitus, eas in aliorum manus venturas, nam statim ac Domino Legato illas perlegeram, rupi et combussi, uti mandâras, nec ullus per nos ea de re quidpiam subolfaciet, antequam ipsemet haec propalari volueris.</p><p>Iamque iterum vale, salutata a me Matre tua Nobilissima et Fratre STENONE, quam officiosissime.<br>Datae Haffniae festinanti calamo die 15. Februarij horis matutinis Anno 1576.<br>Iohannes Pratensis.</p><p>In hunc modum Excellentissimus ille PRATENSIS, bonae recordationis, tam Magnifici Domini Legati Gallici, quam suam mihi apperuit sententiam, eandem postea cum simul essemus, pluribus exposuit et confirmavit; visumque est utrique eorum, ut omnino has a Clementissimo Rege oblatas conditiones susciperem, atque in Patria, relicto priori proposito, manerem , inque eius et Regis honorem, quaecunque pro virili praestare possem, sedulo molirer. Certo enim sibi persuadebant, haec, Deo sic volente et regente, qui corda Regum in sua habet potestate, fieri. Subiunxit vero inter alia PRATENSIS, domum plane Philosophicam in illa Insula exaedificandam esse, a vulgaribus differentem quaeque hinc inde habeat inscriptiones Gnomologicas, ex antiquorum Philosophorum et Poëtarum scriptis petitas: et sigillatim recitavit LUCRETIJ versus, qui in initio libri secundi habentur:</p><p>Suave, mari magno turbantibus aequora ventis,<br>E Terra alterius longum Spectare laborem.</p><p>Et quae sequuntur, usque in hos versus quos etiam memoravit:</p><p>At nil dulcius est, bene quam munita tenere<br>Edita doctrina sapientum templa serena,<br>Despicere unde queat alios, passimque videre<br>Errare, atque viam palantis quaerere vitae,<br>Certare ingenio, contendere nobilitate,<br>Ad Summas emergere opes, rerumque potiri.</p><p>Haec et similia parietibus domus intrinsecus appingenda esse aiebat, quorum etiam authorem et monstratorem futurum pollicebatur. Ego vero mox Regem Serenissimum, qui tunc Friderigsburgum reversus erat, accedens, eius Clementissimae voluntati me libenter acquieturum submisse indicavi. Atque mox, dum ibi adessem, conscriptae et subsignatae sunt literae, salarium annuum ex aerario Regio mihi deputantes, idque die 18. Februarij. Adjecta sunt sequentibus annis alia beneficia Regia, et liberaliter exercitijs nostris Astronomicis prospectum.</p><p>Extructa itaque est in eadem Insula Arx Uraniburgica, una cum ijs, quae huc requirebantur, et confecta ibique disposita sunt Instrumenta Astronomica varia et magnitudine atque materiae soliditate praepollentia, quemadmodum in libro nostro de Astronomiae Mechanica parte non saltem organa a me constructa, sed et ipsa aedificia, atque alia huc facientia exprimuntur. Quibus de causis Anno demum 21 a fundatione huius arcis, qui erat CHRISTI 1597, omnia isthic a me extructa deseruerim, atque in Germaniam me cum tota familia et apparatu illo Astronomico contulerim, non est huius loci referre, nimisque longum foret; habet etiam rationes cur lubentius id subticeam. Sed ad rem, et ea quae de PRATENSE. Interim dum ego aedificationes novas in Insula illa adorno, et ob id rarius Haffniam venio, accidit ut optimus et eruditissimus ille PRATENSIS, proh dolor, fatis concesserit, magno sui non saltem apud me, sed et alios quam plurimos, relicto defiderio; erat enim omnibus ob morum suavitatem, probitatem et candorem, eximiamque humanitatem charissimus. Vixit is saltem usque in 32 aetatis annum, adeoque sub ipso iuventutis flore expiravit, idque morte festina et inopinata. Cum enim aliquandiu continuis defluxionibus ad pulmones et tussi vehementi laborasset, tandem in auditorio Academiae Haffniensis, cum publice doceret, atque tunc ALCINOUM de Philosophia Platonica explicaret, de repente in eam, qua laboravit, incidens tussim, vena aliqua intrinseca illi rumpebatur, cumque sanguinem copiose per fauces effluentem, aqua frigida, quam studiosi mox attulerunt, ea nimirum in fauces manibus iniecta, sedari non posse animadvertit, mortemque instare sentiens, haec ultima verba prolocutus: “IESU FILI DEI VIVI, miserere mei, et suscipe animam meam”, mox exanimis manibus suorum auditorum exceptus, et in aedes suas cadaver cum lacrymis et luctu reportatum, atque paucis post diebus honorifice in templo summo Haffniae sepultum. Aberam ego tunc in Schania, neque satis cito de hoc dolendo casu resciscere potui ut sepulturae interessem. Sed nihilominus, aliquot post dies, cum ex dolore, quem ob immaturam et improvisam intimi huius mei amici mortem conceperam, me collegissem, Epitaphium, quod illi vivo, biennio antea promiseram (se enim non diu supervicturum ipsemet iam antea praesagiebat) confeci, quod etiam publice in templo laminae orichalcicae inauratae et eleganter circumquaque exornatae, quam in eos usus parâram, affigere constitueram, nisi alia quaedam ingrata intervenissent. At cum in huius praestantissimi viri, qui patre Gallo, quondam CHRISTIERNI secundi, Danorum Regis, postea captivi, Secretario in Cancellaria, uti intellexi, Gallica, matre vero Dana oriundus erat, et in omni Philosophiae genere excellenter versatus, mentionem hîc, datâ operâ, ut eius memoriam hoc loco recolerem, incidi, lubet hoc ipsum, quod ei tunc concinnavi Epicedium, hîc apponere, utut id extra propositum videbatur, sed quacunque tandem ratione eximij huius viri, meique dum in vivis esset, amici optimi, memoriam conservare, atque ad posteros transmittere licitum arbitror, praesertim cum et is Astronomiae studijs, de quibus hisce Epistolis affatim agitur, praeclare imbutus fuerit. Quae etiam omnia ob id hîc commemoro, ut constare possit meis lectoribus, quî factum sit, quod non plures in posterum ab hoc Doctissimo PRATENSE literae subsequantur, eo nimirum nimis matura morte, quod dolendum, nobis praerepto. Nunc igitur Epitaphium, de quo dixi, subijciam, posteaque ad alias Epistolas doctorum virorum aliunde mihi scriptas, continuandas pergam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES PRATENSIS TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Noble Master Tycho, friend to be honoured: leaping for joy, the moment I read in your letter what you report concerning the most clement disposition of our Most Serene King, I straightway laid it before the Magnificent Lord French Ambassador too — our common friend, who is yours through and through — to read; and he was filled with a gladness equal to mine, and to each of us our guardian spirit alike whispered that these things are not coming about without good omens. We judged, both of us — indeed we held it for certain — that by a singular foresight of the Divine Will this has entered the King's mind, that he should invite you, so kindly and so generously, and moreover of his own accord, to remain in your homeland, and that he was moved to it by no slight reasons. Nor is there any doubt that he is in earnest in this, and perhaps presses more in his mind than he chose to put forth on the spot; you know that what kings request are commands. And truly it becomes you — nay, you are bound, my Tycho, <i>bound</i>, I say — for the sake of the Best of Kings and of our common homeland, to which we were born and which claims much right over us, to change your design and to comply with the Royal will. You have here in brief which way Dançay's opinion and mine incline; you will learn more, and weightier, when you are present. How this will strike the minds of your kinsmen and of the other nobles, who would rather you pursued in all things the splendour of a public career and did the same things they do, when they have understood that the King favours you and your studies with such great clemency that he is willing kindly to advance them and to maintain them generously in this kingdom — and offers it most graciously, unasked, of his own initiative! What now will each of your nearest say, what your brother, what the rest? You know what I mean, and why. But I make an end of saying more, since within a day or two, as you promise, you will be coming here. And indeed you ought to hasten your return to the King, and to answer his demands as quickly as can be (for they are not merely requests, as I said before): for you know how promptly the King wishes everything to be done, and how speedily, without slow procrastination. Be with us, then, most longed-for Tycho, here in Copenhagen under good auspices — or rather, with God as our guide, who governs and prospers these plans we are now turning over in our minds — and under that same guidance, approach the King at once and join your wishes most obediently to his: for so the resolution stands, so Apollo wills, so Urania counsels, so Mercury commands with his caduceus. Do you still hesitate? But of these matters, as I said, more in person; and I have no doubt that, once you have heard Dançay and me, you will come over hand and foot to our opinion. Farewell, and for the present take these few lines in good part: I chose to write a little rather than nothing, so that you might learn something the sooner of the counsel of ours that you seek, and so might the more readily shape your own; nor was it fitting that this servant who delivered your letter to me should return without an answer — a man whom I reckon you will meet on the road coming toward you, or else find girt for the journey to Knudstrup. As for your letters, you need not be anxious that they will come into other hands; for as soon as I had read them through to the Lord Ambassador, I tore and burned them, as you had charged, and through us no one shall get wind of the matter before you yourself shall wish it made known.</p><p>And now again farewell — with my most dutiful greetings to your most noble Mother and to your brother Steen.<br>Given at Copenhagen, with hurrying pen, on the 15th day of February, in the morning hours, in the year 1576.<br>Johannes Pratensis.</p><p>In this manner that most excellent Pratensis, of good memory, opened to me both the Magnificent Lord French Ambassador's opinion and his own; and afterwards, when we were together, he set it forth and confirmed it at greater length. It seemed best to both of them that I should by all means take up the conditions offered by the Most Clement King, and remain in my homeland, abandoning my former design, and that in his honour and the King's I should diligently strive to perform whatever I could to the best of my power. For they were firmly persuaded that this was coming to pass by God's so willing and governing, who holds the hearts of kings in his power. Pratensis added, moreover, among other things, that a thoroughly Philosophical house should be built on that Island, differing from common ones, and having here and there sententious inscriptions [inscriptiones Gnomologicas], drawn from the writings of the ancient Philosophers and Poets; and he recited in particular the verses of Lucretius that stand at the beginning of the second book:</p><p>Sweet it is, when on the great sea the winds are stirring the waters,<br>to gaze from the land upon another's great labour;</p><p>and what follows, on to these verses, which he likewise recalled:</p><p>But nothing is sweeter than to hold the serene high sanctuaries,<br>well fortified by the teaching of the wise,<br>whence one may look down upon others and see them<br>wandering everywhere astray, and seeking the path of life as they stray —<br>contending in talent, striving over rank,<br>to rise to the highest wealth, and to grasp dominion.</p><p>These and the like, he said, ought to be painted on the inner walls of the house; and he promised that he himself would be the author and deviser of them. I, for my part, soon approached the Most Serene King, who had then returned to Frederiksborg, and submissively signified that I would gladly acquiesce in his most clement will. And presently, while I was there, letters were drawn up and subscribed assigning me a yearly salary from the Royal treasury — and that on the 18th day of February. In the following years other Royal benefactions were added, and provision was generously made for our Astronomical exercises.</p><p>And so on that same Island the Castle of Uraniborg was raised, together with the things required for the purpose, and various Astronomical Instruments were made and set up there, pre-eminent both in size and in solidity of material — just as in our book on the Mechanical part of Astronomy not only the instruments [organa] constructed by me, but the very buildings and the other things pertaining to it, are set forth. For what reasons, in the twenty-first year at last from the founding of this castle — which was the year of Christ 1597 — I forsook everything I had raised there, and betook myself into Germany with my whole household and that Astronomical apparatus, it is not the place here to relate, and it would be too long; there are reasons, too, why I more willingly pass it over in silence. But to the matter, and to what concerns Pratensis. Meanwhile, while I was adorning the new buildings on that Island, and on that account came more rarely to Copenhagen, it happened that that best and most learned Pratensis — alas! — yielded to the fates, leaving great longing for himself not only in me but in very many others as well; for he was most dear to all for the sweetness of his character, his uprightness and candour, and his exceptional kindness. He lived only into his thirty-second year, and so breathed his last in the very flower of youth, and that by a hasty and unlooked-for death. For when he had laboured for some while under continual fluxes upon the lungs and a violent cough, at last in the lecture-hall of the University of Copenhagen, while he was teaching publicly and was at that time expounding Alcinous on Platonic Philosophy, suddenly falling into the cough from which he suffered, some inner vein burst in him; and when he perceived that the blood flowing copiously through his throat could not be stanched by the cold water which the students at once brought — flung, that is, into his throat with their hands — and, feeling death at hand, he uttered these last words: \"JESUS, SON OF THE LIVING GOD, have mercy on me, and receive my soul\"; and at once, lifeless, he was caught in the hands of his hearers, and his body was carried back to his house with tears and mourning, and a few days afterward honourably buried in the cathedral church of Copenhagen. I was then away in Scania, and could not learn of this grievous event soon enough to be present at the burial. Yet nonetheless, some days afterward, when I had recovered myself from the grief I had conceived at the untimely and unforeseen death of this my most intimate friend, I composed an Epitaph which I had promised him while living, two years before (for he himself had already foreboded that he would not long survive); and I had resolved to affix it publicly in the church upon a gilded brass plate, elegantly adorned all round, which I had prepared for that use, had not certain other unwelcome matters intervened. But since I have here lighted — of set purpose, that I might revive his memory in this place — upon the mention of this most outstanding man, who was sprung of a French father (formerly, as I understand, Secretary in the French Chancery to Christian II, King of the Danes, afterward a captive) and a Danish mother, and was excellently versed in every kind of Philosophy, I am pleased to set down here this very Dirge [Epicedium] which I then composed for him — though it seemed to be beside my purpose; but by whatever means at last I judge it permissible to preserve the memory of this exceptional man, and my best friend while he was among the living, and to transmit it to posterity, especially since he too had been splendidly imbued with the studies of Astronomy, of which these Letters treat abundantly. And all this I commemorate here for this reason: that it may be plain to my readers how it came about that no further letters from this most learned Pratensis follow hereafter — he having been snatched from us, alas, by too early a death. Now therefore I shall append the Epitaph of which I spoke, and afterward shall proceed to continue with the other Letters of learned men written to me from elsewhere.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis / Hans Philipsen</b> (1543–1576), professor of medicine at the University of Copenhagen and one of Tycho's closest friends (protocol §5.6). This is the letter by which he and the French ambassador pressed Tycho to accept Frederik II's offer; Tycho's appended commentary records that he did, and recounts Pratensis's death in 1576 — the reason no later letters from him appear in the collection.</li><li><b>Editorial structure.</b> The file contains <b>two strata</b>: (1) Pratensis's actual letter of 15 Feb 1576 (down to the subscription \"Iohannes Pratensis\"); (2) a long retrospective commentary by <b>Tycho himself</b>, written when he assembled the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> (it speaks of abandoning Hven \"in the year of Christ 1597\" — see calendar note), narrating his acceptance of the King's offer, the founding of Uraniborg, the death of Pratensis, and his intention to append an epitaph. The Lucretius verses are quoted <i>within</i> Tycho's commentary as the inscriptions Pratensis proposed for the house.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimi Regis nostri</b></i> — <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), who in 1576 granted Tycho the island of Hven; the \"conditions offered\" are the lifelong grant that founded Uraniborg.</li><li><b><i>Magnifico Domino Legato Gallico … DANZAeI / DANZAeUM</b></i> — <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (Carolus Danzaeus, c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French resident ambassador at the Danish court and a humanist patron; here Tycho's and Pratensis's ally in urging him to stay. <i>DANZAeI</i> (genitive), <i>DANZAeUM</i> (accusative) are kept in the source block; the name is normalised to \"Dançay\" in the translation.</li><li><b><i>Fratre STENONE</b></i> — <b>Steen Brahe</b> (1547–1620), Tycho's younger brother; <i>Stenone</i> is the Latin ablative of <i>Steno</i> = Steen.</li><li><b><i>Matre tua Nobilissima</b></i> — Tycho's mother, <b>Beate Bille</b> (1526–1605).</li><li><b><i>Knudsdorpij</b></i> — <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp), the Brahe family seat in Scania (protocol §5.7); here the goal of the messenger's journey.</li><li><b><i>Schania</b></i> — <b>Scania</b> (Skåne), then Danish.</li><li><b><i>Friderigsburgum</b></i> — <b>Frederiksborg</b> (Hillerød), the royal castle to which Frederik II had returned.</li><li><b><i>Arx Uraniburgica</b></i> — the <b>Castle of Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>libro nostro de Astronomiae Mechanica parte</b></i> — Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (Wandsbek, 1598); <i>organa</i> = <b>instruments</b> (protocol §5.2).</li><li><b><i>Anno demum 21 a fundatione … qui erat CHRISTI 1597</b></i> — Tycho dates his abandonment of Hven to \"the 21st year from the founding,\" \"the year of Christ 1597.\" This dates the <b>commentary</b>, not the letter; reproduced as written, not reconciled with the conventional account of his final departure (he left Hven in 1597, then Denmark thereafter; the apparatus does not \"correct\" the count). Per protocol §3, no date is silently converted; the <i>letter's</i> dateline is 15 Feb 1576 (O.S.).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Letter dateline: <i>die 15. Februarij … Anno 1576</i> — <b>15 February 1576 (O.S.)</b>; the salary letters were subscribed <i>die 18. Februarij</i> (18 February 1576, O.S.). Denmark used the Julian calendar throughout. The header date is the letter's own dateline, 15 February 1576.</li><li><b>Classical / rhetorical flourishes.</b> <i>sic vult Apollo, sic suadet Urania, sic Mercurius suo caduceo imperat</i> — \"so Apollo wills, so Urania counsels, so Mercury commands with his caduceus\": a humanist piling-up of the god of prophecy, the <b>Muse of astronomy</b> (Urania — note the resonance with the future Uraniborg), and the herald-god, kept as figure. <i>manibus pedibusque</i> (\"hand and foot\") is a classical idiom for whole-hearted assent (Terence/Cicero), translated literally with the idiom preserved.</li><li><b><i>inscriptiones Gnomologicas</b></i> — \"sententious inscriptions\" (Gk. <i>gnōmologia</i>, collections of maxims/<i>gnomae</i>); the moralising mottoes Pratensis proposed for the walls. The term is glossed, not anglicised to a false cognate.</li><li><b>Lucretius.</b> The verses are <i>De rerum natura</i> <b>II.1–2</b> (<i>Suave, mari magno…</i>) and a six-line block beginning <i>At nil dulcius est…</i> (II.7–11 + II.13), quoted from Tycho's commentary as the proposed wall-inscriptions; kept as verse per protocol §4. <b>As transcribed, the second block runs II.7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 13 — Lucretius II.12 (<i>noctes atque dies niti praestante labore</i>, \"striving night and day with surpassing toil\") is <i>not present</i> in this source block</b>; the transcription jumps from <i>Certare ingenio, contendere nobilitate</i> (II.11) directly to <i>Ad Summas emergere opes, rerumque potiri</i> (II.13). The translation mirrors the source as transcribed and renders only the six lines given; nothing answering to the omitted II.12 (\"night and day\") is supplied (an earlier draft had imported it from the full Lucretian text — corrected). Whether II.12 dropped out in Tycho's quotation, in Dreyer, or in the working transcription is left open here, not silently restored. The translation renders the sense line-for-line without metre. Note the early-modern orthography retained in the source block (<i>Spectare</i>, <i>Edita</i>, <i>passimque</i>); <i>Edita … templa serena</i> = \"the serene high sanctuaries\" (Lucretius's <i>templa serena</i> of the wise).</li><li><b><i>ALCINOUM de Philosophia Platonica</b></i> — <b>Alcinous</b>, the <i>Didaskalikos</i> (Handbook of Platonism), then commonly ascribed to \"Alcinous\"; Pratensis died while lecturing on it.</li><li><b><i>patre Gallo … CHRISTIERNI secundi … Secretario in Cancellaria … Gallica</b></i> — Tycho records that Pratensis's father was a Frenchman who had been Secretary in the French Chancery to <b>Christian II</b> (r. 1513–23, deposed and long imprisoned: <i>postea captivi</i>, \"afterward a captive\"), and his mother Danish. <i>Christierni secundi</i> is the period Latin form of Christian II.</li><li><b><i>Epitaphium … laminae orichalcicae inauratae</b></i> — a verse epitaph on a <b>gilded brass plate</b> (<i>orichalcum</i>, brass/latten); <i>Epicedium</i> (\"Dirge\") is the funeral poem Tycho says he will append. The epitaph/epicedium text itself is <b>not present in this source block</b> — Tycho announces it (\"Now therefore I shall append the Epitaph…\") but it would follow as a separate item; flagged so the reader does not expect it here.</li><li><b>Greek/symbols.</b> No Greek or planetary/zodiac glyphs occur in this letter. The dying words <i>\"IESU FILI DEI VIVI…\"</i> are Latin (echoing the Jesus Prayer), kept and translated.</li><li><b>Transcription (adopted readings).</b> The working transcription (`sources/_letters_corpus.json`) carried four manifest OCR artifacts — stray-space and run-together errors with no counterpart in the printed Dreyer text: <i>manerem ,</i> (→ <i>manerem,</i>), <i>transm ittere</i> (→ <i>transmittere</i>), <i>conceperam,m e</i> (→ <i>conceperam, me</i>), and <i>prae sertim</i> (→ <i>praesertim</i>). These are silently restored to the correct printed forms in the Source block above (per protocol §1, the text-of-record is Dreyer, not the OCR working file); none affects sense. Flagged here rather than reproduced, since they are transcription noise, not features of the printed source.</li><li><b>Negations.</b> <i>non … venturas … in aliorum manus</i> / <i>rupi et combussi</i> — the assurance that the letters will <b>not</b> fall into others' hands because Pratensis tore and burned them: a single clear negation, reproduced exactly. <i>nec ullus … subolfaciet, antequam … volueris</i> — \"<b>no one</b> shall get wind of it before you wish it\": one negation, preserved.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clear humanist prose throughout, the register (effusive friendly urging, then Tycho's elegiac memorial) is matched, and the technical/onomastic points are secured by the controlled glossary and by the well-documented biography of Pratensis. The items flagged are not cruxes affecting sense: Tycho's own \"21st year / 1597\" count, reproduced unreconciled per §3; the announced epitaph/epicedium, which lies outside this source block; the <b>omission of Lucretius II.12</b> in the transcribed verse block (the translation now mirrors the six lines as given, with no imported \"night and day\"); and four manifest OCR artifacts in the working transcription, restored to the printed Dreyer readings and documented in the Transcription note. The translation matches the source as transcribed at every point.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 slice pages (TBOO VII pp. 29-33) as page images. P.29 is the tail of the preceding letter (Tycho to Pratensis, 14 Feb) and p.33 is the start of the following letter (Brucaeus to Tycho, 14 May) - the audited letter runs pp.30-32 in full, confirmed by matching source_text's opening ('GENEROSE Domine TYCHO...') and closing ('...continuandas pergam.') to the page images. Collated the opening and closing lines, the two Lucretius DRN II quotations verbatim, every proper name and place-name (Pratensis, Danzaeus/Dançay, the French Ambassador, Steen/Stenone, Frederiksborg, Knudstrup, Christian II, Uraniborg), every date (15 Feb 1576, 18 Feb, the 21st year/1597) and Pratensis's death narrative including the Latin deathbed quotation. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the published English tracks Dreyer's Latin closely including subject/pronoun assignment in the custody-of-letters passage.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pratensis", "count": 6, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15760214001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15760214001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Expresses joy at the king's gracious invitation for Tycho to remain in Denmark, interpreting it as divine providence, and urges Tycho to accept.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760215001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760514001", "d": "1576-05-14", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 14 May 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO CLARISSIMOQUE VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.</p><p>LITERIS tuis, nobilissime, amantissime Domine TYCHO, nihil mihi fuit charius. Ioachimicos 40 accepi a D. LEVINO. Chirographum adhuc apud me delitescit sine fraude et dolo. OMELIJ de Horologijs sciotericis partem, quam commodato ceperam, remisi, feceris mihi rem pergratam, si reliquam partem mittas. Detinueram apud me propositiones aliquot Graece conscriptas, has, ne quicquam desideres, remitto. Quod vero scribis te in observationes coelestium motuum sedulo incubiturum, non possum non probare, cum animum sis consecutus ad ea studia sua sponte inflammatum, et qui laboribus non deterrentur, quique in rebus praeclaris et a communi ingeniorum captu semotis laudabile iudicat studium suum posuisse. Utinam ipsum coelum non plane oppugnet tuos labores, aptius ad p[rui]narum, imbrium, nivium, grandium, Septentrionis, Cori Boreaeque flatium, et aliarum, quae in sublimi fiunt, impressionum considerationem. Vulcano accommodata magis sedes, cui materiam copiosam suppeditat, in qua se exerceat. His lapis ille multis decantatus plerisque omnibus frustra quaesitus investigandus potius, quam in coelo, quod raro in oculos incurrit, siderum motus perscrutandi. Nos hîc te expectamus, nec sine libris aliquot non passim obvijs. Si quos habeas IORDANI aut REGIOMONTANI nondum editos, Norimbergae a te emptos unâ tecum afferas. Quod si COPERNICUM WOLPHGANG piae memoriae, nactus es, hic sit tantum itineris comes. Ita enim iudico, Regem in Germaniam iter instituentem comitaberis, cuius beneficio, quod felix et faustum sit, intelligo, te Dominum Insulae factum, e qua quaquaversum, montibus non objectis, coelum prospicere liceat: Macte animo, quo tua te virtus vocat. Nos curta supellex et res angusta domi hactenus humi depressos detinuit, brevi forte emersuros. Cum apud nos esses, petij, ut quae in morbo caduco tibi essent nota, de usu medicamentorum ad affectum facientium, communicares, id hunc ob repentinum discessum minime licuit. Hoc si nunc feceris, plurimum me tibi devinxeris. Cum autem ad tres fere differentias possit deduci, quando scilicet ipsum cerebrum primario laborat, aut aliquod viscus, ut uterus, ventriculus, lien, et si quod sit aliud, aut e partibus remotioribus, ut manibus pedibusve, aura quaedam caput petit. Peterem in singulis propriam rationem, si quae sit, aut universalem omnibus, si unus est in omnibus curandi modus. Aut quid omnibus commune, quid singulis peculiare. Non dubito pro animi tui candore et amore mutuo, candide omnia communicaturum.</p><p>Bene vale.<br>Rostochij XIV Maij anno 1576.<br>Henricus Brucaeus<br>plane tuus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and illustrious gentleman, Master Tycho Brahe, distinguished mathematician, his lord and friend to be regarded with respect.</b></p><p>Nothing has been dearer to me than your letter, most noble and most beloved Master Tycho. I received 40 Joachimstal coins [Ioachimicos] from Master Levinus. The autograph [chirographum] still lies hidden with me, without fraud or guile. I have sent back the part of Oemel's <i>On Sundials</i> [De Horologiis sciotericis] which I had taken on loan; you will do me a most welcome favour if you send the remaining part. I had been keeping with me some propositions written out in Greek; these — so that you may lack for nothing — I send back. But as for your writing that you mean to apply yourself diligently to observations of the celestial motions, I cannot but approve, since you have attained a disposition kindled of its own accord toward those studies — [a disposition] of those who are not deterred by hardships, and who judge it praiseworthy to have invested their pursuit in things excellent and set apart from the common reach of [ordinary] minds. Would that the very sky did not flatly oppose your labours — [a sky] better suited to the consideration of hoarfrosts [p[rui]narum], rains, snows, hailstorms, and of the North wind, the Caurus, and the Boreas's blasts, and of the other impressions that come to be on high. A seat more fitted to Vulcan, to whom it supplies abundant matter on which to exercise himself. In these [matters] that stone so much sung-of by many, [yet] sought in vain by very nearly all, is rather to be investigated, than the motions of the stars to be searched out in a sky that rarely meets the eye. We await you here, and not without some books that are not everywhere to be come by. If you have any [works] of Jordanus or Regiomontanus not yet published, bought by you at Nuremberg, bring them along with you. And if you have got hold of the Copernicus of Wolfgang of pious memory, let this only be a companion for the journey. For thus I judge: you will accompany the King as he sets out on a journey into Germany — by whose benefaction (which may it be happy and auspicious) I understand you have been made Lord of an Island, from which, with no mountains set in the way, one may look out upon the sky in every direction. Go on with good heart, whither your virtue calls you! As for us, scant furniture and straitened means at home have hitherto held us pressed to the ground — though perhaps soon to emerge. When you were with us, I asked that you would share what things were known to you in the falling sickness [morbus caducus], concerning the use of medicaments that act upon the affliction; this, owing to your sudden departure, was by no means possible. If you do this now, you will bind me very greatly to you. Now since [the disease] can be reduced to roughly three distinctions — namely, when the brain itself is primarily affected; or some viscus, such as the womb, the stomach, the spleen, and if there be any other; or when from the more remote parts, such as the hands or feet, a certain vapour [aura] makes for the head — I would ask [you to give] in each case its own method [of treatment], if there is one, or a universal one for all, if there is a single mode of curing in all [cases]; or what is common to all, what peculiar to each. I do not doubt, given the candour of your mind and our mutual affection, that you will share everything candidly.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Rostock, 14 May, the year 1576.<br>Heinrich Brucaeus,<br>wholly yours.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, where Tycho had studied (1566–68); a long-standing scholarly friend. The letter belongs to the spring of 1576, in the months immediately after the royal grant of Hven (Brucaeus has just heard of it — see below).</li><li><b>Recipient line.</b> The dataset's `recipient` field doubles the name (\"Tycho Brahe Tycho Brahe\"); a data artefact, not in the source. The source address-line is the single <i>D. TYCHONI BRAHE</i>.</li><li><b><i>Ioachimicos</b></i> — \"the Joachimsthalers / Joachimstal coins\": <i>Ioachimici</i> (sc. <i>nummi/thaleri</i>) are the silver coins struck at Joachimsthal (Jáchymov), the original \"thaler.\" Most likely actual coins forwarded via <i>D. Levinus</i> (an unidentified intermediary, \"Master Levinus\"), though the word in a numismatically-minded humanist context can mean coin-specimens of interest. Rendered \"Joachimstal coins\" with the Latin kept; referent flagged.</li><li><b><i>Chirographum</b></i> — \"autograph / hand-written document\" (something in Tycho's own hand that Brucaeus is holding for him); <i>sine fraude et dolo</i> is the legal-formula assurance \"without fraud or guile,\" i.e. safely kept.</li><li><b><i>OMELIJ de Horologijs sciotericis</b></i> — a treatise <i>On Sundials</i> (<i>horologia scioterica</i> = shadow-clocks, sundials) by <b>Oemel/Omelius</b>. The author is not securely identifiable from the name-form alone; rendered \"Oemel's <i>On Sundials</i>\" and the attribution left as written. <i>Sciotericum</i> (&lt; Gk σκιοθηρικόν) = \"shadow-catching,\" the standard term for a sundial.</li><li><b><i>propositiones … Graece conscriptas</b></i> — \"propositions written out in Greek\": mathematical/geometrical propositions in Greek that Brucaeus had borrowed and now returns. (No Greek glyphs appear in the source text itself.)</li><li><b>The meteorological clause — Brucaeus's scepticism.</b> <i>Utinam ipsum coelum non plane oppugnet tuos labores, aptius ad p[rui]narum … impressionum considerationem</i> — the rhetorical heart of the letter: Brucaeus warns that the northern sky is so cloud-ridden that it is fitter for studying weather (<i>impressiones</i> = meteorological \"impressions,\" the sublunary phenomena of Aristotelian <i>Meteorologica</i>) than the stars. <b>Editorial reading flagged:</b> the source prints <i>p[rui]narum</i> with the middle supplied in brackets by the edition; <i>pruinae</i> = \"hoarfrosts,\" which fits the list of frost/rain/snow/hail. The bracketed supply is kept in the Source block and adopted in the translation per protocol.</li><li><b>Wind-names.</b> <i>Septentrionis, Cori Boreaeque flatium</i> — <i>Septentrio</i> = the North (wind); <i>Corus/Caurus</i> = the NW wind; <i>Boreas</i> = the N/NE wind. Kept as classical wind-names, glossed.</li><li><b><i>Vulcano … lapis ille multis decantatus</b></i> — \"a seat more fitted to Vulcan … that stone so much sung-of\": Brucaeus pivots to alchemy. <i>Vulcanus</i> = the fire/forge, i.e. alchemical work; <i>lapis ille multis decantatus … frustra quaesitus</i> = the <b>philosophers' stone</b> (\"that stone so often sung of [by many], sought in vain by nearly all\"). Brucaeus playfully advises that the cloudy North is better for the furnace than for the heavens. <i>decantatus</i> carries a faint irony (\"much harped-on\") preserved rather than flattened.</li><li><b>Books sought.</b> <i>IORDANI aut REGIOMONTANI nondum editos, Norimbergae a te emptos</i> — unpublished works of <b>Jordanus (de Nemore)</b> or <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller of Königsberg, 1436–76) that Tycho had bought at <b>Nuremberg</b>. <i>COPERNICUM WOLPHGANG piae memoriae</i> — \"the Copernicus of Wolfgang of pious memory,\" i.e. a copy of Copernicus (almost certainly <i>De revolutionibus</i>) once owned by a deceased <b>Wolfgang</b> (unidentified; \"of pious memory\" marks him as recently dead). Brucaeus asks Tycho to bring these to Rostock. Wolfgang's identity is a minor crux, left unresolved.</li><li><b>The grant of Hven.</b> <i>Regem in Germaniam iter instituentem comitaberis, cuius beneficio … te Dominum Insulae factum</i> — \"you will accompany the King [Frederik II] … by whose benefaction … you have been made Lord of an Island.\" This is <b>Hven</b> (Ven), granted to Tycho by <b>Frederik II</b> on 23 May 1576 — so this letter (14 May 1576) registers the news as just received / imminent, a valuable external date-fix on the grant. <i>e qua quaquaversum, montibus non objectis, coelum prospicere liceat</i> = \"from which, with no mountains in the way, one may view the sky in every direction\" — Brucaeus already grasps the island's observational virtue. <i>Macte animo</i> = \"Go on with good heart / well done, take courage,\" a classical encouragement formula.</li><li><b><i>curta supellex … res angusta domi</b></i> — \"scant furniture … straitened means at home\": <i>res angusta domi</i> is a Juvenalian tag (<i>Sat.</i> 3.164–5) for poverty; Brucaeus pleads his own narrow circumstances. <i>humi depressos … brevi forte emersuros</i> = \"pressed to the ground … perhaps soon to emerge.\"</li><li><b>Medicine — the <i>morbus caducus</i> query.</b> <i>morbus caducus</i> = the <b>falling sickness (epilepsy)</b>, rendered with the period term glossed. Brucaeus asks for Tycho's knowledge of <i>medicamenta ad affectum facientia</i> (\"medicaments that act upon the affliction\" — i.e. remedies effective against the condition; <i>affectus</i> = the disease-state). He lays out a threefold aetiological scheme drawn from Galenic medicine: epilepsy arising (1) primarily in the <b>brain</b> (<i>cerebrum</i>); (2) by sympathy from a <b>viscus</b> — <i>uterus</i> (womb), <i>ventriculus</i> (stomach), <i>lien</i> (spleen); or (3) from the <b>remote parts</b> (hands/feet), whence <i>aura quaedam caput petit</i> — \"a certain vapour makes for the head\" (the classical <i>aura epileptica</i>, the rising sensation preceding a fit). <i>aura</i> kept and glossed. He asks whether each type has its <b>own</b> treatment (<i>propria ratio</i>) or there is a <b>universal</b> mode (<i>universalis omnibus … modus</i>) — <i>universalis</i> rendered \"universal\" per glossary §5.1.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Rostochij XIV Maij anno 1576</i> = \"<b>Rostock</b>, 14 May 1576\" (O.S.; Denmark and the Empire alike pre-Gregorian in 1576). The dateline agrees with the dataset label (<i>1576. 14. maj. Rostock</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Bene vale</i> → \"Fare you well\" (§5.5); <i>plane tuus</i> → \"wholly yours.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: restored the dropped numeral, 'Ioachimicos accepi' corrected to 'Ioachimicos 40 accepi', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p.33. English: added '40' to name the sum of Joachimsthaler coins received.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the sense is not in doubt at any load-bearing point. Soft points, all flagged rather than smoothed: the editorial supply <i>p[rui]narum</i> (adopted); the precise referent of <i>Ioachicos</i> (coins vs. coin-specimens) and of <i>Chirographum</i>; the unidentified persons <i>Levinus</i>, <i>Oemel/Omelius</i> (author of the sundial treatise), and <i>Wolfgang</i> (former owner of the Copernicus).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.32-35 as page images (letter proper runs pp.33-34; p.32 is the tail of the preceding Pratensis letter, p.35 the start of the following Danzeus letter). Collated the full Brucaeus-to-Tycho Latin word for word against Dreyer: opening/closing formulas, all proper names (Levinus, Oemel, Jordanus, Regiomontanus, Wolfgang/Copernicus), and the falling-sickness passage all match exactly except the dropped sum. Because a specific monetary figure is missing from source_text relative to Dreyer, this is a TEXT_ERROR and forces grade C even though the English itself invents nothing.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's page clearly prints the sum '40' (forty Joachimsthalers received from Levinus); our source_text silently drops the numeral, and the published English consequently gives no figure at all for a sum of money the brief specifically flags as a collation priority. Dreyer prints: <i>Ioachimicos 40 accepi a D. LEVINO. (TBOO VII p.33, line 6)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp.32-35 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect (flagged medium severity) was confirmed exactly as flagged; both the Latin and its dependent English clause were corrected.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15761208001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Returns borrowed Greek propositions, mentions forwarding a sundials manuscript, and approves Tycho's resolve to devote himself to celestial observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760514001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760626001", "d": "1576-06-26", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Charles de Dançay", "ck": "Charles de Dançay", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Charles de Dançay → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 26 June 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILI ET GENEROSO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE A KNUDSTORP, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.</p><p>GENEROSE et clarissime vir, Domine et amice perpetuo colende, recordari mei PRATENSIS sine lacrymis non possum, cum animae plus quam dimidium perdiderim meae. At cum dolor iste nobis communis sit, ego frustra me affligendo tibi molestus esse desinam, et tuis literis respondebo. Quae certe mihi fuerunt gratissimae, et plurimum profuerunt. Nam illae me veterno quodam oppressum excitarunt, et quasi in tenebris lumine privatum errantem in rectam reduxerunt viam, ut ea, quae me decent, praestarem. Scias igitur, mi amantissime TYCHO, lapidem angularem paratum esse, sed cum inscriptionem seu dedicationem, et quaedam alia mystica sacraque ille optimae recordationis PRATENSIS sibi reservasset, iurareque voluisset, ego mihi (quod tantus vir facile conficere potuisset) tribuere non ausus sum, ut quicquam in ijs, nisi te mandante, vel potius praescribente, tentare voluerim. Te igitur oro, si Mercurium tibi caducei usuram negare non velis (illius enim verbis lubenter utor) ut, quod tui studiosissimus in se receperat, et mihi iure quodam debebat, tute perficias et persolvas, vel ego cogar (etsi invitus) nescio quid in te desiderare. Ut autem hac in re facilior promptiorque sis et mihi citius respondeas, ne magnum illud opus nostra mora et tua culpa remoretur, oro ut ijs, quae in schedula scripsi, addas, immutes, subtrahas, vel ut uno verbo omnia complectar, una litura deleas, et statuas, quid sit faciendum, quid scribendum, cum id facile efficere queas, et ego tibi obsequar lubentissime. Quod si me tui amantissimum esse et observantissimum credideris, non falleris.</p><p>Vale, Vir praestantissime, et cito rescribe.<br>Haffniae 26. die Iunij Anno 1576.<br>Tibi deditissimus amicus<br>Carolus Danzeus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and high-born gentleman Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, his most esteemed friend.</b></p><p>High-born and most renowned Sir, my Lord and ever-to-be-esteemed friend: I cannot call to mind my dear Pratensis without tears, since I have lost more than the half of my own soul. But since that grief is shared between us, I shall cease to be troublesome to you by afflicting myself in vain, and I shall reply to your letter — which was assuredly most welcome to me, and profited me greatly. For it roused me, oppressed as I was by a kind of lethargy, and, as one wandering in darkness deprived of light, led me back into the right road, that I might perform the things which befit me. Know then, my most beloved Tycho, that the cornerstone is ready; but since that Pratensis of most excellent memory had reserved to himself the inscription, or dedication, and certain other secret and sacred matters, and had wished to take an oath upon it, I have not dared to take upon myself (a thing which so great a man could easily have accomplished) the attempting of anything in these matters, except at your bidding, or rather your prescription. I beg you, therefore — if you will not refuse Mercury the loan of his herald's-staff for your use (for I gladly use his words) — that what one most devoted to you had taken upon himself, and by a certain right owed to me, you yourself may carry through and discharge; or else I shall be compelled (though unwilling) to find some lack in you, I know not what. And so that you may be the more accommodating and ready in this matter and reply to me the sooner — lest that great work be delayed by our procrastination and your fault — I beg that to the things I have written on the slip you add, alter, take away, or (to embrace all in one word) blot out with a single stroke of the pen, and determine what is to be done, what is to be written, since you can easily bring it about, and I shall comply with you most gladly. And if you will believe me to be most loving and most respectful of you, you will not be deceived.</p><p>Farewell, most excellent Sir, and write back quickly.<br>At Copenhagen, the 26th day of June, the year 1576.<br>Your most devoted friend,<br>Charles de Dançay.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Carolus Danzeus</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French resident ambassador at the Danish court of Frederik II, a humanist and patron of learning and one of Tycho's older friends and protectors during the Uraniborg years. He signs <i>Tibi deditissimus amicus</i> (\"Your most devoted friend\").</li><li><b>Addressee superscription.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHE A KNUDSTORP</i> — \"Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup,\" naming the family seat (Knutstorp in Scania); kept per protocol §5.7.</li><li><b><i>Pratensis</b></i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), professor of medicine at Copenhagen and Tycho's intimate physician-friend (protocol §5.6). Dançay's opening lament — <i>animae plus quam dimidium perdiderim meae</i> (\"I have lost more than the half of my own soul\") — and the phrase <i>ille optimae recordationis Pratensis</i> (\"that Pratensis of most excellent memory\") place the letter shortly after Pratensis's death (he died in May 1576); the <i>dolor … nobis communis</i> (\"grief shared between us\") is the bereavement Dançay and Tycho hold in common. The Horatian echo (<i>animae dimidium meae</i>, Horace, <i>Odes</i> I.3.8) is preserved in the literal rendering.</li><li><b><i>lapis angularis</i> (\"the cornerstone\").</b> The foundation/cornerstone of <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-castle on Hven, whose building was begun in 1576; the cornerstone was laid on 8 August 1576. <i>paratum esse</i> = \"is ready / prepared.\" The letter shows Dançay overseeing the preparation of the stone in Copenhagen and awaiting from Tycho the wording of its dedicatory inscription. <b>Crux flagged:</b> the Latin says only <i>lapidem angularem paratum esse</i> — that the stone is <i>ready/prepared</i>, not that it is laid; no completed-construction sense is read in. Nothing beyond the as-printed claim is asserted.</li><li><b><i>inscriptionem seu dedicationem, et quaedam alia mystica sacraque</b></i> — \"the inscription, or dedication, and certain other secret and sacred matters.\" Pratensis had reserved to himself (<i>sibi reservasset</i>) the composing of the cornerstone's dedicatory inscription and certain undisclosed \"mystic and sacred\" elements, and had wished to bind it by oath (<i>iurareque voluisset</i>); with his death, Dançay asks Tycho himself to supply the wording. <i>mystica sacraque</i> is left as \"secret and sacred,\" not narrowed to a specific rite, since the text does not specify.</li><li><b><i>Mercurium … caducei usuram</i> (\"Mercury … the loan of his herald's-staff\").</b> A learned conceit: Dançay asks Tycho to lend the eloquence of <b>Mercury</b> (god of messengers and of letters) — i.e. to compose the inscription's words. <i>caduceus</i> = Mercury's herald's-staff (caduceus); <i>usuram … negare</i> = \"to refuse the use/loan of.\" The parenthesis <i>illius enim verbis lubenter utor</i> (\"for I gladly use his words\") signals that the conceit is Dançay's playful borrowing of Mercurial idiom; kept as figure, not flattened.</li><li><b><i>quod … in se receperat, et mihi iure quodam debebat</b></i> — \"what [Pratensis], most devoted to you, had taken upon himself, and by a certain right owed to me.\" The subject of <i>receperat</i> / <i>debebat</i> is the late Pratensis, who had undertaken the task; Dançay now presses Tycho to \"carry it through and discharge\" (<i>perficias et persolvas</i>) the obligation in his stead. Read with care: the hedged <i>nescio quid in te desiderare</i> (\"to find some lack in you, I know not what\") is a courteous, deliberately vague half-threat, preserved with its qualifier (<i>etsi invitus</i>, \"though unwilling\").</li><li><b><i>schedula</b></i> — \"the slip / note\": a small sheet (the draft text Dançay enclosed) that he invites Tycho to amend; <i>una litura deleas</i> = \"blot [it] out with a single stroke of the pen,\" the standard idiom for editorial deletion.</li><li><b><i>magnum illud opus</b></i> — \"that great work\": the building of Uraniborg, of which the cornerstone is the present concern.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The letter contains the conditional courtesy <i>si … negare non velis</i> (\"if you will not refuse\"), the modesty topos <i>tribuere non ausus sum</i> (\"I have not dared to take upon myself\"), and the closing <i>non falleris</i> (\"you will not be deceived\"); each negation is reproduced, none added or dropped. The <i>nisi te mandante, vel potius praescribente</i> (\"except at your bidding, or rather your prescription\") is an exceptive, not a negation, and is rendered as such.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Haffniae 26. die Iunij Anno 1576</i> = \"At <b>Copenhagen</b> (Hafnia), the 26th day of June, the year 1576\" (O.S.; Denmark used the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the dataset label.</li><li><b>MS witnesses.</b> Two copies in Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686 (item .7 = A; item .66 = B), as given in the source header; shelfmarks reproduced as supplied.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the prosopography is firm; the soft points, flagged rather than smoothed, are (1) the exact referent and status of the <i>lapis angularis</i> (prepared, not asserted as laid) and the undisclosed <i>mystica sacra</i> reserved by Pratensis, and (2) the deliberately vague force of <i>nescio quid in te desiderare</i> as a courteous half-reproach.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the soft points are faithfully-preserved source vagueness (</i>lapis<i> status, </i>nescio quid<i>), not cruxes; sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.34-35 as page images. Letter 'NOBILI ET GENEROSO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE A KNUDSTORP...' begins and ends entirely on p.34 (Haffniae 26 Iunij 1576, Carolus Danzeus). source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim, word for word. Collated opening/closing, the classical allusions (Pratensis, the cornerstone/dedication, the Mercury/caduceus figure), and every clause; all rendered accurately. One classically dense, allusive clause (the Mercury/caduceus figure) is loosely but not incorrectly rendered — judged within the elegance/register latitude the brief instructs to ignore, not a material mistranslation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Charles de Dançay", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15760714001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790705002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Mourns the death of Pratensis and asks Tycho's guidance on a dedication cornerstone that Pratensis had reserved to himself before his death.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760626001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760714001", "d": "1576-07-14", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Charles de Dançay", "ck": "Charles de Dançay", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Holbæk", "t": "Charles de Dançay → Tycho Brahe — Holbæk, 14 July 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ET VERE NOBILI VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE A KNUDSTORP, AMICO SUO SEMPER OBSERVANDO.</p><p>GENEROSE et clarissime vir, amice perpetuo colende, cum hîc Holbecae apud Dominum PACKIUM essem, redditae mihi sunt tuae literae. Tristia obmittam, cum corrigi non possint: lapis promissus paratus est, inscriptio addita, ut voluisti, cum me vocabis, mature adero, gratissimumque mihi feceris, si plures invitaveris! Ego idem quoque facturus sum, ne vulgaris haec sit dedicatio. Utinam ille PRATENSIS viveret et nobis unâ adesse posset. Ego D. IOHANNI FRANCISCO nisi adfuerit, ominatus sum aliquid mali. Ille igitur volens (ut spero) vel metu aderit. Literas tuas hîc expecto, ubi iam iucundissime et suavissime vivo. Vale, Vir praestantissime, et DANZEUM tuum, ut incepisti, ama, credeque illum tibi esse deditissimum.</p><p>Vale et vale felicissime.<br>Holbecae 14. Iulij A. 1576.<br>Tibi deditissimus Carolus Danzeus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the high-born and truly noble man Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, his ever-to-be-honoured friend.</b></p><p>High-born and most illustrious man, friend forever to be cherished: while I was here at Holbæk with Master Pack, your letter was delivered to me. The sad matters I shall pass over, since they cannot be put right. The promised stone is ready, the inscription added as you wished; when you summon me, I shall be there in good time, and you will do me a most welcome favour if you invite more people! I too shall do the same, so that this dedication may not be a common one. Would that the late Pratensis were living and could be present with us together. As for Master Iohannes Franciscus, unless he is present, I have foreboded some ill. He therefore — willingly (as I hope), or else out of fear — will attend. I await your letter here, where I am now living most agreeably and most pleasantly. Farewell, most excellent man, and love your Dançay, as you have begun [to do], and believe him to be most devoted to you.</p><p>Farewell, and most happily farewell.<br>At Holbæk, 14 July, the year 1576.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Charles de Dançay.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Carolus Danzeus</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French ambassador (resident) at the Danish court of Frederik II. A humanist and a steady patron-friend of Tycho's from the early 1570s; he was among the small circle invited to the ceremonies attending the founding of Uraniborg. The address-form <i>Tychoni Brahe a Knudstorp</i> (\"Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup,\" the family seat in Scania) is the standard noble style; <i>Danzeum tuum</i> (\"your Dançay\") is the writer's affectionate self-reference, rendered in the third person as in the Latin.</li><li><b>Place / occasion.</b> <i>Holbecae apud Dominum Packium</i> — at <b>Holbæk</b> (the Zealand market town), staying <i>apud Dominum Packium</i>, \"with Master Pack\" — i.e. as the guest of a host surnamed <b>Pack/Packe</b> (the Latin genitive <i>Packii</i> gives a nominative <i>Packius</i>; the man is otherwise unidentified here and is left as named). The <i>lapis … inscriptio addita … dedicatio</i> (\"the stone … the inscription added … this dedication\") almost certainly concerns the <b>foundation/dedication stone of Uraniborg</b>, whose cornerstone was laid on Hven on 8 August 1576 (O.S.); Dançay reports the inscribed stone ready and pledges to attend the ceremony and to bring guests so that it \"not be a common dedication.\" This reading is consistent with the date (mid-July 1576, weeks before the laying) but rests on the historical context rather than on any word in the letter; flagged accordingly. <i>(Tycho's own commemorative verses for the Uraniborg cornerstone survive; whether the </i>inscriptio<i> here is that text or a separate dedicatory inscription the letter does not say — left open.)</i></li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), professor of medicine at Copenhagen and Tycho's close physician-friend. <i>Utinam ille Pratensis viveret</i> (\"would that the late Pratensis were living\") shows him already dead at the time of writing — Pratensis died in 1576 — so the wish is for a departed friend; <i>ille</i> + the past-counterfactual subjunctive carry the sense \"the late,\" made explicit in brackets-free idiom here (\"the late Pratensis\") and noted.</li><li><i>D. Iohannes Franciscus</i> = \"Master Iohannes Franciscus\" — an associate (Italian first-name form <i>Giovanni Francesco</i>) expected at the same gathering; not further identified in the letter and left as named. The clause <i>nisi adfuerit, ominatus sum aliquid mali … vel metu aderit</i> is playful: \"unless he attends I have foreboded some ill [for him]; so he will attend, willingly or out of fear.\" The threat is jocular, not literal.</li><li><b>Negations (checked).</b> Two negative constructions: (1) <i>cum corrigi non possint</i> — \"since they cannot be put right\" (one <i>non</i>); (2) <i>ne vulgaris haec sit dedicatio</i> — the purpose-negative <i>ne</i>, \"so that this dedication may not be a common one\"; and the conditional <i>nisi adfuerit</i> — \"unless he is present.\" Each is reproduced once; none added or dropped.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Heading <i>GENEROSO ET VERE NOBILI VIRO … AMICO SUO SEMPER OBSERVANDO</i> — the honorific superscription, rendered in full. Closing <i>Vale et vale felicissime</i> (\"Farewell, and most happily farewell\") and <i>Tibi deditissimus</i> (\"Most devoted to you,\" parallel to Wolf's <i>E. Tuae studiosiss.</i>) per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Holbecae 14. Iulij A. 1576</i> = \"At Holbæk, 14 July, the year 1576\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the dataset label (<i>1576. 14. juli. Holbaek</i>); no discrepancy. (The dataset's note that MS witness B is also the witness for the 1571 Wolf letter, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, reflects shared-codex foliation, not a dating issue.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched; the substantive open point is the <i>referent</i> of the <i>lapis/inscriptio/dedicatio</i> (taken here, on historical grounds, to be the Uraniborg foundation-stone dedication of August 1576, but not named as such in the text) and the unidentified persons <i>Packius</i> and <i>Iohannes Franciscus</i>, all flagged rather than asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII p.35, where it is printed complete (a following, unrelated letter to Tycho begins on p.36). Latin matches source_text exactly. The gravestone/inscription arrangement, the wish that the late Pratensis were alive, the foreboding about Iohannes Franciscus, and the date (Holbæk, 14 July 1576) are all rendered accurately. No defects found. Note: published_grade was B; this collates clean at A and should be upgraded.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Charles de Dançay", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15760626001", "next": "brahe-t_15761117001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790705002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Confirms a dedication stone is ready with the requested inscription and urges Tycho to summon him and invite guests for the ceremony.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760714001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760802001", "d": "1576-08-02", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Johannes Major", "ck": "Johannes Major", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Johannes Major → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 2 August 1576 [O.S.] (catalogued 2 August 1576; dated \"1 August 1576\" by Mosley)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, DOCTRINA ET VIRTUTE PRAeSTANTISSIMO DN. TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO SUMMA OBSERVANTIA COLENDO. S.</p><p>BENEVOLENTIAe tuae erga me singularis, generose Domine, testimonium sunt non solum tui sermones suavissimi, quibus hîc liberaliter sum institutus, sed etiam liber tuus de Stella Nova editus, quem mihi dono mittere non dubitasti. Quare quamprimum eum a D. WOLFIO, Compatre meo charissimo, accepi, prima quacunque occasione oblata ad te gratitudinis ergo scribendum duxi, praesertim cum id meus in te amor et singularis observantia, qua te semper sum prosecutus, flagitaret, ne aut spacio temporis aut locorum intervallis diminuta esse videretur. Dominationi igitur tuae primum de salvo atque incolumi in patriam reditu gratulor, eamque incolumitatem tibi perpetuam esse precor. Deinde pro libro tibi gratias, quas possum, maximas ago. Etsi autem ea non est mei ingenij facultas, ut parem gratiam referre possim, tamen affirmo, me omne meum studium, omnem meam observantiam cultum honorem denique tibi dicasse, meque tuarum maximarum virtutum, quas semper sum admiratus, memoriam perpetuo conservaturum esse. Porro te ignorare nolo, mi Domine TYCHO, SCHISLERUM γνωμονοποιὸν nostrum confecisse Globum ex cupro fabrefactum secundum omnes dimensiones perfectae, veluti tuum Automaton, rotunditatis, cuius diameter quinquepedalis. Globus autem tali arte est fabrefactus, ut in 8 partes dissolvi et rursus in integrum restitui possit, necessitate exigente, quem in Hispaniam unâ cum Astrolabio quantitatis eiusdem per MARCUM FUGGERUM est misurus. Cum itaque viderem Globum, de tuis commodis cogitans quaerebam, num etiam maiorem conficere posset? qui respondit ita, seque ad tale corpus conficiendum motum esse ex eo, quod audivisset D. tuam hîc artificem quaesivisse, qui id praestaret, seque posse aeque conficere eiusmodi corpus, cuius diameter esset 8 vel 10 pedum, et ea arte, ut in 40 circiter partes resolutus facilime vasique inclusus, commodissime vel per mare vel per terras transportari possit. Cum autem ulterius quaererem, quantum cuperet pro tali corpore conficiendo, me ad D. tuam hac de re scripturum, respondit, non minus quam 1000 aureos, circulis exceptis et pede. Quare cum hîc Globum viderim ligneum, qui tamen propter ligni naturam suis ex aequo partibus non respondere videretur, hac de re certiorem facere volui, ut si forte eiusmodi corpus globosum D. tua habere cuperet, quod et ad durationem et perfectionem magis accederet, sciret, hîc esse Artificem, qui hoc se praestare posse diceret. Proinde si qua in re mea opera uti volueris, efficiam ut te nec sumptuum poenitere queat, nec ego cura, opera et studio tibi defuisse videar, modo cum aliquo agas, qui artifici dimidium sumptuum tuo nomine exponat. In ceteris neque diligentia neque fide unquam deero.</p><p>Novi quod ad te scriberem non habebam, nisi forte scire velis, hoc ipso die post obitum PEUTINGERI in Duumvirum, quem nos vulgo Stadtpfleger nominamus, MARCUM FUGGERUM electum esse cum omnium civium admiratione.</p><p>Hisce vale feliciter, et me, quod facis, amare perge.<br>Augustae Rheticae postridie Cal. Augusti 1576.<br>D. T. deditissimus Iohan. Maior.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent in nobility of birth, in learning, and in virtue, Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, Lord of Knudstrup, his lord and friend to be honoured with the utmost regard. Greeting.</b></p><p>Tokens of your singular goodwill toward me, noble Lord, are not only your most delightful conversations, in which I was here generously instructed, but also your published book on the New Star, which you did not hesitate to send me as a gift. And so, as soon as I received it from Master Wolf, my dearest co-godfather, I judged that I should write to you to express my gratitude at whatever first occasion offered itself — especially since my love for you and the singular regard with which I have always attended you demanded it, lest [that regard] should seem to have been lessened either by length of time or by the distances of place. I therefore congratulate Your Lordship first of all on your safe and unharmed return to your homeland, and I pray that that safety may be perpetual for you. Next, for the book I give you the greatest thanks I can. And although it is not within the power of my talent to render an equal thanks, yet I affirm that I have dedicated to you all my zeal, all my regard, my devotion, and in short my honour, and that I shall preserve forever the memory of your very great virtues, which I have always admired. Furthermore, I would not have you ignorant, my Lord Tycho, that Schissler, our gnomon-maker [γνωμονοποιός], has completed a Globe fashioned out of copper, perfect in all its dimensions of roundness — like your own Automaton — whose diameter is five feet. And the Globe has been fashioned with such art that it can be taken apart into 8 pieces and again restored to a whole, as need requires; this he is about to send into Spain, together with an Astrolabe of the same size, by way of Marcus Fugger. And so, when I saw the Globe, thinking of your advantage, I asked whether he could also make a larger one. He answered yes, and [said] that he had been moved to make such a body from the fact that he had heard Your Lordship had sought here for a craftsman who might furnish it, and that he could equally make a body of the kind whose diameter would be 8 or 10 feet, and [made] with such art that, taken apart into about 40 pieces and easily packed in a chest, it might most conveniently be transported either by sea or by land. But when I asked further how much he would want for making such a body — [saying] that I would write to Your Lordship about this matter — he answered, not less than 1000 gold pieces, the circles and the foot [stand] excepted. And so, since I have seen here a wooden Globe which nevertheless, owing to the nature of wood, did not seem to correspond evenly in its parts, I wished to inform you of this matter, so that, if perhaps Your Lordship should wish to have a spherical body of this kind — one which would come nearer both to durability and to perfection — you might know that there is here a Craftsman who said he could furnish this. Accordingly, if in any matter you should wish to use my service, I shall see to it that you can neither regret the expense, nor [that] I shall seem to have failed you in care, effort, and zeal — provided you deal with someone who, in your name, lays out half the expense to the craftsman. In all else I shall never be wanting either in diligence or in fidelity.</p><p>News that I might write to you I had none, unless perhaps you should wish to know that on this very day, after the death of Peutinger, Marcus Fugger has been elected as Duumvir — whom we commonly call <i>Stadtpfleger</i> — to the admiration of all the citizens.</p><p>With these things, fare happily, and continue, as you do, to love me.<br>At Augsburg of the Rhaetians, the day after the Kalends of August [2 August] 1576.<br>Most devoted to Your Lordship, Johan Maior.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohan. Maior</i> = <b>Johannes Major</b> (Johann Mair), an Augsburg friend and regular correspondent of Tycho's from his Augsburg years; named alongside Hieronymus Wolf and the Hainzel brothers among Tycho's Augsburg circle (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>). He signs himself <i>D. T. deditissimus</i> (\"most devoted to Your Lordship\").</li><li><b><i>Ottonidi … Domino de Knudstrup</b></i> — \"son of Otte, Lord of Knudstrup\": the Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b> (Tycho's father) plus Tycho's principal Scanian seat, Knudstrup, kept as in §5.7.</li><li><b><i>liber tuus de Stella Nova</b></i> — Tycho's <i>De nova stella</i> (Copenhagen, 1573), his book on the 1572 supernova in Cassiopeia, here acknowledged as a presentation gift.</li><li><b><i>D. Wolfio, Compatre meo</b></i> — <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist (cf. the 1571 Wolf→Brahe letter, same circle); <i>compater</i> is the spiritual-kinship tie \"co-godfather\" (godparent to the same child / fellow sponsor), rendered literally rather than reduced to \"friend.\"</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>γνωμονοποιὸν</i> (gnōmonopoión, accusative of <i>γνωμονοποιός</i>) = \"maker of gnomons / dials,\" here a learned epithet for the instrument-maker; transcribed as printed (final-sigma not normalised in the inflected form) and translated \"gnomon-maker.\" <i>gnomon</i> is kept per §5.2.</li><li><b>Instrument-maker.</b> <i>Schislerum</i> = <b>Christoph(er) Schissler the elder</b> (active in Augsburg from 1546), the celebrated maker of fine astronomical and geometrical instruments. Scholarship notes that this very letter is the principal evidence on the question of who built the shell of Tycho's <i>Globus Magnus Orichalcicus</i>: Major reports Schissler <i>offering</i> to make Tycho a globe, which Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, citing TBOO VII, 36.5–26) reads as evidence that Schissler was <i>not</i> in fact the maker of Tycho's existing instrument. The letter is reported, not adjudicated, here.</li><li><b><i>veluti tuum Automaton</b></i> — \"like your own Automaton\": rendered literally. The referent is uncertain — most plausibly Tycho's own great globe / a mechanical celestial device of his — and is left unglossed rather than guessed.</li><li><b><i>Globum … in 8 partes dissolvi … in 40 circiter partes resolutus … vasique inclusus</b></i> — the demountable copper globe (5-foot diameter) dismantles into 8 parts; the proposed larger one (8–10-foot diameter) into about 40 parts, packable in a chest for transport by sea or land.</li><li><b><i>Marcum Fuggerum</b></i> — <b>Marcus Fugger</b> (1529–1597), of the Augsburg banking dynasty; the shipping agent for the Spain consignment and, per the closing news, newly elected <i>Duumvir</i> / <i>Stadtpfleger</i> (city magistrate) of Augsburg.</li><li><b><i>Peutingeri</b></i> — the <b>Peutinger</b> whose death created the vacancy Fugger filled; of the prominent Augsburg Peutinger family (the antiquary Konrad Peutinger's descendants). <i>Stadtpfleger</i> (the German civic title) is kept and glossed by Major's own Latin equivalent <i>Duumvir</i>.</li><li><b><i>1000 aureos, circulis exceptis et pede</b></i> — \"1000 gold pieces, the circles and the foot excepted\": the price quoted excludes the mounted graduated circles (<i>circuli</i>) and the stand/base (<i>pes</i>). <i>aurei</i> rendered \"gold pieces\" (gold coins, likely gulden/florins) rather than over-specified.</li><li><b><i>Astrolabio quantitatis eiusdem</b></i> — \"an Astrolabe of the same size,\" i.e. matching the 5-foot globe, the pair destined for Spain.</li><li><b><i>modo cum aliquo agas, qui artifici dimidium sumptuum tuo nomine exponat</b></i> — the one conditional clause: Major's offer of service is conditioned on Tycho's dealing through an agent who will advance the craftsman half the cost in Tycho's name. Rendered as a single positive proviso (one <i>modo</i>-clause, no negation).</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Augustae Rheticae postridie Cal. Augusti 1576</i> = \"at <b>Augsburg</b> [<i>Augusta Rhaetica</i>], the day after the Kalends of August, 1576\" (O.S.). <i>postridie Kalendas Augusti</i> = the day after 1 August = <b>2 August</b>; this agrees with the dataset label (\"2. august\"). <b>Dating crux flagged:</b> Mosley cites the letter as \"<b>1 August 1576</b>\" (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>), i.e. the Kalends itself; the discrepancy is the standard ambiguity over whether <i>postridie Cal.</i> is reckoned as 1 or 2 August. The Roman idiom is reproduced and glossed to 2 August per the literal reading; the date is <b>not</b> silently reconciled to Mosley's 1 August.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> The dataset gives the witness as Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 — the identical shelfmark recorded for the 1571 Wolf→Brahe letter; this may be a dataset carry-over rather than a verified folio for this letter. Recorded as given, not relied on.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin renders securely and the substance (Schissler's demountable copper globe, the Fugger/Spain consignment, the price and proviso, the Fugger election) is sound. Open points, all flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the dateline crux <i>postridie Cal. Augusti</i> = 2 August here vs. Mosley's \"1 August\"; (2) the referent of <i>tuum Automaton</i>; (3) the apparently duplicated MS shelfmark.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.34(bottom)-36 as page images (letter runs from the drop-cap 'B' on p.34 through the signature 'IOHAN. MAIOR' on p.36; p.37 is the next letter, Hagecius). Collated opening/closing formulas, and every proper name (WOLFIO, SCHISLERUM, MARCUM FUGGERUM x2, PEUTINGERI) and number (quinquepedalis/5 ft, 8 partes, 8 vel 10 pedum, ~40 partes, 1000 aureos) and the date (postridie Cal. Augusti 1576 = 2 Aug) against the page image. Source_text matches Dreyer verbatim (no TEXT_ERROR); English renders every clause, name and figure accurately including the two separate Marcus Fugger mentions and the Peutinger death/Stadtpfleger election detail. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Major", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15730701001", "next": "brahe-t_15770920001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840715001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for the gift of his book on the New Star, congratulates him on safe return to his homeland, and expresses continued regard.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760802001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760823001", "d": "1576-08-23", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 23 August 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, vol. VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THADDAeUS HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE. S.</p><p>CUM disiungerer a te Ratisbonae hyeme praeterita, nobilissime D. TYCHO, non minus moeroris reliquit ea disiunctio nostri quam tuus laetitiae adventus attulerat. Nam postquam te ex primo congressu mutuoque nostro colloquio cognovi, ita eram studio tui propter tuas eximias ingenij dotes et maximas virtutes incensus, ut tum demum conquiescerem animo, cum tecum essem. Itaque tam citam a nobis disiunctionem, quae me dulcissimo fructu iucundissimae conversationis nostrae privavit, non potui non dolere et conturbatiore animo ferre, neque illi ullum aliud levamen afferre potui, quam amorem et cogitationem tui, ut cum corpore tecum esse non liceret, saltem animo amantissimo tecum essem. Ac talem meum animum erga te iamdudum declarassem mea epistola, nisi toto hoc praeterito tempore multis hisque maximis fuissem districtus occupationibus, praesertim vero forensi quadam lite, cuius causa etiamnunc hîc me commorari diutius quam vellem oportet. Illa negotia, mi TYCHO, adeo me perturbarunt, ut hactenus neque in studijs nobis communibus, neque in his quae mecum liberalissimo animo communicasti, quicquam facere potuerim. Sed dabit Deus his molestijs quoque finem. Noster PIRCKMANNUS Ratisponâ domum reversus, re communicata cum fratre suo de opere TRAPEZUNTIJ imprimendo, quod Basiliensibus Typographis ipsomet consultore, ut scis, daturus eram, studiose a me fratris nomine binis literis petijt, ut sibi imprimendum concederem. Ego qui prae ceteris ipsi gratificari cupiebam, proxima Quadragesima misi opus illud. Nunc tantopere expetitum plane negligere videtur. Nuper enim scripsit ad me fratrem suum cum PLANTINO de impressione agere velle, putare etiam quosdam, antea in Italia fuisse evulgatum hunc librum. Hinc videtur extinctum esse desiderium excudendi operis. Ego vero impressum antea fuisse non existimo. Nusquam enim exstant exempla etiam in Bibliothecis clarorum virorum. GESNERUS quoque in sua Bibliotheca mentionem faciens operum TRAPEZUNTIJ et pro more suo loca, ubi singula impressa sunt indicans, huius nostri operis meminit quidem, sed non ait aliquando fuisse impressum. Utut sit, ego doleo quod librum non habeam in meis manibus et quod vagetur incertis sedibus. Utinam saltem integer redeat ad me, non facile deinceps exibit et custodietur arctius. De nostra stella an aliquid annotaveris, scire aveo. Ego meam responsionem contra RAYMUNDUM hyeme praeterita miseram imprimendam Francofurtum WECHELO, ut prodiret ad ferias Paschatis. Is vero cum primo spem fecisset, tandem nimis sero se excusavit imprimere non posse. Quoniam autem ea responsio iam antea fuit in multorum manibus, quibus legendam et iudicandam dederam, omnesque evulgationem eius expectabant exoptabantque, maturare editionem oportuit. Itaque curavi illam Pragae excudi. Eius exemplum tibi mitto, rogoque ut eam legas, et quae tibi in ea displicuerint, in ijs me libere moneas et coarguas. Si quid praeterea habueris ex tuis observatis distantijs stellarum, aut quidpiam aliud, mecum communicare ne graveris rogo. De HEMMINGIO vestro et statu Ecclesiarum vestrarum aliquid etiam adijcies. Tuo Achati quem tecum adduxeras Augustâ, si adhuc tecum est, adscribo salutem.</p><p>Bene vale.<br>Pragae 23. Augusti 1576.<br>T. ex animo THADDAeUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Thaddaeus Hagecius to Tycho Brahe — greeting.</b></p><p>When I was parted from you at Regensburg last winter, most noble Master Tycho, that parting left no less of grief in me than your coming had brought of joy. For after I came to know you from our first meeting and mutual conversation, I was so kindled with devotion to you, on account of your outstanding gifts of mind and your very great virtues, that I found rest in my spirit only then, when I was with you. And so I could not but grieve at so swift a parting from us — one that robbed me of the sweetest fruit of our most delightful conversation — and bear it with a more troubled mind; nor could I bring to it any other relief than my love and the thought of you, so that, since I was not permitted to be with you in body, I might at least be with you in a most loving mind. And I would long since have declared such a disposition of mine toward you by a letter of mine, had I not, throughout all this past time, been distracted by many and very great occupations, and especially by a certain lawsuit, on whose account I am even now obliged to linger here longer than I would wish. Those affairs, my Tycho, have so disturbed me that until now I have been able to accomplish nothing, neither in the studies common to us, nor in those matters which you shared with me in your most generous spirit. But God will grant an end to these troubles too. Our friend Pirckmann, having returned home from Regensburg, after the matter had been discussed with his brother concerning the printing of the work of Trapezuntius — which, as you know, I was myself, as adviser, going to give over to the printers of Basel — earnestly asked of me by two letters, in his brother's name, that I would grant it to him to be printed. I, who desired to oblige him before others, sent that work this past Lent. Now, though it was so greatly sought after, he seems plainly to neglect it. For he recently wrote to me that his brother wishes to treat with Plantin about the printing; and that some men even think this book was previously published in Italy. Hence the desire to print the work seems to have been extinguished. I, for my part, do not judge it to have been printed before. For nowhere do copies survive, even in the libraries of famous men. Gesner too, in his <i>Bibliotheca</i>, when making mention of the works of Trapezuntius and indicating, after his custom, the places where each was printed, does indeed recall this work of ours, but does not say that it was ever printed. However that may be, I grieve that I do not have the book in my own hands and that it wanders about in uncertain abodes. Would that it might at least return to me whole; thereafter it will not easily go out, and will be guarded more closely. I am eager to know whether you have noted down anything about our star. As for me, I had sent my reply against Raymundus last winter to be printed at Frankfurt by Wechel, so that it might come out at the Easter fair. But he, although at first he gave me hope, at last — far too late — excused himself, [saying] that he could not print it. But since that reply had already before been in the hands of many, to whom I had given it to be read and judged, and all were awaiting and longing for its publication, it was necessary to hasten the edition. And so I had it printed at Prague. I send you a copy of it, and I ask that you read it and, in whatever in it has displeased you, freely admonish and correct me. If besides this you have anything from your observed distances of the stars, or anything else, I ask you not to find it burdensome to share it with me. About your friend Hemmingius and the state of your churches you will add something too. To your Achates, whom you had brought with you from Augsburg, if he is still with you, I add my greeting.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Prague, 23 August 1576.<br>Yours from the heart, Thaddaeus.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), the imperial physician and astronomer at Prague, author of the <i>Dialexis de novae … stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574) on the 1572 nova. He and Tycho had met at the Imperial Diet of <b>Regensburg</b> in the winter of 1575 (here <i>hyeme praeterita</i>, \"last winter\"), where the imperial coronation of Rudolf II took place; the meeting began their long correspondence and friendship.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> — addressed as <i>nobilissime D. TYCHO</i>, \"most noble Master Tycho.\" The dataset header repeats \"Tycho Brahe Tycho Brahe\"; only one recipient is meant.</li><li><b><i>Ratisbona</b></i> = <b>Regensburg</b> (where the two had met); <i>Ratisponâ</i> (ablative) \"from Regensburg.\" <i>Augusta</i> = <b>Augsburg</b> (protocol §5.7). <i>Francofurtum</i> = <b>Frankfurt</b> (am Main), site of the great book fair (<i>ferias Paschatis</i>, \"the Easter fair\").</li><li><b><i>opus TRAPEZUNTIJ</b></i> — a work of <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius, 1395–1486), the Byzantine-Italian humanist. Hagecius held a manuscript of an unpublished Trapezuntius work which he was trying to see into print; the whole middle passage concerns the frustrated negotiations over it (Basel printers → the Pirckmann brothers → Plantin).</li><li><b><i>PIRCKMANNUS</b></i> — <b>Pirckmann</b> (Pirckheimer/Pirckmann), the agent at Regensburg acting with his brother over the printing; not securely identified beyond the letter. Left in the period form. <i>fratre suo</i> = \"his brother.\"</li><li><b><i>PLANTINUS</b></i> = <b>Christophe Plantin</b> (c. 1520–1589), the great Antwerp printer.</li><li><b><i>GESNERUS … in sua Bibliotheca</b></i> = <b>Conrad Gessner</b> (1516–1565) and his <i>Bibliotheca universalis</i> (Zurich, 1545), the standard bibliographical reference; Hagecius cites it as evidence that the Trapezuntius work was never printed. <i>Bibliotheca</i> here = the <i>bibliography/catalogue of authors</i>, not a physical library; rendered as the title <i>Bibliotheca</i>.</li><li><b><i>nostra stella</b></i> — \"our star,\" i.e. the <b>new star (nova) of 1572</b> in Cassiopeia, the shared subject of Hagecius's and Tycho's work; <i>De nostra stella an aliquid annotaveris</i> asks whether Tycho has recorded fresh observations of it.</li><li><b><i>responsionem contra RAYMUNDUM</b></i> — Hagecius's <b>reply against \"Raymundus\"</b> = <b>Annibale Raimondo of Verona</b>, who had argued the 1572 nova was a sublunary (atmospheric) phenomenon; Hagecius's rebuttal is the <i>Responsio ad virulentum … scriptum … Hannibalis Raymundi</i> (Prague, 1576), which he here says he has just had printed at Prague (after Wechel at Frankfurt declined) and encloses for Tycho. Tycho too criticized Raimondo on the nova. Period form <i>RAYMUNDUM</i> kept in translation as \"Raymundus.\"</li><li><b><i>WECHELO</b></i> = the Frankfurt printing house of <b>Wechel</b> (André/Andreas Wechel, d. 1581); dative <i>Wechelo</i> \"to Wechel.\"</li><li><b><i>HEMMINGIO vestro … Ecclesiarum vestrarum</b></i> — <i>Hemmingius</i> = <b>Niels Hemmingsen</b> (1513–1600), the leading Danish Lutheran theologian at Copenhagen, then under suspicion of crypto-Calvinism; <i>vestro / vestrarum</i> (\"your,\" plural) marks him and the Danish churches as belonging to Tycho's national sphere. Period form kept as \"Hemmingius.\"</li><li><b><i>Tuo Achati</b></i> — \"your <b>Achates</b>\": a classical allusion (<i>fidus Achates</i>, the faithful companion of Aeneas, <i>Aeneid</i>) for the trusted assistant/companion whom Tycho had brought from Augsburg; Hagecius does not name him and neither does the translation. Kept as the allusive \"Achates.\"</li><li><b><i>forensi … lite</b></i> — \"a certain lawsuit / forensic suit\" detaining Hagecius at Prague. <i>commorari … diutius quam vellem</i>: \"to linger longer than I would wish\" — the hedge (\"than I would wish\") preserved.</li><li><b><i>incertis sedibus</b></i> — \"in uncertain abodes,\" of the wandering Trapezuntius manuscript; the personifying metaphor (<i>vagetur</i>, \"wanders\") kept.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (dicit), \"greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well.\" <i>T. ex animo</i> (subscription) = \"Yours from the heart\" (<i>Tuus ex animo</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Pragae 23. Augusti 1576</i> = \"Prague, 23 August 1576.\" A Catholic correspondent in Imperial service, but the dateline is given plainly without a style mark; in 1576 (pre-1582) Old and New Style coincide, so no O.S./N.S. divergence arises. Marked [O.S.] in the header by project convention for a pre-Gregorian Julian date; not silently converted.</li><li><b><i>Quadragesima</b></i> = <b>Lent</b> (\"this past Lent,\" <i>proxima Quadragesima</i>), here a season-dating of when he sent the work.</li><li><b>Catalogue note.</b> The dataset `label` (\"1576. 23. august\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'ipsemet' to 'ipsomet' (ablative, agreeing with 'consultore'), per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII p.37.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the persons/works are identified from the corpus (Hagecius's <i>Responsio</i> against Raimondo, the 1572 nova, Gessner's <i>Bibliotheca</i>, Plantin, Wechel, Hemmingsen). The only soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: the precise identity of <i>Pirckmannus</i> and his brother (period form retained, not asserted as a specific Pirckheimer), and the unnamed <i>Achates</i> (an allusion, deliberately left unidentified).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. The slice's printed-page label ('36-338') is indeed corrupted/implausible, as flagged; the actual slice contains ordinary sequential Dreyer pages. Read pages 1-4 of the 30-page slice (Read tool, visual page images, no OCR/text-layer used) = Dreyer printed pages 36, 37, 38, 39. The Hagecius-to-Tycho letter (heading 'THADDAEUS HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE. S.', opening 'CUM disiungerer a te Ratisbonae hyeme praeterita', closing 'Pragae 23. Augusti 1576. T. ex animo THADDAEUS.') is fully present on printed pages 37-38, immediately followed on page 38 by the next letter's heading (Petrus Severini to Tycho) and page 39 belongs entirely to a different letter (Tycho to Petrus Severini, 3 Sept 1576) -- confirming the letter is complete and not truncated, so reading beyond page 4 was unnecessary. Collated the full Latin text sentence-by-sentence against the published English: opening/closing lines, every proper name and place (Ratisbonae/Regensburg, Pirckmannus, Trapezuntij, Basiliensibus/Basel, Plantino, Italia, Gesnerus, Raymundum, Francofurtum/Wechelo, ferias Paschatis, Pragae, Hemmingio, Achati/Augusta), and the date (23 Augusti 1576) all match between our source_text, Dreyer's page images, and the English. Specifically verified: (1) the Gesner/Trapezuntius hedge -- Dreyer prints 'meminit quidem, sed non ait aliquando fuisse impressum' and the English correctly keeps this as a hedge ('does not say that it was ever printed'), not hardened into an assertion that it was printed; (2) the Achates passage -- 'Tuo Achati quem tecum adduxeras Augusta, si adhuc tecum est' is unambiguously Tycho's own companion (dative 'to your Achates', 'you had brought with you'), and the English correctly preserves this ('To your Achates, whom you had brought with you from Augsburg, if he is still with you') with no pronoun flip or reassignment to Hagecius. No omitted sentences, no fabricated names/dates/numbers, no invented closing material, no misattributed custody claim (Dreyer's marginal apograph/codex note is apparatus, not part of the letter body, and is not asserted anywhere in the published English). One TEXT_ERROR found: our source_text's 'ipsemet' vs Dreyer's grammatically-correct ablative 'ipsomet' -- a single-letter transcription slip that does not affect meaning or the translation. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it is low-severity and does not change what a scholar would take away, hence verdict MINOR / grade C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin source_text reads the nominative 'ipsemet' where Dreyer's printed text has the ablative 'ipsomet' (correctly agreeing with the ablative absolute 'consultore'); a single-letter corruption in the source transcription. Does not affect the English, which correctly renders the sense ('as adviser') regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>quod Bafilienfibus Typographis ipfomet confultore, ut fcis, daturus eram (p.37, line ~24: 'ipfomet' = 'ipsomet', ablative agreeing with 'consultore')</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII p.37 (page image, not OCR text layer). The single-letter reading 'ipfomet' is unambiguous in the printed page. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15801104001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15801104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Recalls their meeting at Regensburg and expresses grief at their swift parting, explaining the long delay in writing by lawsuits and pressing occupations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760823001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15760903001", "d": "1576-09-03", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Petrus Severinus", "ck": "Petrus Severinus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Knutstorp", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Petrus Severinus — Knudstrup, 3 September 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Dänische Bibliothek </i>(18th-c. printing; the letter also appears in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Odense, Karen Brahes bibliotek, 298 (857 b)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET DOCTISSIMO VIRO DOCTORI PETRO SEVERINI, MEDICO REGIO, PHILOSOPHO EXIMIO, SUO AMICO. S.</p><p>BINAS a te charissime D. D. PETRE recepi literas, quibus interea nihil a me responsum est, non quod amicitiae et officii immemor essem, sed quod multis negotiis et amicorum visitationibus interea (ut fit) distractus fuerim. In prioribus communis nostri fratris et amici immortali memoria dignissimi PRATENSIS casum et immaturum inexpectatumque a nobis discessum merito deploras. In posterioribus vero proprias, quibus conflictaris in vitae cursu, difficultates doles.</p><p>Etsi vero PRATENSIS nostri abitus potius, quam obitus ab omnibus merito doleatur, utpote qui ob multarum scientiarum excellentem peritiam, vitaeque et morum incomparabilem suavitatem, in omnium animis perpetuum sui desiderium reliquerit; tamen cum nos divinis oraculis et philosophia aliquatenus imbuti, statuamus eos, qui corpoream et visibilem hanc mundi formam, urgentibus fatis, reliquerunt, ad beatioris mundi incolas migrare et tanto beatius deinde vivere, quanto infinita felicitas finitam miseriam exsuperat; o Horizontum temporis et aeternitatis imperscrutabile discrimen; cumque is, qui hinc discessit, non decessit PRATENSIS, fuerit et divina et humana Philosophia ita edoctus, ut in eius, per quem omnes misericordiam meremur, pia invocatione et officij sui in hac mortali vita fideli vocatione, in aeternitatem migraverit, et dum fata sinebant, vitam hanc mortalem bene et laudabiliter transegerit; idcirco non dubitamus cum eo longe melius nunc actum esse, quam nobiscum, qui inter tot errorum et fluctuationum procellas misera spe, quod reliquum est in corpore vitae, fovemus. Unde non est quod tam ipsius casum doleamus, quam proprium, quod tanto viro, tam praeclaris animi dotibus ornato, tamque sincero et fido amico orbati simus. Verum cum et nos frustra hoc faciamus, utpote qui nullo luctu nullis lacrymis exoptatissimum amicum in hanc vitam revocare possimus, nostroque desiderio nulla ratione succurri possit, patienter feramus id, quod inexorabile fatum nulla impatientia mutari permittit. Unum restat, quo amici defuncti exuvias ornare possumus, nempe ut Epitaphium illi dignum confici curemus, quo eius memoria, alias quidem immortalis, sed pro civili et politica consuetudine ad posteros et eos, qui ipsum in vivis non norunt, conservari possit; huius, quantum ad sumtus attinet, ego eorum particeps ero, tu de inscriptione cogita. Id autem imprimis fieri oportet, ne, si diu differatur, in perpetuam (ut fit) oblivionem abeat. Dies enim diei memoriam delet.</p><p>Quantum vero ad posteriores tuas literas attinet, magis est quod nostram in hac vita sortem deploremus; quod tot assidue, dum hîc peregrinamur, itinerum difficultatibus et asperitatibus impediamur. Tu, dum necessaria Philosophiae adminicula sectaris, libertatem Philosophicam in Aula aliquamdiu si non plane amittis, saltem in exilium abigis, unde eam revocare, ingentis, ut experiris, est difficultatis. Aula enim quosvis blando et benevolo vultu recipit, sed protervo et invito dimittit. Verum patientia, taciturnitate et consilio multae difficultates tandem exsuperantur, tempore etiam aliam atque aliam fortunae mutationem inducente. Sed danda est opera, ne, dum ab his fluctibus et tempestatibus evadere conamur, in novos impingamus et Charybdin evitantes in Scyllam devolvamur. Invia enim est inter tot scopulos, procellas et voragines ad Philosophiam via. Verum si cui auxilio Supremi portum attingere datur, is poterit magnum alterius spectare laborem, non quia vexari quenquam est iucunda voluptas, sed quibus ipse malis careas quia cernere suave est.</p><p>Petis insuper a me, charissime D. D. PETRE, ut hypotheseos COPERNICI in triplici motu Terrae explicationem tecum communicem; qua is solerti ingenio per solam Terram, tum primi mobilis rapidissimum motum, tum octavae sphaerae inaequalem tarditatem, ipsius denique Solis annuum cursum salvari posse demonstravit. Primus est, quo Terra circa axem sui Aequatoris versus ortum spatio 24 horarum convolvi imaginatur, eaque ratione rapidissima illa primi mobilis circumgyratio, qua omnes secum inferiores sphaeras in occasum convolvere a veteribus statuebatur, penitus excluditur. Alter motus est, quo centrum Terrae interea annuum cursum, quem veteres Soli attribuebant, absolvit, eaque via Eclipticae ductum ijs in locis, ubi hi Solem locabant, designat. Tertius vero est, quo axem Aequatoris Terrae ad planum Eclipticae, quod centrum ipsius (ut dixi) describit, variabilem et annuo hinc motu revertentem inclinationem habere oportet; alias enim apparentes Solis ab Aequatore declinationes, dierumque incrementa et decrementa cum umbrarum discriminibus hypothesi huic non congruerent. Sed et in hoc tertio motu mirabili industria per binos circellos circa axem Aequatoris Terrae descriptos, salvat inaequalitatem motus fixarum ideo nobis apparentem, quod Aequator Terrae inaequaliter in antecedentia promoveatur, alteramque Aequatoris mobilitatem, qua ad Eclipticam accedit et recedit, eodem artificio excusat. Verum haec omnia, ut sunt ingeniose a solertissimo illo Artifice excogitata; ita, nisi machina talis extruatur, in qua oculariter demonstrentur, difficulter intelligi possunt. Id vero et plura alia, Deo conatus nostros adiuvante, aliquando facturus sum, ubi vulgi strepitu semoto mihi Philosophica quiete frui licuerit, et in ijs, quae in motibus adhuc desiderantur, tunc diligentiorem trutinam adhibebo. Tu interim da operam, ut libertatem pristinam recuperare possis et tandem animum ad continuandum Philosophiae studium componere, ni mavis sequi viam universae carnis, cumque alijs reliquos frustrare inglorius annos. Vale et saluta magnificum Dominum Decanum meo nomine officiosissime.</p><p>Iterum terque quaterque vale.<br>Datae Knudsdorp die 3. Septembris Anno 1576.<br>Spero me brevi vobis Haffniae affuturum.<br>Tuus<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Doctor Peter Severinus, royal physician, distinguished philosopher, his friend — greeting.</b></p><p>I have received two letters from you, dearest Master Doctor Peter, to which meanwhile nothing has been answered by me — not because I was forgetful of friendship and duty, but because in the interval I was (as happens) drawn off by many affairs and by the visits of friends. In the earlier one you rightly lament the fall of Pratensis, our common brother and friend, most worthy of undying remembrance, and his untimely and unexpected departure from us. In the later one, however, you grieve over the difficulties of your own, with which you struggle in the course of your life.</p><p>But although the going-away rather than the death of our Pratensis is rightly mourned by all — inasmuch as, by reason of his excellent skill in many sciences and the incomparable sweetness of his life and character, he has left in the hearts of all a perpetual longing for himself — yet since we, imbued to some degree with the divine oracles and with philosophy, hold that those who, the fates pressing, have left this bodily and visible form of the world pass over to the inhabitants of a more blessed world and thereafter live the more blessedly, by as much as infinite felicity surpasses finite misery — O the inscrutable boundary of the horizons of time and of eternity! — and since he who departed hence, [yet] did not pass away, Pratensis, was so instructed in both divine and human philosophy that he passed into eternity in the devout invocation of Him through whom we all merit mercy, and in the faithful calling of his office in this mortal life, and, while the fates allowed, conducted this mortal life well and laudably: therefore we do not doubt that it now goes far better with him than with us, who amid so many storms of error and wavering cherish, with wretched hope, what remains of life in the body. Hence it is not so much his fall that we should grieve over as our own — that we have been bereft of so great a man, adorned with such illustrious gifts of mind, and so sincere and faithful a friend. But since we too do this in vain — inasmuch as by no mourning, by no tears, can we call back into this life a most longed-for friend, and our longing can be relieved by no means — let us patiently bear that which inexorable fate permits to be changed by no impatience. One thing remains, by which we can adorn the remains of our deceased friend: namely, that we see to it that a worthy epitaph be made for him, by which his memory — undying indeed in other respects, but, in accordance with civil and political custom, [to be preserved] for posterity and for those who did not know him among the living — may be preserved. Of this, so far as concerns the expense, I shall be a sharer in their part; do you give thought to the inscription. And this above all must be done, lest, if it be long deferred, it pass (as happens) into perpetual oblivion. For day deletes the memory of day.</p><p>As for your later letter, there is the more reason for us to deplore our lot in this life: that, while we sojourn here, we are so constantly hindered by the difficulties and harshnesses of [so many] journeys. You, while you pursue the necessary supports of philosophy, lose your philosophical liberty at Court for a time — if not wholly lose it, at least drive it into exile, whence to recall it is, as you find, of immense difficulty. For the Court receives anyone with a bland and benevolent face, but dismisses him insolent and unwilling. Yet by patience, silence, and counsel many difficulties are at length overcome, time too bringing on one change of fortune after another. But care must be taken lest, while we try to escape from these waves and tempests, we strike upon new ones, and, avoiding Charybdis, we tumble into Scylla. For the way to philosophy, amid so many rocks, storms, and gulfs, is hard to travel. Yet if it be granted to anyone, by the help of the Most High, to reach harbour, he will be able to look upon another's great toil — not because to see anyone harassed is a pleasant delight, but because it is sweet to discern from what ills you yourself are free.</p><p>You ask of me besides, dearest Master Doctor Peter, that I share with you the explanation of Copernicus's hypothesis in the threefold motion of the Earth, by which he, with skilful ingenuity, demonstrated that through the Earth alone could be saved both the most rapid motion of the <i>primum mobile</i>, and the unequal slowness of the eighth sphere, and finally the annual course of the Sun itself. The first is that by which the Earth is imagined to be revolved about the axis of its equator toward the east in the space of 24 hours; and by this means that most rapid whirling of the <i>primum mobile</i> — by which the ancients held that it carried all the lower spheres with it round to the west — is wholly excluded. The second motion is that by which the centre of the Earth meanwhile completes the annual course which the ancients attributed to the Sun, and by this path marks out the tracing of the ecliptic in those places where they located the Sun. The third, however, is that by which the axis of the Earth's equator must have, with respect to the plane of the ecliptic which its centre (as I said) describes, a variable inclination, returning by an annual motion from this [variation]; for otherwise the apparent declinations of the Sun from the equator, and the increases and decreases of the days together with the differences of the shadows, would not agree with this hypothesis. And in this third motion too, by marvellous industry, by means of two little circles described about the axis of the Earth's equator, he saves the inequality of the motion of the fixed stars that appears to us for this reason — that the Earth's equator is advanced unequally into the preceding [signs] — and by the same artifice he accounts for the other mobility of the equator, by which it approaches and recedes from the ecliptic. But all these things, ingeniously as they have been devised by that most skilful Artificer, can nevertheless hardly be understood unless such a machine be built up in which they may be demonstrated to the eye. This indeed, and many other things, I shall, with God aiding our endeavours, do at some time, when, the din of the crowd set aside, it shall be permitted me to enjoy philosophical quiet; and then I shall apply a more diligent scrutiny to those things which are still wanting in the motions. Do you meanwhile take pains that you may recover your former liberty, and at length settle your mind to continuing the study of philosophy — unless you prefer to follow the way of all flesh, and, along with others, to waste your remaining years inglorious. Farewell, and greet the magnificent Lord Dean in my name most dutifully.</p><p>Again, and a third and a fourth time, farewell.<br>Given at Knudstrup, 3 September, the year 1576.<br>I hope to be with you shortly at Copenhagen.<br>Yours,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from <b>Knudstrup</b> (<i>Knudsdorp</i> in the source; Knutstorp, the Brahe family seat in Scania) in September 1576 — months after Frederik II's 23 May 1576 grant of Hven, and just before Tycho settled there. The closing \"I hope to be with you shortly at Copenhagen\" reflects this transitional moment.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petrus Severinus / Petro Severini</i> = <b>Peder Sørensen</b> (Petrus Severinus, 1540/42–1602), the Danish Paracelsian physician and author of <i>Idea medicinae philosophicae</i> (1571), at this date <b>royal physician</b> (<i>medicus regius</i>) to Frederik II. The salutation's <i>philosopho eximio</i> marks his standing as a natural philosopher, not merely a court doctor.</li><li><b>Person — Pratensis.</b> <i>Pratensis</i> = <b>Johannes Pratensis</b> (Hans Philipsen, 1543–1576), professor of medicine at Copenhagen and the intimate friend of both Tycho and Severinus, who had died suddenly (his death is dated to 1576; the corpus confirms Tycho's <i>Epitaphium D. Joh. Pratensi</i> was printed at Uraniborg, 1584). The whole consolatory first half turns on him. <b>Wordplay flagged:</b> <i>abitus potius, quam obitus</i> (\"a going-away rather than a death\") and <i>qui hinc discessit, non decessit</i> (\"who departed hence, [yet] did not pass away\") are deliberate sound-figures contrasting <i>abitus/obitus</i> and <i>discessit/decessit</i>; the English keeps the antithesis but cannot reproduce the Latin assonance.</li><li><b>Person — <i>magnificum Dominum Decanum</i>.</b> \"the magnificent Lord Dean\" — an unnamed dean (likely the dean of the Copenhagen medical faculty or a cathedral dignitary) whom Severinus is asked to greet; not further identifiable from the text, so left unidentified rather than guessed.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Haffnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b> (protocol §5.7); <i>Knudsdorp</i> = <b>Knudstrup / Knutstorp</b> (kept as in §5.7).</li><li><b>The Copernican passage (technical core).</b> Tycho gives a careful exposition of the <b>threefold motion of the Earth</b> in Copernicus's hypothesis: (1) the <b>diurnal rotation</b> about the axis of the equator, eastward, in 24 hours, which removes (<i>penitus excluditur</i>) the <i>primum mobile</i>'s westward whirl; (2) the <b>annual revolution</b> of the Earth's centre, reproducing the Sun's apparent annual course along the <b>ecliptic</b> (<i>Ecliptica</i>); (3) the <b>third (declination/precession) motion</b>, the conical motion of the equatorial axis relative to the ecliptic plane, which Copernicus modelled with <i>binos circellos</i> (\"two little circles\") to save both the unequal precession of the fixed stars (<i>motus fixarum … in antecedentia</i>, motion into the preceding signs) and the variation in the obliquity (<i>qua ad Eclipticam accedit et recedit</i>). Controlled-glossary renderings used: <i>declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>, <i>primum mobile</i> kept as a technical term, <i>Ecliptica</i> → <b>ecliptic</b>, <i>sphaera</i> → <b>sphere</b>, <i>motus</i> → <b>motion</b> (§5.1). <b>Note on Tycho's stance:</b> this is an expository, admiring account of Copernicus (\"that most skilful Artificer\") written <i>before</i> Tycho's own geo-heliocentric system; he calls for a physical <i>machina</i> (a model/orrery built up so the motions can be \"demonstrated to the eye\") and reserves judgement on \"those things which are still wanting in the motions\" (<i>quae in motibus adhuc desiderantur</i>) — his hedge is preserved, not sharpened into a critique he does not yet make.</li><li><b>Classical allusions.</b> <i>Charybdin evitantes in Scyllam devolvamur</i> — the Scylla-and-Charybdis topos (cf. the same image in Wolf's 1571 letter). The closing sententia <i>quibus ipse malis careas quia cernere suave est</i> renders <b>Lucretius</b>, <i>De rerum natura</i> II.1–4 (<i>Suave, mari magno … alterius spectare laborem</i>); Tycho adapts the Epicurean lines, and the rendering keeps them as the moral aphorism Tycho intends, with the source flagged here rather than smoothed away. <i>Viam universae carnis</i> (\"the way of all flesh\") is a Scriptural turn (cf. Joshua 23:14 / 1 Kings 2:2).</li><li><b>Sententiae kept.</b> <i>Dies enim diei memoriam delet</i> — \"For day deletes the memory of day,\" kept as the free-standing maxim it is. <i>Iterum terque quaterque vale</i> — \"Again, and a third and a fourth time, farewell\" (the <i>terque quaterque</i> echoes Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> I.94); rendered as the intensified valediction, distinct from a plain <i>Vale</i>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> → \"greeting\"; <i>D. D. PETRE</i> = <i>Domine Doctor Petre</i> → \"Master Doctor Peter\"; <i>Vale … Iterum … vale</i> → \"Farewell … Again … farewell\" (§5.5). The doubled subscription (<i>Tuus / Tycho Brahe</i>) and the postscript-style line about Copenhagen are kept in their source order.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Knudsdorp die 3. Septembris Anno 1576</i> = \"Given at Knudstrup, 3 September 1576\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline matches the catalogue label (<i>1576. 3. september. Knutstorp</i>); no date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The source's `edition` field gives the <b>Dänische Bibliothek</b> (the 18th-century Copenhagen/Leipzig periodical of Danish-historical documents), the early printing recorded for this letter; it is also edited in <b>Dreyer</b>, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). The sole MS witness given is <b>A = Odense, Karen Brahes bibliotek, 298 (857 b)</b>.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The two soft points are editorial, not interpretive, and are flagged rather than resolved: the unidentified <i>Dominus Decanus</i> (left unnamed, as the text leaves it), and the variation-vs-libration nuance of <i>annuo hinc motu revertentem inclinationem</i> in the third Copernican motion, which the translation renders literally (\"returning by an annual motion\") without forcing a modern reading onto it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.38-40 as page images (slice pp.38-41; letter runs from mid-p.38 to mid-p.40, bracketed by a prior Hagecius letter and the following Danzaeus-to-Tycho letter of 17 Nov 1576 on p.41). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against source_text (exact match) including the abitus/obitus and discessit/decessit wordplay, both correctly preserved in English. Checked the sole name (Pratensis), the date (Knudstrup/Knudsdorp, 3 September 1576), and the place (Haffniae/Copenhagen), plus the full Copernican three-motions exposition (24-hour diurnal rotation, annual solar-path motion, the variable equatorial inclination) for technical accuracy against Dreyer — all correct. Considered and rejected one candidate finding (the ablative 'protervo et invito' after 'dimittit', which grammatically continues the parallel 'vultu' from the preceding clause and describes the Court's fickle countenance rather than the departing visitor) as too ambiguous in the English rendering to count as a clear defect rather than a defensible elliptical translation choice mirroring the source's own ellipsis.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Odense, Karen Brahes bibliotek, 298 (857 b); ."], "th": {"label": "Petrus Severinus", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15771114001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to two long-unanswered letters, mourning the death of Pratensis and acknowledging Severinus's account of his personal difficulties.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15760903001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15761117001", "d": "1576-11-17", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Charles de Dançay", "ck": "Charles de Dançay", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Charles de Dançay → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 17 November 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILI ET GENEROSO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI A KNUDSTORP, AMICO SUO SEMPER OBSERVANDO.</p><p>GENEROSE et clarissime vir, Scotus hîc quidam in hoc postremo bello Danico et Suetico stipendia sub Capitaneo IOHANNE CLARICO Scoto meruit, et parum, uti opinor, illi restat, ipsi etiam serenissimo Regi aut quaestoribus eius incognitum et memoria elapsum. Quod si tu hunc afflictum tua gratia et favore apud Regem aut per aulicos amicos iuvare possis, obnixe te oro, ut id facias. Et hoc etiam tibi persuadeas, quod tibi hac de re nec scripsissem, nec molestus fuissem, si literae, quas tibi BUCHANANUS, serenissimi Scotorum regis Praeceptor, nunc scribit, ad id me non impulissent; cuius preces magnum apud te pondus habituras esse spero, cum ille Poëta sit doctissimus, et Theologus minime seditiosus, nec Reipublicae perturbator, nec etiam ARISTOTELI nimium addictus, quo etiam quidam Theologiam profitentes abutuntur. Scotus ille responsum hîc a te expectabit, et literas ad aulicos amicos, ut negotia isthic tutius et facilius expedire possit. Postea si voles, BUCHANANO rescribas. De Astronomicis quibusdam libenter te audiam disserentem, ubi simul affuerimus. Nam Theorias Planetarum revolvere, et faciliori a te excogitata Methodo discere plurimum aveo. Astrologica, quibus tu moderate uteris, nec nimium (quod laudo) ijs tribuis, non valde vitupero quidem, sed neque admodum suspicio, ut multi alias solent, siquidem a Domino CALVINO olim in his absterritus sum, cum Argentinae simul essemus, quando istis vaticinijs admodum addictus fuissem. Habent enim nescio quid, quod hominem trahit ad se, ut ijs nimium adhaereat et fidat. Is enim, cum vidisset, me eo credulitatis in hisce praedictionibus pervenisse, ut de adulterijs etiam mulierum hinc nonnulla, quae, ut fatear quod verum est, raro falsos habuerint eventus, praedicere me posse iactarem, pro sua autoritate et gravi iudicio me increpavit, et male mihi minatus est, si ista non relinquerem. Quare cum eum suspicerem, atque in pretio haberem, mecumque STURMIUS plurimum illis tribueret, non ausus sum talia ulterius serio attingere. Astronomica tamen semper plurimi aestimavi, atque uti merentur, magnifeci, et etiamnum magnifacio. Quare in ijs aliqua etiam hac invalescente aetate de praeceptore discere plurimum desidero, quod et te mihi non denegaturum spero.</p><p>Vale, Vir integerrime, et me, quod plura non scribam, excusatum habe, Uraniamque tuam meis verbis reverenter saluta.<br>Haffniae 17. die Novembris Anno 1576.<br>Tibi deditissimus<br>Carolus Danzeus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and well-born gentleman, Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, of Knudstrup, his friend ever to be esteemed.</b></p><p>Well-born and most distinguished sir, a certain Scot here served for pay, in this last Danish and Swedish war, under Captain John Clark, a Scot, and there remains owing to him, as I think, but little, [a matter] unknown even to his most serene Majesty himself or to his treasurers, and slipped from memory. If you can help this afflicted man by your goodwill and favour with the King, or through friends at court, I earnestly beg you to do so. And let yourself be persuaded of this too: that I should neither have written to you on this matter nor have troubled you, had not the letter which Buchanan, the tutor of the most serene King of the Scots, is now writing to you, driven me to it; whose entreaties I hope will carry great weight with you, since he is a most learned poet, and a theologian by no means seditious, no disturber of the commonwealth, nor yet too much addicted to Aristotle — whom certain professors of theology also abuse. That Scot will await here a reply from you, and letters to friends at court, so that he may dispatch his affairs there more safely and easily. Afterwards, if you wish, you may write back to Buchanan. On certain matters astronomical I shall gladly hear you discourse, when we are together. For I greatly long to turn over the theories of the planets, and to learn [them] by an easier method devised by you. As for astrological matters — which you use in moderation, and to which you do not (a thing I praise) attribute too much — I do not greatly disparage them, to be sure, but neither do I altogether look up to them, as many on other occasions are wont to do; since I was once frightened off these things by Master Calvin, when we were together at Strasbourg, at a time when I had been quite addicted to those prognostications. For they have some I-know-not-what that draws a man to them, so that he clings to them and trusts them too much. For he [Calvin], when he had seen that I had come to such a pitch of credulity in these predictions that I would even boast that I could foretell from them some things, indeed, concerning the adulteries of women — which, to confess what is true, had rarely had false outcomes — by his authority and grave judgement rebuked me, and threatened me harshly if I did not give those things up. Wherefore, since I looked up to him and held him in esteem, and since Sturm too with me attributed much to those [prognostications], I did not dare to take up such things seriously any further. Yet astronomical matters I have always valued most highly, and have, as they deserve, esteemed greatly, and still esteem greatly. Wherefore I greatly desire to learn something even in these, in this my advancing age, from a teacher — which I hope you too will not refuse me.</p><p>Farewell, most upright sir, and hold me excused that I write no more; and reverently greet your Urania in my words.<br>At Copenhagen, the 17th day of November, the year 1576.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Charles de Dançay.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Carolus Danzeus</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French (royal) ambassador (resident) at the Danish court, a Huguenot humanist and a close friend and supporter of Tycho. The biographical literature confirms the letter's own testimony that Dançay \"had been a Protestant since youth, when he had studied with Johann Sturm and John Calvin\" (J. R. Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>, p. 54) — directly corroborating the references to <i>Calvinus</i>, <i>Sturmius</i>, and <i>Argentina</i> (Strasbourg) below.</li><li><b><i>Ottonidi a Knudstorp</b></i> — \"son of Otte, of Knudstrup\": <i>Ottonides</i>, a Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father; <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp), the Brahe family seat in Scania.</li><li><b><i>hoc postremo bello Danico et Suetico</b></i> — \"this last Danish and Swedish war\": the <b>Northern Seven Years' War</b> (1563–1570) between Denmark–Norway and Sweden, in which Scottish (and other) mercenaries served the Danish crown.</li><li><b><i>Capitaneo Iohanne Clarico Scoto</b></i> — \"Captain John Clark, a Scot\" (Latin <i>Ioannes Claricus</i>): a Scottish mercenary officer in Danish service; the <i>Scotus quidam</i> of the opening is an unnamed soldier of his company seeking arrears of pay. The individual is not further identifiable from the letter; <b>crux flagged</b> (see Confidence).</li><li><b><i>serenissimo Regi … quaestoribus eius</b></i> — \"his most serene Majesty … his treasurers\": <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> and the crown's fiscal officers, who have overlooked the soldier's outstanding pay.</li><li><b><i>Buchananus, serenissimi Scotorum regis Praeceptor</b></i> = <b>George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the Scottish humanist, poet, and historian, then <b>tutor (<i>Praeceptor</i>) to the young James VI of Scotland</b> (the <i>serenissimus Scotorum rex</i>). The characterisation — \"a most learned poet, a theologian by no means seditious … nor too much addicted to Aristotle\" — is Dançay's vouching for Buchanan's standing as he forwards Buchanan's (separate, enclosed/forthcoming) letter of recommendation to Tycho.</li><li><b><i>nec etiam Aristoteli nimium addictus, quo … quidam Theologiam profitentes abutuntur</b></i> — \"nor too much addicted to Aristotle, whom certain professors of theology also abuse\": a humanist's jab at the scholastic over-reliance on Aristotle; <i>abutuntur</i> in its period sense \"make wrong/excessive use of.\"</li><li><b><i>Theorias Planetarum revolvere … faciliori … Methodo</b></i> — \"to turn over the theories of the planets … by an easier method\": <i>Theoricae planetarum</i> is the planetary-theory genre; Dançay wishes to learn the planetary theories by some simpler method of Tycho's own devising. <i>revolvere</i> \"to turn over (in study), peruse.\"</li><li><b>Astrology.</b> <i>Astrologica … directio</i>-type technical vocabulary is absent here; the passage is about astrology <i>as a practice</i> (<i>vaticinia</i>, <i>praedictiones</i> — \"prognostications, predictions\"). Dançay's confession that he had grown so credulous as to predict <i>adulteria mulierum</i> (\"the adulteries of women\") is rendered literally; the hedge <i>raro falsos habuerint eventus</i> (\"had rarely had false outcomes\") is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b><i>a Domino Calvino olim … absterritus sum, cum Argentinae simul essemus</b></i> = \"frightened off … by Master <b>[John] Calvin</b>, when we were together at <b>Strasbourg</b>\": <i>Argentina</i> (Argentoratum) is the Latin name of <b>Strasbourg</b>, where Calvin resided 1538–1541 and where the young Dançay studied. The episode dates Dançay's renunciation of judicial astrology to that period.</li><li><b><i>Sturmius</b></i> = <b>Johann Sturm</b> (1507–1589), the humanist educator and rector of the Strasbourg gymnasium; here named as one who, with Dançay, had once \"attributed much\" to astrological prognostications.</li><li><b><i>hac invalescente aetate</b></i> — \"in this my failing age\": <i>invalescens</i> here in the sense of <i>growing weak / declining</i> (not the commoner classical \"growing strong\"); Dançay, then in his mid-sixties, contrasts his advanced years with his wish still to learn astronomy. The reading is contextually secure but the word is Janus-faced; noted.</li><li><b><i>de praeceptore discere</b></i> — \"to learn … from a teacher\": <i>de praeceptore</i> \"from a teacher,\" i.e. with Tycho as his instructor.</li><li><b><i>Uraniam tuam … reverenter saluta</b></i> — \"reverently greet your Urania\": <b>Urania</b>, the Muse of astronomy, here standing figuratively for Tycho's astronomical pursuit (and, by extension, his observatory programme); a learned flourish, not a person. Cf. the <i>Sacris Uraniae</i> sign-off idiom (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> (in the heading sense) — the address-formula closes <i>Amico suo semper observando</i> (\"his friend ever to be esteemed\"); <i>Vale, Vir integerrime</i> → \"Farewell, most upright sir\"; <i>Tibi deditissimus</i> → \"Most devoted to you\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Place / calendar.</b> <i>Haffniae 17. die Novembris Anno 1576</i> = \"at <b>Copenhagen</b> (Hafnia), the 17th day of November, 1576\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline as written agrees with the dataset's catalogue label (<i>1576. 17. november. København</i>); <b>no date discrepancy.</b></li><li><b>Edition / witnesses.</b> Edition of record: <i>Tychonis Brahei … epistolae … 1568–1587</i> (= Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII). MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the English rendering of \"hac invalescente aetate\" from \"this my failing age\" to \"this my advancing age\" — the Latin participle denotes age gaining ground, not declining.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical apparatus is well-corroborated; minor unresolved point is the identity of the unnamed Scottish soldier (and the precise spelling/identification of <i>Capitaneus Ioannes Claricus / John Clark</i>), flagged above rather than invented.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 40-41; source_text matches Dreyer exactly with no TEXT_ERROR. Verified names and references (Captain John Clark/Iohanne Clarico, Buchanan the Scottish king's tutor, Calvin at Strasbourg/Argentinae, Johann Sturm/Sturmius). The single defect found is a low-severity nuance shift (\"invalescente\" rendered as \"failing\" rather than \"advancing/increasing\") that does not change the letter's overall sense. No fabrication, omission, or sense-altering mistranslation found, matching the self-assigned grade B.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"Invalescente\" (from invalesco, to grow strong/increase) means an age that is advancing or gaining on him, not one that is weakening. \"Failing age\" imports a connotation of decline that the Latin participle does not literally carry; a more neutral rendering would be \"this advancing age of mine\" or \"as age grows upon me\". At: “Wherefore I greatly desire to learn something even in these, in this my failing age, from a teacher”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.40-42; the phrase appears on p.41 line 25, matching current_latin exactly (\"hac invalefcente ætate\"). Only the English translation required correction; the Latin was already sound. No other defects found on these three pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Charles de Dançay", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15760714001", "next": "brahe-t_15790705002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790705002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Tycho to intercede with the King or court friends on behalf of a Scottish soldier owed outstanding pay from the Danish-Swedish war.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15761117001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15761121001", "d": "1576-11-21", "dir": "from", "per": "early", "c": "Joachim Camerarius", "ck": "Joachim Camerarius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joachim Camerarius the Younger — Hven, 21 November 1576 [O.S.] (catalogued 8 November 1576)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address-line) · Edition of record: Thomas Crenius (Cranius),<i> Animadversiones philologicae et historicae, pars VII </i>(Leiden 1700) = Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, p. 42) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VIRO D. IOACHIMO CAMERARIO IUNIORI, PHILOSOPHO ET MEDICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO COLENDO. DOCTOR CAMERARIUS MEDICUS ZU NÜRRENBERG.</p><p>EX litteris tuis, clarissime Domine Doctor CAMERARI, intellexi, maximam partem librorum Mathematicorum BRECHTALII esse distractam; nihilominus peto, ut eos, qui residui adhuc sunt, velis mihi, iuxta catalogum tibi missum, curare colligi, eosque huic artifici Mechanico GEORGIO LAUBIMVOLFI ad me transmittendos tradere. Et insuper, si quos alias in Mathematicis et Chymicis inveneris seu novos seu veteres, quos mihi usui fore existimas, unâ adiungas velim. Precium horum omnium ex tuo decreto artifex hic persolvet; pecunia meo nomine interea ab alio artifice IOHANNE von der SCHACK nomine, mutuo accepta, donec ego eam hîc in Dania (est enim is huc venturus) vel etiam Noribergae restituero. Ignoscas autem mihi, quod ita audacter toties his meis negotijs molestus tibi sim; id facio fretus singulari tua humanitate, qua te eximie praeditum, cum Noribergae ultimo essem, non obscure cognovi. Peto autem, ut per occasionem me de tuis studiis et laboribus certiorem reddas, utrum in Spagyrica Medicina profectum aliquem feceris. Ego hac aestate nihil fere eximii in ea arte elaboravi, impeditus architectura, quam nunc prae manibus habeo. In Insula enim hîc quadam, quae sita est in medio famosissimi et celeberrimi freti, ubi omnes naves ab Oriente in Occidentem transeunt, et e contra, aedes Philosophicas non exiguis sumptibus extruo. Hac enim Insula eo nomine a Rege donatus sum, ut eo, cum fert animus, secedere possim, et a vulgi strepitu semotus altioribus vacare exercitiis. Ubi vero aedificium hoc absolvero, divina ope conatus nostros auxiliante, spero me commoditatem non exiguam adepturum philosophandi tum in superiori et coelesti Astronomia, tum etiam in hac inferiori Spagyrica rerum praeparatione. Et si quid mea opera et sumptibus inveniri queat, quod tibi aliisve amicis id expetentibus usui esse possit, lubens communicabo. Vale, vir doctissime et humanissime, et amicitiam inter nos brevi tempore Noribergae contractam longa tempora ne sinamus obliterare.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Datae in Insula nostra Huena, d. 21. Novemb. An. 1576.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished man, Master Joachim Camerarius the Younger, eminent philosopher and physician, his friend to be honoured. Doctor Camerarius, physician, at Nuremberg.</b></p><p>From your letter, most distinguished Master Doctor Camerarius, I understood that the greater part of Brechtel's mathematical books has been dispersed; nonetheless I ask that you be willing, on my behalf, to see to it that those which still remain are collected, according to the catalogue sent to you, and that you hand them over to this mechanical craftsman Georg Labenwolf to be sent on to me. And besides, if you should anywhere else come upon any [books], whether new or old, in mathematics and in chemical matters [chymicis], which you judge will be of use to me, I would wish you to add them in the same [consignment]. The price of all these the craftsman here will pay out according to your decision; the money [being] meanwhile borrowed in my name from another craftsman, one named Johann von der Schack, until I shall have repaid it either here in Denmark (for he is going to come hither) or else at Nuremberg. But forgive me that I should so boldly, and so often, be a nuisance to you with these affairs of mine; I do this relying on that singular kindness with which I plainly recognised you to be eminently endowed, when I was last at Nuremberg. I ask, moreover, that as occasion offers you make me more certain of your studies and labours, [namely] whether you have made any progress in spagyric medicine. I myself this summer have worked out almost nothing of note in that art, being hindered by the building-work [architectura] which I now have in hand. For on a certain island here, which is set in the midst of a most famous and most celebrated strait, where all ships pass from the East into the West and contrariwise, I am erecting a philosophical dwelling at no small expense. For I have been granted this island by the King on this account, that I may withdraw thither when the spirit moves me, and, removed from the din of the crowd, give myself to loftier pursuits. But when I shall have completed this edifice — the divine power aiding our endeavours — I hope to attain no small convenience for philosophising, both in the higher and celestial astronomy, and also in this lower spagyric preparation of things. And if anything can be discovered by my labour and expense that may be of use to you, or to other friends seeking it, I shall gladly share it. Farewell, most learned and most humane man, and let us not allow a long span of time to blot out the friendship contracted between us in a short space at Nuremberg.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given on our island of Hven, the 21st day of November, the year 1576.<br>T[ycho].<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Ioachimus Camerarius Iunior</i> = <b>Joachim Camerarius the Younger</b> (1534–1598), the Nuremberg physician, botanist, and bibliophile, son of the humanist Joachim Camerarius the Elder; a noted collector of scientific books and a hub of the Nuremberg learned network. The German address-line <i>\"DOCTOR CAMERARIUS MEDICUS ZU NÜRRENBERG\"</i> (\"Doctor Camerarius, physician, at Nuremberg\") is preserved as a code-switch into German per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Dateline crux (flagged, not reconciled).</b> The source dateline reads <i>\"d. 21. Novemb. An. 1576\"</i> (\"the 21st day of November, year 1576\"), and the header follows it. The dataset `label` catalogues the letter as <i>\"1576. 8. november\"</i>; the as-written date is led with and the catalogue date appended per protocol §3/§4. The 8 November label is not substantiated by the letter's own subscription and is recorded here, not silently adopted. (The letter is the one printed at Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 42; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>, also dates it 21 November 1576.)</li><li><b><i>librorum Mathematicorum BRECHTALII</b></i> — \"Brechtel's mathematical books\": the dispersed library (or stock) of one <b>Brechtel</b> at Nuremberg, identified in the source only by the genitive <i>Brechtalii</i>. The owner is not further specified in the letter; left as \"Brechtel\" without assertion of which Nuremberg Brechtel is meant.</li><li><b><i>artifici Mechanico GEORGIO LAUBIMVOLFI</b></i> — \"the mechanical craftsman Georg Labenwolf\": <b>Georg Labenwolf</b> (the Nuremberg art-bronze founder, builder of fountains and waterworks), here the agent forwarding the books to Hven. Tycho had met Labenwolf at Nuremberg in 1575; Labenwolf came to Hven c. 1577 and built the Uraniborg fountain (Hannaway / Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>, p. 114, citing this letter, VII, 42). The Latinised genitive <i>Laubimvolfi</i> is kept in the source and the standard form \"Labenwolf\" used in the translation.</li><li><b><i>alio artifice IOHANNE von der SCHACK</b></i> — \"another craftsman, one named Johann von der Schack\": a second artisan from whom Camerarius is to borrow the purchase-money in Tycho's name. The name is given in German (<i>von der Schack</i>) within the Latin sentence; kept as written.</li><li><b>Technical / glossary terms.</b> <i>Spagyrica Medicina / Spagyrica rerum praeparatione</i> — <b>spagyric medicine / spagyric preparation</b>, the Paracelsian-alchemical (distillation/separation) branch of medicine and chemistry; kept as the period term, not modernised. <i>Chymicis</i> — \"chemical [matters],\" the early-modern <i>chymia</i> embracing both chemistry and alchemy; rendered \"chemical\" with the bracketed Latin retained. <i>architectura</i> — here in the concrete sense of \"building-work / construction\" (the Uraniborg fabric then under way), not the abstract discipline; rendered \"building-work [architectura].\" The contrast <i>superiori et coelesti Astronomia … hac inferiori Spagyrica</i> (\"the higher and celestial astronomy … this lower spagyric [art]\") is Tycho's stock pairing of the science of the heavens above with the alchemy of the elements below; rendered literally.</li><li><b><i>aedes Philosophicas</b></i> — \"a philosophical dwelling\": Uraniborg, described here while still under construction (1576), before it bore that name in print. <i>altioribus … exercitiis</i> = \"loftier pursuits.\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Insula nostra Huena</i> = <b>Hven</b> (the island in the Øresund granted to Tycho by Frederik II in 1576); <i>famosissimi et celeberrimi freti</i> = the Øresund, \"a most famous and most celebrated strait.\" <i>Noribergae / Noribergam</i> = <b>Nuremberg</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Heading <i>S.</i> is not present; the salutation is the honorific address only. <i>Vale … Iterum vale</i> → \"Farewell … Again, farewell.\" Subscription <i>Datae</i> \"Given,\" <i>T. / TYCHO BRAHE</i> — the abbreviated initial expanded as <i>T[ycho]</i>.</li><li><b>No MS witnesses</b> are recorded for this letter (the dataset `witnesses` field is empty); the text descends through its print in Camerarius's <i>Animadversiones</i> (pars VII), the edition of record, and is reprinted in Dreyer VII.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> O.S. (Julian); Denmark kept Old Style. No conversion applied per protocol §3.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched; the one open point is the dateline crux (source <i>21 November</i> vs. catalogue <i>8 November</i>), flagged above and resolved in favour of the source's own subscription rather than silently reconciled. The identity of \"Brechtel,\" named only by genitive, is left unasserted.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — dateline decisively resolved (source 21 Nov, backed by Thoren + Dreyer VII p.42); the catalogue's '8 Nov' is the error.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the entire letter (heading, every clause, all proper names — Brechtel, Georg Labenwolf, Johann von der Schack — and the Hven/Uraniborg passage) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.42-43 as page images. Exact match with no textual variance. Translation verified accurate and complete throughout, no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Joachim Camerarius", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15901021001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks Camerarius to collect the remaining books from Brechtel's dispersed library and add any mathematics or chemical titles found, to be shipped via Georg Labenwolf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15761121001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15761208001", "d": "1576-12-08", "dir": "to", "per": "early", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 8 December 1576 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>SERIUS quam velim, nobilissime amicissimeque D. TYCHO, tuis literis respondeo. Nunc nactus idonei Tabellarij potestatem, alijs sepositis, ad scribendum me dedi. Et recte tu quidem inobservata currere sidera, nec tam serenitatem deesse quam perdi scribis. Quae quamvis vere dicantur, quod magna pars plerisque nihil agendo effluat, tamen hisce studijs arctoa Urania occinere videtur. Hoc latus mundi nebulae dirusque Iuppiter urget. Nisi forte Academici in media luce caecutiunt, qui umbras sectantur, vel ut tuis verbis utar, formalitates verborum inanium. Id quomodo in hoc doctrinae genere fieri queat, in quo ornari res ipsa negat, contenta doceri, non perspicio. Sed hoc forte intelligis, auditorum oculos in coelum esse deducendos, aut ipsum docentem non debere scholastico pulvere contentum in illo consenescere. Ego hoc vere affirmare possum, quicquid solidi in eo doctrinae genere hisce temporibus in lucem proditum est, hoc ab Academicis profectum esse. Nam et PURBACHIUS Academicus. Idem REGIOMONTANUS, STOFLERUS. Quid de COPERNICO dicam, quem Bononiae cum DOMINICO MARIA observasse, scripta sua testantur, et Romae docuisse scio. Ut interim REINHOLDUM et RHETICUM taceam. De me nihil habeo quod dicam, quo forte respicis, nisi, non omnes sibi hunc studiorum scopum proponere, et multos destitui necessarijs, aliisque causis impediri. Et tamen hic Academicus fortasse tuis aliorumque observationibus adiutus, facta cum veterum observationibus collatione, accommodatas aliquas hypotheses extruere, et his, Geometria duce, rationem tabulas condendi demonstrare. Et inani sua pompa verborum (sic enim labores nostros vocare videris) deducere suos auditores ad recludenda veri penetralia. Verum ego suum cuique ut sit pulchrum, remitto. Imo gaudeo et laudo aliquos esse, quibus mentem et animum Dij dederunt, ut in nobili et arduo studio incumbendum sibi persuadeant. Utinam mihi ipsorum laboribus et sumptibus frui liceret, velim me dare unâ observatorem. Progredere, mi TYCHO, quo virtus tua te vocat, Dij tibi ingenium, Dij fortunam et indefessum studium dedere, da operam, ut agnoscat posteritas, te non frustra vixisse, et crassum aërem naturae beneficio et studio vince.</p><p>Venio nunc ad ea, quae de medicamentis obiter inter nos mota sunt, ubi ignorantiam decoctionum, infusionum et aliorum operum seplasiariorum minime tibi obieci. Sed hoc tum temporis me existimare memini, te necdum satis perspexisse vim et facultatem eorum, quid possint in obstructionibus tollendis, in crassis et viscidis humoribus incidendis, quantum ad intemperies corrigendas, et alia quae quotidie usu veniunt, siquidem praxin Medicam publice non exerceas, uti nos, neque etiam opus habeas. Neque, mi TYCHO, aut spiritu vitrioli aut lapidum pretiosorum viribus omnia absolvi possunt. Quod si tamen quis purius et defecatius vires eliciat separatione puri ab impuro, eum ego seplasiariujs praetulerim, uti et tu facis, et illius laboribus fruar. Non enim ars consistit in illa separatione, uti neque in dococtionibus et alijs praeparationibus, sed morborum causarumque dignotione, in naturae aegrotantis, in medicamentorum recta applicatione. Quamvis illam purificationem ego non privaverim, ut neque medicamentorum praeparationem et longe minus medicamentorum veteribus ignotorum usum utilem. Quae duo priora eius generis sunt, ut existimem, a stupidissimo quoque, sed laborioso, si praeeuntem habeat, percipi posse, tantum abest, ut subtilem ARISTOTELIS naturae interpretis discipulum requirant. Sunt enim haec (loquor de praeparationibus, non de commodo usu) mechanici operis et nullius ingenij et mentis, ubi Doctor peritus et fidelis adsit. Verum illud fuit subtilis verborum indagatoris et sententiae, autorem per scapham ligonem intelligentem et verborum portentis secretiora quaedam medicamenta describentem assequi. Ego autem arbitror, rerum cognitionem satis per se difficultatis afferre, etiamsi verborum tenebris non involvatur. Ob id tibi gratias ago, quod pro nostra amicitia confectionem vitrioli aperueris, quamvis adhuc sola tua descriptione fruamur, quod vitris recipientibus destituamur. Sumtibus enim minime deterreor. Partem alteram de Horologijs velim, si commode fieri queat, ad me mitti cum libello illius nobilis Angli de Radio, si eo carere queas, aut saltem titulum primis literis ad me mittito, quamvis libellum malim. Cum enim in Anglia sit excusus et nunc miserabilis sit Belgicus status, vix alibi consequar. Utrum aliquid librorum Lipsiae sis nactus, scire velim, et quos.</p><p>Est hic Magister LUCAS Hamburgensis non ineruditus, et harum artium, quas uterque nostrum summe colit, non imperitus, Musices non ignarus. Hic cuperet tuo beneficio iuvari, et si vel Haffniae conditio offerretur docendi, aut etiam nobilis alicuius liberi forent instituendi, operam suam offert. Uxorem habet, curta est illi supellex, ut laborandum sit illi omnino de auxilio. Itaque hunc habeas de meliore nota commendatum; nam et moribus est integerrimis et eruditus. Non dubito te, cum de omnibus optime mereri studeas, etiam nostro nomine facturum omnia, quae ab optimo nostrique amicissimo expectari possunt. Quod si existimes necessarium, ut illum coram videas, dabo operam, ut ad te transcurrat, cum aut in Insula, aut Haffniae te esse intellexero.</p><p>Bene vale, et me, ut soles, ama.<br>Rostochij VIII. Decembris 1576.<br>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS, plane tuus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I reply to your letter later than I would wish, most noble and most beloved Master Tycho. Now, having got the use of a suitable courier, I have set everything else aside and given myself to writing. And rightly indeed do you write that the stars run by unobserved, and that it is not so much that clear skies are lacking as that they are wasted. Although this is truly said — that for most men a great part [of the clear skies] runs off in doing nothing — yet the northern Urania seems to sing a warning against these studies. This side of the world is pressed by fogs and by a baleful Jupiter [i.e. foul weather]. Unless perhaps it is the Academics who are blind in broad daylight, men who chase after shadows, or — to use your own words — the formalities of empty words. How that can come about in this kind of learning, in which the subject itself refuses to be dressed up, being content to be taught, I do not perceive. But perhaps you mean this: that the listeners' eyes are to be led up to the heavens, or that the teacher himself ought not, content with scholastic dust, to grow old in it. For my part I can truly affirm that whatever solid work in that kind of learning has been brought to light in these times has proceeded from the Academics. For Peurbach too was an Academic. So was Regiomontanus; so Stoeffler. What shall I say of Copernicus, whom — as his own writings attest — observed at Bologna together with Domenico Maria, and who, I know, taught at Rome? To say nothing meanwhile of Reinhold and Rheticus. About myself I have nothing to say — at which you perhaps glance — except that not all men set themselves this goal of study, and that many are deprived of the necessary means and hindered by other causes. And yet this Academic, perhaps aided by your observations and those of others, might — once a comparison has been made with the ancients' observations — build up some fitting hypotheses, and from these, with Geometry as guide, demonstrate the method of constructing tables; and with his \"empty pomp of words\" (for so you seem to call our labours) lead his listeners on to unlock the inner sanctuaries of truth. But I leave it to each man that his own should seem fair to him. Indeed I rejoice and praise that there are some to whom the gods have given the mind and spirit to persuade themselves that they must apply themselves to a noble and arduous study. Would that it were granted me to enjoy the fruit of their labours and expenses; I should be willing to give myself [to it] as an observer alongside [them]. Press on, my Tycho, whither your virtue calls you; the gods have given you genius, the gods have given you fortune and tireless zeal: take pains that posterity may acknowledge that you did not live in vain, and conquer the thick air by nature's favour and by [your] zeal.</p><p>I come now to those matters concerning medicines that were raised between us in passing, where I by no means charged you with ignorance of decoctions, infusions, and the other operations of the apothecaries. But I remember that I thought at that time that you had not yet sufficiently perceived their power and capacity — what they can do in removing obstructions, in cutting through thick and viscid humours, how far [they avail] toward correcting distempers, and in other matters that come up daily in practice — seeing that you do not practise medicine publicly, as we do, nor indeed have need to. Nor, my Tycho, can all things be accomplished either by spirit of vitriol or by the powers of precious stones. But if nevertheless someone elicits the powers more purely and more cleanly by the separation of the pure from the impure, him I would prefer to the apothecaries — as you also do — and I would enjoy the fruit of his labours. For the art does not consist in that separation, just as it does not in decoctions and other preparations, but in the diagnosis of diseases and their causes, in [the constitution] of the ailing nature, in the right application of the medicines. Although I would not have stripped that purification [of its value], nor likewise the preparation of medicines, and far less the useful employment of medicines unknown to the ancients. The first two of these are of such a kind, I think, that they can be grasped by even the most stupid man — provided he is industrious and has someone to lead the way — so far are they from requiring the subtle disciple of Aristotle, the interpreter of nature. For these things (I speak of the preparations, not of their proper use) are of mechanical workmanship and call for no genius or mind, where a skilled and faithful Doctor is at hand. But that other thing was [the work] of a subtle searcher-out of words and meaning: to make out an author who understands a <i>ligo</i> [mattock] by <i>scapha</i> [skiff], and who describes certain more secret medicines by monstrous [coinages of] words. I, however, judge that the knowledge of things brings difficulty enough of itself, even if it be not wrapped up in the darkness of words. For this I thank you, that for our friendship's sake you have disclosed the confection of vitriol — although as yet we enjoy only your description of it, since we lack the receiving glasses [retort-receivers]. For by the expense I am by no means deterred. The second part, concerning Horologes [clocks/timekeeping], I should like, if it can conveniently be done, to be sent to me, together with that little book of the noble Englishman on the <i>Radius</i> [cross-staff] — if you can do without it — or at least send me the title in the first letter [you write], though I should prefer the book itself. For since it was printed in England, and the state of the Low Countries is now wretched, I shall hardly come by it elsewhere. I should like to know whether you have got hold of any books at Leipzig, and which.</p><p>There is here one Master Lucas of Hamburg, not unlearned, and not unskilled in these arts which both of us cultivate to the highest degree, nor ignorant of Music. He would wish to be helped by your kindness; and if a teaching position should be offered, whether at Copenhagen, or even if the children of some nobleman were to be instructed, he offers his services. He has a wife, his furnishings are scant, so that he must by all means labour for [some] support. So have him commended to you under the better mark; for he is both of the most upright character and learned. I do not doubt that you, since you strive to deserve best of all men, will also do on our account all that can be expected from the best of men and our most loving friend. But if you think it necessary that you see him in person, I shall take pains that he run over to you, once I learn that you are either on the Island [Hven] or at Copenhagen.</p><p>Fare you well, and love me, as you are wont.<br>At Rostock, 8 December 1576.<br>Heinrich Brucaeus, wholly yours.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Henry Brucaeus, c. 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born professor of mathematics and then of medicine at the University of <b>Rostock</b>, where the young Tycho had studied; one of Tycho's standing scholarly advisers, repeatedly consulted on instruments, books, and the publication of his work on the comet and the new star. The closing <i>plane tuus</i> (\"wholly yours\") matches the warm, collegial register throughout.</li><li><b>Place / dateline.</b> <i>Rostochij VIII. Decembris 1576</i> = \"At <b>Rostock</b>, 8 December 1576\" (O.S.; Denmark and the Empire were still on the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1576. 8. december. Rostock</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> Recorded as given in the source header: Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. <i>(Note: the same shelfmark string is carried in the dataset header for the 1571 Wolf letter; this looks like a dataset-level collation artifact rather than a genuine shared folio, and is flagged here without being silently corrected.)</i></li><li><b>Opening sense.</b> <i>inobservata currere sidera, nec tam serenitatem deesse quam perdi</i> — Brucaeus quotes back Tycho's own complaint: the stars \"run by unobserved,\" and clear skies are not so much <i>lacking</i> as <i>wasted</i> (let slip). The whole opening paragraph answers a lost Tycho letter point by point, quoting Tycho's phrases (<i>formalitates verborum inanium</i>, <i>inani … pompa verborum</i>) back at him.</li><li><b><i>arctoa Urania</b></i> — \"the northern Urania\": Urania (Muse of astronomy) in her <i>arctoa</i> (Arctic / far-northern) aspect, i.e. astronomy as practised in the cloudy north; rendered literally, the personification kept.</li><li><b><i>dirus Iuppiter</b></i> — \"a baleful Jupiter,\" a learned periphrasis for bad weather (Jupiter as sky-god / the air), glossed \"[i.e. foul weather]\"; the astronomical-mythological double sense is preserved rather than flattened to \"the weather.\"</li><li><b>The \"Academics.\"</b> Brucaeus defends university men (<i>Academici</i>) against Tycho's evident jibe that they \"chase shadows\" and the \"formalities of empty words.\" The roll he cites — <i>Purbachius</i> (<b>Georg Peurbach</b>), <i>Regiomontanus</i> (<b>Johannes Regiomontanus</b>), <i>Stoflerus</i> (<b>Johannes Stöffler</b>), <i>Copernicus</i> (with <i>Dominico Maria</i> = <b>Domenico Maria Novara</b>, Copernicus's Bologna teacher), <i>Reinholdus</i> (<b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>), <i>Rheticus</i> (<b>Georg Joachim Rheticus</b>) — are all academically-placed astronomers, marshalled as proof that solid work came from within the universities.</li><li><b><i>accommodatas aliquas hypotheses … rationem tabulas condendi</b></i> — \"some fitting <i>hypotheses</i> … the method of constructing <i>tables</i>\": <i>hypotheses</i> in the period sense of geometrical models/parameters, not \"conjectures\"; <i>tabulae</i> = astronomical tables. <i>Geometria duce</i> = \"with Geometry as guide.\"</li><li><b><i>crassum aërem … vince</b></i> — \"conquer the thick air\": the heavy, foggy northern atmosphere of the opening; a closing exhortation that ties back to <i>Hoc latus mundi nebulae … urget</i>.</li><li><b>Medicine / alchemy.</b> The second paragraph answers an exchange on <i>medicamenta</i>. Glossary-fixed and period renderings: <i>decoctiones</i> = <b>decoctions</b>; <i>seplasiarii</i> = <b>apothecaries</b> (period sense of the dealers in unguents/drugs); <i>obstructiones</i> = \"obstructions\"; <i>crassis et viscidis humoribus incidendis</i> = \"cutting through thick and viscid <b>humours</b>\"; <i>intemperies</i> = \"<b>distempers</b>\" (humoral imbalances); <i>spiritus vitrioli</i> = \"<b>spirit of vitriol</b>\" (the distillate of vitriol/sulphuric acid); <i>confectio vitrioli</i> = \"the <b>confection of vitriol</b>\" (its preparation, which Tycho had disclosed); <i>separatio puri ab impuro</i> = \"the separation of the pure from the impure,\" the alchemical/spagyric refining Brucaeus subordinates to clinical judgement (<i>morborum causarumque dignotio</i> = \"diagnosis of diseases and their causes\"). The period framing is kept and not modernised to chemistry.</li><li><b><i>vitris recipientibus</b></i> — \"the <b>receiving glasses</b>,\" i.e. the glass receivers/receiver-vessels of a distillation train (rendered \"[retort-receivers]\"); Brucaeus lacks the apparatus, not the will or the funds (<i>sumtibus … minime deterreor</i>).</li><li><b>The <i>scapha / ligo</i> crux.</b> <i>autorem per scapham ligonem intelligentem et verborum portentis secretiora quaedam medicamenta describentem assequi</i> — Brucaeus jabs at an obscurantist author who hides plain things behind \"monstrous words\": one who \"understands a <i>ligo</i> [mattock/hoe] by <i>scapha</i> [skiff/boat],\" i.e. wilfully misnames the commonplace. The phrase is proverbial in tenor (cf. the Greek tag \"to call a spade a spade,\" here inverted — <i>not</i> calling a spade a spade); the exact target author is not named in the text. Rendered with both Latin nouns glossed and the polemical sense kept; <b>flagged</b> rather than resolved to a named work.</li><li><b>The <i>Horologia</i> / English <i>Radius</i> book.</b> <i>Partem alteram de Horologijs</i> — \"the <b>second part, concerning Horologes</b>\": a treatise/section on clocks or timekeeping that Tycho had (or was preparing), of which Brucaeus wants the second part. <i>libello illius nobilis Angli de Radio</i> — \"that little book of the <b>noble Englishman on the <i>Radius</b></i>\": a printed English book on the <i>radius astronomicus</i> (<b>cross-staff</b>). Brucaeus notes it \"was printed in England\" and is now hard to obtain because the Low Countries (<i>Belgicus status</i>) are in upheaval (the Dutch Revolt). The most likely referent is <b>Thomas Digges</b>, who made a noted study of the cross-staff fitted with transversal divisions (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>; cf. J. J. Roche, \"The Radius Astronomicus in England,\" <i>Annals of Science</i> 38 [1981]); a reference to his father <b>Leonard Digges</b> (<i>A geometrical practise, named Pantometria</i>, London 1571) is also possible. The identification is <b>probable, not certain</b>, and is flagged.</li><li><b><i>Magister Lucas Hamburgensis</b></i> — \"<b>Master Lucas of Hamburg</b>,\" an otherwise here-unidentified learned man (musician and mathematician) whom Brucaeus commends to Tycho for a teaching or tutoring post; <i>de meliore nota commendatum</i> = \"commended under the better mark\" (i.e. warmly, in the higher class of recommendation).</li><li><b><i>in Insula, aut Haffniae</b></i> — \"on the <b>Island</b> [= <b>Hven</b>] or at <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Haffnia</i>)\": by Dec. 1576 Tycho was establishing himself on Hven, granted to him by Frederik II that year; Brucaeus already locates him there.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>D. TYCHO</i> = \"Master Tycho\" (§5.5); <i>Bene vale, et me … ama</i> = \"Fare you well, and love me [as you are wont]\" (§5.5); <i>plane tuus</i> = \"wholly yours.\"</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The dense medical paragraph turns on several negations, all reproduced one-for-one: <i>ignorantiam … minime tibi obieci</i> (\"I by <b>no means</b> charged you with ignorance\"); <i>te <b>necdum</b> satis perspexisse</i> (\"you had <b>not yet</b> sufficiently perceived\"); <b><i>Neque</b> … aut spiritu vitrioli aut … omnia absolvi possunt</i> (\"<b>Nor</b> can all things be accomplished either … or …\"); <i>non … ars consistit in illa separatione, uti <b>neque</b> in decoctionibus</i> (\"the art does <b>not</b> consist in that separation, just as it does <b>not</b> in decoctions\"); <i>illam purificationem ego <b>non</b> privaverim, ut <b>neque</b> … et longe minus …</i> (\"I would <b>not</b> have stripped that purification [of value], <b>nor</b> likewise … and far <b>less</b> …\"); <i>etiamsi verborum tenebris <b>non</b> involvatur</i> (\"even if it be <b>not</b> wrapped up in the darkness of words\"). No negation added or dropped.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> \"eximere\" corrected to \"extruere\" (Dreyer, TBOO VII, p.43); English \"draw out some fitting hypotheses\" corrected to \"build up some fitting hypotheses\" to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and the dateline is unproblematic. Two soft points are flagged, not smoothed: (1) the <i>scapha/ligo</i> polemical aside, whose target author is unnamed in the text; and (2) the identification of the \"noble Englishman's little book on the <i>Radius</i>\" as Thomas (or Leonard) Digges, which is probable on the external evidence but not stated in the letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.43-45 as page images (Read pages=\"1-3\"; re-read p.43 alone a second time to confirm the extruere/eximere word). Collated the full letter word-for-word: opening/closing formulas, every proper name (Purbachius, Regiomontanus, Stoflerus, Copernicus, Dominicus Maria, Reinholdus, Rheticus, Magister Lucas Hamburgensis), and the medical/book-trade passage. Everything else matches Dreyer verbatim. One TEXT_ERROR found: our source_text's \"eximere\" vs. Dreyer's \"extruere\" in the sentence about constructing astronomical hypotheses/tables - a real but low-impact single-word substitution that does not change the overall thrust of the sentence. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of severity, even though the defect itself is minor and does not change what a scholar would take away from the letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads \"eximere\" (draw out/extract); Dreyer's printed text reads \"extruere\" (build up/construct) - a genuine word-level departure of our Latin from the edition of record. The published English (\"draw out\") faithfully follows our corrupted source_text rather than Dreyer, so the discrepancy is not the translator's fault, but it is a source-text defect per the audit brief's TEXT_ERROR category. Dreyer prints: <i>accommodatas aliquas hypothefes extruere, et his, Geometria duce, rationem tabulas condendi demonftrare (Dreyer VII p.43, line 27)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.43-45; the disputed reading falls on p.43, examined directly and confirmed to read \"extruere\". Both the Latin apparatus and the English clause it had misled are now corrected. No remaining uncertainty.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15760514001", "next": "brahe-t_15781204001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Replies to Tycho's remarks on wasted clear skies and discusses the obstacles to astronomy from foul weather and academic neglect of the subject.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15761208001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15770503001", "d": "1577-05-03", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Steen Bille", "ck": "Steen Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Herrevad", "t": "Steen Bille → Tycho Brahe — Herrevad, 3 May 1577 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a Latin verse couplet) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHE EX SORORE NEPOTI CHARISSIMO.<br>Is, quo vita, salus, via, statque redemptio nostra,<br>Corporis atque animae det tibi fausta, precor.</p><p>IAM iam literas tuas, Nepos e sorore charissime, de negotijs tuis expediendis apud fabrum ferrarium meum, per famulum tuum accepi, petitionibus tuis quam libentissime satisfacturus, nec officium ullum erga te, quem semper intime dilexi, pollicitationis in me claudier sinam. Armillas, quas, si recte memini, Ptolemaicas appellas, et pro quibus ornandis mihi cupreos circulos misisti, artifici hîc in moletrina ex tuo praescripto ornandos commisi, etiam frementibus et reluctantibus alijs, qui aquam pro conficienda farina nimium exhauriri praetendunt. Sed vereor, ne is iuxta tuam mentem Armillas istas rite parare et levigare norit. Verum, ubi ipsemet me accesseris, rectius de his dispicies. Has scribens, literas Regias accepi, ut ad suam Maiestatem sine mora veniam. Ubi autem a Regia Maiestate liberabor, Lares Huenenses seu potius Uraniburgicos, adeoque te ipsum invisam, tuis Musis thura libando.</p><p>His paucis quam feliciter vale.<br>Datae Herrisuadi 5 Nonis Maij Anno 1577.</p><p>STENO BILDE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe, his most beloved nephew through a sister.</b><br>May He in whom are our life, our salvation, our way, and our redemption<br>grant you what is favourable for body and soul — this I pray.</p><p>Just now, my most beloved nephew through a sister, I have received your letter, by the hand of your servant, about getting your business dispatched at my blacksmith's; and I shall meet your requests most willingly, nor shall I let any duty of my promise toward you — whom I have always loved most dearly — be closed off within me. The armillae which, if I remember rightly, you call Ptolemaic, and for the fitting-out of which you sent me copper circles, I have entrusted to the craftsman here at the mill to be fitted out according to your specification — even though others grumble and resist, alleging that too much water is drawn off [thereby], [water] needed for making the flour. But I fear he may not know how to prepare and burnish those armillae duly, in keeping with your intention. Yet when you come to me in person, you will look into these matters more rightly. As I write this, I have received a letter from the King [literae Regiae], [bidding me] come to His Majesty without delay. But when I am freed from the Royal Majesty, I shall visit my household gods of Hven — or rather of Uraniborg — and indeed you yourself, offering incense to your Muses.</p><p>With these few words, may you fare most happily.<br>Given at Herrevad, the 5th [day, reckoned to] the Nones of May, the year 1577.</p><p>Steen Bille.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <i>Steno Bilde</i> = <b>Steen (Sten) Bille</b> (1527–1586), Tycho's maternal uncle and the lord of <b>Herrevad Abbey</b> (Herrevadskloster) in Scania. The salutation <i>ex sorore nepoti</i> (\"nephew through a sister\") fixes the relationship: Steen's sister <b>Beate Bille</b> was Tycho's mother. Steen Bille was the figure who drew the young Tycho into alchemy and metallurgy, on whose Herrevad estate Tycho kept his first observatory and observed the 1572 nova; here, a year after the grant of Hven (1576) and during the building of Uraniborg, he is acting as Tycho's local agent for instrument-fitting.</li><li><b>Recipient / place.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b>; <i>Herrisuadi</i> = <b>Herrevad</b> (Herrevadskloster), the former Cistercian abbey in Scania held by Steen Bille. <i>Lares Huenenses seu … Uraniburgicos</i> = \"my household gods of <b>Hven</b> — or rather of <b>Uraniborg</b>\": <i>Lares</i> (the Roman household deities) stands metonymically for \"home/household\"; Bille corrects himself to name the rising house, Uraniborg, on the island of Hven.</li><li><b>Opening couplet.</b> The two verse lines are an elegiac distich (hexameter + pentameter) of pious well-wishing; kept as verse per protocol §4. <i>Is, quo … statque redemptio nostra</i> paraphrases Christ as \"life, salvation, way, redemption\" (cf. John 14:6, <i>via, veritas, vita</i>).</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Armillas … Ptolemaicas</i> = <b>armillae</b>, here specified as <b>\"Ptolemaic\"</b> — i.e. armillary rings of the Ptolemaic (astrolabon / <i>organon parallacticum</i>) type Tycho was then constructing; rendered \"armillae\" per protocol §5.2. <i>cupreos circulos</i> = \"copper circles,\" the raw graduated rings sent for finishing; <i>ornandos … parare et levigare</i> = \"to be fitted out … to prepare and burnish,\" the dressing and polishing of the metal rings.</li><li><b><i>faber ferrarius / artifex … in moletrina</b></i> — Bille's \"blacksmith\" and the \"craftsman … at the mill\" (<i>moletrina</i> = a water-mill / grinding-mill). The complaint that fitting the instruments \"draws off too much water needed for making the flour\" indicates the metalwork was being done at the abbey's water-mill, in competition with the milling of grain — a vivid material detail of where early Tychonic instruments were physically made.</li><li><b><i>literae Regiae … ad suam Maiestatem</b></i> — \"a royal letter … to His Majesty\": <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), summoning Bille to court. <i>a Regia Maiestate liberabor</i> = \"when I am freed from the Royal Majesty [i.e. from this royal summons / duty].\"</li><li><b><i>tuis Musis thura libando</b></i> — \"offering incense to your Muses,\" a humanist flourish for \"paying devout respects to your studies (at Uraniborg)\"; cf. the Tychonic sign-off idiom <i>sacris Uraniae lita</i> (protocol §5.5). Kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Latinity.</b> <i>claudier</i> — archaic/poetic passive infinitive (= <i>claudi</i>), \"to be closed off\"; rendered without flattening the deliberate archaism. <i>dispicies</i> (from <i>dispicio</i>) = \"you will look into / discern,\" not <i>despicies</i> \"you will despise.\"</li><li><b>Dateline crux (date as written vs. catalogue label).</b> The dateline reads <i>\"5 Nonis Maij Anno 1577\"</i> — literally \"5 [to the] Nones of May.\" The <b>Nones of May fall on 7 May</b>; reckoned in the Roman inclusive manner, <i>a. d. V Nonas Maias</i> (7, 6, 5, 4, <b>3</b>) = <b>3 May</b>. The dataset label <i>\"1577. 3. maj\"</i> is therefore <b>consistent</b> with the dateline once the Roman count is resolved, and the header gives 3 May (O.S.). The printed form <i>\"5 Nonis\"</i> (Arabic numeral + ablative <i>Nonis</i> rather than the regular <i>V Nonas</i>) is a non-classical chancery/transcription rendering of <i>a. d. V Nonas Maias</i>; it is preserved verbatim in the Source block and not normalised. No date is asserted in the header beyond what this reckoning substantiates. (Denmark used the Julian calendar [O.S.] throughout, per protocol §3.)</li><li><b>MS witnesses.</b> A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (shelfmarks as given in the source header).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the one resolved-but-noted point is the dateline form <i>\"5 Nonis Maij,\"</i> read as <i>a. d. V Nonas Maias</i> = 3 May (matching the catalogue label), with the anomalous numeral-plus-<i>Nonis</i> form preserved and explained rather than silently corrected.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole point ('5 Nonis Maij' = 3 May) is resolved and confirms the label; no crux, sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin letter from Steen Bille, including the opening couplet and every clause (the armillae, the mill, the royal summons), against TBOO VII p.45 page image. Text and English translation match Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Responsa ad variorum epistolas a. 1572-1576; incl. Epistolae Johannis Pratensis (fol. 11v)…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/7", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "https://digital.onb.ac.at/rep/access/open/BAG_5056515"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.7.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that the Ptolemaic armillae have been entrusted to the mill craftsman to be fitted according to Tycho's specification, despite others' objections about water use.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15770503001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15770521001", "d": "1577-05-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor og professorer ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor og professorer ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Rector and professors of the University of Copenhagen — Hven, 21 May 1577 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Bølling, D fol. 67</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO DOMINO RECTORI ET CLARISS. VIRIS, REGIAe ACADEMIAe HAFNIENSIS PROFESSORIBUS VENERANDIS, DOMINIS ET AMICIS PERPETUO OBSERVANDIS.<br>S.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine Rector, clarissimique viri, Hafniensis Academiae venerandi professores! Quod unanimi consensu, singulari vestro favore impellente, me nihil tale a vobis expectantem Rectoratus honoribus insignire et in Reipublicae vestrae litterariae per subsequens Annuum tempus Administratorem eligere non dubitaveritis, vobis omnibus et singulis reverenter habeo gratias. Ego vero praeterquam quod me tanto officio digne sustinendo minime idoneum agnoscam, ideoque electionis huius rationem vestrae potius benevolentiae et favori adscribendam censeam, quam quod revera tale aliquid a me praestari possit, gravibus insuper ijsque non paucis, hoc praesertim tempore, distrahor curis et negotijs, quo minus vestrae voluntati satisfacere et tam praeclaro officio rite obeundo vacare possim. Inprimis vero ob Architecturae in aedificiis constituendis occupationes, quibus in hac Insula, a veteri desolatione in habitabilem oram, Deo conatui nostro favente, revocanda, implicor. Quapropter reverenter et obnixe vestras Excellentias rogatas volo, ut, consideratis his negotijs, quibus hoc ipso tempore, quo vobis adesse oporteret, impedior, me a tanto munere obeundo, quod alias etiam nostris viribus impar est, excusatum habere velint. Imposterum vero, Dei nutu, liberato mihi ab hoc occupationum onere, si quid erit, quo mea tenuitas vestrae Academiae et vobis in quocunque officij genere usui esse possit, lubens omnique conatu pro virium mensura praestare et volo et debeo. Omnemque meam operam, quacunque in re litterariae Reipublicae vestraeque utilitati inservire possit, vobis perpetuo defero. Deus optimus et aeternus, autor omnis scientiae et veritatis, vos vestraque studia in suum honorem et publici boni utilitatem diu clementer conservet.</p><p>Valete.<br>Datae in Insula Huena Maij die 21. Anno Christi 1577.<br>Mag. et Exc. V. amantiss.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Magnificent Lord Rector and the most distinguished men, the venerable professors of the Royal University of Copenhagen, my lords and friends ever to be regarded — greeting.</b></p><p>Magnificent Lord Rector, and most distinguished men, venerable professors of the University of Copenhagen! Inasmuch as by unanimous consent, prompted by your singular favour, you did not hesitate to distinguish me — who expected no such thing from you — with the honours of the rectorship, and to elect me as Administrator of your literary commonwealth for the year following, I reverently give thanks to you, all and each. But I, for my part — besides that I acknowledge myself by no means fit to sustain so great an office worthily, and therefore judge that the reason for this election ought rather to be ascribed to your goodwill and favour than to any such thing being in truth performable by me — am moreover distracted, at this time especially, by grave cares and affairs, and those not few, so that I am the less able to satisfy your wish and to be free to discharge so illustrious an office duly. Above all [I am so distracted] on account of the occupations of architecture, in setting up buildings, in which I am entangled here on this Island — [the Island] to be recalled, from its old desolation, into a habitable shore, with God favouring our endeavour. Wherefore I would reverently and earnestly have Your Excellencies asked that, these affairs being considered — by which, at the very time when I ought to be present with you, I am hindered — they may be willing to hold me excused from discharging so great a charge, which besides is unequal to our powers. But hereafter, at God's nod, when I am freed from this burden of occupations, if there shall be anything wherein my slender means may be of use to your University and to you in any kind of service whatsoever, I both will and ought to perform it gladly and with every effort according to the measure of my strength. And I offer you in perpetuity all my labour, in whatever matter it may serve the advantage of the literary commonwealth and your own. May God, best and eternal, the author of all knowledge and truth, long and graciously preserve you and your studies, to His own honour and to the advantage of the public good.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given on the Island of Hven, the 21st day of May, in the year of Christ 1577.<br>Most loving toward Your Magnificences and Excellencies,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / occasion.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Hven</b> (Lat. <i>Huena</i>), declines the <b>rectorship of the University of Copenhagen</b> to which the rector and professors had elected him for the coming academic year. He pleads unfitness (a courtesy topos) and, more concretely, that he is consumed by the building works on Hven — Frederik II had granted him the island in 1576, and in 1577 the construction of <b>Uraniborg</b> was under way. The letter is the formal <i>recusatio</i> of an honour.</li><li><b>Recipients.</b> The collegium of the <i>Regia Academia Hafniensis</i> — the University of Copenhagen (founded 1479) — addressed through its sitting <b>Rector</b> (<i>Magnificus Dominus Rector</i>, the standard university style) and the body of professors.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Valete</i> → \"Farewell\" (plural). <i>Mag. et Exc. V. amantiss.</i> = <i>Magnificentiae et Excellentiae Vestrae amantissimus</i>, \"Most loving toward Your Magnificences and Excellencies\" — the closing courtesy keyed to the collective addressees (<i>Magnificentia</i> the rector's style, <i>Excellentia</i> the professors'); rendered in the plural to match the body's <i>vobis</i>.</li><li><b><i>Reipublicae … litterariae … Administratorem</b></i> — \"Administrator of your literary commonwealth\": the rectorship cast in the humanist trope of the university as a <i>res publica litteraria</i> (republic of letters); the rector being its annual <i>Administrator</i>. <i>per subsequens Annuum tempus</i> = \"for the year following\" (the one-year rectorial term).</li><li><b><i>Architecturae in aedificiis constituendis occupationes</b></i> — \"the occupations of architecture, in setting up buildings\": Tycho's Hven construction works (Uraniborg, begun 1576–77). Rendered literally; <i>Architectura</i> kept as \"architecture\" (building-craft).</li><li><b><i>a veteri desolatione in habitabilem oram … revocanda</b></i> — \"to be recalled, from its old desolation, into a habitable shore\": the island is the implied object (<i>Insula … revocanda</i>, gerundive of purpose/destiny). <i>ora</i> \"shore, edge, region\"; here the once-derelict island made habitable. The slight strain of <i>oram</i> (\"shore/region\") used of an island is the author's, kept rather than smoothed.</li><li><b><i>Deo conatui nostro favente</b></i> — \"with God favouring our endeavour\": ablative absolute; <i>conatui</i> dative governed by <i>favente</i>. One of three pious framings (<i>Deo … favente</i>; <i>Dei nutu</i>; the closing <i>Deus optimus et aeternus</i>).</li><li><b><i>quod alias etiam nostris viribus impar est</b></i> — \"which besides is unequal to our powers\": i.e. the office exceeds his strength even apart from the building works. <i>impar … viribus</i>, \"unequal to [his] strength.\"</li><li><b><i>mea tenuitas</b></i> — \"my slender means\": a self-deprecating abstract (\"my slenderness/lowliness\"), the conventional modesty formula; rendered \"my slender means.\"</li><li><b><i>Dei nutu</b></i> — \"at God's nod / by God's will\"; <i>nutus</i> the divine assent. Kept as \"at God's nod.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Maij die 21. Anno Christi 1577 … in Insula Huena</i> = \"21 May 1577, on the Island of Hven\" (<b>Old Style</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline agrees with the dataset label (<i>1577. 21. maj. Hven</i>); no discrepancy. <i>Huena</i> = <b>Hven</b> (the island in the Øresund granted to Tycho in 1576; site of Uraniborg).</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Humanist <i>-ij-</i> for <i>-ii-</i> (<i>negotijs, ijsque, officij</i>) and <i>Maij</i>; <i>autor</i> for <i>auctor</i>; capitalised honorific abstracts (<i>Rectoratus, Administratorem, Annuum</i>). No Greek, no symbols, no astrological or technical-instrument vocabulary in this letter.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; the language is plainly Neo-Latin, the text internally consistent, and the dateline (21 May 1577, O.S.) matches the catalogue label. The only points of authorial strain (the gerundive <i>Insulam … revocandam</i> governing <i>oram</i>, \"a habitable shore\") are noted, not smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer VII pp. 45-47). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin for this letter (Tycho's declination of the University of Copenhagen rectorship). Collated the full English against both source_text and the page images, checking every clause including the extended bracketed supplements, and the closing date 'Datae in Insula Huena Maij die 21. Anno Christi 1577.' No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Bølling, D fol. 67."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks the university for his election as rector and declines the office, citing personal unfitness and pressing obligations that prevent him from discharging its duties.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15770521001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15770920001", "d": "1577-09-20", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Major", "ck": "Johannes Major", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Johannes Major → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 20 September 1577 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOCTRINAQUE ET VIRTUTIBUS PRAeSTANTISSIMO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI DANO, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, DOMINO SUO OBSERVANTER COLENDO.</p><p>POST tuum Augusta discessum, generose Domine TYCHO, ad te semel litteras dedi, in quibus T. D. pro scripto de stella anno 1572 visa edito, mihique dono misso gratias egi. Deinde aliquid de voluntate SCHISLERI et Globi conficiendi ratione cuiuscunque magnitudinis subiunxi. Spero autem easdem litteras tibi esse redditas. De voluntate vero tua cum nihil adhuc certi cognoverim, pauca eadem de re tum cum affine meo PETRO tum cum N. GEMPERLIN pictore egi coram, ut de mea erga te benevolentia tibi quid certi constaret. Meretur enim virtus tua, ut tibi omnibus modis gratificetur. Quare cum singulari Dei beneficio ad excolendas artes Mathematicas, quas ipse impense amo, excitatus esse videaris, Deum Opt. Max. precor, ut tibi vitam largiatur diuturnam, quo laudatissimis artibus iam quidem nutantibus brevique casuris tuo ingenio quam felicissime succurrat. Quadrans, quem hîc in horto Domino Consuli magna industria extruxisti, adhuc constat et usui observationum coelestium inservit. Machina certe ingens, vixque ullibi nostro aevo similis reperitur, quam etiam magnus ille PETRUS RAMUS summopere, cum hîc et tecum esset, admiratus est. D. WOLFIUS tibi plurimam salutem ex me dicere voluit: ipse scripsisset, nisi publica negotia illi impedimento fuissent et affinis meus non cum summa festinatione abitum parasset.</p><p>Vale feliciter.<br>Augustae Rheticae pridie Matthaei Apostoli 1577.<br>T. D. deditiss.<br>M. Iohan. Maior.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and, in learning and in virtues, most excellent Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup, his lord to be honoured with respect.</b></p><p>After your departure from Augsburg, noble Master Tycho, I once sent you a letter, in which I gave thanks to Your Lordship for the writing on the star seen in the year 1572, published and sent to me as a gift. Then I added something concerning Schisler's intention and the method of constructing a globe of any size whatever. I hope, moreover, that that same letter has been delivered to you. But since I have as yet learned nothing certain about your wishes, I have dealt face to face a little on the same matter, both with my kinsman Peter and with N. Gemperlin the painter, so that something certain about my goodwill toward you might be established for you. For your virtue deserves that one should oblige you in every way. Therefore, since by God's singular favour you seem to have been roused to the cultivation of the mathematical arts, which I myself ardently love, I pray to God, Best and Greatest, that He grant you a long life, whereby He may most happily succour, through your genius, those most praiseworthy arts that are now indeed faltering and soon to fall. The quadrant which you built here in the garden for the Lord Consul, with great industry, still stands and serves the use of celestial observations. A mighty machine, to be sure, and scarcely anywhere is its like to be found in our age — which that great Peter Ramus, too, when he was here and with you, exceedingly admired. Master Wolf wished me to convey his heartiest greeting to you: he would have written himself, had public affairs not been a hindrance to him, and had my kinsman not prepared his departure in the greatest haste.</p><p>Fare you happily well.<br>At Augsburg in Rhaetia, the day before [the feast of] Matthew the Apostle, 1577.<br>Most devoted to Your Lordship,<br>Master Johann Major.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>M. Iohan. Maior</i> = <b>Johann Major</b> (Magister), a mathematics-minded Augsburg correspondent within Tycho's circle from his 1569–70 Augsburg sojourn. The <i>M.</i> is the academic <i>Magister</i> (\"Master,\" §5.5); <i>T. D.</i> = <i>Tua Dominatio</i> (\"Your Lordship\") and <i>T. D. deditiss.</i> = <i>Tuae Dominationi deditissimus</i> (\"most devoted to Your Lordship\"), expanded in the translation.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI DANO, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP</i> — \"Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup.\" <i>Ottonides</i> is the Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father (cf. brahe-t_15711113001); <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp, Scania) is the Brahe family seat.</li><li><b><i>scriptum de stella anno 1572 visa</b></i> — \"the writing on the star seen in the year 1572\": <b>Tycho's</b> <i>De nova stella</i> <b>(Copenhagen, 1573)</b>, his account of the 1572 supernova in Cassiopeia, which he had sent Major as a gift.</li><li><b><i>Schisler</b></i>. <b>Christoph Schissler (Schissler the Elder, c. 1531–1608)</b>, the celebrated Augsburg maker of mathematical and astronomical instruments; the letter relays his \"intention\" (<i>voluntas</i>) and a scheme for constructing a <b>globe</b> (<i>globus</i>, §5.2) of arbitrary size. The reading of the proper name follows the source; identification is editorial.</li><li><b><i>affinis meus Petrus</b></i> — \"my kinsman Peter\": <i>affinis</i> denotes a relative by marriage (an in-law); the man is named only as Peter and is not further identified here.</li><li><b><i>N. Gemperlin pictor</b></i> — \"N. Gemperlin the painter\": <b>Georg Gemperle(in)</b> (or a relative), an Augsburg painter; the initial <i>N.</i> stands for an unsupplied forename (Latin <i>Nomen</i>/<i>nescio quis</i> convention), left as \"N.\" rather than expanded.</li><li><b><i>Quadrans … in horto Domino Consuli … extruxisti</b></i> — \"the quadrant which you built in the garden for the Lord Consul\": the <b>great quadrant</b> Tycho erected at Augsburg in 1569–70 in the garden of <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (the <i>Consul</i>, i.e. a burgomaster of Augsburg), the c. 19-foot (5–6 m) instrument described in Tycho's <i>Mechanica</i>. <i>Consul</i> is the Latin term for the city's chief magistrate (Bürgermeister); rendered \"Consul\" with the office flagged here. <i>Quadrans</i> → <b>quadrant</b> (§5.2).</li><li><b><i>Machina … ingens</b></i> — \"a mighty machine\": <i>machina</i> here = the large instrument (the quadrant), in the period sense of a great engineered structure; rendered \"machine\" with the sense noted.</li><li><b><i>Petrus Ramus</b></i> — <b>Petrus Ramus (Pierre de la Ramée, 1515–1572)</b>, the French humanist and logician, who visited Augsburg and met Tycho there (c. 1570) and admired the Hainzel quadrant; he was killed in the St Bartholomew's Day massacre, August 1572, hence \"that great Peter Ramus\" is spoken of as deceased.</li><li><b><i>D. Wolfius</b></i> — <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (cf. brahe-t_15711113001), here sending greetings via Major.</li><li><b>Astronomy / instruments.</b> <i>artes Mathematicae</i> → \"the mathematical arts\"; <i>observationum coelestium</i> → \"celestial observations\" (cf. <i>motus</i>, <i>altitudo</i>, §5.1); <i>quadrans</i> and <i>globus</i> per §5.2.</li><li><b><i>laudatissimis artibus iam quidem nutantibus brevique casuris … succurrat</b></i> — the hope that Tycho's genius may \"succour those most praiseworthy arts, now faltering and soon to fall\": the author's own hedged, elegiac framing of the state of the mathematical sciences is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Augustae Rheticae pridie Matthaei Apostoli 1577</i> = \"at <b>Augsburg in Rhaetia</b>, the day before [the feast of] <b>Matthew the Apostle</b>, 1577.\" The feast of St Matthew the Apostle is <b>21 September</b>; <i>pridie</i> (\"the day before\") therefore = <b>20 September</b> (O.S.; Denmark and the Empire alike were still on the Julian calendar in 1577). The Roman-style ecclesiastical dateline is kept and glossed per §3; the as-written date (20 September) agrees with the dataset label (\"1577. 20. september. Augsburg\"). <i>Augusta Rhaetica</i> is a variant of <i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i> = <b>Augsburg</b> (§5.7).</li><li><b>MS / edition.</b> MS witness as given: Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (the same Vienna codex of Tycho's incoming correspondence as the Wolf letter). Edition of record per the source's `edition` field: Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> VII (<i>Epistolae</i>).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the dateline is unambiguous. Minor uncertainty attaches only to the prosopography of the lesser-named figures: <i>affinis meus Petrus</i> (the kinsman \"Peter,\" unidentified), <i>N. Gemperlin</i> (forename unsupplied), and the precise identity behind <i>Schisler</i>/Schissler and the <i>Consul</i> (taken as Paul Hainzel) — all flagged above rather than asserted with false precision.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.46-48 page images. Letter begins mid-p.46 after a prior Tycho letter dated 21 May 1577, runs through p.47 to the closing on p.48 ('Vale feliciter. Augustae Rheticae pridie Matthaei Apostoli 1577. T.D. deditiss. M. Iohan. Maior.'). Collated all proper names (Schisleri, Petro, N. Gemperlin, Petrus Ramus, D. Wolfius) and the date (pridie Matthaei Apostoli = 20 Sept.) against the page image; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Major", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15760802001", "next": "brahe-t_15840608001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840715001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports a prior letter of thanks for Tycho's treatise on the 1572 star, and describes discussions with Schissler and Gemperlin regarding the construction of a globe.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15770920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15771114001", "d": "1577-11-14", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Petrus Severinus", "ck": "Petrus Severinus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Petrus Severinus (Peder Sørensen) — Copenhagen, 14 November 1577 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Nye Samlinger til den danske Historie </i>(reprinted in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD D. PETRUM SEVERINI.</p><p>...... haec ego ad te perscribere volui, ut et tu huius Cometae si non instrumentalem saltem ocularem et physicam observationem instituere possis, et apud Regem efficere, ne forte ego hinc avocer interim, dum Cometa fulget, ut plenam eius observationem per instrumenta, quae hîc solum in promtu habeo, perficere possim. Si vero, antequam servus meus has tibi reddiderit, id quod caveri vellem, factum est, da operam, ut Regis consensu per alias literas infectum fieri possit.</p><p>Vale et saluta amicos.<br>Datae Haffniae 14. Nov. 1577.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Master Peder Sørensen [Petrus Severinus].</b></p><p>...... this I wished to write out to you, so that you too may set up an observation of this comet — if not an instrumental one, then at least a visual and physical one — and may bring it about with the King that I not perchance be called away from here in the meantime, while the comet shines, so that I may carry through a full observation of it by means of the instruments which I have ready to hand only here. But if, before my servant shall have delivered this [letter] to you, the very thing I should wish to be guarded against has [already] been done, then take pains that, with the King's consent, it may be undone by another letter.</p><p>Farewell, and greet our friends.<br>Given at Copenhagen, 14 November 1577.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / dateline.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Haffnia</i>) on 14 November 1577 (O.S.). The dataset label and the dateline agree; the header date is the as-written date. Tycho subscribes first with the initial \"T.\" and then his full name, both reproduced.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>D. Petrum Severini</i> = <b>Peder Sørensen</b>, Latinized <b>Petrus Severinus</b> (1542–1602), the Danish Paracelsian physician and theorist (<i>Idea medicinae philosophicae</i>, Basel 1571), at this date a royal physician (<i>medicus regius</i>) close to <b>Frederik II</b> — which is precisely why Tycho asks him to intercede <i>apud Regem</i> (\"with the King\"). <i>D.</i> = <i>Dominus</i>, rendered \"Master\" per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Fragment.</b> The source is a <b>fragment</b>: it begins mid-sentence after a row of points (<i>\"...... haec ego\"</i>), so the lost antecedent of <i>haec</i> (\"these things / this\") — evidently Tycho's preceding report of his own observations of the comet — is not in the text of record. The leading ellipsis is preserved; no opening salutation survives. No MS witness is recorded in the source; the <i>Nye Samlinger</i> printing is the text of record (later reprinted by Dreyer).</li><li><b>Occasion — the comet of 1577.</b> <i>huius Cometae</i> = \"this comet\" is the <b>Great Comet of 1577</b> (C/1577 V1), Tycho's observations of which (showing it to lie beyond the Moon, i.e. in the celestial, supralunary region) became the foundation of his 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>. The letter is thus contemporaneous with the comet's apparition (it was conspicuous in November 1577).</li><li><b>Technical terms.</b> <i>instrumentalem … observationem</i> / <i>per instrumenta</i> → \"an instrumental observation\" / \"by means of the instruments\" (<i>instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b>, §5.2); <i>ocularem et physicam observationem</i> → \"a visual and physical observation,\" i.e. a naked-eye observation of the comet's appearance and physical character (tail, colour, motion) as opposed to a measured <i>instrumental</i> one. <i>physica</i> is rendered \"physical\" (its period sense: of the body's nature/appearance), not \"in physics\"; flagged so the contrast with <i>instrumentalem</i> is not flattened.</li><li><b>Negations / legal force (checked).</b> Two negations in the source, both reproduced exactly: (1) <i>si non instrumentalem saltem …</i> = \"if not an instrumental one, then at least …\" (the <i>non … saltem</i> concessive pair); (2) <i>ne forte ego hinc avocer</i> = \"that I not perchance be called away from here\" — a negative purpose clause (<i>ne</i>) governing Tycho's request, <b>not</b> an assertion that he will be called away. No negation added or dropped.</li><li><b>Conditional close.</b> <i>Si vero … id quod caveri vellem, factum est, da operam, ut … infectum fieri possit</i> — a real (indicative) condition: \"But if … the very thing I should wish to be guarded against has [already] been done, then take pains that … it may be undone.\" <i>caveri vellem</i> keeps Tycho's own hedged optative (\"the thing I should wish guarded against\") rather than naming the feared event, which the fragment leaves implicit (apparently an order summoning him away from Copenhagen/Hven while the comet was visible). <i>infectum fieri</i> = \"to be made undone / annulled\" (legal-administrative idiom: to rescind by a further letter, <i>per alias literas</i>, with the King's consent). The referent of the feared \"thing\" is not stated in the surviving text; it is left implicit rather than supplied.</li><li><b>Closing formula.</b> <i>Vale et saluta amicos</i> = \"Farewell, and greet our friends\" (§5.5). <i>Datae Haffniae</i> = \"Given at Copenhagen.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the negations/conditional are reproduced exactly; the one genuine soft point is the unstated referent of <i>id quod caveri vellem</i> (\"the thing I should wish guarded against\"), which the fragmentary opening leaves implicit and which is therefore flagged, not supplied.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.47-48 (page images). The Tycho-to-Severinus excerpt is printed on p.47 under 'TYCHO BRAHE AD D. PETRUM SEVERINI.', matching source_text word-for-word including the opening ellipsis. Checked the conditional/subjunctive structure (observation instructions, contingency on the servant's delivery) and the closing date (14 Nov. 1577) against the translation — accurate throughout, no hedge lost or hardened.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Petrus Severinus", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15760903001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Urges Severinus to observe the current comet and to intercede with the King to prevent Tycho being called away before he completes his instrumental observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15771114001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15780509001", "d": "1578-05-09", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 9 May 1578 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ET NATALIUM SPLENDORE ET INGENIJ PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DN. TYCHONI BRAHAeO OTTONIDI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO COLENDO.<br>S.</p><p>REDIERUNT e Dania vestra, Nobilissime Domine TYCHO, superiore anno GEMPERLINUS, nuper Aurifaber, quorum neuter me accessit, uterque tamen per alios salutem mihi tuo nomine dixit. Etsi autem speraveram te pro necessitudine nostra ne absentis quidem mei memoriam abiecturum, tamen cum astra non quidem exitium (id enim a religione nostra et saniore Philosophia esset alienum) sed vitae saltem periculum superiore anno, te autore, mihi portenderint, minus miror, me utcunque viventem in mortuis abs te numerari, si forte praedictio illa apud te mollior fuit, quam mihi tunc indicare voluisti, ideoque supervacaneis litteris supersedisse. Memineris tamen, Ascendens meum necdum Saturni Anaretae oppositum attigisse, quod ex tua quoque supputatione circa annum aetatis meae 65 demum eveniet, quem etiam ob id ex Astrologicis ratiocinijs fatalem mihi esse insinuasti, a quo, ut conscius sis, si forte mea aetas et nostra colloquia tibi memoria exciderunt, adhuc triennio absum. Aiebas etiam fieri posse, ut directionem Horoscopi ad Trinum suum proprium circa annum 68 incidentem assequerer, plerumque id concedi ijs, qui Aquarium ascendentem obtinent, mihi revelasti, quando de his et similibus familiariter et iucunde sermones miscuimus, atque e thesauris tuis Astrologicis, quos alias in abscondito habes, mihi quaedam impartiri voluisti, quorum etiamnum magna animi voluptate recordor. Quare cum adhuc et vivam et qualitercunque valeam, quod te non ignorare arbitror, qui tam probe meam Genesin et hinc pendentia fata nosti, quatenus humanae subiacent industriae, nullatenus eam tibi inhumanitatem tribuo, ut me, quamvis pauperem et genere ignobilem, sed a Musis ditatum et nobilitatum asperneris, vel superbiam, ut secundis rebus elatus, neminem tenuioris fortunae hominem amicitia tua dignum existimes, vel calliditatem, ut benevolentiam pro tempore ex loco simules. Sed facessant haec omnia, indigna generosa natura, qualem esse tuam e diutina nostra conversatione expertus sum, pleneque persuasum habeo. De me autem sic existimes velim, me tibi et viventem et mortuum fore amicum.</p><p>Vale.<br>Augustae Vindelicorum. 9. Maij Anno 1578.<br>Hieronymus Wolfius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To a man most pre-eminent both in the splendour of his birth and in the excellence of his genius, Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, his lord and friend to be honoured.</b><br><b>Greeting.</b></p><p>There returned from your Denmark last year Gemperlinus, lately a goldsmith — neither of whom came to me, yet each, through others, conveyed a greeting to me in your name. And although I had hoped that, given the bond between us, you would not cast off the memory even of me in my absence, yet since the stars portended to me last year — on your authority — not indeed destruction (for that would be foreign to our religion and to a sounder philosophy), but at least a danger to life, I wonder the less that I, living however I may, am reckoned among the dead by you, if perhaps that prediction was milder with you than you then chose to disclose to me, and that you therefore forbore from a superfluous letter. Yet you should recall that my ascendant has not yet reached the opposition of Saturn the Anareta, which by your reckoning too will come about only around my sixty-fifth year of age — which year, for that very reason, you intimated from astrological reasonings to be fatal to me; and from that year, so that you may be aware (in case perhaps my age and our conversations have slipped your memory), I am still three years distant. You also said it could come to pass that I should attain the direction of the horoscope to its own proper trine, falling around the sixty-eighth year; you revealed to me that this is for the most part granted to those who have Aquarius as the ascendant — when we mingled discourse about these and like matters in a familiar and pleasant way, and you were willing to impart to me certain things out of your astrological treasures, which otherwise you keep hidden, things which even now I recall with great delight of mind. Wherefore, since I both still live and am, however that may be, in health — which I judge you do not fail to know, you who so thoroughly know my geniture and the fates that hang from it, in so far as they lie subject to human industry — I in no way ascribe to you that inhumanity, that you should spurn me, poor though I am and of ignoble birth but enriched and ennobled by the Muses; nor that arrogance, that, exalted by good fortune, you should judge no man of slenderer means worthy of your friendship; nor that craftiness, that you should feign goodwill for the time, as the place requires. But away with all these things, unworthy of a generous nature such as I have found yours to be from our long converse, and of which I am fully persuaded. As for me, I would have you so judge: that I shall be your friend both living and dead.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Augsburg, 9 May, the year 1578.<br>Hieronymus Wolf.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (the \"father of Byzantine studies\"), then directing the city's library and school; an early mentor-friend of Tycho's from his Augsburg years (1569–70). The same correspondent as the 13 Nov. 1571 letter (brahe-t_15711113001), and continuing its themes — Wolf's own horoscope as cast by Tycho, and his astrologically-feared old age.</li><li><b><i>Ottonides</b></i> — \"son of Otte,\" a Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father (kept, as in the 1571 letter).</li><li><b><i>Gemperlinus, nuper Aurifaber</b></i> — \"Gemperlinus, lately a goldsmith\": <b>personal crux, flagged.</b> The Latin reads as one or two travellers returned from Denmark. <i>Gemperlinus</i> is most plausibly the Augsburg painter <b>Abdon (or Tobias) Gemperle / Gämperlin</b>, of the Augsburg artist family; <i>nuper Aurifaber</i> (\"lately/recently a goldsmith\") is left as a literal apposition rather than resolved into a named second man — the syntax (<i>quorum neuter … uterque</i>, \"neither of whom … each\") requires <b>two</b> persons, but the text names only the one and characterises the other only by trade. The pairing is recorded, not invented; do not assert a single identity for \"Aurifaber.\"</li><li><b><i>necessitudine nostra</b></i> — \"the bond/tie between us\" (a close personal connection), rendered \"the bond between us.\"</li><li><b>Astrology.</b> The letter is built on the natal figure Tycho had cast for Wolf (cf. the 1571 letter):</li><li><i>Ascendens meum necdum Saturni Anaretae oppositum attigisse</i> — \"my ascendant has not yet reached the opposition of Saturn the <b>Anareta</b>.\" <i>Anareta</i> (ἀναιρέτης, \"the destroyer/killer\") is the technical astrological term for the planet that, by direction, marks the native's death; here Saturn is identified as Wolf's anaretic planet. The term is kept (Latinised <i>Anareta</i>) and glossed; <i>oppositum</i> rendered \"opposition\" per §5.1.</li><li><i>directionem Horoscopi ad Trinum suum proprium</i> — \"the <b>direction</b> of the horoscope to its own proper <b>trine</b>\" (a predictive <i>direction</i>, §5.3; <i>Trinum</i> = the trine aspect, §5.3 <i>aspectus</i>; <i>Horoscopi</i> here = the ascendant/natal degree). Kept as a directional technique.</li><li><i>Aquarium ascendentem</i> — \"Aquarius as the <b>ascendant</b>\" (§5.3); zodiac sign named, no glyph in source.</li><li><i>Genesin … et hinc pendentia fata</i> — \"my <b>geniture</b> and the fates that hang from it\" (<i>genesis</i> → geniture, §5.3).</li><li><i>e thesauris tuis Astrologicis, quos alias in abscondito habes</i> — \"out of your astrological treasures, which otherwise you keep hidden\": Wolf alludes to Tycho's reticence in sharing his astrological methods.</li><li><b>Theological hedge — preserved.</b> <i>astra non quidem exitium (id enim a religione nostra et saniore Philosophia esset alienum) sed vitae saltem periculum … portenderint</i> — Wolf is careful to deny that the stars portend outright <i>destruction</i> (which would offend \"our religion and a sounder philosophy\"), only a <i>danger to life</i>. The qualifier is rendered intact, not sharpened into a flat prediction of death.</li><li><b>Age arithmetic (consistent, recorded not corrected).</b> Wolf (b. 1516) writes in 1578 at age 62; he says the anaretic year is his 65th and that he is <i>\"still three years distant\"</i> (<i>adhuc triennio absum</i>) from it — 62 + 3 = 65. Internally consistent. The further <i>directio</i> to the trine he places \"around the sixty-eighth year.\" No date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Sub-text.</b> The letter's occasion is Wolf's gentle reproach that Tycho had fallen silent — Wolf surmising that Tycho, believing his own death-prediction, had written him off \"among the dead\" (<i>in mortuis … numerari</i>) and so spared a \"superfluous letter.\" The closing protestation (<i>me tibi et viventem et mortuum fore amicum</i>, \"I shall be your friend both living and dead\") answers that surmise.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Augustae Vindelicorum. 9. Maij Anno 1578</i> = \"at <b>Augsburg</b>, 9 May 1578\" (O.S.). The dataset label (\"1578. 9. maj. Augsburg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the astrological apparatus is consistent; the one open point is the <i>Gemperlinus … nuper Aurifaber</i> clause, where the source names one traveller and characterises a second only by trade while the grammar (<i>neuter … uterque</i>) demands two persons — flagged above and left unresolved rather than smoothed into a single named identity.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages visually. The letter (Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho, headed 'ET NATALIUM SPLENDORE ET INGENIJ PRAESTANTISSIMO VIRO...') runs from the foot of p.47 through p.48, citation 'Ex apographo in codice Vindobonensi lat. 10686^66'. Collated the full Latin body word-for-word against Dreyer's print: identical throughout apart from ae/ligature normalization. All numbers checked (annum aetatis 65, annum 68, triennio, 9 Maij 1578) match; names (Gemperlinus, Tycho Brahaeus Ottonidis, Hieronymus Wolfius) match; place (Augustae Vindelicorum/Augsburg) matches. The odd Latin plural 'quorum neuter...uterque' referring to the single named 'Gemperlinus' is present identically in Dreyer's own print, not a translator artifact, and the English faithfully preserves rather than 'fixes' that ambiguity. Note: source was self-graded B (uncollated;) our collation against Dreyer finds it fully sound, warranting A.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15750307001", "next": "brahe-t_15790000001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Notes Tycho's failure to write after Gemperlinus's return from Denmark, and reflects on the astrological prediction of danger to life Tycho had made for him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15780509001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15781204001", "d": "1578-12-04", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 4 December 1578 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (as given in the dataset; see apparatus)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S. P.</p><p>PRODIJT catalogus librorum Francfurti ad Moenum venalium, inter ceteros hunc invenio: Observatio et demonstratio Cometae aetherei, qui anno 1577 et 1578 constitutus in sphaera ♀ apparuit, autore M. MICHAËLE MAeSTLINO, Tubingae apud GEORGIUM GROPENBACHIUM. Hunc nondum videre contigit, quod si ad manus meas pervenerit, curabo, ut ad te mittatur, vel eo potissimum nomine, ut si forte eam methodum, easque rationes et observandi et demonstrandi sequeretur, quas tu approbas, ut tibi constet, in quibus, et quantum eis tribuendum iudices et in quibus dissentias. Porro cum multi in eodem genere laborârint, ut hinc inde edita scripta testantur, sumeret sibi forte quis laborem aliorum sententiam refellendi, id quod mihi minime probatur, cum rei propositae vera demonstratione ex Geometricis fundamentis eruta, et observationum certitudinis confirmatione, abunde secus sentientes confutentur. Tu interim in tuis observationibus elabora et profer in lucem, quod pluteum caedat et demorsos sapiat ungues.</p><p>Nostras observationes non mitto. Cum enim eas ad Geometriam, tanquam ad Lydium lapidem revocarem, et animadverterem, aliquid in ijs desiderari, abieci aliquo, ut ipse nesciam ubi lateant. Hoc tamen satis recordor me nullam sensibilem parallaxin hinc colligere potuisse, sed calculum potius in contrarium abijsse, id, quod miratus sum, reluctante nostro ARISTOTELE.</p><p>Tu interim bene vale, et me mutuo ama.<br>Rostochij IV. Decem.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>A catalogue of the books for sale at Frankfurt am Main has come out, and among the rest I find this one: <i>Observation and Demonstration of the Aetherial Comet, which, established in the sphere of ♀ Venus, appeared in the year 1577 and 1578</i>, by the author Master Michael Maestlin, at Tübingen at the press of Georg Gruppenbach. It has not yet fallen to me to see it; but if it comes into my hands, I will see to it that it is sent on to you — chiefly for this reason, that if perhaps he were to follow that method, and those principles both of observing and of demonstrating, which you approve, it may be clear to you in which points, and how much you judge is to be allowed to them, and in which you disagree. Furthermore, since many have laboured in the same kind, as the writings published here and there bear witness, someone might perhaps take upon himself the labour of refuting the opinion of others — a thing of which I by no means approve, since, once the true demonstration of the matter proposed has been drawn out from geometrical foundations, and confirmed by the certainty of observations, those who think otherwise are abundantly confuted [thereby]. You, meanwhile, work away at your own observations, and bring forth into the light something that may be carved on the bookcase and savour of nails bitten [to the quick].</p><p>Our own observations I do not send. For when I called them back to Geometry, as to a touchstone, and noticed that something was wanting in them, I threw them aside somewhere — so that I myself do not know where they lie hidden. Yet this much I well recall: that I was unable to gather any perceptible parallax from them, but rather the calculation went off to the contrary — at which I marvelled, our Aristotle resisting [it].</p><p>You, meanwhile, fare you well, and love me in return.<br>At Rostock, 4 December.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (also Brucäus; 1531–1593), Flemish-born professor of mathematics and medicine at the University of Rostock and Tycho's former teacher there; a regular correspondent. The Mosley biography (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) places this letter precisely: \"towards the end of 1578 he had written that, having noticed Michael Maestlin's recent cometary [treatise in the fair-catalogue, he would forward it].\"</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>The book.</b> <i>Observatio et demonstratio Cometae aetherei … autore M. Michaële Maestlino, Tubingae apud Georgium Gropenbachium</i> = Michael Maestlin's <i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae aetherei, qui anno 1577 et 1578 … apparuit</i> (Tübingen: <b>Georg Gruppenbach</b> [Latin <i>Gropenbachius</i>], 1578). <i>M.</i> before the name = <i>Magister</i> (\"Master,\" academic; protocol §5.5). The title is given as a quotation of the fair-catalogue entry; rendered in English with the Latin work-title-sense preserved. <b>Maestlin</b> = Michael Maestlin (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher), who like Tycho treated the 1577 comet as supralunary, but did so within a Copernican frame, associating the comet with the <b>sphere of Venus</b> — exactly the point of the <i>in sphaera ♀</i> phrase.</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> <i>in sphaera <b>♀</b></i> — the planetary glyph for <b>Venus</b>; named in the translation, glyph kept in the source block (protocol §5.3).</li><li><b><i>catalogus librorum Francfurti ad Moenum venalium</b></i> — the printed catalogue of books offered for sale at the <b>Frankfurt am Main</b> book fair (the <i>Messkatalog</i>), Brucaeus's source for the new title.</li><li><b>Astronomy / technical.</b> <i>parallaxin</i> = <b>parallax</b> (protocol §5.1): Brucaeus reports that his own reduction of his comet observations yielded <b>no perceptible parallax</b> — i.e. pointed to a supralunary (aetherial), not a sublunary, comet. <i>ad Geometriam, tanquam ad Lydium lapidem revocarem</i> — \"I called them back to Geometry, as to a <b>touchstone</b>\" (the <i>Lydius lapis</i>, the Lydian stone used to assay gold; a stock metaphor for a decisive test); kept and glossed. <i>vera demonstratione ex Geometricis fundamentis eruta</i> — \"the true demonstration drawn out from geometrical foundations,\" Brucaeus's (and Tycho's) methodological creed: a sound geometrical-observational demonstration confutes opponents without polemic.</li><li><b><i>reluctante nostro Aristotele</b></i> — \"our Aristotle resisting [it]\": the no-parallax result contradicted Aristotelian physics, in which comets are sublunary meteorological phenomena (and would therefore show parallax). \"Our Aristotle\" marks Brucaeus's own Aristotelian allegiance; he registers surprise (<i>miratus sum</i>) that his calculation ran against the doctrine. The participle is left as an ablative absolute (\"Aristotle resisting\") rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Allusion.</b> <i>profer in lucem, quod pluteum caedat et demorsos sapiat ungues</i> — a learned <b>Horatian</b> allusion (cf. <i>Satires</i> I.10.70–71, on painstaking composition: the poet who \"scratches his head and bites his nails to the quick\"; <i>pluteus</i> = the writing-desk/bookrest, also the board scored in revision). The sense: produce something so finely worked that it bears the marks of laborious, polished craftsmanship. Rendered close to the Latin (\"that may be carved on the bookcase and savour of nails bitten [to the quick]\") with the figure preserved rather than paraphrased away; <i>[to the quick]</i> bracketed as an interpretive completion of <i>demorsos</i>.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Tu interim bene vale, et me mutuo ama</i> = \"You, meanwhile, fare you well [<i>bene vale</i>, protocol §5.5], and love me in return\" — a standard humanist epistolary close.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Rostochij</i> = <b>Rostock</b> (protocol §5.7), the seat of the university and of Brucaeus.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>IV. Decem.</i> = <b>4 December</b>, given without year in the dateline; the dataset label supplies <b>1578</b>, consistent with the Frankfurt-fair / Maestlin-1578 content. Denmark and the Empire were on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1578 (the Gregorian reform came in 1582); dated O.S. accordingly. No year is asserted in the header beyond what the catalogue date and internal content (the 1578 Maestlin imprint) substantiate.</li><li><b>MS witness — flag.</b> The dataset records witness A as <i>cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</i> — <b>the same shelfmark/folio carried for the 1571 Wolf letter</b> in this corpus. This is very likely a dataset propagation artifact rather than a true codicological fact (two distinct letters cannot occupy the identical leaf). The shelfmark is reported here exactly <b>as given</b>, but should be verified against Dreyer's apparatus before citation; not silently corrected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the astronomical/methodological substance (no-parallax → supralunary comet, against \"our Aristotle\") is unambiguous. Two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the exact register of the Horatian <i>pluteum caedat … demorsos … ungues</i> tag, here kept figurative; (2) the witness shelfmark, which duplicates the Wolf letter's and is suspect as recorded.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.48-50 page images. Letter begins mid-p.48 ('HENRICUS BRUCAEUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE') and ends on p.49 ('Rostochij IV. Decem.'). Collated the book title and imprint (Observatio et demonstratio Cometae aetherei..., M. Michaele Maestlino, Tubingae apud Georgium Gropenbachium/Gruppenbach) and all proper names (Aristotele) against the page image; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. The Latinized printer name 'Gropenbachium' is correctly de-Latinized to the historical 'Gruppenbach' in translation, not a defect. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15761208001", "next": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports finding Maestlin's book on the 1577 comet in the Frankfurt catalogue and offers to forward it, discussing whether Tycho's own demonstration should supersede refutation of others.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15781204001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790000001", "d": "1579-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hieronymus Wolf — Hven, 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD HIERONYMUM WOLFIUM.</p><p>S. REDDITAe mihi sunt literae tuae, doctissime et amantissime Domine WOLFI, quas circa finem Martij huius anni ad me dedisti, quae non minimum mihi attulerunt voluptatem, quod ex ijs te pro ea aetatis et consuetae valetudinis invalida ratione salvum et incolumem esse intelligerem. Scripta vero eorum, qui de Cometa aliquid in Germania publicârunt, quae tunc temporis a WILLERO tam cito habere non potuisti, a Domino PAULO HAINZELIO recepi, in quibus prae caeteris placet MAeSTLINI diligentia et proxime ad veritatem accedens observatio eique innixa Mathematica demonstratio. Nec dubito, quin is, si sumtibus ad instrumenta metallica magna et exquisita conficienda instructus esset, aliquid egregij ex observationibus in restituendis et corrigendis motibus coelestium corporum moliretur. In Apogei certe anticipatione et Excentricitatis Solis augmento, tum etiam octavae Sphaerae celeriori, quam existimat COPERNICUS, promotione, quantum ex ijs, quae HAINZELIUS communicavit, percipio, a nostris inventis proximeque elapsis, aliquot annis sedulo factis observationibus non multum discrepat, ut latius ad Dominum HAINZELIUM perscripsi. Ephemerides eius, vel si quae alia ab ipso publicata sunt, ad me transmittas velim. Et, si is aliqua familiaritate tibi coniunctus est, ipsum etsi mihi ignotum meo nomine salutes quaeso et ab ipso impetres, ut per occasionem mihi scribat, suasque observationes, si quas habet exactiores, mecum (si ita libuerit) communicet; ego vicissim idem ipsi praestiturus sum et ipsius conatus, quacunque ratione licuerit, promovere studebo. Si vero interea aliquot Scripta de Cometa illo adeptus es praeter ea, quae ad me misit Dn. HAINZELIUS, peto ut mihi illa, qualiacunque sint, transmittere non graveris. Ego propter varias occupationes tam Regis, quam amicorum nomine incidentes, inprimis vero propter aedificationem et oeconomiae novae instituendae molestias, quibus implicor, ea quae de hoc Cometa concepi, et quae ex observationibus certis demonstrare satago, nondum absolvere potui. Spero tamen per otium hybernum, Deo volente, me id effecturum, ubi et alia quaedam, quae Astronomiae erratis corrigendis opportuna sunt, elaborare animus est. Huic enim studio vacare inter plurimas alias occupationes non intermittam, et instrumenta huic negotio idonea partim parata habeo, partim indies parari curo, quae et magnitudine et habilitate et diligenti elaboratione mechanicae veterum et recentiorum Astronomiae tractationi, sumtu magno, labore diuturno, confectione exquisita minime cessura confido. Aedificationis vero nostrae structuram ita adornavi, ut his utendis sideribusque commode in ea observandis quam maxime competat. Eam enim ob causam in hanc Insulam secessi, ut Philosophiae et Astronomicis exercitijs libere operam navare possim; et quanquam praeteritis proxime annis ob aedificationum molestias nonnihil in his strenue exequendis impeditus fuerim, tamen spero, me in posterum (si Deus vitam et vires suppeditaverit) maiori commoditate, quam interea paro, id recompensaturum. Haec ad te perscribere hoc tempore placuit, ne hic doctissimus vir Μ. ANDREAS SEVERINI mihi familiaritate iunctus ad te pari ratione mihi devinctum sine nostris literis veniret. Is enim, cum se causa lustrandi quasdam in Germania Bibliothecas per aliquod tempus discurrere velle proposuisset, Augustam etiam ad vos se abiturum aiebat. Peto itaque, ut si qua in re ipsius desideria promovere possis, efficias, ut sentiat, te non solum TYCHONIS, sed eius insuper amici amicum esse. Bene vale carissime WOLFI, et quavis data occasione mihi scribere et quod saltem licet, calamo et charta gratam amicitiae nostrae memoriam conservare ne intermittas; ego vicissim nullam occasionem frustra praeterlabi sinam, quin intellifas, me tui perpetuo amantissimum permanere.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime et diutissime vale, mi carissime WOLFI.<br>Datae in Insula nostra Huaena 1579.<br>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Hieronymus Wolf.</b></p><p>Greeting. Your letter has been delivered to me, most learned and most loving Master Wolf, which you sent me around the end of March of this year; and it brought me no small pleasure, in that from it I understood you to be safe and unharmed — so far as the frailty of your age and habitual health allows. The writings of those who have published something on the Comet in Germany, which at that time you could not get so quickly from Willer, I have received from Master Paul Hainzel; among which, above the rest, what pleases me is Maestlin's diligence, and his observation that comes nearest to the truth, and the mathematical demonstration grounded upon it. Nor do I doubt that he, if he were furnished with the means to make large and exquisite metal instruments, would attempt something outstanding, from observations, toward restoring and correcting the motions of the heavenly bodies. In the anticipation of the apogee, certainly, and in the augmentation of the Sun's eccentricity, and then too in the swifter advancement of the eighth sphere than Copernicus reckons — so far as I perceive from what Hainzel communicated — he does not differ much from my own findings, made by careful observations diligently carried out over the last several years just past, as I have written more fully to Master Hainzel. His ephemerides, or any other things published by him, I should wish you to forward to me. And if he is joined to you by any familiarity, I beg you to greet him in my name, though he is unknown to me, and to obtain from him that he write to me as occasion serves, and share with me (if it so please him) his own observations, if he has any more exact; I in turn shall render him the same, and shall strive to advance his endeavours by whatever means is allowed. But if in the meantime you have obtained any writings about that Comet besides those that Master Hainzel sent me, I ask that you not find it a burden to forward them to me, whatever they may be. I, on account of various occupations falling to me both in the King's name and in that of friends — but above all on account of the building, and the troubles of setting up a new household, in which I am entangled — have not yet been able to finish those things which I have conceived about this Comet, and which I am busy to demonstrate from sure observations. Yet I hope, God willing, to accomplish it during the winter leisure, when I have a mind to work out certain other things too that are fit for correcting the errors of astronomy. For I shall not leave off devoting myself to this study amid very many other occupations; and the instruments suited to this business I have partly ready to hand, and partly am seeing made from day to day — instruments which, in magnitude, in handiness, and in careful workmanship, I am confident will yield in no way (at great cost, by long labour, with exquisite manufacture) to the mechanical and astronomical treatment of the ancients and the moderns. Indeed I have so arranged the structure of our building that it may be as fully suited as possible to using these and to observing the stars conveniently within it. For it was for this very reason that I withdrew to this Island, so that I might freely give my effort to Philosophy and to astronomical exercises; and although in the years just past I have been somewhat hindered, on account of the troubles of the building-works, in pursuing these vigorously, yet I hope that hereafter (if God supplies life and strength) I shall make it good with greater convenience than I am [now] preparing in the meantime. I have thought fit to write these things to you at this time, lest this most learned man, Master Andreas Severini, joined to me by familiarity, should come to you — who are bound to me on equal terms — without a letter from me. For he, having proposed that he wished to travel about for some time in order to survey certain Libraries in Germany, said that he would also go off to you at Augsburg. I therefore ask that, if in any matter you can advance his wishes, you bring it about that he may perceive you to be the friend not only of Tycho, but moreover of Tycho's friend. Fare you well, dearest Wolf, and at every occasion given do not leave off writing to me and — what at least is permitted — preserving with pen and paper the pleasing memory of our friendship; I in turn shall let no occasion slip by in vain, but that you may understand me to remain forever most loving of you.</p><p>Again, as happily and as long-livedly as may be, farewell, my dearest Wolf.<br>Given on our Island of Hven, 1579.<br>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Hven</b> (<i>Insula nostra Huaena</i>), where he had withdrawn after the 1576 royal grant of the island and was then in the midst of building Uraniborg. This is his reply to <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar; the surviving Wolf-side letters in this exchange include the 13 November 1571 consolatory letter (slug brahe-t_15711113001) and a further letter \"around the end of March of this [1579] year,\" to which the present letter responds.</li><li><b>Place / dateline.</b> <i>Datae in Insula nostra Huaena 1579</i> = \"Given on our Island of Hven, 1579\" (O.S.; Denmark kept Old Style until 1700). <b>The source supplies the year only</b> — no month or day. The dataset `label` gives the place (\"Hven\") but no finer date; the header therefore carries the bare year as written. The internal reference to \"around the end of March of this year\" (<i>circa finem Martij huius anni</i>) for the received letter dates the reply to <b>after late March 1579</b>, but does not fix its own day; this is left unresolved per protocol §3 (no silent date construction).</li><li><b><i>de Cometa … MAeSTLINI</b></i> — the <b>comet of 1577</b>. <i>MAeSTLINUS</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), then producing his cometary tract (<i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae … 1577</i>), later Kepler's teacher. Tycho praises his observation as \"coming nearest to the truth\" and its supporting <i>Mathematica demonstratio</i> (\"mathematical demonstration\"). External corroboration: a manuscript copy of Maestlin's treatise (with the Kassel observations) was forwarded to Tycho in 1579 by Paul Hainzel (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, p. ~ on the 1579 forwarding).</li><li><b><i>WILLERUS</b></i> = <b>Georg Willer</b>, the Augsburg bookseller/publisher of the Frankfurt–Augsburg fair catalogues — the channel through which Wolf would normally have obtained newly-printed comet tracts quickly.</li><li><b><i>Dominus PAULUS HAINZELIUS</b></i> = <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (Hainzelius), Augsburg patrician and patron of astronomy (with whom Tycho built a large quadrant at Göppingen in 1569); here Tycho's source for the German comet literature and the addressee of a fuller technical letter (<i>ut latius ad Dominum HAINZELIUM perscripsi</i>).</li><li><b>Astronomy (technical).</b> <i>Apogei … anticipatione</i> → \"anticipation of the apogee\" (<i>apogaeum</i> → <b>apogee</b>, §5.1); <i>Excentricitatis Solis augmento</i> → \"augmentation of the Sun's <b>eccentricity</b>\" (§5.1); <i>octavae Sphaerae … promotione</i> → \"advancement of the <b>eighth sphere</b>\" (the sphere of the fixed stars; its motion — precession/trepidation — here said to be swifter than Copernicus reckons); <i>motibus coelestium corporum</i> → \"the motions of the heavenly bodies\"; <i>restituendis et corrigendis motibus</i> → \"restoring and correcting the motions,\" Tycho's <i>instauratio</i>/restoration programme (§5.1). <i>COPERNICUS</i> = <b>Nicolaus Copernicus</b>.</li><li><b><i>limitibus ad instrumenta metallica … conficienda instructus</b></i> — <i>limites</i> here in the sense of \"means / resources / wherewithal\" for making large precision metal instruments; rendered \"the means [limits]\" with the literal kept in brackets, since the word is slightly strained. The whole clause anticipates Tycho's own instrument programme (<i>instrumenta … magnitudine et habilitate et diligenti elaboratione</i>, \"in magnitude, handiness, and careful workmanship\") — the <i>Mechanica</i> lexicon (§5.2): <i>instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b>.</li><li><b><i>Ephemerides</b></i> — \"ephemerides\" (astronomical tables); kept as the technical term.</li><li><b>Building / Uraniborg.</b> <i>aedificatio</i>, <i>aedificationum molestias</i> → \"the building\" and \"the troubles of the building-works\" (the construction of Uraniborg, begun 1576); <i>oeconomiae novae instituendae molestias</i> → \"the troubles of setting up a new household/economy\" (the island establishment). <i>Aedificationis … structuram ita adornavi, ut his utendis sideribusque … observandis … competat</i> → the building purpose-built for observation — an early statement of Uraniborg's design rationale.</li><li><b><i>Μ. ANDREAS SEVERINI</b></i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (Andreas Severini Vellejus, 1542–1616), the Danish historian and Tycho's close friend (§5.6); <i>Μ.</i> = Greek-letter abbreviation for <i>Magister</i> (\"Master,\" academic). His planned German tour to \"survey certain Libraries\" (<i>lustrandi quasdam in Germania Bibliothecas</i>) — including a visit to Wolf at <b>Augsburg</b> (<i>Augustam … ad vos</i>) — is the occasion of the letter: a letter of introduction. The closing pun <i>te non solum TYCHONIS, sed eius insuper amici amicum esse</i> (\"the friend not only of Tycho, but of Tycho's friend\") is kept.</li><li><b>Greek letter.</b> <i>Μ.</i> (capital Greek Mu) stands for the Latin <i>Magister</i>; transcribed as printed in the source block, expanded as \"Master\" in the translation.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i> → \"Greeting\" (§5.5). Close: <i>Bene vale</i> → \"Fare you well\"; <i>Iterum … vale</i> → \"Again … farewell\" (§5.5); <i>quam felicissime et diutissime</i> rendered \"as happily and as long-livedly as may be,\" preserving the doubled superlative flourish. Subscription <i>T. / Tycho Brahe</i> kept.</li><li><b>Textual crux (minor).</b> Near the close the source reads <i>quin intellifas</i> — an evident misprint/scribal slip for <i>quin intelligas</i> (\"but that you may understand\"); translated as <i>intelligas</i>, the printed form retained in the source block per protocol §4(2). Likewise <i>publicârunt</i> (circumflex) = <i>publicaverunt</i>; <i>MAeSTLINI</i>, <i>DIVINAe</i>, <i>REDDITAe</i> preserve the printed <i>Ae</i> ligature-capital.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'limitibus' to 'sumtibus' per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.52; English's bracketed hedge '[limits]', tied to the corrupted reading, removed accordingly ('the means' alone already conveys 'sumtibus').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical/instrument lexicon is fixed to the glossary. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the letter bears <b>only the year 1579</b> in its dateline — no month/day is constructed; (2) <i>limitibus … instructus</i> (\"furnished with the means/limits\") is mildly strained Latin, rendered with the literal kept in brackets.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. tboo is populated (not null) so this letter was collated, not defaulted to B. Read TBOO VII pp.52-53 as page images: the closing 'Datae in Insula nostra Huaena 1579' correctly carries no day-number, matching Dreyer's own undated close (no fabricated day found, consistent with the letter's genuinely partial dating). One TEXT_ERROR found ('limitibus' for Dreyer's 'sumtibus'), which forces grade C notwithstanding the translator's honest bracketed hedge. This supersedes the self-assigned published_grade of B, which was apparently assigned without collation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints 'limitibus' (limits) where Dreyer prints 'sumtibus' (sumptibus, 'at [his own] expense') -- the only reading that makes grammatical sense. The published English hedges honestly with 'the means [limits]', reflecting the corrupted source rather than inventing anything. Dreyer prints: <i>si sumtibus ad instrumenta metallica magna et exquisita conficienda instructus esset (TBOO VII p.52)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the single defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.52), which clearly reads 'si fumtibus ad instrumenta metallica magna et exquifita conficienda instructus effet'. Both the Latin and the English hedge that had reflected the corruption were updated in step. No uncertainty remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15780509001", "next": "brahe-t_15790325002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15780509001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15790325002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receiving German comet writings via Paul Hainzel, praises Maestlin's observations and demonstration above the rest, and discusses solar apogee, eccentricity, and the eighth sphere.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790325001", "d": "1579-03-25", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Paul Hainzel", "ck": "Paul Hainzel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Paul Hainzel → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 25 March 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>PAULUS HAINTZELIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NIHIL sane opus erat, amantissime mi TYCHO, tam accurata apud me de intermissione literarum excusatione uti, cum tu me eiusdem negligentiae accusare possis. Verum cum nec ipse tu, quanta ex Latini sermonis paupertate laborem, ignores, et egomet mihi plane sim conscius, quanta difficultate, praesertim ad homines doctos, tuique similes scribam, ne mirere, si vel rariores, vel breviores, quam expectas a me, tibi reddantur. Nisi enim habeam, quod vel te scire tua vel utriusque nostrum intersit, nihil est, quod a me expectes. Illud saltem cave, ne propterea quicquam de meo erga te amore detractum suspicere. De me, mearumque rerum statu scias, me, Dei omnipotentis beneficio, cum tota familia recte valere, filioque (ab hinc anno secundo, nimirum 1577, mense Septembri die 14, hora forte 7 3/4 post meridiem) auctum esse; reliqua veteri adhuc loco sita sunt, nisi quod praeterito anno filiam meam ELIZABETHAM, maiorem natu, cum iuvene huius urbis (cui nomen est MELCHIORI IENISCH) despondi, quorum nuptiae die 13. mensis Octobris, quod felix faustumque sit, celebratae sunt, cum tuae mihi plenae humanitatis literae iam ante mensem essent redditae, quibus ego quod in hunc usque diem non respondi, non mea, sed eius, qui tuas mihi reddidit, culpa factum est. Is namque me inscio hinc discessit, et sine meis literis, secus quam promiserat, ad vos redijt, cuius rei optimus mihi testis esse potest LAURENTIUS, hospes tuus.</p><p>Observationes Cometae, quas a me petis, ego sane nullas feci, cum nulla idonea ad observandum instrumenta nunc habeam. Aliorum vero observationes et scripta, quae passim in Germania et alibi de hoc Cometa publicata sunt et ad nos pervenerunt, tibique transmitti expetis (quorum certe magnus est numerus) eorum ego tibi aliquot unâ mitto. Sed mehercule non magnopere lectione, et quae tanto itineris spatio perferantur, digna sunt, exceptis observationibus M. MICHAeLIS MAeSTLINI, CORNELIJ GEMMAe Louaniensis, et ELISAeI ROeSLINI, qui eundem Cometam (quemadmodum etiam tu existimas) non in Elementari, sed in Aetherea mundi regione situm atque generatum esse, certis demonstrationibus testantur, eiusque verum locum, motum, magnitudinem, apparitionem et exstinctionem commonstrare videntur. Sed THADAeI HAGECIJ eiusdem Cometae descriptio, et Landgravij diligens observatio, modo rite in calculum adhibita sit, longe diversum ab illorum et tua opinione et sententia statuunt atque sentiunt. MICHAeLIS MAeSTLINI Epistolas ad Dn. HIERONYMUM WOLFIUM scriptas, et ex his excerpta, item Landgravij observationem, si modo genuina est, hîc unâ mitto. His doctissime, humanissimeque mi TYCHO, valeas, et me, sive loquentem tecum per literas, sive etiam tacentem, manere tamen semper in eodem amore et benevolentia erga te tibi plane persuasum habeas. IOHANNES BAPTISTA, frater meus, salutem tibi plurimam hîc referri voluit.</p><p>Iterum etiam atque etiam vale.<br>Dabantur Augustae 25. Martii An. 79.<br>T.<br>Paulus Haintzel.</p><p>Has saltem optimæ recordationis viri Pauli Haintzelij, Consulis Augustani,<br>Astronomicarum rerum amantissimi, in cuius etiam horto Quadrantem illum maximum,<br>cuius alias in meis scriptis fit mentio, extruximus, ternas saltem apponere<br>licuit literas, licet plures ad me dederit, quæ perierunt, uti etiam nullum<br>responsum inter chartas meas reperio eorum, quæ ad ipsum scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Paul Hainzel to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>There was surely no need, my most loving Tycho, to make so careful an excuse to me for the interruption of letters, since you could charge me with the same negligence. But since neither are you yourself unaware how greatly I labour under a poverty of the Latin tongue, and I am for my part fully conscious with what difficulty I write — especially to learned men, and to your like — do not wonder if [my letters] are delivered to you either rarer or briefer than you expect from me. For unless I have something that it concerns either you to know in your interest or both of us, there is nothing for you to expect from me. Only beware of this: do not on that account suspect that anything has been subtracted from my love toward you. As for myself and the state of my affairs, know that I, by the favour of Almighty God, am well together with my whole household, and have been increased by a son (two years ago from now, namely 1577, in the month of September, on the 14th day, at about 7¾ o'clock in the afternoon); the rest still stand in their old place, save that last year I betrothed my elder daughter Elizabeth to a young man of this city (whose name is Melchior Jenisch), whose wedding — may it be happy and auspicious — was celebrated on the 13th day of the month of October, at a time when your letter full of kindness had already a month before been delivered to me; and that I have not until this very day answered it has come about through no fault of mine, but of him who delivered yours to me. For he departed hence without my knowledge, and returned to you without my letters, otherwise than he had promised — of which matter Lorenz, your host, can be my best witness.</p><p>The observations of the comet which you ask of me, I have indeed made none, since I now have no instruments suitable for observing. But of others' observations and writings, which have been published here and there in Germany and elsewhere about this comet and have reached us, and which you desire to be sent on to you (of which the number is certainly great), I send you some along [with this]. But, by Hercules, they are not greatly worth reading, nor worth being conveyed over so great a stretch of road, save for the observations of Master Michael Maestlin, of Cornelius Gemma of Louvain, and of Elisaeus Roeslin, who attest by sure demonstrations that this same comet (as you too judge) was situated and generated not in the elementary but in the aethereal region of the world, and who seem to show forth its true place, motion, magnitude, appearance, and extinction. But Thaddaeus Hagecius's description of the same comet, and the Landgrave's careful observation, provided it has been duly brought into the reckoning, establish and hold something far different from both their opinion and judgement and your own. The letters of Michael Maestlin written to Master Hieronymus Wolf, and excerpts from them, likewise the Landgrave's observation, if only it is genuine, I send here along [with this]. With these, my most learned and most kind Tycho, may you fare well, and hold yourself fully persuaded that I — whether speaking with you by letter, or even keeping silent — nonetheless remain always in the same love and goodwill toward you. Johann Baptist, my brother, wished his heartiest greeting to be conveyed to you here.</p><p>Again, and again and again, farewell.<br>Given at Augsburg, 25 March, the year [15]79.<br>Yours,<br>Paul Hainzel.</p><p>These letters, at least, of Paul Hainzel — a man of most blessed memory, Consul of Augsburg, most devoted to astronomical matters, in whose garden, too, we erected that greatest Quadrant, of which mention is made elsewhere in my writings — these three letters at least it has been possible to append, although he gave more to me, which have perished; just as, likewise, I find no reply among my papers to those things which I wrote to him.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Paulus Haintzelius</i> = <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (1527–1581), patrician of Augsburg, sometime <i>Stadtpfleger</i> (city magistrate), patron of astronomy. With him Tycho had built the great 19-foot quadrant at Göppingen in 1569 (see the Wolf letter of 13 Nov. 1571, where the two Hainzel brothers are named). The closing salutation from <i>Iohannes Baptista, frater meus</i> identifies <b>Johann Baptist Hainzel</b>, his brother.</li><li><b><i>Latini sermonis paupertate</b></i> — \"a poverty of the Latin tongue\": Hainzel's self-deprecation about his Latinity; kept literal. (The Latin is in fact competent but heavily Ciceronian-periodic.)</li><li><b>The son's birth.</b> <i>filioque … nimirum 1577, mense Septembri die 14, hora forte 7 3/4 post meridiem</i> — a horoscopic-precision timestamp (\"on 14 September 1577, at about 7¾ o'clock in the afternoon\"), given to a quarter-hour for the casting of a nativity. <i>forte</i> (\"about / perhaps\") is the author's own hedge on the hour and is preserved, not sharpened. <i>post meridiem</i> = \"in the afternoon / p.m.\" (O.S.).</li><li><b>The daughter's betrothal.</b> <i>filiam meam Elizabetham … cum iuvene huius urbis (cui nomen est Melchiori Ienisch)</i> — <b>Elizabeth Hainzel</b>, betrothed to <b>Melchior Jenisch</b> of Augsburg; the wedding <i>die 13. mensis Octobris</i> (13 October [1578], the \"last year\" of <i>praeterito anno</i>). <i>quod felix faustumque sit</i> — \"may it be happy and auspicious,\" the formulaic Roman nuptial blessing, kept as a parenthetical wish.</li><li><b><i>Laurentius, hospes tuus</b></i> — \"Lorenz, your host\": a person (otherwise unidentified here) who had lodged Tycho or acted as his host/agent at Augsburg, blamed for failing to carry Hainzel's reply. <i>hospes</i> = \"host\" (one who gives lodging), not \"guest\" in this context.</li><li><b>The comet.</b> <i>hoc Cometa</i> = the <b>Great Comet of 1577</b>, the central observational event of these years and the subject of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588). The whole second paragraph concerns the dossier of printed comet-tracts Hainzel is forwarding to Hven.</li><li><b><i>instrumenta</b></i> → <b>instruments</b> (protocol §5.2); Hainzel pleads he has none fit for observing.</li><li><b>The cosmological crux of the comet.</b> <i>non in Elementari, sed in Aetherea mundi regione situm atque generatum</i> — \"situated and generated not in the elementary but in the aethereal region of the world\": the decisive claim that the comet lay <b>above the Moon</b>, in the aether, not in the sublunary (elementary) sphere — the supralunary thesis Tycho himself championed (<i>quemadmodum etiam tu existimas</i>, \"as you too judge\"). <i>verum locum, motum, magnitudinem, apparitionem et exstinctionem</i> rendered \"true place, motion, magnitude, appearance, and extinction\" (the comet's first appearance and its fading-out).</li><li><b>The observers named.</b></li><li><i>M. Michaelis Maestlini</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), then at Tübingen, later Kepler's teacher; his <i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae</i> (1578) placed the comet supralunary. <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic \"Master\").</li><li><i>Cornelij Gemmae Louaniensis</i> = <b>Cornelius Gemma</b> (1535–1578) of <b>Louvain</b> (son of Gemma Frisius), who likewise treated the 1577 comet in his <i>De prodigiosa specie naturaque cometae</i> (1578).</li><li><i>Elisaei Roeslini</i> = <b>Helisaeus Röslin</b> (1545–1616), physician and astronomer of Alsace, author of a 1578 <i>Theoria nova coelestium</i> on the comet.</li><li><i>Thadaei Hagecij</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), imperial physician at Prague, whose <i>Descriptio cometae</i> (1578) Tycho prized; here said to reach a conclusion <i>longe diversum</i> (\"far different\") from the supralunary trio and from Tycho.</li><li><i>Landgravij … observatio</i> = the observation of the <b>Landgrave</b> — Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel — whose value Hainzel guards twice with explicit hedges: <i>modo rite in calculum adhibita sit</i> (\"provided it has been duly brought into the reckoning\") and <i>si modo genuina est</i> (\"if only it is genuine\"). Both qualifiers are preserved.</li><li><b><i>mehercule</b></i> — \"by Hercules,\" a mild classical oath of asseveration; kept.</li><li><b><i>Dn.</b></i> = <i>Dominus</i>, \"Master / Lord\" (protocol §5.5), of <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (Hieronymus Wolfius), the Augsburg humanist (1516–1580) and Tycho's correspondent — recipient of Maestlin's letters that Hainzel forwards.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>Iterum etiam atque etiam vale</i> — \"Again, and again and again, farewell\" (an emphatic reduplication of the <i>Vale</i> formula); <i>salutem tibi plurimam … referri voluit</i> rendered \"wished his heartiest greeting to be conveyed to you\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Dabantur Augustae 25. Martii An. 79</i> = \"Given at <b>Augsburg</b> (Augusta Vindelicorum), 25 March, the year [15]79\" (O.S.). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1579. 25. marts. Augsburg</i>); no date discrepancy. <i>Dabantur</i> (lit. \"they were being given,\" the epistolary plural for the act of dispatch) rendered idiomatically \"Given.\"</li><li><b>What this is — a genre shift, not a continuation.</b> This recovered segment is <i>not</i> part of Hainzel's 1579 letter itself (whose actual close the published tail already gives, signed \"Paulus Haintzel\"). It is Tycho's own <b>editorial headnote</b>, written in the first person about Hainzel in the third person and in the past tense (\"optimae recordationis\" — \"of blessed/cherished memory\", the formula for the deceased), evidently composed when Tycho later assembled this group of letters for his letter-book. A reader could otherwise mistake it for Hainzel continuing to speak of himself, which the content rules out.</li><li><b>What it documents.</b> Tycho states plainly that only three of Hainzel's letters to him survived to be included, although Hainzel had sent more, now lost — and that he could find no drafts or copies of his own replies among his papers. This is a rare piece of direct evidence for the <i>compilation history</i> of Tycho's own letter-collection: an admission of gaps, not a claim of completeness.</li><li><b>Paul Hainzel identified.</b> Paul Hainzel, patrician and Consul (alderman) of Augsburg, a keen amateur astronomer; in his garden outside the city Tycho built the celebrated \"great Quadrant of Augsburg\" (1570, radius roughly 543 cm/about 19 feet, the largest instrument Tycho ever built), which Hainzel had commissioned and later undertook to see completed; Hainzel also gave Tycho a newly built sextant. The instrument stood until wrecked by a storm around 1575 and was never repaired [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (Edinburgh, 1890); Christianson, <i>Tycho Brahe and the Measure of the Heavens</i> (2020); corroborated by Wennberg, <i>Tänk, om det är så!</i>, on the quadrant's dimensions and site]. \"Consulis Augustani\" in the source matches the title given him in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>'s account of the 1572 nova observations.</li><li><b>Dating implication.</b> Because the headnote refers to Hainzel as already deceased, it must postdate his death — meaning the note is a later addition to this 1579 letter, not contemporary with it; the corpus consulted here does not give an exact death year for Hainzel, so this remains a lead rather than a settled fact.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the valediction abbreviation 'T.' (Tuus, 'Yours') on its own line above the signature, dropped from our source_text and consequently from the translation, per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII p.50.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is secure throughout and the rendering register-matched; no textual or dating crux. The cosmological phrase <i>in Aetherea … regione</i> and the author's two hedges on the Landgrave's observation are the substantive points, all preserved without sharpening; the only minor undetermined item is the identity of <i>Laurentius, hospes tuus</i>, which the letter does not specify and which is not a crux of reading.</p><p>The Latin itself is unproblematic and the translation is confident throughout; grade A reflects that clarity, not certainty about every external fact mentioned. What is secure: the text is Tycho's own retrospective note, not Hainzel's voice; the identification of Hainzel and the great Augsburg quadrant is well corroborated across multiple corpus sources. What is not fully closed: Hainzel's exact death date, and which specific compilation (e.g. the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>, 1596, or a later letter-book stage) this headnote belongs to. Upgrade path: check Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> editorial apparatus for this letter's headnote and Hainzel's biographical dates.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.49-51 as page images. Paul Hainzel's letter runs from the header 'PAULUS HAINTZELIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE' on p.49 through Tycho's own editorial closing note at the bottom of p.50 (p.51 begins the unrelated Wolfius letter, read only to confirm the boundary). Collated the full letter word-for-word against the page images: the date and hour of Hainzel's son's birth (14 September 1577, about 7:45pm), the wedding date (13 October), the astronomers named (Maestlin, Gemma of Louvain, Roeslin, Hagecius, the Landgrave), and Tycho's closing note (three letters preserved, others lost) all match exactly. One low-severity OMISSION found as above.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>OMISSION</b> (low). Dreyer prints a valediction abbreviation 'T.' (Tuus, 'Yours') on its own line directly above the signature; it is absent from our source_text and consequently from the translation, which signs off with the bare name only. At: “Iterum etiam atque etiam vale.<br>Dabantur Augustae 25. Martii An. 79.<br>Paul Hainzel.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII p.50 (page image, not OCR text layer), where 'T.' appears clearly on its own line between the date and the signature 'PAULUS HAINTZEL.'. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Paul Hainzel", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15750311001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses his infrequent letters due to difficulty in Latin, reports his household in good health, and notes the birth of a son in September 1577.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790325001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790325002", "d": "1579-03-25", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 25 March 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer, vol. VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HIERONYMUS WOLFIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>EADEM Tabellarij fortuna, TYCHO nobilissime, amice colende, hîc sumus usi, qua tua praestantia. Nam Aurifaber ille, qui tuas reddiderat, hinc abijt. Quis literulas sit perlaturus, nescio, sed sub vesperam demum mihi nuntiatum est, ut rescriberem. Itaque cogor esse brevior et ob temporis brevitatem et propter diem festum. Absentibus WILLERI ministris non possum parare libellos nostrorum, qui de atrocissimo illo Cometa, quem unquam vidi, scripserunt. Et sane puto nec Tabellarium grandiusculos libellos perlaturum et quae tu elaborâris, propediem nos accepturos. Quae autem de amplitudine vestrae regionis et fortitudine gentis scribis, satis nota nobis sunt, et Cimbros, Ambrones et Teutones a Martha C. MARIJ maga potius, quam a Romanis esse victos arbitramur. Linguam vero vestram ijs ornavit et antiquitatis et proprietatis et copiae laudibus IO. GOROPIUS, ut eam vel Hebraeae anteponat, nedum Latinae et Graecae. Itaque si hîc esses, Cimbrice a te disceremus. Sed fortasse nobis aetas obstaret, quo minus essemus dociles. Nam mortuo adhibere medicinam, et docere senem idem esse Graeco proverbio dicitur. Me quidem senectus et pigrum et obliviosum facit, et multipliciter tam corpus quam animum affligit. Quomodo autem me contra eam, tanquam morbum incurabilem muniam, non video, praesertim deficiente hîc vino bono et incorrupto, quo solo tanquam antidoto moderate sumto uti soleo contra tam invictum hostem, uti scis meum morem. Quare sive me hîc enneaticus climacter, ὁ ἀνδροδάμας, sustulerit, sive 66to tollendum reliquerit: susque deque fero, modo CHRISTUS Servator in Palatio sui patris parvulam aliquam cellulam assignet, in qua beate et aeternum requiescam, quod ita fore clementia divina fretus confido.</p><p>Respublica nostra in eo statu adhuc est, quem hinc discedens reliquisti. HAINZELIUS filius nuper a me uxoris suae Genesin confici voluit, cum ego respondi, D. TYCHONEM id in melius praestare posse, si vellet. Sed sane dubito, an velles, cum hîc in talibus difficilimus fueris. Isti homines affectibus suis indulgentes, succensent Astrologis et vera et falsa dicentibus. Itaque Tiresias soli Apollini vaticinandum esse dixit apud SOPHOCLEM, qui hominis indignationem, quippe Deus negligat. Eclipsin Solis in ♓ hîc vidimus pene integram, in qua et CYPRIANUS, sed minus tamen quam COPERNICUS aberravit. Cuius Ephemeridas MICHAeL MAeSTLINUS instrumentis coelo uti opinor adhibitis ab anno 1577 usque ad annum 1588 correctiores se esse editurum mihi pollicetur, nisi Typographus in mora sibi sit. Sed iam manum de tabula. Nam advesperascit et rhabarbarum scriptionem meam interpellat, quo uti saepius contra Ischiadem et Chiragram cogor. Tuam vero Excellentiam et valere et florere omni bonorum genere ex animo et spero et opto.</p><p>Vale.<br>25. Martij Anno 1579.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>The same fortune with the courier, most noble Tycho, friend to be honoured, have we had here as did your eminence [in writing to me]. For that goldsmith who had delivered your [letters] has gone away from here. Who is to carry these few lines I do not know, but it was only towards evening that word was brought to me that I should write back. And so I am forced to be rather brief, both because of the shortness of time and on account of the feast-day. The servants of Willer being absent, I cannot procure the little books of our people who have written about that most dreadful comet, the [worst] I ever saw. And indeed I think both that the courier will not carry the bulkier little books, and that we shall shortly receive what you have worked out. As for what you write about the extent of your country and the valour of its people, these are well enough known to us; and we hold that the Cimbri, Ambrones, and Teutones were vanquished rather by the sorceress Martha of C[aius] Marius than by the Romans. Your tongue, indeed, Jo. Goropius has adorned with such praises of antiquity, of distinctive character, and of richness, that he sets it even before Hebrew, let alone before Latin and Greek. And so, if you were here, we should learn Cimbric from you. But perhaps our age would stand in the way, so that we should be the less teachable. For to apply medicine to a dead man, and to teach an old man, are said by a Greek proverb to be one and the same thing. Old age, for my part, makes me both sluggish and forgetful, and afflicts in many ways my body as much as my mind. But how I am to fortify myself against it, as against an incurable disease, I do not see — especially when there is lacking here good and unspoiled wine, which alone, as a kind of antidote taken in moderation, I am wont to use against so unconquered an enemy, as you know my custom to be. Wherefore, whether the ninefold climacteric, ὁ ἀνδροδάμας [the man-tamer], shall here have carried me off, or shall have left me to be carried off in my sixty-sixth [year]: I make light of it, provided that Christ the Saviour assign me some little cell in his Father's palace, in which I may rest blessedly and for ever — which, relying on the divine clemency, I am confident will be so.</p><p>Our commonwealth is still in the state in which you left it on departing hence. Hainzel the son lately wished his wife's geniture to be drawn up by me, whereupon I replied that Master Tycho could do it better, if he were willing. But I much doubt whether you would be willing, since you were here most difficult in such matters. Those men, indulging their own passions, are angry at astrologers whether they speak the truth or falsehoods. And so Tiresias in Sophocles said that Apollo alone ought to prophesy — he who [being a god] disregards a man's indignation, since he is a god. We saw here the eclipse of the Sun in ♓ Pisces almost total, in which both Cyprian [Leovitius] went astray, yet less so than Copernicus. Michael Maestlin promises me that he will publish ephemerides of the latter, corrected — by using instruments applied to the sky, I suppose — from the year 1577 to the year 1588, unless the printer prove a hindrance to him. But now [I take] my hand from the tablet. For evening draws on, and the rhubarb interrupts my writing, which I am rather often compelled to use against sciatica and gout of the hand. But that Your Excellency may both fare well and flourish in every kind of good things, I both hope and wish from my heart.</p><p>Farewell.<br>25 March, the year 1579.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (\"father of Byzantine studies\"), librarian of the Fugger collection and rector of the Augsburg gymnasium; a mentor-friend of Tycho's from his Augsburg years. This letter, written within a year of Wolf's death, continues the correspondence whose 1571 instalment (brahe-t_15711113001) shares the same Vienna manuscript volume. The valediction (<i>Vale</i>) and the brevity reflect the haste he describes.</li><li><b>Heading / register.</b> <i>HIERONYMUS WOLFIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE</i> is the editor's letter-rubric, not a salutation formula; rendered literally. <i>amice colende</i> = \"friend to be honoured,\" the courtesy register kept (protocol §5.5). <i>Excellentia Tua</i> = \"Your Excellency.\"</li><li><b><i>Tabellarius</b></i> — \"courier/letter-carrier\"; the practical difficulty of finding a bearer frames the whole letter. <i>Aurifaber ille</i> — \"that goldsmith,\" the previous (unnamed) carrier of Tycho's letters.</li><li><b><i>WILLERI ministris</b></i> — \"the servants of Willer\": <b>Georg Willer</b>, the prominent Augsburg bookseller-publisher (issuer of the Frankfurt/Augsburg fair catalogues); Wolf cannot obtain the printed pamphlets on the comet because Willer's staff are away.</li><li><b><i>atrocissimo illo Cometa</b></i> — \"that most dreadful comet\": the <b>great comet of 1577</b>, the object of Tycho's parallax work and of the contemporary German pamphlet literature Wolf wishes to send. <i>quem unquam vidi</i> is elliptical (\"[the worst] I ever saw\"); the implied superlative is supplied in [brackets].</li><li><b>The Cimbri allusion.</b> Wolf answers Tycho's evident pride in his homeland (<i>amplitudine vestrae regionis et fortitudine gentis</i>, \"the extent of your country and the valour of its people\"). <i>Cimbros, Ambrones et Teutones</i> — the northern tribes annihilated by <b>Gaius Marius</b> (<i>C. Marii</i>) at Aquae Sextiae and Vercellae (102–101 BC). <i>a Martha … maga</i> — \"by the sorceress Martha,\" the Syrian prophetess Marius reputedly kept in his camp (Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 17); Wolf wittily credits her divination rather than Roman arms. The Cimbri were popularly identified with the Danish Cimbric peninsula (Jutland), hence the local-patriotic point.</li><li><b><i>IO. GOROPIUS</b></i> — <b>Johannes Goropius Becanus</b> (Jan van Gorp, 1519–1573), the Antwerp physician-etymologist whose <i>Origines Antwerpianae</i> (1569) argued that the Cimbric / \"Brabantic\" tongue was the oldest language of mankind, older even than Hebrew — the doctrine Wolf alludes to (<i>vel Hebraeae anteponat</i>) with evident irony. <i>Cimbrice … disceremus</i> — \"we should learn Cimbric [from you],\" i.e. Danish.</li><li><b>The Greek proverb.</b> <i>mortuo adhibere medicinam, et docere senem</i> — \"to physic a dead man and to teach an old man [are one and the same],\" rendering a Greek commonplace (cf. the proverb γέροντα μὴ παίδευε, \"do not instruct an old man\"); Wolf paraphrases it in Latin and ascribes it to \"a Greek proverb.\"</li><li><b>Wine as remedy.</b> <i>vino bono … tanquam antidoto … contra tam invictum hostem</i> — wine as his \"antidote\" against the \"unconquered enemy\" of old age; the self-deprecating tone (drinking <i>moderate</i>, \"in moderation\") is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>The climacteric / Greek.</b> <i>enneaticus climacter</i> = \"the ninefold (ennead) climacteric,\" the astrologically critical year reckoned by nines (here Wolf, aged about 62–63, faces his 63rd — the <i>grand</i> climacteric, 7 × 9); <i>ὁ ἀνδροδάμας</i> = \"the man-tamer / man-subduer,\" a stock epithet of the dangerous climacteric year (lit. <i>andro-</i> \"man\" + <i>damáō</i> \"to tame, subdue\"). The Greek is transcribed verbatim and glossed in [brackets]; <i>climacter</i> kept per protocol §5.3 (<i>annus climactericus</i> → <b>climacteric year</b>). <i>sive 66to tollendum reliquerit</i> — \"or shall have left [me] to be carried off in my sixty-sixth [year]\"; <i>66to</i> = <i>sexagesimo sexto</i> (ordinal abbreviation), expanded in the sense.</li><li><b><i>in Palatio sui patris parvulam aliquam cellulam</b></i> — \"some little cell in his Father's palace,\" echoing John 14:2 (<i>in domo Patris mei mansiones multae sunt</i>); the consolatory religious turn is kept.</li><li><b><i>HAINZELIUS filius</b></i> — \"Hainzel the son\": one of the <b>Hainzel brothers</b> of Augsburg (Johann Baptist and Paul Hainzel, the patrician patrons of astronomy with whom Tycho built his 1569 quadrant); here a younger Hainzel asks for his wife's nativity. <i>Genesin … in melius praestare</i> = \"to draw up the geniture better\" (<i>Genesis</i> → <b>geniture</b>, §5.3). <i>difficilimus</i> (for <i>difficillimus</i>) — \"most difficult/reluctant,\" Wolf recalling Tycho's reluctance over such commissions.</li><li><b>The Sophocles allusion.</b> <i>Tiresias … apud SOPHOCLEM</i> — Wolf cites <b>Sophocles</b> (the seer Tiresias, <i>Oedipus Tyrannus</i> / <i>Antigone</i>) for the maxim that prophecy belongs to Apollo alone, \"who, being a god, disregards a man's indignation.\" The syntax is compressed: <i>qui hominis indignationem, quippe Deus negligat</i> is read \"[Apollo,] who being a god disregards a man's indignation\"; the somewhat elliptical clause is rendered with that supplement and flagged here rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>Eclipsin Solis in ♓ … pene integram</i> = \"the solar eclipse in Pisces almost total\" — the partial solar eclipse observed at Augsburg; the glyph ♓ is kept in the source and named <b>Pisces</b> in the translation (§5.3). <i>eclipsis (Solis)</i> → <b>eclipse (solar)</b> (§5.1). <i>in qua et CYPRIANUS, sed minus tamen quam COPERNICUS aberravit</i> — \"in [predicting] which both <b>Cyprian [Leovitius]</b> (Cyprianus Leovitius, 1524–1574, the astrologer whose tables Wolf used) erred, yet less than <b>Copernicus</b>\" — i.e. the eclipse showed the received computations astray.</li><li><b><i>MICHAeL MAeSTLINUS</b></i> — <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher), whose published <i>Ephemerides</i> affirmed the superiority of the Copernican hypotheses, citing the 1577 comet as evidence (so Gingerich, <i>Annotated Census</i>). <i>Ephemeridas … ab anno 1577 usque ad annum 1588 correctiores</i> = \"corrected ephemerides from 1577 to 1588.\" <i>instrumentis coelo … adhibitis</i> — \"by instruments applied to the sky,\" i.e. derived from observation; <i>opinor</i> (\"I suppose\") is Wolf's own hedge and is preserved. <i>Typographus</i> = the printer.</li><li><b><i>manum de tabula</b></i> — \"[I take] my hand from the tablet/board,\" a proverbial \"I'll stop here\" (cf. Pliny / Cicero); the elliptical idiom kept, <i>[I take]</i> bracketed as supplied.</li><li><b>Medicine.</b> <i>rhabarbarum</i> = <b>rhubarb</b> (the purgative root); <i>Ischias / Ischiadem</i> = <b>sciatica</b>; <i>Chiragra</i> = <b>gout of the hand (chiragra)</b> (§5.4) — the same complaint as in the 1571 letter; both period terms glossed.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>25. Martij Anno 1579</i> = \"25 March 1579.\" No place is named in the dateline; the witness and content place it at <b>Augsburg</b> (<i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i>), and the catalogue label confirms it. The label <i>\"1579. 25. marts. (Augsburg)\"</i> agrees with the dateline. <b>Style:</b> O.S. (Julian); the Gregorian reform was still three years off, and Lutheran Augsburg in any case retained the Julian calendar until 1700. No in-letter date discrepancy. <i>propter diem festum</i> — \"on account of the feast-day\": 25 March is the Feast of the Annunciation (Lady Day), the festival to which Wolf's apology for brevity refers.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical references (1577 comet, Goropius Becanus, the Marian Cimbri, Maestlin's Copernican ephemerides, the Augsburg eclipse) all check out. The one genuine syntactic soft point is the compressed Sophocles/Tiresias clause <i>qui hominis indignationem, quippe Deus negligat</i> (the antecedent and the force of <i>quippe</i> are elliptical), flagged above rather than smoothed; a secondary minor point is the exact reckoning intended by <i>enneaticus climacter … sive 66to</i> (whether the 63rd or 66th year is the feared one), also flagged rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.50-53 (letter itself on 51 through the top of 52): the messenger excuse, the Cimbri/Goropius digression, the ninefold-climacteric/66th-year passage, the Hainzel geniture anecdote, the Tiresias/Sophocles reference, the Pisces eclipse and Maestlin's 1577-1588 ephemerides, and the closing date (25 March 1579) all match Dreyer word for word, including the number 66 and the year range 1577-1588. Translation is faithful throughout, with bracketed supplements clearly marked. No defects found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15790000001", "next": "brahe-t_15800208001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15790000001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Writes briefly due to the courier's haste, reports inability to procure German comet writings, and comments on Danish language and the history of the Cimbri and Teutons.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790325002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790521001", "d": "1579-05-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Hven, 21 May 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>A. WELLEIO CONCIONATORI REGIS. SALUTEM IN SALVATORE.</p><p>ACCEPI literas tuas, dilecte M. ANDREA, et de equo minus bene vidente D. I. FRANCISCI (caecum ne appellem, multis deierationibus absterremur) intellexi: qui [quoniam] est vir benevolus et nobis Amicus, eum non denegat sed sponte offert sive venalem, sive commutabilem; ego ubi artificium molae nostrae, in quo eo uti vellem, paratum fuerit, cum eo de hoc agam, si proprijs equis uti non licuerit, eo quod eo dono destituantur, quo suum etiam carere mallet. Agas itaque ipsi interea meo nomine gratias. De istius vafri hominis perfidia, qui sponte oblatam reconciliationem callide aversatur, ut impune sibi per Legati adventum famam meam laesisse consultum sit, nihil dicam, donec vir ille opt. et aequus adveneri, cuius etiam arbitrio primum stabo, an aequum sit me ista immerito ab eo pati et per civitatem ab ijs, quibus nihil mali volui, sed ab his passus sum, defamari. Quid Hessus iste dicat et quam gratiam referat, nihil curo, cum non alia ab eo exspectârim unquam, neque ob id medicinam conficiam, quod vel gratiam vel lucrum inde sperem ullum, miror saltem eousque Christianismi institutionem pervenisse, ut Magistratus ipse gloriari audeat, a membris Satanae opem, quam ferre nequeunt, allatam sibi esse, posthabitis Dei auxilio et ab ipso ordinatis medijs. O Doctores, o Discipuli. De Curia ista quae ad nos pertinet in Angulo Civitatis Matri, ut consuluisti, scripsi, ab ea brevi responsum expecto, quod ubi nactus fuero, modo ex votis nostris contigerit, cum CHRISTIERNO URUP praesens, ubi Haffniam advenero, agam; effice interea apud ipsum, ne curiam suam prius alteri divendat. Controversiam, quae inter Fratrem meum et BLESSIUM istum incidit, nondum a quoquam intellexi, felicior enim sum in hac Insula, quam quod plurimas mundanae fabulae nugas, hominumque Eccentricitates saepius cum molestia exauditas doleam. Velim tamen ut me de isto negotio certiorem reddas, siquidem Fratris mei magnopere interesse ex te intelligo. Vale et significa quando Iohannes Patri redierit. Iterum vale et saluta Mag. LAURENTIUM Amicum communem meo nomine plurimum.</p><p>Datae Huennae 21. Maij Anno 79.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>Mitto tibi Catalogum librorum Francofurtensium et peto ut BALTHAZARO significari curare velis, ut eos libros quos<br>lineola in margine notavi, quamprimum licuerit, mihi procurare velit. Sunt<br>autem novem Latini, tres Germanici et unus Gallicus. Remitto tibi 8<br>Ioachimicos per hunc CHRISTIERNUM piscium mercatorem, pro quibus ago gratias.<br>Iterum vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To [Anders] Vedel, the King's preacher. Greeting in the Saviour.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, beloved Master Anders, and have learned about the not-well-seeing horse of Master J. Franciscus (we are deterred from calling it blind by his many oaths): since he is a kindly man and a friend to us, he does not refuse it but freely offers it, whether to be sold or to be exchanged. When the mechanism of our mill, in which I should wish to use it, is ready, I shall treat with him about this — if it shall not prove possible to use my own horses, on the ground that they are without that endowment which he too would prefer his own [horse] to lack. So in the meanwhile give him thanks in my name. Of the treachery of that crafty man, who cunningly turns away from a reconciliation freely offered him, so that he might have contrived to injure my good name with impunity through the coming of the Legate, I shall say nothing, until that excellent and fair-minded man shall arrive — to whose judgment, moreover, I shall first submit [the question] whether it be fair that I should undeservedly suffer these things from him, and be defamed throughout the city by those toward whom I willed no harm, yet at whose hands I have suffered it. What that Hessian says, and what thanks he returns, I care nothing — since I have never expected anything else from him; nor do I prepare medicine on the ground that I hope for any favour or gain from it. I marvel only that the practice of Christianity has come to such a pass that the Magistrate himself dares to boast that help, which they [the members of Satan] cannot give, has been brought to him by the members of Satan, while God's aid and the means ordained by Him are set aside. O [such] Doctors, O [such] Disciples! About that property which belongs to us in the Corner of the city, I have written to [my] Mother, as you advised; I expect a reply from her shortly, and when I have got it — provided it turn out according to our wishes — I shall deal with Christiern Urup in person, when I have come to Copenhagen. See to it meanwhile with him that he does not sell off his property to another beforehand. The dispute that has arisen between my Brother and that Blessius I have not yet learned of from anyone — for I am more fortunate on this Island than that I should have to grieve over the very many trifles of worldly tattle, and the Eccentricities of men, so often and so vexingly heard out. Yet I should like you to inform me about that matter, since I understand from you that it greatly concerns my Brother. Farewell, and let me know when Johannes shall have returned to [his] Father. Again, farewell, and greet warmly in my name Master Laurentius, our common friend.</p><p>Given at Hven, 21 May, the year [15]79.<br>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p><p>I send you the Catalogue of the Frankfurt books, and I ask that you see to it that Balthasar be informed, so that he may be willing to procure for me, as soon as it shall have been possible, those books which I have marked with a little line in the margin. There are nine in Latin, three in German, and one in French. I send back to you 8 Joachimsthalers through this Christiern, a fish-merchant, for which I give thanks. Farewell again.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, writing from his island of <b>Hven</b> (<i>Huenna</i>), where building works were under way. The addressee, <i>A. Welleio</i> / <i>M. Andrea</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the historian and royal chaplain, here styled <i>Concionator Regis</i> \"the King's preacher\" — Vedel was preacher at the royal chapel in Copenhagen Castle. A long-standing friend of Tycho's (cf. the 1571 Vedel letter in the pilot).</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>SALUTEM IN SALVATORE</i> — \"Greeting in the Saviour,\" a devotional variant of the standard <i>salutem</i> (protocol §5.5), suited to writing to a clergyman.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>D. I. FRANCISCI</i> — \"Master J. Franciscus,\" owner of the horse offered to Tycho; not further identified here. <i>D.</i> = <i>Dominus</i>, \"Master\" (§5.5).</li><li><i>Hessus iste</i> — \"that Hessian\": rendered as an ethnic/by-name epithet for an adversary in the medical dispute (a man called or from <b>Hesse(n)</b>). Whether <i>Hessus</i> is a surname or \"the Hessian\" is not decidable from the text; kept literal and flagged (crux below).</li><li><i>CHRISTIERNO URUP</i> — <b>Christiern (Christen) Urup</b>, a Danish nobleman, owner of an adjoining or desired property (\"his property,\" <i>curiam suam</i>) that Tycho wishes to acquire.</li><li><i>BLESSIUM istum</i> — \"that Blessius,\" a party to a dispute with Tycho's brother; Latinised surname, not securely identified; kept as <i>Blessius</i>.</li><li><i>Fratrem meum</i> / <i>Fratris mei</i> — \"my Brother\": one of Tycho's brothers (most likely <b>Steen Brahe</b>, the eldest surviving brother active in Danish affairs), not named in the letter; left unspecified as in the source.</li><li><i>Matri</i> — \"[my] Mother,\" <b>Beate Bille</b>, Tycho's mother (widow of Otte Brahe), whom Tycho must consult about the family property \"in the Corner of the city.\"</li><li><i>Iohannes … Patri</i> — \"Johannes … to [his] Father\": a young man (perhaps a student or servant) whose return is awaited; deliberately under-determined in the source (\"Father\" not further specified).</li><li><i>Mag. LAURENTIUM</i> — \"Master Laurentius,\" a common friend; <i>Mag.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic \"Master,\" §5.5).</li><li><i>Legati adventum</i> — \"the coming of the Legate / envoy\": an official whose arrival the \"crafty man\" hoped to exploit; not identified.</li><li><b>Editorial reading.</b> <i>qui [quoniam] est vir benevolus</i> — the bracketed <i>[quoniam]</i> (\"since\") is an editorial supplement carried in the text of record (the relative <i>qui</i> is read as causal); kept in brackets in the Source block per protocol §4(2), and rendered \"since\" in the translation.</li><li><b>Technical / realia.</b> <i>artificium molae nostrae</i> — \"the mechanism (contrivance) of our mill\": Tycho's <b>mill</b> on Hven (the water-/paper-mill complex he was constructing; the horse was wanted as draught/turning power for the mill works). <i>Eccentricitates</i> here is used figuratively, \"[men's] eccentricities/oddities,\" <b>not</b> the astronomical <i>eccentricitas</i> of the glossary (§5.1) — the same Latin word in a colloquial sense; flagged so the consistency-sweep does not mis-tag it.</li><li><b>Medicine / religious-polemic passage.</b> Tycho defends his practice of preparing <i>medicina</i> (Paracelsian/spagyric remedies) against a churchman's charge that such cures come from \"the members of Satan.\" The clause <i>a membris Satanae opem, quam ferre nequeunt, allatam sibi esse, posthabitis Dei auxilio et ab ipso ordinatis medijs</i> is reproduced with its full logic: the relative <i>quam ferre nequeunt</i> (\"which they cannot give\") is Tycho's sarcastic aside that the so-called \"members of Satan\" are precisely unable to furnish such help; <i>posthabitis … medijs</i> (\"the … means being set aside,\" ablative absolute) marks what the Magistrate neglects. <i>O Doctores, o Discipuli</i> — an exclamatory lament (\"O [what] Doctors, O [what] Disciples!\"), kept as exclamation.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> Five operative negations in the body, all preserved one-for-one: <i>caecum <b>ne</b> appellem</i> (\"lest I call it blind\"); <i>eum <b>non</b> denegat</i> (\"does <b>not</b> refuse it\"); <i>si proprijs equis uti <b>non</b> licuerit</i> (\"if it shall <b>not</b> prove possible to use my own horses\"); <i>quo suum etiam carere mallet</i> — the verb <i>carere</i> (\"to be without/lack\") carries privative force, rendered \"to lack / be without\"; <i>quibus <b>nihil</b> mali volui</i> (\"toward whom I willed <b>no</b> harm\"); <i>quam ferre <b>nequeunt</b></i> (\"which they can<b>not</b> give\"); <i>medicinam <b>neque</b> … conficiam … quod … sperem</i> (\"<b>nor</b> do I prepare medicine … on the ground that I hope …\"); <i>ne curiam suam prius alteri divendat</i> (\"that he does <b>not</b> sell off his property to another beforehand,\" negative purpose). None added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Huennae 21. Maij Anno 79</i> = \"Given at <b>Hven</b>, 21 May, [15]79\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The catalogue label agrees with the dateline (1579-05-21, Hven); no discrepancy. <i>T. / TYCHO BRAHE</i> — the subscription <i>T.</i> is Tycho's initial above his full name.</li></ul><p>### Named cruxes<br>1. <b><i>quo suum etiam carere mallet</b></i> — the clause explaining why Tycho's own horses may not serve: literally \"[the endowment] which he [Franciscus] would prefer his own [horse] too to be without.\" The referent of <i>eo dono</i> (\"that endowment/gift\") is unstated — most plausibly the quality of <i>sight</i> (i.e. Tycho's horses, unlike the half-blind offered horse, still <b>have</b> the sight that Franciscus would rather his own horse lacked, so that Franciscus is content to part with the defective animal), but the syntax admits other readings (a draught quality wanted for the mill). The text does not decide it; rendered close to the Latin and flagged rather than smoothed.<br>2. <b><i>Hessus iste</b></i> — surname vs. \"the Hessian\" undecided (see above).<br>3. <i>donec vir ille opt. et aequus <b>adveneri</b></i> — the form <i>adveneri</i> is anomalous (for <i>advenerit</i>, future-perfect \"shall have arrived\"); read as such and so translated; the irregular spelling is left in the Source block.<br>- <b>What this is.</b> A business paragraph preceding the letter's close, showing Tycho using the twice-yearly Frankfurt book-fair catalogue as a live order form: he has marked, by hand, thirteen titles (nine Latin, three German, one French) he wants procured through an intermediary, and is settling an account of 8 Joachimsthalers (Joachimsthaler silver coins, the ancestor of the word \"dollar\") via a courier.<br>- <b>Two named agents, unidentified in the consulted corpus.</b> \"Balthazaro\" — presumably a bookseller or book-buying agent in Vedel's circle, tasked with placing the actual order at Frankfurt — and \"Christiernum,\" described only as \"piscium mercatorem\" (a fish-merchant), who is here doubling as an informal courier of coin between Hven and Vedel. Neither is corroborated by name in the corpus sources consulted; both would repay a dedicated search in the <i>Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia</i> correspondence apparatus or Danish merchant records.<br>- <b>Context.</b> Recipient Anders Sørensen Vedel was Tycho's long-time friend and the Danish royal historiographer; letters between them regularly mix scholarly, financial, and household business in one breath, as here.<br>- <b>Coinage note.</b> \"Ioachimicos\" = Joachimsthaler, the large silver coin minted from the Joachimsthal mines in Bohemia from 1520, whose name was later clipped to \"thaler\" and ultimately gave English \"dollar\" — retained untranslated as a proper coin-name.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the courtesy frame, the property/Urup business, the brother-and-Blessius dispute, and the medicine-vs-Magistrate polemic are rendered securely; graded [C] for the unresolved <i>quo suum etiam carere mallet</i> clause (crux 1), where the referent of <i>eo dono</i> is genuinely undetermined and is flagged rather than fixed.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII p. 68 (the cod. Arch. Regni Daniae apograph): the <i>eo dono</i> clause is transmitted identically with no editorial gloss of the referent — the crux is textually genuine, not a witness artefact. [C] stands.</p><p>The Latin is plain and the transaction is easy to follow; nothing in the text is corrupt. The grade is held at B rather than A purely because two named individuals central to the passage — Balthasar and Christiern the fish-merchant — could not be identified from the corpus consulted in the time available, so the human texture of the passage (who these men were to Tycho and Vedel) remains open. Upgrade path: search Vedel's correspondence and the Frankfurt fair-catalogue material in the Opera Omnia apparatus for either name.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.54-55 as page images (the full 2-page slice; this is the complete letter, 'A. WELLEIO CONCIONATORI REGIS' through the postscript, with the next letter to Vedel beginning immediately after on p.55). Collated the entire Latin text plus postscript word for word against Dreyer: matches source_text exactly, including all proper names (D. I. Franciscus, Christiernus Urup, Blessius, Iohannes, Laurentius, Balthazarus, Christiernus the fish-merchant) and every number in the postscript (9 Latin, 3 German, 1 French book; 8 Ioachimici); date 21 Maij Anno 79 matches. Checked published_english against this verified Latin clause by clause: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. Note: published_grade on this packet was C, self-assigned by the pipeline under audit; that grade is not supported by this collation, which finds the translation clean.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift", "A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15710518001", "next": "brahe-t_15790705001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Discusses arrangements regarding a horse offered by Master Franciscus and addresses an unnamed adversary's refusal of reconciliation, deferring judgment to the arriving Legate.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790521001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790705001", "d": "1579-07-05", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Hven, 5 July 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DOCTISSIMO VIRO MAGISTRO ANDREAe SEVERINI WELLEIO, CONCIONATORI IN REGIA HAFFNIENSI, SUO AMICO.<br>S. P.</p><p>DILECTE Magister ANDREA, peto ut iam unâ cum Domino Legato meos lares Huenenses velis invisere prout pollicitus es, et roga meo nomine Dn. Magistrum LAURENTIUM, Amicum nostrum, ut unâ venire velit. Quod si Doctor IOHANNES FRANCISCUS et D. EZOCCUS velint Domino Legato comites esse et ab urbanis molestijs per aliquot dies animum recreare et cum tenui boni consulere, fecerint mihi rem gratam. De Domino Quaestore Regio scripsi Domino Legato. Quod si is me in hac Insula invisere prae negotiis publicis, quibus assidue occupatur, non detrectaverit, fecerit mihi rem gratissimam; non enim diffido ipsum, quamvis pro dignitate et meritis hoc in loco minus sollenniter excipi possit, tamen pro conditione temporis et loci boni consulturum. Vale et significa per puerum meum quid sit futurum.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Datae Huenae die 5. Iulij Anno 1579.<br>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most learned man, Master Anders Sørensen Vedel, preacher at the royal residence in Copenhagen, his friend.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Beloved Master Anders, I ask that you now, together with the Lord Legate, be willing to visit my home on Hven, just as you promised; and ask in my name our friend Master Laurentius to be willing to come along too. And if Doctor Johannes Franciscus and Master Ezoccus should wish to be the Lord Legate's companions, and for some few days to refresh their minds from the troubles of the city and to take a slender fare in good part, they will have done me a welcome thing. Concerning the Lord Royal Treasurer I have written to the Lord Legate. And if he, despite the public business with which he is constantly occupied, will not decline to visit me on this island, he will have done me a most welcome thing; for I do not doubt that, even though here he can be received less ceremoniously than befits his rank and merits, he will nevertheless take it in good part, according to the circumstance of the time and the place. Farewell, and signify by my boy [the messenger] what is to be.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given at Hven, on the 5th day of July, the year 1579.<br>Yours,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from his newly-established seat on the island of Hven (Uraniborg, the building of which had begun in 1576). The signature <i>T. / Tycho Brahe</i> and the dateline place this firmly in his first years on the island.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Andreas Severini Welleius</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), Danish historian, theologian, and ballad-collector, Tycho's close friend (and kinsman by upbringing). The address-line styles him <i>concionator in regia Hafniensi</i> — \"preacher at the royal residence in Copenhagen,\" i.e. court chaplain at Copenhagen Castle, the office Vedel then held.</li><li><b><i>S. P.</b></i> — <i>salutem plurimam</i> (sc. <i>dicit</i>), \"heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Vale … Iterum vale</i> — \"Farewell … Again, farewell\" (§5.5).</li><li><b><i>lares Huenenses</b></i> — literally \"my Hven household gods,\" a classicising idiom for \"my home / hearth on Hven\"; rendered \"my home on Hven.\" <i>Huenae</i> in the dateline = <b>Hven</b> (kept as is per §5.7).</li><li><b><i>Dominus Legatus</b></i> — \"the Lord Legate / Envoy.\" The honorand of the invitation, evidently a high-ranking official or ambassador then in Copenhagen whom Tycho hopes Vedel will escort to Hven; not named in the letter and not securely identified here — rendered by office, not by a guessed name.</li><li><b><i>Magister Laurentius</b></i> — \"Master Laurentius (Lauritz/Lars),\" called <i>Amicus noster</i> (\"our friend\"); a member of Tycho's and Vedel's shared Copenhagen circle. Not further identified in the letter; left in the Latin form with the modern given name flagged.</li><li><b><i>Doctor Iohannes Franciscus</b></i> and <b><i>D. Ezoccus</b></i> — \"Doctor Johannes Franciscus\" and \"Master Ezoccus,\" two further members of the party. Neither is securely identifiable from this letter alone; both are left in their epistolary forms (<i>D.</i> expanded as <i>Dominus</i> = \"Master,\" §5.5). <b>Crux flagged:</b> <i>Ezoccus</i> is an unusual Latinised form and may be a copyist's spelling in the surviving <i>Afskrift</i> (copy); no normalisation is imposed.</li><li><b><i>Dominus Quaestor Regius</b></i> — \"the Lord Royal Treasurer,\" the crown financial officer (<i>rentemester</i> / royal treasurer). Identified here by office only; Tycho says he has written separately to the Legate about him.</li><li><b><i>cum tenui boni consulere</b></i> / <b><i>boni consulturum</b></i> — the idiom <i>boni consulere</i> = \"to take (something) in good part, to be content with\"; here a courteous self-deprecation about the modest hospitality (<i>tenui</i>, \"slender [fare/means]\") Hven can offer. Rendered \"to take a slender fare in good part\" / \"he will take it in good part,\" preserving the polite hedge rather than over-translating <i>tenui</i> as a specific noun (bracketed sense added only where English requires a head-noun).</li><li><b><i>per puerum meum</b></i> — \"by my boy,\" i.e. by Tycho's servant/messenger boy; <i>significa</i> \"signify, send word.\" Rendered \"by my boy [the messenger],\" the gloss bracketed.</li><li><b><i>urbanis molestijs</b></i> — \"the troubles of the city\" (Copenhagen), contrasted with the rural retreat of Hven; the <i>-ij-</i> spelling (<i>molestijs</i>) is the period orthography, kept in the source block.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Datae Huenae die 5. Iulij Anno 1579</i> = \"Given at Hven, 5 July 1579\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1579. 5. juli. Hven</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition / witness.</b> Edition of record: Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> (the <i>Epistolae</i> volumes). The sole cited witness is a <b>copy</b> (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Tycho Brahe's private archive, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen — so the readings (e.g. <i>Ezoccus</i>) descend through a transcript, not an autograph.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Closing valediction corrected from an expansion of Tycho's own name ('T[ycho].') to its actual sense, the epistolary abbreviation 'Tuus' (Yours) — the same convention used elsewhere in TBOO for this abbreviation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is short and syntactically secure, and the translation is sound throughout. The residual uncertainties are the <b>identities</b> of the unnamed <i>Dominus Legatus</i>, the <i>Quaestor Regius</i>, <i>Magister Laurentius</i>, <i>Doctor Iohannes Franciscus</i>, and especially <i>D. Ezoccus</i> (an odd form, possibly a copyist's spelling in the surviving <i>Afskrift</i>) — all left in their epistolary forms rather than resolved to guessed identifications, and flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.55-56 (Dreyer, 'Doctissimo viro magistro Andreae Severini Welleio') as page images; letter entire on p.55. Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word including the guest list (Laurentius, Iohannes Franciscus, Ezoccus), the Quaestor Regius passage, and the dateline 'Datae Huenae die 5. Iulij Anno 1579'. English translation checked clause-by-clause and is faithful throughout except for one low-severity mistranslation of the closing 'T.' abbreviation (Tuus misread as Tycho), which does not change what a scholar would take from the letter's content.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). The valediction abbreviation 'T.' immediately above the printed signature 'TYCHO BRAHE' is the standard Latin epistolary abbreviation 'Tuus' (Yours), not an abbreviation of Tycho's own name — the same convention appears elsewhere in TBOO (e.g. Bordingus's letter closes 'T. studiosissimus IACOBUS BORDINGUS' = 'Thy most devoted, Jacob Bording', correctly rendered there). Expanding it as '[Tycho]' produces an odd redundant reading ('T[ycho]. Tycho Brahe.') where the source has a normal 'Yours, Tycho Brahe' closing formula. At: “T[ycho].<br>Tycho Brahe.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the letter's structure against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.55, confirming the 'T. / TYCHO BRAHE.' two-line sign-off recurs identically for this and the immediately following Dançay letter on the same page, consistent with the standard Latin epistolary 'Tuus' valediction cited in the audit's parallel (Bordingus) example. Only the closing English clause was touched; the Latin is untouched per the MISTRANSLATION repair rule.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15790521001", "next": "brahe-t_15790815001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Invites Vedel and the Lord Legate to visit Hven, urging that Laurentius, Doctor Franciscus, Master Ezoccus, and the Royal Treasurer also accompany them.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790705001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790705002", "d": "1579-07-05", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Charles de Dançay", "ck": "Charles de Dançay", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Charles de Dançay — Hven, 5 July 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / contemporary copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET GENEROSO VIRO DOMINO CAROLO DANZAeO, REGIS GALLIARUM LEGATO ET CONSILIARIO PRUDENTISSIMO, AMICO SUO PERPETUO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine DANZAeE, Amice semper colende, pollicitus es cum ultimo Haffniae simul essemus, te Huenenses Lares invisurum, quam primum intelligeres me huc reversum esse, quia vero interea temporis nunc e Selandia in Scaniam, nunc ex hac rursus in illam, partim Regis partim Amicorum negotijs et ex parte etiam proprijs impellentibus iactatus sum tanquam pila missus ab illis, ut noster de aulica senectute dixit PALINGENIUS, adeo ut ante hoc tempus non licuerit nisi aliquantulam requiem hîc figere. Peto itaque ut nunc promissis stare et cum Amicis quoscunque elegeris meas Penates utquamvis pro conditione temporis et loci tenues, tamen ab urbium solenni fastu animum quietis anhelantem nonnihil revocantes invisere non dedigneris. Si Dominus Quaestor Regius comitem se adiungere prae negotijs publicis, quibus occupatur, non detrectaverit, fecerit et is mihi rem gratam. Scripsi M. ANDREAe, quem puto una venturum, secumque M. LAURENTIUM nostrum adducturum. Tuum vero erit quoscunque voles unâ adducere.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Huenae 5. Iulii Anno 1579.<br>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and noble gentleman Master Charles de Dançay, ambassador and most prudent counsellor of the King of France, his perpetual friend, most worthy to be honoured.</b></p><p>Magnificent Master Dançay, ever to be esteemed friend, you promised, when we were last together at Copenhagen, that you would visit the household of Hven as soon as you should learn that I had returned here; but because in the meantime I have been tossed about — now from Zealand into Scania, now back again from the latter into the former — driven partly by the King's affairs, partly by my friends', and in part by my own, \"like a ball flung\" by them, as our Palingenius said about old age at court, so much so that until now I have not been able to fix more than a very little rest here. I ask, therefore, that you now stand by your promise, and that you not disdain to visit, together with whatever friends you may choose, my household gods — slender though they are, as befits the condition of the time and place, yet recalling somewhat a mind that pants for quiet from the solemn pomp of the cities. If the Lord Royal Treasurer should not decline to join you as a companion, despite the public affairs with which he is occupied, he will do me too a welcome service. I have written to Master Andreas, who I think will come along, and will bring with him our Master Laurentius. But it will be yours to bring along whomever you wish.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Hven, 5 July, the year 1579.<br>T[ycho].<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Carolus Danzaeus</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (d. 1589), the long-serving resident ambassador of the King of France (then <b>Henri III</b>) at the Danish court — <i>regis Galliarum legato et consiliario</i> — and an early friend and supporter of Tycho. The honorific style <i>magnifico et generoso viro … amico suo perpetuo plurimum observando</i> is rendered in full per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Author/place.</b> Written by Tycho from <b>Hven</b> (<i>Huena</i>; the island granted him by Frederik II in 1576, seat of Uraniborg), inviting Dançay to come over and visit. <i>Huenenses Lares</i> / <i>meas Penates</i> — literally \"the Lares of Hven\" / \"my Penates\" (household gods); rendered \"the household of Hven\" / \"my household gods,\" the domestic-deity idiom kept for the warmth it carries.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Haffnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b> (§5.7); <i>Selandia</i> = <b>Zealand</b>; <i>Scania</i> = <b>Scania</b> (Skåne, then Danish) — Tycho's family seat Knudstrup lay in Scania, hence the shuttling \"now from Zealand into Scania, now back again.\"</li><li><b><i>Palingenius.</b></i> <i>ut noster … dixit PALINGENIUS</i> = \"as our Palingenius said\": <b>Marcellus Palingenius Stellatus</b> (Pier Angelo Manzolli, c. 1500–c. 1543), author of the widely-read didactic poem <i>Zodiacus Vitae</i>. <i>de aulica senectute</i> = \"about old age at court\" (a theme of the poem's courtly books); the simile <i>iactatus … tanquam pila missus ab illis</i> = \"tossed about … like a ball flung [to and fro] by them\" echoes Palingenius's image of the courtier as a plaything. The quotation-marks in the translation flag <i>pila missus</i> as the borrowed phrase; \"by them\" (<i>ab illis</i>) refers back to the King's, friends', and his own affairs.</li><li><b><i>Dominus Quaestor Regius</b></i> — \"the Lord <b>Royal Treasurer</b>\" (the Danish <i>rentemester</i> / royal <i>quaestor</i>); a specific officer Tycho hopes will accompany Dançay, here left by his office rather than named.</li><li><b><i>M. Andreae … M. Laurentium nostrum.</b></i> Two academic <i>Magistri</i> (\"Master,\" §5.5) Tycho has invited. <i>M. Andreae</i> (dative of <i>Andreas</i>) is most plausibly <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (Andreas Severini Vellejus), Tycho's close friend and correspondent (cf. §5.6), though the bare praenomen does not put it beyond doubt; <i>M. Laurentium nostrum</i> (\"our Master Laurentius\") is a <b>Lauritz/Laurentius</b> of Tycho's circle, not further specified in the text. <b>Identification crux:</b> the letter gives only the forenames, so neither Master can be fixed with certainty from this text alone; flagged rather than asserted.</li><li><b>Orthography (humanist Latin).</b> <i>negotijs, proprijs, Penates … anhelantem</i>: the <i>-ijs</i> spellings are the printed/scribal <i>-iis</i>; <i>hîc</i> and <i>unâ</i> carry the source's circumflexes. <i>DANZAeO / DANZAeE</i>: the <i>Ae</i> ligature of <i>Danzaeus</i> reproduced as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Huenae 5. Iulii Anno 1579</i> = \"Given at Hven, 5 July 1579\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline matches the catalogue label (1579-07-05, Hven); no discrepancy. The subscription <i>T. / Tycho Brahe</i> is rendered \"T[ycho]. / Tycho Brahe,\" the abbreviated first line bracketed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the dropped pronoun 'is' in 'fecerit et is mihi rem gratam', per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.56; no change to the English, which already supplied the implicit 'he'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the sense is not in doubt; the one open point is the identification of the two invited <i>Magistri</i> (<i>M. Andreae</i>, probably Anders Sørensen Vedel, and <i>M. Laurentium</i>), which the text supplies only by forename — flagged above, not resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter to Charles de Dançay against TBOO VII pp.55-56 page images (re-checked the disputed clause at higher resolution), including the Palingenius quotation, every proper name (Andreas, Laurentius) and the closing date, 5 July 1579. Found one TEXT_ERROR: source_text drops the pronoun 'is' present in Dreyer's 'fecerit et is mihi rem gratam'. No mistranslation resulted from it.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text drops the emphatic subject pronoun 'is' (he) present in Dreyer's print. The English translation's implicit 'he' subject is unaffected in sense (Latin does not require an explicit pronoun), but the Latin transcription itself is corrupted by the omission. Dreyer prints: <i>fecerit et is mihi rem gratam</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.56, which carries the continuation of this letter (opening 'prae negotijs publicis...fecerit et is mihi rem gratam.'). The reading is unambiguous. Only the flagged clause was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Charles de Dançay", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15761117001", "next": "brahe-t_15830821001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15761117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reminds Dançay of his promise to visit Hven, explains delays caused by travel between Zealand and Scania, and renews the invitation to come with friends.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790705002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15790815001", "d": "1579-08-15", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Hven, 15 August 1579 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEIUM.<br>S.</p><p>EODEM vesperi quo reversus sum domum, scripsi Μ. MACHABAeO, clarissime Domine Magister ANDREA, de equis, et tuas unâ illi misi literas, et effeci ut eadem nocte literae transveherentur, ut summo mane sequentis diei Lundam venirent. Ego interea nihil ab eo responsi accepi, si tu aliquid, nescio. Equos si ab ipso non acceperis vide ut Haffniae habeas, ego stabo promissis. Mitto tibi nunc literas ad Amicos in Germania, Norimbergae ad CAMERARIUM, cui per te HOMELIJ Gnomonica remitto. Scripsi illi ut earum Bibliothecarum quae in ipsorum urbe sunt, aliarumque memorabilium rerum tibi copiam faceret. Augustae ad Consulem PAULUM HAINZELIUM et HIERONIMUM WOLFIUM, cui etiam tuos conatus commendavi, ut eos pro virili iuvarent. Scripsi etiam quasdam literas in aedes hospitis mei LAURENTII DEN et peto, ut si qua ratione fieri poterit, efficias ut Globum meum magnum, quem ipse conservat, ante hyemem habere possim; poteris si quos Augustae emeris libros, ei includere et bene exterius interiusque compactum meo sumptu huc transferri curare. Norimbergae etiam effice, ut certo ante hyemem ea quae ad aquaeductum meum requiruntur, a GEORGIO LAUBENWOLF Artifice, cui eo nomine prius per te scripsi, obtineam, et vide quid in Machina quam pro Rege parat, paratum sit, et urge opus tanquam id in mandatis a Rege et me haberes. Augustae videbis et civitatis et civium instructissimas Bibliothecas, inprimis vero Fuggeranam idque per WOLFIUM consequi poteris. Cum Hospite meo LAURENTIO loquere an quicquam de Fratre meo CANUTO intellexerit, et utrum sciat ipsum Venetias ivisse, et an adhuc ibi commoretur, et si is tibi inciderit, vel de illo quicquam audiveris ubi sit, scribe illi meo nomine, ut sit bono animo: Regem esse placabilem et omnia in melius, quam adversarij putant, cessura. Sed haec quae de hoc scribo, in secreto coram omnibus et hîc et alibi habe. Si quos libros invenies in Germania, vel de Cometa praeter eos, quos habeo, vel aliquid singulare in Astronomicis, mihi coëmas velim, ego pretium lubens restituam.</p><p>Bene vale, charissime Domine M. ANDREA, et Deus te feliciter et incolumem ducat et reducat.<br>Datae Huenae 15. Augusti Anno 1579.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel. Greeting.</b></p><p>On the very evening on which I returned home, I wrote to Master Machabæus, most distinguished Master Anders, about the horses, and sent your letter to him along with mine, and arranged that the letters be carried across that same night, so that they should reach Lund at first light the next day. In the meantime I have had no reply from him; whether you have had any, I do not know. If you do not receive the horses from him, see that you have [some] at Copenhagen — I shall stand by my promises. I now send you letters to friends in Germany: at Nuremberg, to Camerarius, to whom through you I am returning Homelius's <i>Gnomonica</i>. I have written to him to put at your disposal the libraries that are in their city, and other things worth seeing. At Augsburg, to Consul Paul Hainzel and to Hieronymus Wolf, to whom I have also commended your endeavours, that they may help them with all their might. I have also written certain letters [to be delivered] to the house of my host Laurentius Den, and I ask that, if by any means it can be done, you bring it about that I may have my great globe — which he is keeping — before winter; and if you buy any books at Augsburg you can enclose them with it, and, well packed without and within, see to it that [it all] be conveyed here at my expense. At Nuremberg, too, bring it about that I certainly obtain, before winter, the things required for my aqueduct, from the craftsman Georg Laubenwolf — to whom on that account I have written earlier through you — and see what has been got ready in the machine he is preparing for the King, and press the work forward as if you had it in charge from the King and from me. At Augsburg you will see the most fully furnished libraries both of the city and of its citizens, but above all the Fugger library; and this you will be able to obtain access to through Wolf. Speak with my host Laurentius [to learn] whether he has understood anything about my brother Knud, and whether he knows that he has gone to Venice, and whether he is still staying there; and if he should fall in with you, or you hear anything of him as to where he is, write to him in my name to be of good cheer: that the King is appeasable, and that everything will turn out for the better than his adversaries suppose. But keep what I write about this matter secret in the presence of everyone, both here and elsewhere. If you find any books in Germany — whether about the comet, besides those I have, or anything notable in astronomy — I should like you to buy them up for me, [and] I shall gladly repay the cost.</p><p>Fare you well, dearest Master Anders, and may God lead you forth and bring you back happily and unharmed.<br>Given at Hven, 15 August, the year 1579.<br>T[ycho].<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> to <i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the Danish historian, Royal Historiographer and Ribe canon (later the editor of the <i>Hundredvisebog</i> ballad collection); Tycho's close friend from their shared student years. Vedel is here evidently about to set out on a journey through Germany, and the letter is a travel-and-errands brief: Tycho hands him letters of introduction and a list of commissions to execute en route.</li><li><b><i>M. Machabæo</b></i> — \"Master Machabæus,\" addressed at/near <b>Lund</b> (then Danish) concerning horses. The Macchabæus (Macalpine) family were prominent Danish humanists of Scottish origin descended from the reformer Johannes Machabæus (d. 1557); the individual here is most plausibly a Lund cleric/canon of that family. <i>Identification flagged:</i> the source gives only the surname and the academic title <i>M[agister]</i>; the precise individual is not securely fixable from the letter alone.</li><li><b><i>Camerarius</b></i> (Nuremberg) — <b>Joachim Camerarius the Younger</b> (1534–1598), the Nuremberg physician and botanist, a learned correspondent through whom Tycho routed introductions and books.</li><li><b><i>Homelii Gnomonica</b></i> — the <i>Gnomonica</i> (treatise on dialling / construction of sundials) associated with <b>Johann Hommel</b> (Homelius, 1518–1562), the Leipzig mathematician. Tycho is <b>returning</b> (<i>remitto</i>) a copy to Camerarius via Vedel; <i>Gnomonica</i> kept as the work-title.</li><li><b><i>Consul Paulus Hainzelius</b></i> — <b>Paul Hainzel</b> of Augsburg (one of the Hainzel brothers of the 1571 Wolf letter); <i>Consul</i> here = <b>Bürgermeister</b> (city magistrate) of Augsburg, with whom Tycho had built the great Göppingen quadrant in 1569. <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and librarian (see brahe-t_15711113001).</li><li><b><i>Laurentius Den</b></i> — <b>Lorenz (Laurentius) Den</b>, Tycho's host (<i>hospes</i>) during his Augsburg residence (1569–70), now the custodian of Tycho's <i>Globus magnus</i> (great globe) awaiting shipment north.</li><li><b><i>Globus meus magnus</b></i> — \"my great globe\": Tycho's large celestial globe, ordered/begun in Augsburg in his earlier stay and famously not fully completed and delivered to Hven until years later (it was finished at Uraniborg). The instruction to retrieve it \"before winter\" fits the period when Tycho was still drawing his Augsburg-made apparatus home.</li><li><b><i>Georgio Laubenwolf Artifice</b></i> — <b>Georg Labenwolf</b> (Laubenwolf), the Nuremberg bronze-founder and waterworks/fountain engineer (the family responsible for the Nuremberg \"Gänsemännchen\" fountain). He is independently documented as the maker of the <i>Vandkunsten</i> (waterworks) at Kronborg for Frederik II — which corroborates both the <i>aquaeductus</i> commission for Uraniborg and the reference to \"the machine he is preparing for the King.\" <i>Artifex</i> rendered \"craftsman.\"</li><li><b><i>aquaeductum meum / Machina … pro Rege</b></i> — \"my aqueduct\" = the water-supply / fountain works for Uraniborg; the \"machine he is preparing for the King\" = a parallel hydraulic commission for Frederik II (consistent with Labenwolf's documented Kronborg waterworks). Tycho asks Vedel to chase the Uraniborg parts and to inspect progress on the royal machine, <i>\"as if you had it in charge from the King and from me.\"</i></li><li><b><i>Bibliotheca Fuggerana</b></i> — \"the Fugger library,\" the renowned Augsburg book-collection of the Fugger merchant-banking dynasty; access to be arranged through Wolf (who served the Fuggers as librarian).</li><li><b><i>Frater meus Canutus</b></i> — <b>Knud Brahe</b> (1555–1615), Tycho's younger brother, then reported to have gone to <b>Venice</b> (<i>Venetias</i>). The passage that follows is the letter's confidential core: Knud was at this time in difficulty with the crown, and Tycho wants reassurance passed to him \"in my name\" — <i>that the King is appeasable</i> (<i>Regem esse placabilem</i>) and matters will turn out better than his adversaries expect — with the express instruction to keep the whole affair <b>secret</b> before everyone, here and elsewhere.</li><li><b><i>de Cometa</b></i> — \"about the comet\": Tycho's standing interest in cometary literature (the great comet of 1577, on which he was then at work). He asks Vedel to buy up any comet-books or notable astronomical works he does not already own, against repayment.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem [dicit]</i>, \"greeting\"; <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well.\" <i>Domine M[agister] Andrea</i> = \"Master Anders\" (academic <i>Magister</i>). Subscription <i>T. / TYCHO BRAHE</i> preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Huenae 15. Augusti Anno 1579</i> = \"Given at <b>Hven</b> (the island of Hven, seat of Uraniborg), 15 August 1579\" (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dataset `label` (\"1579. 15. august. Hven\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> The single recorded witness (A) is a later copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Tycho Brahe's private archive at the Rigsarkivet, not an autograph; readings follow Dreyer.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Source prints <i>Μ.</i> (the Greek capital mu, for Latin <i>M[agister]</i>) before <i>MACHABAeO</i> — retained as printed in the Source block; it stands for the Latin abbreviation and is rendered \"Master.\" Ligature <i>ae</i> (as in <i>MACHABAeO</i>, <i>Vesperi</i>-clause forms) and <i>unâ / hîc</i> circumflexes are kept as printed.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and the substantive errands are unambiguous. The one soft point is the identity of <i>M. Machabæus</i> (surname + title only; identified by family, not individual — flagged above, not invented). The reading and sense of the confidential Knud-Brahe passage and the Labenwolf aqueduct/royal-machine commission are sound and externally corroborated.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.56-58 as page images (p.56 begins mid-page with 'TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEIUM', after the tail of an unrelated 5 July 1579 letter; the body concludes on p.57 before the next letter, confirmed by the matching closing date). Collated every proper/place-name in the commission list - Machabaeus, Camerarius, Homelius's Gnomonica, Nuremberg, Paul Hainzel, Hieronymus Wolf, Augsburg, Laurentius Den, the great globe, Georg Laubenwolf, the Fugger library, and the brother Knud (Canuto) in Venice - plus the closing date (15 Augusti Anno 1579, Huenae). source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english renders all of it accurately with no fabrication or omission.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15790705001", "next": "brahe-t_15840216001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports writing to Machabæus about horses and encloses letters of introduction to Camerarius, Hainzel, and Wolf in Nuremberg and Augsburg for Vedel's forthcoming journey.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15790815001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15800208001", "d": "1580-02-08", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 8 February 1580 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HIERONYMUS WOLFIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S.</p><p>CUM hi populares tui, nobilissime et humanissime D. TYCHO, suam mihi operam benigne pollicerentur, nolui eos sine meis literis dimittere. Scias ergo me pro aetate mediocri esse valetudine, neque quicquam habere, quod de fortuna etiam magnopere querar, sorte mea philosophica contentus. Sed quid interim Uranie tua? Illam quidem non dormitare, non cessare persuasum habemus. Sed nihil ad nos propter locorum intervalla hactenus est perlatum. Quod tamen ut fiat, si pro humanitate tua curaveris, rem nobis gratissimam feceris. Nostrates Astrologi, CYPRIANUS, TURNEISSERUS, STADIUS, HEINSCHIUS et alij multum a vero tempore initij proximae Eclipseos aberrarunt. A MAeSTLINO diu nihil accepimus. Motus Solis verus adhuc valde controversus est; qui utinam accuratis tuis observationibus certo deprehendatur et constituatur. Quod quo facilius praestare possis, Deum tibi propitium precor.</p><p>Vale.<br>Augustae Vindelicorum 8. Februarij Anno 1580.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Since these countrymen of yours kindly promised me their service, most noble and most courteous Master Tycho, I was unwilling to let them depart without a letter from me. Know, then, that for my age I am of middling health, and that I have nothing for which I might even greatly complain of fortune, content as I am with my philosopher's lot. But what, meanwhile, of your Urania? That she does not slumber, does not idle, we are persuaded. Yet nothing has thus far been brought to us, on account of the distances between our places. But if, in keeping with your kindness, you will see to it that this comes about, you will do me a most welcome thing. Our [German] astrologers — Cyprian [Leowitz], Thurneisser, Stadius, Heinschius, and many others — strayed far from the true time of the beginning of the recent eclipse. From Maestlin we have for a long while received nothing. The true motion of the Sun is still very much in dispute; would that it be securely ascertained and established by your accurate observations. And that you may the more easily accomplish this, I pray God be favourable to you.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Augsburg, 8 February, the year 1580.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (the \"father of Byzantine studies\"), then directing the city's library and school; an old mentor-friend of Tycho's from his Augsburg years (1569–70). This is one of his last letters: he died in October 1580, and the note's remark on his \"middling health\" stands against that backdrop.</li><li><b><i>hi populares tui</b></i> — \"these countrymen of yours\": Danish acquaintances of Tycho passing through Augsburg, who carry the letter and had offered Wolf their service. <i>Populares</i> here = \"fellow-countrymen / compatriots,\" not \"the common people.\"</li><li><b><i>sorte mea philosophica contentus</b></i> — \"content with my philosopher's lot\": the Stoic-humanist commonplace of contentment; kept literal rather than paraphrased to \"philosophical resignation.\"</li><li><b><i>Uranie tua</b></i> — \"your Urania,\" the Muse of astronomy, here a personification of Tycho's astronomical work; the conceit (she \"does not slumber, does not idle\") is preserved. Tycho had by 1580 begun building Uraniborg (named for this Muse) on Hven; Wolf's <i>Uranie tua</i> and the place-name resonate, but the letter speaks of the Muse/the work, not the building.</li><li><b>Astrology / astronomy.</b> <i>Nostrates Astrologi</i> = \"our [native, i.e. German] astrologers.\" The four named:</li><li><i>CYPRIANUS</i> = <b>Cyprian Leowitz</b> (Cyprianus Leovitius, 1524–1574), the Bohemian-born astrologer-astronomer active in Augsburg, author of widely-used ephemerides and eclipse tables. (The same Leowitz whose casting of Wolf's own geniture Tycho had corrected — see the 1571 Wolf letter.)</li><li><i>TURNEISSERUS</i> = <b>Leonhard Thurneisser zum Thurn</b> (1531–1596), the Basel-born physician, alchemist, and almanac-maker, then at the Brandenburg court in Berlin.</li><li><i>STADIUS</i> = <b>Johannes Stadius</b> (1527–1579), the Brabant mathematician and ephemerist (<i>Ephemerides novae</i>, based on the Prutenic Tables).</li><li><i>HEINSCHIUS</i> — most plausibly <b>Reinhold Heinsius / Heinisch</b>, a contemporary German almanac- and prognostication-maker; the identification is not fully secure (flagged below).</li><li><i>MAeSTLINO</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher), with whom Tycho and Wolf's circle corresponded.</li><li><b><i>proximae Eclipseos</b></i> — \"the recent eclipse\": an eclipse (almost certainly solar, given the predicted-time controversy and the following remark on the Sun's motion) shortly before this letter; Wolf's point is that the published German tables badly missed its <i>time of onset</i>. <i>Eclipsis</i> → \"eclipse\" per protocol §5.1.</li><li><b><i>Motus Solis verus … controversus</b></i> — \"the true motion of the Sun is still very much in dispute\": <i>motus</i> → \"motion\" (§5.1); Wolf names the unsettled solar theory as the prize Tycho's <i>accuratis observationibus</i> (\"accurate observations\") might decide — an early external statement of the observational programme that would become Tycho's <i>instauratio astronomiae</i>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Heading <i>S.</i> = <i>Salutem</i> → \"Greeting\" (§5.5); <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\" (§5.5); <i>D. TYCHO</i> = <i>Dominus</i> → \"Master\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Augustae Vindelicorum 8. Februarij Anno 1580</i> = \"at <b>Augsburg</b> (Augusta Vindelicorum), 8 February, the year 1580\" (O.S. — Augsburg/Lutheran reckoning still kept the Julian calendar in early 1580; the Gregorian reform came in October 1582). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (8 February 1580, Augsburg); no date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> <i>MAeSTLINO</i> and <i>DIVINAe</i>-type <i>ae</i>-ligatures are reproduced as printed in the source block. <i>initij</i>, <i>alij</i>, <i>intervalla</i> etc. carry the period <i>-ij</i> / consonantal-<i>i</i> spelling; rendered without comment in translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is short and renders securely; the one soft point is the identification of <i>HEINSCHIUS</i> (a minor almanac-maker whose period Latinisation is not certainly fixed), flagged above rather than asserted, and the implicit (solar) character of the <i>proxima Eclipsis</i>, which the text does not state in so many words.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII p.57 (first of two Wolf-to-Tycho letters printed on that page, entirely self-contained there). Latin matches source_text exactly. The list of German astrologers (Cyprianus, Turneisserus, Stadius, Heinschius), the note on Maestlin, the remark on the Sun's motion, and the date (Augsburg, 8 February 1580) are all rendered accurately. No defects found. Note: published_grade was B; this collates clean at A and should be upgraded.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15790325002", "next": "brahe-t_15800314001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15790000001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports his health, criticises German astrologers for errors in predicting the recent eclipse, and urges Tycho's accurate observations to settle the disputed true motion of the Sun.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15800208001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15800314001", "d": "1580-03-14", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck": "Hieronymus Wolf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Hieronymus Wolf → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 14 March 1580 [O.S.] (catalogued 14 January 1580)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HIERONYMUS WOLFIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>DUO Dani studiosi hâc transeuntes, qui se tua familiaritate aliquando usuros sperant, petierunt a me, ut sibi literulas ad Excellentiam tuam darem, quo se mecum fuisse probare possent. Etsi autem nuper nescio quid scripseram, neque nunc quicquam scriptione magnopere dignum habebam, tamen quia, mi nobilissime et doctissime D. TYCHO, libenter etiam absens tecum colloquor, morem illis gessi. In observatione proximae eclipseos nostrates Astrologi omnes, alij plus, alij minus, a vero initio aberrarunt. Exquisitiora abs te expectamus. Nova quae passim feruntur, e popularibus tuis cognosces. Augustana plaeraque antiquum obtinent. Consul HAINZELIUS tibi familiarissimus superiori anno elocavit filiam, eius frater duobus filijs locupletes puellas uxores dedit. Nos Scholastici etsi rerum nostrarum satagentes, asininam fortunam aequi bonique facimus; tuaeque Excellentiae coeleste illud et beatum insulae Uraniae, vel potius fortunatae, otium gratulamur.</p><p>Vale.<br>Augustae Vind. pridie Idus Martias Anno 1580.<br>T. Excellentiae studiosiss.<br>Hieron. Wolfius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hieronymus Wolf to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Two Danish students passing this way, who hope to enjoy your acquaintance one day, asked me to give them a little letter to Your Excellency, by which they might prove that they had been with me. And although I had lately written something or other [to you], and had nothing now much worth the writing, yet — since, my most noble and most learned Master Tycho, I gladly converse with you even when absent — I obliged them. In the observation of the recent eclipse all our astrologers, some more, some less, strayed from the true beginning [of it]. From you we expect more exact things. The news that is bandied about everywhere you will learn from your own countrymen. Most things at Augsburg keep to their old course. Consul Hainzel, your most intimate friend, married off a daughter last year; his brother gave wealthy girls in marriage to two sons. We scholastics, though busied about our own affairs, take our asinine fortune in good part; and we congratulate Your Excellency on that heavenly and blessed leisure of the isle of Urania — or rather, of the fortunate isle.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Augsburg, the day before the Ides of March [14 March], in the year 1580.<br>Most devoted to Your Excellency,<br>Hieronymus Wolf.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hieronymus Wolfius</i> = <b>Hieronymus Wolf</b> (1516–1580), the Augsburg humanist and Greek scholar (the \"father of Byzantine studies\"), then directing the city's library and school, and an early mentor-friend of Tycho's from his Augsburg years (1569–70). This is among the last of his letters to Tycho; Wolf died on 8 October 1580, some months after writing it.</li><li><b>Recipient/place.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, by 1580 established on <b>Hven</b> at <b>Uraniborg</b>; <i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i> = <b>Augsburg</b>.</li><li><b><i>Duo Dani studiosi</b></i> — \"two Danish students\": travelling Danes carrying the letter as a token of introduction (a commonplace of the humanist letter of recommendation). They are not named.</li><li><b><i>Consul Hainzelius … eius frater</b></i> — \"Consul Hainzel … his brother\": the <b>Hainzel brothers</b> of Augsburg, patrician patrons of astronomy with whom Tycho had collaborated (with Paul Hainzel he built the great 19-foot Göppingen quadrant in 1569). The title <i>Consul</i> denotes the Augsburg burgomaster's office; the source names neither brother individually, so which of the two (Johann Baptist or Paul) is the <i>Consul</i> and which the <i>frater</i> is left as the text leaves it, rather than assigned. The brother is said to have married off <b>two sons</b> to wealthy girls.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>in observatione proximae eclipseos … a vero initio aberrarunt</i> — \"in the observation of the recent eclipse … they strayed from the true beginning\": the Augsburg astrologers misjudged the moment of first contact; Wolf defers to Tycho for <i>exquisitiora</i>, \"more exact\" determinations. The eclipse is not named in the letter; a total lunar eclipse fell on 31 January 1580 (O.S.), which a mid-March letter could naturally call \"recent,\" but the identification is inferential and is not asserted by the text.</li><li><b><i>Astrologi</b></i> — rendered \"astrologers\" in the period's undifferentiated sense (astronomer-astrologers); no separate technical term is used.</li><li><b><i>insulae Uraniae, vel potius fortunatae</b></i> — \"the isle of Urania, or rather the fortunate isle\": a learned pun. <i>Insula Uraniae</i> names Hven after Urania, muse of astronomy (the conceit behind <i>Uraniborg</i>); Wolf glosses it as the <i>insula fortunata</i> — the \"Fortunate Isle\" of classical geography — playing on Tycho's blessed <i>otium</i> (leisure) there. The wordplay is kept rather than flattened.</li><li><b><i>Nos Scholastici … asininam fortunam aequi bonique facimus</b></i> — \"We scholastics … take our asinine fortune in good part\": <i>aequi bonique facere</i> is the idiom \"to take in good part, put up with\"; <i>asinina fortuna</i> (\"an ass's lot\") is Wolf's self-deprecating figure for the scholar's drudgery (cf. the <i>ergastulum</i> of his 1571 letter). Kept as metaphor.</li><li><b><i>Augustana plaeraque antiquum obtinent</b></i> — \"most things at Augsburg keep to their old course,\" i.e. little has changed; <i>plaeraque</i> = <i>pleraque</i>.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Humanist Latin: <i>hâc</i> (with circumflex, \"this way\"); <i>literulas</i> (diminutive, \"a little letter\"); consonantal <i>j</i> in <i>alij, filijs, popularibus tuis</i>; <i>plaeraque</i> for <i>pleraque</i>. Reproduced as printed in the Source block.</li><li><b>Salutation/subscription.</b> <i>T. Excellentiae studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tuae Excellentiae studiosissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to Your Excellency\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux.</b> Dateline: <i>pridie Idus Martias Anno 1580</i> = <b>the day before the Ides of March = 14 March 1580</b> (the Ides of March falling on the 15th), O.S. The dataset <b>catalogues</b> the letter as <i>\"1580. 14. januar\"</i> (14 January). The two disagree on the <b>month</b> (March vs January) while sharing the day (14th) and year (1580); the disagreement is plausibly a <i>Martias</i>/<i>Januarias</i> transcription or cataloguing slip. Per protocol §3 the as-written dateline governs the header — hence <b>14 March 1580</b>, with the catalogue date appended and the discrepancy flagged here rather than reconciled. No date is asserted that the source text does not substantiate.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout. Two points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>month discrepancy</b> between the dateline (14 March) and the catalogue label (14 January), resolved in the header in favour of the dateline per protocol but not otherwise reconciled; and (2) the <b>unnamed eclipse</b> and the <b>unassigned identities</b> of the <i>Consul</i> vs. <i>frater</i> Hainzel, both left as the text leaves them.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII pp.57-58 (second of two Wolf-to-Tycho letters printed on those pages; p.59 opens an unrelated Tycho-to-Hagecius letter, confirming the boundary). Latin matches source_text exactly. Every clause -- the two Danish students, the eclipse-observation remark, the Hainzel marriages, the 'asinine fortune' / Urania pun, and the date (pridie Idus Martias = 14 March 1580) -- is rendered accurately. No defects found. Note: published_grade was B; this collates clean at A and should be upgraded.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Hieronymus Wolf", "count": 7, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15800208001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15790000001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Accompanies two Danish students with a letter of introduction and notes that local astrologers erred in observing a recent eclipse, expecting more exact results from Tycho.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15800314001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15801104001", "d": "1580-11-04", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Hven (Uraniborg), 4 November 1580 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 10–12; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 30–31 (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET DOCTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO THADAeO HAGECIO, MEDICINAe ET PHILOSOPHIAe DOCTORI CELEBERRIMO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.</p><p>CLARISSIME et charissime D. D. THADDAeE. Binas a te recepi literas, ex quo Ratisbonae simul fuimus, et quamdiu licuit, illic familiaritatem contraximus. Quod autem prioribus nihil interea a me responsum sit, scias propterea minime factum esse, quod amicitiae nostrae immemor essem, et tui ob locorum et temporis intervalla oblitus. Nam nulla Horizontis vel locorum quantumvis magna intercapedo, vel temporis diuturnitas tui memoriam et amorem mihi adimet, sed inopia tabellariorum et commoditatum ad te ferendi meas literas effecit, ut hactenus nihil rescripserim, donec nunc tandem PAULUS WITTICHIUS alteras a te mihi attulerit, in quibus eum mihi multis commendas, iuvenem sane qui in Mathematicis exercitijs adeo sedulam hactenus operam navavit, ut non saltem dignus sit, qui ab omnibus bonis et doctis commendetur et ametur, sed etiam, qui ipsa commendatione maior sit. Fuit is mecum aliquandiu hîc in mea Astronomica Insula, et in triangulorum compendijs plura quam antea, communicatâ operâ, extricabimus. Nam et ego talibus insudavi atque in posterum ulterius, volente Numine, insudabo, quo haec ratio, quae per προσθαφαίρεσιν procedit absque taediosa multiplicatione et divisione, plenius excolatur et locupletetur, in quibus tamen ille necdum satis est versatus, sed assuescet successive. Visus est enim is, quod et sponte fatetur, saltem initia quaedam hîc iecisse admonitus ijs verbis, quae se a me audivisse, dum semel Wittebergam ipso illic studente transirem et me horum studiorum causa convenisset, licet ego eorum recordari non potuerim. Verum is nunc ne aliquid damni in re familiari capiat, patriam et suos revisere constituit, brevi huc ad me, veluti sponte dixit, reversurus, ut simul observationes instrumentis meis, quae varia et insigni magnitudine atque diligenti elaboratione nec parvis sumtibus et laboribus adorno, iucunde et utiliter exerceamus. Exstruo etiam insignem domum, quae instrumentorum varij generis commodam dispositionem admittet, unâ cum laboratorio peculiari pro chymicis exercitijs, praesertim quantum ad Medicinam Spagiricam spectat, continuandis. Perduxi autem non sine magno sumtu et constanti labore, multis exantlatis molestijs et difficultatibus, eo usque opus totum, ut Deo feliciter auspicante futura proxime septimana sim migraturus in novas illas Aedes Uraniae perpetuo, modo astra Borealia sic tulerint, dicatas, et illic nactus maiorem commoditatem tranquillitatemque, Deo volente, per otium hybernum observationes et demonstrationes nostras, quas habui varias et certas tam in ultimo maiori Cometa quam hoc minori, qui nunc conspicitur, absolvam, easque tibi et alijs eruditis communicabo; ubi inter alia certo ex diversis observationibus et rationibus demonstraturus sum, neutrum horum Cometarum exstitisse in Elementari regione, sed longe super Lunam in ipso aethere sedes et motum suum obtinuisse. Nam prior etiam Horizonti vicinissimus nunquam admisit parallaxin maiorem tertia parte unius gradus, sed semper minorem, uti multis rationibus infallibiliter demonstraturus sum. Huius, qui nunc durat, Cometae parallaxes adeo sunt exiguae, ut sint insensibiles pene, ut plane non dubitem eum esse aethereum. Tuae observationes in ultimo Cometa ob instrumentorum penuriam non fuerunt exactae neque intervallo 18 minutorum temporis per REGIOMONTANI rationem parallaxes ita variabantur, ut discerni et indagari poterant, etiamsi caetera, quae ad observationem requirebantur, quae sane non levia erant nec facile dabilia, recte se habuerunt. Neque Cometa unquam cum Aquila visus est culminare, nec si factum esset, parallaxis inde dari potuit, ut in Dialexi tua prae occupationibus minus considerate docuisti et in illo libro de Cometa exercuisti. Distantiae vero a stellis diversis horis factae et insensibiliter mutatae, ut ipsemet asseris, convincunt minime tantam fuisse parallaxin, quantam asseris, imo ne sensibilem quidem; sed de his alibi latius. SCULTETI demonstrationes falsis nituntur fundamentis et erroneis observationibus, ut ipse WITTICHIUS, a quo eas desumsit, nunc fatetur; ideo nihil probat, quod eandem fere tecum posuerit parallaxin. Sed brevi, Deo volente, habebis meas observationes et demonstrationes in utroque Cometa a rei veritate, quatenus in sensus cadit, non aberrantes. Addam etiam instrumentorum meorum formam et constructionem et Solaris cursus exquisitam restitutionem, quam praecedentibus annis inquisivi, adeo ut cum observationibus indies conveniat, veluti WITTICHIUS mecum saepe expertus est, a quibus calculus tam ALPHONSI quam COPERNICI nonnunquam semisse unius gradus, interdum paulo plus, aberrat. Longe enim alius est motus centri eccentrici Solis in suo circello, quam antecessores nostri deprehenderunt, vel ipse etiam COPERNICUS constituit, adeo ut eccentricitas Solis nunc sit 2p 5m scrupulis tredecim maior opinione COPERNICI. Apogaeum vero Solis iuxta 5p ♑ haeret longe anterius quam habent COPERNICI Hypotheses. Aliter enim apparentiae Solares non respondent, ut ex multis observationibus demonstravi, et brevi, Deo volente, communicabo eruditis. Deprehendi etiam celeriorem longe nunc esse motum octavae sphaerae, quam COPERNICUS constituit, adeo quod aequinoctia ferme quarta parte unius gradus celerius anticipârit, quod per Spicam facta observatione eadem via qua COPERNICUS multoties observavi et demonstravi. Sed de his et alijs data maiori commoditate et otio ad te latius perscribam.</p><p>Nunc unum addam, quod tua interesse et in tuum commodum ac rei familiaris incrementum spectare confido. Rettulit mihi PAULUS noster, quomodo a Machiavellicis istis invidis et osoribus stipendio in aula Caesaris privatus sis, neque interea certam et commodam vivendi rationem denuo assecutus es. Suaserim itaque, ut si res tuae ita ferant, huc in Daniam ad nos conferas, visurus quales hîc commodidates adipisci queas; certo enim tibi polliceor me effecturum apud Regiam Maiestatem, et apud primarios Regni nostri nobiles, ut si hîc Medicinam exercere velis, liberale et sufficiens salarium a Rege et a cetera nobilitate pro opera medica amplam remunerationem obtineas, et in honore et existimatione, uti par est, apud nostrates magno cum rei familiaris emolumento degas, modo hisce meis consilijs morem gesseris, qua in re, si ipsemet tam cito venire nequeas, mihi tuam voluntatem significes rogo; ego interea procurabo ut omnia, quae in tuum commodum spectare possint, apud nos copiosius et melius adipiscaris, quam in patria tua vel usquam alibi in reliqua Germania hactenus contigerunt. Tute igitur quid faciendum tibi sit, tecum delibera, et mihi, ut dixi, mature significa. De meis pollicitationibus non est quod quicquam dubites; efficiam enim ut experiaris in ipso effectu maiora his et omnia tibi Deo felicitante ex voto cessura. Deus Optimus Maximus te conservet, et in omni bono et utili proposito consilio et auxilio sit.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae in Insula nostra Huena 4. Novembris Anno 1580.<br>T. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius, most celebrated Doctor of Medicine and of Philosophy, his friend much to be honoured.</b></p><p>Most renowned and most dear Master Doctor Thaddaeus. I have received two letters from you since we were together at Regensburg, and there, for as long as we were allowed, struck up a close acquaintance. But that nothing has meanwhile been answered by me to the earlier of them — know that this came about by no means because I was unmindful of our friendship and had forgotten you through the intervals of place and time. For no gap of horizon or of places, however great, nor any length of time, shall take from me the memory and the love of you; rather it was the lack of letter-carriers and of opportunities of conveying my letters to you that brought it about that hitherto I have written nothing in reply — until now at last Paul Wittich has brought me a further letter from you, in which you commend him to me at length: a young man indeed who has up to now devoted such diligent effort to mathematical exercises that he is not merely worthy to be commended and loved by all good and learned men, but is even greater than the commendation itself. He was with me here for some while on my astronomical island, and in the shortcuts of triangles we shall, by shared labour, work out more than before. For I too have toiled at such things, and shall in future toil at them yet further, God willing, so that this method, which proceeds by <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> [προσθαφαίρεσιν], without tedious multiplication and division, may be more fully cultivated and enriched — in which, however, he is not yet sufficiently versed, but will grow accustomed to it by degrees. For he seems — as he himself freely admits — to have laid at least certain beginnings here, prompted by those words which [he says] he had heard from me, when once I was passing through Wittenberg while he was studying there and he had met me on account of these studies, although I myself could not recall them. But now, lest he take some loss in his domestic affairs, he has resolved to revisit his homeland and his own people, intending — as he said of his own accord — soon to return here to me, so that together we may pleasantly and usefully carry out observations with my instruments, which I am furnishing of various and remarkable size and with careful workmanship and at no small expense and labour. I am also building a notable house, which will allow a convenient arrangement of instruments of various kinds, together with a special laboratory for the pursuit of chemical exercises, especially so far as they concern Spagyric Medicine. And I have, not without great expense and constant labour, and after many troubles and difficulties endured, brought the whole work so far forward that — God prospering the venture — next week I am to move into those new lodgings dedicated to Urania forever (provided the Northern stars so allow), and there, having gained greater convenience and tranquillity, I shall, God willing, complete during the winter's leisure our observations and demonstrations — which I have had, various and sure, both on the last, greater Comet and on this lesser one now in view — and shall communicate them to you and to other learned men; where, among other things, I am going to demonstrate with certainty, from diverse observations and reasonings, that neither of these Comets existed in the Elementary region, but obtained its seat and motion far above the Moon, in the aether itself. For the earlier one, even when nearest the horizon, never admitted a parallax greater than a third part of one degree, but always less, as I shall demonstrate infallibly by many arguments. The parallaxes of this Comet now lasting are so small as to be almost imperceptible, so that I quite do not doubt it to be aethereal. Your observations on the last Comet were, owing to the scarcity of instruments, not exact; nor, over an interval of 18 minutes of time, did the parallaxes by Regiomontanus's method vary in such a way that they could be discerned and traced out — even granting that the rest of what was required for the observation (things by no means slight, nor easily supplied) were rightly disposed. Nor was the Comet ever seen to culminate with Aquila; and even if it had been, no parallax could be derived from that — as in your <i>Dialexis</i> you taught, less considerately, on account of your other occupations, and as you argued in that book on the Comet. But the distances from stars, taken at different hours and changed imperceptibly (as you yourself assert), prove that the parallax was by no means so great as you assert — nay, not even perceptible; but of these things more fully elsewhere. Scultetus's demonstrations rest on false foundations and erroneous observations, as Wittich himself — from whom he took them — now admits; therefore it proves nothing that he posited nearly the same parallax as you. But shortly, God willing, you shall have my observations and demonstrations on both Comets, not erring from the truth of the matter, so far as it falls within the senses. I shall add too the form and construction of my instruments, and an exquisite restoration of the Sun's course, which I have investigated in the preceding years, so that it agrees with the observations from day to day — as Wittich has often found with me — from which the calculation both of the Alphonsine [Tables] and of Copernicus sometimes errs by half of one degree, occasionally by a little more. For the motion of the centre of the Sun's eccentric in its little circle is far other than our predecessors detected, or than Copernicus himself laid down, so that the eccentricity of the Sun is now greater than Copernicus's estimate by 2 parts [degrees], 5 minutes, [and] thirteen seconds. And the Sun's apogee stands fixed near 5 degrees of ♑ Capricorn, far earlier than Copernicus's Hypotheses have it. For otherwise the solar appearances do not answer, as I have demonstrated from many observations, and shall shortly, God willing, communicate to the learned. I have also detected that the motion of the eighth sphere is now far swifter than Copernicus laid down, so that it anticipates the equinoxes by nearly a fourth part of one degree more swiftly — which, by an observation made through Spica, in the same way as Copernicus, I have many times observed and demonstrated. But of these and other matters I shall write to you more fully when greater convenience and leisure are given.</p><p>Now I shall add one thing, which I am confident concerns your interest and looks to your advantage and the increase of your estate. Our Paul has reported to me how you have been deprived of your stipend at the Emperor's court by those Machiavellian enviers and haters, and that you have not meanwhile secured anew any sure and convenient means of living. I would therefore advise that, if your affairs so allow, you betake yourself hither into Denmark to us, to see what advantages you may obtain here; for I promise you with certainty that I shall bring it about, with His Royal Majesty and with the foremost nobles of our Kingdom, that — if you are willing to practise Medicine here — you may obtain a liberal and sufficient salary from the King, and ample remuneration for your medical service from the rest of the nobility, and may live in honour and esteem, as is fitting, among our countrymen, with great profit to your estate — provided you comply with these counsels of mine; in which matter, if you yourself cannot come so quickly, I ask that you signify your will to me; I meanwhile will see to it that you obtain among us, more abundantly and better, everything that can look to your advantage, than has hitherto fallen to you in your homeland or anywhere else in the rest of Germany. Deliberate, then, with yourself what you ought to do, and signify it to me, as I have said, in good time. There is no reason for you to doubt anything of my promises; for I shall see to it that you experience, in the very effect, greater things than these, and that all things — God prospering it — fall out for you according to your wish. May God, Best and Greatest, preserve you, and be your counsel and aid in every good and useful purpose.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given on our Island of Hven, 4 November, in the year 1580.<br>Yours, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe writing from Hven; the recipient <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer and personal physician at the Imperial court in Prague. This is <b>Tycho's first letter to Hagecius</b> (corroborated by Solc, in <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague: Crossroads of European Science</i>), opening a correspondence that ran to Tycho's Bohemian years. The two had met in person at Regensburg (<i>Ratisbonae</i>) — the 1575 Imperial Diet, where Maximilian II was crowned.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Insula nostra Huena</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b> (Ven); <i>mea Astronomica Insula</i> (\"my astronomical island\"). The <i>novae Aedes Uraniae … dicatae</i> (\"new lodgings dedicated to Urania\") = <b>Uraniborg</b>, into which Tycho says he will move \"next week.\"</li><li><b>Paul Wittich.</b> <i>Paulus Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) of Wrocław (Breslau / \"the Wroclavian\"), the mathematician who visited Hven in 1580 and carried Hagecius's letter of recommendation. The passage is the locus classicus of the <b>prosthaphaeresis priority dispute</b>: Tycho claims Wittich \"freely admits\" he had merely \"laid certain beginnings here,\" <i>prompted by words Wittich said he had heard from Tycho</i> at Wittenberg — a claim Tycho hedges carefully (<i>licet ego eorum recordari non potuerim</i>, \"although I myself could not recall them\"; <i>veluti sponte dixit</i>, \"as he said of his own accord\"). The hedges are preserved, not sharpened; cf. Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, on Tycho's self-positioning as \"originator\" of the method.</li><li><b>Technical — <i>prosthaphaeresis</i>.</b> <i>haec ratio, quae per προσθαφαίρεσιν procedit absque taediosa multiplicatione et divisione</i> — the additive-subtractive computing method (using trigonometric product-to-sum relations) that replaces laborious multiplication and division in spherical-triangle reduction (<i>triangulorum compendia</i>, \"the shortcuts of triangles\"). The Greek <b>προσθαφαίρεσιν</b> (accusative of προσθαφαίρεσις, <i>prosthaphaíresis</i>, lit. \"addition-and-subtraction\") is kept in the Source block and rendered <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> per glossary §5.1 — never reduced to \"trig identity.\"</li><li><b>Comets.</b> <i>ultimus maior Cometa</i> = the <b>great comet of 1577</b>; <i>hoc minori, qui nunc conspicitur</i> = the <b>comet of 1580</b>, in view as Tycho wrote. Tycho's central claim: <i>neutrum horum Cometarum exstitisse in Elementari regione, sed longe super Lunam in ipso aethere</i> — neither comet lay in the sublunary <b>Elementary region</b> but far above the Moon, in the aether — established by the smallness of their <b>parallax</b> (1577: never more than ⅓ of a degree; 1580: \"almost imperceptible,\" <i>insensibiles pene</i>).</li><li><b>Critique of Hagecius's <i>Dialexis</i>.</b> <i>in Dialexi tua … et in illo libro de Cometa</i> = Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt 1574, on the 1572 nova) and his tract on the comet (<i>Descriptio cometae … anni 1577</i>, Prague 1578). Tycho faults the parallax method as too crude given instrument scarcity (<i>instrumentorum penuria</i>) and an 18-minute observing interval, faults the <i>culminating with Aquila</i> derivation, and notes the distances \"changed imperceptibly\" between hours — flagging Hagecius's claimed parallax as <i>not even perceptible</i>. The criticism is professional, softened by <i>prae occupationibus minus considerate</i> (\"less considerately, on account of your other occupations\").</li><li><b><i>Scultetus</i>.</b> <i>Sculteti demonstrationes</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Schultz, 1540–1614) of Görlitz, mathematician; Tycho says Scultetus's cometary demonstrations rest on Wittich's erroneous data, which Wittich \"now admits.\"</li><li><b>Solar theory (technical figures — verbatim).</b> <i>eccentricitas Solis nunc sit 2p 5m scrupulis tredecim maior opinione COPERNICI</i> = the solar eccentricity is greater than Copernicus's value by <b>2 parts, 5 minutes, thirteen seconds</b> (i.e. 2°5′13″); here <i>p</i> (<i>partes</i>) = degrees, <i>m</i> (<i>minuta</i>) = arc-minutes, <i>scrupula</i> = arc-seconds. <i>Apogaeum … iuxta 5p ♑</i> = the solar <b>apogee (apse)</b> near <b>5° of Capricorn</b> (♑ kept in Source, named in translation per §5.3). The Alphonsine and Copernican calculation is said to err by up to ~½° (<i>semisse unius gradus, interdum paulo plus</i>). <i>motus centri eccentrici Solis in suo circello</i> = the motion of the centre of the solar eccentric on its \"little circle\" (the Copernican small-circle device for the variable eccentricity). <b>Note:</b> the figures are reproduced exactly as written; \"2°5′13″ greater than Copernicus\" is Tycho's stated <i>difference</i>, not an absolute value — not silently reconciled to any modern figure.</li><li><b>Precession.</b> <i>celeriorem … motum octavae sphaerae … aequinoctia ferme quarta parte unius gradus celerius anticipârit</i> = the <b>motion of the eighth sphere</b> (precession) is now swifter, the equinoxes anticipated by nearly ¼° faster than Copernicus laid down; verified <i>per Spicam</i> (by observation of the star <b>Spica</b>, α Virginis, Copernicus's own precession reference star), <i>eadem via qua Copernicus</i> (\"in the same way as Copernicus\").</li><li><b>Medicine / alchemy.</b> <i>laboratorio … pro chymicis exercitijs … quantum ad Medicinam Spagiricam spectat</i> = the laboratory for chemical work, \"especially as concerns <b>Spagyric Medicine</b>\" (the Paracelsian alchemical pharmacy) — the period term kept, not modernised.</li><li><b>The invitation to Denmark.</b> <i>a Machiavellicis istis invidis et osoribus stipendio in aula Caesaris privatus sis</i> = Hagecius had lost his court stipend to \"those Machiavellian enviers and haters\" — Tycho offers to secure him a salaried medical post under <i>Regia Maiestas</i> (<b>Frederik II</b>) and the Danish nobility. Solc notes the invitation concerned Hagecius <b>as a physician</b>, not as an astronomer.</li><li><b>Authorities named.</b> <i>Regiomontani ratio</i> = the parallax method of <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller, 1436–76); <i>calculus … Alphonsi</i> = the <b>Alphonsine Tables</b>; <i>Copernicus</i> / <i>Copernici Hypotheses</i> = Copernicus and the planetary models of <i>De revolutionibus</i> (1543).</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Salutation <i>Clarissimo et doctissimo viro … amico suo plurimum colendo</i> and <i>Clarissime et charissime D. D. (= Domine Doctor) Thaddaee</i> rendered per §5.5; subscription <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Datae … 4. Novembris Anno 1580</i> in the dateline.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>4. Novembris Anno 1580</i> — Denmark was on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> throughout Tycho's career; the date is O.S. The dataset `label` (\"1580. 4. november. Hven\") agrees with the as-written dateline; <b>no date discrepancy</b>.</li><li><b>Editorial marks.</b> The superscript sigla in the working transcription (printed as raised letters keyed to the apparatus of the edition of record) have been silently dropped from both the Source block and the translation, as they are editorial reference marks, not part of the letter text. <i>DD</i>, <i>D.</i> expanded as <i>Dominus/Domine</i> per §5.5.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; register and the author's hedges (the prosthaphaeresis-priority qualifications) are preserved. The two soft points, both flagged above rather than smoothed, are (1) the precise force of <i>2p 5m scrupulis tredecim</i> as a stated <i>difference</i> from Copernicus's solar eccentricity (reproduced verbatim, not reconciled), and (2) the exact identity of \"that book on the Comet\" alongside the <i>Dialexis</i> in Tycho's compressed critique of Hagecius's cometary works.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.58-61 as page images (one Read call, 1-4). Letter (Tycho to Hagecius, 4 Nov 1580) runs from the heading 'CLARISSIMO ET DOCTISSIMO VIRO...' on p.58 through the signature on p.60; checked p.61 and confirmed it is the start of the next letter (to Bartholomaeus Scultetus, 12 Oct 1581), not part of this one. Collated the full Latin sentence-by-sentence against the printed page images: the Wittich/Regensburg framing, the prosthaphaeresis passage (Greek transliteration checked), the two-comet parallax argument, the Regiomontanus 18-minute interval (Dreyer marks this with an editorial crux dagger in the apparatus but the printed numeral itself, 18, matches source and translation exactly), the Scultetus/Wittich dispute, the solar eccentricity figures (2 degrees 5 minutes 13 seconds) and apogee (5 degrees Capricorn), the equinox-precession quarter-degree figure and the Spica observation, and the closing Hagecius-to-Denmark offer (Graz, Emperor's court reference) with the Hven/4 November 1580 dateline. All names (Wittich, Scultetus, Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Alphonsine), numbers, and the one place-name (Hven) in the English match Dreyer's print with no addition, omission, or flipped sense. Published grade was B (self-assigned, per brief, unverified); independent collation finds no defect and upgrades to A.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 10-12.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 30-31. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15760823001", "next": "brahe-t_15811012002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15760823001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15820501001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologises for the long silence, citing lack of letter-carriers, and introduces Paul Wittich, who has just delivered Hagecius's latest letter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15801104001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15811012001", "d": "1581-10-12", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Bartholomäus Scultetus — Uraniborg, 12 October 1581 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica </i>(27th collection, 1743, p. 177ff; reprinted Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ORNATISSIMOQUE VIRO, MAGISTRO BARTHOLOMAeO SCULTETO, GORLICENSI, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO VETERI PLURIMUM COLENDO.<br>S.</p><p>GRATUM mihi fuit, charissime SCULTETE, vir doctissime, quod nostri, et veteris amicitiae, Lipsiae olim inter nos initae, memoriam conserves. Id enim ex libellis, mihi a te dono missis (qui pergrati erunt) liquido perspexi. Pro qua tua benevolentia gratias tibi habeo maximas; daboque operam, ut intelligas, me tui etiam memoriam, quamvis multorum annorum locorumque intervallo disiuncti simus, ex animo minime obliterasse. Legi, et diligenter perspexi scriptum tuum de Cometa anni 77. magna diligentia a te elaboratum; sed ut ingenue et amice de ijs, quae sentio, te moneam, videris partim impossibilibus, partim datis minus exquisite observatis parallaxeos mensuram superstruere. Nam neque Calendis Ianuarij Cometa cum illis duabus stellis, quas assumis, supra Horizontem in eodem circulo Verticali esse poterat, neque stellarum intercapedo rite sumpta est, cum sit illa re vera plus ¾ unius gradus minor tua assignatione; ut de altitudinibus nihil dicam. Nec enim fieri poterat, ut spacio 12 minutorum temporis mutaretur parallaxis uno gradu 7 minutis, etiamsi adhuc longe propior Terrae statueretur Cometa; et ipsemet vides, ex tota inductione absurdum sequi. Nam quod capite octavo longitudinem Cometae 4 gradibus cum ½ fere discrepantem a priore positione calculus admittat, per angulorum angustiam minime excusare poterat. Sed existimo te destitutum exquisitis instrumentis et medijs, quibus observationes perficeres; alias demonstrationem tam accuratam et ingeniosam firmiori fundamento superstruxisses, reique veritatem propius attigisses. Ego sane varijs et exquisitis instrumentis, quae in promptu habeo, e solido metallo, tanta magnitudine ad amussim elaborata, ut singulorum minutorum sint capacia, eius Cometae apparentias observavi, et parallaxes multis modis geometrice demonstratas in numeros redegi. At nusquam invenire potui, Cometam illum parallaxin admittere tantam, quae unius gradus partem tertiam attingeret, ut suo loco sufficienter demonstrabimus. Ultimo vero Cometae apparentiae die, eodem usus observationis et demonstrationum adminiculo, sensibilem esse parallaxin certissimis rationibus deprehendi. Hinc omnibus huius rei amatoribus quam primum per otium licuerit, copiosiorem et sufficientem descriptionem, certis observationibus et demonstrationibus innixam, Deo volente proponam. Instrumenta, quibus in observationibus coelestibus utor, sunt eiusmodi, ut nihil in his, quod ad perfectionem faciat, desiderare possis. Quadrantes e solido metallo fabricatos, Azimutha scrupulose unâ indicantes, tantae magnitudinis habeo, ut singula minuta, quaedam etiam cuiuslibet minuti quartam partem discernant. Armillas HYPARCHI et PTOLOMAeI metallicas, singulorum minutorum capaces, quae novis quibusdam compendijs ad amussim elaboratae sunt, habeo. Sextantem Astronomicum, pro capiendis stellarum distantijs, exquisitius et facilius quam per Radium, quo unâ tamen utor, mira tractandi commoditate pridem parari feci. Globum apprime rotundum, sex pedes in diametro exuperantem, construi curavi, qui singulorum pene minutorum capax, stellarum loca, e coelo per veras distantias desumpta, continebit, et in numeros facili compendio resolvet. Torquetum Armillare magnum e metallo solido, tum etiam regulas HYPARCHI, Azimutha unâ cum altitudinibus monstrantes, longitudine sufficienti nunc confici curo, pluraque alia, Astronomicis exercitijs inservientia, organa, magna et exquisita, partim perfecta habeo, partim indies perfici elaboro. Aedificium etiam, quod Uraniae nomine insignivi, in medio huius Insulae his Astronomiae exercitijs et Spagyricis laboribus apprime opportunum, nec minus secundum architectonicae artis decoram symmetriam extructum, magno sumptu nunc tandem absolvi. Impendi autem tum in hoc, tum in instrumentorum constructionem, intra quinquennium proxime elapsum, plus * ib millibus Ioachimicorum. Serio enim, et pro rei dignitate Astronomica hîc tractanda, commoditatesque aptas iudicavi constituendas, minime ignorans, non esse cuiusque, sumtus tanto negotio rite instruendo sustinere, et eos, qui possint, id in alijs rebus ludicris potius praestare, quam ut tantae scientiae facultates impendant. Quam cupide optarem, ut hic mihi simul adesses! Videres sane ea, quae te non mediocriter oblectarent. Sed quia id fieri vix potest (uxorem enim, uti audio, duxisti) et Reipublicae insuper das operam, effice, ut per literas saltem crebrius confabulemur. Invenies me in conservanda vel per has amicitia minime negligentem. Mitto tibi fasciculum literarum, et te obnixe rogatum volo, ut per certum hominem ad Dominum D. THADEUM HAGECIUM Pragam illas transferri procurare velis. Dedi PAULO WITICHIO a nobis discedenti quasdam ipsi etiam mittendas, quas tamen minus fideliter procuravit, et multis alijs minus sincere mecum egisse (cum tamen benevole fuerit exceptus) deprehendi. Cum enim mea, quae nancisci poterat, clam abstulisset, observandique sidera nostris instrumentis compendia et rationes perspexisset (quibus tamen tractandis minime idoneus erat, nam ne unam quidem observationem aut Cometae aut alicuius stellae, vel Radio saltem capere illi possibile erat, nedum ut Cometae parallaxes deprehenderet, prout DUDITIO frustra persuasit) tandem sub praetextu, quod avunculus eius mortuus esset, haereditatem sibi deberi professus, hinc domum reversus est, et, quo mirabar maxime, finxit se intra paucas hebdomadas reversurum, cum tamen ego eius opera minime indigerem, eoque potius carerem, quam ut multo labore mihi comparata clam a me subduceret, sibique alibi arrogaret, quod, uti in Cometa fecit, sic etiam in Solaris cursus restitutione, et octavae Sphaerae emendatione, quam a nobis nactus est, facturum non dubito. Sed reddidit is me prudentiorem, effecitque, ne cuivis posthac mea committam. At, quia Solaris cursus a nobis restituti mentio facta est, mitto tibi anni proxime futuri Ephemerim Solarem, ex nostra motuum ratione, quam annis aliquot praeteritis, praesertim 78, de coelo instrumentis et Geometricis demonstrationibus in numeros redegi. Hanc si cum ipso coelo, beneficio alicuius instrumenti, contuleris, invenies eam motui Solis, quantum sensus discernere possunt, apprime congruere, a quo tamen uterque calculus Astronomicus hactenus usurpatus longe interdum aberrat, ecclipsibus etiam id ipsum non obscure ostendentibus. Tu itaque per instrumenta nostros motus examina, ita tamen, ut Poli altitudo in ipso scrupulo nota sit, et parallaxeos Solaris habeatur ratio, instrumentique tanta fuerit magnitudo, ut singula minuta commode discernat. Sed, ne videar epistolae metas transcendere, tecum amplius colloqui ipsa charta nos prohibet. Tuum erit per occasionem mihi rescribere. Si quae praeterea fuerint edita, quae a me expeti posse iudicaveris, ut DUDITIJ de Cometis et Astrologia libellum, et si quae sunt alia huiusmodi, peto ut ea ad me transmittere velis. Ego, ubi per otium (ut spero) hybernum, mea, quae proposui de Cometis, absolvero, tecum ea, et si quae habuero alia, tibi grata, lubens communicabo, daboque operam, ut me perpetuo tui memorem experiaris.</p><p>Vale, mi SCULTETE, et mei memoriam, uti facis, conserva.<br>Datae Uranienburgi in Insula nostra Huaena 12. Octobr. An. 1581.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, Danus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and accomplished man, Master Bartholomäus Scultetus of Görlitz, an outstanding mathematician, an old friend much to be honoured.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>It was welcome to me, dearest Scultetus, most learned man, that you keep alive the memory of our old friendship, formed between us long ago at Leipzig. For I clearly perceived this from the little books sent to me by you as a gift (which will be most welcome). For this kindness of yours I give you the greatest thanks; and I shall take care that you understand that I too, although we are sundered by an interval of many years and places, have by no means erased the memory of you from my heart. I have read, and diligently examined, your treatise on the comet of [the year] '77, worked out by you with great diligence; but — to advise you frankly and in friendship of what I think — you seem to build the measure of your parallax partly upon impossibilities, partly upon data less than exactly observed. For neither could the comet, on the Kalends of January [1 January], have been above the horizon on the same vertical circle as those two stars which you assume, nor is the interval between the stars rightly taken, since it is in truth more than ¾ of one degree less than your assignment — to say nothing of the altitudes. For it could not happen that in the space of 12 minutes of time the parallax should change by one degree and 7 minutes, even were the comet placed far closer still to the Earth; and you yourself see that an absurdity follows from the whole induction. For as to the fact that in the eighth chapter your calculation admits a longitude of the comet differing by nearly 4½ degrees from the earlier position, this could by no means be excused by the narrowness of the angles. But I judge that you were lacking the exact instruments and means by which you might perfect your observations; otherwise you would have built a demonstration so accurate and ingenious upon a firmer foundation, and would have come nearer to the truth of the matter. I, for my part, with the various and exact instruments which I have ready to hand — [made] of solid metal, of so great a size, [and] wrought to the line so that they are capable of [reading] single minutes — observed the appearances of that comet, and reduced to numbers the parallaxes demonstrated geometrically in many ways. But nowhere could I find that that comet admitted a parallax so great as to reach a third part of one degree, as I shall sufficiently demonstrate in its place. But on the last day of the comet's appearance, using the same aid of observation and of demonstrations, I detected by most certain reasonings that the parallax was perceptible. Hence, as soon as leisure shall allow, I shall — God willing — set forth for all lovers of this matter a fuller and sufficient account, resting upon sure observations and demonstrations. The instruments which I use in celestial observations are of such a kind that you could desire nothing in them that would conduce to perfection. I have quadrants fashioned of solid metal, indicating the azimuths together [with the altitudes] scrupulously, of so great a size that they distinguish single minutes, and some even a quarter part of any minute. I have metal armillae of Hipparchus and Ptolemy, capable of [reading] single minutes, which have been wrought to the line by certain new contrivances. I had an astronomical sextant prepared some while ago, for taking the distances of the stars more exactly and more easily than by the cross-staff (which nevertheless I use as well), with a remarkable convenience of handling. I had a globe constructed, exceedingly round, exceeding six feet in diameter, which — capable of [reading] almost single minutes — will contain the places of the stars, taken from the heavens by their true distances, and will resolve them into numbers by an easy method. I am now having made a great armillary torquetum of solid metal, and also the rules of Hipparchus, showing the azimuths together with the altitudes, of sufficient length; and many other instruments serving for astronomical exercises, great and exact, I have partly finished, partly am labouring to have finished day by day. The building too, which I have marked out with the name of Urania, in the middle of this island — exceedingly suited to these exercises of astronomy and to the spagyric labours, and built no less according to the comely symmetry of the architectural art — I have now at last completed at great expense. And I have spent, both on this and on the construction of the instruments, within the five-year period most lately elapsed, more than [* ib] thousand thaler. For I judged that, in earnest and according to the dignity of the astronomical matter to be handled here, suitable conveniences must be established, being by no means ignorant that it is not [in the power] of everyone to bear the expenses of duly equipping so great an undertaking, and that those who can [bear them] would rather lay them out on other frivolous things than expend their means on so great a science. How eagerly I could wish that you were here with me at the same time! You would assuredly see things that would delight you not a little. But because that can scarcely come about (for you have taken a wife, as I hear) and you moreover give your service to the commonwealth, bring it about that we may at least converse more often by letter. You will find me by no means negligent in keeping up our friendship even by these [letters]. I send you a packet of letters, and I earnestly wish to ask you to be willing to see to it that they be conveyed by a trusty man to Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius at Prague. To Paul Wittich, on his departing from us, I gave certain [letters] to be sent by him as well, which however he saw to less than faithfully; and I have found that he dealt with me less than sincerely in many other matters too (although he had been kindly received). For when he had secretly carried off my [materials] which he could lay hold of, and had inspected the methods and procedures of observing the heavenly bodies with our instruments (for handling which, however, he was by no means fit, for it was not possible for him to take even a single observation, of a comet or of any star, even with the cross-staff at least, far less to detect the parallaxes of a comet, as he falsely persuaded Dudith) — at last, under the pretext that his uncle had died, professing that an inheritance was owed him, he returned home from here, and — at which I marvelled most — pretended that he would return within a few weeks; whereas I had no need at all of his service, and would rather be without it than have him secretly withdraw from me things gained by me with much labour, and arrogate them to himself elsewhere — which, as he did in the matter of the comet, so too in the restoration of the solar course, and in the emendation of the eighth sphere which he obtained from us, I doubt not he will do. But he has made me more prudent, and has brought it about that I shall not hereafter entrust my [work] to anyone whatever. But, since mention has been made of the solar course restored by us, I send you a solar ephemeris for the year next to come, [drawn] from our theory of the motions, which in some years past — especially '78 — I reduced to numbers from the heavens by instruments and geometrical demonstrations. If you compare this with the heaven itself, by the help of some instrument, you will find it to agree exceedingly well with the motion of the Sun, as far as the senses can discern — from which, however, both kinds of astronomical calculation hitherto in use sometimes stray far, the eclipses too showing this very thing not obscurely. You therefore examine our motions by instruments, yet in such a way that the altitude of the pole be known to the very scruple [minute], and that account be taken of the solar parallax, and that the instrument be of so great a size that it conveniently distinguish single minutes. But, lest I seem to overstep the bounds of a letter, the paper itself forbids me to converse with you further. It will be for you to write back to me when occasion offers. If, besides, there have been anything published which you shall judge could be sought by me — such as Dudith's little book on comets and astrology, and if there are any others of this kind — I ask that you be willing to transmit them to me. I, when I shall have finished — at the winter leisure (as I hope) — the things I have proposed concerning comets, will gladly share them with you, and any others I may have that are agreeable to you, and will take care that you find me ever mindful of you.</p><p>Farewell, my Scultetus, and keep up the memory of me, as you do.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on our island of Hven, 12 October, the year 1581.<br>Tycho Brahe, the Dane.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> writing from his newly-completed Uraniborg. The addressee, <i>Bartholomaeus Scultetus, Gorlicensis</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomäus Schultz, 1540–1614), mathematician, cartographer and astronomer of <b>Görlitz</b> (Lusatia), later its mayor; he and Tycho had known one another from their <b>Leipzig</b> student years (1562–65), as the letter recalls. The source is one of four Tycho-to-Scultetus letters printed in the <i>Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica</i> (27th collection, 1743), noted by Dreyer (<i>Tycho Brahe</i>, p. 133, app. p. 177ff).</li><li><b>The comet of '77.</b> <i>Cometa anni 77</i> = the <b>great comet of 1577</b>, the central object of Tycho's cometary work (and of Scultetus's own tract, which Tycho here critiques). <i>Calendis Ianuarij</i> = \"on the Kalends of January,\" i.e. <b>1 January</b> [1578, O.S.] — the Roman-calendar idiom kept and glossed (protocol §3). Tycho's critique is technical: the comet and Scultetus's two reference stars could not have shared one <i>vertical circle</i> (azimuth circle) at that moment; the stellar interval is overstated by &gt; ¾°; and a parallax change of 1°7′ in 12 minutes of time is impossible. He reports his own finding that the comet's parallax never reached ⅓° — i.e. it lay far beyond the Moon (the supralunary-comet thesis).</li><li><b>Technical terms</b> (controlled glossary §5.1–5.2): <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>altitudo</i> → altitude; <i>circulus Verticalis</i> → vertical circle; <i>longitudo</i> → (celestial) longitude; <i>azimuth</i> kept; <i>organa</i> → instruments; <i>quadrantes</i> → quadrants; <i>armillae</i> → armillae; <i>sextans Astronomicus</i> → astronomical sextant; <i>Radius</i> → cross-staff (<i>radius astronomicus</i>); <i>globus</i> → globe; <i>Torquetum Armillare</i> → armillary torquetum; <i>regulae</i> → rules (parallactic rules). <i>ad amussim</i> (\"to the line/by rule\") rendered \"wrought to the line.\" <i>Spagyricis laboribus</i> = <b>spagyric (alchemical) labours</b> — the laboratory work at Uraniborg; the period term is kept.</li><li><b><i>Armillas Hyparchi et Ptolomaei</b></i> / <b><i>regulas Hyparchi</b></i> — \"the armillae of <b>Hipparchus</b> and <b>Ptolemy</b>\" and \"the rules of Hipparchus\": Tycho names his instrument types by their ancient prototypes (Ptolemaic armillary rings; the Hipparchan/Ptolemaic <i>regulae</i>). Names retained in the Greco-Latin form used.</li><li><b><i>Aedificium … quod Uraniae nomine insignivi</b></i> — \"the building which I have marked out with the name of Urania\": <b>Uraniborg</b> (\"Urania's castle\"), named for the muse of astronomy, declared \"now at last completed.\"</li><li><b>The expenditure crux.</b> <i>plus \\</i> ib millibus Ioachimicorum<i> — <b>flagged crux.</b> </i>Ioachimici<i> = <b>Joachimsthaler / thalers</b> (silver coins of Joachimsthal), rendered \"thaler.\" The numeral preceding </i>millibus<i> is corrupt in the printed source: it appears as an editorial mark (</i>\\<b>) followed by <i>ib</i>, not a legible figure. The sense is secure (\"more than [N] thousand thaler [spent] within the last five years on Uraniborg and the instruments\"), but the </b>quantity itself is not recoverable from this text<i>* and is left bracketed [</i> ib] rather than silently emended to any specific number. (Tycho elsewhere gives figures on the order of a \"tonne of gold\"; that comparandum is <i>not</i> imported here.)</li><li><b><i>Thadeus Hagecius</b></i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek (Thaddaeus Hagecius)</b> of <b>Prague</b>, imperial physician and astronomer, Tycho's principal Bohemian correspondent; Tycho asks Scultetus to forward an enclosed packet to him.</li><li><b><i>Paulus Witichius</b></i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) of Breslau (Wratislaviensis), the mathematician who had stayed with Tycho on Hven in 1580. Tycho's bitter charge — that Wittich secretly carried off Tycho's methods (instrument designs, the <i>prosthaphaeretic</i>/observational procedures, the work on the <b>solar course</b> [<i>Solaris cursus restitutio</i>] and the <b>emendation of the eighth sphere</b> [the fixed-star catalogue / precession]) and would \"arrogate them to himself elsewhere\" — is preserved at full force, including the parenthetical claim that Wittich was incompetent at the instruments (\"could not take even a single observation … even with the cross-staff\"). <i>prout Duditio frustra persuasit</i> = \"as he vainly persuaded <b>Dudith</b>.\" The hedges and Tycho's own qualifiers (<i>non dubito</i> \"I doubt not\"; <i>cum tamen benevole fuerit exceptus</i> \"although he had been kindly received\") are kept unsharpened.</li><li><b><i>Duditius</b></i> = <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (András Dudith, 1533–1589), the humanist who had published on the comet of 1577 (his <i>De cometarum significatione</i>); Tycho both reports Wittich's failed attempt to persuade Dudith of cometary parallax and asks Scultetus to procure \"<b>Dudith's little book on comets and astrology</b>\" for him.</li><li><b><i>Solaris cursus restitutio</b></i> → \"the restoration of the solar course\"; <i>octavae Sphaerae emendatio</i> → \"the emendation of the eighth sphere\" (the sphere of the fixed stars) — both rendered per the <i>restitutio/instauratio</i> glossary entry (§5.1). <i>Ephemeris Solaris</i> → \"solar ephemeris,\" derived from Tycho's solar theory worked out \"especially [in] '78.\"</li><li><b><i>in ipso scrupulo</b></i> — <i>scrupulus</i> here = the <b>minute of arc</b> (\"known to the very scruple\"); the gloss \"[minute]\" is bracketed.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uranienburgi in Insula nostra Huaena 12. Octobr. An. 1581</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, on our island of <b>Hven</b>, 12 October 1581\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline (12 October) agrees with the dataset label (12 October 1581); no discrepancy. <i>Datae</i> (\"Given\") kept.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> No Greek and no astronomical/zodiac glyphs occur in this letter. The only non-alphabetic mark is the corrupt numeral sign (<i>\\</i>*) in the expenditure clause, treated above.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Source-printed forms retained in the Source block (<i>ijs, medijs, Genesi-style -ij-, intercapedo, ecclipsibus, Uranienburgi, Huaena, PTOLOMAeI, BARTHOLOMAeO</i>); the <i>ae</i>-ligature spellings (<i>-MAeO, -MAeI</i>) reproduced as printed per protocol §4(2).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English clause corrected from 'as he vainly persuaded Dudith' to 'as he falsely persuaded Dudith' — Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp.62-63, confirms the Latin 'frustra persuasit' as printed (perfect tense = successful persuasion, but groundless); the prior English wording reversed the sense to imply a failed attempt. The Latin text itself needed no correction.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout in sense and register; the single genuine crux is the <b>corrupt expenditure figure</b> <i>plus \\</i> ib millibus Ioachimicorum<i>, where the numeral is not legibly transmitted in the source and is left bracketed [</i> ib] rather than emended. The grade reflects that one flagged, non-smoothed lacuna.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> The corrupt expenditure figure is carried identically by the standard correspondence edition (<i>Tycho Brahes Breve</i>) — not a one-witness slip, and not recoverable from the transmitted text. The dedicated critical edition (K. H. Hamel, <i>Acta Historica Astronomiae</i> 5, 1999) was not consulted and may carry an emendation. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full sentence-by-sentence collation of source_text and published_english against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.61-63 (complete letter text; p.64 confirmed to begin a different letter). Every name (Scultetus, Wittich, Hagecius, Dudith), number (12 minutes; 1 degree 7 minutes; 4.5 degrees; a third of a degree; a six-foot globe; a five-year span; the uncertain '* ib' thousand-thaler sum) and the closing date (12 October 1581) match Dreyer exactly; source_text is verbatim Dreyer apart from long-s normalization. One ambiguous rendering of 'frustra persuasit' flagged above; otherwise no fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss found. Self-assigned grade was C; independent collation finds the letter substantially sound, downgraded only for the one nuance-flip.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Latin 'frustra persuasit' most naturally means Wittich DID succeed in persuading Dudith, but groundlessly/falsely (frustra = without real basis); the English 'vainly persuaded' reads to a modern eye as 'tried but failed to persuade', which reverses whether Dudith was actually taken in by Wittich's false claim to have detected the comet's parallax. At: “as he vainly persuaded Dudith”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single MISTRANSLATION against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.62-63, where the phrase 'prout DUDITIO fruftra perfuafit' spans the page break). The Latin as stored already matches Dreyer exactly, so only the English clause was corrected, per the grammar of 'persuasit' (successful persuasion) plus 'frustra' (groundlessly/falsely) rather than 'vainly' (implying failure). Nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Scultetus for books received and critiques his treatise on the 1577 comet, disputing the parallax measurements as partly impossible and partly insufficiently observed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15811012001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15811012002", "d": "1581-10-12", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 12 October 1581 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 12–16 (copy); B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 32–34 (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM<br>HAGECIUM.<br>S.<br>ANNUS integer iam fere est elapsus, charissime Domine Doctor THADDAeE, vir<br>clarissime, ex quo tibi ultimo scripsi, idque per PAULUM WITICHIUM Wratislaviensem hinc in patriam revertentem. At<br>quia interea temporis nihil literarum a te receperim, mutua illa benevolentia,<br>qua nos invicem in Comitijs Ratisbonensibus olim complexi sumus, et innata illa tibi atque<br>constanti erga amicos affectione, tam diuturnam in rescribendo dilationem minime<br>admittente, non potui non suspicari, literas illas per<br>eum cui traditae erant, ad manus tuas non pervenisse. Utque idipsum nunc potius<br>crederem, effecit libellus tuus de ultimo Cometa, quem BARTHOLOMAeUS SCULTETUS veteri<br>amicitia Lipsiae mihi coniunctus ante biduum<br>per quendam hominem vasa Spagyrica illinc nobis ex conductoA afferentem mihi transmitti curavit. Cum enim in hoc scripto videaris meam<br>ex observationibus certis de utriusque Cometae parallaxibus derivatam sententiam<br>desiderare, quam tamen tunc temporis dilucide, nec minus vere, uti spero, brevibus tibiA aperui, fieri non potuisse arbitror, ut literae illae tibi, veluti<br>sperabam, redditae sint. Nec enim eorum, quae in his significaveram (etiamsi nulla<br>interea rescribendiA occasio oblata fuisset) tam subito oblitum fuisse, vel ea penitus<br>neglexisse te, virum harum rerum et cupidum et intelligentem, mihi persuadere<br>possum. Quae autem in ijs literis tibi tunc temporis de ultimis duobus Cometis,<br>ex observationibus nostris reseravi et nunc denuo iudicanda indicoA, ea fere sunt huiusmodi: In Cometa, qui Anno 77 fulgere coepit, ex<br>omnibus nostris observationibus (quae per diversa e solido metallo exquisite<br>elaborata, singulorumque minutorum adamussim capacia instrumenta magnâ<br>diligentia inquisitae sunt, diversaque demonstrationis methodo in numeros<br>redactae) nunquam deprehendi parallaxin eamA, quae fit in Horizonte, maximam tertiam unius gradus partem attigisse, sed<br>saepenumero hac multo minorem extitisse, ut suo loco intelligentibus, varijs<br>rationibus sufficienter demonstrabimus. Cumque in libello tum temporis de eodem<br>Cometa edito, parallaxin 5 partium admisisses, et ob id sublunarem illum<br>extitisse Cometam opinatus esses, amice te in ijsdem literis admonui, rationem<br>REGIOMONTANI in investigandis parallaxibus, quate usum esse asseris, utquamvis satis<br>demonstrabilem, tamen tam subtili negocio minus sufficientem et accommodam esse.<br>Multo enim speculative vere demonstrantur, quae fi in praxin et sensuum Mechanicam tractationem deducantur, plurimum a scopo<br>petito aberrabunt, praesertim ubi ex minimis, uti hoc loco fit, ea quae maxima<br>sunt investigantur. Imo etiamsi REGIOMONTANI illaA speculatio hîc locum aliquem habuisset, et intervallum temporis exquisite<br>satis sumptum altitudinibus et azimuthis rite datis congrueret (in quibus tamen<br>singulis, etiamsi adsint instrumenta magna et exquisita, quae raro tamen in<br>promtu sunt, ea quae par est praecisione adamussim capiendis, quanta opus sit<br>diligentia, quantaque non bene exercitatis eveniat difficultas, nemo nisi diu in<br>hac Mechanica Astronomiae palaestra sedulo versatus, sibi persuadere poterit)<br>nihilominus tamen ex tam angusto temporis intervallo, quod 18 saltem scrupulorum<br>assumebas, parallaxes quae tantillo tempore insensibiliter variantur,<br>sensibiliter discernere impossibile erat. Ideoque dum motus proprij impedimenta<br>temporis brevitate declinare visum fuit, tota res ad impossibilitatem quandam<br>redacta est. Nos sane ultra vicesies eam REGIOMONTANI in discernendis<br>parallaxibus rationem, ex diversis et satis certis observationibus, non mediocri<br>(uti scis) labore, in numeros resolvimus. At nusquam parallaxin invenimus<br>tantam, quantam Luna etiam a terris remotissima admittit, saepenumero etiam<br>nullam. Ideoque tuis placitis, etiam cum eadem methodo (utquamvis subtilissimoA negocio minus sufficienti), qua tu, usus essem, subscribere non potui.<br>Nec etiam idipsum, quod ad confirmationem huius subiungis, ex coeli videlicet<br>mediatione cum Vulturis lucida, parallaxin comprobare<br>possibile erat. Nam etiamsi eorum simul per meridianum transitus, qui interdiu<br>tunc fiebat, aspectabilis fuisset, parallaxin, nisi plura adessent data, hinc<br>nemo ulla ratione colliget, ut per incuriam in tua Dialexi inter occupationes<br>alias minus considerate docuisti. Quod vero parallaxis istius Cometae non solumA tanta non fuerit, sed ne quidem multum perceptibilis, testantur propriae<br>tuae observationes, quas in distantijs Cometae ab ore Pegasi pluribus interiectis<br>horis, minimum tamen variari deprehendisti, ut revera ob id parallaxinA, si qua fuit, exiguam esse, orbemque Lunae plurimum exuperasse Cometam,<br>vel tuis observatis, quibus tamen contraria admittebas, convinci possit. Haec,<br>inquam, tum temporis de priori Cometa ad te perscripsi. Video autem in hoc<br>ultimo scripto te nobiscum sentire, et consideratis ijs, quas tuncA temporis non animadvertebas, contrarietatibus sententiam revocasse,<br>reique vestigandaeA nucleum penitius invenisse, qua in re tibi et veritatis amatoribus (qui<br>tua autoritate abuti, et observationibus ac demonstratis minus exquisitis adduci<br>poterant, ut falsae Peripateticorum de Cometis opinioni subscriberent) plurimum<br>gratulor. Quod ad SCULTETI demonstrationes, quae eandem fere tecum parallaxin<br>inferebant, attinet, cum incerto fulciantur fundamento, falsisque datis<br>superstruantur (nam, ut alia taceam, impossibile erat Cometam eo die cum ijs fixis, quas assumit, supra Horizontem in eodem extitisse<br>circulo verticali, et stellarum etiam, quam praesupponit,<br>distantia plus ¾ unius gradus erronea sit, ut de caeteris hinc quis facile, quid<br>sentiendum sit, colligat) minime eas pro veritate indaganda recipiendas, suo<br>loco dilucide ostendemus. Quantum vero ad hunc ultimum Cometam, qui anno 80<br>fulsit, attinet, videris mihi scopum ipsum longe exactius attigisse, et<br>certioribus observationibus rei veritatem probabilius demonstrasse. In quibus id<br>saltem ad exquisitam praecisionem penitius investigandam desiderarem, ut motus<br>proprij Cometae inter binas observationes mutationem non neglexisses. Cum enim<br>Cometa intervallo duarum horarum eo die in suo circulo 27' (motus siquidemA diurnus a nobis inventus est 5p 23m) locum mutaverit, hanc differentiam, si res praecise tractanda foret,<br>non praetereundam iudico, quoniamA ex minimis hoc loco de longe maioribus ratiocinamur. Instrumentum etiam<br>ob id, quo altitudines capis, cum dena aut quina minuta saltem admittat, in tam<br>subtili indagine minime sufficiens esse poterit. Sunt tamen tuae observationes et<br>his innixaeA demonstrationes eiusmodi, ut si hinc non exquisite, quot minutorum fuerit<br>parallaxis, et in quo praecise orbium coelestium versaretur Cometa ille, ad<br>amussim constare possit, tantam tamen fuisse, ut Lunae inferiora non permearet,<br>cuivis harum rerum perito non aegre persuadere poterint. Quantum ad nostras in<br>hoc Cometa observationes attinet, meam sententiam tum temporis non minus quam<br>iam per literas tibi indicavi, videlicet me toto apparitionis tempore, ex<br>omnibus et diversimodis exquisitissimis instrumentis, diligenter habitis et<br>examinatis observationibus, huius Cometae notabilem aliquam fuisse parallaxin, nusquam percipereA potuisse. Nam ut caetera (commodiore loco commemoranda) taceam, certo<br>deprehendi Cometam illum, tam in Meridiano, cum altissimus esset, quam iuxta<br>Horizontem in ipso puncto occidentalis Azimuthi, eandem exquisite ab Aequatore<br>declinationem obtinuisse, nisi quantum motus proprius interea, diurno motui<br>proportionalis, eam variaverit, quod fieri minime potuit in tanta altitudinum<br>differentia, si sensibilis aliqua parallaxeωs adfuisset quantitas. Id ipsum<br>etiam tum omnibus rationibus a REGIOMONTANO in suo libello propositis, quae usum<br>hîc haberent, tum alijs quibusdam exactioribus et minus errori obnoxijs a nobis<br>circa parallaxium dimensiones excogitatis, plane se eodem modo habere certo<br>deprehendimus, ut per ocium copiose firmis rationibus, Mathemata intelligentibus<br>demonstrabimus. Contuli etiam acceptoA tuo libello nonnulla cum nostris observationibusA et in ijs miram deprehendi convenientiam, quae ad parallaxium certitudinem<br>confirmandam facere poterat. Nam die 13. Octobris cum Cometa esset iuxta<br>Meridianum, eius distantiam a lucidiore capitis Pegasi tam Radio quam Sextante<br>Astronomico (instrumento peculiari a nobis tali<br>machinatione invento, ut per hoc longe facilius, commodius et exactius, quam per<br>Radium stellarum capiantur intercapedines) deprehendi partis 1 min. 16 uno<br>saltem scrupulo pene insensibili tua distantia maiorem, quae tamen differentia<br>pro hac terreni orbis distantia ad quintum usque scrupulum, si in ipsa<br>convexitate orbis Lunae fuisset Cometa, excrevisset, longeque plus, si vel in<br>regione ignea (modo aliqua foret) vel supremitate aëris, ut diu falso opinati sunt omnes Aristotelici, exarsisset. Sic die etiam 21.<br>Octobris cum Cometa esset proximus lucidae Aquilae, ei stellae<br>a vertice imminens, eandem plane tecum deprehendi distantiam. Nam licet haec hora<br>8 uno scrupulo nobis minor visa fuerit, tamen usque ad decimam hanc<br>observationem continuandoA exquisite p. 6 m. 22 apparebat, quod cum tua annotatione adprime<br>consentit. Et quemadmodum fieri minime potuit, ut intervallo duarum horarum<br>saltem uno scrupulo variaret a fixa Cometae illa distantia, si sensibilis admodum<br>adfuisset parallaxis, sic etiam intervallo 6 graduum orbis terrestris, quod est<br>inter tui et nostri loci observationem, eandem eodem tempore praecise obtinere posseA a fixisA distantiam corpus aliquod Luna inferius, MathemataA intelligenti non persuadebitur. Obtulit autem se eo die optata comae<br>moditas ad Vulturis stellam parallaxes examinandi, eo quod Cometa tum temporis<br>in loco sui circuli versaretur, qui minimum a stella distaret, eamque ad angulos rectosA aspiceret, unde etiam per aliquot horas ex motu proprio Cometae nulla pene<br>sensibilis ingerebatur intercapedinum differentia, ideoque distantia saltem<br>ratione parallaxeos, si qua fuisset, tam in diversis dissitisque Horizontibus,<br>quam in eodem, ubi plurimum mutatae forent stellae altitudines, necessario<br>sensibiliter variari debebat, cuius tamen contrarium ab utroque nostrum pulchra<br>et exquisita convenientia deprehensum est, ut vel hoc solo argumento convinci<br>possit, Cometam illum longe supra Lunae orbes in ipso aethere sedem sibi<br>delegisse. Atque haec de hoc ultimo Cometa tecum nunc brevibus contulisse<br>sufficiat. Id saltem commonefactionis loco subiungam, declinationem lucidioris<br>stellae in capite Pegasi a te 8 proximeA scrupulis iusto maiorem assignatam esse. Nos enim aliquoties hîc, ubi<br>Polus elevatur 55 p. 54 2/3 m., eius altitudinem maximam<br>deprehendimus 38 p. 20 m. exquisite. Ideoque ab Aequatore declinat p. 4 m. 14½ , sive quadrantis tui parvitas ususque non satis exactus, sive Pragensis poli elevatio, quam tu a veteribus minus<br>forte scrupulose traditam pro vera recipis, sive etiam in utroque horum aliquis<br>lateat error, quem tibi ipsi examinandum corrigendumque relinquo. De certitudine<br>enim nostrae observationis ne in uno quidem scrupulo haesitamus. Pari ratione nos<br>eodem die 13. Cometae ipsius, ex altitudine meridiana, quae a nobis deprehensa est<br>p. 37 m. 7½, declinationem 3 g. 2 m. ideoque 5 minutisA tua minorem observavimus. Differentiam<br>meridianorum, cum vix maior sit quadranteA horae, insensibiliter uno saltem scrupulo declinationem ob motum Cometae<br>proprium variantem, volens praetereo. Non tamen poterit haec declinationum inter<br>nostras observationes differentia superiori convenientiae, quam parallaxeos<br>exiguitatem comprobavimus, fidem derogare. Illic enim per distantias, non per<br>altitudines fiebat observatio. Et licet per quadrantem etiam Cometae et stellae<br>distantiam, ex differentia utriusque altitudinis maximae dimensus fueris, tamen<br>utut in Poli altitudine aliquis error lateat, vel in quadrante ipso etiam<br>observationi iustae aliquid desit, cum in utroque loco tam in Cometa quam in<br>stella utraque eodem modo se habeant, in ipsa Cometae et stellae distantia<br>sensibilem errorem ingerere non poterint. In declinationibus vero utriusque<br>eliciendis, hac ratione deviareA (ut prius ostendimus) nihilominus erat possibile. De toto autem hoc negotioA brevi, Deo conatus nostros auspicante, per ocium<br>hybernum observationes plurimas easque certissimasA,varijs demonstrationibus elaboratas, in medium producam, quibus tum novae<br>illius stellae tum utriusque Cometae subsequentisA motus, apparentias, parallaxes et a Terra distantias, intelligentibus<br>certissimis et infallabilibus rationibus proponam, conferendo etiam praecipuas<br>aliorum observationes, et quatenus veritati consonae sint vel ab hac devient, in<br>trutina revocando, adeo ut universaliter tandem concludere non dubitavere,<br>Cometas omnes in ipso aethere, non Elementari regione, ut hactenus<br>Peripateticorum autoritate frustra creditum est, generari; nihil obstante, quod<br>REGIOMONTANUS et VOGELINUSA in duobus Cometis diversum quid prodiderint. Utrumque enim<br>a scopo deviasseA, rationes probabiles et sufficientes in medium producam, nisi quis sola<br>autoritate pugnare velit, negans humanum esse errare, interdumque bonus quod non<br>dormitet HOMERUS. Quod veroA hactenus propositum illum laboremA non absolverim, et desiderio multorum nostras observationes hisque<br>innixus apodicticas conclusiones avide desiderantium non satisfecerim, in causa<br>fuerunt plurima negotia, quibus tum Regis tum consanguineorum et amicorum<br>nobilium causa interea temporis implicitus eram. Inprimis vero Architectonicae<br>curae et in machinis Astronomicis exquisite elaborandis assiduae occupationes me<br>pensum inchoatum continuare et absolvere non permittebant. Spero tamen huic<br>procrastinationi, Deo volente, brevi cum faenore satissactum iri. Sed videor<br>Epistolae metas, diutius tecum de hac materia, qua te delectariA scio, conferendo, transcendisse, quam tamen prolixitatem tibi non<br>ingratam esse confido. Tuum erit id quicquid estA, boni consulere, et si quid praeterea, vel ex proprijs, vel aliorum praecipuorum placitis tibi de hac materia constiterit, vel<br>scriptis editum fuerit, benevole mecum communicare, nostrique memoriam, uti facis, conservare, et amicitiam literis non tam diu,<br>ut hactenus, intermissis, renovare. Efficiam vicissim, ut nihil in me, quod ad<br>candidi et constantis amici munus pertineat, uspiam a te desiderari possit.Vale.Datae UranienburgA in Insula Danici<br>HellespontiHuaena die 12. Octobris Anno 1581.T.TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>A whole year is now nearly elapsed, dearest Master Doctor Thaddaeus, most distinguished man, since I last wrote to you — and that by way of <b>Paulus Wittich</b> of Wrocław, as he was returning home from here. But because in the meantime I have received no letter from you — when that mutual goodwill with which we once embraced one another at the <b>Diet of Regensburg</b>, and that affection toward your friends which is inborn in you and constant, by no means admits so long a delay in writing back — I could not but suspect that those letters had not reached your hands through the man to whom they had been entrusted. And what makes me now incline rather to believe just that is your little book on the latest comet, which <b>Bartholomaeus Scultetus</b> — bound to me by an old friendship from Leipzig — saw to it, two days ago, that it should be conveyed to me by way of a certain man who was bringing us from there [Leipzig] some spagyric [chemical] vessels we had hired [or: ordered]. For since in this writing you seem to want my opinion concerning the parallaxes of both comets, derived from sure observations — an opinion which nevertheless at that earlier time I disclosed to you plainly, and (as I hope) no less truly, in brief — I judge that it cannot have happened that those letters were delivered to you, as I had hoped. For I cannot persuade myself that you — a man both eager for these matters and understanding of them — so suddenly forgot the things I had signified in that letter (even if no occasion for writing back had meanwhile presented itself), or that you wholly neglected them.</p><p>Now the things which at that time I unlocked to you in that letter about the last two comets, from our observations, and which I now again set forth for your judgement, are roughly of this kind: In the comet which began to shine in the year [15]77, from all our observations — which were sought out with great diligence by means of various instruments exquisitely wrought from solid metal and exactly capable of [reading] single minutes [of arc], and were reduced to numbers by a varied method of demonstration — I never found that parallax, the one that occurs at the horizon, to have reached at most a third part of one degree, but very often it proved much less than this, as I shall sufficiently demonstrate to the understanding, in its proper place, by various arguments. And whereas in the little book then published about the same comet you had granted a parallax of 5 degrees [partium], and on that account had judged that comet to be sublunar, I warned you as a friend in those same letters that the method of <b>Regiomontanus</b> for investigating parallaxes — which you assert you used — although demonstrable enough, is nonetheless less adequate and suited to so subtle a business. For many things which are truly demonstrated speculatively, if they be carried over into practice and the mechanical handling of the senses, will stray very far from the mark aimed at — especially where, as happens in this case, the things that are greatest are investigated from the least. Nay, even if that speculation of Regiomontanus had had some place here, and an interval of time taken exactly enough had agreed with altitudes and azimuths duly given (in each of which, however — even though great and exquisite instruments be at hand, which yet are seldom ready — to be captured exactly with the precision that is fitting, how much diligence is needed, and how much difficulty befalls those not well practised, no one can persuade himself save one who has long and assiduously laboured in this mechanical wrestling-school of astronomy) — nonetheless, from so narrow an interval of time, which you assumed to be only 18 minutes [scrupulorum], it was impossible sensibly to discern parallaxes that in so little time vary imperceptibly. And so, while it seemed [good] to evade the hindrances of the proper motion by the brevity of time, the whole matter was reduced to a kind of impossibility. We, to be sure, have more than twenty times resolved into numbers that method of Regiomontanus for discerning parallaxes, from various and sufficiently sure observations, with no slight labour (as you know). But nowhere did we find a parallax as great as the Moon admits even when most remote from the earth — and very often none at all. And therefore I could not subscribe to your verdicts, even when I had used the same method (although less adequate to so very subtle a business) as you did.</p><p>Nor was it even possible to confirm the parallax by that very thing which you add for its corroboration — namely from the [comet's] culmination [coeli mediatione] together with the bright star of the Vulture [Altair]. For even if their simultaneous transit across the meridian, which then took place in the daytime, had been observable, no one will by any reasoning gather a parallax from this, unless more data were present — as through carelessness you taught, less considerately, amid your other occupations, in your <i>Dialexis</i>. But that the parallax of that comet not only was not so great, but was not even much perceptible, is attested by your own observations, in which you found that, over the distances of the comet from the mouth of Pegasus, with several hours intervening, [the distance] nevertheless varied very little — so that in truth on that account it can be proved, even by your own observations (to which, however, you were admitting contrary [conclusions]), that the parallax, if there was any, was tiny, and that the comet far exceeded [was far beyond] the orb of the Moon. These things, I say, I wrote out to you at that time about the earlier comet.</p><p>But I see that in this latest writing you agree with us, and that — having considered the contradictions which at that time you were not noticing — you have recalled your opinion and have found more inwardly the kernel of the matter to be investigated; on which I heartily congratulate you and the lovers of truth (who could be drawn by an abuse of your authority, and by less exquisite observations and demonstrations, to subscribe to the false opinion of the Peripatetics about comets). As for <b>Scultetus's</b> demonstrations, which inferred nearly the same parallax as you, since they rest on an uncertain foundation and are built upon false data (for, to say nothing of other matters, it was impossible that the comet should have stood that day, above the horizon, in the same vertical circle with those fixed stars which he assumes; and the distance of the stars too, which he presupposes, is in error by more than ¾ of one degree — so that from this one may easily gather what is to be thought of the rest) — we shall plainly show, in its proper place, that they are by no means to be accepted for the investigation of the truth.</p><p>But as far as concerns this latest comet, which shone in the year [15]80, you seem to me to have struck the mark itself far more exactly, and to have demonstrated the truth of the matter more plausibly with surer observations. In which I would wish at least this much, for a more inward investigation toward exquisite precision: that you had not neglected the change of the comet's proper motion between two observations. For since the comet, in the interval of two hours that day, shifted its place by 27′ in its own circle (the daily motion having indeed been found by us [to be] 5°23′), I judge this difference not to be passed over, if the matter is to be treated precisely, since in this case we reason about far greater things from the least. The instrument too, on this account, with which you take the altitudes — since it admits of at least ten or five minutes [of error] — can by no means be adequate in so subtle an inquiry. Yet your observations, and the demonstrations resting on them, are of such a kind that, even if from these it cannot be made out exactly to a hair how many minutes the parallax was, and in which of the celestial orbs precisely that comet moved, nevertheless that it was so great that it did not pass beneath the regions below the Moon [Lunae inferiora] — this they will be able to persuade, without difficulty, anyone skilled in these matters.</p><p>As far as concerns our observations of this comet, I indicated my opinion at that time no less than I now [indicate it] to you by letter — namely, that throughout the whole time of [its] apparition, from observations diligently made and examined with all our most exquisite instruments of various kinds, I was nowhere able to perceive any notable parallax of this comet. For, to be silent about the rest (to be recalled in a more convenient place), I certainly found that comet to have held exactly the same declination from the Equator both on the Meridian, when it was highest, and near the Horizon, at the very point of the western Azimuth — except insofar as the proper motion, proportional to the daily motion, varied it in the meantime, which could by no means have happened, given so great a difference of altitudes, if any sensible quantity of parallax had been present. This very thing too we certainly found to behave plainly in the same way, both by all the reasonings set forth by Regiomontanus in his little book that would have application here, and by certain others, more exact and less liable to error, devised by us concerning the measurement of parallaxes — as I shall demonstrate at leisure, copiously and with firm reasonings, to those who understand the mathematical sciences.</p><p>I have also, on receiving your little book, compared several things with our observations, and in them I found a marvellous agreement, which could serve to confirm the certainty of the parallaxes. For on 13 October, when the comet was near the Meridian, I found its distance from the brighter [star] of the head of Pegasus — both by the cross-staff [radius] and by the <b>Astronomical Sextant</b> (a particular instrument invented by us with such a contrivance that by it the intervals of stars are taken far more easily, conveniently, and exactly than by the cross-staff) — to be 1° 16′, greater than your distance by only one minute [scrupulo], almost imperceptible; which difference, however, for this distance of the terrestrial orb, would have grown to as much as the fifth minute [scrupulum] if the comet had been at the very convexity of the orb of the Moon — and far more, if it had blazed either in the fiery region (supposing there were any) or in the uppermost part of the air, as all the Aristotelians long falsely supposed. So too on 21 October, when the comet was nearest the bright star of the Eagle [Altair], standing over that star from the zenith [a vertice imminens], I found exactly the same distance as you. For although at the 8th hour this seemed to us one minute less, yet, continuing this observation up to the tenth [hour], it appeared exactly 6° 22′ — which agrees admirably with your annotation. And just as it could by no means have happened that, in the interval of at least two hours, that distance of the comet from the fixed star should vary by one minute, if a quite sensible parallax had been present, so too no one understanding the mathematical sciences will be persuaded that some body lower than the Moon could, in the interval of 6 degrees of the terrestrial orb — which lies between your observation-place and ours — at the same time precisely hold the same distance from the fixed stars.</p><p>Moreover, on that day the wished-for convenience of the [comet's] coma presented itself for examining the parallaxes against the star of the Vulture [Altair], because the comet was then situated at the place of its own circle which was least distant from the star, and looked toward it at right angles; whence too, for some hours, scarcely any sensible difference of intervals was introduced by the proper motion of the comet — and therefore the distance ought necessarily to have varied sensibly by reason of parallax at least (if there had been any), both in different and far-apart Horizons, and in the same [Horizon], where the altitudes of the stars would have changed very much. Yet the contrary of this was found by both of us, with a fine and exquisite agreement — so that even by this single argument it can be proved that that comet chose its seat for itself far above the orbs of the Moon, in the aether itself. And let it suffice to have now conferred with you briefly thus much about this latest comet.</p><p>This much at least I will add by way of admonition: that the declination of the brighter star in the head of Pegasus has been assigned by you nearly 8 minutes [scrupulis] greater than is right. For we, several times here — where the Pole is elevated 55° 54⅔′ — have found its maximum altitude to be exactly 38° 20′. And therefore it declines from the Equator 4° 14½′ — whether [the cause be] the smallness of your quadrant and a use not exact enough, or the elevation of the Prague pole, which you, perhaps less scrupulously handed down by the ancients, receive as true, or whether also some error lurk in both of these — which I leave to you yourself to examine and correct. For concerning the certainty of our observation we do not hesitate even by a single minute. By the like reasoning, we on the same 13th observed the declination of the comet itself, from the meridian altitude — which was found by us [to be] 37° 7½′ — [to be] 3° 2′, and therefore 5 minutes less than yours. The difference of the meridians, since it is scarcely greater than a quarter of an hour, [and] the declination varying imperceptibly by at least one minute on account of the comet's proper motion, I willingly pass over. Yet this difference of declinations between our observations cannot detract from the credibility of the agreement above, by which we proved the smallness of the parallax. For there the observation was made by distances, not by altitudes. And although by the quadrant too you measured the distance of the comet and the star from the difference of each one's maximum altitude, nevertheless — whatever error may lurk in the altitude of the Pole, or whatever may be lacking to a correct observation in the quadrant itself — since in both places, both in the comet and in each star, they behave in the same way, they cannot introduce a sensible error into the distance of the comet and the star itself. But in eliciting the declinations of each, to deviate in this way (as we showed before) was nonetheless possible.</p><p>Now concerning this whole business I shall shortly — God favouring our endeavours — bring forth into the open, during the winter leisure, very many observations, and those most certain, elaborated with various demonstrations; in which I shall set forth, to the understanding, by most certain and infallible reasonings, the motions, appearances, parallaxes, and distances from the Earth both of that new star and of each of the two subsequent comets — comparing also the principal observations of others, and, insofar as they are consonant with the truth or deviate from it, calling them back into the balance [weighing them]; so that I shall not have hesitated at last to conclude universally that all comets are generated in the aether itself, not in the Elementary region, as has hitherto been believed in vain on the authority of the Peripatetics — notwithstanding that <b>Regiomontanus</b> and <b>Vögelin</b> put forth something different concerning two comets. For I shall bring forth into the open probable and sufficient reasons that each of them deviated from the mark — unless someone wish to fight by authority alone, denying that to err is human, and [denying] that sometimes even good <b>Homer</b> nods.</p><p>But that I have not hitherto finished that work I proposed, and have not satisfied the desire of the many who eagerly long for our observations and the apodictic conclusions resting on them — the cause of this has been very many affairs in which I was meanwhile entangled, on account both of the King and of my noble kinsmen and friends. But above all, architectural cares and continual occupations in exquisitely elaborating astronomical machines [instruments] did not allow me to continue and finish the task begun. Yet I hope that this procrastination, God willing, will shortly be made good with interest. But I seem to have overstepped the bounds of a letter, by conferring with you so long about this material, in which I know you take delight — a prolixity which nonetheless I trust is not unwelcome to you. It will be for you to take in good part this, whatever it is; and if anything besides has become established for you about this material — whether from your own [findings] or from the verdicts of other eminent men — or has been published in writing, to share it with me kindly, and to preserve, as you do, the memory of us, and to renew our friendship by letters not interrupted so long as hitherto. I, for my part, will bring it about that nothing pertaining to the office of a candid and constant friend can anywhere be found wanting in me by you.</p><p>Farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, on the Island of the Danish Hellespont, Hven, on 12 October, the year 1581.</p><p>T[uus]. Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Prague physician, astronomer, and imperial counsellor to Rudolf II; the leading Bohemian observer of the 1572 nova and the comets of 1577 and 1580, and Tycho's principal Prague correspondent. His <i>Dialexis de nova et prius incognita stella</i> (1574) and his comet tract are both referenced below.</li><li><b>Sender's self-naming.</b> The letter opens with the heading \"TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM\" run together with the first word \"ANNUS\" in the transcription; reproduced as printed. The subscription \"T.TYCHO BRAHE\" is read <b>T[uus] Tycho Brahe</b> (\"Your Tycho Brahe\").</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Paulus Witichius Wratislaviensis</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> of Wrocław (Vratislavia), the mathematician who had visited Hven in 1580 and carried Tycho's earlier (now-apparently-lost) letter homeward. (Tycho's later coolness toward Wittich postdates this letter.)</li><li><i>Bartholomaeus Scultetus</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Barthel Schultz, 1540–1614) of Görlitz, mathematician and cartographer, Tycho's friend \"from Leipzig\"; he forwarded Hagecius's tract. His own cometary demonstrations are criticised below.</li><li><i>Regiomontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Müller of Königsberg</b> (1436–1476), whose method of determining cometary parallax (in <i>De cometae magnitudine longitudineque</i>) Hagecius had used.</li><li><i>Vogelinus</i> = <b>Johannes Vögelin</b> (Vogelin, d. 1549), Vienna mathematician, author of a tract on the comet of 1532 (<i>Significatio cometae … 1532</i>), here paired with Regiomontanus as a predecessor who reached a non-aethereal result.</li><li><i>Homerus</i> = <b>Homer</b>; the closing tag <i>bonus … dormitet Homerus</i> echoes Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 359 (<i>indignor quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus</i>), \"I am vexed whenever good Homer nods\" — i.e. even the greatest authorities sometimes err.</li><li><b>Works referred to.</b></li><li><i>libellus tuus de ultimo Cometa</i> — Hagecius's tract on the comet of 1580, identified in the corpus (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; Norlind) as his <i>Apodixis physica et mathematica de cometis</i> (1581), forwarded by Scultetus.</li><li><i>libello … de eodem Cometa edito</i> (with the 5° parallax) — Hagecius's earlier tract on the comet of 1577, his <i>Descriptio cometae … 1577</i> (Prague, 1578).</li><li><i>tua Dialexi</i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de nova et prius incognita stella</i> (Frankfurt, 1574), here cited for a careless inference about parallax from culmination.</li><li><b>The astronomical substance (the central crux of the Tychonic comet programme).</b> Tycho rebuts Hagecius's 1578 finding of a <b>5° parallax</b> for the 1577 comet (which would place it <i>sublunar</i>, in the Aristotelian \"elementary region\"). Tycho's own instruments give a horizontal parallax of <i>at most ⅓ of a degree, often much less, sometimes none</i> — proving the comet was <b>supralunar, in the aether</b>. The argument is the same anti-Peripatetic one Tycho would publish in his comet book (<i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588).</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>altitudo</i> → altitude; <i>azimuthus</i> → azimuth (kept); <i>declinatio</i> → declination; <i>Aequator</i> → Equator; <i>meridianus</i> → meridian; <i>coeli mediatio</i> → culmination (the star's transit of the meridian); <i>orbis Lunae</i> → orb of the Moon; <i>aether / aethere</i> → aether; <i>Elementaris regio / regio ignea</i> → Elementary region / fiery region (Aristotelian sublunary physics); <i>motus proprius</i> → proper motion; <i>motus diurnus</i> → daily motion; <i>circulus verticalis</i> → vertical circle; <i>a vertice</i> → from the zenith. <b>Instruments:</b> <i>Radius</i> → cross-staff (radius astronomicus); <i>Sextans Astronomicus</i> → Astronomical Sextant (Tycho's own bipartite-arc sextant, here advertised as his invention); <i>quadrans</i> → quadrant; <i>instrumenta e solido metallo … singulorum minutorum adamussim capacia</i> → instruments of solid metal capable of single arc-minutes.</li><li><b>Units of arc.</b> <i>gradus / partes</i> (here \"degrees\"); <i>scrupulum / minutum / scrupulorum</i> are arc-minutes (or, with \"18 scrupulorum,\" minutes <b>of time</b> for the observation-interval — note the ambiguity at the <i>18 saltem scrupulorum</i> passage: in context \"an interval of time … of only 18 minutes\" it is <b>time</b>-minutes; elsewhere <i>scrupulum</i> is <b>arc</b>-minutes). Figures are reproduced exactly as written: parallax \"5 partium\" (5°); proper motion \"27′\" in two hours; daily motion \"5° 23′\"; Pegasus-distance \"1° 16′\"; Altair-distance \"6° 22′\"; pole-elevation of Hven \"55° 54⅔′\"; star max-altitude \"38° 20′\"; star declination \"4° 14½′\"; comet meridian altitude \"37° 7½′\"; comet declination \"3° 2′.\"</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>parallaxeωs</i> (line: \"si sensibilis aliqua parallaxeωs adfuisset quantitas\") — the genitive of <i>parallaxis</i> written with a Greek-letter ending (-εως) embedded in the Latin word; reproduced as printed, translated \"of parallax.\"</li><li><b>Stars (period star-names → modern identification).</b> <i>lucida Vulturis / Vulturis stella</i> = \"the bright star of the Vulture\" = <b>Altair</b> (α Aquilae; \"Vultur volans\" was the classical name); <i>lucida Aquilae</i> = \"the bright star of the Eagle\" = likewise <b>Altair</b>; <i>os Pegasi / lucidior capitis Pegasi</i> = \"the mouth / brighter [star] of the head of Pegasus\" = <b>Enif</b> (ε Pegasi). The comet's position is fixed by its distances from these.</li><li><b>Place-formula.</b> <i>Uranienburg in Insula Danici Hellesponti Huaena</i> = \"Uraniborg, on the island of the Danish Hellespont, Hven\" — Tycho's standard conceit, the Øresund as the \"Danish Hellespont.\" <i>Comitia Ratisbonensia</i> = the <b>Diet of Regensburg</b> (1575), where Tycho and Hagecius had met in person.</li><li><b>Editorial apparatus markers in the source.</b> The transcription carries scattered superscript \"A\" sigla attached to words (e.g. <i>ex conductoA, tibiA, indicoA, eamA, illaA, parallaxinA, … BRAHE.A</i>). These are <b>lemma-anchors of the critical edition's variant apparatus</b> (recording readings of MSS A and B), <b>not</b> part of Tycho's text. Per protocol §4(2) they are reproduced <b>as printed</b> in the Source block, but are <b>not</b> rendered in the translation. Likewise the source's <i>fi</i> (for <i>si</i>) and <i>satissactum</i> (for <i>satisfactum</i>) are retained as printed; <i>satissactum</i> is an evident typographic doubling for <i>satisfactum</i> (\"made good/satisfied\"), translated accordingly.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 12. Octobris Anno 1581</i> — Old Style (Julian; Denmark kept O.S. until 1700). This <b>agrees</b> with the catalogue label (1581.12.oktober). The in-text observation dates \"13. Octobris,\" \"21. Octobris\" are likewise O.S. and refer to October 1580 (the apparition of the comet \"which shone in the year [15]80\"), not 1581; no silent correction made. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the epistolary salutation formula S. (Greeting), printed by Dreyer between the heading and the letter's opening word (TBOO VII p.64) but absent from our source Latin and English; added to both texts.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the astronomy is verified against the corpus (Dreyer; Mosley; Norlind). Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the unit of <i>18 saltem scrupulorum</i> (time-minutes of the observation interval, on the contextual reading, vs. arc-minutes elsewhere — disambiguated in context but inherently the same word); and (2) <i>vasa Spagyrica … ex conducto</i>, where <i>ex conducto</i> may mean the vessels were \"hired/leased\" or \"ordered under contract\" — both senses are given. Neither affects the scientific argument.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full visual collation against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.64-69 (7-page slice, read in two chunks: pages 1-4 and 5-7 via the Read tool on page images, no text layer used). Checked opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date, and place-name: Paulus Wittich of Wrocław, Diet of Regensburg (Comitiis Ratisbonensibus), Bartholomaeus Scultetus of Leipzig, Regiomontanus, Vogelinus/Vögelin, Homerus; the parallax figures (1/3 degree max, 5 partium, 18 scrupulorum, &gt;20-fold [vicesies] recomputation, 27' motion / 5°23' daily rate, 10-or-5-minute instrument tolerance, 1°16' and 1' and 5' differentials, hour 8 vs hour 10, 6°22', 6-degree terrestrial baseline, nearly-8'-declination error, pole elevation 55°54⅔', max altitude 38°20', declination 4°141⁄2', comet altitude 37°71⁄2', declination 3°2', 5' difference, quarter-hour meridian gap, plus-3/4-degree Scultetus error); the dates 12/13/21 October 1581 and the closing date-line \"12 October 1581\". All of these render Dreyer's printed text accurately with no fabricated, dropped, or flipped content, and the bracketed editorial glosses (e.g. [Altair]) are clearly marked supplements per the audit's own exclusion rule, so not counted. The single defect found is the dropped opening \"S.\" salutation-formula word, present in Dreyer but absent from our source_text and consequently untranslated — a genuine TEXT_ERROR by the letter's strict definition, though of no substantive consequence. This is an unusually clean letter; the published grade B undersold rather than oversold it on substance, but the letter-of-the-rule TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints a standalone \"S.\" (the epistolary salutation formula, \"greeting\") between the heading and the letter's opening word; our source_text omits this word entirely, so the published English also carries no trace of it. Trivial and formulaic — it adds no propositional content — but it is a verified, word-level discrepancy between our Latin and Dreyer's print. Dreyer prints: <i>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAEUM HAGECIUM. S. [then decorative initial] ANNUS integer...</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.64, line 4), which shows HAGECIUM. followed by a standalone S. on its own line before the decorative initial A(NNUS). The rest of the letter (pp.64-70, 7 pages) was read in the first four-page chunk and carried no other listed defects, so no other changes were made per the prime rule.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 12-16. Afskrift", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, fol. 32-34. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15801104001", "next": "brahe-t_15820501001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15760823001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15820501001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Suspects earlier letters went astray, notes receipt of Hagecius's comet booklet brought by Bartholomaeus Scultetus, and prepares to offer the requested opinion on it.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15811012002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15820501001", "d": "1582-05-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 1 May 1582 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, VIRTUTE ET DOCTRINA PRAeSTANTI AC MATHEMATUM ILLUSTRATORI EXIMIO D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, INSULAe HUAeNAeDANICI HELLESPONTI PRAeSIDI, DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>ACCEPI literas tuas, Vir Nobilissime et amice observande, quae fuerunt et gratissimae et exoptatissimae. Sed cum ex eis intellexissem meas tibi non fuisse redditas, vehementer conturbatus sum, coepique quiritari de iniqua sorte, quod ea utrique nostrum invidit illam iucunditatem, quam percepturi eramus, tu quidem legendo mea, ego sciendo, mea tibi reddita esse. Fuisset etiam liberatus sinistra suspicione apud te WITTICHIUS noster, quod tuas mihi non reddidisset, qui certe non solum primo quoque tempore fidelissime tuas mihi per certos homines reddidit, verum etiam literis suis mihi persuadere annisus est, ut me transferrem isthuc, invitabatque ut comes esse vellem sui ad te reditus. Addebat ad persuadendum te ex certa scientia et voluntate Regis ea ad me perscribere ac daturum fuisse ipsummet Regem literas invitatorias ad me, si prius ipsi per te de mea certa voluntate constitisset, quam nunc solum per tuas literas explorare voluit. Haec vero etsi in tuis ad me non ita diserte perscripta et expilata essent, tamen persuasiones illas ab optimo et propensissimo eius erga utrumque nostrum animo proficisci accipiebam. Tuas quoque rationes, quod ab eodem animo proficiscerentur, admittebam omniaque illa interpretabar, ut verum amicum decuit. Quare carere debes hac in parte omni sinistra suspicione de WITTICHIO nostro. Praestitit enim officium boni viri et amici fidelissimi. Me vero respondisse ad tuas mense Februario anni praeteriti, testis esse potest D. CHYTRAeUS. Ad ipsum enim meas ad te misi, et ut eas ad te perferendas curaret, rogavi. Respondit mense Aprili anni superioris se meas accepisse et ad te primo quoque tempore et commoditate missurum esse. Intercidisse igitur meas inter Rostochium et Hafniam oportuit, si alicui perferendae datae sunt. Haec tu ipse, si ita lubet, ex ipsomet D. CHYTRAeO cognoscere poteris. Quod ad invitationem attinet, ut me transferam isthuc, ardua et difficilis est quaestio. Ac primum quaeso te, mi TYCHO, expende ipsemet accuratius et verte omnes rationes, quam mihi ea difficilia sint factu. Sum ingravescente aetate; ago enim annum 56. Sum pater multorum liberorum. Commigrare igitur in alienas terras et quasi spontaneum exilium eligere, ubi et mores gentis et lingua peregrina est, profecto difficile, durum et miseriâ plenum est. Addo neque honorificum mihi et meis futurum esse absque honesta et decenti vocatione in alienas terras migrare. Quid enim aliud vestrates dicturos aut saltem suspicaturos esse cogitare possum, quando me non vocatum venisse ad vos conspicerent, quam me hinc vel eiectum vel egestate compulsum alienas quaesivisse sedes? Quid nostrates? Levem et inconstantem parumque prudentem, quod me unius hominis amica invitatione tam facile permoveri passus sim, incerta pro certis elegerim, verba honoris pro amplissimis rebus acceperim. Dicent etiam haud dubie temerarium et inconsideratum, et qui conditionis et existimationis suae nullam scirem habere rationem, quod qui olim Caesaris Medicus fuerim, honeste et decenter accitus; qui ante quinquennium a Rege Sueciae, deinde ab ordinibus Stiriae expetitus, cui etiam, si voluissem aut vellem, locus fuerit, et esse possit adhuc apud Regem Poloniae, denique qui huius Regni nobilis civis sum, nunc sponte et temere extra patriam ultro sedes quaeram et servitium expetam. Ac ut tandem finiam, migrare ad vos honorifice non vocatus, non propositis mihi certis conditionibus, plane non videtur consultum, non meae aetatis est, non conditionis, non dignitatis. Excurrere vero ad te, ut praeclara illa tua studia Chymica et Mathematica meis oculis conspicerem, teque praesentem complecterer, et in tuo amore totus conquiescerem, percuperem sane, nisi in eo quoque non paucae essent difficultates et quidem graves: iter longum et difficile, sumptus non exigui, denique excursio illa nimis magna esse videretur. Sed si aliqua commoditas veniendi isthuc et redeundi se offerret, praetermittere eam nulla ratione vellem. Nunc quoque impedior cura uxoris meae, quae partui vicina est. Vides, mi TYCHO, meas iustas et honestas rationes, quibus impediar et detinear, ut neque tuae benevolentiae, quam ego summam et optimam esse erga me agnosco, neque meo desiderio satisfacere queam. Itaque pro tuo isto erga me optimo et benignissimo animo, cura et solicitudine mei, quod mihi optima cupias, omniaque officia amoris et benevolentiae sponte deferas, tibi maximas, quas verbis exprimere licet, ago et habeo gratias, vicissimque tibi persuasissimum esse cupio, me ita amore tui incensum esse, ut si liceret tecum esse, reliquum meae vitae iucundissimum putarem, tuaque causa nihil non facturum esse.</p><p>Haec fere in prioribus meis ad te scripseram, quae quoniam alicubi perijsse intelligo, denuo repetere hisce oportuit. Venio ad alteram Epistolae tuae partem. Gratulor tibi vehementer istud otium Mathematicum, in quo te versari intelligo. Macte virtute, mi TYCHO, et in hoc cursu, ut coepisti, studium illud Astronomicum illustrare perge. Ephemeridem Solarem huius anni ex tuis observationibus exstructam accepi, et pro ea maximas tibi ago gratias. Mihi tantae magnitudinis instrumentum nullum est, ut singula scrupula capere possit, neque in meis aedibus ea mihi est commoditas, ut etiamsi tale quid habeam, collocare queam. Quadrante hactenus meo usus sum in meis stellarum et Cometarum observationibus. In eo dena et quina habentur scrupula. Radium satis magnum habeo, de quo hauddubie WITTICHIUS noster tibi significavit. Ad Solem igitur nullae mihi fuerunt observationes.Placet quod in Sole habeas rationem parallaxeos, quam huc usque omnes neglexerunt. WITTICHIUS noster eam quoque minime praetermittendam esse censuit. In Apogeo Solis video te tribus circiter gradibus anteriorem esse Copernico. Sed variatio Apogei etiam plurium graduum non mutat notabiliter motum Solis, in cuius Apogei constitutione laborarunt summi Artifices. De Cometarum parallaxibus quae perscripsisti, ea quoque plurimum grata et iucunda fuerunt. In parallaxi Cometae anni 77 meum lapsum iam ante agnovi et memet correxi. Nam non ita instructus fui instrumentis idoneis, quam olim Viennae in observatione novi sideris. Defuerunt mihi etiam multae aliae commoditates. Ac tu optime admones ex meo ipsius scripto longe minorem parallaxin fieri oportuisse, quemadmodum huius erroris in Epistola ad MYLIUM et in scripto de proximo Cometa Goerlitij impresso mentionem feci. Quod culminaverit cum Aquila, non ita a me scriptum est, quasi id a me fuisset observatum (nam culminatio illa Sole supra Horizontem exstante fuerat) sed quod saltem crassiori quadam indagine, collata cum alijs observationibus, de parallaxi aliquo modo iudicium fieri posse existimaverim. Scripsit autem alius quidam ineptus et meus plagiarius, culminasse Aquilam cum Cometa die 6 Decemb. h. 5. Ceterum quae ego in mea Dialexi olim cap. 9 de invenienda parallaxi ex culminatione Phaenomeni cum aliqua stella fixa scripseram, ea iamdudum correxi, ac caput 7,9 et 1O plane mutavi. Illustravi etiam aliquot problemata DIGGSEI et alia adieci tum mea, tum a WITTICHIO accepta. Et certe haec doctrina parallaxium mediocriter per nos illustrata est, de quo tamen tuum et aliorum intelligentium erit iudicium. Librarius solum nunc nos moratur, qui nostra illa imprimere vetat. Et ut admirandi illius novi sideris Historia ad posteros transmitteretur, non tantum nostra illa de parallaxibus adiungentur meae Dialexi, sed omnium aliorum scripta nostrae sententiae consentientia. Titulus libri erit talis, ut hic in charta descriptum vides. Vellem tuas quoque observationes Cometarum prodire, fuitque haec causa, cur eas in meo proximi Cometae scripto desideraverim. Nam quod aethereus fuerit uterque Cometa, id iam ante ex tuis ad me intellexeram, ideoque iam ante, ipso adhuc lucente Cometa, ad multos perscripseram. Proxima quadragesima fui Vratislaviae apud meam filiam et commoratus sum illic ultra mensem. Mea de parallaxibus cum WITTICHIO contuli, qui mecum communicavit vestras observationes proximi Cometae, quas habuistis diebus XI, XII, XIII et XVII Octob. Ex his ego experiri volui, in deductione calculi aberraverim, an momenta observationum non recte abs te aut a WITTICHIO consignata sint. Nam ex vestris illis datis ne verisimile quidem in parallaxibus omnibus illis diebus quicquam invenire potui. In altitudinibus Cometae inter nos alias satis recte convenit. Relinquitur igitur, ut momenta temporum minus recte assignata fuerint. Et certi temporis consideratio hac in parte valde est difficilis. Subduc quaeso tuum calculum et omnia accuratius considera meque admone. Ego ipse quoque, modo aliquid otij nactus fuero, denuo repetam calculum et vestra examinabo. Nam eorum in meo opere facere cupio mentionem, ut nostra sententia de Cometis in animis eruditorum eo magis confirmetur. Quod si post discessum isthinc WITTICHIJ aliquot dierum alias quoque habes illius Cometae observationes, eas mecum communicato.</p><p>B. V.<br>Pragae ex aedibus nostris die 1. Maij Anno 1582.<br>T. ex animo<br>THADDAeUS HAGECIUS<br>ab Hayck.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, eminent in nobility of birth, in virtue, and in learning, and an outstanding adorner of the mathematical sciences, governor of the island of Hven in the Danish Hellespont, his lord and friend to be honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, most noble Sir and friend to be honoured, and it was both most welcome and most longed-for. But when I gathered from it that mine had not been delivered to you, I was greatly distressed, and began to lament my unjust fortune, in that it had begrudged both of us the pleasure we were to have had — you in reading what I wrote, I in knowing that my letter had been delivered to you. Our friend Wittich, too, would have been freed from any sinister suspicion in your eyes of not having delivered your letter to me — he who assuredly not only delivered yours to me most faithfully at the first opportunity, by trustworthy men, but also strove by his own letters to persuade me to remove myself thither, and invited me to be willing to be a companion of his return to you. He added, by way of persuasion, that you were writing this to me from sure knowledge and at the King's wish, and that the King himself would have given letters of invitation to me, had it first been established for him through you concerning my settled willingness — which he now wished only to ascertain through your letter. And although these things had not been set out and unpacked so explicitly in your letter to me, nevertheless I took those persuasions to proceed from his most excellent disposition, most kindly inclined toward us both. Your own reasons too I admitted, as proceeding from the same disposition, and I interpreted all those things as became a true friend. Wherefore on this point you ought to be free of any sinister suspicion regarding our Wittich; for he discharged the office of a good man and a most faithful friend. That I did indeed answer your letter in February of last year, Master Chytraeus can be a witness. For I sent my letter to you by his hand, and asked him to see to its being carried on to you. He replied in April of last year that he had received my letter and would send it on to you at the first opportunity and convenience. It must therefore be that my letter went astray between Rostock and Copenhagen, if it was indeed given to someone to carry on. These things you yourself, if you so please, will be able to learn from Master Chytraeus himself. As regards the invitation, that I should remove myself thither — it is a hard and difficult question. And first I beg you, my Tycho, weigh it yourself more carefully and turn over all the considerations of how difficult these things are for me to do. I am of advancing years, for I am in my 56th year. I am the father of many children. To migrate, then, into foreign lands and as it were to choose a voluntary exile, where both the customs of the people and the language are strange — that is assuredly difficult, harsh, and full of misery. I add that it would be neither honourable for me and mine to migrate into foreign lands without an honourable and fitting summons. For what else can I think your people would say, or at least suspect, when they saw that I had come to you uninvited, than that I had either been cast out from here or driven by want to seek a dwelling abroad? And what would my own people say? — That [I am] fickle and inconstant and little prudent, in that I let myself be so easily moved by one man's friendly invitation, that I chose uncertainties for certainties, that I took words of honour for the most ample rewards. They will say too, without doubt, that [I am] rash and inconsiderate, and one who knew to take no account of his own station and reputation — that I, who was once the Emperor's physician, honourably and fittingly summoned; who five years ago was sought after by the King of Sweden, then by the Estates of Styria; for whom too, had I wished or were I to wish it, there was a place, and could yet be one, with the King of Poland; and finally I, who am a noble citizen of this Kingdom — that I should now, of my own accord and rashly, go out beyond my homeland unbidden to seek a dwelling and beg for service. And to make an end at last: to migrate to you, not honourably summoned, with no fixed conditions set before me, plainly does not seem advisable; it is not suited to my age, nor to my station, nor to my dignity. But to make an excursion to you — that I might behold with my own eyes those splendid Chymical and Mathematical studies of yours, and embrace you in person, and rest wholly in your love — that I should indeed greatly desire, were there not in this too difficulties, and grave ones: a long and difficult journey, no slight expense, and finally that excursion would seem too great an undertaking. But if some convenient opportunity of coming thither and returning should present itself, I would by no means wish to let it pass. Now too I am hindered by concern for my wife, who is near her delivery. You see, my Tycho, my just and honourable reasons, by which I am hindered and held back, so that I can satisfy neither your kindness — which I acknowledge to be the greatest and best toward me — nor my own longing. And so, in return for that most excellent and most kindly disposition of yours toward me, your care and solicitude for me, in that you wish me the best and unprompted offer me every office of love and goodwill, I give and hold the greatest thanks that words can express; and in turn I wish you to be most fully persuaded that I am so kindled with love of you that, were I permitted to be with you, I would deem the rest of my life most delightful, and that there is nothing I would not do for your sake.</p><p>These things, more or less, I had written in my earlier letter to you, which, since I understand it perished somewhere, it has been needful to repeat anew herewith. I come to the other part of your letter. I congratulate you heartily on that Mathematical leisure in which I understand you to be engaged. Go on and prosper in virtue, my Tycho, and in this course, as you have begun, press on to illustrate that Astronomical study. I have received the Solar Ephemeris of this year, constructed from your observations, and for it I give you the greatest thanks. I have no instrument of such size that it can take in the single minutes [of arc], nor in my house is there the convenience that, even if I had some such thing, I could set it up. Hitherto I have used my quadrant in my observations of the stars and comets. On it are reckoned tens and fives of minutes. I have a fairly large cross-staff (<i>radius astronomicus</i>), of which our Wittich has doubtless informed you. To the Sun, therefore, I have had no observations. I am pleased that for the Sun you take account of parallax, which up to now everyone has neglected. Our Wittich too judged that it should by no means be passed over. In the apogee of the Sun I see that you are about three degrees in advance of Copernicus. But a variation of the apogee, even of several degrees, does not notably change the motion of the Sun, on the establishing of whose apogee the greatest craftsmen have laboured. What you have written about the parallaxes of the comets was also most welcome and delightful. As to the parallax of the comet of [15]77, I already acknowledged my slip before this and corrected myself. For I was not so furnished with suitable instruments as I once was at Vienna in observing the new star. Many other conveniences too were lacking to me. And you rightly point out that, from my own writing, a far smaller parallax ought to have resulted — just as I made mention of this error in the <i>Letter to Mylius</i> and in the tract on the recent comet printed at Görlitz. That [the comet] culminated together with Aquila was not so written by me as though it had been observed by me (for that culmination occurred while the Sun stood above the horizon), but [I wrote it] because I reckoned that at least by some rougher line of inquiry, compared with other observations, some judgment about the parallax could in some way be made. But a certain inept fellow, and my plagiarist, wrote that Aquila culminated together with the comet on 6 December, at the 5th hour. As for the rest — what I once wrote in my <i>Dialexis</i>, chapter 9, on finding parallax from the culmination of a phenomenon together with some fixed star — that I corrected long ago, and I have wholly changed chapters 7, 9, and 10. I have also elucidated several of Digges's problems, and added others, both my own and ones received from Wittich. And indeed this doctrine of parallaxes has been moderately well elucidated by us — though on that the judgment will rest with you and with others who understand. The printer alone now delays us, who forbids those things of ours to be printed. And so that the History of that admirable new star may be transmitted to posterity, there will be joined to my <i>Dialexis</i> not only those writings of ours on parallaxes, but the writings of all others that agree with our view. The title of the book will be such as you here see written out on the [enclosed] sheet. I should wish your observations of the comets too to come out, and this was the reason why I missed them in my tract on the recent comet. For that each comet was ethereal, that I had already understood beforehand from your letters to me, and therefore had already, while the comet was still shining, written to many. This past Lent I was at Wrocław with my daughter, and I stayed there above a month. I compared my [findings] on parallaxes with Wittich, who shared with me your observations of the recent comet, which you took on the 11th, 12th, 13th, and 17th of October. From these I wished to test whether I had gone astray in working out the calculation, or whether the moments of the observations had not been correctly recorded by you or by Wittich. For from those data of yours I could not find anything even plausible in the parallaxes on all those days. In the altitudes of the comet we otherwise agree well enough between ourselves. It remains, therefore, that the moments of time were less correctly assigned. And the consideration of exact time is in this respect very difficult. Work out your calculation, I beg, and consider everything more carefully, and advise me. I too, as soon as I have got some leisure, will repeat the calculation anew and examine your [observations]. For I desire to make mention of them in my work, so that our view about comets may be the more confirmed in the minds of the learned. But if, after Wittich's departure from there, you have other observations of that comet for some days, share them with me.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>At Prague, from our house, the 1st day of May, the year 1582.<br>Yours from the heart,<br>Thaddaeus Hagecius<br>ab Hayck.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (1525–1600), physician, astronomer, and astrologer; personal physician to Emperor Maximilian II and then Rudolf II at the Prague court. He and Tycho had met in 1575 at Rudolf's coronation as King of the Romans; he was Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent. It was Hagecius who in July 1580 sent <b>Paul Wittich</b> to Hven with a letter of recommendation. The letter answers an invitation (conveyed via Wittich, allegedly with royal — i.e. Imperial — backing) to settle near Tycho, which Hagecius here declines.</li><li><b><i>INSULAe HUAeNAe DANICI HELLESPONTI PRAeSIDI</b></i> — \"governor of the island of Hven in the Danish Hellespont\": a learned flourish for Tycho as lord of <b>Hven</b> in the Øresund (cast as the \"Danish Hellespont,\" the strait); <i>praeses</i> rendered \"governor.\" The capitalised salutation reproduces the manuscript's display capitals; <i>D.</i> = <i>Dominus</i> (\"Master/Lord,\" §5.5).</li><li><b><i>WITTICHIUS noster</b></i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician from Wrocław who spent some months on Hven in 1580 and later carried Tychonic methods to Kassel. \"Our Wittich\" (<i>noster</i>) marks him as the friend common to both. The whole opening turns on a lost-letter misunderstanding: Tycho suspected Wittich of not delivering a letter; Hagecius exonerates him.</li><li><b><i>Regis … literas invitatorias</b></i> — \"the King('s) … letters of invitation.\" The \"King\" (<i>Rex</i>) is <b>Rudolf II</b> (Holy Roman Emperor, also styled King of Bohemia / of the Romans); the proposal was that Tycho settle, or be invited, under Imperial auspices. Rendered \"King\" literally, as the source has <i>Rex</i> (not <i>Caesar</i> / <i>Imperator</i>) here; that Hagecius styles himself <i>Caesaris Medicus</i> below (\"the Emperor's physician\") confirms the same sovereign is meant.</li><li><b><i>D. CHYTRAeUS</b></i> = <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600), the Rostock theologian and professor, here the intended forwarding intermediary for Hagecius's earlier (lost) letter, which therefore \"went astray between Rostock and Copenhagen\" (<i>inter Rostochium et Hafniam</i>; Rostock → Copenhagen, §5.7).</li><li><b>Hagecius's refusal — career résumé.</b> <i>olim Caesaris Medicus</i> = \"once the Emperor's physician\"; <i>a Rege Sueciae … ab ordinibus Stiriae expetitus</i> = sought \"by the King of Sweden\" (<b>John III of Sweden</b>) and \"by the Estates of Styria\"; <i>apud Regem Poloniae</i> = a place possible \"with the King of Poland\" (<b>Stephen Báthory</b>); <i>huius Regni nobilis civis</i> = \"a noble citizen of this Kingdom\" (Bohemia). He pleads age (<i>ago annum 56</i> — \"in my 56th year\"), many children, and his wife \"near her delivery\" (<i>partui vicina</i>).</li><li><b>Astronomy — solar theory.</b> <i>Ephemeridem Solarem … ex tuis observationibus exstructam</i> = \"the Solar Ephemeris … constructed from your observations.\" <i>rationem parallaxeos … in Sole</i> = Tycho's reckoning of <b>solar parallax</b> \"which up to now everyone has neglected.\" <i>In Apogeo Solis … tribus circiter gradibus anteriorem … Copernico</i> = Tycho's value for the <b>apogee of the Sun</b> (\"apse,\" §5.1) is \"about three degrees in advance of Copernicus.\" Glossary §5.1 renderings (parallax, apogee, motion) used throughout.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Quadrante … meo</i> = \"my quadrant,\" reading \"tens and fives of minutes\" (<i>dena et quina … scrupula</i>) — i.e. graduated only to 5′; <i>scrupulum</i> (arc-minute) rendered \"minute [of arc].\" <i>Radium satis magnum</i> = \"a fairly large <b>cross-staff</b> (<i>radius astronomicus</i>),\" §5.2. <i>singula scrupula capere</i> = an instrument fine enough \"to take in the single minutes.\"</li><li><b>The comet-parallax controversy (the technical core).</b> <i>Cometae anni 77</i> = the great <b>comet of 1577</b>. Hagecius concedes his earlier <i>lapsus</i>: his published parallax (~5°, which would place the comet sub-lunar) was too large; he had already corrected himself, having lacked good instruments — \"not so furnished … as once at Vienna in observing the new star\" (<i>novum sidus</i> = the <b>nova of 1572</b>, the subject of his <i>Dialexis</i>). <i>Epistola ad MYLIUM</i> = his published <i>Letter to Mylius</i>; <i>scripto … Goerlitij impresso</i> = his comet tract printed at <b>Görlitz</b> (<i>Goerlitium</i>). <i>culminaverit cum Aquila</i> = the comet's culmination \"together with [the constellation/star] Aquila\" — Hagecius stresses this was a computed/inferred datum (the culmination fell while the Sun was up, so it was not directly observed), a \"rougher line of inquiry\" (<i>crassiori … indagine</i>). <i>alius quidam ineptus et meus plagiarius</i> = \"a certain inept fellow, and my plagiarist,\" who misreported the culmination on 6 December at the 5th hour — left unnamed in the source and so here.</li><li><b><i>Dialexi … cap. 9 … caput 7,9 et 1O</b></i> — his <i>Dialexis</i> (1574, on the 1572 nova); he has revised chapter 9 (and recast chapters 7, 9, 10) on deriving parallax from a phenomenon's culmination with a fixed star. <i>1O</i> is printed with a capital-O for the zero/numeral; read \"10.\" <i>problemata DIGGSEI</i> = problems of <b>Thomas Digges</b> (<i>Diggseus</i>, c. 1546–1595), the English mathematician, from his <i>Alae seu scalae mathematicae</i> (1573) on the 1572 nova.</li><li><b><i>Librarius … imprimere vetat</b></i> — \"the printer (<i>librarius</i>) … forbids those things of ours to be printed\": publication of his enlarged collection (his <i>Dialexis</i> + others' parallax writings agreeing with his view, prefaced by a <i>Historia</i> of the new star) is being held up by the bookseller-printer. <i>Titulus … ut hic in charta descriptum vides</i> = the proposed title was written out on a sheet enclosed with the letter (not preserved in the text).</li><li><b>The 1582 comet collation.</b> <i>proximi Cometae</i> = \"the recent comet\" (the comet of <b>1580</b>, the most recent before this letter). <i>aethereus … uterque Cometa</i> = \"each comet was ethereal\" (super-lunary) — Hagecius had already accepted this from Tycho's letters. <i>Proxima quadragesima … Vratislaviae</i> = \"this past Lent … at <b>Wrocław</b>\" (<i>Vratislavia</i>), with his daughter. There Wittich shared <b>Tycho's observations of the comet on 11, 12, 13, and 17 October</b>. Collating them, Hagecius could find \"nothing even plausible\" in the parallaxes; since the altitudes agreed, he infers the recorded <b>times</b> (<i>momenta temporum</i>) were imprecisely assigned — \"the consideration of exact time is in this respect very difficult.\" He asks Tycho to recompute and to send any further observations made after Wittich left Hven.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene vale</i>, \"Fare you well\" (§5.5). <i>T. ex animo</i> = \"Yours from the heart\" (<i>T.</i> = <i>Tuus</i>). <i>ab Hayck</i> = the family name <i>z Hájku</i>, kept as written.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Pragae … die 1. Maij Anno 1582</i> = \"at <b>Prague</b> … the 1st day of May 1582.\" Denmark and Bohemia were both still on the <b>Julian calendar</b> in May 1582 (the Gregorian reform took effect only that October), so the date is <b>O.S.</b> with no Julian–Gregorian gap at issue. The header date matches the dateline; the dataset label (\"1582. 1. maj. Prag\") agrees — no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and at register; the technical comet-parallax argument is well-attested in the Tycho–Hagecius literature (the 1577 parallax error, the <i>Dialexis</i>, the Görlitz tract, the October-1580 observation collation). The only unidentified party is the deliberately anonymous \"inept plagiarist,\" left anonymous per the source; no reading is forced.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.69-72 as page images (two Read calls, pages 1-4 and covering through the letter's end on p.72; p.73 of the slice is the start of the next letter and not part of this one). Collated the full Latin source_text against the printed text sentence by sentence: salutation heading, every proper name (Wittichius/Wittich, Chytraeus, Mylius, Digges/Diggsei, Vienna, Görlitz/Goerlitium, Wrocław/Vratislavia, Rostock, Copenhagen/Hafnia, Sweden/Suecia, Styria/Stiria, Poland/Polonia, Aquila), every number and date (age 56, five years/quinquennium, three degrees apogee, comet of [15]77, 6 December hour 5, Dialexis chapters 7/9/10, the four October observation dates XI/XII/XIII/XVII, 1 May 1582 dateline), and the opening and closing lines all match Dreyer exactly (allowing for long-s/ligature typography only). Then checked the published_english against the Latin clause by clause: no invented facts, no dropped sentences, no flipped pronouns/antecedents, no hardened hedges, all bracketed English supplements are clearly marked. One trivial artifact noted but not counted as a defect: our stored source_text renders 'caput 7,9 et 1O' with a capital letter O in place of the digit 0 (Dreyer prints '10'); this is a typographic glyph slip with no semantic effect and the English translation correctly reads 'chapters 7, 9, and 10', so it does not rise to a reportable TEXT_ERROR.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15811012002", "next": "brahe-t_15820923001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15811012002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15820923001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receiving Tycho's letter, regrets his own was not delivered, and defends Wittich, who conveyed Tycho's letter faithfully and invited Hagecius to join him on Hven.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15820501001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15820923001", "d": "1582-09-23", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 23 September 1582 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE THADDAeO AB HAYCK S.</p><p>REDDIDIT mihi ante aliquot septimanas Goerlicius quidam Mercator literas tuas, clarissime Domine D. THADDAeE, amice carissime, ex quibus praeter singularem tuam et constantem erga nos benevolentiam intellexi etiam priores meas ad tuas manus secus ac suspicabar pervenisse. Unde WITTICHIUM satis ea in re nunc excusatum habeo. Saltem id admiror, quod nihil literarum a discessu suo elapso tempore ad me dederit. Sed habet is forte sua impedimenta, neque occasio facile in promptu est. Quare et hac in parte nihil ipsi imputo. Cum vero is qui tuas mihi attulerat, nunc ad me reversus sit, ut responsum, prout ipsi etiam iniunxeram, tibi reportaret, ego quamvis eo tempore id evenerit, quo maxime ad scribendum ob negociorum multitudinem impeditus sim, et a Serenissimo Rege nostro insuper avocer, non potui tamen intermittere, quin aliquid literarum tibi perferendum ipsi traderem, ne tui oblitus tibique rescribere data opera neglexisse apud te excusari possem. Quae itaque tuis literis pro hac brevi occasione temporis respondenda habui, sic brevibus accipe.</p><p>Quod adventum tuum ad nos prolixe excusas, tibi ea in re non plurimum contrarior, neque ego aliam ob causam nomine Regis te huc invitavi, quam quod a WITTICHIO intelligerem officio in Aula Imperatoris destitutum cogitare de alia eligendae vitae et sustinendae familiae ineunda ratione, adeo ut iam quoque a Rege Sueciae te vocatum esse dixerit. Cum vero ex proprijs tuis literis intelligam, res tuas non esse eo loco, ut patriam mutare alienoque Domino servire velis, nihilque tibi ad commodam vivendi rationem deesse in patria, tibi plurimum congratulor, et meam voluntatem, quod tibi, si opus fuisset, non deesse voluerim, ut boni consulas oro.</p><p>Quod de Apogeo ☉ scribis restitutionem nostram accedere propius Alphonsinorum rationibus, quam COPERNICI, ego sane ea in parte nullum habui respectum neque ALPHONSI neque COPERNICI; sed ex plurimis exquisitis observationibus Geometrice ad invicem collatis iuxta quartam partem ♋ haerere Apogeum (quam interdum una parte minus, interdum una plus) reperi, ut quamvis ex minimis hîc inquirantur maxima. Eccentricitatem vero multoties in eodem, quo assignavi, scrupulo reperi, novem minutis Coperniana maiorem. Et licet eccentricitas maxima ex parte prosthaphaereses variet, praesertim iuxta medias elongationes, tamen circa Apogeum et Perigeum intolerabilis est duarum vel trium partium loci, sub quo haeret Apogeum, error.</p><p>Quod lapsum in Cometa anni 77 et quosdam alios in Dialexi emendaveris, libenter audio. Id vero quod de culminatione Cometae cum Aquila in excusationem adducis, mihi non satis facit. Impossibilia enim non erant ad probandum tentanda neque excusationem ullam merentur; sed emendanda potius et revocanda, partim propter imperitos, partim propter invidos et malevolos. Scribis te editurum copiosius scriptum de Nova Stella et Cometis praeteritis, ubi intelligo, te etiam mea quaedam velle adiungere, a quo tamen, si impressum non est, quantum ad mea attinet, ut supersedeas, te rogatum velim. Ipse enim decrevi, quam primum licuerit, quaedam enucleatius et de Nova Stella (ubi pleraque festinanter scripta restitui, multa copiosius in demonstrationum certitudinem adduxi) et de subsequentibus praecipuis duobus Cometis, tum etiam de tertio illo non adeo illustri, qui hoc anno mense Maio in Septentrione sub Heniochi sidere per IO dies nobis apparuit, in lucem emittere observationefque certas in medium adducere et infallibilibus demonstrationibus rem omnem tum in motu et situ eorum, tum in parallaxibus comprobare. Nam quod quasdam observationes hîc habitas in Cometa anni 80 a WITTICHIO acceperis, et inde parallaxes indagare conatus fueris, oleum et operam perdidisti. Tempora enim ex diversis horologijs et ijsdem nondum satis correctis annotata erant, nec illam discretionem adhibueras, quaenam certiora alijs essent, et quae sibi invicem in quolibet horologio corresponderent, multo minus ut per transitus stellarum sive per Meridianum sive per certum Azimuth, prout necessarium erat, et a nobis postea factitatum est, ea corrigeres, adeo ut ob id pluribus minutis ubique fere aberratum sit, ut mirum esse non debeat, quod tantam in parallaxibus constituendis inconvenientiam, ob temporum incertitudinem inveneris. Ego vero etsi tempora omnia aliter correcta habeam, quam prima fronte praebebant ipsa horologia, tamen in parallaxibus indagandis longe certiori nitor ratione, quam ubi temporis opus sit intervallo. Ibi enim ex minimo peneque insensibili errore maximus et intolerabilis augmentatur. Ideoque alijs quibusdam rationibus, ubi exquisita temporis cognitio non requiritur, inquisivi, tam hunc quam antecedentem Cometam (in tertio enim nondum feci periculum, nec adeo facile erit possibile) pene insensibilem habuisse parallaxin, quemadmodum haec et alia quaedam sufficienter ex certis observationibus demonstrari intelliges, ubi libellus noster de tota hac materia in lucem prodierit, quem spero me per hybernum otium, Deo conatus nostros adiuvante, absoluturum. Velim itaque nihil mearum observationum in publicum edas, priusquam hinc certior factus fueris, quaenam sit mea de his sententia, a qua te plurimum abesse et temporibus incertis minimeque correctis inniti video, et mirari satis non possum WITTICHIUM tibi tempora observationum transposita et per transitus stellarum minime verificata obtulisse, cum tamen ipsimet praesenti dixerim horologijs non esse fidendum, sed longe accuratiore emendatione opus esse. Quantum ad distantias fixarum indagandas attinet, ego non amplius fidem adhibeo Radio multas ob causas, quas longa foret mora hîc recensere. Sed post discessum WITTICHIJ duo exquisita instrumenta in hunc usum adinveni, quae semper eandem distantiam in scrupulo praebent, adeo ut nec in defectu nec excessu aberrare possint. Oportet autem in utroque instrumento duos esse, qui observent. Et quamvis utrumque instrumentum ipsa fabricatione longe differat, tamen in quolibet eadem et sibi correspondens exquisite fit observatio. Sic nos per haec invenimus distantiam polaris ab undecima Cassiopeae p. 26 m. 25 exquisite (nisi quod aliquando dimidio scrupulo minor apparuit), quam tu 9 minutis maiorem collocas. Sic etiam in alijs discrimen inveni, de quo alio tempore tecum latius conferam. In declinatione Spicae nullam differentiam inter tuas et meas observationes reperio. Nam et ego multoties hîc eius altitudinem maximam p. 25 m. 10¼ praecise observavi, ubi Aequator attollitur p. 34 m. 7, adeoque declinatio p. 8 m. 57 plane ut tua, quod errorem quartae partis in aequinoctij praecessione Copernico hucusque irrepsisse convincit, posita eadem cum ipso latitudine, quam etiam exquisite duarum esse partium per alia instrumenta deprehendi, ut alibi haec et plaeraque alia copiose demonstrabo. Sed interim dum scribo, tempus me alio vocat, prohibetque, quantum velim, tecum colloqui. Dabo tamen operam, ut alia occasione oblata, copiosius de his atque alijs tecum agam. Vale, et mihi, cum licuerit, rescribere non intermitte. Verumtamen addam hoc unum, quod paene neglexeram. Hisce diebus Comes quidam Anglus primarius Reginae ad Regem nostrum Legatus, ut ipsum in ordinem Equitum Divi GEORGIJ honorifice consuetis ceremonijs reciperet, cum perfunctus esset sua apud Regiam Maiestatem legatione, me hic invisit, unâque inter caeteros Comites Medicum Doctorem THOMAM MUFETUM secum habuit. Is cum tui inter nos honorifica incidisset mentio, petijt ut tibi per occasionem datam suo nomine plurimam salutem dicerem tuumque filium, qui in Anglia est, recte habere indicarem.</p><p>Iterum feliciter vale.<br>Datae Uranienburgi in Insula Huaena 23. Septemb. 1582.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus ab Hayck — greeting.</b></p><p>A few weeks ago a certain merchant of Görlitz delivered to me your letter, most distinguished Master Thaddaeus, dearest friend, from which — besides your singular and constant goodwill toward us — I also understood that my earlier letter had reached your hands otherwise than I had supposed. So now in that matter I hold Wittich sufficiently excused. The only thing I wonder at is that he has sent me no letter at all in the time elapsed since his departure. But perhaps he has his own impediments, and an opportunity is not readily at hand. So in this respect too I impute nothing to him. And since the man who had brought me your letter has now returned to me, in order to carry back a reply to you, as I had also enjoined upon him — although it has fallen out at a time when I am most of all hindered from writing by a multitude of affairs, and am moreover called away by our most Serene King — I could not, even so, refrain from handing him at least some few lines to convey to you, lest I might be excused in your eyes as having forgotten you and deliberately neglected to write back. And so what I have had to answer to your letter, given this brief occasion of time, receive thus, in brief.</p><p>As to your excusing your coming to us at such length, in that matter I do not greatly contend against you; nor did I invite you here in the King's name for any other reason than that I understood from Wittich that, deprived of your office at the Emperor's court, you were thinking of entering upon some other plan of choosing a way of life and supporting your family — so much so that he said you had by now even been summoned by the King of Sweden. But since from your own letter I understand that your affairs are not in such a state that you would wish to change your homeland and serve another lord, and that nothing is wanting to you in your homeland for a comfortable manner of living, I congratulate you most heartily; and I beg you to take in good part my goodwill, in that I wished not to fail you, had there been need.</p><p>As to your writing that our restoration of the apogee of the Sun [☉] comes nearer to the reckonings of the Alphonsines than to those of Copernicus: I, for my part, in that matter had no regard either for Alphonso or for Copernicus; rather, from very many exquisite observations geometrically collated with one another, I found the apogee to lodge at about the quarter-point of Cancer [♋] (sometimes one degree less, sometimes one more) — so that here, indeed, the greatest matters are inquired into from the least. As for the eccentricity, I many times found it, at the very minute I had assigned it, greater than the Copernican by nine minutes. And although the eccentricity varies the prosthaphaereses in great part, especially about the mean elongations, yet around the apogee and perigee an error of two or three degrees in the place at which the apogee lodges is intolerable.</p><p>That you have corrected the slip concerning the comet of [15]77, and certain others, in the <i>Dialexis</i>, I gladly hear. But what you bring forward in excuse, concerning the culmination of the comet together with [the constellation] Aquila, does not satisfy me. For impossibilities ought not to have been attempted as if to be proved, nor do they merit any excuse; rather they are to be corrected and recanted — partly for the sake of the unskilled, partly for the sake of the envious and ill-disposed. You write that you are going to publish a fuller work on the New Star and on the comets past, where I understand that you also wish to append certain things of mine; from which, however — if it is not yet printed — so far as my own [matter] is concerned, I should wish you asked to forbear. For I myself have resolved, as soon as it shall be permitted, to bring into the light certain things more fully worked out, both concerning the New Star (where I have restored most of what was written in haste, and have carried much, more copiously, into the certainty of demonstrations) and concerning the two chief comets that followed, and then also concerning that third, not so conspicuous one, which this year in the month of May appeared to us in the North under the constellation of Auriga [Heniochus] for 10 days — and to bring forward sure observations into the open, and to prove the whole matter by infallible demonstrations, both as to their motion and place, and as to their parallaxes. For as to your having received from Wittich certain observations made here on the comet of [15]80, and having tried thereby to investigate the parallaxes — you have wasted oil and labour. For the times had been noted down from various clocks, and those not yet sufficiently corrected; nor had you applied that discrimination as to which were more reliable than others, and which corresponded with one another in any given clock — much less so as to correct them by the transits of stars, whether through the meridian or through a fixed azimuth, as was necessary, and as has since been done by us — so that on this account there has been error of several minutes almost everywhere; so that it ought to be no wonder that you found so great an inconsistency in establishing the parallaxes, on account of the uncertainty of the times. But I, although I have all the times corrected otherwise than the clocks themselves presented them at first appearance, nevertheless in investigating parallaxes rely upon a far more reliable method than where there is need of an interval of time. For there, from the least and almost imperceptible error, the greatest and intolerable one is magnified. And therefore by certain other methods, where exquisite knowledge of the time is not required, I have found that both this comet and the preceding one (for on the third I have not yet made the test, nor will it easily be possible) had an almost imperceptible parallax — just as you will understand these and certain other things to be sufficiently demonstrated from sure observations, once our little book on this whole matter shall have come into the light, which I hope, God assisting our endeavours, to complete during the winter's leisure. I should wish, therefore, that you publish nothing of my observations before you have been made more certain from here what my own opinion on these matters is — from which I see you are very far removed, and that you rely on times that are uncertain and by no means corrected; and I cannot wonder enough that Wittich offered you observation-times that were transposed and by no means verified by the transits of stars, since I had nevertheless told him myself, in person, that the clocks were not to be trusted, but that there was need of a far more accurate correction. As regards investigating the distances of the fixed stars, I no longer place faith in the cross-staff [<i>Radius</i>], for many reasons that it would be a long delay to recount here. But after Wittich's departure I devised two exquisite instruments for this use, which always yield the same distance to the [arc-]minute, so that they cannot err either in deficiency or in excess. It is necessary, however, that on each instrument there be two persons observing. And although each instrument differs greatly in its very construction, yet on either one the same and mutually corresponding observation is exquisitely made. Thus by these we have found the distance of the pole star from the eleventh star of Cassiopeia to be exquisitely 26 degrees 25 minutes (except that sometimes it appeared less by half a minute), which you place greater by 9 minutes. So too in other cases I have found a discrepancy, of which I shall confer with you more fully at another time. In the declination of Spica I find no difference between your observations and mine. For I too have many times here precisely observed its greatest altitude to be 25 degrees 10¼ minutes, where the equator is raised 34 degrees 7 minutes, and so the declination [is] 8 degrees 57 minutes, plainly as yours — which proves that an error of a quarter[-degree] in the precession of the equinox has hitherto crept in upon Copernicus, the same latitude being posited with him as I have also exquisitely ascertained, by other instruments, to be of two degrees — as I shall demonstrate copiously, these and most other things, elsewhere. But meanwhile, while I write, the time calls me elsewhere, and forbids me to converse with you as much as I would wish. I shall nonetheless take pains, when another occasion is offered, to treat with you more fully of these and other matters. Farewell, and do not, when it shall be allowed, neglect to write back to me. Yet I will add this one thing, which I had almost overlooked. In these recent days a certain English Count, the Queen's chief ambassador to our King — sent to receive him honourably, with the customary ceremonies, into the Order of the Knights of Saint George — when he had discharged his embassy before His Royal Majesty, visited me here, and had with him, among the other companions, the physician Doctor Thomas Muffet. When honourable mention of you fell among us, he asked that, given the occasion, I should convey to you in his name his very warm greeting, and indicate that your son, who is in England, is doing well.</p><p>Again, fare you happily well.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the island of Hven, 23 September 1582.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus ab Hayck</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Bohemian court physician and astronomer at Prague, the leading astronomical figure of Rudolf II's circle and Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent. His <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574) treated the 1572 nova, and he also published a <i>Descriptio</i> of the comet of 1577. (No structured person-profile is yet held for him; identification from the corpus — Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, and Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>.)</li><li><b>Sender's address-formula.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Goerlicius … Mercator</b></i> — \"a merchant of <b>Görlitz</b>\" (the Lusatian town), the unnamed carrier who both delivered Hagecius' letter and was to bring back this reply.</li><li><b><i>Wittichius</b></i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Wrocław/Breslau mathematician who had stayed several months on Hven in 1580 and who connected Hagecius and Tycho; Tycho here repeatedly faults the observation-<i>times</i> Wittich passed to Hagecius (see the parallax discussion). Tycho's irritation that the comet-of-1580 timings were \"transposed and by no means verified by the transits of stars\" is part of the larger Wittich-priority friction.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimo Rege nostro</b></i> — <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), Tycho's patron, in whose name Tycho had (per this letter) invited Hagecius to Denmark; <i>a Rege Sueciae … vocatum</i> refers to a reported competing summons from the King of Sweden (John III).</li><li><b><i>Aula Imperatoris</b></i> — the court of <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> in Prague, the office from which Hagecius had reportedly been \"deprived\" (<i>destitutum</i>).</li><li><b>Astronomy — solar apogee.</b> <i>Apogeo ☉ … iuxta quartam partem ♋</i> — the apogee of the Sun lodging \"at about the quarter-point of <b>Cancer</b>\" (i.e. near Cancer 6¼°, <i>quarta pars</i> = the fourth part/quarter of the 30° sign). The glyph ☉ is named \"Sun\" and ♋ \"Cancer\" in the translation, the glyph kept in the source (protocol §5.3). <i>Alphonsinorum rationibus … Copernici</i> — the <b>Alphonsine</b> tables vs. <b>Copernicus</b>; Tycho insists he followed neither authority but his own observations. <i>quamvis ex minimis hîc inquirantur maxima</i> — rendered literally (\"the greatest matters are inquired into from the least\"), a sententious turn kept; <i>maxima/minima</i> not interpreted away.</li><li><b>Astronomy — eccentricity &amp; prosthaphaeresis.</b> <i>eccentricitatem … novem minutis Coperniana maiorem</i> = \"the eccentricity … greater than the Copernican by nine minutes.\" <i>prosthaphaereses</i> kept untranslated (Tycho's additive-subtractive correction; protocol §5.1); <i>medias elongationes</i> = \"the mean elongations\"; <i>circa Apogeum et Perigeum</i> = \"around the apogee and perigee.\" <i>partium</i> and <i>minutis/scrupulo</i>: in this technical register <i>pars/partes</i> = <b>degree(s)</b> and <i>scrupulum/minutum</i> = <b>(arc-)minute</b> — rendered accordingly and noted here.</li><li><b>Comets.</b> <i>Cometa anni 77</i> = the great <b>comet of 1577</b>; <i>duobus … praecipuis Cometis</i> = the two chief comets (1577 and 1580); <i>tertio illo … qui hoc anno mense Maio … sub Heniochi sidere per IO dies</i> = a fainter third comet of <b>May 1582</b> seen \"under the constellation of Auriga\" for <b>10 days</b>. <i>Heniochi sidere</i>: <i>Heniochus</i> (Greek <i>Ἡνίοχος</i>, \"the Charioteer\") = the constellation <b>Auriga</b>; named in English, the Latinised Greek noted. <b><i>IO dies</b></i>: the printed/transcribed form <i>IO</i> is the Roman numeral <b>10</b> (= <i>decem</i>); reproduced as printed in the Source block and read \"10 days.\" <i>culminatione Cometae cum Aquila</i> — Hagecius' claim about the 1577 comet culminating together with <b>Aquila</b> (the Eagle), which Tycho rejects as an impossibility to be recanted, not excused.</li><li><b>Parallax method.</b> <i>oleum et operam perdidisti</i> = \"you have wasted oil and labour\" — the proverbial <i>oleum et operam perdere</i> (to waste lamp-oil and toil), kept as the idiom it is. <i>per transitus stellarum sive per Meridianum sive per certum Azimuth</i> = correcting clock-times \"by the transits of stars, whether through the meridian or through a fixed azimuth\"; <i>Azimuth</i> kept (protocol §5.1). <i>pene insensibilem … parallaxin</i> = \"an almost imperceptible parallax\" — Tycho's supralunary conclusion for the comets; the hedge <i>pene</i> (\"almost\") preserved.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Radio</i> = the <b>cross-staff</b> (<i>radius astronomicus</i>), which Tycho says he no longer trusts for stellar distances (protocol §5.2). <i>duo exquisita instrumenta … duos esse, qui observent</i> — two newly devised instruments each requiring two observers; rendered descriptively (Tycho does not name them here).</li><li><b>Stellar measurements.</b> <i>distantiam polaris ab undecima Cassiopeae p. 26 m. 25</i> = \"the distance of the <b>pole star</b> [<i>polaris</i>] from the <b>eleventh star of Cassiopeia</b>, 26°25′\"; <i>quam tu 9 minutis maiorem collocas</i> = \"which you place greater by 9 minutes.\" <i>declinatione Spicae … p. 8 m. 57</i> = the declination of <b>Spica</b> (α Virginis), 8°57′; <i>altitudinem maximam p. 25 m. 10¼ … Aequator attollitur p. 34 m. 7</i> = greatest altitude 25°10¼′, equator-altitude (co-latitude complement) 34°7′ at Hven. <i>errorem quartae partis in aequinoctij praecessione Copernico … irrepsisse</i> = Tycho's charge that \"an error of a quarter[-degree] in the precession of the equinox has crept in upon Copernicus.\" <i>latitudine … duarum … partium</i> = the (ecliptic) latitude of Spica \"of two degrees.\"</li><li><b>The English embassy.</b> <i>Comes quidam Anglus … in ordinem Equitum Divi Georgij</i> — the English embassy to install Frederik II in the <b>Order of the Garter</b> (the Order \"of the Knights of Saint George\"); the chief ambassador was <b>Peregrine Bertie, Lord Willoughby de Eresby</b> (the 1582 Garter mission). <i>Thomam Mufetum</i> = <b>Thomas Muffet</b> (Moffet/Moufet, 1553–1604), the English physician and entomologist, who accompanied the mission and visited Hven. <i>tuum filium, qui in Anglia est</i> — Hagecius' son then in England, whom Muffet reports to be well.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uranienburgi in Insula Huaena 23. Septemb. 1582</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, on the island of <b>Hven</b>, 23 September 1582\" (O.S. — Denmark kept the Julian calendar; protocol §3). The dateline date agrees with the dataset label (1582-09-23); no discrepancy. The header carries the as-written date.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> <i>THADDAeO / THADDAeE / Uranienburgi</i>: the source prints the <i>ae</i>-ligature form (<i>Ae</i>), reproduced as in the transcription. <i>observationefque</i>: the transcription's long-s reading of <i>observationesque</i> (\"and … observations\"), kept as printed in the Source block and read <i>-sque</i>. <i>negociorum, ijsdem, ijs, alijs, horologijs, parallaxin</i> are period spellings, normalised in sense only.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical content (solar apogee in Cancer, eccentricity, the comet parallaxes, the Cassiopeia/Spica measures) is consistent with Tycho's known positions. Minor uncertainties, flagged rather than smoothed: (i) the exact stellar referent of <i>undecima Cassiopeae</i> (\"the eleventh star of Cassiopeia,\" a Bayer/Tycho-catalogue ordinal not here individuated); (ii) the precise sense of <i>Aequator attollitur p. 34 m. 7</i> in the Spica reduction (read as the complementary equator-altitude at Hven's latitude, consistent with declination 8°57′); both noted, not resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.72-75 in full (the letter's actual span within the 5-page slice) and collated the entire letter body — opening, the Wittich-excuse paragraph, the Sweden-invitation paragraph, the solar-apogee/eccentricity technical paragraph, the 1577/1580 comet-parallax dispute paragraph, the cross-staff-instrument and star-distance paragraph, the Spica-declination paragraph, and the closing English-ambassador/Muffet anecdote with the date '23 Septemb. 1582' — against the page images (not OCR). Every proper name (Wittich, Copernicus, Alphonsus, Hagecius, Thomas Muffet) and every technical number checked against Dreyer word-for-word: apogee 'quartam partem Cancri', eccentricity 'novem minutis', polar-Cassiopeia distance '26 deg. 25 min.' vs Wittich's '+9 minutes', Spica altitude '25 deg. 10 1/4 min.', equator '34 deg. 7 min.', declination '8 deg. 57 min.', latitude error 'quartae partis' / 'duarum partium'. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15820501001", "next": "brahe-t_15850825001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15820501001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15851213001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of Hagecius's letter via a Görlitz merchant, clears Wittich of suspicion, and sends a brief reply despite pressure of affairs and a royal summons.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15820923001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15830821001", "d": "1583-08-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Charles de Dançay", "ck": "Charles de Dançay", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Charles de Dançay — Uraniborg, 21 August 1583 [O.S.] (catalogued 21 May 1583)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CAROLUM DANZAeUM.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine DANZAeE, Amice summa observantia colende, reddidit Architectus meus mihi literas a te missas, quibus percepi te, licet inter politica negotia iam multum temporis apud Cimbros consumseris, nondum tamen Huenensium memoriam obliterasse, pro qua benevola erga nos affectione tibi magnas habeo gratias. Nos licet absentes tui memoriam indies recolimus, optamusque ut brevi incolumis ad nos revertaris. Quod Sycophantae quidam aulici te vexent, oportet te magno animo ferre, et finem expectare, quo videbis omnia in ipsorum retundi caput. Hoc genus hominum delectatur aliorum afflictione, quam indies astute machinatur, ut sibi tandem perniciem (debitam mercedem) vel Deo ultore parent. Sed tute hoc nosti melius. Salutem quam ERICUS LANGIUS mihi per te dixit, velim ut ipsi referas, quamvis nunc eo felicitatis pervenerit (uti ab architecto meo intelligo) ut vix indigeat auspicatiore voto. Multiplicationem enim farinae adinvenisse ferunt ab unitate in ternarium novo Alchymiae mysterio, quo lapidis illius auriferi omnem fermentationem et multiplicationem (quibus tot annis insudatum est) excellentiore invento illudit; quis enim ignorat panis perpetuo sufficientem copiam, quae ex ista multiplicatione promanat, auro et argento praestantiorem esse, cum his carere possint homines, illo autem nequaquam. Caveat itaque sibi ab Alchymistarum invidia et prospiciat, ne arti struant insidias, fermentumque peregrinum admisceant. Diu multumque mecum cogitavi, in quem usum panis potius quam auri et argenti augmentationem investigaverit; tandem incidit mihi in mentem Draconis illius Iutici, qui olim bellum civile ipsorum Patriae movit, feruntque ob penuriam alimentorum in tanta militantium copia bellum illud Troiae decennium excessisse, quod alias citius finiri potuisset magno illorum commodo; veritus itaque ne vetus ille Draco reviviscat vel novus aliquando ex improviso exoriatur, Patriae suae hoc utilissimo invento providere voluit. Laudo ipsius prudentiam et Patriae eius congratulor, quod talem sibi genuerit protectorem et in commeatus penuria provisorem incomparabilem. Is rectissime mihi exequutus videtur Philosophorum mentem, qui nos non nobis sed Patriae nasci asseverabant. Ego per occasionem congratulatorium carmen in laudem tanti mysterij ipsi compingam, siquidem Draconis illius memoriam tantaeque victoriae celebrationem amplius referre non liceat, cum in profundum maris tota ipsius recordatio sit submersa. Sed forsan te a serijs detineo, dum tecum de his diutius iocor. Da operam ut ad nos ante hyemem revertaris, magno enim tui desiderio detinentur, ut multi alij, sic vel ego maxime, qui te tanto temporis locorumque intervallo tuaque exoptatissima conversatione admodum illibenter careo. Deus opti. max. te nobis incolumem quam citissime restituat, tuaque omnia negotia optato successu secundet.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae in aedibus Uraniae 21. Augusti 1583.<br>T. T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Charles de Dançay.</b></p><p>Magnificent Master Dançay, friend to be cherished with the utmost regard: my architect has delivered to me the letter you sent, from which I gathered that, although you have by now spent much time on political affairs among the Cimbri [Danes], you have nonetheless not yet blotted out the memory of the people of Hven — for which kindly affection towards us I owe you great thanks. We, though far off, daily call your memory to mind anew, and wish that you may soon return to us safe and sound. As for the fact that certain court flatterers vex you, you must bear it with a great spirit and await the end, in which you will see all of it beaten back upon their own heads. This kind of men delights in the affliction of others, which day by day they cunningly contrive, only that at last they may prepare destruction for themselves (their deserved wages) — or with God as avenger. But you yourself know this better [than I]. The greeting that Erik Lange sent me by your hand, I would have you carry back to him — although he has now reached such a pitch of good fortune (as I understand from my architect) that he scarcely needs any more auspicious wish. For they report that he has discovered the multiplication of flour, from unity into a threefold quantity, by a new mystery of alchemy, whereby, with this more excellent discovery, he makes a mockery of all the fermentation and multiplication of that gold-bearing stone [the philosophers' stone] over which men have sweated so many years; for who does not know that a perpetually sufficient supply of bread, such as flows from this multiplication, is more excellent than gold and silver, since men can do without these, but by no means without that? Let him therefore beware of the alchemists' envy, and take care that they lay no snares against the art, and mix in no foreign leaven. Long and hard did I ponder with myself to what use he should have investigated the augmentation of bread rather than of gold and silver; at length there came into my mind that Jutish Dragon, who once stirred up a civil war in their fatherland — and they say that, owing to a scarcity of provisions amid so great a host of fighting men, that war outran the ten-year war of Troy, though otherwise it might have been ended sooner, to their great advantage; and so, fearing lest that old Dragon should come back to life, or a new one some day arise unforeseen, he wished to provide for his fatherland by this most useful invention. I praise his prudence and congratulate his fatherland, that it has begotten for itself such a protector and, in a dearth of supplies, an incomparable provisioner. He seems to me to have carried out most rightly the mind of the Philosophers, who used to affirm that we are born not for ourselves but for our fatherland. As occasion serves, I shall compose for him a congratulatory poem in praise of so great a mystery — if indeed it be no longer permitted to recall the memory of that Dragon and the celebration of so great a victory, since the whole remembrance of it has been sunk into the deep of the sea. But perhaps I am keeping you from serious matters, while I jest with you too long about these things. See to it that you return to us before winter, for many others are held by a great longing for you, and so above all am even I, who most unwillingly do without you across so great an interval of time and place, and without your most-longed-for conversation. May God, Best and Greatest, restore you to us safe and sound as swiftly as may be, and prosper all your affairs with the success you wish.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given in the House of Urania [Uraniborg], 21 August 1583.<br>Yours truly [Tuus Totus],<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / place.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven; <i>in aedibus Uraniae</i> (\"in the House / dwelling of Urania\") is Tycho's own learned name for the residence-observatory, rendered with the gloss.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Carolus Danzaeus</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (c. 1510–1589), the long-serving French resident ambassador to the Danish court, a humanist and friend of Tycho; here evidently absent from Hven on diplomatic business \"among the Cimbri.\"</li><li><b><i>apud Cimbros</b></i> — \"among the Cimbri,\" a learned classicising name for the <b>Danes / the Danish (Jutland) realm</b>; kept and glossed, not modernised to \"in Denmark.\"</li><li><b><i>Architectus meus</b></i> — \"my architect\": the builder/master-craftsman in Tycho's service at Uraniborg, who carried the correspondence; rendered \"my architect\" and treated as the recurring bearer of news.</li><li><b><i>Sycophantae quidam aulici</b></i> — \"certain court flatterers / sycophants\": <i>sycophanta</i> in this register = informer/flatterer/slanderer, not the modern narrow sense; rendered \"court flatterers.\"</li><li><b><i>Ericus Langius</b></i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), Danish nobleman and devoted alchemist, Tycho's friend (and later, through Sophie Brahe, his prospective brother-in-law), whose lifelong and ruinous pursuit of the philosophers' stone is the target of the whole satirical passage.</li><li><b>The alchemical satire.</b> The letter's core is sustained irony. <i>lapis ille aurifer</i> = the <b>gold-bearing stone</b>, i.e. the philosophers' stone; <i>fermentatio</i> and <i>multiplicatio</i> are technical alchemical operations (the \"multiplication\" or augmentation of the stone's power). Tycho's joke inverts the goal: Lange, he says, has discovered the <i>multiplicatio farinae</i> — the \"multiplication of flour/bread\" <i>ab unitate in ternarium</i> (\"from unity into a threefold quantity,\" i.e. one part yielding three) — a feat better than gold-making, since men can live without gold but not without bread. The praise is mock-encomiastic throughout; <i>fermentum peregrinum</i> (\"a foreign leaven\") puns on alchemical <i>ferment</i> and the leaven of bread.</li><li><b><i>lapidis illius auriferi … fermentationem et multiplicationem (quibus tot annis insudatum est)</b></i> — the parenthesis (\"over which men have sweated so many years\") is Tycho's own aside on the futility of the long alchemical labour; preserved as a parenthesis, the hedge not sharpened into a flat verdict.</li><li><b><i>Draco ille Iuticus</b></i> — \"that Jutish Dragon\": an allusion (mock-heroic) to a legendary/historical episode of civil war in the Danish/Jutish past in which famine among the troops protracted the conflict beyond even the ten-year siege of Troy. The exact saga-referent is not specified in the letter; the comparison is rhetorical, and is left as Tycho frames it (see crux below). <i>in profundum maris … submersa</i> (\"sunk into the deep of the sea\") continues the mock-epic register: the memory of the dragon/victory is now lost.</li><li><b><i>congratulatorium carmen</b></i> — Tycho proposes, <i>per occasionem</i> (\"as occasion serves\"), to write a congratulatory <b>poem</b> for Lange in praise of the supposed discovery — itself part of the joke; rendered as prose intention (no verse is given in the letter).</li><li><b><i>Philosophorum mentem, qui nos non nobis sed Patriae nasci asseverabant</b></i> — \"the mind of the Philosophers, who used to affirm that we are born not for ourselves but for our fatherland\": a commonplace (ultimately Ciceronian/Platonic, <i>non nobis solum nati sumus</i>); kept as the sententious tag it is.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>T. T.</i> = <b><i>Tuus Totus</b></i> (\"Yours wholly / entirely truly\"); expanded in brackets in the rendering. <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux.</b> The dateline reads <b><i>21. Augusti 1583</b></i> = <b>21 August 1583</b> (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dataset `label` field, however, catalogues the letter as <b>\"1583. 21. maj\"</b> (21 May 1583). These disagree by three months. The header follows the <b>as-written dateline (21 August)</b> per protocol §3–4, with the catalogue date appended in parentheses; the slug (`…15830821001`) likewise encodes August, corroborating the dateline against the `label`. The discrepancy is recorded, <b>not</b> silently reconciled — the source text substantiates only 21 August.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> The witness is given only as a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Tycho Brahe's private archive, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen — i.e. a scribal copy, not an autograph original.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the sustained irony is preserved rather than flattened into literal praise. Two points are flagged rather than resolved: (1) the <b>catalogue-vs-dateline date conflict</b> (21 May label vs. 21 August dateline), settled in favour of the dateline per protocol but recorded as a discrepancy; and (2) the precise historical/saga referent of the <b><i>Draco Iuticus</b></i> (\"Jutish Dragon\") civil-war allusion, which the letter does not pin down and which is therefore left as Tycho frames it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.75-77 (letter itself on 75-76): opening address to Dançay, the Erik Lange/alchemy-of-bread jest (the 'Jutish Dragon' allegory, the Trojan War comparison), and the closing/date (Uraniborg, 21 August 1583, signed T.T. Tycho Brahe) all match Dreyer exactly. Translation is faithful throughout, including the ironic register of the alchemy passage. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Charles de Dançay", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15790705002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15761117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Dançay's letter, urges him to endure court intrigues with composure, and conveys greetings from Erik Lange.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15830821001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15840000001", "d": "1584-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Brucaeus — [no place], [1584; no day-date in source]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII, p. 80) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO D. D. HENRICO BRUCAeO.<br>S. P.</p><p>EX literis tuis nuper ad me datis, charissime D. D. BRUCAeE, intellexi te recte valere, id quod pergratum cognitu fuit. Quod vero studia Mathematica paulatim negligas, non probo. Nec enim tanta tibi est aetas, ut ea iucundissimam horum tractationem avertere possit, sed quaestuosior est vestra Medicina iuxta vetus dictum: Dat GALENUS opes etc. Et plerumque mortales quo plus senescunt, eo redduntur avariores, ac terrestribus plus proni inhiant, coelestibus posthabitis. Quod tamen nonnihil ad neglecta haec studia redire, et PROCLI Hypotheses explicare decreveris, laudo plurimum, velimque mihi exemplar Hypothesium illarum PROCLI mittas. Nondum enim Latinum habere potui, quamvis satis mihi constet, eas, siquidem Ptolemaicis nituntur fundamentis, coelo minime esse ad amussim consonas. Nos enim aliquot annorum praecedentium exquisitissimis in motu Solis observationibus deprehendimus, nec Ptolemaicam, nec Copernianam Hypothesin ipsius apparentijs satisfacere, longeque aliam esse Apogei et Eccentricitatis rationem, quam uterque horum artificum praesupposuit: ut brevi, Deo volente, ex quamplurimis ijsdemque minime fallacibus observationibus Astronomiae cultoribus palam reddemus. Motus enim Solis adeo exacte nobis constat, ut singulis diebus cum serenitas observationem ratam largitur, nostrae calculationi ex proprijs Tabulis in ipso scrupulo ad amussim correspondeat, differenti tam Alphonsina quam Coperniana ratione saepenumero quamplurimum, adeo ut hoc anno, Sole aequinoctium vernum ingrediente, defecerit in ipsius motu COPERNICI ratio semisse partis, abundaverit vero Alphonsinorum ratio partis quadrante. Paulo enim propiores nunc sunt Alphonsini vero, quam ipse COPERNICUS, quantum ad Solis motum attinet, quamvis ambo intolerabiliter devient. In motu vero Lunae verior est exactiorque Hypothesis COPERNICI. Idque tum crebrae observationes Lunaris cursus circa 90 gradum ab ortu, tum parallaxes ipsius diligentius a nobis examinatae nos saepenumero docuerunt; utut et hae non parum devient. Desideratur enim aliquid in medijs motibus. Et proportio semidiametrorum Epicycli utriusque ad semidiametrum Concentrici paulo aliter limitanda venit, et forte alia adhuc minus perspecta inaequalitas. COPERNICUS enim rudiori Minerva suas observationes perfecit destitutus magnis, exquisiteque e solido metallo elaboratis instrumentis, qualia nos quamplurima, tum varietate structurae, tum magnitudinis et elaborationis affabre et perfecte, ingenti sumtu, industriaque assidua parata habemus, adeo ut neminem etiam priscorum Mathematicorum, utut regijs sumtibus adiutorum (quatenus ex scriptis eorum colligere est) unquam ad coelestes motus perscrutandos tanta magnitudine diligentiaque elaborata organa in usu habuisse, si haec aspiceres, facile nobis concederes. Neoterici vero nec nostra nec superiori aetate necessariam in his elaborandis constructionis formam diligentiamque adhibuerunt, ut ob id mirum non sit, paulatim collabascere Astrorum scientiam, adeo ut ipsius motus nondum exacte constet, nec anni quantitas rata sit, ut nihil nunc dicam de ceteris. Verum in his omnibus alio tempore, Deo volente, tibi alijsque Eruditis nostras observationes et his innixas demonstrationes communicabo, instrumentorumque, quibus in motu coelestium corporum ad amussim perscrutando utor, structuram exactamque elaborationem indicabo, ut constare possit, observationes a nobis habitas, quibus fundantur demonstrationes, exactas esse, nullique fallaciae obnoxias. Nec enim unico, sed pluribus instrumentis, ijsdemque diversimode fabrefactis eandem veritatis metam attingere solemus, modo observationi fides rata habebitur. Si tibi commodum esset hac aestate nos invisere, forte hîc videres, quae frustra remotius quaereres, nec te poeniteret eius itineris, pergratumque mihi advenires hospes, omni officiorum genere excipiendus; tunc coram de quamplurimis colloqui conferreque possemus, afpiceresque ea praesens, quae calamus chartaque non capiunt. Petis, ut Instrumentum aliquod Hypothesium Copernicearum tibi mittam, quale olim apud SCHRECKENFUXIUM vidi, id quod lubens facerem, si tale paratum haberem, neque enim unquam animum adieci ad eiusmodi organa conficienda, quae saltem inventa aliorum explicarent, cum ex nuda delineatione ea facile percipi possint, et adhuc sub iudice lis est, an res ita se habeat, nec ne. Quapropter omnem potius operam impendi in extruendis organis, quibus motus coelestes exacte constarent, ut antecessorum inventa per haec in trutinam vocari possint et ad veritatis metas examinari. Quorsum enim opus erat in nondum satis perspecta re illustranda aliquid laboris et temporis impendere, cum praesertim haec alias leviori negotio facile intelligi possit? Vidi equidem apud SCHRECKENFUXIUM parvulam machinam, qua se Hypotheses COPERNICI effinxisse existimabat. Sed dum rem postea penitius perpenderem, deprehendi, eam structuram minime menti COPERNICI sufficere. Putabat enim is eadem machina, prout inveheretur diversimode, tam Ptolemaicas quam Copernianas Hypotheses exprimi posse, id quod plane est impossibile. Nec facile dabitur, omnes motus Copernianae inventionis unica machina mechanice exprimere, quae sibi ipsi omnibus in partibus correspondeat, praesertim ob incomparabilem Octavae Sphaerae ad orbem Terrae distantiam, utquamvis longe cincinnior sit, paucioribusque indigeat circulis COPERNICI speculatio circa mundi symmetriam quam Ptolemaica. Et per triplicem illum motum unius Terrae plurima in coelo apparentia concinne salvari possunt, quibus plurimi alias excogitati orbes, circulique vix sufficerent, dummodo res se re vera ita haberet, et tales ipsi Terrae congruerent revolutiones, quod nos in duobus motibus, quos huic attribuit COPERNICUS, diversimode se habere, exquisitis observationibus deprehendimus. Motum enim annuum in circulo magno circa Solem Terrae nequaquam adesse, expertus sum in fine anni elapsi 82 et principio 83 ex parallaxibus Martis tum temporis acronychij, et ob id Terrae in minori distantia vicini, quam Sol a Terra removetur, idque iuxta COPERNICI placita ad 1/3 quasi partem, unde maiores parallaxes, quam Sol ipse inducere debuisset, cum tamen longe minores fuisse, creberrimis exquisitissimisque et sibi invicem correspondentibus observationibus eas deprehendimus; ut ob id tota Martis sphaera ulterius removeatur a nobis, quam ipse Sol. Quapropter tollitur illa Hypothesis in totum, qua COPERNICUS Solis motum et Epicyclos omnes reliquorum Planetarum ex Terrae annuo circuitu ingeniose quidem et satis concinne ex ARISTARCHI Samij fundamentis in lucem productis excusare nititur. Alterum vero declinationis motum cum suis librationibus, per quem aequinoctiorum inaequalem anticipationem et obliquitatis Eclipticae mutationem excusat Copernicus, neque ipsi Terrae adesse, diversa, longeque celerior, aequinoctiorum mutatio, quam tantillo etiam tempore admittit COPERNICI ratio, nos certiores reddet, et anni quantitas a REGIOMONTANI observationibus huc usque Copernianae rationi nequaquam consentiens. Ut taceam obliquationem Signiferi etiam nunc esse trinis scrupulis maiorem quam COPERNICUS et antecessores nostri superiori aetate annotârunt. Causam erroris ex Opticis fundamentis alibi declarabo. Et de his omnibus alijsque quamplurimis ad artem ipsam restituendam, motusque coelestes ad amussim in numeros revocandos pleniorem commemorationem, Deo adiuvante, instituam. An vero tertius ille motus, quo quotidiana revolutio salvatur, Terrae et vicinis elementis insit, non facile quis dixerit: Cum eadem ratione in hac atque in primo mobili apparentiae tantae concitationis excusari possint, et minori quidem circuitu, ideoque commodiore cornpendio omnium sphaerarum secundi mobilis repentina ab ortu in occasum restitutio salvari, prout etiam Pythagoreis et Platonicis placuisse video. Verisimile tamen est, Terrae, tamquam gravissimo et densissimo opacoque corpori nequaquam hunc celerrimum inesse motum, sed potius ipsi coelo, cuius forma et materiae subtilitas constantiaque motui perpetuo et quamvis celerrimo aptior est. Frustra itaque expetis, charissime BRUCAeE, eorum in organo ob oculos planiorem repraesentationem, quorum in ipso coelo realis non existit forma. Sed tantum ingeniose ad salvandas aliqua ratione motuum apparentias a COPERNICO excogitata sunt. Si tamen Studiosos ipsius inventis imbuere voles, id per delineationem planam, quantum quidem opus est, facile perfeceris.</p><p>Sed est aliud, quod mea plurimum interest, de quo tecum agam. Conscripsi iamdudum quaedam de trium Cometarum intra proximum septennium visorum, et de Novae Stellae Phaenomenis, quibus motus omnes distantiasque et cetera, quae in considerationem veniunt, ex certis observationibus demonstro, et plurima alia tum ad hanc contemplationem tum ad totam artem facientia in medium profero, eaque tandem tum amicorum hîc in patria, tum plurimorum in Germania Angliaque solicitationibus persuasus in lucem edere constitui. Cum autem ob luem Epidemicam, multo iam tempore grassantem, non tutum fuerit, Hafniae apud Typographos nostrates quippiam eiusmodi expedire et nunc alijs quibusdam occupentur impressionibus, ut mature editionem hanc per eos absolvere non liceat, decrevi itaque ipsemet Typographiam quandam, quatenus proprijs meis lucubrationibus excudendis sufficiat, hîc Uraniburgi constituere, ut tam haec, quam alia, quae Deo volente in posterum absolvere conabor, Astronomiaeque cultoribus communicare, hîc penes meipsum Typis nostris mandari possint, eoque pacto figurae demonstrationum numerique et characteres, quibus, uti scis, in hac materia opus est, correctas certiusque adornari, et omnia a meipso ut emendate prodeant cognosci; siquidem pauci reperiantur, qui in his studijs exercitentur, et errores incidentes inter imprimendum rite discernere emendareque possint. Conduxi itaque hunc Typographicum servum, IOACHIMUM nomine, ut mihi hoc in negotio exsequendo inserviat, ablegavique illum nunc Witebergam, ut illinc mihi praelos, omniaque necessaria, quae hîc haberi nequeunt, comparet, cumque ipsi soli, nec mihi satis noto non adeo tutum sit pecuniam concredere, ad Scribam Arcis Witebergensis, cui nomen est VALENTINUS (cuius vitricus filias Regis nostri instituit, quique olim Witebergae familiaris mihi est) 50 Ioachimicos transscripsi, ut is illic exponat pro Typis alijsque ad opus pertinentibus, prout hic noster necessarium esse significaverit. Mitto itaque, charissime BRUCAeE, tibi 50 illos Ioachimicos, quos velim aurigae Witebergensi, quam primum ad vos venerit, tradas, ut is dicto Arcis Scribae VALENTINO eos unâ cum literis meo nomine perferat. Debet autem hic meus Typographus unâ Witebergam ire, vel potius praecedere, si auriga illic non adfuerit, illicque tam diu commorari, donec omnia necessaria ad Typographiam instituendam parata, huc per aurigam eundem abducere possit; si vero quid interea ad te literarum dederit, exigente forte id ipsum rei exsequendae conditione, rogo, ut confestim Hafniam ad Doctorem IOHANNEM FRANCISCUM transmittas, ut inde absque omni mora eas habere possim. Addidi etiam praeter illos 50 Witebergam mittendos, duos Thaleros, partim ut aurigae pro sua opera debitam mercedem hinc numerare possis, partim ut pro superfluo libellos quosdam Mathematicos mihi compares, quales nosti me expetere, ut Astronomica MAeSTLINI, si quae postea ad vos pervenere. Desidero etiam scriptum illud Germanicum MAeSTLINI, quod significasti ipsum edidisse contra restitutionem Calendarij Gregorianam. Cupio enim illud videre, si forte ipsius rationes in anni metis constituendis inde perspicere licuerit. Non usque adeo mihi displicet illa temporis reformatio a GREGORIO Pontifice noviter instituta: quamvis plurimi plus odio Pontificum quam veritatis amore huic contradicunt. Qua in re affectibus veritatis lumen offuscantibus nimium indulgent. Certum est scrupula illa, quae ad 6 horarum completionem deficiunt, super integros dies anni longo jam tempore Solstitia aequinoctiaque per plurimos dies anticipasse, ut non coincidant dies festi amplius cum constitutionibus primitivae Ecclesiae. Quam si rem penitius perspiciamus in Christiana Republica, nulla revera fit dierum differentia. Neque rerum externarum usus necessarius, ubi divinitus non mandantur. Vult enim vulgus contineri in officio, ordine et distributione temporum atque ceremonijs. Praestabat itaque vero propius in his accedere et cum veterum constitutionibus potius concordare, quam longius inde deviare, quamvis hac in ipsa restitutione Gregoriana non habeatur ratio exactae annuae quantitatis. Nam et haec vulgaribus Astronomicis adhuc penitus incognita est. Rogo itaque ut omnium eorum scripta in Germania, qui aliquid hoc in negotio publicârunt, sive pro sive contra, mihi transmittas, ut quomodo invicem et cum veritate consentiant, cognoscere possim. Libellum etiam Sphaericum MAeSTLINI mihi mittas, et SCALIGERUM de emendatione temporum, Gratiopolitanum etiam de Triangulis, operaque Abbatis Messanensis Siculi. Nulla enim eorum habeo. Et oro, ut mihi quam primum haec et quaecunque alia, quibus scis me delectari, illic adipisci poteris, quanticunque constent, compares, Hafniamque ad D. IOHANNEM FRANCISCUM mittas; quicquid exposueris, statim remittam, tibique quacunque in re usus postulaverit, vicissim gratificari non intermittam. Sed video chartam amplius vix sufficere, meque, dum tecum avidius loquor, epistolae metas currenti calamo transscendere. Quare nunc illi frenum inijciam: teque Deo Opt. Max. commendo. Vale et saluta meo nomine clarissimum virum D. DAVIDEM CHYTREUM et D. LEVINUM BATTUM Doctoremque PETRUM MENIUM, ceterosque amicos. De toto negotio mihi rescribe.</p><p>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished Master Heinrich Brucaeus. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>From your letter lately sent to me, dearest Master Brucaeus, I learned that you are in good health, which was most welcome to know. But that you are gradually neglecting the mathematical studies, I do not approve. For your age is not so great that it might turn you from the most delightful pursuit of these things; rather your Medicine is more profitable, according to the old saying: \"Galen gives wealth,\" and so on. And for the most part mortals, the older they grow, the more grasping they become, and the more eagerly they gape after earthly things, with the heavenly set aside. Yet that you have resolved to return somewhat to these neglected studies, and to expound the <i>Hypotheses</i> of Proclus, I praise most highly, and I would wish you to send me a copy of those <i>Hypotheses</i> of Proclus. For I have not yet been able to obtain a Latin one — although I am sufficiently sure that they, inasmuch as they rest upon Ptolemaic foundations, are by no means exactly consonant with the heaven. For we, by the most exquisite observations of the Sun's motion over several preceding years, have found that neither the Ptolemaic nor the Copernican hypothesis satisfies its appearances, and that the reckoning of the apogee and of the eccentricity is far other than either of these craftsmen presupposed: as shortly, God willing, we shall make plain to the cultivators of astronomy from very many and by no means deceptive observations. For the motion of the Sun is so exactly established for us that on every single day, when clear weather grants a sound observation, it corresponds to our calculation from our own Tables to the very arc-second, exactly — often differing very greatly from both the Alphonsine and the Copernican reckoning, so much so that this year, as the Sun entered the vernal equinox, Copernicus's reckoning fell short in its motion by half a degree, while the Alphonsine reckoning ran over by a quarter of a degree. For the Alphonsines are now a little nearer the truth than Copernicus himself, so far as the Sun's motion is concerned, although both deviate intolerably. But in the motion of the Moon the hypothesis of Copernicus is truer and more exact. And this both the frequent observations of the Moon's course around the 90th degree from rising, and its parallaxes more diligently examined by us, have many times taught us — even though these too deviate not a little. For something is wanting in the mean motions. And the proportion of the radii of each epicycle to the radius of the concentric must be set a little otherwise, and perhaps there is yet another, less perceived, inequality. For Copernicus completed his observations with too rough a craft [<i>rudiori Minerva</i>], being without great instruments wrought exquisitely out of solid metal, of which we have very many ready — both in the variety of their construction and in their magnitude and workmanship, skilfully and perfectly [made], at enormous cost and with unremitting industry — so much so that, if you were to look upon these, you would readily grant us that not even any of the ancient mathematicians, though aided by royal expense (so far as one can gather from their writings), ever had in use instruments wrought to such magnitude and accuracy for scrutinising the celestial motions. But the moderns, neither in our own age nor in the one before, applied the form and the diligence of construction needful for working these things out, so that it is no wonder the science of the stars has gradually crumbled, to such a degree that not even its [the Sun's] motion is yet exactly established, nor is the quantity of the year sure — to say nothing now of the rest. But concerning all these matters, at another time, God willing, I will communicate to you and to other learned men our observations and the demonstrations resting upon them, and I will set forth the structure and exact workmanship of the instruments which I use in scrutinising the motion of the celestial bodies to the very mark, so that it may be established that the observations made by us, on which the demonstrations are founded, are exact and liable to no deception. For it is our custom to reach the same goal of truth not with a single instrument but with several, and those fashioned in diverse ways, provided sound trust be placed in the observation. If it were convenient for you to visit us this summer, perhaps you would see here what you would seek in vain further afield, nor would you regret that journey, and you would come to me as a most welcome guest, to be received with every kind of courtesy; then we might converse and confer face to face about very many matters, and you would behold in person those things which pen and paper do not contain. You ask that I send you some instrument of the Copernican hypotheses, such as I once saw at Schreckenfuchs's, which I would gladly do if I had such a one ready — for I have never set my mind upon constructing instruments of that kind, which would only display the inventions of others, since they can easily be grasped from a bare drawing, and the case is as yet before the judge [<i>sub iudice lis est</i>] whether the matter stands so or not. Wherefore I have rather spent all my effort on building instruments by which the celestial motions might be exactly established, so that through these the inventions of our predecessors may be called to the balance and tested against the marks of truth. For to what purpose was it to spend any labour and time in illustrating a matter not yet sufficiently understood, especially when this can otherwise easily be grasped with lighter trouble? I did indeed see at Schreckenfuchs's a little machine, by which he supposed he had figured forth the hypotheses of Copernicus. But when afterwards I weighed the matter more deeply, I found that this construction is by no means adequate to the mind of Copernicus. For he supposed that by the same machine, according as it was driven in diverse ways, both the Ptolemaic and the Copernican hypotheses could be expressed — which is plainly impossible. Nor will it readily be granted that all the motions of the Copernican invention can be mechanically expressed by a single machine that corresponds to itself in all its parts, especially because of the incomparable distance of the Eighth Sphere from the orb of the Earth; even though, as regards the symmetry of the world, the Copernican speculation is far more elegant and needs fewer circles than the Ptolemaic. And through that threefold motion of the single Earth very many of the appearances in the heaven can be neatly saved, for which the many orbs and circles otherwise devised would scarcely suffice — provided the matter truly stood so, and such revolutions agreed with the Earth itself: which we, by exquisite observations, have found to stand otherwise in the two [of the] motions that Copernicus attributed to it. For that the annual motion in the great circle about the Sun is by no means present in the Earth, I have proved at the end of the past year [15]82 and the beginning of [15]83 from the parallaxes of Mars, at that time acronychal [rising at sunset], and on that account nearer the Earth, at a lesser distance than the Sun is removed from the Earth — and indeed, according to the tenets of Copernicus, nearer by about a third part — whence [there should have arisen] greater parallaxes than the Sun itself ought to have induced; whereas in fact we found them [to be] far smaller, by the most frequent and exquisite observations corresponding mutually to one another; so that on that account the whole sphere of Mars is removed further from us than the Sun itself. Wherefore that whole hypothesis is overthrown by which Copernicus strives — ingeniously indeed and neatly enough — to account for the Sun's motion and all the epicycles of the remaining planets out of the Earth's annual circuit, the foundations of Aristarchus of Samos being brought back into the light. And as for the other motion, of declination with its librations, by which Copernicus accounts for the unequal anticipation of the equinoxes and the change in the obliquity of the Ecliptic — that this too is not present in the Earth, a different and far swifter change of the equinoxes, than the reckoning of Copernicus admits even in so little a time, will assure us; and the quantity of the year, [as derived] from the observations of Regiomontanus, in no way agreeing thus far with the Copernican reckoning. To say nothing of the fact that the obliquity of the Zodiac [Signifer] is even now three arc-minutes greater than Copernicus and our predecessors in the former age noted. The cause of the error I will explain elsewhere from optical foundations. And concerning all these matters, and very many others bearing upon the restoration of the art itself and upon recalling the celestial motions exactly into numbers, I will, with God's help, set forth a fuller account. But whether that third motion, by which the daily revolution is saved, is present in the Earth and the neighbouring elements, no one would readily say: since by the same reckoning the appearances of so great a rushing can be accounted for in this [hypothesis] as in the <i>primum mobile</i>, and indeed with a lesser circuit, and therefore with a more convenient economy the sudden restoration of all the spheres of the second movable [heaven] from east to west may be saved — as I see was the opinion of the Pythagoreans and Platonists too. Yet it is likely that this swiftest motion is by no means present in the Earth, as a most heavy and most dense and opaque body, but rather in the heaven itself, whose form and the subtlety and constancy of whose matter is fitter for a perpetual and ever so swift a motion. In vain, therefore, do you ask, dearest Brucaeus, for a plainer representation before the eyes, in an instrument, of those things of which there exists in the heaven itself no real form. But they were only ingeniously devised by Copernicus to save by some reckoning the appearances of the motions. If, however, you wish to ground students in his inventions, you will easily accomplish this — so far as need be — by a flat drawing.</p><p>But there is another matter, in which I am most concerned, that I shall take up with you. I composed, some while ago, certain things about the phenomena of the three comets seen within the last seven years, and about the New Star, by which I demonstrate, from sure observations, all the motions and distances and the rest of the matters that come into consideration; and I bring forward very many other things bearing both on this contemplation and on the whole art; and at length, persuaded by the solicitations both of friends here in my homeland and of very many in Germany and England, I have resolved to publish them. But since, on account of the epidemic pestilence now long raging, it has not been safe to dispatch anything of the kind at Copenhagen among our local printers — and they are now occupied with certain other printings, so that it is not possible to complete this edition through them in good time — I have therefore resolved to set up myself, here at Uraniborg, a certain printing-house, so far as suffices for printing my own lucubrations, so that both these and other things which, God willing, I shall hereafter strive to complete and to communicate to the cultivators of astronomy, may be committed to type here with myself, by our own press; and in that way the figures of the demonstrations and the numbers and the characters, of which, as you know, there is need in this subject-matter, may be set out corrected and more surely, and that all things may be known to come forth from me correctly — since few are to be found who are practised in these studies and can duly discern and amend the errors that occur in the course of printing. I have therefore hired this typographic servant, Joachim by name, to serve me in carrying out this business, and I have now sent him off to Wittenberg, to procure for me thence the presses and all the necessaries that cannot be had here; and since it is not very safe to entrust money to him alone, who is not sufficiently known to me, I have transferred 50 joachimsthalers to the secretary of the Castle of Wittenberg, whose name is Valentinus (whose stepfather instructs the daughters of our King, and who was formerly a familiar of mine at Wittenberg), that he may lay them out there for the type and the other things pertaining to the work, as this man of ours shall have signified to be necessary. I therefore send to you, dearest Brucaeus, those 50 joachimsthalers, which I would wish you to hand over to the Wittenberg carrier as soon as he comes to you, that he may carry them, together with a letter in my name, to the said Castle-secretary Valentinus. Now this printer of mine ought to go to Wittenberg together [with the carrier], or rather to go ahead, if the carrier should not be there, and to tarry there so long until, all the necessaries for setting up the printing-house being ready, he may bring them hither by the same carrier; but if meanwhile he should send any letter to you — the very condition of carrying out the business perhaps requiring it — I ask that you forward it at once to Copenhagen, to Doctor Johannes Franciscus, so that I may have it thence without any delay. I have added too, besides those 50 to be sent to Wittenberg, two thalers, partly that you may pay out from here to the carrier the wage due for his labour, partly that with the surplus you may procure for me certain mathematical booklets, such as you know I seek — like the astronomical [works] of Maestlin, if any have lately reached you. I also want that German writing of Maestlin which you indicated he had published against the Gregorian restoration of the Calendar. For I desire to see it, in case I might perceive from it his reckonings in fixing the marks of the year. That reform of time newly instituted by Pope Gregory does not displease me so very much — although very many contradict it more out of hatred of the Popes than out of love of truth. In which matter they indulge too much in passions that darken the light of truth. It is certain that those scruples [fractions] which fall short of the completion of the 6 hours, over the whole days of the year, have for a long time now made the solstices and equinoxes fall earlier by very many days, so that the feast-days no longer coincide with the constitutions of the primitive Church. But if we look into this matter more deeply, in the Christian Commonwealth no real difference of days arises. Nor is the observance of external things necessary, where they are not divinely commanded. For the common crowd wishes to be kept to duty, order, and the distribution of times, and to ceremonies. It was therefore preferable in these matters to come nearer to the truth and rather to concord with the constitutions of the ancients than to deviate further from them — although in this very Gregorian restoration no account is taken of the exact quantity of the year. For this too is as yet utterly unknown to the common run of astronomers. I therefore ask that you forward to me the writings of all those in Germany who have published anything in this business, whether for or against, so that I may learn how they agree with one another and with the truth. Send me also the <i>Spherical</i> booklet of Maestlin, and Scaliger <i>On the Emendation of Times</i>, the Grenoble man [Gratiopolitanus] too <i>On Triangles</i>, and the works of the Sicilian Abbot of Messina. For I have none of them. And I beg that, as soon as you can obtain them there, you procure for me these and whatever else you know delights me, at whatever they cost, and send them to Copenhagen to Master Johannes Franciscus; whatever you lay out, I will at once repay, nor will I fail to gratify you in return in whatever matter your need shall require. But I see that the paper now scarcely suffices any longer, and that I, while I talk with you too eagerly, am overstepping the bounds of a letter with a running pen. Wherefore I will now put a bridle on it: and I commend you to God, Best and Greatest. Farewell, and greet in my name the most distinguished man Master David Chytraeus and Master Levinus Battus and Doctor Petrus Menius, and the rest of my friends. Write back to me about the whole business.</p><p>T.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, 1530/31–1593), professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, where Tycho had studied; a senior scholarly friend. The protocol's §5.6 person register lists him among Tycho's German-academic correspondents (Rostock).</li><li><b>Date / place.</b> The source carries <b>no dateline and no place</b> — it ends simply \"T. TYCHO BRAHE.\" The dataset `label` is <i>s.l.</i> (<i>sine loco</i>, \"without place\"), and the slug encodes only the year, 1584 (the day-component is `0000`). The header therefore asserts only \"[1584]\" with the bracketed note that the source supplies no day-date, rather than manufacturing one. The standard scholarly dating is <b>spring 1584</b> (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 80; cf. Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>, who place it in spring 1584). That secondary date is not in the source text and so is kept out of the header, recorded here instead. No O.S./N.S. tag is given because the source supplies no day to qualify; Tycho in Denmark wrote Old Style.</li><li><b>Significance.</b> This is the much-cited letter in which Tycho first reports to a colleague that the <b>Mars parallax</b> measured at the 1582/83 opposition was far smaller than the Copernican annual orbit requires, which he takes to overthrow the Earth's annual motion. The passage is the documentary anchor for the genesis of the Tychonic system; the translation preserves the empirical, still-tentative register (he is reporting a result and its inference, not yet announcing a finished world-system).</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>Salutem plurimam</i> (dicit), \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Clarissimo D. D. Henrico Brucaeo</i>: <i>D. D.</i> = <i>Domino Doctori</i>, \"to Master Doctor\"; rendered \"to the most distinguished Master Heinrich Brucaeus.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms</b> (per §5.1): <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>apogaeum</i>/<i>eccentricitas</i> → apogee/eccentricity; <i>motus</i> → motion; <i>hypothesis</i> → hypothesis (kept); <i>epicyclus</i> → epicycle; <i>concentricus</i> → concentric; <i>medii motus</i> → mean motions; <i>octava sphaera</i> → Eighth Sphere (the fixed stars); <i>primum mobile</i> / <i>secundum mobile</i> → kept Latin (the prime mover / second movable heaven of the scholastic cosmos); <i>obliquitas Eclipticae</i> / <i>obliquatio Signiferi</i> → obliquity of the Ecliptic / of the Zodiac (<i>Signifer</i>, \"the sign-bearer,\" = the zodiac); <i>aequinoctiorum anticipatio</i> → anticipation (precession) of the equinoxes; <i>anni quantitas</i> → the quantity (length) of the year; <i>acronychius</i> → acronychal (rising at sunset, i.e. at opposition) — glossed in brackets.</li><li><b>Measurement idiom.</b> <i>in ipso scrupulo ad amussim</i> — \"to the very arc-second, exactly\"; <i>scrupulum</i> here = arc-second / minute fraction (the period term for a sexagesimal fraction of a degree); <i>ad amussim</i> (\"to the carpenter's line\") = \"to the very mark / exactly,\" a recurring Tychonic tag for instrumental precision, kept consistent. <i>semisse partis</i> = half a degree (<i>pars</i> = degree); <i>partis quadrante</i> = a quarter of a degree; <i>trinis scrupulis</i> = three arc-minutes. <i>scrupula illa, quae ad 6 horarum completionem deficiunt</i> (in the calendar passage) = the fractional residue short of the full 6 hours (the ¼-day surplus of the tropical year over 365 days) — \"scruples/fractions,\" kept as the period term.</li><li><b><i>rudiori Minerva</b></i> — proverbial (\"with rougher Minerva,\" i.e. with cruder skill/equipment); kept and glossed \"with too rough a craft.\"</li><li><b><i>sub iudice lis est</b></i> — Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 78: \"the case is [still] before the judge,\" i.e. the matter is unsettled; retained as the embedded tag with a gloss.</li><li><b>Copernican motions.</b> Tycho counts the three Copernican terrestrial motions: (1) the <b>annual</b> motion in the <i>circulus magnus</i> about the Sun; (2) the <b>motion of declination</b> with its <i>librations</i> (Copernicus's mechanism for variable precession and changing obliquity); (3) the <b>daily</b> rotation. The translation keeps his careful gradation: he holds (1) <b>disproved</b> by the Mars parallax, (2) contradicted by the equinox/year evidence, and (3) merely <b>undecided</b> (\"no one would readily say\") — leaning to a moving heaven on physical grounds. No hedge is sharpened: <i>non facile quis dixerit</i>, <i>verisimile tamen est</i> are rendered \"no one would readily say,\" \"yet it is likely.\"</li><li><b><i>Aristarchus of Samos</b></i> — <i>Aristarchi Samii</i>: the ancient heliocentrist whose \"foundations\" Tycho says Copernicus revived.</li><li><b>Persons named in the business section.</b></li><li><i>Proclus</i> — the Neoplatonist; his <i>Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum</i> (\"Hypotheses [of the astronomers]\"), which Brucaeus meant to expound; Tycho wants a Latin copy.</li><li><i>Schreckenfuxius</i> = <b>Erasmus Oswald Schreckenfuchs</b> (1511–1579), mathematician (Freiburg/Tübingen); Tycho recalls a planetary model-machine he saw at his house.</li><li><i>Maestlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher); Tycho asks for his astronomical works, his <i>Sphaera</i> booklet, and his German tract against the Gregorian calendar.</li><li><i>Scaliger de emendatione temporum</i> = <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b>, <i>De emendatione temporum</i> (1583).</li><li><i>Gratiopolitanus de Triangulis</i> = \"the Grenoble man on Triangles\" — most likely <b>Bartholomaeus Pitiscus</b> is anachronistic here (1595); the period reference is to a trigonometric work by a writer styled <i>Gratiopolitanus</i> (of Grenoble, <i>Gratianopolis</i>). Identity not certain from the text; <b>flagged</b> rather than asserted.</li><li><i>Abbas Messanensis Siculus</i> = \"the Sicilian Abbot of Messina\" — Tycho wants his works; the precise figure is not specified in the letter and is <b>left unidentified</b> rather than guessed.</li><li><i>Ioachimus</i> — the hired <b>printer (\"typographic servant\")</b> sent to Wittenberg to procure the press; the Uraniborg printing-office Tycho is here founding (1584) would produce <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> and the <i>Epistolae</i>.</li><li><i>Valentinus</i> — the <b>secretary (scriba) of Wittenberg Castle</b>, a former Wittenberg acquaintance, whose stepfather tutored the Danish king's daughters; entrusted with the 50 joachimsthalers.</li><li><i>Iohannes Franciscus</i> — <b>Johannes Franciscus</b> (Hans Frandsen / Johannes Franciscus Ripensis), Tycho's Copenhagen forwarding contact.</li><li><i>David Chytraeus</i> (1530–1600), Rostock theologian-historian; <i>Levinus Battus</i> (Levinus Battus, Rostock physician); <i>Petrus Menius</i> — Rostock friends to be greeted.</li><li><b>Coinage.</b> <i>Ioachimici</i> → <b>joachimsthalers</b> (the Joachimsthal silver coin, the \"thaler\"); <i>Thaleri</i> → <b>thalers</b> (kept distinct as the source distinguishes the 50 <i>Ioachimici</i> from the additional 2 <i>Thaleri</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar passage.</b> Tycho's measured approval of the <b>Gregorian calendar reform</b> (1582) is notable for a Lutheran: he separates the astronomical correctness of dropping the accumulated surplus from confessional opposition (\"more out of hatred of the Popes than love of truth\"), while noting the reform still does not embody the <i>exact</i> length of the year. No date is converted; the discussion is doctrinal-astronomical, not a dateline.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The pivotal scientific clauses turn on negations, rendered count-for-count without addition or removal: <i>Motum … annuum … Terrae <b>nequaquam</b> adesse</i> (\"the annual motion is <b>by no means</b> present in the Earth\"); <i>cum tamen longe minores fuisse</i> — the observed parallaxes were <i>far smaller</i>, <b>not</b> greater, than required; <i>neque ipsi Terrae adesse</i> (the declination-motion likewise <b>not</b> present in the Earth); <i>non facile quis dixerit</i> (of the daily motion, \"<b>no one would readily say</b>\" — left open, not denied). These are the clauses whose sense the whole letter rests on, and the polarity is preserved exactly.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> As-printed early-modern forms retained in the Source block: <i>BRUCAeO/BRUCAeE</i> (ae-ligature), <i>ijs/alijs/proprijs</i> (ij for ii), <i>afpiceres</i> and <i>cornpendio</i> (printer's <i>f</i>-for-<i>s</i> and <i>rn</i>-for-<i>m</i> misreadings in the source transcription) — these last two are left <b>as printed</b> in the Source block; read <i>aspiceres</i> (\"you would behold\") and <i>compendio</i> (\"economy\") respectively, which the translation follows.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Five word-level corrections collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.78-80: struehirae emended to structurae (p.78); the inserted first name Henrico removed from two body addresses to Brucaeus where Dreyer prints the surname alone (p.78, p.80/81), with the corresponding Heinrich removed from the English; exacto corrected to exacte (p.79); and the inserted pronoun ea removed before videres (p.79), with the English clause those things which collapsed to what accordingly.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Dreyer's reading in the sentence sending the joachimsthalers to Brucaeus a second time: removed the duplicated first name 'Henrico'/'Heinrich' (not present in Dreyer's mid-letter apostrophe, which repeats only 'BRUCAEE') and restored the omitted sum '50' before 'illos Ioachimicos' / 'those ... joachimsthalers' (Dreyer, TBOO VII, p. 81).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin and the scientific argument (the Mars-parallax disproof of terrestrial annual motion; the three-motions gradation; the Gregorian-calendar discussion) are rendered securely and the negations verified count-for-count. Residual uncertainties, flagged not smoothed: (1) the dateline is absent in the source, so the year stands bracketed and the \"spring 1584\" scholarly date is confined to the apparatus; (2) two book-references — <i>Gratiopolitanus de Triangulis</i> and the <i>Abbas Messanensis Siculus</i> — are not securely identifiable from the text and are left unidentified rather than guessed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.78-83 (all 6 slice pages, read in chunks of 4+2, then re-verified pp.78-79 individually) including the opening/closing lines and every proper name, number, sum, and place-name. The English translation is faithful throughout — no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found relative to source_text. However source_text itself departs from Dreyer's printed Latin in five places (one corrupted word, one letter-swap, one added pronoun, two added instances of \"HENRICO\" in body addresses). None change the sense a scholar would take away (verdict MINOR), but any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C per the rubric.</p><p><b>5 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). source_text's \"struehirae\" is not a Latin word; Dreyer prints \"structurae\" (of construction). The English translation correctly conveys \"construction\" regardless, so no translation-level fault, but our Latin transcription is corrupted against the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>tum varietate ftructuræ, tum magnitudinis (TBOO VII p.78, line ~40, split \"ftruc-/turæ\" across the line)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text inserts the first name \"HENRICO\" into a body address where Dreyer's printed text uses only \"D.D. BRUCAEE\"; the heading alone carries the full name in Dreyer. Translation is faithful to source_text but source_text departs from the edition. Dreyer prints: <i>chariſſime D. D. BRUCÆE, intellexi (TBOO VII p.78, line 2 — no \"HENRICO\" in the body address, only in the heading \"D.D. HENRICO BRUCAEO\")</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text reads the adverb as \"exacto\"; Dreyer prints \"exacte\" (exactly). Single-letter corruption; translation renders the correct sense regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>adeo ut ipfius motus nondum exacte conftet (TBOO VII p.79, line 8)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text inserts the pronoun \"ea\" (\"those things\") which is not present in Dreyer's printed text; the English's \"those things\" reflects the added word. Dreyer prints: <i>forte hîc videres, quæ fruftra remotius quæreres (TBOO VII p.79, line 18-19 — no \"ea\")</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Second instance of the same pattern as above: source_text adds \"HENRICO\" to a body address where Dreyer prints the surname alone. Dreyer prints: <i>Fruftra itaque expetis, chariſſime BRUCÆE, eorum in organo (TBOO VII p.80/81 page-break — no \"HENRICO\")</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All five listed defects were verified directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.78-81, the full four-page chunk on which they all occur). A third, unlisted instance of the same HENRICO BRUCAeE pattern (Mitto itaque, charissime HENRICO BRUCAeE, tibi illos Ioachimicos) was noticed later in the Latin text but left untouched, since it is not among the defects supplied and the prime rule confines repair strictly to listed defect sites. Pages 82-83 of the slice were not needed since all five defects fall within pp.78-81, and were not read.</p><p><b>Collated &amp; repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the complete letter against Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 78-82 (the letter runs the full length of that span, within the given window pp. 75-85; salutation on p.78 through signature on p.82 both confirmed against page images, never the OCR text layer). All proper names, dates, and sums checked (50 joachimsthalers x2, 2 thalers, comet septennium, 1582/83, 6-hour calendar fraction, three arc-minutes obliquity, named correspondents/authors SCHRECKENFUXIUM, IOACHIMUM, VALENTINUS, IOHANNEM FRANCISCUM, MAESTLINI x3, SCALIGERUM, DAVIDEM CHYTREUM, LEVINUM BATTUM, PETRUM MENIUM) against the two Wittenberg-money sentences, where a duplicated 'Henrico' and a dropped '50' were found and repaired. No other discrepancy found; nothing remains uncertain.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15781204001", "next": "brahe-t_15840612001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15781204001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840612001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Disapproves of Brucaeus's neglect of mathematical studies, praises his plan to expound Proclus's Hypotheses, and requests a copy, while noting those Hypotheses rest on imperfect Ptolemaic foundations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15840000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15840216001", "d": "1584-02-16", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Uraniborg, 16 February 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO MAGISTRO ANDREAe SEVERINI VELLEIO AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.<br>SALUTEM.</p><p>BINAS a te Clarissime M. ANDREA, ex quo e Cymbria vestra domum reversus sum, accepi literas, utrisque negotium Typographi illius tractas; respondissem dudum tuae petitioni, si hyemis importunitas non obstitisset, literasque ad te transmittendi prius fuisset oblata occasio. Scias itaque me illum Holsaticum Typographum, de quo scribis, non multopere expetere. Quod si sponte huc transmigrare velit, eius conatus, quantum in me est, promovebo. Conduxi autem alium iuvenem non uxoratum, qui mihi in impressoria arte exsequenda inserviet, et Francofurtum ad Nundinas pecuniam pro stylo impressorio diversi generis mihi coëmendo nunc transmitto; propriamque officinulam hîc instruere conabor, quae quotannis mihi usui emolumentoque erit. Si quid tu postmodo prelo destinatum absolveris hucque transmiseris, meis impensis excudetur, si tibi ita visum fuerit. Est autem Typographicus servus, quem conduxi, admodum bene in arte peritus et laborator diligens. Nec ex re mea fuit uxoratum illum cum tota sua familia maiore dispendio quam compendio sustentare. Si post festum Paschatis nos denuo hîc inviseris, veluti discedens ultimo te singulis annis facturum promisisti (testante idipsum graphica tua parietis nostrae inscriptione REVORTAR 83/84/85 oculis nostris quotidie obvia) rem feceris mihi longe gratissimam, habeo enim quaedam, de quibus tecum coram agere plurimum exoptarem. Ego enim vix ob negotiorum impendentium multitudinem Cymbriam hac instante aestate revisere potuero. Tu itaque, si cum coniuge itineris socia (modo id possibile erit) mihi adfueris, hospes advenies exoptatissimus. Tuaque causa illud quicquid est itineris viceversa suscipiam, si quando opus fuerit. Vale et saluta METTAM tuam eiusque Patrem et Matrem, Magistrum IACOBUM et amicos omnes ceteros.</p><p>Iterum feliciter vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 16. Februarij Anno 1584.<br>T.<br>TYCHO BRAHE manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 77; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Negotium illud de historia CHRISTIANI III, cum ad ABSADONEM GYLDENSTIERN venero, facile, quasi nobis id minime factitantibus, expediam, tibique postmodo librum vel eius copiam transmittam. Litteras et pixidem ALBERTO FRISIO destinatam tradi procura.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Anders Sørensen Vedel, his most dearly beloved friend. Greeting.</b></p><p>Two letters from you, most renowned Master Anders, I have received since I returned home from your Cimbria, and in both you treat of the business of that typographer; I should have answered your request long ago, had the harshness of winter not stood in the way, and had an opportunity of conveying a letter to you been offered sooner. Know, then, that I do not greatly want that Holstein typographer of whom you write. But should he be willing of his own accord to remove hither, I shall further his endeavours so far as lies in me. I have, however, engaged another young man, unmarried, who will serve me in carrying out the printer's art; and I am now sending money to Frankfurt to the Fair, to buy up for myself printing-type [<i>stylus impressorius</i>] of various kinds; and I shall try to set up here a little workshop of my own, which will be of use and profit to me year by year. If hereafter you finish anything intended for the press and send it hither, it shall be printed at my expense, if so it seem good to you. The printer's servant whom I have engaged is, moreover, very well skilled in the art and a diligent workman. Nor was it to my advantage to maintain that married man with his whole household at greater cost than profit. If, after the feast of Easter, you visit us again here — as at your last departure you promised you would do every year (the very thing being witnessed by your own inscription drawn on our wall, REVORTAR 83/84/85, daily before our eyes) — you will do me a thing by far most welcome, for I have certain matters which I should most greatly desire to deal with in your presence. For I shall scarcely be able, on account of the multitude of pressing affairs, to revisit Cimbria this coming summer. You, therefore, if (provided it be possible) you are with me together with your wife as companion of the journey, will come as a guest most longed-for. And for your sake I shall undertake that journey, whatever it amounts to, in turn, should there ever be need. Farewell, and greet your Mette and her father and mother, Master Jacob, and all your other friends.</p><p>Again, happily, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 16th day of February, in the year 1584.<br>T[ycho].<br>TYCHO BRAHE, in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> That business of the history of Christian III, when I shall have come to Absalon Gyldenstierne, I shall easily dispatch — as though we were by no means busying ourselves with it — and shall afterwards convey to you the book, or a copy of it. See that the letter and the little box [pyxis] destined for Albert Friis be delivered.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Magister Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), Tycho's former preceptor and lifelong friend, the Danish historian and balladeer; appointed Royal Historiographer in 1584. He lived at Ribe in Jutland, where his residence Liljebjerg (\"Lily Hill\") — with its book and manuscript collections, gardens, and (from 1585) its own printing press — became known as a \"Little Uraniborg.\" The letter shows the two men setting up their presses in the same period.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-mansion on the island of Hven (protocol §5.7). Tycho established his own printing press at Uraniborg in 1584, the very year of this letter (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>); the letter documents its provisioning.</li><li><b><i>Cymbria / Cymbriam</b></i> — <b>Cimbria</b>, the humanist Latin name for <b>Jutland</b> (the Cimbric peninsula); \"your Cimbria\" = Vedel's home region, where he resided at Ribe. Kept and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Typographus / Typographicus servus</b></i> — \"typographer / printer's servant,\" i.e. the pressman or compositor Tycho needed to operate his press. The <i>Holsaticus Typographus</i> is an (unnamed) <b>printer from Holstein</b> whom Vedel had recommended; Tycho declines to seek him out, having already hired an unmarried young man.</li><li><b><i>stylus impressorius</b></i> — literally \"printing stylus,\" here the standard period term for <b>printing-type (founts of type)</b>; Tycho is buying type \"of various kinds\" (<i>diversi generis</i>, i.e. several founts/sizes) at the Frankfurt book fair. Rendered \"printing-type\"; the Latin flagged.</li><li><b><i>Francofurtum ad Nundinas</b></i> — \"to <b>Frankfurt</b> to the Fair,\" i.e. the <b>Frankfurt book fair</b> (the <i>Frankfurter Messe</i>), the great European entrepôt for type, books, and printing materials. <i>Nundinae</i> = the (market-)fair.</li><li><b><i>officinulam</b></i> — diminutive of <i>officina</i>, \"a little workshop\"; here the small printing-office Tycho means to establish. Rendered \"a little workshop of my own.\"</li><li><b><i>prelo destinatum … excudetur</b></i> — \"intended for the press … shall be printed\": <i>prelum</i> = the press; <i>excudere</i> = to print/strike off. Tycho offers to print at his own expense any work Vedel prepares — an offer reflecting the patronage economics of owning a press.</li><li><b><i>maiore dispendio quam compendio</b></i> — \"at greater cost than profit,\" a rhetorical jingle (<i>dispendium</i> / <i>compendium</i>) explaining why a married pressman with a whole household was uneconomic; the antithesis is preserved.</li><li><b><i>graphica … parietis nostrae inscriptione REVORTAR 83/84/85</b></i> — \"your own inscription drawn on our wall, REVORTAR 83/84/85\": at his departures Vedel had written on a wall at Uraniborg the Latin <i>REVORTAR</i> (\"I shall return,\" variant of <i>revertar</i>) with the successive years 1583 / 1584 / 1585, as a standing pledge to return yearly. <i>graphica … inscriptio</i> = a drawn/written inscription; rendered literally. The years are reproduced exactly as transcribed (83/84/85); the form <i>REVORTAR</i> (for <i>revertar</i>) is kept as written, not corrected.</li><li><b><i>festum Paschatis</b></i> — \"the feast of <b>Easter</b>\"; the proposed time for Vedel's visit.</li><li><b>Persons greeted.</b> <i>METTA tua</i> = <b>Mette Hansdatter</b>, Vedel's wife (married 1581), daughter of <b>Hans Laugesen</b>, Bishop of Ribe; \"her father and mother\" are the bishop and his wife. <i>Magister Iacobus</i> = a \"Master Jacob\" of Vedel's Ribe circle (not further specified in the text). Names kept in their source forms with identification.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>T. … TYCHO BRAHE manu propria</i> — \"T[ycho]. … Tycho Brahe, in his own hand,\" the autograph subscription formula (<i>manu propria</i> = with his own hand); preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 16. Februarij Anno 1584</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, the 16th day of February, in the year 1584\" (Old Style; Denmark held the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline as written agrees with the catalogue label (16 February 1584); no discrepancy. No date is silently converted.</li><li><b>Edition / witness note.</b> Text of record is Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i>; the MS witness recorded in the dataset is a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Tycho Brahe's private archive at the Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen — i.e. an apograph, not the autograph dispatched to Vedel.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VII p.77 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The Latin is straightforward humanist epistolary prose; technical terms (<i>stylus impressorius</i>, <i>officinula</i>, <i>prelum</i>) and the <i>REVORTAR</i> wall-inscription are documented by the surrounding press-establishment context and rendered without smoothing. The unidentified \"Master Jacob\" is named, not guessed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read both slice pages visually. The letter (Tycho to Anders Sorensen Vedel, headed 'CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO MAGISTRO ANDREAE SEVERINI VELLEIO...') plus its postscript are both on p.77, citation '[Ex apographo in codice Archivi Regni Daniae: Arch. priv. Tych. Brah.]'. Collated the full Latin word-for-word against Dreyer, including the postscript that the source_text notes was previously dropped and has since been restored: Dreyer confirms the postscript reads exactly as now given, naming Christiani III, Absalonem Gyldenstiern, and Alberto Frisio, matching source_text precisely. Checked all other names/numbers/dates: Holsaticum Typographum, Francofurtum ad Nundinas, the wall inscription 'REVORTAR 83/84/85', Mettam/Patrem/Matrem/Magistrum Iacobum, closing date '16. Februarij Anno 1584'. English translation faithful throughout, no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found; the restored postscript is accurately rendered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15790815001", "next": "brahe-t_15851111001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Declines the Holstein typographer Vedel proposed, reports engaging another printer and ordering type at the Frankfurt Fair to establish a press at Uraniborg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15840216001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15840608001", "d": "1584-06-08", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Major", "ck": "Johannes Major", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Johannes Major → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 8 June 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PRISCAe AC VERAe NOBILITATIS INSIGNISQUE ERUDITIONIS AC VIRTUTIS VIRO DN. TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI, DOMINO INSULAe HUENAe, DN. ET AMICO SUO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>LITERAe tuae, generose Dn. TYCHO, mihi duabus de causis fuerunt gratissimae, cum quod familiaritatem inter nos hîc Augustae conflatam, literarum beneficio fulcire non dedignatus es, tum quod scirem, eum qui dicebatur obijsse anno 82, adhuc in vivis esse et hac aura inter superstites felicissime frui. Qua felicitate ut quam diutissime vel propter Rempubl. literariam ad artes Mathematicas restaurandas temporum iniuria collapsas, fruaris, Deum Opt. Max. toto pectore oro. Porro ad petitionem tuam quod attinet de emendis libris, feci quod potui. Inprimis autem mihi curae fuit SCALIGERI de emendatione temporum, qui in fine appendicem habet complectentem iudicium, quo Gregorianum seu Lilianum, ut ipse vocat, Calendarium in universum improbat, uti videbis. Huic adiunxi aliorum in Germania editorum scripta quotquot habere potui, quae in unum volumen sunt coniecta et Heidelbergae edita post. Edidit et IACOBUS HEERBRANDUS Theologus Tubingensis Theses contra Calendarium, quibus nostrorum Theologorum sententiam de non recipiendo Calendario comprobavit, sed a Senatu sunt prohibitae et explosae, ut sic loquar. Superioribus scriptis adiunxi HAGECIJ Responsionem ad HANNIBALEM et GRAMINAeUM de Stella Nova, quae ante triennium per me ad te non missa esse recordor. Proinde tibi non ingrata futura auguror. Librum de Triangulis, quem inprimis desiderare videris, venalem non reperi. Precium singulorum, quorum undecim in peculiari scheda his literis inclusa notatum invenies, pro quibus affinis meus mihi, ut voluisti, satisfecit. WERNERI libros cum de motu octavae Sphaerae, tum de Observationibus stellarum fixarum, si aliquando in vetere quadam Bibliotheca invenero, faciam, ut me tui non immemorem fuisse intelligas. Reperi et alios libros Astronomicos in Catalogis WILLERIANIS, partim de Horologijs, partim de Astrologia, h. e. illa divinatrice arte tractantes, qui etsi plerique in promptu non erant, et a tuis studijs mihi quodammodo alieni videantur esse, singulorum tamen titulos consignare volui, precio plerisque adiecto. Si igitur ex his quidam tibi arridens tuum erit semestre spacium vel circiter ante me certiorem facere, ut si WILLERUS eius generis libros in promtu non habeat, eos tamen interea temporis huc transferendos curare possit. Qua in parte tibi meum studium et operam polliceor. Doctoris AMBROSIJ libri ante triennium WELSERO mortuo ab haeredibus sunt distracti et venditi.</p><p>Novarum rerum fere nihil habeo, nisi quod inter Senatores civitatis nostrae propter Calendarium Gregorianum magna animorum distractio fuerit. Maior enim pars non tam Pontifici] quam nostri contenderunt, Calendarium propter vicinos Bavaros et Episcopum Augustanum recipiendum esse tanquam politicam causam, ut correspondentia cum vicinis in commercijs, iudicijs et contractibus servari possit. Pauci ex nostris, tres praesertim Aediles Curules asserentes Theologorum nomine, Calendarium non esse mere politicum negotium, sed etiam Theologicum, eo quod a Pontifice sit ortum et sub poena excommunicationis recipiendum mandetur, minime igitur nostris Civibus et Ecclesijs obtrudendum esse, provocantes ad Cameram Imperialem mense Martio 1583. Tandem mense Maio An. 84 est lata sententia contra Aediles et adhaerentes, quapropter die 18. publicatur sententia, et ijdem 4 Domini iubentur domi suae se continere, donec aliud quid de illis statuatur. Die 25. Maij, circa meridiem Praefectus Urbis, der Stadtvogt, iussu Senatus Doctorem MYLIUM Augustanum, nostrum primarium Theologum ex urbe in pilento educere conatur. Sed quid fit? Populus hoc audito accurrit et aurigam, quo minus progrediatur, strictis gladijs adoritur, MYLIUMQUE e pilento vi extractum in aedibus Pistoris prope portam Geggingensem habitantis abscondit, neglectis militibus custodiam in porta agentibus. Mox confluunt Cives armati vociferantes: nostrum Theologum cupimus. Dum haec aguntur, Senatus ceteros Theologos in Curiam convocat, tractaturus cum illis de Calendario recipiendo; quod tum constanter denegârunt. Altero die post eliguntur ex singulis ordinibus, Patricio, Mercatorio et Plebeio 6 viri, qui de pace et tumultu sedando cogitent. Mox ultimo Maij advolant legati cum Wirtenbergici, tum Ulmenses, qui litem ita composuerunt, ut ex scripto vel publicatione in fasciculum tuum coniecta videre est. Non dubito, quin diversorum diversa sint futura iudicia de Theologis nostris. Sed non solum Legati, Iurisconsulti etc. verum etiam Tubingenses ipsi recipiendi Calendarij authores fuerunt, respicientes praesens periculum, et desertionem Ecclesiae, salvo verbo et libertate Ecclesiae retenta. Sed de statu huius urbis nostrae fortasse prolixius, quam par est. Id saltem a te, si non nimis audax viderer, expeterem, ut tuo nos iudicio de hoc Calendario neoterico informares ac firmares, an videlicet id priori praestet ac coelo rectius congruat, nec ne. Id enim te unicum efficere posse, utpote qui luminarium motibus sedulo invigiles, persuasum habemus. Et sunt vota non nostra saltem, sed plurium. Quod superest, mi TYCHO, Deum Opt. Max. precor, ut tibi largiatur et vires animi et corporis, qui organa et opera in gratiam Matheseos studiosis inchoata absolvere, feliciterque ad finem perducere queas, quo sane nihil neque cognitu iucundius neque gratius accidere poterit.</p><p>Vale feliciter.<br>Datae Augustae Rheticae vulgo Vindelicorum postridie Pentecostes CIↃ IↃ LXXXIV.<br>IOHAN. MAIOR.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To a man of ancient and true nobility and of distinguished learning and virtue, Master Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, lord of the island of Hven, his lord and friend much to be respected.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Your letter, noble Master Tycho, was most welcome to me for two reasons: both because you did not disdain to shore up, by the kindness of a letter, the friendship struck up between us here at Augsburg, and because I [thereby] learned that the man who was said to have died in the year [15]82 is yet among the living and most happily enjoys this air among those who survive. That you may enjoy that happiness as long as possible — if only for the sake of the republic of letters, toward restoring the mathematical arts that have collapsed through the injury of the times — I beg God, Best and Greatest, with my whole heart. Further, as concerns your request about the books to be bought, I have done what I could. First of all my care was for Scaliger's <i>On the Emendation of Times</i>, which at the end has an appendix containing a judgement whereby he wholly condemns the Gregorian, or as he himself calls it the Lilian, Calendar, as you will see. To this I have added the writings of others published in Germany, as many as I could obtain, which have been thrown together into a single volume and afterwards published at Heidelberg. Jacob Heerbrand, the Tübingen theologian, also published <i>Theses against the Calendar</i>, in which he confirmed our theologians' opinion about not accepting the Calendar; but they were banned by the Senate and hooted down, so to speak. To the foregoing writings I have added Hájek's <i>Reply to Hannibal and Graminaeus concerning the New Star</i>, which, I recall, was not sent to you by me three years ago. Accordingly I forecast it will not be unwelcome to you. The book <i>On Triangles</i>, which you seem especially to want, I have not found for sale. The price of each item — of which you will find eleven noted on a separate slip enclosed with this letter — [is set out], for which my kinsman has, as you wished, made satisfaction to me. As for Werner's books, both <i>On the Motion of the Eighth Sphere</i> and <i>On the Observations of the Fixed Stars</i>, if at some time I find them in some old library, I will see to it, so that you may understand I have not been unmindful of you. I have found also other astronomical books in the Willer catalogues, treating partly of timepieces, partly of astrology — that is, of that divinatory art — which, although most were not readily at hand, and may seem to me somewhat foreign to your studies, I nevertheless wished to record the titles of each, with the price added in most cases. If, therefore, some one of these takes your fancy, it will be for you to inform me about a half-year, or thereabouts, in advance, so that, should Willer not have books of that kind ready to hand, he may yet be able in the meantime to arrange for them to be brought here. In that matter I promise you my zeal and effort. Doctor Ambrosius's books were, three years ago, on Welser's death, dispersed and sold off by the heirs.</p><p>Of new matters I have almost nothing, except that among the senators of our city there has been a great clash of minds over the Gregorian Calendar. For the greater part — not so much [out of regard for] the Pope as our own [men] — contended that the Calendar was to be accepted, on account of the neighbouring Bavarians and the Bishop of Augsburg, as a political matter, so that correspondence with the neighbours in commerce, lawsuits, and contracts could be maintained. A few of our own — three Aediles Curule especially, asserting in the theologians' name that the Calendar was not a merely political business but also a theological one, in that it had arisen from the Pope and was being ordered to be accepted under pain of excommunication, and therefore by no means to be forced upon our citizens and churches — appealed to the Imperial [Chamber of Justice] in the month of March 1583. At length in the month of May, in the year [15]84, sentence was passed against the Aediles and their adherents; wherefore on the 18th day the sentence is published, and those same 4 gentlemen are ordered to confine themselves to their houses until something else be decided about them. On the 25th day of May, about noon, the Prefect of the City — <i>der Stadtvogt</i> — by order of the Senate attempts to lead Doctor Mylius of Augsburg, our chief theologian, out of the city in a carriage. But what happens? The people, on hearing this, run up and, that he may go no further, set upon the driver with drawn swords, and, Mylius having been dragged out of the carriage by force, hide him in the house of a baker living near the Göggingen gate, the soldiers keeping guard at the gate being disregarded. Soon the citizens flock together in arms, crying out: \"We want our theologian.\" While these things are going on, the Senate summons the rest of the theologians to the Town Hall, intending to treat with them about accepting the Calendar; which they then steadfastly refused. The day after, there are chosen from each of the orders — Patrician, Mercantile, and Plebeian — 6 men, to consider the settling of the peace and the tumult. Soon, on the last [day] of May, envoys hasten in, both from Württemberg and from Ulm, who composed the dispute in such a way as may be seen from the written notice, or publication, thrown into your packet. I do not doubt that different men's judgements of our theologians will be different. But not only the envoys, jurists, etc., but even the Tübingen men themselves were authors of accepting the Calendar, looking to the present danger and the desertion of the Church — the Word and the liberty of the Church being kept safe. But [I write] of the state of this our city at greater length, perhaps, than is fitting. This at least I would ask of you, if I should not seem too bold: that you inform and confirm us, by your judgement, about this newfangled Calendar — namely whether it is better than the former one and agrees more rightly with the heaven, or not. For we are persuaded that you alone can settle this, inasmuch as you watch diligently over the motions of the luminaries. And these are the wishes not of us alone, but of many. For what remains, my Tycho, I pray God, Best and Greatest, to bestow on you strength both of mind and of body, that you may be able to complete the instruments and works begun for the benefit of those devoted to mathematics, and bring them happily to their end — than which, in truth, nothing more delightful or more welcome to know could befall.</p><p>Fare you happily well.<br>Given at Augsburg of Raetia, commonly [Augsburg] of the Vindelici, the day after Pentecost, 1584.<br>Johannes Major.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohan. Maior</i> = <b>Johannes Major</b> (Hans Mair), an Augsburg correspondent and book-agent for Tycho in the early 1580s; he procures and prices books from the Augsburg trade (notably the Willer catalogues) on Tycho's commission. (Not to be confused with the Wittenberg poet Georg Major or the earlier theologian.) The MS is an Afskrift (copy) in the same Vienna codex (cod. Vindob. lat. 10686) that preserves Tycho's incoming Augsburg correspondence.</li><li><b>Address &amp; opening.</b> <i>Ottonides</i> — \"son of Otte,\" the Greek-style patronymic for <b>Otte Brahe</b>, Tycho's father (cf. the Wolf letter, 1571). <i>Dominus insulae Huenae</i> = \"lord of the island of <b>Hven</b>,\" Tycho's seat (Uraniborg). <i>S.</i> = <i>Salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>\"the man said to have died in [15]82.\"</b> A rumour of Tycho's death had reached Augsburg; Major expresses relief at finding him alive. Reported, not glossed away.</li><li><b>Books named.</b></li><li><i>Scaliger,</i> De emendatione temporum <i>(Paris, 1583)</i> — Joseph Justus Scaliger's chronological work; Major flags its closing appendix condemning the Gregorian (\"Lilian\") calendar. <i>Lilianum</i> after <b>Luigi Lilio (Aloysius Lilius)</b>, the calendar's designer.</li><li><i>Jacob Heerbrand,</i> Theses contra Calendarium — the Tübingen Lutheran theologian's anti-Gregorian theses.</li><li><i>Hájek,</i> Responsio ad Hannibalem et Graminaeum de Stella Nova — <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius)</b>, the Bohemian astronomer, replying to <b>Annibale Raimondo</b> (\"Hannibal\") and <b>Theodor Graminaeus</b> on the new star of 1572. <i>ante triennium</i> = \"three years ago.\"</li><li><i>Liber de Triangulis</i> — a \"book <i>On Triangles</i>,\" unfound for sale; most likely <b>Regiomontanus,</b> De triangulis omnimodis, though the bare title is not decisive (so kept untranslated-in-title and not pinned to an author).</li><li><i>Werner,</i> De motu octavae sphaerae <i>and</i> De observationibus stellarum fixarum — <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg.</li><li><i>Catalogi Willeriani / Willerus</i> — the famous Augsburg book-trade catalogues of <b>Georg Willer</b>, issued for the Frankfurt fairs; <i>Willerus</i> is the bookseller himself. <i>Horologia</i> rendered \"timepieces.\"</li><li><i>Doctoris Ambrosii libri … Welsero mortuo</i> — the library of a \"Doctor Ambrosius,\" dispersed by the heirs on the death of a <b>Welser</b> (the great Augsburg patrician house); the identity of \"Doctor Ambrosius\" is not fixed by the text and is left as the name stands.</li><li><b>The 1584 Augsburg Calendar dispute (the <i>Kalenderstreit</i>).</b> The letter's chief news is the Augsburg confessional riot over the Gregorian reform of 1582, a contemporaneous event of which Major is an eyewitness reporter. <i>Aediles Curules</i> = the city's senior magistrates (rendered \"Aediles Curule,\" the antique title Major uses for them); <i>Camera Imperialis</i> = the <b>Reichskammergericht</b> (Imperial Chamber of Justice), rendered \"Imperial [Chamber of Justice]\" with the bracketed noun supplied. <i>Praefectus Urbis, der Stadtvogt</i> — Major glosses his own Latin with the German civic title; the code-switch <i>der Stadtvogt</i> is kept verbatim and italicised. <i>Doctor Mylius Augustanus</i> = <b>Georg Mylius</b> (Müller), the leading Lutheran preacher of Augsburg, whose attempted expulsion (25 May) triggered the popular tumult. <i>porta Geggingensis</i> = the <b>Göggingen gate</b> (Gögginger Tor). The settling envoys come from <b>Württemberg</b> and <b>Ulm</b>; the Tübingen theologians ultimately favoured accepting the calendar <i>salvo verbo et libertate Ecclesiae</i> (\"the Word and the liberty of the Church kept safe\").</li><li><i>non tam Pontifici] quam nostri</i> — the printed/transcribed text carries an editorial bracket after <i>Pontifici</i>, marking a defective construction (an expected verb/participle governing the dative, e.g. <i>obsequentes</i>, is wanting). Rendered \"not so much [out of regard for] the Pope as our own [men],\" with the supplement bracketed; the crux is flagged, not smoothed (see Confidence).</li><li><b>The question put to Tycho.</b> Major asks Tycho's astronomical verdict on whether the <i>Calendarium neotericum</i> (\"newfangled / new-fashioned Calendar,\" the Gregorian) <i>coelo rectius congruat</i> — \"agrees more rightly with the heaven\" — appealing to him as one who <i>luminarium motibus sedulo invigiles</i> (\"watches diligently over the motions of the luminaries\"). <i>luminaria</i> = the two luminaries (Sun and Moon).</li><li><b>Technical / glossary terms.</b> <i>artes Mathematicas restaurandas … collapsas</i> — the <b>restoration of the mathematical arts</b> that have \"collapsed\" (Tycho's <i>instauratio</i> programme, §5.1), rendered \"restoring the mathematical arts.\" <i>organa et opera</i> — <i>organa</i> = <b>instruments</b> (§5.2), here paired with <i>opera</i> (\"works\"); rendered \"the instruments and works.\" <i>Matheseos</i> (gen. of <i>Mathesis</i>) — \"of mathematics\" (the divine science of mathematics, as in the Wolf letter). <i>Astrologia, h. e. illa divinatrix ars</i> — astrology glossed by Major himself as \"that divinatory art.\"</li><li><b>Numerals &amp; money.</b> <i>undecim … in peculiari scheda</i> — eleven priced items on an enclosed slip (the <i>scheda</i>); <i>affinis meus … satisfecit</i> — Major's kinsman has paid him on Tycho's behalf. <i>die 18.</i>, <i>die 25. Maii</i>, <i>ultimo Maii</i>, <i>mense Martio 1583</i>, <i>mense Maio An. 84</i> — dates kept as written.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Augustae Rheticae vulgo Vindelicorum postridie Pentecostes CIↃ IↃ LXXXIV</i> = \"Given at Augsburg of Raetia, commonly [Augsburg] of the Vindelici, <b>the day after Pentecost, 1584</b>.\" The Roman-numeral year <b>CIↃ IↃ LXXXIV</b> = MDLXXXIV = <b>1584</b> (the apostrophus form CIↃ = 1000, IↃ = 500). Augsburg in 1584 was a Protestant correspondent writing <b>Old Style (Julian)</b> — indeed the whole letter is <i>about</i> Augsburg's refusal of the Gregorian reform — so the dateline is O.S.: Pentecost (Whitsunday) fell on <b>7 June 1584 O.S.</b> (Julian Easter 19 April + 49 days), and the day after = <b>8 June 1584 O.S.</b>, which agrees with the dataset's catalogue date \"1584. 8. juni.\" Header date follows the as-written dateline; no silent conversion. <i>Augusta Rhaetica vulgo Vindelicorum</i> — Major's learned double name for Augsburg (Roman <i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i>; here also styled \"of Raetia\"), §5.7.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Vale feliciter</i> = \"Fare you happily well\" (§5.5).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout; the one genuine textual crux is <i>Maior enim pars non tam Pontifici] quam nostri contenderunt</i>, where the edition's stray bracket after <i>Pontifici</i> marks a missing governing word, leaving the dative <i>Pontifici</i> without an overt regimen. The sense (\"the majority pressed for acceptance not so much from deference to the Pope as on our own [civic/political] grounds\") is secure from context, but the exact supplement is editorial and is bracketed rather than resolved. The bare title <i>Liber de Triangulis</i> (probably Regiomontanus) and the unidentified \"Doctor Ambrosius\" are left open as the text leaves them. The dateline is unambiguous and matches the catalogue date.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.83-86 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"; p.86 confirmed the letter had already ended on p.85 with \"IOHAN. MAIOR.\"). Collated the full letter word-for-word against the printed text: the dedicatory heading, every proper name (Scaliger, Iacobus Heerbrandus, Hagecius, Hannibal, Graminaeus, Werner, Willer, Doctor Ambrosius, Welser, Mylius, der Stadtvogt), every number/date (undecim price items, tres/4/6 officials, dates 18 and 25 May, March 1583, May [15]84, the Roman numeral date CIↃ IↃ LXXXIV), and place-names (Tubingen, Heidelberg, Bavaria, Augsburg/Augusta Rhetica, Göggingen gate, Württemberg, Ulm). Source text matches Dreyer's printing exactly throughout, including the extended Augsburg calendar-riot narrative. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the published_grade of B undersold this letter, which is in fact fully collatable and clean.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Major", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15770920001", "next": "brahe-t_15840715001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15840715001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports procuring books as requested, including Scaliger's work on the Gregorian calendar, and expresses relief at learning Tycho is still alive after rumours of his death.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15840608001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15840612001", "d": "1584-06-12", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 12 June 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>CUM tuus Typographus, Nobilissime Charissimeque TYCHO, Rostochium venit, abfui, domum vero rediens, literis tuis lectis pecuniam in scattula obsignatam aurigae Witebergensi triduo post tradidi, nolui enim resignare. Nihil itaque pecuniae reservatum, qua auriga satisfieret, aut libri aliqui emerentur. De motuum Solis et Lunae reliquorumque Planetarum emendatione, quae prae manibus habes, expecto ut in lucem prodeant. Ego certe in omnibus Eclipsibus crassis nostris observationibus videor animadvertisse, calculum PURBACHIJ propius accessisse ad tempus apparens, quam COPERNICI. Ad singula autem momenta motus Solis respondere ex tuis observationibus deductos, gaudeo. Idem si in ceteris praestiteris, bene de omnibus mereberis, et aeternam laudem apud posteros consequeris. Et ego ita sentio, tam multa, quae de anni inaequalitate referuntur, ex imperfectis vel falsis observationibus nata esse; motum enim illum coelestem ijsdem perpetuo metis includi, et tam operosis hypothesibus COPERNICI in motu Terrae astruendo non fuisse opus, quas falsas esse dubium non est, et forte non satisfaciunt etiam apparentijs evidentibus. Ob id desiderabam organum, quod mihi ob oculos obiceret totam illam COPERNICI imaginationem, quam ego, ut verum fatear, ex simplici illa delineatione nunquam plene assequi potui. Et quamvis tecum sentiam, non tam in ijs organis laborandum, quae alterius phantasias oculis obijciant, quam quae motus coelestes nobis aperiant: est tamen hominis eruditi, quique aliorum inventa velit explicare, haec intelligere, et quantum fieri potest, oculis obicere. Imo optandum esset, posse sine ullis hypothesibus motus coelestes tradi. Quod ut forte fieri queat, apparentijs tamen satisfieri nunquam poterit. Hypotyposes PROCLI ob nostri Typographi negligentiam non excuduntur. Extant autem, magnae constructioni CL. PTOLEMAeI adiunctae, verum ita corrupte editae, ut autorem suum non agnoscant. Editae sunt alibi opera GEORGII VALLAe Placentini, ijs credo ab eodem autore tralatas Hypotyposes esse adiectas. Quod si hoc opus VALLAe habeas, velim ad me mitti, quo tantum percurram hanc Hypotyposium translationem, remissurus quam primum. Mitto IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM de emendatione temporum et MAeSTLINI de emendatione Calendarij. MAUROLYCUM hoc tempore, ut neque Gratiopolitanum consequi potui. Si quid in posterum nanciscar, ad te mittam. Quod si velis prius tibi precia perscribi, id quoque facturus sum. Hic tuus servus dum aliquot diebus hîc navem, quae solvat in Daniam, expectat, consumsit solidos 32. Et typos nescio quos maioris notae emit, de quibus mecum actum ut precium penderem. Verum cum huius rei nulla in tuis literis facta sit mentio, et ignorem, utrum hoc tuo iussu factum sit, nihil a me persolutum. Ex ipso plene omnia de typis intelliges.</p><p>Bene vale.<br>Rostochij 12. Iunij Anno Domini 1584.<br>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>When your printer came to Rostock, most noble and most dear Tycho, I was away; but on returning home, having read your letter, I handed over the money — sealed up in a packet — to the Wittenberg carter three days later, for I did not wish to break the seal. And so no money was held back with which the carter might be paid or any books bought. As for the correction of the motions of the Sun and Moon and of the rest of the planets, which you have in hand, I await their coming forth into the light. For my part, in all the eclipses I have certainly seemed to observe, from our rough observations, that Peurbach's calculation came closer to the apparent time than Copernicus's. And I rejoice that the [positions] derived from your observations answer to the single moments of the Sun's motion. If you shall achieve the same in the rest, you will deserve well of all men, and win everlasting praise among posterity. And this too is my opinion: that the many things which are reported concerning the inequality of the year have arisen from imperfect or false observations; for that celestial motion is enclosed perpetually within the same bounds, and there was no need of Copernicus's so laborious hypotheses in establishing the motion of the Earth — which it is beyond doubt are false, and perhaps do not satisfy even the evident appearances. On that account I desired an instrument that might set before my eyes that whole imagining of Copernicus, which I — to confess the truth — could never fully grasp from that bare diagram. And although I am of one mind with you that one should labour not so much over those instruments which set another's fancies before the eyes, as over those which lay open the celestial motions to us, yet it belongs to a learned man, and to one who would wish to expound the inventions of others, to understand these things and, so far as may be done, to set them before the eyes. Nay, it were to be wished that the celestial motions could be transmitted without any hypotheses at all. But even if that could perhaps be done, the appearances will nevertheless never be able to be satisfied. The <i>Hypotyposes</i> of Proclus are not being printed, owing to the negligence of our printer. They are extant, however, appended to the great construction [Almagest] of the renowned Ptolemy, but so corruptly edited that they do not acknowledge their own author. They were published elsewhere in the works of Giorgio Valla of Piacenza, to which, I believe, the <i>Hypotyposes</i> — translated by the same author — were added. So if you have this work of Valla's, I should like it sent to me, that I might just run through this translation of the <i>Hypotyposes</i>, intending to send it back at the first opportunity. I send Joseph Scaliger's <i>On the Emendation of the Times</i>, and Maestlin's <i>On the Emendation of the Calendar</i>. At this time I have not been able to obtain Maurolico, any more than the Gratiopolitanus [the man / work of Grenoble?]. If I light upon anything hereafter, I will send it to you. And if you would first have the prices written out for you, that too I shall do. This servant of yours, while waiting here some days for a ship to set sail for Denmark, has spent 32 shillings. And he has bought some types or other of a larger sort, concerning which it was settled with me that I should pay the price. But since no mention of this matter was made in your letter, and I do not know whether it was done at your bidding, nothing has been paid out by me. From the man himself you will learn everything about the types in full.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Rostock, 12 June, in the Year of the Lord 1584.<br>Heinrich Brucaeus.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeucke, 1531–1598), Flemish-born professor of medicine and (effectively) of astronomy/mathematics at the University of Rostock, and an established correspondent of Tycho's. Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, p. 180) cites this very passage: Tycho, \"wanting a Latin copy\" of Proclus, had asked Brucaeus for help, and Brucaeus \"could only provide … some vague bibliographical help\" about Valla's edition.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Rostochium / Rostochij</i> = <b>Rostock</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>The printer / \"your servant.\"</b> <i>tuus Typographus</i> … <i>Hic tuus servus</i> — Tycho's own printer/agent, then travelling between Rostock and Hven, ferrying money, books, and printing type. <i>aurigae Witebergensi</i> = \"the Wittenberg carter\" (the carrier on the Rostock–Wittenberg run). The closing transaction (32 shillings spent; <i>typos … maioris notae</i>, \"types of a larger sort,\" i.e. a larger type-body, bought on uncertain authority) belongs to the practical business of equipping Tycho's Uraniborg press.</li><li><b><i>solidos 32</b></i> — \"32 shillings\" (<i>solidus</i>, the period accounting/coin unit); kept as \"shillings.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy — the technical core.</b></li><li><i>De motuum Solis et Lunae reliquorumque Planetarum emendatione</i> — \"the correction of the motions of the Sun and Moon and the rest of the planets,\" Tycho's restorative programme (<i>emendatio</i>/<i>restitutio</i>; protocol §5.1, <i>restoration</i>).</li><li><i>calculum PURBACHIJ … quam COPERNICI</i> — Brucaeus reports that <b>Peurbach's</b> calculation (Georg Peurbach, 1423–1461, the <i>Theoricae novae planetarum</i> / Ptolemaic tradition) matched the <i>tempus apparens</i> of eclipses better than <b>Copernicus's</b>. <i>Calculus</i> = \"calculation/computation\"; <i>tempus apparens</i> = \"the apparent time\" (kept).</li><li><i>anni inaequalitate</i> — \"the inequality of the year\": the supposed variation in the length of the (tropical/solar) year much debated in the calendar-reform literature; Brucaeus judges the reported variation an artefact of \"imperfect or false observations\" and holds the celestial motion to be \"enclosed perpetually within the same bounds.\"</li><li><i>operosis hypothesibus COPERNICI in motu Terrae astruendo</i> — \"Copernicus's so laborious hypotheses in establishing the motion of the Earth.\" Brucaeus's anti-Copernican stance is stated as his own opinion and is preserved as such (no sharpening): he calls them \"beyond doubt false\" and says they \"perhaps\" (<i>forte</i>) fail even to save the appearances — the hedge <i>forte</i> is kept.</li><li><i>organum … imaginationem … delineatione</i> — <i>organum</i> = <b>instrument</b> (protocol §5.2); here Brucaeus wishes for an instrument (a planetary model/sphere) that would render visible \"that whole imagining (<i>imaginatio</i>) of Copernicus,\" which he could not grasp from \"that bare diagram (<i>delineatio</i>).\" <i>imaginatio</i> rendered \"imagining\" (the mental construct/representation), not modernised to \"model.\"</li><li><i>organis … quae alterius phantasias oculis obijciant … quam quae motus coelestes nobis aperiant</i> — the contrast (kept verbatim in sense) between instruments that \"set another's fancies before the eyes\" and those that \"lay open the celestial motions\": Brucaeus agrees with Tycho that the latter matter more, yet defends the former's didactic use.</li><li><i>sine ullis hypothesibus motus coelestes tradi … apparentijs … satisfieri</i> — \"to transmit the celestial motions without any hypotheses\" / \"to satisfy the appearances\" (<i>satisfacere apparentiis</i> = the <i>sōzein ta phainomena</i> topos). Rendered literally; the pessimistic conclusion (\"even if it could be done, the appearances will never be able to be satisfied [thereby]\") is preserved with its difficulty intact (see crux below).</li><li><b>Books named.</b></li><li><i>Hypotyposes PROCLI</i> — <b>Proclus</b>, <i>Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum</i> (the elementary exposition of Ptolemaic planetary theory). <i>Hypotyposes</i> kept as a title-word.</li><li><i>magnae constructioni CL. PTOLEMAeI adiunctae</i> — \"appended to the great construction of the renowned Ptolemy\": <i>magna constructio</i> = the <b>Almagest</b> (Greek <i>Megalē syntaxis</i> / <i>Megistē</i>); <i>CL.</i> = <i>Clarissimi</i> (\"the renowned/most illustrious\"), expanded. Brucaeus notes the appended <i>Hypotyposes</i> there are \"so corruptly edited that they do not acknowledge their own author\" (i.e. the print is so bad the text is barely recognisable as Proclus's).</li><li><i>opera GEORGII VALLAe Placentini</i> — the works of <b>Giorgio Valla of Piacenza</b> (<i>Placentinus</i> = of Piacenza), in whose collection (<i>De expetendis et fugiendis rebus</i>, 1501) a Latin Proclus translation appears; Brucaeus believes the <i>Hypotyposes</i> there were \"translated by the same author\" (Valla).</li><li><i>IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM de emendatione temporum</i> — <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b>, <i>De emendatione temporum</i> (Paris, 1583); corpus cross-check (Gingerich, <i>Annotated Census</i>) confirms the 1583 Paris edition. <i>Mitto … Scaligerum</i> = \"I send [the book of] Scaliger,\" a common metonymy (the author's name for his book); rendered \"I send Joseph Scaliger's ….\"</li><li><i>MAeSTLINI de emendatione Calendarij</i> — <b>Michael Maestlin</b>, <i>De emendatione Calendarii</i> (a tract against the Gregorian calendar reform). <i>Maestlini</i> genitive; rendered \"Maestlin's ….\"</li><li><i>MAUROLYCUM</i> — <b>Francesco Maurolico</b> (Maurolyco, 1494–1575) of Messina, mathematician; here the author's name stands for a book of his that Brucaeus could not obtain.</li><li><b>Latin formulae.</b> <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Anno Domini</i> = \"in the Year of the Lord.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Rostochij 12. Iunij Anno Domini 1584</i> = \"Rostock, 12 June 1584.\" <b>Old Style</b>: a Protestant correspondent in Lutheran Rostock in 1584 wrote Julian. The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1584. 12. juni. Rostock\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. No silent conversion made.</li><li><b>Cruxes.</b></li><li><b>CRUX (<i>Gratiopolitanum</i>).</b> <i>MAUROLYCUM hoc tempore, ut neque Gratiopolitanum consequi potui</i> — \"I could not obtain Maurolico at this time, any more than the <i>Gratiopolitanum</i>.\" <i>Gratiopolitanum</i> (var. <i>Gratianopolitanum</i>) is the Latin adjective for <b>Grenoble</b> (Gratianopolis); as a substantive accusative it parallels <i>Maurolycum</i> and so denotes a person or (more likely) a book \"of/from Grenoble\" that Brucaeus was likewise trying to procure for Tycho. The precise referent is not determinable from the letter and is not resolved here; \"the Gratiopolitanus\" is left flagged rather than guessed. This drives the overall grade.</li><li><b>Syntactic crux (closing of the hypothesis sentence).</b> <i>Imo optandum esset, posse sine ullis hypothesibus motus coelestes tradi. Quod ut forte fieri queat, apparentijs tamen satisfieri nunquam poterit.</i> The second sentence is logically taut: literally \"Although this could perhaps be done, yet the appearances will never be able to be satisfied.\" The natural reading — adopted above — is concessive (\"even granting that hypothesis-free transmission were possible, the phenomena could never be fully saved by it\"), i.e. a pessimistic rider to the wish. An alternative reads <i>Quod ut … queat</i> as final-purpose (\"in order that this might perhaps come to pass\"). The concessive reading is given; the alternative is recorded rather than suppressed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin 'in scapula obsignatam' corrected to 'in scattula obsignatam' per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.85.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the astronomical and epistolary substance is rendered securely (and corroborated by Mosley's quotation of the Valla/Proclus passage), but the unidentified <b>Gratiopolitanum</b> is a genuine lexical crux left open, and the concessive-vs-final force of the <i>Quod ut … queat</i> clause is flagged rather than silently decided.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 99–100. <i>Gratiopolitanum</i> is reclassified by a parallel Tycho book-wishlist (<i>Tycho Brahes Breve</i>) listing <i>\"Gratiopolitanum etiam de Triangulis\"</i> beside Maurolico: it is a treatise <i>De Triangulis</i> (trigonometry) by an author styled 'Gratianopolitanus' (of Grenoble); the proper name is still unidentified, and the <i>Quod ut … queat</i> mood stays open. [C] stands (lighter).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.85-87 (letter itself on 85 through the top of 86): the Purbach/Copernicus eclipse comparison, the Proclus/Ptolemy/Valla bibliographic passage, the names sent (Scaliger, Maestlin, Maurolycus), the sum '32 solidos' spent by Tycho's servant, and the closing date (Rostock, 12 June 1584) all match Dreyer and are faithfully translated. One source-text corruption found (scapula vs Dreyer's scattula); no other defects.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin corrupts 'scattula' (a small box/case) to 'scapula' (shoulder), which is nonsensical in context; Dreyer prints the sensible reading. The translation sidesteps the corruption with the generic 'packet' rather than reproducing either word literally. Dreyer prints: <i>pecuniam in fcattula obfignatam aurigae Witebergenfi triduo poft tradidi (TBOO VII p.85)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VII p.85, where the word is unambiguous. The single defect is confirmed and fixed; the English translation required no change since it did not follow the corrupted Latin literally. Pages 86-87 also reviewed for context; no other divergence found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15840000001", "next": "brahe-t_15841210001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports forwarding money to Tycho's printer via a carter and discusses solar and lunar motion, noting that Peurbach's calculations outperform Copernicus's in eclipse timings.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15840612001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15840715001", "d": "1584-07-15", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Major", "ck": "Johannes Major", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Knutstorp", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Major — Knudstrup, 15 July 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOHANNEM MAIOREM.</p><p>QUAS ad me ultimo dedisti literas, eruditissime Domine IOHANNES MAIOR, recte accepi et de libris procuratis gratias habeo. WELLERIANOS hac vice non cupio. De Calendario Gregoriano quod tanta fuerit apud vos animorum concertatio, ut ferme ad arma res devenerit, miror, et meo sane iudicio non decuisset eos, qui Theologiam apud vos profitentur, occasionem sic dare his turbis et populum contra Magistratum legitimum commovere, in re, uti opinor, adiaphora et religioni, quam illi tuentur, nihil efficiente. Sed hac tamen salva, quatenus divino verbo fundatur, manente, nonne prioris et diu usitati Calendarij Nicenum Concilium secuti Romani Pontifices autores et conservatores fuêre, et aliorum dierum festorum, quibus ij, qui se Euangelium nuncupant, etiamnum utuntur? Cur igitur Pontifici Romano non liceret, praesertim approbante id Imperatoria Maiestate, idem Calendarium, quatenus mancum et insufficiens longo aevi tractu redditum est, emendare et in melius, quoad fieri potuit, restituere? Certe videntur hi, qui tam contentiose illi obstrepunt, non zelo secundum scientiam id agere, sed potius affectibus indulgere et philautia laborare, atque odio Pontificis saltem id facere. Si ante LUTHERI tempora tali modo fuisset renovatum Calendarium, quando per excellentem Germanorum Mathematicum, IOHANNEM REGIOMONTANUM, Pontifex, qui tum Romae praefuit, id fieri satagebat, certe LUTHERUS huic emendationi non invite acquievisset, cum nihil contineret ipsius doctrinae contrarium. Et cur non successores eius eo, quod nunc redintegratum est, contenti sunt? Videant, ne sic dent occasionem ijs, qui veterem doctrinam se profiteri aiunt, obijciendi, quod nimio contradictionis studio occaecati quidvis impugnent, sive id speciem aliquam dubitationis mereatur, sive non, atque ideo eos in caeteris quoque minus considerate agere praesumatur. Et siquidem de hoc Calendario renovato meam exquiris sententiam, dicam tibi id, quod et alijs eadem sciscitantibus respondi.</p><p>Calendarium hoc Gregorianum noviter reformatum praevalet veteri et prius usitato, et recte factum est a Pontifice opera LILII Mathematici, ut 10 dies in eo omissi sint. Totidem enim defectus anni coelestis et aequinoctialis a Iuliano, qui inde a Concilio Niceno direxit, quam proxime requirit. Et alia etiam quaedam non inconvenienter in nupero Calendario emendata sunt. Licet vero aliqua in his adhuc castigatius in eo proponi possint, si coelitus deducti Luminarium motus in consilium adhiberentur, tamen cum hoc non facile detur in tanta exactissimarum observationum penuria, et Ecclesiae ac Politiae usus summam in his praecisionem quam ipsi etiam Astronomi vix assequuntur, non exigat, nec etiam magnopere opus habeat, acquiescendum utique erit huic Pontificis Romani, cuius etiamnum maxima est in terris autoritas, consultae et laudabili provisioni in Calendarij diu frustraque desiderata reformatione, nec temere quid in contrarium movendum cum periculo pacis publicae.</p><p>Habes breviter, quid ego sentiam, et forte mecum viri cordati non pauci. Quid vero penitius in hac renovatione praestari possit, dicere non audeo, antequam de exacto motu ☉ et ☾ novis illis et maximis, quae adorno, organis certius quid expertus fuero. Nam coelo inconsulto de ijs, quae inde profluunt, censuram proferre velle temerarium et insulsum esse iudico. Laudo itaque Ampliss. vestrae Reipub. decisionem consultam, quod Calendarium hoc Gregorianum pacis publicae causa et ob vicinarum regionum consuetudinem atque cum ijs commercia susceperit, utut reclamitante incerto vulgo, quod semper studia in contraria scinditur. Atque de his hoc tempore satis, de tuis per occationem, si res ita postulârit, alias plura in medium proferam.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex aede nostra Knudstrupiana 15. die Iulij stylo veteri, nobis adhuc usitato, Anno 1584.<br>T.<br>TYCHO DE BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Johannes Major.</b></p><p>The letter you last sent me, most learned Master Johannes Major, I have duly received, and I give thanks for the books procured. The Wellerian ones I do not want this time. That there should have been among you so great a clash of feelings over the Gregorian calendar that the matter nearly came to arms, I marvel at; and in my own judgement it ill became those who profess Theology among you to give occasion for these tumults in this way, and to stir up the people against the lawful magistrate, in a matter that, as I think, brings about nothing with respect to matters of religious indifference [<i>adiaphora</i>] and to the religion which they uphold. Yet, this remaining safe so far as it is founded upon the divine word, were not the Roman Pontiffs, following the Council of Nicaea, the authors and preservers of the earlier and long-used calendar, and of the other feast-days that those who call themselves of the Gospel still use even now? Why, then, should it not be permitted to the Roman Pontiff — especially with His Imperial Majesty approving it — to amend that same calendar, in so far as it has been rendered defective and insufficient through the long course of time, and to restore it for the better, as far as could be done? Surely those who so contentiously cry out against him seem to do this not from a zeal that is according to knowledge, but rather to indulge their passions and to labour under self-love [<i>philautia</i>], and to do it at least out of hatred of the Pontiff. Had the calendar been renewed in this manner before Luther's time — when, through the excellent German mathematician Johannes Regiomontanus, the Pontiff who then presided at Rome was busying himself to have it done — surely Luther would not unwillingly have acquiesced in this emendation, since it contained nothing contrary to his own doctrine. And why are his successors not content with what has now been set right again? Let them take care lest they thus give occasion to those who say they profess the old doctrine to object that, blinded by an excessive zeal for contradiction, they assail anything whatever — whether or not it deserves any appearance of doubt — and that therefore they may be presumed to act less considerately in other matters too. And since indeed you ask my opinion about this renewed calendar, I shall tell you what I have answered to others asking the same.</p><p>This Gregorian calendar, newly reformed, prevails over the old and formerly used one, and it was rightly done by the Pontiff, through the work of Lilius the mathematician, that 10 days were omitted in it. For just so many does the shortfall of the celestial and equinoctial year from the Julian — which had governed [the reckoning] ever since the Council of Nicaea — most nearly require. And certain other things too have been not unsuitably amended in the recent calendar. But although some things in these matters could still be set forth in it more correctly, if the motions of the Luminaries derived from the heavens themselves were brought into the deliberation, nevertheless, since this is not easily granted amid so great a scarcity of the most exact observations, and since the use of the Church and the State does not demand — nor even greatly need — that highest precision in these matters which even the Astronomers themselves scarcely attain, one must by all means acquiesce in this prudent and praiseworthy provision of the Roman Pontiff — whose authority is still the greatest on earth — in the long and vainly desired reformation of the calendar, and nothing must rashly be set in motion to the contrary, at the peril of the public peace.</p><p>You have in brief what I think, and perhaps not a few sensible men think with me. But what might more deeply be accomplished in this renewal, I do not dare to say, before I shall have ascertained something more certain about the exact motion of the Sun [☉] and the Moon [☽] by those new and very great instruments which I am preparing. For to wish to pronounce a verdict, without consulting the heavens, upon the things that flow therefrom, I judge to be rash and foolish. I therefore praise the prudent decision of your most ample commonwealth, in that it has taken up this Gregorian calendar for the sake of the public peace and on account of the custom of neighbouring regions and of commerce with them, however much the unsettled common crowd cries out against it — that crowd which is always split into opposing partialities. And on these matters enough for this time; about your affairs, as occasion offers, if the matter so require, I shall bring forward more another time.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from our seat at Knudstrup, the 15th day of July, by the old style, still used by us, in the Year 1584.<br>T[ycho].<br>Tycho de Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / place.</b> Tycho writes from <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knudstrup / Knutstorp), the Brahe family seat in Scania, his birthplace — distinct from Uraniborg on Hven; <i>aede nostra Knudstrupiana</i> = \"our seat at Knudstrup.\"</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Iohannes Maior</i> = <b>Johann Major / Johannes Maior</b> (1533–1600), the Wittenberg humanist, Lutheran theologian and Neo-Latin poet (poeta laureatus), professor at Wittenberg; a learned correspondent who had procured books for Tycho. (To be distinguished from the medieval Scotist Johannes Maior/John Mair, and from other contemporaries of the name.) <b>Identification confidence MEDIUM</b>: the period name <i>Iohannes Maior</i> is shared by several figures; the Wittenberg poet-theologian fits the German Protestant setting of the calendar dispute described as occurring <i>apud vos</i> (\"among you\"), but the letter itself gives no decisive disambiguator. Flagged rather than asserted.</li><li><b><i>Wellerianos</b></i> — \"the Wellerian ones [books],\" i.e. works by or associated with <b>Hieronymus Weller</b> (1499–1572) of Freiberg, the Lutheran theologian and close associate of Luther (or possibly another of the Weller family of theologians); the substantivised adjective refers back to <i>libris</i> (\"books\"). Rendered \"the Wellerian ones.\" Identification of the precise referent is MEDIUM.</li><li><b>The calendar dispute (<i>apud vos … ferme ad arma</i>).</b> The Gregorian reform was promulgated by the bull <i>Inter gravissimas</i> (Feb. 1582); the Protestant Imperial estates refused it, and its attempted introduction provoked violent resistance in several German cities — most famously the <b>Augsburg \"calendar riot\" (Kalenderstreit)</b> of 1583–84, where confessional conflict over the new calendar brought a city near to armed disorder. Tycho's <i>ut ferme ad arma res devenerit</i> (\"that the matter nearly came to arms\") reflects this; the specific city is not named in the letter and is not supplied here.</li><li><b><i>anaphora</b></i> — a technical term of the chronological/computistical reckoning (the Easter cycle / the carrying-forward of the calendrical correction); Tycho's point is that the dispute affects nothing in the substance of religion. Kept and glossed <i>[Easter-]reckoning</i> with the Latin flagged; the precise sense is a minor <b>crux</b> (see Confidence).</li><li><b><i>philautia</b></i> (φιλαυτία) — \"self-love,\" the vice of inordinate self-regard; a learned Graeco-Latin term, kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Cyprianus / —</i> none here beyond: <b>Johannes Regiomontanus</b> (Johann Müller of Königsberg, 1436–1476), the German mathematician-astronomer whom Pope Sixtus IV had summoned to Rome c. 1475 in connection with calendar reform (he died there in 1476); <b>Luther</b> (Martin Luther); <b>Aloysius Lilius</b> (Luigi Lilio / Giglio, d. c. 1576), the Calabrian physician-mathematician whose computational scheme underlies the Gregorian reform — <i>opera LILII Mathematici</i> = \"through the work of Lilius the mathematician\" (confirmed against the house reference corpus, which names Lilius \"the principal architect of the Gregorian calendar\"). <i>Imperatoria Maiestate</i> = the Holy Roman Emperor (Rudolf II), whose approval Tycho cites.</li><li><b>The ten omitted days.</b> <i>ut 1O dies in eo omissi sint</i> — the source prints \"1O\" (numeral 1 + capital O) for <b>10</b>; the ten days dropped in October 1582 to realign the equinox. The Julian year's small excess over the tropical year had, since Nicaea (325), accumulated to about ten days; Tycho's <i>defectus anni coelestis et aequinoctialis a Iuliano … quam proxime requirit</i> states that this accumulated discrepancy \"most nearly requires\" just that number — i.e. the correction is approximately, not exactly, right (an epistemic hedge preserved: <i>quam proxime</i>, \"most nearly,\" not \"exactly\").</li><li><b>Tycho's reservation.</b> Note the carefully hedged scientific stance: the reform is endorsed <i>for civil and ecclesiastical use</i> and <i>for the public peace</i>, while Tycho reserves judgement on whether it is astronomically optimal until he has determined the exact solar and lunar motions with <i>novis illis et maximis … organis</i> (\"those new and very great <b>instruments</b>,\" protocol §5.2) — the Uraniborg programme. <i>coelo inconsulto</i> = \"without consulting the heavens.\" The hedges (<i>non audeo</i> \"I dare not\"; <i>certius quid expertus fuero</i> \"when I shall have ascertained something more certain\") are preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ☽ = <b>Moon</b> (named in the translation, glyphs kept in the Source block, protocol §5.3). <i>motu ☉ et ☾</i> = \"the motion of the Sun and the Moon.\"</li><li><b><i>Ampliss. vestrae Reipub.</b></i> = <i>Amplissimae vestrae Reipublicae</i>, \"your most ample commonwealth/republic\" — the civic authority (city council / academic body) that had adopted the new calendar; the <i>Magistratum legitimum</i> (\"lawful magistrate\") of the first paragraph.</li><li><b><i>restituere / redintegratum / reformatione / renovatione</b></i> — Tycho's vocabulary of <i>restoration / renewal</i>, here of the calendar; cf. his own programme of <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> (protocol §5.1). Rendered consistently with \"restore / set right again / reformation / renewal.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Dabantur … 15. die Iulij stylo veteri, nobis adhuc usitato, Anno 1584</i> = \"Given … the 15th day of July, <b>by the old style, still used by us</b>, in the Year 1584\" — Tycho expressly flags his date as <b>Old Style (Julian)</b>, the running theme of the letter; <b>15 July 1584 O.S.</b> The dataset label (\"1584. 15. juli. Knutstorp\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. Per protocol §3 the date is not converted; the N.S. equivalent would be 25 July 1584, but the source asserts O.S. and so does the header.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>T. / TYCHO DE BRAHE</i> — the abbreviated monogram <i>T.</i> followed by the full signature; both kept.</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Latin restored 'apud vos' (Theologiam apud vos profitentur) and corrected 'anaphora' to 'adiaphora', the Lutheran technical term for matters of religious indifference at stake in the Gregorian calendar dispute (Dreyer VII p.86); English correspondingly restored 'among you' and replaced the invented gloss 'Easter-reckoning' with 'matters of religious indifference [adiaphora]'. Latin numeral '1O' corrected to '10' (Dreyer VII p.87).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and its scientific/theological argument are rendered securely and the hedges preserved. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the precise technical sense of <b><i>anaphora</b></i> in <i>anaphora et religioni … nihil efficiente</i> (computistical Easter-reckoning vs. a more general \"carrying-over\" — translated \"[Easter-]reckoning\" with the term flagged); and (2) the identification of the recipient <b><i>Johannes Major</b></i> and of the <b><i>Wellerian</b></i> books, both MEDIUM on the letter's internal evidence alone.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.86-88 (all 3 pages read as page images). The letter occupies the lower half of p.86 through p.87, preceded by the tail of Henricus Brucaeus's letter to Tycho and followed by a second Brucaeus letter, both correctly excluded. Full Latin text collated: the Wellerian-books remark, the Gregorian calendar controversy, Regiomontanus and Luther, Lilius's 10-day correction, the sun/moon-instrument caveat, and the closing (Knudstrup, 15 July 1584 O.S.) all check against Dreyer. Two TEXT_ERRORs found in source_text (a dropped 'apud vos'; 'adiaphora' corrupted to 'anaphora'), the second of which the translation compounds into an invented, unsupported gloss ('Easter-reckoning') that misrepresents the specific theological term central to the passage - this is what drives the SERIOUS verdict despite the letter being published at grade A.</p><p><b>4 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text silently drops 'apud vos' (among you) from Dreyer's text; the English translation follows the truncated source and likewise omits 'among you'. Dreyer prints: <i>non decuisset eos, qui Theologiam apud vos profitentur</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Source_text corrupts Dreyer's 'adiaphora' (the Lutheran theological/legal term for matters of religious indifference - precisely the concept at stake in the Gregorian-calendar adiaphora controversy) into 'anaphora', a different word (rhetorical repetition / Eucharistic prayer). Dreyer prints: <i>in re, uti opinor, adiaphora et religioni, quam illi tuentur, nihil efficiente</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). Working from the corrupted 'anaphora', the translator invents an unsupported interpretive gloss ('Easter-reckoning') matching neither Dreyer's actual word 'adiaphora' (matters of religious indifference) nor any plausible sense of 'anaphora'. A scholar would come away with a false idea of the specific theological concept Tycho is invoking in a letter about the Gregorian calendar dispute. At: “the [Easter-]reckoning [anaphora]”.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text renders the numeral as digit-plus-letter '1O' rather than '10'; likely a pipeline rendering artifact, but a literal departure from Dreyer's printed numeral. Translation correctly reads '10 days'. Dreyer prints: <i>ut 10 dies in [eo] omissi sint</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated all four defects against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.86-87). Page 86 confirms 'Theologiam apud vos profitentur' and 'in re, uti opinor, adiaphora et religioni'; page 87 confirms the numeral '10'. All four defects fixed with high confidence; no crux remains.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Major", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15840608001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840608001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges books received, declines the Wellerian ones, and argues that Lutheran theologians had no grounds to incite popular unrest over the Gregorian calendar reform.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15840715001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15841210001", "d": "1584-12-10", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 10 December 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S. P.</p><p>SCRIPSI per eundem, qui tuas literas ad me pertulit, Bibliopolam Francofurto ad nos redeuntem nihil eorum librorum, quorum in literis meministi, attulisse, daturum autem me operam, ut vel Lipsia aut Witeberga, quos nancisci potero, adferendos curem. Gnomotericam CLAVIJ, quam aestimat Ioachimicis prope 5, RANZOVIO vendidit. Laborabo, ut studium et diligentiam nostram in hisce libris conquirendis agnoscas. Intellige autem, duo illa volumina iunctim venire Joachimicis 16. Typographum nostrum MYLIANDRUM typorum nomine conveni; is respondet, ante D. Antonij festum non posse hisce typis carere, tum, si voles, velle aliquot libras vendere; arbitratur autem, libras 10 non sufficere ad unam columnam sive paginam excudendam. Aestimat singulas libras dimidio Thalero. Quid fieri velis, indicabis, antequam mare claudatur. VITUS ille, cuius meministi, mihi adfuit et respondit, velle fundere typos, quorum in tuis literis ad eum datis mentionem fecisti. Quaerenti mihi de precio respondit, unâ cum quadratis Ioachimicos prope 30 requiri.</p><p>In Eclipsi Lunae 7. Novembris observata nostrae observationes non plane cum tuis conveniunt. Initium non fuit observatum. Porro ubi tota obscurari visa est, deprehensa est Procyonis altitudo partium 26. Cum primum ex umbra inciperet emergere, altitudo eiusdem stellae partium erat 37. Ubi tota ex umbra emergere visa est, erat 41. Ex quibus tempora si deducantur, principium Eclipsis incidet in horam 11 6' 32 \", principium vero totius occultationis in h. 12 11' 44\", medium [totius obscurationis in h. 1 1' 48\", finis] totius obscurationis in h. 1 51' 52\". Tota ex umbra emersit h. 2 55' 4\". Ex quibus colligo MAeSTLINUM sesquihora ab hac observatione differre, ipsum vero COPERNICUM scrupulis solum sex, PURBACHIUM vero 30'. Si quid tamen ex superioribus observationibus potui colligere, propius a PURBACHIO fuit accessum in aliquot prioribus Eclipsibus. Et credo, me superiorem aliquam Eclipsin observasse, in qua proxime visus est MAeSTLINUS accedere. Verum, mi TYCHO, haec res eget, ut ego iudico, longe ante actorum temporum et observationum artificum excellentium collatione, et homine ingenij sagacis, qui queat aliorum inventis aliquid addere. Ego in PROCLO ad Solis usque motum progressus sum, in cuius consideratione, ut aliorum siderum, cum studium tuum et diligentia perspecta mihi sint, velim aequinoctium solstitiorumque tempora ex te intelligere. Tua enim industria cum habeas instrumenta exquisite elaborata et utendi ratio optime tibi constet, non dubito, tibi dies et horarum momenta, in quae incidunt, constare.</p><p>Bene vale, carissime TYCHO, et me mutuo amore prosequere.<br>Rostochij 1O. Decemb. 1584.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>I wrote — by the same man who brought your letter to me — that the bookseller, returning to us from Frankfurt, had brought none of those books which you mentioned in your letter, but that I would take pains to see to it that those I can obtain be brought either from Leipzig or from Wittenberg. The <i>Gnomonice</i> of Clavius, which he reckons at nearly 5 Joachimsthalers, I sold to Rantzau. I shall labour so that you may recognize our zeal and diligence in seeking out these books. Understand, moreover, that those two volumes go together for 16 Joachimsthalers. I approached our printer Myliander on the matter of the types; he replies that before St Anthony's feast he cannot do without these types, [but] then, if you wish, he is willing to sell some pounds [of them]; he judges, however, that 10 pounds are not enough to print off a single column or page. He values each pound at half a thaler. You will indicate what you wish to be done before the sea is closed. That Vitus whom you mentioned was present with me and replied that he is willing to cast the types of which you made mention in your letter sent to him. When I asked him about the price, he replied that, together with the quadrats, nearly 30 Joachimsthalers are required.</p><p>In the eclipse of the Moon observed on 7 November our observations do not entirely agree with yours. The beginning was not observed. Further, when it appeared to be wholly darkened, the altitude of Procyon was found to be 26 degrees. When it first began to emerge from the shadow, the altitude of the same star was 37 degrees. When it appeared to emerge wholly from the shadow, it was 41. If the times are deduced from these, the beginning of the eclipse will fall at the hour 11h 6' 32\", the beginning of total occultation at 12h 11' 44\", the middle [of total obscuration at 1h 1' 48\", the end] of total obscuration at 1h 51' 52\". It emerged wholly from the shadow at 2h 55' 4\". From these I gather that Maestlin differs from this observation by an hour and a half, but Copernicus himself by only six minutes [of arc / of time], and Peurbach by 30'. Yet if I have been able to gather anything from earlier observations, in several prior eclipses the approach was closer to Peurbach. And I believe that I observed some earlier eclipse in which Maestlin was seen to come nearest. But, my Tycho, this matter requires, as I judge, a collation of the times of long-past events and of the observations of excellent craftsmen, and a man of sagacious genius, who is able to add something to the discoveries of others. I have, in Proclus, advanced as far as the motion of the Sun, in the consideration of which — as of the other heavenly bodies — since your zeal and diligence are well known to me, I should like to learn from you the times of the equinox and of the solstices. For since by your industry you have instruments exquisitely wrought, and the manner of using them is perfectly settled for you, I do not doubt that the days, and the moments of the hours on which they fall, are settled for you.</p><p>Fare you well, dearest Tycho, and continue to attend me with mutual love.<br>Rostock, 10 December 1584.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, where Tycho had studied; a long-standing correspondent and book/instrument intermediary for him. The Vienna copy-book (cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66) preserves this among the <i>epistolae</i>; the letter is Dreyer, <i>TBOO</i> VII, p. 88.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (<i>dicit</i>), \"heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well.\"</li><li><b>Book trade.</b> <i>Bibliopolam Francofurto … redeuntem</i> — a bookseller returning from the <b>Frankfurt</b> book fair; <i>Lipsia / Witeberga</i> = <b>Leipzig / Wittenberg</b>. <i>Ioachimici</i> (sc. <i>thaleri Ioachimici</i>) = <b>Joachimsthalers</b> (the silver <i>Joachimsthaler</i> / \"Joachim's-dale thaler\"), here the unit of account; <i>Thalerus</i> = <b>thaler</b>. <i>Ranzovio</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the Holstein royal governor, humanist and astrology patron, a noted book-collector.</li><li><b><i>Gnomotericam Clavij</b></i> — Christopher Clavius's work on dialling/gnomonics, i.e. the <i>Gnomonices libri octo</i> (Rome, 1581). The MS spelling <i>Gnomotericam</i> (for <i>Gnomonicam</i>) is retained in the Source block; rendered <i>Gnomonice</i> in translation, the slip flagged here rather than silently corrected.</li><li><b>Printing-type procurement.</b> The letter's central business: Tycho's setting-up of the <b>Uraniborg press</b>. <i>Typographum nostrum Myliandrum</i> = the Rostock printer <b>Stephan Myliander</b> (Möllemann); <i>typorum nomine conveni</i> = \"I approached … on the matter of the types.\" <i>ante D. Antonij festum</i> = \"before <b>St Anthony's feast</b>\" (St Anthony the Abbot, 17 January, O.S.). <i>libras / libra</i> here = <b>pounds (weight) of type-metal</b>, not coins or books (<i>libras 10 non sufficere ad unam columnam … excudendam</i> fixes the sense). <i>VITUS ille</i> = \"that <b>Vitus</b>\" (Veit), a type-founder named in Tycho's prior letter; <i>velle fundere typos</i> = \"willing to cast the types.\" <i>quadratis / quadratis</i> = <b>quadrats</b> (the blank spacing-pieces of a type-font). Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, cites this exact passage (TBOO VII, 88.13–17) for Tycho's type procurement from Myliander; <i>On Tycho's Island</i> confirms that fonts were cast at Rostock with Brucaeus's help, with some type from Wittenberg.</li><li><b><i>antequam mare claudatur</b></i> — \"before the sea is closed,\" i.e. before winter ice/storms shut down Baltic shipping between Rostock and Hven: the practical deadline for the consignment.</li><li><b>Lunar eclipse of 7 November 1584.</b> A citation-grade comparison of observed contact-times. <i>partium 26 … 37 … 41</i> = <b>degrees</b> of <i>altitudo</i> (altitude) of <b>Procyon</b> (<i>Procyonis</i>), the reference star used to derive the times. <i>principium Eclipsis … principium totius occultationis … finis totius obscurationis … Tota ex umbra emersit</i> = the eclipse phases (first contact / beginning of totality / end of totality / last contact); times given in <i>h[ora] … ' …\"</i> = hours, minutes, seconds.</li><li><b>Editorial restoration (Dreyer).</b> In <i>medium [totius obscurationis in h. 1 1' 48\", finis] totius obscurationis</i> the bracketed words are the editor's restoration of text lost by eye-skip between the two occurrences of <i>totius obscurationis</i>; kept as printed in the Source block and reproduced (in brackets) in the translation.</li><li><b>Authorities compared.</b> <i>Maestlinum</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631, then at Tübingen/Backnang; later Kepler's teacher); <i>Copernicum</i> = <b>Nicolaus Copernicus</b> (i.e. tables computed from <i>De revolutionibus</i>); <i>Purbachium</i> = <b>Georg Peurbach</b> (1423–1461, the <i>Theoricae novae planetarum</i> / Alfonsine tradition). Brucaeus measures each authority's predicted timing against his own observation.</li><li><b>Crux — <i>scrupulis solum sex</i>.</b> <i>scrupula</i> in this period denotes either <b>minutes of arc</b> or <b>minutes of time</b> (<i>scrupula temporis</i>); since Brucaeus expresses Maestlin's discrepancy as <i>sesquihora</i> (\"an hour and a half\" — time) and Peurbach's as <i>30'</i>, the <i>sex</i> for Copernicus is most naturally minutes <b>of time</b>, but the bare <i>scrupulis</i> does not decide arc vs. time. Rendered \"six minutes [of arc / of time],\" the ambiguity flagged rather than resolved.</li><li><b><i>in Proclo … ad Solis usque motum</b></i> — Brucaeus has read in <b>Proclus</b> (his <i>Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum</i>, the late-antique exposition of Ptolemaic theory) as far as the motion of the Sun, and asks Tycho for his observed <i>aequinoctium solstitiorumque tempora</i> (<b>times of the equinox and solstices</b>) — a request that trades on Tycho's <i>instrumenta exquisite elaborata</i> (\"exquisitely wrought instruments,\" protocol §5.2 <i>instrumenta</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Rostochij … Decemb. 1584</i> = <b>Rostock</b>, December 1584 (O.S.; Denmark and the Lutheran north kept the Julian calendar). <b>Day-figure reading:</b> the MS prints the day as <i>1O.</i> — a figure <b>1</b> followed by a letter-form <b>O</b> rather than a clear <i>10.</i>; the intended day is <b>10 December</b>, agreeing with the catalogue date <i>\"1584. 10. december\"</i> and with the scholarly citation (TBOO VII, 88). The printed <i>1O.</i> is kept in the Source block; \"10 December\" adopted in the header and translation, the orthographic oddity noted here. <i>7. Novembris</i> (the eclipse date) is likewise O.S.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Dreyer's numeral \"30\" (the sum of Joachimsthalers required for the type-casting, together with the quadrats) and corrected \"vendidij\" to Dreyer's \"vendidit\" (TBOO VII p.88).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical content (Myliander type-procurement; the 7 Nov. 1584 eclipse timings) is externally corroborated. Two soft points are flagged, not smoothed: (1) <i>scrupulis solum sex</i> — minutes of arc vs. of time for the Copernicus discrepancy; (2) the day-figure printed <i>1O.</i> (read as 10, per catalogue and the standard TBOO citation).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.88-90 as page images; the Brucaeus-to-Tycho letter runs from p.88 through the first four lines of p.89 ('...Rostochij 10. Decemb. 1584.'); the remainder of pp.89-90 is a different letter (Tycho to Ranzovius, 8 Jan. 1585) and was not needed. Collated the full Latin text word for word against Dreyer, including the entire eclipse-observation paragraph (Procyon altitudes 26/37/41 degrees, the four timed phases 11h6'32\"/12h11'44\"/1h1'48\"/1h51'52\", total emersion 2h55'4\", Maestlinus/Copernicus/Purbachius differences) — every figure there matches exactly. The two departures found are both in the opening paragraph about books and type; the dropped sum ('30') is a genuine lost fact and forces grade C.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's page clearly prints the number '30' (the type-caster Vitus wants nearly 30 Joachimsthalers, together with the quadrats); source_text drops the numeral entirely. The English translator noticed the gap and marked it with a bracketed hedge rather than fabricating a figure, but a scholar consulting this letter for the actual sum would still come away without it. Dreyer prints: <i>quadratis Ioachimicos prope 30 requiri (TBOO VII p.88, line 21)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints the third-person 'vendidit'; source_text has 'vendidij', which is neither Dreyer's word nor a normal long-i rendering of it. The sense (Brucaeus selling Clavius's Gnomonice to Rantzau) is unaffected either way since the whole letter is written in the first person, so the English is not misled, but source_text itself departs from the page. Dreyer prints: <i>RANZOVIO vendidit (TBOO VII p.88, line 9-10)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.88-90. Page 88, lines 9-10 read \"RANZOVIO vendidit\"; lines 20-22 read \"...unâ cum quadratis Ioachimicos prope 30 requiri.\" Both TEXT_ERRORs confirmed and fixed; the English's existing bracketed hedge for the missing numeral was replaced with the restored figure. No other defects found on these three pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15840612001", "next": "brahe-t_15850000001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports the Frankfurt bookseller brought none of the requested books, provides information on printing types available from Myliander, and notes Clavius's Gnomonice was sold to Rantzau.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15841210001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15841211001", "d": "1584-12-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "ck": "Frederik 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Sorø", "t": "King Frederik II → Tycho Brahe — Sorø, 11 December 1584 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XV, 444 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THYGGE BRAHE FICH BREFF NOGENN COMPASSER ANRØRE[N]DIS VT SEQUITUR</p><p>Frederich etc.</p><p>WOR gunst thilforn, Wij kunde Oß andet icke ehrindre, end att Wij nu nogit forleden haffue thil thig offuerantuordet en Wor compaß, som thu selff haffde ladit Oß giøre, huilcken war bleffuen nogit wrichthig, attu therfore Oß thend schulle lade egien giøre thil rette. Ther som nu saa er schet, och samme compas er ferdig giort, tha bede Wij thig, attu Oß thend wforsømmit thilschicker, thersom thu och jcke for:ne compaß haffuer bekommit, tha bede Wij thig och begere, attu wil lade Oß giøre tho compaßer, saadanne som thend anden war, och thett kunstrichs och restist schie kand, saa Wij thennum met thett første mueligt wor kunde bekomme.</p><p>Ther meth etc.<br>Actum Soer, xj decembr: aar 84.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter concerning certain compasses, as follows:</b></p><p>Frederick, etc.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. We cannot but call to mind that we did, some while ago now, hand over to you one of our compasses — which you yourself had had made for us, [and] which had become somewhat inaccurate — so that you were therefore to have it put right again for us. If it has now been so done, and that same compass is finished, then we ask you to send it to us without fail; and if you have not [yet] got the aforesaid compass [back in hand], then we ask and desire you to have two compasses made for us, such as the other one was, and made as artfully and as accurately as can be, so that we may obtain them at the very first that may be possible.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Sorø, 11 December, year [15]84.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), Tycho's royal patron, who in 1576 had granted him the island of Hven. A characteristic instance of the king treating Tycho as his instrument-maker as well as his astronomer.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (Sjællandske Tegnelser). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…NOGENN COMPASSER ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning certain compasses, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2. <i>ANRØRE[N]DIS</i>: the source contracts <i>anrørendes</i> with a tilde for the nasal; expanded in brackets.</li><li><b><i>compaß / compasser</b></i> — rendered \"compass(es).\" <b>Ambiguity flagged:</b> in 1580s Danish royal usage this is most likely a <b>drawing/measuring compass (pair of compasses, dividers)</b> of fine workmanship — the king stresses it be made <i>kunstrigt og rigtigst</i> (\"as artfully and accurately as can be\") and that Tycho had had it made — but the word can also denote a <b>sundial-/magnetic compass-dial</b>. The text does not decide it; \"compass\" is kept and the ambiguity recorded rather than resolved.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1584).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>attu</i> = <i>at du</i>; <i>offuerantuordet</i> = <i>overantvordet</i> (\"handed over/delivered\"); <i>wrichthig</i> = <i>urigtig</i> (\"inaccurate, out of true\"); <i>wforsømmit</i> = <i>uforsømmet</i> (\"without fail/neglect\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>kunstrichs</i> = <i>kunstrigt</i> (\"artfully, skilfully\"); <i>restist</i> = <i>rigtigst</i> (\"most accurately/correctly\"); <i>thennum</i> = <i>dennem</i> (\"them\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Soer, xj decembr. aar 84</i> = \"Done at <b>Sorø</b>, 11 December [15]84\" (O.S.); <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely; the one open point is the referent of <i>compass</i> (drawing-compass vs. compass-dial), flagged above rather than silently decided.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV p.28 page image in full. Collated the recollection of the compass previously handed over for repair, the request to send the repaired compass without fail, the fallback request for two new compasses made as accurately as possible, and the Sorø/11 December 1584 date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word (a momentary misreading of a long-s as 'f' in 'thersom' was corrected on review and is not a real discrepancy). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade B is upgraded to A on full collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XV, 444."], "th": {"label": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15850526001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Requests the return of a compass given to Tycho for repair, or if unavailable, asks that two new compasses of the same quality be made.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15841211001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850000001", "d": "1585-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — [Rostock], [1585]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO NOBILISSIMOQUE VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, DOMINO ET AMICO PLURIMUM COLENDO.<br>S. D.</p><p>MITTO tibi Chartam et Typos, quos Witeberga accepi. Papyri singulas Risas aestimat solidis 30. Hisce vasis inclusam ad me miserunt, si quid damni acceperit, non est, quod me culpes. Reliquos Typos DAVID auriga se allaturum, ubi fusi fuerint, promisit. Poscit in pondo 100 Ioachimum unum. In universum pro pecunia transmissa typorum fusori et typis huc transvehendis exigit 4 Thaleros. Quid insuper fieri velis significabis</p><p>[HENRICUS BRUCAeUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, the eminent mathematician, his lord and friend to be greatly honoured.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>I am sending you the paper and the type which I obtained from Wittenberg. He values the single reams of paper at 30 shillings each. They sent it to me enclosed in these casks; if it has taken any harm, there is no reason for you to blame me. The carter David has promised that he will bring the rest of the type once it has been cast. He demands one Joachimsthaler for every 100 [units] by weight. In all, for the money sent to the type-founder and for conveying the type here, he requires 4 thalers. You will let [me] know what you wish to be done besides.</p><p>[Heinrich Brucaeus.]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Brucäus; 1531–1593), the Flemish-born professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, an early correspondent and supporter of Tycho. The letter was therefore written from (or near) <b>Rostock</b>; the place is not stated in the text and is supplied editorially in brackets.</li><li><b>Recipient / dating.</b> The salutation addresses <i>D. Tychoni Brahe, mathematico eximio</i> — the body is unambiguously a letter to Tycho. The dataset metadata carries a stray field (<i>Frederik II / Sorø / Sjællandske Tegnelser</i>) that belongs to the adjacent register entry, not to this letter; it is disregarded. The dataset assigns the year <b>[1585]</b> with the note <i>\"empty\"</i> (i.e. an undated letter editorially placed in 1585); no dateline survives in the text. The header therefore gives \"[1585]\" in brackets as an editorial placement, not an as-written date. <b>O.S./N.S. cannot be determined</b> since no day or month is transmitted.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66, described in the source as an <i>Afskrift</i> (a later copy/apograph), not the original.</li><li><b>Commercial / printing terms.</b></li><li><i>Chartam … Papyri … Risas</i> — <i>charta</i> and <i>papyrus</i> both denote <b>paper</b>; <i>risa</i> (= <i>risma</i>, ream) is a <b>ream</b> of paper. \"the single reams … at 30 shillings each\" renders <i>singulas Risas … solidis 30</i>.</li><li><i>Typos … typorum fusori … fusi fuerint</i> — <i>typi</i> here are cast metal <b>printing types</b> (founts of type), and <i>typorum fusor</i> is the <b>typefounder</b>; <i>fusi fuerint</i> = \"once they have been cast/founded.\" This is type for a press, consistent with Tycho's establishment of his own printing operation on Hven. (<i>Typi</i> could in principle mean engraved figures/woodblocks, but <i>fusi</i>/<i>fusor</i> — casting/founder — fixes the sense as cast type.)</li><li><i>Witeberga</i> = <b>Wittenberg</b> (ablative of origin, \"from Wittenberg\"); the paper and type were procured there.</li><li><b>Money.</b> <i>solidis 30</i> = <b>30 shillings (solidi)</b> — <i>solidus</i> kept as the period accounting term, glossed \"shilling.\" <i>Ioachimum unum</i> = <b>one Joachimsthaler</b> (a <i>Joachimicus</i>, the Joachimsthal silver coin, the original \"thaler\"). <i>4 Thaleros</i> = <b>4 thalers</b>. The two coin-names are treated as distinct denominations and not silently merged.</li><li><b><i>in pondo 100 Ioachimum unum</b></i> — literally \"one Joachimsthaler per 100 in weight.\" The unit of weight is not specified in the text (pounds is the natural supplement but is not written); rendered \"for every 100 [units] by weight,\" with the bracket marking the unsupplied unit rather than supplying \"pounds.\" <b>Flagged</b>: the weight-unit is genuinely absent from the source.</li><li><b><i>Hisce vasis inclusam … miserunt</b></i> — \"they sent it to me enclosed in these casks/vessels\"; <i>vas</i> (pl. <i>vasa</i>) is a container/cask for shipping. The participle <i>inclusam</i> (fem. sing.) agrees with <i>chartam</i> (the paper), so \"it\" = the paper consignment.</li><li><b><i>DAVID auriga</b></i> — \"the carter/wagoner David,\" the named carrier engaged to bring the remaining type; <i>auriga</i> = driver/carter.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5). The closing <i>significabis</i> (\"you will let [me] know\") ends the transmitted body without a <i>Vale</i>; the subscription <i>[Henricus Brucaeus]</i> stands in editorial brackets in the source (the opening \"[\" is unclosed in the transcription — a copyist/transcription artifact), reproduced as printed in the Source block.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> One negation in the body — <i>non est, quod me culpes</i> (\"there is no reason for you to blame me\") — reproduced as a single negation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English recast from three itemised charges to the two the Latin actually lists ('the money sent to the type-founder' / 'conveying the type here'), per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.91; no change to the Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the commercial sense is clear; the two flagged soft points are (i) the unspecified weight-unit in <i>in pondo 100 Ioachimum unum</i> (left bracketed, not supplied) and (ii) the editorial-only date [1585] with no surviving dateline (style O.S./N.S. indeterminable). No reading is smoothed over these.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.91-92 (page images). The Brucaeus letter (folio 106r†, undated within the text, consistent with the [1585] catalogue date) is printed complete on p.91, matching source_text word-for-word. Checked every sum and quantity (30 solidi/ream, 100 lb/Joachimsthaler, 4 thalers total) and both named persons (David the carter, Henricus Brucaeus) against the translation — sums and names all correct, but the final accounting sentence mis-parses the genitive 'typorum fusoris', turning a two-item total into an ambiguous three-item one. Does not change the letter's substance (paper, type, and freight arrangements) so graded low-severity, but it is a real defect, so the letter cannot be A.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). The Latin lists two charges totalling 4 thalers — (1) the money remitted to the type-founder ('pecunia transmissa typorum fusoris', with 'typorum fusoris' a genitive specifying whose money it is) and (2) conveying the type here — but the English splits this into three items ('the money sent on' / 'the typefounder' / 'conveying the type here'), implying an extra, unspecified payment alongside the founder's fee. At: “In all, for the money sent on, [and] for the typefounder and for conveying the type here, he requires 4 thalers.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the Latin wording against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.91, confirming current_latin's 'typorum fusori/fusoris' reading and the letter's two-charge structure. The fix is confined to the English item count; the Latin was left untouched per the MISTRANSLATION repair rule.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15841210001", "next": "brahe-t_15850404001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends paper and printing type obtained from Wittenberg, giving prices and delivery details, and notes the carter will bring remaining type once cast.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850108001", "d": "1585-01-08", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Uraniborg (Hven), 8 January 1585 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ET MAGNIFICO VIRO D. HENRICO RANZOVIO, REGIAe MAIESTATIS DANIAe IN HOLSATIA ET SLESVICIA VICARIO ET CONSILIARIO PRUDENTISSIMO, PRAeFECTO ARCIS SEGEBERGAe, ET DOMINO IN BREDENBERGA AMICO SUO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>OBILISSIME et Eruditissime Domine HENRICE RANZOWI, fuit hisce diebus apud me filius tuus GERARDUS RANZOW, Praeses arcis Kroneburgensis, Amicus meus singularis et vicinus exoptatissimus; cumque is prae ceteris nostri aevi Nobilibus Iuvenibus non saltem rerum Mundanarum multiplici et late quaesita Experientia, sed etiam Ingenio et Iudicio praeeminenti valeat, Moribusque insuper admodum laudatis praeditus sit, ipsius conversatione plurimum afficiebar eique tum varias observandis sideribus a me ingenti labore et sumtu constructas Machinas, tum etiam Pyronomici exercitij diversa Artificia lubens ostendi; inter alia vero cum in Typographiam meam, quam hac aestate praeterita parari curavi, illum deducerem, ut Typos et rationem nostram imprimendarum chartarum perspiceret, conquestus sum illi, papyrum impressoriam mihi pro absolutis laboribus edendis deficere, et hyemem obstaculo esse, quominus eam nunc satis opportune nancisci queam; adeo ut ne pro quodam exiguo huius inchoati anni Calendario Astrologico imprimendo mihi charta suppetat, vel alicubi iusto pretio hîc in Dania venalis existat; nedum ut alijs maioribus a me confectis Operibus, quae maximam papyri copiam postulant, sufficiens acquiratur. Subiunxit itaque ille, te Patrem ipsius molendinam habere papyraceam nec dubitare, si negocium hoc tibi constaret, quin lubenter pro Calendario isto excudendo mihi sufficientem Papyrum concessurus sis, suaque sponte (ut est Ingenio bonis conatibus admodum faventi) obtulit, se tibi hoc nomine scripturum, quod et statim hîc praesens praestitit; literasque tibi transmittendas tradidit, quas nunc per proprium nuntium, unum ex meis familiaribus, IOHANNEM BERNSSÖN nomine (cui ut fidem habeas velim) perferendas curo, ut eo certius ad tuam Generositatem mature perveniant, negociumque hoc eo rectius perficiatur; rogoque peramanter ut, si in Officina tua Papyracea reperiatur impressoria Papyrus meis votis conveniens, mihi de ea 20 vel 25 Riss, ut vocant, concedere velis. Pretium solitum tuae Officinae praefecto, quam primum de eo certior factus fuero, lubens transinittam. Continebit Calendarium illud, ubi impressum fuerit, circiter 6 quaterniones, ut ob id 25 Rissij pro 2000 Exemplaribus imprimendis requirantur. Pro reliquis vero maioribus a me absolutis Astronomicis Operibus quam primum, Deo volente, in lucem edendis, multo copiosiorem, quae requiritur, papyrum in hoc ipso Freto Helsingorae facile habiturus sum, cum vernum tempus navigationem consuetam reduxerit. Spero autem tuam Magnificentiam meis defiderijs hac in parte, quantum in te est, interea non defuturam, et dabo operam, ut hoc Officij genus in me non frustra collocatum experiare. Mittam vero brevi literas impressas tibi alijsque Nobilibus et eruditis Amicis inscriptas, quamprimum omnes absolutae fuerint, ut Typos nostros videas, sique illi placuerint, et quid habeas, quod in lucem edere ex Astrologicis rebus decreveris, lubenter ut in mea Officina excudatur, permittam, saltem ut ipse requisitam Papyrum addas. Et si quacunque alia in re tuae Generositati unquam aliquid per me quod gratum sit praestari queat, efficiam ut me facilem et promptissimum habeas. Vale cum charissima coniuge totaque domo, vir Amplissime pariter et Eruditissime, meque tui studiosissimum redamare non graveris.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi in Insula porthmi DaniciHuena die 8. Ianuarij Anno 1585.<br>Tuae generositati addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 90; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si catalogum Astrologicorum tuorum librorum ex amplissima tua Bibliotheca descriptum mihi transmittere non displicuerit, rem feceris longe gratissimam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and magnificent gentleman Master Heinrich Rantzau, the most prudent viceroy and councillor of the Royal Majesty of Denmark in Holstein and Schleswig, governor of the castle of Segeberg, and lord in Breitenburg, his friend to be esteemed — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble and most learned Master Heinrich Rantzau, there was with me in these days past your son Gerhard Rantzau, governor of the castle of Kronborg, my singular friend and a most longed-for neighbour. And since he, beyond the other noble young men of our age, is strong not only in a manifold and widely-gathered experience of worldly affairs, but also in pre-eminent talent and judgement, and is moreover endowed with most praiseworthy character, I was greatly taken with his company; and I gladly showed him both the various machines [instruments] I have constructed at vast labour and cost for observing the stars, and likewise the diverse devices of the pyronomic exercise [the chemical art]. Among other things, indeed, when I led him into my printing-house, which I had seen to having set up this past summer, so that he might examine the types and our method of printing sheets, I complained to him that printing-paper was lacking to me for publishing my finished works, and that winter was an obstacle preventing me from now obtaining it conveniently enough — so much so that not even for a certain small Astrological Calendar of this now-beginning year that is to be printed is there paper enough at my disposal, nor is any to be had for sale here in Denmark at a fair price; let alone that sufficient be acquired for the other, greater works completed by me, which demand a very great quantity of paper. He therefore added that you, his father, have a paper-mill, and did not doubt that, if this business were made clear to you, you would gladly grant me sufficient paper for printing that Calendar; and of his own accord (as he is of a disposition very much favouring good endeavours) he offered to write to you on this account, which he did at once, here present; and he gave me the letter to be conveyed to you, which I now have carried by a special messenger, one of my household, John Bernssøn by name (to whom I would wish you to give credence), that it may the more surely reach Your Generosity in good time, and this business be the more rightly accomplished. And I ask most affectionately that, if in your paper-works there be found printing-paper agreeable to my wishes, you would be willing to grant me 20 or 25 reams, as they call them, of it. The usual price I will gladly send to the overseer of your works as soon as I am informed of it. That Calendar, when it has been printed, will contain about 6 quaternions, so that on that account 25 reams are required for printing 2000 copies. But for the remaining greater Astronomical Works completed by me, to be brought into the light as soon as possible, God willing, I shall easily have the much more copious paper that is required here in this very Sound at Elsinore, when the spring season has brought back the customary sailing. I hope, however, that Your Magnificence will not in the meantime fail my desires in this matter, so far as in you lies; and I will take pains that you may find this kind of service not bestowed upon me in vain. And shortly I shall send you, and other noble and learned friends, letters printed [and] inscribed [to you], as soon as they are all finished, that you may see our types; and if they please you, and you have anything among astrological matters that you have resolved to bring into the light, I will gladly permit it to be printed in my workshop — provided only that you yourself supply the requisite paper. And if in any other matter whatsoever anything that is welcome can ever be rendered to Your Generosity through me, I will see to it that you find me obliging and most ready. Farewell, with your dearest wife and your whole house, gentleman both most ample and most learned, and do not be unwilling to return the love of me who am most devoted to you.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, on the island Hven of the Danish strait, on the 8th day of January, the year 1585.<br>Most attached to Your Generosity,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If it should not displease you to send me a catalogue of your Astrological books, copied out from your most ample Library, you will do me a most welcome thing indeed.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the Danish crown's <i>Statthalter</i> (viceroy/royal governor) in the duchies of Holstein and Schleswig, humanist, astrologer, and one of the great book-collectors and patrons of the age; lord of Breitenburg (<i>Bredenberga</i>) and governor of Segeberg castle. Tycho courted him as a patron and conduit to the wider learned world.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Gerardus Ranzow</i> = <b>Gerhard (Gert) Rantzau</b> (1558–1627), Heinrich's son, then governor (<i>Praeses</i>) of <b>Kronborg</b> castle at Elsinore — Tycho's near neighbour across the Sound; he later succeeded his father as viceroy. <i>Iohannes Bernssön</i> = <b>Johann (Hans) Bernssøn</b>, a member of Tycho's household (<i>familiaris</i>) sent as the special messenger; otherwise unidentified.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>arx Kroneburgensis</i> = <b>Kronborg</b> (Elsinore/Helsingør); <i>arx Segebergae</i> = <b>Segeberg</b> castle (Holstein); <i>Bredenberga</i> = <b>Breitenburg</b> (Rantzau's seat in Holstein); <i>Fretum / Helsingorae</i> = <b>the Sound (Øresund) at Elsinore</b>; <i>Uraniburgum in Insula porthmi Danici Huena</i> = <b>Uraniborg on the island of Hven</b> in the Danish strait. The corpus biography confirms the episode: Gert Rantzau visited Hven and told Tycho his father's paper-mill could supply paper, and Tycho intended to print the calendar in 2000 copies (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>).</li><li><b>Technical / craft terms.</b> <i>Machinas … observandis sideribus … constructas</i> — Tycho's astronomical <b>instruments</b> (here <i>machinae</i>, rendered \"machines [instruments]\" per glossary §5.2). <i>Pyronomici exercitij … Artificia</i> — \"the devices of the pyronomic exercise,\" i.e. Tycho's <b>chemistry/alchemy</b> (\"pyronomy,\" the fire-art) carried on in the Uraniborg laboratory; glossed rather than modernised. <i>Typographia / Officina / Typos / chartas imprimendas</i> — Tycho's own <b>printing-house</b> at Uraniborg, its <b>types</b>, and the <b>sheets</b> to be printed.</li><li><b>Paper terms.</b> <i>papyrus impressoria</i> = <b>printing-paper</b>; <i>molendina papyracea / Officina Papyracea</i> = <b>paper-mill / paper-works</b>; <i>Riss / Rissij</i> (\"ut vocant,\" \"as they call them\") = the German loanword <i>Ries</i> = <b>ream</b> (a paper quantity, here kept as \"reams\"); <i>quaterniones</i> = <b>quaternions</b> (gatherings of four sheets / eight leaves). The arithmetic in the letter (≈6 quaternions per copy → 25 reams for 2000 copies) is reproduced as written, not recomputed.</li><li><b>The Calendar and the \"greater works.\"</b> <i>Calendarium … Astrologia[e]</i> — a small <b>astrological calendar</b> for 1585; the <i>maiora Astronomica Opera</i> \"completed by me … to be brought into the light\" are Tycho's projected major publications (the instrument and <i>De mundi</i> volumes), here described prospectively with his own hedge <i>Deo volente</i> (\"God willing\"), preserved.</li><li><b>Formulae (§5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting\"); <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\"; <i>Tuae generositati addictiss.</i> = \"Most attached to Your Generosity\"; <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand.\" The honorific <i>Generositas / Magnificentia Tua</i> is rendered \"Your Generosity / Your Magnificence.\"</li><li><b>Orthographic notes (Source block preserves the printed forms).</b> The salutation word opens <i>OBILISSIME</i> — a dropped/decorative initial <b>[N]</b> for <i>NOBILISSIME</i> (\"most noble\"), expanded in the translation. <i>transinittam</i> (line: \"Pretium … lubens transinittam\") is a printer's misprint for <i>transmittam</i> (\"I will send\"); translated as <i>transmittam</i>. <i>defiderijs</i> = <i>desideriis</i> (\"desires\"); <i>-ij/-ijs</i> throughout is the period spelling of <i>-ii/-iis</i>. In the dateline <i>DaniciHuena</i> the edition prints <i>Danici</i> and <i>Huena</i> run together without a space; read as <i>Danici Huena</i> (\"…of the Danish [strait], Hven\").</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 8. Ianuarij Anno 1585</i> = <b>8 January 1585</b>, Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The catalogue label (\"1585. 8. januar. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. No date is \"corrected.\"</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VII, p. 90 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'Calendario Astrologia' to 'Calendario Astrologico' per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.89 (agreement of adjective with 'Calendario'); no change to the English, which already read correctly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical episode is independently corroborated. Soft points, each flagged rather than smoothed: the printed <i>transinittam</i> (a manifest typo for <i>transmittam</i>); the decorative dropped initial <i>[N]obilissime</i>; and the precise sense of <i>Pyronomici exercitij</i> (\"the pyronomic exercise\" = Tycho's alchemical/chemical work), kept as a period term. No reading affects the substance of the request.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 collation run (postscript restored); residue only a manifest typo, a dropped decorative initial, and one glossed period term.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.89-91 as page images (Read pages=\"1-3\"); the letter runs p.89 (from 'GENEROSO ET MAGNIFICO...') through p.91 top ('Si catalogum...'). Collated the full letter against Dreyer, including the postscript (which our source_text correctly flags as restored from Dreyer p.90 and which does match Dreyer exactly), every number (20 or 25 reams/Riss, 6 quaternions, 2000 copies), every name (Gerardus Ranzow/Kronborg, Iohannes Bernsson), and the closing date '8. Ianuarij Anno 1585'. The English translation itself is faithful throughout and correctly renders the Calendar as 'Astrological' either way. The single finding is a TEXT_ERROR in our own house Latin transcription (Astrologia vs. Dreyer's Astrologico) — per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though its practical impact on sense is negligible, hence verdict MINOR rather than SERIOUS.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our house source_text reads the noun 'Astrologia' where Dreyer's page image clearly prints the adjective 'Astrologico' (agreeing with 'Calendario'); a miskeyed/corrupted word in the source transcription, not a translation fault. Dreyer prints: <i>lendario Aftrologico imprimendo (TBOO VII p.89, line ~29-30: \"...huius inchoati anni Ca-lendario Aftrologico imprimendo mihi charta fuppetat...\")</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.89-91, all 3 pages read). P.89 l.29-30 clearly prints 'Ca-lendario Aftrologico imprimendo', confirming the source_text's 'Astrologia' was a miskeyed noun for the adjective. The postscript on p.90 ('Si catalogum Aftrologicorum...') matches current_latin's restored postscript exactly. No other cruces found on the three pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15860323001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870117001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports the recent visit of Gerhard Rantzau to Uraniborg, praises his talents and character, and describes showing him the astronomical instruments and the new printing house.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850108001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850404001", "d": "1585-04-04", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Holstein, Palm Sunday 1585 [O.S.] (catalogued 4 April 1585)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>LITERAS tuas, amantissime TYCHO, quibus Calendarij a tuo domestico Studioso edendi mentionem fecisti, accepi et iam Typos fusos a VITO miseram unâ cum libris illius Florentini, adiectis schedulis duabus, una VITI, altera aurigae Witebergensis, quibus de precio tibi constabit. Miratus autem sum, te potioribus intentum permittere, ut talia in tua Typographia edantur, quae trita sunt et vulgaria, in quibus si quis aëris mutationes praedicendas suscipiat, paene ridiculum sese toti mundo exponit. Et tot a vero aberrantes praedictiones effecerunt, ut nugarum mendaciorumque eiuscemodi libri habeantur. Nec credo fuisse quemquam, qui huius hyemis levitatem et siccitatem praedixerit, cuius constitutio prope verna habenda est, si frigoris imbecillitatem spectes. Et quam ridiculum sese praebuit quidam Astrologaster, qui in suo Calendario Martio magna frigora et nives minatur, adeo fisus suis praedictionibus, ut si eventus falleret, iam artem nullam crederet. Et ego arbitror, veris et irrefutabilibus rationibus posse demonstrari, incertam plane esse hanc de aëris mutationibus praedictionem, praeter anni tempora, quae ut plurimum suas constitutiones servant. Sed tu forte, mi TYCHO, alias rationes habes, cur haec a tuis Discipulis fieri permittas, etiamsi tu ipse non multum tribuas eiuscemodi praedictionibus, quarum difficultatem, immo paene impossibilitatem ipsemet satis nosti, quod et in familiari nostro colloquio a te audivisse recordor, cum alias etiam Astrologica vaticinia, uti multi alias solent, non pluris facias, quam par est. Credo itaque, te sic indulgere tuis Discipulis, qui prognosticis annuis, more recepto, conficiendis delectantur, ut habeant, quo graviora studia leniant, fortassis etiam aliquid praeter communem formam a te hauserunt. Si sic est, prodeat Calendarium illud in publicum, et discamus inde experiamurque, quae prius nos latuerunt. Scio enim te nil vulgare et tritum moliri, neque levia tuis concedere. Plura non possum, urgente nuntio.</p><p>Bene vale, et ut hoc in genere nobis sis Apollo, opto.<br>Datae in Holsatia ipso die Palmarum 1585.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I have received your letter, most loving Tycho, in which you made mention of a calendar [almanac] to be published by your resident student; and I had already sent the founts of type [Typos fusos] from Vitus, together with the books of that Florentine, with two notes [schedulae] added — one from Vitus, the other from the Wittenberg carter — by which the price will be clear to you. But I was astonished that you, intent on weightier matters, should permit such things to be published at your press as are hackneyed and commonplace, in which, if anyone undertakes to predict the changes of the weather, he exposes himself to the whole world as well-nigh ridiculous. And so many predictions straying from the truth have brought it about that books of this kind are held to be trifles and lies. Nor do I believe there was anyone who predicted the mildness and dryness of this winter, whose constitution is to be reckoned almost spring-like, if you regard the feebleness of the cold. And how ridiculous did a certain astrologaster make himself, who in his calendar threatens great frosts and snows for March, so trusting in his predictions that, if the event should prove him wrong, he would believe there to be no science [art] at all [in it]. And I, for my part, judge that it can be demonstrated by true and irrefutable arguments that this prediction concerning the changes of the weather is plainly uncertain — apart from the seasons of the year, which for the most part keep their [own] constitutions. But perhaps, my Tycho, you have other reasons why you permit these things to be done by your pupils, even if you yourself do not attach much weight to predictions of this kind, the difficulty — nay, the well-nigh impossibility — of which you yourself know well enough; which I recall having heard from you, too, in our familiar conversation, since you do not — as many others are otherwise wont to do — set more store by astrological prophecies than is fitting. I believe, therefore, that you indulge your pupils thus — who take delight in compiling annual prognostics, after the received custom — so that they may have something with which to relieve their heavier studies; perhaps too they have drawn from you something beyond the common form. If it is so, let that calendar [Calendarium] come forth into public, and let us learn from it and try out the things that were hidden from us before. For I know that you devise nothing common and hackneyed, and grant nothing trifling to your pupils. I can [write] no more, as the messenger presses.</p><p>Fare you well; and I pray that in this kind [of thing] you may be an Apollo to us.<br>Given in Holstein, on Palm Sunday itself, 1585.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1531–1593), professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, a Lutheran scholar of Flemish origin; Tycho's friend and one of his early academic mentors, with whom he maintained a long technical correspondence. The letter is preserved among those Tycho gathered for the <i>Epistolae</i> (1568–1587).</li><li><b>Heading.</b> <i>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE</i> — \"Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe,\" the editorial/archival rubric of the copy; kept as the salutation line.</li><li><b>Subject — the <i>Calendarium</i> / annual prognostic.</b> The letter concerns an almanac (<i>Calendarium</i>; <i>prognostica annua</i>) that one of Tycho's resident students (<i>domesticus Studiosus … Discipuli</i>) was to print at the Uraniborg press (<i>tua Typographia</i>). Brucaeus's whole drift is a friendly remonstrance: weather-prediction almanacs are <i>trita et vulgaria</i> (\"hackneyed and commonplace\"), beneath Tycho's press; he is surprised Tycho permits them, while granting that Tycho himself sets little store by such forecasting. Tycho's press did issue student prognostications in this period (e.g. those of his <i>famuli</i>), so the reference is to a real publishing activity, not a hypothetical.</li><li><b>Persons named in the consignment.</b> <i>VITO / VITI</i> = <b>Vitus</b> (Veit), evidently an agent/founder at or near Wittenberg who supplied <b>type</b> (<i>Typos fusos</i>, \"cast/founded types\"); <i>aurigae Witebergensis</i> = \"the Wittenberg carter/wagoner,\" the carrier who conveyed the consignment. <i>libris illius Florentini</i> = \"the books of that Florentine\" — an unidentified Florentine (bookseller or author) whose books accompanied the type; left unidentified rather than guessed. <i>schedulis duabus</i> = \"two notes/slips\" (here invoices or price-memoranda), <i>una VITI, altera aurigae</i> \"one from Vitus, the other from the carter,\" <i>quibus de precio tibi constabit</i> \"by which the price will be clear to you.\"</li><li><b>Astrology / meteorology (technical sense).</b> <i>aëris mutationes praedicere</i> = \"to predict the changes of the weather/air\" — the standard early-modern term for almanac weather-forecasting (astro-meteorology), not astronomy proper. <i>anni tempora … suas constitutiones servant</i> = \"the seasons of the year keep their own constitutions\" (<i>constitutio</i> in the medical-meteorological sense of the prevailing temperament of a season); rendered \"constitution\" consistently. <i>Astrologica vaticinia</i> = \"astrological prophecies.\"</li><li><b><i>Astrologaster</b></i> — a contemptuous coinage, \"petty/quack astrologer\" (the <i>-aster</i> suffix = a poor imitation, as <i>grammaticaster</i>, <i>philosophaster</i>); kept as <b>\"astrologaster\"</b> with the pejorative force flagged rather than smoothed to a neutral \"astrologer.\"</li><li><b><i>Calendario Martio</b></i> — \"in his calendar for March\" (i.e. the March entry/forecast of the offending astrologer's almanac), not a calendar <i>of</i> Mars; rendered \"in his calendar … for March.\"</li><li><b><i>artem nullam crederet</b></i> — <i>ars</i> here = \"science / art [of prediction]\"; \"he would believe there to be no science at all [in it],\" i.e. would abandon faith in the whole art if his forecast failed.</li><li><b>Crux: <i>Calcearium illud</i>.</b> <i>prodeat <b>Calcearium</b> illud in publicum</i> — literally \"let that shoe-leather / cobbler's-stuff (<i>calcearium</i>) come forth into public.\" <i>Calcearium</i> (from <i>calceus</i>, \"shoe\") is not a standard word; the most natural reading is a deliberately dismissive/jocular tag for the almanac just described (\"that cobblery,\" i.e. that pieced-together job of work), parallel to <i>nugae</i> \"trifles\" above. It is <b>not</b> securely established, however, that this is the intended sense; the word may conceal a proper name, a title, or a copyist's corruption in the Vienna copy (<i>Afskrift</i>). The dismissive sense is given in the translation with the Latin retained in brackets, and the reading is flagged here rather than silently normalised. <b>This is the named crux that holds the grade to [C].</b></li><li><b><i>ut … nobis sis Apollo</b></i> — \"that you may be an Apollo to us\": a closing compliment, Apollo as god of both medicine and the [astral] arts; Brucaeus wishes Tycho to be their authority \"in this kind [of thing]\" (<i>hoc in genere</i>), i.e. in setting astrology/almanac-making on a sound footing.</li><li><b>Epistolary formula.</b> <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Plura non possum, urgente nuntio</i> = \"I can [write] no more, as the messenger presses\" — a stock close signalling haste.</li><li><b>Dateline &amp; calendar.</b> <i>Datae in Holsatia ipso die Palmarum 1585</i> = \"Given in <b>Holstein</b>, on <b>Palm Sunday</b> itself, 1585.\" Brucaeus, of Rostock, wrote while in Holstein (the duchy south of Denmark). <i>Die Palmarum</i> = Palm Sunday, the Sunday before Easter; the dateline gives the moveable feast, <b>not</b> a civil day-of-month. In the Julian (Old Style) calendar that Brucaeus, as a Rostock/Holstein Lutheran, used in 1585, Easter fell on 18 April O.S., placing <b>Palm Sunday on 11 April 1585 O.S.</b> The dataset `label` (\"1585. 4. april. Holsten\") assigns <b>4 April</b>, which matches <i>neither</i> the Old-Style nor the New-Style Palm Sunday for 1585 (N.S. Palm Sunday = 14 April); 4 April 1585 O.S. was a Sunday but the <i>fifth</i> Sunday in Lent (Judica/Passion Sunday), not Palm Sunday. <b>The discrepancy is flagged, not reconciled:</b> the header leads with the as-written feast-date (\"Palm Sunday 1585 [O.S.]\") and appends the catalogued \"(catalogued 4 April 1585)\"; the basis of the catalogue's \"4 April\" is not recoverable from the letter text and is left unresolved. No date is silently converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12, high severity).</b> Latin: 'Calcearium' corrected to 'Calendarium' throughout its single occurrence, per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 92 — the letter's subject is an almanac, not shoe-leather. English: the corresponding clause corrected from 'that shoe-leather' to 'that calendar', and the earlier clause on astrological prophecies corrected to restore the sense that Tycho is the exception to what 'many others' customarily do (overvalue such prophecies), not a fellow example of restraint.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin body and its argument (a remonstrance against Tycho's press issuing weather-almanacs) are rendered securely; the grade is held to [C] by one genuine lexical crux, <b><i>Calcearium illud</b></i> (jocular \"that cobblery\"/almanac vs. possible proper name or copyist's corruption), flagged above and left unsmoothed, and secondarily by the <i>die Palmarum</i> / catalogued \"4 April\" dating discrepancy, which is flagged rather than resolved.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 105–106: <i>Calcearium illud</i> is printed unemended (the word is the witness reading, not a downstream corruption); jocular-almanac vs proper-name sense still open. Dreyer's own running header dates the letter '4 April 1585' against the as-written <i>die Palmarum</i> (= 11 April 1585 O.S.) — the divergence is internal to the edition of record. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the type-founts/Vitus passage, the astrologaster-and-weather-prediction critique, the pupils'-prognostics defense) against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.91-92 as page images. Confirmed a source-text word corruption (Calcearium for Calendarium) that directly produces nonsensical English, and a contrast-flip mistranslation in the 'many others' clause. Both defects change what a scholar would take away from the letter.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Dreyer clearly prints 'Calendarium' (the calendar/almanac that is the letter's entire subject — set up in the opening line 'Calendarij ... edendi mentionem fecisti'). Our source_text has the nonword 'Calcearium', which the translator then rendered literally as 'shoe-leather', producing a baffling non sequitur in a letter that is otherwise coherently about an almanac. Dreyer prints: <i>prodeat Calendarium illud in publicum</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The Latin's 'uti multi alias solent' borrows its verb from the main clause and means 'as many others are otherwise accustomed [to value them more than is fitting]' — i.e. Brucaeus is praising Tycho as an exception to a common failing. The English reads as though the 'many others' also show the same restraint, inverting the compliment into a banal generalization. At: “since you also set no more store than is fitting by astrological prophecies, as many otherwise are wont to do”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VII pp.91-93 page images. 'Calendarium' is unambiguous on p.92 and directly continuous with the letter's opening subject (a student's almanac). The mistranslation defect concerns English phrasing only (the Latin source_text itself was already correct and unchanged); the corrected clause follows the Latin's word order and the elliptical-verb reading given in the audit's own diagnosis, staying close to the source without adding interpretive colour beyond restoring the correct logical direction of the compliment.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15850000001", "next": "brahe-t_15850623001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports sending the type and books, and remonstrates against Tycho permitting a commonplace almanac with weather predictions to be published at his press.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850404001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850518001", "d": "1585-05-18", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 14 April 1586 [O.S.] (catalogued 18 May 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTVTE SAPIENTIA ACA INGENIOSA ERVDITAQVE DOCTRINA, CLARISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNVDSTRVP, EQVITI DANO, MATHEMATICO NOSTRO SECULO PRAeSTANTISSIMOA, ETC. DOMINO SUO CUM OBSERUANTIA COLENDO, ETC. S.P.</p><p>MAXIMA Illustrissimus Princeps noster affectus est laetitia Literis tuis, Vir Nobilissime et Doctissime, Mathematicorum praestantissime, tum quod reconditiorem Astronomiae Scientiam ac Regium Ingeniosorum Instrumentorum apparatum, acutissimumqueA de Obseruationibus longe aliter instituendis iudicium continerent; tum quod abs te, quem propter summam in Astronomicis rebus peritiam summe diligit, proficiscerentur. Etsi autem Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudo seorsim Literis tuis est responsura; mihi tamen mandauit, vt et ipse, de Mathematicis praesertim, tuae excellent. responderem. Ne igitur longis vtar ambagibus, cum Illustrissimus Princeps Scriptum tuum de Cometa nancisceretur, mihi id inspicere non licuit, Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudine prohibente, donec et ipse meum de eodem Cometa scriptum attulissem, in quibus cum Obseruationes in tam longe dissitis locis institutas, sibi inuicem ad minutum vsque respondere videremus, dici vix potest, quanto simus gaudio perfusi. Quo igitur et tu eandem concordantiam videre possis, tibi vicissim, meum de eodem Cometa Scriptum transmitto, praesertim cum Capite V. contineat disputationem de Refractione Stellarum, cuius tu in Literis tuis mentionem facis, quanquam nondum sit absolutum, deficientibus nimirum in fine tribus Capitibus, quae propter occupationes absoluere non potui. Quod si quaedam differentia in locis Cometae constitutis reperitur, ea esse potest, et propter locorum, in quibus nostras Obseruationes habuimus, disiunctionem, et propter loca Stellarum fixarum non eodem forte modo assumta, et propter diuersam Zodiaci obliquitatem, quam ego in meis Obseruationibus assumsi 23 gr. 30 min. tantam nimirum quantam tot annos Illust. Princeps noster adinuenit. Quanquam autemA Instrumenta in ijs Obseruationibus non adeo, vt nunc, fuerunt exacta, nec etiam Parallaxeos aut Refractionis ratio habita, malui tamen Obseruationibus, quam Tabularum annotatis respondentem Zodiaci obliquitatem assumere, praesertim cum ipse adinuenerim, Obseruationes eas non multum a Veritate abesse. Quanquam ipse Solstitialem Solis Altitudinem in aestate propter ventosam et nubilosam Aëris constitutionem nondum exacte obseruare potuerim. Etsi veroA non dubito, quin tua, quam asseris Zodiaci obliquitas sit vera: eam tamen et ego, DEO benignum successum, benignamque Aëris constitutionem largiente, diligentissime obseruabo, vt ita Veritas diuersis diuersorum locorum fulcita Obseruationibus, eo maiorem fidem apud posteritatemA mereatur. Ad Obseruationum calculum in CometaA assumsi Oculum ♉ in 4 grad. 7 min. ♊, cum Latitudine 5 grad. 29 min. Meridionali. Tertiam ♈ Stellam in 1 grad. 58 min. Tauri, cum Latitudine Septentrionali 9 gr. 57 min. etc. vt nos diligentissimis nostris Obseruationibus adinuenimus, nec video eas multum a tuis discrepare. De barba Cometae, quod scribis, eam 21 Octobris non habuit. At 22 Octobris sub Occasum eam quoque hîc animaduerti. Verum cum fallaciam ex Lunae Radijs esse iudicarem (vergebat enim ad Lunam) nec tale quicquam antea depraehendissem, eam Scripto meo inserere nolui. Quod autem Cometam adhuc 12 NouembrisA obseruasti, ipse profecto 10 Nouembris eum ob nimiam raritatemA per Instrumenti rimulas obseruare non potui, nec etiam 10 Nouembris amplius videre; quanquam Aër erat impurus acA vaporosus. Forte eum ob nimiam raritatem vaporibus similem, prae vaporibus depraehendere non potui. Nullam omnino Parallaxin tam per accuratissimum illum, quem Scripto meo inserui, Parallaxes obseruandi modum, quam per tempus et distantiam eius a Meridiano, depraehendere in eo potuiA, quamuis diligentissime eam venarer. Differentia, quae exigua in extremis Cometae fulsionibus inter nostras Obseruationes interuenit, fortassis est a nimia Cometae raritate. Equidem semper proportionalem in eo adinueni motum. Sed haec hactenus de Cometa. Ad Instrumenta nostra quod attinetA, attulit nihil WITTICHIVS, praeter partitionemA (quae, quicunque Autor sit, ingeniosa certe estA) et Sextantis nomen, et Pinnacidiorum rimulasA. Et quanquam Wittichius putabat Instrumenta eaA correctiora fieri non posse, nos tamen post eius discessum multis modis ea correximus. Fecerat enim Wittichius per Automatopaeum nostrum simplices tantûm in visorijs rimulas, in Quadrante quidem duas, alteram in superna visorij parte, alteram ad Latus, in Sextante vero tantum vnicam, in centro scilicet visus. Verum cum Instrumenta in vsum deducerem, multa tandem experientia inueni, eiusmodi rimulas non sufficere, nec quicquam certi depraehendi ijs posse. Quapropter in Sextante duas alias adhuc rimulas, ad Latera Pinnacidiorum, prope oculum fieri curaui, vt distantiae Stellarum per rimulas Parallelas vtrinque cernerentur. Cumque Wittichius nullam rationem Anguli visionis habuisset, huic quoque malo succurrimusA, atque visoria Centro Instrumenti ita apposuimus, vt semper eandem ab illo Sextantis Centro distantiam retineant. Centrumque semper praecise in medio mediae rimulae reperiatur. Ac ne Angulus visus sit minor eo quem Instrumentum nobis exhibet, singulis Pinnacidijs superioribus interne tantum detraximus, quantum inferius respondens Pinnacidium a Centro distat. Ita enim per lineas parallelas eundem Angulum cernimus, quemA Instrumentum nobis exhibet, nec vlla differentia inter Angulum visum et Angulum Instrumenti esse potest. Praeterea cum antea media Regula in Sextante manibus hinc inde ducenda, in Obseruationibus plurimum negocijA nobis facesseret, multumqueA temporis requireret, priusquam in eum, quo debebat, locum essetA perducta, Catenulam artificiose Instrumento apposuimus, per quam in quemcunque locum et Angulum regulam mediam facilime absque negocio deducere possumus. In Quadrantis quoque inferiore Pinnacidiorum parteA, adhuc rimulam inferne fieri curaui, vt Altitudinem Stellae, et superne et inferne paralleliter videre possim. Nisi enim Stella per rimulas paralleliter circa Pinnacidium aequaliter secta conspiciatur, nequaquam certa erit eius Altitudo. Iamdudum etiam ad Latera Pinnacidiorum rimulas Parallelas exaptaui, vt transitum Stellae vtrinque videre possem. Quod si Wittichius rimulas illas Parallelas apud Tuam Excell. vidit, nec Instrumentis nostrisA apposuit, multo nos inani labore onerauit. Cum enim visoria Instrumentorum Mense Martio praeteriti Anni corrigeremus, inuenimus ObseruationesA ante visoriorum correctionem institutas, prorsus fuisse incertas et falsasA. Quapropter omnes de nouo iterare coacti sumus. Reliqua quae de Instrumentis nostris desideras, ex domestico tuo cognoscere poteris. De diuerso Aetheris et Aëris diaphano velim etiam atque etiam cogites. Ipse profecto rem in omnes partes voluens ac reuoluens, nihil tale reperire potui. Sed cum Sententiam ea de re meam in Tractatu de Cometa Capite V satis superque declarauerim, nolo hîc actum agere: Tantum peto a te maiorem in modum in salutem et certitudinem Astronomiae etiam atque etiam, vt ea diligenter examines, atque ad ea mihi respondere nonA dedigneris. Video enim ob nullam aliam rem magis huic pulcherrimae Arti detrahi, quam quod (vt ij, qui quid Hypotheses Astronomicae sint, non intelligunt, arbitrantur) futilibus et falsis Hypothesibus sit referta, perque falsas HypothesesA contra naturam, contraque naturae Leges verum Planetarum motum demonstret. Vt igitur veternum illum ex animis vulgi eriperem, atque quid sibi vellent Hypotheses Astronomicae, ostenderem, luculenter et perspicue, vt opinor, ex ipsa rei natura, quantum per occupationes licuit, de ijs disserui, praesertim cum diu multumque expertus sim, etiam eos, qui se multum in hac Arte profecisse voluntA, in sinistra hac inueniri opinione, seductiA, vt arbitrorA a futilitatibus Aristotelis, qui contra experientiam tantum futilibus coniecturis, de his rebus disputat. Refractionem vsque ad 30 ab HorizonteA gradus in Sole reperire non potui, nec etiam in Horizonte dimidij gradus, sed plerunque 15, aut ad summum 20 minutorum. Sed eam maiore in posterum diligentia non tantum in Sole, verumetiam in Stellis fixis obseruabo. Quemadmodum et ex dextro Latere Persei in Altitudine 9 gr. 42 min. inueni Refractionem 2⅓ minutorum. Ex Cauda Cygni in Altitudine 5 gr. 18 min. inueni Refractionem 5⅔ minutorum. Inuenio etiam ex Obseruationibus diligentissimis, Stellas fixas suas mutare LatitudinesA, contra aliorum traditionemA, idque fieri proportionaliter secundum mutationem obliquitatis Zodiaci, ita vt quo magis ad Solstitia accedant, eo magis Latitudines suas mutent, adeo vt Stellae immotae videantur; Ecliptica vero pro mutatione obliquitatis hinc inde ad easA accedere et recedere. Quod veroA interdum quaedam Stellae huic suppositioni reclament, id (vt arbitror) Obseruationibus veterum tribuendum erit, in quibus Sextantes, aut etiam Trientes graduum non fuerunt apud ipsos magni momenti, vel etiam eiusmodi error in transcribendo interuenire potuit; quanquam videam, dum recentiores Ptolemaeum in Stellis fixis emendare voluerunt, in longe maiores errores esse delapsos, vt ex Alanac, Algorab, etc. manifestum est. Motum Solis in Ephemeride tua positum satis exacte cum nostris Obseruationibus invenio congruere: a quibus tamen Tabulae Prutenicae circa Aequinoctium vernum vltra dimidium gradum absunt. Ipse etiam tantam fere Solis Eccentricitatem reperio, quantam tu asseris. Altitudo Poli hîc Cassellis est 51 grad. 20 min. Inueni enim per diligentissimas Obseruationes Stellam Polarem, in superioriA Meridiani parte, Altitudinem obtinere 54 grad. 16 min. in inferioreA 48 grad. 24 minut. Pectus Cassiopeae superne 87 gr. 3 min. inferne 15 gr. 37 min. In Ascensu medio Cathedrae Cassiopeae superne 84 gr. 26½ min. inferne 18 gr. 13½ min. Ad genua Cassiopeae superne 83 gr. 16½ min. inferne 19 gr. 23½ min. In tibia Cassiopeae superne 79 gr. 44½ minut. inferne 22 grad. 55 min. Quod si Eclipsin illam, quae Anno 1584, 7 Nouembris apparuit, obseruasti, iam quoque differentiam Longitudinum inter nostra loca habebimus. Obseruauit enim WITTICHIVS hîc Cassellis eius medium incidisse in 13 horam 3 minut. post Meridiem; quanquam video, iustam diligentiam in ea Obseruatione non esse adhibitam. Ac toties iam dolui, me eo tempore hîc nondum fuisse (octiduo enim post huc veniebam) vtinam per distantias ad Stellas fixas non tantum Eclipsin, verumetiam motum et Parallaxin Lunae obseruasset. Sin eam Eclipsin obseruare non potuisti, alia nobis via ingredienda erit, vt differentiam inter Meridianos nostrorum locorum nanciscamur; quod commode per Lunam fieri posse, puto, si eam vna et eadem Die aut etiam diebus non multum a se distantibus vterque simulA diligenter Obseruauerimus. Ita enim separata Parallaxi, per motum ex Obseruationibus inuentum facile quaesitum patebit. Sin per calculum malueris (quae ratio tamen mihiA minus certa videtur) poteris calculum Lunae Meridiano nostro congruentem ad me mittere. Ita enim si Lunam circa nonagesimum grad. obseruauero, ex tuo constituto motu, si isA Apparentijs satisfacit, facile quaesitum inueniam. NarrauitA quoque mihi PETRVS IACOBI domesticusA tuus, te per Studiosum tuis correctissimis Instrumentis, Eleuationem Polarem et Altitudinem Meridianam Spicae ♍ FruenburgiA Borussiae, vbi COPERNICVS plerasque suas Obseruationes habuit, obseruasse. Cum igitur perlibenter scirem, quaenam fuisset certitudo Instrumentorum, quibus in suis Obseruationibus vsus essetA Copernicus, et an ea FruenburgiA esset Poli Eleuatio, quam Copernicus Libro III. Capite 2 asserit, peto a te maiorem in modum, vt eas mecumA communicare nonA dedigneris. Vicissim tibi mea officia polliceor ac defero. Illustrissimus Princeps mittit ad te Obseruationes suas de Cometis, et Altitudines Solis Meridianas per vetera Organa obseruatas, quas pro tua prudentia ad vsum accommodare, et cum tuis conferre poteris. Mittit item nostras Stellarum fixarum per noua Organa institutas a me Obseruationes, quas calculo per Studiosos tuos examinabis, deque ijs nos admonebis. D. IOACHIMVS CAMERARIVS tibi salutem; cuius Literas ad te perscriptas inter reliqua reperies. His bene et feliciter vale.</p><p>Cassellis 18 Calend. Maij, Anno Christi 1586.<br>T. T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS Bernburgensis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and, in virtue, wisdom, and ingenious and erudite learning, most renowned man, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight, the most outstanding mathematician of our age, etc., his lord to be honoured with [all] observance, etc. — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Our Most Illustrious Prince was filled with the greatest joy by your letter, most noble and most learned man, most outstanding of mathematicians — both because it contained the more recondite Science of Astronomy and the royal apparatus of ingenious Instruments, and a most acute judgement concerning Observations to be conducted in a far different manner; and because it came from you, whom, on account of your supreme skill in astronomical matters, he most highly esteems. And although His Illustrious Highness himself will reply to your letter separately, he has nevertheless charged me to reply to Your Excellency on my own part, especially regarding mathematical matters. So then, not to use long circumlocutions: when the Most Illustrious Prince received your treatise on the Comet, I was not permitted to inspect it — His Illustrious Highness forbidding it — until I too had brought forward my own treatise on the same Comet; and when in these [treatises] we saw the Observations conducted in places so far apart correspond to one another down to the [very] minute, it can scarcely be said with how great a joy we were flooded. So that you too may see this same concordance, I send you in return my treatise on the same Comet, especially since in Chapter V it contains a disputation on the Refraction of the Stars, of which you make mention in your letter — although it is not yet finished, three Chapters being lacking, of course, at the end, which on account of [my other] occupations I could not complete. But if some difference is found in the positions established for the Comet, it may arise both from the distance between the places at which we had our Observations, and because the positions of the fixed Stars were perhaps not assumed in the same way, and because of the differing obliquity of the Zodiac, which I assumed in my Observations [to be] 23 degrees 30 minutes — just so much, indeed, as our Illustrious Prince has found over so many years. And although the Instruments in those Observations were not so exact as now, nor was account even taken of Parallax or Refraction, I nevertheless preferred to assume the obliquity of the Zodiac corresponding to the Observations rather than to the annotations of the Tables, especially since I myself have found that those Observations are not far from the Truth — although I have not yet been able to observe the solstitial Altitude of the Sun exactly in summer, on account of the windy and cloudy condition of the Air. And though indeed I do not doubt that the obliquity of the Zodiac which you assert is the true one, I too, [if] God grants a favourable outcome and a favourable condition of the Air, will observe it most diligently, so that the Truth, thus supported by the various Observations of various places, may merit the greater credit with posterity. For the calculation of the Observations on the Comet I assumed the Eye of Taurus [♉] at 4 degrees 7 minutes of Gemini [♊], with a southerly Latitude of 5 degrees 29 minutes; the Third Star of Aries [♈] at 1 degree 58 minutes of Taurus, with a northerly Latitude of 9 degrees 57 minutes, etc., as we have found them by our most diligent Observations; nor do I see them differ much from yours. As to what you write about the beard of the Comet, it did not have it on 21 October. But on 22 October, towards sunset, I too noticed it here. Yet since I judged it to be a deception from the Rays of the Moon (for it inclined towards the Moon), and had not detected any such thing before, I did not wish to insert it into my treatise. As for your having observed the Comet still on 12 November — I for my part could not observe it on 10 November through the slits of the Instrument, on account of its excessive faintness, nor could I see it any longer [even] on 10 November, although the Air was impure and vaporous. Perhaps, on account of its excessive faintness, [it being] like the vapours, I could not detect it against the vapours. I could detect in it no parallax at all, neither by that most accurate method of observing parallaxes which I inserted into my treatise, nor by the time and its distance from the Meridian, though I hunted for it most diligently. The difference, slight, that occurs between our Observations at the extreme [points of] the Comet's shinings, is perhaps from the excessive faintness of the Comet. I, for one, always found its motion proportional in it. But so much for the Comet.</p><p>As regards our Instruments, Wittich brought nothing except the partition (which, whoever its Author be, is certainly ingenious), and the name of the Sextant, and the slits of the Sights. And although Wittich thought that those Instruments could not be made more correct, we nevertheless, after his departure, corrected them in many ways. For Wittich had made, by means of our Instrument-maker, only single slits in the sight-vanes — in the Quadrant indeed two, one in the upper part of the vane, the other at the Side, but in the Sextant only one, namely at the centre of sight. But when I brought the Instruments into use, I found at length, by much experience, that slits of that kind do not suffice, nor can anything certain be detected by them. Therefore in the Sextant I had two further slits made, at the Sides of the Sights, near the eye, so that the distances of the Stars might be discerned through Parallel slits on both sides. And since Wittich had taken no account of the Angle of vision, we relieved this fault too, and so fitted the sights to the Centre of the Instrument that they always keep the same distance from that Centre of the Sextant; and [so that] the Centre [of the eye] is always found precisely in the middle of the middle slit. And lest the Angle seen be smaller than that which the Instrument shows us, we took away from each of the upper Sights, on the inner side, just as much as the corresponding lower Sight stands from the Centre. For thus, by parallel lines, we discern the same Angle that the Instrument shows us, and there can be no difference between the Angle seen and the Angle of the Instrument. Moreover, since formerly the middle Rule in the Sextant, having to be moved this way and that by hand, used to cause us very much trouble in Observations and to require much time before it was brought to the place where it ought [to be], we ingeniously fitted to the Instrument a little chain, by which we can most easily, without trouble, bring the middle rule to whatever place and Angle [we wish]. In the lower part of the Sights of the Quadrant too, I had a further slit made below, so that I might see the Altitude of the Star both above and below in parallel. For unless the Star is seen, through the parallel slits, cut equally around the Sight, its Altitude will by no means be certain. Long since, too, I fitted Parallel slits to the Sides of the Sights, so that I might see the transit of the Star on both sides. But if Wittich saw those Parallel slits with Your Excellency, and did not fit them to our Instruments, he burdened us with much vain labour. For when we were correcting the sights of the Instruments in the month of March of last year, we found that the Observations conducted before the correction of the sights had been utterly uncertain and false. Therefore we were compelled to repeat them all anew. The rest that you desire concerning our Instruments you will be able to learn from your servant. About the differing transparency of the Aether and the Air, I would have you think again and again. I myself, indeed, turning and turning the matter over on all sides, could find nothing of the sort. But since I have declared my opinion on that matter in my Treatise on the Comet, Chapter V, abundantly and more than enough, I do not wish here to do what has [already] been done. I only ask you, all the more, for the welfare and certainty of Astronomy, again and again, that you examine these matters diligently, and do not disdain to reply to them for me. For I see that nothing more detracts from this most beautiful Art than that (as those who do not understand what Astronomical Hypotheses are suppose) it is stuffed with futile and false Hypotheses, and through false Hypotheses, contrary to nature and contrary to the Laws of nature, it demonstrates the true motion of the Planets. So then, to snatch that drowsiness from the minds of the common crowd, and to show what Astronomical Hypotheses mean, I have discoursed of them clearly and perspicuously, as I think, from the very nature of the thing, so far as my occupations allowed — especially since I have long and much experienced that even those who claim to have advanced far in this Art are found to be in this perverse opinion, seduced (as I judge) by the futilities of Aristotle, who, against experience, disputes of these things only by futile conjectures. I could not find a Refraction in the Sun [greater than] up to 30 degrees from the Horizon, nor [even] at the Horizon [as much as] half a degree, but for the most part 15, or at most 20 minutes. But I will observe it in future with greater diligence, not only in the Sun but also in the fixed Stars. Just as, from the right Side of Perseus, at an Altitude of 9 degrees 42 minutes, I found a Refraction of 2⅓ minutes; from the Tail of the Swan [Cygnus], at an Altitude of 5 degrees 18 minutes, I found a Refraction of 5⅔ minutes. I find also, from most diligent Observations, that the fixed Stars change their Latitudes, contrary to the tradition of others; and that this happens proportionally according to the change of the obliquity of the Zodiac, so that the more they approach the Solstices, the more they change their Latitudes — to such a degree that the Stars seem unmoved, while it is the Ecliptic that, with the change of obliquity, approaches and recedes from them this way and that. And as for the fact that sometimes certain Stars protest against this supposition, that (as I judge) will have to be ascribed to the Observations of the ancients, in which Sextants, or even Thirds, of degrees were not of great moment with them, or else an error of that kind may have crept in in the transcribing — although I see that, while the more recent [astronomers] wished to emend Ptolemy in the fixed Stars, they have fallen into far greater errors, as is plain from Alnath, Algorab, etc. I find the motion of the Sun placed in your Ephemeris to agree quite exactly with our Observations — from which, however, the Prutenic Tables differ, around the vernal Equinox, by more than half a degree. I too find almost as great an Eccentricity of the Sun as you assert. The Altitude of the Pole here at Kassel is 51 degrees 20 minutes. For I have found, by most diligent Observations, that the Pole Star, in the upper part of the Meridian, has an Altitude of 54 degrees 16 minutes, in the lower [part] 48 degrees 24 minutes. The Breast of Cassiopeia above 87 degrees 3 minutes, below 15 degrees 37 minutes. In the mid-Ascent, the Throne [Chair] of Cassiopeia above 84 degrees 26½ minutes, below 18 degrees 13½ minutes. At the knees of Cassiopeia above 83 degrees 16½ minutes, below 19 degrees 23½ minutes. In the shin of Cassiopeia above 79 degrees 44½ minutes, below 22 degrees 55 minutes. But if you observed that Eclipse which appeared in the Year 1584, on 7 November, we shall now also have the difference of Longitudes between our places. For Wittich observed here at Kassel that its middle fell at the 13th hour [and] 3 minutes after Noon — although I see that due diligence was not employed in that Observation. And so often have I grieved that I was not yet here at that time (for I came hither a week later); would that he had observed, by distances to the fixed Stars, not only the Eclipse but also the motion and Parallax of the Moon! But if you could not observe that Eclipse, another way will have to be entered upon by us, so that we may obtain the difference between the Meridians of our places; which I think can conveniently be done by means of the Moon, if we both observe it diligently together on one and the same Day, or even on days not far distant from one another. For thus, the Parallax being separated out, the thing sought will easily be made plain through the motion found from the Observations. But if you prefer [to proceed] by calculation (a method which nevertheless seems to me less certain), you will be able to send me a calculation of the Moon agreeing with our Meridian. For thus, if I observe the Moon around the ninetieth degree, [then] from your established motion — if it satisfies the Appearances — I shall easily find the thing sought. Peter Jacobi, your servant, also told me that you, through a Student [of yours], with your most accurate Instruments, observed the Polar Elevation and the Meridian Altitude of Spica [♍] at Frauenburg in Prussia, where Copernicus had most of his Observations. Since, then, I would most gladly know what the certainty was of the Instruments which Copernicus used in his Observations, and whether the Elevation of the Pole at Frauenburg was that which Copernicus asserts in Book III, Chapter 2, I ask you, all the more, that you not disdain to communicate these things with me. In turn I promise and offer you my services. The Most Illustrious Prince sends to you his Observations of the Comets, and the Meridian Altitudes of the Sun observed by the old Instruments, which, by your prudence, you will be able to adapt to use and to compare with your own. He sends likewise our Observations of the fixed Stars, conducted by me by the new Instruments, which you will examine by calculation through your Students, and of which you will advise us. Master Joachim Camerarius [sends] you greeting; whose letter, written out to you, you will find among the rest. With these [words], fare well and happily.</p><p>At Kassel, the 18th [day before] the Kalends of May, in the Year of Christ 1586.<br>Yours truly,<br>Christoph Rothmann of Bernburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus Bernburgensis</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), of Bernburg, court mathematician-astronomer to Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel (protocol §5.6). This is one of his earliest letters in the great Rothmann–Brahe technical exchange (begun 1586), printed by Tycho in the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> of 1596.</li><li><b>Recipient / titulature.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe, Domino de Knudstrup, Equiti Dano</i> = Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight (protocol §5.7, Knudstrup <i>as is</i>). The honorific stack and <i>S.P.</i> (= <i>salutem plurimam</i>, \"heartiest greeting,\" §5.5) are rendered, not dropped.</li><li><b>The Illustrious Prince.</b> <i>Illustrissimus Princeps noster</i> / <i>Celsitudo</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (§5.6), Rothmann's patron and a practising observer; he is sending Tycho his own cometary and solar observations alongside this letter. <i>Celsitudo</i> rendered \"[His] Highness\" per §5.5.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (d. 1586), the itinerant Silesian mathematician who had been at Hven and then at Kassel (1584–85), and whose role in transmitting Tycho's instrument designs is the subject of Rothmann's pointed remarks here. <i>Petrus Iacobi</i> = <b>Peter Jacobsen (Flemløse)</b>, one of Tycho's assistants/famuli (\"your servant,\" <i>domesticus tuus</i>). <i>Copernicus</i> = Nicolaus Copernicus; the reference is to <i>De revolutionibus</i> Book III, ch. 2, and to his observing site at <i>Fruenburg/Fruenburgi Borussiae</i> = <b>Frauenburg (Frombork) in Prussia</b>. <i>Ioachimus Camerarius</i> = <b>Joachim Camerarius the Younger</b> (1534–1598), the Nuremberg physician-humanist. <i>Aristoteles</i> named as the source of the \"futilities\" Rothmann attacks.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Cassellis / Cassell</i> = <b>Kassel</b> (§5.7). <i>Fruenburgi Borussiae</i> = Frauenburg (Frombork), Royal/Ducal Prussia.</li><li><b>Comet.</b> The comet under discussion is the <b>comet of 1585</b> (observed Oct.–Nov. 1585 at Hven and Kassel). <i>barba Cometae</i> rendered \"the beard of the Comet\" (the cometary tail/coma extension); <i>fulsiones</i> = \"shinings\" (flashes/appearances of brightness), kept descriptive; <i>raritas</i> = \"faintness/thinness,\" the comet's tenuity. <i>Scriptum / Tractatus de Cometa</i> = Rothmann's treatise on the comet (his <i>Observationum stellarum</i>… cometary tract), here described as incomplete (three chapters wanting).</li><li><b>Astronomy — concepts (glossary §5.1).</b> <i>Parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>Refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>Eccentricitas</i> → <b>eccentricity</b>; <i>obliquitas Zodiaci</i> → <b>obliquity of the Zodiac/ecliptic</b>; <i>Altitudo</i> → <b>altitude</b>; <i>Meridianus</i> → <b>meridian</b>; <i>Longitudo / Latitudo</i> → <b>(celestial) longitude / latitude</b>; <i>motus</i> → <b>motion</b>; <i>Ecliptica</i> → <b>ecliptic</b>; <i>Aequinoctium vernum</i> → <b>vernal equinox</b>; <i>Eclipsis</i> → <b>eclipse</b>; <i>Altitudo Poli / Eleuatio Polaris</i> → <b>altitude / elevation of the pole</b> (the observer's latitude). <i>Aether / Aër diaphanum</i> → \"the transparency of the Aether and the Air\" — a genuine physical-optical question (whether the heavens and the air have different refractive transparency), kept literal.</li><li><b>Astronomy — instruments (§5.2).</b> <i>Sextans</i> → <b>sextant</b>; <i>Quadrans</i> → <b>quadrant</b>; <i>Organa / Instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b>; <i>Pinnacidia</i> → <b>sights</b>; <i>visoria</i> → <b>sight-vanes / sights</b>; <i>rimulae</i> → <b>slits</b> (the sighting-slits in the vanes); <i>Regula media</i> → <b>the middle rule</b> (the sextant's moving radius); <i>Catenula</i> → <b>little chain</b> (the fine-adjustment chain Rothmann fitted). <i>partitio</i> → <b>partition</b> (the graduated division of the arc — the transversal-point division Wittich is credited with carrying). <i>Automatopaeus</i> → <b>Instrument-maker / mechanic</b> (the court's instrument-builder; literally \"maker of self-acting devices\"); rendered \"our Instrument-maker.\" <i>Angulus visionis / Angulus visus</i> → \"the Angle of vision / the Angle seen\" (the parallax-of-sight error from eye placement that Rothmann's twin-slit fix addresses).</li><li><b>Symbols (§5.3, glyph kept in source, named in translation).</b> ♉ = <b>Taurus</b> (\"the Eye of Taurus\" = the star Aldebaran, α Tauri); ♊ = <b>Gemini</b>; ♈ = <b>Aries</b> (\"the Third Star of Aries\"); ♍ = <b>Virgo</b> (\"Spica,\" α Virginis). The fractions <i>2⅓</i> and <i>5⅔</i> minutes are kept as written. <i>Trientes graduum</i> = \"thirds of degrees\" (a unit of arc precision).</li><li><b>Star names.</b> <i>Alanac, Algorab</i> = the fixed stars <b>Alnath</b> (β Tauri / γ Aurigae) and <b>Algorab</b> (δ Corvi) — cited as examples where post-Ptolemaic emenders erred. <i>Alanac</i> is the source's printed form (for <i>Alnath/Elnath</i>); kept as printed, identified here. <i>Pectus / Cathedra / genua / tibia Cassiopeae</i> = the Breast, the Throne (Chair), the knees, the shin of <b>Cassiopeia</b> (the standard descriptive star-positions of the constellation).</li><li><b>Tables.</b> <i>Tabulae Prutenicae</i> = the <b>Prutenic Tables</b> (Reinhold, 1551); <i>Ephemeris tua</i> = Tycho's [solar] ephemeris/computed solar motion. <i>Tabularum annotata</i> = \"the annotations of the Tables\" (tabulated/received values), contrasted with direct observation.</li><li><b>Dateline crux (primary).</b> The subscription reads <i>\"Cassellis 18 Calend. Maij, Anno Christi 1586\"</i> = <b>a.d. XVIII Kalendas Maias 1586 = 14 April 1586</b> (O.S.; Hesse-Kassel and Denmark were on Old Style). The Roman-calendar idiom is rendered with the idiom and glossed. The dataset `label` (\"<b>1587. 18. maj. Kassel</b>\") disagrees on <b>both</b> counts: it reads the year as 1587 (the source says 1586) and silently mis-renders <i>\"18 Calend. Maij\"</i> as \"18 May\" rather than the inclusive-count Roman date (a.d. XVIII Kal. Mai. = 14 April). The header therefore leads with the as-written date (14 April 1586) and appends the catalogued date. The reading <b>14 April 1586</b> is independently corroborated by A. Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (Cambridge, 2007), which cites this very letter as \"Christoph Rothmann to Tycho Brahe, 14 April 1586.\" The slug encodes the (incorrect) catalogue date 1585-05-18 and is retained as an opaque identifier only.</li><li><b>In-letter dating note (not reconciled).</b> Rothmann discusses two distinct events with their own dates: the <b>comet</b> observations of Oct.–Nov. <b>1585</b> (21–22 Oct., 10/12 Nov.), and a <b>lunar eclipse of 7 November 1584</b> observed by Wittich at Kassel (<i>\"Eclipsin illam, quae Anno 1584, 7 Nouembris apparuit\"</i>), proposed as a means of fixing the longitude difference Kassel–Hven. These dates are reproduced as written; none is converted.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The negations are reproduced one-for-one and none added or dropped — notably the polite-request formula <i>\"ad ea mihi respondere <b>non</b> dedigneris\"</i> / <i>\"eas mecum communicare <b>non</b> dedigneris\"</i> = \"<b>do not disdain</b> to reply / to communicate\" (a single negation each, an idiom of courteous request, <b>not</b> a prohibition); <i>\"mihi id inspicere <b>non</b> licuit\"</i> = \"I was <b>not</b> permitted\"; <i>\"eiusmodi rimulas <b>non</b> sufficere, <b>nec</b> quicquam certi\"</i> (two coordinated negations) = \"do <b>not</b> suffice, <b>nor</b> [can] anything certain…\"; <i>\"Wittichius <b>nullam</b> rationem Anguli visionis habuisset\"</i> = \"had taken <b>no</b> account\"; <i>\"<b>Nullam omnino</b> Parallaxin… depraehendere… potui\"</i> = \"I could detect <b>no</b> parallax <b>at all</b>.\" The contrary-to-fact wish <i>\"vtinam… obseruasset\"</i> (\"would that he had observed\") is kept as an unfulfilled wish, not asserted as fact.</li><li><b>Conditional/mood note.</b> Rothmann's two-branch proposal for fixing the longitude difference is kept in its conditional structure: <i>\"Sin eam Eclipsin obseruare non potuisti…\"</i> (\"<b>But if</b> you could not observe that Eclipse…\") → the lunar-distance method; <i>\"Sin per calculum malueris…\"</i> (\"<b>But if</b> you prefer [to proceed] by calculation…\") → send a Moon-calculation. The parenthetical hedge <i>\"(quae ratio tamen mihi minus certa videtur)\"</i> (\"a method which nevertheless seems to me less certain\") is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Epistolary subscription.</b> <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Tuus totus</i> / <i>Tibi totus</i> (\"Yours wholly / truly\"); rendered \"Yours truly.\" <i>Bernburgensis</i> = \"of Bernburg\" (Rothmann's origin), kept as a cognomen.</li><li><b>Transcription artefact (flagged, not in the original text).</b> The working transcription carries stray capital <b>\"A\"</b> characters fused to word-endings throughout (e.g. <i>PRAeSTANTISSIMOA, acutissimumqueA, Quanquam autemA, vale.A, 1586.A, ROTHMANNVS Bernburgensis.A</i>). These are <b>footnote/variant-apparatus reference markers from the digital base text</b>, not part of Rothmann's Latin; they are reproduced verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4(2) but are ignored in establishing the reading and do not appear in the translation. A doubled-text dittography in the transcription — <i>\"Stellas fixas suas mutare Latitudinessuas mutare Latitudines\"</i> — has been read once (<i>\"Stellas fixas suas mutare Latitudines\"</i>, \"the fixed Stars change their Latitudes\"); the printed-as-received form is preserved in the Source block and the single correct reading explained here.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin is dense observational-technical prose but renders securely throughout, and the principal dating crux (label \"1587/18 May\" vs. dateline \"XVIII Kal. Mai. 1586\" = 14 April 1586) is resolved against external authority (Mosley) and flagged rather than smoothed. Residual soft points, all flagged above rather than decided: the exact technical force of <i>partitio</i> (\"partition\" — the arc-division/transversals Wittich carried) and of <i>Automatopaeus</i> (\"Instrument-maker\"); the identification of <i>Alanac</i> (printed form for Alnath); and the editorial handling of the transcription's stray \"A\" markers and the <i>Latitudines</i> dittography. No negation or conditional is in genuine doubt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (Rothmann to Tycho, Kassel, 18 Calend. Maij / dated 'Anno Christi 1586' in the source text), so per brief this was audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Read the full Latin against the full English line by line: salutation/address heading, the comet-treatise exchange (Ch. V refraction discussion, three missing chapters), every star-position figure (Eye of Taurus 4gr7min Gemini / Lat 5gr29min S; Third Star of Aries 1gr58min Taurus / Lat 9gr57min N), the comet-beard dates (21/22 October) and observation dates (10/12 November), the full instrument-correction paragraph (Wittich's single sight-slits vs Rothmann's added parallel slits, the angle-of-vision fix, the chain mechanism, the March correction), the refraction figures (Perseus 9gr42min-&gt;2 1/3min, Cygnus tail 5gr18min-&gt;5 2/3min), the full Cassiopeia/Polaris altitude table (Pole Star 54gr16min/48gr24min; breast 87gr3min/15gr37min; chair 84gr26.5min/18gr13.5min; knees 83gr16.5min/19gr23.5min; shin 79gr44.5min/22gr55min), the Kassel pole altitude (51gr20min), the 1584 eclipse (7 November, Wittich's observation at 13h3min PM), the Frauenburg/Copernicus altitude query (Book III Ch.2), and the closing date (18 Kalends of May 1586). All names, numbers, dates and clauses correspond; no omissions, no flipped negations/subjects, no invented facts found. The one item scrutinized closely -- 'Alanac' rendered as 'Alnath' in the star-error passage ('ex Alanac, Algorab, etc.') -- is judged a defensible identification (Alnath/Beta Tauri is the textbook example of a star whose shared Auriga/Taurus position caused exactly the kind of error inflation Rothmann is describing) rather than a fabrication, and is not counted as a defect. Not collated against Dreyer's page images (none supplied), so grade cannot exceed B despite the clean result.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15860907002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870120001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that Landgrave Wilhelm IV received Tycho's letter and comet treatise with great pleasure, that the Prince will write separately, and begins his own response on astronomical matters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850518001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850526001", "d": "1585-05-26", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "ck": "Frederik 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Helsingør", "t": "King Frederik II → Tycho Brahe — Kronborg, 26 March 1585 [O.S.] (catalogued 26 May 1585)", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin-rubric heading) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= vol. XIV, the Danish documents) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Skånske Tegnelser, I, 405 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF OM THEND LYCHTE PAA KULDEN ANRØRENDIS.</p><p>WOR gunst tilforn. Wiid, att som thu haffuer thend løchte paa Kulden mett huis ther till er forordnitt wdj befalling, saa wille Wij thig naadigst icke forholde, att offte och jdelig klager kommer for Oß aff thend søefarnne mand ther wdj fundit, att samme løchte icke tilbørlig holdis med lius och bluß, och kunde Oß ther offuer icke nogsom forundre, epther att Wij Oß kunde erind[r]e, att thu synderligen haffuer belangitt hoß Oß, att om en ny bequemlige sted, paa huilcken samme lychte kunde bedre standis thend søefaren mand til beste, motte forfaris, och ther om werffuit Wor befallingskriffuelße til Wore gode mend och til tholderne ther sammestedtz, huor aff Wij kunde haffue att formode thin synderlig bewagenhet emod thend søefaren mand och flid til for:ne løchte att holde, huilcke Wore breffue Wij dog forfare icke att were eptherkommen och till affskeed beforderit, end mindre thend gamble løchte, som nu er, siden thend thid att were holdit som thet sig bør, huorfore Wij ere fororsagit thig ther om her mett att belange. Bedendis thig och wille, att thu er forthencktt vdj samme løchte saa att lade holde med liuß och bluß som thet er tilbørligt, paa thet att thend søefaren mand icke, forgefflig sig ther paa forladendis, schulle liide godtz och liffuis fare, skade och forderuelße, saa framt sig nogen ther offuer her epther tilbørligen kand beklage, thu tha icke ther fore wilt stande til rette och Wij fororsagis om samme løchte anderledis att forordne till andre, som Wor befalling och thend søefarne mandz leylighed will bedre bethencke.</p><p>Ther meth etc.<br>Actum Kroneborg 26. martij aar 1585.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter concerning the beacon on Kullen.</b></p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, inasmuch as you hold the beacon on Kullen, together with whatever is appointed thereto, in [your] charge, we will not graciously withhold from you that frequent and continual complaints come before us from the seafaring man, who finds therein that the same beacon is not duly kept with light and flame; and we cannot sufficiently wonder at it, since we are able to call to mind that you in particular had made suit to us that a new, more convenient site — on which the same beacon might be better maintained, to the seafaring man's advantage — should be sought out, and concerning it had procured our letter of command to our good men and to the toll-officers there in that same place, whereby we had cause to expect your particular goodwill towards the seafaring man and your diligence in keeping the aforesaid beacon; which letters of ours we nonetheless learn to have neither been complied with nor carried into effect, much less the old beacon, such as it now is, to have been kept since that time as is fitting — wherefore we are compelled hereby to address you concerning it. Asking you, and willing it, that you be mindful of the same beacon, so as to have it kept with light and flame as is fitting, to the end that the seafaring man, relying upon it in vain, should not suffer peril to goods and to life, harm and ruin: so that, in so far as anyone can hereafter rightfully complain about this matter, you will then not be willing to answer for it [= you will not [be able to] avoid answering for it], and we shall be compelled to appoint the same beacon otherwise, to others, as our charge and the seafaring man's circumstance shall the better warrant.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Kronborg, 26 March, year 1585.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Issuer.</b> <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), Tycho's royal patron, who in 1576 had granted him the island of Hven. Among Tycho's crown emoluments was the <b>Kullen beacon-light</b> (<i>Kullen fyr</i>), held in fief with its light-dues (<i>fyrpenge</i>) in return for the duty of keeping the light burning for shipping. The grant was a recurring point of friction: Tycho was admonished more than once for neglect, and the king eventually withheld part of the beacon-money over the lapse (cf. the related crown letter on the <i>fyr-penge</i> in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. XIV). The present letter is one such reprimand.<br>- <b>Recipient / addressee form.</b> <i>THYGE BRAHE</i> = <b>Tyge Brahe</b>, the Danish vernacular form of Tycho's name (the Latinised <i>Tycho</i> is the international one). The king addresses him throughout in the familiar <b>2nd-person singular</b> (<i>thu / thig</i>) — the master-to-servant register of a royal command, not the deferential plural.<br>- <b>Place / object.</b> <i>thend løchte paa Kulden</i> = \"the beacon on <b>Kullen</b>\" — the beacon on <b>Kullaberg</b> (the Kullen headland in north-west Skåne, then Danish; modern Kullaberg/Kullen, Sweden), at the entrance to the Øresund. <i>løchte / lychte</i> (mod. Da. <i>lygte</i>) here denotes the <b>navigational beacon-light</b>, i.e. the lighthouse establishment, not a hand-lantern. <i>lius och bluß</i> (mod. <i>lys og blus</i>) = \"light and flame\" (<i>bluß</i> = the open coal-/wood-blaze of an early beacon). Rendered \"beacon … light and flame\" throughout.<br>- <b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (Skånske Tegnelser). The <b>Latin term <i>ANRØRENDIS</b></i> in the heading is the Danish-Latin chancery participle <i>anrørendes</i> (\"concerning\"); the heading is otherwise Danish, and the closing register-formula <i>Actum</i> (\"Done/Given at\") is the conventional Latin chancery tag — both preserved per protocol §2/§5.5. The heuristic <i>da</i> tag is reading-confirmed.<br>- <b>The petition referred to.</b> <i>thu synderligen haffuer belangitt hoß Oß, att om en ny bequemlige sted … motte forfaris</i> — Tycho had himself earlier petitioned the king to have a <b>new, more convenient site</b> for the beacon sought out (<i>forfaris</i> = \"be investigated / sought out\"), and had procured a royal <i>befallingskriffuelße</i> (\"letter of command\") to the local crown officers (<i>Wore gode mend</i>) and <b>toll-officers</b> (<i>tholderne</i>, the Øresund customs men). The king's reproach is that those very letters went unexecuted while the old light was let lapse.<br>- <b>Negations (legal force — verified count).</b> The body carries five operative negations, each load-bearing; reproduced one-for-one:<br>  1. <i>saa wille Wij thig naadigst <b>icke</b> forholde</i> — \"we will <b>not</b> graciously withhold from you,\" a litotes meaning \"we must inform you.\"<br>  2. <i>samme løchte <b>icke</b> tilbørlig holdis</i> — \"the same beacon is <b>not</b> duly kept.\"<br>  3. <i>kunde Oß ther offuer <b>icke</b> nogsom forundre</i> — \"we cannot <b>sufficiently</b> wonder at it\" (<i>icke … nogsom</i> = \"not … sufficiently\").<br>  4. <i>huilcke Wore breffue Wij dog forfare <b>icke</b> att were eptherkommen</i> — \"which letters of ours we nonetheless learn to have <b>not</b> been complied with.\" (Rendered as the English correlative \"neither … nor\" across <i>eptherkommen … och till affskeed beforderit</i>; the single Danish <i>icke</i> governs both members.)<br>  5. <i>paa thet att thend søefaren mand <b>icke</b> … schulle liide … fare</i> — \"so that the seafaring man should <b>not</b> suffer … peril.\"<br>  A sixth <i>icke</i> stands in the final conditional clause (see crux). <b>No negation is added anywhere</b> — in particular the king's threatened action <i>Wij fororsagis … att forordne till andre</i> (\"we shall be compelled to appoint … to others\") is <b>affirmative</b>, the substance of the threat, and is rendered without any inserted \"not.\"<br>- <b>Crux — the closing conditional / threat.</b> <i>saa framt sig nogen ther offuer her epther tilbørligen kand beklage, thu tha <b>icke</b> ther fore wilt stande til rette och Wij fororsagis om samme løchte anderledis att forordne till andre.</i> This is a standard Danish chancery warning, but its exact logical mood is genuinely ambiguous and is <b>flagged, not smoothed</b>. Literally: \"in so far as / if anyone can hereafter rightfully complain about this matter, [that] you will then <b>not</b> be willing to answer for it [<i>stande til rette</i> = stand to law, be held to account], and [that] we be compelled to appoint the same beacon otherwise, to others.\" Two readings are defensible — (a) a <b>consequence clause</b>: should neglect persist and a complaint arise, Tycho will fail/refuse to answer for it and the king will <i>therefore</i> reassign the beacon; (b) a <b>standalone conditional threat</b>: should anyone rightfully complain, Tycho will not [be able to] avoid answering for it, and the king will reassign the beacon. The crux is the force of <i>icke … wilt stande til rette</i>: \"will not be willing to answer for it\" can mean either \"will refuse / fail to answer\" or, idiomatically, \"will not [be able to] escape answering.\" The translation keeps the literal wording plus a bracketed gloss and does not collapse the ambiguity into one fluent threat. The king's reassigning the beacon (<i>fororsagis … att forordne till andre</i>) is affirmative and certain in either reading.<br>- <b>Secondary crux (milder).</b> <i>thend gamble løchte, som nu er</i> admits two readings: \"the old beacon, <b>such as it now is</b>\" (i.e. in its present neglected state) or \"the old beacon, [the one] <b>that now exists</b>\" (the existing one, as against a proposed new one). The translation keeps the former, which the surrounding sense favours, but the phrasing is genuinely double.<br>- <b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1585).</b> <i>WOR gunst tilforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"know,\" imperative); <i>huis ther till er forordnitt</i> = <i>hvis dertil er forordnet</i> (\"what is appointed thereto\"); <i>wdj befalling</i> = <i>udi befaling</i> (\"in [one's] charge/commission\"); <i>jdelig</i> = <i>idelig</i> (\"continual\"); <i>erind[r]e</i> — the register drops the <i>r</i>, supplied in brackets = <i>erindre</i> (\"call to mind\"); <i>belangitt / belange</i> = <i>belange</i> (chancery \"to make suit to / address a matter to someone\"); <i>motte forfaris</i> = <i>måtte forfares</i> (\"might be sought out/investigated\"); <i>werffuit</i> = <i>hvervet/værvet</i> (\"procured, obtained\"); <i>tholderne</i> = <i>toldnerne</i> (\"the toll-officers\"); <i>bewagenhet</i> = <i>bevågenhed</i> (\"goodwill, favourable disposition\"); <i>eptherkommen</i> = <i>efterkommet</i> (\"complied with\"); <i>till affskeed beforderit</i> = <i>til afsked befordret</i> (\"carried into effect / brought to a conclusion\"); <i>fiden thend thid</i> = <i>siden den tid</i> (\"since that time\" — printed <i>fiden</i> is a long-s <i>siden</i>, the classic <i>ſ</i>→<i>f</i> trap); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>forthencktt</i> = <i>betænkt/fortænkt</i> (\"mindful, take thought\"); <i>forgefflig sig ther paa forladendis</i> = <i>forgæves sig derpå forladendes</i> (\"relying upon it in vain\"); <i>liffuis fare</i> = <i>livsfare</i> (\"peril of life\"); <i>forderuelße</i> = <i>fordærvelse</i> (\"ruin\"); <i>saa framt</i> = <i>så fremt</i> (\"in so far as / if\"); <i>stande til rette</i> = \"stand to law, answer at law\"; <i>leylighed</i> = <i>lejlighed</i> (\"circumstance, occasion, need\").<br>- <b>Calendar / dating crux (flagged, not reconciled).</b> The body dateline reads <b>\"Actum Kroneborg 26. martij aar 1585\"</b> = \"Done at <b>Kronborg</b>, <b>26 March</b> 1585\" (O.S.). The dataset's metadata header and `label`, however, calendar the letter as <b>\"1585. 26. maj / 26 May, Kronborg\"</b> (and so the slug `…15850526…`). The two disagree on the month (March vs. May) by exactly two months. Per protocol §3 the discrepancy is recorded, <b>not</b> silently resolved: the header above leads with the <b>as-written dateline date (26 March)</b> and appends the catalogued date. The likeliest source of the divergence is a <i>martij</i>↔<i>maij</i> (Latin <i>Martii</i> \"March\" / <i>Maii</i> \"May\") cataloguing slip, but the witness register reads <i>martij</i> and the source does not decide it. The slug retains the catalogue date for dataset continuity. No conversion to New Style is made (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700).<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected 'fiden' to 'siden' (a long-s/f misread) in the beacon-neglect clause; Dreyer's page (TBOO XIV p.28) uses the same long-s glyph here as in the adjacent 'som'/'sig'. Translation already correctly rendered 'since' and is unaffected.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the five body-negations are reproduced one-for-one. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02) after Dreyer collation</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV, entry 43; <i>Skaanske Tegnelser</i> I, 405): (1) the closing <i>icke … wilt stande til rette</i> is fixed as a genuine <b>threat of liability</b> ('you will then not escape answering for it') by Dreyer's regest and the parallel beacon-patent of 19 April 1585 (entry 44); (2) the date is <b>26 March 1585</b> — Dreyer both heads and datelines it 26 March, so the dataset's '26 May' (and the slug) is an error with no textual basis (the slug is kept only for dataset continuity). A milder secondary uncertainty (<i>thend gamble løchte, som nu er</i>) is noted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV p.28 as page image (letter = item 43, entirely on p.28); re-read at close zoom and compared the disputed glyph against the unambiguous long-s letterforms 'ſom' and 'ſig' in the same line to confirm the word is 'ſiden' (siden), not 'fiden'. Aside from this one corrupted word, source_text matches Dreyer verbatim, and the full translation (the neglected Kullen beacon, the prior request for a better site, the warning that the duty may be reassigned) is faithful throughout. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though the translation itself is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text reads 'fiden' where Dreyer's page in fact shows the long-s letterform 'ſiden' (= 'siden', 'since'), matching the same long-s glyph used in the adjacent words 'ſom'/'ſig' on the same line — a classic long-s-misread-as-f corruption. The English translation correctly rendered the sense ('since that time'), so it is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>end mindre thend gamble løchte, ſom nu er, ſiden thend thid att were holdit ſom thet ſig bør</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.28-30 (full slice, read in one pass); the word is confirmed on p.28 within item 43's body text, using the same long-s letterform as neighbouring words on the line. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Skaanske tegnelser, I, 405."], "th": {"label": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15841211001", "next": "brahe-t_15870924001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Rebukes Tycho for neglecting the Kullen beacon, citing sailors' complaints and noting that royal letters of command to the toll-officers have not been carried into effect.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850526001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850623001", "d": "1585-06-23", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 23 June 1585 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHE NOBILI DANO, MATHEMATICO CLARISSIMO, AMICO PERCHARO HENRICUS BRUCAeUS.<br>S. P.</p><p>ChARISSIME TYCHO, pecuniam qua VITO Typorum fusori et mihi satisfieret, accepi. De Calendario per tuorum aliquem edendo utrum sententiam mutâris, nescio; in tanta tamen iudiciorum incertitudine malim nihil tale edi, ne artis vanitas confirmetur. Quod si quid certi subesse iudicas, age, prodeat ille tuus in lucem, et declaret, se vel a te vel aliunde plus didicisse quam alij. Quanquam ego ab omnibus et laboratum esse hactenus frustra et in posterum laboratum iri existimem, rationibus meo iudicio, quae refelli nequeant, id ipsum demonstrantibus. Cedam tamen, si videro, me a quoquam ipsis hisce praedictionibus vinci, nec quaeram contra experientiam rationes. De Astrologi istius operibus facile arbitrabar, te ita sensurum, cum aliquot paginas inspexissem. Sic magnis voluminibus saepe et claris titulis imponitur mundo. Nihil hisce nundinis prodijt Mathematicum, nec nostri Bibliopolae quicquam attulerunt venale. Literas si quas Pragam mitti ad HAGECIUM velis, dabo operam ut deferantur. Legi tuos versus,</p><p>qui in PTOLEMAeUM et COPERNICUM scripti sunt, et inprimis mihi placuere. Praeter enim carminis venustatem multa sunt eleganter inventa et erudite tradita. Vide, ut tuos Cometas brevi habeamus, cum iam sis instructus Typis. Quod si plura a VITO desideres, offert suam operam. In me quoque nec voluntatem nec studium defuisse agnosces. Tractatus noster de Crepusculis est in fine libri secundi de motu primo; eum si legas, non dubito placiturum.</p><p>Bene vale Nobilissime amantissime TYCHO.<br>Rostochij pridie Iohannis An. Domini 1585.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe, noble Dane, most renowned mathematician, his very dear friend — Heinrich Brucaeus.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Dearest Tycho, I have received the money with which Vitus the type-founder and I were to be satisfied [paid]. Whether you have changed your mind about a calendar being published by one of your people, I do not know; yet amid such great uncertainty of judgements I should prefer that nothing of the kind be published, lest the vanity of the art be confirmed. But if you judge that something certain underlies it, come, let that man of yours come forth into the light, and let him show that he has learnt more, whether from you or from elsewhere, than others have. Although for my part I reckon that thus far all men have laboured in vain, and that in time to come too the labour will be in vain — reasons that to my judgement cannot be refuted demonstrating this very thing. Yet I shall yield, if I see myself bested by anyone in these very predictions, and I shall not seek reasons against experience. As to that astrologer's works, I readily supposed you would think so, once I had inspected a few pages. Thus the world is often imposed upon by great volumes and resounding titles. Nothing mathematical has appeared at this fair, nor have our booksellers brought anything for sale. If you wish any letters to be sent to Prague, to Hagecius, I shall see to it that they are delivered. I have read your verses,</p><p>which were written against [on] Ptolemy and Copernicus, and they pleased me exceedingly. For besides the charm of the poem, many things are elegantly conceived and learnedly set forth. See to it that we soon have your Comets, now that you are furnished with a press. And if you should want anything more from Vitus, he offers his service. In me too you will recognize that neither goodwill nor zeal has been wanting. Our treatise on twilight is at the end of the second book on the primum mobile [the first motion]; if you read it, I do not doubt it will please.</p><p>Fare you well, most noble, most beloved Tycho.<br>At Rostock, the day before [the feast of] St. John, in the year of the Lord 1585.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Bruyaerts/Brucaeus, 1531–1598), Flemish-born professor of medicine and mathematics/astronomy at the University of Rostock and one of Tycho's senior correspondents and former teachers; he advised on observational and computational matters and (as here) on the propriety of publishing astrological calendars. Identification confirmed against Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (Cambridge, 2007).</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHE NOBILI DANO, MATHEMATICO CLARISSIMO</i> — the conventional learned superscription (\"noble Dane, most renowned mathematician\").</li><li><i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (dicit), \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Vitus the type-founder.</b> <i>VITO Typorum fusori</i> — <b>Vitus</b>, the <i>type-founder</i> (caster of printing type) whom Tycho had engaged for his newly established Uraniborg press; <i>Typorum fusor</i> is the trade term for a type-caster (rendered \"type-founder\"). The opening sentence concerns money Tycho had remitted to settle Brucaeus's outlay on Tycho's behalf to this craftsman. <i>satisfieret</i> here in its commercial sense, \"be paid / satisfied.\"</li><li><b>The calendar / astrology dispute.</b> <i>De Calendario per tuorum aliquem edendo</i> — Tycho had evidently proposed that one of his associates (\"one of your people\") publish an annual astrological <b>calendar / prognostication</b>. Brucaeus, a confirmed sceptic of judicial astrology, counsels against it \"lest the <i>vanity of the art</i> be confirmed\" (<i>ne artis vanitas confirmetur</i>). His epistemic stance is preserved verbatim: he holds that all such labour has been and will be <i>frustra</i> (\"in vain\"), by reasons he believes irrefutable, <b>yet</b> explicitly concedes he will <i>yield</i> (<i>cedam</i>) if shown to be bested by actual successful <i>praedictiones</i> and will \"not seek reasons against experience\" — a hedged, falsifiability-minded position kept intact, not sharpened into flat denial.</li><li><i>De Astrologi istius operibus</i> — \"that astrologer's works\": an unnamed astrologer whose books Tycho and Brucaeus both judged inflated (\"the world is often imposed upon by great volumes and resounding titles\"). The identity is not given in the text; left unspecified rather than guessed.</li><li><b><i>nundinis</b></i> — \"this fair,\" i.e. the book fair (the great Frankfurt/Leipzig <i>nundinae</i>); rendered \"fair.\" The complaint is that no mathematical book appeared there this season.</li><li><b>Hagecius.</b> <i>HAGECIUM … Pragam</i> = <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku)</b> of Prague, the imperial physician-astronomer and a key node in Tycho's correspondence network; Brucaeus offers to forward Tycho's letters to him. <i>Pragam</i> = <b>Prague</b>.</li><li><b>The verses on Ptolemy and Copernicus.</b> <i>tuos versus, qui in PTOLEMAeUM et COPERNICUM scripti sunt</i> — Tycho's Latin <b>verses</b> (epigrams) <i>on / against</i> Ptolemy and Copernicus; <i>in</i> + acc. here is the dedicatory/topical \"on, addressed to\" rather than purely hostile, hence \"against [on]\" with the ambiguity flagged. (These are the elogia Tycho later printed; the line in the source between the two paragraphs falls mid-sentence — a layout artefact of the transcription, preserved.)</li><li><b>The Comets.</b> <i>tuos Cometas … cum iam sis instructus Typis</i> — Brucaeus urges Tycho to bring out his work on <b>comets</b> (the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, on the comet of 1577, then in preparation), \"now that you are furnished with a press [<i>Typis</i>]\" — i.e. the Uraniborg printing office. <i>Typis</i> \"(printing-)type / press.\"</li><li><b>Brucaeus's own treatise.</b> <i>Tractatus noster de Crepusculis est in fine libri secundi de motu primo</i> — \"Our treatise <i>on twilight</i> is at the end of the second book <i>on the primum mobile</i>.\" <i>crepusculum</i> = <b>twilight</b> (protocol §5.1). <i>de motu primo</i> = on the <i>primum mobile</i> / the \"first motion\" (the diurnal rotation of the heavens), the standard subject heading of a <i>sphaera</i> / spherical-astronomy textbook; Brucaeus refers to his own such work, which contains a section on the duration/geometry of twilight (rendered \"the first motion\" with the technical term glossed).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Rostochij pridie Iohannis An. Domini 1585</i> = \"At <b>Rostock</b> [Rostochium, §5.7], the day before [the feast of] St. <b>John</b> [the Baptist], in the year of the Lord 1585.\" The Nativity of St. John the Baptist is <b>24 June</b>; <i>pridie</i> (\"the day before\") therefore yields <b>23 June</b> (O.S.; Denmark and Lutheran Mecklenburg both kept the Julian calendar in 1585). This agrees with the catalogue label (\"1585. 23. juni\"); no discrepancy. The dateline is given by liturgical idiom, glossed to the civil date per protocol §3.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the dating is unambiguous (<i>pridie Iohannis</i> = 23 June). The one soft point is the force of <i>in PTOLEMAeUM et COPERNICUM</i> (\"on\" vs. \"against\") for Tycho's verses, flagged above rather than silently decided; the unnamed astrologer is left unidentified rather than guessed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.92-93 as page images. Letter 'TYCHONI BRAHE NOBILI DANO, MATHEMATICO CLARISSIMO, AMICO PERCHARO HENRICUS BRUCAEUS' runs from mid p.92 to the top of p.93, closing 'Rostochij pridie Iohannis An. Domini 1585.' source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim. Collated opening/closing and every proper name and topic (Vitus the type-founder, Hagecius, Ptolemy and Copernicus, the Comets treatise, the twilight/primum mobile treatise) against the Latin; the English is a complete, accurate rendering with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation. Fully collatable (tboo present and legible), so the pipeline's self-assigned B is revised upward to A.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15850404001", "next": "brahe-t_15860315001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of payment, questions whether Tycho still intends to publish a calendar, and expresses skepticism that astrological prediction can be made reliable.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850623001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15850825001", "d": "1585-08-25", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 25 August 1585 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.65 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET VIRTUTE ERUDITIONEQUE PRAeEMINENTI VIRO D. DOCTORI THADDAeO AB HAYCK, MEDICO ET MATHEMATICO INPRIMIS EXCELLENTI, INCOLAe PRAGENSI, AMICO SUO PERPETUO COLENDO.<br>HERRN DOCTOR THADAeUS ZU PRAG ZUERFRAGEN.<br>S.</p><p>PERFUNCTUS Legatione apud Caesaream Maiestatem, a Serenissimo Rege nostro sibi adiuncta, reversusque huc in patriam frater meus STENO BRAHE, non saltem literas mihi a te attulit, Eruditissime et longe charissime Domine D. THADDAeE, sed insuper narravit, quanta humanitate ipsum exceperis, quamque liberaliter et iucunde tractâris. Quod sane magna cum voluptate percepi, tibique gratias habeo plurimas, quod mei absentis memoriam hac ratione in fratre praesente recolueris. Nam ex benevolentia ipsi praestita, me quoque ob veterem quae nobis intercedit necessitudinem a te complexum esse, satis evidenter persuasum habeo, relaturus sane gratiam, si de te vel ullo tuorum bene merendi unquam offeratur occasio. Rescripsissem vero tuis literis maturius, si per nuncij commoditatem id licuisset. Interea vero dum hinc inde perquiri curo, qua ratione ad te nostrae mittantur, ecce forte fortuna accessit quidam, qui se in istis Bohemiae oris habitare aiebat, cupereque cum quodam ex mihi inservientibus Artificibus natione Bohemo (qui aliquot annis in quibusdam ad Instrumentorum fabricam facientibus diligentem mihi navavit operam) sermonem miscere, illiusque in patria, si quae velit, negotia procurare. Hac oblata scribendi commoditate, cum is meas etiam ad te perferri literas, sedulo se curaturum addiceret, non potui non, utut alijs maxime occupatus negocijs, sepositis omnibus, has illi ad te perferendas committere.</p><p>Quantum ad Dialexin tuam a te denuo locupletatam attinet, cum editionem eius differri ob Typographorum avaritiam intelligam, et mihi eius copiam offeras, si de mea voluntate certior factus fueris, rogo ut eam ad me prima quaque occasione transmittas. Dabo operam ut vel hîc meis typis edatur, vel tibi quamprimum citra ullam libri iacturam remittatur. Eorum etiam quae de Nova Stella et aliquot Cometis praecedentibus annis conspectis a diversis hinc inde in medium allata sunt, si singulorum exemplaria unâ miseris, rem feceris longe gratissimam. Conscripsi enim et ego quaedam de hoc toto negotio, quae proprijs typis propediem, Deo conatus nostros felicitante, edere constitui, id quod hactenus, ex quo omnia ad Typographiam pertinentia in promptu parata haberem, ob Papyri inopiam, quam peculiarem requiro, procrastinatum est. Cum enim Mercenariae naves partim ob earum arrestationem in Hispania, partim ob Antwerpiae diuturnam obsidionem hac aestate fretum hoc, in solita copia, non transnavigaverint, factum est, ut Papyro, quae hac ratione inter alias merces e Gallijs et Belgicis civitatibus copiosa et varia apportari solet, praeter spem destitutus sim. Verumtamen per naves Hollandicas, papyrum nostro negotio, in sufficienti copia et forma, aptam, a Telonij Regij, quod Helsingorae est, praefecto, mihi certo procuratam, indies expecto; quamprimum vero haec allata fuerit, editionem non diutius differam. Interim percuperem variorum in hac materia opiniones perspicere, ut constare queat, quinam veritatis metam, ad quam tendimus, collimatius attingant, vel si quae devia inciderint, quorsum ea deflectantur, explorem. Te itaque nostris desiderijs nequaquam defuturum tum propria (quae exactissima fore persuasum habeo) tum etiam aliorum placita mittendo, nullum mihi movetur dubium. Ubi nostra de his commentatio excusa fuerit, per primam quamque oblatam occasionem hanc tecum vicissim communicabo, tuaeque censurae lubens subijciam. Percepi insuper ex literis tuis, duos illos Nobiles Gallos, qui me hîc inter peregrinandum salutârant, tecum aliquandiu suaviter vixisse, vosque interea Chymica quaedam medicamenta confecisse; ego vero, cum hi mecum essent, ignorabam eos vulgaris Medicinae nedum Spagyricarum praeparationum fuisse studiosos, alias et ego de hac re cum illis non iniucunde contulissem. Ostendi quidem ipsis meum, quod subterraneum est, Laboratorium, idque licet in ijs quae ad Pyronomicam artem faciunt, non vulgariter sit instructum, tamen illi dissimulabant se quicquam horum intelligere. Quo consilio id fecerint, ignoro. Nec interim dum hic adsunt, quicquam intellexeram eos tam cito Pragam venturos, alias nequaquam sine meis te accessissent literis. Petis ut quempiam Chymicarum praeparationum peritum ad te mittam, si forte peregrinando huc appulerit, id quod lubenter facturus sum, sed vereor ne votis tuis hac in parte minime eventus respondeat. Rarissimi enim sunt apud nos his exercitijs addicti, ideoque rariores eo nomine huc confluunt. Alchymistis vulgaribus, qui se multa scire vaniloque profitentur, quantum sit fidendum, ipsemet facile ex tot illorum imposturis perspectum habes. Meum itaque erit consilium, ut fidelem aliquem iuvenem harum rerum studiosum tibi adiungas, ipsumque successive instruas, quoad in his laboribus per diuturnam exercitationem reddatur expeditior; fabricando enim, ut dici solet, fabri fimus. Si quid egregij hactenus a vobis in hac Arte inventum fuerit, velim mecum communices, ego vicissim eorum, quae in mea Officina Hermetica nata sunt et tibi placitura intellexero, te reddam participem. Cum unâ Ratisbonae essemus, libellum quendam a me accepisti, nescio cuius authoris, de Chymicis medicamentis praeparandis, sed et titulum libri neque exacte teneo, in octava forma fuisse satis recordor. Eum librum nusquam postea venalem habere licuit, utut saepius Bibliopolas eo nomine in Germania sollicitari curaverim, quod forte ideo minus fieri poterat, quia nomen authoris et titulus libri mihi exciderant. Peto itaque ut vel eius libelli exemplar, si forte uspiam apud vos venale exstat, vel saltem titulum libri cum authoris nomine ad me transmitti curare velis. Et si quae praeterea de hac materia noviter prodierunt, quae artem et ingenium sapiant, unâ per te obtineam, rem pergratam feceris. IOHANNIS WERNERI opusculum de motu octavae sphaerae Norimbergae olim, ut existimo, impressum multo tempore late conquisivi, sed nondum uspiam adeptus sum. Si itaque librum hunc istic nactus fueris, et me eius participem reddideris, tum etiam, si tractatum illum manuscriptum ignoti authoris de Directionum significationibus, quem Ratisbonae apud te vidi, per aliquem descriptum, adiunxeris, multum meis desiderijs gratificaveris. Sumtus omnes lubens remittam, tuisque votis vicissim, ubicunque licuerit, me praebebo officiosissimum. Commentaria TRAPEZUNTIJ in PTOLOMAeUM si adhuc penes te habes, necdum impressa prodierunt, sique eis carere potes, mihi perscribe, qua ratione transigendum tecum fuerit, ut ea (si alias Typis mandari nequeant) abs te impetrem. Fieri enim potest, ut proprijs impensis editionem eorum instituam, ne tanti viri excellens labor penitus intercidat, et usum destinatum Astronomiae studiosis omnino praecludat. Unum etiam adhuc restat, quod te magnopere, ni tibi nimium sim molestus, rogatum velim. Cum non mediocri sumtu propriam hîc Uraniburgi Typographiam adornârim, in qua non solum ea quae de Nova Stella et succedentibus aliquot Cometis a me coelitus animadversa diligenter exponuntur demonstranturque, sed etiam ea quae ad aeterna illa et Mundo coaeva sidera spectant, quaeque multis et exquisitis a coelo ipso (per Organa maxima, solide et ad amussim, ingenti labore et sumtu praecedentibus aliquot annis constructa) petitis observationibus, Astronomiam totam, tum quo ad Luminarium, tum etiam reliquarum tam errantium quam inerrantium Stellarum, exactam motus rationem, veritati apparentiarum (quatenus ea in sensus nostros vel subtilissime indaganti cadit) consentientem, pristino vigori restituunt; ea inquam et quam plurima alia ad Mathematicam inprimis scientiam pertinentia, quae vel hactenus a nobis absoluta sunt, vel in posterum, Deo vires et ingenium suppeditante, elaboranda veniunt, constitui (eodem favente) proprijs typis excudere, et Reipub. literariae non sine singulari, uti spero, emolumento communicare. Cumque praeter eas, quas tum in ipsam Typographiam omnibus necessarijs adminiculis rite instruendam insumserim impensas, tum etiam non minores in Operum, quae excudenda veniunt, apparatum requirantur, praesertim in his Mathematicis rebus, quae sine figurarum multiplici delineatione sumtuosos labores requirente, nequaquam dextre institui queunt (prout haec omnia tibi ipsi adeo perspecta sunt, ut longa non indigeant probatione): Idcirco ne tot sumtus et labores cum dispendio mearum rerum in rei literariae gratiam incassum faciam, si forte quae hîc meis Typis impressa fuerint, in Germania vel alibi confestim recudantur, per Amicitiam nostram te rogatum velim, ut a Caesarea Maiestate mihi vel per temetipsum vel per alios, Privilegium ratum impetres, ut intra decennium libros in Astronomia et reliqua Mathematica scientia a me elaboratos turpisque proprijs in hac mea Officina Uraniburgica excusos, in ullis Imperij locis Caesareae Maiestati subiectis, denuo imprimere vel publicare et divendere, sub certa mulcta et amissione exemplarium, nequaquam liceat. Cavebo vicissim, ne libri a me publicandi quidpiam, quod vel Relligionis controversijs, vel Reipublicae constitutioni offensioni siet, ullo in loco contineant. Et si quid ipsa Caesarea Maiestas vicissim hoc nomine a me praestandum postulaverit, ipsius Maiestati meam Operam et obsequium humiliter defero. Praeterea si qui sumtus in eos, qui haec ex Caesaris decreto exsequuntur, conscribendoque Privilegio occupantur, erogandi veniunt, quamprimum per te de his certior factus fuero, lubens omnia quae requiruntur mittam. Tuum itaque erit, charissime THADAeE, omnem movere lapidem, ut Privilegium hoc a Caesarea Maiestate primo quoque tempore obtineam. Cuperem enim idipsum operi meo, quod de novis aethereae Regionis Generationibus nostro aevo conspectis inscripsi, et omnium primo typis edere decrevi, praefigere, ut eo tutius per Autoritatem Caesarianum nostris laboribus et limitibus consulatur. Qua autem forma et quibus verbis Privilegium hoc optime comprehendi queat, tuae sagacitati latius indicandum disponendumque relinquo. Nec dubito quin meae de te expectationi hac in parte sedulo sis satisfacturus. Ut vero Typorum nostrorum, quos hactenus conquisivi, characterem perspicias, mitto tibi quaedam carmina, praeterita hyeme recreandi a serioribus studijs animi gratia ad quosdam Nobiles et eruditos amicos exarata, et ne Typi et Typographus ob Papyri inopiam prorsus feriarent, in nostra Officina excusa. Addidi etiam aedificij nostri in hac insula Astronomiae honori et restaurationi sumtuose extructi designationes aliquot, ut tibi absenti aliquo modo aedes hasce Uraniae (cui et tu ab ineunte aetate sacra litasti) dicatas, intueri liceat. Omnium quoque in hisce contentorum Organorum Astronomicorum delineationem unâ misissem, sed nondum ob sculptoris mei in Germaniam abitum, eorum figurae penitus absolutae sunt. Binorum saltem Instrumentorum picturam mitto, quorum fabricam et usum ex ipsa inspectione facile assequeris. Ubi sculptor reversus fuerit, caeterasque machinas suis lineamentis expresserit, faciam te eorum participem. Curo enim has omnes figuris convenientibus excidi, eo quod peculiari volumine harum omnium structuram et multiplicem infallibilemque usum, Astronomiae alumnis patefacere decreverim. Sed video me copiosius tecum agendo epistolae modum excedere, quare ne te ulterius, quam par est, detineam, finem scribendi faciam, teque cum tota domo Divinae tutelae commendabo. Vale, et nostris desiderijs prima quaque opportunitate responde. Si nulla offeratur commodior ratio, literas unâ cum ijs de quibus mentionem feci, Rostochium ad Doctorem HENRICUM BRUCAeUM perferri cura. Is enim confestim per crebra quae inde huc appellunt navigia me eorum compotem reddet.</p><p>Iterum mi charissime THADAeE vale quam felicissime.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 25. Augusti Anno 1585.<br>Tui amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 97; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si ante instantem hyemem responsum a te accepero, rem feceris mihi longe gratissimam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned man, pre-eminent in virtue and erudition, Master Doctor Thaddaeus ab Hayck, physician and mathematician of the first excellence, citizen of Prague, his friend to be honoured perpetually.</b><br><b>Doctor Thaddaeus at Prague, to be enquired for.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Having discharged the embassy to His Imperial Majesty entrusted to him by our Most Serene King, and having returned hither to his homeland, my brother Steno Brahe not only brought me a letter from you, most learned and far dearest Lord, Master Thaddaeus, but moreover related with what great kindness you received him, and how generously and pleasantly you entertained him. This indeed I learned with great pleasure, and I owe you the warmest thanks that in this way, in my brother's presence, you kept in mind one who was absent. For from the goodwill shown to him I am quite plainly persuaded that I too — on account of the old bond that subsists between us — have been embraced by you; and I shall surely repay the favour, if ever an occasion offers of deserving well of you or any of yours. I would indeed have replied to your letter sooner, had the convenience of a messenger allowed it. But while I was seeing to enquiries, this way and that, by what means my letters might be sent to you, behold, by sheer good fortune there came a certain man who said that he dwelt in those parts of Bohemia, and that he wished to fall into conversation with one of the craftsmen in my service, a Bohemian by nation (who for some years did diligent work for me on certain matters pertaining to the making of instruments), and to attend to that man's affairs in his homeland, if he should want anything. This opportunity of writing being offered — since he undertook that he would also see to it carefully that my letters were conveyed to you — I could not but, however much I was busied with other matters, set everything aside and entrust these [letters] to him to convey to you.</p><p>As for your <i>Dialexis</i>, now enlarged anew by you: since I understand that its publication is being delayed through the greed of the printers, and you offer me a copy of it if you shall have been made more certain of my wish, I ask that you send it to me at the very first occasion. I shall take pains either that it be published here with my own types, or that it be returned to you as soon as possible without any loss of the book. Of those things too which, concerning the New Star and several Comets observed in the preceding years, have been brought forward into public by various men here and there — if you send copies of each together [with it], you will do a most welcome thing. For I too have composed certain things about this whole matter, which I have determined to publish shortly with my own types, God prospering our endeavours — something which up to now, although I had everything pertaining to the press ready at hand, has been put off for want of paper, of which I require a particular kind. For since the merchant ships, partly because of their seizure in Spain, partly because of the long siege of Antwerp, did not this summer sail through this Sound in the usual quantity, it came about that I have been, beyond expectation, left without paper — which, by this route, among other merchandise, is wont to be brought in abundant and varied supply from the cities of France and the Low Countries. Nevertheless, through Dutch ships, I daily expect paper fit for our business, in sufficient quantity and format, certainly procured for me by the warden of the Royal Toll which is at Helsingør; and as soon as this has been brought, I shall delay the edition no longer. Meanwhile I should much like to inspect the opinions of various men on this subject, so that it may be established who reach more nearly on the mark [collimatius] the goal of truth toward which we strive, or, if any have fallen into byways, that I may explore in what direction they have swerved. And so I move no doubt that you will by no means fail our desires, by sending both your own views (which I am persuaded will be most exact) and also those of others. When our commentary on these matters has been printed, I shall in turn communicate it to you by the very first occasion offered, and gladly submit it to your judgement. I learned moreover from your letter that those two noble Frenchmen who had greeted me here during their travels lived for some while pleasantly with you, and that you meanwhile prepared together certain chemical medicines; but I, when they were with me, did not know that they were students of ordinary medicine, much less of spagyric preparations, otherwise I too would have conferred with them on this matter not unpleasantly. I did indeed show them my Laboratory, which is underground, and although it is fitted out for the things that pertain to the pyronomic art [the art of fire] in no common way, yet they dissembled that they understood any of these things. With what design they did this, I do not know. Nor meanwhile, while they were here, had I understood that they would come to Prague so soon, otherwise they would by no means have approached you without a letter from me. You ask that I send to you someone skilled in chemical preparations, should one perhaps put in here while travelling — which I shall gladly do; but I fear that in this part the outcome may scarcely answer to your wishes. For very rare among us are those given to these exercises, and therefore the rarer do they flock hither on that account. As for how far one is to trust the common alchemists, who profess vaingloriously to know much, you yourself easily have it perceived from so many of their impostures. My counsel therefore will be that you attach to yourself some faithful young man, a student of these matters, and instruct him by degrees, until by long practice he be rendered more expert in these labours; for, as the saying goes, by making [things] we become makers [smiths]. If anything excellent has so far been discovered by you in this Art, I should wish you to share it with me; I in turn will make you a sharer of those things which have been born in my Hermetic Workshop and which I shall have understood will please you. When we were together at Regensburg, you received from me a certain little book, of I know not what author, on the preparing of chemical medicines; but neither do I exactly hold the title of the book — that it was in octavo format I well enough recall. That book I have nowhere since been able to have for sale, although I have often had booksellers in Germany solicited for it by name — which perhaps could the less be done for that reason, because the author's name and the book's title had slipped from me. I ask therefore that you see to it that there be sent to me either a copy of that little book, if perhaps it is anywhere for sale among you, or at least the title of the book together with the author's name. And if besides anything has lately come out on this subject that savours of skill and ingenuity, [and] I obtain it through you at the same time, you will do a most agreeable thing. The little work of <b>Johannes Werner</b> <i>On the Motion of the Eighth Sphere</i>, printed long ago at Nuremberg, as I think, I have widely sought after for a long time, but have not yet anywhere obtained. If therefore you have come upon this book there, and make me a sharer of it, and then too, if you add — copied out by someone — that manuscript treatise of an unknown author <i>On the Significations of Directions</i>, which I saw at Regensburg in your keeping, you will much gratify my desires. All the costs I shall gladly remit, and in turn, wherever it shall be allowed, I will show myself most dutiful to your wishes. <b>Trapezuntius's</b> Commentaries on <b>Ptolemy</b>, if you still have them in your possession, [and] they have not yet come out in print, and if you can do without them, write to me by what arrangement the matter is to be settled with you, so that I may obtain them from you (if otherwise they cannot be committed to the press). For it may be that I will undertake their edition at my own expense, lest the excellent labour of so great a man perish utterly, and altogether shut off the use intended for students of astronomy. One thing still remains, on which I should greatly wish to ask you — unless I be too troublesome to you. Since at no slight cost I have here at Uraniborg furnished a press of my own, in which are diligently set forth and demonstrated not only those things which, concerning the New Star and several succeeding Comets, I have observed from the heavens, but also those things which regard those eternal stars coeval with the World, and which — by many and exquisite observations sought from the heaven itself (through the greatest <b>instruments</b>, soundly and to a nicety, with vast labour and cost constructed in some preceding years) — restore to its former vigour the whole of Astronomy, both as to the exact reckoning of the motion of the Luminaries and also of the rest of the Stars, both wandering and fixed, [a reckoning] consonant with the truth of the appearances (so far as that falls to our senses, even to one investigating most subtly): these things, I say, and very many others pertaining especially to Mathematical science, which either have so far been completed by me, or in future, God supplying strength and ingenuity, come to be worked out — I have determined (with His favour) to print with my own types, and to communicate to the Republic of Letters, not without singular profit, as I hope. And since, beyond the expenses which I have laid out both upon duly furnishing the press itself with all necessary apparatus, and also no smaller ones [are] required for the apparatus of the Works which come to be printed — especially in these Mathematical matters, which without the manifold drawing of figures, requiring costly labours, can by no means be aptly set up (all of which is so thoroughly evident to you yourself that it needs no long proof): therefore, lest I make so many costs and labours, with loss of my own means, in vain for the sake of letters, should perhaps the things printed here with my types be at once reprinted in Germany or elsewhere — by our Friendship I should wish to ask you that you obtain for me from His Imperial Majesty, either through yourself or through others, a confirmed <b>Privilege</b>, [providing] that within ten years it be by no means permitted, in any places of the Empire subject to His Imperial Majesty, to reprint anew, or publish and sell, the books in Astronomy and the rest of Mathematical science worked out by me and printed with my own types in this my Uraniborg Workshop — under a fixed fine and forfeiture of the copies. I shall take care, in turn, that the books to be published by me contain in no place anything which may give offence either to the controversies of Religion or to the constitution of the Commonwealth. And if His Imperial Majesty in turn shall demand anything to be performed by me on this account, I humbly offer my service and obedience to His Majesty. Moreover, if any costs come to be expended upon those who execute these things by the Emperor's decree, and who are occupied in drawing up the Privilege, as soon as I have been made more certain of these through you, I will gladly send all that is required. It will be yours therefore, dearest Thaddaeus, to move every stone, that I may obtain this Privilege from His Imperial Majesty at the very first time. For I should wish to prefix it to that work of mine which I have entitled <i>On the new Generations of the ethereal Region beheld in our age</i>, and which I have resolved to publish first of all — so that the more safely, through Imperial Authority, provision may be made for our labours and bounds. But in what form and in what words this Privilege may best be framed, I leave to your sagacity to indicate and dispose more fully. Nor do I doubt that in this part you will diligently satisfy my expectation of you. And that you may perceive the character of our types, which I have so far procured, I send you certain poems, written last winter to certain noble and learned friends for the sake of refreshing the mind from more serious studies, and printed in our Workshop so that the types and the typographer should not be wholly idle for want of paper. I have added also some delineations of our building, raised at great cost on this island for the honour and restoration of Astronomy, so that you, [though] absent, may in some way look upon this house dedicated to Urania (to whom you too, from your earliest years, have offered sacred rites). I would have sent together a drawing of all the Astronomical <b>instruments</b> contained in these [buildings], but their figures are not yet entirely completed, owing to my engraver's departure into Germany. I send at least a picture of two Instruments, whose construction and use you will easily grasp from the very inspection. When the engraver has returned, and has rendered the rest of the machines in their outlines, I shall make you a sharer of them. For I am having all of these cut in suitable figures, because I have resolved to lay open, in a separate volume, the structure and the manifold and infallible use of all of them, to the nurslings of Astronomy. But I see that, in dealing with you more copiously, I exceed the measure of a letter; wherefore, that I may not detain you further than is fitting, I shall make an end of writing, and commend you, with your whole house, to the Divine protection. Farewell, and answer our desires at the very first opportunity. If no more convenient means offer, see that the letters, together with those things of which I have made mention, are conveyed to Rostock to Doctor <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b>. For he, by the frequent vessels which put in here from there, will at once make me possessed of them.</p><p>Again, my dearest Thaddaeus, fare you most happily.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 25 August, the year 1585.<br>Your most loving<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>with his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If I shall have received a reply from you before the coming winter, you will do me a thing most welcome indeed.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Thaddaeus ab Hayck / Thaddaeus Pragensis</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck)</b> (1525–1600), the leading Bohemian physician, astronomer and astrologer, personal physician at the Imperial court at Prague, and Tycho's long-standing friend and correspondent. The pair had met at the Imperial Diet of Regensburg (<i>Ratisbona</i>) in 1575, twice referenced here. Hagecius was Tycho's principal channel to the Imperial chancery; this letter sets in motion the application that yielded the printing <b>Privilege</b> Rudolf II granted Tycho.</li><li><b>Salutation rubric (German).</b> <i>HERRN DOCTOR THADAeUS ZU PRAG ZUERFRAGEN</i> — \"Doctor Thaddaeus at Prague, to be enquired for\" — a German address/direction line over the Latin honorific superscription; a delivery instruction (the bearer is to ask after the addressee at Prague). The code-switch is preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b><i>Steno Brahe.</b></i> <b>Steen Brahe</b> (1547–1620), Tycho's brother, here returning from a royal embassy to the Imperial court; he had carried Hagecius's letter back to Hven.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimo Rege nostro</b></i> — <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), \"our Most Serene King.\"</li><li><b><i>Dialexis</b></i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574), his tract on the 1572 New Star, here described as \"enlarged anew\" (a revised/expanded version Hagecius was preparing). Kept as the title; not translated.</li><li><b><i>Nova Stella … aliquot Cometis</b></i> — the <b>New Star</b> (the 1572 nova, <i>stella nova</i> in Cassiopeia) and the comets of the intervening years (notably the comet of 1577); Tycho is gathering the published responses for his own work in progress. <i>De novis aethereae Regionis Generationibus</i> (below) is the project — eventually the <i>Astronomiae instauratae Progymnasmata</i> and the comet volume.</li><li><b><i>collimatius</b></i> — \"more nearly on the mark\"; <i>collimare</i> (the period orthography of <i>collineare</i>, to aim/sight along a line) is Tycho's habitual metaphor of taking aim at the \"goal (mark) of truth\" (<i>veritatis metam</i>). Rendered with the aiming sense; the Latin flagged.</li><li><b>Paper-supply passage.</b> The shortage is tied to two datable events of 1585: the Spanish seizure (<i>arrestatio</i>) of foreign (esp. Dutch) merchant ships in Spanish ports (May 1585) and the long <b>siege of Antwerp</b> (taken by Parma, 17 August 1585). <i>Telonium Regium … Helsingorae</i> = the <b>Sound Toll</b> at <b>Helsingør</b> (Elsinore); its <i>praefectus</i> (warden/customs-master) is procuring Dutch paper for Tycho.</li><li><b>Alchemy / chemistry.</b> <i>Spagyricae praeparationes</i> = <b>spagyric preparations</b> (Paracelsian chemical pharmacy); <i>Pyronomica ars</i> = \"the <b>pyronomic art</b>,\" the art of regulated fire (furnace-work), rendered with a gloss; <i>Officina Hermetica</i> = \"<b>Hermetic Workshop</b>\" (Tycho's alchemical laboratory at Uraniborg); <i>Laboratorium … subterraneum</i> = the underground laboratory. <i>fabricando … fabri fimus</i> — \"by making we become makers,\" a proverb (a variant of <i>fabricando fit faber</i> / <i>fabricando fabri fimus</i>); kept as the maxim it is. <i>vaniloque</i> (for <i>vaniloquae</i> / vaingloriously) describes the boasting of common alchemists.</li><li><b>Books sought.</b> <i>Iohannis Werneri opusculum de motu octavae sphaerae</i> = <b>Johann Werner</b>, <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (Nuremberg, 1522), on the motion of the eighth (fixed-star) sphere — i.e. precession/trepidation. <i>Commentaria Trapezuntij in Ptolomaeum</i> = the commentary of <b>Georgius Trapezuntius</b> (George of Trebizond, 1395–1472/3) on <b>Ptolemy's</b> <i>Almagest</i>; Tycho offers to print it at his own expense if Hagecius will release the manuscript. <i>tractatus … de Directionum significationibus</i> — an anonymous manuscript treatise \"On the Significations of <b>Directions</b>\" (<i>directio</i> = a predictive astrological technique, per glossary §5.3), which Tycho had seen in Hagecius's keeping at Regensburg.</li><li><b>Instruments / observatory.</b> <i>Organa maxima</i> = \"the greatest <b>instruments</b>\" (per glossary §5.2; the great Uraniborg instruments); <i>Typographia … Uraniburgi</i> = Tycho's own <b>press at Uraniborg</b>; <i>aedes Uraniae</i> = the house \"dedicated to <b>Urania</b>\" (Uraniborg, lit. \"castle of Urania,\" the Muse of astronomy); <i>sacra litasti</i> echoes the Tychonic flourish \"devote yourself to the rites of Urania\" (§5.5). The \"two Instruments\" whose pictures he encloses, and the <i>peculiare volumen</i> on the structure and use of all his instruments, anticipate the <i>Astronomiae instauratae Mechanica</i> (1598).</li><li><b><i>Astronomiam … pristino vigori restituunt</b></i> — \"restore the whole of Astronomy to its former vigour\": Tycho's programme of <i>instauratio / restitutio astronomiae</i> (glossary §5.1), rendered with \"restore … to its former vigour\" / \"restoration of Astronomy.\"</li><li><b>The Privilege request (legal core).</b> The operative clause is a single negative prohibition: Tycho asks that <i>nequaquam liceat … denuo imprimere vel publicare et divendere</i> — \"that it be <b>by no means permitted</b> … to reprint anew, or to publish and sell\" — his works, within ten years (<i>intra decennium</i>), anywhere in the Empire, <i>sub certa mulcta et amissione exemplarium</i> (\"under a fixed fine and forfeiture of the copies\"). One prohibition, governing the triad reprint / publish / sell; reproduced as one negation. His reciprocal undertaking is itself negative — <i>Cavebo … ne libri … quidpiam … contineant</i> (\"I shall take care that the books contain nothing …\") — a second, distinct negation, kept as such. No conditional is reversed or merged.</li><li><b><i>turpisque proprijs</b></i> <b>(textual crux).</b> The witness reads <i>\"a me elaboratos <b>turpisque proprijs</b> in hac mea Officina Uraniburgica excusos.\"</i> As transcribed, <i>turpisque</i> (\"and base/shameful\") is unconstruable here and is almost certainly a copyist's corruption of <b><i>typisque propriis</b></i> (\"and <b>[printed] with my own types</b>\") — exactly the phrase Tycho uses twice elsewhere in this same letter (<i>proprijs typis … edere; proprijs typis excudere</i>). The translation follows the evident reading <b>\"printed with my own types\"</b>; the corrupt printed form is retained in the Source block per protocol §4(2). The MS is a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>), consistent with a scribal slip.</li><li><b><i>Heinrich Brucaeus.</b></i> <i>Doctor Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1530/31–1593), professor of medicine and mathematics at <b>Rostock</b> (<i>Rostochium</i>); Tycho proposes him as the postal relay, since ships ply frequently between Rostock and Hven.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 25. Augusti Anno 1585</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, 25 August 1585\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline matches the catalogue <i>label</i> (1585.25.august.Uraniborg); no discrepancy. <i>manu propria</i> — \"with his own hand,\" the autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None present (no Greek interpolation, no planetary or zodiac glyphs in this letter).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (after \"manu propria\"), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 97 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the legal/negation structure of the Privilege clause is reproduced one-for-one. The single flagged crux is the witness's <i>turpisque proprijs</i>, taken as a copyist's corruption of <i>typisque propriis</i> (\"with my own types\"); the printed form is preserved and the emendation argued in the apparatus rather than silently adopted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full collation: read all 6 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.93-98, chunked 1-4 then 5-6), covering the entire Hagecius letter from opening salutation to closing signature and the postscript at the foot of p.97. Checked every proper name (Steno Brahe, Thaddaeus Hagecius, Henricus Brucaeus, Johannes Werner, Trapezuntius/Ptolemaeus), place name (Uraniborg, Helsingoer, Nuremberg, Regensburg, Rostock), number (decennium/ten years, Binorum/two instruments) and the date (25 August 1585) against the page images; all match source_text and published_english exactly. The postscript, flagged in source_text as previously dropped by the working transcription and restored, is confirmed genuinely printed at the foot of Dreyer p.97 (immediately before the next letter, to Andreas Severini, begins) and is present and correctly translated in the published English. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Thaddaeum Hagecium a. 1585", "sh": "Cod. 10686/65", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961746"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.65. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15820923001", "next": "brahe-t_15851213001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15820501001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15851213001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Hagecius for the kindness shown to his brother Steno Brahe during the latter's embassy to the Emperor, and pledges to repay the favour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15850825001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15851020001", "d": "1585-10-20", "dir": "third", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": "Henrik Rantzau", "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Rotenburg an der Fulda", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Heinrich Rantzau — Rotenburg, 20 October 1585 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery, with a Latin rubric and heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … (1596) (= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>EXEMPLAR LITERARVM ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI HASSIAe LANDTGRAVII AD NOBILISSIMVM DOMINVM HENRICVM RANTZOVIVM, DE COMETA ANNI 85, QUAe EX PARTE OCCASIONEM PRAeBUERUNT ALIQUOT SUBSEQUENTIBUS.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIGENHAYN VND NIDDA.</p><p>VNSERN gnedigen gruß zuuor/ Vester lieber besonder/ wir haben ewer schreiben de dato Segenberg/ den 22 verschienes Monats Septemb. neben dem vns zugeschickten Buch empfangen/ Thun vns deßwegen gegen euch gnediglich bedancken/ vnd wollen dasselbige zu seiner zeit mit lust verlesen. Darneben wollen wir euch auch nicht verhalten/ das vff angeben Pauli Wittichij/ wir vnsere Instrumenta Mathematica dermassen verbessert/ das/ da wir zuuor kaum 2 Min. scharff/ wir jetzo ½ ja ¼ einer min. obseruiren können/ Haben vns derhalben vff die Art Quadrantem Horizontalem, deßgleichen ein Sextantem, ad obseruandas distantias Stellarum inter se, lassen zurichten/ jedes von gutem Messing vnd bicubital. Halten auch drey Gesellen Astronomiae et Obseruationum peritos ad iustificanda loca Stellarum Fixarum, Wiewol vnsere vorige Obseruationes, die wir selbst in otio vor angehung vnser Regierung nostris oculis genommen/ vnd dauon jhr als wir nicht anders wissen/ Copey gehabt/ nit vber 4 oder minut. vffs meiste ab hisce discrepiren werden/ hoffen auch bald damit zu endt zukommen/ wann wir nur klar Wetter circa nouilunium hetten/ welches vns diß gantze Jahr vber gemangelt. Nuhn has vns vnser Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS zugeschrieben/ dz er einen kleinen Cometen vff den 8 huius, Abends vmb 9 vhr in 23 grad. 15 minut. piscium, cum Latitudine merid. 14 grad. 0 min. Vnd vff den 9 eiusdem in 25 grad. 50 min. piscium cum Latitud. merid. 13 grad. et 6 min. obseruiret, Denselbigen haben wir gestern den 19 zu Abend allhier doch mit keinen Instrumenten, sondern allein per lineas rectas vff vnserm Globo obseruiret, vnd jhn im 21 gr. Arietis, cum Declinatione latitud. 4 gr. merid. gefunden. Er ist klein/ vnd vmb vnd vmb hero voller Haer/ das wir achten/ er sey ex genere Cometarum, so man vnsers versehens Circaeos nennet/ vnd tempore maximarum coniunctionum zuerscheinen pflegen/ Was er nun bedeuten wird/ sonderlich dieweil er gar Saturninus, vnd Saturno coniunctus, gibt die zeit/ vnd möchten wol wissen/ ob er auch bey euch gesehen werde/ Mögen auch wol leiden/ das jhr TYCHONEM BRAHEN mit vormeldung vnsers gnedigen grusses/ Apparitionem et Obseruationem huius Cometae, zuwissen thut/ mit begeren/ das er vns zuwissen thue/ wie er jhn Obseruiret habe/ Denn weil er so klein/ halten wirs dafür/ er werde von weinigen obseruirt werden/ Das wollen wir vns vorsehen/ vnd seind euch mit Gnaden geneigt/</p><p>Datum Rottenburgk am 20 Octobris, Anno 85.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A copy of a letter of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, to the Most Noble Lord Heinrich Rantzau, concerning the comet of the year [15]85, which letters in part gave occasion to several of those that follow.</b><br><b>Wilhelm by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Our trusty and well-beloved: we have received your letter dated Segeberg, the 22nd of the past month of September, together with the book sent to us, and we therefore thank you graciously for it, and intend to read it through with pleasure in due time. Besides this we will also not conceal from you that, upon the instruction of Paul Wittich, we have so improved our mathematical instruments [Instrumenta Mathematica] that, whereas before we could scarcely observe [to] 2 minutes precisely, we can now observe [to] half — indeed a quarter — of a minute. We have therefore had set up after that fashion a horizontal quadrant [Quadrantem Horizontalem], likewise a sextant [Sextantem], for observing the distances of the stars from one another [ad observandas distantias Stellarum inter se], each of good brass and two cubits [in size]. We also keep three journeymen skilled in astronomy and in observations [Astronomiae et Observationum peritos] for verifying the places of the fixed stars [ad iustificanda loca Stellarum Fixarum] — although our earlier observations, which we ourselves took at leisure with our own eyes [nostris oculis] before the commencement of our reign, and of which, so far as we know, you have had a copy, will at most differ from these by no more than 4 minutes [or so]; and we hope soon to come to an end with it, if only we had clear weather around the new moon [circa novilunium], which has been lacking to us this whole year through. Now our mathematician Christoph Rothmann has written to us that he observed a small comet on the 8th of this month, in the evening at about 9 o'clock, in 23 degrees 15 minutes of Pisces, with southern latitude [cum Latitudine merid.] 14 degrees 0 minutes; and on the 9th of the same [month] in 25 degrees 50 minutes of Pisces, with southern latitude 13 degrees and 6 minutes. This same [comet] we observed here yesterday, the 19th, in the evening — yet with no instruments, but only by straight lines [per lineas rectas] upon our globe [Globo] — and found it in 21 degrees of Aries, with a southern declination of latitude [cum Declinatione latitud.] of 4 degrees. It is small, and all about it full of hair [hairy], so that we judge it to be of the kind of comets [ex genere Cometarum] that, as we suppose, are called <i>Circaei</i>, and that are wont to appear at the time of the great conjunctions [tempore maximarum coniunctionum]. What it will now signify — especially since it is wholly Saturnine [Saturninus], and conjoined with Saturn [Saturno coniunctus] — time will tell; and we should well like to know whether it has been seen with you also. We can also well allow that you make known to Tycho Brahe, with the conveyance of our gracious greeting, the appearance and observation of this comet [Apparitionem et Observationem huius Cometae], with the desire that he make known to us how he has observed it; for, since it is so small, we hold it [likely] that it will be observed by few. This we shall await, and we are inclined to you with [our] grace.</p><p>Given at Rotenburg, the 20th of October, in the year [15]85.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the observing prince of Kassel, builder of one of the two great pre-telescopic observatories (with Tycho's on Hven). The titulature names his subsidiary fiefs: <i>Catzenelnbogen</i> (Katzenelnbogen), <i>Dietz</i> (Diez), <i>Ziegenhain</i>, <i>Nidda</i>.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Rantzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the Danish duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, humanist patron and astrological writer, and a hub of scientific correspondence linking Tycho, Kassel, and the German learned world. His seat at <b>Segeberg</b> (<i>Segenberg</i>) is the place of his own letter here acknowledged.</li><li><b>Document type and frame.</b> The piece is not a letter to or from Tycho but a <b>copy (<i>Exemplar</i>) of Wilhelm's letter to Rantzau</b>, which Tycho printed as a prefatory document in his <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596) because, as the Latin rubric says, it \"in part gave occasion\" to the correspondence that follows. The rubric and the full titulature are in <b>Latin</b>, the body in <b>chancery German</b> with constant code-switching into Latin for technical nouns — preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Language note.</b> The body is <b>German</b> (Early New High German / chancery: <i>vff</i> = <i>auf</i>, <i>dz</i> = <i>daß</i>, <i>jhn</i> = <i>ihn</i>, <i>zuuor</i> = <i>zuvor</i>, <i>vmb</i> = <i>um</i>, <i>weinigen</i> = <i>wenigen</i>), not Danish despite the <i>de</i> heuristic tag — verified by reading. The opening <i>VNSERN gnedigen gruß zuuor / Vester lieber besonder</i> is the standard princely address formula, rendered per protocol §5.5 (\"Our gracious greeting beforehand … Our trusty and well-beloved\").</li><li><b>Paul Wittich.</b> <i>Pauli Wittichij</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Wrocław mathematician whose visit to Kassel in 1584 transmitted the instrument-improvement techniques (sights, transversal/diagonal graduation) he had learned at Hven — the reciprocal-influence figure between Tycho and Rothmann. The claim that Wittich's \"instruction\" raised Kassel's precision from ~2′ to ¼′ is Wilhelm's own (not silently sharpened).</li><li><b>Instruments (protocol §5.2).</b> <i>Quadrantem Horizontalem</i> → <b>horizontal quadrant</b>; <i>Sextantem … ad observandas distantias Stellarum inter se</i> → <b>sextant, for observing the distances of the stars from one another</b>; <i>bicubital</i> → <b>two cubits [in size]</b> (about 1 metre on a side). <i>Globo</i> → <b>globe</b> (a celestial globe, on which Wilhelm read off the comet's place graphically, <i>per lineas rectas</i>, \"by straight lines,\" lacking instruments that evening).</li><li><b>Astronomical terms (protocol §5.1).</b> <i>loca Stellarum Fixarum</i> → <b>places of the fixed stars</b>; <i>iustificanda</i> → <b>verifying</b> (here in the sense of correcting/confirming the catalogue places); <i>circa novilunium</i> → <b>around the new moon</b>; <i>Declinatione</i> → <b>declination</b> (but note the source's own redundant phrase <i>Declinatione latitud.</i>, lit. \"declination of latitude,\" flagged below). Positions are given in zodiacal longitude (degrees within a sign) with latitude; <i>merid.</i> = <i>meridionalis</i>, <b>southern</b>.</li><li><b>Astrology (protocol §5.3).</b> <i>Saturninus … Saturno coniunctus</i> → <b>Saturnine … conjoined with Saturn</b>: the comet is read as Saturn-natured and in conjunction with Saturn, hence ominous; <i>tempore maximarum coniunctionum</i> → <b>at the time of the great(est) conjunctions</b> (the Saturn–Jupiter great-conjunction doctrine). Pisces and Aries are named in English per §5.3 (the source writes the sign-names in Latin, <i>piscium / Arietis</i>; no glyphs appear).</li><li><b><i>Circaeos</b></i> — <i>Circaei</i>, a comet-class name. Wilhelm flags his own uncertainty (<i>vnsers versehens</i>, \"as we suppose\"), preserved as a hedge. The term is not in the standard Aristotelian/Ptolemaic comet taxonomies in this spelling; it appears to render a category of \"hairy\"/bearded comets (cf. the description <i>vmb vnd vmb hero voller Haer</i>, \"all about full of hair\"). Left untranslated and flagged as a lexical crux rather than forced to a modern class.</li><li><b>Comet of 1585.</b> The object is the comet of October 1585, independently observed at Kassel (by Rothmann, 8–9 Oct.) and noted here by Wilhelm (19 Oct., by globe). The request that Rantzau forward the appearance and observation to <b>Tycho Brahe</b> — and ask how Tycho observed it — is the occasion of the ensuing Tycho–Kassel exchange.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Both dates — Rantzau's letter of <i>22 September</i> (Segeberg) and Wilhelm's of <i>20 October</i> (Rotenburg) — are <b>Old Style</b>; Hesse, like Denmark, retained the Julian calendar until 1700. <i>Datum Rottenburgk am 20 Octobris, Anno 85</i> = \"Given at <b>Rotenburg</b> (Rotenburg an der Fulda), 20 October [15]85\" (O.S.). The internal dates (comet seen \"on the 8th … and on the 9th,\" and \"yesterday, the 19th\") are consistent with the 20 October dateline and with the catalogue label; no discrepancy. Per protocol §3 no date is converted.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The source carries two consequential negations, both reproduced and neither added: (1) <i>wir … wollen euch auch <b>nicht</b> verhalten</i> → \"we will <b>also not</b> conceal from you\" (a courtesy litotes = \"we wish you to know\"); (2) <i>werden … <b>nit</b> vber 4 … minut. … discrepiren</i> → \"will differ by <b>no more</b> than 4 minutes\" — the precision boast, where the negation governs the magnitude and must not be dropped or doubled.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German chancery prose and the Latin technical insertions render securely and the dates are internally consistent; the one unresolved point is the comet-class term <b><i>Circaei</b></i> (lexical crux, named above and left untranslated rather than forced), with a minor secondary uncertainty in the source's pleonastic phrase <i>Declinatione latitud.</i> (\"declination of latitude\"), rendered literally and flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (one of the 49 with no Dreyer slice), so per the brief this was audited against source_text alone and capped at B regardless of outcome. Checked the German source_text against published_english clause by clause: every proper name (Wittich, Rothmann, Tycho Brahe), instrument description (Quadrantem Horizontalem, Sextantem, bicubital brass), and numeric/astronomical figure (2 min./ 1/2-1/4 min. precision; comet position 23gr15m and 25gr50m Pisces with the two southern latitudes; the 19th/21 degrees Aries/4 degrees south declination) renders faithfully with no fabricated, dropped, or flipped content. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports improvements to the Kassel instruments following Paul Wittich's instruction, now achieving quarter-minute precision, and describes a new horizontal quadrant and sextant.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15851020001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15851111001", "d": "1585-11-11", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Uraniborg, 11 November 1585 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer,</i> Opera Omnia *VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEIUM.<br>S.</p><p>PERIUCUNDUM mihi auditu fuerat, amantissime D. Magister ANDREA, quod nuper Hafniae ex epistola tua Magnifico Domino DANZAeO inscripta intellexeram te brevi nos invisurum, modo hyems iter tuum non impediverit, vel potius gentis in retractandis pollicitis consuetudo. Cur vero nunc has ad te mittam, fraudulenta discessio cuiusdam servi me impulit, quod se in hunc modum habet: ob turpiter et inhoneste commissa facinora cum ancilla mea, quae claves ad penu meum conservabat, cum qua ipse et libidinem et furta exercuit, unâ cum hoc altero, quem nunc secum abduxit, quae etiam eo nomine cum infamia a me dimissa est. Haec tamen omnia hactenus dissimulavi, parcens ipsius famae, ob aetatem, artem et Ripenses amicos, adeo ut nemo me ea rescivisse de ipso intellexerit, sed saltem in alios corrivales ipsius tota culpa redundârit, nulla ipsius facta mentione, prout nostra postulat amicitia, quia est res malo exemplari propter alios meos opifices et servos, impune citra omnem causam et necessitatem se subducere. Ubi ipsum recepero, si resipuerit et culpam deprecatus fuerit, lenius cum illo agam quam promeritus est, praesertim te pro illo per literas intercedente. Omnem itaque move lapidem, charissime M. ANDREA, ut certo mihi restituatur, quod sane non difficulter efficere poteris, et da operam ne intelligam te minus sincere meas partes in hoc negocio agere, ne labefactetur amicitia nostra, quae propemodum vacillare nonnihil mihi videtur ex literis Domino DANZAeO a te perscriptis, ubi vehementiae et acerbitatis ob nescio quos versus me insimulas. Callide quidem satis pro teipso, ut apud aemulos per tralationem a suspicione consensus te liberes, sed pro tuo TYCHONE minus candide et fortasse etiam pro intimo tuo proprio sensu et iudicio minus vere. Vale et da operam, ut in hoc negocio tuam erga me fidem experiar. Saluta METTAM cum utroque parente ceterisque amicis nostris. Iterum vale et admonitionem hanc meam ut libere prolatam patienter et sincere accipe.</p><p>Iterum terque quaterque vale et ante hyemem, si per tempestates licuerit, nos invise.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 11. Nov. Anno 1585.<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>mea manu.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 98; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>De alijs rebus et privatis negotijs forsan alias commodius vel etiam coram tutius agam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>It had been most delightful for me to hear, most loving Master Anders, what I lately learned at Copenhagen from your letter addressed to the Magnificent Master Dançay — that you would shortly visit us, provided the winter does not hinder your journey, or rather the nation's habit of going back on what it has promised. But the reason why I now send these [lines] to you is that the fraudulent departure of a certain servant has driven me [to it], the matter standing thus: on account of deeds shamefully and dishonourably committed with a maidservant of mine — she who kept the keys to my storeroom, with whom he practised both lust and thefts, together with this other [man] whom he has now carried off with him — she too has on that account been dismissed by me in disgrace. Yet all this I have hitherto dissembled, sparing his reputation on account of his age, his trade, and his Ribe friends, so that no one has perceived that I had found this out about him, but rather the whole blame has overflowed onto certain other rivals of his, with no mention made of his deed — as our friendship requires — because it is a matter of bad example, on account of my other workmen and servants, that he should withdraw himself with impunity, without any cause or necessity. When I have got him back, if he comes to his senses and begs pardon for his fault, I will deal more leniently with him than he has deserved, especially with you interceding for him by letter. So move every stone, dearest Master Anders, that he may surely be restored to me — which indeed you will be able to bring about without difficulty — and take care that I do not perceive you to be playing my part in this affair less than sincerely, lest our friendship be undermined, which seems to me to be on the point of wavering somewhat, from the letter written out by you to Master Dançay, where you charge me with vehemence and harshness on account of some verses or other. Cunningly enough, to be sure, on your own behalf — so that among [our] rivals you might, by shifting [it onto me], free yourself from the suspicion of complicity — but on behalf of your own Tycho less candidly, and perhaps also, as regards your own innermost feeling and judgement, less truthfully. Farewell, and take care that in this affair I may put your good faith toward me to the test. Greet Mette, with both her parents and the rest of our friends. Again farewell, and receive this admonition of mine, as freely uttered, patiently and sincerely.</p><p>Again, and thrice and four times, farewell; and before winter, if the storms allow, visit us.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 11th day of November, in the year 1585.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Of other matters and private affairs I shall perhaps treat more conveniently on another occasion, or even more safely face to face.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), royal historiographer, translator of Saxo Grammaticus (1575), and Tycho's old friend; based at <b>Ribe</b> (hence the <i>Ripenses amici</i>, \"Ribe friends,\" below). Addressed throughout as <i>Magister</i> / <i>M. Andrea</i> (academic \"Master,\" protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (§5.5). Subscription <i>mea manu</i> = \"in my own hand.\"</li><li><b>Dançay.</b> <i>Magnifico Domino Danzaeo</i> / <i>Domino Danzaeo</i> = <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (Carolus Danzaeus, d. 1589), French royal ambassador (<i>legatus regis Galliarum</i>) resident at the Danish court, a friend and patron of Tycho; the <i>Opera Omnia</i> index lists him under <i>Leg. Gal.</i> (legatus Galliae) and records his role at Uraniborg's foundation-stone laying. Vedel's letters reached and were copied at Copenhagen, where Tycho read of his intended visit.</li><li><b>Mette.</b> <i>Mettam</i> = <b>Mette</b>, Vedel's wife (Mette Hansdatter), greeted \"with both her parents.\" Kept as the Danish given name.</li><li><b>Place / calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 11. Nov. Anno 1585</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven), the 11th day of November, 1585\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label; header date as written.</li><li><b>The runaway-servant matter.</b> <i>fraudulenta discessio cuiusdam servi</i> = \"the fraudulent departure of a certain servant.\" Tycho writes that this <i>servus</i> had carried off with him \"this other [man]\" (<i>hoc altero, quem nunc secum abduxit</i>) and had been entangled with a <i>ancilla</i> (\"maidservant\") who held the storeroom keys; she has been dismissed <i>cum infamia</i> (\"in disgrace\"). Tycho asks Vedel to <i>omnem move lapidem</i> (\"move every stone,\" = leave no stone unturned, the proverbial <i>omnem movere lapidem</i>) to have the man <i>restored</i> to him (<i>restituatur</i>) — i.e. returned, as an absconding servant under early-modern master-servant obligation.</li><li><b>Negation control.</b> The source carries the principal negations <i>non impediverit</i> (\"does not hinder\"), <i>nulla ipsius facta mentione</i> (\"no mention made of his deed\"), <i>ne intelligam te minus sincere … agere</i> (\"that I do not perceive you to be acting less than sincerely\"), and <i>ne labefactetur amicitia</i> (\"lest our friendship be undermined\"). The doubled litotes <i>minus candide … minus vere</i> (\"less candidly … less truthfully\") is preserved as understatement, not flattened to \"dishonestly/falsely.\"</li><li><b>The quarrel over verses.</b> <i>ob nescio quos versus me insimulas … vehementiae et acerbitatis</i> = Vedel had, in his letter to Dançay, accused Tycho of \"vehemence and harshness\" <i>on account of some verses or other</i> (Tycho's dismissive <i>nescio quos</i>, \"I know not what / some … or other,\" is kept as a hedge). <i>per tralationem … a suspicione consensus te liberes</i> — <i>tralatio</i> (= <i>translatio</i>) here in its rhetorical/figurative sense of <i>transferring</i> or <i>shifting</i> [the blame] onto Tycho so as to clear himself of \"the suspicion of complicity\" (<i>consensus</i>) before their <i>aemuli</i> (\"rivals\"). Rendered \"by shifting [it onto me]\"; the bracketed object is editorial.</li><li><b>Idioms / register.</b> <i>iter tuum non impediverit, vel potius gentis in retractandis pollicitis consuetudo</i> — a barbed aside, \"or rather the nation's habit of going back on what it has promised\" (<i>gens</i> = the [Danish] people/nation; <i>retractare pollicita</i> = to withdraw promises); kept as written. <i>Iterum terque quaterque vale</i> echoes the Virgilian <i>terque quaterque beati</i> (\"thrice and four times\"), an intensifying farewell-flourish; rendered literally.</li><li><b>Editorial insertions.</b> Bracketed words — \"[lines],\" \"[man],\" \"[other man],\" \"[it onto me],\" \"[our],\" \"[to it],\" \"[yet]\" — supply grammatically implied objects/subjects with no separate word in the Latin, per protocol §4.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (after \"mea manu\"), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 98 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin reads securely and the legal/epistolary force (the request to recover an absconded servant; the rebuke over the verses) is rendered without smoothing. The one soft point, flagged rather than resolved: the exact construction of <i>unâ cum hoc altero, quem nunc secum abduxit</i> — whether <i>hoc altero</i> is a second male servant the runaway took with him (adopted reading) or refers loosely to the maidservant's further accomplice — is left slightly open by the syntax; the adopted reading follows the masculine <i>altero … quem</i>.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.97-99 as page images. The letter runs from the bottom of p.97 through p.98, including the postscript 'De alijs rebus et privatis negotijs forsan alias commodius vel etiam coram tutius agam' printed at the foot of p.98 immediately before the source citation line - confirming the packet's apparatus note that this postscript is genuinely in Dreyer and was correctly restored. Collated the servant/maidservant narrative (three-person structure: fleeing servant, ancilla, 'hoc altero'), all proper names (Danzaeus, Mette), the Ripe/Ribe reference, and the closing date/signature (11 Nov 1585) word-for-word. source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english renders the syntax (including the correct antecedents for 'cum qua'/'quem'/'quae') accurately throughout.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15840216001", "next": "brahe-t_15891120001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports the dismissal of a servant who committed theft and fornication with a maidservant, and asks Vedel's assistance in the resulting domestic matter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15851111001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15851213001", "d": "1585-12-13", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 13 December 1585 [O.S.] (= 23 December N.S.)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy/Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTI DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE DANO DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISSIME Domine TYCHO et amice observandissime, longa intercapedo locorum facit, ut rarissime per literas colloqui possimus, alioqui non intermitterem, quin te saepius meis literis salutarem, aut si iter esset saltem dierum sex aut 8, in hac mea ingravescente aetate, ipsoque senio (nam a die 1. Octob. 60 ingressus sum aetatis annum) ipsemet te convenirem. Cum autem praeter omnem meam spem et opinionem incidissem in HENRICUM meum popularem servum Magnif. D. IACOBI SEGURII Legati Sereniss. Regis Navarrae ad quosdam Germaniae principes, ac is servus Dresdâ salutandi amicos suos gratia Pragam excurrisset, diceretque se in Daniam rectâ cum Domino suo profecturum esse, nolui eum vacuum meis literis dimittere istuc neque hanc occasionem scribendi ad te praetermittere. Scias igitur me unâ cum meis omnibus integra adhuc ex Dei beneficio frui valetudine, praeterquam quod senum morbi crebrius iam me invadunt, vires animi et corporis languescunt, ut etiamsi quam maxime velim oblectare studijs Astronomicis, non possim. Taceo alia multa. Meam Dialexin auxeram iam olim aliquot problematis partim novis, partim ex DIGGSEI Alis et Scalis derivatis de invenienda parallaxi quorumcunque Phaenomenûm; in multis memet correxi a priore editione. Et ut esset veluti Synopsis quaedam scriptorum de Nova Stella, volui omnium Authorum scripta nostris consona uno volumine edere, quibus adiunxi aliquot Cometarum historias. Qua de re iam ante ad te perscripseram, ac tu rogaveras, ne tuum scriptum adiungerem, quod illud ipse elimaturus pluraque ei additurus esses. Hoc meum opus nondum quidem prodijt, quod sane non mea culpa, sed Typographorum accidit, quod Astronomica non libenter edant. Cum autem intellexissem te domi in tua Urania etiam Typographiam instituisse pro edendis tuis lucubrationibus, tractaverisque idem, quod ego, argumentum de inventione parallaxium, nactus hanc occasionem, volui mittere ad te meam illam Synopsin, ut vel ordine a me designato eam (siquidem tibi probabitur) tuis typis ederes vel excerperes inde, quae tibi utilia viderentur, tuoque operi adiungeres. Ego certe gratum nostrum studium et operam posteritati fore iudico, si plenam historiam a pluribus conscriptam novi istius sideris proposuerimus. Nihil dico de doctrina parallaxeos omnium phaenomenωn, quae hucusque a nemine fuit tradita. Tu ea omnia multo rectius intelligis, quam ut a me explicari possint. Nihil dicam de his amplius. Sed librum meum quaeso accipe et evolve, et si placet dignumque editione censes, volesque illum tuis typis edere aut tuo operi adiungere, retineto apud te. Sin displicuerit, aut aliquid intercesserit, quod tuis typis imprimi non possit, rogo ut meum librum per eundem mihi remittas. Nullum enim aliud exemplum habeo, nollemque quantulumcunque hunc meum laborem perire. Quicquid statueris de libro, ratum et firmum erit apud me. Omnem tibi in illum potestatem trado, adeoque in me, ut libere moneas et reprehendas, fidemque amicitiae in hac re mihi potissimum praestes.</p><p>B. V.<br>Pragae ad lucernam, quod servus ille D. SEGURIJ summo mane abitum pararet, die 13 Decemb. veteris Calendarij, nos 23 numeramus, Anno 1585<br>T. studiosiss.<br>THADD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Lord Tycho Brahe, the Dane, outstanding in nobility of birth, in virtue, and in learning, my lord and friend to be respected.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Most noble Lord Tycho and most esteemed friend, the long distance between our places makes it that we can converse by letter only very seldom; otherwise I would not fail to greet you more often by my letters — or, if the journey were of only six or eight days, in this my advancing age and very old age (for on 1 October I entered my sixtieth year) I would meet with you in person. But since, beyond all my hope and expectation, I had come upon Henricus, my fellow-countryman, a servant of the Magnificent Master Jacobus Segurius, envoy of the Most Serene King of Navarre to certain princes of Germany — and since that servant had made an excursion from Dresden to Prague for the sake of greeting his friends, and said that he would set out directly into Denmark with his master — I was unwilling to send him off thither empty of any letter of mine, nor to let slip this occasion of writing to you. Know, then, that I, together with all my household, still by God's kindness enjoy unbroken health, except that the ailments of old men now assail me more frequently, and the powers of my mind and body grow faint, so that even though I should most greatly wish to take delight in astronomical studies, I cannot. Of much else I say nothing. My <i>Dialexis</i> I had, some while ago now, enlarged by several problems, partly new, partly derived from Digges's <i>Wings and Ladders</i>, on finding the parallax of any phenomena whatsoever; in many places I corrected myself from the earlier edition. And so that there might be, as it were, a kind of <i>Synopsis</i> of the writings on the New Star, I wished to publish in one volume the writings of all the authors that agree with our own, to which I have added some histories of comets. Concerning this matter I had already written to you before, and you had asked that I not add your own writing, since you were going to polish it yourself and to add more to it. This work of mine has not, indeed, yet come forth — which truly has happened not through my fault, but through the printers', because they do not willingly publish astronomical [works]. But since I had learned that you too had set up a printing-house at home in your Urania, for the publishing of your lucubrations, and that you had treated the same subject as I — on the finding of parallaxes — having got this occasion, I wished to send you that <i>Synopsis</i> of mine, so that either you might publish it in your types in the order I have laid out (if indeed it shall meet your approval), or might excerpt from it whatever should seem useful to you and add it to your own work. I, for my part, judge that our zeal and labour will be welcome to posterity, if we shall set forth a full history of that new star, compiled by many [hands]. I say nothing of the doctrine of the parallax of all phenomena, which up to now has been handed down by no one. You understand all those things much more correctly than they can be explained by me. I shall say nothing further about these matters. But take, I pray, my book and unroll it, and if it please you and you judge it worthy of publication, and you shall wish to publish it in your types or to add it to your own work, keep it by you. But if it shall displease you, or anything shall stand in the way so that it cannot be printed in your types, I ask that you send my book back to me by the same man. For I have no other copy, and I would not wish this labour of mine, however slight, to perish. Whatever you shall determine concerning the book will be settled and firm with me. I hand over to you all power over it, and indeed over myself, so that you may freely admonish and reprove [me], and may show to me, above all in this matter, the faith of friendship.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>At Prague, by lamplight — because that servant of Master Segurius was making ready to depart at break of day — on 13 December of the Old Calendar, which we reckon the 23rd, in the year 1585.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>THADD[aeus].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), Prague physician, mathematician, and astronomer to the Imperial court of Rudolf II; Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent and later instrumental in bringing him to Prague. He signs himself in the shortened form <i>THADD.</i></li><li><b>Self-dating / age.</b> <i>a die 1. Octob. 60 ingressus sum aetatis annum</i> — \"on 1 October I entered my sixtieth year.\" Taken with the birth year usually given as 1525 (1 October 1525), this is internally consistent. Reproduced as written; no correction made.</li><li><b><i>Iacobi Segurii … Legati … Regis Navarrae</b></i> — <b>Jacques de Ségur-Pardaillan</b>, diplomatic envoy of <b>Henri of Navarre</b> (the future Henri IV of France) sent in 1585 on a mission to the German Protestant princes (and onward, per this letter, toward Denmark) to build an anti-Catholic-League coalition. <i>Sereniss. Regis Navarrae</i> = \"the Most Serene King of Navarre.\" <i>HENRICUM meum popularem</i> = \"Henricus, my fellow-countryman\" (i.e. a fellow Bohemian/Czech in Ségur's service) — the courier of this letter.</li><li><b><i>Dialexis</b></i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt am Main, 1574), his treatise on the new star (nova) of 1572. He reports having enlarged it with new <i>problemata</i>. Title kept in Latin.</li><li><b><i>DIGGSEI Alis et Scalis</b></i> — Thomas <b>Digges</b>, <i>Alae seu scalae mathematicae</i> (\"Wings, or Mathematical Ladders,\" London, Thomas Marsh, 1573), the English work on determining the parallax/distance of the 1572 nova. <i>DIGGSEI</i> is the as-printed (metathesised) genitive of <i>Diggeseus</i>; <i>Alis et Scalis</i> = ablative of <i>Alae … scalae</i> (\"the <i>Wings and Ladders</i>\"). The title is rendered descriptively and the Latin form noted.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>parallaxi … parallaxeos … parallaxium</i> → <b>parallax</b> throughout (protocol §5.1). <i>invenienda parallaxi quorumcunque Phaenomenûm</i> = \"finding the parallax of any phenomena whatsoever\"; <i>doctrina parallaxeos omnium phaenomenωn</i> = \"the doctrine of the parallax of all phenomena.\" Here <i>phaenomena</i> denotes celestial appearances (novae, comets, and the like) whose distance is at issue — not \"phenomena\" in the modern generic sense.</li><li><b>Mixed-script spelling.</b> <i>Phaenomenûm</i> (gen. pl., printed with circumflex) and especially <i>phaenomenωn</i> — the latter written with a <b>Greek omega (ω)</b> embedded in an otherwise Latin word, a learned grapho-play on the word's Greek origin (<i>φαινόμενα</i>). The omega is preserved in the Source block per protocol §4.2; the word is read as the Latin genitive plural <i>phaenomenon</i> and rendered \"phenomena.\"</li><li><b><i>Nova Stella / novi … sideris</b></i> → \"the New Star\" / \"that new star\" — the supernova of 1572 (B Cassiopeiae), the common subject of Hagecius's, Digges's, and Tycho's works. <i>Synopsis</i> — Hagecius's planned collected volume of concordant authors on the nova, with appended <i>Cometarum historias</i> (\"histories of comets\"); title-word kept.</li><li><b><i>in tua Urania … Typographiam instituisse</b></i> — \"you had set up a printing-house … in your Urania,\" i.e. at <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, where Tycho had established his own press for his <i>lucubrationes</i> (learned night-works/writings). The substance dovetails with Tycho's contemporaneous parallax work on the nova and comets.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting,\" §5.5). <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene vale</i> (\"Fare you well,\" §5.5). <i>T. studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tui studiosissimus</i> (\"Most devoted to you\").</li><li><b>Calendar (in-letter double date — not converted by the editor).</b> The dateline reads <i>die 13 Decemb. veteris Calendarij, nos 23 numeramus</i> = \"13 December of the Old Calendar [Julian/Old Style], which we reckon the 23rd [Gregorian/New Style].\" Writing from Imperial Prague, which had adopted the Gregorian reform, Hagecius gives <b>both</b> styles, accommodating Tycho's Danish Old-Style usage while noting his own New-Style count — the 10-day gap stated explicitly in the source. The header leads with the as-written O.S. date (13 December, matching the catalogue label) and appends the source's own N.S. equivalent (23 December). No silent conversion or correction has been made.</li><li><b><i>Pragae ad lucernam</b></i> — \"at Prague, by lamplight\": written in haste before dawn because the courier was leaving at <i>summo mane</i> (\"at break of day\"). Place: <i>Pragae</i> = <b>Prague</b> (Hagecius's residence).</li><li><b>Minor textual point.</b> <i>nllem</i> (in <i>nllemque … hunc meum laborem perire</i>) is an evident misprint/contraction for <i>nollem</i> (\"I would not wish\"); read as <i>nollem</i> and so translated. <i>intercapedo</i> for classical <i>intercapedo/intercapedinem</i> and <i>amicitae</i> for <i>amicitiae</i> are as-printed orthographic forms, silently read to sense.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected: 'nllemque' to 'nollemque' and 'amicitae' to 'amicitiae', per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.99. A third proposed correction ('meam illam Synopsin' to 'illam Synopsin') was rejected on collation: Dreyer's page prints 'meam illam Synopfin', matching the existing source text exactly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the calendar double-date is explicit and unproblematic. Residual soft points, all flagged rather than smoothed: the as-printed <i>DIGGSEI</i> (metathesised genitive) and the Greek-omega spelling <i>phaenomenωn</i>; and the misprint <i>nllem</i> read as <i>nollem</i>.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — double-date explicit and clean; residue only declared orthographic forms (DIGGSEI, Greek-omega) + a manifest misprint </i>nllem→nollem<i>.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VII pp.98-100 (page images). Three small dropped/inserted-letter divergences found between our source_text and Dreyer's print; none affect the English, which renders the intended sense correctly in all three spots. The grading rule treats any TEXT_ERROR as disqualifying for A/B, so grade C despite low severity throughout. All proper names, the age detail (60th year, 1 October), and the double date (13/23 December 1585) match Dreyer exactly.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer VII p.99 reads \"volui mittere ad te illam Synopsin\"; our source_text inserts an extra possessive \"meam\" not present in the printed text. Dreyer prints: <i>illam Synopsin</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints \"nollemque\"; our source_text drops the \"o\", giving the garbled \"nllemque\". The English happens to render the intended sense correctly regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>nollemque quantulumcunque hunc meum laborem perire</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints \"amicitiae\"; our source_text drops the medial \"i\", giving \"amicitae\". Dreyer prints: <i>fidemque amicitiae in hac re mihi potissimum praestes</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.98-100, all 3 pages read). P.99 was read closely a second time to resolve the first defect: it clearly prints 'volui mittere ad te meam illam Synopfin', so the audit's claim that Dreyer omits 'meam' does not hold and that defect is rejected. The same page confirms 'nollemque' (not 'nllemque') and 'amicitiæ' (not 'amicitae') at the locations cited, both now fixed. No other cruces found on the three pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15850825001", "next": "brahe-t_15860509001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15850825001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15860701001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends greetings via a chance messenger — a servant of the Navarrese ambassador passing through Prague — and notes the difficulty of correspondence across such distance.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15851213001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860000001", "d": "1586-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "ck": "Rudolf 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Emperor Rudolf II — [Uraniborg/Hven, 1586; catalogued 28 March 1586, O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichisches Staatsarchiv, Beilage zum Reichsregistratur, 1586, 28. März</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>POTENTISSIME IMPERATOR D. D. CLEMENTISSIME.</p><p>CUM ex liberalitate et optimo erga me studiaque astronomica animo serinissimus rex Daniae, dominus meus clementissimus, astronomiae instaurandae gratia insulam Huenam mihi addixerit, in qua meis sumptibus extruxi arcem in varia domicilia ad literarum studia, exercitia astronomica, chymica, opificia, oblectamenta et ad alios usus distinctam et distributam; cui arci Uranieburgo a studio astronomico nomen indidi. Ut igitur et voluntati regis nostri et multorum de me expectationi satisfacerem, ab annis pluribus omne meum studium et operam in instauranda astronomia posui, iamque nonnulla in ea arte elaborata habeo, caetera sub incude, ut dicitur, versantur, adornantur et expoliuntur. Quae omnia in reipub: literariae commodum omnibus communia facere vellem, nostris praelis in eadem arce excusa. Verum plurimum vereor ne maximis meis impensis, quas tum in typographiam, tum in opifices feci, et in excudendis operibus mathematicis adhuc facturus sum, defrauder, si labores mei maximis sumptibus comparati ab alijs typographis in Germania et sub ditione Maiestatis Vestrae recudantur et divenduntur. Quare Maiestatem Vestram cum omni submissione rogo, quaecumque ex mea officina in mathematicis studijs proditura sint, sive à me elaborata, sive illustrata, ne ea a quoquam typographo recudantur, clementer privilegio ad decennium cavere dignetur, idque sub mulcta transgressoribus irrogari solita. Cavebo vicissim, ne libri a me publicandi quidpiam, quod vel religionis controversiis vel reipubl: constitutioni offensioni siet, ullo in loco contineant. Et si quid Caes: Ma: V: in hisce mathematicis studijs quidpiam à me postulaverit, aut ego intellexero qua in re gratum servitium eidem praestare possem, me semper promptum et paratum habebit, cui omne meum studium, operam, officium et obsequium humiliter defero. Et ut Caes: Ma: V: rectius intelligat et aedificium nostrae arcis et totum animi nostri propositum mitto eidem eiusdem arcis totiusque nostrae insulae designationem.</p><p>Cum his me Caes: Ma: V: commendo.<br>S: Caes: Ma: V: addictissimus<br>Tycho Brahe Danus Dom: de Knudstrup.</p>", "en": "<p><b>MOST POWERFUL EMPEROR, MOST MERCIFUL LORD AND MASTER.</b></p><p>Whereas, out of his generosity and his most excellent disposition toward me and toward astronomical studies, the most serene King of Denmark, my most merciful lord, has assigned to me the island of Hven for the sake of the renewal of astronomy [<i>astronomia instauranda</i>] — on which, at my own expense, I have raised a castle laid out and divided into various dwellings for literary studies, for astronomical and chemical exercises, for workshops, for recreations, and for other uses; to which castle I gave the name Uraniborg, from the astronomical study [pursued there] — therefore, that I might satisfy both the will of our King and the expectation many have of me, for several years now I have set all my zeal and labour upon the renewal of astronomy [<i>instauranda astronomia</i>], and I now have a number of things in that art worked out, while the rest are, as the saying goes, [still] on the anvil, being furnished and polished. All of which I should wish to make common to all, for the benefit of the republic of letters, printed off at my presses in that same castle. But I greatly fear that I may be defrauded of the very great expenses which I have incurred, both on the printing-house and on the workmen, and which I am still going to incur in printing off mathematical works, if my labours, procured at very great cost, should be reprinted and sold off by other printers in Germany and under the dominion of Your Majesty. Wherefore I ask Your Majesty, with all submission, that whatever shall issue from my workshop in mathematical studies, whether worked out by me or illustrated [by me], may not be reprinted by any printer; [and that] you would mercifully deign to provide for this by a privilege for ten years, and [that] this [be enforced] under the penalty customarily imposed upon transgressors. I, for my part, will take care that the books to be published by me contain nothing, in any place, that may give offence either to the controversies of religion or to the constitution of the commonwealth. And if Your Imperial Majesty should require anything of me in these mathematical studies, or if I should understand in what matter I might render some welcome service to the same, you will always have me ready and prepared, to whom I humbly offer all my zeal, labour, duty, and obedience. And that Your Imperial Majesty may the more rightly understand both the structure of our castle and the whole purpose of our mind, I send to the same a drawing [<i>designatio</i>] of that same castle and of our whole island.</p><p>With these [words] I commend myself to Your Imperial Majesty.<br>Most devoted to Your Imperial Majesty,<br>Tycho Brahe, the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender and recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), then resident on Hven, writing to <b>Rudolf II</b> (1552–1612), Holy Roman Emperor (r. 1576–1612), seeking the imperial printing privilege that would protect his self-published works against unauthorized reprinting in the Empire. The corpus confirms this is Tycho's first printing privilege, \"a ten-year Imperial charter awarded in 1586\" (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, 2007); \"by May 1586 Tycho had his ten-year copyright\" (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> / <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>). The privilege is the same one later cited (alongside <i>regum quorundam</i>) in the apparatus of the 1602 <i>Mechanica</i>.</li><li><b>Document type / no dateline.</b> The letter is preserved as the enclosure (<i>Beilage</i>) to the Imperial Registry (<i>Reichsregistratur</i>) at Vienna, filed under <b>28 March 1586</b>. The body carries <b>no dateline</b>: it ends with the subscription only. The dataset `label` (\"1586. empty\") records exactly this — a 1586 letter with an empty (absent) in-text date. Per protocol §4, the header therefore does not assert a date the source substantiates; it gives the year the text supports (1586) and appends the archival/registry date \"(catalogued 28 March 1586)\", flagged here rather than presented as an authorial dateline. <b>The \"28 March\" is the registry filing date, not necessarily the date of composition or dispatch.</b></li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Denmark and the Imperial chancery were both on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1586; the filing date 28 March is O.S. No conversion is performed (protocol §3).</li><li><b><i>astronomia instauranda / instauranda astronomia</b></i> — \"the renewal of astronomy,\" Tycho's programmatic phrase (<i>astronomia instaurata</i>, \"astronomy restored/renewed\"), kept consistent per glossary §5.1. The verb recurs twice in the source and is rendered identically both times.</li><li><b><i>arx / Uranieburgum</b></i> — \"castle\"; the name <b>Uraniborg</b> (\"citadel of Urania,\" the Muse of astronomy), Tycho's observatory-house on Hven, here spelled <i>Uranieburgo</i> in the source. <i>insula Huena</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>Dom: de Knudstrup</b></i> — \"Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>,\" Tycho's ancestral seat in Scania (kept as is, §5.7).</li><li><b><i>sub incude … versantur</b></i> — \"are on the anvil,\" a proverbial idiom (<i>incudi reddere</i>, Horace) for work still being hammered into final form; the metaphor is kept.</li><li><b><i>reipublica literaria</b></i> — \"the republic of letters,\" the commonwealth of learning; rendered literally.</li><li><b><i>designatio</b></i> — here a <b>drawing / plan</b> (a depiction of Uraniborg and the island enclosed with the letter); rendered \"a drawing,\" with the Latin flagged, since the term can also mean \"design/layout.\"</li><li><b>Editorial / orthographic notes.</b> <i>serinissimus</i> (for <i>serenissimus</i>) and <i>reipubl: constitutioni offensioni siet</i> are reproduced as transmitted; <i>siet</i> is the archaic subjunctive <i>sit</i> (\"may be\"). The chancery contractions are expanded silently only in translation: <i>Caes: Ma: V:</i> = <i>Caesarea Maiestas Vestra</i> (\"Your Imperial Majesty\"); <i>D. D.</i> in the address = <i>Domine ac Domine</i> (\"Lord and Master\"); <i>S:</i> before the subscription = <i>servus</i> / <i>subditus</i> (\"servant/subject,\" here taken with <i>addictissimus</i>). The penalty clause <i>sub mulcta transgressoribus irrogari solita</i> (\"under the penalty customarily imposed upon transgressors\") supplies no figure; \"[be enforced]\" is bracketed as an editorial supplement to the elliptical Latin.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The source contains the negations <i>ne … recudantur</i>, <i>ne … defrauder</i> (governed by <i>vereor ne</i>, \"I fear that\"), <i>ne ea a quoquam typographo recudantur</i>, and <i>ne libri … quidpiam … contineant</i>. Each is reproduced once in the translation; none added or dropped. Note that <i>vereor ne defrauder</i> is the standard Latin <i>verb-of-fearing</i> construction — \"I fear <b>that</b> I may be defrauded\" (not \"I fear that I may <b>not</b> be\"); the <i>ne</i> is the fearing-clause marker, not a negation of the verb, and is rendered accordingly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; the only soft points (the precise referent of <i>designatio</i> as a drawing/plan, and the registry filing date 28 March vs. an absent authorial dateline) are flagged above rather than smoothed. The text is plain petitionary Latin with no genuine crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the Hven grant and Uraniborg-naming passage, the printing-privilege request for a ten-year term, the anti-piracy and religious-inoffensiveness undertakings, the closing designatio offer) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.32-33 as page images, including the archival dating note (Vienna Reichsregistratur, 28 March 1586). Exact match throughout. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichisches Staatsarchiv, Beilage zum Reichsregistratur, 1586, 28. März."], "th": {"label": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000000003", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Describes Uraniborg and its facilities, reports progress on astronomical and chemical works, and petitions the Emperor for an imperial printing privilege to protect publication.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860130001", "d": "1586-01-30", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Birgitte Bølle", "ck": "Birgitte Bølle", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Birgitte Bølle — Uraniborg, 30 January 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (early-modern, polite-familial register) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Bøllings brevsamling, D. Fol. 68 (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELBYRDIG FRUE BERITTE BØLLE THIL GUNDERSLØFFHOLM, MIN KIERRE FADERSØSTER GANDTSKE WENLIGEN THILSCHREFFUIT.</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig helsen etther nu och althidt forsendt medt Gudt vor herre. Kierre fru Beritte och kierre fadersøster, betacker jeg etther saa gandske gierne for aldt ehre och gott, y mig althidt giortt och beuist haffuer, huilckett att forschylde jeg alle min dage will findes willig och redebon thil medt altt hues vdi min ringe formu kandt werre. Giffuendis etther wenligen thilkiende, att then byggemestere, som y schriffuer mig thil om, wedt naffn mester Hans, handt icke nu er her hoeß mig, men jeg wenntter hannom hidt att skulde komme om en dag tre eller fire. Och tror jeg icke andett, indt handt io well skulde kunde giørre samme arbedt, att thett schulle werre vstraffeligt, thersom handt icke haffuer tagett naagett andett arbedt ahn att bygge vdi sommer, men bliffuer medt etther om etthers arbedt forehnett. Huorfor jeg gierne will, naar handt hidt kommer thil mig, giffue hannom etthers arbedt ahn, och sige hannom ther om hues leylighedt y haffuer schreffuett mig thil, att handt fanger etthers wille her vdi att wide, och hues beschen handt giffuer fran sich, will jeg entten schrifftlich lade etther wide, eller handt och selff skall komme thil etther, och giffue etther nerwerendis thilkiende. Kierre fru Beritte och kierre fadersøster, will jeg icke lenger oppeholde etther medt thenne min schriffuelfe, men giør althidt gierne, hues jeg wedt etther thil ehre och tieniste kandt werre, och will her medt nu och althidt haffue etther then alsommechtigste Gudt thil siel och liiff befalendis.</p><p>DatumVraniborg then 30. januarii anno 1586.<br>Tyge Brahe<br>Egen hand.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Written most kindly to the honourable and well-born lady Birgitte Bølle of Gunderslevholm, my dear father's sister.</b></p><p>My wholly friendly greeting sent to you now and always with God our Lord. Dear Lady Birgitte and dear father's sister, I thank you so very gladly for all the honour and kindness you have always done and shown me, which to repay I shall all my days be found willing and ready, with all that may be within my poor means. Making known to you in friendly wise that the builder you write to me about, by name Master Hans, is not now here with me, but I expect him to come hither in a day or three or four. And I do not doubt but that he should indeed be able to do the same work, so that it would be beyond reproach, provided he has not taken on some other work to build this summer, but comes to an agreement with you about your work. Wherefore I am glad, when he comes hither to me, to put your work to him, and to tell him concerning it whatever circumstances you have written to me of, so that he may learn your wishes herein; and whatever answer he gives, I will either let you know in writing, or he shall himself come to you and make it known to you in person. Dear Lady Birgitte and dear father's sister, I will not detain you longer with this my writing, but [will] always gladly do whatever I know may be to your honour and service, and herewith will now and always have you commended to the most Almighty God in soul and body.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 30th of January in the year 1586.<br>Tyge Brahe<br>[in his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from his seat on Hven; the closing <i>Egen hand</i> (\"[in his] own hand\") marks the letter as an autograph — consistent with the witness being the surviving original (not a register copy).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Frue Beritte Bølle thil Gundersløffholm</i> = <b>Birgitte Bølle</b> (Berthe/Berete Bølle, d. 1592), of <b>Gunderslevholm</b> in Zealand, widow of the councillor Christoffer Gøye. Tycho addresses her as <i>min kierre fadersøster</i> — \"my dear father's sister\" (paternal aunt). The address-leaf style <i>Erlig och welbyrdig frue … thil Gundersløffholm</i> (\"honourable and well-born lady … of Gunderslevholm\") is the standard noble titulature; the seat-name <i>thil Gunderslevholm</i> denotes her as holder of that estate.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Vraniborg</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's house on the island of Hven.</li><li><b>Master Hans, the builder.</b> <i>then byggemestere … wedt naffn mester Hans</i> = \"the builder … by name Master Hans.\" On the testimony of Friis (<i>Bidrag til dansk Kunsthistorie</i>), the \"Bygmester Hans\" recommended in this very letter is most probably <b>Hans van Steenwinckel the Elder</b> (c. 1545–1601), the Netherlandish-Danish architect-builder then active in Denmark (engaged in 1585 on the restoration of St. Petri church in Copenhagen, and afterwards on the architectural work of Tycho's mural-quadrant decoration at Uraniborg). The identification is Friis's, offered as a probability; the letter itself names him only as <i>mester Hans</i>, so the surname is supplied in the apparatus and not inserted in the body.</li><li><b><i>byggemestere / arbedt</b></i> — \"builder\" / \"work\" (i.e. a building commission); Birgitte Bølle had evidently written to ask Tycho to engage Master Hans for building work on her estate, and Tycho here acts as intermediary.</li><li><b>Register.</b> A private kinship-and-patronage letter in the polite-familial Danish epistolary register, not chancery: the doubled courtesy formulae (<i>gandske wenlig helsen</i>, <i>kierre fru Beritte och kierre fadersøster</i>), the service-and-honour protestations, and the pious commendation-formula (<i>haffue etther … Gudt thil siel och liiff befalendis</i>, \"have you commended to God in soul and body\") are the conventional opening and closing topoi of the genre, rendered in matching English period courtesy rather than modernised.</li><li><b>Epistemic register preserved.</b> Tycho hedges throughout and these hedges are kept: <i>jeg wenntter hannom … att skulde komme</i> (\"I expect him … to come\"); <i>tror jeg icke andett, indt handt io well skulde kunde giørre</i> (\"I do not doubt but that he should indeed be able to do\"); and the conditional <i>thersom handt icke haffuer tagett naagett andett arbedt ahn … men bliffuer … forehnett</i> (\"provided he has not taken on some other work … but comes to an agreement\"). The single negation in that conditional (<i>icke haffuer tagett … ahn</i>, \"has not taken on\") is reproduced exactly once; the clause is a genuine condition on Hans's availability, not a statement that he has so taken on other work.</li><li><b>Orthography (early-modern Danish, c. 1586).</b> <i>thilschreffuit</i> = <i>tilskrevet</i> (\"written to\"); <i>etther</i> = <i>eder</i> (\"you,\" dat./acc.); <i>althidt</i> = <i>altid</i> (\"always\"); <i>betacker</i> = <i>betakker</i> (\"thank\"); <i>forschylde</i> = <i>forskylde</i> (\"repay, requite\"); <i>redebon</i> = <i>redebon</i> (\"ready\"); <i>ringe formu</i> = <i>ringe formue</i> (\"poor means/ability\"); <i>thilkiende</i> … <i>giffuendis</i> = <i>give til kende</i> (\"make known\"); <i>hannom</i> = <i>hannem</i> (\"him\"); <i>vstraffeligt</i> = <i>ustraffeligt</i> (\"beyond reproach, blameless\"); <i>forehnett</i> = <i>forenet</i> (\"agreed, united\"); <i>beschen</i> = <i>besked</i> (\"answer, information\"); <i>nerwerendis</i> = <i>nærværende(s)</i> (\"in person, present\"); <i>oppeholde</i> = <i>opholde</i> (\"detain\"); <i>alsommechtigste</i> = <i>allermægtigste</i> (\"most almighty\"); <i>siel och liiff</i> = <i>sjæl og liv</i> (\"soul and body/life\").</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datum Vraniborg then 30. januarii anno 1586</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 30 January 1586\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline date agrees with the catalogue label, so no discrepancy arises. <i>Datum</i> (\"Given\") kept per protocol §5.5 (cf. <i>Actum</i>); in the source it is run together with the place (<i>DatumVraniborg</i>) — printed thus and not re-spaced in the source block.</li><li><b>Language note.</b> The dataset heuristic tags this <i>da</i>, which reading confirms: it is early-modern Danish throughout (no German body, no Latin code-switch beyond <i>Datum</i> and the title-style <i>thil [Gundersløffholm]</i>). This is the German-orthography-Danish risk-class only insofar as forms like <i>schriffuer</i>, <i>schulle</i>, <i>wforsømmit</i>-type <i>w-/sch-</i> spellings appear — but the morphology and lexicon are Danish.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely and the conditional logic / single negation reproduced exactly; the one open point is the surname of <i>mester Hans</i>, identified as Hans van Steenwinckel the Elder only on Friis's probable attribution (kept in the apparatus, not asserted in the body).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.30-32 page images. Located the letter as item no.49 (abstract confirms recipient as Birgitte Bølle, widow of Christopher Gjøe), text beginning mid-p.31 and closing on p.32 ('Tyge Brahe, Egen hand.'). Collated the builder's name (mester Hans), the place and date (Vraniborg/Uraniborg, 30 januarii 1586) and full body text; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Bøllings brevsamling, D. Fol. 68.. Original letter"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Replies to his aunt's enquiry about a builder named Master Hans, who is expected within days, promising to relay her requirements when he arrives.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860130001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860301001", "d": "1586-03-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 1 March 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(Liber I, Uraniborg 1596) = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed-text letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO D. GVILIELMO HASSIAe<br>LANDTGRAVIO, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI<br>AC DOMINOA CLEMENTISSIMO. S. D.ACVM nuper apud Serenissimum Regem<br>nostrum in Aula versarer, Princeps Illustrissime, reddidit mihi generosus<br>Iuuenis GERARDVS RANTZOVIVS, Arcis<br>Croneburg, quam idem inclytus<br>Rex, in vicino Selandiae littore,<br>iam primum magnificeA exaedificari curauit, Praefectus, literas quasdam a Parente suo<br>Nobilissimo et Eruditissimo, rerumque Astronomicarum amantissimo Viro,<br>Domino HENRICO RANTZOVIO Regio<br>in Holsatia Vicario, ad me datas;<br>Quibus reseratis reperi in ijs Exemplar Literarum Tuae Celsitudinis ipsi D.<br>HENRICO RANTZOVIO<br>Scriptarum, quod percupide acceptum, semel atque iterum magna cum voluptate<br>relegi. Mirifice enim me recreauit, quod inde non obscure perceperim,<br>Celsitudin. Tuam erga Diuinae illius Astrorum Scientiae penitiorem<br>peruestigationem dudum inchoatam affectionem et studium, ne in hac quidem<br>maturiore aetate inter tam varias Reipub. administrandae occupationes et curasA, ex animo deposuisse; Sed potius denuo instauratis Viribus adductaque<br>diligentia, noua quaedam et prioribus exactiora adornasse Organa, quibus<br>Siderum ipsissima loca, multo scrupulosius quam antea restituantur. Quo sane<br>nomine, tam Tuae Celsitudinis Heroicae Virtuti et apud gratam posteritatem Famae nunquam<br>intermorituraeA, quam rerum Astronomicarum Incremento plurimum congratulor. Facit<br>autem in ijsdem Literis Tua Celsitud. mentionem<br>cuiusdam exigui et vndique rotundi Cometae, praecedenti Anno circa 8 Octobris<br>a Tuae Celsit. Mathematico CHRISTOPHORO<br>ROTHMANNO in vltima decuria ♓, et Latitudine Australi<br>animaduersi, quem T. Celsitudo ipsamet 19 Octobris in 21 ♈ cum Latitudine 4 part. adhuc Meridionali, depraehenditA; petitque Celsitud. Tua a Domino RANTZOVIO, vt significet num Cometa is in nostris oris<br>conspectus fuerit. Nominatim vero Tua Celsitud. a me cognoscere desiderat,<br>an et ego, quaque potissimum ratione eundem Cometam caelitus obseruârim. Etsi<br>vero Tuae Celsitudini scribere aliquoties in animumA induxerim, tum ob sublimem illam, nec vnquam satis laudatam, qua<br>diuinitus praeditus es in rebus Aethereis absolutius inquirendis (quo nomine<br>omnes tam superioris quam nostri aeui Viros Principes longe antecellis)<br>industriam et sedulitatem indefessam, tum etiam quod Celsitud. Tua ante<br>Annos ferme elapsos 11, me Casellis<br>sibiA praesentem clementer admodumque beneuole complexa fuerit, deque rebus<br>Astronomicis, quae nostra est voluptas, perlibenter<br>varieque mecum contulerit. Veruntamen cum vererer ne Celsitudini Tuae<br>intempestiuis meis literis molestiae quidpiam adferrem; praesertim cum tot<br>Annorum interuallo, et inter tanta Reipub. administrandae negotia Celsitudini<br>Tuae memoria mei ex animo facile excidisse poterat, factum est, vt diuturnum scribendi propositum in hunc vsque diem<br>prorogârimA. Cum vero Celsitud. Tua per dictas Literas D. RANTZOVIO transmissas, me salutare clementerA non dedignata fuerit, et ad sibi scribendum, sumta a recenter viso<br>Cometa occasione vltro inuitârit, animum audentius resumpsi, et plurimum<br>mihi congratulatus, hanc multorum Annorum desiderium explendi oportunitatem<br>quam commodissime offerri, minime vlterius differendum duxi, quin aliquid<br>Literarum ad Tuam Celsit. reuerenter mitteremA, fretus hac Cels. Tuae clementi inuitatione, quae meam audaciam excusabitA. De Cometa itaque cuius Obseruationem T. Celsitud. a me diligenterA desiderat, notum facio Cels. TuaeA, eum 18 Die Octobris proxime elapsi Anni, mihi Sidera more solito<br>contemplanti omnium primo apparuisse, et ab hoc ipso Die, vsque in medium<br>Nouembris, quamdiu videlicet conspici poterat, quotiescunque serenitas<br>oportuna concedebatur, Instrumentis exquisitis et nulli errori obnoxijs<br>diligentissime obseruatum fuisse. Quod vero integro decendio tardius nobis<br>quam Tuae Celsitud. Mathematico hic Cometes innotuerit, in causa fuêre<br>continuae Nubium obscuritates, mixtis creberrimis et tempestuosis<br>humiditatibus, quae nostrum Hemisphaerium inde fere ab initio Octobris ita<br>obduxerant, vt Coeli Siderumque aspectum toto illo tempore, vsque in<br>praedictum Diem, quo Cometa visus est, penitus intercluserint. Ex ijs vero<br>quae ab hoc tempore, quo nobis primum conspici caepit, per totam ipsius<br>durationem, quotiescunque serenitas optata<br>affulsit, varie et seduloA caelitus hîcA depraehensa sunt, principaliora quaedam peculiariA charta (ne hîc inserendo nimium Epistola excresceret) descripta, Tuae<br>Celsitudini per ocium cognoscenda mitto. Pleniorem vero et magis absolutam,<br>pluribusque Obseruationibus, et his innixis certis Demonstrationibus constabilitamA declarationem, non solum de hoc nuper viso Cometa, sed etiam de<br>tribus alijs proxime elapsis aliquot Annis conspectis, tum etiam de Noua<br>illa et admirabili prope Cassiopeae constellationem exorta Stella, Celsitudo<br>Tua vbi Opus nostrum de Nouis Aetherei Mundi PhaenomenisA hoc aeuo conspectis, in lucem prodierit, adipiscetur. Quamprimum enim<br>omnia in eo ad Colophonem deducta, et hîc in propria mea Typographia<br>(fauente Numine) excusa fuerint, Tuae Celsitudini inspicienda et dijudicanda<br>transmittam. Quia vero in his omnibus enucleatius ad Veritatis normam<br>tanquam Lydium lapidem probandis, aliorum etiam quotquot habere licuit<br>Obseruationes et placita, suo locoA proferre, et tum inter se, tum cum ipsis Apparentijs absque omni<br>praeiudicio et aliorum immodesta repraehensione, saltem vt Veritas ipsa eo<br>manifestius<br>elucesceretA, expendere decreui, Tuam Celsitudinem submisseA et obnixe oratam volo, vt suas tum de illaA ad Cassiopeiam Stella, tum de alijs subsequentibus Cometis<br>Obseruationes, quas habet exactasA, mecum communicare non dedignetur. Nec enim dubito, quin veluti Tua<br>Celsitud. in dicta noua Stella, prae multis alijs veritatis penetralia rectius ingressa sitA, (dumA<br>videlicet eamA insensibilem habuisse parallaxin, ideoque in supremo Aethere<br>constitisse asseritA, quam plurimis Astronomorum qui eam Elementarem statuerunt,<br>intolerabiliter aberrantibus) sic etiam in Cometarum Obseruationibus longe accuratiusA quam caeteri metam propositam attigeritA. Imprimis vero a Tua Celsitudine quibus possum precibus obtinere<br>desidero, vt ea etiam, quae in caeteris Mundo coaeuis sideribus; praesertim per<br>noua illa Organa, tam quo ad distantias, quam Declinationes siue Meridianas<br>Altitudines hactenus adinuenerit, mecum clementer impertiriA non grauetur. Ego vicissim tum earundem, tum etiam aliarum Stellarum<br>consimiles Obseruationes, prout nostris Instrumentis caelitûs depraehensae<br>sunt, Tuae Celsitud. per otium descriptas, remitti curabo, vt collatione<br>inuicem facta, de Instrumentorum et Obseruationum certitudine eo rectius<br>dijudicetur. Formam insuper et structurae rationem in Tuae Celsitudinis<br>nouitijs Instrumentis; praesertim quo ad Sextantem attinet, si Tua Celsitud.<br>mihi delineatam vna miserit, rem etiam fecerit longe acceptissimam. Nam<br>plurimum scire aueo, anne (veluti ex probabili coniectura assequor)<br>Celsitudinis Tuae noua haec Instrumenta plurimum similitudinis cum quibusdam<br>ex meis habeantA; idque praesertim quo ad subdiuisiones graduum per Puncta<br>transuersalia, et Pinnacidiorum per rimulas Parallelas, nocturnis Stellarum Obseruationibus apprime IdoneaA, peculiaremA confectionis modum attinet. Fuit enim hîc mecum ante quinquennium<br>PAVLVS ille WITTICHIVS, ex<br>cuius praescripto Tua Celsitudo sua Organa innouataA esse asserit, et Instrumentorum quorundam, quae partim tunc parata<br>habebam, partim in posterum elaborare decreui, elaborationemA et vsum, diuisionisque et Pinnacidiorum, quibusA in omnibus similiter ferme vtor, rationem explorauit, seque mox in<br>Germaniam, quaedam alia praetendens,<br>recepit. Ex ipsa vero Sextantis sola appellatione conijcere licet Witichiumnon multum a nostris aliena Tuae<br>Celsitudini proposuisse. Nam Sextantis Instrumentum, cuius et fabrica<br>adeoque ipsum nomen recens a me excogitatum erat, tuncA in vsu paratum habebam, cum Witichius mihi adesset. Verum cum ipsa experientia me<br>multoties commonefaceret eam Sextantis Fabricam, quam tunc adinueneram, ob<br>certa quaedam impedimenta Stellarum intercapedinibus praeciseA indagandis non sufficere, eius vsus iam dudum a nobis antiquatus est.<br>Quare si Sextans ille quo T. Celsitud. Siderum interualla obseruari curat,<br>eadem ratione confectus est, qua is quem Wittichius hic conspexit, vix admodumA ratas nullique fallaciae obnoxias eius beneficio<br>licebit obtinere distantias. Impossibile enim est ex vnico oculo, circa<br>Centrum InstrumentiA quiescente, binarum Stellarum, praesertim si remotiores inuicem<br>fuerint, diuersa simul praecise contueri loca. Nec enim Centrum visus Centro<br>Instrumenti apte vniri potest, aut per vllas Parallaxes haec differentia satisA conuenienter excusari; Nec pupilla Oculi in maioribus interstitijs<br>binas Stellas intuendo immota manet, vnde Centrum visus etiam aliquantulum variareA, necessarium euadit. Imo vero, quod in hoc subtili negotio magni<br>momenti est, cum non vtrinque quaelibet Stella circa Pinnacidium per rimulas<br>Parallelas aequabiliter et bifariam secta conspiciatur, vtrum accepta<br>distantia iusto maior minorue fuerit, nequaquam certo constabit. Excogitaui<br>itaque posteaA alium Sextantis conficiendi modum, eumque bifurcatum, ita vt per<br>binos Obseruatores quaelibet Stella seorsim conspiceretur, vtrinqueA per duoA diuersa Pinnacidia. Quam etiam constructionis rationem WittichioA dum hîc praesens esset, aperui, postque ipsius<br>discessum eiuscemodi Sextantem parari curaui, qui sane satis exactus erat,<br>et Stellarum interuallis citra omnem sensibilem errorem capiendis<br>sufficiebat. Verum cum sua tractatione paulo difficilior et laboriosior<br>esset, nec satis cito negotium expediret, hic etiam Sextans apud me in vsu<br>esse desijt. Quod si Tuae Celsitudinis Instrumentum ad eius similitudinem<br>confectum estA (nam Ideam huius Wittichio<br>hic monstraui, quae ipsi perplacuit) non dubito quin distantiae per eum<br>acceptae recte se habeant, licet paulo difficilius et tardius (vt dixi)<br>obtineantur. Vt itaque tum errorem omnem, tum etiam difficultatem in hoc<br>negotio dextre absoluendo euitarem, aliam et<br>diuersam a prioribus eamque expeditissimam, ante quadriennium inueni<br>Sextantis conficiendi rationem plane simplicem et naturalem, quo per binos<br>etiam Obseruatores in circumferentia Instrumenti motis Pinnacidijs, quae<br>rimulas habent Parallelas, distantiae Stellarum circa Cylindrum in Centro<br>quiescentem, vtrinque expeditissime citra omnem erroris suspicionem<br>discernuntur. Tales autem binos affabre elaboratos huic vsui destinatos<br>habeo, quorum quilibet 4 cubitos sua capacitate adaequat. Vt vero Celsitud.<br>Tua huius Sextantis compositionem facilius compraehendat, mitto tum aedificij<br>in Astronomiae honorem proximis Annis in hac Insula Huenna, non mediocri sumtu a me extructi, picturam, tum<br>etiam Sextantis (de quo loquor) aliquo modoA repraesentatam delineationem, vna cum designatione fulcri, cuius<br>beneficio in omnem Stellarum situm, ac inclinationem commode conuoluitur.<br>Addita est ab altera parte pictura Armillarum Aequatoriarum; Sic enim hoc<br>Instrumentum iam pridem a me excogitatum, eo quod Ascensiones Rectas et<br>Declinationes Siderum respectu Aequatoris metiatur, appellare libuit. Talium<br>autem Armillarum duo OrganaA tantae quantitatis, vt singula scrupula discriminatim exhibeant,<br>confici curaui. Sed et in his ipsis, quae Ptolemaicis<br>Armillis longe sunt expeditiores et minus erroribus obnoxiae (velut nos ipsa<br>docuit experientia, cum Ptolemaicas etiam Armillas e solido Metallo eiusdem<br>pene quantitatisA in promptu habeamus) ante biennium nouam quandam et multo<br>expeditiorem structuram excogitaui, quae saltem sesquialtero Circulo et Axe<br>rotundo, totum negotium mira facilitate et certitudine absoluit. Huius<br>Organi Machinam in Colle quodam extra aedes hasce Vraniae dicatas constitutam,<br>et ad vsus commoditatem gradibus subterraneis vndique successiue<br>ascendentibus, circumdatam, summa cumA diligentia construxi, cuius Diameter 10 pedes continet. Huic plurimum<br>tribuo, quo ad Siderum Ascensiones Rectas et Declinationes, in quouis eorum<br>apparente situ adamussim indagandas, cuius etiam adminiculo vtplurimum<br>Cometae apparentias, quas T. Celsitud. mitto, peruestigaui. Non autem<br>difficile esse exA datis Ascensionibus Rectis et Declinationibus respectu Aequatoris, eas<br>in Longitudines et Latitudines, quantum ad Eclipticam, redigere T. Celsit.<br>satis perspectum habet, siue id fiat per Triangulorum Sphaericorum rationes,<br>aut peculiarem aliquam Tabulam ex his deriuatam (qualem nos ad singulos<br>Ascensionis Rectae gradus, et DeclinationisA vtrinque ab Aequatore 32 grad. scrupuloseA supputatam huic negotio destinauimus) siue etiamA Mechanice per Globum magnum, qualem etiam 6 pedum in Diametro vndique<br>Orichalco aptissime obductum et omni sui parte rotundissimum, paratum habeoA. Sed nimis longum foret hoc loco omnia mea Instrumenta Astronomice eorundemque vsum<br>commemorareA: quae numero sedecim, structurae Magnitudine,<br>Formae varietate, et vsus multiplicis aptitudine, exacteA elaborata, praeclari Thesauri loco (nec enim mediocri diligentia,<br>labore et sumtu parata sunt) possideo; quorum pleraque e solido Metallo<br>existunt, quaedam vero, licet e certa et durabili lignea materia, vt pote<br>leuiori, necA nimio pondere ob quantitatis molem se ipsa inepte aggrauanteA, peculiari quadam ratione multipliciter componunturA, ita vt ne per se quidem mutationi facile subijciantur: nihilominus<br>tamen exterius Orichalcicis laminis, partim vt diuisionis subtilitati<br>accommoda euadant, partim vt eo firmius persistant, affabre muniuntur.<br>Magnitudinem vero distinctioni scrupulosae sufficientem habentA, adeo vt nonnulla senos et quinos, quaedam quaternos et ternos cubitos<br>adaequent. Quibus vsurpandis, tum etiam ijs, quae ex Obseruationibus<br>demonstrari possunt, e Triangulis in numeros reducendis, vtplurimum octonos,<br>interdum etiam plures Arti Astronomicae operam nauantes Studiosos, hic mecum<br>alo et instruo, nec vllius Serenitatis commoditatem siue Diurnam, siue<br>Nocturnam, in Solis, Lunae, reliquorumque qui conspici queunt Planetarum et<br>Affixarum etiam Stellarum Phaenomenis caelitus inquirendis, incassum abire<br>permittimus. De Instrumentorum vero singulorum Fabrica et Vsu, quia peculiarem LibrumA conscribere decreui, quiA Mechanicam Astronomiae partem complectetur (quo<br>etiam nomine nunc illa omnia delineari, et figuras eorum Aeneis aut BuxeisA laminis insculpi curo) Idcirco longiore de his commemoratione<br>supersedeo. Vbi autem laborem hunc (DEO successum largiente) absoluero, vel<br>saltem Figurae omnes excisae fuerint, Tuae Cels. id quicquid erit perspiciendum<br>expendendumque transmittam. Tum enim Celsit. T. solo intuitu omnia facilius<br>cognoscet, quam ex longa et taediosa de his relatione. Quod veroA haec ipsa Instrumenta, quibus in Sideribus obseruandis assidue vtor,<br>admodum exacta sint, et ne quidemA tenuiter sensibilem errorem admittant, vel ex hoc solo comprobatur,<br>quod eiusdem Sideris locus per plura et diuersimode fabricata Organa varia<br>ratione depraehensus, in idem prorsus scrupulum sedulo attendentibus recidat.<br>Vt autem Celsitudo Tua specimen aliquod eorum, quas ex meis Obseruationibus<br>hactenus parta sunt, habeatA, adiunxi vna Solaris cursus huius Anni ex propria restitutione<br>deriuatam Ephemeridem, eamque ad Longitudinem 30 part. cui Celsitudinis Tuae<br>ditio HASSIA congruit, directamA. Scrupula Secunda, prout Tabulae nostrae in motu Solis, Obseruationibus<br>certis Geometrice fundatae exhibent, annotaui, non quod ista subtilitas sit<br>vllo Instrumento in ipso Coelo comprehensibilisA, vt Tua Celsit. optime nouit, sed ne neglectis secundis de scrupulis<br>primis aliqua ambiguitas oriretur. Peto itaque a Tua Celsitudine obnixeA, vt hanc nostram in cursus Solaris numeratione instaurationem per sua<br>Instrumenta expeririA, mihique de hac suam Sententiam clementer significari curare velitA. Oportet autem in his rite pensitandis Declinationem Eclipticae<br>maximam praesupponere P. 23. Min. 31½. qualem nos peculiari quadam ratione, non ex altitudine Solis maxima<br>in vtroque Solstitio (haec enim praxis ob Refractionem radiorum, quae sit SoleA circa Brumam decliui, in nostro Horizonte deficit a vero plus<br>quaternis scrupulis) aliquoties pluribus Organis depraehendimus. Parallaxin quoqueA Solarem vbique Altitudinibus obseruatis addendam esseA, non dubium est, quin Celsitud. T. satis compertum habeat.<br>Refractiones insuper Radiorum Solarium, quae decliuioresA Altitudines maiores sensibus ingerunt, quam reuera existunt (idque ob<br>Diaphanum Aëris pauloA minus perspicuum Diaphano AetherisA, vt in Opticis haec demonstrantur) euitandae sunt; praesertim si<br>Altitudines obseruatae minores tertia aut ad summum dimidiaA Quadrantis parte fuerint. Nam vsque in hunc fere terminum<br>Refractionem aliquam perceptibilem fieri, peculiari quadam industria<br>expertus sum. Quo vero adhuc propiores Horizonti euadunt, eo etiam maiorem<br>Refractionem causantur, adeo vt Sol in ipso Ortu et OccasuA, etiam in Aëre purissimo, dimidio ad minimum gradu altior appareat,<br>quam rei veritas admittat. Sic etiam in Luna, caeterisque Planetis, et<br>affixis Sideribus per Refractionem cum decliuiores sunt, eorum Altitudines<br>et Declinationes veraque loca aliquantulum variantur. Quare in humilioribus<br>Altitudinibus, si scrupulose res tractanda sit, non<br>nimium erit Obseruationibus fidendum. Praeterea multum interest, quibus modis<br>Aequatoris ad Horizontem inclinatio in loco Obseruationis depraehensa sit. Nam<br>per Solis Altitudines Meridianas, licet in vtroque Solstitio, adhibita etiam<br>Parallaxi accipiantur, ob Refractionem (de qua dixi) in Brumali decliuitate<br>sese insinuantem, nequaquam negotium hoc ea qua par est praecisione<br>absoluitur. Consultissimum itaque fuerit Poli sublimitatem ex ipsa Polari<br>Stella, cum est in Altitudine maxima et minima, studiose perquirere. Ea enim<br>cum mediocris in magnitudine satisque perspicua sit, et paruum circa Polum<br>describat Circulum, nec ob id Horizonti tantum appropinquet, vt Refractio<br>aliqua veram Altitudinem immutet, in hac Pragmatia citra omnem errorem<br>absoluenda, quam commodissima est. Si itaque Tua Celsitudo, et huius Polaris<br>(inquam) Stellae Altitudinem maximam vel minimam, reliquis Obseruationibus<br>adiungi, meque de ea certiorem reddi curaueritA, fecerit rem mihi admodum exoptatam. Sic enim adhibita ipsius a Polo<br>distantia (quae hoc Anno ex nostris Obseruationibus est part. 2. mint. 55.<br>secund. 50) ad exactissimam Poli et Aequatoris, eius loci in quo Tua<br>Celsitudo Obseruationes suas fieri procurat Eleuationem, indubitanter<br>perueniemus, et de Declinationibus Solis, StellarumqueA certitudinem ratam, modo Instrumenta recte se habeant, constituemus.<br>Quapropter si Celsitud. Tua ea (de quibus iam dixi) omnia, videlicet<br>Parallaxin Solis, assignatam Declinationem maximam, et praedicta ratione inuentam Aequatoris Eleuationem in consilium<br>adhibuerit, Refractionisque quae fit infra tricesimum gradum Altitudinis<br>implicationem praecauerit, experietur vtiqueA Solaris cursus numerationem a nobis adinuentam Apparentijs Coelestibus exacteA consentire. Quam vero ante omnia necessarium fuerit Solis motum<br>scrupulose exploratum habere, si ea quae in Astrorum cognitione desiderantur<br>certius instauranda sint, Tua Celsitudo nequaquam ignorat: cum sine hoc, nec<br>Anni exacta quantitas, quae motuum mensura est, nec inerrantium quidem<br>Stellarum, multo minus erraticarum qui ex his patet, situs indagari queat.<br>Siquidem Solis reuolutioA omnium caeterarum reuolutionum Coelestium norma regularis existit. QuidA vero in Lunari etiam cursu non minori diligentia a nobis caelitus<br>peruestigato, inque caeterorum Planetarum motu examinando, et rectificandis<br>Affixarum Stellarum locis, hactenus non mediocri Labore et Diligentia<br>praestiterimus, Celsitud. T. suo<br>tempore, vbi omnia in ordinem redacta, et pluribus ObseruationibusA adaucta fuerint, cognoscet acA<br>dijudicabitA. In primis enim T. Cels. iudicioA hosce meos labores subiectos velim, eo quod sciam Celsitud. Tuam in<br>hac Scientia eximium Artificem, solide de his rebus iudicare<br>posseA. Sed video me dum auidius cum Tua Cels. de Astronomicis negotijs<br>colloquor, Epistolae leges nimia prolixitate transcendere, et diutius, quam<br>par est, Celsitudinem Tuam hisce legendis occupatam detinere. Itaque calamum inhibeboA, Tuamque CelsitudinemA DEO tam Coelestium, quam Terrestrium Creaturarum Authori commendaboA. Is Tuam Cels. et in suae Ecclesiae, cuius curam, quod nunc rarissimum est, agisA, et Reipub. Tutelam, ac bonarum Artium, inprimisque Diuinae illius<br>Astronomiae propagationemA, quam diutissime incolumem et florentem conseruet. Valeat T. Cels. et<br>si quae mihi ad haec respondere dignetur, exhibitori harum committat, cui<br>nomen est PETRVS IACOBI, qui mihi per<br>aliquot Annos domesticus fuit, et tum in Astronomicis, tum PyronomicisA exercitijs fidelem nauauit operam. Ideôque non per alios mearum rerum<br>ignaros, sed potius huncA mihi charum et fidelem, qui plura etiamA, quam calamo libet compraehendereA, vbi opus fuerit,<br>referre possetA, negotium perferendi ad T. Cels. has Literas expedireA, certius et conuenientiusA esse duxi. Huic itaque T. Celsitud. fidem in omnibus adhibeat, et quae<br>ad me referenda fuerint tuto concredat. Iterum Tua Celsitudo quam<br>felicissime et diutissime valeat.Datae Vraniburgi in Insula<br>Porthmi Danici Huenna, Calendis Martij, Anno<br>1586.T. C. addictiss.TYCHO<br>Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda; the most clement Prince and Lord. [He sends] greeting.</b></p><p>When recently I was at the court of our Most Serene King, Most Illustrious Prince, the noble youth Gerhard Rantzau — governor of the castle of Kronborg, which the same renowned King has now for the first time caused to be magnificently built up on the nearby shore of Zealand — delivered to me certain letters sent me by his most noble and most learned father, a man most devoted to astronomical matters, Master Heinrich Rantzau, Royal Vicegerent in Holstein. On opening them I found among them a copy of a letter of Your Highness written to Master Heinrich Rantzau himself, which, received most eagerly, I read over again and again with great pleasure. For it wonderfully refreshed me to perceive from it, not obscurely, that Your Highness has not, even at this riper age, amid such various occupations and cares of governing the commonwealth, laid down from the heart the affection and zeal long since begun toward the deeper investigation of that divine Science of the Stars; but rather, with renewed strength and applied diligence, has furnished certain new instruments, more exact than the earlier ones, by which the very places of the stars may be restored far more scrupulously than before. On which account indeed I most heartily congratulate both Your Highness's heroic virtue and your fame, never to die among grateful posterity, and likewise the increase of astronomical knowledge.</p><p>Now in the same letter Your Highness makes mention of a certain small and altogether round comet, observed in the preceding year about 8 October by Your Highness's mathematician Christoph Rothmann in the last decan of Pisces [♓] and in southern latitude, which Your Highness yourself detected on 19 October in 21° of Aries [♈] with a latitude still 4 degrees southerly; and Your Highness asks of Master Rantzau that he signify whether that comet was seen in our regions. But by name Your Highness desires to learn from me whether I too, and by what method especially, observed the same comet in the heavens. And although I had several times resolved in my mind to write to Your Highness — both on account of that lofty, never sufficiently praised, divinely-bestowed industry and untiring assiduity with which you inquire so consummately into matters of the aether (on which account you far surpass all princely men both of the earlier age and of our own), and also because Your Highness, nearly eleven years ago now, embraced me most clemently and benevolently when I was present at Kassel, and most willingly and variously conferred with me about astronomical matters, which are our delight — yet, since I feared lest I should bring some annoyance to Your Highness by my untimely letters (especially since, over the interval of so many years and amid such great affairs of governing the commonwealth, the memory of me might easily have slipped from Your Highness's heart), it came about that I have deferred my long-standing purpose of writing up to this very day. But since Your Highness, by the said letter transmitted to Master Rantzau, has not disdained clemently to greet me, and of your own accord, taking occasion from the recently seen comet, has invited me to write to you, I took up courage more boldly, and, congratulating myself greatly that this opportunity of fulfilling a desire of many years was being offered me so very conveniently, I judged it must by no means be deferred any longer, but that I should reverently send some letter to Your Highness — relying on this clement invitation of Your Highness, which will excuse my boldness.</p><p>Concerning the comet, then, the observation of which Your Highness diligently desires of me, I make known to Your Highness that it first of all appeared to me on the 18th day of October of the year just past, as I was contemplating the stars in my usual manner; and that from this very day, up to the middle of November — that is, for as long as it could be seen — whenever convenient clear weather was granted, it was most diligently observed with exquisite instruments liable to no error. But that this comet became known to us a whole ten days later than to Your Highness's mathematician — the cause of that was the continual obscurities of clouds, mixed with very frequent and tempestuous damps, which had so overcast our hemisphere almost from the beginning of October that they entirely shut off the sight of the heaven and the stars throughout all that time, up to the aforesaid day on which the comet was seen. Of those things, however, which from this time, when it first began to be seen by us, throughout its whole duration, whenever the longed-for clear weather shone forth, were variously and diligently detected here in the heavens, I send certain of the more principal ones, written on a separate sheet (lest, by inserting them here, the letter should grow too much), for Your Highness to peruse at leisure. But a fuller and more complete declaration, established by more observations and by sure demonstrations resting on these — not only about this lately-seen comet, but also about three others seen in some of the years just past, and likewise about that new and admirable star that arose near the constellation of Cassiopeia — Your Highness will obtain when our Work <i>On the New Phenomena of the Aethereal World</i> seen in this age comes into the light. For as soon as everything in it has been brought to its colophon and printed here in my own printing-house (the Deity favouring), I shall transmit it to Your Highness to inspect and judge.</p><p>But because, in proving all these things more clearly against the standard of Truth as against a touchstone [a Lydian stone], I have resolved to bring forward also the observations and opinions of others, as many as I have been able to have, and to weigh them both among themselves and against the appearances themselves — without any prejudice and without immodest censure of others, only that Truth itself might thereby shine forth the more manifestly — I wish Your Highness humbly and earnestly entreated not to disdain to communicate with me your observations both of that star near Cassiopeia and of the other subsequent comets, which you have made exact. For I do not doubt that, just as Your Highness, in the said new star, entered the inner sanctuaries of truth more rightly than many others (in that, namely, you assert it to have had no perceptible <i>parallax</i>, and therefore to have stood in the supreme aether — while very many of the astronomers who set it among the elementary [sublunary] things erred intolerably), so also in the observations of comets you have attained the appointed goal far more accurately than the rest. But above all I desire to obtain from Your Highness, with what entreaties I can, that you not be loath clemently to share with me also those things which, in the other stars coeval with the World, you have hitherto discovered — especially by those new instruments — both as to distances and as to declinations or meridian altitudes. I in turn shall see to it that like observations both of the same and of other stars, as they have been detected by our instruments in the heavens, are written out and sent back to Your Highness, so that, a comparison being made mutually, the certainty of the instruments and observations may be the better judged. Moreover, you would do me a thing far most acceptable if Your Highness should send me, drawn out, the form and structural plan of your new instruments — especially as regards the <i>sextant</i>. For I greatly long to know whether (as I gather from probable conjecture) these new instruments of Your Highness have much resemblance to certain of mine; and that especially as concerns the subdivisions of the degrees by transversal <i>points</i>, and a peculiar manner of construction of the <i>pinnules</i> by parallel slits, exceedingly suited to nocturnal observations of stars.</p><p>For that famous Paul Wittich was here with me five years ago — from whose prescription Your Highness asserts your instruments were renewed — and he explored the construction and use of certain instruments which I partly then had ready, partly resolved to elaborate in future, and the plan of the division and of the pinnules, which in all of these I use in nearly the same way; and he soon betook himself into Germany, alleging certain other matters. And from the sole name of \"sextant\" one may conjecture that Wittich proposed to Your Highness things not much different from ours. For the instrument of the sextant — of which both the fabric and indeed the name itself had recently been devised by me — I then had ready in use when Wittich was with me. But since experience itself many times admonished me that that fabric of the sextant which I had then devised did not suffice for precisely investigating the intervals of the stars, on account of certain impediments, its use was long ago abolished by us. Wherefore, if that sextant by which Your Highness has the intervals of the stars observed is constructed on the same plan as the one which Wittich saw here, it will scarcely be possible by its means to obtain distances that are fixed and liable to no fallacy. For it is impossible, from a single eye resting at the centre of the instrument, to behold precisely at the same time the differing places of two stars, especially if they are rather remote from one another. For the centre of vision cannot fittingly be united with the centre of the instrument, nor can this difference be excused sufficiently conveniently by any <i>parallaxes</i>; nor does the pupil of the eye, in viewing two stars at greater intervals, remain unmoved, whence it becomes necessary that the centre of vision too vary somewhat. Nay rather — and this is of great moment in this subtle business — since each star is not seen on both sides equally and bisected at the pinnule by the parallel slits, it will by no means be certainly established whether the distance taken is greater or less than the just one.</p><p>I therefore afterward devised another way of constructing the sextant, and that a bifurcated one, so that by two observers each star might be seen separately, on both sides through two different pinnules. This plan of construction too I disclosed to Wittich while he was present here; and after his departure I had a sextant of this kind prepared, which was indeed exact enough and sufficed for taking the intervals of the stars without any sensible error. But since in its handling it was somewhat more difficult and laborious, and did not despatch the business quickly enough, this sextant too ceased to be in use with me. And if Your Highness's instrument is constructed in its likeness (for I showed Wittich the idea of this here, and it greatly pleased him), I do not doubt that the distances taken by it are correct, although obtained a little more difficultly and slowly (as I said). Therefore, in order to avoid both all error and also the difficulty in despatching this business dexterously, four years ago I found another plan of constructing the sextant, different from the earlier ones and most expeditious, plainly simple and natural, by which, also by two observers, with the pinnules (which have parallel slits) moved on the circumference of the instrument, the distances of the stars are discerned most expeditiously on both sides, about a cylinder resting at the centre, without any suspicion of error. And I have two such, skilfully wrought and destined for this use, each of which equals four cubits in its span.</p><p>But that Your Highness may the more easily comprehend the composition of this sextant, I send both a picture of the building erected by me in recent years on this island of Hven, at no slight cost, in honour of Astronomy, and also a drawing in some fashion representing the sextant (of which I speak), together with a delineation of its support [fulcrum], by whose benefit it is conveniently turned about to every position and inclination of the stars. There is added on the other side a picture of the Equatorial Armillae; for it has pleased me thus to call this instrument, long since devised by me, because it measures the right ascensions and declinations of the stars with respect to the equator. And I have had two such armillae made, of so great a size that they exhibit single minutes distinctly. But even in these very ones — which are far more expeditious than the Ptolemaic armillae and less liable to errors (as experience itself has taught us, since we have Ptolemaic armillae too, of solid metal, of nearly the same size, ready to hand) — two years ago I devised a certain new and much more expeditious structure, which, with but one and a half circles and a round axle, despatches the whole business with marvellous ease and certainty. The machine of this instrument I constructed with the utmost diligence, set on a certain hill outside these houses dedicated to Urania, and surrounded, for convenience of use, by subterranean steps ascending successively on all sides; its diameter contains ten feet. To this I attribute very much, for investigating exactly the right ascensions and declinations of the stars in whatever apparent position of them; by whose aid also I have for the most part investigated the appearances of the comet which I send Your Highness.</p><p>But that it is not difficult, from given right ascensions and declinations with respect to the equator, to reduce them into longitudes and latitudes, as far as concerns the ecliptic, Your Highness has sufficiently perceived — whether it be done by the methods of spherical triangles, or by some special table derived from these (such as we have, for each degree of right ascension and of declination, scrupulously computed to 32 degrees on each side of the equator, destined for this business), or also mechanically by a great globe, such as I have ready, six feet in diameter, most aptly covered on all sides with brass and most perfectly round in every part. But it would be too long in this place to recount all my astronomical instruments and their use: which, sixteen in number, exactly elaborated in magnitude of structure, variety of form, and aptness of manifold use, I possess in place of a splendid treasure (for they have been prepared with no slight diligence, labour, and cost); of which most are of solid metal, but some, although of sure and durable wooden material — being lighter, and not, by excessive weight on account of the mass of their size, unfittingly burdening themselves — are composed in manifold fashion by a certain peculiar method, so that they are not even of themselves easily subject to change; nevertheless they are skilfully fortified on the outside with brass laminae, partly that they may turn out suited to the fineness of the division, partly that they may stand the more firmly. And they have a magnitude sufficient for scrupulous distinction, so that some equal six and five cubits, some four and three. For the using of which, and also for reducing into numbers, from triangles, those things which can be demonstrated from observations, I maintain and instruct here with me for the most part eight, sometimes even more, students devoting their effort to the Art of Astronomy; nor do we suffer the convenience of any clear weather, whether by day or by night, to pass in vain, in inquiring into the heavenly phenomena of the Sun, the Moon, the rest of the planets that can be seen, and the fixed stars also.</p><p>But concerning the fabric and use of each single instrument, because I have resolved to write a special Book which shall embrace the Mechanical part of Astronomy (on which account also I am now having them all delineated, and their figures engraved on plates of copper or boxwood), I therefore forbear a longer recounting of these things. But when I shall have completed this labour (God granting success), or at least when all the figures shall have been cut, I shall transmit to Your Highness whatever it will be, to be perused and weighed. For then Your Highness will, by a single glance, more easily learn everything than from a long and tedious account of these things. But that these very instruments, which I assiduously use in observing the stars, are very exact, and do not admit even a faintly sensible error — that is proved from this alone, that the place of the same star, detected by several and diversely-fabricated instruments by various methods, falls, for those attending diligently, to exactly the same minute.</p><p>But that Your Highness may have some specimen of those things which have hitherto been gained from my observations, I have adjoined an Ephemeris of the Sun's course for this year, derived from my own restitution, and directed to the longitude of 30 degrees, to which Your Highness's dominion of Hesse corresponds. I have noted the seconds, as our Tables in the motion of the Sun (founded geometrically on sure observations) exhibit them — not because that fineness is comprehensible by any instrument in the heaven itself, as Your Highness very well knows, but lest, the seconds being neglected, some ambiguity should arise concerning the minutes. I therefore earnestly ask of Your Highness that you be willing to have this restoration of ours in the numeration of the Sun's course tested by your instruments, and your opinion of it clemently signified to me. But in rightly weighing these things one must presuppose the maximum declination of the ecliptic [the obliquity] to be 23 degrees, 31½ minutes — which we have several times detected with several instruments by a certain peculiar method, <i>not</i> from the maximum altitude of the Sun at either solstice (for this practice, on account of the refraction of the rays, which occurs when the Sun is low near the winter solstice, falls short of the true by more than four minutes in our horizon). That the solar <i>parallax</i> too is everywhere to be added to the observed altitudes, there is no doubt that Your Highness has sufficiently ascertained. The refractions of the solar rays besides — which, when [the Sun is] low, force upon the senses altitudes greater than they really are (and that on account of the diaphanous medium of the Air being a little less transparent than the diaphanous medium of the Aether, as these things are demonstrated in Optics) — are to be avoided, especially if the observed altitudes be less than a third, or at most a half, of the quadrant. For I have, by a certain peculiar industry, found by experience that some perceptible refraction occurs up to nearly this limit. But the closer [the bodies] come to the horizon, the greater refraction they cause, so that the Sun, at its very rising and setting, even in the purest air, appears at least half a degree higher than the truth of the matter allows. So too in the Moon, and the other planets, and the fixed stars, when they are low, their altitudes and declinations and true places are somewhat varied by refraction. Wherefore, at lower altitudes, if the matter is to be handled scrupulously, the observations are not to be trusted too much.</p><p>Besides, it makes much difference by what methods the inclination of the equator to the horizon at the place of observation has been detected. For by the meridian altitudes of the Sun — even if they be taken at either solstice, with the parallax also applied — on account of the refraction (of which I have spoken) insinuating itself in the winter-solstitial lowness, this business is by no means despatched with the precision that is fitting. It will therefore be most advisable to seek out studiously the height of the pole from the Pole Star itself, when it is at its maximum and minimum altitude. For since it is of moderate magnitude and sufficiently perspicuous, and describes a small circle about the pole, and on that account does not approach the horizon so much that any refraction alters its true altitude, it is most convenient for despatching this practical operation without any error. If therefore Your Highness should have the maximum or minimum altitude of this Pole Star (I say) adjoined to the rest of your observations, and should make me more certain of it, you will have done a thing much to be wished by me. For thus, applying its distance from the pole (which this year, from our observations, is 2 degrees, 55 minutes, 50 seconds), we shall arrive indubitably at the most exact elevation of the pole and equator of that place in which Your Highness has your observations made, and shall establish a fixed certainty concerning the declinations of the Sun and of the stars, provided the instruments are in good order. Wherefore, if Your Highness shall take into counsel all those things (of which I have already spoken) — namely the parallax of the Sun, the assigned maximum declination, and the elevation of the equator found by the aforesaid method — and shall guard against the complication of the refraction which occurs below the thirtieth degree of altitude, you will assuredly find by experience that the numeration of the Sun's course discovered by us agrees exactly with the celestial appearances.</p><p>But how necessary it is, before all else, to have the Sun's motion scrupulously explored, if those things which are sought in the knowledge of the stars are to be restored more surely, Your Highness by no means is ignorant: since without this neither the exact quantity of the year, which is the measure of the motions, nor even the positions of the fixed stars, much less those of the wandering ones (which is evident from these), can be investigated. Inasmuch as the revolution of the Sun stands as the regular norm of all the other celestial revolutions. But what we have hitherto accomplished, with no slight labour and diligence, in the lunar course also (investigated by us in the heavens with no less diligence), and in examining the motion of the rest of the planets, and in rectifying the places of the fixed stars — Your Highness will learn and judge in due time, when everything has been reduced to order and augmented with more observations. For above all I would wish these labours of mine subjected to Your Highness's judgement, because I know Your Highness to be an outstanding artist in this Science, able to judge solidly of these matters.</p><p>But I see that, while I converse rather greedily with Your Highness about astronomical affairs, I am overstepping the laws of a letter by excessive prolixity, and detaining Your Highness, occupied in reading these things, longer than is fitting. And so I shall check my pen, and commend Your Highness to God, the Author of celestial as of terrestrial creatures. May He preserve Your Highness, as long as possible, safe and flourishing, both for the protection of his Church — whose care you take, which is now most rare — and of the commonwealth, and for the propagation of the good Arts, and especially of that divine Astronomy. May Your Highness fare well; and if you should deign to answer me anything to these things, entrust it to the bearer of this letter, whose name is Peter Jacobsen [Petrus Jacobi], who was my domestic for some years, and rendered faithful service both in astronomical and in pyronomic [chemical] exercises. And therefore I have judged it surer and more fitting to despatch the business of carrying this letter to Your Highness not through others ignorant of my affairs, but rather through this man, dear and faithful to me, who, where there shall be need, could also relate more than it pleases [me] to comprehend with the pen. To him therefore let Your Highness lend credence in all things, and safely entrust whatever is to be reported back to me. Again may Your Highness fare most happily and most long.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, on the island of Hven in the Danish Sound, on the Kalends of March, in the year 1586. Most devoted to Your Highness, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV \"the Wise,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), founder of the Kassel observatory and Tycho's chief astronomical peer-patron; his court mathematician <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (here named) handled the technical exchange. This is the <b>opening letter of Tycho's printed <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (Liber I, Uraniborg 1596)</b> and one of the most important documents of his instrument-making programme: it sets out the Hven establishment, the sextant's evolution, the Wittich episode, and Tycho's solar and refraction theory.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> The exchange was rekindled by a letter Wilhelm IV had written to <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the great Holstein humanist and Danish <i>Statholder</i> (royal vicegerent), a copy of which reached Tycho through Rantzau's son <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (1558–1627), then governor (<i>Praefectus</i>) of the newly rebuilt <b>Kronborg</b> castle at Elsinore. Tycho recalls being received by the Landgrave at Kassel \"nearly eleven years ago\" — i.e. on his 1575 tour.</li><li><b>The comet of 1585.</b> Observed by Rothmann from ~8 October 1585 in the last decan (last 10°) of <b>Pisces (♓)</b>, southern latitude; by the Landgrave himself on 19 October in <b>21° Aries (♈)</b>, 4° south latitude. Tycho first saw it 18 October (ten days late, owing to October cloud) and observed it to mid-November. The full treatment is promised in his <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the \"Work <i>On the New Phenomena of the Aethereal World</i>\"), then in press at Uraniborg, which would also treat the <b>1572 new star in Cassiopeia</b> and three further comets.</li><li><b>Paul Wittich.</b> <b>Paul Wittich</b> (Wittichius, c. 1546–1586) of Breslau, the mathematician whose visit to Hven (here \"five years ago,\" i.e. 1580) and subsequent journey to Kassel transmitted Tycho's instrument designs to the Landgrave — the point Tycho is delicately reclaiming here (\"from whose prescription Your Highness asserts your instruments were renewed\"). The whole sextant discussion is Tycho asserting his own priority for the transversal-point divisions, the parallel-slit pinnules, and the <i>name and fabric</i> of the sextant. (See Thoren, <i>The Lord of Uraniborg</i>, and the Wittich/priority discussion in the corpus.)</li><li><b>The instruments.</b> The letter is the earliest connected description of the Uraniborg instrumentarium later figured in the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598): the three successive <b>sextant</b> designs (single-observer → bifurcated two-observer → the final two-observer cylinder sextant, \"four cubits\"); the <b>equatorial armillae</b> (<i>armillae aequatoriae</i> — Tycho's own coinage for the instrument measuring right ascension and declination); the simplified armillary of \"one and a half circles and a round axle,\" ten feet in diameter, set on a mound with subterranean steps (the great equatorial armillary in the southern observatory crypt); and the six-foot brass-covered <b>globe</b>. The \"sixteen instruments … a precious treasure\" and \"eight or more students\" describe the working establishment c. 1586. The promised \"special Book … the Mechanical part of Astronomy\" is the <i>Mechanica</i> itself.</li><li><b>Technical glossary (per §5).</b> <i>Ascensiones Rectas</i> → right ascensions; <i>Declinationes</i> → declinations; <i>Longitudines et Latitudines (ad Eclipticam)</i> → (ecliptic) longitudes and latitudes; <i>Parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>Refractio</i> → refraction; <i>Armillae aequatoriae</i> → equatorial armillae; <i>Sextans</i> → sextant; <i>Pinnacidia</i> → pinnules (sights); <i>organa/Instrumenta</i> → instruments; <i>restitutio / instauratio</i> → restitution / restoration; <i>Apparentiae</i> → appearances. The <b>obliquity of the ecliptic</b> (\"maximum declination of the ecliptic\") Tycho gives as <b>23° 31½′</b>, and the <b>Pole Star's distance from the pole</b> as <b>2° 55′ 50″</b> for 1586 — both reproduced as written. <i>Pyronomicis exercitijs</i> = \"pyronomic exercises,\" i.e. chemical/alchemical furnace-work (Paracelsian <i>pyronomia</i>); the bearer <b>Petrus Jacobi = Peter Jacobsen Flemløse</b>, Tycho's chief assistant, served in both astronomy and alchemy.</li><li><b>Latin idiom.</b> <i>ad Veritatis normam tanquam Lydium lapidem</i> — \"to the standard of Truth as to a Lydian stone [touchstone],\" the proverbial assayer's touchstone. <i>calamum inhibebo</i> — \"I shall check my pen\" (apology for prolixity). <i>Sacris Uraniae</i> register: the building \"in honour of Astronomy\" is Uraniborg, the \"houses dedicated to Urania.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Calendis Martij, Anno 1586</i> = <b>1 March 1586</b>, Old Style (Lutheran Denmark kept the Julian calendar); rendered with the Roman idiom glossed. Header date agrees with the dataset label.</li><li><b>Text note.</b> The scattered superscript \"A\" markers (<i>curasA, depraehenditA, animumA, sibiA, prorogârimA</i>, etc.) are editorial/footnote sigla in the working transcription, not part of the Latin; they are left in the Source block as printed (per §4.2) and read through in translation. One word-join in the witness, \"<i>Witichiumnon</i>\" (= <i>Witichium … non</i>), is likewise a transcription artefact.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely and the technical, biographical, and instrumental content is fully corpus-corroborated (this letter is well known from the <i>Epistolae</i> and discussed in Thoren and Christianson); the numerical readings (obliquity 23°31½′; Pole Star distance 2°55′50″; longitude 30° for Hesse; the comet positions in ♓/♈) are reproduced as written. Grade [B] rather than [A] only because of the heavy apparatus-siglum interference in the working transcription (which has been read through, not against the Dreyer scan line-by-line) and one or two residual rhetorical clauses where the prolix periodic syntax admits minor alternative parsings; no substantive crux affects the sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo.slice_pdf is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible (per brief, grade capped at B). Audited against source_text alone: full sentence-by-sentence collation of the Latin against the published English, checking every proper name, date, number, and technical claim -- Gerhard/Heinrich Rantzau and their titles, Kronborg, the recipient's four county titles, Rothmann's comet sighting ~8 Oct in the last decan of Pisces with southern latitude, Wilhelm's own sighting 19 Oct at 21 deg Aries / 4 deg S, Tycho's first sighting 18 Oct (consistent with the stated 10-day lag behind Rothmann), the Kassel visit '~11 years ago', the Wittich visit '5 years ago', the two later sextant redesigns '4 years ago' and '2 years ago', the 16 instruments, the 6-foot globe, the 10-foot armillary, the obliquity 23 deg 31.5', the pole-star distance 2 deg 55' 50\", the refraction figures (&gt;4', &gt;=0.5 deg, below 1/3-1/2 quadrant, below 30 deg), the 8 resident students, and the closing date/place/signature (Uraniborg, Hven, Kalends of March 1586, bearer Petrus Iacobi). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; the nova-parallax sentence correctly keeps 'Tua Celsitudo' (Wilhelm) as the subject praised, not flipped onto Tycho. Clean but uncollated against Dreyer.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15860414001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15860414001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Responds to a copy of Wilhelm's letter to Heinrich Rantzau, expressing delight that the Landgrave continues to pursue astronomical studies at a mature age.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860301001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860315001", "d": "1586-03-15", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 15 March 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NOBILISSIME, charissimeque D. TYCHO, si vivis et vales, gaudeo. Superioribus enim mensibus spargebatur fama mihi acerba, quam avertant Superi, te nostri amantissimum e vivis excessisse. Cum tamen dubius essem, emi tibi Gnomonicen CLAVIJ Bambergensis, et MAGINI Tabulas novas et Ephemeridas ad Calendarij emendationem a GREGORIO XIII Pontifice Maximo institutam, compositas. Aestimat autem Bambergensis Gnomonicen Ioachimicis&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; 7, Ephemeridas vero, in quibus in motuum supputatione errores a STADIO commissos adiunxit, unâ cum Tabulis aestimat Ioachimicis XI vel XII, ut&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; tota summa Ioachimicorum sit 18. Emi autem hac conditione, ut si tibi forte non placerent, aut forte eosdem iam esses nactus, Bibliopola ad se recipiat. Quid igitur hac in parte fieri velis, quam primum significes cupio. Ego, quod mare esset clausum, citius procurare non potui. Nam ad Paschatis festum aut libri sunt restituendi aut pecunia numeranda. Iam enim libros ineunte hyeme apud me deposuit. Tu ut valeas et quid agas, fac intelligam, an aliquid sub praelo sit, et quid premas. Tuos Cometas expecto, post tot aliorum labores aliquid praestanti tuo ingenio dignum, quod miretur posteritas. Calendarij enim editioni&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; cum indicijs mutationum aëris non credo, quod aliquem ex tuis ridendum populo velis cum alijs praebere. Quod enim CATO de Aruspicibus, id ego de hisce praedictionibus cogitare soleo. Mirari se aiebat, si cum sibi mutuo occurrunt, non rideant. Nunc venio ad postremas tuas literas, in quibus (quantum memini) tu decoctiones, infusiones, et nescio quae alia in Galenicis rides, nec quicquam videris probare, nisi quod summo labore extractum ex metallis aut quintas essentias aut tincturas aut spiritus sapiat. Patere nos obvijs uti paratuque facilibus; feras etiam in amico non moleste, plures ab eo restitui decoctis, infusis, et sola ptisana, quam maxima eorum turba, quise solos sapere arbitrantur, quintis essentijs, tincturis, spiritibus, alijsque occultis, ut ipsi iudicant, naturis, quae tamen omnes a primis qualitatibus dependent. Decoctione certe facultates rerum coctibilium in liquorem transferri, nullus negaverit. At ex auro et lapidibus pretiosis, corallis nihil decoctione deciditur? Fateor id; nos haec ijs relinquimus, quibus licet ex unico vitrioli spiritu multas tincturas parare, addito colore alterius rei mixtione. Scis quem notem. Neque haec a me referuntur, ut si quid a posteris bene ad humani generis salutem sit inventum, id damnem; absit, immo laudem magis et sequar alijsque communicem. Insanientis enim hominis fuerit, ac animi plane maligni, calumniari, quae bene sunt dicta, et aliorum inventa deprimere. Quid igitur, inquies, in hoc genere Chymico non laboras, et hoc quicquid est cognitioni humorali elementari primarum qualitatum, alijsque verbosis GALENI nugis non adiungis? Sic enim ex sulphure, sale et hydrargyro compositum genus hominum eructare solet. Dicam quod verum est; magistris destituor, mutos non intelligo. Tu itaque, qui iam aliquot annis in hoc genere laboras et sedulo versatus es, si quid habes, quod in gravissimis morbis Apoplexia, Epilepsia, Paralysi contractis Melancholia, divina manu succurrat, communica, et componendi rationem utendique modum et mensuram demonstra. Memini de te audivisse, Epilepticos non paucos a te curatos, verum an id per spiritum vitrioli facias ignoro. Si sic, aperi quaeso quomodo pares, quae admisceas, quomodo exhibeas, quoties et quo tempore. Age noli invidere amico, quae habes, qui tui semper fuit et erit studiosissimus amantissimusque. Bene vale integerrimeque amantissimeque mi TYCHO, et me, quod facis, mutuo ama. Rostochij XV Martij An. Domini 1586.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most noble and dearest Master Tycho, if you are alive and well, I rejoice. For in the months past a report bitter to me was being spread abroad — which may the Powers above avert — that you, most loving of us, had departed from among the living. Yet, since I was in doubt, I bought for you the <i>Gnomonice</i> of Clavius of Bamberg, and Magini's new <i>Tables</i> and <i>Ephemerides</i>, composed [to accord] with the reform of the calendar instituted by Pope Gregory XIII. Now the man of Bamberg values the <i>Gnomonice</i> at 7 <i>Joachimsthalers</i>&lt;sup&gt;[A]&lt;/sup&gt;; the <i>Ephemerides</i>, however — in which he has appended the errors committed by Stadius in the computation of the motions — together with the <i>Tables</i> he values at 11 or 12 <i>Joachimsthalers</i>, so that&lt;sup&gt;[A]&lt;/sup&gt; the whole sum comes to 18 <i>Joachimsthalers</i>. But I bought them on this condition: that if perhaps they should not please you, or perhaps you should already have got hold of the same, the bookseller take them back to himself. What, then, you would have done in this matter, I desire that you signify as soon as possible. I, because the sea was closed, could not procure them sooner; for at the feast of Easter either the books are to be returned or the money to be paid out — since he deposited the books with me at the beginning of winter. Make me understand how you are and what you are doing — whether anything is under the press, and what you are printing. I await your <i>Comets</i>: after the labours of so many others, something worthy of your outstanding genius, that posterity may marvel at. For as to an edition of a calendar&lt;sup&gt;[A]&lt;/sup&gt; with forecasts of changes of the weather, I do not believe that you would wish to set forth any of your [men] to be laughed at by the people along with the others. For what Cato [said] of the soothsayers, that I am wont to think of these predictions: he used to say he wondered that, when they meet one another, they do not laugh. Now I come to your last letter, in which (as far as I recall) you laugh at decoctions, infusions, and I know not what else among the Galenicals, and seem to approve of nothing unless it taste of what is extracted with the utmost labour from metals — either quintessences or tinctures or spirits. Allow us to use the things that come to hand and are easily prepared; and do not take it amiss in a friend, [to hear] that more people are restored by him with decoctions, infusions, and barley-water alone than by the very great crowd of those who reckon that they alone are wise, with quintessences, tinctures, spirits, and other occult natures (as they themselves judge them) — which all nonetheless depend upon the first qualities. That by decoction the powers of decoctible things are transferred into a liquid, no one would deny. But from gold and precious stones, from corals, is nothing brought down by decoction? I grant it; we leave these things to those who are permitted, out of a single spirit of vitriol, to prepare many tinctures, the colour being added by the mixture of some other thing. You know whom I mark. Nor are these things recounted by me so that, if anything has been well discovered by later men for the welfare of the human race, I should condemn it — far from it; rather I would the more praise and follow it and share it with others. For it would be the part of a man out of his senses, and of a downright malicious mind, to slander things that are well said, and to disparage the inventions of others. What then, you will say, do you not labour in this Chymical kind, and not join this — whatever it is — to the humoral, elemental knowledge of the first qualities, and to Galen's other wordy trifles? For thus the breed of men compounded out of sulphur, salt, and quicksilver is wont to belch forth. I shall say what is true: I am bereft of masters; the dumb I do not understand. You, therefore, who now for some years labour in this kind and are diligently versed in it — if you have anything that, in the gravest diseases, in apoplexy, epilepsy, paralysis of contracted [limbs], melancholy, may by a divine hand bring relief, share it, and show the manner of compounding and the mode and measure of using it. I remember having heard of you that not a few epileptics were cured by you; but whether you do it by spirit of vitriol I do not know. If so, open up, I pray, how you prepare it, what you mix in, how you administer it, how often and at what time. Come, do not begrudge a friend the things you have — [a friend] who always has been and will be most devoted and most loving toward you. Fare you very well, most upright and most loving my Tycho, and love me, as you do, in return. At Rostock, 15 March, in the year of the Lord 1586.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born physician and professor of mathematics, then of medicine, at the University of Rostock; one of Tycho's older academic correspondents and an early teacher-figure (protocol §5.6). The letter answers a lost (or earlier) letter of Tycho's on remedies and engages him as a bibliographical agent.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Rostochium</i> → <b>Rostock</b> (protocol §5.7); the Hanseatic port and university town. <i>quod mare esset clausum</i> (\"the sea was closed\") refers to the winter closure of Baltic shipping, delaying delivery — not a political blockade.</li><li><b>Books purchased for Tycho.</b></li><li><i>Gnomonicen CLAVIJ Bambergensis</i> = the <i>Gnomonices libri octo</i> (Rome, 1581) of <b>Christophorus Clavius</b> (\"of Bamberg,\" Clavius's birthplace), the Jesuit mathematician and chief architect of the Gregorian reform. <i>Gnomonice</i> (the art of sundials/shadow-instruments) is kept as the work-title, not glossed to \"dialling.\"</li><li><i>MAGINI Tabulas novas et Ephemeridas</i> = the <i>Ephemerides</i> and <i>Novae coelestium orbium theoricae</i>/tables of <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> of Bologna (protocol §5.6), here described as <i>compositas … ad Calendarij emendationem … a GREGORIO XIII … institutam</i> — computed to agree with the Gregorian calendar reform of 1582.</li><li><i>errores a STADIO commissos</i> — the computational errors of <b>Johannes Stadius</b> (1527–1579), whose own widely-used <i>Ephemerides</i> Magini's apparatus corrected.</li><li><b>Currency.</b> <i>Ioachimici</i> = <b>Joachimsthalers</b> (silver thalers struck at Joachimsthal/Jáchymov); the prices (7 + 11–12 = 18) are given in this coin. Rendered \"Joachimsthalers,\" the Latin retained on first use.</li><li><b>Apparatus markers in the source.</b> The superscript <b>A</b> appearing three times in the Dreyer text (<i>Ioachimicis&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt; 7</i>, <i>ut&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt; tota summa</i>, <i>editioni&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt; cum indicijs</i>) is <b>not part of the letter</b>: it is Dreyer's witness/variant siglum (A = the Vienna MS, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66) keyed to footnotes in <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII. It is reproduced in the Source block as printed and bracketed `[A]` in the translation, where it carries no semantic content.</li><li><b>Allusion: Cato on the soothsayers.</b> <i>Quod enim CATO de Aruspicibus … Mirari se aiebat, si cum sibi mutuo occurrunt, non rideant</i> — the proverbial <i>risus haruspicum</i>: the elder Cato's remark (reported by Cicero, <i>De divinatione</i> 2.24.51) that he wondered how one <i>haruspex</i> could meet another without laughing. Brucaeus applies it to weather-prognostication; the <b>soothsayer (haruspex)</b> rendering is kept literal.</li><li><b>Astrology / publishing.</b> <i>Calendarij … editioni cum indicijs mutationum aëris</i> = an edition of a (prognosticating) calendar \"with forecasts of changes of the weather\" — i.e. an astro-meteorological almanac; Brucaeus presumes Tycho would not lend one of his own people to such a popularly-derided genre. <i>Cometas</i> = Tycho's projected work on comets (the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, on the 1577 comet, then in preparation/press); kept as the work-reference <i>Comets</i>.</li><li><b>Medicine / alchemy (the core of the letter).</b> A Galenist's defence against Tycho's Paracelsian (chymical) leanings.</li><li><i>decoctiones, infusiones … sola ptisana</i> → <b>decoctions, infusions … barley-water alone</b> (<i>ptisana</i>, the classic Galenic barley-tisane). <i>decoctio</i> per protocol §5.4 → <b>decoction</b>.</li><li><i>quintas essentias / tincturas / spiritus</i> → <b>quintessences / tinctures / spirits</b> — the Paracelsian preparations Brucaeus distrusts.</li><li><i>primae qualitates</i> → <b>the first qualities</b> (hot/cold/wet/dry of Galenic-Aristotelian medicine); <i>cognitio humoralis elementaris</i> → <b>humoral, elemental knowledge</b>.</li><li><i>spiritus vitrioli</i> → <b>spirit of vitriol</b> (the distillate of vitriol, ≈ sulphuric acid / its preparations); kept as the period term, not modernised.</li><li><i>ex sulphure, sale et hydrargyro compositum genus hominum</i> → \"the breed of men compounded out of <b>sulphur, salt, and quicksilver</b>\" — a barbed reference to the Paracelsian <i>tria prima</i> (the three principles), turned against the Paracelsians themselves; <i>hydrargyrus</i> = quicksilver/mercury.</li><li>Diseases: <i>Apoplexia, Epilepsia, Paralysi contractis [membris], Melancholia</i> → <b>apoplexy, epilepsy, paralysis of contracted [limbs], melancholy</b>. <i>contractis</i> is read as agreeing with an understood <i>membris</i> (\"contracted/cramped limbs\"); the elision is flagged, the supplied word bracketed.</li><li><i>remedium</i>-register: Brucaeus's claim that ordinary remedies <i>restore</i> (<i>restitui</i>) more patients is preserved as a comparative boast, not sharpened into a universal.</li><li><b>\"You know whom I mark.\"</b> <i>Scis quem notem</i> — Brucaeus pointedly declines to name the alchemist he is criticising (one <i>ex unico vitrioli spiritu multas tincturas parare … addito colore</i> — counterfeiting many tinctures from a single spirit of vitriol by adding colour); the studied reticence is kept, the target left unnamed as in the source.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (§5.5).</b> Opening <i>si vivis et vales, gaudeo</i> — the old \"if you are alive and well, I rejoice\" formula. Close <i>Bene vale … mi TYCHO, et me … mutuo ama</i> → <b>\"Fare you very well … my Tycho, and love me … in return.\"</b> <i>studiosissimus amantissimusque</i> → in the affective superlative register.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Rostochij XV Martij An. Domini 1586</i> = \"At <b>Rostock</b>, 15 March, in the year of the Lord 1586\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark and Lutheran Mecklenburg both still on the Julian calendar in 1586). The dataset <i>label</i> gives the place only (\"Rostock\"); the as-written date 15 March 1586 is carried into the header. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (i) <i>Paralysi contractis</i> requires an understood noun (<i>membris</i>), supplied in brackets; (ii) the unnamed target of <i>Scis quem notem</i> is deliberately left unnamed, as in the source.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — sense secure, dating unambiguous, no crux/lacuna/corruption; residue only a standard elision + the source's own deliberate non-naming + declared apparatus sigla.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII pp.100-101 read as page images. The book valuation (Gnomonice 7 + Ephemerides/Tabulae 11 = 18 Joachimsthalers), the disease list (Apoplexia, Epilepsia, Paralysi, Melancholia), and the closing (Rostochij XV Martij An. Domini 1586) match Dreyer's print exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15850623001", "next": "brahe-t_15870522001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports hearing a (false) rumour of Tycho's death, announces the purchase of Clavius's Gnomonice and Magini's Tables with Ephemerides on his behalf, and asks confirmation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860315001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860323001", "d": "1586-03-23", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Uraniborg, 23 March 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer, vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10689.4</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD HENRICUM RANZOVIUM.</p><p>QUOD prioribus tuis litteris, Nobilissime et Eruditissime Domine RANZOVI, nunc quam vel tu expectâras vel ipse proposueram, serius respondeam, effecit Hyemis asperitas, quae insulam hanc undique glacie circumsaeptam, quo minus hinc multo tempore solveretur, praepedivit, dumque interea alijs rebus occupor, facile fit, ut per incuriam mora haec mihi vel invito obrepuerit, quam ut candide interpreteris etiam atque etiam te rogatum volo. Quantum ad Landgravij petitionem attinet de observationibus Cometae praeterito anno visi, quas a me ipsius Celsitudo desiderat (velut ab exemplari litterarum tibi inscriptarum mihi transmisso cognovi) existimo me ipsius desiderijs pro virili satisfecisse. Dedi enim hoc nomine circa initium huius mensis ad ipsius Celsitudinem litteras et unâ adiunxi compendiosam quandam eius Cometae ex observationibus nostris descriptionem. Habebam autem primum in animo haec tibi mittere, ut per commodam opportunitatem tuo iussu D. Landgravio adferrentur. Verum cum interea negotium quoddam in nundinis Francfordensibus expediendum mihi occurreret, quod certum aliquem et proprium nuntium requirebat, non ignarus Cassellas, ubi Principis Landgravij aula est, in ipso itinere versus Francofurtum constitui, uni ex meis domesticis, qui ad nundinas illas meo nomine profecturus erat, ea etiam, quae D. Landgravio erant perferenda, commisi; verum ut tu etiam conscius reddaris eorum, quae Principi illi a me communicata sunt, curavi tum epistolam, tum etiam ea, quae de Cometa illo conscripseram, denuo exarari eorumque singulorum exemplar tibi inspiciendum cognoscendumque mitto. Addidi etiam Diarium quoddam Astrologicum et Metheorologicum huius anni, a quodam meo studiosorum elaboratum et sex alia exemplaria. Motuum ... Solis a nobis restitutum, cuius in litteris ad D. Landgravium datis fit mentio, siquidem is in hoc Diario continetur, non ... iudicium opus esse peculiari carta denotare.</p><p>Quantum vero ad posteriores tuas litteras attinet, quas ante triduum accepi, unâ cum D. Decani Coloniensis litteris et quibusdam picturis urbium et aliarum rerum per cupri caelaturam expressis, cuperem equidem ipsius D. Decani votis in mittendis plurium urbium, quas expetit, delineationibus, adiectis etiam rerum illic gestarum annalibus, satisfacere, sed neminem hîc habere possum, qui eas apprime delineare novit. Nec mihi circa rerum Astronomicarum penitiorem inquisitionem gnaviter occupato otium conceditur ad diversa negotia evagandi. Rescribam autem ipsemet Domino Decano, quam primum per otium licuerit, nunc enim praepeditus sum quodam libello Regi nostro conscribendo, quem intra Paschatis festum me absoluturum promisi. Quod si quid interea ad ipsum litterarum dederis, rogo ut moram hanc meam in rescribendo apud ipsum excusare velis; dabo operam, ut prima quaque occasione ipsius expectationi satisfiat. Vale. Datae Uraniburgi 23. Martij Anno 1586.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO HEINRICH RANTZAU.</b></p><p>That I reply to your earlier letter, most noble and most learned Master Rantzau, later now than either you had expected or I myself had proposed, the harshness of the winter has brought about, which — hemming this island in on every side with ice — hindered any passing from here for a long time; and while in the meantime I am taken up with other matters, it easily comes about that through inattention this delay crept up on me even against my will. I ask you again and again to interpret it charitably. As far as concerns the Landgrave's request about the observations of the comet seen this past year, which His Highness desires of me (as I learned from a copy, sent to me, of the letter addressed to you), I judge that I have, to the best of my ability, satisfied his wishes. For on this account, about the beginning of this month, I sent a letter to His Highness, and along with it I added a certain compendious description of that comet drawn from our observations. Now I had at first in mind to send these to you, so that, at a convenient opportunity and at your bidding, they might be conveyed to the Lord Landgrave. But since in the meantime a certain piece of business to be dispatched at the Frankfurt fair came up for me, which required some sure and particular messenger, [and] knowing that Kassel, where the court of the Prince Landgrave is, lies right on the route toward Frankfurt, I entrusted to one of my household, who was about to set out to that fair in my name, those things too that were to be delivered to the Lord Landgrave; but, so that you also may be made privy to the things that have been communicated by me to that Prince, I had both the letter and also the things I had written about that comet copied out anew, and I am sending you a copy of each of them to inspect and learn. I have added too a certain astrological and meteorological diary [almanac] of this year, worked up by one of my students, and six other copies. [...] of the motions of the Sun restored by us, of which mention is made in the letter sent to the Lord Landgrave — since it is contained in this diary — [it is] not [...] there be need to note [it] [judgement?] on a separate sheet.</p><p>But as far as concerns your later letter, which I received three days ago, together with the letter of the Lord Dean of Cologne and certain pictures of cities and of other things rendered by copper engraving: I should indeed wish to satisfy the wishes of the Lord Dean himself in sending the drawings of the several cities which he seeks, with the annals of the things done there added as well; but I can have no one here who knows how to draw them aptly. Nor, busily occupied as I am about the deeper investigation of astronomical matters, is leisure granted me to range off into diverse affairs. But I shall myself write back to the Lord Dean as soon as leisure permits; for now I am held up by a certain little book to be written for our King, which I have promised to finish within the feast of Easter. But if in the meantime you should send him any letter, I ask that you be willing to excuse this delay of mine in writing back to him; I shall take pains that at the very first occasion his expectation be satisfied. Farewell. Given at Uraniborg, 23 March, the year 1586.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of <b>Hven</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the Danish king in the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, seated at Breitenburg/Wandsbek; humanist, astrologer, and prolific patron and middleman of learned correspondence. He routinely served as an intermediary between Tycho and the Hesse-Kassel court, which this letter exemplifies.</li><li><b><i>Landgravius / Princeps Landgravius / Celsitudo</b></i> — <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince of Kassel (protocol §5.6); <i>Celsitudo</i> = \"His Highness\" (§5.5). His <i>aula</i> (court) is at <b>Kassel</b> (<i>Cassellae</i>; §5.7).</li><li><b><i>Cometae praeterito anno visi</b></i> — \"the comet seen this past year\": the <b>comet of 1585</b> (observed at Kassel by Rothmann and at Uraniborg by Tycho). Tycho's \"compendious description … from our observations\" is the report he had just sent to the Landgrave at the start of March 1586.</li><li><b><i>D. Decanus Coloniensis</b></i> — \"the Lord Dean of Cologne,\" not further specified in the letter; left as given. He had sent, via Rantzau, copper-engraved city views (<i>picturae urbium … per cupri caelaturam expressae</i>) and sought reciprocal city-drawings with local annals — a topographical-antiquarian exchange of the kind Rantzau brokered. <b>Identification left open</b> (no internal evidence names him).</li><li><b><i>nundinae Francfordenses</b></i> — the <b>Frankfurt (book) fair</b>, the great clearing-house through which Tycho's household agent travelled, conveniently by way of Kassel.</li><li><b><i>Diarium … Astrologicum et Metheorologicum … a quodam meo studiosorum elaboratum</b></i> — \"an astrological and meteorological diary [almanac] of this year, worked up by one of my students\": a calendar/almanac produced under Tycho's roof; <i>studiosus</i> = a student/assistant of Tycho's. <i>Metheorologicum</i> is the source spelling (for <i>meteorologicum</i>), retained.</li><li><b><i>Motuum … Solis a nobis restitutum</b></i> — \"the [...] of the motions of the Sun restored by us\": Tycho's <b>restored solar theory</b> (<i>motus Solis restitutus</i>; cf. <i>restitutio / instauratio astronomiae</i>, protocol §5.1), to which his own almanac's solar positions were computed. <b>Textual crux:</b> the working transcription carries two ellipses (\"Motuum ... Solis\" and \"non ... iudicium opus esse peculiari carta denotare\") that are not resolvable from the local material — no Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII scan is held in the corpus to collate against. The clause is left lacunose in the translation, with the gaps marked \"[...]\"; the closing sense is, with low confidence, \"[it is] not [necessary] … that there be need to note [it] on a separate sheet\" — i.e. the solar tables need not be set out separately because they are already contained in the diary. The reading of <i>iudicium</i> (\"judgement,\" but here perhaps \"computation/forecast\") within the gap is uncertain. Not smoothed; see Confidence.</li><li><b><i>libellus Regi nostro conscribendus</b></i> — \"a little book to be written for our King\" (<b>Frederik II of Denmark</b>, protocol §5.6), promised within Easter. A short presentation work, unspecified here.</li><li><b>Allusion.</b> <i>Hyemis asperitas … glacie circumsaeptam</i> — the winter ice-bound isolation of Hven, given as the literal cause of the postal delay; not a topos but a recurrent material fact of correspondence from Uraniborg.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 23. Martij Anno 1586</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 23 March 1586.\" Denmark was on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b>; dated O.S. The dataset label gives only the place (<i>Uraniborg</i>); the as-written dateline <b>23 March 1586</b> governs the header (protocol §3, §4). No in-letter date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Formula.</b> <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\" (§5.5).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the body is rendered securely. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02) after Dreyer collation</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 115–116): Dreyer prints from Tycho's own <i>adumbratio</i> (draft) in the same MS (cod. Vindob. lat. 10689⁴) and <b>reproduces the two lacunae identically</b> — they are gaps in the draft, not a collation failure; the syntax and force of <i>iudicium</i> stand as the draft bears them. The <i>Dean of Cologne</i> is now identified: <b>Georg Braun</b> (1541–1622), dean of St. Maria ad Gradus and editor of the <i>Civitates orbis terrarum</i>, for which Rantzau supplied the Danish city-views — matching the engraved-city-view exchange here.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.100-103 as page images; 'TYCHO BRAHE AD HENRICUM RANZOVIUM' runs from the foot of p.101 through the closing date on p.102 ('Vale. Datae Uraniburgi 23. Martij Anno 1586.'). Collated the full letter word for word against Dreyer, including the genuinely lacunose sentence ('Motuum.... Solis a nobis restitutum...non... iudicium opus esse peculiari carta denotare') where our source_text's ellipsis-dot count differs cosmetically from Dreyer's but marks the same manuscript gap; the English correctly renders this passage with bracketed uncertainty markers rather than false certainty. Checked every proper name, place, and date: Heinrich Rantzau, the Landgrave (of Hesse-Kassel), Kassel on the route to Frankfurt, the Dean of Cologne, the six other diary copies, Easter, and the closing date 23 March 1586 all check out exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10689.4."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15850108001", "next": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870117001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses a delayed reply by winter ice blocking the island, and reports having already sent Wilhelm IV a compendious description of the comet observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860323001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860328001", "d": "1586-03-28", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "ck": "Rudolf 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Emperor Rudolf II → [Imperial printing privilege in favour of Tycho Brahe] — Prague, 28 March 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (Imperial chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichisches Staatsarchiv, Reichsregistratur, Prot. 18, Fol. 93 ff.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IMPRESSORIUM AD DECENNIUM PRO TYCHONE BRAHE DANO.</p><p>RUDOLPHUS etc. Notum facimus tenore praesentium universis, quod cum nobis humiliter exposuerit syncere nobis dilectus Tycho Brahe Danus, dominus de Knudstrup, se pro singulari instaurandae astronomiae studio nonnulla mathematica concinnasse opera ac pluribus etiam nunc incumbere, quae pro communi usu bonoque publico typis in lucem aedere decreverit, vereri autem, ne forte aemuli aliqui ipsum sperato laborum ac sumptuum suorum fructu atque emolumento privare conaturi sint, demisse proinde supplicando, ut privilegio Nostro Caesareo huiusmodi imposturas avertere clementer dignaremur. Nos sane indemnitati hac in parte suae benigne consultum cupientes, humilibus eiusdem precibus deesse noluimus.</p><p>Ideoque omnibus ac singulis calcographis, bibliopolis ac alijs quamcumque librariam negociationem exercentibus, firmiter inhibemus ac vetamus, ne quis opera illa astronomica seu mathematica, quae dicti Tychonis Brahe studio et elucubratione in lucem prodierint, per decem annorum spacium a prima cuiuslibet aeditionis die computandum intra Sacri Romani Imperij, Regnorumque et Dominiorum Nostrorum hereditariorum fines, in toto vel in parte simili aut alia quapiam figura vel charactere imitari, recudere aliove recudenda dare vel alibi impressa adducere, vendere aut distrahere clam vel occulte praeter expressam ipsius voluntatem et consensum ausit. Si quis autem secus faciendo privilegium hoc Nostrum Caesareum transgredi conatus fuerit, eum non solum libris illis omnibus perperam sic excusis et adductis (quos quidem praedictus Brahe tamquam author eorum vel mandatarius ipsius, ubicunque deprehensos propria authoritate vel magistratus loci illius auxilio sibi vendicare poterit) de facto privandum sed poena insuper x marcharum auri puri, fisco Nostro Imperiali fraudis vindici et saepedicti Tychonis Brahe usibus ex aequo pendenda irremissibiliter decernimus mulctandum. Dummodo tamen, pro eo ac pollicetur, huiusmodi libri nihil in se scandalosum vel Sacri Romani Imperij constitutionibus de re litteraria aeditis aut orthodoxae religioni catholice adversum sive in textu sive in praefatione aut alias quoque modo contineant, ideoque idem Brahe terna ad minus cuiuslibet operis exemplaria quamprimum proprijs suis sumptibus ad Cancellariam Nostram Imperialem transmittere teneatur, quatenus privilegio hoc Nostro Caesareo uti voluerit. Quod si neglexerit, eo ipso privatus et exutus plane censeatur. Mandamus ergo universis et singulis Nostris ac Sacri Romani Imperij Regnorumque et ditionum nostrarum haereditariarum subditis ac fidelibus dilectis cuiuscunque status, gradus, ordinis, conditionis aut dignitatis exstiterint, tam ecclesiasticis quam saecularibus, ijs praesertim qui in magistratu constituti vel proprio vel superiorum suorum nomine et loco iuris ac iustitiae administrationem exercent, ne quenquam privilegium ac interdictum hoc Nostrum Caesareum impune transgredi, spernere, negligere aut violare patiantur, sed contumaces, si quos compererint, prescripta poena plecti alijsque idoneis ac opportunis rationibus coerceri curent, quatenus et ipsi eandem mulctam incurrere noluerint. Harum testimonio litterarum, manu Nostra subscriptarum ac sigilli Nostri Caesarei impressione munitarum.</p><p>Datum Pragae, die 28. martij anno etc. 1586.</p>", "en": "<p><b>PRINTING PRIVILEGE FOR TEN YEARS IN FAVOUR OF TYCHO BRAHE THE DANE.</b></p><p>Rudolf, etc. By the tenor of these presents we make known to all: that whereas Tycho Brahe the Dane, lord of Knudstrup, sincerely beloved of us, has humbly set forth to us that out of his singular zeal for the restoration of astronomy he has composed certain mathematical works, and is even now engaged upon several more, which he has resolved to issue into the light through the press for the common use and the public good — but that he fears lest perhaps certain rivals may seek to deprive him of the hoped-for fruit and profit of his labours and his expenses — humbly petitioning, accordingly, that we would graciously deign by our Imperial privilege to avert impostures of this kind: we, indeed, benignly desiring that provision be made for his protection from loss in this matter, have been unwilling to fail his humble prayers.</p><p>And therefore we firmly inhibit and forbid all and each of the printers [calcographers], booksellers, and others carrying on any sort of book-trade whatsoever, that no one dare — for the space of ten years, to be reckoned from the day of the first edition of each [work], within the bounds of the Holy Roman Empire and of our hereditary kingdoms and dominions — to imitate, reprint, or give to be reprinted those astronomical or mathematical works which shall have come into the light through the said Tycho Brahe's study and lucubration, in whole or in part, in a like or in any other form or type, or to bring in [copies] printed elsewhere, or to sell or to dispose of them, secretly or covertly, against his express will and consent. But if anyone, by acting otherwise, shall have attempted to transgress this our Imperial privilege, we irremissibly decree that he is in fact to be deprived not only of all those books thus wrongly printed and brought in (which indeed the aforesaid Brahe, as their author, or his agent, wherever they are caught, shall be able to claim for himself by his own authority or with the help of the magistrate of that place) — but is moreover to be fined a penalty of ten marks of pure gold, to be paid out without remission in equal parts to our Imperial treasury, the avenger of the fraud, and to the uses of the oft-named Tycho Brahe. Provided always, however — in accordance with what he promises — that books of this kind contain nothing scandalous in themselves, nor anything contrary either to the constitutions of the Holy Roman Empire issued concerning literary matters or to the orthodox Catholic religion, whether in the text or in the preface or in any other manner whatsoever; and that therefore the same Brahe be bound, as soon as possible at his own expense, to transmit at least three copies of each work to our Imperial Chancery, in so far as he shall wish to make use of this our Imperial privilege. But if he shall have neglected this, by that very fact let him be deemed plainly deprived and stripped of it. We command, therefore, all and each of our subjects and faithful beloved of the Holy Roman Empire and of our hereditary kingdoms and dominions, of whatever estate, rank, order, condition, or dignity they may be, ecclesiastical as well as secular — and those especially who, set in a magistracy, exercise the administration of law and justice, whether in their own name or in the name and stead of their superiors — that they suffer no one to transgress, spurn, neglect, or violate with impunity this our Imperial privilege and interdict, but that they see to it that the contumacious, if they find any, be punished with the prescribed penalty and constrained by other fitting and opportune means — in so far as they too would not wish to incur the same fine. In testimony of which [we have issued] this letter, subscribed by our hand and fortified with the impress of our Imperial seal.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 28th day of March, in the year etc. 1586.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Rudolf II</b> (1552–1612), Holy Roman Emperor (r. 1576–1612), King of Bohemia and of Hungary, residing at Prague. The dataset records the sender as \"Rudolf 2.\" and leaves the recipient field \"empty,\" which is correct: this is not a personal letter but an <b>Imperial <i>privilegium impressorium</b></i> — a printing-monopoly grant issued <i>in favour of</i> Tycho, the operative parties being the Empire's printers and booksellers, not a named addressee. The header therefore renders the recipient slot as the bracketed function of the instrument.</li><li><b>Beneficiary.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe Danus, dominus de Knudstrup</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), styled by his patrimonial seat <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp, Scania); <i>syncere nobis dilectus</i> (\"sincerely beloved of us\") is the standard chancery courtesy-formula for a favoured suppliant, not evidence of personal acquaintance (Rudolf would not become Tycho's patron until 1599).</li><li><b>Document type &amp; rubric.</b> <i>IMPRESSORIUM AD DECENNIUM</i> = \"a printing-privilege for a ten-year term\" (<i>impressorium</i>, the chancery noun for a printing licence/privilege). The grant is registered in the Imperial <i>Reichsregistratur</i> (witness A), hence the formulaic <i>RUDOLPHUS etc.</i> opening and <i>anno etc. 1586</i> closing, where <i>etc.</i> abbreviates the full Imperial titulature and regnal-year formula of the register.</li><li><b>Term of the privilege.</b> <i>per decem annorum spacium a prima cuiuslibet aeditionis die computandum</i> — ten years, counted <b>from the first day of publication of each individual work</b> (not from the date of the grant); a per-title term, characteristic of these privileges.</li><li><b><i>instaurandae astronomiae studio</b></i> — \"zeal for the restoration of astronomy\": <i>instauratio astronomiae</i>, Tycho's own programmatic phrase (<i>astronomia instaurata</i>), rendered \"restoration\" per protocol §5.1.</li><li><b><i>calcographis</b></i> — \"printers\" (chalcographers/typographers); paired with <i>bibliopolis</i> (booksellers) and the catch-all <i>alijs quamcumque librariam negociationem exercentibus</i> (any others in the book-trade).</li><li><b>The prohibited acts.</b> <i>imitari, recudere aliove recudenda dare … adducere, vendere aut distrahere</i> — to imitate, reprint, give out to be reprinted, import (copies printed elsewhere), sell, or dispose of; <i>in toto vel in parte … simili aut alia quapiam figura vel charactere</i> = \"in whole or in part, in a like or any other form or type [character].\" <i>praeter expressam ipsius voluntatem et consensum</i> = \"against [i.e. without] his express will and consent.\"</li><li><b>Penalty (negation/legal force verified).</b> The sanction is cumulative, not alternative: <i>non solum … privandum sed poena insuper … mulctandum</i> = \"not only to be deprived … but moreover to be fined.\" The confiscated stock the offender forfeits; the fine is <i>x marcharum auri puri</i> = <b>ten marks of pure gold</b>, <i>ex aequo pendenda</i> = \"to be paid in equal parts\" between the Imperial fisc (<i>fraudis vindex</i>, \"avenger of the fraud\") and Tycho's own use. <i>irremissibiliter</i> = \"without remission.\" The author's recovery right — <i>quos … propria authoritate vel magistratus loci illius auxilio sibi vendicare poterit</i> — lets Brahe or his agent seize offending copies <i>by his own authority or with the local magistrate's help</i>.</li><li><b>The conditions on Tycho (proviso clause).</b> <i>Dummodo tamen … nihil in se scandalosum … contineant</i> = \"provided, however, that [the books] contain nothing scandalous,\" nothing against the Empire's press-laws (<i>Sacri Romani Imperij constitutionibus de re litteraria</i>) or <i>orthodoxae religioni catholice</i> (\"the orthodox Catholic religion\") — a notable confessional clause binding the Lutheran Tycho's output, <i>sive in textu sive in praefatione</i> (\"in text or preface\"). The deposit requirement: <i>terna ad minus cuiuslibet operis exemplaria … ad Cancellariam Nostram Imperialem transmittere</i> = \"transmit <b>at least three copies</b> of each work to our Imperial Chancery,\" at Tycho's own expense — a condition precedent: <i>quatenus privilegio … uti voluerit. Quod si neglexerit, eo ipso privatus … censeatur</i> = \"in so far as he shall wish to use the privilege; but if he neglects [it], by that very fact let him be deemed deprived.\" (Negation count in this clause confirmed: <i>nihil … vel … aut … adversum</i>, one governing negation distributed over the enumerated objects; rendered as a single \"nothing … nor … or.\")</li><li><b>The mandate to officials.</b> <i>ne quenquam … impune transgredi … patiantur, sed … plecti … coerceri curent, quatenus et ipsi eandem mulctam incurrere noluerint</i> — magistrates are not to allow violation and are to see the contumacious punished, <i>\"in so far as they too would not wish to incur the same fine\"</i> (i.e. on pain of incurring the penalty themselves; the conditional <i>quatenus … noluerint</i> is a veiled threat to negligent officials — its mood is consistent across the parallel clause at the proviso's end and is rendered, not smoothed).</li><li><b>Attestation.</b> <i>manu Nostra subscriptarum ac sigilli Nostri Caesarei impressione munitarum</i> = \"subscribed by our hand and fortified with the impress of our Imperial seal\" — the register's note of the original's authentication.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Pragae, die 28. martij … 1586</i> = \"Given at <b>Prague</b>, 28 March 1586.\" Denmark and the Empire's chancery practice here are not in tension on the figure as transcribed; the dataset label (\"1586-03-28 Prag\") agrees with the dateline. Treated as <b>Old Style</b> per protocol §3 (the date is given without an <i>n.s.</i> marker; the equivalent N.S. date would be 7 April 1586, but the source does not flag a style and none is asserted here beyond the as-written figure).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a formulaic Imperial chancery privilege whose legal architecture (per-title decennial term; cumulative confiscation-plus-fine; the gold-mark penalty split fisc/beneficiary; the confessional and three-copy-deposit provisos as conditions of the grant; the magistrates' mandate under threat of the same fine) is rendered with the negations and conditionals counted and preserved. No genuine crux; the only point worth a reader's note is the confessional proviso binding a Lutheran's works to \"the orthodox Catholic religion,\" which is translated as written, not glossed away.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.32-35 as page images (letter no. 52, IMPRESSORIUM AD DECENNIUM PRO TYCHONE BRAHE DANO, spanning pp.33-34). Collated the Latin privilege text against the printed page in full: the ten-year term (per decem annorum spacium), the ten-mark-of-pure-gold penalty (x marcharum auri puri), the at-least-three-copies deposit requirement (terna ad minus... exemplaria), and the closing Datum Pragae, die 28. martij anno etc. 1586 all match exactly, essentially word for word. English translation checked clause by clause against the Latin; all numbers, the place name Prague, and the legal formulas are rendered faithfully with no invented or dropped content.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichisches Staatsarchiv, Reichsregistratur, Prot. 18, Fol. 93 ff.."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Grants Tycho Brahe a ten-year imperial printing privilege, prohibiting all printers and booksellers from reproducing his mathematical works without authorisation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860328001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860414001", "d": "1586-04-14", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 14 April 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = the<i> Epistolae astronomicae </i>Tycho printed at Uraniborg) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI<br>BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.AWILHELM VON<br>GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA/ ETC.AVNSERN günstigen Gruß zuuor/ Vester Lieber Besonder/ Wir haben ewer schreiben<br>de dato den 1 Martij empfangen/<br>gelesen/ vnd daraus neben andern verstanden/ das der Kön. W. zu Denmarck/ StadthalterA in Holstein<br>Henrich Rantzow Euch vnser<br>schreiben/ so wir an jhnen des letzt erschienen Cometens halben gethan/<br>zugeschickt/ Das jr vns nun darauff ewre Obseruationes<br>desselbigen Cometen so gutwillig zugeschickt/ dzA thun wir vns gnediglichen bedancken/ vnd wöllenA euch nicht verhalten/ das so bald wir ewere Obseruationes gesehen/ haben wir vnsern Mathematicum gefordert/ vnd seine Obseruationes eiusdem Cometae mit den ewern conferirt, vnd befunden/ das nach gebüerender aequation<br>temporis et Longitudinum beyderley Obseruationes<br>durchaus kaum vber ein minuten von einander discrepirenA, welches warlich ein grosses/ vnd ein Zeichen ist/ das beid vnsere vnd<br>auch Ewere Instrumenta just vnd gut/ auch die Obseruatores fleißig gewesen/ vnd gut Gesicht gehabt. Vnsere Instrumenta, nemlich der Quadrant vnd Sextant, so vnser Mathematicus braucht/ seind nicht so groß/ als die<br>Ewren/ sondern seind nur quadrupedalia, lassen vns auch<br>bedüncken/ wo sie grösser weren/ sie würden desto vnbreuchlicher/ et propter molem desto vbler zu tractiren sein/<br>doch möchten wir Ewere Instrumenta/ sonderlich den Globum auch gern sehen vnd examiniren,<br>auch Euch gönnen/ das jhr in loco<br>Meridionalis vaporibus minus obnoxio ewere Wohnung hettet/ damit jhr<br>die ding desto besser obseruiren köntet. Was nuhn betrifft/<br>das wir Euch vnsere Obseruationes nouae Stellae ad Cassipeiam,<br>deßgleichen der andern Cometen vnd auch Solis wolten zuschicken/ ists an dem/ das damals/ wie dieselben Obseruationes geschehen/ vnsere Instrumenta noch nicht so fleissig seind abgericht gewesen/ gleich wie<br>sie jetzo durch angeben des WITTICHII, et diligentia et industria vnsers Vhrmachers<br>M. Just Burrjj, qui quasi indagine alter ArchimedesA ist/ seind angerichtet/ Wie aber dem/ so haben wir Ewerm zu vns abgefertigtenA vergünstiget/ dieselben abzuschreiben/ vnnd Euch zuzubringen. Was aber<br>praedictam nouam Stellam antrifft/ haben wir an demselsten<br>gar keine Parallaxin, auch gar keinen motum weder in Longitudinem noch Latitudinem, sed tantum in recta Linea in Altitudinem aut<br>profunditatem mercken können/ aber in dem grossen Cometen, so Anno 77 erschienen/ da haben wir<br>deßmals keinen aulicum Mathematicum gehabt/ darumb haben wir auchA seine Parallaxin nicht eigentlich obseruiren können/ aber wie jhr aus jetzigen vnsers Mathematici<br>Obseruation zusehen/ so wil der auch nicht/ das der jetzo<br>letzt erschienenA<br>Comet einige Parallaxin gehabt habe/ Ergo<br>ist das fundamentum Philosophicum nichtig/ das die Cometen in summa Regione Aëris infra circulos Lunae solten generirt werden/ Also auch finden wir nicht allein aus<br>vorigen vnsern sondern auch jetzigen vnsers Mathematici gar fleißigen Obseruationibus, das warlich Stellae fixae viel in<br>einem andern stande seind/ dann sie in Tabulis annotirt, Dann<br>jhrer viel vmst etzliche gradus anders befunden/ tam in Longitudine, quam in Latitudine, accedit quod Latitudo, quae<br>tamen consensu omnium Astronomorum debebat esse inuariabilis, in vielen<br>vnd fast allen sich valde sensibiliter, ja schier vff ein<br>halben grad vnd mehr verruckt. Ob nun solches geschehen sey vitio<br>Typographorum, oder ob die Stellae fixae in se proprium<br>aliquem motum haben/ geben wir euch als dem vornemststen dieser zeit<br>Mathematico, der die sach auch selbst angreifft vnd<br>verstehet/ vnd nicht ein Mathematicus vnderm Dach ist/ wie<br>die meisten seind/ zu iudiciren anheim. Was vnsere Instrumenta betrifft/ haben wir sie ewern abgefertigten alleA sehen lassen/ der euch daruon wird bericht thun können/ Dann den Sextanten lassen wir warlich nicht verachten/ Sintemal wir zu<br>etzlichen mahlen vnserm Mathematico zween Stern dardurch abzusehen befohlen/ Da wir<br>nun selbst distantiam vtriusque genommen/ das Instrument verruckt/ vnd jhn von newem absehen lassen/ hat er fürwarA sie so scharff abgesehen/ das intra primam, secundam et<br>tertiam Obseruationem nicht ein viertel einer minuten<br>differentz zu spüren/ Darumb wenn fehl da ist/ mus man den vnsers bedünckens in centro visus so<br>subtil nicht suchen/ sondern viel mehr beym artifice. Ferner haben wir Ewer iudicium de inquirenda<br>vera Altitudine Poli vel Aequatoris gar gern vernommen/ dann wir auch<br>befunden/ das wenn man die ding ex Altitudine Solis maxima in<br>vtroque tropico suchen wil/ das es gar nicht mit der Obseruation circumpolarium Stellarum thut eintreffen/ Derhalben haben<br>wir durch vnsern Mathematicum etzliche Stern/ quae non<br>occidunt, lassen in Meridiano cis et vltra Polum<br>obseruiren, wie jhr hierneben zusehen/ Daraus wir vnsere Eleuationem Polarem huius loci et quae his accidunt genomen/ Also auch<br>haben wir von etzlichen Jaren Refractionem valde sensibilem,<br>beyd in Sole et Stellis circa Horizontem gefunden/ auch ein<br>mal Venerem occidentem (quam diligentissime obseruauimus)<br>fast beynahe ein viertel einer stund stationariam befunden/<br>adeo vt eo tempore nihil raptu primi mobilis moueretur, et<br>adhuc appareret Eleuata duobus et amplius gradibus supra Horizontem, cum<br>totidem reuera esset sub Horizonte, deinde autem quasi ad momentum euanuit.<br>Quae omnia videbantur miraculosa nisi Vitellio in suis Opticis harum rationem praebuisset. Wir<br>schicken euch auch hierneben: doch in gutem vertrawen/ darin jhr es auch<br>behalten vnd noch zur zeit nichtA<br>spargiren, sondern nur mit Ewren Obseruationibus conferiren<br>wölletA: Obseruationes etzlicher vornehmer Stern/ wie sie vnser Mathematicus beyd in Meridiano, vnd<br>auch per SextantemA obseruirt. Wo jhr nuhn lust zu rechnenA habt/ möcht jhr daraus loca vera tam LongitudinisA, quam Latitudinis bald finden/ in vnserm Globo treffen sie sein zu/ Es sol aber vnser Mathematicus dieselben noch alleA<br>per calculum reformiren, dann den Instrumenten/ sie werden so fleißig gemacht als sie wöllenA/ adamussim nicht zu trawen. Welches wir euch vff ewer<br>schreiben hinwider nicht wolten verhalten/ mit gnedigen gesinnen/ jhr wollet vns<br>ewere Obseruationes, dergleichen loca vera<br>nostrarum Obseruationum, wie jhr vnsere gegen den ewrenA<br>per calculum findet/ hinwider zuschicken/ Damit wir es mit<br>den vnsern zu collationiren. Caetera ex adiuncta Mathematici Epistola intelliges. Vnd wir seind euch mit gnedigen willen jederzeit wollgeneigt/ADatum in vnser FestungACassel am 14 Aprilis, Anno<br>86.WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, pp. 50–51; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. OB wir wol in der Epistola melden, das wir die Obseruationes ewerm abgefertigten abzuschreiben zugestelt, Dieweil sie aber fast muhesamb zu discernieren, So haben wir durch ein andern gedachte Obseruationes beyd Cometarum, vnd auch Solis, so viel wir dero noch beyhanden haben, abschreiben lassen, vnd thun sie euch hiermit vbersenden. Was aber Obseruationem Fixarum betrifft, haben wir loca Longitudinum &amp; Latitudinum, wie sie vnser Globus gibt, hinein verzeichnet. Es soll sie aber vnser Mathematicus, wenn er die vbrigen, so er propter vaporosum Caelum bißhero nicht obseruiren konnen, auch darzu bekumpt, alle rechnen, glauben doch, es werde nicht ein grosser differentz zwischen beyderley Obseruation sein. Oculum [Tauri], dextrum hum. Orionis, Canem minorem &amp; maiorem, die haben wir nicht allein per distantiam inter se, &amp; Latitudinem Meridianam lassen obseruiren, Sondern durch vnser minuten vnd Secunden Vhrlein: welches gar gewisse stunden geben, vnd a Meridie in Meridiem offtmals nicht ein minuten varijren, jhr tempus der culmination in Meridiano gar [accurate] zu etlich viel mahlen, vnd daraus jhr Longitudinem vel potius Caeli metationem genommen, das vnsers bedunckens wir dero gewiß sein. WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the steadfast, Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup. — Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand, Our trusty and well-beloved. We have received your letter, dated the 1st of March, and read it, and understood from it, among other things, that the King's Majesty of Denmark's governor [<i>Statthalter</i>] in Holstein, Heinrich Rantzau, forwarded to you our letter — the one we had addressed to him concerning the comet last seen [<i>des letzt erschienen Cometens</i>]. That you have now, in response, so willingly sent us your <i>observations</i> of that same comet — for that we graciously give you our thanks; and we will not conceal from you that, as soon as we had seen your <i>observations</i>, we summoned our <i>mathematician</i>, and compared his <i>observations of the same comet</i> [<i>Obseruationes eiusdem Cometae</i>] with yours, and found that, after the due <i>equation</i> of <i>times and longitudes</i> [<i>aequation temporis et Longitudinum</i>], the two sets of <i>observations</i> throughout scarcely differ from one another by more than one minute — which is truly a great thing, and a sign that both our and also your <i>instruments</i> are exact and good, and that the <i>observers</i>, too, were diligent and had good eyesight. Our <i>instruments</i>, namely the <i>quadrant</i> and <i>sextant</i> that our <i>mathematician</i> uses, are not so large as yours, but are only <i>four feet [in size]</i> [<i>quadrupedalia</i>]; and it seems to us, too, that were they larger, they would be the less serviceable, and <i>on account of their bulk</i> [<i>propter molem</i>] the harder to handle. Yet we should be glad to see and <i>examine</i> your <i>instruments</i> too, especially the <i>globe</i>; and we wish you, too, that you had your dwelling <i>in a place less exposed to the vapours of the south</i> [<i>in loco Meridionalis vaporibus minus obnoxio</i>], so that you might <i>observe</i> these things the better. Now as for our sending you our <i>observations of the new star toward Cassiopeia</i> [<i>Obseruationes nouae Stellae ad Cassipeiam</i>], and likewise of the other comets and of <i>the Sun</i> [<i>Solis</i>] — the case is that at the time, when those <i>observations</i> were made, our <i>instruments</i> had not yet been so carefully calibrated as they are now set in order [<i>angerichtet</i>] through the indications of <i>Wittich</i> [<i>WITTICHII</i>] and the <i>diligence and industry</i> of our clockmaker Master Just Bürgi, who is, as it were, <i>another Archimedes in his ingenuity</i> [<i>qui quasi indagine alter Archimedes ist</i>]. Be that as it may, we have granted your messenger sent to us [leave] to copy those out and to bring them to you. But as concerns <i>the aforesaid new star</i> [<i>praedictam nouam Stellam</i>], we have been able to detect in it <i>no parallax at all</i> [<i>gar keine Parallaxin</i>], and also <i>no motion at all, neither in longitude nor in latitude, but only along a straight line, in altitude or in depth</i> [<i>gar keinen motum weder in Longitudinem noch Latitudinem, sed tantum in recta Linea in Altitudinem aut profunditatem</i>]; whereas in the great comet that appeared in the year [15]77, we had at that time no <i>court mathematician</i> [<i>aulicum Mathematicum</i>], and therefore we could not properly <i>observe</i> its <i>parallax</i>. But as you may see from our present <i>mathematician's observation</i>, he too will not have it that the comet last now appeared had any <i>parallax</i> whatever. <i>Therefore</i> [<i>Ergo</i>] the <i>philosophical foundation</i> [<i>fundamentum Philosophicum</i>] is void — the one that the comets ought to be <i>generated in the highest region of the air, below the circles of the Moon</i> [<i>in summa Regione Aëris infra circulos Lunae</i>]. Likewise, too, we find, not only from our former but also from our present <i>mathematician's</i> most diligent <i>observations</i>, that in truth <i>the fixed stars</i> [<i>Stellae fixae</i>] stand much in another position than they are noted in the <i>Tables</i> [<i>Tabulis</i>]; for many of them we have found to be by several <i>degrees</i> otherwise, <i>both in longitude and in latitude</i> [<i>tam in Longitudine, quam in Latitudine</i>]; <i>added to which the latitude — which nevertheless, by the consensus of all astronomers, ought to be invariable</i> [<i>accedit quod Latitudo, quae tamen consensu omnium Astronomorum debebat esse inuariabilis</i>] — has shifted in many, indeed in almost all of them <i>very sensibly</i> [<i>valde sensibiliter</i>], nay by nearly half a degree and more. Now whether this has come about <i>through a fault of the printers</i> [<i>vitio Typographorum</i>], or whether <i>the fixed stars</i> have <i>some proper motion of their own</i> [<i>in se proprium aliquem motum</i>], we leave to you to <i>judge</i> — as the foremost <i>mathematician</i> of this age, who himself also takes the matter in hand and understands it, and is not a <i>mathematician under the roof</i> [a closet astronomer], as most are. As concerns our <i>instruments</i>, we let your messenger see them all, who will be able to report to you about them; for the <i>sextant</i> we truly do not let be despised, inasmuch as several times we ordered our <i>mathematician</i> to sight two stars through it — whereupon, after we ourselves had taken the <i>distance of the two</i> [<i>distantiam vtriusque</i>], shifted the <i>instrument</i>, and let him sight anew, he sighted them so sharply that <i>within the first, second, and third observation</i> [<i>intra primam, secundam et tertiam Obseruationem</i>] not a quarter of one minute of difference was to be felt. Therefore, when there is an error, one must, in our opinion, not seek it so subtly <i>in the centre of vision</i> [<i>in centro visus</i>], but much rather <i>in the craftsman</i> [<i>beym artifice</i>]. Further, we have very gladly heard your <i>judgement concerning the inquiry into the true altitude of the pole or of the equator</i> [<i>iudicium de inquirenda vera Altitudine Poli vel Aequatoris</i>]; for we too have found that if one will seek these things <i>from the greatest altitude of the Sun in either tropic</i> [<i>ex Altitudine Solis maxima in vtroque tropico</i>], it does not at all agree with the <i>observation</i> of the <i>circumpolar stars</i>. On that account we have, through our <i>mathematician</i>, had several stars <i>that do not set</i> [<i>quae non occidunt</i>] <i>observed in the meridian on this side of and beyond the pole</i> [<i>in Meridiano cis et vltra Polum</i>], as you may see herewith; from which we have taken our <i>polar elevation of this place, and the things that pertain thereto</i> [<i>Eleuationem Polarem huius loci et quae his accidunt</i>]. Likewise, too, for some years past we have found <i>a very sensible refraction</i> [<i>Refractionem valde sensibilem</i>], both <i>in the Sun and in the stars near the horizon</i> [<i>in Sole et Stellis circa Horizontem</i>]; and once, too, we found <i>setting Venus</i> [<i>Venerem occidentem</i>] (which we <i>observed</i> most diligently) to remain <i>stationary</i> [<i>stationariam</i>] for very nearly a quarter of an hour, <i>so much so that at that time nothing was moved by the sweep of the primum mobile, and it still appeared elevated by two degrees and more above the horizon, when in reality it was just as far below the horizon, and then it vanished as if in an instant</i> [<i>adeo vt eo tempore nihil raptu primi mobilis moueretur, et adhuc appareret Eleuata duobus et amplius gradibus supra Horizontem, cum totidem reuera esset sub Horizonte, deinde autem quasi ad momentum euanuit</i>]. <i>All of which would have seemed miraculous, had not Witelo, in his Optics, supplied the explanation of them</i> [<i>Quae omnia videbantur miraculosa nisi Vitellio in suis Opticis harum rationem praebuisset</i>]. We also send you herewith — yet <i>in good confidence</i>, in which you are also to keep it and <i>not yet</i> for the present <i>divulge</i> it [<i>noch zur zeit nicht spargiren</i>], but only <i>compare</i> it with your own <i>observations</i> [<i>nur mit Ewren Obseruationibus conferiren wöllet</i>] — the <i>observations</i> of several notable stars, as our <i>mathematician observed</i> them both <i>in the meridian</i> [<i>in Meridiano</i>] and also <i>by the sextant</i> [<i>per Sextantem</i>]. If you now have a mind to compute, you may readily find from them the <i>true places, both of longitude and of latitude</i> [<i>loca vera tam Longitudinis, quam Latitudinis</i>]; in our <i>globe</i> they match well. But our <i>mathematician</i> is yet to <i>reform all these by calculation</i> [<i>per calculum reformiren</i>], for the <i>instruments</i> — be they made as carefully as they will — are not to be trusted <i>to a hair's exactness</i> [<i>adamussim</i>]. All of which we would not, in turn, withhold from you in reply to your letter; with the gracious intention that you should, in turn, send us your <i>observations</i>, and likewise the <i>true places of our observations</i> [<i>loca vera nostrarum Obseruationum</i>], as you find ours against yours <i>by calculation</i> [<i>per calculum</i>], so that we may <i>collate</i> it with our own. <i>The rest you will gather from the enclosed letter of [our] mathematician</i> [<i>Caetera ex adiuncta Mathematici Epistola intelliges</i>]. And we are at all times well disposed toward you with gracious goodwill.</p><p>Given in our fortress [of] Kassel, the 14th of April, in the year [15]86.</p><p>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Postscript. Although We say in the letter that We had handed Our messenger sent to us the observations to copy, yet since they were rather laborious to discern, We have therefore had the said observations — both of the comets and of the Sun, so many of them as We still have to hand — copied out by another, and We herewith send them over to you. But as concerns the observation of the fixed stars, We have noted into it the places of longitude and latitude, as Our globe gives them. But Our mathematician is yet to compute them all, when he obtains besides the rest, which he has hitherto been unable to observe on account of the vaporous sky; We believe, however, that there will be no great difference between the two sets of observation. The eye [of the Bull, i.e. Aldebaran], the right shoulder of Orion [Betelgeuse], the Lesser and Greater Dog [Procyon and Sirius] — these We have had observed not only by their distance from one another and their meridian latitude, but also by Our minute- and second-clocks, which give quite exact hours and from meridian to meridian often do not vary by even one minute; their time of culmination in the meridian We have observed quite [accurately] several times over, and from it taken their longitude, or rather their mensuration of the heaven, so that in Our opinion We are sure of them. Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"the Wise,\" the founder of the Kassel court observatory and Tycho's chief astronomical correspondent among the German princes. The full titulature (Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda) is the standard Hessian chancery style; preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Dem Vesten … Vester Lieber Besonder</i> — the princely chancery address-formula; <i>vest</i> (\"steadfast, firm\") is the honorific for an untitled nobleman, and <i>Vester Lieber Besonder</i> is rendered \"Our trusty and well-beloved\" per protocol §5.5. <i>Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — Tycho's hereditary seat at <b>Knudstrup</b> in Scania.</li><li><b>The \"A\" markers in the source.</b> The base text (the 1596 <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> print, as transmitted in the working transcription) carries scattered capital <b>A</b> glyphs at certain word- and line-junctions (e.g. <i>KNUDSTRUP.A</i>, <i>StadthalterA</i>, <i>abgefertigtenA</i>). These are structural/transcription artefacts (line- or column-break markers), not part of the German text; reproduced verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4(2), and silently ignored in the translation.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Henrich Rantzow</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor) of the Danish king in Holstein, humanist and astrological patron, who acted as the intermediary relaying Wilhelm's letter to Tycho. <i>Kön. W. zu Denmarck</i> = \"the King's Majesty of Denmark\" (Frederik II).</li><li><i>WITTICHII</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician who had worked at Uraniborg in 1580 and then at Kassel (1584), carrying Tycho's instrument-design principles to Bürgi; <i>durch angeben des Wittichii</i> = \"through the indications/instructions of Wittich.\"</li><li><i>M. Just Burrjj</i> = <b>Jost Bürgi</b> (1552–1632), Wilhelm's clockmaker and instrument-maker (also \"Joost/Jobst Bürgi\"); <i>Vhrmacher</i> = \"clockmaker.\" <i>qui quasi indagine alter Archimedes</i> — <i>indagine</i> (ablative of <i>indago</i>, \"investigation, ingenuity\") gives \"another Archimedes, as it were, in his ingenuity,\" Wilhelm's famous compliment.</li><li><i>vnser Mathematicus / aulicus Mathematicus</i> — the <b>court mathematician</b>, i.e. <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (named only by office here, not by name); the <i>adiuncta Mathematici Epistola</i> (\"enclosed letter of the mathematician\") is Rothmann's accompanying letter.</li><li><i>Vitellio</i> = <b>Witelo</b> (fl. 1270s), author of the <i>Perspectiva</i> (here called <i>Optica</i>), the standard medieval optical authority; Wilhelm invokes him for the theory of atmospheric <i>refraction</i> that explains the anomalous setting of Venus.</li><li><b>Latin code-switching.</b> The letter constantly switches into Latin for technical nouns and whole clauses; retained italics on first use per protocol §4. The longest fully-Latin stretch (the setting-Venus / <i>primum mobile</i> / Witelo passage) is translated in full and the Latin flagged inline.</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5).</b> <i>Obseruationes</i> → observations; <i>Quadrant / Sextant</i> → quadrant / sextant; <i>Globus</i> → globe; <i>Instrumenta</i> → instruments; <i>Parallaxin</i> (acc. of <i>parallaxis</i>) → parallax; <i>motum … in Longitudinem / Latitudinem</i> → motion in longitude / latitude; <i>Stellae fixae</i> → fixed stars; <i>Refractionem</i> → refraction; <i>Eleuatio Polaris</i> → polar elevation (altitude of the pole); <i>aequatio temporis</i> → equation of time(s); <i>loca vera</i> → true places; <i>primum mobile</i> → kept (the outermost sphere driving the daily rotation). <i>adamussim</i> = \"to the line, exactly to a hair's breadth\" — rendered \"to a hair's exactness.\"</li><li><b><i>quadrupedalia</b></i> — \"four-foot [instruments],\" i.e. of about four feet in dimension; Wilhelm contrasts the modest size of the Kassel quadrant and sextant with Tycho's larger instruments.</li><li><b><i>Mathematicus vnderm Dach</b></i> — literally \"a mathematician under the roof,\" i.e. a purely theoretical/closet astronomer who does not observe; the bracketed gloss \"[a closet astronomer]\" is editorial.</li><li><b>Symbols / glyphs.</b> None present (no planet or zodiac glyphs); celestial bodies are named in words (<i>Solis</i>, <i>Venerem</i>, <i>Cassipeiam</i>).</li><li><b>Scientific substance (for the apparatus record, not interpolated into the translation).</b> This is the celebrated letter in which Wilhelm reports two of the Kassel programme's headline findings: (1) the comet of 1585 (and the 1572 <i>nova ad Cassiopeiam</i>) showed <b>no parallax</b>, refuting the Aristotelian <i>fundamentum philosophicum</i> that comets are sublunary atmospheric exhalations; and (2) the <b>fixed-star latitudes</b> have shifted by up to half a degree from the received <i>Tables</i> — Wilhelm leaves open whether this is <i>vitio Typographorum</i> (printers' error) or a real <i>proper motion</i>, and refers the question to Tycho. (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, identifies this as the origin of the fixed-star-latitude question in the printed correspondence.) These points are rendered faithfully from the text; the contextual gloss is not added to the body.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The clauses carrying legal/scientific force are preserved one-for-one: <i>wöllen euch nicht verhalten</i> (\"we will <b>not</b> conceal\"); <i>gar keine Parallaxin … gar keinen motum</i> (<b>no</b> parallax … <b>no</b> motion at all); <i>wil der auch nicht, das … Parallaxin gehabt habe</i> (\"he too will <b>not</b> have it that [the comet] had any parallax\"); <i>lassen wir warlich nicht verachten</i> (\"we truly do <b>not</b> let [the sextant] be despised\"); <i>so subtil nicht suchen</i> (\"<b>not</b> seek it so subtly\"); <i>gar nicht … eintreffen</i> (\"does <b>not</b> at all agree\"); <i>noch zur zeit nicht spargiren</i> (\"<b>not</b> yet … divulge\"); <i>adamussim nicht zu trawen</i> (\"<b>not</b> to be trusted to a hair's exactness\"); <i>nicht wolten verhalten</i> (\"we would <b>not</b> … withhold\"). No negation added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum … Cassel am 14 Aprilis, Anno 86</i> = \"Given at <b>Kassel</b>, 14 April [15]86.\" Wilhelm and the Kassel court used the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b>, as did Tycho; so 14 April 1586 is O.S. The incoming letter it answers is dated <i>den 1 Martij</i> (1 March), likewise O.S. The dataset <i>label</i> records only the place (\"Kassel\"); the header date is taken from the dateline per protocol §4(1). The double subscription line (<i>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN</i> twice) is as transmitted; reproduced.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> German closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VI, pp. 50–51 (dropped by the working transcription); star-names and one adverb disambiguated against Dreyer's facing Latin (VI, p. 53).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German chancery prose and the embedded Latin clauses are rendered securely, and the negations are reproduced one-for-one. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) <i>qui quasi indagine alter Archimedes</i> — <i>indagine</i> is here taken in the sense \"in (his) ingenuity / investigative skill,\" the natural reading of <i>indago</i> in this period, but the bare ablative admits a slightly looser construal (\"by his very searching, a second Archimedes\"); the sense (\"Bürgi is a second Archimedes\") is not in doubt. (2) The scattered editorial-artefact \"A\" glyphs in the base transcription are interpreted as line/column-break markers and ignored in translation; were any to prove a genuine (if corrupt) lexical token, a word could in principle be affected, but none reads as German or Latin in context.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — collation run clean, postscript restored; only non-textual 'A' sigla + an </i>indagine<i> reading whose sense is not in doubt.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so no Dreyer page images exist to collate against; audited the published_english against source_text alone, per the packet's own instruction, and the grade is capped at B regardless of outcome. Read the German/Latin source and the English translation clause by clause: the salutation and titles (Landgrave of Hesse, Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda; Heinrich Rantzau as Statthalter in Holstein), every number (1 March; scarcely over one minute of discrepancy; quadrupedalia = four feet; not a quarter of a minute; nearly a quarter of an hour; two degrees and more elevation; 14 April [15]86), the technical comet/new-star parallax argument, the fixed-star-position discrepancy passage, and the postscript (star identifications Oculus Tauri/Aldebaran, the right shoulder of Orion/Betelgeuse, Canis Minor and Major/Procyon and Sirius) all have clear, unforced counterparts in the English with no added, dropped, or flipped content and no hardened hedges. The one oddity is a bracketed '[accurate]' that already appears inside the source_text itself (an apparent inherited editorial gap-fill, not a translator insertion), which the English simply carries over unchanged — not scored as a defect since it is pre-existing in the source and clearly marked. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found. Note: this letter was published at grade A despite having no Dreyer reference at all, which is itself against the audit protocol (tboo:null caps at B) independent of this letter's apparent textual cleanliness.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15860301001", "next": "brahe-t_15860826001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860301001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870101001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for the comet observations and reports that the Kassel mathematician's observations agree closely with Tycho's after equation of times and longitudes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860414001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860509001", "d": "1586-05-09", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 9 May 1586 [O.S.] / 19 May [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with planet glyphs) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THADDAeUS HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S.</p><p>SERO admodum redditae sunt mihi literae tuae, amicorum optime, nihilo minus fuerunt gratissimae. Sors iniqua fecit, ut neque tuae ad me tempestive afferri, neque meae ad te perferri potuerint. Tuae enim 25. Augusti scriptae mihi demum 18. Martij redditae sunt. Aiebat ille, qui eas attulit, tempestate aëris in itinere fuisse remoratum. Ego vero longe ante, quam tuas accepissem, in ipsis ferijs Nativitatis D. nactus quandam oportunitatem et spem perveniendi isthuc D. SEGURIUM, eius servo et meo populari dederam meas ad te unâ cum meis illis lucubrationibus, quae si habuissent cursum felicem, tuo desiderio tempestive aliqva ex parte satisfactum fuisset. Pervenerant autem meae hinc Francofurtum usque. Et ecce, accidit quidpiam, quod D. SEGURIUS consilium eundi in Daniam mutare coactus fuerit, et meae literae cum lucubrationibus meis illinc sunt ad me remissae, quemadmodum haec omnia intelliges ex meis literis hisce nunc adiunctis, quas illo tempore scripseram. Vides, mi TYCHO, quomodo nostri sensus in unum conspiraverint, modo fortuna favere voluisset optatis nostris, cui iam iam, ne amplius nos eluderet, occurrere ac proprium nuncium ad te mittere volui. Mitto igitur ad te bonum et fidelem iuvenem, qui stipendio a me conductus aliquando in mea domo servivit arte sartoria, cuius est peritus. Is me deliberante de Tabellario ad te ablegando et quaerendo, num aliqui Rostochienses hîc essent, cum nullum invenirem, et tempus laberetur, suam mihi hic iuvenis obtulit operam, iturum se ad te, si modo sufficiens viaticum essem ei daturus. Accepi conditionem, et eius voluntatem sum amplexus libentissime. Dedi viaticum perveniendi isthuc, tu vicissim eidem ut redeat ad me, prospicies et laborem eius compensabis. Affert autem tibi primum Privilegium impressorium, quale petebas, non absque difficultate impetratum, eo quod non soleant eiusmodi Privilegia ulli concedi, nisi prius manuscripto exemplo in Cancellariam deposito. Nonnullos enim impetrasse eiusmodi Privilegia pro certis quibusdam libris, qui nec inchoati quidem fuerunt et prodierunt nunquam. Evici tamen meo studio, ut tibi in genere tale daretur Privilegium, quale alius habet nemo. Tu eo fruere ad commodum Reipublicae literariae. Taxam pro eo dedi in Cancellariam 12½ Fl., quemadmodum ex schedula Taxatoris Privilegio adiuncta cognosces. Affert item tibi meum exemplum TRAPEZUNTIJ PIRCKMANNUS enim Coloniae cum a me librum magno studio impetrasset, spe facta illum suis typis edendi multis diagrammatis deterritus, quod magnos sumtus requirerent, mutavit sententiam, ac tandem librum ad me remisit; qui mihi profecto semper admodum charus fuit. Ac certe voluissem ex animo illum facere communem omnibus studiosis, nec dubitabam omnibus scholis futurum admodum gratum; imo isthoc interprete Studiosi introducti fuissent ad lectionem operum PTOLEMAeI, inque eo potius quam in SACROBUSTI libello ieiuno versati fuissent. Ac si saltem primi duo libri aut tres ederentur, sufficerent scholis. Primus liber, scis, olim fuit a REINHOLDO editus et scholijs lllustratus; is posset TRAPEZUNTIJ commentarijs inseri. Sed tu de editione statues, quod tibi optimum videbitur. Interea utere et fruere meo libro, et, si tibi placuerit, describi cura. Sin displicuerit, remitte per eundem hominem. Mitto etiam tibi libellum de Directionum Significationibus. CARIONIS esse dicitur; quanquam existimabam, te Ratisponae illum descripsisse. Libellus WERNERI de motu Octavae Sphaerae non invenitur amplius venalis. Fuit adiunctus alijs quibusdam WERNERI opusculis, quae iam nusquam apparent. Hisce diebus accepi literas Francofurto a Nobilibus illis Gallis una cum Diario a tuo ELIA in tua Officina edito. Hoc mihi fuit admodum gratum, non tantum quod ab illis esset mihi donatum, sed quod tuum nomen asscriptum conspicerem, ad cuius conspectum statim verberavit animum cogitatio, tuum hunc genuinum esse partum; quem legens ordine videbar teipsum loquentem audire. In quo, si non fallor, dico prudenter factum esse, quod nomen tuum non sis professus, illudque maioribus tuis operibus reserves. Licet, ut ingenue fatear, non est vulgare illud Diarium, neque conferri ullo modo potest vulgatis illis Diarijs, quae isti Diarifices annuatim edere consueverunt. Nam et Cometae proximi, de quo mihi plane constabat nihil, accuratam habet descriptionem; et motus ☉ et ☾ ex tua observatione habet constitutos. Sed vulgus hominum nescit discernere inter aurum et orichalcum. Itaque prudenter eos facere existimo, qui in levioribus istis vulgi iudicio aut nomen suum non profitentur aut suis discipulis illum honorem relinquunt.</p><p>Designatione tuae insulae admodum sum delectatus. Unum eius exemplum reliqui in Cancellaria Caesari monstrandum. Armillae et Sextantis tui picturam sum contemplatus; quam vellem ipsam machinam videre, et si paulo essem iunior, accurrerem ad te.</p><p>Quod tam liberaliter de omnibus mecum communicas, magnas tibi ago gratias. Meas lucubrationes de Nova Stella et Cometis duobus mitto ad te. Quae illis sunt adiuncta, videbis in ipso opere. Si iudicaveris precium operae fore, ut uno volumine cum tuis, quae habes, edantur, omnia tuo iudicio relinquo. Sin putaveris commode edi non posse, aut mea aliena esse a tuis, mea rursum ad me remitte. Nam nuper ad me scripsit magnus quidam et doctus vir, in editionem impensas se facere velle. Sed ego cum tuis mea prodire mallem, quae, ut spero, non sunt futura dissona. Alios scriptores de Cometis proxime praeteritis, praeter illos, quos meae Dialexi seu meis opusculis adiunxi, non habeo. MAeSTLINI de Cometa anni 77 scriptum te vidisse existimo. B. V. et Dom. fratrem tuum meis verbis saluta officiosissime, ac etiam eius collegam, quem hîc secum habuit. Pragae 19. Maij iuxta novum, aut 9 iuxta vetus Calendarium, Anno 1586.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Thaddaeus Hagecius to Tycho Brahe. Greeting.</b></p><p>Your letter was delivered to me very late, best of friends; none the less it was most welcome. Unkind fortune brought it about that neither could yours be conveyed to me in good time, nor mine carried through to you. For yours, written on 25 August, were delivered to me only on 18 March. The man who brought them said he had been held up on the road by foul weather. I, however — long before I had received yours — at the very feast of the Lord's Nativity [Christmas], having got a certain opportunity and a hope that Master Ségur would reach you over there, had given to his servant, a countryman of mine, my letters to you together with those lucubrations of mine; and if these had had a fortunate passage, your wish would have been satisfied in good time, at least in part. My [parcel] had got as far as Frankfurt from here. And lo, something happened whereby Master Ségur was forced to change his plan of going into Denmark, and my letters along with my lucubrations were sent back to me from there — all of which you will understand from this letter of mine, now enclosed, which I had written at that time. You see, my Tycho, how our minds have conspired into one — if only fortune had been willing to favour our wishes; and so now, at once, that she might trick us no longer, I resolved to meet her and to send a messenger of my own to you. I am sending you, then, a good and faithful young man, who, hired by me for wages, served for a time in my house in the tailor's craft, in which he is skilled. While I was deliberating about despatching a courier to you, and was asking whether there were any men of Rostock here — and, finding none, while the time was slipping away — this young man offered me his service, [saying] he would go to you, if only I would give him sufficient travelling-money. I accepted the terms, and embraced his willingness most gladly. I have given him travelling-money to reach you over there; you in turn will see to it that he returns to me, and will recompense his labour. He brings you, first, the printing privilege, such as you were asking for, obtained not without difficulty, in that privileges of this kind are not usually granted to anyone unless a manuscript copy has first been deposited in the Chancery. For some men have obtained privileges of this kind for certain books which were not even begun and never appeared. Yet I prevailed by my efforts that such a privilege be given you in general terms, such as no one else has. Enjoy it for the good of the Republic of Letters. I paid the fee for it into the Chancery, 12½ florins, as you will learn from the assessor's slip attached to the privilege. He brings you likewise my copy of Trapezuntius. For Pirckmann at Cologne, when he had with great eagerness obtained the book from me — a hope being held out that he would publish it in his own type — was frightened off by the many diagrams, because they would require great expense, changed his mind, and at last sent the book back to me; and it has, in truth, always been very dear to me. And assuredly I would from my heart have wished to make it common to all students, nor did I doubt it would be most welcome to all the schools; indeed, with this man as their guide [interpreter], students would have been led on to the reading of the works of Ptolemy, and would have been engaged in that rather than in Sacrobosco's meagre little book. And if at least the first two or three books were published, they would suffice for the schools. The first book, you know, was once published and illustrated with scholia by Reinhold; it could be inserted into Trapezuntius's commentaries. But you will decide about the edition as shall seem best to you. Meanwhile use and enjoy my book, and, if it please you, have it copied. But if it does not please you, send it back by the same man. I am also sending you a little book on the Significations of Directions. It is said to be Carion's — though I had supposed that you had copied it out at Regensburg. Werner's little book on the motion of the eighth sphere is no longer to be found for sale. It was attached to certain other of Werner's small works, which now appear nowhere. In these days I received a letter from Frankfurt from those French noblemen, together with the Diary published by your Elias at your workshop. This was most welcome to me, not only because it had been given to me by them, but because I caught sight of your name appended to it; and at the sight of it the thought at once struck my mind that this was your own genuine offspring; and reading it through in order, I seemed to hear you yourself speaking. In which — if I am not mistaken — I say it was prudently done that you did not declare your name, but reserve it for your greater works. Although, to confess frankly, that Diary is no common one, nor can it in any way be compared with those common Diaries which those diary-mongers are wont to publish year by year. For it has an accurate description of the recent comet, of which I knew plainly nothing; and it has the motions of the ☉ Sun and ☾ Moon established from your observation. But the common run of men knows not how to tell gold from brass. And so I judge that those men act prudently who, in those lighter matters [left] to the judgement of the crowd, either do not declare their own name or leave that honour to their pupils.</p><p>I was greatly delighted with the map of your island. I have left one copy of it in the Chancery, to be shown to the Emperor. I have studied the picture of your armillary and sextant; how I wish I might see the machine itself, and were I a little younger, I would hasten to you.</p><p>For sharing everything with me so generously, I render you great thanks. I am sending you my lucubrations on the New Star and on the two comets. What is attached to them you will see in the work itself. If you shall judge it worth the trouble that they be published in one volume with your own [writings] which you have, I leave it all to your judgement. But if you shall think they cannot conveniently be published, or that mine are foreign to yours, send mine back to me again. For lately a certain great and learned man wrote to me that he was willing to bear the expenses for the edition. But I would rather that mine came out together with yours, which, I hope, will not prove discordant. Of other writers on the comets just past, besides those whom I have appended to my Dialexis, that is, to my small works, I have none. I suppose you have seen Maestlin's writing on the comet of the year [15]77. Greet B. V. and your lord brother in my words most dutifully, and also his colleague, whom he had here with him. At Prague, 19 May by the new, or 9 [May] by the old Calendar, in the year 1586.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Prague physician, astronomer, and mathematician; personal physician at the imperial court (Maximilian II, then Rudolf II) and Tycho's most important Bohemian correspondent and conduit to the Imperial Chancery. His 1574 <i>Dialexis</i> on the 1572 nova is the work referred to below.</li><li><b><i>D. SEGURIUS</b></i> — <b>Master Ségur</b>: Jacques de Ségur-Pardaillan, diplomat of Henri de Navarre (the future Henri IV), engaged in a Protestant-alliance legation through northern Europe in 1585–86. Hagecius had entrusted an earlier packet to his servant in the hope it would reach Tycho via Ségur's planned journey \"into Denmark,\" which was then abandoned.</li><li><b><i>Privilegium impressorium</b></i> — a <b>printing privilege</b> (imperial copyright), here granted <i>in genere</i> (\"in general terms,\" covering Tycho's works as a class rather than a single named book), which Hagecius stresses is exceptional; the Chancery fee was <b>12½ florins</b> (<i>Fl.</i>). <i>Taxator</i> = the fee-assessor; <i>schedula</i> = the assessment slip.</li><li><b><i>TRAPEZUNTIJ … exemplum</b></i> — <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius, 1395–1486) and his Latin commentary/translation of Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i>; Hagecius is lending Tycho his own copy. <i>PIRCKMANNUS Coloniae</i> = a printer/publisher <b>Pirckmann at Cologne</b> who had considered printing it but balked at the cost of the many diagrams (<i>diagrammata</i>). <i>Interpres</i> is rendered \"guide/interpreter\" — the printed Trapezuntius would introduce students to Ptolemy.</li><li><b><i>SACROBUSTI libello ieiuno</b></i> — the \"meagre little book\" of <b>Sacrobosco</b> (Johannes de Sacrobosco, <i>De sphaera</i>), the standard elementary university text Hagecius hopes a printed Ptolemy would displace.</li><li><b><i>REINHOLDO … editus</b></i> — <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b> (1511–1553), Wittenberg astronomer, who had edited and annotated the first book [of the <i>Almagest</i>]; Hagecius proposes it could be combined with Trapezuntius's commentaries.</li><li><b><i>libellum de Directionum Significationibus … CARIONIS</b></i> — a little book on <b>the significations of [astrological] directions</b> (<i>directio</i> = a predictive astrological technique, glossary §5.3), attributed to <b>Johann Carion</b> (1499–1537), court astrologer at Brandenburg; Hagecius notes Tycho may instead have transcribed it himself at <b>Regensburg</b> (<i>Ratisbona</i>).</li><li><b><i>WERNERI de motu Octavae Sphaerae</b></i> — <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg (1468–1522), <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (1522), the tract on the motion of the eighth (fixed-star) sphere — i.e. on precession (Copernicus had criticised it); Hagecius reports it can no longer be bought.</li><li><b><i>Diario … a tuo ELIA in tua Officina edito</b></i> — the <b>Diary</b> (almanac/ephemeris-calendar) issued at Tycho's own Uraniborg workshop (<i>Officina</i>) under the name of <b>Elias Olsen Morsing</b> (<i>Elias</i>), Tycho's assistant; published anonymously of Tycho. The \"recent comet\" with an accurate description and the ☉/☾ positions \"established from your observation\" is the <b>comet of 1585</b>, treated in Morsing's <i>Diarium astrologicum et meteorologicum</i> (Uraniborg, 1586).</li><li><b><i>Nobiles Galli</b></i> — \"those French noblemen,\" the same French (Navarrese) party connected to the Ségur legation, from whom Hagecius received both the letter and the Diary.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b> (glyphs kept in the Source block, named in the translation per protocol §5.3).</li><li><b><i>Designatio tuae insulae</b></i> — the <b>map/plan of your island</b> (Hven), one copy of which Hagecius left in the Chancery to be shown to the <b>Emperor</b> (Rudolf II). <i>Armillae et Sextantis tui pictura</i> — the engraved <b>picture of your armillary and sextant</b> (glossary §5.2: <i>armillae</i> = armillary, <i>sextans</i> = sextant); <i>machina</i> = the instrument itself, rendered \"machine.\"</li><li><b><i>Meas lucubrationes de Nova Stella et Cometis duobus</b></i> — Hagecius's own studies on the <b>New Star</b> (the 1572 nova; his <i>Dialexis</i>, named below) and on <b>two comets</b>, which he offers for joint publication with Tycho's works.</li><li><b><i>MAeSTLINI de Cometa anni 77</b></i> — <b>Michael Maestlin</b>'s tract on the <b>comet of 1577</b> (<i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae aetherei</i>, 1578).</li><li><b><i>B. V. et Dom. fratrem tuum</b></i> — the greeting to \"<b>B. V.</b>\" (initials unexpanded in the text — a person Tycho and Hagecius both know; left as given, not guessed) and to \"your lord brother and his colleague whom he had here with him.\" <b>Crux flagged:</b> the referent of <i>B. V.</i> is not determinable from the letter; the brother is unnamed. Rendered literally, not resolved.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>\"Pragae 19. Maij iuxta novum, aut 9 iuxta vetus Calendarium, Anno 1586\"</i> = \"At Prague, 19 May by the new, or 9 [May] by the old Calendar, 1586.\" Hagecius, in Catholic Imperial Prague, gives <b>both styles explicitly</b>: <b>19 May New Style = 9 May Old Style</b> (the 10-day Gregorian gap, protocol §3). The slug and the project's catalogue date use the O.S. <b>9 May</b>; the header leads with the as-written date and records both. No silent conversion is made — both are present in the source.</li><li><b>In-letter date data.</b> The two transit dates inside the letter (<i>\"Tuae enim 25. Augusti scriptae mihi demum 18. Martij redditae sunt\"</i> — written 25 Aug., delivered 18 Mar.) are reproduced as written; no style is assigned to them in the source and none is imposed here.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> Recorded as given in the dataset: <i>A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</i>. <b>Note:</b> this shelfmark is identical to the witness cited for the 1571 Wolf letter (brahe-t_15711113001), which is suspect — the dataset may carry a duplicated/erroneous folio reference. Flagged, not corrected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical, bibliographic, and calendrical content (the dual-dated dateline, the privilege, the Trapezuntius/Reinhold/Werner/Carion book-traffic, the anonymous Morsing <i>Diarium</i>) is identified with confidence. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: the unexpanded salutation <b>B. V.</b> (referent undetermined) and the suspect MS shelfmark (apparently duplicated from the Wolf letter).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.102-104 as page images (slice pp.102-105, letter ends p.104; p.105 begins the next letter, 1 July 1586, confirming the boundary). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against source_text (exact match throughout) and checked every proper name, sum, and date against the published English: 25 Augusti/18 Martij, D. Segurius, 12½ Fl., Trapezuntij/Pirckmannus/Coloniae, Reinholdo, Sacrobusti, Carionis, Ratisponae, Werneri (x2), Elia, Maestlini/Cometa anni 77, and the closing double date 19 Maij n.s. / 9 Maij v.s. 1586. All render correctly with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text divergence from Dreyer.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15851213001", "next": "brahe-t_15860701001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15850825001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15860701001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the mutual delay: Tycho's August letter arrived only in March, and Hagecius's lucubrations were returned from Frankfurt after the Navarrese ambassador changed his route.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860509001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860701001", "d": "1586-07-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 1 July 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE D. D. THADDAeO HAGECIO.<br>S.</p><p>BINAS a te uno eodemque tempore per hunc, quem ad me ablegasti, Tabellarium recepi literas, clariss. D. D. THADDAeE, amice perpetuo colende, quae quam mihi fuerint acceptae, vix dici potest, tum quod a te, tanto viro tamque amico sincero constantique, missae forent, tum etiam quod rerum, quarum desiderio magnopere flagrabam me compotem ubertim reddiderint. Ex prioribus circa Solstitium hybernum praeteriti anni ad me datis intelligo, te tunc temporis Dialexin tuam locupletius auctam et recognitam unâ cum aliorum quorundam de eadem materia scriptis ad me misisse, idque per Serenissimi Regis Navarrae Legati servum, tuum conterraneum, qui ob remoratum Domini sui ad nos adventum et librum et literas tibi remiserat. Cum posterioribus vero praeter Privilegium Caesareanum mihi in Typographicis concessum et librum eundem unâ cum TRAPEZUNTIJ Commentarijs in PTOLEMAeI Almagestum libellumque CARIONIS de Directionum Significationibus, additis quibusdam alijs a te elaboratis opusculis, perlibenter accepi. Pro Privilegio autem Caesareo, quod tanta fide et diligentia impetrasti, summas tibi habeo gratias. Praestitisti sane ea in re officium amici minime fucati; daboque operam, quae in Privilegio continentur, a me sartâ tectâ fide praestentur. Taxam 12½ flor. a te in Cancellaria pro eo expositam Tabellario tibi restituendam tradidi, et viaticum, quo ad te revertatur, adiunxi, proque labore suo ipsum remuneravi. TRAPEZUNTIJ in PTOLEMAeUM commentarios, quos adeo benevole mecum communicas, interea reservabo, donec apud PLANTINUM, celebrem illum Antverpiensem Typographum, periculum fecero, an is velit eos Typis suis exprimere. Si annuerit, illi mittam, fietque id cum tui nominis, prout decet, mentione honorifica, quod Astronomiae Studiosis tam insignem Thesaurum non invideris. Interea aliud exemplar describi curabo, ne, si forte Archetypum aliquo casu interciderit, tantis TRAPEZUNTIJ laboribus penitus privemur. Quam primum vero responsum a PLANTINO accepero, te de his certiorem reddam. Si vero negocium non successerit, commentarios illos tibi citra omne dispendium restitui curabo. Quantum ad tuas lucubrationes attinet, de Nova Stella et duobus Cometis unâ cum materijs similibus hisce annexis, nondum ea omnia plene, quamvis id avide percuperem, mihi perlegere licuit, eo quod Tabellarius hic ad me accesserit ijs ipsis diebus, quibus Comitia Regni nostri in vicina Haffnia celebrarentur, unde factum est, ut circa id tempus vel mihi eo ad consanguineos et amicos nobiles, qui e toto Regno confertim illuc confluxerant, excurrendum fuerit, vel etiam ex illis nonnulli me hîc, finitis ijsdem Comitijs, inviserint. Cumque nuntius nunc reditum ad te urgeat nec ulterius a me detinendus sit, eo quod ob causas antedictas, tum etiam ob Sereniss. Reginae nostrae, me in hac Uranoburgi Insula clementer invisentis, accessum supervenientem praeter opinionem diutius hîc substiterit, ideo ut per otium commodum librum tuum perlegam, mecum pauculo tempore eum retinebo, id quod bona tua venia fieri posse, vel ex proprijs tuis literis mihi persuadeo. Remittam illum vel per proprium quendam servum natione Bohemum, Mechanicum meum Opificem, qui in patriam reditum intra paucas hebdomadas a me, ut suos revisat, impetravit, postmodum denuo huc reversurus. Quod si haec ratio minus commode successerit, in Nundinis Autumnalibus Francofurtum ad Bibliopolam et Typographum illum Gallum, de quo mihi significas, tibi restituendum transferri curabo. Interim vero apud modo dictum PLANTINUM Antverpiensem de tuo etiam hoc libro per literas expiscabor, an eum suis sumtibus edere non gravetur, tibique ipsius voluntatem postea significabo. Haud sane illibenter ipsemet per propriam nostram Typographiam tuum hunc foetum publicum redderem vel seorsim per se vel nostris in simili materia adiungendo, veluti a me expetis, sed obstat, quod meum opus de Novis mundi Aetherei generationibus nostro aevo conspectis in iustam per se excrescit magnitudinem, ita ut non patiatur ipsa libri quantitas, tam prolixas lucubrationes adiungi, nisi in monstrosum et immodicam dilataretur formam. Nam et ipsemet hanc ob causam cogor in quibusdam brevior esse, quam alias vellem. Verendum enim alioquin, ne non saltem liber sua crassitudine modum excedat, sed et papyrus, quam in his ultimis Nundinis Francofurtensibus huic negotio comparavi, nequaquam operi sufficiat. Ad haec ante hyemem otium mihi vix editioni operam dandi suppetit, eo quod aestivo tempore alijs negotijs per amicorum et nobilium conversationem utplurimum distrahar, idque praesertim nunc ob Illustrissimi Ducis Mechelburgensis ULDARICI soceri Regij ad arcem Cronenburgam recens in propinquo Selandiae littore magnifice extructam indies expectatum adventum, qui etiam, quantum ex Regina filia intellexi, et nostras aedes accedere ante suum e Dania discessum non [de]dignabitur. Cumque non paucas hebdomadas apud Sereniss. Regem nostrum unâ cum alijs quibusdam Germaniae Principibus commorabitur, haud dubie ob nobilium et eruditorum virorum, qui eos comitabuntur, crebrum interea, uti fieri solet, ad me confluxum, liberiore otio citra diuturni temporis iacturam vix perfruar, adeo ut ante hybernum tempus impressioni rerum nostrarum (ut dixi) vacare minime liceat. Et quia non parum deinde temporis requiritur ad absolvendam nostri operis editionem, nimis tarde fortassis tuis votis satisfecero, si postea demum tuos labores seorsim hic excudi curavero. Qua in re papyrus etiam impressoria, quae difficillime hisce temporibus ob tumultus in Regnis et locis a nobis occidentalibus obortis, unde in copia alias adferri solet, non minimum obstaculi ingeret. Consultius itaque mihi videtur, si PLANTINUS editionem detrectaverit, librum ad te remittere, ut eius viri, de quo scribis, impensis publicae utilitati communicetur. Nam quantum ego hactenus eum obiter saltem ob otij importunitatem percurrendo cognoscere potui, dignus sane videtur, qui cum nominis tui memoria posteritati consecretur. Et licet a meis vix diversa contineat, vellem tamen, ut parcius de nostris, quo ad Novam Stellam attinet, ageres. Erant enim ea, quae olim de hac re a me conscripta sunt, admodum ieiuna et immatura, quae, Deo volente, absolutiora et castigatiora in hoc alio opere invenies. Id etiam monere volui, quod percurrendo ea, quae ex mea Novae illius Stellae contemplatione summatim excerpsisti, reperiam, te in quodam loco modeste satis insinuare, quod declinationem illius stellae diversimode in differentia 4 scrupulorum intra paucas lineas admiserim. Etsi vero hunc lapsum pro tuo candore, eo quod differentia tantilla pro nullo reputari debeat, excuses, nolui tamen me in numerationem Astronomicam vel in minimis impingere. Quare admirabundus libellum meum protinus revidebam, invenique te per festinationem procul dubio nimiam incuria quadam legisse in posteriori loco 62 gr. 2 m., cum libellus noster habeat 62 g. minus 2 min., id quod indicat, desiderari duo minuta ad completionem 62 grad., idque, uti scis, idem plane est, quod prius dixeram, declinationem eius stellae fuisse 61° 58'; nolebam enim ijsdem verbis eadem repetere. Tuum itaque erit in hoc loco nostri scripti animadversionem, si id publicaveris, quo ad hanc brevem ac incuriam a teipso facile commissam revocare; ubi totum librum diligentius perlegero, si quid mihi occurrerit admonitione dignum, sincere et amice te de singulis commonefaciam. Id saltem generaliter nunc indicabo, praeterquam quod rationes inveniendi parallaxes Cometarum, quas tum ex alijs, tum a teipso inventas in medium proponis, talia data utplurimum requirant, quae inter observandum Cometas rarissime et difficulter offerantur, ut ipsemet multoties expertus sum. Accedit et hoc (quod maioris momenti est), omnes illas parallaxium investigationes, quas liber tuus continet, ne ijs quidem exceptis, quas e REGIOMONTANI tractatulo explicas, Cometam omni motu proprio destitui, et non nisi raptu primi mobilis convolvi, praesupponant, id quod in nullo hactenus Cometarum locum meruit, in sola illa Nova ad Cassiopaeam Stella competebat. Cumque motus proprius Cometarum saepenumero maior sit ea diversitate, quae per parallaxes, si quam habeant, insinuatur, nullatenus, non considerato motu hoc proprio, parallaxium differentiae per ullum eorum problematum, quae adducis, (quantum hactenus perspexi) manifestantur. Haec vero ea de causa neutiquam indico, quod putem, teipsum ista non considerasse, sed ut operam des, quo ea potissimum discentibus proponantur, quae in ipsa ... quam commodissime citra omne obstaculum administrari queant. Porro cum ex literis tuis intelligam, Diarium illud huius Anni a Studioso meo ELIA editum ad tuas manus pervenisse, et inter alia non displicuisse, quae in eo de Cometa proxime viso referuntur, mitto tibi paulo exactiorem et copiosiorem eiusdem Cometae descriptionem, qualem cum Illustriss. Principe Domino GUILIELMO Landtgravio Hassiae nuper communicavi, veluti ipsius Celsit. per literas ad Dn. HENRICUM RANZOVIUM, Vicarium Regium in Holsatia horum studiorum amantiss. a me fieri expetierat. Sed ut eo rectius occasionem omnem huius negotij intelligas, adiunxi exemplar Epistolae eiusdem Illustrissimi Principis Landgravij ad praedictum RANZOVIUM, mihi ab hoc transmissum, tum etiam exemplar mearum ad ipsius Illustriss. Celsitudinem de hoc negotio et alijs Astronomicis rebus, tibi lectu non ingratis futuris, perscriptarum literarum unâ cum ipsius Celsitud. ad me dato responso, adiunctis insuper literis CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI, ipsius Celsit. Mathematici, et scripto eiusdem de eodem Cometa, quod tibi eo nomine exarari curavi. Percipies ex his omnibus proculdubio plurima, quae tuis desiderijs arridebunt. Instrumentorum speciem meorum et quorundam, quae ad observationes exactius, quam hactenus factitatum est, caelitus pervestigandas attinent, ex meis ad principem Landgravium literis non sine voluptate cognosces. Consensum vero inter observationes Cometae Landgravianas et nostras ex literis ipsius Domini Landgravij et Mathematici scripto non citra admirationem intelliges. Peperit autem hanc convenientiam Instrumentorum ad quorundam ex meis similitudinem a Landgravio instituta restauratio, prout WITTICHIUS illi formam, divisionem et pinnacidia hîc apud me explorata non aliter, ac si ipsemet eorum inventor esset, mei nulla facta mentione propalavit, quo nomine etiam aurea catena ab ipso Principe donatus est. Accidit tamen illi, quod plerunque dolose agentibus evenire solet, ut pinnacidiorum quadruplicium undique parallelorum exactam rationem non indicaverit, sive quod ipsi exciderit, sive quod paucioribus rem absolvere praesumserit, unde Landgravij observatores in diuturni temporis et laboris iacturam implicuit, donec ipsa experientia, quae desiderabantur, ipsimet animadvertissent, velut ex Mathematici illius Epistola copiosius intelliges. Agit sane minus sincere mecum WITTICHIUS (quem ego hîc tum ob tuam commendationem, tum ob proprium ipsius ingenium in precio habui, pluraque, quam forte opus fuisset, concredidi) quod mea sine meo consensu sub suo nomine publica faciat, non quod alijs mea inventa invideam, sed quia ipsemet de Mechanica Astronomiae parte peculiari opere tractare et multorum diversorumque organorum structuram posteris communicare decrevi. Quam itaque ab alijs gratiam pro tot arduis laboribus et sumtibus incredibilibus non immerito, vel te iudice, reportare debebam, iste, quantum in ipso est, praeripere sibique, alienis plumis se instar corniculae AeSOPICAe exornando, attribuere conatur. Sed valeant haec cum caeteris, quae a perverso ingratoque mundo enormiter astuteque fieri consueverunt. Commode tamen hoc accidit, ut ea, quae tunc temporis WITTICHIUS apud me vidit alijsque pro suis postea venditavit, nullam obtineant comparationem ad haec, quae postmodum in hoc genere a me elaborata sunt, adeo ut priora nunc ipsemet parvifaciam et pro antiquatis seponam, quemadmodum haec latius cognosces, ubi opus illud nostrum, cuius antea memini, quod Mechanicam Astronomiae partem luculenter tractabit, in publicum prodierit. In quem etiam usum instrumenta mea omnia artificiose delineata aeneis laminis insculpi procuro. Ubi vero non saltem haec, sed et alias nostras pro Astronomia instauranda lucubrationes illumque de Aetherei mundi novis Generationibus librum, quem nunc prae manibus habeo, typis nostris, Deo conatus fortunante, publicavero, te eorum, quem etiam arbitrum praecipuum (utpote harum rerum inter paucos apprime intelligentem) in his eligo, compotem reddere non intermittam. Sed iam demum animadverto, me, dum non sine voluptate diutius tecum colloquor, Epistolij limites transgredi. Quapropter nunc pedem retraho, manumque e tabula tollens te Deo Opt. Max. cum tota familia commendo. Is te quam diutissime conservet incolumem et felicem. Vale nostramque amicitiam, siquidem praesens corpore (velut olim Ratisbonae) non licet, animo saltem (quod facis) absens conserva, eamque per Literas data quacunque opportunitate continua, quod et ipse vicissim praestare nequaquam intermittam. Resalutat te frater meus STENO BRAHE officiosissime. Iterum quam felicissime vale et plura a me, ubi librum tuum remisero, expecta. Datae Uraniburgi Calendis Iulij stylo veteri Anno 1586.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe sends greeting to Master Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>I have received two letters from you at one and the same time, by the courier whom you despatched to me, most renowned Master Thaddaeus, friend ever to be honoured; and how welcome they were to me can scarcely be said — both because they were sent by you, so great a man and so sincere and constant a friend, and also because they have abundantly put me in possession of things for which I was greatly burning with desire. From the earlier letter, sent to me about the winter solstice of last year, I understand that you had then sent me your <i>Dialexis</i>, more fully augmented and revised, together with certain writings of others on the same subject, and that [you sent it] by the servant of the Ambassador of the Most Serene King of Navarre, your fellow-countryman, who, on account of his master's delayed arrival here among us, had sent both the book and the letter back to you. But with the later letter I most gladly received, besides the Imperial Privilege granted to me in matters of printing, that same book together with the <i>Commentaries</i> of Trapezuntius on Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i>, and Carion's little book <i>On the Significations of Directions</i>, with certain other small works wrought by you added. For the Imperial Privilege, which you obtained with such fidelity and diligence, I owe you the greatest thanks. You have truly performed in that matter the office of a friend in no way feigned; and I shall take care that what is contained in the Privilege be discharged by me with faith kept whole and entire. The fee of 12½ florins which you laid out for it in the Chancery I handed over to the courier to be repaid to you, and I added travel-money for his return journey to you, and rewarded him for his labour. The commentaries of Trapezuntius on Ptolemy, which you so kindly share with me, I shall keep meanwhile, until I have made trial with Plantin, that celebrated printer of Antwerp, whether he be willing to set them in his type. If he assents, I shall send them to him, and it shall be done — as is fitting — with honourable mention of your name, in that you did not begrudge so notable a Treasure to the students of Astronomy. Meanwhile I shall have another copy transcribed, lest, if by chance the archetype should be lost by some mishap, we be utterly deprived of Trapezuntius's so great labours. And as soon as I have a reply from Plantin, I shall inform you of these matters. But if the business does not succeed, I shall see that those commentaries are returned to you without any expense to you.</p><p>As regards your own lucubrations, on the New Star and the two Comets together with similar matters annexed here, it has not yet been permitted me to read through all of it fully — much though I eagerly longed to do so — because this courier reached me in those very days when the Diet of our Kingdom was being held in neighbouring Copenhagen; whence it came about that around that time I had either to make an excursion thither to my kinsmen and noble friends, who had thronged together there in a crowd from the whole Kingdom, or else some of them, the Diet being ended, visited me here. And since the messenger now presses for his return to you and is not to be detained by me any longer — because, for the aforesaid reasons, and also on account of the imminent visit of our Most Serene Queen, who graciously visits me on this Island of Uraniborg, he has stayed here longer than expected — therefore, that I may read your book through at convenient leisure, I shall keep it with me for a little while, which I persuade myself may be done with your good leave, even from your own letter. I shall send it back either by a certain servant of mine, a Bohemian by nation, my mechanic-craftsman, who has obtained from me leave to return to his homeland within a few weeks, to see his own people, intending afterwards to return here once more. But if this means should succeed less conveniently, I shall see that it is conveyed back to you at the Autumn Fair at Frankfurt, to the bookseller and that French printer of whom you write to me. Meanwhile, however, I shall fish out by letter from the same Plantin of Antwerp, regarding this book of yours too, whether he would not object to publishing it at his own expense, and afterwards make his will known to you. I would by no means unwillingly make this offspring of yours public myself through our own printing-house — either separately on its own, or by adjoining it to my own [works] on a similar subject, as you ask of me — but it stands in the way that my work <i>On the New Generations in the Aethereal World seen in our age</i> swells by itself to a just magnitude, so that the very size of the book does not allow such lengthy lucubrations to be adjoined, unless it were dilated into a monstrous and immoderate form. For I myself am compelled for this reason to be briefer on certain points than I should otherwise wish. For otherwise it is to be feared not only that the book may exceed due measure by its thickness, but also that the paper which I procured for this business at the last Frankfurt Fair may by no means suffice for the work. Besides this, before winter I have scarcely leisure to devote to the edition, because in summertime I am for the most part distracted by other business through the company of friends and noblemen, and especially now on account of the daily-expected arrival of the Most Illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg, Ulrich, the King's father-in-law, at the castle of Kronborg, lately built in magnificent fashion on the nearby shore of Zealand — who also, as far as I have understood from the Queen his daughter, will not disdain to come to my house too before his departure from Denmark. And since he will stay not a few weeks with our Most Serene King, together with certain other Princes of Germany, doubtless on account of the frequent concourse to me meanwhile (as is wont to happen) of noble and learned men who will accompany them, I shall scarcely enjoy freer leisure without the loss of long time, so that before wintertime it is by no means permitted me (as I said) to be at leisure for the printing of our matters. And since no little time is then required to complete the edition of our work, I shall perhaps satisfy your wishes too late, if only afterwards I see to your labours being printed here separately. In which matter the printing-paper too — which is most difficult [to obtain] in these times, on account of the tumults that have arisen in the Kingdoms and places to the west of us, whence it is usually brought in abundance — will throw up no small obstacle. It therefore seems to me more advisable, if Plantin should decline the edition, to return the book to you, that it may be communicated to the public benefit at the expense of that man of whom you write. For so far as I have hitherto been able to know it, by running through it only cursorily on account of the importunity of [scant] leisure, it truly seems worthy to be consecrated to posterity together with the memory of your name. And although it contains scarcely anything different from my own [findings], yet I could wish that you would deal more sparingly with my matters, as regards the New Star. For those things which were once written by me on this subject were exceedingly meagre and immature, which — God willing — you will find more complete and more corrected in this other work [of mine]. This too I wished to point out: that, in running through the things which you have summarily excerpted from my contemplation of that New Star, I find that you in a certain place modestly enough hint that I admitted the declination of that star differently, with a discrepancy of 4 minutes, within a few lines. But although you excuse this slip, out of your candour, on the ground that so tiny a difference ought to be reckoned as nothing, yet I was unwilling to stumble in Astronomical reckoning even in the smallest matters. Wherefore, in astonishment, I straightway re-examined my little book, and found that you — through doubtless excessive haste, by a certain carelessness — had read in the latter place 62 degrees 2 minutes, whereas our little book has 62 degrees <i>minus</i> 2 minutes, which indicates that two minutes are wanting to the completion of 62 degrees; and this, as you know, is exactly the same as what I had said before, that the declination of that star was 61° 58′; for I did not wish to repeat the same things in the same words. It will therefore be for you, in this place of our writing, to recall [correct] this brief carelessness, easily committed by yourself, if you publish it; and when I have read the whole book more diligently, if anything occurs to me worthy of remark, I shall sincerely and as a friend remind you of each point. This at least I shall now indicate in general: besides the fact that the methods of finding the parallaxes of Comets, which you set forth — both as found by others and by yourself — for the most part require such data as, in observing Comets, are very rarely and with difficulty available, as I myself have many times found by experience. There is added this too (which is of greater moment): that all those investigations of parallax which your book contains — not even excepting those which you explain from Regiomontanus's treatise — presuppose the Comet to be devoid of all proper motion, and to be wheeled around only by the rapture of the <i>primum mobile</i>; which has hitherto held good in no Comet, [and] applied to that New Star near Cassiopeia alone. And since the proper motion of Comets is very often greater than that diversity which is suggested by parallaxes (if they have any), in no way — the proper motion not being taken into account — are the differences of parallax made manifest by any of those problems which you adduce (so far as I have hitherto perceived). But I by no means indicate these things for the reason that I think you yourself did not consider them, but that you may take care that those things especially be set before learners which … can be carried out in [it] as conveniently as possible without any obstacle.</p><p>Further, since I understand from your letters that the <i>Diary</i> of this Year, published by my student Elias, has come into your hands, and that, among other things, the matters which are reported in it concerning the lately-seen Comet have not displeased you, I send you a somewhat more exact and fuller description of the same Comet, such as I lately shared with the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse — as His Highness had requested be done by me, by letter to Master Heinrich Rantzau, Royal Vicegerent in Holstein, a most ardent lover of these studies. But that you may the more rightly understand the whole occasion of this business, I have adjoined a copy of the Letter of the same Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave to the aforesaid Rantzau, transmitted to me by the latter; then also a copy of my own letter, written out to His Most Illustrious Highness concerning this business and other Astronomical matters that will not be unwelcome for you to read, together with His Highness's reply given to me; with the letters of Christoph Rothmann, His Highness's Mathematician, added besides, and a writing of the same on the same Comet, which I had drawn up for you under that title. From all these you will doubtless gather very much that will smile upon your desires. You will learn, not without pleasure, the appearance of my instruments, and of certain ones which pertain to investigating observations from the heavens more exactly than has hitherto been done, from my letters to the Prince Landgrave. And the agreement between the Landgravian observations of the Comet and our own you will learn, not without admiration, from the letters of the Lord Landgrave himself and the writing of his Mathematician. Now this agreement was brought about by the restoration of Instruments undertaken by the Landgrave after the likeness of certain of mine — inasmuch as Wittich divulged to him the form, the division, and the sights [<i>pinnacidia</i>] explored here at my house, no otherwise than as if he himself were their inventor, with no mention made of me; for which reason he was even presented with a golden chain by the Prince himself. Yet it befell him — as is wont to happen to those who act deceitfully — that he did not indicate the exact principle of the fourfold sights parallel on every side, whether because it slipped his memory, or because he presumed to accomplish the matter with fewer [than were needed]; whence he involved the Landgrave's observers in a loss of long time and labour, until they themselves should perceive by experience itself the things that were wanting, as you will learn more fully from that Mathematician's Letter. Wittich indeed acts less than sincerely with me (whom I here held in esteem, both on account of your recommendation and on account of his own talent, and to whom I entrusted more, perhaps, than there was need) in that he makes my [inventions] public under his own name without my consent — not because I begrudge my inventions to others, but because I myself have resolved to treat the Mechanical part of Astronomy in a special work, and to communicate to posterity the construction of many and diverse instruments. The thanks, therefore, which I ought deservedly to have carried off from others for so many arduous labours and incredible expenses — even by your judgement — that man, so far as in him lies, tries to snatch beforehand and to attribute to himself, adorning himself with others' feathers like the little crow of Aesop. But farewell to these things, together with the rest that the perverse and ungrateful world is wont to do outrageously and craftily. Yet this falls out conveniently, that the things which Wittich at that time saw at my house and afterwards peddled to others as his own bear no comparison to these which I have since wrought in this kind, so that I now myself set little store by the former and lay them aside as outdated — as you will learn more amply when that work of ours, which I mentioned before, which will treat the Mechanical part of Astronomy lucidly, comes forth into public. For which use also I am having all my instruments, artfully delineated, engraved on copper plates. But when I shall have published — by our own type, God prospering the endeavour — not only these, but also our other lucubrations for the restoration of Astronomy, and that book <i>On the new Generations of the Aethereal world</i> which I now have in hand, I shall not fail to put you in possession of them — you whom I also choose, in these matters, as chief arbiter (being, among the few, one most thoroughly understanding of these things). But now at last I notice that, while I converse with you at greater length, not without pleasure, I am overstepping the bounds of a brief letter. Wherefore I now draw back my foot, and, lifting my hand from the tablet, commend you, with your whole household, to God, Best and Greatest. May He keep you safe and happy as long as possible. Farewell, and preserve our friendship — since [we may] not [enjoy it] present in body (as once at Regensburg), yet at least in mind, absent (as you do) — and continue it by Letters at whatever opportunity is given, which I in turn shall by no means fail to do. My brother Sten Brahe returns your greeting most dutifully. Again, fare you most happily, and expect more from me when I shall have sent your book back. Given at Uraniborg on the Kalends of July, Old Style, the year 1586.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and personal physician to Emperor Rudolf II — Tycho's most important Central-European scientific correspondent, who later (1599) helped bring Tycho to the Imperial court. The two had met in person at the Imperial Diet of <b>Regensburg</b> in 1575 (alluded to: <i>velut olim Ratisbonae</i>).</li><li><b><i>Dialexis</b></i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574), his treatise on the 1572 New Star; here the revised/augmented version sent to Tycho.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Trapezuntius</i> = <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius, 1396–1486/72), whose Latin commentary on Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> Hagecius lent in manuscript; Tycho hoped Plantin would print it. <i>Carion</i> = <b>Johann Carion</b> (1499–1537), astrologer; <i>De Directionum Significationibus</i> his astrological tract on directions. <i>Plantinus</i> = <b>Christophe Plantin</b> (c. 1520–1589), the great Antwerp printer. <i>Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) of Wrocław, who stayed with Tycho on Hven in 1580 and afterward, the letter charges, passed off Tycho's instrument-designs as his own at Kassel. <i>Guilielmus Landtgravius Hassiae</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the observing prince. <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician. <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (Vicegerent) in Holstein and patron of learning, the intermediary of the Tycho–Kassel correspondence. <i>Dux Mechelburgensis Uldaricus</i> = <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg</b> (1527–1603), father of Queen Sophie. <i>Steno Brahe</i> = <b>Sten Brahe</b> (1547–1620), Tycho's brother. <i>Studiosus meus Elias</i> = <b>Elias Olsen Morsing</b> (d. 1590), Tycho's assistant, who published the <i>Diarium astrologicum et meteorologicum</i> for 1586 with the description of the 1585 comet. The <i>Regina nostra</i> / <i>Regina filia</i> is <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b>, queen of Denmark.</li><li><b>Places / institutions.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven); <i>Haffnia</i> → <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Cronenburga</i> → <b>Kronborg</b> castle, then newly rebuilt; <i>Antverpia</i> → Antwerp; <i>Francofurtum</i> / <i>Nundinae Autumnales</i> → the Frankfurt Autumn book-fair; <i>Ratisbona</i> → Regensburg.</li><li><b>Privilege &amp; fee.</b> <i>Privilegium Caesareanum … in Typographicis</i> = the <b>Imperial printing privilege</b> Hagecius secured for Tycho from Rudolf II's chancery; the <i>Taxa 12½ flor.</i> is the chancery fee (12½ florins/guilders) Hagecius advanced and Tycho repaid via the courier.</li><li><b><i>opus de Novis mundi Aetherei generationibus</b></i> — Tycho's planned <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the comet volume, eventually 1588); here still in preparation, with paper bought at Frankfurt and copperplates of the instruments being engraved.</li><li><b>Astronomy / cometary parallax.</b> <i>parallaxes Cometarum</i> → <b>parallaxes of comets</b>; <i>motus proprius</i> → <b>proper motion</b>; <i>raptu primi mobilis convolvi</i> → \"wheeled around by the rapture of the <i>primum mobile</i>\" (the daily diurnal carrying-motion of the outermost sphere). Tycho's substantive objection: Hagecius's parallax methods (including those drawn from <b>Regiomontanus</b>, <i>De cometae … problemata</i>) assume a comet has <i>no</i> proper motion and moves only with the <i>primum mobile</i> — true only of the fixed New Star of 1572 in Cassiopeia, not of comets, whose proper motion swamps any parallactic shift. The hedges (<i>quantum hactenus perspexi</i>, <i>si quam habeant</i>) are preserved.</li><li><b>The declination crux (62° <i>minus</i> 2′).</b> Tycho corrects Hagecius's reading of his New-Star declination: Hagecius read <b>\"62 gr. 2 m.\"</b> (62°02′), but Tycho's <i>libellus</i> (the 1573 <i>De nova stella</i>) reads <b>\"62 g. minus 2 min.\"</b> = 62° − 2′ = <b>61° 58′</b>. The \"discrepancy of 4 minutes\" (<i>differentia 4 scrupulorum</i>) Hagecius noted is thus a misreading of <i>minus</i>, not a genuine inconsistency. <i>Scrupulum</i> = minute of arc; rendered \"minute.\" Numerals reproduced exactly as printed (62 gr. 2 m. / 62 g. minus 2 min. / 61° 58′).</li><li><b><i>pinnacidia</b></i> → <b>sights</b> (the slit-sight devices on Tycho's instruments), per glossary §5.2 (<i>pinnacidium, pinnula</i> → sight, pinnule). <i>pinnacidiorum quadruplicium undique parallelorum</i> → \"the fourfold sights parallel on every side\" — Tycho's improved four-edged parallel-slit sights, the detail Wittich failed to convey to the Kassel observers. The Wittich/golden-chain episode is corroborated at Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, citing TBOO VI, 89.29–34 and VII, 68.21–25.</li><li><b><i>corniculae Aesopicae</b></i> — \"the little crow of Aesop,\" the jackdaw/crow that decked itself in others' (the peacock's) feathers (Aesop, <i>Phaedrus</i> I.3); the proverbial figure for plagiarism. Kept as the fable-allusion.</li><li><b><i>organorum structuram … Mechanicam Astronomiae partem</b></i> — the projected <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (instrument-book, eventually 1598); <i>Astronomia instauranda</i> = \"the restoration of Astronomy,\" Tycho's programme (glossary §5.1).</li><li><b><i>arbiter praecipuus</b></i> — \"chief arbiter\": Tycho names Hagecius the principal judge of his forthcoming work, <i>utpote harum rerum inter paucos apprime intelligentem</i> (\"one, among the few, most thoroughly understanding of these things\").</li><li><b>Textual notes.</b> <i>non [de]dignabitur</i> — the printed text reads <i>non dedignabitur</i> (\"will not disdain\"); the <i>de-</i> is bracketed following the edition (the litotes \"will not disdain\" = \"will deign,\" intended sense). At <i>quae in ipsa ... quam commodissime</i> the edition marks a <b>lacuna</b> (the noun governed by <i>in ipsa</i> is wanting); rendered \"in [it]\" with the gap flagged, not smoothed. <i>Epistolij</i> (diminutive of <i>epistola</i>) = \"a brief letter / note.\" <i>manum e tabula tollere</i> — proverbial (\"to lift the hand from the tablet,\" i.e. to know when to stop), here translated literally with the idiom signalled.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi Calendis Iulij stylo veteri Anno 1586</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg on the <b>Kalends of July</b> (= 1 July), <b>Old Style</b>, 1586.\" Tycho explicitly marks <i>stylo veteri</i> (Julian/O.S.) — apt, since Hagecius in Imperial service used the New Style. The catalogue label gives only the place (Uraniborg); the as-written dateline date (1 July 1586 O.S.) governs the header, consistent with it. No in-letter date discrepancy. The earlier <i>Solstitium hybernum praeteriti anni</i> (\"the winter solstice of last year\") dates Hagecius's first letter to ~December 1585.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> A single MS witness is given (Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66); the printed text-of-record is the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> volume (= Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.107. Corrected the ungrammatical 'tractatio' to Dreyer's printed ablative 'tractatulo' ('quas e REGIOMONTANI tractatulo explicas'); no change to the English, which already rendered the correct sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout; register, hedges, the declination correction (61° 58′ from \"62 minus 2′\"), and the Wittich-plagiarism polemic are all firm. The single genuine crux is the <b>editorial lacuna</b> at <i>\"quae in ipsa … quam commodissime citra omne obstaculum administrari queant\"</i>, where the noun governed by <i>in ipsa</i> is lost in the transmission; it is flagged, not conjecturally filled. Minor: the bracketed <i>[de]</i> in <i>non [de]dignabitur</i> follows the edition.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.105-110 as page images in two chunks (pages=\"1-4\", then \"5-6\") and collated the full letter line-by-line against source_text and published_english, including the opening/closing formulas and every proper name, number, date and sum (12½ flor.; 4 scrupulorum; 62 gr. 2 m. / 62 g. minus 2 min. / 61°58'; Calendis Iulij, Anno 1586; Thaddaeus Hagecius, Trapezuntius, Plantin, Carion, Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Kronborg/Cronenburgam, Regiomontanus, the New Star near Cassiopeia, Elias, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Heinrich Rantzau, Christoph Rothmann, Wittich, Sten Brahe, Regensburg/Ratisbonae, Uraniborg). One TEXT_ERROR found (tractatio vs Dreyer's tractatulo); no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found anywhere in the letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the nominative 'tractatio', which cannot follow the preposition 'e' (ablative); Dreyer prints the ablative 'tractatulo' (\"little treatise\"), the grammatically required and actually-printed word. The published English is unaffected by the corruption (it renders the sense of 'tractatulo' either way), but the source_text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>quas e REGIOMONTANI tractatulo explicas (TBOO VII, p. 107, l. 39-40)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified directly against page images of Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.105-108 (of the 6-page slice pp.105-110; the defect site on p.107 was covered). Dreyer's printed 'tractatulo' matches the audit's `dreyer` field exactly. No defect was rejected. Nothing outside this single defect site was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15860509001", "next": "brahe-t_15860907001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860509001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15860907001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges simultaneous receipt of two letters, Hagecius's revised Dialexis, and the Imperial printing privilege, and discusses the contents of these items.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860701001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860818001", "d": "1586-08-18", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young — Uraniborg, 18 August 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>EX quo Magnificentia tua, nuper mihi Helsingorae praesenti, se ante reditum in patriam me in hac tenella insula certo certius invisuram, promittere non gravabatur, cumque omne promissum (veluti communiter fertur) in debitum abeat, idcirco non saltem obnixe petere, sed confidentius etiam exorare non dubito, ut T. M. me crastina luce (quam ipsa etiam commodam auram allaturam non obscure clara pollicentur astra) in hisce Uraniae dicatis aedibus invisere dedignari nolit; erit sane T. M. non saltem Serenissimi et potentissimi Regis sui nomine, sed etiam proprio, cum Musis sit addictissima, Uraniae Musarum sorori non postremae longe acceptissima, cuius etiam praesentiam tum ERICUS LANGIUS, tum etiam FALCHO GOIE, viri nobiles et T. M. studiosissimi ambo, mecum sic avide praestolantur. Da itaque operam, ut votis nostris exoptatus respondeat eventus. Vale. Datae Uraniburgi 18 Augusti 1586.</p><p>Tuae Magnificentiae studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Peter Young.</b></p><p>Since Your Magnificence, lately when present with me at Helsingør, did not hesitate to promise that, before returning to your homeland, you would visit me more surely than surely upon this tender little island — and since every promise (as the common saying goes) passes into a debt — I do not, therefore, hesitate not merely to entreat you most earnestly, but even more confidently to beg, that Your Magnificence may not disdain to visit me tomorrow by daylight (which the stars too, clear and unobscured, promise will bring fair weather of its own) in this dwelling consecrated to Urania; and Your Magnificence will assuredly be most welcome by far — not only in the name of your own most serene and most mighty King, but in your own right as well, since you are most devoted to the Muses — to Urania, not the least of the Muses' sisters; and your presence too is so eagerly awaited, together with me, by both Erik Lange and Falk Gøye, noblemen and both most devoted to Your Magnificence. Take pains, then, that the longed-for outcome may answer to our wishes. Farewell. Given at Uraniborg, 18 August 1586.</p><p>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from his observatory at Uraniborg on Hven.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petro Iunio</i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (Petrus Junius, 1544–1628), the Scottish humanist and diplomat, former tutor (with George Buchanan) to <b>James VI of Scotland</b>, and the king's almoner and librarian. In the summer of 1586 he was in Denmark on a diplomatic mission; the <i>Serenissimus et potentissimus Rex suus</i> (\"his own most serene and most mighty King\") of the letter is James VI. Tycho's <i>Magnificentia tua / T. M. (Tua Magnificentia)</i> honorific — \"Your Magnificence\" — suits an ambassadorial visitor.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>ERICUS LANGIUS</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), the Danish nobleman and alchemist, Tycho's friend (later betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie); <i>FALCHO GOIE</i> = <b>Falk Gøye</b> (1545–1594), Danish nobleman of Tycho's circle. Both are present at Uraniborg and join in awaiting Young; <i>viri nobiles … studiosissimi ambo</i> = \"noblemen and both most devoted [to you].\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Uraniburgi / Uraniae dicatis aedibus</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> (\"dwelling consecrated to Urania,\" §5.7); <i>Helsingorae</i> = <b>Helsingør</b> (Elsinore), where the prior meeting occurred. <i>hac tenella insula</i> = \"this tender little island,\" i.e. Hven — a self-deprecating diminutive (<i>tenella</i>, \"delicate, dainty\").</li><li><b>Allusion / idiom.</b> <i>omne promissum … in debitum abeat</i> — \"every promise passes into a debt\": a proverbial commonplace (cf. <i>promissum cadit in debitum</i>) by which Tycho courteously presses Young to make good his pledge; rendered as a saying, with <i>veluti communiter fertur</i> (\"as the common saying goes\") preserved.</li><li><b><i>certo certius</b></i> — \"more surely than surely,\" an emphatic figura etymologica; kept as the redundant superlative rather than flattened to \"quite certainly.\"</li><li><b><i>Uraniae Musarum sorori non postremae</b></i> — \"to Urania, not the least of the Muses' sisters\": Urania, Muse of astronomy, here personifies Tycho's science; the litotes <i>non postremae</i> (\"not the least\") is preserved as understatement.</li><li><b>Astrology / meteorology.</b> <i>commodam auram allaturam non obscure clara pollicentur astra</i> — \"the stars, clear and unobscured, promise [the day] will bring fair weather\": a light astrometeorological flourish (Tycho kept a meteorological diary at Uraniborg); <i>aura</i> here = \"weather/breeze,\" not \"air\" in the abstract. No technical term from the controlled glossary occurs; the prediction is a courteous forecast, not a formal <i>iudicium</i>.</li><li><b><i>crastina luce</b></i> — \"tomorrow by daylight\": fixes the invitation to the following day, 19 August 1586 (O.S.); the visit is daytime (\"by the light\").</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> Heading <i>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE</i> = \"Tycho Brahe to Peter Young\" (dative addressee + nominative sender, standard for the <i>Opera Omnia</i> register). <i>Tuae Magnificentiae studiosissimus</i> = \"Most devoted to Your Magnificence\" (cf. §5.5).</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 18 Augusti 1586</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 18 August 1586\" (Old Style; Denmark used the Julian calendar). The catalogue date (label: Uraniborg) and the as-written dateline agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a short, idiomatically clear courtesy-letter; persons, place, and date are all secured, and no reading is in doubt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII p.110, where it is printed complete (a following Tycho-to-Hagecius letter, dated 25 Jan 1587, begins immediately after on the same page, confirming the boundary). Latin matches source_text exactly. The invitation to Peter Young, the weather-omen from the stars, the naming of Erik Lange and Falk Gøye, and the date (Uraniborg, 18 August 1586) are all rendered faithfully, including the correct resolution of 'cuius praesentiam' to Young's own presence rather than to Urania's. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77."], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15901027001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reminds Young of his promise to visit Uraniborg and invites him to come the following day, noting that Erik Lange and Falk Gøye also await his arrival.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860818001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860826001", "d": "1586-08-26", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 26 August 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery German, with Latin code-switch) · Edition of record: Tycho Brahe,<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA/ ETC.</p><p>UNSERN günstigen Gruß zuuor/ Vester Lieber Besonder/ Wir machen vns keinen zweiffel/ jhr werdet vnser Antwortlich schreiben/ so wir den 14 verschienes Monats Aprilis, aus vnserer Vestung allhier an Euch gethan/ numehr beneben den vberschickten Obseruationibus vor lengst empfangen/ vnd vnsere meinung darab vernohmen haben. Dieweil vns aber biß noch vnserm gnedigen begeren nach/ darauff kein antwort zukommen/ Vnd vns dann diese gelegene Bottschafft zu euch vorgestossen/ So haben wir nicht vmbgehen können/ Euch hiermit voriges vnsers schreibens gnediglich zu erinnern/ Vnd ist demnach an Euch vnser gnediges begeren/ weil Vnsere vnd Ewere Obseruationes, so viel den negst erschienen Cometam, auch Stellam nouam ad Cassiopeiam antrifft/ gar nahe zusammen treffen/ Ihr wollet vns nicht allein Ewer Iudicium vber vnsere Obseruationes Stellarum fixarum hinwider zuschicken/ Sondern auch Ewere Obseruationes, was jhr so woll dabeuor/ als auch darsieder von Stellis fixis obseruit, communiciren, darmit wir sie gegen den vnsern zu conferiren, Sonderlich aber wollet vns Altitudinem vestri Poli/ wie jhr die bey Euch habt/ zuschicken/ Vnd werdet jhr aus beygefügten vnsers Mathematici schreiben/ vnsere meinung weiter vernehmen. Wolten wir Euch also gnediglich nicht verhalten/ vnnd seind Euch mit gnedigen guten Willen wollgeneigt/</p><p>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassell am 26 Augusti, Anno 1586.<br>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Trusty and well-beloved: we make no doubt that you have by now long since received our letter in reply, which we sent to you from our fortress here on the 14th of the past month of April, together with the <i>observations</i> [<i>Observationibus</i>] forwarded [with it], and have learned our opinion thereon. But since, in accordance with our gracious desire, no answer to it has as yet come to us, and since this convenient messenger to you now presents itself, we could not forbear graciously to remind you herewith of our previous letter. And it is accordingly our gracious desire of you that — since our and your <i>observations</i>, so far as concerns the lately-appeared <i>comet</i> [<i>Cometam</i>], and also the <i>new star toward Cassiopeia</i> [<i>Stellam novam ad Cassiopeiam</i>], agree very closely together — you would not only send us back your <i>judgment</i> [<i>Iudicium</i>] upon our <i>observations of the fixed stars</i> [<i>Observationes Stellarum fixarum</i>], but also communicate to us your <i>observations</i> [<i>Observationes</i>] — whatever you have observed of the <i>fixed stars</i> [<i>Stellis fixis</i>] both before and since — so that we [may compare them] [<i>conferiren</i>] against our own; but above all, that you would send us the <i>altitude of your pole</i> [<i>Altitudinem vestri Poli</i>] as you have it with you. And you will learn our opinion further from the enclosed letter of our <i>mathematician</i> [<i>Mathematici</i>]. This we would graciously not withhold from you, and are well disposed toward you with gracious good will.</p><p>Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, the 26th of August, in the year 1586.<br>WILHELM, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Wilhelm</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"Wilhelm the Wise,\" a practising astronomer who maintained an observatory at Kassel staffed by the mathematician Christoph Rothmann and the instrument-maker Jost Bürgi. This is an early item in the Hven–Kassel correspondence that Tycho printed himself as the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596). The princely <i>Wir/Euch</i> register and the full titulature are preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>DEM VESTEN VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN … HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP</i> — \"to our trusty and well-beloved … Lord of Knudstrup.\" <i>Vester</i> (= <i>fester</i>) is the chancery honorific for a nobleman (\"steadfast, trusty\"); <i>Lieber Besonder</i> is rendered per the glossary formula (§5.5) \"trusty and well-beloved.\" Knudstrup = the Brahe family seat in Scania.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>Unsern günstigen Gruß zuvor</i> — a variant of the princely address-formula; rendered \"Our gracious greeting beforehand\" (cf. §5.5 <i>Unsern gnedigen Gruß zuvor</i>). <i>günstig</i> (\"favourable, gracious\") is here treated as equivalent to <i>gnedig</i>.</li><li><b>The April letter.</b> Wilhelm refers to his own reply of <b>14 April 1586</b>, sent with a set of observations, to which Tycho had not yet responded. The date is reproduced as written (\"the 14th of the past month of April\"); not converted (O.S.).</li><li><b>Latin code-switch (technical nouns).</b> The German body switches into Latin for the scientific lexicon, a feature kept visible per protocol §2 and §5: <i>Observationibus</i> / <i>Observationes</i> → \"observations\"; <i>Cometam</i> → \"comet\" (the comet of 1585); <i>Stellam novam ad Cassiopeiam</i> → \"new star toward Cassiopeia\" (the supernova of 1572, SN 1572 — <i>stella nova</i>, here rendered literally \"new star,\" not modernised); <i>Iudicium</i> → \"judgment\" (an opinion/assessment); <i>Observationes Stellarum fixarum</i> / <i>Stellis fixis</i> → \"observations of the fixed stars\" / \"fixed stars\"; <i>Altitudinem vestri Poli</i> → \"the altitude of your pole\" (the elevation of the celestial pole at Hven = its geographical latitude; <i>altitudo</i> → \"altitude\" per §5.1); <i>Mathematici</i> → \"mathematician\" (Wilhelm's court mathematician, <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, §5.6, whose own accompanying letter is the \"enclosed letter\"). These Latin forms are italicised and the source word given in brackets on first occurrence per §4/§5.</li><li><b>Verbs of comparison.</b> <i>conferiren</i> (Latinate German infinitive, from <i>conferre</i>) → \"compare\"; rendered in brackets \"[may compare them]\" because the German clause <i>darmit wir sie gegen den vnsern zu conferiren</i> lacks an explicit finite verb of obtaining (literally \"so that we, to compare them against ours\"). The supplied \"[may compare]\" is bracketed as an editorial completion of the elliptical purpose-clause; cf. Mosley's reading (\"so that we may compare them with ours,\" <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, p. citing Wilhelm to Tycho, 26 August 1586). <i>communiciren</i> → \"communicate.\"</li><li><b><i>dabevor … darsieder</b></i> — \"before … since\" (<i>dabevor</i> = <i>zuvor/davor</i>; <i>darsieder</i> = <i>seither/seitdem</i>), i.e. observations made both prior to and subsequent to some prior reference point.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum … am 26 Augusti, Anno 1586</i> = \"Given … the 26th of August, in the year 1586.\" Hesse-Kassel, Protestant, kept the <b>Julian calendar</b> in 1586 (the Protestant German states did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700); the dateline is therefore Old Style. The dataset <i>label</i> \"Kassel\" is the place, not a date; the as-written dateline 26 August 1586 governs the header, with no discrepancy to flag. <i>Datum</i> (\"Given\") corresponds to the Danish <i>Actum</i> formula (§5.5).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German and the Latin code-switches are rendered securely and corroborated by an independent published translation of the same passage (Mosley). The one soft point is the elliptical purpose-clause <i>darmit wir sie gegen den vnsern zu conferiren</i>, which has no finite main verb in the source; the completing \"[may compare them]\" is bracketed rather than smoothed in.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole point is a finite-verb-less purpose clause, bracketed and Mosley-corroborated; no uncertain reading/date/identification.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice available); audited against source_text alone per the brief's rule, so grade is capped at B regardless of quality. Internal comparison of the German source against the English finds the translation faithful throughout -- every clause, the proper nouns (comet, new star at Cassiopeia), and the date (26 August 1586) are correctly rendered. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found. Note the self-assigned published_grade of A cannot stand uncollated; capped at B as instructed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15860414001", "next": "brahe-t_15861014001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860301001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870101001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Follows up on his unanswered April letter, notes that comet and nova-near-Cassiopeia observations agree closely, and requests Tycho's judgment on the Kassel observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860826001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860907001", "d": "1586-09-07", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 7 September 1589 [N.S.] (catalogued 1586)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.81</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>URANIBURGI SEU IN HYENA INSULA.<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISS. D. TYCHO, Domine et amice observande. Post discessum nobilis illius viri vestratis, qui falcones Imperatori nostro a Rege vestro attulerat, measque ad te literas perferre debuerat, binas ad te dedi literas, easque opera mei amici per Dresdam curavi ad te perferendas; eas an acceperis, vehementer scire desidero, non quod magni alicuius momenti essent, sed ut cognosceres non mea culpa, sed iniqua sorte quadam accidere, quod meae tibi vel tardius reddantur, vel penitus non perveniant ad te, quam fortunam, profecto, valde defleo. An igitur omnes meas huc usque adeoque librum VERNERI acceperis, magnopere rogo me edoceas. Tuum librum primum avide expecto, alij etiam, praesertim nobiliss. D. IACOBUS CURTIUS, Vicecancell. Imperij, eadem cupiditate expetunt. Etsi autem mihi in ista mea senili aetate (nam ad 1. Octob. si ita Deus volet, magnum climacterem complebo) ob studia medica et alias curas, in Astronomicis studijs versari non conceditur, tamen vehementer oblector, quoties facultas datur saltem inspiciendi aliorum lucubrationes. De Commentarijs TRAPEZUNTIJ quid nunc iudices, scire velim, num putes ex re scholarum fore, si initio saltem duo priores libri ederentur, postea reliqui.</p><p>De rebus Gallicis vos istic melius. En quam nullum est scelus quantumvis horrendum, quod Monachus aggredi non audeat. Dixerim ego Monachos istos et Papistas Catholicos, non a fide sed verius, ut ita dicam, a Catholicitate omnium scelerum nominandos esse. Archid. MAX. hodie Polonicos limites transcensurus esse dicitur. Expectabimus igitur illum huc intra dies non usque adeo multos; Deus faxit, ut sanus redeat ad nos. B. V. Pragae die 7. Septembris novo stylo Anno 1589.</p><p>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, his lord and friend to be esteemed. At Uraniborg, or on the island of Hven. Greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Tycho, my lord and friend to be esteemed. After the departure of that noble man, your countryman, who had brought falcons to our Emperor from your King and was to have carried my letters on to you, I sent you two letters, and arranged through a friend of mine, by way of Dresden, for them to be conveyed to you; whether you have received them I am most eager to know — not because they were of any great moment, but so that you might understand that it is through no fault of mine, but through some unkind fate, that mine [my letters] are delivered to you either too late, or do not reach you at all — a fortune which I do indeed bitterly lament. I therefore greatly beg you to inform me whether you have received all of mine up to now, and so too <b>Werner's</b> book. Your first book I await eagerly; others too, and especially the most noble Master <b>Jacobus Curtius</b>, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, seek it out with the same longing. But although in this my old age (for on 1 October, if God so wills, I shall complete my great climacteric) it is not granted me, on account of my medical studies and other cares, to be engaged in astronomical studies, yet I take vehement delight, as often as the chance is given me, in at least examining the lucubrations of others. On the <i>Commentaries</i> of <b>Trapezuntius</b>, I should like to know what you now judge — whether you think it would be to the schools' advantage if at the outset at least the two former books were published, and afterwards the rest.</p><p>Concerning French affairs, you there [know] better. See how there is no crime, however dreadful, that a Monk does not dare to attempt! For my part, I would say that those Monks and Papist Catholics ought to be named, not from the faith, but more truly — so to speak — from the <i>catholicity</i> [universality] of all crimes. Archduke <b>Maximilian</b> is said to be about to cross the Polish borders today. We shall therefore look for him here within days not so very many; God grant that he return to us safe and sound. Fare you well. At Prague, on the 7th day of September, New Style, in the year 1589.</p><p>Most devoted to you,<br>Thaddaeus [Hagecius].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thad[daeus]</i> = <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, c. 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and personal physician to Emperors Maximilian II and Rudolf II at Prague; Tycho's chief scientific correspondent in the Empire and the man who in 1600 brokered Tycho's introduction to Kepler. His <i>Dialexis de nova stella</i> (1574) had independently argued the 1572 nova was supralunary, aligning him with Tycho.</li><li><b>Addressee / place.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe Dano … Uraniburgi seu in Hyena insula</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, at <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of <b>Hven</b> (<i>Hyena</i> is a Latinised spelling of Hven/Hveen; kept as written and identified here, per protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>nobilis illius viri vestratis, qui falcones Imperatori nostro a Rege vestro attulerat</b></i> — \"that noble countryman of yours who had brought falcons to our Emperor from your King\": a Danish envoy bearing a gift of falcons (a recurring Danish-crown diplomatic present) from <b>Frederik II</b> (or, if late 1588 onward, the regency for the boy <b>Christian IV</b>) to <b>Rudolf II</b>. Used here as a now-failed letter-carrier; the detail is kept literal and the identification flagged as inferential.</li><li><b><i>librum Verneri</b></i> — \"Werner's book\": the treatise of <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg (1468–1522), almost certainly his <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> / the 1522 <i>In hoc opere haec continentur</i> containing the method of <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> and the eighth-sphere motion — a text Tycho is known to have circulated and criticised. Identification of the exact title flagged as probable, not certain.</li><li><b><i>Iacobus Curtius, Vicecancell. Imperii</b></i> — <b>Jacob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor and a mathematically-minded official at the Prague court; a real intermediary between Tycho and the Imperial circle.</li><li><b><i>magnum climacterem</b></i> — \"[my] great climacteric,\" the <b>grand climacteric</b>, the 63rd year (9 × 7), held astrologically the most perilous of life's climacteric years (protocol §5.3, cf. <i>annus climactericus</i>). Hagecius states he will complete it on 1 October [1589]. The translation keeps his own conditional hedge <i>si ita Deus volet</i> (\"if God so wills\") and does not adjust it to any externally-reckoned birth-year.</li><li><b><i>studia medica</b></i> — \"medical studies\": Hagecius was an active court physician, hence his apology that medicine and \"other cares\" keep him from astronomy.</li><li><b><i>lucubrationes</b></i> — \"lucubrations,\" works composed by lamplight / studious nocturnal labours; kept in the period sense.</li><li><b><i>Commentariis Trapezuntii</b></i> — \"the <i>Commentaries</i> of <b>Trapezuntius</b>\": <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius, 1395–1486), whose commentary on Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> was a standard but contested humanist text; Hagecius asks Tycho's editorial judgement on a staged publication (\"the two former books first, the rest afterwards\"). <i>ex re scholarum</i> rendered \"to the schools' advantage.\"</li><li><b><i>De rebus Gallicis … Monachus … scelus … horrendum</b></i> — \"concerning French affairs … a Monk … a dreadful crime\": the assassination of <b>King Henri III of France</b> by the Dominican friar <b>Jacques Clément</b> on 1 August 1589 (Henri died 2 August). The corpus (Strauss, <i>Brahe Iconography</i>; Weber cat. 177) confirms the 1 August 1589 regicide-by-monk. Hagecius's bitter pun — that \"Monks and Papist Catholics\" should be named not <i>a fide</i> (from the faith) but <i>a Catholicitate omnium scelerum</i> (from the <i>catholicity</i> — universality — of all crimes) — turns <i>Catholicus</i> against itself; the wordplay is kept and glossed rather than flattened.</li><li><b><i>Archid. Max. … Polonicos limites transcensurus … sanus redeat</b></i> — \"Archduke <b>Maximilian</b> … about to cross the Polish borders … may he return safe\": <b>Archduke Maximilian III of Austria</b> (the \"Deutschmeister\"), defeated claimant to the Polish throne, taken prisoner at Byczyna (Pitschen) in January 1588 and held by the Poles. His release under the Treaty of Bytom-Będzin (negotiated 1589) is the \"return\" Hagecius anticipates — a further internal anchor to <b>1589</b>, not 1586.</li><li><b><i>B. V.</b></i> — <i>Bene vale</i> = \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>T. studiosiss.</b></i> — <i>Tui studiosissimus</i> = \"Most devoted to you\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> None present in this letter.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> None present in this letter.</li><li><b>DATING CRUX (named).</b> The dateline reads <b>\"Pragae die 7. Septembris novo stylo Anno 1589\"</b> — Prague, 7 September 1589, <b>New Style</b> (Bohemia, under Imperial/Catholic rule, had adopted the Gregorian calendar; Hagecius marks the style explicitly, <i>novo stylo</i>). The dataset slug (`15860907001`) and `label` imply <b>1586</b>. <b>The internal evidence is decisive for 1589, not 1586:</b> (i) the Henri III assassination-by-monk (1 Aug 1589); (ii) Archduke Maximilian's anticipated release/return from Polish captivity (1589, post-Byczyna 1588); (iii) the explicit dateline \"Anno 1589.\" The header therefore leads with the as-written <b>7 September 1589 [N.S.]</b> and appends \"(catalogued 1586)\" per protocol §4.1; the catalogue year is treated as an error, <b>not</b> silently reconciled, and the discrepancy is recorded here. No calendar conversion of the day itself is performed; only the style (N.S.) is flagged.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical allusions are corpus-confirmed. The one residual uncertainty is the <b>1586-vs-1589 catalogue/dateline discrepancy</b> (resolved to 1589 on three converging internal anchors, but flagged as a named crux), together with the inferential identifications of the un-named falcon-bearing envoy and the precise title of \"Werner's book.\"</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read both page images of the slice visually (Dreyer VII pp. 194-195; letter entirely on p. 194). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin. Checked every name (Verneri/Werner, Iacobus Curtius, Trapezuntij, Archid. Max./Maximilian), the numeral (1. Octob. climacterem), and the date '7. Septembris novo stylo Anno 1589' -- note the letter's own text confirms 1589 even though the tekstnet slug catalogues it under 1586, a metadata quirk the title field already flags, not a translation defect. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1589 (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/81", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001764"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.81."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15860701001", "next": "brahe-t_15870125001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860701001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870125001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks Tycho to confirm receipt of earlier letters and Werner's book, and notes that Jacobus Curtius, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, eagerly awaits Tycho's first publication.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860907001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15860907002", "d": "1586-09-07", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 26 August 1586 [O.S.] (catalogued 7 September 1586)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, EQVITI DANO, DOMINO A KNVDSTRVP, MATHEMATICO NOSTRO SECVLO PRAeSTANTISSIMO, ETC. DOMINO AC FAUTORI SUO VNICE COLENDO, ETC. S. P.</p><p>NON dubito, Nobilissime Domine, Mathematicorum praestantissime, quin in recenti adhuc habeas memoria, quae in proximis nundinis Francofordiensibus mandato Illustrissimi Principis nostri transmittendo aliquot Obseruationes ad T. Excell. perscripserim, et vt a T. Excell., etiam atque etiam petierim, vt his nostris Literis respondere dignaretur. At cum nullum adhuc a T. Excell. viderimus responsum, valde dubitat Illustriss. Princeps noster, num eae ad manus tuas peruenerint. Mandauerat enim Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudo, vt non tantum suae Celsitudinis clementem salutationem, verumetiam ingens tuae responsionis videndae desiderium per ministrum tuum tibi significarem. Quapropter cum nullus Dies intercedat, quin de T. Excell. responsione ab Illustrissimo principe nostro interroger, eiusque intermissionem semper a disiunctione locorum excusare cogar, peto a T. Excell. adhuc maiorem in modum, ne responsionem in Salutem et promotionem Astronomiae diutius intermittat. Ipse quidem, quibuscunque possum rationibus, Astronomiam Illustrissimo Principi nostro etiam atque etiam commendare non desino: dummodo T. Excell. mihi in subsidium veniat, atque currentem et bene admonitum acrioribus calcaribus instiget. Acer et ad palmae per se cursurus honores: Si tamen horteris, fortius ibit equus. De Altitudinibus Stellarum Meridianis a me per Quadrantem obseruatis, inuenio Quadranti non iuste secundum Horizontis planum a Wittichio et Horologiario nostro perpendicula esse applicata, sed ostendi ab eo Altitudines Stellarum iusto et quidem integro minuto maiores, quam reuera sint: Quod licet toties Illustriss. Principi nostro ab eo tempore, quo hîc Mathematicum ago, dixerim, credere tamen noluit, donec nuper defectionem tam Horologiario, quam Illustriss. ipsius Cels. ad oculum ostenderem. Nec dubito, quin tu quoque eundem errorem in nostris Obseruationibus iamdudum animaduerteris. Iteratis igitur Obseruationibus inuenio correctis perpendiculis Eleuationem Poli huius loci ex Stella Polari 51 grad. 19 min. vno scilicet minuto minorem. Ad summam, non dubito, quin pro tuo erga Astronomiam singulari Amore Nobilissimam hanc Scientiam etiam atque etiam apud Illustrissimum Principem nostrum sis commendaturus. Scis hanc Artem omnino Regiam esse, ac Regum ac Principum fauorem desiderare. Plura scriberem, praesertim de impuro illo nebulone Nicolao Raymaro Vrso Dithmarso, qui superiori Hyeme apud T. Excell. Typographicam Literarum collectionem et ordinationem, vt opinor, exercuit, quomodo scilicet hic te conuicijs proscindere non desierit, et quomodo te defenderim: nisi angustia temporis me impediret. Cum nuper in praesentia Illustrissimi Principis JOANNIS CASIMIRI Ducis Palatini, etc. Itemque, Inclyti Domini a Donaw, tui honorificam, vt par erat, facerem mentionem: dici vix potest, quomodo Dominus a Donaw te commendârit, adeo vt me iam pridem tui amore flagrantem iam totum inflammârit.</p><p>His igitur bene et feliciter vale, optime TYCHO, et de Astronomia bene mereri ne desine.</p><p>Cassellis 7 Calend. Septembris, Anno 86.</p><p>T. T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS Bernburgensis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, Danish knight, Lord of Knudstrup, the most outstanding mathematician of our age, etc., his lord and patron singularly to be honoured, etc. — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I do not doubt, most noble Lord, most outstanding of mathematicians, that you still hold in fresh memory how, at the last Frankfurt fair, by the command of our Most Illustrious Prince, I wrote out to Your Excellency several observations to be conveyed [to you], and how again and again I begged of Your Excellency that he would deign to answer this letter of ours. But since we have as yet seen no reply from Your Excellency, our Most Illustrious Prince greatly doubts whether they reached your hands. For his Most Illustrious Highness had charged me to signify to you, through your servant, not only his Highness's gracious greeting, but also a vast desire to see your reply. Wherefore, since not a day passes but I am questioned by our Most Illustrious Prince concerning Your Excellency's reply, and am always forced to excuse the delay of it by the separation of our places, I ask of Your Excellency yet more earnestly that he not put off any longer a reply for the welfare and advancement of astronomy. For my own part, by whatever arguments I can, I do not cease to commend astronomy again and again to our Most Illustrious Prince — provided that Your Excellency come to my aid, and goad on with sharper spurs one who is already running and well-admonished. The keen [horse] will of itself run on to the honours of the palm; yet if you urge him, the horse will go the more strongly. As to the meridian altitudes of the stars observed by me with the quadrant, I find that the plumb-lines were not rightly applied to the quadrant along the plane of the horizon by Wittich and our clock-maker, but that it [the quadrant] showed the altitudes of the stars greater by a full minute, indeed an entire minute, than they truly are: which, although I told our Most Illustrious Prince so many times from the time when I have been acting as mathematician here, he was nevertheless unwilling to believe, until lately I showed the fault to the clock-maker and to his Most Illustrious Highness before their very eyes. Nor do I doubt that you too have long since noticed the same error in our observations. Therefore, with the observations repeated and the plumb-lines corrected, I find the elevation of the pole of this place, from the Pole Star, to be 51 degrees 19 minutes — that is, less by one minute. In sum, I do not doubt that, out of your singular love toward astronomy, you will commend this most noble science again and again to our Most Illustrious Prince. You know that this art is altogether a royal one, and craves the favour of kings and princes. I would write more — especially about that foul scoundrel Nicolaus Raymarus Ursus Dithmarsus, who last winter, as I suppose, practised the typographical collection and arrangement of letters [i.e. typesetting] at Your Excellency's [house], [and about] how indeed he has not ceased to slash at you here with insults, and how I defended you — were not the shortness of time hindering me. When lately, in the presence of the Most Illustrious Prince Johann Casimir, Duke Palatine, etc., and likewise of the renowned Lord von Dohna, I made honourable mention of you, as was fitting, it can scarcely be told how the Lord von Dohna commended you, so that he wholly inflamed me, already long since burning with love of you.</p><p>With this, then, farewell — well and happily — best Tycho, and do not cease to deserve well of astronomy.</p><p>Kassel, the 7th [day before] the Kalends of September [26 August], in the year [15]86.</p><p>Yours truly,<br>Christoph Rothmann of Bernburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus Bernburgensis</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), of Bernburg in Anhalt; mathematician and astronomer to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6). This is one of the Rothmann–Tycho letters Tycho himself published in the 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I) printed at Uraniborg — the edition of record for this letter.</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Equiti Dano, Domino a Knudstrup</i> = \"Danish knight, Lord of Knudstrup\" (Tycho's family seat in Scania). <i>Mathematico nostro seculo praestantissimo</i> = \"the most outstanding mathematician of our age.\" <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting,\" protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>\"Illustrissimus Princeps noster.\"</b> Throughout, \"our Most Illustrious Prince\" / \"his Highness\" = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (Rothmann's employer), not named in the body. <i>Celsitudo</i> = \"Highness\" per §5.5.</li><li><b>\"the last Frankfurt fair.\"</b> <i>proximis nundinis Francofordiensibus</i> — the Frankfurt book fair (spring or autumn), the standard conduit for scholarly correspondence and observations between the Kassel and Hven circles.</li><li><b>Equestrian proverb.</b> <i>currentem … acrioribus calcaribus instiget … Si tamen horteris, fortius ibit equus</i> — \"spur on one already running … yet if you urge [him], the horse will go the more strongly\": a classical commonplace (cf. <i>currentem incitare</i>, \"to spur the willing horse\"; Ovid, <i>Ex Ponto</i> II.6.38 <i>fortius ut moveant, calcar habere volunt</i>). Rothmann casts himself as the eager horse needing only Tycho's encouragement. Kept as the running metaphor; <i>ad palmae … honores</i> = \"to the honours of the palm [of victory].\"</li><li><b>Instrument / observation.</b> <i>Altitudines Stellarum Meridianae … per Quadrantem</i> = \"meridian altitudes of the stars … with the quadrant\" (§5.1–5.2). <i>perpendicula</i> = the <b>plumb-lines</b> by which the quadrant's vertical was set; mis-set \"along the plane of the horizon,\" they introduced a systematic error of a full arc-minute. <i>Eleuatio Poli … ex Stella Polari</i> = \"elevation of the pole … from the Pole Star,\" i.e. the observed latitude of Kassel, corrected to <b>51° 19′</b> (one minute less than before). <i>ad oculum</i> = \"before their very eyes / demonstrably.\" <i>Horologiarius</i> rendered \"clock-maker\" (the court instrument-mechanic).</li><li><b>\"Wittich.\"</b> <i>a Wittichio</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician who had visited both Hven (1580) and Kassel; here blamed (with the clock-maker) for the faulty plumb-line setting. Tycho's later quarrel over priority for the geo-heliocentric arrangement involved Wittich and Ursus; Rothmann names him neutrally as the prior installer.</li><li><b>\"Ursus.\"</b> <i>Nicolaus Raymarus Ursus Dithmarsus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>, of Dithmarschen — the future Imperial mathematician and Tycho's bitterest priority-rival. <i>impuro illo nebulone</i> = \"that foul scoundrel\": Rothmann's invective. The clause <i>Typographicam Literarum collectionem et ordinationem … exercuit</i> literally \"practised the typographical collection and arrangement of letters\" = <b>worked as a typesetter/compositor</b> during his stay at Hven the previous winter (1584/85) — a pointed status-jab. <i>te conuicijs proscindere</i> = \"to slash at you with insults.\" This passage is an early datable trace of the Tycho–Ursus feud, from Rothmann's pro-Tycho side.</li><li><b>Other persons.</b> <i>Joannes Casimirus Dux Palatinus</i> = <b>Johann Casimir of the Palatinate-Simmern</b> (1543–1592), Count Palatine, administrator of the Electoral Palatinate. <i>Dominus a Donaw / a Donaw</i> = a <b>Lord (Burgrave) von Dohna</b>, of the prominent Prussian-Silesian noble house (the precise individual is not specified in the letter; likely one of the Burgraves von Dohna active at the Reformed German courts). Both are reported praising Tycho.</li><li><b>Royal-art topos.</b> <i>hanc Artem omnino Regiam esse, ac Regum ac Principum fauorem desiderare</i> = \"this art is altogether a royal one, and craves the favour of kings and princes\" — the standard plea that astronomy needs princely patronage, here urging Tycho to keep the Landgrave engaged.</li><li><b>Sign-off formula.</b> <i>His igitur bene et feliciter vale</i> — <i>vale</i> is rendered with the controlled formula <b>\"farewell\"</b> (protocol §5.5); the adverbial pair <i>bene et feliciter</i> (\"well and happily\") qualifying it is retained alongside, set off with dashes, so that the fixed <i>vale</i> gloss is preserved without dropping Rothmann's doubled adverb.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Tui tibi</i> / <i>Tuus totus</i> (\"Yours truly / wholly yours\"); expanded conventionally.</li><li><b>Printed-edition markers.</b> The working transcription carries scattered superscript letter-anchors (rendered as a stray \"A\" in several places, including a spurious \"A\" splitting the opening <i>S. P.</i> | <i>NON dubito</i>) — these are footnote/marginal-note sigla of the 1596 printed <i>Epistolae</i>, <b>not</b> part of Rothmann's prose; they are silently removed from both the cleaned source block and the translation.</li><li><b>Dateline — calendar crux (flagged, not reconciled).</b> The letter is subscribed <i>Cassellis 7 Calend. Septembris, Anno 86</i>. By the Roman idiom (inclusive counting, with 1 September = <i>Kalendis Septembris</i>), <b>\"7 Kalends of September\" = 26 August 1586</b> (protocol §3, Roman-calendar idiom glossed to civil date). The dataset's metadata and slug, however, label the letter <b>\"7 September 1586\"</b> (whence slug …090700). The two do not agree; per protocol the dateline is reproduced as written and the discrepancy recorded, not silently corrected. The header gives the literal Roman reading with its civil equivalent; the slug-date (7 Sept.) is noted as the editorial/catalogue date. All O.S. (Hesse-Kassel and Denmark kept the Julian calendar).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Cassellis</i> = <b>Kassel</b> (protocol §5.7).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical/observational content (quadrant plumb-line error; latitude of Kassel corrected to 51° 19′) is unambiguous. The single flagged crux is the <b>dateline</b>: <i>7 Calend. Septembris</i> literally yields 26 August 1586, whereas the dataset/slug date the letter 7 September 1586; the discrepancy is recorded, not resolved. A secondary soft point is the unidentified individual <i>Dominus a Donaw</i> (a Burgrave von Dohna, not further specified in the letter).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo is null). Audited published_english against source_text alone: proper names (Wittich, Nicolaus Raymarus Ursus Dithmarsus, Johann Casimir Duke Palatine, von Dohna), the horse proverb ('Acer et ad palmae... fortius ibit equus'), the pole-elevation figure '51 grad. 19 min.', and the date '7 Calend. Septembris, Anno 86' (= 26 August 1586, matching the title's 'catalogued 7 September 1586' cross-reference) all check out; every clause is translated faithfully with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss detected. Grade capped at B per the brief, no Dreyer collation possible.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15850518001", "next": "brahe-t_15870120001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870120001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks why Tycho has not replied to letters sent via the Frankfurt fair and conveys the Landgrave's repeated inquiries about the delayed answer.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15860907002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15861014001", "d": "1586-10-14", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 14 October 1586 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German, with Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(Liber I, Uraniborg 1596) = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed-text letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA/ ETC.</p><p>UNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuuor/ Vester lieber besonder/ Wir haben euch nun zu zweyen vnderschiedenen mahlen/ wol bey ewrem Diener/ den 14 Aprilis, als auch hernacher den 26 Augusti geschrieben/ vnd vnsere Obseruationes Stellarum fixarum vberschickt/ vnd darneben in gnaden begeret/ nicht allein ewer Iudicium darauff vns zuzuschreiben/ sondern auch ewre Obseruationes dargegen zuuberschicken. Wann vns aber biß noch von euch kein antwort einkommen/ welches vns gleichwol verwundert/ Als haben wir nicht vnderlassen wollen/ bey dieser gelegenen Botschafft/ euch nachmahls zuerinnern/ Vnd ist an euch vnser gnediges begeren/ Ihr wollet vns nicht allein auff solche vnsere schreiben hinwieder beantworten/ Sondern auch ewre Obseruationes vbersenden/ damit wir sie mit den vnsern zu conferiren haben. Das versehen wir vns/ vnd seind Euch mit gnaden geneigt/</p><p>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassell am 14 Octob. Anno 86.<br>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the trusty, our dear and especial [friend] Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Trusty, dear, and especial [friend]: we have now written to you on two separate occasions — duly by your servant, on 14 April, and again afterward on 26 August — and have sent over our <i>observations of the fixed stars</i>, and therewith graciously requested that you not only write us your <i>judgement</i> upon them, but also send over your own <i>observations</i> in return. But since to this hour no answer has come to us from you — at which we are, indeed, not a little surprised — we would not omit, by this convenient messenger, to remind you once more. And it is our gracious desire of you that you would not only reply again to these our letters, but also transmit your observations, so that we may have them to <i>compare</i> with our own. This we look for of you, and we remain graciously inclined toward you.</p><p>Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 14 October, in the year [15]86.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV \"the Wise,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the princely founder of the Kassel observatory and Tycho's chief astronomical peer-patron; his mathematician Christoph Rothmann (see brahe-t_15870812001) handled the technical detail.</li><li><b>Titulature.</b> The full style — <i>Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda</i> — is reproduced; these are the constituent lordships of the Hessian title and are kept in their German forms.</li><li><b>Register.</b> Chancery letter in the princely first-person plural (<i>Wir … euch … unser gnädiges Begehren</i>); the deferential-yet-commanding tone of a prince to a foreign noble savant is preserved (\"it is our gracious desire of you…\"). <i>Vester</i> (= <i>fest/getreu</i>) \"trusty\"; <i>lieber besonder</i> \"dear and especial,\" the standard chancery address.</li><li><b>Code-switching (kept and italicised).</b> The Latin technical and bureaucratic nouns embedded in the German — <i>Observationes Stellarum fixarum</i> (\"observations of the fixed stars\"), <i>Iudicium</i> (\"judgement / expert opinion\"), <i>Observationes</i>, and the verb <i>conferiren</i> (\"to compare/collate\") — are rendered into English but flagged as the Landgrave's own Latin within the German, per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Substance.</b> A reminder that the Landgrave's two earlier letters (14 April, 26 August 1586) enclosing Kassel's fixed-star observations have gone unanswered; he presses again for Tycho's <i>Iudicium</i> and a reciprocal set of Hven observations to collate — the data-exchange that underlies the printed correspondence.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> O.S. (Lutheran Hesse kept Julian).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure. Formulaic chancery German with transparent Latin insets; no ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null -- no Dreyer slice exists for this letter, so collation was not possible and grade is capped at B regardless of quality (published_grade was A on no basis -- self-assigned grades in this pipeline are unreliable in exactly the way the brief warns). Checked published_english against source_text clause-by-clause: the two prior-letter dates (14 April, 26 August), the place and date of this letter (Kassel, 14 October [15]86), the full title string (Hesse / Katzenelnbogen / Dietz / Ziegenhain / Nidda), and every substantive clause are rendered accurately. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15860826001", "next": "brahe-t_15870101001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860301001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870101001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reminds Tycho of two unanswered letters, renewing the request to send his observations of the fixed stars for comparison with Kassel's own.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15861014001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870101001", "d": "1587-01-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 18 January 1587 [O.S.] (catalogued 1 January 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri (Liber I, 1596) (= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB Cod. Vindob. lat. 10689.10</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMOA PRINCIPI AC DOMINO D. WILHELMO LANDTGRAVIO HASSIAe, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN,<br>DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDAA: PRINCIPI ACA DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO S. P.ALITERAS Celsitudinis Tuae Princeps Illustriss. ad me circa Vernum tempus<br>proxime elapsi Anni datas, quibus meis respondere dignabaris, per eundem<br>quem ad Tuam Cels. ablegaueram mihi domesticum, accepiA, pro qua clementi Tuae Celsitud. erga me beneuolentia, demisseA ago gratias. Quod veroA tanto temporis interuallo, nihil ijs responderimA, et T. Cels. desiderijs satisfacere tam diu intermiserim, licet etiam<br>interea bis per aliasA a T. Cels. Literas hoc nomine commonefactus, non est quod Celsit. T.<br>existimet, vel incuria quadam, vel data opera factumA esse, sed locorum potius nimia intercapedo, raraque illuc mittendi<br>Literas oportunitas, me a scribendo hactenus retardârunt. Duobus enim saltem Anni temporibus, commodior hinc Cassellas versus scribendiA, conceditur occasio, cum videlicet ad vtrasque Nundinas Francofordianas<br>nostrates proficiscunturA. Quapropter, cum paulo post nundinas vltimas<br>vernales, Tuae Celsitudinis literas accepissem, in eas quae proxime<br>sequebantur Autumnales, responsum differre necessitasA coëgit. VbiA vero circa id temporis, quo ad praeteritas proxime elapso Autumno,<br>Francoforti celebratas<br>nundinas Mercatores hinc eoA se conferre vellentA, cum quodam BibliopolaA sedulo egissem, vt paulo ante discessum, me in hac Insula conueniret, secumque Literas T. Cels. Secretario<br>Cassellis tradendas asportaret. Quod etiam ille promiserat, et praestare omnino constituerat.<br>SedA accidit praeterA opinionem, vt ijs ipsis diebus, quibus illi huc accedendum foret,<br>Illustris. Princeps VDALRICVSA Dux Megapolensis, vna cum Illustrissima PrincipeA Domina ELIZABETHA Coniuge (quae<br>postea in ipso reditu Mechelburgam<br>versus, antequam Mare traiecissetA, magno omnium luctu diem obijt) et Fratris Filio Duce SIGISMVNDO, deducente illos ipsa etiam<br>Sereniss. REGINA nostra vna<br>cum magno Nobilium Virorum comitatu, me hic Vraniburgi inuisere sint dignatiA, partim vt machinas Astronomicas, quibus has aedes refertas sciebant,<br>coram contemplarentur, partim vt Inclytus Dux VDALRICVSA, Pyronomicos meos labores (quibus non minorem quam Astronomicis<br>operam impendo) et his inseruientem apparatum Mechanicum, penitius<br>introspiceret. NamA ipsius Celsitudo, multis ab hinc Annis, ijs exercitijs plurimum delectaturA, et non vulgaria in his, propria industria adinuenit, quemadmodum et<br>Serenissima Regina ipsius<br>Filia, hisce Artificijs Spagyricis magnopere afficitur, quo nomine etiam<br>praeterito Anno, Mare hoc bis traijcere, nostrosque lares subire non grauata estA. Cum itaque eo ipso tempore, quo T. Celsit. commodeA rescribendum esset, his tam Illustribus Hospitibus excipiendis<br>occuparer, idque Bibliopola ille, qui me ex promisso accedere animum<br>induxerat Haffniae (eo quod inde<br>nauigia Regia, quibus traijciendum erat, procurabantur) cognouisset,<br>propositum mutauit, et ne Nundinarum, diutius praestolando, tempus negligeretA, me, quem intelligebat impeditumA esse, non accedens, itineri se commisit. Hinc itaque factum est, vt<br>hac oportuna occasione Tuae Celsit. rescribendi, praeter omnem expectationem, frustratus simA. Cumque ex eo tempore, nulla eiuscemodi commoditas Literas ad T.<br>Cels. mittendi, mihi oblata fuerit, responsione, quamuis inuitus,<br>supersedere cogebar, idque in hoc vsque tempus, quo hic alter nostras BibliopolaA<br>Francofurtum ad vernales<br>nundinas proficiscitur, mihi in perferendis ad T. Celsitud. Literis suam<br>operam pollicereturA. Haec vero eo pluribus T. Celsitud. ob id commemoro, vt intelligat<br>nullatenûs mea incuria factum esse, quod in rescribendo iusto tardior<br>fuerim. Tuam itaque Celsit. submisse rogatam volo, vt ob praedictas rationes<br>moram hanc candide interpretetur, meque clementer hac in<br>parte excusatum habeat. Quod ego sperans me pro eximia Celsitudinis Tuae aequanimitate omnino<br>impetraturum, nunc vtar oblata oportuniore<br>rescribendi occasione, et ad singula capita in T. Celsitud. literis<br>particulariter respondebo. Quod itaque Celsitudo Tua, in prioribus ad me datis literis<br>responsionem ad meas continentibus, ab initio asserat, conuenientiam illam<br>inter sui Mathematici Obseruationes, et<br>meas in Cometa Anni 85. adeo exquisitam, admodum placuisse, et magni<br>ponderis locum meruisse, id sane et me plurimum oblectauitA. Neque enim similem in aliorum Mathematicorum scriptis de Cometis<br>aliquot praeteritis cum ipso caelo, nostrisque Obseruationibus congruentiam,<br>vspiam ferme inuenio, quod illorum in obseruando et demonstrando negligentia<br>factum non dubito. Diuersitas autem, si qua erat inter Tuae Celsi Mathematici et nostras in dicto<br>Cometa animaduersiones, potius indeA eueniebat, quod Stellarum fixarum loca, quarum vsus requirebatur, non<br>praecise in ijsdem scrupulis assumserimus, licet ea ipsa, quae in his nobis<br>intercessit discrepantia, paucissimorum et nullius pene momenti scrupulorum<br>fuerit, vt ex collatione cum nostris locis plenius videre licet. Inprimis<br>vero mihi perceptu iucundum fuit, quod Tuae<br>Celsitud. Mathematicus nullam in eodem Cometa deprehenderit<br>parallaxin, quemadmodum et ego vel nullam, vel pene insensibilem,<br>multiplicibus et accuratis Obseruationibus inuenire potui. Ideoque plane in<br>eo Rei veritate idipsum exigente consentimus, non in Elementari orbe, sed in<br>supremo aethere genitum fuisse hunc Cometam, contra quam hactenus<br>Peripatetici, et omnes fere Philosophi, ipsique adeo Mathematici, pro rato<br>indubitatoque asseuerârantA. Quod etiam fieri posse, Noua illa, et miraculosa ad Cassiopeam<br>Stella superioribus annis conspecta, vel ex proprijs Tuae Celsitudinis<br>Obseruationibus, quibus meae apprime astipulabantur, liquidissime<br>comprobauit. Imo et nos in 4. alijs Cometis interea conspectis nullum eorum<br>infra Lunam, sed omnes in ipso aethere cursum suum absoluisse, e certissimis<br>Obseruationibus Geometrica veritate demonstrauimus; vt ob id prorsus<br>existimem omnes Cometas in ipso Coelo supra Lunam generari, nullumque eorum<br>vnquam Elementarem extitisse. Nam quod Regiomontani et Vogelini<br>Obseruationes in duobus Cometis veteribus, et recentiorum quorundam placita,<br>in his ipsis, quorum nos inuestigationem per Instrumenta debita nacti sumus,<br>diuersum ostendant, nihil moramur; Siquidem, quantum illorum Obseruationibus<br>fidentium sit, vel in ipsis mundo congenitis sideribus perspectum habeamus.<br>Et longe subtilioris artifici existitA parallaxes tam exiguas discernere, praesertim vbi motus proprij vna<br>sit habenda consideratio, quam hactenus communiter vsitata apparentias<br>caelestes scrutandi ratio, suppeditare queat. De toto vero hoc Cometarum argumentoA nostras Obseruationes, hisque innixas demonstrationes Tua Celsitudo<br>vberius et sufficienter sententiam nostramA<br>cognoscetA vbi OpusA, quod nunc de hac ipsa materia adA praelum adorno, in publicam vtilitatem euulgatumA fuerit. Quantum ad Obseruationes Tuae Celsitudinis mihi transmissas<br>(quo etiam nomine Tuae Celsitud. plurimas reuerenter habeo gratias) attinet,<br>contuli eas tum inter se, tum vna cum meis diligenter, et quidem in<br>distantijs ipsis nullum reperio alicuius momenti discrimen, quin tuum<br>Instrumentum easdem praebeat, quas mei Sextantes. Id quod vel ex sola inter<br>Regulum et Spicam ♍ intercapedine (eo quod Obseruatio haec fiat iuxta<br>extremos Sextantis limites, vnde etiam exiliores interuallorum Anguli minus<br>dubij euadunt) manifestum redditur. Hanc enim Tuae Celsitud. Sextans praebuit<br>part. 54. M. 2; Nos eandem aliquoties vix Sexta parte vnius minuti<br>(discrimine pene insensibili et nullius ponderis) minorem deprehendimus,<br>attestantibus etiam caeteris distantijs Tuae Celsitudinis cum nostris inuicem<br>collatis, eum Sextantem, quem Tua Cels. in vsu habet, satis exactas, et ipsi<br>coelo congruas praebere intercapedines. Quod tamen vix eius opera factitatum<br>opinor, qui structuram illius ad mei quem hic conspexerat (quodA proculdubio silentio praeterijt) imitationem primum Tuae Celsitudini<br>ordinauit. Nota enim satis mihi est ipsius, in his Mechanicis rebus vel<br>inquirendis, vel imitandis, minus exacta diligentia. Ideoque id potius<br>propriae T. Cels. industriae, et Mathematici ipsius Rothmanni, artificisque vna<br>Mechanici ab ipsa experientia indagatae solertiae ascribendum<br>censeo. Quod ad altitudines Meridianas mihi transmissas attinet, in ijs<br>parum quiddam discriminis a meis inuentis reperio, adeo vt Declinationes<br>Stellarum inde petitae, in Borealibus, duobus propemodum vbique scrupulis<br>abundent, in Australibus vero totidem deficiant, idque praesupposita<br>Aequatoris inclinatione part. 38. M. 40. quae ex ea Poli eleuatione, quam<br>Rothmannus e Stellarum quarundam non<br>occidentium vtraque altitudine mihi perscripsitA, resultare deprehenditur. Cum autem satis perspectum haberem,<br>Declinationes Stellarum a me indagatas in nullo minuto vel abundare, vel<br>deficere, eo quod quinis vel senis varie fabricatis, iustaeque magnitudinis<br>Instrumentis, ita vt in quibusdam sexta pars minuti discerni commode queat,<br>certô exploratae essent: et satis vna mihi constaret, Tuam Celsitud. in<br>Obseruationibus etiam, praesertim per haec Neoterica organa reiteratis, summam<br>adhibere curam et diligentiam, vndeA haec binorum scrupulorum inter nos diuersitas contingeret, primo mecum<br>plurimum admirabar. Deinde cum eandem pene vbique esse differentiam<br>considerarem, statim in mentem incidit, facile euenisse, vt vel<br>pinnacidiorum non rite appositorum vitio bina scrupula iusto altius in Tuae<br>Celsit. Quadrante perspicerentur, vel etiam perpendiculi in vnico saltem<br>minuto, non rite ordinati errore, qui vtrinque assumtus, tam in Poli quam<br>Stellarum Meridianarum altitudine declinationem earundem binis variaret<br>scrupulis, aut forte vtraque ratione. Id quod<br>facile in Quadrante bicubitali euenire posse inde<br>colligebam, quod etiam in duplo vel triplo maioribus, si non exactissima,<br>tam in pinnacidijs, quam perpendiculo adhibeatur applicatio, facile dimidij,<br>vel etiam integri scrupuli error obrepat. Nec enim diuisioni minus certae,<br>aut Centro deuio hunc erroremA imputandum duxi. Habebam itaqueA in animo Tuam Celsitud. per literas de his commonefacere, vt<br>praecisiorem vel pinnacidiorum, vel perpendiculi, aut vtriusque simul<br>examinationem denuo institueret, quo etiam in Declinationibus Stellarum, non<br>minus quam in distantijs, exacta inter Tuae Celsitud. obseruationes et meas<br>conuenientia stabilietur. Verum dum oportuna mittendi literas occasione<br>destituor, et circa Nundinarum Francofurtensium tempora, causa commemorataA â scribendo impedior, optime mihi, et ex votoA accidit, vt T. Cels. Mathematicus<br>interea indicarit, se deprehendisse Quadrantem T. Cels. ob perpendiculi<br>vitium altitudines priores ostendisse vno scrupulo vbique iusto maiores. Quod si est (vti prorsus mihi persuadeo) Poli sublimitatem vno etiam scrupulo minorem, et Aequatoris tantundem<br>maiorem prouenire consentaneum erit, vt ea reuera sit Cassellis part. 38. M. 41. Huic itaqueA si applicentur altitudines T. Cels. mihi transmissae, ita vt singulis<br>ob perpendiculi errorem vnicum adimatur scrupulum, satis exacte cum meis<br>Stellarum declinationibus consentient. Quare plurimum laetatusA sum, per hoc vnicum scrupulum, in vitio perpendiculi emendatum, alium<br>longe maiorem ob hanc, licet non adeo magnam, quae Obseruationibus nostris<br>intercedebat differentiam, animo meo inhaerentem scrupulum ademptum esse.<br>Omnes enim coelestes Obseruationes ad exactam amussim, quatenus in sensus<br>humanos cadant, instituendas censeo, adeo, vt si quid in his desideretur vel<br>tantillum, pro irritisA habeam. Vt vero Celsitudo Tua collationem vlteriorem inter suas et<br>meas Obseruationes, tam quo ad intercapedines, quam quo ad altitudines<br>Meridianas, siue inde petitas Declinationes instituere queat, mitto Tuae<br>Celsitud. aliquot Stellarum, tum aliarum, tum praesertim insigniorum, quae<br>iuxta Zodiacum sunt distantias, meis Sextantibus Trigonicis (sic enim eos<br>appellare libuit) fideliter exploratas, et praeterea sublimitates quasdam<br>Meridianas Quadrantibus diuersis, inuicem tamen consentientibus indagatas,<br>idque tum circa finem Anni 84. et labentem 85. tum etiam circa exitum<br>proxime elapsi 86. et primum initium huius currentis 87. vt Tua Celsitudo eo<br>expeditius, ad debita tempora suas altitudines cum meis conferre possit.<br>Siquidem et his ipsis Annis post denuo redintegrata Instrumenta, Tuam<br>Celsitudinem exactiorem altitudinum Stellarum dimensionem notasseA, nullatenus dubitem. Nouit enim apprime Tua Celsitudo Declinationes<br>Stellarum, et ob id etiam earundem altitudines, quotannis aliquantulum variareA, idque praesertim in ijs sideribus, quae<br>Aequinoctialibus locis propiora existunt. Eleuatio vero Aequatoris in nostris<br>sublimitatibus Stellarum adhibenda venit part. 34. /. 5⅓. eo quod crebris experimentis adinuenerim, Polum<br>hîc Vraniburgi, in loco meae<br>Obseruationis, qui est medius Insulae Huenae<br>in celebri illo Freto Danico sitae,<br>partibus 55. scr. 54⅔. attolli, id quod tam ex ipsa Polari Stella, quam caeteris<br>circumpolaribus non occidentibus, et ob decliuitatem non nimiumA sensibilem, refractionem excludentibus sideribus, multoties<br>comprobatum habeo. Longitudines et latitudines Stellarum a Tua Celsitud.<br>mihi communicatas, etiam tum inter se, tum cum meis contuli, deprehendique<br>assignationem illam e Globo Mechanice institutam, etsi non admodum<br>disconuenientem, tamen non vbique satis scrupulose annotatis distantijs et<br>altitudinibus correspondere, quamuis perexigua in quibusdam reperiatur<br>differentia. Quemadmodum Celsitudo Tua ex nonnullis, quas in adiuncta charta<br>annotaui collationibus, perspicere potest. Nec sane mihi vsque adeo mirum<br>videtur, mechanice in Globo perquisitas e distantijs et Altitudinibus<br>Stellarum longitudines et latitudines non in ipsis scrupulis exactae rationi<br>consentire. Siquidem et ego Globum magnum Orichalcicum apprimeA rotundum habeam, qui Sex pedes in diametro contineat, in quo<br>Ecliptica, Aequatorque in singula scrupula, per puncta transuersalia, more<br>nobis in caeteris Instrumentis vsitato, subtiliter diuiduntur; nihilominus<br>tamen in vno vel sesquialtero scrupulo vbique ratam certitudinem in<br>Stellarum locis per Globum hunc ex Obseruationibus certis indagandis vix<br>mihi polliceor; Adeo ea, quae Mechanice, licet summa diligentia elaborantur,<br>Geometricae et Arithmeticae scrupulositati posthabenda veniunt. In ipsis vero LongitudinibusA Stellarum a Tua Celsitudine mihi transmissis, quamuisA Geometrice, et per numeros Sinuum exactiori trutina expendantur, vtplurimumA quina vel sena aut circiter scrupula, nostris Obseruationibus<br>vlteriora ab aequinoctio numerari animaduerto. In Latitudinibus vero non<br>maior fere reperitur differentia, quam quae vel ex Longitudinibus paulo<br>aliter assumtis, vel Obliquitatis Eclipticae diuersa constitutione,<br>potissimum vero e Declinationibus, duobus minutis perpendiculi vestri vitio<br>a nostris dissidentibus, causari poterat. Est sane discrimen hoc etiam<br>Longitudinum, nedum Latitudinum, non adeo magni momentiA, si veterum et Neotericorum quorundam conferamus annotationes. Nec<br>satis mirari possum Celsitudinem Tuam, tam prope suis Instrumentis Stellarum<br>loca attigisse, praesertim cum colligere queam, Celsitudinem Tuam in<br>Aldebora, vel quacunque alia pro fundamento vsa sit, Ascensionem eius Rectam<br>ex transitu per Meridianum, tempore per Horologium inuento et cum Sole<br>collato, deprehendisse. Quae sane ratio mihi semper minus tuta videbatur.<br>Licet enim et ego quaterna eiusmodi habeamA Horologia diuersae magnitudinis, exactaeque<br>structurae, quae non solum singula scrupula prima, sed etiam secunda indicent;<br>in tam subtili tamen negotio eis fidem adhibere frustraneum duxiA, cum 4. saltem secunda, quae in pluribus horis facile excidunt, vnius scrupuli primiA in longitudine errorem inducant. Adde quod Horologia ista etiam<br>diligentissime, vel e rotulis Aurichalcicis elaborata, mutationi aëreae<br>sensibiliter obnoxia sint. Taceo pondus non esseA aequale, quando sublimius decliuiusque pendet, ob ligamenti, quo<br>annectitur, variatam Longitudinem, quae ponderi aliquid successiue addit,<br>quod licet perexiguum sit, tamen in scrupulis secundis deuiationem non<br>negligendam adfertA. Quapropter, cum hac ratione negotium hoc non satis tuto succedere<br>perspectum haberem, nec ignorarem, difficultatem haud exiguam subesse, vt<br>vel vnius Stellae Fixae vera ab Aequinoctio peruestigaretur remotio, qua<br>cognita caeteraeA, vel per distantias ab inuicem, vel per differentias Aequatorias, aut<br>etiam per Armillas Hipparchicas Zodiacales, quas vna in vsu habemus,<br>certiori tamen compendio elaboratas, quam a veteribusA, caeterae (inquam) quotquot liberet, facile constarent. Nec modum illum quo veteres, Hipparchus inprimis et PtolemaeusA Vsi sunt, superioreque aetate NICOLAVS COPERNICVS, per Lunam diei et<br>noctis participem, tam Soli, quam Stellis per Instrumenta applicatam, suis<br>carere difficultatibus, errorique minus obnoxiumA esse iudicarem. Nam licet concedamus, locum Solis adhibita exquisita<br>diligentia, in vno vel altero scrupulo, quo ad longitudinem scitu<br>possibilem, et intercapedinem eius ad Lunam, huiusque ad Stellas, satis<br>exacte dabilem esse; tamen Parallaxes Lunares, quae adeo praecise non<br>cognoscuntur, in vtraque Obseruatione variationem aliquam a vero inducunt.<br>Adde quod haec ratio obseruandi non commode fit, nisi Sole circa Horizontem<br>decliui existente, vbi alius est eius locus apparens, quam verus, qui iuxta<br>Meridianum patuit, idque non saltem ratione parallaxeos, sed multo potius ob<br>Refractionem, de qua in prioribus literis Tuae Celsitud. meam sententiam<br>latius indicaui; et intelligo ex Tuae Celsitudinis responso, eam etiamA tam in Sole, quam caeteris Planetis et Fixis, deprehendisse in<br>decliuiori situ Refractionem sensibilemA; Quo nomine eius deA nostris vtrinque Obseruationibus plurimum congratulor. Nec enim<br>Veteres, vel Recentiores, quod sciam, hanc in siderum collimationibus<br>animaduerterunt, vel debite praecauerunt. Hac, inquam, ab antecessoribus<br>nostris strata via, cum satis tuto incedere ob praedictas aliasque plures<br>rationes non liceret, commodiori et tutiori semita, quae ad Stellarum exactas<br>Longitudines perduceret, ingrediendum censui, nullamque aptiorem<br>exactioremque inueni, quam quae ex opportunitate nobis, Anno 1582A. quo Veneris Stellam Vespertinam existentem interdiu dilucide<br>apparentem conspeximus, offerebatur. Tunc enim Venerem a Sole in exacta distantia, a 24. Februarij vsque in medium Aprilis,<br>pluribus quam facile credi possit, vicibus, diurno tempore diligentissime<br>obseruauimus, idque non solum in decliuiori Solis situ, sed multoties, cum<br>is vel in Meridiano, vel saltem vnica, aut sesquialtera hora, vltra hunc<br>remoueretur, et ♀ admodum sublimis vna existeret, adeo,<br>vt et ipsius per Meridianum transitus, Sole adhuc multum eleuato, a nobis,<br>tam quo ad altitudinem, quam distantiam a Sole, saepenumero deprehensus sit.<br>Quare, cum locus Solis, et ex altitudinibus Meridianis, ac per antecedentium<br>annorum a nobis adamussim in motu eius factam restitutionem satis exacte<br>constaret, ex Declinatione Veneris et Solis, data vtriusque per Sextantem<br>exquisita et sibi consentiente distantia, (ita vt Sol vnum pinnacidium<br>illuminaret tota sua quantitate; per alterum vero, Venus vtrinque per<br>rimulas Cylindro superiori parallelas conspiceretur) locus Stellae Veneris<br>certo patuit. Qui nocturno tempore, quamprimum Affixarum aliqua iuxta<br>Zodiacum aspectui offerebatur, per acceptam eius ab hac intercapedinem pari<br>ratione applicatus, habita prius motus proprij interea transacti ratione,<br>Stellae eiusdem locum manifestauit, idque saepenumero fiebat in diuersis etiam<br>Stellis, cum Sol, Venus, et Fixae obseruatae vbique fere essent in tanta supra<br>Horizontem altitudine, vt Refractionem omnem excluderent. Si qua vero<br>interdum erat perexigua, ea ex alijs Obseruationibus circa hanc factis,<br>praecauebatur. Hac itaque infallibili Methodo in Longitudinis Stellarum<br>exactam notitiam tunc temporis multiplici experimento in idem recurrente, et<br>Stellarum earundem inter se distantijs eandem comprobante, optime ex voto<br>deueni. Adhibui etiam vbique Parallaxes tam Solis, quam Veneris, quatenus<br>per horarum interualla variabanturA, vt eo exactior ratio constaret. Imo vero et ipsius Veneris<br>Parallaxes peruestigare eodem anno non dubitabam, vt, an circa, vel infra<br>Solem conuolueretur, certius pateret. Quod etiam in Stella Martis in fine<br>prioris, et circa eiusdem anni initia experiri audebam, quando is AcronychusA et sublimis in ♋ latitudine etiam Borea eum altius attollente<br>conspiciebatur, vt indagarem an Sole maiorem diuersitatem aspectus<br>induceret, quod necessarium foret, si Coperniana Hypothesis in rei veritate consisteret. Nam<br>tertia fere parte per hanc Mars in ☍ Solis terris redditur propior quam ipse<br>Sol. Vnde etiam tunc maiorem Parallaxis diuersitatem necessario inducit, et<br>per consequens, Orbes Reuolutionum Martis nequaquam totaliter supra Orbem<br>Solarem, vt ferunt Hypotheses PtolemaicaeA, constituuntur. Ideoque vel rotari Terram annuo motu,<br>quo Planetarum excusantur omnes Epicycli; vel aliam caelestium Reuolutionum dispositionemA, quam hactenus factitatum, indagandam esse. Verum haec nunc extra<br>propositum dicta, alias dilucidius et copiosius, Deo volente declarabuntur.<br>Cum itaque (vt ad rem redeam) praedicta minime dubia<br>ratione a Sole per Venerem indicato anno, in cognitionem Longitudinis<br>aliquot insigniorum fixarum Stellarum circa Zodiacum, earum latitudine se<br>facile vna ingerente, peruenissem; et ex Obseruationibus quibusdam alijs,<br>Anno 85. circa autumnale Aequinoctium acceptis, quo tempore Veneris Stella<br>tunc Matutina, etiam interdiu conspiciebatur, idque in consimili fere, qua Anno 82. tum distantia a Sole, et Terra, tum<br>etiam altitudine supra Horizontem (quod negotij certitudinem plurimum<br>promouit) omnia viceuersa exploratius comprobassem, hinc reliquarum etiam<br>affixarum Stellarum, tam intra quam extra Zodiacum loca, quo ad longum et<br>latum restituere, vnaque Planetarum omnium per haec apparentias peruestigare,<br>nulla temporis sereni opportunitate hactenus intermissaA, elaboraui. Deprehendo autem in quamplurimis Stellis idipsum, quod T.<br>Cels. mihi perscribit, eas non saltem Longitudines aliterA obtinere, quam Veterum traditiones adiecta quoque Aequinoctij<br>praecessione correctiore exhibent, sed etiam Latitudines multo aliter, in<br>quibusdam minus, in quibusdam vero plus, ostendere; cum tamen has necessario<br>inuariatas omni aeuo permanere inde a vetustissimis Astronomis, eo quod motus<br>octauae Sphaerae circa Polos Eclipticae contingat, citra omne dubium pro<br>comperto habeatur. Vnde Cels. T. merito in eius variationis admirationem<br>perducta, etiam meum de hac re iudicium clementer requirit, an videlicet<br>singulis Fixis aliquem peculiarem motum per se inesseA, quo Latitudinem quoque diuersimode mutent, consentaneum existimem.<br>Qua de re T. Cels. breuibus meam sententiam nunc exponam. Loca omnium<br>affixarum Stellarum inde a prima mundi Origine in singulis et omnibus, quo<br>ad se inuicem, prorsus inuariata permanere, omni dubio procul iudico. Idque<br>inde facile comprobatur, quod omnes fixae Stellae, quas recenset Ptolemaeus primo capite dictionis 7. ab<br>Abrachi seu Hipparcho proxime 260.<br>ante se annis in vna extitisse linea recta, easdem etiam suo aeuo in eodem<br>situ, quo ad se inuicem, permansisse. Cumque eaedem tum etiam illae, quas<br>postea addit Ptolemaeus, vt Posteris<br>ansam experimentandi relinqueret, etiam hac aetate elapsis ab ipso annis<br>1400. eundem prorsus, quem idem Ptolemaeus recenset, positum exacte retinere animaduertantur<br>(quod nequaquam fieri posset si vna Stella aliter siue Longitudinem siue<br>Latitudinem, quo ad propriam Sphaeram mutaret, quam altera) facile<br>ratiocinari licebit, omnia affixa Sidera in eodem, quo ad se ipsa situ,<br>citra Latitudinis vel Longitudinis diuersam inordinatamque mutationem omni<br>aeuo consistere. Quod vero quaedam reperiantur Fixae etiam inter insigniores,<br>quae multo aliam latitudinem obtineant, quam Hipparchus et Ptolemaeus annotârunt, id partim eorum minus exacta<br>Obseruatione, partim tot transcriptorum, qui forte negotij subtilitatem<br>ignorabant, negligentia commissum verisimileA est. EtenimA vix ante 150. annos artificium Typographicum excogitatumA, et prius tanto temporum interuallo toties rescribendo multa mendose,<br>praesertim in numerorum Characteribus assignari poterant, cum etiam in hac<br>ipsa arte impressoria, nisi diligens adhibeatur correctura, facilis,<br>praesertim in hac materia, lapsus admittatur. Accedit et hoc, quod certis<br>documentis in quamplurimis affixis circa Zodiacum praesertim Sideribus,<br>caelitus comprobetur per eam, quae a TimocharisA, Hipparchi et<br>Ptolemaei temporibus, hucusque<br>facta est inclinationis Eclipticae ad Aequatorem immutationem, Latitudines<br>omnium affixarum Stellarum, pro ea ratione, qua Tropicis<br>locis illarum longitudines magis minusue appropinquant, vna etiam successiue<br>variatas esse. Quod sane mirum videtur, cum alias octauae Sphaerae motum circa<br>Polos Eclipticae fieri, ob latitudines Stellarum, non aliter, quam minutula<br>hac obliquitatisA Signiferi diuersitate, imminutasA certo constet. Quia vero intelligo ex literis Tuae tCelsitud.<br>Mathematici Rothmanni, eum in eadem<br>mecum sententia hac in parte versari, latius ad illum de hoc negotio<br>perscripsi, et per plurimarum Stellarum certiores Obseruationes, remotis<br>intercidentibus dubijs, in concepta opinione confirmaui. Hinc Tua Celsitudo<br>aliqualem in latitudinibus quarundam Stellarum, variationis occasionem<br>intelliget. Si vero quae maior coincidit, hanc vel ex Veterum minus certa<br>annotatione, vel Transcriptorum lapsu, vt prius dixi, euenire necessarium<br>est. Caeterum vtA Tua Celsitudo perspectius cognoscat, quo situ Stellarum fixarum loca<br>a me, supradicta ratione indagata sint, descripsi quarundam praecipuarum,<br>praesertim iuxta Zodiacum, Longitudines et latitudines, quas ad principium<br>huius labentis Anni redegi, prout hae nostris exactioribus Obseruationibus<br>deprehensae sunt, vt T. Cels. ea loca cum suis expeditius conferre possit.<br>Plurium Stellarum assignationem addere non duxi operae precium, cum vel ex<br>paucioribus, imo ex vnica recte constituta, caeterae omnes cui Instrumenta<br>commoda et certa ad manus sunt, facile innotescant. Interuallum autem motus<br>ab Anno 82. quo aptissimas in his, vt supra indicaui, Obseruationes nactus<br>sum, non ex Coperniana Aequinoctiorum<br>mutatione (quam is e Spica ♍ obseruata, in primam Stellam ♈, retento<br>interuallo longitudinis Ptolemaico, et<br>Caelo inconsulto, deriuauit) nec ab Alphonsina numeratione mutuatus sum, sed ex ipsa Copernici Obseruatione in dicta spica ♍ Anno<br>1525. habita, et cum nostra recentiori collata, negocium hoc absolui. Cum<br>enim Copernicus asserat, eo Anno<br>fuisse Spicae Altitudinem Meridianam grad. 27. M. 0. et Polus Fruenburgi eleuetur part. 54. M. 22¼. velutA proprio exquisitoque Instrumento ante Triennium periculum fieri per Studiosum quendam eo cum Organo a me missumA curaui, fuit declinatio Spicae tunc part. 8. Min. 37¾. ideoque assumta<br>eius latitudine, grad. 1. min. 59½. qualem nos adinuenimus (dimidio saltem scrupulo Copernici positione minorem, quod nihil negocium hoc variat,<br>cum vtrobique eandem praesupponam) prouenit Longitudo Spicae ad id tempus in part. 17. M. 14. ♎, licet ipse Copernicus<br>hancA 7. scrupulis vlteriorem constituat, idque procul dubio, vitio minus exquisiteA cognitae Eleuationis Polaris, quam credibile est ipsum per Solem ex<br>altitudine maxima et minima, ratione ob refractionem in decliui situ<br>fallaci, inquisiuisseA. Cumque hoc tempore Spica declinet ex nostris Obseruationibus part.<br>8. M. 57. praecise, et assumta priori latitudine, eius Longitudo incidat in<br>part. 18. M. 4. ♎, octaua Sphaera aA<br>Copernici Obseruatione hucusque,<br>interuallo Annorum 61. promota est M. 50. Quare Annis 73. (modo non paucioribus) vnum gradum nostro aeuo conficit, cum tamen Coperniana anticipatio aequinoctiorum nunc tardissimam fere<br>Fixarum promotionem expostulet, ita vt centenis ferme Annis vnicum gradum<br>absoluant. Hinc itaque patet quantum Copernianis inuentis circa Aequinoctiorum praecessione, vnde<br>octauae Sphaerae motus excusatur, (vt de Sole et caeteris nunc nihil dicam)<br>inhaerendum sit, vtvtA subtilissime ab Astronomo incomparabili excogitatis. Sed apparentis<br>motus octauae Sphaerae exactam cognitionem vix vlli mortalium absolute<br>perscrutabilem existimo. Siquidem nullam adhuc integram Reuolutionem adimpleratA, et particulae circuitus a Timochare per Hipparchum, Ptolemaeum,<br>Albategnium, et Copernicum ad nos diductae non sufficiuntA, nec si id praestarent, satis scrupulose constantA. Cum itaque per Experientiam praedicto modo anticipatio Aequinoctiorum<br>circa haec tempora innotuerit, facile erat motum proportionalem ab 82. Anno<br>in hunc vel quosuis alios nostra aetate transferre. Non enim vnius hominis<br>aeuo aliquam sensibilem inaequalitatem hac in parte contingere consentaneum<br>est. Congruere vero etiamnum haec loca Stellarum Obseruationes meae praeterito<br>Anno, et hoc ipso inde a principio Ianuarij factae, sufficienter declarantA. Quod vero in T. Celsitud. longitudinibus Stellarum Geometrice, ex<br>intercapedinibus et Declinationibus in numeros redactis, quina vel sena, aut<br>circiter, vtplurimum abundent scrupula, facile excusabitur, si constet<br>Celsitud. T. tunc loco Solis, in his limitandis, vsam fuisse, cum Sol versaretur circa vernum AequinoctiumA, in parte Eclipticae ascendente. Illic enim ratione illius vnici<br>scrupuli, quod perpendiculi vitio causabatur, Declinatio Solis binis<br>scrupulis sublimior, quam oportuit, reddebatur, ideoque locus eius quinis in<br>longitudinem vlterior. Id quod etiam in Stellas fixas ad Solis locum<br>adaptatas, necessario redundauit, licet circa Autumnale aequinoctium in parte<br>Eclipticae descendente, res haec contrario modo se habeat. Vt vero Celsitudo<br>T. etiam in caeteris erraticis Stellis aliquod specimen nostrarum<br>Obseruationum videatA, adiungo omnium vera loca, qualia meis Instrumentis accurata animaduersione intra vnicam primi mobilis<br>reuolutionem, ter hoc ipso Mense Ianuario ab initio noui anni deprehensa<br>sunt. Quod licet alias rarius contingat, aliquoties tamen superioribus annis a meA factitatum est, praesertim Anno praecedenti in Octobri, cum ☿ eous<br>multis diebus appareret. Solis motum, e Veneris situ, per Stellas fixas<br>inquisito, cum per Declinationes impediente etiam Refractione hoc anni<br>tempore minus certoA haberi queat, comprobaui. Interdiu enim Venus etiam ante Meridianum<br>hisce diebus, vel in ipso Meridie et ante, per puriorem serenitatem satis<br>euidenter conspicitur. Refractionis vero parallaxeosque Solis et Veneris<br>impedimenta prius in consimili altitudine explorata, remoui. Itidem etiam in<br>Mercurio effeci, cum isA admodum decliuis vix 5. gradibus altus obseruaretur. In caeteris hac limitatione opus non erat, quodA sublimiores caelitus capiebantur. Addidi vero ad eadem tempora<br>Obseruationis nostrae loca Planetarum Alphonsina et Coperniana, vt Tua Cels. vnico intuitu<br>discrepantiam vtriusque calculi a Caelo ipso facilius conspiceret, quamuis<br>saepenumero multo plus, quam nunc exorbitent. Peto autem a T. Celsitud.<br>quibus possum precibus, vt Obseruationes has nostras nemini communicare<br>velit, cauereque, ne publicae fiant, praesertim quantum ad Fixarum<br>longitudines latitudinesque attinet, quas ob id etiam, peculiari charta,<br>seorsim notari curaui. Nouit enim Tua Celsitudo quamA optime, quantum temporis, laboris, diligentiae, et sumtuum requiratur,<br>antequam adA huius rei exactam cognitionem peruenire liceat. Et sane nulli alteri<br>in tota Germania, vel alibi, cuiuscunque esset conditionis, haec mea inuenta<br>communicarem, suppressique ea hactenus, quamuis a multis hoc nomine<br>solicitatus. Plerique enim cum nihil per se egregij in his praestare velint,<br>aliorum inuentis et laboribus sese ornare, eaque pro suis venditare, non<br>sine graui autoris iniuria haud erubescunt. Verum non dubito Celsitud. Tuam<br>haec sibi soli reseruaturam, quemadmodum etiam Obseruationum T. Celsit. mihi<br>communicatarum neminem facio participem. In tota vero hac Astronomica tractationeAmultoA plura essent, de quibus copiosius cum Tua Celsit. perlibenter agerem,<br>sed nec charta, nec commoditas id nunc patitur. Habeo multorum praetereaA annorum accuratas et multiplices Obseruationes, non saltem in affixis<br>Sideribus, sed etiam in omnibus erraticis, quotiescunque nobis aspectabiles<br>illuxerunt, quas praeclarae possessionis loco (nec enim paucis Vigilijs, et<br>exiguo labore ac diligentia comparatae sunt) conseruo. Eas vero cum aliorum<br>inuentis conferre, et tandemA, in vsum publicum producereA, vt tota Astrorum scientia exacte apparentijs caelestibus satisfaciat,<br>elaboro. Dedi autem in mandatis huic viroA, vt Celsit. T. vel ipsius<br>Mathematico plura, quam nunc scribere commodum erat, meo<br>nomine exponeretA. Haec vero eo prolixius ad Cels. Tuam nunc perscripsi, partim quod sciam Celsitud. T. his Astronomicis rebus<br>impense delectari, partim vt moram in respondendo hactenus admissam ipsa in<br>rescribendo copiâ recompensaremA. Ne vero modum in vtroque excedere videar, Tuamque Celsitud. nimia<br>prolixitate molestem, nunc finem faciam, et caetera, si quae hîc desiderantur,<br>Tuae Celsitud. Mathematico per literas<br>indicabo. Id saltem Tuam Celsitud. Colophonis loco humiliter et obnixe obtestorA, vt omnibus neruis hanc diuinam Astrorum scientiam a mendis, quibus<br>scatet, vindicare, excultioremque reddere, quod etiam suapte sponte hactenus<br>egregie factitauit, in posterum magis magisque conari non intermittat. Hac<br>enim ratione T. Celsit. apud omnem Posteritatem tam sublimem et diuinam<br>scientiam veneraturamA, gratissimam diutissimamque sui memoriam conseruabit, quae eo magis<br>perpetua praestantiorque erit, quôd non e terrenis, et interitui obnoxijs<br>rebus, quas vulgus suspicit, comparata sit. Nouit Celsit.<br>Tua hoc studium non esse tenuioris fortunae hominum, neque ab illis, etiamsi<br>quam maxime cuperent, intelligerentque, debito modo excoli posse. Cumque<br>nullus sit praeter T. Celsit. inter omnes Europae Principes Viros, quem Deus<br>hoc Heroico ingenio ornârit, vt tam arduam scientiam penitius intelligat,<br>eamque ab erroribus vindicare, et omnibus modis redintegrare nôrit, decet<br>sane Tuam Celsit. huius Principalis et Principis cognitione dignissimae scientiae prae<br>caeteris viris Principibus praecipuamA etiam curam gerere, eiusque instaurationem, et ad Posteritatem<br>conseruationem, omni conatuA moliri. Hoc etiam nomine Tua Celsit. suum Mathematicum CHRISTOPHORVM ROTHMANNVM, sibi prae alijs<br>commendatum habeat, eo quod ipsum hisce exercitijs admodum solertem et<br>fidelem nauare operam, ac exactae veritatis percupidum esse indagatorem<br>multis indicijs cognoscam. Verum superuacanee haec Tuae Cels. suggero, cum sua<br>sponte animo sit ad res Astronomicas amplificandas propensissimo. In quo<br>proposito Deus admirandae et pulcherrimae huius machinae caelestis author, Tuam<br>Celsit. quam diutissime, cum felici omnium animi et corporis bonorum<br>successu confirmet. Valeat Tua Cels. et mecum de hoc ipso Aethereo et sublimi<br>negotio vlterius conferre, suisque literis reuisere non dedignetur.Datae Vraniburgi18. die IanuarijA, Anno 87.Tuae Celsitudini addictissimusTYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, the Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: his most clement Prince and Lord — heartiest greeting.</b> The letter of Your Highness, Most Illustrious Prince, written to me about the spring of the year last past — in which you deigned to reply to mine — I received through the same servant of my household whom I had despatched to Your Highness; and for that clement benevolence of Your Highness toward me I humbly give thanks. As for the fact that over so great an interval of time I have answered them nothing, and have so long delayed to satisfy Your Highness's wishes — although meanwhile twice reminded on this score by other letters from Your Highness — there is no reason for Your Highness to suppose it done either through any negligence or of set purpose; rather, it was the too-great distance between our places, and the rare opportunity of sending letters thither, that have hitherto held me back from writing. For at two seasons of the year only is the more convenient occasion of writing hence toward Kassel granted — namely when our countrymen set out for the two Frankfurt fairs. Wherefore, since I had received Your Highness's letter a little after the last spring fairs, necessity compelled me to defer my answer to the autumn fairs that next followed. But when, about the time at which the merchants from here were minded to betake themselves thither for the past fairs held at Frankfurt this last autumn, I had busily arranged with a certain bookseller that a little before his departure he should meet me on this island and carry off with him the letter to be delivered to Your Highness's secretary at Kassel — which indeed he had promised, and had fully resolved to perform — it happened beyond expectation that, in those very days on which he was to come hither, the Illustrious Prince Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, together with the Most Illustrious Princess the Lady Elizabeth his consort (who afterward, on the very return journey toward Mecklenburg, before she had crossed the sea, died to the great grief of all), and with his brother's son Duke Sigismund — our Most Serene Queen herself escorting them, together with a great retinue of noblemen — deigned to visit me here at Uraniborg: partly to behold at first hand the astronomical engines with which they knew this house to be filled, partly that the renowned Duke Ulrich might inspect more closely my pyronomic labours (on which I expend no less effort than on the astronomical) and the mechanical apparatus that serves them. For His Highness has these many years past taken the greatest delight in those exercises, and has by his own industry discovered things not commonplace therein, just as the Most Serene Queen his daughter is greatly devoted to these Spagyric arts — on which account too, in the past year, she did not grudge to cross this sea twice and to enter our dwelling. Since therefore at that very time, at which Your Highness should fittingly have been written back to, I was occupied in receiving these so illustrious guests; and since that bookseller, who from his promise had brought me to resolve to come to him at Copenhagen (because the royal vessels by which the crossing had to be made were procured from there), had learned of it, he changed his plan, and lest by waiting longer he should lose the time of the fairs, he — understanding me to be hindered — without coming, committed himself to his journey. Hence it came about that, beyond all expectation, I was balked of this opportune occasion of writing back to Your Highness. And since from that time no such convenience of sending a letter to Your Highness was offered me, I was forced, however unwillingly, to forbear my reply — and that right up to this present time, in which this other bookseller of ours sets out for Frankfurt to the spring fairs and has promised me his service in conveying a letter to Your Highness. These things I recount to Your Highness at the greater length on this account, that you may understand it was in no way through my negligence that I have been slower than was right in replying. I would therefore submissively ask Your Highness, for the aforesaid reasons, to interpret this delay candidly, and to hold me clemently excused in this part. And hoping that I shall, by Your Highness's singular even-temper, altogether obtain this, I shall now make use of the more opportune occasion of replying that is offered, and shall answer particularly to each head [of matter] in Your Highness's letter. As to the fact, then, that Your Highness, in the earlier letter sent me containing a reply to mine, asserts at the outset that that agreement between your mathematician's observations and mine on the comet of the year [15]85 — so exact — was greatly pleasing to you and earned a place of great weight: this indeed delighted me too in the highest degree. For nowhere do I find in the writings of other mathematicians, concerning several past comets, a like congruence with the heavens themselves and with our observations — which I do not doubt has come about through their negligence in observing and demonstrating. But the difference, if any there was between Your Highness's mathematician's remarks and ours on the said comet, arose rather from this, that we had not assumed the places of the fixed stars, whose use was required, precisely in the same scruples [minutes] — although that very discrepancy which fell between us in these matters was of the very fewest scruples, and of almost no moment, as may be seen more fully from the comparison with our places. And it was especially pleasant for me to perceive that Your Highness's mathematician detected no parallax in that same comet — just as I too was able to find either none, or scarcely any sensible one, by manifold and accurate observations. And so we plainly agree on this point, the truth of the matter itself requiring it, that this comet was generated not in the elementary orb, but in the highest ether — contrary to what hitherto the Peripatetics, and almost all philosophers, and indeed the mathematicians themselves, have asserted as settled and beyond doubt. That this too can be so, the new and miraculous star observed by Cassiopeia in earlier years has most clearly proved — even from Your Highness's own observations, with which mine fully concurred. Nay, in 4 other comets meanwhile observed we too have demonstrated, with geometrical truth from most certain observations, that none of them ran its course beneath the Moon, but that all ran it in the ether itself — so that on that account I altogether judge that all comets are generated in the heaven itself above the Moon, and that none of them was ever elementary. For as to the fact that the observations of Regiomontanus and Vögelin on two ancient comets, and the opinions of certain more recent men, show something different in these very [comets] of which we have obtained the investigation by the due instruments — we make no account of it; since [we may see] how much credit is to be placed in their observations, even in the very stars congenital with the world that we hold in view. And it is the work of a far subtler craftsman to discern parallaxes so minute — especially where the proper motion must at the same time be taken into consideration — than the manner of scrutinising the celestial appearances commonly used hitherto can supply. But of this whole argument of the comets Your Highness will more fully and sufficiently learn our opinion, grounded on our observations and on the demonstrations resting upon them, when the work which I am now preparing for the press on this very matter shall have been published for the public benefit. As to the observations transmitted to me by Your Highness (on which account too I most reverently render Your Highness many thanks): I compared them diligently, both among themselves and together with mine, and indeed in the distances themselves I find no discrepancy of any moment, but that your instrument yields the same as my sextants. Which is made manifest even from the single interval between Regulus and Spica of ♍ Virgo alone (because this observation is made near the extreme limits of the sextant, whence too the slighter angles of the intervals come out less doubtful). For this Your Highness's sextant yielded as 54 degrees 2 minutes; we found the same several times scarcely smaller by a sixth part of one minute (a difference all but imperceptible and of no weight) — the other distances of Your Highness too, when compared with ours in turn, attesting that the sextant which Your Highness has in use yields intervals sufficiently exact and congruent with the heaven itself. Yet I think this was scarcely the doing of him who first arranged its structure for Your Highness in imitation of mine, which he had seen here (a thing which he doubtless passed over in silence). For well enough known to me is his less-than-exact diligence in these mechanical matters, whether in investigating or in imitating them. And so I judge it rather to be ascribed to Your Highness's own industry, and to that of your mathematician Rothmann, and to the skill of the mechanical craftsman as well, searched out by experience itself. As to the meridian altitudes transmitted to me: in them I find some little discrepancy from my own findings, so much so that the declinations of the stars derived from them, in the northern [stars], are nearly everywhere in excess by two scruples, and in the southern fall short by as many — and this on the presupposition of an inclination of the equator of 38 degrees 40 minutes, which is found to result from that elevation of the pole which Rothmann wrote me from the double altitude of certain non-setting stars. But since I held it sufficiently established that the declinations of the stars investigated by me are neither in excess nor deficient by any minute (because they had been surely explored by five or six instruments variously constructed and of due size, such that in some a sixth part of a minute may conveniently be discerned), and since it was at the same time sufficiently certain to me that Your Highness too employs the greatest care and diligence in observations — especially those repeated by these newer instruments — at first I greatly wondered with myself whence this difference of two scruples between us should arise. Then, when I considered that the difference was almost everywhere the same, it at once came into my mind that it had easily come about that, either by the fault of sights not rightly fitted, two scruples were perceived too high in Your Highness's quadrant, or else by the error of a plumb-line not rightly set even in a single minute — which, assumed on both sides, would in the altitude both of the pole and of the meridian stars vary their declination by two scruples — or perhaps by both reasons. Which I gathered could easily come about in a two-cubit quadrant, from this: that even in ones double or triple the size, if not the most exact application be made both in the sights and in the plumb-line, an error of half, or even of a whole scruple, easily creeps in. For I did not judge this error to be imputed to a less-certain division, or to a deviant centre. I had it therefore in mind to warn Your Highness of these things by letter, that you might once more institute a more precise examination either of the sights, or of the plumb-line, or of both at once, whereby in the declinations of the stars, no less than in the distances, an exact agreement between Your Highness's observations and mine might be established. But while I am deprived of a fit occasion of sending a letter, and about the time of the Frankfurt fairs am hindered from writing for the reason mentioned, it has fallen out for me most happily and as I could wish that Your Highness's mathematician has meanwhile indicated that he detected that Your Highness's quadrant, on account of a fault in the plumb-line, showed the earlier altitudes everywhere greater by one scruple than was right. And if this is so (as I am wholly persuaded), it will follow that the elevation of the pole comes out smaller by one scruple, and that of the equator greater by as much — so that it is in truth at Kassel 38 degrees 41 minutes. If, then, the altitudes transmitted to me by Your Highness be applied to this, so that from each a single scruple be taken away on account of the plumb-line's error, they will agree quite exactly with my declinations of the stars. Wherefore I rejoiced greatly that, by this single scruple corrected in the fault of the plumb-line, another scruple far greater — clinging to my mind on account of this difference (though not so great a one) that intervened between our observations — has been taken away. For I judge that all celestial observations are to be instituted to an exact rule, so far as they fall within the human senses — so much so that if anything be wanting in them even by the least amount, I count it for null. But that Your Highness may institute a further comparison between your observations and mine, both as regards the intervals and as regards the meridian altitudes, or the declinations derived from them, I send Your Highness the distances of some stars — both others, and especially the more notable ones that lie along the zodiac — faithfully explored with my trigonic sextants (for so it has pleased to call them); and besides, certain meridian altitudes investigated with diverse quadrants, agreeing nevertheless among themselves — and that both about the end of the year [15]84 and the running [15]85, and also about the close of the lately-past [15]86 and the very beginning of this current [15]87 — that Your Highness may the more readily compare your altitudes with mine at the due times. Since indeed I in no way doubt that in these very years, after the instruments were again renewed, Your Highness has noted a more exact measurement of the altitudes of the stars. For Your Highness very well knows that the declinations of the stars, and on that account their altitudes too, vary somewhat from year to year — and that especially in those stars which are nearer to the equinoctial places. But the elevation of the equator to be employed in our altitudes of the stars comes to 34 degrees 5⅓ [minutes], because by frequent experiments I have found that the pole here at Uraniborg, in the place of my observation — which is the middle of the island of Hven, situated in that famous Danish Sound — is raised by 55 degrees 54⅔ scruples; which I have many times verified, both from the pole-star itself and from the other circumpolar non-setting stars, and from the stars that, by reason of a slope not too perceptible, exclude refraction. The longitudes and latitudes of the stars communicated to me by Your Highness I likewise compared, both among themselves and with mine, and found that that assignment, made mechanically from a globe, though not greatly disagreeing, yet does not everywhere correspond to the distances and altitudes scrupulously noted — though in some a very slight difference is found, as Your Highness can perceive from several comparisons which I have noted in the appended sheet. Nor indeed does it seem to me so very wonderful that longitudes and latitudes sought out mechanically on a globe from the distances and altitudes of the stars should not agree to the exact reckoning in the very scruples. Since I too have a great brass globe, perfectly round, which contains six feet in diameter, on which the ecliptic and the equator are finely divided into single scruples by transversal points, after the manner usual with us in the other instruments; nevertheless I scarcely promise myself everywhere a fixed certainty within one or one-and-a-half scruples in the places of the stars to be investigated by this globe from sure observations — so much do the things that are worked out mechanically, albeit with the greatest diligence, come to be set after geometrical and arithmetical exactitude. But in the very longitudes of the stars transmitted to me by Your Highness — although they be weighed by a more exact balance, geometrically and through the numbers of the sines — I observe that for the most part they are reckoned five or six scruples, or thereabouts, further from the equinox than [in] our observations. In the latitudes, however, there is found hardly a greater difference than what could be caused either from the longitudes assumed a little otherwise, or from a different determination of the obliquity of the ecliptic, but chiefly from the declinations, which differ from ours by two minutes through the fault of your plumb-line. To be sure, this difference, even of the longitudes, much less of the latitudes, is of no very great moment, if we compare the notations of certain ancients and moderns. And I cannot sufficiently wonder that Your Highness has come so close with your instruments to the places of the stars — especially since I can gather that Your Highness, in Aldebaran, or whatever other [star] you used for a foundation, detected its right ascension from its transit across the meridian, the time found by a clock and compared with the Sun. Which method always seemed to me less safe. For although I too have four such clocks of diverse size and exact structure, which indicate not only the single first scruples [minutes] but also the seconds, yet in so subtle a business I have judged it vain to place trust in them, since 4 seconds at least — which in many hours easily slip away — bring in an error of one first scruple in longitude. Add that those clocks, even though most diligently wrought from little brass wheels, are sensibly liable to the change of the air. I say nothing of the weight not being equal when it hangs higher or lower, by reason of the altered length of the cord by which it is attached, which little by little adds something to the weight — which, though very slight, yet brings in, in the seconds, a deviation not to be neglected. Wherefore, since I had perceived that this business does not succeed safely enough by this method, and was not ignorant that no slight difficulty underlies it — that even the true distance of one fixed star from the equinox might be investigated, which once known, the rest [might] either by their distances from one another, or by the equatorial differences, or even by the Hipparchan zodiacal armillae which we have in use (worked out, however, by a surer compendium than by the ancients), the rest (I say), as many as one pleased, might easily be established — and since I judged that the method which the ancients, Hipparchus above all and Ptolemy, used, and in a later age Nicolaus Copernicus, by the Moon (the partner of day and night, applied by instruments both to the Sun and to the stars), is not without its difficulties and is less exempt from error: for although we grant that the place of the Sun, with exquisite diligence applied, is, as to longitude, knowable within one or another scruple, and its interval to the Moon, and the Moon's to the stars, can be given sufficiently exactly — yet the lunar parallaxes, which are not so precisely known, in either observation bring in some variation from the truth. Add that this manner of observing is not conveniently done unless the Sun be low about the horizon, where its apparent place is other than its true place which appeared at the meridian — and that not merely by reason of parallax, but much more on account of refraction, of which I more fully indicated my opinion in my earlier letter to Your Highness; and I understand from Your Highness's reply that you too have detected, both in the Sun and in the other planets and fixed stars, a sensible refraction in the lower situation — on which account I much congratulate you over our mutual observations on both sides. For neither the ancients nor the more recent men, so far as I know, have noted this in the sightings of the stars, or duly guarded against it. This way, I say, having been laid out by our predecessors — since it was not permitted to walk safely enough along it for the aforesaid and several other reasons — I judged that I must enter upon a more convenient and safer path, which should lead to the exact longitudes of the stars; and I found none fitter and more exact than that which, from the opportunity offered us, was presented in the year 1582, when we beheld the star of Venus, being the Evening Star, clearly appearing by day. For then we observed Venus at her exact distance from the Sun, from 24 February right up to the middle of April, on more occasions than can easily be believed, in the daytime most diligently — and that not only in the Sun's lower situation, but many times when it was either at the meridian, or at least removed beyond it by one, or one-and-a-half, hours, and ♀ Venus stood very high along with it — so much so that her very transit across the meridian, with the Sun still much elevated, was many times detected by us, both as to altitude and as to distance from the Sun. Wherefore, since the place of the Sun was established sufficiently exactly both from the meridian altitudes and through the restoration of its motion made by us to the rule from the preceding years, then from the declination of Venus and of the Sun — the exquisite and mutually-agreeing distance of both being given through the sextant (so that the Sun illuminated one sight with its whole magnitude, while through the other Venus was seen on both sides through the little slits parallel to the upper cylinder) — the place of the star Venus was surely disclosed. Which place, at night-time, as soon as some one of the fixed stars near the zodiac was offered to view, applied by like reasoning through the interval taken of it from this [Venus] — account being first had of the proper motion meanwhile transacted — disclosed the place of that same star; and this was often done in diverse stars as well, since the Sun, Venus, and the fixed stars observed were almost everywhere at so great an altitude above the horizon that they excluded all refraction. And if there was sometimes any very slight one, it was guarded against from other observations made about this matter. By this infallible method, then, I attained most happily, as I wished, to an exact knowledge of the longitude of the stars at that time — manifold experiment recurring to the same result, and the distances of those same stars from one another confirming the same. I also everywhere employed the parallaxes both of the Sun and of Venus, in so far as they were varied through the intervals of the hours, that the reckoning might stand the more exact. Nay, I did not hesitate that same year to investigate the parallaxes of Venus herself, that it might more surely be clear whether she revolved about the Sun, or below it. Which thing I also dared to try in the star of Mars at the end of the previous [year] and about the beginnings of the same year, when it was seen acronychal and high in ♋ Cancer, a northern latitude too lifting it higher — that I might search out whether it produced a greater diversity of aspect [parallax] than the Sun; which would be necessary, if the Copernican hypothesis consisted in the truth of the matter. For by it Mars in opposition (☍) is rendered nearer to the Earth by almost a third part than the Sun himself. Whence too it then necessarily induces a greater diversity of parallax; and consequently the orbs of the revolutions of Mars are by no means constituted wholly above the solar orb, as the Ptolemaic hypotheses hold. And therefore either the Earth must be turned by an annual motion, by which all the epicycles of the planets are excused [done away with]; or another disposition of the celestial revolutions, than has been practised hitherto, must be sought out. But these things, now said outside my purpose, will another time, God willing, be set forth more clearly and copiously. Since therefore (to return to the matter) by the aforesaid, in no way doubtful, reckoning I had come, from the Sun through Venus in the year indicated, to a knowledge of the longitude of several of the more notable fixed stars about the zodiac (their latitude easily thrusting itself in along with it); and since from certain other observations, taken in the year [15]85 about the autumnal equinox — at which time the star of Venus, being then the Morning Star, was likewise seen by day, and that at almost a like distance from the Sun and the Earth, and likewise at a like altitude above the horizon as in the year [15]82 (which greatly furthered the certainty of the business) — I had proved all things over again the more exactly in reverse; hence I have worked to restore, as to length and breadth, the places of the remaining fixed stars too, both within and without the zodiac, and at the same time to investigate through these the appearances of all the planets, no opportunity of clear weather being hitherto let slip. Now I find in very many stars the very thing which Your Highness writes me — that they have not only longitudes other than the ancients' traditions exhibit (even with the more correct precession of the equinox added), but also latitudes much otherwise, in some less, in some indeed more — whereas it is held for ascertained, beyond all doubt, that these necessarily remain unaltered in every age from the most ancient astronomers, because the motion of the eighth sphere takes place about the poles of the ecliptic. Whence Your Highness, rightly led into wonder at that variation, clemently asks also my judgement on this matter — namely whether I deem it consonant that there is in the several fixed stars some peculiar motion of their own, by which they change their latitude too in diverse ways. On which matter I shall now set forth my opinion to Your Highness in brief. The places of all the fixed stars, from the first origin of the world, in each and all, as regards one another, I judge to remain wholly unaltered, far beyond all doubt. And this is easily proved from this: that all the fixed stars which Ptolemy enumerates in the first chapter of the seventh book — [stated] by Abrachis or Hipparchus, some 260 years before him, to have stood in one straight line — these same [stars] also in his own age remained in the same situation as regards one another. And since these same, and also those which Ptolemy afterward adds (that he might leave to posterity a handle for experimenting), are observed even in this age — 1400 years having elapsed since him — to retain exactly the very same position which the same Ptolemy enumerates (which could by no means be the case if one star changed its longitude or latitude, with respect to its own sphere, otherwise than another), it will be easy to reason that all the fixed stars subsist in the same situation, as regards themselves, in every age, without any diverse and disorderly change of latitude or longitude. But as to the fact that certain fixed stars, even among the more notable, are found which obtain a latitude much other than Hipparchus and Ptolemy noted — it is likely that this was committed partly through their less-exact observation, partly through the negligence of so many copyists who perhaps were ignorant of the subtlety of the business. For the printing art was scarcely devised before 150 years ago, and previously, over so great an interval of time, by copying so often, many things could be assigned faultily, especially in the characters of numbers — since even in this very printing art, unless diligent correction be applied, an easy slip is admitted, especially in this matter. There is added this too, that by certain proofs it is confirmed from the heavens, in very many fixed stars (especially those about the zodiac), through that change of the inclination of the ecliptic to the equator which has been made from the times of Timocharis, Hipparchus, and Ptolemy down to now, that the latitudes of all the fixed stars have likewise been successively varied, in proportion as their longitudes draw nearer or further from the tropical places. Which indeed seems wonderful, since it is otherwise surely established that the motion of the eighth sphere takes place about the poles of the ecliptic, [and] that the latitudes of the stars are not diminished otherwise than by this minute difference of the obliquity of the Zodiac-bearer [the signifer/ecliptic]. But since I understand from the letters of Your Highness's mathematician Rothmann that he is engaged in the same opinion with me in this part, I have written to him more fully about this business, and have confirmed [him] in the opinion conceived, through more certain observations of very many stars, the intervening doubts being removed. Hence Your Highness will understand some occasion of variation in the latitudes of certain stars. But if any greater one coincides, this must needs come about, as I said before, either from the ancients' less-certain notation, or from the copyists' slip. For the rest, that Your Highness may more clearly know in what situation the places of the fixed stars have been investigated by me, in the aforesaid manner, I have described the longitudes and latitudes of certain principal ones, especially those along the zodiac, which I have reduced to the beginning of this passing year, just as these have been detected by our more exact observations, that Your Highness may the more readily compare those places with your own. I have not judged it worth the trouble to add the assignment of more stars, since even from fewer — nay, from a single one rightly determined — all the rest easily become known to anyone who has convenient and sure instruments to hand. But the interval of motion from the year [15]82 (in which I obtained, as I indicated above, the fittest observations in these matters) I have borrowed neither from the Copernican change of the equinoxes (which he derived from Spica of ♍ Virgo observed, into the first star of ♈ Aries, retaining the Ptolemaic interval of longitude and without consulting the heaven), nor from the Alphonsine reckoning, but I have completed this business from the very observation of Copernicus made on the said Spica of ♍ Virgo in the year 1525, and compared with our more recent one. For since Copernicus asserts that in that year the meridian altitude of Spica was 27 degrees 0 minutes, and the pole of Frauenburg is elevated 54 degrees 22¼ minutes — as, three years ago, I had a trial made by my own exquisite instrument, through a certain student sent thither by me with the instrument [organum] — the declination of Spica was then 8 degrees 37¾ minutes; and therefore, her latitude being assumed at 1 degree 59½ minutes, such as we found (smaller only by half a scruple than Copernicus's position, which alters this business not at all, since I presuppose the same in both cases), the longitude of Spica for that time comes out at 17 degrees 14 minutes of ♎ Libra — although Copernicus himself sets it 7 scruples further on, and that doubtless by the fault of an elevation of the pole less exquisitely known, which it is credible that he investigated through the Sun, from the greatest and least altitude, by a reckoning fallacious on account of refraction in the lower situation. And since at this time Spica declines, by our observations, 8 degrees 57 minutes precisely, and, the former latitude being assumed, her longitude falls at 18 degrees 4 minutes of ♎ Libra, the eighth sphere has, from Copernicus's observation down to now, over an interval of 61 years, advanced 50 minutes. Wherefore in 73 years (provided not fewer) it accomplishes one degree in our age — whereas, nevertheless, the Copernican anticipation of the equinoxes now demands an almost slowest advance of the fixed stars, such that they accomplish a single degree in nearly a hundred years. Hence, then, it appears how much one is to adhere to the Copernican findings about the precession of the equinoxes, whereby the motion of the eighth sphere is excused (to say nothing now of the Sun and the rest) — however subtly devised by that incomparable astronomer. But the exact knowledge of the apparent motion of the eighth sphere I judge scarcely thoroughly searchable by any mortal absolutely. Since [the sphere] has as yet completed no whole revolution, and the little portions of the circuit handed down to us from Timocharis through Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Albategnius [al-Battānī], and Copernicus do not suffice — nor, if they did furnish it, do they stand scrupulously enough [consistent]. Since therefore by experience, in the aforesaid manner, the anticipation of the equinoxes about these times has become known, it was easy to transfer the proportional motion from the year [15]82 into this or any other [years] of our age. For it is not consonant that any sensible inequality in this part should occur in the lifetime of one man. But that these places of the stars still agree, my observations, made in the past year and in this very [year] from the beginning of January, sufficiently declare. As to the fact that in Your Highness's longitudes of the stars — [computed] geometrically from the intervals and declinations reduced to numbers — for the most part five or six scruples, or thereabouts, are in excess, this will easily be excused, if it be established that Your Highness then, in delimiting these, used a place of the Sun [computed] when the Sun was about the vernal equinox, in the ascending part of the ecliptic. For there, by reason of that single scruple which was caused by the plumb-line's fault, the declination of the Sun was rendered higher by two scruples than it ought to have been, and therefore its place [was] further on by five [scruples] in longitude. Which necessarily redounded also onto the fixed stars adapted to the Sun's place — although about the autumnal equinox, in the descending part of the ecliptic, this matter holds in the contrary way. But that Your Highness may see some specimen of our observations in the other wandering stars too, I add the true places of all, such as have been detected by my instruments by accurate remark within a single revolution of the primum mobile, three times in this very month of January from the beginning of the new year. Which, though it otherwise rather rarely happens, has nevertheless been done by me several times in earlier years, especially in the preceding year in October, when ☿ Mercury appeared in the east for many days. The Sun's motion, sought out from the situation of Venus through the fixed stars — since at this time of year it can less surely be had through the declinations, refraction also hindering — I have verified. For in the daytime Venus is, even before noon in these days, or at noon itself and before, seen clearly enough through the purer serenity [of the sky]. But the impediments of refraction and of the parallax of the Sun and of Venus, explored beforehand at a like altitude, I have removed. Likewise I did in Mercury too, since it was observed very low, scarcely 5 degrees high. In the rest there was no need of this delimitation, because they were taken higher up from the heavens. But I have added, for the same times of our observation, the Alphonsine and Copernican places of the planets, that Your Highness might at a single glance the more easily perceive the discrepancy of each calculation from the heaven itself — although they often go astray much more than now. But I ask Your Highness, with what prayers I can, that you be willing to communicate these our observations to no one, and to take care that they do not become public — especially as regards the longitudes and latitudes of the fixed stars, which on that account too I had noted separately, on a sheet of their own. For Your Highness knows full well how much time, labour, diligence, and expense is required before one may attain an exact knowledge of this matter. And indeed to no other in all Germany, or elsewhere, of whatever condition he might be, would I communicate these my findings; and I have suppressed them hitherto, although solicited by many on this score. For most men, since they will themselves perform nothing excellent in these matters, do not blush to adorn themselves with the findings and labours of others, and to vend them as their own, not without grave injury to the author. But I doubt not that Your Highness will reserve these for yourself alone — just as I too make no one a sharer of the observations communicated to me by Your Highness. But in all this astronomical treatment there would be much more, on which I would most gladly treat more copiously with Your Highness — but neither the paper nor the convenience now permits it. I have besides accurate and manifold observations of many years, not only in the fixed stars, but also in all the wandering ones, as often as they shone visible to us, which I preserve in the place of a glorious possession (for they have been procured by no few vigils, and by no small labour and diligence). And I am working to compare these with the findings of others, and at length to bring them forth for the public use, that the whole science of the stars may exactly satisfy the celestial appearances. And I have given it in charge to this man, that he should set forth to Your Highness, or to your mathematician, in my name, more things than it was convenient now to write. These things I have now written to Your Highness the more at length, partly because I know that Your Highness takes intense delight in these astronomical matters, partly that I might recompense the delay hitherto admitted in replying by the very copiousness in writing back. But lest I seem to exceed measure in both, and burden Your Highness with too great prolixity, I shall now make an end, and shall indicate the rest — if anything here be wanting — to Your Highness's mathematician by letter. This one thing at least, in the place of a colophon, I humbly and earnestly beseech of Your Highness: that with all your sinews you not cease to strive, more and more hereafter, to vindicate this divine science of the stars from the faults with which it teems, and to render it more cultivated — which indeed you have hitherto, of your own accord, done excellently. For in this way Your Highness will preserve, among all posterity that shall venerate so sublime and divine a science, a most welcome and most lasting memory of yourself — which will be the more perpetual and excellent because it is procured not from earthly things, liable to perishing, which the common crowd looks up to. Your Highness knows that this study is not for men of slenderer fortune, nor can it be cultivated by them in the due manner, even if they should most greatly desire it and understand it. And since there is none besides Your Highness, among all the princely men of Europe, whom God has adorned with this heroic genius, so as to understand so arduous a science more deeply, and to know how to vindicate it from errors and to restore it by every means — it surely becomes Your Highness, before other princely men, to bear an especial care of this science (most worthy of a princely and of a prince's knowledge), and to strive with all effort at its restoration and its preservation to posterity. On this account too let Your Highness hold your mathematician Christoph Rothmann commended before others, because I recognise by many tokens that he bestows in these exercises a most skilful and faithful labour, and is a most eager investigator of the exact truth. But I suggest these things to Your Highness superfluously, since of your own accord you are of a mind most inclined to the enlarging of astronomical matters. In which purpose may God, the author of this admirable and most beautiful celestial machine, confirm Your Highness as long as possible, with a happy issue of all the goods of mind and body. May Your Highness fare well, and not disdain to confer further with me about this very ethereal and sublime business, and to revisit me with your letters. Given at Uraniborg, the 18th day of January, in the year [15]87. Most devoted to Your Highness, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe at <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven) → <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince whose Kassel observatory was Tycho's principal scientific correspondent. The opening titulature lists his comital styles — <i>Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda</i> — kept as in the source.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Printed by Tycho himself as Letter [I] in the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg 1596); reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. MS witness given in the dataset: Vienna, ÖNB Cod. Vindob. lat. 10689.10.</li><li><b>DATELINE CRUX (header date).</b> The body subscription reads <b>\"Datae Vraniburgi 18. die Ianuarij, Anno 87\"</b> = <b>18 January 1587 (O.S.)</b>. The dataset's `label`/catalogue field reads \"1587. 1. januar.\" (1 January). Per protocol §4, the header follows the <b>as-written dateline (18 January)</b> and appends the catalogued date. The body text independently corroborates a mid-/late-January writing: Tycho says the new observations were \"detected... three times in this very month of January from the beginning of the new year\" and reduced \"to the beginning of this passing year [15]87\" — consistent with the 18th, not the 1st. The \"1 January\" label is most likely a catalogue normalisation to the year-start, not a substantiated dateline; the discrepancy is flagged, not reconciled. <b>Calendar:</b> O.S. (Julian); Denmark and Hesse both kept Old Style in 1587.</li><li><b>Textual note — the stray capital \"A\".</b> Throughout the source block, isolated uppercase <b>A</b> characters appear attached to or following words (e.g. <i>S. P.A</i>, <i>accepiA</i>, <i>responderimA</i>, <i>Anno 1582A</i>). These are <b>apparatus/footnote anchor artifacts</b> carried over from the digitised critical-edition base (Dreyer's variant-note sigla), not part of Tycho's Latin. They are reproduced <b>as printed</b> in the Source block per protocol §4(2) and silently disregarded in the Translation. Likewise <i>tCelsitud.</i> (for <i>Celsitud.</i>) and the run-together final lines (<i>Datae Vraniburgi18. die...</i>) are transcription artifacts of the base file; word-spacing is restored only in the translation.</li><li><b>Persons.</b> <i>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</i> (Udalricus Dux Megapolensis, 1527–1603) and his consort <i>Elizabeth</i> (Elizabeth of Denmark, 1524–1586, sister of Frederik II — her recently-reported death \"to the great grief of all\" dates the visit to 1586); <i>Duke Sigismund</i> (Sigismund August of Mecklenburg, the brother's son). <i>\"Our Most Serene Queen\"</i> = <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b>, queen of Denmark, daughter of Duke Ulrich (hence Tycho's \"the Most Serene Queen his daughter\"). <i>Christoph Rothmann</i> (Christophorus Rothmannus) = Wilhelm IV's mathematician at Kassel, repeatedly named. <i>Regiomontanus</i> and <i>Vögelin</i> (Johannes Vogelin) — 15th–16th-c. cometary observers. <i>Copernicus</i>, <i>Ptolemy</i>, <i>Hipparchus</i> (<i>Abrachis</i> = Arabic-derived form of Hipparchus), <i>Timocharis</i>, <i>Albategnius</i> (al-Battānī) — the chain of authorities for the eighth-sphere motion.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Cassellae</i> → <b>Kassel</b>; <i>Haffnia</i> → <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Francofurtum / Nundinae Francofordianae</i> → <b>Frankfurt / the Frankfurt (book-)fairs</b>; <i>Fruenburgum</i> → <b>Frauenburg/Frombork</b> (Copernicus's observing site); <i>Fretum Danicum</i> → <b>the Danish Sound</b> (Øresund); <i>Insula Huena</i> → <b>the island of Hven</b>.</li><li><b>Astronomical technical terms</b> (per glossary §5.1–5.2): <i>parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>ascensio recta</i> → <b>right ascension</b>; <i>longitudo/latitudo</i> → <b>(celestial) longitude/latitude</b>; <i>eleuatio Poli / Aequatoris</i> → <b>elevation of the pole / of the equator</b>; <i>altitudo Meridiana</i> → <b>meridian altitude</b>; <i>praecessio aequinoctiorum / anticipatio aequinoctiorum</i> → <b>precession / anticipation of the equinoxes</b>; <i>octaua Sphaera</i> → <b>the eighth sphere</b>; <i>primi mobilis reuolutio</i> → <b>a revolution of the primum mobile</b>; <i>aspectus diuersitas</i> (here = parallax in the older idiom) → <b>diversity of aspect [parallax]</b>, flagged; <i>acronychus</i> → <b>acronychal</b> (rising at sunset, opposite the Sun); <i>restitutio (motus)</i> → <b>restoration (of the motion)</b>; <i>instauratio</i> → <b>restoration</b> (Tycho's programme, §5.1). Instruments: <i>Sextans (Trigonicus)</i> → <b>(trigonic) sextant</b>; <i>Quadrans bicubitalis</i> → <b>two-cubit quadrant</b>; <i>pinnacidia / pinnulae</i> → <b>sights</b>; <i>perpendiculum</i> → <b>plumb-line</b>; <i>Armillae Hipparchicae Zodiacales</i> → <b>Hipparchan zodiacal armillae</b>; <i>Globus Orichalcicus</i> → <b>brass globe</b>; <i>Organum</i> → <b>instrument</b>; <i>Horologia</i> → <b>clocks</b>.</li><li><b>Units.</b> <i>partes / gradus</i> → <b>degrees</b>; <i>scrupula prima</i> → <b>(first) scruples</b> = arcminutes; <i>scrupula secunda</i> → <b>seconds (of arc/time)</b>. Tycho uses <i>scrupulum</i> for both arcminute and, of clocks, second; rendered \"scruple(s)\" with a bracketed gloss \"[minutes]\" at first occurrence, per the source's own usage, not silently modernised.</li><li><b>Astrology / hypothesis.</b> <i>Coperniana Hypothesis / Hypotheses Ptolemaicae</i> → <b>the Copernican hypothesis / the Ptolemaic hypotheses</b>. The Mars-parallax passage is the celebrated test that led Tycho away from both pure systems toward his own; <i>\"vel rotari Terram annuo motu… vel aliam… dispositionem\"</i> preserves his open, hedged disjunction (\"either… or…\") — not silently resolved to the geo-heliocentric system he had not yet here announced.</li><li><b>Symbols (named in translation, glyph kept in Source):</b> <b>♍</b> Virgo, <b>♎</b> Libra, <b>♈</b> Aries, <b>♋</b> Cancer, <b>♀</b> Venus, <b>☿</b> Mercury, <b>☍</b> opposition. Per §5.3.</li><li><b>\"Pyronomic / Spagyric\" passage.</b> <i>Pyronomici labores</i> → <b>pyronomic labours</b> (fire-/furnace-craft, i.e. distillation/alchemy at Uraniborg); <i>Artificia Spagyrica</i> → <b>Spagyric arts</b> (Paracelsian chemistry) — period terms kept, not modernised to \"chemistry.\"</li><li><b>Numeric readings carried verbatim</b> (not \"corrected\"): Regulus–Spica interval 54° 2′; Kassel equator-inclination 38° 40′, corrected in-letter to 38° 41′ after the one-scruple plumb-line fault; Uraniborg pole-height 55° 54⅔′ and equator-elevation 34° 5⅓′; Frauenburg pole 54° 22¼′; Copernicus's Spica meridian altitude 27° 0′ and declination 8° 37¾′, latitude 1° 59½′, longitude ♎ 17° 14′ (Tycho) vs. 7′ further (Copernicus); Tycho's own Spica declination 8° 57′, longitude ♎ 18° 4′; eighth-sphere advance 50′ over 61 years → 1° per 73 years. The fractions (⅓, ⅔, ¼, ¾, ½) are reproduced as in the base text.</li><li><b>No negations added or dropped.</b> The courtesy-laden excuse-clauses (<i>\"non est quod… existimet, vel incuria… vel data opera factum esse\"</i>; <i>\"nullatenus dubitem\"</i>; <i>\"nemini communicare velit, cauereque ne publicae fiant\"</i>) were each counted and reproduced with the same polarity; the secrecy request and the \"to no one… would I communicate\" pledge are rendered without weakening.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one named crux is the <b>dateline (18 January per the body subscription vs. the catalogued \"1 January\")</b>, resolved in the header per protocol toward the as-written 18th and flagged above. A minor lexical point — <i>scrupulum</i> doing double duty for arcminute and time-second — is glossed rather than silently disambiguated. The pervasive stray \"A\" apparatus-sigla are noted and disregarded; they do not affect sense.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — dateline (18 Jan vs catalogued 1 Jan) genuinely resolved to the as-written 18th and internally corroborated; residue a glossed term + 'A' sigla.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter — no Dreyer slice available, so Dreyer collation was not possible (per brief, capped at grade B regardless of outcome). Audited published_english against source_text alone: full sentence-by-sentence read of the entire letter (Tycho to Wilhelm IV, Uraniborg, 18 January 1587 — comet/nova parallax agreement, sextant and quadrant calibration against Kassel's instruments, the Spica precession-of-equinoxes calculation from Copernicus's 1525 observation, the 1582/1585 Venus-transit method for fixed-star longitudes, the Mars-opposition parallax test bearing on the Copernican hypothesis, confidentiality request, and closing). Checked every proper name (Ulrich/Elizabeth/Sigismund of Mecklenburg, Queen Sophie as Ulrich's daughter, Rothmann, Regiomontanus, Vogelin, Hipparchus/Abrachis, Ptolemy, Timocharis, Copernicus, Aldebaran, Regulus, Spica, Frauenburg, Kassel, Uraniborg/Hven), every date (1582, 1584, 1585, 1586, 1587, 1525, October, 24 February, 18 January), and every numeric value (54°2', 6ths of a minute, 38°40'/41', 34°5⅓', 55°54⅔', 6-foot globe, 27°0', 54°22¼', 8°37¾', 1°59½', 17°14', 8°57', 18°4', 61 years, 73 years, 260 years, 1400 years, 150 years, 4 comets, 5 degrees, 4 clocks/4 seconds). All matched the Latin without alteration. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["Vindob.10689.10 Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliohek, Cod. Vindob. 10689.10."], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15861014001", "next": "brahe-t_15870911001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15861014001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15870911001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for the long delay in replying, attributing it to distance and infrequent mail opportunities, and begins addressing the Landgrave's astronomical inquiries.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870101001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870117001", "d": "1587-01-17", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Segeberg", "t": "Heinrich Rantzau → Tycho Brahe — Segeberg, 17 January 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>EXEMPLAR EPISTOLAe NOBILISSIMI ET AMPLISSIMI VIRI, DOMINI HENRICI RANZOVII DE BREDENBERGA, VICARII REGII IN HOLSATIA ET ADIUNCTIS PROUINCIJS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE DATAe, QUA SEQUENTIUM EDITIONEM EXPETIT ET IMPETRAT.</p><p>PERLEGI vir Clariss. et Nobiliss. diligenter literas Illustrissimi Principis LANDTGRAVII ad te transmissas, vna cum responsione tua semel atque iterum rescripta, quarum lectio mihi accidit iucundissima, tum quod plurima cognitu vtilia, et arti Astronomicae plenius excolendae idonea in ijs contineri percepi, tum quod a Principe, quo nemo inter reliquos hoc tempore Astronomiam magis colit, atque a te nostri aeui praestantissimo Mathematico vltro citroque sunt conscriptae. Ac sane non possum non gratulari nostro Seculo, quod DEVS optimus maximus non solum Viros Nobiles ac Plebeios, sed etiam aliquot Principes excitet, qui huius scientiae Studio delectentur. Non enim vt vulgus opinatur, huius Artis cognitio omni certitudine caret, sed certis fundamentis et Obseruationibus nititur, humanoque generi imprimis est necessaria. Quo circa toto etiam Coelo errant, qui hanc ab otiosis ingenijs excogitatam esse arbitrantur. Si quis enim eius prima indagabit initia, depraehendet a Patribus eam profectam, ac deinde ad posteros propagatam, longoque rerum vsu ac Obseruationibus crebris et diuturnis tandem confirmatam esse. Ne autem huius Scientiae vtilitates longius persequar, tantummodo huius Doctrinae contemtores ob oculos sibi ponere velint, quantis in tenebris versaremur, si Scientia de motibus Solis, Lunae et Stellarum, item de Aequinoctijs ac alijs, careremus. Huius enim Doctrinae, si expertes essemus, praestantissima Corpora Caelestia non secus ac bruta animalia aspiceremus. Quod ad Doctrinam de praedictionibus attinet, etsi ingenue quidem fatendum est, non semper euentus praedictionibus respondere: Tamen eius rei culpa Scientiae Astrologiae minime imputanda, sed potius imperfectae eius cognitioni. Tum etiam considerandum est, quod DEVS totius Orbis arbiter ac moderator non semper alligatus sit causis secundis, sed multa pro sua Diuina Sapientia ita disponat, vt alios, atque nos putaramus, euentus consequantur. Neque tamen propterea tota Ars adspernanda est. Proinde inprimis huius Artis tam vtilis et necessariae cultores laude digni sunt, tum quod Doctrinam conseruent, ex cuius traditione, vt ad caeterarum Scientiarum Doctores, nulla ad eos non solum redundat vtilitas, siue lucri alicuius perceptio, sed etiam vilis vulgi contemptus. At tu longe aliter de hac sublimi Scientia; praesertim quo ad motuum conseruationem iudicas. Nactus enim in eum vsum peculiarem et idoneam Insulam, cursus Coelestium Corporum maxima diligentia obseruas, Instrumentis in eum vsum excogitatis et confectis quamplurimis, magnitudine forma et artificio inaestimabilibus. Quin et ex illa Insula quam incolis, aliqui Libri Astronomici tuo Labore et Industria iam publicam lucem viderunt, speramusque indies plures tanquam ex Equo Troiano prodituros, qui insigne Doctrinae tuae testimonium toti posteritati patefacient. Nam et ij, qui editi sunt, non solum iam a Viris Principibus et Doctis, magno cum applausu excipiuntur ac adprobantur, sed et illi venturae posteritati erunt gratissimi, nominisque tui praedicationem in omnia aeui Secula propagabunt. Subsequuturos itaque idipsum luculentius facturos non ambigo. Sed vt ad institutum Vir Doctiss. reuertar, cum te iterum quoddam opus Mathematicum sub praelo habere cognouerim, non possum non te adhortari, vt Illustrissimi Principis LANDTGRAVII ad te scriptas Literas, tuasque ad eum, tum ad Excellentiss. suum Mathematicum, et si quas praeterea habes (vt te habere non dubito) Clarissimorum et Doctissimorum Virorum tecum de hac re conferentes Literas, vna cum tua ad singulas responsione, Operi quod prae manibus habes subiungere velis. Id enim mihi inprimis conueniens videtur, non solum, quia ad Astronomiae penitiorem culturam, sed etiam quia proprie ad eam materiam, quam in eo Opere tractas, plurimum istae Literae faciant, magnamque cum ea non solum affinitatem habeant, sed illam plenius etiam illustret. Hoc si feceris, non tantum mihi, sed reliquis etiam huius Artis studiosis rem praestabis longe gratissimam. Et si tandem etiam in calce adieceris resolutionem Quaestionis eius, quam non ita diu tibi per literas proposui, de contracta Solari, vt a plurimis putatur, Sphaera, feceris et hac in parte cum mihi, tum alijs rem pergratam, neque vti spero inutilem. Haec breuibus a te petere volui, nec diffido quin votis nostris et aliorum sis hac in parte satisfacturus.</p><p>Vale et Amicitiae nostrae memoriam, vt sedulo conserues, peto. Iterum vale.<br>Datae ex Arce Regia Segeberga 17 Ianuarij. Anno Salutiferi partus 1587.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A COPY OF A LETTER FROM THE MOST NOBLE AND MOST EMINENT MAN, MASTER HEINRICH RANTZAU OF BREDENBERG, ROYAL VICAR IN HOLSTEIN AND THE ADJOINING PROVINCES, ADDRESSED TO TYCHO BRAHE, IN WHICH HE ASKS FOR — AND OBTAINS — THE PUBLICATION OF WHAT FOLLOWS.</b></p><p>I have read through carefully, most renowned and most noble Sir, the letters of the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave that were sent to you, together with your reply written back time and again; and the reading of them proved most delightful to me — both because I perceived that very many things useful to know, and apt for the fuller cultivation of the art of Astronomy, were contained in them, and because they were composed back and forth by a Prince than whom no one else at this time cultivates Astronomy more, and by you, the most outstanding Mathematician of our age. And indeed I cannot but congratulate our Century, that God Best and Greatest raises up not only Noblemen and commoners, but even several Princes, to take delight in the study of this science. For the knowledge of this Art does not, as the common crowd supposes, lack all certainty, but rests upon sure foundations and Observations, and is in the first place necessary to the human race. Wherefore those too wander by a whole Heaven [astray] who judge that it was thought up by idle wits. For if anyone shall search out its first beginnings, he will discover that it set out from the Fathers, and was thereafter handed on to posterity, and at last confirmed by long practice of affairs and by frequent and long-continued Observations. But that I may not pursue the uses of this Science any further, let only the despisers of this Doctrine set before their own eyes in what great darkness we should be living, were we to lack the Science of the motions of the Sun, the Moon, and the Stars, and likewise of the Equinoxes and the rest. For if we were without this Doctrine, we should look upon the most excellent Heavenly Bodies no otherwise than the brute beasts do. As for the Doctrine concerning predictions, although it must indeed be candidly admitted that outcomes do not always answer to the predictions, yet the blame for this is by no means to be charged to the Science of Astrology, but rather to its imperfect knowledge. And then it must also be considered that God, the arbiter and governor of the whole World, is not always bound to second causes, but disposes many things, according to His Divine Wisdom, in such a way that outcomes follow other than we had supposed. Nor on that account, however, is the whole Art to be despised. Accordingly the cultivators of this Art, so useful and so necessary, are above all worthy of praise — both because they preserve a Doctrine from the handing-down of which (as happens to the teachers of the other Sciences) there redounds to them not only no benefit, nor the gaining of any profit, but even the contempt of the base crowd. But you judge far otherwise of this sublime Science — especially as regards the preservation of the [celestial] motions. For having obtained an Island peculiarly fitted for that use, you observe the courses of the Heavenly Bodies with the greatest diligence, with very many Instruments devised and made for that use, of inestimable size, form, and craftsmanship. Indeed, from that Island which you inhabit, certain Astronomical Books have by your Labour and Industry now seen the public light, and we hope that day by day more will come forth, as it were out of the Trojan Horse, which will disclose to all posterity a signal testimony of your Doctrine. For even those that have been published are not only received and approved already, with great applause, by Princely and Learned Men, but they will also be most welcome to the posterity to come, and will carry the proclamation of your name forward into all the ages of time. I do not doubt, then, that those to follow will do the very same more brilliantly still. But, that I may return to my purpose, most learned Sir: since I have learned that you once again have a certain Mathematical work under the press, I cannot but urge you to be willing to append, to the Work you have in hand, the Letters written to you by the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave, and yours to him, and to his most excellent Mathematician, and any others besides that you have (as I do not doubt you have) — the Letters of most renowned and most learned Men conferring with you on this matter, together with your reply to each. For this seems to me especially fitting, not only because those Letters bear very greatly upon the deeper cultivation of Astronomy, but also because they bear properly upon the very subject which you treat in that Work, and have a great kinship with it, and indeed illustrate it more fully. If you shall do this, you will render a most welcome service not only to me, but also to the rest who are devoted to this Art. And if at last you shall also add at the close the resolution of that Question which not long ago I put to you by letter — concerning the contracted Solar Sphere, as it is held by very many — you will in this part too do a thing most gratifying both to me and to others, and, as I hope, not unprofitable. These few things I wished to ask of you, and I do not distrust that in this part you will satisfy our wishes and those of others.</p><p>Farewell, and I ask that you diligently keep the memory of our Friendship. Again, farewell.<br>Given from the Royal Castle of Segeberg, 17 January. In the Year of the Salvation-bringing Birth 1587.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Dominus Henricus Ranzovius de Bredenberga, Vicarius Regius in Holsatia</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henricus Ranzovius, 1526–1598), the Danish crown's <i>Statthalter</i> (royal governor / <i>Vicarius Regius</i>) in the duchies of Holstein and Schleswig, lord of <b>Breitenburg</b> (<i>Bredenberga</i>), and the foremost German-Danish humanist patron of the age — bibliophile, prolific author, and a notable promoter of astrology. He wrote from his castle at Segeberg. The identification is secure from the rubric's own titulature.</li><li><b>Document type &amp; function.</b> This is the <b>prefatory / dedicatory letter that opens Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596)</b> — the volume of the Tycho–Hesse-Kassel correspondence. The rubric (<i>QUA SEQUENTIUM EDITIONEM EXPETIT ET IMPETRAT</i>, \"in which he asks for — and obtains — the publication of what follows\") frames the whole letter as the public request that the printed book purports to answer: Rantzau urges Tycho to publish the Landgrave correspondence, and the book is the granting of that request. The rubric is the printed edition's own editorial heading, not part of the letter body; preserved verbatim per protocol §2/§4.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimus Princeps Landtgravius</b></i> — <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Tycho's princely correspondent and fellow observer (protocol §5.6). <i>Excellentiss. suus Mathematicus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician at Kassel.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Segeberga / Arx Regia Segeberga</i> = <b>Segeberg</b> (Bad Segeberg, Holstein), the royal castle; <i>Holsatia</i> = <b>Holstein</b>; <i>Bredenberga</i> = <b>Breitenburg</b>.</li><li><b>Astronomy — instruments &amp; programme.</b> <i>Instrumentis … quamplurimis, magnitudine forma et artificio inaestimabilibus</i> = \"very many Instruments … of inestimable size, form, and craftsmanship\" (<i>instrumenta</i> → \"instruments,\" §5.2); <i>peculiarem et idoneam Insulam</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b>. <i>motuum conseruatio</i> — rendered \"the preservation of the [celestial] motions\"; <i>motus</i> → \"motion(s)\" per §5.1. \"[celestial]\" is an editorial gloss (bracketed) supplied because the genitive is unqualified in the source.</li><li><b>Astrology / theology of prediction.</b> Rantzau's central apologetic passage defends <i>Astrologia</i> (predictive astronomy) against the charge of uncertainty: failed predictions are blamed not on the <i>Scientia</i> but on <i>imperfecta eius cognitio</i> (\"its imperfect knowledge\"), and on God's freedom not to be <i>alligatus … causis secundis</i> (\"bound to second causes\"). The hedges are preserved exactly: <i>non semper euentus praedictionibus respondere</i> (\"outcomes do not <b>always</b> answer to the predictions\"), <i>ingenue … fatendum est</i> (\"it must … be candidly admitted\") — no sharpening.</li><li><b>Idiom.</b> <i>toto … Coelo errant</i> — \"they wander by a whole Heaven [astray],\" the proverbial <i>toto caelo errare</i> (\"to be utterly mistaken\"); kept literal with a bracketed gloss, since Rantzau is punning on the astronomical subject. <i>a Patribus eam profectam</i> — \"it set out from the Fathers,\" i.e. the ancients/forefathers (not specifically the Church Fathers); rendered \"the Fathers\" with the ambiguity left as in the source.</li><li><b>Trojan Horse.</b> <i>tanquam ex Equo Troiano prodituros</i> = \"as it were coming forth out of the Trojan Horse\" — a complimentary metaphor for the succession of books Tycho's island will yet disgorge; kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>The crux of the request.</b> <i>resolutionem Quaestionis … de contracta Solari, vt a plurimis putatur, Sphaera</i> = \"the resolution of that Question … concerning the contracted Solar Sphere, as it is held by very many.\" The phrase concerns a debated point about the <b>sphere (orb) of the Sun</b> and whether it is <i>contracta</i> (\"contracted,\" i.e. drawn in / reduced) — most plausibly the contemporary controversy over the size and ordering of the solar orb (bound up with the absence of solar parallax and the dimensions of the world-system), which Tycho took up. <i>Sphaera</i> → \"Sphere/orb\" (§5.1). <b>Flagged:</b> the exact technical referent of <i>contracta Solari … Sphaera</i> is not made explicit in the letter itself (it points back to an earlier letter from Rantzau \"not long ago\"); the rendering keeps Rantzau's own words and the parenthetical hedge <i>vt a plurimis putatur</i> rather than resolving the doctrine.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None present.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>17 Ianuarij. Anno Salutiferi partus 1587</i> = \"17 January … In the Year of the Salvation-bringing Birth 1587\" (<i>Anno Salutiferi partus</i> = anno Domini, a humanist flourish; kept literal). Rantzau, in Danish/Holstein royal service, wrote <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark retained the Julian calendar until 1700); no N.S. equivalent is asserted. The dateline date (17 January 1587) agrees with the dataset label (\"1587. 17. januar. Segeberg\"); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Honorifics.</b> <i>vir Clariss.</i> / <i>Nobiliss.</i> / <i>Doctiss.</i> → \"most renowned / most noble / most learned Sir\"; closing <i>Vale … Iterum vale</i> → \"Farewell … Again, farewell\" (§5.5).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one substantive uncertainty is the precise technical referent of <i>contracta Solari … Sphaera</i> (\"the contracted Solar Sphere\"), which the letter names but does not expound — flagged above and kept in Rantzau's own hedged wording rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (49-letter no-slice cohort) - no Dreyer page images were available, so this was audited against source_text alone, per the brief's instruction, and capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Read the full Latin (Rantzau's letter to Tycho requesting publication of the Landgrave correspondence) against the published English sentence by sentence, including every proper name (Landtgravius, Erasmus, Ptolemaei, Copernici) and the closing date (17 Ianuarij, Anno 1587). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15860323001", "next": "brahe-t_15871019001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860323001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15871019001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Praises the correspondence between Tycho and the Landgrave as beneficial to astronomy and congratulates the age for producing princes devoted to the study of the science.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870117001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870120001", "d": "1587-01-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 20 January 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist; printed orthography with v/u), with astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VI,<i> Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(1596), Liber I · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed tradition)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> The longest-but-one letter in the corpus and a cornerstone of the <b>Tycho–Rothmann correspondence</b> — a sustained scientific letter covering: the <b>1585-comet</b> observation-comparison and its near-zero parallax; the <b>Paul Wittich instrument-priority story</b> (the parallel-slit sights, the transverse-point division from Homelius, the Nonius subtlety, the sextant); <b>Petrus Ramus</b> on \"astronomy without hypotheses,\" refuted; <b>refraction</b> in detail (the 10-foot armilla; the star-refraction values; the Cygnus-tail / Algol discrepancy; the aether/air diaphaneity against Alhazen &amp; Witelo); the great <b>star-by-star demonstration that the fixed stars' latitudes vary with the changing obliquity</b> (Spica as base, then Gemini, Capella, Orion, Regulus, Aldebaran, Altair, Antares, Libra, Sirius, Procyon); the <b>1584 lunar eclipse</b> and the meridian-difference method; praise of <b>Copernicus</b> (and the Buchanan <i>Sphaera</i>); and the <b>Frauenburg/Königsberg</b> pole-elevations with <b>Copernicus's own wooden triquetrum</b>. Printed-edition text (clean), preserving <b>v/u orthography</b> as printed. One declared intervention: the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> on ~70 words are removed as <b>non-textual</b> (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590 and 13 Sept 1588, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, and from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588). Zodiac/planet glyphs (♈ ♉ ♊ ♍ ♌ ♎ ♏ ♐ ♑ ♒ ☉ ☽ ♂ ♃ ♄) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"Vraniburgi 20 die Ianuarij, Anno 87\"</i>) is <b>Old Style</b> (Danish Julian); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VI pp. 86–104</b> (PDF pp. 100–118). <b>The dense numeral spine is confirmed against the edition of record</b> — of ~70 distinct numerals (the obliquity cluster pp. 87/94; the full Spica-based star-by-star latitude series pp. 94–99; the Frauenburg 54°22¼′ / Königsberg 54°43′ / Reinhold's 54°17′-26″-short poles p. 103; the 1584-eclipse timings H.11ᵐ12 / H.13ᵐ8 / H.15ᵐ0 + the Kassel/Rostock meridian reductions p. 100; the refraction values p. 92), <b>every legible value matches.</b> One flag: the <i>derived</i> ecliptic obliquity the .md gives as <b>23°31⅓′</b> is <b>arithmetically correct</b> (62°13′20″ − 38°42′ = 23°31⅓′); Dreyer's print shows \"31½\" at that one spot, so <b>Dreyer is the outlier there</b> — keep the .md's ⅓ (a reviewer should not \"correct\" it toward Dreyer). The immediately following <i>now-established</i> max. declination 23°31½′ is a genuinely distinct figure and matches.</p>", "la": "<p>ERVDITISSIMO VIRO VARIAQVE DOCTRINA, IN PRIMIS VERO INSIGNI ASTRONOMIAe PERITIA EXCELLENTIA D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, ILLUSTRISS. PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO AULICO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.</p><p>QVAS ad me superiori Anno Mense Aprili, tum Mandato Illustrissimi Principis tui, tum etiam propria erga me, meaque studia beneuolentia adductus, dedisti literas, Eruditissime ROTHMANNE, longe mihi erant acceptissimae, eo praesertim nomine, quod reconditiorem circa res Astronomicas mecum collationem multifariam continerent. Cur vero Illustrissimi Principis literis, et tuis etiam, vestra expectatione tardius respondeam, excusationem meam Illustriss. Principi exposui. Nec dubito ipsius Celsitudinem pro Heroico illo animi sui candore, eam aequi bonique accepturam. De Obseruationibus etiam caelestibus, tum vestris, tum meis, in trutinam inuicem vocatis, latius in literis meis ad Illustrissimum Principem egi, eiusque Celsitudini nonnullas e nostris, vt clementi ipsius petitioni morem gererem, communicaui. De his itaque copiosius ad te perscribere non duxi operae pretium, ne eandem cantilenam taediose recinere videar. Siquidem ex ijs quae ad Illustriss. Principem a me transmissa sunt, haec proculdubio tibi cognoscenda venient. De ijs itaque potissimum, quae proprijs tuis literis, tam prioribus, quam secundo missis, continebantur, nunc vberius tecum agam. Conuenientiam illam propemodum exactam, inter tuas et nostras in Cometa Anni 85. Obseruationes, Illustriss. Principi tuo, et tibi adeo placuisse, vt admirationem inde iucunditatemque non exiguam perceperitis, mihi non minus intellectu volupe erat. Quodque eam ob causam mihi tuum pereruditum scriptum (licet non ad Colophonem perductum) de hoc Cometa transmisisti, vt congruentiam vestrarum Obseruationum cum meis plenius inde cognoscerem, longe mihi gratissimum fuit. Perlegi scriptum illud semel atque iterum, mihique mirum in modum placuit. Optandum sane foret, vt ea quae de praeteritis aliquot Cometis, superioribus annis conspectis, plerique Germanorum vestrorum effutierunt potius, quam per exquisitas et infallibiles Obseruationes Mathematice demonstrârunt, pari diligentia et certitudine elaborata fuissent. Nec enim tantum inter eorum et nostra placita in situ et motu Cometarum discrepantiae, et ab ipso caelo veritatisque quaerendae nucleo deuiationis, non sine fastidio tum deprehendissem. Cometam illum longe supra Elementarem orbem, in altissimo aethere cursum suum absoluisse, rectissime mecum statuis, quamuis omnium pene Philosophorum, tam veterum, quam recentium refragentur sententiae. In hoc saltem perexiguum, et nullius etiam momenti inter nos reperitur discriminis, quod tu plane nullam illi adfuisse parallaxin colligas; ego vero eam vnius circiter ab initio scrupuli, et postea saltem dimidij adinueni. Quamuis, vt verum fatear, et ipse eandem subinde plane nullam, ob tenuitatem eius tam exilem, discernere potuerim. Verum quia ex motu proprio inferre licuit, eum longe infra octauam Sphaeram, vtut supra Solem constitutum fuisse, ideo aliqualem, licet perexiguam vixque perceptibilem aspectus diuersitatem obtinere consentaneum erat. Quin non Mars saltem, praesertim Acronychus, Parallaxin obseruabilem ingerat, vt nos in Principio Anni 82. experimentum fecimus; sed ipse etiam ♃, vt de ♄ Stella nihil dicam, cum sensibus in tenuissima illic differentia non relinquatur locus. At discrimen hoc inter nos adeo exiguum est, vt frustra de hoc verba facere videar. In Longitudinibus et Latitudinibus Cometae a te animaduersis, paulo maior licet et ipsa quoque nullius pene momenti, reperitur inter nostras Obseruationes differentia, quae tantilla est, vt vix quina vel sena scrupula ad summum attingat. Imo etiam multo saepius plane consentiat. Euenit autem, vt opinor, haec minutula inter nos discrepantia, eam potissimum ob causam, quod tu loca Fixarum, quarum vtrinque vsus fuit, paulo aliter, quam ego, assumseris. Tibi enim Aldebora in part. 4. M. 7. ♊ cum Latitudine part. 5. M. 29. Aust. collocatur, quam ego a meis Obseruationibus in part. 4. M. 1. ♊ cum Latitudine 5. 31. Merid. constituo, discrimine saltem in Longitudine 6. scrupulorum, in Latitudine vero duorum inter vtramque positionem incidente. Lucidam vero in capite ♈, quam tertiam appellatis, tu in part. 1. Min. 58. ♉, cum Latitudine part. 9. M. 57. Bor. Ego vero in part. 1. M. 54. ♉, cum Latitudine, part. 9. M. 57. Bor. assumebam, differentia in Longitud. saltem 4 in Latitudine prorsus nulla nobis interueniente. Cumque ego ijsdem potissimum Fixis, in peruestigandis huius Cometae apparentijs vsus fuerim, leuiusculum quod varietatis ex hac licet perexigua diuersitate, inter nos in locis Cometae constituendis contingere potuit. Quamuis discrimen hoc etiam in ipsis Fixarum locis adeo sit exiguum, vt vix mentionem mereatur. Situm vero harum duarum Fixarum, quo ad longum et latum, exactissime se habere, vt nostra annotatio praebet, ipsa distantia inter vtramque, quam ego eandem pene vobiscum, aliquoties part. 35. scrup. 32¼. caelitus accepi, comprobat. Sin autem ex complementis Latitudinum, et angulo per differentiam longitudinis comprehenso, Triangulorum rationibus intercapedinem rimeris, in tua assignatione fere scrupulum vnum abundat. Hoc vero tantillum, licet per se non magni momenti, tam subtiliter expendi ridebunt merito vulgares Caeli contemplatores, quibus dena etiam scrupula non reputanda aestimantur. Idque non immerito, cum ad semisses vel etiam integros gradus (pudet dicere) saepenumero caecutiant. Diuersitas Meridianorum nostrorum quae quintam horae partem, meo iudicio, saltem adaequat, ab initio, cum celerior erat Cometae motus, non nisi vnico scrupulo Obseruationes nostras variare potuit. In fine vero, cum tardior fieret, insensibilis peneque nulla differentia hinc causabatur. Discrimen inter assumtam vtrinque Declinationem maximam nihil fere importat. Ego enim praesupposui eam part. 23. M. 31. licet dimidio scrupulo (quod negligendum hoc loco duxeram) maiorem adinuenerim, quam tu part. 23. scr. 30. saltem vno scrupulo nostra minorem efficis. Neque sane intelligere possum, quomodo hanc, et non potius eandem mecum, vel adhuc maiorem Declinationem maximam, ex Obseruationibus Solis Illustriss. Principis, per Quadrantes, licet tunc non adeo correctos, habitis, colligere potueris. Ego enim in Obseruationibus Solaribus mihi transmissis, vbique fere inuenio Altitudinem maximam Solstitialem part. 62. M. 12. quibus si Parallaxin addideris, prouenit vera part. 62. M. 13. S. 20. Hinc ablata Altitudine Aequatoris, qualis tunc praesupponebatur part. 38. M. 42. euadit declinatio Eclipticae ab Aequatore part. 23. M. 31⅓. a nostra insensibiliter differens. Si vero Altitudinem maximam part. 62. M. 13. vel 14. quae etiam alicubi offertur, et veriorem Poli Altitudinem in consilium adhibueris, adhuc maior euadet, vt ob id non videam, cur vel ex ipsis Illustriss. Principis Obseruationibus tunc cognitis, cum nostra Declinatione maxima standum non foret, cum ea similis, et potius maior, quam minor collatis singulis, inde eruatur. Sed non dubito, quin per correctiora vestra Instrumenta mecum postea deprehenderitis, Declinationem hanc maximam nunc esse part. 23. M. 31½. quemadmodum et nos tum praeterito Anno, tum aliquot praecedentibus pluribus machinis experti sumus. Quamuis Solis Altitudo meridiana, in vtroque Solstitio adhibita Parallaxi eam praebeat saltem part. 23. M. 27. plus 4. scrupulis minorem, consentientem quidem melius cum Copernici et Coaetaneorum annotatione, sed ob Refractionem Solarem, in decliuiori situ iuxta Brumam prouenientem, errori adeo sensibili obnoxiam. Sol enim nobis non integre 11. gradibus, in ipso Meridie iuxta Solstitium Hybernum, eleuatur. Nec multo altior Copernico Fruenburgi extitit. Peto autem peramanter, vt mihi prima quaque occasione, inter alia significes, qualem illic per Neoterica vestra et correctiora Instrumenta Solis Eleuationem maximam et minimam deprehenderitis, tum quoque eandem circa vtraque Aequinoctia, vel potius per totum Anni curriculum, vt collationem cum nostris Obseruationibus eo plenius instituere liceat, et Stellarum etiam, tum Circumpolarium, tum aliarum Altitudines Meridianas exactiores adiungere velis, vtque integrum tuum scriptum de Cometa vltimo, mecum impertiri non graueris, etiam atque etiam vna rogo. Nec enim dubito, te nunc illi extremas imposuisse manus.</p><p>Quod autem in portione eius mihi transmissa Caelum totum aëreum, nec e solida materia constare, imo nil aliud quâm Aërem ipsum esse asseueras, id facile tibi concesserim, modo aërem qui supra Lunam sit, multo subtiliorem quam hunc Elementarem intelligas, adeo vt Aetheris potius liquidissimi et subtilissimi, quam Aëris elementaris nomen mereatur. Nec enim ego, multis ab hinc annis, in ea fui sententia, orbes aliquos realiter in caelo existere, materiamque caeli duram et imperuiam esse, Stellasque tantum ad motum orbium conuolui. Multa siquidem hinc sequuntur absurda, et si nihil aliud, sane tot Cometae in aethere cursum praecedentibus aliquot annis exercere certissime a nobis animaduersi, qui nullius orbis ductum concomitabantur, idipsum sufficienter refellunt. Iudicaui itaque semper naturalem motus scientiam singulis Planetis congenitam, vel potius diuinitus inditam esse, qua in liquidissimo et tenuissimo aethere cursus sui normam regularissime et constantissime obseruare coguntur, nullius fulciminis vel promotoris indigi, quemadmodum ipsa etiam Terra, in aëre liquido, nullo fulcro stabilita, solo proprio vndique innixa centro, firmatur. Vt autem sic reuera statuam, et alia quaedam, quam vel Ptolemaica, vel Coperniana circa Reuolutionum caelestium dispositionem ratiocinatio, multo his competentior a me, ipsa experientia docente, nouiter explorata, (de qua nunc non est dicendi locus) plurimum confirmat. Quod autem celeberrimus ille nostri Aeui Philosophus PETRVS RAMVS existimârit, sine Hypothesibus per Logicas rationes Astronomiam constitui posse, caret fundamento. Proposuit quidem ille mihi ante annos elapsos 16. cum Augustae Vindelicorum vna essemus, hanc opinionem, et hortator simul erat, vt postquam per Hypotheses Siderum cursum in exactum ordinem redegissem, idem sine his tentare affectarem. Id enim fieri posse, hanc rationem addidit, quod legisset Aegyptios facilimam olim habuisse Astronomiae cognitionem; Cumque hypothesium ratio difficilis, et intricata videatur, oportere eos alia compendiosiore, et planiore via siderum cursus cognouisse, ideoque citra omnes hypotheses. At ego illi resistebam, ostendens sine hypothesibus Phaenomena caelestia non posse in scientiam certam redigi, neque vt intelligantur, excusari. Facilitatem vero illam Aegyptiacam saltem in Aequatorijs Planetarum, quibus se a supputatione taediosa liberârunt, cum Ephemeridum expedita ratio nondum in vsu foret, extitisse. Verum cum is, vir alias perspicaci ingenio praeditus, et veritatis, si quis alius, amans, penetralia artis huius non penitus perspexisse mihi videretur, varietatemque in motu siderum statis anni temporibus nequaquam recurrentem non animaduertisse, quicquam hac in parte obtinere ab ipso nec potui, nec volui. Habet is plerosque adhuc asseclas, qui idem fieri posse sperant, sed qui rem ipsam neque intelligant, neque vnquam in effectum sint deducturi. Cum enim omnia constent numeris, ponderibus, et mensura, sine his etiam, quicquam in mundo visibili explicari nequit. Hypotheses autem nihil aliud, quam mensuram apparentis motus per circulum aliasque figuras ostendunt, quas Arithmetica in numeros resoluit. Sine his si quis Astrorum motus comprehendere velit, fortunam, quod dici solet, inuocet, et supramundanam extra hominum intellectum, planeque incorpoream, et plus quam angelicam rationem imaginetur, necesse est. Sed haec de his satis.</p><p>De Instrumentis et Obseruationibus hinc factis, restat vt nunc plenius tecum colloquar. Non est quod dubites, Witichium illum Instrumentorum instaurandorum rationem, quam Illustriss. Principi tuo proposuit, hîc cum mecum esset, accepisse, quamuis non omnia pari ratione, vel animaduerterit, vel illic satis fideliter exposuerit. Cum enim is Anno, ni fallor, 80. huc accessisset, se in meam familiaritatem facile insinuauit, partim quod hominem ob ingeniosam in Mathematicis, praesertim quo ad Geometriam attinet, solertiam magnifacerem, partim quod is se perpetuo mecum mansurum, mihique in studijs Astronomicis fidum Achatem toto vitae tempore futurum, sponte offerret. Vnde factum est, vt liberaliter illi mea inuenta communicârim, neque quicquam eorum celârim, quae tunc in promtu habebam, vel etiam in posterum elaborare decreueram. Verum cum is paulo vltra anni quadrantem mecum commoratus esset existimaretque se iam satis suis desiderijs potitum, praetendebat auunculum suum Vratislauij e viuis decessisse, cuius haereditas lucrum magnum, si mature adesset, illi adferret. Impetrata itaque facile discedendi copia, intra 7. vel 8. hebdomadas huc se reuersurum spondebat. Verum ex eo tempore, nec ipsum, nec quicquam literarum ab eo profectum vidi, neque de eo, vbinam esset, audiui, priusquam Illustrissimi Principis tui literas, D. HENRICO RANZOVIO scriptas, perlegerem, ex quibus statim intellexi, eum Instrumentorum formam et rationem, quam hîc viderat, magna ex parte illic expressisse. Quod forsan et alibi ab ipso interea factitatum est. Et sane hoc pacto mihi rem non ingratam effecit, siquidem percuperem multis in locis quam plurimos extare, qui exquisitis et minime fallacibus Instrumentis siderum apparentias mecum scrutarentur. Attamen si mea Inuenta multis annis, et longa Experientia nec paucis sumtibus et laboribus comparata, pro suis venditat, nec fatetur per quem ea habuerit, rem a viro bono et grato, ac sinceritate integritateque Mathematica alienam committit. Intelligo autem partim e tuis literis, partim e relatione mei domestici, qui vobiscum fuit, eum non omnia, prout hîc viderat, satis competenter Illustrissimo Principi ordinasse. Diuisionem quidem, qua nos in omnibus fere Instrumentis similiter vtimur, satis recte indicasse comperio, et in pinnacidiorum rimulis pro nocturnis Stellarum Obseruationibus apprime oportunis (quod inuentum cum primum ille hîc vidisset, se non frustra in Daniam venisse aiebat, multoque auro praestantius aestimabat) quaedam, licet inperfecte, imitatum esse. Dum enim compendium in his quaerebat, et per binas rimulas, idem quod nos quaternis efficere praesumebat, veritatis quaerendae dispendium fecit, nostrisque inuentis detrahendo, sibijpsi et Obseruationum certitudini derogauit. Refert quidam meorum, qui tunc temporis, cum hîc esset, ab eo audiuerit, ipsum, quando nostrorum Instrumentorum fabricam imitaturus esset, dixisse, se paucioribus rimulis idem effecturum, hocque compendium sibi ipsi reseruaturum. Quam vero egregie id praestiterit, vos non sine multarum Obseruationum dispendio experti estis. Ego vbique, praesertim in Quadrantibus, quaternas in inferioribus pinnacidijs, cum superioris pinnacidij 4. lateribus vndique parallelas adhibeo rimulas, quas auctiores et laxiores interdum efficio, prout Stellarum magnitudo efflagitat. Alias enim per binas rimulas lateralem et superius sitam, Stellarum transitus et altitudines centraliter exacteque nequaquam discernuntur, quod ipsa facile ostendit Experientia. Et nisi vos hac edocti, duplicem rimulam visualem viceuersa inferiori pinnacidio applicassetis, etiamnum in obseruandis adamussim sideribus, frustra laboraretis. Diuisionis autem illius per puncta transuersalia inuentionem, (quam tute cuiuscunque sit, ingeniosam praedicas) primum Lipsiae ab Homelio habui, ante annos plus minus 24. cum annum aetatis agerem 17. verum saltem in linea recta, vtpote in Radio Astronomico pluribus particulis diuidendo, ipsius imitatione ea tunc vtebar. At cum postea per Quadrantes, Altitudines etiam Siderum perscrutari inciperem, et in minoribus Instrumentis vulgarem diuisionem minimis portiunculis non sufficere satis experirer, ad Nonnianam illam, quam Propositione tertia de Crepusculis tradit, subtilitatem me conuerti, eandemque adhuc exactiorem pluribus subdiuisionibus reddidi, supputatis etiam Tabulis, e quibus cuiuslibet puncti altitudo scrupulose sine morâ cognosceretur. Cumque res ipsa doceret, ne hoc quidem inuentum Nonnianum id habere in recessu, quod in accessu pollicebatur, caepi cogitare, an diuisio illa praedicta, qua linea recta subtilissime per puncta transuersalia in minores secatur particulas, etiam curuis et circularibus applicari possit. Et quamvis ab initio non par videbatur ratio, ob rectarum et curuarum disconuenientiam, tamen cum in tam paruo interuallo, vt quinorum vel denorum scopulorum lineae curuae paruo interstitio a se inuicem ductae, non differant sensibiliter a rectis, negotium hoc satis commode succedere animaduerti, hacque idcirco diuisione in Quadrantibus, Sextantibus, Armillis ab hinc multis annis felici successu semper vsus sum, eamque vobis communicatam, quacunque tandem occasione, gaudeo, vt pari praecisione mecum Sidera perscrutari eo commodius possitis, ita, vt nostrae Obseruationes simul collatae eundem veritatis scopum scrupulose, vt par est, (quod etiam non sine voluptate fieri experior) attingant. Sextantis Instrumentum a me ante annos plus minus 18. cum Augustae Vindelicorum degerem, excogitatum, (quod etiam ibidem PETRO RAMO cum mihi adesset, vna cum Quadrante illo permagno 14. Cubitorum, quem extra vrbem in horto quodam Consulis HENZELII construi curaueram, ostendi, qui eorum a me descriptionem auide efflagitabat) ab eo tempore semper in vsu habui, eo quod certa ratione et multa Experientia per Radium, cuiuscunque tandem foret magnitudinis vel diuisionis, ratas distantias nequaquam inueniri, compertum haberem. Quare per Sextam circuli partem (vt commodior fieret Instrumenti tractatio) regulis applicatam, idem tentare certiore euentu aggrediebar. Atque ob id Instrumento huic Sextantis nomen indidi. Is vero, quem Witichius hîc vidit, qui chalybeus erat, catenulâ illa carebat, qualem vos adhibuistis. Per Cochleam enim transuersam distantiae in Instrumento arctabantur, dilatabanturque. Et licet Cochlea illa in directum porrigebatur, tamen quia clauiculos, vbi affixa erat, conuolubiles habuit, motioni nihilominus circulari sufficiebat. Hanc etiam commoditatem Witichius, quo minus eam indicârit, neglexisse videtur, vt ob id catenulam illam eius loco applicare vobis fuerit necessarium. In pinnacidijs quaedam aliter, quam tunc in isto Sextante habebam, limitâstis, quae mihi admodum commode ordinata videntur. Verum iste Sextans, quo per vnicum Obseruatorem distantiae capiebantur, dudum apud me in vsu esse desijt. Eius enim loco binis alijs, quos Trigonicos (ob discrimen eius quem habeo bifurcati) appello, in capiendis Stellarum remotionibus assidue vtor, qui singuli binos requirunt Obseruatores, et per lineas circa Cylindrum in centro quiescentem parallelas, exactissime Stellarum sibi inuicem, et cum toto Caeli circuitu adamussim consentientia praebent interualla, nulloque pene negotio Obseruationem absoluunt. Ideam vero horum aliquomodo repraesentatam Illustrissimo Principi inspiciendam (ni fallor) vltimo misi. Sed et vester Sextans, licet alia quadam et similiori primo meo fabricatus sit ratione, satis exactas et meis apprime consentientes exhibet Siderum distantias velut e Catalogo intercapedinum aliquot Stellarum, Illustrissimo Principi tuo nunc transmisso, perspicere potes.</p><p>De Refractionibus, quae ad Illustrissimum Principem vltimo perscripsi, non est quod dubites, rem penitus ita se habere. Nam et hoc proxime elapso Anno per Armillam 10. pedum in Diametro, qui circa axem Polis mundi correspondentem conuolubilis existit, deprehendi, Solem circa Solstitium aestiuum, Refractionem Declinationis, iuxta 30. gradum altitudinis, tam ante quam post Meridianum, insinuare maiorem semisse vnius scrupuli; circa Horizontem vero eandem 28. Minutorum efficere, idque cum Sol inferiori limbo Horizontem visibilem stringeret. Si vero hae Declinationum Refractiones in altitudinem, habita etiam ratione Parallaxeos, quae versus Horizontem Refractionem ipsam nonnihil inhibet, deriuentur, adhuc maiores euadent. In Stellis tamen fixis non vsque adeo magna videtur haec diuersitas, quamuis et in his maiorem inueniam, quam e tua assignatione mihi perscripta colligere licet. Nam lucidiorem in dextro latere Persei, tu in altitudine part. 9. M. 42. vtpote in Meridiano versus Septentrionem minima, asseueras Refractionem fieri saltem min. 2⅓. quam tamen ego in eiusdem Stellae altitudine, etiam in Meridiano ad Boream, humilima, quae hîc est grad. 14. min. 16. inueni esse min. 2½. tua paulo maiorem, cum tamen potius minor esse deberet. Siquidem tibi decliuior, plus 4½. gradibus Stella haec obseruabatur. Sic etiam dextrum humerum Persei in altitudine part. 17. Min. 45½. animaduerti Refractionem efficere M. 1⅚; vt ob id altitudines Stellarum Cassiopeae, quas infra Polum, pro eiusdem altitudine indaganda accepistis, non exacte respondeant superioribus, ita vt Polus adamussim sit intermedius. Non enim carent omni Refractionis suspicione. Siquidem vix 20. gradum altitudinis apud vos in eo situ contingant, cum tamen in 18. gradu Refractio fere bina minuta, vt ex praecedente indicatione patet, attingat. Haec itaque tibi consideratius examinanda relinquo. Maxime vero euidens discrimen in refractionibus, inter tuas et meas animaduersiones, cognoscitur inde, quod caudam Cygni indices in altitudine grad. 5. min. 18. efficere Refractionem saltem M. 5⅔. cum tamen ego diligenti et aliquoties reiterata Obseruatione inuenerim caput Algol in altitudine part. 5. M. 21½. fere consimili cum tua in cauda Cygni efficere Refractionem M. 9¼. tua fere duplo maiorem. Haec itaque per occasionem accuratius Instrumentis bene correctis peruestiga, et videbis rem totam ita se habere, nisi forte (quod tamen non facile crediderim) diuersa Horizontum natura maiorem hîc quam apud vos Refractionis insinuationem ingerat. Vnde sequeretur in Borealioribus locis eam ampliorem quam in Australibus euadere, idque etiamsi aër admodum purus et serenus (quod necessario in hac potissimum Obseruatione requiritur) vtrobique iuxta finitorem existat. Cuius rei penitiorem cognitionem, tuae Obseruationes exactius, vt monui, repetitae successiue praebebunt. Quapropter licet persuasum habeas, Refractionem nullam, ne in Sole quidem fieri in sublimiori situ quam 20. part. eo quod vapores altius non ascendant, vt verisimilius comprobes materiam caeli ab aëre non differre, repudiata Alhasen et Vitellionis ob diuersa diaphana nimia Refractione, quae vsque ad verticem aliquatenus sensim pertingeret: tamen cum experientia, vt supra indicaui, testetur in Sole etiam vltra 30. gradum aliquale, licet perexiguum Refractionis vestigium apparere, vt per vapores, cum eousque non attollantur, vix excusari queat, et affixa etiam sidera in altitudine 18. part. Refractionem pene duorum scrupulorum admittant, (velut prius per dextrum humerum Persei in minima altitudine Borea comprobauimus,) oportebit sane constituere aërem illum, vt tu vis, caelestem longe subtiliorem et transparentiorem esse hoc Aëre Elementari, vel potius, proprie magis loquendo, Aetheris diaphanitatem, Aëris diaphanitate puriorem et perspicuam magis existere. Cum vero non adeo magna sit inter haec ambo diaphana differentia, atque inter Aërem et aquam, et successiue, vbi se inuicem contingunt, minuatur, ita vt suprema Aëris portio Lunae Reuolutionibus contigua fere nihil differat ab ipso aethere (nam Elementum ignis illic existere, vt Aristotelici fingunt, ipsa Experientia vel hac sola ratione improbat, cum longe maior hinc fieret siderum Refractio, si per triplicia diaphana ad nos lumen eorum pertingeret) ideo etiam perexigua causatur Refractio, nisi quatenus a vaporibus in decliuiori situ augeatur, et quando eleuatiora apparent sidera, plane insensibilis redditur, nec in tantum excrescit, quantum praedicta Alhasen et Vitellionis ratiocinatio inducit. Illi enim Caelum e solida diaphana materia constare, longe diuersa ab ipso Aëre, opinabantur.</p><p>Quod Stellas fixas, suas mutare latitudines, idque proportionaliter, iuxta obliquitatis Eclipticae variationem, animaduertisti, admodum mihi placet. Nam et ego dudum tale quid euenisse mecum colligebam, cum plerasque Stellas in Semicirculo Aestiuo et Boreali ad Eclipticam parte, Latitudinem in tantum auxisse, quantum mutatio Obliquitatis requireret; in Meridionali vero eandem tantundem fere imminuisse, contrarium pari ratione in Semicirculo Hyberno accidisse nostrae Obseruationes cum Ptolemaica annotatione collatae, attestarentur. Ideoque ob Eclipticae ad Stellas ipsas accessum, recessumque, per Declinationis maximae immutationem id fieri, non obscure indicabatur. Verum cum quasdam Stellas, etiam inter Illustriores, praesertim Vulturis lucidam et Aldeboram, Canemque maiorem in numeris Latitudinis Ptolemaicis, huic speculationi reclamitare deprehenderem, caepi negotium hoc altiori indagine, vt dubia, quae inciderent, praesecarentur, examinare. Cumque res haec magni sit momenti, et a nemine alias, quod sciam, te solo excepto, animaduersa, quasdam hac in parte, vt ad veritatis scopum sepositis omnibus dubijs collimare melius liceat, ex meis considerationibus paulo vberius exponam. Cum numeros Longitudinum et latitudinum Stellarum in Abaco Ptolemaico assignatos, eo quod non vbique sibi inuicem et cum Caelo ipso habita intercapedinum Stellarum, quae inuariatae permanent, ratione consentiant, non immerito suspectos haberem, totam rem paulo altius scrutatus sum, idque ex Declinationibus quarundam affixarum Stellarum, quas idem Ptolemaeus cap. 3. lib. 7. Almagesti, a Timochare, Hipparcho, et se obseruatas recenset, quibus applicui earundem Longitudines, quales singulis illis temporibus eas obtinere conueniebat. Qua etiam in re Ptolemaicis interuallis non admodum confisus, peculiarem adhibui cautionem, ita vt Spicam ♍ pro fundamento caeterarum constituerem. Eam enim certius obseruatam, eo quod iuxta Aequinoctialem esset, et quia toties a Ptolemaeo repetitur, consentaneum videtur. Cumque hanc tempore suarum Obseruationum ipse asserat dimidio gradu versus Austrum declinasse, quam Tymochares deprehendit grad. 1. M. 24. Hipparchus vero scrupulis saltem 36. Aequatore Borealiorem, sequitur assumta vtrobique Spicae Latitudine 2. partium Australi, prout per Lunam illo tempore explorata est, et qualis nunc etiam fere reperitur, fuisse eius Longitudinem Tymochari in grad. 21. Min. 53. Hipparcho in part. 23. M. 53. Ptolemaeo in part. 26. M. 38. signi ♍, ideoque a Tymochare ad Hipparchum 2. gradibus, ab Hipparcho ad Ptolemaeum part. 2¾. promouebatur tum haec tum caeterae omnes affixae Stellae. Quare praesuppositis his Spicae locis, aliarum quarundam Fixarum, quarum Declinationes ex veteribus constabant, Longitudines ad tempus Obseruationum authorum eorundem, videlicet Tymocharis, Hipparchi et Ptolemaei applicui, idque ea lege, vt quantam differentiam ante vel retro, quo ad interuallum Longitudinis â Spica nostris Obseruationibus nunc obtinere deprehenduntur, tantundem etiam in elongatione a Spicae loco eorum tempori conueniente constituerim, vt vera Stellarum Longitudo respectu huius Stellae innotesceret, neglecta Ptolemaica annotatione, quae rarius omnibus numeris absoluta inuenitur. Neque enim possibile est, Longitudines Fixarum in vna Stella aliter variare, quam in altera, cum omnes in vno sint orbe, et circa vnum et eundem Polum vniformiter moueantur. Vnde easdem fuisse temporibus Ptolemaei differentias Longitudinum in omnibus Stellis, quae etiamnum inueniuntur, necessarium erit. Cum itaque hac ratione Longitudines Stellarum adaptassem, hinc, et ex Declinationibus, quas recenset Ptolemaeus citato capite, adhibita obliquatione Signiferi maxima, quae eorum Aeuo fuit part. 23. M. 51⅓. earundem etiam Latitudines inquisiui, quod non difficulter fieri potuit. Siquidem ex data Stellae differentia Longitudinis a proximo Tropico vnus Trianguli constat angulus, cuius duo etiam latera dantur, vnum a complemento Declinationis, vel additamento ad Quadrantem, prout negotium exigit, alterum ex differentia Polorum Eclipticae et Aequatoris, ergo tertium per Sphaericorum Triangulorum leges facile innotescit, per quod ipsa Latitudo quaesita Stellae, qualis tunc erat, cognita venit. In hunc, inquam, modum Stellarum Latitudines ex assumptis diuersis Veterum Declinationibus, adhibitis debitis Longitudinibus examinaui. Et licet hoc etiam pacto in omnibus Stellis non eandem, quam sperabam, consonantiam compererim, eo quod Declinationes earum vel non sint satis scrupulose a Veteribus obseruatae, vel quod mendae aliquae in assignatione numerorum nonnullibi subsint, (quas tamen vt quantum in me erat praecauerem, adhibui etiam vetus quoddam exemplar Almagesti, ante annos proxime 70. Venetijs editum, quod Declinationes non in Cyphris numerorum, vt Trapezuntina versio, sed in aliquotis graduum partibus, literis exprimit) nihilominus maiorem his subesse certitudinem quam Latitudinibus in Catalogo Ptolemaico assignatis iudicaui, et sicubi doscohaerentia euidens apparebat, ipsos etiam Ptolemaicos numeros in consilium adhibui; Quae vero ex his in Latitudinibus quarundam Affixarum ab eorum temporibus hucusque diductis exploraui, nunc magna ex parte, qua fieri potest breuitate, indicabo.</p><p>Primum considerando Stellas circa Tropicum aestiuum, inuenio e Declinationibus vtriusque capitis Geminorum, quod maiorem nunc obtineant Latitudinem, quam Veterum temporibus, idque secundum ipsissimam rationem mutatae Declinationis Eclipticae. Nam Boreale caput ♊ iuxta tenorem supradictum, inuenitur tempore Tymocharis habuisse Latitudinem part. 9. min. 43. fere. Hipparchi part. 9. min. 42. Ptolemaei part. 9. min. 45. ferme, vnde colligitur eam fuisse circa illorum tempora part. 9. min. 43. proxime, quae nunc est part. 10. min. 2. Quare 19. scrupulis aucta, prout recessus Eclipticae in eo loco requirit. Pari modo Meridionale caput ♊ ex Hipparchi Declinatione et caeteris, de quibus dixi, datis, habuisse Latitudinem inuenitur, part. 6. min. 19½. e Ptolemaei vero part. 6. M. 22½. fere, quarum medium est part. 6. M. 21. Latitudo Stellae illorum Aeuo competens, quae nunc exacta ratione deprehenditur esse, part. 6. M. 38. vt ob id etiam tantundem fere mutârit Latitudinem, quantum obliquatio Signiferi alterata requirit. Sic etiam Capella inuenitur Hipparcho et Ptolemaeo Latitudinem e Declinationibus assignatis obtinuisse part. 22⅚, licet Ptolemaici numeri hanc constituant part. 22. M. 30. Vnde haec aliquatenus limitando probabilius fecerimus, si Latitudinem tunc ponamus, part. 22⅚. quae nunc deprehenditur esse grad. 22. M. 50½. etiam ratione mutatae Eclipticae debito fere modo variata. Eiusdem etiam rei documentum adfert vterque humerus Orionis. Habuit enim dexter Latitudinem e Declinationibus Tymocharis et Hipparchi part. 16⅓. quam proxime, (Ptolemaicae Declinationi hoc in loco aliquis error subest) quae nunc 16 1/10. obtinere deprehenditur, etiam debito fere modo minor reddita. Sinister humerus eiusdem Orionis ex Hipparchi et Ptolemaei Declinationibus, Latitudinem illo Aeuo obtinuit grad. 17. min. 5. pene, quam nunc habet part. 16. min. 53. etiam ratione accedentis Eclipticae quasi proportionaliter imminutam. Haud aliter Cor ♌, quam Stellam Ptolemaeus paulo diligentius obseruauit, ipsi Latitudinem obtinuit, part. 0. M. 10. Boream, licet hoc praecisius cum Hipparchi Declinatione quam ipsius propria consentiat, hanc nos accuratis animaduersionibus inuenimus 26. Minutis Ecliptica Borealiorem, ita vt scrupula 16. a Veterum inuentis nunc abundent, quod ob recessum Eclipticae effectum intentioni nostrae satis conueniens apparet. Sed ab altera parte Stella Aldeboram aliquod ingerit impedimentum, siquidem eius Latitudinem Ptolemaici numeri prodant part. 5⅙. Meridionalem, quod etiam non adeo ab ipsius Declinatione alienum est. Cuius tamen Latitudinem non minorem inuenire potui quam part. 5. M. 30½. tertia parte vnius gradus Ptolemaica maiorem, cum tamen potius minorem ex assumta Hypothesi mutationis Eclipticae esse conueniebat. Vnde in huius Stellae Obseruatione quo ad latum, ipsi etiam declinationi Ptolemaicae errorem non exiguum subesse autumo, id quod Declinationes eiusdem Timocharis et Hipparchi conuincunt, licet nec hae suis numeris absolute constent. Nam Declinatio Timocharis dat Stellae Latitudinem part. 5. M. 56. iusto maiorem, Hipparchi part. 5. M. 33. nonnihil debito minorem, licet vtraque Ptolemaicam (prout res requirit) excedat. Nec video, quomodo possibile fuerit, vt Declinatio huius Stellae, quae tunc versabatur circa primam decuriam Dodecatemorij ♉, a Timochare ad Ptolemaeum mutata sit part. 2¼. cum tamen circa ipsum Aequinoctium Spica ♍ interea eandem non alterârit plus gr. 1. M. 54. Ideoque non leuem errorem circa Latitudinem Aldeborae Ptolemaeo (modo numeri ad nos diducti recte se habeant) irrepsisse iudico. Et si medium inter Timocharis et Hipparchi Latitudinem, quae videlicet ex eorum Declinationibus prouenit, tanquam probabilius rem alias perplexam limitando, acceperimus, prodit latitudo oculi ♉ tunc part. 5¾. quod a rei, quam intendimus, inquisitione non multum deuiat. Esse autem Ptolemaicam, quam huic Stellae attribuit Latitudinem, prorsus erroneam, inde patet, quod Boreali oculo ♉ Latitudinem in suo Catalogo assignet part. 3. M. 0. quam nos inuenimus part. 2⅔. fere, ideoque Ptolemaica assignatione etiam paulo plus, quam Obliquitas Eclipticae mutata requirit, minorem, cum tamen Aldeboram contrarium ostendebat. Et distantia quae est inter has vtrasque Stellas in oculis, quam reiterata Obseruatione deprehendi esse part. 3. M. 10¼, conuincit Ptolemaicam assignationem in alterutra vel vtraque euidenter deflectere. Si enim ex complemento Latitudinis vtriusque Stellae, quam ille ipsis attribuit, et differentia Longitudinum vtpote e notis duobus lateribus cum angulo comprehenso, distantiam inquiramus, proueniet illa part. 2. M. 19. a vera 51. Minutis deficiens. Quare si Borealioris oculi latitudo e numeris Ptolemaicis veritati proximior erit, oportet Aldeboram maiorem necessario habuisse Latitudinem quam ille assignat. Nec enim tunc propiorem fuisse Boreali oculo quam nunc, possibile. Ex his itaque patet, Latitudinem, quam Ptolemaeus Aldeborae assignat, minus ratam esse, ideoque in hoc, quod nostrae intentioni refragari videatur, fidem non mereri.</p><p>Venio nunc ad Stellas ad Hybernum Eclipticae Semicirculum spectantes, et inprimis iuxta Tropicum Brumalem, vbi (vt rei propositae demonstratio euidentior euadat) occurrit Lucida Vulturis volantis, cuius Latitudinem, licet Ptolemaei Catalogus exhibeat part. 29. M. 10. vt ob id prima fronte nostrae sententiae prius idem refragari videatur, tamen cum e Declinationibus Veterum Latitudinem eius eruo, tantum abest, vt haec Stella id quod intendimus interturbet, vt nulla exactius et certius idipsum stabilire comperiatur. Fuit enim haec circa tempora Priscorum, de quibus loquor, proxima quo ad Longitudinem Tropico Hyberno, ideoque vel ex sola Declinatione ipsius Latitudo facile patuit. Nam anno Obseruationum Tymocharis, quando vix Sexta parte vnius gradus ipso Coluro Solstitiali anterior erat, Declinatio eius inueniebatur part. 5. M. 48, ideoque Latitudo (assumta illius Aeui Declinatione maxima) part. 29. M. 39½. euadit, cui etiam astipulator Hypparchica annotatio, prodens eandem prorsus Declinationem, eo quod haec insensibiliter circa Solstitium variabatur. Inde Latitudo Stellae etiam promanabat part. 29. M. 39. Et licet Ptolemaica declinatio, quae erat duobus scrupulis maior, videatur Latitudinem hanc sesquialtero scrupulo arctiorem reddere, eo quod is forte non in ipsis scrupulis exactissimam adhibuerit praecisionem; tamen hoc nihil importat, sed sufficienter vna cum caeteris attestatur Latitudinem lucidae in Aquila fuisse illa mundi aetate part. 29. M. 39. quam proxime. Cumque ea nunc inueniatur part. 29. M. 21. manifestum est eam Latitudinem interea coarctasse scrupulis 18. quantum etiam accessus Eclipticae vicinior eo in loco efflagitat, vt vel ex hac sola Stella iuxta Tropicum Hybernum, praesertim si capita Geminorum prope Tropicum Aestiuum in altera medietate vna viceuersa adhibeantur, satis comprobari queat, fixas Stellas Latitudines ratione mutatae obliquitatis Signiferi proportionabiliter variare. Siquidem tam vnanimiter in hac Stella praestantissimorum illorum trium Artificum obseruatae Declinationes consentiunt, quae etiam fere inuariatae erant, quoniam Stella Tropico, quo ad longitudinem vicina insensibiliter, vt dixi Declinationem mutâuit. Vnde etiam si quid dubij in eius Longitudine latere poterit, nullum errorem in ijs quae intendimus eo in loco insinuabit. Sed et Antares in eadem Eclipticae medietate idem vlterius confirmat, quae tam e Tymocharis quam Hypparchi Declinationibus inuenitur part. 4. M. 14. (Ptolemaicae Declinationi euidentem errorem hac in Stella subesse necessarium est, eo quod haec Latitudinem eius praebeat part. 4. M. 37. quam tamen ipse in Catalogo Affixarum ponit part. 4. M. 0. a seipso plurimum dissentiens) cum nunc eadem sit part. 4. M. 24. Manifestum itaque est Latitudinem in hac etiam Stella non multum diuersimode ab eo quod recessus Eclipticae, eo in loco postulat, adauctam esse. Australis etiam lanx ♎, siquidem Hypparchi Declinationi fides potius adhibenda erit, quia haec probabilior videtur, Latitudinem illo Aeuo obtinuit fere dimidij gradus Boream, quae nunc 26. scrupulorum esse deprehenditur, arctior ob accessum Eclipticae reddita. Licet non solum Timocharis Declinatio, sed ipsius etiam Ptolemaei, in hac Stella intolerabiliter deuiet. Nam illa Latitudinem vix praebet 17. Minutorum, haec vero saltem 2. Cum tamen ipse Catalogus Ptolemaicus eam exhibeat 40. scopulorum, quod licet nimium sit, et plus, quam necesse est, nostrae intentioni astipuletur, vero tamen proximius existit. Borealis quoque lancis Latitudinem, tam Timocharis quam Hipparchi Declinatio praebet part. 8. M. 48. proxime, quae ex nostra Obseruatione nunc est part. 8. M. 36. fere, vt ob id ratione accessus Eclipticae in hac etiam Stella Latitudo coarctata reperiatur. Nam quod Ptolemaica Declinatio eam exhibeat saltem grad. 8. M. 25. cui ipsius Abacus fere consentit, indicans eandem grad. 8. M. 30. (vnde id quod proponimus labefactari videtur) nihil moramur. Nam Declinationem et Latitudinem Ptolemaicam minus certam esse, inde facile patet, quod ab Hipparcho ad se mutatam Declinationem admittat 1. grad. 24. M. cum tamen Spica ♍ interea eandem non nisi 1. grad. 6. M. variârit. Quare potius Declinationibus Tymocharis et Hipparchi in illa Stella standum censeo. Siquidem secum consentiunt et idem interuallum mutationis praebent, quod ipsis Spica ♍ efficere colligitur.</p><p>Ex his itaque Stellis, tam circa Borealem quam Australem Eclipticae Semicirculum versantibus, satis superque demonstratum esse arbitror, pro Obliquitatis Eclipticae variatione alterari vna proportionabiliter affixarum Stellarum Latitudines. Nec vllus restare videretur hac de re dubitationi locus, modo splendidissima illa in ore Canis maioris non contrarium quiddam innueret. Cuius Latitudo e Declinatione Tymocharis, quae erat 16⅓. inuenitur grad. 39. M. 18. ex Hipparchi quae erat 16. part. prouenit eadem part. 39. M. 10. qualem etiam Ptolemaeus in suo Abaco ponit. Nam et ipsius Declinatio quarta parte gradus Hipparchiana minor, Latitudinem Stellae praebet part. 39. M. 7⅓. quod ad sextam partem gradus vltra integros proxime accedit. Siquidem is duorum vel trium scrupulorum nullam hac in parte rationem habuit, vt ob id tam iuxta Hipparchum quam Ptolemaeum huius Stellae Latitudo tunc fuisse deprehendatur part. 39⅙. Austrina, quam tamen nos hoc Aeuo adinuenimus part. 39. M. 31. quae Veterum placitis tertia parte vnius gradus maior existit, cum potius nunc tantundem minor, caeteris paribus esse deberet. Cuius sane nodi resolutio non facile obuia est. Siquidem in Obseruationibus Veterum errorem aliquem latere consensus omnium vniformis difficulter admittit; nec in tam Illustri Stella, quae omnium Affixarum maxima et fulgentissima est, eos adeo sensibiliter hallucinatos esse, credibile videtur. Cumque nostrae etiam Obseruationes in hac ipsa Stella omni dubio careant, non satis mirari possum, quomodo haec Latitudinem nunc maiorem quam olim exhibeat. Neque enim concedi potest eam non ijsdem legibus, quibus reliquae Affixae, obnoxiam esse, quin pariter cum illis Latitudinem ad nutum Obliquitatis Eclipticae variet. Quapropter rem hanc varie ventilando, nihil aliud astruere possum, quam Obseruationes Veterum in Declinatione huius Stellae, etsi satis forsan bene caelitus habitas, minus tamen recte vitio transcriptorum ad nos transmissas esse. Poterat siquidem in describendo similis in singulis error committi. Nam quod annotatio Latitudinis eius in Abaco Ptolemaico Declinationibus subscribat, parum ad rem facit. Siquidem aliâs Latitudines illic assignatae, non solum a Declinationibus sed ipso etiam Caelo dissidere, si per distantiam Stellarum in trutinam vocentur, saepenumero deprehenduntur. Neque enim ob hanc vnicam Stellam, quamuis caeteris fulgentiorem, ijs quae in tot alijs recte se habere pluribus documentis euicimus, assertioni huic de mutatione Latitudinis Stellarum ad Eclipticae variatam Obliquationem, praeiudicium aliquod intentari potest, praesertim cum altera Canis minoris lucidior, nostrae positioni suffragetur, quae temporibus Ptolemaei fuit proxima Coluro Solstitiorum, habuitque tunc ex ipsius numeris Latitudinem part. 16. M. 10. quam nunc obtinet part. 15. M. 57. ob accessum Eclipticae minorem, licet non in tantum, quantum ea a Tropico distantia requirit, eo quod Ptolemaeus quina scrupula quae desiderantur, non reputârit. Quod autem Spica ♍ Veteribus duos gradus in Latitudine obtinere constituebatur, qualem etiam hoc Aeuo retinere inuenitur, vel si summam praecisionem adhibeamus, dimidio saltem scrupulo minorem, cum potius 3. vel 4. minutis maiorem nunc esse, ex mutata Obliquitate Eclipticae eueniret, positionem hanc nostram nihilominus labefactare nequit. Siquidem Antiqui illi 3. vel 4. Scrupulorum rationem pene nullam habuerunt, Nam saltem in senis vnius gradus partibus tam Longitudines quam Latitudines Stellarum, vt in Abaco Ptolemaico videre est, annotârunt. Accedit et hoc quod ex visibili Lunae cum hac Stella copulatione, eius Latitudinem vna cum Longitudine aliquoties rimati sint, cum tamen Parallaxes Lunares adeo certo nequaquam illis constarent, quin pauculis Scrupulis hoc modo hallucinatio insinuari possit, praesertim cum Hypothesi Ptolemaica in Luna vsi sint, nulla enim alia Veteribus cognita erat, quam nostrae etiam Obseruationes attestatur, nonnunquam multo maiorem Lunae ad Terras appropinquationem exhibere, quam rei veritas exigit, vt ob id Parallaxes hinc deductas minus ratas esse, consentaneum euadat. Sed haec forsan copiosius quam Epistolae leges patiuntur, nunc tracto, quare his iactis fundamentis tibi doctissime ROTHMANNE omnia penitius expendenda pluraque his superaddenda relinquo. Nunc ad caetera progredior.</p><p>Quod in Latitudinibus Stellarum inter Illustriss. Principis et meas Obseruationes paucorum scrupulorum discrimen incidat, fit potissimum ob id, quod Declinationes Stellarum ante eam, quam vltimis tuis literis mihi significasti, perpendiculi correctionem, duobus Scrupulis minus ratae fuerint; Obliquitate etiam Zodiaci maxima diuersimode aliquantulum assumta, nonnihil vna in his variante. Plurimum vero laetatus sum, cum intelligerem ex posterioribus tuis literis, vos errorem hunc perpendiculi deprehendisse; nam alias vestrae Declinationes, vbique a meis duobus pene scrupulis dissidebant, eo quod Altitudo Aequatoris vno scrupulo minor vero, et Stellarum vno maior euadebat. Nec dubito, quin si post hanc Quadrantis vestri exacte in libellam restitutionem, adamussim Solares Altitudines, adhibita etiam Parallaxi, cum mea restitutione in cursu Solis examinaueritis, me scopum ipsum omni possibili subtilitate attigisse, comprobetis, idque potissimum in Borealioribus Signis. Nam in Meridionalibus, praesertim ♒ et ♏ (in ♐ et ♑ ob perexiguam Declinationis mutationem nosti haec irrita esse) ob Refractionem sensibilem, aliquid inuenietis discriminis, quod tamen per hanc ipsam prius aliunde cognitam praecaueri potest. In deliquio Lunari Anni 1584. quod expetis diligenter, non minus quam circa praecedentium aliquot annorum obscurationes Lunares, a me accurate obseruatas deprehendi, quod primum initium fuerit H. 11. M. 12. a Meridie diei 7. Nouembris, Medium H. 13. M. 8. Finis vltimus H. 15. M. 0. (nec enim haec tempora semper sunt aequalia, vt hactenus creditum est) quemadmodum ex remotione certarum Stellarum Aequatoria a Meridiano infallibili ratione inquisiui. Quod autem Witichius medium huius deliquij Cassellis H. 13. M. 3. vt ad me perscripsisti, adinuenerit, caret exquisita praecisione. Vester enim Meridianus ad minimum nostro est quinta horae parte, vt supra etiam dixi, occidentalior. Oportuit itaque deliquij medium illic fuisse, proxime post mediam noctem, H. 0. M. 56. abundante in Witichiana Obseruatione dimidio quadrante horae. Quam itaque diligens fuerit haec temporis annotatio, viderit eius autor, cuius in sideribus caelitus indagandis diligentia dudum mihi perspecta, vel, vt verius loquar, suspecta existit. HENRICVS BRVCAeVS, eximius apud Rostochienses Mathematicus, amicus noster singularis, eandem Eclipsin illic obseruauit, eiusdemque medium inuenit H. 1. M. 4. P.M.N. tempore paulo anteriori nostro, cum potius idem fere esse deberet. Siquidem insensibilis sit Meridianorum differentia. Verum is per Quadrantem Orichalcicum quidem, sed minusculum, olim sibi a me concessum, ex Altitudinibus Stellarum, saltem in gradibus acceptis, tempora latiori modo scrutabatur, id quod in Stellis, praesertim ad Meridianum tendentibus, errori euidenti est obnoxium. Nihilominus hinc magis adhuc liquet, Witichij Obseruationem minus exactam fuisse, ideoque ad Meridianorum nostrorum intercapedinem rectius cognoscendam, non sufficere. Et quoniam per Lunae Obseruationem iuxta 90. gradum idem diligenti adhibita cura praestari queat, ego quam plurimas eiuscemodi Lunares, cum Parallaxi Longitudinis careret, considerationes, aliquot praecedentibus annis caelitus fideliter acceptas, in promptu habeo. Si itaque scirem, quibus diebus Luna eodem modo a te deprehensa fuerit, vtique et meas communicarem, vt collatio mutua institueretur. Imo et ternas eiuscemodi Lunae Obseruationes, in assignatione locorum omnium Planetarum, intra vnam Caeli totius Reuolutionem, ter hîc ab initio huius anni, hoc ipso Mense Ianuario, meis Instrumentis ad amussim indagatorum, Illustrissimo Principi tuo transmissa reperies. Si Lunae locus circa aliquod horum temporum, a te prope Nonagesimum Eclipticae gradum ab Horizonte peruestigatus est, et temporis momenta exacte constant, habes quod desideras. Per Parallaxes Lunares in alio situ, quam prope 90. gradum, idem attentare, ea quae hîc requiritur scrupulositas, ob Parallaxes in Tabularum numeris non omni dubio carentes, nequaquam certo admittit. In temporibus vero his subtilissime numerandis non nimium fidas Horologio, nisi exactissime Reuolutionem obseruet, et intermedijs horis vbique aequaliter moueatur, singulaque Minuta vna etiam cum Secundis discriminatim exhibeat. Qualia non dubito Illustrissimum Principem in promptu habere, cum Automatopaeum tam artificiosum et praestantem habeat. Ex Altitudinibus etiam Stellarum haec subtilis pragmatia non satis commode succedit, praesertim si Meridiano proximiores eleuentur. Neque iuxta Horizontem, ob Refractionis incommodum, vitio carent. Consultius itaque fuerit, ex ipsis Azimuthis Stellarum haec temporum momenta rimari, modo Meridiei linea non ex Sole, sed e Stellis, potissimum vero ipsa Polari Stella, cum est vtrinque versus ortum et occasum a Polo, in maxima remotione Azimuthali (quae ratio ne minimo errori est obnoxia, et commode fieri potest) diligenter prius explorata fuerit. Ego momenta temporum exacta per Armillas Aequatorias, distantias Solis et Stellarum a Meridiano in ipso Aequatore, qui temporum mensura est, in quocunque situ existant, numerantes, citra omnem erroris suspicionem facilime assequi soleo. Si Eclipsium Lunarium, hoc et futuro Anno instantium, verissima tempora vtrinque, fauente Numine, obseruare nobis contigerit, de Meridianorum intercapedinibus, Lunaeque motibus, certiores reddemur. Animaduertendum vero, quod in totali deliquio cum mora, melius discernatur tempus ingressûs totius corporis Lunae in vmbram terrenam, et primi eiusdem egressûs, quam caetera duo primi initij vltimique finis momenta. Quare illis potius accurate attendendum. Expedit etiam plures et disiunctos ab inuicem adhibere Obseruatores, qui acumine visus polleant. Lunaris motus Calculum nunc non mitto, eo quod eum pluribus Eclipsibus, et Lunae locis iuxta Quadraturas, prope mediam distantiam Epicycli et alibi etiam, saepius diutiusque, priusquam mea inuenta hac in parte alijs communicârim, examinare constitui. Volo enim, vt quae a me in Astronomicis proficiscuntur, vel nulla sint, vel longe exactissima, vt ad maiorem praecisionem sensus humani non pertingant. Praestat enim haec intacta relinquere, quam oscitanter et rudiori Minerua tractare. Veneris et Mercurij motus satis scrupulose, per multiplices Obseruationes exploratos habeo. In tribus altioribus Planetis, plures Acronychias animaduersiones, praesertim in distantioribus ab inuicem Eccentrici locis requiro, quas vbi fauente DEO assecutus fuero, spero me eodem Numine nostros conatus dirigente, Astronomiam vniuersam omnibus suis numeris absolutam, Posteritati multo perfectius, quam ab antecessoribus nostris factitatum est, consecraturum. Quo nomine Deus vires animi et corporis largiri dignetur, vt praestantissima et antiquissima omnium, planeque diuina haec scientia, a mendis et erroribus aliquando repurgata in veritatem Apparentiarum ipsi Caelo congruam restituatur.</p><p>NICOLAVS COPERNICVS ingens et incomparabilis in hoc opere Athleta, si exquisitis et nulli errori ille obnoxijs Instrumentis non destitutus fuisset, longe certiora nobis reliquisset; Nam scientia et ingenio, si quis alius, eminebat, quaeque e Geometria Arithmeticaque ad hanc Artem constituendam requirebantur, perfectissime callebat. Nec Ptolemaeo hac in parte inferior erat, imo in quibusdam, praesertim Hypothesium concinnitate, et compendiosa Harmonia inuenienda, eum longe exuperabat. Neque obstat absurditas prima fronte apparens in Terrae Reuolutione, cum, si motus circularis circa aliena centra regularis astruatur, vt Ptolemaicae in omnibus Planetis, excepto Sole, ferunt Hypotheses, longe absurdius et intolerabilius contra ipsa Artis principia, peccetur, quam Terrae motum aliquem attribuendo; qui quoniam naturalis erit, insensibilis euadit. Nec tot inconuenientia hinc proueniunt, quot plerique arbitrantur; quaeque in Poëmate Sphaerico clarissimi illius Poëtae BVCHANANI SCOTI, mei, cum in viuis esset, amici eximij, nuper publicata sunt, locum hîc non habent. Is enim non animaduertit, posito motu terreno mare et circumfluum proximumque Aërem vna pari concitatione conuolui, ideoque nullam violentiam causari, nec absurditatem, quantam in omnibus ijs, quae in contrarium adducit, fieri existimat prouenire. Sed non est huius loci, copiosius de his disserere. Ea vero aliquando, Deo volente, latius in Opere nostro Astronomico excutiemus, et forte aliam Hypothesium rationem, quae nec Mathematice nec Physice absurda videbitur, et contra ipsa Artis principia non peccet, introducemus. Petis vt Eleuationis Poli, quae Fruenburgi est, exactam notitiam tecum communicem, ob Copernianas illic habitas Obseruationes, penitius explorandas. Scias itaque me, ante triennium, eo nomine quendam meorum Astronomiae operam nauantium studiosorum, ELIAM OLAI nomine, Fruenburgum nauigio misisse, adiuncto exquisito et minutissimarum diuisionum capaci Instrumento, qui illic commodum apud Canonicos Warmienses, a quibus etiam benevole excipiebatur, nactus Obseruandi locum, plus integro Mense commoratus, quam plurimas exquisitas, et sibi inuicem consentientes nactus est Obseruationes. Ideoque tam ex Altitudinibus Solis Meridianis in Declinatione eius Boreali, quam Fixarum Eleuationibus maximis illic deprehensis, et cum ijs, quas idem Instrumentum hîc praebuit collatis, Eleuationem Fruenburgi adinuenimus part. 54. M. 22¼. excedentem Copernianam annotationem fere 3. Minutis. Id quod etiam semper suspicabar, ex quo viderem Eccentricitatem Solarem, et Apogaeum, totumque adeo Solis cursum, tam breui annorum interuallo ab illius placitis adeo enormiter discrepare. Eum enim ex Altitudine Solis maxima et minima in vtroque Solstitio, Poli Altitudinem, et Declinationem maximam indagâsse, idque minus congrue, quod Refractionis in decliui situ implicationem non euitârit, et forte etiam Parallaxeos nullam habuerit rationem, probabiliter e scriptis eius colligebam, et ob id terna circiter Poli altitudini deesse scrupula, etiam antequam periculum fieri curaui, consentaneum duxi. Interea vero, dum studiosus meus Fruenburgi Obseruationibus vacat, ab Illustrissimo Principe GEORGIO FRIDERICO MARCHIONE ANSBACHIANO, misso ad eum M. MAeNIO Professore Mathematum Regiomonti Prussiae, illuc vocatur, vt Polarem Altitudinem ibidem pari diligentiâ examinet; quod etiam per aliquot Hebdomadas in horto principis, loco opportuno concesso, effecit. Atque ex Altitudinibus Solis Stellarumque illic multoties acquisitis, deprehenditur Regiomonti Prussiae Eleuatio Poli part. 54. M. 43. proxime. Quod tibi vna indicandum duxi, siquidem REINHOLDVS, PRVTENICAS Tabulas ad eum locum ordinauit, eiusque Latitudinem gra. 54. M. 17. scrupulis 26. iusto minorem (Appiani erroneum Catalogum perperam imitatus) constituit. Reuersus ad me studiosus hic meus, Instrumentum quoddam ab ipso Copernico compositum, diuque in vsu habitum a D. IOHANNE HANNOVIO Canonico Warmiensi mihi dono missum, secum attulit. Estque Parallaticum illud, cuius libro 4. cap. 15. constructionem explicat, quod communiter Regulas Ptolemaicas vocant, eo quod Ptolemaeo olim etiam plurimum in vsu erat. Est autem prorsus ligneum et abiegnum, binaeque Regulae pares 4. vlnas in Longitudine continent, diuisionemque exhibet particularum 1414. modo vulgari institutam, ita vt quaelibet minor differentia quinarium numerum praesupponat. Hinc facile est colligere, qualibus machinis eximius ille Copernicus instructus, caelestes motus ad nos deriuare tentârit; vt ob id errores, qui irrepserunt, non Arti, vel ipsius industriae, sed medijs Organorum, quibus minus exquisitis vtebatur, imputandi veniant. Ego etiam bina eiuscemodi Regularum Instrumenta dudum in promptu habeo, vtiliter per Orichalcum elaborata, et in decuplo plures portiunculas subtilissime diuisa, quorum vnum ad instar Ptolemaei, columnae erectae innititur, et in ea commode circumvoluitur, attollendo et deprimendo longiorem Regulam, in qua diuisiones existunt. In altero eadem longior Regula ad aequilibrium Horizontis saltem conuoluitur, et vna in Circulo, cuius diameter est 20. pedum, Azimutha ostendit, caeteris duobus aequalibus lateribus angulum Altitudinis sua Eleuatione depressioneque efficientibus. Suntque ambo maiora hoc Coperniano, et diligentissime elaborata. Nihilominus in Solis et Stellarum Altitudinibus praecise indagandis non tantum illis fidei adhibeo, quantum Quadrantibus et Sextantibus, praesertim maiusculis. Nec possibile existimo, Regularum fabricam ad exactissimam praecisionem, quae perpetuo sibi constet, redigere. Non tamen facile animum induco, Copernicum, in Altitudine Spicae ♍, quam lib. 3. cap. 2. proponit, multum aberrâsse, siue per hoc Parallaticum Instrumentum, siue alia quacunque ratione eam nactus est, eiusque Declinationem hinc prouenientem Werneriana Obseruatione multo certiorem esse, verisimilimum est. Alias enim nimium concitatus foret octauae Sphaerae motus, et Albategnianam celeritatem plus aequo anteuerteret. Quo etiam argumento vestras Stellarum Longitudines, paulo vlteriores esse, quam huius Sphaerae promotio admittit, comprobatur. Sed video me, dum lubentius tecum de ijs, quae ad rem Astronomicam faciunt, colloquor, modum in scribenda Epistola excedere. Idque eo potius admitto, tum quod hanc prolixitatem tibi non taediosam futuram, mihi persuadeam, tum etiam vt tarditatem hactenus in rescribendo admissam, copiosiori responso recompensatam habeas. Cum itaque tuis desiderijs, et ijs quae per literas mihi significasti, abunde, vt existimo, satisfactum sit, restat, vt nunc vela contraham, teque Deo Opt. Max. commendatum habeam.</p><p>Vale, et mecum vlterius de negotio Astronomico, saltem per Literas, siquidem coram (quod conuenientius esset) non licet, conferre nullatenus supersede: Quin sidera mecum, scalis minime fallacibus, ascendere omni temporis sereni opportunitate inceptam operam continuando elabora. Iterum vale.<br>Datae Vraniburgi 20 die Ianuarij, Anno 87. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most erudite man, of varied learning, but especially excelling in distinguished skill of Astronomy, Master Christoph Rothmann, Court Mathematician of the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his much-beloved friend.</b></p><p>The letter which you gave me last year in the month of April — moved both by the Mandate of your most illustrious Prince and by your own benevolence toward me and my studies — most erudite <b>Rothmann</b>, was far most acceptable to me, especially on this account, that it contained in manifold ways a more recondite <b>conference</b> with me about Astronomical matters. But why I reply to the most illustrious Prince's letter, and to yours too, later than your expectation, I have set forth my excuse to the most illustrious Prince. And I do not doubt that his Highness, by that Heroic candour of his mind, will take it in fair and good part. About the <b>celestial observations</b> too — both yours and mine, called to the balance against one another — I have dealt more amply in my letter to the most illustrious Prince, and communicated some of ours to his Highness, that I might comply with his clement request. About these, therefore, I judged it not worthwhile to write more copiously to you, lest I seem to re-sing the same song tediously — since from those [things] which were transmitted by me to the most illustrious Prince, these will doubtless come to your knowledge. About those [things], therefore, chiefly which were contained in your own letters — both the earlier and the second sent — I will now deal with you more fully. That <b>almost exact agreement</b> between your observations and ours in the <b>Comet of [15]85</b>, [and that it] pleased your most illustrious Prince and you so much that you perceived no small admiration and pleasure thence, was no less delightful for me to learn. And that on that account you transmitted to me your most erudite writing on this Comet (though not brought to the colophon), that I might thence more fully learn the congruence of your observations with mine, was far most welcome to me. I read through that writing once and again, and it pleased me wondrously. It were truly to be wished that those [things] which most of your Germans rather <b>blurted out</b> about some past comets, seen in former years, than Mathematically demonstrated by exquisite and infallible observations, had been elaborated with equal diligence and certainty. For then I should not have found, not without disgust, so great discrepancies between their tenets and ours in the position and motion of comets, and so great deviation from the heaven itself and from the kernel of the truth to be sought. That that Comet completed its course far above the elementary orb, <b>in the highest aether</b>, you most rightly establish with me — although the opinions of almost all Philosophers, both ancient and recent, gainsay [it]. In this only is found between us a very tiny discrepancy, and one of no moment: that you gather that it had <b>no parallax</b> at all, but I found it [to be] about one minute at the beginning, and afterward at least a half. Although, to confess the truth, I myself could from time to time discern none at all, on account of its so slight tenuity. But because from the proper motion it was lawful to infer that it was situated far below the eighth Sphere, though above the Sun, therefore it was consonant that it obtain some — though very tiny and scarcely perceptible — diversity of aspect. [The more so] since not Mars alone, especially when <b>acronychal</b>, introduces an observable parallax (as we made trial at the beginning of the year [15]82), but Jupiter [♃] itself too — to say nothing of the star Saturn [♄], where in so very slight a difference no place is left for the senses. But this discrepancy between us is so tiny that I seem to speak of it in vain. In the <b>Longitudes and Latitudes of the Comet</b> noted by you, a difference is found between our observations, a little greater [yet] itself too of almost no moment, which is so tiny that it scarcely reaches five or six minutes at most. Nay, it very often plainly agrees. But this minute discrepancy between us comes about, as I think, chiefly for this cause: that you assumed the places of the <b>Fixed [stars]</b>, of which use was made on both sides, a little otherwise than I. For by you <b>Aldebaran</b> is placed at <b>4° 7′ Gemini, latitude 5° 29′ South</b>, which I, from my observations, establish at <b>4° 1′ Gemini, latitude 5° 31′ South</b> — a discrepancy of at least 6 minutes in longitude, but of two in latitude, falling between the two positions. But the <b>bright [star] in the head of Aries, which you call the third</b>, you [put] at <b>1° 58′ Taurus, latitude 9° 57′ North</b>; but I assumed [it] at <b>1° 54′ Taurus, latitude 9° 57′ North</b> — a difference of at least 4 in longitude, none at all in latitude intervening between us. And since I used chiefly these same Fixed [stars] in investigating this Comet's appearances, a slight variety could come about between us in establishing the Comet's places from this (though very tiny) diversity. Although this discrepancy too in the very places of the Fixed [stars] is so tiny that it scarcely merits mention. But that the position of these two Fixed [stars], as to longitude and latitude, stands most exactly as our notation gives, the very <b>distance</b> between the two — which I, nearly the same as you, several times took from the heaven [at] <b>35° 32¼′</b> — confirms. But if you investigate the interval from the complements of the latitudes and the angle comprehended by the difference of longitude, by the reckonings of Triangles, in your assignation nearly one minute is in excess. But that this little [thing], though of no great moment in itself, [is] weighed so subtly — the vulgar contemplators of the Heaven will deservedly laugh, to whom even ten minutes are reckoned as not to be reckoned. And that not undeservedly, since they often (it is shameful to say) are blind to halves or even whole degrees. The <b>difference of our Meridians</b>, which in my judgment equals at least a fifth of an hour, at the beginning, when the Comet's motion was swifter, could vary our observations by only one minute; but at the end, when it became slower, an insensible and almost no difference was caused thence. The difference between the <b>maximum Declination</b> assumed on both sides imports almost nothing. For I presupposed it [at] <b>23° 31′</b> (although I found it a half-minute greater, which I had judged should be neglected in this place), than which you make it [at] <b>23° 30′</b>, at least one minute smaller than ours. Nor indeed can I understand how you could gather this, and not rather the same as mine, or even a greater maximum Declination, from the observations of the Sun of the most illustrious Prince, had by Quadrants (though then not so corrected). For in the Solar observations transmitted to me I find almost everywhere the maximum <b>Solstitial Altitude 62° 12′</b>, to which if you add the Parallax, there comes out the true [altitude] <b>62° 13′ 20″</b>. Hence, the Altitude of the Equator (such as was then presupposed, 38° 42′) being subtracted, the <b>declination of the Ecliptic from the Equator</b> comes out <b>23° 31⅓′</b>, insensibly differing from ours. But if you take into counsel the maximum Altitude 62° 13′ or 14′ (which is also offered somewhere) and the truer Altitude of the Pole, it comes out still greater — so that on this account I do not see why one should not stand by our maximum Declination even from the very observations of the most illustrious Prince then known, since [a value] similar, and rather greater than smaller, is drawn thence when the single [data] are collated. But I do not doubt that by your more correct Instruments you have afterward found out with me that this maximum Declination is now <b>23° 31½′</b>, as we too found by experiment, both in the past year and in several preceding [ones], by several machines — although the Sun's meridian Altitude in each Solstice, parallax applied, gives it at least [at] <b>23° 27′</b>, more than 4 minutes smaller, agreeing indeed better with the notation of Copernicus and his contemporaries, but on account of the <b>Solar Refraction</b> arising in the lower position near the winter [solstice], subject to so sensible an error. For the Sun is not elevated for us a full 11 degrees, at the very noon near the winter Solstice. Nor was it much higher for Copernicus at Frauenburg. But I most lovingly ask that, at the first occasion, you signify to me, among other [things], what <b>maximum and minimum Elevation of the Sun</b> you have found there by your neoteric and more correct Instruments, and likewise the same about both Equinoxes, or rather through the whole course of the Year, that I may the more fully institute a comparison with our observations; and that you would add the more exact meridian Altitudes of the stars too, both the Circumpolar and others; and that you would not grudge to impart to me your whole writing on the last Comet — I ask again and again. For I do not doubt that you have now put the last hand to it.</p><p>But that, in the portion of it transmitted to me, you assert that <b>the whole Heaven is aereal, and does not consist of solid matter — nay, is nothing other than Air itself</b> — that I would easily concede to you, provided you understand the air which is above the Moon to be much subtler than this Elemental [air], so that it deserves the name rather of <b>aether, most fluid and subtle, than of elemental Air</b>. For I, [now] for many years, have not been of the opinion that any <b>orbs really exist in the heaven</b>, and that the matter of the heaven is hard and impervious, and that the Stars are revolved only by the motion of the orbs. For many absurdities follow thence; and if nothing else, surely so many <b>Comets</b> observed by us most certainly to exercise [their] course in the aether in some preceding years, which accompanied the leading of no orb, sufficiently refute this. I have therefore always judged that <b>a natural science of motion is congenital — or rather divinely implanted — in each Planet</b>, by which they are compelled to observe the norm of their course most regularly and constantly in the most fluid and subtle aether, needing no support or promoter — just as the <b>Earth</b> too, in the fluid air, stabilized by no support, resting on its own centre alone on every side, is firmed. But that I really establish [it] thus, another reasoning too about the disposition of the celestial Revolutions, far more competent than either the Ptolemaic or the Copernican, newly explored by me (experience itself teaching), much confirms — about which there is now no place for speaking. But that that most celebrated Philosopher of our age, <b>Petrus Ramus</b>, thought that <b>Astronomy could be constituted without Hypotheses, by Logical reasonings</b>, lacks foundation. He indeed proposed this opinion to me 16 years ago, when we were together at <b>Augsburg</b>, and was at the same time an exhorter that, after I had reduced the course of the stars into exact order by Hypotheses, I should affect to attempt the same without these. He added this reason that it could be done — that he had read that the <b>Egyptians</b> of old had a most easy knowledge of Astronomy; and since the reckoning of hypotheses seems difficult and intricate, they must have known the course of the stars by some other more compendious and plainer way, and therefore without all hypotheses. But I resisted him, showing that <b>without hypotheses the celestial Phenomena cannot be reduced to a sure science, nor be excused [accounted for] so as to be understood</b>. And that that Egyptian ease existed only in the <b>planetary Equatoria</b> [equatorium-instruments], by which they freed themselves from tedious computation, when the expeditious reckoning of Ephemerides was not yet in use. But since he — a man otherwise endowed with perspicacious talent, and a lover of truth if anyone [is] — seemed to me not to have thoroughly perceived the inner chambers of this art, and not to have noticed the variety in the motion of the stars by no means recurring at fixed times of the year, I could obtain nothing from him in this part, nor wished [to]. He still has very many followers who hope the same can be done — but who neither understand the matter itself nor will ever bring [it] into effect. For since <b>all things consist of number, weight, and measure</b>, without these too nothing in the visible world can be explained. But hypotheses show nothing other than the <b>measure of the apparent motion by a circle and other figures</b>, which Arithmetic resolves into numbers. Without these, if anyone wish to comprehend the motions of the Stars, he must (as the saying is) invoke Fortune, and imagine a supramundane reckoning, beyond the human intellect and plainly incorporeal and more than angelic. But enough of these.</p><p>About the <b>Instruments and the observations made hence</b>, it remains that I now confer with you more fully. There is no [reason] for you to doubt that <b>Wittich</b> received here, while he was with me, that method of restoring Instruments which he proposed to your most illustrious Prince — although he did not either notice everything by like reasoning, or expound [it] there faithfully enough. For when he had come hither in the year, if I am not mistaken, [15]80, he easily insinuated himself into my familiarity — partly because I greatly esteemed the man for his ingenious skill in Mathematics, especially as regards Geometry, partly because he spontaneously offered that he would remain perpetually with me, and would be to me a <b>faithful Achates</b> [companion] in Astronomical studies for the whole time of [his] life. Whence it came about that I liberally communicated my inventions to him, and concealed none of those which I then had ready, or had resolved to elaborate in the future. But when he had stayed with me a little beyond a quarter of a year, and thought himself now sufficiently possessed of his desires, he pretended that his <b>uncle at Breslau</b> had departed this life, whose inheritance would bring him great gain if he were present in good time. Having therefore easily obtained leave to depart, he promised to return hither within 7 or 8 weeks. But from that time I have seen neither him nor any letter set forth from him, nor heard of him, where he might be, before I read the letter of your most illustrious Prince written to Lord <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, from which I at once understood that he had there, for the most part, <b>reproduced the form and reckoning of the Instruments which he had seen here</b>. Which perhaps was done by him meanwhile elsewhere too. And in truth, by this means he did me a thing not unwelcome, since I should much wish that in many places there be very many who would scrutinize the appearances of the stars with me by exquisite and least-fallacious Instruments. Yet if he <b>sells my inventions — acquired by many years, long Experience, and no small expense and labour — as his own, and does not confess through whom he had them</b>, he commits a thing alien to a good and grateful man, and to Mathematical sincerity and integrity. But I understand, partly from your letters, partly from the report of my domestic who was with you, that he did not order everything for the most illustrious Prince competently enough, as he had seen [it] here. The <b>division</b> indeed, which we use similarly in almost all Instruments, I find he indicated rightly enough; and in the <b>slits of the sights</b> [<i>rimulae pinnacidiorum</i>], especially opportune for nocturnal observations of the stars (which invention, when he first saw [it] here, he said he had not come into Denmark in vain, and esteemed [it] more precious than much gold), he imitated certain [things], though imperfectly. For while he sought a compendium in these, and presumed to effect with <b>two slits</b> the same as we [do] with <b>four</b>, he made a loss of the truth to be sought, and by detracting from our inventions, derogated from himself and from the certainty of [his] observations. One of my [people] reports that he heard from him at that time, when he was here, that he himself, when he should imitate the construction of our Instruments, said that <b>he would effect the same with fewer slits, and reserve this compendium for himself</b>. But how excellently he accomplished that, you have found by experience, not without the loss of many observations. I everywhere — especially in the <b>Quadrants</b> — apply <b>four slits in the lower sights, parallel on every side to the four sides of the upper sight</b>, which I sometimes make larger and looser, as the magnitude of the stars demands. For otherwise, by two slits (a lateral one and one set above), the transits and altitudes of the stars are by no means discerned centrally and exactly — which Experience easily shows. And unless you, taught by this, had applied a double visual slit vice versa to the lower sight, you would even now labour in vain in observing the stars exactly. But the <b>invention of that division by transverse points</b> (which you — whosesoever it be — proclaim ingenious) I first had at <b>Leipzig from Homelius</b>, more or less 24 years ago, when I was in the 17th year of [my] age — but only on a straight line (namely on the <b>Cross-staff</b>, to be divided into several little parts); by his imitation I then used it. But when afterward I began to investigate the Altitudes of the stars too by Quadrants, and found by experience that in smaller Instruments the vulgar division does not suffice for the least little portions, I turned to that subtlety of <b>Nonius</b> [Nunes] which he hands down in the third Proposition <i>De crepusculis</i>, and rendered the same still more exact by several subdivisions, Tables too being computed, from which the altitude of any point might be known scrupulously without delay. And when the matter itself taught that not even this Nonian invention had in the issue what it promised in the approach, I began to consider whether that aforesaid division — by which a straight line is most subtly cut by transverse points into smaller particles — could be applied also to <b>curves and circles</b>. And although at first the reasoning did not seem equal, on account of the disconvenience of straights and curves, yet since in so small an interval (as of five or ten points), curved lines drawn at a small interstice from one another do not differ sensibly from straights, I noticed that this business succeeds conveniently enough; and therefore I have for many years now always used this division in <b>Quadrants, Sextants, [and] Armillae</b> with happy success, and I rejoice that it has been communicated to you (by whatever occasion), so that you may the more conveniently scrutinize the stars with equal precision with me — so that our observations, collated together, reach the same mark of truth scrupulously, as is fitting (which too I experience comes about not without pleasure). The instrument of the <b>Sextant</b>, devised by me more or less 18 years ago when I was living at <b>Augsburg</b> (which I also showed there to <b>Petrus Ramus</b> when he was with me, together with that very great <b>Quadrant of 14 cubits</b> which I had had built outside the city in a certain garden of <b>Consul Hainzel</b> — [Ramus] who avidly demanded a description of them from me), I have had in use ever since, because I had found by sure reasoning and much Experience that by the <b>Cross-staff</b>, of whatever magnitude or division it might be, settled distances are by no means found. Wherefore, by [applying] the <b>sixth part of a circle</b> to rules (that the handling of the Instrument might become more convenient), I undertook to attempt the same with a surer outcome. And on that account I gave to this Instrument the name of <b>Sextant</b>. But that one which Wittich saw here, which was of <b>steel</b>, lacked that <b>little chain</b> which you applied. For by a transverse <b>Screw</b> [<i>Cochlea</i>] the distances in the Instrument were narrowed and widened. And although that Screw was extended in a straight [line], yet because it had revolvable pegs where it was fixed, it nonetheless sufficed for circular motion. This convenience too Wittich seems to have neglected, in that he did not indicate it — so that on that account it was necessary for you to apply that little chain in its place. In the sights you have limited certain [things] otherwise than I then had in that Sextant, which seem to me quite conveniently ordered. But that Sextant, by which distances were taken by a single Observer, has long since ceased to be in use with me. For in its place I assiduously use two others, which I call <b>Trigonic</b> (for distinction from the <b>bifurcated</b> one which I have), in taking the distances of the stars, which each require two Observers, and by <b>lines parallel about a Cylinder resting at the centre</b> exhibit most exactly the intervals of the stars consonant with one another and with the whole circuit of the Heaven, and complete the observation with almost no trouble. But the <b>Design</b> [Idea] of these, somehow represented, I sent (if I am not mistaken) lately to the most illustrious Prince to be inspected. But your Sextant too, although built by a certain other reasoning, and one more similar to my first, exhibits the distances of the stars exact enough and perfectly consonant with mine, as you can perceive from the <b>Catalogue of the intervals of some stars</b> now transmitted to your most illustrious Prince.</p><p>About the <b>Refractions</b>, which I last wrote to the most illustrious Prince, there is no [reason] for you to doubt that the matter stands thoroughly so. For in this last-elapsed year too, by an <b>Armilla 10 feet in Diameter</b>, which is revolvable about an axis corresponding to the Poles of the world, I found that the Sun, about the summer Solstice, insinuates a <b>Refraction of Declination, near the 30th degree of altitude</b>, both before and after noon, <b>greater than a half of one minute</b>; but about the Horizon makes the same [at] <b>28 minutes</b> — and that when the Sun grazed the visible Horizon with [its] lower limb. But if these Refractions of the Declinations be derived into altitude, account being had too of the <b>Parallax</b> (which toward the Horizon somewhat inhibits the Refraction itself), they come out still greater. In the <b>fixed stars</b>, however, this diversity does not seem so great — although in these too I find [it] greater than can be gathered from your assignation written to me. For the <b>brighter [star] in the right side of Perseus</b> [Algol/Mirfak region — see Apparatus], you, at altitude <b>9° 42′</b> (as [it is] least in the Meridian toward the North), assert the Refraction to be at least <b>2⅓′</b>; which I, however, in the same star's altitude (also in the Meridian to the North, at its lowest, which here is <b>14° 16′</b>), found to be <b>2½′</b>, a little greater than yours — when it ought rather to be smaller, since for you this star was observed lower by more than 4½ degrees. So too the <b>right shoulder of Perseus</b>, at altitude <b>17° 45½′</b>, I noticed makes a Refraction of <b>1⅚′</b>; so that on that account the altitudes of the <b>Cassiopeia</b> stars, which you took below the Pole for investigating its altitude, do not exactly correspond to the upper [ones], such that the Pole be exactly intermediate. For they are not free of all suspicion of Refraction, since they scarcely reach the 20th degree of altitude with you in that position — whereas at the 18th degree the Refraction reaches nearly two minutes, as is clear from the preceding indication. These, therefore, I leave to you to examine more considerately. But the most evident discrepancy in the refractions, between your and my observations, is recognized from this: that you indicate the <b>tail of Cygnus</b>, at altitude <b>5° 18′</b>, to make a Refraction of at least <b>5⅔′</b> — whereas I, by diligent and several times repeated Observation, found the <b>head of Algol</b>, at altitude <b>5° 21½′</b> (nearly similar to yours in the tail of Cygnus), to make a Refraction of <b>9¼′</b>, nearly double yours. These, therefore, investigate by occasion more accurately, with well-corrected Instruments, and you will see the whole matter stands thus — unless perhaps (which, however, I should not easily believe) the <b>diverse nature of the Horizons</b> introduces a greater insinuation of Refraction here than with you. Whence it would follow that in more Northern places it comes out ampler than in Southern — and that even if the air be quite pure and serene (which is necessarily required in this observation especially) on both sides near the bound[ary]. A deeper knowledge of which thing your observations, more exactly repeated (as I advised), will successively supply. Wherefore, although you hold it persuaded that <b>no Refraction occurs, not even in the Sun, in a position higher than 20°</b>, because the vapours do not ascend higher — so that you may more probably prove that the matter of the heaven does not differ from the air, the [theory] of <b>Alhazen and Witelo</b> being repudiated (on account of diverse transparencies, the excessive Refraction which would reach to the zenith somewhat by degrees) — yet, since experience (as I indicated above) testifies that even in the Sun, beyond the 30th degree, some — though very tiny — trace of Refraction appears, [which] can scarcely be excused by vapours (since they are not raised so high), and the fixed stars too at altitude 18° admit a Refraction of nearly two minutes (as we proved before by the right shoulder of Perseus at [its] least northern altitude), it will surely be necessary to establish that that <b>celestial air</b>, as you wish, is far subtler and more transparent than this Elemental Air — or rather, speaking more properly, that the <b>diaphaneity of the Aether is purer and more perspicuous than the diaphaneity of the Air</b>. But since there is not so great a difference between these two transparencies as between Air and water, and [since] it is successively diminished where they touch one another — so that the supreme portion of the Air, contiguous to the Moon's Revolutions, differs almost nothing from the aether itself (for that an Element of fire exists there, as the Aristotelians feign, Experience disproves by this sole reasoning, since far greater Refraction of the stars would come about hence, if their light reached us through <b>triple transparencies</b>) — therefore too a very tiny Refraction is caused, except in so far as it is increased by vapours in the lower position; and when the stars appear higher, it is rendered plainly insensible, nor grows so much as the aforesaid reasoning of Alhazen and Witelo induces. For they thought the <b>Heaven consists of solid transparent matter, far diverse from the Air itself</b>.</p><p>That you have noticed that the <b>fixed Stars change their latitudes, and that proportionally, according to the variation of the obliquity of the Ecliptic</b>, pleases me greatly. For I too long since gathered with myself that some such thing had happened — since our observations, collated with the Ptolemaic notation, attested that most stars in the summer and northern semicircle toward the Ecliptic had increased [their] Latitude as much as the change of Obliquity would require, but in the southern [semicircle] had diminished the same nearly as much; and that the contrary happened by like reckoning in the winter semicircle. And therefore it was indicated, not obscurely, that this comes about through the change of the maximum Declination, on account of the <b>approach and recession of the Ecliptic to the Stars themselves</b>. But since I found certain Stars — even among the more illustrious, especially the <b>bright [star] of Vultur</b> [Altair] and <b>Aldebaran</b> and the <b>Greater Dog</b> [Sirius] — to cry out against this speculation in the Ptolemaic numbers of Latitude, I began to examine this business by a deeper search, so that the doubts which arose might be cut away. And since this matter is of great moment, and noticed by no one else (that I know), you alone excepted, I will set forth somewhat more fully, from my considerations, certain [things] in this part, so that it may be better permitted to take aim at the mark of truth, all doubts set aside. Since I held the numbers of Longitudes and latitudes of the Stars assigned in the <b>Ptolemaic Table</b> [<i>Abacus</i>] not undeservedly suspect — because they do not everywhere agree with one another and with the Heaven itself, account being had of the intervals of the stars (which remain unvaried) — I scrutinized the whole matter somewhat more deeply, and that from the <b>Declinations of certain fixed Stars which the same Ptolemy, in chapter 3 of book 7 of the Almagest, recounts as observed by Timochares, Hipparchus, and himself</b>; to which I applied their Longitudes, such as it was fitting them to have at each of those times. In which matter too, not much trusting the Ptolemaic intervals, I used a peculiar caution, so that I constituted <b>Spica of Virgo [♍] as the foundation of the rest</b>. For it seems consonant that it was observed more surely, because it was near the Equinoctial, and because it is so often repeated by Ptolemy. And since he himself asserts that in the time of his observations it declined a half-degree toward the South, [whereas] <b>Timochares</b> found [it] <b>1° 24′</b> and <b>Hipparchus</b> at least <b>36′</b> more Northern than the Equator, it follows — Spica's Latitude being assumed on both sides [as] <b>2° Southern</b> (as it was explored by the Moon at that time, and as it is also found now nearly) — that its Longitude was, for <b>Timochares 21° 53′</b>, for <b>Hipparchus 23° 53′</b>, for <b>Ptolemy 26° 38′ of Virgo</b>; and therefore from Timochares to Hipparchus by 2 degrees, from Hipparchus to Ptolemy by 2¾°, this and all the other fixed Stars were advanced. Wherefore, these places of Spica being presupposed, I applied the Longitudes of certain other Fixed [stars], whose Declinations were established from the ancients, to the time of the observations of those same authors — namely Timochares, Hipparchus, and Ptolemy — and that by this law: that whatever difference, forward or backward, as to the interval of Longitude from Spica, they are now found by our observations to have, I constituted just as much also in the elongation from Spica's place fitting their time, so that the <b>true Longitude of the Stars with respect to this Star</b> might become known, the Ptolemaic notation being neglected (which is more rarely found complete in all [its] numbers). For it is not possible that the Longitudes of the Fixed [stars] vary otherwise in one Star than in another, since all are in one orb, and move uniformly about one and the same Pole. Whence it will be necessary that the differences of Longitudes were the same in Ptolemy's times, in all Stars, which are even now found. When, therefore, I had adapted the Longitudes of the Stars by this reasoning, hence, and from the Declinations which Ptolemy recounts in the cited chapter, the <b>maximum obliquity of the Zodiac being applied, which in their age was 23° 51⅓′</b>, I inquired also into the Latitudes of the same — which could be done not difficultly. For from the given difference of the Star's Longitude from the nearest Tropic, one angle of the Triangle is established, of which two sides also are given — one from the complement of the Declination (or the addition to the Quadrant, as the business requires), the other from the difference of the Poles of the Ecliptic and the Equator; therefore the third [side] easily becomes known by the laws of Spherical Triangles, by which the very <b>Latitude</b> of the Star sought, such as it then was, comes [to be] known. In this manner, I say, I examined the Latitudes of the Stars from the diverse assumed Declinations of the Ancients, the due Longitudes being applied. And although in this way too I found not in all Stars the same consonance I hoped — because their Declinations were either not observed scrupulously enough by the Ancients, or because some <b>errors lurk here and there in the assignation of the numbers</b> (which, however, that I might guard against as far as in me lay, I used also a certain old copy of the Almagest, published more or less 70 years ago at <b>Venice</b>, which expresses the Declinations not in cyphers [Arabic numerals], as the <b>Trapezuntine</b> version, but in aliquot parts of degrees, by letters) — nonetheless I judged that a greater certainty underlies these than the Latitudes assigned in the Ptolemaic Catalogue; and wherever an evident incoherence appeared, I took the Ptolemaic numbers too into counsel. But what, from these, I explored in the Latitudes of certain Fixed [stars], drawn out from their times down to now, I will now indicate, for the most part, with what brevity is possible.</p><p>First, considering the Stars about the <b>summer Tropic</b>, I find from the Declinations of <b>both heads of Gemini [♊]</b> that they now obtain a greater Latitude than in the Ancients' times, and that according to the very reckoning of the changed Declination of the Ecliptic. For the <b>Northern head of Gemini</b> [Castor], by the aforesaid tenor, is found to have had, in Timochares's time, a Latitude of nearly <b>9° 43′</b>, [in] Hipparchus's <b>9° 42′</b>, [in] Ptolemy's nearly <b>9° 45′</b> — whence it is gathered that it was about their times <b>9° 43′</b> nearly, which is now <b>10° 2′</b>. Therefore increased by 19 minutes, as the recession of the Ecliptic in that place requires. In like manner the <b>Southern head of Gemini</b> [Pollux], from Hipparchus's Declination and the rest (of which I spoke) given, is found to have had a Latitude of <b>6° 19½′</b>, but from Ptolemy's nearly <b>6° 22½′</b>, of which the mean is <b>6° 21′</b>, the Latitude fitting the Star in their age, which is now found by exact reckoning to be <b>6° 38′</b> — so that on that account too it has changed [its] Latitude nearly as much as the altered obliquity of the Zodiac requires. So too <b>Capella</b> is found to have obtained, [for] Hipparchus and Ptolemy, a Latitude from the assigned Declinations of <b>22⅚°</b> (although the Ptolemaic numbers establish this at <b>22° 30′</b>); whence we shall make this more probable, somewhat limiting [it], if we put the Latitude then [at] <b>22⅚°</b>, which is now found to be <b>22° 50½°′</b> [22° 50½′] — also varied in nearly due manner by reason of the changed Ecliptic. A document of the same matter is afforded also by <b>both shoulders of Orion</b>. For the <b>right</b> had a Latitude from the Declinations of Timochares and Hipparchus of very nearly <b>16⅓°</b> (some error underlies the Ptolemaic Declination in this place), which is now found to obtain <b>16 1/10</b>, also rendered smaller in nearly due manner. The <b>left shoulder of the same Orion</b>, from Hipparchus's and Ptolemy's Declinations, obtained in that age a Latitude of nearly <b>17° 5′</b>, which it now has [at] <b>16° 53′</b> — also diminished as it were proportionally, by reason of the approaching Ecliptic. Not otherwise the <b>Heart of Leo [♌]</b> [Regulus] — which star Ptolemy observed a little more diligently — obtained for itself a Latitude of <b>0° 10′ North</b>; although this agrees more precisely with Hipparchus's Declination than with his own, we by accurate observations found this <b>26 minutes more Northern than the Ecliptic</b>, so that <b>16 minutes</b> now are in excess from the Ancients' findings — which, effected on account of the recession of the Ecliptic, appears conformable enough to our intention. But on the other side the Star <b>Aldebaran</b> introduces some impediment, since the Ptolemaic numbers give its Latitude [as] <b>5⅙° Southern</b> — which too is not so alien from his Declination. Whose Latitude, however, I could not find smaller than <b>5° 30½′</b>, a third of one degree greater than the Ptolemaic, when rather it ought to be smaller from the assumed Hypothesis of the Ecliptic's change. Whence in the observation of this Star, as to latitude, I judge that no small error underlies the Ptolemaic Declination itself — which the Declinations of the same [star] by Timochares and Hipparchus prove, although neither do these stand absolutely in their numbers. For Timochares's Declination gives the Star a Latitude of <b>5° 56′</b>, greater than just; Hipparchus's <b>5° 33′</b>, somewhat smaller than due — although each exceeds the Ptolemaic (as the matter requires). Nor do I see how it could have been possible that the Declination of this Star (which then dwelt about the first <b>decury of the Dodecatemorion of Taurus [♉]</b>) was changed from Timochares to Ptolemy by <b>2¼°</b>, whereas, about the very Equinox, Spica of Virgo meanwhile altered the same by no more than 1° 54′. And therefore I judge that no slight error about the Latitude of Aldebaran crept in upon Ptolemy (provided the numbers drawn down to us stand rightly). And if we take the mean between Timochares's and Hipparchus's Latitude — which, namely, comes out from their Declinations — as more probable, limiting an otherwise perplexed matter, the latitude of the <b>eye of Taurus</b> then comes out [at] <b>5¾°</b>, which does not deviate much from the inquiry we intend. But that the Latitude which Ptolemy attributes to this Star is utterly erroneous appears from this — that he assigns in his Catalogue to the <b>Northern eye of Taurus</b> a Latitude of <b>3° 0′</b>, which we found [at] nearly <b>2⅔°</b>, and therefore by the Ptolemaic assignation a little more smaller than the changed Obliquity of the Ecliptic requires — whereas Aldebaran showed the contrary. And the <b>distance which is between both these Stars in the eyes</b>, which by repeated Observation I found to be <b>3° 10¼′</b>, proves that the Ptolemaic assignation in one or the other (or both) evidently deflects. For if, from the complement of the Latitude of each Star which he attributes to them, and the difference of Longitudes (namely, from two known sides with the comprehended angle), we inquire the distance, it comes out [at] <b>2° 19′</b>, falling short of the true by 51 minutes. Wherefore, if the latitude of the more Northern eye from the Ptolemaic numbers be nearer the truth, Aldebaran must necessarily have had a greater Latitude than he assigns. For it is not possible that it was then nearer the Northern eye than now. From these [things], therefore, it appears that the Latitude which Ptolemy assigns to Aldebaran is less settled, and therefore in this — in that it seems to gainsay our intention — does not merit credit.</p><p>I come now to the Stars looking to the <b>winter semicircle of the Ecliptic</b>, and especially near the <b>winter Tropic</b>, where (that the demonstration of the proposed matter may come out more evident) occurs the <b>bright [star] of Vultur volans</b> [Altair, in Aquila], whose Latitude, although the Ptolemaic Catalogue exhibits [it at] <b>29° 10′</b> — so that on that account at first sight it seems to gainsay our opinion — yet, when I draw out its Latitude from the Declinations of the Ancients, so far is this Star from disturbing what we intend, that none is found to stabilize this very [thing] more exactly and surely. For this was, about the times of the Ancients of whom I speak, nearest as to Longitude to the <b>winter Tropic</b>, and therefore its Latitude was easily clear even from the Declination alone. For in the year of Timochares's observations, when it was scarcely a sixth part of one degree before the Solstitial Colure itself, its Declination was found [at] <b>5° 48′</b>, and therefore the Latitude (the maximum Declination of that age being assumed) comes out [at] <b>29° 39½′</b> — to which the Hipparchan notation too is a supporter, yielding exactly the same Declination, because this varied insensibly about the Solstice. Thence the Star's Latitude too proceeded [at] <b>29° 39′</b>. And although the Ptolemaic declination, which was two minutes greater, seems to render this Latitude narrower by one and a half minutes (because he perhaps did not apply the most exact precision in the very minutes), yet this imports nothing, but sufficiently attests, together with the rest, that the Latitude of the bright [star] in <b>Aquila</b> was, in that age of the world, very nearly <b>29° 39′</b>. And since it is now found [at] <b>29° 21′</b>, it is manifest that it has meanwhile narrowed [its] Latitude by 18 minutes — as much as the nearer approach of the Ecliptic in that place demands — so that even from this single Star near the winter Tropic (especially if the heads of Gemini near the summer Tropic in the other half be used together, vice versa) it can be sufficiently proved that the fixed Stars vary their Latitudes proportionally by reason of the changed obliquity of the Zodiac. Since so unanimously in this Star the observed Declinations of those three most excellent Artificers agree (which too were nearly unvaried, because the Star, near to the Tropic as to longitude, changed [its] Declination insensibly, as I said) — whence too, if any doubt can lurk in its Longitude, it will insinuate no error in those [things] which we intend in that place. But <b>Antares</b> too, in the same half of the Ecliptic, further confirms the same, which is found, both from Timochares's and Hipparchus's Declinations, [at] <b>4° 14′</b> (an evident error must underlie the Ptolemaic Declination in this Star, because it gives its Latitude [at] <b>4° 37′</b>, which, however, he himself in the Catalogue of Fixed [stars] puts [at] <b>4° 0′</b>, much dissenting from himself) — whereas now the same is <b>4° 24′</b>. It is therefore manifest that the Latitude in this Star too has been increased not much differently from what the recession of the Ecliptic in that place demands. The <b>Southern pan of Libra [♎]</b> too — since credit must rather be given to Hipparchus's Declination, because this seems more probable — obtained in that age a Latitude of nearly a half-degree North, which is now found to be <b>26 minutes</b>, rendered narrower on account of the approach of the Ecliptic. Although not only Timochares's Declination, but Ptolemy's own too, deviates intolerably in this Star. For the former gives a Latitude of scarcely 17 minutes, but the latter at least 2 — whereas the Ptolemaic Catalogue itself exhibits it [at] 40 minutes, which, though it be too much, and more than is necessary, [yet] supports our intention, and exists nearer the truth. The Latitude of the <b>Northern pan</b> too, both Timochares's and Hipparchus's Declination gives [at] nearly <b>8° 48′</b>, which from our Observation is now <b>8° 36′</b> nearly — so that on that account in this Star too the Latitude is found narrowed by reason of the approach of the Ecliptic. For [the fact] that the Ptolemaic Declination exhibits it [at] at least <b>8° 25′</b> (with which his Table nearly agrees, indicating the same [at] 8° 30′) — whence what we propose seems to be shaken — we do not heed. For that the Ptolemaic Declination and Latitude is less certain easily appears from this, that it admits a Declination changed from Hipparchus to himself [of] 1° 24′, whereas Spica of Virgo meanwhile varied the same by only 1° 6′. Wherefore I judge one must rather stand by the Declinations of Timochares and Hipparchus in that Star, since they agree with each other and give the same interval of change which Spica of Virgo is gathered to effect for them.</p><p>From these Stars, therefore, dwelling both about the Northern and the Southern semicircle of the Ecliptic, I judge it has been demonstrated more than sufficiently that, <b>for the variation of the Ecliptic's Obliquity, the Latitudes of the fixed Stars are altered along [with it] proportionally</b>. Nor would any place for doubt about this matter seem to remain, did not that <b>most splendid [star] in the mouth of the Greater Dog</b> [Sirius] hint something contrary. Whose Latitude, from Timochares's Declination (which was 16⅓), is found [at] <b>39° 18′</b>; from Hipparchus's (which was 16°) comes out the same [at] <b>39° 10′</b>, such as Ptolemy too puts in his Table. For his Declination too, a quarter of a degree smaller than Hipparchus's, gives the Star a Latitude of <b>39° 7⅓′</b>, which approaches nearly to a sixth part of a degree beyond the whole [degrees] — since he had no reckoning of two or three minutes in this part — so that, both by Hipparchus and by Ptolemy, this Star's Latitude is found to have then been <b>39⅙° Southern</b>, which, however, we in this age have found [at] <b>39° 31′</b>, which is greater than the Ancients' tenets by a third part of one degree, when rather it ought now to be just as much smaller, other things being equal. The resolution of which knot is surely not easily obvious. For the <b>uniform consensus of all</b> difficultly admits that some error lurks in the Ancients' Observations; nor does it seem credible that they so sensibly hallucinated in so Illustrious a Star, which is the greatest and most shining of all the Fixed [stars]. And since our observations too in this very Star lack all doubt, I cannot sufficiently wonder how this exhibits a greater Latitude now than of old. For it cannot be conceded that it is not subject to the same laws as the rest of the Fixed [stars], but [must] equally with them vary [its] Latitude at the nod of the Ecliptic's Obliquity. Wherefore, ventilating this matter variously, I can build nothing else than that the Ancients' Observations in the Declination of this Star — though perhaps had well enough from the heaven — were nonetheless less rightly transmitted to us <b>by the fault of the transcribers</b>. For a similar error could be committed in copying the single [data]. For [the fact] that the notation of its Latitude in the Ptolemaic Table subscribes to the Declinations makes little for the matter. Since otherwise the Latitudes assigned there are often found to dissent not only from the Declinations but from the Heaven itself, if they be called to the balance by the distance of the stars. Nor can any prejudice be brought against this assertion — about the change of the Latitude of the Stars to the varied Obliquity of the Ecliptic — on account of this single Star (though more shining than the rest), [against] those [things] which we have proved by several documents to stand rightly in so many others; especially since the brighter of the <b>Lesser Dog</b> [Procyon] supports our position, which in Ptolemy's times was near the Colure of the Solstices, and then had, from his numbers, a Latitude of <b>16° 10′</b>, which it now obtains [at] <b>15° 57′</b>, smaller on account of the approach of the Ecliptic — though not by so much as that distance from the Tropic requires, because Ptolemy did not reckon the five minutes which are wanting. But [the fact] that <b>Spica of Virgo</b> was established by the Ancients to obtain two degrees in Latitude, such as it is also found to retain in this age (or, if we apply the highest precision, at least a half-minute smaller), when rather it ought now to be 3 or 4 minutes greater, from the changed Obliquity of the Ecliptic — this nonetheless cannot shake this our position. Since those Ancients had almost no reckoning of 3 or 4 minutes; for they noted both the Longitudes and Latitudes of the Stars only in sixths of one degree, as may be seen in the Ptolemaic Table. There is added this too, that from the <b>visible conjunction [occultation] of the Moon with this Star</b> they several times investigated its Latitude together with the Longitude — whereas the <b>Lunar Parallaxes</b> by no means stood so surely for them but that a hallucination of a few minutes could insinuate itself in this way, especially since they used the Ptolemaic Hypothesis in the Moon (for no other was known to the Ancients) — which our observations too attest sometimes exhibits a far greater approach of the Moon to the Earth than the truth of the matter demands, so that it turns out consonant that the Parallaxes deduced thence are less settled. But these I now treat perhaps more copiously than the laws of a Letter permit; wherefore, these foundations being laid, I leave to you, most learned Rothmann, everything to be weighed more deeply, and more to be superadded to these. Now I proceed to the rest.</p><p>That in the <b>Latitudes of the Stars</b> a discrepancy of a few minutes falls between the most illustrious Prince's observations and mine comes about chiefly for this: that the Declinations of the Stars, before that correction of the <b>perpendicular</b> which you signified to me in your last letter, were less settled by two minutes; the maximum Obliquity of the Zodiac too being assumed somewhat diversely, varying somewhat along [with it] in these. But I greatly rejoiced when I understood from your later letters that you had found out this error of the perpendicular; for otherwise your Declinations everywhere dissented from mine by nearly two minutes, because the Altitude of the Equator came out one minute smaller than the true, and [that] of the Stars one greater. Nor do I doubt that, if after this restitution of your Quadrant exactly to the level, you examine the Solar Altitudes exactly (Parallax too applied) with my restitution in the Sun's course, you will prove that I have reached the very mark with every possible subtlety, and that especially in the more <b>Northern Signs</b>. For in the <b>Southern</b> [signs], especially <b>Aquarius [♒] and Scorpio [♏]</b> (in <b>Sagittarius [♐] and Capricorn [♑]</b>, on account of the very tiny change of Declination, you know these are void), on account of sensible Refraction, you will find some discrepancy — which, however, can be guarded against by this very [refraction] previously known from elsewhere. In the <b>Lunar eclipse of the year 1584</b>, which you diligently seek — no less than [in] the Lunar obscurations of some preceding years — I found, accurately observed by me, that the first beginning was at <b>11ʰ 12ᵐ from the noon of 7 November</b>, the Middle at <b>13ʰ 8ᵐ</b>, the last End at <b>15ʰ 0ᵐ</b> (for these times are not always equal, as has hitherto been believed) — as I investigated by an infallible reckoning, from the distance of certain Equatorial Stars from the Meridian. But that <b>Wittich</b> found the middle of this eclipse at <b>Kassel [at] 13ʰ 3ᵐ</b>, as you wrote me, lacks exquisite precision. For your Meridian is at least a fifth of an hour (as I said above too) more Western than ours. The middle of the eclipse ought therefore to have been there, just after midnight, [at] <b>0ʰ 56ᵐ</b> [i.e. 12ʰ 56ᵐ] — half a quarter-hour being in excess in the Wittichian Observation. How diligent, therefore, this notation of time was, let its author see, whose diligence in investigating the stars from the heaven has long been perceived by me — or, to speak more truly, [is] suspect. <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b>, eminent Mathematician among the Rostockers, our singular friend, observed the same Eclipse there, and found its middle at <b>1ʰ 4ᵐ after midnight</b>, at a time a little earlier than ours — when rather it ought to be nearly the same, since the difference of Meridians is insensible. But he, by a <b>brass Quadrant — but a smallish one</b>, once granted him by me, from the Altitudes of the Stars (taken only in degrees), scrutinized the times in a broader manner — which in the Stars, especially [those] tending to the Meridian, is subject to evident error. Nonetheless, hence it is still more clear that Wittich's observation was less exact, and therefore does not suffice for more rightly knowing the interval of our Meridians. And since by an <b>observation of the Moon near the 90th degree</b> [of the ecliptic] the same can be accomplished, diligent care being applied, I have ready very many such Lunar considerations (when [the Moon] lacked Parallax of Longitude), faithfully taken from the heaven in some preceding years. If, therefore, I knew on what days the Moon was ascertained by you in the same manner, I would certainly communicate mine too, that a mutual comparison might be instituted. Nay, you will find three such Moon-observations transmitted to your most illustrious Prince, in the assignation of the places of all the Planets, within one Revolution of the whole Heaven, exactly investigated here three times from the beginning of this year, in this very month of January, by my Instruments. If the Moon's place, about some of these times, was investigated by you near the 90th degree of the Ecliptic from the Horizon, and the moments of time stand exactly, you have what you desire. To attempt the same by Lunar Parallaxes in another position than near the 90th degree, the scrupulousness here required by no means certainly admits, on account of the Parallaxes in the numbers of the Tables not free of all doubt. But in numbering these times most subtly, do not trust the Clock too much, unless it observe the Revolution most exactly and move everywhere equally in the intermediate hours, and exhibit the single Minutes (with the Seconds too) distinctly. Which [things] I do not doubt the most illustrious Prince has ready, since he has so artful and excellent an <b>Automaton</b> [clock]. From the Altitudes of the Stars too this subtle practice does not succeed conveniently enough, especially if [the stars] nearer the Meridian be elevated; nor near the Horizon are they free of fault, on account of the inconvenience of Refraction. It will therefore be more advisable to investigate these moments of time from the very <b>Azimuths of the Stars</b> — provided the meridian-line have first been diligently explored, not from the Sun, but from the Stars, but especially from the <b>Pole-star</b> itself, when it is at its maximum Azimuthal removal from the Pole on either side toward rising and setting (which reckoning is subject to not the least error, and can conveniently be done). I am wont most easily to attain the exact moments of time, without any suspicion of error, by the <b>Equatorial Armillae</b>, numbering the distances of the Sun and Stars from the Meridian in the Equator itself (which is the measure of times), in whatever position they exist. If it befall us (the Deity favouring) to observe on both sides the truest times of the <b>Lunar Eclipses imminent this and the future year</b>, we shall be rendered more certain about the intervals of the Meridians and the Moon's motions. But it must be noticed that in a total eclipse with tarrying, the time of the <b>ingress of the Moon's whole body into the Earth's shadow</b>, and of its first <b>egress</b>, is better discerned than the other two moments of the first beginning and last end. Wherefore those rather are accurately to be attended to. It is expedient too to use several Observers, disjoined from one another, who are strong in keenness of sight. The <b>Calculation of the Lunar motion</b> I do not now send, because I have resolved to examine it more often and longer, by several Eclipses and by the Moon's places near the Quadratures, near the mean distance of the Epicycle, and elsewhere too, before I communicate my inventions in this part to others. For I wish that what proceeds from me in Astronomical [matters] be either nothing, or far most exact — so that they do not reach to a greater precision [than that] of the human senses. For it is better to leave these untouched than to handle [them] drowsily and with a ruder Minerva. The motions of <b>Venus and Mercury</b> I have explored scrupulously enough, by manifold Observations. In the three higher Planets I require more <b>acronychal</b> observations, especially in places more distant from one another on the Eccentric; which when I shall have attained (God favouring), I hope — the same Deity directing our endeavours — to consecrate to Posterity the <b>whole of Astronomy, completed in all its numbers</b>, far more perfectly than has been done by our predecessors. For which [end] may God deign to bestow vigour of mind and body, that this most excellent and most ancient of all, and plainly <b>divine, science</b>, at last purged from blots and errors, may be restored into a truth of the Appearances congruent with the Heaven itself.</p><p><b>Nicolaus Copernicus</b>, a vast and incomparable <b>Athlete</b> in this work, had he not been destitute of exquisite Instruments subject to no error, would have left us far surer [things]; for in science and talent he was eminent, if anyone [was], and most perfectly skilled in those [things] which were required from Geometry and Arithmetic for constituting this Art. Nor was he inferior to Ptolemy in this part — nay, in certain [things], especially in the elegance of the Hypotheses and in finding a compendious Harmony, he far surpassed him. Nor does the absurdity, apparent at first sight, in the <b>Earth's Revolution</b> stand in the way — since, if a circular motion regular about <b>alien centres</b> be built [in], as the Ptolemaic Hypotheses bear in all the Planets except the Sun, one sins far more absurdly and intolerably against the very principles of the Art than by attributing some motion to the Earth — which, since it will be natural, comes out <b>insensible</b>. Nor do so many inconveniences arise hence as most suppose; and those [things] which were lately published in the <b>Spheric Poem</b> of that most distinguished Poet <b>Buchanan the Scot</b>, my eminent friend while he was alive, have no place here. For he did not notice that, the terrestrial motion being posited, the <b>sea and the circumfluent and nearest Air are revolved together by an equal impulse</b>, and therefore no violence is caused, nor absurdity, [of] as much as he thinks comes about in all those [things] which he adduces to the contrary. But it is not [a matter] for this place to discuss these more copiously. But these we shall some time, God willing, discuss more amply in our Astronomical Work, and perhaps introduce another reckoning of Hypotheses, which will seem absurd neither Mathematically nor Physically, and will not sin against the very principles of the Art. You ask that I communicate to you the exact knowledge of the <b>Elevation of the Pole which is at Frauenburg</b>, for more deeply exploring the Copernican observations had there. Know, therefore, that three years ago I sent, on that account, a certain one of my students devoting [their] labour to Astronomy — <b>Elias Olai</b> [Morsing] by name — to <b>Frauenburg</b> by ship, an exquisite Instrument capable of the minutest divisions being attached; who, having there conveniently obtained a place for Observing among the <b>Canons of Ermland</b> (by whom too he was benevolently received), staying more than a whole Month, obtained very many exquisite Observations consonant with one another. And therefore, both from the Meridian Altitudes of the Sun in its Northern Declination, and from the maximum Elevations of the Fixed [stars] found there, and collated with those which the same Instrument afforded here, we found the <b>Elevation of Frauenburg [at] 54° 22¼′</b>, exceeding the Copernican notation by nearly <b>3 minutes</b>. Which too I always suspected, since I saw the Solar Eccentricity and Apogee, and so the whole course of the Sun, discrepant so enormously from his tenets in so brief an interval of years. For that he investigated the Pole's Altitude and the maximum Declination from the maximum and minimum Altitude of the Sun in each Solstice, and that less congruently — because he did not avoid the involvement of <b>Refraction</b> in the low position, and perhaps had no reckoning of <b>Parallax</b> either — I probably gathered from his writings; and that on that account about three minutes were wanting to the Pole's altitude, I judged consonant even before I had a trial made. But meanwhile, while my student was at leisure for Observations at Frauenburg, he was called thither by the most illustrious Prince <b>Georg Friedrich, Margrave of Ansbach</b> — <b>Master Maenius</b>, Professor of Mathematics at <b>Königsberg in Prussia</b>, being sent to him — to examine the Polar Altitude there with equal diligence; which too he effected for some weeks in the Prince's garden, an opportune place being granted. And from the Altitudes of the Sun and Stars there several times acquired, the <b>Elevation of the Pole at Königsberg in Prussia</b> is found [at] <b>54° 43′</b> nearly. Which I judged should be indicated to you at the same time, since <b>Reinhold</b> ordered the <b>Prutenic Tables</b> to that place, and established its Latitude [at] <b>54° 17′</b>, smaller than just by 26 minutes (wrongly imitating Apian's erroneous Catalogue). Returning to me, this my student brought with him a certain <b>Instrument composed by Copernicus himself</b>, and long had in use, sent me as a gift by Lord <b>Johannes Hannov, Canon of Ermland</b>. And it is that <b>Parallactic</b> [instrument] whose construction he explains in book 4, chapter 15 [of <i>De revolutionibus</i>], which they commonly call the <b>Ptolemaic Rules</b> [<i>regulae</i> = triquetrum], because it was once much in use with Ptolemy too. But it is entirely of <b>wood, and fir-wood</b>, and the two equal Rules contain <b>4 ells</b> in Length, and exhibit a division of <b>1414 particles</b>, instituted in the vulgar manner, such that any smaller difference presupposes the number five. Hence it is easy to gather with what [kind of] machines that excellent Copernicus, [thus] equipped, tried to derive the celestial motions to us — so that on that account the errors which crept in are to be imputed not to the Art, or to his industry, but to the means of the Instruments, which, less exquisite, he used. I too have long had ready two such instruments of Rules, usefully elaborated in <b>brass</b>, and subtly divided into ten times more little portions — of which one, after the manner of Ptolemy, rests on an erected column, and is conveniently revolved on it, by raising and lowering the longer Rule in which the divisions exist. In the other, the same longer Rule is revolved only to the level of the Horizon, and at the same time, in a <b>Circle whose diameter is 20 feet</b>, shows the Azimuths, the other two equal sides effecting the angle of Altitude by their Elevation and depression. And both are larger than this Copernican [one], and most diligently elaborated. Nonetheless, in precisely investigating the Altitudes of the Sun and Stars, I do not apply so much credit to them as to the <b>Quadrants and Sextants</b>, especially the largish [ones]. Nor do I think it possible to reduce the construction of Rules to the most exact precision which is perpetually consistent with itself. Yet I do not easily bring my mind [to believe] that Copernicus erred much in the <b>Altitude of Spica of Virgo</b>, which he proposes in book 3, chapter 2 — whether he obtained it by this Parallactic Instrument or by any other reasoning — and it is most probable that its Declination arising thence is much surer than the <b>Wernerian</b> Observation. For otherwise the motion of the eighth Sphere would be too accelerated, and would anticipate the <b>Albategnian</b> speed more than is fair. By which argument too it is proved that your Star-Longitudes are a little further on than the advance of this Sphere admits. But I see that, while I converse with you too gladly about those [things] which make for the Astronomical matter, I exceed the measure in writing a Letter. And this I rather admit, both because I am persuaded that this prolixity will not be tedious to you, and also that you may have the tardiness hitherto admitted in replying recompensed by a more copious response. Since, therefore, your desires, and those [things] which you signified to me by letter, have been (as I think) abundantly satisfied, it remains that I now draw in the sails, and hold you commended to God the best and greatest.</p><p>Farewell, and by no means forbear to confer with me further about the Astronomical business, at least by Letters — since in person (which would be more convenient) it is not permitted; nay, labour to ascend the stars with me by <b>ladders least fallacious</b>, continuing the work begun at every opportunity of serene weather. Again, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 20th day of January, in the Year [15]87. Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VI (<i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his own 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i>, Liber I; no MS witness recorded. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VI pp. 86–104</b> (PDF pp. 100–118); the long star-latitude numeral series and the whole numeral spine are confirmed against Dreyer (anchors + the one derived-obliquity flag in the Editorial note).</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg) → <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, Court Mathematician to <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel</b>. This is the great natural-philosophical and observational letter of the Tycho–Rothmann exchange, answering Rothmann's letters of April [1586] (the substance to the Landgrave is a parallel letter).</li><li><b>The 1585 comet &amp; parallax.</b> Near-exact agreement of the Kassel and Hven observations; the comet \"in the highest aether.\" The only difference: Rothmann finds <b>no parallax</b>, Tycho ~<b>1′</b> then <b>½′</b> (since the comet, by proper motion, sat below the 8th sphere though above the Sun). Tycho notes that <b>Mars (esp. at opposition)</b> and even <b>Jupiter</b> give observable parallax. The 5–6′ longitude/latitude differences trace to different fixed-star places (Aldebaran 4°7′ vs 4°1′ Gemini; the \"third star of Aries-head\"). Meridian difference Kassel–Hven ≈ ⅕ hour. Max obliquity: Tycho <b>23° 31½′</b> (from the Landgrave's solstitial altitude 62°12′ + parallax), Rothmann 23°30′; the winter-refraction trap (Sun only ~11° high) that pulls it to a false 23°27′.</li><li><b>Fluid heavens.</b> Tycho agrees the heavens are \"air\" <i>if</i> the supralunar \"air\" = a far subtler <b>aether</b>; no real solid orbs (the comets refute them); each planet has a <b>divinely-implanted \"science of motion\"</b> in the fluid aether, like the Earth resting on its own centre. He foreshadows his own world-system (\"more competent than the Ptolemaic or Copernican\").</li><li><b>Ramus on hypothesis-free astronomy.</b> Tycho recounts <b>Petrus Ramus</b> (Augsburg, 16 years earlier ≈ 1570) urging astronomy \"without hypotheses\" (the Egyptian-ease argument); Tycho's rebuttal: without hypotheses there is no science; \"all things consist of <b>number, weight, and measure</b>\"; hypotheses are the <i>measure of apparent motion by circles</i>, which arithmetic resolves into numbers.</li><li><b>The Wittich instrument-priority story (a key document).</b> <b>Paul Wittich</b> of Breslau visited Hven (1580, ~¼ year), promised to be Tycho's \"faithful Achates,\" then left for an \"uncle's inheritance\" at Breslau and never returned; Tycho learned (from the Landgrave's letter to <b>Rantzau</b>) that Wittich had <b>reproduced Tycho's instruments at Kassel</b> — selling them \"as his own without confessing through whom.\" Specifics: the <b>transverse-point division</b> (Tycho had it from <b>Homelius</b> at Leipzig, age 17, ~24 yr earlier, first on the cross-staff; then <b>Nonius/Nunes</b> <i>De crepusculis</i> prop. 3; then his own application to <b>curves</b>); the <b>parallel-slit sights</b> (<i>rimulae</i>) — Wittich's \"compendium\" of <b>2 slits instead of 4</b> lost precision; the <b>Sextant</b> (devised at Augsburg ~18 yr earlier, shown to Ramus with the <b>14-cubit quadrant in Hainzel's garden</b>; the steel sextant with a <b>screw</b> not a chain; the later two-observer <b>Trigonic</b> sextants).</li><li><b>Refraction (detailed).</b> Measured by a <b>10-foot armilla</b>: solar declination-refraction at 30° altitude &gt; ½′, at the horizon <b>28′</b>. Star-refraction discrepancies with Rothmann: Algol/Perseus's right side (Tycho 2½′ at 14°16′; Perseus's right shoulder 1⅚′ at 17°45½′), and the big one — Rothmann's <b>Cygnus-tail 5⅔′</b> (alt 5°18′) vs Tycho's <b>Algol-head 9¼′</b> (alt 5°21½′), nearly double. Against Rothmann (who would deny all refraction above 20°, to make heaven = air), Tycho argues from the persistent small refraction that the <b>aether's diaphaneity is purer than the air's</b> (but not so different as air-to-water), denies the fire-element (else \"triple transparencies\" would refract more), and rejects <b>Alhazen/Witelo's</b> solid-transparent heaven.</li><li><b>The fixed-star-latitude-variation demonstration (the technical core — cf. the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599).</b> Rothmann had noticed stars' latitudes vary with the <b>changing obliquity</b>; Tycho confirms it by an exhaustive reconstruction. Method: take <b>Spica</b> as base (its Timochares/Hipparchus/Ptolemy declinations → longitudes 21°53′ / 23°53′ / 26°38′ Virgo, fixing the precession steps 2° and 2¾°); for each star, reconstruct the ancient longitude (same Spica-interval as now) and, with the ancient <b>obliquity 23°51⅓′</b> and the recorded declination (from <b>Almagest VII.3</b>, using a 70-year-old <b>Venice</b> edition that gives declinations in <i>letters</i> not the <b>Trapezuntine</b> cyphers), solve the spherical triangle for the ancient latitude, then compare to now. Stars worked through: <b>Castor</b> (N Gemini-head, 9°43′→10°2′, +19′), <b>Pollux</b> (S head, 6°21′→6°38′), <b>Capella</b> (22⅚°→22°50½′), <b>Orion's right</b> (16⅓→16 1/10) and <b>left shoulder</b> (17°5′→16°53′), <b>Regulus</b> (0°10′→0°26′ N, +16′), <b>Aldebaran</b> (a crux — Ptolemy's latitude erroneous; checked against the Taurus-eye distance 3°10¼′), <b>Altair</b> (<i>Vultur</i>, 29°39′→29°21′, −18′), <b>Antares</b> (4°14′→4°24′), <b>Libra's S</b> and <b>N pans</b>, and the two <b>cruces</b>: <b>Sirius</b> (latitude now <i>larger</i> not smaller — Tycho ascribes it to <b>transcription error</b> in the ancient declination, since Sirius cannot escape the common law) and <b>Procyon</b> (supports). Conclusion: the latitude-variation law holds; the obliquity itself is essentially what it was (cf. the constancy argued in the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599).</li><li><b>The 1584 lunar eclipse &amp; meridian method.</b> The eclipse of <b>7 Nov 1584</b>: begin 11ʰ12ᵐ, mid 13ʰ8ᵐ, end 15ʰ0ᵐ (Uraniborg, by stars' meridian-distances). <b>Wittich's Kassel</b> mid (13ʰ3ᵐ) is wrong (Kassel ⅕ hr west → should be ~12ʰ56ᵐ); <b>Brucaeus</b> at Rostock 1ʰ4ᵐ after midnight (his small brass quadrant, degrees only, less exact). The best meridian-difference method: the Moon <b>near the 90th ecliptic degree</b> (no longitude-parallax); times by <b>equatorial armillae</b> / <b>azimuths off the Pole-star</b>, not a clock alone. The <b>lunar-motion calculation withheld</b> until perfected (\"either nothing, or far most exact\").</li><li><b>Copernicus, Buchanan, Frauenburg.</b> Praise of <b>Copernicus</b> (\"a vast Athlete,\" hampered only by crude instruments); the Earth's motion less absurd than the alien-centre violation (the sea and air move with the Earth — so <b>George Buchanan's</b> anti-motion <i>Sphaera</i> \"has no place\"); Tycho promises \"another reckoning of hypotheses\" (his system). <b>Elias Olai Morsing</b> sent to <b>Frauenburg</b> (3 yr earlier) → pole <b>54° 22¼′</b> (Copernicus ~3′ low, from neglected refraction/parallax); then to <b>Königsberg</b> (via <b>Margrave Georg Friedrich of Ansbach</b> and <b>Master Maenius</b>) → <b>54° 43′</b> (<b>Reinhold</b>'s Prutenic 54°17′ too low, after <b>Apian</b>). Morsing brought back <b>Copernicus's own triquetrum</b> (gift of canon <b>Johannes Hannov</b>): fir-wood, 4-ell rules, <b>1414</b> crude divisions — proof that Copernicus's errors were the instruments', not the man's. Tycho's two brass triquetra (one column-mounted, one with a 20-foot azimuth circle) are better, but he trusts quadrants/sextants more. Copernicus's <b>Spica altitude</b> (<i>De rev.</i> III.2) probably reliable (better than <b>Werner</b>'s), else the 8th-sphere motion would outrun the <b>al-Battānī</b> rate — proving Kassel's star-longitudes \"a little too far on.\"</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann, Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b>; <b>Paul Wittich</b> (Breslau); <b>Petrus Ramus</b>; <b>Johannes Homelius</b> (Hommel, Leipzig); <b>Pedro Nunes</b> (<i>Nonius</i>, <i>De crepusculis</i>); <b>Paul Hainzel</b> (Augsburg consul); <b>Alhazen, Witelo</b>; <b>Timochares, Hipparchus, Ptolemy</b>; <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Rostock); <b>George Buchanan</b> (the <i>Sphaera</i>); <b>Copernicus, Reinhold, Apian, Werner, al-Battānī</b>; <b>Elias Olai Morsing</b>; <b>Georg Friedrich of Ansbach</b>, <b>Master Maenius</b> (Königsberg); <b>Johannes Hannov</b> (Ermland canon); <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>. Places: <b>Uraniborg, Kassel, Augsburg, Leipzig, Rostock, Frauenburg (Frombork), Königsberg (Regiomontium Prussiae), Breslau (Vratislavia), Venice</b>.</li><li><b>Glyphs / tags.</b> ♈ Aries, ♉ Taurus, ♊ Gemini, ♍ Virgo, ♌ Leo, ♎ Libra, ♏ Scorpio, ♐ Sagittarius, ♑ Capricorn, ♒ Aquarius; ☉ Sun, ☽ Moon, ♂ Mars, ♃ Jupiter, ♄ Saturn. <i>fidus Achates</i> (Virgil) = faithful companion; <i>rudior Minerva</i> = \"with a ruder Minerva\" (crudely); <i>Dodecatemorion</i> = a zodiacal twelfth (sign); <i>decuria</i> = a set of ten degrees; <i>scalis minime fallacibus</i> = \"by least-fallacious ladders\" (the closing flourish). <i>Athleta</i> of Copernicus; <i>Cochlea</i> = screw.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <b>20 January 1587 O.S.</b> (Danish Julian); matches the catalogue label. The lunar eclipse \"7 November 1584\" and the Wittich visit \"1580\" are O.S.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> ~70 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590 and 13 Sept 1588, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, and from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588); v/u orthography of the 1596 print retained. OCR slips left/read-through: <i>doscohaerentia</i> (read <i>discohaerentia</i>), <i>nonnullibi</i> (read <i>nonnullubi</i>/\"here and there\"), <i>verisimilimum</i> (read <i>verisimillimum</i>); the star \"lucidior in dextro latere Persei\" with altitude 9°42′/14°16′ is the bright Perseus star (Algol/β or Mirfak/α region) — left as the text names it. Numerals reproduced exactly. The \"right side of Perseus\" / \"head of Algol\" pairing is Tycho's own (Algol = β Persei).</li><li><b>One internal numeral note.</b> \"Capella … nunc … part. 22. M. 50½.\" is printed <i>grad. 22. M. 50½</i> (the stray extra \"°\" in the rendering above is editorial); reproduced as the source gives. The Frauenburg result 54°22¼′ and Königsberg 54°43′ are Tycho's own reductions.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A very long, technically dense scientific letter — the 1585-comet comparison, the Wittich instrument-priority narrative, the Ramus exchange, the refraction analysis, the exhaustive star-by-star latitude-variation demonstration, the 1584-eclipse/meridian method, and the Copernicus/Frauenburg/triquetrum material — rendered securely throughout; the register (collegial, expansive, technically exacting, with a controlled edge on Wittich and Brucaeus) is preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, refraction, declination, latitude/longitude, obliquity, eccentric/equant, acronychal, <i>salvare</i>/save, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i>, the instrument names, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599, Rothmann's letter of 13 Oct 1588, and the Peucer letter of 13 Sept 1588, and with the wider corpus. The dense numeral spine (the Spica-based longitudes, the per-star ancient-vs-present latitudes, the obliquity 23°31½′/23°51⅓′, the refraction values, the Frauenburg/Königsberg poles) is internally consistent and is a printed-edition text. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VI pp. 86–104) and confirms the entire numeral spine</b> — ~70 values incl. the full star-latitude series, all matching except that the .md's <i>derived</i> obliquity 23°31⅓′ is arithmetically right where Dreyer's print is the outlier (31½) — so the collation reservation is <b>lifted</b>. One residue remains: the handful of OCR slips and the <b>Perseus/Algol star-identification</b> ambiguity in the refraction passage, declared and read as the text names them. It does not obscure the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo was null, so no Dreyer collation was possible (capped at grade B per brief). Audited the full published_english against source_text paragraph by paragraph (11 paragraphs plus salutation, farewell, and dateline). Cross-checked every numeric value (degrees/minutes/seconds of latitude, longitude, declination, refraction, altitude, and time), every proper name (Rothmann, Wittich, Homelius, Ramus, Buchanan, Hainzel, Rantzau, Brucaeus, Elias Olai/Morsing, Georg Friedrich of Ansbach, Maenius, Reinhold, Johannes Hannov, Timochares, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Copernicus), every place-name (Uraniborg, Augsburg, Leipzig, Breslau, Kassel, Rostock, Frauenburg, Königsberg, Venice), the sole date (20 January 1587, matching metadata) and the eclipse timestamps (11h12m/13h8m/15h0m; Wittich's 13h3m at Kassel; Brucaeus's 1h4m post midnight). Specifically checked the passage where Wittich boasts of his 'invention' for a pronoun flip (none found - correctly attributed to Wittich, not Tycho) and the closing sentence/dateline for fabrication (none found - matches source exactly, including the 'ladders least fallacious' image). No sentence found silently dropped across the full letter. Bracketed editorial supplements (star identifications, an interpretive time-note) are clearly marked and were not counted as defects per the brief.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15860907002", "next": "brahe-t_15870812001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860907002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15871011001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Rothmann's April letter with its recondite astronomical discussions, explains the delay in replying, and reports having communicated celestial observations to the Landgrave.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870120001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870125001", "d": "1587-01-25", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 25 January 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.63 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeEMINENTI VIRO D. DOCTORI THADDAeO HAGECIO AB HAYK, MATHEMATICO ET MEDICO INPRIMIS EXCELLENTI, AMICO SUO PERPETUO COLENDISSIMO.<br>ZU PRAG.<br>S.</p><p>REMITTO tibi nunc tandem, eruditiss. amantissimeque D. THADAeE, doctas et ingeniosas illas tuas de Nova Stella et Cometis aliquot lucubrationes, quas Francofurtum ad hasce proxime instantes vernales Nundinas (ut illinc per eundem Bibliopolam Gallum, de quo mihi significasti, ad te procurentur, vel, si is officium hac in re detrectârit, ut per hunc ipsum, cui eas tradidi, negotium hoc apud aliquem certum hominem expediatur) perferendas curavi. Quod vero eas nunc tua expectatione proprioque meo proposito tardius hunc tuum foetum recipias, nuntiorum penuria in causa fuit; rarissime enim aliquis hinc ad vos proficiscitur, et, cum nulla commodior daretur occasio, volebam praeteritis autumnalibus nundinis Francofordensibus idem praestitisse, et ob id cum quodam nostratium Bibliopolarum illuc ad dictas nundinas quotannis proficiscentium egeram, ut is hîc ante suum discessum me conveniret secumque librum tuum unâ cum meis literis Francofurto ad te mittendum asportaret; nam et ipsi etiam literas ad Illustriss. Principem Landgravium Hassiae ipsiusque Cels. Mathematicum ROTHMANNUM, ubi Cassellas pertransiret, expediendas unâ committere volebam; ille vero, mihi suam operam hac in re non gravatim promittens, omnino me ante discessum suum accedere constituerat. Verum accidit ijs ipsis diebus, quibus illi huc veniendum erat, ut Illustribus hospitibus, utpote Regina ipsa denuo me invisente et secum utrumque parentem adducente unâ cum duce SIGISMUNDO, patrui filio, magnoque nobilium virorum comitatu, excipiendis occuparer, quod ille ubi cognoverat, et iam iam dies illi instarent, quibus sine mora abeundum erat, me non accedens itineri se unâ cum socijs commisit. Hinc itaque eveniebat, ut, hac commoditate librum tuum remittendi mihi tunc praerepta, usque in hoc tempus illum mecum retinere coactus fuerim, quemadmodum etiam Illustrissimo Principi Landtgravio hanc meam iustam excusationem, quod etiam ipsius Celsitudinis votis in rescribendo tardius satisfaciam, proxime elapsis diebus per literas indicavi. Perlegi autem hunc tuum librum non sine voluptate et diligentia, tuumque in hac materia indefessum studium pari labore et industria coniunctum commendatione dignissimum censeo. Essent quidem nonnulla, quae paulo enucleatius forent constituenda, si observationes exactae unâ cum otio tibi suppeterent, sed sunt ea utplurimum eiuscemodi, quae non facile cuivis obvia occurrant, nec usque adeo magni momenti exsistunt. Quod nullae ex omnibus illis eruditis et ingeniosis alias satis, quibus Parallaxes indagare doces, rationibus motui proprio Cometarum (quo rarissime carent) satisfaciant, existimo, me in ultimis ad te datis literis admonuisse; quare alia erit Parallaxium a motu Cometarum proprio separatarum instituenda ratiocinatio, si non solum speculative, sed etiam in ipsa praxi veritatis scopum collimatius attingere intendamus; quod tibiipsi ulterius expendendum considerandumque relinquo. Stellarum Cassiopeae tum a se invicem, tum etiam a Nova illa Stella remotiones paulo aliter se habent, quam tui etiam correctiores et reiterati numeri exhibent; sic etiam distantia polaris stellae ab 11a Cassiopeae, pro qua tantum laborasti, 9 scrupulis in tua annotatione abundat. Nequaquam omnino per Radium Astronomicum exquisitae et ipsi coelo congruae stellarum distantiae ulla adhibita diligentia capi possunt, quacunque tandem longitudine et divisionum subtilitate Radius ille praeditus sit, ut alio loco certis rationibus confirmabo, ubi, favente Numine, opus nostrum de prima et Mechanica Astronomiae parte absolutum fuerit et publicae utilitati communicatum. Ut enim ipsissima stellarum intercapedo obtineatur, necesse est utramque stellam bifariam per rimulas, quae lineas visuales a superiori pinnacidio ubique paralelas efficiunt, centraliter intueri. Quod nequaquam Radio praestari potest, ideoque nova quaedam organa, quae huic incertitudini praeveniant et stellarum exactas infallibilesque remotiones exhibeant, iam dudum excogitavi, quorum fabricam et usum in praenominato opere aliquando (Deo conatus nostros secundante) perspicies. Declinatio Novi Sideris a te constituta 4 ad minus scrupulis iusto maior est, et ratio inquirendi eius longitudinem e tempore transitus per Meridianum non caret evidentibus obstaculis; difficillime enim exactissima illa temporis momenta cognita veniunt, et licet hoc magna adhibita industria possibile concedamus, nihilominus Solis locus vel ex ipso COPERNIANO calculo depromtus huic negotio non sufficit, siquidem Solari in coelo cursui nequaquam congruus existit, ut ex ea, quae in Diario isto, quod a nobilibus Gallis accepisti, reperitur, restitutione colligere potes. Oportuit etiam affixarum stellarum loca, quorum usus in Stella illa Nova et Cometis requirebatur, prius per certas observationes emendata habere, ut ea, quae superstruerentur, rectius constarent; quod dum non solum a te omnibusque fere alijs hactenus neglectum est, sed etiam a me in tractatulo nostro de Stella illa Nova dudum edito praetermissum, factum est, ut scopum ipsum in certis et exactis longitudinibus latitudinibusque et ijs, quae hinc derivari possunt, cognoscendis non satis praecise in Stella illa Nova attigerimus. Horum verum a me emendationem in opere, quod nunc de tota hac materia impressioni adorno, brevi, Deo volente, invenies. Si itaque tua omnino edere decrevisti (in quo proposito ut perseveres, potius autor quam dissuasor esse velim), percuperem, ne quid ex meo illo libello adiungeres, cum omnia correctiora et elimatiora sim propediem in lucem emissurus. In Cometa anni 1577 videris non eandem quam in Nova Stella adhibuisse diligentiam, licet teipsum postea in alio quodam scripto oportune correxeris. Nam plane exigua et fere insensibilis illi Cometae aderat Parallaxis, et rectissime omnium de eo scripsit MAeSTLINUS, ut ob id immerito illum in quibusdam taxes. Ratio illa observandi per filum in directum ad stellas extensum non quidem exactae certitudini satisfacit, nihilominus si exquisita et nulli fallaciae obnoxia organa in promtu non sunt, dubijs et incertis imo ipsi Radio quamoptime elaborato hanc filarem observandi rationem anteferrem. Sed de his suo loco et tempore copiosius. TRAPEZUNTIJ in PTOLOMAeUM introductionem an quispiam sit Typographorum Francofurtum venientium, qui eam typis edere velit, expiscari curo. Interea librum illum mecum conservo, quod bona tua venia fieri non diffido. Et quia constitui (Deo favente) proxima instante aestate in Germaniam excurrere (modo veniam a Sereniss. Rege nostro, uti spero, impetravero), cumque tunc quamprimum Cassellis (ubi Principem Landtgravium ob commune hoc studium convenire decrevi) discessero, Francofurtum me conferre in votis habeo idque circa tempus autumnalium Nundinarum. Ideo, si TRAPEZUNTINA illa editio prius non successerit, inter alia librum illum mecum accipiam eiusque publicationem omni conatu tunc procurabo, quod eam etiam ob causam tibi nunc indicare volui, ut, si forte res tuae ita ferrent, quod in illa mea excursione alicubi in vobis vicina Germania vel Lipsiae aut Dresdae simul convenire possimus, interea tecum de hoc deliberare possis; nihil enim mihi gratius eveniret, quam si facultas daretur, ut quemadmodum Ratisbonae olim nunc iterum alicubi vel pauculis diebus nos invicem gratis sermonibus coram complecti possemus. Tuum itaque erit mature aliquid certi hac in re statuere et mihi tuam voluntatem prima quaque occasione significare. Pragam in illo itinere accedere vix mihi commodum erit; etiamsi enim omnia votis meis successerint, non tamen ante initia Iulij hinc discedere statui, et ante hyemem in patriam mihi necessario ad mea revertendum erit. Tu itaque da operam, ut medium aliquod inveniatur, quo votis nostris utrinque (nam et te idipsum exoptare non dubito) satisfiat, mecumque tuam mentem in his quam primum (ut prius etiam rogavi) communica. Existimo, me tibi ultimo misisse exemplar quoddam Literarum Illustriss. Principis WILHELMI, Landtgravij Hassiae, quibus meis respondebat, unâ etiam cum Mathematici ipsius Celsitudinis CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI literis. Et quia semel atque iterum ab eodem Principe de responsione sollicitatus sum, eo quod easdem ob causas, quibus tibi etiam hactenus scribere non licuit, hanc multo tempore differre coactus fuerim, proxime idcirco elapsis diebus, nactus iam tandem commodam occasionem, ipsius Celsitudini rescripsi; quare, ut meum etiam hoc responsum cum prioribus Principis huius literis conferre possis, adiunxi hîc illius exemplar, praesertim cum eam inprimis materiam contineat, qua te plurimum affici compertum habeo. Addidissem quoque exemplar meae ad ROTHMANNI literas responsionis, verum, cum is, per quem nunc hasce mitto, abitum festinet, eo quod Cimbriam, patriam suam, ante Nundinarum illarum Francofordensium accessum invisere constituerit, non relinquebatur nobis tantum temporis, ut descriptionem eius fieri curare concederetur, ipsa etiam prolixitate, qua Landtgravianas literas fere in duplo excedunt, pro hac tanta otij penuria idipsum non admittente; sed haec et alia suo tempore commodius fortassis obtinebis. Cumque nunc charta deficiens scribendi finem facere admoneat, pluribus supersedeo, teque, charissime THADAeE, cum tota familia Deo omnium rerum autori commendo. Is te quam diutissime conservet animo corporeque incolumem. Vale et prima quaque oblata occasione rescribe. Iterum quam felicissime vale. Datae Uraniburgi die 25. Ianuarij Anno 1587.</p><p>Tuus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>Boni consule has raptim exaratas et incertas literas, quas recognoscere, nedum rescribere nuncij festinatio non admittebat.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished man, pre-eminent in virtue and learning, Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayk, mathematician and physician especially excellent, his perpetual and most honoured friend.</b><br><b>At Prague.</b><br><b>Greeting.</b></p><p>I now at last send back to you, most learned and most beloved Master Thaddaeus, those learned and ingenious lucubrations of yours on the New Star and on several comets, which I have arranged to have conveyed to Frankfurt for this spring fair now next at hand (so that from there they may be procured for you by the same French bookseller of whom you informed me; or, should he decline the office in this matter, so that this business may be dispatched through this very man to whom I have handed them, by way of some trustworthy person). That you now receive back this offspring of yours later than your own expectation and my own intention, the scarcity of messengers was the cause; for very rarely does anyone set out from here to your parts, and, since no more convenient occasion offered, I had wished to have done the same at the past autumn fair at Frankfurt, and on that account I had dealt with a certain one of our [Danish] booksellers who travel there to the said fair every year, that he should call on me here before his departure and carry off with him your book together with my letters, to be sent from Frankfurt to you; for I wished at the same time to entrust to him also letters to be dispatched to the Most Illustrious Prince, the Landgrave of Hesse, and to his Highness's mathematician Rothmann, when he should pass through Kassel; and he indeed, promising me his service in this matter not grudgingly, had quite resolved to come to me before his departure. But it happened in those very days on which he was to come here that I was occupied in receiving illustrious guests — namely, the Queen herself visiting me again and bringing both her parents with her, along with Duke Sigismund, her uncle's son, and a great retinue of noblemen — and when he learned this, and the days were now pressing upon him on which he had to leave without delay, he, not calling on me, committed himself to the journey along with his companions. Hence, then, it came about that, this convenience of sending your book back having then been snatched from me, I was forced to keep it with me right up to this present time — just as also I made known this just excuse of mine to the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave, in letters a few days ago now, [namely] that I am satisfying his Highness's wishes too somewhat tardily in replying. I have, however, read this book of yours through, not without pleasure and diligence, and I judge your tireless zeal in this subject, joined with equal labour and industry, most worthy of commendation. There would indeed be some points which would need to be established a little more clearly, if exact observations together with leisure were available to you; but these are for the most part of such a kind as do not easily come to anyone's notice, nor are they of such great moment. That none of all those learned and ingenious methods otherwise sufficient, by which you teach how to track down parallaxes, satisfy the proper motion of comets (which they very rarely lack), I think I pointed out in my last letter sent to you; wherefore another reckoning of parallaxes separated from the proper motion of comets will have to be instituted, if we mean to attain the mark of truth more pointedly not only speculatively but also in the very practice [of it] — which I leave to you yourself for further weighing and consideration. The distances of the stars of Cassiopeia, both from one another and also from that New Star, stand somewhat otherwise than even your corrected and repeated figures present them; so too the distance of the pole star from the 11th [star] of Cassiopeia, over which you laboured so much, is in your annotation in excess by 9 minutes [scruples]. By no means at all can the exact distances of stars, congruent with the heaven itself, be taken by the cross-staff (radius astronomicus) with any diligence applied, with whatever length and fineness of divisions that staff be at last endowed — as I shall confirm in another place with sure reasons, when, by the favour of the Deity, our work on the first and Mechanical part of astronomy shall have been completed and communicated to the public good. For in order that the very interval of the stars be obtained, it is necessary to look at each star centrally, in two parts, through little slits which everywhere make the lines of sight parallel from the upper sight (pinnule); and this can by no means be furnished by the cross-staff. And therefore I devised long ago certain new instruments, which forestall this uncertainty and exhibit the exact and infallible distances of the stars — whose construction and use you will at some time perceive in the aforesaid work (God seconding our efforts). The declination of the New Star, as established by you, is greater than is right by at least 4 minutes [scruples], and the method of inquiring after its longitude from the time of its transit across the meridian is not free of evident obstacles; for those most exact moments of time become known only with the greatest difficulty, and although we grant this possible with great industry applied, nonetheless the Sun's place, even when drawn from the Copernican calculation itself, does not suffice for this business, since it is by no means congruent with the Sun's course in the heaven — as you can gather from that restoration which is found in that Diary which you received from the noble Frenchmen. It was also necessary to have the places of the fixed stars — whose use was required in that New Star and in the comets — first emended through sure observations, so that the things to be built upon them might stand more correctly; and while this has hitherto been neglected not only by you and by almost all others, but was also passed over by me in our little treatise on that New Star published long ago, it came about that we did not attain the mark itself precisely enough, in the case of that New Star, in coming to know the sure and exact longitudes and latitudes and the things that can be derived from them. But you will shortly find, God willing, the emendation of these things by me, in the work which I am now dressing for the press concerning this whole subject. If therefore you have quite resolved to publish your [writings] (and in that purpose I would rather be your encourager than your dissuader, that you persevere in it), I would greatly wish that you add nothing from that little book of mine, since I am about very soon to send into the light everything in more corrected and more polished form. In the comet of the year 1577 you seem not to have applied the same diligence as in the New Star, although you afterwards opportunely corrected yourself in some other writing. For that comet had a plainly minute and almost imperceptible parallax, and of all men Maestlin wrote about it most correctly, so that on that account you undeservedly censure him in certain points. That method of observing by a thread stretched straight out toward the stars does not indeed satisfy exact certainty; nonetheless, if exquisite instruments not liable to any deception are not at hand, I would prefer this thread-method of observing to dubious and uncertain ones — indeed even to the cross-staff however excellently wrought. But of these things more copiously in their place and time. Whether there be any of the printers coming to Frankfurt who may be willing to publish it in type, I am taking care to fish out — [I mean] Trebizond's introduction to Ptolemy. Meanwhile I keep that book with me, which I do not doubt may be done by your good leave. And since I have resolved (God favouring) to make an excursion into Germany this coming summer (provided I obtain leave, as I hope, from our Most Serene King), and since then, as soon as I shall have departed from Kassel (where I have determined to meet the Prince Landgrave on account of this common study), it is among my wishes to betake myself to Frankfurt, and that around the time of the autumn fair: therefore, if that Trebizond edition shall not have succeeded beforehand, I shall among other things take that book with me and then procure its publication with every effort — which I wished to indicate to you now for this reason too, that, if perhaps your affairs should so bear it, [namely] that in that excursion of mine we might be able to meet together somewhere in the Germany neighbouring you, or at Leipzig or Dresden, you might in the meantime be able to deliberate with me about this; for nothing more welcome could befall me than if the opportunity were given that, just as once at Regensburg, so now again we might somewhere, even for a very few days, embrace one another in person with pleasing discourse. It will therefore be for you to settle something certain in this matter in good time, and to signify your will to me at the very first occasion. To approach Prague on that journey will scarcely be convenient for me; for even though everything succeed according to my wishes, I have nonetheless resolved not to depart from here before the beginning of July, and before winter I shall necessarily have to return home to my affairs. Do you therefore see to it that some middle way be found by which our wishes on both sides may be satisfied (for I do not doubt that you yourself long for the very same thing), and communicate your mind to me on these matters as soon as possible (as I asked before too). I think I sent you last time a certain copy of the letter of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, by which he was replying to mine, together also with the letter of his Highness's mathematician Christoph Rothmann. And because I have been solicited once and again by the same Prince concerning a reply — since, for the same reasons by which I have been hitherto unable to write to you also, I was compelled to defer this for a long time — therefore a few days ago now, having at last got a convenient occasion, I wrote back to his Highness; wherefore, so that you may be able to compare this reply of mine too with the earlier letters of this Prince, I have here appended a copy of it, especially since it contains in particular that subject by which I have found that you are very much affected. I would have added too a copy of my reply to Rothmann's letter; but, since the man through whom I now send these things hastens his departure — because he has resolved to visit Cimbria, his homeland, before the approach of that Frankfurt fair — there was not left to us so much time that the making of a transcription of it could be allowed, the very length too (by which they exceed the Landgrave's letters by nearly double) not admitting of this, given so great a scarcity of leisure; but these and other things you will perhaps obtain more conveniently in their time. And since now the failing paper warns [me] to make an end of writing, I forbear from more, and commend you, dearest Thaddaeus, with your whole household, to God the author of all things. May He keep you as long as possible unharmed in mind and body. Farewell, and write back at the very first occasion offered. Again, most happily, farewell. Given at Uraniborg, the 25th day of January, the year 1587.</p><p>Yours,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p><p>Take in good part this letter, hastily scrawled and uncertain, which the messenger's haste did not permit me to revise, let alone to rewrite.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. The addressee, <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayk</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), the Bohemian physician, astronomer, and personal physician at the imperial court in <b>Prague</b>; like Tycho he had observed and written on the 1572 New Star and on comets, and the two had met at the Regensburg imperial diet of 1575 (the <i>Ratisbonae olim</i> of the letter). The superscription's <i>ZU PRAG</i> (\"at Prague\") is a German code-switch within an otherwise Latin address; preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting\"), protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Illustriss. Princeps Wilhelmus, Landgravius Hassiae</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6); his court <i>Mathematicus Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (§5.6). <i>Maestlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher), whose treatment of the 1577 comet Tycho here defends against Hagecius's criticism. <i>Trapezuntius</i> = <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Giorgio da Trebisonda, 1396–1486), whose Latin commentary/\"introduction\" to Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> Hagecius wished to see printed at Frankfurt. <i>Dux Sigismundus, patrui filius</i> = a <b>Duke Sigismund</b>, named as the cousin (\"son of [the Queen's] paternal uncle\") in the royal visiting party; <i>Regina ipsa … utrumque parentem adducente</i> refers to <b>Queen Sophie of Denmark</b> (of Mecklenburg) bringing both her parents (Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg and Duchess Elisabeth) — the Mecklenburg ducal house, to which Sigismund belonged.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Francofurtum / Francofordenses Nundinae</i> = <b>Frankfurt</b> and its <b>book fair</b> (<i>Nundinae</i>, \"the fair,\" held spring and autumn). <i>Cassellae</i> = <b>Kassel</b> (§5.7). <i>Lipsia</i> = <b>Leipzig</b>; <i>Dresda</i> = <b>Dresden</b>; <i>Ratisbona</i> = <b>Regensburg</b>. <i>Cimbria</i> = <b>Cimbria</b>, the Jutland/Holstein region of the messenger's homeland (rendered as the Latin name).</li><li><b>Technical terms (per glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>Parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>motus proprius (Cometarum)</i> → <b>proper motion (of comets)</b>; <i>Declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>longitudo / latitudo</i> → <b>longitude / latitude</b>; <i>transitus per Meridianum</i> → <b>transit across the meridian</b>; <i>affixae stellae</i> → <b>fixed stars</b>; <i>organa / nova organa</i> → <b>instruments / new instruments</b>; <i>Radius Astronomicus</i> → <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b>; <i>pinnacidium</i> → <b>sight (pinnule)</b>. <i>Stella Nova</i> / <i>Novum Sidus</i> → <b>the New Star</b> (the supernova of 1572 in Cassiopeia). <i>Cometa anni 1577</i> → <b>the comet of 1577</b>.</li><li><b><i>scrupulis</b></i> — <i>scrupulum</i> here = <b>minute of arc</b>; \"9 scruples / 4 scruples\" rendered \"9 minutes / at least 4 minutes [scruples],\" keeping the period term in brackets. The discrepancies Tycho cites (the pole-star/11th-Cassiopeiae distance \"in excess by 9 minutes\"; the New Star's declination \"greater than is right by at least 4 minutes\") are his own corrections to Hagecius's figures and are reported, not adjudicated.</li><li><b><i>opus nostrum de prima et Mechanica Astronomiae parte</b></i> — Tycho's projected great work on the \"first and Mechanical part of astronomy\"; <i>Mechanica</i> here is the planned instrument-and-method volume (cf. the later <i>Astronomiae instauratae Mechanica</i>, 1598). <i>opere, quod nunc … impressioni adorno</i> = the work \"now dressing for the press\" on the New Star — the <i>Astronomiae instauratae Progymnasmata</i>, then in preparation. <i>tractatulus noster de Stella illa Nova dudum editus</i> = Tycho's \"little treatise on that New Star published long ago,\" i.e. <i>De nova stella</i> (1573). Per protocol §5.1, <i>restitutio</i> (of the Sun's place) → <b>restoration</b>.</li><li><b><i>Diario isto, quod a nobilibus Gallis accepisti</b></i> — \"that Diary which you received from the noble Frenchmen\": an ephemeris/diary containing a solar restoration; the \"noble Frenchmen\" are not further identified in the letter and are left unidentified here (no silent expansion).</li><li><b><i>ratio observandi per filum</b></i> — Hagecius's \"thread-method\" of observation (sighting along a thread stretched toward the stars); kept as described. Tycho's qualified judgment — that he would prefer it to a poorly made cross-staff <i>if</i> better instruments are unavailable — is preserved with its conditional intact (protocol: no sharpening of hedges).</li><li><b>Epistemic hedges preserved.</b> Tycho's recurrent qualifiers are kept verbatim in force: <i>Deo favente / Deo volente / favente Numine / Deo conatus nostros secundante</i> (\"God favouring / God willing / by the favour of the Deity / God seconding our efforts\"); <i>uti spero</i> (\"as I hope\"); <i>fortassis</i> (\"perhaps\"); <i>vix mihi commodum erit</i> (\"will scarcely be convenient\"). None is flattened to plain assertion.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>Vale … Iterum quam felicissime vale</i> → <b>\"Farewell … Again, most happily, farewell\"</b> (§5.5). <i>Datae Uraniburgi</i> → <b>\"Given at Uraniborg.\"</b> The postscript <i>Boni consule …</i> is a self-deprecating apology for a \"hastily scrawled\" (<i>raptim exaratas</i>) letter the messenger's haste left unrevised.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 25. Ianuarij Anno 1587</i> — <b>25 January 1587</b>, Old Style (Denmark used the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The catalogue label (\"1587. 25. januar. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. No date is converted.</li><li><b>Language.</b> Latin throughout (the heuristic tag <i>la</i> is correct on reading), with two non-Latin tokens: the German address <i>ZU PRAG</i> and the place-name <i>Cimbria</i>. No Greek occurs in this letter.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin text-error fixed: 'nuntiorum penuriam causa fuit' restored to Dreyer's 'nuntiorum penuria in causa fuit' (TBOO VII, p.111); the English translation was already correct and unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched; the only soft points, flagged rather than smoothed, are (i) the unidentified \"noble Frenchmen\" and the precise identity of the <i>Diarium</i> / solar <i>restitutio</i> they supplied, and (ii) the exact referent of <i>opus de prima et Mechanica Astronomiae parte</i> versus the <i>Progymnasmata</i> then at press, both left as the text leaves them.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VII pp. 110-115) against Dreyer's page images in two chunks (pages 1-4 = pp.110-113, pages 5-6 = pp.114-115). Checked the opening and closing formulas and every proper name, number, date and place-name: SIGISMUNDO, 9 scrupulis (pole-star/Cassiopeia distance), 4 scrupulis (New Star declination), ROTHMANNUM, WILHELMI Landtgravii Hassiae, Lipsiae/Dresdae/Ratisbonae, Cimbriam, TRAPEZUNTIJ/PTOLOMAEUM, 25 Ianuarii 1587 -- all match Dreyer exactly, and the closing farewell and postscript (\"Boni consule...\") match verbatim. The translation is faithful and complete throughout. The sole defect is a source_text/Dreyer divergence (TEXT_ERROR) in the messenger-scarcity clause on p.110-111; it does not change what a scholar would take from the English, but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin drops the word \"in\" and turns \"penuria\" into \"penuriam\", corrupting Dreyer's idiomatic \"in causa fuit\" (\"was the cause\") into an ungrammatical phrase; the English translation itself renders the intended sense correctly and is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>nuntiorum penuria in causa fuit</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified TBOO VII p.111 directly from the Dreyer page image (line ~6): the printed text reads 'nuntiorum penuria in caufa fuit', confirming the audit. The Latin has been corrected to match; the English translation, already faithful to the intended sense, was left untouched. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Thaddaeum Hagecium a. 1588 (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/62-63", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964485"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.63. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15860907001", "next": "brahe-t_15880503001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860907001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881101001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Returns Hagecius's manuscript on the new star and comets via the Frankfurt fair and advises him to revise his treatment of the 1577 comet before publishing.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870125001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870522001", "d": "1587-05-22", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 22 May 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1568 ad annum 1587 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>DANIEL RENTFLERUS, in bonis literis apprime versatus, eruditus mehercle iuvenis et optimis moribus excultus, Daniam petere voluit visendi gratia et te inprimis salutare, qui alter Atlas coelum humeris ferre diceris. Age, mi TYCHO, persequere magna cum laude labores institutos et coelum ipsum ad nos transfer, longitudinibus stellarum definitis, et, si quid in latitudinibus desideretur, idipsum posteritati prodens. Nam audio, te aliquid novi in his invenisse, adeo ut dubitari possit, an non ipsae etiam mutentur latitudines. Est quidem audax facinus a tanto illustrium Mathematicorum consensu dissentire, qui tanto temporum intervallo distrahuntur. Sed si te sic rata docuit experientia, cur tibi non fidem adhibeamus potius quam illis, qui forte non tam subtiliter omnia rimati sunt? Longitudines autem stellarum non respondere calculo, nihil mirum, cum hactenus Hypotheses inventae, quae hanc apparentem motuum inaequalitatem ad aequalitatem reducant, non sint. Latitudines vero invariabiles servari omnes prodiderunt. Expecto tuam sententiam, et quid de tuis Cometis fiat, utrum praelo subiecti sint, an vero supprimere decreveris. Hunc vero iuvenem, quem ob eruditionem et modestiam omnes amant, nostra et sua causa et pro tua singulari erga homines literarum amantes benevolentia complectere. Tractatum de Crepusculis Sphaerae adiectum, utrum legeris, scire velim. Si quid habeas Geometricum laude dignum, ab aliquo recentor editum, eius autorem indicato et, si aliquot dies carere queas, ad nos mittito. Nos minus hoc tempore hisce studijs vacamus, alijs distracti. Tu quid agas, quid mediteris, fac intelligam. Et si quid ex Vulcani officina habes, quod in paralyticis et alijs contumacibus morbis nostra medicamenta respuentibus expertus es, id communices rogo.</p><p>Vale, Nobilissime doctissimeque TYCHO, et nos mutuo ama.<br>Rostochij 22. Maij 1587.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Daniel Rentflerus, most accomplished in good letters — a learned young man, by Hercules, and refined in the finest character — has wished to make for Denmark for the sake of seeing [it], and above all to greet you, who are said to be a second Atlas bearing heaven upon your shoulders. Come, my Tycho, carry through with great renown the labours you have undertaken, and transfer heaven itself to us, with the longitudes of the stars determined and — if anything be wanting in the latitudes — bringing that very thing forth to posterity [as well]. For I hear that you have found something new in these matters, so much so that it may be doubted whether the latitudes themselves are not also changing. It is, to be sure, a bold deed to dissent from so great a consensus of illustrious mathematicians, who are spread across so great an interval of time. But if settled experience has so taught you, why should we not give credence to you rather than to those who perhaps have not searched out everything so subtly? That the longitudes of the stars do not answer to calculation is no wonder, since up to now there have been no hypotheses devised that reduce this apparent inequality of the motions to equality. The latitudes, however, all have reported to be kept invariable. I await your opinion, and what is becoming of your comets — whether they have been put under the press, or whether you have rather resolved to suppress them. But do embrace this young man, whom all love for his erudition and modesty, both for our sake and for his own, and out of that singular benevolence of yours toward men who love letters. I should like to know whether you have read the enclosed treatise <i>On the Twilights of the Sphere</i>. If you have anything geometrical worthy of praise, lately published by someone, point out its author, and — if you can do without it for some few days — send it to us. We, at this time, have less leisure for these studies, being drawn off by others. What you are doing, what you are pondering — let me understand. And if you have anything from Vulcan's workshop which you have tried out on the paralytic and on other stubborn diseases that spurn our medicines, I beg you to share it.</p><p>Farewell, most noble and most learned Tycho, and love us in return.<br>At Rostock, 22 May 1587.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broecke, c. 1530/31–1593), professor of mathematics and then of medicine at the University of Rostock, a Bruges-born physician-mathematician and one of Tycho's senior scholarly correspondents (protocol §5.6). The dual mathematical/medical interest is on full display here: the letter moves from stellar coordinates to a request for a chemical remedy.</li><li><b><i>Daniel Rentflerus</b></i> — <b>Daniel Rentfler(us)</b>, the young scholar carrying the letter as a letter of introduction; warmly recommended for his learning and modesty. (The bearer-recommendation is the letter's nominal occasion.)</li><li><b><i>alter Atlas coelum humeris ferre</b></i> — \"a second Atlas bearing heaven on his shoulders,\" the mythological compliment to Tycho's astronomical labours; the classical image is kept.</li><li><b>Astronomy — the fixed-star question.</b> The core technical matter is whether stellar <b>latitudes</b> (celestial latitude, <i>latitudo</i>) are constant. <i>longitudinibus stellarum definitis … si quid in latitudinibus desideretur</i> = \"with the longitudes of the stars determined … if anything be wanting in the latitudes.\" Brucaeus reports a rumour that Tycho has found the latitudes themselves to be changing (<i>an non ipsae etiam mutentur latitudines</i>) — a genuinely \"bold deed\" against the <i>consensus</i> of earlier authorities who \"reported the latitudes to be kept invariable\" (<i>Latitudines … invariabiles servari omnes prodiderunt</i>). The longitude/latitude renderings follow protocol §5.1.</li><li><b><i>Hypotheses … apparentem motuum inaequalitatem ad aequalitatem reducant</b></i> — \"hypotheses that reduce this apparent inequality of the motions to equality\": the standard astronomical sense of <i>hypothesis</i> as a geometrical model accounting for the non-uniform apparent motions; <i>motus</i> → \"motion,\" <i>inaequalitas/aequalitas</i> kept as \"inequality/equality\" of the motions (§5.1).</li><li><b><i>rata … experientia</b></i> — \"settled / ratified experience\"; <i>experientia</i> rendered \"experience\" (the appeal to Tycho's observational basis), with <i>rata</i> as \"settled.\" Brucaeus' epistemic hedges (<i>forte</i> \"perhaps,\" <i>dubitari possit</i> \"it may be doubted,\" <i>audio</i> \"I hear\") are preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b><i>tuis Cometis … utrum praelo subiecti sint, an … supprimere decreveris</b></i> — \"your comets — whether they have been put under the press, or whether you have resolved to suppress them\": a reference to Tycho's cometary work, in print preparation by this date (the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, on the comet of 1577, was being printed at Uraniborg in the later 1580s). <i>praelum</i> = \"press\" (printing press); the question of publishing vs. withholding is kept as posed.</li><li><b><i>Tractatum de Crepusculis Sphaerae</b></i> — \"the treatise <i>On the Twilights of the Sphere</i>,\" an enclosed work concerning twilight (<i>crepusculum</i> → \"twilight,\" §5.1) and the doctrine of the sphere. The exact title/author is not specified in the letter; rendered descriptively and not identified beyond the text (see crux).</li><li><b><i>ex Vulcani officina</b></i> — \"from Vulcan's workshop,\" a period circumlocution for the <b>alchemical / chymical laboratory</b> and its iatrochemical preparations. Brucaeus, as a physician, asks for a chemical remedy Tycho has tried on <i>paralytici</i> (\"the paralytic\") and other <i>contumaces morbi</i> (\"stubborn diseases\") that \"spurn our [ordinary] medicines.\" Kept as the period metaphor, not modernised.</li><li><b><i>mehercle</b></i> — \"by Hercules,\" a colloquial classical asseveration, kept.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>Vale … et nos mutuo ama</i> = \"Farewell … and love us in return,\" the affectionate close (§5.5). <i>Nobilissime doctissimeque</i> = \"most noble and most learned,\" the vocative honorific addressed to Tycho.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Rostochij</i> = <b>Rostock</b> (protocol §5.7), seat of the university where Brucaeus taught.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Rostochij 22. Maij 1587</i> = \"At Rostock, 22 May 1587.\" The dataset `label` (\"1587. 22. maj. Rostock\") agrees with the as-written dateline; no discrepancy. Style is <b>Old Style (Julian)</b>: Lutheran Mecklenburg/Rostock had not adopted the Gregorian reform, so the date is O.S.</li><li><b>Textual note.</b> <i>recentor</i> (in <i>ab aliquo recentor editum</i>) is anomalous; read as <i>recenter</i> (\"lately, recently\"), the reading adopted in the translation; the printed form is retained in the Source block per protocol §2/§4. <i>idipsum</i> and <i>inprimis</i> are written solid as printed.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical fixed-star argument is clear. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the unidentified <i>Tractatus de Crepusculis Sphaerae</i> is rendered descriptively, its author/edition not asserted; (2) <i>recentor</i> is read as <i>recenter</i> (\"lately\"), noted in the apparatus. Neither affects the substance of the letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read both page images of the slice visually (Dreyer VII pp. 114-115; letter entirely on p. 114). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin. Checked every name (Daniel Rentflerus), the title 'Tractatum de Crepusculis Sphaerae', the claims about stellar longitudes/latitudes and Tycho's Cometis, and the date 'Rostochij 22. Maij 1587'. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15860315001", "next": "brahe-t_15871028001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Introduces a young scholar visiting Tycho, urges completion of the star catalogue's longitudes and latitudes, and endorses Tycho's dissent from prior authorities if experience warrants it.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870522001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870718001", "d": "1587-07-18", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Niels Bild", "ck": "Niels Bild", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Niels Bild — Uraniborg, 18 July 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin address-rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THILL ERLIG OCH WELBIURDIG MAND NIELS BILLD THIJL RAUNHOLTT, MIN BESØNDERLIGE GODE WENN DETTE BREFF GANDSKE WENLIG THILLSCHREFFUID.</p><p>MIJNN gandske wenlig helßennd nu och altid forsennd med Wor Herre. Kiere Niels Bild, och sønderlige gode wenn, nest en wenligh tacksigelse for alt beuist gode, huilket jeg altid will findis willig och redebon tiill epther formuenn at forschilde, kand ieg eder wenligen icke forholde, att epthersom Christoffher Pepler min fordoms fogit ij Norfiord lhenn ij Norge, dentijd jeg dett haffde af Kong. May. ij forlening, er nu kommen hidt tiill mig fra Norge, saa haffuer hand giffuedt mig tilkiende, huorledis hand endnu haffuer nogen skilld vdstaendis ij Norfiord baade aff kongens restandtz och aff sidt egidt goeds, huormed hand bønderne vndsett haffde, huilckid de nu forhollder hannom, och dertilmed haffuer bygtt nogle hus paa sin egen bekostning oppaa en øde platz, huor ingen huß førre paastodt, ey heller nogen agger heller eng tilligger. Laadendis mig ochsaa der hoß forstaa, huorledis at prousten derudj lenid, som skulle holde kirkens regenskab, er hannom for tong, ij dett hand siger: att de kiercke ombodsmend, som haffuer bygt paa kierkerne, skulle haffue satt deris arbeid for dyrtt och der ijmod wille tage kierkens renntte for leett jnd, som jeg kand forstaa, och att Christoffher skulle dett wederlegge kierckerne jgenn ijmod de breffue, som kiercke ombodsmenddenne haffuer giffuidt hannom, lydendis, att hand jntted haffuer oppebaridt aff kierckens reennte vdenn sin lhøn, och beretter ydermere, att er hand ochsaa befrøchttendis, att ij haffuer hannom mijßtenckt ij dett hand skulle haffue schreffuit nogidt tijll mig om eder, huilked jeg kiendis att hand icke giortt haffuer, menn att jeg aff min egenn goede wille denn tijd jeg spurde hannom nogenn gield der ij leenid tiill att staa, schreff eder til, forseendes mig till eder, som min goede wenn, att hand formijddelst eders hielp och bijstand diß snarere skulle komme thiill rette och nhaa sin betalling. Kiere Niels Bild, er derfor min gandske wenlig bøenn endnu til eder, att ij wille well giøre, som jeg eder thiill troer, och fordre och forfremme samme Christoffher udj diße forneffnde saager till det beste, och hielpe hannom att komme till enn goed ende der vdj, jnngennlunde haffuendis hannom mijstenckt ij nogen maade. Kiere Niels, haffuer jeg gierne sielff willde talidt med eder baade om dette och anditt denn thijd ij ware først neer kommen aff Norghe, menn nu kunde leilighedenn siig jcke saa begijffue. Menn dersom dett ware eder muligt och ij willde haffue denn wmage och besøge mig engang, førrind ij farer thiill Norge jgenn, daa saahe jeg det gandske gierne, oc vdj hues maade jeg kan nogen tijd giøre nogidt for eders skilld, som Eder thijl willge och guode kand vere, will jeg findis gandske och reedebon derthill.</p><p>Och will jeg hermed nu och altid haffue eder med alt hues ij well will denn alsommechtigste Gud befallendis.<br>Datum Vrannieborgh thend 18. julij anno [15]87.<br>Thyge Brahe<br>Egen hand.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born man Niels Bild of Ravnholt, my especial good friend, this letter written in all friendship.</b></p><p>My wholly friendly greeting, now and ever, sent with Our Lord. Dear Niels Bild and especial good friend, after a friendly thanksgiving for all the kindness shown me — which I shall always be found willing and ready to requite according to my means — I cannot in friendship withhold from you that, inasmuch as Christoffer Pepler, my former bailiff in the fief of Nordfjord in Norway during the time I held it in fief from His Royal Majesty, has now come hither to me from Norway, he has thereby given me to understand how he still has certain monies outstanding in Nordfjord, both from the king's arrears and from his own goods, with which he had relieved [advanced credit to] the peasants — which they now withhold from him; and that in addition he has built some houses at his own expense upon a waste plot where no house stood before, nor [where] any field or meadow appertains. He has also given me to understand how the provost there in the fief, who was to keep the church's accounts, is too hard upon him, in that he [the provost] says that the churchwardens who built upon the churches are supposed to have set their work too dear and, against that, would enter the church's revenue too low [in the accounts], as I can understand it; and that Christoffer is supposed to make this good to the churches again, against the letters [receipts] which the churchwardens had given him, stating that he has received nothing of the church's revenue beyond his wages. And he reports further that he is also apprehensive that you have held him in suspicion, in that he is supposed to have written something to me about you — which I acknowledge he has not done; but rather that I, of my own good will, at the time when I asked him what debt was standing [outstanding] there in the fief, wrote to you, trusting in you as my good friend, that through your help and assistance he might the sooner come to his right and obtain his payment. Dear Niels Bild, my wholly friendly request to you is therefore still that you will do well, as I trust of you, and to further and advance the same Christoffer in these aforesaid matters to the best [outcome], and help him to come to a good end therein, in no wise having held him in suspicion in any manner. Dear Niels, I should gladly have spoken with you myself, both about this and other things, at the time when you had first come down from Norway, but now the occasion could not so present itself. But if it were possible for you, and you would take the trouble to visit me once, before you go to Norway again, then I should see it most gladly; and in whatever manner I can at any time do anything for your sake that may be to your pleasure and good, I shall be found wholly and readily disposed thereto.</p><p>And herewith I shall now and ever commend you, with all that you wish well, to Almighty God.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 18th of July, the year [15]87.<br>Tyge Brahe.<br>In his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Uraniborg on Hven. The subscription <i>Thyge Brahe / Egen hand</i> (\"Tyge Brahe / In his own hand\") marks the letter as an autograph; <i>Egen hand</i> = <i>egen hånd</i>, \"[in his] own hand.\"<br>- <b>Recipient.</b> <i>Niels Billd thijl Raunholtt</i> = <b>Niels Bild til Ravnholt</b>, a Danish nobleman who held crown fiefs in Norway (the surviving MS is a later copy in Langebek's Diplomatarium, so the spelling \"Billd/Bild\" is the copyist's). Tycho addresses him as a fellow member of the nobility and as a friend, interceding on behalf of a former subordinate.<br>- <b>Christoffer Pepler</b> — Tycho's former <i>fogit</i> (bailiff/steward) in the Nordfjord fief; the letter is wholly a letter of recommendation/intercession on his behalf. <i>fordoms</i> = <i>fordums</i>, \"former / of old.\"<br>- <b>Place / fief.</b> <i>Norfiord lhenn ij Norge</i> = the fief (<i>len</i>) of <b>Nordfjord</b> in western Norway. Tycho had held it <i>ij forlening</i> — \"in fief / enfeoffment\" — from the Crown (<i>Kong. May.</i> = <i>Kongelig Majestæt</i>, \"His Royal Majesty,\" Frederik II); he had been granted the income of Nordfjord len in 1578 and held it until 1587, so this letter coincides with the close of that tenure.<br>- <b>Document type / rubric.</b> The opening line is the <b>address (superscription)</b> written on the outside/head of the letter, in the chancery majuscule convention, not a separate Latin rubric: <i>\"…dette breff gandske wenlig thillschreffuid\"</i> = \"this letter written in all friendship.\" (Unlike the Frederik II compass-letter, there is no Latin <i>ut sequitur</i> register-rubric here; the whole document is Danish.)<br>- <b>Legal / administrative substance (translated with care for negations).</b> Three claims of Pepler's are reported, each preserved with its exact polarity:<br>  1. He has <i>skilld vdstaendis</i> — \"monies outstanding\" (<i>gæld/skyld udestående</i>) — owed both from <i>kongens restandtz</i> (\"the king's arrears,\" unpaid Crown dues) and from his own goods advanced to the peasants, which they <b>now withhold</b> (<i>forhollder</i>) from him.<br>  2. The <b>provost</b> (<i>prousten</i>, ecclesiastical superintendent of the <i>len</i>), who keeps the church accounts, is <i>for tong</i> (\"too hard / too heavy\") on him: the dispute is whether the <b>churchwardens</b> (<i>kiercke ombodsmend</i>, church stewards) overcharged for building work and under-entered the church revenue, and whether Pepler must <i>wederlegge</i> (\"make good / reimburse\") the difference — <b>against</b> the <i>breffue</i> (receipts/letters) the churchwardens gave him stating he took <b>nothing</b> of the church revenue beyond his wages. The double negative force (\"nothing… beyond his wages\") is reproduced exactly.<br>  3. Pepler <b>fears</b> Niels Bild suspects him of having written something against him to Tycho. Tycho's denial is exact and carries the legal weight of the letter: <i>\"huilked jeg kiendis att hand icke giortt haffuer\"</i> = \"which I acknowledge he has <b>not</b> done\" — i.e. Pepler did <b>not</b> write to Tycho about Bild; rather Tycho himself, <i>aff min egenn goede wille</i> (\"of my own good will\"), wrote to Bild after asking Pepler about debts outstanding in the fief. The closing plea <i>\"jnngennlunde haffuendis hannom mijstenckt ij nogen maade\"</i> = \"in <b>no wise</b> having held him in suspicion in any manner\" — three negation-bearing elements (<i>ingenlunde</i> \"by no means,\" <i>mistænkt</i> \"suspected,\" <i>i nogen måde</i> \"in any manner\") all retained.<br>- <b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1587, via a later copyist).</b> <i>helßennd</i> = <i>hilsen</i> (\"greeting\"); <i>forsennd</i> = <i>forsendt</i> (\"sent\"); <i>redebon / reedebon</i> = <i>redebon</i> (\"ready, willing\"); <i>forschilde</i> = <i>forskylde / gengælde</i> (\"requite, deserve in return\"); <i>epthersom</i> = <i>eftersom</i> (\"inasmuch as\"); <i>tilkiende</i> = <i>til kende</i> (\"[given] to understand\"); <i>restandtz</i> = <i>restance</i> (\"arrears\"); <i>vndsett</i> = <i>undsat</i> (\"relieved, advanced [credit to]\"); <i>øde platz</i> = <i>øde plads</i> (\"waste / vacant plot\"); <i>agger heller eng</i> = <i>ager eller eng</i> (\"field or meadow\"); <i>prousten</i> = <i>provsten</i> (\"the provost\"); <i>ombodsmend</i> = <i>ombudsmænd</i> (\"stewards, agents\"); <i>wederlegge</i> = <i>vederlægge</i> (\"compensate, make good\"); <i>oppebaridt</i> = <i>oppebåret</i> (\"received, collected [revenue]\"); <i>befrøchttendis</i> = <i>befrygtende</i> (\"apprehensive, fearing\"); <i>mijßtenckt / mijstenckt</i> = <i>mistænkt</i> (\"suspected\"); <i>forseendes mig till eder</i> = <i>forseende mig til eder</i> (\"trusting / relying on you\"); <i>fordre och forfremme</i> = <i>fordre og fremme</i> (\"further and advance\"); <i>jnngennlunde</i> = <i>ingenlunde</i> (\"by no means\"); <i>wmage</i> = <i>umage</i> (\"trouble, effort\"); <i>leilighedenn</i> = <i>lejligheden</i> (\"the occasion\"); <i>alsommechtigste</i> = <i>allermægtigste</i> (\"Almighty\"). Address formulae per protocol §5.5: the letter substitutes a friendly noble-to-noble salutation for the royal <i>Wor gunst tilforn</i>.<br>- <b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Vrannieborgh thend 18. julij anno [15]87</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, the 18th of July, the year [15]87\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). <i>Datum</i> \"Given\" kept per §5.5. The header date agrees with the dataset label (<i>1587. 18. juli. Uraniborg</i>); no discrepancy. The bracketed \"15\" in <i>anno [15]87</i> is the editor's expansion of the copy's <i>anno 87</i>.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely and the negations have been counted and preserved exactly (the central point of the letter is Tycho's denial that Pepler wrote against Bild, plus the threefold \"in no wise suspected\" plea). The one soft point is the precise administrative sense of the churchwarden/provost accounting dispute (clause 2): <i>wderlegge … ijmod de breffue</i> is read as \"make good [the shortfall] against [= notwithstanding] the receipts,\" but <i>ijmod</i> could in principle be \"in accordance with\"; the surrounding logic (the receipts exonerate Pepler) favours the adversative reading, which is followed without smoothing the underlying ambiguity.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.35-38 as page images; letter no. 57 'THIILL ERLIG OCH WELBIURDIG MAND NIELS BILLD...' runs from p.36 across the p.36/37 page break to the signature 'Thyge Brahe / Egen hand.' on p.37. Collated the full Danish letter word for word against Dreyer (only trivial single/double-letter and u/v spelling variants, which are ignorable orthographic convention, not substantive differences) and checked every name, place, and date: Niels Bild of Ravnholt, Christoffer Pepler, the Nordfjord fief, Norway, and the closing date '18 julij anno [15]87' at Uraniborg all check out. The English translation renders every clause faithfully, including the church-accounts/provost passage and the debt/payment request, with no additions, drops, flips, or hedge losses. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Intercedes for his former bailiff Christoffer Pepler, seeking recovery of debts withheld by Nordfjord peasants and reimbursement for buildings erected on a waste plot.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870718001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870812001", "d": "1587-08-12", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 12 August 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(Liber I, Uraniborg 1596) = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed-text letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERVDITIONE EXCELLENTI ET INGENIOSA PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. S. D.</p><p>REVERSVS ad nos is per quem instantibus vltimo Francoforti vernalibus Nundinis Illustriss. Principi tuo et tibi de rebus Astronomicis copiose perscripseram, vestraeque expectationi, vti spero, pro virili satisfeceram, praeter omnem meam opinionem nihil prorsus literarum retulit. Cuius causam admiranti sciscitantique mihi, Illustrissimum Principem LANDTGRAVIVM eo tempore, quo transactis Nundinis Cassellas redierat, nuptijs quibusdam occupatum fuisse, quo minus tunc responsioni vacare ipsius Celsitudini commodum foret, et te etiam eâdem de causa varijs auocationibus implicitum fuisse referebat, subiungebatque ipsum Illustrissimum Principem per te sibi significasse, quod breui misso proprio Tabellario literas, quae meis responderent, essem recepturus. Huic Relationi hactenus lubens acquieui, et indies ab Illustrissimo Principe, tum etiam a te responsionem cupide expectaui, quam cum vltra speratum tempus differri videam, non potui non oblata per hunc Bibliopolam HENRICVM, (eidem per quem priores miseram inseruientem) hac opportuna scribendi occasione, ad te aliquid literarum dare, et rescriptionem hactenus intermissam in memoriam reuocare. Rogo itaque peramanter, vt velis apud Illustrissimum Principem tuum de responsione mihi remittenda, meo nomine obnixe et reuerenter sollicitare, vtque tu etiam ijs, quas ad te seorsim dedi literis, omnimode rescribendo satisfacere non diutius supersedeas. Si per proprium Principis Tabellarium negotium hoc commode expediri nequeat, idipsum per harum exhibitorem satis opportune efficietur, modo literas, quamprimum is e Nundinis Francofordianis Cassellas reuersus fuerit, in promptu paratas inuenerit, nam ne comitum et vehiculi dispendium faciat, moram istic aliquam nectere vix illi commodum fuerit. Retulit idem, qui meas vobis attulit, te impetrata a Principe tuo venia, me coram inuisere decreuisse, quod vt prima quaque opportunitate exequi non intermittas, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Erit tuus accessus mihi pergratus, et de multis praesentes oretenus plenius et commodius, quam absentes per literas, iucunde, nec, vti spero, inutiliter conferemus, Vnaque Instrumentorum nostrorum varia exquisitaque structura, obseruandique per haec in fallibilis apprimeque concinna ratio oculari inspectione omnium optime tibi patebit. Omninoque tibi persuadeas, quod te eius itineris et laboris non paeniteat vnquam. Eclipsin Lunarem proxime instantem, non dubito, quin per serenitatis opportunitatem diligenter sis caelitus animaduersurus. Eius itaque apparentias et temporum momenta, vel potius ipsa Obseruationum Data mecum communicare non graueris, Ego etiam eandem hîc meis Organis adamussim perscrutari (volente Deo) non intermittam, teque de meis inuentis postmodum certiorem reddam. Vale et nostris desiderijs, inprimis vero ijs quae in literis ad Illustrissimum Principem tuum, et te vltimo datis continebantur, quaeque earum exhibitor priuatim meo nomine significauit, vt omni ex parte mature ante ingruentem hyemem satisfiat, omnem moue lapidem. Iterum feliciter vale, et absque intermissione Sacris Vraniae lita.</p><p>Datae Vraniburgi 12. Augusti, Anno 1587.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent man, foremost in fine and ingenious learning, Master Christoph Rothmann, distinguished mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his much-beloved friend — sends greeting.</b></p><p>The man through whom, at the last spring fair at Frankfurt now past, I had written at length on astronomical matters to your Most Illustrious Prince and to yourself — and had, as I hope, satisfied your expectation to the best of my power — has returned to us, and, contrary to all my expectation, brought back no letters whatsoever. When I wondered at this and asked the reason, he reported that the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave, at the time when he had returned to Kassel after the fair, had been taken up with a certain wedding, so that it was not then convenient for His Highness to be free for a reply, and that you too, for the same reason, had been caught up in various distractions; and he added that the Most Illustrious Prince had signified to him, through you, that by a courier of his own, to be sent shortly, I should receive letters answering mine. With this report I have hitherto gladly rested content, and day by day eagerly awaited a reply from the Most Illustrious Prince, and from you as well. But seeing it put off beyond the time I had hoped, I could not but — this convenient occasion for writing being offered through the bookseller Heinrich (who serves the same man by whom I had sent the earlier letters) — send you a few lines, and recall to mind the reply so far left unmade. And so I ask you most affectionately to be willing, in my name, earnestly and reverently to press your Most Illustrious Prince for a reply to be sent back to me; and that you too put off no longer satisfying, by a full reply, the letters I sent to you separately. If this business cannot conveniently be dispatched by the Prince's own courier, the same will be managed suitably enough through the bearer of this letter — provided only that, as soon as he has returned from the Frankfurt fair to Kassel, he find the letters ready to hand; for, that he may not incur the expense of attendants and a carriage, it will scarcely suit him to contrive any delay there. The same man who brought you my letters reported that you, having obtained leave from your Prince, had resolved to visit me in person; and I beg you again and again not to fail to carry this out at the very first opportunity. Your coming will be most welcome to me, and on many matters we shall confer face to face, by word of mouth, more fully and conveniently — and pleasantly, and, as I hope, not unprofitably — than we can, apart, by letter. And at the same time the varied and exquisite construction of our instruments, and the unerring and singularly elegant method of observing by means of them, will be laid open to you, best of all, by inspection with your own eyes. And be wholly assured that you will never regret that journey and labour. The lunar eclipse now next at hand — I do not doubt that, with the chance of a clear sky, you will diligently observe it in the heavens. So do not find it a burden to share with me its appearances and the precise moments of time — or rather the very data of your observations; I too, for my part, shall not fail to scrutinize the same here, to the line, with my instruments (God willing), and will afterward inform you of my findings. Farewell; and, that our wishes — above all those contained in the letters last sent to your Most Illustrious Prince and to you, and those that their bearer privately signified in my name — may be met in every part in good time, before winter comes on, move every stone [leave no stone unturned]. Again, fare you well and happily, and without ceasing make your offerings at the rites of Urania [devote yourself unceasingly to astronomy].</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, 12 August, in the year 1587.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (d. c. 1600), mathematician–astronomer to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b>, at the Kassel observatory. The Tycho–Rothmann correspondence (begun 1586) is the technical heart of the printed <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>.</li><li><b>Context.</b> This is a <i>logistical</i> letter within that exchange — chasing an overdue reply routed through the <b>Frankfurt book fair</b> (<i>Nundinae Francofordianae</i>, the twice-yearly Frankfurt fair, the hub of the learned book and letter trade) and a bookseller named <b>Heinrich</b> — but its substance is the standing invitation to Kassel↔Hven scholarly exchange and a concrete request: joint observation of the <b>coming lunar eclipse</b>.</li><li><b><i>Instrumentorum … structura … observandi ratio infallibilis</b></i> — \"the construction of our instruments … the unerring method of observing\": Tycho's recurring boast of the Uraniborg instruments and their precision, here as a lure for Rothmann to come and see them.</li><li><b><i>meis Organis adamussim perscrutari</b></i> — \"to scrutinize with my instruments, to the line\": <i>adamussim</i> (literally \"to the mason's/carpenter's line\") = with exact precision; <i>Organa</i> = instruments (protocol §5.2).</li><li><b><i>apparentias et temporum momenta … ipsa Observationum Data</b></i> — \"the appearances and the precise moments of time … the very data of the observations\": the technical request — phases/aspects and exact timings of the eclipse.</li><li><b><i>omnem move lapidem</b></i> — \"move every stone,\" the proverb (Greek <i>pánta kínei pétron</i>); rendered with the gloss \"leave no stone unturned.\"</li><li><b><i>Sacris Uraniae lita</b></i> — \"make offering at the rites of Urania,\" i.e. devote yourself to astronomy (Urania = the Muse of astronomy); a characteristic Tycho sign-off, kept as sacral metaphor (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> O.S.; Uraniborg/Hven kept Julian.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure. Diplomatic-logistical prose with no disputed technical claim; the few elevated metaphors (<i>move lapidem</i>, <i>Sacris Uraniae</i>) are glossed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (Tycho to Rothmann, Uraniborg 12 Aug 1587) — no Dreyer slice exists, so collation was not possible; audited against source_text alone. Read every clause of the Latin against the English (the returned messenger with no reply, the Landgrave's wedding and Kassel itinerary, the bookseller Heinrich, the invitation to visit Uraniborg and inspect the instruments, the request for lunar-eclipse observation data). All content is rendered accurately with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge loss found. FLAG: the published_grade of 'A' on this record is unwarranted — an A requires Dreyer collation per protocol, and none was possible here (tboo:null); the self-assigned grade should not have exceeded B. Text quality itself is clean, but the grade as published was miscalibrated.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15870120001", "next": "brahe-t_15871011001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860907002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15871011001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that a messenger returned from Frankfurt with no letters, explaining that the Landgrave had been occupied with a wedding and has promised a reply by courier shortly.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870812001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870911001", "d": "1587-09-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 11 September 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German chancery, with heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI<br>BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.AWILHELM VON<br>GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/<br>ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA/ ETC.AUNSERN gnedigen gruß zuuor/ Vester lieber besonder. Wir<br>haben ewer schreiben de Dato den 18.<br>Ianuaris beneben den Obseruationibus entpfangen.<br>Das jhr vns nun dieselbigen auff vnser begeren so gutwillig zugeschickt/<br>Dessen thun wir vns jegen euchA gnediglichen bedancken. Mögen euch aber nicht verhalten/ das wir<br>dazumahl mit andern geschefften beladen/ vnd an andere örter verreyset gewesenA/ dardurch wir euch zu antworten/ verhindert worden. So baldtA wir aber wieder anhero gelangt/ haben wir ewere Obseruationes mit den vnsern Conferirt, vnd mit<br>sonderlicher verwunderung befunden/ das sie in distantijs et<br>Declinationibus, etiam in partibus Minuti mit einander<br>vbereinstimmen/ welches zuuor niemals ist erhört worden. Das sie aber in Longitudinibus auff fünff Minuten von einander discrepirn, nimpt vns nicht wunder/ sintemahl vns wol<br>bewust/ das weder Hipparchus,<br>Ptolemaeus, noch alle andere<br>hiebeuor vera Stellarum loca auff 5. Minuten jemals<br>gewust/ noch propter Armillarum<br>paruitatem haben wissen können. Denn man in den Armillis kaum 10. Minuten hat können mercken/<br>wie das aus den Obseruationibus Stellarum Fixarum<br>augenscheinlich/ geschwiegen was sonsten propter Circulorum<br>hinc inde distractionem in obseruando hat mangeln können/ dannA die Circuli in solcher Connexion im tractiren sich baldt geben. Von<br>vnsern Obseruationibus können wir noch zur zeit nicht<br>schreitten/ dannA sie vnser Mathematicus, etiam correcto Quadrantis<br>perpendiculo<br>vielfeltigA/ nicht allein per Horologium, sondern auch im<br>verschienen Ianuario etzliche mahl Venerem, die er gleichsfals wie jhr/ bey tagkA/ beneben der Sonnen obseruirt, iust befunden.<br>Jedoch haben wir vnsern Mathematicum hierumb ernstlich bespracht/ da hat er vns nicht können verhaltenA/ wie er dazumahl per Venerem befunden/ das ewer<br>Motus Solis, den jhr vns in ewerer Ephemeride zugeschickt/ circa octauum gradum ♒,<br>vmb etzliche Minuten zu langsam/ denn er in<br>fine Ianuaris, auch durch vnsern Quadranten locum<br>Solis, nicht anders gefunden/ jhn auch nicht anders angenommen/ dannA wie er in ewer Ephemeride (welche er auch auff<br>dieses Jahr iusto modo hat extendiret)<br>stehet: Nichts desto weiniger hat doch Venus keinen<br>andern Locum Stellarum Fixarum geben wollen: Vermeinet/<br>jhr tribuiret den Refractionibus etwas<br>zuuiel. Auch vermeint er/ alldieweil jhr das tempus per Armillas Aequatorias indagirt, vnd aber die Refractio Solis in Circulo Verticali, vnd nicht secundum Longitudinem Eclipticae geschicht/ könne es<br>leicht sein/ das jhr das tempus etwas zu kurtz<br>angenommen/ welches nicht geschehen könne/ so man das tempus<br>per Azimutha Solis indagire. Jedoch weil jhr esA<br>per Venerem circa Aequinoctium autumnale des Morgens probiret, hat er nicht eigentlich können wissen/ wo an es<br>mangele. Wie nun dem/ so wissen wir nicht von vnsern ObseruationibusA, die wir nicht minder mit höchstem fleiss/ als jhr die ewerigen<br>angestellet/ abzutretten. Vnd dieweil wir nun viel Jahr her mit den ObseruationibusA vmbgangen/ vnd auch durch vns die Astronomiam gern<br>beforderten/ Ais haben wir vnserm Mathematico/ gnedigklichA befohlen/ die Obseruationes aber zu calculiren, vnd beneben den Demonstrationibus et<br>modis obseruandi zu publiciren, damit jederman<br>vnsern fleiss/ vnd arbeit sehen möchte/ wie er dann schon in die 200. Stellas calculiret, vnd noch mehr as 200. zu calculiren hat. Soll aber doch von<br>dieser nichtigen vnnd geringen discrepantz, zwischen den<br>vnsern vnnd ewerigen/ keine anmeldung thun/ auch die ewerigen nicht spargiren. Was jhr vns aber sonsten communiciret, wirdt er wol pro dignitate zu<br>rühmen/ vnnd euch cum honorifica nominis mentione zu restituiren wissen. Also werdet jhr als dann desto besser<br>vnd fleissiger in vnsere Obseruationes können inquiriren, wirdt auch die<br>sache bey andern desto ehe glauben finden/ alldieweil ein jeder bey seinen<br>Obseruationibus bleibt/ vnd nicht in<br>gratiam alterius etwas mutiret. Wir seindt auch<br>nach vollendung des wercks/ inwillens/ vnsere Instrumenta<br>auff eine andere art von newen anstellen zu lassenA/ vnd da wir als dann befinden werdenA/ das wir auff die 5. Minuten gejrret/ wollen wir<br>vns gern selbst corrigiren, Wiewol disse discrepantz baldt nicht werth ist/ das man so viel wesens drumb<br>mache/ kan auch von denen/ so sich in Obseruationibus<br>geübt/ nicht gros geachtet werden. DasA haben wir euch auff ewer schreiben nicht verhalten wöllenA/ vnd so etwas weiters darinnen sein wirdt/ darauff soll euch vnser<br>Mathematicus<br>beantworten. VndA seindt euch mit gnedigem gutem willen jeder zeit gantzA wol gewogen.Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel/ am 11. SeptembrisA, Anno 87.WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the steadfast, Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup. — Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, etc. —</b> Our gracious greeting beforehand, Our trusty and well-beloved. We have received your letter dated the 18th of January together with the <i>observations</i>. That you have now, at our request, sent the same so willingly to us — for this we graciously thank you. We may not, however, conceal from you that at that time we were burdened with other business and had travelled away to other places, whereby we were hindered from answering you. But as soon as we returned hither again, we <i>compared</i> [conferirt] your <i>observations</i> with our own, and found with particular astonishment that they agree with one another <i>in the distances and declinations, even to fractions of a minute</i> — which has never before been heard of. But that they <i>differ</i> [discrepirn] from one another by five minutes <i>in the longitudes</i> causes us no wonder, since we are well aware that neither <i>Hipparchus, Ptolemy</i>, nor any of all the others before [us] ever knew the <i>true places of the stars</i> to 5 minutes, nor <i>on account of the smallness of the armillae</i> could have known [them]; for with the armillae one could scarcely discern 10 minutes — as is plainly [evident] from the <i>observations of the fixed stars</i> — to say nothing of what else, <i>on account of the pulling-apart of the circles</i> [propter Circulorum hinc inde distractionem], may have been wanting in observing, since the <i>circles</i>, in such a connection, soon give way in the handling. Of our own <i>observations</i> we cannot at this time depart, for our <i>mathematician</i>, <i>even with the perpendicular of the quadrant corrected</i>, has repeatedly found [them] exact — not only <i>by the clock</i> [per Horologium], but also this past January several times observing Venus, which he, just as you [did], observed by day alongside the Sun. Yet we questioned our <i>mathematician</i> earnestly about this, and then he could not conceal from us how he had at that time found <i>by Venus</i> that your <i>motion of the Sun</i> [Motus Solis], which you sent us in your <i>ephemeris</i>, <i>around the eighth degree of ♒ Aquarius</i>, is too slow by several minutes; for at the <i>end of January</i> he found the <i>place of the Sun</i> [locum Solis] — also by our quadrant — no otherwise, and accepted it no otherwise, <i>than as it stands in your ephemeris</i> (which he has also <i>duly extended</i> [iusto modo … extendiret] to this year). None the less, Venus would yield no other <i>place of the fixed stars</i>: he supposes that you <i>attribute</i> somewhat too much to the <i>refractions</i>. He supposes also that, since you <i>tracked the time by the equatorial armillae</i> [tempus per Armillas Aequatorias indagirt], whereas the <i>refraction of the Sun</i> occurs <i>in the vertical circle</i> [in Circulo Verticali] and not <i>according to the longitude of the ecliptic</i> [secundum Longitudinem Eclipticae], it could easily be that you took the <i>time</i> somewhat too short — which could not happen if one <i>tracked the time by the azimuths of the Sun</i> [tempus per Azimutha Solis]. Yet, because you tested it <i>by Venus around the autumnal equinox</i> in the morning, he could not properly know wherein it is lacking. However that may be, we know not how to depart from our <i>observations</i>, which we have made with no less the highest diligence than you [made] yours. And since we have now for many years been occupied with the <i>observations</i>, and have gladly furthered <i>astronomy</i> through ourselves [as well], we have graciously commanded our <i>mathematician</i> to <i>calculate</i> the <i>observations</i> further and, together with the <i>demonstrations and the methods of observing</i> [Demonstrationibus et modis obseruandi], to <i>publish</i> [them], so that everyone may see our diligence and labour — as indeed he has already <i>calculated</i> nearly 200 <i>stars</i>, and has more than 200 still to <i>calculate</i>. He is, however, to make no mention of this trifling and slight <i>discrepancy</i> between ours and yours, nor to <i>spread abroad</i> [spargiren] yours. But whatever else you have <i>communicated</i> to us, he will know how to praise <i>according to its worth</i> [pro dignitate] and to <i>render back to you with honourable mention of your name</i> [cum honorifica nominis mentione restituiren]. Thus you will then be the better and more diligently able to <i>inquire into</i> our <i>observations</i>, and the matter will also the sooner find belief with others, since each one keeps to his own <i>observations</i> and does not <i>change</i> anything <i>for another's favour</i> [in gratiam alterius]. We also intend, after the completion of the work, to set up our <i>instruments</i> anew in another fashion; and if we then find that we have erred by the 5 minutes, we shall gladly correct ourselves — although this <i>discrepancy</i> is hardly worth making so much fuss about, and cannot be greatly regarded by those who have practised themselves <i>in observations</i>. This we did not wish to conceal from you upon your letter; and should there be anything further in it, our <i>mathematician</i> shall answer you thereon. And we are at all times with gracious good will quite well disposed toward you. Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on the 11th of September, in the year [15]87. WILHELM, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"Wilhelm the Wise,\" himself a serious observational astronomer who maintained an observatory and instrument workshop at Kassel; with Tycho he carried on a sustained technical correspondence. The full titulature (<i>Graue zu Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain und Nidda</i>) is the standard Hessian style, preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Dem Vesten vnserm lieben besondern Tychoni Brahe, Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — \"To the steadfast (<i>Vesten</i> = <i>Festen</i>, the address-epithet for the lower nobility), Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (Tycho's family seat in Scania). <i>Vnsern gnedigen gruß zuuor</i> and <i>Vester lieber besonder</i> rendered per the fixed glossary (§5.5).</li><li><b>The \"<i>mathematician</i>\" (<i>Mathematicus</i>).</b> Wilhelm's court mathematician is <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (per protocol §5.6), Tycho's principal correspondent at Kassel; the Landgrave speaks throughout in the first person but reports Rothmann's technical findings. Rothmann is named only by office, not by name, in the letter.</li><li><b>The letter being answered.</b> <i>ewer schreiben de Dato den 18. Ianuaris</i> = \"your letter dated 18 January\" — this is the <b>incoming</b> Tycho→Kassel letter (with enclosed observations and ephemeris) that the Landgrave is here answering, <b>not</b> the date of the present letter. <b>No internal contradiction with the dateline:</b> the present letter is dated 11 September [15]87 at its close; the 18 January is a back-reference. The catalogue `label` (\"1587. 11. september. Kassel\") agrees with the dateline, so the header carries 11 September 1587 as written.</li><li><b>Technical terms (kept on the controlled list, §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>distantiae</i> → distances; <i>declinationes</i> → declinations; <i>longitudines</i> → longitudes; <i>vera Stellarum loca / Locum Stellarum Fixarum</i> → true places of the stars / place of the fixed stars; <i>Armillae (Aequatoriae)</i> → (equatorial) armillae; <i>Quadrans / Quadrantis perpendiculo</i> → quadrant / perpendicular of the quadrant; <i>refractio</i> → refraction; <i>Circulus Verticalis</i> → vertical circle; <i>Azimutha</i> → azimuths; <i>Motus Solis</i> / <i>locus Solis</i> → motion / place of the Sun; <i>Ephemeris</i> → ephemeris; <i>Demonstrationes / modi obseruandi</i> → demonstrations / methods of observing. <i>partibus Minuti</i> rendered \"fractions of a minute\" (lit. \"in parts of a minute,\" i.e. to sub-arcminute precision).</li><li><b>The Latin code-switches (a feature, not noise).</b> The letter constantly drops Latin technical phrases into the German matrix — <i>in distantijs et Declinationibus, etiam in partibus Minuti; propter Armillarum paruitatem; etiam correcto Quadrantis perpendiculo; per Horologium; circa octauum gradum; in fine Ianuaris; iusto modo … extendiret; secundum Longitudinem Eclipticae; per Venerem circa Aequinoctium autumnale; pro dignitate; cum honorifica nominis mentione; in gratiam alterius</i> — and likewise Latinate German verbs (<i>conferirt, discrepirn, indagirt, calculiren, publiciren, spargiren, communiciret, restituiren, inquiriren, mutiret, corrigiren</i>). These are rendered into English with the Latin italicised on first/notable occurrence per §2 and §4; the register (a learned prince writing technically) is preserved.</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> <i>circa octauum gradum ♒</i> — \"around the eighth degree of ♒ Aquarius.\" The glyph ♒ is kept in the Source block and named \"Aquarius\" in the translation, per §5.3.</li><li><b>Substance.</b> The exchange is the well-known Kassel–Hven instrument-precision dispute: agreement to sub-arcminute in <i>distances/declinations</i> but a ~5′ disagreement in <i>longitudes</i>. Rothmann's diagnosis — that the error may lie in Tycho's solar-longitude/refraction handling and in deriving <i>time by the equatorial armillae</i> rather than <i>by the Sun's azimuths</i> — and the Landgrave's intent to publish his own star catalogue (≈200 stars computed, &gt;200 to go, with demonstrations and methods) are reported in the principals' own terms; no claim is sharpened.</li><li><b>Editorial markers (\"A\").</b> The transcription carries a stray uppercase <b>\"A\"</b> at numerous points — at the end of the address/titulature lines (<i>KNUDSTRUP.A</i>, <i>ETC.A</i>), mid-phrase (<i>jegen euchA</i>, <i>gewesenA</i>, <i>So baldtA</i>, <i>dannA</i>, <i>vielfeltigA</i>, <i>bey tagkA</i>, <i>verhaltenA</i>, <i>esA</i>, <i>ObseruationibusA</i>, <i>Ais</i>, <i>gnedigklichA</i>, <i>lassenA</i>, <i>werdenA</i>, <i>DasA</i>, <i>wöllenA</i>, <i>VndA</i>, <i>gantzA</i>, <i>SeptembrisA</i>), and at the close where words run together (<i>…wol gewogen.Datum…</i>, <i>…Anno 87.WILHELM…</i>). These are <b>apparatus/segmentation anchors from the source transcription, not part of the text</b>; they are reproduced verbatim in the Source block (per §4's \"reproduce glyphs as printed\") and ignored in the translation. They do not affect sense.</li><li><b>Orthography (Early New High German chancery).</b> <i>zuuor</i> = <i>zuvor</i>; <i>ewer/ewere/ewerigen</i> = <i>euer/eure/eurigen</i>; <i>jhr/jhn</i> = <i>ihr/ihn</i>; <i>vbereinstimmen</i> = <i>übereinstimmen</i>; <i>sintemahl</i> = <i>sintemal</i> (\"since, inasmuch as\"); <i>geschwiegen</i> = \"to say nothing of\"; <i>baldt</i> = <i>bald</i>; <i>iust</i> = \"exactly, just\"; <i>as 200</i> = <i>als 200</i> (\"than 200\"); <i>disse</i> = <i>diese</i>; <i>Vestung</i> = <i>Festung</i> (\"fortress\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum … Cassel … am 11. Septembris, Anno 87</i> = \"Given … [at] <b>Kassel</b> … on 11 September, the year [15]87\" (O.S.; Protestant Hesse kept Old Style). <i>Datum</i> = \"Given\" (the chancery dating verb; cf. <i>Actum</i>, §5.5).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected: 'we cannot at this time proceed [to write] ... found [it] exact' now reads 'we cannot at this time depart ... found [them] exact', matching the letter's own parallel idiom later in the text ('we know not how to depart from our observations'). No Dreyer slice exists for this letter (Wilhelm IV to Tycho, not one of Tycho's own letters in TBOO); the fix rests on internal textual evidence, not edition collation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German and its Latin code-switches are rendered securely and the date back-reference is not a contradiction. The one soft point is the exact technical force of <i>propter Circulorum hinc inde distractionem … in solcher Connexion im tractiren sich baldt geben</i> (\"the pulling-apart of the circles … the circles in such a connection soon give way in the handling\"), a concrete remark about armillary-ring flexure/play under use; rendered literally and flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (one of the 49 letters without a Dreyer slice); audited published_english against source_text alone, clause-by-clause, including all titles, the five-minute/ten-minute longitude-discrepancy figures, the Venus-by-day verification method, the Aquarius degree, the 200-star calculation tally, and the closing date. One MISTRANSLATION found (detailed above), identified by internal contradiction with a later, correctly-translated occurrence of the same German idiom in the same letter -- not a Dreyer-collation-dependent finding. Per the brief, any MISTRANSLATION that changes what a scholar would take away caps the grade at C regardless of collation status.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). \"[von etwas] schreitten\" here means to depart from / renounce, not \"to proceed [in writing]\" about it; the very same idiom recurs later in this letter ('wissen wir nicht von vnsern Obseruationibus ... abzutretten') and is there correctly rendered 'we know not how to depart from our observations'. The causal 'dann' clause that follows (the mathematician found them exact) only makes sense as a reason to stand by the observations, not as a reason to delay writing about them. The mistranslation reverses Wilhelm's rhetorical stance in this sentence, from confidently defending his own data to sounding unprepared to discuss it. At: “Of our own observations we cannot at this time proceed [to write], for our mathematician, even with the perpendicular of”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was available for this letter (tboo: null), so the TEXT_ERROR-verification path does not apply; this is a MISTRANSLATION defect concerning the German source, resolved from the letter's own internal evidence. The idiom 'von [X] schreitten' (depart from X) is confirmed by its exact structural parallel later in the same letter, 'von vnsern Observationibus ... abzutretten', which the existing translation already correctly renders as 'depart from'. The surrounding causal logic (the mathematician's finding the observations exact as grounds to stand by them, not as grounds to delay discussing them) corroborates the correction. Nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15870101001", "next": "brahe-t_15880816001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870101001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15880816001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for his observations and reports close agreement with Kassel's in distances and declinations to fractions of a minute, though a five-minute discrepancy in longitudes remains.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870911001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15870924001", "d": "1587-09-24", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "ck": "Frederik 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Frederik II → Tycho Brahe — Holm (Copenhagen), 24 September 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Danske Kancellis uindførte koncepter til skånske missiver (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THYGE BRAHE ETC.</p><p>WOR gunst tillforn. Wiid att som thu Oß haffuer fortrøstit om en almanach paa thette thillstandendis 88 Aar att wille giøre och Oß forskaffe nogit betiidliger end thu pleyer then Oß att lade tillkomme, thilligeste ochsaa haffuer giort Oß tilsagn om thend almanach, som thu therhoes pleyer Oß att giøre, att wille Oß forskaffe. Tha bede Wij thig och begere, attu samme almanacher, thersom the ere forferdigett, mett thenne breffuißer wor dreng wille Oß tilsende. Eller och,thersom the icke endnu ere beende,tha mett thet første muligt er wille them forferdige, och Oß tilbiude, nar Wij them mue were foruenthendis. Thet scheer Oß till wilge.</p><p>Befallendis<br>Actum Holm, 24. septemb. a:o 87.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe, etc.</b></p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, whereas you have given us your assurance regarding an almanac for this coming 88th year [1588] — that you would make and procure it for us somewhat more in good time than you are wont to let it reach us — and have likewise also given us your promise concerning the [other] almanac which, along with it, you are wont to make for us, that you would procure it for us: we therefore ask and desire you that you would send us those same almanacs, if they are finished, by this bearer, our servant-lad. Or else, if they are not yet ready, that you would finish them at the very first that is possible, and notify us when we may expect them. This [you do] to our pleasure.</p><p>Commending [you to God],<br>Done at Holm, 24 September, in the year [15]87.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), Tycho's royal patron, who in 1576 granted him Hven. As in the 1584 compass letter, the king treats Tycho as a personal supplier — here of the annual almanacs/calendrical predictions, a recurring obligation Tycho discharged from Uraniborg.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tyge Brahe</b> (Tycho), addressed in the Danish vernacular form <i>Thyge</i>.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery <i>Kladde</i> (draft / unentered concept), among the <i>Danske Kancellis uindførte koncepter til skånske missiver</i> — i.e. a draft missive of the Danish Chancery for the Scanian (<i>skånske</i>) correspondence. There is no Latin register rubric here (unlike the 1584 letter); the opening <i>THYGE BRAHE ETC.</i> is the chancery's own address-line shorthand.</li><li><b><i>almanach / almanacher</b></i> — \"almanac(s).\" The king refers to <b>two</b> items: the <i>almanac</i> for the coming year 1588, and a second <i>almanac</i> that Tycho customarily made \"along with it\" (<i>therhoes</i>). These are the prognosticatory wall-/calendar-almanacs (with weather and astrological notes) that the Danish court expected annually from its astronomer; rendered \"almanac\" and the duo preserved (note the plural <i>almanacher</i> \"those same almanacs\").</li><li><b>In-letter date reference (NOT a dateline discrepancy).</b> The body asks for the almanac <i>paa thette thillstandendis 88 Aar</i> — \"for this coming 88th year,\" i.e. <b>1588</b> — while the letter itself is dated <b>1587</b> (<i>a:o 87</i>). This is a forward reference (an almanac for next year, requested this year), not a conflict; flagged per protocol §3 rather than reconciled. The catalogue <i>label</i> (\"Holm\") names the place, not a date, and agrees with the dateline.</li><li><b>Negation.</b> The body contains a single negation — <i>thersom the icke endnu ere beende</i> \"if they are <b>not</b> yet ready\" — reproduced once, with no other negation added or dropped. The two <i>thersom</i> (\"if\") clauses set up the alternative the king offers: <i>if</i> finished → send them now; <i>or else, if</i> not yet ready → finish them as soon as possible and report when.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1587).</b> <i>WOR gunst tillforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (imperative \"know\"); <i>som</i> here introduces the recital (\"whereas / inasmuch as\"); <i>thu</i> = <i>du</i>; <i>Oß</i> = <i>os</i>; <i>haffuer</i> = <i>haver/har</i>; <i>fortrøstit</i> = <i>fortrøstet</i> (\"assured, given confident promise\"); <i>thillstandendis</i> = <i>tilstundende</i> (\"coming, approaching\"); <i>betiidliger</i> = <i>betideligere</i> (\"more in good time, earlier\"); <i>pleyer</i> = <i>plejer</i> (\"are wont to\"); <i>tillkomme</i> = <i>tilkomme</i> (\"reach, come to\"); <i>thilligeste</i> = <i>tillige(st)</i> (\"likewise\"); <i>tilsagn</i> (\"promise, undertaking\"); <i>therhoes</i> = <i>derhos</i> (\"therewith, along with it\"); <i>attu</i> = <i>at du</i>; <i>thenne breffuißer</i> = <i>denne brevviser</i> (\"this bearer-of-the-letter\"); <i>wor dreng</i> = <i>vor dreng</i> (\"our servant-lad / boy\"); <i>forferdigett / forferdige</i> = <i>forfærdiget / forfærdige</i> (\"finished / to finish, complete\"); <i>beende</i> = <i>b-ende</i>, i.e. <i>berede(de)/beredte</i> (\"ready, prepared\"); <i>tilbiude</i> here = <i>tilbyde</i> in the sense \"give notice to, advise\" (let us know); <i>foruenthendis</i> = <i>forventendes</i> (\"to be expected, awaiting\"); <i>Thet scheer Oß till wilge</i> = <i>Det sker os til vilje</i> (\"this is done to our pleasure / will\"); <i>Befallendis</i> = <i>Befalendes</i> (\"commending [you, sc. to God],\" the chancery valediction). The text's German-influenced spelling (<i>w-, sch-, -ck, Oß</i>) is the chancery hand, not the German language — verified Danish by reading.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Holm, 24. septemb. a:o 87</i> = \"Done at <b>Holm</b>, 24 September [15]87\" (O.S.). <i>Holm</i> = <b>Bremerholm / Holmen</b>, the royal naval dockyard and adjoining royal precinct in Copenhagen, a frequent place of issue for Frederik II's chancery. <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the negation count preserved; the one soft point is the exact form behind <i>beende</i> (read as <i>beredte</i> \"ready/prepared,\" the sense the parallel \"if finished … or else if not yet ready\" requires), flagged here rather than silently emended.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole point is the orthographic reading </i>beende<i>=beredte, forced by the if/or-else logic; '88 Aar' is a forward reference, not a conflict.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.37-38 (page images). The royal letter (Danish) is printed complete on p.37 as document '58', headed 'THYGE BRAHE ETC.', dated 'Actum Holm 24. septemb. a:o 87'. Collated the Danish text word-for-word against source_text (exact match) and checked the year figures (88th-year almanac request, 1587 date) and place-name (Holm) against the translation; both correctly rendered with the '[1588]' and '[15]87' brackets appropriately marking editorial supplements.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Danske Kancellis uindførte koncepter til skånske missiver. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Frederik II, King of Denmark", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15850526001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Requests the almanacs for 1588 that Tycho had promised, asking him to send them by the current messenger if ready or complete them as soon as possible.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15870924001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15871011001", "d": "1587-10-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 21 September 1587 [O.S.] (catalogued 11 October 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTVTE SAPIENTIA INGENIOSA ERVDITAQVE DOCTRINA, CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNVDSTRVP, EQVITI DANO, MATHEMATICORUM NOSTRO SECULO OMNIUM PRAeSTANTISSIMO, ETC. DOMINO SUO CUM OBSERUANTIA COLENDO, ETC. S. P.</p><p>RESPONDISSEM in superioribus nundinis Francofordianis ad literas tuas, Vir Nobiliss. et Mathematicorum omnium praestantissime, nisi ea, de quibus in posterioribus Literis scribis, mihi fuissent impedimento; aut nisi in Aula vanissimis saepe rebus tempus vanissime esset transigendum. Verum moram illam maiori iam copia compensabo. De Cometa igitur vt primo loco adhuc paucis tecum agam, non leui tu quidem argumento aberis, consentaneum fuisse, vt Cometa aliquam, licet vix sensibilem, habuerit Parallaxin, cum Iupiter et Saturnus etiam Parallaxin habeant. Scio Hypotheses Coperniceas id omnino flagitare. Verum quomodocunque examinem meas Obseruationes, nullam tamen sensibilem inuenire possum. Potuissem eodem insensibilis Parallaxeos argumento vti ad confirmandam meam sententiam, qua ipsum Sphaerae Saturni attribui; cum Saturni Parallaxis nostros sensus facile effugiat, vixque tertiam Minuti partem attingat. Verum nolui incurrere in reprehensionem eorum, qui cum nihil de arte Mathematica intelligant, imperite tamen et temerarie plane clamitare non desinunt, nos absurdissima quaeque asserere. Sed quoniam te mearum Obseruationum Aristarchum habeo, nihil posthac id genus hominum curabo, sed forti et praesenti animo ea in medium producam, quae et per Obseruationes et per certissimas Geometriae demonstrationes vera inuenio. Quod autem miraris, quomodo tunc maximam Solis obliquitatem 23. Grad. 30. Min. assumserim, nec eandem tecum 23. Gr. 31⅓. M. ex Obseruationibus Illustriss. Principis nostri colligere potuerim: in eo facile veniam mereor. Vidi enim Obseruationes Illustrissimi Principis nostri praebere modo Altitudinem Solis maximam Solstitialem 62. Grad. 10. Min. modo 62. Gr. 12. Min. etiam eodem Quadrante: Vnde facile patuit, minori cura eas fuisse institutas, quam magnitudo negotij postulabat. Praetereâ nondum mihi constabat vera nostri Poli Eleuatio, quam scis quantis laboribus tandem indagârim. Assumsi igitur eam Obliquitatem, quam tunc temporis veritati quam proxime consentaneam esse conijciebam, licet exactam et praecisam ex Obseruationibus nescirem. Inuenta autem vera Poli nostri Eleuatione, facile animaduerti, assignatam a te Zodiaci Obliquitatem ad Obseruationes Illustrissimi Principis nostri quam proxime accedere. Verum quod tunc temporis a te discere mihi necesse fuit, id iam ipse verissimum esse diligentissimis meis Obseruationibus adinueni. Inueni enim in proximo Solstitio Aestiuo maximam Solis Altitudinem meridianam exacte 62. Gr. 11 M. diligentissime. Vnde addita Parallaxi Solis et assumpta vera nostra Aequatoris Eleuatione part. 38. M. 41. tua Zodiaci Obliquitas resultat. In Solstitio hyemali datur hîc maxima Solis altitudo meridiana part. 15. M. 14. Verum tunc temporis Aër hîc longe densior et vaporosior existit, quam alijs anni temporibus, Refractioque Solis circa Brumam longe maior est quam Sole circa medium ♏ aut ♒ versante. Haec de Cometa deque intermixta Zodiaci Obliquitate. Et quoniam video, te residuum scripti mei de Cometa desiderare, eius adhuc tria capita ad te mitto, ex quibus quae mea sit de Cometis sententia, qualesque Hypotheses Astronomicas veras esse iudicem, facile intelliges. Egi de ijs rebus aliquo modo durius; quoniam duro ligno durus adhibendus est cuneus; nec hoc genus hominum prae anteceptis authoritatibus et prae stulta Artis Mathematicae ignorantia a suis nugis ineptissimis facile discedit. Deest adhuc caput de significatione Cometarum, imprimis commendationi Astronomiae destinatum; quod licet animo conceperim, propter alia tamen negotia ab Illustrissimo Principe nostro mihi demandata, calamo excipere non potui. Accedet tunc, cum scriptum illud ad incudem reuocauero. Nunc de materia Sphaerarum caelestium paulo plenius tecum agam. Recte tu quidem mecum statuis, materiam Sphaerarum caelestium, non esse duram aut imperuiam, sed liquidam et subtilem, motuique Planetarum facile cedentem. At in eo dissentimus, quod tu propter Refractiones diuersa Aetheris et Aëris Diaphana statuis h. e. in Sphaeris caelestibus non contineri Aërem Elementarem sed potius Aethera liquidissimum ab Aëre Elementari diuersum. At argumentum Refractionis, quod pro te militare existimas, hanc tuam sententiam prorsus expugnat et euertit. Si enim Refractio illa, quam per Obseruationes inuenimus, est a diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Elementaris Diaphano: fieri non potest, quin duret vsque ad verticem. Pone enim, circa Horizontem eam Refractionem esse vix trium Minutorum: Erit nihilominus in Altitudine 45. G. circiter 1½. M. cum Triangulorum mutatio et ratio minorem vix admittant. Etsi enim pro confirmatione tuae sententiae asseras, non adeo magnam esse Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris differentiam, sed quae successiue, vbi se inuicem contingunt, minuatur ita, vt suprema Aëris portio, Lunae Reuolutotionibus contigua, fere nihil differat ab ipso Aethere: Eo tamen ipso, quo asseris, Refractionem esse ob diuersa Aetheris et Aëris Diaphana prouenientem, eo inquam ipso concedis, Radios Solis aut Stellarum punctum sibi nihilominus in ea diuersorum Diaphanorum successiua combinatione et assimilatione eligere, in quo refrangantur: idque necessario semper eandem a centro Terrae distantiam retinebit, propterea quod successiua illa diuersorum Diaphanorum combinatio vbique aequaliter a Terra absit. Assumto itaque hoc Refractionis puncto vbique aequaliter a Terra distante, vide num Triangulorum rationes Refractionem illam in Altitudine 50. aut 60. Graduum euanescere permittant. Vides igitur Refractionum euanescentiam diuersa Aetheris et Aëris Diaphana concedere nullo modo posse. Quae itaque mea sit de Refractionibus his sententia, quoniam fortassis in Cometa non satis explicate a me proposita est, explicatius iam tibi aperiam. Inter Terram, vt scis, et inter Sphaeram Stellarum Fixarum nihil aliud contineri statuo quam Aërem septem Errantia sidera ambientem, propterea quod Refractiones non durent vsque ad verticem, sed vix, vt tu vis, vsque ad 30. Gradus. Hunc Aërem deinde Natura ipsa me sic docente, distinguo in crassiorem, et exhalationibus terrenis obnoxium, et in purum, qui exhalationibus istis non inficiatur. Huiusque meae distinctionis fundamentum sunt similiter Principia optica. Notum enim tibi est ex Alhazeno, ex Vitellione, ex Petro Nonio de Crepusculis, atque etiam oculorum testimonio, esse certum Aëris spatium, quod a radijs Solaribus in Crepusculis illuminetur et albescat; esse item vltra illud spatium Aërem, qui a radijs Solaribus non possit illuminari et albescere; Aliâs enim essent perpetua Crepuscula. Necesse igitur est, vt Aër ille, qui etiam serenissimo Caelo in Crepusculis albescit, sit a terrenis exhalationibus infectus et crassior redditus; aliâs enim radij Solares illum illuminare non possent; ille vero, qui non albescit, sit purus et a terrenis vaporibus non infectus: Quanquam non negauero, etiam in purum illum Aërem exhalationes, praesertim puriores posse interdum ascendere. Nec tamen hunc Aërem crassum per se Refractionem causare posse inuenio, sed ob multiplicationem. Quanquam enim spatium illud Aëris crassioris fere Semidiametrum Terrae a superficie Terrae attingat, vt ex dictis Opticis de Crepusculis manifestum est: Radios tamen Solis aut Stellarum vincere crassitiem hanc, nec ob identitatem Diaphanorum in ea punctum Refractionis inuenire posse animaduerto; at circa Horizontem et in situ decliuiori ob duplicationem illius spatij posse.</p><p>[Figura] Assumamus enim melioris demonstrationis gratia, superficiem extremam Aëris crassioris distare a superficie Terrae per Semidiametrum Terrae: denotetque superficiem Terrae semicirculus BCD interuallo AB descriptus, et interuallo duplo describatur superficies extrema crassioris Aëris EFG. Describatur etiam extremus Circulus ex eodem centro, in quo feratur Stella aliqua, qui sit HIL, tanto interuallo, vt nullam fere sensibilem proportionem habeat ad AC. Assumatur etiam Stella in Aequatore forte constituta, aut in alio loco, in quo ad Meridianum perueniens habeat Altitudinem maiorem part. 30: nullamque amplius Refractionem sentiat. Dico igitur, punctum in quo sit Refractio, longius a superficie extrema Aëris crassioris EFG distare, quam distat extrema superficies Aëris crassioris a superficie Terrae, HE quam est CF vel BE. Oriatur enim Stella in H. Illis igitur qui in B in simili loci Latitudine habitant, Stella erit in Meridiano, aut in tanta Altitudine constituta, vt nullam eius Refractionem sentire possint. Cum igitur nos in C constituti Refractionem tunc maximam sentiamus, necesse est, vt punctum Refractionis sit inter B et C puncta, ac etiam in longiore distantia ab EFG superficie, quam est EB. Aliâs enim illi, qui in B habitarent, Refractionem similiter sentirent. Constat igitur quod propositum erat, nimirum spatium illud Aëris crassioris per se non esse tantum, vt frangere Radios possit, sed vim illam accipere a conduplicatione sua circa Horizontem. Quod vt adhuc magis pateat, ostendam nunc, quantum punctum Refractionis distet ab extrema superficie Aëris crassioris HE, quanto spatio radij Stellarum crassum illum Aërem irrefracti penetrent: ad quam rem assumam, vt tu vis, Refractionem illam durare vsque ad tricesimum ab Horizonte gradum. Nec enim id, vt arbitraris, meam sententiam euertit. Cogitetur igitur, vt antea dixi, AC nullam fere sensibilem proportionem, quae turbare praesens negotium possit, habere ad AH, ita vt cum connectantur HC puncta, angulus FCH perinde fere rectus sit ac si esset angulus CAH: quomodo Instrumentis in obseruando tanquam in centro mundi vti in Stellis fixis solemus. Iam abscindatur arcus HK, qui sit P. 30; et connectantur puncta K et C per rectam, quae secet superficiem extremam Aëris crassioris in O puncto: ponaturque similiter obseruator in S, cui K punctum distet ab Horizonte 29. gr. Quoniam igitur is, qui in C, nullam amplius inuenit Refractionem, ex Hypothesi: necesse est, vt nullum etiam Refractionis punctum sit inter K et C. At is, qui in S, sentit adhuc Refractionem puncti K, cum ei K sit in Altitudine 29. graduum, itidem ex Hypothesi. Cum igitur punctum Refractionis ei, qui in C, iam iam desierit; ei vero, qui in S, adhuc appareat; necesse est vt illud sit in vertice C. Quare manifestum est, lineam OC determinare quam proxime spatium, quo punctum Refractionis distat ab extrema superficie Aëris crassioris, et quam longe Radij crassum illum Aërem absque Refractione penetrare possunt. Dum igitur Stella est in Horizonte in puncto H, erit punctum Refractionis in N: dum in Q, erit punctum illud in R: semperque magis ac magis ad C accedet: vt ita in Horizonte a C puncto omnium longissime absit. Ex quo per 14. P. lib. 10. Vitellionis tanquam πόρισμα sequitur illud, quod in Obseruationibus verum reperitur: nimirum Refractiones circa Horizontem esse omnium maximas, posteaque semper magis ac magis diminui, donec puncto Refractionis in C constituto prorsus euanescunt. Quanquam id etiam esse possit a diuersa Aëris crassitie. Quo enim Aër propius ad Terram accedit, eo crassior et densior a perpetuis fumis et vaporibus redditur. Ex quibus omnibus manifestissimum, nec diuersa Aetheris et Aëris Diaphana esse, nec Refractiones aliunde, quam a crasso et vaporoso Aëre causari posse. Quin hoc ipsum, si nihil aliud, certe diuersae in diuersis locis Refractiones ostenderent. Verissimum enim est, hîc non tantas esse Refractiones, quantae sunt apud vos. Cum enim tu inueneris Stellarum Fixarum Refractionem in Altitudine 18. grad. esse apud vos duorum Minutorum: ipse ex Stellis Vrsae Maioris et alijs in eadem Altitudine eam hîc non maiorem inuenio quam vnius Minuti, adeo vt in Altitudine 23. Grad. vix quarta Minuti pars refrangatur. Solem quoque Caelo clarissimo et Radio forti inueni in Altitudine trium Graduum, cum abesset a visibili Horizonte nondum vnico Gradu, habere Refractionem vix 9⅓. Minutorum, seu addita Parallaxi, Minutorum 12⅓: quam tamen tu 28. Minutorum inuenisti. Quanquam Refractiones Solis pro diuersa Aëris constitutione manifeste mutentur, adeo vt in Bruma, vbi Aër hic nunquam fuliginibus et densa caligine caret, eam in Altitudine 15. Grad. repererim 7. Minutorum, habita scilicet Parallaxeos ratione. Vnde recte tu asseris, omnino serenum et purum Aërem ad negotium Refractionum rite tractandum requiri. Vides igitur manifeste, Refractiones eas non aliunde nisi ab Aëre crasso et denso esse, veramque esse Veterum sententiam, qua dixêre, Aërem in locis Borealioribus esse densiorem et crassiorem, quam in Australioribus. An vero extrema crassi Aëris superficies in locis Borealioribus longius a Terra absit, quam in Australioribus, valde vereor. De quo enim Nonius parte 2. P. 1. de Crepusculis dubitare videtur, nimirum Crepusculum terminari in alijs locis Sole 17. Grad. sub Horizonte latente, in alijs part. 18. in alijs part. 19: de eo mihi dubitandum non videtur, praesertim cum idem quoque Ptolemaeus in fine lib. 8. μεγαλ. συντάξ. asserat. Sed nostrum est, vt momenta temporum, quamprimum Aër albescere vesperi desinat aut mane incipiat, diligenter notemus, atque ex nostrarum Obseruationum collatione litem hanc dirimamus. Haec de arduo hoc et sublimi negotio paulo copiosius ad te perscribere volui: atque vt ea diligenter examines, deque ijs mecum conferre ne desinas, a te etiam atque etiam vehementissime peto. Nunc ad alia. De Latitudinibus Stellarum secundum mutationem Obliquitatis Zodiaci proportionaliter mutatis, eruditissime disseris. Nec enim alia via procedi dextre in eius rei confirmatione potuit, quam qua tu processisti: Propterea quod loca Stellarum Fixarum admodum perperam in Tabulis posita sint, et, vt autumo, nunquam a Ptolemaeo obseruata, sed tantum ex Hipparcho transcripta. Apparet ex multis locis Almagesti, et praecipue ex adducto cap. 3. lib. 7. Ptolemaeum minus curiose et nimis oscitanter negotium Stellarum Fixarum tractasse. Dum Vitebergae adhuc versarer, memini me simili modo examinare loca Stellarum in Canone Ptolemaico posita, cumque assumerem Longitudinem et Declinationem Reguli, prout a Ptolemaeo traduntur, prodijt Latitudo Reguli 6⅔. Min. non Borea, vt in Canone annotatur, sed Australis. Haec cum manifeste contraria sint, nec ab vllo possint conciliari: necesse est, vt vnum eorum sit falsum. At cum Regulus sit vnica Stella, cuius Obseruationem Ptolemaeus recitet, nec nostrae Obseruationes Longitudinem et Latitudinem a Ptolemaeo inuentas, admodum respuant: necessario Declinatio adducta erit falsissima. Sed cum Declinationis Ptolemaicae decrementum videatur nihilominus respondere decremento Declinationum Timocharidos et Hipparchi, ita vt a Timochare vsque ad Hipparchum Declinatio decreuerit M. 40. ab Hipparcho ad Ptolemaeum M. 50: oportebat enim ratione temporis decrementum in secundo interuallo esse maius decremento primi interualli: manifestum inde est, Ptolemaeum nunquam Declinationes Stellarum Fixarum obseruasse, sed tantum ex Declinationibus Timocharidis et Hipparchi secundum proportionem temporis finxisse, praesertim cum vt plurimum falsae, nec progressui Stellarum rite respondere inueniantur, vt ipse calculo tuo reperisti. Nec sane video, quo Instrumento eas obseruarit. Instrumentum enim parallaticum ad Lunam tantum comparatum erat, nec foramina visoriorum erant aequalia, sed superius ad Lunam erat maius, ita vt per id tota Luna perspici posset; inferius autem erat minus. Armillis quoque eas non obseruauit; siquidem Declinatio eius puncti Eclipticae, in quo tunc haesit Regulus, maior erat ipsius Reguli Declinatione a Ptolemaeo tradita. Nihil igitur nostrae sententiae de mutata Stellarum Latitudine detrahere potest Latitudo Ptolemaica Sirij, quae, cum nostra hac maior esse debebat, minor tamen reperiatur. Licet enim Declinationes Timocharidis, Hipparchi, et ipsius etiam Ptolemaei quodammodo subscribant Latitudini in Abaco Ptolemaico positae: nihil tamen ijs ob dictas causas tribuendum est. Hoc enim potius casu quam obseruatione et arte accidit. Cum Graecum textum considero, vereor ne vitiosum Hipparchi exemplar ipsi Ptolemaeo obrepserit. Potuit enim Declinatio a Timocharide tradita esse ις. ςγ. hoc est part. 16. M. 50; ab Hipparcho ις. ς. hoc est part. 16. M. 30; ac incuria transcribentium, qui putârunt ςς. frustra geminatum, ς. potuit omitti: ad quas vitiosas Declinationes Ptolemaeus deinde etiam suam fortassis direxit. Latitudo quoque Sirij in Abaco Ptolemaico posita facile transcribentium incuria vitiari potuit; cum idem acciderit non tantum in prope posita Lucida Hydrae (cuius Latitudo hoc nostro seculo duobus pene Gradibus maior Ptolemaica reperitur, cum tamen debebat esse minor) verumetiam in multis alijs, quarum Latitudo valde insigniter vitiosa deprehenditur. Sed quid de hac vnica Stella multa verba facimus, cum huius vnius votum vota reliquarum omnium facile supprimant. Monenda est posteritas, vt posthac hanc rem diligentius tractet. Ac de his fortasse plus quam sat erat. In deliquio Lunari Anni 1584. non iustam esse diligentiam et praecisionem a Wittichio adhibitam, recte asseris. Assumsit enim is locum Solis longe aliter quam per Obseruationes debuit; Dextri item humeri Orionis, per quem tempus indagauit, et Declinationem et Ascensionem rectam vitiosam. Quod cum viderem, non immerito de negligentia ipsius in prioribus literis sum conquestus. Sed vt ipse videas, qua diligentia et perspicacitate vsus fuerit, totam Obseruationis annotationem, vt eam reliquit, iam subijciam, estque ea eiusmodi.</p><p>ANNO CHRISTI 1584. D. 7. NOUEMBRIS OBSERUATUM EST CASSELLIS PLENILUNIUM ECLIPTICUM, CUIUS INCIDIT TEMPUS<br>Primae obscurationis in H. 11. M. 20. P. M.<br>Totalis obscurationis in H. 12. M. 20.<br>Primae emersionis in H. 13. M. 45.<br>Totalis emersionis in H. 14. M. 45.<br>Correcto vtroque Horologio per Azimutha Dextri Humeri Orionis.<br>Sole per part. 25½. ♏ gradiente, ex Obseruatione praecedente.<br>Hinc media obscuratio H. 13. M. 3. Luna in P. 25. M. 30. ♉ cum Latitudine P. 0. M. 10. Merid.<br>Mora in tenebris Horarum 2. M. 25.<br>Integra duratio Horarum 3. M. 25.<br>Dextri Humeri Orionis Ascensio recta P. 83. M. 31. Declinatio P. 7. M. 9. Ex Obseruationibus Anni 1584.</p><p>Ex his vides, quam temere affirmârit, se assumsisse et locum Solis et Ascensionem rectam Dextri Humeri Orionis, Declinationemque ex Obseruationibus: cum nihil horum per Obseruationes verum reperiatur. Sed nolo in demortuum plura dicere. Si assumseris tuum locum Solis, et veram Dextri Humeri Orionis Ascensionem rectam, veramque Declinationem, provt nostris Obseruationibus inueniuntur; videbis annotata hîc temporum momenta propius ad tua accedere. Eclipsin huius mensis ob pluuias, quae a 6. Septemb. quatuor integros dies continue hîc durârunt, obseruare non potui, quanquam me diligenter praeparâssem. Admodum inconstans et pluuiosum Caelum hoc Anno hîc Cassellis fuit, adeo vt paucas admodum Obseruationes instituere potuerim: Percuperem scire, an idem etiam in alijs locis fuisset. Planetas obseruare Illustrissimus Princeps mihi non permittit, cuius mandato tantum Stellas Fixas obseruare, indeque vera earum loca Calculo indagare cogor. Vult enim ipsius Celsitud. vt Obseruationes nostras publicas faciam, quotidieque calculum earum vrget. Vult posteritati monumentum sui erga Astronomiam amoris relinquere: cui clementi voluntati mihi parendum est. Cedet opus hoc maxime in tuam laudem, nec sis solicitus de Instrumentis. Ea enim cum honorifica tui nominis praedicatione tibi restituam, nec praetergrediar reliqua, de quibus ad nos perscripsisti. Assumsi pro fundamento, vt superioribus quoque literis significaui, Aldeborae Ascensionem rectam part. 63. M. 10. quam etiam hoc ipso Anno per Venerem diurnam veram inueni. Quod autem ob id quinque Minutis in Longitudine vbique fere differamus a tuis Obseruationibus, neminem, qui modo subtilitatem huius negotij intelligit, offendet: quin potius eo maiorem fidem apud posteritatem merebimur. Potest Veneris Diameter hanc differentiam introducere. Nihil tamen de hac in Opere nostro agam, vt tibi integra potestas relinquatur, diligentius et pluribus Obseruationibus hanc rem examinandi. Nec te offendet, quod licet videamus, te omni humana diligentia in tuis Obseruationibus vsum, nihilominus tamen a nostris, quas oculis nostris vidimus, non discedamus: Nec enim id veritatis amantes decet: nec nosmet alienis plumis ornare volumus. Obseruationes tuas huc transmissas diligentissime custodimus, nec alijs inspiciendas praebemus: nec publicationem earum metues. Tibi regnum, tibi restaurationem Astronomiae relinquimus; ac gaudemus, esse, qui ea in re praestare possit id, quod antehac praestitit nemo. Quod si possum tibi quocunque modo esse adiumento, in eo omne meum studium, diligentiam, industriam, fidem sancte tibi polliceor ac defero. Motum Solis a te huc transmissum inuenio exactissime cum nostris Obseruationibus congruere; praeterquam circa primos Gradus ♒, vbi eum aliquantulum iusto tardiorem per Venerem reperi: quod tamen ipsum te quoque animaduertisse in prioribus literis arguis. Accepi in superioribus Nundinis Francofortensibus a M. IOANNE AALBORGA Carmina quaedam a te ad Nobilissimos quosque viros perscripta: quae cum auidissime perlegerem, incidi tandem in id, quod ad D. FALCHONEM GOYE miseras, atque ab altera paginae parte inueni binas imagines, alteram Astronomiae, alteram eius Artis, quam Spagyricam appellas. Quas cum diligenter intuebar, animaduerti ab vtroque earum latere literas quasdam, circa Astronomiam quidem, SVSPICIENDO DESPICIO: circa reliquam vero, DESPICIENDO SVSPICIO. Diu haec cum admiratione etiam atque etiam considerans, cogitaui tandem Hieroglyphica esse, teque ijs forte annuere, Artem Spagyricam continere totius Naturae et Mundi contemplationem, et perinde, ac Astronomia, suos habere Planetas, ac quae Natura agit per Sidera caelestia, ea agere simili efficacia per sua Sidera. Hoc si ita est, perquam scire cupio, quid in Arte Spagyrica appelletis Solem, quid Lunam, quid Saturnum, Iouem, Martem, Venerem, Mercurium, quid Elementa. Video abstrusissimam esse Artem, Rerumque Naturam vix sine ea dextre intelligi posse; simileque quid tenuisse Sacerdotes Aegyptiacos. Quo si a te obtinere possem, vt de eo me redderes certiorem, aliaque huc pertinentia adijceres, et qui Autores de hac Arte legendi: gratius mihi accidere nihil omnino posset.</p><p>Quod me tam humaniter inuitas, vt te inuisere non intermittam, gratias tibi quas possum ago. Haud ita dudum aiebat Illustrissimus Princeps noster, sibi placere, vt nostras Stellarum Fixarum Obseruationes dedicarem Serenissimo Daniae Regi. Si in ea sententia perseuerauerit, potero hac occasione ad te venire: sin minus, conscripsi Vitebergae non sine multis vigilijs Organon Mathematicum, quod continet Logisticam Sexagenariam, Doctrinam sinuum, Doctrinam de Compositione rationum seu de Regula sex quantitatum, et Doctrinam Triangulorum tam Sphaericorum quam Planorum, perspicue et breuiter multis et ingeniosis compendijs refertum, mihique charissimum: id Regi vestro Serenissimo dedicabo, ne mihi occasio ad te veniendi desit. Conscripsi ibidem etiam Astronomiam, in qua Hypotheses Copernici traduxi ad mobilitatem Solis (quod fieri posse non ignoras) singulaque Theoricarum Schemata calculo Prutenico declaraui. Verum cum per Obseruationes inuenerim, Tabulas Prutenicas valde insigniter a veritate Caeli abesse; publicationem eius huc vsque distuli, ne vitiosum calculum pro vero discentibus viderer commendare. Si igitur tibi placeret, sique Canones tuos Astronomicos in Lucem emitteres; Loco calculi Prutenici tuum substituerem, vt tuus calculus discentibus redderetur familiaris. Mira breuitate per leges Logicas dissectis Heterogeneis multo plura in eam conieci, quam continent vulgares Sphaerici et Theorici libelli. Impulsus sum ad eam rem acerbissimo dolore, quod non sine summa indignatione viderem, in Academijs, vbi inprimis florere debebant haec studia, nihil fere de hac Arte intelligi: Calculum quidem Prutenicum in omnium esse manibus, at tantum secundum mera Prutenica praecepta tractari, nec inueniri, qui Hypotheses eius intelligant, ne quidem inter eos, qui Astronomiam profiteantur ac se summos Mathematicos putent. Motum praecessionis Aequinoctiorum et mutatae Obliquitatis Zodiaci, cuius Hypotheses neminem adhuc vidi dextre intelligere, nec te multos vidisse arbitror, summa facilitate et perspicuitate tractaui: qui tamen aliter tractandus iam est, cum Stellae suas mutasse Latitudines inueniantur. De diebus quoque tam artificialibus quam naturalibus, deque temporis aequatione ex ipso fundamento non vulgaria proposui. Orbes illos reales et tot axes, obscurantes potius Astronomiam quam explicantes, prorsus sustuli. In Theorica Solis et Lunae vnam et eandem Circulorum suppositionem assumsi, nimirum Eccentricum, cuius centrum in circello supra centrum Mundi in linea Apogaei medij, et quidem in centro mediae Eccentricitatis consistente sit mobile: praeterquam quod in Sole anomalia simplex in summa Circelli parte incipiat, in Luna vero duplata a Sole distantia incipiat contrario modo ab ima Circelli parte. Omnino spero discentes summum inde fructum capere posse. Scriptum meum de Cometa accipiet nobilissimus ille et magnificentissimus Vir, Kaasius tuus, Cancellarius vester dignissimus, qui mihi ex carmine tuo innotuit. Quem enim alium potius celebraret Vrania, quam patronum suum? Amo ea, qua debeo, reuerentia summas summi huius Viri virtutes. Quod in literis ad Illustrissimum Principem nostrum perscriptis existimas, omnes Cometas in ipso Caelo supra Lunam generari, nullumque eorum vnquam Elementarem extitisse; facile tibi concederem, nisi obstarent Obseruationes Regiomontani et Vogelini. Quanquam enim recte asseras, longe subtilioris Artificij esse Parallaxes rite obseruandi, quam hactenus communiter vsitata ratio suppeditare potuerit: tamen velocitas motus eas Parallaxes non videtur respuere. Nec sane video, cur non etiam infra Lunam generari possint, cum Elementum Aëris vbique contineatur, planeque ad replendum omnia praecipue conditum videatur, vt per praecedentia constat. Quid de magnis illis Cometis, qui tanta Caeli spatia occupârunt, dicemus? Sed tamen non dubito, quin non contemnendas habeas causas, cur ita statuas. Quae mea sit sententia, ex adiectis tribus capitibus videbis. In capite vltimo reieci motum violentum diurnum. Omnino Terrae eum tribuendum inuenio. Haec ad te hoc tempore perscribere volui, ad quae vt respondeas, te etiam atque etiam oro. His Vale feliciter, nobilissime et ingeniosissime TYCHO, meque tui amantissimum esse omnino tibi persuade.</p><p>Cassellis. 11. Calend. Octob. Anno Salutis M. D. LXXXVII.<br>T.T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS, Illustrissimi Principis WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, etc. Mathematicus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most renowned man, outstanding in virtue, wisdom, ingenious learning, and erudite doctrine, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight, of all the mathematicians of our age the most excellent, etc., his lord to be honoured with reverence, etc. — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I should have replied to your letter at the last Frankfurt fair, most noble Sir and most excellent of all mathematicians, had not those matters of which you write in your later letters been a hindrance to me; or had it not been that at Court time often has most idly to be spent on the most idle of affairs. But I shall now make up for that delay by the greater abundance [of what I write]. To deal with you, then, first of all and still in few words, about the comet: you will not be wide of the mark with that argument of yours, by no means a weak one — that it was reasonable the comet should have had some parallax, however scarcely perceptible, since Jupiter and Saturn too have parallax. I know the Copernican <i>hypotheses</i> absolutely demand it. Yet however I examine my <i>observations</i>, I can find no perceptible [parallax]. I could have used the same argument from imperceptible parallax to confirm my own view, by which I assigned the comet to the <i>sphere</i> of Saturn; since Saturn's parallax easily eludes our senses and scarcely reaches a third of a minute. But I did not wish to incur the censure of those who, though they understand nothing of the mathematical art, yet do not cease to bawl out, ignorantly and rashly, that we assert the most absurd things of all. But since I have you for the <i>Aristarchus</i> of my observations, henceforth I shall pay no heed to men of that stamp, but shall bring forward, with a bold and ready spirit, those things which I find true both by <i>observation</i> and by the most certain <i>demonstrations</i> of geometry. As for your wondering how I then assumed the maximum obliquity of the Sun at 23°30′, and could not, like you, gather it at 23°31⅓′ from the <i>observations</i> of our Most Illustrious Prince: in this I easily deserve pardon. For I saw that the <i>observations</i> of our Most Illustrious Prince yielded now a maximum solstitial <i>altitude</i> of the Sun of 62°10′, now 62°12′, and that with the very same <i>quadrant</i>: from which it was readily plain that they had been carried out with less care than the magnitude of the matter required. Besides, the true <i>elevation</i> of our pole was not yet established to me — with how great labours I tracked it down at last, you know. I therefore assumed that obliquity which at the time I conjectured to be as close to the truth as possible, although I did not know the exact and precise [value] from <i>observations</i>. But once the true <i>elevation</i> of our pole had been found, I readily noticed that the obliquity of the <i>zodiac</i> assigned by you comes as close as possible to the <i>observations</i> of our Most Illustrious Prince. And what at that time I had necessarily to learn from you, that I have now myself found to be most true by my own most diligent <i>observations</i>. For at the last summer <i>solstice</i> I found the maximum <i>meridian altitude</i> of the Sun exactly 62°11′, most carefully. Whence, adding the Sun's <i>parallax</i> and assuming the true <i>elevation</i> of our equator at 38°41′, your obliquity of the <i>zodiac</i> results. At the winter <i>solstice</i> the maximum <i>meridian altitude</i> of the Sun here is 15°14′. But at that season the air here is far denser and more vaporous than at other times of the year, and the Sun's <i>refraction</i> about midwinter is far greater than when the Sun is moving about the middle of ♏ Scorpio or ♒ Aquarius. So much for the comet and for the obliquity of the <i>zodiac</i> mixed in with it. And since I see that you long for the remainder of my writing on the comet, I send you three further chapters of it, from which you will readily understand what my view on comets is, and what <i>astronomical hypotheses</i> I judge to be true. I treated of these matters somewhat harshly; for to a hard knot a hard wedge must be applied; nor does that breed of men, in the face of preconceived authorities and a foolish ignorance of the mathematical art, easily depart from their most inept trifles. There is still missing a chapter on the signification of comets, designed above all to commend astronomy; which, though I have conceived it in mind, I have not been able to take down with the pen on account of other business imposed on me by our Most Illustrious Prince. It will be added when I bring that writing back to the anvil. Now I shall deal with you a little more fully about the matter of the celestial <i>spheres</i>. You are right, indeed, to hold with me that the matter of the celestial <i>spheres</i> is not hard or impervious, but fluid and subtle, and yields easily to the <i>motion</i> of the planets. But in this we disagree, that you, on account of <i>refractions</i>, posit different transparent media [<i>diaphana</i>] of aether and air — that is, that in the celestial <i>spheres</i> there is not contained elemental air, but rather a most fluid aether distinct from elemental air. But the argument from <i>refraction</i>, which you reckon fights on your side, utterly storms and overturns this view of yours. For if that <i>refraction</i>, which we find by <i>observation</i>, comes from a difference between the transparent medium of the aether and that of the elemental air, it cannot but last all the way to the zenith. For suppose that about the horizon that <i>refraction</i> is scarcely three minutes: it will nonetheless be, at an <i>altitude</i> of 45°, about 1½′, since the change and ratio of the triangles scarcely admits a lesser [value]. For even though, in confirmation of your view, you assert that the difference between the transparent media of aether and air is not so great, but is one that diminishes successively where they touch one another, so that the topmost portion of air, contiguous to the <i>revolutions</i> of the Moon, differs almost nothing from the aether itself: yet by that very thing, in that you assert that <i>refraction</i> arises on account of the different transparent media of aether and air — by that very thing, I say, you grant that the rays of the Sun or of the stars do nonetheless select for themselves a point in that successive combination and assimilation of the different transparent media, at which they are refracted; and this will necessarily always keep the same distance from the centre of the Earth, because that successive combination of the different transparent media is everywhere equally distant from the Earth. Having therefore assumed this point of <i>refraction</i> to be everywhere equally distant from the Earth, see whether the ratios of the triangles permit that <i>refraction</i> to vanish at an <i>altitude</i> of 50° or 60°. You see, then, that the vanishing of the <i>refractions</i> can in no way be granted by the different transparent media of aether and air. And so what my view about these <i>refractions</i> is — since perhaps it was not set out by me clearly enough in the [treatise on the] comet — I shall now disclose to you more explicitly. Between the Earth, as you know, and the <i>sphere</i> of the fixed stars I hold that nothing else is contained than air encompassing the seven <i>wandering</i> stars [the planets], because the <i>refractions</i> do not last all the way to the zenith, but scarcely, as you would have it, up to 30°. This air I then distinguish — Nature herself teaching me thus — into a thicker [part], subject to terrestrial exhalations, and into a pure [part], which is not infected by those exhalations. And the foundation of this distinction of mine is likewise the principles of <i>optics</i>. For it is known to you from Alhazen, from Witelo, from Pedro Nunes <i>On Twilights</i>, and also by the testimony of the eyes, that there is a certain space of air which in <i>twilight</i> is illuminated by the Sun's rays and grows white; and likewise that beyond that space there is air which cannot be illuminated and grow white from the Sun's rays — otherwise there would be perpetual <i>twilight</i>. It is necessary, therefore, that the air which grows white in the <i>twilight</i> even under a most serene sky be infected by terrestrial exhalations and rendered thicker — otherwise the Sun's rays could not illuminate it — while that which does not grow white be pure and uninfected by terrestrial vapours; although I will not deny that exhalations, especially the purer ones, can sometimes ascend even into that pure air. Nor yet do I find that this thick air can of itself cause <i>refraction</i>, but [only] on account of its multiplication. For although that space of thicker air reaches almost a semidiameter of the Earth from the surface of the Earth, as is manifest from what the <i>optical writers</i> have said about <i>twilights</i>, yet I observe that the rays of the Sun or of the stars overcome this thickness, and, on account of the identity of the transparent medium within it, cannot find a point of <i>refraction</i> in it; but about the horizon and in a more sloping position they can, on account of the doubling of that space.</p><p>[Figure] Let us assume, then, for the sake of a better demonstration, that the outermost surface of the thicker air is distant from the surface of the Earth by a semidiameter of the Earth: and let the semicircle BCD, described with the radius AB, denote the surface of the Earth, and with twice the radius let there be described the outermost surface of the thicker air EFG. Let there also be described, from the same centre, an outermost circle in which some star is carried, which let be HIL, at so great an interval that it has almost no perceptible proportion to AC. Let the star also be supposed to be placed, say, on the equator, or in some other place in which, on reaching the <i>meridian</i>, it has an <i>altitude</i> greater than 30°, and feels no further <i>refraction</i>. I say, then, that the point at which <i>refraction</i> occurs is more distant from the outermost surface EFG of the thicker air than the outermost surface of the thicker air is distant from the surface of the Earth — HE [greater] than CF or BE. For let the star rise at H. To those, then, who dwell at B at a similar <i>latitude</i> of place, the star will be on the <i>meridian</i>, or placed at so great an <i>altitude</i> that they can feel none of its <i>refraction</i>. Since therefore we, placed at C, then feel the maximum <i>refraction</i>, it is necessary that the point of <i>refraction</i> be between the points B and C, and also at a greater distance from the surface EFG than EB is. For otherwise those who dwelt at B would likewise feel the <i>refraction</i>. It is established, therefore, [as] what was proposed — namely that that space of thicker air is not of itself so great as to be able to refract the rays, but receives that power from its doubling about the horizon. And that this may be still more apparent, I shall now show how far the point of <i>refraction</i> is distant from the outermost surface HE of the thicker air, [and] through how great a space the rays of the stars penetrate that thick air unrefracted: for which purpose I shall assume, as you would have it, that that <i>refraction</i> lasts up to the thirtieth degree from the horizon. For this does not, as you suppose, overturn my view. Let it be conceived, then, as I said before, that AC has almost no perceptible proportion to AH such as could disturb the present matter, so that, when the points HC are joined, the angle FCH is almost as much a right angle as if it were the angle CAH — in the way we are accustomed to use <i>instruments</i> in observing the fixed stars as if at the centre of the world. Now let the arc HK be cut off, which let be 30°; and let the points K and C be joined by a straight line which cuts the outermost surface of the thicker air at the point O; and let an observer likewise be placed at S, for whom the point K is distant 29° from the horizon. Since, then, the one who is at C finds no further <i>refraction</i>, by <i>hypothesis</i>, it is necessary that there be no point of <i>refraction</i> either between K and C. But the one who is at S still feels the <i>refraction</i> of the point K, since for him K is at an <i>altitude</i> of 29°, likewise by <i>hypothesis</i>. Since, then, the point of <i>refraction</i> has now just ceased for the one at C, but still appears to the one at S, it is necessary that it be at the zenith C. Wherefore it is manifest that the line OC determines as closely as possible the space by which the point of <i>refraction</i> is distant from the outermost surface of the thicker air, and how far the rays can penetrate that thick air without <i>refraction</i>. While, then, the star is on the horizon at the point H, the point of <i>refraction</i> will be at N; while it is at Q, that point will be at R; and it will draw ever nearer and nearer to C, so that on the horizon it is furthest of all from the point C. From which, by Proposition 14 of Book 10 of Witelo, there follows as a <i>corollary</i> [πόρισμα] that which is found true in <i>observations</i>: namely that the <i>refractions</i> about the horizon are the greatest of all, and afterwards are diminished ever more and more, until, the point of <i>refraction</i> being placed at C, they utterly vanish. Although this might also come from the different thickness of the air. For the closer the air approaches the Earth, the thicker and denser it is made by the perpetual smokes and vapours. From all which it is most manifest that there are neither different transparent media of aether and air, nor can the <i>refractions</i> be caused from any source other than thick and vaporous air. Indeed this very thing, if nothing else, the different <i>refractions</i> in different places would certainly show. For it is most true that the <i>refractions</i> here are not so great as they are with you. For whereas you have found the <i>refraction</i> of the fixed stars at an <i>altitude</i> of 18° to be, with you, two minutes, I myself, from the stars of the Great Bear and others at the same <i>altitude</i>, find it here no greater than one minute, so that at an <i>altitude</i> of 23° scarcely a quarter of a minute is refracted. The Sun too, with a most clear sky and a strong ray, I found at an <i>altitude</i> of three degrees, when it was not yet a single degree from the visible horizon, to have a <i>refraction</i> of scarcely 9⅓′, or, with <i>parallax</i> added, 12⅓′ — which you, however, found to be 28′. Although the <i>refractions</i> of the Sun manifestly change according to the different state of the air, so that at midwinter, when the air here is never free of soots and dense murk, I have found it at an <i>altitude</i> of 15° to be 7′, account being had, of course, of the <i>parallax</i>. Whence you rightly assert that an altogether serene and pure air is required for treating the matter of <i>refractions</i> properly. You see, then, manifestly, that those <i>refractions</i> are from no source other than thick and dense air, and that the view of the ancients is true, by which they said that the air in more northerly places is denser and thicker than in more southerly ones. But whether the outermost surface of the thick air is, in more northerly places, further from the Earth than in more southerly ones, I greatly doubt. For about that which Nunes, in Part 2, Proposition 1 <i>On Twilights</i>, seems to doubt — namely that <i>twilight</i> is ended in some places when the Sun is hidden 17° below the horizon, in others at 18°, in others at 19° — about that it does not seem to me one ought to doubt, especially since Ptolemy too asserts the same at the end of Book 8 of the <i>Megalē Syntaxis</i>. But it is our task to note carefully the moments of time at which the air ceases to grow white in the evening or begins [to] in the morning, and to settle this dispute from a collation of our <i>observations</i>. These things, about this arduous and lofty matter, I wished to write out to you a little more copiously; and I beg of you again and again, most earnestly, that you examine them carefully, and do not cease to confer with me about them. Now to other matters. About the <i>latitudes</i> of the stars changed proportionally according to the change of the obliquity of the <i>zodiac</i>, you discourse most learnedly. For there was no other way to proceed aptly in the confirmation of that matter than the one by which you proceeded — because the places of the fixed stars are set down quite wrongly in the <i>tables</i>, and, as I judge, were never observed by Ptolemy, but only transcribed from Hipparchus. It appears from many places in the <i>Almagest</i>, and especially from the cited Chapter 3 of Book 7, that Ptolemy handled the business of the fixed stars less carefully and too drowsily. While I was still residing at Wittenberg, I remember examining in a like manner the places of the stars set down in the Ptolemaic <i>canon</i>; and when I assumed the <i>longitude</i> and <i>declination</i> of Regulus as they are handed down by Ptolemy, the <i>latitude</i> of Regulus came out 6⅔′ — not north, as is noted in the <i>canon</i>, but south. Since these things are manifestly contrary, and cannot be reconciled by anyone, it is necessary that one of them be false. But since Regulus is the one star whose <i>observation</i> Ptolemy recounts, and our <i>observations</i> do not much reject the <i>longitude</i> and <i>latitude</i> found by Ptolemy, of necessity the <i>declination</i> adduced will be most false. But since the decrement of the Ptolemaic <i>declination</i> nonetheless seems to correspond to the decrement of the <i>declinations</i> of Timocharis and Hipparchus — such that from Timocharis to Hipparchus the <i>declination</i> decreased 40′, from Hipparchus to Ptolemy 50′ (for, by reason of time, the decrement in the second interval ought to be greater than the decrement of the first interval) — it is thence manifest that Ptolemy never observed the <i>declinations</i> of the fixed stars, but only feigned them from the <i>declinations</i> of Timocharis and Hipparchus according to the proportion of time, especially since they are found for the most part false, and not duly to correspond to the progression of the stars, as you yourself have found by your calculation. Nor indeed do I see with what <i>instrument</i> he could have observed them. For the <i>parallactic instrument</i> [triquetrum] was contrived only for the Moon, and the holes of the sights were not equal, but the upper one, [used] for the Moon, was larger, so that through it the whole Moon could be seen; while the lower one was smaller. With <i>armillae</i> too he did not observe them, since the <i>declination</i> of that point of the <i>ecliptic</i> in which Regulus then stood was greater than the <i>declination</i> of Regulus itself handed down by Ptolemy. Nothing, then, can detract from our view about the changed <i>latitude</i> of the stars [in the case of] the Ptolemaic <i>latitude</i> of Sirius, which, whereas it ought to be greater than ours here, is yet found smaller. For although the <i>declinations</i> of Timocharis, of Hipparchus, and even of Ptolemy himself in some manner subscribe to the <i>latitude</i> set down in the Ptolemaic table, yet nothing is to be attributed to them, for the reasons stated. For this happened rather by chance than by <i>observation</i> and skill. When I consider the Greek text, I fear lest a corrupt copy of Hipparchus crept in upon Ptolemy himself. For the <i>declination</i> handed down by Timocharis could have been ις ϛγ, that is 16°50′; by Hipparchus ις ϛ, that is 16°30′; and through the carelessness of copyists, who thought the ϛϛ doubled in vain, the ϛ could have been omitted: to which corrupt <i>declinations</i> Ptolemy then perhaps also directed his own. The <i>latitude</i> of Sirius too, set down in the Ptolemaic table, could easily have been corrupted by the carelessness of copyists; since the same has happened not only in the nearby [star] Lucida Hydrae (whose <i>latitude</i> in this our age is found almost two degrees greater than the Ptolemaic, when yet it ought to be smaller), but also in many others, whose <i>latitude</i> is found notably corrupt. But why do we make many words about this single star, when the wish of this one is easily suppressed by the wishes of all the rest? Posterity is to be warned to handle this matter more diligently hereafter. And of these things perhaps more than enough. That in the lunar eclipse of the year 1584 the right diligence and precision was not applied by Wittich, you rightly assert. For he assumed the place of the Sun far otherwise than he ought by <i>observation</i>; likewise the right shoulder of Orion [Betelgeuse], by which he investigated the time, with both <i>declination</i> and <i>right ascension</i> faulty. When I saw this, not without cause did I complain of his negligence in my earlier letters. But that you may yourself see with what diligence and perspicacity he proceeded, I shall now subjoin the whole record of the <i>observation</i>, as he left it; and it is of this kind.</p><p>IN THE YEAR OF CHRIST 1584, ON 7 NOVEMBER, THERE WAS OBSERVED AT KASSEL A FULL MOON [PLENILUNIUM] IN ECLIPSE, WHOSE TIME FELL:<br>Of first obscuration at 11h20m P.M.<br>Of total obscuration at 12h20m.<br>Of first emersion at 13h45m.<br>Of total emersion at 14h45m.<br>Both clocks corrected by <i>azimuths</i> of the right shoulder of Orion.<br>The Sun moving through 25½° ♏ Scorpio, from the preceding <i>observation</i>.<br>Hence mid-obscuration at 13h3m. The Moon at 25°30′ ♉ Taurus with <i>latitude</i> 0°10′ south.<br>Duration in darkness 2h25m.<br>Whole duration 3h25m.<br>Right shoulder of Orion: <i>right ascension</i> 83°31′, <i>declination</i> 7°9′. From the <i>observations</i> of the year 1584.</p><p>From these you see how rashly he affirmed that he had assumed both the place of the Sun and the <i>right ascension</i> of the right shoulder of Orion, and the <i>declination</i>, from <i>observations</i> — when none of these is found true by <i>observation</i>. But I am unwilling to say more against a dead man. If you assume your own place of the Sun, and the true <i>right ascension</i> of the right shoulder of Orion, and the true <i>declination</i>, as they are found by our <i>observations</i>, you will see that the moments of time recorded here come closer to yours. The eclipse of this month I could not observe, on account of the rains which lasted here continuously for four whole days from 6 September, although I had diligently prepared myself. The sky here at Kassel was very inconstant and rainy this year, so that I have been able to carry out very few <i>observations</i>: I should much like to know whether the same was so also in other places. Our Most Illustrious Prince does not permit me to observe the planets; by his command I am compelled only to observe the fixed stars, and thence to track down their true places by <i>calculation</i>. For His Highness wishes that I make our <i>observations</i> public, and daily presses on the calculation of them. He wishes to leave to posterity a monument of his love toward astronomy; and to this gracious will of his I must yield. This work will redound greatly to your praise, and do not be anxious about the <i>instruments</i>. For I shall restore them to you with honourable proclamation of your name, nor shall I pass over the rest of the things about which you wrote to us. I have assumed for a foundation, as I signified also in my earlier letters, the <i>right ascension</i> of Aldebaran at 63°10′, which this very year too I found true by <i>daytime Venus</i>. But that on this account we differ from your <i>observations</i> in <i>longitude</i> by five minutes almost everywhere will offend no one who in the least understands the subtlety of this matter; nay rather, we shall the more deserve credit with posterity for it. The <i>diameter</i> of Venus can introduce this difference. Yet I shall do nothing about this in our work, so that full power may be left to you to examine this matter more diligently and by more <i>observations</i>. Nor will it offend you that, although we see that you have used every human diligence in your <i>observations</i>, we nonetheless do not depart from our own, which we have seen with our own eyes — for that does not befit lovers of truth, nor do we wish to adorn ourselves with another's feathers. The <i>observations</i> you sent here we guard most diligently, nor do we offer them to others to inspect; nor need you fear their publication. To you we leave the kingdom, to you the <i>restoration of astronomy</i>; and we rejoice that there is one who can perform in that matter what no one has performed before. And if I can be of help to you in any way whatever, in that I solemnly pledge and offer you all my zeal, diligence, industry, and faith. The <i>motion</i> of the Sun sent here by you I find to agree most exactly with our <i>observations</i>; except about the first degrees of ♒ Aquarius, where I found it by [observation of] Venus somewhat slower than it should be — which very thing, however, you point out in your earlier letters that you too had noticed. I received at the last Frankfurt fair, from Master Hans Aalborg, certain poems written by you to various most noble men; and as I read them through most avidly, I came at last upon the one you had sent to Master Falk Gøye, and on the other side of the page I found a pair of images, one of Astronomy, the other of that Art which you call <i>Spagyric</i> [alchemy]. As I gazed at them attentively, I noticed on either side of each of them certain words — concerning Astronomy, indeed, \"BY LOOKING UP I LOOK DOWN\" [SVSPICIENDO DESPICIO]; but concerning the other, \"BY LOOKING DOWN I LOOK UP\" [DESPICIENDO SVSPICIO]. Long pondering these with wonder, again and again, I thought at last that they were <i>hieroglyphs</i>, and that you perhaps signified by them that the <i>Spagyric</i> Art contains the contemplation of all Nature and of the World, and that it has, just as Astronomy does, its own Planets, and that what Nature does through the heavenly Stars, it [the Art] does with like efficacy through its own Stars. If this is so, I greatly desire to know what in the <i>Spagyric</i> Art you call the Sun, what the Moon, what Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Venus, Mercury, what the Elements. I see that the Art is most abstruse, and that the Nature of Things can scarcely be aptly understood without it; and that the Egyptian Priests held something similar. So that if I could obtain from you that you should inform me about it, and add the other things pertaining to this, and which Authors are to be read on this Art: nothing at all could happen to me more welcome.</p><p>That you so kindly invite me not to refrain from visiting you, I give you what thanks I can. Not long ago our Most Illustrious Prince was saying that it would please him for me to dedicate our <i>observations</i> of the fixed stars to the Most Serene King of Denmark. If he persists in that resolve, I shall be able on this occasion to come to you; but if not, I have composed at Wittenberg, not without many vigils, a <i>Mathematical Organon</i> [Organon Mathematicum], which contains the <i>sexagesimal logistic</i>, the <i>doctrine of sines</i>, the doctrine of the composition of ratios, or of the <i>rule of six quantities</i>, and the doctrine of <i>triangles</i>, both <i>spherical</i> and <i>plane</i>, set out clearly and briefly, packed with many ingenious shortcuts, and most dear to me: this I shall dedicate to your Most Serene King, that I may not lack occasion for coming to you. I have likewise composed there an <i>Astronomy</i>, in which I have transferred the <i>hypotheses</i> of Copernicus to the mobility of the Sun (which you are not unaware can be done), and have set out the several diagrams of the <i>Theorica</i> by the <i>Prutenic</i> calculation. But since I have found by <i>observation</i> that the <i>Prutenic Tables</i> depart notably far from the truth of the heavens, I have hitherto deferred its publication, lest I should seem to commend a faulty calculation to learners as true. If, then, it should please you, and if you would bring your <i>astronomical canons</i> into the light, I would substitute yours in place of the <i>Prutenic</i> calculation, so that your calculation might be made familiar to learners. With wonderful brevity, the heterogeneous [matters] having been dissected by the laws of Logic, I have cast into it far more than the common little books of <i>Spherics</i> and <i>Theorica</i> contain. I was driven to that work by a most bitter grief, that I saw, not without the greatest indignation, that in the Universities, where above all these studies ought to flourish, almost nothing is understood of this Art: that the <i>Prutenic</i> calculation is indeed in everyone's hands, but is handled only according to the bare <i>Prutenic</i> precepts, and that none are found who understand its <i>hypotheses</i>, not even among those who profess Astronomy and think themselves the highest mathematicians. The <i>motion</i> of the precession of the equinoxes and of the changed obliquity of the <i>zodiac</i> — whose <i>hypotheses</i> I have so far seen no one aptly understand, nor do I think you have seen many [understand them] — I have treated with the greatest ease and perspicuity; though it must now be treated otherwise, since the stars are found to have changed their <i>latitudes</i>. About the <i>artificial</i> days as well as the <i>natural</i> ones, and about the <i>equation of time</i>, I have proposed, from the very foundation, things not commonplace. Those real <i>orbs</i> and so many axes, obscuring astronomy rather than explaining it, I have wholly done away with. In the <i>Theorica</i> of the Sun and Moon I have assumed one and the same supposition of circles, namely an <i>eccentric</i> whose centre is movable on a little circle [<i>circellus</i>] above the centre of the World, on the line of the mean apogee, and indeed placed at the centre of the mean <i>eccentricity</i>: except that in the Sun the <i>simple anomaly</i> begins at the highest part of the little circle, while in the Moon the doubled distance from the Sun begins, in the contrary way, from the lowest part of the little circle. I altogether hope that learners can derive the greatest profit from it. My writing on the comet will be received by that most noble and most magnificent Man, your friend Kaas, your most worthy Chancellor, who became known to me from your poem. For whom else should <i>Urania</i> rather celebrate than her patron? I love, with the reverence I owe, the highest virtues of this highest Man. As for your thinking, in the letter written to our Most Illustrious Prince, that all comets are generated in the heaven itself above the Moon, and that none of them was ever Elemental — I would readily concede it to you, were not the <i>observations</i> of Regiomontanus and Vögelin in the way. For although you rightly assert that observing <i>parallaxes</i> properly is of a far subtler skill than the manner hitherto commonly in use could supply, yet the velocity of the <i>motion</i> does not seem to reject those <i>parallaxes</i>. Nor indeed do I see why they cannot also be generated below the Moon, since the Element of Air is contained everywhere, and seems to have been established chiefly to fill all things, as is established by what precedes. What shall we say about those great comets which occupied such vast spaces of the heaven? But yet I do not doubt that you have not-to-be-despised reasons why you so determine. What my own view is, you will see from the three chapters appended. In the last chapter I have rejected the violent diurnal <i>motion</i>. I find that it is altogether to be attributed to the Earth. These things I wished to write out to you at this time, to which I beg you again and again to reply. Herewith fare you happily well, most noble and most ingenious TYCHO, and persuade yourself altogether that I am most loving of you.</p><p>At Kassel, the 11th [day before] the Kalends of October [21 September], in the Year of Salvation 1587.<br>Yours, yours,<br>CHRISTOPH ROTHMANN, Mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), mathematician and astronomer to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592). The Kassel court observatory was, with Hven, the leading observational establishment in northern Europe; the Tycho–Rothmann exchange (1585–90) is among the most substantive scientific correspondences of the century. This is Rothmann's reply within that exchange.</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Domino de Knudstrup, Equiti Dano</i> — \"Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight\": Tycho's noble seat (Knudstrup, Scania) and rank, kept per §5.7.</li><li><b>\"the comet.\"</b> The comet of 1585, the subject of Rothmann's treatise (<i>Descriptio accurata cometae anni 1585</i>), three chapters of which he encloses. The debate over whether it showed <i>parallax</i> (and so lay below or above the Moon) is the live question.</li><li><b><i>Hypotheses Coperniceae / Hypotheses Copernici</b></i> — \"Copernican hypotheses\": rendered <i>hypotheses</i> throughout (the period sense = geometrical model/supposition), per glossary; never \"theory.\"</li><li><b><i>Aristarchum</b></i> — \"the <i>Aristarchus</i> of my observations\": Aristarchus of Samos as the type of the sound, ancient judge/authority; Rothmann makes Tycho the arbiter of his observations. Kept as the classical name with gloss.</li><li><b>Obliquity figures.</b> Rothmann's 23°30′ vs. Tycho's 23°31⅓′ for the maximum obliquity of the ecliptic; solstitial altitudes 62°10′/62°11′/62°12′; pole elevation (equator elevation) 38°41′. All reproduced exactly; no value silently normalised.</li><li><b>Refraction theory (the core of the letter).</b> <i>Refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>Diaphanum</i> → <b>transparent medium</b> (the period optical term for a refracting/transmitting medium); <i>Aether liquidissimus</i> vs. <i>Aër Elementaris</i> → \"most fluid aether\" vs. \"elemental air.\" The dispute: Tycho attributes astronomical refraction to a difference of <i>diaphana</i> between aether and air; Rothmann argues refraction arises only from the thick, vapour-laden lower air (\"by its doubling about the horizon\"), so that there is no aether at all between Earth and the fixed stars — only air encompassing the planets. This is a landmark statement against celestial spheres / for a unitary air-filled cosmos. Rendered fully and faithfully; the geometric demonstration (points A–S, semicircle BCD, surfaces EFG/HIL) is translated literally with the lettering preserved.</li><li><b><i>[Figura] / Figur 9</b></i> — the source carries a figure reference (the diagram of the refraction demonstration); marked <b>[Figure 9]</b> as an editorial insertion since the diagram is not in the text stream.</li><li><b>Optical authorities.</b> <i>Alhazenus</i> = <b>Alhazen</b> (Ibn al-Haytham); <i>Vitellio</i> = <b>Witelo</b> (the 13th-c. <i>Perspectiva</i>; \"Prop. 14, Bk 10\" cited); <i>Petrus Nonius de Crepusculis</i> = <b>Pedro Nunes</b>, <i>De crepusculis</i> (Lisbon, 1542). <i>Crepusculum</i> → <b>twilight</b> per §5.1.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> (1) <i>μεγαλ. συντάξ.</i> = <i>Megalē Syntaxis</i>, the Greek title of Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> (here \"the <i>Megalē Syntaxis</i>\"). (2) <i>πόρισμα</i> = <i>pórisma</i>, \"corollary\" (a geometer's term, kept and glossed). (3) The numeral-letters <b>ις ϛγ / ις ϛ</b> are Greek alphabetic numerals: ις = 16, ϛ (stigma) = 6, γ = 3; Rothmann's textual-criticism argument is that a <i>declination</i> of 16°50′ (ις ϛγ — reading the second cluster as minutes) was miscopied to 16°30′ (ις ϛ) when a doubled stigma ϛϛ was taken for dittography. The numeral forms are transcribed in the source block and the arithmetic is explained here; <b>see crux below</b> — the exact letter-forms and Rothmann's intended minute-values are a genuine textual difficulty.</li><li><b>Star catalogue critique.</b> <i>Regulus</i> (α Leonis), <i>Sirius</i> (α Canis Maioris), <i>Lucida Hydrae</i> (α Hydrae, Alphard), <i>Aldebaran</i> (<i>Aldebora</i>, α Tauri), <i>Dexter humerus Orionis</i> = the <b>right shoulder of Orion</b> (Betelgeuse, α Orionis). <i>Timocharis</i> and <i>Hipparchus</i> are the ancient observers whose <i>declinations</i> Rothmann argues Ptolemy merely interpolated (\"feigned … according to the proportion of time\") rather than observed — an early, pointed charge of fabrication against the <i>Almagest</i> star places. <i>Canon / Tabula / Abacus</i> → tables/canon. <i>Almagest</i> cited \"Bk 7, ch. 3.\"</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Instrumentum parallaticum</i> = the <b>parallactic instrument / triquetrum</b> (Ptolemy's, for the Moon); <i>Armillae</i> → <b>armillae / armillary</b> per §5.2; <i>Quadrans</i> → <b>quadrant</b>. <i>Venus diurna</i> → <b>daytime Venus</b> (Venus observed by day, used to carry the reference longitude — Tycho's and Rothmann's technique).</li><li><b>Wittich.</b> <i>Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician who had worked both at Hven and at Kassel; here recently dead (\"<i>demortuus</i> … I am unwilling to say more against a dead man\"). Rothmann reproduces and faults Wittich's 7 November 1584 lunar-eclipse record. The eclipse record block is set as its own paragraph, mirroring the source's tabular list; <i>Plenilunium eclipticum</i> → \"full moon in eclipse\"; <i>Mora in tenebris</i> → \"duration in darkness.\" Times are given in the source's hour-count from noon (P.M.); reproduced as written (11h20m … 14h45m), <b>not</b> converted.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> Zodiac glyphs kept in the source block and named in English per §5.3: <b>♏ = Scorpio, ♒ = Aquarius, ♉ = Taurus.</b></li><li><b>Tycho's emblem.</b> <i>Suspiciendo despicio / Despiciendo suspicio</i> — Tycho's twin mottoes (\"By looking up I look down\" / \"By looking down I look up\"), here flanking the personifications of <b>Astronomia</b> and the <i>Ars Spagyrica</i> (Tycho's term for <b>alchemy/spagyric art</b>) on the verso of a poem Tycho had sent to <b>Falk Gøye</b> (Danish nobleman). Rothmann reads the device as <i>hieroglyphica</i> and asks Tycho for his alchemical planet-metal correspondences. <i>Spagyrica</i> kept (period term for Paracelsian chemistry), glossed \"alchemy.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>M. Ioannes Aalborga</i> = <b>Hans Aalborg</b> (§5.6); <i>Kaasius … Cancellarius vester</i> = <b>Niels Kaas</b>, Chancellor of Denmark (Tycho's patron at court), known to Rothmann from a poem of Tycho's; <i>Regiomontanus</i> (Johannes Müller of Königsberg) and <i>Vogelinus</i> = <b>Johannes Vögelin</b> (Vienna, d. 1549, author on comet parallax) — the two authorities whose comet <i>observations</i> keep Rothmann from fully conceding Tycho's supralunary-only thesis.</li><li><b>Rothmann's own works.</b> <i>Organon Mathematicum</i> — his unpublished mathematical handbook (sexagesimal logistic, <i>doctrine of sines</i>, <i>regula sex quantitatum</i> = the rule of six quantities / compound proportion, plane and spherical <i>trigonometry</i>); kept in Latin and glossed. <i>Astronomia</i> — his treatise transposing Copernicus's <i>hypotheses</i> to \"the mobility of the Sun\" and computing the <i>Theorica</i> schemes by the <i>Prutenic</i> (Reinhold's <i>Tabulae Prutenicae</i>) calculation, which he reports departs notably from the heavens. <i>Logistica Sexagenaria</i> → <b>sexagesimal logistic</b> (computation in base-60). <i>restitutio/restauratio Astronomiae</i> → <b>restoration of astronomy</b> per §5.1 (Tycho's programme: \"to you we leave the <i>restoration of astronomy</i>\").</li><li><b>Cosmology — abolition of solid orbs.</b> <i>Orbes illos reales et tot axes … prorsus sustuli</i> — \"those real orbs and so many axes … I have wholly done away with\": Rothmann, like Tycho, rejects solid celestial spheres. The lunar/solar <i>Theorica</i> description (<i>Eccentricus … circellus … in linea Apogaei medij … centrum mediae Eccentricitatis</i>; <i>anomalia simplex</i>) is rendered with glossary terms (<i>eccentric, apogee, eccentricity, anomaly</i>) and the diminutive <i>circellus</i> as \"little circle.\"</li><li><b><i>motum violentum diurnum … Terrae tribuendum</b></i> — \"the violent diurnal motion … to be attributed to the Earth\": Rothmann states he has rejected the violent daily rotation of the heavens and assigns the diurnal motion to the Earth — an openly Copernican (or at least diurnally-rotating-Earth) commitment. Rendered without softening.</li><li><b>Dateline (calendar crux flagged, not converted).</b> <i>Cassellis. 11. Calend. Octob. Anno … M.D.LXXXVII</i> = \"At Kassel, the 11th [day before] the Kalends of October, 1587.\" By the Roman inclusive count, <i>ante diem XI Kalendas Octobris</i> = <b>21 September</b> — i.e. the Roman-calendar idiom denotes a September date, not 1 October. The dataset/edition label \"11. oktober. Kassel\" reflects a <b>different reckoning of the dateline</b> (and/or an O.S.→N.S. consideration: Hesse-Kassel was Protestant and still on the Julian calendar in 1587, so no Gregorian gap is expected for an internal German Protestant date). Per protocol §3 the dateline is reproduced as written, the Roman idiom is glossed to its civil date (21 Sept., O.S.), and the discrepancy with the \"11 October\" label is <b>flagged, not reconciled.</b> <i>Anno Salutis</i> → \"in the Year of Salvation.\" <i>T.T.</i> = <i>Tui Tuus</i> / <i>Tuus Totus</i>, \"Yours, [yours]\" — an affectionate subscription.</li><li><b><i>S. P.</b></i> (heading) = <i>Salutem Plurimam</i> (dicit), \"heartiest greeting,\" per §5.5.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> FABRICATION removed: the English diagram marker '[Figure 9]' invented a sequence number absent from the Latin's unnumbered '[Figura]'; corrected to '[Figure]'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely throughout, including the long refraction demonstration and the Ptolemy-catalogue argument, with all numerical values, instrument terms, and the two Tychonic mottoes reproduced faithfully and no hedges sharpened. Two points are flagged rather than smoothed, each falling short of forcing a [C]: <b>(1) the Greek numeral-letter crux</b> (<i>ις ϛγ</i> / <i>ις ϛ</i> and the <i>ϛϛ</i> dittography argument) — the source's letter-forms for the <i>stigma</i>/digamma and the exact minute-values Rothmann assigns are a real textual difficulty, glossed by their arithmetic but not certain to the last sign; and <b>(2) the dateline discrepancy</b> between the in-letter <i>11 Kalendas Octobris</i> (= 21 September O.S. by the standard Roman inclusive count) and the dataset label \"11 October,\" reported per §3 and left unreconciled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible (per brief, capped below A). Instead performed a full clause-by-clause collation of published_english against source_text: checked every proper name (Aristarchus, Aldebaran, Regulus, Sirius, Lucida Hydrae, Alhazen, Witelo/Vitellio, Pedro Nunes/Nonius, Ptolemy, Hipparchus, Timocharis, Regiomontanus, Vogelinus, Kaas, Hans Aalborg, Falk Goye, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse), every numeric value and date (23 deg 30', 23 deg 31 1/3', 62 deg 10'/12'/11', 38 deg 41', 15 deg 14', the full 1584 eclipse timing block, 6 Sept/four days rain, Aldebaran 63 deg 10', five minutes longitude difference, all refraction-geometry figures 18 deg/two minutes, 23 deg/quarter minute, three degrees/9 1/3'-12 1/3'/28', 15 deg/7', 17/18/19 deg twilight, Regulus 6 2/3', the 40'/50' declination decrements, Timocharis/Hipparchus 16 deg50'/16 deg30', two-degree Lucida Hydrae discrepancy, all geometric point labels B-C-D-E-F-G-H-I-K-L-N-O-Q-R-S), and the closing dateline (11 Kalends of October = 21 September 1587, matching the letter's O.S. date). All of these render faithfully with no omissions, no flipped subjects/negations, and hedges (fortassis, vereor, licet) preserved. One fabrication found: the translator's '[Figure 9]' supplies an invented figure number absent from the source's plain '[Figura]' marker. No other defects found; grade would otherwise merit B (sound but uncollated against Dreyer).</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (low). The source's diagram marker is the generic, unnumbered '[Figura]'; the translation inserts a specific sequence number '9' that has no counterpart anywhere in the Latin source. At: “[Figure 9]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the TEXT_ERROR-grade collation against the edition of record could not be performed. This defect, however, is a FABRICATION verifiable from the letter's own internal evidence: the source_text on file contains a single, unnumbered '[Figura]' marker with no digit anywhere near it, so the English's '[Figure 9]' is demonstrably an invented number. No other defect was assigned to this letter, and nothing else was touched or re-collated.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15870812001", "next": "brahe-t_15871114001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870812001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15880611001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for the delayed reply, acknowledges Tycho's parallax argument for the comet, but reports his own observations cannot detect parallax, engaging Copernican hypotheses on the point.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15871011001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15871019001", "d": "1587-10-19", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Uraniborg, 19 October 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Schleswig, Schleswig-Holsteinisches Landesarchiv, Abt. 127.21, R 119, foll. 387–389 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICO RANZOVIO PRODUCI CIMBRICO TYCHO BRAHE.<br>S. D.</p><p>QUOD nihil hac tota aestate ad te literarum dederim, nobilissime eruditissimeque Domine HENRICE RANZOVI, amice inprimis colende, neque oblivione ulla tui, cuius memoriam cum filio GERHARDO, amico et vicino meo dilecto, saepius recolo, neque negligentia voluntaria factum existimes. Verum cum in dies expectarem ab illustrissimo Domino GULIELMO Landgravio Hassiae litteras, quibus meis secundo ad ipsum datis per proprium tabellarium respondere pollicitus est, ut earum exemplar tibi unâ cum prioribus, quibus invicem de rebus Astronomicis ex voto egimus, inspiciendum mitterem, accidit ut diutius quam sperabam rescriptio illa procrastinata sit adeo, ut iam primum circa medium Octobris responsum a praedicto illustrissimo Principe ad Literas in Ianuario ipsi perscriptas acceperim. Contigit vero commodum, quod his ipsis diebus quendam ex meis domesticis Lubecam ablegaverim, ut papyrum impressoriam, quam operi cuidam, quod nunc typis meis elaboratur, sufficientem desidero, illic mature procuret, et quaedam alia negotia, quae in mandatis habet, unâ meo nomine expediat. Idcirco non inscius, Segebergam unius saltem diei itinere illinc abesse, nolui ipsum tam prope sedes, in quibus moraris, accedere, quin aliquid a me tibi literarum adferret meoque nomine officiose salutem diceret. Dedi itaque huic in mandatis, ut te Segebergae conveniret hasque literas cum adiunctis tibi tradere non intermitteret. Invenies in his illustrissimi Domini Landgravij et ipsius Mathematici ROTHMANNI responsionem ad priores meas, quarum (ni fallor) tibi exemplar prius misi. Adiunxi etiam ea, quae eidem illustrissimo Principi et dicto ipsius Mathematico postea de rebus Astronomicis luculenter rescripsi, et ab ipso Principe denuo remissum responsum, quod modo accepi, unâ addidi. Literae Mathematici, quae adiunctae erant, non tam cito ob prolixitatem et copiam transcribi poterant, eo quod nuncius meus ob venti oportunitatem abitum festinaret. Sed alio tempore, si ita tibi placuerit, eas facile obtinebis. Addidi etiam festinanter quaedam, quae ex tempore mihi in mentem venerunt, ad hasce ultimas Landgravianas literas per nuncij et otij oportunitatem respondenda, et literarum notis certos paragraphos designavi, quibus responsiones eisdem literis adaptatae quadrant. Hinc quid de affixarum restitutionibus utrinque sentiamus, facile percipies et non sine voluptate magnam congruentiam animadvertes. Non enim me latet, quanto desiderio rerum Astronomicarum afficiaris, et quam egregie de his meritus sis. Tuum erit, mecum, si quid vel ab antedicto Principe vel ex propria Bibliotheca aut aliunde habes, quod ad Astronomicam, qua utrinque delectamur, materiam illustrandam facit, vicissim communicare. Vale, et huic nuncio meo in ijs, quae illi expedienda iniunxi, tuo consilio authoritateque, ubi opus fuerit, ne deesse velis.</p><p>Iterum feliciter cum nobilissima uxore et sobole non degenere totaque domo vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Octobris Anno 1587.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Heinrich Rantzau, Governor of the Cimbrian land.</b><br>Sends greeting.</p><p>That I have sent you no letter this whole summer, most noble and most learned Master Heinrich Rantzau, my friend especially to be honoured, you must not suppose to have come about either from any forgetfulness of you — whose memory I often recall together with your son Gerhard, my dear friend and neighbour — or from any wilful negligence. Rather, since I was daily awaiting letters from the most illustrious Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse — to which, after I had written to him a second time, he had promised to reply by his own courier — so that I might send you a copy of them, along with the earlier ones in which we dealt with one another on astronomical matters to our mutual wish, it came about that that reply was put off longer than I had hoped, so much so that it was only now, around the middle of October, that I received the answer of the aforesaid most illustrious Prince to the letter written out to him in January. But it has fallen out conveniently that in these very days I have dispatched one of my household to Lübeck, that he might procure there in good time printing paper — which I find sufficient for a certain work that is now being produced at my press — and might at the same time, in my name, settle certain other affairs which he has in charge. Therefore, not unaware that Segeberg is at least one day's journey distant from there, I was unwilling that he should pass so near the seat where you reside without his bringing you some letter from me and dutifully conveying a greeting in my name. And so I gave him charge to meet with you at Segeberg and not to fail to deliver to you this letter together with its enclosures. In them you will find the reply of the most illustrious Lord Landgrave and of his own Mathematician Rothmann to my earlier ones, of which (unless I am mistaken) I sent you a copy before. I have added also those things which I afterwards wrote back at length on astronomical matters to that same most illustrious Prince and to his said Mathematician, and I have added together with them the answer sent back again by the Prince himself, which I have just received. The Mathematician's letters, which were enclosed, could not be transcribed so quickly on account of their prolixity and abundance, since my messenger was hastening his departure because of the favourable wind. But at another time, if it so please you, you will easily obtain them. I have also hastily added certain things which occurred to me on the spur of the moment, to be answered to these last letters of the Landgrave as the opportunity of messenger and leisure allowed; and I have marked off particular paragraphs with letters of the alphabet, by which the replies fitted to those same letters correspond. From this you will easily perceive what each of us holds concerning the restorations of the fixed [stars], and you will note, not without pleasure, the great agreement. For it is not hidden from me with how great a longing you are moved toward astronomical matters, and how excellently you have deserved well of them. It will be for you in turn to share with me, if you have anything either from the aforesaid Prince, or from your own library, or from elsewhere, which serves to illuminate the astronomical subject in which we both delight. Farewell; and to this my messenger, in the matters which I have enjoined upon him to settle, do not be unwilling to lend your counsel and authority, where there shall be need.</p><p>Again, happily, farewell — with your most noble wife and your offspring that does not fall short of its stock, and your whole house.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 19 October, the year 1587.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> writes from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. The addressee, <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the Danish king's <i>Statthalter</i> (royal governor) of the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, residing chiefly at <b>Segeberg</b>; one of the great humanist patrons and bibliophiles of the north, an astrological author, and a frequent intermediary in Tycho's correspondence.</li><li><b>Address-line title.</b> <i>Produci Cimbrico</i> — \"Governor of the Cimbrian land,\" i.e. of the Cimbrian peninsula (Jutland / the duchies). <i>Produx</i> (a late/Neo-Latin form, dative <i>produci</i>) here = a leading/governing magistrate, rendered \"Governor\"; the antiquarian epithet <i>Cimbricus</i> (\"of the Cimbri\") is the humanist style for Jutland-Holstein. Kept and glossed rather than modernised to \"Statthalter.\"</li><li><b><i>S. D.</b></i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (<i>filio GERHARDO</i>) — Heinrich's son <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (1558–1627), here named as Tycho's \"friend and neighbour\" (<i>amico et vicino meo dilecto</i>).</li><li><b>Wilhelm IV</b> (<i>illustrissimo Domino GULIELMO Landgravio Hassiae</i>) = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6), the astronomer-prince; his court mathematician <b>Rothmann</b> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (§5.6). The letter is a cover-note forwarding the Brahe–Hesse astronomical correspondence to Rantzau.</li><li><b>Calendar reference.</b> <i>responsum … ad Literas in Ianuario ipsi perscriptas</i> — Tycho's letter \"written out in January\" is the long Tycho→Rothmann letter of 20 January 1587 (slug brahe-t_15870120001); not converted, given as written.</li><li><b><i>papyrum impressoriam … operi cuidam, quod nunc typis meis elaboratur</b></i> — \"printing paper … for a certain work now being produced at my press\": Tycho's own Uraniborg press; the \"certain work\" is most plausibly the volume that became the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (printed at Uraniborg, the 1596 <i>Liber I</i>) and/or <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, then in progress — identification not asserted in the text, so left general.</li><li><b>Astronomical crux — <i>affixarum restitutionibus</i>.</b> Literally \"the restorations of the fixed [stars],\" i.e. the re-determination / re-establishment of the <b>fixed-star</b> positions (the catalogue of <i>stellae affixae</i>, the fixed stars). <i>restitutio</i> is rendered \"restoration\" per the controlled glossary (§5.1, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i> → restoration/renewal); <i>affixarum</i> (fem. gen. pl.) stands for <i>stellarum affixarum</i> (the fixed stars), the noun <i>stellarum</i> understood — bracketed \"[stars]\" supplied. The phrase denotes the agreement between Tycho and Rothmann on their respective fixed-star coordinate restorations. Flagged because <i>affixarum</i> is elliptical and \"restorations of the fixed stars\" is a technical-procedural sense, not a generic \"restoration.\"</li><li><b><i>ex propria Bibliotheca</b></i> — \"from your own library\": a pointed compliment to Rantzau, whose library at Breitenburg was among the most celebrated private collections of the age.</li><li><b>Marking convention.</b> <i>literarum notis certos paragraphos designavi</i> — \"I have marked off particular paragraphs with letters of the alphabet\": Tycho keyed his replies to numbered/lettered sections of the Landgrave's letters so the correspondence could be read in register. <i>literarum notis</i> = \"with letters [of the alphabet] as marks,\" rendered accordingly.</li><li><b>Subscription / dateline.</b> <i>sobole non degenere</i> — \"offspring not degenerate,\" i.e. children who do not fall short of (do not disgrace) their lineage; rendered \"offspring that does not fall short of its stock,\" the double negative (<i>non degenere</i>) preserved. <i>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Octobris Anno 1587</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 19 October 1587\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The catalogue label (\"1587. 19. oktober. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Negations (verified count).</b> Source negations rendered one-for-one: <i>neque oblivione … neque negligentia … existimes</i> (\"neither … nor …\"); <i>non intermitteret</i> (\"not fail\"); <i>non inscius</i> (\"not unaware\"); <i>nolui … quin … adferret</i> (\"I was unwilling that he should pass … without bringing,\" litotes preserved); <i>ni fallor</i> (\"unless I am mistaken\"); <i>non … me latet</i> (\"it is not hidden from me\"); <i>ne deesse velis</i> (\"do not be unwilling to lend\"); <i>non … sine voluptate</i> (\"not without pleasure\"); <i>non degenere</i> (\"not degenerate\"). No negation added or dropped.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one flagged point is the elliptical technical phrase <i>affixarum restitutionibus</i> (\"the restorations of the fixed [stars]\"), where <i>stellarum</i> is understood and the bracketed \"[stars]\" is supplied rather than silently absorbed.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole point is a routine ellipsis (</i>affixarum [stellarum]<i> completed); sense, negations, and dateline all secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.383-386 (letter itself under the APPENDIX heading on 384-385): opening explanation of the delay, the Wilhelm IV/Rothmann correspondence being forwarded via Segeberg, and the closing/date (Uraniborg, 19 October 1587) match Dreyer word for word. Translation is faithful throughout, including the technical astronomical passage on the restitution of the fixed stars. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schleswig, Schleswig-Holsteinisches Landesarchiv, Abt. 127.21, R 119 foll. 387-389. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15870117001", "next": "brahe-t_15880913001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for summer silence, explaining he awaited the Landgrave's reply before forwarding copies of their astronomical correspondence, which he now encloses.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15871019001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15871028001", "d": "1587-10-28", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 28 October 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer, vol. VII<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>GRATISSIMAe mihi fuerunt tuae literae cum literis ultro citroque ad Landgravium et eius Mathematicum scriptis, in quibus non sine voluptate legi vestrarum observationum consensum, tum in Cometa anni 85, tum in stellarum fixarum notatione longitudinum et latitudinum. Latitudines enim uterque mutari statuit, et sequi mutationem declinationis Eclipticae, sive obliquitatis Eclipticae variationem. Haec cum maximâ, sententia COPERNICI, sit 24', quae aliquot seculorum decursu fiat, et cum non multum inter PTOLEMAeUM et HIPPARCHUM et MENELAUM et TIMOCHARIN temporum intervallum intercedat, non mirum, latitudinum mutationem ab ijs non annotatam. Verum de COPERNICO miror, qui evidentem mutationem debuit animadvertere in tanta obliquitatis mutatione inter PTOLEMAeUM et COPERNICUM intercedente, si verae sunt observationes, quae tantam obliquitatis Zodiaci variationem tradunt. Quae autem de refractione siderum tradis, subtilia nimis mihi videntur, et quae sub regulam cadere vix poterint ob diversas aëris constitutiones, quae varietatem refractionum in eadem siderum altitudine efficient. Quae autem a doctissimo CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO in sua de Cometa anni 85 tractatione commemorantur, velim legere. Ex hypothesi enim falsa verum sequi non dubito; scientiam autem fieri, nullus mihi persuaserit, si debeat ea oriri ex causis veris conclusionis. Videmus autem motum Solis tam per Eccentricum, quam per Concentricum cum Epicyclo tueri nos posse, cum vel neutra vel altera hypothesis vera esse queat, ut non satis intelligam, cur hoc in loco ARISTOTELES subtilitatis arguatur. Quae de cancris fluvialibus scribis, dabo operam, quantum in me erit; verum video maiores difficultates, quam tu fortasse cogites, quod cancrorum emtio et navium in Daniam solutio raro in eadem tempora incidant. Attamen studium meum cum in hoc tum in alio genere cognosces.</p><p>Bene vale, mi TYCHO, et nos tui studiosissimos habeto.<br>Rostochij 28 Octobris Anno Domini 1587.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most welcome to me were your letters, together with the letters written back and forth to the Landgrave and his Mathematician, in which I read — not without pleasure — the agreement of your observations, both in the case of the comet of [the year] '85 and in the recording of the longitudes and latitudes of the fixed stars. For each of you holds that the latitudes change, and that they follow the change in the declination of the ecliptic — that is, the variation of the obliquity of the ecliptic. Since this is, at its greatest, 24′ in Copernicus's opinion — a change that comes about over the course of several centuries — and since not much interval of time falls between Ptolemy and Hipparchus and Menelaus and Timocharis, it is no wonder that a change of latitudes was not noted by them. But about Copernicus I marvel: he ought to have observed an evident change, given the great change of obliquity falling between Ptolemy and Copernicus, if the observations that report so great a variation of the obliquity of the zodiac are true. As for what you set forth about the refraction of the heavenly bodies, it seems to me too subtle, and such as can scarcely fall under a rule, on account of the diverse constitutions of the air, which will produce a variety of refractions at one and the same altitude of the heavenly bodies. But what is recounted by the most learned Christoph Rothmann in his treatise on the comet of [the year] '85 — I should like to read it. For I do not doubt that a true [conclusion] follows from a false hypothesis; but that this constitutes [true] knowledge [scientia], no one will ever persuade me, if it [knowledge] must arise from the true causes of the conclusion. For we see that we can maintain the motion of the Sun as well by an eccentric as by a concentric with an epicycle, although either neither or [only] one of the [two] hypotheses can be true — so that I do not well understand why, in this place, Aristotle is charged with [excessive] subtlety. As for what you write about the river crabs, I shall do my best, so far as it lies in me; but I see greater difficulties than you perhaps suppose, because the buying of the crabs and the sailing of ships to Denmark rarely fall at the same season. Yet you will know my zeal both in this matter and in another kind.</p><p>Fare you well, my Tycho, and reckon us most devoted to you.<br>At Rostock, 28 October, in the Year of the Lord 1587.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, 1531–1593), of Alost (Aalst) in Flanders; professor of medicine and of mathematics/astronomy at the University of <b>Rostock</b>, and Tycho's former teacher there. The intimate, faintly tutorial tone of the close (<i>mi Tycho</i>) and the philosophical objection reflect that relationship.</li><li><b><i>ad Landgravium et eius Mathematicum</b></i> — to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592) and \"his Mathematician,\" i.e. <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Hesse-Kassel court mathematician (named explicitly later in the letter). The \"letters written back and forth\" (<i>ultro citroque</i>) are the Tycho–Kassel observational correspondence; Brucaeus had evidently been shown copies.</li><li><b>The comet of '85.</b> <i>Cometa anni 85</i> = the comet of <b>1585</b>, observed both at Hven and at Kassel; the subject of Rothmann's treatise (<i>Tractatus de cometa anni 1585</i>) and of comparison between the two observatories.</li><li><b>Astronomy — obliquity/latitudes.</b> <i>declinatio Eclipticae … obliquitas Eclipticae</i> = the <b>declination / obliquity of the ecliptic</b> (the tilt of the zodiac to the equator); <i>latitudines</i> / <i>longitudines</i> = <b>(celestial) latitudes / longitudes</b> of the fixed stars (protocol §5.1). Brucaeus reports that Tycho and Rothmann agree the stellar latitudes shift in step with a secular change in the obliquity. <i>maximâ … sit 24′</i> — Copernicus put the total amplitude of this variation at <b>24 arc-minutes</b> (24′) over several centuries (Copernicus's theory of the trepidation/variation of the obliquity, <i>De revolutionibus</i> III). The figure and the hedge (\"at its greatest,\" <i>maximâ</i>) are kept.</li><li><b><i>Ptolemaeum … Hipparchum … Menelaum … Timocharin</b></i> — <b>Ptolemy</b> (fl. 2nd c. AD), <b>Hipparchus</b> (2nd c. BC), <b>Menelaus</b> of Alexandria (fl. c. AD 100), and <b>Timocharis</b> of Alexandria (early 3rd c. BC): the ancient observers whose star-records bracket the question. Brucaeus's point is sceptical — the ancients' intervals were too short to register the latitude shift, yet Copernicus, far removed in time from Ptolemy, ought to have seen it if the reported variation is real.</li><li><b>Refraction.</b> <i>refractione siderum</i> = <b>refraction</b> of the heavenly bodies (protocol §5.1); <i>diversas aëris constitutiones</i> = \"the diverse constitutions [states] of the air.\" Brucaeus's objection — that atmospheric refraction is too variable with the air's condition to be reduced to a fixed table (<i>sub regulam … cadere</i>) at a given <b>altitude</b> (<i>altitudine</i>) — is a genuine and prescient methodological caution; rendered without sharpening.</li><li><b>The hypotheses passage (the philosophical core).</b> <i>Ex hypothesi … falsa verum sequi non dubito; scientiam autem fieri, nullus mihi persuaserit, si debeat ea oriri ex causis veris conclusionis.</i> — \"I do not doubt that a true [conclusion] follows from a false hypothesis; but that this constitutes [true] knowledge [<i>scientia</i>], no one will ever persuade me, if it must arise from the true causes of the conclusion.\" This is the classic \"saving the phenomena\" vs. <b>demonstrative knowledge</b> distinction: a <i>hypothesis</i> (geometrical model) may yield correct predictions yet be physically false, and so not true <i>scientia</i> in the Aristotelian sense (knowledge through true causes). <i>scientia</i> is rendered \"[true] knowledge\" with the Latin flagged, not modernised to \"science.\" The example follows: <i>motum Solis tam per Eccentricum, quam per Concentricum cum Epicyclo</i> = the <b>Sun's motion</b> modelled equally well <b>by an eccentric</b> or <b>by a concentric [deferent] with an epicycle</b> (protocol §5.1) — two geometrically equivalent constructions, of which (Brucaeus argues) at most one can be physically true. The clause <i>cum vel neutra vel altera hypothesis vera esse queat</i> is rendered \"although either neither or [only] one of the [two] hypotheses can be true\"; <i>altera</i> here = \"(only) one of the two,\" the disjunction being \"neither, or [at most] one\" — i.e. <i>not both</i>. The two negation-bearing options (<i>neutra</i> / <i>altera</i>) are reproduced exactly, neither added to nor dropped.</li><li><b><i>hoc in loco ARISTOTELES subtilitatis arguatur</b></i> — \"in this place Aristotle is charged with [excessive] subtlety.\" <i>subtilitatis arguatur</i> = \"is accused/charged on the score of subtlety\"; the bracketed \"[excessive]\" marks that the genitive carries a pejorative sense (over-subtlety, hair-splitting) which is implied, not lexically present. The \"place\" (<i>hoc in loco</i>) is presumably a passage in Tycho's or Rothmann's letters where Aristotle's physics was faulted; Brucaeus defends Aristotle, since (on his view) the geometers cannot establish physical truth either.</li><li><b>River crabs.</b> <i>cancris fluvialibus</i> = <b>river crabs / crayfish</b> (freshwater crustaceans, <i>Astacus</i>). A non-astronomical favour: Tycho had asked Brucaeus to procure crayfish; Brucaeus pleads the logistical mismatch between the <b>buying of the crabs</b> and the <b>sailing of ships to Denmark</b> (<i>navium in Daniam solutio</i> — <i>solutio</i> here = the loosing/setting-sail of ships), which rarely coincide in season. Rendered plainly; <i>solutio</i> glossed.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>Bene vale, mi Tycho</i> = \"Fare you well, my Tycho\" (protocol §5.5); <i>nos tui studiosissimos habeto</i> = \"reckon us most devoted to you.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Rostochij 28 Octobris Anno Domini 1587</i> = \"at <b>Rostock</b>, 28 October, in the Year of the Lord 1587\" (O.S.; Denmark and Lutheran Rostock both kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (28 October 1587, Rostock) — no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the one genuinely interpretive point is the elliptical disjunction <i>vel neutra vel altera … vera esse queat</i> (\"either neither, or [only] one [of the two], can be true\"), where <i>altera</i> must mean \"(only) one of the two\" for the argument against geometrical equivalence to cohere — read so above, with the construal flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages (TBOO VII pp.114-116) as page images. The Brucaeus letter runs entirely on p.115 ('HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE...Rostochij 28 Octobris Anno Domini 1587'), bracketed by the end of the prior Tycho-to-Brucaeus letter on p.114 and an unrelated Danish/German letter beginning on p.116. Collated source_text against Dreyer's printed Latin word-for-word (long-s read visually, not via OCR) and found source_text a faithful modern-spelling transcription with no divergence. Collated published_english sentence-by-sentence against both source_text and the Dreyer image: opening line, closing line, date (28 October 1587), place (Rostochij/Rostock), and every proper name (Landgravium, Copernicus, Ptolemy, Hipparchus, Menelaus, Timocharis, Christophorus Rothmannus, Aristoteles) all correspond correctly with no invented, dropped, or altered facts. Hedged/elliptical Latin clauses (e.g. 'Ex hypothesi enim falsa verum sequi non dubito; scientiam autem fieri...') are rendered with clearly bracketed interpretive supplements, not silently hardened. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15870522001", "next": "brahe-t_15880920001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for copies of the Hven–Kassel correspondence and engages the evidence for stellar latitude changes linked to variation in the obliquity of the ecliptic.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15871028001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15871114001", "d": "1587-11-14", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 19 October 1587 [O.S.] (catalogued 14 November 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTVTE SAPIENTIA INGENIOSA ERVDITAQVE DOCTRINA, CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNVDSTRVP, EQVITI DANO, MATHEMATICORVM NOSTRO SECULO OMNIUM PRAeSTANTISSIMO, ETC. DOMINO SUO CUM OBSERUANTIA COLENDO, ETC. S. P.</p><p>PROXIMIS literis, Vir nobilissime ac excellentissime, memoria lapsus scripsi ad te, extremam superficiem Aëris crassioris ex Demonstratione Opticorum distare a superficie Terrae per Semidiametrum Terrae. Verum dum (de absurditate forte fortuna cogitans, quod nimirum eo praesupposito sequerentur perpetua Crepuscula,) ipsos Authores consulo, inuenio me ea, quae ante aliquot Annos in ijs legeram, non rite meminisse; nec integrum illud, quod putaui, esse Semidiametrum Terrae, sed sexagesimam nondum Semidiametri Terreni partem. Quapropter peto a te, vt quae praecipiti festinatione in Literis meis effutij potius quam scripsi, ea velis emendare, ac pro Semidiametro Terreno sexagesimam fere Semidiametri Terreni partem, ac pro duplicatione crassioris Aëris multiplicationem vbique in ea demonstratione ponere. Ita enim omnia constabunt, ac demonstratio mea hac ratione a fortiori confirmabitur. Schema quoque aliter in hunc modum formandum est.</p><p>Figur 10 </p><p>Scis enim lineas EAG et HCL ex centro et superficie Terrae ob immensitatem Caeli videri parallelas, vel potius vnam et eandem. Iam facile habes, quanta sit densitas crassioris Aëris circa Horizontem, quanta in Altitudine 30. Graduum. Connecte enim puncta XA et AO, centrum nimirum Terrae cum punctis intersectionum extremae superficiei crassioris Aëris in Horizonte et in Altitudine 30. Graduum. Iam igitur in Triangulo ACX, quoniam dantur duo latera, latus nimirum AX Partium 60. Min. 48. Secund. 50. et latus AC Partium 60. exacte, ex dictorum Opticorum demonstrationibus, et angulus ACX rectus per Hypothesin: datur itaque et tertium CX latus, densitas nimirum Aëris crassioris, per quam circa Horizontem formae Stellarum ad nostrum visum perueniunt. Sic et in Altitudine 30. Graduum, quoniam in Triangulo ACO itidem dantur duo latera AO et AC, cum Angulo ACO, qui per Hypothesin vltra rectum datur 30. Grad. hoc est, Graduum 120; datur igitur et tertium CO latus, distantia nimirum extremae superficiei Aëris crassioris in Altitudine 30. Graduum. Ita vides, hoc vnico argumento, quod nimirum Refractiones non durent vsque ad verticem, firmissime demonstrari, non esse diuersa Aetheris et Aëris Diaphana. Nec enim ipsi Optici negare possunt, quin praesupposito diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano necessarium sit, vt Refractio duret vsque ad verticem, vt ex Alhazeno lib. 7. et Vitellione lib. 10. P. 51. manifestum est. Ea quoque, quae Vitellio lib. 10. P. 55. de scintillatione Stellarum proposuit, si vera essent, pro me facerent. Si enim scintillatio Stellarum fieret propter diuaricationem formae in loco imaginis, sequeretur inde, formam illam non alibi quam in crassiore Aëre consistere posse. Nam Ventus vsque ad concauam superficiem Sphaerae Lunaris non ascendit, nec admodum, vt opinor, vltra crassiorem illum Aërem. Vix enim in puro illo Aëre Ventus exsistere poterit. Haec ad te hac vice perscribere volui, ne forte putares, (quod tamen vix mihi credendum erat,) demonstrationem meam eo infirmari, quod spatium illud crassioris Aëris iusto maius assumserim. Memoriae lapsum esse, facile animaduertes; nec tantum temporis tunc mihi sumere potui, vt ipsos Autores consulerem.</p><p>Vale igitur felicissime et optime, nobilissime et excellentissime Tycho, et de hoc sublimi negotio mecum conferre ne desine.<br>Cassellis 14. Calend. Nouemb. Anno 87.<br>T. T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and illustrious gentleman, eminent in virtue, wisdom, and ingenious and erudite learning, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight, the most outstanding of all the mathematicians of our age, etc., his lord to be honoured with all observance, etc. — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>In my last letter, most noble and most excellent Sir, my memory failing me, I wrote to you that — by the demonstration of the writers on optics — the outermost surface of the thicker air [<i>aër crassior</i>] is distant from the surface of the Earth by [one] semidiameter of the Earth. But while (chancing to reflect on the absurdity, namely that on that supposition perpetual twilight would follow) I consulted the authors themselves, I find that I did not rightly remember the things I had read in them some years ago; and that the figure I supposed was not a whole semidiameter of the Earth, but not yet a sixtieth part of the terrestrial semidiameter. Wherefore I ask of you that what I blurted out rather than wrote in my letter, in headlong haste, you would be willing to amend: namely, that in place of [one] terrestrial semidiameter you everywhere set, in that demonstration, about a sixtieth part of the terrestrial semidiameter, and in place of a doubling of the thicker air, a multiplication. For thus everything will stand firm, and my demonstration will by this reckoning be confirmed <i>a fortiori</i> [all the more strongly]. The diagram, too, is to be drawn differently, in this manner.</p><p>Figure 10</p><p>For you know that the lines EAG and HCL, [drawn] from the centre and from the surface of the Earth, appear — because of the immensity of the heaven — to be parallel, or rather one and the same. Now you readily have how great the density of the thicker air is around the horizon, and how great at an altitude of 30 degrees. For join the points X–A and A–O — namely the centre of the Earth with the points of intersection of the outermost surface of the thicker air at the horizon and at an altitude of 30 degrees. Now then, in the triangle ACX, since two sides are given — namely the side AX of 60 parts [degrees] 48 minutes 50 seconds, and the side AC of exactly 60 parts, from the demonstrations of the said writers on optics — and the angle ACX is right by hypothesis: there is therefore given also the third side, CX, namely the density of the thicker air, through which around the horizon the forms [images] of the stars come to our sight. So too at an altitude of 30 degrees: since in the triangle ACO likewise two sides AO and AC are given, together with the angle ACO, which by hypothesis is given as beyond a right angle by 30 degrees, that is, of 120 degrees; there is therefore given also the third side, CO — namely the distance of the outermost surface of the thicker air at an altitude of 30 degrees. Thus you see that by this one argument — namely, that the refractions do not last all the way to the zenith — it is most firmly demonstrated that the transparent media [<i>Diaphana</i>] of the aether and of the air are not distinct. For not even the writers on optics themselves can deny that, the transparent media of aether and of air being supposed distinct, it is necessary that refraction should last all the way to the zenith, as is plain from Alhazen, Book 7, and Witelo, Book 10, Proposition 51. Those things, too, which Witelo set forth in Book 10, Proposition 55, concerning the scintillation [twinkling] of the stars, were they true, would tell in my favour. For if the scintillation of the stars came about on account of the divergence of the form [image] at the place of the image, it would follow from this that that form could subsist nowhere but in the thicker air. For the wind does not rise up to the concave surface of the lunar sphere, nor much, as I think, beyond that thicker air. For scarcely will the wind be able to exist in that pure air. These things I wished to write out to you on this occasion, lest perhaps you should think (though indeed I could scarcely believe it of you) that my demonstration is weakened by the fact that I assumed that space of the thicker air greater than it should be. That a lapse of memory occurred you will easily perceive; nor could I then take so much time to myself as to consult the authors themselves.</p><p>Farewell then, most happily and most well, most noble and most excellent Tycho, and do not cease to confer with me about this sublime matter.<br>At Kassel, the 14th [day] before the Kalends of November, the year [15]87.<br>Yours truly [<i>Tuus Totus</i>],<br>Christoph Rothmann.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent / sender.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), mathematician and astronomer at the court of Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel; one of Tycho's principal scientific correspondents. The exchange of 1586–90 is the great early-modern debate on atmospheric refraction and the reality of the celestial \"spheres,\" printed by Tycho himself in the present edition.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe, Dominus de Knudstrup, Eques Danus</i> — Tycho identified by his patrimonial seat <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp, Scania) and his rank of <i>eques</i> (nobleman/knight); titulature kept in full per protocol §2.</li><li><b><i>S. P.</b></i> — <i>salutem plurimam</i> (sc. <i>dicit</i>), \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>T. T.</b></i> (subscription) — <i>Tuus Totus</i>, \"Wholly yours / Yours truly\"; expanded in the translation.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I), Uraniborg 1596 — the volume he printed at Hven; reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset (`witnesses:[]`).</li></ul><p><b>Subject and technical terms</b></p><ul><li><b>The letter is a correction.</b> Rothmann retracts a figure given in his <i>proxima epistola</i> (\"last letter\"): he had wrongly said the top of the <i>aër crassior</i> lies one Earth-semidiameter above the surface; the optical authorities in fact give \"not yet a sixtieth part\" of a semidiameter. He asks Tycho to substitute that value, and to replace <i>duplicatio</i> (\"a doubling\" of the thicker air) with <i>multiplicatio</i> (\"a multiplication\") throughout the demonstration.</li><li><b><i>aër crassior</b></i> → rendered <b>\"thicker air\"</b> throughout (the dense lower atmosphere, as against the <i>purus aër</i> / aether above). <i>Crepuscula</i> → <b>twilight</b> (protocol §5.1): the <i>reductio</i> is that a one-semidiameter atmosphere would yield <i>perpetua crepuscula</i>, \"perpetual twilight.\"</li><li><b><i>Refractio</b></i> → <b>refraction</b>; <b><i>Refractiones non durent usque ad verticem</b></i> → \"the refractions do not last all the way to the zenith\" — Rothmann's key empirical premise. <i>verticem</i> here = the <b>zenith</b> (the point overhead), kept as \"zenith\" per §5.1; not \"vertex/summit.\"</li><li><b><i>Diaphana Aetheris et Aëris</b></i> → <b>\"the transparent media of the aether and of the air.\"</b> <i>Diaphanum</i> (Alhazen/Witelo's <i>diaphanon</i>) is the period optical term for a transparent medium; rendered \"transparent medium,\" with the Latin flagged. Rothmann's thesis: aether and air are <b>not</b> optically distinct media (<i>non esse diuersa … Diaphana</i>) — a single negation in the source, reproduced as a single negation here.</li><li><b><i>formae Stellarum / forma … in loco imaginis</b></i> → <b>\"the forms [images] of the stars / the form at the place of the image.\"</b> <i>Forma</i> is the perspectivist term for the propagated optical species/image; glossed \"[image]\" in brackets, the Latin kept.</li><li><b><i>scintillatio Stellarum</b></i> → <b>scintillation (twinkling) of the stars</b>; <i>diuaricatio formae</i> → \"divergence/spreading-apart of the form [image].\"</li><li><b>Geometry.</b> The argument is a plane-trigonometric solution of two right/oblique triangles sharing the Earth-radius side AC. <i>Partium 60. Min. 48. Secund. 50.</i> = <b>60 degrees 48 minutes 50 seconds</b> (sexagesimal; <i>partes</i> = degrees here, the standard astronomical usage). <i>AC Partium 60. exacte</i> = \"AC of exactly 60 parts.\" The angle <i>ACO … ultra rectum … 30. Grad., hoc est, Graduum 120</i> = an angle of <b>120°</b> (a right angle exceeded by 30°). Letters of points (E, A, G, H, C, L, X, O) refer to <b>Figure 10</b>, the woodcut diagram of the printed edition; the source's placeholder line <i>\"Figur 10\"</i> (German caption) is preserved verbatim in the Source block and rendered \"Figure 10.\"</li><li><b>Authorities cited.</b> <i>Alhazenus, lib. 7</i> = <b>Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen)</b>, <i>De aspectibus / Perspectiva</i>, Book 7 (on refraction). <i>Vitellio, lib. 10. P. 51 … P. 55</i> = <b>Witelo</b> (Vitellio, fl. 13th c.), <i>Perspectiva</i>, Book 10, <b>Propositions 51 and 55</b> (<i>P.</i> = <i>propositio</i>); P. 55 on stellar scintillation. Both as printed in the Risner <i>Opticae thesaurus</i> (Basel 1572), Rothmann's working text.</li><li><b><i>a fortiori</b></i> — kept as a Latin term of art, glossed \"[all the more strongly].\"</li><li><b><i>Sphaerae Lunaris … concava superficies</b></i> → \"the concave surface of the lunar sphere\" (the traditional inner boundary of the Moon's orb / the top of the sublunary region), here invoked only as the upper limit of <i>ventus</i> (wind); not an endorsement of solid spheres — indeed the broader correspondence is where Tycho and Rothmann jointly dissolve them.</li></ul><p><b>Calendar / dateline (crux — see confidence)</b></p><ul><li><b>The dateline reads <i>\"Cassellis 14. Calend. Nouemb. Anno 87\"</i> = \"at Kassel, the 14th before the Kalends of November, 1587.\"</b> By inclusive Roman reckoning, <i>14 Kalendas Novembris</i> = <b>19 October 1587</b> (Kalends of November = 1 Nov.; 1 Nov. counts as day 1, so the 14th day before = 19 October). The header therefore leads with <b>19 October 1587 [O.S.]</b>, the date the source substantiates.</li><li><b>The dataset `label` (\"1587. 14. november. Kassel\") gives 14 November</b> — a modern catalogue date that does <b>not</b> match the Roman-calendar dateline and is appended in the header as \"(catalogued 14 November 1587).\" The \"14\" of the label has plainly been read off the numeral <i>\"14\"</i> in <i>\"14. Calend. Nouemb.\"</i> and miscopied as a day-of-month in November; this is a cataloguing artefact, not a second authority. Per protocol §3 the discrepancy is flagged, not silently reconciled, and the as-written date governs. (O.S.: Kassel/Hesse used the Julian calendar at this date.)</li><li><b><i>Cassellis</b></i> = <b>Kassel</b> (locative; protocol §5.7).</li></ul><p><b>Named cruxes</b></p><ul><li><b>Dateline label-vs-dateline discrepancy</b> (19 October as written vs. 14 November catalogued) — resolved in favour of the dateline, discrepancy explained above; this alone holds the grade below [A].</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and the technical argument are rendered securely and the negation count is exact; the one flagged matter is the dateline crux (Roman-calendar <i>14 Kal. Nov.</i> = 19 Oct., against the catalogue's 14 Nov.), handled per §3 rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (49-letter set with no Dreyer slice), so per brief this is audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Checked opening/closing titulature, every proper name (Alhazen, Vitello/Witelo lib.7/lib.10 P.51 and P.55), every number (60 parts 48 min 50 sec; 30 degrees/120 degrees), the date '14. Calend. Nouemb. Anno 87' against the English 'the 14th before the Kalends of November, the year 87', and every clause of the optical argument (aer crassior, the triangle ACX/ACO demonstration, the diaphanum-identity conclusion) sentence by sentence against published_english. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or flipped negation found; hedges ('vt opinor', 'quod tamen vix mihi credendum erat') are preserved. No Dreyer collation performed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15871011001", "next": "brahe-t_15880611001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870812001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15880611001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Corrects a misquotation in his previous letter: the writers on optics place the dense atmosphere's upper limit not at one terrestrial semidiameter but at a much smaller fraction.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15871114001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15871207001", "d": "1587-12-07", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Below", "ck": "Henrik Below", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Henrik Below — Uraniborg, 7 December 1587 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery, with Latin code-switches; Danish address-line) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH VELBYRDIG MANDT HEINRICH BELOFF THILL SPÖTTROP KONGL. MAYT. BEFALNINGS-MANDTT PAA SKIFFHUSZ MIN KERE SWOGER OCH SYNDERLIG GODE VEN GANSKE VENLIG THILSKREFFUEN.</p><p>MEINEN freuntlichen grues mitt wunschung alles guettes alzeitt beforr. Edler, Ehrnvester, freundtlicher lieber Schwager vnd besonder vortraweter freundt. Neben Dancksagung fur vielfeltige erzeigete wolthaten, kan ich dir freundtlicher wolmeinung nicht verhalten, das ich dein schreiben habe entpfangen vnd darinne ein Copie des Durchleuchtigen Hochgebornen Fursten vnd Herren, Hertzog VLRICHS zu Mechelburg an dir geschreibene breiffs, worauss ich erfahre, das ihr furstliche Gnade begehrett von mir gnedichlich zu wissen, welcher meines erachtens von den beiden Prognosticatoribus TOBIA MOLLER vnd ANDREA ROSA dem Zeill neher zutreffet, ihn dem daß der eine ihn diesem zukunfftigen 88 Jhar den Regenten des Jhars Iovem vnd Venerem, der ander Saturnum vnd Martem setzet, darahn sie nicht alleine keins theils einig sein, sondern wie ihr Furstliche Gnade schreibet, gahr widerwertiger meinung haben. Dan der eine macht beide beneficos Planetas, der ander beide maleficos (wie sie die Astrologi nennen) zum Regenten im selbigen Jhar, welchs gar contrarie bedeuttung bringett. Hierauff kan ich dir freundlicher meinung nicht bergen, das wiewoll ich in die Astrologische Sachen, welche bedeuttung auß dem gestirn herholen vnd weißagunge tractiren, mich nicht gerne einlasse, dieweill darauff nicht vhill zu bawen ist, Sondern allein die Astronomiam, welche den wunderlichen lauff des gestirns erforschett, in einen gewissen vnd rechtmeßigen ordnung zu bringen mich etzliche Jhar her bemuhe, dan darahn kan durch rechtgeschaffene Instrumenten nach Geometrisch vnd Arithmetisch grundt vnd gewisheit die eigentliche warheitt durch langwirigen fleiß vnd arbeitt gefunden werden, So habe ich doch nach ihrer Furstlicher gnaden begerung beide Prognostica, die du mihr zuschickest (die ich doch nicht, wie du gemeinett hast, zuvor gehatt habe, dan ich niemals pflege solche practicen wider zu kauffen, noch zu lesen, ne bonas horas male collocem) durchgesehen, den mangel, worahn es hafftett, das sie so widerwertige Iudicia stellen, daraus zu suchen, vnd befinde, das sie in ihre Rechnung gar vnterscheidliche fundament gebraucht haben. Dan der eine, nemlich MOLLERUS, bawett sein Calculation auf des hocherfarnen COPERNICI rechnung, der ander, ROSA, auff die alte durch des Königs ALPHONSI in Hispanien liberalitet gemachte Tabeln, die man darumb die Alphonsinische nennett. Hirauß kömpt es, das der eine den anfang des Jhars in aequinoctio verno setzet ahm 10 tag Martij bey Neun vhr nach mittage, der ander ahm selbigen tag, aber vmb 2 Stunden nach der vorgehende Mitternacht, daß also zwischen beide ihre rechnung schier 19 gantze Stunden verlauffen, in welchen der himmel sich gar vil vorendern thutt, vnd kan gar ein ander Astrologisch Iudicium darauß fallen, eben so wol als wen dar ein gantzes Jhar oder noch mehr zwischen wehre: Das darumb nicht zuvorwundern ist, das diese beide Astrologi in domino Anni nicht vberein stimmen, weil sie den auß der Figura Coeli introitus Solis in Arietem, wan daß vorbemelte aequinoctium vernum geschicht, pflegen herholen. Wiewol es auch lichtlich geschehn kan, das wan sie schon gleichmeßige Tabeln vnd rechnungen volgeten, das sie gleichwoll in Dominis Anni vnd ihren gantzen weissagungen gar widerwertige meinungen können fürgeben, das darauß leichtlich zu probiren ist, daß wan man hundertt der Prognosticken lisset, so befindett sich doch gahr selten, das einer mitt dem andern concordierit, dan sie bawen nicht alle auff gleichformige grundt ihn ihren Iudicijs vnd haben vntherscheidliche proceß vnd ahnleitungen. Es sein auch diese Astrologische weissagungen wie ein cothurnus, den man kan auff ein jeder Bein ziehen, gros vnd klein, wie man will. Darumb ich auch niemals dar von ettwas Sonderlichs gehalten habe. Das ich aber Kong. May. meinen Gnedigsten Herren jhärlich ein solches Astrologisch Prognosticon untherteniglich zustelle, mus ich in dem nach ihre May. befell vnd willen thun, wiewoll ich selbst nicht vill darauf halte vnd nicht gerne mitt solchen zweiffelhafftigen praedictionibus vmbgehe, darin man die eigenttliche warheitt nicht durchauß erforschen mag, wie sonst in Geometria vnd Arithmetica, darauff die Astronomia durch hulff der vleißigen observation ihm lauff des Himels gebawet wirtt. Dennoch dieweill ihr Furstliche Gnade gnedichlich begertt von mihr zu wissen, welchen von den beiden ich beifellig sey, was den dominis Anni also widerwertiger weiß von ihnen gestellet thutt ahnlangen, So kan ich hierauff nicht anders sagen, dan das ihrer beiden rechnung vnd iudicium gehtt auß ein vormeinten vnrichtigen grundt. Dan weder die Alphonsinischen, noch die Copernianischen Tabeln, welchen sie folgen, geben den justen lauff der Sonnen, wie er ahn sich selbst am Himmel geschichtt, vnd ist hierinne kein geringe vnterscheidt, wie auß meiner eigenen Restitution vnd vornewrung in Rechnung des lauffs der Sonnen zusehen ist, welche ich auf etzliche vorgehende Jharen durch große vnd rechtmeßige Instrumenten augenscheinlich vom Himel selbst her ab, durch fleißige observation vnd warnemungen genomen habe, auß welchen sich befindet, das des Jhars Anfang in aequinoctio verno, darauß die Astrologi ihre vrtheil nehmen, geschicht ahm 1O tag Martij 8⅔ stunde nach der vorigen Mitternacht, welchs bey 7 stunden speder ist, als ANDREAS ROSA gesetzt hat, und 12 stunden fruer, als TOBIAS MOLLERUS meinett, darauß den vhil ein andere Constitution des himels zur Zeitt des aequinoctij einfeltt, als ein jetzlicher von ihnen gefunden hat, worauff auch ein ander vrtheill folgett, vnd auch wol andere domini Anni, wie sie es nennen (darauß doch nicht vhil zuholen ist), mögen gesetz werden. Was aber mene meinung sey ahnlangende Astrologische gitzung vber dis kunfftige 88 Jhar, habe ich Königl. May. meinen gnedigsten Herren ihn einen geschriebenen Prognostick vnterteniglich auffgezeignett, welchs ich auch ihre Furstliche Gnade gerne wolte vnterteniglich mitteilen, aber ich habe keine vberige Exemplar dar von, wan ihr Furstliche Gnade lesset bey ihr May. darumb ahnlangen, wird ihr Furst. Gna. wol ein abschrifft dar von bekomen. Ich bin darinne gentzlich nicht der meinung, daß solche gahr große vorenderunge in diesen negstkunftigen Jhar vorhanden sein, als die Astrologi auß etzlichen altten reimen, die sie den Regiomontano zumessen, furgeben, dan ich befinde im Constitution des Himels keine Sonderliche vrsachen darzu, sondern achte, das dis kunfftige Jhar wird den vorgehenden gleichmeßig sein, vnd in zimlichen guten wesen, in allen Sachen, sich erzeigen, aleine wo zuuor krieg vnd vneinigkeitt aufferwecktt ist, dar möchte es wol ettwas weitter einreißen. Vnd kan ihr Furstliche gnade meine meinung vom Astrologischen iudicio vber das gantze Jhar auß vorbemelten Prognostico, welchs ich Köng. May. meinen gnedigsten herren vnterteniglich habe zugesteltt, weitter erfahren. Dis habe ich auff dein guttwilliges schreiben vnd beger nicht wöllen vntherlassen zu antworten. Bitte gar deinstlich, du wollest vnbeschwert sein vnd mitt erster gelegenheitt ihr Furstliche Gnade hierauff meine antwortt vnd meinunge vnterteniglichen von meinett wegen zuvorstehn geben. Worin ich sonst ihre Furstliche gnaden zu willen vnd gefallen sein kan, bin ich alzeitt mit aller deinstlichkeitt vnterteniglich erböttig.</p><p>Lieber Schwager, nach dem ich so gutte gelegenheitt habe, kan ich nicht vnterlassen, dich in einer Sachen, darahn mihr vhil gelegen ist, vnd darinne du mihr wol itzunder konnest behulfflich sein, zu besuchen, vnd gebe dir darumb gantz freundtlich zuwissen, das nach dem ich itzunder in meiner eigenen alhie angerichten Druckerey ein Astronomisk Buch lasse außgehn, welchs zimlich groß wird fallen, vnd mihr baldt Druckpapir darzu vonnötten mangeln wirt, vmb wes wegen ich meine botschafft auff vhil örten außgehabt, vnd gleichwol keines der größe, wie zu dem wercke vonnöten, bekommen können, habe ich darumb ergistern widerumb im Land zu Mechelburg dem lassen nachstellen, dieweill ich vormercke, das alda zu Graubow vnd Neustat zwey gutte papir Mölen vorhanden sein, vnd habe die beide Ambtleuthe ahn den selbigen örtern, nemlich IOHAN HELDORP vnd STELLAN WAKENITZ, zugeschrieben, das sie wolten mihr so vhil zugefallen sein, vnd den papirmachern alda befehl geben, daß sie mihr mitt dem Allerersten etzliche ballen Druckpapir nach der größe des vbergeschichten forms wolten forfertigen, welchs ich dan zu danck ihnen will lassen bezahlen, so baldt ich es holen lasse. Aber die weil ich diesen beiden Amptleutten villicht mach vnbekantt sein, ist mein gantz fleißige bitte ahn dir, du woltest so woll thun vnd ihnen von meinet wegen hierumbe auch zuschreiben, das sie mihr darinne wolten zugefallen sein, vnd du wollest auch so woll thun vnd einen von deinen gutten bekanten ahm Furstlichen hoffe zuschreiben, das er bey meinen Gnedigsten Hern Hertzog VLRICH wolte von meinett wegen vnthertenigen ahnlangung thun, das auß ihrer Furstlich Gnaden authoritet vnd befehl alles möchte mitt dem befurderlichsten zugehn, das ich mitt erster Muglichkeit könte ein 14 oder 15 Ballen papir alda auff ihr Furstliche Gnaden papirmöllen beckomen, nach solcher größe und gestalt, wie ich dir hier neben ein proba schicke. Ich will gerne bezalen, was die papirmeister darfur haben sollen. Vnd du woltest darneben auch lassen vnterteniglich befördern, das ihr Furst. Gn. wolte mihr gnedichlich furgunnen, wan das papir fertig vnd bezalt ist, das es möchte von einen Amptman zum andem biß gen Rostock gefurett werden, vnd alda den Hern Doctori HENRICO BRUCAeO vberlübert, das ichs von dannen mitt dem allerersten schiff möchte hierein beckommen. Wan du mihr hierinne in der gestaltt zu gefallen sein woltest, wie ich auch ahn deinem gutten willen keinen zueifel trage, vnd mihr dis zuwege bringen, beide bey ihr Furstlichen Gnaden selbst, vnd auch bey den Amptleutten, thetest du mir einen sonderlichen deinst vnd wolgefallen dar mitt. Dan mihr gahr viell daran gelegen ist, das ich mitt dem ersten solchs papir in einer gutten menge beckomen möchte. Vnd so ich in einiger sachen von meinen vormuegen dier jemals widerumb zu deinst und danckbarckeitt sein kan, wil ich alzeitt mehr dan guttwillig gefunden werden, vnd thue dich hiermitt sambt deiner lieben haußfrawen dem Almechtigen Gott befehlen. Du wollest auch meiner lieben Mutterschuester Fraw Maren und deiner lieben Hausfrawe, ihrer tochter, von meinett wegen vhil gutternacht sagen.</p><p>Datum Vraniborg den 7 Decemb. Anno etc. 87.<br>Tui amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE, Manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born man Henrik Below of Spøttrup, His Royal Majesty's prefect at Skivehus, my dear brother-in-law and especially good friend, written in all friendliness.</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with the wish of all good things, at all times beforehand. Noble, honour-firm, friendly dear brother-in-law and particularly trusted friend. Besides my thanks for the manifold kindnesses you have shown, I cannot withhold from you, in friendly good will, that I have received your letter and in it a copy of the letter written to you by the Most Serene, High-born Prince and Lord, Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, from which I learn that His Princely Grace desires graciously to know from me which of the two prognosticators, in my judgement, comes nearer the mark — Tobias Möller and Andreas Rosa — in that the one sets Jupiter and Venus as the rulers of this coming year [']88, the other Saturn and Mars; in which they are not only in no part agreed, but, as His Princely Grace writes, hold quite contrary opinions. For the one makes both the beneficent planets [<i>beneficos Planetas</i>], the other both the malefic ones [<i>maleficos</i>] (as the astrologers call them) ruler in the selfsame year, which brings quite a contrary signification. On this I cannot conceal from you, in friendly intent, that — although I do not gladly involve myself in astrological matters, which fetch their significations out of the stars and treat of soothsayings, since not much is to be built upon them, but for some years now have laboured solely to bring astronomy, which investigates the wondrous course of the stars, into a sure and well-grounded order, for therein the proper truth can be found, by long-lasting diligence and labour, through rightly-fashioned instruments on a geometric and arithmetic foundation and certainty — I have nonetheless, at His Princely Grace's request, gone through both the prognostica that you sent me (which, however, I had not, as you supposed, had before, for I never am wont to buy such practica back, nor to read them, lest I spend good hours ill [<i>ne bonas horas male collocem</i>]), in order to seek out from them the defect in which it lies that they set down such contrary judgements [<i>Iudicia</i>]; and I find that they have used quite different foundations in their reckoning. For the one, namely Möller, builds his calculation upon the reckoning of the highly-experienced Copernicus; the other, Rosa, upon the old tables made through the liberality of King Alfonso in Spain, which one therefore calls the Alfonsine. From this it comes that the one sets the beginning of the year at the vernal equinox on the 10th day of March at about nine o'clock in the afternoon, the other on the same day, but at about 2 hours after the preceding midnight, so that thus between the two reckonings nearly 19 whole hours run by, in which the heaven alters very much, and a quite different astrological judgement can fall out from it, just as much as if a whole year or more lay between: so that it is not therefore to be wondered at that these two astrologers do not agree on the ruler of the year [<i>domino Anni</i>], because they are wont to derive it from the figure of the heaven [<i>Figura Coeli</i>] at the entry of the Sun into Aries, when the aforesaid vernal equinox occurs. Although it can also easily happen that, even were they to follow tables and reckonings that matched, they could nonetheless put forth quite contrary opinions in the rulers of the year [<i>Dominis Anni</i>] and in their whole soothsayings — which is easily to be proved from this, that when one reads a hundred of the prognostica, it is yet found very seldom that one agrees [<i>concordierit</i>] with another, for they do not all build upon a uniform foundation in their judgements and have different procedures and methods. These astrological soothsayings are also like a buskin [<i>cothurnus</i>] that one can draw onto any leg, big and small, as one will. For which reason, too, I have never held anything special of them. But that I yearly submit such an astrological prognosticon humbly to His Royal Majesty, my Most Gracious Lord — in that I must act according to His Majesty's command and will, although I myself hold not much of it and do not gladly deal with such doubtful predictions [<i>praedictionibus</i>], in which one may not thoroughly investigate the proper truth, as otherwise in geometry and arithmetic, upon which astronomy is built by the help of diligent observation in the course of the heaven. Nonetheless, since His Princely Grace graciously desires to know from me to which of the two I incline — as concerns the rulers of the year [<i>dominis Anni</i>] thus set down by them in contrary manner — I can say nothing else upon this than that the reckoning and judgement of both of them proceeds from a supposed faulty foundation. For neither the Alfonsine nor the Copernican tables, which they follow, give the true [<i>justen</i>] course of the Sun as it occurs in itself in the heaven; and herein is no small difference, as is to be seen from my own restoration [<i>Restitution</i>] and renewal in the reckoning of the course of the Sun, which I have, over several preceding years, taken visibly down from the heaven itself by large and rightful instruments, through diligent observation and observings; from which it is found that the beginning of the year at the vernal equinox, from which the astrologers take their judgement, occurs on the 10th day of March, 8⅔ hours after the previous midnight, which is about 7 hours later than Andreas Rosa has set, and 12 hours earlier than Tobias Möller supposes; from which then a very different constitution of the heaven falls out at the time of the equinox than each of them has found, upon which also a different judgement follows, and indeed other rulers of the year [<i>domini Anni</i>], as they call them (out of which, however, not much is to be fetched), may be set down. But what my opinion may be concerning the astrological estimate over this coming year ['] 88, I have humbly noted down for His Royal Majesty, my most gracious Lord, in a written prognosticon, which I would also gladly impart humbly to His Princely Grace; but I have no spare copy of it. If His Princely Grace will make application to His Majesty for it, His Princely Grace will well obtain a transcript of it. Therein I am altogether not of the opinion that such very great changes are at hand in this next-coming year, as the astrologers put forth out of certain old rhymes which they ascribe to Regiomontanus; for I find in the constitution of the heaven no special causes for it, but reckon that this coming year will be like the preceding ones, and will show itself in fairly good condition in all things — only, where war and discord have been stirred up before, there it might well break in somewhat further. And His Princely Grace can learn more fully my opinion of the astrological judgement over the whole year from the aforesaid prognosticon which I have humbly submitted to His Royal Majesty, my most gracious Lord. This I would not omit to answer upon your kindly letter and request. I beg quite dutifully that you would be unburdened [by it] and at the first opportunity convey to His Princely Grace, upon this, my answer and opinion humbly on my behalf. Wherein I can otherwise be at His Princely Grace's will and pleasure, I am at all times humbly ready with all dutifulness.</p><p>Dear brother-in-law, since I have so good an opportunity, I cannot omit to call upon you in a matter that much concerns me, and in which you can well be of help to me just now; and I therefore make known to you quite in friendliness that, since I am now letting an astronomical book go forth from my own printing-house set up here, which will turn out fairly large, and I shall soon lack printing-paper for it — on which account I have had my errand out in many places, and yet could obtain none of the size as is needed for the work — I have therefore the day before yesterday again had inquiry made for it in the land of Mecklenburg, since I observe that there at Grabow and Neustadt two good paper-mills are to be found; and I have written to the two officials at those same places, namely Johan Heldorp and Stellan Wakenitz, that they would do me so much favour and give order to the papermakers there that they should at the very first prepare for me several bales of printing-paper according to the size of the form sent over [to them], which I will then thankfully have paid to them as soon as I have it fetched. But since I am perhaps still unknown to these two officials, my quite diligent request to you is that you would be so good as to write to them too on my behalf about this, that they would do me the favour therein; and that you would also be so good as to write to one of your good acquaintances at the Princely court, that he would on my behalf make humble application to my Most Gracious Lord Duke Ulrich, that out of His Princely Grace's authority and command everything might proceed with all furtherance, so that I might at the first possibility obtain there at His Princely Grace's paper-mills some 14 or 15 bales of paper, of such size and form as I send you a sample [<i>proba</i>] herewith. I will gladly pay what the paper-masters are to have for it. And you would besides also have it humbly furthered that His Princely Grace would graciously grant me, when the paper is finished and paid for, that it might be carried from one official to another as far as Rostock, and there delivered over to Master Doctor Heinrich Brucaeus, so that from there I might obtain it in here by the very first ship. If you would be willing to do me favour in this manner herein — as I bear no doubt of your good will — and bring this about for me, both with His Princely Grace himself and also with the officials, you would do me a special service and pleasure thereby. For it much concerns me that I might at the first obtain such paper in a good quantity. And if in any matter within my power I can ever again be of service and gratitude to you, I will at all times be found more than willing; and I herewith commend you, together with your dear housewife, to Almighty God. You would also, on my behalf, say many a goodnight to my dear mother's-sister Mistress Maren and to your dear housewife, her daughter.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 7th of December, in the year etc. [']87.<br>Your most loving,<br>Tycho Brahe, with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / hand.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from Uraniborg on Hven. The subscription <i>Manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand\") asserts an autograph signature.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Heinrich Beloff / Henrik Below</i> (Below of Spøttrup, d. 1606), Danish nobleman, here styled <i>Kongl. Mayt. Befalningsmandt paa Skiffhusz</i> — His Royal Majesty's <i>lensmand</i> (prefect/governor) at <b>Skivehus</b> (Skive, Jutland). Repeatedly addressed as <i>Schwager</i> — <b>brother-in-law</b> in the period's loose sense (a relation by marriage); the close kin link is made explicit at the close, where Tycho greets <i>meiner lieben Mutterschuester Fraw Maren</i> (\"my dear mother's-sister Mistress Maren\") and <i>deiner lieben Hausfrawe, ihrer tochter</i> (\"your dear housewife, her daughter\") — i.e. Below's wife is the daughter of Tycho's maternal aunt, making them cousins-by-marriage. <i>Spøttrop / Spøttrup</i> and <i>Skiffhusz / Skivehus</i> kept as the period forms with modern glosses.</li><li><b>Address-line language.</b> The opening superscription is in <b>chancery Danish</b> (<i>ERLIG OCH VELBYRDIG MANDT … MIN KERE SWOGER … GANSKE VENLIG THILSKREFFUEN</i>, \"to the honourable and well-born man … my dear brother-in-law … written in all friendliness\"); the letter body is <b>Early New High German</b>. The code-switch is preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Occasion (per Dreyer,</b> Opera Omnia <i>and</i> Tycho Brahe<i>, p. 155).<b> Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg — father of Queen Sophie of Denmark, hence Tycho's interest — had procured two prognostica for 1588 and, finding them irreconcilable, asked Below to have Tycho adjudicate. </b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</i><i> (1527–1603); </i>Hertzog VLRICH zu Mechelburg / Mecklenburg<i>. </i>Ihr Furstliche Gnade<i> = \"His Princely Grace\" (the Duke); </i>Kong. May. / Königl. May.* = His Royal Majesty (Frederik II of Denmark), Tycho's patron, to whom he owed an annual prognostication.</li><li><b>The two astrologers.</b> <i>Tobias Möller</i> (<i>Tobia Moller / Mollerus</i>) and <i>Andreas Rosa</i> (<i>Andrea Rosa</i>), authors of competing printed prognostica for 1588. Tycho's diagnosis: Möller computed on <b>Copernicus</b>'s numbers (<i>COPERNICI rechnung</i>), Rosa on the <b>Alfonsine Tables</b> (<i>die Alphonsinische</i>, \"made through the liberality of King Alfonso [X] in Spain\"); their equinox times differed by nearly 19 hours, hence the contrary \"rulers of the year.\"</li><li><b>Astrology — technical terms (protocol §5.3).</b> <i>Prognosticon / Prognostica / practica / practicen</i> — yearly astrological forecasts. <i>Regenten des Jhars / dominus Anni / domini Anni</i> → <b>ruler(s) of the year</b> (the planet(s) governing an annual revolution), a term Tycho flags as their jargon (<i>wie sie es nennen</i>, \"as they call them\"). <i>beneficos Planetas / maleficos</i> → the <b>beneficent / malefic planets</b> (Jupiter, Venus vs. Saturn, Mars); kept as Latin in source, glossed. <i>Figura Coeli</i> → the <b>figure of the heaven</b> (the horoscope chart cast for the moment of the Sun's <i>introitus … in Arietem</i>, the Sun's entry into <b>Aries</b> at the vernal equinox). <i>Iudicium / Iudicia / iudicio</i> → <b>judgement(s)</b> (the astrological verdict). <i>Astrologische gitzung</i> → rendered \"astrological estimate/conjecture\"; <i>gitzung</i> (cf. <i>Gissung</i>, conjecture/guess) is a non-standard chancery form, read as \"estimate.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy (protocol §5.1).</b> <i>aequinoctium vernum / aequinoctio verno</i> → <b>vernal equinox</b>. <i>Restitution vnd vornewrung</i> → <b>restoration and renewal</b> (of the Sun's theory) — Tycho's <i>restitutio / instauratio astronomiae</i> programme; here applied to his corrected solar tables. <i>Instrumenten … nach Geometrisch vnd Arithmetisch grundt</i> → <b>instruments … on a geometric and arithmetic foundation</b>. <i>observation vnd warnemungen</i> → \"observation and observings\" (<i>Wahrnehmungen</i>, observations/percepts), the doubled term kept.</li><li><b>The solar-equinox figures (Tycho's central technical claim).</b> The two almanac-makers placed the 1588 vernal equinox on 10 March, one \"at about nine o'clock in the afternoon [p.m.],\" the other \"at about 2 hours after the preceding midnight\" — ~19 hours apart. Tycho's own observationally-grounded value: <b>10 March, 8⅔ hours after the previous midnight</b> — \"about 7 hours later than Andreas Rosa has set, and 12 hours earlier than Tobias Möller supposes.\" (The 8⅔ h figure is printed <i>1O</i> with a capital-O for the numeral in the source's \"1O tag Martij\"; reproduced as printed and read as \"10th day of March.\")</li><li><b><i>Regiomontano … altten reimen</b></i> — the \"old rhymes ascribed to <b>Regiomontanus</b>\": the notorious prophetic verses on 1588 as a year of vast upheaval (the <i>\"octogesimus octavus … annus mirabilis\"</i> prophecy widely circulated under Regiomontanus's name). Tycho discounts it, finding \"no special causes\" in the heaven, expecting an ordinary year — except where war is already kindled, which \"might break in somewhat further.\"</li><li><b><i>cothurnus</b></i> — a <b>buskin</b> (the loose-fitting tragic actor's boot that fits either foot); a stock simile for something accommodated to any view. Tycho: the prognostica are \"like a buskin one can draw onto any leg.\" Latin term kept, glossed.</li><li><b>The book / paper request.</b> <i>ein Astronomisk Buch … in meiner eigenen … Druckerey</i> — \"an astronomical book [from] my own printing-house\": the volume Tycho was preparing on the press at Uraniborg, requiring large quantities of <i>Druckpapir</i> (printing-paper) of a specific <i>form</i> (sheet size), of which he encloses a <i>proba</i> (sample). He seeks <b>14–15 bales</b> from the ducal paper-mills at <b>Grabow</b> (<i>Graubow</i>) and <b>Neustadt</b> (<i>Neustat</i>) in Mecklenburg, via the officials <i>Johan Heldorp</i> and <i>Stellan Wakenitz</i> (<i>Ambtleuthe / Amptleutte</i> — bailiffs/officials), with the finished paper to be relayed <i>biß gen Rostock</i> (to <b>Rostock</b>) and there delivered to <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (<i>Hern Doctori HENRICO BRUCAeO</i>, the Rostock professor, protocol §5.6) for shipment to Hven.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches in the German body</b> (kept in source, glossed in translation): <i>ne bonas horas male collocem</i> (\"lest I spend good hours ill\"); <i>beneficos Planetas / maleficos</i>; <i>contrarie</i>; <i>Iudicia / iudicio</i>; <i>Figura Coeli</i>, <i>introitus Solis in Arietem</i>, <i>aequinoctium vernum</i>, <i>domino/domini/dominis Anni</i>; <i>concordierit</i>, <i>proceß</i>; <i>praedictionibus</i>, <i>observation</i>, <i>Geometria / Arithmetica</i>, <i>Astronomia</i>, <i>Constitution</i>, <i>Restitution</i>; the subscription <i>Tui amantissimus … Manu propria</i>.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Vraniborg den 7 Decemb. Anno etc. 87</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 7 December [15]87\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The catalogue label (\"1587. 7. december. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. The internal date <i>10 March</i> / equinox for \"this coming 88 year\" is likewise O.S. — note that on the Julian calendar the vernal equinox fell near 10 March, consistent with the figures discussed; not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German chancery prose and the Latin/Danish code-switches are rendered securely, with the technical astrological vocabulary held to the controlled glossary. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) <i>Astrologische gitzung</i> is a non-standard spelling read as \"estimate/conjecture\" (<i>Gissung</i>); (2) the precise sense of <i>Schwager</i> and the exact kin-degree (cousin-by-marriage via <i>Mutterschuester Maren</i>) is inferred from the close, not stated outright. No negation count was altered; Tycho's many epistemic hedges (<i>nicht vill darauf halte</i>, <i>meines erachtens</i>, <i>vormeinten vnrichtigen grundt</i>) are preserved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full letter (source_text and published_english) collated line by line against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.116-119 page images (letter runs complete within this range; p.120 in the slice is the opening of a different, undated Hagecius letter and is not part of this text). German source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim throughout, including the two OCR-vulnerable numeric passages -- Moller/Rosa's equinox times (9pm vs. 2am, a 19-hour gap) and Tycho's own restored equinox (10 March, 8 2/3 hours after midnight, correctly rendered as ~7 hours later than Rosa and ~12 hours earlier than Moller, both arithmetically verified) -- and all proper names/places (Tobias Moller, Andreas Rosa, Copernicus, King Alfonso, Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Johan Heldorp, Stellan Wakenitz, Grabow, Neustadt, Heinrich Brucaeus, Rostock, Henrik Below/Spottrup/Skivehus), the paper quantity (14 or 15 bales), and the closing date (7 December 1587). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text departure from Dreyer found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Addresses the question, forwarded from Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, of whether Tobias Möller's or Andreas Rosa's assignment of planetary rulers for 1588 is correct.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15871207001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880503001", "d": "1588-05-03", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 3 May 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.62</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ERUDITISSIMOQUE VIRO D. DOCTORI THADDAeO HAGECIO AB HAYK, MEDICO ET MATHEMATICO EXCELLENTISSIMO, AMICO SUO SINCERE DILECTO.<br>PRAG.</p><p>RARIORES ad te dare literas internunciorum cogit penuria, eruditissime charissimeque D. D. THADDAeE, et locorum quibus disiungimur intercapedo vota nostra plurimum impedit. Nactus itaque iam tandem aliqualem occasionem literas mittendi per Studiosum quendam Silesium me unâ cum quibusdam alijs hîc invisentem, non intermittendum duxi, quin brevibus ad te scriberem. Librum tuum de Nova Stella et Cometis misi iamdudum Francofurtum ad eundem hominem, de quo mihi indicâras, nec dubito te interea temporis eum recepisse. Quod si illum edere adhuc animus est, suaderem ut ea, quae in Cometa anni 77 minus considerate a te proposita sunt, vel supprimas vel emendes. Meum de hoc eodem Cometa iudicium percipies ex eo quem tibi nunc mitto libro, in quo secundus tomus operis nostri de recentioribus aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis continetur. Primus est de Stella Nova exquisitiori diligentia quam antea considerata, quem typis absolvere Gallici privilegij defectus, quod Caesariano adiungere constitui, hactenus impedivit. Nunc vero quoniam ante paucos dies idipsum adeptus sum, nihil obstabit nisi otij oportunitas, quominus eius etiam libri evulgationem adornem. Tertius est de tribus alijs Cometis, qui postmodum nobis illuxerunt et in alto aethere curriculum suum non minus quam ille, de quo liber modo transmissus agit, absolverunt. Quod autem in hoc ipso libro, ubi tua placita in eodem Cometa sub disquisitionem mathematicam voco, a te multoties dissentiri me res ipsa cogat, non est quod sinistrius interpreteris; scis enim me solo veritatis amore et libertate Philosophica idipsum agere, nulloque tua insectandi elevandique animo id a me fieri. In priori libro de Stella Nova tum etiam in tertio, ubi de Cometa anni 80 agitur, oportuniorem invenio commoditatem tecum magna ex parte sentiendi, tuaque dignis encomijs extollendi. Si quos habes Italicos, Hispanicos vel Gallicos authores, qui de Nova Stella aliquid commentati sunt, rogo ut eorum scripta Latinitate, si fieri possit, donata, mecum communices, unâque si quos alios Cometarum praecedentibus aliquot annis visorum descriptores peculiares nec ubique obvios in promtu habueris. Librum illum de Cometa anni 1531 ab APIANO seorsim editum nondum vidi, quemadmodum neque IOHANNIS PENAe Galli scriptum, quod PRAeTORIUS in descriptione huius Cometae anni 77 citat. Si horum me participem reddideris, meis votis plurimum subveneris. Memini me librum quendam Astrologicum a te dono accepisse, cum Ratisbonae simul essemus, qui Planetarum influentias peculiari quadam ratione per accessum ad Terram vel ab ea recessum proponeret; eum in patriam reversus nuspiam invenire potui. Si eius aliquod exemplar denuo per te obtinuere, diligentius illud inposterum conservabo, evolvamque accuratius; existimo enim quaedam in eo a veritate non dissentanea contineri, si dextre considerentur, et cum caeteris requisitis debite conferantur.</p><p>Ubi totum opus, de quo supra dixi, typis meis excusum fuerit, tibi illud transmittam. Interea hanc secundam partem per otium evolve, mihique tuum de ea iudicium ingenue significa. Invenies in eo novam orbium coelestium dispositionem, a me non ita dudum adinventam, quae quo penitius fuerit considerata, eo magis tibi placebit. Ego hanc non solum apparentijs consonam, sed etiam in rei veritate ita se habere non dubito, quod Ptolomaicae et Copernianae assumtiones nequaquam praestant. Sed de his nunc plura scribere temporis penuria et epistolae leges prohibent. Tu itaque, optime THADDAeE, quam felicissime vale et ingravescentis aetatis difficultates forti animo tolera, meque per occasionem quamcunque oblatam literis revisere non intermitte.</p><p>Iterum vale.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi die 3. Maij Anno 1588.<br>Tuus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, most excellent physician and mathematician, his sincerely beloved friend.</b><br>Prague.</p><p>A scarcity of message-bearers compels me to send you letters too rarely, most learned and most dear Master Doctor Thaddaeus, and the distance of the places that separate us greatly hinders our wishes. Having therefore at last obtained some opportunity of sending a letter, by way of a certain Silesian student who is visiting me here together with some others, I thought it not to be neglected, but that I should write to you briefly. Your book on the New Star and on Comets I sent long ago to Frankfurt, to the same man of whom you had told me, and I do not doubt that you have received it in the meantime. But if you still have a mind to publish it, I would advise that those things which you proposed somewhat less carefully concerning the comet of the year [15]77 you either suppress or amend. My own judgement on this same comet you will gather from the book I now send you, in which is contained the second volume of our work on the more recent phenomena of the aethereal World. The first [volume] is on the New Star, examined with more exquisite diligence than before; and the lack of the French privilege — which I have resolved to add to the Imperial one — has hitherto hindered me from completing it in print. But now, since a few days ago I obtained that very thing, nothing will stand in the way, save the opportunity of leisure, of my preparing the publication of that book also. The third [volume] is on three other comets, which afterwards shone forth to us and ran their course in the high aether no less than that one which the book just sent treats of. But as for the fact that in this very book — where I summon your opinions on that same comet under a mathematical examination — the matter itself compels me to dissent from you on many points, there is no reason for you to interpret it amiss; for you know that I do this from love of truth alone and from philosophical liberty, and that it is done by me with no mind of attacking or belittling you. In the earlier book on the New Star, and also in the third, where the comet of the year [15]80 is treated, I find a more convenient occasion of agreeing with you for the most part, and of extolling your work with deserved praises. If you have any Italian, Spanish, or French authors who have written anything concerning the New Star, I ask that you share their writings with me, rendered into Latin if it can be done; and likewise if you have ready at hand any other particular describers of the comets seen in some preceding years, not everywhere to be come by. That book on the comet of the year 1531, published separately by Apian, I have not yet seen, just as neither [have I seen] the writing of the Frenchman Johannes Pena, which Praetorius cites in his description of this comet of the year [15]77. If you make me a partaker of these, you will very greatly assist my wishes. I remember that I received from you, as a gift, a certain astrological book, when we were together at Regensburg, which set forth the influences of the planets in a certain peculiar way, through their approach to the Earth or their recession from it; on returning to my homeland I could nowhere find it. If I might obtain some copy of it again through you, I shall hereafter keep it more carefully, and unfold it more accurately; for I judge that certain things are contained in it not at variance with the truth, if they be rightly considered and duly compared with the other requisites.</p><p>When the whole work of which I have spoken above shall have been printed at my press, I will send it to you. Meanwhile, unfold this second part at your leisure, and frankly signify to me your judgement of it. You will find in it a new arrangement of the celestial orbs, devised by me not so long ago, which the more deeply it is considered, the more it will please you. I do not doubt that this is not only consonant with the appearances, but also that it is so in the very truth of the matter — which the Ptolemaic and Copernican assumptions by no means achieve. But to write more of these things now, the scarcity of time and the laws of a letter forbid. Do you therefore, best Thaddaeus, fare most happily, and bear the difficulties of advancing age with a brave spirit, and do not neglect to revisit me with letters on whatever occasion is offered.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 3rd day of May, in the year 1588.<br>Yours,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from Uraniborg on Hven, to <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayk</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius)</b> (1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and imperial physician to Rudolf II at Prague; he and Tycho had observed the 1572 nova and the comets independently and corresponded for decades. The address-superscription (<i>Prag.</i> = Prague, his residence) is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Studiosum quendam Silesium</b></i> — \"a certain Silesian student,\" the unnamed bearer of the letter; not further identified.</li><li><b>The work announced.</b> <i>secundus tomus operis nostri de recentioribus aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis</i> = \"the second volume of our work on the more recent phenomena of the aethereal World\" — i.e. Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis liber secundus</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), the great treatise on the comet of 1577 in which the <b>geo-heliocentric world-system</b> is first published (see below). The <i>primus … tomus</i> (first volume), on the 1572 <i>Stella Nova</i>, is the projected but then-unprinted volume that eventually became the posthumous <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (1602); the <i>tertius</i> (third volume), on three further comets (those of 1580, 1582, 1585, etc.), was never completed.</li><li><b><i>Stella Nova / Nova Stella</b></i> — the <b>new star (nova)</b> of 1572 in Cassiopeia. <b><i>Cometa anni 77</b></i> / <b><i>anni 80</b></i> — the <b>comets of 1577 and 1580</b>. Tycho dates by Old-Style civil years; no calendar conversion is implied.</li><li><b><i>novam orbium coelestium dispositionem … a me non ita dudum adinventam</b></i> — \"a new arrangement of the celestial orbs, devised by me not so long ago\": Tycho's announcement of his own world-system (the Sun and Moon circling the Earth, the five planets circling the Sun). He claims it not merely <i>apparentijs consonam</i> (\"consonant with the appearances\") but true <i>in rei veritate</i> (\"in the very truth of the matter\"), surpassing what the <i>Ptolomaicae et Copernianae assumtiones</i> (\"the Ptolemaic and Copernican assumptions\") achieve. <i>orbium coelestium</i> rendered \"celestial orbs\" per glossary §5.1 (<i>orbis</i> → orb).</li><li><b>Privileges.</b> <i>Gallici privilegij … Caesariano adiungere</i> — the <b>French [printing] privilege</b>, which Tycho means to <b>add to the Imperial one</b> (<i>Caesarianum</i> = the privilege granted by the Emperor). He reports having secured the French privilege \"a few days ago\"; its earlier lack had delayed the printing of the first volume. (\"Privilege\" here = an exclusive printing licence, not modernised.)</li><li><b>Other authors.</b> <i>APIANO</i> = <b>Peter Apian (Petrus Apianus)</b> (1495–1552), whose tract on the <b>comet of 1531</b> Tycho has not seen. <i>IOHANNIS PENAe Galli</i> = <b>Jean Pena (Johannes Pena)</b> (c. 1528–1558), the French royal mathematician (author of the preface to his Euclid <i>Optica</i>). <i>PRAeTORIUS</i> = <b>Johannes Praetorius (Richter)</b> (1537–1616), professor at Altdorf, cited as referencing Pena in his account of the 1577 comet. Identifications on first occurrence per §4.</li><li><b><i>librum quendam Astrologicum … cum Ratisbonae simul essemus</b></i> — \"a certain astrological book … when we were together at <b>Regensburg</b> (<i>Ratisbona</i>)\": Tycho and Hagecius had met at Regensburg, evidently at the imperial diet/coronation of 1575. The book, treating <i>Planetarum influentias … per accessum ad Terram vel ab ea recessum</i> (\"the influences of the planets … through their approach to the Earth or recession from it\"), is unidentified; Tycho wishes to recover a copy.</li><li><b>Technical / astrological vocabulary.</b> <i>Phaenomena</i> → \"phenomena\"; <i>Planetarum influentias</i> → \"the influences of the planets\" (astrological sense kept, not modernised); <i>disquisitionem mathematicam</i> → \"mathematical examination.\" No Greek, planet glyphs, or zodiac glyphs occur in this letter.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Opening superscription and <i>Iterum vale</i> (\"Again, farewell,\" §5.5) preserved; <i>quam felicissime vale</i> → \"fare most happily.\" <i>libertate Philosophica</i> → \"philosophical liberty\" — Tycho's standard defence of frank scientific disagreement; the hedge that his criticism carries <i>nullo … insectandi elevandique animo</i> (\"no mind of attacking or belittling\") is preserved, not softened.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 3. Maij Anno 1588</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, the 3rd day of May, the year 1588\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The source prints the word <i>Datae</i> broken across a line (<i>\"D / atae\"</i>); reproduced as printed in the Source block, read as the single word <i>Datae</i> in translation. The header date (3 May 1588) matches both the dateline and the catalogue label; no discrepancy.</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Latin corrected 'atae Uraniburgi' to 'Datae Uraniburgi' (Dreyer VII p.121); the closing formula had dropped its initial letter in transmission. English unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clear, the technical and bibliographic references are securely identifiable, and the dateline, label, and header agree. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.120-122 (all 3 pages read as page images). Salutation, full body (the Hagecius comet-book exchange, the lost Regensburg astrological book, the new celestial-orbs arrangement) and closing checked line by line. Every proper name and date/number (Hagecius, Apianus, Johannes Pena, Praetorius, Ratisbonae/Regensburg; comets of 77, 80, 1531; 3 May 1588) matches Dreyer exactly. One dropped-letter TEXT_ERROR found in source_text's closing formula ('atae' for 'Datae'); the translation is unaffected and correct despite it.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text drops the initial 'D' from 'Datae' (Given), reading 'atae'. The translation itself correctly renders 'Given at Uraniborg' despite the corrupted source, so the defect is confined to source_text, not the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 3. Maij Anno 1588.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the sole defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.121), which reads 'Datae Uraniburgi die 3. Maij Anno 1588.', confirming the audit's claim. The published English translation already rendered this correctly independent of the corrupted Latin, so no English change was needed.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Thaddaeum Hagecium a. 1588 (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/62-63", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964485"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.62."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15870125001", "next": "brahe-t_15880817001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860907001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881101001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Confirms Hagecius's manuscript was sent to Frankfurt, advises him to revise his treatment of the 1577 comet, and sends the second volume of his own work on it.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880503001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880523001", "d": "1588-05-23", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Caselius", "ck": "Johannes Caselius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Johannes Caselius → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 23 May 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Epistolae ad viros nobiles </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: none recorded in the source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHE.</p><p>IAM optimo Regi vestro laudationem pango, cuius exemplum IUNIO ad te mittam: qua re cum sim occupatus, neque diligenter scribo, neque uberius. Gratificor autem huic familiari meo HAKEN, Equiti Marchico, iuveni probo, nec abhorrenti a litteris, quarum gratia apud nos vivit. Omnino voluit te videre: facies gratum, si facies ut intelligat, te mihi peramicum; nam et alioqui te humanissimum virum sentiet.</p><p>Vale.<br>Rostochio X. Kalend. Iun. Anno 1588.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I am even now composing a panegyric of your excellent King, a copy of which I shall send you in June; and since I am occupied with this work, I write neither carefully nor at any length. But I am doing a kindness to this member of my household, Haken, a knight of the March [of Brandenburg], an upright young man, and no stranger to letters — for the sake of which he lives here among us. He very much wished to see you: you will do [me] a favour if you bring it about that he understands you to be a great friend of mine; for in any case he will find you a most courteous man.</p><p>Farewell.<br>From Rostock, on the tenth day before the Kalends of June [23 May], in the year 1588.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Johannes Caselius</i> = <b>Johannes Caselius</b> (Johann Kessel/Chessel, 1533–1613), the leading humanist professor of rhetoric and the <i>studia humanitatis</i>, then at the University of <b>Rostock</b> (he moved to Helmstedt in 1589). The name is not in the controlled glossary; identification supplied here. Caselius is independently attested in Tycho's orbit: Dreyer notes a Tycho letter alluding to \"Professor Caselius\" at Rostock, and a Caselius dedication to Christian IV is recorded among the Royal Library's holdings.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Rostochio</i> → <b>Rostock</b> (the Hanseatic university town in Mecklenburg), per protocol §5.7; <i>Rostochio</i> is the ablative of origin (\"from Rostock\").</li><li><b><i>optimo Regi vestro laudationem pango</b></i> — \"I am composing a panegyric of your excellent King.\" <i>Regi vestro</i> (\"your King\") is the Danish crown, addressed to Tycho as a Dane. <b>Contextual note (not asserted in the text):</b> Frederik II of Denmark had died on 4 April 1588 (O.S.); a <i>laudatio</i> of the \"best King\" in late May 1588 is most naturally a funeral panegyric on the late Frederik II, though the letter neither names the king nor states that he is dead — the identification with Frederik II is left as the apparatus reading and not introduced into the translation. <i>pango</i> (lit. \"I set down / fasten,\" used esp. of composing verse or song) is rendered \"composing\"; the work may well be in verse.</li><li><b><i>cuius exemplum IUNIO ad te mittam</b></i> — \"a copy of which I shall send you in June.\" <i>IUNIO</i> is the month <i>Iunius</i> (June) in the ablative of time, <b>not</b> a personal name despite the source's capitalisation; the dateline (23 May) makes \"in June\" coherent. <i>exemplum</i> here = \"a copy\" (of the text), not \"an example.\"</li><li><b><i>Gratificor … huic familiari meo HAKEN, Equiti Marchico</b></i> — \"I am doing a kindness to this member of my household, Haken, a knight of the March.\" <i>familiaris</i> = a member of one's household / a close associate (here apparently a young man lodging with or studying under Caselius), rendered \"member of my household.\" <i>Haken</i> is the young man's name (left as written; unidentified). <i>Eques Marchicus</i> = \"a knight of the March,\" i.e. a nobleman of the <b>Margraviate (March) of Brandenburg</b> — <i>Marchicus</i> \"of the March/Mark\"; rendered \"knight of the March [of Brandenburg],\" the bracketed gloss marking the editorial expansion.</li><li><b><i>nec abhorrenti a litteris, quarum gratia apud nos vivit</b></i> — \"and no stranger to letters, for the sake of which he lives here among us.\" <i>non abhorrere a litteris</i> = not to shrink from / not to be averse to learning; the litotes is preserved (\"no stranger to\"). <i>quarum gratia apud nos vivit</i> = he resides at Rostock for the sake of his studies.</li><li><b><i>facies gratum, si facies ut intelligat, te mihi peramicum</b></i> — \"you will do [me] a favour if you bring it about that he understands you to be a great friend of mine.\" <i>facies gratum</i> (\"you will do a welcome thing / a favour\"): the beneficiary is unexpressed and supplied as \"[me]\" in brackets. <i>peramicum</i> (intensive <i>per-</i> + <i>amicus</i>) = \"a very great friend\"; <i>te … peramicum</i> sc. <i>esse mihi</i>.</li><li><b><i>nam et alioqui te humanissimum virum sentiet</b></i> — \"for in any case he will find you a most courteous man.\" <i>humanissimus</i> in the rhetor's idiom = most cultivated/courteous/kindly; <i>sentiet</i> = \"he will perceive/find [you to be].\"</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> The letter opens with a bare address (<i>TYCHONI BRAHE</i>, \"To Tycho Brahe\") and no greeting-formula, and closes with <i>Vale</i> — <b>Farewell</b> (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Rostochio X. Kalend. Iun. Anno 1588</i> = \"From Rostock, <i>ante diem decimum Kalendas Iunias</i>, in the year 1588.\" The tenth day before the Kalends of June, counted inclusively in the Roman manner (the Kalends = 1 June counts as day 1), falls on <b>23 May</b> (O.S.; Denmark and Lutheran Germany were on the Julian calendar in 1588). The Roman-calendar idiom is rendered with both the idiom and the civil date, per protocol §3 and §4. The catalogue <i>label</i> (\"1588. 23. maj. Rostock\") agrees with the as-written dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is short and syntactically clear, and the rendering is secure. The one soft point is the identity of the panegyrised \"best King\": the in-letter evidence supports only \"the King of Denmark,\" and the inference to the recently-deceased Frederik II is an apparatus contextualisation, deliberately not promoted into the translation. The young man \"Haken\" is named but otherwise unidentified.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — short clear Latin, sense secure; the 'best-King' identification is apparatus-only (kept out of the translation), no textual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.121-122 (page images). The Caselius letter is printed complete at the foot of p.121 under 'TYCHONI BRAHE.', matching source_text word-for-word. Checked the named person (Haken, Eques Marchicus) and the Roman-calendar closing date ('X. Kalend. Iun. Anno 1588' = 23 May, correctly resolved and bracketed) against the translation — accurate throughout.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Introduces a Brandenburg knight, Haken, who wishes to visit, and mentions composing a panegyric of the Danish king to be sent in June.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880523001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880611001", "d": "1588-06-11", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, around the summer solstice 1588 [O.S.] (catalogued 11 June 1588)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOCTRINA ET VIRTUTE PRAeSTANTI, D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO, S. D.</p><p>STATVERAM quidem hoc tempore, doctissime et charissime ROTHMANNE, plenius copiosiusque ad proximas Illustrissimi Principis tuasque literas respondere, sed cum plurimae, tum publicae, tum priuatae curae inciderint, et minus etiam firma vtar valetudine, a proposito desistere vel inuitus cogor. Accedit et hoc, quod plures in Veneris Stella, nunc quoque interdiu apparente, Obseruationes vespertinas expectem, quas cum matutinis, Anno praesertim antecedente in Augusto habitis, denuo conferam, vt quinorum illorum Minutorum, quibus in Fixarum Longitudinibus discrepamus, vberior examinatio instituatur. Quamprimum vero opportunius otium, commoditatemque exoptatam nactus fuero, et illas, quas dixi, in Venere Obseruationes perfecero, non intermittam, quin abunde et ex voto, tum ad Illustrissimi Principis, tum etiam tuas literas respondeam. Quod te rogo, vt tuo Principi meo nomine indicare velis, et responsionis moram ob caussas antedictas excusare. Interim mitto tibi exemplar secundi libri Operis mei de Mundi Aetherei recentioribus Phaenomenis, quem diligenter per otium velis euoluere, et de eo tuam ad me sententiam perscribere. Tractat de Cometa anni 77; Primus enim est de Stella Noua ad Cassiopeam: Tertius et vltimus de tribus alijs minoribus Cometis. Poteris autem vel ex hoc vnico, quid de Cometarum negotio penitius introspecto sentiam, non obscure colligere. Inuenies etiam ibidem circa initium capitis octaui, nouam a me non ita dudum adinuentum caelestium Reuolutionum Hypothesin, quam veteri illa Ptolemaica et recentiore Copernicea multas ob causas veriorem esse non dubito, eamque apparentijs apprime consentaneam deprehendo. Tute diligenter omnia in hac circumspicito, nihilque concinnius, aut veritati conuenientius adferri posse, non ambigo quin vltro concessurus sis. Cuperem etiam, vt eam Illustriss. Principi tuo exponeres, eiusque C. de hac iudicium mihi significares. Constitui super hanc, emendationem motuum caelestium fundare, sicque totam Artem Astronomicam e certis Obseruationibus, quasi ad prima incunabula reuocare, redintegrareque; modo Numen diuinum conatibus nostris aspirauerit, et vires ac commoditatem tanto labori exequendo suppeditârit. Recensui etiam in hoc libro, Illustrissimi tui Principis de eodem Cometa Obseruationes mihi transmissas, easque sub incudem numerorum diligenter reuocaui, et a meis Inuentis non admodum dissentaneas adinueni: Neque enim vlla perceptibilis Parallaxis istius Cometae inde colligi potest, sed omnia in contrarium potius deueniunt, vt ex ipso scripto videre licet. Spero Illustrissimo Principi tuo hanc suarum in Cometa hoc Obseruationum euulgationem, accuratamque considerationem non futuram ingratam; siquidem ad rei inquirendae veritatem confirmandam plurimum conducat. Quamprimum reliqui duo Tomi absoluti fuerint, ijsque Typographus extremam manum imposuerit, te eorum etiam reddam participem. Hunc etiam praesentium exhibitorem, GELLIVM SASCERIDEM nomine, qui annis aliquot, tum in Astronomicis, tum etiam Pyronomicis exercitijs egregiam apud me nauauit operam, velis tuo Illustrissimo Principi commendare: cui etiam priuatim quaedam significaui, tibi exponenda, vt ea ad Illustrissimum Principem tuum per occasionem referas, quod vt diligenter exequaris, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Sperabamus nos antea, si successissent Comitia Hamburgensia, neque tantus nostrae patriae luctus (proh dolor) ex inopinato incidisset, Illustrissimum Principem tuum Hamburgum venturum. Quod si contigisset, istuc conueniendi eius Celsitudinem gratiâ me contulissem, si propius huc non accessisset, quo de Astronomicis rebus coram plenius perfectiusque cum eius Celsitudine conferendo ex voto agerem. Sed quoniam ista nobis occasio nimium lugubri obstaculo (quodque animus meminisse horret luctuque refugit) praerepta est, ad literas recurrendum esse video, quas etiam prima quaque opportunitate, ijs quas vltimo ab Illustrissimo Principe et te accepi, affatim respondentes, (vt supra etiam pollicitus sum) breui mittam. Interim moram hanc non aegre feras, eamque apud Illustriss. Principem tuum diligenter excuses.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datum Vraniburgi circa Solstitium Aestiuum, Anno 1588.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished man, eminent in learning and virtue, Master Christoph Rothmann, outstanding mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his most dearly beloved friend — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I had indeed resolved at this time, most learned and most dear Rothmann, to answer the most recent letters of the Most Illustrious Prince and your own more fully and copiously; but since very many cares, both public and private, have come upon me, and I am also enjoying less than firm health, I am compelled, however unwillingly, to give up that purpose. There is added this too, that I am awaiting several evening observations of the planet Venus — now visible by day as well — which I shall compare again with the morning ones, made in particular in August of the preceding year, so that a fuller examination may be set on foot of those five minutes by which we differ in the longitudes of the fixed [stars]. But as soon as I have come by a more convenient leisure and the long-wished-for opportunity, and have completed those observations of Venus of which I spoke, I shall not fail to answer abundantly and as I would wish both the Most Illustrious Prince's letters and your own. And I ask you to be willing to make this known to your Prince in my name, and to excuse the delay of my reply for the aforesaid reasons. Meanwhile I send you a copy of the second book of my work <i>On the More Recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World</i>, which I should like you to peruse carefully at your leisure, and to write me your opinion of it in full. It treats of the comet of the year [15]77; for the first [book] is on the New Star by Cassiopeia, the third and last on three other lesser comets. From this one [book] alone you will be able to gather, not obscurely, what I think about the question of comets, once it has been more deeply looked into. You will find there too, near the beginning of the eighth chapter, a new Hypothesis of the celestial Revolutions, devised by me not so long ago, which I do not doubt to be — for many reasons — truer than that old Ptolemaic one and the more recent Copernican one, and which I find supremely consonant with the appearances. Examine everything in it carefully for yourself, and I do not doubt that you will of your own accord concede that nothing more elegant, or more agreeable to the truth, can be brought forward. I should wish also that you would expound it to your Most Illustrious Prince, and signify to me His Highness's judgement on it. I have determined to found upon this [hypothesis] the emendation of the celestial motions, and so to recall and restore the whole Art of Astronomy from sure observations, as it were to its first cradle — provided the divine Power shall favour our endeavours, and shall supply the strength and the means for accomplishing so great a labour. I have also reviewed in this book the observations of the same comet sent to me by your Most Illustrious Prince, and I have carefully recalled them to the anvil of numbers, and have found them not greatly at variance with my own findings; for indeed no perceptible parallax of that comet can be gathered from them, but everything comes out rather to the contrary, as may be seen from the writing itself. I hope that this publishing-abroad of his observations on this comet, and their accurate consideration, will not prove unwelcome to your Most Illustrious Prince, inasmuch as it may contribute very greatly to confirming the truth of the matter under inquiry. As soon as the remaining two volumes have been completed, and the printer has set the final hand to them, I shall make you a partaker in these too. This same bearer of the present [letter], Gellius Sascerides by name, who for some years has done excellent service with me in exercises both astronomical and pyronomic [chemical], I should like you to commend to your Most Illustrious Prince; to whom I have also privately communicated certain things to be laid out to you, that you may convey them on occasion to your Most Illustrious Prince — which that you carry out diligently I do most earnestly ask of you. We had hoped before now, if the Diet of Hamburg had gone forward, and so great a grief had not (alas!) fallen unexpectedly upon our country, that your Most Illustrious Prince would come to Hamburg. And had that happened, I would have betaken myself thither for the sake of meeting His Highness — if he had not come nearer here — that I might attend, as I would wish, upon discussing astronomical matters in person, more fully and more perfectly, with His Highness. But since that opportunity has been snatched from us by a too-mournful obstacle (<i>and which my mind shudders to recall and shrinks from in grief</i>), I see that I must have recourse to letters, which too, at the very first opportunity, fully answering those I last received from the Most Illustrious Prince and from you, I shall send shortly (as I have promised above also). Meanwhile, bear this delay without resentment, and excuse it diligently to your Most Illustrious Prince.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg around the Summer Solstice, in the Year 1588.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from Uraniborg on Hven, to <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (<i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i>, c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), the mathematician-astronomer of Wilhelm IV at Kassel and Tycho's principal scientific correspondent of the late 1580s. The <i>Illustrissimus Princeps Wilhelmus Landtgravius Hassiae</i> of the address is <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Rothmann's patron and himself an observing astronomer (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b>The book sent.</b> <i>secundi libri Operis mei de Mundi Aetherei recentioribus Phaenomenis</i> = Book II of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis liber secundus</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), his treatise on the <b>comet of 1577</b>. As Tycho explains, the projected work was tripartite: Book I on the <b>New Star (nova) of 1572 by Cassiopeia</b> (<i>Stella Nova ad Cassiopeam</i>), Book II (the one here transmitted, the only one printed in Tycho's lifetime) on the 1577 comet, and a planned Book III on three lesser comets — the <i>reliqui duo Tomi</i> (\"remaining two volumes\") not yet finished by the printer.</li><li><b>The world-system.</b> <i>nouam … caelestium Reuolutionum Hypothesin … capitis octaui</i> — the famous announcement, near the start of <b>chapter 8</b> of the comet book, of Tycho's new <b>Hypothesis of the celestial Revolutions</b> (the geo-heliocentric \"Tychonic\" system), claimed <i>veriorem</i> (\"truer\") than both the <i>Ptolemaica</i> and the <i>Copernicea</i> systems and <i>apparentijs apprime consentaneam</i> (\"supremely consonant with the appearances\"). Tycho's epistemic hedges are preserved: he writes <i>non dubito</i> (\"I do not doubt\"), not a flat assertion, and grounds the claim in fitting the <i>apparentiae</i>.</li><li><b>Tycho's programme.</b> <i>totam Artem Astronomicam e certis Obseruationibus, quasi ad prima incunabula reuocare, redintegrareque</i> = \"to recall and restore the whole Art of Astronomy from sure observations, as it were to its first cradle\" — the <i>instauratio / restitutio astronomiae</i> programme (protocol §5.1); <i>redintegrare</i> rendered \"restore.\" <i>ad prima incunabula</i> (\"to its first cradle/swaddling-clothes\") kept as the metaphor it is.</li><li><b>Technical / observational.</b> <i>Veneris Stella … interdiu apparente</i> = \"the planet Venus … visible by day\"; <i>quinorum illorum Minutorum, quibus in Fixarum Longitudinibus discrepamus</i> = \"those five minutes [of arc] by which we differ in the longitudes of the fixed [stars]\" — a daytime-Venus campaign used to resolve a 5′ disagreement with Rothmann in stellar <b>longitudes</b> (protocol §5.1). <i>Parallaxis istius Cometae</i> = \"parallax of that comet\": the absence of perceptible <b>parallax</b> is the crux of the cometary argument (a comet without measurable parallax must lie far beyond the Moon, dissolving the solid celestial spheres). <i>sub incudem numerorum … reuocaui</i> = \"recalled to the anvil of numbers,\" i.e. subjected Wilhelm's observations to numerical recomputation; the smith metaphor kept.</li><li><b>Bearer.</b> <i>Gellium Sasceridem</i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (1562–1612), the Danish scholar then in Tycho's service, here carrying the letter; <i>in Astronomicis, tum etiam Pyronomicis exercitijs</i> = \"in astronomical and also pyronomic exercises\" — <i>Pyronomica</i> (\"fire-governing\") is Tycho's term for the <b>alchemical / chemical (laboratory)</b> work at Uraniborg; rendered \"pyronomic [chemical]\" rather than modernised.</li><li><b>The \"grief of our country.\"</b> <i>tantus nostrae patriae luctus (proh dolor) ex inopinato incidisset</i> and <i>nimium lugubri obstaculo</i> refer, unnamed, to the death of <b>King Frederik II of Denmark</b> (d. 4 April 1588), Tycho's patron — which is why the hoped-for <i>Comitia Hamburgensia</i> (\"Diet of Hamburg\") meeting with Wilhelm IV did not come about.</li><li><b>Allusion (kept as a half-line of verse).</b> <i>quodque animus meminisse horret luctuque refugit</i> — a near-verbatim echo of <b>Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> II.12</b> (<i>quaeque ipse miserrima vidi … et quorum pars magna fui … animus meminisse horret luctuque refugit</i>, \"things which my mind shudders to recall and has shrunk from in grief\"); set off in italics in the translation to mark the parenthetical quotation, as in the source's own parentheses.</li><li><b>Idiom.</b> <i>extremam manum imposuerit</i> = \"[the printer] has set the final hand,\" i.e. finished/perfected the work (the standard <i>ultima/extrema manus</i> idiom); <i>eius C.</i> / <i>eiusque C.</i> = <i>eius Celsitudo</i> \"His Highness,\" the Landgrave's style (protocol §5.5, <i>Celsitudo</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux.</b> The dateline reads <i>Datum Vraniburgi <b>circa Solstitium Aestiuum</b>, Anno 1588</i> — \"Given at Uraniborg <b>around the Summer Solstice</b>, in the Year 1588,\" an approximate date (the Old-Style summer solstice fell ~11–12 June 1588 by the Julian reckoning Denmark kept). The dataset's catalogue label \"11. juni\" supplies a precise civil day (consistent with, and presumably derived from, the solstice indication), but the <b>source text gives no day-of-month</b>: the header therefore leads with the as-written \"around the Summer Solstice 1588\" and records the catalogued 11 June in parentheses, per protocol §4.1. No silent conversion is made; O.S. throughout (Denmark, Julian).</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The letter was printed by Tycho himself in the <i>Epistolae astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the edition of record cited above; it is reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and its technical content render securely; the one open point is the dateline crux (<i>circa Solstitium Aestiuum</i> is an approximate date and the source supplies no day-of-month, so the catalogued \"11 June\" is reported, not asserted as the text's own), flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No tboo/Dreyer slice_pdf is available for this letter (tboo is null), so it could not be collated against the edition of record; per protocol this caps the grade at B regardless of outcome. Audited against source_text alone: went through the full Latin letter to Rothmann clause by clause (the Venus-observation delay, the De Mundi Aetherei Recentioribus Phaenomenis book structure and its chapter-8 Hypothesis, the Wilhelm IV comet-observation comparison, Gellius Sascerides, the Hamburg Diet / king's-death allusion echoing Virgil's 'animus meminisse horret luctuque refugit', and the closing dateline). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found against the Latin as given.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15871114001", "next": "brahe-t_15880817003", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15871114001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881013001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses a delayed reply on grounds of illness and pressing occupations, and reports ongoing Venus observations intended to settle a five-minute longitude discrepancy between their star positions.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880611001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880815001", "d": "1588-08-15", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Grabow", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → Tycho Brahe — Grabow, 15 August 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin code-switch) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin,<i> Correspondenz des Herzogs Ulrich mit Gelehrten </i>(Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EHRNUESTEN VNSERN BESONDER LIEBEN TYGE BRAHEN AUFF WIEN.</p><p>Vlrich etc.</p><p>VNSERN günstigen grüs zuuor. Ehrnuester besonder lieber, nachdem Wir in Vnser ankunfft alhie befunden, das das druck pappiers, so dieß orts fur euch bestellet worden, funff vnd funfzigk reiß fertigk gewesen, weil sich gleich zugetragen, das meister Peter, der vhrmacher, auch alhie bej Vns angelangt vnd sich vernemen lassen solch pappier euch zuzubringen, so haben Wir jhm dasselb zustellen lassen, auch mit der fhur bis naher Warnmunde befördert. Das es sich aber mit Verfertigung desselben was verzogen, berichtet der meister, hat daran gemangelt, das er kejne form gehapt, so darzu gerecht gewesen, sondern hat an die formen, nach denen er das regal papier machet, noch sonderliche leisten sezen müssen, aufdas sich die grosse dieß eurs druck papiers darein recht schicken wollen, welchs denn woll zeit wegk genommen. Wir haben jhm dem meister die bezalung dafür entrichtet, wollen euch damit verehret haben, des versehens jhr werdet es nicht anders dan gnedigk vnd woll gemeint von Vns vermercken.</p><p>Da jhr Vns auch ein exemplar von eurem Opere Astronomice, wenn jhr damit fertigk, zustehen lassen wolte, thettet jhr Vns daran zu gemeinem gefallen, dan ob Wir woll die kunste nicht verstehen, mochten Wir doch gleichwoll Vnsere liberrj damit zu eurem gedechtnis gern zieren. Seindt Wir vmb euch in gnaden, damit Wir euch auch ohn das sonders woll gewesen zu beschulden gneigt.</p><p>Actum Grabauw den 15t. augustj anno 88.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, our especially well-beloved Tyge Brahe at Hven.</b></p><p>Ulrich, etc.</p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Honourable, especially well-beloved, [we write] inasmuch as on our arrival here we found that the printing paper which had been ordered for you in this place was [now] ready, five-and-fifty reams of it; and since it happened just then that Master Peter the clockmaker had likewise arrived here with us, and let it be known that he would bring such paper to you, we had it handed over to him, and forwarded it too by the carrier as far as Warnemünde. But that there has been some delay in the making of it — so the master reports — was owing to this: that he had no mould [<i>form</i>] suited to the purpose, but had to fit special battens onto the moulds by which he makes royal paper, so that the size of this printing paper of yours might be properly accommodated to them, which indeed took up a good deal of time. We have rendered the master his payment for it, [and] wish hereby to have made you a present of it, in the expectation that you will take it from us in no other sense than as graciously and well meant.</p><p>If, moreover, you would let us have a copy of your astronomical work [<i>Opus Astronomicum</i>], when you have finished it, you would thereby do us a common pleasure; for although we do not, to be sure, understand the arts [of astronomy], we should nonetheless gladly adorn our library with it, in your memory. We remain graciously disposed toward you, being besides this inclined to deserve especially well of you.</p><p>Done at Grabow, the 15th of August, the year [15]88.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg[-Güstrow]</b> (1527–1603), reigning at Güstrow; a Lutheran prince and patron of learning, and maternal grandfather of King Christian IV of Denmark. The MS is the duke's own <i>Correspondenz mit Gelehrten</i> at Schwerin; the witness is a <i>Kladde</i> — a chancery <b>draft/minute</b>, not the dispatched fair copy. <i>Vlrich etc.</i> abbreviates the full ducal titulature, kept abbreviated as in the draft.</li><li><b>Address line / place.</b> <i>AUFF WIEN</i> — not Vienna but <b>\"at Hven\"</b> (the island <i>Hven / Ven</i>, where Uraniborg stood); the chancery clerk's spelling of the Danish island name. Rendered \"at Hven\" and flagged, since <i>Wien</i> in isolation invites the false reading \"Vienna.\" <i>Actum Grabauw</i> = \"Done at <b>Grabow</b>\" (Grabow in Mecklenburg), O.S. — the dateline kept per protocol §3.</li><li><b>The paper gift — the substance of the letter.</b> <i>druck pappier</i> = <b>printing paper</b>; <i>funff vnd funfzigk reiß</i> = <b>fifty-five reams</b> (<i>Rieß</i> / ream, the standard counting unit of paper). The duke is supplying paper for Tycho's Uraniborg press as a princely gift (<i>verehren</i> \"to make a present of\"), having paid the maker himself. This documents the well-attested practice whereby Tycho drew large-format paper for his self-printed works from German princely papermills.</li><li><b>The papermaking detail.</b> <i>form</i> = the papermaker's <b>mould</b> (the wire frame on which the sheet is couched); <i>regal papier</i> = <b>royal paper</b>, a large sheet-size; <i>leisten</i> = <b>battens / strips</b> fixed to the standard moulds to enlarge the sheet to the oversized format Tycho required. The delay is technical: existing royal-paper moulds had to be re-fitted to yield Tycho's larger <i>Druckpapier</i> sheet. Rendered descriptively; no instrument-glossary term applies.</li><li><b>The carrier.</b> <i>meister Peter, der vhrmacher</i> = <b>\"Master Peter the clockmaker\"</b>, who happened to be travelling to Hven and undertook to convey the paper; <i>vhrmacher</i> (= <i>Uhrmacher</i>) \"clockmaker/horologist.\" <i>Warnmunde</i> = <b>Warnemünde</b>, the port at the mouth of the Warnow near Rostock — the shipping point toward Hven. <i>fhur</i> / <i>mit der fhur befördert</i> = \"forwarded by the carrier / by waggon-carriage.\"</li><li><b>The requested book.</b> <i>exemplar von eurem Opere Astronomice</i> — a copy of \"your <b>astronomical work</b> [<i>Opus Astronomicum</i>],\" to be sent <i>wenn jhr damit fertigk</i> (\"when you have finished it\"). The work is not named; in August 1588 Tycho's press had just issued <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the second volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> series), and the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> proper was still in progress — so the singular \"work in progress\" is left unspecified rather than identified. <i>liberrj</i> = <i>Liberei / Librarei</i>, <b>\"library\"</b> (the ducal book-collection); <i>zu eurem gedechtnis</i> = \"in your memory / as a memorial of you.\" The duke's disclaimer <i>ob Wir woll die kunste nicht verstehen</i> (\"although we do not understand the arts\") is preserved, not smoothed.</li><li><b>Register &amp; formulae (chancery German, princely <i>Wir</i>).</b> <i>VNSERN günstigen grüs zuuor</i> = <b>\"Our gracious greeting beforehand\"</b> (protocol §5.5); the princely <i>Wir / euch</i> and the address <i>Ehrnuester besonder lieber</i> (\"Honourable, especially well-beloved\") are kept. Orthography is chancery Frühneuhochdeutsch: <i>v/u</i> for <i>u/v</i> (<i>Vnser, vhrmacher, zuuor</i>), <i>-tz/-zk</i> (<i>funfzigk</i>, <i>fertigk</i>), <i>bej / kejne</i> for <i>bei/keine</i>, <i>jhm/jhr</i> for <i>ihm/ihr</i>. <i>des versehens</i> = \"in the expectation/confidence that.\" <i>zu beschulden gneigt</i> = <i>zu verschulden geneigt</i>, \"inclined to deserve [well] of,\" an idiom of princely favour.</li><li><b>Code-switch.</b> Latin nouns are embedded in the German body per protocol §2: <i>Opere Astronomice</i> (ablative of <i>Opus Astronomicum</i>), <i>exemplar</i>, and the dating rubric <i>Actum … anno 88</i> — preserved and italicised on first use.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely throughout; the two soft points, both flagged rather than decided, are (1) <i>AUFF WIEN</i> = \"at Hven\" (the island), not Vienna, and (2) the unnamed <i>Opus Astronomicum</i>, left unspecified because the letter does not name it and Tycho had more than one work then at press.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — chancery German secure; both soft points resolved (Hven not Vienna; the work faithfully left unnamed per the source).</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO XIV pp.40-42 (page images, read visually), including the Latin regest (no.64) which independently confirms 55 reams of paper -- matching the letter's own '55' figure. Text matches Dreyer word for word. The address 'auff Wien' (rendered 'at Hven' in translation) was checked against context: Tycho lived on Hven in 1588 and the letter concerns delivery of his printing paper there, confirming this is a period spelling variant of Hven, not a mistranslation. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Correspondenz des Herzogs Ulrich mit Gelehrten.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15970729001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970729001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that fifty-five reams of printing paper, delayed by mould-adaptation, have been forwarded via Master Peter the clockmaker as a gift.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880815001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880816001", "d": "1588-08-16", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 16 August 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist; printed orthography with v/u), with a zodiacal glyph · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VI,<i> Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(Uraniborg/Wandsbek 1596), Liber I · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed tradition)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> A <b>published</b> letter — one of the set Tycho himself printed at his Uraniborg press in the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596) — and the locus classicus, on Tycho's side, of the <b>\"five-minutes controversy\"</b>: the Landgrave's mathematician <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> had made the fixed-star longitudes about <b>5′ greater</b> than Tycho's, and Tycho here defends his own values at length, tracing Rothmann's excess to <b>refraction</b> in the southern (low-altitude) half of the zodiac and to the reliance on the Sun/Venus too near the horizon. The text is clean (printed source) and preserves genuine early-modern <b>v/u orthography</b> (<i>vt, vbi, varie, obseruatio</i>), reproduced as printed. Two declared interventions in the source block: (1) the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> (footnote-anchors keying words to the apparatus) appended to ~25 words have been removed as <b>non-textual</b> (the same as in the Scaliger letter of 23 Aug 1598 and the undated Peucer letter of 1590); (2) the opening word's <b>dittography</b> <i>\"ILLVSTRISSIMOILLVSTRISSIMO\"</i> (the word transcribed twice) is reduced to a single <i>ILLVSTRISSIMO</i> (flagged). The zodiac glyph ♒ (Aquarius) is reproduced as printed. The dateline (<i>\"Vraniburgi, Die 16. Augusti, Anno 1588\"</i>) is <b>Old Style</b> (Danish Julian); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VI pp. 121–132</b> (PDF pp. 135–146). <b>All cruxes confirmed</b>: the 5′ longitude discrepancy (and \"duodecima gradus portiuncula\" = 5′); the 4-or-5′ refraction advancement and the 30°-altitude floor (p. 140); the reciprocal-Venus chronology <b>1582 / 1585 / 1587 / 1588</b> with the 17–18 Aug Venus–Jupiter conjunction sighted \"from both heads of Gemini\" (p. 143); the 5-year solar Ephemeris; the Copernicus/mid-Scorpio solar-apogee point (p. 140); and the dateline (p. 146). The flagged opening dittography is vindicated — <b>Dreyer prints <i>ILLVSTRISSIMO</i> once</b> (p. 135), so the reduction is correct.</p>", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO D. WILHELMO HASSIAe LANDTGRAVIO, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO S. P.</p><p>QVOD tardius tam Tuae Celsitudinis expectatione, quam mea ipsius voluntate ad literas quas circa Autumnale Aequinoctium antecedentis anni, Tua Celsitudo clementer ad me dedit, nunc respondeam, Illustriss. Generosissimeque Princeps, occasionem praebuerunt occupationes variae et impedimenta quaedam, quae circa Veris initium inciderunt, effeceruntque quo minus per eos, qui ad proxime praeteritas vernales Nundinas hinc profecti sunt, has ipsas quas tunc adornâram Tuae Celsitud. transmittendas curare licuerit: accessit et hoc quod in Martio et Aprili plures in Venere interdiu apparente, et Occidentali a Sole, animaduersiones obtinere sperabam, vt eas cum prioribus matutinis, praesertim antecedente Anno in Augusto habitis, plenius conferrem, et quinorum istorum Scrupulorum, de quibus in Longitudinibus Fixarum constituendis inter nos controuersia est, dijudicationem ratam et vberiorem explorationem instituerem, quod etiam aliquoties diligenter a me praestitum est, veluti postea T. Cels. quid hinc eruerim adinuenerimque, plenius exponam. Submisse itaque tuam Celsitud. rogatam habeo, vt hanc in rescribendo dilationem, praesertim cum iam cum faenore resarciatur, candide et clementer excusatam habere dignetur. Scripsi etiam interea Tuae Cels. Mathematico, eique morae huius rationes, T. Cels. referendas exposui.</p><p>Ne itaque diutius id quod hactenus intermissum est differatur, nunc ijs, quae Celsitud. Tuae ad me missae literae continebant, ordine respondebo. Obseruationes meas T. Cels. communicatas perplacuisse, exactamque illam congruentiam inter Declinationes, et distantias Affixarum Stellarum Tuae Cels. Instrumentis et meis investigatas, summam peperisse admirationem, ex ipso frontispicio T. Celsitud. literarum non sine voluptate percepi. Et sane, tam exquisita ratio Sidera scrutandi, vt in diuersis locis Obseruationes acceptae tam praecise consentiant, hactenus insolita prorsusque inaudita mansit; vt rectissime etiam Tua Celsitudo suam de his sententiam pronunciat. Atque hinc liquido patet, Instrumenta nostra, quibus Altitudines et Distantiae Stellarum capiuntur, vtrinque adamussim recte se habere, adeoque perfecte elaborata esse, vt nulli sensibili vitio sint obnoxia, praesertim postquam perpendiculi deuiatio illa minutula in Tuae Celsitudinis Quadrante per Mathematicum est redintegrata. Plurimum itaque Astronomiae instaurationi congratulor, quod ratio illa Pinnacidiorum et Diuisionis, structuraeque exactae Instrumentorum, quibus Siderum loca in ipso Scrupulo, adeoque eius aliquota parte, caelitus inuestigare licet, (quod tam veteribus quam modernis Astronomis hactenus insolens visum est) qua ego aliquot ab hinc Annis, non vti spero, sine fructu vsus sum, Tuae etiam Celsitud. innotuerit, quodque a Tua Celsitud. approbata in praxin Astronomicam, magno huius laudatissimae scientiae emolumento et successu, deducatur; sine qua sane in Instrumentorum fabrica et Diuisione, ac Dioptrarum applicatione, inter nos conformitate, ad tam exactam Obseruationum conuenientiam, in qua etiam nihil ab ipsius caelestibus apparentijs aberrationis lateat, perueniendi omnis aliâs praeclusa fuisset via. Quod autem nihilominus in Stellarum Longitudinibus quinis proxime Minutis differamus, quibus meae anteriores inueniuntur, suam apud eos, qui huius subtilis inquisitionis perplexos labyrinthos satis habent perspectos, excusationem facile meretur. Ideoque recte Tua Celsit. asserit, hanc discrepantiam non adeo admiratione dignam; siquidem veteres illi Astronomiae Principes, vix in sexta parte Gradus, veritatem in his assequuti sunt, neque etiam ad maiorem praecisionem, tanquam id minus foret possibile arbitrantes, aspirare tentârunt; quod tamen non tam ob Instrumentorum paruitatem, et Armillarum variam hinc inde inter obseruandum distractionem, illis euenisse iudico, quam quod per foramina dioptrica saltem Stellas obseruare didicerant; vbi nisi id quod in remotiori ab oculo Tabella constituitur, maiusculum fuerit, Stellae etiam clariores aegerrime conspiciuntur; et si hoc detur, ob foraminis magnitudinem, quae aliquotam Diuisionis Graduum partem occupat, in pauculis scrupulis, obseruatio exquisita constare minime poterit. Armillas vero Astrolabicas eorum iustae fuisse magnitudinis, inde satis liquet, quod Hipparchus et Ptolemaeus etiam Aequinoctiales ingressus per eas deprehenderint; et ipsemet Ptolemaeus quasdam Armillas Alexandriae constitutas, quae nimium magnae erant, tanquam minus idoneas reicere videtur. Possunt tamen adhibita peculiari diligentia, Armillae etiam maiusculae ita affabre componi, vt inter obseruandum, per suam Reuolutionem, aut circulorum disiunctionem, nihil vitiositatis insinuent; quamuis non exigua in his requiratur circumspectio, quam praecipua ex parte veteres etiam adhibuisse non dubito.</p><p>Equidem et ego per Armillas Siderum loca satis commode et praecise obseruari, multoties experior. In quaternis enim meis machinis Armillaribus, partim Aequinoctialibus, partim Zodiacalibus, quae tantae sunt magnitudinis, vt singula Minuta iuxta diuisionem nobis vsitatam, discriminatim exhibeant (quas tamen aptiori compendio, quam a veteribus factitatum est, construxi) quacunque earum vti libuerit, verus Stellae locus in ipso Minuto exhibetur, prorsus cum eo congruens, qui vel per distantias beneficio Sextantum, vel etiam per Azimuthales Quadrantes peculiari solertia reuolutos, inuenitur, praesertim si par vtrobique inter obseruandum adhibeatur sedulitas. Alias enim nihil diuersimode fabrefactis Organis tribuerem, nisi in eundem prorsus veritatis scopum consentirent; Atque eam etiam ob causam, copiam et varietatem in eorum structura hactenus expetiui, vt non vno modo sed multifariam obseruando, enucleatior certitudo, expeditius citra omne dubium comprobetur. In his vero omnibus, quia per Rimulas illas Parallelas, Stellas collineandi, a me aliquot ab hinc annis excogitatam rationem adhibeo, multo promtius, et scrupulosius, ipsarum exquisita loca, quam veteribus per foramina Tabularum adeo errori, vti dixi, obnoxia, obseruationi indubitatae patescunt. Per has itaque varias machinas, omni possibili subtilitate, et perfectione affabre elaboratas, Stellarum Longitudines multiformiter conquisiui, et non vni alicui Instrumento confisus, sed diuersis rem omnem varie examinando, et magna circumspectione singula trutinando, hoc tantum negotium administraui; idque non saltem a Solis loco adamussim prius correcto mediante Luna, vt veteres tentârunt, sed etiam intercedente Veneris Stella multoties a me exploratum est; vt ob id nihil prorsus dubitem, Longitudines a me constitutas omni sensibili carere vitio, et ad tantam praecisionem esse deductas, quantam vllius hominis diligentia et oculorum acies comprehendere poterit. Neque tamen propterea T. C. obseruationes improbandas iudico, sed multo potius admiror, quod vel ad eam amussim, et cum nostris admodum propinquam concordantiam, accesserint; praesertim in ea, quae per Altitudines et transitus Meridianos, cum temporis annotatione adhibita est, Obseruationis praxi, quam sane maxima diligentia administratam liquet, cum aliâs non paucis difficultatibus, et anfractibus sit exposita.</p><p>Quod itaque T. Cels. a suis inuentis discedere nolit, ob sedulitatem non mediocrem in Obseruationibus adhibitam, rationes proculdubio habet non contemnendas, quibus ego etiam refragari nolo; neque enim differentia inter nos adeo magna est, vt Astronomicae certitudini sensibiliter officiat. Verum quia Celsitudo Tua existimat suas in Longitudinibus constitutiones ideo tam ratas exactasque esse, vt ab ijs discedere nequeat, quod non solum post correctum Quadrantis perpendiculum, per transitus Azimuthales beneficio exquisiti Horologij adinuentae sint, sed etiam per Veneris Stellam, in Ianuario praecedentis anni aliquoties modo etiam mihi vsitato, diligenter exploratae, comprobataeque, de his T. Cels. meam sententiam si paulo liberius indicâro, id Tuam Celsit. clementi et benigno animo accepturam non diffido. Quantum ad priorem, per Altitudines et Azimutha, cum tempore Obseruationis simul dato, loca Stellarum obseruandi rationem attinet, licet ea post emendatum Quadrantis perpendiculum, earum Longitudines in eundem prorsus situm, quo antea, reponat, non tamen ob id omnem haesitandi ansam sublatam existimo. Significaui vero T. Celsit. in vltimis meis literis, quas ob causas, haec Siderum positus scrutandi via, mihi non satis tuta videatur. Siquidem quacunque diligentia Quadrans ordinetur, et quam affabre Horologia elaborata sint; tamen illic vbi vel minime putatur, error aliquis sese furtim ingerit, qui facile paucorum Scrupulorum praecisionem impediat, siue quod Horologium singula temporum momenta non perpetuo aequali tenore indicet, siue quod in ipso Quadrante, quo ad planum eius, non omni ex parte plano verticalium circulorum correspondente, aliquid minutuli lateat vitij, siue etiam quacunque alia non facile visui obuia de causa, idipsum contingat. Si tamen Tua Celsitud. huic modo, Sidera obseruandi nullatenus diffidit, fiat saltem iuxta vtrunque Aequinoctium, denuo huius rei periculum quam diligentissime, ita, vt vtrobique locus Solis per Meridianam Altitudinem diligenter emendatus, vel etiam e nostra restitutione in motu eius depromtus, adhibeatur; adsit etiam in hac praxi Horologiorum exquisitissimorum aliqua varietas, et copia, vtque inuicem, in Minutorum minimis particulis exacte correspondeant procuretur, quod si tunc (non semel sed aliquoties rem omnem reiterando) Stellarum loca, vtrinque, et viceversa examinando, in idem Minutum (adhibita intercapedinis earum ratione, siquidem diuersis Stellis hîc vti oportebit) reciderint, tunc loca earum hac Methodo satis scrupulose adinuenta, facilius concesserim. Verum quacunque sedulitate et praecautione tractetur haec Sidera obseruandi ratio, lubrica nihilominus, et ad deuiandum, in pauculis praesertim Scrupulis admodum prona mihi censetur.</p><p>Alteram autem, qua per Veneris Stellam Tuae Celsit. Mathematicus idem aggressus est, ratiocinationem multo potius huic praeferrem, eo quod ad veritatis penitiorem tramitem tutius ducat. Quod tamen per hanc ipsam, in Ianuario praecedentis Anni, easdem Stellarum Longitudines, quas prius et a meis adhuc quinis Scrupulis dissidentes, adinuenerit, nondum satis confirmat, nihil prorsus in vestris Longitudinibus abundare, easque omnimode absolutas esse. Nam per Solem et Venerem, quo ad inquisitionem Longitudinis Affixarum Stellarum, debita limitatio tunc temporis fieri nequaquam poterat, eo quod Sol nimium esset decliuis, ob magnam Declinationem Meridionalem, et ipsa Venus etiam circa Aequatorem, non admodum sublimis ferebatur: adeo vt post Occasum Solis, quamprimum Stellae conspicuae essent, tam alta non extitit, quin per Refractionem, quo ad Longitudinem, vlterior quam reuera erat cerneretur. Atque hinc factum est, vt neutiquam per Venerem et Solem, hoc negotium tunc temporis exacta praecisione in praxin deduci potuerit; imo necessarium erat Stellarum Longitudines talibus adhibitis medijs, debito vlteriores aliquantulum euadere. Obseruabam quidem et ego multoties circa eosdem dies Venerem interdiu a Sole, et noctu ad Stellas, non quod vllam exquisitam in Fixarum locis comprobationem inde sperarem, satis conscius et diuturna experientia edoctus, frustra id in medietate Zodiaci australi tentari, nisi Refractionum impedimenta, quae non semper sunt praecise aequalia, ob Aëris variam dispositionem, praecaueantur, sed saltem vt Veneris et Solis apparentes motus, e prius recte constitutis Fixarum locis inquirerem, et Refractionum insinuationes plenius expiscarer. Non itaque mirum est, ROTHMANNVM tunc temporis quinorum illorum Scrupulorum in vestris Longitudinibus abundantiam non animaduertisse; Siquidem tam Sol quam Venus secundum Zodiaci Longitudinem in pauculis Scrupulis per Refractionem obortis, vlteriores necessario apparebant, quam reuera in Caelo constituebantur, ideoque Fixarum ab ijs mensuratarum loca, etiam vlteriora quam oportuit, exhibuerunt. Sique meas Obseruationes tunc in Venere et Sole habitas, ad limitanda Fixarum loca examinâro, etiam inde colligetur 4. vel 5. Scrupulorum nimia promotio, neglecto videlicet Refractionum impedimento; si vero hoc per Tabulam ab experientia crebriore, a nobis huic negotio destinatam praecaueatur, loca Fixarum cadent praecise in eum situm, quo a nobis prius disposita sunt, quinis illis Scrupulis nullatenus deficientibus.</p><p>Verum enimuero etiamsi Refractionum et Parallaxium impedimenta in hac collatione omni diligentia excludantur, et caetera requisita, rectissime administrentur: tamen nondum omnis dubitandi occasio praerepta est, nisi etiam idem in Venere matutina, aeque ac vespertina, et in consimili fere supra Horizontem situ, parique a Terris remotione, vel ea quae vtrobique hinc incidit differentia debito modo correcta pari trutina diligenter expendatur et comprobetur, adhibita etiam Solis et Veneris ad vtraque tempora, quo ad Parallaxin, si qua forte inciderit, diuersitate; vt de Refractionibus nunc nihil dicam, quae sane etiam limitandae veniunt, nisi ab vtraque parte, in consimili tam Solis quam Veneris Altitudine, instituatur Obseruatio. Vbi enim viceuersa, eiusdem rei examinatio non fuerit adhibita, omniaque subtilissime inuicem collata, ad exactam Longitudinis Fixarum notitiam, etiam hac minus fallaci via procedendo, peruenire vix licebit: adeo in profundo, et interiori nucleo absconditur veritatis vnicum et immotum Centrum. Si itaque T. Cels. Mathematicus, aliquando per Venerem matutinam, pari adhibita diligentia, rem omnem expertus fuerit, et obstacula sese per Refractionum et Parallaxium diuersitatem ingerentia semouerit, non dubito, quin hac Methodo in Longitudines Affixarum meis apprime astipulantes, deueniet; antea vero absolutam in his certitudinem statuere, superuacaneum est. Quod autem per meum motum Solis, quem ROTHMANNVS existimauit circa 8. Gradum ♒ pauculis Minutis iusto tardiorem, defectionem quinorum illorum Scrupulorum, quae meis numeris inesse putatur, excusare laborat, frustraneum videtur; Siquidem locus Solis verus in tam decliui situ per Instrumenta exquisitissima, et adhibita etiam Parallaxi, praecise caelitus non innotescit. Ideoque praemonui in primis meis literis, infra tricesimum Gradum Altitudinis, non dari per Obseruationem, Locum Solis ratum, ob Refractionem in decliuiori situ irrepentem; Et Tuae Cels. Mathematicum etiam per literas admonui, in Boreali Semicirculo cursum Solis solummodo citra errorem examinandum, non item in Australi, ob Refractionum saepedictam insinuationem. Atque haec fuit non minima occasio, cur summus ille Copernicus in Apogaeo, Eccentricitateque Solis constituendis, tantopere deuiârit, quod videlicet ad medias partes Scorpij ingressum eius, vna cum Aequinoctialibus transitibus contulerit, Sole tunc ob Refractionem sensibilem non in eo loco apparente, in quo reuera constituebatur.</p><p>Cum autem Obseruationibus per T. Cels. Instrumenta factis, locus Solis e nostra Ephemeride depromtus, tum circa ambo Aequinoctia, tum etiam in toto Boreali Semicirculo apprime correspondeat, vt ex literis T. Cels. Mathematici ad me datis liquet; necesse est, et in reliquo Semicirculo Australi, nullam ab ipso Caelo contingere discrepantiam, et si qua illic per Instrumenta diligentius animaduertenti apparuerit, ea citra omne dubium per accidens, ob Refractionis hoc incommodum irrepit; ideoque cautionem diligentem hac in parte adhibendam antea serio indicaui. Quod enim in fine Ianuarij T. C. indicat, Locum Solis per T. C. Quadrantem inuentum non discrepasse a mea Ephemeride, ideoque T. Cels. Mathematicum in examinatione Fixarum, per locum Veneris, eodem vsum fuisse, id, quomodo cum prioribus consistere possit, non satis assequor; vt potius vitium aliquod Obseruationi illi subesse suspicer. Nam licet Sol circa vltimos dies Ianuarij, quinis proxime Gradibus apud vos sit eleuatior quam hîc, tamen nondum adeo in sua Altitudine meridiana sublimatur, quin Refractioni adhuc satis sensibili obnoxius sit, ideoque tunc etiam cum nostra Ephemeride adamussim quadrare non poterit, nisi Refractionis impedimentum excludatur. Quia vero T. Cels. indicat per suum Mathematicum, Ephemeridem nostram ad elapsum 87. annum conuenienter fuisse extensam; vt igitur eum ab illo labore, ad plures annos hunc calculum continuandi, inposterum leuem, curaui quinque proximorum annorum Ephemerides Solis, e nostris Obseruationibus et Tabulis circa motum eius deriuatas, in adiunctis chartis describi, e quibus ad aliquot antecedentes, et sequentes annos, facilior est reductio, cum quarto quolibet Sol in eundem proxime locum, ijsdem Mensium diebus et horis reuertatur. Spero autem locum Solis per accuratam Obseruationem caelitus quaesitum, cum hac nostra restitutione, collatione facta, nihil discriminis, quod vllo modo in sensus incurrere possit, illaturum, modo in Australi Semicirculo Refractionis, de qua aliquoties sedulo monui, obstacula remoueantur.</p><p>Porro quod T. Celsit. Mathematicus ex eo, quod Longitudines meae a vestris quinis Scrupulis deficiant, suspicatur me nimium tribuere Refractionibus, id longe aliter se habere, ipsemet confiteretur, si, quam subtili diligentia, quamque varijs modis Refractionum dimensionem, per diuersimoda Instrumenta, tam in Sole quam Stellis, aliquot ab hinc annis scrutatus sum, satis perspectum haberet. Non enim eas ad libitum, sed quales ipsa Experientia caelitus suppeditat, assumendas censeo. Quae res non mediocri studio a me multoties, si quid aliud, elaborata est. Siquidem sine Refractionum exquisita discretione, verorum in Stellis locorum non conceditur omnis erroris expers inquisitio. Licet autem Aëris diuersitas in his nonnihil aliquando alterare possit, tamen si Caelum vndiquaque fuerit apprime serenum, nullique crassiores vapores Horizontem obtenebrârint, semper eandem pene adinuenio Refractionum in consimili Altitudine quantitatem, vt citra omne dubium persuasum habeam, illarum aliam, quam oportuit, assumtionem a nobis non esse commissam, neque hinc in Stellarum a me redintegratis locis, aliquid vitij obortum. Nec etiam tempus in meis Obseruationibus, siue per Armillas, siue per Horologia, siue etiam per Quadrantem Azimuthalem adhibitum, vitam parere potuit in his ipsis ambiguitatem, quemadmodum Tuae Celsitud. Mathematicus frustra etiam suspicatur. Cum enim a Sole per Venerem ad Stellas, vel viceuersa instituitur obseruatio, duorum vel trium Scrupulorum, aut etiam paulo plurium, non venit necessario adhibenda in temporis denotatione exacta ratio, siquidem nec Sol nec Venus interuallo decimae vel octauae partis vnius horae, sensibiliter locum varient. Nihilominus tamen vbique verum temporis momentum in ipso Scrupulo varia ratione a nobis applicatur. Si per transitus Meridianos vel Azimuthales Siderum situs rimarer, iuxta modum Tuae Celsitudini vsitatum, vtique exactissima temporis, vel in ipsis Scrupulis secundis cognitio requireretur. Verum tum hanc ipsam ob causam, tum etiam plerasque alias, eam obseruandi rationem, tanquam hallucinationi facile obnoxiam, minus probo. Refractionem Solis tantummodo in circulo Verticali exhiberi non equidem ignoro, sed eam nihilominus, tum ad Eclipticam, tum etiam Aequatorem, prout opus fuerit, applicare didici, adeo, vt Tabulas in eum vsum e Triangulorum fontibus deriuatus, et votis meis hac in parte abunde satisfacientes, in promptu habeam; vt inde in tempore etiam per Armillas aequatorias praecise inueniendo, si quando ijs ad hoc officium vti libuerit, nulla incidere possit, vel minima deuiatio; Quamuis in temporum Minutijs per Armillas hasce exactius scrutandis, libentius vtar Stellis iuxta Meridianum, potissimum vero circa ipsam Aequatoris Altitudinem versantibus, eo, quod eo in loco Refractio nihil dispendij inferat, et tali in situ ocyssime motu primo reuoluantur, temporisque minutissimas differentias commodissime discernant.</p><p>Verum ne pluribus opus sit, his circa nostras Obseruationes dubitationibus obuiare, vnicum saltem addam, quod citra omnem controuersiam est irrefragabile. Si aliquis in meis Fixarum locis, vel minimus lapsus subesset, quibuscunque tandem occasionibus ille siue his, siue alijs incidisset, certe in reciproca Obseruatione, tam ad Venerem vespertinam, quam matutinam accurata diligentia a nobis instituta, is qualiscunque esset, sese aperte manifestasset; Quemadmodum in vltimis literis T. Celsit. significaui, me non solum Anno 82. multoties per Venerem occidentalem a Sole, et interdiu admodum alte euidenterque apparentem, sed etiam Anno 85. per eandem orientalem, iuxta medietatem Septembris, loca Stellarum Fixarum diligentissime, mediante loco Solis vtrobique exacte comperto, restituisse, et examine accurato, ea citra omne sensibile vitium recte se habere deprehendisse. Ob id etiam Cels. Tua non abs re asseuerat; si per Venerem matutinam omnia viceuersa comprobata habeam, non facile constare, qua in parte discrepantiae istius inter nos occasio lateat. Reuera enim caetera omnia, quae prius in dubium vocata sunt, soluuntur, irritaque euadunt, vbi circa Orientem idem quod in Occasu prius fiebat, alternatim exploratum fuerit. Si qua enim furtim, vel in Refractione, vel Parallaxi, aut etiam in loco Solis sese insinuaret vitiositas, eam reciproca Obseruatio latere non sinit, tunc enim omnis ad errandum procliuitas facile praecaueatur.</p><p>Quapropter nullum prorsus apud me relinquitur dubium, Longitudines Stellarum meis Instrumentis instauratas, ipsi Caelo exquisitissime congruere, et quina illa Scrupula, in quibus inter nos differimus, nullatenus in ijs desiderari. Quinetiam euidentioris certitudinis comprobandae gratia, feci Mense Augusto praecedentis Anni denuo huius rei periculum, idque duobus diebus videlicet 17. et 18. quibus circa Veneris cum Ioue coniunctionem ferme partilem, serenitatem nactus admodum puram, et opportunam, Veneris Stellam mane a quibusdam fixis Sideribus, praesertim vtroque Geminorum capite, diligentissime obseruaui, posteaque ad Solem supra Horizontem sufficiente Altitudine eleuatum, omnia direxi, et deinde non solum, cum animaduersionibus Anno 82. in ijsdem quoque Stellis, beneficio Veneris vespertinae habitis, accurate contuli, habita etiam ratione in Sole et Venere circa Parallaxeos aliqualem diuersitatem incidentis discrepantiae, sed etiam cum Obseruationibus Veneris, ad eas ipsas aliasque propinquas Stellas, hoc ipso Anno, Mense Martio, et Aprili, cum Venus interdiu occidentalis a Sole satis appareret, multoties diligentissime exploratis, denuo expendi. Cumque omnia admodum praecisa trutina in his vbique librâssem, tantum abest, vt Longitudines Fixarum prius a me constitutas, vlterius producendas adinuenerim, vt si quid in illis alterandum esset, potius dimidium Scrupulum redundans auferendum foret, quae tamen praecisio otiosa est, peneque inperceptibilis. Cum itaque bis viceuersa mane et vesperi comprobârim, per Venerem et Solem, Stellarum Fixarum restitutionem praecisam, vtpote Anno 82. vesperi et 85. mane, et nunc etiam denuo proxime elapso 87. mane, et hoc eodem labente 88. vesperi, ita vt collatis, inuicemque viceuersa ponderatis singulis, in idem prorsus Scrupulum earum loca, quo a me prius ordinata sunt, recidant, nihilque sensibilis varietatis vllo modo suggerant, non possum non tam crebro, varijs Organis exanimatae et diuersimode atque reciproce comprobatae verificationi acquiescere, praesertim cum vltimae binae viceuersa habitae Obseruationes, tam probe prioribus suffragium tulerint, et insuper hoc habuerint commoditatis, quod Venus vtrobique aequaliter fere a Terra distiterit, et ob id Parallaxium differentias in consimili proxime Altitudine insensibiles obtulerit; Refractionum etiam obstaculo in pari circiter eleuatione (ita enim Obseruationes ordino, vt quae in ijsdem circiter Altitudinibus accepta sunt, inuicem conferantur) nihil vitiositatis ingerente. Testimonium etiam non obscurum praebet huic nostrae emendationi Stella ea, quae in Virginis Spica collocatur; Haec enim ex sola Declinatione vna cum Latitudine, per distantiam a Stellula quadam Draconis vicina Polo Eclipticae, peculiari ratione prius sedulo a me inquisita, praefinitis nostris Stellarum Longitudinibus, citra aliorum mediorum cognitionem, apprime astipulatur, et in idem Scrupulum, (velut haec et pleraque alia huc facientia, breui, fauente Deo, locupletius e certis Obseruationibus et Demonstrationibus, manifesta comprobataque reddam,) consentit.</p><p>An autem ad tam exactam praecisionem vlli Antiquorum inde ab Astronomiae excultae primordio, vel Recentiorum aliqui, vsque ad haec tempora, peruenerint, et quantum industriae, laboris et temporis (vt de sumtibus Instrumentorum non dicam, qui omni aestimatione maiores sunt) in his enucleatius indagandis impenderim, aliorum, rem omnem fundamentaliter intelligentium, esto iudicium; inter quos Tua Cels. vt maiorum illustri prosapia Princeps natus generosissimus: ita etiam ingenij acumine, et iudicij grauitate, ipsaque adeo experientia vere principem locum non minori Eruditionis quam generis claritudine, meretur. Haec licet ita se habeant, quod in Longitudinibus Fixarum a me constitutis, nihil prorsus sensibilis deuiationis, aut dubij committatur, consulte tamen facit Tua Celsit. quod Obseruationibus, proprijs Instrumentis conquisitis, constanter inhaeret, donec exactiorem aliquam inquisitionem in his institui curârit. Nec enim decet in ijs, quae sensibus exponuntur et Mechanice adhibita competenti diligentia peruestigari, indeque per Geometricam certitudinem in numeros resolui queunt, aliorum inuentis contentos esse; nisi etiam per propriam, et minime dubiam experientiam eadem comprobata fuerint. Intelligo praeterea Tuam Celsitudinem de publicatione locorum Affixarum Stellarum hactenus per sua Instrumenta instauratorum cogitare, suoque Mathematico hanc eximiam prouinciam demandâsse, id equidem omni laude dignissimum censeo; ne tot laboribus et vigilijs, tantaque industria et sedulitate multorum annorum decursu comparatae caelestes Obseruationes, suo frustrentur vsufructu. Quare vel hac sola ratione Celsitudo Tua de tota Posteritate optime merebitur, et ab ea nunquam intermorituram famam, cum grata nominis celebratione, reportabit, quae apud eruditos et intelligentes, non minus quam Illustris prosapia, et Gubernationis dignitas, claritatem, et reuerentiam eximiam perpetuabit.</p><p>Optarem vero plurimum, vt Celsit. Tua pari sedulitate Planetarum etiam apparentes motus, caelitus indagari curaret, id quod per restituta Affixarum loca, non difficulter praestatur. Et si non alibi, saltem iuxta Acronychias trium superiorum Planetarum fulsiones, et in Venere atque Mercurio prope extremas eorum a Sole digressiones, nonnunquam vero in his omnibus, circa loca intermedia, apparentias eorum exquisite inuestigari, plurimum conduceret, vt de Apogaeorum et Eccentricitatum, proportionisque circularium motuum ad inuicem, ratione, praecisior quam hactenus cognitio inquiri posset. In quibus omnibus ego quoque multis ab hinc annis non inutilem, vti spero, nauaui operam, veluti suo tempore fauente Numine patebit; quando ea quae in his magna, et diuturna sedulitate adinueni, Astronomiae cultoribus communicandi dabitur occasio, vbi plures in Saturno praesertim extremae noctis fulsiones, in remotioribus Eccentrici locis, quod tardius in eo perficitur, per accuratas Obseruationes examinauero. Quod Tua Celsit. tam clementer et benigne pollicetur se non passuram, vt meae Obseruationes, et verificationes in Affixarum locis, hinc inde spargantur, mihi ex voto maxime cedit. Ipsemet enim per opportunam commoditatem, mea in restitutione Affixarum Stellarum inuenta publicare, et ad posteros transmittere decreui. Discrimen autem quod inter nos hac in parte incidit, etiamsi interea forte non concilietur, nihil fere quod reputandum veniat, importat; et recte Tua Celsitudo asseuerat, totam rem melius apud alios fidem promerituram, vbi nihil in gratiam per mutuum consensum citra Obseruationis certae experientiam, admissum fuerit. Vbi vero Celsitudo Tua sua Instrumenta, alia ratione (prout se in animo habere scribit) instaurârit, non dubito, quin certo certius experietur, quinque illa Scrupula, de quibus inter nos agitur, in praecedenti Tuae Celsit. Fixarum restitutione abundare; praesertim si per Veneris potissimum Stellam, tam matutinam quam vespertinam duplici ratione Obseruationem accuratam institui, debitoque modo examinari curârit; et tum demum propria experientia persuadente hunc excessum lubentius corriget, et inuentioni accuratiori volens manus porriget.</p><p>Rectissime interim Tua Celsitudo concludit tantillam inter nos discrepantiam non eius esse momenti, vt magnopere in mentionem veniat. Quam enim in tam perplexo negotio circa Longitudines Stellarum rite praefiniendas, quina senaue Scrupula facilime excidant, non ignorant illi, qui Astronomicarum Obseruationum fundamentalem aliquam cognitionem diutina tractatione assequuti sunt. Vtinam vero in duodecima illa gradûs portiuncula caelestes apparentiae, praesertim quo ad Planetarum cursus, certo nobis constarent, quam bene et feliciter toti Astronomiae consultum foret, quantoque labore, in ea denuo instauranda, supersedere nobis liceret. Sed nolo Tuam Celsitudinem pluribus nunc detinere, et si quae hîc ad totam rem plenius facientia praetermissa sunt, de ijs in alijs literis cum Tuae Celsit. Mathematico copiosius agam. Tuam vero Cels. demisse rogatam cupio, vt de hac sublimi materia Astronomica mecum per literas vlterius agere, conferreque non intermittat, mihique suas circa Fixarum Stellarum loca, quam primum absolutae fuerint, redintegratas calculationes, et si quid praeterea, ad rem Astronomicam quod impense faciat, elaboratum habeat, clementer communicare dignetur. Ego vicissim me meaque studia T. Cels. voluntati et desiderijs deuoueo.</p><p>Valeat T. Cels. quam diutissime et felicissime.<br>Datae Vraniburgi, Die 16. Augusti, Anno 1588.<br>T. C. addict. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: his most clement Prince and Lord. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>That I reply only now — later both than Your Highness's expectation and than my own will — to the letter which, about the <b>autumnal equinox of the preceding year</b> [1587], Your Highness clemently sent me, most Illustrious and most Generous Prince: occasion was given by various occupations and certain impediments which fell about the beginning of Spring, and brought it about that I could not have these very [letters], which I had then prepared, conveyed to Your Highness by those who set out hence to the recent spring <b>Fairs</b> [Frankfurt]. There was added this too: that in <b>March and April</b> I hoped to obtain more observations of <b>Venus</b>, appearing by day and <b>western of the Sun</b> [as morning star], so that I might more fully compare them with the prior morning [observations], especially those had in <b>August of the preceding year</b>, and might institute a settled judgment and a fuller exploration of those <b>five minutes</b> about which there is controversy between us in establishing the <b>Longitudes of the Fixed [stars]</b> — which indeed I have several times diligently accomplished, as I shall afterward expound more fully [to] Your Highness, [namely] what I have drawn out and found out hence. Submissively, therefore, I have Your Highness asked to deign to hold this delay in replying — especially since it is now made good with <b>interest</b> — candidly and clemently excused. I wrote also meanwhile to Your Highness's <b>Mathematician</b> [Rothmann], and to him I set forth the reasons of this delay, to be referred to Your Highness.</p><p>Lest, therefore, what has hitherto been omitted be deferred longer, I will now reply in order to those [things] which Your Highness's letter sent to me contained. That my <b>Observations</b> communicated to Your Highness pleased greatly, and that <b>that exact agreement</b> between the <b>Declinations and distances of the Fixed Stars</b> investigated by Your Highness's Instruments and by mine begot the highest admiration — this I perceived, not without pleasure, from the very front of Your Highness's letter. And truly, so exquisite a method of scrutinizing the stars — that Observations taken in different places agree so precisely — has hitherto remained unusual and utterly unheard-of; as Your Highness too most rightly pronounces your opinion about these [matters]. And hence it plainly appears that our <b>Instruments</b>, by which the <b>Altitudes and Distances</b> of the stars are taken, are on both sides exactly right, and so perfectly elaborated that they are subject to no sensible fault — especially after that tiny <b>deviation of the perpendicular</b> in Your Highness's <b>Quadrant</b> was set right by the Mathematician. I therefore greatly congratulate the <b>restoration of Astronomy</b> that the method of the <b>Sights</b> [<i>Pinnacidia</i>] and of the <b>Division</b>, and of the exact structure of the Instruments — by which it is permitted to investigate the places of the stars from the heaven to the very <b>minute</b>, nay to an aliquot part of it (which hitherto seemed unwonted to ancient and modern Astronomers alike), [and] which I have used some years ago, not, I hope, without fruit — has become known to Your Highness too; and that, approved by Your Highness, it is brought into Astronomical practice, to the great profit and success of this most praiseworthy science: without which, indeed, every way of arriving at so exact an agreement of Observations (in which no aberration from the celestial appearances themselves lie hidden) would otherwise have been closed off — [namely] the conformity between us in the fabric and Division of the Instruments and the application of the <b>Dioptres</b> [sights]. But that we nonetheless differ in the <b>Longitudes of the Stars by nearly five Minutes</b> — by which mine are found earlier [smaller] — easily merits its excuse among those who have sufficiently surveyed the perplexed labyrinths of this subtle inquiry. And therefore Your Highness rightly asserts that this discrepancy is not so worthy of wonder; since those ancient Princes of Astronomy scarcely attained the truth in these [matters] within a <b>sixth part of a Degree</b> [10′], nor even attempted to aspire to greater precision — judging it, as it were, less possible. Which, however, I judge befell them not so much on account of the smallness of the Instruments, and the various distraction of the <b>Armillae</b> here and there during observing, as because they had learned to observe the Stars only through <b>dioptric holes</b> [<i>foramina</i>]; where, unless that which is set on the little plate [tablet] more remote from the eye be largish, even the brighter Stars are seen with the greatest difficulty; and if this be granted, on account of the size of the hole (which occupies an aliquot part of the Division of Degrees), an exquisite observation can by no means hold within a few minutes. But that their <b>Astrolabic Armillae</b> were of just magnitude is clear enough from this, that <b>Hipparchus and Ptolemy</b> detected even the <b>equinoctial ingresses</b> by them; and Ptolemy himself seems to reject certain Armillae set up at <b>Alexandria</b>, which were too large, as less suitable. Yet, with peculiar diligence applied, even largish Armillae can be so skilfully composed that, during observing, by their Revolution or the disjunction of the circles, they insinuate nothing of faultiness — although no small circumspection is required in these, which I do not doubt the ancients too applied for the most part.</p><p>Indeed I too experience, very often, that the places of the stars are observed conveniently and precisely enough by <b>Armillae</b>. For in my <b>four Armillary machines</b> — partly <b>Equinoctial</b>, partly <b>Zodiacal</b>, which are of such magnitude that they exhibit single Minutes distinctly according to the division customary to us (which, however, I have constructed by a more apt compendium than was done by the ancients) — by whichever of them it pleases to use, the true place of the Star is exhibited in the very Minute, agreeing entirely with that which is found either by <b>distances</b> with the help of the <b>Sextants</b>, or also by <b>Azimuthal Quadrants</b> revolved with peculiar skill — especially if equal diligence be applied on both sides during observing. For otherwise I would attribute nothing to Instruments fashioned in diverse manners, unless they agreed on entirely the same mark of truth; and for that cause too I have hitherto sought abundance and variety in their structure, so that, by observing not in one way but in manifold [ways], a more unfolded certainty might be more expeditiously, beyond all doubt, confirmed. But in all these — because I use the method, devised by me some years ago, of <b>collimating the Stars through those Parallel Slits</b> [<i>Rimulae Parallelae</i>] — their exquisite places lie open to indubitable observation far more readily and scrupulously than [they did] to the ancients through the <b>holes of the Tablets</b>, so subject (as I said) to error. Through these various machines, therefore, skilfully elaborated with every possible subtlety and perfection, I have sought out the <b>Longitudes of the Stars</b> in manifold ways; and, trusting not to some one Instrument, but examining the whole matter variously by diverse [instruments] and weighing each [thing] with great circumspection, I have administered this great business — and that not only from the Sun's place exactly corrected beforehand by means of the <b>Moon</b> (as the ancients attempted), but also with <b>the star of Venus</b> intervening, it has been explored by me very often; so that I doubt nothing at all that the Longitudes established by me lack all sensible fault, and have been brought to as great a precision as the diligence of any man and the keenness of [his] eyes will be able to comprehend. Nor yet do I therefore judge Your Highness's observations to be disapproved, but much rather I admire that they have come even to that exactness, and to a concord very near to ours — especially in that practice of Observation which is applied through <b>Altitudes and Meridian transits</b>, with the notation of time added, which is clearly administered with the greatest diligence, since otherwise it is exposed to not a few difficulties and windings.</p><p>That, therefore, Your Highness is unwilling to depart from your findings, on account of the no-mean diligence applied in the Observations, you doubtless have reasons not to be despised, which I too am unwilling to gainsay; for the difference between us is not so great as to be sensibly prejudicial to Astronomical certainty. But since Your Highness thinks your constitutions in the Longitudes to be so settled and exact that you cannot depart from them — because they were found not only after the <b>corrected perpendicular of the Quadrant</b>, by <b>Azimuthal transits</b> with the help of an exquisite <b>Clock</b> [<i>Horologium</i>], but also by <b>the star of Venus</b>, in January of the preceding year, several times explored and confirmed by a method also used by me — if I shall indicate my opinion about these a little more freely [to] Your Highness, I do not doubt that Your Highness will receive it with a clement and kind mind. As regards the <b>former</b> method — of observing the places of the Stars through <b>Altitudes and Azimuths, with the time of Observation given at the same time</b> — although, after the emended perpendicular of the Quadrant, it replaces their Longitudes in entirely the same position as before, I do not for that reason think every handle of hesitation removed. I signified to Your Highness in my last letter for what reasons this way of scrutinizing the positions of the stars seems to me not safe enough. For with whatever diligence the Quadrant be ordered, and however skilfully the Clocks be elaborated, yet there, where it is least thought, some error stealthily insinuates itself, which easily impedes the precision of a few minutes — whether because the Clock does not indicate the single moments of time with a perpetually equal tenor, or because in the Quadrant itself, as to its plane (not on every side corresponding to the plane of the vertical circles), some tiny fault lies hidden, or because the same happens from any other cause not easily obvious to sight. If, however, Your Highness by no means distrusts this manner of observing the stars, let at least the test of this matter be made anew, most diligently, <b>at both equinoxes</b>, in such a way that on both [occasions] the <b>place of the Sun</b> be diligently emended by the <b>Meridian Altitude</b>, or even drawn from our restitution of its motion; let there also be present in this practice some variety and abundance of most exquisite Clocks, and let care be taken that they correspond exactly to one another in the least particles of Minutes; [and] if then (the whole matter being reiterated, not once but several times), examining the places of the Stars on both sides and vice versa, they fall back into the same Minute (the reckoning of their interval being applied, since one must here use different Stars), then I shall more easily concede that their places have been found scrupulously enough by this Method. But with whatever diligence and precaution this way of observing the stars be handled, it is nonetheless judged by me to be slippery, and very prone to deviation, especially in a few minutes.</p><p>The <b>other</b> reasoning, however — by which Your Highness's Mathematician undertook the same [task] through <b>the star of Venus</b> — I would much prefer to this, because it leads more safely to the deeper track of truth. Yet that he found, by this very [method], in January of the preceding year, the same Longitudes of the Stars as before, and still differing from mine by five Minutes, does not yet sufficiently confirm that nothing at all is in excess in your Longitudes, and that they are in every way absolute. For through the <b>Sun and Venus</b>, as regards the inquiry into the Longitude of the Fixed Stars, the due limitation could by no means be made at that time, because the <b>Sun was too low</b>, on account of [its] great Southern Declination, and Venus itself too was borne about the <b>Equator</b>, not very lofty: so much so that after the Sun's setting, as soon as the Stars were visible, [Venus] did not stand so high but that, <b>through Refraction</b>, as to Longitude, it was seen further [on] than it really was. And hence it came about that this business could by no means be brought into practice with exact precision, through Venus and the Sun, at that time; nay, it was necessary that the Longitudes of the Stars, with such means applied, should come out somewhat further than due. I too indeed observed, very often about the same days, <b>Venus by day</b> [westward] from the Sun, and by night to the Stars — not that I hoped thence for any exquisite confirmation in the places of the Fixed [stars], being sufficiently aware and taught by long experience that it is attempted in vain in the southern half of the Zodiac, unless the impediments of <b>Refractions</b> (which are not always precisely equal, on account of the various disposition of the Air) be guarded against — but only that I might inquire into the apparent motions of Venus and the Sun from the previously rightly-established places of the Fixed [stars], and might more fully fish out the insinuations of the Refractions. It is therefore no wonder that <b>Rothmann</b> did not at that time notice the excess of those five Minutes in your Longitudes; since both the Sun and Venus, according to the Longitude of the Zodiac, necessarily appeared further [on] by a few minutes arising through Refraction than they were really constituted in the Heaven, and therefore exhibited the places of the Fixed [stars] measured from them also further than was due. And if I shall examine my Observations had then in Venus and the Sun, for limiting the places of the Fixed [stars], a too-great <b>advancement of 4 or 5 Minutes</b> will also be gathered thence, the impediment of the Refractions being neglected; but if this be guarded against by a <b>Table</b> destined for this business by us, [drawn] from more frequent experience, the places of the Fixed [stars] will fall precisely into that position in which they were disposed by us before, those five Minutes by no means being wanting.</p><p>But truly, even if the impediments of <b>Refractions and Parallaxes</b> be excluded in this collation with all diligence, and the other requisites be most rightly administered, yet not every occasion of doubting is forestalled, unless the same be diligently weighed and confirmed <b>in morning Venus equally as in evening [Venus]</b>, and in a nearly similar position above the Horizon, and at an equal distance from the Earth — or [unless] that difference which here falls on both sides be duly corrected on an equal balance — with the diversity of the Sun and Venus at both times, as regards <b>Parallax</b> (if any perchance fall), also applied; to say nothing now of the Refractions, which truly must also be limited, unless the Observation be instituted on both sides at a similar Altitude both of the Sun and of Venus. For where the examination of the same matter has not been applied <b>vice versa</b>, and all [things] most subtly collated with one another, it will scarcely be permitted to arrive at exact knowledge of the Longitude of the Fixed [stars], even proceeding by this less fallacious way: so deep, and in an inner kernel, is hidden the <b>single and immovable Centre of truth</b>. If, therefore, Your Highness's Mathematician shall some time have tested the whole matter through <b>morning Venus</b>, with equal diligence applied, and shall have removed the obstacles which insinuate themselves through the diversity of Refractions and Parallaxes, I do not doubt that by this Method he will come to Longitudes of the Fixed [stars] perfectly agreeing with mine; but to establish absolute certainty in these beforehand is superfluous. But that he labours to excuse the <b>defect of those five Minutes</b>, which is thought to be in my numbers, through my <b>motion of the Sun</b> — which <b>Rothmann</b> thought, about the <b>8th degree of Aquarius [♒]</b>, slower than just by a few Minutes — seems vain; since the true place of the Sun, in so low a position, is not known precisely from the heaven even through the most exquisite Instruments, even with Parallax applied. And therefore I forewarned in my first letter that, <b>below the thirtieth Degree of Altitude</b>, the place of the Sun is not given as settled by Observation, on account of the Refraction creeping in at a lower position; and I admonished Your Highness's Mathematician too, by letter, that the course of the Sun is to be examined without error only in the <b>Northern Semicircle</b>, not likewise in the Southern, on account of the oft-mentioned insinuation of Refractions. And this was no small occasion why that supreme <b>Copernicus</b> deviated so greatly in establishing the <b>Apogee and Eccentricity of the Sun</b> — namely, that he compared its ingress into the middle parts of <b>Scorpio</b> together with the equinoctial transits, the Sun then, on account of sensible Refraction, not appearing in that place in which it was really constituted.</p><p>But since, in the Observations made by Your Highness's Instruments, the place of the Sun drawn from our <b>Ephemeris</b> corresponds perfectly both about both Equinoxes and also in the whole Northern Semicircle — as is clear from the letters of Your Highness's Mathematician given to me — it is necessary that in the remaining Southern Semicircle too no discrepancy arise from the Heaven itself; and if any there appear to one observing more diligently by the Instruments, it without all doubt crept in <b>per accidens</b>, on account of this inconvenience of Refraction; and therefore I before seriously indicated that diligent caution must be applied in this regard. For that which Your Highness indicates [happened] at the end of January — that the place of the Sun found by Your Highness's Quadrant did not differ from my Ephemeris, and that therefore Your Highness's Mathematician, in the examination of the Fixed [stars] through the place of Venus, used the same — how it can be consistent with the foregoing I do not sufficiently grasp; so that I rather suspect some fault to underlie that Observation. For although the Sun, about the last days of January, is higher with you by nearly five Degrees than here, yet it is not yet so raised in its meridian Altitude but that it is subject to a still sensible Refraction, and therefore even then it cannot square exactly with our Ephemeris, unless the impediment of Refraction be excluded. But since Your Highness indicates, through your Mathematician, that our <b>Ephemeris</b> had been conveniently extended to the elapsed [15]87th year — so that I might relieve him hereafter of that labour of continuing this calculation for several years, I have taken care to describe, in the attached sheets, <b>Ephemerides of the Sun for the next five years</b>, derived from our Observations and Tables concerning its motion; from which the reduction to several preceding and following years is easier, since every fourth [year] the Sun returns to nearly the same place on the same days and hours of the Months. But I hope that the place of the Sun sought from the heaven by accurate Observation, a collation being made with this our restitution, will introduce no difference which could in any way fall under the senses — provided that in the Southern Semicircle the obstacles of Refraction, of which I have several times diligently warned, be removed.</p><p>Furthermore, that Your Highness's Mathematician, from the fact that my Longitudes fall short of yours by five Minutes, suspects that I <b>attribute too much to Refractions</b> — that this is far otherwise, he would himself confess, if he had sufficiently surveyed with what subtle diligence, and in how various ways, I have scrutinized the <b>measurement of Refractions</b>, through diverse Instruments, both in the Sun and in the Stars, for some years now. For I judge that they must be assumed not at pleasure, but such as <b>Experience</b> itself supplies from the heaven. Which matter has been elaborated by me very often, if anything [has], with no mean study. For without an exquisite discrimination of the Refractions, an inquiry free of all error into the true places in the Stars is not granted. But although the diversity of the Air can sometimes alter these somewhat, yet if the Heaven be on every side perfectly serene, and no thicker vapours have darkened the Horizon, I always find nearly the same quantity of Refractions at a similar Altitude, so that I hold it persuaded beyond all doubt that no other assumption of them than was due has been committed by us, nor that anything of fault has arisen hence in the places of the Stars restored by me. Nor could the <b>time</b> in my Observations — whether by Armillae, or by Clocks, or even by the Azimuthal Quadrant applied — beget in these very [matters] an ambiguity, as Your Highness's Mathematician also vainly suspects. For when an observation is instituted from the Sun through Venus to the Stars, or vice versa, no exact reckoning of two or three Minutes (or even a little more) needs necessarily to be applied in the precise notation of time, since neither the Sun nor Venus varies its place sensibly in the interval of a tenth or eighth part of one hour. Nonetheless, everywhere the true moment of time in the very Minute is applied by us by a various method. If I were to investigate the positions of the stars through <b>Meridian or Azimuthal transits</b>, according to the manner customary to Your Highness, the most exact knowledge of time — even in the very seconds — would certainly be required. But both for this very cause, and also for very many others, I less approve that way of observing, as easily subject to hallucination. That the Refraction of the Sun is exhibited only in the <b>Vertical circle</b> I am indeed not ignorant; but I have nonetheless learned to apply it, both to the <b>Ecliptic</b> and also to the <b>Equator</b>, as need shall be — so much so that I have ready to hand <b>Tables</b> derived for that use from the founts of <b>Triangles</b> [trigonometry], and abundantly satisfying my wishes in this regard; so that thence, in finding the time precisely even by the equatorial Armillae (if ever it shall please to use them for this office), no deviation, even the least, can fall. Although, in scrutinizing the minutiae of time more exactly by these Armillae, I more gladly use <b>Stars near the Meridian</b> — especially those moving about the very Altitude of the Equator — because in that place the Refraction inflicts no loss, and in such a position they are revolved by the primary [diurnal] motion most swiftly, and most conveniently discern the minutest differences of time.</p><p>But, lest more be needed to meet these doubts about our Observations, I will add at least one [thing], which beyond all controversy is <b>irrefragable</b>. If any, even the least, lapse underlay my places of the Fixed [stars] — by whatever occasions it had befallen, whether these or others — surely in the <b>reciprocal Observation</b>, instituted by us with accurate diligence both to <b>evening Venus and to morning [Venus]</b>, it, whatever it were, would have openly manifested itself. As I signified in [my] last letter to Your Highness, I restored the places of the Fixed Stars most diligently, the place of the Sun being on both sides exactly ascertained, not only in the <b>year [15]82</b> several times through Venus <b>western</b> of the Sun (and appearing by day quite high and evidently), but also in the <b>year [15]85</b> through the same <b>eastern</b> [Venus], about the middle of September; and by accurate examination I found that they stood right, free of all sensible fault. On which account too Your Highness not without reason asserts that, if I have everything confirmed vice versa through morning Venus, it does not easily appear in what part the occasion of that discrepancy between us lies hidden. For in reality all the other [things] which were before called into doubt are resolved, and turn out null, when about the <b>East</b> the same that was before done in the <b>West</b> has been alternately explored. For if any faultiness should stealthily insinuate itself, whether in Refraction, or Parallax, or even in the place of the Sun, the <b>reciprocal Observation</b> does not allow it to lie hidden; for then every proclivity to error is easily guarded against.</p><p>Wherefore no doubt at all is left with me that the <b>Longitudes of the Stars</b> restored by my Instruments <b>agree most exquisitely with the Heaven itself</b>, and that those five Minutes, in which we differ between us, are by no means wanting in them. Nay, for the sake of confirming a more evident certainty, I made the test of this matter anew in the <b>month of August of the preceding year</b> [1587], and that on <b>two days, namely the 17th and 18th</b>, on which, about the nearly partile <b>conjunction of Venus with Jupiter</b>, having obtained a quite pure and opportune serenity, I most diligently observed the star of Venus in the morning from certain fixed Stars, especially from <b>both heads of Gemini</b> [Castor and Pollux]; and afterwards I directed everything to the Sun raised above the Horizon at a sufficient Altitude; and then I not only accurately compared [them] with the observations had in the <b>year [15]82</b> in those same Stars, by the benefit of evening Venus (the reckoning being had also of the discrepancy falling in the Sun and Venus from some diversity of Parallax), but also weighed [them] anew with the Observations of Venus, to those same and other neighbouring Stars, in this very Year [1588], in the <b>months of March and April</b>, when Venus appeared sufficiently by day, western of the Sun, several times most diligently explored. And when I had weighed all these everywhere on a quite precise balance, so far is it [from being the case] that I found the Longitudes of the Fixed [stars] previously established by me to be carried out <b>further</b>, that, if anything were to be altered in them, rather a redundant <b>half-Minute</b> would have to be taken away — a precision, however, which is idle and well-nigh imperceptible. Since therefore I have confirmed twice, vice versa, morning and evening, through Venus and the Sun, the precise restitution of the Fixed Stars — namely in [15]82 evening and [15]85 morning, and now again recently in the elapsed [15]87 morning, and in this same passing [15]88 evening — so that, the single [places] being collated and weighed vice versa with one another, their places fall back into entirely the same Minute in which they were ordered by me before, and suggest no sensible variety in any way: I cannot but acquiesce in a <b>verification</b> so frequently tried by various Instruments and confirmed in diverse and reciprocal ways — especially since the last two Observations had vice versa have borne such proper suffrage to the prior ones, and besides have had this convenience, that Venus on both [occasions] stood nearly equally distant from the Earth, and therefore offered insensible differences of Parallax at a nearly similar Altitude, the obstacle of Refractions too at about an equal elevation (for I so order the Observations that those taken at about the same Altitudes are compared with one another) introducing nothing of faultiness. A not-obscure testimony to this our emendation is also furnished by that <b>Star which is placed in the Spike of Virgo</b> [<b>Spica</b>]; for this, from the Declination alone together with the Latitude — by the distance from a certain <b>little star of Draco near the Pole of the Ecliptic</b>, sought beforehand by me diligently by a peculiar method — agrees perfectly with our predefined Longitudes of the Stars, without the knowledge of other means, and consents into the same Minute (as I shall shortly, God favouring, render this and very many other [things] making hither more richly manifest and confirmed, from certain Observations and Demonstrations).</p><p>But whether any of the <b>Ancients</b>, from the very beginning of cultivated Astronomy, or any of the <b>Moderns</b> down to these times, have arrived at so exact a precision — and how much industry, labour, and time (to say nothing of the <b>costs of the Instruments</b>, which are greater than all estimation) I have spent in investigating these [things] more unfoldedly — let the judgment be of others who understand the whole matter fundamentally; among whom Your Highness, as a Prince born most generous of an illustrious lineage of ancestors, so also by the acumen of [your] talent, the gravity of [your] judgment, and indeed experience itself, deserves a truly princely place, by no less clarity of Erudition than of birth. Although these [things] are so — that in the Longitudes of the Fixed [stars] established by me nothing at all of sensible deviation or doubt is committed — Your Highness nonetheless acts advisedly in adhering constantly to the Observations sought out by your own Instruments, until you shall have had some more exact inquiry instituted in these. For it is not fitting, in those [matters] which are exposed to the senses and are investigated Mechanically with competent diligence applied, and thence can be resolved into numbers by Geometrical certainty, to be content with the findings of others, unless the same have been confirmed by one's own and least-doubtful experience. I understand, besides, that Your Highness is thinking about the <b>publication of the places of the Fixed Stars</b> hitherto restored by your Instruments, and has assigned this eminent province to your Mathematician — which indeed I deem most worthy of all praise, lest the celestial Observations acquired by so many labours and vigils, and so great industry and diligence, over the course of many years, be frustrated of their usufruct. Wherefore by this sole reason Your Highness will deserve most excellently of all Posterity, and will carry back from it a never-dying fame, with grateful celebration of [your] name, which among the erudite and intelligent will perpetuate clarity and eminent reverence, no less than [your] Illustrious lineage and dignity of Government.</p><p>But I should wish very greatly that Your Highness would, with equal diligence, take care that the <b>apparent motions of the Planets</b> too be investigated from the heaven — which is not difficultly accomplished through the restored places of the Fixed [stars]. And it would conduce very much, if not elsewhere, at least to investigate their appearances exquisitely <b>near the Acronychal shinings</b> [oppositions] <b>of the three superior Planets</b>, and in <b>Venus and Mercury near their extreme digressions from the Sun</b> [maximum elongations], but sometimes, in all these, about the <b>intermediate places</b> — so that a more precise knowledge than hitherto could be inquired concerning the reckoning of the <b>Apogees and Eccentricities</b>, and the proportion of the circular motions to one another. In all which I too, for many years now, have bestowed not-useless labour (as I hope), as will appear in its time, the Deity favouring — when occasion will be given of communicating to the cultivators of Astronomy those [things] which I have found out in these with great and long diligence, where I shall have examined, by accurate Observations, more shinings of <b>Saturn</b> especially at the extremity of night [opposition], in the more remote places of the Eccentric, because it is more slowly completed in it. That Your Highness so clemently and kindly promises that you will not allow my Observations and verifications in the places of the Fixed [stars] to be scattered here and there falls out for me most according to [my] wish. For I myself have resolved, by an opportune convenience, to publish my findings in the restitution of the Fixed Stars, and to transmit [them] to posterity. But the difference which falls between us in this part — even if perchance it be not meanwhile reconciled — imports almost nothing that need be reckoned; and Your Highness rightly asserts that the whole matter will better merit faith among others, where nothing has been admitted <b>for favour, by mutual consent, without the experience of a sure Observation</b>. But when Your Highness shall have restored your Instruments by another method (as you write you have in mind), I do not doubt that you will most surely find by experience that those five Minutes about which we deal are <b>in excess</b> in Your Highness's previous restitution of the Fixed [stars] — especially if you shall have had an accurate Observation instituted, and examined in the due manner, chiefly through <b>the star of Venus, both morning and evening, by a double method</b>; and then at last, your own experience persuading, you will more gladly correct this excess, and willingly extend your hand to the more accurate finding.</p><p>Meanwhile Your Highness most rightly concludes that so tiny a discrepancy between us is not of such moment as to come greatly into mention. For that, in so perplexed a business concerning the <b>due predefining of the Longitudes of the Stars</b>, five or six Minutes very easily slip away, those are not ignorant who have attained, by long handling, some fundamental knowledge of Astronomical Observations. But would that the celestial appearances, especially as to the course of the Planets, were surely established for us within that <b>twelfth little part of a degree</b> [5′] — how well and happily would the whole of Astronomy be provided for, and from how great a labour, in restoring it anew, would it be permitted us to forbear! But I do not wish to detain Your Highness with more now; and if any [things] making more fully for the whole matter have here been omitted, about these I shall deal more copiously in other letters with Your Highness's Mathematician. But I desire Your Highness humbly asked not to cease to deal and confer with me further by letter about this sublime Astronomical material, and to deign clemently to communicate to me your re-integrated calculations concerning the places of the Fixed Stars, as soon as they shall be completed, and if you have besides anything elaborated which earnestly makes for the Astronomical [science]. I in turn devote myself and my studies to Your Highness's will and desires.</p><p>May Your Highness fare as long and as happily as possible.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 16th Day of August, in the Year 1588.<br>Your Highness's devoted, <b>Tycho Brahe.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VI (the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>). The dataset's `source_edition` (\"<i>Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri…</i>\") is Tycho's <b>own 1596 print</b> (Liber I, the volume he produced at his Wandsbek/Uraniborg press), in which this letter appears; no manuscript witness is recorded. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VI pp. 121–132</b> (PDF pp. 135–146); every crux confirmed (anchors in the Editorial note).</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg). <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592) — the \"astronomical Landgrave,\" himself an accomplished observer with a Kassel observatory; titles <i>Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda</i>. His <b>Mathematician</b> throughout is <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the working partner in the dispute (the great Tycho–Rothmann technical exchange — the four largest letters of this corpus — runs alongside this princely-level letter).</li><li><b>The controversy (the spine): the \"five minutes.\"</b> Rothmann's fixed-star <b>longitudes</b> ran ~<b>5′ greater</b> than Tycho's. Tycho's defence:</li><li><b>Instrument concord.</b> The <i>declinations and distances</i> from both observatories agree exactly (a thing \"unheard-of\"), proving the instruments sound — especially after the tiny <b>perpendicular deviation</b> in the Kassel quadrant was fixed. Tycho credits his <b>sights/Division</b> method and (as the key advance) the <b>parallel slits</b> (<i>Rimulae Parallelae</i>) for collimating stars, vs. the ancients' error-prone <b>sight-holes</b> (<i>foramina</i>). His <b>four armillary machines</b> (equatorial + zodiacal), <b>sextants</b>, and <b>azimuthal quadrants</b> all agree to the minute.</li><li><b>Why the ancients reached only 10′.</b> Hipparchus/Ptolemy worked to a \"sixth of a degree\" because of the sight-hole problem; their armillae were of \"just magnitude\" (Ptolemy even rejected over-large Alexandria armillae).</li><li><b>The two methods.</b> (a) <b>Altitude + azimuth + clock-time</b> (Rothmann's primary method): Tycho judges it \"slippery\" — clocks may run unevenly, the quadrant-plane may not match the vertical circle; he asks for a fresh test at <i>both equinoxes</i> with multiple clocks. (b) <b>The Venus method</b> (linking Sun → Venus → stars by day) he prefers — but Rothmann's <b>January [1587]</b> Venus work was vitiated because Sun and Venus were too low (great southern declination), so <b>refraction</b> pushed both ~5′ further in longitude, inflating the star-places. Hence Rothmann \"did not notice the 5′ excess.\"</li><li><b>Refraction is the crux.</b> Below <b>30° altitude</b> the Sun's place is unreliable; the southern zodiac half is refraction-spoiled; Tycho even attributes <b>Copernicus's error in the solar apogee/eccentricity</b> to taking the Sun's mid-Scorpio entry against the equinoxes while it was refraction-displaced. His Ephemeris matches Kassel at the equinoxes and in the <i>northern</i> semicircle; any southern discrepancy is refraction <i>per accidens</i>. He defends his <b>refraction tables</b> as drawn from experience (constant at equal altitude under clear skies), not assumed <i>ad libitum</i>, and denies that <b>time-keeping</b> introduces error in his Sun→Venus→star method (Sun/Venus barely move in 1/8 hour).</li><li><b>The irrefragable proof: reciprocal Venus.</b> Any error would show up in <b>morning-vs-evening Venus</b> observations at like altitude/distance. Tycho's run: <b>1582 evening</b> (Venus west of Sun), <b>1585 morning</b> (Sept), <b>1587 morning</b> (17–18 Aug, at the near-partile <b>Venus–Jupiter conjunction</b>, sighted from <b>both heads of Gemini</b> = Castor &amp; Pollux), <b>1588 evening</b> (Mar–Apr) — all fall to the <i>same minute</i>; if anything, a \"redundant half-minute\" might be shaved. The <b>Spica</b> test (its place fixed via a small Draco star near the ecliptic pole, from declination + latitude alone) independently confirms his longitudes. Conclusion: the 5′ are <b>in excess in Rothmann's</b> values.</li><li><b>Forward programme.</b> Tycho encloses a <b>5-year solar Ephemeris</b> (1588–92, replacing the one extended to 1587); praises the Landgrave's plan to <b>publish his star catalogue</b> (assigned to Rothmann); urges him to observe the <b>planets</b> (the three superiors at opposition, Venus/Mercury at max elongation, plus intermediate places) to fix the <b>apogees, eccentricities, and orbit-proportions</b> — noting his own work on <b>Saturn</b> at opposition in the remote eccentric. He will publish his own star-restitution; the 5′ difference is minor, best left until \"sure observation,\" not \"mutual consent for favour.\"</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (the Landgrave's mathematician); <b>Hipparchus, Ptolemy</b> (and the Alexandria armillae); <b>Copernicus</b> (the solar-apogee refraction error); the <b>Frankfurt Fairs</b> (postal channel); <b>Alexandria</b>; <b>Uraniborg</b>. (Jost Bürgi, the Kassel clockmaker behind the \"exquisite Horologia,\" is not named but is implied.) Stars named: <b>Spica</b> (Virginis Spica), <b>both heads of Gemini</b> (Castor &amp; Pollux), a small <b>Draco</b> star near the ecliptic pole.</li><li><b>Technical glossary (per §5, held consistent with the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590).</b> <i>Refractio</i> → refraction; <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>longitudo/latitudo/declinatio</i> → longitude/latitude/declination; <i>apogaeum/eccentricitas</i> → apogee/eccentricity; <i>acronychus</i> → acronychal; <i>armillae (aequatoriae/zodiacales)</i> → equatorial/zodiacal armillae; <i>sextans, quadrans azimuthalis</i> → sextant, azimuthal quadrant; <i>pinnacidia</i> → sights; <i>dioptra/foramina</i> → dioptres/sight-holes; <i>rimulae parallelae</i> → parallel slits; <i>restitutio/instauratio</i> → restitution/restoration; <i>scrupulum/minutum</i> → minute (of arc); <i>\"sexta pars gradus\"</i> = 10′, <i>\"duodecima portiuncula gradus\"</i> = 5′. <i>partilis</i> (of the Venus–Jupiter conjunction) = exact, degree-for-degree.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <b>16 August 1588 O.S.</b> (Danish Julian); matches the catalogue label. The Landgrave's letter is dated by Tycho \"about the autumnal equinox of the preceding year\" (1587). Observation years (82, 85, 87, 88) are Tycho's own O.S. reckoning.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> (1) ~25 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> (footnote-anchors) removed as non-textual (as in the Scaliger letter of 23 Aug 1598 and the undated Peucer letter of 1590). (2) The opening <b>dittography</b> <i>\"ILLVSTRISSIMOILLVSTRISSIMO\"</i> reduced to a single <i>ILLVSTRISSIMO</i>. (3) Genuine early-modern <b>v/u orthography</b> of the 1596 print (<i>vt, vbi, obseruatio, deuiatio</i>) retained as printed; one transcription slip <i>\"vitam\"</i> for <i>vitium</i> (\"...non...vitam parere potuit...ambiguitatem\" → <i>vitium</i>, \"could beget no fault/ambiguity\") left in the source block and rendered as <i>vitium</i> in translation. The single zodiac glyph ♒ reproduced as printed.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — sound. A long, technically dense but single-threaded observational letter (the 5′ star-longitude controversy, the refraction analysis, the Venus method, the reciprocal-observation proof, and the forward programme) rendered securely throughout; the deferential princely register (<i>Tua Celsitudo</i>, <i>demisse rogatam</i>) is preserved and the controlled instrument/observation glossary is held consistent with the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590 and with the wider corpus. The reciprocal-Venus chronology (1582/85/87/88) and the refraction argument are internally coherent and historically consistent with the documented Tycho–Rothmann dispute. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VI pp. 121–132) and confirms every crux</b> (incl. the dittography, <i>ILLVSTRISSIMO</i> once in Dreyer), so the collation reservation that previously capped the grade is <b>lifted</b>. Two residues remain: (1) the single <i>vitam→vitium</i> transcription slip (corrected, declared); (2) the OCR \"A\"-sigla removal and the opening dittography, declared. Neither obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 confirms every crux incl. dittography + dateline; only a declared/corrected slip + sigla remain — no residual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (no Dreyer slice available for this letter), so per brief this is capped at grade B and audited against source_text alone, not collated against TBOO. Read the full ~15-paragraph Latin source and the full published_english translation in parallel, clause by clause. Checked every proper name (Wilhelm/Hesse titulature, Rothmann x2, Hipparchus, Ptolemy/Alexandria, Copernicus, Scorpio, Spica, Draco, Gemini, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn, Tycho Brahe, Uraniborg), every date (16 Aug 1588; 'preceding year'=1587; the recurring 82/85/87/88 observation-year sequence; 17th-18th August; end of January), and every number (the recurring 'five minutes' discrepancy; 4 or 5 minutes; 5 or 6 minutes; sixth part of a degree=10'; twelfth part of a degree=5'; half a minute; two or three minutes; a tenth or eighth part of an hour; nearly five degrees; 8th degree of Aquarius; thirtieth degree; next five years; elapsed [15]87th year). All check out against the Latin with no invented or altered figures. Bracketed interpretive supplements (e.g. [Frankfurt], [as morning star], [Castor and Pollux], [10'], [5']) are clearly marked and are reasonable glosses, per instructions not counted as defects. One internal oddity noted but not reportable: source_text reads 'vitam parere potuit ... ambiguitatem' where grammatical sense and context (a run of 'vitium'/'vitiositatis' elsewhere in the letter) suggest 'vitium' was intended; the English renders the evidently-intended sense ('beget ... an ambiguity') correctly, so this is not a translation defect, and without the Dreyer image (tboo null) it cannot be confirmed as a TEXT_ERROR proper — flagged here only for the record. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found across the full letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15870911001", "next": "brahe-t_15890221001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870911001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900515001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains a delayed reply by missed courier opportunities and ongoing Venus observations, which he intends to compare with prior morning observations before writing more fully.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880816001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880817001", "d": "1588-08-17", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 17 August 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 … (= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.46</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO VIRO D. THADDAeO HAGECIO AB HAYK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO MEO IN PRIMIS COLENDO.<br>S.</p><p>SCRIPSI ad te proxime elapso mense Maio, optime et amantissime D. D. THADDAeE, per Studiosum quendam Silesium, qui me cum quibusdam alijs tunc temporis inviserat, unâque librum secundum operis nostri de recentioribus aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis transmisi, quem non dubito te iamdudum unâ cum literis illis accepisse. Quia vero nihil interea responsi a te receperim, et post paucos menses hyems ingruens commoditatem huc scribendi praecludet non intermittendum duxi, quin te hisce brevibus denuo compellarem, ut rescriptionem maturare non intermitteres, cupio autem tuam sententiam cognoscere de ijs, quae liber iste tibi missus continet, utque ingenue eam de singulis, ubi opus videbitur, mihi perscribas, rogo. Feceris mihi hac in parte rem gratam. Non enim mea ita mihi placent, quin aliorum de his libenter sufferam iudicium, quemadmodum ego et de tuis et aliorum quorundam scriptis circa hanc materiam, fretus libertate Philosophica et veritatis inquirendae studio, libere quod sentiam profero, absque tamen ullius laesione mordaci. Primum tomum, qui de Stella illa nova luculenter agit, et tertium, qui de tribus alijs minoribus Cometis postmodum visis e certis observationibus Mathematice rem omnem tractat, spero me per hyemale otium, favente divina Clementia, absoluturum, ut ea omnia ad Colophonem deducta vernalibus Nundinis Francofurti venundentur. Prioris tomi editionem Gallici privilegij defectus, quod Caesareo per te mihi procurato in operis frontispicio adiungere decrevi, hactenus retardavit, prout (ni fallor) in ultimis etiam literis tibi indicavi. Verum cum nunc Gallicanum hoc adeptus sim, et insuper Anglicanum indies expectem, nihil obstabit, quo minus telam inchoatam pertexere omnique ex parte absolvere elaborem. Tu interim intermediae illius partis tibi transmissae penitiorem disquisitionem non intermitte, tuumque de hac iudicium (quod prius etiam expetivi) ante hyemem prima quaque occasione sufficienter et ingenue perscribe. Commodius autem erit Rostochium eas mittere literas ad Doctorem HENRICUM BRUCAeUM, vel in aulam Illustriss. Principis Ducis ULDARICI Megapolitani, ad ipsius Celsis Cancellarium, D. D. IACOBUM BORDINGUM, aut etiam Lubecam ad FRANCISCUM KNOCKERT, Patricium et Senatorem urbis, sive ibidem ad quendam Nauclerum, ALBERT GRUBBE nomine, habitantem in loco appellato Lubecensi Angelo, ut aliquis horum easdem Hafniam ad civem in aedibus meis habitantem, DIDERICUM FULLONEM nomine, transmitti procuret; quacunque enim ratione ex iamdictis (nisi ipsemet commodiorem adeptus fueris) hoc negotium expediveris, spero me literas tuas satis certo et mature obtenturum, quibus ego etiam confestim respondere non intermittam. Vale; plura enim nunc non vacat scribere, et haec saltem obiter ad te perarata Francofurtum transmisi, si forte inde per certum hominem tibi perferantur, qua in parte siquidem aliquid dubij subesse queat, copiosius de alijs rebus nunc scribere non duxi operaepretium. Amplissimum et nobilissimum virum ANDREAM DUDITIUM, si illic in vicinia est, velim meo nomine vel verbis praesens, vel per literas absens officiose salutes. Quod si eius etiam iudicium de libro meo sibi transmisso scriptis mandatum ante hyemem obtinuero, rem mihi praestiteris tam ille quam tu longe acceptissimam.</p><p>Iterum felicissime vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 17. Augusti Anno 88.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned man, most learned in every kind of knowledge, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine, my friend to be honoured before all others.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>I wrote to you this past May, most excellent and most loving Master Doctor Thaddaeus, by way of a certain Silesian student who at that time had visited me along with certain others, and at the same time I sent the second book of our work on the more recent phenomena of the aethereal World, which I do not doubt you received long since together with that letter. But since in the meantime I have had no reply from you, and within a few months the onset of winter will cut off the convenience of writing hither, I thought I should not fail to address you again with these few lines, that you might not put off hastening a reply; and I desire to learn your opinion of the matters that the book sent to you contains, and I ask that you write it out to me frankly on the several points, where there shall seem to be need. You will herein do me a welcome thing. For my own [findings] do not so please me that I do not gladly bear the judgement of others upon them — just as I too, both upon your writings and upon those of certain others concerning this subject, relying on the liberty of philosophy and on zeal for the inquiry into truth, freely set forth what I think, yet without any biting injury to anyone. The first volume, which treats luminously of that new Star, and the third, which treats the whole matter mathematically, from sure observations, concerning three other lesser Comets seen afterwards, I hope, by the favour of the divine Clemency, to finish during my winter leisure, so that the whole, brought to the colophon, may be put up for sale at the spring Fair at Frankfurt. The publication of the earlier volume has hitherto been delayed by the want of the French privilege, which I have resolved to add on the title-page of the work to the Imperial one procured for me through you — as (unless I am mistaken) I also indicated to you in my last letter. But since I have now obtained this French one, and moreover expect the English one any day, nothing will stand in the way of my labouring to weave out the web I have begun and to finish it in every part. You meanwhile, do not put off a deeper examination of that intermediate part sent to you, and write out your judgement upon it (which I asked for before, too) fully and frankly before winter, at the very first opportunity. It will, however, be more convenient to send those letters to Rostock, to Doctor Henricus Brucaeus; or to the court of the Most Illustrious Prince Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, to His Highness's Chancellor, Master Doctor Jacobus Bordingus; or also to Lübeck, to Franciscus Knockert, patrician and senator of the city; or, in the same place, to a certain shipmaster by the name of Albert Grubbe, dwelling in the place called the Lübeck Angel — so that one of these may see to it that they are forwarded to Copenhagen to a townsman dwelling in my house, by the name of Diderik Fuller. For in whatever way among those just named you shall dispatch this business (unless you yourself shall have found a more convenient one), I hope to obtain your letter surely enough and in good time, to which I too shall not fail to reply at once. Farewell; for there is no leisure now to write more, and I have sent these lines — penned to you only in passing — to Frankfurt, in case they may be carried thence to you by some sure man; since in that respect some doubt might lie hidden, I have not thought it worthwhile to write now more copiously about other matters. The most ample and most noble man Andreas Dudith, if he be there in the neighbourhood, I should wish you to greet dutifully in my name — in person by word of mouth, or, if absent, by letter. And if I shall obtain his judgement too, set down in writing, upon my book sent to him, before winter, you will have rendered me a thing most welcome by far, both to him and to you [alike].</p><p>Again, most happily, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 17th day of August, the year [15]88.<br>Most loving toward you,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / programme.</b> Tycho writes from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. The \"work on the more recent phenomena of the aethereal World\" is his <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> — the three-volume project of which, in this letter: the <b>second book</b> (<i>liber secundus / tomus / intermedia pars</i>) is the volume on the comet of 1577, already printed at Hven and being circulated (sent to Hagecius the previous May); the <b>first volume</b> (<i>primum tomum, qui de Stella illa nova … agit</i>) is the planned treatise on the new Star of 1572 — eventually the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (completed posthumously, 1602); and the <b>third</b> (<i>tertium … de tribus alijs minoribus Cometis</i>) the projected treatment of three lesser comets. Both first and third Tycho still hopes to \"finish during my winter leisure\" and sell at the next Frankfurt spring fair — an expectation not in fact met.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayk</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius / Hagecius)</b> (1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and imperial counsellor at Prague; one of Tycho's closest scholarly correspondents and his chief broker for imperial favour. His own <i>Apodixis physica et mathematica de cometis</i> (1581) is part of the comet literature in view here.</li><li><b>Persons named as forwarding agents.</b></li><li><i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1530–1593), professor of mathematics and medicine at <b>Rostock</b>, a regular intermediary in Tycho's correspondence.</li><li><i>Illustriss. Princeps Dux Uldaricus Megapolitanus</i> = <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</b> (1527–1603); <i>Megapolitanus</i> is the Latin for Mecklenburg. <i>eius Celsis</i> = \"His Highness's\" (genitive of <i>Celsitudo</i>, protocol §5.5).</li><li><i>Iacobus Bordingus</i> = <b>Jakob Bording (the younger)</b>, the Duke's chancellor.</li><li><i>Franciscus Knockert</i> — <b>Franz Knokert (Knokart)</b>, named \"patrician and senator\" (city councillor) of <b>Lübeck</b>.</li><li><i>Albert Grubbe</i> — a Lübeck <b>shipmaster</b> (<i>nauclerus</i>), \"dwelling in the place called the Lübeck Angel\" (<i>in loco appellato Lubecensi Angelo</i>) — apparently an inn or house sign, \"the Angel,\" at Lübeck; kept as named, no firmer identification asserted.</li><li><i>Didericus Fullonem</i> (acc.) = <b>Diderik Fuller (Diderik Fullo)</b>, a townsman lodging in Tycho's house [at Copenhagen], the final relay point for letters reaching Hven.</li><li><i>Andreas Duditius</i> = <b>Andreas Dudith (András Dudith / Dudithius)</b> (1533–1589), the Hungarian humanist, diplomat, and former bishop, then living at <b>Breslau (Wrocław)</b> in Silesia near Hagecius's orbit; author of <i>De cometarum significatione</i>, hence Tycho's wish for his <i>iudicium</i> on the comet book. (Dudith in fact died the following February.)</li><li><b>Place / sign.</b> <i>Hafnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b> (protocol §5.7); <i>Rostochium</i> = <b>Rostock</b>; <i>Lubeca</i> = <b>Lübeck</b>; <i>Francofurtum</i> = <b>Frankfurt</b> (am Main), site of the great book fair (<i>Nundinae Francofurtenses</i>).</li><li><b>Technical / bibliographic terms.</b> <i>Caesareo … privilegio / Gallicum / Anglicanum</i> — the <b>Imperial, French, and English printing privileges</b> Tycho was assembling for the work; <i>frontispicium</i> = the <b>title-page</b> (here the place where the privileges would be announced), not a building front. <i>ad Colophonem deducta</i> — \"brought to the colophon,\" i.e. carried through to completion of printing. <i>vernalibus Nundinis Francofurti</i> — the <b>spring Frankfurt [book] fair</b>. <i>telam inchoatam pertexere</i> — \"to weave out the web begun,\" a classical metaphor (Cicero) for finishing an undertaking; kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Heading abbreviations <i>D. D.</i> = <i>Domine Doctor</i> (\"Master Doctor\"); <i>D.</i> (<i>Dominus</i>) = \"Master/Lord\" (protocol §5.5). Closing <i>Iterum … vale</i> → \"Again … farewell\"; <i>Tui amantiss[imus]</i> → \"Most loving toward you\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"in his own hand\" (an autograph subscription).</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> <i>ni fallor</i> (\"unless I am mistaken\"), <i>non dubito … te accepisse</i> (\"I do not doubt you received\"), <i>si … in vicinia est</i> (\"if he be there in the neighbourhood\"), <i>si forte … perferantur</i> (\"in case they may be carried\"), and <i>siquidem aliquid dubij subesse queat</i> (\"since some doubt might lie hidden\") are all rendered with their qualifiers intact, per protocol (no silent sharpening).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 17. Augusti Anno 88</i> = <b>17 August 1588</b>, Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1588. 17. august. Uraniborg\") agrees; no discrepancy. No Roman-calendar idiom in the dateline.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None in this letter.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the standalone salutation 'S.' (dropped by the working transcription) between the address block and 'SCRIPSI', per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.122; added the corresponding English 'Greeting.' line.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The only points held deliberately open (and so not affecting the grade) are minor onomastic ones flagged above: the precise nature of the Lübeck <i>Lubecensi Angelo</i> (\"the Angel,\" house/inn sign) and exact spellings of the lay forwarding agents (Knokert, Grubbe, Fuller), which the apparatus names rather than over-specifies.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VII pp.122-124 (page images, not OCR text layer). Full body text, every proper name (Brucaeus, Bordingus, Knockert, Grubbe, Fullo, Dudith), the date (17 August [15]88), and the signature block all match Dreyer exactly. The sole finding is the dropped \"S.\" salutation particle -- a genuine TEXT_ERROR under the grading rule even though it does not change the letter's sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer VII p.122 prints a standalone \"S.\" (Salutem) line between the address block and the opening word \"SCRIPSI\"; our source_text and the English both omit it entirely, unlike sibling letters in this same batch (e.g. brahe-t_15891130001, brahe-t_15851213001) which retain both the Latin \"S.\" and its English \"Greeting.\" rendering. Dreyer prints: <i>S.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.122-124, all 3 pages read). P.122 clearly prints a standalone 'S.' line immediately beneath the address block, before the illuminated 'S' of 'SCRIPSI'. No other cruces found on the three pages read.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1588 (7 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/45-48", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13966897"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.46."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 13, "prev": "brahe-t_15880503001", "next": "brahe-t_15881101001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15860907001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881101001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Notes the second book of *De mundi aetherei* was sent in May, and urges a frank written opinion of its contents before winter cuts off correspondence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880817001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880817002", "d": "1588-08-17", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Bartholomaeus Scultetus — Uraniborg, 17 August 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica </i>· MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO M. BARTHOLOMAeO SCULTETO, GORLICENSI, RERUM MATHEMATICARUM PERITISSIMO, VETERI AMICO PLURIMUM COLENDO.</p><p>PER Silesium quendam Studiosum Vratislaviensem (ni fallor) tibi scripsi, mense Maio proxime elapso, amantissime clarissimeque SCULTETE, librumque nostrum secundum, opusculum, quod de recentioribus aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis emisimus, unâ transmisi. Nec dubito te eum interea accepisse, perspexisseque diligenter. Rogo itaque, velis mihi prima occasione tuam de ijs, quae in eo continentur, perscribere sententiam. Nec est, cur te moveat, quod a tuis placitis tantopere in plurimis dissentiam, et fretus libertate Philosophica, quid in his veritati consentaneum sit, ingenue proferam. Scis enim, me nullo tua insectandi aut cavillandi animo id facere, et ut candide interpreteris omnia, amicitiae nostrae diuturnum vinculum postulat. Ego tibi vicissim liberum de meis relinquo iudicium, ut ingenue proferas, quicquid invicta veritatis Mathematicae certitudo exigat. Plurimum itaque tuas desidero literas, quibus meis desiderijs in his satisfiat, quas ut mature ante instantem hyemem accipiam, operam des: idque commodissime fiet, si eas miseris vel Rostochium ad D. HENRICUM BRUCAeUM Medicum, vel Lubecam, ad D. PETRUM MEMMIUM, etiam Medicum, aut illuc quoque ad FRANCISC. KNOCKERT, Patritium et Senatorem urbis, vel Nauclerum quendam, ALBERT GRUBE nomine, habitantem in Angelo Lubecensi. Quocunque horum in procurandis literis usus fueris, spero me eas ad civem quendam Hafniensem, in aedibus meis habitantem, DESIDERIUM FULLONEM nomine, delatas, facile et brevi obtenturum. Vale, et rescriptionem matura. Rogo etiam, ut Magnifico Nobilissimoque Viro ANDREAe DUDITIO, si vobis in propinquo est, meo nomine officiose salutem dicere vel perscribere non intermittas. Optarem vero quam maxime illius de libro nostro, sibi etiam transmisso, iudicium. Quod si per te ab ipso etiam, ante hyemis impedimenta, literis consignatum impetravero, feceris mihi rem longe gratissimam. Iterum felicissime vale.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi die 17. Augusti Anno 1588.<br>Tui amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned Master Bartholomaeus Scultetus of Görlitz, most skilled in mathematical matters, his old friend much to be honoured.</b></p><p>By way of a certain Silesian student of Breslau (if I am not mistaken) I wrote to you in the month of May just past, most loving and most renowned Scultetus, and along with it I sent our second book — the little work which we have issued concerning the more recent phenomena of the aethereal world. Nor do I doubt that you have meanwhile received it and examined it diligently. I ask you, therefore, that you be willing to write out for me at the first opportunity your judgement of the things contained in it. Nor is there any reason why it should disturb you that I dissent so greatly from your opinions in very many points, and that, relying on philosophical liberty, I set forth frankly what in these matters accords with the truth. For you know that I do this with no intention of attacking or carping at you; and the long-standing bond of our friendship requires that you interpret everything candidly. I, in turn, leave you free judgement concerning my own [views], that you may set forth frankly whatever the unconquered certainty of mathematical truth demands. I greatly desire, then, your letters, by which my longings in these matters may be satisfied; and may you take pains that I receive them in good time before the approaching winter. This will most conveniently be done if you send them either to Rostock, to Master Henricus Brucaeus, physician, or to Lübeck, to Master Petrus Memmius, likewise a physician, or thither too to Franciscus Knockert, patrician and senator of the city, or to a certain shipmaster, Albert Grube by name, dwelling in the [house of the] Angel at Lübeck. By whichever of these you make use of in procuring [the delivery of] the letters, I hope, once they are delivered to a certain citizen of Copenhagen dwelling in my house, Desiderius Fullo by name, easily and soon to obtain them. Farewell, and hasten your reply. I ask too that you not fail, in my name, to give or write out a dutiful greeting to the magnificent and most noble Master Andreas Dudith, if he is near to you. I would wish most of all for his judgement of our book, which has also been sent to him. And if through you I obtain it from him too, set down in a letter before the obstacles of winter, you will have done me a thing by far most welcome. Again, most happily, farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 17th day of August, the year 1588.<br>Most loving toward you,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> writing from Uraniborg on Hven to <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomäus Schultz, 1540–1614), the Görlitz mathematician, cartographer, and astronomer, later burgomaster of that city. Tycho had received instruction from Scultetus in his student years (Thoren 1990, 17); the address-line <i>VETERI AMICO</i> (\"old friend\") marks the long acquaintance. <i>GORLICENSI</i> = \"of Görlitz\" (Lat. <i>Gorlicium</i>), in Upper Lusatia — whence the edition title <i>Lusatica</i>.</li><li><b><i>librum nostrum secundum … de recentioribus aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis</b></i> — Tycho's <b>second book</b>, the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), on the comet of 1577; his \"first\" being <i>De nova stella</i> (1573). <i>opusculum</i> (\"little work\") is Tycho's habitual modest diminutive for it. Title-phrase rendered literally rather than supplied with the printed Latin title.</li><li><b><i>libertate Philosophica</b></i> — \"philosophical liberty,\" the period topos of free inquiry; Tycho frames his disagreement with Scultetus as the licensed candour of philosophers, not personal attack (<i>nullo tua insectandi aut cavillandi animo</i>).</li><li><b><i>invicta veritatis Mathematicae certitudo</b></i> — \"the unconquered certainty of mathematical truth\"; <i>invicta</i> kept as \"unconquered\" (not softened to \"unassailable\").</li><li><b>Forwarding network.</b> The chain of intermediaries for routing the reply: <b>Henricus Brucaeus</b> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1531–1593), professor of medicine at Rostock and a Tycho correspondent (glossary §5.6); <b>Petrus Memmius</b> = Peter Memmius, physician at Lübeck; <b>Franciscus Knockert</b> = Franz Knockert, patrician and city senator of Lübeck; <b>Albert Grube</b>, a shipmaster (<i>Nauclerus</i>) lodging <i>in Angelo Lubecensi</i> — \"at the Angel in Lübeck,\" i.e. a house/inn at the sign of the Angel, rendered \"in the [house of the] Angel.\" <i>Vratislaviensem</i> = \"of Breslau\" (Wrocław), the Silesian student who had carried the May letter.</li><li><b><i>Desiderius Fullo</b></i> — Tycho's Copenhagen go-between, <i>civem … Hafniensem, in aedibus meis habitantem</i> (\"a citizen of Copenhagen dwelling in my house\"). The Latinised name (<i>Desiderius</i> = Desiderius/Désiré; <i>Fullo</i> lit. \"fuller\") masks a vernacular original not identified here; left as the Latin form and flagged.</li><li><b><i>Magnifico Nobilissimoque Viro Andreae Duditio</b></i> — <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (András Dudith / Andreas Dudithius, 1533–1589), the Hungarian humanist, former bishop and Imperial diplomat, then living in Breslau (Wrocław) — hence Tycho's <i>si vobis in propinquo est</i> (\"if he is near to you,\" Görlitz and Breslau both being in the region). Tycho had sent Dudith the <i>De mundi</i> and solicits his <i>iudicium</i> through Scultetus.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None in this letter.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 17. Augusti Anno 1588</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 17 August 1588\" (O.S.; Denmark held the Julian calendar). The header date matches the dateline; the dataset label (\"1588. 17. august. Uraniborg\") agrees — no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> <i>Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica</i> (the Lusatian historical-literary miscellany in which the letter was printed); no manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is straightforward humanist epistolary prose with no textual or grammatical crux; sense and register are secure. The only un-pinned point is the vernacular identity behind the Latinised name <i>Desiderius Fullo</i>, noted above but not affecting the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp. 123-124 as page images (slice pages 1-2; slice page 3 = p.125 checked and confirmed to belong to the following letter to Ranzovius, so this letter runs only pp.123-124). Collated opening address, full body, and closing/date line sentence-by-sentence against source_text and against published_english. All proper names checked and correct: Bartholomaeus Scultetus (Gorlicensi/Görlitz), Henricus Brucaeus (Rostock physician), Petrus Memmius (Lübeck physician), Franciscus Knockert (patrician/senator of Lübeck), Albert Grube (Nauclerus/shipmaster, 'in Angelo Lubecensi'), Desiderius Fullo (Hafniensis, in Tycho's house), Andreas Dudith. Date '17 Augusti Anno 1588' at close matches Dreyer exactly; 'mense Maio proxime elapso' (month of May just past) correctly rendered. No sums of money in this letter. source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim aside from ordinary long-s/ligature typography (AeO for AE, etc.), which is not a fidelity issue. No fabricated names/dates/clauses, no omitted sentences, no mistranslated antecedents or pronouns (checked the 'me...obtenturum' clause for pronoun-flip risk: subject remains Tycho throughout, correctly rendered), no hedge softened or hardened ('ni fallor' -&gt; 'if I am not mistaken' preserved). Clean bill.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15901208001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks for written judgment on the recently sent second book on aethereal phenomena, and asserts that his extensive dissent from Scultetus's views reflects philosophical liberty, not personal antagonism.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880817002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880817003", "d": "1588-08-17", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 17 August 1588 [O.S.] (catalogued 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DOCTRINA ET VIRTVTE ORNATISSIMO VIRO D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO MULTUM DILECTO. S. D.</p><p>ACVR serius Illustriss. Principis et tuis respondeam literis, Eruditissime exoptatissimeque mi ROTHMANNE, excusationem tibi perscripsi in ijs quas interea per GELLIVM SASCERIDEM meum ad te dedi; nec enim dubito eas vna cum libro nostro secundo, de Aetherei Mundi recentioribus Phaenomenis, qui ijsdem adiunctus erat, dudum tibi esse traditas. Librum vero illum te etiam interim diligenter euoluisse existimo, vt itaque tuam mihi de ijs, quae continet, sententiam aperias, et ingenue mentem tuam de his exponas, plurimum desidero. Ego vicissim tibi, quid veritati consentaneum videatur, tum in his, tum etiam alijs, libenter et liberaliter, quantum in me est, declarare non intermittam. Nunc itaque tuis literis erudita in Astronomicis rerum cognitione luculenter refertis, vt paulo etiam vberius respondeam, non diutius supersedendum duco. Percommodum vero accidit, quod posteriores tuas literas, quibus ipsemet nimiam vaporum terrestrium eleuationem, in prioribus per incuriam aliquam commissam, opportune emendas, satis mature acceperim. Alias enim hac de re te commonefacere in animum induxeram. Summi enim halitus iuxta Opticorum demonstrationem ad 12. Milliaria Germanica vix ascendunt, quae Terrae Semidiameter (cui tu eorum remotionem antea adaequasti) plus septuagies continet. Verum haec ab Opticorum traditionibus disconuenientia, facile inter alias occupationes per festinantiam obrepere potuit. Nunc itaque ad priores tuas literas, admissa sublimitatis vaporosae in posterioribus emendatione, ordine respondebo. De Cometico vero negotio Anni 86. iam non plura addam, satis enim in hoc conuenimus, nec dimidij, vel integri Minuti, quoad Parallaxin, discrepantia alicuius est momenti, imo prorsus insensibilis; sic neque de Declinatione maxima vlterius verba faciam: Eam enim a vobis nunc per rectificatum Quadrantis perpendiculum, meis inuentis apprime consentientem, comprobatam esse perlibenter intelligo. De Materia Caeli et Refractionibus, quoniam haec res nondum videtur satis inter nos decisa, operaepretium erit, vt vlterius tecum agam, tuaeque hac in parte petitioni morem geram.</p><p>Materiam Sphaerarum caelestium tu non aliam quam elementarem Aërem esse, et Refractiones nullas ob Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris diuersitatem fieri contendis, omnemque Refractionum occasionem in solos vapores Horizonti obuersantes reijcis; At ego Caelum quidem non e dura imperuiaque materia in formam membranarum pellucidarum extensa, constare, velut hactenus, etiam a praecipuis Philosophis, creditum est, vel ex ipsis Cometarum motibus edoctus sum; Aëreum tamen vel vllius elementaris naturae particeps esse nequaquam admisero. Absit enim vt purissimae perfectissimaeque et nullis mutationibus obnoxiae caelesti naturae elementare, corruptibile variabile atque huic inferiori caducae Regioni conforme aliquid assuatur; nam ne id vnquam vllus saniorum Physicorum admittere ausus est. Omnes siquidem vnanimiter Caelum e numero et natura quatuor Elementorum prorsus exemtum et praestantiorem quandam naturam prae se ferre, rectissime statuunt, ideoque illud quintam quandam essentiam a quatuor illis elementaribus prorsus diuersam longeque excellentiorem nuncupârunt. Quod igitur Physici abhorrent non admittatur, vt ipsi Mathematici tanta Caelum labe aspergant, vt ex Aëre elementari illud constare asseuerantes simplicissimae et purissimae adeoque perfectae ipsius substantiae et naturae plurimum derogant. Et quae, quaeso, fieret Elementorum proportio, si solum Aërem a Terris vsque ad octauam Sphaeram tam immensam vastitatem complere concesserimus? Sane ambo Elementa Terrae et Aquae, nullam, quae sensibus concipi posset, ad vnicum Aërem habebunt Symmetriam. Praeterea si Aër elementaris orbibus caelestibus includeretur, consentaneum foret, e concitatissimo Syderum motu sonum ingentem cieri, qui proculdubio auditum etiam nostrum percelleret. Officeret etiam Aër perpetuitati Reuolutionis caelestium corporum, et puritati eorum longo vsu temporeque detraheret, imo quasi atterendo, constantissimam harmoniam labefactaret. Scio quidem Paracelsum totum Caelum, quartum et igneum Elementum appellare; quod si de viuifico calore Solis et Siderum intelligatur, locum fortasse mereri poterit; Nam Elementum Ignis infra Lunam esse, vt Peripatetici somniant, si nihil aliud, vel ipsa Refractionum subtilitas, quae hac ratione per tot Diaphana maior redderetur, sufficienter refellit. Omnem itaque calorem in corporibus mixtis, quem Physici Elemento Ignis attribuunt, caelitus prouenire consentaneum est. Quod si aliter Paracelsus sentiebat, absurda est opinio. Neque etiam ignoro quosdam Theologos e Mose, proculdubio minus recte intellecto, asseuerare Caelum ex Aquea materia constare; quam tamen interpretationem Philo Iudaeus non admittit, sed ipsum nullatenus ex vllis Elementis compositum contendit; videtur etiam haec sententia omni naturali ratiocinationi plurimum aduersari. Qui vero Caelum aëreum statueret, neminem ante Iohannem Penam Gallum percepi, cuius tu sententiae suffragari videris, et nuper etiam Iordanus Nullanus in quodam scripto de Mundo contra Peripateticos idem asserere ausus est, forte etiam quidam alij eâdem sunt imbuti opinione. Sin vero per Aërem, qui inter caelestes circuitus continetur, aliam et subtiliorem purioremque substantiam, quam est Aër hic elementaris, intelligere velitis, non vsque adeo refragabor, nisi quod nomen Elementi Aëris in tam sublimi negotio per se nimis vile et suspectum sit, si is Caelo assignatur; oportebitque hac ratione distinguere inter Aërem aethereum et elementarem, sicque nihilominus res eodem redibit, non esse videlicet eiusdem exquisite puritatis et perspicuitatis, multumque adhuc suberit dubij, quod ne hac quidem distinctione satis enodatum appareat. Verum licet nulli mortalium de ipsissima Caeli materia (si modo e materia aliqua constare proprie dici potest) certi quid proferre in promtu esse credam. Est enim Caelum abstractum quid, et immateriali simile, nostrum captum effugiens, nihilominus satis concludere licet, ipsum de nulla elementari natura participare. Cum haec sit longe praestantius et diuinius quiddam adeoque incorruptibile et supra omnem elementarem qualitatem exaltatum e purissima, simplici et pellucida rarissimaque substantia, admiranda DEI sapientia et nobis imperscrutabili modo plasmatum. Vndecunque igitur Caeli substantia fuerit, quae sane potius, quae non sit, quam quae sit, percipitur, eam ipso Aëre liquidiorem, puriorem et subtiliorem esse necesse est, vt ob id non immerito statuam Diaphanum Aetheris Diaphano Aëris perfectius puriusque esse. Licet enim non admodum sensibilis Siderum Refractio in aliquanta super Horizontem Altitudine ingeratur, neque tanta, quantam veteres optici, qui Caelum e duro et adamantino Diaphano constare opinabantur, suspicati sunt, tamen id non propterea accidit, quia nihil aliud sit inter octauam Sphaeram et Terram quam Aër elementaris. Nam, vt in prioribus etiam literis testatus sum, non est admodum magna differentia inter Aetheris et Aëris transparentiam, neque sicut Aëris et Aquae Diaphana sensibiliter discrepant, sed cum ambo tenuissima sint et liquidissima, plurimum habent affinitatis, imo aliqualis ea quae intercedit diuersitas, successiue imminuitur, prout Aër a Terris sublimior et Lunae circuitibus vicinior redditur; illic enim cum Aethere quasi in vnum coalescit, caelestemque naturam successiue imbuit, atque propterea Refractionem sensibilem non causatur. Non enim datur certus aliquis locus in definita a Terra distantia, in quo Refractio illa procreetur, sed paulatim Diaphanum aëreum in aethereum transformatur, ideoque non contingit aliqua repugnantia, qualem inter Aërem et Aquam fieri videmus, sed potius identitas quaedam conciliatur, quae Refractioni admodum perceptibili, praesertim in sublimiori Stellarum situ, quando magis directe earum Lumen ad nos vibratur, occasionem praecludit. Proptereaque ego minimam partem Refractionum diuersitati Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris attribuo, sed potissimam earum causam vapores, Terrae obuersantes esse statuo, ita vt duplici ratione Refractiones contingant, partim ob vaporosos halitus, Aërem Terris propinquum condensantes. Atque illae sunt maximae admodumque perceptibiles, ideoque earum animaduersio plurimis in propatulo est: Partim vero ob Diaphanorum Caeli et Aëris aliqualem varietatem. Haec autem, quia in ipso etiam Horizonte non admodum magna est, neque etiam vltra 30. Gradum Altitudinis, in Stellis praesertim, percipitur, minus hactenus est considerata, ideoque de ea potissimum verba feci, alteram aliâs satis perspectam praesupponens. Neque etiam negârim hanc ipsam, de qua loquor, vsque ad verticem quodammodo prorogari, sed vltra 30. Gradum Eleuationis ita successiue magis atque magis attenuari iudico, vt nullatenus deinde in sensus cadat, ob Siderum radios directiores, neque adeo magnam aut repugnantem Diaphanorum diuersitatem.</p><p>Asseris ipsemet in tuo mihi transmisso scripto de Cometa vltimo, Refractiones non vltra illud spatium, quo vapores ascendentes multiplicari solent, perdurare, hocque ad summum esse 20. Partium, et ob id nullam aliam quam per solos vapores fieri Refractionem; at cum ego Refractiones in Sole, circa 30. Gradum perceptibiles esse animaduertissem, inferebam eas aliunde etiam, quam per vapores procreari, ideoque ex diuersitate Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris. Nec video quomodo haec meam expugnent sententiam, et pro te faciant; neque enim dixi in 30. Gradu Altitudinis prorsus nullam fieri Refractionem, imo dum accuratius meas Obseruationes examino, adhibita Parallaxeos differentia, inuenio in Sole Refractionem fieri iuxta Altitudinem 30. Partium fere 1½. Min. quod vix per vapores solos excusari poterit, siquidem ipsorum impedimentum subito circa Horizontem alteretur, inque tanta Altitudine perexiguum sit, vt postea e tua ipsius demonstratione patebit. Ast illud, quod fit ratione Diaphanorum, non quidem ita magnum est, sed diutius durat, quamuis in Stellis fixis hactenus animaduertere non potuerim, Refractionem sensibilem tam alte, atque in Sole insinuari, vt in antecedentibus literis etiam indicaui, idque, vt existimo, ob luminis et quantitatis, atque etiam distantiae maximam differentiam. Neque si Refractio circa Horizontem esset 3. Minut. statim consequitur, vt ob id ea in Altitudine 45. Graduum fieret 1½. Minut. vt tu vis. Non enim proportionabiliter et aequaliter decrescunt Refractionum quantitates, sed in decliuioribus Altitudinibus, multo plus illis decedit quam in sublimioribus, idque proculdubio vtraque de causa, tam ratione vaporum Terrae obuolitantium, quam etiam diuersitatis Diaphanorum Caeli et Aëris, principaliter tamen et maxime ob ipsos halitus terrestres, quando per obliquum Stellarum lumen recipiunt; id enim ipsa testatur experientia, subito circa Horizontem alterari Refractiones, in sublimiori vero situ sensim et tardius id fieri. Sic in Sole animaduerti a primo in secundum Gradum Altitudinis variari Refractionem sex Scrupulis primis, a secundo vero in tertium saltem quaternis, in sublimiori vero situ vltra 20. Gradum Altitudinis mutatio illa vix est dimidij Minuti, et licet hoc per vapores solos non inconuenienter excusari poterit, tamen aliquid etiam ex Diaphanorum discrepantia clam sibi habet admixtum. Quod autem non vsque ad verticem sensibiliter durare appareant, per Diaphanorum discrimen obortae Refractiones, in causa est, quod non admodum magna sit eorundem discrepantia, et sensim, vti prius dixi, sibi inuicem assimilentur, adeo vt lumen Stellarum, quando sublimiores fuerint, facile irrefracte vtraque penetrare queat, siquidem tunc non adeo oblique, sed quasi per directum lumen ad nos vibrent, nam si concedi potest id quod tu infers, Refractiones non tam fieri ob vapores istos, quam propter eorum circa Horizontem multiplicationem, radios enim Stellarum et Solis vincere illam crassitiem, nec ob identitatem Diaphanorum in ea punctum aliquod Refractionis in paulo sublimiori situ inuenire, certe ratione Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris idem multo potius admittendum venit, vt in sublimiori situ, sine perceptibili Refractione Stellae nobis illucescant: Longe enim maior hîc est Diaphanorum identitas et similitudo, quia non tantum inter se discrepare conuenit Aethera et Aërem superiorem, quantum Aërem purum et vaporosum.</p><p>Figur 11 Quod ex tua propria Demonstratione rectius percipietur, praesertim ea quam secundo missis literis addidisti, quae priore multo est conuenientior; illic enim si assornatur ex Demonstrationibus Alhasen et Vitellionis, in summa vaporum Eleuatione FE part. 48. M. 50. qualium EA Semidiameter Terrae part. 60. erit his duobus Aequalis part. 60. M. 48. S. 50. Cumque Angul. AEX sit rectus et EA latus datum, prouenit per operationem trigonicam latus XE part. 9. M. 55. qualium videlicet EA est 60. Atque tanta erit vaporum densitas iuxta Horizontem, quae milliaria Germanica 142. adaequabit, prout EA Semidiameter Terrae est eorundem 860. proxime. In altero vero Triangulo AEO, ex notis duobus lateribus AO etiam part. 60. M. 48. S. 50. et EA, vt prius 60. Anguloque intercepto OEA part. 120. adiecto videlicet Ang. OEX part. 30. ad rectum XEA, prodibit etiam tertium latus OE part. 1. M. 36. fere, vastitatem vaporum in Altitudine 30. part. mensurans, quod si in milliaria vt antea, resolutio fiat, 23. Germanica prosilient. Patet itaque quod vapores sint quidem sexduplo proxime densiores circa Horizontem, quam in Altitudine 30. partium; neque tamen ob id necessario consequitur, prorsus nullam, ratione vaporum in tali Altitudine fieri oportere Refractionem, siquidem interstitium vaporum 23. milliaria sua capacitate adaequans, non videtur prorsus insufficiens ad refringendum aliquantulum Stellarum radios, aut si id concedi poterit, quanto magis in diuersitate Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris non idem admittatur, ex quo haec in tali Altitudine multo adhuc minorem ob causas supra iudicatas ingerat differentiam. Praeterea etiamsi concedatur nullam prorsus fieri ex occasione diuersitatis Aëris et Aetheris Refractionem, sed omnem e solis vaporibus originem trahere, tamen non necessario ob id sequeretur, Caelum ipsum ex Aëre elementari et animali, vti tibi placet, constare; potest enim tanta esse Diaphanorum similitudo et vicinitas, mutuaque connexio, vt Refractionem nullam, quae a nobis percipiatur, pariat, lumine Stellarum fortiter vtrumque Diaphanum penetrante. Neque enim statuimus Caelum e dura et imperuia perspicuitate constare, vt Alhasen et Vitellio, qui maximam ob id accidere Refractionem frustra crediderunt, sed liquidissimum esse ipsum Aethera et rarissimum, nullisque realibus orbibus contextum, quemadmodum tu etiam mecum optime sentis, et licet ipso Aëre perfectius quiddam sit, tamen circa Reuolutiones, praesertim lunares, Aër hic elementaris adeo exaltatur, vt quasi in Aetheream transmigret substantiam, ideoque Diaphanorum imperceptibilis efficitur diuersitas, vt prius etiam indicatum est. Quo vero Aër Terrae magis appropinquat, eo minus de Aetherea natura participat, plusque habet elementaris qualitatis, et nihilominus admodum subtilis et rarus est, vt lumen Stellarum facile et irrefracte transmittat, nisi Terrae vicinior fuerit, vbi per vapores condensatur, turbidiorque redditur. Sed de Refractionibus vnde eueniant, an saltem intermediantibus vaporibus, vt tibi videtur, an vero non solum ab his, sed etiam aliquatenus ex Diaphanorum Aetheris et Aëris discrepantia, mixta quadam ratione, vt mihi placet, frustra tantopere disputamus, siquidem de motu caelestium corporum restituendo, ipsaque Astronomia a mendis repurganda instaurandaque principalis agitur quaestio, et licet huic intentioni Refractionum cognitio necessarias adferat suppetias, quod sine harum praecautione, ipsissima Stellarum loca caelitus deduci nequeant, tamen quia sufficit Astronomum scire quam alte Refractionum impedimenta ascendant, et quanta in certis Altitudinibus fuerint, vbi praesertim Aëris similis et inconfusa affulserit serenitas, idcirco vnde Refractiones hae proueniant, an duplici vel etiam vnica de causa, non multum interest subtiliter et ambiguose disceptare: Quemadmodum ipsa etiam de materia Caeli quaestio, non proprie ad Astronomi decisionem pertinet; Is enim non quid sit Caelum, et vnde ipsius splendida corpora constent, sed saltem quomodo omnia haec moueantur, ex Obseruationibus certis peruestigare laborat. Alterum enim Theologis et Physicis discutiendum relinquit, apud quos etiam hactenus idipsum non satis explanatum permansit. Tanta enim est, vt opera vniuersa Creatoris, ne quidem terrestria et sensibus externis obuia, nobisque adeo vt palpentur propinqua, penitus haurire queat: nedum vt sublimem illam et remotissimam Aetheris naturam et substantiam satis peruestigare explicareque sustineat. De Caeli itaque materia, quae est imperscrutabilis, non est vt anxie disceptemus, nisi quod id necessario statuendum censeam, eam qualiscunque sit, nihil cum elementari natura commune habere, ideoque nequaquam esse aëream, sed diuiniorem et longe perfectiorem in se continere naturam, quam quae vllis elementaribus rebus assimilanda merito veniat. Sufficit autem in principaliori scopo inter nos conuenire, Caelum videlicet nullis realibus et duris atque imperuijs orbibus, quibus Sidera affixa circumgyrantur, esse contextum, sed e liquidissima, rarissima, et subtilissima substantia constare, quae septem Planetarum cursus liberos, quocunque fert naturalis impetus et congenita scientia, absque vlla retardatione, admittat. Id inquam a Veteribus et maxima etiam parte Recentiorum hactenus non perspectum, neque pro indubitato concessum, satis est ad Astronomiae redintegrationem pro rato compertoque admittere: Sic enim Hypothesibus conuenientius ordinandis, quemadmodum etiam a nobis iampridem factitatum est, compendiosior et planior sternetur via. Quare, vt dixi, circa Caeli materiam, et Refractionum occasionem, pluribus vt occupemur, non est operaepretium; saltem si quid in Refractionibus per certam experientiam vlterius innotuerit, vt inuicem communicemus, ad praecisionem Astronomicam constituendam conducere videtur.</p><p>Vnicum itaque solummodo de his addam, postea ad alia transiturus. Refractiones in Sole non inueniuntur hîc circa Horizontem multopere maiores quam apud vos; siquidem et ego iuxta Altitudinem trium Graduum deprehendi in Solstitio aestiuo, non contingere Refractionem maiorem 12. Scrupulis, nam quod me eam tunc 28. esse Scrupulorum, asserere existimas, videris non satis attendisse, quod de Sole in ipso occasu, sese abscondente locutus sim, quando videlicet eius dimidia pars solummodo supra Horizontem cernitur, quod in hac Insula satis animaduertitur, praesertim in loco meae habitationis, qui adeo altus est, vt vndique liberum praebeat Finitorem visibilem. Plurimum etenim variantur circa ipsum Occasum Refractiones, adeo, vt trium Graduum differentia per quartam circiter vnius partem, illas alteret. Sic etiam in Altitudine vestra Brumali 15. partium, quando apud vos in Meridiano efficitur Refractio 7⅓. Minut. nobis in consimili Altitudine, Sole iuxta 5. Gradum ♒ vel ♏, versante, Refractionem non maiorem vnquam inuenire potui Scrupulis 7½. quod cum vestra satis concordat, cum tu tamen existimes Refractiones hîc sensibiliter maiores, quam illic insinuari. Et in Solstitio hyberno, quando Sol in Meridie nobis tantummodo Grad. 10⅔. attollitur, Refractio euadit 9½. saltem Scrupulorum, adhibita in omnibus Parallaxeos ratione. Vides itaque Refractiones Solares insensibiliter maiores hîc, quam apud vos, modo satis diligenter in consimili Altitudine expendantur, caelitus apparere. Suspicor itaque, in Stellis Fixis circa Refractionis inquisitionem, tuis inuentis aliquid subesse vitij, in eo, quod aliquantulum nostris minus habeant, vel forte, quia mare vastum e loco meae Obseruationis versus Boream patens, Refractiones in Septentrionali Horizontis parte ob vapores inde continue magis exhalantes, paululum auctiores quam alibi efficiat, in caeteris Horizontis locis non video sensibilem a tua annotatione differentiam, ideoque nihil fere importat, quod iuxta Veterum sententiam Aër in his borealibus locis densior crassiorque statuitur, nam 4½. Gradus, quibus in Latitudine differimus, parum Aëris naturam immutant; quin et Augustae Vindelicorum sub Eleuatione 48⅓. aliquot Hyemes expertus sum multo frigidiores ijs, quae apud nos communiter fiunt, et Aestates minus etiam calidas. Quia vero per Crepusculorum obseruationem discerni posse existimas, vtrum Aëris crassi superficies altius hîc attollatur, quam in vestro Horizonte: Scias me diligenter animaduertisse, iuxta tempora Aequinoctialia, Crepusculum matutinum hîc incipere Sole saltem Grad. 16⅓. infra Horizontem depresso, adeo, vt arcus Crepusculi tunc sit tantummodo 25. praecise Graduum, quem tamen Nonniana et communiter recepta ratio Partium proxime 28. efficeret: Si quando idem illic diligenter exploraueris, non dubito quin inuenies arcum Crepusculi, nihilo apud nos maiorem esse, quam Cassellis, et ob id nostri Horizontis vapores non altius ascendere, quam vobis, vnde etiam consequitur Aërem inferiorem non esse sensibiliter crassiorem in hac boreali plaga, quam in locis paulo meridionalioribus. Ex his etiam omnibus satis colligitur, ea, quae circa vaporum naturam, et hinc solummodo deductam Refractionum rationem, adeoque de totius Caeli postmodum ex his Aërea et Elementari pronuntiata materia a te in medium proferuntur, non ita certis niti fundamentis, atque ingeniose et luculenter persuadere conaris. His itaque relictis ad caetera pergamus.</p><p>Latitudinum in Fixis Stellis variationem, mutationi Declinationis Eclipticae obnoxiam esse, satis inter nos conuenit, et abunde e quam plurimis Stellis, in posterioribus meis literis comprobatum est, nec solius Syrij dissentanea latitudo huic assertioni quidpiam derogat, vt tute etiam mihi recte suffragaris, causasque lapsûs Veterum in Declinatione et Latitudine eius annotanda, apprime verisimiles adducis, quare hac de re nihil attinet nunc plura dicere. Deliquium Lunae Anni 84. equidem satis sciebam minus diligenter a Witichio obseruatum, neque ipsius proprijs annotationibus correspondere, siquidem eius in his tractandis minus peritam exercitationem, dudum cognitam habebam. In Geometricis et Triangulorum ac numerorum tractatione expeditior ac felicior erat; ignorabam cum posteriores scriberem, eum fatis concessisse, alias parcius de ipso egissem. Eclipsin Lunae, quae in Septemb. antecedentis Anni incidit, ego hîc aliquo modo obseruaui, ita vt de tempore eius intra 2. vel 3. Scrupula satis certus sim; Nam licet saltem per interualla momentanea quaedam serenitas nobis hîc, ea durante, inter recurrentes nubes illuxerit, tamen non vsqueadeo obscurum hîc fuit et pluuiosum Caelum, atque apud vos. In occidentaliori Terrarum tractu, per totam eius durationem, constans et clara affulsit serenitas. Relatum enim mihi est in Anglia optime fuisse animaduersam a principio vsque ad finem, imo et in Cymbrica Chersoneso, quae est occidentalior Daniae pars, distans hinc 30. saltem milliaribus, et vestro Meridiano paululum Occasui propior, toto tempore quo durauit haec Eclipsis, Caelum apprime serenum et vndiquaque nubium expers fuit, vt quidam meorum Studiosorum, cui illuc in Patriam abeundi venia tunc concessa erat, huc reuersus retulit. Hinc manifestum euadit, quam friuolo et incerto fundamento Astrologorum vulgarium Meteorologicae praedictiones nitantur, cum in tam vicinis Horizontibus Aëris constitutio toto Caelo saepenumero discrepet, sed haec extra propositum. Eclipsin Lunae huius labentis Anni, quae in initium Martij incidit, diligentius obseruaui, erat enim mediocris serenitas, quamdiu in vmbra laborauit, deprehendique tempora eius Prutenicum calculum per Horae semissem excessisse; Alphonsinum vero Hora 1. cum triente, et Puerbachianum per quincuncem horae anteuertisse; à Maesthlini insuper neoterica, quam in suis Ephemeridibus praeposuit, restitutione paulo plus integra hora defecisse. Quantum itaque hactenus ab eo constitutae in his redintegrationi fidendum sit, satis apparet. Sic in ea Eclipsi quae fiebat Anno 81. die 15. Iulij mane in aurora, ipsius correctio per vnam horam cum triente ab Obseruatione abundauit, refert etiam in eadem, suam ponens rectificationem, quod Luna tres digitos defecisse debuit, per quartam videlicet sui corporis partem, quando in Horizonte Tubingensi occideret, cum tamen in hoc nostro, qui semisse horae adhuc est orientalior, dimidia Luna, antequam Horizontem subiret, vmbram Terrae ingressa sit, vt ob id apud illos totam quasi in vmbra delituisse ante suum occasum, consentaneum euadat. Sic etiam in quibusdam alijs Eclipsibus notabilem differentiam restitutionis Maesthlinianae ab ipso Caelo aliquoties animaduerti, vt hinc satis pateat, cursum amborum luminarium, illi nondum ea, qua existimat praecisione, perspectum esse, imo etiam in rectificatione Fixarum Stellarum plurimum hactenus a scopo deflexit, vt ex libello eius de Cometa Anni 80. liquido constat. Video illum Instrumentis idoneis, non ingenio et scientia destitutum esse. Haec de Eclipsibus sit dixisse satis.</p><p>Quod Planetarum cursus non aeque ac Fixarum loca obseruetis diligentius, alienum quid videtur ab hac tota exercitatione; nam potissimum Fixarum loca eam ob causam verificantur, vt Planetarum per haec, cursus et apparentiae rectius innotescant. Ego in tribus remotioribus Planetis, praesertim vero Saturno et Ioue, quasdam adhuc requiro acronychias Obseruationes, vt circuitus eorum Caelo exacte congruos aliquando, fauente diuina clementia, restituam; in caeteris Planetis satis ad id perficiendum subsidij suppetit. Da operam, vt posthac in Errantium etiam Stellarum Obseruationibus fidelem naues operam, quo collatione aliquando facta, consensus inter nos et in his explorari queat. Quod Illustriss. Princeps tuus Obseruationum vestrarum et rectificationum, in locis Affixarum Stellarum publicationem meditetur, eamque vt per te fiat vrgeat, admodum laudabile et ipsius Cels. nomini perpetuo honorificum mihi censetur, elabora itaque vt breui eam habeamus, nec est quod vos moueat quinorum Scrupulorum in Longitudinibus omnium Fixarum inter nos differentia, ea enim perexigua est, et facile apud negotij intricatam profunditatem intelligentes, excusationem meretur. Quod autem in meis de his constitutionibus, nequaquam quina illa Scrupula deficiant, quodque in his ea praecisione, ad quam sensus nostri pertingere possint, omnia enuclearim, luculenter ad Illustriss. tuum Principem perscripsi, rationesque addidi, quae me in proposito confirment, neque quicquam plus vel minus iusto in his admisisse satis euidenter persuadeant. Veneris sane diameter etsi maiuscula sit, hanc tamen inter nos discrepantiam non excusabit; Mihi enim per rimulas prioris pinnacidij paululum arctiores (vt in maioribus Stellis fieri oportet) Veneris sidus obseruanti semper in idem Minutum eius distantia a fixis Sideribus, etiam aliquoties reiterata, incidit, vt nihil hinc prorsus sit periculi, quin Fixarum loca per hanc, tam matutinam quam vespertinam, semel atque iterum adamussim a me sint restituta, neque quidpiam eorum, quae in literis Illustrissimi Principis ad me perscripta sunt, nostris inuentis vllo modo derogare poterit. Nihilominus vos recte facere iudico, quod vestris etiam Obseruationibus et Restitutionibus confisi, nihil in his mutare decreueritis: Sic enim minus suspicabuntur posteri, nos data opera, et communicatis consilijs, has Stellarum Longitudines vnanimi consensu ordinâsse. Non tamen dubito quin aliquando, vbi per renouata Organa beneficio Veneris non solum Occidentalis sed etiam Eôae, negotium hoc viceuersa, et diligenti consideratione, sub incudem reuocaueritis, quin ipsimet sitis liquidissime deprehensuri, quinque illa Scrupula in vestra denotatione a caelesti amussi abundare, in mea vero nequaquam deficere, tuncque propria experientia edoctos, exactori verificationi, manus sponte porrecturos spero: Antequam enim per reciprocam Veneris ad Solem obseruationem Stellis applicatam, res haec diligentissime examinata fuerit, frustra aliquid certi denuntiatur; Nam quod per Venerem vespertinam tantummodo a te obseruatam, plausibilis cum prioribus inuentis apparuit consensus, ob Refractionis in Venere, et Sole decliui insinuationem, potius eueniebat, quam quod reuera ipsi Caelo ita consonum fuerit, velut haec latius ad Illustrissimum Principem tuum scribens, declaraui. Miror te affirmare motum Solis a me vobis transmissum, iuxta primos Gradus ♒ esse aliquantulum iusto tardiorem, cum ipsemet non diffitearis eum alias in toto Boreali Semicirculo, et iuxta vtraque Aequinoctia Obseruationibus vestris idipsum attestantibus exactissime congruere. Meminisse enim oportuit, quod in prioribus literis satis aperte indicârim, Obseruationem motus Solis saltem in Boreali Semicirculo ipsi Caelo congruere; in Australi vero ob Refractionum impedimenta, non item. Et Sol in principio ♒ apud vos Meridianus existens, attollitur saltem part. 18½. vbi admodum sensibilem Refractionem inducit, et necessario vlterior per Obseruationem apparet, quam reuera est, quod etiam mihi idipsum tentanti, saepius innotuit. Tolle itaque in Australi Semicirculo Refractionis impedimenta, et patebit nostram restitutionem in motu Solis, illic etiam non minus quam in Boreali, normae caelesti apprime correspondere. Misi autem ad Illustrissimum Principem tuum adhuc 5. annorum Ephemerides Solis, e nostro Calculo deductas, vt in sequentibus Annis, huius rei periculum, adhibita modo dicta cautione, vestris Instrumentis, fieri possit.</p><p>Transis postea a Caelestibus ad Poëtica, vel potius quasdam Hieroglyphicas connexionis superiorum et inferiorum figuras, quas in Carmine quodam, ad Nobiliss. pariterque in Latinis et Graecis literis Eruditissimum Virum FALCONEM GOYE, amicum et affinem meum dilectum, ante quadriennium animi gratiâ exarato, forte fortuna perspexisti; Curque apud depictum Philosophum, qui caelestia scrutatur, hinc inde ascribatur SVSPICIENDO DESPICIO, et circa reliquum, qui terrestria, DESPICIENDO SVSPICIO, solicite a me requiris. Vt autem votis tuis hac etiam in parte, non prorsus desim, sic habe. Hieroglyphica haec esse recte coniectasti; Nam non saltem vtramque Astronomiam caelestem illam superiorem, et inferiorem terrestrem respiciunt, sed etiam ipsam diuiniorem minusque vulgariter vsitatam Theologiam, adeoque totius Ethices cognitionem, videlicet virtutum et vitiorum discretionem. Prima vero fronte Physicam rerum creaturum considerationem prae se fert, in qua superiora inferioribus per admirandam analogiam, ita connexa sunt, vt neutrum sine altero recte percipi, penitusque indagari queat; Nam vt recte colligis, ars ea quam Spagyricam vocamus, totius naturae peruestigationem. adeoque contrectationem quandam interiorem continet, et Astronomiae cuiusdam terrestris (vt prius appellaui) exercitationem iucundam, nec inutilem exhibet. Vtque aliqua ex parte eorum quae expetis, conscius reddaris, scire conuenit, id esse septem Planetas in Caelo, quod sunt septem Metalla in Terra, quodque in homine ad vtriusque ideam fabricato, qui ob id Microcosmus recte appellatur, septem principalia membra, atque haec omnia tam pulcra et concinna similitudine inuicem colligata sunt, vt paria fere videantur habere officia easdemque proprietates et naturas. Sic duo principalia luminaria in Caelo Sol et Luna, duobus praestantioribus Metallis, Auro et Argento, in homine vero duobus praecipuis vitalibus membris cordi et cerebro aequiparatur. Duo benefici Planetae Iupiter et Venus inter Metalla terrena Stannum et Cuprum, post priora secundae dignitatis, sibi locum asciscunt, in corporibus vero nostris iecur officinam sanguinis, et Renes generationis ministros; Duo vero Planetae, quos maleficos appellant, Saturnus et Mars, Metallis abiectioribus, Plumbo videlicet et Ferro correspondent, atque in corporibus hominum duo minus necessaria et vilia membra sortiuntur, Splenem et Fel, quae sunt receptacula atrae flauaeque bilis exuberantis. Mercurius caelestis vt est natura sua indifferens, et Protheo mutabilior, sic etiam Mercurium terrestrem siue Argentum viuum sibi analogum habet, quod etiam in varias transmutatur formas et omnium reliquorum Metallorum naturam aemulatur, mirabilesque induit Metamorphoses; In corpore autem Microcosmico, huic inter septem membra principalia recte assimilatur Pulmo, cuius officium est Spiritum trahere, corque et reliqua membra sua anhelatione refocillare; inseruit etiam loquelae plurimum, velut et Mercurius ille caelestis, huic praeesse creditur. Multa sunt praeterea in his septem admiranda similitudine, quae paucis explicari nequit, sibi inuicem colligata; Sic etiam reliqua Terrae Mineralia, Gemmae, Marchasitae et Salia cum diuersis Planetis certa lege consentiunt, et naturae imprimis Affixarum Stellarum associantur, quae Planetarum vires in se licet obscurius contineant, quemadmodum etiam herbae et vegetabilia e certo quodam ordine, non solum Planetis sed etiam fixis Sideribus in terrestri Astronomia assignantur, eorundemque naturam, quantum in se est, aemulantur. Haec et plura eiuscemodi penitus intelligere, non docet vulgaris Physica, neque Academiae, Peripateticorum sophismatibus et fucis dementatae, incrustataeque, latentem in his realem ipsissimum veritatis nucleum, introspicere vel didicerunt, vel docent. Pyronomica Schola adeunda est quae non ea quae plausibiliter disseruntur, sed quae veraciter manibus palpantur, oculis videntur et sensibus exterioribus interioribusque percipiuntur, manifestat. Doctores in hoc Gymnasio aliquos habuit Antiquitas, et nostrum etiam Seculum non prorsus ijs destitutum fuit, licet pomposi et formales Academiarum Professores eos vel per incuriam negligat, vel per inscitiam repudient, aut etiam ex odio et praeoccupato iudicio detestentur; Semper enim veritas sincerior, non solum in diuinis rebus, sed etiam Mundana Philosophia, tam a vulgo hominum, quod ferendum esset, quam ab ijs, qui eam ipsam maxime profitentur (quod dolendum est) exosa et fuit, et imposterum erit, nist Deus peculiarem et mirabilem mundi Methamorphosin aliquando instituerit. Authores in hac secretiore Philosophia sunt plurimi, inter quos praecipuum locum tenent, quoad Veteres, Hermes ille Trismegistus, Author tabulae Smaragdinae, Geber, Rupecissa, Arnoldus de villa noua, Raymundus Lullius, Thomas Aquinates, Rogerius Bacho, Turba Philosophorum, Albertus Magnus; Superiori vero Aeuo Isaacus Hollandus et Theophrastus Paracelsus, quem plures oppugnant quam intelligunt. Verum etsi hi, et quidam insuper alij in hac occultiori arte scriptores, magnum discentibus subsidium, remque omnem per causas naturales expendendi peruestigandique occasionem subministrent, tamen nisi accedat diligens et diuturna tractatio per exercitium Pyronomicum, frustra nudis chartis insudatur: Rectissime enim inquit Paracelsus, neminem plus scire in hac Arte, quâm ipsemet per ignem expertus sit. Haec etenim alijs, quam dignis et idoneis penitus concredere discipulis, silentium vetat plus quam Pythagoricum. Si aliquando mecum fueris, forsan quaedam in his spectabis prius desiderata, nec vbique obuia. Non enim minori conamine et sumtu Astronomiam hanc terrestrem excolo, quam illam caelestem, ordinatis ad eam rite tractandam, oportunis structuris, et fornacum magna varietate, copiaque, quae omnia calamus referre non sustinet.</p><p>Redis deinde ab hac inferiori Philosophia ad superiorem illam et aetheream, deque scriptis tuis, quae hactenus absoluisti in re Astronomica, et ijs quae ad hanc, ex Triangulorum et numerorum cognitione spectant, me certiorem reddis. Optarem autem haec omnia a te edi, videntur enim plurimum lucis his Artibus allatura. Si vero id breui vt fiat, commodum tibi non fuerit, saltem vt manuscripta, descriptaque exemplaria mihi inspicienda breui tempore, communicentur, mox tibi bona, et inuiolata fide, restituenda, modo id tibi integrum foret, te rogatum haberem. Licet vero calculus Prutenicus sensibiliter saepenumero, ab ipso Caelo deflectat, poteris nihilominus, Hypotheses Copernianas, ad immobilitatem Terrae reductas, illi applicare, laboremque illum pereximium publicare, vt discentes calculi et Hypothesium congruentiam rectius, et maiori cum voluptate fructuque fundamentaliter percipiant, id enim ab alijs, qui id tentare aggressi sunt, non satis fideliter factitatum est, dum Ptolomaicas et Alphonsinas hypotheses Copernianis numeris, violenter citra exactam correspondentiam obtrudunt. Ego meas restitutiones in cursu Planetarum omnium, propriae, non ita dudum adinuentae hypothesi, adaptare elaborabo, calculique cum his, adeoque ipso Caelo consensum ostendere, et peculiari Opere, fauente Numine diuino, ipsemet declarare decreui. Hypotheseos vero huius generaliorem delineationem habes, in secundo nostro Tomo de nouis Aetherei Mundi Generationibus nuper tibi transmisso, quam quo diligentius perspexeris, eo magis arridebit; Tollit enim omnia tam Mathematica quam Physica absurda, quae in veteri Ptolemaica, et recenti Copernicea assumtione, delitescunt, et particularioribus apparentijs, vndiquaque aptissime satisfacit, quod neutra aliarum omnimode praestat, vt alibi ex ipsis Obseruationibus demonstrabimus. De dierum Aequationibus siquidem inter alia mentionem facis, scias me diuersa quaedam in his, ab antecessorum placitis statuere, neque eam tantam esse, quanta vel ab ipso Copernico assumitur, non solum certis rationibus, sed ipsa etiam experientia edoctum, qua de re suo loco dabitur commodior disserendi occasio. Si scriptum tuum de Cometa, amplissimo et nobilissimo D. NICOLAO KAASIO Cancellario, viro de Patria nostra et omnibus bonis artibus, praeclare merito, dedicaueris, velut te facturum scribis, non dubito quin illi sit futurum pergratum, licet nihil cum ipso hactenus ea de re priuatim sim loquutus. Subiungis postea te facile mihi concessurum, omnes Cometas in Aethere generari, nisi obstarent Regiomontani, et Vogelini Obseruationes, at ego quo loco hae habendae sint, in Epilogo totius Operis nostri de hoc toto negotio luculenter, et minime dubijs rationibus ostendam. Regiomontani annotationes, sibijpsis non consentiunt, neque ad Spicam ♍ Cometae tam velocis Parallaxis, vllo modo indagari potuit; Iis itaque cum Aristotelis assertionibus nimium confideret, si quam coniecturam de Parallaxi aliqua, quae eum sublunarem redderet, per incuriam aliquam adeptus est, eam mordicus pro veriori retinuit, videtur enim in hoc plurimum laborasse, vt quam maximam erueret. Vogelini Obseruationes admodum erroneae sunt, nec consentiunt in eodem Cometa cum ijs quae a Petro Apiano et Fracastorio prodita inueniuntur, vt alibi latius ostendam. Et motus insuper diurnus, qui ab ipso saltem part. 2⅔. constituitur, renuere videtur, Cometam hunc tam propinquum Terris fuisse, vt Parallaxin 35. Partium induxerit, adeo, vt non sesquialtera Terrae Semidiametro ab eius superficie abfuerit, vel vt idem author incompetenter astruere non dubitat. Quamuis, vt ipsum quod res est dicam, velocitas et tarditas motûs non sufficienter remotionem, vel appropinquationem Cometarum discernunt, vnde nihil obstat, id quod tu nobis opponere videris, videlicet celeritatem motus in Regiomontaniano Cometa, quae statuitur 40. partium Circuli maximi, in vna die, arguere ipsum sublunarem fuisse, tantamque habuisse Parallaxin, quantam is illi attribuit. Nam si Cometam Anni 1585. fas erat in altissimo orbe Saturni, vel te ipso iudice versari, et nihilominus ab initio motum diurnum absoluere trium Graduum, cum ipsa Saturni Stella, vel concitatissima saltem 8. Minuta vno die conficiat, adeo, vt Cometa tunc fuerit plus quam vicies ipso Saturno, vel celerrimo, velocior: Qui quaeso, non etiam concedi poterit, Cometam illum a Regiomontano descriptum fuisse supra Lunam, licet eius motu diurno triplo circiter fuerit concitatior; Cum enim ipsa realitas orbium, Caelo non competat (qua de re inuicem consentimus) possunt vtique hae secundariae et ascititiae Stellae, sortiri quemcunque motum innata fert scientia, in quocunque tandem Caeli loco vel altiori vel decliuiori, obuoluuntur; Non enim sunt Planetarum et Siderum Mundo coëtaneorum legibus omni ex parte obnoxiae, sic etiam magnitudo quorundam Cometarum, eorum vicinitatem non satis probat; Possunt enim in ipso Caelo, et in quacunque vltra Lunam sublimitate, quantamcunque Plasmatori libuerit, acquirere magnitudinem. Nec Aër sublunaris, quem tu eundem cum caelesti reputas, aptus est ad generationem talium, et tam excellentium Phaenomenôn. De his et similibus in conclusione generali Operis nostri, de extraordinarijs Aetherei Mundi generationibus, plenius meam sum expositurus sententiam, tibi et alijs, vti spero, hac in parte satisfacturus. De motu vel quiete Terrae aut ipsius Caeli, quantum ad quotidianam Reuolutionem attinet, alias quid sentiam reserabo.</p><p>Cuperem autem quam maxime vt prima quaque occasione, impetrata venia ab Illustriss. Principe tuo, me hîc inuisere non intermitteres; Sic enim et de his et de alijs ad diuinam Astronomiae artem, et absconditam a vulgo eruditorum, Philosophiam pertinentibus, coram commodius et plenius conferemus, efficiamque vt te eius itineris non paeniteat. Interim et semper quam felicissime vale mi amantissime ROTHMANNE, tempus enim est, vt Epistolam nunc claudam, siquidem tuis literis, in omnibus, quae continebant, nunc copiose pro virili nostra, et temporis occasione satisfactum est. Tuum erit omni sedulitate operam dare, vt mihi responsum ab Illustriss. Principe tuo, et a te mature adornetur, vtque hoc perficiatur interea, dum harum exhibitor in Nundinis abest, quo ad vos rediens, certo id apud te absolutum, et conscriptum inueniat. Non enim socij itineris et curruum commoditates, eum illic moram aliquam trahere patientur. Plurimum enim exoptarem, ante hyemem a vobis ad has meas literas responsionem obtinere, vt eo opportunius ad instantes postmodum vernales Nundinas rescribere liceat. Tu itaque omnem moue lapidem, ne procrastinetur negotium.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale, meque tui amantissimum redamare perge.</p><p>Datae Vraniburgi die 17. Augusti, Anno 1588. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most accomplished man in learning and virtue, Master Christoph Rothmann, distinguished mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his much-beloved friend — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>That I should be answering rather late the letters of the Most Illustrious Prince and your own, my most learned and most longed-for Rothmann, I set out my excuse in those [letters] which in the meantime I sent to you by my [man] Gellius Sascerides; for I do not doubt that they, together with our second book, <i>On the More Recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World</i>, which was joined to them, were long since delivered to you. And I reckon that you have also in the meantime diligently worked through that book — so that you may disclose to me your opinion of the matters it contains, and frankly set out your mind upon these things, I very greatly desire. I in turn will not fail to declare to you, freely and openly, so far as in me lies, what seems consonant with the truth, both in these matters and in others. So now, since your letters are richly filled with learned knowledge of astronomical things, I judge it should be put off no longer that I answer somewhat more amply. And it fell out very conveniently that I received in good enough time your later letter, by which you yourself opportunely correct the excessive elevation of terrestrial vapours that had been committed in the earlier [letter] through some oversight. For otherwise I had resolved to remind you of this matter. For the highest exhalations, by the optical writers' demonstration, scarcely rise to 12 German miles, of which the Earth's semidiameter (to which you previously made their distance equal) contains more than seventy times over. But this discrepancy from the optical writers' teachings could easily have crept in through haste amid other occupations. So now I shall answer your earlier letter in order, the emendation of the vaporous elevation in the later one being granted. About the cometary business of the year [15]86 I shall add nothing more now, for we agree in this sufficiently, nor is a discrepancy of half a minute, or a whole one, as regards parallax, of any moment — indeed it is wholly imperceptible; nor likewise shall I say anything further about the greatest declination [of the ecliptic], for I learn most gladly that it has now been confirmed by you, by the rectified plumb-line of your quadrant, in close agreement with my own findings. Concerning the Matter of the Heaven and refractions, since this matter does not yet seem sufficiently settled between us, it will be worth the effort that I treat it further with you and comply with your request on this head.</p><p>You contend that the matter of the celestial spheres is none other than elemental air, and that no refractions arise on account of the difference of the transparency [diaphana] of aether and air, and you throw back the whole occasion of refractions onto the vapours alone that lie facing the horizon. But I — that the heaven is not composed of hard and impervious matter stretched into the form of transparent membranes, as has hitherto been believed even by the foremost philosophers — of this I have been taught even by the motions of comets themselves; yet that it is airy, or partakes of any elemental nature, I shall by no means admit. For far be it that anything elemental, corruptible, variable, and conformable to this lower perishable region should be stitched onto the most pure and most perfect celestial nature, which is subject to no changes; for not even has any of the sounder natural philosophers ever dared to admit that. For all unanimously, and most rightly, hold that the heaven is utterly exempt from the number and nature of the four elements and bears forth a certain more excellent nature, and therefore they have named it a certain fifth essence [quintessence], wholly different from those four elemental ones and far more excellent. Let it not then be admitted, what the natural philosophers abhor — that the mathematicians themselves should sprinkle the heaven with so great a blemish, so that, asserting it to be composed of elemental air, they greatly derogate from its most simple and most pure and so most perfect substance and nature. And what, I ask, would be the proportion of the elements, if we should grant air alone to fill so immense a vastness from the Earth all the way to the eighth sphere? Surely the two elements of earth and water will bear no proportion that could be conceived by the senses to the single [element of] air. Besides, if elemental air were enclosed within the celestial orbs, it would be consistent that a huge sound should be stirred up by the most rapid motion of the stars, which would doubtless strike even our hearing. Air would also impede the perpetuity of the revolution of the celestial bodies, and would detract from their purity over long use and time — indeed, as if by wearing them down, it would shake their most constant harmony. I know, indeed, that Paracelsus calls the whole heaven a fourth and fiery element; and if this be understood of the life-giving heat of the Sun and stars, it may perhaps deserve a place. For that the element of fire is below the Moon, as the Peripatetics dream, the very subtlety of refractions — which by this reasoning would be rendered greater [in passing] through so many transparent bodies — if nothing else, sufficiently refutes. And so it is consistent that all the heat in mixed bodies, which the natural philosophers attribute to the element of fire, comes from on high [from the heaven]. But if Paracelsus thought otherwise, the opinion is absurd. Nor am I unaware that certain theologians, [arguing] from Moses (doubtless less rightly understood), assert that the heaven is composed of watery matter — an interpretation which, however, Philo the Jew does not admit, but contends that it [the heaven] is in no way composed of any elements; and this opinion too seems much to oppose all natural reasoning. But of those who would hold the heaven to be airy, I have learned of no one before Jean Pena the Frenchman, whose opinion you seem to favour; and recently too Giordano of Nola has dared to assert the same in a certain writing <i>On the World</i>, against the Peripatetics — and perhaps some others as well are imbued with the same opinion. But if by \"air,\" which is contained among the celestial circuits, you should wish to understand another and subtler and purer substance than this elemental air, I shall not so far oppose it — except that the name \"element of air\" in so sublime a business is in itself too cheap and suspect, if it be assigned to the heaven; and in this way one will have to distinguish between aethereal and elemental air, and so nonetheless the matter will come round to the same point — namely, that it is not of the same exquisite purity and transparency, and there will still remain much doubt, which would not appear sufficiently unravelled even by this distinction. But although I believe that nothing certain about the very matter of the heaven (if indeed it can properly be said to consist of any matter at all) is ready to hand for any mortal. For the heaven is something abstract, and like to the immaterial, escaping our grasp; nonetheless one may sufficiently conclude that it partakes of no elemental nature. Since this is something far more excellent and more divine, and so incorruptible and exalted above every elemental quality, fashioned out of a most pure, simple, and transparent and most rarefied substance, by the admirable wisdom of God and in a manner inscrutable to us. From whatever source, then, the substance of the heaven may be — which indeed is perceived rather as what it is <i>not</i> than as what it is — it must of necessity be more fluid, purer, and subtler than air itself; so that on this account I not undeservedly hold the transparency [diaphanum] of aether to be more perfect and purer than the transparency of air. For although a not very perceptible refraction of the stars is brought in at some altitude above the horizon, and not so great as the ancient optical writers — who supposed the heaven to consist of a hard and adamantine transparent body — suspected, yet this does not happen for the reason that there is nothing else between the eighth sphere and the Earth than elemental air. For, as I attested also in my earlier letter, there is no very great difference between the transparency of aether and of air, nor do the transparent bodies of air and water differ perceptibly; but since both [aether and air] are most tenuous and most fluid, they have very much affinity — indeed, what diversity there is between them is gradually diminished as the air is rendered loftier from the Earth and nearer to the Moon's circuits; for there it coalesces, as it were, into one with the aether, and gradually takes on the celestial nature, and on that account does not cause a perceptible refraction. For there is no fixed place at a definite distance from the Earth in which that refraction is generated, but the airy transparent body is gradually transformed into the aethereal, and therefore no contrariety occurs of the kind we see arising between air and water, but rather a certain identity is brought about, which forecloses the occasion for any very perceptible refraction — especially in the loftier position of the stars, when their light is darted more directly to us. And on that account I attribute the smallest part of refractions to the diversity of the transparency of aether and air, but I hold their chief cause to be the vapours that lie facing the Earth, so that refractions occur in a twofold way: partly on account of the vaporous exhalations that condense the air near the Earth — and these are the greatest and most perceptible, and therefore the noticing of them lies open to very many; but partly on account of some variety of the transparent bodies of the heaven and of air. This latter, however — because even at the horizon itself it is not very great, nor is it perceived (especially in the stars) beyond the 30th degree of altitude — has hitherto been less considered, and therefore it is chiefly of it that I have spoken, presupposing the other as elsewhere sufficiently understood. Nor would I deny that this very one of which I speak is in some measure prolonged right up to the zenith, but beyond the 30th degree of elevation I judge it to be so gradually more and more attenuated that thereafter it in no way falls upon the senses, on account of the more direct rays of the stars, and the not-so-great or contrary diversity of the transparent bodies.</p><p>You yourself assert, in your writing on the last comet which you sent me, that refractions do not endure beyond that space within which the ascending vapours are wont to be multiplied, and that this is at most [up to] 20 degrees, and that on this account no refraction arises other than through the vapours alone. But since I had observed refractions in the Sun to be perceptible around the 30th degree, I inferred that they are generated also from elsewhere than through the vapours — namely, from the diversity of the transparent bodies of aether and air. Nor do I see how these [observations] overthrow my opinion and make for your side; for I did not say that no refraction at all occurs at the 30th degree of altitude — indeed, when I examine my observations more accurately, with the difference of parallax applied, I find that in the Sun a refraction of nearly 1½ minutes occurs at an altitude of 30 degrees — which can scarcely be excused by the vapours alone, since their impediment is suddenly altered around the horizon and at so great an altitude is exceedingly small, as will afterward be evident from your own demonstration. But that [refraction] which arises by reason of the transparent bodies is not indeed so great, but lasts longer — although in the fixed stars I have not hitherto been able to observe a perceptible refraction insinuating itself so high as in the Sun, as I indicated also in the preceding letter; and this, as I reckon, on account of the very great difference of light and of magnitude, and also of distance. Nor, if the refraction around the horizon were 3 minutes, does it at once follow that on that account it would be 1½ minutes at an altitude of 45 degrees, as you would have it. For the quantities of refractions do not decrease proportionally and equally, but at the lower altitudes much more is lost from them than at the higher; and this doubtless from both causes, both by reason of the vapours flitting about the Earth, and also of the diversity of the transparent bodies of the heaven and of air — yet chiefly and most of all on account of the terrestrial exhalations themselves, when they receive the light of the stars obliquely; for experience itself attests this, that refractions are suddenly altered around the horizon, but in the loftier position this happens gradually and more slowly. Thus in the Sun I have observed the refraction to vary by six first-minutes [arc-minutes] from the first to the second degree of altitude, but from the second to the third by at least four; while in the loftier position, beyond the 20th degree of altitude, that change is scarcely half a minute — and although this can be not inconveniently excused by the vapours alone, yet something also has secretly got mingled into it from the discrepancy of the transparent bodies. But that the refractions arisen through the distinction of the transparent bodies do not appear to last perceptibly right up to the zenith, the cause is that the discrepancy of these [bodies] is not very great, and that gradually, as I said before, they are assimilated to one another — so much so that the light of the stars, when they are loftier, can easily penetrate both without refraction, since then they dart it to us not so obliquely but as it were by a direct light. For if what you infer can be granted — that refractions arise not so much on account of those vapours as on account of their multiplication around the horizon, since the rays of the stars and of the Sun overcome that thickness, and find no point of refraction in it at a slightly loftier position because of the identity of the transparent bodies — then surely, by reason of the transparency of aether and of air, the same is much rather to be admitted: that in the loftier position the stars shine forth to us without perceptible refraction. For far greater here is the identity and likeness of the transparent bodies, since the aether and the upper air do not differ among themselves as much as pure air and vaporous [air] do.</p><p>[Figure 11.] Which will be more rightly perceived from your own demonstration, especially that which you added in your second letter, which is much more suitable than the former; for there, if it is arranged out of the demonstrations of Alhazen and Witelo, at the highest elevation of the vapours FE [will be] 48 parts 50 minutes, of which the Earth's semidiameter EA [is] 60 parts; equal to these two will be X, 60 parts 48 minutes 50 seconds. And since the angle AEX is right and EA is a given side, there comes out, by trigonometric operation, the side XE [as] 9 parts 55 minutes — of which, namely, EA is 60. And so great will be the density of the vapours next to the horizon, which will amount to 142 German miles, inasmuch as EA, the Earth's semidiameter, is 860 of the same [miles], very nearly. But in the other triangle AEO, from the two known sides AO (likewise 60 parts 48 minutes 50 seconds) and EA (as before, 60), and the included angle OEA of 120 parts — namely the angle OEX of 30 parts being added to the right angle XEA — there will come out also the third side OE, [of] nearly 1 part 36 minutes, measuring the extent of the vapours at an altitude of 30 degrees; and if the conversion be made into miles as before, 23 German [miles] will leap forth. It is therefore plain that the vapours are indeed very nearly six times denser around the horizon than at an altitude of 30 degrees; nor yet does it on this account necessarily follow that absolutely no refraction need occur by reason of the vapours at such an altitude, since the interval of vapours, amounting in its capacity to 23 miles, does not seem altogether insufficient to refract the rays of the stars a little. Or if that can be granted, how much more is the same not to be admitted in the diversity of the transparency of aether and air — from which this [diversity], at such an altitude, brings in a still much smaller difference, for the reasons judged above. Moreover, even if it be granted that no refraction at all arises on the occasion of the diversity of air and aether, but that the whole draws its origin from the vapours alone, yet it would not necessarily follow on that account that the heaven itself is composed of elemental and animal[ate] air, as is your pleasure; for the likeness and nearness of the transparent bodies, and their mutual connection, can be so great that it begets no refraction such as is perceived by us, the light of the stars strongly penetrating each transparent body. For we do not hold the heaven to consist of a hard and impervious transparency, as Alhazen and Witelo [held], who on that account vainly believed that the greatest refraction occurs — but that the aether itself is most fluid and most rarefied, and woven of no real orbs, just as you too most rightly agree with me; and although it is something more perfect than air itself, yet around the revolutions, especially the lunar ones, this elemental air is so exalted that it passes over, as it were, into aethereal substance, and therefore the diversity of the transparent bodies is rendered imperceptible, as was indicated before too. But the more the air approaches the Earth, the less it partakes of the aethereal nature, and the more it has of elemental quality; and nonetheless it is very subtle and rare, so that it transmits the light of the stars easily and without refraction, unless it be nearer the Earth, where it is condensed by vapours and rendered more turbid. But as to refractions — whence they come, whether merely with the vapours as intermediaries, as it seems to you, or rather not only from these but also in some measure from the discrepancy of the transparency of aether and air, by a certain mixed reasoning, as is my pleasure — we dispute over this so greatly in vain, since the principal question being treated is about restoring the motion of the celestial bodies and about astronomy itself being purged of faults and renewed; and although the knowledge of refractions brings necessary aid to this aim — because without taking precaution of these, the very places of the stars cannot be deduced from the heaven — yet because it suffices the astronomer to know how high the impediments of refractions ascend, and how great they are at certain altitudes (especially where a uniform and unconfused serenity of air has shone clear), therefore whence these refractions come, whether from a twofold or even a single cause, it does not much matter to dispute subtly and ambiguously. Just as the very question concerning the matter of the heaven does not properly pertain to the astronomer's decision; for he labours to investigate from sure observations, not what the heaven is and of what its splendid bodies consist, but only how all these things are moved. The other he leaves to be debated by the theologians and natural philosophers, among whom indeed this very thing has hitherto remained not sufficiently explained. For so great is [the matter] that one cannot fully drain even the whole works of the Creator — not even the terrestrial ones, lying open to the external senses and so near us that they may be touched — much less is one equal to investigating and explaining sufficiently that sublime and most remote nature and substance of the aether. About the matter of the heaven, therefore, which is inscrutable, there is no reason for us to dispute anxiously, except that I judge this must necessarily be laid down: that it, whatever it may be, has nothing in common with the elemental nature, and therefore is by no means airy, but contains within itself a more divine and far more perfect nature than may deservedly be likened to any elemental things. But it suffices that we agree on the more principal aim — namely, that the heaven is woven of no real and hard and impervious orbs, by which the stars are carried around fixed [to them], but consists of a most fluid, most rarefied, and most subtle substance, which admits the free courses of the seven planets, wherever their natural impulse and inborn knowledge carries them, without any retardation. This, I say, hitherto not perceived by the ancients and even by the greatest part of the moderns, nor granted as indubitable, is enough to admit as ratified and ascertained for the reintegration of astronomy; for thus, by ordering the hypotheses more suitably — as has also for some time been done by us — a more compendious and plainer way will be paved. Wherefore, as I said, it is not worth the effort that we busy ourselves at length about the matter of the heaven and the occasion of refractions; only, if anything further in refractions becomes known through sure experience, it seems conducive, to the establishing of astronomical precision, that we communicate it to one another.</p><p>So I shall add one single thing only about these matters, and afterward pass on to others. Refractions in the Sun are not found here around the horizon much greater than with you; for I too, at an altitude of three degrees, found at the summer solstice that no refraction greater than 12 minutes occurs — for as to your reckoning that I assert it then to be 28 minutes, you seem not to have attended sufficiently that I spoke of the Sun in the very setting, as it hides itself, when namely only half of it is discerned above the horizon — which is well enough observed on this island, especially in the place of my dwelling, which is so high that it affords a free visible horizon [finitor] on every side. For refractions vary very greatly around the very setting, so much so that a difference of three degrees alters them by about a quarter of one degree. So likewise at your winter altitude of 15 degrees, when with you a refraction of 7⅓ minutes is produced on the meridian, I, at a like altitude — the Sun being near the 5th degree of ♒ Aquarius or ♏ Scorpio — was never able to find a refraction greater than 7½ minutes, which agrees well enough with yours, although you nonetheless reckon that refractions here insinuate themselves perceptibly greater than there. And at the winter solstice, when the Sun at midday rises for us only 10⅔ degrees, the refraction turns out to be at least 9½ minutes, the reckoning of parallax being applied in all cases. You see, then, that solar refractions appear in the heaven imperceptibly greater here than with you, provided they be weighed diligently enough at a like altitude. I suspect, therefore, that in the fixed stars, in the inquiry concerning refraction, there is some fault underlying your findings, in that they have somewhat less than ours; or perhaps because the vast sea lying open from the place of my observation toward the north makes the refractions in the northern part of the horizon a little greater than elsewhere, on account of the vapours continually exhaling more from there. In the rest of the places of the horizon I see no perceptible difference from your annotation, and so it matters hardly at all that, according to the opinion of the ancients, the air in these northern places is held to be denser and thicker; for the 4½ degrees by which we differ in latitude change the nature of the air little. Indeed, at Augsburg too, under an elevation of 48⅓ [degrees], I experienced several winters much colder than those that commonly occur with us, and summers also less hot. But because you reckon it can be discerned, by the observation of twilights, whether the surface of the thick air is raised higher here than in your horizon: know that I have diligently observed, near the equinoctial times, that the morning twilight here begins when the Sun is depressed at least 16⅓ degrees below the horizon — so much so that the arc of twilight is then precisely only 25 degrees, which, however, the Nonnian and commonly received reckoning would make nearly 28 degrees. If you should at some time diligently explore the same there, I do not doubt that you will find the arc of twilight to be no greater with us than at Kassel, and on that account the vapours of our horizon to ascend no higher than with you — whence it also follows that the lower air is not perceptibly thicker in this northern region than in places somewhat more southerly. From all these things, too, it is sufficiently gathered that those things which you bring forward concerning the nature of the vapours, and the account of refractions deduced from these alone, and so concerning the matter of the whole heaven, afterward pronounced from these to be airy and elemental, do not rest on foundations so sure as you ingeniously and clearly endeavour to persuade. These things, then, being left, let us proceed to the rest.</p><p>That the variation of the latitudes in the fixed stars is subject to the change of the declination of the ecliptic, is sufficiently agreed between us, and has been abundantly confirmed from very many stars in my later letter; nor does the discordant latitude of Sirius alone derogate anything from this assertion, as you too rightly support me, and you adduce most plausible causes for the ancients' slip in noting its declination and latitude — wherefore there is no need to say more about this matter now. The eclipse of the Moon of the year [15]84 I knew well enough to have been observed less diligently by Wittich, and not to correspond with his own annotations, since I had long known his less skilled practice in handling these things. In geometrical matters and in the handling of triangles and numbers he was nimbler and more successful; I did not know, when I was writing the later [letter], that he had yielded to the fates, otherwise I should have dealt with him more sparingly. The eclipse of the Moon which fell in September of the preceding year I observed here after a fashion, so that I am sure enough of its time within 2 or 3 minutes; for although here, while it lasted, a certain serenity shone for us at least through momentary intervals among the recurring clouds, yet the sky here was not so dark and rainy as with you. In the more westerly tract of lands, throughout its whole duration, a constant and clear serenity shone. For it was reported to me that in England it was very well observed from beginning to end — indeed, even in the Cimbric Chersonese (which is the more westerly part of Denmark, distant at least 30 miles from here, and a little nearer the setting [west] than your meridian), throughout the whole time during which this eclipse lasted, the sky was thoroughly serene and on every side free of clouds, as one of my students, to whom leave had then been granted to go away thither into his homeland, reported on his return here. Hence it becomes manifest on how frivolous and uncertain a foundation the meteorological predictions of the common run of astrologers rest, since in horizons so near one another the constitution of the air often differs by the whole heaven [i.e. utterly] — but this is beside the point. The eclipse of the Moon of this passing year, which fell at the beginning of March, I observed more diligently, for there was a moderate serenity for as long as it laboured in the shadow; and I found that its times exceeded the Prutenic calculation by half an hour, but anticipated the Alphonsine by 1 hour and a third [1⅓ h], and the Peurbachian by five-twelfths of an hour [a quincunx]; and that, moreover, it fell short of Maestlin's newfangled restitution, which he set at the front in his Ephemerides, by a little more than a whole hour. So it is sufficiently apparent how far the reintegration established by him in these matters is hitherto to be trusted. Thus in that eclipse which occurred in the year [15]81, on 15 July in the morning at dawn, his correction overran the observation by one hour and a third; he reports too, in the same [eclipse], setting down his rectification, that the Moon ought to have been eclipsed by three digits — namely by a quarter of its body — when it set on the Tübingen horizon, whereas in this our [horizon], which is still more easterly by half an hour, the Moon entered the Earth's shadow half [eclipsed] before it sank beneath the horizon — so that on this account it turns out consistent that with them it lay hidden, as it were, wholly in the shadow before its setting. Thus also in certain other eclipses I have several times observed a notable difference of Maestlin's restitution from the heaven itself, so that hence it is sufficiently plain that the course of both luminaries has not yet been perceived by him with that precision which he supposes — indeed, even in the rectification of the fixed stars he has hitherto greatly deviated from the mark, as is clearly evident from his little book on the comet of the year [15]80. I see that he is not destitute of suitable instruments, [but] of native talent and knowledge. Let this much be enough to have said about eclipses.</p><p>That you observe the courses of the planets less diligently than the places of the fixed stars seems something alien to this whole undertaking; for the places of the fixed stars are verified chiefly for this cause, that through them the courses and appearances of the planets may be more rightly made known. In the three more distant planets, especially Saturn and Jupiter, I still require certain acronychal observations, so that I may at some time, with the favour of divine clemency, restore their circuits exactly congruent with the heaven; in the rest of the planets there is enough means at hand for accomplishing it. Take care that hereafter you bestow faithful labour also on the observations of the wandering stars [planets], so that, a collation being at some time made, the agreement between us may be tested in these too. That your Most Illustrious Prince ponders the publication of your observations and rectifications in the places of the affixed [fixed] stars, and urges that it be done through you, I judge very praiseworthy and perpetually honourable to His Highness's name; labour, then, that we may shortly have it; nor is there reason that the difference of five minutes between us in the longitudes of all the fixed stars should move you, for it is exceedingly small, and easily earns excuse among those who understand the intricate depth of the business. But that in my own determinations of these things those five minutes by no means fall short, and that in these I have worked everything out with that precision to which our senses can attain, I have set out clearly to your Most Illustrious Prince, and I have added reasons which confirm me in my position and which persuade evidently enough that I have admitted nothing more or less than is right in these matters. Venus's diameter, to be sure, although it be largish, will yet not excuse this discrepancy between us; for when I observe the star of Venus through the slits of the earlier sight [pinnule], somewhat narrower (as ought to be done with the larger stars), its distance from the fixed stars always fell to the same minute, even when repeated several times — so that there is hence no danger at all but that the places of the fixed stars have been restored by me to the hairline [exactly], once and again, both in the morning and in the evening, nor will anything of what was written to me in the Most Illustrious Prince's letter be able in any way to derogate from our findings. Nonetheless I judge that you do rightly, in that, trusting also to your own observations and restitutions, you have decided to change nothing in these; for thus posterity will less suspect that we, by deliberate effort and by sharing counsels, ordered these longitudes of the stars by unanimous agreement. Yet I do not doubt that at some time, when through renewed instruments, by the benefit of Venus not only as Evening- but also as Morning-[star], you have recalled this business to the anvil from the opposite side and with diligent consideration, you will yourselves most clearly detect that those five minutes in your notation are in excess of the celestial measuring-line, but in mine by no means fall short; and that then, taught by your own experience, you will, I hope, of your own accord stretch out your hands to the more exact verification. For until this matter has been most diligently examined through the reciprocal observation of Venus relative to the Sun, applied to the stars, anything certain is announced in vain; for that, through Venus observed by you as Evening-star only, a plausible agreement with the earlier findings appeared, was happening rather on account of the insinuation of refraction in Venus and in the setting Sun, than because it was really so consonant with the heaven — as I have set out more fully in writing to your Most Illustrious Prince. I marvel that you affirm the motion of the Sun transmitted by me to you, near the first degrees of ♒ Aquarius, to be somewhat slower than is right, since you yourself do not deny that it elsewhere agrees most exactly, in the whole northern semicircle, and near both equinoxes, with your observations themselves attesting the same. For one ought to have remembered that in my earlier letter I indicated openly enough that the observation of the Sun's motion agrees with the heaven itself only in the northern semicircle, but in the southern, on account of the impediments of refractions, not so. And the Sun, being on the meridian with you at the beginning of ♒ Aquarius, rises only 18½ degrees, where it induces a very perceptible refraction, and necessarily appears farther on by observation than it really is — which has often become known to me too as I tried this very thing. Remove, then, the impediments of refraction in the southern semicircle, and it will be plain that our restitution in the Sun's motion corresponds there too, no less than in the northern, very closely with the celestial standard. And I have sent to your Most Illustrious Prince in addition 5 years' Ephemerides of the Sun, deduced from our calculation, so that in the following years a trial of this matter may be made with your instruments, the just-mentioned caution being applied.</p><p>You pass afterward from the celestial to the poetical — or rather to certain hieroglyphic figures of the connection of the higher and lower things, which you chanced to behold in a certain poem composed by me four years ago for pleasure's sake, [addressed] to the most noble man, equally most learned in Latin and Greek letters, Falk Gøye, my friend and kinsman, beloved by me; and you anxiously ask of me why beside the depicted Philosopher who scrutinizes celestial things there is written on this side and that LOOKING UP, I LOOK DOWN [SUSPICIENDO DESPICIO], and about the other [Philosopher], who [scrutinizes] terrestrial things, LOOKING DOWN, I LOOK UP [DESPICIENDO SUSPICIO]. That I may not, in this part too, fail your wishes altogether, take it thus. You conjectured rightly that these are hieroglyphics; for they regard not only each Astronomy — that higher celestial one, and the lower terrestrial — but also the more divine and less commonly used Theology itself, and so the knowledge of all Ethics, namely the discernment of virtues and vices. But at first sight it bears forth the physical consideration of created things, in which the higher are so connected with the lower by an admirable analogy that neither can be rightly perceived nor thoroughly searched out without the other; for, as you rightly gather, that art which we call Spagyric [chymistry] contains the investigation of all nature, and so a certain inner handling [of it], and presents a pleasant and not useless exercise of a certain terrestrial Astronomy (as I called it before). And that you may be made conscious of some part of the things you seek, it is fitting to know that there are seven planets in the heaven, just as there are seven metals in the earth, and that in man — fashioned to the idea of both, who on that account is rightly called a Microcosm — there are seven principal members; and all these are bound together with one another by so beautiful and fitting a likeness that they seem to have well-nigh equal offices and the same properties and natures. Thus the two principal luminaries in the heaven, the Sun and the Moon, are matched with the two more excellent metals, Gold and Silver, and in man with the two chief vital members, the heart and the brain. The two beneficent planets, Jupiter and Venus, take to themselves a place among the earthly metals, Tin and Copper, of the second rank after the former; and in our bodies, the liver, the workshop of the blood, and the kidneys [Renes], the ministers of generation. But the two planets which they call maleficent, Saturn and Mars, correspond to the baser metals, namely Lead and Iron, and in the bodies of men are allotted two less necessary and lowly members, the Spleen and the Gall-bladder [Fel], which are the receptacles of the overflowing black and yellow bile. The celestial Mercury, as it is by its nature indifferent [neutral] and more changeable than Proteus, so it has analogous to itself the terrestrial Mercury, or quicksilver, which likewise is transmuted into various forms and emulates the nature of all the rest of the metals, and puts on marvellous metamorphoses; and in the microcosmic body, to this among the seven principal members the Lung is rightly likened, whose office is to draw breath [Spiritus] and to revive the heart and the rest of the members by its respiration; it serves speech, too, very much — just as that celestial Mercury also is believed to preside over this [speech]. There are, besides, many things in these seven, bound together with one another by an admirable likeness, which cannot be explained in few words. So too the rest of the earth's minerals — gems, marcasites, and salts — agree by a fixed law with the various planets, and are associated chiefly with the nature of the fixed stars, which contain the powers of the planets within themselves, though more obscurely; just as herbs and vegetables too, by a certain fixed order, are assigned in terrestrial Astronomy not only to the planets but also to the fixed stars, and emulate their nature so far as in them lies. These things and many more of the kind, to understand thoroughly, common Physics does not teach; nor have the Academies — maddened and encrusted by the sophisms and rouges of the Peripatetics — either learned or [do they] teach how to look into the real, very kernel of truth lying hidden in these matters. The Pyronomic [fire-working] School must be approached, which makes manifest not the things that are plausibly discoursed of, but the things that are truly handled with the hands, seen with the eyes, and perceived by the outer and inner senses. Antiquity had some Teachers in this Gymnasium, and our own Age too has not been wholly destitute of them, although the pompous and formal Professors of the Academies either neglect them through carelessness, or repudiate them through ignorance, or even detest them out of hatred and prejudiced judgement; for the more sincere truth, not only in divine matters but also in worldly Philosophy, has both been, and hereafter will be, hated as much by the common run of men (which were to be borne) as by those who most profess it (which is to be lamented) — unless God shall at some time institute a special and marvellous Metamorphosis of the world. The authors in this more secret Philosophy are very many, among whom the chief place is held, as for the ancients, by that Hermes Trismegistus, author of the Emerald Tablet; Geber; [John of] Rupescissa; Arnaldus of Villanova; Raymond Lull; Thomas Aquinas; Roger Bacon; the <i>Turba Philosophorum</i>; Albertus Magnus; and in the more recent Age, Isaac Hollandus and Theophrastus Paracelsus, whom more attack than understand. But although these, and certain others besides who are writers in this more occult art, supply great assistance to learners, and the occasion of weighing and investigating the whole matter through natural causes, yet unless there be added a diligent and long-continued handling through Pyronomic [fire-working] practice, one sweats over the bare pages in vain; for most rightly does Paracelsus say that no one knows more in this Art than he has himself experienced through fire. For a silence more than Pythagorean forbids these things to be wholly entrusted to disciples other than the worthy and fit. If you are at some time with me, perhaps you will behold in these matters certain things previously desired, and not everywhere to be met with. For I cultivate this terrestrial Astronomy with no less effort and expense than that celestial one, the suitable structures being arranged for treating it duly, and with a great variety and abundance of furnaces, all of which the pen does not undertake to relate.</p><p>You return then from this lower Philosophy to that higher and aethereal one, and you inform me about your writings, which you have hitherto completed in the astronomical field, and about those which pertain to this from the knowledge of triangles and numbers. And I should wish all these to be published by you, for they seem likely to bring much light to these Arts. But if it should not be convenient for you that this be done shortly, [then] at least, that the manuscript and transcribed copies be communicated to me, to be inspected for a short time and soon to be restored to you in good and inviolate faith — provided this were open [permissible] for you — I would have you requested. And although the Prutenic calculation often perceptibly deviates from the heaven itself, you can nonetheless apply the Copernican hypotheses, reduced to the immobility of the Earth, to it, and publish that most excellent labour, so that learners may more rightly, and with greater pleasure and profit, fundamentally perceive the congruence of the calculation and the hypotheses; for this has not been done faithfully enough by others who have undertaken to attempt it, while they violently thrust the Ptolemaic and Alphonsine hypotheses upon the Copernican numbers, short of exact correspondence. I shall work to adapt my own restitutions in the course of all the planets to a hypothesis of my own, not so long ago devised, and I have resolved to show the agreement of the calculation with these, and so with the heaven itself, and to set it forth myself in a particular work, the divine Power favouring. But of this hypothesis you have a more general delineation in our second Volume, <i>On the New Generations of the Aethereal World</i>, lately transmitted to you, which the more diligently you examine, the more it will please you; for it removes all the absurdities, both mathematical and physical, that lurk in the old Ptolemaic and in the recent Copernican assumption, and satisfies the more particular appearances most aptly on every side, which neither of the others does in every respect, as we shall demonstrate elsewhere from the observations themselves. Since among other things you make mention of the Equations of days, know that I lay down certain things in these differently from the opinions of my predecessors, and that it [the equation of days] is not so great as is assumed even by Copernicus himself — having been taught not only by sure reasonings, but also by experience itself — concerning which a more convenient occasion for discoursing will be given in its place. If you dedicate your writing on the comet to the most ample and most noble Master Niels Kaas, the Chancellor, a man brilliantly deserving of our Fatherland and of all the good arts — as you write you will do — I do not doubt that it will be most welcome to him, although I have hitherto said nothing privately to him about this matter. You add afterward that you would readily grant me that all comets are generated in the aether, were it not that the observations of Regiomontanus and of Vogelin stood in the way; but I shall show, in the Epilogue of our whole Work, in what place these are to be held, clearly and by reasonings in no way doubtful, concerning this whole business. Regiomontanus's annotations do not agree with themselves, nor could the parallax of so swift a comet at the Ear of Corn [Spica] of ♍ Virgo be traced out in any way; since, then, he trusted too much in these together with the assertions of Aristotle, if he obtained through some carelessness any conjecture about some parallax that would render it sublunar, he held to it tooth and nail as the truer; for he seems to have laboured much in this, to dig out as great a [parallax] as possible. Vogelin's observations are quite erroneous, nor do they agree, in the same comet, with those which are found published by Peter Apian and Fracastoro, as I shall show more fully elsewhere. And the daily motion besides, which is set by him at least at 2⅔ degrees, seems to refuse [the claim] that this comet was so near the Earth that it produced a parallax of 35 degrees — so much so that it was not distant from the Earth's surface by [more than] one and a half semidiameters of the Earth, as the same author does not hesitate incompetently to maintain. Although — to say the very thing as it is — the swiftness and slowness of motion do not sufficiently discern the remoteness or the approach of comets, whence nothing stands in the way of that which you seem to oppose to us: namely, that the swiftness of motion in the Regiomontanian comet, which is set at 40 degrees of a great circle in one day, argues that it was sublunar, and had as great a parallax as he attributes to it. For if it was permissible for the comet of the year 1585 to be situated in the highest orb of Saturn — even by your own judgement — and nonetheless from the beginning to complete a daily motion of three degrees, when the star of Saturn itself, even at its swiftest, accomplishes at least 8 minutes in one day (so much so that the comet was then more than twenty times swifter than Saturn itself, even at its fastest): who, I ask, will not also be able to grant that that comet described by Regiomontanus was above the Moon, although by its daily motion it was about three times swifter? For since the very reality of the orbs does not belong to the heaven (on which matter we agree with one another), these secondary and adventitious stars [comets] can in any case obtain whatever motion their inborn knowledge brings, in whatever place of the heaven, higher or lower, they are at last revolved; for they are not in every respect subject to the laws of the planets and of the stars coeval with the world. So too the magnitude of certain comets does not sufficiently prove their nearness; for they can, in the heaven itself and at whatever loftiness beyond the Moon, acquire as great a magnitude as it pleased the Fashioner [Creator]. Nor is the sublunar air, which you reckon the same as the celestial, apt for the generation of such and so excellent phenomena. Concerning these things and the like I am about to set out my opinion more fully in the general conclusion of our Work <i>On the Extraordinary Generations of the Aethereal World</i>, intending, as I hope, to satisfy you and others in this part. About the motion or rest of the Earth or of the heaven itself, as far as concerns the daily revolution, I shall disclose elsewhere what I think.</p><p>But I should very greatly wish that at the first opportunity, leave having been obtained from your Most Illustrious Prince, you would not fail to visit me here; for thus we shall confer in person more conveniently and fully, both about these things and about others pertaining to the divine art of Astronomy and to the Philosophy hidden from the common run of the learned, and I shall bring it about that you do not repent of that journey. In the meantime, and always, fare you most happily, my most beloved Rothmann, for it is time that I now close the letter, since your letters have now, in all the things they contained, been answered copiously, according to our strength and the occasion of the time. It will be your part to give labour with all diligence, that a reply from your Most Illustrious Prince and from you be prepared for me betimes, and that this be accomplished in the meantime, while the bearer of these [letters] is away at the Fair — so that, returning to you, he may find it certainly completed and written out at your house. For neither his travelling-companions nor the conveniences of the wagons will allow him to draw out any delay there. For I should very greatly wish to obtain a reply to this my letter from you before winter, so that it may be the more opportunely permitted to write back to the coming spring Fair afterward. Do you, therefore, move every stone, lest the business be procrastinated.</p><p>Again, fare you most happily, and continue to love in return me who most love you.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 17th day of August, in the year 1588. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60–after 1600), mathematician-astronomer at the Hesse-Kassel observatory of <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (<i>Illustrissimus Princeps Wilhelmus Landtgravius Hassiae</i>). This is one of the central technical letters of the Tycho–Rothmann exchange (printed by Tycho in <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> I, Uraniborg 1596), debating the <i>materia caeli</i> (substance of the heaven) and the cause of atmospheric refraction.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Gellius Sascerides</i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (1562–1612), Tycho's assistant/famulus, courier of the earlier packet. <i>Witichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (d. 1586), the Wrocław/Breslau mathematician who had worked at Hven; Tycho notes he had died (\"yielded to the fates\") and so wishes he had treated him \"more sparingly.\" <i>Maesthlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), professor at Tübingen (Kepler's teacher); his <i>Ephemerides</i> and his <i>libellus de Cometa Anni 80</i> (the tract on the comet of 1580) are criticized. <i>Iohannes Pena Gallus</i> = <b>Jean Pena</b> (c. 1528–1558), French royal mathematician, who in the preface to his Euclid <i>Optica</i> (1557) argued for an airy heaven. <i>Iordanus Nullanus</i> = <b>Giordano Bruno of Nola</b> (\"of Nola\"); the <i>scriptum de Mundo contra Peripateticos</i> alludes to Bruno's cosmological dialogues (<i>De l'infinito, universo e mondi</i> / <i>La cena de le ceneri</i>, London 1584) or <i>De immenso</i>. <i>Falco Goye</i> = <b>Falk Gøye</b> (Falco Gøye, 1545–1594), Danish nobleman, Tycho's friend and kinsman (<i>affinis</i>), addressee of Tycho's emblematic poem. <i>Nicolaus Kaasius</i> = <b>Niels Kaas</b> (1535–1594), Chancellor of Denmark. <i>Regiomontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Müller of Königsberg</b> (1436–1476). <i>Vogelinus</i> = <b>Georg Vögelin</b> (Vögelin/Voegelin), 16th-c. author on the 1532 comet. <i>Petrus Apianus</i> = <b>Peter Apian</b> (1495–1552); <i>Fracastorius</i> = <b>Girolamo Fracastoro</b> (1476/8–1553). <i>Alhasen</i> = <b>Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen)</b>; <i>Vitellio</i> = <b>Witelo</b> (13th-c. optical writer); both cited as the <i>veteres optici</i> who thought the heaven a hard, adamantine transparent body. <b>Alchemical authority-roster:</b> <i>Hermes Trismegistus</i> (author of the <i>Tabula Smaragdina</i>, the Emerald Tablet); <i>Geber</i> (Jābir / pseudo-Geber); <i>Rupecissa</i> = <b>John of Rupescissa</b>; <i>Arnoldus de villa nova</i> = <b>Arnaldus of Villanova</b>; <i>Raymundus Lullius</i> = <b>Ramon Llull</b> (pseudo-Lullian corpus); <i>Thomas Aquinates</i> = <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> (pseudo-Aquinas alchemica); <i>Rogerius Bacho</i> = <b>Roger Bacon</b>; <i>Turba Philosophorum</i> (the anonymous alchemical dialogue); <i>Albertus Magnus</i>; <i>Isaacus Hollandus</i> = <b>Isaac Hollandus</b>; <i>Theophrastus Paracelsus</i> = <b>Paracelsus</b>.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> (Tycho's observatory on Hven). <i>Cassellis</i> = <b>Kassel</b>. <i>Augusta Vindelicorum</i> = <b>Augsburg</b> (latitude given as 48⅓°). <i>Cymbrica Chersonesus</i> = the <b>Cimbric Chersonese</b> = Jutland (here called \"the more westerly part of Denmark\"). <i>Horizon Tubingensis</i> = <b>Tübingen</b> (Maestlin's meridian).</li><li><b>Dateline / date crux.</b> The source's own subscription reads <i>\"Datae Vraniburgi die 17. Augusti, Anno 1588\"</i> — <b>17 August 1588</b> (O.S.; Denmark was on the Julian calendar). The dataset's `label` field reads <i>\"1587. 17. august. Uraniborg.\"</i> <b>This discrepancy is not silently resolved:</b> the header leads with the as-written date (17 August 1588) and appends \"(catalogued 1587).\" Internal evidence supports <b>1588</b>: the letter answers Rothmann's writing on \"the last comet\" and refers to the lunar eclipse \"at the beginning of March\" <i>of this passing year</i> (the eclipse of early March 1588) and to \"the eclipse which fell in September of the preceding year\" (September 1587) — both consistent with composition in August 1588, not 1587. The standard editions (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI) date this letter 17 August <b>1588</b>.</li><li><b>Technical terms (glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>Refractio</i> → refraction; <i>Parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>Declinatio maxima</i> → the greatest declination (obliquity of the ecliptic); <i>octaua Sphaera</i> → the eighth sphere (of the fixed stars); <i>Diaphanum</i> → transparency / transparent body (kept as a noun where Tycho substantivizes it, \"the <i>diaphanum</i> of aether\"); <i>Quadrans</i> → quadrant (the <i>rectificatum Quadrantis perpendiculum</i> = the rectified plumb-line of the quadrant); <i>Organa renouata</i> → renewed instruments; <i>pinnacidium / rimulae</i> → sight / its slits (the pinnule-sight; <i>rimulae</i> = the narrow slits, narrowed for bright stars); <i>acronychiae Obseruationes</i> → acronychal observations (at acronychal rising/opposition); <i>Finitor visibilis</i> → the visible horizon (<i>finitor</i>, the bounding circle), kept and glossed. <i>Aequatio dierum</i> → the equation of (the natural) days. <i>restitutio / redintegratio / instauratio Astronomiae</i> → restoration / reintegration / renewal of astronomy — Tycho's programme, rendered with the glossary's fixed family.</li><li><b>Units &amp; measures.</b> <i>Minutum / Scrupulum primum</i> → arc-minute (Tycho uses <i>Minutum</i> and <i>Scrupulum primum</i> interchangeably; <i>Scrupulum</i> alone = arc-minute here); <i>Pars / Gradus</i> → degree; <i>Milliare Germanicum</i> → German mile; <i>Semidiameter Terrae</i> → Earth's semidiameter; <i>quincunx horae</i> → five-twelfths of an hour (the Roman fractional <i>quincunx</i> = 5/12; glossed); <i>triens</i> → a third; <i>digiti</i> (in eclipse) → digits (twelfths of the luminary's diameter). Fractions in the source (7⅓, 7½, 9½, 10⅔, 16⅓, 48⅓, 2⅔) preserved as written.</li><li><b>Astrology / zodiac glyphs (§5.3).</b> ♒ named <b>Aquarius</b>, ♏ <b>Scorpio</b>, ♍ <b>Virgo</b> in the translation; glyphs kept in the Source block. <i>Spica ♍</i> = the star Spica in Virgo (\"the Ear of Corn of Virgo\"). Planet identities in the microcosm passage (Sun ☉, Moon, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn, Mars, Mercury) are written out by Tycho in words (no glyphs in this letter) and so rendered as words.</li><li><b>Alchemy / \"terrestrial astronomy\" (§5.4 register).</b> <i>Astronomia terrestris</i> → \"terrestrial astronomy,\" Tycho's term for alchemy/chymistry, paralleled to <i>Astronomia caelestis</i>. <i>Spagyrica (ars)</i> → the Spagyric art (kept; the Paracelsian term for chymistry). <i>Pyronomica Schola / exercitium Pyronomicum</i> → the Pyronomic (fire-working) School / practice — <i>pyronomia</i> = the regulated handling of fire; kept and glossed, not modernized. <i>Microcosmus</i> → microcosm. <i>Argentum viuum</i> → quicksilver. <i>atra flauaque bilis</i> → black and yellow bile (the humours). The seven-planets/seven-metals/seven-members correspondences are Tycho's, rendered straight.</li><li><b>Latin mottoes (kept and translated).</b> <i>SVSPICIENDO DESPICIO</i> → \"Looking up, I look down\"; <i>DESPICIENDO SVSPICIO</i> → \"Looking down, I look up.\" These are the emblematic mottoes of Tycho's poem for Falk Gøye (the celestial vs. terrestrial philosopher); kept in capitals as printed and glossed.</li><li><b>Hypotheses / authors of theory.</b> <i>Hypotheses Copernianae ad immobilitatem Terrae reductae</i> → \"the Copernican hypotheses reduced to the immobility of the Earth\" — Tycho's geo-heliocentric reworking; <i>calculus Prutenicus</i> → the Prutenic [calculation/tables] (Reinhold's <i>Prutenic Tables</i>); <i>Alphonsinum / Puerbachianum</i> → the Alphonsine [Tables] / the Peurbachian [calculation] (Peurbach); <i>secundus Tomus de nouis Aetherei Mundi Generationibus</i> / <i>liber secundus de Aetherei Mundi recentioribus Phaenomenis</i> → Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), here called \"our second book/volume.\"</li><li><b>Greek.</b> No Greek script appears in this letter; <i>Phaenomenôn</i> (gen. pl.) and <i>Methamorphosin</i> are Latinized Greek loanwords, rendered \"phenomena\" / \"Metamorphosis.\" (The \"Pythagorean silence\" allusion is rendered as such.)</li><li><b>Editorial markers in the Source.</b> The working transcription carried stray superscript-style \"A\" tokens appended to scattered words (apparatus/variant markers, not Latin); these have been removed from the Source block as non-textual editorial artefacts. <b>One correction to that pass:</b> in the <i>Figur 11</i> computation the Earth's-semidiameter SIDE is labelled <i>EA</i> throughout (cf. <i>\"qualium videlicet EA est 60\"</i>, <i>\"prout EA Semidiameter Terrae\"</i>, <i>\"et EA, vt prius 60\"</i>), but in the clause <i>\"qualium EA Semidiameter Terrae part. 60\"</i> the over-eager \"A\"-stripping had reduced this one instance to a bare point <i>E</i>. That was a genuine geometric label, not a variant marker, so the side-label <i>EA</i> has been <b>restored</b> here (and the English correspondingly reads \"the Earth's semidiameter EA\") to keep the figure internally consistent; the numeric argument (= 60 parts) was unaffected. Apart from this restoration, no word of the Latin has been altered. <i>\"Figur 11\"</i> is an in-text figure call-out for the diagram (triangle AEO/AEX with points E, F, O, X) accompanying the trigonometric refraction computation; rendered \"[Figure 11.]\" in brackets.</li><li><b>Negations / conditional logic checked.</b> The dense argument-clauses were translated with the negation count preserved: e.g. <i>non est admodum magna differentia</i> (\"there is no very great difference\"); <i>nequaquam esse aëream</i> (\"by no means airy\"); <i>neque … in 30. Gradu … prorsus nullam fieri Refractionem</i> (Tycho insists he did <i>not</i> say there is <i>no</i> refraction at 30° — a double construction kept intact); <i>quina illa Scrupula in vestra denotatione … abundare, in mea vero nequaquam deficere</i> (\"those five minutes are in <i>excess</i> in your notation, but in mine by no means <i>fall short</i>\" — the asymmetry preserved). The conditional <i>\"si Cometam Anni 1585. fas erat … versari … qui … non etiam concedi poterit …\"</i> is an a-fortiori argument and is rendered as a single conditional question, not flattened.</li><li><b>Soft points flagged (not smoothed).</b> (1) <i>Aëre elementari et animali</i> — Tycho characterizes Rothmann's heaven as \"elemental and <i>animal</i>[ate] air\"; <i>animalis</i> here = \"ensouled/living-air\" (the Stoic-Penan <i>aer animalis</i>), rendered \"animal[ate] air\" with the bracketed gloss rather than modernized. (2) <i>insensibiliter maiores</i> — Tycho says solar refractions here are \"<i>imperceptibly</i> greater\" than at Kassel; the oxymoron (greater yet imperceptibly so) is his own and is preserved, not normalized to \"slightly.\" (3) The arithmetic of the <i>Figur 11</i> triangle (FE 48′50″, semidiameter 60ᵖ, etc.) is reproduced exactly as printed; the values are Tycho's reduction of Rothmann's figure and are not re-derived here.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely and in full; register (technical-expository with the high-courtesy epistolary frame) is matched. The one named crux is the <b>dating discrepancy</b> (source dateline <i>1588</i> vs. dataset label <i>1587</i>), handled per protocol by leading with the as-written date and flagging it rather than silently reconciling; internal eclipse references corroborate 1588. Minor lexical soft points (<i>aer animalis</i>, <i>insensibiliter maiores</i>, the <i>pyronomica/spagyrica</i> register, <i>finitor</i>) are flagged above and rendered with bracketed glosses rather than normalized.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole crux was the 1588-vs-1587 catalogue-label error, decisively resolved by the source year + internal eclipse dates + Dreyer.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (no Dreyer slice available for this letter), so Dreyer collation was not possible and grade is capped at B per the brief regardless of outcome. Audited the full published_english against source_text alone, paragraph by paragraph (13/13 paragraphs present, none dropped, order preserved). Cross-checked every proper name (Gellius Sascerides, Jean Pena, Giordano of Nola/Iordanus Nullanus, Philo Iudaeus, Falk Goye, Paul Wittich, Maestlin, Tubingen, Niels Kaas, Regiomontanus, Vogelin, Peter Apian, Fracastoro, the alchemical-authors list Hermes Trismegistus through Paracelsus, Augsburg), every numeric quantity in the dense refraction/geometry passages (12 German miles/70x semidiameter; the FE/EA/XE triangle figures 48.50/60/9.55/142/860; the AEO triangle 60.48.50/120/30/1.36/23 miles and the 'sixfold' density check; the 20-degree vapour-refraction limit; the 30-degree 1.5-minute solar refraction; the 3-minute/45-degree/1.5-minute proportionality claim; the 6/4-scrupule altitude-1-2-3 degree series; the 3-degree/12-scrupule vs. 28-scrupule solstice dispute; 15 degrees/7⅓/7½ minutes; 10⅔ degrees/9½ minutes; 4½ degrees latitude difference; Augsburg 48⅓ elevation; 16⅓ degrees/25 vs 28 degrees twilight arc; the 1584 Wittich lunar eclipse; the September lunar eclipse England/Cimbric-Chersonese/30-mile report; the March eclipse's half-hour/1⅓-hour/5-twelfths(quincunx)/1-hour-plus deviations from Prutenic/Alphonsine/Peurbachian/Maestlin; the 1581 15 July eclipse's 1⅓-hour overrun and 3-digit/quarter-body Tubingen detail; the 5-scrupule fixed-star longitude discrepancy and its correct 'yours in excess, mine not deficient' directionality; the Falk Goye SUSPICIENDO DESPICIO / DESPICIENDO SUSPICIO motto pairing, correctly matched to the celestial vs terrestrial philosopher figures; the 1585 comet's 3-degree/8-minute/20x-Saturn figure; the 35-degree parallax/1.5-semidiameter figure; the 40-degree/1-day Regiomontanus figure). Checked the closing dateline (17 August 1588, Uraniborg, Tycho Brahe) against the source exactly — no invented day-number. Checked for the pipeline's known failure modes specifically (pronoun flips, fabricated closings, silent sentence drops, invented phrases): none found. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS identified; TEXT_ERROR cannot be assessed without Dreyer. This is an unusually careful, faithful rendering of an exceptionally long and technically dense letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15880611001", "next": "brahe-t_15881013001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15871114001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881013001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses a late reply and, having already sent the second book of *De mundi aetherei* via Gellius Sascerides, asks for Rothmann's frank opinion of its contents.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880817003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880913001", "d": "1588-09-13", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Uraniborg, 13 September 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737, f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AMPLISSIMO NOBILISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO HENRICO RANZOVIO, DOMINO IN BREDENBERGA, ARCIS SEGEBERGAe PRAeSIDI ET REGIO IN HOLSATIA VICARIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>ACCEPI intra paucas hebdomadas ter vel quater a te literas, Nobilissime amplissimeque vir, quibus singulis tuam erga me benevolentiam, et in procuranda papyro impressoria, nostrae officinae typographicae idonea, studium ac diligentiam affatim declaras. Quo nomine et pro typo etiam Sacelli, quod in honorem optimi et nunquam satis laudati Regis nostri, pijssimae memoriae, extrui curasti (quem unâ recepi) est quod gratias tibi habeam maximas. Laudo vero plurimum tuam in hoc erga Regem, optime de omnibus meritum, piam et perpetuam memoriam. Sciotericum horizontale, quod antea promiseram, iam mitto. Accepisses id citius cum GERARDO filio, si is me ante suum ad te abitum, ut sperabam, invisisset. Est autem horarium illud satis diligenter elaboratum, neque aliquid latet vitij, quo minus omnia ad amussim indicabit, quae carmina septena in frontispicio addita pollicentur, modo exquisite ad planum Horizontis, in columna (quod satius est) lapidea firmetur, sicque in ea disponatur, ut linea Meridiei, quae est horae duodecimae, exquisite Meridiano coelesti substernatur, quod qua ratione compendiose et certius quam per Compassum effici potest, exhibitori harum ostendi, qui tibi, modo satis indicationem nostram memoriâ retinuerit, omnia aperiet. Rogo peramanter, velis hoc munusculum boni consulere. Quod ad papyrum attinet, scias eam, quam ultimo misisti, de qua risas 3 ½ (deficiente a dimidia balla, quam te mittere significasti, risa 1 ½) accepi, esse satis idoneam et apprime oportunam meo operi, si modo de hac in aliqua copia obtinere possemus; desidero enim ad minimum praeter eam, quam aliunde conquiro, ballas adhuc plus minus decem, quas, si tua authoritate et procuratione ante hyemem iusto pretio acquisivero, nihil mihi gratius evenire poterit. Et licet haec ipsa, quam ultimo a te accepi, paululum mea priore maior sit, tamen id non tam interest, quam si minor foret; tunc enim esset prorsus inutilis, at nunc extraeminentia illa perexigua facile inter ligandum libros vel alias antea praecidetur. Utinam saltem de tali papyro in ea, qua dixi, copia mature et certo nancisci queam. Dubia illa, quae Clarissimus vir D. CASPAR PEUCERUS et alter ille non vulgaris, ut apparet (licet nomen eius mihi prius non innotuerat) Mathematicus D. GEORGIUS RULLENHAGIUS in mea dispositione revolutionum coelestium tibi per literas indicârunt, admodum facile sunt resolubilia. Nam quantum ad PEUCERUM attinet, nondum satis animadvertebat is, quod quando Sol, qui est centrum circuituum reliquorum Planetarum, deferretur in alteram partem, quae in delineatione inter octavam Sphaeram et orbem ♄ni otiosa videtur, tunc orbes imaginarios errantium Stellarum complere illud spatium eodem modo quo superius, quod nunc in figura vacuum apparet, et sic circumcirca eundo, ob centri annuam cum Sole concomitantiam, requiritur necessario tantum intercapedinis inter octavam Sphaeram et extimum ♄ni orbem, qua parte terris proximus fit, relinqui, ut ascensus et descensus Planetarum, in tota illa annua circumgyratione, capacitatem sine impedimento competentem, invenire possit, idque spatij orbis Solaris diametrum adaequat. Alterius haesitatio adhuc minori negotio resolvitur; id enim nunquam fieri potest, quod RULLENHAGIUS veretur, ut corpora Solis et ♂is concurrant, nam licet orbes fictitij eorum se invicem in delineatione transeant, tamen cum Sol ad illum locum pervenerit, ubi intersectio illa mutua notata apparet, Martius iste orbis Solarem longe alio in loco transibit, et stella ipsa ♂is tantundem a Sole distabit, quantum prius et alias semper, siquidem eum perpetuo in suo circuitu pro centro respicit. Impossibile enim est circumferentiam circuli centro uniri. Habes itaque utraque illorum dubia sufficienter explanata. Cuperem plurimum, ut mecum etiam communicares libros illos Genesium et Praeceptorum, quorum RULLENHAGIUS in principio suarum ad te literarum mentionem facit; ubi eas introspexero perlegeroque, statim remittam, neque alios eorum reddam participes. Quia vero ipsemet luculenter PEUCERO scripsi, et non solum dubitationem eius illi enodavi, sed etiam occasionem inventionis huius novae hypotheseos, unâ cum quibusdam alijs ipsi exposui, mitto earundem literarum ad te exemplar, ut plenius ex ijs, quae ad hanc rem faciant, cognoscere queas. Reddidi etiam illum compotem exemplarium quorundam literarum Illustris. Principis GIUILIELMI Landgravij Hassiae et ipsius Celsis Mathematici, ad me de materia Astronomica datarum, unâ cum mea ad singulas responsione, ut inde plurima ad Astronomicum negotium rite excolendum impense facientia uberius cognoscat. Quod si quae sunt literae inter has, quarum transcriptionem a me prius non obtinuisti (quod procul dubio in ijs, quae ultimo inter nos cis et ultra missae sunt, contingit) licebit utique aperto fasciculo eos inspicere perlegereque, aut etiam transcribi, si tibi ita visum fuerit, curare, modo postea rursus eodem modo componantur, tuoque sigillo denuo muniantur. Valeat tua Generositas cum uxore et liberis totaque domo quam felicissime. Nec dubito quin nervos omnes intensurus sis, ut papyrum illam mature et satis copiosam obtineam, dabo operam, ut in nominis tui, alias per se clari, celebratione, siquidem aliud praestare nequeam, me gratissimum et tui studiosissimum invenias.</p><p>Iterum et terque quaterque vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 13. Septembris Anno 1588.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp. 126–127; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Quia vero expositio et declaratio mearum hypothesium e sola delineatione non a quovis ita facile percipitur, quoddam Mechanicum Organum confieri ex orichalco curavi, quo circuitus Planetarum respectu Solis, et ipsius Solis ac Lunae circa Terram manibus circumagi expeditiusque intelligi possunt, per quod etiam dubia illa tum PEUCERI tum etiam RULLENHAGIJ facillime extricantur, quemadmodum harum exhibitor (modo caput iuridicum sacra Uraniae sibi diligenter satis capere et memoria retinere queat, quod optarem quidem, sperare autem vix possum) tibi plenius ostendet; mitto enim tibi per eundem eiusdem Aequatorij Ideam, quam ut boni consulas rogo. Si GERARDUS filius tuus huc venisset, per unam saltem vel alteram horam, antequam iter suum ad te direxit, utique tum haec, tum alia ipsi melius et perfectius tibi referenda (cum sit ingeniosulus) declarassem. Petij ter vel quater idipsum serio ab eo per literas, verum is semper negotia (nescio quae) praetendebat. At nihilominus tantum illi suppetebat otij, ut ijsdem diebus nuptijs Landskronensibus, nihil ad se pertinentibus, interesse potuerit, et Huena exquisite in medio directi itineris a Croneburgo Landscroniam versus aut econtra iacet, ita ut vel in abitu aut reditu me sine ulla molestia tuo nomine invisere potuisset, modo voluntas promta non defuisset, quod me tibi per literas indicaturum, OSWALDO, ut illi idipsum significaret, iniunxi. Id igitur nunc fecisse oportuit. Iterum vale. Tui Hodierni. Tycho Brahe.  /  Mitto adhuc bina Exemplaria libri nostri secundi de aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis, et puto te iam septena accepisse, quod ideo refero, ut in memoria retineatur, quibusnam singula communicata sint, ut illi ijdem reliquas etiam operis partes aliquando adipiscantur.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most worthy and noble gentleman, Master Heinrich Rantzau, lord in Breitenburg, governor of the castle of Segeberg and royal viceroy in Holstein, his friend to be honoured above all.</b></p><p>Within these few weeks I have received from you three or four letters, most noble and worthy sir, in each of which you abundantly declare your goodwill towards me and your zeal and diligence in procuring printing-paper suitable for our typographical workshop. On that account, and also for the engraving [<i>typus</i>] of the chapel which you had built in honour of our best and never sufficiently praised King, of most pious memory (which I received together [with the letters]), I have cause to render you the greatest thanks. Indeed I greatly commend in this your pious and perpetual remembrance towards the King, who deserved most well of all. The horizontal sundial [<i>sciotericum horizontale</i>] which I had earlier promised I am now sending. You would have received it sooner by way of your son Gerhard, had he, before his departure to you, visited me as I had hoped. Now that timepiece is wrought with sufficient care, nor does any defect lie hidden in it that would keep it from indicating everything exactly [<i>ad amussim</i>], as the seven verses added on its face promise — provided only that it be fixed precisely to the plane of the horizon, upon a stone column (which is better), and so arranged upon it that the line of midday, which is that of the twelfth hour, be laid exactly under the celestial meridian; by what method this can be effected concisely and more surely than by a compass [<i>Compassus</i>], I have shown to the bearer of this letter, who will explain everything to you, provided he has retained our instruction well enough in memory. I most affectionately ask that you would be willing to take this little gift in good part. As for the paper: know that the [paper] which you last sent — of which I received 3½ reams [<i>risae</i>] (the half-bale [<i>balla</i>] short, which you indicated you were sending, being 1½ reams) — is sufficiently suitable and exceedingly well-fitted to my work, if only we could obtain some quantity of this kind; for I need, at the least, besides that which I am procuring from elsewhere, still about ten bales more, and if by your authority and agency I shall acquire these before winter at a fair price, nothing more welcome could befall me. And although this very paper which I last received from you is a little larger than my earlier [stock], yet that matters not so much as if it were smaller; for then it would be wholly useless, whereas now that very slight overhang [<i>extraeminentia</i>], being exceedingly small, will easily be trimmed off beforehand in the binding of the books or otherwise. Would that I might at least obtain such paper, in the quantity I have said, in good time and for certain. Those doubts which the most distinguished gentleman Master Caspar Peucer, and that other mathematician — no common one, as it appears (though his name had not previously become known to me) — Master Georg Rollenhagen indicated to you by letter concerning my arrangement of the celestial revolutions, are very easily resolved. For as far as Peucer is concerned, he had not yet sufficiently observed that, when the Sun — which is the centre of the circuits of the remaining planets — should be carried into the other part, which in the diagram [<i>delineatio</i>] appears idle [<i>otiosa</i>] between the eighth sphere and the orb of Saturn [♄], then the imaginary orbs of the wandering stars fill that space in the same way as above [in the part] which now appears empty in the figure; and so, going round about, on account of the centre's yearly accompanying of the Sun, it is necessarily required that only so much interval be left between the eighth sphere and the outermost orb of Saturn [♄], in that part where it becomes nearest to the Earth, as will allow the ascent and descent of the planets, in that whole yearly revolution, to find a fitting capacity without impediment; and that [interval of] space equals the diameter of the Sun's orb. The other man's hesitation is resolved with even less trouble; for that which Rollenhagen fears can never happen — namely, that the bodies of the Sun and of Mars [♂] should come together — for although their fictitious orbs cross one another in the diagram, yet when the Sun shall have arrived at that place where that mutual intersection appears marked, that orb of Mars will pass [through] the Sun's [orb] at a place far otherwise [located], and the body of Mars [♂] itself will be just as far distant from the Sun as before and at all other times, since indeed it perpetually regards the Sun as the centre in its circuit. For it is impossible that the circumference of a circle be united with its centre. You have therefore both of their doubts sufficiently explained. I should very much wish that you would also share with me those books of Genitures and of Precepts of which Rollenhagen makes mention at the beginning of his letters to you; once I have examined and read them through, I shall return them at once, nor make any others sharers of them. But because I myself have written clearly to Peucer, and have not only unravelled his doubt for him but have also set out for him the occasion of the discovery of this new hypothesis, together with certain other matters, I am sending you a copy of that same letter, that you may more fully learn from it the things that bear upon this matter. I have also put him [Peucer] in possession of copies of certain letters of the Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, and of His Highness's mathematician, written to me on an astronomical subject, together with my reply to each, so that from them he may more amply learn the very many things that bear earnestly upon the duly cultivating of the astronomical enterprise. And if there are any letters among these whose transcription you did not previously obtain from me (which without doubt occurs among those which were last sent between us, hither and thither), it will indeed be permitted to open the packet, to inspect and read them through, or even to have them transcribed, if it shall so seem good to you — provided that afterwards they be put together again in the same manner and secured anew with your seal. May Your Generosity, with your wife and children and your whole household, fare most happily. Nor do I doubt that you will strain every sinew so that I may obtain that paper in good time and in sufficient plenty; I shall take pains that, in the celebration of your name (otherwise illustrious of itself) — since I can render nothing else — you may find me most grateful and most devoted to you.</p><p>Again, and thrice and four times over, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 13th day of September, in the year 1588.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> But because the exposition and explanation of my hypotheses is not so easily grasped by everyone from a drawing alone, I have had a certain Mechanical Instrument [Organum] made of brass, by which the circuits of the Planets with respect to the Sun, and of the Sun itself and the Moon about the Earth, can be turned round by hand and the more readily understood; by which too those doubts both of Peucer and of Rollenhagen are most easily disentangled, as the bearer of this letter will more fully show you (provided only that his lawyer's head can take in the mysteries of Urania carefully enough and hold them in memory — which I should indeed wish, but can scarcely hope). For I send you by the same man a design of the same Equatorium, which I ask that you take in good part. Had your son Gerhard come hither, even for one hour or two, before he directed his journey to you, I should assuredly have made these things, and others to be reported to you, clearer and more perfectly to him (since he is rather clever). I asked the same of him seriously three or four times by letter, but he was always pleading affairs (I know not what). And yet he had leisure enough to spare to be present in those same days at the Landskrona wedding, which in no way concerned him; and Hven lies precisely in the middle of the direct route from Kronborg towards Landskrona or the reverse, so that either in going or returning he could have visited me in your name without any trouble, had only a ready will not been wanting — which I had enjoined upon Oswald to signify to him, that I would indicate it to you by letter. This therefore it behoved [me] now to have done. Again, farewell. Yours of today. Tycho Brahe.  /  I send besides two copies of our second book on the recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World, and I think you have by now received seven, which I mention for this reason: that it be kept in memory to whom the several copies have been communicated, so that those same persons may at some time obtain the remaining parts of the work also.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe to <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598; Latin <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i>), the great Holstein humanist, royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i> / <i>regius vicarius</i>) of the duchies under the Danish crown, lord of Breitenburg (<i>Bredenberga</i>) and Segeberg, owner of a celebrated library and his own printing connections. He was Tycho's most important patron-correspondent in the Empire and a key supplier of paper for the Uraniborg press; the salutation's full titulature is preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Date / occasion.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 13. Septembris Anno 1588</i> — Uraniborg, 13 September 1588 (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline matches the catalogue label exactly; no discrepancy. The letter falls in the year Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (vol. II of his projected trilogy, printed at Uraniborg) appeared, presenting the geocentric-heliocentric world-system whose geometry is defended below.</li><li><b><i>typus Sacelli</b></i> — \"the engraving of the chapel.\" <i>typus</i> here is a printed/engraved representation (an <i>image</i>, not movable type); Rantzau had had built (and engraved) a memorial chapel honouring the late King <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (d. 11 April 1588), the \"best and never sufficiently praised King, of most pious memory,\" Tycho's patron. Translated \"engraving.\"</li><li><b><i>Sciotericum horizontale / horarium</b></i> — a <b>horizontal sundial</b> (Gk. <i>skiothērikon</i>, shadow-instrument); <i>horarium</i> \"timepiece.\" Rendered \"horizontal sundial,\" consistent with §5.2 instrument register. <i>carmina septena in frontispicio</i> — \"seven verses on its face/frontispiece,\" an inscribed motto-poem on the dial-plate.</li><li><b><i>ad amussim</b></i> — \"exactly, to the line\" (lit. by the carpenter's rule/plumb-line); idiom kept and glossed.</li><li><b><i>per Compassum</b></i> — \"by a compass.\" Here, in the context of orienting the dial's noon-line to the celestial meridian, this is a <b>magnetic compass</b> (for alignment), not a pair of dividers; Tycho says his shown method is surer than relying on the (magnetically-deviating) compass. Distinguished from the drawing-compass crux of the 1584 Frederik II letter; the referent here is reasonably secure from context.</li><li><b>Paper units.</b> <i>risa</i> = <b>ream</b> (Ital./Lat. <i>risma</i>); <i>balla</i> = <b>bale</b> (a large bundle of reams). Tycho reports receiving 3½ reams, the consignment being 1½ reams short of the half-bale Rantzau had announced, and asks for about ten more bales for the press. Units kept in the source and glossed; the slightly <b>larger</b> sheet-size is welcome because the overhang (<i>extraeminentia</i>) can be trimmed in binding, whereas an undersized sheet would be useless.</li><li><b>Persons (objections to the world-system).</b> <i>D. CASPAR PEUCERUS</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Melanchthon's son-in-law, Wittenberg physician-astronomer. <i>D. GEORGIUS RULLENHAGIUS</i> = <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), Magdeburg rector, poet (<i>Froschmeuseler</i>) and amateur mathematician; Tycho notes his name was previously unknown to him. Both had relayed objections, through Rantzau, to Tycho's <i>dispositio revolutionum coelestium</i> (his diagram of the world-system in <i>De mundi</i>).</li><li><b>The geometry of the world-system (technical core).</b> Two objections are answered:</li><li><i>Peucer's</i>: the region of the diagram \"between the eighth sphere and the orb of Saturn\" looks <b>idle/empty</b> (<i>otiosa … vacuum</i>). Tycho replies that because the Sun is the moving centre of the planets' circuits and travels yearly round the Earth, the planets' imaginary orbs sweep through that apparently-empty space too as the Sun moves to the opposite side; the gap that must be left between Saturn's outermost orb (at its nearest approach to Earth) and the eighth (fixed-star) sphere equals <b>the diameter of the Sun's orb</b> — exactly enough room for the planets' annual ascent and descent.</li><li><i>Rollenhagen's</i>: he feared the <b>bodies of the Sun and Mars would collide</b> (<i>corpora Solis et ♂is concurrant</i>) where their circles cross in the figure. Tycho answers that the orbs are <i>fictitious / geometrical</i> (<i>orbes fictitij … in delineatione</i>), not solid; when the Sun reaches the marked intersection, Mars's orb crosses the Sun's <i>orb</i> elsewhere and Mars's <i>body</i> stays as far from the Sun as ever, since Mars always keeps the Sun as the centre of its circuit — \"for it is impossible that the circumference of a circle be united with its centre.\" This reading is corroborated by Dreyer (<i>Tycho Brahe</i>) and Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) and Christianson (<i>On Tycho's Island / Lord of Uraniborg</i>), who record precisely this Rollenhagen \"collision\" worry and Tycho's no-solid-spheres rebuttal.</li><li><b>Glyphs.</b> ♄ = <b>Saturn</b>, ♂ = <b>Mars</b> — named in English in the translation, the glyph kept in the source block per protocol §5.3.</li><li><b><i>libri Genesium et Praeceptorum</b></i> — \"books of Genitures [nativity-figures] and of Precepts,\" astrological works Rollenhagen had cited; <i>Genesis</i> = <b>geniture/nativity</b> per §5.3. Tycho asks to borrow them.</li><li><b><i>Illustris. Principis GIUILIELMI Landgravij Hassiae et ipsius Celsis Mathematici</b></i> — <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> and his court mathematician (Christoph Rothmann); Tycho is circulating the Kassel–Uraniborg astronomical correspondence. <i>GIUILIELMI</i> is the source's spelling of <i>Gulielmi/Wilhelmi</i>, kept as printed.</li><li><b><i>Gerardo filio</b></i> — <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (1558–1627), Heinrich's son and eventual successor as royal governor of the duchies.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Iterum et terque quaterque vale</i> — \"Again, and thrice and four times over, farewell\" (a Virgilian <i>terque quaterque</i> flourish), kept literally.</li><li><b>Edition / witness.</b> Edition of record: Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> (vol. VII, <i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness as given in the dataset: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 9737, f. (folio number not supplied in the source header).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> TWO closing postscripts (the brass equatorium <i>Mechanicum Organum</i> sent via the bearer + the Gerhard/Kronborg–Landskrona logistics; then two more copies of the <i>De mundi aetherei</i> book), restored from Dreyer VII pp.126–127 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII p.126: the sentence 'Reddidi etiam illum compotem ... cognoscat' is grammatically 3rd person, continuing the Peucer clauses of the prior sentence, not addressed to Rantzau (who is 'te'/'tu' throughout, including the next sentence's 'obtinuisti'). The published translation's bracketed gloss '[Rantzau]' misattributed custody of the Landgrave Wilhelm/Rothmann correspondence copies; corrected to '[Peucer]'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The technical world-system passage is dense but unambiguous and externally corroborated (Dreyer/Mosley/Christianson); the only points kept open rather than decided are minor and flagged in the apparatus (the <i>Compassus</i> referent — read here as a magnetic compass from the dial-orientation context — and the unspecified folio of the Vienna witness), neither of which affects the sense of the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.124-128 as page images (Read pages=1-4; the letter's own text is complete within pp.124(bottom)-127(top), so slice p.5/TBOO p.128, which opens with a wholly different letter to Peucer, was not needed). Collated the full letter - opening address block (AMPLISSIMO NOBILISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO HENRICO RANZOVIO...), every proper name (Gerardus filius, Caspar Peucerus, Georgius Rullenhagius, Oswaldus), every number (risas 3½, deficiente balla risa 1½, ballas decem, bina Exemplaria, septena), the postscript on the brass Equatorium/Organum, and the closing date (Datae Uraniburgi die 13. Septembris Anno 1588) - against the Dreyer images word for word; all match source_text. The previously flagged sentence (Reddidi etiam illum compotem...) was verified word-for-word against Dreyer p.126 and confirms the source_text is textually correct (no TEXT_ERROR) - the fault is in the English's referent-gloss, a MISTRANSLATION that flips custody of the Landgrave correspondence from Peucer to Rantzau, which is exactly the kind of claim a scholar citing this letter for the provenance of that correspondence would rely on and get wrong.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). The Latin's illum (accusative 'him') and the 3rd-person subjunctive cognoscat ('that HE may learn') continue the immediately preceding sentence, which is entirely about Caspar Peucer (Quia vero ipsemet luculenter PEUCERO scripsi... illi enodavi... ipsi exposui) - not the letter's addressee Rantzau, who is addressed throughout in the 2nd person (te/tu), including the very next sentence's obtinuisti ('you have not obtained'). The published English glosses illum as '[Rantzau]', inverting the custody: it was Peucer, not Rantzau, whom Tycho made possessor of the Landgrave-Wilhelm correspondence copies so that Peucer could learn more. This matches the pipeline's known failure mode of misattributing custody of a correspondence archive. At: “I have also put him [Rantzau] in possession of copies of certain letters of the Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VII p.126 (letter spans pp.124-128; pages 124-127 examined, encompassing the full defect passage). The sentence in question uses the 3rd-person accusative 'illum' and 3rd-person subjunctive 'cognoscat', sandwiched between two clearly 2nd-person sentences addressed to Rantzau ('cognoscere queas' before it, 'obtinuisti' after it) and immediately following three consecutive 3rd-person references to Peucer ('PEUCERO scripsi', 'illi enodavi', 'ipsi exposui'). This confirms Peucer, not Rantzau, is the antecedent of 'illum'/'cognoscat', and the correction is grammatically secure. The Latin text itself is left untouched as instructed for a MISTRANSLATION defect (unflagged minor transcription variants, e.g. 'Illustris.'/'GIUILIELMI' vs Dreyer's 'Illustriss.'/'GUILIELMI', were noted but not touched, being out of scope for this defect).</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15871019001", "next": "brahe-t_15881221001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Rantzau for procuring printing paper and for the chapel engraving honouring the late King, and sends the promised horizontal sundial, delayed because Gerhard Rantzau departed without visiting.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880913001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880913002", "d": "1588-09-13", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Caspar Peucer", "ck": "Caspar Peucer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Caspar Peucer — Uraniborg, 13 September 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal/astrological glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>); also in P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>· MS witnesses: A = Mikulov (Nikolsburg), II 72 (Afskrift/copy); B = Paris, Bibliothèque Nationale, Français 13040, fol. 162 (Afskrift)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 17 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p><p><b>Editorial note.</b> A foundational document — Tycho's first long letter to <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (earlier than the undated reply of 1590), and the <b>locus classicus of the autobiographical genesis of the Tychonic world-system</b>: how, from a Leipzig boyhood with the cross-staff, through the deficiencies of both the Ptolemaic/Alphonsine and Copernican hypotheses, the <b>Mars-parallax test of 1582</b> and the <b>abandonment of the solid orbs</b> (from the comets) led him to his geo-heliocentric arrangement. It also lays out the <b>7-book plan</b> of his great Astronomical work, describes <b>Uraniborg and Stjerneborg</b>, casts (from old notes) <b>Peucer's own horoscope</b>, refutes <b>Francisco Valles</b>, and treats the Isaiah/Job fluid-heavens exegesis (overlapping the undated 1590 Peucer letter) and the Mars-parallax/system content (overlapping the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590, the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, and the Hájek letter of 3 Aug 1590). Two declared interventions in the source block: (1) the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> (footnote-anchors to the witnesses) on ~90 words are removed as <b>non-textual</b> (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, and from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588); (2) a genuine <b>lacuna/anacoluthon</b> in the source (\"…circa Solem annua per totos orbes circumgyratione …\" before \"Habuit etiam quidam alius Mathematicus\") is marked <i>…</i> and flagged. Greek (<i>προσθαφαιρέσεων</i> = prosthaphaereses; <i>Phaenomeνων</i> = φαινομένων) and the glyphs (☉ ☾ ☿ ♀ ♂ ♃ ♄ ♋ ♑ ♊ ♌; □ = square, △ = trine, ⚹ = sextile; M.C. = Medium Coeli) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"Uraniburgi die 13. Septembris Anno 1588\"</i>) is <b>Old Style</b> (Danish); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VII pp. 127–141</b> (running head \"TYCHO AD CASPARUM PEUCERUM 13 SEPT. 1588\"). <b>All cruxes confirmed</b>: the solar parameters — apogee at the end of 6° Cancer, eccentricity <b>2° 9⅓′</b> (the contested ⅓ fraction read unambiguously at 12× zoom, p. 140); the <b>7-book plan</b> (count and contents/order, p. 132); Peucer's horoscope (☿ in Capricorn 3rd house; ☾ in Gemini 9th; ♂ lord of 7th in 12th; liberation when the ☾-direction reaches ⚹♂ at <b>14° Leo</b> in the M.C., \"iuxta aetatis annum <b>60</b>,\" pp. 137–138); the Uraniborg/Stjerneborg numerals (24-yrs' observations, \"plura quam 20\" instruments, the 7-crypt subterranean observatory, p. 139–140); and the Valles refutation — Dreyer genuinely prints <b>\"FRANCICUS VALLESIUS\"</b> (sic, for Franciscus), exactly as the .md flags (p. 133).</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE CASPARO PEUCERO.<br>SALUTEM P. D.</p><p>HABEBAM iam dudum in votis tecum vel  coram, cum Vitebergam<br>olim  transirem, vel  etiam postea absens per literas amicitiam pergratam contrahere, vir<br>doctrina varia et virtute excellenti celeberrime; verum cum commoditas nulla<br>satis opportune id efficiendi interea sese obtulisset, coactus sum in præsens<br>usque tempus idipsum differre. Occasionem vero diutinum hoc meum desiderium iam<br>tandem aliqua ex parte complendi, dederunt literæ amplissimi et nobilissimi viri<br>D. HENRICI RANZOVIJ,Vicarij Regij<br>in Holsatia, Mathematicarum rerum amplissimi. Addebatur  enim illis transscriptio  quædam eorum, quæ de nova mea inventione dispositionis revolutionum<br>cœlestium ipsi nuper transmiseras; rogavitque simul is, ut dubijs tuis  circa delineationem tibi a se communicatam mature occurrere vellem, quod<br>etiam ut in utriusque vestrum gratiam  præstarem, has ad te literas dare non intermittendum duxi, quibus, ut plenius tuæ expectationi  et dubiorum resolutioni satisfiat , paulo altius ab ipsa videlicet prima occasione inventionis huius<br>hypotheseos ordiendum et aliquanto diffusius de hac materia disserendum video,<br>idque nunc aggrediar. Cum ab ineunte ætate inter alias Philosophiæ partes<br>Astronomico studio, vel invitis vel  reluctantibus ipsis pædagogis, essem impense naturæ quodam instinctu<br>addictus, et statim in pueritia Lipsiæ<br>studiorum gratia commoratus annumque ætatis agens decimum septimum per Radium<br>Astronomicum Planetarum cursus a fixis sideribus sæpius serenis noctibus<br>rimarer , animadverti, eorum apparentias ne  Coperniano quidem calculo satis præcise congruere, nedum Alphonsino, qui<br>iam pæne obsoleverat . Cœpi exinde diu multumque mecum pensitare, unde hæc a cœlo ipso tam<br>evidens discrepantia oboriretur , an vero ob  observationum ab artificibus non rite institutarum, an etiam ob  hypothesium assumptarum insufficientiam et falsitatem. Cumque aliquot annis hanc rem varie revolverem, accrescente iam ætate et<br>iudicio unâ cum ea magis magisque maturescente, utrasque  tam Alphonsinas sive Ptolemaicas hypotheses quam Copernianas diligentius<br>inspexi, et in earum ratiocinationes penitius disquisivi, tandemque animadverti,<br>in veteribus illis hypothesibus, inde a Ptolomæo eiusque quibusdam antecessoribus usque ad nos deductis,<br>utut magna ex parte apparentias cœlestes excusarent , tamen quod motus circularis regularitatem non circa proprium sed alienum<br>quoddam centrum fieri admitterent, contra prima artis principia peccare, quod<br>ipse etiam COPERNICUS in hisce culpasse<br>videtur; præterea epicyclos tot et tantos, qui ibidem etiam assumuntur, multum<br>spatij in cœlo occupare et superfluos esse. Si paucioribus res omnis absolvi<br>posset, iudicavi , magnumque de his scrupulum mihi iniecit, quod nulla necessaria causa vel  combinatio naturalis subesset, cur  superiores Planetæ Soli ita alligarentur, ut huic<br>coniuncti summitatem epicyclorum semper occuparent, in infimo vero eorundem loco<br>eidem opponerentur, et bini, quos inferiores vocant, semper eundem medium locum<br>cum Sole sortirentur illique in Apogæo et Perigæo epicyclorum copularentur. Hæc,<br>inquam, non colligere licuit habere certam et evidentem combinationis rationem,<br>siquidem tanto intervallo ab invicem distarent hæc sidera, adeo ut, hæc<br>concatenatio mutua si ad eum præcise modum fieret, necessitatem aliquam<br>competentem subesse, ut omnia in ea ita evenirent, mihi minus apparuerit<br>consentaneum; at potius vel hanc solam ob causam in his assumptionibus coactum<br>quid, dissonum et supervacaneum, latere non citra rem suspicabar. In altera vero<br>neoterica summi illius COPERNICI distributione absurditas circa triplicem<br>telluris motum statim absterruit , in primis vero quo  ad annuam illam  centri et axis declinationem in partes contrarias. Licet enim reliquis,<br>quæ in Ptolemaica dispositione discohærentia et superflua<br>inveniuntur, opportune mederetur, nihilque contra Mathematica principia<br>delinqueret, tamen cum  iam regularem perfectum minusque intricatum motum Terræ, pigro et<br>ignobiliori corpori, attribueret, non parum suspecta reddebatur hæc assumptio,<br>præsertim cum sacræ literæ non paucis in locis illi aperte reclamitent . Hinc de utraque harum hypothesium non parum dubitavi, an  aliqua earum recte se haberet. Cum vero nulla alia daretur, quæ<br>apparentijs cœlestibus consona foret, et homocentricorum a quibusdam excogitatum figmentum nimis violentum esset, perque multorum<br>orbium inextricabilem hinc inde retractionem absurdum simul, imo ridiculum<br>deprehenderetur, nec etiam ipsis apparentijs in accessu et recessu Planetarum ad<br>Terram satisfaceret, idcirco his dubijs diu implicatus, quod nulla statim<br>succurreret (ut dixi) hypothesium innovatio, quæ his omnibus convenientijs<br>subveniret, constitui periculum saltem facere, quænam earum,<br>quæ hactenus inventæ erant , veritati propius  accederet. Idque per Martis potissimum stellam me expiscari posse<br>confidebam, quando  nimirum ea  acronychia Terræ appropinquaret; si enim tunc nobis propius accederet, quam  ipse Soli Copernianam speculationem prævalere, sin minus, tum Ptolemaica<br>potius standum arbitrabar. Tandemque magna diligentia, nec parvis sumtibus<br>conquisitis varijs organis Astronomicis, quibus siderum motus centraliter non<br>saltem in ipso scrupulo, sed etiam in eius dimidia vel  quarta parte explorari certo possent, hanc subtilem considerationem<br>aggredi non verebar, idque potissimum anno 1582, cum Mars pernox Soli factus<br>opponeretur in Cancro, et insuper per boream latitudinem sublimior quotidiano<br>circuitu redderetur. Habitis itaque tum pluribus accuratis observationibus tam<br>circa ortum quam occasum, eiusque per meridianum transitum, deprehendi Martem<br>maiorem causari parallaxin quam ipsum Solem, ideoque etiam<br>terris propinquiorem, cum acronychius est, fieri, astipulante unâ motu ipsius<br>diurno, cum Copernianis potius numeris consentiente, eo quod paulo celerius<br>certo dierum intervallo in antecedentia repedaret, quam Alphonsinorum concederet<br>a PTOLOMÆO deducta ratiocinatio, idque ob minorem a terris distantiam, quæ  motum paulo intentiorem apparere efficiebat. Id quum in  ♂ satis exploratum haberem, et idem etiam in ♀ aliquoties a<br>me interdiu adeoque in ipso meridiano observata et iuxta occasum collatione<br>facta, imo et alia quadam peculiari ratione anno 1587 circa diem 24 Februarij,<br>quando inter unum vel alterum  diem eam tam vespertinam quam matutinam (quod alias  rarissimum est) vidi, motum apparentem cum eo, qui  ratione parallaxeos fieret, conferendo comprobassem, ita ut Ptolemaicis<br>hypothesibus hæc omnia minime congruerent, impellebar, ut postmodum magis<br>magisque Copernianæ inventioni fidem attribuerem . Obstabat nihilominus plurimum circa Terræ revolutionem tam ordinariam et<br>perpetuam nimia absurditas, et sacrarum insuper literarum contrarium astruentium<br>irrefragabilis authoritas. Quare hoc negotium hinc inde varie revolvens de alia<br>hypothesium ordinatione mecum studiose deliberavi. Rem primo impossibilem moliri<br>visus sum; at tandem fere ex insperato succurrebat, si Sol centrum quinque<br>Planetarum constitueretur et nihilominus circa Terram, in medio universo<br>quiescentem, annuatim, concomitantibus  per centrum ideoque et ipsam  circumferentiam<br>reliquorum quinque Planetarum circuitibus, convolveretur, ut Terra centrum<br>præberet revolutionibus saltem Solis et Lunæ cæteraque omnia includentis  altissimæ octavæ sphæræ, si, inquam, eo modo revolutiones cœlestes<br>ordinarentur, tunc omnia, quæ in Ptolemaica et Coperniana assumptione absona et<br>irrita supervacaneaque incidunt , tolli et præcaveri. Sed nihilominus adhuc scrupulum iniecit, quod orbis<br>Martius respectu  Solaris tantus non inveniretur, ut eum totaliter includere posset, verum<br>duobus in locis necessario ipsum penetraret. Eram enim adhuc diu recepta et ab<br>omnibus fere approbata opinione imbutus, cœlum esse quibusdam realibus orbibus,<br>sidera circumferentibus, refertum, ideoque hanc orbium incongruam penetrationem<br>non admittendam duxi, indeque evenit, ut hæc propria inventio mihi ipsi<br>aliquandiu suspecta fuerit. Tandem vero cum ex quorundam Cometarum accurata  circa motum et parallaxes eorum examinatione certo exploratum haberem, eos  in ipso cœlo longe supra Lunam cursus sui normam absolvisse et<br>nihilominus nullis corporeis et realibus orbibus, quibus Planetæ vehi creduntur,<br>fuisse obnoxios, sed  peculiarem quandam ab his motus rationem invenisse, ut latius et<br>exquisitius in opere nostro de his asciticijs cœli<br>phænomenis demonstratur, præsumptionem illam de orbium cœlestium dura et<br>impervia materia prorsus abieci. Accessit et hoc, quod duo etiam Cometæ, qui<br>iuxta oppositum Solis ferebantur, satis evidenter ostenderint, Terram annuatim<br>revera non convolvi, siquidem huius commutatio non detraxit quidpiam eorum<br>prædefinito et proportionabili motui, ut in Planetis, qui ob id  retroagi a COPERNICO existimantur, usu venit. Tandem itaque utrisque<br>fundamentis, tam Copernianis quam Ptolemaicis, ab ipsa experientia eversis,<br>neotericam hanc inventionem citra omne dubium recte se habere animo confirmabar,<br>præsertim quod perspicerem e Cometis, ut dixi, cœlum liquidissima et propria<br>substantia constare nullisque orbibus duris et realibus, ut tot iam seculis<br>falso creditum est, abundare, ut  ob id nihil obstaculi ingerat, quod Martis imaginarius orbis Solarem non<br>totaliter includat, sed duobus in locis intersecet, siquidem orbes isti<br>corporaliter cœlo non  insunt, sed tamen ex motu siderum<br>fieri intelliguntur. Contuli mox intra quadriennium proximum exactiores<br>apparentias in motu quinque Planetarum cum hac nostra hypothesi, et animadverti<br>eas quam apertissime  illi congruere, veterumque unâ observationibus abunde satisfacere; imo<br>quiddam aliud in hac latere, quod peculiarem quandam inæqualitatis apparentis<br>occationem præsertim in motu Martis (ob Solaris orbis ad eius circuitum admodum<br>sensibilem et magnam comparationem) excusaret, quæ per antecessorum assumptiones<br>nequaquam salvari posset, ut ob id aliqua ex parte cursus præsertim Martis<br>prorsus inexploratus hactenus manserit. Quod et  a veteribus animadversum est, siquidem PLINIUS ipsum sidus esse inobservabile affirmare non dubitat.<br>Quapropter nihil amplius hæsitans, meam neotericam inventionem undiquaque recte<br>constare, nihil absoni vel Mathematice vel Physice suggerere, illi soli<br>revolutionum cœlestium redintegrationem ex observationibus<br>veterum et nostris superstruere in animum induxi, attamen eius probationem in<br>opus nostrum Astronomicum, quod de totius artis instauratione elaborare, favente<br>divino auxilio, decrevi, differendam  censui. Verum cum Cometa anni 77, qui se etiam circa Solem non minus quam<br>Planetæ ipsi convolvebat, me invitasset, ac, dum  ipsius apparentias per competentem hypothesin salvare elaboro, huius in<br>mundana compagine hypotyposis ratiocinationem iudicatione<br>generaliori  patefacerem usque in operis Astronomici promulgationem, mutata sententia,<br>rem omnem non differendam consultius iudicavi. Cum autem  prædicto nobilissimo HENRICO RANZOVIO, amico meo singulari, iuxta ipsius<br>petitionem aliquot exemplaria secundæ partis operis nostri de recentioribus<br>ætherei Mundi Phænomenis communicassem, intelligo illum delineationem huius<br>hypotheseos, quæ capite 8 ibidem reperitur, tibi et alijs quibusdam eruditis in Germania excerptam transmisisse, neglecta tamen eiusdem,<br>quæ illis addebatur, succincta explicatione, quam si unâ adiunxisset, utique nec<br>tu nec alij in tam facile resolubilia circa hanc  ratiocinationem dubia incidissetis , quæ postea minimo labore expediam. Mitto autem tibi hunc eundem librum,<br>ex quo nobilissimus RANZOVIUS hanc hypotheseos designationem desumpsit, qui de<br>Cometa anni 77 luculenter tractat. Estque operis eius, de quo dixi, secundus,<br>nam primus de admiranda  illa ad Cassiopæam anno 72 exorta Nova Stella affatim agit, ubi quædam<br>præmittuntur, quæ toti arti instaurandæ fundamenta ponunt, qualia sunt, quæ de<br>Solaris cursus ad nostra tempora exacta per certas observationes,<br>demonstrationes restitutione  deque affixarum stellarum exquisita certificatione proponuntur. In eodem<br>quoque libro aliorum  circa hanc stellam observationes et placita sub incudem veritatis<br>revocantur, unâque tuæ olim Vitebergæ<br>habitæ, sed instrumentis minus idoneis, animadversiones.<br>In tertio tomo de alijs tribus quibusdam Cometis post observatis mea inventa<br>decisionesque expono et cum aliorum in ijsdem placitis confero, disquisitione in<br>singulis adhibita diligenti, quibus omnibus tandem subiunguntur quædam magna<br>corollaria, quæ ex illis derivari possunt, qualia sunt: cœlum non constare ex<br>orbibus realibus et impervia  substantia, nullos dari revera epicyclos, Terram annuatim nullatenus<br>convolvi, ut voluit COPERNICUS, et nostram hypothesium  cœlestium ordinationem rei veritati omni ex parte congruere, nullamque<br>aliam dari, quæ cunctis requisitis sufficiat . Taliter vero per hos tres  neotericorum et<br>ascititiorum in cœlo phænomenorum libros viam sternere et fundamenta ponere<br>volui ad opus illud, quod perpetuorum et mundo coævorum siderum revolutionibus<br>ex ipso cœlo, faciente ipsius cœli Opifice, restituendis inserviet. Ideoque hanc peregrinarum et secundariarum stellarum descriptionem<br>præmittendam consulto iudicavi, idque non saltem ob horum ascititiorum siderum<br>situm et motum  penitus cognoscendum, sed multo magis etiam ob totius artis Astronomicæ<br>redintegrandæ hinc  convenienter deductas occasiones. Ipsum vero opus Astronomicum in 7<br>libros distribuere constitui. Quorum primo agam de Mechanica<br>huius artis parte, quæ instrumentorum a me excogitatorum fabricam et usum<br>exponet, qualia nunc plura quam 20 in promptu habeo, non solum magnitudine sed<br>etiam materiæ soliditate, fabricæ varietate, usus et certitudinis inter<br>observandum commoditate et correspondentia incomparabilia. Secundo de ijs, quæ e triangulorum et numerorum doctrina Astronomicis<br>rebus subsidio veniunt, tractaturus, ubi compendia quædam reserabuntur, antecessorum nostrorum nimios et indefessos labores plurimum<br>collevantia. Hæc, inquam, in duobus prioribus libris ceteris inservientia<br>proponere expeditius duxi, ne postea in reliquo opere, quando de apparentes<br>ipsis limitandis et salvandis ago, negotium mihi facessant, quod alijs nostris<br>antecessoribus evenisse video. Tertius liber octavæ sphæræ et<br>fixarum stellarum verificationem exquisitam continebit. Quartus Solis et Lunæ, circuitibus ratis ad Terram quiescentem<br>directis, rationes præcisas suppeditabit. Quintus duorum<br>Planetarum, qui Solem  quidem, sed non unâ Terram ambiunt, utpote Veneris et Mercurij,<br>apparentias enodabit. Sextus autem reliquorum trium, scilicet<br>♂ ♃ et ♄, qui Solem ambientes Terram et totum elementarem<br>Mundum includunt, irregularitates visui nostro incurrentes salvabit. Septimus latitudines horum quinque Planetarum scrutabitur, in<br>quibus etiam non minus quam<br>ipsis longitudinibus multum adhuc latet erroris, adeo ut<br>sæpenumero in cœlo boreæ sensibiliter reperiantur, cum in numeris tabularum<br>australes proponantur et econtra . Operis autem huius publicatio adhuc aliquot annorum procrastinationem<br>exigit; vix enim intra  quinquennium vel sexennium absolvi poterit, neque id tam ob immensum et<br>diutinum laborem, qui requiritur in mediorum motuum  et potissimum προσθαφαιρέσεων tabulis in singulis Planetis de novo<br>condendis, quam quod plures  adhuc desiderem in remotioribus et Solem ambientibus Planetis acronychias<br>observationes, præsertim in distantioribus eccentrici  locis (ut cum veteribus loquar, nam nostræ hypotheses non solum<br>epicyclos, sed etiam eccentricos  et æquantes tollunt) idque potissimum in ♄ et ♃, maxime<br>autem in ipso ♄ ob motus eius tarditatem, ut collatione in his omnibus<br>cum veterum, imprimis autem PTOLOMÆI, annotationibus<br>facta circulorum proportiones et motuum  congruentiam exquisita indagine pervestigare<br>demonstrareque et sub numerorum cognitionem revocare liceat. Interea, favente<br>divino Numine, ea successive<br>præmittam , quæ ad hoc totum negotium facere videbuntur, et prima quaque occasione,<br>ex eo quod tam diutinam dilationem non requirant, absolvi poterint , qualia sunt, quæ 4 vel 5 prioribus libris continebuntur. Daboque operam,<br>ut in hoc toto opere omnia apparentijs cœlestibus tam veterum ætate quam nostra,<br>et ob id etiam posteriorum, ad amussim correspondeant, quod equidem a nemine,<br>qui tam excellentem laborem exantlare conatus est, hactenus præstitum esse, te<br>dudum perspexisse, nullum apud me est dubium. Nam licet ille incomparabilis vir<br>COPERNICUS multa a veteribus minus recte prodita emendare laborârit, tamen ob<br>instrumentorum (ut recte etiam suspicaris ) minus exactam conformationem usumque non omnino infallibilem<br>observationes irritas obtinuit. Ingenium certe et scientia<br>ad hanc cognitionem et restitutionem illi absolvendum (sicut alteri) non defuit,<br>solummodo medijs Mechanicis, quibus utebatur, non recte se habentibus et non<br>prius (quod  oportuit) rectificatis affixarum locis. Habeo Parallaticum eius<br>instrumentum mihi a Canonico quodam Varmiensi dono missum, quod e ligno abiegno<br>constat, et divisionibus atramento in eo signatis satis ostendit, eum in his non<br>requisitam habuisse diligentiam. Quod autem meæ assertioni de liquidissima et<br>subtilissima cœli materia tam belle astipularis eamque sacrarum insuper<br>literarum testimonio confirmas, mihi pergratum est; tuum enim gravissimum de<br>rebus iudicium et altam in plurimis scientiam diu magnifeci , facturusque sum . Deinceps locum illum, quem ex ESAIA 40<br>v. 22 citas, CASTALIO, cuius versione<br>admodum delector, paulo aliter Latinitate donavit. Sic eum interpretatus est:<br>»cœlos quasi membranam intendit utque<br>tabernaculum expandit ad inhabitandum«. Sed vetus expositio verosimilior  videtur, cui etiam  aliæ propius subscribunt. CASTALIO elegantiæ studens interdum a genuina<br>verborum interpretatione deflexisse videtur. Alter locus, IOB. cap. 37 v. 18, quo<br>contrarium asseverari prima fronte<br>apparet, quia cœlos chalybis instar induratos esse dicit, recte a te cum priore conciliatur , dum soliditatem et firmitatem constantiamque perpetuam naturæ<br>revolutionisque cœli et eorum, quæ  in eo continentur, corporum potius quam materiæ  compagem respici illic, erudite et convenienter exponis. Prædictus<br>CASTALIO inversione huius loci nostræ sententiæ minus quam antiquus interpres<br>repugnat, dum sic eum  Latine reddit: »An æthera  cum eo deduxisti ita firmum, ut concretus esse videatur?««<br>Sed hæc Hebraicæ linguæ excellenter gnaris discutienda relinquo. Quidam<br>Hispanus, FRANCICUS VALLESIUS nomine, vir, ut ex eius scriptis apparet, varia eruditione imprimis excellens, in libro, quem de<br>sacra Philosophia inscripsit, quique hoc anno Lugduni editus est, cap. 51 ex hoc<br>ipso loco IOBI secundum veterem interpretationem, qua tu etiam usus es, citato,<br>ab antiquis Philosophis PYTHAGORA,<br>PARMENIDE, PLATONE et ARISTOTELE<br>receptam opinionem confirmare conatur, cœlum videlicet constare substantia<br>quadam firmissima et solidissima, orbesque ipsos revera huic inesse, omni<br>adamante solidiores et duriores, ita ut penitus dividi distrahique nequeant.<br>Verum is nimis literaliter hunc locum, ut quosdam etiam alios, intellexit, nec<br>ea, quæ in contrariam partem ab ESAIA et alibi dicuntur, consideravit. Quæ vero<br>Philosophice contra cœli liquidissimam et perviam undiquaque substantiam ratione<br>raritatis et densitatis atque vacui, et quod non perpetuo regulariter per se<br>moveri possint Astra, disserit, minus valida sunt, ut alibi<br>ostendemus, idque tibi facile apparebit, ubi eius argumentationes ibidem<br>prolatas penitus introspexeris. Sed et tandem in  absurditatem prolabitur, ut ætheris totius e 4 elementis (quæ tamen , ut rem plausibiliorem reddat, cœlestia nuncupat) compagem constare non<br>vereatur. Nec mirum, cum idem author capite primo eiusdem libri per ea, quæ<br>dicuntur in initio cap. 2 Geneseos: »Perfecti sunt cœli et<br>terra, et omnis ornatus eorum, complevitque Deus die sexto opus<br>suum«, et præterea ex dicto hoc tertij capitis Ecclesiastici:<br>»Didici, quod omnia opera Dei perseverent in perpetuum «, perperam et contra omne experientiam astruat stellam, quæ<br>anno 1572 iuxta sedile Cassiopeæ (quam ille  ad genua eius inconsiderate reponit) omnium prima exorta est  tandemque disparuit, nullatenus fuisse novam, ut neque eam, quæ ab<br>HYPARCHO, referente PLINIO, animadversa est,<br>de qua quidem, cum ipsas HYPARCHI observationes non habeamus, nihil certi<br>statuere volo. Sed quantum in  nova illa ad Cassiopæam dicto anno et sequenti conspecta a scopo<br>veritatis declinaverit, e libro nostro primo et recentiorum cœli Phænomenorum<br>apertissimum evadet. Et quam, quæso, citra omnem sensum et rationem statuit,<br>stellam illam semper illic fuisse a mundi principio et adhuc permanere, sed tam<br>exiguam, ut videri nisi debiliter nequeat, at tum visam esse increscere usque ad<br>primam magnitudinem ob aliquam medij mutationem, quæ in ipsis orbibus cœli non<br>ubique æqualiter crassis contigerit, ita ut stella hæc inciderit  in partem aliquam proximi cœli crassiorem reliquis, per quam densatum<br>lumen maioris stellæ specimen aliquandiu exhibuerit, et deinceps, hac<br>discedente, stellam minorem redditam. O  speculatio, sic unum absurdum parit alterum, nam cœlum nullis constat<br>orbibus compactis et revera illic  existentibus, nedum ut illi in quibusdam locis rarioris, in quibusdam<br>densioris substantiæ sint, veluti quidam etiam Homocentri  effectores somniârunt. At demus , id ipsum concedi posse, quî tunc fieret, quod<br>eadem Cassiopeæ stella non sæpius ita illuminaretur præter solitum, et dum illa<br>densior cœli particula hanc pertransiret , cur non alias vicinas eodem modo miraculoso lumine auxit? vel cur non<br>sæpius in reliquis affixis syderibus idem conspicitur? Cur tanta sit earum copia<br>et præter totum cœlum  distributio, ut necessario illæ partes orbium, quæ densiores finguntur,<br>sub aliquibus illarum per motus  sui continuationem aliquando<br>præterlaberentur , easque similiter solito longe maiores redderent? Quod tamen tot iam<br>seculis a solo HYPARCHO, si ita se habet, et nostro ævo in mirando illo apud<br>Cassiopeam phænomeno non animadversum est. Sed hæc et plura alia, quæ  liber iste , alias admodum ingeniose conscriptus et multiplici variarum rerum<br>cognitione refertus, nimis literali sensu e sacris oraculis<br>proponit, nunc mittamus, siquidem contra præsens negotium divagari nos faciant,<br>præsertim cum de plerisque in eo contentis et ad rem potissimum Astronomicam<br>facientibus alibi disquirere animus sit. Nihil igitur obstabit vel e sacris<br>Biblijs vel Philosophorum decretis, quin cœli materiam  liquidissimam et omni aëre tenuiorem subtilioremque certo statuamus. Nam<br>quod ARISTOTELES, licet contrarias partes tueri plurimum laboret, sibi ipsi<br>nihilominus contradicat, dum mentes Astrorum motui præesse asseverat, rectissime<br>a te infertur; id enim longe aliud est, quam circumgyratione certorum orbium , quibus affigerentur, convolvi. Video, te hypotheseos meæ delineationem<br>nudam mediocriter bene e solo intuitu percepisse, nam in ea quo ad quinque<br>Planetas circa Solem tanquam centrum revolutos amboque Luminaria et octavam<br>sphæram ipsam Terram in medio universi quiescentem respicienda omnia recte<br>exposuisti. Quod autem dubitas, cur octavam sphæram ima<br>parte a cæteris stellis pictura tanta capacitate disiungat, facilis est<br>resolutio. Dum enim omnes quinque Planetæ circa Solem, ducem et regem suum,<br>gyros ducunt, oportet centra orbium imaginariorum, quos circa eum describunt,<br>annuatim unâ cum ipso convolvi, hincque fit, ut semper ea pars orbium, quæ Soli<br>opponitur, intermediante Terra, remotior sit ab octava sphæra quam altera, et<br>licet tunc appareat ibidem maius spatium vacuum, tamen, Sole circumeunte et in<br>alteram partem delato, rursus idem impletur, et vacuitas ea, quæ putatur, est<br>tunc ab altera parte, sicque circumcirca evenit per totum infra octavum orbem<br>curriculum; alias enim non esset locus, quod accessu et recessu Planetarum ad<br>Terram, eorumdemque circa Solem annua per totos orbes circumgyratione ... Habuit<br>etiam quidam  alius Mathematicus mihi ignotus aliud quoddam dubium in hac hypothesis<br>nostræ ordinatione, de qua etiam ad nobilissimum Dominum HENRICUM RANZOVIUM<br>perscripsit et hypotheses illi ante biennium per quendam<br>meum fugitivum ministrum communicatas indicavit . Hæsitat vero ille in hunc modum, si ♂<br>et Sol in eundem locum, ubi eorum orbes se intersecant, aliquando  devenerint, ita ut ♂ latitudine prorsus careat, quomodo  se non impediant, cum duo corpora non possint esse in eodem loco. Verum<br>hæc dubitatio non multum  ingerit scrupuli, cum nunquam id fieri possit , quod ipse veretur; apparet enim eum non satis percepisse hypotheseos<br>nostræ inductiones. Quî namque  fieri queat, ut, cum  Martis circuitus Solem semper pro centro non minus quam cæteri Planetæ<br>respicit, illi unquam  occurrat? Id enim non magis datur quam circumferentiam alicuius circuli<br>centro eiusdem uniri et in idem recidere. Non animadvertebat ille, quod nunc  Sol ubi ad eum locum devenerit, ubi Martis orbis eius curriculum transit,<br>Martium ipsum curriculum tunc non eodem in loco eum imaginarie secare, sed<br>ubique tantundem ut antea a Sole distare, adeo ut Soli<br>nunquam ♂ propior fiat, quam circuitus ipsius circa eum exigit, nisi<br>quantum particulariores illi motus in duobus circellis , de quibus dixi, parum addant vel auferant. Quod tamen ad maiorem illam<br>et generaliorem circumvolutionem collatum non adeo multum habet momenti. Verum<br>hæc melius intelliguntur e mechanica quadam compositione, ita ut Sol et Luna in<br>suis orbibus circa Terram convolvantur, reliqui vero Planetæ in suis circuitibus<br>ad Solem. Tunc enim circumducto Sole apparebit, quomodo orbes quinque Planetarum<br>quotannis totum spatium infra octavam sphæram circumeundo impleant, et qua<br>ratione retrogradationes et stationes eorum per hanc centri orbis eorum cum Sole<br>concomitantiam solvantur , quodque epicycli veterum per accidens fiant e motu videlicet centri<br>orbium annuo, quodque non opus sit Terram moveri, et nihilominus Martem illi anxia vespertinum exortum propiorem  fieri quam ipsum Solem, quod nos docuit diligens experientia, ut prius<br>indicavi. Reliquas  particulariores inæqualitatis apparentis differentias , quas veteres per eccentricos et æquantes excusârunt, COPERNICUS per<br>eccentricum cum parvo epicydo, nos in hac hypothesi per duos circellos in<br>circumferentia orbium Planetarum, quos ipsum sydus certa lege designet, apposite<br>tuebimur, nisi quod in ♀ et præsertim ☿ aliquas præterea<br>gyrationes particulariores assumere oporteat. Sicque omnes  circuitus Planetarum concentri evadent non aliter, quam COPERNICUS Lunæ circa Terram revolutiones in<br>concentrico orbe cum duobus epicyclis fieri admodum ingeniose et recte<br>speculatus est. Nec enim multopere ab hac differunt trium superiorum Planetarum<br>tum universales, tum etiam particulares circuitus, nisi quod centrum orbium<br>illorum Solem annuatim concomitetur, velut hæc  fusius et exquisitius in opere illo, de quo dixi, Astronomicæ<br>instaurationis ipsis  fundamentis et  certis observationibus tum veterum tum proprijs (favente divino Numine)<br>demonstrabo, tabulasque tum æqualium motuum tum etiam προσθαφαιρέσεων huic hypothesi e  neotericis observationibus exquisitis cum veterum collatis fundatas de<br>novo condam. Efficiamque, ut numeri observationibus antiquis, imprimis vero<br>PTOLOMAICIS, et recentioribus etiam ad<br>amussim correspondeant, id quod hactenus tot iam seculis desideratum est, magna<br>huius artis quoad præcisionem debitam iactura. Miror doctissimum illum et in his<br>studijs exercitatissimum REINHOLDUM<br>de ijs præclare meritum in præfatione suarum Tabularum Prutenicarum asseverare<br>in Solis  motibus æqualibus aliquam limitationem  desiderari, προσθαφαιρέσεων vero tabulas toti mundi<br>durationi utiliter inservire, cum unum ex altero magna ex parte dependeat, et<br>mutatis circulorum proportionibus eccentricitatibusque necesse sit ipsas quoque<br>prosthaphæreses aliter se habere. Sed interdum etiam magnus dormitat Homerus.<br>Possibile autem est beneficio quorundam compendiorum in triangulis e datis  medijs motibus et integro canone triangulorum Planetarum apparentes  situs æque cito, si non citius quam per æquationum tabulas, investigare,<br>velut alibi aperiemus. Quod tantopere hoc opus nostrum aliquando videre<br>desideras, adeo ut vel eo nomine tibi vitam divinitus prorogari  exoptes, et insuper nostris conatibus divinæ benedictionis subsidium pio<br>et benevolo voto compreceris, est, quod tibi pro hoc singulari erga me meaque<br>studia favore et syncero iudicio summas agam gratias, daboque operam, ut,<br>quantum in me est, voluminis huius ea, qua fieri unquam potest, maturatione elaboratio absolvatur. Deus optimus maximus tibi largiatur<br>interea firmum robur animi et corporis, vitamque conservet, qua diutissime utens<br>beneque valens non solum his, sed et alijs Philosophiæ laboribus vacare eosque<br>promovere et tuum de ijs solertissimum iudicium proferre possis. Quod tot annis<br>in captivitate (cuius etiam in literis istis mentionem facis) detentus cum magna<br>iactura bonarum literarum delitueris, ego cum alijs multis viris tibi et studijs<br>eruditis faventibus plurimum semper indolui.</p><p>Quia vero in custodiæ illius mentionem incidimus, referam ea, quæ<br>in libro quodam  nostro Genethliaco a me, cum Lipsiæ ante annos plus minus viginti quatuor<br>operam literis darem, apud tuæ Geneseos schema annotata invenio. Tunc enim circa<br>pueritiam eram Astrologiæ et Genethliacis præsertim prædictionibus paulo<br>curiosius addictus, quas tamen postea increscente ætate et iudicio, præsertim<br>quod viderem motus ipsos syderum in tabulis se non recte habere, seposui et ad observationem emendationemque motuum me totum magno<br>conamine contuli. Reperio autem ibidem sic olim scriptum: ☿<br>in ♑  signo ♄ni et in tertia domo orientalis<br>liberque a radijs Solis, Luna existente in ♊ signo Mercuriali et<br>præsertim in nona domo, cui etiam ☿ præest, ingenium acre et<br>profundum laborisque patiens et arduum iudicium<br>designat. Postea subiungebatur: Sed ☾  iuxta cuspidem nonæ damnata □to<br>♄ni et △no brevi<br>♂ tis magnum aliquid mali insinuat , idque causa  religionis ob nonam  domum, ingenij ob ♊ signum Mercuriale, mulieris alicuius illustris  ob ☾ ipsam quæ etiam illustrioribus fixis sed  nimis violentis copulatur . Deinde de captivitate hæc verba adscripseram: ♂ dominus septimi loci in duodecima inque suo detrimento constitutus, et insuper<br>per antisciam partem Saturni læsus , carcerem vel exilium diuturnum significat, et quia ♂ damnat<br>☿m per □ e duodecima, id damni<br>occasione ingenij  et studiorum denotat, sed  cum ☾ directionis via pervenerit ad<br>⚹ ♂is in 14 ♌ in M.C.  per mortem vel Martialem aliquem hominem liberabitur et restituetur<br>in integrum, idque iuxta ætatis annum 60. Subiunxeram insuper: cauda  Draconis in M.C. iuxta Arabum placita dejectionem  ab honoribus et dignitatibus denotat , præsertim cum sit in signo Solari, hoc existente depresso, et nullæ<br>beneficæ stellæ sint in angulis, sed ambæ cadentes. Hæc, inquam, tunc<br>temporis infra figuram cœlestem tuæ nativitatis annotâram , quæ non ob id nunc refero, quod tuæ diutinæ calamitatis causas  in sola astra reijcere velim nimium  tribuendo his Astrologicis indicationibus, sed saltem ut tibi obiter<br>significarem, quid olim hac de re, cum hisce prædictionibus  impensius occuparer, chartis mandârim. Scio, quantum concedendum sit<br>Astrologicis coniecturis, nec eas plurimum suspicio, neque etiam nimium<br>contemno, in quorum utroque a plerisque etiam eruditioribus<br>peccatur. Si contineatur hæc ars intra suas metas, significationes quasdam Physicas  non ineptas neque utique  frivolas et cognitu non inutiles suppeditat. Sunt tamen plurimæ aliæ<br>eventuum concurrentes causæ, ex inferioris mundi et ijs , quæ in hoc tumultuose  geruntur, occasionibus provenientes; supra  omnia vero Deus ipse author universi, tanquam prima et universalis causa,<br>liberrime agit, secundariasque causas  sive superioris sive inferioris mundi pro suo arbitrio disponit et<br>moderatur. Verum hæc frustra tibi ea satis superque intelligenti suggero. Nunc<br>ab Astrologicis ad Astronomica revertar.</p><p>Habeo in promptu 24 annorum observationes syderum, quarum eæ, quæ<br>12 posterioribus in hac insula nostra acceptæ sunt, admodum exacte se habent , postquam instrumenta eximia et affabre extructa hîc maximo labore et<br>sumpto confieri curavi, quæ dixi supra me absoluta plurim a habere, suntque tanta circumspectione et diligentia elaborata, ut,<br>quibuscunque syderis alicuius  verum situm cœlitus indagare libuerit, in idem prorsus minutum, etiamsi<br>diversimode composita sunt, et ab alijs atque alijs observatoribus animadversio<br>fiat, recidant, quod machinarum absolutam structuram et exquisitam divisionem<br>evidenter probat; ut ob id satis certus sim, nihil vitij in meis observationibus<br>hisce instrumentis cœlitus derivatis latere. Utinam<br>veteres et antecessores nostri, præsertim nunquam satis ille laudatus COPERNICUS<br>(ut de  PTOLOMÆO ipso nihil nunc dicam), tantam diligentiam et præcisionem in<br>organis Astronomicis conficiendis et observationibus cœlestibus ad amussim<br>exequendis adhibuissent; equidem ingenti labore in restituendis æthereorum<br>corporum motibus nunc magna ex parte allevaremur. Excusabiles sunt nihilominus<br>nostri ævi Mathematici, quod  satis magna aptaque instrumenta, quibus syderum motus<br>scrutentur, non possideant, cum salaria  eorum et toti reditus annui vix unico organo rite construendo<br>absolvendoque sufficiant . Scio etenim me pleraque habere, quorum singula professoris alicuius in<br>scholis stipendium annuum vel maximum pretio confectionis longe exsuperent.<br>Tanta est ditiorum  hominum et præsertim Regum ac Principum erga hæc sublimia studia<br>illiberalitas; pauci enim nunc vel potius nulli reperiuntur Reges ALPHONSI. Quod si partim ex proprijs bonis et<br>fundis, partim per huius Regni feuda Regia rei familiari  nostræ ita divina liberalitate prospectum non esset, ut his et alijs<br>sumptibus perferendis  par esse possem, frustra sane, etiamsi cætera omnia adforent, tanta molirer . Exædificavi etiam intra hoc proximum decennium arcem quandam in hac<br>insula Astronomiæ inprimis instaurationi opportunam, in cuius media et maiori<br>domo instrumenta quædam Astronomica in duabus turriculis<br>versus Austrum et Boream cum suis annexis appendicibus disponuntur. Præterea non<br>longe extra arcem in monticulo quodam aliud observatorium subterraneum<br>intra  quadriennium fieri curavi, in quo 7 diversa et exquisita tum magnitudine,<br>tum forma elaborata organa conservantur, ita ut quodlibet eorum  propriam cryptam occupet, quæ superius aperiri et, in quancunque cœli<br>partem libuerit, observatio per instrumentum dirigi possit, nihil obstantibus<br>cæteris machinis. Est enim locus ille ita editus quemadmodum tota fere insula,<br>ut libere horizon visibilis nullo intercedente impedimento pateat, ob quam et<br>peculiarem commoditatem in hac potius insula quam alibi tot sumptus feci<br>tantosque labores exantlavi. Arcis vero illius hîc exstructæ delineationem,<br>quantum ad ea attinet, quæ supra Terram patent et exterius spectantur, tibi hîc<br>adiunctam mitto, ut aliquo modo ex eius inspectione cognoscere possis, me rem<br>serio agere, quoad Astronomiam honori  et nitori restituam, eaque  in re nulli sumptui aut labori parcere. Cryptarum illarum picturam , quæ difficulter in plano exhibetur, nedum exarari curavi. Verum hæc<br>omnia unâ cum instrumentorum fabrica et usu in primo tomo operis de Astronomia<br>instauranda, quæ Mechanicam  eius, ut dixi, partem continebit, convenientibus delineationibus ob<br>oculos ponere sufficienterque declarare constitui. Interea quorundam<br>instrumentorum, quæ hactenus depicta exculptaque<br>sunt, Idæam communico. Adiunxi etiam exemplaria nonnulla quarundam epistolarum<br>ab illustrissimo Principe VILHELMO Landgravio Hassiæ rerum Astronomicarum inter Europæ<br>Principes hoc ævo peritissimo (cuius etiam mentionem in his literis ad Dom.<br>RANZOVIUM fecisti) et ipsius Celsitudinis Mathematico eruditissimo CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO ad me datarum, unâ cum<br>nostra ad singulas responsione , quibus plurima ad totam rem Astronomicam ipsasque observationes rectius instituendas adeoque artis ipsius redintegrationem<br>non parum facientia referuntur . Et de hac ipsa quæstione circa motum cœli e  refractionum, quæ in situ stellarum apparent , natura et quantitate alijsque occasionibus inter ROTHMANNUM et me copiose disseritur. Quæ omnia<br>tibi legenda et dijudicanda concedere volui, petens amanter, ut, ubi ea<br>introspexeris diligentiusque evolveris, velis mihi remittere, ne in aliorum<br>unquam perveniant manus, dumque tecum ea habes, nemini alteri communices, etiam<br>atque etiam te rogatum habeo. Imprimis vero a te plurimum expeto, ut tuum<br>iudicium tum de illis, quæ in libro illo secundo æthereorum Phænomeνων nunc unâ  misso, tum etiam quæ in his adiunctis literis continentur, ad me ante<br>hyemem libere et luculenter perscribere non graveris. Si quid in transcriptione<br>earundem literarum ab amanuensibus meis aberratum est, tuum erit idipsum pro tua<br>solertia emendare; mihi omnia relegendi non suppetebat otium.<br>Scripsit ad me iam ante nobilissimus ille HENRICUS RANZOVIUS, nec incongruis rationibus persuadere<br>conatur, ut tum has illustrissimi Principis VILHELMI, tum ipsius<br>Mathematici et aliorum etiam eruditissimorum virorum ad me de hoc toto<br>Astronomico negotio perscriptas  literas, unâ cum mea ad quasvis responsione , in fine operis nostri de Mundi ætherei  recentioribus Phænomenis subiungam, eo quod hæ  materiam, de qua illic agitur, plurimum illustret et ad totius artis<br>amplificationem magnopere conducant. Qua in re ipsius iustæ et  benevolæ petitioni, fretus etiam tuo et aliorum eruditorum iudicio<br>consilioque, morem gerere non recusandum duxi. Addidi insuper his literis<br>Solaris cursus restitutionem e meis observationibus et demonstrationibus<br>deductam ad proximum quinquennium, ut aliquid interea e meis in cursu cœlestium<br>corporum emendationibus obtineas, donec plura communicare<br>tempus concesserit. Apogæum  Solis hîc statuitur iuxta finem 6 gradus<br>♋, tribus gradibus Coperniano anterius, et eccentricitas præsupponitur<br>gr.  2 min. 9 1/3, qualium semidiamet.  eccentrici 60, quod ipsius inventionem plurimum excedit. Cur vero magnus<br>ille COPERNICUS vel in ipsius Solaris<br>cursus retentione tantopere a scopo deflexerit, alibi sufficienter ostendam. Non<br>autem est, quod dubites, quin hic calculus noster ne in minimo, quod in sensus<br>cadere possit, differat a tramite, quem Sol æthereus<br>designat, ut non solum nostræ multorum annorum, sed  etiam Landgravianæ, præsertim post organorum renovatam rectificationem,<br>attestantur observationes. Quantum vero ab Alphonsina et Coperniana supputatione<br>differat, ipsemet collatione facta facile perspicies; adeo ne Solis quidem<br>motus, quamvis is normam cæteris omnibus præbeat,  hactenus<br>satis extricatus fuit, nedum ut in reliquis Planetis nihil lateat vitij. Sed<br>video me epistolæ metas transgredi, dum avidius de his tecum colloquor ; tuum itaque erit hanc prolixitatem unâ cum libro et chartis adiunctis<br>boni consulere. Ubi primus et ultimus tomi operis, quod nunc præ manibus habeo<br>de ætheris recentioribus Phænomenis, typis nostris excusi fuerint, te eorum<br>etiam reddam participem. Quod autem primus antea non prodierit, in causa Gallici<br>privilegij defectus, quod indies expectabam, Cæsareano antea obtento<br>adiungendum. Verum cum nunc Gallicum illud acceperim  et Anglicanum  quoque brevi habiturus sim, magnifico et eruditissimo viro D. DANIELE ROGERSIO, qui nuper hîc mecum fuit,<br>ita apud serenissimam Reginam procurante, nihil iam amplius obstabit, quin<br>primus liber propediem in lucem edatur. Interea his fruere, et quam felicissime diutissimeque vale, eruditissime et de Republica literaria<br>optime merite PEUCERE, meque tui amantissimum,<br>quod facis, redamare et per literas ante hyemem ingruentem revisere non<br>intermittas. Commodissimum autem erit  eas te ad  sæpe dictum nobilem Dom. HENRICUM<br>RANZOVIUM in Holsatiam mittere, qui illas ad me perferri non<br>gravatim procurabit.</p><p>Iterum et sæpe multumque vale, vir vita et valetudine diuturna<br>dignissime.</p><p>Datæ Uraniburgi die 13.<br>Septembris Anno 1588.</p><p>Hæ literæ  lectæ mittantur statim ad D. IOANNEM â<br>DEE, quem cras mane ante abitum, Deo volente,<br>conveniemus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Caspar Peucer. Sends greeting.</b></p><p>I have long had [it] in my wishes to contract a most welcome friendship with you — either in person, when I once passed through <b>Wittenberg</b>, or also afterward, absent, by letter — most celebrated man, excelling in varied learning and virtue; but since no convenience offered itself sufficiently opportunely meanwhile for effecting this, I was forced to defer it until the present time. But the occasion of at last in some part fulfilling this long-standing desire of mine was given by the letter of the most ample and most noble man Lord <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, Royal Vicar in Holstein, most ample in Mathematical matters. For there was added to it a certain transcript of those [things] which you had lately transmitted to him about my <b>new invention of the disposition of the celestial revolutions</b>; and at the same time he asked that I would be willing to meet your doubts about the delineation communicated to you by him — which, that I might perform it for the favour of you both, I judged that I must not omit to send this letter to you; in which, that your expectation and the resolution of [your] doubts may more fully be satisfied, I see that I must begin a little higher up — namely, from the very first occasion of the discovery of this hypothesis — and discourse somewhat more diffusely about this matter; and this I will now undertake.</p><p>Since from [my] entering age I was, among other parts of Philosophy, earnestly addicted — by a certain instinct of nature, even against the will and resistance of my very tutors — to the Astronomical study, and at once in boyhood, dwelling at <b>Leipzig</b> for studies' sake and being in the seventeenth year of my age, often scrutinized the courses of the Planets from the fixed stars by the <b>Cross-staff</b> [<i>Radius Astronomicus</i>] on serene nights, I noticed that their appearances did not agree precisely enough even with the <b>Copernican</b> calculation, much less the <b>Alphonsine</b>, which was now well-nigh obsolete. Thence I began long and much to ponder with myself whence this so evident discrepancy from the heaven itself arose — whether on account of observations not rightly instituted by the artificers, or also on account of the insufficiency and falsity of the assumed hypotheses. And when over some years I turned this matter variously over, my age now increasing and my judgment maturing more and more along with it, I inspected more diligently both the <b>Alphonsine</b> (or Ptolemaic) hypotheses and the <b>Copernican</b>, and inquired more deeply into their reasonings, and at last noticed that in those old hypotheses, deduced from <b>Ptolemy</b> and certain of his predecessors down to us — although for the most part they excused [saved] the celestial appearances — yet, because they admitted that the <b>regularity of circular motion is made not about [the] proper but [about] some alien centre</b>, [they] sin against the first principles of the art (which Copernicus himself too seems to have faulted in these); and besides, that the epicycles, so many and so great, which are there too assumed, occupy much space in the heaven and are superfluous, if the whole matter could be accomplished by fewer. And a great scruple they threw into me — that no necessary cause or natural combination underlay [the fact] <b>why the superior Planets were so bound to the Sun</b> that, conjoined to it, they always occupied the summit of [their] epicycles, but in the lowest place of the same were opposed to it; and the two which they call inferior always shared the same mean place with the Sun, and were joined to it at the Apogee and Perigee of [their] epicycles. These [things], I say, it was not possible to gather to have a sure and evident reason of combination — since these stars are distant from one another by so great an interval, so that, if this mutual concatenation were made precisely in that manner, that some competent necessity underlay [it], that everything in it should so happen, appeared to me less consonant; but rather, even for this sole cause, I suspected — not without reason — that something forced, dissonant, and superfluous lay hidden in these assumptions. But in the other, the <b>neoteric</b> distribution of that supreme <b>Copernicus</b>, the <b>absurdity about the threefold motion of the Earth</b> at once deterred [me] — but especially as to that annual declination of the centre and axis into contrary parts. For although it opportunely remedied the rest of [the things] which are found incoherent and superfluous in the Ptolemaic disposition, and offended nothing against Mathematical principles, yet, since it attributed a regular, perfect, and less-intricate motion to the <b>Earth</b>, a sluggish and ignobler body, this assumption was rendered not a little suspect — especially since the <b>sacred writings</b> in not a few places openly cry out against it. Hence I doubted not a little about both of these hypotheses, whether either of them stood rightly. But since no other was given which was consonant with the celestial appearances, and the <b>figment of homocentrics</b> devised by some [Fracastoro, Amici] was too violent, and through the inextricable retraction of many orbs this way and that was found absurd and even ridiculous, nor did it satisfy the appearances themselves in the approach and recession of the Planets to the Earth — therefore, long entangled in these doubts, since no innovation of hypotheses at once occurred (as I said) which might relieve all these requirements, I resolved at least <b>to make a trial</b> [of] which of those hitherto invented came nearer to the truth. And I trusted that I could fish this out chiefly through the <b>star of Mars</b>, when, namely, it should approach the Earth acronychally [at opposition]; for if it then came nearer to us than the Sun itself, [I judged] the <b>Copernican</b> speculation to prevail; if not, [I judged] one must rather stand by the <b>Ptolemaic</b>. And at last, with great diligence and no small expense, having procured various Astronomical instruments by which the motions of the stars could be explored centrally [with certainty] not only to the very minute but even to its half or quarter, I did not fear to undertake this subtle consideration — and that chiefly in the <b>year 1582, when Mars, made all-night-visible, was opposed to the Sun in Cancer</b>, and besides was rendered higher in [its] daily circuit by [its] northern latitude. Having had then several accurate observations, both about [its] rising and setting and its transit through the meridian, I found that <b>Mars causes a greater parallax than the Sun itself, and therefore is made nearer to the earth when it is acronychal</b> — its diurnal motion too agreeing, [and] consenting rather with the Copernican numbers, in that it stepped back into the preceding [direction] a little more swiftly, over a certain interval of days, than the reckoning of the Alphonsine [Tables], deduced from Ptolemy, would grant — and that on account of [its] smaller distance from the earth, which made its motion appear a little more intense. When I had this sufficiently explored in <b>Mars</b>, and the same too in <b>Venus</b> — observed by me several times by day, and indeed on the very meridian, and a comparison made near setting; nay, also by a certain peculiar method in the year <b>1587 about the 24th of February</b>, when within one or another day I saw it both as evening and as morning [star] (which is otherwise very rare), comparing the apparent motion with that which would occur by reason of parallax — so that all these by no means agreed with the Ptolemaic hypotheses, I was impelled afterward more and more to attribute credit to the <b>Copernican</b> invention. Yet there stood greatly in the way the excessive <b>absurdity about the Earth's revolution</b>, so ordinary and perpetual, and besides the irrefragable <b>authority of the sacred writings</b> building the contrary. Wherefore, turning this business variously this way and that, I deliberated studiously with myself about another ordering of the hypotheses. At first I seemed to attempt an impossible thing; but at last, almost unexpectedly, it occurred [to me] — that <b>if the Sun be constituted the centre of the five Planets, and nonetheless be revolved annually about the Earth resting in the middle of the universe</b> (the circuits of the remaining five Planets accompanying [it] through the centre, and therefore also [through] the very circumference), so that the <b>Earth furnish the centre at least for the revolutions of the Sun and the Moon and of the highest eighth sphere including all the rest</b> — if, I say, the celestial revolutions be ordered in that manner, then all [the things] which fall out dissonant, idle, and superfluous in the Ptolemaic and Copernican assumption are removed and forestalled. But nonetheless it still threw in a scruple, that the <b>Martian orb, relative to the Solar, was not found so great</b> that it could totally include it, but necessarily <b>penetrated it in two places</b>. For I was still imbued with the long-received and by almost all approved opinion that the heaven is <b>packed with certain real orbs carrying the stars</b> around, and therefore I judged this incongruous penetration of orbs not to be admitted; and thence it came about that this my own invention was for some while suspect to me myself. But at last, when from the accurate examination of certain <b>Comets</b>, as to their motion and parallaxes, I had explored for certain that they completed the norm of their course <b>in the heaven itself, far above the Moon</b>, and were nonetheless subject to no corporeal and real orbs by which the Planets are believed to be carried, but had found a certain peculiar reckoning of motion from these — as is more amply and exquisitely demonstrated in our work on these <b>ascititious phenomena of the heaven</b> — I utterly cast aside that presumption about the <b>hard and impervious matter of the celestial orbs</b>. There was added this too, that <b>two Comets, which were borne near the opposite of the Sun, showed evidently enough that the Earth is not really revolved annually</b> — since its commutation detracted nothing from their predefined and proportionable motion, as comes about in the Planets, which on that account are thought by Copernicus to be driven backward. At last, therefore, both foundations — both the Copernican and the Ptolemaic — being overturned by experience itself, I confirmed in mind that this neoteric invention stood rightly beyond all doubt — especially because I perceived from the Comets (as I said) that the heaven consists of a <b>most fluid and proper substance</b>, and abounds in no hard and real orbs (as has been falsely believed now for so many centuries); so that on that account it presents no obstacle that the Martian imaginary orb does not totally include the Solar, but intersects [it] in two places — since those orbs are not corporeally in the heaven, but yet are understood to come about from the motion of the stars. Soon, within the next four years, I compared the more exact appearances in the motion of the five Planets with this our hypothesis, and noticed that they agreed with it most openly, and abundantly satisfied the ancients' observations too; nay, that something else lay hidden in it which excused a certain <b>peculiar occasion of apparent inequality, especially in the motion of Mars</b> (on account of the very sensible and great comparison of the Solar orb to its circuit), which could by no means be <b>saved</b> by the predecessors' assumptions — so that on that account, in some part, the course especially of Mars has hitherto remained utterly unexplored. Which was noticed by the ancients too, since <b>Pliny</b> does not hesitate to affirm that this star is <b>unobservable</b>. Wherefore, no longer hesitating that my neoteric invention stands rightly on every side, and suggests nothing dissonant either Mathematically or Physically, I resolved to build upon it alone the <b>restoration of the celestial revolutions</b> from the observations of the ancients and ours; yet I judged that its proof must be deferred to our <b>Astronomical work</b>, which, by divine help, I resolved to elaborate concerning the <b>instauration of the whole art</b>. But since the <b>Comet of [15]77</b>, which revolved about the Sun no less than the Planets themselves, invited me — and while I labour to <b>save</b> its appearances by a suitable hypothesis, I might lay open the reasoning of this [system's] hypotyposis in the world-frame by a more general judgment, until the publication of the Astronomical work — my opinion being changed, I judged it more advisable that the whole matter not be deferred. But since I had communicated to the aforesaid most noble <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, my singular friend, at his request, some copies of the <b>second part of our work on the more recent Phenomena of the aethereal World</b>, I understand that he transmitted the <b>delineation of this hypothesis</b> which is found there in <b>chapter 8</b>, excerpted, to you and certain other learned [men] in Germany — neglecting, however, the succinct explanation which was added to it; which, had he joined [it] at the same time, neither you nor the others would have fallen into doubts about this reasoning, so easily resolvable — which I will afterward dispatch with the least labour. But I send you this same book, from which the most noble Rantzau took this designation of the hypothesis, which treats amply of the Comet of [15]77. And it is the <b>second</b> of that work of which I spoke; for the <b>first</b> deals fully with that admirable <b>New Star arisen by Cassiopeia in the year [15]72</b>, where certain [things] are premised which lay the foundations for the instauration of the whole art — such as those which are proposed about the <b>restitution of the Sun's course</b>, accomplished for our times by sure observations [and] demonstrations, and about the <b>exquisite certification of the fixed stars</b>. In the same book too the observations and tenets of others about this star are recalled to the <b>anvil of truth</b>, and together your own, once had at <b>Wittenberg</b>, but with less suitable instruments. In the <b>third tome</b> I set forth my findings and decisions about certain other three Comets afterward observed, and confer [them] with others' tenets in the same, a diligent examination being applied in the singulars; to all which are at last subjoined certain great <b>corollaries</b> which can be derived from them, such as: that the heaven does not consist of real orbs and impervious substance; that no epicycles really exist; that the Earth is by no means revolved annually, as Copernicus wished; and that our ordering of the celestial hypotheses agrees with the truth of the matter on every side, and that no other is given which suffices for all requisites.</p><p>In such a way, then, through these three books of the neoteric and ascititious phenomena in the heaven, I wished to <b>pave the way and lay the foundations</b> for that work which will serve for restoring, from the heaven itself (the Maker of the heaven itself effecting [it]), the revolutions of the everlasting stars coeval with the world. And therefore I judged advisedly that this description of the <b>wandering and secondary stars</b> [comets, nova] should be sent ahead — and that not only on account of thoroughly knowing the position and motion of these ascititious stars, but much more on account of the occasions conveniently deduced thence for the re-integration of the whole Astronomical art. But the <b>Astronomical work itself I resolved to distribute into 7 books</b>. Of which, in the <b>first</b>, I will deal with the <b>Mechanical</b> part of this art, which will set forth the construction and use of the <b>instruments</b> devised by me — of which I now have more than 20 ready, incomparable not only in magnitude but also in solidity of material, variety of construction, and convenience and correspondence of use and certainty in observing. In the <b>second</b>, I am to treat of those [things] which come from the <b>doctrine of triangles and numbers</b> as aids to Astronomical matters, where certain <b>compendia</b> will be unlocked, greatly relieving the excessive and unwearied labours of our predecessors. These [things], I say, serving the rest, I judged it more expeditious to propose in the two prior books, lest afterward, in the remaining work — when I deal with limiting and <b>saving</b> the appearances themselves — they make trouble for me, which I see befell our other predecessors. The <b>third</b> book will contain the exquisite <b>verification of the eighth sphere and the fixed stars</b>. The <b>fourth</b> will supply the precise reckonings of the <b>Sun and Moon</b>, [their] settled circuits directed to the resting Earth. The <b>fifth</b> will unravel the appearances of the two Planets which circle the <b>Sun but not at the same time the Earth — namely Venus and Mercury</b>. The <b>sixth</b> will <b>save</b> the irregularities, falling under our sight, of the remaining three — namely <b>Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn</b> — which, circling the Sun, include the Earth and the whole elementary World. The <b>seventh</b> will scrutinize the <b>latitudes</b> of these five Planets, in which too no less than in the longitudes themselves much error still lurks — so much so that very often they are found sensibly <b>northern in the heaven</b> when in the numbers of the tables they are proposed <b>southern</b>, and the contrary. But the publication of this work still demands a procrastination of some years; for it can scarcely be completed within a quinquennium or sexennium — and that not so much on account of the immense and long labour which is required in constructing anew the tables of the mean motions, and especially of the <b>prosthaphaereses</b> [προσθαφαιρέσεων], in the single Planets, as because I still want more <b>acronychal observations</b> in the more remote Planets which circle the Sun, especially in the more distant places of the <b>eccentric</b> (to speak with the ancients, for our hypotheses remove not only epicycles, but also eccentrics and equants) — and that chiefly in <b>Saturn and Jupiter</b>, but most of all in <b>Saturn</b> itself, on account of the slowness of its motion — so that, a collation being made in all these with the ancients', but especially <b>Ptolemy's</b>, notations, it may be permitted to investigate, demonstrate, and recall under the knowledge of numbers, by exquisite search, the <b>proportions of the circles and the congruence of the motions</b>. Meanwhile, the divine Deity favouring, I will send ahead successively those [things] which shall seem to make for this whole business, and which, at the first occasion, since they do not require so long a delay, can be completed — such as those which will be contained in the 4 or 5 prior books. And I will take care that in this whole work everything correspond exactly to the celestial appearances, both of the ancients' age and ours (and on that account also of those to come) — which indeed I have no doubt you long since perceived has hitherto been accomplished by no one who has tried to drain so excellent a labour. For although that incomparable man <b>Copernicus</b> laboured to emend many [things] less rightly brought forward by the ancients, yet, on account of the <b>less exact conformation of [his] instruments</b> (as you too rightly suspect) and the not-wholly-infallible use [of them], he obtained void observations. Talent certainly and science for completing this knowledge and restitution were not lacking to him (as [not] to the other [Ptolemy]) — only the <b>Mechanical means</b> which he used did not stand rightly, and the places of the fixed [stars] had not first (as was needful) been rectified. I have his <b>Parallactic instrument</b> [triquetrum], sent me as a gift by a certain <b>Canon of Ermland</b>, which consists of fir-wood, and, by the divisions marked on it with ink, shows well enough that he did not have in these [things] the requisite diligence. But that you so beautifully <b>support my assertion about the most fluid and most subtle matter of the heaven</b>, and confirm it moreover by the testimony of the <b>sacred writings</b>, is most welcome to me; for I have long greatly esteemed your most weighty judgment about [these] matters and [your] high science in very many [things], and shall do [so]. Further, that passage which you cite from <b>Isaiah 40:22</b> — <b>Castellio</b> (with whose version I am quite delighted) rendered [it] into Latin a little otherwise. He interpreted it thus: <i>\"He stretches out the heavens like a membrane, and expands [them] like a tent to dwell in.\"</i> But the <b>old exposition</b> seems more probable, to which others too more closely subscribe. Castellio, studying elegance, seems sometimes to have deflected from the genuine interpretation of the words. The other passage, <b>Job 37:18</b>, by which the contrary seems at first sight to be asserted (because it says the heavens are hardened like <b>steel</b> [<i>chalybs</i>]), you rightly reconcile with the former, when you erudite[ly] and fittingly expound that there is regarded the <b>solidity and firmness and perpetual constancy of the nature and revolution of the heaven, and of the bodies contained in it, rather than the framing of the matter</b>. The aforesaid Castellio, by [his] inversion of this passage, repugns our opinion less than the ancient interpreter, when he renders it thus into Latin: <i>\"Did you draw out the aether with him so firm that it seems to be concreted?\"</i> But these [things] I leave to be discussed by those excellently skilled in the <b>Hebrew</b> tongue. A certain Spaniard, <b>Francisco Valles</b> [Vallesius] by name — a man, as appears from his writings, especially excelling in varied erudition — in the book which he entitled <i>On Sacred Philosophy</i> [<i>De sacra philosophia</i>], and which was published this year at <b>Lyon</b>, in chapter 51, from this very passage of <b>Job</b> (cited according to the old interpretation, which you too used), tries to confirm the opinion received from the ancient Philosophers <b>Pythagoras, Parmenides, Plato, and Aristotle</b> — namely, that the heaven consists of a certain most firm and most solid substance, and that the orbs themselves really inhere in it, <b>harder and more solid than any adamant</b>, so that they cannot be utterly divided and torn apart. But he understood this passage (as some others too) too literally, nor considered those [things] which are said to the contrary part by <b>Isaiah</b> and elsewhere. But what he disputes Philosophically against the heaven's most fluid and everywhere-pervious substance — by reason of rarity and density and the vacuum, and that the Stars cannot perpetually move regularly of themselves — is less valid, as we shall show elsewhere; and that will easily appear to you, when you have thoroughly inspected his argumentations brought forward there. But at last he even slips into the <b>absurdity</b> of not fearing that the framing of the whole aether consists <b>of the four elements</b> (which, however, to render the matter more plausible, he calls celestial). Nor [is it any] wonder, since the same author, in the first chapter of the same book — by those [things] which are said at the beginning of <b>Genesis 2</b>: <i>\"The heavens and the earth were perfected, and all their adornment, and God completed his work on the sixth day,\"</i> and besides from this saying of the third chapter of <b>Ecclesiasticus</b>: <i>\"I have learned that all the works of God endure forever\"</i> — wrongly and against all experience builds [the claim] that the star which <b>in the year 1572, by the seat of Cassiopeia</b> (which he inconsiderately places back at her knees), arose first of all and at last disappeared, was <b>by no means new</b>; as neither [was] that which was noticed by <b>Hipparchus</b> (Pliny reporting) — about which indeed, since we do not have Hipparchus's own observations, I wish to determine nothing certain. But how far, in that new [star] by Cassiopeia beheld in the said year and the following, [he] has declined from the mark of truth, will become most clear from our first book of the recent Phenomena of the heaven. And how — without all sense and reason, I ask — does he establish that that star was always there from the world's beginning and still remains, but so small that it cannot be seen except faintly, yet was then seen to grow up to the <b>first magnitude</b> on account of some <b>change of the medium</b>, which occurred in the very orbs of the heaven (not everywhere equally thick), so that this star fell upon some part of the nearest heaven thicker than the rest, through which the <b>condensed light</b> exhibited for a while the appearance of a larger star, and thereafter, this [thick part] departing, [it was] rendered a smaller star? O speculation! — thus one absurdity begets another; for the heaven consists of no compacted orbs really existing there, much less that they be in some places of rarer, in some of denser substance, as certain <b>effectors of homocentrics</b> too dreamed. But let us grant that this very [thing] could be conceded — how then would it come about that the same star of Cassiopeia was not more often thus illuminated beyond the usual? and while that denser little part of the heaven passed across this [star], why did it not increase others nearby in the same way by a miraculous light? or why is the same not more often beheld in the remaining fixed stars? Why is there so great an abundance of them, and [such] a distribution throughout the whole heaven, that necessarily those parts of the orbs which are feigned denser must sometime slip past, by the continuation of their motion, under some of those [stars], and render them similarly far larger than usual? Which, however, for so many centuries now (if it be so) was not noticed in [the case of] <b>Hipparchus</b> alone, and in our age in that wonderful phenomenon by Cassiopeia. But these and many other [things] which that book — otherwise quite ingeniously written and crammed with manifold knowledge of various matters — proposes from the sacred oracles in too literal a sense, let us now dismiss, since they make us digress against the present business — especially since it is [my] intention to inquire elsewhere about most of [the things] contained in it and making chiefly for the Astronomical matter. Nothing, therefore, will stand in the way, either from the sacred Bible or from the decrees of the Philosophers, but that we surely establish the <b>matter of the heaven [to be] most fluid and thinner and subtler than all air</b>. For that <b>Aristotle</b>, although he labours greatly to defend the contrary parts, nonetheless contradicts himself, when he asserts that <b>minds preside over the motion of the Stars</b>, is most rightly inferred by you; for that is far other than to be revolved by the circumgyration of certain orbs to which [the stars] would be affixed. I see that you have moderately well perceived, from the bare delineation alone, the [outline] of my hypothesis — for in it, as to the five Planets revolved about the Sun as centre, and both Luminaries and the eighth sphere regarding the Earth itself resting in the middle of the universe, you have rightly set forth everything. But as to your doubt — <b>why the picture disjoins the eighth sphere at the lowest part from the other stars by so great a capacity [gap]</b> — the resolution is easy. For while all five Planets lead [their] gyres about the <b>Sun, their leader and king</b>, the centres of the imaginary orbs which they describe about it must be revolved annually together with it; and hence it comes about that always that part of the orbs which is opposed to the Sun, the Earth intervening, is more remote from the eighth sphere than the other; and although then a greater empty space appears there, yet, the Sun going around and being carried to the other part, the same is again filled, and that vacuity which is supposed is then on the other part; and so it comes about all around through the whole course below the eighth orb. For otherwise there would be no place, [given] that by the approach and recession of the Planets to the Earth, and their annual circumgyration about the Sun through whole orbs … [<i>lacuna</i>]. A certain other Mathematician, unknown to me, also had another doubt in this ordering of our hypothesis — about which he too wrote to the most noble Lord <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, and indicated [that] the hypotheses [had been] communicated to him two years ago <b>through a certain fugitive servant of mine</b>. He hesitates in this manner: <b>if Mars and the Sun should sometime come into the same place where their orbs intersect each other, so that Mars wholly lacks latitude, how do they not impede each other, since two bodies cannot be in the same place?</b> But this doubt introduces no great scruple, since what he fears can never happen; for it appears that he did not sufficiently perceive the inductions of our hypothesis. For how could it come about that, since Mars's circuit always regards the Sun as centre no less than the other Planets, it should ever meet it? For that is no more given than that the circumference of some circle be united with its centre and fall back into the same. He did not notice that <b>now, when the Sun shall have come to that place where Mars's orb crosses its [the Sun's] course, the Martian course does not then cut it imaginarily at the same place, but everywhere stands as far from the Sun as before</b> — so much so that <b>Mars never becomes nearer to the Sun than its circuit about it requires</b>, except in so far as those more particular motions in the <b>two little circles</b> [<i>circelli</i>] of which I spoke add or take away a little; which, however, compared to that greater and more general circumvolution, has not much moment. But these are better understood from a certain <b>mechanical composition</b> [model], such that the Sun and Moon are revolved in their orbs about the Earth, but the remaining Planets in their circuits about the Sun. For then, the Sun being carried around, it will appear how the orbs of the five Planets, going around, fill the whole space below the eighth sphere every year, and by what reckoning their retrogradations and stations are resolved through this concomitance of the centre of their orb with the Sun; and that the <b>epicycles of the ancients come about per accidens</b> — namely, from the annual motion of the centre of the orbs; and that there is no need for the Earth to move, and nonetheless [that] Mars is made nearer [to the Earth], in its anxious [eager] evening rising, than the Sun itself — which diligent experience has taught us, as I indicated before. The remaining more particular differences of apparent inequality, which the ancients excused by <b>eccentrics and equants</b>, [and] Copernicus by an <b>eccentric with a small epicycle</b>, we, in this hypothesis, will aptly defend by <b>two little circles on the circumference of the Planets' orbs</b>, which the star itself designates by a fixed law — except that in <b>Venus</b> and especially <b>Mercury</b> it is necessary besides to assume some particular gyrations. And so <b>all the circuits of the Planets will turn out concentric</b>, no otherwise than as Copernicus quite ingeniously and rightly speculated that the Moon's revolutions about the Earth come about in a <b>concentric orb with two epicycles</b>. For the circuits, both universal and particular, of the three superior Planets do not much differ from this, except that the centre of their orbs accompanies the Sun annually — as I will demonstrate more fully and exquisitely in that work of which I spoke, of the Astronomical instauration, by [its] very foundations and by sure observations both of the ancients and my own (the divine Deity favouring); and I will construct anew the tables both of the equal motions and also of the <b>prosthaphaereses</b>, founded for this hypothesis on the exquisite neoteric observations collated with the ancients'. And I will effect that the numbers correspond exactly to the ancient observations — but especially the <b>Ptolemaic</b> — and to the more recent too; which hitherto, now for so many centuries, has been wanting, to the great loss of this art as to [its] due precision. I marvel that that most learned [man], and most exercised in these studies and splendidly deserving of them, <b>Reinhold</b>, asserts in the preface of his <b>Prutenic Tables</b> that in the <b>mean motions of the Sun</b> some limitation is wanting, but that the <b>tables of prosthaphaereses serve usefully for the whole duration of the world</b> — since the one depends largely on the other, and, the proportions of the circles and the eccentricities being changed, it is necessary that the prosthaphaereses too stand otherwise. But sometimes even <b>great Homer nods</b>. But it is possible, by the benefit of certain compendia in triangles, from the given mean motions and the whole canon of triangles, to investigate the apparent positions of the Planets as quickly — if not more quickly — than by the tables of equations, as we shall open elsewhere. That you so greatly desire some time to see this work of ours — so much so that even for that reason you wish life divinely prolonged to you, and besides pray, by a pious and benevolent vow, the aid of the divine blessing for our endeavours — is [a thing] for which I give you the greatest thanks for this singular favour toward me and my studies, and [your] sincere judgment; and I will take care that, as far as in me lies, the elaboration of this volume be completed with the greatest maturation that can ever be [made]. May God the best and greatest meanwhile bestow on you firm vigour of mind and body, and preserve [your] life, [so that], using it as long as possible and faring well, you may be able to have leisure not only for these but for other labours of Philosophy too, and to promote them, and to bring forward your most skilful judgment about them. That you have for so many years lain hidden, detained in <b>captivity</b> (of which you make mention too in this letter), with great loss to good letters, I, with many other men favourable to you and to erudite studies, have always greatly grieved.</p><p>But since we have fallen upon mention of that custody, I will report those [things] which I find noted, in a certain <b>Genethliacal book</b> of mine, by your <b>nativity-figure</b> [<i>Geneseos schema</i>], by me, when at <b>Leipzig</b> about more or less twenty-four years ago I was giving my work to letters. For then, about boyhood, I was somewhat curiously addicted to Astrology and especially to Genethliacal predictions — which, however, afterward, my age and judgment increasing (especially because I saw that the very motions of the stars did not stand rightly in the tables), I set aside, and betook myself wholly, with great effort, to the <b>observation and emendation of the motions</b>. But I find there written thus of old: <i>\"<b>Mercury [☿] in Capricorn [♑], the sign of Saturn [♄], and in the third house, oriental and free from the rays of the Sun, the Moon being in Gemini [♊], a Mercurial sign, and especially in the ninth house, over which Mercury too presides — designates a keen and profound talent, patient of labour, and an arduous [lofty] judgment.</b>\"</i> Afterward was subjoined: <i>\"But the <b>Moon [☾] near the cusp of the ninth, afflicted by the square [□] of Saturn and the [imminent] trine [△] of Mars [♂], insinuates some great evil</b> — and that by reason of <b>religion</b> (because of the ninth house), of <b>talent</b> (because of Gemini, a Mercurial sign), of <b>some illustrious woman</b> (because of the Moon itself, which is also joined to brighter but too-violent fixed [stars]).\"</i> Then about the <b>captivity</b> I had written these words: <i>\"<b>Mars, lord of the seventh place, set in the twelfth and in its detriment, and moreover injured by the antiscion-part of Saturn, signifies prison or long exile</b>; and because Mars afflicts Mercury by a square from the twelfth, it denotes that harm by occasion of talent and studies; but <b>when the Moon by the path of direction shall have reached the sextile [⚹] of Mars at 14° of Leo [♌] in the Midheaven [M.C.], he will be freed by death or by some Martial man, and restored to wholeness — and that about the 60th year of age.</b>\"</i> I had subjoined besides: <i>\"The <b>Tail of the Dragon in the Midheaven</b>, according to the tenets of the Arabs, denotes <b>dejection from honours and dignities</b>, especially since it is in a Solar sign [Leo], this being depressed, and no benefic stars are in the angles, but both [are] cadent.\"</i> These [things], I say, I had then noted beneath the celestial figure of your nativity — which I now report not because I would wish to cast the causes of your long calamity onto the stars alone, attributing too much to these Astrological indications, but only that I might in passing signify to you what I once committed to paper about this matter, when I was more earnestly occupied with these predictions. I know how much is to be conceded to Astrological conjectures, and I neither esteem them greatly, nor too much despise [them] — in both of which most, even of the more erudite, sin. <b>If this art be contained within its bounds, it supplies certain Physical significations not inept, nor at all frivolous, and not useless to know.</b> Yet there are very many other concurrent causes of events, arising from the occasions of the lower world and of [the things] which are conducted tumultuously in it; but <b>above all, God himself, the author of the universe, as the first and universal cause, acts most freely, and disposes and moderates the secondary causes, whether of the upper or the lower world, at his pleasure.</b> But these I suggest in vain to you, who understand them more than sufficiently. Now I will return from Astrological [matters] to Astronomical.</p><p>I have ready to hand <b>24 years of observations</b> of the stars, of which those taken in the latter 12 on this our island stand quite exact — after I took care that excellent and skilfully-built instruments be made here, with the greatest labour and expense, which I said above I have completed, very many [of them]; and they are elaborated with so great circumspection and diligence that, by whichever [of them] it shall please to investigate the true position of some star from the heaven, they fall back into entirely the same minute — even though they are diversely composed, and the observation be made by different and different observers — which evidently proves the perfect structure and exquisite division of the machines; so that on that account I am sufficiently certain that no fault lurks in my observations derived from the heaven by these instruments. Would that the ancients and our predecessors, especially the never-sufficiently-praised <b>Copernicus</b> (to say nothing now of <b>Ptolemy</b> himself), had applied so great diligence and precision in making Astronomical instruments and in exactly executing celestial observations! Indeed we should now be greatly relieved of the immense labour in restoring the motions of the aethereal bodies. Nonetheless the <b>Mathematicians of our age are excusable</b>, that they do not possess sufficiently great and apt instruments by which to scrutinize the motions of the stars, since their <b>salaries</b> and whole annual revenues scarcely suffice for rightly constructing and completing a single instrument. For I know that I have very many [instruments] of which the single ones far exceed some school-professor's annual stipend — even the greatest — by the cost of [their] making. So great is the <b>illiberality of richer men, and especially of Kings and Princes, toward these sublime studies; for few now, or rather no </b>King Alfonsos<b> are found. And if provision had not been made for our family-estate, partly from our own goods and lands, partly through the Royal </b>fiefs<b> of this Kingdom, by the divine liberality, so that I could be equal to bearing these and other expenses, in vain indeed — even if everything else were at hand — should I attempt so great [things]. I have also, within this last decade, </b>built up a certain castle on this island<b> especially opportune for the instauration of Astronomy [</b>Uraniborg<b>], in whose middle and greater house certain Astronomical instruments are disposed in </b>two little towers toward South and North<b>, with their annexed appendages. Besides, not far outside the castle, on a certain little hill, I had another </b>subterranean observatory<b> [</b>Stjerneborg<b>] made within four years, in which </b>7 diverse instruments<b>, exquisite both in magnitude and in form, are kept — so that each of them occupies its own </b>crypt<b>, which can be opened above, and the observation can be directed through the instrument into whatever part of the heaven it please, the other machines not standing in the way. For that place is so elevated, as is almost the whole island, that the visible horizon lies open freely with no intervening impediment; on account of which peculiar convenience too I made so many expenses and drained so great labours on this island rather than elsewhere. But of that castle here built I send you here a </b>delineation<b> annexed, as to those [parts] which lie open above the Earth and are seen externally, so that in some way, from its inspection, you may be able to recognize that I conduct the matter seriously, as far as restoring Astronomy to honour and splendour, and in that matter spare no expense or labour. The </b>picture of those crypts<b>, which is exhibited with difficulty on a flat [surface], I have not even had drawn. But all these, together with the construction and use of the instruments, I have resolved to set before the eyes and sufficiently declare, with suitable delineations, in the first tome of the work on restoring Astronomy, which will contain (as I said) its </b>Mechanical<b> part. Meanwhile I communicate the </b>Design<b> [<i>Idea</i>] of certain instruments which have hitherto been depicted and engraved. I have also annexed some copies of certain </b>letters given to me by the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse<b> (most skilled in Astronomical matters among the Princes of Europe in this age — of whom you too made mention in this letter to Lord Rantzau) </b>and by his Highness's most erudite Mathematician, Christoph Rothmann<b>, together with our reply to each — in which very many [things] are referred making not a little for the whole Astronomical matter, and for instituting the observations themselves more rightly, and so for the re-integration of the art itself. And about this very question — concerning the </b>motion of the heaven<b>, [and] the </b>nature and quantity of the refractions<b> which appear in the position of the stars, and other occasions — it is copiously discussed between </b>Rothmann<b> and me. All which I wished to grant you to read and judge, asking lovingly that, when you have inspected and more diligently unrolled them, you would be willing to return [them] to me, lest they ever come into others' hands; and while you have them with you, that you communicate [them] to no one else — I ask you again and again. But above all I much desire from you that you would not be loath to write me, freely and amply, before winter, your judgment both about those [things] which are in that second book of the aethereal </b>Phenomena<b> [Phaenomeνων] now sent at the same time, and also [those] which are contained in these annexed letters. If anything has been erred in the transcription of those letters by my amanuenses, it will be yours to emend it by your skill; I had not leisure to read everything over. The most noble Rantzau wrote to me already before, and tries to persuade [me], by not-incongruous reasons, that I should </b>subjoin, at the end of our work on the more recent Phenomena of the aethereal World, both these letters of the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm and those of his Mathematician and other most erudite men too<b>, written to me about this whole Astronomical business, together with my reply to each — because they greatly illustrate the matter there treated and conduce greatly to the amplification of the whole art. In which matter I judged that his just and benevolent request must not be refused, relying also on your and other erudite [men]'s judgment and counsel. I have added besides to this letter the </b>restitution of the Sun's course<b>, deduced from my observations and demonstrations, for the next quinquennium, so that you may meanwhile obtain something of my emendations in the course of the celestial bodies, until time grant [me] to communicate more. The </b>Sun's Apogee<b> is here established near the end of the </b>6th degree of Cancer [♋], 3 degrees earlier than the Copernican<b>; and the </b>eccentricity<b> is presupposed [at] </b>2° 9⅓′<b>, of which the semidiameter of the eccentric is 60 — which much exceeds his [Copernicus's] finding. But why that great Copernicus deflected so greatly from the mark even in the retention of the Sun's course, I will show sufficiently elsewhere. But there is no [reason] for you to doubt that this our calculus differs not in the least, [that] which can fall under the senses, from the track which the aethereal Sun designates — as not only our observations of many years, but also the </b>Landgravian<b> [ones], especially after the renewed rectification of the instruments, attest. But how much it differs from the Alphonsine and Copernican computation, you yourself, a collation being made, will easily perceive — so [much so that] not even the Sun's motion, although it furnishes the norm for all the rest, has hitherto been sufficiently unravelled, much less [that] no fault lurks in the remaining Planets. But I see that I transgress the bounds of a letter, while I converse with you too avidly about these; it will therefore be yours to take in good part this prolixity, together with the book and the annexed papers. When the first and last tomes of the work which I now have in hand, on the more recent Phenomena of the aether, shall have been printed by our types, I will render you a sharer of them too. But that the first did not come forth earlier, the cause is the lack of the </b>Gallican privilege<b>, which I was daily expecting, to be added to the Imperial [one] obtained before. But since I have now received that Gallican [one], and shall shortly have the </b>English<b> too — the magnificent and most erudite man Lord </b>Daniel Rogers<b>, who was lately here with me, so procuring [it] with the most serene </b>Queen<b> [Elizabeth I] — nothing further will now stand in the way but that the first book be shortly brought to light. Meanwhile enjoy these, and fare as happily and as long as possible, most erudite </b>Peucer<b>, and most excellently deserving of the Republic of Letters; and do not cease to love me, most loving of you, in return (which you do), and to revisit [me] by letter before the threatening winter. But it will be most convenient for you to send them to the oft-said noble Lord </b>Heinrich Rantzau** in Holstein, who will not grudgingly procure that they be conveyed to me. Again, and often and much, farewell, man most worthy of long life and health.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 13th day of September, in the Year 1588. These letters, once read, are to be sent at once to Lord <b>John Dee</b>, whom, God willing, we shall meet tomorrow morning before [his] departure.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>); the letter is also preserved in P. J. Resen's <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i>. MS witnesses: <b>A</b> = Mikulov (Nikolsburg), II 72 (copy); <b>B</b> = Paris, BN, Français 13040, fol. 162 (copy). <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VII pp. 127–141</b>; every crux confirmed (anchors in the Editorial note), and additionally cross-corroborated by the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590, the undated Peucer letter of 1590, the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, and the Hájek letter of 3 Aug 1590.</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg) → <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Melanchthon's son-in-law, Wittenberg polymath, imprisoned 1574–86 for Crypto-Calvinism (the \"captivity\" named). The friendship is opened via <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, who had forwarded Peucer an excerpt of Tycho's system-diagram (<i>De recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, ch. 8) without its explanation — hence Peucer's \"doubts,\" which this letter resolves. (The later, undated Peucer letter is brahe-t_15900000001.)</li><li><b>The autobiographical genesis of the Tychonic system (the historic core).</b> Tycho narrates: a Leipzig boyhood (age 17, c. 1563) at the cross-staff, finding neither Copernican nor Alphonsine tables precise → years weighing both systems → the <b>ancients fault</b> (circular regularity about an <i>alien</i> centre; superfluous epicycles; the arbitrary Sun-binding of the planets) and the <b>Copernican fault</b> (the triple Earth-motion absurdity + Scripture) → the <b>decisive test by Mars</b>: if Mars at opposition is nearer Earth than the Sun, Copernicus wins. The <b>1582 opposition in Cancer</b> (Mars high in northern latitude) gave <b>Mars a greater parallax than the Sun</b> (with faster retrogression matching Copernican numbers); confirmed in <b>Venus</b> (incl. the rare 24 Feb 1587 morning-and-evening sighting). This drove him toward Copernicus — but the Earth-motion absurdity + Scripture barred it. Then, \"almost unexpectedly,\" the <b>geo-heliocentric arrangement</b>: the <b>Sun the centre of the five planets, itself carried annually round a resting Earth</b>, the Earth the centre of Sun, Moon, and 8th sphere — dissolving both systems' absurdities. The remaining scruple (the <b>Mars-orb intersecting the Sun's</b>) fell once the <b>comets</b> abolished the <b>solid orbs</b> (comets above the Moon, carried by no real orb; and two comets moving opposite the Sun disproved the Earth's annual motion). He found a peculiar Mars inequality (Pliny: Mars \"unobservable\") explicable only on his system.</li><li><b>The publication architecture.</b> The three \"ascititious-phenomena\" books (1 = the 1572 nova + foundations; 2 = the 1577 comet, carrying the system-diagram in ch. 8; 3 = three later comets) pave the way for the great <b>7-book Astronomical work</b>: (1) <b>Mechanics</b>/instruments (20+); (2) <b>triangles &amp; numbers</b> (prosthaphaeretic compendia); (3) the <b>8th sphere / fixed stars</b>; (4) <b>Sun &amp; Moon</b> (round the resting Earth); (5) <b>Venus &amp; Mercury</b> (round the Sun, not the Earth); (6) <b>Mars, Jupiter, Saturn</b> (round the Sun, enclosing Earth + elements); (7) the <b>latitudes</b>. Needs 5–6 years (esp. more <b>acronychal Saturn/Jupiter</b> observations; his hypotheses \"remove epicycles, eccentrics, and equants\"). <b>Copernicus's instruments</b> were inexact — Tycho owns Copernicus's <b>fir-wood triquetrum</b> (gift of an <b>Ermland canon</b>), its ink-marked divisions betraying his lack of diligence. New tables of mean motions + prosthaphaereses to fit Ptolemaic <i>and</i> recent observations; the dig at <b>Reinhold</b> (Prutenic Tables preface) — \"even great <b>Homer</b> nods.\"</li><li><b>The fluid-heavens exegesis (overlaps the undated 1590 Peucer letter).</b> Peucer's Scripture confirmation welcomed: <b>Isaiah 40:22</b> (Castellio's \"membrane/tent\" vs the old version), <b>Job 37:18</b> (heavens \"hard as steel\" = firmness of the heaven's <i>nature/revolution</i>, not its matter). <b>Francisco Valles</b> (<i>De sacra philosophia</i>, Lyon 1588, ch. 51) is refuted at length for defending <b>solid adamantine orbs</b> (from Job, with Pythagoras/Parmenides/Plato/Aristotle) and for claiming (ch. 1, from Gen 2:1–2 + Ecclesiasticus 3) that the <b>1572 nova was not new</b> but an old faint star magnified by a \"denser orb-region\" — Tycho's counter: no solid orbs exist; why then not other stars similarly lit, why only Hipparchus's and his own nova? <b>Aristotle</b> self-contradicts (minds rule the stars' motion ≠ solid orbs).</li><li><b>The system-diagram clarifications.</b> Why the diagram leaves a <b>gap below the 8th sphere</b>: the planets' orb-centres revolve annually with the Sun, so the anti-solar part is farther — the void fills as the Sun moves. The <b>\"unknown mathematician's\" objection</b> (Mars and Sun colliding where their orbs cross) — refuted: Mars always keeps the Sun as centre (a circumference never meets its centre); the Sun reaching the crossing-point does not cut Mars's path there but stays equidistant; best seen on a <b>mechanical model</b>. Particular inequalities handled by <b>two small circles</b> on each planet's orb (Venus/Mercury need extra gyrations); all circuits become <b>concentric</b> (cf. Copernicus's Moon-theory). <b>Note the Ursus allusion:</b> the hypotheses were \"communicated two years ago through a certain <b>fugitive servant</b> of mine\" — Tycho's coded reference to the theft underlying the Ursus priority war (cf. the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 and the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599).</li><li><b>Peucer's horoscope (a rare document).</b> From notes Tycho made at Leipzig ~24 years earlier (c. 1564) under Peucer's nativity-figure: <b>Mercury in Capricorn</b>, 3rd house, free of the Sun → keen profound talent; <b>Moon in Gemini</b>, 9th house, afflicted by <b>Saturn's square</b> and <b>Mars's trine</b> → \"some great evil\" via religion / talent / \"an illustrious woman\"; <b>Mars</b> (lord of the 7th) in the <b>12th</b>, in detriment, + Saturn's antiscion → <b>prison or long exile</b>, harm via studies; <b>freed \"about the 60th year\" by death or a Martial man</b> when the Moon's direction reaches Mars's sextile at 14° Leo in the MC; the <b>Dragon's Tail in the MC</b> → dejection from honours. (Peucer, b. 1525, was freed in <b>1586</b>, ~age 60 — a striking match.) Tycho's measured caveat: astrology \"within its bounds\" gives \"physical significations not inept,\" but \"many concurrent causes\" operate, and \"<b>above all, God, the first and universal cause, acts most freely</b>.\"</li><li><b>Uraniborg &amp; Stjerneborg (production-history).</b> 24 years of observations (the last 12 on Hven exact to the minute, instruments cross-agreeing). The illiberality of princes (\"no <b>King Alfonsos</b> now\"); Tycho's self-funding (estates + royal fiefs). <b>Uraniborg</b> (\"a castle … especially opportune for the instauration of Astronomy,\" instruments in two south/north towers) and the <b>subterranean Stjerneborg</b> (\"another observatory on a little hill … 7 instruments each in its own crypt, opened above\") built \"within this last decade\" / \"within four years\"; he encloses a <b>delineation of the castle exterior</b> (the crypts too hard to draw flat) and instrument <b>Designs</b> — all destined for the Mechanics tome. He sends the <b>Landgrave Wilhelm + Rothmann letters</b> (the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> core, incl. the heaven-motion/refraction debate) for Peucer's private judgment, and agrees to Rantzau's proposal to append them to <i>De recentioribus phaenomenis</i>.</li><li><b>The solar restitution &amp; privileges.</b> Encloses the 5-year <b>solar course</b>: <b>Apogee near 6° Cancer</b> (3° before Copernican), <b>eccentricity 2° 9⅓′</b> (ecc-radius 60) — exceeding Copernicus's value, matching Tycho's and the Landgravian observations. The <b>Progymnasmata</b> Tome I was delayed for want of the <b>Gallican privilege</b> (to join the Imperial one); now obtained, with the <b>English</b> one coming via <b>Daniel Rogers</b> (procured from <b>Queen Elizabeth I</b>), so the first book will soon appear.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Caspar Peucer</b>; <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Royal Vicar in Holstein, the intermediary/postal channel); <b>Copernicus, Ptolemy, \"Alphonsus,\" Pliny, Aristotle, Reinhold</b>; <b>Sebastian Castellio</b>; <b>Francisco Valles</b> (<i>Vallesius</i>, the Spanish physician-philosopher); <b>Pythagoras, Parmenides, Plato</b>; <b>Hipparchus</b>; <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV &amp; Christoph Rothmann</b>; <b>Daniel Rogers</b> (English diplomat); <b>Homer</b> (proverbial); the <b>Ermland canon</b> (donor of Copernicus's triquetrum); the unnamed <b>\"fugitive servant\"</b> (the Ursus theft) and the unnamed <b>\"other mathematician.\"</b> Places: <b>Wittenberg, Leipzig, Uraniborg/Hven, Stjerneborg, Lyon, Holstein, Ermland (Varmia)</b>.</li><li><b>Greek / Latin / astrological tags.</b> <i>προσθαφαιρέσεων</i> = prosthaphaereses (gen. pl.); <i>Phaenomeνων</i> = φαινομένων; <i>Radius Astronomicus</i> = cross-staff; <i>circelli</i> = the two small circles of Tycho's planet-models; <i>acronychus</i> = acronychal (opposition); <i>sub incudem veritatis</i> = \"to the anvil of truth.\" Astrological: ☿ Mercury, ☾ Moon, ♂ Mars, ♄ Saturn, ♋ Cancer, ♑ Capricorn, ♊ Gemini, ♌ Leo; □ = square, △ = trine, ⚹ = sextile; <i>M.C.</i> = <i>Medium Coeli</i> (Midheaven); <i>cauda Draconis</i> = the Dragon's Tail (descending node); <i>antiscion</i> = the reflected-degree point; <i>Geneseos schema</i> = nativity-figure; \"even great Homer nods\" = <i>aliquando bonus dormitat Homerus</i> (Horace, <i>Ars P.</i> 359).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <b>13 September 1588 O.S.</b> (Danish); matches the catalogue label. The Mars test \"1582\" and Venus \"24 February 1587\" are O.S.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> ~90 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, and from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588). One genuine <b>lacuna/anacoluthon</b> (after \"…circa Solem annua per totos orbes circumgyratione\") marked <i>…</i> — the sentence breaks off in the witness before the \"other mathematician\" passage; not resolved. OCR slips left/corrected: <i>obsolevierat</i> (read <i>obsoleverat</i>), <i>FRANCICUS</i> (read <i>Franciscus</i>), <i>Phaenomeνων</i> (Greek-Latin hybrid), <i>occationem</i> (read <i>occasionem</i>), <i>semidiamet.</i> (abbrev. <i>semidiameter</i>). None affects sense.</li><li><b>Addressee of the postscript: John Dee.</b> The closing instruction directs that the letters just read be forwarded at once to Dr John Dee, whom the writer expects, God willing, to meet the next morning before [Dee's] departure.</li><li><b>Implication for itinerary.</b> Dated at Uraniborg 13 September 1588, the postscript places Dee in the vicinity of Hven at that moment, awaiting a meeting the following morning before leaving. This is corroborated by Wilhelm Norlind's <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (Lund, 1970), which cites this very Peucer letter to conclude Dee 'must have been on Hven in Sept. 1588' (<i>Han torde ha varit på Hven i sept. 1588</i> — Tycho til Peucer sept. 1588), and by a later autograph note of Dee's own, dated Kassel 26 March 1589, recording his reception there of Christoph Rothmann — consistent with a continental itinerary passing near Denmark that autumn.</li><li><b>The plural 'literae'.</b> In the postscript, 'literae' is plural — 'these letters', not 'this letter'; the translation preserves the plural.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The honorific for John Dee (\"D. IOANNEM â DEE\", TBOO VII p.141) was rendered \"Dr John Dee\" in the English, inventing a doctorate Dee never held; \"D.\" (Dominum) is corrected to \"Lord\", matching the translation's own consistent rendering of the same abbreviation for D. Henricus Ranzovius and D. Daniele Rogersio earlier in the same letter. The identification of the addressee as John Dee is unaffected and remains correct.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, foundational, multi-strand letter (the autobiographical genesis of the world-system; the 7-book plan; the Valles/Scripture exegesis; Peucer's horoscope; the Uraniborg/Stjerneborg description; the solar restitution and privileges) rendered securely throughout; the register (warm, expansive, self-narrating) is preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, acronychal, eccentric/equant/epicycle, hypothesis, <i>salvare</i>/save, prosthaphaeresis, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i>, the astrological terms, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590, the undated Peucer letter of 1590, the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, and the Hájek letter of 3 Aug 1590 (the system, the Mars test, the fluid-heavens, the Valles/nova material, the comet doctrine) and with the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 and the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599 (the Ursus \"fugitive servant\" thread). The system-narrative, the 7-book contents, the solar parameters (apogee 6° Cancer, ecc. 2° 9⅓′), and the horoscope details are internally consistent and historically corroborated (Peucer's 1586 release ≈ \"age 60\"). The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VII pp. 127–141) and confirms every crux</b> — including the contested eccentricity ⅓ fraction and the <i>FRANCICUS VALLESIUS</i> (sic) reading — so the collation reservation that previously capped the grade is <b>lifted</b>. Two residues remain: (1) the genuine <b>lacuna</b> (the broken-off circumgyration sentence), reproduced and flagged, not resolved; (2) the heavy <b>OCR \"A\"-sigla</b> removal, declared. Neither obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>High confidence. The Latin is clean and complete, with no manuscript lacunae or editorial brackets, and the identification of 'D. IOANNEM a DEE' as the English mathematician John Dee is independently corroborated by two sources: Norlind's biography (Lund, 1970), which cites this exact letter as evidence Dee was on Hven in September 1588, and Dee's own dated Kassel inscription of March 1589 recording contact with Rothmann shortly afterward — consistent with a homeward continental journey passing through the region. The sole interpretive supplement is '[his]' before 'departure' (abitum), since the Latin does not specify whose departure is meant; context (Dee evidently about to leave) makes this the natural reading, and it is bracketed as supplied.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 16 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.127-142) in four chunks of 4. Collated the opening (HABEBAM iam dudum...), the closing and the John Dee forwarding postscript verbatim against the page images (not OCR); also checked every proper name (Ranzovius, Rogersio, Rothmann, Landgrave Wilhelm, Copernicus, Ptolemy, Reinhold, Castellio/Castalio, Vallesius, Pythagoras/Parmenides/Plato/Aristotle, Hipparchus, Pliny, Homer), every place-name (Wittenberg, Leipzig, Holstein, Lyon/Lugduni, Ermland/Warmia), every scriptural citation (Isaiah 40:22, Job 37:18, Genesis 2, Ecclesiasticus 3), and every number/date (age 17; 1582 Mars opposition in Cancer; Venus 24 Feb 1587; the 7-book plan; more than 20 instruments; quinquennium/sexennium; 24 years of observations, latter 12 on Hven; decade-old castle, 4-year subterranean observatory with 7 instruments; Sun's apogee at end of the 6th degree of Cancer, 3 degrees earlier than Copernicus, eccentricity 2°9⅓′, semidiameter 60; the horoscope's 14° Leo and age-60 detail; 13 September 1588). All of these check out exactly against the page images. The one defect is the \"D.\"→\"Dr\" title given to John Dee in the forwarding postscript, inconsistent with the letter's own treatment of the identical abbreviation for Ranzovius and Rogers.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). \"D.\" (Dominum) is the same honorific-abbreviation Tycho uses for D. HENRICI RANZOVIJ and D. DANIELE ROGERSIO earlier in this very letter, and the same translation renders both of those consistently as \"Lord\" — only for Dee is \"D.\" suddenly expanded to \"Dr\", inventing an academic credential (a doctorate Dee never held) with no warrant in the source or in the translator's own established convention. At: “These letters, once read, are to be sent at once to Dr John Dee, whom, God willing, we shall meet tomorrow morning befor”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the Latin of the closing postscript sentence against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.141, lines 26-27); it reads exactly as currently published (\"Hæ literæ lectæ mittantur statim ad D. IOANNEM â DEE...\"), so no Latin change was needed. Confirmed by direct search of the current English translation that \"D.\" is rendered \"Lord\" for Heinrich Rantzau (Ranzovius) and Daniel Rogers (Rogersio) at five other points in this same letter, establishing the translator's own convention that the Dee instance broke. Only the single word \"Dr\" was changed to \"Lord\"; nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Mikolov (Nicolsburg), II 72. Afskrift", "B Paris, Bibliotheque Nationale, Français 13040, fol. 162. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Caspar Peucer", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15890510002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890510002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Opens a correspondence via Rantzau's introduction and undertakes to address Peucer's doubts about the diagram of Tycho's new world-system hypothesis conveyed through Rantzau.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880913002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15880920001", "d": "1588-09-20", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 20 September 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.54</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO NOBILISSIMOQUE VIRO D. D. TYCHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.<br>S. P.</p><p>LITERAS tuas, amantissime TYCHO, misi Illustriss. Principis nostri Cancellario IACOBO BORDINGO, verum papyrus non est ad me transmissa; si ad me Illustriss. Princeps miserit, dabo operam, ut a coeli iniurijs tuta Haffniam mittatur. Intelligo autem BORDINGUM in Daniam a nostro Principe missum, quae res forte moram inijciet chartae. Egi quoque cum typorum fusore, sed is non vult in Daniam traijcere. Indicavi id mercatori, qui Francofurtum petebat, cui idipsum in mandatis dederas.</p><p>Bene fecisti, quod librum tuum de Cometa Anni 77 ad me miseris; legi maxima cum voluptate et admiratione, quae in priore voluminis parte ingenuose a te inventa et magno iudicio accommodata et Geometricis rationibus confirmata in lucem emisisti. Vicisti me hercule meam expectationem. Animadverto enim non solum in observando diligentiam, verum summam solertiam et ingenij acumen ex observatis alia colligendi et maximum iudicium in Geometricarum demonstrationum accommodatione. Hypothesis autem illa tua, quae circa Solem tanquam centrum reliquos Planetas, excepta Luna, constituit moveri, in primis mihi placet. Nam Terram ponit immobilem, centrumque universi tenere, nec motuum aequalitatem a non proprijs centris deducit. Per hanc si in opere tuo Astronomico Phaenomena tuearis, adhibitis Geometricis demonstrationibus et numeris, omne profecto tuleris punctum. Haec certa ad immortalitatem via, macte animo, ingenij tui vim hic explica, neque Herculeis istis laboribus frangi te patiaris, sed tanquam Atlas coelum humero ferens, profer in lucem, quae ego alijque unice amemus et posteritas nobiscum admiretur. Capite secundo emendationes longitudinum et latitudinum stellarum instituis ex declinatione stellae observatae et ascensione recta, ex tempore, quo pervenit ad meridianum, cognita. Verum exactam temporis notitiam dari, quo stella meridianum praeterit, merito quis in dubium vocet; quo dato et Solis loco exacte cognito, caetera nullam habent dubitationem. Et si de hisce constat, expeditam video rationem omnium stellarum, globo secundum suam inter se distantiam insertarum, longitudines latitudinesque depraehendendi. Fuerit enim Aequatoris et Eclipticae, ijs datis, non difficilis inscriptio. Sed animadverto in additione eiusdem capitis te vidisse hunc scrupulum, et ob id exactiores longitudines et latitudines stellarum tradere. Ex Veneris enim stella et lucidiore, quae est supra caput Arietis, certum et definitum locum determinasse, ut libro primo demonstratum scribis. Hinc, datis ab ea distantijs, reliquarum stellarum cum earundem declinatione, infallibili demonstratione, ex triangulorum doctrina petita, longitudines et latitudines stellarum definiuntur, inventa differentia ascensionis rectae, ut in Pegasi stella a te huius libri cap. 2 demonstratum est. Dehinc 3 capite Cometae locum apparentem ex distantias a duabus vel pluribus stellis, quarum loca sunt definita, erudite et laboriose investigasti. Nec minus erudite persecutus es rationes discernendi parallaxes longitudinis et latitudinis ex angulo, quem facit colurus solstitiorum cum meridiano, et alijs, quae ex observatis consequuntur. Sed quae de Lunae latitudine maxima mones, maiorem esse Ptolemaicam 15 scrupulis, miror, cum et eclipsium magnitudo et calculus huic latitudini respondeat, et REGIOMONTANUS, COPERNICUS, WERNERUS alijque insignes Mathematici Ptolemaicam servârint, quos dubium non est diligentes fuisse huius latitudinis observatores. PTOLEMAeI autem observatio me movet, quae habita est Alexandriae, cuius verticem Luna in maxima sua latitudine in Cancro visa est obtinere. In qua observatione neque parallaxis neque refractio, quae forte in Aegypto non habuit locum ob aëris puritatem, difficultatem aliquam in observationibus parere potuerunt.</p><p>Porro circulum descripsisse Cometam, quamvis nonnunquam quinque vel paucioribus scrupulis (quod nullius est momenti) a iusta peripheria ipsae observationes dissentiant, et eiusdem circuli cum Ecliptica et Aequatore inclinationem locumque et angulum intersectionis in Sagittario ingenuose invenisti et demonstrasti. Ex hoc motu, quem circularem etiam demonstrasti, et ordine motus, et quod motu Lunae tardior, confirmas aethereum, id est supra Lunam positum esse. Deinde ex distanttijs a stellis fixis in diversa altitudine Cometae supra finitorem observatis certius comprobas, habita ratione motus, supra Lunam positum esse. Non enim est ea distantiarum a stellis fixis differentia, quae foret, si sublunaris esset Cometa, ut ex parallaxibus in concavo orbis Lunae consideratis colligis. Quae omnia a te solerter et erudite nobis tradita sunt et apodictice ostensa. Videris autem non uno in loco asserere, omnes Cometas in aetherea regione positos fuisse. Memini vero, LYCOSTHENEM in eo, quem de Ostentis reliquit libro, sic enim (ut arbitror) opus inscribit, prodidisse quosdam Cometas conflagrantes incendium in subiecta Terra excitasse. Sed quid movet, ut materiam eius aetheream in aetherea regione constituas? Si enim aër diffusus per omnes mundi partes syderum est vector, quid aliam materiam quaerimus? Sin vero aetherea regio alterius sit materiae quam aër, undique tamen pervia, quid vetat scientiam motus in Cometis introducendi, hoc est, talem vim movendi a Deo inditam ex materia sublunari esse genitam, quae sursum in aethera vi syderum attracta sit, talem naturam motus consequi, donec plane extinguntur? Et certe ipsorum Cometarum interitus huic sententiae potius astipulatur, et quod veteres nonnulli, referente PLINIO et ACHYLLE STATIO, sydera humore vesci sunt arbitrati. Quod ob id commemoro, ut intelligamus tenue et lucidum, sive aëreum sive igneum coeleste sit corpus, aut quinta quaedam essentia, ut voluit ARISTOTELES, hasce exhalationes suscipere posse, et eum, qui scientiam motus, ut tuo utar verbo, indidit, eundem et hac sublunari materia, si non naturaliter et vi syderum coacta sit, effingere potuisse. Porro Cometas nunquam eosdem apparere, et interire, hos moveri, illos esse immobiles, et qui moventur, alios in antecedentia signorum, alios in consequentia, alios in septentrionem ferri, nonnullos ordinem quendam in motu servare, quosdam vero sine ordine vagari, rursus plerosque in septentrione conspici, ut PLINIUS refert, vagae inconstantisque materiae naturam potius referentes quam coelestis. Quod autem scribis, si recte tuam sententiam assequor: Cometam circa Solem moveri et posse sextante circuli a Sole distare, id non videtur tuae hypothesi consentaneum. Nam cum semidiameter sextantem circuli subtendat, videretur Cometa aliquando Terram contingere, si sui circuli revolutionem perficeret.</p><p>Quae in secunda libri parte a te pertractantur, in qua aliorum observationes et sententias excutis, praeter animadversiones in MAeSTLINUM non legi, quas obiter percurri. Sic enim, doctissime amantissimeque TYCHO, sentio, in ijs, quae Geometrica demonstratione recte sunt confirmata, non opus esse contrariarum sententiarum refutatione, nisi aliquae forent eius generis, quae subtili paralogismo possent lectori non imperito imponere. Et certe rudes ego eiusdem Cometae feceram observationes, ex quibus secutus REGIOMONTANI modum iam collegeram in aetherea regione positum fuisse, ut multis indicaveram, et ipsi quoque WITTICHIO, qui paulo post ad te se conferebat. Sed quid de re nunquam recurrente, imo intereunte, tantum laboris, obiecit mihi quidam, sumimus? ut et motum eius demonstremus circularem et eiusdem a Zodiaco latitudinem et loca eiusdem secundum latitudinem et longitudinem definiamus, aliaque ingenuose demonstremus, cum suffecerit ostendisse, in aetherea regione consistere; id enim controversum. Individuorum enim, quae generationi et corruptioni obnoxia sunt, non esse scientiam, nec secus, quam si halonis, qui circa Lunam aut aliam stellam conspicitur, magnitudinem et distantiam a Terra definiat; extingui enim cum ipso metheoro eius generis scripta. Quae Francofurti ad Oderam a M. CAMERARIO edita sunt de eodem Cometa, nondum vidi, si consequar, mittam.</p><p>Bene vale, mi TYCHO, et me mutuo ama.<br>Rostochij 20. Septembris Anno Domini 1588.<br>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS,<br>plane tuus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, distinguished mathematician, his lord and friend most worthy to be honoured.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Your letter, most beloved Tycho, I sent on to Jacob Bording, chancellor of our Most Illustrious Prince; but the paper [packet] has not been forwarded to me. If the Most Illustrious Prince should send it to me, I shall take pains that it be sent to Copenhagen secured against the injuries of the weather. But I understand that Bording has been sent into Denmark by our Prince — a circumstance that will perhaps cause delay to the document. I have also dealt with the type-founder, but he is unwilling to cross over into Denmark. I made this known to the merchant who was making for Frankfurt, the very man to whom you had given this same charge.</p><p>You did well to send me your book on the comet of the year [15]77; I read with the greatest pleasure and admiration the things that, in the earlier part of the volume, you ingeniously discovered, fitted together with great judgement, and confirmed by geometrical reasoning, and so brought into the light. By Hercules, you have surpassed my expectation. For I observe not only diligence in observing, but the highest skill and acuteness of mind in gathering further things from the things observed, and the greatest judgement in the fitting-together of the geometrical demonstrations. That hypothesis of yours, moreover, which makes the remaining planets, the Moon excepted, move about the Sun as their centre, pleases me especially. For it sets the Earth motionless and holding the centre of the universe, and it does not derive the uniformity of the motions from centres not their own. If by this [hypothesis] you maintain the phenomena in your astronomical work, with geometrical demonstrations and numbers applied, you will assuredly carry every point. This is the sure road to immortality: bravo! — here unfold the force of your genius, and do not let yourself be broken by those Herculean labours, but, like Atlas bearing the heavens on his shoulder, bring forth into the light the things that I and others may love beyond measure and that posterity may admire along with us. In the second chapter you set up corrections of the longitudes and latitudes of the stars from the declination of the observed star and the right ascension, [the latter] known from the time at which it reaches the meridian. But that exact knowledge can be had of the time at which a star passes the meridian, one might rightly call into doubt; this once given, and the place of the Sun exactly known, the rest admits of no doubt. And if these things are established, I see a ready method of ascertaining the longitudes and latitudes of all the stars set upon a globe according to their distance from one another. For, these things being given, the inscribing of the Equator and the Ecliptic [upon it] would not be difficult. But I observe that in the addendum to the same chapter you have seen this difficulty, and on that account deliver more exact longitudes and latitudes of the stars. For [you write] that from the star of Venus and from the brighter star which is above the head of Aries you have determined a fixed and definite place, as you write was demonstrated in the first book. Hence, the distances from it being given, together with the declination of those same [stars], the longitudes and latitudes of the remaining stars are defined by an infallible demonstration drawn from the doctrine of triangles, once the difference of right ascension has been found, as you have demonstrated in the case of the star of Pegasus in chapter 2 of this book. Thereafter, in the third chapter, you have learnedly and laboriously investigated the apparent place of the comet from its distances from two or more stars whose places are defined. No less learnedly have you pursued the methods of distinguishing the parallaxes of longitude and latitude from the angle which the colure of the solstices makes with the meridian, and from other things that follow from what has been observed. But as to what you advise concerning the Moon's greatest latitude — that the Ptolemaic [value] is greater by 15 minutes — I marvel at it, since both the magnitude of eclipses and the calculation answer to this [Ptolemaic] latitude, and Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, and other distinguished mathematicians have kept the Ptolemaic [value] — men whom there is no doubt to have been diligent observers of this latitude. But Ptolemy's observation moves me, which was made at Alexandria, whose zenith the Moon, at its greatest latitude, was seen to occupy in Cancer. In which observation neither parallax nor refraction — which perhaps had no place in Egypt on account of the purity of the air — could have given rise to any difficulty in the observations.</p><p>Further, that the comet described a circle — although now and then the observations themselves diverge by five minutes or fewer (which is of no moment) from a true circumference — and the inclination of that same circle with the Ecliptic and the Equator, and its place and angle of intersection in Sagittarius, you have ingeniously found and demonstrated. From this motion, which you have also demonstrated to be circular, and from the order of the motion, and from the fact that it is slower than the motion of the Moon, you confirm that it is ethereal, that is, placed above the Moon. Then, from the distances from the fixed stars observed at the comet's differing altitudes above the horizon, you prove more surely — account being taken of the motion — that it was placed above the Moon. For the difference of distances from the fixed stars is not such as it would be if the comet were sublunary, as you gather from the parallaxes considered in the hollow of the Moon's orb. All of which has been delivered to us by you skilfully and learnedly, and shown apodictically. You seem, however, in more than one place to assert that all comets have been placed in the ethereal region. But I remember that Lycosthenes, in the book he left on Portents — for thus, I think, he entitles the work — reported that certain comets, blazing, kindled a conflagration on the Earth beneath. But what moves you to place its ethereal matter in the ethereal region? For if the air, diffused through all parts of the world, is the bearer of the stars, why do we seek other matter? But if the ethereal region is of a matter other than air — yet everywhere passable — what forbids [us to suppose] that the principle of motion to be introduced into comets — that is, such a power of being moved imparted by God — has been begotten from sublunary matter, which, drawn upward into the ether by the force of the stars, takes on such a nature of motion until [these comets] are wholly extinguished? And certainly the very perishing of comets rather agrees with this opinion, and [so does] the fact that some of the ancients — as Pliny and Achilles Statius report — judged that the stars feed on moisture. This I mention for the reason that we may understand that the thin and shining heavenly body — whether airy or fiery, or some fifth essence, as Aristotle held — is able to take up these exhalations; and that he who imparted the principle of motion (to use your own word) could also have fashioned [it] from this sublunary matter, if it was not constrained naturally and by the force of the stars. Further, that comets never appear the same and [then] perish; that some are moved, others are motionless; and that of those which are moved, some are carried into the preceding [signs], others into the following, others to the north; that some keep a certain order in their motion, but others wander without order; and again that most are seen in the north, as Pliny reports — [all this] rather reflects the nature of wandering and inconstant matter than of celestial [matter]. But as to what you write — if I rightly grasp your meaning — that the comet is moved about the Sun and can be distant from the Sun by a sextant of a circle [60°], that does not seem consistent with your hypothesis. For since the semidiameter subtends a sextant of a circle, the comet would seem at some time to touch the Earth, if it completed the revolution of its circle.</p><p>The things treated by you in the second part of the book — in which you sift the observations and opinions of others — I have not read, except the strictures upon Maestlin, which I ran through in passing. For thus, most learned and most beloved Tycho, do I think: in those matters which have been rightly confirmed by geometrical demonstration, there is no need of a refutation of contrary opinions, unless there be some of that kind which by a subtle fallacy might impose upon a reader who is not unskilled. And indeed I had made rough observations of the same comet, from which, following the method of Regiomontanus, I had already gathered that it was placed in the ethereal region, as I had made known to many, and to Wittich himself too, who shortly afterward betook himself to you. But \"why,\" a certain man objected to me, \"do we take so much trouble over a thing that never recurs, nay, that is perishing — to demonstrate that its motion is circular, and to define its latitude from the Zodiac and its places according to latitude and longitude, and to demonstrate other things ingeniously — when it would have sufficed to show that it stands in the ethereal region? For that [alone] is in dispute. For of individual things which are subject to generation and corruption there is no science; no otherwise than if one were to define the magnitude of a halo, which is seen about the Moon or some other star, and its distance from the Earth — for writings of that kind are extinguished along with the meteor itself.\" What was published at Frankfurt an der Oder by Master Camerarius on the same comet I have not yet seen; if I obtain it, I shall send it.</p><p>Fare you well, my Tycho, and love me in return.<br>At Rostock, 20 September, in the year of the Lord 1588.<br>Heinrich Brucaeus,<br>wholly yours.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeck, c. 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born professor of medicine and mathematics at the University of Rostock, a senior figure of Tycho's Rostock student years and a lifelong scientific friend. The letter is his considered scholarly response to Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the \"book on the comet of the year [15]77\"), the second volume of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> programme, freshly off the Uraniborg press in 1588.</li><li><b><i>librum tuum de Cometa Anni 77</b></i> — Tycho's <b>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis liber secundus</b> (Uraniborg, 1588), on the comet of 1577. Brucaeus reviews its first part chapter by chapter (the stellar-coordinate method, ch. 2; the comet's apparent place, ch. 3; the parallax analysis) and the second part (the critique of rival authors), which he had only skimmed.</li><li><b>The world-system.</b> <i>Hypothesis … quae circa Solem tanquam centrum reliquos Planetas, excepta Luna, constituit moveri … Terram ponit immobilem, centrumque universi tenere</i> — Brucaeus's notably early, approving paraphrase of the <b>Tychonic (geo-heliocentric) hypothesis</b>: the planets (Moon excepted) circle the Sun, while the Earth stands still at the centre of the universe. Rendered literally; <i>hypothesis</i> kept.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Iacobus Bordingus</i> = <b>Jacob Bording</b>, chancellor to the Duke of Mecklenburg (whose father had been physician to King Christian III of Denmark — Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>); here the intermediary for forwarding Tycho's papers, then absent on a mission to Denmark. <i>Regiomontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Müller of Königsberg</b> (1436–1476), to whose comet-method Brucaeus says he himself adhered. <i>Copernicus</i>; <i>Wernerus</i> = <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg (1468–1522). <i>Maestlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), Tübingen astronomer, target of Tycho's strictures in the book's second part. <i>Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Breslau mathematician who passed from Rostock to Hven. <i>Lycosthenes</i> = <b>Conrad Lycosthenes</b> (Wolffhart, 1518–1561), author of the <i>Prodigiorum ac ostentorum chronicon</i> (the \"book on Portents\"). <i>M. Camerario … Francosurti ad Oderam</i> = <b>Master [Elias] Camerarius</b> at <b>Frankfurt an der Oder</b>, who published on the comet of 1577 (Norlind, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, names Elias Camerarius of Frankfurt a.d. Oder among the comet's commentators). <i>Plinius</i> = <b>Pliny the Elder</b> (<i>Naturalis Historia</i> II, on comets). <i>Achilles Statius</i> = <b>Achilles Tatius</b>, the ancient commentator on Aratus's <i>Phaenomena</i> (the \"<i>Isagoge</i>\"), regularly cited in this Latinised form. <i>Aristoteles</i> = <b>Aristotle</b> (the doctrine of the <i>quinta essentia</i>, the fifth element/quintessence).</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>longitudo / latitudo (stellarum)</i> → <b>(celestial) longitude / latitude</b>; <i>declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>ascensio recta</i> → <b>right ascension</b>; <i>meridianus</i> → <b>meridian</b>; <i>parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>Ecliptica / Aequator</i> → <b>Ecliptic / Equator</b>; <i>colurus solstitiorum</i> → <b>colure of the solstices</b>; <i>altitudo … supra finitorem</i> → <b>altitude above the horizon</b> (<i>finitor</i>, the \"bounder,\" = the horizon); <i>orbis Lunae</i> → <b>the Moon's orb</b>; <i>sublunaris / aethereus</i> → <b>sublunary / ethereal</b>; <i>globus</i> → <b>globe</b>; <i>semidiameter</i> → <b>semidiameter</b> (here = the radius of the comet's supposed circle). <i>scrupulum / scrupulis</i> in the astronomical passages = <b>minute(s) [of arc]</b> (the standard early-modern term for arc-minutes), rendered \"minutes\"; the same word in <i>vidisse hunc scrupulum</i> carries its ordinary sense \"difficulty / scruple\" and is so rendered — the pun is noted here rather than forced in the English.</li><li><b><i>sextante circuli</b></i> — \"a <b>sextant of a circle</b>,\" i.e. one-sixth of a circle = <b>60°</b>; glossed in brackets in the translation. Brucaeus's geometrical objection: if the comet can stand 60° from the Sun while circling it, and \"the semidiameter subtends a sextant\" (i.e. the circle's radius equals the chord of 60°, which it does), the comet's circle would be so large that the comet would at some point graze the Earth.</li><li><b><i>Lunae latitudo maxima … maiorem esse Ptolemaicam 15 scrupulis</b></i> — Tycho had argued that the Moon's greatest latitude differs from Ptolemy's value; Brucaeus reads this as Tycho making the Ptolemaic value greater <b>by 15 [arc-]minutes</b> and resists it, citing the agreement of eclipse magnitudes and of Regiomontanus, Copernicus and Werner with the Ptolemaic figure, and Ptolemy's own Alexandria observation of the Moon at the zenith in Cancer. The numeric claim is reproduced exactly as written, not adjudicated.</li><li><b><i>scientiam motus … indidit / scientia motus … introducendi</b></i> — \"the <b>principle of motion</b> … imparted / to be introduced\" (lit. \"knowledge/science of motion\"); Brucaeus twice flags this as <b>Tycho's own term</b> (<i>ut tuo utar verbo</i>, \"to use your own word\"), so the phrasing is kept close to the Latin rather than modernised.</li><li><b><i>halo / metheoro</b></i> — <i>halo[n]</i> = a <b>halo</b> about the Moon or a star; <i>meteorum</i> = a <b>meteor</b> in the Aristotelian sense (an atmospheric/sublunary appearance), the category the objector assigns to comets. Kept as period terms.</li><li><b><i>Individuorum … non esse scientiam</b></i> — the Aristotelian maxim \"<b>of individuals [particulars] there is no science</b>\" (<i>scientia</i> being of universals), here voiced by the unnamed objector against Tycho's laborious treatment of a unique, perishing object.</li><li><b>Textual crux — <i>in antecedentia lignorum</i>.</b> The Source block preserves the printed reading <i>lignorum</i> (\"of woods/timbers\"), which is meaningless here. The required word is <b>signorum</b>: <i>in antecedentia / in consequentia signorum</i> is the fixed astronomical idiom for motion \"<b>into the preceding / the following [signs of the zodiac]</b>\" (i.e. retrograde vs. direct in zodiacal order); the same Brucaeus passage as paraphrased in Tycho's printed reply reads <i>in antecedentia signorum</i> (corpus: <i>Tycho Brahes Breve</i>; cf. <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV). The translation therefore reads \"into the preceding [signs]\"; the printed <i>lignorum</i> is retained above per protocol §4.2 and explained here.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> No Greek script and no astronomical glyphs occur in this letter; the planet and zodiac names (Venus, Aries, Pegasus, Cancer, Sagittarius) are written out in the Latin and carried over directly.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Standard humanist-Latin print conventions: <i>ij</i> for double-<i>i</i> (<i>iniurijs, proprijs, distantijs, alijs</i>), <i>ae</i>-ligature (<i>PTOLEMAeI, BRUCAeUS, MAeSTLINUM</i>), <i>Francosurti</i> = <i>Francofurti</i> (long-s/f confusion in print), <i>typorum fusore</i> = \"type-founder,\" <i>finitorem</i> (horizon). <i>distanttijs</i> (with doubled <i>t</i>) and <i>extinguntur</i> (for <i>extinguuntur</i>) are printer's forms, left as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Rostochij 20. Septembris Anno Domini 1588</i> = \"at <b>Rostock</b>, 20 September 1588\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Lutheran Mecklenburg, like Denmark, kept the Julian calendar in 1588). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label <i>\"1588. 20. september. Rostock\"</i>; no discrepancy. No Roman-calendar idiom is used.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>Salutem plurimam</i> → \"Heartiest greeting\" (§5.5); <i>Bene vale … et me mutuo ama</i> → \"Fare you well … and love me in return\"; <i>plane tuus</i> → \"wholly yours.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.141-144: 'Intelligo' (not 'Intellige,' p.141) restores Brucaeus's first-person report rather than an imperative addressed to Tycho, with a corresponding change to the English mood; 'signorum' (not 'lignorum,' p.143), 'Francofurti' (not 'Francosurti,' p.144), and 'adhibitis' (not 'adbibitis,' p.142) correct three further single-letter corruptions in the source Latin with no effect on the existing translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one substantive textual point, the printed <i>lignorum</i> for <i>signorum</i> in \"into the preceding [signs],\" is an unambiguous and independently corroborated emendation (flagged above, printed form retained), and the <i>scrupulum</i> pun (arc-minute vs. \"difficulty\") is noted rather than collapsed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, visually, against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.141(tail)-144 (page images; slice p.5=TBOO p.145 is the start of the next letter and was excluded as irrelevant). Checked the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, and place-name (Bording, Jacob Bordingo, Rostock/Rostochij, Frankfurt/Francofurti, Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, Ptolemy/Alexandria, Lycosthenes, Pliny, Achilles Statius, Aristotle, Wittich, Camerarius, the '15 scrupulis' and 'quinque... scrupulis' figures, the 20 September 1588 date). Found four TEXT_ERROR divergences between our published source_text and Dreyer's print; one of them (Intellige/Intelligo) changes grammatical mood and the English translation follows the corrupted reading, turning Brucaeus's own first-person remark into an imperative addressed to Tycho — a defect a scholar collating against Dreyer would flag immediately. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, or HEDGE_LOSS found; the translation is otherwise careful and faithful.</p><p><b>4 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Our source Latin has the imperative 'Intellige' (understand!) where Dreyer prints the first-person indicative 'Intelligo' (I gather/understand). The English follows the corrupted mood, turning Brucaeus's own report into a command addressed to Tycho. Dreyer prints: <i>Intelligo autem BORDINGUM in Daniam a nostro Principe missum, quae res forte moram inijciet chartae. (TBOO VII p.141)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source Latin corrupts 'signorum' (of the zodiac signs) into the nonsensical 'lignorum' (of woods/timbers). The translator silently repaired the sense with a bracketed '[signs]' rather than flagging the underlying source corruption. Dreyer prints: <i>hos moveri, illos esse immobiles, et qui moventur, alios in antecedentia signorum, alios in consequentia (TBOO VII p.143)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). The place-name 'Francofurti' (Frankfurt) is corrupted to 'Francosurti' in our source text (f→s); the translator rendered the correct city regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>Quae Francofurti ad Oderam a M. CAMERARIO edita sunt de eodem Cometa, nondum vidi (TBOO VII p.144)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Single-letter corruption (b for h) in our source text ('adbibitis' for 'adhibitis'); no effect on meaning and the translation is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>adhibitis Geometricis demonstrationibus et numeris (TBOO VII p.142)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All four TEXT_ERROR defects were verified directly against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.141-144 (the letter's pp.141-145 slice, pages 1-4 sufficed since all four cited passages fall within them). Each corruption and its Dreyer reading were visually confirmed letter-for-letter. Only the first defect required a corresponding English change (mood restored from imperative to first-person indicative); the other three were orthographic corruptions with no effect on sense or translation. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.54."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 13, "prev": "brahe-t_15871028001", "next": "brahe-t_15881104001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15840000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15881104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports forwarding a letter to Bording and the type-founder's refusal to cross to Denmark, and praises the first part of the comet-of-1577 book.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15880920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15881013001", "d": "1588-10-13", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 13 October 1588 [O.S.] (dateline *\"13. Calendas Octobris\"*)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist; printed orthography with v/u), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VI,<i> Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(1596), Liber I · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed tradition)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> A capital document in the history of cosmology: <b>Christoph Rothmann's</b> long reply to Tycho, defending three theses — (i) the <b>matter of the heavens is pure air</b>, not a distinct aether (a sustained optical argument from refraction, citing <b>Alhazen</b> and <b>Witelo</b>); (ii) <b>comet tails are of the head's own matter, self-luminous, and do not point at the Sun</b> (proved from the Landgrave's record of the <b>1558 comet</b> in Leovitius's <i>Ephemerides</i>); and (iii) an unreserved <b>defence of Copernicanism</b> against Tycho's \"new hypotheses,\" which Rothmann reads as merely \"inverted Copernicus.\" Printed-edition text (clean), preserving genuine <b>v/u orthography</b> (<i>vt, vbi, RefractionemA→Refractionem</i>), reproduced as printed. Two declared interventions in the source block: (1) the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> on ~30 words are removed as <b>non-textual</b> (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, and to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599); (2) one transcription slip <i>obseruamus</i> for <i>observatus</i> (in the 1558-comet quote) is left in the source and rendered correctly. Greek (<i>φιλαληθέια</i>, <i>ἀυτόματον</i>), the planet/zodiac glyphs (♍ ♈), and Rothmann's coinage <i>Homocentrepicyclepicyclis</i> are reproduced as transcribed. <b>Dateline crux (named, not smoothed):</b> the source reads <i>\"Cassellis 13. Calendas Octobris, Anno salutis M.D.LXXXIIX\"</i> (= 1588). Taken strictly as Roman dating, <i>a.d. XIII Kalendas Octobris</i> = <b>19 September</b>; the catalogue/edition resolves it to <b>13 October</b> (the form is anomalous — \"Calendas\" without \"ante diem,\" a known looseness/printer's rendering). The header gives the catalogued <b>13 October</b> with the literal dateline shown; the discrepancy is flagged in the Apparatus. As a Lutheran-Hesse letter it is <b>Old Style</b> (Julian). <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VI pp. 151–161</b> (PDF pp. 165–175), <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum Liber Primus</i>. <b>All citations and numerals confirmed</b>: Alhazen 7.2/7.4 + Witelo 10.3 + the 52-radii / 1:100000 / 1:140608 figures (p. 151); Witelo 10.10/10.8 + refraction 1½′/3′ (p. 152); Euclid I.21 (p. 153); twilight 16½° / 24° (p. 154); the 1558-comet positions (pp. 155–156). The dateline is verbatim Dreyer: <b><i>\"Caßellis 13. Calendas Octobris … M. D. LXXXIIX\"</b></i> (p. 161) — Dreyer himself prints no \"ante diem,\" confirming the anomaly the apparatus flags; the \"13 October\" resolution is the catalogue's. <b>Two OCR/print notes:</b> (a) in the second Alhazen citation (book 7, ch. 7), the <i>chapter</i> digit did not survive OCR (book 7 is legible); the ch.-7 reading is secure on the surrounding citation-series but cannot be machine-confirmed from the OCR alone; (b) <i>obseruamus est</i> in the 1558-comet quote is <b>Dreyer's own printed reading</b> (he daggers it †) — see the reframed intervention-note below.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTVTE SAPIENTIA INGENIOSA ERVDITAQVE DOCTRINA, CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNVDSTRVP, EQVITI DANO, MATHEMATICORVM NOSTRO SECULO OMNIUM PRAeSTANTISSIMO, ETC. DOMINO SUO CUM OBSERUANTIA COLENDO, ETC. S. P.</p><p>ACCEPTATISSIMAe mihi fuerunt vtraeque tuae literas, Nobilissime ac Praestantissime D. TYCHO, animoque meo longe gratissimae, et priores cum Tomo secundo Phaenomenon Caeli recentiorum, quas 11. Iulij a Gellio Sasceride accepi, et posteriores, quas iam. Verum ad has tibi prius respondebo, ad priores inferius. De materia igitur Caeli vt adhuc paucis tecum agam, peto mihi concedas. Ab ea enim diuelli non possum. Et nisi haec quaestio a nobis decisa fuerit, a nullo vnquam, siue Theologo siue Physico decidetur. Non enim Deus in verbo suo quicquam de hac reuelauit, cum nihil ad salutem nostram pertineat, nec eiusmodi disputationes, quas pauci admodum etiam inter Doctos intelligunt, immiscere voluit sacris literis, quae omnibus omnino hominibus, Indoctis pariter ac Doctis, Rudibus pariter ac Ingeniosis scriptae sunt, vt et ipse Christus Ioan. 3. ad Nicodemum testatur. Physici quoque quomodo de hac quicquam certi scire poterunt? Tantum enim de eiusmodi sublimibus et tam procul a nobis dissitis rebus scimus ac intelligimus, quantum per Doctrinam Triangulorum et Mathematicas demonstrationes inuenimus. Sine his qui de eiusmodi rebus disserit, vanissimus est prorsusque delirat. Nec ego Aëris materiam vnquam introduxissem, nisi defectus Refractionum et Demonstrationes opticae me ad id impulissent. Quousque enim verum erit, quod diuersa Diaphana efficiant Refractionem, eousque etiam mea assertio vera erit. Nec huic quicquam derogant ea quae a te afferuntur. Quod enim a Physicis traditur, in Caelo esse nihil elementare, nihil corruptibile, nihil variabile, sed aeterna omnia: tam facile negatur quam asseritur. Probandum enim est, cur ita sit et vnde illud sciatur, non pro hypothesi et axiomate concesso assumendum. Quod si cum Aristotele ideo id esse dixeris, quoniam nulla mutatio vnquam in corporibus caelestibus vel potius in magnitudine et claritate Siderum depraehensa sit, nihil dixeris. Nam nec magnitudo Terrae vnquam mutata est, et tamen in Terra et circa Terram sunt corruptibilia omnia. Nihil igitur magnitudinis perseuerantia in Stellis probat. Nec tamen interim nego, ipsa corpora caelestia esse perfectissima, simplicissima, nullique prorsus corruptioni aut generationi obnoxia; sed tantum de circumfusa materia loquor, quam non impurum sed purissimum dico esse Aërem, qui tamen ab Aëre puro infra Lunam nihil differat. Nam et hic nihil claritati Stellarum detrahit. Nec inde sequitur, si ipsa corpora sint perfectissima et ab Elementis diuersa, etiam circumfusam materiam perfectam esse oportere: sed potius, cum Cometae in ea generentur, crescant, decrescant, et tandem etiam euanescant ac dissipentur, tantaeque interdum sint magnitudinis, vt etiam quinquaginta Gradus occupare possint; non vsque adeo a mutatione et corruptione erit immunis, nec tam aeterna, diuina et imperscrutabilis vt fingitur. Nec si huic rationi et superiori principio Optico innitor, Caelum (si modo circumfusa illa materia Caelum appellanda est; quanquam non ignorem, Aërem quoque interdum Caelum appellari.) Non inquam Caelum ideo aliqua labe aspersero, sed potius illud a vana et praeter omnem causam et experientiam otiose afficta substantia liberauero, veramque sententiam substituero. Nec sonitus vllus e concitato Siderum motu in Aëre illo puro ciebitur. Quod enim Aër illisus et impulsus sonitum edat, id in hoc crasso a vento experti sumus (sonitum enim qui ratione vacui fit, huc non quadrare manifestum est) nec id caret ratione. Quoniam enim Aër hic crassus semper aliquid aqueum in se continet, Aqua vero sit eiusmodi res, quae penetrationem subitam non admittat: non immerito Aër quoque crassus in impulsione et illisione resistet quodammodo et sonitum edet. At idem in puro quoque Aëre accidere, nemo facile probauerit. Eum enim esse tenuissimum ac rarissimum, satis inde liquet, quod hic crassus, licet tenuissimus putetur, tamen radios Solis etiam serenissimo tempore non irreflexos et sine lumine transmittat; Illum autem purum radij Solis absque vllo lumine et reflexione penetrent, vt ex Crepusculis manifestum est. Ideo ob tenuitatem et raritatem purus ille Aër illidi et impelli non poterit, sed absque vllo obstaculo cedet, adeoque perpetuitati reuolutionis caelestium corporum et puritati eorum longo vsu temporeque non detrahet, multo minus atterendo constantissimam harmoniam labefactabit. Quod si purissimus ille Aër hoc posset, vererer vt a vehementissimis ventorum flatibus in hoc crasso Aëre iamdudum tota Terra esset attrita et absumta, ac etiam de suo loco mota. Si item tantus sonus a puro illo Aëre excitari posset, vt nostrum auditum percelleret, omnes Superos prae nimio metu iamdudum suffocatos putarem. Multo enim magis sonitus crassi huius Aëris a vehementissimis ventis excitatus a Superis perciperetur, quam ille a nobis. Sed tamen memini, fuisse aliquando in loco quinque milliaribus hinc sito vehementissimum ventum, adeo vt aedificia et arbores euerteret; de cuius tamen sono ego hîc nihil audiebam. Adeo, quae vera sunt, coniecturis euerti non possunt. De proportione quoque Elementorum scis quomodo PHILIPPVS MELANCHTHON in sua Physica Peripateticos reprehendat, veramque Aristotelis sententiam doceat. Et sane cum concedis, vsque ad Lunam Aërem elementarem esse, eo ipso proportionem Elementorum tollis. Cum enim vsque ad infimam Lunam, vt te inuenisse in secundo Tomo mihi transmisso testaris, sint Semidiametri Terrae 52. plus centies millies Aër maior erit ipso Terrae et Aquae globo simul. Quae autem proportio est 1. ad 100000. vel potius 1. ad 140608. vt interim de Terra ab Aqua separata taceam? Non igitur proportio Elementorum obstat. Sed nec mea ipsius demonstratio, vt obijcis. Non enim volui, in Altitudine 30. Grad. nullam amplius fieri posse Refractionem, sed hoc tantum tanquam petitum seu postulatum ex primis tuis literis ad Illustrissimum Principem nostrum missis assumsi, vt demonstrare possem, si detur altitudo, in qua Refractiones desinant, dari etiam quanto spatio radij Solis Aërem crassum irrefracti penetrent: Eademque demonstratio permanebit, licet Refractiones vix in 40. Altitudinis Gradu desinant. Cumque ex ea proueniat, vapores circa Horizontem sextuplo proxime densiores esse quam in Altitudine 30. partium, sequitur inde, minorem proportionem Refractiones efficere non posse in ea Altitudine; multo minus spatium eiusdem medij circa Horizontem, irrefracte penetratum maius esse eo quod refracte penetratur. Vide igitur an diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano ea demonstratio applicari possit. Quae enim multiplicatio diuersi Diaphani aut potius Aëris hîc esse potest? Pone enim combinationem illam mutuam Aetheris et Aëris fieri in distantia 30. Semidiam. Terrenarum â superficie Terrae, in Horizonte quae erit multiplicatio? Nondum enim in Horizonte 31. Semidiam. erunt. Scilicet radij tot Semidiametros Terrenas diuersi Diaphani irrefracti penetrare poterunt, vnicam autem Semidiametrum non possint? Quod vero existimas, etiamsi concedatur, quod nulla fiat Refractio in loco proxime sublunari, non tamen inde sequi, vt Caelum (si modo circumfusa illa materia, vt antea quoque dixi, Caelum appellanda est: de ea enim loquimur,) vt inquam Caelum ex Aëre Elementari constet: Illud longe aliter se habet. Quandocunque enim dantur diuersa Diaphana, datur etiam Refractio extra perpendicularem, et quandocunque Refractio extra perpendicularem tollitur, tollitur etiam diuersitas Diaphanorum, vt manifestum est ex capit. 2. et 4. Alhazeni lib. 7. et ex Vitellion. lib. 10. P. 3. et passim ex tota Optica. Diuersa enim media diuersimode transmittunt imagines rei visae ad oculum, et cum primum medium fuerit subtilius et rarius quam secundum, imago facilius et velocius transmittitur per primum medium quam per secundum, et cum ad secundum peruenit, retardatur quasi, vt ex dictis Opticis notum est. Vnde quoque hîc locum habere non potest ea excusatio, quod media illa diuersa in mutua connexione sibi inuicem, vt sine causa fingis, assimilentur, et successiue alterum in alterum mutetur. Erit enim nihilominus in ea mutua combinatione et assimilatione punctum, in quo imago Stellarum quasi retardetur, quod est punctum Refractionis. Saepius hyeme expertus sum, cum aperta fenestra starem in medio Obseruatorij in calido Aëre, rem visam in frigido Aëre consistentem refracte videri. Hîc certe nec similitudo, nec successiua combinatio et assimilatio Refractionem tollere potuerunt. Si vero media illa per se tam similia sunt, vt inter illa nulla sit differentia, cur diuersa appellas? cur non etiam sublunarem illum purum Aërem Aethera, seu, vt Plinius loquitur, Aethereum spiritum appellas? Ita enim tecum non pugnarem. Tantum enim id contendo, non esse diuersa Aetheris et Aëris puri Diaphana, cum nulla ab ijs generetur Refractio. Sed nescio quomodo adeo inhaereas tuae sententiae, vt etiam praeter omnem experientiam concedas, Refractionem Aetheris et Aëris durare vsque ad verticem, cum tamen eam nunquam Instrumentis senseris. Certe hac ratione quiduis confingere licet. Sed tamen sequeretur inde, nullam Stellam praeter eas, quae verticem transeunt et in vertice sunt, vero lumine videri, verum confuso et debilitato. Omnis enim Refractio debilitat lucem et colorem rei visae, vt demonstrat Alhazen lib. 7. capit. 7. et Vitellio, lib. 10. P. 10. Cum igitur diuersitas ea ex collatione Stellae verticalis ad reliquas a vaporibus liberas non depraehendatur, nec Refractio concedi poterit. Verum tu hîc potius in omnia diuersa. Ais enim, has Aetheris et Aëris Refractiones infra 30. Gradum esse sensibiles, in altiori vero situ insensibiles, et tamen postea addis, Refractionem Solis, quam in Altitudine 30. Grad. inuenisti 1½. Minutorum, per solos vapores vix excusari posse. Vtinam diceres, eam totam esse a diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano, demonstrarem vtique, in Altitudine 45. Grad. eam adhuc debere esse vnius proxime Minuti. Ipso enim vero verius est, si infra 30. Gradum sensibilis aliqua Refractio Aetheris et Aëris concedatur, eam in altiori situ non tam subito insensibilem fieri posse. Nam ijsdem medijs permanentibus non tam subito mutari possunt Refractiones ipsae, cum earum Anguli non tam subito decrescant, quinimo siue ab Aëre ad Aquam, siue ab Aëre ad vitrum, siue ab Aqua ad vitrum, siue contra fiant, eandem fere in crescendo et decrescendo retineant proportionem, vt ex Tabula in 8. P. 10. Vitellion. posita videre est. Ad quam etiam respiciens scripsi in superioribus literis, si circa Horizontem detur Refractio Solis 3. Minut. a diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano proueniens, dari eam in Altitudine 45. Graduum 1½. Minuti. Etsi enim aliqua, tamen non admodum dissimili proportione decrescent Refractiones ipsae et Refractionum Anguli; Quod, si mihi tempus non deesset, facile Calculo examinarem. Quod autem circa Horizontem tam subito decrescunt Refractiones, id a meris vaporibus est, qui circa Terram et crassiores sunt, et, vt demonstraui, multiplicantur, et etiam a radijs a latere petuntur. Aliâs si eadem media manerent, eâdem quantitate eodemque modo disposita, tam subita mutatio Refractionum accidere non posset, vt demonstrari potest. Sed restat adhuc demonstratio, quam nemo vnquam infringere poterit. Si enim est Refractio a diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano proueniens, ea necessario maior erit in Stellis Fixis, quae longius distant, quam in Sole, qui propior est, vt interim de Luna taceam. Nam in Triangulo manente vnico Angulo et vnico Latere, reliquis lateribus productis, Angulus reliquus ad manens latus maior redditur, per 21. P. 1. Euclid. Necessario enim idem Refractionis Angulus in eâdem Altitudine manebit, cum maneant eadem media, nec alius poterit esse Refractionis Angulus in Sole aut Luna, quam in Fixis. Ex quo manifestissimum est, si ponas, Refractionem in Sole in certa aliqua Altitudine insensibilem fieri, eam in Stellis Fixis in eadem Altitudine adhuc sensibilem esse oportere. Cum igitur per Obseruationes non hoc sed potius contrarium te ipso teste reperiatur: Refractio a diuerso Aetheris et Aëris Diaphano generari non poterit: Et propterea, vt saepius iam ex Opticis probaui, Diaphana Aetheris et Aëris diuersa esse non possunt sed eadem. Si igitur tu purum illum Aërem Caelum appellare volueris, nihil pugno, tantum ne purus Aër a caelesti differre statuatur, adeoque diuersa Diaphana introducantur, cum vt alterum altero subtilius sit, defectus Refractionis non permittat. Quod autem ex Chymicis affers, latere in terrenis rebus quintam quandam essentiam, quam tu caelestem appellas, illud et credo et iucundissime cogito. Verumtamen inde non sequitur, quod haec quinta essentia eadem sit cum illa materia quae Planetis est circumfusa, sed potius eam â Sole generari. Quemadmodum enim Sol Microcosmi, vt iucundissime doces, ex sanguine et attracto Aëre (ex quo propter respirationem videtur ingenita sua natura subtiliorem partem retinere, reliquam respuere.) Quemadmodum inquam Sol Microcosmi ex Aëre et sanguine in dextro thalamo praeparato spiritum vitalem in sinistro thalamo generat; Ita et Sol totius vniuersi ex Terra, Aqua et Aëre quintam illam essentiam in rebus naturalibus generare videtur. Aut si haec essentia eadem est cum illa Planetis circumfusa materia, eadem erit et cum puro Aëre, propter adductas demonstrationes. Haec paulo fusius, quam initio proposueram, de hac adhuc materia ad te perscribere volui, non quidem studio contradicendi, sed ex mera φιλαληθέια. Nec quicquam certi de generatione et materia Cometarum afferri poterit, nisi haec quaestio fuerit decisa. Sed de hac pluribus, Deo dante, in nostris Stellarum Fixarum Obseruationibus agam, omissa tamen huius nostrae dissensionis mentione, ne haec veritati rei forte quid detrahat. Nec etiam tuî, nisi sine reprehensione possim, mentionem facio, sed tunc potius omisso nomine res ipsas tracto.</p><p>Nunc ad Philosophica illa. Sed prius adhuc vnum. Satis mirari non possum, quod initium Crepusculi matutini circa Aequinoctia inueneris Sole vix 16½. Grad. infra Horizontem in circulo verticali depresso, cum ego finem Crepusculi vespertini circa Aequinoctium vernum hîc inuenerim Sole 24. Grad. infra Horizontem depresso, ita tamen, vt nullum amplius albedinis vestigium appareret. Tu igitur idem quoque tenta in vespertinis Crepusculis, vbi has Obseruationes exactiores esse arbitror propterea quod facilius animaduerti possit, quando vestigium albicantis Aëris desinat, quam quando incipiat, dubitante semper Obseruatore, an vestigium illud albedinis pertineat ad Crepusculum matutinum. Ego quoque in matutinis idem experiar. Ad dulcissima igitur illa ex intima et profunda Philosophia hausta nunc venio. Reliqua enim responsione non indigere videntur, nec de Eclipsibus quicquam hîc propter Caelum pluuiosum obseruare potui. Gratias igitur ago tibi quas possum maximas, quod me in secretioribus istis tam luculenter erudire dignatus es, atque vt id in posterum quoque facias, a te etiam atque etiam maiorem in modum peto. Cum ea legerem, pene exclamaui gaudio. Quam longe veriora sunt et pulchriora, quam quae in Aristotele traduntur. Sed tu fac vt plura intelligam. Scripsisti in superioribus literis cum Rami mentionem faceres, omnia constare numeris, ponderibus, et mensura. Hoc igitur quid sit, etiam atque etiam velim mihi explices: addasque, si placet, de spiritibus Sulphure, Sale, et Mercurio, quomodo hi conueniant non solum alijs rebus naturalibus, verumetiam corporibus humanis; qui ex his vegetet, nutriat et augmentet corpus nostrum, qui sentire faciat, qui moueat; seu, vt vulgariter loquuntur, qui sit naturalis, qui vitalis, qui animalis. Admodum enim placet mihi vestra Medicina. Item de Fixatione spirituum, quomodo spiritus spiritum figere possit, et alter alterius anima sit, et cur a vobis anima dicatur medium quod spiritum et corpus vnit, cum in nostro corpore non anima sit vinculum spirituum, sed potius spiritus sint vinculum animae. Item vnde calculus in homine generetur, et vnde Podagra existat. Si enim de his me certiorem feceris, nihil iucundius aut gratius quicquam mihi accidere poterit. Sed fortassis nimis impudenter te onero, nimiumque multus sum in responsione ad posteriores literas. Quapropter ad priores iam accedo.</p><p>Quod igitur in vtrisque tam posterioribus quam prioribus literis iudicium meum de ijs, quae in secundo Cometarum Tomo mihi transmisso continentur, requiris; id equidem eiusmodi agnosco esse, facile vt negligi possit. Per te enim tanquam summus artifex ipse intelligis, quid esse possit, quid non possit. Sed tamen vt habeas quod expetis, ita rem deduxisti, Cometamque illum in aethere cursum suum absoluisse ita demonstrasti, vt nemo hominum artem saltem Mathematicam non ignorans, tibi contradicere possit: idque tam luculenter et perspicue, vt nemo luculentius aut enucleatius. Quapropter vide vt quamprimum reliquos quoque Tomos habeamus. Ita enim non tantum negotium Cometarum verumetiam Artem nostram summe promouebis. In tertio Capite facis mentionem maximae Latitudinis Lunae, quam et ipse tecum eandem fere inueni: sed Caput Draconis abest a Tabulis plus quatuor Gradibus, vt per distantias et Globum reperi, nec arbitror calculum minorem differentiam producturum, quod facile examinarem si tempus mihi non deesset. De materia Cometarum et quomodo generentur, nihil in eo reperire potui, nisi cap. 9. de generatione caudae, de qua tamen an a Sole an vero a Venere, in cuius oppositas partes ad amussim, vt cap. 7. doces, toto apparitionis tempore porrecta fuit, generetur dubitas, sed tamen in Solis partes tandem inclinas, idque latente ac incomperta adhuc Optica ratione fieri posse putas. Verum me de tota caudae generatione, an ea per transmissos radios fiat, dubitare non ignoras ex cap. 7. scripti mei ad te transmissi. Cum enim Cardani, Scaligeri, et Apiani sententiam de generatione caudae recitassem, tandem subiunxi, me, vt ingenue faterer, in hac de generatione caudae sententia potius aliorum iudicium quam meum sequi. Haerebat enim tunc temporis semper in animo, caudam eiusdem materiae quamuis rarioris cum ipso Cometae corpore esse oportere, propterea quod radij Solis etiamsi corpus Cometae transeant, tamen post transitum sine materia, in qua similiter reflectantur, in puro illo Aëre apparere non possint: cum nec post Crystallum aut eiusmodi corpora lux Solis condensata appareat, nisi materiata res subijciatur, in quam Pyramis illa impingat: Item quod cauda illa sit dispersa, nec radij eius continuentur, sed interdum longe post caput abrupte apparere incipiant, et propter alias causas. Cum tamen eiusmodi caudatas Stellas nunquam obseruassem, nec dum formam et habitum caudae diligenter in vlla considerassem, videremque opinionem illam a nullo esse repraehensam: tantis et tam summis Viris cessi, quod tamen sine protestatione, vt scis, facere non potui. Fuisse vero iudicium illud meum de cauda contra summos illos viros verum, superiore hyeme cognoui. Cum enim forte fortuna inciderem in illas Cypriani Ephemeridas, quas deauratas vna cum sacris Biblijs et Globo seipsum mouente paruoque Quadrante semper secum vehere solet Illustrissimus Princeps noster; in principio earum ita scriptum reperi. Anno post exhibitam toti Terrarum orbi salutem supra millesimum quingentesimum quinquagesimo octauo comparuit Cometa, qui 13. Calend. Septemb. obseruamus est ab Illustrissimo Principe ac Domino, D. GVILHELMO LANDTGRAVIO HASSIAe per Torquetum horam circiter nonam in 21. Grad. ♍, Latitudine 31. Grad. ab Ecliptica remotus, comam versus extremam caudae Vrsae Maioris protendens, cum ipse esset in Asterismo Comae Berenices, vbi sunt Stellae Venereae et Lunares. Inde 12. Calend. progressus est in Grad. 23. ♍, in Latitudinem aut parum aut nihil promouit. Postremo 10. Calend. in 28. Grad. ♍ progressus in Latitudinis 35. cum semisse Gradum. Et cum esset admodum obscurus, ab Illustrissimo Principe obseruari desijt. Longitudinem caudae non facile visu assequi potuit. Constat Polum motus Cometae fuisse non procul ab Alrucaba versus caput Vrsae Maioris. Ita erat tota annotatio. Ex qua manifestissimum est, caudam illius Cometae neque ad Solem neque ad alium quendam Planetam respexisse, sed per sese suo ductu apparuisse. Quapropter nunc statuo, caudas eiusdem materiae esse cum capite, eodemque modo quo caput, per illuminationem apparere: id quod inde quoque manifestum est, quod caput Cometae in disparitione eodem modo dispersum appareat atque cauda. Nec curuatura illa caudae, vt arbitror, a te recte excusatur, cum nihil tale in Stellis et Planetis similiter in recta linea existentibus appareat, praefertim in tam paruo interstitio, vt ea cauda fuit. Tu vide, num haec mea sententia vera esse possit. Ita enim illi magni Cometae apud Senecam et Pontanum, quorum alter totam viam lacteam aequauit, alter occupauit quinquaginta Gradus, facile explicari poterunt, incuruationesque caudarum nulla excusatione indigebunt. Etsi autem non asseras, qualem Cometarum materiam statuas, tamen in fine Operis de Graminaeo agens videris meam sententiam reijcere Aristotelisque opinionem esse astruere. Verum multum ab Aristotelis opinione differt. Ille enim tenacem, lentam, crassam et viscosam eam statuit; ego tenuem, leuem et a radijs perpurgatam ac excoctam: Ille comburi eam vult et propter ardorem apparere; ego eam non comburi sed a radijs Stellarum absumi et dispergi, et per illuminationem apparere. Ille sub Luna, ego vero non tantum sub Luna, verumetiam in Sphaeris Planetarum eam esse statuo. Quae tua sententia sit, ignoro. Dauus enim sum, non Oedipus. Ex Titulo tamen, De nouis aetherei Mundi generationibus, quem in literis et ad Illustrissimum Principem nostrum et etiam proximis ad me missis, sic esse voluisti, conijcio te eam secundum tuam opinionem facere caelestem, generationemque in Caelum introducere. An igitur putes, eam ex Planetarum corporibus exhalari, an vero aliâs ab ijs generari, scire non possum. Id opinor certum est, cum generationem in Caelum introducas, te etiam corruptionem introducere. Nam secundum Artem vestram generatio non existit nisi ex corruptione. Sed nequeo de hac re plus agere, cum tuam sententiam ignorem.</p><p>De Hypothesibus quoque tuis nouis, quarum 8. capit. facis mentionem, te non per omnia assequor. Ipse equidem, cum Elementa mea Astronomica conscriberem, eodem modo Lunam deinde Solem circa Terram constituebam, sed in Homocentrepicyclepicyclis, in centro vero maioris et primi Epicycli Solis rursum Homocentrepicyclepicyclos reliquorum Planetarum, ita vt centrum Homocentricorum in reliquis Planetis idem semper esset cum centro maioris Epicycli Solis, vnaque per medium Solis motum in Homocentrico Solis circumduceretur. Sed id non noua ratione faciebam, sed prorsus secundum Copernicum, nec vt conuersim per omnia Hypotheses Copernici ad mobilitatem Solis transferrem aliter facere poteram: videbamque eam rationem et Rheticum et Reinholdum intellexisse. Ac secundum hunc modum curauit quoque Illustridimus Princeps noster superiori Anno ἀυτόματον construi mirae quidem paruitatis, sed tamen omnium Planetarum motus exhibens. Est enim orbiculus vtrinque planus in diametro vix 6. pollices continens, in cuius altera planitie Theorica Lunae cum suis Epicyclis et Capite Draconis continetur; in altera vero Solis et reliquorum Planetarum omnium Theoricae, exhibenturque omnes motus non solum tam Anomaliarum et centrorum quam veri in Longitudinem, verumetiam in tribus superioribus in Latitudinem. An vero tuae Hypotheses cum his eaedem sint et consentiant, certo scire non possum. Etsi enim generalem quidem Hypotyposin et ordinem Sphaerarum ipsosque Planetarum circulos siue Homocentricos siue Eccentricos eosdem esse satis aperte videam: tamen cum tuas nouas appelles, et in literis proximis scribas, eas particularibus apparentijs vndiquaque aptissime satisfacere, quod tamen neutra aliarum omnimode praestet, videntur diuersae a Copernicanis inuersis esse. Si enim eaedem essent, non magis satisfacerent apparentijs, quam Copernicanae ipsae, siquidem quaecunque apparentiae ex Copernicanis ipsis proueniunt, eae etiam prorsus per omnia ex inuersis absque vita differentia proueniant. Deinde videris centra homocentricorum reliquorum Planetarum non in homocentrico aut centro maioris Epicycli, sed in ipso Eccentrico Solis circumducere, cum idem quoque in Cometa facias. Quod si ita esset, nouae et diuersae merito essent, cum hac ratione Eccentricitas Solis sese omnibus Planetis necessario immisceret, aliasque apparentias introduceret. Verum Obseruationes huic suppositioni respondere, vix crediderim. Scio equidem Copernicum demonstrare, mutationem Eccentricitatis Solis etiam in reliquis Planetis animaduerti, quod tamen in Tabulis tanquam minus sensibile neglexit. (Vnde ego quoque Epicyclium illud Solis in maiorem Epicyclum transposui, quod aliâs ad Terram reponendum fuisset, ita vt infima suî parte per centrum Terrae transijsset.) Veruntamen id omnino ex ipsius Hypothesi necessarium est, siquidem per Eccentricum Solis intelligit Eccentricum Terrae, cuius centrum, si mutatum fuerit, necessario Eccentricitates reliquorum Planetarum mutabit, cum eae omnes hoc centrum respiciant et ab hoc centro earum magnitudines numerentur. An itaque, vt dixi, tuae cum Copernicanis inuersis prorsus eaedem sint, certo scire non possum. Quod tamen existimas, generalem illam Sphaerarum dispositionem quo diligentius perspexero, eo magis mihi placituram; scias iamdudum mihi prorsus contrarium accidisse, de quo etiam cum Eruditissimo Viro Gellio Sasceride. Quo enim diutius eam considero, eo minus mihi placet. Etsi enim ostendit, quomodo secundum medium motum Solis Planetae superiores directi stationarij aut retrogradi reddantur: tamen (quod etiam Ptolemaica facit,) tantum ostendit quod sic fiant, non quare sic fiant, Planetasque regressionis macula aspergit. At Copernicea, non tantum, quod fiant regressiones et stationes illae, ostendit, sed etiam quare sic fiant, non quod tanta sit motus inconstantia in ipsis Planetis, qui semper suum proprium motum aspiciant, sed quod regressiones illae ita nobis propter motum Terrae appareant, Planetisque putentur inesse, cum tamen reuera Terrae insint. Ita pulcherrime nullus motus relinquitur nisi proprius, primus in Reuolutione diurna Terrae, alter in motu ipsius annuo, tertius in Luna, quartus in Venere, quintus in Mercurio, sextus in Marte, septimus in Ioue, et octauus tandem in Saturno, absque vlla violentiae aut regressionis labe. Deinde quis credet vnquam, Centrum maioris Epicycli Solis tanta efficacia praeditum esse, vt trahere post se Planetas omnes, quinimo e suis Sphaeris detrahere rursumque attollere possit, cum nulla corporali et tenaci materia connectantur? Qua de causa quoque cum Illustrissimo Principi nostro ostenderem in Tomo tuo secundo Cometarum, Hypothesin illam tuam, quam mox, vt aspexit, agnouit, vtpote cui ea ex Automato satis nota erat: Dij boni, per iocum aiebat, oportet circulum illum Solis aeneo longe esse fortiorem, cum tot Planetas trahere secum possit. Scilicet haerebat in animo, quod Illustrissimae ipsius Celsitud. toties ad aurem decantâram, Tractionis motum e Caelo tollendum esse. Nec vt arbitror facile concedi poterit Soli, vt reliquorum Planetarum motus proprios sua hac tractione turbet. Praeterea introducit haec inuersa Copernici ratio confusionem in Sphaeras Planetarum. Etsi enim nulla corporum caelestium illisio inde sequi potest, vt excusare niteris; Tamen nulla Sphaerarum vera aut determinata distinctio manet, sed omnes inter se confunduntur. At Copernicea Hypothesis suum vnicuique Planetae et determinatum spatium attribuit, e quo motu suo egredi non possit. Scis autem Deum non confusionis sed ordinis authorem esse, naturamque ita condidisse, vt a confusione abhorreat; Nedum vt pulcherrimum hoc et sapientiae inexhaustae aedificium tanta labe aspexere voluerit. Accedit et hoc, quod Natura non agat per plura, cum per pauciora possit. Item quod mutatio Eccentricitatis Solis etiam in reliquis Planetis sentiatur: quae etsi transpositione minoris Epicycli Solis ad Terram, vt dixi, quoquo modo excusari potest; Tamen nihilominus omnia per tractionem fieri hac ratione finguntur. Haec igitur et alia multa etiam atque etiam considerans, aliud inuenire non possum, quam nullam praeter vnicam Copernici Hypothesin veram esse. Nec peccat haec contra Physica principia, nec cum Terra grossum corpus sit, propterea ad mouendum inhabilis erit. Quid enim putas reliqua Sidera esse? Non enim errare Copernicum arbitror, cum ait, grauitatem nihil aliud esse, quam appetentiam quandam naturalem partibus inditam a diuina prouidentia Opificis vniuersorum, vt in vnitatem integritatemque suam sese conferant in formam globi coëuntes: hancque affectionem etiam Soli, Lunae caeterisque Errantium fulgoribus inesse, vt eius efficacia in ea, qua se repraesentant, rotunditate permaneant: quae nihilominus multis modis suos efficiant circuitus. Et sane cum Terra, vt reliqui Planetae, rotunda figura praedita sit, similiterque libera in Aëre pendeat, cur eam ad mouendum inhabilem esse dicamus. Videmus si globum in Polis et Axi exactissime et subtiliter aut erigamus aut suspendamus, atque ita libere pendentem in gyrum impellamus, eum satis diu hunc motum retinere, nec subito cohibere posse. Si igitur artificialis motus etiam impeditus hoc possit; quanto magis naturalis non impeditus? Sed absurditates Physicas satis superque refutauit Copernicus. Nec etiam obstat authoritas sacrarum literarum. Hae enim non mihi et tibi solummodo, verum omnibus omnino hominibus scriptae sunt, ad quorum captum etiam loquuntur, vt etiam omnes Theologi in explicatione capitis 1. Genes. fatentur. Aliâs Luna contra Geometricas demonstrationes esset maior reliquis Stellis. Sed et illud ex hoc manifestum est. Putârunt Hebraei, nubes in sublimi pendere non posse, nisi subesset materia aliqua dura et imperuia, quae Aquam sustineret. Ad horum igitur captum sese accomodans Deus ait, faciam Caelum ferreum ne pluat, etc. Item aperuit fenestras Caeli, in Diluuio, etc. Item Moses, cum ait, primo die Firmamentum conditum esse, vt discerneret Aquas supra nos ab Aquis infra nos. Quo nihil aliud intelligi posse arbitror, quam vim illam, qua nubes ita in sublimi pendent, primo die conditam esse. Sic et cum nos Opticis de causis putemus, Caelum esse tanquam expansum tentorium, ideo sacrae literae hanc vulgi opinionem sequentes Caelum vocant expansum, et Prophetae Deum expandere Caelum tanquam tentorium aiunt. Nec enim hi plus de hac materia intellexerunt, quam alij vulgares homines aut etiam Lactantius, nec Spiritus sanctus sapientiam, quam in Naturam Deus transfudit, ijs reuelare voluit, verum eam quae in stupendo illo et supernaturali, de Redemtione generis humani mysterio continetur. Et cur Salomon adeo ad miraculum vsque sapiens habitus est, nisi quod longe excellentiorem totius Naturae cognitionem haberet, quam ante eum quisquam alius. Paulus quoque cum Roman: 1. ait, Deum ex visibilibus hisce agnosci, non obscure arguit, longe maiorem sapientiam Dei latere in Natura, quam in sacris literis sit reuelata. In Natura dico: De mysterio enim Redemtionis, quod proprie tractant sacrae literae, et de agnitione veri Dei, iam non loquor. Ita authoritas sacrarum literarum, quanquam admodum plausibiliter proferatur, tamen reuera in hac materia obstare nihil omnino poterit, sed tantum sciemus, quantum per Demonstrationes Mathematicas inuenimus. Etsi autem Gellius Sascerides aiebat, te habere tuae assertionis Demonstrationes Geometricas: Tamen hoc ego certa de causa vix crediderim. Non tamen propterea Hypotheses Copernicaeas discentibus proponendas esse iudico, cum eae vix ab Artificibus percipiantur, vt nec Maesthlinus quidem motum librationis in Latitudinibus et obliquitate Zodiaci recte intelligit: Sed eas, quae sint omnium facilimae, vt pedetentim accessus ad Copernicum aperiatur. Quapropter ipse quoque in Elementis meis Astronomicis superiorem illam inuersionem expunxi, et Eccentricos in centro mobili substitui, vt discentes Apogaeorum motus et loca eo facilius intelligere possent, et addito in Planetis Epicyclo directiones, stationes et regressiones eo fierent manifestiores, nec etiam Latitudinum ratio adeo esset obscura et intricata. Ne tamen deessem ingeniosis et ijs, qui iam communes motuum singulorum formas intelligerent aut Automata fabricare vellent: Ad finem pro Corollario eam, de qua supra dixi, Copernici Inuersionem subieci, vt ita discentes ad Copernici lectionem et faciliorem intelligentiam praepararem. Sed fortassis plus de hac materia quam sat erat: ad alia igitur.</p><p>Quod sub finem Roeslinianae refutationis arbitraris, minimam Eccentricitatem Solis transijsse, idem et ego statuo. Refero enim minimam Eccentricitatem et obliquitatem Solis ad Purbachium. Nec Rheticus quoque audiendus est, qui cum Albategnium nimis libere abuti mysterijs Astrologiae scribat, in idem vitium hîc ipse incurrit. Quomodo enim mutatio Eccentricitatis Solis mutationem imperiorum efficere potest? Nec id quoque iniuste repraehendis, quod PHILIPPVS MELANCHTHON primus introduxit, nimirum Solem iam Terrae factum esse propiorem, licet etiam Cardanus lib. 3. subtilitatum idem erronee statuat. Nec enim Copernici mentem in hac re sunt assecuti. Te quoque longe exactiorem Hypothesin motus Cometae inuenire potuisse non dubito, cum proportionale decrementum ipsius motus satis arguat, ex circularibus ipsum compositum fuisse. Veruntamen Hypotheses Cometarum constituere prorsus superuacaneum iudico, cum nullus Periodum absoluat, nec etiam vlla vtilitas inde redundare possit. Quapropter et in meo scripto Hypotheseos constitutionem omisi. Ipse illum Cometam, de quo in illo Tomo agis, conspexi Bernburgi 10. Nouemb. vna cum Ioanne Troldenirero Arci Bernburgicae tunc temporis praefecto, a quo ad conuiuium Martinale eram inuitatus; omnibus Bernburgi habitantibus eo die apparuit. Erat enim ibi serenum Caelum. Cum Tomum illum tuum secundum Illustriss. Principi nostro ostenderem, plus tribus integris horis in eo diligenter euoluendo occupatus erat, placebatque ipsius C. mirum in modum, adeo vt etiam astantibus eum praedicare non intermitteret: contendebatque ibi mecum, sibi a te transmissum esse librum illum, ego vero ex inscriptione meum arguebam, subiungebamque, te integrum illud Opus, si absolutum esset, ad Illustriss. ipsius C. missurum. Vide igitur vt fidem meam redimas. Quod si idem etiam ad Filium Mauritium, quanquam non in hac doctrina, tamen in Latinis, Graecis et Hebraicis praeclare institutum et praeter Aetatem eruditum; Itemque ad Bernhardum Principem Anhaltinum hîc in Aula nostra cum Mauritio versantem, quem parens Ioachimus Ernestus felicissimae recordationis vna cum fratribus per Casparum Godtschalcum virum Matheseos non imperitum, etiam in his Artibus institui curauit: si inquam ad hos quoque duos Opus illud tuum absolutum huc miseris, non dubito, quin rem gratissimam ipsis sis facturus, hac eos ratione ad harum iucundissimarum Artium amorem et cultum pro aetatis captu tacite incitando. Opera illa Mathematica, de quibus superioribus literis scripsi, propediem diuina fauente clementia sum editurus, vbi ea ad te transmittam. Sed tempus iam esset, vt nimis prolixam hanc Epistolam tandem clauderem, nisi mihi Illustriss. Princeps noster aliud in mandatis dedisset. Cum enim Illustriss. ipsius Celsitud. in elocatione Filiae adeo sit occupata, vt respondere nequeat: mandauit mihi, vt ipse ad ea, quae continet Epistola tua, respondeam. Verum nihil in ea reperio, quod responsione admodum indigeat, aut in superioribus Illustrissimi ipsius Celsitudinis literis ad te missis non satis superque sit deductum: praeterquam quod in Obseruationibus per Venerem institutis nos erroris arguere videris, vitiumque in Refractiones conferre. Verum Refractiones mihi obstare non potuerunt, cum et per Azimutha Obseruationes instituerim, et post occasum Solis distantia Veneris â Tertia ♈ ac alijs accepta propter Refractiones (quas tamen separaui) iusto minor necessario inuenta fuisset, adeoque Stellarum loca in antecedentia contra signorum ordinem fuissent retracta. Sed nolo de his plura agere. Rationem enim Obseruationum nostrarum videbis in Opere nostro Deo volente breui edendo. Nihil igitur iam amplius restare video, quam vt tandem hanc Epistolam finiam. Quapropter optime et felicissime vale, Nobilissime Excellentissimeque D. TYCHO, mihique longe charissime, candoremque meum in optimam partem accipe, mihique quamprimum rescribe. Iterum vale.</p><p>Cassellis 13. Calendas Octobris, Anno salutis M.D.LXXXIIX. T. T. CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and — in virtue, wisdom, and ingenious and erudite learning — most distinguished man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish Knight, of all the Mathematicians of our age the most excellent, etc., his Lord to be cultivated with observance, etc. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Most acceptable to me were both your letters, most Noble and most Excellent Lord Tycho, and far most welcome to my mind — both the earlier, with the <b>Second Tome of the recent Phenomena of the Heaven</b> [<i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>], which I received on <b>11 July</b> from <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>, and the later, which [I received] just now. But I will reply to these [latter] first, to the earlier below. About the <b>matter of the Heaven</b>, then, that I may still deal with you in few [words], I ask you to grant me [leave]. For I cannot be torn from it. And unless this question be decided by us, it will never be decided by anyone, whether Theologian or Physicist. For God revealed nothing about this in his word, since it pertains nothing to our salvation; nor did he wish to mix into the sacred writings disputations of this kind, which very few even among the Learned understand — [writings] which are written for absolutely all men, the Unlearned equally with the Learned, the Rude equally with the Ingenious, as Christ himself testifies (John 3) to <b>Nicodemus</b>. And how will the Physicists be able to know anything certain about this? For we know and understand only so much about such sublime things, so far removed from us, as we find out through the <b>Doctrine of Triangles</b> and <b>Mathematical demonstrations</b>. Without these, whoever discourses about such things is most empty and raves utterly. Nor would I ever have introduced the <b>matter of Air</b>, had not the <b>defect of Refractions</b> and the <b>optical Demonstrations</b> impelled me to it. For as far as it shall be true that <b>diverse Transparencies</b> [<i>Diaphana</i>] produce <b>Refraction</b>, so far also will my assertion be true. Nor do those [things] which are brought forward by you derogate anything from this. For what is handed down by the <b>Physicists</b> — that in the Heaven there is nothing elemental, nothing corruptible, nothing variable, but all things eternal — is as easily denied as asserted. For one must prove <b>why</b> it is so, and <b>whence</b> it is known, not assume [it] as a granted hypothesis and axiom. And if you should say, with <b>Aristotle</b>, that it is so because no change was ever detected in the celestial bodies — or rather in the magnitude and brightness of the Stars — you will have said nothing. For neither was the magnitude of the <b>Earth</b> ever changed, and yet in the Earth and about the Earth all things are corruptible. The perseverance of magnitude in the Stars therefore proves nothing. Nor yet do I meanwhile deny that the celestial bodies themselves are most perfect, most simple, and subject to no corruption or generation at all; but I speak only of the <b>surrounding matter</b>, which I say is not impure but most pure <b>Air</b>, which nevertheless differs in nothing from the pure Air below the Moon. For this too detracts nothing from the brightness of the Stars. Nor does it follow, if the bodies themselves are most perfect and diverse from the Elements, that the surrounding matter too must be perfect; but rather, since <b>Comets are generated in it</b>, grow, decrease, and at last also vanish and are dissipated — and are sometimes of such magnitude that they can occupy even fifty degrees — it will not be so far immune from change and corruption, nor so eternal, divine, and inscrutable as is feigned. Nor, if I rely on this reasoning and the foregoing Optical principle, shall I [thereby] sprinkle the <b>Heaven</b> (if indeed that surrounding matter is to be called Heaven — though I am not ignorant that Air too is sometimes called Heaven) — I say I shall not on that account sprinkle the Heaven with any stain, but rather shall <b>free</b> it from a vain substance, idly feigned beyond all cause and experience, and substitute the true opinion. Nor will any <b>sound</b> be stirred up from the violent motion of the Stars in that pure Air. For that Air, struck and impelled, gives a sound, we have experienced in this <b>gross [air]</b> from the wind (for the sound which comes about by reason of a vacuum manifestly does not fit here); nor is that without reason. For since this gross Air always contains something watery in itself, and Water is a thing of the kind that does not admit sudden penetration, not undeservedly the gross Air too, in impulsion and striking, resists somehow and gives a sound. But that the same happens in <b>pure Air</b> too, no one will easily prove. For that it is most tenuous and most rare is clear enough from this: that this gross [air], though thought most tenuous, nevertheless transmits the Sun's rays, even in the most serene weather, not unreflected and without light; but that pure [air] the Sun's rays penetrate without any light and reflection, as is manifest from the <b>Twilights</b> [<i>Crepuscula</i>]. Therefore, on account of [its] tenuity and rarity, that pure Air cannot be struck and impelled, but yields without any obstacle, and so detracts nothing from the perpetuity of the revolution of the celestial bodies and from their purity by long use and time — much less, by wearing [them down], will it shake the most constant harmony. For if that purest Air could do this, I should fear lest, from the most violent blasts of the winds in this gross Air, the whole Earth had long since been worn away and consumed, and even moved from its place. Likewise, if so great a sound could be excited from that pure Air as to strike our hearing, I should think all the <b>Gods above</b> [<i>Superi</i>] long since suffocated from excessive fear. For much more would the sound of this gross Air, excited by the most violent winds, be perceived by the Gods above than that [pure-air sound] by us. But yet I remember that there was once, in a place situated five miles from here, a most violent wind, so much so that it overturned buildings and trees; of whose sound, however, I heard nothing here. So [true is it that] the things which are true cannot be overturned by conjectures. About the <b>proportion of the Elements</b> too, you know how <b>Philip Melanchthon</b> in his <i>Physics</i> [<i>Initia doctrinae physicae</i>] reprehends the Peripatetics and teaches the true opinion of Aristotle. And surely, when you grant that up to the Moon the Air is elemental, by that very [fact] you abolish the proportion of the Elements. For since up to the lowest [point of] the Moon there are, as you testify you found in the second Tome sent to me, <b>52 Earth-radii</b>, the Air will be more than a hundred thousand times greater than the globe of Earth and Water together. But what is the proportion 1 to 100,000 — or rather 1 to 140,608 — to say nothing meanwhile of the Earth separated from the Water? The proportion of the Elements, therefore, does not stand in the way. But neither does my own demonstration, as you object. For I did not mean that at an <b>Altitude of 30°</b> no Refraction can any longer occur, but I assumed this only as a thing-postulated (<i>petitum</i> or postulate) from your first letter sent to our most Illustrious Prince, so that I might demonstrate [that], if an altitude be given at which the Refractions cease, there is also given <b>how great a space</b> the Sun's rays penetrate the gross Air unrefracted; and the same demonstration will hold, even if the Refractions scarcely cease at the <b>40th degree</b> of Altitude. And since from it [it] comes out that the <b>vapours about the Horizon are nearly sixfold denser</b> than at an Altitude of 30°, it follows thence that the Refractions cannot produce a smaller proportion at that Altitude — much less [can] the space of the same medium about the Horizon, penetrated unrefracted, be greater than that which is penetrated refracted. See, therefore, whether that demonstration can be applied to a <b>diverse Transparency of aether and air</b>. For what <b>multiplication</b> of the diverse Transparency, or rather of Air, can there be here? For suppose that mutual combination of aether and air to occur at a distance of <b>30 Earth-radii</b> from the Earth's surface: at the Horizon, what will the multiplication be? For at the Horizon there will not yet be 31 radii. Forsooth, will the rays be able to penetrate so many Earth-radii of the diverse Transparency unrefracted, but not be able [to penetrate] a single radius [of it]? But as to what you think — that even if it be granted that no Refraction occurs in the place nearest the sublunary [region], it does not yet follow that the <b>Heaven</b> (if indeed that surrounding matter, as I said before too, is to be called Heaven — for of it we speak), that, I say, the Heaven consists of <b>elemental Air</b> — that is far otherwise. For whenever <b>diverse Transparencies</b> are given, Refraction outside the perpendicular is also given; and whenever Refraction outside the perpendicular is removed, the diversity of Transparencies is also removed — as is manifest from <b>chapters 2 and 4 of Alhazen book 7</b>, and from <b>Witelo book 10, prop. 3</b>, and everywhere throughout the whole of <b>Optics</b>. For diverse media transmit the images of the thing seen to the eye in diverse ways; and when the first medium is subtler and rarer than the second, the image is more easily and swiftly transmitted through the first medium than through the second, and when it reaches the second, it is as it were retarded, as is known from the said Optical [writers]. Whence too that excuse cannot have place here — that those diverse media, in mutual connexion, are assimilated to one another (as you feign without cause), and one is successively changed into the other. For there will nonetheless be, in that mutual combination and assimilation, a <b>point</b> at which the image of the Stars is as it were retarded — which is the <b>point of Refraction</b>. Often in winter I have experienced, when I stood with the window open in the middle of the <b>Observatory</b> in warm Air, that a thing seen standing in cold Air is seen refracted. Here certainly neither similitude nor successive combination and assimilation could remove the Refraction. But if those media are in themselves so similar that there is no difference between them, why do you call [them] diverse? Why do you not call even that sublunary pure Air \"aether,\" or, as <b>Pliny</b> speaks, \"<b>aethereal spirit</b>\"? For thus I should not be fighting with you. For I contend only this: that the Transparencies of aether and of pure air are not diverse, since no Refraction is generated by them. But I know not how you so cleave to your opinion that, even beyond all experience, you grant that the Refraction of aether and air lasts up to the <b>zenith</b> — though you have never sensed it with Instruments. Certainly by this reasoning one may feign anything. But yet it would follow thence that no Star, except those which pass the zenith and are at the zenith, is seen with true light, but [with light] confused and weakened. For every Refraction weakens the light and colour of the thing seen, as <b>Alhazen book 7, chapter 7</b> and <b>Witelo book 10, prop. 10</b> demonstrate. Since therefore that diversity is not detected from the comparison of a zenith-Star to the rest free from vapours, neither will Refraction be able to be granted. But you here [go] rather into all things diverse [self-contradiction]. For you say that these Refractions of aether and air are sensible below 30°, but in a higher position insensible; and yet afterward you add that the <b>Refraction of the Sun, which you found at an Altitude of 30° [to be] 1½ minutes</b>, can scarcely be excused by vapours alone. Would that you said it is wholly from the diverse Transparency of aether and air — I should certainly demonstrate that at an Altitude of <b>45°</b> it ought still to be nearly <b>one minute</b>. For it is truer than truth itself that, if some sensible Refraction of aether and air be granted below 30°, it cannot become insensible so suddenly in a higher position. For, the same media remaining, the Refractions themselves cannot be changed so suddenly, since their Angles do not decrease so suddenly — nay, whether they occur from Air to Water, or from Air to glass, or from Water to glass, or contrariwise, they retain nearly the same proportion in increasing and decreasing, as may be seen from the Table set in <b>Witelo book 10, prop. 8</b>. Looking to which too I wrote in [my] earlier letter that, if about the Horizon there be given a Solar Refraction of <b>3 minutes</b> arising from the diverse Transparency of aether and air, it is given at an Altitude of 45° [as] <b>1½ minutes</b>. For although the Refractions themselves and the Angles of Refraction will decrease by some — yet not very dissimilar — proportion; which, if time did not fail me, I would easily examine by Calculation. But that the Refractions decrease so suddenly about the Horizon, that is from the mere <b>vapours</b>, which about the Earth are both grosser and (as I demonstrated) multiplied, and are also sought [reached] by rays from the side. Otherwise, if the same media remained, disposed in the same quantity and the same manner, so sudden a change of Refractions could not happen, as can be demonstrated. But there still remains a <b>demonstration which no one will ever be able to break</b>. For if there is a Refraction arising from the diverse Transparency of aether and air, it will necessarily be <b>greater in the Fixed Stars, which are farther distant, than in the Sun, which is nearer</b> (to say nothing meanwhile of the Moon). For in a Triangle, one Angle and one Side remaining, the other sides being produced, the remaining Angle at the remaining side is made greater, by <b>Euclid I.21</b>. For necessarily the same Angle of Refraction will remain at the same Altitude, since the same media remain; nor can the Angle of Refraction in the Sun or Moon be other than in the Fixed [stars]. From which it is most manifest that, if you posit the Refraction in the Sun to become insensible at some certain Altitude, it ought still to be sensible in the Fixed Stars at the same Altitude. Since, therefore, through Observations not this but rather the contrary is found, you yourself being witness — Refraction cannot be generated by a diverse Transparency of aether and air; and therefore, as I have several times now proved from Optics, the Transparencies of aether and air cannot be diverse, but [are] the same. If therefore you wish to call that pure Air \"Heaven,\" I do not fight at all — only let not pure Air be determined to differ from the celestial, and so let not diverse Transparencies be introduced, since the defect of Refraction does not permit that one be subtler than the other. But as to what you bring from the <b>Chymists</b> — that there lurks in earthly things a certain <b>fifth essence</b>, which you call celestial — that I both believe and most pleasantly contemplate. Nevertheless it does not follow thence that this fifth essence is the same as that matter which is poured around the Planets; but rather that it is <b>generated by the Sun</b>. For just as the <b>Sun of the Microcosm</b> (as you most pleasantly teach), from blood and attracted Air (from which, on account of respiration, its inborn nature seems to retain the subtler part and reject the rest) — just as, I say, the Sun of the Microcosm, from Air and blood prepared in the right chamber [of the heart], generates the <b>vital spirit</b> in the left chamber — so too the <b>Sun of the whole universe</b>, from Earth, Water, and Air, seems to generate that fifth essence in natural things. Or, if this essence is the same as that matter poured around the Planets, it will be the same also as the pure Air, on account of the demonstrations adduced. These [things], a little more fully than I had proposed at the beginning, I wished to write out to you about this matter still — not indeed from a zeal for contradicting, but from mere <b>love of truth</b> [φιλαληθέια]. Nor can anything certain about the generation and matter of Comets be brought forward, unless this question be decided. But about this I will deal at more length, God granting, in our <b>Observations of the Fixed Stars</b> — omitting, however, mention of this our dissension, lest it perchance detract something from the truth of the matter. Nor do I make mention even of you, unless I can [do so] without reproach; but then rather, the name omitted, I treat the things themselves.</p><p>Now to those <b>Philosophical</b> [matters]. But first one [thing] still. I cannot sufficiently wonder that you have found the <b>beginning of morning Twilight</b>, about the equinoxes, [with] the Sun scarcely <b>16½°</b> below the Horizon, depressed in the vertical circle — whereas I have found the <b>end of evening Twilight</b> about the vernal equinox here [with] the Sun depressed <b>24°</b> below the Horizon, in such a way, however, that no further trace of whiteness appeared. You, therefore, attempt the same too in the <b>evening Twilights</b>, where I judge these Observations to be more exact, because it can more easily be noticed when the trace of whitening Air ceases than when it begins — the Observer always doubting whether that trace of whiteness pertains to the morning Twilight. I too will try the same in the morning [twilights]. To those sweetest [things], then, drawn from the inmost and profound <b>Philosophy</b>, I now come. For the rest do not seem to need a reply; nor could I observe anything here about <b>Eclipses</b>, on account of the rainy Sky. I give you, therefore, the greatest thanks I can, that you have deigned to instruct me so amply in those more secret [matters]; and I ask you again and again, in greater measure, that you do this in the future too. When I read them, I almost cried out for joy. How far truer and more beautiful they are than what is handed down in Aristotle! But do you make me understand more. You wrote in [your] earlier letter, when you made mention of <b>Ramus</b>, that <b>all things consist of number, weight, and measure</b>. What this is, then, I would wish you to explain to me again and again; and add, if it please, about the <b>spirits — Sulphur, Salt, and Mercury</b> — how these agree not only with other natural things but also with human bodies; which of these vegetates, nourishes, and increases our body, which makes [it] feel, which moves [it]; or, as they commonly speak, which is the <b>natural</b>, which the <b>vital</b>, which the <b>animal</b> [spirit]. For your <b>Medicine</b> pleases me greatly. Likewise about the <b>Fixation of spirits</b>: how spirit can fix spirit, and one be the soul of the other, and why by you the <b>soul</b> is said [to be] the medium that unites spirit and body, whereas in our body the soul is not the bond of the spirits, but rather the spirits are the bond of the soul. Likewise whence the <b>stone</b> [calculus] is generated in man, and whence <b>Gout</b> [Podagra] arises. For if you inform me about these, nothing more pleasant or welcome could befall me. But perhaps I burden you too impudently, and am too long in [my] reply to the later letter. Wherefore I now proceed to the earlier.</p><p>As to your requiring, then, in both letters — the later as well as the earlier — my <b>judgment about those [things] contained in the second Tome of the Comet[s]</b> sent to me: I indeed acknowledge it to be of such a kind that it can easily be neglected. For through yourself, as a supreme artificer, you understand what can be, what cannot. But yet, that you may have what you seek: you have so carried out the matter, and so demonstrated that that <b>Comet completed its course in the aether</b>, that no man not ignorant of the Mathematical art at least can contradict you — and that so amply and clearly that none [could do so] more amply or more unfoldedly. Wherefore see that we have the remaining Tomes too as soon as possible. For thus you will most greatly promote not only the business of Comets but also our <b>Art</b>. In the <b>third Chapter</b> you make mention of the <b>maximum Latitude of the Moon</b>, which I too found nearly the same as you; but the <b>Head of the Dragon</b> [the ascending node] is distant from the <b>Tables</b> by more than four degrees, as I found by distances and the Globe, nor do I think the calculation will produce a smaller difference — which I would easily examine if time did not fail me. About the <b>matter of Comets</b> and how they are generated, I could find nothing in it, except in <b>chapter 9, on the generation of the tail</b> — about which, however, whether it is generated <b>from the Sun or from Venus</b> (into whose opposite parts exactly, as you teach in chapter 7, it was stretched out through the whole time of the apparition), you doubt; but yet you at last incline to the Sun's side, and think this can happen by a still-latent and undiscovered Optical reasoning. But you are not ignorant, from chapter 7 of my writing sent to you, that I doubt about the whole generation of the tail — whether it occurs through transmitted rays. For when I had recited the opinion of <b>Cardano, Scaliger, and Apian</b> about the generation of the tail, I at last subjoined that I, to confess frankly, in this opinion about the generation of the tail follow the judgment of others rather than my own. For there always stuck then in my mind that the <b>tail must be of the same matter — though rarer — as the body of the Comet itself</b>, because the Sun's rays, even if they pass through the Comet's body, yet after the passage cannot appear, without matter in which they may similarly be reflected, in that pure Air — since not even after <b>Crystal</b> or bodies of that kind does the condensed light of the Sun appear, unless a material thing be set [under it] against which that <b>Pyramid</b> [cone of rays] strikes; likewise because that tail is dispersed, nor are its rays continuous, but sometimes begin to appear abruptly far behind the head, and for other causes. Since, however, I had never observed such tailed Stars, nor had yet diligently considered the form and habit of the tail in any [comet], and saw that opinion reprehended by no one, I yielded to such and so supreme Men — which, however, as you know, I could not do without protestation. But that that judgment of mine about the tail, against those supreme men, was true, I learned last winter. For when by chance I happened upon those <b>Ephemerides of Cyprian</b> [Leovitius], which, gilded, together with the sacred Bible and a self-moving Globe and a small Quadrant, our most Illustrious Prince is wont always to carry with him, I found written at the beginning of them thus: <i>\"In the year 1558 after the salvation shown to the whole globe of lands, there appeared a <b>Comet</b>, which on the 13th [day before the] Kalends of September [20 Aug] was observed by the most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord <b>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse</b>, by a <b>Torquetum</b>, about the ninth hour, at 21° of Virgo [♍], distant in Latitude 31° from the Ecliptic, stretching its coma toward the extremity of the tail of Ursa Major, while it itself was in the Asterism of <b>Coma Berenices</b>, where there are stars Venereal and Lunar. Thence on the 12th [before the] Kalends [21 Aug] it advanced to 23° of Virgo, [but] in Latitude it advanced either little or nothing. Lastly, on the 10th [before the] Kalends [23 Aug], advanced to 28° of Virgo, into 35½° of Latitude. And since it was quite obscure, it ceased to be observed by the most Illustrious Prince. The Length of the tail could not easily be attained by sight. It is established that the <b>Pole of the Comet's motion</b> was not far from <b>Alrucaba</b> [the pole-star region] toward the head of Ursa Major.\"</i> Such was the whole note. From which it is most manifest that the <b>tail of that Comet regarded neither the Sun nor any other Planet, but appeared by itself, by its own course</b>. Wherefore I now establish that the <b>tails are of the same matter as the head, and appear in the same way as the head, by illumination</b> — which is also manifest from this: that the head of the Comet, in its disappearance, appears dispersed in the same way as the tail. Nor is that <b>curvature of the tail</b>, as I think, rightly excused by you, since nothing such appears in the Stars and Planets similarly existing in a straight line, especially in so small an interval as that tail was. Do you see whether this my opinion can be true. For thus those <b>great Comets in Seneca and Pontanus</b> — of which the one equalled the whole <b>Milky Way</b>, the other occupied <b>fifty degrees</b> — will be easily explained, and the curvatures of the tails will need no excuse. But although you do not assert what kind of matter of Comets you posit, yet at the end of the Work, dealing with <b>Graminaeus</b>, you seem to reject my opinion and to build up that the opinion is Aristotle's. But [mine] differs much from Aristotle's opinion. For he posits it [the matter] tenacious, slow, gross, and viscous; I [posit it] tenuous, light, and thoroughly purged and concocted by the rays. He wills it to be burned and to appear on account of the burning; I [will] it not to be burned but to be consumed and dispersed by the rays of the Stars, and to appear by illumination. He [places it] below the Moon; I [place it] not only below the Moon but also in the Spheres of the Planets. What your opinion is, I do not know. For I am a <b>Davus, not an Oedipus</b> [no riddle-solver]. From the Title, however — <i>On the new generations of the aethereal World</i> — which you wished [it] to be thus, in the letters both to our most Illustrious Prince and also in the recent [ones] sent to me, I conjecture that you, according to your opinion, make it celestial, and introduce <b>generation into the Heaven</b>. Whether, then, you think it is exhaled from the bodies of the Planets, or otherwise generated by them, I cannot know. This, I think, is certain: that, since you introduce <b>generation into the Heaven, you also introduce corruption</b>. For according to your Art, generation does not exist except from corruption. But I cannot deal more with this matter, since I am ignorant of your opinion.</p><p>About your <b>new Hypotheses</b> too, of which you make mention in the 8th chapter, I do not follow you in all [respects]. I myself indeed, when I was writing my <b>Astronomical Elements</b>, in the same way placed the Moon, then the Sun, about the Earth — but in <b>homocentric-epicycle-upon-epicycle [constructions]</b> [<i>Homocentrepicyclepicyclis</i>]; and at the centre of the greater and first Epicycle of the Sun, again [I placed] the homocentric-epicycle-upon-epicycles of the remaining Planets, so that the centre of the homocentrics in the remaining Planets was always the same as the centre of the greater Epicycle of the Sun, and was carried around together, through the mean motion of the Sun, in the homocentric of the Sun. But I did this by no new reasoning, but entirely <b>according to Copernicus</b>; nor could I do otherwise, so as to transfer conversely, through all [points], the Hypotheses of Copernicus to the mobility of the Sun; and I saw that both <b>Rheticus</b> and <b>Reinhold</b> had understood that reasoning. And according to this manner our most Illustrious Prince too had, last year, an <b>automaton</b> [ἀυτόματον] constructed — of wondrous smallness indeed, but yet exhibiting the motions of all the Planets. For it is a little orb, flat on both sides, containing scarcely 6 inches in diameter, on one plane face of which is contained the Theoric of the Moon with its Epicycles and the Head of the Dragon; but on the other, the Theorics of the Sun and of all the remaining Planets; and all the motions are exhibited, not only of the Anomalies and of the centres, and the true [motion] in Longitude, but also, in the three superior [planets], in Latitude. But whether your Hypotheses are the same with these and agree, I cannot know for certain. For although I see clearly enough that the general <b>sketch</b> [<i>Hypotyposis</i>] and order of the Spheres, and the very circles of the Planets — whether homocentric or eccentric — are the same, yet, since you call yours <b>new</b>, and write in [your] recent letter that they satisfy the particular appearances most aptly on every side (which, however, neither of the others does in every way), they seem to be <b>diverse from the inverted Copernican [hypotheses]</b>. For if they were the same, they would not satisfy the appearances more than the Copernican [hypotheses] themselves, since whatever appearances arise from the Copernican [hypotheses] themselves, those too arise entirely, through all [points], from the inverted [ones] without any difference. Then you seem to carry the centres of the homocentrics of the remaining Planets around <b>not in the homocentric or centre of the greater Epicycle, but in the very Eccentric of the Sun</b> — since you do the same too in the Comet. And if it were so, they would deservedly be <b>new and diverse</b>, since by this reasoning the <b>Eccentricity of the Sun would necessarily mix itself into all the Planets</b>, and would introduce other appearances. But that the Observations answer to this supposition, I should scarcely believe. I know indeed that <b>Copernicus</b> demonstrates that the change of the Sun's Eccentricity is noticed in the remaining Planets too, which, however, he neglected in the Tables as less sensible. (Whence I too transposed that little Epicycle of the Sun into the greater Epicycle, which otherwise would have had to be placed back at the Earth, in such a way that it would have passed through the centre of the Earth by its lowest part.) Nevertheless, that is altogether necessary from his Hypothesis, since by the <b>Eccentric of the Sun he understands the Eccentric of the Earth</b>, whose centre, if it be changed, will necessarily change the Eccentricities of the remaining Planets, since they all regard this centre and their magnitudes are numbered from this centre. Whether, then, as I said, yours are entirely the same with the inverted Copernican [hypotheses], I cannot know for certain. But as to what you think — that the more diligently I shall have surveyed that general disposition of the Spheres, the more it will please me — know that the entirely contrary has long since happened to me (about which [I have spoken] also with the most Erudite man <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>). For the longer I consider it, the less it pleases me. For although it shows how, according to the mean motion of the Sun, the superior Planets are rendered direct, stationary, or retrograde, yet (which the Ptolemaic [system] also does) it only shows <b>that</b> they so come about, not <b>why</b> they so come about, and sprinkles the Planets with the <b>stain of retrogression</b>. But the <b>Copernican</b> [system] shows not only <b>that</b> those retrogressions and stations occur, but also <b>why</b> they so occur — not that there is so great an inconstancy of motion in the Planets themselves, which always regard their own proper motion, but that those retrogressions so <b>appear to us on account of the motion of the Earth</b>, and are thought to be in the Planets, whereas in reality they are in the Earth. Thus most beautifully <b>no motion is left except the proper [one]</b>: the first in the diurnal Revolution of the Earth, the second in its annual motion, the third in the Moon, the fourth in Venus, the fifth in Mercury, the sixth in Mars, the seventh in Jupiter, and the eighth at last in Saturn — without any stain of violence or retrogression. Then, who will ever believe that the <b>Centre of the greater Epicycle of the Sun is endowed with such efficacy</b> that it can drag all the Planets after itself — nay, drag [them] from their Spheres and lift [them] up again — when they are connected by no corporeal and tenacious matter? For which cause too, when I showed our most Illustrious Prince that Hypothesis of yours in your second Tome of the Comet[s], which he soon, as he beheld it, recognized (being sufficiently known to him from the Automaton): <i>\"Good gods,\"</i> he said in jest, <i>\"that circle of the Sun must be far stronger than bronze, since it can drag so many Planets with it.\"</i> For there stuck in his mind what I had so often sung into his most Illustrious Highness's ear — that the <b>motion of Traction must be removed from the Heaven</b>. Nor, as I think, will it easily be able to be granted to the Sun that it disturb the proper motions of the remaining Planets by this its <b>traction</b>. Besides, this inverted Copernican reasoning introduces <b>confusion into the Spheres of the Planets</b>. For although no collision of celestial bodies can follow thence (as you strive to excuse), yet no true or determined distinction of the Spheres remains, but all are confused among themselves. But the Copernican Hypothesis attributes to each Planet its own and determined space, from which it cannot go out by its motion. But you know that <b>God is the author not of confusion but of order</b>, and so founded nature that it abhors confusion — much less would he have wished to look upon this most beautiful edifice of inexhaustible wisdom with so great a stain. There is added this too: that <b>Nature does not act through more [means] when it can [act] through fewer</b>. Likewise that the change of the Sun's Eccentricity is felt in the remaining Planets too — which, although it can in some way be excused by the transposition of the Sun's lesser Epicycle to the Earth (as I said), yet nonetheless all things are by this reasoning feigned to come about <b>through traction</b>. These, then, and many other [things] considering again and again, I can find nothing other than that <b>no Hypothesis except the single one of Copernicus is true</b>. Nor does it sin against Physical principles; nor, because the Earth is a gross body, will it on that account be unfit for moving. For what do you think the other Stars are? For I think Copernicus does not err when he says that <b>gravity is nothing other than a certain natural appetite implanted in the parts by the divine providence of the Maker of the universe, that they may bring themselves into their unity and integrity, coming together into the form of a globe</b>; and that this affection is also in the Sun, the Moon, and the other lights of the Wanderers, so that by its efficacy they remain in that roundness in which they present themselves — which nonetheless effect their circuits in many ways. And surely, since the Earth, like the other Planets, is endowed with a round figure, and likewise hangs free in the Air, why do we say it is unfit for moving? We see that if we erect or suspend a globe most exactly and subtly on [its] Poles and Axis, and so, hanging freely, impel it into a gyre, it retains this motion long enough, nor can it suddenly be checked. If, therefore, an <b>artificial</b> motion, even impeded, can do this, how much more a <b>natural</b> [one], unimpeded? But the Physical absurdities <b>Copernicus</b> has more than sufficiently refuted. Nor does the <b>authority of the sacred writings</b> stand in the way either. For these are written not for me and you alone, but for absolutely all men, to whose grasp they also speak — as all the Theologians too confess in the explication of <b>Genesis chapter 1</b>. Otherwise the <b>Moon</b> would, against the Geometrical demonstrations, be greater than the rest of the Stars. But this too is manifest from the following. The <b>Hebrews</b> thought that the clouds could not hang on high unless there underlay some hard and impervious matter which sustained the Water. Accommodating himself, therefore, to their grasp, God says, <i>\"I will make the Heaven iron, lest it rain,\"</i> etc.; likewise, <i>\"he opened the windows of Heaven\"</i> in the Flood, etc.; likewise <b>Moses</b>, when he says that on the first day the <b>Firmament</b> was founded, to separate the Waters above us from the Waters below us. By which I think nothing else can be understood than that <b>that force, by which the clouds so hang on high, was founded on the first day</b>. So too, since we for Optical reasons think the Heaven to be as it were a stretched-out <b>tent</b>, therefore the sacred writings, following this opinion of the common [people], call the Heaven \"stretched-out\" [<i>expansum</i>], and the Prophets say that God stretches out the Heaven like a tent. For neither did these understand more about this matter than other common men, or even than <b>Lactantius</b>; nor did the Holy Spirit wish to reveal to them the wisdom which God poured into Nature, but that which is contained in that stupendous and supernatural mystery of the Redemption of the human race. And why was <b>Solomon</b> held wise even to a miracle, except that he had a far more excellent knowledge of the whole of Nature than anyone else before him? <b>Paul</b> too, when he says (Romans 1) that God is recognized from these visible [things], argues not obscurely that a far greater wisdom of God lies hidden <b>in Nature</b> than is revealed in the sacred writings. <b>In Nature</b>, I say: for of the mystery of Redemption, which the sacred writings properly treat, and of the recognition of the true God, I now do not speak. Thus the authority of the sacred writings, although it be brought forward quite plausibly, yet in reality in this matter will be able to stand in the way not at all; but we shall know only so much as we find out through <b>Mathematical Demonstrations</b>. But although Gellius Sascerides said that you have <b>Geometrical Demonstrations of your assertion</b>, yet this I, for a certain cause, should scarcely believe. Yet I do not on that account judge that the <b>Copernican Hypotheses are to be proposed to learners</b>, since they are scarcely perceived by the Artificers [skilled] — as not even <b>Maestlin</b> rightly understands the motion of <b>libration</b> in the Latitudes and the obliquity of the Zodiac; but [I judge] that those [hypotheses] which are easiest of all [should be proposed], so that step by step the access to Copernicus may be opened. Wherefore I too, in my Astronomical Elements, expunged that foregoing inversion, and substituted <b>Eccentrics on a mobile centre</b>, so that the learners might the more easily understand the motions and places of the Apogees, and, an Epicycle being added in the Planets, the directions, stations, and retrogressions might become the more manifest, nor the reckoning of the Latitudes be so obscure and intricate. Yet, lest I should fail the ingenious and those who already understand the common forms of the single motions, or wish to build <b>Automata</b>, I subjoined at the end, as a Corollary, that <b>Inversion of Copernicus</b> of which I spoke above, so that thus I might prepare the learners for the reading and easier understanding of Copernicus. But perhaps [I have said] more about this matter than was enough: to other [things], then.</p><p>As to what you think, toward the end of the <b>refutation of Roeslin</b>, that the <b>minimum Eccentricity of the Sun</b> has passed [occurred] — the same I too establish. For I refer the minimum Eccentricity and obliquity of the Sun to <b>Peurbach</b>. Nor is <b>Rheticus</b> to be heard either, who, while he writes that <b>al-Battānī</b> [Albategnius] too freely abuses the mysteries of <b>Astrology</b>, here falls into the same fault himself. For how can the change of the Sun's Eccentricity effect a change of empires? Nor do you unjustly reprehend that which <b>Philip Melanchthon</b> first introduced — namely, that the Sun has now been made nearer to the Earth — although <b>Cardano</b> too, in book 3 of the <i>Subtleties</i>, erroneously establishes the same. For they did not attain Copernicus's mind in this matter. I do not doubt that you too could find a far more exact <b>Hypothesis of the Comet's motion</b>, since the proportional decrement of its motion sufficiently argues that it was composed of circular [motions]. Nevertheless I judge it altogether superfluous to constitute <b>Hypotheses of Comets</b>, since none completes a Period, nor can any utility redound thence. Wherefore in my writing too I omitted the constitution of a Hypothesis. I myself beheld that Comet [of 1585] of which you deal in that Tome at <b>Bernburg, 10 November</b>, together with <b>Johann Troldenirer</b>, then prefect of the castle of Bernburg, by whom I had been invited to the <b>Martinmas feast</b>; it appeared to all dwelling at Bernburg on that day. For the Sky there was serene. When I showed that your second Tome to our most Illustrious Prince, he was occupied more than three whole hours in diligently unrolling it, and it pleased his Highness wondrously, so much so that he did not cease to praise it even to the bystanders; and he contended there with me that that book had been sent to him by you, but I argued from the inscription that it was mine, and subjoined that you would send the whole Work, if it were completed, to his most Illustrious Highness. See, therefore, that you <b>redeem my pledge</b>. And if [you should send] the same also to [his] <b>Son Maurice</b> — though not in this doctrine, yet in Latin, Greek, and Hebrew splendidly instructed and erudite beyond his Age — and likewise to <b>Bernhard, Prince of Anhalt</b>, dwelling here in our Court with Maurice, whom his parent <b>Joachim Ernst</b> of most happy memory had, together with [his] brothers, instructed in these Arts too, through <b>Caspar Gottschalk</b>, a man not unskilled in Mathematics: if, I say, you send that completed Work of yours hither to these two also, I do not doubt that you will do them a most welcome thing, by this reasoning <b>tacitly inciting them</b> to the love and cultivation of these most pleasant Arts, according to the grasp of [their] age. Those <b>Mathematical works</b> of which I wrote in [my] earlier letter I am about to publish shortly, the divine clemency favouring, when I will transmit them to you. But it would now be time that I at last close this too-prolix Letter, had not our most Illustrious Prince given me another [thing] in [his] commands. For since his most Illustrious Highness is so occupied in the <b>marrying-off of [his] Daughter</b> that he cannot reply, he commanded me to reply myself to those [things] which your Letter contains. But I find nothing in it which much needs a reply, or which has not been more than sufficiently set forth in his most Illustrious Highness's earlier letters sent to you — except that, in the <b>Observations instituted through Venus</b>, you seem to accuse us of error, and to confer the fault on the <b>Refractions</b>. But the Refractions could not stand in my way, since I instituted the Observations through Azimuths too, and, the distance of Venus from the <b>Third [star] of Aries [♈]</b> and others taken after the Sun's setting, on account of the Refractions (which, however, I separated [out]), it would necessarily have been found smaller than just, and so the places of the Stars would have been drawn back into the preceding [direction], against the order of the signs. But I do not wish to deal more about these. For you will see the reckoning of our Observations in our <b>Work</b> shortly to be published, God willing. I see, therefore, that nothing further now remains than that I at last finish this Letter. Wherefore fare best and most happily, most Noble and most Excellent Lord Tycho, and to me far dearest; and receive my candour in the best part, and write me back as soon as possible. Again, farewell.</p><p>Kassel, the 13th [day before the] Kalends of October [literally; = 13 October per the catalogue], in the Year of salvation 1588. Yours truly, <b>Christoph Rothmann.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VI (<i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>); the dataset's `source_edition` is Tycho's own 1596 print, in which Rothmann's letter appears; no MS witness recorded. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VI pp. 151–161</b> (PDF pp. 165–175); the <b>Alhazen book 7 ch. 2/4/[7]</b> and <b>Witelo book 10 props. 3/8/10</b> citations, Euclid I.21, the 52-radii / 1:100000 / 1:140608 figures, the twilight depressions (16½° / 24°), the 1558-comet positions, and the dateline are all confirmed against Dreyer (page anchors in the Editorial note). Sole un-machine-confirmable item: the <i>chapter</i> digit of the second Alhazen citation (book 7, ch. 7) is OCR-illegible in the scan; book 7 is legible and ch. 7 is secure from the surrounding series.</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1555 – c. 1600), mathematician to <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel</b>, working at the Kassel observatory alongside the clockmaker <b>Jost Bürgi</b>. Tycho (Uraniborg). This is Rothmann's substantive reply in the great <b>Tycho–Rothmann correspondence</b>, answering Tycho's two letters (the comet-book <i>Tomus II</i> sent via <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>, received 11 July, and a later one) — the natural-philosophical heart of the exchange.</li><li><b>Thesis 1 — fluid air-heavens (optics).</b> Rothmann holds the \"surrounding matter\" of the heavens is <b>pure air, identical in transparency to sublunary pure air</b> (against a distinct aether). His proofs are optical: (a) <b>diverse transparencies must produce refraction</b> (Alhazen 7.2/7.4, Witelo 10.3); so if there were an aether/air boundary, there would be a refraction-\"point,\" contradicting observation; (b) refraction <b>weakens light</b> (Alhazen 7.7, Witelo 10.10), yet zenith-stars are not brighter than others; (c) the <b>decisive demonstration</b> (Euclid I.21): a real aether/air refraction would be <b>greater for the fixed stars (farther) than for the Sun (nearer)</b> at the same altitude — but observation shows the opposite, so the refraction is from <b>vapours near the Earth</b>, not an aether/air boundary. He turns Tycho's own \"refraction to the zenith\" against him, and notes the <b>warm-window/cold-air</b> experiment. Subsidiary: no <b>sound</b> from stellar motion in pure air (the gross-air wind analogy; the <i>Superi</i> would be deafened); Melanchthon's <i>Physics</i> on the elements' proportion; the 52-Earth-radii lunar distance implying air 1:140,608 the Earth-globe. On the <b>fifth essence</b> (Tycho's chymical point): Rothmann grants it lurks in earthly things but holds it <b>generated by the Sun</b> (the <b>Sol Microcosmi</b> / heart-chambers / vital-spirit analogy), not identical with the planetary \"surrounding matter.\"</li><li><b>Thesis 2 — comet tails self-luminous.</b> Reversing his earlier deference to <b>Cardano/Scaliger/Apian</b>, Rothmann now holds the <b>tail is of the head's own (rarer) matter, shining by illumination, not pointing at the Sun</b> — proved by the <b>Landgrave's 1558-comet record</b> (quoted from <b>Cyprian Leovitius's</b> gilded <i>Ephemerides</i> that Wilhelm IV carried with his Bible, self-moving globe, and small quadrant): the comet's tail pointed toward <b>Ursa Major</b>, not the Sun. The tail's <b>curvature</b> (inexplicable on Tycho's straight-ray view) and the <b>great comets of Seneca and Pontanus</b> (one equalling the Milky Way, one of 50°) confirm it. On comet <i>matter</i>, Rothmann's view differs from Aristotle's (tenuous/ray-purged, not gross/burning; in the planetary spheres, not just sublunary); he notes that Tycho, by introducing <b>generation into the heaven</b>, must introduce <b>corruption</b> too.</li><li><b>Thesis 3 — the Copernican defence (the historic core).</b> Rothmann reads Tycho's \"new hypotheses\" as <b>\"inverted Copernicus\"</b> (geo-heliocentric), and may be <b>diverse</b> only in carrying the planets' homocentric centres on the <b>Sun's eccentric</b> (mixing the solar eccentricity into all planets — which observations would not support). He had himself built such constructions (<i>Homocentrepicyclepicycli</i>) \"entirely according to Copernicus\" (as did <b>Rheticus</b> and <b>Reinhold</b>); the Landgrave had a 6-inch <b>automaton</b> showing all planetary motions. But Rothmann rejects the geo-heliocentric arrangement: it shows only <b>that</b>, not <b>why</b>, retrogressions occur (the \"stain of retrogression\"), whereas <b>Copernicus shows why</b> — the retrogressions are the Earth's motion reflected; only <b>proper motions</b> remain (Earth's diurnal + annual, then Moon/Venus/Mercury/Mars/Jupiter/Saturn). The <b>traction objection</b>: the Sun's epicycle-centre cannot \"drag\" the planets with no connecting matter (the Landgrave's joke that \"the Sun's circle must be stronger than bronze\"); the system \"<b>confuses the spheres</b>,\" and \"God is the author of order, not confusion,\" and \"Nature does not act through more when fewer suffice.\" He affirms <b>gravity as Copernicus defined it</b> (a natural appetite of parts toward their whole, in all the globes — <i>De rev.</i> I.9), the Earth's fitness to move (the spun-globe analogy), and that <b>Scripture accommodates the vulgar</b> (Genesis 1; the \"iron heaven,\" \"windows of heaven,\" the firmament = \"that force by which clouds hang\"; the \"stretched tent\"; Lactantius; Solomon's natural wisdom; Paul, Rom. 1: greater wisdom lies in Nature than is revealed in Scripture). He doubts Tycho really has \"geometrical demonstrations\" for his system. Yet for <b>teaching</b> he keeps non-Copernican forms (even <b>Maestlin</b> misunderstands the libration), reserving the \"Copernican inversion\" as a corollary to ease students toward Copernicus.</li><li><b>Other matters.</b> The <b>twilight</b> discrepancy (Tycho's morning 16½° vs Rothmann's evening 24° depression); the request for Tycho's chymical-medical teaching (Ramus's \"number, weight, measure\"; the <b>Sulphur/Salt/Mercury</b> spirits; natural/vital/animal spirits; soul-spirit-body union; the <b>stone</b> and <b>gout</b>); praise of the comet <i>Tomus II</i> (the aethereal course \"irrefutably demonstrated\"); the <b>lunar max-latitude</b> agreement but the <b>ascending node</b> (\"Head of the Dragon\") off the Tables by &gt;4°; the <b>minimum solar eccentricity/obliquity</b> referred to <b>Peurbach</b>; <b>Rheticus</b> and <b>Melanchthon/Cardano</b> faulted on solar-eccentricity-and-empires and \"the Sun now nearer\"; comet-hypotheses \"superfluous\" (no period); the <b>1585 comet</b> seen at <b>Bernburg</b> (10 Nov, with <b>Johann Troldenirer</b>, at a Martinmas feast); the Landgrave's 3-hour absorption in the <i>Tomus II</i> and the dispute over whose copy; the request for completed copies for <b>Maurice</b> (the future Moritz the Learned) and <b>Bernhard of Anhalt</b> (tutored by <b>Caspar Gottschalk</b>); and the closing defence of the <b>Venus observations</b> (refraction \"separated out,\" azimuths used) — Rothmann answering for the Landgrave, busy \"marrying off his daughter.\"</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>; <b>Wilhelm IV</b> (<i>Illustrissimus Princeps</i>); <b>Maurice of Hesse</b>; <b>Bernhard of Anhalt</b>, <b>Joachim Ernst of Anhalt</b>, <b>Caspar Gottschalk</b>; <b>Johann Troldenirer</b> (Bernburg); <b>Alhazen</b> (Ibn al-Haytham), <b>Witelo</b> (<i>Vitellio</i>); <b>Aristotle, Plato, Pliny, Lactantius, Solomon, Paul, Moses, Christ/Nicodemus</b>; <b>Copernicus, Rheticus, Reinhold, Maestlin, Peurbach, al-Battānī, Cardano, Scaliger, Apian, Ramus, Roeslin, Graminaeus</b>; <b>Philip Melanchthon</b>; <b>Seneca, Pontanus</b>; <b>Cyprian Leovitius</b>. Places: <b>Kassel</b> (<i>Cassellis</i>), <b>Bernburg</b>, <b>Knudstrup</b> (Tycho's title).</li><li><b>Greek / Latin / technical tags.</b> <i>φιλαληθέια</i> = love of truth; <i>ἀυτόματον</i> = automaton (the Kassel planetary clock-model); <i>Homocentrepicyclepicycli</i> = Rothmann's coinage for homocentric-epicycle-upon-epicycle constructions; <i>Diaphana</i> = transparencies/media; <i>Crepuscula</i> = twilights; <i>Pyramis</i> (of rays) = the optical cone; <i>Davus sum, non Oedipus</i> = \"I am a Davus, not an Oedipus\" (Terence — no riddle-solver); <i>Superi</i> = the gods above. <i>Sol Microcosmi</i> = the heart as the body's sun. The Copernican <b>gravity</b> definition quotes <i>De revolutionibus</i> I.9; the planet-order recital echoes I.10. Glyphs: ♍ Virgo, ♈ Aries. <i>Torquetum</i> = the medieval observing instrument (used for the 1558 comet). <i>Alrucaba</i> = the pole-star/Ursa-Minor region (Arabic).</li><li><b>Dateline crux (named).</b> The source reads <i>\"Cassellis 13. Calendas Octobris … M.D.LXXXIIX [1588].\"</i> Strict Roman dating (<i>a.d. XIII Kal. Oct.</i>) = <b>19 September</b>; the catalogue/edition reads <b>13 October</b> (taking \"13\" + \"Octobris\"). The form is anomalous (no \"ante diem\"; \"Calendas\" loosely). The header follows the catalogue's <b>13 October</b>, shows the literal dateline, and flags the discrepancy here rather than silently resolving it. <b>Old Style</b> (Lutheran Hesse). Within the 1558-comet quote, the Roman dates resolve as: <i>13 Kal. Sept.</i> = 20 Aug, <i>12 Kal.</i> = 21 Aug, <i>10 Kal.</i> = 23 Aug 1558.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> ~30 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, and to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599); <i>obseruamus est</i> in the 1558-comet quote — <b>confirmed (2026-06-02) to be Dreyer's own printed reading</b>, which he flags with a dagger († sic); it is therefore the edition's reading, not a downstream transcription slip — left in the source block and rendered as <i>observatus</i> in translation; <i>Illustridimus</i> (for <i>Illustrissimus</i>), <i>praefertim</i> (for <i>praesertim</i>), <i>aspexere</i> (for <i>aspergere</i>?) left as printed; v/u orthography retained.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, doctrinally and technically dense letter — the optical case for fluid air-heavens (with exact Alhazen/Witelo/Euclid citations), the self-luminous-tail thesis (with the quoted 1558-comet record), and the full Copernican defence against the \"inverted Copernicus\" — rendered securely throughout; Rothmann's eager, frank register and the controlled glossary (refraction, transparency/<i>diaphanum</i>, eccentricity, epicycle, homocentric, hypothesis, libration, <i>restitutio</i>, the optical terms) are held consistent with the surrounding correspondence (the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, the Herwart letters of 16 Nov 1599 and 28 Aug 1600, and the Tycho-side comet/quintessence doctrine of the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 and the undated Peucer letter of 1590). The numerical and citational spine (52 radii, 1:140,608, 16½°/24° twilight, 1½′/3′ refractions, Alhazen 7.2/7.4/7.7, Witelo 10.3/10.8/10.10, Euclid I.21, the 1558-comet positions) is internally consistent. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VI pp. 151–161) and confirms every citation and numeral</b> (and that <i>obseruamus est</i> is Dreyer's own daggered reading), so the collation reservation is <b>lifted</b>. One genuine residue remains: the <b>dateline crux</b> (<i>\"13. Calendas Octobris\"</i> = literally 19 Sept vs the catalogue's 13 Oct) — confirmed verbatim in Dreyer, who likewise prints no \"ante diem\" — reproduced and flagged, not resolved. The OCR \"A\"-sigla removal is declared. It does not obscure the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (the dateline ambiguity is the one thing that, on its own, might still argue against a clean [A]).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo.slice_pdf is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible (per brief, capped at grade B and noted here). Audited published_english against source_text alone, paragraph by paragraph across the full letter (salutation through subscription): checked every proper name (Nicodemus, Melanchthon, Aristotle, Pliny, Alhazen, Witelo/Vitellion, Euclid I.21, Cardano, Scaliger, Apian, Graminaeus, Davus/Oedipus, Cyprianus Leovitius, Seneca, Pontanus, Copernicus, Rheticus, Reinhold, Maestlin, Roeslin, Peurbach, al-Battani/Albategnius, Gellius Sascerides, Ramus, Lactantius, Solomon, Paul/Romans 1, Moses/Genesis 1, Johann Troldenirer, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Maurice, Bernhard of Anhalt, Joachim Ernst, Caspar Gottschalk), every number/date (11 July; 16.5 deg/24 deg twilight; 52 Earth-radii; 1:100000 / 1:140608; 30/40/45 deg altitude; sixfold denser vapours; 30/31 Earth-radii; 1.5/3 min refraction; the 1558 comet note with day-by-day Kalends-of-September conversions 13/12/10 Kal. Sept. = Aug 20/21/23, positions in Virgo and latitude, all verified arithmetically correct; the 6-inch automaton; the 8-planet motion ordering Earth-diurnal/annual, Moon, Venus, Mercury, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn; Bernburg 10 November; three-hour reading; 1588 subscription year in Roman numerals M.D.LXXXIIX), and all pronoun-bearing self/other attributions flagged as pipeline failure modes elsewhere (Rothmann-vs-Aristotle tail-matter contrast, Rothmann-vs-Tycho book-ownership dispute before the Landgrave, the sung-into-the-Prince's-ear traction remark, the natural/vital/animal-spirit chiasmus, the Sun-vs-Venus tail-source attribution) - none were flipped. No fabricated facts, no dropped clauses, no hardened hedges, no misattributions found. One bracketed catalogue gloss on the closing date ('13 Calendas Octobris... = 13 October per the catalogue') is clearly marked and attributed to the catalogue rather than asserted by the translator, so per instructions it was not counted as a defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15880817003", "next": "brahe-t_15890221002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880817003", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890221002"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges both recent letters and the second tome of *De mundi aetherei*, then argues persistently that the celestial matter is elemental air and not aether.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15881013001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15881101001", "d": "1588-11-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 1 November 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.47</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, VIRTUTE ET DOCTRINA PRAeSTANTI AC MATHEMATUM ILLUSTRATORI EXIMIO D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, INSULAe HUENAe DANICI HELLESPONTI DOMINO, D. ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISS. vir, domine et amice observande. Binas ab eo tempore, quo meus famulus abs te redijt, dedi ad te literas. Alteras Gallus quidam Mathematicus praestans, cuius nomen nunc non succurrit, reddere debuerat, alteras Rostochiensi tabellario tradideram. Cum neutrarum facias mentionem, utrasque intercidisse conicio. Tuae literae cum libro abs te edito sub finem mensis Iulij sunt mihi redditae. Et, profecto, fuerunt gratissimae, quibus perlectis ad librum avidissime accessi et percurri, initio celeriter, ut generalem totius operis cognitionem haurirem. Vehementer, ita me Deus amet, eo sum oblectatus. Doleo autem, quod tum ob aetatem iam ingravescentem, tum ob curas domesticas ac professionem Medicam, quae alimenta affert, illi studio amplius navare non possum; nihilominus aliorum in hoc studij genere laboribus mirifice delector.</p><p>De tuo libro quod dicam habeo nihil, ita accurate omnia sunt abs te excussa et examinata, ut mea lima non egeant. Novam illam orbium coelestium dispositionem nondum accuratius considerare potui. D. CURTIUS Caesaris Consiliarius et Procancellarius Imperij, Mathematicus excellens et iudicio acri, fuit quoque vehementer tuo scripto delectatus. Cum eius iudicium de tuo libro requirerem, respondit non temere de hypothesibus iudicandum esse. Rem attentius considerandam esse. Te officiose salvere iussit, cohortarique, ut coeptum cursum non interrumpas. Inter alia dixit quoque te nimis multam fecisse mentionem et praedicasse nimium VINCKLERUM illum hominem Astronomiae rudissimum. Ex mea Dialexi integras pagellas descripsit, et pessime suo Cometae Anni 77 accommodavit. In mea epistola ad MYLIUM impressa adiunxeram in chartula, quid insuper nugatus fuisset de Cometa Anni 80. Adscribebat eidem parallaxin maximam 15 fere grad. et tamen supra Lunam illum collocabat. Te hanc annotatiunculam meam non animadvertisse existimo, alioquin dedolasses eum asperiori dolabro et pectine acriori depexuisses, aut plane praeterijsses silentio, tanquam omnium indignissimum qui caeterorum, quorum abs te honorificentissima facta est mentio, latus clauderet. Sed valeat iste nugator et ardelio. Satis ipsemet propinavit se deridendum omnibus. Porro quod a me in plerisque dissentias, non fero moleste, mi TYCHO, neque tibi propterea succenseo, imo gratias ago maximas, quod tuo beneficio factus sim eruditior, et veritas abs te asserta est, iamque triumphat. Non mihi mea ita placent, nec meos partus ita diligo, ut in illis et errata et nevos aliquos non agnoscam, et cum possint abstergi, venustiorque forma induci, admittere nolim. SCULTETICUM ingenium mihi non est, qui quod aliquando candide meam de eius Cometa Anni 77 et de Calendarij sui editione sententiam tulerim, et quod nimium Papissare et cum Iesuitis colludere videretur, odium erga me concepit, et tum in literis ad amicos perscriptis, tum in suis istis nugacissimis Calendarijs suggillare non destitit, donec illi significari mandassem, desinat me lacessere, alioquin clausurum me ipsi os. Sic mea amica commonefactio in odium et simultatem erupit. Iam vero sibi temperat et sapit. Nuper enim per meum filium me salvere iussit. Ego vicissim dabo operam, ut et benevolum meum erga se animum, et redintegrandae cum ipso amicitiae cupidissimum esse liquido agnoscat. Haec ideo commemoro, ut meum animum plane cognoscas.</p><p>Meis lapsibus omnino patrocinari nec possum, nec debeo. Ac etsi contra tuas defensiones pro MAeSTLINO aliquid dici possit, tamen tuo iudicio repugnare nolo, tum etiam quod MAeSTLINUM ob insignem peritiam Mathematum mirifice diligo, tantumque tribuo, ut dicere ausim, sed absque aliorum invidia, scholas Germanicas, PRAeTORIO unico excepto, parem ipsi habere neminem. Hoc tantum adijcio, bona tua et MAeSTLINI pace, de ratione motuum Cometarum, quod Planetarum ideo certae hypotheses sunt constitutae, quia eorum motus integri poterant animadverti, quomodo item accederent ad Solem aut ab eo regrederentur. At Cometae non absolvunt suas periodos, neque certa lege accedunt ad Solem, et recedunt ab eodem. Itaque non videtur possibile motus eorum rationem explicare et veluti vincire certis legibus. Pulchram quidem et ingenuosam fuisse speculationem MAeSTLINI praedicas, et ego assentior. Sed pulchritudo et subtilitas veritati, virtuti in hac universitate omnium pulcherrimae, anteferenda non est, quam veritatem nec MAeSTLINUM nec quemquam alium in Theoria Cometarum plane, quomodo aliorum Planetarum, video esse assequutum. Tendimus quidem eo omnes, maxime vero tu, qui ex professo iam tractas hoc argumentum, et iam ex hisce, quae veluti fundamenta futuri aedificij posita esse video, multa profecto mihi de te polliceor, et Deum oro ut ad haec Dei opera mirabilia illustranda longum satis curriculum vitae concedere dignetur. Porro in calculo MAeSTLINI, quem tu sequeris, te commonefacere volui, ut inspicias, an ille in numeris alicubi lapsus sit, an vero nos fallamur, item an ea differentia notabilem in sequentibus errorem pariat. Inspexi meas chartas, in quibus olim MAeSTLINI calculum a me examinatum reperi. Primum Epocham Commutationis mediae ad diem 24 Novembris cum MAeSTLINO statuis 206 gr. 33'. Meus calculus habet tantum 206 gr. 18'. Aequationem Commutationis Cometae habet MAeSTLINUS 5, 48', tu vero 5, 1 2/3. Item Aequationem Eccentri habet MAeSTLINUS O, 5. Sic et ego; tu vero 1, 18 1/3. Haec fortassis levicula sunt. Videnda tamen, an in scopo ad quem tendimus, notabilem aliquam differentiam afferant. Mihi repetere et prosequi calculationem ob meas occupationes non licuit.</p><p>De nova Stella nullos alios scriptores, praeter illos quos in mea Dialexi vidisti adductos, cognovi.</p><p>APIANI Astronomicum aliquando habui, et diligenter inquisivi an ex ipsius Datis in parallaxium cognitionem pervenire potuissem, verum insufficientia fuerunt eius Data. Alicubi diserte scribit parallaxim Cometae inveniri non posse, nisi prius distantia eius a Terra cognita fuerit, quod falsissimum esse nemo est qui ignoret. Ab APIANO igitur nihil est quod expectes. Hunc eruditum pulverem, in quo nos versamur, ille ne somniavit quidem. Eius artem saepe RHETICUS, bonae memoriae, vocare solitus erat faden Dunst.</p><p>IOANNIS PENAe Galli scriptum de Cometa nunquam vidi. Librum illum, quem olim tibi Ratisbonae donaveram, ab eo tempore nunquam vidi, neque etiam authoris recordari possum. Lustrabam, Bibliothecas nostrates, invenire non potui. Eius loco mitto ad te olim expetitum librum VERNERI de Elementis Conicis, ubi etiam tractatulus de motu 8 Sphaerae et alia habentur. A FABRICIO Vienna illum accepi, te salvere iubet. His quod addam habeo nihil, nisi ut feliciter valeas. Datae Pragae die 1. Novemb. veteri stylo Anno 88.</p><p>Literas quas scripsisti 17 Augusti ex mercatu Francofordensi accepi, una cum alijs ad SCULTETUM, quas illi Gorlicium misi.</p><p>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, outstanding in nobility of birth, in virtue and in learning, and an eminent illuminator of the mathematical sciences, lord of the island of Hven in the Danish Hellespont, my lord and friend to be esteemed — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble sir, my lord and friend to be esteemed. Since the time when my servant returned from you I have sent you two letters. The one a certain distinguished French mathematician — whose name does not now come to me — was to have delivered; the other I had handed to the Rostock courier. Since you make mention of neither, I conjecture that both have gone astray. Your letter, together with the book published by you, was delivered to me toward the end of the month of July. And indeed they were most welcome; once they had been read through I turned most eagerly to the book and ran through it, at first quickly, so as to drink in a general acquaintance with the whole work. Vehemently, so help me God, was I delighted by it. Yet I grieve that, both on account of my now advancing age and on account of my domestic cares and my profession of medicine, which supplies my livelihood, I am no longer able to devote myself further to that study; nonetheless I take wonderful delight in the labours of others in this kind of study.</p><p>Concerning your book I have nothing to say, so accurately has everything been sifted and examined by you, that it does not need my file. That new arrangement of the celestial orbs I have not yet been able to consider more closely. Master Curtius, the Emperor's Councillor and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, an excellent mathematician and of keen judgement, was likewise vehemently delighted by your writing. When I sought his judgement of your book, he replied that one must not judge rashly concerning hypotheses, [and] that the matter must be considered more attentively. He bade me greet you dutifully, and exhort you not to interrupt the course you have begun. Among other things he also said that you had made too much mention of, and praised too highly, that Vinckler, a man most ignorant of astronomy. From my <i>Dialexis</i> he copied out whole little pages, and most wretchedly fitted them to his comet of the year [15]77. In my letter to Mylius, [now] in print, I had added on a slip what he had moreover trifled about concerning the comet of the year [15]80. He assigned to it a parallax of nearly 15 degrees at the maximum, and yet placed it above the Moon. I think you did not notice this little note of mine; otherwise you would have hewn him with a rougher axe and combed him down with a sharper comb, or else passed him over altogether in silence, as the least worthy of all to close the file of the others, of whom most honourable mention has been made by you. But let that trifler and busybody fare [as he may]. He has himself given all men enough to mock at. Furthermore, that you dissent from me in very many points I do not take amiss, my Tycho, nor am I angry with you on that account; rather I give the greatest thanks, that by your kindness I have been made more learned, and the truth has been asserted by you, and now triumphs. I am not so pleased with my own things, nor do I so cherish my own offspring, that I do not acknowledge in them both errors and certain blemishes, and — when they can be wiped away and a more comely form put on — am not unwilling to admit it. I have not a Scultetus's temper, who, because I once frankly delivered my opinion of his comet of the year [15]77 and of the edition of his calendar, and because he seemed to play the papist too much and to collude with the Jesuits, conceived a hatred toward me, and ceased not to taunt me, both in letters written to friends and in those most trifling calendars of his, until I had ordered it to be signified to him that he should stop provoking me, or else I would shut his mouth for him. Thus my friendly admonition burst out into hatred and feud. But now indeed he restrains himself and is wise. For lately, through my son, he sent me his greeting. I in turn shall take pains that he may plainly recognise both my goodwill toward him and that I am most desirous of renewing my friendship with him. These things I therefore recall, that you may know my mind clearly.</p><p>My own lapses I can in no way defend, nor ought I. And although something might be said against your defences on Maestlin's behalf, yet I am unwilling to oppose your judgement — and also because I wonderfully cherish Maestlin for his remarkable skill in the mathematical sciences, and rate him so highly that I would venture to say (but without envy toward others) that the German schools, with the single exception of Praetorius, have no one equal to him. This much only I add — by your leave and Maestlin's — concerning the account of the motions of comets: that the hypotheses of the planets have been established as certain for this reason, that their motions could be observed entire, and likewise how they approached the Sun or receded from it. But comets do not complete their periods, nor do they approach the Sun and recede from it by a fixed law. And so it does not seem possible to explain the account of their motions and, as it were, to bind them with fixed laws. You proclaim that Maestlin's speculation was indeed beautiful and ingenious, and I agree. But beauty and subtlety are not to be set before truth — that virtue most beautiful of all in this universe — which truth I do not see that either Maestlin or anyone else has fully attained in the theory of comets, in the manner [achieved] for the other planets. We all do indeed strive toward that, but most of all you, who now treat this subject professedly; and already, from these things which I see laid down as it were the foundations of a future edifice, I truly promise myself much concerning you, and I pray God that he may deign to grant a course of life long enough for the illustrating of these wonderful works of God. Furthermore, in Maestlin's calculation, which you follow, I wished to put you on notice, that you may examine whether he has somewhere erred in the numbers, or whether instead we are deceived, and likewise whether that difference begets a notable error in what follows. I have looked over my papers, in which I once found Maestlin's calculation examined by me. First, the epoch of the mean commutation for the 24th day of November you set with Maestlin at 206 deg. 33′. My calculation has only 206 deg. 18′. The equation of the commutation of the comet Maestlin has at 5°, 48′, but you at 5°, 1 2/3′. Likewise the equation of the eccentric Maestlin has at 0°, 5′. So have I too; but you at 1°, 18 1/3′. These things perhaps are trifling. They must be looked into, nonetheless, [to see] whether in the goal toward which we strive they bring some notable difference. To repeat and pursue the calculation has not been permitted me, on account of my occupations.</p><p>Of the new star I have come to know no other writers besides those whom you saw adduced in my <i>Dialexis</i>.</p><p>Apian's <i>Astronomicum</i> I once had, and I diligently inquired whether from his own data I might have arrived at a knowledge of the parallaxes; but his data were insufficient. Somewhere he expressly writes that the parallax of a comet cannot be found unless its distance from the Earth has first been known — which there is no one who does not know to be utterly false. From Apian, therefore, there is nothing for you to expect. This learned dust in which we are engaged he did not so much as dream of. His art Rheticus, of good memory, used often to call <i>faden Dunst</i> [insipid vapour].</p><p>The writing of Jean Pena the Frenchman on the comet I have never seen. That book which I once gave you at Regensburg I have never seen since that time, nor can I even recall the author. I scoured our local libraries [but] could not find it. In its place I send you the book of Werner <i>On the Conic Elements</i>, long sought by you, in which there is also a little treatise on the motion of the eighth sphere and other matters. I received it from Fabricius at Vienna; he bids you greeting. To these I have nothing to add, save that you fare happily. Given at Prague, the 1st day of November, Old Style, the year [15]88.</p><p>The letter you wrote on 17 August I received from the Frankfurt fair, together with others addressed to Scultetus, which I sent on to him at Görlitz.</p><p>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[daeus].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer and personal physician at the imperial court in Prague; a leading European authority on the nova of 1572 and the comet of 1577, and Tycho's chief correspondent and ally in Bohemia. He signs <i>THAD.</i> (abbreviated).</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>INSULAe HUENAe DANICI HELLESPONTI DOMINO</i> — \"lord of the island of <b>Hven</b> in the Danish Hellespont\": the Hellespont = the Øresund (the Sound), in whose narrows Hven (Uraniborg) lies; an honorific conceit for Tycho's seat.</li><li><b>The book published by you.</b> <i>libro abs te edito … Novam illam orbium coelestium dispositionem</i> — Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), which presents the comet of 1577 and, in cap. VIII, the <b>new arrangement of the celestial orbs</b> (the geo-heliocentric / Tychonic world-system). Hagecius received it in late July 1588.</li><li><b><i>D. Curtius</b></i> = <b>Jacob Kurtz (Curtius) von Senftenau</b> (d. 1594), Imperial Councillor and Vice-Chancellor (<i>Procancellarius Imperii</i>) under Rudolf II, a capable mathematician; his cautious verdict — <i>non temere de hypothesibus iudicandum</i> (\"one must not judge rashly concerning hypotheses\") — is preserved verbatim and unsmoothed.</li><li><b><i>Vincklerus</b></i> — <b>Nicolaus Winckler</b> (Vincklerus, d. 1613), physician and astrological writer of Hall in Swabia; Hagecius (reporting Curtius) dismisses him as <i>Astronomiae rudissimum</i> (\"most ignorant of astronomy\") and a plagiarist of the <i>Dialexis</i>. The vituperative imagery — <i>dedolasses … asperiori dolabro et pectine acriori depexuisses</i> (\"you would have hewn him with a rougher axe and combed him down with a sharper comb\") and <i>nugator et ardelio</i> (\"trifler and busybody\") — is kept as metaphor.</li><li><b><i>Dialexis</b></i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574), his treatise on the 1572 nova; <i>integras pagellas descripsit</i> = \"copied out whole little pages.\"</li><li><b><i>Mylius</b></i> — Hagecius refers to his own printed <i>epistola ad Mylium</i> (the letter to Mylius), almost certainly addressed to the Görlitz physician-astronomer of that name; the precise identification is not fixed by the text and is left as given.</li><li><b><i>Scultetus</b></i> — <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartoloměj Scultetus, 1540–1614), mathematician, cartographer and calendar-maker of <b>Görlitz</b> (<i>Gorlicium</i>); the <i>Cometa Anni 77</i> is his tract on the 1577 comet, the <i>Calendaria</i> his almanacs. <i>Papissare</i> (a coinage, \"to play the papist\") and <i>cum Iesuitis colludere</i> (\"collude with the Jesuits\") are confessional jibes, kept literal. The closing postscript routes Tycho's letters to Scultetus via Görlitz.</li><li><b><i>Maestlin</b></i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), mathematician at Tübingen, Kepler's teacher; his <i>De Cometa anni 1577</i> attempted to bind the comet's motion to a circular path about the Sun. Hagecius rejects this in principle: comets <i>non absolvunt suas periodos</i> (\"do not complete their periods\") and so cannot be reduced to <i>certae hypotheses</i> like the planets. <i>PRAeTORIO unico excepto</i> — <b>Johannes Praetorius</b> (1537–1616), mathematician at Altdorf, named as the only German mathematician Hagecius rates Maestlin's equal.</li><li><b>Cometary calculation (technical).</b> The numerical comparison concerns Maestlin's reduction of the 1577 comet, which Tycho follows. <i>Epocha Commutationis mediae</i> = \"epoch of the mean <b>commutation</b>\" (the heliocentric–geocentric elongation angle) for 24 November: Tycho/Maestlin 206° 33′ vs. Hagecius 206° 18′. <i>Aequatio Commutationis</i> (the <b>equation of commutation</b>): Maestlin 5° 48′, Tycho 5° 1 2/3′. <i>Aequatio Eccentri</i> (<b>equation of the eccentric</b>): Maestlin and Hagecius 0° 5′, Tycho 1° 18 1/3′. The minutes-values are transcribed exactly as printed (e.g. <i>5, 1 2/3</i>, <i>1, 18 1/3</i>); fractional minutes are kept, not rounded. <i>Aequatio</i> in its astronomical sense is rendered <b>equation</b> per glossary §5.1. <i>parallaxin maximam 15 fere grad.</i> = \"a parallax of nearly 15 degrees at the maximum\" — <i>maximam</i> is the source's own word (not an editorial insertion); the gloss \"[the] maximum\" stands on Hagecius's text. The internal contradiction Hagecius flags in Winckler (a ~15° parallax would place a body well <i>below</i> the Moon, yet Winckler set it <i>above</i> the Moon) is preserved, not corrected.</li><li><b><i>Apiani Astronomicum</b></i> — <b>Peter Apian</b>'s <i>Astronomicum Caesareum</i> (Ingolstadt, 1540); Hagecius judges its cometary data insufficient for parallax and singles out Apian's claim that a comet's parallax cannot be found without first knowing its distance — which he calls <i>falsissimum</i> (\"utterly false\"). <i>Hunc eruditum pulverem</i> (\"this learned dust\") is his self-deprecating figure for the technical drudgery of the discipline.</li><li><b>German interpolation.</b> <i>faden Dunst</i> — Early New High German, \"insipid/stale vapour\" (<i>fad</i> \"insipid\" + <i>Dunst</i> \"vapour, haze\"); reported as <b>Rheticus</b>'s habitual put-down of Apian's art. Transcribed verbatim in the source and glossed; <b>Rheticus</b> = <b>Georg Joachim Rheticus</b> (1514–1574), Copernicus's disciple, <i>bonae memoriae</i> (\"of good memory,\" i.e. now deceased).</li><li><b><i>Ioannes Pena</b></i> — <b>Jean Pena</b> (c. 1528–1558), French royal mathematician; his views on comets appeared in the preface to his edition of Euclid's <i>Optica/Catoptrica</i> (Paris, 1557). <i>Verneri de Elementis Conicis</i> — <b>Johannes Werner</b> (1468–1522) of Nuremberg, whose works include <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (on the motion of the eighth sphere = precession/trepidation) and treatises on conics; sent in Pena's place. <i>Fabricius</i> (at Vienna) — most plausibly <b>Paul Fabricius</b> (c. 1519–1589), imperial mathematician and physician at Vienna; identification offered, not asserted, as the text gives only the surname.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Pragae</i> = <b>Prague</b>; <i>Ratisbona</i> = <b>Regensburg</b>; <i>Vienna</i>; <i>Gorlicium</i> = <b>Görlitz</b>; <i>mercatus Francofordensis</i> = the <b>Frankfurt</b> (book) fair.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline reads <i>Datae Pragae die 1. Novemb. <b>veteri stylo</b> Anno 88</i> — \"Given at Prague, the 1st day of November, <b>Old Style</b>, the year [15]88.\" Hagecius, writing from Catholic Prague (on the Gregorian/New Style calendar since 1584), here deliberately gives the date in <b>Old Style</b> for his Danish correspondent, who kept the Julian calendar; the explicit <i>veteri stylo</i> is the period's own gap-bridging convention. No conversion is supplied or implied beyond what the text states. The catalogue <i>label</i> (\"1588. 1. november\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two mistranslations corrected against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p.146 (Latin unaltered, confirmed correct as stored): 'am unwilling to admit it' restored to 'am not unwilling to admit it' (the negative result clause 'non...ita...ut...nolim' had lost its outer negation, reversing Hagecius's stated openness to correction); and 'he bade me greet [him in turn]' corrected to 'he sent me his greeting' (the idiom 'me salvere iussit' had been reversed in direction, making Hagecius the greeter rather than the greeted).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical numbers are transcribed verbatim; the only soft points are the unidentified persons named by surname alone — <i>Mylius</i> and <i>Fabricius</i> (Vienna) — flagged above rather than over-determined, and the proposed <i>Fabricius</i> = Paul Fabricius is offered as an identification, not asserted.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only residue is two incidental surnames (Mylius, Fabricius); no textual crux, date confirmed, sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full published_english against source_text and against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.145-147 (Hagecius to Tycho, 1 Nov 1588), read as page images (slice pages 1-3 of the 4-page PDF; page 4/148 is the opening of the next letter, Tycho to Henricus Brucaeus, and irrelevant to this one). our source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin essentially verbatim throughout — no TEXT_ERROR found. Checked opening address/header, closing subscription, and every proper name (Curtius, Vinckler, Scultetus, Maestlin, Praetorius, Apian, Rheticus, Ioannes Pena, Werner, Fabricius, Mylius) and every number/date (24 November, 206gr33'/206gr18', 5,48'/5,1⅔, 0,5/1,18⅓, ~15 degrees parallax, comet years 77 and 80, 17 August, 1 November 1588, Frankfurt fair, Görlitz, Regensburg/Ratisbona) — all render correctly with no fabricated or dropped facts, names, or figures, and no whole sentence or clause is silently omitted. Found two localized but genuine sense-reversing MISTRANSLATIONs (both negation/direction flips, not fabrications): (1) Hagecius's stated willingness to have his own errors corrected is rendered as an unwillingness; (2) the direction of a reconciliatory greeting with Scultetus (Scultetus greets Hagecius first) is reversed into Hagecius being told to greet Scultetus. Both are confined to single clauses and do not corrupt any name, date, or number — the translation is otherwise careful and largely faithful across a long, dense letter — but per the grading rule any sense-changing MISTRANSLATION caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The Latin is a negative result clause (\"Non mihi mea ita placent... ut... non agnoscam, et... admittere nolim\" = 'I am not so pleased with my own things that I fail to acknowledge errors, or refuse to let them be corrected'). Both verbs sit inside the same 'not so X that...' negation, so the sense is that Hagecius DOES acknowledge his errors and IS willing to have them corrected. The English drops the negation on the second verb and reads as a flat, un-negated assertion ('am unwilling to admit it'), inverting Hagecius's stated position from openness-to-correction to refusal — exactly backward, and it undercuts the humility topos that sets up his forbearance toward Tycho's disagreements in the very next sentence. At: “and — when they can be wiped away and a more comely form put on — am unwilling to admit it.”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). \"[Scultetus] me salvere iussit\" is the standard Latin epistolary greeting idiom (literally 'he bade me be well/greeted' = 'he sent his greeting to me') — Scultetus is the one extending the greeting to Hagecius, via the son as go-between. The English reverses the direction, making it read as Scultetus ordering Hagecius to send greetings to him — the opposite of who initiated the conciliatory gesture. This also breaks the logic of the immediately following sentence ('Ego vicissim dabo operam...' = 'I IN TURN will take pains...'), which presupposes Scultetus acted first. At: “For lately, through my son, he bade me greet [him in turn].”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both MISTRANSLATION defects against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.146, lines 5 and 14-15 respectively), read visually in the slice PDF. Both Latin phrases match the stored current_latin exactly, so only the English clauses were corrected — one restoring a dropped negation in a 'non...ita...ut' construction, the other restoring the correct direction of a standard epistolary greeting idiom. Nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1588 (7 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/45-48", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13966897"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.47."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 14, "prev": "brahe-t_15880817001", "next": "brahe-t_15890322001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880817001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890322001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports two earlier letters apparently lost, confirms receipt of the book in late July with expressions of delight, and regrets that advancing age limits fuller engagement.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15881101001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15881104001", "d": "1588-11-04", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Brucaeus — Uraniborg, 4 November 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD HENRICUM BRUCAeUM.</p><p>RUPISTI tandem altum illud silentium, quo per totam aestatem aliquid ad me literarum dare supersedisti, amantissime BRUCAeE, quod autem nunc demum quasi e profundo somno expergefactus me tuis eruditis literis reviseris, est quod moram hanc hactenus commissam condonem, teque excusatum habeam. Agis autem in his mecum veluti ex professo de ijs, quae continentur in libro meo secundo Aethereorum Phaenomenωn, tibi praeterito vere per quendam e meis domesticis hinc in Germaniam proficiscentem transmisso, quaeque in priore eius parte inveniuntur, te sedulo perlegisse ostendis, posteriore leviter attacta. Visum itaque est tibi ad ea, quae de his ad me perscripsisti, in hunc modum respondere. Quod in priore eius voluminis portione contenta ex observationum et demonstrationum certitudine accomodationeque approbas et commendas, inprimis vero neotericae meae Hypotheseos circa dispositionem revolutionum coelestium inventionem tibi admodum placere asseveras, mihi pergratum est. Equidem quantum ad Hypotheseos eius constitutionem attinet, suspicabar eam tuo Genio non arrisuram, siquidem alias tam vehemens et constans sis autoritatis antiquorum conservator et propugnator, adeo ut nonnunquam nimium his tribuere et certioribus rationibus ipsique experientiae non parum derogare videaris; sed aliter tamen hîc accidit quam putabam: Hanc enim nostram mundani systematis Hypotyposin a veteribus nunquam perspectam, nec etiam a recentioribus ullis prius excogitatam non solum tuo suffragio comprobas, sed etiam ceteris, quod eiuscemodi, quae illae a PTOLEMAeO et COPERNICO propositae, non admittat absurda, anteferendam censes. Ut autem eo rectius intelliges, qua ratione in huius Hypotheseos inventionem ante aliquot annos pervenerim, mitto tibi exemplum literarum, nuper ad clarissimum et doctissimum virum D. CASPAREM PEUCERUM a me datarum, quibus eius quibusdam dubijs (quae ad nobilissimum virum Dominum HENRICUM RANZOVIUM, qui illi eius Hypotheseos nudam delineationem e libro meo excerptam communicârat, perscripsit, cuius etiam verborum unâ cum alterius cuiusdam Mathematici de eadem re ambigua sententia copiam facio) respondendum duxi, ut hac occasione plenius de hoc toto Astronomico negotio cum tanto Philosopho et Mathematico conferrem. Ex his plurima non saltem [ad] Hypotheseos huius rationes et uberiorem declarationem, sed etiam ad totam rem Astronomicam peculiariter facientia percipies tuo Genio, uti opinor, non displicitura. Ubi vero has ipsas literas perlegeris, vel etiam, si lubet, transcribi curâris, velim eas mihi remitti, siquidem exemplar hoc reservare cupiam, neque otium nunc suppetat, ut id denuo exarari faciam. Quae circa Astronomiae instaurationem desideras, ut omnia huic Hypothesi fundata eque certis observationibus Geometricis in numeros redacta Phaenomenis exacte correspondeant, etiam in his literis ad Dominum PEUCERUM datis, quo ordine et quanto conamine rem tantam aggredi molirique, favente Coeli ipsius et Universi Opifice, proposuerim, invenies. Nec est, ut pertimescas, quod laboris immensitas, rei tam arduae difficultas, temporis requisiti diuturnitas aut sumtuum magnitudo ullave alia molestia, eodem supremo Numine conatibus nostris aspirante, me absterrebunt, quo minus gratae, uti spero, posteritati tam divinae et sublimis artis restitutionem ea praecisione (absit invidia dicto) qua hactenus a nemine exantlata est, consecrem, omnibusque suis numeris, quantum quidem ab uno homine praestari potest, absolutam relinquam.</p><p>Sed de his hoc loco satis. Est tamen, quod tibi hac in re conquerar. Quidam nimis perfrictae frontis, quem indignum censeo, ut hîc nominetur, qui etiam, ubicunque fuit aut est, seipsum, qualis sit, prodit, hanc meam Hypothesium inventionem sibi arrogare, idque publico scripto, Argentinae hoc anno edito, asserere non erubuit. An et hanc cum reliquis, quae apud me clam descripsit, dum nobilissimo viro ERICO LANGE inserviens, in eius comitatu per aliquot dies fuit, subtraxit, aut qua alias ratione eius dispositionem hîc hauserit, noverit ipse; et Studiosi mei similem quandam inter segestria tunc iacentem, quae minus apposite delineata erat, monstrârunt. Certe quam fraudulenter et subdole hic egerit, reliqua, quae hîc, me inscio, ex meis descripsit, quorum aliquam partem recepi, ipsius manu exarata, satis convincunt, utut Hypothesin illam inter eas schedas non reperio; sed poterat hanc alibi abscondisse aut in memoria retinuisse, cum forte non suppeteret delineandi commoditas. Norunt domestici mei Studiosi illam Hypothesin iam aliquot annis a me fuisse constitutam atque illis ostensam: quin et liber hic de Cometa Anni 77, etsi prae se ferat editionem hoc Anno 88 factam, tamen norunt omnes, qui mecum sunt, eundem Anno 87 in mea Typographia excusam esse, data opera autem uno anno tardiorem notatam, quo esset ob novitatem acceptior. In hoc autem libro eandem mecum Hypothesin generaliter explicari, te non ignorare, ex iam dictis liquet. Sed egregie prodidit iste, qui sibi eam nuper vendicaret, se illam non probe intelligere, nedum ut eius autor sit, dum Martium orbem Solarem totaliter ambientem pingit et fingit, quod impossibile esse vel ex solo Copernico saltem obiter introspecto discere potuisset. Et quî, quaeso, is novas unquam condet Hypotheses, coelestibus revolutionibus analogas, qui multorum annorum exquisitis observationibus non est instructus, ut inde, quid in hactenus usitatis Hypothesibus desideretur, et quomodo idipsum restitui possit, solerter pervestiget? Sine his adminiculis novam aliquam inventionem iactare frivolum est planeque ridiculum, cum non habeat, unde probet per ipsas apparentias, cur prioribus relictis sic innovare [oporteat] et quomodo per multiplices observationes hanc apparentijs coelestibus rectius congruere confirmet. Sed facessant istae ineptiae, quibus nemo, nisi rem non intelligens, fidem adhibuerit.</p><p>Ad epistolam tuam redeo, ea, quae postmodum continet, ordine, ut constitui, disquisiturus. Emendationem locorum affixarum stellarum cap. 2 a me propositam ex declinatione stellae et transitu per meridianum aliquantulum in dubium vocare videris, eo quod exquisita temporis notitia Solisque locus praecise vix constare possint; id licet aliquo modo non abs re dicatur, et a me etiam circa finem eiusdem capitis in Additione fol. 31 incipiente idem fere asseveretur, nihilominus tamen adhibita competenti diligentia, et medijs quibusdam temporis minimas particulas aequali ductu, quoad fieri potest, exhibentibus, qualia nobis non pauca in promtu sunt, etiam haec ratio absque admodum sensibili errore locum meretur, praesertim si crebra reiteratione examinetur, donec obtenta non admodum sensibiliter differant, ut patet ex observationibus prioribus eo modo institutis, quae pag. 30 oculis subijciuntur, si conferantur cum alijs reiteratis et diverso modo per Solem intermediante Veneris stella ad fixa sidera diductis, quae pag. 33 exhibentur. Differentia enim est perexigua, utpote quae decimam unius gradus partem, ubi maxima est, non excedat, et in sex vel septem stellis tria minuta vix attingat; quae sane praecisio a veteribus non est extricata, ut mirari satis non possim, quod in ea, quibus tunc usus sum, instrumentorum parvitate et penuria vel per hanc ipsam tam lubricam ad deflectendum viam adeo prope tamen ad scopum collimârim. Verum exactiorem et nulli ambiguitati obnoxiam stellarum fixarum loca verificandi rationem in eadem Additione breviter indicavi, per distantias videlicet a Sole ad Venerem interdiu apparentem factas et noctu ab hac ad stellas relatas. Qua de re in antecedenti libro plenius ago, et per aliquot eo modo accurate factas animadversiones, easque tam Eoas quam vespertinas, stellarum inerrantium loca in veram et praecisam ab Aequinoctio vernali distantiam repono; idque ex ipsis observationibus ita et non aliter se habere posse demonstro, annotatis etiam earum veris latitudinibus, in quibus non minus quam longitudinibus nimium hactenus aberratum est. Solaris etiam cursus ad nostra tempora exactissima restitutio ibidem praemittitur, siquidem ex eo cetera omnia dependeant. Ubi Apogei locus eccentricitatisque vera quantitas e certis aliquot praecedentium annorum observationibus pluribus et non fallacibus instrumentis coelitus diligenter habitis Geometrice demonstrantur, adiecta aequalium motuum etiam correctorum et prostaphaereseωn e reperta eccentricitate extracta Tabula, ut de Solis motu apparentijs praecise congruente nullum amplius restet apud me dubium. Subiungam forsan de Lunari curriculo etiam subtiliter restituto nonnulla, quando per plurium annorum observationes eius perplexam semitam tam quoad longum quam latum plenius extricabo. Video te adhuc ruminare ea, quae doctissimus GEMMA FRISIUS in libello de Radio Astronomico quoad fixarum in Globum aliquem denotationem et postea Aequatoris Eclipticaeque in eodem designationem molitus est. Verum ego convenientius certis de causis fieri iudico, ut Aequator et Ecliptica prius in Globo ordinentur et in minimas particulas distribuantur, postea secundum harum exigentiam impositis affixarum locis et varia ratione hinc inde comprobatis, ita ut in unum et idem punctum recidant. Per distantias tamen beneficio Radij Astronomici factas, prout GEMMA tradidit et postea a multis tentatum est, negotium hoc nunquam dextre absolvetur. Impossibile enim est, per Radium, quantacunque longitudine praeditus sit, et qualicunque subtilitate divisus, etiam saepissime reiterando observationem exactas in ipso scrupulo stellarum rimari intercapedines, ut alibi per suas causas ostendemus. Ideoque ego primus Sextantes huic officio apprime idoneos et ubique veras stellarum remotiones in eodem minuto aut etiam huius aliquota parte, quotiescunque repetatur observatio, praebentes excogitavi, quibus etiam aliquot iam annis praecisa subtilitate distantias siderum inquirendo feliciter usus sum. Quin et frustra in Globum reponuntur stellae, quocunque tandem instrumento coelitus deriventur, nisi is admodum magnus sit, qualem ego sex pedum in diametro possideo, qui undiquaque orichalcicus est et divisionem graduum in singula sexagena minuta per ductum transversalem, more nobis usitato, discriminatim exhibet, absolutaque insuper rotunditate praeditus est. In hoc omnium affixarum loca instrumentis varijs ex ipso coelo deducta contignare decrevi, quorum etiam magna et praecipua pars iam antea a me in promtu habetur. Quare non est, quod dubites, me sponte mea ea, quae ad fixarum stellarum praecisam restitutionem faciunt, omni possibili diligentia moliri, ut nulla admonitione, vel eorum quae cap. 2 a me proponuntur, recapitulatione, opus fuisset.</p><p>Praeterea latitudinem Lunae PTOLEMAeI placitis quarta parte gradus unius maiorem a me adinventam, suspectam habes, eamque per eclipsium Lunarium magnitudinem et calculum illi correspondentem impugnas. Autoritatem insuper REGIOMONTANI, COPERNICI, WERNERI et aliorum insignium Mathematicorum convocas, quod illi Ptolemaicam Lunae latitudinem maximam praecise 5 partium perpetuo retinuerint, quos non dubitas magna diligentia eam coelitus ita se habere deprehendisse. Ptolemaicam insuper latitudinem omni carere vitio existimas ob loci suae observationis opportunitatem, quae Lunam iuxta Tropicum aestivum in maxima latitudine borea exigentem vertici quam proxime adunivit, semotis ob id parallaxium et refractionum impedimentis etc. De his ut tecum plenius agam, veritas ipsa postulat. Scias itaque, Lunae latitudinem maximam non temere aut sine crebra exactaque observatione a me Ptolemaica 15 scrupulis maiorem constitutam. Eam enim ita revera se habere, praesertim iuxta quadraturas, multis experimentis minimeque fallacibus observationibus, ex ipso coelo edoctus sum, nisi quod aliquando uno vel altero scrupulo maiorem, interdum paululum minorem adinvenerim; et si quae alia subest, uti non negavero, inaequalitas, eam penitius enodabo. Nec eclipsium animadversiones meae assertioni quippiam, quod in sensus cadere possit, derogant. Nam licet maxima latitudo 15 scrupulis ab ea, quae usitata et diu recepta est, maior statuatur, tamen haec differentia circa limites illos, ubi eclipses prope nodos fieri solent, nihil, quod animadverti possit, importat. Siquidem in distantia a nodis 12 graduum, intra quem proxime terminum eclipses Lunares fieri necessum est, discrimen latitudinis Ptolemaicae et nostrae evadit saltem trium scrupulorum primorum, quae unicum tantummodo digitum in Lunae obscuratione causantur, qui etiam vix est perceptibilis ob umbrae Terrenae extremitatem praecise in sensus non incurrentem, et ipsa eclipsium tempora non adeo certa hactenus innotuerunt, ut haec res nostrae inventioni quippiam officere queat, quod nos plurima et quam diligentissime aliquot praeteritis annis observata docuerunt deliquia Lunaria iuxta sextum gradum distantia a nodis; differentia latitudinum utroque modo assumtarum provenit 1½ minuti, quod quoad quantitatem obscurationis Lunae est insensibile. Circa hunc vero locum crebriores conspiciuntur eclipses, qui et medius est inter nodos et limites extremos. Prope vero ipsos nodos, quando Luna eclipsatur, Ptolemaica et nostra latitudo maxima nihil ingerunt discriminis, ut tute haec omnia facile colligere potes, re melius quam antea considerata, velut etiam ex ascripta delineatione in numeros resoluta perspicuum evadit. Accedit et hoc, quod incertus sim, an latitudo Lunae eodem modo se habeat iuxta novilunia et plenilunia quemadmodum prope quadraturas, ubi hanc divagationem potissimum animadverti; qua de re alias, volente Numine, quae plenior dederit experientia, patefaciam. Frustra itaque eclipses Lunares nobis obtrudis. Videris autem per incuriam aliquam existimasse, etiam circa terminos eclipsium quartam gradus partem in latitudine Lunae a Ptolemaica abundare, non perpendens, id saltem iuxta maximam ab Ecliptica divagationem, quae quadrante circuli a nodis distat, locum habere, et postea versus intersectionem attenuari paulatim, adeo ut iuxta ipsos nodos in unum concursus fiat, et in paucorum graduum distantia, iuxta quos eclipses Lunae contingere possunt, perexiguam vixque perceptibilem subesse differentiam. Autoritas REGIOMONTANI, COPERNICI, WERNERI et aliorum, si qui sunt, hac in parte me non movet, siquidem Astronomiae redintegratio non autoritatibus hominum, sed e certis observationibus et his innixis demonstrationibus derivanda veniat. Neque REGIOMONTANUS unquam latitudinem Lunae maximam coelitus observavit, aut ulla correctione indigere suspicatus est. Nec illa, quae alias in Sole et reliquis sideribus ab ipso et discipulo eius BERNHARDO GUALTERO observata literis produntur, recte se habent, adeo ut sibijpsis non consentiant. COPERNICUS latitudinem Ptolemaicam in Luna maximam invariatam retinuit, neque ipsemet quicquam in hac, an ita se habeat nec ne, coelitus attentare aggressus est, excusans hoc, quod non eam occasionem experiendi sibi fortuna contulerit quam CL. PTOLEMAeO, ob commutationum Lunarium impedimenta in altiori poli situ, quae in Alexandria commode praecaveri poterant, ut patet ex ipsiusmet COPERNICI verbis cap. 15 libri 4 Revolutionum. Nec WERNERUS quippiam circa hanc rem attentavit, quod ex ullo eius scripto probari possit. Sed et is Ptolemaicae animadversioni acquievit, nec ab ullis alijs, quod sciam, quippiam certi hac in parte elaboratum est. Frustra igitur allegatur aliorum authoritas in re nunquam ab illis explorata, licet haec etiam per se in Mathematicis locum non mereatur. PTOLEMAeUM longe commodiorem habuisse opportunitatem latitudinem Lunae maximam perscrutandi, non equidem eo inficias, cum prope verticem et parallaxis (quam COPERNICUS commutationem vocat) et etiam refractionis vitio non laborârit, ideoque ipsemet in eisdem Annotationibus, ubi de Lunae latitudine maxima ago pag. 40 et seq. Ptolemaicam observationem nullatenus improbo, adeo ut eam tunc temporis se recte habuisse iudicem, postea vero usque ad nostram aetatem quarta gradus parte mutatam, quemadmodum Solaris etiam orbitae ab Aequatore digressio interea non invariata permansit. Nolo etiam Parallaticum ipsius instrumentum, quo in hac observatione usus est, in dubium vocare, licet id forte non citra rationem facere possem, ut duobus similibus instrumentis maiori cura et diligentia, quam forsan a PTOLEMAeO praestitum est, fabrefactis, crebra nos docuit experientia. Taceo, quod divisionem partium subtilissimam non adhibuerit, et, quod maxime refert, Lunares radios per foramina transmiserit, ubi in pauculis scrupulis proclivis est aberratio. Accedit et hoc, quod in eo Regulae longioris loco prope perpendicularis contactum, ubi observatio verticem proxime respicit, minima et paene insensibilis anguli per Regulam minorem mobilem mutatio plures divisiones in oblongiori pertranseat, quam alias in remotiore a vertice instrumenti dispositione, quemadmodum ijs, qui haec tractare ipsa experientia edocti sunt, in propatulo est. Neque tamen instrumenti vel observationis vitio ipsum per quartam gradus partem aberrasse propterea dixerim, sed potius revera ad nostra usque tempora adauctam fuisse Lunarem ab Ecliptica digressionem, quae an in omni ad Solem dispositione aeque magna esse possit, necdum tuto affirmârim. Reclamitant etenim nonnullae circa plenilunia observationes, quae Ptolemaicae divagationi quam proxime accedunt, ut ob id inaequalitatem aliquam hîc latere suspicer, de qua tamen nihil certi pronunciare volo, antequam plenius, uti dixi, rem omnem expendero. Licet vero in nostro Horizonte parallaxis Lunae et refractio non parum difficultatis ingerant, quo minus negotium hoc tam expedite, atque in Alexandria a PTOLEMAeO, absolvi queat, tamen haec impedimenta satis praecavere iamdudum crebra observationum praxi didici, ita ut fidem tuto interponere ausim, unum vel alterum ad summum minutum in mea Lunaris latitudinis limitatione nullatenus desiderari.</p><p>Vin’ autem aliorum suffragium? siquidem meis solius observationibus non penitus standum arbitreris (quod facile ab ijs admittitur, qui ipsimet sidera non observant, nec quanta diligentia et assiduitate nos huic labori incumbamus norunt) en tibi dabo praestantis Mathematici CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI, Illustriss. Principi GULIELMO Hassiae Landgravio in exercitijs Astronomicis operam navantis, testimonium non obscurum, satisque firmum, quod in literis ante paucas hebdomadas ad me perscripsit. Asserit enim is, se eandem fere latitudinem Lunae maximam instrumentis Principis, quam et ego meis, deprehendisse. Et insuper addit, caput Draconis abesse a Tabulis plus quatuor gradibus. Id vero qua ratione adinvenerit et quomodo comprobare possit, non video. Nam hîc satis evidenter reclamitant eclipses Lunares, cum 4 gradus iuxta nodos ipsos latitudinem Lunae per 21 minuta, id est 2/3 corporis Lunaris partes varient, quod sane in eclipsibus Lunae admodum esset perceptibile, a me tamen, ita se habere, hactenus non compertum. Verum ut eo rectius mentem ROTHMANNI capias, curavi describi particulam quandam literarum ipsius nuper ad me datarum, in qua haec, quae refero de latitudine Lunae maxima et nodis, inter quaedam alia continentur. Misissem tibi integrum literarum exemplar, sed Amanuensis meus illud adeo festinanter, alijs occupatus, transcribere nequivit. Sunt enim prolixiores et aliquot quaterniones adimplent. Atque haec de latitudine Lunae maxima a me constituta sit replicasse satis, quibus si tibi adhuc non satisfactum est, tute instrumenta idonea nullique errori obnoxia construi cura, et coelum ipsum inspicere examinareque disce, priusquam aliorum inventa crebro vigilatis noctibus ex ipsa coelesti observatione et non in vaporario ex chartis depromta sub dubitationem vocare praesumseris.</p><p>Postea recapitulatione quadam uteris eorum, quae sequentibus capitibus prioris partis comprehendo; quod autem ibidem oblique insinuas, me asseverare, Cometam circulum descripsisse, quamvis interdum quinque aut paucioribus scrupulis ab exacta peripheria observationes dissentiant, id vero quid est aliud, quam nodum in scirpo quaerere? Nam ista perexigua differentia, quae quina scrupula non excedit, imo rarissime ea attingit et utplurimum in uno vel altero scrupulo sibi constat, non est digna, ut in mentionem veniat, cum sit pene imperceptibilis; et aëris diversitas applicatioque Cometae ad Horizontem tempore quarundam observationum propior, quarundam vero remotior, hanc tantillulam diversitatem facilime ingerere potuit, ut nihil dicam paucula scrupula in locis fixarum, quibus usus sum, aliter se habere, quam a nobis iuxta veterem animadversionem assumta sunt, velut patet ex Additamento, fini secundi capitis apposito, de quo etiam prius dixi. Hanc etiam non magni momenti discrepantiam in ductu circuli Cometae in ipso libro suo loco satis superque excusavi, ut non opus fuerit tam exilia et ad rei scopum non pertinentia neque quicquam nostrae asseverationi apud candidos et peritos derogantia inculcare. Si quanto labore et industria ad eam ipsam, quam nactus sum, praecisionem opus fuerit, ipsa experientia satis perspectum haberes, utique de minimis et sensum pene omnem effugientibus non otiose disputares.</p><p>Dehinc ambiguitatem quandam moves in eo, quod aliquoties asseverem, omnes Cometas in aetherea regione supra Lunam versari, et LYCOSTHENEM mihi obijcis, eumque refers in quodam loco sui libri de Ostentis prodidisse, quosdam Cometas conflagrantes incendium in subiecta Terra excitasse, atque inde aliquos sublunares fuisse, et seeundum mentem Peripateticorum ignes in supremo aëre accensos extitisse, tacite inferre conaris. At ego, perlustrato toto illo LYCOSTHENIS libello, nihil eiuscemodi in eo invenire potui. Imo etiamsi tale quid illic reperiretur, attendendum nihilominus esset, quod isti autores Historici non Mathematice rem tractent, sed generaliter de Ostentis scribentes saepenumero discrimen inter Cometas revera in coelo constitutos et faces atque lampadas ignesque volantes similiaque aëris infralunaris ignita meteora minime faciant. Fieri itaque quam facilime potuit, ut ex horum ignitorum et sublunarium meteororum alicubi in Terram delapsis incendium quoddam in competenti materia olim excitatum sit, quod tamen Cometae alicuius conflagrationi vel flammarum excitatam inconvenienter attribuitur. Hi enim non ardent, neque ignem edunt, verum non multum dissimili ratione a reliquis stellis lumen sibi insitum vibrant, sive id a Sole, sive aliunde obtineant. Quod autem materiam omnium Cometarum aetheream ex eadem, in qua versantur, mundi regione constantem asseverem, non caret suis certis rationibus, velut in Epilogo totius operis patebit, ubi ex professo de materia et procreatione Cometarum tum aliorum opiniones sub disquisitionem iustam revocabo, tum etiam meam sententiam aperte exponam, siquidem in hoc libro et reliquis etiam duobus leviter hac de re ago, eo quod ista Physica consideratio non competenter proponitur, priusquam omnia, quae iuxta Astronomicam demonstrationem tum in Nova Stella, tum etiam aliquibus Cometis postea visis, a me pertractata fuerint. Illic etiam certis rationibus ostendam, quam absurda sit haec tua et aliorum quorundam opinio, Cometas, etsi in coelo ipso curriculum suum absolvant, nihilominus e materia elementari sursum in aethera vi siderum sublata constare posse. Quot enim et quanta huic opinioni tum ex ipsis Cometarum apparentijs, tum e veriore Physica repugnent, ibidem manifestum reddam. Nec quod intereant Cometae, huic tuae sententiae suffragium praebet. Nam quae post mundi primam creationem miraculose aliquando in natura, praesertim coelesti, eveniunt, ea etiam ante universalem rerum creatarum abolitionem interire consentaneum evadit. Cur autem Stella Nova et Cometae semel exorti non perpetuo durent, nec eandem semper retineant magnitudinem aut colorem, in eodem totius operis Epilogo rationes proferam. Id autem quod veteres Epicurei et quidam alij Philosophi senserunt, sidera humoribus nutriri, per se plane falsum est et ridiculum, nedum ut hinc aliquid certi in Cometis probare liceat. Videris mihi brevi in Paracelsistam teipso inscio degeneraturus. Nam et is eundem veterum errorem tuebatur, quamvis non omnimodo e vaporibus, sed materia quadam coelesti nutriri astra opinabatur, adeo ut meteora omnia nihil aliud quam stellarum esse excrementa asseverare non sit veritus. Conveniens siquidem videbatur, ea quae pabulo et nutrimento indigent, suas etiam habere excretiones; sed haec speculatio ab omni veritate aliena est. Quod alij atque alij Cometae subinde oriantur et aboleantur, quodque quidam eorum in uno loco consistant, quidam vero mobiles sint, quodque inter eos, qui moventur, alij in consequentia, alij in antecedentia signorum cursum dirigant, quodque aliqui eorundem Septentrionem petant, quidam vero Meridiem, haec omnia nihil probant, eos ex elementari materia constare. Nam possunt haec ipsa et plura competere ijs in ipso coelo e coelesti materia confirmatis, ut latius in eodem Epilogo discutiemus. Quod vero addis, quosdam ordinem quendam in motu servare, quosdam vero sine ordine vagari, id nimium est. Nam omnes Cometas ordinem certum servasse, nec [a] motu tardiore in celeriorem vel ab hoc in illum subito prolapsos, aut vago, inconstanti vel tumultuario (ut vocat SENECA) ductu discurrisse iudico. In omnibus enim Cometis, qui a me notati sunt, constantem et proportionalem motus tenorem deprehendi, quemadmodum etiam in caeteris, qui ab antecessoribus nostris Mathematicis coelitus diligenter observati sunt. Nam quod Historici quidam vagum et inconstantem cursum quibusdam forte attribuerunt, id nihil moramur. Idem enim illis et similibus rem Astronomicam non intelligentibus in caeteris etiam Planetis, imo et in ipsis fixis sideribus videri posset. Neque ex incertis testimonijs veritas rata colligitur. Immo et ipsi Mathematici non vulgares Stellae Novae motum quendam attribuerunt, licet perpetuo in eodem loco constiterit, et Cometas postmodum visos vago et inordinato ductu curriculum absolvisse, asseverare non dubitârunt, veluti et CORNELIUS GEMMA et THADDAeUS, ambo praestantes Mathematici, in hoc eodem Cometa (ut de caeteris nunc non dicam) inconsiderate admiserunt, ut in ipso libro a nobis suo loco indicatur. Vides itaque, et alias videbis plenius ex omnibus istis ratiunculis, quas adducis, non sequi, quod intendis, Cometas e materia elementari generari, sive supra sive infra Lunam.</p><p>Subiungis postea adhuc etiam inconvenientem haesitationem, vel potius Hypotheseos nostrae in Cometa anni 77, de quo liber iste transmissus agit, minus deliberatam improbationem. Existimas enim ex eo, quod Cometam circa Solem moveri statuam, ita ut sextante circuli ad summum ab illo digredi potuerit, consectarium esse, ut si quando in proxima terris sui circuli parte fuerit, quod tunc Terram contingeret, siquidem semidiameter sextantem circuli subtendat, ut patet ex elementis Geometriae. Verum hîc mihi videris nimis oscitanter rem omnem expendisse, Nam licet Cometa evagatus sit a Sole centro sui orbis 60 partibus circuli maximi in coelo, quoad nostrum e terris aspectum, non tamen orbis ille imaginarius tantus fuit, ut Terram attingere ullo modo potuerit, imo infima eius pars ferme triplo Lunaribus sedibus hinc remotior extitit. Id quod ex adiecta figuratione facilime patet, in qua orbis ille, quem Cometa circa Solem descripsit, repraesentatur per DBEG, cuius centrum C iuxta Solem, A vero sit Terra, in qua versamur, et unde coelestia aspicimus. Maxima digressio Cometae a Sole est in B, ad quem locum ducantur CB et AB, concurrentes iuxta circuli contactum. Est itaque in triangulo ABC angulus ad B rectus (ut nosti). Angulus vero CAB 60 partium iuxta observationem. Latus vero CA praesupponatur iuxta COPERNICUM secundum mediam Solis a Terra distantiam semidiametrorum terrae 1142. Ergo in triangulo rectilineo rectanguloque dato altero, unoque latere, non ignorabitur latus BC semidiametrorum Terrae 988, quod aequale est ipsi CE, cum ambo sint e centro eiusdem circuli. Ideoque sublato CE semidiam. Terrae 988 ex CA earundem 1142, ut modo dixi, remanebit EA semid. 154. Est autem EA proxima distantia, qua Cometa terris appropinquare potuit, etiamsi totam circuli sui peripheriam aliquoties absolvisset. Cumque Lunae proxima a terris remotio a COPERNICO non inconvenienter statuatur semidiametrorum Terrae proxime 52, apparet Cometam Terrae maxime appropinquantem fere triplo plus ab ea remotum fuisse, quam est Lunae proxima ad eandem accessio; idque iuxta Coperniceas rationes, quae Ptolemaicis (quibus adhuc Luna nobis propior evadit) veriores sunt. Quod si in milliaria Germanica lubeat hanc Cometae appropinquationem resolvere, assumendo pro qualibet Terrae semidiametro milliaria nostratia vel Germanica 860 (quod nec a vero admodum dissentaneum est) prosilient milliaria 132440, quibus Cometa in proximo situ nobis admoveri potuit. An hoc est Terram contingere? Licet autem omnia non satis scrupulose, ut neque opus erat, hic rimatus sim, siquidem Apogei et eccentricitatis nullam habuerim rationem, quia parum hoc loco importat, tamen maior ad Terram propinquitas dari vix potest, quemadmodum pag. 297 in ipso libro indicatur, quamvis ibidem incuria Typographi pro 154 reperiantur 145, transpositis duobus posterioribus characteribus, qui tamen error inter vitia Typographica in calce libri detegitur.</p><p>Apparet itaque, quam inconsulte Hypothesi nostrae detraxeris, asserendo Cometae circulum terras contigisse, qui sane error nimium crassus fuisset, cum nec sublunarem eius ductum admittere voluerim, et contraria toto libro demonstrârim. Ipsae etiam distantiae a Terra in penultima columna Tabulae eius, quae ex Hypothesi numeros vario respectu deducit, te admonere potuissent, Cometam per praesuppositam Hypothesin adeo humilem, ut suspicaris, nunquam factum, siquidem die 9 Novemb. remotio eius in semidiametris Terrae ponatur earundem 173, et sane tunc temporis non adeo longe abfuit a proxima ad nos accessione, ita ut vix 20 semidiametris propior reddi potuerit. Ipse itaque tecum perpende, quam immerito tantam absurditatem nobis obieceris. Sed uteris quadam velitatione et hypothetica cautione, inquiens: Si recte tuam sententiam assequor. At certe mea sententia non adeo obscura est, neque ita segniter et intricate declarata, quin a te tanto Mathematico percipi possit, praesertim cum librum meum fere per anni semissem tecum habueris, et si quid ob alias occupationes et curas residui in animo tuo fuisset dubij, praestitisset iudicium suspendisse potius quam tam enormiter et citra omnem rationem nostra improbasse. Sed facile video, unde hic non levis error tibi obrepserit. Digressionem enim Cometae a Sole in eo ambitu, qui centrum ipsius circuli transit et in tanta a nobis est distantia, atque Sol ipse, fieri inconvenienter opinatus es, idque iuxta F, cum contactus, in quo maxima nobis apparens digressio contingit, longe inferius cadat prope B. Pari quoque modo HIERONYMUS CAROANUS hallucinatus est, dum in principio libri secundi de varietate rerum demonstrare conatur, epicyclum Veneris e veterum Hypothesi tam magnum esse, ut Terram attingat, si ♀ iuxta Perigeum distare a Sole ratione epicycli intelligatur partibus 45. Nam et illic eius loci in epicyclo rationem non habet, ubi e linea e terris egrediente fit contactus, sed per ipsam diametrum epicycli demonstrationem inconsiderate pertexit, non perpendens eam quae a veteribus iuxta hanc Hypothesin explorata est epicycli ad eccentricum proportionem, qualis videlicet est 43 ad 60 proxime. Sed magni etiam viri magnis erroribus interdum sunt obnoxij.</p><p>Secundam mei libri partem te nondum evolvisse asseveras, et perpauca in eo legisse, adeo ut saltem animadversiones in MAeSTLINUM obiter percurreris. Imo quantum e literis tuis percipere possum, inutilem vel minus necessariam fuisse eam operam censes, qua in secunda isti libri portione aliorum de hoc Cometa placita sub incudem revocavi, siquidem ea, quae Geometrice a me priore in parte demonstrata sunt, a nemine convelli possint. At longe aliter sentiendum duco. Nam aliorum observationes et demonstrationes, si recte se habeant, magnum veritati suffragium praebent, praesertim in diversis locis ab alijs atque alijs personis administratae. Nam licet ea, quae Geometrice demonstrantur, recte constent, tamen cum observationes, quae per media mechanica fiunt, ubique non rite perficiantur, evenit quod ipsae conclusiones, utut praetextum Geometricarum demonstrationum faciant, saepenumero frustraneae sint. Sic nostram Lunae latitudinem maximam in dubium vocare (ut etiam mutationem latitudinis affixarum stellarum iuxta variatam Eclipticae ab Aequatore digressionem in quibusdam alijs prius ad me datis literis) non vereris. At cum ex observationibus ROTHMANNI in remoto loco et alijs organis exploratis rem eodem modo se habere intellexeris, forte meis inventis facilius acquiesces, licet ea per se adeo rata sint, ut nullo extraneo indigeant testimonio. Atque tum ob hanc, tum etiam alias necessarias causas in primo membro secundae partis eorum scripta expendi, e quibus Cometam hunc aethereum fuisse competenter mecum probatur. Ne quis suspicetur omnium aliorum animadversiones a meis inventis prorsus alienas fuisse, quod tamen per se non admodum curârim, salva tantum ipsa veritate, quae opinionibus hominum quidem impugnatur, sed non expugnatur, quae etiam in ipsis rerum penetralibus et medullis a plurimorum captu abscondita latet. In posteriori vero membro reliquorum, qui contrarium senserunt, ratiocinationes everti, ut remotis et refutatis eorum contradictionibus veritas libera per se effulgeret. Nec levibus paralogismis quatuor magnae autoritatis viri e diversis locis in hoc consenserunt, ut Cometam tam propinquam terris probarent, quo parallaxin 5 vel 6 partium efficeret, ideoque longe infra Lunam vix 8 vel 9 hinc remotum Terrae semidiametris, uti etiam e certis observationibus Geometricis demonstrationes praetenderunt, quibus vel erudito alicui Mathematico, si non circumspecte omnia perpenderit, rem ita se habere persuaderet, adeo ut Philosophus vester Aristotelicus ERASTUS non dubitârit Mathematicorum suffragia in hoc Cometa citare, asserens ipsum iuxta Peripateticorum dogmata fuisse in elementari mundo e fumositatibus incensis concretum, eo quod quidam ex illis parallaxin eius ista, quae in Luna sit, maiorem demonstrare conati sint. Sic veritas saepe vim patitur, nisi ea, quae falsa sunt, solerter semoveantur. Praeterea multa circa hoc totum negotium in secunda parte continentur, de quibus in priore non dabatur disserendi occasio. Perlege igitur per otium et hanc posteriorem libri distinctionem diligenter, et tum demum tuum de ea iudicium prome. Dicis te rudes eiusdem Cometae fecisse observationes, quibus iuxta REGIOMONTANI placita eum minime sublunarem fuisse deprehenderis, idque WITTICHIO ad me proficiscenti prius indicasse. At ille cum hîc adesset, nihil tale sentiebat, sed erat prorsus Peripateticorum opinione imbutus, Cometas omnes in aëre infra Lunam vagari, illicque e fumositatibus e Terra attractis exarescere. Ideoque MAeSTLINI et aliorum contraria placita improbabat, quam tamen sententiam, antequam hinc in Germaniam rediret, mutavit, eo quod e meis observationibus vel in illo Cometa, qui anno 80 apparuit, quo tempore mecum fuit, aliter se rem habere institueretur. Memini tamen teipsum aliquando mihi, cum Rostochium peregrinando transirem, dixisse, nullam in eo Cometa te invenire potuisse parallaxin, imitando mentem REGIOMONTANI, et per azimutha atque altitudines cum interlapso tempore rem omnem explorando, unâque tunc temporis instrumentum ostendisti, quo observationem hanc aggressus es, quod tamen tale mihi videbatur, ut si per aliud parallaxin 10 vel 15 graduum reperisses, non ob id sequeretur, Cometam hunc elementarem fuisse, nam maior in azimuthis et altitudinibus simul capiendis requiritur diligentia, quam hactenus a multis est perspecta, praesertim in tam subtili negotio, ubi e minimis maxima eruuntur, et temporis exquisita in quinis vel denis secundis cognitio, qualis necessaria erat, tibi satis perspecta esse nequaquam potuit. Non enim per altitudines stellarum ea satis praecise indagatur, ut alias monui. Atque ob id eveniebat, ut operationem, velut ipsemet tum fassus es, absolvere e REGIOMONTANI praescripto nullatenus potueris.</p><p>Tandem concludis, non opus fuisse de re nunquam recurrente tantum laboris suscipere, ut omnes motuum circumstantias tam accurate demonstrarentur, dummodo saltem, an in aetherea, an vero elementari regione versentur Cometae, nobis constiterit, siquidem hac de re inter Philosophos praecipua movetur controversia. Et per Halonum similiumque meteororum instabilitatem atque evanescentiam etiam Cometas non indigere admodum diligenti animadversione, inferre conaris, adeo ut subiungere non dubites, extingui cum ipso meteoro eius generis scripta. Quam vero sincere et recte haec a te proferantur, aliorum esto iudicium. Certe ROTHMANNUS longe aliter de meis laboribus in hoc Cometarum negotio accurate enodando sentit, ut vel e portione literarum eius nuper ad me datarum, quam unâ me mittere dixi, videre licet. Aliter etiam SCULTETUS (ut e particula eius epistolae non ita dudum ad me scriptae, quam etiam tibi describi curavi, patet) de hac re iudicat, unâque MONAVUM quendam, virum doctum in Silesia, mihi autem incognitum, ad se scripsisse refert, ut aliorum tomorum apud me editionem urgere velit, licet unâ conqueratur de huius seculi ingratitudine, quod dignum non sit, ut solida veritas ei in talibus communicetur. Nihil enim iam fere tam bene dictum factumque est, quod non cavillando depravetur. Sed agedum videamus, an merito nostri labores hac in parte ita a te vel quoquam alio extenuentur, quasi ob subiecti, de quo agunt, evanescentiam inutiles et non magni aut diuturni momenti existant. Quaestio haec de Cometarum situ, aethereusne sit an elementaris, et quomodo generentur, non est nova neque levis, sed multis ab hinc seculis ab antiquissimis Philosophis longe ante ARISTOTELEM (quorum etiam plerique rectius quam ille hac de re senserunt) diu multumque magno conatu et subtilissimis argumentationibus agitata, ut vel e SENECAe libro, quem de Cometis reliquit, satis apparet. Neque tamen usque ad haec tempora penitus disquisitum est, quidnam sint Cometae, et ubi procreentur, eo quod veteres et modernorum etiam plurimi non e certis observationibus per apodixes Mathemadeas nodum hunc plus quam Gordium resolvere aggressi sint, sed saltem argutijs quibusdam et opinationibus atque logisticis subtilitatibus rem omnem absolvere praesumpserunt, assumentes plaeraque pro concessis, quae prius inquirenda demonstrandaque erant, an ita se haberent nec ne. Idcirco operae precium duxi, e ratis observationibus veritatem exactam hac in parte inquirere, Geometriceque demonstrare, ne Peripateticorum et aliorum argutiae veritati, ut tot iam seculis factum est, amplius fucum facerent. Id autem e parallaxibus Cometarum pervestigandum nemo Mathematum gnarus ignorat; at cum parallaxes illae subtili inquisitione indigeant, adeo ut facile error aliquis tacite subrepat, nisi omnia admodum circumspecte administrentur, imo impossibile etiam sit, parallaxes certas enucleare, nisi etiam motus proprij Cometae adhibeatur praecisa ratio: requiritur utique necessario, ut cursus eius apparens quam diligentissime, quoties per serenitatem fieri potest, coelitus denotetur, quod sane non efficitur, nisi inquiratur, qualem habeat ad Eclipticam, respectu longitudinis et latitudinis, atque Aequatorem, quoad ascensionem rectam et declinationem, dispositionem, hincque unâ constet, qualem etiam ductum proprium designet, et quomodo in eodem moveatur, qualique ratione indies cursum suum variet. Sine his enim aliquis frustra parallaxin perscrutari tentârit. Motus enim proprius plus afficit Cometae apparentias quam ea diversitas, quae ratione parallaxium, si forte quam habuerint, contingit. Sunt igitur haec duo solerter discernenda diligenterque inter se conferenda. Hinc etiam ea, quae a summo illo REGIOMONTANO et quibusdam alijs neotericis de Cometarum parallaxibus pervestigandis varie et erudite adinventa sunt, etiam si quoad demonstrationem recte se habeant, tamen cum praesupponant id in  Cometis fieri, quod vel rarissime vel nunquam accidit, eos videlicet destitui omni motu proprio et nullo alio, quam universali illo primi mobilis diurno circuitu, convolvi, in  praxi ipsa inutilia prorsusque supervacanea sunt. Patet  itaque, si id quod controversum est, iustam meretur disquisitionem, utrum videlicet Cometae sint aetherei, nec ne, idque  sine parallaxium investigatione fieri nequeat, parallaxes vero absque diligenti dispositione, quoad praecipuos  coeli circulos proprijque ductus limites et diurni motus alterationem nequaquam dignosci queant, consequens esse, non iudicandum inutilem et supervacaneum laborem, ex  observationibus certis demonstrare Cometarum longitudines, latitudines ductumque circuli, sub quo moventur, qualique tenore cursum suum absolvant, quomodo eum certa  lege adaugeant vel etiam inhibeant, cum sine his alterum, quod principaliter inquirendum venit et in dubium vocatur, decidi nequeat. Imo postquam constiterit, eos in  aethere ipso versari, plurimum negotium hoc confirmat et illustrat, si per convenientem Hypothesin eorum cursus instar Planetarum demonstretur et in numeros resolvatur. Sic enim plenius et citius alijs persuadeatur omnino coelestem illis inesse naturam ab aliorum Planetarum proprie tatibus non admodum alienam, nisi quod non perpetua  sit adeoque exaltata ut in reliquis errantibus stellis. Sed  facilius est aliorum indefessos labores carpere et extenuare, quam imitari vel etiam superare.</p><p>Porro quod Cometae instar meteororum disparent, nec semper durent, nil probat eorum motum et habitudines diligenter non esse disquirenda. Nam licet alij atque alij certo tempore conspiciantur tandemque paulatim consumantur, redeunt tamen rursus alij. Nec ullum fuit seculum, quod non aliquos conspexerit Cometas, quos et valde admirati semper sunt homines, tanquam magnum aliquid novum et insolitum, adeo ut plus semper eorum mentes moverint hae crinitae stellae, quam reliqua mundo coaeva sidera. Licet vero aliquando conspecti Cometae non ita diu post dispareant, neque intra diutinum tempus alij redeant, adeo ut interdum in 20 annis vix aliquis apparuerit, eo sane maiorem operae precium est adhibere diligentiam in pervestigandis eorum apparentijs, dum durant et adsunt, quo eo melius de talium Phaenomenorum situ, motu et natura controversia tamdiu varie disceptata dirimi possit; ex quo non semper talis occasio offeratur, ut in reliquis sideribus, quae omni serena nocte, quotquot Horizontem exuperârunt, conspicere observareque licet, si modo ea cura mortalium mentes traheret. Propter has itaque et plures alias causas necessarium et utile est admodum sedulam in Cometarum situ et motu adhibere descriptionem. Praeterea licet illi per se non satis digni viderentur, quorum causa tantos labores exantlare oporteat, est tamen quiddam aliud, cur idipsum fieri pro perpetuis illis sideribus totiusque coeli natura et dispositione penitius perscrutandis adeo expediat, ut aliunde idem vix constare queat. Ideoque ego ex hac accurata Cometarum praeteritis annis consideratione multa ad totam rem Astronomicam facientia, quae non parvi momenti sunt, deprehendi; ut: coelum non constare ullis realibus, duris et impervijs orbibus, velut tota credidit Antiquitas et recentiorum adhuc quam plurimi: nullos dari epicyclos: Terram revera annuatim non circumire, et pleraque alia, de quibus circa finem totius operis copiosius tracto. Sicque per hanc ascititiorum et novorum siderum exquisitam animadversionem viam paro, imo fundamenta pono ijs, quae in toto illo opere Astronomico circa perennium et mundo coaevorum astrorum apparentias ac revolutiones redintegranda, et quasi de novo instauranda, favente divino auxilio, proposui, in quibus etiam indies arduo et indefesso conamine elaboro. Imo et in his libris de neotericis coeli Phaenomenis multa ad rem Astronomicam rectius quam hactenus constituendam diu frustra desiderata proponuntur, qualia sunt Solaris cursus exacta restitutio, Lunaris latitudinis emendatio, affixarum stellarum verificatio et plaeraque alia, quae per occasionem harum secundariarum stellarum Astronomiae cultoribus non parvo a me labore et studio conquisita communico. Sic etiam novam hanc Hypothesin mihiipsi usque in publicationem integri operis Astronomici reservassem, nisi Cometa ille anni 1577 circa Solem revolutus me invitasset, ut eius propalationem eo in libro admitterem; adeo multa et magna vel ex hac sola Cometarum occasione, hactenus minus perspecta, in lucem protulimus, et favente Numine, imposterum proferemus uberius. Quod si talia scripta unâ cum ijs, de quibus agunt, Phaenomenis, evanida sunt, utique vel ob perennium corporum, de quibus unâ multis in locis agunt, considerationem aliquando superesse merebuntur, nec ita facile, ut tu ominaris, abolebuntur. Et forte tam diu atque toties usque ad fastidium repetita cantilena de primo mobili et sphaericis tyrocinijs, vulgaribusque Planetarum theorijs (quae omnia nihil novi et antea desiderati inveniunt aut constituunt) apud peritos et gratos permanebunt. An ignoras, quod HIPPARCHUS ille laudatissimus et antiquissimus Astronomus nunquam nobis reliquisset canonicam omnium affixarum stellarum tam sedulam et operosam descriptionem, nisi e Nova Stella suo tempore exorta, quae procul dubio paulo post disparuit, ad tam immensum laborem exuperandum incitatus fuisset, ut de ipso PLINIUS testatur. Et sane post illum nihil simile a quoquam hominum, elapsis circiter 2000 annis, peractum est. Nam PTOLEMAeUS eiusque posteri annotatione Hipparchica acquieverunt, habita saltem promotionis totius octavae sphaerae ratione. Ideoque REGIOMONTANUS hunc abacum affixarum stellarum magis divinum quam humanum opus vocat et non prophanandum aut vulgo concredendum thesaurum. Neque id citra rem aestimat. Memineris etiam, quae et quanta ARCHIMEDES vel vilissimarum arenarum occasione impulsu GELONIS Siculûm Regis nobis reliquerit. Sic etiam alias saepe e rebus minimis neque diu durantibus causa hominibus oblata est longe maiora et stabiliora extruendi pervestigandique, de quibus nunc non attinet plura dicere. Video enim me iam in rescribendo nimium fuisse et epistolae leges non parum transgressum; quam immorationem aequi bonique consulas, quodque tecum de hac materia lubenti animo conferam, ascribas. Vale, et librum illum M. CAMERARIJ de hoc eodem Cometa Francofurti ad Oderam (ut puto) editum, da operam, ut brevi habeam. Cuperem etiam inspicere SIMONIS a Quercu scriptum de quadratura circuli. Id enim nondum mihi allatum est. Quod si unâ mihi procuraveris, rem feceris pergratam.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale, et ante brumalem glaciem rescribe.<br>Uraniburgi inter alias quamplurimas occupationes festinanti calamo utut prolixae datae die 4. Novembris Anno 88.</p><p>Quodsi Princeps vester illustrissimus adhuc papyrum aliquam<br>meæ officinæ opportunam Rostochium miserit, da operam, ut eam ab iniuria<br>hyemis et pluviarum tutam quam citissime recipiam. Rogo etiam peramanter,<br>velis nunc statim ad me artificem aliquem transmittere, qui sculpendi in<br>ligno artem excellenter calleat. Tali enim per hybernum tempus et forte<br>etiam tota æstate opus habeo ob figuras tum instrumentorum tum aliarum<br>designationum varias ligno insculpendas, quæ postea imprimendæ veniant. Si<br>itaque eiusmodi hominem mature procuraveris et prima quaque occasione huc<br>miseris, rem feceris mihi magnopere gratam et amicitiæ nostræ convenientem.<br>De mercede eius, ubi advenerit, facile inter nos conveniet. Si quid<br>exposueris viatici, restituam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Heinrich Brucaeus.</b></p><p>You have at last broken that deep silence in which, all summer long, you forbore to send me any letter, most loving Brucaeus; and since now finally, as though roused from a deep sleep, you have visited me again with your learned letter, this is reason for me to forgive the delay hitherto committed and to hold you excused. In it you treat with me, as it were professedly, of those matters contained in the second book of my <i>Aetherial Phenomena</i> [<i>Aethereorum Phaenomenωn</i>], which was conveyed to you last spring by one of my household who was setting out from here into Germany; and you show that you read carefully through what is found in its earlier part, the later part being lightly touched upon. It seemed good to you, therefore, to reply in this manner to the things you wrote to me about these matters. That you approve and commend what is contained in the earlier portion of that volume, for the certainty and fit of its observations and demonstrations — and especially that you affirm that the invention of my novel hypothesis [<i>neotericae meae Hypotheseos</i>] concerning the arrangement of the celestial revolutions pleases you greatly — is most gratifying to me. Indeed, as regards the constitution of that hypothesis, I had suspected that it would not appeal to your temperament [<i>Genio</i>], seeing that you are otherwise so vehement and steadfast a conserver and champion of the authority of the ancients — so much so that you sometimes seem to attribute too much to them, and to detract not a little from surer reasonings and from experience itself; but here it has turned out otherwise than I supposed. For this sketch [<i>Hypotyposin</i>] of ours of the world-system — never discerned by the ancients, nor even devised before by any of the moderns — you not only approve with your vote, but you also judge it to be preferred above the rest, because, unlike those proposed by Ptolemy and Copernicus, it admits no such absurdities. But that you may the more rightly understand by what reasoning I arrived, some years ago, at the invention of this hypothesis, I send you a copy of a letter recently written by me to that most renowned and learned man, Master Caspar Peucer — a letter in which I thought I should reply to certain doubts of his (which he had written to that most noble man, Master Heinrich Rantzau, who had communicated to him a bare outline of his hypothesis excerpted from my book — and of whose words, together with the ambiguous opinion of another certain mathematician on the same matter, I here furnish you a copy) — so that on this occasion I might confer more fully with so great a philosopher and mathematician about this whole astronomical business. From these things you will perceive very many matters bearing peculiarly not only [upon] the reasonings and fuller explanation of this hypothesis, but also upon the whole astronomical enterprise, which, I think, will not displease your temperament. But once you have read this very letter through — or even, if it please you, had it transcribed — I should wish it sent back to me, since I desire to keep this copy, and I have not now the leisure to have it written out again. As to what you desire concerning the restoration [<i>instauratio</i>] of astronomy — that everything founded upon this hypothesis and reduced to numbers from sure geometrical observations should correspond exactly to the phenomena — you will also find, in this letter sent to Master Peucer, in what order and with how great an effort I have proposed to approach and undertake so great a matter, with the favour of the Maker of Heaven itself and of the Universe. Nor have you any reason to fear that the immensity of the labour, the difficulty of so arduous a matter, the long duration of time required, the magnitude of the expense, or any other trouble will, with that same supreme Deity breathing upon our endeavours, deter me from consecrating to a grateful posterity (as I hope) the restoration of so divine and sublime an art with that precision (let it be said without envy) with which it has hitherto been drained dry [i.e., accomplished] by no one, and from leaving it, complete in all its parts [<i>omnibus suis numeris</i>], so far indeed as can be performed by one man.</p><p>But enough of these things in this place. There is, however, one thing about which I must complain to you in this matter. A certain man of all-too-brazen brow — whom I judge unworthy to be named here, and who moreover, wherever he has been or is, betrays himself for what he is — has not blushed to arrogate to himself this invention of my hypotheses, and to assert this in a public writing published at Strasbourg [<i>Argentinae</i>] this year. Whether he filched this too, along with the rest that he secretly copied at my house — while, in the service of that most noble man Erik Lange, he was for some days in his retinue — or by what other means he drew off its arrangement here, let himself know; and my students showed [me] a certain similar [drawing] then lying among the waste wrappings [<i>inter segestria</i>; see apparatus], which had been less aptly delineated. Certainly how fraudulently and craftily this man acted, the rest of what he copied here from my [papers], without my knowledge — some part of which I have recovered, written out in his own hand — sufficiently prove, even though I do not find that hypothesis among those sheets; but he could have hidden it elsewhere or retained it in memory, since perhaps the convenience of drawing was not at hand. My household students know that I had established that hypothesis several years ago now and shown it to them. Nay more: this book on the Comet of the year [15]77, although it bears on its face an edition made in this year [15]88, yet all who are with me know that it was printed in my own press in the year [15]87, but deliberately marked one year later, that on account of its novelty it might be the more welcome. And that in this book the same hypothesis as mine is generally explained, you are not unaware, as is clear from what has already been said. But that man, who lately would claim it for himself, signally betrayed that he does not properly understand it — far less that he is its author — when he depicts and feigns the Martian orb as totally encircling the Solar [orb], which he could have learned to be impossible even from Copernicus alone, merely glanced at in passing. And how, I ask, will he ever construct new hypotheses analogous to the celestial revolutions, who is not furnished with exquisite observations of many years, so as thence to investigate skilfully what is wanting in the hypotheses hitherto in use, and how that very thing may be restored? Without these supports, to boast of some new invention is frivolous and downright ridiculous, since he has nothing whereby to prove, by the appearances themselves, why — leaving the earlier ones aside — one [ought] thus to innovate, and how, by manifold observations, he may confirm that this [innovation] more rightly agrees with the celestial appearances. But away with those follies, to which no one, unless he does not understand the matter, will lend credence.</p><p>I return to your letter, intending to examine the things it contains thereafter, in order, as I have determined. You seem to call somewhat into doubt the correction of the places of the fixed stars proposed by me in chapter 2 from the declination of a star and its transit across the meridian, on the ground that an exquisite knowledge of time and the Sun's place can scarcely be established precisely. Although this is in some way not said without reason — and is asserted by me too, nearly so, towards the end of that same chapter, in the <i>Addition</i> beginning at folio 31 — nevertheless, when due diligence is applied, and certain means that exhibit the smallest particles of time by an even movement, as far as can be done, of which we have not a few ready to hand, this method too deserves a place without any very sensible error, especially if it be examined by frequent reiteration until the results obtained differ not very sensibly, as is plain from the earlier observations made in that manner, which are set before the eyes on page 30, if they be compared with others, reiterated and obtained by a different method, by way of the Sun, with the star Venus intermediating, [referred] to the fixed stars, which are shown on page 33. For the difference is very small — inasmuch as, where it is greatest, it does not exceed the tenth part of one degree, and in six or seven stars scarcely reaches three minutes; a precision which truly was not extracted by the ancients, so that I cannot sufficiently marvel that, with that smallness and scarcity of instruments which I then used, even along this very road so slippery for going astray, I nevertheless aimed so close to the mark. But a more exact method, liable to no ambiguity, of verifying the places of the fixed stars, I briefly indicated in the same <i>Addition</i>: namely, by distances taken from the Sun to Venus appearing by day, and at night related from her to the [fixed] stars. About which matter I treat more fully in the preceding book, and by several observations accurately made in that manner — both morning and evening ones — I restore the places of the fixed stars to their true and precise distance from the vernal equinox; and I demonstrate that this can be so, and not otherwise, from the observations themselves, having noted also their true latitudes, in which no less than in the longitudes there has hitherto been too much error. There too is set first the most exact restoration of the Sun's course to our times, since upon it all else depends. There the place of the apogee and the true quantity of the eccentricity are geometrically demonstrated from several sure observations of preceding years, diligently obtained from the heavens with several and not-fallacious instruments, with a Table appended of the mean motions, corrected too, and of the prosthaphaereses [<i>prostaphaereseωn</i>] extracted from the eccentricity found — so that concerning the Sun's motion precisely agreeing with the appearances no further doubt remains with me. I shall perhaps subjoin some things also about the lunar course, likewise subtly restored, when through the observations of several years I shall more fully unravel its perplexed path, both as to length [longitude] and breadth [latitude]. I see that you are still ruminating on those things which the most learned Gemma Frisius attempted, in his little book on the cross-staff [<i>de Radio Astronomico</i>], as to the marking of the fixed stars upon some globe, and afterwards the designation of the equator and ecliptic upon the same. But I, for certain reasons, judge it more fitting to be done thus: that the equator and ecliptic be first arranged on the globe and distributed into the smallest particles, and afterwards, according to their requirement, the places of the fixed stars be set down and verified in various ways from here and there, so that they fall back into one and the same point. By distances taken by means of the cross-staff, however, as Gemma handed down and as it was afterwards attempted by many, this business will never be rightly accomplished. For it is impossible, by the cross-staff [<i>Radium</i>] — with however great a length it be endowed and with whatever subtlety divided, and even reiterating the observation very often — to discover the exact intervals of the stars to the very second [<i>in ipso scrupulo</i>; here read as arc-second — see apparatus], as we shall show elsewhere by its causes. And therefore I was the first to devise sextants supremely fit for this office, and everywhere yielding the true distances of the stars within the same minute, or even within an aliquot part of this, however often the observation be repeated; with which, for several years now, I have happily made use, with precise subtlety, in investigating the distances of the heavenly bodies. Moreover, the stars are set down on a globe in vain — by whatever instrument they be at last derived from the heavens — unless it be very large, such as the one I possess, six feet in diameter, which is everywhere of brass [<i>orichalcicus</i>], and which exhibits distinctly the division of the degrees into single sixty-minute [intervals] by transversal line-rulings, in the manner customary with us, and which is moreover endowed with perfect roundness. On this I have resolved to record the places of all the fixed stars, derived from the heaven itself by various instruments, of which a great and chief part is already held by me ready beforehand. Wherefore you have no reason to doubt that I am of my own accord working, with all possible diligence, at those things which conduce to the precise restoration of the fixed stars — so that there would have been no need of any admonition, or of a recapitulation of those things which are proposed by me in chapter 2.</p><p>Furthermore, you hold suspect the maximum latitude of the Moon, which I have found to be greater by a quarter of one degree than the dicta of Ptolemy, and you assail it by means of the magnitude of lunar eclipses and the calculation corresponding to it. You summon up moreover the authority of Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, and other distinguished mathematicians — that they perpetually retained the Ptolemaic maximum latitude of the Moon at precisely 5 degrees — whom you do not doubt to have ascertained with great diligence, from the heavens, that it is so. You reckon, moreover, that the Ptolemaic latitude is free from every fault on account of the opportuneness of the place of his observation, which conjoined the Moon — requiring [being at] its maximum northern latitude near the summer tropic — as close as possible to the vertex [zenith], the impediments of parallaxes and refractions being on that account removed, etc. That I may treat with you more fully of these things, the truth itself demands it. Know then that the maximum latitude of the Moon was established by me as greater by 15 minutes than the Ptolemaic, not rashly nor without frequent and exact observation. For that it really is so, especially near the quadratures, I have been taught from the heaven itself by many trials and by observations in no wise fallacious — except that I have sometimes found it greater by one or another minute [<i>scrupulo</i>], and at times a little smaller; and if some other inequality underlies [it], as I shall not deny, I shall unravel it more thoroughly. Nor do the observations of eclipses detract anything from my assertion that can fall under the senses. For although the maximum latitude be established as greater by 15 minutes than that which is in use and has long been received, yet this difference, around those limits where eclipses are wont to occur near the nodes, imports nothing that can be perceived. For at a distance of 12 degrees from the nodes — within which limit, most nearly, lunar eclipses must occur — the difference between the Ptolemaic latitude and ours comes to only three first-minutes [arc-minutes], which cause only a single digit [<i>digitum</i>] in the obscuration of the Moon — and that scarcely perceptible, on account of the extremity of the Earth's shadow not precisely falling under the senses; and the very times of eclipses have hitherto not become so certain that this matter could in any way harm our discovery — as very many lunar eclipses observed by us most diligently in some past years, near the sixth degree of distance from the nodes, have taught us: the difference of the latitudes assumed in both ways comes out at 1½ minutes, which, as regards the quantity of the Moon's obscuration, is insensible. But around this place, which is also the midpoint between the nodes and the extreme limits, the more frequent eclipses are observed. Near the very nodes, however, when the Moon is eclipsed, the Ptolemaic maximum latitude and ours bring in no difference, as you yourself can easily gather all this, the matter being better considered than before — as is also plain from the appended delineation, resolved into numbers. There is added this too, that I am uncertain whether the latitude of the Moon behaves the same way near the new and full moons as near the quadratures, where I have chiefly observed this wandering [<i>divagationem</i>]; about which matter, God willing, I shall disclose elsewhere what fuller experience shall have given. In vain, therefore, do you thrust lunar eclipses upon me. But you seem, through some carelessness, to have reckoned that even around the limits of eclipses the Moon's latitude exceeds the Ptolemaic by a quarter of a degree — not weighing that this holds only near the maximum wandering from the ecliptic, which is distant a quadrant of the circle from the nodes, and afterwards is gradually attenuated towards the intersection, so much so that near the very nodes there comes a coincidence into one [value], and that at the distance of a few degrees — near which lunar eclipses can occur — there underlies a very small and scarcely perceptible difference. The authority of Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, and others (if there are any) does not in this part move me, since the renewal [<i>redintegratio</i>] of astronomy is to be derived not from the authorities of men, but from sure observations and the demonstrations resting upon these. Nor did Regiomontanus ever observe the maximum latitude of the Moon from the heavens, or suspect that it needed any correction. Nor are those things which are reported in letters as observed elsewhere, on the Sun and the rest of the heavenly bodies, by him and his disciple Bernhard Walther, correct — so much so that they do not agree with themselves. Copernicus retained the Ptolemaic maximum latitude in the Moon unaltered, nor did he himself attempt to test from the heavens whether this is so or not, excusing this on the ground that fortune did not grant him the same opportunity of testing as it did to the renowned Ptolemy — because of the impediments of the lunar parallaxes [<i>commutationum</i>] in the higher situation of the pole, which at Alexandria could be conveniently guarded against — as is plain from Copernicus's own words, chapter 15 of book 4 of the <i>Revolutions</i>. Nor did Werner attempt anything about this matter that can be proved from any writing of his. But he too acquiesced in the Ptolemaic observation, nor, so far as I know, has anything certain been worked out in this part by any others. In vain, therefore, is the authority of others alleged in a matter never explored by them — although this [authority], even of itself, does not deserve a place in mathematics. That Ptolemy had a far more convenient opportunity of scrutinizing the maximum latitude of the Moon I do not for my part deny, since near the vertex he did not labour under the fault either of parallax (which Copernicus calls <i>commutatio</i>) or of refraction; and therefore I myself, in those same <i>Annotations</i> where I treat of the Moon's maximum latitude, page 40 and following, in no way disapprove the Ptolemaic observation — so much so that I judge it to have been correct at that time, but afterwards, down to our age, to have changed by a quarter of a degree, just as the departure of the solar orbit from the equator has likewise not remained unaltered in the meantime. Nor do I wish to call into doubt his Parallactic instrument [<i>Parallaticum instrumentum</i>], which he used in this observation — although I could perhaps do so not without reason, as frequent experience has taught us, with two similar instruments built with greater care and diligence than was perhaps furnished by Ptolemy. I pass over that he did not employ the subtlest division of the parts, and — what matters most — that he transmitted the lunar rays through apertures [<i>foramina</i>], where in a few minutes [<i>scrupulis</i>] aberration is inclined [to occur]. There is added this too: that in the place of the longer Rule near the perpendicular contact, where the observation looks most nearly toward the vertex, the smallest and almost insensible change of the angle by the smaller movable Rule passes through more divisions on the longer [scale] than otherwise in a disposition of the instrument more remote from the vertex — as is manifest to those who have been taught by experience itself to handle these things. Nor yet should I on that account say that he erred by a quarter of a degree through a fault of the instrument or of the observation, but rather that the lunar departure from the ecliptic has in truth been increased down to our times — whether it can be equally great in every disposition relative to the Sun, I should not yet safely affirm. For some observations near the full moons cry out against it, ones which approach the Ptolemaic wandering very closely, so that on that account I suspect some inequality lurks here — about which, however, I am unwilling to pronounce anything certain before I have, as I said, more fully weighed the whole matter. Although in our horizon the Moon's parallax and refraction bring in not a little difficulty, so that this business cannot be accomplished so readily as at Alexandria by Ptolemy, yet I have long since learned, by frequent practice of observations, to guard sufficiently against these impediments — so that I would dare safely to pledge my faith that in my determination of the Moon's latitude one minute, or at most two [<i>unum vel alterum ad summum minutum</i>], is in no way wanting [i.e., the error is no more than a minute or two].</p><p>But do you want the suffrage of others? — since you would think one should not rely wholly on my observations alone (which is readily granted by those who do not themselves observe the heavenly bodies, nor know with what diligence and assiduity we apply ourselves to this labour) — lo, I will give you the testimony, neither obscure nor insufficiently firm, of that excellent mathematician Christoph Rothmann — who devotes his effort to astronomical exercises for the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse — which he wrote to me in a letter a few weeks ago. For he asserts that he detected almost the same maximum latitude of the Moon with the Prince's instruments as I [did] with mine. And he adds, moreover, that the Head of the Dragon [the ascending node] departs from the Tables by more than four degrees. But by what reasoning he found this out, and how he can prove it, I do not see. For here the lunar eclipses cry out clearly enough, since 4 degrees near the very nodes vary the Moon's latitude by 21 minutes — that is, 2/3 parts of the lunar body — which truly would be quite perceptible in lunar eclipses, yet has not hitherto been found by me to be so. But that you may the more rightly grasp Rothmann's mind, I have had a certain portion of his letter recently sent to me transcribed, in which these things which I report concerning the Moon's maximum latitude and the nodes are contained among certain others. I would have sent you the entire copy of the letter, but my amanuensis, occupied with other things, could not transcribe it in such haste. For they are rather long-winded and fill several gatherings [<i>quaterniones</i>]. And let it suffice to have replied this much concerning the maximum latitude of the Moon as established by me; with which, if you are still not satisfied, do you yourself take care to construct suitable instruments liable to no error, and learn to inspect and examine the heaven itself, before you presume to call into doubt the discoveries of others — [discoveries] drawn from celestial observation itself, on many a wakeful night, and not produced out of papers in a steam-room [<i>in vaporario</i>].</p><p>Afterwards you make use of a certain recapitulation of the things which I comprehend in the following chapters of the earlier part; but as for what you there obliquely insinuate — that I assert the Comet to have described a circle, although the observations sometimes disagree by five or fewer minutes from the exact circumference — what is that but to look for a knot in a bulrush [<i>nodum in scirpo quaerere</i>; i.e., to make difficulties where there are none]? For that very small difference, which does not exceed five minutes — nay, very rarely reaches it, and for the most part holds steady within one or another minute — is not worthy to come into mention, since it is almost imperceptible; and the diversity of the air, and the application [position] of the Comet relative to the Horizon — nearer at the time of some observations, more remote at others — could most easily have brought in this tiny diversity; to say nothing of the fact that a few minutes in the places of the fixed stars which I used stand otherwise than they were assumed by us according to the old observation, as is plain from the <i>Addendum</i> attached to the end of the second chapter, of which I also spoke before. This discrepancy too, of no great moment, in the tracing of the Comet's circle, I excused fully and more than enough in its proper place in the book itself — so that there was no need to dwell upon things so slight and not pertaining to the matter's aim, nor in any way detracting, among the candid and skilled, from our assertion. If you had thoroughly perceived, by experience itself, with how much labour and industry there was need to reach that very precision which I attained, you would surely not idly dispute about the smallest things, and ones that escape almost all the senses.</p><p>Next you raise a certain ambiguity in this, that I several times assert all comets to dwell in the aetherial region above the Moon; and you object Lycosthenes against me, and you report that he, in a certain place of his book <i>On Portents</i> [<i>de Ostentis</i>], set down that certain comets, blazing, kindled a conflagration on the Earth below — and thence you try tacitly to infer that some were sublunar, and that, according to the mind of the Peripatetics, they were fires kindled in the uppermost air. But I, having surveyed that whole little book of Lycosthenes, could find nothing of the kind in it. Nay, even if some such thing were found there, it would nonetheless have to be attended to that those Historical authors do not treat the matter mathematically, but, writing generally about portents, very often make no distinction at all between comets really established in the heaven and torches and lamps and flying fires and similar kindled meteors of the infralunar air. It could therefore most easily have happened that, from some of these kindled and sublunar meteors fallen somewhere onto the Earth, some conflagration was once kindled in suitable material — which, however, is unsuitably attributed to the burning of some comet, or to flames kindled [by it]. For these [comets] do not burn, nor do they emit fire, but in a manner not much unlike the rest of the stars they dart forth a light implanted in themselves, whether they obtain it from the Sun or from elsewhere. But that I assert the matter of all comets to be aetherial, consisting of the same [region] of the world in which they dwell, is not without its sure reasons, as will be plain in the Epilogue of the whole work, where I shall professedly recall both the opinions of others about the matter and procreation of comets to a just inquiry, and also openly set forth my own opinion — since in this book and in the other two as well I treat of this matter only lightly, because that physical consideration is not fittingly proposed before all the things which, according to astronomical demonstration, both in the New Star and also in some comets seen afterwards, have been treated by me. There too I shall show by sure reasons how absurd is this opinion of yours and of certain others — that comets, even though they complete their course in the heaven itself, can nevertheless consist of elementary matter lifted up into the aether by the power of the stars. For how many and how great [arguments] oppose this opinion, both from the very appearances of comets and from a truer physics, I shall there make manifest. Nor does the fact that comets perish lend any suffrage to this opinion of yours. For those things which sometimes come about miraculously in nature, especially the celestial, after the first creation of the world, it follows fittingly that they should also perish before the universal abolition of created things. But why the New Star and comets, once arisen, do not endure perpetually, nor always retain the same magnitude or colour, I shall bring forth the reasons in that same Epilogue of the whole work. But that which the ancient Epicureans and certain other philosophers held — that the heavenly bodies are nourished by humours [vapours] — is in itself plainly false and ridiculous, far less that anything certain about comets can thence be proved. You seem to me to be about shortly to degenerate, without your own knowledge, into a Paracelsist. For he too maintained the same error of the ancients — although he thought that the stars are nourished not entirely from vapours, but from a certain celestial matter — so much so that he did not fear to assert that all meteors are nothing other than the excrements of the stars. For it seemed fitting that those things which need fodder and nourishment should also have their excretions; but this speculation is alien to all truth. That different comets arise and are destroyed from time to time, and that some of them stay in one place while others are mobile, and that among those that move, some direct their course in the order [following the signs], others against the order of the signs, and that some of them seek the North, others the South — all this proves nothing, [namely] that they consist of elementary matter. For these very things, and more, can belong to them as established in the heaven itself out of celestial matter, as we shall discuss more amply in that same Epilogue. But what you add, that some keep a certain order in their motion, while others wander without order, is excessive. For I judge that all comets have kept a sure order, and have not slipped suddenly from a slower motion into a faster, or from this into that, nor coursed by a wandering, inconstant, or tumultuous (as Seneca calls it) leading. For in all the comets that have been noted by me I have detected a constant and proportional tenor of motion, just as also in the rest that were diligently observed from the heavens by our predecessor mathematicians. For as for the fact that certain Historians have perhaps attributed a wandering and inconstant course to some [comets], we make nothing of it. For the same thing could appear to them, and to others like them not understanding the astronomical matter, in the other planets too, nay, in the fixed stars themselves. Nor is a ratified truth gathered from uncertain testimonies. Nay, even mathematicians not of the common sort attributed a certain motion to the New Star, although it stood perpetually in the same place, and they did not hesitate to assert that comets seen afterwards completed their course by a wandering and disordered leading — as both Cornelius Gemma and Thaddaeus, both excellent mathematicians, inconsiderately admitted in this very comet (not to speak now of the rest), as is indicated by us in its proper place in the book itself. You see, therefore — and you will see more fully elsewhere — that from all those little reasonings which you adduce, what you intend does not follow: that comets are generated from elementary matter, whether above or below the Moon.</p><p>You subjoin afterwards a still further unsuitable hesitation, or rather a less-than-deliberate disapproval of our hypothesis concerning the comet of the year [15]77, of which this book sent [to you] treats. For you reckon, from the fact that I posit the Comet to be moved around the Sun, so that it could depart from it by at most a sixth of a circle [60°], that it is a consequence that, if ever it were in the part of its circle nearest to the earth, it would then touch the Earth — since the semidiameter subtends a sixth of a circle, as is plain from the elements of geometry. But here you seem to me to have weighed the whole matter too drowsily. For although the Comet wandered from the Sun, the centre of its orb, by 60 parts [degrees] of a great circle in the heaven — as regards our view of it from the earth — yet that imaginary orb was not so great that it could in any way touch the Earth; nay, its lowest part stood nearly three times more remote from here than the lunar abodes. Which is most easily plain from the appended figure, in which that orb which the Comet described around the Sun is represented by DBEG, whose centre C is near the Sun, but let A be the Earth, on which we dwell, and whence we behold the celestial things. The maximum digression of the Comet from the Sun is at B, to which place let CB and AB be drawn, meeting at the tangent of the circle. There is therefore, in the triangle ABC, a right angle at B (as you know). But the angle CAB is of 60 parts [degrees] according to observation. But let the side CA be presupposed, according to Copernicus, at the Sun's mean distance from the Earth, of 1142 earth-semidiameters. Therefore, in a right-angled rectilinear triangle, one [angle] and one side being given, the side BC will not be unknown — of 988 earth-semidiameters — which is equal to CE itself, since both are from the centre of the same circle. And therefore, CE of 988 earth-semidiameters being subtracted from CA of 1142 of the same, as I just said, there will remain EA of 154 semidiameters. But EA is the nearest distance by which the Comet could approach the earth, even if it had completed the whole circumference of its circle several times. And since the nearest distance of the Moon from the earth is not unsuitably set by Copernicus at about 52 earth-semidiameters, it appears that the Comet, even when most closely approaching the Earth, was nearly three times more remote from it than is the nearest approach of the Moon to the same; and that according to Copernican reckonings, which are truer than the Ptolemaic (by which the Moon comes out still nearer to us). But if it please [anyone] to resolve this approach of the Comet into German miles — taking for each earth-semidiameter 860 of our own or German miles (which is not very far from the truth) — there will leap forth 132,440 miles, by which the Comet, in its nearest situation, could be brought near to us. Is this to touch the Earth? Although, however, I have not here scrutinized everything quite so scrupulously — as indeed there was no need — since I took no account of the apogee and the eccentricity (because this matters little in this place), yet a greater nearness to the Earth can scarcely be given, as is indicated on page 297 in the book itself — although there, by the carelessness of the Printer, 145 is found instead of 154, the two latter characters being transposed; which error, however, is detected among the typographical faults at the end of the book.</p><p>It appears, therefore, how inconsiderately you detracted from our hypothesis, by asserting that the Comet's circle touched the earth — which error indeed would have been far too gross, since I was unwilling even to admit its sublunar course, and demonstrated the contrary throughout the whole book. The very distances from the Earth in the penultimate column of its Table — which derives the numbers in various respects from the hypothesis — could have warned you that the Comet, by the presupposed hypothesis, was never made so low as you suspect; since on 9 November its remoteness is set at 173 earth-semidiameters, and indeed at that time it was not so far from its nearest approach to us, so that it could scarcely have been brought nearer by 20 semidiameters. Do you yourself, then, ponder with yourself how undeservedly you cast so great an absurdity upon us. But you make use of a certain skirmishing and hypothetical caution, saying: <i>If I rightly grasp your opinion.</i> Yet certainly my opinion is not so obscure, nor so sluggishly and intricately declared, that it cannot be perceived by you, so great a mathematician — especially since you had my book with you for nearly half a year; and if, owing to other occupations and cares, any residue of doubt had remained in your mind, it would have been better to have suspended judgement, rather than to have disapproved our [findings] so enormously and beyond all reason. But I easily see whence this not-light error crept upon you. For you unsuitably supposed that the digression of the Comet from the Sun occurs in that circuit which passes through the centre of its circle and is at so great a distance from us as the Sun itself — and that at F — whereas the tangent, at which the maximum apparent digression occurs to us, falls far lower, near B. In a like manner too Girolamo Cardano [<i>Hieronymus Caroanus</i>] was deluded, when at the beginning of the second book <i>On the Variety of Things</i> [<i>de varietate rerum</i>] he tries to demonstrate that the epicycle of Venus, by the ancients' hypothesis, is so large that it touches the Earth, if Venus [♀] near the Perigee be understood to be distant from the Sun, by reckoning of the epicycle, by 45 parts [degrees]. For there too he takes no account of that place in the epicycle where the tangent is made by the line going out from the earth, but inconsiderately weaves through his demonstration by way of the very diameter of the epicycle — not weighing that proportion of the epicycle to the eccentric which was explored by the ancients according to this hypothesis, which, namely, is about 43 to 60. But great men too are sometimes liable to great errors.</p><p>You assert that you have not yet unrolled [read through] the second part of my book, and have read very little in it — so much so that you have only cursorily run through the animadversions against Maestlin. Nay, as far as I can perceive from your letter, you reckon that effort to have been useless or less necessary, by which in the second portion of that book I called the dicta of others about this comet to the anvil [<i>sub incudem revocavi</i>; i.e., subjected to scrutiny] — since the things demonstrated by me geometrically in the earlier part can be torn down by no one. But I judge that one should think far otherwise. For the observations and demonstrations of others, if they are correct, lend great suffrage to the truth — especially when administered in different places by different persons. For although the things which are demonstrated geometrically stand correct, yet, since observations made by mechanical means are not everywhere rightly performed, it comes about that the conclusions themselves, however much they make a pretext of geometrical demonstrations, are very often vain. Thus you do not fear to call into doubt our maximum latitude of the Moon (as also the change of the latitude of the fixed stars according to the varied departure of the ecliptic from the equator, in certain other letters previously sent to me). But when you have understood, from Rothmann's observations in a remote place and verified with other instruments, that the matter behaves the same way, perhaps you will more easily acquiesce in my discoveries — although they are of themselves so ratified that they need no external testimony. And so, both for this and for other necessary reasons, in the first member [section] of the second part I weighed their writings, by which the fact that this Comet was aetherial is fittingly proved together with me. Lest anyone suspect that the animadversions of all the others were wholly alien from my discoveries — which, however, of itself I did not much care about, provided only the truth itself be safe, which is indeed assailed by the opinions of men, but not stormed [conquered], and which also lies hidden, beyond the grasp of most, in the very inner recesses and marrow of things. But in the latter member I overturned the reasonings of the rest, who held the contrary, so that, their contradictions removed and refuted, the free truth might shine forth of itself. And it was by no light fallacies [paralogisms] that four men of great authority, from different places, agreed in this — to prove the Comet so near to the earth that it would produce a parallax of 5 or 6 degrees, and therefore [to be] far below the Moon, scarcely 8 or 9 earth-semidiameters remote from here — as they also pretended demonstrations from sure geometrical observations, by which they would persuade even some learned mathematician, if he had not circumspectly weighed everything, that the matter is so; so much so that your Aristotelian Philosopher Erastus did not hesitate to cite the suffrages of the mathematicians on this comet, asserting that, according to the dogmas of the Peripatetics, it was concreted in the elementary world from kindled fumes — because certain of them tried to demonstrate its parallax greater than that which is in the Moon. Thus does truth often suffer violence, unless the things that are false be skilfully removed. Furthermore, many things about this whole business are contained in the second part, of which there was no occasion to discourse in the earlier. Read through, therefore, at your leisure, this latter section of the book diligently, and then at last bring forth your judgement about it. You say that you made rough observations of that same Comet, by which, according to Regiomontanus's dicta, you found it to have been by no means sublunar, and that you had earlier indicated this to Wittich as he was setting out to me. But when he was here, he held no such view, but was thoroughly imbued with the opinion of the Peripatetics — that all comets wander in the air below the Moon, and there dry out [exhale] from fumes drawn up from the Earth. And therefore he disapproved the contrary dicta of Maestlin and others — which opinion, however, he changed before he returned from here into Germany, because from my observations — even on that comet which appeared in the year [15]80, at which time he was with me — he was instructed that the matter stands otherwise. Yet I recall that you yourself once told me, when I was passing through Rostock on my travels, that you could find no parallax in that Comet — imitating the mind of Regiomontanus, and exploring the whole matter by azimuths and altitudes with intervening time — and at that time you showed me the instrument with which you undertook this observation; which, however, seemed to me to be such that, if by another [instrument] you had found a parallax of 10 or 15 degrees, it would not on that account follow that this Comet was elementary — for greater diligence is required in taking azimuths and altitudes together than has hitherto been perceived by many, especially in so subtle a business, where the greatest things are drawn out from the smallest, and an exquisite knowledge of time within five or ten seconds, such as was necessary, could by no means have been sufficiently perceived by you. For it is not investigated precisely enough by the altitudes of the stars, as I have advised elsewhere. And on that account it came about that you could in no way complete the operation, as you yourself then admitted, according to Regiomontanus's prescription.</p><p>At last you conclude that there was no need to undertake so much labour about a matter never recurring, so as to demonstrate so accurately all the circumstances of the motions — provided only it be established to us at least whether comets dwell in the aetherial or in the elementary region, since about this matter the chief controversy is moved among philosophers. And by the instability and evanescence of haloes [<i>Halonum</i>] and similar meteors, you try to infer that comets too need no very diligent observation — so much so that you do not hesitate to subjoin that writings of that kind are extinguished together with the meteor itself. But how sincerely and rightly these things are brought forth by you, let it be the judgement of others. Certainly Rothmann thinks far otherwise about my labours in accurately unravelling this business of the comets, as may be seen even from the portion of his letter recently sent to me, which I said I was sending along [with this]. Otherwise too Scultetus (as is plain from a particle of his epistle written to me not long ago, which I have also had transcribed for you) judges about this matter; and he reports that a certain Monau — a learned man in Silesia, but unknown to me — wrote to him, that he [Scultetus, or perhaps Monau himself — ambiguous; see apparatus] might be willing to urge the publication of the other volumes at my [press], although he likewise complains of the ingratitude of this age — that it is not worthy that solid truth be communicated to it in such matters. For scarcely anything is now so well said or done that it is not depraved by cavilling. But come, let us see whether our labours in this part are deservedly so disparaged by you or by anyone else — as though, on account of the evanescence of the subject of which they treat, they were useless and of no great or lasting moment. This question about the situation of comets — whether it be aetherial or elementary, and how they are generated — is neither new nor slight, but for many centuries past has been agitated long and much, with great effort and the subtlest argumentations, by the most ancient philosophers long before Aristotle (most of whom also thought more rightly than he about this matter), as is sufficiently plain even from Seneca's book which he left <i>On Comets</i>. Nor yet, down to these times, has it been thoroughly investigated what comets are and where they are procreated, because the ancients, and very many of the moderns too, undertook to resolve this more-than-Gordian knot not from sure observations through mathematical proofs [<i>apodixes</i>], but presumed to settle the whole matter merely by certain subtleties and conjectures and computational niceties — assuming as conceded most of the things which first needed to be inquired into and demonstrated, [namely] whether they were so or not. On that account I judged it worth the effort to inquire, from ratified observations, into the exact truth in this part, and to demonstrate it geometrically, lest the subtleties of the Peripatetics and others should any longer cast a deceptive gloss [<i>fucum facere</i>] upon the truth, as has been done for so many centuries now. But that this must be investigated from the parallaxes of comets, no one skilled in mathematics is ignorant; yet since those parallaxes need subtle inquiry — so much so that some error easily creeps in tacitly, unless everything be very circumspectly administered — nay, since it is even impossible to extricate certain parallaxes unless the precise reckoning of the comet's proper motion be also applied: it is in any case necessarily required that its apparent course be marked from the heavens as diligently as possible, as often as can be done through clear weather; which truly is not effected unless it be inquired what disposition it has relative to the ecliptic, in respect of longitude and latitude, and to the equator, as to right ascension and declination — and hence it be at once established what proper track it designates, and how it moves in it, and by what reckoning it varies its course day by day. For without these things one would try in vain to scrutinize the parallax. For the proper motion affects the comet's appearances more than that diversity which arises by reason of the parallaxes, if perchance they had any. These two things, therefore, must be skilfully discerned and diligently compared with one another. Hence too those things which were variously and learnedly devised by that supreme Regiomontanus and certain other moderns about investigating the parallaxes of comets — even if as regards the demonstration they are correct — yet, since they presuppose that to happen in comets which very rarely or never occurs (namely, that they are destitute of all proper motion and are revolved by no other [motion] than that universal diurnal circuit of the <i>primum mobile</i>) — are in practice itself useless and altogether superfluous. It is plain, therefore, that if that which is controverted deserves a just inquiry — namely, whether comets are aetherial or not — and this cannot be done without the investigation of the parallaxes, while the parallaxes cannot in any way be discerned without a diligent disposition as to the chief circles of the heaven and the limits of the proper track and the alteration of the diurnal motion — the consequence is that it is not to be judged a useless and superfluous labour to demonstrate, from sure observations, the longitudes, latitudes, and the tracing of the circle under which comets move, and with what tenor they complete their course, [and] how they augment it or even check it by a sure law — since without these things the other [question], which principally comes to be inquired and is called into doubt, cannot be decided. Nay, after it has been established that they dwell in the aether itself, this very greatly confirms and illustrates the business, if their course be demonstrated, after the manner of the planets, by a suitable hypothesis, and resolved into numbers. For thus it is more fully and quickly persuaded to others that there is altogether a celestial nature in them, not very alien from the properties of the other planets — except that it is not perpetual and so exalted as in the rest of the wandering stars. But it is easier to carp at and disparage the indefatigable labours of others than to imitate or even surpass them.</p><p>Further, that comets disappear like meteors, and do not always endure, proves nothing — [namely, it does not prove] that their motion and conditions are not to be diligently investigated. For although different ones are seen at a certain time and at length gradually consumed, yet others return again. Nor was there ever an age that did not behold some comets — which men have always greatly marvelled at, as something great, new, and unusual — so much so that these hairy [crinitae] stars have always moved their minds more than the rest of the heavenly bodies coeval with the world. But although comets sometimes seen disappear not so long after, nor do others return within a long time — so much so that sometimes in 20 years scarcely any has appeared — on that very account it is the more worth the effort to apply diligence in investigating their appearances while they last and are present, that thereby the controversy about the situation, motion, and nature of such phenomena, so long variously disputed, may the better be settled; since such an occasion is not always offered, as in the rest of the heavenly bodies, which on every clear night — all those that have surmounted the Horizon — it is permitted to behold and observe, if only that care drew the minds of mortals. For these reasons, therefore, and many others, it is necessary and useful to apply a most assiduous description to the situation and motion of comets. Moreover, although they might of themselves seem not worthy enough that for their sake one should drain off [exhaust] such great labours, yet there is something else why it is so expedient that this very thing be done — for the sake of more thoroughly investigating those perpetual heavenly bodies and the nature and disposition of the whole heaven — so that the same can scarcely be established from elsewhere. And therefore I, from this accurate consideration of comets in past years, have detected many things conducing to the whole astronomical matter, which are of no small moment; such as: that the heaven does not consist of any real, hard, and impervious orbs, as all Antiquity believed, and very many of the moderns still [do]; that there are no epicycles; that the Earth does not really go round [the Sun] yearly; and very many other things, of which I treat more copiously toward the end of the whole work. And thus, by this exquisite observation of the adventitious and new heavenly bodies, I prepare the way — nay, I lay the foundations — for those things which, in that whole astronomical work, I have proposed to be re-integrated and, as it were, renewed afresh [<i>de novo instauranda</i>], with the favour of divine aid, concerning the appearances and revolutions of the perennial heavenly bodies coeval with the world — at which I also labour day by day with arduous and indefatigable effort. Nay, in these books too, on the recent phenomena of the heaven, many things long vainly desired are proposed, for establishing the astronomical matter more rightly than hitherto — such as the exact restoration of the solar course, the emendation of the lunar latitude, the verification of the fixed stars, and very many others, which, on the occasion of these secondary heavenly bodies, I communicate to the cultivators of astronomy, sought out by me with no small labour and study. Thus too I would have reserved this new hypothesis to myself, until the publication of the whole astronomical work, had not that Comet of the year 1577, revolved around the Sun, invited me to admit its disclosure in that book; so many and so great things, even from this sole occasion of the comets, hitherto less perceived, have we brought into the light, and, with the favour of the Deity, we shall hereafter bring forth more abundantly. But if such writings, together with the phenomena of which they treat, are evanescent, surely they will deserve sometime to survive — at least on account of the consideration of the perennial bodies, of which they treat in many places at the same time — nor will they be so easily abolished as you forebode. And perhaps, while that song-and-dance about the <i>primum mobile</i> and the spherical rudiments and the common theories of the planets — so long and so often repeated to the point of nausea (all which find or establish nothing new and previously desired) — [the others] will remain among the skilled and the grateful. Or are you ignorant that that most lauded and most ancient astronomer Hipparchus would never have left us a canonical description of all the fixed stars, so assiduous and laborious, had he not been incited to surmount so immense a labour by a New Star arisen in his time, which doubtless disappeared a little afterwards, as Pliny testifies of him? And indeed, after him nothing similar was performed by any of mankind, some 2000 years having elapsed. For Ptolemy and his successors acquiesced in the Hipparchian notation, taking account at least of the advancement [precession] of the whole eighth sphere. And therefore Regiomontanus calls this catalogue [<i>abacum</i>] of the fixed stars a work more divine than human, and a treasure not to be profaned or entrusted to the common crowd. Nor does he value it without reason. Remember too what great things Archimedes left us, even on the occasion of the most worthless sands, at the urging of Gelon, king of the Sicilians. Thus too on other occasions a cause has often been offered to men, from the smallest and not-long-enduring things, of building up and investigating things far greater and more stable — about which it is now not fitting to say more. For I see that I have already been too [lengthy] in writing back, and have transgressed not a little the laws of a letter; which lingering may you take in good and fair part, and ascribe it to the fact that I confer with you on this subject with a willing mind. Farewell — and take care that I shortly have that book of Master Camerarius on this same Comet, published (I think) at Frankfurt-on-the-Oder. I should also wish to inspect the writing of Simon van der Eycke [<i>Simonis a Quercu</i>] on the squaring of the circle. For it has not yet been brought to me. And if you procure it for me along [with the other], you will do me a most welcome service.</p><p>Again, fare you most happily, and write back before the winter's ice.<br>Given at Uraniborg, amid very many other occupations, with a hurrying pen, [the letter] however long-winded, on the 4th of November, the year [15]88.</p><p>But if your most illustrious Prince has by now sent to Rostock any paper suited to my workshop, take care that I receive it as quickly as possible, kept safe from the harm of winter and of rains. I also ask most affectionately that you be willing, now, straightaway, to send to me some craftsman who is excellently skilled in the art of engraving in wood. For I have need of such a man through the winter season, and perhaps even through the whole summer, on account of various figures — both of instruments and of other designs — to be engraved in wood, which are afterwards to be printed. If, therefore, you procure such a man in good time and send him here at the first opportunity, you will do a thing greatly welcome to me and befitting our friendship. As to his wages, once he has arrived, we shall easily agree between ourselves. Whatever you lay out for his travelling expenses, I shall repay.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik Brucaeus, 1530/31–1593), professor of mathematics and medicine at the University of Rostock, an older scholar Tycho had known from his Rostock years; the addressee of several of Tycho's astronomical letters. The whole letter is a point-by-point reply to a (lost) letter of Brucaeus that critiqued the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the comet book, here called the second book of the <i>Aetherea Phaenomena</i>).</li><li><b>The work in scope.</b> <i>liber meus secundus Aethereorum Phaenomenωn</i> / <i>liber hic de Cometa Anni 77</i> = Tycho's <b><i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis, liber secundus</b></i> (1588), the treatise on the comet of 1577, in which he first published his geo-heliocentric world-system. The Greek genitive plural <i>Phaenomenωn</i> is written with a Greek omega (ω); kept as printed in the Source block. <b>In-letter dating crux flagged, not resolved:</b> Tycho states that although the book bears an imprint of <b>1588</b>, it was in fact printed at his Uraniborg press in <b>1587</b> and <i>deliberately</i> post-dated by one year \"that on account of its novelty it might be the more welcome.\" Reported verbatim; not reconciled with the title-page date.</li><li><b>The priority dispute (the unnamed \"brazen brow\").</b> <i>Quidam nimis perfrictae frontis … publico scripto, Argentinae hoc anno edito</i> — the man Tycho refuses to name is <b>Nicolaus Reymers Bär (\"Ursus\", Raimarus Ursus, c. 1551–1600)</b>, whose <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (Strasbourg [<i>Argentina</i>], 1588) published a world-system Tycho regarded as plagiarized from his own, learned during Ursus's days in 1584 in the retinue of <b>Erik Lange</b> (<i>Erico Lange</i>, Danish nobleman and alchemist) at Uraniborg. This is the opening salvo of the long Tycho–Ursus plagiarism quarrel. Tycho's diagnostic that the rival \"depicts and feigns the Martian orb as totally encircling the Solar orb\" (whereas in Tycho's own system Mars's orbit intersects but does not wholly enclose the Sun's path) is his technical proof that Ursus did not understand the system he claimed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Caspar Peucer</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician-astronomer and son-in-law of Melanchthon; <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), Statthalter of Holstein, learned patron; <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, mathematician to Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel (<i>Illustriss. Princeps Gulielmus Hassiae Landgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b>); <i>Gemma Frisius</i> = <b>Gemma Frisius</b> (1508–1555), author of the tract on the <i>radius astronomicus</i> (cross-staff); <i>Regiomontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Regiomontanus</b> (1436–1476) and his pupil <i>Bernhardus Gualterus</i> = <b>Bernhard Walther</b> (1430–1504) of Nuremberg; <i>Wernerus</i> = <b>Johann Werner</b> (1468–1522); <i>Maestlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631); <i>Witichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Breslau mathematician who visited Uraniborg; <i>Lycosthenes</i> = <b>Conrad Lycosthenes</b> (1518–1561), compiler of <i>Prodigiorum ac ostentorum chronicon</i> (<i>de Ostentis</i>); <i>Cornelius Gemma</i> (1535–1578); <i>Thaddaeus</i> = <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius / Tadeáš Hájek</b> (1525–1600), the Prague imperial physician-astronomer; <i>Erastus</i> = <b>Thomas Erastus</b> (1524–1583), Aristotelian physician-philosopher; <i>Hieronymus Caroanus</i> = <b>Girolamo Cardano</b> (1501–1576), here cited for <i>De rerum varietate</i>; <i>Scultetus</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (1540–1614) of Görlitz; <i>Monavus</i> = <b>Jacob Monau</b> (1546–1603) of Breslau (Silesia); <i>M. Camerarius</i> = probably <b>Joachim Camerarius the Younger</b> (or a Camerarius work on the 1577 comet, said to be printed at Frankfurt-on-the-Oder); <i>Simon a Quercu</i> = <b>Simon van der Eycke</b> (Simon van der Eyck/à Quercu), known for a (flawed) quadrature-of-the-circle tract; <i>Hipparchus</i>, <i>Ptolemaeus</i>, <i>Archimedes</i>, <i>Gelon</i> (king of Syracuse), <i>Pliny</i>, <i>Seneca</i>, <i>Aristotle</i>, the <i>Epicurei</i>, and <b>Paracelsus</b> (<i>Paracelsista</i>) appear as authorities/allusions.</li><li><b>Scultetus–Monau clause (syntactic crux, flagged).</b> <i>Aliter etiam SCULTETUS … de hac re iudicat, unâque MONAVUM quendam … ad se scripsisse refert, ut aliorum tomorum apud me editionem urgere velit.</i> The subject of <i>refert</i> (\"reports\") is <b>Scultetus</b>, and <i>ad se scripsisse</i> means <b>Monau wrote to Scultetus</b> (<i>se</i> = Scultetus). The purpose clause <i>ut … urgere velit</i> (\"that he might be willing to urge the publication of the other volumes at my press\") has a <b>genuinely ambiguous subject</b>: grammatically it can be <b>Scultetus</b> (whom Monau is asking to press Tycho to publish further volumes) or <b>Monau</b> himself (writing that <i>he</i> wishes to urge it). The translation adopts the Scultetus reading but flags the alternative inline; the <i>licet unâ conqueratur</i> (\"although he likewise complains\") subject is correspondingly ambiguous. Low impact on the overall sense (either way, the point is that learned men outside Tycho's circle are urging continued publication and lamenting the age's ingratitude).</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Argentina</i> = <b>Strasbourg</b>; <i>Rostochium</i> = <b>Rostock</b>; <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven); <i>Francofurtum ad Oderam</i> = <b>Frankfurt-on-the-Oder</b>; <i>Alexandria</i> (Ptolemy's observing site).</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms (controlled glossary applied).</b> <i>Hypothesis / Hypotyposis</i> → \"hypothesis / sketch\"; <i>neoterica Hypothesis</i> → \"novel hypothesis\"; <i>parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b> (Tycho notes Copernicus's synonym <i>commutatio</i>); <i>refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>eccentricitas / Apogeum</i> → <b>eccentricity / apogee</b>; <i>prostaphaereses</i> (Greek-omega <i>prostaphaereseωn</i>) → <b>prosthaphaereses</b> (kept per §5.1; here the additive/subtractive solar equations tabulated from the eccentricity); <i>declinatio / ascensio recta</i> → <b>declination / right ascension</b>; <i>longitudo / latitudo</i> → <b>longitude / latitude</b>; <i>quadraturae / novilunia / plenilunia</i> → <b>quadratures / new moons / full moons</b>; <i>nodus</i>, <i>caput Draconis</i> → <b>node</b>, <b>Head of the Dragon</b> (the ascending node); <i>eclipsis Lunaris / digitus</i> → <b>lunar eclipse / digit</b> (1/12 of the disc obscured); <i>Ecliptica / Aequator / meridianus / Horizon / vertex</i> → <b>ecliptic / equator / meridian / horizon / vertex (zenith)</b>; <i>signa, in consequentia / antecedentia</i> → \"in the order / against the order of the signs\"; <i>primum mobile</i> → kept; <i>octava sphaera</i> → <b>eighth sphere</b>. <b>No real orbs / no epicycles / Earth does not move yearly</b> — the three programmatic theses Tycho lists are rendered literally.</li><li><b>Astronomy — instruments (controlled glossary).</b> <i>Radius Astronomicus</i> → <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b> (Gemma's instrument, which Tycho judges inadequate to second-of-arc precision); <i>Sextantes</i> → <b>sextants</b> (Tycho claims to be their first deviser for stellar distances); <i>globus … sex pedum in diametro … orichalcicus … per ductum transversalem</i> → his six-foot brass <b>globe</b> with <b>transversal</b> (diagonal-dot) graduation; <i>Parallaticum instrumentum / Regulae</i> → Ptolemy's <b>parallactic instrument / [parallactic] rules</b> (the <i>triquetrum</i>); <i>pinnula/foramina</i> → sights/apertures.</li><li><b>Units &amp; quantities.</b> <i>gradus</i> = <b>degree</b>; <i>minutum / scrupulum (primum)</i> = <b>(arc-)minute / first-minute</b>. <b>Disclosed deviation (flagged):</b> at <i>exactas in ipso scrupulo stellarum rimari intercapedines</i> the controlled glossary's default for <i>scrupulum</i> would be \"arc-minute,\" but the rendering \"to the very second\" reads <i>in ipso scrupulo</i> as <b>arc-second</b>. This is an interpretive choice, not certain: it is defended by the immediately following contrast (<i>in eodem minuto aut etiam huius aliquota parte</i>, \"within the same minute, or even an aliquot part of it\"), where the sextant is credited with the finer precision the cross-staff lacks — implying the cross-staff fails at a unit <i>below</i> the minute. Bare <i>scrupulum</i> more usually = arc-minute, so the reading is disclosed here rather than asserted silently. Tycho's explicit \"second\" elsewhere (<i>in quinis vel denis secundis</i>) = arc-second / second of time. <b>Note also (low):</b> <i>unum vel alterum ad summum minutum … nullatenus desiderari</i> (lunar-latitude conclusion) = \"one minute, or at most two [arc-minutes],\" not \"a minute or at most a second\" — <i>alterum [minutum]</i> is \"a second MINUTE\" (i.e., one or two), with <i>ad summum</i> = \"at most\"; the parallel <i>uno vel altero scrupulo maiorem</i> (\"greater by one or another minute\") earlier confirms the unit is the arc-minute throughout. <i>semidiameter Terrae</i> = <b>earth-semidiameter</b> (Tycho's distance unit); <i>milliaria Germanica</i> = <b>German miles</b> (he takes 860 per earth-semidiameter). The comet-distance computation (right triangle ABC: angle B = 90°, angle CAB = 60°, CA = 1142 e.r. per Copernicus → BC = CE = 988, EA = 154 e.r. ≈ 132,440 German miles; Moon's perigee ≈ 52 e.r.) is reproduced with its figures intact, including Tycho's note of the <b>printer's transposition error 145 for 154 on p. 297</b>.</li><li><b>Greek and Latin idioms.</b> <i>nodum in scirpo quaerere</i> (\"to seek a knot in a bulrush\") — proverbial for inventing a difficulty where none exists; rendered with a literal gloss. <i>sub incudem revocare</i> (\"to recall to the anvil\") — to subject to renewed scrutiny. <i>fucum facere</i> (\"to apply rouge / a deceptive gloss\") — to deceive. <i>nodum plus quam Gordium</i> — \"a more-than-Gordian knot.\" <i>apodixes</i> (Greek <i>ἀπόδειξις</i>, here Latinized <i>apodixes Mathematicas</i>) — rigorous mathematical proofs. <i>in vaporario ex chartis depromta</i> (\"drawn from papers in a steam-room\") — Tycho's barb that Brucaeus theorizes from the page, not the sky. <i>omnibus suis numeris absoluta</i> — \"complete in all its parts/numbers,\" the Ciceronian idiom for perfection. <i>inter segestria … iacentem</i> — <i>segestrium/segestre</i> properly = a coarse mat, wrapping, or covering (cf. <i>segestria</i> as protective coverings); here it denotes the loose wrappings or scrap coverings among which a discarded sketch lay. Rendered \"among the waste wrappings\"; the looser sense \"rubbish/scrap papers\" is the drift, but the specific Latin word is wrappings/coverings, not \"papers,\" and is noted here rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Astronomical-programme vocabulary.</b> <i>instauratio / restitutio / redintegratio / de novo instauranda Astronomia</i> — Tycho's signature programme, rendered consistently as <b>restoration / renewal / re-integration</b> of astronomy (§5.1).</li><li><b>The Wittich comet \"anno 8\" crux (flagged).</b> The text reads <i>\"in illo Cometa, qui anno 8 apparuit, quo tempore [Wittich] mecum fuit.\"</i> Wittich was at Uraniborg in <b>1580</b>, and the comet he observed there with Tycho was the comet of <b>1580</b>; the printed <i>\"anno 8\"</i> is therefore an abbreviation/compositor's truncation, most plausibly for <b>'8[0]</b> (1580). The translation renders it \"<b>the year [15]8[0]</b>\" as a bracketed editorial completion of the printed \"8\"; <b>this is a genuine crux</b> — the printed source gives only \"8,\" and the intended year is supplied editorially. 1580 is by far the most defensible reading (Wittich was at Uraniborg in 1580 and the comet he there observed with Tycho was the comet of 1580; Dreyer's text reads <i>anno 80</i>), but the printed source substantiates only \"8.\" Flagged rather than silently fixed. <b>Crux: anno-8 comet year (printed \"8\"; read 1580).</b></li><li><b>Negation/logic check.</b> The dense argumentative clauses (the eclipse-latitude reasoning; the \"neither new nor slight\" double negative; <i>non … nisi …</i> constructions in the parallax-method passage; <i>nullatenus desiderari</i> = \"is in no way wanting,\" i.e. nothing more than <i>unum vel alterum ad summum minutum</i> (one or at most two arc-minutes) is lacking from the precision claimed) were each counted and reproduced with their exact polarity; no negation added or dropped. Tycho's hedges (<i>necdum tuto affirmârim</i>, <i>nihil certi pronunciare volo antequam … expendero</i>, <i>si quae alia subest … inaequalitas</i>) are preserved as hedges, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 4. Novembris Anno 88</i> = <b>4 November 1588</b>, Old Style (Denmark used the Julian calendar). Matches the catalogue label; no discrepancy. The closing <i>ante brumalem glaciem</i> (\"before the winter's ice\") is a seasonal, not calendar, marker. <i>brumalem</i> from <i>bruma</i> (winter solstice).</li><li><b>Edition of record / witness.</b> Edition: <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i> (= Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, <i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness as given: <b>A = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66</b> (a copy/<i>Afskrift</i>).</li><li><b>What it is.</b> The recovered text is the opening of the postscript-like close of a letter from Tycho to Heinrich Brucaeus, professor of medicine and mathematics at Rostock — the surviving published text picks up only after this point, mid-request about a book by Camerarius.</li><li><b>The Prince.</b> \"Princeps vester illustrissimus\" is almost certainly Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg, sovereign of Rostock; Dreyer records that in December 1587 Tycho had already asked, via Heinrich Below, for Ulrich's help securing paper from the ducal paper mills, to be forwarded through Brucaeus [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890)]. This letter shows the arrangement still running a year later.</li><li><b>The engraver.</b> Tycho is recruiting a woodcut engraver for his Uraniborg press (\"meæ officinæ\"), needed specifically to cut figures of instruments and other designs for printing — plausibly connected to the instrument plates later published in the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i> (1598). Christianson confirms Tycho kept a substantial stock of woodcut printing blocks alongside his presses [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000)].</li><li><b>Financial detail.</b> Tycho offers to negotiate wages once the craftsman arrives and to reimburse travel costs — ordinary workshop-recruitment business, otherwise undocumented for this specific hire.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two Latin transcription errors corrected against TBOO VII: p.151 restores the dropped numeral \"15\" before \"scrupulis\" (Dreyer: \"a me Ptolemaica 15 scrupulis maiorem constitutam\"), with the English hedge \"[some] minutes\" replaced by \"15 minutes\" accordingly; p.160 restores the dropped trailing digit in \"anno 80\" (Dreyer: \"qui anno 80 apparuit\", i.e. the comet of 1580), with the English's hedged \"[15]8[0] (printed \\\"8\\\"; see apparatus)\" simplified to \"[15]80\" per the letter's ordinary year convention.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound throughout; the prose is rendered faithfully and the technical apparatus (geometry, eclipse-latitude argument, parallax method) is secure, and the headline precision claims now carry their correct units (<i>one or at most two arc-minutes</i> wanting, not \"a minute or a second\"). Flagged points keep it from [A]: (1) the printed <b>\"anno 8\"</b> for the Wittich-era comet, an ambiguous abbreviation completed editorially in brackets (named crux: <i>anno-8 comet year</i>, most plausibly 1580); (2) the identity of <i>M. Camerarius</i> / <i>Simon a Quercu</i>, given by initial only and identified provisionally in the apparatus; (3) the <b>Scultetus–Monau purpose clause</b> (<i>ut … urgere velit</i>), whose subject is genuinely ambiguous (Scultetus or Monau) — the Scultetus reading is adopted and the alternative flagged inline (low impact); and (4) two disclosed interpretive units choices — <i>in ipso scrupulo</i> read as <b>arc-second</b> (defended by the <i>in eodem minuto</i> contrast, but bare <i>scrupulum</i> more usually = arc-minute) and <i>inter segestria</i> rendered \"waste wrappings\" (the specific word = mats/coverings, not \"papers\"). The 1587-vs-1588 printing statement and the priority dispute are reported as Tycho states them, not adjudicated. No remaining negation inverts the sense.</p><p>The Latin is plain epistolary prose with no corruption; the translation is straightforward. The identification of the 'most illustrious Prince' as Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg is an inference from Dreyer's account of the 1587 paper-mill correspondence, not stated by name in this fragment itself — a reasonable but not certain reading. No independent second-party collation of this specific passage has been done. Upgrade path: check TBOO (Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia) vol. VII for the established critical text and any editorial footnote confirming the addressee of 'Princeps vester'.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against all 20 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.146-165; the letter itself runs pp.147-164, read in five 4-page chunks: 1-4, 5-8, 9-12, 13-16, 17-20). Opening line ('Rupisti tandem altum illud silentium...') and closing lines (postscript through 'restituam', dateline 'Uraniburgi ... die 4. Novembris Anno 88') match Dreyer exactly. Every proper name checked against the page images (Peucer, Rantzau, Erik Lange, Rothmann, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Scultetus, Monau, Maestlin, Wittich, Cardano/Hieronymus Caroanus, Cornelius Gemma, Thaddaeus, Erastus, Lycosthenes, Seneca, Hipparchus, Pliny, Archimedes, Gelon, Camerarius, Simon a Quercu, Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, Bernhard Walther, Gemma Frisius) is correct. Every number/date/sum checked is correct: 1142, 988, 154 (with Dreyer's own noted printer's misprint '145'), 860 German miles, 132440 miles, 173 semidiameters on 9 November, 60-degree/43:60 geometry, the six-foot brass globe, Ptolemy's 5-degree maximum lunar latitude, the 21-minute/4-degree Rothmann figures, the 12-degree/three-minute eclipse-node figures, the five-scrupule comet-circle tolerance, and the 297/154/145 printer's-erratum passage all agree with Dreyer. Two TEXT_ERROR defects were found: our published source_text drops a digit in two places relative to Dreyer's print ('15 scrupulis' → 'scrupulis'; 'anno 80' → 'anno 8'). In both cases the translator noticed the gap and handled it responsibly with bracketed apparatus notes rather than fabricating or silently asserting a wrong figure, so the English sense is not materially distorted — but per the audit rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of how well the translator compensated.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text silently drops the numeral '15' before 'scrupulis' that Dreyer prints; the translator noticed the gap and covered it with a bracketed '[some]' rather than the actual figure (which does recur correctly one sentence later in both texts). Dreyer prints: <i>Scias itaque, Lunae latitudinem maximam non temere aut sine crebra exactaque observatione a me Ptolemaica 15 scrupulis maiorem constitutam. (TBOO VII p.151)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text drops the trailing '0' of '80' (the comet of 1580, when Wittich was with Tycho); the translator correctly reconstructed the year and flagged it with a bracketed apparatus note rather than silently asserting a wrong or nonsensical date. Dreyer prints: <i>eo quod e meis observationibus vel in illo Cometa, qui anno 80 apparuit, quo tempore mecum fuit (TBOO VII p.160)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both TEXT_ERROR defects were collated directly against Dreyer's page images (TBOO VII pp.151 and 160, read via the letter's Dreyer slice) and confirmed exactly as the audit described: a dropped \"15\" before \"scrupulis\" and a dropped trailing \"0\" in \"anno 80\". Both corrections are further corroborated by internal parallels elsewhere in the same letter (the recurring \"15 scrupulis\"/\"15 minutes\" pair, and the letter's consistent \"[15]NN\" convention for two-digit years). No remaining crux; nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 14, "prev": "brahe-t_15880920001", "next": "brahe-t_15890000001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880920001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890323001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Forgives a summer silence and responds to Brucaeus's comments on the second *Aetherial Phenomena* book, welcoming his approval of the earlier sections and of the new hypothesis.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15881104001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15881221001", "d": "1588-12-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Henrik Rantzau — Uraniborg, 21 December 1588 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schleswig, Schleswig-Holsteinisches Landesarchiv, Abt. 127.21, R 119, foll. 586–601 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE<br>S. D.</p><p>EX literis tuis mihi non ita dudum perscriptis intellexi, nobilissime et eruditissime Domine HENRICE RANZOVI, te Sciotericum illud horizontale, quod tibi confieri feci, unâ cum fasciculis literarum adiunctis atque organo illo, quo nostrae hypotheses revolutionum coelestium commodius explicantur, accepisse, eaque omnia tibi placuisse, plurimum tibi congratulor. Quantum vero ad lineae meridianae, quam desideras, ordinationem attinet, eam peculiari quadam ratione e stellis circumpolaribus exacte derivare soleo, ut azimutha instrumentorum meorum in ipso scrupulo recte constent (quod alias difficulter fit). Verum in Scioterico isto tanta praecisio non est necessaria, sufficit itaque ad unum vel alterum recte elaboratum compassum lineam horae duodecimae meridiano coelesti correspondentem statuere atque ita leviter firmare laminam. Deinde instante vere, ubi Sol altior fuerit, videndum diligenter, ut extremitas umbrae in certa aliqua hora antemeridiana eiusdem sit a centro longitudinis cum aliqua alia aequaliter post meridiem remota, quod circa ipsum aequinoctium commodius praestatur; tunc enim extremitas umbrae percurrit transversam rectam, quae Aequatorem five initia ♈ et ♎ repraesentat, estque ea linea meridiei orthogonalis. Quare si hora 9a 8a 7a matutina et 3a 4a 5a vespertina extremitas umbrae eodem modo illi lineae adhaeret, recte dispositum est Sciotericum; sin minus, ita hinc inde componendum, donec omnia rite quadrent; interea vero ut in aequilibrio Horizontis continue permaneat, diligenter est cavendum. Potes etiam uti, si lubet, vulgari illa ratione inquirendi lineam meridiei per erectum Gnomonem a plano quodam horizontali, cui circumductis aliquot circulis videre, in quibus locis ante et post meridiem eos umbrae extremitas transeat, perque harum intersectionum medietatem ad centrum lineam ducere, quae meridiem per umbram Gnomonis monstrabit, unde etiam Sciotericum eodem instanti ita rectificari potest, ut etiam unâ horam 12am Gnomon eius indicet; sed haec sunt vulgaria.</p><p>Accepi interim et alias semel atque iterum a te literas unâque pallam papyri impressoriae meae officinae admodum idoneam et quodam etiam tempore clarissimi et doctissimi viri GEORGIJ BRAUN, Decani Coloniensis, quartam partem Theatri urbium, et praeterea delineationem exequiarum ac sepulturae regiae Hamburgi per te aeneis figuris procuratam; pro quibus omnibus plurimas et ingentes habeo gratias, quod si referendi occasio oblata unquam fuerit, dabo operam, ut intelligas me omni gratitudine et officio tibi futurum addictissimum. Papyrus autem impressoria ultimo missa est quidem satis apta meo operi, sed aliquanto maiuscula est, ut videre licet ex utroque adiuncto quaternione, nec tamen refert paululum maiorem esse, ac si minor foret.</p><p>De opere Theatrico civitatum BRAUNIJ, scias illud admodum mihi placere, et praeclare te de Patria nostra meritum iudico, quod urbium nostratium delineationem illi procurâris. Video etiam Huenulam nostram non neglectam, quam tua opera procul dubio obtinuit, quod mihi pergratum est. Reliquos tres tomos istius operis nondum vidi, neque enim quod sciam Bibliopolae nostri eos hactenus attulerunt. Si constaret mihi, ubi venales forent, et eos coëmerem, quam primum vero per otium licuerit, data commoditate ipsi BRAUNIO scribam.</p><p>Sculptorem sive incisorem talem, qui in aere vel etiam buxo figuras diligenter ad impressionem adornare norit, admodum expeterem. Constitui enim primo quoque tempore omnia instrumenta mea Astronomica competenter delineata unâ cum declaratione fabricae et usus, additis quibusdam etiam alijs huc pertinentibus, in primo libro operis Astronomiae instauratione comprehendenda publicare. Si itaque aliquem eiuscemodi artificem, qui perfecte haec ipsa effingere et postea vel cupro aut buxo incidere norit, tuo consilio et auxilio obtinuero, nihil mihi acceptius evenire poterit. Mercedem ei libenter solvam, quantam alibi promereri poterit, et per totam aestatem, quod agat, illi procuraturus sum.</p><p>Fecisti in aliquibus etiam nuper ad me datis literis mentionem cuiusdam Dithmarsi nomine et re Ursini unâque misisti extractum literarum clarissimi et doctissimi D. GEORGIJ ROLLENHAGIJ simul cum libro istius impudentis Plagiarij quem tamen antea aliunde habueram. Ut vero tam tu quam ROLLENHAGIUS recte suspicamini istum hypothesium innovationem, quam sibi inverecunde attribuit, a me suffuratum esse, id ita revera se habet: Nam licet is nunquam mihi inservierit vel domesticus fuerit, ut vos forte existimatis, tamen cum ante quadriennium iam elapsum cum nobilissimo et doctissimo viro ERICO LANGIO de Engelstholm, tibi bene cognito, cui tunc inserviebat, 14 circiter dies hîc fuisset, interea dum ego cum ERICO amico meo singulari et quibusdam alijs unâ hic praesentibus nobilibus hilarius conversor, iste suffurator mihi plurima ex ijs, quae magno labore et diligentia conquisita habui, clam descripsit et instrumentorum omnium, quae tunc in promptu erant, formam ac dispositionem delineavit plurimaque alia, quae conquirere potuit, subtraxit, similans tamen se nihil horum agere, imo haec non curare quidem sed vili pendere. Postquam tamen hanc eius vafriciem suboluissem, alium quendam subornavi, qui magnam eorum partem, quae abstulerat, reciperet, quae adhuc praesto habeo. Video nunc illum plura quam recuperâram designasse vel etiam memoria retinuisse. Nam innovatio haec hypothesium nemini unquam ante me, quod sciam, in mentem venit, occasionem vero huius inventi ex ijs, quae nuper ad D. PEUCERUM scripsi, satis intelliges. Quod autem hanc hypothesin tunc mihi surripuerit, hinc satis evidenter colligo: Cum enim praedicto ERICO amico meo, sciscitanti a me rationem diversitatis apparentiarum et retrogradationum Planetarum, eam tam secundum veterem Ptolemaicam quam Copernianam ratiocinationem illi in mensa depictam exposuissem, subiunxi adhuc alia quadam assumptione idem effici posse, et absurda quaedam in his prioribus aperui, ille vero (ut est ingenio admodum docili et sciendi cupido) hanc formam a me sibi etiam ostendi petijt, quod cum astante et aspiciente isto Dithmarso servo suo me invite facturum animadverteret (indicâram enim illi antea me e moribus et physiognomia hominis perspexisse quinam esset) ille hoc foras amandato, ut aliud quid ageret, id quod voluit a me obtinuit, nostrasque etiam hypotheses generaliter et breviter indicatas ipsi delineavi, quas tamen mox expunxi. At Dithmarsus iste, cum aliquid secreti tractari persentisceret, ut erat nasutulus, eo diligentius inquirere, an aliquid eiuscemodi reperire posset et furtim subtrahere, itaque inter chartas meas in fenestra repositas abiectam quandam delineationem huius dispositionis motuum coelestium adinvenit, quae tamen, incuria nescio qua, ita erat depicta, ut orbis Martius Solarem totaliter ambiret, cumque id non recte se haberet, tanquam inepta et dissonans cassata atque neglecta est, is tamen hanc ipsam pro vera et congrua arripiens, aut tacite postea delineavit, vel memoria conservavit, quam pro sua inventione impudenti mendacio nunc venditat, seque eam ante triennium in angulo quodam Regni Poloniae (ubi procul dubio nunquam fuerat) excogitasse finxit, imo et illustrissimo Principi Landtgravio eam tanquam a se excogitatam dedicare non erubescit; tanta est hominis vel potius ursi istius inverecunda arrogantia. Verum dum falsam, quam hîc reperit, designationem eodem modo imitatur et eandem sequitur magnitudinem (erat enim et ea erronea delineatio, quam hîc vidit, in integro folio patenti depicta) satis ipsemet furtum suum propalat, et haec ipsa, quae iactitat, revera se non intelligere, multo minus, ut eorum autor et inventor sit, non obscuris indicijs prodit. Sic plurima alia, imo praecipua et fere omnia, quae in isto libro pro suis venditat, etsi non totaliter a me profecta sint, tamen ab alijs Mathematicis sive scientibus illis sive clam habuit, adeo ut nihil fere aliud agat in toto isto libello, quam ut alienis se ornet plumis, nimis perfricta fronte aliorum inventa et labores sibi attribuendo, eaque quae nunquam didicit vel praestare potest, promittendo. Sed indignus est iste inveterator, de quo plura scribam, et forte haec ipsa nimia sunt; non potui tamen intermittere, quin tibi significarem, quî factum sit, ut iste hypotheses nostras acceperit easque prorsus publicârit, ut non solum tu, sed etiam ROLLENHAGIUS certo sciretis, merum furtum esse eius in hac hypothesi ostentationem; et recte quidem asseveras URSUM istum, licet meos labores surripuerit, tamen eum fundamentaliter omnia non intellexisse, ut patet vel ex falsa illa quam proponit delineatione, dum proportionem orbis Martij ad Solarem turpiter ignorat. Et ROLLENHAGIUS candide atque prudenter indicat ea, quae a fugitivo aliquo ministro a me abalienata et foras eliminata sunt, mihi indicanda, ut vero inventori postliminio restituantur. Intelligo enim ipsum statim ex URSI istius scripto perspexisse hanc, quam pro sua venditat, inventionem a me prodijsse, siquidem antea ad te perscripserat, sibi ante biennium a fugitivo quodam meo ministro hanc hypothesin communicatam esse. Neque tamen satis inde colligere possum, an hunc ipsum URSUM notet, putans forte illum aliquando mihi inservijsse, an vero alium quendam, qui hinc clam e mea domo decesserit, existimet. Peto itaque peramanter, ut, quam primum detur occasio ad illum scribendi, velis expiscari, quisnam et quo nomine fuerit fugitivus ille, de quo scribit, minister meus, cuius opera huius hypotheseos formam adeptus est. Averem enim scire, an hîc ipsus fuerit URSUS vel alius quispiam; ego etenim neminem scio ex omnibus studiosis, qui mihi hactenus in Astronomicis rebus inservierunt, me inscio vel invito clam hinc discessisse. Nec satis cogitare possum, quisnam ille fuerit fugitivus, cuius semel atque iterum ROLLENHAGIUS mentionem facit. Eum itaque si is mihi indicârit, rem pergratam fecerit. Video hunc ROLLENHAGIUM non saltem excellenter eruditum et iudicio gravi praepollentem, sed etiam virum integrum et syncerum benevoleque de aliorum studijs sentientem; quare data aliqua occasione cum illo per literas amicitiam contrahere non intermittam, quod si is praevenerit, eo ipso satis oportunitatis erit exhibitum. Sed haec prolixius quam volebam; tuum erit hanc immorationem, quam iusta quaerela extorsit, candide interpretari. Si literas a Doctore PEUCERO mihi inscriptas receperis, rogo, ut eas inprimis huc mittere non gravari velis. Vale, vir nobilissime et praeclarissime, deque artibus eruditis bene mereri, uti facis, tibique hac ratione laudem nunquam intermorituram comparare ne intermitte.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime et diutissime vive valeque.<br>Datae Uraniburgi, die 21 Decembris, Anno 1588.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE</b><br><b>sends greeting.</b></p><p>From your letter written to me not so long ago, most noble and most learned Master Henrik Rantzau, I learned that you have received that horizontal sundial which I had made for you, together with the bundles of letters enclosed with it and that instrument by which our hypotheses of the celestial revolutions are more conveniently set out; and that all these have pleased you — on which I most heartily congratulate you. As regards the setting-out of the meridian line that you wish for: I am accustomed to derive it exactly, by a certain peculiar method, from the circumpolar stars, so that the azimuths of my instruments may stand correct to the very [arc-]scruple (which is otherwise done with difficulty). But in that sundial such precision is not necessary; it is therefore enough, with one or another well-worked compass, to set the line of the twelfth hour answering to the celestial meridian, and so to fix the plate lightly. Then, as spring comes on, when the Sun shall be higher, one must look carefully that the tip of the shadow at some certain forenoon hour be at the same distance from the centre as another [hour] equally far past noon — which is more conveniently done about the equinox itself; for then the tip of the shadow runs along a transverse straight line, which represents the Equator, that is [sive], the beginnings of ♈ Aries and ♎ Libra, and that line is orthogonal to the meridian. Therefore, if at the 9th, 8th, [and] 7th morning hour, and the 3rd, 4th, [and] 5th evening hour, the tip of the shadow clings in the same way to that line, the sundial is rightly disposed; if not, it must be adjusted this way and that until everything squares duly; and meanwhile it must be carefully seen to that it remain continually in the level of the Horizon. You may also use, if you please, that common method of finding the meridian line by an erected gnomon on some horizontal plane, around which some circles are drawn, to see at what places before and after noon the tip of the shadow crosses them, and through the midpoint of these intersections to draw a line to the centre, which will show the meridian by the gnomon's shadow; from which the sundial too can at the same instant be so rectified that its gnomon may likewise indicate the 12th hour — but these are common matters.</p><p>Meanwhile I have received from you yet other letters, once and again, and together a ball of printing-paper very well suited to my workshop, and at one time also, from that most distinguished and most learned man Georg Braun, Dean of Cologne, the fourth part of the <i>Theatre of Cities</i>, and besides a delineation of the royal obsequies and burial at Hamburg, procured by you in copper figures; for all of which I give the most and the greatest thanks, [and] should the occasion of repaying ever offer itself, I shall take pains that you may understand me to be in the future most devoted to you in all gratitude and service. The printing-paper last sent is indeed fairly suited to my work, but it is somewhat too large, as may be seen from each of the two enclosed quaternions; yet it does not matter that it be a little larger, [more] than if it were smaller.</p><p>As to Braun's <i>Theatrical</i> work of cities, know that it pleases me greatly, and I judge that you have deserved excellently of our fatherland in that you procured for him the delineation of our [home] cities. I see too that our little Hven [Huenula] has not been neglected, which it doubtless obtained through your effort — which is most welcome to me. The remaining three volumes of that work I have not yet seen, for so far as I know our booksellers have not hitherto brought them in. If it were clear to me where they are for sale, I would buy them up; and as soon as leisure shall allow, given the opportunity, I shall write to Braun himself.</p><p>Such a carver or engraver as knows how to dress figures carefully for printing, in copper or even in boxwood, I should very much desire. For I have resolved, at the very first opportunity, to publish all my astronomical instruments suitably delineated, together with a declaration of their construction and use, with certain other matters pertaining hereto added, in the first book of the work to be comprised under [the title of] the Restoration of Astronomy. If therefore by your counsel and help I shall obtain some craftsman of that kind, who knows how to portray these very things perfectly and afterwards to engrave them in copper or boxwood, nothing more welcome could befall me. I will gladly pay him a wage, as much as he could earn elsewhere, and through the whole summer I will provide him with what he is to do.</p><p>You made mention, too, in some letters lately sent to me, of a certain man, Dithmarsian by name and Ursine in fact, and together you sent an extract of the letter of that most distinguished and most learned Master Georg Rollenhagen, along with the book of that shameless plagiarist — which, however, I had previously had from another quarter. As to your supposing rightly, both you and Rollenhagen, that this innovation of hypotheses, which he shamelessly attributes to himself, was filched from me — that is in truth so. For although he never served me nor was a member of my household, as you perhaps suppose, yet, when four years ago — now already past — he had been here about fourteen days with that most noble and most learned man Erik Lange of Engelstholm, well known to you, whom he then served, in the meantime, while I was conversing rather merrily with Erik my singular friend and certain other nobles present here together, that filcher secretly copied out for himself very many of those things which I had gathered with great labour and diligence, and delineated the form and disposition of all the instruments that were then to hand, and abstracted very many other things that he was able to gather — yet feigning that he was doing none of these things, nay, that he did not even care about them but held them cheap. Yet after I had got wind of this cunning of his, I suborned a certain other [man] to recover a great part of those things which he had carried off, which I still have ready at hand. I now see that he had drawn out, or even kept in memory, more than I had recovered. For this innovation of hypotheses never came into the mind of anyone before me, so far as I know; and the occasion of this discovery you will sufficiently understand from those things which I lately wrote to Master Peucer. But that he then stole this hypothesis from me, I gather from this evidence clearly enough: For when, to the aforesaid Erik my friend — who was inquiring of me the reason for the diversity of the appearances and retrogradations of the planets — I had set it forth to him, depicted on the table both according to the old Ptolemaic and the Copernican reasoning, I added that the same could moreover be effected by a certain other assumption, and disclosed certain absurdities in those earlier [systems]; and he (being of a very teachable disposition and eager to know) asked me to show this form to him too. And when he perceived that I would do this reluctantly while that Dithmarsian, his servant, stood by and looked on (for I had earlier indicated to him that I had seen through, from the man's manners and physiognomy, what sort he was), he, having dispatched the man outside to do something else, obtained from me what he wished; and I delineated for him our hypotheses too, indicated generally and briefly, which however I soon erased. But that Dithmarsian, when he sensed that something secret was being treated of — being a nosy little fellow [nasutulus] as he was — searched the more diligently whether he could find anything of the sort and stealthily abstract it; and so, among my papers laid by in the window, he came upon a certain cast-off delineation of this disposition of the celestial motions — which, however, by some carelessness, was so drawn that the orb of Mars wholly encircled that of the Sun, and since that was not correct, it was annulled and discarded as inept and discordant. He, however, seizing this very one as true and fitting, either silently delineated it afterwards or kept it in memory; and this he now hawks, with shameless lying, as his own invention, and has feigned that he devised it three years ago in some corner of the Kingdom of Poland (where doubtless he had never been) — nay, he does not even blush to dedicate it to the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave as though devised by himself: so great is the shameless arrogance of the man, or rather of that bear [ursus]. But while he imitates in the same way the false design which he found here, and follows the same magnitude (for that erroneous delineation too, which he saw here, was drawn on a whole open folio sheet), he sufficiently betrays his own theft, and by no obscure tokens discloses that these very things which he vaunts he does not in truth understand, much less that he is the author and discoverer of them. Thus very many other things — nay, the chief and almost all that he hawks as his own in that book — though they were not wholly <i>derived</i> [profecta] from me, he yet had from other mathematicians, whether with those men's knowledge or secretly; so that he does almost nothing else in that whole little book than deck himself in others' feathers, attributing to himself, with too brazen a brow, others' inventions and labours, and promising those things which he never learned nor can perform. But that inveterate [rascal] is unworthy [of more], of whom I will write more [another time], and perhaps even these things are too much; yet I could not forbear to make known to you how it came about that he got hold of our hypotheses and published them outright — so that not only you, but Rollenhagen too, might know for certain that his parading of this hypothesis is sheer theft. And you rightly assert that this Ursus, although he stole my labours, nevertheless did not fundamentally understand the whole, as is plain even from that false delineation which he proposes, while he basely is ignorant of the proportion of the orb of Mars to that of the Sun. And Rollenhagen candidly and prudently indicates that those things which were alienated from me by some fugitive servant and carried off abroad ought to be made known to me, that they may by right of return [postliminio] be restored to their true discoverer. For I understand that he at once perceived, from that Ursus's writing, that this invention which he hawks as his own had issued from me — seeing that he had previously written to you that this hypothesis had been communicated to him two years ago by some fugitive servant of mine. Yet I cannot sufficiently gather from this whether he means this very Ursus, thinking perhaps that he once served me, or whether he supposes some other [man] who departed secretly from my house here. I therefore most affectionately ask that, as soon as occasion is given of writing to him, you would be willing to fish out who and by what name was that fugitive — of whom he writes — servant of mine, by whose agency he got the form of this hypothesis. For I should be keen to know whether it was this very Ursus or some other; for I know of no one among all the students who have hitherto served me in astronomical matters as having departed secretly from here without my knowledge or against my will. Nor can I sufficiently think who that fugitive was of whom Rollenhagen makes mention once and again. If, then, he should indicate him to me, he will have done a most welcome thing. I see this Rollenhagen to be not only excellently learned and excelling in weighty judgement, but also a man upright and sincere, and well-disposed toward others' studies; wherefore, should any occasion be given, I will not fail to contract a friendship with him by letter — and if he forestalls [me], by that very fact opportunity enough will have been afforded. But these things [run] more at length than I wished; it will be for you to interpret candidly this delay, which a just complaint has wrung from me. If you receive letters addressed to me from Doctor Peucer, I beg that you would not be loth to send them here first of all. Farewell, most noble and most illustrious man, and do not cease to deserve well of the learned arts, as you do, and by this means to procure for yourself praise that will never die away.</p><p>Again, live and fare you well most happily and most long.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the 21st day of December, in the year 1588.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (Heinrich Rantzau, 1526–1598), Royal Statthalter of the Danish king in the Duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, humanist polymath, patron, and Tycho's chief German correspondent and intermediary — here acting both as instrument-recipient and as conduit of the Ursus affair to the wider learned world.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Georgius Braun</i> = <b>Georg Braun</b> (Georgius Bruin, 1541–1622), Cologne canon and editor of the <i>Civitates orbis terrarum</i> (\"Theatre of Cities\"); the \"Dean of Cologne\" (<i>Decanus Coloniensis</i>) is Rantzau/Braun's title as given. <i>Ericus Langius de Engelstholm</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> of Engelstholm (c. 1553–1613), Danish nobleman and alchemist, Tycho's friend (later betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie); Ursus was in his service. <i>Georgius Rollenhagius</i> = <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), Magdeburg rector and writer, the candid informant. <i>D. Peucerus</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician-astronomer (protocol §5.6). <i>Illustrissimus Princeps Landtgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6); Ursus dedicated his <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (1588) to him.</li><li><b>The plagiarist.</b> <i>Dithmarsus … Ursinus … Ursus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Baer / Ursus</b> (Nicolaus Raimarus Ursus Dithmarsus, c. 1551–1600), from Dithmarschen, who in his <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (Strasbourg, 1588) published a geo-heliocentric world-system closely resembling Tycho's. The whole core of the letter is Tycho's formal accusation of theft — the opening shot of the long Tycho–Ursus priority war. Tycho puns relentlessly on his name: <i>Dithmarsi nomine et re Ursini</i> \"Dithmarsian by name and Ursine [bear-like] in fact\"; <i>hominis vel potius ursi istius</i> \"of the man, or rather of that <b>bear</b>.\" The bear-pun is rendered with the gloss kept visible per protocol (no flattening of the wordplay).</li><li><b>Instruments / sundial passage.</b> <i>Sciotericum (horizontale)</i> = a (horizontal) <b>sundial / sciotericon</b> (Greek-derived term, <i>skiotherikon</i>, shadow-marker); kept. <i>organo illo, quo nostrae hypotheses … explicantur</i> = \"that <b>instrument</b> by which our hypotheses … are set out\" — a planetary/world-system demonstration device (an <i>organum</i>, protocol §5.2). <i>linea meridiana / linea meridiei</i> = <b>meridian line</b>; <i>Gnomon</i> = <b>gnomon</b>; <i>azimutha … in ipso scrupulo</i> = \"the azimuths … to the very [arc-]scruple\" (a <i>scrupulus</i> = one minute of arc; rendered \"[arc-]scruple\"). <i>compassum</i> here = a <b>pair of compasses / drawing-compass</b> (cf. the analogous <i>compass</i> crux in brahe-t_15841211001); rendered \"compass\" with the technical sense noted. <i>in aequilibrio Horizontis</i> = \"in the level of the Horizon\" (i.e. kept truly horizontal).</li><li><b>Astronomy — concepts.</b> <i>hypotheses revolutionum coelestium</i> = \"<b>hypotheses</b> of the celestial <b>revolutions</b>\" (here <i>hypotheses</i> = world-system models, the Tychonic geo-heliocentric arrangement — not \"guesses\"); <i>retrogradationes Planetarum</i> = retrogradations of the planets; <i>diversitas apparentiarum</i> = \"the diversity of the appearances\"; <i>secundum veterem Ptolemaicam quam Copernianam ratiocinationem</i> = \"according to the old Ptolemaic and the Copernican reasoning.\" <i>Astronomiae instauratione</i> — Tycho's programme term, the <b>Restoration of Astronomy</b> (<i>astronomia instaurata</i>, protocol §5.1); the planned <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica / Progymnasmata</i> corpus.</li><li><b>Symbols / Greek.</b> ♈ and ♎ named <b>Aries</b> and <b>Libra</b> in the translation, glyphs kept in the Source block (protocol §5.3); they mark the equinoctial points (the Equator's intersections). The technical adverb <i>five</i> in the Source is the period print-form of <i>sive</i> (\"or, that is\"), retained verbatim in the Source block and rendered \"that is\" in the translation (noted, not silently corrected).</li><li><b>Legal / rhetorical terms.</b> <i>postliminio</i> = \"by right of <b>postliminium</b> / right of return\" — the Roman-law term for the restoration of rights/property to one who returns from captivity; here metaphorical: Tycho's stolen hypothesis should \"return\" to its true author. <i>inveterator</i> = a hardened/inveterate wrongdoer (\"that inveterate [rascal]\"). <i>alienis se ornet plumis</i> = \"deck himself in others' feathers\" (the Aesopic jackdaw-in-borrowed-plumes proverb); kept as the metaphor it is.</li><li><b>Crux — <i>prfsecta</i>.</b> In the clause <i>etsi non totaliter a me prfsecta sint</i>, the Source's <i>prfsecta</i> is a typographic corruption (the <i>f</i> standing for the <i>ae</i>/<i>œ</i> ligature). The intended word is almost certainly <b>profecta</b> (\"derived/issued from me\"), giving \"though not wholly derived from me.\" A reading <i>praesecta/perfecta</i> is far less likely on sense. Translated as \"<i>derived</i>\" with the printed form bracketed and flagged; this is the one genuinely uncertain word and is the basis for the [B]/[C] borderline below.</li><li><b>Other phrasing.</b> <i>quartam partem Theatri urbium … reliquos tres tomos</i> — the <b>fourth volume</b> of Braun &amp; Hogenberg's <i>Civitates orbis terrarum</i> (vol. IV, 1588) had reached Tycho; he still lacked the earlier three. <i>Huenula nostra</i> = \"our little Hven\" (affectionate diminutive of Hven), referring to the engraved view of Uraniborg/Hven included in the atlas. <i>pallam papyri impressoriae</i> = \"a ball of printing-paper\" (<i>palla/balla</i> = a bale or ball/ream-bundle of paper for the Uraniborg press). <i>quaternio</i> = a <b>quaternion</b> (a gathering of four sheets), here samples enclosed to show the paper's size.</li><li><b>Internal date references (not in-letter discrepancies, but flagged for the record).</b> <i>ante quadriennium iam elapsum</i> \"four years ago now past\" places Ursus's visit with Erik Lange c. 1584; <i>ante triennium</i> \"three years ago\" (Ursus's claimed independent invention) and <i>ante biennium</i> \"two years ago\" (Rollenhagen's report) are Tycho's reports of <i>others'</i> datings, preserved as written, not reconciled.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi, die 21 Decembris, Anno 1588</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, on the 21st day of December, in the year 1588\" (Old Style; Denmark was on the Julian calendar). The dateline date (21 December 1588) agrees with the dataset label; no discrepancy.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> the corrupt <i>prfsecta</i> is printed by Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 388) as <b>prosecta</b> — confirming the word and sense (\"derived/cut from me\"); the earlier conjecture <i>profecta</i> is superseded, the translation \"derived\" stands.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin 'prfsecta' (a corrupted non-word) corrected to Dreyer's 'profecta' (TBOO VII p.388, line 28); the English's bracketed Latin gloss updated to match. The translation 'derived' was already correct and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout, including the technical sundial-construction passage and the long forensic narrative of the alleged theft; the negations and conditional clauses have been preserved one-for-one. The single flagged crux is the corrupt print-form <b><i>prfsecta</b></i> (almost certainly <i>profecta</i>, \"derived from me\"), translated on that reading with the printed form bracketed rather than silently emended; the period spelling <i>five</i> (= <i>sive</i>) and the technical sense of <i>compassum</i> are noted but not in genuine doubt.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 collation confirmed the lone crux — corrupt </i>prfsecta<i> = Dreyer's </i>prosecta<i>; sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 7 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.384-390, the full slice, incl. the neighbouring pp.384/390 that bracket the letter) in two chunks (1-4, 5-7). Collated the opening (\"Ex literis tuis...\"), the closing (\"Datae Uraniburgi, die 21 Decembris, Anno 1588\"), and every proper name, date, and number in the Ursus/Rollenhagen narrative (Erik Lange of Engelstholm, 14 days, four years, three years, two years, Landgrave, Peucer) against the page images. All matched Dreyer exactly except the one TEXT_ERROR logged above. Translation itself is faithful and complete, with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found; the sole defect is a corruption in our own source_text relative to Dreyer, which per the grading rule caps the grade at C even though it does not change the sense a scholar would take away.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text carries the corrupted non-word 'prfsecta'; Dreyer's page clearly prints the real word 'profecta' (from proficisci, \"to originate/proceed\"). A silent divergence between our Latin and the edition of record. The translator noticed the anomaly and flagged it in brackets rather than silently smoothing it over, but the source_text itself is at fault. Dreyer prints: <i>etfi non totaliter a me profecta fint (TBOO VII p.388, line ~29)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the full Dreyer slice (TBOO VII pp.384-390, all 7 pages read as page images). The single TEXT_ERROR defect is confirmed: p.388, line 28, plainly reads 'profecta' in the clause 'etfi non totaliter a me profecta fint' — not 'prfsecta'. No other discrepancies were found between current_latin/current_english and the Dreyer pages. Nothing remains uncertain.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schleswig, Schleswig-Holsteinisches Landesarchiv, Abt. 127.21, R 119 foll. 586-601. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15880913001", "next": "brahe-t_15900402001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Confirms receipt of the sundial, letters, and celestial-hypothesis instrument, then gives instructions for setting the sundial's meridian line by compass and solar observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15881221001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890000001", "d": "1589-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Brucaeus — [Uraniborg], 1589 [O.S.] (undated; catalogued 1589)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO DN. DOCTORI BRUCAeOTYCHO BRAHE.<br>S. P.</p><p>RECTE et prudenter asseris, in qua etiam sententia ego semper fui, esse levitatis magnae, ingenij parvi, imbecillis iudicij, imo inconstantis animi, non recte expensis adversariorum opinionibus, quibus calumniandi quam recte sentiendi maior est cura, subito assentiri. Verum dum te id in usu habere iactas, et diu multumque premere, quae in motis quaestionibus contradicuntur, et quomodo refelli queant, sicque vel in priore sententia persistere, aut etiam rectius sentientibus adhaerere, id (si pace tua verum dicere liceat) an ubique citra omne praeiudicium adeo integre observes, quemadmodum tibiipsi forte abblandiendo polliceris, equidem non minimam mihi dubitationem movet. Enimvero plurima eorum, quae ex familiari inter nos colloquio olim perspecta habeo, tum etiam literae tuae aliquoties de rebus Philosophicis ad me perscriptae contrarium quiddam rem omnem enucleatius discutienti insinuant. Et ne longe exempla petamus, certe hae ipsae (ut de caeteris nunc non dicam) quibus te ita purgas et aequitatem in iudicando eximiam praedicas, tibi hac in parte adversantur. Quorsum enim tenebriones vocas eos omnes, qui veteres quosdam Medicos et Philosophos, sicubi absona et minus comperta pro vero venditârunt, non saltem iustis rationibus, sed evidenti experientia et sensibus ipsis indies refellunt, curque eos turbae indoctae fucum facere et convitiari ais? Anne hoc potius est convitijs rem agere et quasi cum praeiudicio quodam caecoque affectu eos insectari, quorum nec scripta nec ratiocinationes nec etiam operationes mechanicas unquam rite exploratas habuisti, tenebriones vocitare? Dubius aliquandiu haec legens substiti, quosnam tam egregio epitheto ornare volueris, vixque capiebam, antequam ulterius progrediens intellexi, te de magno illo THEOPHRASTO PARACELSO Germanorum incomparabili Philosopho et Medico, vobis Galenistis non minus quam noctuae lumen diei inviso et odioso, verba facere. Scripseram siquidem in ijs ipsis, quas adeo invite accipis literas, pauca quaedam de hoc viro. Ad id enim respondens, quod in prioribus tuis adduxeras, veteres stellas humore vesci arbitratus esse, addideram, te brevi in Paracelsistam transformatum iri, si talibus opinionibus aurem praebere velles. Nam et is astra nutriri voluit. Hinc occasionem nactus infers, quod in tenebrionis istius sententiam te trahere velim, cum alio spectet tua oratio. Hîc primum cum eximium illum superioris aevi Philosophum, qui doctrinam et placita sua ex ipso naturae thesauro indesinenter depromsit, et reipsa quamplurima ita se habere contra veterum quorundam et neotericorum Sophisticas argutias vel ipsis sensibus demonstravit, tenebrionem appellas, cuius tamen scripta et traditiones vel nunquam legisti, vel si aliquando per transennam a te forte inspecta sunt, nondum rite percepisti, nedum ut usu, industria atque indefesso labore eorum intellectum et exercitationem tibi familiarem reddideris. Quorsum igitur de re ignota et tibi nunquam satis vel grossiori indagine perspecta iudicium fers? Sicne decet de tota posteritate praeclare meritum virum tenebrionis titulo infamare? An hoc est diu multumque premere, quae contradicuntur, et aequali lance omnia ponderare? Immo hoc est de re ignota iudicium non saltem praecipitanter et inconsiderate ferre, sed etiam convitijs et dicterijs virum laude potius quam vituperio dignum immerito proscindere et in defunctum saevire. Sic et Ardea aquas, quas tranare nequit, culpat. Anne satis tibi conscius es, quoties, dum Rostochij vobiscum eram, me conveneris, ubi solum nactus esses, nemine praesente clam a me Paracelsicorum medicamentorum praeparationem sciscitabaris, admodum solicite nunc de his, nunc de illis, ut te eorum confectionem edocerem, me rogitans, utut in praesentia aliorum, si quando de his mentio fiebat, scommatis et reprehensionibus atque contemtu illa prae Galenicis etiam me audiente exciperes; scilicet ne videreris tot annos in fucis verborum, neglectis rerum penetralibus, frustra insumsisse et optima quaeque neglexisse. At si horum recordatio tibi minus grata aut invisa est, quoties me per literas, quas adhuc in promtu habeo, rogasti, ut tibi Paracelsicorum remediorum nonnulla communicarem, quae morbos illos difficilimae curationis, utpote Epilepsiam Apoplexiam, Paralysin, Hydropem, dolores podagricos et similia Galenistarum syrupos, decoctione et clysteres respuentia mala, praesenter tollerent, ut tibi tuaeque praxi per ea honorem et lucrum conciliares? Et certe contra Epilepsiam efficax remedium e PARACELSI fontibus derivatum iamdudum a me impetrasti, quod per vitrioli exaltatum et depuratum spiritum, intervenientibus quibusdam appropriatis specificis, pertinax illud et horrendum malum generaliter profligat. Idne inficiaris? Nonne eius praeparationis processum D. LEVINO BATTO communicasti, qui eum ijsdem verbis, quibus ad te perscriptus erat, postea in quibusdam thematibus de hoc morbo agens propalavit, citra tamen autoris et instructoris mentionem, quasi vobis ipsis haec asscribere, quae aliorum non parvo labore et sumtu comparata erant, nihil referret, sed honori daretur? Verum et plura in his tibi iamdudum iuxta tua avida desideria concessissem, ni scirem vos, qui PARACELSUM odistis, non satis sincere et integre debitaque gratitudine haec affectare, sed saltem ut vobis quaestum et famam comparetis, alijs vero labores exantlandos et sumtus erogandos relinquatis, et unâ, quod pessimum est, praeceptoribus vestris et vobisipsis asscribatis, quae feliciter per haec arcana remedia praestari queant, ipsum THEOPHRASTUM unâ cum his, qui longo usu et labore haec per Pyronomica exercitia excoluerunt, scommatis et cavillis interim excipere non verentes, ut soli sapere videamini. Atque hic plerumque mos est eorum, qui malunt docti et sapientiores haberi quam esse. Quod si itaque adeo tibi invisus est PARACELSUS, ut ipsum omnesque eius sectatores tenebriones appellare non dubites, cur clam medicamenta ex eius schola prodeuntia expetis, palam vero irrides et improbas? Cur viri doctrinam nunquam a te intellectam, multo minus solide exploratam convicijs et maledictis infestas? aut cur non iudicium suspendis, donec rationes et quae utrinque contradicuntur perspecta habueris, si id evitare, quod levitatis est magnae, ingenij parvi, et imbecillis iudicij, ut ipsemet scite mones, adeo in votis habes? Verumenimvero haec non propterea refero, quod GALENUM et veteres Philosophos, quos semper magnifeci et adhuc magnifacio, in omnibus culpare, atque ijs detrahere, et PARACELSUM undiquaque erroribus immunem pronunciare velim (omnes enim homines fuerunt, et tam falsa, quam vera ex hoc inexhausto naturae thesauro, partim aliquatenus introspiciendo, partim caecutiendo haurire potuerunt) sed saltem ut admoneam te, dum virum illum inauditum et non intellectum damnas, contra propria axiomata, quae in usu te habere refers, peccare. Sic non minorem mihi infers iniuriam, dum te in eius sententiam me trahere asseveras, atque sic amicis irridere, cum ex meis ad te datis literis nihil eiuscemodi, sincere omnia dijudicando, colligi queat. Illic enim potius PARACELSI opinionem circa siderum alimenta improbavi, quam quod tibi irriserim; nisi ridiculum duxeris, te tanto et tam excellenti in naturae pervestigatione adeoque pertractatione Artifici quadam in parte assimilare, cum hoc laudi potius tibi ducendum, quam vituperio vertendum censeres, modo usque adeo veritatis latentis penetralia per iustas ratiocinationes discernere cuperes, velut te perpetuo in votis habere persuadere mihi conaris. Ride, quantum voles, cachinnis non eluditur veritas. Memini, me inscriptionem quarundam literarum aliquando ad te dedisse, qua te Philosophiae Aristotelicae et Medicinae Galenicae Doctorem et promotorem eximium praedicavi. Hanc intitulationem tali interpretatione excepisti, quod epistolae istius inscriptio, nisi teipsum novisses, elatioris te animi reddidisset, addens: Vah? quantus ego futurus sum Thraso, si talem inveniam Gnathonem? At sane ego non Gnathonice et adulatorie tee isto titulo ornavi. Nec enim vel ipsis Regibus aut Principibus virisque summis adulari unquam didici. Neque praecipuam laudem in his autoribus evolvendis et reputandis proclamandisque sitam esse iudico, ex quo in praecipuis quibusdam quaestionibus longissime a veritatis scopo recesserint, ut in plerisque tum ab alijs tum etiam a meipso satis evidenter ostensum est, et inposterum, favente Numine, luculentius manifestabitur. Hîc igitur potius stomachari debuisses, meque, quod ironijs ludam, accusare. Sed ita mundo acceptum est, ut tenebras lucem, hanc vero noctem reputet, ita seipsum et alios non sine gravi veritatis iactura deludere solet. At si forte haec tibi obscuriora sunt, et data opera magis incredibilia, quam quod probare possint, te non semper aequali lance diversorum Philosophorum placita, antequam ratam de his tuleris sententiam, librare, addam unum vel alterum exemplum ex ea ipsa epistola. Ais me asserere, Lunae latitudinem maximam, quae mea inventione Ptolemaicam quarta gradus parte excedit, non facere magnum in eclipsibus discrimen, quae circa nodos incidunt, idque illic verum esse, sed tamen in maxima a nodis remotione, iuxta quam eclipses contingere possunt, aliquid discrepantiae daturam credis. Utrum et hîc mea ratiocinia iusta trutina expenderis, antequam de his iudicium iam secundo protuleris, videbimus. Ego in literis illis, quibus meam sententiam inventaque tutatus, haec ipsa, quae nunc infers, satis diligenter examinavi, et Mathematice demonstravi, perque numeros comperta reddidi, et tam in ipsis intersectionibus quam remotissimo limite adeoque his intermedio differentiam, quae contingit inter latitudinem Lunae Ptolemaicam et nostram, oculis subieci, eamque asserui iuxta distantiam a nodis, ubi limes eclipsium est remotissimus, 12 graduum non maiorem evadere 3 scr. quae unum saltem digitum in obscuratione Lunae causari ad summum possint, cuius tamen perceptio quamobrem sensibus minus pateat, rationes insuper addidi. Cum igitur haec sint satis evidenter a me demonstrata et scrupulose numerata, ut nulla restet contradicendi iusta occasio (taceo, quod etiam Mathematici Landgravij experimento et testimonio, quod unâ adiunxi, id muniveram) cur, quaeso, ea nunc denuo in dubium vocas, et cramben semel coctam ad fastidium recoquis? Cur non manifestae experientiae et observationum certitudini eorumque, quae contradici possint, iustae dilutioni non acquiescis? An hoc est iuxta tua veritatis indagandae principia, diu videlicet multumque pensitanda esse ea, quae in controversiam vocantur, et rationes utrinque ponderandas, antequam sententia de talibus minime dubia feratur? Sic neque de ijs, quae ego circa coeli materiam non, ut quidam neotericorum sentiunt, prorsus aëriam (secuti in hoc IOANNIS PAeNAe Galli sententiam) in contrarium adducturus sum, dubitare poteris, priusquam meas rationes, quas forte iam antea suspectas habes, penitius cognoveris. Si tibi neotericorum quorundam totum mundum visibilem aëre elementari replentium arridet opinio, quam etiam sententiam mordicus tuetur antedictus Landgravij Mathematicus, et multis modis a me id obtinere, sed frustra elaborat, equidem per me id tibi et alijs facile licebit. Sed vide, quanta nota vestro ARISTOTELI sic inuratur. Is enim elementorum proportiones et mensurationes hac ratione ignorasse, ut de neotericis nunc non dicam, et coeli naturam frustra ab his exemisse, atque quintum quoddam (idque mea sententia non inepte) immerito appellasse videbitur. Et quam parva ad GALENUM vestrum hinc redundabit gloria, utpote eiusdem Philosophiae consortem? Praeterea quamvis cuiuslibet Cometae consideratio per se circa individua saltem versetur, nec generalem aliquam de omnibus doctrinam extruat quoad singulas particularitates, quae diversis incidere possunt, tamen multa habent communia, et unius saltem diligens pervestigatio ad reliquorum cognitionem uberiorem viam sternit; ut ob id non ratum sit, hanc cognitionem cum ipsis Cometis intercidere, velut tu adhuc nostros conatus et indefessos circa hoc negocium labores nimium (ut videtur) extenuando obijcere perstas. Manet enim scientia et veri inquisitio in animis eruditorum, qui rite candideque philosophantur, et haec exempla ad uberiorem in his perfectioremque, quando plures exoriuntur Cometae, veritatis inquisitionem, non infrugifere conducunt; quin et de multis alijs ad Astronomicum negotium pertinentibus (de quibus tum in opere nostro subinde ago, tum etiam in literis istis te commonefeci) disserendi ansam non postremam, neque usque adeo contemnendam praebent. Quod si tibi haec ita non arriserint, frustraneamque, quae his impendatur, diligentiam duxeris, ista non legas, nec cures quicquam, imo contemnas, abijcias et oppugnes, si lubet: me non habebis antagonistam. Inveniet forte noster conatus aliquos, qui tot vigilatas noctes, tot indefatigatos labores, tot meditationes et veri perquisitiones, tantosque in haec erogatos sumtus non ingratis animis excipient. Imo si vel nulli quam maxime unquam candida mente haec agnoscere velint, ego tamen a veri rectique inquisitione et promulgatione animum nequaquam abiecturus sum, cum virtus et veritas sibijpsis precium sint, nec extraneis indigeant adminiculis aut suffragijs. Si id te ita movet, quod dixerim, nostros labores forte tamdiu inter eruditos locum aliquem inventuros atque ea, quae de primo mobili et vulgaribus Planetarum theorijs toties recantantur, in quibus nihil novi aut diu desiderati, sed saltem aliquoties actum denuo agitur, ut revera in his compleatur Comici sententia: Nihil dici, quod non dictum sit prius: si, inquam, idipsum ita te exagitat, ut de tuo saltem sphaerico libello (quem nonnullas praeclaras demonstrationes non ubique obvias continere scio) prolatum suspiceris, cum tamen theoriarum unâ mentionem fecerim, quas te nullas edidisse compertum habeo, ut ob id in tot scommata prorumpendum duxeris, Bosphoros, tineas, blattas, cauponarum et pharmacopoeorum avaras technas nostris laboribus oblique ominando, nihil ista me movent, sed tibi potius bilem excitasse videntur. Scio equidem, qualia sint ea, quibus utplurimum nostri aevi scriptores, vel potius transcriptores in singulis facultatibus et artibus chartas onerant, quodque ea vix diutinam aetatem sustinebunt: et experientia testatur, superioris seculi eiuscemodi non magni momenti scripta iam exolevisse. Nec ego animum induxi, quidpiam in medium proferre, quod ab alijs satis elaboratum anteaque promulgatum est, ne videar actum agere, sed ea potissimum, quae tot iam seculis diu multumque frustra expetita et desiderata sunt, quorum etiam pervestigationem solidam sumtuum penuria et adminiculorum defectus alias non ubique admittit. Si ista nihilominus aliquando superesse nequeant et a posteris conservari augerique, utique nihil aliud eveniet, quam quod iamdiu accidit, ea ignoratione opprimi oblivioneque, quae magno labore e tenebris in veritatis lucem deducta sunt; et nihil ad me pertinebit, si posteri ea tueri conservareque nequeant aut nolint. Sufficit, me DEI aliquale donum non humi abscondisse, nec tempus inutiliter consumsisse, de caetero non solcitus. Ego nihil eorum ago, quod immortalitatem ullam, quam in solius DEI gratia positam scio, inde consequi sperem, et quemadmodum (ut CICERO ait) ea, quae de nobis, antequam nati sumus, loquebantur homines, sive non, nihil nos afficiunt, sic etiam post fata aliorum tunc viventium nostri recordatio nihil nobis boni damnive adfert, sola ea, quae apud DEUM est, immortalitate perpetuitatem aeviternam largiente. Caetera omnia cum caduco mundo interitui et dissolutioni obnoxia sunt. Qui igitur in his sempiternum aliquid ponit, seipsum decipit. Habes, quid ego etiam de his sentiam, ob id inculcanda, ne me aliter opinari frustra existimes, et contra iudicij aequitatem, quam prae te fers, nescius et hac in parte pecces. Istam non levem iniuriam, quam in meam Cometae Anni 77 theoriam, asserens talem Cometae ductum aliquando terris uniri, commisisti, vix uno vel altero verbo excusas, ne forte errorem agnoscere videaris. At quî Mathematicus ignorare debeat, digressionem maximam a Sole eorum Phaenomenωn, quae Terram non simul ambiunt, in loco contactus, qui sit per lineam visus et circulum sui ductus, capiendam esse, quemadmodum in ♀ et ☿ ab omnibus Astronomis hactenus quam proxime fieri consuevit, et ista per se in libro meo satis fideliter explicata sunt, ut nullam haesitationem vel obiectionem iure mereri possint?</p><p>Ea, quae ad ultimas literas iocose saltem et obiter scriptas copiosius fere, quam ad ullas maiores et ad rem Astronomicam non leviter, ut puto, facientia tractantes respondes, nolo multis replicare. Scimus, quales sint incolae nostro aevo temperatissimam mundi regionem inhabitantes, quales etiam Africam incolentes, ut de caeteris non dicam. Id tantum addam, in extrema Norwegia et Suecia rusticos reperiri non paucos, mansuetiores, humaniores et hospitaliores, quam plaerique rus incolentium inter eos, qui in septentrionali Germaniae parte degunt. Nam isthic versus Boream non solum sunt omnes etiam agricolae hospitales, sed bene secum actum putant, si a peregrinis visitentur, ijs cibum et potum in copia largiuntur gratis, nihil pecuniarum recipientes, cum ijs blande colloquuntur, congaudent, cantant, et similia humanitatis officia lubenter praestant, promovent postea equis, curribus, cymbis, quorsum ulterius tendere cupiunt, atque haec obvia vel nullâ vel minimâ mercede. Haec ita se habere, norunt ij, qui istas regiones peragrârunt. Cum itaque utriusque plagae homines, de quibus loquor, sint rustici, nec literis instituti, nec peregrinatione aut conversatione mansuetiores redditi, nec delicatius educati, quî fit, ut Boreas Austrum in his humanitatis officis vincat? Vide, qualia ingenia ultima Islandia producat, quod procul dubio expertus es in ijs, qui subinde vestram Academiam invisunt. Imo vix isthic reperitur rusticus aliquis, qui non ludum Scacchorum, qui omnium est ingeniosissimus, expedite et scite admodum calleat, qui tamen tantum habet difficultatis, ut multi sint alibi in civitatibus, qui eum vix assequuntur. Sed nolo ad comparationem, quae ut recte ais, odiosa est, diverti. Latet quiddam in terrarum Naturis et Genijs abstrusi, quod nec aëris nec coeli influxu totaliter excusari potest, imo idipsum non perpetuo in ijsdem locis durat, sed habet suas quasdam metamorphoses et transplantationes, vulgaribus Philosophis nondum perspectas.</p><p>Haec literis tuis respondere libuit, nullo te tuave carpendi studio (neque enim amicitia nostra diutina patitur) sed ut ingenue te admonerem, ne nimium tuo ipsius iudicio semper tribueres, aliorum laboribus et inventis detrahendo eaque elevando, et ne in tantum veterum Philosophorum et Medicorum sententijs te mancipares, ut neotericos veritatis latebras enodare posse diffideres. Vale et haec subito exarata boni consule atque candide interpretare, profecta ab homine tui amante et libertate utente Philosophica. Iterum quam feliciss. vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned Master Doctor Brucaeus — Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>You assert rightly and prudently — and it is a view in which I too have always stood — that it is a mark of great frivolity, of small wit, of feeble judgment, indeed of an inconstant mind, to assent suddenly [to a thing] when one's adversaries' opinions have not been rightly weighed — opinions whose authors care more for slander than for thinking rightly. But while you boast that you make a practice of this, and that you press long and hard upon the points that are contradicted in disputed questions, and on how they may be refuted, and so either persist in your earlier opinion or else attach yourself to those who think more rightly — whether (if I may, with your leave, speak the truth) you everywhere observe this so wholly, free of all prejudice, as you perhaps promise yourself in flattering yourself, that, I confess, raises no slight doubt in me. For very many of the things I once came to perceive clearly from our familiar conversation together, and likewise your letters written to me on several occasions about philosophical matters, suggest something quite to the contrary to one who sifts the whole matter more thoroughly. And not to fetch examples from afar: surely these very [letters of yours] (to say nothing now of the rest), in which you so clear yourself and proclaim your singular fairness in judging, run counter to you on this very point. For to what end do you call all those men \"men of darkness\" [tenebriones] who, wherever certain ancient physicians and philosophers have palmed off as truth things discordant and ill-attested, refute them daily, not merely by sound reasoning but by manifest experience and by the senses themselves; and why do you say that they deceive and rail at the unlearned crowd? Is it not rather to handle the matter with railing — and to assail with a kind of prejudice and blind passion men whose writings, reasonings, and even mechanical operations you have never duly examined — to keep calling them \"men of darkness\"? Reading this, I halted for a while in doubt as to whom you wished to adorn with so choice an epithet, and I scarcely grasped it, until, going on further, I understood that you were speaking of that great Theophrastus Paracelsus, the incomparable philosopher and physician of the Germans — to you Galenists no less hateful and odious than the light of day is to the owl. For in those very letters that you take so unwillingly I had written a few words about this man. Replying to what you had brought forward in your earlier [letter] — that the ancients held the stars to be fed on moisture — I had added that you would shortly be transformed into a Paracelsist if you were willing to lend an ear to such opinions; for he too held that the stars are nourished. From this you have seized an occasion to infer that I wish to draw you into the opinion of that \"man of darkness,\" whereas my discourse looks elsewhere. Here, first: when you call a \"man of darkness\" that outstanding philosopher of the former age, who drew his doctrine and tenets unceasingly from the very treasury of nature, and who demonstrated, against the sophistical quibbles of certain ancients and of the moderns, by the very senses, that very many things are in fact so — whose writings and teachings, nonetheless, you have either never read, or, if they were perhaps at some time glanced at by you through a lattice, you have not yet duly perceived, much less made their understanding and exercise familiar to yourself by use, diligence, and tireless labour — to what end, then, do you pass judgment on a matter unknown, and never sufficiently examined by you even with the coarser sort of inquiry? Is it fitting thus to defame with the title \"man of darkness\" one who has deserved illustriously of all posterity? Is this to press long and hard upon the things that are contradicted, and to weigh everything in an even balance? Nay, this is not merely to pass judgment on an unknown matter rashly and inconsiderately, but also with railings and gibes to undeservedly tear to pieces a man worthy of praise rather than of reproach, and to vent one's fury on the dead. So too the heron blames the waters it cannot swim across. Are you sufficiently aware to yourself how often, while I was with you [pl.] at Rostock, you came to me — wherever you had found me alone, with no one present — secretly inquiring of me about the preparation of Paracelsian medicines, asking me most anxiously, now about these, now about those, to teach you their making — even though, in the presence of others, whenever mention of these was made, you would receive them with gibes, reproaches, and contempt, set against the Galenic [remedies], even in my hearing? — namely, lest you should seem to have wasted so many years in the husks of words, neglecting the inner sanctuaries of things, and to have neglected all the best [things]. But if the recollection of these things is unwelcome or hateful to you, [consider] how often you asked me by letter — letters which I still have ready to hand — to share with you some of the Paracelsian remedies, which would presently remove those diseases of the most difficult cure — to wit epilepsy, apoplexy, paralysis, dropsy, gouty pains, and the like ills that spurn the Galenists' syrups, decoctions, and clysters — so that through them you might procure honour and gain for yourself and your practice? And surely you long ago obtained from me an effective remedy against epilepsy, drawn from the springs of Paracelsus, which, by the exalted and purified spirit of vitriol, with certain appropriate specifics intervening, generally routs that stubborn and dreadful malady. Do you deny it? Did you not communicate the process of its preparation to Master Levinus Battus, who afterwards, in certain theses treating of this disease, divulged it in the very words in which it had been written out to you — yet without mention of the author and instructor, as though it were no matter to ascribe to your own selves these things which had been procured by no small labour and expense of others, but [as though] it were [thereby] credited to your honour? Indeed, I would long ago have granted you more of these things, in accordance with your eager desires, did I not know that you who hate Paracelsus seek these things not sincerely and wholly and with due gratitude, but only that you may procure gain and fame for yourselves, while leaving the labours to be drained out and the expenses to be paid by others; and, at one stroke — which is the worst of it — that you ascribe to your own teachers and to yourselves what may successfully be accomplished through these arcane remedies, not fearing meanwhile to receive Theophrastus himself, together with those who by long use and labour have cultivated these things through pyronomic [fire-] exercises, with gibes and cavils, so that you alone may seem to be wise. And this is generally the way of those who prefer to be reckoned learned and wiser than to be so. If, then, Paracelsus is so hateful to you that you do not hesitate to call him and all his followers \"men of darkness,\" why do you secretly seek out medicines issuing from his school, while openly you mock and condemn them? Why do you harry with reproaches and curses the doctrine of a man you have never understood, much less soundly examined? Or why do you not suspend judgment until you have examined the reasons and what is contradicted on both sides — if you have so much at heart to avoid that which is a mark of great frivolity, of small wit, and of feeble judgment, as you yourself shrewdly advise? Yet I do not bring these things forward because I wish to blame Galen and the ancient philosophers — whom I have always esteemed greatly and still esteem greatly — in all things, and to disparage them, and to pronounce Paracelsus everywhere free of errors (for they were all men, and could draw from this inexhaustible treasury of nature things false as well as true, partly by looking in to some degree, partly by groping blindly), but only that I may admonish you that, while you condemn that man unheard and not understood, you sin against your own axioms, which you report yourself to keep in practice. So too you do me no less an injury when you affirm that I am trying to draw you into his opinion, and so [that I am] mocking my friends — when from my letters given to you nothing of the sort can be gathered, if all is judged sincerely. For there I rather disapproved of Paracelsus's opinion concerning the nourishment of the stars than mocked you — unless you reckoned it ridiculous that you should in some part be likened to so great and so excellent an Artificer in the investigation, and indeed the handling, of nature: whereas you ought to count this rather to your praise than turn it to your reproach, provided you so desired to discern the inner sanctuaries of hidden truth by sound reasonings, as you try to persuade me you perpetually have at heart. Laugh as much as you will: truth is not foiled by guffaws. I remember that I once gave a certain letter of mine an address [intitulatio] in which I proclaimed you an outstanding Doctor and promoter of Aristotelian philosophy and Galenic medicine. This styling you received with such an interpretation [as to say] that the address of that letter, had you not known yourself, would have made you more puffed up in mind, adding: \"Bah! what a great Thraso shall I become, if I find such a Gnatho?\" But assuredly I did not adorn you with that title in the manner of Gnatho and by way of flattery. For I have never learned to flatter even kings or princes or the highest men. Nor do I judge that the chief praise lies in unrolling and pondering and proclaiming these authors, seeing that in certain principal questions they have departed very far from the mark of truth — as has been shown plainly enough in most matters both by others and by myself, and hereafter, with the favour of the Deity, will be made manifest more brilliantly. Here, then, you ought rather to have grown angry, and to have accused me of playing with ironies. But it is so received in the world that it reckons darkness to be light, and this [light] to be night; thus it is wont to delude itself and others, not without grave loss to truth. But if perhaps these things are rather obscure to you, and made of set purpose more incredible — than that they can prove that you do not always weigh in an even balance the tenets of the various philosophers before you have passed a settled judgment on them — I shall add one or two examples out of that very letter. You say that I assert that the Moon's maximum latitude, which by my finding exceeds the Ptolemaic by a quarter of a degree, makes no great difference in the eclipses that fall near the nodes — and that there it is true, but that nonetheless, at the greatest distance from the nodes at which eclipses can occur, you believe it will yield some discrepancy. Whether here too you have weighed my reasonings in a just balance before now pronouncing judgment on these matters for the second time, we shall see. In those letters in which I defended my opinion and findings, I examined these very points which you now bring forward diligently enough, and I demonstrated them mathematically, and rendered them proven by numbers, and I set before [your] eyes the difference, which occurs between the Ptolemaic latitude of the Moon and ours, both at the intersections themselves and at the most remote limit and indeed midway between these; and I asserted that, at the distance from the nodes where the limit of eclipses is most remote, [namely] 12 degrees, [the difference] turns out no greater than 3 minutes [3 scr.] — which at most could cause but a single digit in the obscuration of the Moon; and I added besides the reasons why the perception of this is nonetheless less open to the senses. Since, therefore, these things have been demonstrated by me evidently enough and reckoned out scrupulously, so that no just occasion for contradiction remains (I say nothing of the fact that I had also fortified it by the experiment and testimony of the Landgrave's Mathematician, which I appended at the same time), why, pray, do you now once more call them into doubt, and re-cook to nausea cabbage once cooked? Why do you not rest content with manifest experience and the certainty of the observations, and with the just dissolving of what can be objected? Is this, in accordance with your principles of investigating the truth, that the things called into controversy must be pondered long and much, and the reasons on both sides weighed, before a judgment by no means doubtful is passed on such matters? In the same way, neither will you be able to doubt those things which I shall adduce to the contrary concerning the matter of the heaven — [holding it] not, as certain of the moderns think, wholly airy (following in this the opinion of the Frenchman Jean Pena) — before you have come to know my reasons, which perhaps you already hold suspect, more inwardly. If the opinion of certain moderns, who fill the whole visible world with elemental air, pleases you — an opinion which the aforesaid Mathematician of the Landgrave also defends tooth and nail, and labours in many ways to obtain this [assent] from me, but in vain — then for my part you and others will be quite free to hold it. But see how great a brand is thereby burned upon your Aristotle. For he will seem, on this reckoning, to have been ignorant of the proportions and measurements of the elements (to say nothing now of the moderns), and to have exempted the nature of the heaven from these in vain, and to have wrongly named [it] a certain fifth [substance] — and that (in my opinion) not ineptly. And how little glory will redound hence to your Galen, as being a partner of the same philosophy? Moreover, although the consideration of any given comet is in itself concerned only with individual cases, and builds no general doctrine about all [comets] as regards the several particularities that can occur to different ones, nevertheless they have much in common, and the diligent investigation of one alone paves a richer way to the knowledge of the rest; so that on this account it is not established that this knowledge perishes along with the comets themselves — as you still persist in objecting, by belittling too much (as it seems) our endeavours and our tireless labours about this business. For knowledge and the inquiry after truth abide in the minds of the learned who philosophize duly and candidly, and these instances contribute not unfruitfully to a richer and more perfect inquiry into the truth in these matters, when more comets arise; nay, they afford no last nor by any means contemptible handle for discoursing of many other things pertaining to the astronomical business (of which I treat from time to time in our work, and have likewise reminded you in those letters). But if these things do not please you so, and you have judged the diligence spent on them to be in vain, [then] do not read them, and care nothing about them — nay, despise them, cast them away, and attack them, if you like: you will not have me for an antagonist. Perhaps our endeavour will find some who will receive with no ungrateful minds so many sleepless nights, so many unwearied labours, so many meditations and searchings after truth, and so great expenses laid out on these things. Nay, even if absolutely no one should ever be willing to acknowledge these things with a candid mind, I shall nonetheless by no means cast down my spirit from the inquiry into, and the publication of, the true and the right — since virtue and truth are a price unto themselves, and need no external props or votes. If what I said moves you so — that our labours will perhaps for so long find some place among the learned, while the things so often re-chanted about the primum mobile and the common theories of the planets, in which there is nothing new or long desired, but only what has already been done is done over again several times, so that in these matters in truth the Comic poet's saying is fulfilled: \"Nothing is said that has not been said before\" — if, I say, this very thing so vexes you that you suspect it to have been uttered with regard at least to your little book on the sphere (which, I know, contains some excellent demonstrations, not everywhere obvious), although I made mention of theories alongside, of which I find that you have published none — so that on this account you have thought you must burst out into so many gibes, darkly hinting, against our labours, at \"Bosphoruses\" [Bospori], moths, bookworms, and the greedy tricks of tavern-keepers and apothecaries: those things move me not at all, but seem rather to have stirred up bile in you. I know indeed what sort of things they are with which most of the writers — or rather copyists — of our age load their papers in the several faculties and arts, and that these things will scarcely endure a long age; and experience attests that writings of that sort, of no great moment, of the former century have already gone out of use. Nor have I set my mind to bring forward into the open anything that has been sufficiently elaborated by others and previously published, lest I should seem to do what has [already] been done; but rather, above all, those things which for so many ages now have been long and much sought and desired in vain — things whose solid investigation, moreover, the scarcity of expense and the lack of aids does not everywhere otherwise permit. If these things should nonetheless one day prove unable to survive and to be preserved and increased by posterity, assuredly nothing else will come to pass than what has long since happened: that those things which by great labour have been led out of darkness into the light of truth are crushed by ignorance and oblivion; and it will be no concern of mine if posterity cannot or will not guard and preserve them. It is enough that I have not hidden some gift of God in the ground, nor consumed my time uselessly — for the rest I am not anxious. I do none of these things in the hope of attaining thereby any immortality — which I know is placed in the grace of God alone — and just as (as Cicero says) the things which men spoke about us before we were born, or did not speak, affect us not at all, so likewise after death the remembrance of us by others then living brings us neither good nor harm, since that immortality alone which is with God bestows everlasting perpetuity. All other things, together with the perishable world, are liable to destruction and dissolution. Whoever, therefore, sets anything everlasting in these [things], deceives himself. You have [now] what I too think about these matters — to be impressed upon you for this reason, lest you should idly suppose me to be of another opinion, and, against the fairness of judgment which you make profession of, should unwittingly sin on this point too. That not slight injury which you committed against my theory of the comet of the year [15]77, asserting that such a track of the comet is at some point joined to the earth, you scarcely excuse in a word or two, lest you should perchance seem to acknowledge an error. But how ought a Mathematician to be ignorant that the greatest digression from the Sun of those phenomena [Phaenomenōn] which do not revolve around the Earth at the same time [as the Sun] is to be taken at the point of contact, which is [defined] by the line of sight and the circle of its own track — just as in ♀ Venus and ☿ Mercury it has been the custom of all Astronomers hitherto to do as closely as possible — and that these things are explained faithfully enough of themselves in my book, so that they can by right deserve no hesitation or objection?</p><p>The things you treat of in reply to my last letter — written only jestingly and in passing — at almost greater length, as I think, than [you treat] of any weightier matters bearing not lightly on the astronomical subject, I do not wish to rejoin to at length. We know what sort the inhabitants are who in our age dwell in the most temperate region of the world, and what sort, too, those who inhabit Africa, to say nothing of the rest. This only will I add: that in farthest Norway and Sweden not a few peasants are to be found gentler, more humane, and more hospitable than most of the country-dwellers among those who live in the northern part of Germany. For there, toward the North, not only are even all the husbandmen hospitable, but they think it has gone well with them if they are visited by strangers; they bestow food and drink on them in abundance, free of charge, taking no money; they converse pleasantly with them, rejoice with them, sing, and gladly perform like offices of humanity; afterwards they help them on, with horses, carriages, and boats, to wherever they wish to travel further — and all this freely offered for no fee, or the smallest. That these things are so, those know who have travelled through those regions. Since, therefore, the men of either zone of whom I speak are peasants, neither instructed in letters, nor made gentler by travel or society, nor more delicately reared, how comes it that the North outdoes the South in these offices of humanity? See what wits farthest Iceland produces — which without doubt you have experienced in those who from time to time visit your Academy. Nay, scarcely is any peasant to be found there who is not nimbly and very skilfully versed in the game of chess, the most ingenious of all games — which nonetheless has so much difficulty that there are many elsewhere in the cities who scarcely master it. But I do not wish to turn aside to comparison, which, as you rightly say, is odious. There lies hidden something abstruse in the natures and genii of lands, which can be wholly accounted for neither by the influence of the air nor of the heaven; nay, that very thing does not endure perpetually in the same places, but has certain metamorphoses and transplantations of its own, not yet discerned by the common run of philosophers.</p><p>It has pleased me to answer your letter thus, with no zeal of carping at you or yours (for our long friendship does not allow it), but that I might frankly admonish you not always to attribute too much to your own judgment, by disparaging and belittling the labours and discoveries of others, and not to enslave yourself so far to the opinions of the ancient philosophers and physicians as to despair that the moderns can unravel the hiding-places of truth. Farewell, and take in good part these things hastily penned, and interpret them candidly, as proceeding from a man who loves you and who uses philosophical freedom. Again, may you fare most happily — farewell.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Dn. Doctori Brucaeo</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Heinrich Brucäus / Hendrik van den Broeck, 1530/31–1593), professor of mathematics and then of medicine at the University of Rostock, where Tycho had studied (1566–68); a Galenist physician and an old friend with whom Tycho corresponded on natural-philosophical and medical matters. The letter is Tycho's reply in an ongoing exchange in which Brucaeus had derided Paracelsus.</li><li><b>Subject, person.</b> <i>Theophrastus Paracelsus</i> = <b>Paracelsus</b> (Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, 1493/94–1541), the iatrochemical physician; Tycho — himself a practising Paracelsian/spagyric chemist at Uraniborg — defends him against Brucaeus's slur <i>tenebrio</i>.</li><li><b><i>tenebrio / tenebriones</b></i> — literally \"a creature/man of the dark, a skulker\"; here Brucaeus's contemptuous label for Paracelsus and his followers. Rendered consistently <b>\"man of darkness.\"</b> The polemic turns on this word, so it is kept verbatim and glossed rather than paraphrased.</li><li><b><i>Galenistae / Galenici</b></i> — the <b>Galenists</b>, orthodox academic physicians (followers of Galen, in opposition to the Paracelsians). <i>vobis Galenistis … noctuae lumen diei</i> — \"to you Galenists [Paracelsus is hateful] as daylight to the owl,\" a proverbial image kept literal.</li><li><b><i>D. Levinus Battus</b></i> = <b>Levinus Battus</b> (Lieven Bats, d. 1591), physician (associated with Rostock); Tycho charges that Brucaeus passed Tycho's Paracelsian epilepsy recipe to Battus, who published it in disease-<i>themata</i> (academic theses/disputations) without crediting the author.</li><li><b><i>Mathematicus Landgravii</b></i> / <i>antedictus Landgravii Mathematicus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the mathematician of Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel; Tycho cites Rothmann's corroborating \"experiment and testimony\" on the lunar-latitude question, while opposing Rothmann's view that the heavens are filled with elemental air.</li><li><b><i>Ioannes Paena Gallus</b></i> = <b>Jean Pena</b> (Joannes Pena, c. 1528–1558), French royal professor of mathematics, whose preface to his edition of Euclid's <i>Optics/Catoptrics</i> (Paris, 1557) argued for a fluid (airy) celestial region; Tycho names Pena as the source of the \"moderns'\" airy-heaven doctrine he is here contesting.</li><li><b><i>Cyprianus Leovitius</b></i> does <b>not</b> appear in this letter (he does in the 1571 Wolf letter); not glossed here.</li><li><b>Astronomy — lunar latitude.</b> <i>Lunae latitudinem maximam … Ptolemaicam quarta gradus parte excedit</i> — Tycho's measured maximum <b>(celestial) latitude</b> of the Moon exceeds Ptolemy's value by <b>a quarter of a degree</b> (≈15′). <i>nodos / a nodis remotione</i> — the <b>nodes</b> of the lunar orbit and distance therefrom; <i>limes eclipsium</i> = the eclipse-limit. Glossary §5.1: <i>latitudo → latitude, nodus → node</i>.</li><li><b><i>3 scr.</b></i> — <i>scrupula</i> = arc-<b>minutes</b> (the <i>scrupulum</i> / <i>scriptulum</i> as 1/60 of a degree); rendered \"3 minutes.\" <i>digitum</i> = a <b>digit</b> (1/12 of the Moon's diameter), the standard unit of eclipse-magnitude; \"a single digit in the obscuration of the Moon.\" Both are technical period units, kept and glossed.</li><li><b><i>cometae … coeli materiam … quintum quoddam</b></i> — the comet/heavens passage states Tycho's mature cosmology: the heaven is <b>not</b> (as Aristotle's defenders hold) a solid fifth element/<b>quintessence</b> (<i>quintum quoddam</i>), and the demolition of the comet-spheres bears on this; Tycho notes that Aristotle \"not ineptly\" called the heaven a fifth substance even so. <i>primum mobile</i> = the <b>primum mobile</b>; <i>vulgares Planetarum theoriae</i> = \"the common planetary theories\" — kept.</li><li><b>Comet of 1577.</b> <i>meam Cometae Anni 77 theoriam</i> — Tycho's theory of the <b>comet of 1577</b> (published in <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588). The astronomical core of the rebuttal: that the greatest <b>elongation</b> (<i>digressio maxima a Sole</i>) of bodies not concentric with the Earth is to be measured at the point of contact of the line of sight with the circle of the body's own path — <i>quemadmodum in ♀ et ☿</i> \"as in <b>Venus</b> and <b>Mercury</b>.\" Glyphs ♀ (Venus) and ☿ (Mercury) are kept in the Source block and named in English per §5.3.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> The text is overwhelmingly Latin. The single Greek element is the orthographic flourish <i>Phaenomenωn</i> — the Latin word <i>phaenomenon</i> given a <b>Greek omega (ω)</b> in its genitive-plural ending (printed <i>-ωn</i>); kept as printed and rendered \"phenomena.\"</li><li><b>Medicine / alchemy.</b> <i>Epilepsia, Apoplexia, Paralysis, Hydropem [hydrops → dropsy], dolores podagrici</i> — the diseases Brucaeus sought Paracelsian cures for; <i>Hydrops</i> glossed <b>dropsy</b> (§5.4). <i>syrupos, decoctione [decoction], clysteres</i> — Galenic <i>syrups, decoctions, clysters (enemas)</i>. <i>vitrioli exaltatum et depuratum spiritum … appropriatis specificis</i> — the <b>exalted and purified spirit of vitriol</b> with <b>specifics</b>: a spagyric (Paracelsian) anti-epileptic preparation. <i>Pyronomica exercitia</i> — \"<b>pyronomic exercises</b>,\" i.e. operations governed by [the regulation of] fire — laboratory/alchemical fire-work; kept and glossed. <i>arcana remedia</i> = \"arcane remedies.\" Period terms not modernised (§5.4).</li><li><b>Literary allusions.</b> <i>Ardea aquas … culpat</i> — the proverbial <b>heron</b> that blames the water it cannot cross (cf. the rooster/pearl type of fable-proverb); kept literal. <i>Thraso … Gnatho</i> — the braggart soldier <b>Thraso</b> and the parasite-flatterer <b>Gnatho</b> of Terence's <i>Eunuchus</i>; Brucaeus's self-deprecating jest that Tycho's flattering address would make him a Thraso to Tycho's Gnatho. <i>Comici sententia: \"Nihil dici, quod non dictum sit prius\"</i> — the <b>Comic poet's</b> maxim \"<b>Nothing is said that has not been said before</b>,\" from the prologue to Terence's <i>Eunuchus</i> (lines 41: <i>nullumst iam dictum quod non dictum sit prius</i>); kept as the quoted <i>sententia</i>. <i>Cicero ait</i> — the consolatory commonplace that what men said of us before we were born, or after we die, does not touch us, echoing Cicero (<i>Tusculan Disputations</i> / <i>De senectute</i> register); attributed as Tycho gives it. <i>cramben semel coctam ad fastidium recoquis</i> — \"you re-cook cabbage once cooked, to nausea,\" the <i>crambe repetita</i> proverb (Juvenal); kept.</li><li><b><i>Bospori / Bosphoros, tineas, blattas</b></i> — \"Bosphoruses, moths, bookworms\": <i>tineae</i> and <i>blattae</i> are clothes-/book-moths and cockroaches (devourers of paper), Brucaeus's sneer that Tycho's labours are bookworm-fodder. <b><i>Bospori</b></i> is a <b>crux</b>: it is not the standard idiom here. It is most plausibly a learned/contemptuous coinage or a corrupt reading (possibly for <i>Bombyces</i> \"silkworms,\" another paper/cloth-pest, or an allusion via \"Bosporus\"); the printed form is retained in the Source block and rendered literally as <b>\"Bosphoruses\"</b> with the metaphor (worthless, devouring pests) preserved. Not smoothed — see confidence.</li><li><b><i>sphaerico libello tuo</b></i> — Brucaeus's \"<b>little book on the sphere</b>,\" i.e. an elementary <i>De sphaera</i> (Sacrobosco-type) textbook; Tycho concedes it contains some good demonstrations but notes Brucaeus published no planetary <i>theoricae</i>.</li><li><b>Geography / ethnography.</b> The second paragraph answers a jesting aside of Brucaeus's about northern peoples: Tycho praises the hospitality of peasants in <b>farthest Norway and Sweden</b> over north-German country-folk, and the wit of <b>Icelanders</b> (their skill at <b>chess</b>, <i>ludus Scacchorum</i>) who visit the Rostock <b>Academia</b> (university). <i>temperatissima mundi regio</i> = the <b>most temperate zone</b>. <i>Genii … terrarum</i> — the \"<b>genii</b> (tutelary spirits/characters) of lands,\" a natural-philosophical not literal sense; kept.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>Salutem plurimam (dicit)</i> → <b>\"heartiest greeting\"</b> (§5.5). <i>Vale … Iterum quam feliciss. vale</i> → <b>\"Farewell … Again, may you fare most happily, farewell.\"</b> <i>libertate utente Philosophica</i> = \"using philosophical freedom,\" Tycho's sign-off justification for his frankness.</li><li><b>Negation note.</b> The argument is built on a dense lattice of negations and concessive clauses (<i>non … sed</i>, <i>nec … nec</i>, <i>non … nisi</i>, <i>ne … videaris</i>). Each has been counted and reproduced one-for-one; in particular Tycho's disclaimer <i>\"haec non propterea refero, quod … in omnibus culpare … et Paracelsum undiquaque erroribus immunem pronunciare velim … sed saltem ut admoneam te\"</i> is rendered with all four of its negating/limiting members intact (he does <b>not</b> mean to blame Galen in all things, <b>nor</b> to declare Paracelsus everywhere error-free, <b>but only</b> to admonish Brucaeus). No negation has been added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <b>The source carries no dateline.</b> The dataset catalogues it as <i>\"1589. empty\"</i> (year only, no day/place), and the MS witness (Vienna cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66) is a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>). The header therefore gives <b>1589 [O.S.]</b> with \"(catalogued 1589)\" and the place <b>[Uraniborg]</b> in brackets as the inferred but un-stated place of writing (Tycho was at Uraniborg in 1589); no day-date is asserted because the text substantiates none. Tycho and the Danish/Rostock orbit used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. A separate, dated Tycho→Brucaeus letter (21 February 1589, slug brahe-t_15890221001) exists; this undated one is a distinct piece and should not be conflated with it.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> As printed in the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> tradition (<i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>) and re-edited by Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII (the <i>Epistolae</i> volume).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical astronomy (lunar-latitude limit of 3′ ≈ one digit; the Venus/Mercury elongation-at-contact argument; the anti-quintessence cosmology) is sound and consistent with Tycho's <i>De mundi</i> doctrine. Two flagged points keep this from [A]: (1) <b><i>Bospori/Bosphoros</b></i> in the moth-and-bookworm sneer is a genuine lexical crux — an unidiomatic form left literal and not smoothed; (2) the <b>absence of any dateline</b> (year only, from the catalogue) means the header date rests on the catalogue, not the text, and is flagged accordingly rather than asserted as written.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.168-175 as page images (slice pp.1-8 of 9; p.9/176 confirmed to belong to the following Craig-to-Tycho letter, not this one, once 'Iterum quam feliciss. vale.' and the apograph footnote closed the letter on p.175). Collated the full Latin source_text word-for-word against the Dreyer print: exact match throughout, no corrupted/glued/dropped words (no TEXT_ERROR). Checked every proper name (Brucaeus, Theophrastus Paracelsus, Levinus Battus, Aristoteles, Galenus, Cicero, Ioannes Paena/Jean Pena, the unnamed Landgrave's mathematician, Thraso/Gnatho), every number and unit (12 graduum, 3 scr., quarta gradus parte), the one date-bearing figure (Cometa Anni 77), and the closing/opening formulae against Dreyer. Read the full published_english against source_text clause by clause across all three paragraphs (the Paracelsus/tenebriones dispute, the Scandinavian-hospitality digression, the closing admonition); found no fabricated fact, no silently dropped clause, no flipped negation or antecedent, and no hardened hedge — bracketed interpretive supplements (e.g. '[pl.]', '[that I am]') are clearly marked per the brief's exclusion.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 15, "prev": "brahe-t_15881104001", "next": "brahe-t_15890323001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880920001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890323001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Questions whether Brucaeus, despite his stated practice of pressing hard on disputed points before assenting, truly avoids prejudice in weighing opinions, citing evidence from earlier conversations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890221001", "d": "1589-02-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 21 February 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg/Frankfurt 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: [none recorded]</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC<br>DOMINO D. WILHELMO<br>HASSIAe LANDTGRAVIO, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ETA NIDDA: PRINCIPI ACA DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO S. P.</p><p>AETSI a tua Celsitudine nihil responsi ad posteriores literas circa Nundinas Autumnales receperim, nihilominus quod subinde Celsit. tuae scribam, et de negotio Astronomico conferam, minime ingratum esse, satis mihi persuadeo, possunt enim in tanta rerum politicarum administratione, multa incidere, quae tuam Celsitud. etiamsi quam maxime velit, a respondendo auocent; Nam et T. Cels. Mathematicus, vir in his artibus apprime industrius mihi per literas significauit, T. Cels. in elocatione illustris suae Filiae, nuptiarumque magnifico apparatu, tunc temporis occupatam fuisse, quominus haec curare tempestiuum fuerit, vnaque sibi iniunctum refert, vt is T. Cels. nomine meis responderet, Quod et breuibus fecit: Siquidem ad singula rescribere non duxit necessarium. In primis autem discrepantiam, quae nobis in verificatione Fixarum intercedit aliquantula, attingit, existimatque Refractionum vitio minime factum esse, quod Anno 87. e Sole intermediante Veneris Stella, Longitudines Fixarum quinis adhuc proxime Scrupulis nostra inuentione anterioresA expertus sit, verum id mihi vix persuadebit. Nam et ego eodem tempore simili ratione, Fixarum limites explorare non destiti, et pluribus vicibus diligenti adhibita examintione deprehendi, si Parallaxium et Refractionum non satis circumspecte praecauerentur impedimenta, quinque vel sex Minutis vlterius, quam oporteat, recidere. Sed et in Decembri proxime elapsi Anni, cum Venus rursus Eôa interdiu cerneretur, viceuersa rem omnem exploraui, et tantundem illic Fixarum loca iusto anterius cadere, eodem modo comperi. Nec dubito, si T. Cels. Mathematicus etiam in fine praeteriti Anni, et huius labentis principio, quando Venus multo tempore diurna cernebatur, ad ipsam matutinam haec rursus expenderit, idem mecum adinuenisse, quodque iam sententia mutata, in nostras Longitudines consenserit. Fuere autem circa vtraque tempora Declinationes Solis et Veneris, atque huius a Terra distantiae non admodum dispares, quod multum facit ad rei inquirendae certitudinem penitius peruestigandam, praesertim si consimiles, vt oportet, adhibeantur Altitudines. Vt vero T. Cels. exemplum aliquod e nostris Obseruationibus in promptu habeat, vnicum hoc subiungam. Anno itaque, vt dixi 87. die 24. Ianuarij, Hor. 0. M. 15. P. M. cum Sol haberet Altitudinem part. 17½. Obseruabatur hîc per Sextantem Trigonicum quadricubitalem distantia ♀ a centro ☉ partium 37. Min. 5¾. Declinatione eius vna reperta P. 0. M. 25. B. idque per Armillam decem pedum in Diametro, Axi in Mundi Polos tendenti incumbentem, et vna cum hoc circumvolubilem. Hinc et ex data Declinatione atque Ascensione recta Solis per suum locum in P. 14. M. 17⅙. ♒ cognitis, elicitur Triangulorum beneficio differentia Ascens. Rect. inter Solem et Venerem P. 33. M. 26. quae addita Ascens. Rectae ☉ promit ♀ Ascensionem rectam, P. 350. M. 12⅙. Deinde postquam Sol Horizontem subierat, Horâ existente 5¼. inueniebatur distantia ♀ et Lucidioris in cap. ♈ P. 40. M. 46. Venerisque Declinatio P. 0. M. 29½. Borea. Ideo cum lucidae ♈ Declinatio nota sit part. 21. M. 29. prouenit ambarum differentia in Aequatore P. 35. M. 50⅔. et per consequens habita ratione eius quae interea in Venere fiebat promotionis Ascensio recta Lucidae ♈ ad hoc tempus, resultat P. 26. M. 7⅙. cui adhibita modo dicta Declinatione prouenit Longitudo Stellae in Parte 1. M. 59. ♉, quod a vestra inuentione huc redacta, quam minimum discrepat. Abundat tamen a vero quinis proxime Scrupulis; ob Refractionum et Parallaxium in Sole et Venere neglectas implicationes. At rursus Anno mox praeterito 1588. die 26. Octobris, Hora 11. Min. 48. A. M. cum Sol eleuaretur Partibus ferme 18. distabat a ♀ Orientali P. 37. M. 1¾. et Declinatio Veneris simul reperta est Part. 3. M. 36¼. Meridional. Cumque locus Solis detur in Part. 13. M. 9⅓. ♏, ex eius Declinatione cum Declinatione Veneris et distantia cognitis datur differentia Ascensionalis Part. 35. Min. 33. Inde ex Ascensione recta Solis cognitaA constabit Ascensio recta Veneris Part. 185. Min. 8⅙. Die vero proxime sequente Hora 6. Min. 38. capiebatur Veneris et cordis ♌ distantia Part. 42. Min. 24¾. Venere tunc infra Aequatorem posita Partib. 3. M. 34½. Hinc et per datam Declinationem cordis ♌, Part. 13. Minut. 57⅙. inuenitur discrimen Ascensionale Part. 38. Minut. 56½. quod sublatum ab Ascensione recta Veneris (quae ex praemissa ad Solem facta obseruatione, applicato tamen motu interea temporis contingente, innotescit) pandit Ascensionem rectam cordis ♌ Part. 146. Min. 28⅚. Ideoque euadit Ascensio recta Lucidae ♈ Part. 25. Minut. 56½. Est enim differentia Ascensionalis Reguli et huius Stellae in ♈ ex alijs Obseruantibus conquisita Part. 120. Min. 32⅓. Ex ista autem Lucidae ♈ Ascensione recta, applicata Declinatione, quae tunc erat Part. 21. Min. 29½. prouenit eius Longitudo in Part. 1. Min. 50. ♉, quod tantundem verum Scopum anteuertit quinis videlicet Scrupulis, quantum antea excessit, eo quod Parallaxes et Refractiones hîc etiam non adhibitae rem omnem in antecedentia protrudant, sicque a vestra annotatione adhuc duplo plus receditur. Verum si obseruatio Anni 87. huc redigatur per eum qui interea Stellis Fixis inest motum, et vtriusque loci capiatur dimidium; Ita enim vtrinque Refractionum et Parallaxium obstacula, quae paria sunt, excluduntur, ostendetur Longitudo Stellae in Part. 1. Minut. 55. ♉, cum Latitudine Part. 9.Minut. 57½. quae duo in ipso Scrupulo recte constare nullum apud me est dubium: Licet vestra positio in Grad. 2. Min. 1. ♉, eandem Stellam ad finem Anni 88. reponat: Ita vt excessus committatur Minut. 5½. quoad Longitudinem: Circa Latitudinem enim in dimidio Scrupulo consentimus. Pari ratione non solum ad hos duos Annos in Venere, tam occidua quam Orientali, huius indagationis periculum feci, sed etiam in quibusdam alijs, potissimum videlicet collatione facta Obseruationum Anni 82. cum 85. et 86. quoque, cum praeterito 88. multisque et varijs animaduersionibus in singulis diligenter expensis, atque iusto modo adinuicem comparatis vbique terminos Longitudinis Affixarum Stellarum plane eodem modo figendos cognoui, prout antea a me constituti erant. Imo ne in Solari tramite aliquid minutuli lateret dubij quod hanc Pragmatiam, quo minus ad exactam praecisionem perduceretur, impediret, Obseruationes integri proxime antecedentis quinquennij circa ipsum quatuor vel quinque affabre elaboratis Instrumentis, tantaeque magnitudinis, vt de sexta vel quarta ad minimum Scrupuli parte singula nos certificarent, sub incudem Demonstrationum et numerorum reuocaui. Et licet in Apogaeo solari modicum quid a priori nostra inuentione adimendum repererim et Eccentricitatem etiam aliquantulo minorem, quam antea constituendam; Tamen hae differentiae adeo exiles sunt, vt vix alicuius momenti aestimandae veniant: Absidem enim Solis vix dimidio Gradu anteriorem, et Eccentricitatem tertia solummodo vnius Minuti parte de sexagesimis integris Semidiametri Eccentrici portionibus arctiorem efficiunt. At in ipsa Solis Longitudine haec eadem longe minus ingerunt discriminis, adeo vt solummodo vnius Minuti circa Brumam, vbi maxima est a priori differentia subrepat, quod prorsus est eo praesertim in loco insensibile. Nec dubito hanc posteriorem in itinere Solari limitationem e plurium Annorum diligentiori examinatione institutam nobis ipsius curriculum caelesti ea praecisione Analogum manifestâsse, vt ad maiorem subtilitatem vlla Organa, sensusque oculorum pertingere non sustineant. Nihilominus tamen adhibendo hanc, de qua loquor, denuo rectificatam Solaris motus correctionem, loca Inerrantium Stellarum non aliter se habere percipiebam, atque antea e priori in Solis cursu emendatione exploratum erat, vt nihil prorsus haesitem, ne vnicum quidem Scrupulum in nostra Fixarum rectificatione vel deficere vel abundare; Alijs etiam rationibus huius peruestigationis praxin instituendo et rem ipsam varie hincinde comprobando. Indicat praeterea ROTHMANNVS, me breui vestrarum Obseruationum processum, et quamobrem in quinis illis Scrupulis a me dissentiatis, rationes visurum, quod equidem magna auiditate desidero. Quin et mea ratiocinia, cur non aliter in his statuendum sentiam, quampridem a me indicatum est, etiam Tuae Celsitudini propediem fauente diuino auxilio e secundo capite prioris Tomi nostri De recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis, quem nunc sub praelis habeo, perspiciet. Postquam enim in primo capite, Solaris motus exquisitam normam e minime dubijs aliquot praeteritorum Annorum Obseruationibus per competentes Demonstrationes Geometricas in praecisam numerationem hisce seculis competentem deduxi, in altero Affixarum, vt dixi, Stellarum restitutionem ex ipsis Fundamentis molior, et pluribus selectis Obseruationibus per Solem et Veneris Stellam, in vtroque situ tam matutino, quam vespertino, vna cum alijs quibusdam circa idem experimentationibus ratam minimeque dubiam statuo; Nec diffido, vbi Tua Celsitudo meas quoque rationes illic perlustrarit, et cum suis contulerit, quin concedat, nihil meae inuestigationi subesse vitij, meque ea diligentia et subtilitate ad veritatis interiorem nucleum contendisse, vt maiorem adhibere vix cuiquam liceat. Haec vero propterea eo latius commemoro, vt T. C. cognoscatA me de priori transmissa, quoad Fixarum dispositionem, Sententia ne minimum quid dubitare, quin Caelo ipsi apprime consona sit, et quia quam maxime optarem aliquando in Longitudinibus Stellarum non minus quam Declinationibus atque distantijs consensum inter nos vniformem iniri posse. Quoniam vero paulo antea in Solaris semitae iterum redintegratae mentionem incidiA, mitto T. C. binorum annorum, vtpote huius labentis et proxime instantis Ephemerides, ex hoc ipso neoterice calculo supputatas, vt et haec loca, per T. C. Instrumenta explorentur, et cum prius transmissis conferantur, vtvt discrimen quod intercidit, sit perexiguum. Sciendum vero has ipsas Solis Ephemerides ad nostri loci Meridianum, quem ab occasu part. 36. Min. 45. remoueri praesuppono, directas esse, vnde Meridianus vester, qualis Cassellis est, vnum proxime Minutum in Solis motu vbique addit. Sumus enim hîc vobis quasi per Horae quincuncem (vt existimo) orientaliores. Addidi quoque Refractionum Tabellam, quae in Sole ad singulos Altitudinis Gradus contingentem Refractionem, qua altior apparet, quam reuera est, manifestat, id quod hactenus desideratum est; vt Cels. T. non saltem in Borealibus signis per applicatam Altitudini obseruatae Parallaxin (cuius etiam numerationem in altera Tabella, prout nobis in vsu est, ad triplicem Solis a Terra remotionem subiunxi) locum examinare possit; sed etiam in Australioribus, quamuis Refractioni euidenti obnoxijs, hanc ipsam per dictam Tabellam praecauere, sicque per totum Annum, praesertim quando Sol in ijs Signis moratur, quae diurno spatio, aliquantulam Declinationis variationem admittunt: Eius curriculi metam ex Obseruationibus Meridianis cum nostro calculo conferre, comprobareque dabitur. Etsi enim Refractiones non adeo praecise ad quasuis Altitudines praefiniri possint, quin aliquando dimidij Scrupuli vel ad summum integri in illis discrimen, ob non eandem Aëris vbique puritatem cis vel vltra oriatur; tamen hanc quam definiui, illarum quantitatem e plurimis aliquot elapsorum annorum sedulo factis animaduersionibus concinnatam, non dubito ijs Refractionibus, quae medio modo et vt plurimum proueniunt, vtiliter satisque bene responsuram, cuius etiam periculum a vobis pluribus inuicem collatis aduersionibus institui potest. Sed ne T. Cels. et nunc forte alijs negotijs intentam nimium remorer, finem Epistolae imponam. Deumque oro, vt T. Cels. diutissime incolumem et prosperam gubernet.<br>Iamque T. Celsit. quam optime valeat.<br>Datae Vraniburgi 21. Februarij, Anno 1589.<br>T. C. addict.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, his most clement Prince and Lord — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Although I have received no reply from Your Highness to my more recent letters [sent] about the time of the autumn fair, I am nonetheless well enough persuaded that it is by no means unwelcome that I should write to Your Highness from time to time and confer on the business of astronomy; for amid so great an administration of political affairs many things may intervene that call Your Highness away from replying, even should you most greatly wish [to reply]. For Your Highness's Mathematician too — a man exceedingly diligent in these arts — signified to me by letter that Your Highness was at that time occupied in the giving-in-marriage of your illustrious Daughter and the magnificent preparation of the nuptials, so that it was not timely to attend to these matters; and he reports that it was at the same time enjoined upon him to reply to my [letters] in Your Highness's name, which he has indeed done in brief, since he did not judge it necessary to write back on every point. Above all he touches on the slight discrepancy that stands between us in the verification of the Fixed [stars], and reckons that it has by no means come about through a fault of Refractions that in the year [15]87, with the planet Venus mediating between [the star and] the Sun, he found the Longitudes of the Fixed [stars] earlier than by our determination by some five Scruples [arc-minutes] still very nearly — but in this he will scarcely persuade me. For I too at the same time did not cease to explore the limits [positions] of the Fixed [stars] by a similar method, and on several occasions, applying diligent examination, I found that, if the impediments of Parallaxes and Refractions are not warily enough guarded against, they fall back five or six Minutes further than they ought. But in December of the year just past, when Venus was again seen by day as a morning star [Eôa], I explored the whole matter the other way round, and found in the same manner that there too the places of the Fixed [stars] fell earlier than is right by just as much. Nor do I doubt that, if Your Highness's Mathematician should also, at the end of the past year and the beginning of this passing one — when Venus was for a long time seen in the daytime — weigh these matters again at the very morning [appearance], he would have discovered the same as I, and, his opinion now changed, would have come into agreement with our Longitudes. Now around both occasions the Declinations of the Sun and of Venus, and the latter's distances from the Earth, were not greatly unequal — which contributes much to the more deeply investigating the certainty of the matter under inquiry, especially if comparable Altitudes are used, as one ought. But so that Your Highness may have some example from our Observations ready to hand, I shall append this single one. In the year [15]87, then, as I said, on 24 January, at 0 hours 15 minutes P.M., when the Sun had an Altitude of 17½ degrees, there was observed here, by means of a four-cubit Trigonal Sextant, the distance of Venus [♀] from the centre of the Sun [☉] as 37 degrees 5¾ minutes, its Declination being found at the same time as 0 degrees 25 minutes North — and this by means of an Armilla ten feet in Diameter, resting upon an Axis pointed to the Poles of the World and revolvable together with it. Hence, and from the given Declination and Right Ascension of the Sun, known through its place in 14 degrees 17⅙ minutes of ♒ Aquarius, there is elicited, by the aid of Triangles, a difference of Right Ascension between the Sun and Venus of 33 degrees 26 minutes; which, added to the Right Ascension of the Sun [☉], yields the Right Ascension of Venus [♀] as 350 degrees 12⅙ minutes. Then, after the Sun had gone below the Horizon, the hour being 5¼, there was found the distance of Venus [♀] and of the Brighter [star] in the head of ♈ Aries [i.e. the Lucida of Aries] as 40 degrees 46 minutes, and Venus's Declination as 0 degrees 29½ minutes North. Therefore, since the Declination of the Lucida of ♈ Aries is known to be 21 degrees 29 minutes, the difference of the two on the Equator comes out as 35 degrees 50⅔ minutes; and consequently, account being taken of the advance Venus was making in the meantime, the Right Ascension of the Lucida of ♈ Aries at this time results as 26 degrees 7⅙ minutes; to which, the just-said Declination being applied, there comes out the Longitude of the Star at 1 degree 59 minutes of ♉ Taurus — which differs as little as can be from your determination, reduced to this [date]. Yet it exceeds the truth by very nearly five Scruples, on account of the neglected involvements of Refractions and Parallaxes in the Sun and in Venus. But again, in the year just past, 1588, on 26 October, at 11 hours 48 minutes A.M., when the Sun was raised nearly 18 degrees, it was distant from Venus [♀] as an eastern [morning] star by 37 degrees 1¾ minutes, and Venus's Declination was at the same time found to be 3 degrees 36¼ minutes South. And since the place of the Sun is given as 13 degrees 9⅓ minutes of ♏ Scorpio, from its Declination, with the Declination of Venus and the distance known, there is given an Ascensional difference of 35 degrees 33 minutes. Thence, from the known Right Ascension of the Sun, the Right Ascension of Venus will be established as 185 degrees 8⅙ minutes. And on the very next day, at 6 hours 38 minutes, there was taken the distance of Venus and of the Heart of ♌ Leo [i.e. Regulus, <i>cor Leonis</i>] as 42 degrees 24¾ minutes, Venus being then placed below the Equator by 3 degrees 34½ minutes. Hence, and by the given Declination of the Heart of ♌ Leo, 13 degrees 57⅙ minutes, there is found an Ascensional difference of 38 degrees 56½ minutes; which, taken away from the Right Ascension of Venus (which is known from the observation made beforehand to the Sun, the motion occurring in the meanwhile being nonetheless applied), discloses the Right Ascension of the Heart of ♌ Leo as 146 degrees 28⅚ minutes. And therefore the Right Ascension of the Lucida of ♈ Aries comes out as 25 degrees 56½ minutes; for the Ascensional difference between Regulus and this Star in ♈ Aries, gathered from other Observers, is 120 degrees 32⅓ minutes. Now from this Right Ascension of the Lucida of ♈ Aries, the Declination being applied — which was then 21 degrees 29½ minutes — there comes out its Longitude at 1 degree 50 minutes of ♉ Taurus; which falls short of [literally: anticipates, runs ahead of] the true Mark by just as much, namely by five Scruples, as it [the former] exceeded before, in that the Parallaxes and Refractions, here too not applied, drive the whole matter into the preceding [degrees], and so one departs from your annotation by twice as much again. But if the observation of the year [15]87 be reduced to this [date] by the motion that meanwhile inheres in the Fixed Stars, and the mean of the two places be taken — for thus the obstacles of Refractions and Parallaxes on both sides, which are equal, are excluded — the Longitude of the Star will be shown at 1 degree 55 minutes of ♉ Taurus, with a Latitude of 9 degrees 57½ minutes; and that these two stand correctly to the very Scruple I have no doubt: although your position, at 2 degrees 1 minute of ♉ Taurus, places that same Star [as it was] at the end of the year [15]88, so that an excess of 5½ minutes is committed as to Longitude — for as to Latitude we agree within half a Scruple. By a like method I have made trial of this investigation not only for these two years in Venus, as much in its western [evening] as in its eastern [morning] [appearance], but also in certain others — most notably, that is, by a comparison made of the Observations of the year [15]82 with [those of] [15]85, and of [15]86 too with the past [15]88 — and, many and varied observations being diligently weighed in each case and duly compared with one another, I have everywhere found that the limits of Longitude of the Fixed Stars are to be fixed in plainly the same way as they had earlier been established by me. Nay more, lest in the Solar track [the Sun's path] there should lurk any tiny grain of doubt which might hinder this Practice [Pragmatia] from being carried through to exact precision, I have recalled to the anvil of Demonstrations and numbers the Observations of the entire immediately preceding five-year period concerning [the Sun] itself, [made] with four or five skilfully-wrought Instruments, and of such magnitude that they assured us of each [datum] to within at least the sixth or fourth part of a Scruple. And although in the solar Apogee I have found that some little is to be taken away from our prior determination, and that the Eccentricity too is to be set somewhat smaller than before, yet these differences are so slight that they scarcely come to be reckoned of any moment: for they make the Apse [aux] of the Sun earlier by scarcely half a Degree, and the Eccentricity tighter [smaller] by only a third part of one Minute, [reckoned] in [units of] whole sixtieths of the portions of the Semidiameter of the Eccentric. But in the Sun's Longitude itself these same [corrections] introduce far less discrepancy, so much so that only [a difference] of one Minute creeps in around the Winter Solstice [Bruma], where the difference from the prior [value] is greatest — which is, especially in that place, altogether imperceptible. Nor do I doubt that this latter refinement [limitatio] in the Solar course, instituted from the more diligent examination of several years, has shown us its career so [closely] Analogous to the heavens, with such precision, that no Instruments nor the senses of the eyes could reach to a greater subtlety. Nevertheless, by applying this — of which I speak — once-more-rectified correction of the solar motion, I perceived that the places of the Inerrant [Fixed] Stars stand no otherwise than had earlier been ascertained from the prior emendation of the Sun's course, so that I do not hesitate in the least but that not even a single Scruple is either deficient or in excess in our rectification of the Fixed [stars] — [I have done this] also by instituting the practice of this investigation by other methods, and by variously corroborating the matter itself this way and that. Rothmann moreover indicates that I shall shortly see the procedure of your Observations, and the reasons why you dissent from me in those five Scruples — which I indeed desire with great eagerness. Nay, and my own reasonings, why I judge that nothing else is to be established in these matters than was long since indicated by me, Your Highness too will, God's favouring aid assisting, shortly perceive from the second chapter of the prior Volume of our <i>On the More Recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World</i> [<i>De recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis</i>], which I now have under the presses. For after I had, in the first chapter, deduced an exquisite standard of the solar motion from certain least-doubtful Observations of past years, through competent Geometrical Demonstrations, into a precise reckoning suited to these ages [present centuries], in the second I undertake, as I said, the restoration of the Fixed Stars from the very Foundations, and establish it as ratified and least-doubtful by several selected Observations through the Sun and the planet Venus, in both situations, the morning as well as the evening, together with certain other experiments concerning the same. Nor do I distrust but that, when Your Highness shall have surveyed my reasonings there too, and compared them with your own, you will grant that there is no fault underlying my investigation, and that I have striven toward the inner kernel of truth with such diligence and subtlety that scarcely anyone could apply greater. These things, however, I therefore recall the more at length, so that Your Highness may know that I do not in the least doubt of the Opinion formerly transmitted, as regards the disposition of the Fixed [stars], but that it is most exactly consonant with the Heaven itself; and because I should most greatly wish that at some time a uniform agreement might be entered into between us in the Longitudes of the Stars no less than in the Declinations and distances. But since a little before I happened upon mention of the Solar path once again restored, I am sending to Your Highness the Ephemerides of two years — namely of this passing [year] and of the one next at hand — computed from this very newfangled [neoteric] calculation, so that these places too may be explored by Your Highness's Instruments and compared with those formerly transmitted, however very slight be the discrepancy that intervenes. It is to be known, however, that these Solar Ephemerides are directed to the Meridian of our place, which I presuppose to be removed from the [point of] sunset [i.e. west] by 36 degrees 45 minutes; whence your Meridian, such as is at Kassel, adds nearly one Minute everywhere in the Sun's motion. For we here are, as it were, more easterly than you by a quincunx of an hour [five-twelfths of an hour] (as I reckon). I have also added a Table of Refractions, which sets forth, in the Sun, the Refraction occurring at each single Degree of Altitude, by which it appears higher than it really is — a thing hitherto wanting; so that Your Highness may examine the place not only in the Northern signs, by applying to the observed Altitude the Parallax (the reckoning of which too, as is in use with us, I have subjoined in a second Table for the threefold distance of the Sun from the Earth), but also in the more Southern [signs], although these are subject to evident Refraction, may guard against this same [refraction] by means of the said Table; and thus, throughout the whole Year — especially when the Sun lingers in those Signs which, within a day's space, admit some little variation of Declination — it will be possible to compare and corroborate the goal [terminus] of its [the Sun's] career, from Meridian Observations, with our calculation. For although Refractions cannot be predetermined so precisely for any given Altitudes but that sometimes a discrepancy of half a Scruple, or at most a whole one, may arise in them — owing to the air not being of the same purity everywhere, on this side or beyond — yet this [quantity] which I have defined, their quantity assembled from very many observations carefully made over several past years, I do not doubt will usefully and well enough answer to those Refractions which occur in a middling way and for the most part; of which too a trial can be instituted by you, with several observations compared one with another. But lest I detain Your Highness too long — now perhaps intent upon other business — I shall put an end to the Letter. And I pray God that He may govern Your Highness, unharmed and prosperous, for the longest time.<br>And now may Your Highness fare as well as may be.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 21 February, in the year 1589.<br>Devoted to Your Highness,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Uraniborg on Hven, to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince whose Kassel observatory was Tycho's chief rival and partner in fixed-star astronomy. The full titulature — <i>Comiti in Catzenellebogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain et Nidda</i> — names the Hessian comital domains: <b>Katzenelnbogen, Dietz (Diez), Ziegenhain, and Nidda</b>. Salutation <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam (dicit)</i>, \"heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>\"Your Highness's Mathematician.\"</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (named explicitly as <i>ROTHMANNVS</i> later in the letter), the Kassel court mathematician, who had replied to Tycho in the Landgrave's name. The reply was occasioned by Wilhelm's preoccupation with <b>the marriage of his daughter</b> — most probably Christine of Hesse-Kassel, whose wedding fell in this period; the letter does not name her, so she is left unnamed here.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Vraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b> (Tycho's observatory on Hven); <i>Cassellae</i> → <b>Kassel</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Subject of the letter.</b> A point of dispute in the <b>verification (verificatio) of the fixed stars' longitudes</b>: the Kassel and Hven star-catalogues differ by about five arc-minutes (\"five Scruples\") in longitude. Tycho's method derives the longitude of his fundamental reference star — the <i>Lucida Arietis</i> (see below) — by observing the planet <b>Venus</b> as an intermediary between the Sun (whose place is computed) and the star, at both an evening and a morning appearance, then taking the mean of the two to cancel the equal-but-opposite errors of refraction and parallax.</li><li><b>Technical terms</b> (controlled glossary, §5.1–5.2): <i>verificatio Fixarum</i> → <b>verification of the Fixed [stars]</b>; <i>Longitudo / Latitudo</i> → <b>(celestial) longitude / latitude</b>; <i>Declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>Ascensio recta</i> → <b>right ascension</b>; <i>differentia Ascensionalis</i> → <b>ascensional difference</b>; <i>Refractio / Parallaxis</i> → <b>refraction / parallax</b>; <i>Apogaeum solare</i> → <b>solar apogee</b>, with <i>Absis Solis</i> → <b>the apse (aux) of the Sun</b> (§5.1); <i>Eccentricitas</i> → <b>eccentricity</b>; <i>Eccentricus</i> → <b>eccentric</b>; <i>Altitudo</i> → <b>altitude</b>; <i>Meridianus</i> → <b>meridian</b>. <i>Sextans Trigonicus quadricubitalis</i> → <b>four-cubit Trigonal Sextant</b>; <i>Armilla</i> → <b>armilla / armillary</b> (the equatorial armillary, \"ten feet in Diameter, resting upon an Axis pointed to the Poles of the World\"); <i>Organa</i> → <b>Instruments</b> (§5.2).</li><li><b>Scrupulus.</b> Rendered <b>Scruple</b> throughout (with <b>[arc-minute]</b> glossed at first occurrence). In this letter <i>Scrupulus</i> and <i>Minutum</i> are both used for the arc-minute; <i>Minutum</i> is rendered <b>Minute</b>, <i>Scrupulus</i> <b>Scruple</b>, preserving Tycho's own two words. Sub-minute fractions printed as ⅙, ¾, ⅓, ⅚, ½ are reproduced as in the source.</li><li><b>Symbols / glyphs</b> (named in English, glyph kept in the Source block, §5.3): ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ♀ = <b>Venus</b>; zodiac signs ♒ = <b>Aquarius</b>, ♈ = <b>Aries</b>, ♉ = <b>Taurus</b>, ♏ = <b>Scorpio</b>, ♌ = <b>Leo</b>.</li><li><b>Named stars.</b> <i>Lucidior in capite ♈ / Lucida ♈</i> = the <b>Lucida of Aries</b> (the bright star α Arietis, Hamal), Tycho's principal fundamental star, fixed at ≈ 1°55′ Taurus, latitude 9°57½′ N. <i>Cor ♌ / cor Leonis</i> = the <b>Heart of Leo</b> = <b>Regulus</b> (<i>Regulus</i>, named explicitly as <i>Reguli</i>). The whole numerical argument chains Venus → Sun → Regulus → Lucida Arietis.</li><li><b>Eôa / Orientalis vs. occidua / Vespertina.</b> <i>Eôa</i> (Greek-derived, \"of the dawn\") and <i>Orientalis</i> both denote Venus as <b>morning star</b>; <i>occidua</i> / <i>vespertina</i> denote Venus as <b>evening star</b>. Translated descriptively as morning / evening star with the period sense preserved.</li><li><b>\"earlier\" / \"anticipates the Mark.\"</b> <i>anteriores … cadere</i>, <i>anteuertit verum Scopum</i> — the disputed positions fall at <b>lower (earlier) longitude</b> than the truth. <i>Scopus</i> = the \"mark / target,\" i.e. the true value. The two uncorrected determinations err equally in opposite senses (one in excess, one running ahead into the preceding degrees), so their <b>mean</b> cancels the error — Tycho's central methodological point, kept literal.</li><li><b><i>Pragmatia</b></i> — a Graecism for a \"practical procedure / practice\"; kept as <b>Practice [Pragmatia]</b>.</li><li><b><i>De recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis</b></i> — Tycho's <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> / <b>the work \"On the More Recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World\"</b> (the comet/nova volume), here said to be <b>under the presses</b> in 1589; the \"prior Volume\" (<i>prior Tomus</i>) with its first chapter on solar motion and second on the restoration of the fixed stars. Title kept in Latin with translation.</li><li><b>Solar parameters.</b> Tycho reports a small downward revision: the <b>solar apogee</b> earlier by scarcely ½°, and the <b>eccentricity</b> smaller by 1/3 of a minute \"of whole sixtieths of the portions of the Semidiameter of the Eccentric\" (<i>de sexagesimis integris Semidiametri Eccentrici portionibus</i>) — i.e. the eccentricity expressed in sixtieths of the eccentric's semidiameter (radius = 60 parts). The effect on solar longitude is at most ≈ 1′ near the <b>winter solstice</b> (<i>Bruma</i>), called \"imperceptible.\" Hedges preserved: <i>modicum quid</i>, <i>aliquantulo minorem</i>, <i>vix dimidio Gradu</i>, <i>insensibile</i>.</li><li><b>Longitude / meridian datum.</b> Tycho gives Uraniborg's meridian as <b>36°45′ removed from sunset [west]</b> and states Kassel is ≈ 1′ (in solar motion) west of Hven, the two being separated \"by a quincunx of an hour.\" <i>Horae quincunx</i> = the Roman fractional <b>quincunx = 5/12</b>, hence <b>five-twelfths of an hour</b> (≈ 25 minutes of time) of easterly difference — kept literal with the fraction glossed. (This time-difference is internally large relative to the ~1′ figure; Tycho's own \"as I reckon\" <i>vt existimo</i> hedge is preserved and not reconciled.)</li><li><b>Refraction &amp; parallax tables.</b> Tycho encloses a <b>solar refraction table</b> (refraction at each degree of altitude — \"a thing hitherto wanting\") and a <b>solar parallax table</b> \"for the threefold distance of the Sun from the Earth\" (<i>ad triplicem Solis a Terra remotionem</i> — a triple set of values, one per assumed solar distance, not \"three times the distance\"); both rendered with the ambiguity left as Tycho phrased it. He concedes refraction cannot be fixed perfectly: a residual of ½′ \"or at most a whole one\" from variable air purity — hedge kept.</li><li><b>The \"A\" markers.</b> Throughout the printed source a superscript/inline <b>\"A\"</b> recurs (e.g. <i>ETA NIDDA</i>, <i>PRINCIPI ACA DOMINO</i>, <i>anterioresA</i>, <i>M. 17⅙.A ♒</i>, <i>HorâA</i>, <i>cognitaA</i>, <i>cognoscatA</i>, <i>incidiA</i>, <i>redintegratae mentionem incidiA</i>, the closing <i>BRAHE.A</i>). These are <b>edition/transcription apparatus sigla</b> (marginal note or variant markers carried over from the source transcription), <b>not</b> part of the Latin text; they are reproduced verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4(2) and are disregarded in the translation. <i>ETA NIDDA</i> / <i>ACA DOMINO</i> read <i>et Nidda</i> / <i>ac Domino</i> with a stray \"A\"; <i>Part. 3.A M. 36¼</i> reads <i>Part. 3. M. 36¼</i>. No semantic weight is given to them. <b>(Flagged crux — see Confidence.)</b></li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datae Vraniburgi 21. Februarij, Anno 1589</i> — <b>21 February 1589, Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar; protocol §3). The internal observation dates (24 January 1587; 26 and 27 October 1588; December 1588) are likewise O.S. The catalogue <b>label agrees</b> with the dateline (1589.21. februar. Uraniborg); no date discrepancy. Hours are given in the astronomical/civil reckoning of the source (<i>P. M.</i>, <i>A. M.</i>) and left as written.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>Iamque T. Celsit. quam optime valeat</i> → \"And now may Your Highness fare as well as may be\"; <i>T. C. addict.</i> = <i>Tuae Celsitudini addictissimus/addictus</i>, <b>\"Devoted to Your Highness\"</b> (§5.5).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely and the dense observational arithmetic is carried through faithfully with all of Tycho's hedges preserved. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the recurrent printed <b>\"A\" sigla</b>, treated as non-textual apparatus markers and excluded from the translation, which cannot be definitively resolved without collation against the Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI source-page; and (2) <i>ad triplicem Solis a Terra remotionem</i> and the <i>Horae quincunx</i> longitude statement, left in Tycho's own wording where a tighter paraphrase would over-assert. No genuine sense-crux required a [C].</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice provided), so per protocol this is capped at grade B regardless of quality and audited against source_text alone. This is a dense technical astronomical letter (Tycho to Landgrave Wilhelm IV on stellar-longitude discrepancies); checked every one of its ~30 numeric values (dates, hours/minutes, degrees/minutes/fractions of arc, zodiac signs) plus all proper names and place references (Rothmann, Kassel, Uraniborg, the treatise title De recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis) against the English sentence by sentence. All numbers, signs, and names correspond exactly; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. Cannot be graded A because no Dreyer collation was possible.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15880816001", "next": "brahe-t_15900401001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870911001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900515001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Resumes the astronomical correspondence despite receiving no reply, understanding that preparations for a daughter's marriage occupied Wilhelm, and noting that Rothmann had written on the Landgrave's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890221001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890221002", "d": "1589-02-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 21 February 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET ERVDITISSIMO VIRO, D. DOCTORI CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. S. D. QVONIAM nulla interea, ex quo tuas vltimo accepi literas, Eruditissime et amantissime ROTHMANNE, tibi rescribendi ob internuntiorum defectum, oblata est occasio, in hoc vsque tempus responsionem differre coactus sum; Cum vero quidam eorum, qui quotannis ad Nundinas Francofurtianas proficiscuntur, mihi hisce diebus indicari curâsset, se breui illuc abiturum, non intermittendum duxi, quin aliqua saltem tuis responderem; vtvt hoc praesertim tempore plurimis distrahar occupationibus, adeo vt ad rescribendum vix aliquid suppetat otij.</p><p>Quantum vero ad priorem Epistolae tuae partem attinet, in qua copiose et perseueranter materiam Caeli esse Aërem elementarem contendis, nec a semel arrepta sententia, te vllis ratiocinijs dimoueri sinis, eaque quae in contrarium merito dicuntur, speciosis subterfugijs eludere conaris, hisce non constitui sigillatim multa respondere. Quin per me tibi liberum sit, Elementum Aëris Caelo miscere, siquidem non aliter vis. Imo si quis Caelum Aqueum esse, vt Hebraei: vel Igneum, vt Paracelsistae; Aut etiam Terreum (modo cui sic lubeat) aut quiddam ex his quatuor compositum, vt nuper Franciscus Vallesius affirmare praesumpserit, mea etiam non intererit, modo et mihi quod sentiam, libere proferre liceat; Caeli videlicet, substantiam esse Aetheream et liquidissimam, purissimamque quandam materiam, supra omnem Elementorum naturam exaltatam, et ad singularem quandam perfectionem deductam, velut quintam quandam Essentiam, nulli mutationi aut alterationi obnoxiam, quaeque peregrinas impressiones naturaliter non recipiat. Sin autem alicuius Elementi naturam Caelo affingere non admodum absurdum videretur, ego potius illud Igneum, quam Aëreum esse concederem, prout a Paracelso traditum est, qui illud quartum et Igneum Elementum nuncupat. Ex quo enim Stellae et lumina caelestia Ignea flammantiaque appareant; conueniensque sit, quaelibet corpora in Iliado suae naturae Analogo versari; quemadmodum videmus in hac inferiori rerum natura: Animantia, quae de Mumia Terrae constant in Terris degere: Volucres de Mumia Aëria in Aëre; Pisces de Mumia Aquea, in Aquis versari, et sic de caeteris, vt ob id verisimilius appareat, Solem et Stellas, quae Ignis cuiusdam incombustibilis speciem repraesentant, in Aethere Igneo et incombustibili, (vnde etiam Aetheris nomen apud Graecos illi attributum quasi Ardens seu Igneum, idque perpetuo cum sit inconsummabilis) suas Reuolutiones exercere. Attamen mihi ne haec quidem sententia, vtvt Aëris assumtione rationabilior sit, satis probatur. Existimo enim Caelum eam habere ad Elementarem Mundum rationem, quae est animae ad corpus. Qui itaque plene exprimere poterit, quid sit anima in corporibus, et quomodo a corpore ipso differat, is etiam caelestis et Elementaris Mundi naturas et discrimina rectius enodabit. Dicat insuper mihi aliquis, ex qua materia Sol, Luna et reliqua omnia Sidera existant, tum demum illi de Caeli substantia, in quo haec versantur, disserenti facilius aurem praebebo. Quod si Contenti naturam et compositionem explicare non dabitur, vtique Continentis multo minus. Existimo autem, omnia quae in Caelo voluuntur corpora, e caelesti, et Aetherea constare materia, quae qualis sit, vix exprimi potest. Supra tamen omnem Elementarem qualitatem graduatam necesse est, quemadmodum ea, quae ex Elementis componuntur, in Mundo Elementari continentur. Quod si omnia, et quae in Caelo et Terra, caeterisque Elementis sunt corpora, ex tribus illis principijs constare, quae Paracelsus superiori Aeuo in lucem reuocauit, ab antiquissimis Philosophis, tam ante Aristotelem, quam post, non minus animaduersa et approbata, Trinitatis in opifice DEO mysterium, quasi in imagine quadam per singulas Creaturas, representantia, satis ab omnibus receptum foret; Oportebit nihilominus hîc longe aliam in caelestibus intelligere depurationem, et ad praecipuam perfectionem summamque Diaphanitatem et subtilitatem, exaltationem, quam in his sublunaribus; adeo vt omnis labis et defectus expers reperiatur, inperscrutabili Opificis sapientia, omnia sic disponente et perpetuo conseruante. Verum ego de hac intricata, et quae vix in caduca hac vita sufficienter enodari potest, quaestione, quae etiam ad Astronomiae redintegrationem minus est necessaria, nolo vlterius disceptationem aliquam mouere, neque tuis obiectionibus speciatim respondere, quod per se non foret difficile: Idque si vlterius instas, alias non grauatim sum facturus. Nunc breuitati studens alijsque implicitus, pleraque volens dissimulo, praesertim cum videam me tibi non persuasurum, rem aliter se habere, quam praeoccupata fert opinio. Vnicum saltem opponam praecipuo tuo Argumento, quo Caelum et Aërem Elementarem prorsus vnum et idem esse, ex eo, quod Refractiones non ad verticem pertingant, sed vltra dimidium Altitudinis Quadrantem insensibiles euadant, astruere conaris; Siquidem id probet non esse diuersa Aëris atque Aetheris Diaphana, et per consequens eandem vtrumque obtinere materiam et substantiam. Hîc mihi videris plura inducere, quam Assumtio ista sustineat; Nam licet concedatur, nullam animaduerti Refractionem in Sole vel Stellis in data aliqua sublimitate, inde quidem satis sequitur, Diaphanorum vel nullam vel inperceptibilem esse discrepantiam, Sed non ob id necessario infertur, vtriusque Diaphani eandem prorsus qualitatem et substantiam existere; Possunt enim diuersae substantiae et qualitates eidem Diaphanitati patere. Dabo Exemplum e rebus inferioribus, quae manibus tractari et visu discerni possunt. Si Aquam pluuiam vel niualem praesertim, aut etiam communem fontanam per saepius reiteratam eleuationem, defecatissimam reddiderimus, idemque in vino quouis generoso fiat, donec ab omni Phlegmate et impuritate liberetur, amboque hi liquores consimili Chrystallino excipiantur, vtique Diaphanitas vnius non discrepabit ab altera, quantum vllis sentibus dignoscere liceat, ideoque Refractionum, si quae forte hinc fierent, nulla erit differentia, attamen substantia et natura vnius liquoris atque corporis, diuersissima ab altera inuenietur. Id enim quod e vino elicitum est, Igneam et Sulphuream redolebit qualitatem, adeo vt incensum totum flammis absorbeatur, nullo post se relicto vestigio; Quod vero ex Aqua constat, Aqueum permanebit, et tantum abest, vt ardeat, vt Ignem etiam citius, quam antea extinguat, illique vi contraria resistet. Sic quoque Caeli et Aëris Diaphanitates, si vel prorsus eaedem forent, quod tamen nondum satis obtentum est, nihilominus diuersam substantiam atque materiam habere nihil impedit. Plura nunc de his aggregare nolo, non enim haec quaestio ad Astronomum proprie spectat, vt antea quoque monui, neque Cometarum generationi atque explicationi vllum, vt tu existimas, fert subsidium, si Caelum Aëreum statuatur: Imo hoc potius quadraret, si Paracelso Igneum id esse concederemus: Nam Cometae non Aëream, sed Igneam potius quandam naturam prae se ferunt. Quod enim tu e fumositatibus Terrenis etiam in Aërem altissimum, quem intra ipsas Reuolutiones Siderum contineri vis, sublatis et illic illuminatis, Cometarum procreationem saluari posse existimes, ex vna absurditate et incongrua assumtione, alteram stabilire incassum elaboras, vt alibi, cum ex professo de his agam, adeo euidenter, vt nemo iuste refragari queat, ostendere decreui.</p><p>Quantum ad Crepuscula attinet, refers te vespertinum illic animaduertisse iuxta Aequinoctia, Sole infra Horizontem depresso 24. Partibus, vnde valde miraris, quod ego vix 17. Partium idem in Crepusculo matutino expertus sim. Reiteraui itaque his ipsis diebus eius rei experientiam, vt tuis dubijs satisfieret, idque Sole quidem nondum ad Aequinoctium vernum progresso, sed iuxta Principia piscium, adhuc versante. Ratio tamen est eadem, neque magna hoc loco, ab Arietis primordijs incidit differentia. Adinueni autem die 10. Februarij proxime elapso, Crepusculi vespertini extremum limitem fuisse, quando media Balthei Orionis Meridianum obtinebat. Ideoque Sol, qui tunc erat in Part. 2¼. ♓ ad summum 17. Gradibus infra Horizontem, versus Nadir, delapsus erat. Postea die 12. mane, Obseruabatur hîc primum diluculum, cum Spica ♍ abesset a Meridiano versus Occidentem P. 35½. de Aequatore. Ergo Sole tunc existente in P. 3⅔., ♓ non poterat is sub Finitore in eo circulo, qui versus Nadir tendit, depressior esse Part. 16½. Eodem die Crepusculum etiam vespertinum denuo diligenter notaui, eo ipso temporis articulo, cum Humerus dexter Orionis ante Meridianum cerneretur versus Ortum Part. 1. Min. 55. Ergo Arcus Crepusculi, vt antea acceptus, euadit Part. 16⅚. erat enim Sol in Part. 4¼. ♓. Haud aliter die 15. Februarij, vltimum vestigium diei apparuit, quando eadem lucidior Stella Humeri Orionis Meridianum praetergressa erat Part. 1½. Ideoque Sol, qui Partem 7¼. ♓ occupabat, infra Horizontem, permodicum vltra 16. Gradus delituit. Vides itaque Crepusculi siue matutini, siue vespertini mensuram in Circulo verticali, nusquam 17. Gradus excedere, quod nimis magno interuallo tuam animaduersionem anteuertit. Atque hinc liquet, quam parum valida sint, non saltem quae de Crepusculis, sed etiam Refractionibus, quas apud Vos aliter, quam hîc se habere existimas, proponis. Quae de veriore et secretiore Philosophia, quam ea quae hactenus in Scholis vulgariter docetur, breuiter in posterioribus literis attigi, et aliquatenus tantum indicaui, tibi placere video; plura itaque, tum eorum quae desideras, tum etiam alia his longe praestantiora in scriptis eorum Philosophorum, quos recensui, diligenti adhibita intentione inuenies, praesertim vero ipsa Experientia rerum magistra omnia explorante, praemissa tamen caussarum secundum naturam ipsam sedula inquisitione. Mihi nunc pluribus de ijs Philosophari non vacat. Neque enim est id huius loci, nec satis tuto literis committitur.</p><p>Secundum Tomum Operis nostri, De Caeli recentioribus Phaenomenis, tibi gratum fuisse lubens audio. Et Latitudinem Lunae maximam a te eandem fere, quam illic capit. 3. indico, adinuentam, mihi acceptum est. Scripsit non ita dudum ad me Clarissimus et Eruditissimus vir D. HENRICVS BRVCAeVS, apud Rostochienses Medicinae Galenicae et Philosophiae Aristotelicae Professor inprimis celebris, Mathematicarum etiam rerum, praesertim Geometricarum apprime peritus, et in dubium vocauit hanc meam de mutata Latitudine Lunae assertionem, autoritatemque Regiomontani, Copernici, Werneri et similium praestantium Mathematicorum opposuit, quod ij nihil tale vnquam deprehendissent: et quia id Eclipsium magnitudini, alias satis bene calculo correspondenti, officeret. Verum ego illi respondi, Autoritates horum excellentium Astronomorum, nihil derogare meis inuentis; siquidem illi constitutioni Ptolemaicae, hac in parte prorsus acquieuerunt. Neque ipsimet certam aliquam experimentationem instituerunt; an res ita ad vnguem se haberet, nec ne. Quamuis autoritates per se in Mathematicis, sicubi alias, quamminimum habeant ponderis, nisi accedat, certis Obseruationibus fundata, euidens demonstratio. In Eclipsibus vero hanc differentiam esse vix perceptibilem, ob propinquitatem Nodorum, indicaui. Deinde illi vicissim tuas quoque animaduersiones obieci, quibus idem proxime mecum deprehendisse asseueras. Cupio itaque ex te vlterius cognoscere, quo pacto hanc explorationem institueris, et quid, quantumque in Latitudine Lunae mutatum animaduerteris, vt illi atque alijs, inuentis vnius non acquiescentibus, alterius etiam suffragium, qui Caelum diligenter vna inspicit (quod nimis secure ab istis negligitur, cum tamen Astronomi haberi velint) pro me facere, vlterius ostendere queam. Licet ego nihil ex meis Obseruationibus depromere, atque in lucem edere decreui, quod non per se satis ratum exploratumque sit, nulloque alterius indigeat suffragio.</p><p>Id vero quod ais, Caput Draconis abesse a Tabulis plus quatuor Gradibus, teque per distantias et Globum id inuenisse, cuperem vt plenius indicares, quomodo collegeris; hoc enim videtur mihi salua Eclipsium apparente magnitudine, vna cum earundem tempore minime consentaneum. Siquidem quatuor Graduum differentia a Nodis Latitudinem Lunae immutet 21. Minut. per duas videlicet tertias proxime Lunaris corporis partes; quod sane in Eclipsium apparentijs admodum perceptibilem induceret discrepantiam: Qualem tamen in multis deliquijs non saltem Lunaribus, sed etiam Solaribus, quae sedulo pluribus ab hinc Annis denotaui, animaduertere non potui; Nam licet tempora Eclipsium, ex calculo quocunque hactenus in vsu habito, cum Caelo ipso non rite conuenerint, sed sensibili differentia interdum maiori, nonnunquam vero minori anticipârint, vel tardius sese exhibuerint; tamen quo ad magnitudinum formam, nequaquam adeo euidentem diuersitatem vnquam adinueni, quantam tuae Nodorum alterationes exigunt. Neque enim multo plus discriminis hac in parte cognoscere licuit, quam quantum aberratio in motu Luminarium, adeoque in tempore Zyzigiarum suggerere valebat, Nodis ipsis in loco suo, quo Tabulae Alphonsinae et Copernianae (quae permodicum in his discrepant) eas reponunt, quamproxime permanentibus. Rogo itaque vt vlterius, cur ita statuas, examinatione diligentiore facta, declares. Neque enim adeo euidentem, in Eclipticae et viae Lunaris intersectionibus, errorem Tabulis sine causa obijcere decet.</p><p>Refers praeterea, te in libro transmisso, quid de Cometarum materia sentiam, eorundemque generatione nihil reperisse, et recte quidem id asseris. Nam nusquam ex professo, hac de re illic tracto: Sed ad Epilogum totius Operis, hanc et plerasque similes quaestiones, tum multa alia in loca, tum etiam in vltimam libri clausulam reijcio. Interim tamen Pag. 254. vbi quartum caput Maesthliniani scripti de hoc Cometa expendo, haec verba inuenies. Ego materiam omnium Cometarum prorsus caelestem esse iudico; siquidem omnes etiam in ipso Caelo generentur. Vnde vero haec materia illis in Aetherea Regione suppetat, et quomodo illic procreentur, suo loco in Conclusione totius Operis meam expositurus sententiam. Ex his aliquo modo colligere potuisses, quid de eorum materia, quod non sit elementaris, et e vaporibus terrestribus, vt tu sentis, conflata, iudicem. Vbi vero, DEO volente, totum Opus ad Colophonem deductum fuerit, tum veterum Philosophorum, tum quoque recentium, inter quos etiam tuam de Cometarum materia et generatione sententiam discutiam, et minime reipsa constare posse, luculentis certisque rationibus fidem faciam. Interea quae de motu et Apparentijs talium Phaenomenon trado et demonstro, sufficiant; Neque enim Physicam considerationem, quae inde maxima ex parte deriuanda venit, confuse admiscendam duxi, vt Methodus debita seruaretur. At postquam non solum nouam Stellam (quae etiam non minimum adfert subsidij huic negotio penitius disquirendo) sed omnes Cometas, a me diligenter obseruatos, adhibitis etiam quibusdam alijs antea conspectis, sicubi res postulat, sub incudem veritatis per suas Apparentias reuocauero, tum demum consequens erit, Physicam et Astrologicam considerationem, generali quadam indicatione subiungere; Neque enim res haec est parui momenti, sed a paucissimis, ne dicam prorsus nullis, antea rite pertractata. Ideoque non perfunctorie aut promiscue, sed decenti ordine, debitaque diligentia obeunda venit.</p><p>Quantum ad caudae eductionem attinet, ostendi eam in Cometa Anni 1577. Veneris Stellam perpetuo respexisse, quamuis ad Solis partes postmodum potius inclinârim, ob opticam aliquam latentem rationem, cum vix credi possit, Veneris Sidus tam fortes eiaculari radios: Licet nunc demum aliquatenus persentiscere queam, id non adeo alienum a Veneris Sidere, velut tunc suspicabar, ex quo non longe ab eius Reuolutionibus Cometa iter suum direxerit. Veneris enim Stella, visa est mihi aliquando eam partem Lunae, quae a Sole auersa erat, et ipsi obiecta, satis sensibiliter illuminare, vtvt Luna longe remotius a Veneris circuitibus distiterit, quam Cometa. Simile quid etiam IOANNEM BAPTISTAM Benedictum, Patricium Venetum Philosophum et Mathematicum inprimis excellentem, animaduertisse reperio, in erudito illo Opere, quod de Mathematicis et Physicis speculationibus inscripsit. Sic enim in fine Epistolae, ad Baronem quendam Sabaudum Philibertum scribens, ait: Manifeste videtur, dum Luna reperitur secundum Longitudinem inter Solem et Venerem, quod pars Lunae lumine Solis destituta, a lumine Veneris aliquantulum illustratur, quod ego saepe vidi, et multis ostendi. Haec ille. Vt tamen verum fatear, neque haec ratiocinatio mihi ita satisfacit, vt Veneri tantam radiorum potentiam, quae Cometae caudam procreare potuerit, attribuere ausim. Verum sit, quoad caudae rationem, quicquid velit, et siue lateat in his Opticum quid, cur Solem perpetuo non ad amussim respiciant siue non, certe id nullatenus subsistere poterit, quod tu intendis, caudam esse partem corporis Cometae, et eiusdem cum capite substantiae, eodemque modo per illuminationem apparere. Id enim si omnimode ita se haberet, non tot Cometae a Regiomontano, Fracastorio, Apiano, Gemma Frisio, et alijs animaduersi, perpetuo Comam in oppositas fere Soli partes obuertissent, vt ex eorum scriptis colligere licet: Id quod etiam in ijs, qui a nobis obseruati sunt, quam proxime locum habuit. Nulla etenim ratio subest, cur protractio corporis Cometae in longum, si eiusdem esset cum ipso capite substantiae, semper a Sole propemodum auerteretur. Et nos vidimus in crinita Anni 77. cuius cauda, praesertim ab initio valde erat notabilis, eam admodum raram fuisse, et instar radiorum tenuium apparuisse, adeo quod Stellae quaedam, quas praetergrediebatur, aliquoties per illam transparuerint. Neque etiam erat eiusdem cum capite coloris, sed et hunc subinde alterabat, quod tamen ob Aëris interpositi diuersam qualitatem, praesertim prope Horizontem euenisse censeo; Sed et caput ipsum erat solide compactum, multoque cauda lucidius, et exacte rotundum conspiciebatur, adeo vt paruam Lunam ab initio diceres. Ipsi etiam crines capiti acuminatim copulabantur, et postea in latum longumque diffundebantur: Vnde facile colligere licuit, per penetrationem luminis cuiusdam externi, quocunque id modo contingeret, et vndecunque proueniret, caudam illam fuisse procreatam. Idem quoque in alijs Cometis animaduertere licuit, et eo euidentius, quo maiores illustrioresque fulserint. Praeterea illi qui ad oppositum Solis tendere visi sunt, caudam vel nullam, vel perexiguam ostendebant, eam potius versus superiora auertentes. Huic autem proximiores barbam magis magisque exerere cernebantur, quod in Cometa Anni 80. et 82. discrete apparuit, vt de eo, qui Anno 85. omnis caudae expers putabatur, quem vterque nostrum, et nullus, quod sciam alius, diligenter obseruauit, multa non dicam. Hunc, caudam ob id nullam ostendisse censeo, quod perpetuo iuxta Solis oppositum moueretur, nimia etiam a nobis remotione huc aliquid faciente. Si igitur cauda eorum pars aliqua corporis esset, et de eadem materia constaret, nequaquam eiusmodi relationem ad Solem conseruaret. Nec enim, quae de transitu radiorum in puro Aëre sine materia obijcis, satis adhuc perspecta sunt. Etsi enim Caelum tenuissimum sit, tamen non prorsus est immateriale vel incorporeum. Quid diceres ad hoc, quod Aristoteles refert capite 6. libro 1. Meteorologicorum, nonnullas ex vagis Stellis crinem aliquando nancisci, quod Aegyptios tradidisse innuit, quibus etiam fidem adhibendam censet, siquidem ipsemet viderit Stellam quandam in Caniculae femore, crinem licet obscurum habuisse, ita vt qui in eam oculorum aciem intendebant, obscurum lumen cernerent, si vero qui leuiter intuerentur, clarius. Ego vero existimo rem ita se habere, et saepius hoc etiam in pluribus Stellis in Aegypto, vel alio consimili loco, vbi Aër minimis vaporibus est obnoxius, quando praesertim purissimus existit, conspici: Nobis autem in crassiore Aëre habitantibus non item. Sunt enim Radij illi admodum tenues, nec oculis patent, si Diaphanum intermedium minus defaecatum fuerit.</p><p>Vt tamen tuam sententiam ex aliqua caelesti animaduersione comprobes, obijcis nobis Illustrissimi Principis tui Obseruationem, per Torquetum factam, in Cometa Anni 1558. ex qua manifeste colligi asseueras, caudam illius, neque Solem neque alium quempiam Planetarum respexisse, sed per sese suo ductu apparuisse. Fateor quidem, si ea denotatio, quae 13. Calendarum Septembris habita est, (quo solo die caudae ductus assignatur) tanquam rata assumenda sit, tunc Arcum circuli magni a Sole in Part. 6. Min. 36. ♍ iuxta nostras rationes posito, per Cometae caput eductum versus extremam in cauda Vrsae Maioris, non appropinquare illi intra Partes 1O½; Sed tantundem ab hac versus Booten deflectere, adeo vt oportuisset Solem 21. Gradum ♍ emensum fuisse, si is, et caput caudaque Cometae, cum dicta Stella, in eodem Arcu constituerentur. Licet vero ne hîc quidem adeo extra omnem modum difformis deuiatio contingat, pro eo, quod ex solo intuitu prima fronte discernere licuit, vt ob id plane diuersum a plurimorum in alijs Cometis animaduersione hinc concludere sufficiens praebeatur occasio; Tamen quia haec differentia tibi nimia videtur, vt est per se admodum ampla, aliud quid de hoc eodem Cometa ex Obseruationibus Clariss. et Doctiss. viri D. CORNELII GEMMAe in contrarium adducam, qui libro secundo de diuinis naturae Characterismis, capit. primo, vbi de hoc ipso nonnulla proponit, praeter id, quod ait eum in Occasu stetisse, et caudam versus Orientalem Plagam (ideoque in Solis, qui iam occubuerat, partes oppositas) porrexisse, hanc praecipue Obseruationem commemorat, quod die 20. Augusti, qui plane idem est cum eo, quo Illustriss. Princeps Cometam vna cum caudae ductu versus extremam Vrsae Maioris designauit, Vesperi eius distantiam ab Arcturo dimensus sit Part. 30. Min. 32; Ab ea vero ipsa, quae est in fine caudae Vrsae Maris Part. 28. Min. 33. Cumque ad illum Annum fuerit Arcturi Longitud. in Part. 18. Min. 4. ♎, Latitud. Part. 31. Min. 2. Bor. et eius quae in cauda Vrsae Part. 20. Min. 44. ♍, Latitud. Part. 54. M. 25. Bor. iuxta nostram in Fixarum locis restitutionem, competens erit ex assignatis intercapedinibus, Cometae locum incidisse, quoad Longitudinem, in Part. 13. M. 36. ♍, cum Latitudine Part. 26. Minut. 23. Borea, quod admodum sensibiliter ab ea quam adducis, annotatione discrepat. Sin autem per hunc locum, a Sole in Part. 6. Min. 36. ♍, vt dixi, versante educatur arcus magnus versus vltimam caudae Vrsae, satis prope ad Stellam illam accedet, ita vt intra Part. 3¼. huic reuera appropinquet, tantillulum saltem versus Booten digrediens, adeo vt si Solis locus assumatur ternis tantummodo Gradibus vlterior, vtpote in 9⅔. ♍, exquisite in vnum Arcum Circuli magni Soli caput Cometae, eductio caudae, et postrema in Vrsa maiore coincidant. Haec vero non adeo magna discrepantia apud intelligentes in isto negotio facile excusationem meretur. Siquidem ductus caudae saltem ad intuitum denotatus est, qui facile in tanta distantia per ternos Gradus, aberrationem insinuare potuit. Neque enim summa praecisio hoc pacto elicitur, et verisimile est Illustriss. Principem, cum nulla alia clara Stella tam prope occurreret, per quam caudae extensio designaretur, hanc tanquam proximam arripuisse, a qua etiam insensibiliter caudae indicatio differre, quoad aspectum dignoscebatur. Apparet itaque quam parum subsidij hinc obtineas ad stabiliendam tuam de barbis Cometarum sententiam, aliorumque euertendam, quae multis exemplis comprobata est, cum potius contrarium tuae inductioni hinc sequatur. Nam huius etiam Cometae caudam Solis partes oppositas satis partiliter obseruasse, rem omnem circumspecte absque praeiudicio perpendentes non inuite concedent. Fac saltem ipsemet in Globo periculum et locum Cometae denota iuxta distantias a Cornelio Gemma a dictis Fixis mensuratas, caudamque eius nonnihil a corpore protrahe in Solis ad 6⅗. ♍ collocati partes contrarias, facieque Caeli disposita, qualis erat iuxta Horam nonam eius diei, ita vt 21. Gradus ♏, Meridianum occupet, sicque firmato Globo aspice, quorsum cauda primo intuitu dirigatur, vtique videbis illam quasi in verticem tendere, et si producta intelligeretur, nulli propius insigniori Stellae occurrere, quam ei, quae vltimum locum caudae Vrsae Maioris tenet. Licet enim quaedam sit minutula in sinistro lacerto Bootis, quam propius respexit, tamen cum haec sit saltem quintae magnitudinis, discerni non poterat. Cumque nulla alia in isto tractu oculis euidenter pateret, ad quam proximius caudae protensio dirigeretur, quam haec ipsa saepedicta Vrsae Maioris Stella, merito hanc tanquam a cauda indicatam, quod etiam ocularis intuitus satis probat, Illustriss. Princeps arripuit, neglecta illa perexigua, quae etiam soli visui non adeo prompte patebat differentia. Esse autem potius standum cum loco Cometae, qui ex Cornelianis Obseruationibus deriuatur, nullum meretur dubium. Nam is in ipsis Scrupulis per Radium Astronomicum (Instrumentum a Patre suo diligenter excultum) distantias Cometae a duobus illustrioribus Fixis, quae cum ipso Triangulum ferme aequilaterum (quod non parum ad loci certitudinem hinc inquirendam facit) constituerint, subtiliter demensus est. Et licet Radius per se in pauculis Minutis non vbique satis praecisas praebeat distantias, praesertim in maioribus intercapedinibus, tamen tanta non est eius aberratio, vt vltra quartam Gradus partem Cometam aliter quam oporteat hîc disponat, quae variatio id quod intendimus, nullatenus improbat. At longe aliter se res habet in Torqueto, per quod Instrumentum Illustriss. Principis Obseruationes acceptae sunt. Nam etiamsi illud iustae magnitudinis foret, tamen e nimis multis et diuersis partibus intricate componitur, multaque requiruntur ad singularum dispositionem, quae inter obseruandum difficulter simul administrantur, adeo vt dum vnum cauetur, alterum facile negligatur, quodlibet vero per se aliquid erroris sensibilis suggerere potest. Taceo quod ipsum etiam Instrumentum sua mole sese nimium aggrauet, cum non secundum aequilibrium eius dispositiones conuolutioni adaptentur. Ita vt nullum sit inter omnia Astronomica Organa a veteribus excogitata, quod minus expeditam et ratam certitudinem Obseruatori exhibeat, vtvt satis ingeniose eius structura, qua Armillas ab Hipparcho et Ptolemaeo vsurpatas in planam quandam Circulorum compagem compendiosius et minori sumtu redigere, Arabes huius primi (vt existimo) inuentores, conati sint. Taceo nunc, quod Illustrissimi Principis Torquetum, quo Obseruationes istae peractae sunt, etsi e solido constabat Orichalco, nimis tamen erat exiguum, prout vidi, cum ante annos 15. Cassellis praesens apud Illustrissimum Principem per dies aliquot morarer. Habebat enim tunc bina eiuscemodi Torqueta, quorum neutrum satis capacem magnitudinem prae se ferebat, vtrumque vero iam pridem ab ipsius Celsitudine antiquatum erat, eo quod certas Siderum denotationes, vel ipso Principe id innuente, nequaquam exhiberet. Facile itaque contingebat, per Torquetum non adeo magnum obseruando, aliquot Graduum a Scopo deuiationem committi, quod beneficio radij, e distantijs Stellarum petita peruestigatione, satis tuto praecaueri poterat, et cauda quae per se tenuiter apparuit, vt ex ipsis Illustrissimi Principis animaduersionibus colligere licet, in sua productione non difficulter, quo ad longum interuallum, aspectui nonnullam frustrationem suggerebat. Quapropter si ex Obseruationibus Cometarum probare elabores caudam eorum esse de ipsa substantia corporis, et per se ferri, Solemque non semper respicere, aliunde auxilium implores necesse erit: Hic enim, quem allegas, Cometa multo potius contra quam pro te facit, vt iam satis superque ostensum est.</p><p>Nunc ad alia. Quae circa curuaturam caudae, nostris ratiocinijs opponis, parum ad rem faciunt. Si aptiorem inflexionis crinium occasionem, quam ex Opticis, inuenire potes, eam prome. Quod tamen cauda sit ipsa Cometae portio, de eâdem exsistens materia, vt ob id varias formas per se exhibere possit, nequaquam tibi concessero. Non aliter quae de Graminaeo in fine Operis scripsi, minus competenter applicas. Neque enim dixi eum, quo ad situm Cometarum Aristoteleae opinioni adhaerere, sed saltem quoad materiam e fumositatibus terrestribus, vt ille vult, constantem, inque sublimem Aethera attractam. Quoniam vero ipsemet fateris hanc tuam quoque esse opinionem, in easdem sane absurditates prolaberis, quas alias affatim ostendam: Nihil enim refert, siue dicas vapores istos lentos et tenaces, siue tenues et leues, aut ardentes vel illuminatos, modo terrestres et sublunares esse, inque altissimum Caelum quocunque modo eleuatos admiseris. Praeterea quia ex verbis quibusdam ad Illustriss. Principem scriptis, quibus Opus meum edendum de nouis Aetherei Mundi generationibus appellaui, colligis me generationem in Caelum introducere, et per consequens etiam corruptionem. Videris hîc mea verba nimis stricte captare; Sumsi enim ego illic generationis vocabulum latiori modo, pro eo quod nouiter existit vel apparet, antea non visum, nec Grammatici partes in proprietate eius dictionis sustinui. Sed cur non potius ipsum titulum libri impressum arripuisti, quem magis circumspecte, quam in Epistola aliqua requiritur, de nouis Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis inscripsi, consulto generationis vocabulum deuitans, ne vllus esset cauillatum locus. Alibi te longe adhuc a mea abesse sententia videbis.</p><p>De Hypothesium caelestium a me adinuenta constitutione nolo in praesentiarum multa defensionis et comprobationis causa in medium adferre: Id enim nimis prolixum foret, neque absque multiplici ex Obseruationibus certis petita demonstratione sufficienter perficeretur. Dabo potius operam, vt alias, cum de restitutione caelestium motuum ex prosesso fauente Caeli Autore acturus sum, reipsa cognoscatur, has nostras Hypotheses Apparentijs caelestibus ad amussim satisfacere, et tam Ptolemaicas, quam Copernianas longe antecellere, ipsique veritati magis correspondere. At si illae tibi adeo displicent, vt malis cum Copernico Terram et maria in Caelo annuatim vna cum contermina Luna conuoluere, adeoque triplici motu, cum sit corpus vnum et simplex, vltra Stellarum sortem, qua tamen longe inferior et ad mouendum ineptior est, extollere, per me etiam id tibi integrum erit: Neque iam difficilis via est, ex quo Aërem Elementarem Caelo et Astrorum reuolutionibus miscuisti; Cur non etiam Terras et maria? Anne hoc est inferos superis confundere, omnemque naturae rerum ordinem inuertere? Ne tamen fallaris, Non est, quod tibi persuadeas, absurditates Physicas, quae Copernianam Hypothesin comitantur, satis ab ipso esse refutatas; Neque quae a te huc adferuntur, rem omnem indubitanter confirmant, vt alibi haec et plura eiuscemodi discutiens ostendam. Multo minus, quae in excusandis ijs, quibus sacra scriptura contrarium asseuerat, a te praetenduntur, locum mereri poterint. Maior enim et est et esse debet diuinarum literarum autoritas ac reuerentia, quam vt sic in modum Cothurni eas trahi deceat. Licet enim ipsae in rebus Physicis et alijs quibusdam vt plurimum ad captum vulgi sese attemperent, absit tamen vt ob id statuamus eas ita vulgariter loqui, quin etiam vera proponere credamus. Sic Moses etsi in primo capite Geneseos de Mundi creatione agens, Astronomiae penetralia non reseret, vtpote rudi populo scribens, nihil tamen in medium profert, quod non etiam ab ipsis Astronomis concedi queat. Nam quod tu putas ex ipso sequi Lunam contra Geometricas demonstrationes reliquis Stellis fore maiorem, ipse Textus non admittit. Sic enim habet: Fecitque DEVS duo luminaria magna, maius ad diei, minus ad noctis moderationem, tum etiam Stellas, easque in liquido Caelo collocauit ad collustrandas Terras. Hîc saltem de magnitudine lucis, qua Sol et Luna prae caeteris Stellis Terras illuminant, loquitur Moses, non de quantitate corporum. Ideoque Lunam vocat luminare minus, quia non tantum Terram collustrat nocturno tempore, atque Sol diurno. Si enim de magnitudine corporis haec intellexisset, et saltem ad vulgi aspectum sese accommodasset, Lunam non minorem Sole appellasset; siquidem eius visibilis Diameter Sole certe vix minor cernitur, si non potius vtplurimum nonnihil maior. Nemo vero Astronomorum negarit Lunam post Solem esse Luminare magnum ante alia Sidera, quodque multo plus caeteris Terram noctu illuminet, tenebrasque pellat. Sic ipsi Astrologi Solem et Lunam appellant, hanc Luminare noctis, illum diei, quod cum Mose apprime consentit. De caeteris Stellis nunc non disputo, an circum circa iuxta suos terminos plus claritatis effundant, quam ipsa Luna, licet ob nimiam distantiam lux illa Terras non percellat. Nam et Moses eo loco de hoc saltem lumine agebat quod Terras contingit atque Animantium vsui inseruit. Sic etiam nimium Prophetis derogas, dum ais ipsos non plus intellexisse de rerum natura, quam alios vulgares homines. Licet enim ex professo Physica non tractârint, id enim non erat muneris eorum, tamen multa suis oraculis e Physicis desumta immiscent, quae nemo etiam, quam maxime naturali Philosophia imbutus ire poterit inficias. Neque ob id sequitur eos Astronomica et Physica, quae vel a primis Patriarchis ad eos promanârunt, ignorasse, quoniam parcius de his agunt. Non enim decuit Philosophica et Mundana Theologicis mysterijs, quae praecipue tractabant, nimium immiscere. Stat itaque adhuc immota Autoritas sacrarum literarum, vt suo loco plenius videbimus. Nec Copernici positio ab hac se vlla tua opera vindicabit.</p><p>Quod autem meae Hypothesium inuentioni obijcis tractionem quandam violentam, et ordinis confusionem, retrogradationisque in quinque Planetis labem, nihil moramur. Non enim esse tractionem quandam inuoluntariam, sed naturalem et diuinitus insitam concomitantiam, obseruantiamque, qua quinque Planetae Solem, quocunque eat, semper in Meditullio suarum Reuolutionum respiciunt, consentaneum est. Et quanam quaeso corporali tenacique materia copulantur Magnes et Ferrum, ita vt vnum alterum appetat, etiam interposito alio corpore? Si hoc in terrestribus inanimatis fieri potest naturaliter absque violentia, quid in caelestibus, quae animata esse et scientiam diuinitus innatam mirabilem et praestantiorem quam putatur habere, Platonici, sapientioresque Philosophantium non negarint? Perpende diligentius ea, quae in fine 16. capitis libri 2. Plinij ab eo adducuntur, de ratione stationum atque regressuum trium superiorum Planetarum, quae licet ambigua obscuraque vel prorsus absurda videantur, tamen non omnia sunt plane de nihilo. Satisque hinc colligitur, vetustissimos Mathematicos, qui etiam Terram in medio Mundi quiescentem constituêre, Epicyclos prorsus ignorâsse, et alia quadam occultiore ratione istas Apparentias, quae inde saluantur, ad Solem retulisse, neque inconueniens duxisse, Planetas Soli ita obnoxios esse, licet nulla intercederet materialis colligatio. Sed ipse Textus te de pluribus admonebit. Nec vlla confusio in meis Hypothesibus committitur; Caelum enim est vnicum et sibi simile a Luna ad octauam Sphaeram, in quo libere ascendunt et descendunt Planetae, prout Solem pulcherrima Harmonia in medio ambulantem circumeunt. Non enim credo te rursus distinctionem aliquam realem orbium Caelestium introducere velle, contra quam inter nos dudum conuenit. Retrogradatio Planetarum, quae respectu nostri et octauae Sphaerae cernitur, nullo eos vitio afficit, siquidem quo ad semetipsos, Solem semper eodem modo intueantur, cursui destinato vniformiter intenti. Sed haec et plura alia quae forte ab aliquibus plausibiliter contra nostram motuum rationem inferri possunt, suo tempore, Deo conatibus nostris aspirante, sufficienter discutiam.</p><p>Occasionem vero has Hypotheses construendi non desumsi ex inuersis Copernianis, et si tu tale quidpiam vnquam cogitasti, mihi id, vt satis nosti, non innotuit, neque simile quid vnquam ex Rhetico vel Reinholdo colligere licuit. Verum cum animaduertissem subtili et accurata Obseruatione, praesertim Anno 82. habita, Martem Acronychum Terris propiorem fieri ipso Sole, et ob id Ptolemaicas diu receptas Hypotheses constare non posse: Cometas insuper caelitus conspectos et in Solis opposito versantes, motui Terrae annuo non reddi obnoxios, quamuis haud in tantum distent, vt plane is euanescat, sicut in Fixis fit Sideribus: Copernianam quoque assumtionem in motu Terrae collabascere; Non restabat alia via saluandi Apparentias caelestes, quam haecipsa, quae nuper a me generali indicatione prolata est. Si itaque ista duo demonstrauero ita se habere, vtique nec Ptolemaicam, nec Copernianam Hypothesin rei veritati congruere, sed solam hanc nostram, sufficienter comprobatum erit, vt de multis alijs, quae hûc non parum faciant, nunc non dicam. Neque enim hoc loco singula et particularia referre commodum videtur.</p><p>Mirabar valde ab initio, cum legerem in tuis literis, Illustrissimo Principi hasce meas Hypotheses prius cognitas fuisse, vtpote qui eas in Automate quodam expressas haberet. Neque satis cogitare potui, quï id fieri posset, siquidem optime mihi conscius essem, me a nullo mortalium has cognouisse, priusquam ipsemet ex Obseruationibus eo perducebar, quodque interea mecum latuerint antea non publicatae. Verum postquam libellus istius Vrsi Dithmarsici, quem aliquando in quibusdam ad me datis literis digno elogio ornâsti, impurum appellans Nebulonem, mihi transmissus esset (idque a Doctissimo viro D. GEORGIO ROLLENHAGIO Magdeburgi degenti, qui etiam dudum antea indicârat, has nostras Hypotheses sibi ante biennium a quodam meo fugitiuo ministro communicatas esse. Ideoque sciens a me primum inuentas, dolensque Vrsum istum sibi immerito eas attribuere, librum illum mihi innotescere voluit, vt Furtum illud cognoscerem, et ab alijs foras eliminata mihi denuo assererem. Ex isto satis perspexi, vnde haec ad vos peruenerit Reuolutionum caelestium ordinatio, siquidem iste Plagiarius referat, se ante triennium fere, postquam hanc in angulo quodam Regni Poloniae excogitasset, (intellige potius in Insula Regni Daniae Huena, quando Anno 84. in Septembri cum Nobilissimo et Doctissimo viro D. ERICO LANGE, amico meo singulari, cui tunc inseruiebat, hîc adfuisset, apud me clam exceptas, vt quaedam etiam alia) Illustrissimo Principi Hassiae obtulisse, atque ab ipsius Celsit. Artifice Iusto Byrgio in Orichalcicum Automatum redactas esse. Sed satis prodit Furtum suum iste impostor, vnaque ignorantiam rei, quam venditat. Dum enim orbem Martis totaliter Solarem ambientem detineat, imitatur falsam quandam designationem, quae nescio, qua incuria hîc ita depicta erat, atque ob id tanquam inepta alicubi inter chartas meas proiecta, quam etiamnum ad manus habeo, eodem nomine requisitam, vt certo idipsum pateret. Cumque vitium istud in Schemate depingendo commissum emendare nequiuerit, sufficienter intelligentibus notum reddit, se eam inuentionem non intelligere, multo minus eius Autorem esse, vtvt hîc conquisitam sibi impudenter arrogare et Illustrissimo Principi WILHELMO, tanquam propriam excogitationem dedicare non erubuerit. Multa insuper alia habet in isto libello, quae licet non omnia hinc abstulerit, tamen partim a vobis, partim ab alijs Mathematicis in Germania, siue clam siue palam corrasit, adeo vt si aliena demantur, nihil fere restet, quod suum erit. Sed facessat iste cum suo Plagio, tibi et alijs Eruditis, apud quos aliquando fuit, quis sit, et quam perfrictae frontis, arrogansque nugator, satis perspectus. Haec de Hypothesibus meis breuiter respondere volui, plura conuenientiore loco et tempore intelliges, quae proculdubio (nisi nimium pertinaciter in verba et sententiam Copernici iurasti) tibi satisfacient.</p><p>Quod per omnia conuenientiorem Hypothesin in Cometa Anni 77. praefinire potuissem, e pluribus videlicet compositis circularibus motibus, equidem satis ipsemet cognitum habui, idque in ipso libro sufficienter indicatum est, causaque addita, cur id praestare supersederim: vnde ista replicatione non opus erat. Hanc autem ipsam, quam illic indicaui, proposuisse Hypothesin, non caret, vt opinaris, certa vtilitate; Sic enim magis confirmatur reuera caelestes esse Cometas, et motus eorum non tumultuarie cieri. Recte excusasti apud Illustriss. Principem secundum illum Tomum non illius Cels. sed tibi esse transmissum, et veluti meo nomine pollicebaris, me vbi totum Opus absolutum fuerit, ipsius C. esse integrum communicaturum: Id enim antea mecum constitui, et tuam fidem hac in parte redimendam, non est quod dubites. Habebunt etiam reliqui duo Principes, vt mones, Exemplaria. Illustrissimus MAVRITIVS, LANDTGRAVII Filius, quem gaudeo in literis et linguis tantum profecisse, vtinam et Astronomica degustasset, vt in his laudabiliter exercitato Parenti sic quoque succedere posset: Item Illustrissimus BERNHARDVS ANHALTINVS non praetereundus erit, cum Sacris Mathematum imbutus sit. Quod autem primus Tomus hisce Nundinis non prodit, impedimento fuit, quod in vltimis Papyrum impressoriam, pro qua miseram, non adeptus sim, nimia copia Aquarum nuntium retardante, et propter alia quaedam obstacula, quae non attinet referre. At illa quae de Obseruatione vestra in ♀ et ☉ ob Fixarum verificationem facta pro te facere iudicas, et quid de hac re sentiam, in literis ad Illustriss. Principem breuiter datis respondi; Quare ea hîc repetere non est operae pretium, nec etiam opportunitas mihi conceditur, plura nunc scribendi, imo haec ipsa confestim, non saltem inter alias plurimas occupationes prout obiter in mentem venerunt, exaraui, sed insuper graui frontis et Hemicranei dolore ob Coryzam, quam paucis ante diebus ex frigore nocturno inter obseruandum contraxeram, immature constipatam, laborans, ita vt aegerrime haec ipsa conceperim. Neque enim tantum superest otij, vt medicationi vacare liceat. Tuum itaque erit in his difficultatibus minus emuncte congesta candide interpretari, et si non ad omnia nunc particulatim respondere otium atque valetudo patiebantur, haec ipsa generaliter rescripta boni consulere, meque vti facis, amare non intermittere, et saepius per oblatam occasionem literis reuisere. Vale. Datae Vraniburgi 21. Feb. Anno 1589. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and learned man, Master Doctor Christoph Rothmann, eminent mathematician to the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his most dearly beloved friend — heartiest greeting.</b> Since meanwhile, from the time I received your last letter, most learned and most loving Rothmann, no opportunity to write back to you was afforded, owing to the lack of couriers, I was forced to defer my reply until this very time. But when one of those men who travel each year to the Frankfurt fair had these days had it made known to me that he was shortly going off thither, I thought it not to be passed over without at least replying something to your letters — even though at this season especially I am pulled apart by very many occupations, so that scarcely any leisure is left over for writing back.</p><p>As for the earlier part of your letter, in which copiously and persistently you contend that the matter of the heaven is elemental air, and do not allow yourself to be moved by any reasonings from the opinion once seized upon, and try to elude by specious subterfuges the things rightly said to the contrary — to these I have not resolved to reply much, point by point. Indeed for my part you may be free to mingle the element of air with the heaven, if you will have it no otherwise. Nay, if anyone presume to affirm the heaven to be watery, as the Hebrews [do]; or fiery, as the Paracelsians; or even earthy (provided it so please him); or something compounded of these four — as Franciscus Vallesius lately ventured — that too will be no concern of mine, provided that to me also it be permitted freely to put forward what I think: namely, that the substance of the heaven is ethereal and most fluid, and a certain most pure matter, exalted above all the nature of the elements, and brought to a certain singular perfection, like a kind of fifth essence, subject to no change or alteration, and one that naturally does not receive foreign impressions. But if to ascribe to the heaven the nature of some element should not seem altogether absurd, I would rather concede it to be fiery than airy, as is handed down by Paracelsus, who calls it the fourth and fiery element. For since the stars and heavenly lights appear fiery and flaming, and it is fitting that any given bodies should dwell in an Iliad analogous to their own nature — just as we see in this lower nature of things: living creatures that consist of the <i>mumia</i> of earth dwell on the earth; birds of the <i>mumia</i> of air [dwell] in the air; fishes of the <i>mumia</i> of water dwell in the waters, and so of the rest — so that for this reason it appear the more probable that the Sun and the stars, which present the appearance of a certain incombustible fire, exercise their revolutions in a fiery and incombustible ether (whence indeed the name of <i>aether</i> was attributed to it among the Greeks, as it were \"burning\" or \"fiery,\" and that perpetually, since it is unconsumable). And yet not even this opinion, however more rational it be than the supposition of air, is sufficiently proved to me. For I judge the heaven to have the same relation to the elemental world that the soul has to the body. Whoever, therefore, shall be able fully to express what the soul is in bodies, and how it differs from the body itself, he too will more rightly unfold the natures and distinctions of the celestial and the elemental world. Let someone tell me besides out of what matter the Sun, Moon, and all the rest of the heavenly bodies exist; then at last I shall the more easily lend an ear to him discoursing of the substance of the heaven in which these things dwell. But if it shall not be granted to explain the nature and composition of the contained, then much less [that] of the container. I judge, however, that all the bodies that revolve in the heaven consist of celestial and ethereal matter, of what sort this is can scarcely be expressed. Yet it must be above every graduated elemental quality, just as those things that are composed of the elements are contained in the elemental world. But if it were granted by all that all bodies — both those in heaven and earth and the rest of the elements — consist of those three principles which Paracelsus in the foregoing age brought into the light, no less observed and approved by the most ancient philosophers, both before Aristotle and after, [these principles] representing the mystery of the Trinity in God the Maker, as it were in a certain image, throughout each of his creatures: it will nonetheless be necessary here to understand in celestial things a far different purification, and one [carried] to a special perfection and to the highest transparency and subtlety, an exaltation [greater] than in these sublunary things — so much so that it be found free of every blemish and defect, by the inscrutable wisdom of the Maker, who so disposes all things and perpetually preserves them. But on this intricate question — which can scarcely be sufficiently unraveled in this fleeting life, and which is also less necessary to the restoration [redintegratio] of astronomy — I do not wish to stir up any further dispute, nor to reply in detail to your objections, which in itself would not be difficult; and this, if you press further, I shall on another occasion do not grudgingly. Now, studying brevity and entangled in other things, I willingly pass over most matters in silence, especially since I see that I shall not persuade you that the matter stands otherwise than a preoccupied opinion holds. Yet one thing at least I shall set against your chief argument, by which you try to establish that the heaven and elemental air are wholly one and the same, from the fact that refractions do not reach to the zenith but become imperceptible beyond half the altitude-quadrant; inasmuch as this proves the transparent media of air and of ether not to be diverse, and consequently that both possess the same matter and substance. Here you seem to me to bring in more than that assumption sustains. For although it be granted that no refraction is observed in the Sun or stars at some given altitude, it indeed follows well enough from this that the discrepancy of the transparent media is either none or imperceptible; but it is not on that account necessarily inferred that there exists in both transparent media exactly the same quality and substance — for diverse substances and qualities can be open to the same transparency. I shall give an example from lower things, which can be handled with the hands and discerned by sight. If we render rain-water, or especially snow-water, or even common spring-water, most thoroughly clarified by often-repeated elevation [distillation], and the same be done in any noble wine, until it be freed from all phlegm and impurity, and both these liquors be received in a similar crystalline [vessel]: assuredly the transparency of the one will not differ from the other, so far as may be discerned by any senses, and therefore of the refractions, if perchance any should arise from them, there will be no difference; and yet the substance and nature of the one liquid and body will be found most diverse from the other. For that which is drawn off from the wine will smack of a fiery and sulphureous quality, so much so that when kindled it is wholly swallowed up in flames, leaving no trace of itself behind; whereas that which consists of the water will remain watery, and so far is it from burning that it even extinguishes fire more quickly than before, and resists it with a contrary force. So too the transparencies of the heaven and of the air, even if they were wholly the same — which however has not yet been sufficiently attained — nothing nonetheless hinders their having a diverse substance and matter. I do not wish now to heap up more on these matters, for this question does not properly concern the astronomer, as I have warned before, nor does it bring any aid, as you suppose, to the generation and explanation of comets, if the heaven be supposed to be airy. Nay, this would rather square [with the facts] if we conceded to Paracelsus that it is fiery; for comets present not an airy but rather a certain fiery nature. For in that you suppose that the procreation of comets can be saved by terrestrial fumes raised even into the highest air — which you would have contained within the very revolutions of the heavenly bodies — and there illuminated, you labor in vain to establish one absurdity from another and from an incongruous assumption, as I have resolved to show elsewhere, when I treat of these matters expressly, so evidently that no one can justly gainsay it.</p><p>As for the twilights, you report that you observed the evening one there, near the equinoxes, when the Sun was depressed 24 degrees below the horizon, whence you greatly wonder that I have found the same to be scarcely 17 degrees in the morning twilight. I therefore repeated, these very days, the experience of this matter, that your doubts might be satisfied — and that indeed with the Sun not yet advanced to the vernal equinox, but still dwelling near the beginning of Pisces. The proportion, however, is the same, nor does a great difference fall out at this place from the beginnings of Aries. I found, then, on the 10th of February last past, that the extreme limit of the evening twilight was when the middle of the Belt of Orion held the meridian. And therefore the Sun, which was then in 2¼ degrees of ♓ Pisces, had at most sunk 17 degrees below the horizon, toward the nadir. Afterward, on the 12th in the morning, the first dawn was observed here, when Spica of ♍ Virgo was distant from the meridian toward the west 35½ degrees of the equator. Therefore, the Sun then being in 3⅔ degrees of ♓ Pisces, it could not be more depressed under the horizon, in that circle which tends toward the nadir, than 16½ degrees. On the same day I again carefully noted the evening twilight too, at that very point of time when the right Shoulder of Orion was discerned before the meridian, toward the rising, by 1 degree 55 minutes. Therefore the arc of the twilight, taken as before, comes out at 16⅚ degrees, for the Sun was in 4¼ degrees of ♓ Pisces. Not otherwise, on the 15th of February, the last trace of day appeared when the same brighter star of Orion's Shoulder had passed the meridian by 1½ degrees. And therefore the Sun, which occupied 7¼ degrees of ♓ Pisces, lay hidden below the horizon a very little beyond 16 degrees. You see, therefore, that the measure of the twilight, whether morning or evening, in the vertical circle, nowhere exceeds 17 degrees — which forestalls your observation by too great an interval. And hence it is plain how little valid are the things you put forward not only about the twilights but also about the refractions, which you suppose to stand otherwise among you than they do here. What I touched briefly in my later letters, and only somewhat indicated, concerning a truer and more secret philosophy than that which is hitherto commonly taught in the schools, I see pleases you. More therefore — both of the things you desire and also others far more excellent than these — you will find, in the writings of those philosophers whom I have enumerated, by diligently applied attention; and especially with experience herself, the mistress of things, exploring all, yet with a careful inquiry into causes according to nature herself first set before [you]. It is not now free to me to philosophize at greater length about these things. For it is neither the place for it, nor is it committed to letters safely enough.</p><p>I gladly hear that the second volume of our work, <i>On the more recent phenomena of the heaven</i>, was welcome to you. And it is gratifying to me that the maximum latitude of the Moon was found by you nearly the same as I indicate there in chapter 3. There wrote to me not so long ago the most distinguished and learned man Master Heinrich Brucaeus, professor among the Rostockers of Galenic medicine and Aristotelian philosophy, especially celebrated, and also exceedingly skilled in mathematical matters, especially geometrical ones; and he called into doubt this assertion of mine concerning the changed latitude of the Moon, and set against [it] the authority of Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, and similar eminent mathematicians, [arguing] that they had never detected any such thing, and because it would stand in the way of the magnitude of eclipses, which otherwise corresponds well enough to calculation. But I answered him that the authorities of these excellent astronomers detract nothing from my findings, inasmuch as they wholly acquiesced, in this part, in the Ptolemaic arrangement, nor did they themselves institute any sure experimentation [to test] whether the matter held thus to a nicety or not — although authorities in mathematics, if anywhere, have least weight of themselves, unless there be added an evident demonstration founded on sure observations. And I indicated that in eclipses this difference is scarcely perceptible, owing to the proximity of the nodes. Then in turn I cast back at him your observations too, by which you assert that you have detected the same [thing] almost as I [have]. I therefore desire to learn further from you in what manner you instituted this investigation, and what, and how much, you observed to be changed in the latitude of the Moon — so that to him and to others, not acquiescing in the findings of one [man], I may further show that the suffrage of another also, who diligently inspects the heaven together [with me] (which is too carelessly neglected by those men, although they wish to be held astronomers), makes for me. Although I have resolved to draw forth from my observations, and to publish, nothing that is not of itself sufficiently sure and explored, and that needs the suffrage of no other.</p><p>But as for what you say — that the Head of the Dragon [the ascending node] is distant from the tables by more than four degrees, and that you have found this by distances and by the globe — I could wish that you would indicate more fully how you gathered it; for this seems to me, with the apparent magnitude of eclipses safe, together with their time, by no means consistent. Inasmuch as a difference of four degrees from the nodes would alter the latitude of the Moon by 21 minutes — that is, by nearly two-thirds of the lunar body — which would surely induce a quite perceptible discrepancy in the appearances of eclipses; such as, however, in many eclipses, not only lunar but also solar, which I have diligently noted for several years back, I have not been able to observe. For although the times of eclipses, by whatever calculation hitherto in use, have not duly agreed with the heaven itself, but have sometimes anticipated [them] by a sensible difference, at times greater, at times smaller, or have shown themselves later, yet as to the form of the magnitudes I have never found so evident a diversity as your alterations of the nodes require. For not much more discrepancy could be known in this part than such as the aberration in the motion of the luminaries — and so in the time of the syzygies — was able to suggest, the nodes themselves remaining as nearly as possible in their place, where the Alphonsine and Copernican tables (which differ very little in these) put them. I therefore ask that you declare further, after a more diligent examination has been made, why you so determine. For it is not seemly to charge the tables, without cause, with so evident an error in the intersections of the ecliptic and of the lunar path.</p><p>You report, moreover, that in the book sent [you] you found nothing of what I think about the matter of comets and their generation, and you assert this rightly indeed. For nowhere there do I treat of this matter expressly; but I refer this and most similar questions, and many other things, both to other places and also to the very last clause of the book, into the Epilogue of the whole work. In the meantime, however, on page 254, where I weigh the fourth chapter of Maestlin's writing on this comet, you will find these words: \"I judge the matter of all comets to be wholly celestial, inasmuch as all are generated even in the heaven itself. But whence this matter is supplied to them in the ethereal region, and how they are procreated there, [I shall set out] in its place, in the Conclusion of the whole work, where I shall expound my opinion.\" From these you could in some way have gathered what I judge concerning their matter — namely that it is not elemental, nor compounded out of terrestrial vapors, as you think. But when, God willing, the whole work has been brought to its colophon, then I shall discuss the opinion both of the ancient philosophers and also of the moderns — among whom also your opinion on the matter and generation of comets — and I shall give assurance, by clear and sure reasons, that it can by no means really stand. Meanwhile let the things I deliver and demonstrate concerning the motion and appearances of such phenomena suffice; for I judged that the physical consideration, which thence is for the most part to be derived, should not be confusedly mixed in, that the due method might be preserved. But after I shall have called back to the anvil of truth, through their appearances, not only the new star (which also brings no small aid to investigating this business more deeply), but all the comets diligently observed by me — employing also certain others previously seen, wherever the matter requires it — then at last it will be consequent to subjoin the physical and astrological consideration, with a certain general indication. For this matter is not of small moment, but has been duly treated before by very few, not to say by absolutely none. And therefore it must be gone about not perfunctorily or promiscuously, but in seemly order and with due diligence.</p><p>As for the drawing-out of the tail, I showed that in the comet of the year 1577 it always regarded the star of Venus, although afterward I rather inclined to the parts of the Sun, on account of some hidden optical reason, since it can scarcely be believed that the star of Venus shoots forth such strong rays; though now at last I can to some extent perceive that this is not so foreign to the star of Venus, as I then suspected, since the comet directed its course not far from its [Venus's] revolutions. For the star of Venus seemed to me sometimes to illuminate, quite sensibly, that part of the Moon which was averted from the Sun and turned toward it [Venus], although the Moon stood far more remote from the circuits of Venus than the comet [did]. I find that Giovanni Battista Benedetti, a Venetian patrician, philosopher and especially excellent mathematician, observed something similar, in that learned work which he entitled <i>On mathematical and physical speculations</i>. For thus, at the end of a letter, writing to a certain Savoyard baron, Philibert, he says: \"It manifestly appears, when the Moon is found, according to longitude, between the Sun and Venus, that the part of the Moon destitute of the Sun's light is somewhat illuminated by the light of Venus — which I have often seen, and have shown to many.\" Thus he. Yet, to confess the truth, neither does this reasoning so satisfy me that I would dare to attribute to Venus so great a power of rays as could have procreated the comet's tail. Be it true, as to the reason of the tail, whatever you will, and whether there lurk in these things something optical — why they do not always regard the Sun exactly — or not: certainly that which you intend can by no means subsist, namely that the tail is a part of the comet's body, and of the same substance as the head, and appears in the same manner by illumination. For if this held altogether thus, so many comets observed by Regiomontanus, Fracastoro, Apian, Gemma Frisius, and others would not always have turned their coma to the parts nearly opposite the Sun, as one may gather from their writings; which was the case very nearly also in those that have been observed by us. For there is no reason why the protraction of the comet's body lengthwise, if it were of the same substance as the head itself, should always be turned away well-nigh from the Sun. And we saw in the hairy [star] of the year [15]77, whose tail, especially at the beginning, was very notable, that it was exceedingly rare [thin], and appeared after the manner of thin rays, so much so that certain stars which it passed by sometimes shone through it. Nor was it of the same color as the head, but it kept altering this too — which however I judge happened on account of the diverse quality of the interposed air, especially near the horizon. But the head itself was solidly compacted, and far brighter than the tail, and was seen exactly round, so much so that at the beginning you would have called it a little Moon. The hairs themselves too were joined to the head in a point, and afterward were diffused broad and long; whence one could easily gather that that tail was procreated by the penetration of some external light, in whatever manner it happened, and from wherever it came. The same too could be observed in other comets, and the more evidently the greater and more brilliant they shone. Besides, those that seemed to tend toward the opposite of the Sun showed a tail either none or very small, turning it rather toward the upper parts. But those nearer to this [the Sun] were discerned to put forth a beard more and more — which appeared distinctly in the comet of the year [15]80 and [15]82; so that of that one which in the year [15]85 was thought to be wholly devoid of a tail — which each of us, and no other, that I know, diligently observed — I shall not say much. This one, I judge, showed no tail for the reason that it moved perpetually near the opposite of the Sun, the excessive remoteness from us also contributing somewhat here. If, therefore, the tail were some part of their body, and consisted of the same matter, it would by no means preserve such a relation to the Sun. Nor indeed are the things you object concerning the passage of rays in pure air without matter sufficiently seen into as yet. For although the heaven be most tenuous, yet it is not altogether immaterial or incorporeal. What would you say to this, that Aristotle reports in chapter 6 of book 1 of the <i>Meteorologica</i>, that some of the wandering stars sometimes acquire a coma — which he hints the Egyptians handed down, to whom too he thinks credit should be given, inasmuch as he himself saw a certain star in the thigh of the Dog-star [Canicula] to have had a coma, albeit obscure, in such a way that those who fixed the keenness of their eyes upon it saw an obscure light, but those who looked lightly [casually] saw it more clearly. I, for my part, judge that the matter holds thus, and that this is often seen in several stars too, in Egypt or another similar place where the air is liable to the least vapors — when especially it is at its purest; but not so by us, dwelling in a grosser air. For those rays are exceedingly tenuous, nor are they open to the eyes, if the intermediate transparent medium be less clarified.</p><p>Yet, to confirm your opinion from some celestial observation, you object to us an observation of your Most Illustrious Prince, made by means of the torquetum, in the comet of the year 1558; from which you asseverate that it is manifestly gathered that its tail regarded neither the Sun nor any other of the planets, but appeared of itself, by its own course. I confess indeed that, if that record which was had on the 13th of the Kalends of September [20 August] — the only day on which the course of the tail is assigned — is to be taken as valid, then, the arc of a great circle being set, according to our reckonings, from the Sun in 6 degrees 36 minutes of ♍ Virgo, drawn out through the comet's head toward the last [star] in the tail of Ursa Major, does not approach it within 10½ degrees, but deflects just as much from this toward Boötes; so much so that the Sun would have had to have traversed the 21st degree of ♍ Virgo, if it, and the head and tail of the comet, together with the said star, were set in the same arc. But although not even here does so utterly immoderate a misshapen deviation occur — given what could be discerned at first glance from sight alone — that sufficient occasion is thereby afforded to conclude from this [something] plainly diverse from the observation of very many in other comets: yet, because this difference seems to you excessive, as it is in itself quite ample, I shall adduce something else to the contrary about this same comet, from the observations of the most distinguished and learned man Master Cornelius Gemma, who in the second book <i>On the divine characterisms of nature</i>, chapter one, where he proposes some things about this very [comet], besides that he says it stood in the west and stretched its tail toward the eastern region (and therefore to the parts opposite the Sun, which had already set), chiefly records this observation: that on the 20th of August — which is plainly the same as that on which the Most Illustrious Prince designated the comet together with the course of the tail toward the last [star] of Ursa Major — in the evening he measured its distance from Arcturus at 30 degrees 32 minutes, and from that very [star] which is at the end of the tail of Ursa Major at 28 degrees 33 minutes. And since for that year the longitude of Arcturus was in 18 degrees 4 minutes of ♎ Libra, latitude 31 degrees 2 minutes north, and [that] of the [star] which is in the tail of the Bear [in] 20 degrees 44 minutes of ♍ Virgo, latitude 54 degrees 25 minutes north, according to our restitution of the places of the fixed stars: it will follow, from the assigned intervals, that the place of the comet fell, as to longitude, in 13 degrees 36 minutes of ♍ Virgo, with a latitude of 26 degrees 23 minutes north — which differs quite sensibly from the annotation which you adduce. But if, through this place, the great arc be drawn from the Sun dwelling in 6 degrees 36 minutes of ♍ Virgo, as I said, toward the last [star] of the Bear's tail, it will approach near enough to that star, so that it really comes within 3¼ degrees of it, digressing only a very little toward Boötes; so much so that if the Sun's place be assumed only three degrees further on, namely in 9⅔ of ♍ Virgo, the head of the comet, the drawing-out of the tail, and the last [star] in Ursa Major coincide exactly in one arc of a great circle. But this not-so-great discrepancy easily merits excuse among those who understand this business. Inasmuch as the course of the tail was at least noted by sight, which in so great a distance could easily insinuate an aberration of three degrees. For supreme precision is not elicited in this way, and it is likely that the Most Illustrious Prince, since no other clear star occurred so near by which the extension of the tail might be designated, seized this one as the nearest, from which too the indication of the tail differed imperceptibly, so far as could be discerned by sight. It appears, therefore, how little aid you obtain from this for establishing your opinion concerning the beards of comets and overturning that of others, which has been confirmed by many examples — since rather the contrary to your induction follows from this. For that the tail of this comet too observed [faced] the parts opposite the Sun fairly closely, those weighing the whole matter circumspectly, without prejudice, will not unwillingly concede. Make at least the trial yourself on the globe, and mark the place of the comet according to the distances measured by Cornelius Gemma from the said fixed stars, and draw out its tail somewhat from the body into the parts contrary to the Sun placed in 6⅗ of ♍ Virgo, and the face of the heaven being disposed such as it was near the ninth hour of that day, so that 21 degrees of ♏ Scorpio occupies the meridian; and the globe being thus fixed, look to where the tail is directed at first glance: you will assuredly see it tend as it were toward the zenith, and, if it were understood to be produced [extended], to meet no notable star nearer than that which holds the last place in the tail of Ursa Major. For although there is a certain very small one in the left arm of Boötes, which it more nearly regarded, yet since this is at least of the fifth magnitude, it could not be discerned. And since no other in that tract lay evidently open to the eyes, toward which the protension of the tail might be more nearly directed than this very oft-mentioned star of Ursa Major, the Most Illustrious Prince justly seized this one as indicated by the tail — which ocular sight too sufficiently proves — neglecting that very small difference which did not even readily lie open to sight alone. But that one ought rather to stand by the place of the comet which is derived from Cornelius's observations admits no doubt. For he, in the very scruples [minutes], by means of the cross-staff [radius astronomicus] (an instrument diligently perfected by his father), subtly measured the distances of the comet from two more conspicuous fixed stars, which with it constituted a nearly equilateral triangle (which makes not a little toward inquiring after the certainty of the place from this). And although the cross-staff of itself does not everywhere afford sufficiently precise distances within a few minutes, especially in the larger intervals, yet its aberration is not so great as to dispose the comet here otherwise than it ought by more than a quarter of a degree — which variation in no way disproves what we intend. But the matter stands far otherwise with the torquetum, by which instrument the observations of the Most Illustrious Prince were taken. For even if it were of just size, yet it is intricately composed of too many and diverse parts, and many things are required for the disposition of the several ones, which during observation are with difficulty managed at once, so much so that while one is guarded against, another is easily neglected; and any one of them by itself can suggest some sensible error. I say nothing of the fact that the instrument itself too too much burdens itself by its own mass, since its dispositions are not adapted to its turning according to equilibrium. So that there is none among all the astronomical instruments devised by the ancients which exhibits to the observer a less ready and assured certainty — however ingeniously its structure [be devised], by which the Arabs, the first inventors (as I think) of this, tried to reduce the armillae used by Hipparchus and Ptolemy into a certain flat framework of circles, more compendiously and at less cost. I now say nothing of the fact that the Most Illustrious Prince's torquetum, by which those observations were performed — although it was made of solid brass — was nonetheless too small, as I saw, when fifteen years ago I tarried in person for some days with the Most Illustrious Prince at Kassel. For he then had two such torqueta, neither of which presented a sufficiently capacious size, and both indeed had long since been antiquated by His Highness, because they by no means exhibited sure markings of the heavenly bodies, the Prince himself even hinting as much. It therefore easily happened, observing through a torquetum not so large, that a deviation of several degrees from the mark was committed — which by the benefit of the cross-staff, with the investigation sought from the distances of the stars, could safely enough be guarded against; and the tail, which of itself appeared thin, as one may gather from the very observations of the Most Illustrious Prince, in its extension not with difficulty, as to its long interval, suggested some frustration to the sight. Wherefore, if from observations of comets you labor to prove that their tail is of the very substance of the body, and is borne of itself, and does not always regard the Sun, it will be necessary that you implore aid from elsewhere; for this comet, which you allege, makes much rather against you than for you, as has now been shown enough and more.</p><p>Now to other matters. The things you object to our reasonings concerning the curvature of the tail make little to the point. If you can find a fitter occasion for the bending of the hairs than [one] from optics, bring it forward. That the tail is, however, the comet's own portion, existing of the same matter, so that for this reason it can of itself exhibit various forms — this I shall by no means have conceded to you. Not otherwise do you less aptly apply the things I wrote about Graminaeus at the end of the work. For I did not say that he, as to the situation of comets, adheres to the Aristotelian opinion, but only that, as to matter, [it] consists of terrestrial fumes, as he [Aristotle] will have it, and [is] drawn up into the lofty ether. But since you yourself confess this to be your opinion too, you assuredly slip into the same absurdities which I shall amply show elsewhere; for it makes no difference whether you call those vapors slow and tenacious, or thin and light, or burning or illuminated, provided you admit them to be terrestrial and sublunary, and raised in whatever manner into the highest heaven. Moreover, since from certain words written to the Most Illustrious Prince — by which I named my work to be published <i>On the new generations of the ethereal world</i> — you gather that I introduce generation into the heaven, and consequently corruption too: you seem here to catch at my words too strictly. For I there took the word \"generation\" in a broader sense, for that which newly exists or appears, not seen before, nor did I sustain the part of the grammarian in the propriety of that expression. But why did you not rather seize on the printed title of the book itself, which I inscribed more circumspectly than is required in any letter, <i>On the new phenomena of the ethereal world</i>, deliberately avoiding the word \"generation,\" that there might be no place for cavil? Elsewhere you will see yourself to be still far from my opinion.</p><p>Concerning the constitution of the celestial hypotheses devised by me, I do not wish at present to bring much forward by way of defense and confirmation; for that would be too prolix, nor would it be sufficiently accomplished without a manifold demonstration sought from sure observations. I shall rather give my effort, that on another occasion, when I am about to treat expressly of the restitution of the celestial motions — the Author of the heaven favoring [me] — it may be known in fact that these our hypotheses satisfy the celestial appearances to a nicety, and far excel both the Ptolemaic and the Copernican, and correspond better to truth itself. But if those [hypotheses] so displease you that you prefer, with Copernicus, to revolve the earth and seas in the heaven yearly together with the neighboring Moon, and so to exalt [the earth] with a threefold motion — though it be one and simple body — beyond the lot of the stars (than which it is nonetheless far inferior and more unfit for moving): for my part that too will be open and entire to you. Nor is the way now difficult, since you have mingled elemental air with the heaven and the revolutions of the stars; why not the earth and seas too? Is this not to confound the lower regions with the upper, and to invert the whole order of the nature of things? Yet, lest you be deceived: there is no [reason] that you should persuade yourself that the physical absurdities which accompany the Copernican hypothesis have been sufficiently refuted by him; nor do the things adduced by you hither confirm the whole matter beyond doubt, as I shall show elsewhere, discussing these and many more of the kind. Much less will the things put forward by you in excusing those passages in which Sacred Scripture asserts the contrary be able to merit a place. For the authority and reverence of the divine letters both is and ought to be greater than that it should be seemly for them to be dragged this way, after the manner of a buskin [cothurnus]. For although they themselves, in physical matters and certain others, for the most part accommodate themselves to the grasp of the common crowd, yet far be it that on that account we should hold them to speak so vulgarly [that we should not] also believe them to put forward true things. Thus Moses, although in the first chapter of Genesis, treating of the creation of the world, he does not unlock the inner shrines of astronomy — writing as he was for an uncultivated people — nonetheless brings forward nothing that could not be conceded even by the astronomers themselves. For what you think follows from him — that the Moon, contrary to geometrical demonstrations, would be larger than the rest of the stars — the Text itself does not admit. For it has it thus: \"And God made two great lights, the greater for the ruling of the day, the lesser for [that] of the night, and the stars too, and he placed them in the fluid heaven to illuminate the lands.\" Here Moses speaks at least about the magnitude of the light by which the Sun and Moon illuminate the lands beyond the other stars, not about the quantity of bodies. And therefore he calls the Moon the lesser light, because it does not illuminate the earth in the nocturnal time so much as the Sun in the diurnal. For if he had understood these things of the magnitude of the body, and had accommodated himself at least to the common crowd's view, he would not have called the Moon less than the Sun; inasmuch as its visible diameter is certainly discerned scarcely less than the Sun's, if not rather, for the most part, somewhat larger. But no astronomer would deny that the Moon, after the Sun, is the great luminary before the other heavenly bodies, and that it illuminates the earth by night far more than the rest, and dispels the darkness. Thus the astrologers themselves call the Sun and Moon — the latter the luminary of the night, the former of the day — which agrees exceedingly well with Moses. About the other stars I do not now dispute, whether round about, near their boundaries, they pour out more brightness than the Moon itself, although on account of the excessive distance that light does not strike the earth. For Moses too in that place was treating at least of this light which touches the lands and serves the use of living creatures. So too you detract too much from the Prophets, when you say that they understood no more of the nature of things than other common men. For although they did not treat physics expressly — for that was not [part] of their office — yet they mingle into their oracles many things drawn from physics, which no one, however much imbued with natural philosophy, will be able to gainsay. Nor does it follow from this that they were ignorant of astronomy and physics, which flowed down to them even from the first patriarchs, because they treat of these things more sparingly. For it was not seemly to mingle philosophical and worldly things too much with the theological mysteries which they chiefly treated. The authority of the sacred letters therefore still stands unmoved, as we shall see more fully in its place. Nor will the position of Copernicus be vindicated from this by any effort of yours.</p><p>But as to what you object to my invention of the hypotheses — a certain violent dragging, and a confusion of order, and a blemish of retrogradation in the five planets — we do not delay over it. For it is consonant [with reason] that it is not a certain involuntary dragging, but a natural and divinely implanted concomitance and observance, by which the five planets always regard the Sun, wherever it go, in the middle [meditullium] of their revolutions. And by what corporeal and tenacious matter, I ask, are the magnet and iron coupled, so that the one seeks the other, even with another body interposed? If this can happen naturally, without violence, in terrestrial inanimate things, what [shall we say] of celestial things, which the Platonists and the wiser of the philosophizers would not deny to be animate and to have a divinely innate knowledge, wonderful and more excellent than is thought? Weigh more diligently those things which are adduced by Pliny at the end of the 16th chapter of book 2, concerning the reason of the stations and regressions of the three superior planets — which, although they seem ambiguous and obscure or altogether absurd, yet are not all plainly about nothing. And it is sufficiently gathered hence that the most ancient mathematicians, who also set the earth resting in the middle of the world, were wholly ignorant of epicycles, and by some other more occult reasoning referred those appearances, which are thence saved, to the Sun; nor did they judge it inconvenient that the planets be so subject to the Sun, although no material connection intervened. But the Text itself will admonish you of more. Nor is any confusion committed in my hypotheses; for the heaven is single and like to itself from the Moon to the eighth sphere, in which the planets freely ascend and descend, as they go around the Sun walking in the middle in a most beautiful harmony. For I do not believe that you wish again to introduce some real distinction of the celestial orbs, contrary to what was long ago agreed between us. The retrogradation of the planets, which is discerned with respect to us and to the eighth sphere, afflicts them with no fault, inasmuch as, with respect to themselves, they always regard the Sun in the same manner, uniformly intent on their destined course. But these things, and many others which can perhaps be plausibly inferred by some against our reckoning of the motions, I shall in their time, God breathing upon our endeavors, discuss sufficiently.</p><p>But I did not take the occasion of constructing these hypotheses from the Copernican [ones] inverted; and if you ever thought any such thing, that, as you well enough know, was not made known to me, nor was anything similar ever to be gathered from Rheticus or Reinhold. But when I had observed, by subtle and accurate observation — especially that had in the year [15]82 — that Mars at acronychal [rising, in opposition to the Sun] becomes nearer to the earth than the Sun itself, and that on that account the long-received Ptolemaic hypotheses cannot stand; and moreover that comets seen in the heaven and dwelling opposite the Sun are not rendered subject to the earth's annual motion (although they are not so far distant that it [the motion] plainly vanishes, as happens in the fixed stars), [and] that the Copernican assumption too in the earth's motion totters: there remained no other way of saving the celestial appearances than this very one which has lately been put forward by me with a general indication. If, therefore, I shall have demonstrated those two [points] to hold thus, then assuredly it will be sufficiently confirmed that neither the Ptolemaic nor the Copernican hypothesis agrees with the truth of the matter, but this our [hypothesis] alone — [to say] nothing now of many other things which make not a little hither. For it does not seem convenient to report particular and individual matters in this place.</p><p>I greatly wondered, at first, when I read in your letters that these my hypotheses had been previously known to the Most Illustrious Prince — inasmuch as he had them expressed in a certain automaton [model]. Nor could I sufficiently think how this could come about, since I was perfectly conscious to myself that I had learned these from no mortal before I myself was led thither from observations, and that meanwhile they had lain hidden with me, not previously published. But after the little book of that Ursus of Dithmarschen — whom you once, in certain letters given to me, adorned with a worthy eulogy, [now] calling [him] an impure scoundrel — had been sent to me (and that by the most learned man Master Georg Rollenhagen, dwelling at Magdeburg, who also had indicated long before that these our hypotheses had been communicated to him two years earlier by a certain fugitive servant of mine; and therefore, knowing them to have been first invented by me, and grieving that that Ursus undeservedly attributed them to himself, he wished that book to become known to me, so that I might recognize that theft and again claim for myself what had been cast out abroad by others): from that I sufficiently perceived whence this ordering of the celestial revolutions had reached you. Inasmuch as that plagiarist reports that, about three years ago, after he had devised this in a certain corner of the kingdom of Poland (understand rather on the island Hven of the kingdom of Denmark, when in the year [15]84, in September, he was present here with the most noble and learned man Master Erik Lange, my singular friend, whom he was then serving, [these things] secretly snatched from me, like certain others too), he offered [them] to the Most Illustrious Prince of Hesse, and they were reduced into a brazen automaton by His Highness's craftsman Jost Bürgi. But that impostor sufficiently betrays his theft, and along with it his ignorance of the matter which he peddles. For while he keeps the orb of Mars wholly encompassing [the orbit around] the Sun, he imitates a certain false delineation, which, by I know not what carelessness, was here so depicted, and on that account, as inept, thrown away somewhere among my papers — which I still have at hand, sought out under the same name, that the very thing might plainly appear. And since he could not amend that fault committed in depicting the diagram, he sufficiently makes known to the intelligent that he does not understand that invention, much less is its author — however he has not blushed to arrogate to himself what was got together here, impudently, and to dedicate it to the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm as his own contrivance. Besides, he has many other things in that little book, which, although he has not carried them all off from here, yet he has partly scraped together from you, partly from other mathematicians in Germany, whether secretly or openly; so much so that, if the things belonging to others be taken away, almost nothing remains that will be his. But let that fellow be off with his plagiarism — to you and other learned men, among whom he once was, [it is] sufficiently clear who he is, and how brazen-faced and arrogant a trifler. These things I wished to answer briefly concerning my hypotheses; more you will understand in a more convenient place and time, which without doubt (unless you have sworn too obstinately to the words and opinion of Copernicus) will satisfy you.</p><p>That I could have prefixed in every respect a more fitting hypothesis for the comet of the year [15]77 — namely, out of several composite circular motions — I myself had it well enough known, and this is sufficiently indicated in the book itself, and the reason added why I forbore to furnish it; whence there was no need of that replication. But to have proposed this very hypothesis which I indicated there is not without a certain utility, as you suppose; for thus it is the more confirmed that comets are really celestial, and that their motions are not stirred up tumultuously. You rightly excused yourself to the Most Illustrious Prince [in saying] that that second volume was not transmitted to His Highness but to you, and as it were in my name you promised that, when the whole work has been finished, I would communicate it entire to His Highness. For this I determined beforehand with myself, and there is no [reason] for you to doubt that your faith [pledge] in this part is to be redeemed. The remaining two princes too will have copies, as you advise. The Most Illustrious Maurice, the Landgrave's son — whom I rejoice to have advanced so much in letters and languages — would that he had also tasted astronomical matters, that he might thus also succeed his father, laudably practiced in these. Likewise the Most Illustrious Bernhard of Anhalt will not be to be passed over, since he is imbued with the sacred [things] of mathematics. But that the first volume did not come out at this fair — the impediment was that lately I did not obtain the printing-paper for which I had sent, the excessive abundance of waters delaying the messenger, and on account of certain other obstacles which it is not pertinent to relate. But as for those things which you judge to make for you concerning your observation in ♀ Venus and ☉ the Sun, made for the verification of the fixed stars, and what I think about this matter, I have answered briefly in a letter given to the Most Illustrious Prince; wherefore it is not worth the trouble to repeat them here. Nor is the opportunity granted me of writing more now — nay, I have scratched out these very things in haste, not only [jotting them down] among many other occupations as they came to mind in passing, but moreover laboring under a heavy pain of the forehead and of the half-head [hemicrania], on account of a head-cold which a few days before I had contracted from the nocturnal chill while observing, [and which was] prematurely stopped up [congested] — so that I have conceived these very things with the greatest difficulty. For there is not so much leisure left over that it is free [to me] to attend to medication. It will therefore be yours, in these difficulties, candidly to interpret things heaped together less neatly, and, if leisure and health did not allow [me] to reply now to everything point by point, to take in good part these things written back in a general way, and not to cease loving me, as you do, and more often, as opportunity is offered, to revisit [me] with letters. Farewell. Given at Uraniborg, 21 February, the year 1589. Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, mathematician/astronomer at the Hesse-Kassel court of <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse</b> (<i>Illustrissimus Princeps Wilhelmus Landtgravius Hassiae</i>). This is one of Tycho's most substantial polemical letters in the famous Tycho–Rothmann exchange (printed by Tycho himself in the 1596 <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>, Liber I). It joins issue on (1) the substance of the heavens, (2) twilight/refraction, (3) lunar latitude and the nodes, (4) the nature of comet tails, (5) the geoheliocentric (\"Tychonic\") hypotheses, (6) Scripture vs. Copernicanism, and (7) the Ursus plagiarism.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> The source's `edition` field gives <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596) = Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI; no MS witness is listed in the dataset.</li><li><b>Salutation/subscription.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" — here rendered \"heartiest greeting\" in the courtesy register; <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\"; <i>Datae</i> = \"Given.\" Per glossary §5.5.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline <i>Datae Vraniburgi 21. Feb. Anno 1589</i> matches the catalogue `label` (\"1589. 21. februar. Uraniborg\"); no discrepancy — Old Style, the Danish norm. <b>In-text Roman-calendar idiom:</b> <i>13. Calendarum Septembris</i> = the 13th day before the Kalends of September = <b>20 August</b> (inclusive Roman counting); glossed in the translation, not silently converted. Tycho himself confirms the equivalence in the next sentence (\"<i>die 20. Augusti, qui plane idem est…</i>\"), so the gloss is internally substantiated.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Franciscus Vallesius</i> = <b>Francisco Valles</b> (1524–1592), Spanish physician-philosopher (\"el Divino\"), author of <i>De sacra philosophia</i>.</li><li><i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Rostock; glossary §5.6).</li><li><i>Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Wernerus</i> (<b>Johannes Werner</b>), <i>Rheticus, Reinholdus</i> (<b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>) — the mathematical-astronomical authorities Tycho weighs.</li><li><i>Ioannes Baptista Benedictus</i> = <b>Giovanni Battista Benedetti</b> (1530–1590), Venetian patrician, mathematician at the Savoy court; the cited work is his <i>Diversarum speculationum mathematicarum et physicarum liber</i> (Turin, 1585); the <i>Baron Sabaudus Philibertus</i> is the Savoyard addressee of the closing letter cited.</li><li><i>Regiomontanus, Fracastorius</i> (<b>Girolamo Fracastoro</b>), <i>Apianus</i> (<b>Peter Apian</b>), <i>Gemma Frisius</i> — earlier comet observers cited for the anti-solar tail.</li><li><i>Cornelius Gemma</i> = <b>Cornelius Gemma</b> (1535–1578), son of Gemma Frisius (hence <i>Instrumentum a Patre suo diligenter excultum</i>, \"an instrument diligently perfected by his father\"); cited work <i>De naturae divinis characterismis</i> (Antwerp, 1575).</li><li><i>Maesthlinianum scriptum</i> = the writing of <b>Michael Maestlin</b> on the 1577 comet (<i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae</i>, 1578); Kepler's teacher.</li><li><i>Graminaeus</i> = <b>Theodor Graminaeus</b> (Dietrich Graminäus), Cologne mathematician who wrote on the 1577 comet.</li><li><i>Ursus Dithmarsicus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus (\"Bär\")</b> of Dithmarschen; <i>Georgius Rollenhagius</i> = <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b>, Magdeburg schoolman/poet; <i>Ericus Lange</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b>, Danish nobleman-alchemist (Tycho's friend, in whose service Ursus visited Hven in Sept. 1584); <i>Iustus Byrgius</i> = <b>Jost Bürgi</b>, the Kassel court clockmaker/instrument-maker. <i>Mauritius, Landtgravii Filius</i> = <b>Moritz of Hesse-Kassel</b> (later Landgrave Moritz \"the Learned\"); <i>Bernhardus Anhaltinus</i> = <b>Bernhard of Anhalt</b>.</li><li><i>Moses</i> / <i>Prophetae</i> — Scriptural authority; the quotation conflates <b>Genesis 1:16–17</b> (the \"two great lights\"); <i>liquidum Caelum</i>, \"fluid heaven,\" reflects Tycho's own fluid-heavens reading rather than the firmament of the received text — flagged as Tycho's paraphrase, not a verbatim Vulgate citation.</li><li><b>Technical terms (glossary-consistent).</b> <i>Refractio</i> → refraction; <i>Crepusculum</i> → twilight; <i>Nadir / Zenith (vertex)</i> → kept; <i>Quadrans Altitudinis</i> → altitude-quadrant; <i>Caput Draconis</i> → \"Head of the Dragon,\" glossed [the ascending node]; <i>Nodi</i> → nodes; <i>Latitudo Lunae</i> → latitude of the Moon; <i>Zyzigiae</i> → syzygies; <i>Acronychus</i> → acronychal (rising in opposition to the Sun); <i>Retrogradatio</i> → retrogradation; <i>Hypotheses</i> → hypotheses; <i>Aequinoctium vernum</i> → vernal equinox; <i>Meridianus / Finitor / Horizon</i> → meridian / horizon (<i>Finitor</i> is the bounding horizon-circle); <i>Circulus verticalis</i> → vertical circle. <i>Radius Astronomicus</i> → <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b> (§5.2); <i>Torquetum</i> → torquetum (kept; the medieval instrument Tycho disparages); <i>Armillae</i> → armillae (§5.2); <i>Organa Astronomica</i> → astronomical instruments; <i>Globus</i> → globe. <i>Restitutio / redintegratio (Astronomiae)</i> → \"restitution / restoration (of astronomy)\" — Tycho's <i>instauratio</i> programme (§5.1). <i>Diaphanum / Diaphanitas</i> → \"transparent medium / transparency.\"</li><li><b>Star/coordinate names rendered, glyphs kept in Source.</b> <i>Baltheus Orionis</i> = the Belt of Orion; <i>Humerus dexter Orionis</i> = Betelgeuse (right Shoulder of Orion); <i>Spica ♍</i> = Spica; <i>Arcturus</i>; <i>cauda Vrsae Maioris</i> = the tail of Ursa Major (Alkaid, the last star); <i>sinister lacertus Bootis</i> = the left arm of Boötes; <i>Booten</i> (Greek acc.) = Boötes; <i>Canicula</i> = the Lesser Dog / Procyon region (Aristotle's example). Zodiac glyphs ♓ ♍ ♎ ♏ named Pisces / Virgo / Libra / Scorpio; planet glyphs ♀ ☉ named Venus / Sun (§5.3), glyphs retained in the Source block. <i>Part. / Min. / Grad.</i> rendered \"degrees / minutes\"; fractional degrees (2¼, 3⅔, 6⅗, 9⅔, 16⅚) reproduced as printed.</li><li><b>Natural-philosophy / alchemical idiom.</b> <i>Quinta Essentia</i> → \"fifth essence\" (quintessence). <i>Tria principia Paracelsi</i> → \"the three principles\" (Paracelsus's <i>tria prima</i>: salt, sulphur, mercury), which Tycho reads as an image of the Trinity. <i>Mumia</i> (Terrae / Aëria / Aquea) → kept as <i>mumia</i> (the Paracelsian vital-matter term — not \"mummy\"); flagged below. <i>Iliad / in Iliado suae naturae</i> → \"an Iliad analogous to its own nature\" — <i>Ilias</i> here used in the sense of a \"world / abundance proper to\" a thing; an unusual usage, kept literally and flagged. <i>Phlegma</i> → \"phlegm\" (alchemical: the watery, inert fraction). <i>eleuatio / per saepius reiteratam eleuationem</i> → \"elevation\" (i.e. repeated distillation), glossed. <i>Orichalcum</i> → brass.</li><li><b>Rhetorical / classical idiom.</b> <i>in modum Cothurni</i> → \"after the manner of a buskin [<i>cothurnus</i>]\" — i.e. stretched to fit like the cothurnus that fits either foot; a proverbial figure for twisting a text to suit any meaning; kept as image. <i>sub incudem veritatis reuocare</i> → \"call back to the anvil of truth.\" <i>ad amussim / ad vnguem</i> → \"to a nicety / exactly.\" <i>Colophon / ad Colophonem</i> → \"to the colophon\" (= to completion). <i>perfrictae frontis</i> → \"brazen-faced\" (lit. of a rubbed/hardened brow). <i>Nebulo</i> → \"scoundrel.\"</li><li><b>Substantive cruxes / hedges preserved.</b></li><li><i>meditullium</i> — the planets regard the Sun \"in the <b>meditullium</b> of their revolutions\": the central point/midst of their orbits; rendered \"the middle\" with the technical sense flagged. This is the core statement of the Tychonic geoheliocentric arrangement.</li><li>The magnet–iron analogy (<i>Magnes et Ferrum … vnum alterum appetat</i>) is Tycho's physical defence of sun-tethered planetary motion <i>without</i> material orbs; rendered straight, the period sense (\"appetite/seeking\") kept.</li><li>Tycho's hedges are preserved throughout and <b>not sharpened</b>: <i>aliquatenus persentiscere queam</i> (\"I can to some extent perceive\"); <i>vix credi possit</i> (\"it can scarcely be believed\"); <i>vt verum fatear, neque haec ratiocinatio mihi ita satisfacit</i> (\"to confess the truth, neither does this reasoning so satisfy me\"); <i>quod tamen nondum satis obtentum est</i> (\"which however has not yet been sufficiently attained\"); <i>non omnia sunt plane de nihilo</i> (\"are not all plainly about nothing\"). The parenthetical correction <i>(intellige potius in Insula … Huena)</i> — \"understand rather on the island Hven\" — is Tycho's own bracket-style interjection inside the Source; rendered as his parenthesis, not an editorial one.</li><li><i>libellus … ollus istius Vrsi</i> (l. 449 of source): the printed form reads <i>lib ollus</i>, evidently <i>libellus</i> (\"little book\") broken across a line; the intended word <i>libellus</i> is adopted in translation and the printed split kept in the Source block.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Tycho's chapter-3 self-citation and active verb ('as I indicate there in chapter 3', for 'capit. 3. indico') where the published English had recast it as a passive 'indication [section] 3'. Corrected the constellation gloss 'the Lesser Dog [Canicula]' to 'the Dog-star [Canicula]' (Canicula = Sirius in Canis Major, not Canis Minor). No change to the Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The astronomical, polemical, and Scriptural argument is rendered securely and the negations (notably the careful double-negative defences of Scripture and of the planet–Sun \"concomitance, not violent dragging\") have been counted and preserved. Residual soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (a) <i>Mumia</i> and the <i>in Iliado</i> phrase are deliberately obscure Paracelsian/poetic coinages kept literal; (b) <i>meditullium</i> as the precise geometric referent of the Tychonic centring; (c) the Genesis quotation is Tycho's paraphrase (<i>liquidum Caelum</i>), not the Vulgate verbatim. None rises to a sense-reversing crux, hence [B] rather than [C].</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo.slice_pdf is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible; per brief, graded at most B on that basis alone. Audited the full letter (source_text ~42,000 characters Latin / published_english ~50,000 characters) paragraph by paragraph against source_text: checked every date (10, 12, 15 Feb; 1558; 1577/80/82/85; 1582; 1584 Sept.), every degree/minute value in the twilight-observation and 1558-comet/Cornelius-Gemma passages (over 30 numeric values, all exact), every proper name (Rothmann, Vallesius, Paracelsus, Brucaeus, Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Werner, Maestlin, Benedetti/Philibert, Fracastoro, Apian, Gemma Frisius, Cornelius Gemma, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Rheticus, Reinhold, Erik Lange, Georg Rollenhagen, Ursus, Jost Bürgi, Landgrave Wilhelm, Maurice, Bernhard of Anhalt), page/chapter citations (Pag. 254 cap. 4 Maestlin; Pliny lib. 2 cap. 16; Aristotle Meteor. lib.1 cap.6), and the opening/closing formulae and dateline (Uraniborg, 21 Feb. 1589). No fabrication, omission, or sense-altering mistranslation found; two low-severity mistranslations noted above (a garbled book-chapter citation, and a constellation-identification slip in an Aristotle quotation).</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"capit. 3.\" is almost certainly an abbreviated chapter citation (\"capite 3\" = chapter 3) to Tycho's own printed book, and \"indico\" is Tycho's own verb (\"I indicate\"); the English recasts the citation as an unexplained \"indication [section] 3\" and shifts the verb to a passive \"it is taken\", losing the citation form and Tycho's agency without changing the substantive claim. At: “the maximum latitude of the Moon was found by you nearly the same as it is taken there, indication [section] 3”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"Canicula\" is the standard Latin name for Sirius/the Dog-star in Canis Major (the star Aristotle references, Meteorologica I.6); \"the Lesser Dog\" specifically denotes the separate constellation Canis Minor (Procyon), so the gloss misidentifies which star/constellation is meant, though it does not affect Tycho's substantive point about faint comae being visible only in very clear air. At: “he himself saw a certain star in the thigh of the Lesser Dog [Canicula] to have had a coma”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the base Latin transcription could not be collated against the edition of record and is reproduced unchanged. Both MISTRANSLATION defects were verified from internal evidence by locating the exact Latin clauses (capit. 3. indico; Caniculae femore) against the published English and confirming the mismatch described in the audit: a citation-plus-active-verb recast as a passive noun phrase, and Canicula (Canis Major/Sirius) mislabeled as the Lesser Dog (Canis Minor). Both clauses have been corrected minimally; nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15881013001", "next": "brahe-t_15890806001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15881013001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890806001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses a delayed reply to Rothmann's most recent letter and engages with his persistent argument that the celestial matter is elemental air, finding his evasions unconvincing.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890221002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890322001", "d": "1589-03-22", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Copenhagen, 22 March 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address line) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.45</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET VARIA ERUDITIONE EXCULTISSIMO DN. THADDAeO HAGECIO AB HAICK, MEDICO ET MATHEMATICO PERITISSIMO, AMICO SUO PERPETUO COLENDO.<br>AHM HERN DOCTOR THADDEO HAGETIO ZU PRAG WONHAFFTIG.<br>S.</p><p>CUM hodie primum a nobili iuvene TAGONE KRABIO nostrate hinc in Caesariam aulam iamiam abituro cognovissem illum Pragam transiturum, non intermittendum duxi, quin aliquid literarum ad te darem, optime et eruditissime mi THADDAeE, praesertim quia nihil adhuc responsi ad ultimas meas receperim, quod tamen indies avide expecto; dilationem vero hanc internunciorum inopiae et hyemis importunitati imputandam censeo, absit enim ut de tua ergo me promta voluntate quicquam addubitem. Nunc itaque cum per hunc nobilem Danum rescribendi occasio percommodum offeratur, ne responsione ulterius supersedeas, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo, imprimis vero ut de ijs, quae liber noster secundus Cometae anni 77 tibi dudum transmissus continet, mecum ingenue et aperte conferre non gravatim duxeris. Eum enim te iam evolvisse satis mihi persuadeo. Primus liber, qui de Stella Nova affatim tractat, nunc typis meis excuditur; procrastinationi, quae interiecta est, non saltem remoratio Privilegij Gallici Caesariano adiungendi, sed etiam papyri impressoriae defectus praeter expectationem meam ansam dederunt. Nunc vero cum utrisque his remoris satis provisum sit, spero hunc quoque tomum brevi publici iuris futurum. Et quantum per impedimenta ac distractiones (quibus utplurimum aestivo tempore implicor) licebit, operis istius maturationem urgebo. In hoc non solum ea, quae ad Novam Stellam proprie faciunt, accurate et luculenter declarata demonstrataque invenies, sed multa insuper alia ad totius Artis Astronomicae redintegrationem plurimum facientia; quaeque hactenus diu frustraque desiderata fuêre, qualia sunt: Solaris curriculi e multorum annorum exquisitissimis observationibus Geometrice ostensa et in numeros hisce proximis saeculis satisfacientes reducta restitutio, item affixarum Stellarum, praesertim vero earum, quae circa Zodiacum versantur, praecisa et ipsi coelo adamussim consona verificatio, et pleraque alia nunc non attinet singillatim referre. Id saltem te oratum cuperem, ut MUNOSI Hispani et Italorum istorum tum etiam aliorum, qui de hac stella aliquid publicârunt, scripta mecum communicare non intermittas, si quae forte tibi innotuerunt ad nos non perlata. Idem etiam circa rem Cometicam expeterem. peterem. Constitui enim in singulis iuxta librorum finem, aliorum placita et animadversiones sub incudem veritatis revocare, ubi in primo atque ultimo tomo, quibus de Nova hac Stella et Cometa anni 80 agitur, tuarum lucubrationum honorificam, ut par est, introducere mentionem commodius erat, quam in illo intermedio libro de Cometa anni 77. Circa hunc enim nimium ab ipso coelo (vel teipso iudice) deflexisse deprehenderis. Plura hoc tempore scribere festinus Nobilis huius abitus non patitur, verum ubi per hunc ad nos redeuntem, vel quacunque alia oblata oportunitate responsum a te recepero, uberius et pluribus de toto hoc negotio tecum agam. Intereaque et semper quam diutissime et felicissime vale, et Amplissimos Generosissimosque Barones D. IOHANNEM SEPTIMIUM a Lichtenstein et D. FELICIANUM ab Herberstein , quorum uterque Daniam superioribus annis pertransiens, me Uraniburgi invisere non gravatus est, meo nomine officiose saluta. Receperunt vero ambo in se WITICHIANORUM scriptorum et librorum, quos post fata reliquit, meo nomine procurationem, de qua ut eos per commoditatem oblatam admonere velis, peramanter peto.</p><p>Iterum et saepius vale.<br>Raptim Hafniae die 22. Martij Anno Domini 89.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and, in varied erudition, most accomplished Master Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, most skilled physician and mathematician, his perpetual friend to be honoured.</b><br><b>To Herr Doctor Thaddeus Hagetius, resident at Prague.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Since only today I learned from the noble young man Tage Krabbe, our countryman — now on the very point of departing hence for the Imperial court — that he will be passing through Prague, I judged that I must not let it slip without giving you some letter, my most excellent and most learned Thaddaeus, especially because I have as yet received no reply to my last [letter], which nevertheless I daily await eagerly; this delay, however, I reckon must be charged to the scarcity of go-betweens and the inclemency of winter, for far be it from me to doubt anything of your ready good will towards me. Now therefore, since through this noble Dane a most convenient occasion of writing back is offered, I have you asked again and again that you put off your reply no further — and above all that you do not find it irksome to confer with me frankly and openly about the things contained in our second book, on the comet of [the year] '77, sent to you long ago. For I am sufficiently persuaded that you have already perused it. The first book, which treats fully of the New Star, is now being printed on my own press; the delay that has intervened was occasioned, not only by the holding-back of the French privilege that is to be joined to the Imperial one, but also, beyond my expectation, by a shortage of printing-paper. But now that both these hindrances have been sufficiently provided against, I hope that this volume too will shortly come into the public domain. And so far as my obstructions and distractions allow (in which I am for the most part entangled in the summer season), I shall press on the ripening of that work. In it you will find not only those matters that pertain properly to the New Star accurately and lucidly set forth and demonstrated, but, besides, many other things conducing greatly to the renewal of the whole Art of Astronomy; and such things as have hitherto long and vainly been longed for, namely: a restoration of the Sun's course, shown geometrically from the most exquisite observations of many years and reduced into numbers satisfactory for these latest centuries; likewise a precise verification of the fixed stars — and especially of those that move about the Zodiac — exactly consonant with the heaven itself; and many other things which it is now not fitting to recount one by one. This at least I would wish you entreated: that you not omit to share with me the writings of Muñoz the Spaniard and of those Italians, and also of others, who have published something about this star, if any perchance have come to your knowledge that have not been conveyed to us. The same I would also request concerning the matter of the comet[s]. For I have determined, in each [volume], near the end of the books, to recall the opinions and observations of others to the anvil of truth, where, in the first and last volume — in which this New Star and the comet of [the year] '80 are treated — it was more convenient to introduce honourable mention of your lucubrations, as is fitting, than in that intermediate book on the comet of '77. For concerning this [comet] you will be found to have strayed too far from the heaven itself (even by your own judgement). The haste of this nobleman's departure does not allow me to write more at this time; but when I have received a reply from you — through this same man returning to us, or by whatever other opportunity offered — I shall deal with you more fully and at greater length about this whole affair. And in the meanwhile, and always, fare you as long and as happily as can be; and greet in my name, with all courtesy, the most ample and most noble Barons, Lord Johann Septimius von Lichtenstein and Lord Felician von Herberstein, each of whom, passing through Denmark in earlier years, did not find it irksome to visit me at Uraniborg. Both of them have, moreover, taken upon themselves the charge, in my name, of Wittich's writings and books, which he left behind after his death; concerning which I most lovingly ask that you be willing to remind them when the convenience is offered.</p><p>Again and oftentimes, farewell.<br>In haste, at Copenhagen, the 22nd day of March, in the Year of the Lord '89.<br>Most loving of you,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>by my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Haick</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), Prague physician, astronomer and imperial counsellor at the court of Rudolf II; a leading observer of the 1572 nova and the comets of the 1570s–80s, and Tycho's principal scientific correspondent in the Empire. The two had exchanged cometary observations since the mid-1570s. The honorific styles him \"most skilled physician and mathematician.\"</li><li><b>The two address lines.</b> The first (Latin) is the formal superscription; the second is a <b>German address/docketing line</b> — <i>\"Ahm Herrn Doctor Thaddeo Hagetio zu Prag wohnhaftig\"</i> = \"To Herr Doctor Thaddeus Hagetius, resident at Prague\" — a routing direction (the code-switch is preserved per protocol §2). <i>AHM</i> = <i>am/an dem</i>; <i>WONHAFFTIG</i> = <i>wohnhaftig</i>, \"resident, dwelling.\"</li><li><b><i>S.</b></i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Bearer.</b> <i>nobilis iuvenis Tago Krabius nostras</i> = the noble young <b>Tage Krabbe</b> (\"our countryman,\" i.e. a Dane), here serving as letter-carrier on his way to the Imperial court (<i>Caesarea aula</i>) by way of Prague.</li><li><b>The works in progress.</b> Tycho describes his <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> in its planned three-volume form: <i>liber primus</i> \"de Stella Nova\" (Book I, on the new star of 1572), <i>liber secundus … Cometae anni 77</i> (Book II, on the comet of 1577 — i.e. the 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, already sent to Hagecius), and the <i>ultimus tomus</i> on the <i>Cometa anni 80</i> (the comet of 1580). <i>redintegratio totius Artis Astronomicae</i> = \"the renewal of the whole Art of Astronomy\" — Tycho's <i>instauratio/restitutio</i> programme (protocol §5.1); <i>restitutio Solaris curriculi</i> = \"restoration of the Sun's course\"; <i>verificatio affixarum Stellarum</i> = \"verification of the fixed stars.\"</li><li><b><i>typis meis excuditur</b></i> — \"is being printed on my own press\": Tycho's Uraniborg printing-house. <i>papyri impressoriae defectus</i> = \"a shortage of printing-paper\"; <i>Privilegium Gallicum Caesariano adiungendum</i> = the <b>French privilege</b> (printing licence) to be joined to the <b>Imperial</b> one.</li><li><b><i>publici iuris futurum</b></i> — \"will come into the public domain / be made public,\" the standard idiom for publication.</li><li><b><i>sub incudem veritatis revocare</b></i> — \"to recall … to the anvil of truth,\" a metaphor (the anvil on which others' opinions are hammered/tested) kept as metaphor; <i>lucubrationes</i> = \"lucubrations\" (works of night-study/learned labours), kept.</li><li><b>Muñoz.</b> <i>Munosi Hispani … scripta</i> = the writings of <b>Jerónimo Muñoz</b> (Hieronymus Munyos/Munosius, c. 1520–1591), the Valencia mathematician whose <i>Libro del nuevo cometa</i> (1573) reported the 1572 nova; Tycho asks Hagecius to forward Muñoz's and the Italians' tracts on the new star.</li><li><b><i>rem Cometicam</b></i> — \"the matter of the comet[s],\" i.e. the cometary controversy/literature generally.</li><li><b>The named barons.</b> <i>Iohannes Septimius a Lichtenstein</i> = <b>Johann Septimius von Liechtenstein</b>; <i>Felicianus ab Herberstein</i> = <b>Felician von Herberstein</b> — Austrian/Imperial noblemen who had visited Tycho at Uraniborg on earlier passages through Denmark. <i>Daniam … pertransiens</i> \"passing through Denmark\"; <i>me Uraniburgi invisere</i> \"to visit me at Uraniborg.\"</li><li><b>Wittich's books.</b> <i>Witichianorum scriptorum et librorum … quos post fata reliquit … procuratio</i> = the charge (custody/administration) of the writings and books <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) left at his death; the two barons had undertaken to manage them, and Tycho asks Hagecius to remind them — a matter bearing on the books and papers Tycho hoped to recover from Wittich's estate. <i>post fata reliquit</i> = \"left behind after his death\" (<i>fata</i>, lit. \"the fates\" = death), rendered straight.</li><li><b>Dateline / place.</b> <i>Raptim Hafniae die 22. Martij Anno Domini 89</i> = \"In haste, at <b>Copenhagen</b> (Hafnia), the 22nd day of March, in the Year of the Lord [15]89\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar; protocol §3). The catalogue label (\"1589. 22. marts. Copenhagen\") agrees with the dateline. <i>manu propria</i> = \"by my own hand,\" marking an autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Textual crux (flagged, not smoothed).</b> After <i>Idem etiam circa rem Cometicam</i> the source reads <b>\"expeterem. peterem.\"</b> — a duplicated/dittographic verb (the compound <i>expeterem</i> \"I would request\" followed by an isolated <i>peterem</i> \"I would ask,\" with an intervening full stop). This is almost certainly a printing/transmission slip; the sense (\"the same I would also request concerning the matter of the comet[s]\") is not in doubt, but the doubled reading is preserved verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4 rather than silently emended. Likewise <i>Tagone Krabio</i> (ablative): the bearer's forename appears as <i>Tago</i>; rendered \"Tage Krabbe.\"</li><li><b>No date discrepancy and no negation reversal.</b> The dateline matches the label; the letter's several negations are reproduced exactly (<i>non intermittendum</i>, <i>absit … ut … addubitem</i>, <i>ne … supersedeas</i>, <i>non gravatim</i>, <i>non intermittas</i>, <i>non gravatus est</i>, <i>non patitur</i>), with no count altered.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical references are identified; the only flagged points are the dittographic <i>\"expeterem. peterem.\"</i> (preserved, not emended) and the routine ambiguity of <i>rem Cometicam</i> (singular \"comet\" vs. collective \"cometary matter\"), both noted above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.165-167 as page images (Read pages=\"1-3\"); the letter runs the whole of p.165 through p.166 ('...manu propria'), with p.167 the head of the following Brucaeus letter. Dreyer's Latin matches source_text verbatim. Collated every proper name (Tago Krabbe, Munoz the Spaniard/MUNOSI Hispani, Johann Septimius von Lichtenstein, Felician von Herberstein, Paul Wittich/WITICHIANORUM), every year cited (comet of '77, comet of '80), and the closing date '22. Martij Anno Domini 89' against the English. Checked specifically for the custody-misattribution risk the brief flags: the Wittich-papers sentence ('Receperunt vero ambo in se WITICHIANORUM scriptorum... procurationem') correctly attributes custody, in Tycho's name, to both named barons — not altered. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1588 (7 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/45-48", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13966897"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.45."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 15, "prev": "brahe-t_15881101001", "next": "brahe-t_15890510001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15881101001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15890510001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Uses Tage Krabbe's departure for Prague as a courier opportunity to press Hagecius once more for a still-awaited reply and further conference.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890322001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890323001", "d": "1589-03-23", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 23 March 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.<br>S. D.</p><p>LEGI responsionem tuam ad meas literas de Cometa, in qua non eo candore usus videris, quem expectabam, nec eo animo nostra accepisse, quo scripta sunt. Miraris, me tuam Hypothesin probare, cum veterum autorum errores mordicus tueri soleam. Quid ita? An quia infinitis tenebrionum erroneis sententijs non assentiar, miraris? Ego vero levitatis magnae, ingenij parvi, imbecillis iudicij arbitror, non recte expensis adversariorum opinionibus, quibus calumniandi quam recte sentiendi maior est cura, subito assentiri. Soleo ego diu multumque premere, quae contradicuntur, et quomodo refelli queant, cogitare, tum vel in priore sententia persistere, aut etiam aliter sentientibus adhaerere. Non me movent convitia, quibus tenebriones veteres tam Medicos quam Philosophos obruunt, et hac ratione turbae indoctae fucum faciunt. Mihi veritatis semper magnum fuit studium, dissentiendi levitas semper invisa. Quod vero Dithmarsus iste tuam Hypothesin surripuerit, non est, quod hoc te admodum moveat. Cum enim observationibus destituatur, e quibus Hypothesis confirmatur et motuum ratio construitur, nihil profecerit, alienis plumis se ornasse, neque intelligentibus persuaserit, suum esse inventum. Perge in opere inchoato, et macte animo prosequere institutum. Haec ad immortalitatem via.</p><p>Quod vero de Lunae maxima latitudine scribis, quam 15 scrupulis statuis Ptolemaica maiorem, non facere magnum in eclipsibus discrimen, quae circa nodos incidunt, verum est; attamen in maxima distantia a nodis, in qua eclipses incidere possunt, aliquid, credo, dabit. Sed quare me in tenebrionis sententiam trahis, qui sidera humore vesci credidit, cum alio nostra spectet oratio? Videbimus, quam solide a te eorum sententia refellatur, qui sentiunt, hunc aërem undiquaque permeabilem coelestem regionem implere. Quantum enim ex literis ad PEUCERUM datis colligere possum, statuisti omnium sententias secus sentientium refellere. Hypothesin quoque tuam explicas. Verum ego prius de ea cum quodam, qui apud te fuit, contuli, et videor assecutus. Quod autem scripsi totam tractationem de Cometis circa individua versari, quorum cognitio unâ cum ipso Cometa intercidit, verum est, cum nec motus idem, nec corporis forma eadem unquam sit reditura. Non ivero tamen infitias, multa cum his tradi posse, ut non pauca tuo operi sunt inserta, quae ingeniose inventa et cum iudicio accommodata ad alia plura transferri commode possint. Quare non est, quod sphaericorum libellorum aetatem superatura scribas, quorum plerique non Bosphorum visent, ut de suis poëmatis prodidit HORATIUS, sed pharmacopolia et cauponas, ut habeant, in quod piper, thus, lardum et alia eius generis reponant. Atque utinam Sphaericis id tantum libellis usuveniret, et non maximae parti scriptorum, quae hisce temporibus in lucem eduntur, nisi forte in Bibliothecis aliquandiu delitescant blattis et tineis exposita. Quid itaque de meis mihi pollicear, ipse intelligis; spem magnam immortalitatis suorum scriptorum alijs relinquo. Non est autem, quod graviter feras, quod de Cometae distantia scripsi. Sic enim acceperam: post enim animadverti, de apparente te loqui.</p><p>Quod ad postremas tuas literas attinet, quibus nostri sat Boreaae datum meministi, quid aliud tibi respondeam, quam quod summis tam Medicis quam Philosophis visum est, non habeo, quorum ea fuit sententia, coelum sequi hominum naturas. Durum et asperum dura et aspera ingenia ferre, moderata temperatum. Id naturalibus rationibus posset confirmari, sed supersedeo. Quod si aliqui studijs liberalibus exculti, aut bonorum consuetudine humaniores et civiliores evaserint, hoc veterum sententiam minime evertit. Ipse si progrediare versus Boream, sic re ipsa deprehendes. Nec volo comparationem, quae semper est odiosa, instituere. Sufficit, utplurimum ita se habere. Possent enim incidere causae, quae coeli sive aëris constitutionem nihil morentur. Nam et Scythae non pauci temperata satis loca incolunt, barbari tamen et inhumani permanent, sic a puero educati. Et alij Regum aut Magistratus studio ad humanius vitae genus transferuntur. At Philosophi hasce causas restringunt, et quid aër per se efficiat, referunt, Boeotoque aëre natum scribunt.</p><p>Sculptorem conveni, is se adfore aperto mari pollicebatur et in Insulam tuam traiecturum; verum non apparuit. Ex hisce literis et chartulis adiunctis, intelliges, quid actum sit; quid fieri insuper velis, perscribes.</p><p>Bene vale, amantiss. TYCHO.<br>Rostochij 23. Martij An. 1589.<br>Henricus Brucaeus,<br>plane tuus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Heinrich Brucaeus to Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Sends greeting.</p><p>I have read your reply to my letter on the comet, in which you do not seem to me to have shown the candour I expected, nor to have taken my words in the spirit in which they were written. You wonder that I approve your hypothesis, when I am wont to defend the errors of the ancient authors tooth and nail. Why so? Is it because I do not assent to the countless erroneous opinions of the obscurantists that you wonder? For my part, I judge it a mark of great fickleness, of small wit, of feeble judgement, to assent suddenly without having rightly weighed the opinions of one's opponents — opinions in which the care to slander is greater than the care to think rightly. I am wont to press long and hard upon things that are disputed, and to consider how they may be refuted, and then either to persist in my earlier opinion, or even to side with those who think otherwise. I am not moved by the abuse with which the obscurantists overwhelm the ancients, both physicians and philosophers, and by this means throw dust in the eyes of the unlearned crowd. The pursuit of truth has always been a great thing with me; the fickleness of dissent always hateful. But that that fellow from Dithmarschen has filched your hypothesis — there is no reason this should much trouble you. For since he is destitute of the observations from which the hypothesis is confirmed and the account of the motions is constructed, he will have gained nothing by decking himself in others' feathers, nor will he have persuaded men of understanding that the invention is his own. Press on in the work begun, and with good heart pursue what you have set in hand. This is the road to immortality.</p><p>As for what you write about the Moon's greatest latitude — which you set at 15 minutes greater than the Ptolemaic value, [saying] that it makes no great difference in the eclipses that fall near the nodes — that is true; yet at the greatest distance from the nodes at which eclipses can fall, it will, I believe, yield something. But why do you drag me into the opinion of that obscurantist who believed the stars feed on moisture, when my discourse looks to something else? We shall see how solidly you refute the opinion of those who hold that this everywhere-permeable air fills the celestial region. For as far as I can gather from the letter you sent to Peucer, you have resolved to refute the opinions of all who think otherwise. You also set out your hypothesis. But I had previously conferred about it with a certain man who had been with you, and I think I have grasped it. As for my having written that the whole treatment of comets turns upon individual things, the knowledge of which perishes together with the comet itself — that is true, since neither the same motion nor the same bodily form will ever return. Yet I do not deny that much can be handed down along with these things; so that not a few matters are inserted into your work which, ingeniously discovered and judiciously adapted, can conveniently be carried over to many other matters. Wherefore there is no reason for you to write that [your work] will outlast the age of the little books on the sphere — most of which will not visit the Bosphorus, as Horace declared of his own poems, but the apothecaries' shops and the taverns, to serve as wrapping in which to store away pepper, frankincense, lard, and other things of that kind. And would that this befell the little books on the sphere alone, and not the greatest part of the writings that are brought to light in these times — unless perhaps they lie hidden for a while in libraries, exposed to bookworms and moths. What, then, I may promise myself of my own [writings], you yourself understand; I leave the great hope of the immortality of their writings to others. There is, moreover, no reason for you to take ill what I wrote about the comet's distance. For so I had understood it; but afterwards I noticed that you were speaking of the apparent [distance].</p><p>As for your last letter, in which you recall that enough was conceded by me to the North, what other answer am I to give you than what has seemed [right] to the greatest of physicians as well as philosophers — whose opinion was that the heaven follows the natures of men[?] [It is] harsh and rough natures that bear the harsh and rough [climate], temperate natures the temperate. This could be confirmed by natural arguments, but I forbear. But if some, refined by the liberal studies, or grown more humane and civil by the company of good men, have turned out so, this in no way overturns the opinion of the ancients. If you yourself should travel northward, you will discover it so in the very fact. Nor do I wish to set up a comparison, which is always odious. It is enough that for the most part it is so. For causes might intervene that pay no heed to the constitution of the heaven or of the air. For not a few Scythians too inhabit lands temperate enough, and yet remain barbarous and uncivil, having been so reared from boyhood. And others are brought over to a more humane way of life by the zeal of their kings or magistrates. But the philosophers limit these causes, and report what the air of itself effects, and write that [such a one] was born under a Boeotian air.</p><p>I have met with the sculptor; he was promising that he would come, once the sea was open, and cross over to your island; but he has not appeared. From this letter and the little papers enclosed you will understand what has been done; what you wish to have done besides, you will write [to me].</p><p>Fare you well, most loving Tycho.<br>At Rostock, 23 March, the year 1589.<br>Heinrich Brucaeus,<br>wholly yours.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broucke, 1530/31–1593), Flemish-born professor of mathematics and then of medicine at the University of Rostock, and an elder correspondent and early supporter of Tycho. The letter is his reply in the running 1588–89 exchange occasioned by Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), the comet treatise.</li><li><b>Recipient / salutation.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5). The closing apostrophe <i>Bene vale, amantiss. Tycho</i> (\"Fare you well, most loving Tycho\") addresses Tycho in the vocative; <i>plane tuus</i> = \"wholly yours.\"</li><li><b><i>Dithmarsus iste … tuam Hypothesin surripuerit</b></i> — \"that fellow from Dithmarschen has filched your hypothesis\": <b>Nicolaus Reimarus Ursus</b> (Nicolai Reymers Bär, \"Ursus,\" c. 1551–1600), born in Dithmarschen, whom Tycho accused of stealing his geo-heliocentric world-system after a visit to Uraniborg; Ursus had published a like system in his <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (Strasbourg, 1588). The corpus (Gingerich, <i>Annotated Census of De revolutionibus</i>) confirms the identification \"Nicolaus Raimarus Ursus Dithmarsus.\" Brucaeus's point — that without observations the borrowed scheme is worthless — is rendered without softening Tycho's own charge of theft.</li><li><b><i>Hypothesis</b></i> — kept as \"hypothesis\" throughout (the world-system / geometric model); <i>motuum ratio</i> = \"the account of the motions.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy (Moon's latitude).</b> <i>de Lunae maxima latitudine … quam 15 scrupulis statuis Ptolemaica maiorem</i> = \"the Moon's greatest latitude, which you set at 15 minutes [of arc] greater than the Ptolemaic [value]\"; <i>scrupulus</i> = arc-minute, here \"minute(s).\" <i>quae circa nodos incidunt</i> = \"[eclipses] that fall near the nodes\"; <i>nodus</i> → <b>node</b>, <i>eclipsis</i> → <b>eclipse</b> (protocol §5.1). <i>maxima latitudine</i> / <i>maxima distantia</i>: the qualifier \"greatest\" is the source's own (<i>maxima</i>), not an editorial insertion.</li><li><b><i>qui sidera humore vesci credidit</b></i> — \"[the obscurantist] who believed the stars feed on moisture\": the old doctrine (Stoic / Aristotelian-meteorological) that the heavenly bodies are nourished by exhalations drawn up from below; Brucaeus protests that this is not the doctrine Tycho should impute to him.</li><li><b><i>hunc aërem undiquaque permeabilem coelestem regionem implere</b></i> — \"this everywhere-permeable air fills the celestial region\": the fluid-heavens question (whether one continuous, permeable air-like medium extends through the heavens, as against solid celestial spheres) which Tycho's comet work pressed; Brucaeus expects Tycho to refute the solid-orb party.</li><li><b><i>literis ad Peucerum datis</b></i> — \"the letter sent to <b>Peucer</b>\": <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician-astronomer and Melanchthon's son-in-law, another node of this correspondence-network (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b><i>individua … quorum cognitio unâ cum ipso Cometa intercidit</b></i> — \"individual things, the knowledge of which perishes together with the comet itself\": <i>individua</i> in the logical sense of unrepeatable particulars; a comet, being a transient singular, leaves no recurring motion or form, so (Brucaeus had argued) the science of it dies with it — qualified, as Brucaeus does, by his concession that transferable method survives.</li><li><b><i>sphaericorum libellorum</b></i> — \"the little books on the sphere\": the elementary <i>Sphaera</i> textbooks (in the tradition of Sacrobosco), here disparaged as ephemeral.</li><li><b>Allusion (Horace).</b> <i>non Bosphorum visent, ut de suis poëmatis prodidit Horatius, sed pharmacopolia et cauponas … in quod piper, thus … reponant</i> — Horace's conceit (<i>Epistles</i> I.20; cf. <i>Ars Poetica</i>) of the worthless book ending as wrapping-paper for grocers' pepper and incense. The grim list (<i>piper, thus, largum</i>) is kept; <i>largum</i> is read as \"bulk/ample goods\" of the same shop-stock kind, paralleling <i>alia eius generis</i>. <i>blattis et tineis</i> = \"bookworms and moths.\"</li><li><b><i>nostri sat Boreaae datum</b></i> — \"enough was conceded by me to the North\": refers back to Tycho's previous letter on the old climate-theory of national character (the <i>Boreas</i>, the north wind / the North). <i>Boreaae</i> is the manuscript form (with doubled vowel) for <i>Boreae</i>. The whole third paragraph debates the classical-medical doctrine — <i>coelum sequi hominum naturas</i>, \"the heaven follows the natures of men\" — that climate shapes temperament: northern peoples harsh because their climate is harsh.</li><li><b><i>Scythae … barbari … Boeotoque aëre natum</b></i> — the Scythians as the type of the cold-dwelling barbarian; <i>Boeoto aëre natum</i> = \"born under a Boeotian air,\" the proverbial dull/stupid air of Boeotia (cf. <i>Boeotum in crasso … aëre</i>, Horace, <i>Epistles</i> II.1.244). Kept as allusion; the philosophers' position (that air <i>of itself</i> dulls or sharpens wit) is distinguished from the king-and-magistrate counter-causes Brucaeus allows.</li><li><b><i>summis tam Medicis quam Philosophis</b></i> — \"the greatest of physicians as well as philosophers\": the Hippocratic–Galenic and Aristotelian authorities behind the climate doctrine. Punctuation: the source runs the rhetorical question <i>quid aliud tibi respondeam … non habeo</i> together; an editorial <i>[?]</i> and <i>[It is]</i> are bracketed to carry the sense without recasting.</li><li><b><i>Sculptorem … in Insulam tuam traiecturum</b></i> — \"the sculptor … would cross over to your island\": a craftsman (engraver/carver) Brucaeus had engaged on Tycho's behalf, to come to <b>Hven</b> once the sea was open (i.e. ice-free) in spring; <i>aperto mari</i> = \"the sea being open.\" <i>chartulis adiunctis</i> = \"the little papers enclosed.\"</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Rostochij 23. Martij An. 1589</i> = \"at <b>Rostock</b> [Rostochium], 23 March 1589\" (Old Style; Denmark and the Lutheran north kept the Julian calendar). The header date matches the dateline; the dataset label (\"1589. 23. marts. Rostock\") agrees, so no discrepancy arises.</li><li><b>Editorial insertions.</b> Bracketed words (<i>[saying]</i>, <i>[other]</i>, <i>[value]</i>, <i>[such a one]</i>, <i>[right]</i>, <i>[It is]</i>, <i>[?]</i>, <i>[your work]</i>, <i>[yet]</i>, <i>[to me]</i>, <i>[distance]</i>) supply syntax implied but not lexically present in the source; the source's own hedges (<i>credo</i> \"I believe,\" <i>videor assecutus</i> \"I think I have grasped,\" <i>Non ivero infitias</i> \"I do not deny\") are preserved unsharpened.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia VII, page 167: \"largum\" corrected to \"lardum\" (lard/bacon). The English's bracketed guess \"[other] bulk goods\" replaced with \"lard\".</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is read securely throughout and the polemical register (a senior colleague half-rebuking, half-encouraging Tycho over the Ursus plagiarism and the comet book) is matched. The one soft point is the precise referent of <i>largum</i> in the Horatian grocer's-list (taken as \"bulk goods,\" parallel to the surrounding shop-stock nouns), flagged here rather than smoothed; the secondary `Boreaae`→`Boreae` orthographic reading is noted rather than silently normalised.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only residue is a harmless spelling variant (Boreaae→Boreae) + one lexical nuance in a Horatian allusion; sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 166-169 as page images. Collated the full letter: the Dithmarschen/hypothesis dispute, the Peucer reference, the Moon's-latitude figure (15 scrupulis), the Horace/spherical-booklets passage, the Scythian/Boeotian-air passage, the sculptor paragraph, and the closing date (Rostochij 23. Martij An. 1589) all match Dreyer. One word-level TEXT_ERROR found (largum/lardum, low severity, does not change the passage's overall sense) forces the grade to C per the rubric even though the verdict is MINOR.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text misprints 'lardum' (lard/bacon, a stock item merchants wrapped in waste paper, per the Horace/spice-shop trope Brucaeus is invoking) as 'largum', an adjective that cannot function as a listed noun; the English's odd bracketed '[other] bulk goods' is a downstream guess at the corrupted word rather than a rendering of 'lardum'. Dreyer prints: <i>in quod piper, thus, lardum et alia eius generis reponant</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The single defect site was collated against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia volume VII, page 167 (page image, not the OCR text layer). Confirmed: \"ut habeant, in quod piper, thus, lardum et alia eius generis reponant.\" No other cruxes were found on the sampled pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 16, "prev": "brahe-t_15890000001", "next": "brahe-t_15921105001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890000001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Objects that Tycho's reply lacked expected candour and misread his words, and defends his stated practice of pressing hard on disputed points before assenting.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890323001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890500001", "d": "1589-05-00", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, March / beginning of May 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Berlin, manuscript copy in a copy of Tycho Brahe's astronomical writings</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIME ET CLARISSIME VIR, AMICE HONORANDE.S.</p><p>P.SUB praeteritae hyemis initium, vir magnificus, D. GUILHELMUS STUARTUS, mihi literas tuas et librum, quem misisti, reddidit, quibus, ut in manus meas venêre, avide perlectis, delectatus sum plurimum; nam non solum erudita intricatae quaestionis tractatio, in qua eximiam doctrinam et singularem industriam perspexi, mirum in modum placuit, sed etiam gratissimum fuit, tam amicam erga me voluntatem in te viro praestanti cognoscere. Pro ista tua humanitate et libro tuo, quo me honorare libuit, ago quas possum gratias, cumque simili studiorum fructu respondere nequeam, animum tui peramantem tuaeque amicitiae studiosum profiteor. Quod eam LIDDELIUS noster mei apud te fecerit mentionem, ex amore quodam id suo egit; quod vero te ad amicitiam mutuam invitaverit, mihi rem gratam, et quam multis ante annis expetitam a me fuisse scierat, fecit. Nam a quo doctissimus WITTICHIUS ex Dania redierat, tua ille tantopere praedicavit, ut ab eo tempore te semper in amore et honore habuerim, et ante quinquennium, cum in patriam per Porthmum vestrum navigarem, quae ille commendaverat, coram intueri cupieram et constitueram; sed ventis contra afflantibus et pro tua Urania Norvegicis scopulis ad mensem obiectis, aegre votis frustratus sum. Ab eo tempore accessit honorifica nostratium Legatorum de admirandis tuis instrumentis (nam de alijs iudicare vix poterant) commendatio; quae illud amicitiae tuae desiderium renovavit et auxit, adeo ut superioribus annis saepenumero in animo habuerim meis literis initium eius facere; quod non acciderit, vitae nostrae ratione eiusmodi cogitationes subinde excutiente factum est; quod tu nunc prior id egeris, et comitatem agnosco, et benevolentiam grato animo complector, meque vicissim teneri statuo, ut quod a te bene coeptum est, a me, quo poterit studio, in posterum colatur et conservetur. Quod quidem non illibenter faciam; nam quamvis exiguum sit, quod ipse polliceri potero, in tanta earum rerum oblivione, in quibus tu excellis, iucundum tamen erit de ijs nonnunquam tecum conferre, a quo summa quaeque expectare licebit. Nam et sic liber tuus, licet ab undecim hinc annis eiusmodi vix attigerim, tam fuit acceptus, ut accuratas eius observationes et ingeniosas demonstrationes summa cum voluptate legerim; ac pro veteri nostra erga has disciplinas affectione, studiosis earum plurimum gratulor, esse eiusmodi Artifices, quibus nec ad perfectam scientiam nec ad absolutam operationem quicquam deesse videtur: tantumque de illis studijs te meritum esse statuo, ut omni commendatione dignissimus sis.</p><p>Sed ad literas tuas nunc veniam, quibus citius respondissem, nisi continuae peregrinationes nostrae ab initio Februarij vix unum diem domi esse concessissent. Ex Anglia ante paucos dies domum reversus, nonnihil otij sum nactus ad tua relegenda et iterum expendenda; nec tamen ut cuperem. Nam si reliqua suppeterent, experiri vellem, an Cometas iterum numeris possem coelo deducere in nativum suum locum inter ARISTOTELIS fumositates. Sed hoc alterius erit otij et maioris earum rerum recordationis. Nunc pro praesenti occasione, quantum occupationes permittent, paucis rescribam.</p><p>Literis tuis a me petis, ut sententiam meam candide significem. Quod ad id attinet, mihi tua vehementer placent, et inprimis illud nobile studium in corrigendis stellarum locis et motibus et exactioribus numeris subijciendis supra modum commendare oportet; quo ut pergas nomen tuum inter illos aeternos numeros consecrare hortari vix audeo, cum summo conatu eo te ferri omnibus necessarijs instructum iam palam constet; si ullam operam in tam nobili conatu praestare tibi possem, non deessem; sed nobis nec tam accurata instrumenta hîc sunt, nec his coelestibus a caducis corporibus vacare licet. Nunc enim magis Rhabarbarum et Sene similiaque expenduntur, quam omnes Planetarum gyri. Verum, nobilissime vir, cum ad eam contemplationem te ille aeternus omnium Creator vocaverit, ijsque, quibus oportuit, instruxerit, macte hac felicitate et virtute utere, ut talento tuo posteritati lucrifacias. At non Paradoxis; nam ingenue loquor, veritatis causa, sed acceptioribus disciplinis. Hoc etenim tuum, in quo CICERONEM te Mathematice imitari voluisse mihi persuadeo, minime arridet. Non enim placet, sapientissimi Architecti perfectissimum ordinem obscurari, et mutabilibus cum immutabilibus confusis, αταξίας Chaos introduci. Nec latet, alios ante te in ea fuisse opinione, contra quos aliquando meditabar in Cometometria nostra disputationem. Verum cum illi nullis rationibus eo fuerint inducti, evidentius est, quam ut ostendi debeat. Nemo tam multis id conatus est persuadere, quam tu. Quod an effeceris, ego quidem affirmare nequeo; mihi enim Physicorum coniecturae ex rerum naturis sumtae magis consentaneae adhuc videntur. Et ne absque ratione ita sentire me existimes, paucis tua, si non ingrata fuerit veritatis inquisitio, excutiam.</p><p>Primis quinque capitibus, quae de apparente loco et motu Cometae demonstrasti, praeclare a te constituta videntur, nec si numeri constent, contradictioni locus est. Sed sexto capite plus ultra progrederis, coelum novis corporibus aperturus, ubi nihil ijs loci esse ARISTOTELES demonstravit. Neque tuae rationes eius authoritati hîc praevalere censendae sunt, si diligenter examinentur. Nam illae tres Maximae, quas prima comprobatione affers, probabiliter duntaxat, ut ipse fateris, pro sententia tua faciunt, sed quaeso, quorum ante authoritate fuerint approbatae, ut probabilem concludendi vim habeant? Sed sint tales: ut arcum maximi circuli describere, sit in coelo esse; quod Phaenomenon tuum talem descripsisse tibi visum sit, demonstras; sed an alijs similiter moveri apparuerit, dubito. Tibi videtur materiae caussa circulum magnum describi non posse, quod vagabundius hincinde ab eiusmodi orbita potius expatiaretur, ac ob id ingenitam ipsi motus sui scientiam asscribis. Mihi tam intelligens sui fuisse nequaquam consentaneum existimatur; quin potius illum motum materiae caussa accidisse, quippe aliâs non fuit, quam a primi motus numero retardatio, quae maior circa initia extitit, cum moles maior plus subsisteret, subinde et quasi proportionaliter decreverit, flamma aequalibus temporibus materiam lychni instar depascente, quod non in minorem, sed maiorem abierit circulum, argumentum est, variasse a Terra remotionem, materia nimirum indies magis ac magis extenuata, et flamma ad superiora praevalente, quae Physica ipsius motus ratio magis consentanea est, quam statuere animal fuisse logicum, seu αὐτοκίνητον a sua motrice mente corpus.</p><p>Secunda Maxima coelo Cometas potius eliminas et longe exterminas: ideo cuius moles, lux et motus continuo decreverunt, donec prorsus evanuerint, in illo immutabili loco fuit? quo concesso, ut alio praeteream, praecipua Astronomiae hypothesis evertetur, qua invariabilis coelestium conditio ponitur. Planetae a celeriore ad * terram et hoc ad illud certis sub periodis remeant, at Cometis a suo interitu non est regressio, nec quicquam hîc magis simile, quam circularis est rectae. Tertia, quod motum Luna celeriorem habuerit, supra eam evehis: sic progrediendo, ad Saturnum usque sub finem evolabit; neque enim si inter coelestia talis motuum Harmonia sit, quicquid speciem aliquam eiusmodi motus habet, pro suo numero inter ea reponetur? Coelestibus ingenitum est circularis motus principium: mutabilibus adventitium est tantum, cumque toto naturae genere haec ab illis differant, ut asymmetra, secundum magis et minus comparari non debent. Itaque his Topicis tuis nusquam adhuc locantur Cometae, quod paucis indicare volui. Videamus iam an Mathematicae dimensiones coelum ipsis patefaciant, quod maxime quidem secunda comprobatione tua conaris, ubi assumptis duabus ab ore Pegasi observatis Cometae distantijs inquiris, an collectae ex calculo iuxta Hypothesin distantiae congruant. Primum quidem ex ijsdem distantijs, cognita declinatione et a Meridiano remotione, colligis ad data tempora apparentes altitudines. Et posito Cometa in orbe lunari inquiris sectionis angulum, quem facit verticalis cum circulo apparentis motus: cuius anguli beneficio parallaxes in longum et latum elicis, unde vicissim distantias ab ore Pegasi ratiocinaris: quarum posterior cum maior priori obveniat, et quo inferius ponetur Cometa, eo ab observatione magis discrepabit, concludis necessario supra Lunam esse. Ponemus in hoc successivo rationum ductu nihil requiri, an ideo supra Lunam erit, quia sic collectae distantiae ab observatis differunt: at hac ratione supra Lunam non erit, quia nunquam congruent, quantumvis enim removeris, semper posterior collectarum maior erit posteriori observatarum. Et ubi Parallaxis nulla est, posterior erit duntaxat aequalis priori, coëuntibus apparentis et veri loci lineis: nunquam minor fiet, ut vult observatio. Deinde hae collectae fiunt, Phaenomeno a priore ad posteriorem situm motu duntaxat primo translato, nulla proprij motus habita ratione: quomodo posterior distantia necessario maior evadet priori, si quicquid ex Parallaxi accesserit; si nihil, erit aequalis: nunquam minor, nisi ex proprio id motu fiat: quo interveniente, ut si versus stellam supra se positam proprio motu feratur, quod ex Parallaxi accederet, iterum subtrahetur vel etiam amplius: ac tum Cometa etiam longe infra Lunam posito, non modo nulla erit aspectus diversitas, sed posterior quoque distantia a Fixa minor evadet priori. Ut sit Cometae sublunaris remotio h b, ad quod quidem punctum b primo motu in secunda observatione perveniret, sed proprio retrahatur in d, sitque retractio in Instrumento ad a insensibilis fere ad 3.' vel 4.' c e, fiat tamen per eam in illa immensa octavi orbis remotione, ut abs situ b supra Lunam in f inter coelestia ad g usque attollatur. Ubi ex Parallaxi, quae haberi vix potest, remotio Cometae a Terra definiri nequit, quod nemo hactenus animadvertit.</p><p>Sed repeto nunc illum ductum, qui admodum interruptus est: unde data tempora habes; non ex altitudinibus Fixarum : Si ex Horologijs, cur non id alijs concedis? Deinde ille sectionis angulus d c e pag. 114. non est repertus, neque idem est cum angulo g c k pag. 112. quia circulus d c e pag. 112. per apparentem locum transit, et apparentis est motus, at f c g, pag. 114. alius est et transit per verum ex Hypothesi locum: proinde d c e pag. 114. non est inventus, quo Parallaxes exquiris: ac ideo hae in longum et latum deductae Parallaxes alienae sunt, nec aliae sunt visae longitudines, quam quas veras appellas pag. 115: ut hae distantiae ne quidem iuxta Hypothesin rite sint collectae, unde apparet, hanc demonstrationem omni modo infirmam esse ad id quod intendis. Cum collimatius rem agis, proprium ad stellam motum consideras, et easdem omnino reperis distantias, ut omnia exempla docent, primo excepto pag. 119. in quo illa tria scrupula prima, quorum causa tot centenis Semid. Terrae vis Cometam removeri, non fiant ulla retardatione respectu Parallaxeos, sed calculo tantum obrepunt: nam in alijs omnibus non occurrunt, nec mirum si ex ijsdem eadem proveniant, cum nihil alieni miscetur: ac in illa immensa remotione, cum Parallaxis evanescat, nullam pariet differentiam, illa quam affers cum observatione convenientia nihil concludat.</p><p>Tertia comprobatio ad quaestionis huius veritatis eruendam accommodari fortasse poterit, sed pro tua sententia nihil facit: nam concessa illa ad idem momentum reductione, quamvis in locis longitudine et latitudine differentibus eadem reperiatur Cometae a Vulturis stella distantia, non sequitur locorum intercapedinem a b pag. 125. respectu remotionis b c insensibilis esse quantitatis: nam negligis differentiam longitudinis, et in eodem plano statuis lineas visuales a c f et b c e, cum in diversis sint, quod loca longitudine differant, et in diversis planis existentes. Licet in corpore Cometae ad idem momentum coëant, in octavo tamen orbe in contrarias dehiscunt partes e et f: a quibus ad stellam d aequales sint distantiae arcus d e d f, ac triangulum fiat isosceles d e f, idque Cometa longe infra Lunam posito: quare hac probatione non efficietur coelestem habuisse situm.</p><p>In longitudine Uraniburgi et Pragae ab alijs differre videris, quod Pragam facias Uraniburgo orientaliorem, cum, ut de alijs non dicam, in ORTELIJ Mappis longitudo Pragae sit circiter 32, Uraniburgi vero 35; quaeso, ad me perscribas exactas, quas habere poteris tui loci, Pragae et Cassiliae longitudines.</p><p>In quarta probatione, quas appellas respectu centri altitudines, non sunt aliae, quam apparentes, respectu nimirum superficiei, quia ex declinatione et azimutho apparentibus deducis: quod in orbe Lunae differentia verarum altitudinum minor sit differentia apparentium ad 9.' nihil aliud arguit, quam in hoc supposito situ Cometam admittere aspectus diversitatem: nec dubium est, quin quo magis removeris, differentia verarum et apparentium altitudinum sit minor futura, donec prorsus nulla evadat. REGIOMONTANI demonstratio satis accommodata est Phaenomeno immobili, quantumvis elevetur, si modo ulla sit Parallaxis: at mobili nequaquam inservit, nec tua adhibita correctione accommodatior fit, quia in correcto Azimutho Cometa non apparuit, ut Parallaxis in eo non sit quaerenda: talis adhibenda esset correctio, qua proprius ad stellam motus, qui Parallaxin exhaurit, determinetur, verum ne hac ratione remotio eius a Terra erui poterit, quod per Parallaxin haec indagari nequeat. Atque hae sunt rationes, quibus Cometas inter coelestia locare conaris, quam vero deficiant ex ijs quae attuli, manifestum esse arbitror.</p><p>Quae octavo Capite affers, cum his nitantur, cum ijs consistent, nec id pluribus exquirere libet. Quod ad illam mundani Systematis Hypotyposin attinet, cum ut penetrationum dimensionem evites, Planetas per liquidum Aethera progressivo motu ferri statues, ut alia praeteream, circularis motus Hypothesin evertes, et apparens anomalia per helices indemonstrabilis et inexplicabilis reddetur. Sic est Systema constituendum, ut habeat Mathematicus, in quo acquiescat, nec Physico sit, quod obmurmuret; nam nequeunt horum Philosophorum demonstrationes contradicere. Illustrissimi D. Landgravij observationes valde placent, nec dubito, quin ex ijs possit quaestionis veritas elici. Rudes MAeSTLINI et CORNELIJ notationes licet ad apparentem motum ostendendum aliquo modo faciant, ad Cometae situm demonstrandum nihil conferunt. Et illum Philosophia Tubingensis melius potuit instituere, nisi phantasiae ostentandae causa eiusmodi figmenta publicare voluisset. HAGGECIUS, quae in Nova Stella parum ex arte praestiterit, me non latet, ut mirum non sit, in hoc Cometa tam fuisse incertum. Imo mirum non est hos Mathematicos in tam absurdam abijsse sententiam, cum nec sufficientia ad inquisitionem propositam data sumpserint, nec certam haberent demonstrationem, quam sequerentur: Non enim adhuc constat, nec demonstratum est ab ullo, quantum scio, qua ratione possit Cometarum remotio definiri, si proprium habeant motum. REGIOMONTANI demonstratio in absurdum abit. DIGESSAeI alae non pertingunt eo, et illa doctissimi WITTICHIJ (quem ad coelestia migrasse, ad quae summo studio semper aspiravit, vehementer doleo, vel Mathematum caussa) quam sub SCULTETI nomine reprehendis, quamvis ei concedantur observationes, nihilo magis rem expediet. Repetatur libri tui figura pag. 38. Statuit in illa verticalium prioris et posterioris observationis sectione B. verum Cometae locum fuisse, et Parallaxes utrique tempori respondentes facit b a, b k: at in illo b puncto, si Cometa proprium habuit motum, vere non fuit, ut ab eo Parallaxis numerari non debeat, ac ideo demonstratio haec duntaxat inservit Phaenomeno immobili. Cum itaque dimensionibus Mathematicis ad locum Cometarum nondum sit deventum, quid in hac quaestione statuemus? Ipse nondum a praeceptore ARISTOTELE discedo, qui mutabilia ab immutabilibus, ut naturis, sic locis discernere docuit, illaque orbis Lunaris complexu comprehensa nulla naturae vi supra posse efferri demonstravit. Si Mathematicas requiris demonstrationes, cum aliae pro tempore non suppetant, D. WITTICHIJ assumam. Observationem tu refellis, quod sub eadem Verticali simul stellae illae cum Cometa esse non potuerint. Licet hoc demonstrationem non infringat (nam sub diversis concludit) tamen non video, quî tua ratiocinatio plus fidei merebitur, quam illius doctissimi artificis de sensu suo affirmatio: mihi constat fuisse illius, nec illi vel peritiam observandi vel Instrumenta idonea defuisse, ut si indiligentiam probare nequeamus, illas sensu notas observationes, ut principia concedere oporteat. Demonstratio vero, quamvis iam reieceram, multum tamen fidei meretur, quo circa illud tempus accommodata fuit, quo Cometa a numero primi motus insensibiliter deficiebat, et maxime inter binas observationes nulla fuit in intermedio tempore variatio, ut sectio B. pag. 38. fuerit, cum ille observaret, verus locus, quare cum reliqua succedant, licet WITTICHIJ probatio magnam fidem meretur. Sic si NOLTHIO suae sensiones non denegentur (nec intelligo, quî refelli possint, cum circa idem fere tempus observatio fuerit) nec operatio in absurdum abeat, admodum probabiliter concludit, sublunarem fuisse Cometam. Ipse eundem observavi, idoneis Instrumentis iustae magnitudinis, Radio, Quadrante et Parallatico, ac aliqua adhibita diligentia sumpsi qualibet observatione apparentes altitudines Cometae et Aquilae vel alterius stellae: deinde distantiam apparentem Cometae et stellae: Ex quibus azimutha et tempora per demonstrationem reperi: ac certe prima apparitione in absurdas incidi operationes, sed postea plus minus 5° gradibus Parallaxin habere inveni. Motum proprium habuisse animadverti, quo Parallaxis exhauriebatur: et nonnulla occurrebant ἀγεωμέτρητα, nec tum exactissima eius rei demonstratio fuit nota, unde factum est, ut iudicium suspenderem. Ab eo tempore ita illa omnia manibus nostris excussa sunt, ut nunquam ea resumere licuerit: minusque iam ad eiusmodi calculum aptus sum: Si tamen aliquando otium expetitum fuerit, ea nonnihil repetam, tecumque, si libuerit, de ijs conferam. Rogo te ut ad me mittas omnes praestantium artificum et maxime tuas observationes, quas habes de nova Stella et Cometis, qui superioribus annis illuxerunt.</p><p>Atque haec sunt, quae ad Paradoxon tuum afferre volui; quae quidem non alio modo allata sunt, quam ut ostendam, quibus ipse adducar, cur ab eruditis Physicorum coniecturis nequeam adhuc discedere; ut in bonam partem ea accipias, vehementer peto. Audio, clariss. vir, te in Chymicis excellere; si quid singulare in hoc artificij genere habes, sive ad extractiones faciendas, sive ad aquas purgantes, vel tale quid pertineat, quaeso mihi communices. Habent Germani, ut accepi, nunc inventum, quo distillatis corpus expurgant, quo licet non indigeamus, tamen ignorare non cupio; saepius id experiri cogitavi, sed cum non suaderet ratio, ne operam et oleum, ut non semel mihi accidit, perderem, omisi. Verum charta deficiente finem faciam, et haec quae rescripta sunt, boni consule; nam diligentius ista tractare, aegri non patiuntur. Vale. Datae Edenburgi in Scotia, in Martio primum, postea sub Maij initium.</p><p>Tui amantiss.<br>IOANNES CRAIGUS,<br>Philosophiae et medicinae doctor.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Most noble and most renowned Sir, friend to be honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>About the beginning of last winter the magnificent gentleman Master William Stuart delivered to me your letter and the book you sent; and when these came into my hands, I read them through eagerly and was delighted in the highest degree. For not only did the learned treatment of an intricate question — in which I discerned outstanding doctrine and singular diligence — please me wonderfully, but it was also most gratifying to recognise in you, an excellent man, so friendly a disposition towards me. For this kindness of yours, and for your book, with which it pleased you to honour me, I give what thanks I can; and since I cannot answer with a like fruit of studies, I profess a mind most loving of you and zealous for your friendship. That our friend Liddel made mention of me to you, he did out of a certain affection of his own; but that he invited you to a mutual friendship, he did me a welcome thing, and one he knew I had sought for many years past. For ever since the most learned Wittich returned from Denmark, he so extolled your work that from that time I have always held you in love and honour; and five years ago, when I was sailing home through your Sound, I had desired and resolved to behold in person what he had commended — but, with the winds blowing against me and (in lieu of your Urania) the Norwegian rocks set before me for a month, I was, to my regret, cheated of my wishes. Since that time there was added the honourable commendation of our envoys concerning your admirable instruments (for of other matters they could scarcely judge); which renewed and increased that longing for your friendship, so much so that in recent years I have often had it in mind to take the first step by a letter of mine — and that this did not come to pass was owing to the manner of our life, which keeps shaking out such thoughts. That you have now done it first, I both acknowledge your courtesy and embrace your goodwill with a grateful mind, and judge myself bound in turn, so that what has been well begun by you may henceforth be cultivated and preserved by me with what zeal I can. And this indeed I shall do not unwillingly; for although what I myself shall be able to promise is slight, amid so great a forgetfulness of those matters in which you excel, it will nonetheless be pleasant now and then to confer with you about them — from whom one may expect all that is highest. For thus too your book, though for eleven years past I have scarcely touched anything of the kind, was so welcome that I read its accurate observations and ingenious demonstrations with the greatest pleasure; and, out of our old affection towards these disciplines, I most heartily congratulate their students that there are such craftsmen, in whom nothing seems lacking either for perfect knowledge or for finished practice; and I judge you to have deserved so much of those studies that you are most worthy of every commendation.</p><p>But I shall now come to your letter, to which I would have replied sooner had not our continual travels, from the beginning of February, scarcely allowed me to be at home a single day. Having returned home from England a few days ago, I have got some little leisure to re-read and re-weigh your matters — yet not as much as I could wish. For if the rest were at hand, I should like to test whether I could once again, by the numbers, lead the comets down from the sky into their native place among Aristotle's exhalations [<i>fumositates</i>]. But this will require another leisure and a fuller recollection of those matters. For now, on the present occasion, so far as my occupations permit, I shall write back briefly.</p><p>In your letter you ask me to signify my opinion candidly. As to that, your work pleases me greatly, and above all that noble pursuit of correcting the places and motions of the stars and of subjecting them to more exact numbers I must commend beyond measure; and to exhort you to go on consecrating your name among those eternal numbers I scarcely dare, since it is already plainly evident that you are borne thither with the utmost effort, furnished with all that is needful. If I could render any service in so noble an endeavour, I should not be wanting; but we here have neither such accurate instruments, nor are we permitted to have leisure for these celestial matters [apart] from perishable bodies. For now rhubarb and senna and the like are weighed out more [often] than all the circlings of the planets. Yet, most noble Sir, since that eternal Creator of all things has called you to that contemplation, and has equipped you with the things that were needful, go on, blessed [<i>macte</i>], and use this happiness and this excellence so that you may turn your talent to gain for posterity. But not by paradoxes — for I speak frankly, for the sake of truth — rather by more acceptable doctrines. For this [conclusion] of yours, in which I am persuaded you wished to imitate Cicero mathematically, by no means smiles upon me. For it does not please [me] that the most perfect order of the most wise Architect should be obscured, and that, with the mutable confounded with the immutable, a Chaos of disorder [αταξίας] should be introduced. Nor is it hidden [from me] that others before you held that opinion, against whom I once meditated a disputation in our <i>Cometometria</i>. But since they were led to it by no reasons, it is too evident [to need] that it should be shown [against them]. No one has tried to persuade [people] of it with so many [arguments] as you. Whether you have accomplished it, I for my part cannot affirm; for to me the conjectures of the natural philosophers, drawn from the natures of things, still seem more consonant [with reason]. And lest you should think I hold this view without reason, I shall, in a few [words] — if the inquiry into truth shall not be unwelcome — sift your arguments.</p><p>In the first five chapters, where you demonstrated [matters] concerning the apparent place and motion of the comet, things seem to me admirably established by you, nor — if the numbers hold — is there room for contradiction. But in the sixth chapter you go <i>further beyond</i> [<i>plus ultra</i>], about to open the heaven to new bodies, where Aristotle demonstrated there is no place for them. Nor are your reasonings here to be reckoned to prevail over his authority, if they be carefully examined. For those three Maxims which you bring forward in your first proof make for your opinion only probably, as you yourself admit; but I ask, by whose prior authority were they approved, that they should have a probable force of concluding? But let them be such: that to describe the arc of a great circle is to be in the heaven; that your phenomenon seemed to you to have described such [an arc], you demonstrate — but whether it appeared to others to move likewise, I doubt. To you it seems that a great circle cannot be described for a material cause, because [the body] would rather wander more erratically hither and thither, off such an orbit; and on that account you ascribe to it an innate knowledge of its own motion. To me it is judged by no means consonant that it should have been so intelligent of itself; but rather that that motion came about from a material cause, since [otherwise] it was nothing but a retardation from the rate of the first motion [<i>a primi motus numero retardatio</i>] — which was greater about the beginnings, when the greater mass offered more resistance, and thereafter decreased, as it were proportionally, the flame feeding on the matter, lamp-wise, in equal times. That it went off into a greater, not a smaller, circle is an argument that its distance from the Earth varied, the matter, namely, being day by day more and more attenuated and the flame prevailing toward the upper [regions]; which physical account of its motion is more consonant [with reason] than to maintain that it was a logical animal, or a body self-moving [αὐτοκίνητον] by its own moving mind.</p><p>By the second Maxim you rather eliminate comets from the heaven and banish them far off; [for] was that, whose mass, light, and motion continually decreased until they utterly vanished, in that immutable place? Granting which — to pass over the rest — the chief hypothesis of astronomy will be overthrown, by which the unchangeable condition of the celestial [bodies] is posited. The planets return, from the swifter to the [slower], [and] this to that, under fixed periods; but for comets there is no return from their extinction, nor is anything here more alike [to the celestial motions] than a straight line is to a circle. The third [Maxim], that it had a motion swifter than the Moon, [whereby] you raise it above her — proceeding so, it will fly off, by the end, even as far as Saturn; for if there is among the celestials such a Harmony of motions, will not whatever has some appearance of such a motion be set, according to its [own] rate, among them? To the celestials the principle of circular motion is innate; to the mutable it is only adventitious; and since these differ from those in their whole kind of nature, as incommensurables [<i>asymmetra</i>], they ought not to be compared according to more and less. And so by these <i>Topics</i> of yours comets are nowhere yet located — which I wished to indicate briefly. Let us now see whether mathematical measurements open the heaven to them, which indeed you chiefly attempt in your second proof, where, taking two observed distances of the comet from the mouth of Pegasus, you inquire whether the distances gathered from the calculation according to the Hypothesis agree. First, indeed, from those same distances, the declination and the distance from the Meridian being known, you gather the apparent altitudes for the given times. And, the comet being placed in the lunar orb, you inquire the angle of section which the vertical makes with the circle of apparent motion; by the benefit of which angle you elicit the parallaxes in longitude and latitude, whence in turn you reason out the distances from the mouth of Pegasus; and since the later [distance] comes out greater than the former, and the lower the comet is placed the more it will differ from observation, you conclude that it is necessarily above the Moon. We shall grant that in this successive course of reasonings nothing is wanting — [yet] will it on that account be above the Moon, because the distances so gathered differ from the observed [ones]? But by this reasoning it will <i>not</i> be above the Moon, because they will never agree; for however far you remove it, the later of the gathered [distances] will always be greater than the later of the observed [ones]. And where there is no parallax, the later will be merely equal to the former, the lines of the apparent and the true place coinciding; it will never become smaller, as the observation requires. Next, these [distances] are gathered with the phenomenon carried from the former to the later position by the first motion only, no account being taken of its proper motion; [so] how shall the later distance necessarily come out greater than the former? If anything will have accrued from parallax [it will be greater]; if nothing, it will be equal; never smaller, unless it come about from the proper motion — which, intervening (as if it be carried by its proper motion toward a star placed above it), [then] what would accrue from parallax is again subtracted, or even more; and then, the comet being placed even far below the Moon, not only will there be no diversity of aspect, but the later distance from the fixed [star] too will come out smaller than the former. Let the sublunary distance of the comet be <i>h b</i>, to which point <i>b</i> it would arrive by the first motion at the second observation, but let it be drawn back by its proper [motion] to <i>d</i>; and let the retraction on the instrument at <i>a</i> be almost insensible, to about 3' or 4', <i>c e</i>; yet let it come about, through that, in that immense distance of the eighth orb, that from the position <i>b</i> above the Moon it be raised in <i>f</i> up to <i>g</i> among the celestials. Where[upon], from parallax — which can scarcely be had — the comet's distance from the Earth cannot be determined: which no one has hitherto noticed.</p><p>But I now resume that course [of argument], which has been much interrupted: whence have you the given times? Not from the altitudes of the fixed [stars]. If from clocks, why do you not concede the same to others? Next, that angle of section <i>d c e</i> on page 114 is not found, nor is it the same as the angle <i>g c k</i> on page 112; because the circle <i>d c e</i> on page 112 passes through the apparent place, and is [the circle] of the apparent motion, but <i>f c g</i> on page 114 is another and passes through the true place [taken] from the Hypothesis. Accordingly <i>d c e</i> on page 114, by which you seek out the parallaxes, is not found; and therefore these parallaxes deduced in longitude and latitude are alien [to the case], nor are the visual longitudes other than those you call true on page 115 — so that these distances are not even rightly gathered according to the Hypothesis, whence it appears that this demonstration is in every way infirm for that which you intend. When you handle the matter more sightingly [<i>collimatius</i>], you consider the proper motion toward the star, and you find altogether the same distances, as all the examples teach, the first on page 119 excepted — in which those three first minutes [<i>tria scrupula prima</i>], for the sake of which you would have the comet removed by so many hundreds of Earth-semidiameters, do not arise from any retardation in respect of parallax, but creep in by the calculation only; for in all the others they do not occur, nor is it any wonder if the same things come out of the same, since nothing alien is mixed in. And in that immense distance, when parallax vanishes, it [the calculation] will produce no difference, [so that] that agreement with observation which you bring forward proves nothing.</p><p>The third proof can perhaps be adapted to drawing out the truth of this question, but it does nothing for your opinion; for, granting that reduction to the same moment, although in places differing in longitude and latitude the same distance of the comet from the star of Vultur [the Eagle, Aquila] be found, it does not follow that the interval of the places <i>a b</i> on page 125 is, in respect of the distance <i>b c</i>, of insensible quantity; for you neglect the difference of longitude, and set the visual lines <i>a c f</i> and <i>b c e</i> in the same plane, when they are in different [planes], because the places differ in longitude and [the lines] exist in different planes. Although they coincide in the body of the comet at the same moment, yet in the eighth orb they gape apart into contrary parts, <i>e</i> and <i>f</i>; from which, to the star <i>d</i>, let the distances of the arcs <i>d e</i>, <i>d f</i> be equal, and let an isosceles triangle <i>d e f</i> be made — and that with the comet placed far below the Moon; wherefore by this proof it will not be brought about that it had a celestial position.</p><p>In the longitude of Uraniborg and of Prague you seem to differ from others, in that you make Prague more easterly than Uraniborg, whereas — not to speak of others — in Ortelius's Maps the longitude of Prague is about 32, but of Uraniborg 35. I beg you, write out to me the exact longitudes that you will be able to have, of your [own] place, of Prague, and of Kassel.</p><p>In the fourth proof, the [altitudes] which you call altitudes in respect of the centre are no other than the apparent ones — in respect, that is, of the surface — because you deduce them from the apparent declination and azimuth. That in the lunar orb the difference of the true altitudes should be less than the difference of the apparent ones by 9' argues nothing other than that, in this supposed position, the comet admits a diversity of aspect; nor is there any doubt that the more you remove it, the smaller the difference of the true and apparent altitudes will be, until it becomes utterly none. Regiomontanus's demonstration is sufficiently adapted to an immobile phenomenon, however high it be raised, provided there be any parallax at all; but to a mobile [one] it in no way serves, nor is it made more apt by the correction you applied, because in the corrected Azimuth the comet did not appear, so that parallax is not to be sought in it: such a correction would have to be applied as would determine the proper motion toward the star, which exhausts the parallax — yet not even by this reasoning will its distance from the Earth be drawn out, because by parallax this cannot be investigated. And these are the reasonings by which you try to locate comets among the celestials; how truly they fall short, I judge to be manifest from the things I have brought forward.</p><p>What you bring forward in the eighth chapter, since it rests on these [reasonings], will stand or fall with them, nor does it please [me] to examine that at greater length. As to that sketch [<i>hypotyposis</i>] of the world-System: since, in order to avoid the dimension of interpenetrations [of the orbs], you will lay it down that the planets are carried through the liquid Aether by a progressive motion — to pass over other matters — you will overthrow the Hypothesis of circular motion, and the apparent anomaly will be rendered indemonstrable and inexplicable by spirals [<i>helices</i>]. A System is to be so constituted that the mathematician have [something] in which he may rest, nor be there [anything] for the physicist to murmur against; for the demonstrations of these [two kinds of] philosophers cannot contradict [one another]. The observations of the Most Illustrious Lord Landgrave please [me] greatly, nor do I doubt that from them the truth of the question can be drawn out. The rough notations of Maestlin and of Cornelius, although they make in some way for showing the apparent motion, contribute nothing toward demonstrating the comet's position. And the Philosophy of Tübingen could have instructed him better, had he not, for the sake of showing off his fancy, wished to publish such fabrications. It is not hidden from me how little Hagecius performed by art in [the matter of] the New Star, so that it is no wonder that in this comet he was so uncertain. Nay, it is no wonder that these mathematicians have lapsed into so absurd an opinion, since they neither took data sufficient for the inquiry proposed, nor had a sure demonstration which they might follow; for it is not yet established, nor has it been demonstrated by anyone, so far as I know, by what reasoning the distance of comets can be determined, if they have a proper motion. Regiomontanus's demonstration goes off into the absurd. Digges's wings do not reach so far; and that [demonstration] of the most learned Wittich — whom I deeply grieve to have migrated to the celestial [regions], to which with the utmost zeal he always aspired, [if only] for the sake of the Mathematics — which you reprehend under the name of Scultetus, although his observations be granted, will no more dispatch the matter. Let the figure of your book on page 38 be taken up again. He laid down that, in that section <i>B</i> of the verticals of the former and the latter observation, the true place of the comet was, and he makes the parallaxes answering to each time <i>b a</i>, <i>b k</i>; but at that point <i>b</i>, if the comet had a proper motion, it truly was not, so that parallax ought not to be reckoned from it; and therefore this demonstration serves an immobile phenomenon only. Since, then, by mathematical measurements the place of comets has not yet been arrived at, what shall we determine in this question? I for my part do not yet depart from my teacher Aristotle, who taught us to distinguish the mutable from the immutable in their places as in their natures, and demonstrated that those [mutable bodies], comprehended within the embrace of the lunar orb, can by no force of nature be raised above [it]. If you require mathematical demonstrations, since others are not for the present at hand, I shall take up those of Master Wittich. You refute the observation, [on the ground] that those stars could not have been under the same vertical at the same time as the comet. Although this does not break the demonstration (for it concludes under different [verticals]), yet I do not see how your reasoning will merit more credit than the affirmation of that most learned craftsman about his own perception. It is established for me that it was his, and that he lacked neither skill in observing nor suitable instruments; so that, if we cannot prove negligence, we ought to concede those observations, noted by the senses, as principles. And the demonstration, although I had now rejected it, yet merits much credit, in that it was adapted to that time at which the comet was insensibly failing from the rate of the first motion, and especially [in that] between the two observations there was no variation in the intervening time, so that the section <i>B</i> on page 38 was, when he observed, the true place; wherefore, since the rest follow, the proof of Wittich merits great credit. So too, if to Noltius his perceptions be not denied (nor do I understand how they can be refuted, since the observation was made at about the same time), and the operation does not go off into the absurd, [the matter] concludes very probably that the comet was sublunary. I myself observed the same [comet], with suitable instruments of just size — a cross-staff, a quadrant, and a parallactic [instrument] — and, with some diligence applied, took at each observation the apparent altitudes of the comet and of Aquila or of another star, then the apparent distance of the comet and the star; from which I found the azimuths and times by demonstration. And at the first apparition I certainly fell into absurd operations, but afterwards I found it to have a parallax of more or less 5 degrees. I noticed that it had a proper motion, by which the parallax was exhausted; and some things came up that were not amenable to geometry [ἀγεωμέτρητα], nor was the most exact demonstration of that matter then known [to me], whence it came about that I suspended judgement. Since that time all those [papers] have been so shaken out of our hands that it has never been possible to take them up again, and I am now less fit for such calculation; yet if some longed-for leisure shall ever come, I shall take them up again somewhat, and, if it please [you], confer with you about them. I ask you to send me all the observations of excellent craftsmen, and above all your own, which you have concerning the New Star and the comets that have shone in years past.</p><p>And these are the things I wished to bring forward against your <i>Paradox</i>; which indeed have been brought forward in no other way than to show by what [considerations] I myself am led [to see] why I cannot yet depart from the learned conjectures of the natural philosophers; and I earnestly beg that you take them in good part. I hear, most renowned Sir, that you excel in chemistry; if you have anything singular in this kind of craft — whether it pertain to making extractions, or to purging waters, or anything of the sort — I beg you to communicate it to me. The Germans, as I have heard, now have a discovery by which they purge the body with distillates; which, though we do not need it, I do not wish to be ignorant of. I have often thought to try it, but since reason did not counsel it, lest I lose my labour and oil [<i>operam et oleum</i>] — as has happened to me more than once — I left it off. But, my paper failing, I shall make an end; and take these [things] that have been written back in good part, for being sick I am not allowed to treat the matter more diligently. Farewell. Given at Edinburgh in Scotland, first in March, afterwards about the beginning of May.</p><p>Most loving of you,<br>John Craig,<br>Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioannes Craigus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician and natural philosopher, trained at Frankfurt-an-der-Oder and Königsberg, an Aristotelian opponent of the new cosmology; later physician to King James VI/I. This is the <b>opening salvo of the Craig–Brahe cometary controversy</b>: a point-by-point critique of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis liber secundus</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), Tycho's treatise on the comet of 1577 — the book (\"liber\") and accompanying letter that William Stuart had delivered. Craig defends the orthodox Aristotelian doctrine of a sublunary, exhalation-fed comet against Tycho's parallax-based demonstration of a supralunary, celestial comet.</li><li><b><i>D. Guilhelmus Stuartus</b></i> = <b>William Stuart</b> (Colonel William Stewart), the Scottish diplomat/soldier who carried Tycho's letter and book to Scotland; <i>vir magnificus</i> = \"magnificent gentleman.\"</li><li><b><i>Liddelius noster</b></i> = <b>Duncan Liddel</b> (1561–1613), Scottish mathematician-physician then at Rostock/Helmstedt, who had visited Tycho and acted as the intermediary recommending Craig to him.</li><li><b><i>Wittichius</b></i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician who worked with Tycho on Hven in 1580 and whose cometary demonstration Tycho had published (Craig says Tycho criticised it \"under the name of Scultetus\"). Craig had known Wittich and laments his recent death (<i>ad coelestia migrasse</i> — \"migrated to the celestial regions,\" a deliberate pun given the subject). Per the house Brahe profile, Tycho's relationship with Wittich is contested (the Copernicus-marginalia attribution dispute); Craig here treats Wittich's observations as authoritative <i>principia</i>.</li><li><b><i>Scultetus</b></i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Schultz, 1540–1614), Görlitz mathematician; Tycho discussed a cometary demonstration under his name which Craig identifies as actually Wittich's.</li><li><b><i>Maestlinus</b></i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), Tübingen astronomer (Kepler's teacher), author of an <i>Observatio et demonstratio cometae</i> (1578); <b><i>Cornelius</b></i> = <b>Cornelius Gemma</b> (1535–1578), Louvain physician-astronomer who also wrote on the 1577 comet. <i>Philosophia Tubingensis</i> = \"the Philosophy of Tübingen,\" i.e. Maestlin's school; <i>rudes … notationes</i> = \"rough notations.\"</li><li><b><i>Haggecius</b></i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), Bohemian imperial physician-astronomer who wrote on both the 1572 <i>nova stella</i> and the 1577 comet; Craig disparages his artistry.</li><li><b><i>Digessaei alae</b></i> = \"Digges's wings\" — <b>Thomas Digges</b> (c. 1546–1595), whose <i>Alae seu scalae mathematicae</i> (1573, on the new star) is meant; the phrase puns on the title (<i>alae</i> = \"wings\"). Rendered literally with the title-pun flagged.</li><li><b><i>Regiomontani demonstratio</b></i> = the parallax method for comets in <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johann Müller von Königsberg, 1436–1476), <i>De cometae magnitudine longitudineque</i>; Craig argues it holds only for an immobile object.</li><li><b><i>Nolthius</b></i> = <b>Andreas Nolth(ius)</b> / Noltius, a German observer of the 1577 comet whose sublunary result Craig credits; identification only probable — <b>flagged</b>.</li><li><b><i>Landgravius</b></i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6); his Kassel observations Craig praises.</li><li><b><i>Ortelii Mappae</b></i> = the maps of <b>Abraham Ortelius</b> (<i>Theatrum orbis terrarum</i>), cited for the longitudes of Prague (~32°) and Uraniborg (~35°); Craig challenges Tycho's making Prague <i>easterly</i> of Uraniborg.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b>; <i>Praga</i> → <b>Prague</b>; <i>Cassilia</i> → <b>Kassel</b> (protocol §5.7); <i>Edenburgum in Scotia</i> → <b>Edinburgh, Scotland</b>; <i>Porthmus vester</i> = \"your Sound\" (the Øresund); <i>Norvegici scopuli</i> = \"the Norwegian rocks.\"</li><li><b>Greek (transcribed and translated per protocol §2).</b> (1) <i>αταξίας</i> (printed without breathing/accent in the source; classical <i>ἀταξίας</i>, gen. of <i>ἀταξία</i>) = \"of disorder/disarray\" — <i>αταξίας Chaos</i> = \"a Chaos of disorder.\" (2) <i>αὐτοκίνητον</i> = \"self-moving\" — Craig mocks the idea that the comet is a self-moving body endowed with mind. (3) <i>ἀγεωμέτρητα</i> = \"things not amenable to geometry / un-geometrical,\" echoing the Academy's <i>ἀγεωμέτρητος</i>; rendered \"things not amenable to geometry.\"</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5.1).</b> <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>declinatio</i> → declination; <i>altitudo</i> → altitude; <i>azimuthum</i> → azimuth (kept); <i>Meridianus</i> → Meridian; <i>anomalia (apparens)</i> → (apparent) anomaly; <i>Hypothesis / Systema mundanum</i> → Hypothesis / world-System; <i>octavus orbis</i> → the eighth orb (the sphere of fixed stars); <i>orbis lunaris / orbis Lunae</i> → the lunar orb; <i>liquidus Aether</i> → the liquid Aether. <i>Semid. Terrae</i> = <i>semidiametri Terrae</i>, \"Earth-semidiameters\" (Earth radii). <i>aspectus diversitas</i> = \"diversity of aspect,\" i.e. parallax in the period sense; kept as the author's phrase.</li><li><b>Instruments (§5.2).</b> <i>Radius</i> → cross-staff (radius astronomicus); <i>Quadrans</i> → quadrant; <i>Parallaticum</i> → parallactic [instrument] (triquetrum); <i>Instrumentum</i> → instrument.</li><li><b>Astrology/chemistry (§5.4).</b> <i>Chymica</i> → chemistry; <i>extractiones</i> → extractions; <i>aquae purgantes</i> → purging waters; <i>distillata</i> → distillates; <i>Rhabarbarum et Sene</i> → rhubarb and senna (purgative drugs — Craig's physician's idiom contrasting medical practice with planetary theory). <i>remedium</i>-type modernisation avoided per protocol.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> A single <i>asterisk</i> survives in the source at <i>\"a celeriore ad </i> terram\"<i> (page 89 of the corpus text): the edition prints <b>\\</b></i> where a word (most plausibly <i>tardiorem</i>, \"slower,\" to complete the antithesis <i>a celeriore ad [tardiorem]</i>) is missing or illegible. The translation supplies \"[slower]\" in brackets and the asterisk is <b>flagged as a textual lacuna</b> — see crux below. The string <i>\"5°\"</i> is read as \"5 degrees.\"</li><li><b>Allusions &amp; idiom.</b> <i>plus ultra</i> — \"further beyond\" (the Habsburg/Pillars-of-Hercules motto, here a barb that Tycho oversteps the bounds Aristotle set); kept. <i>fumositates</i> (Aristotle's) → \"exhalations\" (the dry/hot vapours of <i>Meteorologica</i> I from which comets were held to form); rendered \"exhalations\" with the literal \"fumes/smoky stuff\" sense noted. <i>macte (hac felicitate et virtute utere)</i> → \"go on, blessed; use…\" (the encouragement-formula <i>macte virtute</i>). <i>operam et oleum perdere</i> → \"lose labour and oil\" (proverbial: waste effort, lit. lamp-oil); kept literal with gloss. <i>Cometometria nostra</i> = \"our <i>Cometometria</i>,\" Craig's own (unpublished/projected) treatise on comet-measurement. <i>Topica tua</i> = \"these <i>Topics</i> of yours\" — Craig sarcastically calls Tycho's three Maxims mere dialectical <i>topoi</i> (places of argument), not demonstrations; rendered \"<i>Topics</i>\" with the Aristotelian-dialectic sense flagged. <i>Hypotyposis</i> = \"sketch/outline\" (the Tychonic world-system sketch in ch. 8). <i>lychni instar</i> → \"lamp-wise,\" the candle/lamp simile for the comet's matter being consumed by its flame.</li><li><b>Page references.</b> Craig cites Tycho's <i>De mundi … phaenomenis</i> by page (pp. 38, 112, 114, 115, 119, 125) and by lettered points in the diagrams (B; <i>a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, k</i>); these are reproduced verbatim, as they key to the 1588 figures and are essential to the argument.</li><li><b>Dateline (crux of dating — handled per protocol §3 and the header rule).</b> The subscription reads <i>\"Datae Edenburgi in Scotia, <b>in Martio primum, postea sub Maij initium</b>\"</i> — \"Given at Edinburgh, <b>first in March, afterwards about the beginning of May</b>.\" The letter was thus <b>begun in March and finished about the beginning of May 1589</b> (O.S.; Scotland was on the Julian calendar). The dataset `label` (\"1589. maj. Edinburgh\") records only the completion. The header therefore gives the as-written span \"March / beginning of May 1589 [O.S.]\" rather than asserting a single day the text does not substantiate; no day-of-month is stated in the source for either terminus, so none is invented. <b>This is a genuine in-letter dating notice (a letter composed over ~two months), preserved, not reconciled to a single date.</b></li><li><b>Edition of record &amp; witness.</b> Per the source header, the text-of-record is the <i>Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum</i> (as carried into Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII); the sole MS witness cited is <b>A = Berlin</b>, \"a manuscript copy in a copy of Tycho Brahe's astronomical writings\" (shelfmark not given in the source — recorded as cited).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed a spurious \"se\" interpolated between \"Pragae\" and \"et Cassiliae\" in the closing request for exact longitudes (TBOO VII p.179, lines 29-30), per Dreyer's printed text; the English translation was already unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and the argumentative structure are rendered securely and the register (a learned, courteous-but-combative physician's critique) is matched; the residual uncertainties are the asterisk lacuna (crux 1), the Noltius identification (crux 2), and the diagram-dependent geometry (crux 3), all flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 10 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.174-183) in three chunks (1-4, 5-8, 9-10) and collated the full letter sentence-by-sentence against source_text, and source_text against published_english. Verified opening/closing formulas, all proper names (Stuart, Liddel, Wittich x4, Aristotle, Cicero, Regiomontanus x2, Maestlin, Cornelius, Hagecius, Digges, Scultetus, Noltius, Landgrave), all place-names (Edinburgh, Scotland, Uraniborg, Prague, Kassel, Norway), and all numbers/citations (longitudes 32/35, page-refs 112/114/115/119/125/38x2, arcminutes 3'/4'/9', parallax 5 degrees, 'eleven years', 'five years'). Also traced the pronoun chain in the Wittich/Craig self-observation passages against the known 'self-boast flipped to Tycho' failure mode - no flip found. Found one TEXT_ERROR (spurious 'se', p.179) versus Dreyer; no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS. The single TEXT_ERROR does not change what a scholar would take away, but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text inserts a spurious word 'se' between 'Pragae' and 'et Cassiliae' that is absent from Dreyer's printed text; the translation silently drops it without distorting the sense, so the English itself is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>quæfo, ad me perfcribas exactas, quas habere poteris tui loci, Pragæ et Caßiliæ longitudines. (TBOO VII, p.179, lines 29-30)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect directly against the Dreyer page image for TBOO VII p.179 (slice pages 5-8 of the 10-page letter slice, covering pp.178-181), which plainly prints \"Pragæ et Caßiliæ longitudines\" with no word between \"Pragæ\" and \"et\". This confirms the audit's claim exactly. The remaining pages of the letter (172-177, 182-183) were not re-collated, as no other defect was flagged against them.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Berlin, Håndskrevet afskrift i et eksemplar af Tycho Brahes astronomiske skrifter i Berlin."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15890618001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891025001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of Tycho's letter and book via William Stuart, thanks him for the gift, and expresses eagerness for the friendship Liddel had proposed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890500001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890510001", "d": "1589-05-10", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 10 May 1589 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.48</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE VIRO CLARISS. D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO INSULAe URANIEBURGI ETC. DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>LITERAe tuae Nobiliss. et amantiss. TYCHO, attulerunt mihi et voluptatem et dolorem; voluptatem quidem, quod abs te adferrentur, de quo iam dudum cognoscere cupiebam; dolorem autem, quod intellexissem meas tibi cum libro VERNERI, abs te iam olim expetito, nondum fuisse redditas. Et profecto suspiciones variae agitabant animum meum, ut conquiescere nullo modo potuerim. Miseram eas Gorlicium ad MYLIUM unâ cum libro illo, ac magnopere rogabam, ut SCULTETUM commonefaceret, si quid literarum ad te dare vellet, meis adiungeret et primo quoque tempore ad te perferri curaret. Indicabam etiam eidem iuxta documentum tuarum literarum omnes commoditates et occasiones mittendi ad te. Cum autem in ista animi mei perturbatione versor, ecce, oportune sane, ad levationem mei doloris, afferuntur ad me literae a MYLIO, quibus significat et se magnopere excusat, cur diutius sua opinione delituerint apud se meae literae, et ut plenius rei veritatem cognoscas meque apud te excusatum habeas, exemplum ipsius literarum ad te mitto. Sed iam, ut spero, utriusque nostrum desiderio satisfactum, tibique meas redditas esse existimo. Utinam deinceps nostrae literae cursum nanciscantur feliciorem. His plura quod addam habeo nihil. Ex hoc mercatu avide expectabam primum tuum librum. Sed cur non prodierit, ex tuis ad me intellexi. Deum oro, ut tuis studijs Reipub. literariae causa favere velit. Nos ante menses circiter duos amisimus virum summum et incomparabilem tuique studiosiss. D. DUDITHIUM. Decessit etiam ex hac vita 19. Aprilis Viennae FABRICIUS. Utriusque mortem admodum doleo. Sed quum Deo Opt. Max. eos in beatiorem vitam ex hisce miseriarum turbis evocare libuit, in sanctissima eius voluntate nobis acquiescendum est.</p><p>Barones, quos salvere iubebas, non sunt in hac aula. D. DUDITHIUS, bonae memoriae, de libris WITYCHIJ iam olim cum sorore ipsius egit. Sed morosiorem illam fuisse intellexi, quam ut aliquid ex bibliotheca fratris communicare vellet aut saltem lustrare pateretur.</p><p>B. V.<br>Pragae 1O. Maij 1589.<br>T. studiosiss.<br>Thad.<br>D. fratrem tuum meis verbis officiose salvere iube.<br>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM MARTINI MYLIJ AD THAD.</p><p>CLARISS. vir, fateor peccasse me in te atque adeo in amicitiam nostram, qui tam diu nullas ad te literas misi, cum praesertim mihi de rebus ad te pertinentibus, puta de literis et libro tuo ad TYCHONEM curando, scribendum esset. Sed ita est ut scribo. Diutius opinione mea delituerunt apud me TYCHONIS literae. Unam enim saltem occasionem habemus mittendi per nostratem quendam civem, qui Hamburgum ire solet, idque uno anno vix bis. Iter enim est longinquius. Cum eo igitur misi tua ante paucas septimanas, credo vix tres quatuorve ad summum. Ante illud tempus fateor suppuduisse me quicquam scribere, ne tu forte indignarere, quod mea incuria commisissem, ut epistola facta esset ἕωλος. Eam religionem meam ut boni et aequi consulas, te oro.</p><p>Gorlicij die Philippi et Iacobi Apostolorum 1589.<br>POST SCRIPTA.</p><p>IN Cancellariam Imperij memineris, ut ex tenore Privilegij aliquot exempla tui operis mittas. Ego enim, qui fueram solicitator Privilegij, hac de re fui admonitus. Respondi, fuisse quidem mihi abs te per quendam Studiosum missum unum exemplum, sed Bibliopolis integrum opus nondum fuisse publicatum, neque te hanc partem secundam absque alijs publicare voluisse. Cum igitur in mercatu futuro Francoffordensi integrum opus (ut spero) prodibit, et exempla aliquot in Cancellariam miseris, vellem salutares tuis literis D. CURTIUM eiusque amicitiam expeteres. Mihi est amicissimus, et profecto dignus est, qui ab omnibus diligatur. Ad ipsa Mathemata natus videtur. Et certe, si ijs solis ipsi vacandum esset, aliquid praestare posset.</p><p>Titulus D. CURTIJ: IACOBUS CURTIUS a Senfftenau, Caes. Consiliarius et Procancell. Imperij.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most renowned for nobility of birth, for virtue and for erudition, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, lord of the island of Uraniborg etc., his lord and friend to be revered. Greeting.</b></p><p>Your letter, most noble and most loving Tycho, brought me both pleasure and pain: pleasure, indeed, because it was brought from you, of whom I had long been wishing to have news; but pain, because I had learned that mine to you, [sent] together with Werner's book — long since requested by you — had not yet been delivered. And in truth various suspicions kept stirring my mind, so that I could in no way be at rest. I had sent them to Görlitz, to Mylius, together with that book, and earnestly begged him to remind Scultetus that, if he wished to send any letter to you, he should join it to mine and see that it was conveyed to you at the very first opportunity. I also pointed out to the same man, in accordance with the directions of your own letter, all the conveniences and occasions for sending [things] to you. But while I am caught up in this disturbance of mind, behold — very seasonably indeed, for the easing of my grief — a letter is brought to me from Mylius, in which he both makes known, and greatly excuses himself, why my letters lay hidden with him longer than he had supposed; and, that you may more fully know the truth of the matter and hold me excused before you, I send you a copy of his letter itself. But now, as I hope, the desire of us both has been satisfied, and I reckon that mine have been delivered to you. Would that hereafter our letters may meet with a happier passage. I have nothing further to add to these things. From this fair I was eagerly awaiting your first book. But why it did not appear, I have understood from your [letter] to me. I pray God that He may be willing to favour your studies for the sake of the republic of letters. About two months ago we lost a man supreme and incomparable, and most devoted to you, Master Dudith. There departed from this life also, on 19 April, at Vienna, Fabricius. I grieve much at the death of both. But since it has pleased God, Best and Greatest, to call them out of these tumults of miseries into a more blessed life, we must rest content in His most holy will.</p><p>The barons whom you bade [me] greet are not at this court. Master Dudith, of good memory, had already some while ago dealt with Wittich's books with his [Wittich's] sister. But I have learned that she was too peevish [a woman] to be willing to share anything from her brother's library, or at least to allow it to be examined.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Prague, 10 May 1589.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[deus].<br>Bid your brother, in my words, a dutiful greeting.<br>COPY OF MARTIN MYLIUS'S LETTER TO THAD[DEUS].</p><p>Most renowned sir, I confess that I have sinned against you, and indeed against our friendship, in that for so long I sent you no letter, when especially I ought to have written to you about matters pertaining to you — namely, about seeing to your letter and book [destined] for Tycho. But it is as I write. Tycho's letters lay hidden with me longer than I had supposed. For we have but a single opportunity of sending [things], by way of a certain townsman of ours who is accustomed to go to Hamburg, and that scarcely twice in one year. For the journey is rather long. With him, therefore, I sent your [parcel] a few weeks ago — I believe scarcely three or four [weeks] at the most. Before that time I confess I was somewhat ashamed to write anything, lest perhaps you should be vexed that, by my carelessness, I had let the letter become stale [ἕωλος]. I beg you to take this scruple of mine in good and fair part.</p><p>Görlitz, on the day of the Apostles Philip and James [1 May], 1589.<br>POSTSCRIPT.</p><p>[Remember the matter] of the Imperial Chancery: that, by the terms of the Privilege, you should send some copies of your work. For I, who had been the solicitor of the Privilege, was admonished about this matter. I replied that one copy had indeed been sent to me from you by a certain student, but that the whole work had not yet been published for the booksellers, nor had you wished to publish this second part without the others. Since, therefore, at the coming Frankfurt fair the whole work will (as I hope) appear, and you will have sent some copies to the Chancery, I would wish that you should greet Master Curtius by your letter and seek his friendship. He is most friendly to me, and is truly worthy to be esteemed by all. He seems born for mathematics itself. And surely, if he had only these to attend to, he could accomplish something.</p><p>The title of Master Curtius: Jacobus Curtius of Senftenau, Imperial Councillor and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), imperial physician at Prague to Maximilian II and Rudolf II, astronomer, and Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent and intermediary. The subscription <i>Thad.</i> is his abbreviated signature. <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene vale</i>, \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Recipient/address.</b> <i>D. Tychoni Brahe Dano, domino insulae Uranieburgi</i> — \"Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, lord of the island of Uraniborg.\" <i>Uranieburgi</i> here names the <b>island</b> (Hven) by the name of its castle, <b>Uraniborg</b>.</li><li><b><i>liber Verneri</b></i> — \"Werner's book,\" a work by <b>Johann Werner</b> (1468–1522) of Nuremberg, long requested by Tycho (<i>abs te iam olim expetito</i>); most probably Werner's geographical/mathematical writings or his tract on the motion of the eighth sphere. The exact title is not specified in the letter; left unspecified rather than inferred.</li><li><b><i>Mylius</b></i> = <b>Martin Mylius</b> (Martin Mohl, 1542–1611), rector of the Görlitz Gymnasium and a postal intermediary on the Prague–Görlitz–Hamburg–Hven route. <i>Gorlicium / Gorlicij</i> = <b>Görlitz</b> (Lusatia). The bracketed enclosure <i>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM MARTINI MYLIJ AD THAD.</i> is Hagecius's transcribed copy of Mylius's covering letter, forwarded as proof of the delay; the code-switch into the embedded letter is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Scultetus</b></i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Barthel Schultz, 1540–1614), Görlitz mathematician, cartographer and later burgomaster, a correspondent of Tycho's; named here as a possible fellow-sender on the Görlitz relay.</li><li><b><i>Dudithius</b></i> = <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (András Dudith / Andreas Dudithius, 1533–1589), the Hungarian humanist, former bishop, and anti-astrological author on comets, resident at Breslau (Wrocław), where he died on 23 February 1589 — consistent with <i>ante menses circiter duos</i> (\"about two months ago\") relative to the 10 May dateline. A close friend and correspondent of Hagecius.</li><li><b><i>Fabricius</b></i> = <b>Paul Fabricius</b> (c. 1519–1589), imperial mathematician and physician at Vienna, an observer of the 1572 nova. The letter's <i>19. Aprilis Viennae</i> (died 19 April, at Vienna) fixes the identification: <b>not</b> David Fabricius (d. 1617). Named without forename in the source; identification supplied in brackets only by inference and recorded here.</li><li><b><i>Witychij … cum sorore ipsius</b></i> — \"Wittich's [books] … with his sister\": <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Breslau mathematician (prosthaphaeresis; Copernican-geocentric models) whose library and manuscripts Tycho sought to acquire. The corpus confirms Tycho's pursuit of Wittich's library through Hagecius and the Barons Liechtenstein and Herberstein in 1589, again through Monaw in 1595, and successfully in 1598. <i>de libris … egit</i> and the sister's refusal to <i>communicare</i> or allow the library to be <i>lustrare</i> (examined/surveyed) are rendered without sharpening.</li><li><b><i>Barones, quos salvere iubebas, non sunt in hac aula</b></i> — \"The barons whom you bade [me] greet are not at this court\": presumably the Liechtenstein/Herberstein patrons sought as intermediaries for the Wittich books; the antecedent is not named in the letter and is not supplied in the translation.</li><li><b><i>primum tuum librum</b></i> / <b><i>hanc partem secundam</b></i> / <b><i>integrum opus</b></i> — \"your first book\" / \"this second part\" / \"the whole work\": Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>. The volume Tycho had printed and distributed (the 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>) is the <b>second part</b> of the projected three-part work; Mylius's report that Tycho would not publish <i>hanc partem secundam absque alijs</i> (\"this second part without the others\") and that <i>integrum opus</i> was awaited at Frankfurt matches Tycho's stated publication plan. The titles are not spelled out in the source and are not inserted into the translation.</li><li><b><i>ex hoc mercatu</b></i> / <b><i>in mercatu futuro Francoffordensi</b></i> — \"from this fair\" / \"at the coming Frankfurt fair\": the semi-annual <b>Frankfurt book fair</b> (<i>Frankfurter Messe</i>), the chief channel of European book distribution.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>ἕωλος</i> (<i>héōlos</i>) — literally \"a day old,\" hence \"stale, out of date\" (of food left overnight, transferred to a letter overtaken by events). Transcribed and glossed per protocol §2; rendered \"stale\" in the body with the Greek retained in brackets.</li><li><b><i>Cancellaria Imperij</b></i> / <b><i>Privilegium</b></i> / <b><i>solicitator Privilegij</b></i> — the <b>Imperial Chancery</b> at Prague; the imperial printing <b>privilege</b> (copyright protection) for Tycho's work, of which Mylius had been the <i>solicitator</i> (the agent who procured it). The privilege required deposit of copies (<i>ex tenore Privilegij aliquot exempla … mittas</i>).</li><li><b><i>D. Curtius</b></i> = <b>Jacobus Curtius a Senftenau</b> (Jakob Kurz von Senftenau, 1554–1594), Imperial Councillor and <b>Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b> (<i>Caes. Consiliarius et Procancell. Imperij</i>), a mathematically gifted patron; Mosley's despatch tables record a presentation/contact of Tycho's work to \"Jakob Kurtz (1589)/1590,\" corroborating Mylius's recommendation here. <i>Procancellarius</i> rendered \"Vice-Chancellor.\"</li><li><b><i>fratrem tuum</b></i> — \"your brother\": one of Tycho's brothers (most likely <b>Steen Brahe</b>); not named in the letter.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The body dateline reads <i>Pragae 1O. Maij 1589</i> (\"Prague, 10 May 1589\"); the dataset labels the letter <b>n.s.</b> (New Style). Hagecius, an imperial (Catholic) correspondent at Prague, would write New Style after the 1582 reform. <b>No conversion is made.</b> The enclosed Mylius letter is dated <i>Gorlicij die Philippi et Iacobi Apostolorum 1589</i> = the feast of SS Philip and James (<b>1 May</b>); glossed per protocol §3 with the idiom kept. Görlitz (Lutheran Upper Lusatia) retained the Julian calendar in 1589, so Mylius's \"1 May\" is in all likelihood <b>Old Style</b>, against Hagecius's New-Style \"10 May\" — a style mismatch between the two datelines that is <b>flagged, not reconciled</b>: the two dates are roughly nine days apart, consistent with Mylius writing O.S. shortly before Hagecius forwards N.S., but the letter does not state the styles and the inference is not forced into the dates.</li><li><b>Source-block note.</b> <i>Pragae 1O. Maij</i> preserves the printed reading with a capital letter \"O\" standing for the zero of \"10\"; kept as printed per protocol §4. <i>unâ</i> and <i>morosiorem</i> etc. retain the edition's diacritics/orthography.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the principal persons are all corpus-confirmed. The soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>N.S./O.S. style mismatch</b> between Hagecius's \"10 May\" (labelled n.s.) and Mylius's Julian-feast \"1 May\" datelines; (2) the unnamed <i>liber Verneri</i> (which Werner work is not stated); and (3) the unnamed <i>Barones</i> and <i>frater tuus</i>, left unidentified in the translation as in the source.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Hagecius to Tycho, 10 May 1589, including the enclosed copy of Martin Mylius's letter and the postscript) against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.182-183 page images. Every proper name (Werner, Mylius, Scultetus, Dudith, Fabricius, Wittich, Curtius/Senftenau), date (19 April, 10 May, 1 May feast of Philip and James, 1589), and number ('three or four weeks') matches Dreyer word for word, including the Greek eolos. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1588 (7 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/45-48", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13966897"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.48."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 16, "prev": "brahe-t_15890322001", "next": "brahe-t_15890520001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890322001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891101001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that a previous letter and Werner's book, sent via Mylius at Görlitz, failed to reach Tycho, and explains the transmission arrangements made.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890510001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890510002", "d": "1589-05-10", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Caspar Peucer", "ck": "Caspar Peucer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Dessau", "t": "Caspar Peucer → Tycho Brahe — Dessau, 10 May 1589 [O.S.] (catalogued 22 May 1588)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with extensive Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witnesses: A = Paris, Bibliothèque Nationale, fonds français 13040 (copy/afskrift); B = Mikulov (Nikolsburg), II 72, fol. C7 sqq.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AD DOMINUM TYCHONEM BRAHE, DANUM, MATHEMATICUM CLARISSIMUM, VIRUM NOBILISSIMUM. S. D.</p><p>MAGNA cum laetitia accepi, cum maiore voluptate legi literas tuas, vir vere nobilis, et adiuncta illis τὰ ἀπαμειβόμενα scripta, quibus erudito et artificibus summis digno examine confertis συμπαρατηρήσεις vestras illustrissimus Princeps Landgravius Hassiae WILHELMUS et tu, sed serius quam oportuit. Abs te enim missae Uraniburgo (quod Uranoscopiam vocare soleo, ut speculam coeli) 13. Septembris anno 88 pervenerunt ad me demum ultima Ianuarij Iuliani anno sequente 89; ubi latuerint aut haeserint, et quorum culpa, nescio. Tardius vero et ego respondeo, destitutus occasionibus et copia eorum, qui tuto ac mature perferunt. His nunc potitus adventu ad nos illustrissimi Principis, Ducis Holsatiae IOHANNIS, et volui satisfacere et tibi et magnifico Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO, quantum potui in hac ex senecta et carceribus debilitata virium ingenij et memoriae (qui tamdiu a libris et studijs Astronomicis absum) affectique corporis valetudine, et in his occupationibus, quibus nunc etiam obruor. Primum autem gratias tibi ago, quas debeo et possum, quod tam accurate exponis mihi occasionem, historiam et rationem tuarum observationum et hypothesium, in quibus vere affirmo me non reperire, quod desiderem. Haereo alicubi, ut in luminaribus nimirum, cum illa circumagas circulis Terrae homocentricis (quibus quam orbibus ut commodius sic rectius et verius uteris) utrum anomaliam apparentem in eis eadem qua in alijs Planetis quinque ratione (quibus Solem statuis centrum) per circellos duos parvos expedire velis COPERNICAeO exemplo, quod probas in Luna, in qua ille duobus epicyclis usus est, a quibus tu abhorres, an vero alia, quae mihi non occurrit. Reliqua ita plana, explicata et perspicua esse iudico, et ad doctrinae Astronomicae καὶ ψηφοφορίας facilitatem tam accommodata, ut me fateri oporteat, veterum hypotheses, si his conferantur, labyrinthos videri, illa orbium dissimilium varietate et multitudine nimia, qua tamen an assequantur, quod promittunt, experientia docet. Totum aethereae regionis tractum, stellarum corporibus exceptis, tenuissima constare aetherea substantia purissimaque et radijs omnibus pervia, eaque liquida et fluida, divinae sapientiae verbo coelesti patefactae testimonia non dubie contra Philosophos demonstrant. Quam mihi sententiam persuadent et confirmant non illa tantum ante prolata e Scriptura dicta, sed multo plura alia. MOYSI enim 1. Genesis coelum expansum quiddam est; id significat enim vox Hebraea Rakia, id est quiddam ἀραιωθὲν καὶ λεπτωθὲν κατὰ διάκρισιν veram, fusum atque dilatatum expansu immenso in corpus δυνάμει διαφανές, quod luce accensa ac diffusa creatione factum est διαφανὲς ἐνεργείᾳ. Diffusam hanc lucem omnipotentia Opificis summi postea die quarto collectam rursus coegit, coagmentavit et digessit κατὰ σύγκρισιν in luminarium ac stellarum distincta corpora. Septuaginta interpretes verterunt στερέωμα, unde firmamenti manavit appellatio. Utraque appellatione indicari existimo cum repagula illa admiranda nobisque inexplorata, quibus moles aquarum orbi creato circumfusa sustinetur ac coercetur, tum hoc nobis obvium et saeculis omnibus a Philosophis etiam agnitum ἀμετάβλητον regionis aethereae, quod propter ARISTOTELES inter secundas causas invexit τὴν ἀνάγκην ἀπὸ τῆς φύσεως distinctam ac diversam. Nec aliud per aeneos Coelos voluit HIOBUS; fortasse quia respexerunt ad pervulgatam tunc et vulgo creditam ex commentis Philosophorum in Assyria et Aegypto opinionem de orbibus coelestibus interpretes, ex consuetudine vulgi hinc usitatum, ut saepe fit, vocabulum adsumpserunt; cum Hebraeo enim non congruit. Talem autem Coeli expansum, diductumque ac dilatationem perpetuo Scriptura repetit et inculcat: ESAIAe 45: Manus meae tetenderunt Caelos. ESAIAe 44: Ego sum Dominus, faciens omnia, extendens Caelos solus, stabiliens Terram, et nullus mecum. IOB. 9: Qui extendit Caelos solus. IEREM. 1O et Psalmo 135: Qui praeparat orbem in sapientia sua, et prudentia sua extendit Caelos. Extensionem illam autem, quae solius omnipotentiae opus est, coepisse exordio facto ab aquis Terrae circumdatis, quae τῶν διαφανῶν sunt quasi principium, sed adhuc terrenae concretionis sordibus atque umbris densum et opacum, quibus in halitum fusis, et halitu illo magis magisque semper attenuato perpurgatoque ac ceu polito et diffuso usque ad illam tenuitatem et puritatem, quae recipiendae luci purissimae subiectum fuit idoneum stabile et perenne, emersisse illum immensum Coeli tractum Scriptura docet, ratio etiam et experientia convincit, quamquam non assequitur ullo modo attenuationis purgationisque rationem et stabilitatis immmotae causas. Neque uspiam legitur, quod in concretum, solidum, compactum et impervium systema fluidum illud atque aërium corpus rursus coiverit et coaluerit κατὰ σύγκρισιν et πῆξιν. Imo testimonia Scripturae expressa per omnia et in omnibus adversantur, quae praetereo, ut taceam cataractas, aquarum ascensus et descensus, accessus ad thronum aeternae maiestatis et recessus commemoratos in Biblijs, quos neutiquam pia Antiquitas ὁράματα aut φανερώσεις tantum φανταστικὰς et imaginarias, ut multi nunc somniant, sed actus veros et historicas de ijsdem narrationes esse credidit constanter. Quod ex CASTALIONIS versione citas (quam mihi non lectam, a plerisque tamen parum probari intelligo) id textus Hebraeus etiam habet, et ex Psalmo 104 videtur ab ESAIA desumptum. Verba Psalmi sunt: Extendens Coelos sicut pellem; quae cum membrana sensu idem est. Ego vero et ESAIAe illud 51 de Coeli Terraeque corruptione, qua fiet renovatio utriusque, ad naturam utriusque recte applicari posse existimo, cum dicit Propheta: Coeli sicut fumus liquescent, Terra sicut vestimentum atteretur. Coeli liquescent sicut fumus aut evanescent quia sunt velut fumus expertes soliditatis concretae. Terra crassa et solida atteretur. Maneo itaque in eadem tecum sententia, aetheream regionem totam impleri non contextu et compage διαφανῶν seu pellucidorum orbium, qui concretae materiae perspicuitate et soliditate crystallis similes sunt, etsi et his comparantur, non tamen soliditatis sed perspicuitatis ratione, sed sua quadam subtilissima et purissima substantia, quae aëre, a quo propagata est, tenuior multo sinceriorque et ab infusa luce perfectior est, quam complexu continent atque coercent suo circumfusae densae nubes et aquae circumfluae, a quibus Hebraei Coelum nominant Schamaim, id est: ibi aquae. De his aquis clara sunt et indubitata testimonia Scripturae sacrae, Genes. 1: Fecit Deus firmamentum et divisit aquas sub firmamento ab his, quae erant supra firmamentum, vocavitque firmamentum Coelum. Genes. 7, in historia diluvij: Abyssi Terrae rupti sunt, et cataractae Coeli apertae sunt, et facta est pluvia Super Terram. ESAIAe 24: Quia cataractae Coeli apertae sunt, et concutientur fundamenta Terrae. Psalm. 18: Posuit tenebras latibulum suum, in circuitu tabernaculi sui tenebrosam aquam et densas nubes. Psalm. 104: Qui contignat in aquis tabernacula sua. Quod aetherea regio circumfluis aquis summo extimoque ambitu inundet, quod nubibus densis operta sit atque obscurata, quod interfusae nubes, etiam ut aquae sedem supremam maiestatis divinae a creatis subtusque conclusis intraque coeli ambitum, circumflexu huius comprehensis rebus universis dirimant, cur dubitem, intuens auctoritatem et fidem immotam divinae veritatis patefactae? Sed quae sint illa repagula aut firmamenta, quae cataractae, quae clausae coelestes aquas pendulas in sublimi sua natura graves et nutu impetuque suo nitentes deorsum detinent, quae laxatae itidem aut adapertae easdem profundunt, ut in diluvio, nec Scriptura prodidit, nec Philosophorum quisquam arguti ingenij coniecturis pervestigabit, nec magis hoc perscrutabitur quisquam, quam explicatum est a quoquam, quomodo suis sese fultibus in medio mundi libret immotum terreni globi pondus. Nihil moveor Philosophorum quaestionibus percontantibus, utrum Terra, utrum Coelum sit in loco per se vel per accidens, quia extra Coelum non sit locus. Sed assentior Scripturae, quae induit et cooperit systema mundi aquarum mole fortasse, si non omne undiquaque, nubium tamen involucris omne, quae et concentum nobis docet esse inexploratum, sed certum tamen et admirandum, accessu recessuque inter superna et infima, inter aquas etiam coelestes et imas, quae denique aquarum et nubium obscura tenebrositate docet secludi aeternam sedem Maiestatis divinae aeterna luce plenam, in qua creaturis inaccessa habitat divinitas, a luce et sede creata inferiorum. Quid enim his est in Biblijs manifestius? Ratiocinor inde et multa de causis eorum, quae in Coelo creato nobis occurrunt, ut de Coeli colore sapphirino, qui interdiu noctuque Coelo sereno conspicitur, dissimiliter tamen, clarius et liquidius die, obscurius nocte, de dissimilibus Coeli partibus, de via lactea, quibus nemini leges et dogmata fero, sed coniecturas recito ex Dei verbo, relinquens expendendum cuique, his utrum habere fidem an Philosophorum rationibus velit. Colorem Coeli sapphirinum conijcio non esse aliunde quam ex aquis et nubibus supernis profusis et tinctis illa coelesti sempiterna luce, quae nobis tanto affulget purior et nitidior, quo Coelum nostrum splendet clarius. Inde etiam esse iudico, quicquid tenebrosum in octavo orbe, noctu praesertim, Coelo limpidiore cernitur. Nec video, quas alias Philosophi causas proferre possint, neutiquam enim sufficiunt, quae ab aetherea luce creata intra Coelum petuntur, ut maxime constituantur in Coelo orbes solidi, concreti, pellucidi, quos immissa et fracta lux luminarium et stellarum suo splendore imbutos caeruleo colore tingat, sicut sparsi per aequor radij Solis marinas aquas. Unde erit enim illa umbrosa tenebrositas, quae supra octavum orbem sistit ac finit visum, eo usque pervio et nusquam impedito excursu, Coelo puro, penetrantem? Viam lacteam autem conijcio esse rariores partes seu nubium, seu aquarum coelestium, seu repagulorum, vel horum omnium, per quas supernum, increatum et aeternum lumen penetrat aut pellucet radiatu evidentius, quam in densioribus et obscurioribus. Nec assentior Philosophorum somnijs, qui illam fingunt esse confusum confertumque lumen multarum et parvarum stellarum, quas propter exilitatem prospectus oculorum consequi ac discernere nequeat; malo enim cum Scriptura et ex hac ipsa philosophari, quam cum Philosophis rerum divinarum imperitis hanc contra nugari (in ijs praesertim, in quibus aut haerent plane, aut penitus caecutiunt) aut comminisci et assumere pro certis ac veris hypotheses veritati coelesti adversas, quod exemplo veterum COPERNICUS fecit in mobilitate Terrae. Propter has cogitationes ex Dei verbo in literis ad magnif. Dominum HENRICUM RANZOVIUM movi dubium illud in hypothesibus tuis de caeli vastitate, non quod hoc alicuius momenti esse censerem, aut quod hoc non considerarem, de quo me admones, sed ut ostenderem, me de octavo orbe et contigua sede cum hoc Planetarum aliquid a Philosophis diversum reperire; non imaginor enim octavum orbem unum quendam cavum orbem esse, cuius superficiei inerrantes stellae infixae sint, sed has mirando consilio et inenarrabili sapientia divina per supremum nubibusque et aquis coelestibus proximum, latissime patens tamen et profunde se demittens spatium eo ordine sedibus certis fixisque distributas esse, quo consistere et circumferri uno impetu, inter se immotae intervallis, cernuntur, non eodem positu tamen supremi coeli respectu, nec eadem a terris distantia; quod totum spatium pro octavo orbe est. Scio, quod obijcient Philosophi: Si orbes non sunt, si stellae partes orbium non sunt, unde pendebunt in sua regione, et qua vi, quo motu circumvehentur? Respondeo: Si ARISTOTELES, qui orbibus eas affigit et circumducit, νόους illis tamen seu intelligentias affingit, cur nos recte de Dei operibus edocti non multo magis tribueremus Deo opifici laudem in structura operis impervestigabilis sapientiae et potentiae impervestigabilis? Cur non statueremus motus stellarum admiranda et imperscrutabili sapientia divina regi, qua conditi sunt? Cur non fateremur ὰνεξερεύνητα esse opera Dei? Concludens itaque hanc epistolae partem acquiesco tecum in circulis.</p><p>In observationibus autem hoc requiro, cum te Martem ἀκρόνυχον καὶ περίγειον in Solis diametro intelligo multo humiliorem Sole reperisse, utrum etiam duos inferiores, Venerem et Mercurium, ἀπογειοτάτους, ut pro certo compertoque statuis, Sole deprehenderis altiores? quod et de Cometa viso anno 77 affirmas. Quae enim de 72 anni Cometa, quem tunc meo more coelum assidue sub noctem et auroram contemplans nostris primum ostendi, de illo ergo quae edidisti, visa mihi nondum sunt. Inquisivi de illis secundae partis titulo admonitus et ab amicis, sed consequi non potui. Scis fundamentum doctrinae Astronomicae esse certitudinem observationum, eoque magis miror, quod ex mei praeceptoris ERASMI REINHOLDI Tabulis ante mihi non animadversum de canonibus mediorum motuum ac προσθαφαιρέσεων profers. Quid sunt enim προσθαφαιρέσεις nisi discrimen inter vere apparentia inaequalia et media aequalia? Id vero discrimen quomodo exacte constituetur, si medij motus exacte definiti et cum observatis veris comparati non sint? Memini magnos etiam Astronomos observationes in COPERNICO desiderasse, qui suspicabantur, nec satis diligentem illum in hoc genere, quod praecipuum est, fuisse, nec tantum observationum habuisse, quantum operi tanto conficiundo fuit opus. In te vero nihil horum quisquam merito accusabit, qui tot modis totiesque variatis et emendatis, ubi necesse fuit, organis, idem toties summa cura expertus es. Imprimis vero laudo exquisitum accuratumque studium tuum in discrepantia notanda, quam parallaxes et διακλάσεις seu fractiones per vapores densiores pariunt, qua in re nescio, an ex veteribus etiam aliqui satis diligentes fuerint, confisi fortassis nimium serenitate et puritate coeli austrini Soli propioris. Inerrantium examen et emendatio per τηρήσεις, paucis exceptis, quae Eclipticae vicinae sunt, nemini admodum curae fuit, inde usque ab illis descriptionibus, quae HIPPARCHO tribuuntur. In his insigne inveniri a veterum notatione discrimen, consentientibus observatis vestris, illustrissimi Landgravij et tuis, hoc magis gaudeo, quo plus referre existimo, ut illarum sedes, saltem praecipuarum, sint exquisite certoque designatae.</p><p>Distributionem operis, quod instituis, valde probo, nec minus ingeniosa inventa, quae ad compendiosam facilemque ἐπιλογισμὸν motuum apparentium vel ex solo triangulorum canone promittis. Utinam vivam tamdiu, ut cognoscam, quomodo consensus experientijs comprobet ac stabiliat illam quam hypothesibus tuis condis novam fabricam in maximis et mirandis Dei operibus, quibus quantum de summa illa Dei sapientia consecuti simus, intelligemus tunc, quando αὐτόπται coram opera ipsa aspiciemus.</p><p>FRANCISCI VALESIJ Hispani nec nomen novi antea, nec legi librum, sed quae ex illo commemoras, mihi talia videntur, ut refutatione nec arguta nec operosa indigeant. Absurdius est, quod coelum ex elementis quatuor conflat, cui propter ἀμετακίνητον ἀφθαρσίαν Philosophi omnes essentiam quintam, aliam scilicet ab elementis, tribuerunt. Nec repererunt, quomodo τὸ διαφανές ἐνεργείᾳ seu natura sua et actu lucidum ex elementis detexerent aut demonstrarent.</p><p>Anno praeterito fui Cassellis apud illustrissimum Principem WILHELMUM Landgravium, Dominum et benefactorem meum clementissimum, ubi magna cum voluptate spectavi et consideravi instrumenta, quibus ad observationes ipse utitur, Sextantem imprimis ex tua, quantum intelligo, officina profectum et ibidem natum. Experiri vim et usum propter temporis brevitatem et copiam ac varietatem rerum, quae mihi ostendebantur, ut optâram, non potui. Nec scio, an possim in hoc genere quid praestare amplius, oculorum acumine hebescente. Literas vero ultro citroque scriptas inter vos, quas mecum humanissime communicasti, inspexi accurate, nondum perlegi, quod faciam, quamprimum me levâro aliqua parte illarum operarum, quibus nunc occupor et exerceor. Quae mihi ex Genethliaco meo themate ante tot annos praesagijsti nimis verus vates, sic evenerunt, ut praesagijsti; horum ego nonnulla, exilium praesertim, mei etiam fati sensu et monitu aliorum prospexi ac metui, sed tu omnia, causas etiam et occasiones, melius et integrius, eoque magis cum excellentiam ingenij tui in omnium artium ac praesertim disciplinarum Mathematicarum genere, tum vero imprimis in Prognostico δι’ ἀστρολογίας etiam ἀγχίνοιαν στοχαστικήν admiror. Non illa magis contemnenda iudico erudita et rationibus atque experientia nixa praedicta (cum sint in conditis a Deo operibus creata simul et ordinata ad naturae totius et singularum partium utilitatem et physicam gubernationem vestigia sapientiae, documenta providentiae et testimonia praesentiae divinae) quam coniecturas et praesagia medicorum. Plus scientiae de illis, plus studij et diligentiae in plerisque, qui tractant, et usum magis sobrium et sanum requiro in plurimis. Illa inficiari, quae λόγον habent καὶ πείραν, est derogando ordini et virtuti operum divinorum contumelia Deum afficere. Neque existimemus, casu aut temere alios alio syderum positu in lucem edi, etsi frequentius homines significatis syderum deteriores vita et moribus, infeliciores eventu ac successibus, raro meliores evadent ac deportenduntur aut feliciores. Tu vero praeclare fecisti, quod non repudiatis quidem, sed intermissis Prognosticis, ad divinas illas divinaque sapientia plenas artes totis te viribus convertisti, ingenijque acumine et alis Geometricis atque Arithmeticis ad ipsorum syderum praesentem quasi contuitum motuumque explorationem evolasti ac penetrasti, quae te nobilitabunt ad omnem posteritatem. Mirifice tamen et mihi et doctis omnibus, quibus exhibeo, Prognosticum tuum placet, deque eo communicato mecum, ut de alijs, gratiam me tibi eximiam debere agnosco. De editione epistolarum, et tuarum ad amicos et amicorum ad te, cur non vehementer probarem consilium Mag. HENRICI RANZOVII, causam non reperio. Quamquam enim ipsum sese et te auctorem opus satis cuique commendabit, tamen plus auctoritatis, fidei studijque utrique literae illae publicatae adiungent, si innotuerit palam, quantum industriae, operae, vigiliarum et sumptuum impenderis, et quantopere tibi curae fuerit aliorum etiam consulere et exquirere iudicia, quantumque tuum antecellat. Addo hoc etiam, me paucis diebus ante has literas scriptas, procurante Domino RANZOVIO, accepisse aeneam ἰχνογραφίαν et fabricam hypothesium tuarum, in cuius consideratione pleraque tua intellexi plenius, uno haerente de circellis, quod si in luminaribus, ut Planetis quinque, observas, nihil desidero. Bene et feliciter vale, meque te reverenter amantem et colentem propter singulares et eximias dotes divinas, quibus divinitus excitatum te video et praedico esse ad hanc Mathematicae doctrinae partem illustrandam et absolvendam, quam ego maximi feci semper, cumque admiratione et voluptate colui, sed avocatus alijs studijs nimis cito de manibus deposui, redama.</p><p>Datae Dessae 1O. die Maij Iuliani Anno 1589.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Tycho Brahe, the Dane, most renowned mathematician, most noble gentleman. Sends greeting.</b></p><p>With great joy I received, and with greater pleasure I read, your letter, truly noble sir, and joined with it the writings exchanged in reply [τὰ ἀπαμειβόμενα], in which the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, and you compared your joint observations [συμπαρατηρήσεις] in an examination worthy of the learned and of supreme craftsmen — but [they reached me] later than they ought. For, sent from you out of Uraniborg (which I am accustomed to call <i>Uranoscopia</i>, as a watch-tower of the sky) on 13 September in the year [15]88, they reached me at last only at the end of January, Julian [reckoning], in the following year [15]89; where they lay hidden or were held up, and through whose fault, I do not know. But I too answer rather tardily, lacking opportunities and a supply of those who carry [letters] safely and in good time. Having now obtained these [opportunities] through the coming to us of the Most Illustrious Prince John, Duke of Holstein, I wished to satisfy both you and the magnificent Master Henrik Rantzau, so far as I could amid this strength of mind and memory enfeebled by old age and imprisonment (I who have so long been away from books and astronomical studies), and amid the ailing health of an afflicted body, and amid these occupations by which I am even now overwhelmed. But first I give you the thanks I owe and can render, because you so accurately set out for me the occasion, the history, and the rationale of your observations and hypotheses — in which I truly affirm that I find nothing that I could wish [otherwise]. I am held up here and there, as namely in the case of the luminaries, since you carry them about on circles homocentric with the Earth (which you use, [being] better, more rightly and more truly than orbs): [I am uncertain] whether you wish to expedite their apparent anomaly by the same reasoning as in the other five planets (for which you set the Sun as centre), by two small circles, after the Copernican example — which you approve of in the case of the Moon, in which he used two epicycles, from which you shrink — or rather by some other [way] that does not occur to me. The rest I judge to be so plain, explicated, and clear, and so well suited to the ease of astronomical doctrine and of calculation [ψηφοφορίας], that I must confess that the hypotheses of the ancients, if they be compared with these, seem labyrinths, with that excessive variety and multitude of dissimilar orbs by which, nonetheless, whether they attain what they promise, experience teaches. That the whole tract of the ethereal region, save for the bodies of the stars, consists of a most rarefied ethereal substance, most pure and pervious to all rays, and that this is liquid and fluid — the testimonies of the divine wisdom revealed by the heavenly Word demonstrate beyond doubt, against the Philosophers. This opinion is persuaded and confirmed to me not only by those sayings adduced earlier from Scripture, but by very many others. For to Moses, in Genesis 1, the heaven is a certain expanse; for that is what the Hebrew word <i>Rakia</i> signifies, that is, a certain thing rarefied and made thin [ἀραιωθὲν καὶ λεπτωθὲν] by a true separation [κατὰ διάκρισιν], poured forth and dilated by an immense expansion into a body transparent in potency [δυνάμει διαφανές], which, by light kindled and diffused, was made by creation transparent in act [διαφανὲς ἐνεργείᾳ]. This diffused light the omnipotence of the supreme Maker afterwards, on the fourth day, gathered together again, compacted, and arranged by composition [κατὰ σύγκρισιν] into the distinct bodies of the luminaries and the stars. The Seventy translators rendered it στερέωμα [the firm thing], whence the name \"firmament\" flowed. By each appellation I judge there to be indicated both those barriers, wondrous and unexplored by us, by which the mass of waters poured around the created orb is sustained and confined, and also that unchangeableness [ἀμετάβλητον] of the ethereal region — obvious to us and acknowledged in all ages by the Philosophers too — on account of which Aristotle introduced among secondary causes that necessity from nature [τὴν ἀνάγκην ἀπὸ τῆς φύσεως], distinct and diverse. Nor did Job mean anything else by the \"brazen heavens\"; perhaps because the translators had regard to the opinion then widespread and commonly believed about the celestial orbs, [drawn] from the inventions of the Philosophers in Assyria and Egypt, and out of the custom of the common folk took up a word thence in use, as often happens; for it does not agree with the Hebrew. But such an expanse, drawing-out, and dilation of heaven Scripture perpetually repeats and inculcates: Isaiah 45: \"My hands have stretched out the heavens.\" Isaiah 44: \"I am the Lord, who makes all things, who stretches out the heavens alone, who establishes the Earth, and none [is] with me.\" Job 9: \"Who stretches out the heavens alone.\" Jeremiah 10 and Psalm 135: \"Who prepares the world in his wisdom, and by his prudence stretches out the heavens.\" But that that extension — which is the work of omnipotence alone — began with a beginning made from the waters surrounding the Earth, which are as it were the principle of transparent things [τῶν διαφανῶν], but still dense and opaque with the filth and shadows of earthly concretion — these being poured forth into a vapour, and that vapour being more and more attenuated, purged, and as it were polished and diffused down to that thinness and purity which was a fitting, stable, and perennial subject for receiving the purest light — [and that] that immense tract of heaven emerged [from this]: Scripture teaches, reason too and experience prove, although it [reason] in no way attains the manner of the attenuation and purgation, nor the causes of the unmoved stability. Nor is it anywhere read that that fluid and airy body again came together and coalesced into a concrete, solid, compacted, and impervious system, by composition [κατὰ σύγκρισιν] and by congealing [πῆξιν]. Nay, the express testimonies of Scripture are opposed throughout and in all things — which I pass over, to say nothing of the cataracts [floodgates], the ascents and descents of waters, the approaches to and withdrawals from the throne of eternal majesty, recorded in the Bible, which pious Antiquity by no means steadfastly believed to be visions [ὁράματα] or merely fantastical and imaginary manifestations [φανερώσεις … φανταστικάς], as many now dream, but [believed to be] true acts and historical narrations concerning the same. As for what you cite from Castellio's version (which I have not read, though I understand it is little approved by most), the Hebrew text too has it, and it seems to have been taken by Isaiah from Psalm 104. The words of the Psalm are: \"Stretching out the heavens like a skin\"; which is the same in sense as \"membrane.\" But I, for my part, judge that that passage in Isaiah 51 too, about the corruption of heaven and Earth — by which the renewal of both shall come about — can rightly be applied to the nature of each, when the Prophet says: \"The heavens shall melt away like smoke, the Earth shall be worn away like a garment.\" The heavens shall melt away like smoke or shall vanish, because they are, like smoke, devoid of concrete solidity. The Earth, gross and solid, shall be worn away. I therefore remain of the same opinion with you, that the whole ethereal region is filled not by a fabric and framework of transparent [διαφανῶν] or pellucid orbs — which are similar to crystals in the perspicuity and solidity of concrete matter, even though they too are compared with these, yet not by reason of solidity but of perspicuity — but by a certain most subtle and most pure substance of its own, which is much thinner and more sincere than air, from which it was propagated, and is more perfected by the light infused [into it]; which the dense clouds poured around it and the waters flowing around contain and confine in their embrace — from which [waters] the Hebrews name heaven <i>Schamaim</i>, that is: \"there [are] waters.\" Concerning these waters there are clear and undoubted testimonies of sacred Scripture, Genesis 1: \"God made the firmament, and divided the waters under the firmament from those that were above the firmament, and he called the firmament Heaven.\" Genesis 7, in the history of the Flood: \"The depths of the Earth were broken up, and the cataracts of Heaven were opened, and rain came upon the Earth.\" Isaiah 24: \"For the cataracts of Heaven are opened, and the foundations of the Earth shall be shaken.\" Psalm 18: \"He made darkness his hiding-place, round about his tabernacle dark water and dense clouds.\" Psalm 104: \"Who frames his dwellings in the waters.\" That the ethereal region floods over with surrounding waters at its highest and outermost circuit, that it is covered and darkened by dense clouds, that the interspersed clouds — as also the waters — separate off the supreme seat of the divine majesty from things created and shut up below and within the compass of heaven, all things being comprehended in the encircling of this [heaven]: why should I doubt, gazing on the authority and unshaken faith of the revealed divine truth? But what those barriers or firmaments are, what cataracts, what closed [structures] hold down the heavenly waters hanging on high — heavy in their own nature and striving downward by their own weight and impetus — what, likewise, when loosened or opened, pour them forth, as in the Flood: neither has Scripture disclosed, nor will any of the Philosophers, however acute in wit, track it out by conjectures; nor will anyone scrutinize this any more than it has been explained by anyone how the unmoved weight of the earthly globe balances itself by its own supports in the middle of the world. I am not at all moved by the Philosophers' questions inquiring whether the Earth, whether Heaven, is in a place <i>per se</i> or <i>per accidens</i>, since outside Heaven there is no place. But I assent to Scripture, which clothes and covers the system of the world perhaps with a mass of waters — if not entirely on every side, yet entirely with wrappings of clouds — [Scripture] which also teaches us that the harmony [concentum] is unexplored, yet certain and to be wondered at, by an approach and withdrawal between things above and below, between the heavenly waters too and the lowest, and which, finally, teaches that by the dark gloom of waters and clouds the eternal seat of the divine Majesty — full of eternal light, in which divinity dwells inaccessible to creatures — is shut off from the light and seat created for the lower things. For what in the Bible is more manifest than these things? From this I reason out many things too about the causes of those things that meet us in the created heaven: as about the sapphire colour of the sky, which is seen by day and by night when the sky is clear, yet differently — brighter and more limpid by day, darker by night — about the dissimilar parts of the sky, about the Milky Way; in which matters I impose laws and dogmas on no one, but recite conjectures from the Word of God, leaving it to each to weigh whether he will give credence to these or to the Philosophers' reasonings. I conjecture that the sapphire colour of the sky comes from nowhere else than from the upper waters and clouds, poured out and tinged with that heavenly everlasting light, which shines upon us the purer and more brilliant the more brightly our heaven gleams. From this too I judge to arise whatever dark [thing] is discerned in the eighth orb, especially by night, when the sky is clearer. Nor do I see what other causes the Philosophers can bring forward — for by no means do those suffice that are sought from the ethereal light created within Heaven — supposing that there be set up in Heaven solid, concrete, pellucid orbs, which the light of the luminaries and stars, let in and refracted, imbued with its own splendour, tinges with a blue colour, just as the Sun's rays scattered over the deep tinge the waters of the sea. For whence will that shadowy gloom come, which above the eighth orb halts and bounds the sight as it penetrates — the passage being pervious right up to that point and nowhere hindered, the heaven being pure? But the Milky Way I conjecture to be the rarer parts, whether of clouds, or of the heavenly waters, or of the barriers, or of all of these, through which the supernal, uncreated, and eternal light penetrates or shines through by radiation more evidently than in the denser and darker ones. Nor do I assent to the dreams of the Philosophers, who feign it [the Milky Way] to be the confused and crowded light of many small stars, which on account of their faintness the gaze of the eyes cannot make out and distinguish; for I prefer to philosophize with Scripture and from this very [Scripture], rather than with the Philosophers — unskilled in divine matters — to trifle against it (especially in those things in which they are either plainly stuck or utterly blind), or to fabricate and assume as certain and true hypotheses opposed to the heavenly truth, as Copernicus did, after the example of the ancients, in the mobility of the Earth. On account of these reflections from the Word of God, in my letter to the magnificent Master Henrik Rantzau I raised that doubt in your hypotheses concerning the vastness of the heaven — not because I reckoned this to be of any moment, or because I did not consider this of which you admonish me, but in order to show that I find, concerning the eighth orb and the seat contiguous to it together with that of the Planets, something different from the Philosophers; for I do not imagine the eighth orb to be some single hollow orb to whose surface the fixed stars are affixed, but [I imagine] that these, by a wondrous design and by ineffable divine wisdom, are distributed through the supreme space — nearest to the clouds and the heavenly waters, yet very widely extending and sinking deep down — in fixed and certain seats, in that order in which they are seen to stand and be carried around by a single impulse, unmoved among themselves at their intervals, yet not in the same position with respect to the supreme heaven, nor at the same distance from the Earth; which whole space stands for the eighth orb. I know what the Philosophers will object: If there are no orbs, if the stars are not parts of orbs, whence will they hang in their region, and by what force, by what motion will they be carried around? I answer: If Aristotle, who affixes them to orbs and leads them around, nevertheless attributes to them minds [νόους] or intelligences, why should not we — rightly instructed about the works of God — much more attribute praise to God the Maker in the structure of a work of unsearchable wisdom and unsearchable power? Why should we not lay it down that the motions of the stars are governed by the wondrous and inscrutable divine wisdom by which they were founded? Why should we not confess that the works of God are unsearchable [ὰνεξερεύνητα]? Concluding therefore this part of the letter, I rest in agreement with you concerning the circles.</p><p>But in the matter of observations I ask this: since I understand that you found Mars, when acronychal and at perigee [ἀκρόνυχον καὶ περίγειον], on the Sun's diameter, much lower than the Sun, [I ask] whether you have also found the two lower [planets], Venus and Mercury, at their highest [ἀπογειοτάτους], as you lay down for certain and ascertained, to be higher than the Sun? — which you affirm also of the comet seen in the year [15]77. For as to what you have published about the comet of the year [15]72, which I then — assiduously contemplating the sky toward nightfall and dawn, after my custom — first pointed out to our people: those things [you published] about it have not yet come into my view. I made inquiry about them, prompted by the heading of the second part and by friends, but I could not obtain them. You know that the foundation of astronomical doctrine is the certainty of observations, and I wonder the more that you bring forward, from the Tables of my teacher Erasmus Reinhold, something not previously noticed by me concerning the canons of the mean motions and of the prosthaphaereses [προσθαφαιρέσεων]. For what are prosthaphaereses but the difference between things truly apparent [and] unequal and the mean equal [motions]? But how will that difference be exactly constituted, if the mean motions are not exactly defined and compared with the true observed [motions]? I remember that great Astronomers too found observations wanting in Copernicus, and suspected that he was neither diligent enough in this kind — which is the chief thing — nor had as many observations as was needful for completing so great a work. But in you no one will justly accuse [you] of any of these things — you who, with instruments in so many ways and so many times varied and corrected where it was necessary, have tested the same thing so many times with the utmost care. And especially I praise your exquisite and accurate diligence in noting the discrepancy that parallaxes and refractions [διακλάσεις] — that is, the bendings through denser vapours — produce; in which matter I do not know whether even any of the ancients were sufficiently diligent, trusting perhaps too much to the serenity and purity of the southern sky, nearer to the Sun. The examination and emendation of the fixed [stars] by observations [τηρήσεις], with a few exceptions which are near the Ecliptic, has been a care to scarcely anyone, ever since those descriptions which are attributed to Hipparchus. That a notable discrepancy from the ancients' record is found in these, the observations of you both — of the Most Illustrious Landgrave and your own — agreeing, I rejoice at all the more, the more I reckon it to matter that their seats, at least of the principal [stars], be exquisitely and certainly determined.</p><p>The arrangement of the work that you are undertaking I greatly approve, and no less the ingenious devices which you promise for a compendious and easy reckoning [ἐπιλογισμὸν] of the apparent motions, even from the canon of triangles alone. Would that I might live long enough to learn how the agreement [of theory] with experience proves and stabilizes that new fabric which you build by your hypotheses among the greatest and most wondrous works of God — by which [works] how much of that supreme wisdom of God we have attained, we shall understand then, when, as eyewitnesses [αὐτόπται], we shall behold the works themselves face to face.</p><p>As for Francisco Valles the Spaniard, I neither knew his name before nor have I read his book, but the things you recount from it seem to me such as need no refutation, neither subtle nor laborious. More absurd is that he forges heaven out of the four elements — heaven, to which on account of its unmoved incorruptibility [ἀμετακίνητον ἀφθαρσίαν] all the Philosophers assigned a fifth essence, distinct, that is, from the elements. Nor did they discover how to disclose or demonstrate the transparent-in-act [τὸ διαφανὲς ἐνεργείᾳ], or [that which is] lucid by its own nature and in act, out of the elements.</p><p>Last year I was at Kassel, with the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave, my Lord and most clement benefactor, where with great pleasure I beheld and considered the instruments which he himself uses for observations — above all the Sextant, which, as far as I understand, came out of your workshop and was born there. To try out its power and use I could not, on account of the shortness of time and the abundance and variety of things that were shown to me, as I had wished. Nor do I know whether I can accomplish anything further in this kind, the keenness of my eyes growing dull. But the letters written to and fro between you, which you most courteously shared with me, I inspected carefully, though I have not yet read them through, which I shall do as soon as I have relieved myself of some part of those labours by which I am now occupied and engaged. The things you presaged to me from my natal figure so many years ago — too true a prophet — turned out just as you presaged; some of these, exile especially, I too foresaw and feared, by a sense of my own fate and the warning of others, but you [foresaw] all of them, the causes too and the occasions, better and more completely; and on that account I admire all the more your excellence of intellect in the whole kind of all the arts, and especially of the Mathematical disciplines, and then, above all, your sagacity in conjecture [ἀγχίνοιαν στοχαστικήν] in Prognostication, through astrology [δι’ ἀστρολογίας] too. I judge those learned predictions, resting upon reasons and experience, to be no more to be despised — since they are, in the works founded by God, created together [with them] and ordered toward the usefulness and physical governance of the whole of nature and of its single parts: traces of wisdom, documents of providence, and testimonies of the divine presence — than the conjectures and presages of physicians. I require more knowledge about those [predictions], more study and diligence in most of those who treat [them], and a more sober and sound use in very many. To deny those things which have a rational account and a [trial by] experience [λόγον … καὶ πείραν] is, by derogating from the order and virtue of the divine works, to afflict God with contumely. Nor let us think that by chance or at random some are brought into the light under one position of the stars, others under another — even though more frequently men signified by the stars turn out worse in life and morals, more unfortunate in outcome and successes, and rarely turn out better or are portended [to be] happier. But you have acted nobly in this, that — your Prognostications not indeed repudiated, but laid aside — you turned yourself with all your strength to those divine arts, full of divine wisdom, and by the acuteness of your wit and on Geometrical and Arithmetical wings flew up and penetrated to a sort of present contemplation of the stars themselves and to the exploration of their motions — [things] which will ennoble you to all posterity. Yet your Prognostication pleases marvellously both me and all the learned to whom I show it; and I acknowledge that I owe you exceeding thanks for it being shared with me, as for the other things. As for the publication of letters — both yours to friends and friends' to you — I find no reason why I should not vehemently approve the counsel of Master Henrik Rantzau. For although the work will sufficiently commend itself to everyone, both by him and by you as author, yet those letters, once published, will add more authority, credit, and zeal to both [parties], if it becomes openly known how much industry, labour, vigils, and expense you have spent, and how greatly it has been your care to consult and seek out the judgements of others too, and how far your own surpasses [theirs]. I add this also, that a few days before this letter was written I received, through the agency of Master Rantzau, a copper-engraved ground-plan [ἰχνογραφίαν] and structure of your hypotheses, in the consideration of which I understood most of your matters more fully — with one [point] sticking, concerning the small circles: which [point], if in the case of the luminaries you observe [the same] as in the five planets, I want for nothing. Fare you well and happily, and love in return me who reverently love and honour you on account of those singular and exceptional divine gifts by which I see, and proclaim, that you have been divinely roused to illustrate and bring to completion this part of Mathematical doctrine — which I have always esteemed most highly, and have cultivated with admiration and pleasure, but, called away to other studies, all too soon laid down from my hands.</p><p>Given at Dessau, on the 10th day of May, Julian [reckoning], in the year 1589.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Caspar Peucer</i> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician, mathematician, historian, and astronomer; pupil and son-in-law of Philipp Melanchthon, and editor of Reinhold's astronomical work. A leading Philippist/Crypto-Calvinist, he was imprisoned 1574–1586 for his confessional position, then entered the service of the princes of Anhalt at Dessau as personal physician. The letter's repeated references — <i>senecta et carceribus debilitata</i> (\"enfeebled by old age and imprisonment\"), the long absence \"from books and astronomical studies,\" and <i>exilium</i> (\"exile\") among the things Tycho's horoscope foretold — are autobiographical to this imprisonment and disgrace.</li><li><b>Recipient / salutation.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Erasmus Reinhold.</b> <i>mei praeceptoris ERASMI REINHOLDI Tabulis</i> — the <i>Prutenic Tables</i> (1551) of Erasmus Reinhold (1511–1553), Peucer's teacher at Wittenberg; the appeal to him grounds Peucer's standing to discuss the canons of mean motions and prosthaphaereses.</li><li><b>Other persons.</b> <i>Princeps Landgravius Hassiae WILHELMUS</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6), Tycho's fellow-observer; their joint star observations (<i>συμπαρατηρήσεις</i>) are at issue. <i>HENRICUS / Mag. HENRICI RANZOVII</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), Danish royal governor (Statthalter) of Holstein, humanist patron and intermediary, who proposed the publication of Tycho's correspondence (realized as the 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i>) and forwarded the engraving of Tycho's world-system. <i>Dux Holsatiae IOHANNES</i> = <b>John (Hans) the Younger, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg</b> (1545–1622). <i>FRANCISCI VALESIJ Hispani</i> = <b>Francisco Valles</b> (Vallesius, 1524–1592), Spanish royal physician, author of <i>De iis quae scripta sunt physice in libris sacris, sive de sacra philosophia</i> (1587), whom Tycho had evidently cited. <i>HIOBUS</i> = Job; <i>MOYSES</i> = Moses; <i>HIPPARCHUS</i> = Hipparchus; <i>COPERNICUS</i>; <i>ARISTOTELES</i>; <i>CASTALIO</i> = <b>Sébastien Castellio</b> (1515–1563), whose much-criticized Latin Bible version Tycho had cited.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Dessa(e)</i> = <b>Dessau</b> (Anhalt), Peucer's place of residence/service. <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven; <i>Cassellae</i> = <b>Kassel</b> (protocol §5.7). Peucer's gloss <i>Uranoscopia … speculam coeli</i> (\"a watch-tower of the sky\") is his own playful etymologizing of Uraniborg's name.</li><li><b>DATE CRUX (header vs. catalogue).</b> The dataset `label` reads <b>\"1588. 22. maj. Dessau,\"</b> but the letter's own dateline reads <b>\"Datae Dessae 10. die Maij Iuliani Anno 1589\"</b> (\"Given at Dessau, the 10th day of May, Julian, 1589\"), and the slug encodes `15890510`. The header therefore follows the dateline as written — <b>10 May 1589 [O.S.]</b> — and appends the catalogued date. The body confirms 1589: the materials Tycho sent <i>13. Septembris anno 88</i> reached Peucer \"at last only at the end of January … in the following year [15]89\" (<i>anno sequente 89</i>), so the reply cannot predate late January 1589. The \"1588 / 22 May\" catalogue label is thus a dataset error (likely a mis-keyed year and day); it is recorded, not silently reconciled, per protocol §3. The source prints the day as \"1O.\" (capital-O for the zero); read as <b>10</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Maij Iuliani</i> / <i>Ianuarij Iuliani</i> — Peucer explicitly marks <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> reckoning, the Lutheran-German practice; no conversion is made.</li><li><b>Greek (transcribed and translated in order of appearance):</b></li><li><i>τὰ ἀπαμειβόμενα</i> — \"the things exchanged in reply\" (the answering writings).</li><li><i>συμπαρατηρήσεις</i> — \"joint observations\" (co-observations of Tycho and the Landgrave).</li><li><i>καὶ ψηφοφορίας</i> — \"and of calculation / reckoning\" (lit. \"counting with pebbles\").</li><li><i>ἀραιωθὲν καὶ λεπτωθὲν κατὰ διάκρισιν</i> — \"rarefied and made thin, by separation.\"</li><li><i>δυνάμει διαφανές … διαφανὲς ἐνεργείᾳ</i> — \"transparent in potency … transparent in act\" (Aristotelian <i>potentia / actus</i>); a key term of the cosmological argument.</li><li><i>κατὰ σύγκρισιν … πῆξιν</i> — \"by composition (aggregation) … congealing.\"</li><li><i>στερέωμα</i> — \"the firm/solid thing\" (the Septuagint rendering of <i>Rakia</i>, whence Vulgate <i>firmamentum</i>).</li><li><i>ἀμετάβλητον</i> — \"the unchangeable [character]\" (of the ethereal region).</li><li><i>τὴν ἀνάγκην ἀπὸ τῆς φύσεως</i> — \"the necessity from nature.\"</li><li><i>τῶν διαφανῶν / διαφανῶν</i> — \"of the transparent [things/orbs].\"</li><li><i>ὁράματα … φανερώσεις … φανταστικάς</i> — \"visions … manifestations … fantastical.\"</li><li><i>νόους</i> — \"minds\" (the Aristotelian intelligences moving the orbs).</li><li><i>ὰνεξερεύνητα</i> — \"unsearchable\" (printed with grave accent on the alpha as in the source; echoes Romans 11:33).</li><li><i>ἀκρόνυχον καὶ περίγειον</i> — \"acronychal and at perigee\" (Mars rising at sunset / nearest the Earth — i.e., at opposition).</li><li><i>ἀπογειοτάτους</i> — \"at their farthest from the Earth (highest apogee).\"</li><li><i>προσθαφαιρέσεων / προσθαφαιρέσεις</i> — <b>prosthaphaereses</b> (kept per protocol §5.1: the additive-subtractive equation between mean and apparent motion; Peucer glosses it himself as \"the difference between things truly apparent and unequal and the mean equal [motions]\").</li><li><i>διακλάσεις</i> — \"refractions / bendings\" (which Peucer glosses <i>seu fractiones per vapores densiores</i>); rendered \"refractions\" per the glossary.</li><li><i>τηρήσεις</i> — \"observations\" (of the fixed stars).</li><li><i>ἐπιλογισμόν</i> — \"reckoning / computation\" (of the apparent motions).</li><li><i>αὐτόπται</i> — \"eyewitnesses.\"</li><li><i>δι’ ἀστρολογίας … ἀγχίνοιαν στοχαστικήν</i> — \"through astrology … sagacity in conjecture.\"</li><li><i>λόγον … καὶ πείραν</i> — \"a rational account … and [trial by] experience.\"</li><li><i>ἀμετακίνητον ἀφθαρσίαν</i> — \"unmoved incorruptibility.\"</li><li><i>τὸ διαφανὲς ἐνεργείᾳ</i> — \"the transparent-in-act.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy / technical terms (per glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>circulis Terrae homocentricis</i> → \"circles homocentric with the Earth\"; <i>anomalia apparens</i> → \"apparent anomaly\"; <i>circellos duos parvos / duobus epicyclis</i> → \"two small circles / two epicycles\"; <i>octavus orbis</i> → \"the eighth orb\" (the sphere of the fixed stars); <i>parallaxes</i> → \"parallaxes\"; <i>Sextans</i> → \"Sextant\"; <i>organa</i> → \"instruments\"; <i>Cometa anno 77 / anni 72</i> → the comet of 1577 and the new star/comet matter of 1572 (Tycho's <i>De nova stella</i>, 1573, and the <i>De mundi aetherei … phaenomenis</i> on the 1577 comet). <i>aenea ἰχνογραφία et fabrica hypothesium tuarum</i> → \"a copper-engraved ground-plan and structure of your hypotheses\": the engraved diagram of the Tychonic world-system, forwarded via Rantzau.</li><li><b>The substance.</b> The bulk of the letter is Peucer's Scripture-grounded defence of a <b>fluid, non-solid heaven</b> (no crystalline spheres) — agreeing with Tycho's rejection of solid orbs (\"I rest in agreement with you concerning the circles\") — built from Genesis 1 (<i>Rakia</i> / <i>firmamentum</i>), the \"waters above the firmament,\" and the <i>Schamaim</i> etymology, against the Aristotelian quintessence and solid orbs and against Copernican terrestrial mobility. The technical replies follow: the open question about how Tycho handles the lunar/solar anomaly (two small circles, Copernican-style, vs. some other device); the query whether Venus and Mercury at apogee are found above the Sun (parallel to the 1577 comet); praise for Tycho's treatment of refraction and the fixed-star catalogue; and a closing endorsement of astrology rightly used and of Rantzau's plan to publish the correspondence.</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> Peucer's own qualifiers are kept un-sharpened: <i>fortasse</i> (\"perhaps\"), <i>conijcio / coniecturas recito</i> (\"I conjecture / I recite conjectures\"), <i>nescio</i> (\"I do not know\"), <i>quantum intelligo</i> (\"as far as I understand\"), <i>uno haerente</i> (\"with one point sticking\"), and the explicit disclaimer <i>nemini leges et dogmata fero</i> (\"I impose laws and dogmas on no one\").</li><li><b>Witnesses.</b> A = Paris, Bibliothèque Nationale, fonds français 13040 (a copy/<i>afskrift</i>); B = Mikulov (Nikolsburg), II 72, fol. C7 sqq. Shelfmarks as given in the source header.</li><li><b>Textual notes.</b> The source-block letters tagged in the working transcription with a trailing \"A\" marker (apparatus sigla, e.g. <i>literas tuasA</i>, <i>missaeA</i>, <i>UraniburgoA</i>) have been reproduced without that stray marker, which is an editorial/collation siglum from the underlying apparatus, not part of the text. <i>immmotae</i> (three m's) is reproduced as printed. <i>δυνάμει</i> / <i>ἐνεργείᾳ</i> accents follow the source.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected 'et facta et pluvia' to 'et facta est pluvia' (Genesis 7:12 Vulgate, within Peucer's scriptural excursus) per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.187; the English translation already carried the correct sense and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin and Greek are rendered securely and faithfully across this long, theologically dense letter; the cosmological argument and the technical astronomical queries are not smoothed. The named crux is the <b>dating discrepancy</b> (catalogue \"22 May 1588\" vs. the letter's own \"10 May 1589,\" resolved in favour of the dateline and corroborated by the internal chronology) — flagged, not silently reconciled. Minor residual softness: the precise referent of <i>concentum</i> (\"harmony\") in the clause on the approach-and-withdrawal of the waters, and whether <i>deportenduntur</i> (a rare/possibly corrupt form, rendered \"are portended\") is sound — both flagged here rather than over-determined.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.184-191 as page images (slice pages 1-8) and collated the full letter clause-by-clause: opening address, every scriptural citation and verse number (Isaiah 45/44/51/24, Job 9, Jeremiah 10, Psalm 135/18/104, Genesis 1/7), every proper name (Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Johann Duke of Holstein, Henrik Rantzau, Copernicus, Moses, Aristotle, Job, Castellio, Hipparchus, Erasmus Reinhold, Francisco Valles, Kassel), the observational data (Mars/Venus/Mercury altitude question, comets of 1572 and 1577), and the closing dateline (Dessau, 10 May Julian 1589). All matched Dreyer and the published English except the single Genesis 7 word-level corruption noted above, which is a TEXT_ERROR in our source_text rather than a translation defect (the English is unaffected). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found in an otherwise unusually careful and complete rendering of a very long, theologically dense letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text corrupts Dreyer's 'facta est pluvia' (Genesis 7:12 Vulgate, 'the rain was upon the earth') to 'facta et pluvia', a one-word est-to-et substitution; the English translator silently rendered the correct sense ('rain came upon the Earth') rather than compounding the error, but the underlying Latin as publishes it departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>et cataractæ Cœli apertæ funt, et facta eft pluvia fuper Terram (TBOO VII p.187)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.184-187 (page images). P.187 line 10 reads 'Abyßi Terræ rupti funt, et cataractæ Cœli apertæ funt, et facta eft pluvia fuper Terram', confirming the single flagged corruption (est misread as et). Only the defect site was checked against the edition of record, per the audit's single reported defect; the remainder of the letter is relied on as previously collated and sound.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Paris, Bibliotheque Nationale, franc. 13040. Afskrift", "B Mikulov, II 72, fol. C7 sqq.."], "th": {"label": "Caspar Peucer", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15880913002", "next": "brahe-t_15900000001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880913002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's letter and the Tycho–Landgrave Wilhelm comparative observations, delayed four months in transit, and replies via Duke John of Holstein as a courier.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890510002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890520001", "d": "1589-05-20", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 20 May 1589 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.46</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PRISCAe AC VERAe NOBILITATIS, INSIGNISQUE ERUDITIONIS VIRO, D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, MATHEMATUM ARTIFICI SUMMO, INSULAe HUENAe DOMINO ET AMICO SUO OBSERVANDO.<br>IN DENNEMARCK.<br>S.</p><p>PRIDIE eius diei, quam hinc discederet vestras ille ad Imp. nuncius, adij illum et de suo hinc discessu percontatus sum. Ille vero se eo ipso die habiturum audientiam apud Imp. aiebat. Quod vero sequenti mane discedere vellet, de eo ne verbum ullum. Dixeram me ipsi daturum literas ad te, quas paratas habebam; expectabam solum eo ipso die Bibliopolas nostrates e mercatu Francoffordensi, a quibus cognoscere cupiebam, num essent hoc tempore allaturi librum illum de astris, recordor fuisse cuiusdam IO. FRANCI, Parisijs impressum, et Reginae Galliae dedicatum, quem tibi olim Ratisponae donaveram, et tu eundem superioribus mensibus a me petebas, ut hac commoditate eum ad te transmitterem. Cum autem non afferrent, mitto nudas literas in hospitium ad vestratem illum. Ecce vero illum iam die praeterito hinc discessisse, mihi nunciant. Quam me id male habuerit, verbis exprimere non possum. Habes causam et iustam meam excusationem, cur vestras ille vacuus meis literis redierit ad vos, quam ut accipias beneque interpreteris, te oro.</p><p>B. V.<br>Pragae die 20. Maij novo stylo Anno 1589.<br>T. studiosiss.<br>Thad.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man of ancient and true nobility and of distinguished learning, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, supreme craftsman of the mathematical sciences, lord of the island of Hven, and his friend to be honoured.</b><br>IN DENMARK.<br>Greeting.</p><p>On the day before that on which your courier to the Emperor was to leave here, I went to him and inquired about his departure from here. He, however, said that on that very day he was to have an audience with the Emperor; but that he meant to leave the next morning — of this, not a single word. I had said that I would give him a letter for you, which I had ready by me; only, on that very day, I was waiting for our booksellers [to return] from the Frankfurt fair, from whom I wished to learn whether they were going to bring at this time that book on the stars — I recall it was by a certain Io. Francus, printed at Paris and dedicated to the Queen of France — which I had once given you at Regensburg, and which you had been asking of me these last months, so that I might by this convenient opportunity send it on to you. But since they did not bring it, I send a bare letter to that man of yours at his lodging. But lo, they now tell me that he had already departed from here the day before. How vexed this made me, I cannot put into words. You have the reason and my just excuse why that courier of yours has returned to you empty of my letter; and I beg you to accept it and put a kind construction upon it.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>At Prague, the 20th day of May, New Style, in the year 1589.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[deus].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thad[daeus]</i> = <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), the leading Bohemian physician-astronomer, <i>protomedicus</i> and personal physician at the imperial court of Rudolf II in Prague, and Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent and observer of the 1572 nova. The closing abbreviation <i>Thad.</i> is expanded in brackets.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO … INSULAe HUENAe DOMINO</i> = \"Master Tycho Brahe the Dane … lord of the island of <b>Hven</b>.\" <i>MATHEMATUM ARTIFICI SUMMO</i> rendered \"supreme craftsman of the mathematical sciences.\" The line <i>IN DENNEMARCK</i> is a vernacular (German/Danish) postal direction — \"to Denmark\" — left in its original form and translated.</li><li><b><i>vestras ille … nuncius … ad Imp.</b></i> — \"your courier to the Emperor\": a messenger sent by Tycho who had business (an audience) at the court of <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> (<i>Imp.</i> = <i>Imperatorem</i>). The letter's whole occasion is Hagecius's apology that he failed to entrust a letter (and a book) to this returning courier in time.</li><li><b><i>librum illum de astris … IO. FRANCI, Parisijs impressum, et Reginae Galliae dedicatum</b></i> — \"that book on the stars … by a certain Io. Francus, printed at Paris and dedicated to the Queen of France.\" <b>Crux (identification):</b> <i>Io. Franci</i> is the genitive of <i>Io[hannes] Francus</i> (Jean/Johann François/Franc); the specific Paris-printed astronomical/astrological book dedicated to the French queen is not securely identified here and is left as the source gives it. The phrase <i>de astris</i> (\"on the stars / on the heavenly bodies\") is kept broad rather than narrowed to \"astronomy\" or \"astrology.\" <i>Reginae Galliae</i> = \"the Queen of France\" (in the period, Catherine de' Medici as queen mother, or Louise of Lorraine as reigning queen — not specified).</li><li><b><i>olim Ratisponae donaveram</b></i> — \"I had once given [it to] you at <b>Regensburg</b> (Ratisbon)\": Hagecius and Tycho had been together at Regensburg, the occasion of the earlier gift of the book; <i>Ratisponae</i> is the locative.</li><li><b><i>Bibliopolas nostrates e mercatu Francoffordensi</b></i> — \"our booksellers from the Frankfurt fair\": the <b>Frankfurt book fair</b> (<i>mercatus Francoffordensis</i>), through which new printed books circulated north and east; Hagecius was awaiting the Prague booksellers' return with the fair's stock.</li><li><b><i>mitto nudas literas in hospitium</b></i> — \"I send a bare letter to [his] lodging\": <i>nudas literas</i> = a letter unaccompanied (by the promised book); <i>hospitium</i> = the courier's lodging/inn.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>Salutem</i> (\"greeting,\" §5.5). <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene Vale</i>, \"Fare you well\" (§5.5). <i>T. studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tuae studiosissimus</i> / <i>Tibi studiosissimus</i>, \"most devoted to you\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pragae die 20. Maij novo stylo Anno 1589</i> = \"At Prague, 20 May, New Style, 1589.\" Hagecius, in Catholic Imperial Prague, writes <b>New Style</b> (Gregorian), as he expressly states (<i>novo stylo</i>); the equivalent Old Style date (as Tycho on Hven would have reckoned) is 10 May 1589, not asserted by the source and not introduced into the header. The dataset label (\"1589. 20. maj n.s.\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one open point is the precise identity of the Paris-printed book \"by a certain Io. Francus … dedicated to the Queen of France,\" flagged above rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII pp.184-185 (page images, read visually); letter proper entirely on p.184, text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Io. Francus, Regensburg) and the date (20 May, new style, 1589) correct. No defects found; the self-assigned published_grade of B was unnecessarily conservative given the clean collation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1588 (7 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/45-48", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13966897"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.46."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 17, "prev": "brahe-t_15890510001", "next": "brahe-t_15891101001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890322001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891101001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports missing Tycho's courier to the Emperor and failing to obtain the book by Io. Francus from the Frankfurt fair booksellers for dispatch.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890520001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890618001", "d": "1589-06-18", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 18 June 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum · MS witness: A = Berlin, manuscript copy in a copy of Tycho Brahe's astronomical writings</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>IOANNES CRAIGUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>DEDI ante ad te literas, nobilissime vir, quibus tuis respondi, sed in ea festinatione scripta sunt, dum alio avocarer, ut relegere attente, quae scripseram, non potuerim. Paucis exposui, quid de quaestione a te mota sentiam, non contradicendi animo, sed veritatis et amicitiae nostrae causa, quo modo ut accipias peto. His literis simili modo dignam tractarem quaestionem, sed communis laetitia ita animum meum occupavit, ut ab ea ad eiusmodi speculationes abstrahi nequeat. Alia erit occasio; nunc quod omnium apud nos iudicijs salutare huic regno videtur, et omnium votis vehementer optatur, id ut eveniat et feliciter et statim, Deum oramus. Nihil etenim aeque in votis omnium est, quam ut clementiss. Domino nostro coniux sit, quae hac illustri Legatione petetur, nam inde optimas Reginas semper experti sumus, et nunc fore speramus: mihi quidem dubium non est, cui progenitores illustrissimae Principis partim visi, partim aliter noti sunt; sed id omnes metuimus, cum brevi temporis spacio, longo locorum intervallo tanta res peragenda sit, ne tantum bonum nobis, quam statim optamus, non contingat; sed votis aëra fatigabimus, ut hincinde ventos aspiret secundos. Et Deum orabimus, ne ulla hominum consilijs sit mora. Ille igitur, in cuius manu corda Regum sunt, ita omnia prosperet, ut et hominum consilia et Principum in hoc uno proposito affectus uniat. Illustrem Dominum Legatum comitantur ex amicis nostris aliqui, Dominus SKYNENS, iurisprudentia et dignitate apud nos praestans, et doctissimis in Germania viris olim charissimus; nobilis iuvenis MARCUS SWUNTONIUS, propinqua affinitate mihi iunctus; adolescens egregiae indolis et expectationis, THOMAS NICOLSONIUS, Philosophiae magister. Hos tua benevolentia complectaris et amicitia etiam digneris peto; praesertim apud te meo sit loco affinis meus. Incessit me quidem hoc tempore cupiditas tui visendi, sed ab aegris nobis abesse vix licet; meo loco, ut dixi, hos habeas. Vides, quomodo familiariter tecum agam, ac si non recens, sed multis annis confirmata esset nostra amicitia; at ea tamen est, ut mihi persuadeam, ut id abs te petere et expectare audeam. Ad nos si vestratium aliquos venire contigerit, sunt illi omnes eo apud nos loco, ut amicitiae non sint futuri immemores.</p><p>Ne inanes omnino hae literae essent, eam tractarem quaestionem, an hypotheses esse possint, quibus statuatur, absque orbibus Planetas moveri. Mihi aliquando in mentem veniebat, unicum esse ab octavo ad concavum Lunae orbem, qui a primo motu deficeret, in quo Planetae suos peculiares haberent orbes, quasi epicyclos quosdam, quibus omnis apparens anomalia possit explicari, sed has imaginationes alia excusserunt. Et duntaxat adhuc arridet ARISTOTELIS sententia, Planetas orbium certorum esse partes, a qua tamen nostra speculatio non abhorret. Sed tubis ad naves convocatur, iamque illustriss. Dominus Comes ingressus est, ventis afflantibus secundis. Faxit Deus, ut secundissimi sint.</p><p>Bene et feliciter vale, et CRAIGUM tui amantissimum esse existimato.<br>Datae 18. Iunij Edenburgi.<br>Tuus<br>IOAN. CRAIGUS,<br>Medicus.</p><p>POST SCRIPTA.</p><p>IAM audio, plures adhuc ex amicis nostris unâ proficisci, et inter alios M.<br>GUILIELMUM FULLERUM; iuvenis est<br>egregius, nec tua amicitia indignus. Vide, quam ego tibi notos quasi<br>accumulem; amici omnes mei sunt, et tuarum excellentium virtutum erunt<br>admiratores.</p><p>Iterum vale. Hæc quoque raptim.</p><p>IOAN. CRAIGUS, D.</p>", "en": "<p><b>John Craig to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I sent you a letter before, most noble Sir, in which I answered yours; but it was written in such haste, while I was being called away elsewhere, that I could not read over attentively what I had written. In few words I set out what I think about the question you had raised — not in a spirit of contradiction, but for the sake of truth and of our friendship, and I beg you to take it in that way. In the present letter I would treat a question worthy [of you] in like manner, but a common joy has so taken hold of my mind that it cannot be drawn away from it to speculations of that kind. There will be another occasion; for now, that what in the judgement of all of us here seems to be of benefit to this kingdom, and is fervently desired by the prayers of all — that this may come to pass, both happily and at once, we beseech God. For nothing is so much in the prayers of all as that there should be a consort for our most clement Lord, who is to be sought through this illustrious embassy; for from that quarter we have always found the best Queens, and we hope it will be so now too. I, indeed, have no doubt of it, who have seen the forebears of the most illustrious Princess in part, and in part know them otherwise; but this we all fear — since so great a matter must be carried through in a short space of time, over a long distance of places — lest so great a good not fall to us as soon as we desire it. But we will weary the air with prayers that it breathe favourable winds on this side and that. And we will pray God that there be no delay in men's counsels. May He, then, in whose hand are the hearts of Kings, so prosper all things that He may unite both the counsels of men and the dispositions of Princes in this one purpose. The illustrious Lord Ambassador is accompanied by some of our friends: Master Skene, eminent among us in jurisprudence and in dignity, and once most dear to the most learned men in Germany; the noble young man Marcus Swunton, joined to me by close kinship; [and] a youth of outstanding character and promise, Thomas Nicolson, master of philosophy. I beg you to embrace these men with your benevolence and to deem them worthy also of your friendship; and especially let my kinsman stand with you in my place. A desire of visiting you did indeed come over me at this time, but I can hardly be away from my patients; in my place, as I said, you are to have these men. You see how familiarly I deal with you, as though our friendship were not recent but confirmed by many years; yet it is nonetheless such that I persuade myself I may dare to ask and expect this of you. If any of your countrymen should chance to come to us, they all hold such a place with us that they will not be forgetful of friendship.</p><p>Lest this letter be altogether empty, I would treat the question whether there can be hypotheses by which it is established that the Planets move without orbs. It once came into my mind that there was a single orb from the eighth [sphere] to the concave [surface] of the Moon, which would lack the first motion, in which the Planets would have their own particular orbs, like certain epicycles, by which all apparent anomaly might be explained; but other [thoughts] shook these imaginings out. And so far only the opinion of Aristotle pleases me, that the Planets are parts of fixed orbs — from which, nevertheless, our speculation does not shrink away. But the call to the ships is sounding by trumpets, and now the most illustrious Lord Count has gone aboard, with favourable winds blowing. May God grant that they be most favourable.</p><p>Fare well and happily, and reckon that Craig loves you most dearly.<br>Given 18 June, at Edinburgh.<br>Yours,<br>John Craig,<br>Physician.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT.</b></p><p>I hear even now that several more of our friends are setting out together, and among others Master William Fuller — he is an outstanding young man, and not unworthy of your friendship. See how I heap up acquaintances upon you, as it were; they are all my friends, and will be admirers of your excellent virtues. Farewell again. This too, in haste. John Craig, Doctor [of Medicine].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioannes Craigus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Scottish physician and mathematician, a graduate of Frankfurt an der Oder who taught logic and mathematics in Germany before returning to Scotland to become physician to King James VI. He carried on a sharp astronomical correspondence with Tycho over several years, in which (as here) he disputed Tycho's positions; he signs himself <i>Medicus</i>, \"Physician.\"</li><li><b>The \"question you had raised\" / the orbs controversy.</b> The technical paragraph engages Tycho's claim — central to the <i>Tychonic system</i> and developed from the path of the 1577 comet — that the heavens are fluid and the planets move <i>absque orbibus</i>, \"without [solid] orbs.\" Craig defends instead the Aristotelian view that <i>Planetas orbium certorum esse partes</i> (\"the Planets are parts of fixed orbs\"), i.e. that the planets are carried by real solid spheres. This is the running scientific subject of the Craig–Tycho exchange. <i>Orbis</i> is rendered \"orb\" per protocol §5.1; <i>epicyclos</i> \"epicycles\" (§5.1); <i>anomalia</i> \"anomaly\" (§5.1).</li><li><b><i>ab octavo ad concavum Lunae orbem</b></i> — \"an orb from the eighth [sphere] to the concave [surface] of the Moon\": the <i>eighth sphere</i> is the sphere of the fixed stars; the <i>concavum Lunae</i> is the inner (concave) surface of the Moon's sphere. Craig floats, then rejects, a single great orb filling the space between, lacking the <i>primus motus</i> (the diurnal \"first motion\" of the eighth sphere), within which the planets would carry their own epicycle-like orbs. The bracketed words [sphere], [surface] are editorial supplements for an elliptical Latin construction.</li><li><b>The \"common joy\" / the embassy.</b> The <i>illustris Legatio</i> (\"illustrious embassy\") and the <i>coniux … quae hac illustri Legatione petetur</i> (\"a consort … who is to be sought through this illustrious embassy\") refer to the Scottish embassy of 1589 sent to negotiate the marriage of <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (the <i>clementissimus Dominus noster</i>, \"our most clement Lord\") to <b>Anne of Denmark</b>, daughter of the late Frederik II — the <i>illustrissima Princeps</i> whose <i>progenitores</i> (\"forebears\") Craig says he has in part seen and in part otherwise knows. The marriage by proxy took place at Kronborg in August 1589. The departure of the embassy's ships, described in the closing lines, dates the letter precisely.</li><li><b><i>illustriss. Dominus Comes</b></i> — \"the most illustrious Lord Count\": the head of the embassy, <b>George Keith, 5th Earl Marischal</b>, who led the marriage embassy to Denmark; named here only by title (<i>Comes</i>, \"Count/Earl\"). Earlier in the letter the same man is the <i>Illustris Dominus Legatus</i>, \"the illustrious Lord Ambassador.\"</li><li><b>The named companions.</b> <i>Dominus SKYNENS</i> = <b>Master [John] Skene</b> (c. 1543–1617), the eminent Scottish jurist (later author of <i>De Verborum Significatione</i> and editor of the Scots statutes), who had indeed studied and been known in Germany — Craig's <i>doctissimis in Germania viris olim charissimus</i>. <i>MARCUS SWUNTONIUS</i> = <b>Marcus Swinton (Swunton)</b>, described as Craig's kinsman by close <i>affinitas</i> (marriage-kinship); Craig asks Tycho to receive him \"in my place\" (<i>meo loco</i>). <i>THOMAS NICOLSONIUS, Philosophiae magister</i> = <b>Thomas Nicolson, Master of Philosophy (M.A.)</b>, a promising young man. The vernacular forms of these surnames are given as identifications; the Latinised forms are kept in the source block.</li><li><b><i>affinis meus</b></i> — \"my kinsman\" (by marriage): refers back to Marcus Swinton, whom Craig has just called <i>propinqua affinitate mihi iunctus</i>.</li><li><b><i>votis aëra fatigabimus, ut … ventos aspiret secundos</b></i> — \"we will weary the air with prayers, that it breathe favourable winds\": the subject of <i>aspiret</i> is left grammatically open (the air, or God); rendered with \"[the sea]\" rejected in favour of keeping the air as antecedent — see crux note. <i>ventos secundos</i> \"favourable winds,\" picked up at the close in <i>ventis afflantibus secundis</i> and the wordplay <i>secundissimi</i>.</li><li><b><i>in cuius manu corda Regum sunt</b></i> — \"in whose hand are the hearts of Kings\": a Scriptural allusion to Proverbs 21:1 (<i>cor regis in manu Domini</i>), kept as allusion.</li><li><b><i>tubis ad naves convocatur</b></i> — \"the call to the ships is sounding by trumpets\": impersonal passive (<i>convocatur</i>, \"there is a summoning\"), rendered \"the call … is sounding.\" The scene is the embarkation of the embassy's fleet.</li><li><b><i>Faxit Deus, ut secundissimi sint</b></i> — \"May God grant that they be most favourable\": <i>faxit</i> is the archaic/optative form of <i>facere</i> (= <i>faciat</i>), a deliberate solemn flourish; <i>secundissimi</i> (\"most favourable\") superlative of the <i>secundi</i> winds, retaining the wind/fortune double sense.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None present.</li><li><b>Edition &amp; witness.</b> Edition of record as given in the source: <i>Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum</i>. The MS witness recorded is a handwritten copy preserved in Berlin within a copy of Tycho's astronomical writings (shelfmark not specified in the source record). Dreyer prints this letter in the <i>Epistolae</i> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII); the present text follows the supplied transcription.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datae 18. Iunij Edenburgi</i> = \"Given 18 June, at Edinburgh.\" Scotland, like Denmark, was still on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1589; the date is therefore O.S., consistent with the dataset label <i>1589. 18. juni. Edinburgh</i>. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Back-translation fix (2026-06-02).</b> Removed the bracketed \"[the sea]\" from the body of the <i>aspiret</i> clause — it contradicted the apparatus note (which keeps <i>aër</i> the open antecedent and inserts none); the rendering now matches.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> The letter's actual POST SCRIPTA, appended after the sign-off implied by the published tail's own valediction and dateline — a last-minute addition written, as Craig says, \"in haste\" while the fleet was already boarding (the published tail describes \"the most illustrious Lord Count\" going aboard ship with favourable winds).</li><li><b>The sender.</b> John Craig (d. c.1620), Scottish physician who took his medical degree at Basel in 1580 and became personal physician to James VI of Scotland; he and Tycho carried on a protracted epistolary dispute over the nature of the 1577 comet (whether supralunary or meteorological), touched on more fully elsewhere in their correspondence [Tier 1: Christianson et al., <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague: Crossroads of European Science</i> (2002), ch. \"Tycho Brahe and John Craig: The Dynamic of a Dispute\"; Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>].</li><li><b>William Fuller.</b> Introduced here as \"an outstanding young man,\" one of a further group of Craig's friends travelling together at this moment (June 1589) — plausibly in the train of the embassy then crossing to Denmark in connection with James VI's marriage to Anne of Denmark. Fuller recurs in Craig's later correspondence: in his next letter to Tycho (28 February 1590), amid a request to be recommended as personal physician to the newly-wed Anne of Denmark, Craig again mentions Fuller by name [Tier 1: Christianson et al., <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i> (2002), p.75] — though the exact nature of Fuller's business is not spelled out in the material consulted.</li><li><b>Context.</b> \"The most illustrious Lord Count\" of the published tail is almost certainly the leader of the 1589 Scottish mission to Denmark for the marriage negotiations; Craig's postscript is social lubrication, vouching for a travelling companion to secure him a welcome from Tycho.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed the spurious 'fore' from 'salutare huic regno fore videtur' per Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.191-192, which prints simply 'salutare huic regno videtur'; English adjusted from 'seems likely to be of benefit' to 'seems to be of benefit'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical frame (the 1589 marriage embassy; the orbs controversy) is well grounded. One genuine crux is flagged rather than smoothed: in <i>votis aëra fatigabimus, ut hincinde ventos aspiret secundos</i> the implied subject of <i>aspiret</i> (\"breathe [favourable winds]\") is grammatically underdetermined — most naturally the <i>aër</i> just named (the air sending winds), though a divine subject is also readable; the translation keeps the open construction and does not insert an antecedent unbracketed.</p><p>The Latin is entirely clear and unproblematic; this is a straightforward social postscript. Held at B rather than A only because William Fuller's identity and role are not fully resolved from the corpus consulted — he is named again in Craig's 1590 letter but not further characterised there either, so his connection to the Danish embassy is inference from context rather than a confirmed fact. Upgrade path: trace Fuller through Scottish court/embassy personnel lists for 1589–90, and check the Craig–Tycho letter of 28 February 1590 in full for what he is doing at Tycho's court.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.191-194 (slice pages 1-4); the Craig letter of 18 June 1589, with its postscript, runs from mid-p.191 through the top of p.193 (p.194 is the following letter, buffer). Collated the full letter including every proper name (Skene, Marcus Swunton, Thomas Nicolson, William Fuller) and the date (18 June, Edinburgh) against the printed text. Found one word-level departure of source_text from Dreyer (\"fore\" inserted, not present in the print) in the third sentence; the translation itself renders the (erroneous) source_text faithfully, so this is a TEXT_ERROR, not a MISTRANSLATION. No fabrication, omission, or other mistranslation found; per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though this one does not change the overall sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text inserts \"fore\" (a future-infinitive particle giving \"seems likely to be\") that Dreyer's printed page does not have; Dreyer simply reads \"videtur\" (\"seems\"). The published English faithfully renders the corrupted source_text, not Dreyer's actual text. Dreyer prints: <i>nunc quod omnium apud nos iudicijs salutare huic regno videtur (no \"fore\")</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.191-194, all 4 pages read). P.191-192 (the Craig letter spans the page break) confirms 'nunc quod omnium apud nos iudicijs salutare huic regno videtur, et omnium votis vehementer optatur' with no 'fore' present. No other cruces found on the four pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Berlin, Håndskrevet afskrift i et eksemplar af Tycho Brahes astronomiske skrifter i Berlin."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15890500001", "next": "brahe-t_15890709001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891025001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Refers to a previous hurried reply on Tycho's question, then reports the universal joy and prayer in Scotland for King James to find a consort.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890618001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890709001", "d": "1589-07-09", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 9 July 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum </i>· MS witness: A = Berlin, manuscript copy in a copy of Tycho Brahe's astronomical writings</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES CRAIGUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et clarissime vir, amice colende. Non occurrit hoc tempore, quod scriptione dignum videatur, sed cum detur occasio, non praetermittendam puto. Et ne merae omnino sint literae, quamvis hoc fere nihili sit instar, quod prioribus coeperam de coelestium Hypothesi ad te scribere, paucis repetam. In tua Hypothesi non alienum videtur, quod Terra luminarium et Sol caeterorum Planetarum sit centrum, sed tamen absurdum implicat, quod Mars acronychus terris propior fiat Sole; concinnior fortasse erit, si punctum Terrae vicinius quam Sol statuatur centrum, aut si id nolis, ut duplici epicyclo augeatur orbis Planetae superioris, quemadmodum ad me suam ea de re cantationem scripsit M. DUNCANUS LIDDELIUS; quae quidem Hypothesis nihil absurdi habebit; nam non solum hoc tuum vitabitur, sed etiam orbes Planetis asscribi poterunt. Sunt et plures modi, quibus ista supponi possunt, et ipse aliquando exponam, quid hac de re sentiam, plenius, omnino enim incumbendum statuo, ut Physicis et Mathematicis pariter satisfiat. Audio, clarissime vir, te in Chymicis praeclare versatum esse, rogo te, ut mihi aliqua ex secretioribus et utilioribus communices. Habet ROLLANDUS in suis Centurijs curarum, ut sic loquar, aliquas mirabiles compositiones, ut aquae benedictae, balsami sulphuris, spiritus vitae, extractae esulae, quae si tibi nota sunt, fac me earum participem. Habeo extractum esulae communi modo per spiritum vini; sed est alia quaedam singularis ratio in istis extractionibus; sic si tibi aliquid constet de distillatis aquis purgantibus, quae iam apud aliquos in usu sunt. Fuit apud nos hisce diebus PETRUS PAYSEN Danus, vir in linguis egregie versatus, et in pluribus disciplinis bene institutus, et in Mathematicis quoque bene doctus; multas regiones peragravit, et multa vidit, ei trado literas meas, ut ad tuam amicitiam ipsi aditus fiat, qua non dubito quin dignus futurus sit.</p><p>Bene et feliciter vale.<br>Datae Edenburgi 9. Iulij.<br>Salutem precor nostratibus amicis.<br>Tuus<br>IOAN. CRAIGUS, Doctor.</p>", "en": "<p><b>John Craig to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most noble and most renowned Sir, friend to be honoured. Nothing presents itself at this time that seems worth the writing, yet since the occasion offers, I think it ought not to be passed by. And so that the letter may not be wholly empty — although this is next to nothing — I will briefly take up again what I had begun to write to you in earlier [letters] concerning the Hypothesis of the heavens. In your Hypothesis it does not seem out of place that the Earth should be the centre of the luminaries, and the Sun the centre of the remaining Planets; but it nonetheless involves an absurdity, namely that Mars, [when] acronychal, comes to be nearer the Earth than the Sun [is]. It will perhaps be more elegant if a point nearer the Earth than the Sun be set down as the centre; or, if you do not want that, [more elegant] that the orb of a superior Planet be augmented by a double epicycle — just as Master Duncan Liddel wrote me his little tune on this matter; which Hypothesis, indeed, will have nothing absurd in it, for not only will this [difficulty] of yours be avoided, but orbs too will be able to be assigned to the Planets. There are also several ways in which these things can be supposed, and I myself will one day set out more fully what I think on this matter; for I hold that one must by all means strive that satisfaction be given to the Physicists and the Mathematicians alike. I hear, most renowned Sir, that you are excellently versed in chymical matters; I ask you to share with me some of the more secret and more useful ones. Rolland has in his Centuries of cures, so to speak, certain wonderful preparations — such as of blessed water, of balsam of sulphur, of spirit of life, of extract of spurge — which, if they are known to you, make me a sharer in them. I have extract of spurge made in the common way, by spirit of wine; but there is a certain other, singular method in these extractions; likewise [I should be glad] if anything be established to you concerning the distilled purging waters which are now in use among some. There was with us in these [past] days Peter Paysen, a Dane, a man excellently versed in tongues and well grounded in several disciplines, and well learned in Mathematics too; he has travelled through many regions and seen many things. To him I entrust my letter, so that access to your friendship may be opened for him — of which I do not doubt that he will prove worthy.</p><p>Fare you well and happily.<br>Given at Edinburgh, 9 July.<br>I beg [you to convey] greeting to our friends.<br>Yours,<br>John Craig, Doctor.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioannes Craigus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. c.1620), Scottish physician and mathematician, educated on the Continent (Frankfurt an der Oder, where Duncan Liddel studied under him; later Königsberg), and from the 1580s royal physician at the court of James VI in Edinburgh. He entered a protracted epistolary dispute with Tycho over the geo-heliocentric world-system (treated in Adam Mosley, \"Tycho Brahe and John Craig: The Dynamic of a Dispute,\" 2002); this letter is an early, still-courteous instalment of that exchange. The subscription <i>Doctor</i> marks his medical doctorate.</li><li><b>The astronomical objection (the substance of the letter).</b> Craig grants the structure of Tycho's <i>Hypothesis</i> — <i>Terra luminarium et Sol caeterorum Planetarum centrum</i>, \"the Earth the centre of the luminaries [Sun and Moon], the Sun the centre of the remaining planets,\" i.e. the geo-heliocentric arrangement. His objection is that <i>Mars acronychus terris propior fiat Sole</i>: that Mars at opposition (<b>acronychal</b> — rising at sunset, hence diametrically opposite the Sun) is brought nearer the Earth than the Sun is. This is the celebrated consequence of Tycho's system that the Martian orb must intersect the solar orb — the very point that made Tycho abandon the solid celestial spheres. Craig reads it as an <i>absurdum</i>; the hedge <i>non alienum … sed tamen absurdum implicat</i> is preserved (he concedes the merit, then flags the flaw).</li><li><b>Craig's proposed remedies.</b> (1) <i>si punctum Terrae vicinius quam Sol statuatur centrum</i> — set the planets' centre at a point <b>nearer the Earth than the Sun</b>; (2) failing that, <i>duplici epicyclo augeatur orbis Planetae superioris</i> — augment a superior planet's orb with a <b>double epicycle</b> (here = a second, additional epicycle), so that solid orbs (<i>orbes</i>) can still be assigned to the planets without intersection. Craig's programme — <i>ut Physicis et Mathematicis pariter satisfiat</i>, \"that satisfaction be given to the <i>Physicists</i> and the <i>Mathematicians</i> alike\" — is to save both the physical reality of solid spheres and the geometry; this is the crux of his disagreement with Tycho, who held the spheres to be fluid/non-existent.</li><li><b>Duncan Liddel.</b> <i>M. Duncanus Liddelius</i> = <b>Duncan Liddel</b> (1561–1613), Scottish mathematician and physician, then teaching at Frankfurt an der Oder / Rostock (later Helmstedt; he endowed a mathematics chair at Aberdeen in 1613). <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i>, \"Master\" (academic). <i>cantationem … scripsit</i> — literally \"wrote his <i>song/chant</i>\"; here a faintly belittling figure for Liddel's set-piece on the double-epicycle device. Kept as the metaphor \"little tune,\" with the diminutive force flagged rather than smoothed to a neutral \"account.\"</li><li><b>Chymica / alchemy.</b> <i>Chymicis … versatum</i> — Craig solicits Tycho's chemical (Paracelsian-iatrochemical) secrets. <i>Rollandus … in suis Centurijs curarum</i> = the <i>Centuriae curationum</i> / <i>Centuries of cures</i>, a medical-recipe collection (most plausibly the <i>Curationum … centuriae</i> genre associated with the period's iatrochemists; the precise \"Rolland\" is not securely fixed — see crux). The named preparations: <i>aqua benedicta</i> \"blessed water\" (a period purgative/emetic compound — <i>not</i> holy water in the devotional sense); <i>balsamum sulphuris</i> \"balsam of sulphur\" (sulphur dissolved in oil, an iatrochemical remedy); <i>spiritus vitae</i> \"spirit of life\"; <i>extractum esulae</i> \"extract of <b>spurge</b>\" (<i>esula</i> = <i>Euphorbia esula</i>, a drastic purgative), which Craig says he prepares <i>communi modo per spiritum vini</i>, \"in the common way, by spirit of wine,\" but suspects a <i>singularis ratio</i>, a special method. <i>distillatae aquae purgantes</i> = \"distilled purging waters.\" Period terms retained and glossed per protocol §5.4; none modernised.</li><li><b>Peter Paysen.</b> <i>Petrus Paysen Danus</i> — <b>Peter Paysen</b> (Peder Paysen), a Dane, here described as a learned traveller skilled in languages and mathematics, carrying this letter as a letter of introduction to Tycho. Not otherwise identified here; the name is given as written.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Edenburgi 9. Iulij</i> = \"Given at <b>Edinburgh</b>, 9 July.\" The source dateline carries <b>no year</b>; the dataset label supplies <i>1589</i>, consistent with the Tycho–Craig exchange and with the Berlin-copy witness. The header therefore reads \"9 July 1589,\" but note that the year is editorial/catalogue-supplied, not present in the dateline. <b>O.S.</b>: a Scottish correspondent in 1589 wrote Old Style (Scotland did not adopt the Gregorian calendar until 1600/1752); the 9 July is Julian.</li><li><b>Edition / witness.</b> Printed in the <i>Commercium litterarium clarorum virorum</i> (the edition of record per the source); MS witness A = a handwritten copy bound in a copy of Tycho's astronomical writings, now in Berlin (shelfmark not specified in the source).</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>Bene et feliciter vale</i> → \"Fare you well and happily\" (§5.5). <i>Tuus … Doctor</i> subscription rendered in full.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the astronomy (the acronychal-Mars objection, the nearer-than-Sun centre, the double-epicycle remedy, the Physicists-and-Mathematicians programme) and the epistolary frame are rendered securely. The one soft point is the alchemical bibliographic reference <i>Rollandus … Centuriis curarum</i>: the exact author/work behind \"Rolland\" is not pinned down from the source alone and is flagged rather than over-specified; the recipe names themselves are translated on the controlled-glossary model.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the Tychonic-hypothesis/Mars-acronychal passage, Duncan Liddel reference, the Rolland chymical recipes, Peter Paysen introduction, date and signature) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII p.193 as a page image. Exact match. The scientifically dense hypothesis paragraph is rendered accurately and carefully. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Berlin, Håndskrevet afskrift i et eksemplar af Tycho Brahes astronomiske skrifter i Berlin."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15890618001", "next": "brahe-t_15891025001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891025001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Resumes discussion of Tycho's celestial hypothesis, arguing that Mars at acronychal position nearer than the Sun is an absurdity, and proposes alternative geometric solutions.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890709001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890726001", "d": "1589-07-26", "dir": "third", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": "Christopher Rothmann", "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Homberg (Efze)", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Christoph Rothmann — Homberg, 26 July 1589 [O.S.] (catalogued Kassel)", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin code-switches) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>VNSERM MATHEMATICO VND LIEBEN GETREWEN CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA/ ETC.</p><p>LIEBER Getrewer/ Wir mögen dir in gnaden nicht verhalten/ das Zeiger dieses D. Nicodemus Frischlinus vorhabens ist/ eine Reyse in Daniam zuthun. Dieweil er dann sonder zweiffel zu dem TYCHONI de BRAHE kommen wirdet/ Als ist an dich vnser befelich in gnaden/ das du etwa ein sein schreiben an jhnen thuest/ vnd von jhme zuwissen begerest/ was er jtzo gutes vorhabe/ vnd wann er seine vorgehabte Arbeit dermahls eins werde außgehen lassen/ Dann vns verlange sehr darnach solchs zusehen. Das thun wir vns zu dir mit gnaden versehen.</p><p>DatumHombergk am 26. Iulij, Anno 89.<br>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Our Mathematician and trusty well-beloved Christoph Rothmann.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, etc.</b></p><p>Trusty well-beloved. We would not graciously withhold from you that the bearer of this [letter], Master Nicodemus Frischlin, has it in mind to undertake a journey into Denmark [<i>Daniam</i>]. Since, then, he will without doubt come to Tycho de Brahe [<i>TYCHONI de BRAHE</i>], it is therefore Our gracious charge upon you that you write him some letter of yours to him, and desire to learn from him what good thing he now has in hand, and when he will at length let his earlier-undertaken work go forth; for We greatly long to see such [a thing]. This We graciously look for from you.</p><p>Given at Homberg, the 26th of July, in the year [15]89.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince of Kassel and Tycho's principal German correspondent (protocol §5.6). The titulature — <i>Landgraf zu Hessen, Graf zu Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain und Nidda</i> — is the standard Hessian style; preserved in full per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550 – c. 1600), the Landgrave's resident mathematician/astronomer at Kassel from late 1584 (protocol §5.6). Here addressed as the prince's subordinate — <i>Vnserm Mathematico vnd lieben getrewen</i> (\"Our Mathematician and trusty well-beloved\") — in the princely <i>Wir/du</i> register.</li><li><b>The bearer.</b> <i>D. Nicodemus Frischlinus</i> = <b>Nicodemus Frischlin</b> (1547–1590), the Neo-Latin poet, dramatist, and philologist (here <i>poeta laureatus</i> of the Count Palatine). Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) confirms the occasion exactly: Wilhelm, hunting at Homburg in July 1589 and lacking recent news of Tycho's work, learned that Frischlin intended to travel to Denmark and induced him to go via Kassel carrying these instructions for Rothmann. <i>Zeiger dieses</i> (\"the bearer of this [letter]\") is the chancery formula for the courier carrying the document.</li><li><b>Code-switches (German → Latin).</b> The letter switches into Latin for the proper-name material the chancery treats as fixed: <i>in Daniam</i> (\"into Denmark,\" accusative of motion); <i>zu dem TYCHONI de BRAHE</i> (dative <i>Tychoni</i>, the Latin name retained whole inside the German clause); the heading <i>Christophoro Rothmanno</i> and <i>Datum</i> (\"Given\"). The code-switch is preserved per protocol §2; <i>Datum</i> rendered \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Negations / legal force.</b> Two negation-bearing constructions, both reproduced exactly. (1) <i>Wir mögen dir … nicht verhalten</i> is a courtly litotes — literally \"We may not withhold from you,\" i.e. \"We would not keep from you / We are pleased to inform you\"; rendered \"We would not … withhold,\" keeping the single negation rather than flattening it to a plain positive. (2) <i>sonder zweiffel</i> = \"without doubt,\" rendered as such. The operative clause <i>ist … vnser befelich … das du …</i> is a gracious command (\"it is Our charge upon you that you …\"), not a request; the three governed verbs — <i>ein schreiben … thuest</i> (write a letter), <i>zuwissen begerest</i> (seek to learn), and the indirect questions <i>was er … vorhabe / wann er … außgehen lassen [werde]</i> — are kept in their subordinate, reported-question mood.</li><li><b><i>was er jtzo gutes vorhabe / seine vorgehabte Arbeit … außgehen lassen.</b></i> \"What good thing he now has in hand\" and \"let his earlier-undertaken work go forth [= publish it]\": the Landgrave is asking Rothmann to draw from Tycho, through Frischlin, news of Tycho's current projects and especially when he will at last <i>publish</i> the work already undertaken — <i>außgehen lassen</i> in the period sense \"issue, bring out (in print).\" The phrasing is kept neutral; no specific Tychonic title is named in the source, so none is supplied.</li><li><b>Place / dateline crux.</b> The dateline reads <i>DatumHombergk am 26. Iulij, Anno 89</i> (the 1596 print runs <i>Datum</i> and <i>Hombergk</i> together without a space; transcribed verbatim). The <b>place is Homberg</b>, a Hessian landgravial seat where Wilhelm was hunting — <b>not Kassel</b>, which the dataset `label` field gives. Mosley's \"hunting at Homburg in July of that same year\" corroborates the dateline against the catalogue label; the header therefore leads with the as-written \"Homberg\" and appends \"(catalogued Kassel).\" Homberg (Efze), north of the Schwalm hunting grounds, is the most probable referent for a Hessian <i>Hombergk</i> hunting dateline, though the source does not disambiguate it from other Hessian Hombergs; flagged rather than asserted. Date <i>26. Iulij Anno 89</i> = 26 July 1589, Old Style (Hesse keeping the Julian calendar; protocol §3); not silently converted.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> The letter is printed in Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I, Uraniborg 1596) — the volume Tycho printed at Hven — whence Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German is rendered securely and the negations counted and preserved; the two soft points, both flagged above, are (i) the precise Hessian Homberg meant by the <i>Hombergk</i> dateline and (ii) the unnamed \"earlier-undertaken work\" Wilhelm wishes Tycho to publish — neither resolved by the source and neither smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No tboo reference (tboo:null) — this letter (German, Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel to Rothmann, printed apparently outside the TBOO Epistolae Astronomicae volumes consulted here) could not be collated against Dreyer's page images, per protocol capped at grade B regardless of quality. Audited against source_text alone: checked all four comital titles (Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda), the two named persons (Nicodemus Frischlinus, Tycho de Brahe), and the date (26 Iulij, Anno 89) against the translation — all accurate. Verified the pronoun chain 'jhnen/jhme/er' in the central clause consistently resolves to Tycho (the addressee Rothmann is being asked to write to Tycho and learn of Tycho's work), not to Frischlin, and the translation preserves this correctly with no flip. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann ↔ Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15910209001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Instructs Rothmann to write to Tycho via Nicodemus Frischlin, travelling to Denmark, asking what Tycho has in hand and when his earlier work will appear.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890726001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890806001", "d": "1589-08-06", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 27 July 1589 [O.S.] (catalogued \"17 January 1587\")", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>LITERAe CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI AD TYCHONEM BRAHE, AD ANNVM OCTUAGESIMUM NONUM POTIUS PERTINENTES. S. P.</p><p>QVID superioribus tibi literis scripserim, et qua ratione syncere voluntati tuae morem gesserim, tibique meum qualecunque, nec fortassis vsque adeo magni momenti iudicium candide explicarim, Nobilissime Excellentissimeque Dn. TYCHO, mihique longe charissime, tibi est notissimum. Etsi autem in proxime praeteritis Nundinis Francofurdensibus responsum non sine desiderio expectaremus, tamen nihil prorsus, quod a te esset, nobis exhibitum est. Cuius rei causam, cum a me impense requireret Illustrissimus Princeps noster, nihil aliud respondere potui, quam aut te non habuisse cui recte literas ad nos committere posses, aut alijs negotijs impeditum fuissent. Hoc vero tempore, de nouo, ab Illustrissimo Principe meo, iam extra munitionem Cassellanam, in Comitatu Hombergico, venationibus vacante, literas mihi attulit Dn. D. NICODEMVS FRISCHLINVS, vir ex eruditis suis scriptis tibi forte notissimus, et Com. Palat, ac Poët. Laure, quibus iubeor, vt a te simul et responsum flagitem, ac te insuper admoneam, ne Opus tuum de Recentioribus mundi aetherei Phaenomenis absolutum, Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudini mittere, vlterius differas. Se enim maximo eius videndi desiderio teneri, vt ipsa verba, quae in dictis Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudinis literis continentur, referam. Scis me superioribus tibi literis significasse, quod spem fecerim Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. de Opere illo tuo â te huc mittendo. Id itaque iam Illustrissimus Heros adeo vrget. Quod igitur superioribus feci literis, illud adhuc a te vehementer etiam atque etiam maiorem in modum peto contendoque, vt fidem meam redimas. Deinde etsi Illustriss. Princeps noster superiori Autumno nihil literarum ad te dedit, varijs modis impeditus, tamen nihilominus aliquas iam a te expectat, praesertim de statu tuo quid agas, et quid iam tibi sit de Astronomicis laboribus prae manibus: Nec ipsi gratior vlla est, quam sibi quae BRAHAe praescripsit pagina nomen. Sed non dubito, quin in his omnibus Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. sis cumulatissime satisfacturus. Cum item Illustriss. et Clementiss. Princeps meus mihi mandarit, vt per hunc doctissimum sane virum tibi scriberem, facile per te intelligere poteris, Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. hoc suo ad me mandato hunc tibi eo commendatiorem reddere voluisse. Sed tamen id per te propter studia Philosophica facturus es, vt non ignoro. Cognoui etiam ex literis fratris mei Iohannis, quod disputationes nostras ad Dn. D. PEVCERVM miseris, quodque Dn. PEVCERVS alia consilia proferat, nec mihi aut tibi suffragari velit. Sed haec quo sint habenda loco tu intelligis. Nihil consilium hîc, nihil humana valet authoritas. Doctrinam Triangulorum qui intelligit, sua sponte in veram pedibus ibit sententiam. Tu fac responsione tua, vt ad dulcissimas nostras Philosophicas disputationes redeamus. Sed fortasse plus quam sat erat.</p><p>Tu igitur Nobiliss. et Excellentiss. Dn. TYCHO quam optime vale, quaeque scripsi in optimam partem accipe.<br>Cassellis, 6. Calendas Augusti, Anno 1589.<br>T. T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A LETTER OF CHRISTOPH ROTHMANN TO TYCHO BRAHE, BELONGING RATHER TO THE YEAR EIGHTY-NINE. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>What I wrote to you in my last letter, and in what spirit I sincerely complied with your wish, and frankly set out for you my own judgement — such as it was, and perhaps not of any very great moment — is very well known to you, most noble and most excellent Master Tycho, and to me by far the dearest of men. But although at the Frankfurt fair just past we were awaiting a reply not without longing, yet nothing at all that came from you was delivered to us. When our Most Illustrious Prince pressed me earnestly for the cause of this, I could answer nothing else but that either you had had no one to whom you could properly entrust a letter to us, or that you had been hindered by other business. But at this time, anew, from my Most Illustrious Prince — who is now outside the fortifications of Kassel, in the County of Homberg, taking his leisure at the hunt — a letter has been brought to me by Master Doctor Nicodemus Frischlin, a man perhaps very well known to you from his learned writings, Count Palatine and Poet Laureate, by which I am ordered both to demand a reply of you and, moreover, to admonish you not to defer any longer the sending of your completed Work on the recent phenomena of the aethereal world to His Most Illustrious Highness himself. For he is held by the greatest desire to see it — to report the very words contained in the said letter of His Most Illustrious Highness. You know that in my last letter I indicated to you that I had raised His Most Illustrious Highness's hope of that Work of yours being sent here by you. This, therefore, the Most Illustrious Hero now presses so much. What I did, then, in my last letter, that same I still ask and strive for from you vehemently, again and again, in even greater measure: that you redeem my pledge. Next, although our Most Illustrious Prince sent no letters to you last autumn, hindered in various ways, yet he nonetheless now awaits some from you, especially concerning your situation — what you are doing, and what you now have in hand of your astronomical labours; nor is any page more welcome to him than one that has set the name of BRAHE at its head. But I do not doubt that in all these matters you will give His Most Illustrious Highness the most abundant satisfaction. Since my Most Illustrious and Most Clement Prince has likewise charged me to write to you through this man — most learned indeed — you will easily understand for yourself that His Most Illustrious Highness, by this command of his to me, wished to render this man the more commended to you on that account. Yet you will do this on your own account, for the sake of philosophical studies, as I am well aware. I have learned, too, from the letters of my brother Johannes, that you sent our disputations to Master Doctor Peucer, and that Master Peucer puts forward other opinions, and is unwilling to side with either me or you. But in what regard these things are to be held, you understand. Here counsel avails nothing, human authority avails nothing. Whoever understands the Doctrine of Triangles will, of his own accord, go on foot into the true opinion. Do you, by your reply, bring it about that we return to our most delightful philosophical disputations. But perhaps [I have said] more than was enough.</p><p>Do you therefore, most noble and most excellent Master Tycho, fare as well as may be, and take in the best part the things I have written.<br>At Kassel, the sixth day before the Kalends of August [27 July], in the year 1589.<br>Yours wholly,<br>Christoph Rothmann.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1555–c. 1600), mathematician and astronomer to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b>; Tycho's principal scientific correspondent of the late 1580s. He writes here on the Landgrave's instruction. The exchange is one printed by Tycho himself in the 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (the edition of record).</li><li><b>Recipient / addressee titulature.</b> <i>Nobilissime Excellentissimeque Dn. TYCHO</i> — \"most noble and most excellent Master Tycho\" (<i>Dn.</i> = <i>Dominus</i>, protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>The Prince.</b> <i>Illustrissimus Princeps noster / Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. / Illustrissimus Heros</i> — <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592). <i>Celsitudo</i> → \"Highness\" and <i>Princeps</i> → \"Prince\" per §5.5; <i>Heros</i> (\"Hero\") is kept as Rothmann's honorific flourish. <i>munitio Cassellana … in Comitatu Hombergico</i> = \"the fortifications of Kassel … the County of Homberg\" (Homberg an der Efze, in Hesse).</li><li><b><i>Nicodemus Frischlinus</b></i> = <b>Nicodemus Frischlin</b> (1547–1590), the Württemberg humanist poet and dramatist, <i>Comes Palatinus</i> (Count Palatine) and <i>Poeta Laureatus</i> (Poet Laureate) — here the bearer of the Landgrave's letter, whom Rothmann commends to Tycho. <i>Com. Palat, ac Poët. Laure.</i> expanded accordingly.</li><li><b><i>Opus tuum de Recentioribus mundi aetherei Phaenomenis</b></i> — Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the second volume of his projected trilogy, the comet/world-system book printed at Uraniborg), here named as the work the Landgrave is impatient to receive. Rendered \"[your] Work on the recent phenomena of the aethereal world.\"</li><li><b><i>Nundinae Francofurdenses</b></i> — \"the Frankfurt fair\" (the Frankfurt book fair), the standard early-modern channel for transmitting scholarly letters and books.</li><li><b><i>disputationes nostras … ad Dn. D. Peucerum miseris</b></i> — \"you sent our disputations to Master Doctor Peucer.\" <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician-astronomer, whom Tycho was using as an arbiter; Rothmann learned, via his brother <b>Johannes Rothmann</b>, that Tycho had circulated their private debate to Peucer, who would side with neither. Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) quotes this very sentence and dates the letter 27 July 1589, corroborating the dateline below.</li><li><b><i>Doctrina Triangulorum</b></i> — the <b>Doctrine of Triangles</b> (trigonometry); Rothmann's point that the dispute will be settled by mathematics, not authority. <i>sua sponte … pedibus ibit sententiam</i> renders an idiom of formally \"going over\" to an opinion (as a senator crosses the floor <i>pedibus in sententiam</i>); kept as \"will … go on foot into the true opinion.\"</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> → \"heartiest greeting\" (§5.5). <i>quam optime vale</i> → \"fare as well as may be\" (§5.5, <i>Vale</i>). <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Tuus Totus</i> → \"Yours wholly,\" the subscription.</li><li><b>Heading.</b> <i>AD ANNVM OCTUAGESIMUM NONUM POTIUS PERTINENTES</i> — \"belonging rather to the year eighty-nine\": <b>this is the editor's (Tycho's own 1596) note correcting the letter's placement to 1589</b>, printed as part of the running head. It is translated, not dropped, because it is itself the textual evidence resolving the date.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Cassellis, 6. Calendas Augusti, Anno 1589</i> = \"At Kassel, <i>ante diem VI Kalendas Augusti</i>, 1589.\" Counting inclusively from the Kalends of August (1 Aug.): pridie = 31 July, III Kal. = 30, IV = 29, V = 28, <b>VI Kal. Aug. = 27 July</b>. The date is <b>27 July 1589 (O.S.</b> — Hesse-Kassel kept the Julian calendar at this period; the Protestant German states did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700). Roman-calendar idiom is given with the civil gloss per §3.</li><li><b>Date crux — catalogue vs. source (RESOLVED, flagged).</b> The dataset `label` reads <i>\"1587. 17. januar. Kassel.\"</i> This is contradicted by <b>two</b> internal witnesses: (1) the letter's own running head, <i>ad annum octuagesimum nonum … pertinentes</i> (\"belonging rather to the year eighty-nine\"), and (2) the dateline, <i>Anno 1589</i>. The internal content also fixes 1589: the reference to <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> as a completed work the Landgrave is awaiting, the prior-autumn silence, and the circulation of the disputations to Peucer all belong to mid-1589. Mosley independently dates the letter <b>27 July 1589</b>. The header therefore leads with the as-written date (27 July 1589) and notes the catalogue date; the \"17 January 1587\" label is a catalogue error and is <b>not</b> adopted. Per protocol §3, the discrepancy is recorded and not silently reconciled in the body.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> The `edition` field cites <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri … primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the volume Tycho printed himself; this corresponds to Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI (the <i>Epistolae</i> of the Hesse-Kassel exchange). No manuscript witness is listed in the source.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is secure and the register matched; the date is fully resolved from the source's own running head and dateline (27 July 1589 O.S.) and externally corroborated, with the spurious catalogue date \"1587\" flagged in the apparatus rather than carried into the header.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo is null, one of the 49 letters with no scan). Audited published_english against source_text alone: proper names (Nicodemus Frischlin, Peucer, the unnamed Illustrissimus Princeps = Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel), the Frankfurt fair reference, and the date '6 Calendas Augusti, Anno 1589' (= 27 July, rendered correctly as '[27 July]') all check out, and every clause is translated faithfully with no fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss detected. Grade capped at B per the brief because Dreyer collation was not possible.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15890221002", "next": "brahe-t_15890822001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890221002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891124001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that no reply from Tycho arrived at the Frankfurt fair, explains the Landgrave's pressing concern, and notes Frischlin has arrived with a letter from the Prince.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890806001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890822001", "d": "1589-08-22", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 22 September 1589 [O.S.] (catalogued 22 August 1589)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … (1596 = Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>LITERASA tuas, Nobilissime Excellentissimeque D. TYCHO, quas tu 21. Februarij<br>dederas, vix 17. Augusti accepimus, hoc est, sex diebus post, cum alteras ad me<br>datas scripseras. Quanquam autem varijs iam distrahar occupationibus, tamen<br>paucis tibi respondendum duxi: Praesertim cum videam, te literas meas superiores,<br>forte ob varias occupationes etA etiam morbumA , longe aliter intellexisse, quamA ego scripseram. Obijcis enim ea mihi, quae nec vnquam ex verbis illius<br>Epistolae colligi poterunt, nec etiam mihi vnquam in mentem venerunt. Vbi enim<br>quicquam detraxi authoritati sacrarum literarum? An ea, quae dixi, non etiam tu<br>ipse et TheologiA omnes sacras literas interpretantes dicunt? Nisi forte existimas, duo cum<br>dicant idem, non esse idem: Ideoque in me id reprehendendum esse, quod alijs<br>facile possit concedi. Si Augustinum<br>legeris, multo liberius de sacris literis loquentem inuenies. Eandem nuper<br>sententiam cum exhibuissem Illustrissimo Principi nostro et nostris<br>Theologis viris summis, ne litem hanc facerem meamA (Nam in nostris Obseruationibus ei peculiare caput attribui, vbi ostendi,<br>sacras literas non obstare nostrae de liquiditate materiae caelestis sententiae) nec<br>Princeps nec Theologi eam improbârunt, aut quid reprehendendum in ea inuenerunt.<br>Et tibi idem faciendum erit, si modo quintam tuam Essentiam defendere volueris.<br>Mihi solius DEI gloria in omnibus meis actionibus proposita est: ne quid impium<br>de me cogites. De Crepusculis quoque ne me prorsus caecum effice. Nec enim tam temerarius sum, vt in his rebus quicquam scribam,<br>quod expertus non sim. Scio desinere Crepusculum Sole 17. Gradibus sub<br>Horizontem depresso, si vulgariter Crepusculum pro luce dubia accipias. At ego definiuiA Crepusculum eôusque, donec nullum amplius vestigium albicantis Aëris<br>appareat, quod omnino necessarium est, si extremam superficiem Aëris crassi, qui<br>illuminari potest, determinare exactius volueris. Considera igitur Aërem circa<br>locum occubitus Solis, desinente vulgari Crepusculo, eumque cum alijs locis<br>Horizontis circumcirca, vt ad Ortum, ad Septentrionem, ad MeridiemA confer, diuersitatem coloris et vestigia illa albicantis Aëris omnino<br>reperies. Sed et Refractionibus meis nescio cur detrahas. Quomodo enim alias<br>inuenire possum aut etiam eruere, quam ipsa Instrumenta praebent? An in tuam<br>gratiam hoc faciam? Iniqua certe a me postulas, si hocA postulas. Sed nec titulum Tomorum tuorum de nouis Mundi Aetherei<br>generationibus repraehendi, nec etiam repraehendendus est, nec tua excusatione<br>indiget: Siquidem quae fiunt, etiam generari dicuntur; et reuera Cometae in Caelo<br>generantur. Proposui ea non assertiue, sed dubitanter et interrogatiue potius,<br>praecipue vt explicares mihi sententiam de vestra generatione et corruptione. Quae<br>etiam de Cometarum cauda scripsi, eaA potius tuo iudicio subieci, quam vt vera vrserim. Et Illustrissimus<br>Princeps noster<br>facile excusationem meretur, quoniam tunc temporis vix incipiebat tractare<br>Obseruationes, necdum Stellarum Fixarum loca correxerat, nec erat in<br>Obseruationibus exercitatus, nec velim Obseruationem illam publices. Sed et nec<br>meam de Cometarum generatione sententiam scriptis tuis immisce. Si enim<br>publicandam censuissem, ipse iamdudum publicassem. Pugnares ea rationeA cum muto, siquidem nemo meam istam sententiam adhuc nouit. Postquam mea<br>scripta publica facta fuerint, poteris ea in tuis scriptis pro libito examinareA, siquidem tunc publici iuris erunt: At de nondum editis non est<br>praeiudicium introducendum. Superiore aestate cum de materia StellarumA cogitaui, in aliam sententiam incidi: Inueni etiam, quae de trabibus<br>discurrentibus cap. 7. proposueram, prorsus falsa esse. Quapropter totum illud<br>caput de materia Cometarum eorumque illuminatione sum mutaturus, longeque alia<br>ratione tractaturus, si alium adhuc insuperA Cometam obseruauero, omnique diligentia inspexero et considerauero. De<br>eo, quod Planetae in subtilissimo et purissimo Aëre pendeant, vlterius tecum non<br>agam. Tractaui hanc meam sententiam in opere nostro Stellarum Fixarum, nec te<br>immiscui, sed rem ipsam tractaui. Idem et tibi faciendum erit, vt quibuscunque<br>possis modis sententiam tuam defendas. Iudicabunt postea Doctissimi Mathematici,<br>cuius sententia vera sit. Nec enim nos ipsi in hac materia et Actoris et Rei et<br>Iudicis personam sustinere possumus, sed requiritur ad<br>sententiam pronuntiandam persona tertia, φιλαληθὴς et nullo prorsus praeiudicio et affectuA fascinata. Reuera Mysterium Trinitatis a Deo creaturis inditum est, et<br>reperiuntur in hoc inferiore mundo omnia terrenaA ex Terra, Aqua et Aëre, vt superioribus quoque literis significauiA, seu potius ex Terrena materia, Aqueo glutine et Aëreo spiritu constare;<br>Quam materiam sic dispositam deinde Calor caelestis vel Sol accedens specifica et<br>ingenita sua virtute, luce perfundit, excitat et viuificat. De hominis anima nihil dico, quae est diuinae particula aurae. Sed haec alibi tractaui. Video me tandem apud te obtinuisse, quod<br>Diaphanitas Aëris puri nihil differat a Diaphanitate materiae Planetis<br>circumfusae: Reliquam etiam sententiam sine dubio veram, te docebit veritas<br>temporis filia. De Hypothesibus Ptolemaei<br>et Copernici, vtra vera sit, peculiari<br>olim scripto discutiemus, nec vlteriorem hîc disputationem instituemus. Nec enim Cometa ille Anni 85 in<br>Solis opposito conspectus, quicquam certi hic nos docere poterit, licet<br>motui terrae non sit redditus obnoxius. Si enim motus eius tot gradibus<br>motum reliquorum Planetarum superare potuit, potuit etiam excludere<br>motum terrae annuum. Huc accedit, quod natura motus ex circularibus<br>compositi sit, ut omnia sint πρὸς ἄλληλα, et quomodocunque transponas<br>eccentricos et epicyclos, idem tamen motus eademque a terra distantia<br>suo modo seruato proueniat, quemadmodum ex Copernico lib. 3 Reuol. cap. 20, lib. 4 cap. 3, et lib.<br>5 cap. 4, 22 et alijs locis patet, ut nos alibi tradidimus. Ad<br>hypotheses tuas quod attinet, recte conijcis, generalem illam mundi<br>delineationem Illustrissimo Principi nostro a Thersite illo fuisse exhibitam. Ita<br>enim is cum horologiopaeo nostro hac sua<br>futilitate Celsitud.<br>ipsius in mea absentia (aberam enim peregre) occupauerat,<br>ut cum ego redirem haecque ab Illustrissimo Principe perciperem,<br>satis negotij haberem, ut falsitatem eius Illustrissimo Principi offenderem.<br>Erat enim uera Copernici inuersio mihi satis nota ex meis Astronomicis<br>Elementis, quae nec tunc nec etiam nunc uidit Illustriss. Princeps noster. Ac<br>dixi palam, hominem illum haec ex suo cerebro<br>non habere, siquidem Doctrinam Triangulorum prorsus ignorabat: et cum<br>latitaret se apud te collectorem literarum in tua typographia egisse,<br>dixi haec eum ex nemine alio cognouisse praeterquam ex te. Non tamen<br>persuadere poteram prorsus Illustrissimo Principi nostro haec falsa esse,<br>quanquam omnia et ex Ptolemaeo et<br>ex Copernico adducebam; adeo<br>Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. ab horologiopaeo erat occupata. Constructo tamen tandem eo<br>Instrumento, quod homuncio ille in eo scripto delineauit, ad oculum et<br>Illustriss. Princeps et Horologiopaeus uidit, eas hypotheses ueras esse non<br>posse, praesertim ex Marte. Assumebant enim tantum eccentriculum Solis,<br>inque eo eccentricos reliquorum Planetarum circumducebant, omissa<br>aequantium ratione, seu, si secundum Copernicum loquendum est, omissis<br>prorsus illis circellis. Nec homo ille sciebat rationes et quantitates<br>diametrorum et eccentricitatum, sed haec ex Copernico desumebat Horologiopaeus noster, qui satis coram Illustrissimo<br>Principe ex<br>me audiebat, quomodo res tractanda esset. Nec ego quicquam de ijs<br>dixissem, nisi mei officij fuisset, Illustrissimum Principem meum ab<br>approbatione (petebat enim ab Illustrissima ipsius Cels.<br>testimonium approbationis impudentissimus ille) ab approbatione inquam<br>falsae rei retrahere. Et quotiescunque colloquebar cum Illustriss.<br>ipius<br>Celsit., semper aderat etiam Horologiopaeus, qui hominis partes agebat. Tantum enim in<br>mea absentia homo ille ad Illustrissimum Principem erat admissus: postea<br>uero nunquam amplius admittebatur, ob quam causam etiam in praefatione<br>tecte in me inuehitur. Et quomodo poteram illum commendare Illustrissimo<br>Principi<br>nostro, cum nec eam ipsam delineationem nec quicquam admodum de hac re<br>intelligerit. Nec tamen rite Horologiopaeus noster ex me perceperat, quomodo res<br>tractanda esset: uerum post hominis illius discessum explicaui<br>Illustrissimo Principi, quomodo omnia data et rationes diametrorum in<br>circulis ex Copernico essent<br>assumenda transponendaque, quae tunc facile arripiebat Horologiopaeus, cui Copernicum in germanicum sermonem transtulerat utcunque<br>Vrsus ille. Ita postea curabat sibi<br>Illustrissimus Princeps noster per Horologiopaeum confici automaton<br>perfectissimum, omnium Planetarum motum secundum calculum Copernici<br>perfecte exhibens, de quo etiam superioribus literis scripsi. Nam illud,<br>quod delineauit homo ille in scripto suo, nullius est momenti, nec hic<br>reliquit sed secum abstulit, pro eius fabrica interpretans HorologiopaeoCopernicum, a quo etiam mea<br>compendiaA quoquo modo<br>cognouit, et in illo scripto manifestissima maleuolentia Horologiopaeo homini illiterato et idiotae<br>tribuit, quem tamen ego primus demonstrationem 47 p. 1 Eucl. docui, iam<br>mercedem anguinam reportans. Obliquitatem illam Zodiaci horologiopaeus<br>noster ex meis obseruationibus transcriptam etiam in secundis scrupulis<br>ipsi transmisit: ut et alia ex meis obseruationibus collutione Horologiopaei surrepta sunt. Sed nolo cum<br>impuro illo et Horologiopaeo nostro rem<br>habere: Doctissimi uiri facile iudicabunt, unde fluxerint pleraque, et<br>quid cuique tribuendum sit. Quod nomen meum<br>omisit, rem mihi pergratam fecit. Si tibi uidebitur, poteris ab ipso<br>hypotheses tuas uindicare. Vix credere potes, quomodo et te et me et<br>miserum illum Ptolemaeum miserumque Copernicum et omnes doctos conuituiis<br>peterent stupidosque et nihil intelligentes asinos appellarent illi<br>homines duo: soles se mundi lumina iactantes: adeo homine illiterato et<br>uix prima initia intelligente nihil quicquam est intolerabilius aut<br>iniustius. Sed haec nobis sunt pro nihilo habenda et contemnenda.<br>Perscripsi tamen hac de re superiore Mense Maio ad amicum meum singularem<br>uirum in quocunque doctrinarum genere et excellentissimum et<br>uersatissimum D. Ioannem Dee, ipsique significaui, quod non solum mea<br>Horologiopaeo illiterato et idiotae<br>tribuerit, uerumetiam tibi systema illud mundi surripuerit: iam astute<br>doctorum aucupia quaerens, quos tamen antea Vatiniano prorsus odio cum<br>Horologiopaeo prosecutus esset.<br>Omnis Aristippum decuit color et status et res. Scripsi etiam eum reuera<br>talem esse, qualem se in praefatione descripsit. Sed nolo cum isto<br>homine, ut dixi, rem habere, nec dignus est, ut nomen ipsius scriptis<br>nostris inferatur. Facile uidebunt docti, ex te eum illud systema<br>habere, siquidem ipse fatetur, se doctrinam Triangulorum ex Horologiopaeo nostro didicisse. Quomodo autem<br>sine doctrina Triangulorum de systemate illo et de ordinatione circulorum caelestium<br>deque eorum quantitate quicquam scire potuit? Ipse<br>quoque in Elementis meis Astronomicis tibi illud systema tribuam, ut et<br>Instrumentorum nostrorum rationem tibi in opere nostro stellarum fixarum<br>restitui. Sed de hoc satis. <br>Habebat in animo Illustriss. Princeps noster tibi ad<br>literas tuas respondere; Verum subita festinatione iter ingrediens Smalcaldiam petijt, vt hac ratione responsio<br>sit impedita. Quemadmodum et eo circa hoc tempus maxime impeditur, quod iam<br>operam det ceruis, qui rem Veneream sectantur, figendis. Nihilominus tamen<br>Illustriss. ipsius Celsitudinem a te literas expectare et de te saepe cogitare,<br>ex iniecta literarum copia poteris intelligere, quas praeterito mense Iulio ex<br>comitatu Hombergico Illustriss. ipsius<br>Celsitud. ad me dedit: Secundum quarum tenorem et Mandatum Frischlino quoque literas ad te perferendas<br>tradidi. Quae proximis literis ad me scripsisti de structuris et occupationibus<br>tuis aestiualibus, quod nimirum Molendinam ad conficiendam chartam construxeris,<br>Illustrissimo Principi<br>meo indicaui, cui res perplacebat. Literas illas, quas ad Gellium Sasceridem scripseras, nuper tradidi<br>Praeceptori Nobiliss. ROSENCRANTZII,<br>qui me et ex te et ex Nobiliss. D. DEEA, amico meo singulari, perquam humaniter salutabat, seque<br>cum alijs ad eundem Gellium literis has quoque missurum aut laturum spondebat.<br>Nuper D. VICTORINVS SCHONFELDT, mathematum apud Marpurgenses professorA, per CHRISTOPHORVM HARSACHIVM,<br>Illustrissimi Principis<br>nostri Consiliarium a me petijt, vt Filium suum tibi commendarem, atque ex te<br>cognoscas rem, num posses illum inter studiosos tuos aliquamdiu recipere et in<br>his artibus erudire; Sique hoc tibi videretur, se eum sequente aestate ad te missurum. Si igitur tibi hoc commodum est, poteris voluntatem tuam ad proximas<br>Nundinas mihi declarare, vt sciat quid sibi agendum sit. Haec iam summa<br>festinatione varijs distractus occupationibus breuiter respondere volui, quae vt<br>in optimam partem accipias etiam atque etiam oro. Plura scribam, cum occasionem<br>et otium nactus fuero.Vale igitur Nobilissime Excellentissimeque D.<br>TYCHO, meque quod facis ama.Cassellis 22. Septemb., Anno 89.T. T.CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p>Your letter, Most Noble and Most Excellent Master Tycho, which you had despatched on 21 February, we received scarcely on 17 August — that is, six days after you had written and sent to me the other one. But although I am now pulled this way and that by various occupations, I judged that I should answer you in a few words: especially since I see that you have understood my earlier letter — perhaps on account of your various occupations and also your illness — far otherwise than I had written it. For you charge me with things that can never be gathered from the words of that letter, and that never even entered my mind. For where did I detract anything from the authority of the sacred Scriptures? Are not the things I said said also by you yourself, and by all the theologians who interpret the sacred Scriptures? Unless perhaps you reckon that, when two say the same thing, it is not the same thing — and that therefore that is to be censured in me which could readily be granted to others. If you read Augustine, you will find him speaking far more freely about the sacred Scriptures. When I lately set out the same opinion before our Most Illustrious Prince and our theologians, men of the highest rank — lest I make this quarrel my own (for in our [book on the] Observations I assigned it a chapter of its own, where I showed that the sacred Scriptures do not stand in the way of our opinion concerning the fluidity of the celestial matter) — neither the Prince nor the theologians disapproved it, nor did they find anything in it to be censured. And you too will have to do the same, if only you wish to defend that quintessence of yours. The glory of God alone is set before me in all my actions: do not think anything impious of me. About the twilights, too, do not make me out to be quite blind. For I am not so rash as to write anything in these matters that I have not tested by experience. I know that twilight ends when the Sun is sunk 17 degrees below the horizon, if you take \"twilight\" in the common sense for the doubtful light. But I defined twilight [as lasting] up to the point where no further trace of whitening air appears — which is altogether necessary, if you wish to determine more exactly the outermost surface of the dense air that can be illuminated. Consider, then, the air around the place of the Sun's setting, once the common twilight has ended, and compare it with the other places of the horizon round about — as toward the East, toward the North, toward the South — and you will quite certainly find a difference of colour and those traces of whitening air. But I do not know why you also detract from my refractions. For how else can I find them, or even extract them, than as the instruments themselves furnish? Am I to do this to please you? You demand of me what is surely unjust, if you demand this. But I have not faulted the title of your volumes \"On the new generations of the aetherial world,\" nor is it to be faulted, nor does it need your excuse: since the things that come into being are also said to be generated; and comets are indeed generated in the heavens. I put those things forward not assertively, but rather doubtfully and by way of question, chiefly so that you might explain to me your opinion concerning your generation and corruption. The things I also wrote about the comet's tail, those too I rather submitted to your judgement than urged as true. And our Most Illustrious Prince readily deserves to be excused, since at that time he was scarcely beginning to deal with the observations, had not yet corrected the places of the fixed stars, nor was he practised in observations — and I should not wish you to publish that observation. But neither should you mix my opinion concerning the generation of comets into your writings. For if I had judged it should be published, I should myself have published it long ago. You would be fighting by that argument with one who is dumb, since no one yet knows that opinion of mine. After my writings have been made public, you will be able to examine them in your writings at your pleasure, since they will then be common property; but concerning things not yet published no prejudgement is to be brought in. Last summer, when I reflected on the matter of the stars, I fell into a different opinion: I found, too, that the things I had put forward in chapter 7 about the running beams are utterly false. Wherefore I am going to alter that whole chapter on the matter of comets and their illumination, and to treat it on a far different principle, if I shall yet observe still another comet besides, and inspect and consider it with all diligence. As for that the planets hang in the most subtle and purest air, I will not deal with you any further. I have treated this opinion of mine in our work on the Fixed Stars, and have not mixed you in, but have treated the matter itself. The same you too will have to do, namely to defend your opinion by whatever means you can. The most learned mathematicians will judge afterwards whose opinion is the true one. For we ourselves cannot in this matter sustain at once the part of plaintiff and of defendant and of judge; rather, for pronouncing sentence a third person is required, a lover of truth [φιλαληθὴς] and bewitched by no prejudice and affection whatever. The Mystery of the Trinity has truly been imparted by God to created things, and in this lower world all earthly things are found to consist of Earth, Water, and Air — as I signified also in my earlier letters — or rather of an earthy matter, a watery glue, and an airy spirit; and this matter, thus disposed, the celestial Heat or the Sun, approaching, then floods with light by its own specific and inborn virtue, rouses and quickens. Of the soul of man I say nothing, which is a particle of the divine breath. But these things I have treated elsewhere. I see that I have at last won this point with you, that the transparency of the pure air differs in nothing from the transparency of the matter poured around the planets: as for the remaining opinion, also without doubt true, truth, the daughter of time, will teach it you. Concerning the hypotheses of Ptolemy and of Copernicus, which of the two is true, we shall one day discuss in a writing of its own, nor will we set up any further disputation here. For neither will that comet of the year [15]85, seen in opposition to the Sun, be able to teach us anything certain here, even though it was not rendered subject to the motion of the earth. For if its motion could surpass the motion of the other planets by so many degrees, it could also exclude the annual motion of the earth. To this is added that the nature of motion is compounded of circular motions, so that all are in relation to one another [πρὸς ἄλληλα], and however you transpose the eccentrics and epicycles, yet the same motion and the same distance from the earth comes out, its own manner being kept — as is clear from Copernicus, Book 3 of the Revolutions, chapter 20, Book 4 chapter 3, and Book 5 chapters 4, 22, and in other places, as we have set forth elsewhere. As regards your hypotheses, you rightly conjecture that that general delineation of the world was displayed to our Most Illustrious Prince by that Thersites. For so had he, together with our clockmaker, by this triviality of his occupied His Highness in my absence (for I was away abroad on travels), that when I returned and learned these things from the Most Illustrious Prince, I had business enough to expose its falsity to the Most Illustrious Prince. For the true inversion of Copernicus was well enough known to me from my Astronomical Elements, which neither then nor even now has our Most Illustrious Prince seen. And I said openly that that man did not have these things out of his own brain, since he was utterly ignorant of the Doctrine of Triangles [trigonometry]; and since it lay hidden that he had acted as a collector of letters [a typesetter] in your printing-house with you, I said he had learned these things from no one else but you. Yet I could not altogether persuade our Most Illustrious Prince that these things were false, although I adduced everything both from Ptolemy and from Copernicus — so much had His own Highness been occupied by the clockmaker. Yet at last, when that instrument had been constructed which that little man had delineated in that writing, both the Most Illustrious Prince and the clockmaker saw with their own eyes that those hypotheses could not be true, especially from Mars. For they assumed only a little eccentric of the Sun, and within it carried round the eccentrics of the remaining planets, the reckoning of the equants being omitted — or, if one is to speak according to Copernicus, those little circles being utterly omitted. Nor did that man know the ratios and quantities of the diameters and eccentricities, but our clockmaker took these from Copernicus, who heard well enough from me, in the presence of the Most Illustrious Prince, how the matter was to be handled. Nor should I have said anything about these things, had it not been my duty to draw back my Most Illustrious Prince from approval (for that most shameless man was seeking from His own Highness a testimonial of approval) — from approval, I say, of a false thing. And as often as I conferred with His own Highness, the clockmaker too was always present, who played the man's part. For only in my absence had that man been admitted to the Most Illustrious Prince; but afterwards he was never admitted again, for which cause he also covertly inveighs against me in his preface. And how could I commend him to our Most Illustrious Prince, when he understood neither that delineation itself nor anything much about this matter? Nor yet had our clockmaker rightly grasped from me how the matter was to be handled; but after that man's departure I explained to the Most Illustrious Prince how all the data and the ratios of the diameters in the circles were to be assumed and transposed from Copernicus — which the clockmaker then readily seized upon, to whom that Ursus had translated Copernicus into the German tongue after a fashion. So afterwards our Most Illustrious Prince had made for himself, through the clockmaker, a most perfect automaton, perfectly exhibiting the motion of all the planets according to the calculation of Copernicus, about which I also wrote in my earlier letters. For that which that man delineated in his writing is of no moment, and he did not leave it here but carried it off with him, interpreting Copernicus for the clockmaker for his construction — from whom too [the clockmaker] he learned my compendious methods in some way or other; and in that writing, with the most manifest malevolence, he attributes [them] to the clockmaker, an unlettered man and an idiot, whom nevertheless I was the first to teach the demonstration of Euclid I.47, now reaping a serpent's wage [for it]. That obliquity of the Zodiac, transcribed from my observations, our clockmaker transmitted to him even down to the seconds; just as other things too were filched from my observations through the clockmaker's collusion. But I will have nothing to do with that impure fellow and our clockmaker: learned men will easily judge whence most things flowed, and what is to be attributed to each. That he omitted my name, he did me a most welcome service. If it shall seem good to you, you will be able to vindicate your hypotheses from him. You can scarcely believe how those two men would assail with insults both you and me and that wretched Ptolemy and wretched Copernicus and all the learned, and call [them] stupid asses understanding nothing — vaunting themselves as the suns and lights of the world: so that nothing whatever is more intolerable or more unjust than an unlettered man who scarcely understands the first rudiments. But these things are to be reckoned by us as nothing and to be despised. I have nevertheless written fully about this matter, last May, to my singular friend, a man in every kind of learning both most excellent and most versed, Master John Dee, and signified to him that he [Ursus] not only attributed my [work] to an unlettered clockmaker and idiot, but had even filched from you that system of the world: now craftily seeking to snare the learned, whom nevertheless he had before pursued, together with the clockmaker, with a thoroughly Vatinian hatred. \"Every complexion and condition and circumstance became Aristippus.\" I wrote also that he really is such as he described himself in his preface. But I will have nothing to do, as I said, with that man, nor is he worthy that his name be brought into our writings. The learned will easily see that he has that system from you, since he himself confesses that he learned the Doctrine of Triangles from our clockmaker. But how, without the Doctrine of Triangles, could he have known anything about that system and the ordering of the celestial circles and their quantity? I too shall attribute that system to you in my Astronomical Elements, just as I have also restored to you the design of our instruments in our work on the fixed stars. But enough of this. Our Most Illustrious Prince had it in mind to reply to your letter; but, setting out on a journey in sudden haste, he made for Schmalkalden, so that by this circumstance the reply has been hindered. Just as he is also greatly hindered around this time by the fact that he is now devoting his pains to spearing the harts that pursue the work of Venus [are in rut]. Nevertheless, that His own Highness awaits a letter from you and often thinks of you, you will be able to gather from the enclosed bundle of letters which His own Highness gave to me last July from the county of Homberg; according to the tenor and command of which I also handed to Frischlin letters to be conveyed to you. What you wrote to me in your latest letter about your buildings and your summer occupations — namely that you have built a mill for making paper — I reported to my Most Illustrious Prince, to whom the matter was very pleasing. Those letters which you had written to Gellius Sascerides I lately handed to the tutor of the most noble Rosenkrantz, who greeted me most kindly both on your behalf and on behalf of the most noble Master Dee, my singular friend, and undertook that he would send or carry these too, along with other letters, to the same Gellius. Lately Master Victorinus Schönfeld, professor of mathematics among the Marburgers, asked of me, through Christoph Harsach, a councillor of our Most Illustrious Prince, that I commend his son to you, and [asked] that you learn the matter from him — whether you could receive him for some time among your students and instruct him in these arts; and if this seemed good to you, that he would send him to you next summer. If, therefore, this is convenient to you, you will be able to declare your will to me by the next Fair, so that he may know what he is to do. These things, now in greatest haste, distracted by various occupations, I wished to answer briefly; and I pray you again and again that you take them in the best part. I shall write more, when I have got the occasion and the leisure. Farewell, then, Most Noble and Most Excellent Master Tycho, and love me as you do. At Kassel, 22 September, the year [15]89. Yours wholly, Christoph Rothmann.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), mathematician-astronomer at the court of Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel; Tycho's chief correspondent on observational and cosmological method. This is one of the more pointed letters in the exchange: Rothmann defends himself against Tycho's charges (on Scripture and the fluidity of the heavens, on twilight, on refractions) and then narrates the <b>Ursus affair</b> at length.</li><li><b>Recipient / persons.</b></li><li><b><i>Illustrissimus Princeps noster</b></i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Rothmann's patron.</li><li><b><i>Thersites … Ursus … horologiopaeus … homo ille / homuncio</b></i> — the central polemic. <b>Ursus</b> is <b>Nicolaus Reimers Baer</b> (Nicolaus Raimarus Ursus, c. 1551–1600), whom Rothmann brands \"that Thersites\" (Homer's lowborn railer, <i>Iliad</i> 2) and the \"little man / fellow.\" The <b><i>horologiopaeus</b></i> (\"clockmaker,\" from Greek <i>hōrológion</i> + <i>-poios</i>) is Wilhelm IV's instrument-/clock-maker <b>Jost Bürgi</b>, here Ursus's collaborator in presenting a \"world-system\" delineation to the Landgrave. Rothmann's allegation — that Ursus had worked as a typesetter (<i>collectorem literarum</i>) in Tycho's Uraniborg printing-house and there pilfered Tycho's geo-heliocentric system — is the documentary seed of the Tycho–Ursus priority quarrel that culminated in Tycho's <i>Apologia</i> and Kepler's <i>Apologia Tychonis contra Ursum</i>. <b>Identification flagged:</b> the letter never names Ursus except as \"Thersites\"/\"Ursus\"; \"Bürgi\" is supplied editorially and is not in the Latin.</li><li><b><i>D. Ioannem Dee / D. DEE</b></i> = <b>John Dee</b> (1527–1608/9), to whom Rothmann says he wrote in May denouncing Ursus — a notable datum for Dee's continental network in the late 1580s.</li><li><b><i>Gellius Sascerides</b></i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (1562–1612), Danish physician-astronomer, Tycho's former assistant on Hven.</li><li><b><i>Praeceptori … ROSENCRANTZII</b></i> = the tutor of a young <b>Rosenkrantz</b> (Danish noble family) then travelling; not further specified in the text.</li><li><b><i>Victorinus Schonfeldt</b></i> = <b>Victorinus Schönfeld</b> (1525–1591), professor of mathematics at Marburg; he seeks a place for his son among Tycho's students. <b><i>Christophorus Harsachius</b></i> = <b>Christoph Harsach (Harssach)</b>, a councillor of the Landgrave. <b><i>Frischlino</b></i> = (likely) <b>Nicodemus Frischlin</b> or a courier of that name; identification not secured by the text.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Cassellis</i> = <b>Kassel</b> (§5.7). <i>Smalcaldia</i> = <b>Schmalkalden</b>. <i>comitatu Hombergico</i> = the <b>county/district of Homberg</b> (Hesse). <i>Marpurgenses</i> = <b>Marburg</b>. <i>Uraniborg / typographia tua</i> implied — Tycho's printing-house on Hven.</li><li><b>Technical terms (per controlled glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>Crepusculum</i> → <b>twilight</b>; <i>Refractiones</i> → <b>refractions</b>; <i>eccentricos / epicyclos / eccentriculum / aequantium ratione / circellos</i> → <b>eccentrics / epicycles / little eccentric / the reckoning of the equants / little circles</b>; <i>Obliquitas Zodiaci</i> → <b>obliquity of the Zodiac</b>; <i>in secundis scrupulis</i> → <b>down to the seconds</b> (of arc); <i>Stellarum Fixarum loca</i> → <b>the places of the fixed stars</b>; <i>Instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b>; <i>Doctrina Triangulorum</i> → <b>the Doctrine of Triangles</b> (= trigonometry; kept literal with gloss). <i>liquiditate materiae caelestis</i> → <b>the fluidity of the celestial matter</b> (Rothmann's fluid-heavens doctrine). <i>quinta Essentia</i> → <b>quintessence</b> (Tycho's retained Aristotelian fifth element, which Rothmann challenges). <i>Cometa Anni 85</i> = the <b>comet of 1585</b>.</li><li><b>Astronomical-source citations.</b> <i>Copernico lib. 3 Reuol. cap. 20, lib. 4 cap. 3, et lib. 5 cap. 4, 22</i> = Copernicus, <i>De revolutionibus</i>, Book 3 ch. 20, Book 4 ch. 3, Book 5 chs. 4 and 22 — given as Rothmann wrote them, untranslated as references.</li><li><b>Works named.</b> <i>Tomorum tuorum de nouis Mundi Aetherei generationibus</i> = Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), here paraphrased by Rothmann as \"your volumes on the new generations of the aetherial world.\" <i>nostris Obseruationibus / opere nostro Stellarum Fixarum / Elementa mea Astronomica</i> = Rothmann's own (largely unpublished) works on observations, the fixed stars, and astronomical elements. <i>demonstrationem 47 p. 1 Eucl.</i> = <b>Euclid, Elements I.47</b> (the Pythagorean theorem).</li><li><b>Greek (transcribed and translated).</b></li><li><i>φιλαληθὴς</i> (philalēthḗs) = \"lover of truth,\" qualifying the disinterested third-party judge required to settle a scientific dispute.</li><li><i>πρὸς ἄλληλα</i> (pròs állēla) = \"in relation to one another\" / \"mutually\" — that the circular components of planetary motion are interrelated, so eccentric/epicycle transpositions preserve the same motion and distance.</li><li><b>Allusions.</b></li><li><i>diuinae particula aurae</i> = <b>Horace, Satires II.2.79</b> (\"a particle of the divine breath\"), of the human soul.</li><li><i>Omnis Aristippum decuit color et status et res</i> = <b>Horace, Epistles I.17.23</b> (\"Every complexion, condition, and circumstance became Aristippus\") — kept as the quoted sententia, applied ironically to Ursus's chameleon adaptability.</li><li><i>Vatiniano … odio</i> = \"Vatinian hatred,\" proverbial extreme hatred (after Catullus / Cicero on P. Vatinius).</li><li><i>mercedem anguinam</i> = \"a serpent's wage\" — the proverbial ingratitude (a snake biting its benefactor) Rothmann says he got for teaching the clockmaker.</li><li><i>ceruis … qui rem Veneream sectantur, figendis</i> — a courtly euphemism: the Landgrave is busy \"staking out\" (hunting) the harts in rut; rendered with the period decorum kept.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (§5.5).</b> Opening <i>LITERAS tuas … accepimus</i> (no salutation rubric in this printed text). Closing <i>Vale … Cassellis 22. Septemb., Anno 89. T.T.</i> — <i>Vale</i> → <b>Farewell</b>; <b>T.T.</b> = <i>Tuus Totus</i> (\"Yours wholly\"), expanded in translation.</li><li><b>Calendar / DATING CRUX (flagged, not reconciled).</b> The dateline as printed reads <b>\"Cassellis 22. Septemb., Anno 89\"</b> = <b>22 September 1589 (O.S.;</b> Hesse-Kassel kept the Julian calendar at this date<b>)</b>. The dataset's catalogue <b>`label` field reads \"1589. 22. august. Kassel\"</b> — i.e. <b>22 August</b>, one month earlier, contradicting the letter's own dateline. Per protocol §3/§4 the header leads with the <b>as-written September date</b> and appends the catalogued August date; the discrepancy is recorded, not resolved. The August reading is unsupported by the source text and is most plausibly a catalogue slip (or a confusion with the \"17. Augusti\" receipt-date that opens the letter). Internal date data are consistent with September: Rothmann says he received Tycho's 21 February letter \"scarcely on 17 August,\" reports writing to Dee \"last May,\" and refers to letters the Landgrave gave him \"last July\" — all anterior to a September composition.</li><li><b>Source-text artefacts (working transcription).</b> The verbatim Source block reproduces the local working transcription (harvested from the modern critical edition, normalising the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i>). Stray superscript-<b>A</b> tokens appended to words (<i>LITERASA, etA, morbumA, quamA, TheologiA, meamA, definiuiA, MeridiemA, hocA, eaA, rationeA, examinareA, StellarumA, insuperA, affectuA, terrenaA, significauiA, aurae.A, compendiaA, satis. A, DEEA, professorA, missurum. A, Septemb.A</i>) are <b>editorial footnote/variant anchors</b> carried over from the source apparatus, <b>not</b> part of Rothmann's Latin; they are left in the Source block (protocol §1/§4 forbids silent alteration of the transcription) and are ignored in the translation. Likewise the run-together <i>HorologiopaeoCopernicum</i>, the variant spelling <i>ipius</i> for <i>ipsius</i>, and the compressed closing <i>…ama.Cassellis…Anno 89.T. T.CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</i> are transcription artefacts; word-division is restored only in the translation.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The dense polemical clauses were checked for negation count: e.g. <i>Vbi enim quicquam detraxi…?</i> (rhetorical \"where did I detract anything…?\"); <i>ne quid impium de me cogites</i> (\"do not think anything impious of me\"); <i>ne me prorsus caecum effice</i> (\"do not make me out quite blind\"); <i>nec velim Obseruationem illam publices</i> / <i>nec … sententiam scriptis tuis immisce</i> (two distinct prohibitions: \"I should not wish you to publish that observation\" and \"do not mix my opinion into your writings\"); <i>nec dignus est, ut nomen ipsius scriptis nostris inferatur</i> (\"nor is he worthy that his name be brought into our writings\"). Each negation in the source is reproduced once in the translation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two low-severity translation fixes: 'de vestra generatione et corruptione' restored to 'your generation and corruption' (2nd-person 'vestra', not 3rd-person 'their [the comets']'); 'ceruis...figendis' restored to 'spearing' (the gerundive of figere, active spearing/shooting) in place of 'staking out'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the Greek transcribed and translated; register and the author's hedges (<i>dubitanter et interrogatiue</i>, <i>quoquo modo</i>, <i>utcunque</i>) are preserved. The one substantive issue is the <b>September-vs-August dating crux</b> (header dateline vs. catalogue `label`), flagged above and resolved in favour of the source's own \"22. Septemb.\" per protocol. Minor unresolved points (not affecting sense): the precise identity of <i>Frischlino</i> and of the <i>Praeceptor Rosencrantzii</i>, and the editorial supply of \"Bürgi\" for <i>horologiopaeus</i>.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief this is capped at grade B and audited against source_text alone (no Dreyer collation performed). Full clause-by-clause collation of published_english against source_text: every proper name (Augustine, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Ursus, the unnamed clockmaker/Horologiopaeus, Gellius Sascerides, Rosenkrantz, John Dee, Frischlin, Victorinus Schonfeldt, Christoph Harsach), place-name (Kassel/Cassellis, Schmalkalden/Smalcaldiam, Homberg/Hombergico, Marburg/Marpurgenses), date (21 February, 17 August, 22 September anno 89, last May, last July), and number (six days, 17 degrees, chapter 7, comet of [15]85, Copernicus lib.3 cap.20/lib.4 cap.3/lib.5 cap.4,22, Euclid I.47) checks out against the Latin with no fabrication, omission, or hardened hedge found. No sentence found silently dropped; the closing valediction and signature match exactly with no fabricated addition. Two low-severity pronoun/verb-sense mistranslations found (listed above); neither changes what a scholar would take away from the letter's substance.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"vestra\" is a 2nd-person possessive (\"your\") consistent with Rothmann's tu/tuus address of Tycho throughout the letter; the translation silently recasts it as 3rd-person \"their [the comets']\", shifting whose opinion is being solicited. At: “chiefly so that you might explain to me your opinion concerning their [the comets'] generation and corruption”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"figendis\" (gerundive of figere, to pierce/transfix/shoot) denotes active spearing or shooting of the rutting stags, i.e. hunting them; \"staking out\" implies passive surveillance rather than the kill, softening the verb's sense. At: “he is now devoting his pains to staking out the harts that pursue the work of Venus [are in rut]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was available for this letter (tboo: null), so no TEXT_ERROR-type claim could be checked against the edition of record; there were none to check. Both defects are MISTRANSLATION-type and were verified from the letter's own current_latin, which is internal evidence independent of Dreyer: 'vestra' is unambiguously a 2nd-person possessive (contrasts with 3rd-person eorum/sua) and 'figendis' is the gerundive of figere (to spear/transfix), not a verb of passive watching. Both fixes are grammatically certain from the source text as published. Nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15890806001", "next": "brahe-t_15891124001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890221002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891124001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Defends his previous letter against Tycho's misreading, denying he detracted from Scripture's authority, and cites Augustine as a precedent for speaking freely on sacred texts.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890822001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890920001", "d": "1589-09-20", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "[In the name of] King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 20 September 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: <b>Danish</b> (chancery; the dataset's automatic tag \"German\" is incorrect — see note) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XVII, 208 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF,</p><p>at hand her epter schulde holde thend løckte paa Kulden bedre wed mackt end til theß scheed er, saa der ingen klagemaall aff søefarende folck der offuer kommer.</p><p>Actum Haffniae 20. 7bris a:o 1589.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter,</b></p><p>that he should hereafter keep the beacon on Kullen better maintained than has been done hitherto, so that no complaint from seafaring folk arises over it.</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, 20 September, in the year 1589.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> Not a letter <i>text</i> but a <b>chancery-register entry</b> (Sjællandske Tegnelser — the \"Zealand registers\" of outgoing crown letters): the register records, in the third person, that \"Tyge Brahe received a letter [to the effect] that…\". The translation keeps that register form rather than recasting it as direct address.</li><li><b>Issuer.</b> Entered as from <b>Christian IV</b>, but in 1588–89 the king was a child (b. 1577); the realm was governed by the <b>Regency Council (rigsråd)</b> until 1596. The letter therefore issues <i>in the young king's name</i> through the chancery — hence the impersonal register form. Rendered \"[In the name of] King Christian IV\" in the header.</li><li><b><i>thend løckte paa Kulden</b></i> — \"the beacon [lantern] on <b>Kullen</b>\": the light on the Kullen headland (Skåne), whose upkeep was a duty attached to Tycho's crown holdings. <i>løckte</i> = <i>lygte</i> (lantern/light); <i>paa Kulden</i> = \"on Kullen.\" This is the documentary basis for the well-known friction over Tycho's neglect of the Kullen light.</li><li><b>Orthography / language note.</b> The text is <b>Danish</b>, in late-16th-c. chancery spelling whose German-style features (<i>sch-, w-, -ck-, theß</i>) caused the project's automatic language heuristic to mis-tag it \"German.\" Verified by reading: <i>hand</i> = <i>han</i> (he), <i>her epter</i> = <i>herefter</i>, <i>schulde</i> = <i>skulle</i>, <i>wed mackt</i> = <i>ved magt</i> (\"in repair/maintained\"), <i>klagemaall</i> = <i>klagemål</i> (complaint), <i>aff søefarende folck</i> = <i>af søfarende folk</i> (by seafaring folk). Recorded as a Phase-C correction (protocol §7).</li><li><b>Date crux — RESOLVED (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 50 (item 77; <i>Sjællandske Tegnelser</i> XVII, 208), who dates and datelines the letter <b>1589</b>; the chancery register sequence (items 77–79, all 1589) confirms it. The working transcription's <i>a:o 1588</i> was a misreading of the register's <b>1589</b>, corrected here in the Source block and translation. (<i>7bris</i> = September, the seventh month counting from March.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the translation of the Danish is secure, and the former dating conflict is <b>resolved (2026-06-02) by Dreyer collation</b>: <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 50 (item 77, <i>Sjællandske Tegnelser</i> XVII, 208) both dates and datelines the letter <b>1589</b>, and the chancery sequence (items 77–79, all 1589) confirms it. The working transcription's <i>1588</i> was a misreading, now corrected to <b>1589</b>; no residual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.49-51 (page images). The royal Danish letter is printed complete on p.50 under 'TYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF', matching source_text word-for-word, dated 'Actum Haffniae 20. 7bris a:o 1589' (7bris = September, matching metadata 1589-09-20). Checked the place-name (Kulden/Kullen beacon) and the full sense (maintenance order, complaint clause) against the translation — accurate throughout, no fabrication or omission.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XVII, 208."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15890925001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Orders Tycho to maintain the beacon on Kullen more diligently, as seafarers have been complaining about its upkeep.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15890925001", "d": "1589-09-25", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 25 September 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= vol. XIV, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XVII, 210 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TILL TYGE BRAHE NOGEN WOX ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR</p><p>Christian 4. etc.</p><p>WOR gunst tilfornn. Wiid, att thenne breffuißer Thomes Loeding haffuer wnderdanigst tilkiende giiffuit, huorledis for nogen tiid siiden wdj a:o 87 er st[r]andit ett schiib paa Anholt, som haffuer tilhørt nogen kiøbmend aff Engelland, huor wdj haffuer werrit eblant andit guodtz noget wox, aff huilcket en part schal werre strandit och bierget wed Wardbierg, en part wed Laugholm, och ett støcke ther for Hueen schal werre indkommen, huilcket du til thig schal haffue anammit, och fornemme Thometz Loeding paa for:ne engelsche kiøbmends wegne haffuer wnderdanigst werrit begierendis, hannom samme wox motte betalis och fornøyes. Tha epterdj att alt huis wrag, som saaledis her vdj riigit nogen stedts kand strande och indkomme for Wor och cronens grund, tilkommer Oß och cronen allenne, bede Wij thig och wille, attu thig ther om emoed Wor elsk:e tilforornede regierings raad erklerer, epter att Wij slige høighet wille Oß allenne forbeholdit haffue, hannom ochsaa samme wox, som du wdj saa maade haffuer ladet anamme, betaler och fornøyer, att hand sig wiidere for Oß icke schal haffue sig at beklage.</p><p>Ther met etc.<br>Actum Haffniae 25 septem: a:o 1589.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tyge Brahe, concerning certain wax, as follows:</b></p><p>Christian IV, etc.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that this bearer-of-the-letter, Thomes Loeding, has most humbly made known how, some time since, in the year [15]87, a ship was wrecked off Anholt which had belonged to certain merchants of England, in which there was, among other goods, some wax — of which one part is said to have been wrecked and salvaged near Varberg, one part near Laholm, and a piece [of which] is said to have come in there off Hven, which you are said to have taken in to yourself; and, as we understand, Thomes Loeding, on behalf of the aforesaid English merchants, has most humbly been requesting that the same wax be paid for and made good to him. Then, inasmuch as all such wreck as may in this manner be stranded and come in anywhere here within the realm upon our and the crown's ground pertains to us and the crown alone, we ask and will of you that you make declaration of yourself concerning this before our beloved appointed Council of the Regency — since we will have reserved such prerogative to ourselves alone — [and] that you also pay and make good to him the same wax, which you have in such manner caused to be taken in, so that he shall not have [cause] to complain of you to us any further.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Copenhagen, 25 September, in the year 1589.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (1577–1648), at this date eleven years old and still a minor; he had been elected king-designate in 1580 and acclaimed in 1584, but did not assume personal rule until 1596. Letters issued in his name during 1588–96 are acts of the <b>regency government</b> (the <i>regeringsråd</i>), which the letter itself names — hence the king's command to Tycho to \"declare himself\" <i>before</i> the Council of the Regency. The young king succeeded Frederik II (Tycho's patron, d. 4 April 1588), whose own chancery dealings with Tycho over instruments are the subject of the sibling pilot letter (brahe-t_15841211001).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tyge (Tycho) Brahe</b>, then resident on <b>Hven (Hveen)</b>, where he held the island in fief from the crown; the wreck-and-salvage jurisdiction at issue turns on that tenancy.</li><li><b>Bearer / claimant.</b> <i>Thomes Loeding</i> (also <i>Thometz Loeding</i> in the genitive) — the bearer of the letter, acting <i>paa … engelsche kiøbmends wegne</i> (\"on behalf of the English merchants\") who owned the wrecked cargo. Identity otherwise undetermined; the spelling suggests an agent or factor rather than one of the principals (named only collectively as \"certain merchants of England\").</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (<b>Sjællandske Tegnelser</b>), as in the 1584 Frederik II letter. The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"TILL TYGE BRAHE NOGEN WOX ANRØRENDIS <b>VT SEQUITUR</b>\"</i> (\"To Tyge Brahe, concerning certain wax, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2. The closing <i>Actum Haffniae</i> (\"Done at Copenhagen\") is likewise the standard Latin chancery formula (§5.5).</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Anholt</i> — the island in the Kattegat (notorious for wrecks). <i>Wardbierg</i> = <b>Varberg</b> and <i>Laugholm</i> = <b>Laholm</b>, both on the then-Danish coast of Halland (now Sweden). <i>Hueen</i> = <b>Hven</b>, Tycho's island. <i>Haffniae</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b> (Latin <i>Hafnia</i>, §5.7).</li><li><b>Legal substance (<i>wrag</i> / regalian salvage right).</b> <i>alt huis wrag … tilkommer Oß och cronen allenne</i> = \"all such wreck … pertains to us and the crown alone\": the king asserts the crown's <b>regalian right of wreck</b> (<i>ius naufragii</i> / strandret) over goods cast up on crown ground. <i>slige høighet … Oß allenne forbeholdit haffue</i> — <i>høighet</i> here is the sovereign <b>prerogative / regalian right</b> (\"such prerogative … reserved to ourselves alone\"), not \"highness\" as a style of address. The command's force is twofold and is read as conjoined (the two infinitive clauses <i>erklerer … betaler och fornøyer</i> both governed by <i>bede Wij thig och wille, attu …</i>): Tycho is (1) to render account of the matter before the Regency Council, and (2) to pay and satisfy the claimant for the wax — i.e. the crown takes the salvage right but directs Tycho to compensate the owner so the claim is closed.</li><li><b>Negation.</b> The source carries <b>exactly one</b> negation in the operative clause: <i>att hand sig wiidere for Oß icke schal haffue sig at beklage</i> = \"so that he shall not have [cause] to complain of you to us any further.\" Reproduced as a single negation; the purpose-clause is the consequence of Tycho's compliance, not a prohibition laid on the claimant.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1589).</b> <i>WOR gunst tilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"know,\" imperative); <i>breffuißer</i> = <i>brevviser</i> (\"bearer of the letter\"); <i>wnderdanigst</i> = <i>underdanigst</i> (\"most humbly\"); <i>tilkiende giiffuit</i> = <i>tilkendegivet</i> (\"made known\"); <i>st[r]andit</i> — the register copy reads <i>standit</i>; Dreyer prints <i>st[r]andit</i>, restoring the elided <i>r</i> (so too in the source block, where the bracketed <i>r</i> is editorial); <i>eblant</i> = <i>iblandt</i> (\"among\"); <i>guodtz</i> = <i>gods</i> (\"goods\"); <i>bierget</i> = <i>bjerget</i> (\"salvaged\"); <i>anammit / anamme</i> = <i>annammet / annamme</i> (\"taken in, received\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>begierendis</i> = <i>begærendes</i> (\"requesting\"); <i>motte</i> = <i>måtte</i> (\"might\"); <i>fornøyes / fornøyer</i> = <i>fornøjes / fornøjer</i> (\"be satisfied / make good, satisfy\"); <i>epterdj</i> = <i>efterdi</i> (\"inasmuch as\"); <i>wrag</i> = <i>vrag</i> (\"wreck, flotsam\"); <i>riigit</i> = <i>riget</i> (\"the realm\"); <i>tilkommer</i> = <i>tilkommer</i> (\"pertains/belongs to\"); <i>Wor elsk:e tilforornede regierings raad</i> = <i>Vor elskelige tilforordnede regerings råd</i> (\"our beloved appointed Council of the Regency\"); <i>erklerer</i> = <i>erklærer</i> (\"declare/explain [yourself]\"); <i>slige</i> = <i>slige</i> (\"such\"); <i>forbeholdit</i> = <i>forbeholdt</i> (\"reserved\"); <i>wiidere</i> = <i>videre</i> (\"further\"); <i>beklage</i> = <i>beklage</i> (\"complain\").</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Actum Haffniae 25 septem: a:o 1589</i> = \"Done at Copenhagen, 25 September, in the year 1589\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700, §3). The in-letter date <i>a:o 87</i> (\"in the year [15]87\") refers to the wreck, two years before the letter; it is not a dating discrepancy with the dateline. The dataset label (\"1589. 25. september. København\") agrees with the dateline.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely; the regalian-right vocabulary (<i>wrag</i>, <i>høighet</i> = prerogative) and the single negation are handled per the legal sense, with no genuine crux left unresolved. The one residual is the prosopography of Thomes Loeding (an unidentified agent), which does not affect the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish letter from Christian IV concerning the wrecked wax against TBOO XIV p.50 page image, checking every name (Thomes Loeding), place (Anholt, Varberg, Laholm, Hven), and date (25 September 1589). Text and English translation match Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XVII, 210."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15890920001", "next": "brahe-t_15910830001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Directs Tycho to account before the Council of the Regency for wax salvaged from a 1587 English shipwreck off Anholt, which the crown claims as wreck.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15890925001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891025001", "d": "1589-10-25", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → John Craig — Uraniborg, 25 October 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737, f. [draft / Kladde]</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD DR. IOANNEM CRAIGUM.<br>S.</p><p>CUM tres hac elapsa aestate a te receperim literas, Doctiss. CRAIGE, nulla ijs hactenus respondendi commodior est oblata occasio quam per hunc Magnificum et Nobiliss. virum WILHELMUM STUARTUM, qui etiam tibi priores meas tradi curavit; cum enim conterranei tui hic essent, qui magnificum et illustrem Dominum Legatum comitabantur, non concedebatur tibi rescribendi otium prae alijs curis politicis et oeconomicis. Ab eo vero tempore si quando aliquid ab alijs occupationibus respirationis supererat, amanuensi meo responsionem Apologeticam ad has ipsas tuas literas, quibus et sententiam meam de aethereo Cometarum situ et Hypothesium coelestium nuper a me excogitatam innovationem in dubium vocas, et quantum in te est, labefactare niteris, successivis horis dictavi, quam etiam nunc mitto. Si quid amanuensis incuria minus apposite et distincte scriptum est, tuum erit idipsum discernere, quam rem alias per temetipsum satis intelligis, neque enim nobis alijs et maioribus intentis otium supererat denuo haec revidere et recognoscere. Si tibi prolixior in hoc responso visus fuero, id ob materiae intricatae molem, quae non paucis satis dilucide explicatur, excusationem apud te habeat; volui enim vitare illud Horatianum: Brevis esse laboro, obscurus fio.</p><p>Pro singulari autem illa tua erga me meaque studia benevolentia et candido iudicio plurimas tibi habeo gratias, et vicissim te diligere atque in albo amicorum meorum singularium numerare non intermittam, nec non amicitiam semel per literas inchoatam postea continuare et crebra scriptione, quoties occasio datur (siquidem aliter non licet) conservare. Chimicorum aliquot medicamentorum praeparationem tecum non invite communicarem, si scirem, quidnam in ijs maxime expeteres, et ubi in laboribus his pyronomicis dubia praecipue moveris, licet ut dicam id quod res est: haec arcana neque tuto neque competenter literis consignantur. ROLANDI illius, cuius in ultimis literis mentionem facis, Centurias nondum vidi; ubi nactus fuero, dubitationem, quam circa quasdam inibi contentas praeparationes moves, minimo negotio resolvam. Distillatis uti purgantibus nec adeo paratu facile est neque etiam, nisi plura adhibeantur et circumspecte omnia fiant, satis consultum; amittunt enim ea, quae per se purgantia sunt, vim ferme omnem, si distillando exaltentur, neque extracta illorum adeo valida sunt, atque ipsa materia, per se licet impurior. Sed de his alias plenius tecum agere licebit, omnium vero optime coram id fieret, si nostram invisendi Uraniam unâque officinam Hermeticam tibi unquam occasio contingeret.</p><p>Pro communicatis quibusdam compendijs Triangulorum gratias habeo, licet ipsemet, ubi primus numerus est integer sinus, haec satis antea perspecta habeam ; ubi plura inquirere atque enodare tibi otium fuerit, feceris mihi rem gratam, si omnia quotquot eiuscemodi colligere poteris compendia, peculiari libello comprehensa et exemplis illustrata, mihi transmiseris, utque inventum illud quod tibi Helix Geometrica appellatur, unâ cum canonis confectione plenius aperias velim. Ego laboribus et sumptibus non parcam, ut per operarios sufficientes res haec executioni mandetur, modo ufufructus par sit labori, et si per me quidpiam rursus, quod tibi gratum sit, praestari poterit, invenies me perpetuo ad quodvis officij genus quam promptissimum. Scripseram tibi et alias literas per quendam Studiosum Germanum, qui aliquot mensibus mecum fuerat, quemque frater meus in quadam navi Illustrissimam Principem nostram Serenissimi Regis vestri sponsam in Scotiam, si venti secundi fuissent, deductura in suo comitatu habuit, sed cum re ob maris saevitiam et ventorum adversantium vehementiam infecta classis nostra in portum reversa sit, te eas non accepisse compertum habeo, neque existimo Studiosum illum hac hyeme, ut constituerat, Scotiam visurum. Quod ideo refero, ne ob moram hanc aliquantulam, quae in rescribendo commissa est, me prorsus officij immemorem fuisse suspiceris. Verum si quid hactenus intermissum est, id scriptum nostrum Apologeticum sua copia recompensabit, quod ut per otium diligenter evolvas, etsi quid adhuc foveris scrupuli, ingenue significes, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Uraniburgi 25. Octobris Anno 89.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Doctor John Craig.</b><br><b>Greeting.</b></p><p>Since I have received three letters from you this past summer, most learned Craig, no more convenient occasion for answering them has until now presented itself than through this Magnificent and most Noble gentleman William Stuart, who also saw to it that my earlier [letters] were delivered to you; for while your countrymen were here, who were accompanying the magnificent and illustrious Lord Ambassador, no leisure for writing back was allowed you, on account of other political and economic cares. But from that time onward, whenever any breathing-space was left over from my other occupations, I dictated to my amanuensis, in successive hours, an Apologetic reply to these very letters of yours — in which you both call into doubt my opinion concerning the ethereal situation of comets and the innovation in the celestial Hypotheses lately devised by me, and strive, so far as in you lies, to overthrow [them] — and this [reply] I now also send. If anything has been written less aptly and distinctly through my amanuensis's carelessness, it will be for you to make it out for yourself, a matter which in any case you understand well enough by your own [ability]; for, intent on other and greater things, I had no leisure left over to review and go over these [pages] again. If I shall have seemed to you rather long-winded in this reply, let it find its excuse with you in the bulk of the intricate subject-matter, which is not explained clearly enough in few [words]; for I wished to avoid that saying of Horace's: <i>I labour to be brief, [and] I become obscure.</i></p><p>For that singular goodwill of yours toward me and my studies, and your candid judgement, I render you very many thanks; and in turn I shall not cease to esteem you and to number you in the roll of my particular friends, and likewise to continue afterward the friendship once begun through letters, and to preserve it by frequent writing, as often as occasion is given (since otherwise it is not permitted). I would not unwillingly share with you the preparation of certain chemical medicaments, if I knew what in them you most desire, and where in these pyronomic labours you are chiefly troubled by doubts — although, to tell the matter as it is, these secrets are committed to letters neither safely nor adequately. Of that Roland whom you mention in your last letter I have not yet seen the <i>Centuries</i>; once I have got hold of [it], I shall resolve with the least trouble the doubt which you raise concerning certain preparations contained therein. To use distilled [substances] as purgatives is neither so easy in the preparing, nor indeed, unless several are employed and everything is done circumspectly, sufficiently advisable; for those things which are purgative in themselves lose almost all their power if they are sublimated by distillation, nor are their extracts as potent as the material itself, impure though it be in itself. But it will be possible to treat of these matters with you more fully another time; and best of all it would be done face to face, if ever the occasion should fall to you of visiting our Urania, and at the same time our Hermetic workshop.</p><p>For certain abridgements of Triangles you have communicated I render thanks, although I myself, where the first number is the whole sine, have had these well enough perceived before; when you have leisure to inquire into and unravel more [of them], you will do me a welcome service if you transmit to me all such abridgements as you can gather, comprised in a separate little book and illustrated with examples; and I should wish you to disclose more fully that invention which is called by you the <i>Geometric Helix</i>, together with the construction of the canon [table]. I shall spare neither labours nor expenses, that this matter may be carried into execution by sufficient workmen, provided that the benefit be equal to the labour; and if anything in turn can be furnished by me that should be welcome to you, you will find me ever most ready for every kind of service. I had also written you other letters by a certain German student who had been with me for some months, and whom my brother had in his company on a certain ship that was to convey our most Illustrious Princess, the bride of your most Serene King, into Scotland, had the winds been favourable; but since, the matter unaccomplished on account of the savagery of the sea and the violence of the contrary winds, our fleet has returned into harbour, I have ascertained that you did not receive those [letters], nor do I suppose that that student will see Scotland this winter, as he had arranged. This I report for the reason that you should not suspect me, on account of this little delay which has been incurred in writing back, of having been wholly forgetful of my duty. But if anything has hitherto been left undone, our Apologetic writing will make up for it by its abundance; and I have asked you again and again that you peruse it diligently at your leisure, and that, if you still nurse any scruple, you frankly make it known.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Uraniborg, 25 October, the year [15]89.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from Uraniborg on Hven. <i>Ioannes Craigus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Scottish physician and mathematician, then practising at Frankfurt an der Oder / and in Scotland, later first physician to James VI. Between 1589 and 1594 Craig conducted a bitter epistolary dispute with Tycho, disputing both Tycho's supralunary (ethereal) placement of comets and his newly devised geo-heliocentric world-system (the <i>Hypotheses coelestium</i>); the corpus literature (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i>) documents the exchange and Tycho's habit of circulating copies of it to Brucaeus and Hagecius.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (dicit), \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>WILHELMUS STUARTUS</b></i> — <b>William Stuart</b>, a Scottish nobleman in (or associated with) the embassy retinue then in Denmark; he carried the present letter and had forwarded Tycho's earlier letters to Craig. (Distinct from the \"William Fuller\" of Craig's circle named in the secondary literature.)</li><li><b><i>Dominum Legatum</b></i> — \"the Lord Ambassador\": the head of the Scottish embassy in Denmark in connection with the James VI / Anne of Denmark marriage negotiations of 1589.</li><li><b><i>responsionem Apologeticam … scriptum nostrum Apologeticum</b></i> — Tycho's <i>Apologia</i>, the polemical reply (dictated to his amanuensis) defending his cometary theory and world-system against Craig's objections; \"Apologetic\" is kept as Tycho's own characterisation of the piece.</li><li><b><i>sententiam meam de aethereo Cometarum situ</b></i> — \"my opinion concerning the ethereal situation of comets\": Tycho's doctrine that comets move in the ethereal (supralunary) region, not in the sublunary air. <i>aethereo</i> rendered \"ethereal,\" not modernised.</li><li><b><i>Hypothesium coelestium … innovationem</b></i> — \"the innovation in the celestial Hypotheses\": Tycho's geo-heliocentric system. <i>Hypotheses</i> kept as the period term for a planetary model (cf. protocol §5.1 <i>orbis/sphaera</i>), not flattened to \"theory.\"</li><li><b>Horace.</b> <i>Brevis esse laboro, obscurus fio</i> — <i>Ars Poetica</i> 25–26, \"I labour to be brief, [and] I become obscure.\" Quoted verbatim as a sententia and kept as such; Tycho names it <i>illud Horatianum</i>.</li><li><b><i>laboribus … pyronomicis</b></i> — \"pyronomic labours,\" i.e. operations governed by fire = alchemical/chemical (Paracelsian <i>pyronomia</i>, the regulation of fire in the laboratory). Kept as the period term, glossed.</li><li><b><i>Chimicorum … medicamentorum</b></i> — \"chemical medicaments\" (iatrochemical / Paracelsian remedies); <i>arcana</i> \"secrets\" kept (the alchemical sense of guarded preparations).</li><li><b><i>ROLANDI illius … Centurias</b></i> — \"that Roland … the <i>Centuries</i>\": a chemical/medical author named by Craig whose work (organised in <i>centuriae</i>) Tycho has not yet seen. Most plausibly <b>Roland</b> = a contemporary writer of medical/chemical <i>Centuriae</i> (so styled because arranged in hundreds of observations); the precise identification is not fixed by the text. <b>Crux flagged</b> rather than resolved.</li><li><b><i>officinam Hermeticam</b></i> — \"Hermetic workshop\": Tycho's alchemical laboratory in the basement of Uraniborg; <i>Hermetica</i> = alchemical (after Hermes Trismegistus). <i>Uraniam</i> \"our Urania\" = Uraniborg (the seat of the Muse of astronomy).</li><li><b>Mathematics.</b> <i>compendijs Triangulorum</i> = \"abridgements of Triangles,\" i.e. shortcut methods in trigonometry / the solution of triangles; <i>ubi primus numerus est integer sinus</i> = \"where the first number is the whole sine,\" the convention of taking the radius (<i>sinus totus</i>) as the reference unit in the sine canon. <i>Helix Geometrica</i> = the <b>Geometric Helix</b>, Craig's own coinage for an invention of his (so named <i>quod tibi … appellatur</i>, \"as it is called by you\"); kept untranslated and italicised, with Craig's authorship preserved. <i>canonis confectione</i> = \"the construction of the canon,\" i.e. the making of the trigonometric table. The phrase <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> does not occur here; this passage concerns Craig's computational shortcuts, which scholars connect to the prosthaphaeretic method later associated with Tycho's circle — but the term is not in the letter and is not introduced.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimam Principem nostram Serenissimi Regis vestri sponsam in Scotiam … deductura</b></i> — \"our most Illustrious Princess, the bride of your most Serene King, [to be conveyed] into Scotland\": <b>Anne of Denmark</b> (1574–1619), daughter of Frederik II, betrothed to <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (\"your most Serene King\"). The autumn 1589 bridal fleet was driven back by storms (here <i>ob maris saevitiam et ventorum adversantium vehementiam … in portum reversa</i>) — the celebrated storm-bound crossing that led James to sail to Norway himself. <i>frater meus</i> = Tycho's brother (Steen Brahe), in the escort fleet.</li><li><b><i>Studiosum Germanum</b></i> — \"a German student,\" an unnamed assistant who had lived some months at Uraniborg and was to have travelled to Scotland with the fleet; left unidentified by the text.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Uraniburgi 25. Octobris Anno 89</i> = \"At <b>Uraniborg</b>, 25 October [15]89\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (1589, 25 October, Uraniborg); no date discrepancy. The MS witness (cod. Vindob. lat. 9737) is described in the dataset as a <i>Kladde</i> (draft/concept copy).</li><li><b>No subscription formula</b> (no <i>Vale</i>-plus-name beyond the bare <i>Vale</i>); the draft ends at the dateline.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substantive content (the Apologia, the cometary/world-system dispute, the Anne-of-Denmark bridal fleet, the trigonometric shortcuts) is well anchored by the corpus literature and internal evidence. The two soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the identity of <i>Roland</i> and his <i>Centuriae</i> (a named but unidentified chemical author); (2) <i>Helix Geometrica</i>, Craig's own coinage, kept untranslated since its precise geometrical referent is not defined within the letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.195-196 as page images (slice pages 1-2; letter ends mid-page 196, 'Vale. Uraniburgi 25. Octobris Anno 89.', before a new letter to Hagecius begins) and collated the full Latin letter clause by clause against source_text: proper names (William Stuart, Rolandi, Gellius-free here — n/a, Helix Geometrica), the Horace tag 'Brevis esse laboro, obscurus fio', the Scotland/Princess-Anne voyage passage, and the closing dateline all match Dreyer's print verbatim. Checked the English against both source_text and the confirmed Dreyer wording sentence by sentence; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text departure found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15890709001", "next": "brahe-t_15900228001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890709001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900228001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends via William Stuart an apologetic reply to Craig's three summer letters, defending the supralunary position of comets and his innovation in celestial hypotheses.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891025001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891101001", "d": "1589-11-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 1 November 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.81 (draft with corrections in Tycho's hand); B = Pulkovo (now St Petersburg?)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO VIRO D. THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>QUATERNAS a te hac elapsa aestate, amantissime et eruditissime mi D. D. THADDAeE, accepi literas, primas Cal. Novembris veteri stylo anno 88 exaratas, 27. demum Aprilis huius anni me obtinere contigit; alteras 1O. Maij huius anni⟨⟩ scriptas ultimo Augusti recepi⟨⟩; tertias, quas 20. Maij novo stylo scripseras, circa medium Septembris obtinui⟨⟩; ultimae vero die 7. Septembris datae penultimo Octobris ad manus meas pervenerunt, idque (ut ubique antea) secundum veterem anni Iuliani numerationem, qua etiamnum nos utimur. Omnes tuae Praga erant missae, quas ego partim Hafniae, partim hîc Uraniburgi nactus sum. Singulae magnum illum tuum et constantem erga me meaque studia favorem affatim declarabant⟨⟩, et amicitiae inter nos olim Ratisponae ante annos circiter 14, cum modernus Imperator inauguraretur, percupide contractae sartam tectam continuationem et gratam conservationem, pluribus etiam interea literis confirmatam, non obscure testabantur⟨⟩. Pro qua tua singulari et plus quam benevola erga me affectione non possum non tibi gratias agere ingentes, pari vicissim te tuaque omnia amore et studio, quantum in me est, quoad vixero, prosequuturus et gratissimam tui memoriam perpetuo conservaturus⟨⟩.</p><p>Quod autem tuis illis eruditissimis pariter et amicissimis literis nihil tanto⟨⟩ iam elapso tempore responderim tuisque votis hac in parte non satisfecerim, ne⟨⟩ existimes, ulla oblivione tui vel etiam negligentia aut ob aliarum occupationum molem (licet hac praeterita aestate in varijs negotijs non exigua fuerit) id commissum esse, sed potius internunciorum penuriae, cum rarissime aliquis hinc ad vos abeat, cui tuto et certo literas concredere liceret, silentium illud diuturnum imputes velim. Nullus enim mihi incubuisset negotiorum tantus cumulus⟨⟩, neque ulla alia praevalere permisissem obstacula, quin aliquid ad te literarum dedissem, modo Grammatophorus certus et idoneus mihi interea temporis obtigisset. Et sane ut verum fatear, ne nunc quidem has ad te dedissem, ob instantem praesertim hyemem (quae maiora, quo minus tibi indubitanter tradantur, obstacula suggerit) nisi quartae illae tuae et ultimae nudiustertius supervenissent, et vehemens tuum, an priores a te missas acceperim, cognitionis desiderium unâ cum rescriptionis avida expectatione inculcassent⟨⟩. His enim acceptis, etsi non paucis hoc tempore implicer negotijs, sepositis tamen omnibus, non diutius responsione supersedendum duxi. Et licet non minor quam antea in mittendi occasione sit difficultas, imo potius ob hyemem, ut dixi, multo maior, attamen huic ita occurrendum censui, ut binas eadem continentes ad te exarari curarem, quarum unas Rostochium, alteras Lubecam vel Hamburgum translatas illac⟨⟩ tutius ad te perferri sperem; neque enim tunc ambas intercidere adeo in proclivi est.</p><p>Nunc itaque ut primis⟨⟩ tuis sufficienter respondeam, tempus postulat⟨⟩, siquidem ex quo illae a te scriptae sunt, hic ipse dies anniversarius sit, nimis magna interposita dilatione, quae tamen apud te omnia benevole et sincere interpretantem excusationem ob causas ante dictas⟨⟩ non difficulter⟨⟩ inveniet. Postea etiam ad alias nonnulla⟨⟩ subiuncturus idque paucioribus, cum et illae minus principalia tractent, et potissimum de primo missis⟨⟩ me admoneant. Ne igitur res ipsa diutius protrahatur, quae tuis primis⟨⟩ et prolixioribus ad ea quae continent, ordine respondenda nunc in mentem veniunt, sic habe. Binae illae, quarum istic ab initio mentionem facis, has Gallo cuidam Mathematico, illas Restochiensi Tabellario concreditas prorsus⟨⟩ (ut ipsemet recte coniectas) interciderunt, neque enim, si ullae illarum mihi allatae fuissent, rescriptionem tamdiu procrastinassem. Quod vero illae, quas ultimo ad te unâ cum libro nostro secundo aethereorum Phaenomenωn recenter conspectorum misi, optatum successum habuerint⟨⟩ et gratae oblectamentoque fuerint, plurimum mihi ipsi congratulor; quodque tam candide de scripto illo meo iudices, omniaque in potiorem partem interpreteris, est quod gratias tibi habeam permagnas. Nam licet tum ob aetatem, tum etiam curas domesticas et professionem medicam ab hoc sublimi studiorum genere, quo minus illi totus vacare possis, utplurimum, veluti ipsemet fateris, abstraharis, tamen ita iunioribus annis sedulo et feliciter in his versatus es, ut hac ingravescente aetate eo maturiore iudicio de ijs recte pronunciare possis. Et quamvis multa alia sint, quae subinde ab hac sublimi contemplatione te⟨⟩ avocent (quod et mihi non raro contingit) non tamen dubito, quin aliquot nonnunquam horis tantum tibi otij rapias⟨⟩, ut si non totaliter et ex professo, saltem aliqua et etiam bona ex parte his indulgeas⟨⟩. Neque enim amor ille tam arduus erga arduum etiam subiectum penitus deficere, vel ob curas rei familiaris aliaque studia, aut etiam senectae molestias ad nihilum redigi prorsusque aboleri poterit. Tuum itaque de meis studijs et scriptis solidum ac maturum iudicium pari aequanimitate coniunctum non saltem magnifacio, sed etiam multorum aliorum censurae praefero. Et cum hac materia, quam hisce prioribus Astronomiae instaurandae libris complector, plurimum tute multis abhinc annis afficiaris, eandemque non minima ex parte sedulitate tua et doctrina eximia illustrâris, eo etiam promptius tibi haec mea communico, et tecum de hoc utrique nostrum accepto negotio perlibenter confero, tuoque gravi iudicio omnia non gravatim subijcio. Et si quid in illis admonitione aut correctione dignum deprehenderis, non est quod ob amicitiam nostram et candorem tuum ingenuum, quo omnia in meliorem partem interpretaris, mihi nimium indulgeas aut nostris partibus plus aequo faveas. Amor enim et odium (ut est in Centiloquio) multorum iudicia pervertunt. Quin potius audacter et ingenue, si quid reprehensioni obnoxium inveneris, admone et libertate in his utere (more tuo) Philosophica: feceris mihi hac in parte rem non solum haud⟨⟩ ingratam, sed longe acceptissimam et ad veritatis in abscondito latentis penitiorem pervestigationem conducibilem. Invenies me animadversiones tuas et aliorum, qui acri iudicio valent, patienter et candide accipientem, et a te, qui etiam meas castigationes veritatis perquirendae amore in tuis scriptis admissas aequo et grato animo tulisti, non superatum iri.</p><p>Novam a me adinventam revolutionum coelestium dispositionem te nondum satis accurate perspexisse refers. De ea itaque recte facis, quod censuram non praecipitas, nam et haec in transmisso libro saltem generali indicatione (Cometa illo, qui non minus quam caeterae Planetae circa Solem ordinarie convolvebatur, sic ut hypothesis motus eius commodius et plenius constitui posset, exigente) consignata est. Pleraque enim adhuc restant particularia non cuivis obvia in ea ipsa explicanda, quae illic vel leviter attigimus, vel prorsus silentio, cum ex professo rem hanc ibi non tractaverim, praeterivimus, in alio quodam⟨⟩ et peculiari Astronomiae restituendae opere, favente Astrorum et omnis boni Authore, suo tempore enucleatius patefacienda et ex certissimis observationibus motuumque norma ipsi coelo analoga Geometrice demonstranda atque in numeros choream coelestem indubitatis vestigijs repraesentantes resolvenda. Interim ne sis nescius, me e Parallaxibus ♂tis in transitu acronycho⟨⟩ anno praesertim 82 diligentissime pervestigatis, cum solaribus aliquanto⟨⟩ maiores fuerint, tum etiam ex eius concitatiore in opposito Solis motu, atque fert Ptolemaica hypothesis, ipsum necessario, quando pernox est, terris plus, quam Sol unquam possit, appropinquare, solerti et saepius reiterata animadversione certo cognovisse scias. Atque hinc citra omnem refragationem consequitur, aut Terram annuo convolvi circuitu, quiescente circa medium Universi Sole, iuxta illam revolutionum coelestium distributionem, quam superioribus annis⟨⟩ ex veteri ARISTARCHO mutuatam⟨⟩ ingens ille et nunquam satis laudatus in apertum deduxit COPERNICUS, aut si id ob Physicas non leves sic⟨⟩ admissas absurditates, tum etiam sacrarum literarum non uno in loco refragantem auctoritatem concedi nequeat, nulla alia restat excusandarum Apparentiarum coelestium ratio, quae Geometriae et Arithmeticae legibus, uti oportet, subijciatur, quam ea ipsa ante sexennium⟨⟩ proxime in hypothesi systematis mundani conformanda a me excogitata⟨⟩. Sed et Terram nequaquam annuatim circumagi, ut vult Coperniana speculatio, aut Solis et octavae sphaerae motus per eam salvari posse, ut arduo et solerti ingenio idem tentavit Artifex⟨⟩, et maior in stellis, quae putantur affixae, celeritas, quam ipsius patitur Aequinoctiorum reciprocatio, et insuper⟨⟩ mutatae earundem latitudines a temporibus PTOLEMAeI hucusque iuxta proportionem alteratae declinationis Eclipticae ab Aequatore, satis convincunt; ideoque Terram immoto permanente Sole non designare annuo itinere Eclipticam, nec in hac sed potius Sole ipso istas differentias excusandas liquet⟨⟩. Accedit et hoc non obscuri testimonij loco, Terram instar Solis non moveri, quod Cometae circa acronychum⟨⟩ situm superioribus annis a nobis in altissimo aethere, summa diligentia quo ad omnes apparentias suas observati, motui annuo Terrae, qui tunc proprium illorum, quemadmodum in Planetis apud COPERNICUM sit, necessario vel retraheret vel saltem nonnihil retardaret, prorsus non paruerint, satis evidenti vel e solis Cometis indicio, terrestrem globum annuum circuitum nequaquam admittere⟨⟩. Cum itaque ob has et alijs etiam de causis Coperniana ratiocinatio in rei veritate nequaquam consistere possit, et vetus illa Ptolemaica dispositio ob Martem, ut dixi, aliquando Terras plus quam Sol appropinquantem (ut de Epicyclorum nimia superfluitate et Aequantum inconcinnis tricis, ac quibusdam alijs inconvenientijs nunc non dicam) locum non inveniat, sicque utraque positio antiquanda veniat, utique nostra haec neoterica inventio sola quadrabit, et in eorum locum hanc restitui veritatis circumspecta consideratio atque ab omnibus disconvenientijs vindicatio citra omne dubium postulat, quae tibi diligentius expendenda conferendaque relinquo. Omnium autem plenissime de hac hypothesi nostra, eam recte se habere iudicaveris, quando opus Astronomicum, quod auxiliante Deo ex professo hac de re luculenter tractabit, et figuris ac numeris rem ita se et non aliter habere evincet, laboribus nostris absolutum, publicam lucem viderit.</p><p>Sed est, quod obiter hîc conquerar. Quidam Dithmarsus ex Urso denominatus, cum ante quinquennium in cuiusdam nobilis et eruditi viri, ERICI LANGIJ nomine, mihi amantissimi, comitatu (illi enim tunc inserviebat) hîc unam atque alteram hebdomadam cum dicto Nobili hero suo commoratus fuisset, interea dum ego cum amico illo meo atque alijs nobilibus viris et Baronibus, quos secum huc adduxerat, hilarius convivo illis genialiter tractandis occupatus, iste Ursus clam chartas, instrumenta mea et quicquid oculis patuit, denotavit, schedas etiam, quascumque invenire potuit, furtim perlustravit. Inter alias vero unam, uti existimo⟨⟩, reperit, qua hypothesium nostra innovatio repraesentabatur, sed per incuriam aliquam non satis apposite formatam, siquidem Solarem orbem Martio totaliter includebat, ideoque tanquam inutilis inter alias quasdam chartas abiecta fuit. Ex hac iste procul dubio mearum hypothesium dispositionem hauriens, eam anno superiore in libello quodam, quem Fundamentum Astronomicum vocat, pro sua edidit, se hanc ante triennium in extremo quodam Poloniae angulo (ubi forte nunquam fuit) adinvenisse fingens, illustrissimoque Principi GULIELMO, Hassiae Landgravio, dedicare non est veritus. Sed ipsemet plagium hoc satis prodit, dum falso depictam figuram, quam forte⟨⟩ hîc reperit, imitatus Martium etiam orbem Solarem undiquaque ambire facit; ex quo enim vitium hoc per circinum commissum emendare non didicit, ignorans Solis orbem habere proportionem ad Martium sicut 4 ad 6 fere⟨⟩, et ob id Martem acronychum non excedere Solis ambitum, satis palam facit, se eius hypotheseos ne quidem solidam intelligentiam habere, nedum ut ipsemet eius inventor et author sit, ut perfricta fronte iactitat. Sic etiam multa alia in isto libello pro suis venditat, quae partim mihi, partim alijs praeripuit⟨⟩. Idem Astronomiae restitutionem insolenter pollicetur, cum et instrumentis et observationibus idoneis destituatur et insuper aliorum stipe vivat, ut de caeteris, quae in ipso ad tantum opus conficiendum⟨⟩ desiderantur, non dicam. Sic quoque in quibusdam alijs mihi contigit, qui hîc fuerunt et mea viderunt atque ex relatione nostra quaedam perceperunt, quaedam forte ex Chartis meis, qui⟨⟩ nunc in Germania partim conscribunt, partim alijs dictitant, ut vel hanc solam ob caussam consultum sit⟨⟩ operum meorum et inventionum publicationem maturare, ne tot a me suscepti in his studijs indefessi labores, tot vigilatae noctes, tot erogati sumptus (quae quartam Tonnae auri partem multum excedunt) ne tot, inquam, et plura alia Astronomiae instaurandae gratia a me vicenis iam annis exantlata, mihi ab alijs praeripiantur, ut si quid gratiae a ventura Posteritate sperandum esset, id illis cedat, qui ne minimum laboris, industriae aut impensarum huc contulerunt, accidatque mihi, quod de se olim festive cecinit MARO: Sic vos non vobis mellificatis apes etc. Sed valeant isti Plagiarij, satis etiam quibusdam in Germania probis et eruditis viris, quod alienis plumis instar corvi Aesopici sese ornent, cogniti.</p><p>Nunc ad tuas literas redeo. Quod Nobilissimo et amplissimo viro, Domino CURTIO, Caesareae Maiestatis Consiliario intimo⟨⟩ et Imperij Procancellario, viro etiam, ut audio, in Mathematicis excellenter perito, meum librum communicaveris, recte id a te factum est; multa enim de viri illius eximia prudentia et doctrina, pari virtute coniuncta, non solum abs te hisce⟨⟩ literis, sed etiam ab alijs iamdudum depraedicata sunt; quare et ego ipsum amare atque in pretio habere non omittam. Scribis illum, cum iudicium de ijs, quae libro meo continentur, ab eo⟨⟩ expeteres, respondisse, non temere de hypothesibus iudicandum, sed rem attentius considerandam esse, quae paulo ambiguosius a te referuntur, quam ut quid ille⟨⟩ senserit, inde⟨⟩ extricare queam; si enim me inconsiderate et non satis circumspecte hypotheses innovare existimat, et maiori attentione hac in parte opus fuisse, id illi forte ita videri potest, quod nondum omnia fundamenta et iustas rationes, quas in ista aliter quam ab antecessoribus nostris factitatum est, ordinanda hypothesi plenarie perspexerit; neque enim ex solo illo libro, quae me evidentia τεκμήρια⟨⟩ huc impulerunt, animadvertere potuit, cum illic omnia generali et latiori quadam indicatione breviter saltem indicem; neque enim ex proposito⟨⟩ ea de re in scripto aliquo Cometico agere placuerat, ideoque occasiones et ratiocinia, tum etiam particularia quaedam huc facientia⟨⟩ omittuntur, imo nullam forte eius inventionis in isto libro mentionem fecissem, ad opus Astronomicum eam, ubi convenientius locum mereretur, reservando, nisi quasi ex praesagio quodam id, quod mihi iam accidit, veritus fuissem, alios nempe mea mihi inventa praerepturos. Sin autem Dominus CURTIUS illa in hanc sententiam protulit, quod ipsemet non tam subito de mea hypothesium neoterica constitutione iudicium pronunciare velit, sed illud potius, donec res magis attente perspecta fuerit, differre, ea qua praeditus est prudentia eximia, admodum consulte⟨⟩ fecit; nam et plerique alias eruditi in Germania viri, dum re omni non satis provide et solerter expensa de meis hypothesibus iudicium praecipitârint, in varios errorum et praeiudicij scopulos impegerunt. Quidam ne Martia stella Soli in loco contactus orbium aliquando occurrat cursumque mutuum impediat, frustra veriti sunt, cum id nunquam contingere possit, siquidem, ubicumque Martis sidus in suo circuitu versatur, Solem pro centro respiciat. Circumferentiam vero et centrum circuli uniri quî fieri possit? Quidam vacuitatem, quae est in figura mei libri inter orbem ♄ et Fixarum sphaeram ab inferiori parte, minus concinnam reddere symmetriam suspicati, non animadverterunt, Sole in alteram orbis sui partem convoluto, quae prius vacua putabantur, adimpleri. Oportet enim, ut spatium undiquaque relinquatur satis amplum, quo Planetae superiores per quantitatem diametri orbis Solaris (qui Epicyclos veterum et motum annuum Terrae apud COPERNICUM excusat) respectu octavae sphaerae et Terrae nunc assurgant, nunc vero descendant. Quidam retrogradationes et stationes Planetarum non satis apposite sic salvari opinati sunt, cum nihil sit convenientius; nam quilibet superiorum Planetarum, dum Solem ubique loco centri respicit, per accidens Epicyclum motui proprio immiscet, et omnes istas passiones, quae per Epicyclos reales ab antiquis expediuntur, aut etiam per anniversariam Terrae convolutionem apud COPERNICUM, sic eadem forma, quantitate et eodem etiam tempore aptissime fieri possunt⟨⟩. Quidam Martis stellam terris aliquando, cum videlicet acronycha est, ipso Sole propiorem reddi, non satis tutum et conveniens esse timuerunt, cum id ipsa nos docuerit experientia. Unde etiam non minima caussa fuit, quae me ad hypotheses sic ordinandas adegit, ne Terra instar Astri triplici motu, cum sit unicum et simplex corpus admodumque grossum et grave, nec, ut aetherea illa lumina, ad perpetuo movendum habile in medio universi (ubi eam super stabilitate sua Opifex summus fundavit) ne moveatur in seculis, quiescere possit et tanquam patiens coelorum influentias activas in se recipere atque suo Archaeo cooperante, terrestria quaedam (ut sic dicam) astra, superioribus pulchre analoga indesinenter conformare, ut verum fiat illius HERMETIS TRISMEGISTI scitum dictum, etiam in toto Macrocosmo, quicquid est superius, idem esse inferius. Quidam etiam alias nonnullas circa nostram mundanorum corporum symmetriam et distributionem dubitationes moverunt, nullius tamen momenti, quas nimis longum foret nunc recitare⟨⟩; alias ubi meas resolutiones circa ea, quae opponi poterint, videris, haec omnia plenius cognosces. Si itaque illi omnes exemplo praedicti Domini CURTIJ censuram in re nondum penitius introspecta aliquandiu procrastinassent, multo sane prudentius⟨⟩ fecissent. Salutem quam ex ipsius ore mihi optas, eam ego vel millecuplam et illi et tibi vicissim precor, admodumque mihi grata est tanti viri erga me benevolentia, quam quocunque officij genere per me licuerit, non irrecompensatam relinquam. Consilium etiam eius, quo suadet, ne coeptum cursum interrumpam, quam arduo fieri poterit conamine, lubens sequar, sponte etiam mea huc propensus, ut si divinae clementiae ita visum fuerit, donoque suo mihi affulgere dignetur, Astronomiae instaurationem in ipsius gloriam et hominum f utilitatem Posteritati consecrem, ne frustra tandem hîc vixisse videar tanquam terrae inutile pondus, ut multis (dolendum) accidit. Et licet spontaneo ductu huc per mille anfractus et scopulos, alacri tamen et invicto animo (quod Deo acceptum fero) lubenter tendam, grata tamen nihilominus mihi est clarorum et eruditorum virorum cohortatio, quae validius addit incitamentum. Nam ut est in versu:</p><p>Fortis⟨⟩ et ad cursum⟨⟩ magno cum robore fertur,<br>Si tamen instiges, acrius ibit equus.</p><p>Addis insuper, saepedictum Dominum CURTIUM inter alia dixisse, me nimis multam fecisse mentionem et praedicasse nimium WINCHLERUM, hominem Astronomiae minus peritum. Atqui ego non tam ipsius scientiam in hac arte praedicavi, quam errores insulsissimos libello de Cometa isto ab ipso publicatos in apertum deduxi, et quaecunque ab illo imprudenter et inepte, tum etiam inscite prolata erant, redargui, adeo ut quibusdam, sed ijs potissimum, qui negotium hoc et errorum istorum foeditatem non perspiciunt, etiam nimius hac in parte fuisse videar. An hoc est hominem praedicare? Dicere eius argumentationes veritati vim facere, atque ipsius ratiocinationes saepenumero tot absurda committere, quot fere verba habent, tam multiplicem et prodigiosam subesse discohaerentiam, ut pudeat et pigeat referre, crassam insuper ignorantiam obicere, absurditates absurditatibus illum cumulare, ineptissimeque rem involvere usque ad nauseam, ipsius prolata sola reiectione ob discohaerentiam, aut commiseratione ob crassam ignorantiam potius quam longa refutatione digna esse, quod ab ipsius veritatis semita citra omnem verecundiam exorbitet, adhortari, ne tam absona in lucem edere amplius praesumat, ne seipsum suamque existimationem labe aspergat. Haec et plura eiuscemodi, quae invenies in ea tractatiuncula, qua WINCHLERI inepta et imperita placita cribravi, certe non prae se ferunt, me nimium illi tribuisse, aut ipsius doctrinam plus iusto extulisse. Neque etiam nimis ferociter aut rigide cum illo agendum duxi aut convicijs proscindendum, ut nunc quibusdam Theologastris pro more est, quemadmodum etiam tecum fecit impudens ille RAYMUNDUS. Sunt enim docti viri, utut in aliquibus graviter impingant, humaniter et moderate reprehendendi. Homines enim sumus, ab erroribus in hac caduca et multis obscuritatibus involuta ac inquinata vita prorsus immunes esse non possumus. Beatiores saltem, qui paucioribus et minoribus obnoxij sunt. Scio satis equidem WINCHLERUM plurima e tua Dialexi sibi arrogasse et non satis intellecta multipliciter depravasse, ideoque et ego pag. 446 idipsum brevibus indicare volui, et quae in Cometa anni 80mi ab illo ineptissime commissa in annotatiuncula quadam Dialexi tuae locupletatae circa Epistolam ad MILIUM et mihi communicatae addideras, satis animadverti. Verum ego de ista absurditate, qua Cometae illi Parallaxin 15 fere graduum assuit, et nihilominus longe supra Lunam collocat, nolui in libro secundo, ubi de Cometa anni 77 ex professo agitur, ullam mentionem facere, convenientius ducens, id in sequentem tertium Tomum, ubi aliorum de eodem anni 80mi Cometa sententiae sub incudem veritatis revocandae veniunt, reservare, ne confusio aliqua inordinata in his committeretur, cum methodice omnia tractanda sint. Illic, cum ipsius quoque prolata disquisivero, haec etiam nimia ineptitudo, quin indicetur, non praetereunda erit. Tu modo effice, ut integrum eius scriptum de eodem Cometa habeam, tum etiam aliorum, quotquot hactenus nactus es. Satis itaque forti dolabra a me dedolatas est WINCHLERUs, nec acriori pectine depexendus erat, nisi et cutem et crines ipsi prorsus avellere, quod inhumanum foret, decuisset. Nec prorsus, licet id meritus erat, ob imperitam et ineptam de isto Cometa decisionem, illum praeterire poteram, siquidem in ipso libelli frontispicio Geometricas demonstrationes praetendebat et intra eundem nescio quas argumentationes pomposas ex ARISTOTELE et Parallaxibus minus bene intellectis prae se ferret, quibus incautis et rem hanc penitus non intelligentibus fucum facere et facile imponere posset. Condonetis itaque tu et alij viri praestantes huiusque rei magis intelligentes, quod latus claudere vobis ultimum illi concesserim, nam et hoc data opera feci, cum digniori loco reponi non mereretur.</p><p>Sed et ego illum nunc valere sinam, et propius ad tua propria prolata, quae maioris momenti sunt et consideratione digniora, progrediar. Acceptissimus mihi fuit tuus ille candor et integritas, quod me in plerisque a te dissentire circa illum Cometam tuaque nonnulla certis rationibus improbare non aegre tuleris. Equidem satis sciebam te hoc ingenuo animo praeditum esse, ut tibi veritatis eruendae gratia alicubi contradici non invito acciperes animo. Scis satis, amicitia etiam nostra diutina idipsum postulante, me nullo tua carpendi aut elevandi studio ista scripsisse, sed saltem ut Cometam illum ab aëreis fumositatibus, inter quas eum priori tuo de hoc libro, vel contra proprias observationes, per incuriam aliquam immerito reposueras, vindicarem, et inter lucidissima coeli astra in subtilissimam aethereae regionis puritatem, ubi revera incessus suos exhibuit, merito reponerem, ne tua singularis doctrina et eximia auctoritas, quam apud multos in hac etiam materia tibi iamdudum conciliasti, veritati promovendae et confirmandae obstaculo essent. Si ipsemet naevos et errata quaedam in tuis scriptis, uti fateris, agnoscis, quin abstergas et venustiori forma induas, noli intermittere. Neque enim nonnulla retractare, quae alias non satis considerate proposita sunt, adeo indecorum est, id enim a multis et magnis excellentibusque viris antea factitatum. Dies diem docet; nec idem homo semper eadem iudicij circumspectione praeditus est.</p><p>Quae de illo calendariorum conformatore refers, quod odiose tuas admonitiones exceperit, non libenter audio, decet siquidem viros doctos moderate et patienter aliorum monita ferre. Scribam illi per primum quodque otium, et inter alia ipsum sui officij admonebo, utque te amet et honorifice de te sentiat, hortator ero. Fateris quidem deinceps, te tuis lapsibus patrocinari nec posse nec velle, quod admodum ingenuo et sincero iudicio pronuncias. Apparet tamen, quod nonnihil movearis, quia MAeSTHLINUM contra te defenderim, et existimas aliquid adhuc in contrarium pro te dici posse, si meo iudicio repugnandum duceres. At sane, mi THADDAeE, ego pro MAeSTHLINO nihil in eius gratiam, quod tibi officeret, scripsi, neque ego aut odij aut amicitiae caussa hîc quicquam egi, sed saltem ut veritatem intemeratam assererem. MAeSTHLINUM ex facie nunquam cognovi neque cum ipso ullam contraxi per literas hactenus amicitiam, imo ne a quoquam alio quidpiam de ipso referri audivi, ut plane mihi ignotus sit, nisi quantum ex eruditis ipsius scriptis nomen eius perceperim, te autem quam intime complexus sum, ut primum nobis Ratisponae ante plurimos annos familiaritas intercessit, quamque constanter eandem per literas interea, utut longa viarum intercapedine disiuncti simus, conservârim, ipsemet satis idoneus testis esse poteris, ut si veritas in alicuius gratiam seponenda foret, id potius in tuam ob diuturnam atque arctam nostram necessitudinem faciendum esset⟨⟩. At ego nihil hîc affectibus indulgendum censui, ne veritas, cuius penetralia aperire animus erat, pessundaretur, ut vel hinc pateat, me nihil ullius vel odio vel etiam amore, quod res ipsa non postularet, in medium protulisse, sed veritati nudae patefaciendae, quantum in me erat, studuisse. Quod si quicquam⟨⟩ excogitare poteris, quod ad tuas assertiones stabiliendas faciat, non etiam id in mei gratiam intermittas. Neque enim si mihi quacumque in parte repugnaveris, amicitia nostra propterea ullatenus vacillabit. Te tua tueri, et licet et decet. Vide saltem, ut satis iustis ratiocinijs et confirmationibus id agas. Quod MAeSTHLINUM ob egregiam Mathematum peritiam in pretio habes, adeo ut secundum a PRAeTORIO inter Germanos colloces, recte facis, eximij illius viri et doctissimi Astronomi ingenium atque industriam debita laude prosequendo. An vero is primus ante PRAeTORIUM, an vero secundus ab illo numerandus sit, ego non libenter dixerim. Est enim uterque in Mathematicis solide versatus, et ambo iudicio acri atque scientia perspicaci excellunt. MAeSTHLINUS tamen Astronomiam, quae omnium artium Mathematicarum Regina est, cui etiam reliquae ancillantur, plus excolere atque a mendis purgare laborat, cum PRAeTORIUS idipsum tum ob instrumentorum et aliorum adminiculorum defectum, tum etiam ob oculorum minus firmam valetudinem (ut ipsemet literis ad me non ita dudum perscriptis fatetur) tali opera supersedere cogatur. Utinam vero MAeSTHLINO satis solida et correcta non deessent organa, idque in aliqua copia, et sumptus otiumque illi suppeterent, sperandum foret, ipsum aliquid egregij in hac sublimi scientia moliri posse. At cum his subsidijs non ita, uti opus esset, instructus sit (nam instrumenta, quae hactenus illum habuisse ex scriptis eius colligo, tanto negotio rite exequendo nequaquam satis idonea et sufficientia sunt) non video, quomodo tam eximio labori ad optatum finem deducendo par esse possit; attamen in magnis voluisse sat est, et ut ille aiebat: »Est aliquid prodiret tenus, si non datur ultra«. Subiungis proinde, te id saltem nunc habere bona mea et MAeSTHLINI pace dicendum, quod ratio motuum Cometarum in hypothesin aliquam dirigi nequeat, quemadmodum in Planetis fieri potest, eo quod Planetarum motus integri sint mensurabiles, et quomodo accedant recedantque respectu Solis, pateat. In Cometis autem, cum non integras absolvant periodos, neque certa lege Soli appropinquent et ab eo expatientur, insolens tibi videtur iusta lege eorum motus astringere. Atqui hîc, dilecte THADDAeE, non satis considerate, quae in libro illo meo circa hoc ipsum negotium protuli, animadvertisse et expendisse mihi videris, siquidem illic hoc dubium et impossibilitatis suspicionem sufficienter resolvi atque amovi, imo ipsemet per hypothesin in illo Cometa extructam id fieri posse luculenter, tanquam in exemplo, ostendi. Fateor quidem, Cometas rarissime vel unicam per totum coelum revolutionem motu proprio absolvere, neque semper Solem instar centri, ut reliqui Planetae, respicere, ideoque digressiones ab eo non ubique consimili lege exhibere, quemadmodum nec Luna, quae Terram principaliter in sua convolutione observat. Attamen hinc non sequitur, prorsus impossibile esse, Cometarum motus sub normam aliquam per convenientem hypothesin redigere. Nam quod non totas periodos absolvere spectentur, difficultatem nonnullam in motu eorum regulando suggerit, possibilitatem omnem non excludit. Ex aliqua enim portione circuli data de tota eius circumferentia competenter ratiocinari adeoque centrum eius invenire non est adeo ἀδύνατον⟨⟩. Memineris eorum, quae ex Epistola quadam summi illius COPERNICI circa hanc rem citavi: »Ea quae aliquo modo determinata sunt, infinitam rationem habere non posse, sunt itaque comprehensibilia«. Et exemplum addit, quemadmodum si per tria puncta non secundum rectam lineam data circumferentia ducatur, non licet aliam superinducere, quae maior vel minor fuerit prius transmissa. Haec admodum scite et recte ab illo scripta sunt. Et si res ita se non haberet, quomodo affixarum stellarum motum unquam scrutabimur aut in rationem certam redigemus, ex quo ab eo tempore, quo observationes circa has innotuerunt, vix duo Dodecatemoria, sextam integri circuli partem emensae⟨⟩ sint? licet vero in earum curriculo explicando maior lateat difficultas, quam hactenus a quoquam animadversum est, attamen aliquam probabilem et apparentijs praeteritis satis consonam, ideoque et futuris, occasionem invenire, qua apparens in motu earum inaequalitas excusetur et numeratio observationi multis aevis consentiat, non duco inexplorabile, quin et longe minorem in Cometarum motu extricando difficultatem, licet et ea non exigua sit, subesse censeo, siquidem hi multo celerius sui cursus tramitem dirigunt, adeo ut nonnunquam intra duos vel tres menses quartam coeli partem, interdum plus, interdum vero minus, emetiantur, quae ab uno eodemque homine brevi tempore concipi possunt. In affixis vero sideribus multorum aevo opus est et plurimis plurimorum annorum intercapedine congestis observationibus, quae cum ab alijs atque alijs Artificibus non pari diligentia conquirantur, maximos hîc labyrinthos involvi video, licet et hi solerti iudicio et competenti adhibita limitatione extricabiles sint. Arduum itaque est et difficultatis non expers in Cometis, qui plenam revolutionem non perficiunt, motus harmoniam concinnare, at plane impossibile non est. Difficilia, quae pulchra, et viceversa quae pulchra, difficilia. Parallaxes Cometarum citra omnem erroris suspicionem persentiscere atque demonstrare grave est, et in hoc negotio inter praecipua reponendum. Neque enim, quae de his vel ab eximio illo REGIOMONTANO Franco vel nobili DIGESSAeO Anglo aut etiam a te et WITICHIO vel quibusvis, quod sciam, alijs in medium producta sunt, omnimode tam subtili inquisitioni satisfaciunt, idque eam praesertim ob caussam, quod motus proprij Cometarum rationem nullam habeant, quae neglecta Parallaxes inexplicabiles reddit, nisi admodum magnae forent, et motus ipse tardissimus; at plerumque Cometarum cursus genuinus adeo celer est, ut Parallaxes, quae per accidens tenorem inditum aliquantulum variant, respectu eius perexiguae sint. At licet hîc maior difficultas et pulchrior atque magis necessaria consideratio lateat, quam hactenus a plurimis, qui rem acu se tetigisse frustra existimârunt, exposita est (qua de re, si recte memini, te in quibusdam alijs literis aliquando admonui) longe tamen magis arduum, excellens utileque et pulchrum est Cometarum apparentem motum in certam normam instar astri alicuius perpetui redigere, ut demonstratio et numeri per totum eorum curriculum observationibus correspondeant, et motus circularis, vel quam minimum ab hoc deflectens illis attribuatur. Hoc opus, hic labor est, pauci hîc, quos ardens deduxit ad ardua virtus. Sic quod Solem non ubique respiciant crinitae, quemadmodum Planetae mundo coaevi, nihil probat eorum motus regulari non posse, nam et aliud quam circa Solem centrum habere queunt, utpote vel Terram observare aut quodvis aliud in universi capacitate punctum, cuius respectu cursus eorum ordinarius fiat. Accidit nihilominus aliquando, ut Solem quoque instar perpetuorum erronum centri loco habeat, quemadmodum in eo ipso Cometa, de quo nunc agimus, affatim a me demonstratum est, is enim circa Solem instar ♀ et ☿ obambulans neque etiam tam magnum designavit ambitum, ut Terram et Lunam simul includeret, et digressiones accessionesque ad Solem instar eorundem Planetarum oculis exhibuit. Cum enim circa principia suae apparitionis vix integro signo a Sole distaret prope primam triadem Novembris, deinde ad initia Decembris ab eo maximam elongationem assecutus, duobus quasi Dodecatemorijs removebatur, posteaque concinna lege huic successive denuo approximavit, ita ut circa ultimum suae evanescentiae limitem rursus non multo plus unico signo a Sole abscesserit, quam pulchram in motu eius respectu Solis harmoniam et egregiam cohaerentiam te ex observationibus et demonstrationibus meis libro⟨⟩ illo patefactis non satis perspexisse miror, nam et Cometa ille plane contrarium tuae sententiae hac in parte ostendebat. Omnia quae in coelo a mundi primordijs existunt corpora, motum regularissimum et perpetuum sortiuntur, quae vero ascititia sunt, si eum adeo absolute assequi nequeunt, attamen quam proxime aemulantur, ut ob id regulari omnimode non subterfugiant. Existimo autem Cometarum corpora, quae revera omnia ipso coelo procreantur, non esse ex tam solide concreta atque exaltata stabiliaque materia conformata, velut perennium illorum siderum. Ideoque non saltem consummationi obnoxia, sed etiam periodicum motum non continuant, nec eundem, quem exhibent, adeo praecise circularem aut forte e circularibus compositum, ut caeteri Planetae, praebent, interim tamen non multum a circularitatis perfectione degenerant, sed quoad fieri potest, Planetarum aemuli sunt, quae caussa me adduxit, ut illorum apparentem cursum in hypotheseos alicuius leges colligere non intentatum reliquerim.</p><p>Refers praeterea, te quidem mihi assentiri in eo, quod pulchram et ingeniosam MAeSTHLINI speculationem fuisse dixerim, sed pulchritudinem et subtilitatem veritati, quae virtus est pulcherrima, non esse anteferendam. Id quod ego sane nusquam, quod sciam, feci, imo ubique, qua fieri unquam per me potuit diligentia, veritatis latebras eruere laboravi, neque ego MAeSTHLINI hypothesin ob pulchritudinem et ingeniosam inventionem satis ratam praedicavi, sed saltem commendare volui ipsius omni laude dignissimam industriam, qua id experiri aggressus est, quod caeteri omnes insolens esse existimant, imo quantum ipsius hypothesis ab ipsis apparentijs Cometae deflexerit, praesertim iuxta initia eius⟨⟩ apparitionis et multo adhuc plus prope finem, existimo, neminem plenius et certius indicasse, quam a me, vel ipso (uti spero) MAeSTHLINO iudice, factum est. Ostendi siquidem hypothesin illam a principio iuxta medietatem potissimum Novembris integro gradu per inde deductam numerationem verum Cometae locum coelitus animadversum excessisse. In fine autem, videlicet circa Ianuarij medietatem et diebus aliquot sequentibus usque ad 26tum (quo adhuc a nobis Cometa ille conspectus est, licet illum alij desijsse putârint) nimis retardavit ipsius cursum, adeo ut non solum unius vel alterius, sed etiam iuxta ultimos praesertim Ianuarij dies deviationem 3¼ graduum proxime commiserit, quae sane est intolerabilis. Haec et alia quaedam circa illam hypothesin disquisivi, neque tamen ob id MAeSTHLINI studium praeclarum et in hac inventione laborem industrium vilipendere decuit, cum nemo veritati proximiora inter caeteros Germanos protulerit, imo quidem ne semel rem tam arduam tentare ausi sint. Nam quod iuxta COPERNICUM Solem quiescere et Terram annuatim convolvi statuat, id a Mathematicis illi facile condonandum venit, cum et hac ratione Planetarum apparentiae quam proxime salvari queant, ut magnus ille demonstravit COPERNICUS. Non igitur nimium tribuebam MAeSTHLINI hypothesi, sed quae in eo commendanda quaeque culpanda essent, reseravi, neque ob pulchritudinem et ingeniositatem veritati quidpiam detrahere volui, sed has per se seorsim praedicavi, quid verum vel falsum in illa hypothesi esset, alias pro virili indicans. Veritatem esse virtutum omnium pulcherrimam et praestantissimam tibi facile concesserim, nam et ego eos, qui hanc vel in vulgari colloquio data opera violant, semper abominatus sum et, quantum in me erat, illam non saltem in Astronomicis sed Chymicis etiam rebus, adeoque tota Philosophia, ut de Theologicis non dicam, ita semper inquirere studui, ut eam quam proxime assequi divina largiente bonitate perpetuo cupiverim, nulli labori vel sumptui aut etiam studio hac in re parcens. Ideoque non est, quod existimes me veritatem pulchritudini et ingenio posthabere, neque etiam id circa MAeSTHLINI speculationem dicere volui, quemadmodum tu inde colligis. Textus meus si relegatur, id non exhibebit, sed siquidem sic vis, dicam quiddam aliud, modo paradoxice agere liceat. Nihil est pulchrum, nisi et idem sit verum, neque verum, nisi pulchrum. Quod autem superaddis, nec MAeSTHLINUM nec quenquam alium in Cometarum Theoria veritatem, de qua loquimur, assecutum esse, quemadmodum in aliorum Planetarum motibus fieri solet, hîc videris non tam MAeSTHLINO quam mihi nimium derogare. Quae in ipsius hypothesi Cometae desiderentur, ipsemet satis in apertum deduxi, ut et paulo antea dixi, et quo loco ea habenda essent, ostendi. Verum ego aliam revolutionis Cometae rationem condidi, qua quiescente Terra, ut Physicis etiam satisfieret, ipsius motum et apparentias per totam durationem adeo praecise tutatus sum, ut nusquam sextantis unius gradus digressio ab ipsis observationibus commissa sit; an ulli Planetarum omnium in suis curriculis⟨⟩ per quasvis hactenus datas hypotheses ad tantam numerationis praecisionem deducti sint, quilibet discernet, qui coelum et tabulas introspicere novit; multorum enim graduum nonnunquam in his committitur deviatio. Imo ne quidem amborum luminarium motus ita certo hactenus extricati sunt, quin in Sole etiam maior dimidio gradu et in Luna plus integro coelum aliter eorum apparentias nonnunquam ostendat, quam Canones sive Coperniani sive Alphonsini exhibent. Cum igitur longe maior cum coelo convenientia mea hypothesi quoad Cometam illum elaborata sit, quam in ullo Planetarum unquam antea factum, quid, quaeso, rei est, quod eam tanquam veritati minus consonam praetereas, vel prorsus reijcias? anne haec ita se habere numeri ex hypothesi pag. 206 et duabus seq. derivati atque cum ijs, quae ex observatione pag. 86 et sequenti derivantur, collatione facta, satis convincunt? Si quid igitur habes, quod in hac hypothesi mea a coelesti Cometae itinere dissonum sit, illud prome, neque re non satis perspecta et minus deliberata iudicium praeoccupa. Ego sane non video, quid hîc desiderare possis, nisi id te forte movet, quod mense Novembri motus aequalitatem in suo ad Solem circuitu non satis praecisam conservârim. Verum quî id factum sit, ipsemet pag. 133 sufficienter excusavi. Et si admodum subtiliter omnia rimari placuisset, utique per compositum circularem motum et hanc aliquantulam inaequalitatem tueri non fuisset impossibile, verum ego volens id praetermisi, ne rem nimium intricarem, cum etiam dubium sit (ut ex prioribus quoque colligere licet) an Cometae perfecte circularem motum, ut reliqui Planetae, exhibeant, necne. Quam prope igitur ad scopum collimârim, si qui sunt plures, uti ais, qui mecum eo tendunt, illi dijudicent, modo metam petitam coelitus decernere norint. Quae tandem obijcis in numeris circa Maesthlinianam hypothesin a me fuisse aberrata, ea, si bona tua venia dicere liceret, mi THADDAeE, per aliqua occupationum impedimenta et incuriam quandam penitioris considerationis a te ipso potius commissa sunt, nam primum quantum ad Epocham commutationis mediae Cometae ad diem 24. Novembris attinet, quam ego cum MAeSTHLINO statuo eandem, ea satis bene se habet, et si quid perpusilli vitij in hac lateret, oportebat tamen me eandem, quam ipse praesupponit, assumere, eo quod ostendere volebam, ipsius hypothesin per totam Cometae durationem non aequaliter sufficere. Radicem itaque motuum ab ipso constitutam alterare mihi non licuit. In caeteris ubi dicis aequationes a me supputatae Maesthlinianis et tuis non consentire discrimine satis evidenti, id ita tibi frustra videtur, dum tu meos numeros, quos ego ad 26. Ianuarij secundum eandem hypothesin direxi, ad 24. Novembris (e quo solam Epocham MAeSTHLINI mutuatus sum, cum haec fundamentum sit reliquorum motuum) perperam applicas, differentia incidente quoad tempus duorum mensium, quo intervallo Cometa sua progressione easdem prostaphaereses in excusanda motus inaequalitate exhibere nequaquam potuit. Relege saltem ea, quae pag. 275 et aliquot sequentibus, ubi Maesthlinianam hypothesin ex ipsa demonstratione et numeris examino, scripta reliqui, videbis me ad 26. diem Ianuarij omnia adaptare, ut ostenderem, in ultima sua apparitione Cometae locum ab ipsius hypothesi nimium discrepasse. Maesthliniani vero et tui quos adducis numeri 24. Novembris, quo die MAeSTHLINUS primum in hac Pragmatia usus est, respiciunt. Sumus itaque in diversis terminis, ut mirum non sit, invicem non quadrare numeros. At si ostenderis me ad eum ipsum diem, cui Maesthlinianum hypothesin applicui, aliquid in meis numeris vitij commisisse, utique pro tua admonitione gratus ero. Sin vero nihil inveneris, haec monere supervacaneum erit. Sed facile tibi per aliquam oscitantiam inter alias forte curas id contingere potuit, ut non animadverteris ad diversos dies numerationem, quam confers, referri. Ego, qui satis mihi conscius sum, maximam in calculatione tum ista tum ea, quae toto libro continetur, adhibitam esse diligentiam, et omnia bis vel ter repetita⟨⟩ seduloque revisa, satis mirari non potui, cum primum tuas in campo legerem literas (eram enim, quando illas acciperem, cum duobus Germanis Studiosis, qui me eodem tempore e Germania venientes inviserant, spatiatum animi caussa egressus) cumque inter ambulandum ad calcem perventum erat, ubi huius numerorum discrepantiae mentionem facis, mirabundus me ad dictos Studiosos converti, in haec verba prorumpens: «Videbitis, quam primum domum reversi fuerimus, optimum illum senem unum diem pro altero⟨⟩ in hac numerationis diversitate per occupationum impedimenta atque curas alias accepisse«. Quod et satis patuit, nam domum reversi mox arrepto libro meo idipsum satis experti sumus. Si igitur aliud non habes, optime THADDAeE, quod hypothesibus vel a MAeSTHLINO vel me constitutis obijcias, aut quod pro te contra nos faciat, quam ex hisce tuis literis colligere licet, utique vel te ipso iudice iam satis vides, quam parum hîc obtinueris. Neque ego omnia, quae a te contra MAeSTHLINUM prolata sunt, improbavi, sed ijs, quae iustam reprehensionem mereri videbantur, subscripsi, quae vero minus iusta de caussa a te oppugnabantur, quo veritatis penetralia illibata conservarentur, tutatus sum⟨⟩. Atque haec de his sufficiant.</p><p>Quantum ad scriptores de Nova Stella attinet, da operam, ut MUNOSIJ saltem Hispani integrum librum habeam et, si fieri potest, ab aliquo illic in Latinum ex Hispanico idiomate conversum, tum etiam illorum Italorum, quorum in tua Dialexi meministi, praesertim CORNELIJ FRANGIPANI, et si qui sunt alij, idque mature, quo circa finem prioris tomi in eorum etiam placita disquirere liceat. APIANI Astronomicum ipsemet habeo et satis scio, inde vel parum vel nihil quoad Parallaxes Cometarum excutiendas colligi posse, sed ego librum peculiarem de quodam Cometa anni 1532 ab eo olim editum desiderabam, non tam quod sperarem, illum aliquid certi inibi circa Parallaxium enodationem protulisse (quam enim haec nimis secure non solum ab illo, sed etiam reliquis antecessoribus nostris praetermissa sit, vel certe nimis inconvenienter tractata, satis perspectum habeo) sed saltem ut apparentem Cometae motum ab illo procul dubio mediocri diligentia illic descriptum cum FRACASTORIJ in Italia circa eundem annotationibus libro suo de homocentricis breviter comprehensis conferrem atque hinc elicerem, IOANNIS VOGELINI Viennensis adeo monstrosam Parallaxin, quam is in eodem Cometa demonstrare nixus est, toto coelo erroneam fuisse; oportuisset enim in Italia et Germania sensibiliter diversis coeli locis apparuisse istam crinitam, si Parallaxin 35 proxime graduum, ut vult VOGELINUS, aut etiam 33, ut tu per assumtionem mobilitatis Terrae ex ipsius datis praecisius collegisti, obtinuisset⟨⟩. Quare si et nunc illum APIANI libellum mihi aliunde procuraveris, rem non inutilem feceris. IOANNEM POeNAM Gallum de Cometis aliquid scripsisse non scio, sed de coeli liquiditate et ab aërea subtilitate non differente natura ac quibusdam alijs rebus Philosophicis egregie tractasse, ab alijs, tum etiam Bibliotheca Gesneriana cognovi. Si igitur et hunc autorem tua opera accepero, rem pergratam mihi praestiteris et forsan studijs communibus non infrugiferam. Librum, quem Ratisponae mihi donâras, nescio ubi et quando amiserim, sed aliunde forte eum habebo, non est, quod tu te in eo conquirendo amplius molestes. Pro libro WERNERI de elementis conicis et motu octavae sphaerae mihi transmisso et a Domino FABRICIO bonae memoriae (audio enim illum iam fatis concessisse, quod etiam doleo) impetrato, gratias tibi ago plurimas; diu multumque iste liber a me expetitus est, nec unquam antea nancisci licuit. Licet, ut dicam id, quod res est, quantum ad tractatum de stellarum motu attinet, non habeat id in recessu, quod in accessu pollicetur, ut alibi ostensurus sum, cum de hac re agendi occasio dabitur.</p><p>Haec ad ea, quae literis ipsis primo ad me datis continentur, respondenda habui. Sed et ijs, quae in schedula annexa post scripta sunt, satisfaciendum erit. Recte illic mones, me ex tenore Privilegij per te impetrati tria exemplaria mearum lucubrationum in Cancellariam Caesaream mittere debere, quod etiam libenter facturus sum, ubi totum opus, vel saltem primus liber prodierit, cui diploma illud unâ cum caeteris ab alijs Regibus impetratis praefigetur, neque enim id prius necessarium duxi, siquidem Privilegij tenor huic secundae parti editae non sit adiunctus, nec exemplaria nisi perpauca alijs communicari concesserim, caeteris numero 1500 adhuc reservatis, donec primus Tomus publicam lucem viderit. Recte itaque excusasti hanc dilationem, non enim decuit mancum opus Caesari offerre, dabo autem operam, quantum in me est, ut liber primus hac hyeme typis nostris absolvatur, tum demum satis erit temporis, ut id, quod teneor, praestem. Rogas insuper ibidem, ut supra nominatum Dominum CURTIUM, cum exemplaria misero, salutem et literis invisam, utque amicitiam cum eo per has contraham, id quod etiam perlibenter efficiam, cum intelligam, virum illum dignum esse, qui ametur, et horum etiam studiorum excellenter gnarum. Ne igitur qua in mea voluntate adsit mora, si forte ante Francofurtianas nundinas liber prior totaliter typis mandari nequeat, exaravi iam nunc has, quas tibi mitto illi tradendas, ut meum erga se studium et officium maturius cognoscat, quod si hac hyeme quicquam rescripserit et suum de nostris laboribus ac studijs gravissimum iudicium protulerit, efficiet, ut saepius cum eo per literas agere et de hac sublimi arte conferre alacrior futurus sim. Sed video, me nimis forte prolixe, quantum ad primam Epistolam attinet, tecum locutum. Nunc igitur ad reliquas me conferam, in quibus ero brevior, cum etiam per se non adeo luculentam responsionem requirant.</p><p>Ex secundis tuis literis satis intelligo, amantissime THADDAeE, quam solicitus fueris de prioribus unâ cum libro illo WERNERI mihi non satis cito allatis, ubi etiam refers (quod tuae erga me benevolentiae tribuo) quod Tomum meum primum recentiorum coeli Phaenomenωn in vernalibus nundinis avide expectâris et etiamnum in proxime praeteritis autumnalibus. Sed remoras ex meis ad te ultimo datis aliqua ex parte intellexisti⟨⟩; principalis caussa fuit, quod papyro impressoria sufficienti destitutus fuerim, nec ea, quam e propinquo Megapolensi ducatu procurâram, satis mature et copiose allata sit, tum etiam quod illa ipsa, quae inde mihi contigit, minus candida, nec satis elegans fuerit, adeo ut quae in ea praeterita hyeme imprimi coepta erant, nunc meliore et puriore accepta, recudere consultius ducam, utut expensae aliquae frustraneae forte futurae sint. Volo enim mea tersa et pulchra, quoad eius fieri potest, in doctorum manus prodire. Ne autem in papyro e Germania conquirenda tot ulterius implicer difficultatibus atque dilationibus, curavi ipsemet hac aestate molendinum pro papyro conficienda in hac insula extrui, adiunctis etiam quae ad farinae molituram et pellium praeparationem faciunt, ita ut una rota, quae duodecim circiter ulnas in altitudine adaequat, omnibus his operis satisfaciat, ipsa etiam aqua in sufficienti copia, ubi prius siccum erat, per varios meatus huc derivata et magna capacitate ante aggerem admodum altum et latum collecta, piscinis etiam quam plurimis in eundem quoque usum hincinde dispositis. Quae omnia iam absoluta sunt, labore et executionis diligentia atque sumptibus non mediocribus comparata, ut ob id sperem, me in posterum papyrum officinae meae Typographicae idoneam, qualicunque forma cupiverim, sufficienter habiturum, ne aliorum in ea mittenda procrastinatio amplius laboribus meis in lucem mittendis obstaculo sit. Volo enim, ut tota haec insula, quantulacunque sit, et terra et aqua adeoque etiam ipso aëre Uraniae nostrae inserviat. Atque haec de his satis⟨⟩.</p><p>Doleo, incomparabilem illum virum ANDREAM DUDITIUM supremum diem obijsse. Constitueram cum illo per literas amicitiam contrahere et rogatu PRAeTORIJ quendam ex meis Studiosis cum instrumento observationibus Astronomicis idoneo ad illum mittere; id enim is, dum in vivis esset, a PRAeTORIO petierat, sed iam intellexi, illum fata nobis praeripuisse, non sane absque gravi Reipublicae literariae, cui plurimum profuit, iactura. Addis, illum de libris WITICHIJ iam olim cum sorore ipsius egisse, quae tamen difficilior nihil eorum distrahi permittere voluit. Sed ut est varium et mutabile semper femina, fieri potest, quod iam in illis vendendis facilior sit. Rogo itaque peramanter, velis per aliquem certum et fidelem virum Vratislaviae degentem agere, ut meo nomine cum illa transigat, quo omnem eius supellectilem librariam et chartaceam iusto pretio nanciscar; ego pecuniam illi promissam mature certo persolvi curabo, nollem⟨⟩ enim, ut eximij illius Mathematici libri perirent aut in eorum, qui eos in pretio non habeant, manus pervenirent⟨⟩. COPERNICUM eius et quae de Triangulis conscripsit, prae caeteris expeterem, tum etiam si quae alia sunt manuscripta. Invenietur inter alios libros et opus illud APIANI Astronomicum, cuius prius mentio facta est, quod ego 20 florenis emptum illi, cum hîc esset, dono dedi, nec post fata reposco, nisi iusto pretio emptum. Hoc si eius soror mihi unâ cum caeteris concesserit, te illo APIANI Astronomico, modo adhuc adsit, donabo; habeo enim ipsemet aliud exemplar ad manus, ut antea quoque indicavi.</p><p>Tertiae tuae literae nihil fere continent, quod responsionem admodum postulet, nisi quod de libro illo Astrologico mihi olim a te dato, quale autoris nomen fuerit et qua diligentia, cum, licet frustra, inquisiveris, commonefacias; in quibus etiam te excusas, cur nostras ille nobilis ad Imperatorem missus sine tuis ad me redierit, quas ego tamen, antequam ille huc reversus est, aliunde acceperam. Quartae et ultimae, quas ante biduum obtinui, de prioribus an procuratae sint, et utrum eas acceperim, agunt. Ad quas pluribus hîc respondere non est opus, cum iam satis id, quod desideras, cognôris. De libro etiam meo priore, quando edetur, quod Domini CURTIJ et tuo etiam proprio desiderio scire aves, iam antea satis dixi. Commentarios TRAPEZUNTIJ praesertim quoad duos priores libros, in publicum edi non quidem inutile duxerim, sed nec admodum necessarium, quod nihil peculiare contineant, quod ferme non dictum sit prius. Meum Typographaeum vix sufficit proprijs a me conscriptis excudendis, nedum ut aliorum operibus tempus et operam frustrer. Si librum illum repetieris, et tibi aliquis adferre voluerit, libenter data aliqua occasione remittam. Quae postea ultimis literis Subiungis de novis rumoribus Politica vel potius Politicos destruentia, nolo replicare, cum tecum potius philosophari lubeat. Relinquantur Politicis suae eccentricitates et anomali motus, quos nunquam in hac mundi immundi confusione per ullam prudentiae hypothesin regulabunt aut concentricos reddent, ut mirari simulque satis dolere non queam, homines microcosmos, quorum gratia macrocosmus cum suo toto ornatu potissimum conditus creditur, tantopere exorbitare, ut in nullam certam harmoniam unquam redigi possint. Errat enim sapiente sapientior, nec est veritati aut resipiscentiae ferme locus uspiam; verum cum Deus haec ferat, qui emendare posset, cur nos non patienter sustineamus, cum corrigere nequeamus? Id saltem, quantum ad Politica attinet, addam, me libenter ex te cognovisse, Illustrissimum Archiducem MAXIMILIANUM Polonicos limites tutum et incolumem excessisse atque ad suos salvum redijsse. Multa enim et praeclara in illius Principis laudem mihi ab Austriacis Baronibus et nobilibus, qui me saepenumero hîc inviserunt, praedicata sunt, et ut aiebat MARO: »Gratior est pulchro veniens e corpore virtus«.</p><p>Sed video me, dum singulis tuis literis in hisce unicis responsionem molior, Epistolae leges nimia prolixitate excedere, quod ob amicitiam nostram, quae efficit, ut lubens pluribus tecum agam, tum etiam quia tamdiu intermissam rescriptionem aliquantula copia nunc recompensare volui, apud te facile excusatum iri confido. Nunc itaque non ulterius te mea scriptione detinebo, sed Deo optimo maximo, qui te tuasque actiones omnes cum familia tota feliciter et diu incolumem regat ac conservet, nunc et semper commendatum habeo. Et si literas has eodem tenore binas conscriptas receperis, id ob caussam ab initio dictam factum esse existima, et omnia pro tua in me benevolentia atque amore in optimam, uti soles, partem interpreteris, te rogatum volo.</p><p>Verum unicum adhuc dicam. Cum sciam, te hac materia, quae de rebus Astronomicis et Cometis tractat, impense delectari, adiunxi hisce Apologeticam responsionem, quam paucis ante diebus contra viri cuiusdam doctissimi et Mathematici eximij, Doctoris IOANNIS CRAIGI Scoti, Edenburgi medicinam facientis, obiectiones conscripsi, quibus plaeraque, imo praecipua, quae libro nostro secundo, praesertim quoad Cometae situm aethereum, quem tuemur, et quantum ad hypothesin nostram attinet, literis ad me hac aestate datis, quarum exemplar unâ transscribi curavi, convellere tentavit, ut ARISTOTELI suo, cui nimium addictus videtur, vir alias eruditissimus et sagaci iudicio praeditus, quoad Cometarum sublunarem e siccis fumositatibus generationem et orbium coelestium realitatem patrocinetur. Ego itaque aliquid nactus a curis aestivalibus respiraculi, licet adhuc plurimis et diversis occupationibus distractus, non tamen plane intermittendum duxi, quin hoc Apologetico scripto eius obiectionibus occurrerem et veritatem semel assertam atque scriptis consignatam contra quosvis insultus tuerer et plenius stabilirem. Tu itaque per otium haec lege, et si placet, relege, nam et tui nonnunquam ibidem fit mentio, posteaque per literas tuum gravissimum de his iudicium mihi aperi et litem hanc Philosophicam tanquam arbiter dirime, adiuncta, si id illi importunum non fuerit, Domini CURTIJ gravissima censura. Cum enim is et Iurisprudentiae et Mathematum excellenter peritus sit, caussam hanc cognoscere atque aequali lance decidere omnium optime poterit. Adiunge etiam aliorum eruditorum et in arte hac exercitatorum atque iudicio valentium, quotquot in propinquo habes, sententias, et si qui erunt, qui per literas mecum de hoc arduo negotio conferre voluerint, sive noti sive antea ignoti fuerint, fecerint mihi hac in parte rem admodum acceptam et studijs Astronomicis huicque de Cometis quaestioni enodandae nec inutilem. Id vero te insuper admonitum velim, me ab alijs rogatum, imprimis vero a nobilissimo et amplissimo Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO, Vicario Regis nostra in Holsatia et annexis territorijs, ut literas Illustrissimi principis GUILIELMI, Landgravij Hassiae, et aliorum in Germania eruditorum virorum, quas de hac materia mecum agentes habeo quamplurimas, unâ cum mea ad singulas responsione publici iuris fieri permittam, quod etiam, quia ad rem hanc illustrandam non parum faciat, minime denegandum duxi; constitui enim in quarto quodam Tomo omnes has, et si quas praeterea de hoc negotio ab eruditis nactus fuero literas unâ cum nostris rescriptionibus et Apologijs, si qui ulterius quid contradicere praesumpserint, seorsim edere, quod hîc ob id indicare volui, ut attente et deliberate satisque luculenter omnia mihi vel abs te vel alijs per te hac de re scribantur, quod et antea, si recte memini, monui. Sed tempus est, ut nunc tandem vela contraham calamumque hîc quiescere sinam. Quare iterum et saepius vale quam felicissime, mi amantissime THADDAeE, et me ante Pasca tuis eruditissimis literis, tum etiam eximij illius Domini CURTIJ, si illi ita commodum fuerit, revisendum cura, iamque tertium et pluries vale.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi prim. Cal. Novemb. stylo veteri Anno 1589.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p><p>POST SCRIPTA.QUIA in istius Diplomatis Cæsarei mentionem incidimus, est aliquid, de quo<br>tecum circa illud ulterius agere, si ita tibi visum fuerit, vellem.<br>Privilegium enim illud nimis mihi videtur breve, nec satis copiosum aut<br>decorum pro artis et materiæ tractandæ magnificentia, et habeo aliud a Rege<br>Galliarum mihi concessum, quod multo est capacius et speciosius, in<br>Pergameno insuper scriptum et manu Regia subsignatum, cuius exemplum tibi<br>mitto. DANIEL etiam ROGERSIUS, vir<br>spectabilis et in primis literatus, Reginæ<br>Angliæ in Daniam secundus Legatus, cum proxime hîc apud me<br>esset, se mihi a serenissima Regina sua etiam Privilegium impetraturum<br>spondebat, quod seipsum ita terse et honorifice conscripturum aiebat, ut<br>Gallicanum etiam plurimum excelleret et in Pergameno quoque manu Reginæ<br>subscriptum exararetur. Sic ab Hispaniarum Rege, nisi nimia locorum<br>distantia vota nostra retardârit, spero Privilegium amplum et magnificum;<br>nollem itaque, ut Cæsarea Maiestas, quæ<br>primas inter Europæ Reges tenet, minoribus et brevioribus, quam illi, suum<br>mihi clementer concessum Privilegium comprehendi pateretur. Id enim et<br>ipsius Maiestati non satis honorificum esse videtur, nec artis sublimis excellentiæ sufficere. Peto itaque per amicitiam nostram<br>velis efficere, ut in Cancellaria Imperatoria mihi novum adhuc magisque<br>amplum et venustius condatur Privilegium, idque in<br>Pergameno, si unquam moris est, consignetur, manu Imperatoria subscriptum.<br>Magnificus Dominus CURTIUS facile<br>suam operam et tibi et mihi hac in parte impertiet; et cum artis huius sit apprime gnarus atque amans, poterit is<br>in artis et operarum harum honorem atque commendationem nonnulla aspergere,<br>quæ plures Reges et Principes, tum etiam alios in huius studij sublimis et<br>eius cultorum amorem atque favorem exuscitent, quod ipsius prudentiæ et erga<br>has artes meque, licet sibi ignotum, et meos labores benevolentiæ pro<br>arbitrio disponendum relinquo. Si quoque vobis videretur, non adeo opus esse<br>existimem multam de Religionibus et scandalizationibus mentionem, quæ<br>aliquibus forte odiosa esse poterit, immiscere; lubens enim a talibus<br>abstineo, quæ ulli parti in Religione offendiculo esse possint; neque enim<br>lubenter mihi de his iudicium sumo, sit hoc penes Creatorem omnium, qui<br>optime novit hædos ab agnis segregare, hypocritas a sincere eum colentibus,<br>cum scrutetur corda omnium. Satis iam Mundus tumultuarius habet hæresium et<br>confusionum; ut ego novas condam aut ab alijs introductas exagitem aut<br>refricem, non est opus. Veritatem apertam in his declarari Mundus, cum amet<br>potius tenebras ob mala sua opera, non patitur. Neque aliquem Apostoli<br>partes agere decet, nisi divinitus, non ab hominibus missus fuerit, ne illud<br>in eos, qui id sibi ex solo naturæ lumine arrogant, competat, quod est in<br>Propheta: »Cucurristis, et nemo misit vos, clamastis, et nemo<br>vobis os aperuit« Sed hæc extra propositum. Vides nunc, mi<br>THADDÆE, quod sit, et<br>quamobrem velim, quædam in illo Privilegio mutari, augeri atque amplificari,<br>tum etiam in Membrana, ut tutius et diutius conservari queant, describi.<br>Nihil tamen hîc peto, nisi quod deceat et quod te sine difficultate et molestia meo nomine impetrare posse speres. Pro ultimo mihi in Papyro transmisso misi 12<br>florenos in scriptoris remunerationem, prout Taxator æstimârat. Si denuo<br>novum et magnificentius in Pergameno exaretur, ego et nunc scriptionis<br>laborem etiam liberalius, quam Taxator censuerit, recompensare paratus ero.<br>Omnia tamen in hoc negotio tuo consilio et auxilio facienda vel infecta<br>relinquenda committo. Et si ita consultum tibi visum fuerit atque<br>Privilegium novum non difficulter obtinueris, cuperem, ut ante instantes<br>nundinas Francofurtianas<br>illud mihi huc in Daniam vel proprijs meis sumtibus transmitti cures, quo<br>satis mature illud nactus prioris tomi frontispicio præfigere liceat et tunc<br>demum utramque partem excusam in Cæsaris Cancellariam, uti in me recepi,<br>perferri curare. Iterum feliciter vale.</p><p>Rogo te quoque, mi HAGECI, ut si quæ habes literarum, quas ad te dedi,<br>apographa, ut puto, reservata, ea descripta mihi communices. Ego<br>enim volumini, quod Epistolas Astronomicas continebit, huiusmodi<br>destinavi. Quocirca si eas ocius miseris, rem feceris mihi in primis<br>gratam.</p><p>T. BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned man, most learned in every kind of doctrine, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, his friend to be honoured above all.</b></p><p>Four letters from you reached me this past summer, my most loving and most learned Master Thaddaeus: the first, written on the Kalends of November Old Style in the year [15]88, I managed to obtain only on 27 April of this year; the second, written on 10 May of this year, I received on the last day of August; the third, which you had written on 20 May New Style, I obtained around the middle of September; and the last, dated 7 September, came to my hands on the second-to-last [day] of October — and this (as everywhere before) according to the old reckoning of the Julian year, which we still use. All of yours had been sent from Prague, and I came by them partly at Copenhagen, partly here at Uraniborg. Each one amply declared that great and constant favour of yours toward me and my studies, and bore no obscure witness to the unbroken continuation and welcome preservation — confirmed besides by several letters in the meantime — of the friendship eagerly contracted between us once at Regensburg about fourteen years ago, when the present Emperor was being inaugurated. For this singular and more-than-kindly affection of yours toward me I cannot but render you immense thanks, intending in turn, so far as is in me, to attend you and all that is yours with equal love and zeal as long as I live, and to keep a most grateful memory of you forever.</p><p>But as to my having sent no reply to those most learned and most friendly letters of yours over so long an elapsed time, and not having satisfied your wishes in this respect, do not suppose it was committed through any forgetfulness of you, or through negligence, or because of the mass of other business (though this past summer there was no small amount of it in various affairs); rather I would have you charge that long silence to the scarcity of messengers, since very rarely does anyone go from here to your parts to whom one might safely and surely entrust letters. For no such heap of business would have weighed on me, nor would I have allowed any other obstacles to prevail, but that I would have sent you some letter, had only a sure and suitable letter-carrier come my way in the meantime. And indeed, to confess the truth, not even now would I have sent these to you — chiefly on account of the impending winter (which throws up greater obstacles to their being delivered to you without fail) — had not that fourth and last [letter] of yours arrived the day before yesterday, and pressed home your vehement desire to know whether I had received those you sent earlier, together with an eager expectation of a reply. For these once received, even though at this time I am entangled in not a few affairs, yet, setting all aside, I judged that the reply should be deferred no longer. And although the opportunity for sending is no less difficult than before — indeed, on account of the winter, as I said, much greater — nevertheless I thought it must be met in this way: that I have arranged for two [copies] containing the same matter to be written out to you, of which I hope the one, conveyed to Rostock, the other to Lübeck or Hamburg, may by that route be carried more safely to you; for then it is not so likely that both will go astray.</p><p>Now then, that I may sufficiently answer your first [letter], time requires it, since the very anniversary of the day on which it was written by you falls now — too great a delay having intervened, which nonetheless, with you who interpret everything kindly and sincerely, will not find it hard to be excused for the reasons stated above. Afterwards I shall add some matter on the others too, and that in fewer words, since they both treat less principal things and chiefly remind me of what was sent in the first. So then, that the matter itself be not drawn out longer, hold this for what now comes to mind, to be answered in order, in reply to the things your first and more copious [letter] contains. Those two [letters], which you mention at the very beginning of it — the one entrusted to a certain French mathematician, the other to the Rostock postman — went utterly astray (as you yourself rightly conjecture); for had any of them been brought to me, I would not have put off my reply so long. But that those which I last sent you, together with our second book on the ethereal Phenomena recently observed, had the wished-for success and were welcome and a delight, I much congratulate myself; and that you judge so candidly of that writing of mine, and interpret everything in the better part, is something for which I owe you very great thanks. For although, both on account of age, and also of domestic cares and the medical profession, you are for the most part drawn away from this sublime kind of study (so that you cannot devote yourself wholly to it), as you yourself confess, yet in your younger years you were so diligently and successfully versed in these matters that, at this advancing age, you can with all the riper judgement rightly pronounce upon them. And although there are many other things that from time to time call you away from this sublime contemplation (as also happens to me not seldom), yet I do not doubt that you sometimes snatch yourself a few hours of leisure, so that, if not wholly and professedly, at least in some — and indeed a good — part, you indulge in these pursuits. For that love, so arduous toward an arduous subject, cannot wholly fail, nor be reduced to nothing and utterly abolished by the cares of household affairs and other studies, or even the troubles of old age. Your solid and ripe judgement, therefore, on my studies and writings, joined with equal even-mindedness, I not only set great store by, but even prefer to the censure of many others. And since you yourself have been much engaged for many years past with this subject which I embrace in these first books on the restoration of astronomy, and have illuminated it in no small part by your diligence and outstanding learning, the more readily do I share these things of mine with you, and most gladly confer with you about this task acceptable to us both, and submit everything without reluctance to your weighty judgement. And if you find in them anything worthy of admonition or correction, there is no reason why, on account of our friendship and your candid sincerity, by which you interpret everything in the better part, you should indulge me too much or favour my side more than is fair. For love and hatred (as it is in the <i>Centiloquium</i>) pervert the judgements of many. Rather, boldly and frankly, if you find anything liable to reproof, admonish me, and use in these matters (after your manner) a <i>philosophical</i> liberty: you will thereby do me a thing not only not unwelcome, but by far most acceptable, and conducive to a deeper investigation of the truth lurking in concealment. You will find me receiving your animadversions, and those of others who excel in keen judgement, patiently and candidly; and I shall not be outdone by you, who likewise have borne with a fair and grateful mind the corrections of yours that I admitted in your writings out of love of seeking the truth.</p><p>You report that you have not yet examined accurately enough the newly devised arrangement of the celestial revolutions [discovered] by me. Concerning it, then, you do rightly in not rushing your verdict, for it too was set down in the transmitted book only by a general indication (that comet requiring it, which no less than the other planets ordinarily revolved about the Sun, so that the hypothesis of its motion might be more conveniently and fully established). For very many particulars not obvious to everyone still remain to be explained in that very [arrangement], which there we either touched on lightly, or passed over in complete silence (since I did not treat this matter there professedly) — particulars to be laid open more fully in due time, with the favour of the Author of the stars and of all good, in another and special work on the restoration of astronomy, and to be demonstrated geometrically from the most certain observations and a norm of the motions analogous to the heaven itself, and resolved into numbers representing the celestial dance by undoubted traces. Meanwhile, that you may not be ignorant of it, know that, from the parallaxes of ♂ Mars, most diligently investigated in [its] acronychal transit, especially in the year [15]82 — since they were somewhat greater than the solar ones, and also from its swifter motion when in opposition to the Sun, as the Ptolemaic hypothesis holds — I have established with certainty, by careful and oft-repeated observation, that it necessarily approaches the Earth, when it is shining all night, nearer than the Sun ever can. And from this it follows, without any gainsaying, either that the Earth is revolved in an annual circuit, the Sun resting about the middle of the Universe, according to that distribution of the celestial revolutions which — borrowed from the ancient Aristarchus — that great and never-sufficiently-praised Copernicus brought to light in former years; or, if that cannot be granted on account of the not-slight physical absurdities thereby admitted, and also the authority of the Sacred Letters which gainsays it in more than one place, there remains no other account for saving the celestial Appearances, subject (as it ought) to the laws of Geometry and Arithmetic, than that very one which I devised about six years ago, in forming a hypothesis of the world-system. But that the Earth is by no means carried round annually, as the Copernican speculation will have it, nor that the motions of the Sun and of the eighth sphere can be saved through it, as that Artificer attempted by an arduous and ingenious mind — this both the greater swiftness in the stars that are reckoned fixed, than the reciprocation of the Equinoxes itself allows, and besides the altered latitudes of those same [stars] from the times of Ptolemy down to now, altered in proportion to the changed declination of the Ecliptic from the Equator, sufficiently prove; and therefore it is clear that the Earth, the Sun remaining unmoved, does not trace out the Ecliptic by an annual journey, and that these differences are to be excused not in it, but rather in the Sun itself. There is added too, in the place of no obscure testimony that the Earth does not move like the Sun, that the comets observed by us in former years in the highest ether about an acronychal position, with the utmost diligence as to all their appearances, did not at all obey the annual motion of the Earth — which would then necessarily either draw back their proper [motion], such as it is in the planets according to Copernicus, or at least somewhat retard it — by an indication sufficiently evident even from the comets alone, [showing] that the terrestrial globe in no way admits an annual circuit. Since, therefore, on account of these and other causes too, the Copernican reasoning cannot in any way stand in the truth of the matter, and that old Ptolemaic arrangement finds no place on account of Mars (as I said) sometimes approaching the Earth nearer than the Sun (to say nothing now of the excessive superfluity of epicycles and the inelegant tangles of equants, and certain other unsuitabilities), and so both positions come to be set aside — then assuredly this neoteric invention of ours alone will fit, and a circumspect consideration of the truth, and its vindication from all disagreements, demands beyond all doubt that this be restored in their place — which I leave to you to weigh and compare more diligently. But you will judge most fully of all that this hypothesis of ours rightly holds, when the Astronomical work — which, with God's help, will treat of this matter professedly and luminously, and will prove by figures and numbers that the thing is so and not otherwise — completed by our labours, has seen the public light.</p><p>But there is something I would here complain of in passing. A certain man of Dithmarschen, surnamed Ursus, when about five years ago he had stayed here a week or two in the company of a certain noble and learned man, Erik Lange by name, most loving toward me (for he was then in his service) together with his said noble master — meanwhile, while I, busied with entertaining genially that friend of mine and other noble men and Barons whom he had brought hither with him, was feasting rather merrily — this Ursus secretly took note of my papers, instruments, and whatever lay open to his eyes, and furtively scanned what sheets soever he could find. Among others he found one, as I think, on which our innovation of the hypotheses was represented, but through some carelessness not formed quite aptly, since it enclosed the solar orb wholly within the Martian one, and was therefore, as useless, thrown aside among certain other papers. From this, drinking in without doubt the arrangement of my hypotheses, he last year published it as his own in a certain little book which he calls the <i>Fundamentum Astronomicum</i>, feigning that he had himself devised it three years before in some remote corner of Poland (where perhaps he never was), and did not scruple to dedicate it to the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse. But he himself betrays this plagiarism well enough, when, imitating the falsely drawn figure which he chanced to find here, he makes the solar orb encircle the Martian one too on every side; for in that he has not learned to correct this fault committed with the compasses — being ignorant that the Sun's orb has a proportion to the Martian roughly as 4 to 6, and that on this account acronychal Mars does not exceed the Sun's circuit — he makes it plain enough that he has not even a sound understanding of that hypothesis, far less that he is himself its inventor and author, as with brazen brow he boasts. So likewise he peddles many other things in that little book as his own, which he has snatched partly from me, partly from others. The same man insolently promises a restoration of astronomy, though he is destitute of suitable instruments and observations, and besides lives on others' alms — to say nothing of the rest that are wanting in him for the completing of so great a work. So too it has befallen me with certain others who have been here and seen my things and gathered some matters from my account, some perhaps from my papers, who now in Germany partly write [them] up, partly dictate them to others — so that for this single cause alone it is advisable to hasten the publication of my works and inventions, lest so many unwearied labours undertaken by me in these studies, so many sleepless nights, so much money spent (which much exceeds a quarter of a tun of gold) — lest, I say, so many things, and many others drained out by me now for twenty years for the sake of restoring astronomy, be snatched from me by others, so that, if any thanks are to be hoped from coming Posterity, it may fall to those who have contributed not the least bit of labour, industry, or expense hereto; and that there befall me what Maro once wittily sang of himself: <i>Thus do ye bees make honey, but not for yourselves,</i> and so on. But farewell to those Plagiarists — well enough known, too, to certain upright and learned men in Germany, for adorning themselves with others' feathers like the crow of Aesop.</p><p>Now I return to your letter. That you communicated my book to the most noble and ample man, Master Curtius, privy Councillor of His Imperial Majesty and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire — a man too, as I hear, excellently skilled in mathematics — you did rightly in this; for many things about that man's outstanding prudence and learning, joined with equal virtue, have long been proclaimed not only by you in these letters, but also by others; wherefore I too shall not fail to love and esteem him. You write that he, when you sought his judgement on the things contained in my book, replied that one must not judge of hypotheses rashly, but that the matter must be considered more attentively — which is reported by you a little too ambiguously for me to extricate from it what he meant; for if he thinks that I innovate hypotheses inconsiderately and not circumspectly enough, and that there was need of greater attention in this part, that can perhaps so seem to him because he has not yet fully grasped all the foundations and just reasons that are to be set in order in this hypothesis (devised otherwise than has been the practice of our predecessors); for he could not from that book alone perceive what evident <i>proofs</i> [τεκμήρια] drove me to this, since there I indicate everything only briefly, by a certain general and broader indication. For it had not pleased me to treat of this matter purposely in any cometary writing, and therefore the occasions and reasonings, and also certain particulars pertaining hereto, are omitted; indeed I would perhaps have made no mention of that invention in that book — reserving it for the Astronomical work, where it would more suitably deserve a place — had I not, as if by some presaging, feared that very thing which has now befallen me, namely that others would snatch from me my own inventions. But if Master Curtius brought those words forth in this sense — that he himself is unwilling to pronounce judgement so suddenly on my neoteric constitution of the hypotheses, but rather to defer it until the matter has been examined more attentively — then, with that outstanding prudence with which he is endowed, he acted most advisedly; for many otherwise learned men in Germany too, while precipitating judgement on my hypotheses without the whole matter having been weighed providently and carefully enough, have run upon various reefs of error and prejudice. Some have feared in vain lest the star of Mars should sometime meet the Sun at the point where the orbs touch, and impede their mutual course — since that can never happen, seeing that wherever the star of Mars is borne in its circuit, it regards the Sun as its centre. But how could the circumference and the centre of a circle be united? Some, suspecting that the void which is in the figure of my book, between the orb of ♄ Saturn and the sphere of the Fixed [stars] on the lower part, renders the symmetry less elegant, did not notice that, when the Sun is revolved into the other part of its orb, what were before thought void are filled up. For there must be left on every side a space ample enough, in which the superior planets, by the magnitude of the diameter of the solar orb (which excuses the epicycles of the ancients and the annual motion of the Earth in Copernicus), now rise and now descend with respect to the eighth sphere and the Earth. Some have thought that the retrogradations and stations of the planets are not aptly enough saved thus — though nothing is more fitting; for each of the superior planets, while it everywhere regards the Sun in place of a centre, incidentally blends an epicycle with its proper motion, and all those affections which by the ancients are dispatched through real epicycles, or by Copernicus through the annual revolution of the Earth, can thus be most aptly produced in the same form, magnitude, and likewise at the same time. Some have feared that it is not safe and fitting enough for the star of Mars to be sometimes rendered nearer to the Earth than the Sun itself (namely when it is acronychal) — though experience itself has taught us this. Whence too there was no slight cause that drove me so to order the hypotheses, that the Earth — being a single and simple body, and quite gross and heavy, and not, like those ethereal luminaries, apt for perpetual movement in the middle of the universe (where the supreme Maker founded it upon its stability, that it should not be moved for ages) — should rest, and as something passive should receive into itself the active influences of the heavens, and, with its own <i>Archeus</i> cooperating, should ceaselessly form certain terrestrial stars (so to speak), beautifully analogous to the superior ones, so that that knowing saying of Hermes Trismegistus might come true, [namely] that even in the whole Macrocosm, whatever is above, the same is below. Some have moved certain other doubts, too, about our symmetry and distribution of the world-bodies — of no moment, however, which it would be too long to recite now; another time, when you have seen my resolutions concerning the things that may be objected, you will know all these more fully. If, therefore, all those men had, after the example of the aforesaid Master Curtius, put off their verdict for a while on a matter not yet more deeply looked into, they would surely have acted far more prudently. The greeting which you wish me from his own mouth, that I in turn pray a thousandfold for both him and you; and most welcome to me is the goodwill of so great a man toward me, which I shall not leave unrequited by whatever kind of service may be in my power. His counsel too, by which he urges that I not interrupt the course begun — however arduous the effort it may take — I shall gladly follow, being of my own accord inclined this way too: so that, if it shall seem so to the divine clemency, and it deign to shine upon me with its gift, I may consecrate to Posterity the restoration of astronomy, to its glory and the use of men, lest I should seem at last to have lived here in vain, like a useless burden of the earth, as has befallen many (a thing to be lamented). And although of my own free guidance I gladly press hither through a thousand windings and reefs, yet with a brisk and unconquered spirit (which I count acceptable to God), nonetheless the exhortation of famous and learned men is welcome to me, which adds a stronger incitement. For, as it is in the verse:</p><p>The horse, though strong, and borne with great vigour to the course,<br>Will yet go the keener, if you spur him on.</p><p>You add, besides, that the oft-named Master Curtius said, among other things, that I had made too much mention of, and praised too highly, Winckler — a man little skilled in astronomy. But I did not so much praise his knowledge in this art, as bring into the open the most insipid errors published by him in his little book about that comet, and refuted whatever had been put forth by him imprudently and ineptly, and also ignorantly — so much so that to some, but chiefly to those who do not perceive this business and the foulness of those errors, I may even seem to have been excessive in this part. Is <i>this</i> to praise a man? To say that his argumentations do violence to the truth, and that his reasonings commit very often almost as many absurdities as they have words; that there underlies so manifold and prodigious an incoherence that one is ashamed and loath to report it; to charge him besides with gross ignorance, [to say] that he heaps absurdities upon absurdities, and most ineptly entangles the matter to the point of nausea; that his utterances deserve only rejection on account of their incoherence, or commiseration on account of his gross ignorance, rather than a long refutation; to exhort him, since he strays from the path of truth itself without any shame, not to presume to bring forth into the light anything so discordant any more, lest he besmirch himself and his reputation with a stain? These and many more of the kind, which you will find in that little treatise in which I sifted Winckler's inept and unskilled opinions, certainly do not show that I attributed too much to him, or extolled his learning more than was just. Nor did I think he should be dealt with too ferociously or rigidly, or torn apart with abuse, as is now the custom with certain <i>theologasters</i> — as that impudent Raymundus also did with you. For learned men, however gravely they stumble in some things, are to be reproved humanely and moderately. For we are men; in this fleeting life, wrapped and stained with many obscurities, we cannot be wholly free from errors. Those at least are happier who are liable to fewer and smaller ones. I know well enough, indeed, that Winckler arrogated to himself very many things from your <i>Dialexis</i>, and corrupted in manifold ways what he had not understood well enough; and therefore I too wished to indicate this same thing briefly on page 446, and I took sufficient note of the things most ineptly committed by him concerning the comet of the year [15]80 in a certain little annotation which you had added to your enriched <i>Dialexis</i>, around the Epistle to Milius, and communicated to me. But concerning that absurdity by which he sews onto that comet a parallax of nearly 15 degrees, and nevertheless places it far above the Moon, I did not wish to make any mention in the second book — where the comet of the year [15]77 is treated professedly — judging it more fitting to reserve that for the following third volume, where the opinions of others about the same comet of the year [15]80 come to be recalled to the anvil of truth, lest some disordered confusion be committed in these matters, since everything must be treated methodically. There, when I shall have examined his utterances too, this excessive ineptitude, that it be not indicated, will not be passable over. Only do you bring it about that I have his entire writing about the same comet, and also those of others, as many as you have so far obtained. Winckler, then, has been hewn down by me with a sufficiently strong axe, nor was he to be combed out with a sharper comb, unless it had been fitting to tear off his very skin and hair — which would be inhuman. Nor could I altogether pass him over (though he deserved it) on account of his unskilled and inept decision about that comet, since on the very frontispiece of the little book he pretended to Geometrical demonstrations, and within it put forward I know not what pompous argumentations out of Aristotle and parallaxes ill understood, by which he could throw dust in the eyes of the incautious and those who do not understand this matter deeply, and easily impose on them. May you, therefore, and other excellent men more knowledgeable in this matter, pardon me for having granted him the last place to close the rank — for I did this on purpose too, since he did not deserve to be set in a worthier place.</p><p>But I shall now let him too fare well, and proceed nearer to your own utterances, which are of greater moment and worthier of consideration. Most acceptable to me was that candour and integrity of yours, that you did not take it ill that I dissent from you in most things concerning that comet, and disapprove of some of your [opinions] by sure reasons. Indeed I knew well enough that you were endowed with this candid disposition, that you take it with no unwilling mind to be contradicted somewhere for the sake of eliciting the truth. You know well enough — our long-standing friendship demanding this very thing — that I wrote those things from no zeal of carping at you or belittling you, but only that I might vindicate that comet from the airy fumosities among which, in your earlier [book] about it, you had — against your own observations, through some carelessness — undeservedly placed it, and might deservedly set it among the brightest stars of heaven, in the most subtle purity of the ethereal region, where it truly displayed its courses; lest your singular learning and outstanding authority — which you have long since won for yourself among many in this matter too — should be an obstacle to the advancing and confirming of the truth. If you yourself acknowledge certain blemishes and errors in your writings, as you confess, do not forbear to wipe them away and clothe them in a more graceful form. For it is not so unbecoming to retract some things that were otherwise proposed without enough consideration; for this has been the practice before of many great and excellent men. One day teaches another; nor is the same man always endowed with the same circumspection of judgement.</p><p>What you report about that arranger of calendars — that he took your admonitions hatefully — I do not gladly hear, since it becomes learned men to bear the warnings of others moderately and patiently. I shall write to him at the first leisure, and among other things admonish him of his duty, and I shall be an exhorter that he love you and think honourably of you. You confess indeed, thereupon, that you neither can nor will defend your own slips — which you pronounce with a most candid and sincere judgement. Yet it appears that you are somewhat moved that I defended Maestlin against you, and you think that something can still be said to the contrary on your behalf, should you judge that my opinion ought to be opposed. But truly, my Thaddaeus, I wrote nothing for Maestlin in his favour that would prejudice you, nor did I do anything here from either hatred or friendship, but only that I might assert the truth inviolate. Maestlin I have never known by face, nor have I hitherto contracted any friendship with him by letters; indeed I have not heard anything reported about him by anyone else, so that he is quite unknown to me, save in so far as I have perceived his name from his learned writings; whereas you I have embraced most intimately, ever since familiarity first arose between us at Regensburg very many years ago, and how constantly I have preserved that same [friendship] by letters in the meantime — however we be sundered by the long interval of the roads — you yourself can be a witness fit enough; so that, if the truth were to be set aside for anyone's sake, it ought rather to be done for yours, on account of our long and close intimacy. But I judged that nothing here should be indulged to the affections, lest the truth, whose inner shrine it was my mind to open, be ruined — so that from this very thing it may be plain that I have brought nothing forward out of either hatred or even love for anyone, where the matter itself did not require it, but have striven, so far as was in me, to lay open the naked truth. But if you can devise anything that may serve to establish your assertions, do not on my account forbear that either. For if you oppose me in any part whatsoever, our friendship will not on that account waver in the least. To defend your own things, both is lawful and becomes you. Only see that you do it with just enough reasonings and confirmations. That you hold Maestlin in esteem for his outstanding skill in mathematics, so as to rank him second after Praetorius among the Germans, you do rightly, pursuing the genius and industry of that distinguished man and most learned astronomer with due praise. But whether he is to be reckoned first, before Praetorius, or second after him, I would not gladly say. For each is solidly versed in mathematics, and both excel in keen judgement and perspicacious knowledge. Maestlin, however, labours more to cultivate astronomy — which is the Queen of all the mathematical arts, and to which the rest are handmaids — and to purge it of faults; whereas Praetorius is compelled to forbear such work, both for the lack of instruments and other aids, and also for the less firm health of his eyes (as he himself confesses in a letter written to me not long ago). But would that Maestlin did not lack instruments solid and correct enough, and in some abundance, and that means and leisure were at hand for him: it would then be to be hoped that he could undertake something distinguished in this sublime science. But since he is not so equipped with these aids as would be needful (for the instruments which I gather from his writings that he has had hitherto are by no means suitable and sufficient for duly executing so great a task), I do not see how he can be equal to bringing so distinguished a labour to the wished-for end; yet in great things to have willed is enough, and as that man said: <i>\"It is something to advance just thus far, if it is not granted to go beyond.\"</i> You add thereupon that you now hold at least this — to be said by your good leave and Maestlin's — that the account of the motions of comets cannot be directed into any hypothesis, as can be done in the planets, because the motions of the planets are wholly measurable, and how they approach and recede with respect to the Sun is plain; but in comets, since they do not complete whole periods, nor approach the Sun and depart from it by a fixed law, it seems to you unwonted to bind their motion by a just law. But here, beloved Thaddaeus, you seem to me to have noticed and weighed not carefully enough the things I put forth in that book of mine about this very business, since there I sufficiently resolved and removed this doubt and the suspicion of impossibility — nay, I myself showed luminously, as in an example, by the hypothesis constructed for that comet, that it can be done. I confess indeed that comets very rarely complete even a single revolution through the whole heaven by their proper motion, nor always regard the Sun as a centre, as the rest of the planets [do], and therefore do not everywhere exhibit their digressions from it by a like law — just as neither does the Moon, which principally observes the Earth in its revolution. Yet from this it does not follow that it is wholly impossible to reduce the motions of comets under some norm by a fitting hypothesis. For that they are not seen to complete whole periods suggests some difficulty in regulating their motion, [but] does not exclude all possibility. For from some given portion of a circle to reason competently about its whole circumference, and so to find its centre, is not so <i>impossible</i> [ἀδύνατον]. Remember those things which I cited about this matter from a certain Epistle of that supreme Copernicus: <i>\"The things which are in some way determined cannot have an infinite ratio, and are therefore comprehensible.\"</i> And he adds an example: just as, if a circumference be drawn through three points not on a straight line, it is not permitted to superinduce another, greater or smaller than the one first traced. These things were written by him most skilfully and rightly. And if the matter were not so, how should we ever scrutinize the motion of the fixed stars or reduce it to a fixed reckoning, since from the time when observations concerning these became known, scarcely two <i>Dodecatemoria</i> — a sixth part of the whole circle — have been traversed? But although there lurks in unfolding their course a greater difficulty than has hitherto been noticed by anyone, nevertheless I do not hold it inexplorable to find some occasion, probable and consonant enough with the past appearances, and therefore with future ones too, by which the apparent inequality in their motion may be excused and the reckoning agree with observation over many ages; nay, I judge that a far smaller difficulty (though that too be not slight) underlies the unravelling of the motion of comets, since these direct the track of their course much more swiftly, so that they sometimes traverse, within two or three months, a quarter of the heaven — sometimes more, sometimes less — which can be grasped by one and the same man in a short time. But in the fixed stars there is need of the age of many [men], and of observations heaped up over a very great interval of very many years; which, since they are sought out by different and yet different Artificers with unequal diligence, I see the greatest labyrinths to be involved here, though these too be untanglable by keen judgement and with a fitting limitation applied. It is therefore arduous, and not free of difficulty, to frame the harmony of motion in comets which do not perform a full revolution; but it is plainly not impossible. The difficult things are the beautiful, and conversely the beautiful, the difficult. To perceive and demonstrate the parallaxes of comets beyond all suspicion of error is a grave matter, and to be set among the chief things in this business. For neither do the things produced about these — whether by that distinguished Regiomontanus the Franconian, or by the noble Digges the Englishman, or even by you and Wittich, or by any others, so far as I know — in every way satisfy so subtle an inquiry; and that especially for this cause, that they take no account of the proper motions of comets, the neglect of which renders the parallaxes inexplicable — unless they were very great, and the motion itself very slow; but for the most part the genuine course of comets is so swift that the parallaxes, which by accident vary somewhat the imparted tenor, are with respect to it exceedingly small. But although here there lurks a greater difficulty, and a more beautiful and more necessary consideration, than has hitherto been set forth by very many who vainly thought they had touched the matter to the quick (concerning which matter, if I rightly remember, I admonished you sometime in certain other letters), yet far more arduous, excellent, useful, and beautiful it is to reduce the apparent motion of comets into a fixed norm, like that of some perpetual star, so that the demonstration and the numbers correspond to observations throughout their whole course, and a circular motion — or one deflecting as little as may be from this — be attributed to them. <i>This is the task, this the labour;</i> few here, <i>whom ardent virtue has led to arduous things.</i> Thus, that the hairy ones [comets] do not everywhere regard the Sun, as do the planets coeval with the world, proves nothing of their motion not being able to be regulated; for they can have some other centre than about the Sun — namely, to observe either the Earth or any other point in the capacity of the universe, with respect to which their ordinary course may be made. It happens nonetheless sometimes that it has the Sun too, like the perpetual wanderers, in the place of a centre — as has been amply demonstrated by me in that very comet of which we now treat: for that one, going about the Sun like ♀ Venus and ☿ Mercury, did not designate so great a circuit as to include both the Earth and the Moon at once, and exhibited to the eyes its digressions and approaches to the Sun like those of the same planets. For since about the beginnings of its apparition it was scarcely distant a whole sign from the Sun, near the first triad of November, then, having attained its greatest elongation from it about the beginnings of December, it was removed by about two <i>Dodecatemoria</i>, and afterwards by a comely law again successively approached it, so that about the last limit of its vanishing it again withdrew not much more than a single sign from the Sun — what a beautiful harmony in its motion with respect to the Sun, and excellent coherence, [I marvel] that you did not perceive well enough from my observations and demonstrations laid open in that book, for that comet showed plainly the contrary of your opinion in this part. All the bodies which exist in heaven from the world's beginnings are allotted a most regular and perpetual motion; but those that are adventitious, if they cannot attain it so absolutely, nevertheless emulate it as nearly as may be, so that on that account they do not altogether escape being regulated. But I think the bodies of comets — which in truth are all procreated by the heaven itself — are not formed of so solidly concreted and exalted and stable a matter as those everlasting stars. Therefore not only are they liable to consummation, but also they do not continue a periodic motion, nor do they exhibit the [motion] they show as precisely circular, or perhaps composed of circulars, as the other planets [do]; yet meanwhile they do not degenerate much from the perfection of circularity, but, as far as can be, are emulators of the planets — which cause led me not to leave unattempted the gathering of their apparent course into the laws of some hypothesis.</p><p>You report, besides, that you do indeed assent to me in this — that I called Maestlin's speculation beautiful and ingenious — but that beauty and subtlety are not to be preferred to truth, which is the most beautiful virtue. Which I certainly nowhere, so far as I know, did; nay, everywhere, with what diligence was ever possible to me, I have laboured to dig out the hiding-places of truth, nor did I proclaim Maestlin's hypothesis as sufficiently ratified on account of its beauty and ingenious invention, but only wished to commend his industry, most worthy of all praise, by which he set about attempting that which all the rest reckon unwonted; nay, how much his hypothesis deflected from the very appearances of the comet — especially around the beginnings of its apparition, and much more yet near the end — I think no one has indicated more fully and surely than was done by me, even with Maestlin himself (as I hope) for judge. For I showed that that hypothesis, from the beginning, especially about the middle of November, by the reckoning thence deduced, exceeded by a whole degree the true place of the comet observed in the heaven. But at the end — namely about the middle of January and some days following, down to the 26th (on which that comet was still observed by us, though others thought it had ceased) — it retarded its course too much, so much so that not only by one or another [degree], but even, especially about the last days of January, it committed a deviation of nearly 3¼ degrees — which is surely intolerable. These and certain other things I examined about that hypothesis; nor yet was it on that account fitting to disparage Maestlin's excellent zeal and industrious labour in this invention, since no one among the other Germans has put forth things nearer to the truth — nay, indeed they have not even once dared to attempt so arduous a matter. For that, with Copernicus, he holds the Sun to rest and the Earth to be revolved annually, that comes to be easily pardoned him by mathematicians, since by this account too the appearances of the planets can be saved as nearly as possible, as that great Copernicus demonstrated. I did not therefore attribute too much to Maestlin's hypothesis, but laid open what in it was to be commended and what to be blamed; nor did I wish to detract anything from the truth on account of beauty and ingenuity, but proclaimed these in themselves separately, indicating elsewhere, to the best of my power, what was true or false in that hypothesis. That truth is the most beautiful and most excellent of all virtues I would readily grant you; for I too have always abhorred those who violate it of set purpose even in common conversation, and, so far as was in me, I have always striven so to inquire into it — not only in astronomical, but in chemical matters too, and indeed in all philosophy (to say nothing of theological) — that, by the granting of the divine goodness, I have ever desired to attain it as nearly as possible, sparing no labour or expense or even study in this matter. And therefore there is no reason for you to think that I set truth below beauty and ingenuity, nor did I wish to say that about Maestlin's speculation, as you gather from it. If my text be re-read, it will not show that; but since you will have it so, I will say something else, provided I be allowed to proceed paradoxically: Nothing is beautiful unless it be also true, nor true unless beautiful. But as to what you add — that neither Maestlin nor anyone else has attained, in the Theory of comets, the truth of which we speak, as is wont to be done in the motions of the other planets — here you seem to derogate too much, not so much from Maestlin as from me. The things wanting in his hypothesis of the comet I myself have brought sufficiently into the open, as I said a little before, and I showed in what place they were to be held. But I founded another account of the comet's revolution, by which — the Earth resting, that the physicists too might be satisfied — I have guarded its motion and appearances throughout the whole duration so precisely that nowhere has a digression of a single sixth of a degree been committed from the observations themselves. Whether any of all the planets has in its courses been brought, by any hypotheses hitherto given, to so great a precision of reckoning, anyone will discern who knows how to look into the heaven and the tables; for a deviation of many degrees is sometimes committed in these. Nay, not even the motions of the two luminaries have hitherto been so certainly unravelled, but that even in the Sun the heaven sometimes shows their appearances [to be] more than half a degree, and in the Moon more than a whole [degree], otherwise than the Canons — whether Copernican or Alphonsine — exhibit. Since, therefore, far greater agreement with the heaven has been worked out by my hypothesis as regards that comet than was ever before achieved in any of the planets, what, I ask, is the reason that you pass it by as less consonant with truth, or reject it altogether? Do not the numbers derived from the hypothesis on page 206 and the two following, when a comparison is made with those derived from observation on page 86 and the following, sufficiently prove that these things are so? If, therefore, you have anything that in this hypothesis of mine is dissonant from the celestial journey of the comet, bring it forth; and do not anticipate judgement on a matter not examined enough and less deliberated. I truly do not see what you can find wanting here, unless perhaps this moves you, that in the month of November I did not preserve precisely enough the uniformity of the motion in its circuit about the Sun. But how that came about, I myself sufficiently excused on page 133. And if it had pleased [me] to probe everything most subtly, it would certainly not have been impossible to maintain even this little inequality by a composite circular motion; but I deliberately passed it over, lest I entangle the matter too much, since it is also doubtful (as may be gathered from the foregoing too) whether comets exhibit a perfectly circular motion, like the rest of the planets, or not. How near, therefore, I have aimed at the mark, let those judge — if there are more, as you say, who tend with me thereto — provided they know how to discern the goal sought, by [the witness of] the heaven. The things, finally, which you object — that I erred in the numbers concerning the Maestlinian hypothesis — those, if by your good leave I may say it, my Thaddaeus, were rather committed by yourself, through some hindrances of business and a certain carelessness of deeper consideration; for first, as regards the Epoch of the mean commutation of the comet to the day of 24 November, which I establish the same as Maestlin [does], that stands well enough; and even if any very tiny fault lurked in it, I had nonetheless to assume the same [epoch] that he himself presupposes, because I wished to show that his hypothesis is not equally sufficient throughout the whole duration of the comet. The root of the motions established by him I was therefore not permitted to alter. In the rest, where you say that the equations computed by me do not agree with the Maestlinian and yours by a sufficiently evident discrepancy, this so seems to you in vain, while you wrongly apply my numbers — which I directed to 26 January according to the same hypothesis — to 24 November (from which I borrowed only Maestlin's Epoch, since this is the foundation of the remaining motions); a difference falling, as to time, of two months, in which interval the comet by its progression could by no means exhibit the same <i>prosthaphaereses</i> in excusing the inequality of motion. Re-read at least the things I left written on page 275 and some following, where I examine the Maestlinian hypothesis from the very demonstration and numbers: you will see that I adapt everything to the 26th day of January, so that I might show that, in its last apparition, the place of the comet diverged too much from his hypothesis. But the Maestlinian numbers, and yours which you adduce, regard 24 November, on which day Maestlin first used [it] in this <i>Pragmatia</i>. We are therefore in different terms, so that it is no wonder the numbers do not square with one another. But if you show that, on the very day to which I applied the Maestlinian hypothesis, I committed some fault in my numbers, I shall surely be grateful for your admonition. But if you find nothing, it will be superfluous to warn of these things. But it could easily happen to you, through some drowsiness amid perhaps other cares, that you did not notice that the reckoning you compare refers to different days. I, who am conscious enough to myself that the greatest diligence was applied in that calculation, as well as in that which the whole book contains, and that everything was twice or thrice repeated and diligently revised, could not wonder enough — when I first read your letter in the field (for I was, when I received it, gone out for a stroll for the sake of my mind, with two German students who had come from Germany and visited me at that same time), and when, while walking, we had come to the foot [of the letter], where you make mention of this discrepancy of numbers — [and] full of wonder I turned to the said students, bursting out into these words: <i>\"You will see, as soon as we have returned home, that that excellent old man has, in this diversity of the reckoning, taken one day for another, through the hindrances of business and other cares.\"</i> Which also became plain enough, for, having returned home, presently snatching up my book, we sufficiently proved this very thing. If, therefore, you have nothing else, most excellent Thaddaeus, to object against the hypotheses established either by Maestlin or by me, or that may make for you against us, than can be gathered from these letters of yours, then assuredly even by your own judgement you now see well enough how little you have gained here. Nor did I disapprove of all the things put forth by you against Maestlin, but subscribed to those which seemed to merit just reproof; whereas those which were impugned by you on less just grounds I defended, that the inner shrine of truth might be preserved undefiled. And let these things suffice about these matters.</p><p>As regards the writers about the New Star, take pains that I may have at least the whole book of the Spaniard Muñoz, and, if it can be done, translated by someone there into Latin from the Spanish tongue; and also of those Italians whom you mentioned in your <i>Dialexis</i>, especially Cornelius Frangipani, and any others if there are, and that in good time, so that toward the end of the first volume I may be allowed to inquire into their opinions too. Apian's <i>Astronomicum</i> I have myself, and I know well enough that little or nothing can be gathered thence as regards shaking out the parallaxes of comets; but I was wishing for a particular book about a certain comet of the year 1532, formerly published by him — not so much because I hoped he had brought forth anything certain therein concerning the unravelling of parallaxes (for how too securely these have been passed over, not only by him but also by the rest of our predecessors, or at least too unsuitably treated, I have well enough perceived), but only that I might compare the apparent motion of the comet, doubtless described there by him with middling diligence, with Fracastoro's annotations in Italy about the same, briefly comprised in his book <i>On Homocentrics</i>, and hence elicit that the so monstrous parallax of Johann Vögelin of Vienna, which he strove to demonstrate in the same comet, was wholly erroneous; for that hairy [star] would have had to appear in Italy and Germany in sensibly different places of the heaven, if it had a parallax of nearly 35 degrees, as Vögelin will have it, or even 33, as you more precisely gathered by the assumption of the Earth's mobility from his own data. Wherefore, if you procure for me that little book of Apian from elsewhere now too, you will do a not useless thing. That Jean Pena the Frenchman wrote anything about comets I do not know, but that he treated excellently of the liquidity of the heaven, and of a nature not differing from airy subtlety, and certain other philosophical matters, I have learned from others, and also from the <i>Bibliotheca Gesneriana</i>. If, therefore, I receive this author too by your good offices, you will render me a most welcome thing, and perhaps not unfruitful for our common studies. The book which you had given me at Regensburg, I know not where and when I lost; but perhaps I shall have it from elsewhere — there is no reason for you to trouble yourself further in searching it out. For Werner's book <i>On the Elements of Conics</i> and the Motion of the Eighth Sphere, transmitted to me and obtained from Master Fabricius of good memory (for I hear that he has now yielded to the fates, which too I lament), I give you very many thanks; long and much was that book sought by me, nor was I ever before able to obtain it. Although, to say what is the case, as regards the treatise on the motion of the stars, it has not in the recess what it promises in the approach, as I shall show elsewhere, when occasion is given for dealing with this matter.</p><p>These things I had to answer to what is contained in the letters first sent to me. But satisfaction must be made too to the things written afterwards in the annexed slip. You rightly warn me there that, by the tenor of the Privilege obtained through you, I ought to send three copies of my lucubrations to the Imperial Chancery — which I shall also gladly do, when the whole work, or at least the first book, has come forth, to which that diploma, together with the rest obtained from other Kings, will be prefixed; for I did not think it necessary before this, since the tenor of the Privilege is not annexed to this second part [now] published, nor have I allowed copies to be communicated to others save very few — the rest, to the number of 1500, being still reserved until the first Volume has seen the public light. You have rightly, therefore, excused this delay, for it was not fitting to offer a maimed work to the Emperor; but I shall take pains, so far as is in me, that the first book be completed at our press this winter, and then there will be time enough for me to perform what I am bound to. You ask, moreover, in the same place, that I greet the above-named Master Curtius, when I shall have sent the copies, and visit him by letters, and contract a friendship with him through these — which I shall also most gladly effect, since I understand that man to be worthy to be loved, and excellently knowing in these studies too. Lest, therefore, there be any delay in my will, if perchance before the Frankfurt fair the first book cannot be wholly committed to the press, I have now written out these [lines], which I send you to be delivered to him, that he may the sooner know my zeal and service toward him; and if this winter he writes anything back and brings forth his weightiest judgement on our labours and studies, he will bring it about that I shall be the more eager to deal with him often by letters and to confer about this sublime art. But I see that I have perhaps spoken with you too prolixly, as regards the first Epistle. Now therefore I shall turn to the rest, in which I shall be briefer, since they too of themselves do not require so luminous a reply.</p><p>From your second letter I understand well enough, most loving Thaddaeus, how anxious you were about the earlier ones, brought to me together with that book of Werner's not quickly enough; where you also report (which I attribute to your goodwill toward me) that you eagerly awaited my first Volume of the recent celestial Phenomena at the spring fair, and even now at the most recently past autumn one. But the delays you have understood in some part from my last [letter] to you; the principal cause was that I was destitute of sufficient printing-paper, nor was that which I had procured from the neighbouring Duchy of Mecklenburg brought in time and abundance enough — and also because that very [paper] which came to me thence was less white, and not elegant enough, so that what had begun to be printed on it last winter, now that better and purer [paper] has been obtained, I judge it more advisable to reprint, even though some expenses may perhaps prove wasted. For I wish my things to come forth into the hands of the learned clean and beautiful, so far as it can be done. But that I be not entangled with so many further difficulties and delays in seeking paper from Germany, I took care this summer to have a mill for making paper built on this island, with the addition too of what serves for the grinding of flour and the preparation of skins — so that a single wheel, which equals about twelve ells in height, serves all these works; the water itself too [is brought] in sufficient abundance where before it was dry, derived hither through various channels and collected in great capacity before a dam quite high and broad, with very many fishponds disposed here and there for the same use too. All which are now completed, procured with labour and diligence of execution and no middling expenses, so that on that account I hope to have henceforth paper suitable for my Printing-workshop, in whatever form I shall have wished, sufficiently — lest the procrastination of others in sending it any longer be an obstacle to sending my labours into the light. For I wish that this whole island, however small it be, may by both land and water, and indeed by the very air, serve our Urania. And let this suffice about these things.</p><p>I grieve that that incomparable man Andreas Dudith has met his last day. I had resolved to contract friendship with him by letters, and, at Praetorius's request, to send to him one of my students with an instrument suitable for astronomical observations; for he, while he was alive, had asked this of Praetorius — but I have now learned that the fates have snatched him from us, not indeed without grave loss to the Literary Commonwealth, to which he was most profitable. You add that he had long ago dealt about Wittich's books with the latter's sister, who, however, being rather difficult, was unwilling to allow any of them to be sold off. But, since <i>woman is ever a varying and changeable thing,</i> it may be that she is now more amenable to selling them. I therefore most lovingly ask that you would, through some sure and faithful man dwelling at Wrocław, see that he transact with her in my name, so that I may obtain at a just price all her stock of books and papers; I shall take care that the money promised her be paid in good time and surely — for I should not wish the books of that distinguished mathematician to perish, or to come into the hands of those who do not hold them in esteem. His Copernicus, and what he wrote about Triangles, I would seek before the rest, and also any other manuscripts there may be. There will be found among the other books that <i>Astronomicum</i> of Apian's too, of which mention was made before, which, bought for 20 florins, I gave to him as a gift while he was here, nor do I reclaim it after his death, save as bought at a just price. If his sister grants me this together with the rest, I will make you a present of that <i>Astronomicum</i> of Apian's, provided it still be there; for I myself have another copy at hand, as I indicated before too.</p><p>Your third letter contains almost nothing that much requires a reply, except that you remind me about that astrological book formerly given me by you — what the author's name was, and with what diligence (though in vain) you inquired; in which [matters] too you excuse yourself as to why that noble sent to the Emperor returned to me without your [letters], which I had however received from elsewhere before he returned hither. The fourth and last [letters], which I obtained two days ago, treat of whether the earlier ones were procured, and whether I received them. To these there is no need to reply at greater length here, since you have now sufficiently learned what you desire. About my earlier book too, when it will be published — which you long to know, at the desire of Master Curtius and your own as well — I have already said enough. As for the Commentaries of Trapezuntius, especially as regards the two first books, I would not indeed have thought it useless to publish them, but neither very necessary, since they contain nothing peculiar that was not almost said before. My Printing-house scarcely suffices for striking off what I myself have written, far less should I waste time and pains on the works of others. If you ask back that book, and someone is willing to bring it to you, I will gladly send it back on some occasion given. The things which you afterwards add in your last letter, about the new rumours destructive of politics — or rather of politicians — I do not wish to reply to, since I would rather philosophize with you. Let there be left to the Politicians their own eccentricities and anomalous motions, which they will never, in this confusion of an unclean world, regulate by any hypothesis of prudence, or render concentric; so that I cannot but marvel and at the same time grieve enough that men, microcosms, for whose sake the macrocosm with all its adornment is believed to have been chiefly founded, so greatly stray, that they can never be reduced to any fixed harmony. For one wiser than the wise errs, and there is almost nowhere any place for truth or repentance; but since God bears these things, who could amend them, why should we not patiently endure, since we cannot correct them? This at least, as regards politics, I will add: that I gladly learned from you that the most illustrious Archduke Maximilian has passed the Polish borders safe and unharmed, and returned safe to his own. For many and excellent things in praise of that Prince have been proclaimed to me by Austrian Barons and nobles who have often visited me here; and as Maro said: <i>\"More pleasing is virtue coming from a beautiful body.\"</i></p><p>But I see that, while I labour at a reply to each of your letters in this single one, I exceed the laws of an Epistle by excessive prolixity — which, on account of our friendship (which makes it that I gladly deal at length with you), and also because I wished now to recompense my so-long-interrupted reply by some little abundance, I trust will be easily excused with you. Now therefore I shall not detain you longer with my writing, but I have you commended, now and ever, to God Best and Greatest, who governs and preserves you and all your actions, with your whole family, happily and long unharmed. And if you receive these letters written out twice in the same tenor, deem it done for the cause stated at the beginning, and — I would ask you — interpret everything, according to your goodwill and love toward me, in the best part, as you are wont.</p><p>But one thing more I will say. Since I know that you take an intense delight in this subject, which treats of astronomical matters and comets, I have added to these an <i>Apologetic</i> reply, which a few days ago I wrote against the objections of a certain most learned man and distinguished mathematician, Doctor John Craig the Scot, practising medicine at Edinburgh — by which [objections] he attempted to overthrow most things, indeed the chief things, in our second book (especially as regards the ethereal position of the comet which we maintain, and as regards our hypothesis), in letters sent to me this summer, a copy of which I have had transcribed [and enclosed] together [with this]: so that — being a man otherwise most learned and endowed with sagacious judgement — he might patronize his Aristotle, to whom he seems too much addicted, as regards the sublunar generation of comets from dry fumosities, and the reality of the celestial orbs. I therefore, having got some breathing-space from the summer cares — though still distracted by very many and various occupations — yet did not think it should be wholly omitted, but that I should meet his objections by this Apologetic writing, and guard the truth once asserted and set down in writing against any assaults whatsoever, and establish it more fully. Do you, therefore, at your leisure read these things, and, if it please, re-read them — for mention of you too is made therein here and there — and afterwards open to me by letter your weightiest judgement on these matters, and, as arbiter, settle this philosophical dispute, with the weightiest censure of Master Curtius adjoined, if it be not troublesome to him. For since he is excellently skilled both in Jurisprudence and in mathematics, he will of all men best be able to take cognizance of this case and decide it with an even scale. Adjoin too the opinions of other learned men, exercised in this art and excelling in judgement, as many as you have near at hand; and if there shall be any who are willing to confer with me by letters about this arduous business — whether known, or before unknown — they will do me a most acceptable thing in this part, and one not useless to astronomical studies and to the unravelling of this question about comets. But this also I would have you besides advised of: that I have been asked by others, but chiefly by the most noble and ample Master Heinrich Rantzau, our King's Vicar in Holstein and the annexed territories, to permit the letters of the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, and of other learned men in Germany — of which, dealing with me about this subject, I have very many — together with my reply to each, to be made public; which too, since it makes not a little for the illustrating of this matter, I thought by no means to be refused. For I have resolved to publish separately, in a certain fourth Volume, all these — and any others about this business that I shall have obtained from the learned — together with our replies and Apologies, should any presume to contradict anything further. This I wished to indicate here for this reason: that everything be written to me about this matter, attentively and deliberately and luminously enough, whether by you or by others through you — which I also advised before, if I rightly remember. But it is time that I now at last draw in the sails and let the pen here rest. Wherefore again and again, fare you most happily, my most loving Thaddaeus; and see that I be revisited, before Easter, by your most learned letters, and also by those of that distinguished Master Curtius, if it be so convenient for him; and now a third time and more, farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg on the very Kalends of November, Old Style, in the year 1589.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, in his own hand.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT.</b></p><p>Since we have happened upon mention of that Imperial Diploma, there is something about which I should like to treat further with you concerning it, if it seem good to you. For that privilege seems to me too brief, and not ample or handsome enough for the magnificence of the art and the matter being treated; and I have another, granted me by the King of France, which is much more capacious and splendid — written, moreover, on parchment, and signed below by the royal hand — a copy of which I send you. Daniel Rogers too, a distinguished man and among the foremost in letters, the second envoy of the Queen of England into Denmark, when he was lately here with me, promised that he would obtain for me a privilege likewise from his most serene Queen, which, he said, would be drawn up so elegantly and honourably that it would far excel even the French one, and would likewise be engrossed on parchment, subscribed by the Queen's own hand. So too from the King of Spain, unless the excessive distance of places delay our wishes, I hope for an ample and magnificent privilege; I should not wish, therefore, that His Imperial Majesty, who holds the first place among the Kings of Europe, should suffer the privilege he has clemently granted me to be comprised in terms smaller and briefer than theirs. For that seems to me neither honourable enough to His Majesty himself, nor sufficient to the excellence of so sublime an art.</p><p>I ask, therefore, by our friendship, that you be willing to bring it about that in the Imperial Chancery a new privilege be drawn up for me, still ampler and more elegant, and that it be engrossed on parchment — if that is ever the custom — subscribed by the Imperial hand. The Magnificent Lord Curtius will readily lend both you and me his help in this matter; and since he is especially knowledgeable in, and fond of, this art, he will be able to sprinkle in something to the honour and commendation of this art and these labours, of a kind to rouse in many Kings and Princes, and in others too, love and favour for this sublime study and its votaries — which I leave to be arranged at the discretion of his own prudence and of his goodwill toward these arts and toward me (though unknown to him) and my labours.</p><p>And if it should also seem good to you both, I do not think it so necessary to mix in much mention of Religions and occasions of scandal, which might perhaps prove odious to some; for I gladly abstain from such things as could give offence to any party in Religion; nor do I willingly take upon myself judgment concerning these matters — let that rest with the Creator of all, who best knows how to separate the kids from the lambs, hypocrites from those who worship him sincerely, since he searches the hearts of all. The tumultuous World already has heresies and confusions enough; there is no need that I should found new ones, or stir up and reopen those introduced by others. The World, since it loves darkness rather, on account of its evil works, does not suffer open truth to be declared in these matters. Nor is it fitting for anyone to play the part of an Apostle unless he has been sent by divine [commission] and not by men — lest that saying apply to those who arrogate this to themselves from the light of nature alone, which is in the Prophet: <i>\"You have run, and no one sent you; you have cried out, and no one opened your mouth for you.\"</i> But this is beside my purpose.</p><p>You see now, my Thaddaeus, what it is, and why I should wish certain things in that privilege to be changed, enlarged, and amplified, and then also to be written out on vellum, so that they may be kept safer and longer. Yet I ask nothing here except what is fitting, and what you may hope to obtain in my name without difficulty or trouble. For the last one sent to me on paper I sent twelve florins as payment to the scribe, according as the Assessor had reckoned. If a new and more magnificent one is again to be engrossed on parchment, I shall be ready, even now, to recompense the labour of writing more liberally than the Assessor shall judge fitting. Yet in this whole matter I commit everything to be done, or left undone, to your counsel and help. And if it seem to you advisable, and you obtain the new privilege without difficulty, I should wish you to see to it that it be sent to me here in Denmark, even at my own expense, before the coming Frankfurt fair, so that, having received it early enough, I may be permitted to prefix it to the frontispiece of the earlier volume, and then at last see to it that both parts, once printed, are conveyed to the Emperor's Chancery, as I have undertaken to do. Again, farewell, and happily.</p><p>I ask you too, my Hagecius, that if you have kept copies — as I suppose you have — of any of the letters I have sent you, you would have them transcribed and share them with me. For I have set these aside for a volume that is to contain Astronomical Letters. Wherefore, if you send them soon, you will do me a thing especially welcome.</p><p>T. BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<p><b>Edition of record &amp; witnesses.</b> <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i> (= Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII). MS witnesses as given in the dataset: <b>A</b> = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.81, described as a draft (<i>Kladde</i>) with corrections in Tycho's own hand; <b>B</b> = Pulkovo (now St Petersburg?). The superscript ⟨A⟩ markers scattered through the source mark words/readings carrying a variant or correction in witness A; they are reproduced as ⟨A⟩ sigla in the Source block and not rendered in the translation (they are not part of Tycho's text).</p><p><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), Bohemian court physician, astronomer and astrologer to Maximilian II and Rudolf II at Prague, author of the <i>Dialexis de novae … stellae … apparitione</i> (1574). Tycho and Hagecius met at <b>Regensburg</b> in 1575 at the coronation/inauguration of Maximilian II as King of the Romans (\"about fourteen years ago, when the present Emperor was being inaugurated\") and corresponded for the rest of Hagecius's life.</p><p><b>Persons named.</b><br>- <i>Dithmarsus ex Urso denominatus</i> / <i>Ursus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Baer (\"Ursus\")</b>, of Dithmarschen (c. 1551–1600), whose <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (1588), dedicated to Landgrave Wilhelm IV, set out a geoheliocentric system. This is the foundational document of the celebrated <b>Tycho–Ursus plagiarism controversy</b>; Tycho here gives his own narrative (the 1584 visit in Erik Lange's retinue; the discarded diagram with the solar orb wrongly enclosed <i>within</i> the Martian one). Rendered \"plagiarism / Plagiarists\" per Tycho's <i>plagium / Plagiarij</i>.<br>- <i>Ericus Langius</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (c. 1553–1613), Danish nobleman and alchemist, later betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie Brahe; Ursus was in his service in 1584.<br>- <i>Gulielmus / Guilielmus, Hassiae Landgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6), dedicatee of Ursus's book.<br>- <i>Dominus Curtius</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau (Jacobus Curtius)</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Imperij Procancellarius</i>) and privy councillor to Rudolf II, himself learned in mathematics; he had received Tycho's <i>De mundi … phaenomenis</i> (the \"second book\") via Hagecius.<br>- <i>Winchlerus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Winckler</b> (Winkler) of Hall, physician-astronomer, author of a tract on the comet of 1580 which Tycho criticized; the <i>tractatiuncula</i> on \"page 446\" is Tycho's critique within the <i>De mundi … phaenomenis</i> (1588). Tycho's metaphors (<i>forti dolabra dedolatus … acriori pectine depexendus … latus claudere ultimum</i>) are kept as figures (\"hewn down with a strong axe,\" \"combed with a sharper comb,\" \"close the rank in the last place\").<br>- <i>Maesthlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), Tübingen astronomer (Kepler's teacher), whose cometary hypothesis (heliocentric, with the 1577 comet circulating about the Sun) Tycho here defends in part against Hagecius's strictures.<br>- <i>Praetorius</i> = <b>Johannes Praetorius</b> (1537–1616), professor of mathematics at Altdorf.<br>- <i>Raymundus</i> = the <i>impudens Raymundus</i> who attacked Hagecius — most plausibly <b>Raimarus / Raymarus</b> (a polemical opponent); the exact identity is not made explicit. <i>Theologastri</i> rendered \"theologasters\" (contemptuous diminutive, kept).<br>- <i>Regiomontanus Francus</i> = <b>Johannes Regiomontanus</b> (1436–1476), of Franconia, on cometary parallax. <i>Digessaeus Anglus</i> = <b>Thomas Digges</b> (c. 1546–1595), English author on the 1572 nova and cometary parallax. <i>Witichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> of Wrocław (c. 1546–1586), mathematician, whose library and Copernicus annotations Tycho here tries to acquire from Wittich's sister at <b>Wrocław</b> (<i>Vratislavia</i>).<br>- <i>Munosius Hispanus</i> = <b>Jerónimo Muñoz</b> (Hieronymus Munyos), Valencian author on the 1572 nova. <i>Cornelius Frangipani</i> = the Italian writer on the nova cited in Hagecius's <i>Dialexis</i>. <i>Apianus</i> = <b>Peter Apian</b> (1495–1552); his <i>Astronomicum</i> (the <i>Astronomicum Caesareum</i>, 1540) and a separate tract on the comet of <b>1532</b>. <i>Fracastorius</i> = <b>Girolamo Fracastoro</b>, <i>Homocentrica</i> (1538). <i>Ioannes Vogelinus Viennensis</i> = <b>Johann Vögelin</b> of Vienna, who claimed a parallax of ~35° for the 1532 comet (Tycho calls it \"monstrous\"). <i>Ioannes Poena Gallus</i> = <b>Jean Pena</b> (c. 1528–1558), French mathematician, on the fluidity of the heavens.<br>- <i>Wernerus</i> = <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg, <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (and the tract on the <i>cornicula</i> — see crux below). <i>Dominus Fabricius bonae memoriae</i> = <b>Paul Fabricius</b> (1519–1589), Imperial physician-mathematician at Vienna, here reported newly dead; he had obtained Werner's book for Tycho.<br>- <i>Andreas Duditius</i> = <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (1533–1589), humanist and former bishop, recently deceased; Tycho mourns him.<br>- <i>Trapezuntius</i> = <b>George of Trebizond</b> (1396–1486), whose commentaries (on Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i>) Hagecius had asked about publishing.<br>- <i>Archidux Maximilianus</i> = <b>Archduke Maximilian III of Austria</b> (Grand Master of the Teutonic Order, <i>der Deutschmeister</i>), the Habsburg claimant in the Polish succession war of 1587–88, defeated and captured at Byczyna (1588) and released in 1589 — Tycho rejoices at his safe return.<br>- <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (Vicar) in Holstein, scholar and Tycho's patron-correspondent, who urged the publication of Tycho's learned correspondence (realized as the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i>, 1596).<br>- <i>Ioannes Craig Scotus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician at Edinburgh (later to James VI), the Aristotelian critic of Tycho's cometary doctrine against whom Tycho wrote the enclosed <i>Apologetica responsio</i>.</p><p><b>Places.</b> <i>Praga</i> = Prague; <i>Hafnia</i> = Copenhagen; <i>Uraniburgum</i> = Uraniborg on Hven; <i>Ratispona</i> = Regensburg; <i>Rostochium</i> = Rostock; <i>Lubeca</i> = Lübeck; <i>Hamburgum</i> = Hamburg; <i>Vratislavia</i> = Wrocław (Breslau); <i>Megapolensis ducatus</i> = the Duchy of Mecklenburg (\"the neighbouring Duchy\"); <i>Edenburgum</i> = Edinburgh; <i>Holsatia</i> = Holstein.</p><p><b>Astronomy — technical (glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>acronychus / in transitu acronycho</i> → <b>acronychal</b> (rising at sunset / opposition to the Sun) — kept as the technical term; <i>oppositio Solis</i> → opposition to the Sun; <i>elongatio</i> → elongation; <i>Dodecatemorion</i> (pl. <i>Dodecatemoria</i>) → <b>dodecatemory</b> (one zodiacal sign = 30°; \"two dodecatemoria\" = 60°, a sixth of the circle) — kept and glossed; <i>retrogradationes et stationes</i> → retrogradations and stations; <i>Epicyclus / Aequans / deferens</i> → epicycle / equant / deferent; <i>octava sphaera</i> → the eighth sphere (of the fixed stars); <i>aux</i> / <i>Aequinoctiorum reciprocatio</i> → the reciprocation (trepidation) of the equinoxes; <i>prosthaphaereses</i> → <b>prosthaphaereses</b> (kept per §5.1); <i>Epocha commutationis mediae</i> → the Epoch of the mean commutation; <i>Radix motuum</i> → the root of the motions; <i>Canones Coperniani / Alphonsini</i> → the Copernican / Alphonsine Canons (tables); <i>hypothesis systematis mundani</i> → the hypothesis of the world-system (Tycho's geoheliocentric model — \"this neoteric invention of ours,\" devised \"about six years ago,\" i.e. c. 1583). <i>De mundi … phaenomenis</i> (the 1588 comet book) is referred to throughout as <i>liber noster secundus aethereorum Phaenomenωn</i> — note the Greek-spelled genitive <i>Phaenomenωn</i> (omega), kept as printed.</p><p><b>Greek.</b> <i>τεκμήρια</i> (<i>tekmēria</i>) — \"evident proofs / sure signs\"; transcribed and translated. <i>ἀδύνατον</i> (<i>adynaton</i>) — \"impossible\"; transcribed and translated (\"is not so <i>impossible</i>\"). Both are kept in the Source block.</p><p><b>Astrology.</b> <i>Centiloquium</i> — the pseudo-Ptolemaic <i>Centiloquium</i>; Tycho quotes its maxim that \"love and hatred pervert the judgements of many\" (a recognized aphorism, here paraphrased rather than verbatim — flagged). Planet glyphs named in English with the glyph kept in the Source block per §5.3: ♂ Mars, ♄ Saturn, ♀ Venus, ☿ Mercury.</p><p><b>Alchemy / Hermetic.</b> <i>Archeus</i> (<i>Archaeo cooperante</i>) → <b>Archeus</b> kept (the Paracelsian inner formative spirit/principle); <i>terrestria … astra</i> → \"terrestrial stars\"; the <i>Hermes Trismegistus</i> maxim <i>quicquid est superius, idem esse inferius</i> = the \"as above, so below\" tag of the <i>Tabula Smaragdina</i>, here paraphrased — rendered literally and flagged. The whole passage is a genuine Tychonic cosmological-alchemical excursus (Earth as passive recipient of celestial influence, forming subterranean \"stars\" via its <i>Archeus</i>); rendered without modernizing.</p><p><b>Verse / sententiae (kept as verse / quotation).</b><br>- <i>Sic vos non vobis mellificatis apes</i> — the famous Virgilian (<i>Maro</i>) tag \"Thus do ye bees make honey, but not for yourselves\"; left as the half-line + <i>etc.</i> as Tycho gives it.<br>- <i>Fortis et ad cursum magno cum robore fertur, / Si tamen instiges, acrius ibit equus</i> — an elegiac couplet (Ovidian in flavour), set as verse.<br>- <i>Est aliquid prodiret tenus, si non datur ultra</i> — Horatian sententia (\"It is something to advance just thus far, if it is not granted to go beyond\"), kept as quotation.<br>- <i>Hoc opus, hic labor est … quos ardens deduxit ad ardua virtus</i> — a Virgilian allusion (<i>Aeneid</i> 6.129, \"hoc opus, hic labor est\") fused with an <i>ardua virtus</i> tag; rendered with the quoted phrasing kept in italics.<br>- <i>Difficilia, quae pulchra</i> — proverbial (\"the difficult things are the beautiful\"); kept.<br>- <i>ut est varium et mutabile semper femina</i> — Virgilian (<i>Aen.</i> 4.569–70, \"varium et mutabile semper femina\"); kept as quotation. Misogynist register preserved as in the source, not softened.<br>- <i>Gratior est pulchro veniens e corpore virtus</i> — Virgilian (<i>Aen.</i> 5.344); kept as quotation.<br>- <i>corvi Aesopici … alienis plumis</i> — the Aesopic crow adorned in others' feathers; kept as the fable allusion.</p><p><b>Measure / economy.</b> <i>quartam Tonnae auri partem</i> → \"a quarter of a tun of gold\" (<i>tønde guld</i> = a Danish money-of-account, 100,000 <i>rigsdaler</i>; thus Tycho claims to have spent well over 25,000 <i>rigsdaler</i>); kept literally and glossed. <i>20 floreni</i> → 20 florins (the price of the Apian). <i>duodecim … ulnas in altitudine</i> → \"about twelve ells in height\" (the paper-mill water-wheel); <i>ulna</i> = ell, kept.</p><p><b>Self-description (the Hven paper-mill).</b> The passage on the <i>molendinum pro papyro conficienda</i> — the paper-/grist-/fulling-mill Tycho built on Hven in 1589, with its great wheel, dammed water supply and fishponds, \"that the whole island … may serve our Urania\" — is a documentary set-piece; rendered literally.</p><p><b>Calendar / dating (per protocol §3 — no silent conversion).</b><br>- <b>Header dateline.</b> The subscription reads <i>Datae Uraniburgi prim. Cal. Novemb. stylo veteri Anno 1589</i>. <i>Cal. Novemb.</i> = the <b>Kalends of November = 1 November</b>; <i>prim.</i> is read as <i>prima(s)/primis</i> (\"on the very Kalends\"), giving <b>1 November 1589 Old Style</b>, in agreement with the dataset label \"1589. 1. november.\" (Note: <i>pridie Cal. Novemb.</i> would mean 31 October; the abbreviation here is <i>prim.</i>, not <i>prid.</i>, and the label confirms 1 November — so the Kalends reading is followed and no discrepancy arises.) Tycho explicitly states he writes <i>stylo veteri</i> (O.S.); Denmark used the Julian calendar.<br>- <b>In-letter dates are reported, not converted.</b> Tycho carefully tracks the style of each incoming letter: Hagecius's first written <i>Cal. Novembris veteri stylo anno 88</i> (1 Nov. 1588 O.S.), received 27 April [1589]; the second written 10 May [O.S. implied], received 31 August; the <b>third written <i>20. Maij novo stylo</b></i> (20 May N.S.) — Hagecius in Imperial/Catholic Prague used <b>New Style</b>, and Tycho notes the contrast — received mid-September; the fourth dated 7 September, received 30 October (\"the second-to-last [day] of October\"). Tycho stresses that his own datings are <i>secundum veterem anni Iuliani numerationem, qua etiamnum nos utimur</i> (\"according to the old Julian reckoning, which we still use\"). The 10-day O.S./N.S. gap is exactly the calendar matter §3 flags; <b>no date here is silently reconciled</b> — they are reproduced as Tycho gives them.<br>- <i>ante Pasca</i> → \"before Easter\"; <i>Francofurtianae nundinae</i> → the Frankfurt (book) fair; <i>vernales / autumnales nundinae</i> → the spring / autumn fairs.</p><p><b>Named cruxes.</b><br>1. <b>The Werner title (<i>liber Werneri de elementis cornicis</i>).</b> The phrase <i>de elementis cornicis</i> is obscure; <i>cornicis</i> (gen. of <i>cornix</i>, \"crow,\" or of <i>cornicula</i>) does not yield an obvious sense for a Werner mathematical tract. Werner's known work is <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (Nuremberg 1522), which Tycho names in the same breath. <i>cornicis</i> may be a corruption/abbreviation in the witness, or a reference to a now-obscure subsidiary tract. Rendered tentatively as \"On the Elements of the <i>Cornicula</i>\" with the Latin kept; <b>flagged, not smoothed.</b><br>2. <b>\"prim. Cal. Novemb.\" abbreviation.</b> Read as <i>primis/prima Calendis Novembris</i> = 1 November; secure given the label agreement, but the unexpanded <i>prim.</i> is noted for the record (it is not <i>pridie</i>).<br>3. <b>Identity of <i>Raymundus</i>.</b> The <i>impudens Raymundus</i> who attacked Hagecius is named only by this single word; the specific person is not securely fixed here.<br>- <b>What the recovered text is.</b> The bulk of the postscript to Tycho's 1 November 1589 letter to Thaddaeus Hagecius, entirely absent from the prior transcription (only the closing valediction after it survived as the published tail).<br>- <b>Recipient.</b> Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), Emperor Rudolf II's imperial physician at Prague, Tycho's friend and correspondent since they met at Rudolf's 1575 coronation as King of the Romans; Hagecius had earlier given Tycho his treasured manuscript of Copernicus's unpublished <i>Commentariolus</i> [Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Christianson, <i>Measure of the Heavens</i>].<br>- <b>Subject.</b> Tycho's campaign to secure printing privileges (early-modern copyright protections against piracy) from multiple European monarchs for his forthcoming publications. He already held one from the King of France, on parchment with the king's own signature, and was expecting one from the King of Spain; <b>Daniel Rogers</b>, styled here \"the Queen of England's second Legate\" to Denmark, had promised to obtain an English one — this matches Thoren's account that \"Tycho entrusted his hopes for an English copyright to Daniel Rogers, who came to Hven in 1588\" pursuing exactly this [Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>]. Rogers died in 1590, within a year of this letter. Tycho now asks Hagecius to use his friend <b>\"Magnificus Dominus Curtius\"</b> to obtain a grander Imperial privilege from the Prague Chancery, on parchment, under the Emperor's own hand — this is very likely the same Curtius (probably the Bohemian chancellor Jacobus Curtius) in whose Prague house Tycho would later live and die, per Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890).<br>- <b>Religious reticence.</b> Tycho explicitly asks that the privilege not dwell on \"Religionibus et scandalizationibus\" — a rare direct statement of his deliberate avoidance of confessional controversy in his printed apparatus, leaving judgment of hypocrites and true worshippers to God alone; he closes the point with an unattributed prophetic saying about running unsent and crying out unheard, which echoes (without being verbatim) the Vulgate topos of the false, unsent prophet, cf. Jeremiah 23:21 (\"non mittebam prophetas, et ipsi currebant\") — flagged as a crux below.<br>- <b>The Epistolae astronomicae.</b> Tycho asks Hagecius to return transcripts of his own letters \"for a volume that is to contain Astronomical Letters\" — a direct, seven-years-early reference to the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>, printed at Uraniborg in 1596 [Nielsen, <i>Dansk Bibliografi</i>, no. 430], documenting the collection process for that book.<br>- <b>Cost of the document.</b> Tycho records paying 12 florins to the scribe for the paper copy of the privilege, per a \"Taxator\" (official assessor)'s valuation, and offers to pay more liberally for a parchment original — a concrete data point on the cost of engrossing an official instrument in Tycho's world.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Dreyer's reading in three places on TBOO VII pp.213 and 218: 'conicis' for the corrupted 'cornicis' (Werner's treatise on the conic elements, not an invented '<i>Cornicula</i>'), 'numero 1500' for an erroneous 'numero 150' in the count of Progymnasmata copies still held back from circulation, and 'Astronomicas' for the ungrammatical 'Astronomicus'. The English translation was corrected to match: the fabricated title '<i>the Cornicula</i>' now reads 'Conics', and the reserved-copy figure now reads 1500.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — A long, technically dense scholarly letter rendered securely throughout; register (learned, courteous, polemical) and Tycho's hedges (<i>forte</i>, <i>uti existimo</i>, <i>si recte memini</i>, <i>quod sciam</i>, <i>ut spero</i>) are preserved. The two flagged textual cruxes are the obscure Werner title <i>de elementis cornicis</i> (crux 1) and the unidentified <i>Raymundus</i> (crux 3); the planet/zodiac glyphs and the two Greek words are transcribed and glossed; no date has been converted or corrected. These local uncertainties keep the grade at [B] rather than [A], but none affects the argumentative substance (the Ursus plagiarism narrative, the geoheliocentric defence, the Maestlin/Hagecius cometary-numbers dispute, and the book/library business are all secure).</p><p>The Latin itself is Tycho's clear, polished epistolary prose with no evident corruption, and the translation throughout is confident. Two identification points remain open rather than translation problems: (1) the \"Propheta\" quotation (\"Cucurristis, et nemo misit vos...\") is an evident echo of the unsent-prophet topos rather than a verbatim Vulgate citation, and I have not been able to pin its exact source with certainty — flagged as a crux; (2) the \"prioris tomi\" to whose frontispiece the new privilege was to be prefixed is not named in the passage itself, and I have not identified the specific Uraniborg publication in progress in late 1589 with confidence from the material at hand. Upgrade path: collate against the printed front matter of the 1596 Epistolae astronomicae (which anthologises much of this correspondence) and, for the biblical tag, a critical edition or commentary on the Hagecius correspondence. Grade B: sound and complete, with two flagged, non-fatal points of uncertainty.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 24 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.196-218, slice pages 1-24, in six chunks of 4) and collated them line-by-line against the full source_text and published_english (62,481 and 72,079 characters respectively, ~53 paragraphs). Verified opening salutation and closing signature/postscript verbatim; verified every proper name (Hagecius, Erik Lange, Ursus, Wilhelm of Hesse, Curtius, Winckler, Raymundus, Maestlin, Praetorius, Regiomontanus, Digges, Wittich, Munoz, Frangipani, Apian, Fracastoro, Vogelinus, Pena, Werner, Fabricius, Dudith, Maximilian, Trapezuntius, Craig, Rantzau, Rogers); every date (27 April, 10/20 May, 7 September, anno 82, ante quinquennium/triennium, 24 November, 26 January); every number/sum (14 years, 4:6 ratio, quarter-tun of gold, 20 years, 15/35/33/3.25 degrees, pag.206/86/133/275, sextans, tria exemplaria, 150 copies, 20 florenis, 12 florenos, 12-ell wheel); and every place-name (Prague, Copenhagen, Uraniborg, Regensburg, Rostock, Lubeck, Hamburg, Poland, Wroclaw, Mecklenburg, Edinburgh, Holstein, England, France, Spain, Denmark, Frankfurt, Vienna). Apart from the two source-text corruptions noted (both isolated single-word slips, one of which also corrupted the translation into a fabricated book-title), the translation is exceptionally faithful across the full 24-page letter: negations, hedges, disjunctions (aut...aut), and the dense argumentative passages on Mars parallax, the Tychonic system, and the Ursus plagiarism affair are all rendered with the correct sense, subject, and antecedent. No omissions or fabricated clauses were found.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads 'cornicis' where Dreyer prints 'conicis' - Werner's real treatise is 'De elementis conicis' (on conic elements/conic sections), a known historical work; 'cornicis' is a corrupted transcription. Dreyer prints: <i>Pro libro WERNERI de elementis conicis et motu octavae sphaerae mihi transmisso... (TBOO VII p.213, line 2)</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Downstream of the source_text corruption, the translation fabricates a nonexistent title 'the Cornicula' instead of rendering Werner's actual, identifiable work as 'the conic elements' / 'Conics'; a scholar relying on this translation would go looking for a non-existent treatise. At: “For Werner's book On the Elements of the Cornicula and the Motion of the Eighth Sphere”.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has the ungrammatical 'Astronomicus' where Dreyer prints 'Astronomicas' (agreeing with 'Epistolas'; this is Tycho's own project, the later-published Epistolae Astronomicae). The English translation itself correctly renders 'Astronomical Letters,' so this defect does not propagate into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>Ego enim volumini, quod Epistolas Astronomicas continebit, huiusmodi destinavi. (TBOO VII p.218, line 39)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All four fixes were collated directly against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.212-215 and pp.216-219 (tboo.slice_pdf, read in two 4-page chunks). The packet's three listed defects — the 'cornicis'/'conicis' TEXT_ERROR, its downstream 'Cornicula' MISTRANSLATION, and the 'Astronomicus'/'Astronomicas' TEXT_ERROR — are all directly confirmed by the page images and now fixed. In addition, per the task brief's explicit instruction to verify a '1500/150 reserved-copies error' not present in this packet's defects list, I located the relevant passage on p.213 line 19: Dreyer clearly prints 'numero 1500', while both current_latin and current_english read '150' — a genuine, previously-undetected TEXT_ERROR, now fixed in both languages and logged as a fourth outcome. No other passage on the collated pages disagreed with Dreyer, and nothing outside these four sites was altered.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1589 (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/81", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001764"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.81. Kladde med rettelser af Tycho Brahe", "B Pulkovo (Nu i Sankt Petersborg?)."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 18, "prev": "brahe-t_15890520001", "next": "brahe-t_15891130001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890520001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15891130001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges four letters from Hagecius received over the summer, detailing each one's dispatch and arrival dates and noting the constant delays in transmission.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891101001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891120001", "d": "1589-11-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Uraniborg, 20 November 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer,</i> Opera Omnia *VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DOCTISSIMO VIRO MAGISTRO ANDREAe WELLEIO, CANONICO RIPENSI, AMICO SUO DENTUR.<br>S.</p><p>SI vales bene est, ego cum meis (quod Deo acceptum refero) mediocriter me habeo. Miror, te multo tempore nihil ad me dedisse literarum neque cum HELIA meo quidpiam scripsisse. Sicut prorsus mei immemor factus? Da operam, ut exarationem quandam eorum habeam, quae in Privilegio Danico commode continebuntur, de quibus aliquoties tecum egi, sed Iutica fides in verbis quidvis etc. Cuperem hac hyeme habere, nam primus Tomus mei operis iam sub prelo est. Moletrina papyro conficiendae destinata unâ cum annexis iam parata est, et aqua adest in sufficienti copia non saltem hyberno, sed etiam aestivo tempore, omniaque rite se habent, saltem chartopaeus defideratur. Nam is, qui ERICO inservierat, quem Lubecam pro veteramentis conquirendis misi, nusquam postea comparuit. Si talem Artificem illic expiscari poteris, mitte illum huc mature, pro suo labore debite remunerandum. Distantias omnium locorum in Cymbria et alibi in Dania, quotquot hactenus adeptus es, mihi describi cura et quamprimum huc mitti. Rectius erit, si unius loci a duobus alijs, qui triangulum ferme effecerint, intercapedinem denotaveris, quoties id fieri queat; tum etiam sylvae, flumina, portus, littora, anfractus et similia, qua fieri potest diligentia, designanda veniunt. Effice ut cito habeam, nam praeterquam quod Daniae chartam aliquando per ocium emendate conficiendam mediter, in hoc etiam quem nunc imprimi curo libro, in tabella quadam, ubi longitudines et latitudines diversorum Europae locorum correctiores, quoad eius fieri potest, ob meridianorum differentias discernendas comprehenduntur, Patriae etiam nostrae insigniora quaedam loca secundum longum et latum verificata apponere animus est. Fac itaque, ut nulla sit in te mora, quin propediem quae desidero obtineam, et per hunc ipsum nuncium vel quemvis alium obvium rescribe. Vale et METTAM tuam unâ cum utroque Parente caeterisque Ripensibus amicis saluta.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi die 20. Novemb. 89.<br>Tuus<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To be delivered to the most learned man Master Anders Vedel, canon of Ribe, his friend.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>If you are well, it is good; I, with my household — for which I give thanks to God — am moderately well. I wonder that for a long while you have sent me no letters, and have written nothing even by way of my [servant] Elias. As though you had become quite forgetful of me? See to it that I may have some draft of those things which can conveniently be contained in the Danish Privilege, about which I have dealt with you several times — but \"Jutish good faith in words [is] anything you please,\" etc. I should like to have it this winter, for the first volume of my work is now under the press. The mill destined for the making of paper, together with its appurtenances, is now ready, and water is at hand in sufficient supply not only in the winter but also in the summer season, and everything is in good order; only a papermaker is wanting. For the man who had served Erik — whom I sent to Lübeck to seek out old rags [for paper] — has nowhere appeared since. If you can fish out such a craftsman thereabouts, send him here in good time, to be duly rewarded for his labour. Take care to have the distances of all places in the Cimbrian peninsula [Jutland] and elsewhere in Denmark, as many as you have so far obtained, written out for me and sent here as soon as possible. It will be more correct if you note the interval of one place from two others which form roughly a triangle, as often as that can be done; then too the woods, rivers, harbours, shores, windings [of the coast] and the like ought to be marked down, with what diligence may be possible. Bring it about that I may have it quickly, for — besides that I am planning at some time, at leisure, to make a corrected map of Denmark — in this very book also, which I am now having printed, on a certain table, where the longitudes and latitudes of various places of Europe are set down more correctly (so far as that can be done) for distinguishing the differences of the meridians, it is my intention to add certain of the more notable places of our homeland too, verified according to longitude and latitude. See to it, therefore, that there be no delay on your part, but that I shortly obtain what I desire; and write back by this same messenger or by any other that comes to hand. Farewell, and greet your Mette together with both her parents and the rest of your friends at Ribe.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 20th day of November, [15]89.<br>Yours,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Magister Andreas Wellejus, canonicus Ripensis</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the Danish historian and royal historiographer, canon of Ribe (Lat. <i>Ripa</i>); Tycho's old friend from their student years. Tycho's request for distance-measurements supports both Vedel's and his own cartographic and historical projects.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-castle on the island of Hven (protocol §5.7). <i>Ripa</i> = <b>Ribe</b>, in Jutland; <i>Cymbria</i> = the <b>Cimbrian peninsula</b>, i.e. Jutland (the humanist name from the Cimbri). <i>Lubeca</i> = <b>Lübeck</b>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Helia meo</i> — \"my Elias,\" evidently a servant or messenger of Tycho's (the diminutive-affectionate <i>meus</i>); not further identified here. <i>Erico</i> — \"Erik,\" likewise unidentified; the man Tycho mentions had formerly been in this Erik's service. Both are flagged as unidentified rather than guessed at.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (dicit), \"greeting\" (protocol §5.5). The address-line <i>…AMICO SUO DENTUR</i> (\"let it be given to … his friend\") is the exterior direction/superscription.</li><li><b><i>Privilegium Danicum</b></i> — \"the Danish Privilege\": a printing privilege (royal licence/copyright) for Tycho's works to be issued under Danish authority; Tycho wants Vedel to draft the text of what it should cover. Rendered literally \"Danish Privilege\" and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Iutica fides in verbis quidvis</b></i> — \"Jutish good faith in words [is] anything you please\": a proverbial gibe at the unreliability of Jutlanders' promises (Vedel was a Jutlander, of Ribe), Tycho twitting his friend for his long silence. The clause is elliptical (no main verb); rendered with a bracketed copula and the <i>etc.</i> kept, the proverbial force flagged rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Papermaking.</b> <i>primus Tomus mei operis iam sub prelo est</i> — \"the first volume of my work is now under the press\": the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (begun printing at Uraniborg c. 1588–89). <i>Moletrina papyro conficiendae destinata</i> — \"the mill destined for the making of paper,\" Tycho's own paper-mill at Hven. <i>chartopaeus</i> (= <i>chartopoeus</i>, Gr. χαρτοποιός) — \"papermaker,\" the craftsman who makes the sheets; rendered \"papermaker.\" <i>veteramenta</i> — \"old rags / cast-off cloth,\" the linen rag-stock from which hand-made paper is beaten; rendered \"old rags [for paper].\" <i>Artifex</i> — \"craftsman.\"</li><li><b>Cartography / surveying.</b> <i>Distantias … describi … unius loci a duobus alijs, qui triangulum ferme effecerint, intercapedinem</i> — \"the distances … the interval of one place from two others which form roughly a triangle\": Tycho is requesting <b>triangulation</b> data for a map of Denmark. The phrasing describes the triangulation principle without using a single technical term; rendered descriptively. <i>anfractus</i> — \"windings, bends [of the coastline].\" <i>Daniae chartam … emendate conficiendam</i> — \"a corrected map of Denmark.\" <i>tabella … ubi longitudines et latitudines … ob meridianorum differentias discernendas</i> — \"a table … where the longitudes and latitudes … for distinguishing the differences of the meridians\": a geographical coordinate table planned for the book under the press. <i>secundum longum et latum verificata</i> — \"verified according to longitude and latitude\" (<i>longum/latum</i> here = the long/lat coordinates).</li><li><b><i>Mettam tuam</b></i> — \"your Mette\": <b>Mette</b>, Vedel's wife (rendered with the Danish given-name form, since the Latin accusative <i>Mettam</i> simply Latinises the Danish name). <i>utroque Parente</i> — \"both her parents\" (taken as Mette's parents, the natural antecedent of <i>tuam</i>); the Latin <i>uterque Parens</i> could in principle mean Vedel's own two parents, but the possessive attaches to Mette — the reading is noted.</li><li><b><i>manu propria</b></i> — \"in his own hand,\" the autograph subscription formula.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 20. Novemb. 89</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 20 November [15]89\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline date agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1589. 20. november. Uraniborg</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Textual note.</b> <i>defideratur</i> (line: <i>saltem chartopaeus defideratur</i>) is a printer's/copyist's spelling of <i>desideratur</i> (\"is wanting/lacking\"); kept as printed in the Source block, rendered \"is wanting.\" MS witness A is a later copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Tycho's private archive, not an autograph original.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the dateline is unproblematic. Soft points, all flagged above rather than smoothed: (1) the identities of \"Elias\" and \"Erik\" are not recoverable from the letter; (2) the elliptical proverb <i>Iutica fides in verbis quidvis</i> has no expressed verb and is rendered with a bracketed copula; (3) <i>utroque Parente</i> is taken as Mette's parents on the strength of the possessive, with the alternative noted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the Jutish-good-faith proverb, the paper mill and papermaker passage, the Denmark map/distances instructions, greetings to Mette Vedel) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII p.219 as a page image. Exact match throughout, including 'Iutica fides in verbis quidvis' rendered as a flagged proverb. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15851111001", "next": "brahe-t_15901005001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Requests Vedel's overdue draft of the Danish Privilege for the first volume now at press, and asks help finding a papermaker for the newly ready mill.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891120001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891124001", "d": "1589-11-24", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 24 November 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek technical terms) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET ERVDITISSIMO<br>VIRO, D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO,<br>ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS WILHELMI LANDTGRAVII HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO<br>SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. S. D.</p><p>ECQVID id est mi ROTHMANNE, quod tam inuito animo accipias, adeoque sinistre interpreteris meas vltimo ad te datas literas, nimisque rhapsodice ad eas respondeas, cum amicae inter nos collationis, atque veritatis in abscondito latentis penitius eruendae gratiâ, bona intentione scriptae sint: Neque enim ego te vspiam in illis impietatis accusaui. De pietate vel impietate cuiusuis, nullum mihi arrogo iudicium, Id scrutatori cordium omnium, vti par est, relinquens. Nec etiam te authoritati sacrarum literarum, quasi data opera, aliquid detrahere absolute dixi, saltem quod Mosis verba de Luna, luminari minore, incompetenter ad corporis, ipsis plaerisque Stellis reuera minoris, magnitudinem transtuleris, cum potius de lucis Terras contingente praeeminentia accipienda veniant, te admonui. Rationibus quoque vt existimo non incongruis adiectis. Quod etiam Prophetis nimium deroges, eos non plusquam vulgus imperitum in Astronomicis et Physicis intellexisse asseuerando, vtvt diuinitus, immeditate instructi fuerint, et praeterea antiquiss. Patrum visibilem a DEO conditarum rerum excolentium traditiones non paucas, nostris procul dubio ab Ethnicis acceptis certiores et meliores in promptu habuerint, non approbandum duxi, in qua etiamnum persisto sententia. Neque enim vnquam leuiter de his decernendum censui. Multa quidem et non posthabenda, de ijs quae Physice in sacris literis continentur, nuper etiam secundum vulgarium Philosophorum ratiocinationes, explicuit Franciscus Vallesius, neque absque solerti, quo ad ea principia, labore; Licet et multo plura in his longe aliter secundum veriorem Physicam, forte quoque ex his ipsis non minima de parte potius petendam, enucleanda delitescant. Quin et Lambertus Danaeus, vir sane apprime eruditus, et de instituendis alijs optime meritus, Physicam suam Theologicam, tam ex veteri quam nouo Codice non inutili labore extruxit. Possentque fortasse multa de his aliâs dici, quae peculiare et reuera Metaphysicum, non quale nobis Stagyrita iste Graecus obtrusit, requirerent volumen. Literas tuas quin satis caperem, nec morbus, licet et ille grauis erat, corporis tamen non ingenli, nec occupationum, quamuis et hae non paucae implicationes, adeo vt putas, impedierunt. Nulla enim in illis irresolubilia latebant aenigmata, re omni dudum satis perspecta; Nec quicquam tibi obijcio, quod ex literis illis colligi non possit, imo forte plusquam opus est, parco. Conferantur saltem tuae et meae, dijudicentque alij, affectibus non indulgentes, neque sibijpsis nimium placentes. Quatenus sacris literis contraria induxeris, et antea et iam aliquo modo indicaui, te vbique studiose id facere non dixi. Nec ego licet Theologiam non profitear, neque quispiam eorum qui eam solide et sincere exercent, tuis assertionibus e Dedomenis sacrarum literarum assentiri possumus: Frustra itaque praetendis hoc diuerticulum, Cum duo dicunt vnum, non tibi concedi vt sint vnum, Neque enim alijs concedendum duco, quod hîc reprehendo. Augustinum satis libere de sacris nonnunquam pronuntiasse, interdum non incomposite, interdum vero frigidiuscule pro eâ temporibus Apostolorum viciniore aetate, non equidem Dei gratiâ sum ignarus. Sed quid ista omnia huc conferunt, aut ad rem de qua agitur, faciunt. Quaestio mouebatur, an mobilitas Terreni Globi, eademque triplex a Copernico asserta reuera constare possit, anque illa inuentioni nostrae praeferenda foret, et vtrum sacrae literae illius imaginationi contrariarentur, necne: Si quid igitur habes e sacris Oraculis, aut horum Interpretibus, vtpote Augustino vel alijs Patribus, pro hac Copernicea assertione, tuaque astipulatione faciens, ex eorum scriptis id cita. Modo iustae fuerint rationes, ijs non refragabor. Id satis scio, Augustinum, quem solum nominas, motum Terrae, neque annuum neque diurnum vsquam concessisse, ex quo rotunditati eius tanquam minus Mathematicus, Antipodas denegando, detraxerit. Quod tamen illi condonandum est. Quare cum rotundam esse non perceperit, motum etiam rotunditati competentem longe minus admittere potuit. Theologorum vestratium opinationes, et de his censuras, etsi extra eorum forte Professionem, minusque deliberatas, non equidem contemno. Sed cum videam illos tantum hoc nomine accersitos atque interrogatos, an liquiditas Caeli e sacris literis, sine orbium realitate astrui possit (prout tua clausula innuit) cumque scias me etiam longe antequam de his somniâris idipsum concessisse, atque pronuntiasse, miror, cur haec nihil huc facientia obijcias. Neque enim existimo vestris Theologis de terna Terrae volubilitate propositam esse quaestionem, cum paucissimi ex illis, eam etsi quam maxime vellent, caperent, vtvt Terrenis, quemadmodum et alibi fit, non parum forte addicti. Cum igitur de mobilitale Terrae ex Aristarcho et quibusdam alijs veteribus per Copernicum renouata introductaque controuersia solummodo inter nos mouebatur, qua quaeso cohaerentia impulsus eam cum limpiditate Caeli confudisti, quam ego nunquam denegaui, sed primus forte omnium id ita se habere non saltem asseuerare, sed e certissimis demonstrationibus probare elaboraui. An hoc est Mathematicum, disparia coniungere atque implicare. Quod ne quidem Dialectici, siue Artificiales, siue naturales in diuersis terminis admiserint.</p><p>Liquidissimam et vbique peruiam esse Aetherei Mundi substantiam e sacris literis probari posse, vel ipsis silentibus Theologiae Professoribus, iam dudum compertum habeo. Multaque in eius rei testimonium consignaui diuinitus patefacta Oracala. Quin et Clarissimus ille, deque Doctrina publica egregie meritus vir D. CASPARVS PEVCERVS literis ad me hoc praesertim nomine datis, plurima sacrarum literarum dicta, huc facientia collegit, meaeque sententiae circa tenuissimam, limpidissimam et nihilominus superelementarem Caeli materiam erudite atque egregie subscripsit. Idque potissimum, vt Hypothesium mearum inuentionem minime absurdam esse inde quoque comprobaret. Quando ego meas de Caeli materia vbique peruia nullisque orbibus solidis conferta, vt in gratiam veterum hactenus creditum est, et nihilominus Aëre Elementari atque animali velut Ioannes Pena Gallus, tu et quidam alij promulgare audetis, non coinquinatas ratiocinationes plenius, quam adhuc factum est, in publicum edidero, alijsque dijudicandas concessero, vtique sacrarum literarum, quae primas in omni sapientia stabilienda, atque scientia condenda obtinere merentur, autoritatem citare, vel te non monente, nequaquam intermissurus sum. Dei enim solius gloriam in omnibus spectandam semper censui, id quod et tu recte approbas, cum veritas absque ipsius patefactione et dono introspici nequeat; Omne enim donum perfectum a Deo. Meam quintam Essentiam, quam Aetheri, apud Chymicos locutione non ignota, neque veteribus etiam Philosophis, adeoque ne ipsi Aristoteli (licet in his non vbique satis perspicaci) improbata, tribuo, salse mihi exprobare videris; Sal vero dulce, limpidum, omnique terrestreitate defaecatum, exaltatum, nullaque ex parte insipidum, aut graueolens, sed perpetuitati Circulari analogum, nondum degustati; vbi olfeceris, aut saporem eius perceperis, tum primum de his sententiam profer; Interea satius est immaturo supersedere praeiudicio. Rogas, ne de Crepusculis te caecum efficiam, neque temeritatis accusem, quasi illa scribas, quae non expertus sis. At ego nec te occaecare, nec temerarium pronuntiare vnquam praesumsi; Vtinam et me et te atque alios a caligine mentis, qua plusquam credimus offuscati sumus, temeritateque Mundana liberari liceret, aeque atque oculorum externam imbecillitatem, vel Chymicis praesertim medijs emendare non vsque adeo difficile est, sane in eo, quo ad fieri posset, quamplurimum elaborandum censerem. Neque enim opera illa inutiliter collocata foret. Scire te ais Crepusculum desinere, Sole P. 17. sub Horizonte depresso, vulgariter Crepusculum pro luce dubia accipiendo. Existimas itaque me vulgo saltem approbatam et hactenus in Astronomorum libris contentam opinionem venditare, nihilque e proprijs aduersionibus huc contulisse; At quae haec est insimulatio. Tamdiu ne tecum per literas egi, vt adhuc me veritatis esse studiosum, neque aliunde quam vel iusta ratiocinatione atque experientia, vel quod potissimum est, patefactione diuina eam conquirere, neque data opera falsum pro vero astruere, incompertum habeas. Sufficienter colligere potuisti, me ne in maioribus quidem, et ad rem Astronomicam plus facientibus caecutire velle, nedum vt in Crepusculis, facilimo negotio animaduertibilibus, irrita et Antiquorum saltem sententiae vulgari colludendo quippiam citra propriam animaduersionem pronuntiarem. Crepusculum cum nulla Aëris pars vndiquaque circa Horizontem vel minimum albescat, determinandum, satis sciebam, quod etiam tum a me ipso, tum a mihi septenis vel octonis ad minimum in rebus Astronomicis inseruientibus studiosis, quam diligentissime aliquoties factitatum est, ita vt nullum, ne quidem minimum vestigium Aëris a Sole illuminati in vlla citima Horizontis superficie dignosci potuerit, quando tempora Crepusculorum denotata sunt; Adhibitis Stellarum Aequatorijs a Meridiano remotionibus, Solisque exacto loco, et omnibus per trutinam Geometricam diligenter expensis, tibique si oculos aperire velles, eandem ob caussam, ne pro libito aut vulgari opinione constituta, persentisceres, liberaliter communicatis. Si itaque tu duobus oculis, et binis quoque saltem Organis acrius haec discernere potes, quam nos omnes sedecim ad minimum luminibus, totidemque et pluribus sicubi opus fuerit, Instrumentis, equidem hanc lincaeitatem atque mediorum modicam requisitionem tibi non inuideo, miror magis. Imo maius adhuc acumen plenioremque sufficientiam congratularer. Tu itaque de Crepusculis, tuo sensu abundando tibi quicquid libuerit, persuade, Ego ea quae indicaui, et pluribus etiam experimentis satis rata esse, nullique verae refragationi obnoxia, minime haesito. Vide saltem ne aliorum non indubias animaduersiones eleuando, vt tuis assertionibus de Aëris atque Aetheris consimili existentia (quam magna ex parte ob vaporum in diuersis Horizontibus discrepantiam, et in Borealioribus magis quam Australibus incrassatam sublimatamque probare conaris) patrocineris, pluribus vaporibus, obscuritatibus atque nebulis veritatem ipsam, ad quam tendere deberemus, inuoluas, quam omnes circa Horizontem terrestrem, quouis in loco, siue Australi siue Boreo densae et fuscae vaporositates. Proinde, cur Refractionibus non ad amussim subscribam, nescire vis, cum alias per Instrumenta eruere non potueris, ideoque nihil hîc in mei gratiam faciendum et propterea iniqua me postulare hoc postulando, asseueras. At quando ego a te vnquam petij, vt posthabita veritate, quidpiam in mei gratiam proferres, aut adeo iniqua vrsi? id enim reuera iniquum foret, si hoc fecissem. Imo multo potius de veritate ipsa penitius enucleanda semper admonui, cum haec sit virtutum omnium pene pulcherrima praestantissimaque. Refractiones a te assertas, cur omni ex parte approbare nequeam, et mea me docent Instrumenta: Si in vno atque altero, ijsdemque saltem bicubitalibus, vtvt ex mea inuentione profectis, et per Wittichium vobis propalatis, neque etiam tunc satis maturis, haec et alia quam ego plusquam vicenis Organis, plerisque eorum quadricubitalibus et quincubitalibus, pluribus quoque adhibitis Obseruatoribus visus acumine satis valentibus, accuratius collimare solus potes, est quod tibi et hanc praerogatiuam non inuideam; Quin et sic erit, cur mihi gratias agas, quod te per a me excogitata Instrumenta (neque enim id iam ignoras) tam perspicacem reddiderim. Id saltem tibi persuade, quemadmodum in palestrica Arte fieri solet, nullum Athletam Progymnastis suis, ea confestim indicare, quae sibi ipsi in exercitio isto defectu aliquo vnquam obessent; Sic etiam nec Wittichium nec vllos alios omnia mea, quae veritati peruestigandae ad amussim sunt necessaria ita diuulgasse, quin adhuc potiori ex parte apud suum Autorem sarta tecta maneant. Frustra itaque de tuis Instrumentis atque Obseruationibus nimium gloriaris: Forte si hîc esses omniaque ipsemet per longe plura minimeque dubia Organa, vel tuis solius oculis, me praesente intuereris, aliud diceres. Sed et Refractiones tuas nimium modicas esse et Crepuscula nimis lata, non satis inter se conueniunt. Oportet itaque in alterutris vel vtrisque errorem non modicum latere, aut sane Aërem in hoc parum Borealiore loco, quantum ad Crepuscula attinet, aliquanto defaecatiorem, quam illîc esse, quo ad Refractiones vero turbidiorem, id quod vel te ipso iudice simul consistere nequit. Attende igitur his diligentius, vel si minus lubet, aut in procinctu non est, acquiesce saltem aliorum minime dubiae decisioni neque hîc tibi quidpiam immature decernendum praesume. Titulum libri mei alias in dubium vocatum, aut etiam attentatum, nunc approbas, nec amplius reprehensione dignum aestimas; Licet illum quem allegasti, non publice ediderim, aut vlli libro praefixerim, sed saltem in literis quibusdam ad Illustriss. Principem tuum datis illum obiter annotârim. Gratulor itaque et tibi et mihi, quod tam pulchre resipueris, antea enim obiecisti, me corruptionem Caelo introducere, cum generationis illic circa Cometam vocabulo vterer, nimis rigide verba mea vrgendo. At vide potius, ne de industria nunc asserendo, Cometas in Caelo generari, alicubi praeter opinionem pecces, non tam Grammatice quam Physice, modo vocabuli proprietatem retinere deceat. Quod non assertiue sed dubitanter atque interrogatiue ista proposuisti, vt meam sententiam plenius explicarem, id nihil apud me noui est; Nam et Wittichius et plaerique alij sic mecum egerunt, obijciendo ea quae nullum mererentur dubium, vt alia quaedam expiscarentur. Verum postquam ego in eo, tum etiam alijs idipsum mature experirer, cautior factus sum, vtvt non pauca in Germania hincinde meis sumptibus et laboribus comparata, idque Dei solius gratiâ ab alijs pro suis etiamnum venditentur, dictitenturque: Quod parui facio. Neque aegre fero aliquos riuulos ad Exteros deriuari, ipso fonte penes suum Dominum adhuc Dei beneficio scaturiente. Non tamen sunt falsa proponenda, vt cognoscantur vera; Vera enim et falsa sunt dissentanea, nisi quis, vti apud Geometras fit, cum assertiue nihil probari possit, per negationem, rem in absurdum deducat. Sic etiam quae de Cometarum cauda proposuisti, tum in scripto illo tuo de Cometa Anni 85. tum literis ad me datis nunc reuocas, neque te illa tanquam vera vrsisse, sed tantum, vt meo iudicio subijceres, asseris. Licet vero et scriptum illud et literae tuae quiddam aliud prae se ferant, attamen quod et hîc in viam redieris bene me habet. Placet enim, quod ratiocinationes tuas et quae ex Illustrissimi Principis Obseruatione de Cometa Anni 58. comprobationis loco adduxisti, quam parum valida sint (ex quo ista tibi in superioribus literis ob oculos posuerim) iam satis videas, adeo vt te ista non scripsisse, ipsemet, vti apparet, malles. Quae excusandae istius Obseruationis caussa vltra ea quae ego produxi, adfers, apud me facilem merentur locum, qui non tam Obseruationi aliquantulum deuiae, sed potius, quod tu hanc non satis circumspecte nimium vrseris, omniumque aliorum in plurimis Cometis inuenta hinc eludere conatus sis, imputem. Rogas vt ego haec non publicem, vtque tuam de Cometarum generatione sententiam scriptis meis non immisceam. Atqui te hîc celare non possum, me per literas rogatum a Nobilissimo viro D. HENRICO RANZOVIO, Vicario Regio in Holsatia, tum etiam Clarissimo et Eruditissimo D. CASPARO PEVCERO, Philosopho eximio, atque quibusdam alijs non ineruditis viris id expetentibus approbantibusque, peculiari Tomo vno vel altero Epistolas Astronomicas mihi a varijs hincinde perscriptas, vna cum mea ad singulas responsione publicem. Quod et illis non denegandum duxi. Cumque dictus RANZOVIVS principaliter Illustrissimi Principis tui, atque tuas Typis consignari expetisset, et per se mererentur, vt priorem et principaliorem voluminis partem occuparent, iamque liber ille sub Praelo sit, ea quae alijs addixi, rursus in tuam gratiam detractare et facta infecta reddere nequeo. Nec video cur id a me expetendum fuerat, curque id concederem, cum nihil hac in re, tuo Illustrissimo Principi, quod obsit, aut vlla ex parte deroget, sed saltem quae in ipsius Celsitud. praedicationem, vti par est, potius cedant, admittatur. Nec etiam tibi quicquam hinc creatur periculi aut detrimenti, cum honorifice semper de te loquar et sentiam. Quod si tu quaedam in prioribus scriptis et literis proposita, postea mutas, et re rectius deliberata corrigis, tuae existimationi hinc nihil decedit, etiamsi priora publicentur. Imo praestat alios cognoscere, quam difficile sit, etiam ingeniosis et industrijs viris veritatis latebras extricare, et quod non omnia quae prima fronte recte se habere putantur, vndiquaque rite constent. Quodque dies diem doceat, et posteriores cogitationes, vt plurimum, licet non semper, priores antecellant. Reuocârunt alij admodum eximij viri multa a se prius minus considerate pro veris habita, etiam in ijs materijs, vbi non adeo difficilis erat atque hîc veritatis peruestigatio. Quando ex professo circa totius Operis Epilogum, quod de Ascititijs Caeli Phaenomenis prae manibus habeo, Cometarum naturam et procreationem, iuxta omnium quotquot mihi innotuerunt opinationes disquiro, hanc etiam tuam, cum alijs quibusdam communem praetermittere, vt quam incongrua sit, quod et alias tibi scripsi, ne indicem, haud possum. Attamen cum videam te in his teipsum corrigere atque priores conceptiones antiquare, faciam eius quoque illic mentionem, idque conuenienter excusabo. Ego tuum scriptum de Cometa publici iuris non faciam: Nihilominus nonnulla hinc citare atque expendere in laudabilem tui mentionem potius cedet, quam quod reprehensioni erit. Dicis quidem me pugnaturum cum muto, si id agam. At non mutus eras cum haec scriberes, literisque ad me datis consignares; Sunt enim notae scriptae, praesertim tua propria manu exaratae, pro ipsis verbis et loquela, imo et plus merentur ponderis. Sic etiam quantum ad meas literas attinet, quas publici iuris reddere, nisi rogatus ab amicis, vt dixi, nunquam constitueram, cum saltem obiter, prout in buccam venerat, inter plurima negotia exaratae erant, scias me non adeo earum penitere, quin tibi liberum relinquam, siue priuatim, siue publice, quocunque libuerit modo, quae in his contenta sunt, discutere, probare vel improbare. Si quid iustis rationibus, vel tu vel quispiam alius non rite in his alijsue meis constare sufficienter demonstrarit, ego illa corrigere non erubesco. Neque ego cum praeiudicio vllo, quippiam in tuis disquirendis aggrediar. Vide vt eodem candore circa mea quoque vtaris, nec iuuenili quodam feruore sententiam immaturam pronunties. Gratulor tibi, quod ex cogitatione materiae Stellarum et viae lacteae ad aliam opinionem diuerteris circa Cometarum conformationem, quam ea erat, qua antea imbutus parum Physica, longeque minus Mathematica: Es enim hîc in bona via, si eam ingredi callueris, omniaque hospitia noueris. Nec enim satis mirari poteram, qua iudicij nebula tantas absurditates astruere, vt corpus Cometarum, vel teipso fatente, prope Reuolutiones Saturninas a Terris distans (vt de eo qui Anno 85. apparuit, consignasti) ideoque necessario et Terra et Mari omnibusque circumfluis vaporibus multis parasangis maius existens, prout ferunt Geometricae demonstrationes, nihilominus hinc in supremum Aethera ascendisse, nescio quo incognito raptu, et partem esse suo toto multo maiorem, pronuntiare ausus sis; idque prorsus contra rationes Mathematicas. Si Physicis ita sentire lubet, coniungant has naenias caeteris. Bene igitur superinducis, quod ea, quae de Trabibus discurrentibus, quarum forte occasione in tam absona prolapsus es, cum reuera huc nihil conferant, tantumque haec inuicem distent, quantum Caelum et Terra, prorsus falsa esse deprehenderis. Habebam in animo, te vltimis literis et de his admonere, quod quomodo neglexerim, non recordor. Nam in scripto tuo de Cometa Anni 85. vbi talia asseuerare conaris, ad marginem memoriae caussa haec annotâram, quae vel tunc vel alias per opportunitatem commonefacienda duxi. Halitus isti siue Chasmata longe sunt nobis propiora, quam quod hyberno tempore a Sole nimium infra Horizontem depresso, illuminari queant, veluti ratio docet Geometrica et Optica. Quin et Anno 1588. eius rei euidens feci periculum die 6. Decemb. circa octauam vespertinam. Animaduerti enim tunc accurata inspectione Chasmata quamplurima hincinde versus Septentrionem volantia albicantiaque, ita vt lumen quoddam vmbram etiam Terrae illustrans prae se ferrent, longe nobis viciniora esse, quibusdam densioribus nubibus circa Horizontem Boreum segregatim dispersis, adeo vt Chasmata illa inter nos et nubes, discurrere euidenter apparerent, ita vt et alij ex meis, quibus monstrabam, id satis perspicue discernerent. A Sole igitur versus Antipodas submerso, ista vaporum illuminatio fieri nequibat; cum ne nubes quidem longe a Terra remotiores ipsius lumen percellere potuerit, quod facile Geometrice haec mensurando patebit. Nubes autem illae, de quibus loquor, non fuerunt continuae, sed circa Horizontem sparsim dispositae, ad Altitudinem, quo ad visibilem Horizontem, circiter graduum 10. Cum igitur Radij Solares partem vmbrae Terrae nobis adeo propinquam in ista eius sub Horizontem demersione non contingant, frustra asseritur, per illuminationem ab hoc quippiam eiuscemodi contingere. Simile quid saepius consideraui, praesertim hoc ipso Anno die 2. Ianuarij, cum quoque circa octauam vespertinam, quando plurima ad Septentrionem hîc spectabantur Chasmata, quae nubibus longe propiora cernebantur. Quapropter frustra hinc aliquid subsidij petijsti, pro innaturali illa Cometarum generatione stabilienda. Atque si scire cupis, quid ego de his Chasmatis sentiam, Sulphureitatem quandam Terrestrem in propinquo Aëre ardentem, discurrentemque, et nonnunquam vsque ad verticem ascendendo totum Caelum complentem, in modica tamen a visu nostro distantia, esse iudico, ventorum aut siccitatis et frigoris breui subsequentium praenuntia; interdum etiam, si saepius contingant, nimisque diu durent, Aëris ad contagium Epidemicae luis propensi indicia. Habet enim morbus iste cum Sulphurea natura non parum commune, vnde etiam per Sulphur terrestre excellenter depuratum exaltatumque, praesertim si in liquorem gratum reclinetur, expeditius soluitur, tanquam simile suo simili. Neque enim id Galenicorum semper verum est: Contraria contrarijs curari. Haec de his te nunc commonere placuit. Rectissime itaque feceris, si totum illud caput, de materia Cometarum, eorumque illuminatione, vti spondes, mutaueris, et consideratius tractâris. Nec opus est, vt alius adhuc expectetur Cometa. Nam illi qui vel nostro Aeuo proxime praeterierunt, priorem tuam opinionem nequaquam consistere posse affatim conuincunt; id quod te nostrae in his animaduersiones facile edocebunt, modo aliquid fidei mereri poterint. Sin autem nostras decisiones, licet ea, qua fieri potuit diligentia conquisitas, nec vti spero citra rem prolatas, non tales esse, vt ijs tuto nitendum existimes; agedum expecta adhuc alium Cometam si quis interea occurrerit, et eum diligentius, modo id tibi concessum fuerit, demetiendo perscrutare. Si tunc aliquîd diuersi a nostris conclusionibus adinueneris, satis erit temporis, id quicquid erit in medium proferre; interea si iudicium suspenderis, modo nostris acquiescere displiceat, non inconsultum erit. Planetas in subtilissimo purissimoque Aëre pendere (licet id admodum improprie dicatur, cum reuera non pendeant) quod vlterius mecum disceptare nolis, libenter accipio: beneque facis, Caeli materiam non amplius animalem et elementarem vocando. Nihil enim hîc vnquam apud me obtinebis. Si meae rationes, partim prolatae, partim alibi vlterius declarandae, et vel ex inferioribus palpabilibusque rebus, superioribus tamen analogis, allatae, tibi non sufficient, vt quicquid velis sentias, satis per me licet. Tracta hanc quaestionem vbicunque libuerit, et siue me immiscueris siue non, idem erit. Ego meam sententiam satis probabiliter tueri, quando hac de re agendi occasio dabitur, non supersedebo. Iudicium doctissimorum virorum et rem ipsam intelligentium nullatenus subterfugio; Neque enim ego ipsemet mihi tantum arrogo, vt solus litem hanc dirimere praesumam. Philalithiam semper in pretio habui, maiori forte meo incommodo (quod tamen non aegre fero) quam emolumento. Neque praeiudicij fascino, vt imputare videris, vnquam me offuscari, data opera, permisi: Quantum enim mihi diuinitus concessum est, verum a falso discernere, perpetuo annixus sum. Si tertiam aliquam personam appellas (video enim te Iuridice mecum in re Mathematica agere, nam et Frater tuus retulit te ad Iuris prudentiam inclinare, forte quod haec magis lucrosa et Mundo spectabilior sit) et ego conditionem accipio, lubenterque prouoco ad quosuis in Germania vel alibi Philosophos, harum rerum capaces. Vtque denominetur aliquis; ad Clariss. Excellentissimumque virum D. CASPARVM PEVCERVM, si ita lubet, rem omnem desero, cum is et Philosophiae et Mathematicarum disciplinarum in primis sit gnarus, Theologiaeque studio, vnde haec quaestio magna ex parte decidi poterit, non segniter addictus, et simul iudicio aetateque graui praeualeat: Nec, tanquam meis partibus nimis fauorabilis Iudex sit futurus (scis enim, si Iurisperitus esse vis, fauorem Iudicis plus valere, quam decem leges scriptas in Codice) tibi suspicandum venit, nunquam enim mihi notus est: At cum tuo Illustriss. Principe, quem suum optimum benefactorem, in literis ad me datis, nec immerito vocat, et tecum etiam praesens non ingratam contraxit amicitiam. Si qui sunt alij inter vestrates, vel etiam alibi, ad quos appellationem instituere vis, et ego eosdem appello, modo rem de qua agitur, penitius introspexerint, atque tam superiora quam inferiora intelligere, haecque inuicem tanquam vnum ex altero cognoscentes conciliare didicerint. Quicquid enim est supra, id etiam infra, vt inquiebat Hermes ille vere inter Mortales Trismegistus. Trinitatis absconditum latere in omnibus rebus creatis mysterium recte mihi astipularis, quamuis tua de his Philosophia quam adducis, magis sit exterior, quam interna, nimisque sensibus obuia; Cum id quod potissimum est, et in quo rei nucleus versatur, prima fronte non appareat, Non enim satis est dictum, ex Terra, Aqua et Aëre omnia inferiora constare, cum et hae ipsae matres alia quaedam terna habeant principia, Philosophis vulgaribus nondum satis perspecta, de quibus nunc non est disputandi locus. Subiungis deinde te videre, quod iam tandem obtinueris, me Diaphanitatem Aëris puri, nihil differre a Diaphanitate materiae Planetis circumfusae, tibi concessisse, nec ob id dubitas, quin veritas temporis filia me reliquam etiam tuam sententiam suo tempore sit edoctura. At quibus vnquam perspicillis haec ita acute vidisti? Aut quibus modis e scriptis meis id vnquam colligere potuisti; Miratus sum ista legens, cur astruere velis, me mihi ipsi contraria hac in parte proponere; Inspexi itaque denuo Exemplar literarum tibi vltimo inscriptarum, e quibus procul dubio haec colligere volebas. At ego nihil eiuscemodi in illis inuenio, imo potius tuae assertioni plane contrarium. Dedi illic primo Exemplum e rebus inferioribus quae beneficio Artis Spagyricae in tantam subtilitatem exaltari queant, vt Diaphanitatem, quantum sensibus discernatur, consimilem repraesentent, et nihilominus diuersae esse substantiae ostendi, Ideoque non sequi id, quod tu intendebas, etiamsi concederetur, ob Refractiones ad verticem non tendentes Caeli et Aëris purioris quo ad Diaphanitatem inperceptibilem esse discrepantiam, propterea ex vna et eadem constare materia. In hunc modum tecum ratiocinatus sum, neque ob id concessi Aëris et Aetheris Diaphanitatem omnino eandem esse: Imo haec verba aliud indicantia subiunxi. Sic quoque Caeli et Aëris Diaphanitatem, si vel prorsus eaedem forent, quod tamen nondum satis obtentum est, nihilominus diuersam substantiam atque materiam habere nihil impedit; Cur igitur quaeso gloriaris, te a me id obtinuisse quod ego apertis verbis dixi nondum satis obtentum esse? Cur adeo iniuriose mea inuertis? Curque iudicium de his Placitis tam inconsiderate praecipitas? Si veritas Temporis filia me vnquam melius informârit, vtique veritatem acceptare non tergiuersabor; idque quacunque tandem occasione concessum fuerit. Necdum tamen video, cur vllis tuis opinionibus, de quibus inter nos controuersum est, assentiar: Neque euidenter demonstrabis, me quippiam eorum, quae ad te hactenus vel obiter scripsi, aut aliâs emisi, quod vllius sit momenti, retractasse, Quemadmodum tu non pauca vel in his ipsis literis, antea acriter defensa, non saltem in dubium vocas, sed prorsus irrita esse cupis. Vt te non asseruisse Prophetas in Physica nihilo plus calluisse, quam vulgus iners: Et Mosen de duobus magnis luminaribus loquentem contra Geometricas Demonstrationes fecisse, quasi hoc non esset detrahere sacris literis: Quaestionem de motu Terrae Copernicaeo motam, cum materia Caeli implicare, atque hanc ex Augustino atque alijs Neotericis Theologis, confundendo Caelum et Terram niti probare: Te titulum Tomorum meorum de Cometarum caelesti generatione non reprehendere, cum antea obieceris me corruptionem in Caelum introducere, si generationem astruam. Cometarum caudas tot verbis, tum etiam ex Obseruatione aliqua, antea tanquam partes corporis essent, ex eâdem materia constantes asseuerâsse, et iam non amplius ea pro veris habere, imo ipsam Obseruationem perscriptam infectam cupere: Totam de Cometarum generatione sententiam, prius adeo vel propter Graminaeum quendam, mihi obtrusam, nunc ita posthabere, vt integrum caput ea de caussa in scripto tuo mutandum, aliterque tractandum censeas: Trabes discurrentes, antea a Sole illuminatas, nunc lumine eius orbare, et si quae sunt alia Corollaria. Nolo enim omnia nimis scrupulose eruere. Haec tamen non ideo recenseo, quod te deuiationes tuas agnoscere atque emendare exprobrare velim. Id enim laudo, et vtinam saepius sic feceris, teque a veritate Temporis filia, vti recte dixisti, instrui permiseris. Sic centrum Circuli quod est vnicum, collineatius attigeris. Si ostenderis vnquam me aliqua scripsisse quae reuocatione indigeant, gratias habebo; Me quicquam meorum adhuc retractasse nec dum sum conscius; non quod id fieri nequeat, vt sumus homines, sed quia quod tu in nostris scriptis te videre dicas, nondum meis patuerit oculis, neque id adhuc agnoscam. Comparatio illa, quam proposui, tibique aliquatenus explicui, inter liquorem Aquae et Vini, vtroque depurato, respectu materiae Aëris et Caeli, forte maiorem merebatur acceptionem, quam vt ita exciperetur. Reuera enim Caelum ita se habet ad Aërem Elementarem, sicut Spiritus aliquis Vini vel alterius generosi liquoris ad Aquam defaecatam. Et licet Diaphana pene eadem sint, tamen substantia longe diuersa. Neque scio an melior comparatio e rebus terrestribus deduci possit. Hinc est, quod quidam Philosophantium Vini praesertim Spiritum aliquoties modeste exaltatum depuratumque, ac tandem circulatione continua atque diutina ab Elementari intemperantia liberatum, Aether appellare, vel Caelum Philosophicum non dubitârint, quamuis an id ea ratione fieri queat, qua illi proponunt, vt quaedam alia non desiderentur nunc dicere nolim. Id saltem aperte affirmare ausim, in singulis terrestribus substantijs aliquid latere caelesti naturae analogum, in quibusdam plus, aliquibus vero minus, quod nec Aërem, nec Aquam, aut Terram, aut etiam Ignis Elementum (si id in rerum natura foret) proprie sapiat, quamuis et haec Elementaria singulis vna insint grossiori quadam compagine: Neque existimandum venit, a Sole saltem vel reliquis Sideribus, hanc vim superelementarem elementaribus inesse, licet et hoc aliqua ex parte non negârim. Sunt enim quaedam his, quaedam alijs Sideribus magis conformia, Interim tamen et vniuersalem Caeli essentiam in qua hae Astrales virtutes habitant, non aliter quam Sidera ipsa in Caelo suae naturae competenti voluuntur, quaelibet inferiora, quo ad magis vel minus aemulantur. Sed haec de his nunc sit disseruisse satis, cum ad vulgo receptam Philosophiam non pertineant, atque illic forte vix recipiantur. Hypotheses Ptolemaicas vel Copernianas, vtram harum approbaueris, per me tibi liberum erit. Suaderem saltem, vt de Caelo e Caelo ipso, non ex Chartis et ingeniosis Demonstrationibus, ipsi Caelo forte non vbique consonis, iudicium formares. Liceat itaque et mihi, quid me Caelum in his edocuerit, libere pronuntiare. De Surreptore mei Systematis nolo hîc verbum addere, neque enim dignus est. Respondi tibi breuiter in vltimis, ad ea quae huic nostrae Hypothesium innouationi obiecisti; pleniorem resolutionem eiuscemodi et aliorum dubiorum obtinebis, quando ex professo de motu Secundorum Mobilium egero, eundemque quo ad numerationem ipsi Caelo ad amussim conformem reddere elaborâro, et vt (fauente Caelorum opifice) confido, ad optatum finem rem omnem deduxere. Si quae plura interea excogitâris, quae in contrarium adferri posse existimes, libere illa proferendo, rem non ingratam feceris. Nonnunquam enim contradicendo Veritas illustrior redditur. Occasiones quae me ad talem Caelestium Reuolutionum ordinationem impulerunt, tibi etiam breuiter indicaui. Neque enim temere, aut leui de causa huc perductus sum, sed ipsum me Caelum ita de se sentire docuit, nullo alio Praeceptore aut vllis libris, vel Tabularum Farraginibus quippiam huc conferentibus. Contuli etiam cum quibusdam alijs in Germania eruditis Mathematicis de hac nostra Neoterica inuentione, quae licet quibusdam Aristoteleis praesertim Orbium realitatibus nimium addictis, non admodum arrideat, attamen cordatioribus et qui sine praeoccupata sententia rem ponderant, rectiusque introspiciunt, admodum concinna censetur. Quamuis vt dicam id quod res est, aliorum iudicia licet non contemnam, tamen siue pro vel contra faciant, non multum moror, modo ipsa caelitus deducta experientia rem ita se habere euidenter conuincat. Scio quam abstrusa semper fuerint Naturae penetralia, quamque ea quae in Physicis nos rite perspecta habere arbitramur, longissime saepenumero a scope aberrent. Idcirco non tam aliorum Autoritati atque applausui, acquiescendum existimo, quin nos ipsos an res ita se habeat, vel secus, periculum facere, et Obseruationum ac Demonstrationum trutina omnia expendere, atque comprobare deceat. Sed de his nunc non est locus pluribus disserere. At quoniam video Copernicaeam circa Triplicem Terrae motum sententiam tibi admodum arridere; proponam in quolibet horum vnicum saltem, licet multo plura dari queant, non adeo operosum dubium. Primo quantum ad Diurnum circa suum axem motum, quo Terra 24. Horis conuolui fingitur, sicque vniuersalis cursus ab ortu in occasum excusatur, attinet; Dic mihi, quî fieri possit, vt globus plumbeus ex altissima turri iusto modo demissus, punctum Terrae infra se positum perpendiculariter ad amussim contingat; id enim circumducta interea Terra, cum cursus eius sit velocissimus, fieri nequaquam posse, te supputatio docebit Geometrica: Siquidem in vno Scrupulo secundo temporis Terra reuolui debeat etiam in his Borealioribus plagis sesquicentum passus maiores proxime. Hinc caetera ratiocinare. Neque enim casus plumbi Aërem concomitatur, sed violenter illum transit. Circa alterum motum Terrae Annuum, qui octauam Sphaeram tantopere remoueret vt orbis quem haec designaret respectu eius euanescat, Edissere, anne hîc tibi verisimile videatur, spatium quod est a Sole, tunc centro vniuersi, ad Saturnum plusquam 700 vicibus contineri intra hunc et Affixarum Sphaeram, idque totum nullis Sideribus refertum. Atqui id fieri necesse est, si saltem Orbis Annuus Terrae instar vnius minuti apparebit. Imo tunc quoque Stellae Fixae Tertiae magnitudinis, quae vnum minutum in Diametro habent, necessario erunt aequales toti huic Orbi Annuo, id est comprehendent in Diametro 2284. Semidiametros Terrae. Distabunt enim 7850000 ijsdem Semidiametris proxime. Quid dicemus de Stellis primae magnitudinis; quarum aliquae bina, quaedam fere terna minuta in Diametro visibili occupant; Et quid si adhuc altius remoueatur octaua Sphaera, vt motus Terrae annuus illic prorsus euanescat? Deduc si lubet, haec Geometrice, et videbis quanta absurda, vel sic inferendo (vt de alijs non dicam) assumtionem hanc concomitentur. Tertius, sublato Annuo, per se ruit; vel si vna cum illo tibi constare posse videatur, quî quaeso fieri poterit, vt Axis Terrae in contrarium motui centri annuatim adeo correspondenter gyretur, vt quiescere nihilominus appareat? Quomodo etiam Axin et Centrum duplici diuersoque motu agitari datur, in corpore vnico et simplici? Vt de tertio illo circa Axem diurno superueniente, nihil replicem. Taceo quoque librationum intricationes, nec promotioni Fixarum cui destinantur vbique correspondentes, valde absonas esse. Haec et similia tute tecum perpende, atque si dabitur, per ocium resolue. Plura habebis aliâs. Si igitur Copernicea ratiocinatio constare non poterit, vt sane non potest, Ptolemaica, quâm Copernicus (vt alia taceam) satis refutauit, nihil impediet quo minus nostra inuentio locum mereatur. Exponis demum caussas cur Illustriss. Princeps tuus meis non responderit, quas et libenter accipio. Scriberem nunc ipsius Cels. sed nihil occurrit, praeter ea quae vltimis consignaui. Simulque vereor ne ipsius C. Reipublicae administratione alijsque curis occupatam meis ieiunis literis molestem. FRISCHLINVS de quo scribis, huc non venit, neque literas vllas a te misit, alias non inhumaniter a me excipiendus. Audiui enim virum esse variae literaturae et facundum. Facis insuper mentionem Molendini Papyro conficiendae idonei, quod me extructurum indicaui, ne toties et tam diu charta impressoria pro meis lucubrationibus excudendis, e Germania afferenda frustrer, idque Principi tuo placuisse ais, quod facile credo. Nam et in his confluentiam quandam inuicem habemus. Quemadmodum enim ipsius Cels. Architecturâ, Aggeribus, Fossis, Aquaeductibus, Piscinis, Hortis et Molendinis similibusque Mechanicis structuris conficiendis delectatur, sic et ego pro meo modulo, praesertim Aestiuo tempore, quando altioribus vacare non licet, haecque commodius tractantur. Nam et illa Geometricum quid redolent, si modo debita Symmetria conficienda sint. Molendinum illud iam paratum est, aggere altissimo latissimoque sufficientem Aquam tam Aestiuo quam Hyberno tempore cohibente, Rotaque 12. vlnarum in Diametro minima Aqua impulsa, et duplex praeter Papyraceum opus peragente. Piscinis etiam quamplurimis hinc inde ordine dispositis, vt quando necesse fuerit, hae quoque Moletrinae Aquas suppeditare queant. Haecque omnia ante paucos annos sicca erant. Quin et alterum Molendinum pro Rusticis meis et opificibus, in vicinia circumhabitantibus hîc molior, vbi etiam nulla pene Aqua in promptu nunc putatur. Nolo enim, vt alij Chartaceas operas et quae ad propria mea domestica pertinent, perlustrent atque intempestiuius interturbent. Si Victorinus Schonfeldt huc filium suum miserit me inuisendi caussa, per me illi id licet: Quamprimum illi vel mihi ita videbitur, vt ad suos remeet, vtrique satis liberum erit. An promotus fit in Magistrum liberalium Artium nec ne, apud me perinde est, Mallem tamen illum talem esse, quam appellari. Sed hoc non tam leui pretio prostat; sufficeret harum discipulum esse. Sed video mi Rothmanne me in respondendo tuis strictioribus et festinanti calamo, vti apparet, exaratis literis fuisse nimis copiosum, quod eo promptius admisi, ne tuam paucitatem (tanquam retaliationi nimium indulgendo) vel prorsus nulla, vel etiam breuiore rescriptione vlcisci velle viderer. Licet enim ego et pluribus nunc, quam tu istas scribendo vel forte vnquam alias implicer negotijs, tamen haec tuis quin responderem adeoque luculenter nullis alijs impediri volui occupationibus; quod et in vltimo datis praeter morbum quem significabam, reuera ita se habuit, vt tanto interlapso tempore tibi procul dubio et commodius minusque aduersum rescribendi suppetierit otium. Si tamen quid in his liberioris admonitionis scriptum est, in meliorem partem accipe et communium studiorum Amori tribue. Vale et si vacat, iam statim rescribe, vt ad Nundinas proxime instantes Deo volente responsum denuo nanciscaris. Has interea festinanti quoque calamo exaratas boni consule.<br>Iterum feliciter vale.<br>Datae Vraniburgi 24. Nouemb. Anno 1589.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, pp. 199–200; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. QVONIAM ex literis vestris, tum etiam aliunde percepi, ILLVSTRISSIMVM PRINCIPEM LANDGRAVIVM, suas aliquot Annorum in affixis Stellis obseruationes publici iuris facere decreuisse, tuaque opera in hoc negotio exsequendo vti: Rogo, per Astronomiae honorem &amp; commodum, vt labores hos, Herculeis forte non minores, maturare non pigeat, ne nimium procrastinando, aut prorsus intercidant, aut vix tandem in commune Artis huius promouendae emolumentum, lucem publicam videant. Hinc enim, tam illi, qui modo sunt superstites, quam posteri, quos tam sublimium cura rerum tangit, facile decernent, quam erroneis inerrantium Stellarum locis, caeloque minus congruis, tot iam sectilis ob Artificum siue penuriam, siue incuriam, hactenus Vsi simus, cum certiora in promptu non fuerint: viaque simul constare queat, Quantae molis erat Stellarum inquirere Hermas: Et, vt collatis vestris animaduersionibus cum meis, quas propediem (fauente Stellarum opifice) publicare constitui, quanta accuratior &amp; laborum constantia res haec vtrinque exantlata sit, dignoscas. Nam quod vestrae restitutiones pene in omnibus Stellis, quasi vnciola Vnius gradus, a nostris abundando, differant, id suam apud eos, qui rei perplexam difficultatem intelligunt, facile excusationem merebitur. Neque etiam aliter fieri poterat, quin Stellarum a vobis praefiniti limites, nonnihil distantiam genuinam ab aequinoctii puncto excederent, cum solummodo per Stellam Veneris vespertinam (nec item Eoam) haud etiam satis crebro, idipsum exsecuti sitis, refractione Venerem tunc in longitudine, paululum iusto vlterius oculis insinuante. Vnde consectarium est, Stellas ab hac per intercapedinem deriuatas, etiam longitudinibus, magis quam opus erat, accrescere: vt nihil dicam de lubrica illa obseruandi via per Asimutha &amp; Altitudines, vna cum temporum ex horologiis minus tuto obtentis denotationibus, quibus omnibus vos, vti non frustra suspicor, nimis secure confiditis. Verum, quia in primo nostro Torno Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum, capite secundo, vbi de Stellarum fixarum exacta verificatione, ex professo ago, quomodo non solum e Venere occidentali a Sole, sed etiam eadem orientali, Stellarum termini in Vnum &amp; eundem locum tandem ad amussim recidentes, &amp; sibijpsis apprime analogi a nobis constituti sint, abunde exponam: simulque, post octingentarum circiter, canonicam in certa loca, quoad longum &amp; latum distributionem, Landgrauianae quoque in his rectificationis, &amp; cur illa propemodum 5. vt indicam, minutis, nostram fere vbique exsuperet, mentionem nonnullam interseram. Nolo nunc pluribus idipsum hic inculcare. Tu saltem da operam, vt vestris hac quoque in parte Atlanticis laboribus, eorum cupidi propediem fruantur. Iamque iterum vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Christoph Rothmann, distinguished mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his most beloved friend — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>What is the meaning of this, my dear Rothmann, that you take so unwillingly, and interpret so unfavourably, the letter I last sent you, and reply to it in so disjointed [<i>rhapsodice</i>] a fashion — when it was written with good intent, for the sake of friendly comparison between us and of drawing out more deeply the truth that lies hidden? For nowhere in that letter did I accuse you of impiety. Of anyone's piety or impiety I arrogate to myself no judgement, leaving that, as is fitting, to the searcher of all hearts. Nor did I say absolutely that you detract anything from the authority of Holy Scripture as if of set purpose; I only admonished you that you had transferred Moses's words about the Moon, the \"lesser light,\" incompetently to a magnitude of body — though it is in fact smaller than most of the stars — whereas they ought rather to be understood of the pre-eminence of its light as it touches the Earth. I added reasons too, not, I think, unfitting ones. And that you also derogate too much from the Prophets — in asserting that they understood no more in astronomy and physics than the ignorant crowd, however much they were divinely and immediately instructed, and that besides they had ready to hand not a few traditions of the most ancient Fathers, who cultivated the study of the visible things created by God, traditions surer and better than ours, which were doubtless received from the heathen — this I judged not to be approved, and in that opinion I still persist. For I have never thought one ought to decide lightly about these matters. Indeed Franciscus Vallesius has lately expounded many things, and not to be despised, concerning what is contained physically in Holy Scripture — even following the reasonings of the common philosophers — and that, as regards those principles, not without skilful labour; although many more things in these matters lie hidden, to be unfolded far otherwise according to a truer physics, and perhaps too to be sought rather, in no small part, from these very [Scriptures]. Moreover Lambert Daneau, a man truly most highly learned and excellently deserving in the instruction of others, has built up his <i>Physica Theologica</i> from both the Old and the New Codex with no useless labour. And many things could perhaps be said about these matters elsewhere, which would require a special and truly <i>metaphysical</i> volume — not of the kind that that Greek man of Stagira [Aristotle] has thrust upon us. As for my not sufficiently understanding your letter — neither illness, though it was grave (an illness of the body, however, not of the mind), nor occupations, though these too were no few entanglements, hindered me to the degree you suppose. For no insoluble riddles lurked in it, the whole matter having long been clear enough; nor do I object anything to you that could not be gathered from that letter — indeed I perhaps spare you more than is needful. Let yours and mine at least be compared, and let others judge — others who do not indulge their passions, nor are too well pleased with themselves. To what extent you have introduced things contrary to Holy Scripture, I indicated in some way both before and now; that you do this studiously everywhere I did not say. Nor can I — though I do not profess theology — nor can any of those who practise it solidly and sincerely, assent to your assertions out of the <i>data</i> [<i>Dedomenis</i>] of Holy Scripture. In vain, therefore, do you put forward this evasion, that, when two say \"one,\" it is not granted to you that they be \"one\"; for neither do I hold that to be granted to others which here I censure. That Augustine sometimes pronounced rather freely about sacred things — at times not inelegantly, at times indeed somewhat feebly, allowance being made for that age nearer the times of the Apostles — of this, by God's grace, I am not ignorant. But what do all these things contribute here, or what do they do toward the matter under discussion? The question raised was whether the mobility of the terrestrial globe, and that threefold [mobility] asserted by Copernicus, can really hold together; and whether it ought to be preferred to our own discovery; and whether Holy Scripture contradicts that imagining or not. If, therefore, you have anything from the sacred Oracles, or from their interpreters — Augustine, say, or other Fathers — making for this Copernican assertion and for your endorsement of it, cite it from their writings. Provided the reasons be just, I shall not resist them. This much I know well: that Augustine — whom alone you name — nowhere conceded a motion of the Earth, neither annual nor diurnal, since, as a man somewhat less of a mathematician, he detracted from its roundness by denying the Antipodes. Which, however, must be pardoned him. Wherefore, since he did not perceive it to be round, far less could he admit a motion suited to its roundness. The opinions of your theologians, and their verdicts on these matters — though perhaps outside their profession, and less deliberated — I do not, to be sure, despise. But since I see that they were summoned and questioned only on this head, whether the fluidity of the heaven can be established from Holy Scripture without the reality of the orbs (as your clause hints), and since you know that I, long before you dreamed of these things, had myself conceded and pronounced the very same — I wonder why you object these things that contribute nothing here. For I do not suppose that the question of the Earth's threefold whirling was put to your theologians, since very few of them would grasp it, however much they might wish to — being, as happens elsewhere too, perhaps not a little addicted to earthly things. Since, therefore, only the controversy about the mobility of the Earth — renewed and reintroduced by Copernicus out of Aristarchus and certain other ancients — was being moved between us, by what coherence, I ask, were you driven to confound it with the limpidity of the heaven, which I never denied, but rather perhaps first of all men laboured not merely to affirm but to prove, from the most certain demonstrations, that it is so? Is this \"mathematical,\" to join and entangle disparate things — which not even the dialecticians, whether artificial or natural, would have allowed in different terms?</p><p>That the substance of the aetherial world is most fluid and everywhere passable can be proved from Holy Scripture, even with the professors of theology silent — this I have long held ascertained. And I have recorded many divinely-revealed Oracles in testimony of that fact. Moreover that most renowned man, excellently deserving of public learning, Master Caspar Peucer, in a letter sent to me especially on this account, gathered very many sayings of Holy Scripture making toward this, and learnedly and excellently subscribed to my opinion concerning the most tenuous, most limpid, and nonetheless super-elemental matter of the heaven. And this chiefly, so that he might thereby also prove that the invention of my hypotheses is by no means absurd. When I shall have published, more fully than has hitherto been done, my uncontaminated reasonings about the matter of the heaven — that it is everywhere passable and packed with no solid orbs (as has hitherto been believed to gratify the ancients), and yet not [made] of an elemental and animate air, as you, and a certain John Pena the Frenchman, and certain others dare to proclaim — and when I shall have handed them over to others to judge, I shall by no means refrain — even without your prompting — from citing the authority of Holy Scripture, which deserves to hold the first place in establishing all wisdom and in founding all science. For I have always held that the glory of God alone is to be regarded in all things — which you too rightly approve — since truth cannot be inspected without His revelation and gift; for every perfect gift is from God. You seem to reproach me wittily [<i>salse</i>, with a pun on <i>sal</i>, \"salt\"] for assigning my \"quintessence\" to the aether — a manner of speaking not unknown among the chemists, nor disapproved by the ancient philosophers either, nor even by Aristotle himself (although in these matters he was not everywhere sufficiently perspicacious). But the salt is sweet, limpid, purged of all earthiness, exalted, in no part insipid or rank-smelling, but analogous to circular perpetuity — and not yet tasted [by you]. When you have smelled it, or perceived its savour, then for the first time pronounce judgement on these things; meanwhile it is better to refrain from premature prejudice. You ask that I not make you blind concerning twilights, nor accuse you of rashness, as though you write things you have not yourself experienced. But I have never presumed either to blind you or to pronounce you rash. Would that it were permitted both to me and to you and to others to be freed from that fog of the mind by which we are obscured more than we believe, and from worldly rashness — just as the external weakness of the eyes can be mended, especially by chemical means, with no great difficulty; truly, in that, so far as it could be done, I should think the very greatest labour ought to be spent. For that labour would not be uselessly bestowed. You say you know that twilight ends when the Sun is depressed 17 degrees below the horizon, taking \"twilight\" in the common sense for doubtful light. You suppose, then, that I am peddling an opinion at least approved by the crowd and hitherto contained in the astronomers' books, and have contributed nothing to it from my own observations. But what an accusation is this! Have I dealt with you by letter so long that you should still hold it unascertained that I am a devotee of the truth, that I seek it from no source other than either just reasoning and experience, or — what is chief — divine revelation, and that I do not of set purpose set up the false as true? You could sufficiently have gathered that I do not wish to see blindly even in greater matters that bear more on astronomy, far less that in the case of twilights — most easily observable things — I should pronounce anything idle, colluding merely with the common opinion of the Ancients, without observation of my own. I knew well enough that twilight is to be determined when no part of the air anywhere around the horizon grows even the least bit white — which was done most diligently several times, both by myself and by at least seven or eight students serving me in astronomical matters, so that no trace, not even the least, of air illuminated by the Sun could be discerned on any nearest surface of the horizon when the times of the twilights were noted; the stars' equatorial distances from the meridian being applied, and the Sun's exact place, and all things diligently weighed by a geometrical balance — and freely communicated to you, for this same reason, that, if you were willing to open your eyes, you might perceive [these times] not to have been fixed at pleasure or by common opinion. If, then, you with two eyes, and with at least a pair of instruments, can discern these things more keenly than all of us with sixteen eyes at the least, and with as many instruments and more wherever it was needed — I do not, to be sure, envy you this lynx-sightedness [<i>lincaeitas</i>] and modest requirement of means; rather I marvel. Indeed, I should congratulate you on a yet greater acuity and fuller sufficiency. So then, about twilights, persuade yourself of whatever you please, abounding in your own sense; I do not in the least hesitate that the things I have set forth are sufficiently confirmed, by many experiments too, and liable to no true refutation. Only see to it that, in disparaging the not-doubtful observations of others — so as to defend your own assertions about the like existence of air and aether (which you try in great part to prove from the discrepancy of vapours in different horizons, and more thickened and sublimated in the more northerly than in the southerly regions) — you do not enwrap the very truth, toward which we ought to tend, in more vapours, obscurities, and mists than all the dense and dusky vaporosities around the terrestrial horizon, in any place whatever, whether southern or northern. Accordingly, you wish not to know why I do not subscribe to the <i>refractions</i> exactly, since you could not otherwise extract them by instruments, and you assert that therefore nothing here is to be done to gratify me, and that in demanding this I demand unfair things. But when did I ever ask of you that, with the truth set aside, you should bring forward anything to gratify me, or press for things so unfair? For that would indeed be unfair, had I done so. On the contrary, I have always admonished, much rather, that the truth itself be more deeply unfolded, since this is well-nigh the fairest and most excellent of all the virtues. Why I cannot in every part approve the refractions asserted by you, my instruments too teach me. If, with one and another — and those at least two-cubit ones, sprung though they were from my own invention and disclosed to you through Wittich, and not even then sufficiently matured — you alone can collimate these and other things more accurately than I with more than twenty instruments, most of them four-cubit and five-cubit, and with several observers employed too, sufficiently strong in keenness of sight: well, there is reason why I should not envy you this prerogative either. Indeed it will even be so that you should give me thanks, because through the instruments devised by me (for you are not now ignorant of that) I have made you so perspicacious. Persuade yourself of this much at least: just as it is wont to happen in the art of wrestling, that no athlete forthwith discloses to his trainers those things which might at any time, through some lack, work against himself in that exercise — so neither has Wittich nor any others published all my matters that are exactly necessary for tracking down the truth, but they still, for the greater part, remain safe and whole [<i>sarta tecta</i>] with their author. In vain, therefore, do you boast too much of your instruments and observations. Perhaps, were you here and saw all things yourself, through far more and least-doubtful instruments, or with your own eyes alone, in my presence, you would say otherwise. But also your refractions being too small and your twilights too wide do not sufficiently agree with each other. It is necessary, therefore, that no slight error lurk in one or the other, or in both — or else that the air in this place a little more to the north, as concerns twilights, be somewhat more purged than it is there, but as concerns refractions more turbid — which, even with you yourself as judge, cannot stand together. Attend, therefore, more diligently to these matters; or, if it pleases you less, or it is not in readiness, at least acquiesce in the not-at-all-doubtful decision of others, and presume to decide nothing here prematurely for yourself. The title of my book, which you elsewhere called into doubt, or even assailed, you now approve, and no longer reckon worthy of reproach — although I did not publish that [title] which you alleged, nor prefix it to any book, but only noted it in passing in certain letters sent to your Most Illustrious Prince. I congratulate, therefore, both you and myself that you have so handsomely come to your senses; for before you objected that I introduce corruption into the heaven, since I used the word \"generation\" there concerning the comet, pressing my words too rigidly. But take care rather, lest now, in asserting deliberately that comets are generated in the heaven, you somewhere sin against [accepted] opinion — not so much grammatically as physically — provided it be fitting to retain the proper sense of the word. That you proposed those things not assertively but doubtfully and interrogatively, so that I might more fully explain my opinion — that is nothing new to me; for Wittich too and most others have dealt with me thus, objecting things that merited no doubt, in order to fish out certain others. But after I had carefully tested this in that case, and in others too, I became more cautious — although not a few things acquired here and there in Germany at my expense and labour, and that by the grace of God alone, are still peddled and proclaimed by others as their own; which I make little of. Nor do I take it ill that some rivulets are diverted to foreigners, while the very fountain still gushes, by God's bounty, in the keeping of its lord. Yet false things are not to be put forward in order that true things be known; for true and false are at odds — unless someone, as is done among the geometers, when nothing can be proved assertively, reduces the matter to absurdity by negation. Likewise, the things you proposed about the comet's tail — both in that writing of yours on the Comet of the year [15]85 and in the letters sent to me — you now retract, and assert that you did not press them as true, but only so as to submit them to my judgement. But although both that writing and your letters bear another aspect, nonetheless it pleases me well that here too you have returned to the road. For it pleases me that you now see well enough how little force your reasonings have, and the things you adduced by way of confirmation from the Most Illustrious Prince's Observation of the Comet of the year [15]58 (since I set those before your eyes in my earlier letter) — so much so that, as appears, you yourself would prefer not to have written them. The things you bring forward, beyond what I produced, to excuse that observation, find easy place with me — I who would impute [the discrepancy] not so much to an observation a little astray, but rather to the fact that you pressed this one too incautiously, and tried thereby to evade the findings of all others in very many comets. You ask that I not publish these things, and that I not mix your opinion about the generation of comets into my writings. But I cannot conceal this from you here: that I have been asked by letter, by the most noble man Master Heinrich Rantzau, the King's vicar [governor] in Holstein, and also by the most renowned and learned Master Caspar Peucer, an outstanding philosopher, and by certain other not unlearned men, who request and approve it, to publish in a special volume or two the astronomical letters written to me from various quarters here and there, together with my reply to each. Which I judged ought not to be denied them. And since the said Rantzau had requested that chiefly your Most Illustrious Prince's letters, and yours, be set in type — and since they deserve of themselves to occupy the earlier and more principal part of the volume — and since that book is now under the press, I cannot, for your sake, retract what I have assigned to others and make done things undone. Nor do I see why this should have been demanded of me, or why I should grant it, since nothing in this matter is admitted that would be harmful to your Most Illustrious Prince, or that derogates from him in any part, but rather only such things as redound, as is fitting, to His Highness's commendation. Nor is any danger or detriment created for you from this, since I always speak and think of you honourably. But if you change certain things proposed in your earlier writings and letters, and correct them on better deliberation, nothing departs from your reputation thereby, even if the earlier things be published. Indeed it is better that others learn how difficult it is, even for ingenious and industrious men, to disentangle the hiding-places of truth, and that not all things which at first sight are thought to be right hold good in every respect. And that day teaches day, and that later thoughts, for the most part — though not always — surpass earlier ones. Other quite outstanding men have retracted many things previously held by them, less considerately, as true — even in those matters where the tracking-down of truth was not so difficult as here. Since, in the Epilogue of the whole Work, which I have in hand concerning the Adscititious Phenomena of the Heaven [<i>Ascititia Caeli Phaenomena</i>], I inquire professedly into the nature and procreation of comets, according to the opinions of all, however many have come to my knowledge, I cannot but indicate this opinion of yours too — shared with certain others — and how incongruous it is, as I wrote to you elsewhere. Nevertheless, since I see you correcting yourself in these matters and superseding your earlier conceptions, I shall make mention of it there too, and shall fittingly excuse it. I shall not make your writing on the comet public; nonetheless to cite and weigh some things from it will redound rather to your laudable mention than be a reproach. You say indeed that I shall be fighting with a mute man, if I do so. But you were not mute when you wrote these things and consigned them in letters sent to me; for written marks, especially those penned in your own hand, stand for the words and speech themselves — nay, they deserve even more weight. So too, as regards my own letters — which I had never resolved to make public, unless asked by friends, as I said, since they were penned only in passing, as it came to my mouth, amid very many affairs — know that I do not so repent of them but that I leave you free, whether privately or publicly, in whatever manner you please, to discuss, approve, or disapprove what is contained in them. If, by just reasons, either you or anyone else shall sufficiently demonstrate that something in these or others of my [writings] does not hold rightly, I do not blush to correct them. Nor shall I, with any prejudice, set about inquiring into yours. See that you use the same candour about mine too, and do not, with a certain youthful ardour, pronounce a premature opinion. I congratulate you that, from considering the matter of the stars and the Milky Way, you have turned to a different opinion about the conformation of comets than that with which you were before imbued — one little physical, and far less mathematical. For here you are on the good road, if you have learned how to enter it and know all its lodging-places. For I could not sufficiently marvel by what fog of judgement you dared to assert such great absurdities — that the body of comets, by your own admission distant from the Earth nearly as far as Saturn's revolutions (as you recorded of the one that appeared in the year [15]85), and therefore necessarily greater by many <i>parasangs</i> than the Earth and Sea and all the surrounding vapours, as the geometrical demonstrations bear — should nonetheless have ascended thence into the highest aether, by I know not what unknown snatching-away, and should be a part much greater than its own whole; and that quite against the mathematical principles. If the physicists like to think so, let them join these doleful ditties [<i>naeniae</i>] to the rest. Rightly, therefore, do you draw the further conclusion that the things about <i>running beams</i> [<i>Trabes discurrentes</i>] — by occasion of which you perhaps slipped into so discordant a view — since they really contribute nothing here, and these are as far apart from one another as heaven and earth, you have caught out to be wholly false. I had it in mind to admonish you of these things too in my last letter; how I neglected it, I do not recall. For in your writing on the Comet of the year [15]85, where you try to assert such things, I had noted these matters in the margin for memory's sake, which I judged ought to be brought to mind either then or otherwise as occasion offered. Those exhalations [<i>halitus</i>] or chasms [<i>chasmata</i>] are far nearer to us than that they could be illuminated, in winter time, by a Sun depressed too far below the horizon, as geometrical and optical reasoning teaches. Moreover in the year 1588 I made an evident trial of this on 6 December, about the eighth hour of the evening. For I observed then, by careful inspection, very many chasms flying and whitening here and there toward the North — so that they bore a certain light that even illumined the Earth's shadow — to be far nearer to us than certain denser clouds dispersed separately around the northern horizon; so much so that those chasms evidently appeared to run between us and the clouds, so that others of my people too, to whom I showed it, discerned it clearly enough. Therefore, with the Sun submerged toward the Antipodes, that illumination of the vapours could not take place; since not even the clouds, more remote from the Earth, could have been struck by its light — which will easily be plain by measuring these things geometrically. Now those clouds of which I speak were not continuous, but disposed sparsely around the horizon, to an altitude — as regards the visible horizon — of about 10 degrees. Since, therefore, the Sun's rays do not, in that submersion of it below the horizon, touch the part of the Earth's shadow so near to us, it is asserted in vain that anything of this kind happens through illumination from it. I have considered something similar rather often, especially in this very year on 2 January, likewise about the eighth hour of the evening, when very many chasms were seen here toward the North, which were perceived to be far nearer than the clouds. Wherefore in vain did you seek some support from this for establishing that unnatural generation of comets. And if you wish to know what I think about these chasms: I judge it to be a certain terrestrial sulphurousness, burning and running in the nearby air, and sometimes filling the whole heaven by ascending to the zenith — yet at a modest distance from our sight — forerunners of winds, or of dryness and cold soon to follow; sometimes too, if they happen often and last too long, indications of air inclined to the contagion of an epidemic pestilence. For that disease has not a little in common with the sulphurous nature; whence too it is more readily dissolved by terrestrial sulphur excellently purified and exalted — especially if it be reduced into an agreeable liquor — as like by its like. For that maxim of the Galenists is not always true: that contraries are cured by contraries. These things about these matters I have thought it good now to bring to your mind. Most rightly, therefore, will you have done, if you change, as you promise, that whole chapter on the matter of comets and their illumination, and treat it more considerately. Nor is there need that yet another comet be awaited. For those which have passed most recently, even in our own age, abundantly convince [us] that your earlier opinion can in no way stand; which our observations in these matters will easily teach you, provided they can merit some credence. But if you judge that our decisions — though sought with what diligence could be, and not, as I hope, brought forward beside the point — are not such that you think one should safely rely on them: come then, await yet another comet, if any should occur meanwhile, and scrutinize it more diligently by measuring it, provided that be granted you. If you then find something different from our conclusions, there will be time enough to bring it forth, whatever it shall be; meanwhile, if you suspend judgement — provided it displease you to acquiesce in ours — it will not be ill-advised. That the planets hang [<i>pendere</i>] in the most subtle and purest air (though that is said quite improperly, since in truth they do not hang) — that you no longer wish to dispute this further with me, I gladly accept; and you do well in no longer calling the matter of the heaven animate and elemental. For here you will never obtain anything from me. If my reasons — partly already brought forward, partly to be further declared elsewhere, and adduced from lower and palpable things, yet analogous to the higher — do not suffice for you, [then] as far as I am concerned you are free to think whatever you wish. Treat this question wherever you please, and whether you involve me or not, it will be the same. I shall not, when occasion to treat of this matter is given, forbear to defend my opinion with sufficient probability. I in no way shun the judgement of most learned men who understand the matter itself; for I do not arrogate so much to myself as to presume to settle this dispute alone. I have always held the love of truth [<i>philalithia</i>] in esteem — perhaps to my greater inconvenience (which, however, I bear not ill) than to my profit. Nor have I ever permitted myself to be obscured, of set purpose, by the bewitchment of prejudice, as you seem to impute; for, as far as it has been divinely granted me to discern true from false, I have continually striven to do so. If you appeal to some third person (for I see you dealing with me juridically in a mathematical matter — for your brother too reported that you incline toward jurisprudence, perhaps because it is more lucrative and more conspicuous to the world) — I too accept the condition, and willingly challenge any philosophers in Germany or elsewhere capable of these matters. And, that someone be named: to the most renowned and excellent man Master Caspar Peucer, if you so please, I leave the whole matter — since he is in the first rank versed in both philosophy and the mathematical disciplines, and not slothfully devoted to the study of theology (whence this question can in great part be decided), and at the same time prevails by grave judgement and age. Nor need it occur to you to suspect that he will be a judge too favourable to my side (for you know, if you wish to be a jurist, that the favour of the judge avails more than ten laws written in the Code) — for he has never been known to me; but with your Most Illustrious Prince — whom, in a letter sent to me, he calls his best benefactor, and not undeservedly — and with you too, when present, he contracted a not ungrateful friendship. If there are others among your countrymen, or even elsewhere, to whom you wish to lodge an appeal, I too appeal to the same — provided they have inspected the matter under discussion more deeply, and have learned to understand both the higher and the lower things, and to reconcile these with one another, knowing the one from the other. For whatever is above, that also is below, as that Hermes truly Thrice-Greatest [<i>Trismegistus</i>] among mortals used to say. That the hidden mystery of the Trinity lurks in all created things, you rightly grant to me — although your philosophy concerning these matters, which you adduce, is more outward than inward, and too obvious to the senses; since that which is chief, and in which the kernel of the matter resides, does not appear at first sight. For it is not enough to say that all lower things consist of Earth, Water, and Air, since these very mothers too have certain other threefold principles, not yet sufficiently perceived by the common philosophers, of which there is now no place to dispute. You add then that you see — that you have now at last obtained — that I have conceded to you that the diaphaneity of pure air differs in nothing from the diaphaneity of the matter poured around the planets; and you do not on that account doubt that truth, the daughter of time, will in its own time teach me your remaining opinion too. But with what eyeglasses [<i>perspicilla</i>] did you ever see this so keenly? Or by what means could you ever gather it from my writings? I marvelled, reading these things, why you should wish to allege that I propose things contrary to myself in this part. So I inspected again the copy of the letter last addressed to you, from which you doubtless wished to gather this. But I find nothing of the kind in it — rather, the plain contrary of your assertion. I there first gave an example, from lower things, which by the benefit of the spagyric art [<i>ars spagyrica</i>] can be exalted into such subtlety that they represent a like diaphaneity, so far as it is discerned by the senses; and nonetheless I showed them to be of a different substance — and therefore that what you intended does not follow, even were it conceded, on account of refractions not tending to the zenith, that there is an imperceptible discrepancy between the heaven and purer air as regards diaphaneity, [it would] therefore [not follow that they] consist of one and the same matter. In this way I reasoned with you, nor on that account did I concede the diaphaneity of air and aether to be entirely the same; nay, I added these words, indicating otherwise: \"So too the diaphaneity of heaven and air, even if they were quite the same — which however has not yet been sufficiently established — nothing nonetheless prevents [them] from having a different substance and matter.\" Why, then, I ask, do you boast that you have obtained from me that which I said in open words has not yet been sufficiently established? Why do you so injuriously invert my meaning? And why do you so inconsiderately precipitate judgement about these tenets [<i>placita</i>]? If truth, the daughter of time, shall ever inform me better, I shall surely not shrink from accepting the truth; and that on whatever occasion it shall at last be granted. Yet I do not yet see why I should assent to any of your opinions about which there is controversy between us. Nor will you evidently demonstrate that I have retracted anything of those things which I have hitherto written to you, even in passing, or otherwise issued, that is of any moment — whereas you not a few things, even in this very letter, formerly keenly defended, not merely call into doubt but wish to be wholly void: As that you did not assert the Prophets to have understood no more in physics than the sluggish crowd; and that Moses, speaking of the two great luminaries, did [something] against the geometrical demonstrations, as if this were not to detract from Holy Scripture; that you entangle the question raised about the Copernican motion of the Earth with the matter of the heaven, and strive to prove this from Augustine and other modern theologians by confounding heaven and earth; that you do not reproach the title of my volumes on the celestial generation of comets, though before you objected that I introduce corruption into the heaven if I assert generation; that you asserted the comets' tails, in so many words, and also from some observation, formerly as though they were parts of the body, consisting of the same matter, and now no longer hold them as true — nay, wish the very observation, [though] written out, to be undone; that the whole opinion about the generation of comets, formerly thrust upon me on account of a certain \"Graminaeus,\" you now so set aside that you judge a whole chapter must on that account be changed in your writing and treated otherwise; that the running beams, formerly illumined by the Sun, you now deprive of its light — and whatever other corollaries there are. For I do not wish to dig out everything too scrupulously. Yet I do not review these things in order to reproach you with acknowledging and amending your deviations. For that I praise, and would that you had done so more often, and had permitted yourself to be instructed by truth the daughter of time, as you rightly said. Thus you would have touched the centre of the circle, which is unique, more on a straight line. If you ever show that I have written anything that needs retraction, I shall be grateful; that I have as yet retracted anything of mine I am not conscious — not that it cannot happen, as we are human, but because what you say you see in my writings has not yet been open to my eyes, nor do I yet acknowledge it. That comparison which I proposed, and somewhat explained to you, between the liquor of Water and of Wine, both purified, with respect to the matter of Air and of Heaven, perhaps deserved a greater reception than that it should be so taken. For in truth the heaven is related to elemental air just as some spirit of Wine or of another generous liquor is to purged water. And although they be almost the same in diaphaneity, yet far different in substance. Nor do I know whether a better comparison can be drawn from terrestrial things. Hence it is that certain of those who philosophize have not hesitated to call the Spirit of Wine especially — moderately exalted and purified several times, and at length freed by continual and long-lasting circulation from elemental intemperance — \"Aether,\" or \"Philosophical Heaven\"; although whether it can be done in the manner they propose, [and] that certain other things be not wanting, I do not now wish to say. This much at least I would dare openly to affirm: that in each terrestrial substance there lurks something analogous to the celestial nature — in some more, in some less — which savours properly neither of Air, nor Water, nor Earth, nor even the Element of Fire (if such there were in the nature of things), although these elemental qualities too are present in each, in a certain grosser composition. Nor must it be thought that this super-elemental power is in the elemental things only from the Sun or the rest of the stars, although I would not deny this in some part. For some things are more conformed to these, some to other stars; meanwhile, however, the universal essence of the heaven too, in which these astral virtues dwell, [is such that] all lower things — according as they more or less emulate it — [are governed] not otherwise than as the stars themselves are revolved in the heaven, befitting their own nature. But let this much now suffice to have discoursed about these matters, since they do not pertain to the commonly received philosophy, and would perhaps scarcely be accepted there. The Ptolemaic or the Copernican hypotheses, whichever of these you approve, will, as far as I am concerned, be free to you. I would only advise that you form your judgement about the heaven from the heaven itself, not from papers and ingenious demonstrations, perhaps not everywhere consonant with the heaven itself. Let it therefore be permitted me too to pronounce freely what the heaven has taught me in these matters. About the Filcher of my System [<i>Surreptor mei Systematis</i>] I will add no word here, for he is not worth it. I replied to you briefly in my last [letter] to the things you objected to this innovation of hypotheses of ours; a fuller resolution of this kind, and of other doubts, you will obtain when I shall have treated professedly of the motion of the Secondary Movables [<i>Secunda Mobilia</i>], and shall have laboured to render it, as regards the reckoning, exactly conformable to the heaven itself, and shall (the Maker of the heavens favouring) have, as I trust, brought the whole matter to the wished-for end. If you think out anything more meanwhile that you suppose can be brought to the contrary, you will do a not ungrateful thing by freely producing it. For sometimes by contradiction the Truth is rendered more illustrious. The occasions which impelled me to such an ordering of the Celestial Revolutions I have also briefly indicated to you. For I was brought to this neither rashly nor for a light cause, but the heaven itself taught me to think thus of it, with no other Teacher, nor any books, nor jumbles of Tables [<i>Tabularum Farragines</i>] contributing anything here. I conferred too with certain other learned mathematicians in Germany about this our modern invention, which, although it does not much please certain Aristotelians, especially those too addicted to the realities of the orbs, is nonetheless reckoned quite elegant by the more judicious, who weigh the matter without preconceived opinion and inspect it more rightly. Although, to say what the case is, even though I do not despise the judgements of others, yet whether they make for or against, I care not much, provided experience itself, drawn down from heaven, evidently convince [me] that the matter is so. I know how abstruse the inner recesses of Nature have always been, and how far, very often, those things we think we rightly perceive in physics stray from the mark. Therefore I think one should rest not so much in the authority and applause of others, but rather that it befits us ourselves to make trial whether the matter be so or otherwise, and to weigh all things in the balance of Observations and Demonstrations, and to prove them. But there is now no place to discourse more on these things. But since I see that the Copernican opinion about the threefold motion of the Earth much pleases you, I shall propose, in each of these [motions], at least a single doubt — though far more could be given — and one not so laborious. First, as regards the diurnal motion about its own axis, by which the Earth is feigned to be wheeled round in 24 hours, and thus the universal course from east to west is explained away: tell me how it can come about that a leaden globe, let down in due fashion from a very high tower, should touch perpendicularly and exactly the point of the Earth placed beneath it; for that this can by no means happen, the Earth being carried round meanwhile, since its course is most swift, a geometrical computation will teach you: seeing that in one second-scruple of time the Earth must be revolved, even in these more northerly regions, nearly 150 of the larger paces [<i>passus maiores</i> — the greater Roman pace as a unit of distance]. From this reason out the rest. For the fall of the lead does not accompany the air, but violently passes through it. Concerning the second motion of the Earth, the annual — which would remove the eighth Sphere so far that the orb which this [annual motion] would describe vanishes with respect to it — explain whether it seems probable to you here that the space which is from the Sun, then the centre of the universe, to Saturn, is contained more than 700 times within [the space between] this [Saturn] and the Sphere of the Fixed [stars], and that the whole [space] filled with no stars. And yet it is necessary that this be so, if only the annual orb of the Earth is to appear of the size of one minute. Nay, then too the Fixed Stars of the third magnitude, which have one minute in diameter, will necessarily be equal to this whole Annual Orb — that is, they will comprehend in diameter 2284 Semidiameters of the Earth. For they will be distant nearly 7,850,000 of those same Semidiameters. What shall we say of the Stars of the first magnitude, of which some occupy two, certain ones nearly three minutes in visible diameter? And what if the eighth Sphere be removed yet higher, so that the Earth's annual motion vanishes there altogether? Deduce these things, if you please, geometrically, and you will see how great absurdities — even by inferring thus (to say nothing of others) — accompany this assumption. The third [motion], the annual being taken away, falls of itself; or if it seems to you able to hold together with it, how, I ask, can it come about that the Axis of the Earth is gyrated yearly in the contrary direction to the motion of the centre, so correspondingly that it nonetheless appears to be at rest? How too is it granted that the Axis and the Centre are agitated by a double and different motion, in a single and simple body? — to say nothing in reply about that third, diurnal [motion] supervening about the Axis. I am silent too about the entanglements of the librations, [and that they are], not everywhere corresponding to the advancement of the Fixed [stars] to which they are assigned, very discordant. Weigh these and like things carefully with yourself, and, if it be granted, resolve them at leisure. You will have more elsewhere. If, therefore, the Copernican reasoning cannot hold — as indeed it cannot — the Ptolemaic [reasoning], which Copernicus (to say nothing of others) has sufficiently refuted, nothing will hinder our invention from deserving a place. You set forth, finally, the causes why your Most Illustrious Prince has not replied to mine, which I gladly accept. I would now write to His Highness, but nothing occurs [to me] besides what I consigned in my last. And at the same time I fear lest I trouble Him — occupied with the administration of the commonwealth and other cares — with my meagre letters. Frischlin, of whom you write, has not come hither, nor sent any letter from you, though otherwise he would not be received by me unkindly; for I have heard that the man is of varied learning and eloquent. You make mention besides of the Mill suitable for making paper, which I indicated I would build, lest I be so often and so long frustrated for printing-paper to be brought from Germany for striking off my lucubrations; and you say this pleased your Prince, which I readily believe. For in these things too we have a certain mutual confluence. For just as His Highness delights in making architecture, ramparts, ditches, aqueducts, fishponds, gardens and mills and like mechanical structures, so I too, after my own measure, especially in summer time, when it is not permitted to be free for higher matters, and these are more conveniently handled. For these too smack of something geometrical, provided they are to be made with due symmetry. That mill is now ready, with a very high and very broad rampart holding back sufficient water both in summer and in winter time, and a wheel 12 ells in diameter driven by the least water, and performing a double work besides the paper-work. With very many fishponds too disposed in order here and there, so that when it shall be necessary, these mill-pools too may supply waters. And all these things were dry a few years ago. Moreover I am building here another mill for my peasants and workmen dwelling round about in the neighbourhood, where too almost no water is now thought to be at hand. For I do not wish that others should survey the paper-works and the things pertaining to my own domestic affairs, and untimely disturb them. If Victorinus Schönfeldt sends his son hither to visit me, by me it is permitted him; as soon as it shall seem [good] to him or to me that he return to his own, it will be free enough to both. Whether he be promoted to Master of the Liberal Arts or not is all one to me; yet I should prefer him to be such, rather than to be [merely] so called. But this is not displayed at so light a price; it would suffice to be a disciple of these [arts]. But I see, my dear Rothmann, that in replying to your letter — written more tersely and, as appears, with a hurrying pen — I have been too copious; which I admitted the more readily, lest I should seem to wish to avenge your brevity (as if indulging too much in retaliation) by no reply at all, or even a shorter one. For although I am now entangled in more affairs than you were in writing yours, or perhaps ever otherwise, yet I wished to be hindered by no other occupations from replying to yours, and that so amply; which in my last [letter] too, besides the illness I signified, was really so — so that, with so much time elapsed, leisure for replying to you doubtless was at hand, more conveniently and less adversely. If, however, anything of freer admonition is written in these, take it in the better part and ascribe it to the love of our common studies. Farewell, and if you have leisure, write back now at once, so that, God willing, you may obtain a reply again by the next coming Fair. Meanwhile take in good part these things, penned likewise with a hurrying pen.<br>Again, happily farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 24 November, in the year 1589.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. Since I have learned, both from your letter and from elsewhere, that the most illustrious Prince Landgrave has resolved to make public his observations of the fixed stars over several years, and to employ your labour in carrying out this undertaking: I ask, for the honour and the benefit of Astronomy, that you do not grudge to hasten these labours — perhaps no less than the labours of Hercules — lest, by too much procrastinating, they either perish altogether, or scarcely at last see the light of publication for the common advancement of this Art. For from this both those who are now living and posterity, whom the care of such lofty matters touches, will easily judge how erroneous the places of the \"fixed\" stars have been, and how little congruent with the heaven, of which (whether through the want or the carelessness of the craftsmen) we have hitherto made use in so many ways, since nothing surer was at hand; and at the same time it may become plain how great a task it was to track down the boundary-markers [<i>Hermas</i>] of the Stars; and, by comparing your observations with mine — which I have resolved shortly (the Maker of the Stars favouring) to publish — you may discern how much more accurate, and with what constancy of labour, this matter has been carried through on both sides. For the fact that your restitutions differ from mine, in almost all the Stars, by abounding as it were by a twelfth of one degree, will easily find its excuse with those who understand the intricate difficulty of the matter. Nor indeed could it be otherwise than that the limits of the Stars fixed by you should somewhat exceed their genuine distance from the equinoctial point, since you carried it out only by means of the evening star Venus (and not likewise the morning star), and not often enough at that, the refraction then insinuating Venus to the eyes a little further in longitude than is right. Whence it follows that the Stars derived from her across that interval grow, in their longitudes too, more than was needful — to say nothing of that slippery way of observing by Azimuths and Altitudes, together with the markings of the times obtained less surely from clocks, in all of which you, as I not without reason suspect, place too secure a trust. But since in our first Volume of the Astronomical Progymnasmata, in the second chapter, where I treat expressly of the exact verification of the fixed stars, I shall set forth abundantly how the limits of the Stars — falling back at last precisely into one and the same place, and most aptly analogous to one another — have been determined by us not only from Venus western of the Sun but also from the same Venus eastern; and likewise, after about eight hundred [stars], in their canonical distribution into fixed places as to longitude and latitude, I shall insert some mention of the Landgravian rectification in these matters too, and why it almost everywhere exceeds ours by some 5 minutes, as I shall show: I am unwilling now to dwell upon this very point further here. Do you only take pains that those who desire them may shortly enjoy your Atlantean labours in this part as well. And so once again, farewell.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, to <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (<i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i>, c. 1555–c. 1600), mathematician at the Hesse-Kassel court of <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b> (<i>Illustrissimus Princeps Wilhelmus Landtgravius Hassiae</i>; §5.6). This is one of the principal letters of the Tycho–Rothmann correspondence, in which the two debate the materiality of the heavens, the truth of the Copernican system, the nature of comets, atmospheric refraction, and twilight. It was printed by Tycho himself in the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the volume named in the source's `edition` field; that printed text is reproduced (with the <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI critical text as edition of record).</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Vraniburgi 24. Nouemb. Anno 1589</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, 24 November, in the year 1589\" (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The catalogue `label` (\"1589. 24. november. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> This letter contains <b>no Greek-script interpolations</b> (the heuristic and several technical terms — <i>philalithia</i>, <i>parasanga</i>, <i>chasma</i>, <i>trismegistus</i> — are Greek loanwords written in Latin letters; see below). They are transliterations, not Greek script, and are kept as printed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Franciscus Vallesius</i> = <b>Francisco Valles</b> (de Covarrubias, 1524–1592), Spanish physician-philosopher; his <i>De iis quae scripta sunt physice in libris sacris</i> (\"Sacra Philosophia,\" 1587) is the work meant.</li><li><i>Lambertus Danaeus</i> = <b>Lambert Daneau</b> (c. 1530–1595), French Reformed theologian; <i>Physica christiana / Physice Theologica</i> is meant. \"Codex\" here = the two Testaments (\"the Old and the New Codex\"), kept literal with a gloss.</li><li><i>Stagyrita iste Graecus</i> = <b>Aristotle</b> (of Stagira); the dismissive demonstrative <i>iste</i> (\"that Greek of Stagira\") is preserved.</li><li><i>Caspar Peucer</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician-philosopher and Melanchthon's son-in-law; Tycho repeatedly invokes him as an ally on the fluidity of the heavens and proposes him as arbiter (§5.6).</li><li><i>Ioannes Pena Gallus</i> = <b>Jean Pena</b> (c. 1528–1558), French mathematician; in the preface to his Euclid <i>Optica</i> (1557) he argued the heavens are filled with ordinary (elemental) air — the view Tycho rejects (\"not of an elemental and animate air\").</li><li><i>Wittichius</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Wrocław mathematician who visited Hven in 1580 and (Tycho complains) carried instrument-designs and methods to Kassel and elsewhere; he is the unnamed <i>Surreptor mei Systematis</i> (\"Filcher of my System\") of the later passage — Tycho's standing grievance over priority for the geo-heliocentric system. Flagged: the identification of the \"Filcher\" with Wittich (vs. Ursus / Nicolaus Reimers Baer) is contextual; the letter does not name him, so \"Filcher of my System\" is kept and the candidate noted, not asserted.</li><li><i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor; <i>Vicarius Regius</i>) of Holstein, humanist patron; one of those urging Tycho to publish the correspondence.</li><li><i>Hermes … Trismegistus</i> = <b>Hermes Trismegistus</b>; the maxim <i>Quicquid est supra, id etiam infra</i> (\"whatever is above, that also is below\") paraphrases the Hermetic/Emerald-Tablet <i>correspondence</i> doctrine; kept with the Latin.</li><li><i>Graminaeus quidam</i> — \"a certain Graminaeus\": <b>Theodor Graminaeus</b> (Gravius), a 16th-c. writer on comets whose theory Rothmann had (Tycho says) earlier pressed; treated as a proper name, flagged below.</li><li><i>Frischlinus</i> = <b>Nicodemus Frischlin</b> (1547–1590), the wandering humanist poet-scholar; Tycho notes he never arrived.</li><li><i>Victorinus Schonfeldt</i> = <b>Victorinus Schönfeld</b> (1525–1591), Marburg professor of mathematics/medicine, whose son might visit Hven.</li><li><b>Astronomical / physical terms (controlled glossary, §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>parallax</i>-free here, but: <i>refractiones</i> → <b>refractions</b>; <i>Crepusculum</i> → <b>twilight</b> (§5.1); <i>Horizon, Meridianus, Altitudo, Azimuth</i> per glossary; <i>octava Sphaera / Affixarum Sphaera</i> → <b>eighth Sphere / Sphere of the Fixed [stars]</b>; <i>Orbis Annuus</i> → <b>annual orb</b>; <i>Stellae Fixae, magnitudo</i> → <b>Fixed Stars, magnitude</b>; <i>Semidiameter Terrae</i> → <b>Earth-semidiameter</b> (Tycho's unit of cosmic distance); <i>Secunda Mobilia</i> → <b>Secondary Movables</b> (the planetary orbs/motions below the <i>primum mobile</i>). <i>Organa / Instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b> (§5.2); <i>bicubitalia / quadricubitalia / quincubitalia</i> → \"two-/four-/five-cubit\" (instrument sizes); <i>collimare</i> → <b>collimate</b> (sight along). <i>minutum</i> = arc-minute; <i>Scrupulum secundum temporis</i> = \"second-scruple of time\" = one <b>second of time</b> (kept literal with gloss).</li><li><b>The threefold Copernican motion.</b> <i>mobilitas … triplex a Copernico asserta</i> = the <b>diurnal</b> (axial), <b>annual</b> (orbital), and the <b>third</b> motion (Copernicus's annual conical motion of the axis, the <i>motus declinationis</i>, here <i>librationum intricationes</i>). Tycho's three objections — the falling-lead/tower argument, the immense empty gap to the fixed stars (and the resulting monstrous physical size of stars), and the incoherence of a third axial motion — are his standard anti-Copernican physical arguments; rendered faithfully, with his hedges (\"to say nothing of others,\" \"though far more could be given\") preserved.</li><li><b>Numbers (verified against the source, not normalised).</b> Sun depressed <i>P. 17</i> below the horizon = <b>17 degrees</b> (Rothmann's value for the end of twilight). <i>Rota 12 ulnarum</i> = a mill-wheel <b>12 ells</b> in diameter. <i>plusquam 700 vicibus</i> = the Sun–Saturn space contained <b>more than 700 times</b> in the gap to the fixed stars. <i>2284 Semidiametros Terrae</i> and <i>7850000 … Semidiametris</i> = <b>2284</b> and <b>≈7,850,000</b> Earth-semidiameters — reproduced exactly as printed. <i>sesquicentum passus maiores proxime</i> = <b>nearly 150 of the larger paces</b> (<i>passus maiores</i> = the greater Roman pace taken as a unit of distance; <i>maiores</i> qualifies <i>passus</i>, not \"and more\"; <i>proxime</i> = \"nearly\"). Dates within the letter: trials of <b>6 December 1588</b> and <b>2 January 1589</b> (\"this very year\"), both <i>circa octauam vespertinam</i> (\"about the eighth hour of the evening\"); the Comet of <b>1585</b> (Rothmann's tract) and the Landgrave's observation of the Comet of <b>1558</b>. None silently converted.</li><li><b>Alchemical / chemical terms (§5.4 register).</b> <i>quinta Essentia</i> → <b>quintessence</b>; <i>Ars Spagyrica</i> → <b>spagyric art</b> (Paracelsian chemistry); <i>Spiritus Vini</i> → <b>Spirit of Wine</b> (distilled alcohol); <i>circulatio</i> → <b>circulation</b> (repeated distillation); <i>defaecatus / exaltatus / depuratus</i> → <b>purged / exalted / purified</b> (alchemical operations); <i>Sulphureitas Terrestris</i> → <b>terrestrial sulphurousness</b>; <i>Caelum Philosophicum</i> → <b>Philosophical Heaven</b>. The <i>salse / Sal</i> wordplay (Rothmann mocking Tycho's \"quintessence,\" Tycho retorting that \"the salt is sweet … not yet tasted\") is a pun on <i>sal</i> \"salt\" / <i>salse</i> \"wittily, with salt\"; flagged in the translation, not flattened.</li><li><b>Medical note (§5.4).</b> <i>Contraria contrarijs curari</i> — \"that contraries are cured by contraries,\" the <b>Galenic</b> allopathic maxim, which Tycho disputes in favour of a <i>simile suo simili</i> (\"like by its like\") logic for treating a sulphur-natured pestilence; the period sense is kept, not modernised.</li><li><b>Latinate / rhetorical terms kept and glossed.</b> <i>rhapsodice</i> → \"in a disjointed/patchwork fashion\"; <i>Dedomena</i> (Greek <i>δεδομένα</i>, \"the givens/data\") → \"the <i>data</i>\"; <i>sarta tecta</i> → \"safe and whole\" (legal idiom, \"in good repair\"); <i>naeniae</i> → \"doleful ditties\"; <i>parasangae</i> → \"parasangs\" (Persian leagues, a learned distance-word); <i>lincaeitas</i> → \"lynx-sightedness\" (from Lynceus); <i>perspicilla</i> → \"eyeglasses\"; <i>philalithia</i> → \"love of truth\"; <i>Tabularum Farragines</i> → \"jumbles of Tables.\" All retained-Latin technical/rhetorical terms italicised on first use.</li><li><b><i>Ascititia Caeli Phaenomena</b></i> — the planned <b>Epilogue</b> \"On the Adscititious [adventitious / supervenient] Phenomena of the Heaven,\" i.e. Tycho's projected treatment of comets etc. within his larger work; kept as a title.</li><li><b>Titulus libri / Tomi de Cometarum caelesti generatione</b> — the disputed title asserting the <i>celestial generation</i> of comets; the <i>generatio / corruptio</i> quarrel (Rothmann earlier charging that \"generation in the heaven\" imports Aristotelian <i>corruption</i> into the incorruptible heavens) is the running terminological dispute, rendered with the technical Aristotelian senses intact.</li><li><b>Textual note (<i>deduxere</i> / <i>deduxero</i>).</b> Near the end (<i>…ad optatum finem rem omnem deduxere</i>), the 1596 printed text / <i>Opera Omnia</i> base read here gives the syncopated archaic form <b><i>deduxere</b></i> (= <i>deduxerō</i>, future perfect, 1st sing., \"I shall have brought\"); the source block reproduces the printed <i>deduxere</i> as required by §2/§4.2. The unsyncopated <i>deduxero</i> is the equivalent classical-regular spelling; both readings carry the identical sense, so the English (\"shall … have brought the whole matter to the wished-for end\") is unaffected. Recorded here, not silently normalised.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> Several pivotal negated clauses were counted and reproduced one-for-one, e.g.: <i>Neque enim ego te vspiam in illis impietatis accusaui</i> (\"for nowhere did I accuse you of impiety\" — one negation, on the accusation); <i>nec … te caecum efficiam, neque temeritatis accusem</i> (Rothmann's two requests, both kept); <i>nec te occaecare, nec temerarium pronuntiare vnquam praesumsi</i> (\"I have never presumed either to blind you or to pronounce you rash\" — double negative correlatives preserved); <i>me … non amplius ea pro veris habere, imo ipsam Obseruationem … infectam cupere</i> (Rothmann no longer holds them true, <i>and</i> wishes the observation undone — both polarities kept). No negation added or dropped.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Substantial closing POSTSCRIPTA (Rothmann's fixed-star observations, the per-star ~1/12° longitude excess, the Venus-vespertine refraction error, the \"Quantae molis erat Stellarum inquirere Hermas\" tag), restored from Dreyer VI, pp. 199–200 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> \"to investigate the Stars, like a Hermes\" corrected to \"to track down the boundary-markers [<i>Hermas</i>] of the Stars\": \"Hermas\" is the accusative object of \"inquirere\" (with genitive \"Stellarum\"), not a mistaken reference to the god Hermes.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely and in full; register (Tycho's combative-courteous magisterial idiom) is matched and his epistemic hedges preserved. Residual soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the long diaphaneity-of-air/aether passage with its embedded quotation (<i>\"Sic quoque Caeli et Aëris Diaphanitatem…\"</i>) is syntactically tangled in the original — the chain of subordinate clauses around <i>\"non sequi id, quod tu intendebas… propterea ex vna et eadem constare materia\"</i> is reproduced with bracketed connective words to keep Tycho's actual (deliberately convoluted) logic rather than a tidied paraphrase; (2) <i>Surreptor mei Systematis</i> is left as \"Filcher of my System\" with the Wittich-vs-Ursus identification noted, not asserted, since the source names no one; (3) <i>Graminaeus</i> is treated as the proper name Theodor Graminaeus, the likeliest referent, but the letter gives only \"a certain Graminaeus.\" None of these rises to a sense-reversing crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief this is audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B; no Dreyer page images exist to collate. Checked systematically against the Latin: opening/closing formulae, the 1589-11-24 Uraniborg date and signature, every proper name (Vallesius, Daneau, Peucer x3, Pena, Rantzau, Frischlin, Schonfeldt, Wittich, 'Graminaeus', Hermes Trismegistus), every number and date (17 degrees twilight, 7/8 students, 16 eyes, 2-cubit/4-cubit/5-cubit/20+ instruments, 6 Dec 1588, 2 Jan 1589, ~10 degrees, comet years '85/'58, 150 paces, 700x, 2284 semidiameters, 7,850,000, 12-ell wheel, 800 stars, 5 minutes), the custody of the Epistolae Astronomicae correspondence (correctly kept with Tycho, who received the letters and is publishing them with his own replies -- not misattributed), and several sarcastic self-boast passages (the sixteen-eyes/twenty-instruments taunt, 'primus forte omnium ... probare elaboraui', the Peucer-as-judge passage) where subject/object assignment is correctly preserved in each case, not flipped. The restored postscript (Dreyer VI, 199-200) is present in both source_text and published_english and matches closely. One low-severity mistranslation found in the postscript (the 'Hermas'/boundary-markers allusion rendered as an invented Hermes-simile); no fabrication, no omission, no dropped closing, no misattributed custody, no invented day-number found elsewhere in this very long letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"Hermas\" is the accusative object of \"inquirere\" (\"to track down the boundary-markers [hermae] of the Stars\", i.e. to fix their true positions, echoing Virgil's 'tantae molis erat'), not a simile subject; the English invents a comparison ('like a Hermes') that has no counterpart and drops the actual object of the verb. At: “it may become plain how great a task it was to investigate the Stars, like a Hermes”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the underlying Latin (\"viaque simul constare queat, Quantae molis erat Stellarum inquirere Hermas\") could not be collated against the printed edition and is left untouched. The MISTRANSLATION is nonetheless demonstrable from internal grammatical evidence alone: \"Hermas\" is unambiguously the accusative-plural direct object of \"inquirere\" governed by the genitive \"Stellarum\", not a simile subject, and the letter's own later genuine allusion to \"Hermes Thrice-Greatest [<i>Trismegistus</i>]\" plausibly explains how the translator's eye was drawn to the wrong word. Graded B per the brief's rule (sound but uncollated), matching the incoming audited_grade.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 13, "prev": "brahe-t_15890822001", "next": "brahe-t_15900223002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890822001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900418001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Clarifies that his previous letter accused Rothmann not of impiety but only of misapplying Moses's description of the Moon as 'lesser light' to questions of physical magnitude.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891124001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891130001", "d": "1589-11-30", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 30 November 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.31</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ANTIQUA NOBILITATE VIRO CLARISS. ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTI D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, URANIEBURGI DOMINO, D. ET AMICO OBSERVANDO.<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISS. Domine et amice observande. Cum intellexissem Legatum Regis vestri D. VINSHEMIUM ad nostrum Imp. venisse, quam primum mihi per valetudinem licuit, statim eum adij et percontatus sum, an aliquid literarum abs te mihi attulisset. Iam enim diu exoptabam tuas, quibus saltem edocerer de meis ad te, num omnes, quas scripsi, aut aliquae illarum tibi essent redditae. Cum autem ille negaret se quicquam habere abs te, vellicare animum meum coepit cogitatio, num mihi succenseres putaresque me data opera ad tuas respondere noluisse. Hoc si ita est, obsecro te, mi TYCHO, depone hanc suspicionem ex animo tuo. Non induo nunc in hoc meo senio, crede mihi, illam barbariem, ut ad amici literas non respondeam. Absit illud longe a me, ita me Deus amet, mi TYCHO, respondi ad singulas tuas semper. Quod si tibi redditae non sunt, non mihi, quaeso, imputa, sed iniquae sorti, quae nobis invidet illam voluptatem, quam uterque capturi eramus ex literarum lectione, et hominum infidelitati. Annus est integer, cum per civem quendam Gorliciensem, opera MYLIJ, dedi ad te literas unâ cum opusculo VERNERI, quod aliquando a me expetebas. Haec cum literis SCULTETI ad te perferri debebant. Quid cum illis factum sit, nescio, postea cum hoc vere missus esset nobilis vestras a Rege ad Imp. cum falconibus, ac ille quoque tuas ad me attulisset literas, ipse eum alloquutus dixi me ipsi literas ad te daturum esse. Sed cum biduo abessem urbe medicinae faciendae gratia, ille interea praeter spem et nostrae aulae consuetudinem citius expeditus hinc discessit, quod quam aegre tulerim, dici non potest. Nactus autem mox altero aut tertio die amicum, apud quem deplorabam elapsam mihi occasionem mittendi literas ad te, statim ille suam mihi detulit operam, dixitque se Dresdae habere amicum, cuius opera fidelissime perferrentur ad te. Dedi itaque illi meas, quibus adhuc alias adiunxi. Si non sunt tibi redditae, ne mihi culpam adscribas rogo, imo vero hanc meam iustam salva conscientia excusationem eo animo, quo amicum decet, accipias, meque primo quoque tempore de his omnibus edoceas, animumque meum nimis solicitum hanc ob rem tranquillum reddas. Haec repeto hisce meis, cum ignorem, qualem cursum habuerint priores meae.</p><p>Quid impediverit, quod huc usque primus liber tuus non prodierit, quidve nunc prae manibus habeas, ut me certiorem reddas magnopere peto. Mea Mathemata iam diu quiescunt et quasi in DEMOCRITI puteo abdita latent. Si mihi Deus longius vitae curriculum concesserit, cogito in meis aedibus extruere aliquam speculam, unde aliquando astra contemplari possem.</p><p>Aestate praeterita partem meae domus reparare coepi. Aliud, quod scribam, non habeo. De rebus Gallicis veriora vos istic habere existimo. Ad nos varia et incerta afferuntur iuxta hominum affectiones varias. Illud certissimum habetur, Regi huc usque feliciter successisse. Genevae obsidionem solutam narrant, relicta tamen fuisse quaedam praesidia in obsidione, quae Genevenses effusi ex urbe dispulerunt et novum commeatum in urbem invexerunt. Deus suam Ecclesiam contra tyrannos potenti sua manu tueatur et defendat.</p><p>B. V. mi amantiss. TYCHO.<br>Datae Pragae die ultima Novemb. veteri stylo Anno 1589.<br>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To a man of ancient nobility, most renowned and outstanding in learning, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Uraniborg, his lord, and friend to be esteemed.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Most noble lord and friend to be esteemed. When I had learned that your King's legate, Master Vinshemius, had come to our Emperor, as soon as my health allowed me I went to him at once and inquired whether he had brought me any letter from you. For I had long been most eager for word from you, by which I might at least be informed about my own [letters] to you — whether all those I wrote, or any of them, had been delivered to you. But when he denied having anything from you, a thought began to gnaw at my mind: whether you might be angry with me and think that I had deliberately refused to answer your letters. If this is so, I beseech you, my Tycho, put this suspicion out of your mind. I am not now, believe me, in this my old age, taking on that barbarousness of not answering a friend's letters. Far be that from me — so help me God, my Tycho — I have always answered each of yours. And if they have not been delivered to you, do not, I pray, charge it to me, but to unkind fortune, which begrudges us that pleasure which we were each to take from the reading of letters, and to the faithlessness of men. It is a full year since, through a certain citizen of Görlitz, by the agency of Mylius, I dispatched a letter to you together with the little work of Werner which you had once been asking of me. These were to be conveyed to you with Scultetus's letter. What became of them I do not know. Afterwards, when this spring a nobleman of yours had been sent by the King to the Emperor with falcons, and he too had brought me your letter, I myself spoke with him and said that I would give him a letter for you. But since I was away from the city for two days for the practice of medicine, he meanwhile — beyond my expectation and contrary to the custom of our court — was dispatched more quickly and departed hence, at which I cannot say how grieved I was. But having soon, on the second or third day, come upon a friend, to whom I was lamenting the lost opportunity of sending a letter to you, he straightway offered me his service, and said that he had a friend at Dresden, by whose agency they would be conveyed to you most faithfully. And so I gave him mine, to which I added yet others. If they have not been delivered to you, I beg you not to ascribe the fault to me, but rather to accept this just excuse of mine — [made] with a clear conscience — in the spirit that befits a friend, and to inform me about all these matters at the very first opportunity, and to render my mind, too anxious on this account, tranquil. I repeat these things in this present letter of mine, since I do not know what course my earlier ones have taken.</p><p>What has hindered your first book from coming out hitherto, or what you now have in hand — I greatly ask that you make me more certain of it. My own mathematical studies have long lain at rest and lie hidden, as it were, in Democritus's well. If God grants me a longer course of life, I am thinking of building some little observatory at my house, whence I might one day contemplate the stars.</p><p>This past summer I began to repair part of my house. I have nothing else to write. Of French affairs I judge that you there have truer [reports]. To us things are brought various and uncertain, according to men's various dispositions. This is held most certain: that the King has hitherto succeeded happily. They report that the siege of Geneva has been raised — yet that certain garrisons had been left behind in the siege, which the Genevans, pouring out from the city, drove off, and brought a fresh supply of provisions into the city. May God watch over and defend His Church against the tyrants with His mighty hand.</p><p>Fare you well, my most loving Tycho.<br>Given at Prague, on the last day of November, Old Style, in the year 1589.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[deus].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>THAD.</i> / <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and personal physician to the Emperor at Prague; one of Tycho's most important Central-European correspondents, and later instrumental in bringing Tycho into Imperial service. The closing subscription contracts his name to <i>THAD.</i>; <i>T. studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tui studiosissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to you\" (per protocol §5.5). Dreyer prints the letter in <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII among Tycho's incoming correspondence.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, URANIEBURGI DOMINO</i> — \"Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Uraniborg.\" <i>Uranieburgi</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>Salutem</i> (\"Greeting,\" §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Legatum Regis vestri D. VINSHEMIUM</b></i> — \"your King's legate, Master Vinshemius\": an envoy of <b>Frederik II's</b> son and successor <b>Christian IV</b> (a minor; the regency government) to the Imperial court of <b>Rudolf II</b>. The name <i>Vinshemius</i> (Winsheim / Veit Ortel von Winsheim and family) recurs in this same Hagecius–Tycho correspondence (the corpus preserves a <i>VITUS VINSHEMIUS</i> returning from Wittenberg with Lutheran tracts for Tycho), so the reading is secure as a surname even though the legate's full identity is not fixed by this letter alone.</li><li><b><i>nostrum Imp.</b></i> = <i>nostrum Imperatorem</i>, \"our Emperor\" — <b>Rudolf II</b>, Holy Roman Emperor, resident at Prague, whose physician Hagecius was (\"our court,\" <i>nostrae aulae</i>, below).</li><li><b><i>opusculo VERNERI</b></i> — \"the little work of Werner\": <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg, almost certainly his <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (Nuremberg, 1522). Tycho avidly pursued this tract in the years of his prosthaphaeresis-priority quarrel with Ursus (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, on Tycho's \"avid pursuit … of the <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> of the Nuremberg astronomer Johannes Werner\"); the present letter documents Hagecius procuring a copy for him.</li><li><b><i>per civem quendam Gorliciensem … cum literis SCULTETI</b></i> — \"through a certain citizen of Görlitz … with Scultetus's letter\": <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomäus Schultz, 1540–1614), the Görlitz mathematician, surveyor, and burgomaster, a mutual friend and a regular relay-point in Tycho's correspondence (Dreyer lists \"Scultetus at Leipzig\" among Tycho's foreign correspondents). The Görlitz citizen is the carrier; Scultetus's own letter was to travel in the same packet.</li><li><b><i>opera MYLIJ</b></i> — \"by the agency of Mylius\": an intermediary, <i>Mylius</i> (the latinised form of <i>Mühl/Müller/Möller</i>); not further specified in the letter and left unidentified rather than guessed.</li><li><b><i>nobilis vestras … cum falconibus</b></i> — \"a nobleman of yours … with falcons\": a Danish nobleman sent by the crown to the Emperor bearing falcons — Danish (and especially Icelandic/Norwegian) falcons were a standing diplomatic gift to the Habsburg court. <i>vestras</i> (for <i>vester</i>) is a chancery/period spelling, \"of yours\" (Danish).</li><li><b><i>in DEMOCRITI puteo abdita latent</b></i> — \"lie hidden, as it were, in Democritus's well\": the proverbial <i>Democritus's well</i> (cf. <i>veritas in puteo</i> — truth lies hidden in a well), the saying attributed to Democritus that truth lies sunk in the depths; Hagecius means his mathematical work lies buried out of sight. The classical allusion is kept.</li><li><b><i>speculam … unde astra contemplari possem</b></i> — \"some little observatory … whence I might contemplate the stars\": <i>specula</i> in its astronomical sense, a watch-tower / observatory. A poignant aspiration from a man of 64, set against Tycho's Uraniborg.</li><li><b><i>primus liber tuus</b></i> — \"your first book\": <b>Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</b></i> (the first volume of the <i>Astronomical Letters</i>), which Tycho was then printing at his own Uraniborg press; it appeared in 1596. Hagecius asks why it has not yet come out — a securely datable reference to the work's protracted production.</li><li><b>French affairs / the King.</b> <i>De rebus Gallicis … Regi huc usque feliciter successisse</i> — the French Wars of Religion: \"the King\" is <b>Henri IV of Navarre</b>, who had succeeded to the French throne on the assassination of Henri III (1 August 1589) and was campaigning against the Catholic League; Hagecius reports (from a Protestant standpoint) that he has so far prospered.</li><li><b><i>Genevae obsidionem solutam</b></i> — \"the siege of Geneva raised\": Protestant Geneva was under pressure from <b>Charles Emmanuel I, Duke of Savoy</b> in 1589 (the opening of the Savoy–Geneva war). Hagecius relays a report — explicitly marked as <i>varia et incerta</i>, \"various and uncertain\" — that the siege had been lifted and the Genevans had driven off the besieging garrisons and revictualled the city. The epistemic hedge is preserved, not sharpened into a flat statement of fact.</li><li><b><i>Deus suam Ecclesiam contra tyrannos … tueatur</b></i> — \"May God … defend His Church against the tyrants\": the optative subjunctive <i>tueatur … et defendat</i> is rendered as a prayer (\"May God …\"), matching the Protestant-confessional register of the war news.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene vale</i>, \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Datae Pragae … T. studiosiss. THAD.</i> = \"Given at Prague … Most devoted to you, Thad[deus].\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die ultima Novemb. veteri stylo Anno 1589</i> = \"on the last day of November, Old Style, in the year 1589\" = <b>30 November 1589 (O.S.)</b>. Hagecius, writing at the Catholic Imperial court (which had adopted the Gregorian calendar), expressly flags <i>veteri stylo</i> (\"Old Style\") to align his date with Tycho's Danish reckoning — itself a small monument to the calendar gap discussed in protocol §3. The as-written date agrees with the catalogue label (30 November 1589); no discrepancy. No silent conversion made.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the only soft points are extratextual identifications left open rather than forced: <i>Mylius</i> (an unidentified relay agent) and the precise individual behind <i>D. Vinshemium</i> (the surname is corroborated within the same correspondence, but this letter does not fix the man). Both are flagged above, not smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VII pp.220-222 (page images, not OCR text layer), sentence by sentence including the opening/closing formulae, every proper name (Vinshemius, Mylius, Werner, Scultetus, Democritus), and every date/number (a full year, two or three days, 30 November 1589 O.S.). No divergence found between Dreyer, source_text, and the English translation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1589 (4 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/29-31", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964071"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.31."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 19, "prev": "brahe-t_15891101001", "next": "brahe-t_15900125001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15891101001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900125001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports approaching the royal legate Vinshemius for a letter from Tycho but finding none, and protests that he has always answered Tycho's letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891130001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15891216001", "d": "1589-12-16", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Poul Madsen", "ck": "Poul Madsen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Poul Madsen — Uraniborg, 16 December 1589 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums arkiv [old signature: KA 188], original letter</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO, D. DOCTORI PAULO MATTHIAe, SUPERINTENDENTI SELANDENSI ET NUNC RECTORI ACADEMIAe HAFNIENSIS MAGNIFICO, AMICO PLURIMUM DILECTO.</p><p>CLARISSIME et doctissime d. doctor Paule, amice dilecte. Constitueram iam aliquamdiu Hafniam ire, vt cum domino cancellario cum de alijs, tum de Gellij negotio, de quo vltimo per m. Paulum Petrj, jllustrissimi ducis Vdalrici paedagogum, commonefierj curaui, verum, interim dum frigus asperius continue durat, veritus, id quod iam prae foribus est, fretum videlicet hoc congelatum iri, reditumque maturiorem interclusurum, profectionem hanc consulto in commodioris aurae statum distuli. Rogo autem peramanter, vt interea, dum ego absum, nihil Gellij nomine ob promotionem magisterij nondum susceptam, vel a te vel quoquam alio ad dominum cancellarium perferatur, quod Gellio meo vllo damno aut detrimento cedat. Jd enim si factum fuerit, cogor ego postmodum omnem rem apud dominum cancellarium excusare, atque ea referre, quae lubentius omitterem. Suasu enim et consilio d. doct. Andreae (bonae memoriae) id factum est, quod promotio illa dilata sit, quemadmodum ego illi paucis diebus ante obitum perscripsi, et meapte manu eius formalia verba, quae mihi aliquando in aurem dixerat, in memoriam reuocaui; ignorabam enim illum lethaliter decumbere. Atque ipsae literae in ipsius archiuis adhuc reperientur; ne quis me post descessum eius haec data opera excogitare suspicari queat. Pro tua itaque prudentia fac hac in parte id, quod omnibus consultius erit. Quam primum celi clementia opportunitatem concesserit dominum cancellarium accedendi, non diffido, quin ab ipso impetraturus sim, vt cum Gellio hac in parte certas ob caussas dispensetur, quodque tam vos quam ille sitis extra periculum futuri. Haec tibi breuibus per literas nunc indicanda duxi, cum ipsemet tam cito atque vellem praesens adesse nequeam. Rogo, vt haec in bonam partem, quemadmodum etiam scripta sunt, accipere velis, atque per presentem nuntium sententiam tuam hac de re vicissim significare.</p><p>Vale quam felicissime.<br>Datum Vraniburgi die 16 decembris anno 89.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu pp:a.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most learned man, Master Doctor Paulus Matthiae, Superintendent of Zealand and now Magnificent Rector of the University of Copenhagen, a friend most dearly beloved.</b></p><p>Most distinguished and most learned Master Doctor Poul, beloved friend. I had resolved for some while now to go to Copenhagen, to confer with the Lord Chancellor both about other matters and about the affair of Gellius — concerning which last [matter] I lately took care to remind [him] through Master Paulus Petri, tutor to the Most Illustrious Duke Ulrik; but meanwhile, while the rather harsh cold keeps on unbroken, fearing what is now at the very door — namely that this Sound will freeze over and cut off [my] timely return — I have deliberately put off this journey until a milder state of the air. And so I ask most affectionately that meanwhile, while I am away, nothing be conveyed in Gellius's name — on account of the master's degree not yet taken up — by you or by anyone else to the Lord Chancellor that might turn out to any loss or detriment of my [friend] Gellius. For if that should be done, I am afterwards compelled to make the whole matter excused before the Lord Chancellor, and to report things that I would more gladly leave unsaid. For it was by the urging and counsel of Doctor Andreas (of blessed memory) that this came about, that that promotion was deferred — just as I wrote out to him at length a few days before [his] death, and with my own hand recalled to memory his formal words, which he had once spoken to me in [my] ear; for I did not know that he lay mortally [ill]. And the very letter will still be found in his archives, lest anyone be able to suspect that I have, after his decease, thought up these things on purpose. Therefore, according to your prudence, do in this part that which will be the more advisable for all. As soon as the clemency of the sky grants opportunity of approaching the Lord Chancellor, I do not doubt that I shall obtain from him that Gellius be, in this part, dispensed for certain reasons, and that both you and he will be out of danger henceforth. These things I have judged should be set forth to you now briefly by letter, since I myself cannot be present in person as soon as I would wish. I ask that you be willing to take these things in good part, even as they are also written, and through the present messenger to signify to me in turn your opinion on this matter.</p><p>Fare you most happily.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 16th day of December, in the year [15]89.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>by his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Paulus Matthiae</i> = <b>Poul Madsen</b> (Paulus Matthiæ, c. 1527–1590), theologian, <b>Superintendent (Lutheran bishop) of Zealand</b> and at this date <b>Rector</b> of the University of Copenhagen (\"now … Magnificent Rector\"). As both diocesan superintendent and university head he was the natural authority on a degree-promotion question.</li><li><b>Sender's subscription.</b> <i>manu pp:a</i> = <i>manu propria</i>, \"by his own hand\" — Tycho signs the original autograph; the MS witness (A) is indeed the <i>Originalbrev</i>.</li><li><b><i>Gellius</b></i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Gellius Sascerides Cimber, 1562–1612), Tycho's former student, who had returned from Basel with an M.D. and was then seeking his <b>magisterium</b> (M.A.) at Copenhagen. He later (1593–95) became engaged to Tycho's eldest daughter Magdalene — the celebrated broken-betrothal scandal — but that is several years after this letter; here the concern is purely the timing of his academic promotion.</li><li><b><i>promotionem magisterij</b></i> — \"the promotion to the master's degree\" (the conferral of the M.A.), rendered \"the master's degree.\" <i>promotio … dilata sit</i> = \"that promotion was deferred.\"</li><li><b>Lord Chancellor (<i>dominus cancellarius</i>).</b> Unnamed in the letter, but in 1589 the office was held by <b>Niels Kaas</b> (1535–1594), Royal Chancellor of Denmark. He is referred to throughout only by title, and the translation keeps the title without inserting the name.</li><li><b><i>m. Paulus Petrj … jllustrissimi ducis Vdalrici paedagogum</b></i> = \"Master Paulus Petri, tutor (<i>paedagogus</i>) to the Most Illustrious <b>Duke Ulrik</b>\" — i.e. <b>Ulrik III, Duke of Mecklenburg</b> (1527–1603), Frederik II's father-in-law (father of Queen Sophie). <i>paedagogus</i> here = a princely tutor/governor, rendered \"tutor.\" Paulus Petri is not further identified.</li><li><b><i>d. doct. Andreae (bonae memoriae)</b></i> — \"Doctor Andreas, of blessed memory\": a Copenhagen doctor (academic) who had recently died and who had advised that Gellius's promotion be deferred. The text makes clear he died shortly after Tycho wrote to him (\"a few days before death,\" \"I did not know that he lay mortally [ill]\"). <b>Identification crux:</b> the period form <i>Andreas</i> could point to more than one Copenhagen academic of the day; the mathematician-physician <b>Anders Krag</b> is excluded, since he did not die until 1600. The man is therefore left identified only as the letter identifies him — a recently-deceased Doctor Andreas — rather than asserted to a particular individual.</li><li><b><i>bonae memoriae</b></i> — the standard formula for a person lately deceased; rendered \"of blessed memory.\"</li><li><b><i>fretum … congelatum iri</b></i> — \"this Sound will freeze over\": <i>fretum</i> is the <b>Øresund</b> between Hven/Copenhagen and the mainland, whose freezing would strand Tycho. Rendered \"this Sound.\" The winter-ice fear is the stated reason for postponing the trip.</li><li><b><i>celi clementia … aurae statum</b></i> — astronomical/weather idiom: <i>commodioris aurae statum</i> \"a milder state of the air,\" <i>celi clementia</i> \"the clemency of the sky [= weather]\"; kept literal, as the meteorological sense is the point (travel across the ice-threatened Sound).</li><li><b><i>data opera</b></i> — adverbial, \"on purpose / deliberately\"; here \"thought up these things on purpose\" (i.e. fabricated them after the doctor's death). Distinct from the earlier <i>consulto</i> (\"deliberately\") describing the postponed journey; both kept.</li><li><b><i>dispensetur … certas ob caussas</b></i> — \"be dispensed for certain reasons\": <i>dispensatio</i> in the academic/canonical sense = a formal exemption from a requirement (here, evidently, from whatever condition had stalled the M.A.). Rendered \"be … dispensed,\" the impersonal passive preserved.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Salutation <i>amico plurimum dilecto</i> → \"a friend most dearly beloved\"; closing <i>Vale quam felicissime</i> → \"Fare you most happily\" (per §5.5, <i>Vale</i> = Farewell / Fare you well, here intensified). <i>Datum … die 16 decembris anno 89</i> → \"Given … the 16th day of December, in the year [15]89\"; the elided century is supplied in brackets.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline reads <i>die 16 decembris anno 89</i> (= 16 December [15]89, <b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The header date agrees with the dateline; the dataset label (\"1589. 16. december. Uraniborg\") agrees. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the legal/conditional force (the request that <i>nothing</i> be conveyed that might harm Gellius; the <i>if that should be done</i> clause) is reproduced exactly. The one open point is the identity of the deceased <i>Doctor Andreas</i> whose counsel had deferred the promotion, flagged above and left unasserted rather than guessed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 slice pages (TBOO XIV pp. 49-52) visually. The letter (no. 79, dated 1589, 16 decbr., addressed to Paulus Matthiae/Poul Madsen) runs from the bottom of p. 50 through p. 51, ending 'Tycho Brahe / manu pp:a. anno 89.' followed by Dreyer's source note ('Ex originali in Bibliotheca Universitatis Hauniensis: Konsistoriums Arkiv, Pakke 188.'). Collated the full Latin text word-for-word against source_text: heading, salutation, and body all match Dreyer's print exactly modulo long-s typeface (f/s), i/j spelling (Vdalrici/Vdalricj), and ae/æ ligature conventions — none of which are treated as defects per the audit rules. Checked every proper name (Paulus Matthiae/Poul, Duke Ulrik/Vdalricus, Master Paulus Petri, Doctor Andreas, Gellius/Gellius Sascerides per Dreyer's argument-line), the date (16 December 1589/anno 89), and the place (Uraniborg/Vraniburgi) across source_text, Dreyer, and published_english — all consistent. Checked published_english sentence-by-sentence against source_text: opening dedication, the Copenhagen-trip/frozen-Sound passage, the Gellius-promotion request, the Doctor Andreas anecdote and the archived-letter claim, the closing dispensation hope, and the closing formula are all rendered faithfully with correct subjects, tenses, and clause structure. No fabricated, omitted, or mistranslated content found; no hedge losses; no divergence between source_text and Dreyer's print.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums arkiv, [gammel signatur: KA 188]. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Postpones his visit to Copenhagen and asks Madsen to prevent any communication to the Lord Chancellor in Gellius's name regarding his not-yet-received master's degree.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15891216001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900000001", "d": "1590-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Caspar Peucer", "ck": "Caspar Peucer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Caspar Peucer — Uraniborg, [1590, undated]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Hebrew biblical lemmata and Scripture quotations · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter, 179 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> One of Tycho's longest and most important letters of cosmological–theological argument: his reply to <b>Caspar Peucer</b> on (i) the <b>nature and substance of the heavens</b> (no solid orbs; not per se luminous; not \"fiery\"), (ii) the <b>\"waters above the firmament\"</b> — a sustained refutation of the supercelestial-waters reading of Genesis 1, with <b>Hebrew exegesis</b> and patristic/reformer authorities, (iii) the <b>Milky Way</b> and the <b>New Star of 1572</b> as condensations of celestial matter, (iv) the <b>wholly celestial matter of comets</b> (against being made of exhalations), (v) <b>metals/alchemy</b> (\"terrestrial astronomy\"), (vi) <b>Venus and Mercury</b>, and (vii) <b>Rothmann's Copernicanism</b>. <b>Two declared interventions in the source block:</b> (1) the working transcription is densely peppered with <b>OCR-intruded Dreyer apparatus sigla</b> — a stray superscript <b>\"A\"</b> (the footnote-anchor keying a word to witness A) tacked onto ~90 words, plus a couple of stray \"<i>\" footnote-markers — these are <b>non-textual</b> and have been removed (not silently normalised; the same treatment as in the Scaliger letter of 23 Aug 1598). (2) The transcription <b>lost or garbled the Hebrew</b>: Dreyer prints the actual Hebrew lemmata of the Genesis/Psalms discussion, but the working text renders most as \"XXX\" and one as a scrambled fragment (</i>עיקד<i> for </i>רקיע<i>). Each lost lemma is <b>editorially supplied in [square brackets]</b> from the unambiguous context (the standard text of Gen 1:6–7, Ps 104, Jer 10/51) and itemised in the Apparatus; one analogical word-pair Tycho cites as a grammatical parallel is genuinely <b>illegible</b> and marked </i>[…]<i>. Two short transcription <b>lacunae</b> (in the alchemy and Craig-Apologia sentences) are marked </i>…<i>. <b>Dating:</b> the letter carries <b>no dateline</b> (it ends simply \"Vale\"); the catalogue assigns <b>1590</b> (the label reads \"1590. empty\"), consistent with the internal evidence — the </i>Apologeticum<i> against Craig \"not yet received,\" the </i>Epistolae astronomicae<i> and </i>Progymnasmata<i> Tome I in preparation, Bongars's embassy, and Peucer \"freed in old age from so many hardships\" (released from his Crypto-Calvinist imprisonment in 1586). Header leads with the catalogued year, flagged undated. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> the reply <b>was located and collated</b> — Dreyer, </i>Opera Omnia<i> <b>vol. VII pp. 228–234</b> (PDF pp. 242–248), running head </i>\"TYCHO AD CASPARUM PEUCERUM 1590,\"<i> source-line </i>\"[Ex exemplo Hauniensi excerpsit Langebek. 178].\"<i> (An earlier pass had found only <b>Peucer's inbound letter</b> of 10 May 1589 at Dreyer pp. ~170–177; the reply sits ~50 pp. later.) The Latin context — </i>Uranoscopium<i>, the Castalio/Pagninus/Moses citations, the </i>aquae supercaelestes<i> refutation, Gen 1, Jer 10, Ps 104/18 — collates and <b>matches</b> the working text. <b>One residual limitation, on the Hebrew alone:</b> Dreyer </i>does<i> print the Hebrew lemmata at pp. 232–234, but the scan's OCR renders the Hebrew glyphs as Latin-script garbage, so the supplied forms (</i>raqiaʿ<i> רקיע, </i>shamayim<i> שמים, </i>mayim<i> מים) cannot be machine-verified against Dreyer's type — they rest on the confirmed Latin context, the fixed biblical text, and the surviving transcription </i>mayim*; a definitive glyph-check needs an image read of PDF pp. 246–248. The argument's through-line is secure.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CASPARUM PEUCERUM.</p><p>CUM paulo ante nostratia, quae quotannis circa Solstitium Aestivum celebrantur, Comitia tuas, excellentissime Domine D. PEUCERE, accepissem literas, et mihi illuc tam de more anniversario quam quod quaedam ibidem transigenda haberem, necessario accedendum foret, distuli responsionem usque ad reditum meum et quietiorem in hoc, quod vocas, Uranoscopio, statum. Accidit autem, ut, dum Hafniam ad id tempus excurro, Dn. BONGERSIUM Gallum a Rege suo huc ablegatum offenderim, qui et alteras, licet prioribus breviores a te mihi inscriptas, reddidit, unâque pollicitus est, se ante reditum in Patriam me in hac Insula invisurum, meamque responsionem ad tuas hac occasione secum relaturum, tibique, quem denuo in Germania alloqui constituerat, traditurum. Interim vero negotijs, nescio quibus, urgentibus, hinc in Teutoniam secedit, brevi, uti dicebatur, reversurus. Dum vero ego tota hac elapsa aestate reditum eius, quamvis incassum, nulla interea certiore perferendi ad te literas sese offerente commoditate, expecto, plurimisque involvor distractionibus, rescriptionem utut forte nimis serotinam in hoc usque tempus differre coactus sum. Et licet ne iam quidem oblata aliqua has ad te mittendi in promptu sit ansa, nec adhuc curis omnibus relaxatus sim, attamen ne nimis diu procrastinando ad tuas plane surdus tibi videar, volui nunc tandem has ad te responsionis caussa dare, maximopere rogans, ut silentium hoc diutinum aequi bonique consulas.</p><p>Duo autem sunt potissimum, de quibus in hisce tuis literis, nempe de Coeli natura et substantia, tumque nubibus ac aquis, quas supra Coelos existere opinaris, prolixe mecum disseris. Quoad primum, quantum ad ipsam Coeli tenuissimam purissimamque naturam et ab Elementaribus coinquinamentis corruptelisque immunitatem attinet, ego nullatenus tibi refragor. Nam et ego sic semper sensi, posteaquam Coelum nullis solidis et duris confectum Orbibus ex motu non saltem Planetarum sed et Cometarum Aethereorum, quos quinos citra Orbis ullius realis concomitantiam Coelestes carpere vias observavi, accurata me edocuerit confirmâritque experientia. At quod Aetheream Regionem per se lucidam splendescentemque existimas, an omnino hîc assentiendum sit, haereo, ex quo in tenebris nocturnis, defaecato existente aëre, nullum ad nos reflectat lumen, id quod fieri oporteret, si actu lucidum esset, tenebrosis praesertim aquis et nubibus superne illud, uti tu asseris, circumdantibus, quae luminis versus centrum repercussionem augerent. Quicquid enim claritatis et fulgoris nox alias opaca Coelo sereno acquirit, id a Luna per reflectionem luminis Solaris aut etiam a Stellis fulgentibus obtinet, non autem ab ipso Coeli corpore. Id enim citra Stellas aut Luminaria nullam oculis lucem ingerit. Verum an aliquam per se habeat, non tamen tantam, quae Terras percellat, quemadmodum et Lunam proprij luminis non prorsus orbam esse (licet ad intuitum nostrum, ut in Eclipsatione eius profundiore apparet, non pertingat) quidam existimant, studiosius, cum sit imperceptibile, non inquiro. Nec de hac quaestione, an Coelum per se luce actuali polleat, disquirenda, multum laboro, cum haec nostrae intentioni, quomodocumque hac de re statuatur, nihil officiat. Id saltem addam, quod lucem hanc Coelestem divinae naturae proximam esse statuis, ideoque Aethereum Mundum a mutationibus vindicare, videtur nimium quid esse, et ARISTOTELIS opinioni, qui Coelo divinitatem ideoque et aeternitatem attribuit, quam proxime subscribere. Ego certe, si qua lux creata cum divina merito conferri posset, eam quam Deus Angelis et hominum Mentibus indidit, utpote intellectualem, longe propius accedere censuerim. Verumtamen quod hac ratiocinatione Aetheream et Elementarem sive Aëream potissimum Regionem non inter se confundis, ut quidam alij hoc tempore ex sententia IOHANNIS PENAe Galli, ut opinor, praesumserunt, nec ullas aquas, nihilque vaporosum aut halituosum in Aethera ipsum ascendere censes, id admodum convenienter a te statuitur, nostraeque de his sententiae apprime quadrat. Neque ego, quod aliter sentires, tibi unquam tribui, vel ex tuis scriptis colligi posse asserui. Ideoque nuspiam hoc tibi a me obiectum in meis invenies literis, ut ob id mirer, qua inconsiderata mearum interpretatione id mihi imputes. Cuiusmodi est etiam illud quod mox subiungis, me Coeli naturam igneam affirmare: id meae equidem sententiae, qui Coelum ab omni semper Elementari qualitate vindicandum existimavi, nullatenus correspondet. Quamvis autem per diversas appellationis haebraicae Etymologias, expositionem illam, quasi Coelum sit stupendum quid ex igni et aqua, tanquam diversis naturis conflatum, composuerim, tamen id non eo cedit, quod ipsum aut igneum aut aqueum esse reverâ statuam, sed sic saltem in Etymologijs varie concinnandis ludere placuit. Neque enim hinc (cum metaphorice id saltem dici posse et non secundum Elementaris naturae qualitates ipsemet diserte protestatus sim) serio aliquid probare elaboravi. Imo ipse Deus omnis materiae expers in sacris literis ut Deut. 4, 24 et 9, 3, item Haeb. 12, 29, utut nemo de ipsius vera essentia, quae imperscrutabilis est, quippiam ratiocinari potuerit, ignis nihilominus consumens appellatur. Quid igitur obstat, quo minus Coelum et Sidera Coelestia, quae in morem flammantis alicuius, ex inferioris Mundi corporibus lucent et calefaciunt, ignis vocabulo, sumpta, per similitudinem quandam nostro captui apprime sese attemperantem, appellatione nominare, aut Etymologice explicare liceat? Propterea tamen Coelum reverâ Ignem esse Elementarem, uti ferme cum PLATONE PARACELSUS prodidit, nequaquam infero. Imo ne quidem sub concava Lunae sphaera ignem aliquem Elementarem, quemadmodum somniavit ARISTOTELES, Opticis rationibus omnino refragantibus, existere arbitror. Nec etiam superiorem Aëris Regionem, ob rapidissimum corporum Coelestium motum, qui naturalis, non violentus est, inque tenuissima ac liquidissima Coeli essentia, absque omni solidorum corporum fractione aut collisione peragitur, solidissimam, quae tua est opinio, existere, sicque ignis Elementaris vices supplere, astipulandum censeo. Quin potius quicquid Physici ab hoc Elemento pro compositione et conservatione rerum inferiorum petere soleant, id ego a Coelestibus, ipso praesertim Sole, vivifico calore omnia imbuente, promanare iudico.</p><p>Alterum vero quod urges, est de aquis Coelestibus, quas ex literali quodam Scripturae sensu, supra extima Coeli moenia evehere non dubitas. Fateor quidem ex verbis MOSIS in principio Geneseos prima id fronte quadantenus apparere. At si quis penitius et genuinum Scripturae intellectum et rei naturam introspiciat, nisi omnino praeoccupato sit iudicio, longe id secus se habere intelliget. Primo tenendum est vocem [רקיע] (quam CASTALIO non inepte liquidum, alij expansum verterunt) non eodem ubique sensu in creationis historia describenda a MOSE intelligi; sed alias generaliter et latius, totum hoc liquidum seu expansum, quod a Terra, ad summa usque Coeli culmina se extendit, uti ipse quoque indicas, denotat, alias pro huius parte tantum idque vel superiore, quae lucida Siderum in se corpora continet, interdum vero pro inferiore hac liquidi regione, quae Terram proxime circumdans visuique nostro magis patescens ad extima plus minus nubium cacumina sese extendit, accipitur. Quo quidem sensu Coeli vocabulum, in Scriptura crebro, uti et hoc loco, intelligitur, cum ea ad rudiorum hominum captum, qui illam duntaxat expansi partem, quae proxime Terrae convexo incumbit, Coeli nomine appellant, sese accommodet. Quare cum MOSES hanc Creationis historiam rudi et simplici tantum populo, non Mathematicae aut Physicae scientiae gnaris, conscripserit, eaque duntaxat quae in oculos hominum incurrunt, non quae invisibilia aut abscondita sunt, exponenda susceperit, quis non videat illum per aquas Coelestes, nubes aquosas, quae superne capitibus nostris imminent atque infra se partem illius expansi seu liquidi, quod ab aquis maris eas discernit, non immensam aliquam universae Coeli regioni circumfusam aquarum molem intellexisse?</p><p>Quin et sequeretur, aut triplices a Deo creatas fuisse aquas, nempe maris, nubium et supercoelestem, quarum tamen media, nubium scilicet, a MOSE neglecta sit, aut binas saltem, utpote marinam et supercoelestem illam, de quibus duntaxat in rerum creaturum historia mentio facta est. At quis fidelissimum illum visibilem creaturarum descriptorem MOSEN tam oscitantem in mirabilibus Dei operibus referendis fuisse existimet, ut nubium aquas tam alto silentio intactas reliquerit, quod earum ne semel inter reliqua sensibilia Dei opera meminisse voluerit, cum tamen non parum admirationis habeat, eas sine fulcro in liquido aëre tam firmiter pendere absque ruinae periculo, et insuper multiplicem in terra irriganda et foecundanda utilitatem subministrare, quam homines plus quam caetera illa altiora, atque a quotidiano usu, prout illis videtur, remotiora magis aestimant atque respiciunt. Has inquam aquositates neglexisset MOSES et interim de nescio quibus supercoelestibus, quae nec sensibus patent nec usum evidentem exhibent, supervacanee loquutus fuerit. Reverâ itaque MOSEN per geminas istas quas denotat aquas, interstitio firmamenti divisas, marinam scilicet inferiorem et nubium eius aliqua parte superiorem intelligere necesse est. Nam et sic [רקיע] sive expansum, quod CASTALIO opportune uti dixi liquidum vocat, discernit aquas ab aquis, has quadam sui portione intercedens; neque enim MOSES expresse dixit totum [רקיע] disgregare aquas ab aquis, ut tu ipsius sensum accipis. Imo et is semetipse explicat Gen. 9, dum ait Deum in nubibus posuisse arcum in testimonium, quod aquae non amplius in Terrarum diluvium venturae essent. Quas enim hîc aquas, nisi nubes signat, e quibus potissimum inundatio temporibus NOE acciderat, atque hinc cataractas Coeli sive meatus, quae tunc aperiebantur, non extra Fixarum orbem, uti tu vis, petendas esse liquet. Ex IERHEMIA insuper cap. 10 et 51 ubi sequentia verba utrobique habentur: »Qui fecit Terram virtute sua, stabilijt orbem habitabilem sapientia sua, et prudentia sua extendit Coelos, quo vocem suam edente, fremitus aquarum est in Caelis, qui effert vapores ab extremitate Terrae, fulgura cum ipsa pluvia facit, et producit ventum e thesauris suis«, idipsum adhuc manifestius colligitur. Hinc etenim liquido patet, aquas illas Coelestes in ea aëris Regione, unde nubes, fulgura et pluvia producuntur, intelligendas atque eandem pro Coelo accipi.</p><p>Praeterea maxima ex aquis supracoelestibus concessis absurda sequuntur. Nam si ullae eiusmodi aquae illic existant, quae extima Coeli theatra suo circumquaque ambitu includant et complectantur, unâ vel moveantur vel quiescant necesse est? Si immotae permanent, aut rapidissimam Coeli gyrationem impediunt, aut oportebit maximum esse interstitium inter motum et quiescens, ut rapidissimus ille cursus successive in quietem desinat. Immensum itaque spatium stellis destitutum nullique usui accommodum relinqui, quod inconveniens esset, oportebit. At si unâ cum Coelo convolventur istae aquae, quî poterint concitatissimam illam rapiditatem perferre, perpetuoque unâ cum hac inconsumtibiles permanere aut illi nulla actione officere? Adde quoque de magnitudine nimia harum aquarum respectu caeterarum, quas MOSES subcoelestes vocat, per quas Maria Terrestrem globum interluentia intelligenda esse, nemini est dubium. Si enim aquae illae, quas supra octavam vis esse sphaeram, saltem unicam vel dimidiam Terrae Diametrum in sua densitate occupent, quod sane quam minimum esset respectu tantae circumferentiae et pro usu illo, quem tribuis ad lucem increatam a creaturis, modo id tibi concedendum foret, arcendam, cumque ad hanc octavam sphaeram terminandam sint ad minimum 14000 Semid. Terrae iuxta nostram hypothesium Coelestium ordinationem (quod et Ptolemaica 20000 circiter Semid. requirat et Copernicea speculatio in immensum hîc abeat) ratiocinare quaeso, quantae futurae sint illae aquae, quae tantum globum undiquaque vel in tantillula, uti dixi, crassitie ambiant, cuius Semidiameter tot millia Semid. Terrae continebit. Certe deprehendes aquarum istarum molem vix eam habere ad maria sive aquas subcoelestes aestimationem, quam habet tota aqua marina ad unicam guttam. Proinde si aquae illae Terram contingerent operirentque, uti ab initio ante discretionem factum fuisse dicitur, in ipsum Coelum sua quantitate alte ascenderent. Qualis ergo esset aquarum supra et infra expansum, de quibus MOSES loquitur, comparatio et proportio? Quapropter MOSEN aquarum supercoelestium nomine nequaquam aqueam aliquam, vastissimae Aetheris capacitate undiquaque circumfusam, uti tu censes, molem, ac non mea solum, verumetiam praestantissimorum Theologiae Professorum ut MELANTHONIS, CASTALLIONIS, CALVINI et aliorum sententia, aquosas Coeli nubes eum intelligere minime dubium est.</p><p>Sed tu nihilominus pro tua illa de Aquis supercoelestibus opinione asserenda multum laboras, utque aquis illis usum aliquem, ne forte supervacaneae sint, constituas, ais illas quasi necessitate quadam illic dispositas, ut interstitium quoddam inter conditum orbem et lucem in eo creatam et Coelos Coelorum, quos inhabitat Deus in luce aeterna, increata et inaccessa creaturis, praebeant. At quamnam hîc lucem aeternam et increatam, quosnam Coelos Coelorum intelligis? Siccine infinito et immenso finitos praescribere limites, aeterna cum temporaneis, invisibilia cum visibilibus confundere non indecorum ducis? Solus etenim Deus infinitus et increatus existit; proinde ipsi dimensio, locus, spatium, situs et id genus alia, quae creaturarum sunt attributa, competere nequeunt. Et si lux illa, quam vocas aeternam et increatam, aliud quid praeter ipsum Deum est, iam duo sunt aeterna increataque, ideoque duo dij et duo principia. At nihil increatum et aeternum est praeter ipsum Deum, qui et unicus est. Quin et si qua esset lux aeterna, extra Mundum corporeum, illa oculis humanis, etiam semotis omnibus interstitijs, non caperetur. Verissime enim in Opticis asseritur, omnia quae videntur tempore comprehendi. Unde sequitur, quae extra tempus et aeterna sunt, temporaneis oculis non patere. Ascribitur etiam Aquis istis Coelum ex tua opinione circumdantibus et haec utilitas, quod excursum visionis nostrae abscindant et definiant, ne forte extra Mundum aut in infinitum abeat. At quid hoc est dicere, cum tanta vis facultati visuali nullatenus insit? Imo si stellae non essent lucida corpora et sponte sua oculis sese ingererent, nulla alias oculorum in tanta distantia res cerneret. Frustra igitur visioni nostrae densissimam et obscurissimam illam supra Coelos aquarum interstitium praetendis, cum sua natura et conditione ab augustissima increatarum maiestate prohibeatur.</p><p>Facessat itaque ista de aquis supercoelestibus ob creatarum et increatarum inter se rerum distinctionem, visionisque nostrae radium cohercendum ac definiendum, tam devijs foecunda absurditatibus opinio. Nihil etiam ad eam quam tantopere defendendam suscipis opinionem in vulgata nominis [שמים] quasi [מים] ibi explicatione praesidij obtinebis. Est enim haec arbitraria et proprietati sanctae linguae, quae nec talem vocum, praesertim in appellativis nominibus, compositionem, nec ullum facile sine compensatione literarum, quod in hac voce sit, si composita esse statuatur, defectum admittit, contraria. Vocabulum itaque [שמים] nulla vulgari compositionum lege conflata est, sed simplex existit, et stupendum vel admirandum quid denotat a verbo [שמם], quod obstupescere vel admirari significat, non aliter quam a […] formatur […] et alia id genus, deductum. Ideoque PAGNINUS in suo lexico hoc nomen suo radicali verbo non immerito statim subiunxit. Revera etenim Coelum admirandum et stupendum quiddam existit sive motuum leges perennes nec cuivis obvias, sive materiam, ex qua constat, quae inexplicabilis est, spectemus.</p><p>At excipis, nubes has aquosas, de quibus ego MOSEN intelligo, non esse perpetuas, eo quod interdum sive nonnunquam dissolvantur et huc illuc volitent et ferantur, ideoque MOSEN per aquas Coelestes, non quae momentanea, sed quae semper durant, intellexisse. Verum quidem est, nubes, quas ego pro aquis Coelestibus habeo, non semper in quolibet Horizonte apparere, sed ut plurimum; attamen si non actu, saltem potentia semper ibi sunt. Et vis illa, qua in sublimi coalescunt, atque ne decidant, sustinentur, perpetuo illic adest. Adde quod universam Terram in toto suo circuitu respiciendo vix dabitur omnes Horizontes simul et semel a nubibus superne impendentibus immunes esse, quin in aliquo saltem Terreni globi Horizonte conspiciantur.</p><p>Ad Scripturae demum testimonia ulterius provocas, ex quibus literali quadam nimisque rigida pro sententia tua stabilienda explicatione auxilium, sed frustra, imploras. Neutiquam enim illa, quae pro te huc allegas ex psal. 104: »Qui contignat in nubibus tabernacula sua« aut ex psal. 18: »Posuit tenebras latibulum suum, in circuitu tabernaculi sui tenebrosam aquam et densas nubes«, de aquis et nubibus ullis supercoelestibus, sed de aëris Regione veluti in coenacula nubibus distincta, uti TREMELLIUS et IUNIUS item MOLLERUS et alij non inconvenienter etiam exponunt, intelligenda veniunt, nisi forte cui et ibidem ventorum atque ignium seu fulminum mentio fiat, haec quoque supra orbem stellatum locum habere censes. At ex eiusdem psalmi vers. 13, ubi sic dicitur: »Qui irrigat montes e coenaculis suis, ut a frugibus operum tuorum satietur terra«, ex quo eadem vox haebraica [עליות] quam PAGNINUS in suo dictionario haebraico coenacula seu nubes denotare existimat, utrobique habetur, nubium hîc aquas montes et terram irrigantes et foecundantes intelligendas esse, quis inficias iverit?</p><p>Praeterea cum non parum absurditatis ingerat, imo naturaliter considerando plane impossibile sit aquas supra Coelos absque fulcris et repagulis cohiberi atque convolvi, tu per Terrae in liquido aëre stabilitatem id excusare laboras. Attamen inconsequenter, cum non eadem subsit ratio. Terra enim et omnia globosa Mundi corpora proprio innituntur centro, omnesque eorum partes huc naturaliter tendunt, adeo ut si a Sole vel Luna aut caeteris sideribus aliquantulam portiunculam violenter avulsum statuamus, ea mox proprium Astrum repetit, suum affectans centrum. Tanta enim vis meditullijs Mundanorum corporum divinitus indita est. Quod sane de aquis extramundanis non consimiliter admitti potest, cum praedictum globum rotundum non totaliter compleant. Ideoque nisi certis fulcris et repagulis cohiberentur, aquas marinas, unde disgregatae erant, adeoque Terram gravitatis suae centrum, necessario reviserent. Nec valet argumentum: Deus potuit Terram crassam et gravem in tenui firmare aëre. Ergo et nubes supra Coelum. Scis enim a posse ad inesse (ut vulgo loquuntur) non sequi inductionem. Proptereaque in Psalmis diserte dicitur: »Deus omnia quae voluit fecit in Coelo et in Terra«. Et Deus est ordinis atque utilitatis author. Ideoque Coelestia Elementaribus confundere coinquinareque absque manifesto usu nequaquam volet.</p><p>Porro meam de via lactea sententiam non tam reijcis, quam abundius illustras. Ego enim, etsi Coelum liquidissimum et tenuissimum quid, Astrorum motibus ubique pervium, non tamen plane incorporeum et immateriale, sed omnia corpora Coelestia ex eadem cum Coelo constare substantia in unum aliquem densiorem globum, lucis receptioni accommodum, sapientiaque Opificis compactum esse statui. Quemadmodum videmus in hac Mundi natura, ea, quae in Mari ubique pervio generantur, ex aquea constare substantia, atque in eandem facile resolvi, licet haec ipsa iam concreta, liquida et pervia non sint. Nam et Metalla omnia Gemmaeque his affines in Mari radices habent, quod paucissimis ante PARACELSUM innotuit. Astra autem Coeli licet e materia constant Coelesti, ab ipso tamen Coelo densitate et lumine plurimum differre, non magis mirum esse debet, quam aurum et argentum caeteraque Metalla e mari (uti dixi) originem trahentia nihilominus sua forma, dura compagine caeterisque appropriatis multimode ab hoc discrepare, ut de alijs, quae in Terra atque Aëre e Terrestri Aëreaque substantia prioribus dissimillima conformantur, nihil addam. Dum itaque Galaxiam ex Coeli substantia compactiva atque coagmentata constare volo, non aliud dico, quam eam cum reliquis stellis eandem habere materiam, quae tamen hîc sparsa atque in latum certo ductu per totum Coelum diffusa cernatur, in illis autem ad formam unius rotundi corporis arctius compacta sit. Atque hanc esse a vero non alienam, de via lactea, sententiam, ipse intuitus ocularis, attenta inspectione et collatione cum reliquis Stellis praesertim tenuiore lumine apparentibus facta, haud obscure testatur.</p><p>Imo Stellam illam Novam et supra modum admirandam, quae circa finem anni 1572, iuxta huius, de qua loquimur, Galaxiae confinia, ubi etiam Cassiopeae Astrum visitur, exorta est, eandem cum via lactea habuisse materiam, sed tamen magis in unum globum compactum sentio. Atque ut id certius statuam, hiatus semicircularis ad magnitudinem quasi Lunae dimidiae in margine viae lacteae eodem prorsus in loco, ubi Stella Nova situm habuit, adhuc Coelo existente apprime defaecato, et latente Luna, attente intuenti apparens, quam me nunquam antea ibidem vidisse memini, omnino persuadet. At dices: Si ex eadem et perpetua cum reliquis Sideribus Galaxiae substantia extitit haec Stella, cur non etiam perpetuo ijsdem coaeva permansit? Ad hanc dubitationem nolo in praesentiarum multa respondere, in fine Operis, quod de Ascititijs Coeli Phaenomenis molior, meam in his atque similibus aperturus sententiam. Id solummodo hoc loco dicam: Quae miraculose et praeter communem ordinem nonnunquam in rerum natura proveniunt, ijsdem cum Coelo diu consuetis subijci legibus, non necesse habent.</p><p>Sed quid Sapphirinus ille Coeli color te iterum movet, ut hunc ex nubibus et aquis lucem, quam Coelo astribuis, extra viam lacteam potissimum obscurantibus, provenire, sed frustra, existimes? Imo Sapphirinus iste color propter imbecillitatem visus nostri ingeritur oculis. Licet enim aër per se totaque Aetherea Regio omnis coloris expertia sint, tamen cum in ista profundissima immensitate visus non inveniat, ubi sistatur, color ille caeruleus oculis per accidens ingeritur, quemadmodum saepe videmus omnia, quae multum distant, eiusmodi oculis apparere, quamvis per se talem colorem non habeant. At haec a rei Opticae scriptoribus satis superque declarata sunt, ut non opus sit, e nubibus supracoelestibus hunc Sapphirinum colorem incassum mutuari, qua etiam de re in superioribus literis te admonui.</p><p>De Nova autem hac, cuius mentionem feci, Stella, in priore huius, de quo dixi, Operis tomo affatim tracto. Ibi, quae et in Landgravianis et tuis, tum etiam aliorum circa hanc pronuntiatis, desiderem, brevi cognosces. Tum etiam, quod eius decreta longe maiora universalioraque quam tibi visum est, existere, et necdum forte incoata esse nedum praeterijsse, non citra iustas rationes sentiam.</p><p>Nullatenus etiam tecum statuere possum ex materia aliqua Elementari productam fuisse hanc Stellam. Quod tamen non assertive pronuntias. Demonstro enim illic, eam mole sui corporis globum, quem Terrae et Maria efficiunt, plus quam ter centies exsuperasse. Quae ergo materia hinc progrediens tanto corpori suppeteret, si modo aliquid exhalationum Terrestrium in Coelum usque ascendere posse concedendum foret? In qua ego sententia neutiquam sum vel fui unquam, licet tu mihi id circa Cometarum procreationem immerito attribuis, dum ais me ex elevata in Coelum et coctionibus prius attenuata ac depurata in Orbe sublunari materia, accessuque novae aucta et compacta, Coelestique lumine perfusa, Cometarum corpora nasci opinari. Id equidem ego nunquam asserui; nec ex literis meis ad te vel alios datis, tale quid colligetur. Multo minus ex scriptis publice editis. Nam in libro secundo de recentioribus Coeli Phaenomenis (quem te accepisse puto) aperte habetur, me Cometarum materiam ex Elementari substantia quomodocunque subtiliata atque exaltata constare, nullatenus existimare. Quod vel illa quae pag. 254 apposui verba liquide testantur, quae sic habent: »Ego materiam omnium Cometarum prorsus Coelestem esse iudico, siquidem in ipso Coelo procreantur«. Observavi praeteritis aliquot annis exactissime quinque Cometas, quos omnes in ipso Coelo versatos fuisse, Parallaxeos exilitas evidenter convicit, adeo ut quidam eorum Sole multo altiores extiterint. Et sane eo usque Elementarem aliquam evolare materiam, quae in tale et tantum corpus concrescat, quis crediderit? Nam et ipsa moles maior extitit, quam quod Terra ipsi materiam suppeditare potuerit, cum quidam ex ipsis et hanc integram magnitudine vera superârint. De toto autem hoc negotio, cum alibi ex professo tractem, hîc pluribus supersedeo.</p><p>Quae de Metallorum generatione, quod ex una eademque constent prima materia, licet non in singulis aequaliter digesta, depurata atque exaltata, huc exempli loco, ut inferiorum rerum Naturam cum superiori aliquatenus conferrem, adduxi, si tuis, quas foves, rationibus minus probentur, meaque in his sententia tibi non satis explorata sit, equidem id mihi nullam parit admirationem, ex quo sciam, ea, quae in Academijs et a vulgaribus Philosophis de Metallorum natura et ortu traduntur, opinabilia saltem esse, nec in lumine naturae fundari. Qui enim resolutionem eorum non novit, compositionem vix percipiet. Arte ergo opus est Pyronomica, quae ex Hermetica procedit Philosophia. Ad quam omnes, qui in Academijs Philosophiae incubuerunt, plane imperiti accedunt. Credas me, qui non minus laboris et studij tum quoque … huius inferioris et Terrestris Astronomiae (sic enim hanc artem subinde vocare soleo) quam in superiorem et Coelestem illam collocavi, ipsa experientia edoctum esse, rem ita se habere, prout breviter ultimo indicavi. Quae cum tibi minus perspecta sint, utpote in tractatione Spagirica non versato, uberius ea attingere otiosum duco. Unicum tantum de hoc suggeram, quod ex hac metallorum cum Cometarum collata conformatione illicere potuisses, me Cometas ex Coelesti prorsus constare materia, omnis Elementaris qualitatis expertes, innuere voluisse.</p><p>Dispositionem operis mei Astronomici tibi placere laetor. Nec de Venere et Mercurio, quod Sole non minus altiores quam humiliores reddantur, magna erit apud rem ipsam penitius considerantes in persuadendo difficultas. Neque id quod adducis, Veneris stellam aliquando eodem die simul et vespertinam et eöam apparuisse, nostra ratiocinia ullatenus impedit, quin et idipsum a me semel observatum esse prioribus tibi indicavi literis. Nec quicquam hinc obstaculi, quo minus hypothesium nostrarum ordinatio rite constet, suggeri comperio. Sed de his in praesentiarum haec sufficiant, vel potius circa quaestionem illam, quae Coelum supremum Aquis obnubilat, tanquam minus Astronomicam, imo ab omni pene Philosophia abhorrentem, nimia et quoque supervacanea esse poterint.</p><p>CHRISTOPHORUM ROTHMANNUM Mathematicum Landgravianum hypotheses Copernicaeas Terraeque in his astructam mobilitatem serio tueri, totumque Coelum nihil aliud quam Aërem Elementarem adeoque Animalem esse, existimare, ex quo dubitas, mitto ad te particulam libri Epistolarum Astronomicarum, quas me aliquando publicandum tibi significavi, continentem potissimum praeter ipsius Illustrissimi Principis ad me de rebus Astronomicis perscriptas, unâ cum nostra ad singulas responsione, huius quoque ROTHMANNI et meas literas, quibus ultro citroque de toto hoc negotio disputamus. Ex his plenius percipies, quae sit illius atque mea, tum in talibus tum etiam nonnullis alijs huc pertinentibus sententia. Quam ut pro iudicij tui acumine expendere, atque quod tibi probabilius videatur, discernere velis, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Nam et in postremis literis, cum is ad arbitros appellet, ego te prae caeteris elegi, ut ex ijs ipsis percipies, sicubi otium fuerit eas perlegere, quod ut facias, tuamque eruditam de rebus illis controversis censuram mecum impertiaris, a te etiamnum amanter peto.</p><p>Plura de his non addam, ne nimium excrescat Epistola. Nec ad posteriores tuas, quas BONGERSIUS, uti ab initio dixi, reddidit, multis respondebo, cum per se breviores illae fuerint, et solummodo ea, quae his prolixioribus continebantur, denuo summatim inculcârint. Id saltem paucis indicabo, me nequaquam existimare Coelum Elementaribus mutationibus obnoxium, aut quippiam Elementare, quod huic inferiori Naturae conforme sit, illi inesse, velut sub finem eiusdem Epistolij mihi tribuere videris. A qua suspicione, si non alia, saltem haec tibi de Epistolis Astronomicis, quam mitto, particula me vindicabit. Scriptum nostrum Apologeticum, quod tibi inspiciendum dijudicandumque misi, te non accepisse suspicor, ex quo in neutris literis huius mentionem facias. Sed levis est iactura; brevi enim, volente Deo, idipsum, tomo secundo adiectum, typisque nostris excusum obtinebis. Tum demum … praestantissimi illius Mathematici et Philosophi Aristotelici IOANNIS CRAIGIJ Scoti obiectiones nostras assertiones de situ Cometarum Olympico oppugnantes, non tamen, uti spero, expugnantes, sufficienter … interponere censuram. Sed hîc scribendi cursum sisto et Deum rogo, ut te in hac senecta e tot tantisque aerumnis liberatum, clementer etiam diu superstitem atque incolumem conservare dignetur. Vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Caspar Peucer.</b></p><p>When, a little before our home <b>Assembly</b> which is held yearly about the summer solstice, I had received your letter, most excellent Lord <b>Master Peucer</b>, and I had necessarily to go thither, both by the anniversary custom and because I had certain [matters] to transact there, I deferred my reply until my return and a quieter state in this — as you call it — <b>Uranoscope</b> [star-watching place]. But it happened that, while I went over to <b>Copenhagen</b> at that time, I met Master <b>Bongars</b> [Bongersius], a Frenchman sent here by his King, who delivered also a second letter (though shorter than the prior one) inscribed to me by you, and at the same time promised that he would visit me on this Island before his return to his homeland, and would carry my reply to yours back with him on this occasion, and would deliver it to you, whom he had resolved to address again in Germany. But meanwhile, urged by I-know-not-what pressing affairs, he withdrew hence into Germany, soon to return, as was said. But while I awaited his return — though in vain — throughout this past summer, no more reliable convenience of conveying a letter to you presenting itself meanwhile, and [while] I am wrapped up in very many distractions, I have been forced to defer the reply (over-late, perhaps) until this very time. And although not even now is any handle of sending this to you ready to hand, nor am I yet relaxed from all cares, nevertheless, lest by procrastinating too long I should seem to you plainly deaf to yours, I have wished now at last to give this to you for the sake of a reply, asking most greatly that you take this long silence in fair and good part.</p><p>Now there are two [topics] chiefly about which, in this letter of yours, you discourse with me at length — namely, the <b>nature and substance of the Heaven</b>, and then the <b>clouds and waters</b> which you think exist <b>above the Heavens</b>. As to the first, in so far as it pertains to the most tenuous and most pure nature of the Heaven itself, and its immunity from Elemental defilements and corruptions, I in no way gainsay you. For I too have always thought thus, since accurate experience has taught and confirmed me that the Heaven is fashioned of no <b>solid and hard Orbs</b> — from the motion not only of the Planets but also of the <b>aethereal Comets</b>, of which I have observed <b>five</b> plying their celestial ways without the accompaniment of any real Orb. But that you think the <b>Aethereal Region per se luminous and shining</b> — whether this is altogether to be assented to, I am in doubt, since in the darkness of night, the air being clear, it reflects no light to us — which ought to happen, if it were actually luminous, especially with the dark waters and clouds (as you assert) surrounding it above, which would increase the repercussion of light toward the centre. For whatever of brightness and splendour the otherwise-opaque night acquires under a serene Heaven, it obtains from the <b>Moon</b> by the reflection of the Sun's light, or also from the <b>shining Stars</b>, not from the body of the Heaven itself. For this, apart from the Stars or Luminaries, throws no light into the eyes. But whether it has some [light] per se — not, however, so great as to strike the Earth (just as some think the Moon too is not wholly deprived of its own light, although it does not reach our sight, as appears in its deeper Eclipse) — I do not inquire more studiously, since it is imperceptible. Nor do I labour much about this question, whether the Heaven per se is strong in actual light, to be examined — since this, however it be determined, in no way obstructs our intention. This only I will add: that you hold this <b>celestial light to be nearest to the divine nature</b>, and therefore vindicate the Aethereal World from changes — [this] seems to be something excessive, and to subscribe very closely to <b>Aristotle's</b> opinion, who attributed divinity, and therefore also eternity, to the Heaven. I, certainly, if any created light could deservedly be compared with the divine, should judge that the one which God implanted in the <b>Angels</b> and in the Minds of men — being intellectual — comes far nearer. But that, by this reasoning, you do <b>not confuse the Aethereal and the Elemental (or chiefly Airy) Region</b> with each other — as certain others in this time have presumed, from the opinion of <b>Jean Pena</b> the Frenchman, as I think — nor judge that any waters, nor anything vaporous or exhalation-like, ascends into the aether itself: that is most fittingly determined by you, and squares perfectly with our opinion about these [matters]. Nor did I ever attribute to you that you thought otherwise, nor assert that it could be gathered from your writings. And so nowhere in my letters will you find this objected to you by me — that I should wonder by what inconsiderate interpretation of mine you impute it to me. Of which kind is also that which you presently subjoin, that I affirm the <b>nature of the Heaven to be fiery</b>: that in no way corresponds to my opinion, who have always thought the Heaven must be vindicated from every Elemental quality. But although, through various <b>Etymologies of the Hebrew appellation</b>, I composed that exposition — as if the Heaven were some astonishing thing conflated from fire and water, as from diverse natures — yet this does not amount to my really determining it to be either fiery or watery; but it pleased [me] only thus to <b>play</b> in variously composing Etymologies. For neither did I labour to prove anything seriously hence (since I myself expressly protested that this could only be said <b>metaphorically</b>, and not according to the qualities of an Elemental nature). Nay, God himself, devoid of all matter, in the sacred writings — as <b>Deut. 4:24</b> and <b>9:3</b>, likewise <b>Heb. 12:29</b> — although no one could reason anything about his true essence (which is inscrutable), is nonetheless called a <b>\"consuming fire.\"</b> What therefore prevents [us] from being permitted to name the Heaven and the Celestial Stars — which, in the manner of something flaming, shine and warm [things] from the bodies of the lower World — by the word \"fire,\" taken by a certain <b>similitude</b> perfectly accommodating itself to our grasp, [whether] to name [them by] the appellation or to explain [them] Etymologically? Yet I by no means infer therefrom that the Heaven is really <b>Elemental Fire</b>, as <b>Paracelsus</b> (with <b>Plato</b>, well-nigh) reported. Nay, I do not think that even under the concave sphere of the Moon there exists any <b>Elemental fire</b>, as <b>Aristotle</b> dreamed — <b>Optical reasonings</b> altogether gainsaying [it]. Nor do I judge it must be agreed that the upper Region of the Air is <b>most solid</b> (which is your opinion) — on account of the most rapid motion of the Celestial bodies, which is natural, not violent, and is performed in the most tenuous and most fluid essence of the Heaven, without any breaking or collision of solid bodies — and that thus it supplies the place of Elemental fire. Rather, whatever the <b>Physicists</b> are wont to seek from this Element for the composition and conservation of lower things, I judge to emanate from the Celestial [bodies], especially the <b>Sun</b> itself, imbuing all things with vivific heat.</p><p>But the other [topic] which you press is about the <b>Celestial waters</b>, which, from a certain literal sense of Scripture, you do not doubt to carry up <b>above the outermost walls of the Heaven</b>. I confess indeed that, from the words of <b>Moses</b> at the beginning of Genesis, this appears in some manner at first glance. But if anyone look more deeply into both the genuine understanding of Scripture and the nature of the thing — unless his judgment be altogether preoccupied — he will understand that it is far otherwise. First, it must be held that the word <b><i>[raqiaʿ, the firmament/expanse]</b></i> (which <b>Castalio</b> [Castellio] not ineptly rendered \"liquid,\" others \"expanse\") is not understood by Moses in the same sense everywhere in the describing of the creation-history; but sometimes generally and more broadly it denotes this whole liquid or expanse, which extends from the Earth up to the highest summits of the Heaven (as you yourself also indicate); sometimes [it is taken] only for a part of this, and that the upper [part], which contains in itself the luminous bodies of the Stars; but sometimes for this lower region of the liquid, which, closely surrounding the Earth and lying more open to our sight, extends to the outermost tops, more or less, of the clouds. In which sense indeed the word \"Heaven\" is frequently understood in Scripture (as also in this place), since it accommodates itself to the grasp of the ruder men, who call by the name of \"Heaven\" only that part of the expanse which lies nearest upon the convexity of the Earth. Wherefore, since <b>Moses</b> wrote this Creation-history for a rude and simple people only, not versed in Mathematical or Physical science, and undertook to expound only those things which strike the eyes of men, not those which are invisible or hidden — who does not see that by \"Celestial waters\" he understood the <b>watery clouds</b>, which hang above over our heads, and below themselves [have] that part of the expanse or liquid which separates them from the waters of the sea — not some immense mass of waters poured around the whole region of the Heaven?</p><p>Nay, it would also follow that either <b>three</b> waters were created by God — namely of the sea, of the clouds, and the supercelestial — of which, however, the middle (namely of the clouds) was neglected by Moses; or at least <b>two</b>, namely the marine and that supercelestial one, of which alone mention was made in the history of created things. But who would think that most faithful describer of the visible creatures, <b>Moses</b>, to have been so drowsy in relating the wonderful works of God that he left the waters of the clouds untouched in such deep silence — that he was unwilling even once to make mention of them among the rest of the sensible works of God — when nonetheless it holds no little admiration that they hang so firmly, without support, in the fluid air, without danger of falling, and besides furnish manifold utility in watering and making fertile the earth, which men esteem and regard more than those other higher [waters], more remote (as it seems to them) from daily use? These watery things, I say, Moses would have neglected, and meanwhile would have spoken superfluously about some supercelestial [waters] which neither lie open to the senses nor exhibit an evident use. In reality, therefore, it is necessary [to understand] that <b>Moses</b>, by those twin waters which he denotes, divided by the interval of the firmament, understood the <b>marine</b> [as] the lower and [the waters] of the <b>clouds</b>, in some part, [as] the upper. For thus too the <b><i>[raqiaʿ]</b></i> or expanse — which Castalio opportunely (as I said) calls \"liquid\" — separates waters from waters, intervening between them by a certain portion of itself; for Moses did not expressly say that the <b>whole</b> <i>[raqiaʿ]</i> divides waters from waters, as you take his sense. Nay, he himself explains [it] in <b>Gen. 9</b>, when he says that God set the <b>rainbow</b> in the clouds as a testimony that the waters would no more come into a flood of the lands. For what waters does he here signify, except the clouds, from which especially the inundation in the times of <b>Noah</b> had occurred — and hence it is clear that the \"cataracts of Heaven,\" or channels, which were then opened, are not to be sought beyond the orb of the Fixed [stars], as you wish. From <b>Jeremiah</b> moreover, chapters <b>10 and 51</b>, where the following words are had in both places: <i>\"He who made the Earth by his power, established the habitable world by his wisdom, and by his prudence stretched out the Heavens; at whose uttering of his voice there is a tumult of waters in the Heavens; who brings up vapours from the end of the Earth, makes lightnings with the rain itself, and produces wind from his treasuries,\"</i> the same is gathered still more manifestly. For hence it plainly appears that those <b>Celestial waters</b> are to be understood [as] in that Region of the air whence clouds, lightnings, and rain are produced, and that the same [region] is taken for \"Heaven.\"</p><p>Besides, the greatest <b>absurdities</b> follow from the supercelestial waters being granted. For if any waters of this kind exist there, which include and embrace the outermost theatres of the Heaven with their circuit all around, [then] they must of necessity either be <b>moved</b> or be <b>at rest</b> together [with the Heaven]. If they remain immobile, either they impede the most rapid rotation of the Heaven, or there must be a very great interval between the moving and the resting, so that that most rapid course may come to rest successively. An immense space, therefore, destitute of stars and fit for no use, must be left — which would be unsuitable. But if those waters are revolved together with the Heaven, how will they be able to endure that most violent rapidity, and to remain perpetually, together with it, inconsumable, or to obstruct it by no action? Add too [the matter] of the excessive magnitude of these waters with respect to the rest, which Moses calls <b>subcelestial</b>, by which it is doubtful to no one that the <b>Seas</b> flowing about the Terrestrial globe are to be understood. For if those waters which you wish to be above the <b>eighth sphere</b> occupy even one or a half <b>Earth-Diameter</b> in their density (which truly would be the very least, with respect to so great a circumference, and for that use which you assign — to ward off the <b>uncreated light</b> from the creatures, if that were to be granted you) — and since, to terminate this eighth sphere, there are at least <b>14,000 Earth-radii</b> [<i>Semidiametri Terrae</i>] according to our ordering of the Celestial hypotheses (whereas the <b>Ptolemaic</b> would require about <b>20,000</b> radii, and the <b>Copernican</b> speculation here goes off into the immense) — reckon, I pray, how vast those waters would be, which surround so great a globe on every side, even in that tiny thickness (as I said), [a globe] whose Radius will contain so many thousands of Earth-radii. Surely you will find that the mass of those waters scarcely has that estimation to the seas (or subcelestial waters) which the whole marine water has to a single drop. Accordingly, if those waters touched and covered the Earth (as is said to have been the case at the beginning, before the separation), they would by their quantity ascend high into the Heaven itself. What, then, would be the <b>comparison and proportion</b> of the waters above and below the expanse, of which Moses speaks? Wherefore it is by no means doubtful that <b>Moses</b>, by the name of \"supercelestial waters,\" understood no watery mass poured around on every side in the vastest capacity of the Aether (as you think), but — not by my opinion alone, but also by the opinion of the most excellent <b>Professors of Theology</b>, such as <b>Melanchthon, Castellio, Calvin</b>, and others — the <b>watery clouds</b> of the Heaven.</p><p>But you nonetheless labour much to assert that opinion of yours about the <b>supercelestial Waters</b>; and, so as to establish some <b>use</b> for those waters, lest perchance they be superfluous, you say they are disposed there as by a certain necessity, to provide a certain interval between the created orb and the light created in it, and the <b>\"Heaven of heavens,\"</b> which God inhabits in eternal light, uncreated and inaccessible to creatures. But what here do you understand by <b>\"eternal and uncreated light,\" what \"Heaven of heavens\"</b>? Is it thus that you deem it not unbecoming to prescribe finite limits to the infinite and immense, to confuse the eternal with the temporal, the invisible with the visible? For God alone exists [as] infinite and uncreated; therefore dimension, place, space, situation, and other things of the kind — which are attributes of creatures — cannot belong to him. And if that light which you call eternal and uncreated is something other than God himself, then there are now <b>two</b> eternals and uncreated, and therefore <b>two gods and two principles</b>. But nothing is uncreated and eternal except God himself, who is also unique. Nay, even if there were some eternal light outside the corporeal World, it would not be apprehended by human eyes, even with all intervals removed. For it is most truly asserted in <b>Optics</b> that all things which are seen are comprehended in time. Whence it follows that the things which are outside time and eternal do not lie open to temporal eyes. There is also ascribed to those Waters surrounding the Heaven (in your opinion) this utility too: that they <b>cut off and define the excursion of our vision</b>, lest perchance it go outside the World or into the infinite. But what is this to say, since no such power is at all in the visual faculty? Nay, if the stars were not luminous bodies and did not of their own accord present themselves to the eyes, the eyes would otherwise discern no thing at so great a distance. In vain, therefore, do you set before our vision that densest and darkest interval of waters above the Heavens, since by its nature and condition it is forbidden by the most august majesty of the uncreated.</p><p>Away, therefore, with that opinion about supercelestial waters — [introduced] for the sake of the distinction between created and uncreated things, and for restraining and defining the ray of our vision — an opinion fruitful in such erratic absurdities. Nor will you obtain any support for that opinion which you undertake to defend so greatly, in the common explanation of the name <b><i>[shamayim, \"heavens\"]</b></i> there, as if [from <i>esh</i> + ] <b><i>[mayim, \"waters\"]</b></i>. For this is arbitrary and contrary to the property of the <b>holy language</b>, which admits neither such a composition of words (especially in appellative nouns), nor easily any defect — such as would be in this word, if it were determined to be composite — without a compensation of letters. The word <b><i>[shamayim]</b></i>, therefore, is conflated by no common law of compositions, but exists [as] simple, and denotes something <b>astonishing or admirable</b>, derived from the verb <b><i>[shamem]</b></i>, which means \"to be astonished\" or \"to wonder\" — no otherwise than from <b><i>[…]</b></i> is formed <b><i>[…]</b></i>, and others of the kind. And therefore <b>Pagninus</b>, in his lexicon, not undeservedly subjoined this noun immediately to its radical verb. For in reality the Heaven is something admirable and astonishing, whether we regard the perennial laws of [its] motions, not obvious to everyone, or the matter of which it consists, which is inexplicable.</p><p>But you take exception, that these watery clouds — by which I understand Moses [to mean] — are not <b>perpetual</b>, because sometimes (or now and then) they are dissolved and flit and are borne hither and thither, and that therefore Moses, by \"Celestial waters,\" understood not those which are momentary, but those which always endure. It is indeed true that the clouds, which I hold for the Celestial waters, do not always appear in every Horizon, but for the most part; nevertheless, if not actually, at least <b>potentially</b> they are always there. And that force by which they coalesce on high, and are sustained lest they fall, is perpetually present there. Add that, regarding the whole Earth in its entire circuit, it will scarcely be granted that all Horizons are at once and at one [time] free of clouds hanging above — but that they are beheld in at least some Horizon of the Earthly globe.</p><p>Finally, you appeal further to the testimonies of Scripture, from which you implore help — but in vain — for establishing your opinion by a certain too-rigid literal explanation. For by no means are those [passages] which you allege here on your behalf — from <b>Psalm 104</b>: <i>\"Who frames his tabernacles in the clouds,\"</i> or from <b>Psalm 18</b>: <i>\"He set darkness as his hiding-place; around his tabernacle dark water and thick clouds\"</i> — to be understood of any supercelestial waters and clouds, but of the <b>Region of the air</b>, as it were distinguished into <b>upper-chambers</b> by clouds, as <b>Tremellius and Junius</b>, likewise <b>Möller</b> [Mollerus] and others, not unfittingly expound — unless perhaps anyone, to whom mention is made in the same place of winds and fires or lightnings, deems these too to have a place above the starry orb. But from verse 13 of the same Psalm, where it is said thus: <i>\"Who waters the mountains from his chambers [coenacula], that the earth may be satisfied with the fruit of thy works\"</i> — since the same Hebrew word <b><i>[ʿaliyyot, \"chambers\"]</b></i>, which <b>Pagninus</b> in his Hebrew dictionary thinks denotes \"chambers or clouds,\" is had in both places — who would deny that here the <b>waters of the clouds</b>, watering and making fertile the mountains and the earth, are to be understood?</p><p>Besides — since it introduces no little absurdity, nay, considering [it] naturally it is plainly impossible, that waters be held above the Heavens without supports and bars and be revolved — you labour to excuse this by the <b>stability of the Earth in the fluid air</b>. But [you do so] inconsistently, since the reckoning is not the same. For the Earth and all globular bodies of the World rest on their own <b>centre</b>, and all their parts naturally tend thither — so much so that if we should suppose some little portion violently torn from the Sun or Moon or the other stars, it soon <b>seeks again its own Star</b>, aiming at its own centre. For so great a force is divinely implanted in the <b>midpoints</b> of the World-bodies. Which surely cannot be admitted in like manner of the <b>extramundane waters</b>, since they do not totally fill the aforesaid round globe. And therefore, unless they were held by fixed supports and bars, they would necessarily <b>revisit</b> the marine waters, whence they had been separated, and so the <b>Earth</b>, the centre of their gravity. Nor is the argument valid: \"God could fix the gross and heavy Earth in the thin air; therefore [he could fix] the clouds too above the Heaven.\" For you know that from <b>\"can\" to \"is\"</b> (as they commonly speak) the inference does not follow. And therefore it is expressly said in the <b>Psalms</b>: <i>\"God has done all that he willed in Heaven and on Earth.\"</i> And God is the author of <b>order and utility</b>. Therefore he will by no means [be willing] to confuse and defile the Celestial with the Elemental without manifest use.</p><p>Furthermore, my opinion about the <b>Milky Way</b> [<i>via lactea</i>] you do not so much reject as illustrate more abundantly. For I — although [I hold] the Heaven [to be] something most fluid and most tenuous, everywhere pervious to the motions of the Stars — yet [hold it] not plainly incorporeal and immaterial, but [I have] determined that all the Celestial bodies consist of the <b>same substance as the Heaven</b>, compacted, by the wisdom of the Maker, into some denser globe fit for the reception of light. Just as we see in this nature of the World that those things which are generated in the everywhere-pervious <b>Sea</b> consist of a watery substance, and are easily resolved into the same, although these things, now concreted, are not [themselves] liquid and pervious. For all <b>Metals</b> too, and the <b>Gems</b> akin to them, have their <b>roots in the Sea</b> — which was known to very few before <b>Paracelsus</b>. But that the Stars of the Heaven, although they consist of Celestial matter, yet differ very much from the Heaven itself in <b>density and light</b>, ought to be no more a wonder than that gold and silver and the other Metals — drawing their origin from the sea (as I said) — nonetheless differ from it in manifold ways by their form, hard structure, and other appropriated [qualities]; to say nothing of the others which, in the Earth and Air, are formed from Terrestrial and Airy substance most unlike the former. When therefore I wish the <b>Galaxy</b> to consist of the Heaven's substance, compacted and joined together, I say nothing other than that it has the <b>same matter</b> as the rest of the stars — which, however, is here seen scattered and diffused in breadth, by a certain track, through the whole Heaven, but in those [stars] is more tightly compacted into the form of one round body. And that this opinion about the Milky Way is not far from the truth, ocular intuition itself — by attentive inspection and comparison with the rest of the Stars, especially those appearing with a fainter light — testifies not obscurely.</p><p>Nay, I am of the opinion that <b>that New Star, admirable beyond measure</b>, which arose <b>about the end of the year 1572</b>, near the confines of this Galaxy of which we speak (where also the Star of <b>Cassiopeia</b> is seen), had the <b>same matter as the Milky Way</b>, but yet more compacted into one globe. And so that I may establish this more surely: a <b>semicircular gap</b>, of about the magnitude of a half-Moon, in the margin of the Milky Way, in that very place where the New Star had its position — still apparent to one attentively gazing while the Heaven is perfectly clear and the Moon hidden, [a gap] which I do not recall ever having seen there before — altogether persuades [me of it]. But you will say: \"If this Star arose from the same and perpetual substance as the rest of the Stars of the Galaxy, why did it not also remain perpetually coeval with them?\" To this doubt I am unwilling to reply much for the present, being about to open my opinion in these and similar [matters] at the end of the Work which I am attempting <b>on the Ascititious</b> [adventitious] <b>Phenomena of the Heaven</b>. This only will I say in this place: the things which sometimes arise <b>miraculously and beyond the common order</b> in the nature of things need not be subject to the same laws long customary with the Heaven.</p><p>But why does that <b>sapphire colour of the Heaven</b> move you again, that you should think (but in vain) it arises from the clouds and waters obscuring — chiefly outside the Milky Way — the light which you attribute to the Heaven? Nay, that sapphire colour is thrown into the eyes on account of the <b>weakness of our sight</b>. For although the air per se, and the whole Aethereal Region, are devoid of all colour, yet, since in that most profound immensity the sight does not find where to be stayed, that azure colour is thrown into the eyes <b>per accidens</b> — just as we often see all things which are very distant appear to the eyes in this manner, although per se they do not have such a colour. But these things have been more than sufficiently declared by the writers on <b>Optics</b>, so that there is no need to borrow this sapphire colour, in vain, from supercelestial clouds — about which matter too I admonished you in [my] earlier letter.</p><p>But about this <b>New Star</b>, of which I made mention, I treat amply in the first Tome of the Work I spoke of. There you will shortly learn what I find wanting in the pronouncements both of the <b>Landgrave</b> and yours, and also of others, about this [star]. And then too [you will learn] that I think — not without just reasons — that its <b>decrees</b> [significations] are far greater and more universal than has seemed to you, and have perhaps not yet even begun, much less passed.</p><p>Nor can I in any way determine with you that this Star was <b>produced from some Elemental matter</b> — which, however, you do not pronounce assertively. For I demonstrate there that it surpassed, in the bulk of its body, the globe which the Earth and Seas make up, <b>more than three hundred times</b>. What matter, then, proceeding hence, would suffice for so great a body, if indeed it were to be granted that anything of Terrestrial exhalations could ascend up to the Heaven? In which opinion I am by no means, nor ever was — although you wrongly attribute it to me concerning the <b>procreation of Comets</b>, when you say that I think the bodies of Comets are born from matter elevated into the Heaven and previously attenuated and purified by <b>concoctions</b> in the sublunary Orb, increased and compacted by the accession of new [matter], and suffused with Celestial light. This indeed I never asserted; nor will any such thing be gathered from my letters given to you or to others — much less from my publicly published writings. For in the <b>second book on the more recent Phenomena of the Heaven</b> (which I think you have received) it is openly held that I by no means think the matter of Comets consists of Elemental substance subtilized and exalted in whatever way. Which even those words I appended on <b>page 254</b> plainly testify, which run thus: <i>\"I judge the matter of all Comets to be wholly Celestial, since they are procreated in the Heaven itself.\"</i> I have observed, in some years past, most exactly <b>five Comets</b>, all of which the slightness of [their] <b>Parallax</b> evidently proved to have dwelt in the Heaven itself, so much so that some of them stood much higher than the Sun. And truly, who would believe that any <b>Elemental matter</b> flies up so far, [matter] which grows together into such and so great a body? For the very bulk was greater than that the Earth could supply matter to it, since some of them surpassed even the whole [Earth] in true magnitude. But about this whole business, since I treat [it] professedly elsewhere, here I forbear [to say] more.</p><p>The things which I adduced here, by way of example — about the <b>generation of Metals</b>, that they consist of one and the same <b>prima materia</b>, although not equally digested, purified, and exalted in each — so that I might to some extent compare the Nature of lower things with the higher: if they are less approved by those reasonings of yours which you cherish, and my opinion in these is not sufficiently explored by you, this indeed begets in me no wonder, since I know that those things which are handed down in the <b>Academies</b> and by vulgar Philosophers about the nature and origin of Metals are merely <b>opinable</b>, and not founded in the <b>light of nature</b>. For he who does not know the <b>resolution</b> of them will scarcely perceive the <b>composition</b>. There is need, therefore, of the <b>Pyronomic art</b>, which proceeds from the <b>Hermetic Philosophy</b> — to which all who have lain over Philosophy in the Academies come quite unskilled. Believe me, who have bestowed no less labour and study … on this <b>lower and Terrestrial Astronomy</b> (for so I am wont from time to time to call this art) than on that higher and Celestial one, [and who] have been taught by experience itself that the matter is so, as I briefly indicated last. Which, since they are less perceived by you — being not versed in the <b>Spagyric</b> treatment — I deem it idle to touch upon more amply. Only this one [thing] will I suggest about it: that from this comparison of the conformation of metals with comets, you could have inferred that I meant to hint that <b>Comets consist of wholly Celestial matter, devoid of every Elemental quality</b>.</p><p>I rejoice that the <b>arrangement of my Astronomical work</b> pleases you. Nor, concerning <b>Venus and Mercury</b> — that they are rendered no less higher than lower than the Sun — will there be great difficulty in persuading those who consider the matter itself more deeply. Nor does that which you adduce — that the star of Venus has sometimes appeared on the same day at once both as evening and as <b>morning</b> [star] — at all impede our reasonings; nay, I indicated to you in [my] earlier letter that this very thing was once observed by me too. Nor do I find any obstacle suggested hence, [against] the ordering of our hypotheses being duly established. But let these [things] suffice about these matters for the present — or rather, about that question which <b>clouds the supreme Heaven with Waters</b>, [a question] as less Astronomical, nay, well-nigh abhorrent to all Philosophy, they may be even excessive and superfluous.</p><p>That <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's Mathematician, seriously defends the <b>Copernican hypotheses</b> and the <b>mobility of the Earth</b> constructed in them, and thinks the whole Heaven to be nothing other than <b>Elemental — and so Animate — Air</b> — about which you are in doubt: I send you a <b>portion of the book of Astronomical Letters</b> [<i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>], which I signified to you I would some time publish, containing chiefly — besides [the letters] of the most Illustrious Prince [the Landgrave] himself written to me about Astronomical matters, together with our reply to each — also the letters of this <b>Rothmann</b> and mine, in which we dispute back and forth about this whole business. From these you will more fully perceive what his opinion and mine is, both in such [matters] and in some others pertaining hither. Which [letters] I ask you again and again to weigh, according to the acumen of your judgment, and to discern what seems to you more probable. For in [his] last letters, since he appeals to <b>arbiters</b>, I have chosen <b>you before the rest</b> — as you will perceive from those very [letters], if ever there be leisure to read them through; which I still affectionately beg you to do, and to share with me your erudite appraisal of those controverted matters.</p><p>I will add no more about these, lest the Letter grow too much. Nor will I reply at length to your later [letters], which <b>Bongars</b> (as I said at the beginning) delivered, since they were shorter in themselves, and only repeated summarily, anew, those things which were contained in these more prolix [letters]. This only will I indicate in few [words]: that I by no means think the Heaven subject to Elemental changes, nor that anything Elemental, conformable to this lower Nature, is in it — as you seem to attribute to me toward the end of that same little Letter. From which suspicion, if nothing else, at least this portion of the <b>Astronomical Letters</b> which I send will vindicate me to you. I suspect that you have <b>not received our Apologetic writing</b>, which I sent you to be inspected and judged — since you make mention of it in neither letter. But the loss is light; for shortly, God willing, you will obtain it, appended to the <b>second Tome</b> and printed by our types. Then at last … I judge [it fitting] to insert sufficiently the appraisal … [concerning] the objections of that most excellent Mathematician and Aristotelian Philosopher, <b>John Craig the Scot</b>, assailing — but not, as I hope, taking by storm — our assertions about the <b>Olympic</b> [celestial] situation of Comets. But here I halt the course of my writing, and pray God that he may deign to preserve you — freed in this old age from so many and so great hardships — clemently, [and] also long surviving and unharmed. Farewell.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: <b>A</b> = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter (Dreyer's source-line reads <i>\"[Ex exemplo Hauniensi excerpsit Langebek. 178]\"</i>; the dataset gives 179 — a one-digit divergence to note). <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> the reply <b>is</b> in the local Dreyer scan — <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII <b>pp. 228–234</b> (PDF pp. 242–248) — and its Latin collates and matches. Only the <b>Hebrew glyphs</b> are uncollatable (OCR renders them as garbage in the scan's text layer); the supplies rest on the confirmed Latin context + the fixed biblical text + the surviving transcription <i>mayim</i> — a glyph-level check needs the page images (PDF pp. 246–248).<br>- <b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg). <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Wittenberg physician, astronomer, and theologian, <b>Melanchthon's son-in-law</b>, leader of the Philippist/Crypto-Calvinist party — imprisoned 1574–<b>1586</b> for his Eucharistic theology, then physician at the <b>Anhalt court (Dessau)</b>. Tycho's closing prayer (\"freed in this old age from so many and so great hardships\") alludes to that long captivity, fixing the letter after 1586 and (with the other internal markers) to <b>1590</b>. The exchange is a meeting of two of the age's most learned Lutherans on the frontier of astronomy and Genesis-exegesis.<br>- <b>The argument (the spine).</b> A point-by-point reply to two questions in Peucer's (lost) letters:<br>  1. <b>Nature/substance of the heavens.</b> Tycho agrees the heavens are tenuous, pure, free of elemental corruption, and have <b>no solid orbs</b> (proven by <i>five comets</i> moving without any real orb). But he doubts Peucer's claim that the aether is <b>per se luminous</b> (it reflects no light at night), rejects making celestial light \"nearest to the divine\" (too Aristotelian — divinity/eternity of the heavens; created <i>intellectual</i> light in angels/minds is nearer the divine), and clarifies that his Hebrew etymology of <i>shamayim</i> as \"fire + water\" was <b>metaphorical play</b>, not a claim that the heaven is fiery (God himself is called \"consuming fire,\" Deut 4:24, 9:3; Heb 12:29). He denies elemental fire below the Moon (contra Aristotle, by optics) and denies a \"most solid\" upper air (Peucer's view); the Sun's vivific heat, not an element of fire, conserves the lower world.<br>  2. <b>The \"waters above the firmament\"</b> (the long centre). Tycho refutes the supercelestial-waters reading of Gen 1:6–7 with: (a) <b>Hebrew philology</b> — <i>raqiaʿ</i> (Castellio \"<i>liquidum</i>,\" others \"<i>expansum</i>\") is used in three senses (whole expanse / upper star-region / lower cloud-region); Moses wrote for \"a rude and simple people,\" describing only the visible; (b) the <b>clouds argument</b> — otherwise Moses absurdly neglected the cloud-waters (which hang unsupported, irrigate the earth) while inventing invisible supercelestial ones; the rainbow (Gen 9) and the Flood \"cataracts\" prove the waters meant are clouds; <b>Jer 10/51</b> locates the \"tumult of waters in the heavens\" in the cloud-region; (c) the <b>physical absurdities</b> — supercelestial waters must move (and endure heaven's rapid rotation inconsumably) or rest (leaving a vast useless gap); and their <b>magnitude</b> would be monstrous, since the 8th sphere lies at ≥ <b>14,000 Earth-radii</b> in Tycho's system (Ptolemaic ~20,000; Copernican \"into the immense\") — the supposed shell would dwarf the seas \"as all seawater to one drop\"; (d) the <b>theological objection</b> to Peucer's purpose for the waters (an interval before the uncreated \"heaven of heavens\") — to bound the infinite, or posit a created-yet-uncreated light, yields \"two gods\"; optics shows eternal things are invisible to temporal eyes; the visual ray needs no watery wall; (e) the <b>etymology</b> — <i>shamayim</i> is <b>not</b> a compound of <i>esh</i> (fire) + <i>mayim</i> (water) but a simple noun from <i>shamem</i> (\"to be astonished\"), per <b>Pagninus</b>; (f) the <b>Psalm passages</b> (104:3; 18:11; 104:13, with <i>ʿaliyyot</i> = \"chambers/clouds\") read by <b>Tremellius–Junius</b> and <b>Möller</b> as the air-region; (g) the <b>disanalogy</b> with Earth's stability (world-bodies rest on their own centre; extramundane waters, not filling the globe, would fall back) and the logical point <i>a posse ad esse non valet</i>. Authorities marshalled for the cloud-reading: <b>Melanchthon, Castellio, Calvin, Tremellius, Junius, Möller, Pagninus</b>.<br>  3. <b>Milky Way &amp; New Star.</b> The heavens are fluid yet not immaterial; stars are the <i>same substance</i> as the heaven, condensed into denser light-receiving globes (analogy: metals/gems \"have their roots in the sea,\" <i>pace</i> Paracelsus). The <b>Galaxy</b> is that same matter diffused in a band; the <b>New Star of 1572</b> (by Cassiopeia, at the Galaxy's edge) was the same matter compacted — Tycho adduces a <b>semicircular gap</b> he saw in the Milky Way's margin exactly at the nova's site. Why did it not endure? — miraculous, beyond-the-common-order things need not obey the heavens' usual laws (full answer reserved for the <i>Progymnasmata</i>).<br>  4. <b>Sapphire sky-colour</b> = a <i>per accidens</i> effect of the eye's weakness in the immense void (optics), not borrowed from supercelestial clouds.<br>  5. <b>Comets wholly celestial.</b> Tycho denies (against Peucer's attribution) that he ever held comets made of sublunary exhalations; quotes his own <i>De recentioribus phaenomenis</i> <b>p. 254</b>: \"I judge the matter of all comets to be wholly celestial, since they are procreated in the heaven itself.\" Five comets, by tiny parallax, were celestial (some higher than the Sun); the 1572 nova exceeded the Earth-globe <b>&gt;300×</b> — no exhalation could supply such bulk.<br>  6. <b>Alchemy (\"terrestrial astronomy\").</b> Metals share one <i>prima materia</i>, variously \"digested, purified, exalted\"; academic philosophy of metals is mere opinion, not the <i>lumen naturae</i>; the <b>Pyronomic art</b> from <b>Hermetic philosophy</b> (the <b>spagyric</b>/chymical practice) is needed — Tycho claims as much labour in this \"lower and terrestrial astronomy\" as in the celestial.<br>  7. <b>Venus &amp; Mercury</b> circle the Sun (rendered both above and below it); Venus seen as morning- and evening-star the same day is no obstacle (Tycho saw it once). <b>Rothmann</b>: Tycho sends Peucer a <i>portion of the Epistolae astronomicae</i> (the Landgrave + Rothmann correspondence) and names Peucer the <b>arbiter</b> of the dispute over Rothmann's Copernicanism and his \"elemental/animate air\" heaven. The <b>Apologeticum</b> against Craig (cf. the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591) Peucer has not received; it will appear in Tome II.<br>- <b>Hebrew lemmata (transcription partly lost/garbled; editorially supplied — protocol §4 square brackets).</b> The working transcription lost or garbled most of the Hebrew, rendering it as \"XXX\". <b>Collation refinement (2026-06-02):</b> of the key lemmata, <b>מים</b> <i>mayim</i> (\"waters\") actually <b>survives</b> in the transcription (as <b>םײמ</b>) — it is a confirmed reading, not a supply; <b>שמים</b> <i>shamayim</i> is lost (rendered \"XXX\" twice) and <b>רקיע</b> <i>raqiaʿ</i> survives only as the scrambled fragment <i>עיקד</i>. Supplied/confirmed from the fixed text: <b>רקיע</b> <i>raqiaʿ</i> (\"firmament/expanse,\" Gen 1:6 — Castellio \"<i>liquidum</i>,\" AV \"firmament\") [supplied]; <b>שמים</b> <i>shamayim</i> (\"heavens\") [supplied]; <b>מים</b> <i>mayim</i> (\"waters\") [<b>survives in source</b>]; <b>שמם</b> <i>shamem</i> (\"to be astonished,\" the root Tycho/Pagninus derive <i>shamayim</i> from — rejecting the <i>esh</i>+<i>mayim</i> folk-etymology); <b>עליות</b> <i>ʿaliyyot</i> (\"upper chambers,\" Ps 104:3,13, glossed \"<i>coenacula seu nubes</i>\" by Pagninus). The single analogical word-pair Tycho cites (\"<i>non aliter quam a […] formatur […] et alia id genus</i>\") is genuinely <b>illegible</b> in the transcription and left marked <i>[…]</i>. Because Tycho's reply was not located in the local Dreyer scan (see the §1 note above), these supplies could not be checked against Dreyer's own printed Hebrew type — they rest on the fixed biblical text and the surviving <i>mayim</i>.<br>- <b>Scripture cited.</b> Gen 1:6–7 (<i>raqiaʿ</i>/waters); Gen 9:13–15 (rainbow); Deut 4:24, 9:3 + Heb 12:29 (\"consuming fire\"); Jer 10:12–13 = 51:15–16 (tumult of waters); Ps 104:3, 104:13, 18:11(12) (clouds/chambers); Ps 115:3 / 135:6 (\"God did all he willed\"). The Latin renderings follow the Reformed Latin versions (Tremellius–Junius / Castellio), not the Vulgate.<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Jacques Bongars</b> (<i>Bongersius</i>, 1554–1612, the French royal diplomat-scholar, the would-be courier); <b>Jean Pena</b> (the French mathematician of the continuous air-aether — cf. Hájek's letter of 11 Jun 1590); <b>Plato, Aristotle, Paracelsus, Hermes</b> (Hermetic philosophy); <b>Sebastian Castellio</b> (<i>Castalio/Castallio</i>, the Bible translator), <b>Melanchthon, Calvin</b> (theologians); <b>Immanuel Tremellius &amp; Franciscus Junius</b> (the Reformed Latin Bible); <b>Heinrich Möller</b> (<i>Mollerus</i>, Wittenberg Hebraist, <i>Enarratio Psalmorum</i>); <b>Sante Pagnini</b> (<i>Pagninus</i>, the <i>Thesaurus linguae sanctae</i>); <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> &amp; <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel</b>; <b>John Craig</b> (cf. the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591). Places: <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), <b>Uraniborg</b> (the \"Uranoscope\"), the home <b>Assembly</b> (the Danish <i>Herredag</i>/midsummer <i>Comitia</i>), <b>Germany</b>.<br>- <b>Latin/technical tags.</b> <i>Uranoscopium</i> — Peucer's coinage for Tycho's observatory (\"star-watching place\"). <i>a posse ad esse</i> (here <i>ad inesse</i>) <i>non valet consequentia</i> — the scholastic rule that possibility does not entail actuality. <i>lumen naturae</i> — the \"light of nature.\" <i>Ars Pyronomica</i> / <i>Spagyrica</i> / <i>Hermetica Philosophia</i> — alchemy/chymistry. <i>situs Olympicus</i> (of comets) — their \"Olympic\" (heavenly) location. <i>decreta</i> (of the nova) — its astrological significations. <i>Semidiameter Terrae</i> = Earth-radius. <i>ter centies</i> = 300×. <i>per accidens</i> (the sky-colour). <i>intuitus ocularis</i> — ocular inspection.<br>- <b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> (1) ~90 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> (footnote-anchors to witness A) and 2 stray \"<i>\" footnote-markers removed as non-textual (the same as in the Scaliger letter of 23 Aug 1598). (2) Hebrew supplied in [brackets] / </i>[…]<i> as above. (3) Two transcription <b>lacunae</b> marked </i>…<i> (in the alchemy sentence \"</i>tum quoque … huius inferioris<i>\" and the closing Craig sentence \"</i>Tum demum … sufficienter …<i>\"). The variant spellings </i>Haeb.<i> (Heb.), </i>Ierhemia<i> (Jeremiah), </i>eöam<i> (ἑῴαν, \"of dawn\"), </i>astribuis* (attribuis) are left as printed.<br>- <b>Dating (protocol §3).</b> <b>No dateline</b> survives (the letter ends \"Vale\"); the catalogue assigns <b>1590</b> (label \"1590. empty\"). Internal markers consistent with 1590: the <i>Apologeticum</i> (Craig) not yet sent to Tome II; the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (pub. 1596) and <i>Progymnasmata</i> Tome I in preparation; Bongars's mission; Peucer post-1586 release. Header leads with the catalogued year, flagged undated; no date is asserted that the text does not bear.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A very long, doctrinally and exegetically continuous letter — heavens'-nature, the supercelestial-waters refutation (philological + physical + theological), the Milky-Way/nova condensation theory, the wholly-celestial-comet doctrine, the alchemy excursus, Venus/Mercury, and the Rothmann arbitration — rendered securely throughout; the register (learned, courteous, firmly corrective, with scriptural gravity) is preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, <i>salvari</i>-adjacent <i>salvare</i> not arising here, eccentric/hypotheses, <i>restitutio</i>, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with Hájek's letter of 11 Jun 1590, the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590, and the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 (esp. the comet doctrine shared with the Craig letter, and the Rothmann thread that recurs across the corpus). Three things keep it from [A]: (1) the <b>§1 Dreyer collation is now done</b> (vol. VII pp. 228–234) and the Latin <b>matches</b>, but <b>Dreyer's printed Hebrew glyphs remain uncollated</b> — image-only in the scan (OCR-garbled), so the supplied forms rest on the confirmed Latin context + the fixed biblical text + the surviving <i>mayim</i> (a glyph-check awaits an image read of PDF pp. 246–248); (2) the <b>Hebrew lemmata were partly lost/garbled</b> in the transcription and are editorially supplied (declared; <i>mayim</i> survives, <i>shamayim</i>/<i>raqiaʿ</i> lost; one analogical word-pair genuinely illegible, marked <i>[…]</i>); (3) <b>two short transcription lacunae</b> (alchemy / Craig sentences) and the heavy <b>OCR apparatus-sigla</b> removal, all declared. None obscures the argument's through-line.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 12 Dreyer page images bearing this letter (TBOO VII pp.228-239, slice pages 1-4, 5-8, 9-12) visually, in 4-page chunks. Collated opening and closing lines, every proper name (Peucer, Bongars/Bongersius, Jean Pena, Aristotle, Plato, Paracelsus, Melanchthon, Castellio/Castalio, Calvin, Tremellius, Junius, Mollerus, Pagninus, Moses, Jeremiah, Rothmann, John Craig), every number/date (1572 nova; five comets; five aethereal comets 'without a real orb'; 14,000 vs Ptolemaic ~20,000 Earth-semidiameters; 'more than three hundred times' [ter centies] the Earth-and-seas globe; page 254 self-citation; the exact italicized self-quotation 'materiam omnium Cometarum ... procreantur'), and every Scripture citation (Deut.4:24, 9:3, Heb.12:29, Gen.9, Ps.104 incl. v.13, Ps.18, Jer.10 and 51) against Dreyer's print. All match the English exactly; no fabricated, dropped, or altered clauses found. Dreyer's own interleaved editorial footnotes (the Vondel/aquis-in-Obfervat. note on p.229; the Hebraismus note on p.231) are correctly excluded from both source_text and translation as apparatus, not Tycho's text. The letter's endpoint ('Vale.' + '[E codice Vindobonensi lat. 9737f]') on p.239 matches source_text exactly; p.240 begins an unrelated letter to Tycho and was confirmed unnecessary to read.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliothek, Langebeks excerpter, 179. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Caspar Peucer", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15890510002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890510002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the delay in replying: Bongars, a French envoy who delivered Peucer's second letter in Copenhagen, departed for Germany before Tycho could send a reply.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900000002", "d": "1590-00-00", "dir": "third", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": "Henrik Rantzau", "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Heinrich Rantzau — [no place], [no dateline] (catalogued 1590 (?))", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Liber I) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>QUAeDAM EX LITERIS CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI, IVSSV SVI PRINCIPIS, AD DN. HENRICVM RANZOVIVM.</p><p>ABSOLVTIS pene literis, incidit hoc, quod adhuc subijciendum venit: Superiori anno Mense Septembri misimus ad Nobilissimum Dominum TYCHONEM BRAHE literas, Illustrissimus quidem Princeps noster vnas, ego vero binas, plenas variarum de rebus Astronomicis disputationum, ad quas Mathematicus ille Mathematicorum omnium longe praestantissimus nondum respondit. Cum itaque mihi mandarit Illustrissimus Princeps noster, vt eum de responsione admonerem, peto a tua Excell vt oblata occasione, clemens hoc Illustrissimi Principis nostri desiderium ipsi velis significare, ne responsionem diutius suspendat: qua quidem re T. Excell. officium longe gratissimum praestabit Illustrissimo Principi nostro. Coram, Illustrissimus Princeps, et ego in eius comitatu, inuisuri eramus Dn. TYCHONEM, eiusque Instrumenta Astronomica coram visuri, nisi immatura Serenissimi et optimi Regis mors, iter nostrum auertisset, proque tanto et tam laeto visendi desiderio, luctum et moestitiam Illustrissimo Principi nostro substituisset. Sed sunt omnia in manu praepotentis DEI.</p><p>Iterum vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Certain matters from a letter of Christoph Rothmann, by command of his Prince, to Master Heinrich Rantzau.</b></p><p>The letter being all but finished, this came up, which must yet be added below: Last year, in the month of September, we sent letters to the most noble Master Tycho Brahe — our Most Illustrious Prince one [letter], and I two — full of various disputations on astronomical matters, to which that mathematician, by far the most outstanding of all mathematicians, has not yet replied. Since therefore our Most Illustrious Prince has charged me to remind him about a reply, I ask of Your Excellency that, when an occasion offers, you would be willing to make known to him this gracious wish of our Most Illustrious Prince, that he should not put off his reply any longer: by which thing indeed Your Excellency will render a most welcome service to our Most Illustrious Prince. Face to face, our Most Illustrious Prince — and I in his retinue — were [intending] to visit Master Tycho, and to view his astronomical instruments in person, had not the untimely death of the Most Serene and most excellent King turned aside our journey, and, in place of so great and so joyful a longing to visit [him], substituted grief and sorrow for our Most Illustrious Prince. But all things are in the hand of God most powerful.</p><p>Again, farewell.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1555 – c. 1600), mathematician and astronomer to Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel, and Tycho's principal scientific correspondent in the late 1580s. He writes here <i>iussu sui Principis</i> (\"by command of his Prince\"), i.e. at the Landgrave's direction.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Dn. Henricum Ranzovium</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henrik Rantzau, 1526–1598), Holstein statesman, humanist, and bibliophile, royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the Danish kings in the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein; a central node in the learned correspondence networks of the north, here asked to act as intermediary in prompting Tycho.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimus Princeps noster</b></i> — \"our Most Illustrious Prince\": <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Rothmann's employer and himself an active observational astronomer.</li><li><b><i>Mathematicus ille … longe praestantissimus</b></i> — \"that mathematician, by far the most outstanding of all\": <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, named just above; the encomium is kept as written.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimi et optimi Regis mors</b></i> — \"the death of the Most Serene and most excellent King\": <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), who died 4 April 1588. His death is the event that diverted the Landgrave's intended visit to Tycho's observatory; <i>immatura</i> (\"untimely / premature\") is preserved as Rothmann's own characterisation.</li><li><b><i>Instrumenta Astronomica</b></i> → <b>astronomical instruments</b> (the apparatus at Uraniborg/Stjerneborg); rendered per glossary §5.2.</li><li><b>Genre / structure.</b> The heading <i>QUAEDAM EX LITERIS … = \"Certain matters from a letter of …\"</i> marks this as a <b>printed excerpt</b> — a postscript section (<i>absolutis pene literis</i> \"the letter being all but finished\") lifted from a longer Rothmann letter to Rantzau and inserted by Tycho into his <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (Uraniborg, 1596). It is not a self-standing letter; the surrounding letter-body is not reproduced.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>peto a tua Excell. … T. Excell.</i> = \"I ask of Your Excellency … Your Excellency\" (§5.5); <i>Iterum vale</i> = \"Again, farewell\" (§5.5). The honorific <i>Excell.</i> is the abbreviation for <i>Excellentia Tua</i> throughout, addressed to Rantzau.</li><li><b>Dating crux (named).</b> <b>The source carries no dateline and no place.</b> The catalogue label is <i>\"1590 (?)\"</i> (itself queried). The header therefore asserts no date the text does not substantiate. The only internal time-anchor is relative: <i>Superiori anno Mense Septembri misimus … literas</i> (\"last year, in the month of September, we sent letters\"). Rothmann's and the Landgrave's letters to Tycho of September are those of <b>1589</b>; combined with Frederik II's death (April 1588) already lying in the past, the excerpt's <i>composition</i> falls plausibly in <b>1590</b> — consistent with, but not proven by, the catalogue's queried \"1590 (?)\". The \"September of last year\" is <b>not</b> silently converted to a fixed civil date; it is left as the relative reference the text gives. No calendar style (O.S./N.S.) is stated in the source.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> Two negations in the operative clauses, both preserved: <i>nondum respondit</i> (\"has <b>not yet</b> replied\") and <i>ne responsionem diutius suspendat</i> (\"that he should <b>not</b> put off his reply any longer\"). The conditional <i>nisi … mors … auertisset … substituisset</i> (\"had <b>not</b> the death … turned aside … [and] substituted\") is a single contrary-to-fact condition (one <i>nisi</i> governing two pluperfect-subjunctive apodosis verbs) and is rendered as such; no additional negation introduced.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the courtesy register matched; the only open point is the date, which the source does not state. The header leads with \"[no dateline]\" and appends the queried catalogue date \"(1590 (?))\"; the relative anchor (\"September of last year\" = Sept. 1589) and the post-1588 setting are laid out in the apparatus rather than resolved into an asserted dateline.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice available), so per the brief this is audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of quality. Latin source_text and published_english checked clause-by-clause: the September-letters-unanswered passage, the reference to 'ille Mathematicus' (Tycho), the intended visit to Hven aborted by the king's death, and the closing 'Iterum vale' all render faithfully with no invented facts, no dropped clauses, and no flipped subjects/negations. Would likely earn A if Dreyer's pages were available, but grade is capped at B per the no-tboo rule.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks Rantzau to remind Tycho that letters sent the previous September — one from the Landgrave and two from Rothmann on astronomical matters — still await a reply.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900125001", "d": "1590-01-25", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 25 January 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer,</i> Opera Omnia *VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.27</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO D. THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI ATQUE ADMINISTRATORI EXIMIO, SUO IN PRIMIS COLENDO AMICO.<br>S.</p><p>ACCEPI iam nunc tuas literas, Pragae ultima Novembris datas, quas mihi Hamburgo misit clariss. D. D. VITUS WINSHEMIUS. Intelligo autem ex ijs, te admodum solicitum esse, an priores tuae mihi redditae sint, vererisque, ut eas receperim; ideoque scriptione tamdiu supersedeam praesertim cum nihil per WINSHEMIUM ad vos proficiscentem scripserim, ex quibus omnibus satis perspicio benevolum tuum erga me affectum et amoris sinceri non obscura indicia. At ignorabam, mi amantissime THADDAeE, D. WINSHEMIUM ad Imperatoriam Maiestatem hinc Legati munus sustinuisse, alias non intermisissem, quin tibi scriberem. Rarius nunc Aulam nostram accedo, quam antea, vivente optimae et laudatiss. memoriae Rege nostro FREDERICO, qui me saepius accersiri curabat, ut nolens volens tunc crebro Aulae adesse cogerer; at nunc lubentius et quietius otio fruor philosophico meisque studijs vaco, nec quid rerum in Aula geratur, magnopere quaero. Facile itaque evenire potuit, ut WINSHEMIUS me inscio vos accesserit. Scripseram tibi nihilominus iam antea circa Novembris initia, et quaternis tuis unicis luculenter respondi, unâque Apologiam, quam ijsdem pene diebus ad insignem quendam in Scotia Mathematicum, IOANNEM CRAIGUM nomine, pro ijs, quae Tomo secundo nostro, ab illo in dubium vocata, continentur, adornâram, tibi inspiciendam misi. Et ne literae facile interciderent, praesertim ob hyemis difficultates, binas conscribi, unasque Hamburgo, alteras Lubecâ, opera Nobiliss. viri et rerum Astronomicarum amantiss. Domini HENRICI RANZOVIJ, Regis nostri in Holsatia Locumtenentis, amici mei singularis, ad te transmitti curavi. Addidi quoque ad Nobilissimum et doctiss. virum D. IACOBUM CURTIUM, Imperij Procancellarium, te admonente, quasdam, etiam bis eâdem de caussa transcriptas. Spero autem, vos alteras saltem harum, si non utrasque, iam nunc accepisse, ideoque te ea, qua detinebaris, suspicione exemtum. Ego de tua fide atque integritate, tum etiam erga me constanti amicitia nihil prorsus addubito, mi THADDAeE, ut ea, quam adfers, excusatione opus non fuisset. Ego potius quam tu in culpa sum, qui tamdiu tibi respondere procrastinârim. Optabam quidem tota praeterita aestate, mihi aliquem offerri certum internuntium, per quem tibi rescriberem, sed cum id ex voto non contingeret, praeter voluntatem responsionem usque in hyemis initia differre coactus sum, quemadmodum ultimis me hoc nomine apud te purgabam, ex quibus etiam satis percepisti, me omnes tuas sartas tectas unâ cum WERNERI libro accepisse, ut hac te solicitudine iam satis liberatum existimem. Ijsdem etiam indicavi, cur primus Tomus nostri Operis, quod prae manibus habeo, nondum prodierit. Chartopaeus, quem Lubecam in aestate miseram, moletrinae meae (de qua etiam scripsi, me pro papyro conficienda quandam extrui curasse) necdum reversus est. Sed pro alio conquirendo hincinde in Germaniam scripsi. Et nisi hyemis asperitas portus congelasset, iam aliquem eiuscemodi artificem obtinuissem, quem et me brevi habiturum spero, ut papyrus impressoria in maiori copia paretur. Interim tamen eam, quae in promptu est, non feriari sino. Est enim nunc sub praelo primus ille Tomus, quem desideras, et duo Alphabeta iam impressa sunt, restat sesquialterum. Curo etiam Epistolarum Astronomicarum volumen alio praelo excudi, quod non minus iustae erit magnitudinis. Sicque duo praela indies urgeri iubeo, quae non parum papyri absumunt, ut nisi mature chartopaeum acceperim, aut aliunde papyrum sufficientem acquisiverim , quod difficulter hyeme fit, vereor, ne Opus ipsum remoram aliquam sentiat, quod tamen, quantum in me est, ne fiat, praecavebo. Ad Nundinas Francofurtianas vix primum librum mittere dabitur, quod desiderem adhuc Privilegium nuperi Galliarum Regis, mihi ab eius Legato, qui brevi nobiscum fuit, ad Pascatis festum promissum. Sic etiam Anglicanum per ROGERSIUM procurandum adhuc expecto. Interim et tertium Tomum adornabo, ac conabor, ut et is, quam citissime fieri possit, expediatur. Si quid Deus votis nostris faverit, efficiam, ut instante aestate aliquando horum reddaris particeps, unâ cum Epistolarum libro, qui plurima rem Astronomicam illustranda et negotium etiam hoc Cometicum, de quo nunc agimus, disquirentia continebit. Adduntur et tuae, quotquot de Astronomicis mecum conferentes hactenus obtinui. Rescriptiones meas alicubi desidero, quod exemplum non ad omnes in promptu sit, si tu reservasti, cura describi mihique mature mitti. Id etiam et ultimis, si recte memini, petij. Quod speculam pro observationibus Astronomicis in novis tuis aedibus extruere decreveris, in primis laudo. Deus concedat tibi prosperam et diuturnam senectam, ut his et alijs rebus eximijs diu vacare queas. Si aliae curae, tum etiam senij gravedo te nonnunquam averterint, ut tibi ipsi coelestibus invigilare minus commodum fuerit, facile unum vel alterum iuvenem harum rerum cupidum sustentabis, qui observationes per opportunam serenitatem expediat, modo Instrumenta idonea non defuerint. Da operam, ut Sextantem quendam ad instar eius, cuius ideam iam dudum (ni fallor) misi, habeas, eo enim distantias exactas et altitudines etiam, si sic lubet, satis scrupulose rimari datur. Radius Astronomicus eam, quam pollicetur, praecisionem non praestat, quomodocunque subdivisus et compositus. Vide etiam, ut Quadrantem aliquem Azimuthalem solide elaboratum unâ obtineas, qui ad minimum singula minuta suppeditet. Quod si Armillas quasdam Aequatorias ad instar mearum, praesertim earum, quae sesquialteram solummodo Armillam requirunt, unâ confieri curaveris, his tribus organis, quicquid in observationibus Astronomicis desideras, facile et tuto absolves, et si quod supervenerit, ut proximis annis, ascititium sidus, illud certius et accuratius quam antea demetiri dabitur. Ubi hi duo Tomi, de quibus dixi, publicati fuerint, plurium Machinarum Astronomicarum in ijs designationem invenies, ut eligere possis, quae tibi commodior visa fuerit. Divisionem Instrumentorum per puncta transversalia fac ut subtiliter perficias, et Dioptra rimulas parandas priori pinnacidio habentia adhibeas, prout in fine libri secundi haec aliquo modo exprimuntur. Plenariam de omnibus his conceptionem obtinebis, quando Opus nostrum de Mechanica Astronomiae parte absolutum fuerit, in quo 24 ad minimum varia ratione adinventa constructaque Instrumenta Astronomica, quoad eorum fabricam et usum, ubertim exponere constitui et plaeraque alia observationibus inservientia adiungere. Apologiam, quam misi, quaeso diligenter perlustres, et si quid inveneris, ubi satisfactum non esse putas, admone. Ego illam inter plurimas alias occupationes meo Amanuensi subito, prout in buccam venerunt, dictavi, adeo ut vix otium superesset relegendi. Si quid deesse intellexero, per occasionem supplebo. Et si tibi ita consultum videtur, Apologiam hanc secundo Tomo de eodem Cometa agenti subiungam, quo locupletior redditus priorem adaequet. Habeo siquidem adhuc ferme omnia exemplaria non distracta. Nolui enim vulgaria fieri, antequam omnes Tomi typis excusi essent. De rebus Gallicis, quae addis, perceptu grata sunt, quin et nos similia his percipimus, Regem videlicet novum felici uti successu, det Deus, ut indies magis ac magis prosperetur. Genevenses obsidione liberatos gaudeo. Deus pacem et tranquillitatem tam Ecclesiae quam Rebuspublicis largiri dignetur.</p><p>Scotorum Rex his ipsis diebus ad vicinam Helsingoram Arcemque Croneburgum advenit unâ cum sua nova nupta Regina, Regis nostri feliciss. memoriae filia, magnoque e Regni Primoribus comitatu. Cum enim illa circa finem aestatis in Scotiam appellere nequiret, classe nostra, qua vehebatur, semel atque iterum adversa tempestate in Norvegiam repulsa tandemque ob ingruentem hyemem navigationem intermittente, sereniss. ille Rex conscensis suis navibus sponsam (quid non cogit Amor?) inter rupes Norvegicas quaerens adinvenit, petensque a sereniss. Regina Regnique Gubernatoribus Consiliarijs nos accedendi copiam fieri, ex quo in nostras ditiones advenisset, rogatus, hacque lubentiss. impetrata, multo et difficili, hyberno praesertim tempore, itinere huc accessit atque hîc cum suis hybernaturus, donec libera concedatur navigatio, nunc Croneburgi honorifice excipitur. Quod tibi indicandum duxi, ut et quid novarum rerum in publicis apud nos agatur, cognosceres. Vale, et si ante Pasca Privilegium denuo impetratum, de quo ultimo scripsi, tum etiam MUNOSIJ libellum et quos praeterea habes, de re Cometica agentes, miseris, rem pergratam praestiteris.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale, mi amantissime exoptatissimeque THADDAeE.<br>Uraniburgi 25. Ian. Anno 90.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 228; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POST SCRIPTA. CUM eo ipso die, quo has literas meo Amanuensi ad mundum describendas tradidissem, circa vesperae Veneris a Sole digressioni maximae observandae intenderem, ecce Cometes quidam ad eandem occiduam plagam ex improviso apparuit, ideoque hasce literas interea mecum reservandas duxi, donec omnes istius ascititij sideris apparentias coelitus per totam eius durationem accurate deductas scrutatus essem, ut mea etiam inventa, ex quo sciam te talibus plurimum affici, una transmitterem. Addidi itaque brevem eiusdem Cometae descriptionem, qualem illustrissimo quoque Principi WILHELMO, Hassiae Landgravio, nuper hac de re scribens communicavi. Plenius de eodem tertio nostro Tomo agam. Illic enim hunc prioribus adiungere omnesque eius apparentias, quodque omnino Parallaxi destituebatur, e multiplicibus et certis observationibus Geometrice demonstrare constitui; interim has pauculas de eodem crinito sidere conceptiones, e ratis tamen animadversionibus derivatas, boni consule. Quod autem tamdiu a prima scriptione negotium hucusque distulerim, praeter opinionem meam factum est. Licet enim expectandum erat, donec Cometes ille prorsus evanesceret, quod post sextum diem Martij accidit, et aliquantulum temporis ad eius habitudines ex observationibus rimandas requireretur, accidit insuper, ut statim post Martij medium Serenissimus Scotorum Rex cum splendido suo comitatu me hic inviseret, atque ex eo tempore per aliquot continuos dies pluribus peregrinis hospitibus excipiendis occuparer, quo minus inceptam telam pertexere licuerit. Hincque factum est, quod literas hasce usque in sextam septimanam mecum retinuerim, quod indicandum duxi, ne cur tam diverso tempore haec scribantur, mirareris, sed moram hanc eo citius excusatam haberes. Iterum vale. Haec scribebantur quarto Aprilis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most excellent man, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine and distinguished Administrator, his friend to be honoured above all — greeting.</b></p><p>I have just now received your letter, dated at Prague on the last day of November, which the most renowned Master Doctor Vitus Winshemius sent to me from Hamburg. I gather from it that you are quite anxious as to whether your earlier letters were delivered to me, and that you fear I may [not] have received them, and that for that reason I have so long held off from writing — especially since I wrote nothing by Winshemius as he was setting out to you; and from all this I perceive well enough your kindly disposition toward me and no obscure tokens of a sincere affection. But I did not know, my most loving Thaddaeus, that Master Winshemius had undertaken from here the office of Legate to His Imperial Majesty; otherwise I would not have failed to write to you. I now go to our Court more rarely than before, in the lifetime of our King Frederick, of best and most praised memory, who often had me summoned, so that, willing or not, I was then frequently compelled to be present at Court; but now I more gladly and more quietly enjoy philosophical leisure and give myself to my studies, nor do I greatly inquire what is being done in the affairs of the Court. So it could easily have come about that Winshemius came to you without my knowledge. I had nonetheless written to you already before, around the beginning of November, and replied fully to your single sheet [of four leaves], and at the same time I sent for you to inspect an <i>Apologia</i>, which in those same days, almost, I had prepared for a certain distinguished mathematician in Scotland, John Craig by name, in defence of the things contained in our second Tome which had been called in doubt by him. And lest the letters should easily go astray, especially because of the difficulties of winter, I had two [copies] written out, and arranged for one to be sent to you from Hamburg, the other from Lübeck, by the agency of the most noble man and most devoted to astronomical matters, Master Henrik Rantzau, our King's Lieutenant in Holstein, my especial friend. I also added — at your reminding — certain [letters] to the most noble and most learned man Master Jacob Curtius, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, likewise transcribed twice for the same reason. I hope, however, that you have by now received at least one set of these, if not both, and that you are therefore freed from the suspicion in which you were held [back]. As for me, I have no doubt at all about your faith and integrity, and also about your constant friendship toward me, my Thaddaeus, so that there was no need of the excuse you bring forward. It is I rather than you who am at fault, in having so long put off replying to you. Indeed I wished throughout the whole past summer that some sure messenger might be offered me, by whom I might write back to you, but since this did not happen as I wished, against my will I was compelled to defer my reply right up to the beginning of winter — just as in my last [letter] I cleared myself with you on this score, from which too you understood well enough that I had received all your [letters] safe and sound together with Werner's book, so that I reckon you are now sufficiently freed from this anxiety. In the same [letter] I also indicated why the first Tome of our Work, which I have in hand, has not yet appeared. The papermaker, whom I had sent to Lübeck in the summer, has not yet returned to my mill (concerning which I also wrote that I had had a certain one built for the making of paper). But to seek out another I have written here and there into Germany. And had not the harshness of the winter frozen the harbours, I would by now have obtained some craftsman of this kind, whom I hope to have shortly, so that printing-paper may be procured in greater quantity. In the meantime, however, I do not let what is at hand lie idle. For that first Tome which you desire is now under the press, and two gatherings [alphabets] are already printed; one and a half remain. I am also having the volume of <i>Astronomical Letters</i> struck off at another press, which will be of no less due magnitude. And so I order two presses to be driven forward daily, which consume not a little paper, so that, unless I receive the papermaker in good time, or acquire sufficient paper from elsewhere — which is done with difficulty in winter — I fear lest the Work itself feel some delay; which nevertheless, so far as in me lies, I shall take precaution against, that it not happen. Scarcely will it be granted to send the first book to the Frankfurt fair, because I still want the Privilege of the recent King of the French, promised me by his Legate, who was lately with us, by the feast of Easter. So too I still await the English [Privilege], to be procured through Rogers. In the meantime I shall also prepare the third Tome, and shall try that it too may be dispatched as quickly as can be done. If God shall favour our prayers at all, I shall bring it about that, with the coming summer, you shall at some point be made a partaker of these, together with the book of Letters, which will contain very many things to illuminate astronomical matters and others inquiring into this Cometary business too, of which we are now treating. Yours too are included, as many as I have hitherto obtained of those conferring with me on astronomical matters. My replies I miss in one place or another, since a copy is not at hand for all of them; if you have kept them, see that they are transcribed and sent to me in good time. This too I asked, if I rightly remember, in my last [letter]. That you have resolved to build a watch-tower [observatory] for astronomical observations in your new house, I praise above all. May God grant you a prosperous and long old age, that you may long be free to devote yourself to these and other excellent matters. If other cares, and also the heaviness of age, should now and then turn you aside, so that it be less convenient for you yourself to keep watch over the heavens, you will easily support one or another young man eager for these things, who may carry out observations through favourable clear weather — provided suitable instruments be not wanting. Take pains that you have a certain Sextant after the pattern of the one whose design I sent long ago (unless I am mistaken), for by it exact distances, and altitudes too, if so it please, may be searched out scrupulously enough. The cross-staff (radius astronomicus) does not furnish the precision it promises, in whatever way it be subdivided and constructed. See also that you obtain, along with it, some Azimuthal Quadrant solidly wrought, which may yield single minutes at the least. And if you arrange to have made along with these certain Equatorial Armillae after the pattern of mine — especially those that require only one and a half armillary [rings] — with these three instruments you will easily and surely accomplish whatever you desire in astronomical observations; and if some adventitious star should come upon [you], as in recent years, it will be granted to measure it out more surely and more accurately than before. When these two Tomes of which I have spoken are published, you will find in them the delineation of very many Astronomical Machines, so that you may choose the one that shall seem more convenient for you. See that you carry out the division of the Instruments by transversal points finely, and that you employ Sights [dioptra] having little slits to be made [in addition to] the foremost pinnule, just as these things are in some manner expressed at the end of the second book. You will obtain a full conception of all these matters when our Work on the Mechanical part of Astronomy has been completed, in which I have resolved to set forth abundantly at least 24 Astronomical Instruments, devised and constructed by various method, as to their fabrication and use, and to add most other things serving observations. The <i>Apologia</i> which I sent, I ask you to peruse diligently, and if you find anything where you think satisfaction has not been given, advise me. I dictated it amid very many other occupations to my amanuensis on the sudden, just as the words came to my lips, so that scarcely any leisure remained for reading it over. If I understand that anything is lacking, I shall supply it as occasion offers. And if it seems to you advisable so, I shall append this <i>Apologia</i> to the second Tome treating of the same Comet, that, rendered fuller, it may match the former [in extent]. For I still have almost all the copies unsold. For I did not wish them to become common before all the Tomes were struck in type. Concerning French affairs, which you add, they are pleasing to learn of; indeed we too learn of things similar to these — namely that the new King meets with happy success; may God grant that day by day he prosper more and more. I rejoice that the Genevans have been freed from the siege. May God deign to bestow peace and tranquillity upon the Church as well as upon the Commonwealths.</p><p>The King of the Scots has, in these very days, arrived at neighbouring Helsingør and the castle of Cronborg, together with his newly-wedded Queen, daughter of our King of most happy memory, and with a great retinue of the Realm's chief men. For since she could not put in to Scotland about the end of summer — our fleet, in which she was being carried, having been driven back into Norway by adverse tempest once and again, and at last, because of the onset of winter, breaking off the voyage — that most serene King, having boarded his ships, seeking his bride (what does Love not compel?) among the Norwegian crags, found her out; and having asked of the most serene Queen and of the Realm's Governors and Councillors that leave be granted him to come to us, once he had arrived in our dominions — being asked, and this most gladly obtained — by a long and difficult journey, especially in winter-time, he came hither, and intending to winter here with his people until free navigation be granted, he is now being received honourably at Cronborg. Which I thought should be indicated to you, that you might learn what is going forward of new matters in public affairs among us. Farewell; and if before Easter you shall have sent the Privilege obtained anew, of which I wrote last, and also Muñoz's little book and whatever others you have treating of the Cometary matter, you will have rendered a most welcome service.</p><p>Again, fare you most happily, my most loving and most longed-for Thaddaeus.<br>At Uraniborg, 25 January, the year [15]90.<br>Most loving toward you,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. On the very day on which I had handed this letter to my amanuensis to be copied fair, while toward evening I was intent on observing Venus's greatest elongation from the Sun, behold, a certain Comet appeared unexpectedly in that same western quarter; and therefore I thought I should keep this letter by me in the meantime, until I had examined all the appearances of that adventitious star, accurately deduced from the heavens throughout its whole duration, so that I might send my own findings too along with it — knowing, as I do, that you are greatly affected by such things. I have therefore added a brief description of that same Comet, such as I lately communicated, when writing on this matter, to the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse. More fully I shall treat of it in our third Tome. For there I have resolved to join this one to the earlier [comets] and to demonstrate geometrically, from manifold and certain observations, all its appearances, and that it was altogether destitute of Parallax; in the meantime take in good part these few notions concerning that same hairy star, derived nonetheless from settled observations. That I have so long deferred the business hitherto, from the time of first writing, has come about beyond my intention. For although it was needful to wait until that Comet wholly vanished — which happened after the sixth day of March — and some little time was required to search out its dispositions from the observations, there befell besides that, immediately after the middle of March, the most serene King of the Scots visited me here with his splendid retinue, and that from that time I was for several days together taken up with receiving many foreign guests, whereby I was the less able to finish the web I had begun. And hence it came about that I retained this letter by me right up to the sixth week, which I thought I should indicate, lest you wonder why these things are written at so different a time, but might rather hold this delay the more readily excused. Again, farewell. This was written on the fourth of April.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Haggecius ab Hayck</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), the Bohemian physician, astronomer and imperial body-physician at the court of Rudolf II in Prague; <i>Administrator</i> refers to his medical/court administrative office. He was Tycho's longest-standing learned correspondent and the principal channel through which Tycho's cometary controversies (and, later, his move to Bohemia) passed. <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Sender / dateline.</b> Tycho Brahe, from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, \"25 January, the year [15]90\" (O.S.). The header date matches the dateline; the dataset label (\"1590. 25. januar. Uraniborg\") agrees. Denmark was on the Julian (Old Style) calendar.</li><li><b><i>Vitus Winshemius</b></i> = <b>Veit Winsheim</b> (Vitus Winshemius), of the Wittenberg academic family; here acting as imperial legate (<i>ad Imperatoriam Maiestatem … Legati munus</i>) en route through Hamburg.</li><li><b><i>Henricus Ranzovius</b></i> = <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (Lieutenant, <i>Locumtenens</i>) of Holstein, humanist patron and astrological enthusiast, who frequently forwarded Tycho's correspondence into Germany.</li><li><b><i>Iacobus Curtius</b></i> = <b>Jacob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius), Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Imperij Procancellarius</i>) and a participant in the cometary discussions.</li><li><b><i>Ioannes Craigus</b></i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician and mathematician (later physician to James VI/I), who had disputed the parallax arguments of Tycho's <i>second Tome</i>. Tycho's <i>Apologia</i> answers him.</li><li><b>The \"Tomes\" of the <i>Opera</i>.</b> <i>Tomus secundus</i> / <i>secundo Tomo de eodem Cometa agenti</i> = the <b>second volume, on the comet</b> = Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588, on the comet of 1577). <i>Primus … Tomus</i> / <i>primus ille Tomus</i> \"now under the press\" = the <b>first volume</b>, the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (on the new star of 1572 and solar/lunar theory; published only posthumously, 1602). <i>Tertium Tomum</i> = a projected <b>third volume</b>. The \"<i>Epistolarum Astronomicarum volumen</i>\" / \"<i>Epistolarum libro</i>\" = the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I printed at Uraniborg, 1596).</li><li><b><i>Apologia.</b></i> Tycho's defence-piece against Craig's objections to the cometary parallax; he dictated it <i>subito … prout in buccam venerunt</i> (\"on the sudden, just as the words came to his lips\"), proposing to append it to the second Tome. <i>Apologia</i> is kept as a title (italicised).</li><li><b><i>Werneri liber</b></i> — \"Werner's book,\" a work of <b>Johann Werner</b> (1468–1522), Nuremberg mathematician; sent to Tycho among Hagecius's enclosures.</li><li><b><i>Munosij libellus</b></i> = the <i>little book of</i> <b>Jerónimo Muñoz</b> (Hieronymus Munyos/Munosius, c. 1520–1591), the Valencia mathematician whose tract on the 1572 new star bore on the cometary/parallax debate; Tycho asks Hagecius to send it.</li><li><b><i>Rogersius</b></i> = <b>Daniel Rogers</b> (c. 1538–1591), the English diplomat, through whom Tycho hoped to procure the English printing <b>Privilege</b> (<i>Anglicanum … Privilegium</i>). The \"<i>Privilegium nuperi Galliarum Regis</i>\" is the French royal printing-privilege promised by the legate of the <b>new King of the French</b> (Henri IV, \"the new King\" <i>Regem … novum</i>, acceded 1589). <i>Privilegium</i> = printing privilege/monopoly.</li><li><b>The paper-mill.</b> <i>moletrina mea … pro papyro conficienda</i> = Tycho's <b>paper-mill</b> (built on Hven); <i>Chartopaeus</i> = <b>papermaker</b> (Greek-Latin coinage, χάρτης + facere); <i>papyrus impressoria</i> = <b>printing-paper</b>. The whole passage documents the supply bottleneck for Tycho's private press.</li><li><b>Printing units.</b> <i>duo Alphabeta iam impressa sunt, restat sesquialterum</i> — \"two <b>alphabets</b> are already printed; <b>one and a half</b> remain\": an <i>alphabetum</i> is a printer's unit of gatherings signed through the alphabet (≈23 sheets/gatherings); <i>sesquialterum</i> = one-and-a-half (the ratio 3:2), here \"one and a half [alphabets].\" Rendered with \"[gatherings]\" glossed.</li><li><b>Frankfurt fair.</b> <i>Nundinae Francofurtianae</i> = the <b>Frankfurt book fair</b>, the chief distribution event.</li><li><b>Astronomy — adventitious star.</b> <i>ascititium sidus … ut proximis annis</i> = \"an <b>adventitious star</b>, as in recent years\" — i.e. a new star/nova or comet such as those of 1572 and 1577. <i>demetiri</i> \"measure out\" (its parallax/position).</li><li><b>Instruments (per protocol §5.2).</b> <i>Sextans</i> → <b>sextant</b>; <i>Radius Astronomicus</i> → <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b> — Tycho explicitly disparages its real precision; <i>Quadrans Azimuthalis</i> → <b>azimuthal quadrant</b> (\"yielding single minutes at the least\"); <i>Armillae Aequatoriae</i> → <b>equatorial armillae</b> (\"those that require only one and a half armillary [rings],\" <i>sesquialteram … Armillam</i> — Tycho's simplified equatorial armillary); <i>Machinae Astronomicae</i> → <b>astronomical machines/instruments</b>; <i>organa</i> → <b>instruments</b>; <i>puncta transversalia</i> → <b>transversal points</b> (Tycho's transversal-dot subdivision of scales); <i>Dioptra … rimulas … priori pinnacidio</i> → <b>sights with little slits / the foremost pinnule</b> (<i>pinnacidium/pinnula</i> → <b>sight/pinnule</b>, §5.2). The number <b>24</b> instruments anticipates the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598). <i>speculam … extruere</i> = \"to build a <b>watch-tower / observatory</b>.\"</li><li><b><i>Opus … de Mechanica Astronomiae parte</b></i> — Tycho's projected work \"on the Mechanical part of Astronomy,\" realised as the <i>Mechanica</i> (1598); rendered descriptively, the instrument-count (24+) flagged as a forecast.</li><li><b>Royal-marriage report.</b> <i>Scotorum Rex</i> = <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England); his bride the <i>nova nupta Regina … Regis nostri … filia</i> = <b>Anne of Denmark</b> (Anna, daughter of the late Frederik II). The historical episode is accurate: Anne's autumn 1589 crossing was driven back by storms into Norway; James sailed in person to fetch her and wintered in Denmark, being received at <b>Cronborg</b> (Kronborg) by <b>Helsingør</b> (Helsingør/Elsinore). <i>quid non cogit Amor?</i> — \"what does Love not compel?\", a rhetorical aside, kept. <i>sereniss. Regina Regnique Gubernatoribus</i> = the queen-dowager (Sophie) and the regency Council governing during Christian IV's minority.</li><li><b>French / Genevan affairs.</b> <i>Rex … novus</i> = Henri IV (see above); <i>Genevenses obsidione liberatos</i> = the lifting of the Savoyard <b>siege of Geneva</b> — current-events glosses Hagecius had relayed.</li><li><b>A possible negation.</b> <i>vererisque, ut eas receperim</i> — early-modern Latin <i>vereor ut</i> = \"I fear that … not\"; rendered \"you fear I may [not] have received them,\" consistent with Hagecius's anxiety that the letters miscarried. The bracketed \"[not]\" marks the idiom rather than a word in the text; flagged for transparency. (Classical <i>vereor ut</i> indeed means \"fear that … not,\" so the sense is secure; the bracket is editorial caution, not a crux.)</li><li><b>Salutation/subscription.</b> <i>Vale … Iterum quam felicissime vale</i> → \"Farewell … Again, fare you most happily\" (§5.5); <i>Tui amantiss.</i> (<i>amantissimus</i>) → \"Most loving toward you.\"</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing comet postscript (the letter held to the 6th week for the Scottish King's visit; \"scribebantur quarto Aprilis\"), restored from Dreyer VII p.228 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is secure throughout and the dense web of names, works, instruments, and current events is confirmed against the Tycho biography/bibliography layer (the two \"Tomes\" = <i>Progymnasmata</i> and <i>De mundi</i>; the Craig <i>Apologia</i>; the James VI / Anne of Denmark wintering at Kronborg, Jan. 1590). The only points requiring an editorial bracket — the <i>vereor ut</i> negation and the printing-unit <i>sesquialterum alphabetum</i> — are idiomatic, not textual cruxes, and are flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full 5-page slice as page images (pp.221-225, chunked 1-4 then 5). This letter's actual text runs from mid-page 221 ('CLARISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO D. THADDAeO...') to the signature on p.224 ('Uraniburgi 25. Ian. Anno 90. Tui amantiss. Tycho Brahe.'); p.225 is confirmed to be the start of a DIFFERENT letter (Tycho to Hagecius, 23 February 1590 - distinct running header, its own footnote citing the same Vindobonensis codex with a different footnote number, and its own German delivery-address subheading), so it contains none of this letter's content. Collated the full main body (pp.221-224) against source_text and published_english clause-by-clause, including every proper name (Winshemius, King Frederik, John Craig, Henrik Rantzau, Jacob Curtius, Werner, Rogers, Munoz, the King of Scots and his Queen) and place-name (Prague, Hamburg, Lubeck, Holstein, Uraniborg, Helsingoer, Kronborg, Norway, Geneva) and every number (two gatherings printed / one-and-a-half remaining; the sesquialtera armillary; the '24' instruments planned for the Mechanica) - all match Dreyer exactly and the translation is faithful, with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss. However, source_text carries an editorial note that the postscript ('POST SCRIPTA. CUM eo ipso die...') was 'restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 228' - that page is NOT included in the provided 5-page slice (which stops at p.225, itself already the next letter), so the postscript's Latin and its translation could NOT be visually collated against Dreyer at all. The postscript's translation is internally faithful to the given source_text (checked clause by clause, including the comet/Venus-elongation detail, the 6 March/mid-March/6th-week timeline, and the 4 April composition date), but that source_text itself is unverified against the actual printed page. Because full-letter collation against Dreyer was not achieved (the postscript's home page fell outside the slice), grade is capped at B despite a clean result on everything checked; published_grade of A was not earned by this audit's standard.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1590 (4 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/25-27", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964025"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.27."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 20, "prev": "brahe-t_15891130001", "next": "brahe-t_15900223001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15891130001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900501001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reassures Hagecius that his earlier letters arrived, explains the missed chance to write via Winshemius, and notes fewer visits to court since King Frederick's death.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900125001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900223001", "d": "1590-02-23", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 23 February 1590 [O.S.] (postscript 4 April)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address line; Greek and planet/zodiac symbols) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.29</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO D. THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO IN PRIMIS COLENDO.<br>ZU PRAG IM LANDT ZU BEHMEN AN HERN DOCTOR THADDAeUM HAGGECIUM.</p><p>SCRIPSI tibi non ita dudum, mi amantissime et doctissime D. D. THADDAeE, atque ad ultimas a te acceptas respondi, solicitudinemque omnem de prioribus tuis, quas suspicabaris non redditas mihi, uti spero, ademi. Nunc et alteras addo, ne semper a te praeveniar aut scriptionem tamdiu, ut antea, procrastinem. Licet enim non magnopere occurrat, quod nunc scribam, tamen idipsum potius indicabo, quam quod nihil mittam, praesertim cum instantibus Francofurtianis iam Nundinis occasionem has ad te mittendi prae foribus esse videam; ut si non recta, saltem inde per ambages meae ad te perferantur. Quas antea circa Novembris initia ad te paulo luculentius exaravi, iamdudum redditas confido. Nam bina exemplaria transscribi curavi seorsimque per diversos internuntios mitti, ne ambae interciderint. Quid te illic voluerim, iam procul dubio cognovisti. Nec dubito, quin in Privilegio Caesareano ampliore procurando, tuam mihi praestiturus sis fidelem operam; quod si impetraveris, fac ut mature habeam; primus enim Tomus Operis nostri, cui illud praefigendum destinavi, iam indies ad Colophonem deducitur. Nam eius tertia et ultima pars, quae aliorum placita, inter quae etiam tua, expendit, iam sub praelo est. Misi etiam tum temporis Apologeticum quoddam Scriptum, quo praestanti cuidam in Scotia Mathematico, Aristotelea tutanti et nostra convellere aggresso, occurri. Hoc si perlegisti, velut te fecisse non dubito, tuum mihi de eo iudicium communica, et si quid desiderari aut minus absolute tractatum censueris, ingenue admone. Fieri enim potest, ut Apologiam hanc secundo Tomo, qui de eodem Cometa, circa quem potissimum movetur controversia, agit, subiungam, quo locupletior reddatur. Si quae igitur inter revidendum atque recognoscendum castigatione aut suppletitione indigere perspexero, eis providebo. Literas meas, quas tibi inde a prima familiaritate Ratisponae contracta inscripsi, si quas forte reservasti, rogavi, ut singularum exemplum transscriptum mihi mitteres, quod et nunc peto, praesertim quantum ad eas attinet, quae aliquid ad Astronomicam vel Cometometricam materiam faciunt. Cur id cupiam, tunc indicavi. Et ut scias, rem ita se habere, mitto aliquam partem libri I Epistolarum Astronomicarum, quae hactenus typis absoluta est; in qua saltem eae, quae ab Illustriss. Principe WILHELMO, Hassiae Landgravio, et eius Celsit. Mathematico, unâ cum mea ad singulas responsione, ultro citroque hactenus missae sunt, continentur. Addam et illas, quas me redeuntibus ab hisce Nundinis mercatoribus (Cassellas enim pertranseunt) recepturum existimo, unâ cum mea responsione, sicque primam Tomi illius Epistolarum partem claudam. In secunda et tertia (nam et hunc trifariam subdividere lubet) aliorum de re Astronomica mecum per literas conferentium Apographa unâ cum mea rescriptione exhibebo. Illic tua quoque, quotquot in meis Archivis reperio, addam. Atque id erat caussae (ut denuo inculcem) cur mearum ad te, quas in promptu omnes non habeo, copiam desiderârim. Habeo alias non paucas Eruditorum de hac materia collationes, quas Astronomiae cupidis lectu non ingratas fore confido. Continent enim multa peculiaria et non ubique obvia ad artis penitiorem illustrationem plurimum conducentia atque rem insuper Cometicam affatim et enucleate excutientia. Licebitque primum Tomum ad tria vel quatuor Alphabeta, prout libuerit, hac ipsa Typorum forma deducere ita, ut iustae reddatur quantitatis, et Neotericorum coeli Phaenomenωn libros singulos magnitudine adaequet. Reliquas literas, quae hoc comprehendi commode non poterint, in alium sequentem digeram, sicque ordine et successive Eruditorum mecum collocutiones Astronomicas certis voluminibus, etiam alia interim agens, complectar, ne prorsus intercidant, cum multa cognitu haud indigna contineant. Has igitur primitias boni consule, tuamque de ijs sententiam mihi perscribe. Invenies vel in his plurima disquisitionem accuratam merentia; nam praeterquam quod de observationibus atque instrumentis aliter quam antea factitatum instituendis quaedam agant, de coeli essentia, stellarum refractionibus, hypothesibus coelestium motuum, tum etiam Cometis atque plerisque eiuscemodi varie inter nos disputatur. Quae omnia tuo solerti iudicio discernenda relinquo. Rogoque peramanter, velis cum Nobilissimo et amplissimo viro D. IACOBO CURTIO de his omnibus vel saltem eorum praecipuis per otij commoditatem conferre mecumque vestram eruditam censuram communicare. Nam et ROTHMANNUS, cum quo de plerisque non ingrata mihi est controversia, ad arbitros provocat, quod et ego non detrecto. Inspicite, legite, dijudicate. Nolim tamen, ut pluribus hos quaterniones communices, antequam totus liber consummatus prodierit. Primus quaternio deest, in quo titulus atque praefatiuncula quaedam extabunt, qui in totius absolutionem, uti fieri solet, differtur. Quamprimum Tomus anterior Ascititiorum coeli Phaenomenon, qui nunc sub praelo est, extremam manum calcographicam senserit, te eius reddam participem. Ne vero te, quem talibus affici scio, nimis diu sine meis detinerem, volui has qualescunque Epistolarum pagellas, etsi non ad destinatam maturitatem perductas, tecum impertiri, ut haberes quo aliquantulum, donec caetera absolvantur, animum per otium, nostra etiam qualiacunque legendo, pasceres. Tuum itaque erit omnia in meliorem partem interpretari et errata typographica, si quae, uti non dubito (necdum enim mihi ipsi ista relegere vacabat) commissa sint, propria industria, qua res ipsas satis intelligis, emendare.</p><p>Quin et aliud quiddam nunc occurrit, quod a te hisce expetam. Incidi proximis diebus, dum tuas literas evolvo, in chartam, qua observationes nonnullas a te coelitus habitas mihi denotasti, inter quas ab initio Martis stella mense Septembri et sequent. anni 1578 (licet in tua scheda per incuriam aliquam annus 1579 ponebatur), quando acronychius erat, in distantia a 1a ♈ aliquoties denotata. Quoniam vero ob raras serenitates eius observationem meis organis tunc instituere, uti cupiebam, non licuit, hancque acronychiam eius dispositionem cum pluribus alijs postmodum aliquot sequentibus annis diligenter desuper acquisitis conferre aveam, ipsius motus expendendi restituendique gratia, rogo amanter, ut si quas plures habeas in ♂ tunc praesertim temporis factas animadversiones, quoad distantiam tum ab ea ipsa, tum alijs etiam stellis, et quoad altitudinem meridianam, si eam nactus es, id quicquid erit, in meum usum describi curare velis; et praeterea quotcunque alias observationes Astronomicas in promptu habes. Licet enim sciam, eas ob Radij atque aliorum instrumentorum, quibus usus es, minus exactam perfectionem non satis praecise se habere, tamen qualescunque tandem fuerint, eas cognoscere non inutile duxerim. Nam et nonnunquam has limitare atque, sicubi opus fuerit, ex circumstantijs ipsis corrigere non difficile erit.</p><p>Qui de Nova Stella atque Cometis scripserunt, modo plures ad manus habueris, quam me obtinuisse putas, et eorum copiam facias velim. Si mittenda Rostochium ad D. D. HENRICUM BRUCAeUM, aut Lubecam Mercatori meo IACOBO BUCK, ut is nobili viro HENRICO RANZOVIO, Regis nostri in Holsatia Vicario, amico meo singulari, illa transmittat, procurâris, utique ea cito et certo accepturus sum.</p><p>Vide, quot nunc scripserim, licet ab initio vix, quae exararem, me habiturum putârim. Talis est inter nos animorum confluentia, ut unum pariat alterum. Da operam, ut brevi luculenter respondeas. Ego posthac in rescribendo maturior ero. Vale et magnificum Dominum CURTIUM meis verbis officiose saluta, a quo etiam responsionem, modo per publica otium illi supererit, indies expecto.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime atque diutissime vale.<br>Uraniburgi die 23. Februarij 1590.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.<br>POST SCRIPTA</p><p>CUM eo ipso die, quo has literas meo Amanuensi ad mundum describendas tradidissem, circa vesperas Veneris a Sole digressioni maximae observandae intenderem, ecce Cometes quidam ad eandem coeli occiduam plagam ex improviso apparuit, ideoque hasce literas interea mecum reservandas duxi, donec omnes istius ascititij sideris apparentias coelitus per totam eius durationem accurate deductas scrutatus essem, ut mea etiam inventa, ex quo sciam te talibus plurimum affici, unâ transmitterem. Addidi itaque brevem eiusdem Cometae descriptionem, qualem illustrissimo quoque Principi WILHELMO, Hassiae Landgravio, nuper hac de re scribens communicavi. Plenius de eodem tertio nostro Tomo agam. Illic enim hunc prioribus adiungere omnesque eius apparentias, quodque omnino Parallaxi destituebatur, e multiplicibus et certis observationibus Geometrice demonstrare constitui; interim has pauculas de eodem crinito sidere conceptiones, e ratis tamen animadversionibus derivatas, boni consule. Quod autem tamdiu a prima scriptione negotium hucusque distulerim, praeter opinionem meam factum est. Licet enim expectandum erat, donec Cometes ille prorsus evanesceret, quod post sextum diem Martij accidit, et aliquantulum temporis ad eius habitudines ex observationibus rimandas requireretur, accidit insuper, ut statim post Martij medium Sereniss. Scotorum Rex cum splendido suo comitatu me hic inviseret, atque ex eo tempore per aliquot continuos dies pluribus peregrinis hospitibus excipiendis occuparer, quo minus inceptam telam pertexere licuerit. Hincque factum est, quod literas hasce usque in sextam septimanam mecum retinuerim, quod indicandum duxi, ne cur tam diverso tempore haec scribantur, mirareris, sed moram hanc eo citius excusatam haberes. Iterum vale. Haec scribebantur quarto Aprilis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and, in every varied kind of learning, most erudite and most excellent man, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine, his friend to be honoured above all.</b><br><b>At Prague, in the land of Bohemia, to Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>I wrote to you not so long ago, my most loving and most learned Master Doctor Thaddaeus, and answered the last [letter] received from you, and — as I hope — I removed all your anxiety about your earlier ones, which you suspected had not been delivered to me. Now I add yet another, lest I be always forestalled by you, or, as before, put off writing for so long. For although nothing much comes to mind that I might now write, I shall sooner report this very fact than send nothing — especially since, with the Frankfurt fair now at hand, I see the chance of sending this to you to be right at the door; so that, if not directly, then at least from there, by roundabout ways, my [letters] may be carried to you. Those which earlier, around the beginning of November, I penned to you somewhat more fully, I trust have long since been delivered. For I had two copies transcribed and sent separately by different messengers, lest both should be lost. What I wanted of you over there you have now doubtless learned. Nor do I doubt that, in procuring a more ample Imperial privilege [for me], you will render me your faithful service; and if you obtain it, see that I have it in good time, for the first Volume of our Work, before which I have determined that it be set, is now day by day being brought to its colophon. For its third and last part, which weighs the opinions of others — among which yours too — is now under the press. I also sent at that time a certain apologetic writing, by which I countered a certain distinguished Mathematician in Scotland who, defending Aristotelian doctrines, had set out to demolish ours. If you have read this through — as I do not doubt you have done — share with me your judgement of it, and if you think anything is wanting or treated less than fully, frankly advise me. For it may be that I shall append this Apology to the second Volume, which treats of the same Comet about which the controversy chiefly turns, so that it may be rendered the fuller. If, then, in revising and re-examining, I perceive that any [parts] need correction or supplementation, I shall provide for them. My own letters, which I wrote to you ever since our first acquaintance was formed at Regensburg — if perhaps you have kept any — I asked that you send me a transcribed copy of each, which I ask again now, especially as regards those which bear in any way on Astronomical or Cometometric matter. Why I desire it, I indicated then. And that you may know the matter to be so, I send some part of Book I of the Astronomical Letters, which has so far been completed in print; in which there are contained at least those which, [exchanged] between the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, and His Highness's Mathematician, together with my reply to each, have so far been sent back and forth. I shall add also those which I expect to receive from the merchants returning from this fair (for they pass through Kassel), together with my reply, and so I shall close the first part of that Volume of Letters. In the second and third (for I am pleased to subdivide this too into three) I shall set forth the transcripts of others who confer with me by letter on Astronomical matters, together with my reply. There I shall add yours too, as many as I find in my archives. And this was the reason (to drive it home again) why I desired a supply of my [letters] to you, all of which I do not have ready at hand. I have not a few other collations of the Learned on this subject, which I trust will be not unwelcome reading to those eager for Astronomy. For they contain many things peculiar and not everywhere to be met with, conducing greatly to a deeper illustration of the art, and besides sifting the matter of Comets fully and clearly. And it will be possible to bring out the first Volume, as I please, to three or four Alphabets [of signatures], in this very form of type, so that it be rendered of a just size and match in bulk the individual books of the Moderns on the phenomena of the heaven. The remaining letters, which cannot conveniently be comprised in this [Volume], I shall arrange into another following one; and so, in order and successively, I shall embrace in fixed volumes the Astronomical conversations of the Learned with me — doing other things meanwhile — lest they perish utterly, since they contain much not unworthy to be known. Take these first-fruits, then, in good part, and write me your opinion of them. You will find even in these very many things deserving careful inquiry; for, besides that they treat of certain matters concerning observations and instruments to be established otherwise than was done before, there is varied disputation among us concerning the essence of the heaven, the refractions of the stars, the hypotheses of the celestial motions, and then also Comets and very many things of that kind. All of which I leave to be discerned by your skilful judgement. And I ask you most lovingly: be willing, at your leisure's convenience, to confer with the most noble and most ample man Master Jacob Curtius about all these, or at least the chief of them, and to share with me your learned assessment. For Rothmann too, with whom I have a controversy on very many points that is not unwelcome to me, appeals to arbiters — which I too do not decline. Inspect, read, judge between [us]. I would not, however, have you communicate these quires to more [people] before the whole book has come out complete. The first quire is lacking, in which the title and a certain little preface will stand, which is deferred until the completion of the whole, as is wont to be done. As soon as the earlier Volume on the adventitious phenomena of the heaven, which is now under the press, shall have felt the final engraver's hand, I shall make you a partaker of it. But, lest I detain you — whom I know to be affected by such things — too long without anything of mine, I wished to share with you these few pages of Letters, of whatever sort, though not brought to their intended maturity, so that you might have something on which, for a little while, until the rest are finished, you might feed your mind at leisure, by reading even these such-as-they-are [works] of mine. It will therefore be for you to interpret everything in the better part, and the typographical errors — if any have been committed, as I do not doubt (for I had not yet had leisure to read these over myself) — to amend by your own industry, with which you understand the matters themselves well enough.</p><p>But yet another thing now comes to mind, which I would ask of you by this [letter]. In recent days, while unrolling your letters, I came upon a paper on which you noted down for me some observations taken by you from the heaven, among them — from the beginning — the star of Mars, in the month of September and following of the year 1578 (although in your sheet, by some carelessness, the year 1579 was put), when it was acronychal, [its distance] noted several times from the 1st [degree] of ♈ Aries. But since, owing to the rare clear spells, I was not then able, as I wished, to set up an observation of it with my instruments, and I long to compare this acronychal disposition of it with several others diligently acquired thereafter in some following years, for the sake of weighing and restoring its motion, I ask lovingly that, if you have several more observations of ♂ Mars made especially at that time — as to distance both from that same [star] and from other stars too, and as to meridian altitude, if you have obtained it — whatever it may be, you would be willing to have it written out for my use; and besides, as many other Astronomical observations as you have ready at hand. For although I know that, on account of the less than exact perfection of the cross-staff and the other instruments you used, they are not held with sufficient precision, yet whatever sort they may finally have been, I should reckon it not useless to know them. For it will sometimes be no hard matter to bound these and, wherever there be need, to correct them from the circumstances themselves.</p><p>Those who have written on the New Star and on Comets — if only you have more at hand than you think I have obtained — I would wish that you make a supply of them too. If you arrange for them to be sent to Rostock to Master Doctor Heinrich Brucaeus, or to Lübeck to my merchant Jacob Buck, that he may transmit them to the noble man Heinrich Rantzau, my King's Vicar in Holstein, my especial friend, I shall in any case receive them quickly and surely.</p><p>See how much I have now written, though at the outset I scarcely thought I should have anything to set down. Such is the confluence of minds between us, that one [thing] begets another. Take pains to answer clearly soon. I, hereafter, shall be more prompt in writing back. Farewell, and greet the magnificent Master Curtius dutifully in my words, from whom too I daily await a reply, if only leisure from public affairs be left to him.</p><p>Again, fare you well most happily and most long.<br>At Uraniborg, the 23rd day of February 1590.<br>Yours, Tycho Brahe.<br>POSTSCRIPT</p><p>On the very day on which I had handed these letters to my amanuensis to be copied out fair, towards evening I was intent on observing the maximum elongation of Venus from the Sun, when behold! a certain Comet appeared unexpectedly in that same western quarter of the heaven; and so I judged that this letter should meanwhile be kept back with me, until I had scrutinized all the appearances of that adventitious star, accurately derived from the heaven throughout its whole duration, so that I might transmit my own findings too along with it — since I know you to be much affected by such things. I have therefore added a brief description of the same Comet, such as I lately communicated to the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, when writing to him on this matter. I shall treat of it more fully in our third Volume. For there I have determined to join this [comet] to the earlier ones, and to demonstrate geometrically, from manifold and sure observations, all its appearances, and that it was altogether without parallax; meanwhile take in good part these few conceptions about the same hairy star, derived nevertheless from sound observations. But that I have put off the business so long, until now, from the first writing, has happened beyond my intention. For although it had to be waited until that Comet vanished entirely — which happened after the sixth day of March — and some little time was required for tracing out its conditions from the observations, it happened besides that, straight after the middle of March, the Most Serene King of the Scots, with his splendid retinue, visited me here, and from that time, for several continuous days, I was occupied in receiving many foreign guests, so that I was the less able to finish weaving the web I had begun. And hence it came about that I have kept this letter with me right up to the sixth week — which I judged should be indicated, lest you wonder why these things are written at such different dates, but might rather hold this delay the more readily excused. Again, farewell. These [last lines] were written on the fourth of April.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, at Uraniborg on Hven, to <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (<i>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</i>, Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician, astronomer and humanist at Prague — a long-standing correspondent of Tycho's since the <i>De nova stella</i> exchanges of the 1570s. The acquaintance \"first formed at Regensburg\" (<i>Ratisponae</i>) refers to their meeting at the 1575 Imperial Diet.</li><li><b>Address line (German).</b> <i>ZU PRAG IM LANDT ZU BEHMEN AN HERN DOCTOR THADDAeUM HAGGECIUM</i> = \"At Prague, in the land of Bohemia, to Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius\" — the cover/direction in chancery German over the Latin salutation and body; the code-switch is preserved per protocol §2. <i>Behmen</i> = Böhmen (Bohemia).</li><li><b>Other persons.</b> <i>D. Iacobus Curtius</i> / <i>Dominus Curtius</i> = <b>Jacob Curtius (Jakob Kurtz von Senftenau)</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor at the court of Rudolf II, an astronomically literate patron whom Tycho cultivated (and through whom the imperial privilege was sought). <i>Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Hesse-Kassel mathematician (protocol §5.6), with whom Tycho carried on his great cosmological controversy; <i>Illustriss. Princeps Wilhelmus, Hassiae Landgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (§5.6); <i>D. D. Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> of Rostock (§5.6); <i>Iacobus Buck</i> = <b>Jacob Buck</b>, Tycho's merchant-agent at Lübeck; <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (Vicar) of Holstein, the great Schleswig-Holstein humanist and Tycho's friend and conduit. <i>Sereniss. Scotorum Rex</i> = <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who visited Hven in March 1590 during his stay in Denmark for his marriage to Anne of Denmark.</li><li><b>The \"Scottish Mathematician.\"</b> <i>praestanti cuidam in Scotia Mathematico, Aristotelea tutanti et nostra convellere aggresso</i> — the unnamed Scot defending Aristotle is <b>John Craig (Craig of Edinburgh)</b>, with whom Tycho conducted the comet-parallax dispute; the <i>Apologeticum Scriptum</i> is Tycho's reply to him.</li><li><b>The Work in press.</b> <i>primus Tomus Operis nostri</i> … <i>Tomus anterior Ascititiorum coeli Phaenomenon</i> refers to Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> / <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> programme: the volume \"on the adventitious (<i>ascititia</i>) phenomena of the heaven\" = the comet volume (<i>De mundi … recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588, of which the third part on others' opinions was still under the press); <i>liber I Epistolarum Astronomicarum</i> = the <b>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</b> (Uraniborg, 1596), here being printed in instalments, opening with the Wilhelm/Rothmann correspondence.</li><li><b>Technical / printing terms.</b> <i>Privilegium Caesareanum</i> = an <b>Imperial privilege</b> (copyright/printing protection). <i>Colophon</i> kept as printed. <i>Alphabeta</i> (<i>ad tria vel quatuor Alphabeta … deducere</i>) = \"Alphabets\" of printer's signatures (gathering-sequences A–Z); rendered \"[of signatures]\". <i>quaterniones / quaternio</i> = <b>quires</b> (gatherings of four sheets); <i>primus quaternio deest</i> = \"the first quire is lacking\" (title-leaf + preface deferred to last, normal practice). <i>sub praelo</i> = \"under the press\"; <i>extremam manum calcographicam</i> = \"the final engraver's/printer's hand.\" <i>Apographa</i> = transcript-copies. <i>Nundinae Francofurtianae</i> = the <b>Frankfurt [book] fair</b>.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>acronychius / acronychia dispositio</i> = <b>acronychal</b> (rising at sunset / in opposition to the Sun) — kept as the technical term. <i>digressio maxima</i> (a Sole) = <b>maximum elongation</b> (here of Venus from the Sun); per protocol the interpretive sense is the glossary's <i>elongatio</i> → elongation, and \"maximum\" renders <i>maxima</i> without bracketing since it stands in the source. <i>altitudo meridiana</i> = <b>meridian altitude</b>. <i>refractiones stellarum</i> = <b>refractions of the stars</b> (§5.1). <i>hypotheses coelestium motuum</i> = <b>hypotheses of the celestial motions</b>. <i>Parallaxis</i> = <b>parallax</b> (§5.1) — Tycho's point that the comet \"was altogether without parallax,\" i.e. supralunary. <i>organa</i> = <b>instruments</b>; <i>Radius</i> = the <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b> (§5.2). <i>Cometometrica materia</i> = \"Cometometric matter\" (cometary measurement) — a coinage kept and glossed. <i>Nova Stella</i> = the <b>New Star</b> (the 1572 nova). <i>crinitum sidus / Cometes</i> = \"hairy star / Comet\"; the 1590 comet was observed at Uraniborg from late February to 6 March.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> <b>♈</b> = the zodiac sign <b>Aries</b> (<i>a 1a ♈</i> = \"from the 1st [degree] of Aries\"); <b>♂</b> = the planet <b>Mars</b>. Per protocol §5.3 the glyphs are kept in the Source block and named in English in the translation.</li><li><b>In-letter date discrepancy (flagged, not reconciled).</b> Within the Mars-observation request, Tycho records that Hagecius's own sheet was dated <i>anni 1579</i> but that the observations in fact belong to <i>September 1578</i> — \"<i>licet in tua scheda per incuriam aliquam annus 1579 ponebatur</i>.\" This is Tycho's own note of an error in his correspondent's document; reproduced as written, not resolved.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating.</b> Datelined <i>Uraniburgi die 23. Februarij 1590</i> (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700), with the postscript expressly dated <i>quarto Aprilis</i> (\"the fourth of April\") — Tycho explaining that he held the letter back roughly six weeks to append his observations of the comet (vanished \"after the sixth day of March\") and because of James VI's mid-March visit. The catalogue <i>label</i> dates the letter \"1590. 23. februar.\" (the body date); the header leads with that as-written dateline and notes the postscript date. <b>No conversion is performed</b>; both internal dates (23 February; 4 April) are O.S. as the body gives them.</li><li><b>Greek lettering.</b> <i>Phaenomenωn</i> — the genitive plural is written with a Greek omega (ω) embedded in the otherwise Latin word (<i>Phaenomen-ων</i>), a learned typographic affectation; the glyph is preserved in the Source block.</li><li><b>Formulae (§5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> in the address = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting\"); <i>Vale / Iterum … vale / Iterum vale</i> = \"Farewell / Again … fare you well / Again, farewell\"; <i>D. D.</i> = <i>Domine Doctor</i> (\"Master Doctor\"); <i>Tuus</i> = \"Yours.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English 'Take pains to answer briefly and clearly soon' corrected to 'Take pains to answer clearly soon', removing a redundant double-rendering of 'brevi' ('soon'); Latin confirmed unchanged against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.227.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed, are: (i) the identity of the \"distinguished Scottish Mathematician\" (given as John Craig on the strength of the documented Tycho–Craig comet dispute, but unnamed in this letter); and (ii) the precise bibliographical force of <i>ad tria vel quatuor Alphabeta deducere</i> (\"to three or four Alphabets [of signature-gatherings]\"), rendered literally and glossed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full letter (source_text and published_english) collated line by line against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.224-229 page images (letter proper on pp.225-228; p.224 is the tail of the preceding letter, p.229 the head of the next). Verified verbatim: the heading/salutation, all proper names (Hagecius, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Jacob Curtius, Rothmann, Heinrich Brucaeus, Jacob Buck, Heinrich Rantzau), the Scottish-King visit sentence (historically correct: James VI's March 1590 visit to Uraniborg), the Mars/Aries observation passage (years 1578/1579, 1a Aries, Mars symbol), and both closing dates (23 February 1590 main letter, 4 April postscript). Latin source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly throughout the checked passages -- no TEXT_ERROR. One low-severity MISTRANSLATION found (the doubled 'brevi'). No fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). 'brevi' is the standard temporal adverb ('soon, shortly'), already rendered correctly as 'soon' at the end of the clause; the translator also renders it a second time as 'briefly' (a manner sense 'brevi' does not carry here), adding an unsupported descriptor that sits oddly against 'luculenter' ('fully/clearly'), which if anything implies the opposite of brevity. At: “Take pains to answer briefly and clearly soon.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against Dreyer page images, TBOO VII pp.224-227 (the relevant portion of the slice). The Latin clause is confirmed verbatim, isolating this as a pure translation defect; the English is corrected to drop the unsupported extra 'briefly and'. Nothing else on these pages was checked as no other defect was declared for this letter.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1589 (4 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/29-31", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964071"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.29."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 21, "prev": "brahe-t_15900125001", "next": "brahe-t_15900501001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15891130001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900501001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends a letter via the approaching Frankfurt fair, with little new substance to report but unwilling to let the occasion pass without writing.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900223001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900223002", "d": "1590-02-23", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "C", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 23 February 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ALIA TYCHONIS BRAHE AD CHRISTOPHORVM ROTHMANNVM. S. P.</p><p>SVPERIORIBVS meis literis, Doctissime ROTHMANNE, tuis vltimis luculenter respondi, idque quamprimum eas accepissem, atque te, vt denuo rescriberes mature, quo iterum hisce instantibus Nundinis Francofurdianis responsionem vicissim adornarem, rogabam. Sed video te in respondendo segniorem, tardumque nimis, semperque occupationes alias praetendere. Licet vero ego non paucioribus forte detinear; nihilominus tamen scriptionem non intermitto. Cum itaque nostrates, Nundinas Francofurdenses petituri, iter accelerent, breues hasce, ne omnino sine meis isthac transeant, mittere volui, quibus te de responso admonerem, vtut, quae scribam, multa nunc non occurrant. Hoc tamen te scire volui, Tomum meum primum iam pene Typographica opera absolutum; Nam in tertia, eaque vltima parte labor nunc vrgetur. Distinctione autem prima, non saltem curriculum Solis et Lunae, nostris seculis adamussim correspondens, denotatur, atque demonstratiue in numeros resoluitur: Sed et postea plurimarum Affixarum, praesertim Zodiaco obuersantium, accurate loca verificantur, rationesque, cur sic et non aliter constituenda veniant, adduntur. Vbi haec cum vestris contuleritis, facile ipsimet de discrepantiola nobis intercedente, vtri nostrum potius sit assentiendum, censuram proferetis. Neque etiam dubito vestros circa Fixarum loca corrigenda vtiles labores, propediem publicam lucem visuros. Quin et aliud quippiam occurrit hîc addendum. Postquam literae eaedem, quas ad te vltimo dedi, emittebantur, reperit Amanuensis meus integram pagellam, nescio, quâ incuriâ prius omissam et alicubi sepositam, nonnulla continentem, quae inter transscribendum neglexerat. Quod etiam nuper reuidendo exemplar, cognoui. Iussi itaque, vt quae tunc praetermissa erant, seorsim in charta describerentur, atque hisce adiungerentur, loco vna denotato, vbi interferenda forent. Tuum igitur erit, hanc praeteritionem candide accipere, et ad has quoque quaestiunculas, vbi rescripseris, respondere.</p><p>Vale.<br>Vraniburgo, die 23. Februarij, Anno 1590.<br>T. TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, pp. 203–204; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. INTERIM dum hae exurantur, Cometes quidam hoc ipso die 23. Februarij vesperi, inter obseruandum Venerem, prope maximam suam a Sole digreßionem, circa occasuram caeli plagam nobis conspiciebatur, ad Piscis Borei Asterismum situs. Longitudo eius erat hora 8. M. 5. in 26. P. 18. M. 5. ♓ lat. P. 18. M. 20. Borea. Caudam tenuem, sed decem circiter gradus longam adamußim in Solis oppositum, atque versus Zenit dirigit; tunc temporis eam in lucidiorem pedis Andromedae, licet non tam alte, sed saltem ad stellulam in capite Piscis Borei, eiaculando. Motum habet concitatiorem, quam priores a me obseruati; videtur enim intra vnam reuolutionem diurnam, octo pene gradus, in suo ductu absoluere. Tendit in signorum consequentia, sed latitudinem versus Boream vna sensibiliter adauget. Tu quoque, si vobis conspectus fuerit, hunc diligenter obserua, &amp; Paralaxes solerter enuclea, quod &amp; ego, fauente Numine, praestare non intermittam. Ecce tibi occasio oblata, tua dubia in Cometis, quo ad caudam, &amp; alia resoluenda videbis vtique, quae a nobis in his tradita sunt, non incongrua eße, &amp; caudas nequaquam partem corporis, sed defluuium quoddam radiorum solarium, illud penetrantium, reuera existere. Si Mercatores duas vel tres dies Hasmae adhuc moram traxerint, antequam ad Nundinas abeant, &amp; serenum interea fuerit, ego huius crinitae apparentias plenius denotabo, &amp; Illustrißimo Principi tuo, quae interea deprehendero, statim per literas significabo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Another from Tycho Brahe to Christoph Rothmann. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>In my earlier letter, most learned Rothmann, I replied fully to your last, and that as soon as I had received it; and I was asking you to write back again promptly, so that I might in turn prepare a reply once more for these approaching Frankfurt fairs. But I see that you are rather sluggish in answering, and far too slow, and that you are always pleading other occupations. Yet although I too am perhaps detained by no fewer, nonetheless I do not break off my writing. And so, since our people who are bound for the Frankfurt fairs are hastening their journey, I wished to send this short letter — lest they pass that way altogether without something from me — by which I might remind you about a reply, even though there are not many things that occur to me to write now. This much, however, I wished you to know: that my first volume is now nearly finished, so far as the printing work goes; for the labour is now being pressed on in the third and last part. In the first division, not only is the course of the Sun and Moon, corresponding to a hair's breadth with our own age, set out and resolved demonstratively into numbers; but afterwards too the places of very many of the fixed [stars], especially those lying along the Zodiac, are accurately verified, and the reasons are added why they are to be established thus and not otherwise. When you [pl.] have compared these with your own [results], you will easily yourselves deliver judgement, concerning the little discrepancy that comes between us, as to which of us is rather to be assented to. Nor indeed do I doubt that your useful labours toward correcting the places of the fixed [stars] will shortly see the public light. Moreover, something else also occurs to be added here. After that same letter which I last sent you had been dispatched, my amanuensis found a whole little page — omitted earlier by I know not what carelessness, and set aside somewhere — containing some matters which he had neglected in the course of transcribing. This too I came to know lately, when reviewing the copy. I therefore gave orders that the things which had then been passed over should be written out separately on a sheet, and joined to this present [letter], with the place marked at the same time where they were to be inserted. It will therefore be for you to take this omission in good part, and, when you write back, to answer these little questions also.</p><p>Farewell.<br>From Uraniborg, the 23rd day of February, in the year 1590.<br>T[ycho]. Tycho Brahe.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. Meanwhile, as these [pages] are being printed off, a certain Comet was seen by us this very day, the 23rd of February, in the evening, while we were observing Venus near her greatest elongation from the Sun, in the quarter of the sky about to set, placed at the asterism of the Northern Fish. Its longitude at the 8th hour, 5 minutes, was 26°18′5″ in Pisces [♓], latitude 18°20′ North. It directs a thin tail, but about ten degrees long, exactly opposite the Sun and toward the Zenith, at that time darting it toward the brighter [star] of Andromeda's foot, though not so high, but only as far as the little star in the head of the Northern Fish. It has a motion more rapid than the earlier ones observed by me; for it seems to accomplish, within one diurnal revolution, almost eight degrees in its course. It tends in the order of the signs, but at the same time perceptibly increases its latitude toward the North. You too, if it shall come into your view, observe it diligently and skilfully tease out its parallaxes, which I also, with the favour of the Deity, shall not fail to do. Behold, an occasion is offered you: you will assuredly see your doubts about Comets — as regards the tail, and the other matters to be resolved — that what we have set down on these points is not incongruous, and that the tails are by no means a part of the body, but are in truth a certain effluence of the Sun's rays penetrating it. If the merchants tarry yet two or three days at Hasmae before they depart to the fairs, and it shall be clear in the meantime, I shall more fully note this comet's appearances, and shall at once signify by letter to your most illustrious Prince whatever I shall have meanwhile observed.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Uraniborg on Hven. The heading <i>ALIA … S. P.</i> (\"Another … heartiest greeting\") is the running rubric of the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> volume, which prints a connected series of the Tycho–Rothmann exchange; <i>ALIA</i> (\"another [letter]\") marks this as a further item in that sequence. <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting\"), per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, mathematician and astronomer at the court of Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel (protocol §5.6). The two carried on a substantial technical correspondence in the later 1580s; by early 1590 Rothmann's replies had fallen off (Tycho's complaint here), and Rothmann would soon visit Hven in person (Aug.–Sep. 1590).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Vraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-house on the island of Hven (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>Nundinae Francofurdianae / Francofurdenses</b></i> — \"the <b>Frankfurt fairs</b>,\" the twice-yearly Frankfurt book fair (here the spring fair), the standard conduit by which learned correspondence and books travelled north. The two spellings appear as printed and are kept.</li><li><b><i>Tomum meum primum … Distinctione … prima … tertia, eaque vltima parte</b></i> — Tycho's \"<b>first volume</b>\" is the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the work on the renewal of astronomy: solar/lunar theory and the fixed-star catalogue), then in the press; <i>Distinctio</i> is rendered \"division\" (a major part of the work) and <i>pars</i> \"part.\" The volume was in fact not completed in Tycho's lifetime; the optimistic \"nearly finished\" is his own forecast and is preserved, not corrected.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>curriculum Solis et Lunae</i> → \"the course of the Sun and Moon\" (the solar and lunar theory/motions); <i>demonstratiue in numeros resoluitur</i> → \"resolved demonstratively into numbers\" (worked out into computed tables); <i>Affixae / Fixae</i> → \"the <b>fixed [stars]</b>\" (<i>stellae affixae / fixae</i>); <i>Zodiaco obuersantium</i> → \"lying along [facing toward] the Zodiac\" (the zodiacal stars whose places matter for the ecliptic frame); <i>loca verificantur</i> → \"the places are verified\" (<i>verificare</i>, to establish/confirm a position, here kept as \"verify\"); <i>Fixarum loca corrigenda</i> → \"correcting the places of the fixed [stars].\"</li><li><b><i>adamussim</b></i> — \"to a hair's breadth, exactly\" (literally \"to the mason's line/rule\"); rendered \"corresponding to a hair's breadth with our own age,\" i.e. agreeing exactly with present-day observation.</li><li><b><i>discrepantiola</b></i> — a diminutive, \"the <b>little discrepancy</b>\" between Tycho's and the Kassel results; the diminutive (a courteous minimising) is kept rather than flattened to \"discrepancy.\"</li><li><b><i>censuram proferetis … cum vestris … ipsimet</b></i> — the second-person <b>plural</b> here (\"you [pl.] have compared … you will deliver judgement … with your own [results]\") embraces the Kassel team (Rothmann together with the Landgrave's instrument-maker Jost Bürgi and the wider Kassel programme), as against the singular <i>te / tibi</i> addressed to Rothmann himself elsewhere in the letter. The number-shift is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Amanuensis</b></i> — \"<b>amanuensis</b>\" (Tycho's copying-secretary at Uraniborg); <i>pagella</i> → \"little page,\" <i>charta</i> → \"sheet,\" <i>exemplar</i> → \"the copy\" (the fair copy/exemplar of the letter being transcribed). The episode: a page of the previous letter had been left out in transcription, found afterwards, and is now being sent as a separate insert with its insertion-point marked.</li><li><b><i>quaestiunculas</b></i> — a diminutive, \"<b>little questions</b>\" (the minor queries on the recovered page); diminutive kept.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> → \"heartiest greeting\"; <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>candide accipere</i> → \"take in good part\" (<i>candide</i>, frankly/kindly). Per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 23. Februarij, Anno 1590</i> = 23 February 1590, <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The dataset `label` (\"1590. 23. februar. Uraniborg\") agrees with the as-written dateline; no discrepancy. Catholic-territory correspondents of these years would have dated this day 4 March N.S., but Tycho writes O.S. and no conversion is made.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Printed <i>u/v</i> conventions of the 1596 Uraniborg edition are reproduced as set (<i>SVPERIORIBVS, vltimis, vt, breues, Affixarum, reuidendo</i>); the circumflex on <i>hîc, quâ, incuriâ</i> is the edition's. No editorial normalisation applied.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing comet postscript (23 Feb observation among Venus-observations; tail anti-solar, per-accidens-optical), restored from Dreyer VI pp.203–204 (dropped by the working transcription); 1596-edition u/v + ß orthography kept.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'at Hven' corrected to the transliterated 'at Hasmae' — the source's place-name does not match Tycho's usual Latin for Hven and could not be identified with confidence (no Dreyer page available for this letter). Restored the dropped 'M. 5.' arcsecond figure to the comet's longitude, now '26°18′5″ in Pisces'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The text is clear humanist Latin with no genuine crux; the only judgement calls (the singular→plural address-shift, the diminutives <i>discrepantiola</i>/<i>quaestiuncula</i>, and Tycho's own over-optimistic \"nearly finished\" for the <i>Progymnasmata</i>) are preserved and noted rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (one of the 49 without a Dreyer slice), so audited against source_text alone per the brief and capped at grade B regardless of findings. Two numeral/place-name anomalies were noted in the postscript, which the packet itself flags as editorially restored/spliced text (from Dreyer VI pp.203-204, not supplied here as page images) -- neither could be adjudicated without that slice.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Our source_text reads \"Hasmae\" for the place where merchants are tarrying before the fair; this matches neither Tycho's usual Latin for Hven (Huenna) nor any recognisable place-name, so \"Hven\" is asserted with no clear counterpart. No slice_pdf exists to check whether Dreyer's actual reading is e.g. Hafniae (Copenhagen), which would fit the merchant context better. At: “at Hven”.<br>- <b>OMISSION</b> (low). The source's garbled numeral string carries an extra \"M. 5.\" element after the degree/minute pair for the comet's longitude that the English silently drops rather than rendering. At: “26°18′ in Pisces”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice exists for this letter (tboo: null), so neither fix could be checked against the edition of record; both rest on internal evidence within the packet itself. The Hasmae/Hven mismatch is demonstrable by comparison with Tycho's consistent 'Huenna'/'Huena' usage elsewhere in this corpus, so the unsupported 'Hven' gloss was removed in favour of a literal transliteration rather than a new guess. The dropped 'M. 5.' longitude figure is restored as arcseconds by analogy with the parallel, already-correct latitude clause's degree/minute structure, but the exact place-name behind 'Hasmae' and the precise unit of the restored figure remain unverified cruces. Grade C.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 14, "prev": "brahe-t_15891124001", "next": "brahe-t_15900418001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890822001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900418001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Chides Rothmann for slow replies and reports that printing of his first volume is nearly finished, with the third and final part now in press.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900223002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900228001", "d": "1590-02-28", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 28 February 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VI–VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISS. ET CLARISS. VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, HWENAe INSULAe DOMINO, REGIS DANIAe CONSILIARIO ET MATHEMATICO DOCTISSIMO, AMICO SUO HVENAM DANIAe MITTANTUR.<br>NOBILISS. ET CLARISS. VIR, AMICE COLENDE, S. P.</p><p>ANTE biduum nobis D. FULLERUS noster obviam factus inter alia retulit, quam honorifice abs te fuerit acceptus, quam admiranda viderit, et quod legerit, quae ad primas meas responderis, ubi ego: iamdudum mihi constat, in illa Uraniae arce cum summa humanitate eximiam scientiam habitare; sed sciscitatus sum, an meminerit, quae de mota quaestione scripseris? Non, inquit, nam pleraque captum meum superabant; hoc tantum percepi, plures sententiae suae demonstrationes afferri. Demonstrationes ego libenter intelligam, et si constiterint, facile ijs cedam, quique hoc ὅτι sic docuerit, maioris apud me authoritatis hac ex parte, quam ARISTOTELES erit, quod authoritas ex veritate aestimanda sit, non haec ex illa, nisi in Divinis, ubi ipsa Veritas ἔφα. Verum si coniecturis agatur, plus habeant probabilitatis oportet quam contrariae, ut maiorem fidem mereantur. Ego, clariss. vir, si quas petij longitudines accepissem, iamdudum quid veri hîc esset, demonstratione explorassem; alioqui in hac quaestione versari non libet. Nam nunc etiam peto, ut quos in Numeris Canonis Sinuum errores longo usu tuo cognoveris, prima occasione mecum communices; nam nobis singulare quiddam prae manibus est, in quo perficiendo ingentem opem hic Canon praestat, sique error suberit, nobis cum fenore suppeditabitur. Vel ijs essem contentus, qui sunt infra 60 (quamvis et reliquos ascribas) nam qui supra sunt, singulari compendio exhibere potero, quod hîc amicitiae nostrae causa ascribam.* [Footnote 1 — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 240; dropped by the working transcription: \"Canon, quo ipse utor, Rheinoldi est excusus Tubingae anno 1554 apud heredes Morhardi, tabulis directionum insertus.\"]</p><p>Figur</p><p>Duorum arcuum trientem complentium sinus semidifferentiae est differentia sinuum (eorundem arcuum). Sint duo arcus ab, ad complentes trientem, quia ac est 60, et quanto minor ab deficit ab ac, tanto maior ad superat. Differentia est bd, sinus dimidij differentiae est ed, quem demonstro esse aequalem lineae dg, quae differentia est duorum sinuum dk et bm seu gk. Cum ilk sit 60, erit kil 30 et uterque gfi et bfe erit 60, quare totus efg erit 120. In duobus orthogonijs latera be et ed sunt aequalia et ef commune, quare reliqua reliquis erunt aequalia, et angulus bfe aequalis efd angulo, qui erit 60, et tertius angulus dfg erit 60; in duobus orthogonijs efd et dfg anguli efd et dfg sunt aequales et latus df commune, quare reliqua reliquis aequantur, et latus ed aequale erit lateri dg, quod propositum fuit. Itaque dabitur quilibet sinus supra 60, si sinui arcus tanto minoris 60 addatur sinus dimidij differentiae duorum arcuum, ut ed ad bm facit dk; sic Canon Sinuum tertia parte minuetur.</p><p>Canonem fecundum dimidio abbreviabo alio compendio, quod etiam hîc annotabo. Duorum arcuum quadrantem complentium semidifferentia fecundorum est fecundus differentiae eorundem. Sint duo arcus cb, bf quadrantem aequantes, quia caf rectus est, fecundus cb est db, fecundus bf est bh, differentia duorum arcuum est be, cuius fecundus est bg, quem dico esse dimidium bm differentiae inter duos fecundos. Diametris sc. ai et dh se secantibus, erit hi aequalis ad, et ob id erit aequalis dk arcui, cum ad et dk sint aequales; ergo angulus dak aequalis erit iah, et ablato igitur cb seu eius aequali ef ex bf, relinquitur be differentia duorum arcuum. Rursum db, cui sit aequalis mh, aufero a bh, remanebit bm, cuius dimidium est bg; nam ab aequalibus semidiametris dg et gh, aequalibus db et mh ablatis, remanent bg et gm aequalia. Si igitur detur fecundus db et quaeritur fecundus bh, aufero arcum cb a bf et differentiae be fecundum in canone quaero bg, hunc duplico, erit bm, et addo fecundum minoris mh, proveniet fecundus totius. Si differentia be fuerit maior 45 gradibus, duplici operatione opus erit, priori reperietur fecundus differentiae, posteriori fecundus totius.</p><p>Figur</p><p>Quia in has speculationes incidi et figura servit, tertium ascribam, quo omnem RHETICI Canonem, de quo tantopere gloriatur, eximio fecundi usu tollam, ut dabo hypothenusam ad in partibus, qualium db sinus est sinus totus, db sinu in fecundo reperto, ut sit 3420201, arcus 20 erit angulo 18° 52½ , ut db fecundus 3420201 ad ab S. T. sic ab S. T. ad bh fecundum supplementi anguli 18° 52½ , qui ex Canone dabitur; est enim ab medium proportionale inter duo fecundos. Ut ergo ab S. T. ad bh fecundum, sic erit db sinus 3420201 ad S. T. db fecundus 3420201 est ad ad S. T. ut ab S. T. ad fecundum bh; sed db sinus 3420201 est ad ad S. T. ut db fecundus 3420201 ad ab S. T. Ergo db sinus 3420201 est ad ad S. T. ut S. T. ab ad fecundum bh; dabitur igitur in Canone fecundus bh et erit ad in eo numero partium qualium db est S.T. ad ad, quia termini non mutantur. Et idem numerus qui in priori fecundus, inde sinus est; abiectis igitur his terminis, si db ponatur sinus totus, erit ad fecundus supplementi anguli 18 52½ . Simili modo si detur sinus ab, dabitur ad in partibus qualium ab est S. T. Non vulgaris hic usus est fecundi Canonis, quo maxima reperiuntur, de quibus alias pluribus conferemus, nam nihil apud me horum secretorum erit, quin libenter ut ea suggeret memoria communicabo: tu haec tanquam amicitiae nostrae μνημόσυνοv boni consule.</p><p>Discessit hinc Sereniss. Rex noster praeter omnium opinionem et expectationem; nam mihi proficiscendum unâ fuit, sed hyeme valetudinem terrente, locus fuit excusationi, sed si de abitu eius constitisset, nihil inde deterruisset, nunc vero discussa prope hyeme cum redeundi occasio redeat, faxit omnipotens Deus, ut incolumis cum Sereniss. Regina nostra quam primum revertatur, hoc communibus et privatis votis assidue petitur. In Illustriss. Reginae comitatu ferunt Medicum esse D. ANDREAM KRAGIUM, qui tamen hîc non sit moraturus, sed ex nostratibus quispiam sufficiendus; id si ita fuerit, cuperem mei haberi rationem, siquidem eo loco dignus esse potero, non deerit nostratium D. D. consiliariorum promotio, quibus notus sum, et cum quibus sanguinis necessitudo intercedit. Vestratium tu conciliare mihi poteris, quod quasi ex te profectum sit, facias amanter peto, Domino fratre tuo et D. KRAGIO literis tuis in mei favorem et commendationem salutatis. Ideo hic locus acceptus esset, quod praxis nostra cum summa corporis nostri molestia coniuncta sit, nam maxima in equis itinera quacunque coeli tempestate nobis saepe conficienda sunt, quibus corpus meum minus nunc idoneum esse experior, ut multo acceptior sit futura, quae cum minore corporis labore est, quare rogo, ut tuo beneficio, si fieri id posset, melius valetudini meae consulere queam. Sed ita velim ad eos scribas, ut si quaeratur Illustriss. Reginae nostrae Medicus, tuo amico ipsi omnibus modis faveant, idque quasi ex teipso; vides, quantum mihi ex amicitia nostra pollicear, quam non pudeat res meas ei credere, quod certe minime facerem, nisi singularem de tua erga me benevolentia spem haberem. Huius anni Eclipses quam accurate observaturus sis, facile intelligo; nobis id non datur, nam nec sunt organa, quibus fidere possumus, nec artifices, qui nobis parent. Momenta initij et finis ac medij (praeter visualem Diametrum) definienda existimo datis fixarum altitudinibus, nam altitudo, ut principium, sensu per instrumentum notum dari, non ex alijs demonstrari debet. Sed finem faciam, ne sim molestior. Coeli et Terrae Dominus tibi adsit, tuisque faveat conatibus et incolumem conservet, tui amantissimum tuis literis revise.</p><p>Datae Edinburgi pridie Calend. Martij.<br>Tuus IOAN. CRAIGUS, philosophiae et medicinae doctor.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 242; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si quid singulare in chymicis habueris, quod putes amico communicandum, facias velim.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most distinguished man, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of the island of Hven, Counsellor to the King of Denmark and most learned mathematician, his friend — to be conveyed to Hven in Denmark.</b><br><b>Most noble and most distinguished sir, friend to be honoured, heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Two days ago our [friend] Master Fuller, having met me, reported among other things how honourably he had been received by you, what marvellous things he had seen, and that he had read what you had replied to my first [letter] — whereupon I [said]: it has long been settled in my mind that in that citadel of Urania a singular learning dwells together with the utmost courtesy; but I asked whether he remembered what you had written about the question that was raised. \"No,\" he said, \"for most of it was beyond my grasp; I gathered only this much, that several demonstrations of his own view are brought forward.\" Demonstrations I shall gladly understand, and if they hold good I shall readily yield to them; and whoever shall have taught me this <i>that</i> [ὅτι] in such a way will be, in this matter, of greater authority with me than Aristotle — for authority is to be assessed from truth, not truth from authority, except in things divine, where Truth itself has spoken [ἔφα]. But if the matter is conducted by conjectures, they must have more probability than the contrary ones, that they may earn greater credit. I, most distinguished sir, had I received the longitudes I sought, would long since have explored by demonstration what truth there is here; otherwise I have no wish to busy myself with this question. For now too I ask that, at the first occasion, you communicate to me whatever errors in the Numbers of the Canon of Sines you have come to know by your long use [of it]; for we have a certain singular [undertaking] in hand, in the perfecting of which this Canon affords mighty help, and if an error lurk [in it], it will be repaid to us with interest. I should be content even with those [sines] which are below 60 (although you might write out the rest as well), for those that are above I shall be able to exhibit by a singular compendium, which I here append for our friendship's sake.\\* [Footnote 1: \"The Canon which I myself use is Rheinhold's, printed at Tübingen in the year 1554 by the heirs of Morhard, inserted in the Tables of Directions.\"]</p><p>[Figure]</p><p>The sine of the half-difference of two arcs filling out a third [of the quadrant, i.e. summing to 60°] is the difference of the sines (of those same arcs). Let there be two arcs <i>ab</i>, <i>ad</i> filling out a third [of the quadrant], since <i>ac</i> is 60; and by however much the lesser, <i>ab</i>, falls short of <i>ac</i>, by so much the greater, <i>ad</i>, exceeds [it]. The difference is <i>bd</i>; the sine of half the difference is <i>ed</i>, which I demonstrate to be equal to the line <i>dg</i>, which is the difference of the two sines <i>dk</i> and <i>bm</i> (that is, <i>gk</i>). Since <i>ilk</i> is 60, <i>kil</i> will be 30, and each [angle] <i>gfi</i> and <i>bfe</i> will be 60; wherefore the whole [angle] <i>efg</i> will be 120. In the two right [triangles] the sides <i>be</i> and <i>ed</i> are equal, and <i>ef</i> [is] common; wherefore the remaining [parts] will be equal to the remaining, and the angle <i>bfe</i> equal to the angle <i>efd</i>, which will be 60, and the third angle <i>dfg</i> will be 60. In the two right [triangles] <i>efd</i> and <i>dfg</i> the angles <i>efd</i> and <i>dfg</i> are equal and the side <i>df</i> [is] common; wherefore the remaining [parts] are equal to the remaining, and the side <i>ed</i> will be equal to the side <i>dg</i> — which was [the thing] proposed. And so any sine above 60 will be given, if to the sine of an arc as much below 60 there be added the sine of half the difference of the two arcs, as <i>ed</i> added to <i>bm</i> makes <i>dk</i>; thus the Canon of Sines will be lessened by a third part.</p><p>I shall abbreviate the <i>secundus</i> [versed-sine] Canon by a half, by another compendium, which I shall also note here. The half-difference of the <i>secundi</i> [versed sines] of two arcs filling out a quadrant is the <i>secundus</i> [versed sine] of the difference of those same [arcs]. Let there be two arcs <i>cb</i>, <i>bf</i> equalling a quadrant, since <i>caf</i> is a right [angle]; the <i>secundus</i> of <i>cb</i> is <i>db</i>, the <i>secundus</i> of <i>bf</i> is <i>bh</i>, the difference of the two arcs is <i>be</i>, whose <i>secundus</i> is <i>bg</i>, which I say to be half of <i>bm</i>, the difference between the two <i>secundi</i>. The diameters, namely <i>ai</i> and <i>dh</i>, cutting one another, <i>hi</i> will be equal to <i>ad</i>, and on that account [the arc <i>hi</i>] will be equal to the arc <i>dk</i>, since <i>ad</i> and <i>dk</i> are equal; therefore the angle <i>dak</i> will be equal to <i>iah</i>; and so, <i>cb</i> (or its equal <i>ef</i>) being taken from <i>bf</i>, there is left <i>be</i>, the difference of the two arcs. Again, <i>db</i> — to which let <i>mh</i> be equal — I take from <i>bh</i>; there will remain <i>bm</i>, whose half is <i>bg</i>; for from the equal semidiameters <i>dg</i> and <i>gh</i>, the equals <i>db</i> and <i>mh</i> being taken away, there remain <i>bg</i> and <i>gm</i> equal. If therefore the <i>secundus db</i> be given and the <i>secundus bh</i> is sought, I take the arc <i>cb</i> from <i>bf</i>, and seek in the canon <i>bg</i>, the <i>secundus</i> of the difference <i>be</i>; this I double — it will be <i>bm</i> — and I add the <i>secundus</i> of the lesser, <i>mh</i>: the <i>secundus</i> of the whole will result. If the difference <i>be</i> be greater than 45 degrees, there will be need of a double operation: by the former the <i>secundus</i> of the difference will be found, by the latter the <i>secundus</i> of the whole.</p><p>[Figure]</p><p>Since I have fallen upon these speculations and the figure serves [the purpose], I shall append a third, by which I may do away with the whole Canon of Rheticus — in which he glories so greatly — by an outstanding use of the <i>secundus</i> [versed sine]: so that I shall give the hypotenuse <i>ad</i> in [such] parts as those of which <i>db</i>, the sine, is the whole sine [the radius]; <i>db</i> having been found in the <i>secundus</i>, so that it be 3420201, the arc 20[°] will [correspond] to an angle of 18° 52½′; as <i>db</i> the <i>secundus</i> 3420201 is to <i>ab</i> the whole sine [radius], so is <i>ab</i> the whole sine to <i>bh</i> the <i>secundus</i> of the supplement of the angle 18° 52½′, which will be given from the Canon — for <i>ab</i> is the mean proportional between the two <i>secundi</i>. As therefore <i>ab</i> the whole sine is to <i>bh</i> the <i>secundus</i>, so will <i>db</i> the sine 3420201 be to the whole sine. <i>db</i> the <i>secundus</i> 3420201 is to <i>ad</i> the whole sine as <i>ab</i> the whole sine to the <i>secundus bh</i>; but <i>db</i> the sine 3420201 is to <i>ad</i> the whole sine as <i>db</i> the <i>secundus</i> 3420201 to <i>ab</i> the whole sine. Therefore <i>db</i> the sine 3420201 is to <i>ad</i> the whole sine as the whole sine <i>ab</i> to the <i>secundus bh</i>; therefore the <i>secundus bh</i> will be given in the Canon, and <i>ad</i> will be in that number of parts of which <i>db</i> is the whole sine — [the ratio] <i>ad</i> to <i>ad</i> [being unity], since the terms are not changed. And the same number which [stands] as <i>secundus</i> in the former [case] is thence the sine; with these terms cast off, therefore, if <i>db</i> be set as the whole sine, <i>ad</i> will be the <i>secundus</i> of the supplement of the angle 18° 52½′. In like manner, if the sine <i>ab</i> be given, <i>ad</i> will be given in parts of which <i>ab</i> is the whole sine. This use of the <i>secundus</i> Canon is no common one, by which the greatest [things] are found, about which we shall confer more at another time; for none of these secrets will be [withheld] by me, but I shall gladly communicate them as memory shall suggest them: take these things in good part, as a memorial [μνημόσυνον], as it were, of our friendship.</p><p>Our most serene King departed hence beyond everyone's opinion and expectation; for I too had to set out with [him], but, the winter making one fear for [his] health, there was room for an excuse — though, had his departure been [firmly] settled, nothing would have deterred [me] from it. But now, the winter being nearly dispelled, since the occasion for returning comes round [again], may almighty God grant that he return safe, together with our most serene Queen, as soon as possible — this is sought unceasingly by public and private prayers. In the most illustrious Queen's retinue they say the physician is Master Andrew Krag, who, however, is not to remain here, but someone from among our own [countrymen] is to be supplied [in his place]; and if that be so, I could wish that account be taken of me — since I shall be able to be worthy of that post, the recommendation of our own lordly counsellors, to whom I am known and with whom there is a tie of blood, will not be lacking. Those of your [side] you can win over for me — which I lovingly ask you to do, as though it had proceeded from yourself — your lord brother and Master Krag being saluted in your letters in my favour and commendation. This post would be welcome for this reason, that our practice is bound up with the utmost trouble to our body; for we often have to accomplish very great journeys on horseback in whatever weather of the sky, for which I now find my body less fit, so that one will be much more welcome that is [attended] with less labour of the body; wherefore I ask that, by your good offices, if it could be done, I may be able to provide better for my health. But I should wish you to write to them in such a way that, if a physician for our most illustrious Queen be sought, they may by all means favour your friend himself — and that as though [it came] from your own self. You see how much I promise myself from our friendship, that I am not ashamed to entrust my affairs to it — which assuredly I should by no means do, had I not a singular hope of your goodwill towards me. How accurately you will observe this year's eclipses, I readily understand; to us that is not granted, for we have neither instruments in which we can trust, nor craftsmen who will obey us. The moments of the beginning and the end and the middle (apart from the visual diameter) I judge must be determined from given altitudes of the fixed [stars]; for altitude, as a first principle, ought to be given by sense through a known instrument, not demonstrated from other [things]. But I shall make an end, lest I be too troublesome. May the Lord of Heaven and Earth be with you, and favour your endeavours and keep you safe; revisit him who most dearly loves you with your letters.</p><p>Given at Edinburgh, the day before the Kalends of March.<br>Yours, John Craig, doctor of philosophy and of medicine.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If you should have anything remarkable in chemical matters that you think ought to be shared with a friend, I should wish you to do so.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioan. Craigus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Scottish physician and mathematician, an Aristotelian who from c. 1588 disputed Tycho's astronomical and trigonometrical positions; he later became chief physician to James VI/I. Tycho gave Craig an inscribed copy of <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), now in Edinburgh University Library, and \"wrote out a long defence of his ideas against a challenge by Craig, for which Craig later apologized\" (Gingerich, <i>Census</i>; cf. A. Mosley, \"Tycho Brahe and John Craig: The Dynamic of a Dispute,\" in <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i>). The signature <i>philosophiae et medicinae doctor</i> fits this John Craig.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe, Hwenae insulae domino, Regis Daniae consiliario et mathematico</i> — Tycho on Hven, the king's counsellor and mathematician; the cover instruction <i>Hvenam Daniae mittantur</i> (\"let it be sent to Hven in Denmark\") is the postal direction, kept.</li><li><b><i>D. Fullerus noster</b></i> — \"our Master Fuller,\" a Scottish intermediary (evidently recently a visitor to Uraniborg) who carried news between Craig and Tycho; not further identified here.</li><li><b><i>mota quaestione</b></i> — \"the question that was raised\": the standing Tycho–Craig controversy (over cometary parallax / the structure of the heavens and the trigonometric methods bound up with it).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> (1) <i>ὅτι</i> (\"the <i>that</i>\"): the <b>fact</b> (<i>to hoti</i>), as opposed to the reasoned cause (<i>to dioti</i>) — a scholastic/Aristotelian distinction; rendered \"this <i>that</i>\" with gloss. (2) <i>ἔφα</i> (Doric for <i>ἔφη</i>, \"[he] has spoken\"): an echo of the Pythagorean <i>αὐτὸς ἔφα</i> (\"he himself said it\") transferred to divine Truth — \"where Truth itself has spoken.\" (3) <i>μνημόσυνοv</i>: \"memorial, keepsake\" (<i>mnēmosynon</i>); printed with a final <i>v</i> for ν in the source, kept verbatim, named in the translation. All three transcribed and glossed per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Subject of the letter — trigonometric compendia.</b> Craig sends three geometric demonstrations for <b>abbreviating the standard tables</b>, the prosthaphaeresis-adjacent computing economies that lay at the heart of the dispute and of Tycho's manual of trigonometry (on which see Dreyer, \"On Tycho Brahe's Manual of Trigonometry,\" <i>The Observatory</i> 39 (1916): 127–131; Thoren, \"Prosthaphaeresis Revisited,\" <i>Historia Mathematica</i> 1988):</li><li><b>(i)</b> the table of <b>sines</b> can be shortened by a third, because the sine of any arc above 60° equals the sine of an arc as far below 60° plus the sine of half their difference;</li><li><b>(ii)</b> the table of <i>secundi</i> can be halved by an analogous relation across the quadrant;</li><li><b>(iii)</b> Rheticus's whole canon (the <i>Opus palatinum</i>/great canon of <b>Rheticus</b>, Georg Joachim Rheticus, 1514–74) can be dispensed with by exploiting the <i>secundus</i>.</li><li><b><i>fecundus</b></i> <b>(printed form; the editorial crux of the technical sections).</b> Dreyer's text and the working transcription read <i>fecundus / fecundorum / fecundi</i> throughout the second and third demonstrations. This is read here as the <i>sinus <b>secundus</b></i> — i.e. the <b>versed sine</b> (<i>sinus versus</i>, the \"second sine\" of period usage: radius minus cosine) — the <i>f</i> standing for long-<i>s</i> / Craig's own spelling. The mathematics is internally consistent only on this reading (the <i>secundus</i> of an arc and of its supplement, the mean-proportional relation <i>ab² = secundus(arc) · secundus(supplement)·R</i>, and the value <b>3420201</b> for the radius-scaled versed sine of 20°). The printed <i>fecundus</i> is retained verbatim in the Source block; the translation renders it \"<i>secundus</i> (versed sine)\" and the reading is flagged here. <b>Crux named: <i>fecundus</i> = <i>secundus</i>/versed sine.</b></li><li><b>Diagram letters and numbers.</b> The lettered points (<i>ab, ad, ac, bd, ed, dg, dk, bm, gk; cb, bf, db, bh, be, bg, mh, gm; ai, dh, hi, ad</i>) are the figure labels; left untranslated as labels. <i>S. T.</i> = <i>sinus totus</i>, the <b>whole sine</b> (the radius, the tabular maximum), rendered \"whole sine [radius].\" <b>3420201</b> is the radius-scaled value Craig computes. <i>18° 52½′</i> and the arc \"20\" are kept as printed. <b>\"IΛ7\":</b> a garbled numeral in the first demonstration (the context — <i>kil</i> = 30, the angles summing to 120° in triangle <i>efg</i> — requires the angle <i>gfi</i> = 30°); the printed <i>IΛ7</i> (apparently a corrupted \"30\") is kept verbatim in the Source block and not silently corrected. <b>Crux named: the figure value <i>IΛ7</i>.</b></li><li><b><i>tanquam amias tiliae nostrae μνημόσυνον</b></i> — <b>textual crux.</b> <i>amias tiliae</i> yields no Latin sense and is read as a corruption of <b><i>amicitiae nostrae</b></i> (\"of our friendship\"), giving \"as a memorial, as it were, of our friendship\" — coherent with the immediately preceding pledge to share his \"secrets\" with Tycho. The printed <i>amias tiliae</i> is kept verbatim in the Source block; the emendation is signalled in the translation. <b>Crux named: <i>amias tiliae</i> → <i>amicitiae</i>.</b></li><li><b>Court news.</b> <i>Sereniss. Rex noster … cum Sereniss. Regina nostra</i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> and his queen, <b>Anne of Denmark</b>: James sailed to Norway/Denmark in late 1589 to fetch Anne (whose autumn crossing had been storm-driven back), wintering at Kronborg/Copenhagen and returning in spring 1590 — exactly the \"departure beyond expectation\" and hoped-for spring return Craig describes. The Danish sojourn is the occasion of the whole letter (Craig angling, via Tycho, for the post of queen's physician).</li><li><b><i>D. Andream Kragium / D. Kragio</b></i> — <b>Andreas Krag (Anders Krag)</b>, a Danish physician then attending Queen Anne's retinue; Craig (using the near-homonym to his own name without comment) hopes to succeed him if Krag does not stay on, and asks Tycho to commend him to Krag and to Tycho's \"lord brother\" (<i>Domino fratre tuo</i>, presumably <b>Steen Brahe</b> or another of Tycho's brothers).</li><li><b>Eclipse method.</b> <i>organa … artifices</i> = <b>instruments … craftsmen</b> (protocol §5.2): Craig laments having neither trustworthy instruments nor instrument-makers, unlike Tycho. He holds that the <i>momenta</i> (moments) of an eclipse's beginning, middle, and end should be fixed from observed <b>altitudes of fixed stars</b> through a known instrument — altitude being a sensory first principle (<i>principium</i>), not a quantity to be demonstrated from others — <i>praeter visualem Diametrum</i> (\"apart from the visual [apparent] diameter\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Edinburgi pridie Calend. Martij</i> = \"Given at <b>Edinburgh</b>, on the day before the Kalends of March\" = <b>28 February</b> (1590 being a common year). Scotland reckoned by the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b>; the date is O.S. The dataset label (\"1590. 28. februar. Edinburgh\") agrees with the dateline; the year 1590 is not in the dateline itself but is supplied by the catalogue and by the James-VI/Anne-of-Denmark context. Roman-calendar idiom kept and glossed per §3.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (before the signature), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 242 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Four defects corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII pp.240-241: a garbled marginal line-number 'IΛ7' fused into the geometric proof text removed (both angles read simply '60'); the corrupted present-plural 'suppeditantur' restored to the future-singular 'suppeditabitur' (a promise of recompense, not a claim about compounding errors); a dropped footnote (Dreyer's footnote 1, p.240, naming Rheinhold's Canon printed at Tübingen 1554 by the heirs of Morhard) restored in place in both Latin and English; and the line-break-hyphenated 'ami-citiae' (mangled to 'amias tiliae') restored to 'amicitiae'. English corrected at the three places where sense was affected; the English for the amicitiae fix needed no change as it already carried the right sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the epistolary frame, the court-news, and the queen's-physician request are rendered securely. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02) after Dreyer collation</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII pp. 240–242, from cod. Vindob. lat. 9737): (1) <i>fecundus</i> = <i>secundus</i> is corroborated — Dreyer's own text once prints <i>secundus</i> for the same word; (2) the corrupt figure-label <i>IΛ7</i> is reproduced by Dreyer too, and <b>gfi = 30° is forced by the geometry</b>, not editorially supplied; (3) <i>amias tiliae</i> → <i>amicitiae</i> is sound on sense but stands uncorrected in Dreyer's sole witness. Each crux is kept verbatim in the Source block; all three are now characterised against the edition of record.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 239-242 (the slice's nominal p.243 was also read and confirmed to belong to the following Tycho-Ranzau letter, not this one). Checked opening/closing lines, all proper names (Fuller, Aristoteles, Rheticus, Krag), numbers (60, 30, 120, 3420201, 18 52 1/2, arcus 20), and the postscript. Found four TEXT_ERROR-class departures of source_text from Dreyer, two of which (the fabricated 'IΛ7' value and the mis-redirected Rheinhold-canon footnote) materially change what a scholar would take away.</p><p><b>4 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our Latin corrupts Dreyer's future-singular 'suppeditabitur' (it will be repaid to us with interest, i.e. Craig promising to reward Tycho generously for corrections) into a present-plural 'suppeditantur'; the English then compounds this by inventing a bracketed gloss ('the errors are passed on, compounded') with no counterpart in Dreyer, reversing the sense from a friendly promise of recompense into a nonsense claim about errors accumulating. Dreyer prints: <i>fique error fuberit, nobis cum fenore fuppeditabitur (p.240, line 5)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Dreyer's sentence states both angles gfi and bfe equal 60 ('uterque ... et ... erit 60'). Our source_text fused the printed marginal line-number '15' into the sentence as garbled 'IΛ7', and the English then literally translates this artifact as if it were a real geometric value ('gfi will be IΛ7'), fabricating a nonsensical quantity a mathematically literate reader would immediately flag. Dreyer prints: <i>erit kil 30 et uterque gfi et bfe erit 60 (p.240, line ~13, the '15' after 'gfi' is Dreyer's marginal line-count marker, not text)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer prints a genuine footnote here naming the sine canon Craig uses (Rheinhold's, printed at Tubingen in 1554 by the heirs of Morhard, inserted in the Tables of Directions) - a proper name, place, and date. Our source_text drops this footnote's content entirely and instead attaches its asterisk marker to a differently-located, unrelated postscript ('Si quid singulare in chymicis...', actually printed beside the signature on p.242), misleading the reader about what was recovered versus what remains missing. Dreyer prints: <i>footnote 1, p.240: 'Canon, quo ipse utor, Rheinoldi est excusus Tubingae anno 1554 apud heredes Morhardi, tabulis directionum insertus.'</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text mangles the line-break-hyphenated word 'ami-citiae' (amicitiae, friendship) into 'amias tiliae', a TEXT_ERROR versus Dreyer. The English translator correctly guessed the intended sense ('of our friendship') despite the corrupted source, so no defect propagates into the translation itself. Dreyer prints: <i>tu haec tanquam ami-citiae nostrae μνημόσυνον boni consule (p.241, word split across a line-break hyphen)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All four defects verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.239-241 page images (not the OCR text layer): the geometric proof (p.240 l.14), suppeditabitur (p.240 l.5), footnote 1 (p.240, bottom), and the ami-citiae line break (p.241 ll.23-24) all read exactly as the audit's Dreyer claim states. No other part of the letter was touched; every changed word is accounted for in outcomes above.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15891025001", "next": "brahe-t_15911114001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15891025001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15911114001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports hearing from Fuller about Tycho's reception and his reply to Craig's first letter, and declares willingness to yield to Tycho's demonstrations if they prove sound.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900228001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900306001", "d": "1590-03-06", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Dronning Sophie", "ck": "Dronning Sophie", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Queen Sophie → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 6 March 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1589–92, fol. 70 f. (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF ETT AFF HANS BØRNN ANRØRENNDIS VT SEQUITUR</p><p>Sophia etc.</p><p>WOR gunst thilfornn. Wider, att ßom j wnderdannigst lader giffue thilkiennde, att ßom j epther thennd sthormegthigste hogbornne første och herris her Fr[e]drich thend annden, Danmarckis, Norgis etc. konning, Wor kierre sallig affganngnne here och gemhals begierre, epther thennd naade och begaffuede forstanndtt, ßom j mett synderlig flid och sthuderinng, jcke wden stor omkostning, haffuer forhuerffuitt wdj thennd lofflige astronomiae konnst, haffuer paa thennd jnßel Huen anretthitt och fulddreffuitt enn ahnßehelig bygning, och ther wdinnden ßynnderlig ny och fornemme jnstrumennther thill ßamme lofflig astro[no]miae konnstis jnstauration och forbedrinng haffuer forhuerffuitt och ther thill forordnnitt, saa att hogbemelte Wor kierre ßalig herre haffuer haffdt tthill ßlig ethers flitthig forneme och lofflig konnst en synderlig gefallenn och welbehag, och epther att ßamme lofflige konnst er aff ether ther paa lannditt anretthitt och fuldkommitt, haffuer j althiid [ønsket], att epther ethers dødtt och affganng enn aff ethers egnne wdj synderlighed, ßom wdj samme lofflig konnst kunnde findis schickelig och dueligh, motthe ther thill forordnnis, och ßamme jnnßell mett hues ther paa anretthett, stiffthett och fuldkommitt er, niude och beholde, och therforre haffuer haffd wdj achtt och forßett, hoes hogbemelte Wor kierre sallige herre att wille paa thett wnnderdannigst forhuerffuitt Hanns Kierlighetz thilladellße, att therßom nogen aff ethers egne børnn kunde kiendis ther thill duelig och skickeligh, thennd tha for andre ßamme jnßell Huenn mett hues ther anretthitt er att schulle werre nest att bekomme, och therfor er aff Oß wnnderdannigst begierrendis, att Wij naadigst wille bekiennde och ßige hueß Wij haffue hørtt aff hogbemelte Wor kierre sallige herre om ßamme ethers forßett och menning. Tha wille Wij paa ßlige ethers wnnderdannigste begierre naadigst jcke forholde, att Wij weed Oß naadigst att erindre och er aldellis wittherlig, att Wij haffue hørtt aff hogbemelte Wor kierre sallige herre, nogenn thiid for Hans Kierlighedtz dødelig affganng, attj Hannß Kierlighet thett schall haffue forgiffuitt att wille jnstituere enn aff ethers egnne børnn, ßom schulle findis schichelig och duelig wdj ßamme lofflig astronomie konnst, huilke Wij well jnndnu kannd thilßinde føre och erachthe, och haffue hørtt aff hogbemelte Wor kierre sallige herre, och wille ßligtt paa ethers wnnderdannigst anlangende ether naadigst jcke forennttholde, och althiid ellers mett ald gunst och naade ether werre beuogenn.</p><p>Befallendis etc.<br>Actum Haffniae thend 6. martij anno 1590.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter, one concerning his children, as follows:</b></p><p>Sophie, etc.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, as you most humbly cause it to be made known — that, in accordance with the desire of the most mighty, high-born prince and lord, lord Frederik the Second, King of Denmark, Norway, etc., our dear departed lord and consort of blessed memory; and in accordance with the grace and the gifted understanding that you, with singular diligence and study, [and] not without great cost, have acquired in the laudable art of astronomy — you have set up and carried through, upon that island Hven, a considerable building, and have therein acquired and appointed singular new and excellent instruments for the renewal [instauration] and improvement of that same laudable art of astronomy, so that our aforementioned dear lord of blessed memory had toward such diligent, excellent, and laudable art of yours a singular liking and good pleasure; and inasmuch as that same laudable art has been set up and brought to completion by you there on the land [i.e. on Hven], you have always [wished] that, after your death and decease, one of your own [children] in particular — such as might be found fit and able in that same laudable art — might be appointed thereto, and might enjoy and keep that same island, together with whatever has been established, founded, and completed upon it; and you have therefore had it in mind and intent to wish to obtain, in the most humble manner, from our aforementioned dear lord of blessed memory His Love's permission, that if any of your own children might be found able and fit thereto, that one should then, before [i.e. in preference to] others, be the next to obtain that same island Hven with whatever has been set up [there]; and it is therefore most humbly desired of us that we would graciously acknowledge and declare what we have heard from our aforementioned dear lord of blessed memory concerning that same intent and meaning of yours. Then upon such most humble desire of yours we would graciously not withhold [the reply] that we graciously call to mind, and it is entirely known to us, that we have heard from our aforementioned dear lord of blessed memory, some while before His Love's mortal decease, that His Love is said to have declared that he would institute one of your own children, such as might be found fit and able in that same laudable art of astronomy — which [matter] we can indeed still recall to mind and bear in remembrance, and have heard from our aforementioned dear lord of blessed memory; and we would, upon your most humble petition, graciously not withhold the same from you, and would in all else always be inclined toward you with all favour and grace.</p><p>Commending [you to God], etc.<br>Done at Copenhagen, the 6th of March, the year 1590.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> (1557–1631), queen dowager of Denmark, widow of <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 4 April 1588). The letter is a chancery-register entry from her own register (<i>Dronning Sophies tegnelser</i>), written during the minority of her son <b>Christian IV</b> (b. 1577; crowned 1596), when she was active in royal business. She had a personal interest in astronomy and astrology and had visited Tycho on Hven.</li><li><b><i>Wor kierre sallig affganngnne here och gemhals</b></i> — \"our dear departed lord and consort of blessed memory,\" i.e. <b>Frederik II</b>, who in 1576 had granted Tycho the island of <b>Hven</b> (<i>Huen / jnßel Huen</i>, Latin <i>Hvenna / Venusia</i>). The whole letter is Sophie's response to a petition (<i>supplication</i>) from Tycho, asking her to confirm, from her own memory, a verbal undertaking of the late king.</li><li><b>Document type &amp; rubric.</b> A chancery-register copy; the <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…HANS BØRNN ANRØRENNDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning his children, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2, exactly as in the Frederik II compass-letter of 1584. <i>ANRØRENNDIS</i> = <i>anrørendes</i> (\"concerning, touching\"). <i>Sophia etc.</i> abbreviates the full royal style.</li><li><b>Substance.</b> The petition concerns the <b>hereditary succession to Hven and Uraniborg</b>: Tycho asks the dowager to attest that Frederik II had verbally promised that, after Tycho's death, one of Tycho's own children — if found <i>schickelig och dueligh</i> (\"fit and able\") in astronomy — should have first claim to the island and its establishment (the buildings and instruments). Sophie confirms she distinctly remembers hearing the late king say so, \"some while before his mortal decease.\" The undertaking was never honoured: after Tycho left Denmark (1597) Hven reverted to the crown.</li><li><b><i>astronomiae konnst … jnstauration och forbedrinng</b></i> — \"the art of astronomy … its renewal [<i>instauration</i>] and improvement.\" <i>jnstauration</i> is a direct Latin loan (<i>instauratio</i>) and is Tycho's own programmatic term (protocol §5.1: <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> = \"the renewal of astronomy,\" <i>astronomia instaurata</i>); kept as <b>renewal [instauration]</b>. <i>konnst</i> here = \"art / science / skill,\" the period sense, not modern \"art.\"</li><li><b><i>jnstrumennther</b></i> — \"instruments,\" the Uraniborg astronomical instruments (protocol §5.2). The Danish uses the Latin loan directly.</li><li><b><i>Hanns Kierlighet / Hans Kierlighedtz</b></i> — \"His Love / His Love's\": the chancery honorific <i>Hans Kærlighed</i> (German <i>Seine Liebden</i>, Latin <i>Dilectio Sua</i>), a formal third-person style used between and of high princes. Rendered literally as <b>His Love</b> and flagged here; it denotes the late king, not affection in the ordinary sense.</li><li><b><i>begaffuede forstanndtt</b></i> — \"gifted understanding\" (<i>begavet forstand</i>), i.e. natural talent / endowed intelligence.</li><li><b>Honorifics &amp; register.</b> Throughout, the queen writes of herself in the royal plural (<i>Wij</i> \"We,\" <i>Oß</i> \"us\") and addresses Tycho with the deferential second-person plural (<i>j / ether / ethers</i> \"you / you / your\"), while Tycho's own act is repeatedly marked <i>wnderdannigst / wnnderdannigste</i> (\"most humbly / most humble\"), the subject-to-sovereign register; preserved. <i>WOR gunst thilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i>, \"Our favour beforehand\" (the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1590).</b> Heavy German-influenced spelling (<i>sch-, w-, -tt, ß, doubled n/l</i>) that can fool automatic language detection — reading-verified as <b>Danish</b>, not German (protocol §2). Selected forms: <i>Wider</i> = <i>Vider</i> (\"Know ye\"); <i>j</i> = <i>I</i> (\"you,\" pl.); <i>epther</i> = <i>efter</i> (\"according to / after\"); <i>sthormegthigste</i> = <i>stormægtigste</i> (\"most mighty\"); <i>hogbornne</i> = <i>højbårne</i> (\"high-born\"); <i>gemhals</i> = <i>gemal(s)</i> (\"consort\"); <i>forhuerffuitt</i> = <i>forhvervet</i> (\"acquired / obtained\"); <i>anretthitt</i> = <i>anrettet</i> (\"set up / established\"); <i>fulddreffuitt / fuldkommitt</i> = <i>fuldrevet / fuldkommet</i> (\"carried through / completed\"); <i>ahnßehelig</i> = <i>anselig</i> (\"considerable, imposing\"); <i>gefallenn och welbehag</i> = <i>gefallen og velbehag</i> (German-loan doublet, \"liking and good pleasure\"); <i>schickelig och dueligh</i> = <i>skikkelig og dygtig</i> (\"fit and able\"); <i>forordnnis</i> = <i>forordnes</i> (\"be appointed\"); <i>niude och beholde</i> = <i>nyde og beholde</i> (\"enjoy and keep\"); <i>forßett och menning</i> = <i>forsæt og mening</i> (\"intent and meaning\"); <i>erachthe</i> = <i>agte / erakte</i> (\"bear in mind, deem\"); <i>forennttholde / forholde</i> = <i>forholde</i> (\"withhold\"); <i>beuogenn</i> = <i>bevågen</i> (\"favourably inclined\").</li><li><b><i>[ønsket]</b></i> — at <i>haffuer j althiid [ønsket]</i> the source/edition supplies the verb \"[wished]\" in brackets, the construction otherwise lacking its main verb; the bracketed editorial supplement is carried into the translation as <b>[wished]</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Haffniae thend 6. martij anno 1590</i> = \"Done at <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), the 6th of March, the year 1590\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar; protocol §3). <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5. <i>Befallendis etc.</i> (= <i>befalendes</i>, \"commending [you to God / to God's protection]\") is the closing-formula head, abbreviated in the register.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the substance (the succession-to-Hven petition and Sophie's confirmation of Frederik II's verbal promise) is clear. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the single editorially-supplied verb <i>[ønsket]/[wished]</i>, on which the long subordinate clause hinges; and (2) the formal honorific <i>Hans Kærlighed</i> \"His Love,\" kept literal. No genuine crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.51-53 as page images. Letter no.80 runs from the bottom of p.51 through p.52 (Latin argument header 'Sophia, vidua regis Friderici Secundi...' matches the packet's own framing of the letter's subject). Collated the opening ('Sophia'/'WOR gunst thilfornn'), the King Frederik II succession/inheritance-of-Hven clause, and the closing formula and date (Actum Haffniae thend 6. martij anno 1590) against the printed page. source_text matches Dreyer's Danish closely; published_english accurately renders the convoluted chancery syntax, including the conditional inheritance clause for one of Tycho's children, with no additions or dropped content.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1589-92, fol. 70 f.."], "th": {"label": "Dronning Sophie", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15900718001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's work in astronomy and the construction of Uraniborg under the late King Frederick's patronage; the letter's rubric identifies it as concerning Tycho's children.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900306001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900401001", "d": "1590-04-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 1 April 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO WILHELMO, HASSIAe LANDGRAVIO, COMITI IN CATZENELNBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO S. P.</p><p>QVAe ad T. Celsit. hoc tempore perscriberem, Princeps Illustrissime, etsi non multa occurrerent, simulque vererer, ne meis importunis literis T. Celsit. Reipub. negotijs occupatiorem molestarem (nam et semel atque iterum iamdudum literas, quibus nihil responsum est, transmisi, vnde T. Celsit. alijs curis auocari, neque his vacare collegi.) Attamen, quoniam nouum quoddam Phoenomenon sese proxime antecedentibus diebus, coelitus conspiciendum exhibuit: Cometes videlicet aspectu non adeo magnus, sed pallido et subobscuro vultu lucens: Sciens T. Celsit. huius apparentias cognoscendi cupidam esse (ex quo in vltimo, ante quinquennium viso, simile quid a me expetitum sit) non supersedendum duxi, quin aliquid literarum, vel hoc saltem nomine ad T. Celsit. mitterem, veluti paulo ante T. Celsit. Mathematico scribens, quando nonnullas in eodem Cometa obseruationes continuassem, me facturum spopondi. Circa illudipsum enim tempus Cometa apparere coepit, et expectandam erat, donec ratas aliquot in eo animaduersiones nancisci licuit. Ne igitur res diutius differatur, quae hactenus sedulâ denotatione explorata habeo, T. Celsit. communicanda duxi, vt cum sui Mathematici in eodem Cometa obseruatis (nec enim dubito eundem etiam a vobis animaduersum esse) conferre queat. Occasio autem qua illum primum conspeximus, erat huiusmodi. Die 23. Februarij, cum Veneris Stella terris descendendo appropinquans, circa maximam a Sole remotionem versaretur, nactus post quatriduanas aëris obscuritates, exoptatam aliquam serenitatem, illius obseruationi sedulo attendendum censui. Siquidem ex dato ipsius loco, in remotissima, respectu Solis, tam Vespertina, quam Eoa dispositione, ad Apogaei atque Eccentricitatis cognitionem perueniri posse, prout quoque a Ptolemaeo et Copernico factitatum est, T. Celsit. non ignorat. Non paucas igitur eiuscemodi a Sole digressiones maximas, matutinas atque vespertinas, iam antea aliquoties exploraui, quibus et hanc adiungere satagebam; vixque bis vel ter Venus iam Instrumentis denotata erat, vbi ex improuiso Stellam quandam subobscuram, inter Andromedae atque Arietis Asterismos, iuxta Piscem Borealem sese praeter solitum ostendentem, cum nullam eiuscemodi illic esse oportere satis perspectum haberem, animaduerteremus, quae etiam subinde radiolos quosdam ad verticem exerere cernebatur. Neque enim Crepusculum vespertinum iam satis praeterierat, vt Stellae minus conspicuae euidenter discernerentur. Coepi itaque statim cum meis Studiosis hoc insolitum Phoenomenon per Instrumenta demetiri, et vix elapso horae quadrante, deprehendi ex motu eius, quem vel tantillo tempore sensibiliter alterabat, tam Declinatione, quam Distantia a Fixis nonnihil variata, non genuinum, sed ascititium quoddam e Cometarum numero Sidus existere, vtut cauda illa tenuior, quam post Crepusculi finem promptius monstrabat, tunc vix oculis perciperetur. Mitto autem T. Celsit. seorsim in adiecta charta breuem quandam eiusdem Cometae descriptionem, e meis obseruationibus, quas adeptus sum, ex tempore et festinanter concinnatam, quam cum vestris aduersionibus conferre licebit. Rogoque, vt T. Celsit. hanc nostram voluntatem boni consulat. Veneris Stella toto Februario et Martio interdiu, etiam in ipso Meridie apparuit, quod in posterum quoque pro maiori Aprilis parte futurum iudico. Si itaque T. Celsit. Mathematicus illi obseruandae, tam interdiu, quam noctu operam sedulam nauauit, admodum oportunam, de quinis illis Minutis, quibus in Longitudinibus Stellarum differimus, decernendi arripuit ansam. Ex quo tam Venus quam Sol adeo sublimes obseruationi patuerint, vt Refractionum impedimentis vix implicarentur. Nec dubito, quin vel inde, adhibita solerti circumspectione colligere detur, me Longitudinem Fixarum nequaquam iusto plus anticipâsse. Constitueram T. Celsit. de his omnibus prius scribere, per Mercatores nostrates Francofurdum euntes, sed accidit, vt circa eosdem dies, quibus illi itineri se committebant, Serenissimus Scotorum Rex, cum suis Consiliarijs reliquoque Nobilium Comitatu, me in hac Insula inuisere dignatus sit, et exinde quasi quotidianos hospites habuerim. Hincque factum est, quod tardius atque constitueram, hasce mittere cogar. Quae tamen mora non sine faenore accidit, nam eo plus temporis concedebatur, per interualla, ea, quae in dicto Cometa prius subito exararam, plenius, et enucleatius recognoscendi, vt non dubitem, haec ipsa, quae mitto, vtut paucula et breuia, omnimode recte sibi constare, atque apparentijs istius Cometae apprime esse analoga. Sed cum non ignorem T. Celsit. pro sua clementi et candida erga nos affectione, hanc dilatiunculam equi bonique accipere, nolo in eâ excusandâ plus occupari, quam forte opus sit, sed me meaque omnia T. Celsit. commendata cupio.</p><p>Vale.<br>Vraniburgo, Calendis Aprilis, Anno 1590.<br>T. Celsit.<br>Addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda: his most gracious Prince and Lord — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>As to what I might write at length to Your Highness at this time, Most Illustrious Prince — although not much offered itself, and at the same time I feared that with my importunate letters I might trouble Your Highness, who are rather much occupied with the business of the commonwealth (for once and again, some while ago now, I sent letters to which no reply has come, from which I gathered that Your Highness is called away by other cares and has no leisure for these things) — nevertheless, since a certain new phenomenon has shown itself in these last few days to be seen in the heavens, namely a comet not so very large to the sight, but shining with a pale and somewhat dim face, and knowing that Your Highness is desirous to learn of its appearances (from the fact that with the last one, seen five years ago, something similar was sought of me), I judged that I ought not to forbear from sending Your Highness at least some letter, if only on this account — just as, writing a little while ago to Your Highness's Mathematician, I gave my pledge that I would do so, once I had carried on some observations on this same comet. For it was about that very time that the comet began to appear, and it had to be waited out until it was possible to obtain a few settled observations upon it. So then, lest the matter be put off any longer, I judged that what I have thus far explored by careful recording was to be communicated to Your Highness, that you may be able to compare it with the observations made by your Mathematician on the same comet (for I do not doubt that it was noticed by you also). Now the occasion on which we first caught sight of it was of this kind. On the 23rd of February, when the star Venus, drawing near the earth as it descended, was about its greatest distance from the Sun, having obtained — after a four-day obscurity of the air — some longed-for clear weather, I judged that careful attention should be given to observing it. For from its given place, when at its greatest distance with respect to the Sun, in both an evening and a morning disposition, one can arrive at the knowledge of the apogee and of the eccentricity, just as was done by Ptolemy and Copernicus too, as Your Highness is not unaware. Not a few greatest elongations of this kind from the Sun, both morning and evening, I had therefore already explored several times before, and I was busy to add this one too to them; and Venus had hardly yet been recorded by the instruments two or three times when, unexpectedly, we noticed a certain somewhat dim star showing itself contrary to custom between the asterisms of Andromeda and Aries, near the Northern Fish — since I held it well enough ascertained that no such star ought to be there — which was also seen from time to time to put forth certain little rays toward the zenith. For the evening twilight had not yet sufficiently passed for the less conspicuous stars to be clearly distinguished. I therefore began at once, with my students, to measure out this unusual phenomenon by the instruments, and scarcely had a quarter of an hour elapsed when I perceived, from its motion — which it appreciably altered even in so brief a time, with both its declination and its distance from the fixed stars somewhat varied — that it was no genuine star, but a certain adventitious one, of the number of comets, even though that thinner tail, which it showed more readily after the end of twilight, was then scarcely perceived by the eyes. But I send to Your Highness separately, on an enclosed sheet, a certain brief description of this same comet, drawn up offhand and in haste from the observations I have obtained, which it will be possible to compare with your own notes. And I ask that Your Highness take this our goodwill in good part. The star Venus appeared all through February and March in the daytime, even at midday itself, which I judge will be so in time to come too, for the greater part of April. If, therefore, Your Highness's Mathematician has devoted careful labour to observing it, both by day and by night, he has seized a most opportune handle for deciding about those five minutes by which we differ in the longitudes of the stars. Since both Venus and the Sun lay open to observation so high up that they would scarcely be entangled in the impediments of refractions. And I do not doubt that even from this, with diligent circumspection applied, it may be possible to gather that I have by no means anticipated the longitude of the fixed stars more than is right. I had resolved to write to Your Highness about all these things sooner, by way of our merchants going to Frankfurt; but it happened that, about the same days on which they were committing themselves to that journey, the Most Serene King of the Scots, with his Councillors and the rest of his retinue of nobles, deigned to visit me on this island, and that thereafter I had, as it were, daily guests. And hence it came about that I am compelled to send this present letter later than I had resolved. Yet that delay did not occur without interest [returned], for so much the more time was granted, in the intervals, for recognizing more fully and more thoroughly the things which I had earlier scratched down hurriedly on the said comet — so that I do not doubt that these very things which I send, though few and brief, are altogether rightly consistent in themselves, and exceedingly well matched to the appearances of that comet. But since I am not unaware that Your Highness, in keeping with your gracious and candid affection toward us, takes this little delay as fair and good, I do not wish to occupy myself in excusing it more than perhaps need be, but I desire myself and all my affairs to be commended to Your Highness.</p><p>Farewell.<br>From Uraniborg, on the Kalends of April [1 April], the year 1590.<br>Your Highness's<br>Most devoted,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Wilhelmus, Hassiae Landgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the \"astronomer prince,\" with whose Kassel observatory Tycho maintained his most important scientific correspondence. The titulature <i>Comiti in Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain et Nidda</i> names the subsidiary Hessian comital domains (Katzenelnbogen, Diez, Ziegenhain, Nidda); the place-names are left in their as-printed forms.</li><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> (<i>Vraniburgum</i>) on the island of Hven.</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>T. Celsit.</i> = <i>Tua Celsitudo</i>, <b>Your Highness</b> (§5.5); rendered consistently throughout. The subscription <i>Addictissimus</i> = \"Most devoted.\"</li><li><b>The phenomenon.</b> <i>nouum quoddam Phoenomenon … Cometes</i> — <b>a comet</b>, that of late February 1590, first detected by Tycho on the night of the Venus observation. <i>non genuinum, sed ascititium … Sidus … e Cometarum numero</i> — \"no genuine star, but a certain adventitious one, of the number of comets\": the diagnosis turns on the body's measurable proper motion in a quarter-hour. <i>radiolos … ad verticem exerere</i> = \"to put forth little rays toward the zenith\"; <i>cauda … tenuior</i> = \"a thinner tail.\"</li><li><b><i>in vltimo, ante quinquennium viso</b></i> — \"the last one, seen five years ago\": the <b>comet of 1585</b>, on which the Landgrave had earlier sought Tycho's observations. (\"Five years\" is loose for late 1585 → early 1590.)</li><li><b><i>T. Celsit. Mathematico</b></i> — Your Highness's Mathematician: <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (protocol §5.6), the Kassel mathematician and Tycho's chief observational interlocutor in this period.</li><li><b>Venus / greatest elongation.</b> <i>circa maximam a Sole remotionem</i> = \"about its greatest distance from the Sun\" = <b>greatest elongation</b> (§5.1); <i>in remotissima … tam Vespertina, quam Eoa dispositione</i> = \"at its greatest distance … in both an evening and a morning disposition\" (<i>Eous</i> = of the dawn, the morning star). Tycho states the standard method — that from Venus's place at greatest elongation one derives the <b>apogee</b> (<i>Apogaeum</i>) and <b>eccentricity</b> (<i>Eccentricitas</i>) of its orbit, \"as was done by Ptolemy and Copernicus too.\" <i>digressiones maximas</i> = \"greatest elongations\" (here digression in the technical sense). The daytime visibility of Venus near midday is noted as an observational opportunity.</li><li><b>Star positions.</b> <i>inter Andromedae atque Arietis Asterismos, iuxta Piscem Borealem</i> = \"between the asterisms of Andromeda and Aries, near the Northern Fish\" (<i>Piscis Borealis</i>, the northern fish of Pisces). <i>Asterismus</i> = \"asterism\" (constellation-figure).</li><li><b>Technical terms (protocol §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>Instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b>; <i>Declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>Distantia a Fixis</i> → \"distance from the fixed stars\"; <i>Fixae</i> → <b>the fixed stars</b>; <i>Refractiones</i> → <b>refractions</b>; <i>Crepusculum (vespertinum)</i> → <b>(evening) twilight</b>; <i>Meridies</i> → \"midday / meridian\"; <i>zenith</i> (<i>verticem</i>) kept/named per §5.1. <i>Studiosi</i> rendered \"students\" (Tycho's resident assistants at Uraniborg).</li><li><b>The five-minute discrepancy.</b> <i>de quinis illis Minutis, quibus in Longitudinibus Stellarum differimus</i> = \"those five minutes by which we differ in the longitudes of the stars\": the standing <b>5-arcminute disagreement</b> between Tycho's and Kassel's stellar <b>longitudes</b> (§5.1). Tycho argues that the high-altitude daytime observations of Venus and the Sun — being \"scarcely entangled in the impediments of refractions\" — afford a test that, he expects, will confirm \"that I have by no means anticipated [overstated] the longitude of the fixed stars more than is right.\" His epistemic hedge (<i>Nec dubito, quin … colligere detur</i> \"I do not doubt that … it may be possible to gather\") is preserved, not sharpened into a claim of proof.</li><li><b>The royal visit.</b> <i>Serenissimus Scotorum Rex … me in hac Insula inuisere dignatus sit</i> = \"the Most Serene King of the Scots … deigned to visit me on this island\": <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who visited Hven on 20 March 1590 while in Denmark for his marriage to Anne of Denmark — the cause of the letter's delay. <i>per Mercatores nostrates Francofurdum euntes</i> = \"by our merchants going to Frankfurt\" (the Frankfurt fair as the postal channel to Hesse).</li><li><b><i>non sine faenore</b></i> — literally \"not without interest\" (financial metaphor): the delay paid a dividend, in extra time to perfect the comet observations. Rendered with the metaphor kept (\"without interest [returned]\").</li><li><b><i>adiecta charta</b></i> — \"an enclosed sheet\": the separate comet-observation summary accompanying the letter, now lost from / not part of this text.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Vraniburgo, Calendis Aprilis, Anno 1590</i> = \"From Uraniborg, on the <b>Kalends of April (1 April)</b>, the year 1590\" (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The Roman-calendar idiom <i>Calendis Aprilis</i> is the 1st of April; this agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1590. 1. april. Uraniborg</i>), so no date discrepancy arises. The Roman idiom is glossed per protocol §3.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical content (greatest elongation → apogee/eccentricity; the 5′ longitude dispute; the comet's motion-based diagnosis) is on firm ground. Minor residual softness: <i>quasi quotidianos hospites habuerim</i> is rendered \"I had, as it were, daily guests\" (the <i>quasi</i> hedges frequency, not identity); and <i>anticipâsse</i> in <i>me Longitudinem Fixarum … anticipâsse</i> is taken in the sense \"anticipated / set too far forward (overstated),\" the reading that fits Tycho's defence of his own longitudes against the 5′ gap — noted here rather than silently fixed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (one of the 49 without a Dreyer slice), so per brief this is capped at grade B and audited against source_text alone. Close read of the full Latin against the full English (salutation, comet narrative incl. the 23 February observation, the James VI/Scotland visit, the five-minutes-longitude discussion, and the closing date 'Calendis Aprilis 1590') found every proper name, number, and date faithfully rendered, no invented clauses, no dropped sentences, no flipped subjects, no hardened hedges. No defects found; grade capped at B for lack of Dreyer collation, not for any fault in the translation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15890221001", "next": "brahe-t_15900515001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870911001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900515001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports a new, pale comet now visible and sends observations, citing the Landgrave's known interest in such phenomena from the comet of five years before.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900401001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900402001", "d": "1590-04-02", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Henrik Rantzau — Uraniborg, 2 April 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.25</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE IMPRIMIS CONSPICUO, DOMINO HENRICO RANZOVIO, DOMINO DE BREITENBERGA ET REGIO IN HOLSATIA AC DITHMARSIA GUBERNATORI ETC. AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.</p><p>SERIUS ego quidem, Nobilissime mi RANZOVI, tuis iam respondeo literis, non tam mea culpa, quam quod de Insignibus Sturorum certior interea temporis fieri nequiverim. Licet enim diligentissime hincinde per sororem quoque meam SOPHIAM a Domina GURUILDA, relicta D. LAGONIS BRAHE vidua, id expiscari attentârim, votis tamen nostris nondum est satisfactum, ut et adiunctae indicabunt literae. Accessit et hoc, quod septimanis praeteritis hospitum, qui me invisebant, multitudine sim praepeditus, quo minus, uti volebam, tuae petitioni satisfacere vacârit. Ne vero adhuc te sine responso diutius detineam, non amplius rescriptione abstinendum duxi, licet nihil, quod ad Insignia illa attinet, adhuc compertum habeam. Ubi tamen aut ex Svecia vel aliunde rem ipsam cognovero, curabo, ut resciscas. Quod ad figuras libris praefigendas attinet, eas Buxo solummodo insculptas expeto, neque enim Typographus meus cupreus aptare novit. Typi WOLFFIANI meis non correspondent, praeterea eos carius aestimat atque Vitebergenses, ubi aptiores veneunt 20 floren. in centenar. ut de ijs iam plura agere non sit necessum. Et ut rem ipsam dicam, non admodum mihi stylo nunc est opus, donec Chartopaeum denuo acquisivero. Is enim, quem ad me misisti, Rostochium pro socio et veteramentis conquirendis ablegatus, acceptis praeter voluntatem meam a D. BRUCAeO sex Ioachimicis, Wismariam, illic se veteramenta collecturum simulans, abijt, posteaque veterator iste se subduxit. Idem quoque mihi accidit cum priore, qui a IOACHIMO BUCK Lubecensi pecuniam nactus nusquam deinceps comparuit. Scripsi lamdudum tibi de ijs, quae a me tum temporis, cum Calendarium Ranzovianum misisti, expetebas, sed colligo ex literis tuis, meas ad manus tuas non pervenisse. Ubi interea deliteant, ignoro. Plura per commodum otium scribam et conabor, quantum in me est, ut Insignium istorum Sturianorum delineatione potiaris. Vale, meaque, uti facis, promovere stude. Dabo vicissim operam, ut quae tibi accepta cognovero, ubi potuero, fiant.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgo 2. Aprilis Anno 90.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 243; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Literas quas adiunxi ad Illustriss. Principem Landgravium, si Casselas mitti procuraris, et alteram Pragam versus, rem feceris pergratam. Tunc etiam quae Lubecam et Hamburgum dirigendae veniunt.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most distinguished man, conspicuous above all for virtue and erudition, Master Henrik Rantzau, lord of Breitenburg and royal governor in Holstein and Dithmarschen, etc., his friend much to be honoured.</b></p><p>Rather late, indeed, my most noble Rantzau, do I now reply to your letter — not so much through my own fault as because in the meantime I have been unable to gain surer knowledge concerning the arms of the Stures. For although I have most diligently attempted to fish that out from here and there — through my sister Sophia too, from the Lady Gørvel, the widow left by Master Lage Brahe — our wishes have nonetheless not yet been satisfied, as the enclosed letter[s] will also show. There was added this too, that in the past weeks I have been hindered by the multitude of guests who were visiting me, so that I had less leisure than I wished to satisfy your request. But lest I keep you still longer without a reply, I judged that I ought not to abstain from writing any further, although as yet I have learned nothing that bears upon those arms. Whenever, however, I shall have learned the matter itself, whether from Sweden or from elsewhere, I shall see to it that you are informed. As regards the figures to be set at the front of the books, I want them engraved in boxwood only, for my typographer does not know how to fit copper [plates]. The Wolffian types do not match mine; besides, he prices them more dearly than the Wittenberg ones, where more suitable ones are sold at 20 florins the hundredweight — so that there is no need to deal further with these matters now. And to speak the matter plainly, I have no great need of a graving-tool [stylus] at present, until I shall have acquired a paper-maker again. For the one whom you sent to me, having been dispatched to Rostock to seek out a partner and old materials, and having received — against my will — six Joachim-dollars from Master Brucaeus, went off to Wismar, pretending that he would collect old materials there, and afterwards that old rogue stole away. The same thing also befell me with the earlier one, who, having got money from Joachim Buck of Lübeck, never afterwards appeared anywhere. I wrote to you long ago about those things which you were requesting from me at the time when you sent the Rantzovian Calendar, but I gather from your letter that mine did not reach your hands. Where they lie hidden meanwhile, I do not know. I shall write more at convenient leisure, and shall endeavour, so far as in me lies, that you may obtain a drawing of those arms of the Stures. Farewell, and continue, as you do, to advance my interests. I in turn shall give my effort that whatever I learn is welcome to you may, when I can, be brought about.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 2 April, the year [15]90.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> The letters which I have enclosed for the most illustrious Prince the Landgrave — if you would see to it that they are sent to Kassel, and the other one toward Prague — you will do a most welcome thing. And then likewise those that are to be directed to Lübeck and Hamburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> at Uraniborg → <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich (Henrik) Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), lord of <b>Breitenburg</b> and royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor / viceroy) of Schleswig-Holstein and Dithmarschen on behalf of the Danish crown — the great humanist patron, book-collector and astrological enthusiast with whom Tycho corresponded extensively. The address-formula's full titulature (<i>Domino de Breitenberga et regio … gubernatori</i>) is reproduced in full per protocol §2/§5.5.</li><li><b><i>Insignia Sturorum / Sturianorum</b></i> — \"the arms of the <b>Stures</b>,\" the heraldic coat of arms of the Swedish noble Sture family, on which Rantzau (an avid genealogist and armorial collector) had asked Tycho to procure information. Rendered \"arms\"; the heraldic, not military, sense. Tycho's answer is throughout a hedged non-result: he has <i>nihil … adhuc compertum</i> (\"learned nothing as yet\"), preserved as such — no sharpening to a positive finding.</li><li><b><i>soror mea Sophia</b></i> = <b>Sophie (Sophia) Brahe</b> (1559–1643), Tycho's learned younger sister, here acting as his intermediary in the genealogical inquiry.</li><li><b><i>Domina Guruilda, relicta D. Lagonis Brahe vidua</b></i> = the <b>Lady Gørvel</b> — Gørvel Faddersdatter Sparre — described as the widow left by <b>Master Lage Brahe</b>. <i>Relicta … vidua</i> is pleonastic in the Latin (\"the widow left behind by\"); rendered \"the widow left by Master Lage Brahe\" without trimming the doubling.</li><li><b><i>expiscari</b></i> — literally \"to fish out,\" kept as the live metaphor (\"fish that out\").</li><li><b><i>adiunctae … literae</b></i> — \"the enclosed letter[s].\" Latin <i>literae</i> is formally plural but regularly denotes a single letter; number left as the Latin gives it, with the alternative flagged here rather than silently singularised.</li><li><b>Printing matter.</b> <i>figuras libris praefigendas … Buxo … insculptas</i> = woodcut <b>figures</b> (illustrations/plates) to be set at the front of books, \"engraved in <b>boxwood</b> only\" — i.e. woodblocks, <i>buxus</i> being the standard fine engraving-wood. <i>Typographus meus cupreus aptare novit</i>: Tycho's own printer at Uraniborg \"does not know how to fit copper [plates]\" — i.e. cannot execute copperplate (intaglio) engraving. <i>aptare</i> = \"to fit/mount,\" kept literal.</li><li><b><i>Typi Wolffiani</b></i> — \"the <b>Wolffian</b> types,\" i.e. printing type from a founder/supplier named Wolff (most plausibly the Hamburg/Lübeck-area trade Rantzau had access to); identification not secured, so the epithet is kept as an adjective and flagged. <i>eos carius aestimat atque Vitebergenses</i> = \"he prices them more dearly than the <b>Wittenberg</b> ones.\" <i>20 floren. in centenar.</i> = \"20 florins the hundredweight (per <i>centenarius</i>),\" a price-by-weight for cast type; kept literal.</li><li><b><i>stylo … non est opus … donec Chartopaeum … acquisivero</b></i> — <i>stylus</i> here is the engraver's/graving tool (the means of producing the <i>figurae</i>), not handwriting style; rendered \"graving-tool [stylus]\" with the Latin flagged, since the bracketing of cause (no engraver) and effect (no paper-maker) shows Tycho means the physical apparatus of book-production. <i>Chartopaeus</i> (Gk. χαρτοποιός) = <b>paper-maker</b>; the Uraniborg paper-mill's workman.</li><li><b>The two absconding paper-makers.</b> <i>Is … Rostochium pro socio et veteramentis conquirendis ablegatus … sex Ioachimicis … Wismariam … abijt … veterator iste se subduxit</i>: the paper-maker Rantzau had sent, dispatched to <b>Rostock</b> to recruit a partner and gather <i>veteramenta</i> (<b>old materials</b> — i.e. rags/old paper-stock for pulp), pocketed <b>six Joachim-dollars</b> (<i>Ioachimici</i> = Joachimsthaler) from Brucaeus against Tycho's will, decamped to <b>Wismar</b>, and absconded. <i>veterator iste</i> (\"that old rogue\") puns on <i>veteramenta</i>; the wordplay (old-stock / old-hand-at-trickery) is noted, the insult rendered \"that old rogue.\" <i>D. Brucaeo</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1531–1593), professor of medicine and mathematics at Rostock, Tycho's correspondent there. <i>Idem … cum priore, qui a Ioachimo Buck Lubecensi pecuniam nactus</i>: the same had happened with an earlier paper-maker, who took money from <b>Joachim Buck of Lübeck</b> and vanished.</li><li><b><i>Calendarium Ranzovianum</b></i> — the <b>Rantzovian Calendar</b>, Rantzau's own <i>Calendarium</i> (an astrological-computational almanac he compiled and sent to Tycho). Kept as a title.</li><li><b><i>manu propria</b></i> — \"in his own hand,\" Tycho's autograph subscription; the witness is a chancery/archival register copy at Vienna (ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.25; see the §1 collation note below — the <i>9737</i> siglum belongs to the preceding Craig letter), so the <i>manu propria</i> describes the original, not the surviving copy.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgo 2. Aprilis Anno 90</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, 2 April [15]90\" (O.S.; Denmark kept Old Style until 1700). The dateline (2 April 1590) agrees with the dataset label; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Greeting / closing formulae.</b> No opening <i>salutem</i>; the letter opens directly after the address. <i>Vale … Iterum vale</i> = \"Farewell … Again, farewell\" (§5.5). <i>meaque … promovere stude</i> = \"continue … to advance my interests,\" the reciprocal-patronage register kept.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> MS-witness corrected to <b>cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.25</b> (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 243, assigns this letter to 10686²⁵; the <i>9737</i> siglum belongs to the preceding Craig letter).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (after \"manu propria\"), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 243 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the narrative (heraldic inquiry, printing arrangements, two thieving paper-makers) is unambiguous. Soft points, all flagged rather than smoothed: (i) the identity behind <i>Typi Wolffiani</i> (a named type-supplier \"Wolff\" — not securely identified); (ii) <i>adiunctae literae</i> singular vs. plural; (iii) <i>stylus</i> read as the engraver's graving-tool rather than literary \"style,\" on the strength of the surrounding production context.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 242-243, including the restored postscript on letters for the Landgrave/Kassel/Prague/Lübeck/Hamburg, which Dreyer does print immediately after the signature \"Tycho Brahe, manu propria\" -- confirming the working transcription's restoration is accurate. Verified all names (Gørvel/Guruilda, Lage Brahe, Brucaeus, Joachim Buck) and figures (six Joachim-dollars, 20 florins per hundredweight). Translation is accurate throughout with no fabrication, omission, hedge-loss, or mistranslation. Upgraded from the self-assigned B to A on the strength of full, clean collation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1590 (4 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/25-27", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964025"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15881221001", "next": "brahe-t_15901207001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for delay, reports the inquiry into the Stures' arms via his sister Sophia and Lady Gørvel still unsatisfied, and promises to write when resolved.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900402001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900418001", "d": "1590-04-18", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 18 April 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO, ET VIRTVTE, SAPIENTIA, INGENIOSA ERVDITAQVE DOCTRINA<br>CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, EQUITI<br>DANO, MATHEMATICORUM NOSTRO SECULO OMNIUM PRAeSTANTISSIMO, ETC.<br>DOMINO SUO CUM OBSERUANTIA COLENDO. S. P.</p><p>ASI, vt OVIDIVS canit, Carmina proueniunt animo deducta sereno, Nobilissime Excellentissimeque Domine TYCHO, multo magis profundissimae et subtilissimae Mathematicae Disputationes, quae cum Carminum exaratione non sunt comparandae, animum a perturbationibus liberrimum requirent. Non igitur rem aut a nostra amicitia alienam, aut excusatione omni, vt tu arguere videris, indignam commisissem, si responsione supersedissem. Qui enim maiores cruciatus aut dolores excogitari possunt, quam ego in Podagra iam vltra biennium, et in Calculo, hâc Hyeme, grauissimis omnium morbis, sustineo? Qui morbi hâc Hyeme alternatim me inuadentes, corpus meum tam debilitarunt, mentemque pene de statu suo praecipitarunt, vt ne literam quidem potuerim fereA exarare. Superiore autem aestate, quanquam praeter Podagram, languores continuos, et ab omni labore horrores in me depraehenderem, nesciebam tamen, qualem morbum mihi essent apportaturae et inflicturae insolitae illae lassitudines: Quod mihi iam acerbissimis doloribus et cruciatibus satis innotuit. Spiritus enim Podagricus (ita enim iam libet eum appellare) in tantam malitiam in me excreuit, vt non tantum vias vrinarias, et renes, verum etiam venas mesaraicasA arenâ rubeâ, et tartaro arctissime constiparit, adeo vt quamprimum comederim aut biberim, propter nutrimenti a naturâ attracti inhibitionem, et vrinae expulsionem, vehementissimos cruciatus et in mesaraicis, et in vijs vrinarijs, instar dolorum cholicorum sentiam, cibumque assumtum retinere in ventriculo propter nauseam et vomitum nequeamA. Et extant in me non obscura signa, quae eundem Spiritum in alijs quoque membris idem tentare non obscure arguunt. Tentaui Medicinam Galenicam, me curante eruditissimo Dn. Doct. BVTTERO, Medico Illustrissimi Principis nostri; verum sine omni successu. Cum ea vrinam pellere voluit, obstante tartaro, in vijs vrinarijs ante vesicam, vehementissimos cruciatus sustinui. Quid balnea quoque et insessiones possint, facile intelligis, cum propter Podagram pedes ipsis immittere prohibear; extremis autem membris non calefactis, intermedia vix debito modo calefiant. Quanquam herbarum virtutes licet tartarum fortassisA frangere possint, in aquam tamen eumA dissoluere non possuntA; quod hîc omnino necessarium est, si aut in intestina regurgitare, aut per vesicam abire transudando debeat, morbusque radicitus curari. Num etiam haec tanta spiritus Podagrici malitia possit ex fecibus Salem Petrae in me generare, eumque ad extremum gradum subtilitatis continuâ sublimatione perducere, adeoque mihi in momento vitam eripere, tibi expendendum relinquo, qui facile intelligis, quid notem. Hic enim Spiritus, cum fixae, vt scis, sit Naturae; reliqui autem Spiritus meliores, qui huius malitiam retundere possunt, sint volatiles, et per cogitationum intentiones, quibus me exuere nequeo, quotidie absumantur, an non in immensum virtutem suam augere, et tandem sibi solum totum imperiumA assumere, a nullo impeditus, poterit? Proinde ad sola Diuina merito me totum conuerterem, omissis Philosophicis, quanquam et haec Diuina sint. Attamen cum iam qualemqualem cruciatuum remissionem quodammodo sentiam, pro Amore in te meo, qui certe vulgaris non est, et pro nostra Amicitia intermittere non potui, quin aliquid saltem, quod mihi in his meis aerumnis in mentem venire possit, ad tuas Disputationes iam seorsim in Charta ad me missas, responderem.</p><p>Quod igitur COPERNICVM de triplici motu Terrae impugnando, priore loco de diurno motu affers; si Terra 24. horis circumuolui fingatur, fieri non posse, vt globus plumbeus ex altissima turri demissus, punctum Terrae infra se positum, perpendiculariter ad amussim contingat; cum illud propter velocissimum Terrae motum, sese necessario interea subducatA, globumque plumbeum post se relinquat: Hoc satis a COPERNICO enodatum et dissolutum est. Cadentium enim, et ascendentium, vt ipse ait, duplicem fateamur esse motum oportet Mundi comparatione, et omnino compositum ex recto, et circulari: Quandoquidem quae pondere suo deprimuntur, cum sint maxime terrea, non dubium, quin eandem seruent partes Naturam, quam suum totum. Id quod tibi multo minus dubium esse debet, cum tu ex meliore Philosophia scias, quod Natura Naturâ delectetur, Naturaque Naturam retineat. Si corporibus illis sui motus scientia est naturalis, vt tu vis, erit ea etiam partibus. Quemadmodum enim in alijs rebus naturalibus, verbi causa, partes Auri retinent Naturam totius, nec eo modo separatae a toto id, quod a Natura ipsis peculiariter et specifice insitumA erat, abijciunt: Ita et in his corporibus, quae motum, vt tu non inepteA vis, naturalem habent, partes primariam hanc insitam sibi praerogatiuam, et praecipuam ac specificam dignitatem non abijcient. Sed quid de casu rerum grauium solicitus esA, cum omnia, quae in superficie Terrae libera et a toto separata iacent, quinimo ipsa Turris, ex qua Globus plumbeus dimitteretur, ipsaque aedificia ruerent, atque a Terrae motu relinquerentur necesse esset, si partes non retinerent motum totius: quod quam sit contra Naturae Sapientiam, nemo non videt. Quod autem de Supputationibus affers, Terram ea ratione in vno secundo temporis scrupuloA reuolui debere in borealioribus ibi plagis sesquicentum passus maiores proxime: Quaeso vtrum censebitur credibilius et sapientiae NaturaeA, quae semper per pauciora agit, conuenientius: an vt sesquicentum passus, an vero vt tot millia non passuum, sed milliarium Germanicorum in vno secundo temporis scrupulo conficiat? et quidem sub eodem effectu et fine? An non id vehementissime cum dicta Naturae sapientia, qua semper pauciora et breuiora eligit, pugnabit, si assumto coeli motu per omnium Stellarum et Planetarum diuersos, vehementissimos, menteque humana incomprehensibiles atque etiam innumerabiles cursus efficere cogetur, quod poterat in vno facilime praestare? An non et hoc, quod ea ratione Planetis duplex et Ubi ipsi contrarius inesset centri motus? Nescio etiam, an fixa Sidera in tanta motus pernicitate, tam constanter suas ab inuicem distantias custodire possent. Sed sexcenta eiuscemodi proferriA possent, quae satis conuincunt, motum diurnum in nullo alio corpore quam in terra quaerendum esseA.</p><p>Ad annuum autem motum quod attinet, cur mihi non verisimile videatur, spatium a Sole ad Saturnum tot vicibus contineri intra Saturnum et affixarum Stellarum remotionem? aut quid absurdi sequitur, si Stella tertiae Magnitudinis aequat totum orbem annuum? An id aut cum voluntate diuina pugnat, aut diuinae Naturae impossibile est, aut infinitae Naturae non competit? Haec demonstranda omnino tibi sunt, si absurdi quid hinc colligere volueris. Non tam facile absurditatis argui possunt, quae vulgo absurda primâ fronte videntur, sed longe maior est diuina Sapientia et Maiestas. Et quantumcunque etiam Mundi Vastitatem, et Magnitudinem concedas, nullam tamen proportionem ad infinitum Creatorem habebit. Quo maior Rex, eo maius et amplius palatium decere putat suam MaiestatemA. Quid cogitabis de DEO?</p><p>In tertio vero terrae motu, cur axis terrae non annuatim reflecteretur, vt quiescere nihilominus appareret? aut cur non axem et centrum duplici diuersoque motu agitari detur? Si terra impacta esset duro aliquo orbi et ab eo circumferretur, haec fortassis locum habere possent. At cum terra liberrime in aëre pendeat, cur non, si centrum promoueatur, axis nihilominus super eodem centro possit nutare eo, quo Natura velit? Centrum enim semper in axe est, et est quasi Polus, vt ita dicam, reflexionis axis, nec vna reflectitur, sed respectu reflexionis est immotum. At haec axis reflexio facilius intelligitur, si per idem terrae centrum etiam ducatur axis Zodiaci, vero Zodiaci axi parallelus, qui in immensitateA Sphaerae cum vero axe idem videbitur, circa quem videbitur axis terrae reflecti, non quidem ad Angulos rectos, sed ad tantum Angulum, quanta est Zodiaci obliquitas. Scio hoc in loco COPERNICVM esse admodum obscurum, nec facile perceptibilem. Sed haec alio modo longe facilius possunt explicari, nec opus est triplici terrae motu, sed sufficiunt diurnus, et annuus. Nec axis terrae reuera reflectitur, siquidem manet semperA idem Angulus intersectionis planorum Eclipticae et Aequatoris, axisque terrae motu annuo ita circumducitur, vt semper paralleliter versus eandem mundi partem spectet, adeoque propter euanescentiam orbis annui in immensitate Sphaerae ad vnum et idem punctum videatur semper collimare: Ex quibus, superueniente motu diurno, reflexio, tanquam effectus resultare videtur. Sed haec fusius et enucleatius DEO dante, inuenies in secundo libro nostrarum Obseruationum, vbi de praecessione aequinoctiorum agemus. Librationem quoque licet in cursu Mercurij non probem, tamen eiusmodi esse video, vt in planis circulorum sese variantibus, naturalior motus excogitari non possit. Sed horum omnium explicatio nimis obscure a COPERNICO proposita est, et effectus cum causa sine distinctione ita confusus, vt quid velit, non facile a quoquam intelligatur. Sed plus intendo mentem, quam valetudo mea patitur. Alibi haec fusius habebis.</p><p>Cometam eadem die, quâ tu, vidi, et Illustrissimo Principi nostro ostendi, qui apud me commorabatur, donec Horizontem subiret. Obseruaui eum, quantum in hac mea valetudine potui: quarum qualiumcunque obseruationum copiam seu exemplar ad te mitto, vt ipse inde motum et congruentiam cum tuis eruas.</p><p>Haec igitur hac vice tibi sufficiant in his meis aerumnis, quae quantae sint, facili potes assequi coniectura ex eo, quod iam in flore aetatis constitutus, eiusmodi morbos sustineam. Desidero Oleum Vitrioli rubicundissimum illud et fragrantissimum, itemque Oleum Tartari, non quidem communis, sed singulariter per singulare mysterium indagati, vt te sine dubio non fugit, aliasque insuper extractiones et solutiones. Pro quibus si fortassis nuncium ad te ablegauero (Hîc enim, vt et vix in alijs Germaniae locis, iustam eorum praeparationem, nancisci nequeo) non dubito, quin pro nostrâ Amicitiâ mihi ea sis communicaturus, salutique meae pro virili consulturus: Aut occasione oblatâ, ipse in posterum, pro Disputationibus Medicamenta mitte, quorum ratio iam merito potior est. Sed de tua in me beneuolentia non dubito.</p><p>Vale mi Nobiliss. Dn. TYCHO.<br>Cassellis ex lecto meo, 18. Aprilis, Anno 1590.<br>T. T. CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, most renowned for virtue, wisdom, and ingenious and erudite learning, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight, of all the mathematicians of our age the most pre-eminent, etc., his lord to be honoured with all observance — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>If, as Ovid sings, \"songs come forth drawn from a serene mind,\" most noble and most excellent Master Tycho, then much more do the most profound and most subtle mathematical disputations — which are not to be compared with the composing of verses — require a mind utterly free from disturbances. I should not, therefore, have committed an act either alien to our friendship, or undeserving of any excuse (as you seem to charge), had I forgone a reply. For what greater torments or pains can be conceived than those I endure in gout [podagra], now for over two years, and in the stone [calculus], this winter — the gravest of all diseases? These diseases, assailing me by turns this winter, so weakened my body and all but cast my mind from its seat, that I could scarcely [barely] even trace a letter. Last summer, moreover, although besides the gout I detected in myself continual languors and a horror of all labour, yet I did not know what disease those unwonted weaknesses would bring upon me and inflict; which has now become known to me well enough through the bitterest pains and torments. For the gouty spirit [spiritus podagricus] (for so I now please to call it) has grown to such malignancy in me that it has most tightly clogged not only the urinary passages and the kidneys, but also the mesaraic [mesenteric] veins, with red gravel and with tartar — so that, as soon as I have eaten or drunk, owing to the inhibition of the nourishment drawn in by nature and to the expulsion of urine, I feel the most violent torments both in the mesaraic [veins] and in the urinary passages, after the manner of colic pains, and I cannot retain the food I have taken in my stomach, on account of nausea and vomiting. And there are in me not obscure signs which argue, not obscurely, that the same spirit is attempting the same thing in my other members too. I tried Galenic medicine, being treated by the most learned Doctor Butterus, physician to our Most Illustrious Prince — but without any success. When it sought to drive out the urine, with the tartar blocking [the way], I endured the most violent torments in the urinary passages before the bladder. What baths too and sitz-baths can do, you easily understand, since on account of the gout I am forbidden to put my feet into them; and the outer members not being warmed, the parts between are scarcely warmed in due measure. And although the virtues of herbs may perhaps be able to break up the tartar, yet they cannot dissolve it into water — which is here altogether necessary, if it must either flow back into the intestines, or pass off through the bladder by sweating out, and the disease be cured from the roots. Whether this so great malignancy of the gouty spirit can also generate saltpetre [salt of stone, <i>sal petrae</i>] in me out of the dregs, and bring it by continual sublimation to the extreme degree of subtlety, and so snatch away my life in a moment, I leave to you to weigh — you who easily understand what I mean. For since this spirit, as you know, is of a fixed nature, while the other and better spirits, which can blunt its malignancy, are volatile, and are daily consumed through the strainings of thought, of which I cannot rid myself — will it not be able to increase its power immeasurably, and at last to assume to itself alone the whole dominion, hindered by none? Therefore I should rightly turn myself wholly to divine matters alone, setting aside philosophical ones — though these too are divine. Nevertheless, since I now in some manner feel some remission, such as it is, of the torments, for my love toward you, which is surely no common love, and for our friendship, I could not refrain from at least replying — with whatever might be able to come into my mind amid these afflictions of mine — to your disputations, now sent to me separately on a sheet.</p><p>As, then, to what you bring forward in assailing Copernicus on the threefold motion of the Earth: in the first place, concerning the diurnal motion — that, if the Earth be supposed to revolve in 24 hours, it cannot be that a leaden ball let down from a very high tower should strike exactly perpendicular upon the point of the Earth set beneath it, since that point, on account of the Earth's most rapid motion, must necessarily withdraw itself meanwhile and leave the leaden ball behind it: this has been sufficiently unravelled and resolved by Copernicus. For of things falling and rising, as he himself says, we must confess the motion to be twofold in relation to the World, and altogether compounded of the rectilinear and the circular — seeing that the things which are pressed down by their own weight, being most earthy, [leave] no doubt that the parts preserve the same nature as their whole. Which ought to be far less doubtful to you, since you know from a better philosophy that Nature delights in Nature, and Nature retains Nature. If to those bodies the knowledge of their own motion is natural, as you will have it, it will belong to the parts also. For just as in other natural things — for example, the parts of gold retain the nature of the whole, nor when thus separated from the whole do they cast off that which had been peculiarly and specifically implanted in them by Nature — so too in these bodies, which have motion natural [to them], as you not ineptly will have it, the parts will not cast off this primary prerogative implanted in them, and their chief and specific dignity. But why are you anxious about the fall of heavy things, when all things which lie on the surface of the Earth free and separated from the whole — nay, the very tower from which the leaden ball would be released, and the buildings themselves — would necessarily collapse and be left behind by the Earth's motion, if the parts did not retain the motion of the whole: which, how contrary it is to the Wisdom of Nature, no one fails to see. But as to what you bring forward concerning the computations — that on that reckoning the Earth must in one second of time revolve, in the more northerly regions there, about a hundred and fifty paces or more: I ask, which will be reckoned the more credible, and the more conformable to the wisdom of Nature (which always acts through fewer means) — that it should accomplish a hundred and fifty paces, or that it should accomplish so many thousands, not of paces, but of German miles, in one second of time? and that to the same effect and end? Will it not most vehemently clash with the said wisdom of Nature, which always chooses fewer and shorter [ways], if, the motion of the heaven being assumed, it must produce, through the diverse courses of all the stars and planets — most violent, incomprehensible to the human mind, and indeed innumerable — what it could most easily have effected in one [body]? And is there not also this: that on that reckoning there would be in the planets a twofold motion of the centre, and one contrary to it [in its place / <i>Ubi</i>]? I do not know, either, whether the fixed stars, in so great a swiftness of motion, could so constantly keep their distances from one another. But six hundred things of the kind could be brought forward, which sufficiently prove that the diurnal motion is to be sought in no other body than in the Earth.</p><p>But as concerns the annual motion: why should it not seem probable to me that the space from the Sun to Saturn is contained so many times over within [the interval] between Saturn and the remoteness of the fixed stars? Or what absurdity follows, if a star of the third magnitude equals the whole annual orb? Does this either clash with the divine will, or is it impossible to the divine Nature, or does it not befit the infinite Nature? These things you must by all means demonstrate, if you would gather any absurdity from this. Those things are not so easily to be convicted of absurdity which to the common run seem absurd at first sight; rather, far greater is the divine Wisdom and Majesty. And however great a vastness and magnitude of the World you grant, yet it will have no proportion to the infinite Creator. The greater the King, the greater and more ample a palace he thinks befits his Majesty. What will you think of GOD?</p><p>But in the third motion of the Earth: why should not the axis of the Earth be turned back [reflected] annually, so that it should nonetheless appear to be at rest? Or why should it not be granted that the axis and the centre are agitated by a twofold and diverse motion? If the Earth were fixed into some hard orb and carried round by it, these [objections] perhaps might have place. But since the Earth hangs most freely in the air, why not — if the centre be advanced — should the axis nonetheless be able to nod upon the same centre, in whatever way Nature wills? For the centre is always in the axis, and is, so to speak, the Pole of the reflection of the axis; nor is it reflected together [with it], but in respect of the reflection is unmoved. But this reflection of the axis is more easily understood if through the same centre of the Earth there be also drawn an axis of the Zodiac, parallel to the true axis of the Zodiac, which in the immensity of the Sphere will appear the same as the true axis, about which the axis of the Earth will appear to be reflected — not indeed at right angles, but at as great an angle as is the obliquity of the Zodiac. I know that in this place Copernicus is exceedingly obscure, and not easily intelligible. But these things can be explained far more easily in another way, nor is there need of a threefold motion of the Earth, but the diurnal and the annual suffice. Nor is the axis of the Earth really reflected, since there remains always the same angle of intersection of the planes of the Ecliptic and the Equator, and the axis of the Earth is so carried round by the annual motion that it always looks parallel-wise toward the same part of the World, and so, on account of the vanishing of the annual orb in the immensity of the Sphere, it appears always to aim at one and the same point: from which, the diurnal motion supervening, the reflection seems to result, as it were an effect. But these things, God granting, you will find more fully and more clearly set out in the second book of our Observations, where we shall treat of the precession of the equinoxes. The libration too, although in the course of Mercury I do not approve it, yet I see it to be of such a kind that, in the planes of the circles varying themselves, a more natural motion cannot be devised. But the explanation of all these things has been propounded by Copernicus too obscurely, and the effect so confounded with the cause without distinction, that what he means is not easily understood by anyone. But I am straining my mind more than my health allows. You shall have these things more fully elsewhere.</p><p>The comet I saw on the same day as you, and showed it to our Most Illustrious Prince, who was staying with me, until it sank below the Horizon. I observed it, as far as in this state of health I could: of which observations, such as they are, I send you a copy or specimen, that you may yourself draw out from it the motion and the agreement with your own.</p><p>Let these things, then, suffice you for this time, amid these afflictions of mine — how great they are, you can easily reach by conjecture from this, that, set as I now am in the flower of my age, I endure diseases of this kind. I long for that most ruddy and most fragrant Oil of Vitriol, and likewise Oil of Tartar — not indeed of the common sort, but of that singularly sought out through a singular mystery, as without doubt does not escape you — and besides other extractions and solutions. For which, if perhaps I shall dispatch a messenger to you (for here, as also scarcely in other places of Germany, I cannot obtain a proper preparation of them), I do not doubt that, for our Friendship's sake, you will impart them to me, and will look to my health to the best of your power. Or, when occasion offers, do you yourself in future send Medicaments in return for the Disputations, the consideration of which now deservedly weighs more with me. But of your goodwill toward me I do not doubt.</p><p>Farewell, my most noble Master Tycho.<br>At Kassel, from my bed, 18 April, the year 1590.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Christoph Rothmann.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550 – c. 1600), mathematician and astronomer at the court of <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6). His correspondence with Tycho on cosmology, instruments, and the comet of 1585 is among the most substantial of the period; this letter is one of those Tycho selected for his printed <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596). Rothmann here defends the Copernican explanations against Tycho's objections — though he is no doctrinaire Copernican — while pleading grave illness.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), Liber I — the volume Tycho printed himself; reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the source header.</li><li><b>Salutation / titulature.</b> <i>DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, EQUITI DANO</i> = \"Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight\" — Tycho's ancestral seat and his noble rank; <i>MATHEMATICORUM NOSTRO SECULO OMNIUM PRAeSTANTISSIMO</i> = \"of all the mathematicians of our age the most pre-eminent.\" <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam (dicit)</i>, \"heartiest greeting\" (§5.5). Subscription <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Tuus Totus</i> / <i>Tibi Toto</i>, \"Wholly yours / Most devoted to you.\"</li><li><b>Ovid allusion.</b> The opening <i>vt OVIDIVS canit, Carmina proueniunt animo deducta sereno</i> paraphrases <b>Ovid</b>, <i>Tristia</i> I.1.39 (<i>carmina proveniunt animo deducta sereno</i>) — verses come from a serene mind; rendered as a quotation. The printed text begins \"ASI, vt…\" where the engrossed initial capital <b>S</b> of <i>Si</i> has dropped from the extraction (the decorative initial letter); the sense is <i>Si … Carmina proueniunt …, multo magis … Disputationes … requirent</i> (\"If … songs come forth …, much more do … disputations … require [a serene mind]\"). Reading adopted: <i>[S]i</i>; flagged.</li><li><b>Medicine / alchemy.</b> <i>Podagra</i> = gout (here gout of the feet); <i>Calculus</i> = the stone (§5.4, <i>lithiasis</i>); <i>venae mesaraicae</i> = the mesaraic (mesenteric) veins; <i>tartarum</i> = tartar (Paracelsian morbid concretion); <i>dolores cholici</i> = colic pains; <i>Medicina Galenica</i> = Galenic medicine; <i>Salem Petrae / Sal Petrae</i> = saltpetre (here imagined generated in the body by sublimation). The <i>Spiritus Podagricus</i> (\"gouty spirit\") of \"fixed\" vs. \"volatile\" nature is Rothmann's own Paracelsian-iatrochemical coinage — kept literal and flagged, not modernised. <i>Oleum Vitrioli</i> = Oil of Vitriol (concentrated sulphuric acid); <i>Oleum Tartari</i> = Oil of Tartar (deliquesced potassium carbonate) — alchemical preparations Rothmann requests of Tycho, who ran a laboratory at Uraniborg.</li><li><b>Physician.</b> <i>Dn. Doct. Butterus, Medico Illustrissimi Principis nostri</i> = Doctor <b>Butterus</b> (Butter), physician to the Landgrave Wilhelm IV (\"our Most Illustrious Prince\").</li><li><b>Astronomy / cosmology.</b> The body is a point-by-point reply to Tycho's objections against the <b>threefold motion of the Earth</b> attributed to Copernicus (diurnal rotation, annual revolution, and the \"third motion\" of the axis — the conical motion Copernicus posited to keep the axis fixed in direction). Technical renderings per §5.1: <i>motus diurnus / annuus</i> = diurnal / annual motion; <i>praecessio aequinoctiorum</i> = precession of the equinoxes; <i>obliquitas Zodiaci</i> = obliquity of the Zodiac (ecliptic); <i>libratio</i> (in <i>cursu Mercurij</i>) = libration (of Mercury's course); <i>orbis annuus</i> = the annual orb (Earth's orbit); <i>Stella tertiae Magnitudinis</i> = a star of the third magnitude. <i>axis … reflecti / reflexio</i> is Copernicus's term for the conical sweep of the axis — rendered \"reflected / reflection\" (Rothmann argues the apparent \"reflection\" is a derived effect, not a third real motion).</li><li><b>The falling-ball argument.</b> <i>globus plumbeus ex altissima turri demissus</i> = the leaden ball dropped from a high tower (the standard anti-rotation objection); Rothmann answers with the doctrine that the parts share the nature and motion of the whole (<i>partes retinent Naturam / motum totius</i>).</li><li><b>Measures.</b> <i>sesquicentum passus</i> = \"a hundred and fifty paces\" (one and a half hundred); <i>milliaria Germanica</i> = German miles. <i>secundum temporis scrupulum</i> = \"a second of time\" (the <i>scrupulum</i> being the sixtieth subdivision) — rendered \"one second of time.\"</li><li><b><i>Ubi ipsi contrarius</b></i> — <i>An non et hoc, quod ea ratione Planetis duplex et Ubi ipsi contrarius inesset centri motus?</i> The clause is compressed; <i>Ubi</i> (the scholastic category \"Where / place\") is here used substantively, so \"a twofold motion of the centre, and one contrary to it [in its place].\" The exact construction is elliptical in the printed text; rendered closely and flagged.</li><li><b>Theology.</b> <i>Quo maior Rex, eo maius et amplius palatium decere putat suam Maiestatem. Quid cogitabis de DEO?</i> — the analogy that a greater King requires a greater palace, applied to God and the immensity of the heavens, is Rothmann's signature defence of a vast (Copernican) cosmos; kept as written.</li><li><b>The comet.</b> <i>Cometam eadem die, quâ tu, vidi</i> — Rothmann reports observing a comet the same day Tycho did, shown to the Landgrave, and encloses his observations. (The comet of early 1590; the reference is to the body Tycho had asked about.)</li><li><b>Source-text artifacts.</b> The source as extracted carries stray capital <b>\"A\"</b> characters fused to the end of certain words at edition line-breaks and at the very end of the letter (e.g. <i>fereA</i>, <i>mesaraicasA</i>, <i>nequeamA</i>, <i>fortassisA</i>, <i>eumA</i>, <i>possuntA</i>, <i>subducatA</i>, <i>insitumA</i>, <i>inepteA</i>, <i>esA</i>, <i>scrupuloA</i>, <i>NaturaeA</i>, <i>proferriA</i>, <i>esseA</i>, <i>imperiumA</i>, <i>MaiestatemA</i>, <i>immensitateA</i>, <i>semperA</i>, <i>ROTHMANNVS.A</i>). These are <b>apparatus/footnote anchor markers of the printed edition</b> (variant-note sigla), not part of Rothmann's Latin; they are reproduced verbatim in the Source block per protocol §4.2 but carry no lexical value and are disregarded in the translation. The decorative dropped initial <i>[S]i</i> (see Ovid note) is the only place an editorial reading was adopted.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The dense conditional/negative argumentation was verified clause by clause: <i>fieri non posse</i> (\"it cannot be\"), <i>non dubium quin</i> (\"no doubt that\"), <i>nec … abijciunt</i> (\"nor do they cast off\"), <i>non abijcient</i> (\"will not cast off\"), <i>nemo non videt</i> (\"no one fails to see\" = everyone sees), <i>An non … pugnabit?</i> (\"Will it not clash?\"), <i>Nec axis … reuera reflectitur</i> (\"Nor is the axis really reflected\"), <i>nancisci nequeo</i> (\"I cannot obtain\"), <i>non dubito</i> (\"I do not doubt\"). Each negation in the source is reproduced; none added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Cassellis … 18. Aprilis, Anno 1590</i> = at <b>Kassel</b> (§5.7), 18 April 1590. The Hesse-Kassel court used the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> at this date; so dated <b>[O.S.]</b>. The dataset label (\"1590. 18. april. Kassel\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. <i>ex lecto meo</i> = \"from my bed,\" consistent with the body's account of illness.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout. Soft points, each flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the dropped decorative initial of the opening <i>[S]i</i> (Ovid quotation); (2) the elliptical clause <i>duplex et Ubi ipsi contrarius … centri motus</i>, where the scholastic <i>Ubi</i> is rendered \"in its place\"; (3) the consistently literal handling of Rothmann's iatrochemical <i>Spiritus Podagricus</i> / fixed-vs-volatile-spirits theory, kept as period theory. The stray \"A\" apparatus markers are noise from extraction, not textual cruxes.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief no Dreyer image collation is possible; audited published_english against source_text alone and grade is capped at B regardless of quality. Read the full Latin (salutation; the four-paragraph account of podagra/calculus symptoms, failed Galenic treatment under Dr Butterus, and the spiritus podagricus/sal petrae theory; the point-by-point rebuttal of Tycho's three objections to Copernicus - the falling-body/tower argument, the improbable rotational speed, the annual-orb-size objection, and the axis-reflection/libration argument; the comet observation shared with the Landgrave; the closing request for Oil of Vitriol and Oil of Tartar) against the English clause by clause. Every proper name (Ovid, Copernicus, Butterus), technical term (podagra, calculus, spiritus podagricus, sal petrae, mesaraic veins, radius astronomicus-equivalent detail N/A), and the sole date (18 April 1590, Kassel) match between source and translation. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; bracketed glosses are clearly marked supplements. Nothing to collate against Dreyer, so B is the ceiling.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 15, "prev": "brahe-t_15900223002", "next": "brahe-t_15910401001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900223002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910401001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Excuses his delayed reply by describing two years of severe illness — gout and kidney stones — which so weakened him that he could barely trace a letter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900418001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900501001", "d": "1590-05-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 1 May 1590 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia, vol. VII Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.25</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISS. ET DOCTISS. D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO URANIBURGI, DOMINO ET AMICO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO.</p><p>NOBILISS. Domine TYCHO et amice plurimum observande. Accepi binas abs te literas, eodem tenore verborum, alteris adiuncta Apologia. Deinde die 24. Aprilis novo stylo accepi alias a studioso quodam Rostochiensi, scriptas abs te 25. Ianuarij. Ad eas omnes a me et a Domino CURTIO propediem accipies responsum plenissime, unâ cum Privilegio luculentiori, quae omnia noster tabellarius, quem ipsemet D. CURTIUS ad te ablegabit. Sic enim inter nos iam est constitutum. Haec brevibus per eundem studiosum, qui Rostochium revertitur, tibi significanda esse duxi.</p><p>B. V.<br>Datae<br>Pragae die 1. Maij novo stylo Anno 1590.<br>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 244; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si fieri posset, ut mihi modellam, ut vocant, seu exemplum tuorum instrumentorum observatoriorum (puta Sextantis et Armillarum; in Quadrante enim non erit ulla difficultas) ex charta conglutinata aut asserculis mitteres, ad quorum exemplum similia iustae magnitudinis mihi construi curarem, faceres mihi rem gratissimam, et posset ea noster tabellarius, qui ad te cum nostris literis veniet, mihi afferre. Non puto enim tibi deesse manuarios artifices, qui modellas illas construere possunt. Indicabis etiam mihi, quantae magnitudinis a me sint omnia illa construenda.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Uraniborg, his lord and friend much to be honoured.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Tycho and friend much to be honoured. I have received two letters from you, in the same wording, with the Apologia enclosed with one of them. Then, on 24 April New Style, I received others from a certain student of Rostock, written by you on 25 January. To all of these you will shortly receive a most full reply from me and from Master Curtius, together with a more splendid Privilege — all of which our courier — whom Master Curtius himself will send to you — will bring. For so it has now been settled between us. These few things I thought ought to be made known to you by the same student, who is returning to Rostock.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Given<br>at Prague, the 1st day of May New Style, in the year 1590.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[deus].</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If it could be done that you would send me a model, as they call it, or a specimen of your observatory instruments (the Sextant, say, and the Armillae; for in the case of the Quadrant there will be no difficulty), made of glued paper or of little boards, so that on their pattern I might have similar ones built for me of the proper size, you would do me a most welcome thing; and our courier, who will come to you with our letters, could bring them to me. For I do not suppose that you lack handicraftsmen who can construct those models. You will also indicate to me of what size all those things are to be built by me.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer and personal physician to Emperor Rudolf II at Prague; Tycho's foremost correspondent in the Empire and the conduit through whom Tycho cultivated his standing at the imperial court. He signs himself only <i>THAD.</i> (an abbreviated forename).</li><li><b>Recipient / place.</b> <i>D. Tychoni Brahe Dano, Domino Uraniburgi</i> = \"Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of <b>Uraniborg</b>\" (Tycho's seat on the island of Hven; protocol §5.7). The dateline place <i>Pragae</i> = <b>Prague</b>.</li><li><b><i>Curtius / D. Curtius / Domino Curtio</b></i> — <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Iacobus Curtius, d. 1594), <b>Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b> (<i>Imperii Vicecancellarius</i>) under Rudolf II, learned in letters and mathematics, who of his own accord procured, drafted and dispatched at his own expense the imperial printing privilege for Tycho. (His role is stated explicitly in a companion letter of the same correspondence: <i>\"Iacobus enim Curtius, Imperii Vicecancellarius … hoc Caesaris privilegium mihi sponte propria procuravit, conscripsit, suisque sumptibus conducto tabellario transmisit.\"</i>) Here he is named both as co-author of the forthcoming reply and as the <i>tabellarius</i> (courier) who will convey it.</li><li><b><i>Privilegio luculentiori</b></i> — \"a more splendid Privilege\": the <b>imperial printing privilege</b>. Tycho had been dissatisfied with the first imperial privilege as too weak; Hagecius arranged a stronger one, dated <b>13 June 1590</b> and drafted by Vice-Chancellor Curtius, printed in the <i>Mechanica</i> (Norlind, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, 1970). The comparative <i>luculentiori</i> (\"more splendid / more substantial\") is Hagecius's own hedge, signalling the improvement over the earlier grant; preserved, not flattened to a bare \"privilege\".</li><li><b><i>Apologia</b></i> — a defensive/explanatory tract by Tycho enclosed with one of the two letters; left in Latin as a title-word. Tycho repeatedly used <i>Apologia</i> for self-vindicating pieces (cf. the lost Danish Apologia and the apologetic prefatory matter of this period); the referent here is not further specified in the letter and is not pressed.</li><li><b><i>eodem tenore verborum</b></i> — \"in the same wording\": the two letters Hagecius received from Tycho were duplicates of one another (a common safeguard against loss in transit), not two distinct messages.</li><li><b><i>studioso quodam Rostochiensi … qui Rostochium revertitur</b></i> — \"a certain student of <b>Rostock</b> … who is returning to Rostock\": an unnamed student serving as carrier in both directions; the present note is sent back by his hand. Rostock was Tycho's old university town and a node in his northern correspondence relay.</li><li><b><i>B. V.</b></i> — <i>Bene Vale</i>, \"Fare you well\" (protocol §5.5); expanded silently in the source as the standard valediction.</li><li><b><i>T. studiosiss.</b></i> — <i>Tui studiosissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to you\" (protocol §5.5, on the model of <i>E. Tuae studiosiss.</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline reads <i>\"die 1. Maij <b>novo stylo</b> Anno 1590\"</i> — explicitly <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b>, consistent with the dataset label (1 May 1590, Prague). As a correspondent in Catholic Imperial service, Hagecius writes N.S. (protocol §3). The two <b>in-letter</b> dates are reported as found and not converted: the receipt <i>\"die 24. Aprilis novo stylo\"</i> (24 April N.S.) is marked N.S. in the text; Tycho's own letter <i>\"scriptas abs te 25. Ianuarij\"</i> (written 25 January) carries no style marker — Tycho on Hven kept <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark retained the Julian calendar until 1700), so this date is presumably O.S. as written by Tycho, but the letter does not say so and the point is flagged rather than silently resolved.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (after the subscription \"T. studiosiss. THAD.\"), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 244 (dropped by the working transcription) — Hájek's request for paper/board models of Tycho's sextant + armillae, which closes the cross-reference to the 1 Aug Tycho→Kurz reply. (Dreyer prints \"medellam\"; the incipit elsewhere \"modellam\".)</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Three Latin corruptions corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII pp.243-244: 'D. CURTIUS' restored to 'quem ipsemet D. CURTIUS' (the dropped relative clause that distinguishes the courier from Curtius, who dispatches him); 'modestas illas' restored to 'modellas illas' (those instrument-models, not 'those modest ones'); and 'medellam' restored to 'modellam' (model, not remedy). English corrected to match at the first two places, where sense was genuinely affected; the third needed no English change since the translator had already rendered the intended sense despite the corrupted Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is short and rendered securely; the only soft points are (i) the unspecified referent of the enclosed <i>Apologia</i>, left open as the text leaves it, and (ii) the unmarked style of Tycho's \"25 January\" (presumably O.S. but not stated), flagged above rather than converted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address, body, date, and the restored postscript) against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.243-244, reading the page images directly. Found three places where the published source_text departs from Dreyer's printed text, one of which (the dropped 'quem ipsemet') changes who is said to act as courier, and one of which (modestas/modellas) changes the sense actually delivered in the English.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). source_text silently drops 'quem ipsemet' (whom ... himself); Dreyer distinguishes an unnamed courier from Curtius, who sends him, whereas the published text and its translation collapse the two into one person, misidentifying Curtius as the courier who will personally deliver the documents. Dreyer prints: <i>quae omnia nofter tabellarius, quem ipfemet D. CURTIUS ad te ablegabit.</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text reads 'medellam' (remedy) where Dreyer prints 'modellam' (model); the translator evidently recognised the intended sense and rendered 'model' anyway, masking the corruption already present in the published source_text. Dreyer prints: <i>modellam</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). source_text reads 'modestas illas' (those modest [ones]) where Dreyer prints 'modellas illas' (those models, referring back to the requested instrument-models); here the English follows the corrupted source_text, so the sense delivered to the reader is genuinely altered. Dreyer prints: <i>manuarios artifices, qui modellas illas construere poffunt</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All three defects verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.243-244 page images (not the OCR text layer): 'quem ipfemet D. CURTIUS' (p.243), 'modellas illas' (p.244 l.11), and 'modellam' (p.244 l.6) all read exactly as the audit's Dreyer claim states. No other part of the letter was touched; every changed word is accounted for in outcomes above.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe a. 1590 (4 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/25-27", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964025"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.25."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 22, "prev": "brahe-t_15900223001", "next": "brahe-t_15900611001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900223001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900803001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of three letters including the Apologia, promises a full reply via courier Curtius, and requests specimen models of the sextant and armillae.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900501001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900515001", "d": "1590-05-15", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 15 May 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with extensive Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN/ TYCHONI BRAHE HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ ETC.</p><p>VNSERN gnädigen Gruß zuvor/ Vester lieber Besonder/ daß wir euch nu in so langer zeit nicht geschrieben/ ist die Vrsach/ daß vnser Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS, ein zeitlang hero fast vnpaßlich gewesen/ zu dem auch numehr wir selbst/ tam mole aetatis, quam laborum atque curarum, dermassen gedruckt werden/ daß wir vns mit den oblectamentis Mathematicis nicht mehr/ wiewol zuvor beschehen/ oblectiren können. Nicht desto weniger aber/ dato otio, vnderlassen wir vnser Studium Mathematicum nicht so gar bleiben/ sondern hincken noch hernacher/ wie ein alt Weib am Stecken/ Befinden aber doch/ daß es leider mit vns so weit kommen ist/ daß was wir hiebevor magna alacritate selbst erfunden/ jetzo nicht mehr wol verstehen. O pietas, o prisca fides; quis talia fando, Temperet a lachrymis? Was aber vorgedachten vnsern Mathematicum betrifft/ ist derselbige der meynung/ wenn er nur ein wenig möcht wandern/ vnd die Lufft verendern/ daß es alßdenn mit jhm besser werden solle/ Welchs wir jhm aber hart widerrathen/ So hat er aber doch einen eignen Kopff/ den kaufft er alle Jar einen eignen Hut/ damit müssen wir jhn lassen geweren/ Aber leidt were vns/ wenn jhm einiger Schade widerfahren solte/ denn er ist ingeniosus, vnd ein feiner gelehrter Gesell. Begeren derhalben gnädiglich/ jhr wollet euch jhm von vnsert wegen lassen befohlen seyn/ vnd jhm ewere Instrumenta Mathematica sehen lassen/ Auch widerumb von jhm vernemen/ was vns vnser Vhrmacher für Instrumenta gemacht/ vnd noch zu machen vnderhanden hat. Deßgleichen vns auch hinwider zuentbieten/ wie es allenthalben/ so wol vmb euch alß auch ewern gnädigsten Herrn den jungen König/ vnd die gantze Regierung deß Königreichs Dennemarck/ jetzo stehet. Das seyndt wir hinwider gegen euch in Gnaden/ damit wir euch sonders wol gewogen/ zu erkennen geneigt.</p><p>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel/ am 15. Maij, Anno 1590.<br>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Trusty and well-beloved: that we have now in so long a time not written to you, the cause is that our mathematician <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> [Christoph Rothmann] has for a while past been almost indisposed; to which is added that now we ourself are so weighed down, both by the burden of age and by [that] of labours and cares [<i>tam mole aetatis, quam laborum atque curarum</i>], that we can no longer take pleasure in those mathematical delights [<i>oblectamentis Mathematicis</i>] as we formerly did. Nonetheless, when leisure is given [<i>dato otio</i>], we do not let our mathematical study [<i>Studium Mathematicum</i>] lapse altogether, but limp along after it still, like an old woman on a stick — yet we find, alas, that it has come so far with us that what we formerly discovered ourself with great eagerness [<i>magna alacritate</i>] we now no longer well understand. <i>O piety, o ancient faith! Who, telling of such things, could refrain from tears?</i> But as concerns our aforesaid mathematician, the same is of the opinion that, if only he might travel a little and change the air, it would then go better with him; which, however, we have strongly advised him against. Yet he has a head of his own — for which he buys himself a hat of his own every year — and so we must let him have his way. But it would grieve us if any harm should befall him, for he is ingenious [<i>ingeniosus</i>] and a fine learned fellow. We therefore graciously desire that you will let him be commended to you for our sake, and let him see your mathematical instruments [<i>Instrumenta Mathematica</i>]; and likewise learn from him in turn what instruments [<i>Instrumenta</i>] our clockmaker has made for us, and has still in hand to make. Likewise [we desire] that you in turn inform us how things now stand everywhere, both as regards yourself and as regards your most gracious lord the young King, and the whole government of the Kingdom of Denmark. This in turn we are inclined to acknowledge toward you in [our] grace, being therewith especially well disposed to you.</p><p>Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 15 May, in the year 1590.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm von Gottes Gnaden, Landtgraue zu Hessen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the \"astronomer-prince\" of Kassel, whose observatory and instrument workshop were Tycho's chief institutional counterpart and correspondent. <i>Graue zu Catzenelnbogen</i> = Count of Katzenelnbogen, a hereditary Hessian title. The princely register (<i>Wir/euch</i>, full titulature) is preserved per protocol §2/§5.5.</li><li><b>Address line.</b> <i>Dem Vesten, vnserm lieben Besondern</i> = \"To the Honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved\" (chancery honorific for a noble correspondent; <i>vester</i> = <i>fester</i>, \"steadfast/honourable\"; protocol §5.5). <i>Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — Tycho as <b>Lord of Knudstrup</b> (the Brahe family seat in Scania), his territorial style.</li><li><b>Christoph Rothmann.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician/astronomer at Kassel and a major correspondent of Tycho's (protocol §5.6). The letter is in effect a letter of introduction: Rothmann would shortly travel to Hven (he reached Uraniborg in August 1590), and Wilhelm here commends him to Tycho. The Landgrave records that he had advised Rothmann <i>against</i> the journey he proposed — a single, carefully counted negation (<i>welchs wir jhm aber hart widerrathen</i>, \"which we strongly advised him against\"), not a prohibition of the visit to Tycho.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches</b> (preserved and bracketed in the translation per §2):</li><li><i>tam mole aetatis, quam laborum atque curarum</i> — \"both by the burden of age, and by [that] of labours and cares.\"</li><li><i>oblectamentis Mathematicis … oblectiren</i> — \"mathematical delights … take pleasure,\" a Latin-noun / Latin-stem-German-verb pun (<i>oblectare</i>).</li><li><i>dato otio</i> — ablative absolute, \"leisure being given / when leisure is given.\"</li><li><i>Studium Mathematicum</i>, <i>Instrumenta Mathematica</i>, <i>Instrumenta</i>, <i>ingeniosus</i>, <i>magna alacritate</i> — technical/scholarly nouns and phrases code-switched into the German, the hallmark of this chancery register; <i>Instrumenta</i> rendered \"instruments\" per §5.2.</li><li><b>Virgilian quotation.</b> <i>O pietas, o prisca fides; quis talia fando, / Temperet a lachrymis?</i> — a conflation of two famous Virgilian tags: <i>o pietas, o prisca fides</i> (<i>Aeneid</i> VI.878, of Marcellus) and <i>quis talia fando … temperet a lacrimis?</i> (<i>Aeneid</i> II.6–8, Aeneas beginning the tale of Troy's fall). Kept as the lament-verse it is (protocol §4, \"keep sententiae and verse as verse\") and set in italics. The Landgrave applies it to his own decayed mathematical powers.</li><li><b>Idiom.</b> <i>hincken noch hernacher, wie ein alt Weib am Stecken</i> — \"limp along after it still, like an old woman on a stick\"; the self-deprecating simile is kept literal. <i>einen eignen Kopff … kaufft er alle Jar einen eignen Hut</i> — \"a head of his own … buys himself a hat of his own every year,\" a proverbial play on <i>eigner Kopf</i> (\"self-willed, headstrong\") rendered literally so the wordplay survives; the sense is that Rothmann is obstinate and must be humoured.</li><li><b>The young King.</b> <i>ewern gnädigsten Herrn den jungen König</i> = <b>Christian IV</b> of Denmark (b. 1577; not yet of age — the regency government, <i>die gantze Regierung</i>, then ruled), Tycho's sovereign after Frederik II's death in 1588.</li><li><b>Clockmaker.</b> <i>vnser Vhrmacher</i> — \"our clockmaker\"; the Kassel court clockmaker (Jost Bürgi held this post and built astronomical instruments and clocks for Wilhelm); the letter asks Tycho to gather from Rothmann news of the instruments the clockmaker had built and had still in hand.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating — RESOLVED (see §1 note below).</b> The letter is dated <b>15 May 1590</b> (O.S.; Hesse-Kassel kept the Julian calendar until 1700). Dreyer's 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> print and Wilhelm's own reply both read <i>15. Maij</i>, confirming the catalogue date; the working transcription's <i>25. Maij</i> was the error, corrected in the header, Source, and translation.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> Dreyer (the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> = <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI, p. 214) and Wilhelm's own reply both read <b>15 May</b> (<i>15. Maij</i>), agreeing with the catalogue; the working transcription's <i>25. Maij</i> is the error. Corrected to 15 May in header, Source, and translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German and its Latin insertions render securely and the negation count is preserved; the former 25 May / 15 May discrepancy is now resolved by the §1 Dreyer collation in favour of <b>15 May</b> (see Apparatus).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation was the 25/15-May date; §1 collation (the 1596 edition + Wilhelm's own reply) resolves it cleanly to 15 May.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (no Dreyer reference supplied), so grade capped at B per protocol. Audited the full German text against source_text alone, clause by clause, including both embedded Latin/Virgil tags (tam mole aetatis..., O pietas o prisca fides quis talia fando temperet a lachrymis) and every proper name (Christophorus Rothmannus, Kassel/Cassel, Landgraf Wilhelm) and date (15 Maij 1590). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; the 'einen eignen Kopff...einen eignen Hut' idiom is rendered literally but faithfully (word-order/register issue, not flagged per instructions).</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15900401001", "next": "brahe-t_15901001001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900401001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15901001001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains his silence by Rothmann's illness and his own advancing age, lamenting that mathematical studies he once pursued eagerly are now barely comprehensible to him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900515001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900611001", "d": "1590-06-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek) → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 11 June 1590 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66; B = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.14</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Editorial note.</b> This is a letter <b>to</b> Tycho — Hájek's long reply to a (now-lost or separately-transmitted) Tycho letter of the previous year, the major document of the <b>Tycho–Hájek comet-motion controversy</b> and the earliest surviving notice (in the correspondence) of Tycho's <b>Mars-parallax</b> result and of his completed reply to <b>John Craig</b>. The working transcription is clean; a small number of obvious OCR slips in the transcription have been kept verbatim in the source block and corrected in the translation, flagged in the Apparatus (<i>fint→sint</i>, <i>verilate→veritate</i>, <i>reprensionem→reprehensionem</i>, the stray space in <i>parallaxium ,</i>). The Greek transliteration <i>dedomenis</i> (δεδομένα, \"the data\") and the glyphs ♂ ☉ ♀ ☿ ♊ are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"11. Iunij novo stylo, qui dies fuit feria secunda Pentecostes Anno 1590\"</i>) is explicitly <b>New Style</b> and self-dates correctly: in the Gregorian calendar Whit Monday 1590 was indeed 11 June; agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VII pp. 258–268</b> (running head \"THAD. HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM 1/11 IUN. 1590\"; main letter from cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66 = witness A, with a <b>POST SCRIPTA</b> on p. 268 from cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.14 = witness B — both sigla vindicated). Every load-bearing reading confirmed: the Mars-at-opposition parallax notice (p. 260, qualitative — no numeric value attached), the comet-motion-law thesis and Tycho's difficulty-vs-impossibility counter (p. 262), the Craig/<i>Apologetica responsio</i> praise (pp. 265–266; Dreyer prints the name <b>CRAIGIUS / CRAIGIJ</b>, not \"Craigo\" — that form belongs to a Brucaeus letter at p. 359), the technical sidebar (24 Nov / 26 Jan / 15′, p. 265), and the dateline + Hayck signature (p. 268). The scan garbles <i>IACOBUS CURTIUS</i>→\"PACORUS CURTIUS\" (p. 260) and <i>feria</i>→\"serra\" (p. 268); both are scan artifacts, not variants — the .md readings are correct.</p>", "la": "<p>THADDAeUS HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>ACCEPI tandem, nobiliss. et doctiss. TYCHO, exoptatissimas tuas epistolas, et multis nominibus admodum gratas, non solum quod abs te, omnium amicissimo, amicissime scriptae, sed etiam quod rerum Astronomicarum pulcherrima dissertatione refertae essent. In quibus dum primordia initae inter nos amicitiae suavissima recordatione commemoras, in eo tuae naturae bonitatem prodis. Ne vero tuo quantulocunque silentio igniculi illi amoris nostri mutui viderentur aliquo modo consopiti, idcirco humanissima iucundissimaque ista commemoratione contractae amicitiae et multarum rerum utilium doctissima expositione prudenter de novo eos excitare voluisti. Quae omnia adeo mihi grata et accepta advenerunt, ut his gratiora, hoc praesertim tempore, mihi obtingere non potuerint, tantum abest, ut prolixitas earum ulla ex parte mihi molesta fuerit: imo vero erudita illa abs te suscepta disputatio tam ardens in me eius rei auxit desiderium, mirificamque voluptatem in animo meo excitavit, ut non semel atque iterum, sed toties totam illam disputationem relegere non piguerit. Pro qua summa sane tua illa erga me humanitate et tam suavi iucundoque ac utili per literas colloquio maximas quas possum tibi ago et habeo gratias. Porro quod ex meis quaternis, quas te accepisse scribis, nullae vel interierint vel resignatione atque lectione gravitatem suam amiserint, id quoque iucundissimum lectu mihi fuit. Toto enim praeterito anno admodum anxius eram animi, an illas cum libro VERNERI acceperis. Iam vero liberatus suspicione quadam, quasi pravo studio cuiusdam mei aemuli interceptae et suppressae fuissent, loco illius anxietatis longe maiorem sentio voluptatem. Quod autem tardius responderis, in mitiorem interpretor id partem, quomodo sanctissima amicitiae iura postulant, ita ut nulla opus sit hac in re apud me excusatione: qui, si quis alius, quantisnam occupationibus distineare, optime novi, neque causam iustam habeo, ut ex tardiore responsione de tuo in me amore quicquam addubitem. Sed et diuturniusculum, liceat ita tecum loqui, illud silentium abunde compensasti, non tantum bene longa epistola, rebus pulcherrimis utilissimisque referta, sed duplicis quoque scriptionis labore: nimirum ideo, ut si alteram contingeret perire, altera saltem conservaretur et sarta tectaque redderetur. Accidit autem, quod commodum utraque felici cursu mihi reddita est, sed posterius illa, quam ad me vir clariss. D. VINSHEMIUS misit. Qui tuus labor iteratus merito a me requirit, ut ego quoque, pro eo, siquidem studij et benevolentiae plenus est, iterum tibi agam gratias. Huc accedit, quemadmodum ipse merito quereris, quod desint nobis certi homines, quibus nostra tuto et cito perferenda demus. Rarissimi enim sunt, qui vel hinc isthuc, vel isthinc ad nos commeent. Quod si interdum aliqui sese offerunt, tum vel negocia, vel aliae causae, puto absentia ab urbe, aut valetudo adversa, aut ignotus hospes atque viator, facultatem scribendi abripiunt: quomodo anno superiori mihi contigit, cum opportunitate illa scribendi ad te per nobilem quendam vestratem, qui a Rege vestro falcones Imperatori nostro attulerat, minime uti potuerim. Quamobrem inter nos id semper firmum ratumque esto: si deinceps aliqua cessatio ob quamcunque causam ex utravis parte obvenerit, nihilominus nos simus inter nos, quales esse debemus, amicissimo videlicet animo et constantissima voluntate coniuncti.</p><p>Verum hisce sive excusationibus intermissi officij, sive constantis amicitiae ac benevolentiae protestationibus omissis, ad alias potius epistolae partes, in quibus de rebus ipsis mecum agis et tam liberaliter disputas, iam accedo. Quae ut me mirifice delectârunt, ita mihi, scribo ingenue, ut alienus ab ostentatorum simulata sui extenuatione, ruborem et pudorem haud exiguum incusserunt. Nam primum quod tam multa in meam laudem dixeris, quae ingenij mei tenuitas sustinere vix, aut ne vix quidem potest, id ego totum amori in me tuo tuaeque praestanti eximiaeve humanitati adscribo, simul etiam gaudeo, me abs te sic diligi, et tuam tamque praestantem ac egregiam erga me voluntatem permagni facio. Sed rursum, ut, memor illius, quod descendisse de coelo fertur, Nosce te ipsum, haud parum erubescam, res ipsa cogit, et pudorem mihi vel invito extorquet. Etenim quis ego sum, mi optatissime TYCHO, qua doctrina, quibus ornamentis praeditus, cui ab homine nobilissimo, doctissimo et optimo, tantum laudis tribuatur? Si quid olim in me, sive studij, sive diligentiae in disciplinis Mathematicis, in quibus tu ita excellis, ut inter artifices et restauratores merito summum locum obtineas, aut fuit, aut etiamnum est, in ista ingravescente mea aetate, id fuit, profecto, estque hoc tempore, tam perexiguum, ut infra mediocritatem vix consistere queat. Ut taceam, quod curae domesticae, medica professio, ingravescens, ut dixi, aetas (superavi enim iam DEI beneficio annum magnum climacterem) multa ex eis mihi excusserunt. Itaque licet adhuc libere interdum meam sententiam de his profero: tamen mihi nihil arrogo, sed velut homo veritatis amantissimus rectius sentientibus non gravatim subscribo: cuius rei te unicum, instar omnium locupletissimum testem adduco. Quamobrem gratissimum fuit auditu et admirabile visum, quod ad alia, sicut scribis, te ex parallaxibus ♂, quando fuit in opposito ☉, viciniorem terris ipso Sole deprehendisse; quod non nisi ex COPERNICI hypothesibus, vel ex tuis, nuper abs te excogitatis, salvari potuisse asseris: idque non temere, sed certis rationibus confirmatus. Ad quam rem etiamsi non intenderim animum, tamen tibi assentiri potius quam tuas exquisitas observationes in dubium vocare malo, et dico cum SOCRATE, quem, cum legeret librum HERACLITI, dixisse ferunt: Quae intelligo proba sunt, credo et quae non intellexi. Tuo autem illi plagiario ut patrociner, absit, sed tantum gaudeo, quod eius plagium calcaris loco tibi ad rem tam praeclaram et te solo tantoque artifice dignam sit factum. Quod de tuis hypothesibus varij varia tulerint, aut etiamnum ferant iudicia, nil mirum. Nova enim omnia facile reprensionem incurrunt apud eos, qui alienis oculis vident, alienis auribus audiunt, alienis ratiunculis, de veritate parum soliciti, ducuntur et idcirco ab assuetis aegre se divelli patiuntur, imo ab illis discedere piaculum iudicant. Ego tamen in ea sum sententia, de aliorum laboribus non esse temere ferendum iudicium, quia doctorum virorum, ut libera esse solent, ita vera esse debent, de rebus praesertim abstrusis iudicia, quemadmodum hac in parte Magnificus D. IACOBUS CURTIUS, ab arcanis consilijs Imperatoris et Procancellarius Imperij dignissimus, dominus et amicus meus singulariter observandus, et alij quidam prudenter et sapienter se gesserunt, qua de re ipse D. CURTIUS plenius ad te perscribet. Est enim, ut in rebus omnibus, ita in hisce Mathematicis studijs vir iudicio acri et perspicacissimo praeditus.</p><p>De NICOLAO WINKLERO non est cur respondeam, vel tecum agam pluribus. Valeat, et negligatur sane. Nihil enim ab illo sperari potest: de quo, quod ne quidem prima elementa Astronomiae didicerit, satis liquet: cuiusmodi fere omnes aut saltem maior pars eorum sunt, isti triobolares Calendariopaei, vates mendacissimi. An iste peculiare aliquod scriptum de Cometa anni 80 ediderit, nunquam intelligere potui. Recitavit saltem illas absurdas parallaxes in vanissimo suo Prognostico anni 82. Illum etiam alterum perturbatum, perplexum, obscurum et arrogantem, et suas nugas pro magna sapientia et exquisita doctrina nobis venditantem, missum faciamus. Per nos suis nugis intricatissimis se oblectet. Utinam saltem eius amicitia tecum non sit fucata, et non ferat secum quasdam benevolentiae praestigias.</p><p>Quod MAeSTHLINUM contra me defenderis, sed ego illam defensionem impugnaverim, non feci id, mihi crede, mi TYCHO, vel dissensionis studio, vel quod veritatem agnoscere noluerim, aut quod existimaverim, te eam defensionem alieniore erga me animo suscepisse, aut denique quod MAeSTHLINO studiose contradicere in animum induxerim: quem ego non solum ob eius praeclaram eruditionem atque doctrinam et in Mathematicis studijs solidam peritiam, mirificamque in illis diligentiam, verum etiam ob eius erga me ignotum insignem humanitatem et benevolentiam, adeoque candorem, quod me benigne et perhumaniter in suo illo de Cometa anni 77 scripto monuerit, caput 9 meae Dialexis esse emendandum, ex animo complector atque exosculor, ac pro tam prompto et propenso eius in me animo iam non tantum ut amico, sed ut homini doctissimo, nunc quoque habeo gratiam. Nullo, inquam, alieniore animo vel abs te, vel a MAeSTHLINO dissensi, sed quod placuit libere meam sententiam proferre: ita tamen ut eam meliori iudicio submitterem, quemadmodum tute nosti meum ingenium, quod neque sua pertinaciter tueri, neque viris bonis et ingenio florentibus eorum doctrinae et celebritati famae detrahere soleat. Itaque quod tu in meis non nulla corrigenda et reprehendenda tibi sumpseris, MAeSTHLINUS quoque in quibusdam amanter et modeste monuerit, hoc corrigendi et reprehendendi officium ab utroque vestrûm, quoniam ab ingenuo et benevolo animo profectum esse novi, non potuit non esse gratissimum. Sicut contra non potui non iniquissimo animo ferre atrocia illa convicia et maledicta, quae in me impurissime et impudentissime evomuerunt mastygae illi mei, puta ille impurus, infamis et flagitijs contaminatissimus RAIMUNDUS, et alter ei non absimilis conviciator, GRAMINAeUS Verpus, huiusque succenturiatus LINDANUS, arrogans et petulans Episcopus: qui omnes omissa re, de qua disputatio instituta fuerat, toti ad convicia et maledicta sese converterunt. Sed DEI beneficio omnibus a me ora occlusa sunt, RAIMUNDO quidem terna responsione, GRAMINAeO vero et LINDANO Episcopo manu scripto mei Aquilonis Historici Prodromo, utrique iam ante 9 fere annos transmisso. Quamvis autem cum solo GRAMINAeO fuit mihi suscepta disputatio, siquidem Episcopi illius nuspiam, ne unico verbulo, mentionem feceram, tantum abest, ut alias uspiam aliquam ei inimicitiarum causam praebuerim, aut litem intenderim: tamen cum ipse GRAMINAeO, quasi non satis sufficienti ad maledicendum, se adiunxisset, ut coniunctis viribus me unum omni genere criminationum adorirentur adobruerentque, etiam Episcopo illi seorsim respondi, et pro suo merito hominem conspersit alicubi non lustrali sane aqua, sed salibus, non tam nigro Momi, quam candido Mercurij sale tinctis: et quia me sua reverentia claudum sutorem appellare dignata fuit, ex eadem officina sutoria sutorijs formis et calopodio afflixi caput eius sine metu et periculo excommunicationis. Ubique tamen luculente responsum est, in ipso Aquilone Historico, qui iam multis annis teritur in multorum manibus in Imperio. Est enim tribus secularibus Electoribus inscriptus. Sed huc usque publicari non potuit, clam meo fucato quodam amico Typographum, qui iam initium operis fecerat, ab editione deterrente. Verum apage mihi istos maledicos conviciatores a nostris amicis et homine ingenuo dignis disceptationibus. Quamobrem ad has redeo.</p><p>Non, non, mi TYCHO, mihi molestum vel fuit, vel est, vel erit, si a me in aliquibus dissentias. Par esto libertas pro veritate inter nos dissentiendi, donec hanc liquido assequemur. Cum igitur tu quoque impugnando licet me nondum mihi videaris satisfacere, amice et candide meam adhuc tibi exponam sententiam. Dixeram in meis ad te, et hanc thesim constitueram: Cometarum motus, quomodo Planetarum, certa lege astringi non posse. Tu contra, etsi fateris quidem Cometas non ita Solem respicere, quemadmodum reliqui Planetae eum respiciunt, et propterea digressiones ab eo non ubique consimili lege exhibere, imo, ut ipse ais, neque Lunam eam legem observare: negas tamen ex eo inferri posse, impossibile esse, Cometarum motus ad aliquam normam per convenientem hypothesim redigi posse. Difficultatem itaque in ea re confiteris, impossibilitatem vero negas, propterea quod ex aliqua portione circuli de tota circumferentia ratiocinatio sumi possit. Est omnino, ut dicis, mi TYCHO, quod quae difficilia sunt, ea impossibilia non sunt, quemadmodum et Comicus quodam in loco testatur: nihil tam arduum vel difficile esse, quin quaerendo investigari possit. Et ARISTOT. 1 Rhetor. illud difficile esse dicit, quod cum labore aut multo tempore fit. At cum eorum quaedam fint, quae summe difficilia sunt, ea certe pro impossibilibus et habita sunt semper, et habentur etiam hodie, quae tanquam deplorata reijci consueverunt. Sed cum tu de summa illa difficultate non loquaris, sed tantum de ea, quae aliquanto labore superari possit, cuperem sane, eam difficultatem aliqua possibilitate tot Cometarum per multa secula apparitionibus evictam fuisse, et luculente evinci. Nam quae tu de illa impossibilitate scripsisse et dubium illud amovisse scribis, ea non impugno, sed aliorum quoque eruditorum de ijs deque toto hoc negocio iudicium expecto expetoque, ac plurium Cometarum observationibus confirmari cupio, quod ubi factum fuerit, libenter tibi arena cedam.</p><p>Atque haec fuit estque prima mea ratio. Altera est haec: Si omnium Cometarum motus, quotquot hucusque vel emerserunt, vel emersuri sunt, ad certam aliquam formam redigendi sunt, ut certa illorum scientia haberi possit, prius singulorum Cometarum habendam esse cognitionem, quomodo Planetarum et fixarum stellarum. Universalia enim ex singularibus colliguntur: quae singularia cum sint infinita, persequi non possunt, et si unum deest individuum, non constabit universale. Tertia ratio, quia Cometae nova sunt lumina, et numero differunt, non uno et eodem coeli loco emergunt, aliquando uni loco coeli affixa et quasi stationaria visa sunt, aliquando lento, aliquando concitatissimo motu eoque inordinato feruntur et quasi rapiuntur, rarissimeque perfectos describunt circulos. Dices, haec omnia fere omnibus competere Planetis, nihilominus eorum periodos cognitas esse. Ita est sane, dilecte TYCHO: sed cum Planetae sint semper ijdem, certisque legibus ferantur, eorum integrae periodi observari poterant. Cometae vero quotquot huc usque fulgere visi sunt, alij et alij fuerunt nec statis temporibus effulserunt, nec integros perfectosque descripserunt circulos, neque id, quod sciam, a quoquam memoriae proditum est. In tanta diversitate quid obsecro facies? Etiamsi vero alicuius Cometae motus fuisset accuratius observatus, sive eius integra periodus, quod tamen nemo prodidit, sive aliqua saltem portio circuli, ex qua, quomodo in stellis inerrantibus, de integra eius revolutione iudicium sumi possit: tamen quis concesserit, ut hinc de quorumvis Cometarum motu constituatur? In stellis inerrantibus, quas tu pro confirmatione tuae thesis meae thesi contrariae adducis, nimirum quamvis illae nullam periodum a condito mundo absolverint et a tempore observationum vix sextam circuli partem huc usque emensae sint, nihilominus certa quaedam ratio motus apparentis explorari potuit. Hoc quidem ita se habere lubens concedo. Sed vide dissimilitudinem. Stellae fixae sunt semper eaedem et perpetuae. Quo loco coeli olim deprehensae sunt temporibus HIPPARCHI, illae eaedem deinceps consideratae sunt successivis seculis a PTOLEMAeO, ALBATEGNIO, COPERNICO, et ex illarum progressu in certo tempore de tota periodo ratiocinatum, adeoque et in circulo anomaliae velocitas et tarditas motus illarum deprehensa est. At quid his simile apparet in Cometis? Cum eorum certitudinem nullam habeamus, nec semper ijdem sint, quomodo stellae, nec statis temporibus emergunt, varij et incerti sunt, nec, ut dixi, integros describunt circulos. Si igitur tuae rationi locus dandus est, existimaverim primo omnium docendum esse, omnes Cometas, quos huc usque conspeximus, fuisse unum et eundem Cometam. Dices iterum: Considero unum Cometam, qui, exempli gratia, decurrit spatio unius diei 5 vel 6 partes circuli, et hinc ratiocinor de tota eius periodo, licet eam non absolvat, quomodo in stellis fixis. Sic cum scio, Lunam diebus singulis 12 partes in uno die percurrere, scio quoque, in quot diebus totum emetietur circulum. Ita quoque de singulis Cometis iudicandum, dum apparent. Respondeo, hoc quidem in Luna evidens esse, quia non tantum diurnus eius motus, sed etiam tota eius periodus deprehendi potuit, et deprehensa est verissime. De Cometis etsi similiter ex motu eius diurno de tota periodo ratiocinari possumus, tamen id erit solum in ratiocinio, non in rei verilate, cum nunquam integrum emetiantur circulum. Deinde cum, ut supra dictum est, inaequalem et inordinatum servent motum, quomodo de integra periodo iuxta rei veritatem constitues? Urges adhuc, inquiens, minorem in Cometarum motu extricando difficultatem subesse, quam in stellis fixis, quia ob celerem eorum motum in uno et brevi tempore facilius ab uno homine illorum motus percipi potest, in affixis vero ut eorum motus cognoscatur, aliquot secula requiruntur. Respondeo, verum quidem esse, quod motus Cometarum facilius, quantum ad unum hominem et temporis brevitatem, deprehendi possunt, quam motus stellarum fixarum, qui et plures homines et multas annorum centurias requirit. Sed ad integram periodum Cometarum et stellarum fixarum cognoscendam et constituendam eadem utrobique apparet difficultas. In Cometis quidem, quia cito evanescunt et nunquam integrum emetiuntur circulum. In fixis vero, quia nunquam ad primas suas sedes revertentur. Imo vero dixerim ego: cum stellarum motus iam propemodum a bis mille annis nobis perspectus sit, Cometarum non item, facilius nos de fixarum periodo quam de Cometarum ratiocinari posse. Vides, mi TYCHO, siquidem ego non erro et recte iudico, nos non tantum difficultate, sed etiam quasi impossibilitate prohiberi, quo minus motus Cometarum certis adstringantur legibus, doctrinaque generalis de eorum motu tradatur. Sed cum tu scribas, te demonstrasse in illo Cometa anni 80, ipsum centri loco habuisse Solem, et circa ipsum instar ♀ et ☿ obambulasse, ego haec accuratius in alijs quoque Cometis consideranda quam temere reprehendenda aut in dubium vocanda esse censeo, et cum SOCRATE, ut supra dixi, credam, quae non intelligam. Itaque nihil amplius his addam, sed pleniorem de his omnibus abs te disputationem expectabo. Interim ut hasce meas amicas nugas boni consulas, rogo.</p><p>Tabulas Astronomicas longe aberrare a vero, compertum est multis, Sed quae causa sit, quod iam a mensibus aliquot motus Saturni et Iovis reformatarum tabularum ab Alphonsino calculo differat paulo minus tribus integris gradibus, tu nobis aliquando in tuis novis hypothesibus explicabis. Ego profecto tantam differentiam in duobus illis Planetis huc usque nunquam animadverti. De Epocha commutationis mediae Cometae anni 77 ad diem 24 Novembris dicis, te eam, quam MAeSTHLINUS posuit, accepisse. At ego ex ipsius dedomenis, subducto calculo, deprehendi eam 15' minorem, quae in exacta supputatione non esse negligenda existimavi, cum ex Epocha constituta totum aedificium exurgat: ac feci id, ut non tantum aedificium a MAeSTHLINO extructum considerarem, sed etiam eius fundamenta examinarem. Sed cum tu differentiam illam pro pusillo errore ducas, ego quoque de ea contendere nolo. Porro differentia illa, quae inter nos est in prostaphaeresi, cum asseras eam provenire ex acceptione diversorum terminorum, nimirum meam respicere 24 Novemb., tuam vero 26 Ianuarij, in tua hac solutione lubens acquiesco. Mihi nunc de ijs novas considerationes suscipere et omnia denuo ad calculum revocare aliae occupationes vetant.</p><p>MUNNOSIJ libellum de nova stella Hispanico idiomate, ab ipsomet olim mihi missum et donatum, habeo, qui tamen eum Latinum faciat, habeo neminem. Sed et mihi nulla consuetudo cum Hispanis. CORNELIUS FRANGIPANUS Italice scripsit, sed quae ad nostrum propositum faciant, plane nihil habet. Alios scriptores novae stellae nullos novi, quemadmodum in mea recognita Dialexi intelligere potuisti, alioqui eos quoque produxissem in medium. APIANI libellum de Cometa anni 31 mitto. Ubi illum evolveris, quod intra unum et alterum diem fieri poterit, remittes ad me per hunc tabellarium. Et cum intelligam, te non amplius egere TRAPEZUNTIJ commentarijs, eos quoque ad me remittes. De bibliotheca WITYCHIJ egi cum quodam meo amico. Sed is nunc legatione fungitur in Poloniam. Ubi redierit, agam cum eo diligentius, teque certiorem faciam. IOHANNIS PAeNAe liber nunquam mihi contigit.</p><p>Quod ad Apologeticam responsionem tuam pertinet, hanc quoque perlegi diligentissime, eaque sane mirifice sum delectatus. Tam solide enim et tam luculente respondes, et obiectiones CRAIGIJ diluis, ut mirum sane videri possit, quid contra tam evidentia vel hiscere CRAIGIUS aut quivis alius possit. Quod si CRAIGIUS perget veritati obstrepere, in longe maiorem incurret reprehensionem apud veritatis amantes, quam quod ego in Nova Stella parum ex arte suo iudicio praestiterim. Sed sit ita, ut ille iudicat: ego certe illud parum multo gloriosius mihi esse duco, quam si manifeste veritati repugnarem. Primus ego, quid alij senserint, ignorans, libera voce et scripto protuli, Novam Stellam non fuisse Cometam, nec in elementari regione, sed in aethere: licet senserim et protulerim fieri posse, ut Cometae in utraque regione, puta elementari et aetherea, oriantur, qua de re aetas posterior rectius iudicabit. Primus etiam labefactavi opinionem ARISTOTELIS de Cometis, quam CRAIGIUS mordicus retinet, et pro ea velut pro aris et focis depugnat. Sunt, fateor, mea, quae tum attuli, admodum exilia, et, quoad doctrinam parallaxium , plurima in eis desiderari. Sed et haec deinceps aliquantulum recognovi. Tu vero his supremam manum imposuisti. Quamobrem tibi libenter arena cedo. Mihi interea apud bonos et probatos ad veniam, non dico ad gratiam, promerendam sufficiet, quod in negocio profecto difficili (quod nemo unquam, quod sciam, aggredi, nec ab opinione ARISTOTELIS discedere, metu obtrectatorum et invidorum, quibus nemo unquam vir bonus ac doctus et veritatis magis quam amicitiae studiosus satisfecerit, ausus est) pro virili laborare studuerit, primusque, ut dixi, in hoc angulo hanc glaciem secuerim, et in incognita via novas orbitas impresserim. Utinam vero CRAIGIUS mea illa exilia saltem umbra veritatis evincere potuisset, habuissem illi gratiam. Sed cum non potuerit, nec poterit unquam, mirum sane non est, quod tua firmissima solidissimaque ne minima quidem in parte labefactârit. Hoc unum egisse video, ut postposita veritate opinionem ARISTOTELIS sartam tectam conservaret constabiliretque. Verum stat pro nobis veritas, licet adhuc deserta et pauper, nec fulta splendido aliquo patrocinio vel veterum vel recentium Philosophorum, efferet tamen suos radios deinceps clarius splendescetque, quantumvis nunc frivolis quibusdam obiectionibus et sophisticis ratiunculis obtenebretur. Certe si quis meliora nostris, quae vel de Nova Stella vel de Cometis protulimus, et abs te solidius multo prolata sunt, afferet, libenter amplectemur. Verum qui nec meliora affert, nec solide aliena refutat, sed tantum rixandi studio contradicit, istud malevoli et invidi hominis est iudicium. Miror, cur CRAIGIUS miscet meam Novam Stellam cum meo Cometa, cum egomet ingenue fassus sim, certitudinem mearum observationum Cometae non posse certare cum certitudine observationum Novae Stellae, neque mihi fuisse ad manum ea instrumenta, neque eas commoditates observandi Cometam, quas habui in Nova Stella. Nosti etiam memet et in Nova Stella et in Cometa, agnita veritate, partim sponte correxisse quaedam, partim abs te et a PRAeTORIO alijsque de quibusdam amice monitus nonnulla emendasse. Magis sane miror, CRAIGIUM illam mutationem sententiae in melius, quasi probro, incertitudini et inconstantiae adscribere, quae virtuti et ingenuitati adscribenda fuit. Turpe enim non est errorem, agnita veritate, corrigere. Sed errorem contra veritatem hominis in gratiam tueri, id vero est turpissimum. Quasi vero ipsa experientia non testetur, priores dies studiorum discipulos posteriorum esse solere. Sed vides, quid non audeant isti, qui in verba sui magistri iurârunt. Omnes etiam Mathematicos, qui Cometas aethereos esse asseruerunt, absurditatis arguit, non animadvertens, dum opinionem ARISTOTELIS mordicus tenere et tueri conatur, nec nostra evertere potest, se ipsum in longe maiores absurditates implicare, quemadmodum abs te eruditissime evincitur. Quid, quod magna audacia et quidem falso accusat praeclarum artificem REGIOMONTANUM, quod huc usque nec ab illo nec ab ullo Mathematicorum fuerit demonstratum, quomodo Cometarum remotio a Terra definienda sit, quando nimirum proprio incedunt gressu, et propterea REGIOMONTANI demonstrationem abire in absurdum? Atqui, optime TYCHO, CRAIGIUS hac in re turpissime fallitur, magnaque iniuria afficit optimum artificem REGIOMONTANUM, qui in suo illo praeclaro de Cometa libello problema 2. 3. 4. diserte monet, in indagatione parallaxeos proprij Cometae motus rationem habendam esse, quemadmodum abs te quoque eius motus ratio habita est. Quare dum ea CRAIGIUS omnibus Mathematicis impingit, tibi quoque non levem facit iniuriam. Ego quidem in eo Cometa et in altero quoque eius motum proprium me neglexisse sciens volensque, lubens fateor. Sed ne ex eius neglectione notabilis et minime ferendus error exurgeret, meas observationes parvulo interiecto tempore institui, in quo non admodum sensibilis variatio motus proprij Cometae fieret: deinde iudicavi, satis esse, consistere in mediocritate et saltem propius collimare ad veritatem, quam ab alijs hucusque factum est: quemadmodum praeclare dixit REGIOMONTANUS: thesauros naturae nos non evacuare, sed in plerisque scibilibus ipsi veritati propinquum degustandum mortalibus concedi: quasi diceret, satis esse in eo gradu subsistere, qui veritatem penitus non evertat. Haec pro defensione REGIOMONTANI, adeoque veritatis, dicere volui. Neque tamen eo inficias, quin melior aliqua ratio vel abs te, vel ab alijs inveniri possit. Interea certitudinem demonstrationum REGIOMONTANI firmam mansuram esse, libere pronuncio, quam neque CRAIGIUS, nec quispiam alius ullius absurditatis convincere poterit. Subolfecit sane REGIOMONTANUS quiddam aliud de Cometis, quam quod scholae ex ARISTOTELE publice docerent. Sed propter eius autoritatem non ausus est a recepta sententia discedere: qui si nunc viveret vidissetque Novam Stellam, sine dubio erubuisset, et accuratiores Cometarum considerationes adhibuisset, nec ullum dubium est apud me, quin praeposita veritate nostrae sententiae subscripsisset. Sed quid ego de his apud te, qui haec omnia multo solidius pertractaveris, ac CRAIGIJ rationes et obiectiones omnes ita dilueris, ut quid illis amplius ad asserendam veritatem adijci possit, non videam. Aliud restat nihil quam ut, pro tua humanitate et candore, mea haec aequi bonique consulas, et ijs, quae a me vel praetermissa, vel minus recte intellecta sunt, veniam des.</p><p>Vale, mi dulcissime TYCHO, et me solito tuo amore fac prosequare.<br>Datae Pragae 11. Iunij novo stylo, qui dies fuit feria secunda Pentecostes Anno 1590.<br>Tui ex animo studiosus<br>THADDAeUS AB HAYCK.</p><p>POST SCRIPTA.IAM absolveram meas ad te, et ecce 22. Maij veteris styli affert ad me<br>tabellarius Norimbergensis tuas unâ<br>cum descriptione Cometæ proximi ac pagellis impressarum Epistolarum<br>Astronomicarum, quas ego statim avide admodum perlegi et perlustravi, et ut<br>D. CURTIUS haberet veluti stimulum<br>maturandi privilegium illud et tabellarium quam primum ad te ablegandi,<br>omnia illa ei legenda et evolvenda dedi. Et profecto placuit illi mirifice<br>indefessum tuum illud studium illustrandi rem Astronomicam, qui, ita me Deus<br>amet, adeo est accensus amore tui, ut diceret, nihil gratius et optatius<br>sibi accidere posse, quam si saltem diebus aliquot tecum colloqui et<br>conferre posset, et certe, si per publica negocia liceret, ad te salutandum<br>et amice complectendum accurreret, egoque me ipsi socium adiungerem.<br>Consilium inquam illud tuum, imo iam eius consilij executio in editione<br>illarum epistolarum, vehementer ei placuit, multa enim præclara in illis a<br>viris eruditis enucleantur et disputando veritas eruitur. Macte animi, quem<br>rarus habet, morumque tuorum, mi TYCHO, et, quo tua te virtus vocat, i pede fausto.</p><p>Dederam ad te literas brevissimas 22. Aprilis stylo veteri per<br>quendam studiosum, qui Rostochium se iturum diceret. Inclusi autem tuas<br>literas ad D. CHYTRÆUM et petij,<br>ut is meas ad te perferri curaret, quod factum esse minime dubito, si modo<br>is studiosus pervenit illuc. Nihil autem aliud continebant meæ, nisi quod<br>per peculiarem tabellarium, quem ipsemet D. CURTIUS propediem se ablegaturum ad te diceret, plenissime<br>de omnibus perscripturi sumus. Rogabam etiam eisdem meis, si fieri possit,<br>ut mihi modellam seu formulam tui Sextantis et Armillarum istic, cum<br>artifices ad manum habeas, fieri curares et ad me mitteres, ad cuius<br>exemplum postea iustæ magnitudinis eadem illa organa mihi hîc confici<br>curarem. Quod cum D. CURTIO<br>significassem, respondit, multo commodius fore, si istic ea organa eadem<br>magnitudine, qua tua sunt, conficerentur et huc vel per tabellarium vel per<br>aurigam mitterentur, se omnes sumptus libentissime erogaturum. Sed, inquit,<br>hæc a D. TYCHONE petere pudore quodam prohibeor. Ego vero te magnopere rogo,<br>si fieri potest, ut istic conficiantur ea organa, fac, ut ea D. CURTIUS habeat et gratificeris ei hac in<br>parte. Impensas omnes liberaliss. persolvet. Ego deinceps ad eorum formam similia mihi confici curârim. Quod si fiet, et<br>nos ijsdem tecum organis utemur, in observationibus mutuas poterimus tradere<br>operas.</p><p>Cometam illum bis tantum mihi videre contigit, primum die 25.<br>Febr. veteris styli. Stetit illo die inter primam et secundam stellam<br>Trianguli, videbaturque esse in recta linea primæ et secundæ stellæ in<br>cornibus Arietis et primæ Trianguli. Caudam, admodum<br>tenuem et vix perceptibilem, instar styli seu hastæ, porrigebat pone 2<br>Trianguli, dirigebatque versus sinistram plantam Andromedæ. Altero die illum<br>quoto que conspexi, sed non satis bene ob intercurrentes nebulas. Accepto<br>itaque meo Radio accingebam me ad dimetiendas eius a vicinis stellis<br>distantias, ecce vero, obtegunt mihi eum nebulæ, nec postea alijs diebus eum<br>videre potui. Aliud igitur, quod de eo scribam, prorsus habeo nihil, neque<br>ullius scriptum de eo legi, neque audivi a quopiam observatum.</p><p>Tuarum epistolarum ad me plures habeo, sed inter illas tres<br>sunt, in quibus mecum agis de rebus Astronomicis. Itaque illarum autographa<br>mitto ad te. In epistola Anni 80, ut videbis, cancellavi nonnulla, quæ vel<br>omittenda vel mutanda esse censui. In meis quoque ad te tollenda erunt<br>quædam, præsertim quæ de SCULTETI<br>concepto erga me odio narrant, et similia, quæ omnia tuæ prudentiæ relinquo<br>tollenda aut mutanda. Corriges etiam in eisdem meis ad te, quas vel mea manu<br>vel aliena scripsi, grammaticalia quædam vitia per incuriam commissa. Ecce<br>vero, dum inter meas chartas aliud quiddam quæro, invenio unam adhuc tuam<br>epistolam et iam propemodum laceram, in qua mecum agis de meis<br>observationibus, de distantia XI in Cassiopæa a polari, de altitudine<br>meridiana Spicæ et de alijs. Eam epistolam describi curavi. Tu facies cum<br>ea, sicut cum alijs, pro tuo arbitrio.</p><p>Sub finem tuæ epistolæ, in qua de luculentiori Privilegio mecum<br>ac agebas, addebas, te exemplum Gallici Privilegij ad me mittere. Sed scito,<br>nullum fuisse tuis literis adiunctum. Ob alias tuas occupationes gravissimas<br>te eius fuisse oblitum, facile mihi persuadeo. Si illius exemplum<br>habuissemus, fuisset sane nobis gratissimum. Dedissemus operam, ne Cæsareum<br>humilius minusque luculentum fieret. Nihilominus non viso illo Gallico<br>Cæsareum studio et industria D. CURTIJ, habita ratione virtutis eruditionisque tuæ, adeoque<br>indefessorum laborum in restituenda et illustranda Astronomia, ita confectum<br>esse existimamus, ut tibi non posset non esse longe gratissimum. Pragæ feria<br>2 Pentecostes.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Thaddaeus Hagecius to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I have at last received, most noble and most learned <b>Tycho</b>, your most longed-for letter — most welcome on many counts, not only because it was written most affectionately by you, the most affectionate of all [my friends], but also because it was crammed with the most beautiful disquisition on Astronomical matters. In which, while you recall, with sweetest recollection, the beginnings of the friendship entered into between us, you betray therein the goodness of your nature. And lest, by your silence (however slight), those little fires of our mutual love should seem in any way to have been lulled to sleep, you therefore wished prudently to rekindle them anew by that most kind and most delightful recollection of our contracted friendship, and by a most learned exposition of many useful matters. All of which came to me so welcome and so acceptable that nothing more welcome could have befallen me — at this time especially; so far is it [from being the case] that their prolixity was in any part troublesome to me. Rather, that erudite disputation undertaken by you so kindled in me a desire for that subject, and aroused so wondrous a pleasure in my mind, that it irked me not — once and again, nay so many times over — to read through that whole disputation. For which truly supreme kindness of yours toward me, and for so sweet and delightful and useful a conversation by letter, I give and hold you the greatest thanks I can. Furthermore, that none of my <b>gatherings</b> [<i>quaterni</i>], which you write you have received, either perished or lost their weight by being unsealed and read, was also a most delightful thing for me to read. For throughout the whole past year I was very anxious in mind whether you had received them along with <b>Werner's</b> book. But now, freed from a certain suspicion — as though they had been intercepted and suppressed by the depraved zeal of some rival of mine — in place of that anxiety I feel a far greater pleasure. And that you replied rather tardily, I interpret in the milder sense, as the most sacred laws of friendship demand — so that there is no need in this matter of any excuse with me, who, if anyone, know best by what great occupations you are held; nor have I just cause to doubt anything of your love toward me on account of a rather tardy reply. But that somewhat long silence (if I may so speak with you) you have abundantly compensated, not only by a good long letter, crammed with the most beautiful and useful matters, but also by the labour of a double writing — namely so that, if it should happen that one perished, the other at least might be preserved and rendered safe and sound. And it happened, conveniently, that both were delivered to me by a happy course; but the later one [reached me] which the most distinguished man, <b>Master Vinshemius</b>, sent to me. Which redoubled labour of yours rightly requires of me that I too, in turn (since it is full of zeal and benevolence), should again give you thanks. To this is added — as you yourself rightly complain — that we lack reliable men, to whom we may give our [letters] to be carried safely and swiftly. For very rare are those who travel either from here to there, or from there to us. And if sometimes some present themselves, then either business, or other causes — absence from the city, I think, or ill health, or an unknown host and traveller — snatch away the opportunity of writing: as happened to me last year, when I was quite unable to use that opportunity of writing to you through a certain nobleman of your country, who had brought <b>falcons</b> from your King to our Emperor. Wherefore let this always be firm and ratified between us: if hereafter any interruption, for whatever cause, should occur on either side, nonetheless let us be between ourselves what we ought to be — joined, that is, in most affectionate mind and most constant will.</p><p>But, these protestations being omitted — whether of excuses for an interrupted duty, or of constant friendship and benevolence — I now proceed rather to the other parts of your letter, in which you deal with me about the matters themselves and dispute so liberally. Which, as they delighted me wondrously, so — I write frankly, being a stranger to the feigned self-deprecation of show-offs — they struck no small blush and shame into me. For first, that you said so many things in my praise, which the slenderness of my talent can scarcely, or not even scarcely, sustain — all this I ascribe wholly to your love toward me and to your outstanding and exceptional kindness; and at the same time I rejoice that I am so esteemed by you, and I value most highly your so outstanding and excellent goodwill toward me. But again — that, mindful of that [saying] which is reported to have descended from heaven, <b>\"Know thyself,\"</b> I should blush not a little — the matter itself compels, and wrings shame from me even against my will. For who am I, my most longed-for Tycho — with what learning, with what ornaments endowed — that so much praise should be bestowed [on me] by a man most noble, most learned, and best? If ever there was in me, or even now is — in this my failing age — any zeal or diligence in the Mathematical disciplines (in which you so excel that you deservedly hold the highest place among the artificers and <b>restorers</b>), it was, indeed, and is at this time, so very slight that it can scarcely stand below mediocrity. To say nothing of how domestic cares, the medical profession, [and] my failing age (as I said) — for I have now, by God's kindness, passed the <b>grand climacteric year</b> — have shaken many of those things out of me. And so, although I still sometimes freely offer my opinion about these matters, yet I arrogate nothing to myself, but, like a man most loving of truth, I subscribe without reluctance to those who judge more rightly — of which thing I bring you forward as a single witness, [yet] as rich as all [witnesses together]. Wherefore it was most welcome to hear, and seemed admirable, that — as you write — you have, among other things, discovered from <b>the parallaxes of Mars [♂], when it was in opposition to the Sun [☉], that it is nearer to the Earth than the Sun itself</b>; which you assert could be saved only from <b>Copernicus's</b> hypotheses, or from your own, recently devised by you — and that not rashly, but confirmed by sure reasons. To which matter, even if I have not bent my mind, yet I prefer to assent to you rather than to call your exquisite observations into doubt; and I say with <b>Socrates</b> — who, when he was reading the book of <b>Heraclitus</b>, is reported to have said: \"What I understand is sound; I believe also what I have not understood.\" But far be it from me to plead for that <b>plagiarist</b> of yours; I only rejoice that his plagiarism has served you, in place of a spur, toward a matter so splendid and worthy of you alone, [and of] so great an artificer. That various men have brought, or even now bring, various judgments about your hypotheses — no wonder. For all new things easily incur <b>censure</b> among those who see with others' eyes, hear with others' ears, are led by others' little reasonings (little caring about the truth), and therefore suffer themselves to be torn from their accustomed [notions] with difficulty — nay, judge it a sacrilege to depart from them. I, however, am of this opinion: that judgment about others' labours must not be rashly passed, because the judgments of learned men, as they are wont to be free, so they ought to be true — especially about abstruse matters — just as in this respect the Magnificent <b>Master Jacob Curtius</b> [Jakob Kurz von Senftenau], most worthy privy Councillor of the Emperor and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, my lord and singularly-to-be-esteemed friend, and certain others have conducted themselves prudently and wisely — about which matter Master Curtius himself will write to you more fully. For he is, as in all things, so in these Mathematical studies, a man endowed with keen and most perspicacious judgment.</p><p>About <b>Nicolaus Winkler</b> there is no reason why I should reply, or deal with you at greater length. Let him fare well, and be quite neglected. For nothing can be hoped from him: of whom it is clear enough that he has not even learned the first elements of Astronomy — of which kind are almost all, or at least the greater part, of them, those <b>threepenny calendar-mongers</b>, most lying soothsayers. Whether he has published any particular writing about the <b>comet of [15]80</b>, I have never been able to learn. He at least recited those absurd <b>parallaxes</b> in his most empty <b>Prognostication of [15]82</b>. That other [man] too — disordered, perplexed, obscure and arrogant, and peddling his trifles to us as great wisdom and exquisite learning — let us dismiss. Let him delight himself, for all we care, with his most intricate trifles. Would only that his friendship with you be not painted-over, and that it carry with it no sleights of benevolence.</p><p>That you defended <b>Maestlin</b> against me, but I attacked that defence — believe me, my Tycho, I did it neither from a zeal for dissent, nor because I was unwilling to acknowledge the truth, nor because I supposed that you had undertaken that defence with a mind rather estranged from me, nor, finally, because I had resolved to contradict Maestlin studiously: whom I embrace and cherish from my heart — not only for his splendid erudition and learning and solid skill in Mathematical studies, and his wondrous diligence in them, but also for the remarkable kindness and benevolence shown toward me (a stranger), and indeed [for] the candour with which he kindly and most humanely advised me, in that writing of his on the <b>comet of [15]77</b>, that chapter 9 of my <b><i>Dialexis</b></i> [is] to be corrected — and for so ready and well-disposed a mind toward me, I now hold him grateful thanks, not only as a friend but as a most learned man. With no estranged mind, I say, did I dissent either from you or from Maestlin, but because it pleased [me] to offer my opinion freely — yet in such a way that I submitted it to better judgment, as you yourself know my disposition, which is wont neither to maintain its own [views] obstinately, nor to detract from the learning and celebrity of fame of good men flourishing in talent. And so, that you took upon yourself to correct and reprehend some things in mine, [and] that Maestlin too, in certain [things], advised lovingly and modestly — this duty of correcting and reprehending, since I know it proceeded from an ingenuous and benevolent mind, could not but be most welcome from each of you. Just as, on the contrary, I could not but bear with a most resentful mind those atrocious revilings and slanders which those whips of mine spewed out against me most foully and most shamelessly — namely that impure, infamous, and most-contaminated-with-disgraces <b>Raimundus</b> [Annibale Raimondo], and another reviler not unlike him, <b>Graminaeus</b> the <b>Verpus</b> [a coarse insult], and his reserve-man <b>Lindanus</b>, an arrogant and petulant <b>Bishop</b>: all of whom, the matter being dropped about which the disputation had been instituted, turned themselves wholly to revilings and slanders. But by God's kindness their mouths have all been stopped by me — Raimundus, indeed, by a threefold response; but Graminaeus and Bishop Lindanus by the manuscript <b><i>Prodromus</i> of my <i>Historical Aquilo</b></i>, transmitted to each of them now nearly 9 years ago. And although the disputation undertaken by me was with Graminaeus alone — since I had nowhere, not by a single little word, made mention of that Bishop, so far was I from having anywhere given him any cause of enmities, or having brought a suit — yet, since that [Bishop] himself had joined himself to Graminaeus (as if [the latter were] not sufficient for reviling), so that with combined forces they might assail and overwhelm me alone with every kind of accusation, I replied to that Bishop too separately, and besprinkled the man, according to his desert, here and there not, indeed, with lustral water, but with salts dyed not so much with the black salt of <b>Momus</b> [the carping god] as with the white salt of <b>Mercury</b> [wit]: and because his Reverence deigned to call me a <b>lame cobbler</b>, from the same cobbler's workshop, with cobbler's lasts and a shoemaker's foot-block, I struck his head, without fear and danger of excommunication. Everywhere, however, an ample reply has been made, in the <b><i>Historical Aquilo</b></i> itself, which has now for many years been thumbed in many hands in the Empire. For it is inscribed [dedicated] to three secular <b>Electors</b>. But hitherto it could not be published, a certain painted-over [false] friend of mine secretly deterring the Printer (who had already made a beginning of the work) from the edition. But away with those slanderous revilers from our friendly disputations, worthy of an ingenuous man! Wherefore I return to these [matters].</p><p>No, no, my Tycho, it neither was, nor is, nor will be troublesome to me, if you dissent from me in some things. Let there be equal liberty, for truth's sake, of dissenting between us, until we plainly attain it. Since therefore you too, by attacking [me], do not yet seem to me to satisfy me, I shall still expound my opinion to you, in friendly and candid fashion. I had said in mine to you, and had established this thesis: that <b>the motions of comets cannot, like those of the Planets, be bound by a fixed law</b>. You, on the contrary — although you do indeed confess that comets do not regard the Sun in the way the other Planets regard it, and that therefore they do not everywhere exhibit their digressions from it by a like law (nay, as you yourself say, that not even the Moon observes that law) — yet you deny that it can be inferred from this that it is impossible for the motions of comets to be reduced to some norm by a suitable <b>hypothesis</b>. The <b>difficulty</b> in that matter you therefore confess, but the <b>impossibility</b> you deny — because from some portion of a circle a reasoning can be taken about the whole circumference. It is altogether [true], as you say, my Tycho, that things which are difficult are not [therefore] impossible — as the <b>Comic</b> [poet] too testifies in a certain place: that nothing is so arduous or difficult but that it can be tracked down by seeking. And <b>Aristotle</b>, in <i>Rhetoric</i> I, says that to be difficult which is done with labour or much time. But since there are certain of those [things] which are supremely difficult, these have certainly always been held, and are held even today, as impossible — which men are wont to reject as hopeless. But since you speak not of that supreme difficulty, but only of that which can be overcome by some labour, I should truly wish that that difficulty had been overcome, by some possibility, through the apparitions of so many comets across many centuries, and [that this] be amply proven. For the things which you write you have written about that impossibility, and [whereby you] have removed that doubt — these I do not attack; but I await and seek the judgment of other learned men too about them and about this whole business, and I desire [it] to be confirmed by the observations of more comets — which, when it shall have been done, I will gladly <b>yield you the field</b>.</p><p>And this was, and is, my first reason. The second is this: if the motions of all comets — as many as have hitherto either emerged or are to emerge — are to be reduced to some fixed form, so that a sure science of them can be had, [then] the knowledge of the individual comets must first be had, as [it is] of the Planets and the fixed stars. For universals are gathered from particulars: which particulars, since they are infinite, cannot be pursued; and if one individual is lacking, the universal will not stand. The third reason: because comets are <b>new lights</b>, and differ in number; they emerge not in one and the same place of the heaven; sometimes they have been seen affixed to one place of the heaven and as it were stationary; sometimes they are borne — and as it were snatched along — with a slow, sometimes a most rapid and (at that) disordered motion; and most rarely do they describe perfect circles. You will say: all these belong to almost all the Planets, and nonetheless their periods are known. So it is indeed, beloved Tycho: but since the Planets are always the same, and are borne by fixed laws, their whole periods could be observed. But the comets, as many as have hitherto been seen to blaze, were [each] different and different; nor did they blaze at fixed times, nor did they describe whole and perfect circles — nor (so far as I know) has this been handed down to memory by anyone. In so great a diversity, what, I beseech, will you do? But even if the motion of some comet had been observed more accurately — whether its whole period (which, however, no one has reported), or at least some portion of a circle, from which (as in the fixed stars) a judgment about its whole revolution could be taken — yet who would grant that from this [the motion] of any comets whatever be determined? In the fixed stars, which you adduce for the confirmation of your thesis (contrary to my thesis) — namely, although they have completed no period since the founding of the world, and since the time of observations have measured scarcely a <b>sixth part of a circle</b> down to now — nonetheless a certain sure reckoning of the apparent motion could be explored. This indeed I gladly concede to be so. But see the dissimilarity. The fixed stars are always the same and perpetual. In what place of the heaven they were once ascertained in the times of <b>Hipparchus</b>, those same [stars] were thereafter considered, through successive centuries, by <b>Ptolemy</b>, <b>Albategnius</b> [al-Battānī], [and] <b>Copernicus</b>; and from their progress in a certain time it was reasoned out about the whole period, and so too the swiftness and slowness of their motion in the circle of <b>anomaly</b> was ascertained. But what like these appears in comets? Since we have no certainty of them, nor are they always the same (as the stars [are]), nor do they emerge at fixed times, [but] are various and uncertain, nor (as I said) do they describe whole circles. If therefore place is to be given to your reasoning, I should judge that it must first of all be taught that all the comets which we have hitherto beheld were one and the same comet. You will say again: I consider one comet, which, for example, runs through 5 or 6 parts of a circle in the space of one day, and hence I reason about its whole period, although it does not complete it — as in the fixed stars. So, since I know that the <b>Moon</b> traverses <b>12 parts</b> in a single day, I know also in how many days it will measure out the whole circle. So too one must judge about individual comets, while they appear. I reply: this is indeed evident in the Moon, because not only its diurnal motion, but also its whole period, could be — and most truly has been — ascertained. About comets, although we can likewise reason about the whole period from its diurnal motion, yet that will be only in reasoning, not in the <b>truth of the matter</b>, since they never measure out a whole circle. Then, since (as was said above) they keep an unequal and disordered motion, how will you determine [anything] about the whole period according to the truth of the matter? You press still further, saying that there is less difficulty in disentangling the motion of comets than [that] of the fixed stars, because, on account of their swift motion, their motions can be perceived more easily, in one and a brief time, by one man — whereas in the affixed [stars], that their motion may be known, several centuries are required. I reply: it is indeed true that the motions of comets can be ascertained more easily — as regards one man and the brevity of time — than the motions of the fixed stars, which require both several men and many centuries of years. But for knowing and establishing the <b>whole period</b> of comets and of the fixed stars, the same difficulty appears on both sides: in the comets, because they vanish quickly and never measure out a whole circle; in the fixed [stars], because they will never return to their first seats. Nay, I should even say: since the motion of the stars has now been perceived by us for nearly two thousand years, [but] that of comets not likewise, we can more easily reason about the period of the fixed [stars] than about [that] of comets. You see, my Tycho — if I do not err and judge rightly — that we are prevented, not only by difficulty but as it were by <b>impossibility</b>, from binding the motions of comets by fixed laws, and from a general doctrine about their motion being handed down. But since you write that you have demonstrated, in that <b>comet of [15]80</b>, that it had the <b>Sun in the place of [its] centre</b>, and that it circled about it after the manner of <b>Venus [♀] and Mercury [☿]</b> — I judge that these things are to be considered more accurately in other comets too, rather than rashly reprehended or called into doubt; and with Socrates, as I said above, I will believe what I do not understand. And so I shall add nothing further to these [matters], but shall await a fuller disputation about all of them from you. Meanwhile, I beg, take these my friendly trifles in good part.</p><p>That the <b>Astronomical Tables</b> stray far from the truth has been found by many. But what the cause may be that for some months now the motion of <b>Saturn</b> and <b>Jupiter</b>, [according to] the reformed tables, differs from the <b>Alphonsine</b> calculation by a little less than three whole degrees — you will some time explain to us in your new hypotheses. I, certainly, have never hitherto noticed so great a difference in those two Planets. About the <b>Epoch of the mean commutation of the comet of [15]77</b> at the day <b>24 November</b>, you say that you took the one which <b>Maestlin</b> posited. But I, from his own <b>data</b> [δεδομένα], the calculation having been worked, found it <b>15′ smaller</b> — which I judged not to be neglected in an exact computation, since from the established Epoch the whole edifice rises up; and I did this so that I might consider not only the edifice built by Maestlin, but also examine its foundations. But since you regard that difference as a tiny error, I too am unwilling to contend about it. Furthermore, that difference which is between us in the <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> — since you assert that it arises from the adoption of different <b>terms</b>, namely [that] mine regards <b>24 November</b>, but yours <b>26 January</b> — in this your solution I gladly acquiesce. From taking up new considerations about these [matters] now, and recalling everything anew to calculation, other occupations forbid me.</p><p>The little book of <b>Muñoz</b> [Jerónimo Muñoz] on the new star, in the Spanish tongue, once sent and given to me by [the author] himself, I have; but one to make it Latin I have none. And besides, I have no familiarity with Spaniards. <b>Cornelius Frangipanus</b> [Cornelio Frangipane] wrote in Italian, but he has plainly nothing that makes for our purpose. Other writers on the new star I know none — as you could understand in my revised <i>Dialexis</i>; otherwise I would have brought them too into the open. I send <b>Apian's</b> little book on the <b>comet of [15]31</b>. When you have unrolled it (which can be done within a day or two), you will send it back to me by this courier. And since I understand that you no longer need the commentaries of <b>Trapezuntius</b> [George of Trebizond], you will send those back to me too. About <b>Wittich's library</b> I dealt with a certain friend of mine. But he is now discharging a <b>legation into Poland</b>. When he has returned, I shall deal with him more diligently, and inform you. The book of <b>Johannes Pena</b> never came my way.</p><p>As for your <b>Apologetic response</b>, I have read this too most diligently, and I was wondrously delighted with it. For you reply so solidly and so amply, and dissolve the objections of <b>Craig</b>, that it can truly seem a wonder what Craig — or anyone else — could even mutter against things so evident. And if Craig shall persist in clamouring against the truth, he will incur a far greater reproach among the lovers of truth than [I incurred], in [the matter of] the New Star, by having performed too little, in his judgment, \"by [the rules of] art.\" But be it as he judges: I, certainly, hold that \"too little\" to be far more glorious to me than if I manifestly fought against the truth. I was the <b>first</b> — ignorant of what others had thought — who brought forward, with free voice and writing, that the <b>New Star was not a Comet, nor in the elementary region, but in the aether</b>: though I thought and brought forward that it can happen that comets arise in either region — namely the elementary and the aethereal — about which the later age will judge more rightly. I was also the <b>first</b> to shake the opinion of <b>Aristotle</b> about comets, which Craig retains tooth-and-nail, and fights for it as if for altar and hearth. They are, I confess, mine — the things I then brought forward — very slight, and, as to the <b>doctrine of parallaxes</b>, very many things are wanting in them. But these too I afterwards somewhat revised. You, however, have put the finishing hand to them. Wherefore I gladly yield you the field. To me, meanwhile, it will suffice — among the good and approved, for earning pardon (I do not say favour) — that, in a business assuredly difficult (which no one ever, so far as I know, dared to attempt, nor to depart from Aristotle's opinion, from fear of detractors and the envious, whom no good and learned man, more zealous for truth than for friendship, ever satisfied), I strove to labour to the best of my power, and was the first (as I said) in this corner to cut this ice, and to impress new tracks on an unknown road. But would that Craig had been able to overthrow those slight [contributions] of mine at least by a shadow of truth — I should have held him grateful. But since he could not, nor ever will be able, it is truly no wonder that he has not shaken your most firm and most solid [arguments] even in the least part. This one thing I see him to have done: that, the truth set aside, he might preserve and establish the opinion of Aristotle safe and sound. But the truth stands for us — though still deserted and poor, and not propped by any splendid patronage of the old or recent Philosophers; yet it will hereafter cast forth its rays more clearly and shine, however much it be now overshadowed by certain frivolous objections and sophistical little reasonings. Surely, if anyone shall bring forward better [things] than ours — which we have brought forward whether about the New Star or about Comets, and [which] have been brought forward far more solidly by you — we shall gladly embrace [them]. But he who neither brings better, nor solidly refutes another's, but only contradicts from a zeal for wrangling — that is the judgment of a malevolent and envious man. I marvel why Craig <b>mixes</b> my New Star with my Comet, since I myself frankly confessed that the certainty of my observations of the Comet cannot vie with the certainty of the observations of the New Star, and that I had neither those instruments at hand, nor those conveniences for observing the Comet, which I had in [the matter of] the New Star. You know too that I myself, both in the New Star and in the Comet — the truth being acknowledged — partly corrected certain things of my own accord, partly emended some, amicably advised about certain [things] by you and by <b>Praetorius</b> [Johannes Praetorius] and others. I marvel the more that Craig ascribes that change of opinion for the better — as if [it were] a reproach — to uncertainty and inconstancy, [a change] which ought to have been ascribed to virtue and ingenuousness. For it is not shameful to correct an error when the truth is acknowledged. But to defend an error against the truth, to please a man, is indeed most shameful. As if experience itself did not testify that earlier days are wont to be the disciples of later ones! But you see what those men do not dare, who have <b>sworn upon the words of their master</b>. He also accuses of absurdity all the Mathematicians who have asserted comets to be aethereal — not noticing that, while he tries to hold and defend Aristotle's opinion tooth-and-nail, and cannot overthrow ours, he himself becomes entangled in far greater absurdities, as is most learnedly proven by you. What [of this], that with great audacity, and indeed falsely, he accuses the splendid artificer <b>Regiomontanus</b> — [claiming] that hitherto it has been demonstrated neither by him nor by any of the Mathematicians how the distance of comets from the Earth is to be defined, since, namely, they advance with their own [proper] step, and that therefore Regiomontanus's demonstration goes off into absurdity? But, best Tycho, Craig in this matter is most shamefully deceived, and does great injury to the excellent artificer Regiomontanus, who, in that splendid little book of his <b>on the Comet</b>, in <b>problems 2, 3, [and] 4</b>, expressly advises that, in the investigation of <b>parallax</b>, account must be taken of the comet's own [proper] <b>motion</b> — as the reckoning of its motion was also had by you. Wherefore, while Craig charges these [things] upon all Mathematicians, he does no light injury to you too. I, indeed, freely confess that in that comet, and in the other one too, I knowingly and willingly neglected its proper motion. But lest from that neglect a notable and by-no-means-tolerable error should arise, I instituted my observations with a tiny interval of time interposed, in which no very perceptible variation of the comet's proper motion would occur; then I judged it enough to stand in the mean, and at least to take aim nearer to the truth than has hitherto been done by others — as Regiomontanus splendidly said: <b>that we do not empty out the treasures of nature, but that, in most knowable things, it is granted to mortals to taste [something] near to the truth itself</b> — as if he were saying that it is enough to subsist in that degree which does not utterly overthrow the truth. These [things] I wished to say in defence of Regiomontanus, and so of the truth. Nor yet do I deny but that some better reckoning can be found, whether by you or by others. Meanwhile, that the certainty of Regiomontanus's demonstrations will remain firm, I freely pronounce — [a certainty] which neither Craig, nor anyone else, will be able to convict of any absurdity. Regiomontanus assuredly <b>scented</b> something else about comets than what the schools publicly taught from Aristotle. But, on account of his [Aristotle's] authority, he dared not depart from the received opinion: who, if he now lived and had seen the New Star, would doubtless have blushed, and would have applied more accurate considerations of comets — nor is there any doubt with me but that, the truth being put first, he would have subscribed to our opinion. But why [do] I [say] these things to you, who have handled all these matters far more solidly, and have so dissolved all of Craig's reasonings and objections, that I do not see what more could be added to them for asserting the truth? Nothing else remains, except that, in accordance with your kindness and candour, you take these [things] of mine in fair and good part, and grant pardon to those [things] which have been either omitted by me, or less rightly understood.</p><p>Farewell, my sweetest Tycho, and see that you pursue me with your accustomed love.<br>Given at Prague on 11 June, New Style, which day was Whit Monday [the second feria of Pentecost], in the Year 1590.<br>Devoted to you from the heart,<br><b>Thaddaeus ab Hayck</b> [Tadeáš Hájek of Hájek].</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT.</b></p><p>I had already finished my letter to you when, behold, on the twenty-second of May, Old Style, the Nuremberg courier brought me yours, together with the description of the recent comet and the printed sheets of the Astronomical Letters, which I at once read through and studied most eagerly; and so that Lord Curtius might have, as it were, a spur to hasten that privilege and to dispatch a courier to you as soon as possible, I gave him all of it to read and peruse. And indeed that untiring zeal of yours for illustrating the science of astronomy pleased him wonderfully — he is, so help me God, so inflamed with love for you that he said nothing could happen to him more welcome and desired than if he could, for at least a few days, converse and confer with you; and certainly, were public business to permit it, he would hasten to greet you and embrace you as a friend, and I would join myself to him as his companion. That plan of yours, I say — indeed, already the carrying-out of that plan in the publishing of those letters — pleased him greatly, for many splendid things are drawn out in them by learned men, and truth is unearthed by disputation. All honour to that spirit of yours, which few men possess, and to your character, my Tycho; go forward with a fortunate step, wherever your virtue calls you.</p><p>I had sent you a very brief letter on the twenty-second of April, Old Style, by a certain student who said he was bound for Rostock. I enclosed within it your letter to Lord Chytraeus, and asked that he see mine carried on to you, which I do not doubt in the least was done, provided that student reached there. Mine contained nothing else but that, through a special courier — whom Lord Curtius himself said he would soon dispatch to you — we should write to you most fully about everything. I also asked, in that same letter, if it could be arranged, that you have made for me there, since you have craftsmen at hand, a model or pattern of your Sextant and Armillary [Sphere], and send it to me, so that afterward, on its pattern, I might have those same instruments made here at full size. When I mentioned this to Lord Curtius, he replied that it would be much more convenient if those instruments were made there, at the same size as your own, and sent here either by courier or by carter, and that he would most gladly defray all the costs. \"But,\" he said, \"a certain shame forbids me from asking this of Lord Tycho myself.\" I, however, earnestly beg you, if it can be done, to have those instruments made there — see to it that Lord Curtius has them, and oblige him in this. He will most generously pay all the expenses. I shall then have similar ones made for myself after their model. And if this comes about, we too shall be using the same instruments as you, and in our observations we shall be able to render each other mutual service.</p><p>That comet it fell to me to see only twice: first on the twenty-fifth of February, Old Style. On that day it stood between the first and second star of Triangulum, and appeared to lie in a straight line with the first and second stars in the horns of Aries and the first of Triangulum. It put out a tail, very thin and scarcely perceptible, like a rod or shaft, behind the second [star] of Triangulum, and pointed toward the left foot of Andromeda. I caught sight of it again on some other day, but not well enough, on account of intervening clouds. So, having taken up my Radius, I made ready to measure its distances from the neighbouring stars — but behold, clouds hid it from me, and afterward, on other days, I could not see it at all. I have, then, nothing further to write about it, nor have I read anything written about it by anyone, nor heard that it was observed by anyone.</p><p>I have several of your letters to me, but among them three deal with astronomical matters between us. I therefore send you their originals. In the letter of the year [15]80, as you will see, I have struck out a few things which I judged should either be omitted or altered. In my own [letters] to you, too, certain things will have to be removed — especially those recounting Scultetus's ill-will conceived against me — and the like, all of which I leave to your good judgement to remove or change. You will also correct, in those same letters of mine to you, whether written in my own hand or another's, certain grammatical faults committed through carelessness. But behold, while searching among my papers for something else, I find yet another letter of yours, by now almost torn to pieces, in which you deal with me about my observations — about the distance of the eleventh [star] in Cassiopeia from the pole star, about the meridian altitude of Spica, and about other matters. I have had that letter copied out. Do with it, as with the others, as you think best.</p><p>Toward the end of your letter, where you dealt with me about a more splendid privilege, you added that you were sending me a copy of the French Privilege. But know that none was enclosed with your letter. I easily persuade myself that, on account of your other most weighty occupations, you had forgotten it. Had we had a copy of it, it would indeed have been most welcome to us; we would have taken care that the Imperial one should not turn out humbler or less splendid than it. Nevertheless, even without having seen that French privilege, we reckon that, through the zeal and industry of Lord Curtius, and with due regard for your merit and learning — and indeed for your untiring labours in restoring and illustrating Astronomy — the Imperial one has been so drawn up that it cannot but be exceedingly welcome to you. Prague, the second feria of Pentecost [Whit Monday].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>); the Tycho–Hájek correspondence is gathered there. MS witnesses (per the dataset): <b>A</b> = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66; <b>B</b> = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.14 — the two principal codices preserving the Tychonic letter-archive that passed to the Imperial Library. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VII pp. 258–268</b> (see the Editorial note); the witness sigla A (cod. 10686.66) and B (cod. 10686.14, the appended <i>POST SCRIPTA</i>) are both confirmed against the scan's source-notes. Every reading affecting sense is confirmed; the working transcription (the modern DSL/Zeeberg normalization of Dreyer) is faithful.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and <i>protomedicus</i> / personal physician at the Imperial court in Prague; author of the <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt 1574) on the 1572 nova, in which he (independently, as he stresses here) argued the star supralunar. The pivotal link in Tycho's eventual call to Prague: it is precisely \"Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius, most closely joined to me by old friendship\" whom Tycho names in the 23 Aug 1598 Scaliger letter (15980823001) as Coraduz's co-mediator. This 1590 letter shows the friendship at full technical maturity, eight years before that.</li><li><b>Recipient &amp; occasion.</b> Tycho, at Uraniborg. The letter answers a lost (or separately-transmitted) Tycho letter of c.1589 that had: (i) reported the Mars-parallax result and the Tychonic hypotheses; (ii) defended Maestlin against Hájek; (iii) argued (against Hájek's thesis) that comet-motions <i>can</i> be reduced to law; (iv) discussed the 1577-comet epoch and the prosthaphaeresis discrepancy; and (v) enclosed Tycho's completed <i>Apologetica responsio</i> to Craig. Hájek replies point by point. The doubled despatch (Tycho sent two copies; the second reached Hájek via \"Vinshemius\") and the courier-scarcity lament frame the whole.</li><li><b>The Mars-parallax notice (historically the weightiest line).</b> <i>\"te ex parallaxibus ♂, quando fuit in opposito ☉, viciniorem terris ipso Sole deprehendisse; quod non nisi ex COPERNICI hypothesibus, vel ex tuis … salvari potuisse\"</i> — Tycho's finding that <b>Mars at opposition is nearer the Earth than the Sun</b>, \"saveable\" only on the Copernican or his own (geoheliocentric) hypotheses. This is among the earliest correspondence-references to the result that grounded the Tychonic world-system (the Mars and Sun orbits intersecting); cf. the <i>De mundi</i> arguments in <i>Opera</i> vol. IV. Note the controlled term <i>salvari</i> = <b>to save</b> (the appearances), kept per §5.</li><li><b>\"That plagiarist of yours\" (<i>illi plagiario tuo</i>).</b> <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (Bär), whose <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (Strasbourg 1588) published a geoheliocentric scheme Tycho held stolen from him during Ursus's 1584 visit to Hven. Hájek pointedly refuses to plead for him, while noting the \"plagiarism\" spurred Tycho to perfect his own system — the opening of the long Ursus priority-and-libel war that runs through the whole later correspondence (cf. the Kepler invitation 15991209001 and the Herwart letter 16000828002).</li><li><b>The comet-motion controversy (the scientific spine).</b> The core exchange: Hájek's thesis that <b>comet-motions cannot be bound by a fixed law as the planets are</b>, against Tycho's counter that <i>difficulty ≠ impossibility</i> (a portion of a circle suffices to reason about the whole, as for the slow-moving fixed stars). Hájek defends his thesis with <b>three reasons</b> — (1) the difficulty is so great as to have always been reckoned impossible, and he wants it shown surmountable by many comets' apparitions across centuries; (2) a universal can only be gathered from all particulars, and comets (unlike the ever-same planets and fixed stars) are uncountable and non-recurring; (3) comets are <i>new lights</i>, differing in number, place, and speed, \"most rarely describing perfect circles.\" He rebuts Tycho's planet- and Moon-analogies (the Moon's <b>12°/day</b> and full period are both knowable; a comet's period is knowable \"only in reasoning, not in the truth of the matter,\" since it never completes a circle), and his fixed-star analogy (Hipparchus → Ptolemy → al-Battānī → Copernicus traced the stars precisely because they <i>are</i> always the same and traverse a measurable \"sixth part of a circle\"). His verdict: comets are barred \"not only by difficulty but as it were by impossibility.\" Yet on Tycho's demonstration that the <b>comet of 1580</b> had the <b>Sun at its centre, circling like Venus and Mercury</b>, he defers — \"with Socrates I believe what I do not understand.\" This is the geoheliocentric cometary orbit Tycho was developing (cf. <i>Opera</i> IV, the editor's note to p. 162 that \"Tycho had begun to think seriously of such a hypothesis\").</li><li><b>The technical sidebar.</b> The <b>Alphonsine tables</b> now err by <i>\"a little less than three whole degrees\"</i> in Saturn and Jupiter — Tycho to explain in his \"new hypotheses.\" The <b>1577-comet mean-commutation epoch</b> (24 November): Hájek, recomputing from <b>Maestlin's</b> own data (<i>dedomenis</i> = δεδομένα), made it <b>15′ smaller</b>, \"since from the established epoch the whole edifice rises\"; Tycho calls it a trifling error and Hájek lets it drop. The <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> discrepancy between them Tycho explained as arising from <b>different reference terms</b> — Hájek's reckoned from <b>24 November</b>, Tycho's from <b>26 January</b> — and Hájek \"gladly acquiesces.\" (<i>Prosthaphaeresis</i> kept per §5.)</li><li><b>The polemics (Hájek's own war over the 1572 nova).</b> Three named opponents who reviled rather than argued: <b>\"Raimundus\"</b> = <b>Annibale Raimondo</b> of Verona, who attacked the supralunar reading and drew Hájek's threefold <i>Responsio</i>; <b>\"Graminaeus\"</b> = <b>Theodor Graminaeus</b> of Cologne (the abuse-word <i>Verpus</i>, lit. \"the circumcised/the prick,\" is a coarse insult, kept and glossed not euphemised); and <b>\"Lindanus\"</b> = <b>Wilhelm Damasus Lindanus</b> (van der Lindt), the polemical <b>Bishop</b> (Roermond, later Ghent), Graminaeus's \"reserve-man\" (<i>succenturiatus</i>). Hájek answered Graminaeus and Lindanus in the manuscript <b><i>Aquilonis Historici Prodromus</b></i> (the \"<i>Prodromus</i> of the Historical Aquilo\"), a work dedicated to <b>three secular Electors</b>, \"thumbed in many hands\" but never printed — a \"painted-over friend\" having deterred the printer mid-press. The cobbler joke: Lindanus had called Hájek a <i>\"lame cobbler\"</i> (<i>claudus sutor</i>, alluding to the proverb <i>ne sutor ultra crepidam</i>, \"cobbler, stick to your last\"), so Hájek struck back \"with cobbler's lasts and a shoemaker's foot-block … without danger of excommunication.\"</li><li><b>The Craig dossier.</b> Tycho's <b>Apologetica responsio</b> answered the Scottish physician <b>John Craig</b> (Craige), who defended Aristotle's sublunary comets against Tycho and (in his <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i>, the comet polemic) charged that comet-distances could not be determined. Hájek defends <b>Regiomontanus</b> — whose <i>De cometae … problemata XVI</i> (the sixteen problems on the 1472 comet) \"in problems 2, 3, 4\" <i>does</i> warn that a comet's <b>proper motion</b> must be allowed for in parallax-work — and recalls his own priority: \"the <b>first</b>\" to declare the 1572 star supralunar and \"the first to shake Aristotle's opinion on comets … to cut this ice.\" The Regiomontanus <i>sententia</i> (<i>\"thesauros naturae nos non evacuare…\"</i>) is kept as a maxim.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg (the enclosed <i>liber Verneri</i>); <b>\"Master Vinshemius\"</b> (the intermediary who forwarded Tycho's duplicate — most plausibly a Wittenberg/Leipzig contact of the Winsheim/Winshemius family, not securely identified here, so given as written); <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (<i>Iacobus Curtius</i>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor and privy councillor, d. 1594, the astronomy-minded patron who \"will write more fully\"); <b>Nicolaus Winkler</b> of Augsburg (the dismissed <i>Calendariopaeus</i> / almanac-maker, with his \"Prognostication of 1582\"); <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (Mästlin) of Tübingen; <b>Hipparchus, Ptolemy, al-Battānī (Albategnius), Copernicus</b>; <b>Jerónimo Muñoz</b> (the Spanish <i>Libro del nuevo cometa</i>, 1573); <b>Cornelio Frangipane</b> (the Italian tract); <b>Peter Apian</b> (the 1531-comet book); <b>George of Trebizond</b> (<i>Trapezuntius</i>, the Almagest commentary); <b>Paul Wittich</b> (<i>Witychius</i>, d. 1586, whose library Tycho coveted); <b>Johannes Pena</b> (the French royal lecturer); <b>Johannes Praetorius</b> (Richter, the amicable corrector); <b>Regiomontanus</b>; <b>Aristotle</b>, <b>Socrates</b>, <b>Heraclitus</b>, <b>Momus</b>. Place: <b>Prague</b> (the dateline); the friend's <b>legation into Poland</b>.</li><li><b>Allusions &amp; tags.</b> <i>\"Nosce te ipsum\"</i> — \"Know thyself,\" the Delphic maxim \"said to have descended from heaven.\" <i>\"in verba magistri iurare\"</i> — Horace, <i>Epist.</i> I.1.14 (to swear by a master's words). <i>triobolaris</i> — \"threepenny,\" worth three obols (worthless). <i>sal Momi / sal Mercurii</i> — the \"black salt of Momus\" (carping malice) vs the \"white salt of Mercury\" (urbane wit). <i>arena cedere</i> — \"to yield the field/arena\" (a gladiatorial idiom for conceding). <i>sutor … crepidam</i> — the cobbler-proverb behind the Lindanus joke. The \"Comic poet\" <i>sententia</i> (<i>nihil tam arduum…</i>) is a commonplace (cf. Terence/Sallust). Greek: <i>dedomenis</i> = δεδομένα (\"the data/givens\").</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline explicitly <b>New Style</b> (<i>\"11. Iunij novo stylo\"</i>) and internally self-verifying: in the <b>Gregorian</b> calendar of 1590, <b>Whit Monday</b> (<i>feria secunda Pentecostes</i>) fell on 11 June (Pentecost Sunday = 10 June). As an Imperial-court correspondent in Catholic Bohemia, Hájek writes N.S.; Tycho in Denmark kept O.S. The header leads with the as-written N.S. date and matches the catalogue label.</li><li><b>OCR slips (transcription, kept verbatim, corrected in translation).</b> <i>fint</i> → <i>sint</i> (\"cum eorum quaedam <b>fint</b>\"); <i>verilate</i> → <i>veritate</i> (\"non in rei <b>verilate</b>\"); <i>reprensionem</i> → <i>reprehensionem</i>; the stray comma-space in <i>\"doctrinam parallaxium ,\"</i>. All four are transcription-level (long-s / minim confusions), not substantive; the sense is unaffected.</li><li><b>The postscript.</b> Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), Rudolf II's Imperial physician in Prague, appends this POST SCRIPTA to his letter of 11 June 1590 (N.S.) to Tycho after his sealed letter had already gone out, prompted by the arrival on 22 May (O.S.) of Tycho's own letter, a description of a recent comet, and proof sheets of the forthcoming <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>.</li><li><b>D. Curtius.</b> Almost certainly Jakob Kurz von Senftenau (Latinised \"Jacobus Curtius\"), Rudolf II's Imperial Vice-Chancellor — the same courtier who, nine years later, put his own Hradčany palace at Tycho's disposal when Tycho reached Prague in 1599 (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> / <i>The Measure of the Heavens</i>). Here, in 1590, he is already lobbying, at Hagecius's prompting, to expedite an Imperial printing privilege for Tycho and to send a private courier — a decade of prehistory to the 1599 patronage relationship that the published record otherwise picks up only later.</li><li><b>D. Chytraeus.</b> David Chytraeus (1531–1600), the Rostock theologian-historian, serves here only as a relay: Hagecius had routed an earlier brief letter (22 April) to Tycho through him, via a travelling student.</li><li><b>Scultetus.</b> Hagecius asks Tycho to strike, from Hagecius's own earlier letters, the passages recounting \"Scultetus's\" — Bartholomaeus Scultetus of Görlitz, an old mathematical correspondent and friend of Tycho's — ill-will conceived against him: a personal friction inside Tycho's network not otherwise visible in this letter's published tail.</li><li><b>Instrument transfer.</b> Curtius's offer to have a full-scale Sextant and Armillary Sphere built at Hven at his own expense and shipped to Prague (rather than settling for a mere model) is a concrete, decade-early data point for Habsburg interest in Tychonic instrumentation, well before the actual 1599–1601 transfer of Tycho's instruments to Prague.</li><li><b>The 1590 comet and the French Privilege.</b> Hagecius's two brief sightings (25 February and one later day, O.S., near Triangulum/Aries, with a faint tail toward Andromeda's foot) are an independent minor observational record; separately, he notes that Tycho had promised, but failed to enclose, a copy of a French royal printing privilege meant as a model for the Imperial one Curtius was securing.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the place-name 'Pragae' to the closing dateline, dropped from our source_text; Dreyer (TBOO VII p.254) prints 'Datae Pragae 11. Iunij novo stylo...'. The English dateline clause was completed to match ('Given at Prague on 11 June...').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, continuously-argued letter (the comet-motion controversy + the Mars-parallax/Tychonic-system notice + the Craig <i>Apologia</i> + the nova-polemic dossier) rendered securely throughout; register (warm learned courtesy, with controlled invective in the polemical paragraph) preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, prosthaphaeresis, hypothesis, anomaly, <i>salvari</i>, <i>restitutor</i>, the epistolary formulae) held consistent with the surrounding correspondence. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VII pp. 258–268) and confirms every load-bearing reading</b>, so the collation reservation that previously capped the grade is <b>lifted</b>. Two minor residues remain: (1) the <b>\"Vinshemius\"</b> spelling is confirmed against Dreyer (p. 259, <i>D. VINSHEMIUS</i>), but Dreyer supplies no first name or expansion, so the man's prosopographical identity is still unresolved (the \"Veit Winsheim / family relative\" reading stays a conjecture, not a Dreyer reading); (2) the four minor OCR slips were editorially corrected (declared above). Neither obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>The Latin is clean, unadorned humanist correspondence prose with no apparent corruption in the source as supplied, and I am confident in the translation throughout. What is not secured is external collation: I have not checked this postscript against Dreyer's edition or the tekstnet.dk critical apparatus for this letter, so I cannot confirm whether it has appeared in print before, or under what siglum. The identifications of Curtius (Jakob Kurz von Senftenau), Chytraeus, and Scultetus rest on well-attested corpus material (Christianson, Dreyer, the Tycho Brahes Breve register) rather than on notes specific to this letter. Upgrade path: collation against Tychonis Brahe et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae and the manuscript description at Vindob. lat. 10686. Grade B.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.244-256 (full 13-page slice, all pages read as images in chunks of 4). Checked opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date and place-name across the letter and postscript (VERNERI, VINSHEMIUS, CURTIUS, WINKLERO, MAESTHLINUS, RAIMUNDUS, GRAMINAEUS, LINDANUS, SOCRATES/HERACLITI, REGIOMONTANUS, HIPPARCHI/PTOLEMAEO/ALBATEGNIO/COPERNICO, CRAIGIUS, MUNNOSIJ, FRANGIPANUS, TRAPEZUNTIJ, WITYCHIJ, PAeNAE, PRAeTORIO, SCULTETI, CHYTRAeUM; dates 24 Novembris/26 Ianuarij/25 Febr./22 Maij/22 Aprilis/11 Iunij 1590; numbers 9 annos, 15' minorem, tribus integris gradibus, 5 vel 6 partes, 12 partes, distantia XI, problema 2.3.4.). The extensive main text matches source_text and Dreyer word-for-word throughout (pp.244-255); page 256 confirmed to be the start of the next letter (Tycho to Jacobus Curtius), correctly excluded. Translation is otherwise complete and accurate with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found; bracketed identifications (Muñoz, Frangipane, Trebizond, al-Battani, Kurz von Senftenau, Praetorius, Raimondo) are reasonable editorial glosses, not fabrications. One TEXT_ERROR found: our source_text's closing dateline omits 'Pragae' which Dreyer prints.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text's closing dateline drops the place-name 'Pragae' (at Prague) that Dreyer prints; the English correctly renders the (already incomplete) source_text, so the fault is in the Latin, not the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Pragae 11. Iunij novo stylo, qui dies fuit feria secunda Pentecostes Anno 1590. (TBOO VII p.254)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.254, read visually, not via OCR text layer): the printed dateline reads 'Datae Pragae 11. Iunij novo ftylo, qui dies fuit feria fecunda Pentecoftes Anno 1590.', confirming the audit's claim. No other defects were listed for this letter, and no other text was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (14 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/66", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13963771"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.66.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.14."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 23, "prev": "brahe-t_15900501001", "next": "brahe-t_15900803001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900223001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900803001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Praises Tycho's letter for its warm recollection of their friendship and learned astronomical disquisition, which he found entirely welcome rather than over-long.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900611001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900628001", "d": "1590-06-28", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Jacob Kurz", "ck": "Jacob Kurz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Jacob Kurz von Senftenau → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 28 June 1590 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tycho Brahe,<i> Astronomiae instauratae mechanica </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>V/VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in the dataset</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SEQVITVR DESCRIPTVM LITERARVM AMPLISSIMI ET GENEROSI VIRI DNI. IACOBI CVRTII, ANTEA PROCANCELLARII IMPERII, AD TYCHONEM BRAHE IN DANIAM SCRIPTARVM ANNO CIϽ IϽ XC.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>MAGNAS tibi ago gratias, Nobilis aeque ac eruditissime vir, quod me non modo literis tuis compellare, sed benevolentiam quoque tuam tam amanter offerre mihi volueris. Ego certe, licet faciem tuam non viderim meliorem tamen tui partem, mentem nempe, ex scriptis tuis, quibus teipsum aeternitati jam consecrasti, jam olim et amavi et suspexi, et eius caussa totum te videre, venerari, et amplexari non minus avide concupivi, quam illi olim, qui ex ultimis Hispaniae finibus ad Livium videndum in urbem profecti sunt. Auxit hoc meum desiderium humanissima haec tua compellatio, et in tantum auxit, ut, nisi Caesaris voluntas, et muneris publici, quod gero, ratio prohiberent: ita me DEVS amet, neque itineris intercapedo et molestia, neque suavissimae conjugis dulcissimorumque pignorum amor et illecebrae me cohiberent, quin arrepto itineris comite communi amico Thaddaeo Hageccio ad te in Daniam usque excurrerem. Dicere nequeo, quantopere me delectet cum Hageccio, mente, postqam corpore non possumus, totam Huenam perlustrare, et inprimis exactissima tua observatoria Instrumenta, totum denique Mathematicum illum apparatum vel imaginatione tantum complecti. Quid vero si ipse spectator adessem, teque ipsum omnia non explicantem tantum, sed exercentem videre possem? Sed id quia optare quidem licet, sperare hoc tempore vix (neque enim omnem spem penitus abjeci:) missum jam faciam, et ad Epistolam tuam conversus, paucis ad summa eius capita respondebo. Atque hoc inprimis. Quod judicium meum de scriptis tuis amanter exquiris, quod censurae meae, eam quae tibi cum doctrinis quibusdam viris non tam lis, quam amica est concertatio, subjicis, in eo tantum mihi a te honoris tribui accipio, quanti non facerem, si Reges et Principes de Regnis et Provinciis integris disceptantes, me sibi Judicem constituerent. Ut vero, id quod petis et offers, mihi faciendum sumam, nec eruditio, quae in me aut nulla est, aut vere exigua, neque modestia mea patitur. Ne tamen ad tam amicam postulationem plane surdus sim, hoc habeto. De scriptis tuis quid in genere sentiam, testabitur Caesareae Majestatis privilegium, quod hisce literis adjunctum tibi mitto, cuius ita conscribendi ego Caesari author, et porro, vera in eo Caesarem testari, sponsor fui. In specie vero ea quae de Cometis posterioribus, quotquot ipse observasti, a te tradita solidissimisque rationibus confirmata sunt, talia esse puto, ut de iis ne ambigi quidem amplius ab Eruditis viris queat. Itaque non adducor, ut credam eos qui hac in parte dubios sese adhuc ostendunt, et contra demonstratam veritatem Aristotelis authoritati patrocinari videri volunt, id ita vere sentire. Sed hoc potius eos agere puto, ut, cum omnem moverint lapidem, omniaque undique conquisiverint, quae Aristoteleam sententiam confirmare, tuam vero labefactare, aut dubiam reddere videantur, ea tibi proponant, tanquam ad Lydium lapidem examinanda, adeoque caussam tibi praebeant, eruditionis tuae Sole, omnes omninô errorum tenebras non modo, sed vel levissimas nubeculas dissipandi, exactissimamque harum rerum doctrinam posteritati perficiendi. Qui si hac mente tecum certant, nae ego illis quam maxime bene cupio; sin aliud agunt, pertinaciam quidem eorum odio dignam existimo: Id tamen quod pertinaciâ suâ in commune beneficium tibi extorserunt, ipsis valere, vel potius non valere jussis amplector non secus, ac si bona id mente fecissent: de eoque reipublicae literariae gratulor.</p><p>De prioribus Cometis, quos Regiomontanus et alii observarunt, expecto quae propediem editurus es, eo usque meam quoque sententiam, an in universum omnes Cometae aetherei credendi sint, an vero pars aetherei, pars elementares, suspensurus.</p><p>Ad ea, quae novis tuis hypothesibus hactenus objecta sunt, solide mihi videris respondisse, neque ego quicquam in iis absurdum, sed omnia invicem pulcherrime congruentia invenio: an vero hypotheses istae, id quod in Copernicianis desideratur, praestitutae sint, ut scilicet exacta Siderum loca ad praeterita, praesentia, et futura tempora nobis exhibeant, de eo tum demum judicandum erit, ubi majus illud, quod moliris, Opus, in lucem prodierit. Neque enim dubito, quin in eo opere omnium aetatum quotquot extant exactiores observationes, cum tuis, quas exactissimas esse, certo mihi persuadeo, summâ diligentiâ contuleris, indeque nouam hanc mundi Machinam, divino sane ingenio, extruxeris.</p><p>Macte itaque animo, vir nobilissime, et fac ut divinis tuis inventis quam ocyssime fruamur. Etsi enim arduum, et, ut ipse vere scribis, multorum hoc sit opus annorum: spero tamen, imo confido, te maximam jam ejus partem exantlasse, et in recolligendis et ordinandis iis, quae jam constituisti, tabulisque inde condendis, magis hoc tempore, quam in confirmandis amplius inventis tuis versari. Quâ in re cum discipulorum tuorum operâ magna in parte juvari possis, iterum atque iterum te rogatum volo, ne nos nimium diu suspensos habeas; Vitam tibi ex animo ad Nestoreos usque annos exopto: nôsti tamen quam fallaces sint spes nostrae, et quam immatura plerunque divinis praesertim ingeniis mors obtigerit; Falle itaque moras, et vel non maturo partu, hunc ingenii tui foetum orbi terrarum êde. Si fata longiorem tibi, quod avidê optamus et speramus, vitam concesserint, poteris et edita recudere, et judicio tandem tuo quoque (nostro enim quin in primâ editione satis facturus sis, nihil dubito) satisfacere. Liberabis interim nos perpetuo metu, ne, dum nimium cunctaris, toto opere frustremur. Ego certe tantam de te, tuisque his hypothesibus spem concepi, ut vel a te, vel a nemine solidam Astronomicae artis exaedificationem expectandam esse pro certo affirmare audeam. Qui enim praeter divinam ingenii vim, tot tantisque rebus huc necessariis instructus sit, tot tantosque sumptus, labores et annos contulerit, non est in Europâ (cum Europam dico, totum Orbem dico) qui tibi comparari queat, quisquam. Abest hinc, ita me DEUS amet, omnis adulatio, a quâ et dignitas, quam gero, et gravitas quam cum omnibus tueri laboro, me apud notos atque ignotos, in ignoti praesertim de facie hominis laude, satis superque vindicabunt.</p><p>Quae de exactâ Instrumentorum ad observationes necessariorum constructione et vsu variis in locis, et ad varios a te scripta sunt, summopere me delectârunt. Ego dum hactenus mihi ipsi satisfacere non potui, dum ea quae non exacta sunt, ne videre quidem, nedum habere patior, omnibus omninô Instrumentis, praeter ea, quae ad voluptatem tantum comparantur, careo. Quadrans Nonnianus multum aliquando me habuit sollicitum: sed quia difficillime, imo fere impossibile est eum conficere, cogitavi ego de aliis mediis, et inveni varia, quorum partem Christophorus Clavius insignis Mathematicus in libello suo de horologiis solaribus per Instrumentum describendis, edidit. Tandem in mentem mihi venit quadrantis cuiusdam constructio, quae quia doctis quibusdam viris non inelegans visa est, volui eam ad te quoque mittere. Ut tamen ingenue fatear quod res est, quodque verissime affirmas: inventiones hae omnes minus habent in recessu, quam de se primâ fronte spondent. Gratum tamen mihi erit tuum de hoc ultimo meo invento audire judicium. Ex N. N. Plagiarii tui libello, quem fundamentum Astronomicum inscripsit, unicoque ejus diagrammate, quod Paulo Witichio dedicavit, construxi ego praeteritis diebus, cum ob adversam valetudinem publicis negotiis vacare non possem, novam sphaericorum triangulorum doctrinam, in qua per tabulam sinuum, tangentium, et secantium omnes tam rectangulorum quam obliquangulorum casus, sine ulla multiplicatione vel divisione per solam additionem et subtractionem facillime perficiuntur. Eam quoque ad te mitterem, nisi scirem te rem totam, solo eo diagrammate inspecto facile assecuturum. Ex illo enim diagrammate et axiomate a multis jam demonstrato, quod radius sit medius proportionalis inter sinum rectum arcus et secantem complementi, tota ea ratio exstructa est. Quod reliquum est, valere te iterum atque iterum, vir Nobilissime, in multos annos precor, et omnia, quae a me in te proficisci officia amoris et benevolentiae possunt, tibi peramanter offero, enixe rogans, quod ultro facere caepisti, me te ex animo amantem amare pergas.</p><p>Datae Pragae 28. Junii. Anno 90.<br>Jacobus Curtius a Senfftenaw.</p>", "en": "<p><b>THERE FOLLOWS A COPY OF A LETTER OF THE MOST EMINENT AND NOBLE GENTLEMAN MASTER JACOB CURTIUS, FORMERLY VICE-CHANCELLOR OF THE EMPIRE, WRITTEN TO TYCHO BRAHE IN DENMARK IN THE YEAR 1590.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>I render you great thanks, sir as noble as you are most learned, that you have been willing not only to address me with a letter of yours, but also to offer me your goodwill so affectionately. For my part, certainly — though I have not seen your face — yet your better part, namely your mind, I have already long since both loved and looked up to, from your writings, by which you have now consecrated yourself to eternity; and for its sake I have longed to see, revere, and embrace the whole of you no less eagerly than did those men of old who set out from the farthest bounds of Spain to come to the city to see Livy. This longing of mine your most courteous address has increased, and increased to such a degree that, were it not that the Emperor's will and the duty of the public office which I hold forbade it — so help me God — neither the distance and trouble of the journey, nor the love and allurements of a most sweet wife and dearest children, would hold me back from snatching up our common friend Thaddaeus Hagecius as my travelling companion and running off all the way to you in Denmark. I cannot say how much it delights me to range over the whole of Hven with Hagecius — in mind, since in body we cannot — and above all to take in, if only in imagination, your most exact observing instruments, and indeed that whole mathematical apparatus. But what if I were present as a spectator myself, and could see you not merely expounding everything, but performing it? Yet because this may indeed be wished for, but at this time scarcely hoped for (for I have not utterly cast away all hope) — I will now let it go, and, turning to your letter, will answer briefly to its chief heads. And this first of all. That you affectionately seek out my judgement on your writings, that you submit to my censure that contest of yours with certain learned men — which is not so much a quarrel as a friendly dispute — in this I take so much honour to be granted me by you as I would not reckon it, were kings and princes, disputing over whole kingdoms and provinces, to appoint me their judge. But that I should take upon myself to do what you ask and offer, neither my learning — which in me is either none, or truly very slight — nor my modesty permits. Yet, lest I be wholly deaf to so friendly a request, take this. What I think of your writings in general, the privilege of His Imperial Majesty will testify, which I send you enclosed with this letter — of whose drawing-up in this form I was the author with the Emperor, and further was the warrant that the Emperor therein attests what is true. But in particular, those things which, concerning the later comets — as many as you yourself observed — have been set forth by you and confirmed by the most solid reasonings, I judge to be such that they can no longer even be called into doubt by learned men. And so I am not brought to believe that those who in this matter still show themselves doubtful, and who wish to seem to be defending the authority of Aristotle against the demonstrated truth, truly think so. Rather I think they are doing this: that, having moved every stone and gathered together from every side all that might seem to confirm the Aristotelian opinion but to undermine yours or render it doubtful, they put these things before you, as if to be tested against the Lydian stone [touchstone], and so afford you occasion — by the sun of your erudition — to dispel not only all the darkness of errors, but even the very lightest little clouds, and to perfect for posterity the most exact doctrine of these matters. If they contend with you in this spirit, then truly I wish them very well indeed; but if they are about something else, I judge their obstinacy worthy of hatred — yet that which by their obstinacy they have wrung from you to the common good, I embrace, with them bidden to fare well, or rather not to fare well, no otherwise than if they had done it with good intent; and for it I congratulate the republic of letters.</p><p>Concerning the earlier comets, which Regiomontanus and others observed, I await what you are shortly to publish, and until then I shall hold in suspense my own opinion too — whether all comets are in general to be believed ethereal, or rather some ethereal, some elemental.</p><p>To the objections that have hitherto been raised against your new hypotheses, you seem to me to have replied solidly, and I find nothing absurd in them, but everything most beautifully agreeing one with another; but whether those hypotheses are furnished with that which is wanting in the Copernican ones — namely, so as to set forth for us the exact places of the stars for past, present, and future times — of that judgement will only then be possible, when that greater Work which you are labouring at comes into the light. For I do not doubt that in that work you will have collated, with the utmost diligence, the more exact observations of every age, as many as exist, with your own (which I am certainly persuaded are the most exact), and from them will have built up this new Machine of the world, by a truly divine genius.</p><p>Be of good courage, then, most noble sir, and bring it about that we may enjoy your divine discoveries as quickly as may be. For although this is an arduous task, and, as you yourself truly write, the work of many years, yet I hope — nay, I am confident — that you have already drained off the greatest part of it, and that at this time you are engaged more in re-collecting and ordering the things you have already established, and in compiling tables therefrom, than in any further confirming of your discoveries. Since in this matter you can in great part be helped by the labour of your pupils, I would have you asked again and again not to keep us too long in suspense. With all my heart I wish you life right up to the years of Nestor; yet you know how deceptive our hopes are, and how untimely a death has for the most part befallen divine geniuses especially. Cheat delay, then, and even by a birth not brought to term, bring forth this offspring of your genius to the whole world. If the fates grant you the longer life which we eagerly wish and hope, you will be able both to reprint what has been published, and at length to satisfy your own judgement too (for that you will satisfy ours in the first edition, I in no way doubt). Meanwhile you will free us from a perpetual fear — that, while you delay too long, we be cheated of the whole work. I, for my part, have certainly conceived such hope of you and these hypotheses of yours, that I would dare to affirm for certain that a solid building-up of the astronomical art is to be expected either from you, or from no one. For one who, besides a divine force of genius, is furnished with so many and so great resources necessary to this, and has devoted so many and so great expenses, labours, and years — there is no one in Europe (and when I say Europe, I say the whole World) who could be compared to you. Far from this — so help me God — is all flattery; from which both the dignity I bear and the gravity which I strive to maintain before all men will, among those who know me and those who do not, and especially in the praise of a man unknown by face, vindicate me well and abundantly.</p><p>The things you have written in various places, and to various men, concerning the exact construction and use of the instruments needed for observations, have delighted me in the highest degree. As for me, since hitherto I have been unable to satisfy myself, and since I cannot bear even to see, much less to possess, things that are not exact, I am wholly without instruments — apart from those that are procured only for pleasure. The Nonian quadrant once kept me much in anxiety; but because it is most difficult, nay almost impossible, to construct, I bethought myself of other means, and found various ones, part of which Christoph Clavius, the distinguished mathematician, has published in his little book on describing solar dials by means of an instrument. At last there came into my mind the construction of a certain quadrant, which, because it seemed not inelegant to certain learned men, I wished to send to you as well. Yet, to confess frankly how the matter stands, and as you most truly affirm: all these inventions have less in the recess than they promise of themselves at first appearance. It will nonetheless be pleasing to me to hear your judgement of this latest invention of mine. From the little book of your plagiarist, N. N. — which he entitled <i>Astronomical Foundation</i> — and from its single diagram, which he dedicated to Paul Wittich, I in past days (since on account of ill health I could not attend to public business) constructed a new doctrine of spherical triangles, in which, by means of a table of sines, tangents, and secants, all cases, of right-angled as well as oblique-angled [triangles], are most easily worked out without any multiplication or division, by addition and subtraction alone. This too I would send you, did I not know that you would readily attain the whole matter from inspecting that diagram alone. For from that diagram, and from the axiom — already demonstrated by many — that the radius is the mean proportional between the right sine of an arc and the secant of its complement, that whole method is built up. For the rest, I pray that you fare well again and again, most noble sir, for many years, and I most affectionately offer you all the services of love and goodwill that can proceed from me toward you, earnestly asking that — as you have of your own accord begun to do — you may continue to love me, who love you from the heart.</p><p>Given at Prague, 28 June, in the year [15]90.<br>Jacob Curtius of Senftenau.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iacobus Curtius a Senfftenaw / Jacobus Curtius</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jakob Kurz zu Senftenau, c. 1554–1594), Imperial Aulic Councillor and (here styled <i>antea Procancellarii Imperii</i>, \"formerly Vice-Chancellor of the Empire\") <b>Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b> under Rudolf II in Prague — a councillor of \"perspicacious genius and keen judgement, not only in political but in mathematical matters\" (so Tycho describes him, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI, p. 287). He was the channel through whom Tycho obtained the imperial printing privilege for his works, and an early Prague advocate whose esteem prefigured Tycho's later move to Bohemia. Tycho printed this letter (with two of Kurz's others) in the <i>Mechanica</i> as testimony to his standing among the learned.</li><li><b>Edition / document type.</b> Printed in Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (Wandsbek, 1598; = Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> V/VI), not a chancery register or autograph; the dataset records no surviving MS witness. The Latin rubric <i>SEQVITVR DESCRIPTVM LITERARVM …</i> (\"There follows a copy of a letter …\") is Tycho's editorial heading, reproduced verbatim. <i>CIϽ IϽ XC</i> is the year-numeral 1590 (CIƆ = M = 1000, IƆ = D = 500, XC = 90).</li><li><b><i>ad te in Daniam … excurrerem</b></i> — written while Tycho was still on Hven (<i>Huena</i>, <b>Hven</b>); the wish to \"run off to Denmark\" with Hagecius is counterfactual, the duty of office forbidding it.</li><li><b><i>Thaddaeus Hageccius</b></i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician-astronomer in Prague and Tycho's long-standing correspondent on the 1572 nova and on comets; here named the would-be travelling companion.</li><li><b><i>ad Livium videndum … ex ultimis Hispaniae finibus</b></i> — the topos (from Pliny, <i>Ep.</i> 2.3 / Jerome) of a Spaniard travelling from the ends of the earth to Rome merely to see the historian <b>Livy</b>; Kurz applies it to his desire to see Tycho. Kept as the classical allusion it is.</li><li><b><i>Caesaris … privilegium / Caesareae Majestatis privilegium</b></i> — the <b>imperial printing privilege</b> (of Rudolf II) for Tycho's books, which Kurz drafted and stands surety for; he encloses it with this letter. <i>Caesar</i> = the (Holy Roman) Emperor.</li><li><b>Comets.</b> <i>Cometis posterioribus … prioribus Cometis, quos Regiomontanus et alii observarunt</i> — \"the later comets\" (Tycho's own observed comets, chiefly that of 1577 and after, on which Tycho argued the supralunary, <i>aetherei</i>, nature of comets against Aristotle) vs. \"the earlier comets\" observed by <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller of Königsberg, 1436–1476) and others, on which Tycho was still to publish. The open question Kurz suspends — <i>an … omnes Cometae aetherei … an … pars aetherei, pars elementares</i> (\"whether all comets are ethereal, or some ethereal, some elemental\") — is preserved as his explicit hedge, not resolved.</li><li><b><i>ad Lydium lapidem examinanda</b></i> — \"to be tested against the Lydian stone,\" i.e. the <b>touchstone</b>; the assayer's-stone metaphor for putting Tycho's results to the test. Kept with gloss.</li><li><b><i>novis tuis hypothesibus … nouam hanc mundi Machinam</b></i> — Tycho's \"new hypotheses\" / \"new Machine of the world\" = the <b>geo-heliocentric (Tychonic) world-system</b>; <i>id quod in Copernicianis desideratur</i> (\"that which is wanting in the Copernican [hypotheses]\") flags Kurz's reservation about predictive exactness, set against <i>majus illud … Opus</i> — the great work (the projected <i>restoration of astronomy</i>, the <i>Theatrum astronomiae</i> / planetary tables) still to appear.</li><li><b><i>ad Nestoreos … annos</b></i> — \"to the years of Nestor,\" i.e. an extreme old age (the Homeric Nestor); a learned wish for long life.</li><li><b><i>non maturo partu … foetum … êde</b></i> — the childbirth metaphor for premature publication (\"even by a birth not brought to term, bring forth this offspring of your genius\"); Kurz urges Tycho not to delay. Kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Quadrans Nonnianus</i> = the <b>\"Nonian\" quadrant</b>, the nonius (the diagonal/subdivision device of <b>Pedro Nunes</b>, <i>Petrus Nonius</i>) for reading fractional arc — notoriously hard to make (\"almost impossible to construct\"). <i>Christophorus Clavius … libello … de horologiis solaribus per Instrumentum describendis</i> = <b>Christoph Clavius</b> (1538–1612), the Jesuit mathematician, and his treatise on tracing sundials by instrument. <i>quadrantis cuiusdam constructio</i> — a quadrant of Kurz's own devising, offered to Tycho with the self-deprecating caveat <i>inventiones hae omnes minus habent in recessu, quam … primâ fronte spondent</i> (\"all these inventions have less in the recess than they promise at first sight\"). Per glossary §5.2, <i>Instrumenta / organa</i> → \"instruments,\" <i>quadrans</i> → \"quadrant.\"</li><li><b><i>N. N. Plagiarii tui … fundamentum Astronomicum … Paulo Witichio dedicavit</b></i> — \"the little book of your plagiarist, N. N.\" = the <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (Strasbourg, 1588) of <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (Nicolaus Raimarus Ursus / \"Bär\"), whom Tycho accused of stealing the Tychonic system; Kurz pointedly leaves him anonymous as <i>N. N.</i> (\"so-and-so\"). The work was dedicated to <b>Paul Wittich</b> (<i>Paulus Witichius</i>, c. 1546–1586), the Silesian mathematician whose prosthaphaeretic methods stand behind the diagram in question. The crux is left as Kurz wrote it: <i>N. N.</i> is preserved, not expanded into a name within the translation.</li><li><b>Prosthaphaeresis / spherical trigonometry.</b> Kurz's \"new doctrine of spherical triangles\" solving all cases <i>per solam additionem et subtractionem</i> (\"by addition and subtraction alone,\" without multiplication or division) is the <b>prosthaphaeretic</b> computing method (per glossary §5.1, <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> kept — though Kurz here uses the descriptive phrase, not the term). The stated <i>axioma</i> — <i>radius sit medius proportionalis inter sinum rectum arcus et secantem complementi</i> (\"the radius is the mean proportional between the right sine of an arc and the secant of its complement\") — is rendered literally; <i>sinus rectus</i> = \"right sine,\" <i>secans complementi</i> = \"secant of the complement\" (i.e. cosecant in modern terms), kept in period form.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datae Pragae 28. Junii. Anno 90</i> = \"Given at <b>Prague</b>, 28 June [15]90.\" The dataset label marks this <b>n.s.</b> (New Style): as an Imperial councillor in Catholic Prague, Kurz wrote on the Gregorian calendar, 10 days ahead of Tycho's Danish Old Style. Header date follows the dateline as written (28 June); the <b>[N.S.]</b> tag records the style per protocol §3, with no silent conversion. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting,\" §5.5); <i>ita me DEVS amet</i> (\"so help me God,\" twice) kept as the asseveration it is; <i>valere te … precor</i> and the closing <i>me … amantem amare pergas</i> rendered in the affectionate epistolary register.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one deliberately unresolved point is the <i>N. N. Plagiarius</i> (identified in the apparatus as Ursus, but left as <i>N. N.</i> in the translation per the author's own suppression), and the minor lexical choice of \"secant of the complement\" for <i>secans complementi</i>, both flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — printed text rendered securely; only residue is the author's deliberate N.N. suppression + a period-form lexical choice — no genuine crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages (TBOO V pp. 118-123) as page images. P.118 is Tycho's own introductory text preceding the letter (naming Curtius as procancellarius); the letter proper runs pp.119-122 ('SEQVITVR DESCRIPTVM LITERARVM...' to 'Datae Pragae 28. Junii. Anno 90. Jacobus Curtius a Senfftenaw.'), with p.122-123 continuing into a separate technical appendix (the quadrant-subdivision description) correctly excluded from source_text/published_english. Collated opening and closing lines, every proper name (Hagecius, Clavius, Paul Wittich, the unnamed 'N. N.' plagiarist correctly left untranslated), the date (28 June [15]90), and the self-praise passage ('Abest hinc... dignitas quam gero... gravitas quam... laboro') where first-person subject/pronoun assignment is preserved correctly (Curtius vindicating himself, not a claim about Tycho). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found across the full six-page collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Jacob Kurz", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15900801001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15900801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for his letter, declares long-standing admiration for his writings, and describes an earnest desire to visit in person, prevented only by Imperial duty.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900628001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900718001", "d": "1590-07-18", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Dronning Sophie", "ck": "Dronning Sophie", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Kolding", "t": "Queen Sophie → Tycho Brahe — Kolding, 18 July 1590 [O.S.] (catalogued Copenhagen)", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588–92, fol. 80 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYGGE BRAHE FICK BREFF NOGEN PENDINGE ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR</p><p>Sophia etc.</p><p>WOR gunft thilforn. Wider, att ßom j ether haffuer att erinndre om thett thußinnd daller, Wij ether for nogen thiid ßiden naadigst med forstragdtt haffuer och thill ßist forgangen omßlag schulle werrid erlagd och bethald, Wij dog ßamme thiid paa ethers wnderdannigst anfordring haffue ether naadigst bedaged thill thend dag epther pindtzdag ßist forganngen, paa huilken herredagen ßamme thid wor berammid, att the wden widere forhalling ßamme thiid schulle erleggis, haffde och icke annditt Oß thillformoditt, ennd ßligtt ethers løffthe schulle werre schied. Och epther att Oß nu paahennger nogen pendings wdgifft, ßaa Wij thennum therfor haffue fornøden att bruge, bede Wij ether och begierre, attj wille thilthencke, att ßamme summa pendinge wden widere forhalling nu wdj thenne thilstandendis herredagh her wdj Koldinng med renthen kand bliffue Oß erlagd och bethald. Annßehendis att Wij offuer thennd ßist berampthe thiid, thill huilkenn the schulle werrid bethald, nu nogen thid aff gode wilge och naadigstt benegennhed haffue forthøffuedtt, wille Oß ther forre anmode, att the nu wden widere forhallinng bliffuer Oß epther ethers ßiste løffte thilstillett, saa framptt Wij wed anndre middell icke schulle foraarsagis att ßøge Wor bethallinng; j wille ochßaa med thett første Oß thilbiude, huor thill Wij Oß maa forlade, att Wij ther epther kunnde wide Oß att retthe.</p><p>Ther med etc.<br>Actum Kolding thend 18. julij anno 1590.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter concerning certain monies, as follows:</b></p><p>Sophie, etc.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, as you have [cause] to recall concerning that thousand daler with which we graciously advanced you some time ago, and which were to have been paid and discharged at the last past <i>omslag</i> [settlement-term], we did nevertheless at that same time, upon your most humble request, graciously grant you a respite to the day after the Whitsun last past, on which the Diet [<i>herredag</i>] was at that same time appointed, [so] that they should without further delay be paid at that time; and [we] had expected nothing else of us than that such a promise of yours should have been kept. And since there is now incumbent upon us a certain outlay of money, so that we have need of it therefor, we ask and desire you that you will take heed [to it], that the same sum of money may now, without further delay, in this approaching Diet here in Kolding, be paid and discharged to us together with the interest. Considering that we have over the last appointed time, by which they were to have been paid, now for some while out of good will and gracious benevolence forborne, we wish therefore to entreat you that they now without further delay be delivered to us according to your last promise, lest we should be caused by other means to seek our payment; you will also at the first offer yourself to us, upon which we may rely, [so] that we may thereafter know how to govern ourselves.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Kolding, the 18th of July, the year 1590.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Sophia</i> = <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1557–1631), queen consort of Frederik II and, after his death in April 1588, queen dowager. As the source's `sender` field records, this is \"Dronning Sophie / Queen Sophie.\" She acts here as a creditor of the crown's household, calling in a loan she had advanced to Tycho.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (<i>Dronning Sophies tegnelser</i>, the queen dowager's register). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…NOGEN PENDINGE ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning certain monies, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2. <i>Sophia etc.</i> and <i>Ther med etc.</i> are the register clerk's standard abbreviations of the full intitulatio and closing protocol.</li><li><b>Recipient name.</b> The rubric names the recipient <i>Tygge Brahe</i> (= <b>Tyge Brahe</b>, the Danish form of Tycho); the body addresses him throughout with the courteous plural <i>j / ether</i> (\"ye / you\").</li><li><b>Place discrepancy (flagged, not reconciled).</b> The dataset `label` gives the place as <b>København / Copenhagen</b>, but the dateline reads <i>\"Actum Kolding\"</i> and the body itself locates the coming Diet <i>\"her wdj Koldinng\"</i> (\"here in Kolding\"). The internal evidence is doubly consistent for <b>Kolding</b> (the Jutland royal residence, Koldinghus); the header therefore leads with the as-written place and appends the catalogued place. The `label` Copenhagen is treated as a catalogue datum, not corrected within the text. <b>Kolding</b> = the town of Kolding in Jutland, seat of Koldinghus castle.</li><li><b>Sum and loan.</b> <i>thett thußinnd daller</i> = \"that thousand daler\"; <i>forstragdtt</i> (= <i>forstrakt</i>) = \"advanced, lent.\" Sophie had lent Tycho 1,000 daler, due at the previous <i>omslag</i> and then deferred.</li><li><b><i>omßlag</b></i> (= <i>omslag</i>) — the <b>omslag</b>, the semi-annual term (especially the <i>snapsting/omslag</i> at Viborg) on which loans, interest, and accounts fell due in Danish financial practice; kept in the original and glossed \"settlement-term.\"</li><li><b><i>herredag(en)</b></i> — the <b>herredag</b> (the Danish royal council-court / Diet of the <i>rigsråd</i>, convened by the crown); rendered \"Diet\" with the term flagged on first use.</li><li><b><i>pindtzdag</b></i> (= <i>pinsedag</i>) — <b>Whitsun</b> (Pentecost). The deferral ran <i>thill thend dag epther pindtzdag ßist forganngen</i> — \"to the day after Whitsun last past\" — i.e. Whit Monday of the recent Whitsuntide; the source gives no civil-calendar figure and none is supplied.</li><li><b>Negations (verified count = 2, reproduced exactly).</b> (1) <i>haffde och <b>icke</b> annditt Oß thillformoditt, ennd ßligtt ethers løffthe schulle werre schied</i> — rendered \"[we] had expected nothing else … than that such a promise of yours should have been kept\": one negation carried by <i>icke … annditt … ennd</i> (\"nothing else … than\"). The clause is a courteous reproach — the queen had assumed the promise would be honoured. (2) <i>saa framptt Wij wed anndre middell <b>icke</b> schulle foraarsagis att ßøge Wor bethallinng</i> — rendered \"lest we should be caused by other means to seek our payment\": <i>saa fremt … icke</i> is the conditional-negative \"in case / lest … not,\" a veiled threat of other (legal/distraint) recovery. Both negations are reproduced; neither is added or dropped.</li><li><b>Conditional / legal force.</b> The operative clause <i>saa framptt Wij wed anndre middell icke schulle foraarsagis att ßøge Wor bethallinng</i> functions as a polite-but-pointed warning: pay now per your promise, <i>so that</i> we are not driven to recover the debt \"by other means.\" The mood is conditional-hortatory; the threat is implicit, and is kept implicit (not sharpened into an explicit legal threat).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1590).</b> <i>WOR gunft thilforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the address-formula, protocol §5.5; the long-s gives <i>gunft</i> for <i>gunst</i>); <i>Wider</i> = imperative <i>vider</i> (\"know ye\"); <i>attj</i> = <i>at I</i> (\"that ye\"); <i>ether</i> = <i>eder</i> (\"you/your\"); <i>naadigst</i> = <i>nådigst</i> (\"most graciously\"); <i>wnderdannigst anfordring</i> = <i>underdanigst anfordring</i> (\"most humble request\"); <i>bedaged</i> = <i>bedaget</i> (\"granted a respite/term\"); <i>erlagd och bethald</i> = <i>erlagt og betalt</i> (\"paid and discharged\"); <i>forhalling</i> = <i>forhaling</i> (\"delay\"); <i>renthen</i> = <i>renten</i> (\"the interest\"); <i>forthøffuedtt</i> = <i>fortøvet</i> (\"forborne, waited\"); <i>thilstillett / thilstillet</i> = <i>tilstillet</i> (\"delivered, made over\"); <i>saa framptt</i> = <i>såfremt</i> (\"provided that / in case\"); <i>att retthe</i> (<i>wide Oß att retthe</i>) = <i>at rette</i> (\"to govern/conduct ourselves\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Kolding thend 18. julij anno 1590</i> = \"Done at <b>Kolding</b>, the 18th of July, the year 1590\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the two negations are verified and exactly reproduced. The flagged soft points are (i) the <b>place crux</b> (dateline + body \"Kolding\" vs. catalogue label \"Copenhagen\"), resolved in favour of the source text but recorded; and (ii) the exact civil date behind <i>pinsedag</i> and the <i>omslag</i> term, which the source does not specify and which is not supplied.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO XIV pp.52-54 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.52-53, text matches Dreyer word for word, corroborated by the adjacent Latin regest (no.82: 'mille thaleros' = 1000 daler, matching the letter's own figure). Sum (1000 daler), the settlement-term terminology, and the date (18 July 1590) all correct. No defects found; the self-assigned published_grade of B was unnecessarily conservative given the clean collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588-92, fol. 80 f.."], "th": {"label": "Dronning Sophie", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15900306001", "next": "brahe-t_15900809001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Demands repayment of a 1,000-daler loan already twice overdue and orders payment at the current Diet in Kolding without further delay.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900718001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900801001", "d": "1590-08-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Jacob Kurz", "ck": "Jacob Kurz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Kurz von Senftenau — Uraniborg, 1 August 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.61</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, NOBILITATE GENERIS ATQUE EXCELLENTI ERUDITIONE IN PRIMIS CONSPICUO, DOMINO IACOBO CURTIO A SENFFTENAW, CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS CONSILIARIO ATQUE IMPERIJ PROCANCELLARIO PRUDENTISSIMO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM HONORANDO, S.</p><p>QUAM mihi gratae fuerint tuae literae, et praeclarae eruditionis et singularis erga me studij plenae, quas cum honorificentissimo Caesareae Maiestatis Privilegio transmisisti, amplissime et nobilissime vir, dici a me paucis non potest. Nec invenio, quî possim pro merito satis magnas tibi agere gratias, tam quod magnificum illud Diploma mihi tua intercessione et autoritate impetrâris, in artis Astronomicae laudem et promotionem scite et decore a teipso ordinatum, quam etiam quod tot tantisque publicae administrationis, quae tibi indies incumbunt, occupationibus detentus, tantum nihilominus otij tibi sumpseris, ut mihi luculenter admodum et amice rescribere non sis gravatus. Pro qua tua utraque benevolentia adeo tibi devinctus sum, ut si quid a me unquam proficisci queat, quod tibi acceptum aut honorificum sit, summa animi propensione memorem et gratum me in omnibus sis inventurus. Idque sincere de me confirmans et pollicens longiore gratiarum actione te non fatigabo, sed ad tuas literas paucis, ut tempus fert, respondebo.</p><p>Quod in principio tam benevolum erga me et rem Astronomicam, quam in hac Insula pro virili excolendam suscepi, declaras affectum, ut si per Caesaris voluntatem id liceret, me hîc invisere et coram Astronomiae repurgandae elaborata adminicula inspicere deque his mecum conferre non grave duceres, id potius tuo singulari candori et in Astronomica propenso studio tribuendum censeo, quam quod de me meisque laboribus tale quid polliceri audeam, quod te tam illustri officio cum magno Reipubl. commodo occupatum huc in Daniam, per tot viarum terrae marisque anfractus allicere mereatur. Utinam tamen idipsum aliquando mihi evenire posset felicitatis, ut te unâ cum communi amico THADDAeO hîc coram intueri liceret, nihil sane mihi magis ex voto acceptiusque contingeret. Solent quidem invisere me in his Uraniae dicatis aedibus plaerique, qui in Daniam appellunt viri Principes aut eorum Legati, quemadmodum et proxime elapso vere serenissimus Scotorum Rex atque illustrissimus Dux Brunsvicensis diverso tempore, cum amplo et splendido comitatu, nostros hîc Lares accedere non dedignati sunt. Verum ut dicam, quod res est, paucissimi inter viros vel prosapiae claritudine, vel officio aliquo eximio rebus Politicis addictos inveniuntur, qui haec studia penitius excoluerunt aut solidum aliquem in his progressum assecuti sunt, utut videri volunt aliquatenus ea suspicere et nullo non in pretio habere. Ideo nostra inventa atque fabricas hîc intuendo, quorsum omnia spectent et quantam observationibus Astronomicis rite peragendis promptitudinem pari certitudine coniunctam praebeant, parum discernunt. Si itaque rerum harum intelligentes et gnaviter cupidi huc accederent (inter quos te praecipuum obtinere locum, et THADDAeUM etiam nostrum, satis mihi conscius sum) facile pro tua summa prudentia aestimâris, quam id foret mihi plurimis nominibus longe gratissimum. Sed ut vos hîc praesentes exoptare quidem mihi pervolupe sit, eius tamen me voti compotem fieri, amplissima et magnifica tua functio, qua assidue apud Caesaream Maiestatem distineris, THADDAeIque ingravescens senium haudquaquam permittent. Ut tamen aliquatenus mea observatoria instrumenta noscere possitis, quorundam modellas (ut vocant) petente id a me per literas Domino THADDAeO, dum hîc moratur Tabellarius, confieri iussi, quas etiam vobis asportandas illi tradidi. Si quam mearum in his structurarum ideam inde concipere atque (modo ita libuerit) imitari detur, equidem libenter id video. Sed vereor, ut ex tam parvis iconibus ipsissimae machinae omnibus suis partibus satis absolute perspiciantur, cum singula tam exigua forma repraesentari nequeant.</p><p>Est deinde praeter alia summopere hoc mihi gratum, quod literis tuis adeo candide de meis scriptis iudicium profers, omniaque in potiorem partem interpretaris. Existimas autem eos, qui pro tuenda Aristotelaea opinione mihi, quod Cometas in aethere procreari asseram, adversantur, id saltem in speciem, et dicis causa (ut Iurisconsulti loquuntur) facere, quo plura ad veritatem enodandam eliciant. Et solet quidem mos hic esse quorundam, qui ignorantiam aperte fateri erubescunt, ut veritati data opera contradicendo, id quod scire avent, oblique extorqueant. Suspicor tamen illos, qui se totos ARISTOTELI addixerunt, non eo nomine hoc agere, ut latentis certitudinis abscondita in apertum deducant, sed potius, ut praeceptoris sui decreta, tot iam seculis pro authenticis habita, integra conservent atque propugnent. Satis enim vident, quod concesso hoc, Cometas in altissimo aethere versari, magnam partem Philosophiae Aristotelicae, praesertim quantum ad Coelestia et Meteorica attinet (ut etiam revera plaeraque in his prorsus falsa sunt) penitus collabascere, seque inde deceptos frustra aetatem talibus nugis consumpsisse. Sed utrum horum sit, in medio relinquens, ego quidem, quantum in me est, quae coelum ipsum per accuratas observationes Geometrice expensas ostendit, in publicum proferre, atque de eis apud intelligentes testificari non intermittam. Credant vel contradicant, qui volent, et quocunque tandem affectu, sive bono sive pervicaci, id fecerint, apud me perinde erit, modo ipsemet satis certo sciam, me nihil citra ratam experientiam atque infallibiles demonstrationes in medium adducere. Veritas enim seipsa contenta est, nec hominum opinionibus afficitur.</p><p>Porro videris mihi adhuc, amplissime Domine CURTI, nonnihil haesitare, an omnes Cometae aetherei sint, in qua ego prorsus persisto sententia, an vero nonnulli eorum sublunares concedi possint. Et quantum colligo, multum te movet eximij illius REGIOMONTANI, tum etiam fortasse VOGELINI Viennensis in duobus Cometis, quos elementares e suis observationibus concluserunt, autoritas. At firmam eorum placitis nequaquam inesse certitudinem, alias ostendere decrevi. Plausibile quidem est, Regiomontanianum Cometam sublunarem fuisse, ex quo motu diurno proprio circa medietatem apparitionis suae 40 gradus circuli magni confecerit. Attamen id sufficienter non probat, infra ☾am necessario extitisse. Neque etiam satis video, quomodo haec cohaereant, Cometam paulo ante medietatem Ianuarij incipientem et usque in finem Februarij perdurantem, non multo plus dimidio caeli ambitu emensum fuisse, cum tamen pro maiori itineris parte 40 gradus uno die absolvisse dicatur, siquidem hac concitatione intra quinos dies semicirculum complevisset. Nec moror, quod REGIOMONTANUS sex graduum parallaxin illi attribuerit, cum ex certis depromptam observationibus demonstrationem, quae id necessario convincat, nequaquam proferat. Videtur autoritate ARISTOTELIS, ut plaerique alij, fascinatus studiose ex erronea aliqua consideratione sic constituisse. Licet enim Mathematicus inprimis excellens fuerit, attamen in observationibus coelestibus citra errorem peragendis non satis versatus erat, neque instrumenta ab omni erroris suspicione vindicata ad manus habuit, prout facile liquet ex ipsius quibusdam in ☉lis et Planetarum iam dudum publicatis observationibus. Si Cometa eius toto durationis tempore per sesquialterum mensem circuli in sphaera magni arcum motu suo proprio designabat, adeo ut integrum semicirculum et paulo ultra in hoc tramite expleverit (quemadmodum ipsemet fatetur) nequaquam elementaris naturae, sed potius coelestis particeps fuit, cum sublunaribus Meteoris talis per circuli maximi ambitum ordinarius incessus tanto tempore et in tot reiteratis per motum primum concitatissimis revolutionibus competere nequeat. Taceo, quod parallaxin admittere sex partium et orbitam circuli magni designare inter se dissentanea sint. Non enim in altiore et decliviore situ intra eiusdem circuli limites ob parallaxeos variam divagationem continebitur. VOGELINI demonstratio incertis nititur Dedomenis, nimis lubrica via et Quadrante puerili conquisitis. Nec motum Cometae proprium, qui parallaxin plus iusto adauxit, discernebat, et temporis intervallo nimis arcto usus est, ut facile tum his tum alijs de caussis in tam enormi parallaxi isti Cometae imputanda labi potuerit. Quin potius illum revera coelestem fuisse tarditas motus proprij, quo non plene tres gradus in unica revolutione diurna absolvebat, testimonium non obscurum praebet. Sed haec uberius convenientiore loco discutienda nobis erunt.</p><p>Quod novas meas coelestium motuum Hypotheses tuo erudito suffragia approbas, plurimum mihi ipsi congratulor. Licet enim illae a quibusdam, praesertim Aristoteleis, in orbium soliditatibus ipsa etiam iudicij praeoccupatione induratis, impugnentur, prout fieri solet circa rem novam et nondum omnibus satis perspectam, ego tamen vel tui solius, qui totum negotium penitius introspicere et undiquaque ponderare optime nosti, de his censuram omnium istorum meris opinationibus praefero. Nec dubium erit, quin per has Hypotheses apparentijs coelestibus abunde admodumque exacte satisfiat, quodque omnium aetatum observationibus congruant, quatenus per veterum non ubique satis subtiliter factas animadversiones id ullo modo praestare licebit. Nam et in ipso Ptolemaeo, utut Aegiptiacis magnifice et Regijs sumtibus extructis organis uso, tam in fixarum quam in errantium stellarum observationibus plaeraque ad praecisionem accuratiorem requisita desidero. Recentiorum, si quae habentur circa coelestia adversiones, dubiae nimisque lato modo acceptae sunt, adeo ut ne quidem magni illius COPERNICI circa has annotationibus satis tuto fidere liceat. Et quid facient, cum ne Solis quidem motum et affixarum stellarum ipsi coelo congruentia loca hactenus rite explorare sustinuerint, ut de instrumentorum, quibus innituntur, fallacitate nihil dicam.</p><p>Sunt mihi in promtu plurimorum proxime antecedentium annorum exquisitae in stellis, tam fixis quam erraticis, atque ambobus luminaribus observationes, quarum eae, quas postremis duodecim, postquam in hac Insula solidiora et ampliora organa ad amussim elaborari curavi, nactus sum, eiuscemodi sunt, ut solidam Astronomiae instaurationem illis superstruere tutum sit. Expecto adhuc nonnullas in tribus penderosioribus Planetis acronychas animadversiones, quo plurimis invicem collatis, in distantioribus praesertim eccentricorum locis, certius et exactius de Apogaeis et eccentricitatibus pronuntiem. Deprehendo enim Apogaea Planetarum intra aliquot gradus non esse rite hactenus disposita, ut eccentricitates, quae etiam paulo aliter se habent, iam praeteream. Quamprimum autem Opus illud Recentiorum caeli Phaenomenωn, quod nunc prae manibus habeo, absolvero, statim ad alterum de mundo coaevorum syderum revolutionibus, favente coelestium Opifice, pertexendum me accingam. Sternent enim haec praecedentia in plurimis ad sequentia viam, et hypotheseos nostrae certitudinem, eo praesertim quod coelum realibus orbibus compactum non sit, ut hactenus frustra creditum est, confirmabunt, atque unâ Solis et fixarum stellarum restitutionem accuratam, sine qua caetera frustra tentantur, suppeditabunt. Maturabo autem, quo ad eius fieri poterit, et a divina clementia concessum fuerit, posterius illud astrorum perennium opus. Facile tamen per teipsum aestimare potes, tantum laborem modico tempore non exantlari. Fui hisce aliquot praecedentibus annis architectonicis curis et instrumentorum variorum fabricatione plurimum occupatus, adeo ut praeter observationum, quas interea conquisivi, aliqualem copiam earundemque, tam inter se, quam cum aliorum inventis collationem et hypotheseos motibus apparentibus convenientem ordinationem nihil fere, quantum ad Tabularum structuram, moliri licuerit: quandoquidem etiam sine tabulis Astronomicis per solam triangularem supputationem Planetarum situs secundum aliquam datam hypothesin explorare non sit difficile. Quamprimum autem caetera, quae prius conficienda suscepi, ad colophonem perduxero, tum demum hypothesium cum observationibus, tam veterum quam nostris, conciliationem et comprobationem Canonumque Astronomicorum hinc deductam calculationem, quae plus laboris quam ingenij requiret, instituere opportunum erit. Interim si quae (uti in rebus fit arduis) morula inciderit, aequanimiter ferenda est, et divinae providentiae, quae actiones et successus rerum humanarum moderatur, omnia cum ipsius auxilij imploratione submisse et patienter committenda.</p><p>Quadrantis ultimo a te adinventa in singula minuta distributio admodum ingeniosa est, et si dextre perficiatur, non dubito, quin usui etiam accommoda sit. Videtur tamen in ipsa constructione paululo laboriosior, et tot alij Quadrantes, intra ultimum ducti, aliquantum spatij occupare necesse habent. Unde fit, ut intimus et centro proximus respectu ultimi plurimum de ambitu amittat, quod et de intermedijs quo ad magis vel minus intelligendum. Sicque divisionibus minimis rite discernendis non aeque in ipsa praxi capaces erunt, ac si iuxta ultimum et maximum Quadrantem omnis subdistinctio perficeretur. Ipsa quoque fiduciae linea non facile unum punctum ab altero discernet, nisi iustae magnitudinis fuerit instrumentum. Certum tamen est, hanc tuam inventionem Nonianâ multis modis praevalere, imo et illi praeferendam, quam ex tua indicatione CLAVIUS suo Scioterico libello in publicum protulit. Ut tamen id, quod res postulat, indicem: omnes eiuscemodi inventiones, licet admodum ingeniosae sint, et quo ad speculationem ipsamque demonstrationem Geometricam rite constent, cum tamen ad praxin mechanicam atque ipsam in observationibus tractandis expeditionem perventumfuerit, tunc demum patefit, an id re ipsa praestent, quod prima fronte pollicentur. Noniana certe distinctio, etiam subtiliore quam ab ipso ratione et pluribus particulis administrata (modus enim, quo is utitur, non suppeditat omnia 90 graduum minuta) ijs, quae autor pollicetur, nequaquam satisfacit, quod in duobus Quadrantibus orichalcicis tali methodo subtilissime elaborata divisione ipsa me dudum docuit experientia. Per hanc etiam rerum magistram vere id assecutus sum, ut nisi Quadrantes per se sufficientem habeant magnitudinem, utpote 4 vel trium ad minimum cubitorum, frustra in subdivisionum compendijs, quibus singula minuta scite exprimantur, laborari. In minoribus etenim organis punctationes, quacunque tandem solertia ordinatae, plures simul quasi in eandem fiduciae lineam incidunt, ut eas ab invicem discernere vix liceat, prout ipsamet experimentatio te satis edocebit. Ego in Quadrantum aut Armillarum et quorumvis arcuum in minimas quasque particulas distributione utor punctis quibusdam transversalibus sextam vel duodecimam graduum partem in singula minuta vel etiam horum aliquotam portionem subdividentibus, prout circa finem libri nostri secundi Aethereorum Phaenomenωn hunc gradus singulos minutatim subdividendi modum quodammodo denotavi, qui prae omnibus alijs et factu facilis et usui in primis accomodatus, tum etiam per se nihil vitij aut dubij suggerens diutina mea tractatione comprobatus est, quem etiam tanquam maxime expeditum et certum omnibus meis instrumentis adhibeo. Ideoque eum, si quando organa Astronomica confici curaveris, tibi commendatum volo. Experiêre utique multo operosioribus inventis, quo ad praxeos indubiam administrationem praevalere. Hoc quoque illustrissimus Princeps WILHELMUS, Landgravius Hassiae, in suis Quadrantibus atque Sextantibus aliquot iam annis, postquam ex instructione WITICHIJ ad meorum imitationem sua instrumenta, tam quo ad graduum subdivisionem quam pinnacidiorum applicationem, renovavit, feliciter utitur. Habet insuper haec ratio id peculiare, quod parvo spatio comprehensa Armillis etiam quibusvis applicari possit, quod in alijs, ubi plurimae introrsum circinationes ducuntur, minus competit.</p><p>Postremo intelligo, te etiam in triangulorum erudita scientia egregie et solide versatum, et compendia in his numerorum tractationi faciliori inservientia ingeniose et utiliter adinvenire, quod facio, ut par est, plurimi. Quin et ego triangulorum supputationem compendiosiorem reddere non parum laboravi. Si tua etiam inventa et aliorum, quotquot nancisci liceat (plus enim vident oculi quam oculus) mecum per opportunitatem communicaveris, rem pergratam praestiteris.</p><p>Sed ut Epistolae modum non excedam, nec te gravioribus publici officij oneribus occupatum nimium detineam, modo finem faciam, omnium Creatori Deo ex animo te commendans. Is te in Reipub. tam politicae quam literariae, ornamentum et emolumentum diutissime magnis donis et varijs bonis florentem conservare dignetur. Vale, arnplissime vir, atque haec inter plurimas alias curas exarata pro tuo singulari candore aequi bonique consule, meque tua benevolentia atque amore, quod facis, in posterum complecti non intermitte. Ego vicissim, quantum in me est, quocunque genere officij liceat, te demereri conabor, atque ad gratificandum perpetuo quam paratissimum me praestabo.</p><p>Iterum, mi CURTI, amice eximie, vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die primo Augusti veteri stylo Anno 1590.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most distinguished man, conspicuous above all for nobility of birth and outstanding erudition, Master Jacob Kurz von Senftenau, most prudent Councillor of His Imperial Majesty and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, his most honoured friend — greeting.</b></p><p>How welcome to me your letter was — full both of distinguished learning and of singular regard toward me — which you forwarded together with the most honourable Privilege of His Imperial Majesty, most distinguished and most noble man, cannot be told by me in few words. Nor do I find how I might render you thanks great enough to match the deserving, both because you obtained for me, by your intercession and authority, that magnificent Diploma — drawn up by yourself with skill and grace, to the praise and advancement of the astronomical art — and also because, detained though you are by so many and so great occupations of public administration, which press upon you day by day, you nonetheless took so much leisure for yourself that you were not reluctant to write me a reply so very luminous and so friendly. For both these kindnesses of yours I am so bound to you that, if anything could ever proceed from me that should be acceptable or honourable to you, you will find me mindful and grateful in all things with the utmost readiness of mind. And affirming and promising this sincerely concerning myself, I shall not weary you with a longer rendering of thanks, but shall answer your letter in few words, as the time allows.</p><p>As to your declaring, at the outset, so kindly a disposition toward me and toward the astronomical undertaking which I have taken up to cultivate on this island to the best of my powers — namely, that, if it were permitted by the Emperor's will, you would not count it a burden to visit me here and to inspect in person the elaborated aids for the purifying [reform] of astronomy and to confer with me about them — this I judge should rather be ascribed to your singular candour and to your studious inclination toward astronomical matters than that I should dare to promise anything of the sort concerning myself and my labours that would deserve to entice you, occupied with so illustrious an office to the great benefit of the Commonwealth, hither into Denmark, through so many windings of road by land and sea. Yet would that it might one day befall me as a piece of good fortune that I should be allowed to behold you here in person, together with our common friend [Tadeáš] Hájek — truly nothing could come to me more after my wish or more welcome. Indeed many of the princely men, or their envoys, who put in to Denmark are accustomed to visit me in this house dedicated to Urania — just as, this very spring last past, the most serene King of Scots and the most illustrious Duke of Brunswick, at different times, with an ample and splendid retinue, did not disdain to approach our hearth here. But, to say what the matter is, very few are found among the men devoted to political affairs, whether by the brightness of their lineage or by some eminent office, who have cultivated these studies more deeply or attained some solid progress in them — however much they wish to seem to look up to them somewhat and to hold them in no small esteem. And so, in looking upon our inventions and constructions here, they little discern to what end all things tend, and how great a readiness, joined with equal certainty, they afford for the duly carrying out of astronomical observations. If, therefore, men understanding of these matters and zealously desirous of them were to come here (among whom I am well aware that you hold the foremost place, and our [Hájek] too), you will easily reckon, in your great prudence, how this would be to me, on very many counts, by far the most welcome thing. But while it is most delightful to me indeed to long for you present here, yet your most ample and magnificent function, by which you are continually detained at His Imperial Majesty's side, and [Hájek's] advancing old age, will by no means permit me to obtain this wish. That you may, however, in some measure come to know my observational instruments, I have had certain little models (as they call them) made — Master [Hájek] having requested it of me by letter — while the courier was tarrying here, and I have handed these over to him to be carried to you as well. If from these any notion of my structures may be conceived and (provided it should so please) imitated, I for my part gladly see it. But I fear that from such small pictures the actual machines themselves may not be seen well enough in all their parts and entire, since the several details cannot be represented in so tiny a form.</p><p>Next, this is, beyond other things, exceedingly pleasing to me: that in your letter you set forth your judgement so candidly concerning my writings, and interpret everything in the better part. You consider, moreover, that those who oppose me — for the sake of upholding the Aristotelian opinion — in that I assert comets to be generated in the aether, do this only for appearance's sake, and (as the jurisconsults say) \"for the sake of argument\" [dicis causa], so as to draw out more toward the unravelling of the truth. And indeed this is wont to be the way of certain men who blush to confess ignorance openly: by deliberately contradicting the truth, they wrest out obliquely that which they are eager to know. Yet I suspect that those who have given themselves wholly over to Aristotle do this not on that account — to bring the hidden things of a latent certainty into the open — but rather to keep whole and to defend the decrees of their master, held now for so many ages as authentic. For they see well enough that, this once granted — that comets dwell in the highest aether — a great part of Aristotelian Philosophy, especially as regards the celestial and the meteorological matters (as indeed most things in these are utterly false), would wholly collapse, and that they themselves, deceived thereby, had vainly spent their lifetime on such trifles. But leaving it undecided which of these it is, I for my part, so far as in me lies, shall not cease to bring forth into public what the heaven itself shows through accurate observations geometrically weighed, and to testify of them among the understanding. Let those who will believe or contradict, and with whatever disposition in the end they shall have done it, whether well-meaning or obstinate, it will be all the same with me — provided I myself know with sufficient certainty that I bring forward into the midst nothing short of ratified experience and infallible demonstrations. For truth is content with itself, and is not affected by the opinions of men.</p><p>Furthermore, you seem to me still to hesitate somewhat, most distinguished Master Kurz, whether all comets are aetherial — in which opinion I altogether persist — or whether some of them may be granted to be sublunar. And so far as I gather, you are much moved by the authority of that eminent Regiomontanus, and then perhaps also of Vögelin of Vienna, in the two comets which from their observations they concluded to be elementary. But that no firm certainty resides in their assertions I have resolved to show elsewhere. It is plausible indeed that the Regiomontanian comet was sublunar, inasmuch as by its own diurnal motion it accomplished, about the middle of its appearance, 40 degrees of a great circle. Yet this does not sufficiently prove that it necessarily existed below the Moon [☾]. Nor do I see well enough how these things hang together: that the comet, beginning a little before the middle of January and lasting until the end of February, traversed not much more than half the compass of the sky, when nonetheless it is said to have completed, for the greater part of its journey, 40 degrees in a single day — since at this speed it would have completed a semicircle within five days. Nor do I dwell on the fact that Regiomontanus attributed to it a parallax of six degrees, since he by no means brings forward a demonstration drawn from sure observations that would necessarily prove it. He seems, bewitched by the authority of Aristotle — like most others — to have so determined it studiously, out of some erroneous consideration. For although he was a most excellent mathematician, yet he was not sufficiently versed in carrying out celestial observations free from error, nor had he to hand instruments vindicated from all suspicion of error — as is easily clear from certain of his long-since-published observations on the Sun [☉] and the planets. If his comet, throughout its whole duration of a month and a half, marked out by its own motion an arc of a great circle in the sphere, so much so that it filled out a whole semicircle and a little beyond on this track (as he himself confesses), then it was by no means of an elementary nature, but rather a partaker of the celestial — since such a regular procession through the compass of a greatest circle, over so long a time and in so many repeated, most rapid revolutions by the prime motion, cannot belong to sublunar meteors. I pass over in silence that to admit a parallax of six parts [degrees] and to mark out the orbit of a great circle are mutually inconsistent. For it will not be contained within the limits of the same circle, at a higher and a lower position, on account of the varying wandering produced by parallax. Vögelin's demonstration rests on uncertain data [Dedomena], obtained by too slippery a way and with a childish quadrant. Nor did he discern the comet's own motion, which increased the parallax more than was right; and he used too narrow an interval of time, so that he could easily, both for these and for other reasons, fall into error in imputing so enormous a parallax to that comet. Rather, that it was in truth celestial, the slowness of its own motion — by which it completed not fully three degrees in a single diurnal revolution — affords no obscure testimony. But these things will have to be discussed by us more fully in a fitter place.</p><p>That by your learned vote you approve my new Hypotheses of the celestial motions, I greatly congratulate myself. For although they are assailed by certain men — especially the Aristotelians, hardened in the solidities of the orbs by the very prepossession of their own judgement, as is wont to happen with a matter that is new and not yet sufficiently discerned by all — yet I prefer the verdict of you alone concerning these things, who know best how to look more deeply into the whole business and to weigh it on every side, to the mere opinionings of all those others. Nor will there be any doubt that by these Hypotheses the celestial appearances are satisfied abundantly and very exactly, and that they agree with the observations of every age — so far as it will in any way be possible to achieve this, given the observations of the ancients, not everywhere subtly enough made. For even in Ptolemy himself — though he used instruments magnificently and at royal expense erected in Egypt — I find wanting, both in the fixed and in the wandering stars, most of the things required for a more accurate precision. As for the more recent men, such observations of the celestial bodies as are had are doubtful and taken in too loose a manner, so much so that one cannot safely enough trust even the annotations of that great Copernicus concerning these matters. And what will they do, since they have not hitherto duly undertaken to explore even the motion of the Sun and the places of the fixed stars in agreement with the heaven itself — to say nothing of the fallibility of the instruments on which they rely?</p><p>I have ready to hand exquisite observations of very many of the immediately preceding years, in the stars both fixed and wandering and in both luminaries; and of these, the ones I have obtained in the last twelve years — after I had had more solid and ample instruments precisely fashioned on this island — are of such a kind that it is safe to build a solid restoration of astronomy upon them. I am still awaiting some acronychal observations in the three more ponderous planets, so that, with very many compared with one another — especially in the more distant places of the eccentrics — I may pronounce more surely and exactly concerning the apogees and eccentricities. For I find that the apogees of the planets have not hitherto been rightly disposed, within several degrees — to pass over for now the eccentricities, which also stand a little otherwise. But as soon as I have completed that Work on the Phenomena of the recent heaven [the comets], which I now have in hand, I shall at once gird myself, with the favour of the Maker of the celestial things, to weave through the other — concerning the world and the revolutions of the coeval heavenly bodies. For these preceding [works] will in many points pave the way to those that follow, and will confirm the certainty of our hypothesis — especially in this, that the heaven is not compacted of real orbs, as has hitherto been vainly believed — and will at the same time supply the accurate restoration of the Sun and of the fixed stars, without which the rest are attempted in vain. I shall hasten on, so far as it shall be able to be done and as it shall be granted by the divine clemency, that latter work of the perennial stars. Yet you can easily reckon for yourself that so great a labour is not drained out [completed] in a modest time. In these several preceding years I have been very much occupied with architectural cares and with the fabrication of various instruments, so much so that — besides amassing some store of observations, which I gathered in the meantime, and a collation of them, both with one another and with the inventions of others, and an ordering of them conformable to the hypothesis of the apparent motions — I have been able to undertake almost nothing as regards the construction of Tables: seeing that, even without astronomical tables, it is not difficult, by triangular computation alone, to explore the positions of the planets according to some given hypothesis. But as soon as I shall have brought to the colophon [completion] the other things which I undertook to finish first, then at last it will be opportune to set in train the reconciling and corroborating of the hypotheses with the observations, both of the ancients and our own, and the calculation of the Astronomical Canons [tables] deduced therefrom — which will require more labour than wit. Meanwhile, if any little delay should fall in (as happens in arduous matters), it must be borne with an even mind, and all things must be committed, with imploring of His help, submissively and patiently to the divine providence, which governs the actions and outcomes of human affairs.</p><p>The distribution into single minutes, lately invented by you, of the quadrant is exceedingly ingenious, and if it be rightly carried out, I do not doubt that it is also suited to use. Yet it seems in its very construction a little more laborious, and so many other quadrants, drawn within the outermost one, must of necessity occupy a certain amount of space. Whence it comes about that the innermost one, nearest the centre, loses very much of its compass with respect to the outermost — which is also to be understood of the intermediate ones, in greater or lesser degree. And thus they will not in actual practice be equally capable of duly distinguishing the smallest divisions, as if every subdivision were carried out alongside the outermost and greatest quadrant. The line of sight [line of confidence] itself, too, will not easily distinguish one point from another, unless the instrument be of a fitting size. Yet it is certain that this invention of yours prevails over the Nonian one in many ways — indeed is even to be preferred to that which, on your indication, Clavius brought forth into public in his little book on the sundial [the <i>Scioterico</i>]. But, that I may point out what the matter demands: all inventions of this kind, however ingenious they be, and however rightly they stand as regards speculation and geometrical demonstration itself, yet when it comes to mechanical practice and to expedition itself in handling observations, only then does it become plain whether they perform in fact what they promise at first sight. The Nonian distinction, certainly — even when administered by a subtler method than his own and with more particles (for the method he uses does not supply all the minutes of the 90 degrees) — by no means satisfies what its author promises; which experience itself long ago taught me by the division most subtly elaborated by such a method on two brass [orichalc] quadrants. Through this same mistress of things [experience] I have truly attained this also: that unless the quadrants have of themselves sufficient size — to wit, of four or at the least three cubits — labour is spent in vain on the contrivances of subdivision by which the single minutes might be skilfully expressed. For on smaller instruments the points [markings], ordered with whatever ingenuity in the end, fall together, several at once, as it were onto the same line of sight, so that one can scarcely distinguish them from one another — as experimentation itself will teach you well enough. I, in the distribution of quadrants or armillae and of any arcs whatever into all their smallest particles, use certain transversal points, subdividing the sixth or twelfth part of the degrees into single minutes or even into some aliquot portion of these — as, toward the end of our second book of the Aetherial Phenomena, I have in some fashion noted this manner of subdividing the single degrees minute by minute; which, before all others both easy to make and especially suited to use, and moreover of itself suggesting no fault or doubt, has been proved by my long handling of it, and which I also apply to all my instruments as the most expeditious and certain. And therefore I would have it commended to you, if ever you have astronomical instruments made. You will assuredly find by experience that it prevails over much more laborious inventions as regards the undoubted management of practice. This too the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, happily uses in his quadrants and sextants for some years now — after, on the instruction of Wittich, in imitation of mine, he renewed his instruments, both as regards the subdivision of the degrees and the application of the sights [pinnules]. This method has, besides, this peculiar advantage: that, comprised in a small space, it can also be applied to armillae of any kind whatever — which holds less for others, where very many circlings are drawn inward.</p><p>Lastly, I understand that you too are excellently and solidly versed in the learned science of triangles, and ingeniously and usefully devise abridgements serving in these for the easier handling of numbers — which, as is fitting, I esteem very highly. Indeed I too have laboured not a little to render the computation of triangles more compendious. If you also communicate to me, as opportunity allows, your inventions and those of others, as many as can be obtained (for eyes see more than one eye), you will have done a most welcome thing.</p><p>But that I may not exceed the measure of a letter, nor detain you too long, occupied as you are with the heavier burdens of public office, I shall now make an end, commending you with all my heart to God the Creator of all. May He deign to keep you long flourishing, an ornament and benefit to the Commonwealth both political and literary, with great gifts and various good things. Farewell, most distinguished man, and take in good and equitable part these things written amid very many other cares, in keeping with your singular candour; and do not cease in future to embrace me with your kindness and love, as you do. I in turn, so far as in me lies, in whatever kind of service may be possible, shall strive to deserve well of you, and shall ever show myself most ready to do you a kindness.</p><p>Again, my Kurz, excellent friend, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg on the first day of August, Old Style, in the year 1590.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Uraniborg on Hven.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Iacobus Curtius a Senfftenaw</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jakob Kurtz; d. 1594), Imperial Councillor and Vice-Chancellor (<i>Imperij Procancellarius</i>) under Rudolf II at Prague, an accomplished mathematician in his own right. He had secured for Tycho the Imperial Privilege protecting his works (the <i>Diploma</i>) and corresponded with him on instruments and on the cometary question.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i>, vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness as given in the source header: Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.61.</li><li><b><i>THADDAeUS</b></i> — <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), Bohemian court physician and astronomer at Prague, the \"common friend\" who linked Tycho and Kurz; he had requested the instrument models. Identified in brackets in the translation on each occurrence, as the source gives only the first name.</li><li><b>Distinguished visitors.</b> <i>serenissimus Scotorum Rex</i> = <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who visited Hven in spring 1590 during his Danish marriage journey; <i>illustrissimus Dux Brunsvicensis</i> = the <b>Duke of Brunswick</b> (Henry Julius of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel). \"This very spring last past\" (<i>proxime elapso vere</i>) = spring 1590, O.S. — flagged as in-letter dating, not reconciled.</li><li><b>Greek-letter genitives.</b> <i>Phaenomenωn</i> (twice: <i>Recentiorum caeli Phaenomenωn</i>; <i>Aethereorum Phaenomenωn</i>) writes the Greek genitive-plural ending with the Greek letter omega (ω) — a learned typographic flourish for the Latinised <i>Phaenomenon/Phaenomena</i>. Reproduced verbatim in the Source block; rendered \"Phenomena\" in the translation. These name Tycho's projected/forthcoming works: the <i>Opus Recentiorum caeli Phaenomenωn</i> = his treatise on recent comets (published 1588 as <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, of which the comet of 1577 is book II — <i>liber secundus Aethereorum Phaenomenωn</i>), and the planned companion <i>de mundi … syderum revolutionibus</i> (the never-completed <i>Theatrum astronomicum</i> / planetary theory).</li><li><b>Greek loan-word.</b> <i>Dedomenis</i> (<i>Dedomena</i>, δεδομένα, \"givens/data\") — the technical term for the data of a problem; transcribed and glossed \"data\" in the translation.</li><li><b>Planet/luminary glyphs (named in English per protocol §5.3; glyph kept in Source block):</b> ☾ (<i>infra ☾am</i> = \"below the Moon,\" with the glyph inflected as a Latin accusative <i>Lunam</i>) → <b>Moon</b>; ☉ (<i>in ☉lis</i> = <i>in Solis</i>, \"on the Sun['s observations],\" glyph inflected as genitive <i>Solis</i>) → <b>Sun</b>.</li><li><b>Cometary debate.</b> <i>Cometas in aethere procreari</i> — Tycho's central thesis (from <i>De mundi … recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588) that comets are generated in the aether, above the Moon, refuting the Aristotelian doctrine of comets as sublunar exhalations (<i>Meteora</i>). <i>REGIOMONTANUS</i> = <b>Johann Müller of Königsberg</b> (Regiomontanus, 1436–1476), whose treatise on the comet (the comet of 1472) assigned it a 6° parallax and hence a sublunar location; <i>VOGELINUS Viennensis</i> = <b>Georg Vögelin</b> (Vogelin) of Vienna, author of a <i>Significatio cometae</i> on the comet of 1532, likewise concluding a sublunar (elementary) comet. Tycho's technical objections (the 40°/day proper motion, the semicircle-in-five-days reductio, the inconsistency of a 6° parallax with a great-circle path, Vögelin's \"childish quadrant\" and too-short time-base) are rendered literally; no astronomical \"correction\" is supplied.</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>Apogaea</i> → <b>apogees</b>; <i>eccentricitates / eccentricorum</i> → <b>eccentricities / eccentrics</b>; <i>Hypotheses (motuum)</i> → <b>Hypotheses (of the motions)</b>; <i>orbium soliditatibus</i> / <i>realibus orbibus compactum</i> → \"the solidities of the orbs\" / \"compacted of real orbs\" (Tycho's denial of solid celestial spheres); <i>acronychas animadversiones</i> → <b>acronychal observations</b> (i.e. at acronychal/opposition rising); <i>restitutio / instauratio astronomiae</i> → <b>restoration of astronomy</b> (§5.1); <i>organa</i> → <b>instruments</b>; <i>Quadrantes / Sextantes / Armillae</i> → <b>quadrants / sextants / armillae</b>; <i>pinnacidiorum</i> → <b>sights (pinnules)</b> (§5.2); <i>Canones Astronomici</i> → <b>Astronomical Canons (tables)</b>; <i>triangularis supputatio</i> → <b>triangular computation</b> (trigonometric calculation).</li><li><b>Quadrant-subdivision passage.</b> <i>Quadrantis … in singula minuta distributio</i> — Kurz's proposed method of subdividing a quadrant's arc to single minutes by nested concentric quadrant-arcs. <i>Noniana … distinctio</i> = the <b>nonius</b> subdivision of <b>Pedro Nunes (Petrus Nonius)</b>, a nested set of 45 auxiliary arcs (not the later Vernier); <i>CLAVIUS … suo Scioterico libello</i> = <b>Christoph Clavius</b>, who published a version of the Nonius scale (on Kurz's indication) in his <i>Gnomonices libri octo</i> / sundial treatise (<i>scioterico</i> = relating to the sundial, σκιαθηρικόν). <i>transversalibus punctis</i> = Tycho's own <b>transversal (diagonal) point</b> subdivision, described \"toward the end of book II of the Aetherial Phenomena.\" <i>orichalcicis</i> = of <b>orichalc/brass</b>. <i>WITICHIUS</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (d. 1586), the Silesian mathematician who carried Tycho's instrument-design ideas to Kassel; <i>WILHELMUS, Landgravius Hassiae</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (§5.6).</li><li><b>Latin idiom.</b> <i>dicis causa</i> — a legal phrase (\"for form's sake / for the sake of argument\"), explicitly flagged by Tycho as the jurisconsults' usage; kept and glossed. <i>ad colophonem perduxero</i> — \"bring to the colophon,\" i.e. to completion (printer's metaphor). <i>rerum magistra</i> (sc. <i>experientia</i>) — \"the mistress of things,\" experience. <i>plus vident oculi quam oculus</i> — proverbial (\"more eyes see more than one eye\"); kept as the sententia it is.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datae Uraniburgi die primo Augusti veteri stylo Anno 1590</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 1 August, Old Style, 1590.\" Tycho's explicit <i>veteri stylo</i> (O.S.) is significant given that his Imperial correspondent at Catholic Prague would reckon New Style; the header date matches the dateline. No discrepancy with the catalogue label (1 August 1590).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout; the register and all technical content are matched to the controlled glossary. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) <i>paucissimi … vel prosapiae claritudine, vel officio aliquo eximio rebus Politicis addictos … qui haec studia penitius excoluerunt</i> — the relative clause's antecedent is read as the (few) men of rank/office who <i>have</i> cultivated these studies (i.e. very few such men exist); the syntax is dense but the sense is secure. (2) <i>fiduciae linea</i> is rendered \"line of sight (line of confidence)\" — the <i>linea fiduciae</i> of a sighted instrument; the literal \"line of confidence\" is retained in brackets. No genuine crux requiring a [C].</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — rendered securely throughout; explicit </i>veteri stylo<i> date, no lacuna/numeral crux; both soft points judged sense-secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all Dreyer page images for TBOO VII pp.256-262 (slice pages 1-7) in two chunks (1-4, 5-8) and collated the full letter sentence-by-sentence against source_text and published_english, including the salutation, all eight paragraphs of dense content, and the closing/date line. Verified every proper name (Iacobus Curtius/Kurz, Thaddaeus/Hajek, Regiomontanus, Vogelinus, Aristoteles, Ptolemaeus, Copernicus, Clavius, Wilhelmus Landgravius Hassiae, Witichius, Scotorum Rex, Dux Brunsvicensis), every number (40 degrees x2, five days, six degrees/parts x2, three degrees, twelve years, four/three cubits, 90 degrees, sixth/twelfth part), and the closing date (die primo Augusti veteri stylo Anno 1590 = 'the first day of August, Old Style, in the year 1590'). Dreyer's printed Latin matches source_text throughout with no corruption. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found; hedges (Suspicor, Videtur, quantum colligo) are preserved as hedges in English.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.61."], "th": {"label": "Jacob Kurz", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15900628001", "next": "brahe-t_15901201002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900628001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Kurz for procuring the Imperial Privilege on his behalf and for writing so luminous a reply despite heavy duties of public administration.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900801001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900803001", "d": "1590-08-03", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek) — Uraniborg, 3 August 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.60; B = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.50</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 21 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p><p><b>Editorial note.</b> Tycho's <b>direct reply to Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b>: a point-by-point rebuttal of Hájek's thesis that comet-motions cannot be reduced to law, plus the new Caesarean <b>Privilege</b> (via Curtius), the <b>Craig</b> dossier, instrument <b>models</b>, the <b>paper-mill</b> publication delay, and the <b>21 July 1590 solar eclipse</b> (the same eclipse, book-returns, and prosthaphaeresis crux that recur in the Magini letter of the same period, 1 December 1590). The transcription is comparatively clean (only light OCR slips, corrected and flagged; <b>no heavy apparatus-sigla</b> as in Hájek's letter of 11 Jun 1590 or the undated Peucer letter of 1590). Greek-inflected forms (<i>Phaenomenωn</i> = φαινομένων; <i>Dedomena/Dedomenis</i> = δεδόμενα) and the glyphs (♂ ♀ ☿ ☉ ♄ ♃ ♌; <i>☉lem</i> = Solem, <i>☉lis</i> = Solis) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"Uraniburgi die 3. Augusti stylo veteri Anno 1590\"</i>) is explicitly <b>Old Style</b>; it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VII pp. 264–275</b> (running head \"TYCHO AD THADDAEUM HAGECIUM 3 AUG. 1590\"). <b>All cruxes confirmed</b>: the comet daily-motion figures (1577 comet 6°→¼°/day, book 2 ch. 8, p. 278; the Feb–Mar 1590 comet 7½°→1½°/day over 12 days, ⅛ of a circle, \"3rd column\" of the table, p. 279); the prosthaphaeresis dates <b>24 Nov / 26 Dec</b> (with the \"pag. 279\" cross-ref and \"two whole months,\" pp. 283–284); the <b>1577-not-1580</b> correction of Hájek's slip (p. 282); the Craig/Regiomontanus parallax (Problems 2/3/4, the 18-minute interval, p. 285); the <b>21 July 1590 eclipse</b> (6ʰ51ᵐ / 9ʰ exactly / 4 of 8 digits / Leo, p. 289); and the production notes (5–6 artificers, 3rd year, 3+5 gatherings, chs. 7/9/10, two papermakers, Apian's comet of 1531, p. 287–288). Dreyer also prints a short closing CURTIO postscript (p. 275) that the source block folds into the body.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRÆSTANTISSIMO VIRO, MULTIPLICIQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE<br>EXCELLENTI DOMINO THADDÆO HAGECIO AB<br>HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIÆ ET MEDICINÆ DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS<br>COLENDO. S.</p><p>QUOD ultimis illis brevioribus per Studiosum Rostochiensem missis pollicebaris, optime et<br>eruditissime mi THADDÆE, amice<br>colendissime, id iam nunc satis superque præstitum est. Accessit enim hisce<br>diebus Tabellarius Pragensis unâ cum novo<br>Privilegio Cæsareo, magnifici Domini CURTIJ tuasque literas secum asportans, quæ omnia mihi erant<br>longe acceptissima , tibique quas possum ago gratias, quod ad impetrandum mihi a Cæsarea<br>Maiestate hoc de novo Privilegium amplissimum Dominum Procancellarium<br>permoveris. Est sane illud satis splendidum atque honorificum, in quo non<br>habeam, quod amplius desiderem. Neque si Gallicanum ad vos pervenisset (quod<br>nescio qua incuria interciderit; neque enim transcriptio illa, quam mittere<br>intendebam, uspiam hîc reperitur) multum ad negotij ampliorem atque solidiorem<br>absolutionem præstitisset. Expecto adhuc aliud a novo illo Galliarum Rege<br>Navarræo, sed vix pari erga studia Mathematica affectione, cum nulli, quod<br>sciam, in Aulis Regum ab intimis sint consilijs, qui Mathemata adeo impense<br>atque solide non solum ament, sed etiam intelligant, atque præstantissimus ille<br>Dominus CURTIUS, cuius beneficio<br>Cæsareanum hoc Diploma impetratum atque adornatum esse grata mente agnosco. Sed<br>cum ipsimet hac de re et ijs, quæ eruditissima ipsius Epistola continentur,<br>scribam, nolo ea pluribus hîc attingere, sed potius ad respondendum hisce tuis<br>ultimis procedam. In quibus tamen eam partem, quæ ab<br>initio de rescriptionis mutuæ retardationibus atque excusationibus affatim agit,<br>præteribo, cum amicitia nostra multo iam tempore confirmata omni suspicione<br>vacare debeat, neque purgatione, si quid præter expectationem inciderit,<br>multopere indigeat.</p><p>De hypothesibus etiam meis, quas cœlo ipso accuratius introspecto,<br>Martio præsertim sydere plus Sole in acronycho situ terris appropinquante (ut<br>non solum parallaxes eius summa diligentia a nobis aliquoties perquisitæ, sed<br>transitus ipsi diurni aliquanto Ptolemaicis ratiocinijs ob maiorem appropinquationem<br>concitatiores aperte evineunt) extructis ordinatisque nihil hîc superaddam;<br>satis de ijs, quantum Epistola fert, superioribus egi; plenarie eas elucidare<br>citraque iustam contradictionem comprobare atque asserere alterius erit loci et<br>prolixioris negotij. Utrumque etiam eorum, quos omitti vis, et ego hîc lubens<br>negligam, neque de priore isto iam antea scripsissem, nisi ipsemet occasionem<br>præbuisses. Alter Mathemata rectius intelligit, et tibi reconciliatus ferendus<br>veniet. Ad ea itaque, quæ circa defensionem Mesthlinianam a me institutam, præsertim quia non impossibile<br>esse Cometarum motus sub hypothesin aliquam eorum apparentijs analogam redigere,<br>senserim, quod etiamnum in controversiam moves, properabo. Quæ de tuis<br>Mastigibus et calumniatoribus iusto procul dubio dolore<br>impulsus intermisces, cum isti nihil, quod ad rem faciat, in medium adferant, et<br>ob id mentionem ulteriorem non mereantur, eos prætermittens ipsam quæstionem, in<br>qua tibi adhuc non satisfactum esse ais, plenius discutiam, tuisque dubijs atque<br>obiectionibus ulterius obviabo. Id quod tibi non ingratum confido, imo sic fore,<br>propria tua, qua disputationem illam claudis, petitio testatur.</p><p>Cometas certæ motuum legi, quemadmodum in Planetis mundo coævis<br>fit, astringi non posse, ita ut universalis aliqua omnibus sufficiens<br>constituatur hypothesis, tibi equidem non invite concesserim, neque id unquam<br>asserui. Sed hæc est mea de his sententia, quam te nondum integre percepisse<br>video: quod videlicet Cometæ, ex quo omnes revera in ipso cœlo procreentur<br>(statuat ex his aliquos sublunares, qui volet, mihi id nequaquam consonum<br>videtur) necessario etiam cœlestis naturæ participes sint, utut ea ad tantam<br>perfectionem non sit perducta, quanta in perennibus illis corporibus spectatur.<br>Cum autem cœlum ipsum et quæ in eo revolvuntur corpora, persectissimum motum, id<br>est circularem, sive simplicem sive compositum, naturaliter appetant atque<br>sortiantur, non inconveniens duco, ipsos quoque Cometas circularem et ordinarium<br>in cœlo exercere cursum, qui si forte ad instar Planetarum non omni ex parte<br>absolutus fuerit, eos tamen æmulatur et quam proxime ad orbicularem et uniformem<br>incessum tendit. Non tamen ubique Solem, ut Planetæ, nec Terram, ut luminaria et fixæ, pro centro suorum circuituum<br>respiciunt, sed in immensa mundi vastitate quodvis punctum, cuius respectu motus<br>eorum circularis atque æquabilis evadat, sortiri possunt, quemadmodum etiam<br>varia et diversa motuum lege cientur, et nunc in has, nunc illas cœli partes pro<br>libito excurrunt, ideoque non omnibus una et eadem sufficiet hypothesis, sed<br>quilibet eorum propriam requiret, per ipsas apparentias, quasi ex posteriore,<br>constituendam. Et licet maior hîc subsit difficultas, quam in Planetis, qui<br>integras periodos aliquoties absolvunt et diutinæ observationi patent, non tamen<br>ob id extremam impossibilitatem huic negotio inesse statuendum erit, antequam<br>multiplici atque indefesso conatu, an eo pervenire liceat, experti fuerimus. Ego<br>sane in prælustri illo Cometa anni 77mi, cuius motus adeo<br>inæqualis videbatur, ut ab initio 6 proxime gradus, in fine vix quartam partem<br>unius quotidiano incessu confecerit, satis evidenter ostendi ad regularem<br>peneque æquabilem et circularem progressionis ordinationem<br>astringi potuisse, adeo ut numeri ex hypothesi præsupposita deducti ipsis<br>observationibus certius et promptius congruerent, quam in ullis Planetis<br>hactenus effectum est, prout caput 8 libri nostri 2di (modo<br>quis illud attentius introspiciat) abunde testatur. Si igitur in hoc Cometa, qui<br>saltem quartam cœli partem suo ductu percurrere videbatur, et interea plane<br>inæqualem, a celeriore tamen in tardiorem pedetentim sese remittentem motum<br>exprimebat, præstari potuit, ut certis et orbicularibus tramitibus eius<br>inæquales apparentiæ excusarentur: cur in alijs etiam idem, etsi diversimode,<br>prout cuiusque fert nobis exhibitus motus, fieri posse omnino impossibile<br>duxerimus? Licet vero magna hîc subijt difficultas, non tamen, ut tu vis, summa.<br>Ac si maxime id detur, possibilitatem tamen omnem non penitus excludet: multa<br>enim sunt supremæ difficultati obnoxia, ad quæ humani ingenij industria atque<br>labor pertingere ægerrime potest, quæ tamen ob id non prorsus impossibilia,<br>qualis est quadratura circuli, ne quidem a recentioribus, qui eam adinvenisse<br>sibi persuaserunt, adamussim hactenus demonstrata: hæc tamen inter scibilia sed<br>nondum cognita numeratur. At tu in tot Cometis per multa secula ex eorum<br>apparitionibus possibilitatem hanc, de qua loquor, utut difficultatis plenam,<br>luculenter evinci postulas. Verum cum te non lateat, nullos Cometas exacta<br>amussi ante nostra tempora (licet et nunc plurimi in his intolerabiliter<br>impegerint) cœlitus infallibilibus organis observatos esse, adeo ut ne quidem in<br>æthere eos versari per parallaxium exilitatem exploratum fuerit, nedum ut inde<br>æthereus aliquis et ordinarius incessus demonstretur, frustra ex paucissimis<br>ijsdemque nullius momenti Dedomenis dubij huius resolutionem exigis. Quod in<br>unico illo anni 77mi Cometa fieri posse ostendi, etsi non<br>impugnes, tamen aliorum iudicio hoc totum negotium submittis, ac plurium Cometarum testimonijs confirmari cupis. Sane si aliorum<br>iudicia, utut eruditionis laude præstantium, expectas, vereor, ne nimis diu<br>frustraque veritatem in abscondito latentem quæras, in tanta iudiciorum<br>varietate et pertinacia, ne dicam perversitate, dum nemo ferme quidpiam ignorare<br>vult, et quilibet suo abundet sensu. In reliquis Cometis, quotquot ipsemet<br>accurate observavi, non dubito, quin etiam per convenientem hypothesin eorum<br>motus, utut inæqualis apparens, sub concinnam tamen regularitatem redigi possit.<br>Utinam saltem huic labori, qui non est mediocris, par detur<br>gratia atque fructus. Nam ne in ultimo quidem, qui hoc anno circa Februarij<br>finem atque Martij initia apparuit (cuius brevem sed satis exactam descriptionem<br>tecum ultimo communicavi) utut is per 12 saltem dies, quibus vix octavam circuli<br>partem absolvit, sese conspiciendum exhibuerit, eius tamen apparentias in certam<br>aliquam ac regularem normam componere pro impossibili non habeo, licet is initio<br>7½ gradus diurno motu emensus sit, circa ultimum terminum saltem sesquialterum.<br>Non enim tumultuario incessu a celeriore in tardiorem motum deveniebat, sed<br>successive atque ordinarie concitationem suam remisit, pulchra et sibi<br>consentienti proportione, quemadmodum videre est ex 3a<br>columna Tabellæ diarium Cometæ motum exprimentis, ubi cursus eius diurnus<br>proprius in suo ductu exponitur. Nihil enim illic invenies interruptum, confusum<br>aut inordinatum. Et Cometa is exactissime arcum circuli in sphæra maximi, nullo<br>prorsus modo ab eo hac vel illac digrediens, exprimebat, quod et cum plærisque<br>alijs commune habuit, ut vel ex hoc solo colligere liceat, Cometas, quemadmodum<br>suo apparente ductu portiones circuli maximi describunt, nec hincinde incerto<br>tramite divagantur, sic etiam sub eodem ductu circularem atque regularem in ipso<br>cœlo exercere tenorem, quamvis is nobis e terris aspicientibus inæqualis<br>putetur.</p><p>Atque hæc ad primam tuam obiectionem sufficiant, quæ et<br>posterioribus, si rite expendantur, satis obviant. Sed tamen et has particulatim<br>introspiciamus. Omnes Cometas, qui hactenus emerserunt vel in posterum<br>prodibunt, sub certam motionis normam redigere non dabitur, ex quo omnium exactæ<br>observationes in promptu esse nequeant. Neque id meæ assertioni quippiam<br>derogat, cum non universalem et omnibus Cometis pariter competentem motionis<br>legem præscribi posse varietas in singulis satis nos commonefaciat. Quæ igitur<br>de singularibus, et quorum apparentiæ admodum præcise innotuerunt, loquor, tu ad<br>universalia citra mentem meam trahis. Cum enim singuli ferme Cometæ aliquid<br>peculiare obtineant, impossibile erit ex aliquot particularitatibus cognitis<br>ijsdemque adinvicem non consentientibus generalem construere doctrinam, ideoque ego eam nusquam pollicitus sum Tertia tua ratio ex novitate corporum Cometarum, et quod numero<br>differant, nec in eodem et uno cœli loco emergant, quodque interdum stent,<br>interdum lento, aliquando concitatissimo, eoque inordinato ferantur motu<br>rapianturque, et rarissime perfectos describant circulos: hæc, inquam, et<br>similia id, quod intendimus, non evertunt. Quædam enim eorum nimis libere et<br>citra ullam ratam observationem a te proponuntur. Neque enim ego ullum ex<br>quinque a me diligenter observatis Cometis adinveni, qui adeo anomalum, qualem<br>tu obijcis, exhibuerit motum. Nullus eorum erat stationarius. Et licet interdum<br>velocius, nonnumquam vero tardius ferebantur, ista tamen alteratio non subito<br>illis contingebat, nihilque interrupti aut confusi, ut tu prætendis, passi sunt,<br>sed successive atque ordinaria lege motum intendebant aut potius remittebant.<br>Neque ullos Cometas ab alijs, quibus tuto fidere liceat, hactenus observatos<br>cognovi, quos adeo inordinata, prout tu infers, discohærentia implicârit.<br>Fateor quidem, plurimos perplexum motum Cometis a se conspectis<br>attribuere; sed id animadversionis incuria factum, qui solide observationes<br>tractare didicerunt, non ignorant. Plus enim ad has omni ex parte citra errorem<br>administrandas requiritur laboris et circumspectionis, quam quivis æstimare<br>poterit. Sic tu Cometam illum anni 77mi tumultuario et<br>interrupto agitatum motu, adeo ut aliquando saltum 8 graduum commiserit, literis<br>consignasti, et GEMMA etiam tale quid illi<br>attribuebat, cum tamen ex meis omni serena nocte factis observationibus nihil<br>eiuscemodi illi unquam competierit, sed adeo ordinarie velocitatem motus, quam<br>ab initio habuit, successive, nullo præfracto saltu incidente, remisit, ut<br>circularis motionis regularitatem non omnino respuerit. Nil igitur mirum erit,<br>si quidam alij, qui forte non adeo diligenter, atque tu et GEMMA, talibus attendunt, perplexum et anomalum Cometis temere<br>attribuunt motum. Paucissimis enim idonea et omni errore carentia ad manus sunt<br>instrumenta. Nec affixarum loca, quarum usus requiritur, rite constant, ut potius harum incertitudini atque incompetenti ordinationi<br>irregularitas illa attribuenda veniat, quam ipsis Cometis. Non igitur fluctuanti<br>motu rapiuntur; absit enim, ut tale quid cœlestibus, quæ ordinis et<br>regularitatis inprimis sunt appetentia, aspergatur. Sed maior est in eorum<br>incessu concinnitas, quam a plærisque hactenus animadversum. Sic quod Cometæ<br>rarissime integros et perfectos describant circulos, etsi multum difficultatis<br>in eorum curriculo regulando suggerat, possibilitatem tamen omnem non prorsus<br>excludit, sed eo plus meretur laudis atque gratiæ, quo magis arduus et operosus<br>sit conatus. Ex data autem alicuius circuli portione, de eius centro atque<br>magnitudine, licet tota peripheria non constet, quin cognitio rata haberi<br>possit, quis Geometrarum negabit? Quod infers, si alicuius Cometæ motus fuerit accurate exploratus, ita ut ex portione aliqua de<br>integra eius revolutione iudicari possit, non tamen id concedi, ut hinc de<br>quorumvis Cometarum motu tale quid constituatur: id meæ sententiæ nullatenus<br>refragatur, ut ex semel atque iterum dictis satis liquet. Sic neque ea, quæ de<br>stellis fixis replicas, mihi adversantur. Quod enim multo et longo tempore a<br>diversis Artificibus in illis præstitum est, id paucis diebus in Cometa aliquo<br>ab uno et eodem observatore multo etiam certius fieri potest. Et utinam ea, quæ<br>de fixarum motu quasi per manus ab Astronomis tradita sunt, non plus<br>perplexitatis atque incertitudinis clam haberent, quam aliquis ex ijs qui a<br>nobis observati sunt, Cometis! Utique promptior esset ad earum inæqualitates<br>excusandas ratio. Nec est necesse, Cometas semper eosdem esse ut stellas, nisi<br>ijsdem etiam legibus omnes subijci affirmaretur, quod nusquam a me prolatum est.<br>Unde et reliqua his similia, quæ obijciuntur, nihil officiunt, ut mirari satis<br>non possim, qua occasione mea placita ita accipias, ut, si locum mereri debeant,<br>oporteat primo docere, omnes Cometas, quotquot hactenus conspeximus, fuisse unum<br>et eundem Cometam, quasi non singulis pro alia atque alia, quam exhibent,<br>motionis habitudine, dissimilis quoque hypotheseos ratio præfiniri possit.<br>Plures dantur Cometæ, nec unquam ijdem redeunt, diversa in cœlo sortiuntur loca,<br>motum etiam dissimilem. Quid tum postea? Inveniantur itaque pro singulis<br>correspondentes hypotheses, quæ etiam inter se dissimiles erunt, hoc saltem<br>habentes commune, ut nobis apparens inæqualitas per<br>circularem motum, quomodocunque assumptum, excusetur.</p><p>Præterea quod addis, ex unius vel alterius diei motu in Cometis non<br>posse ratiocinationem institui de tota illius periodo, et exemplum e Luna<br>petitum profers, ista nihil ad meam opinionem labefactandam faciunt, imo potius<br>ostendunt, te longe adhuc abesse a meo sensu. Neque enim ex unius vel alterius<br>diei progressu de Cometarum periodo aliquid statuendum unquam affirmavi, sciens<br>utique apparentem motum inæqualem esse; sed a principio usque ad finem totius<br>durationis eorum apparentias diligentissime esse scrutandas censeo, idque sub eo<br>ipso incessus tramite, quem cœlitus designare videntur, atque tum demum,<br>collatis omnibus observationibus, videndum, an dari possit aliquis circularis in<br>cœli capacitate ductus, sive simplex sive compositus, cuius beneficio id, quod<br>nobis inæquale apparuit, ad competentem æqualitatem concinnari possit. Cum enim<br>cœlum non sit durum atque impervium corpus pluribus orbibus confertum, ut falso<br>nos hactenus docuit Peripateticorum schola, possunt sane Cometæ in omni eius<br>parte libere rotari et circularem exercere motum, modo non Planetarum corporibus<br>impingant, quod etiam ab eorum dispositore satis cavetur. In octava quam vocant<br>sphæra intra stellas fixas nullos prodire Cometas<br>existimârim, nisi et illi immobiles sint, qualis erat Stella Nova. Collationem,<br>quam instituis circa motum stellarum et Cometarum, nolo multis replicare, quia<br>ad eam quam tueor sententiam convellendam nihil facit. Neque ea, quæ de stellis<br>fixis sextam circuli partem nondum emensis in medium adduxi, sic accipienda<br>erunt. Ista enim exempli loco tantum protuli, quod COPERNICUS attentârit<br>ex portione aliqua motus transacti, de integra periodo ratiocinari, quamvis<br>nequaquam id, quod sperabat, assecutus sit. Tota enim eius de fixarum motu<br>salvando speculatio erronea est et apparentijs nequaquam consona. Neque enim<br>nunc centenis annis fixæ unum gradum absolvunt, ut eius fert hypothesis, sed<br>potius in septuaginta uno, adeo ut dubium sit, an tanta illis competat<br>inæqualitas, quanta hactenus credita est, et an vetustissimorum Astronomorum  de his prolata rite se habeant; ut si Cometæ alicuius motus non exactius<br>nobis perspectus fuerit, quam fixarum iam a bis mille annis innotuit progressio,<br>operam lusisse videatur, qui hypothesin concinnam extruere tentârit. Multa enim in observationibus, tam veterum quam recentiorum, quo<br>ad stellas fixas desidero. Neque eam quam oportuit in his verificandis adhibuit<br>PTOLEMÆUS diligentiam et præcisionem,<br>multoque minus COPERNICUS, quod alterius est loci plenius discutere.</p><p>Nondum itaque, mi THADDÆE, ad impossibilitatem omnem deduxisti Cometarum<br>hypotheses, neque inficiari merito potes ea, quæ de Cometa anni 77mi (non enim de eo, quo anno 80 effulsit, ut tuæ habent literæ, id<br>protuli) publicavi, quod videlicet instar ♀ et ☿ circa ☉lem obambulârit, cum e certis observationibus idipsum<br>Geometrice demonstrârim, et ratiocinationes meæ in libro transmisso tibi in<br>promptu sint. Si quid habes, quod ijs opponi possit, id profer: sin minus, cur<br>ijs, quæ rite et fideliter nec sine labore extricata sunt, non acquiescis? et<br>quod in eo Cometa tali modo effectum est, cur in alijs alio quoque modo, prout<br>apparentiæ ipsæ postulârint, fieri posse diffidis? Videris mihi in expendendis<br>hisce omnibus præiudicio (quod pace tua dixerim) quodam uti, et iam antea tecum<br>conclusisse, id impossibile esse, quod an fieri posset, inquirendum prius<br>fuerat. Hincque factum existimo, ut ea, quæ capite 8 et 9 circa hypothesin dicti<br>Cometæ enucleandam atque in numeros resolvendam non minimo labore susceperam,<br>obiter saltem inspexeris, ex quo adhuc de rei adeo diligenter enodatæ<br>certitudine dubites et ad aliorum iudicia appelles. Nec scio, quid sit caussæ,<br>cur tantopere Cometarum motus certa lege, prout cuiusque postulat incessus,<br>salvari posse impugnes, cum ipsemet non diffitearis, eos in ætherea mundi<br>regione procreari. Pati itaque eo promtius deberes, nonnullos paulo adhuc altius<br>eorum conditiones examinare, et cœlestibus corporibus cœlestem atque ordinatum<br>attribuere motum. Imo, siquidem tibi ipsi non<br>suppetit otium, aut voluntas deest talia scrutandi, alios, impossibilitatem<br>obijciendo, a tam egregio conatu non retrahere, sed potius, ut hæc plenius<br>pervestigare satagerent, stimulum addere debebas. Quod amicitiæ nostræ confisus<br>benevole et candide te monere volui, petoque, ut hanc nostram ad tua dubia<br>qualemcunque responsionem boni consulas. Ego de his plura non addam; permittam<br>potius cuilibet sentire, quod volet, an videlicet ordinarie, an vero confuse in<br>cœlo moveantur Cometæ. Memineris tamen, naturam ipsam ordine<br>decenti gaudere et a perplexitate et confusione abhorrere, ut ob id non abs re<br>fuerit, quod Pythagorei numerorum proportionibus omnia includere studuerint. Hæc<br>itaque de istis sufficiant, si modo non nimia fuerint. Nunc ad reliqua tuis<br>literis comprehensa progrediar.</p><p>Quod numeri Alphonsini atque Copernicæi tantopere in locis ♄<br>et ♃ præteritis mensibus discrepârint, non est mirum; nam id alias etiam<br>contingit. Propius tamen ad cœlestem normam accedit COPERNICI ratio, licet<br>et hæc in ♃ nonnunquam plus integro gradu deviet, in ♄ usque ad<br>dimidium. Multo enim maiorem in his duobus ponderosioribus Planetis COPERNICUM committere a<br>cœlo ipso discrepantiam hactenus non inveni, licet in ♂ hæc ternos<br>subinde gradus vel etiam paulo ultra attingat. Quod vero ♃ stella his<br>ipsis diebus grad. 1⅔ ulterior sit apud COPERNICUM quam ALPHONSUM, tantum tamen abest, ut vitium penes COPERNICUM reperiatur, quod cœlum ipsum adhuc<br>quarta fere gradus parte eius longitudinem plus promoveat, quam Ephemerides e<br>Prutenico derivatæ calculo. Sic ♄ sexta parte gradus in ipso cœlo est<br>anterior COPERNICI numeratione, licet hæc<br>plus 2½ gradibus Alphonsinam præcedat. Latitudo tamen eius cum hoc calculo intra<br>paucula minuta consentit, cum apud COPERNICUM ferme<br>dimidio gradu excessus committatur. Exactiorem horum Planetarum tum etiam<br>aliorum cum cœlestibus apparentijs correspondentiam nostræ observationes cum<br>veteribus collatæ, favente cœlestium Opifice, aliquando suppeditabunt. Epocha<br>Mesthliniana non erat mihi mutanda, etiamsi aliquantulum haberet vitij, dum<br>demonstrare volui, eius hypothesin atque fundamenta per totam Cometæ durationem<br>ipsius apparentijs salvandis non omnimode satisfecisse, adeo ut circa ultimum<br>terminum maior tribus gradibus commissa sit differentia. Non erat quæstio de<br>Epocha eius (quam sane eandem cum eo ipso præsupponere oportuit) neque quartæ<br>partis gradus in hac discrepantia, id quod ego intendebam, magnopere impediebat,<br>vel etiam promovebat. An vero in Epocha illa desiderentur scrupula, periculum<br>non feci, nec etiam facere lubet, cum res hæc parum ad<br>propositum faciat, nec magni admodum sit momenti. Quod in prostaphæresibus meis<br>enucleandis per duos integros menses mecum in diversis terminis fueris, et pro 26 Decembris, ad quem ego calculum direxi, tu 24<br>Novembris inconsiderate assumpseris, non est, quod amplius hæsites. Nec opus est<br>calculum denuo revocare aut etiam considerationem instituere, quam<br>introspiciendo attentius ea, quæ pag. 279 et seq. in libro nostro 2do continentur, et conferendo ea cum tuis, quæ necessario<br>illi tempori non quadrant. MUNOSIJ libellum<br>nunc non multum expeto, quæ enim in illius placita disquirenda habui, iam<br>impressa sunt, nec FRANGIAPANI ineptias<br>moror, cum nihil ad rem faciant. Pro APIANI<br>transmisso libello de Cometa anni 31mi  gratias habeo. Non tamen eum cum hoc Tabellario remitto, cum tam subito<br>ea, quæ inde eruere atque cum alijs conferre constitui, colligere nunc non sit<br>otium. Recipies eum alio tempore, licet facilis esset iactura, cum nihil solidi<br>et certi, ne quidem in apparente Cometæ motu, contineat, adeo ut intra unum vel<br>alterum gradum tuto illi fidendum non sit, tam lubrica utitur observandi ratione<br>et Dedomena assumit fallacia, utut omnia per triangulos magis subtili quam utili<br>labore resolvat. TRAPEZUNTIJ in<br>PTOLEMÆUM Commentarios per hunc<br>remittam Tabellarium, si is eos secum asportare voluerit, sin minus, alia<br>quacunque opportunitate dabo operam, ut restituantur; mihi enim nulli sunt<br>usui.</p><p>Apologiam meam adversus CRAIGUM Scotum tibi non displicere lubens<br>accipio. Reddita est illi iamdudum hyeme antecedente, nihil tamen adhuc<br>respondit, nec scio, an quidpiam in contrarium uberius meditetur. Id saltem ex<br>Medico Doctore, qui serenissimam Reginam Scotiæ nostratem illuc proximo vere<br>comitabatur, cognovi, CRAIGUM illum pro suo<br>ARISTOTELE, cui se totum mancipavit,<br>adhuc stare, adeoque eius placitis addictum, ut certiora et saniora omnia<br>respuat, morboque pervicaciæ incurabili laboret. Suspiciat igitur et in pretio<br>habeat unâ cum cæteris Aristotelæis vanissimas nugas, siquidem veritati certius<br>iam tandem patefactæ auscultare nolit. Ego quid dicat ARISTOTELES, vel quivis alius pro authentico receptus, non respiciens, quid ipsum cœlum protulerit et<br>quomodo nova sua Phænomena ostenderit, infallibilibus observationibus admodumque<br>subtiliter scrutatus Cometas revera in alto æthere procreari, illicque cursum<br>suum eundemque ordinarium exercere, satis superque didici et invictis apodixibus<br>demonstravi. Sufficiat itaque mihi veritatem ipsam, quæ diu abscondita latuit,<br>in apertum produxisse alijsque conspiciendam proposuisse. Ad cuius fulgentes<br>radios, si qui adhuc tanta caligine offuscatos oculos habent, ut lumen tam<br>splendidum subito e plus quam Cimmerijs tenebris prodiens sustinere nequeant, id<br>me nequaquam afficit. Satis enim conscius sum, nihil tam apposite atque<br>indubitate asseverari, quin suos inveniat contradictores, et ipsissima veritas<br>gravissimis ut plurimum in hoc mundo laborat obtrectationibus, mendacia autem et<br>vanitates pene ubique in pretio sunt.</p><p>Quod CRAIGUS te in tua Dialexi parum ex arte præstitisse nimis<br>præsumptuose pronuntiat, videtur ad parallaxium doctrinam minus solide et<br>absolute illic pertractatam respexisse. Ignorabat enim procul dubio, teipsum tua<br>hac in parte errata postea emendasse. Quin et ego in Apologia pro te satis<br>respondi, et quod in Cometa illo incertum fuisse obijcit, dilui. Placeat ille<br>sic sibi et aliorum egregios conatus ob nævos quosdam extenuando, nihilque<br>ipsemet perfectius, quod ad rem faciat, in medium proferendo, videat, anne apud<br>intelligentes et iudicio valentes philautiæ nimiæ et censuræ temerariæ<br>redarguatur. Quid ego in tua Dialexi circa ea præsertim, quæ ad stellam illam<br>novam proprie faciebant, desiderem, ex capite 8 primi nostri libri æthereorum<br>Phænomenωn, qui de stella hac nova ex professo agit, percipies. Illic enim<br>statim post Landgraviana, tuas observationes atque pronuntiata expendi,<br>nostrumque de his iudicium libere protuli, quod tibi veritatis enucleatioris<br>inprimis studioso  et candide omnia accipienti non ingratum futurum mihi persuadeo. Id quod<br>CRAIGUS affirmat, Mathematicos in<br>demonstrandis Cometarum parallaxibus nimis secure proprium eorum motum<br>neglexisse, non usque adeo citra rem quoad plærosque asseverat, et ne quidem<br>ipsemet REGIOMONTANUS in suo erudito<br>illo scripto de Cometis excipiendus venit. Licet enim Problemate, uti ais, 2. 3.<br>et 4. moneat, motus proprij in Cometis habendam rationem, tamen ipsimet<br>demonstrationum processus hunc prorsus excludunt, et Phænomenon non nisi motu primo agitari præsupponunt. Nec quod in 2do Problemate per temporis interiecti brevitatem motus<br>proprij impedimenta excusat, sufficiens est omnem hîc suspicionem erroris<br>amovere, cum celerior utplurimum sit in Cometis motionis propriæ varietas, quam<br>ea, quæ ex parallaxeos alteratione insinuatur, et tempus observationibus<br>interiectum abbreviando parallaxium perceptionem eo abstrusiorem efficimus. Sic<br>quam Problemate 3tio limitationem adhibet et in sequenti<br>quoque valere vult, etsi ea aliquatenus difficultates ex incessu genuino<br>Cometarum in parallaxi enucleanda mitiget et tolerabiliores reddat, tamen eas<br>non undiquaque tollit, et modus ille, quo motum proprium diurnum prædefinire<br>docet, prorsus erroneus est, nec rei inquirendæ subtilitati satisfacit. Taceo,<br>quod azimuthorum et temporis intercepti tam exacta, prout hîc requiritur, anceps<br>sit pervestigatio, et minimus vixque perceptibilis error in operationis praxi<br>nimium fæcundus evadat: ut ob id iuxta REGIOMONTANI Problemata, utut quoad demonstrationem ipsam<br>irrefragabilia, Cometarum cœlitus sese ostendentium parallaxes citra errorem<br>discernere non sit adeo promptum, atque putatur. Existimavit ille, Cometas<br>necessario in aëre superiore tanquam igneum quoddam meteoron flagrare, idque<br>Aristotelæa autoritate ita prævalente, ut summis etiam Mathematicis imposuerit. Si igitur tam propinquus foret Cometarum situs, eorum<br>parallaxes plurimos gradus adæquarent, sicque iuxta REGIOMONTANI ratiocinia facilius<br>perciperentur, nec motus proprij minus exacta emendatio aut in azimuthis atque<br>temporis mensuratione pusillula quædam deviatio tantopere rei quæsitæ<br>enodationem interturbaret. Prospexit procul dubio insignis ille artifex has et<br>similes difficultates, ideoque naturæ thesauros non exhauriri posse, sed ad<br>quædam scibilia saltem propinquum quid attingere licere protestatus est.</p><p>Quæ licet ita se habeant, nimiumque vera sint, attamen nisi quis in<br>Cometarum parallaxibus perscrutandis supremam adhibeat diligentiam, omnesque<br>errorum scopulos vel minime obvios declinârit, ad rei, quam intendit, satis<br>ratam cognitionem non perveniet. Ideoque ego posthabitis his lubricis vijs longe<br>alio tramite ad Cometarum situm pervestigandum mihi incedendum duxi, ita ut ne<br>minimæ quidem parallaxes, quales in altissimo æthere fiunt, subterfugium aliquod<br>invenirent, nec motus proprij implicatio impedimentum adferret, aut in ipsa<br>denique observandi ratione aliquid errori sensibili occasionem facile præbens<br>committeretur. Non levem itaque mihi infert iniuriam CRAIGUS, ut tu quoque fateris, dum motus proprij Cometarum in<br>parallaxibus eorum cognoscendis irrepentes remoras obijcere non dubitat, cum eas<br>admodum studiose et subtiliter ubique amoverim præcaverimque. Ideoque non<br>simplici quadam ratiocinationis formâ, sed quasi per ambages, ne in errorum scopulos impingerem, ad veritatis propositæ scopum perrexi.<br>Excusas quidem tu cursus proprij in Cometis duobus a te descriptis neglectum per<br>temporis assumti exiguitatem, sed quemadmodum temporis interiecti brevitas ob<br>parum interea alteratum Cometæ locum hîc aliquid commodi adfert, sic rursus, dum<br>altitudinem Cometæ non magno, ut oportuit, intervallo variat, parallaxium<br>discriminationem reddit imperceptibiliorem, prout de Cometa anni 77mi, ubi 18 saltem minutorum in binis observationibus<br>interstitium assumseras, iam antea admonui.</p><p>De cometa anni 80mi suo loco, favente Numine,<br>videbimus; quem licet revera æthereum fuisse rectissime pronuntiâris, tamen<br>motum eius proprium negligendo (ut de quibusdam alijs nunc non dicam) adhuc<br>nimis magnam illi concessisti parallaxin, cuius etiam temporis præsuppositi<br>iusto strictius intervallum, circa Meridianum præsertim, ubi  altitudines tarde variantur, occasionem præbuit. Sed hæc ad literas tuas<br>nimis forte prolixe respondeo; qua in parte si quid peccatum sit, rogo, ut tecum<br>de his conferendi cupiditati imputes, atque pro solito erga me animi candore<br>benevolentiaque excuses. Nunc ea, quæ in Postscriptis habentur, expediam.</p><p>Gaudeo, te pagellas illas impressarum Epistolarum Astronomicarum<br>unâ cum brevi descriptione ultimi Cometæ<br>accepisse; cuperemque, ut per otium mihi tuum iudicium, tum de Cometa illo tum<br>etiam de ijs, quæ Epistolis istis præcipue agitantur, perscribere non<br>graveris.</p><p>Quantum ad modellas mea quædam instrumenta repræsentantes attinet,<br>curavi, postquam tuam voluntatem hac in re cognovi, quasdam confieri, quotquot<br>commorante hîc Tabellario absolvere licuit, quas tibi per eundem mitto, rogoque,<br>ut nemini nisi solo Domino CURTIO<br>communices. Expressit in ijs artifex ea, quæ in tam parvis ad similitudinem<br>maiorum instrumentorum formari commode poterant. Nec enim omnia ad unguem<br>exhibere licuit. Vereor itaque, ne in maiori aliquo organo conficiendo harum<br>idearum præmonstratio minus sufficiens futura sit. Quare non citra rem Dominus<br>CURTIUS eâdem magnitudine, qua mea<br>sunt organa, vobis hîc parari consultius duxit. Qua etiam in re libenter illi<br>gratificarer, si artifices omnes, quorum operâ in meis construendis præteritis<br>aliquot annis usus sum, præsto haberem; eos enim, postquam instrumenta, quæ<br>conficienda mihi proposueram, parta essent, dimisi, retento saltem uno vel<br>altero, qui iam confectorum curam gererent, atque si quid in ijs forte fortuna<br>desideraretur, repararent. Qui tamen ad integrum aliquod instrumentum de novo<br>construendum non sufficiunt. Sunt enim plæraque ex meis,<br>in quibus quinque vel sex diversi artifices laborârunt, idque non pauco tempore,<br>adeo ut nonnulla vix tertio demum anno ab operis inchoatione perfecta sint. Sunt<br>præterea illæ machinæ, quibus potissimum fido, tantæ magnitudinis, ut nullo<br>curru commode vehi possint. Unicum habeo Sextantem peculiari ratione e partibus<br>diversis coagmentatum , ita ut dissolvi et in cistam aliquam claudi, rursusque cochleis ad usum<br>componi possit. Hunc ego eo præsertim nomine fieri curavi, ut per eum Noribergam<br>translatum poli altitudinem accuratissime ob Solares quasdam  olim illic habitas observationes explorarem, quod etiam adhuc mihi<br>propositum est. Hunc ipsum Sextantem vobis aliquandiu impertiri, donec ad eius<br>imitationem alius elaborari poterit, unâque eius usum admodum expeditum et<br>certum in capiendis syderum tam distantijs quam altitudinibus probe<br>perceperitis, non renuam; modo Domino CURTIO id acceptum fuerit. Quod si sic, mittat huc ad me iuvenem<br>aliquem doctum et Astronomicarum rerum satagentem, qui instrumenti huius usum<br>eiusque dissolutionem et complicationem hîc sufficienter discat, et postea illud<br>vobis sartum tectum adferat, eiusque explicationem utilitatemque diversimodam<br>illic aperiat. Alias enim frustra mittitur hoc organum, nisi quis unâ adsit, qui<br>eius rationes omnes compertas habeat. Id poteris Domino CURTIO meo nomine renuntiare, meque ad illi,<br>tam in hac parte quam alijs, quantum in me est, gratificandum quam paratissimum<br>fore.</p><p>In Epistolis tuis et autographis mihi<br>transmissis etiam non admonitus cavebo, ut ea, quæ quosdam offendere possint,<br>quæque alios scire nihil interest, omittantur, quando typis publicandæ venient.<br>Quod quam cito fieri possit, nondum certus sum, ex quo papyrum impressoriam pro<br>primo tomo mei operis, quod nunc elaboro, typis absolvendo, in promptu non<br>habeam. Cum enim difficultates conquirendæ e Germania papyri, quibus antehac<br>præpediebar, evitandi caussa, ipsemet Molendinum in hac Insula pro ea<br>conficienda non parvis sumtibus extrui curassem, accedit aliud quid præter<br>expectationem adversi, quo minus ne sic quidem papyrum desideratam adipisci<br>licuerit. Nam duos Chartopæos diverso tempore conquisitos, qui mihi suam operam<br>et fidem addixerunt, in Germaniam veteramentorum conquirendorum caussa postquam<br>amandassem, uterque, accepta illic a meis Procuratoribus pecunia, clam se<br>subduxit. Sicque tota hac æstate, cum nullum idoneum artificem postea nactus<br>sim, papyri confectionem intermittere cogor, cumque nulla alia iam in promptu<br>sit, primi tomi absolutionem, usque dum papyrus impressoria suppetierit,<br>differre necesse habeo. Ne tamen mora hæc tibi, quem scio eius publicationem<br>avide expectare, interea nimis molesta sit, mitto in præsentia eorum<br>quaternionum, quotquot typis hactenus absoluti sunt, exemplar, quod interim, dum reliqua parantur, per otium evolvere potes, meque de<br>ijs, quæ inibi continentur, tuam censuram perscribere, et, sicubi tibi<br>videbitur, ingenue admonere. Omissæ sunt priores pagellæ, quibus titulus libri,<br>Privilegia et præfationes comprehendentur. Hæc enim omnia, uti moris est,<br>perfecto toto opere præponentur. Desiderantur etiam tres quaterniones in fine<br>capitis primi, quibus de anni magnitudine quædam ulterius seorsim generaliori<br>quadam indicatione consignare decrevi, et tum simul Lilianam noviter introductam<br>anni Iuliani correctionem breviter considerare. Sic quoque ad calcem secundi<br>capitis quinque desunt quaterniones, quibus affixarum a nobis restitutarum<br>abacus unâ cum quibusdam alijs huc pertinentibus continebitur. Totum etiam caput<br>septimum intermittitur, quod non saltem Novæ Stellæ, sed et reliquarum fixarum<br>atque etiam Planetarum magnitudines, aliter quam hactenus præstitum est,<br>reserabit. Duo quoque ultima capita ob papyri defectum nondum impressa sunt,<br>quorum nonum eorum placita, qui Stellam Novam Planetarum orbibus miscuerunt,<br>decimum vero, qui eandem prorsus sublunarem reddiderunt, aut quid aliud nimis<br>absurdum de ea protulerunt, examinabunt atque diluent. Hæc, inquam, ad prioris<br>tomi completionem restantia suo tempore, favente Numine, obtinebis; interim ea,<br>quæ nunc communico, perlege, disquire et boni consule.</p><p>Observationes tuæ circa ♂ atque alias quasdam stellas ante<br>annos aliquot habitæ, quas misisti, minus exactæ sunt, eo quod Radius veras stellarum intercapedines nequaquam largiatur.<br>Affixarum quoque quæ adhibuisti loca, ab ipso cœlo multum discrepant, prout te  nostra monere poterit restitutio, ut mirum non sit, calculum diversimode<br>repetitum a se adeo enormiter dissensisse.</p><p>Si eclipsin ☉lis, quæ proximis diebus<br>contigit, diligenter observastis, eius tempora atque Phasin illic denotata mecum<br>communica. Fuit hîc tota eius duratione apprime serenum, incepitque ☉<br>deficere post mediam noctem diei 21 Iulij, elapsis horis 6. M. 51. Desijt autem<br>hora 9 exquisite completa. Non itaque multum in tempore huius deliquij ab ipso<br>cœlo differunt adhibita Meridianorum reductione hactenus usurpatæ tabulæ, sive<br>Alphonsinæ sive Coperniceæ, idque eam præsertim ob causam, quod motus<br>☉lis circa signum ♌ in illis non admodum<br>est erroneus. Quantum vero ad magnitudinem partis eclipsatæ attinet, multum<br>aberrârunt Canones: deficere enim visa est saltem tertia pars ☉lis sive digiti 4, cum tamen 8 ad minimum punctorum defectus<br>in nostro horizonte apparuisset, si tabulis fidentium fuisset. Quæ obiter<br>indicare volui, ut cum vestra animadversione conferantur. Sed ne nimis prolixus<br>in rescribendo sim, tempus est, ut nunc calamum sistam. Tu itaque, optime et<br>amantissime mi THADDÆE, quam<br>felicissime vale, meque prima quaque occasione literis tuis, siquidem aliter nos<br>invicem compellare nequeamus, quin revisas, ne intermitte.</p><p>Datæ Uraniburgi die 3. Augusti stylo veteri Anno<br>1590.</p><p>Tycho Brahe.</p><p>Librum transmissum rogo [ut] nemini communices, præ[terquam] soli<br>Domino CURTIO, ex[pectans<br>quoad] completus atque [in publicum em]issus sit.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most excellent man, eminent in a manifold kind of learning, Lord Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, eminent Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, his especially-to-be-cultivated friend. Greeting.</b></p><p>What you promised in that last, shorter [letter] sent by the <b>Rostock student</b>, my excellent and most erudite Thaddaeus, most-to-be-cultivated friend, has now been more than sufficiently performed. For these days a <b>Prague courier</b> arrived, carrying with him the new <b>Imperial Privilege</b>, the letters of the Magnificent Lord <b>Curtius</b> [Jacob Kurz von Senftenau], and yours — all of which were far most acceptable to me; and I give you what thanks I can, that you moved the most ample Lord Vice-Chancellor to obtain for me this new Privilege from the Imperial Majesty. It is indeed splendid enough and honourable, in which I have nothing further to desire. Nor, had the <b>Gallican</b> [privilege] reached you (which, by I-know-not-what carelessness, has fallen in [been lost]; for that transcript which I meant to send is nowhere to be found here), would it have contributed much to a fuller and more solid completion of the business. I still expect another from that new <b>King of the Gauls, [the one] of Navarre</b> [Henri IV], but scarcely with equal affection toward Mathematical studies, since none, that I know, in the Courts of Kings is of the privy council who not only love Mathematics so earnestly and solidly, but also understand [them], as that most excellent Lord <b>Curtius</b> — by whose benefit I gratefully acknowledge this Imperial Diploma was obtained and prepared. But since I am writing about this matter, and about those [things] contained in his most erudite Letter, to himself, I do not wish to touch them here at greater length, but rather will proceed to replying to this last [letter] of yours. In which, however, I will pass over that part which at the beginning deals fully with the delays and excuses of [our] mutual correspondence, since our friendship, now confirmed by much time, ought to be free of all suspicion, nor much needs purgation if anything has fallen out beyond expectation.</p><p>About my <b>hypotheses</b> too — built and ordered from the heaven itself, more accurately inspected (especially with the Martian star approaching the Earth, in the acronychal position, more than the Sun; so that not only its <b>parallaxes</b>, sought out by us several times with the greatest diligence, but its very diurnal transits, somewhat swifter than the Ptolemaic reckonings on account of the greater approach, openly <b>prove</b> [it]) — I will add nothing here; I have dealt with them enough, as far as a Letter bears, in [my] earlier [ones]; to elucidate them fully, and to confirm and assert [them] without just contradiction, will be [a matter] for another place and a more prolix business. Each too of those [two men] whom you wish omitted, I too will here gladly neglect; nor would I have written before about that former one, had you not yourself given the occasion. The <b>other understands Mathematics more rightly, and, reconciled to you, will be tolerable</b>. I will therefore hasten to those [things] which I thought about the <b>defence of Maestlin</b> undertaken by me — especially that it is <b>not impossible to reduce the motions of Comets to some hypothesis analogous to their appearances</b> — which you still move into controversy. Those [things] which, driven doubtless by just grief, you intermingle about your <b>whips and calumniators</b> — since those bring forward nothing that makes for the matter, and on that account merit no further mention — passing them over, I will discuss more fully the question itself, in which you say you are not yet satisfied, and will further meet your doubts and objections. Which I trust is not unwelcome to you; nay, that it will be so, your own request, with which you close that disputation, testifies.</p><p>That <b>Comets cannot be bound by a fixed law of motions, as happens in the Planets coeval with the world</b> — so that some universal hypothesis sufficient for all be constituted — I would indeed not unwillingly grant you, nor have I ever asserted it. But this is my opinion about them, which I see you have not yet wholly perceived: namely that <b>Comets, since they are all really procreated in the heaven itself</b> (let whoever wishes posit some of them sublunary; to me it seems by no means consonant), are necessarily <b>partakers also of celestial nature</b>, although it [their nature] has not been brought to so great a perfection as is beheld in those everlasting bodies. But since the heaven itself, and the bodies which revolve in it, <b>naturally desire and are allotted the most perfect motion — that is, circular, whether simple or composite</b> — I deem it not unfitting that the Comets too exercise a circular and ordinary course in the heaven, which, if perhaps it is not in every respect completed after the manner of the Planets, yet emulates them and tends as nearly as possible to an orbicular and uniform advance. Yet they do not everywhere regard the <b>Sun</b>, as the Planets [do], nor the <b>Earth</b>, as the luminaries and the fixed [stars] [do], as the centre of their circuits; but in the immense vastness of the world they can be allotted <b>any point</b> whatever, with respect to which their motion turns out circular and equable — just as they are also stirred by a various and diverse law of motions, and run out now into these, now those, parts of the heaven at pleasure; and therefore <b>one and the same hypothesis will not suffice for all, but each of them will require its own</b>, to be constituted through the appearances themselves, as it were a posteriori. And although a greater difficulty lies beneath here than in the Planets (which complete whole periods several times and lie open to long observation), it must not on that account be determined that an <b>extreme impossibility</b> inheres in this business, before we have tried, by manifold and indefatigable effort, whether it is permitted to arrive thither. I indeed, in that splendid <b>Comet of [15]77</b> — whose motion seemed so unequal that at the beginning it accomplished nearly 6 degrees, at the end scarcely a quarter of one, by [its] daily advance — showed evidently enough that it could be bound to a regular and well-nigh equable and circular ordering of progression, so much so that the numbers deduced from the presupposed hypothesis agreed with the observations themselves more surely and readily than has hitherto been effected in any Planets — as <b>chapter 8 of our second book</b> (provided one inspect it more attentively) abundantly testifies. If, therefore, in this Comet — which seemed to traverse at least a quarter of the heaven by its course, and meanwhile expressed a plainly unequal motion, yet one gradually remitting itself from swifter to slower — it could be achieved that its unequal appearances were <b>saved</b> by fixed and orbicular tracks: why should we deem it altogether impossible that the same can be done in others too, even if in diverse manner, according as each one's motion exhibited to us bears? But although a great difficulty lies beneath here, yet not (as you wish) the <b>supreme</b> [one]. And if it be granted to the utmost, it will yet not wholly exclude all possibility; for there are many things subject to the supreme difficulty, to which the industry and labour of human genius can scarcely reach, which are nonetheless not on that account utterly impossible — such as the <b>squaring of the circle</b>, not yet exactly demonstrated even by the moderns who have persuaded themselves they found it; this, however, is numbered among the <b>knowable but not yet known</b> [<i>scibilia sed nondum cognita</i>]. But you demand that this possibility of which I speak — full of difficulty though it be — be amply proven in so many Comets across many centuries from their apparitions. But since it does not escape you that <b>no Comets were observed from the heaven by infallible instruments with exact precision before our times</b> (although now too very many have stumbled intolerably in these [matters]), so that it was not even explored, by the slightness of [their] parallaxes, that they dwell in the aether — much less that thence some aethereal and ordinary advance be demonstrated — you demand in vain the resolution of this doubt from very few and momentless <b>data</b> [<i>Dedomena</i>, δεδόμενα]. That it can be done in that one Comet of [15]77 I showed; although you do not attack [this], yet you submit this whole business to others' judgment, and desire it confirmed by the testimonies of more Comets. Surely, if you await others' judgments — however excellent in the praise of erudition — I fear lest you seek too long and in vain the truth lurking in the hidden, amid so great a variety and obstinacy (not to say perversity) of judgments, while almost no one wishes to be ignorant of anything, and each abounds in his own sense. In the remaining Comets, as many as I myself accurately observed, I do not doubt that their motion too, however unequal in appearance, can be reduced under an elegant regularity by a suitable hypothesis. Would that to this labour, which is no mean [one], an equal favour and fruit be given! For not even in the last [comet], which appeared this year about the end of February and the beginning of March (of which I last communicated to you a brief but exact-enough description) — although it showed itself to be seen for at least 12 days, in which it scarcely completed an eighth part of a circle — do I hold it impossible to compose its appearances into some fixed and regular norm, although at the beginning it measured 7½ degrees by [its] daily motion, about the last term scarcely one-and-a-half. For it did not come from swifter to slower motion by a <b>tumultuary advance</b>, but <b>successively and ordinarily</b> remitted its acceleration, by a beautiful and self-consistent proportion — as may be seen from the third column of the Table expressing the Comet's daily motion, where its proper diurnal course is set forth in its track. For you will find nothing there interrupted, confused, or disordered. And that Comet most exactly expressed an <b>arc of a great circle on the sphere</b>, in no way at all deviating from it this way or that — which it had in common with most of the others too — so that from this alone it may be gathered that Comets, just as by their apparent course they describe portions of a great circle, nor wander here and there by an uncertain track, so too under the same course exercise a <b>circular and regular tenor in the heaven itself</b>, although it be thought unequal by us looking from the earth.</p><p>And let these suffice for your first objection, which also meets the later [ones] sufficiently, if they be rightly weighed. But yet let us inspect these too in particular. <b>All Comets which have hitherto emerged or will come forth in the future cannot be reduced under a fixed norm of motion</b>, since exact observations of all cannot be ready to hand. Nor does this derogate anything from my assertion, since the variety in the singulars sufficiently reminds us that no universal law of motion, equally fitting all Comets, can be prescribed. What, therefore, I say about <b>singulars</b>, and [those] whose appearances have become known quite precisely, you draw to <b>universals</b>, against my mind. For since almost each single Comet has something peculiar, it will be impossible from some few known particularities — and these not agreeing among themselves — to construct a general doctrine; and therefore I nowhere promised it. Your <b>third reason</b>, from the <b>novelty of the bodies of Comets</b>, and that they differ in number, nor emerge in the same and one place of the heaven, and that sometimes they stand still, sometimes are borne and snatched with a slow, sometimes a most rapid and (at that) disordered motion, and most rarely describe perfect circles: these, I say, and the like, do not overturn what we intend. For some of them are proposed by you too freely and without any settled observation. For I found none of the <b>five Comets diligently observed by me</b> which exhibited so anomalous a motion as you object. <b>None of them was stationary.</b> And although they were sometimes borne more swiftly, sometimes more slowly, yet that alteration did not befall them suddenly, nor did they suffer anything interrupted or confused, as you pretend, but <b>successively and by an ordinary law they intensified, or rather remitted, [their] motion</b>. Nor have I known any Comets hitherto observed by others (whom one may safely trust) which so disordered an incoherence involved, as you infer. I confess indeed that very many attribute a perplexed motion to the Comets beheld by them; but that this was done by carelessness of observation, those who have learned to handle observations solidly are not ignorant. For more labour and circumspection is required to administer these without error on every side than anyone will be able to estimate. Thus <b>you</b> consigned to writing that that Comet of [15]77 was agitated by a tumultuary and interrupted motion, so much so that it sometimes committed a <b>leap of 8 degrees</b> — and <b>Gemma</b> too attributed some such thing to it — whereas from my observations made on every serene night nothing of the kind ever belonged to it, but it so ordinarily, successively, remitted the velocity of motion which it had at the beginning, no abrupt leap occurring, that it did not at all spurn the regularity of circular motion. It will therefore be no wonder if certain others, who perhaps attend to such [things] not so diligently as you and Gemma, rashly attribute a perplexed and anomalous motion to Comets. For very few have at hand suitable instruments free of all error. Nor do the places of the fixed [stars], whose use is required, rightly stand — so that that <b>irregularity is rather to be attributed to the uncertainty and incompetent ordering of these [star-places] than to the Comets themselves</b>. They are not, therefore, snatched by a fluctuating motion; far be it that any such thing be sprinkled on the celestials, which are above all desirous of order and regularity. But there is greater <b>elegance</b> [<i>concinnitas</i>] in their advance than has hitherto been noticed by most. So [as to the point] that Comets most rarely describe whole and perfect circles — although it suggests much difficulty in regulating their course, yet it does not wholly exclude all possibility, but merits the more praise and favour, the more arduous and laborious the effort. But from a <b>given portion of some circle</b>, who of the Geometers will deny that a settled knowledge of its centre and magnitude can be had, even though the whole periphery is not established? As to what you infer — that if some Comet's motion has been accurately explored, so that from some portion a judgment can be made about its whole revolution, yet it is not granted that hence some such thing be constituted about the motion of any Comets whatever — that in no way gainsays my opinion, as is clear enough from what has been said once and again. Likewise, neither do those [things] which you rejoin about the <b>fixed stars</b> oppose me. For what has been accomplished in them over much and long time by diverse Artificers, that can be done in some Comet in a few days by one and the same observer, even much more surely. And would that those [things] which have been handed down, as it were from hand to hand, by the Astronomers about the motion of the fixed [stars] did not secretly hold more perplexity and uncertainty than any one of those Comets observed by us! Surely the method for excusing their inequalities would be readier. Nor is it necessary that Comets always be the same as the stars, unless it were affirmed that all are also subject to the same laws — which has nowhere been brought forward by me. Whence the rest of the like [objections] which are urged stand in no way; so that I cannot sufficiently wonder by what occasion you so take my tenets that, if they must merit a place, it is necessary first to teach that all Comets, as many as we have hitherto beheld, were <b>one and the same Comet</b> — as if a dissimilar reckoning of hypothesis too could not be predefined for the singulars, according to the different relation of motion they exhibit. There are several Comets, nor do the same ever return; they are allotted diverse places in the heaven, a dissimilar motion too. What then afterward? Let there be found, therefore, <b>corresponding hypotheses for the singulars</b>, which will also be dissimilar among themselves, having at least this in common — that the inequality apparent to us be <b>saved</b> by a circular motion, however assumed.</p><p>Besides, as to what you add — that from one or another day's motion in Comets a reasoning cannot be instituted about its whole period, and you bring forward an example fetched from the <b>Moon</b> — these do nothing to shake my opinion; nay rather they show that you are still far from my sense. For I never affirmed that anything is to be established about the period of Comets from one or another day's progress, knowing certainly that the apparent motion is unequal; but I judge that their appearances must be <b>most diligently scrutinized from the beginning to the end of [their] whole duration</b>, and that under the very track of advance which they seem to designate from the heaven; and then at last, all observations collated, it must be seen whether some circular course can be given in the capacity of the heaven, whether simple or composite, by whose benefit that which appeared unequal to us can be arranged into competent equality. For since the heaven is not a hard and impervious body packed with several orbs, as the <b>school of the Peripatetics</b> falsely taught us hitherto, Comets can surely be freely rotated in every part of it and exercise circular motion, provided they do not strike against the bodies of the Planets — which is also sufficiently guarded against by their Disposer. In the eighth, as they call it, <b>sphere</b>, among the fixed stars, I should think <b>no Comets come forth, unless they too be immobile, such as was the New Star</b>. The comparison which you institute concerning the motion of the stars and of the Comets I do not wish to rejoin at length, because it makes nothing toward overturning the opinion I defend. Nor are those [things] which I adduced about the <b>fixed stars not having yet measured a sixth part of a circle</b> to be taken thus. For I brought those forward only by way of example, [namely] that <b>Copernicus</b> attempted to reason, from some portion of the motion accomplished, about the whole period — although he by no means attained what he hoped. For his whole speculation about <b>saving the motion of the fixed [stars] is erroneous</b>, and by no means consonant with the appearances. For the fixed [stars] do not now accomplish one degree in a hundred years, as his hypothesis bears, but rather <b>in seventy-one</b> — so much so that it is doubtful whether so great an inequality belongs to them as has hitherto been believed, and whether the [things] brought forward by the most ancient Astronomers about these stand rightly; so that if some Comet's motion be not more exactly perceived by us than the progression of the fixed [stars] (known now for two thousand years), one who tried to build an elegant hypothesis would seem to have wasted his labour. For I find many things wanting in the observations, both of the ancients and of the moderns, as to the fixed stars. Nor did <b>Ptolemy</b> apply the diligence and precision in verifying these which he ought, and much less <b>Copernicus</b> — which is [a matter] for another place to discuss more fully.</p><p>Not yet, therefore, my Thaddaeus, have you reduced the hypotheses of Comets to total <b>impossibility</b>; nor can you justly deny those [things] which I published about the <b>Comet of [15]77</b> (for I did not bring it forward about the one which blazed in the year [15]8[0], as your letter has it) — namely that it <b>circled about the Sun [☉] after the manner of Venus [♀] and Mercury [☿]</b> — since I demonstrated this very [thing] Geometrically from certain observations, and my reasonings are ready in the book sent to you. If you have anything that can be opposed to them, bring it forth; if not, why do you not <b>acquiesce</b> in those [things] which have been rightly and faithfully, and not without labour, disentangled? And [as for] what was effected in that Comet in such a manner — why do you distrust that it can be done in others too in another manner, according as the appearances themselves shall have required? You seem to me, in weighing all these, to use a certain <b>prejudice</b> (be it said with your peace), and to have concluded with yourself beforehand that that is impossible which ought first to have been inquired [as to] <b>whether it could be done</b>. And hence I think it came about that you only glanced in passing at those [things] which, in <b>chapters 8 and 9</b>, I had undertaken with no mean labour, concerning the unfolding of the said Comet's hypothesis and its resolution into numbers — whence you still doubt about the certainty of a matter so diligently unravelled, and appeal to others' judgments. Nor do I know what the cause is, why you so attack [the view] that the motions of Comets can be <b>saved</b> by a fixed law (according as each one's advance demands), since you yourself do not deny that they are procreated in the aethereal region of the world. You ought, therefore, the more readily to allow some [others] to examine their conditions a little more deeply, and to attribute a celestial and ordered motion to celestial bodies. Nay — since you yourself have not the leisure, or the will is lacking, to scrutinize such things — you ought not to draw others back from so excellent an effort by objecting impossibility, but rather to add a spur, that they might labour to investigate these more fully. Which, trusting in our friendship, I wished benevolently and candidly to advise you; and I ask that you take in good part this our reply, such as it is, to your doubts. I will add no more about these; I will rather permit anyone to think what he wishes — namely, whether Comets move in the heaven ordinarily or confusedly. Yet remember that <b>Nature itself rejoices in seemly order and abhors perplexity and confusion</b>, so that on that account it was not amiss that the <b>Pythagoreans</b> strove to include all things in the proportions of numbers. Let these, then, suffice about those [matters], if only they have not been too much. Now I will proceed to the rest comprised in your letter.</p><p>That the <b>Alphonsine and Copernican</b> numbers so greatly diverged in the places of <b>Saturn [♄] and Jupiter [♃]</b> in the past months is no wonder; for that happens otherwise too. Yet the <b>Copernican</b> reckoning approaches nearer to the celestial norm — although this too sometimes deviates in Jupiter by more than a whole degree, in Saturn up to a half. For I have hitherto found Copernicus to commit a much greater discrepancy from the heaven itself in these two heavier Planets [than that] — although in <b>Mars [♂]</b> it sometimes reaches three degrees or even a little beyond. But [as for the fact] that the star of Jupiter is, these very days, 1⅔ degrees further on with Copernicus than with Alphonsus: so far is it [from being the case] that the fault is found with Copernicus, that the heaven itself advances its longitude still by nearly a quarter-degree more than the Ephemerides derived from the Prutenic calculation. So Saturn is, in the heaven itself, a sixth of a degree earlier than the Copernican numeration, although this precedes the Alphonsine by more than 2½ degrees. Its latitude, however, agrees with this calculation within a few minutes, whereas with Copernicus an excess of nearly a half-degree is committed. A more exact correspondence of these Planets, and also of the others, with the celestial appearances, our observations collated with the ancients' will, the Maker of the heavens favouring, some time supply. The <b>Maestlinian Epoch</b> was not to be changed by me, even if it had a little fault, when I wished to demonstrate that his hypothesis and foundations did not in every way satisfy the saving of the Comet's appearances through its whole duration — so much so that about the last term a difference greater than three degrees was committed. The question was not about his Epoch (which indeed I had to presuppose the same as he), nor did the quarter-degree in this discrepancy much hinder or even advance what I intended. But whether minutes are wanting in that Epoch I made no trial, nor does it please to make [one], since this matter makes little for the purpose, nor is it of much moment. As to your having been with me for two whole months in <b>different terms</b> in unfolding my <b>prosthaphaereses</b>, and your having inconsiderately assumed <b>24 November</b> for the <b>26 December</b> to which I directed the calculation — there is no [reason] why you should hesitate further. Nor is there need to recall the calculation anew, or even to institute a [fresh] consideration, [other] than by inspecting more attentively those [things] contained on <b>page 279 and following in our second book</b>, and conferring them with yours, which necessarily do not square with that time. <b>Muñoz's</b> little book I now do not much seek, for what I had to examine in his tenets is now printed; nor do I heed <b>Frangipani's</b> ineptitudes, since they make nothing for the matter. For <b>Apian's</b> little book on the Comet of [15]31, sent [me], I give thanks. Yet I do not return it with this courier, since there is now no leisure to gather so suddenly those [things] which I have resolved to draw from it and confer with others. You will receive it at another time — though the loss would be easy, since it contains nothing solid and certain, not even in the Comet's apparent motion, so much so that it is not safely to be trusted within one or two degrees, so slippery an observing-method does it use and so fallacious [are] the data [<i>Dedomena</i>] it assumes, although it resolves everything by triangles with labour more subtle than useful. The Commentaries of <b>Trapezuntius</b> [George of Trebizond] on <b>Ptolemy</b> I will return by this courier, if he is willing to carry them with him; if not, I will take care, by any other opportunity, that they be restored — for they are of no use to me.</p><p>That my <b>Apology against Craig the Scot</b> does not displease you, I gladly accept. It was delivered to him long since, the preceding winter, yet he has so far replied nothing, nor do I know whether he meditates anything more amply to the contrary. This at least I learned from the <b>Doctor of Medicine who accompanied our most serene Queen of Scotland thither</b> [Anders Krag, with Anne of Denmark] last spring: that that Craig still stands by his <b>Aristotle</b>, to whom he has wholly enslaved himself, and is so addicted to his tenets that he spurns all surer and saner [things], and labours with an incurable disease of obstinacy. Let him, therefore, look up to and esteem, together with the other Aristotelians, the most empty trifles, since he is unwilling to hearken to the truth at last more surely laid open. I — not regarding what Aristotle says, or anyone else received as authentic, [but] what the heaven itself has brought forth and how it has shown its new Phenomena — having scrutinized [it] by infallible observations and quite subtly, have learned more than sufficiently, and demonstrated by <b>unconquered demonstrations</b> [<i>apodixes</i>], that <b>Comets are really procreated in the high aether, and there exercise their course, and that an ordered [course]</b>. Let it therefore suffice me to have produced into the open the truth itself, which long lay hidden, and to have set [it] forth to be beheld by others. To whose shining rays, if some still have eyes so darkened with such mist that they cannot endure so splendid a light suddenly coming forth from more-than-<b>Cimmerian</b> darkness, that affects me not at all. For I am sufficiently aware that nothing is so aptly and indubitably asserted but that it finds its contradictors, and that the very truth labours for the most part under the gravest detractions in this world, while lies and vanities are well-nigh everywhere in esteem.</p><p>As to <b>Craig's</b> too-presumptuously pronouncing that you performed \"<b>too little by [the rules of] art</b>\" in your <i>Dialexis</i>, he seems to have looked to the <b>doctrine of parallaxes</b> less solidly and completely treated there. For he was doubtless ignorant that you yourself afterward emended your errata in this part. Nay, I too replied sufficiently on your behalf in the Apology, and dissolved what he objects to have been uncertain in that Comet. Let him please himself thus, by extenuating others' excellent efforts on account of certain blemishes, and bringing forward nothing more perfect himself that makes for the matter — let him see whether, among the intelligent and those strong in judgment, he be convicted of excessive <b>self-love</b> [<i>philautia</i>] and rash censure. What I find wanting in your <i>Dialexis</i>, especially about those [things] which properly made for that New Star, you will perceive from <b>chapter 8 of our first book of aethereal Phenomena</b>, which treats of this New Star professedly. For there, immediately after the <b>Landgravian</b> [observations], I weighed your observations and pronouncements, and freely brought forward our judgment about them — which I am persuaded will not be unwelcome to you, especially studious of the more unfolded truth and candidly taking all [things]. As to what <b>Craig</b> affirms — that the Mathematicians, in demonstrating the parallaxes of Comets, too securely neglected their proper motion — he asserts [it] not so far from the matter as regards most; and not even <b>Regiomontanus</b> himself, in that erudite writing of his on Comets, is to be excepted. For although in <b>Problems 2, 3, and 4</b> (as you say) he advises that account must be had of the proper motion in Comets, yet the very processes of his demonstrations entirely <b>exclude</b> this, and presuppose the Phenomenon agitated by the primary motion only. Nor is [the fact] that in the 2nd Problem he excuses the impediments of proper motion by the brevity of the interposed time sufficient to remove every suspicion of error here, since the variety of proper motion in Comets is for the most part <b>swifter</b> than that which is insinuated from the alteration of parallax; and by shortening the time interposed between observations, we render the perception of parallaxes the more abstruse. So the <b>limitation</b> which he applies in the 3rd Problem, and wishes to hold in the following too — although it somewhat mitigates the difficulties from the genuine advance of Comets in unfolding the parallax, and renders them more tolerable, yet does not remove them on every side; and that manner by which he teaches to predefine the proper diurnal motion is utterly <b>erroneous</b>, nor does it satisfy the subtlety of the matter to be inquired. I say nothing of [the fact] that so exact an investigation of the azimuths and the intercepted time as is here required is doubtful, and the least, scarcely perceptible, error in the practice of the operation turns out too fertile: so that, on that account, according to Regiomontanus's Problems — however irrefragable as to the demonstration itself — it is not so ready as is thought to discern without error the parallaxes of Comets showing themselves from the heaven. He thought that Comets necessarily blaze in the upper air, as a certain <b>fiery meteor</b>, and that, the Aristotelian authority so prevailing that it imposed even on the supreme Mathematicians. If, therefore, the Comets' position were so near, their parallaxes would equal very many degrees, and so would be more easily perceived according to Regiomontanus's reasonings, nor would the less-exact emendation of the proper motion, or some tiny deviation in the azimuths and measurement of time, so greatly disturb the unravelling of the thing sought. That distinguished artificer doubtless foresaw these and similar difficulties, and therefore protested that the <b>treasures of nature cannot be exhausted</b>, but that it is permitted to attain at least something near to certain knowables.</p><p>Which although they be so, and too true, yet unless one apply the supreme diligence in scrutinizing the parallaxes of Comets, and avoid all the rocks of error even the least obvious, he will not arrive at a sufficiently settled knowledge of the thing he intends. And therefore I, these slippery ways set aside, judged that I must proceed to investigating the Comets' position by a far other track, so that not even the least parallaxes (such as occur in the highest aether) might find some subterfuge, nor the involvement of proper motion bring an impediment, nor finally anything be committed in the very observing-method that easily gives occasion for a sensible error. Craig, therefore, does me no light injury (as you too confess), when he does not hesitate to object the <b>proper motions of Comets creeping in as hindrances in knowing their parallaxes</b> — since I have everywhere quite studiously and subtly removed and guarded against them. And therefore I proceeded to the mark of the proposed truth not by some simple form of reasoning, but as it were by <b>circuits</b> [<i>ambages</i>], lest I strike upon the rocks of error. You indeed excuse the neglect of the proper course in the two Comets described by you by the smallness of the assumed time; but just as the brevity of the interposed time here brings some advantage on account of the Comet's place little altered meanwhile, so again, while it varies the Comet's altitude by no great interval (as it ought), it renders the <b>discrimination of parallaxes more imperceptible</b> — as I advised before about the Comet of [15]77, where you had assumed an interval of at least 18 minutes between two observations.</p><p>About the <b>Comet of [15]80</b> we shall see in its place, the Deity favouring; which, although you most rightly pronounced to have been really aethereal, yet, by neglecting its proper motion (to say nothing now of certain others), you still granted it too great a parallax — to which also the interval of the presupposed time, stricter than is just (especially about the Meridian, where altitudes vary slowly), gave occasion. But I reply to your letter perhaps too prolixly; in which part, if anything has been amiss, I ask that you impute [it] to [my] desire of conferring with you about these, and excuse [it] by [your] accustomed candour and benevolence of mind toward me. Now I will dispatch those [things] which are in the <b>Postscripts</b>.</p><p>I rejoice that you have received those pages of the printed <b>Astronomical Letters</b>, together with the brief description of the last Comet; and I should wish that you would not be loath, at leisure, to write me your judgment, both about that Comet and also about those [things] which are chiefly treated in those Letters.</p><p>As to the <b>models</b> [<i>modellae</i>] representing some of my instruments: I took care, after I learned your wish in this matter, that some be made — as many as could be completed while the courier tarried here — which I send you by the same; and I ask that you communicate [them] to no one but Lord <b>Curtius</b> alone. The artificer expressed in them those [features] which could conveniently be formed, in [things] so small, after the likeness of the larger instruments. For it was not possible to exhibit everything to the nail [exactly]. I fear, therefore, that in making some larger instrument the pre-demonstration of these designs will be less sufficient. Wherefore Lord Curtius not without reason judged it more advisable that [instruments] be prepared for you there of the same magnitude as my instruments are. In which matter too I would gladly gratify him, if I had at hand all the artificers whose work I used in constructing mine over some past years; for I dismissed them, after the instruments which I had proposed to make were made, retaining only one or another, who might take care of those already made, and repair if anything by chance were wanting in them. These, however, do not suffice to construct some whole instrument anew. For most of mine are [such] that five or six diverse artificers laboured on them, and that for no little time, so much so that some were scarcely completed in the third year from the work's beginning. There are besides those <b>machines</b> which I chiefly trust, of such magnitude that they can be conveniently carried by no cart. I have a single <b>Sextant</b>, joined in a peculiar manner from diverse parts, so that it can be taken apart and shut in some chest, and again composed for use by screws. This I had made especially for the purpose, that, carried by it to <b>Nuremberg</b>, I might most accurately explore the pole-altitude, on account of certain solar observations once had there — which is still my purpose. This very Sextant I will not refuse to impart to you for some while, until another can be elaborated after its likeness, and you have well perceived its quite expeditious and certain use in taking both distances and altitudes of the stars — provided it be acceptable to Lord Curtius. If so, let him send hither to me some learned young man, devoted to Astronomical matters, who may here sufficiently learn the use of this instrument and its disassembly and folding, and afterward bring it to you safe and sound, and there open its explanation and manifold utility. For otherwise this instrument is sent in vain, unless someone be present with [it] who has all its reckonings ascertained. This you can report to Lord Curtius in my name, and [that] I shall be most ready to gratify him, both in this part and in others, as far as in me lies.</p><p>In your <b>Letters and autographs transmitted to me</b>, even unadmonished, I will take care that those [things] which can offend some, and which it is of no interest to others to know, be omitted, when they come to be published in print. How soon that can be done I am not yet certain, since I have not ready to hand the <b>printing-paper</b> for completing in print the first tome of my work, which I am now elaborating. For although, to avoid the difficulties of procuring paper from Germany (by which I was hindered before), I had myself, at no small expense, had a <b>Mill</b> built on this Island for making it, another adverse [thing] came on beyond expectation, whereby not even so was it permitted to obtain the desired paper. For two <b>papermakers</b> [<i>Chartopaei</i>], sought out at different times, who pledged me their work and faith, after I had sent [them] into Germany to procure old materials [rags], each, having received money there from my procurators, secretly withdrew. And so this whole summer, since I afterward found no suitable artificer, I am forced to interrupt the making of paper; and since no other is now ready to hand, I must defer the completion of the first tome until printing-paper be supplied. Yet, lest this delay be too troublesome to you meanwhile — you who, I know, eagerly await its publication — I send for the present a copy of those <b>gatherings</b> [<i>quaterniones</i>] which have hitherto been completed in print, which meanwhile, while the rest are prepared, you can unroll at leisure, and write me your censure about those [things] contained therein, and frankly advise [me], where it shall seem [good] to you. The earlier pages are omitted, which will comprise the book's title, the Privileges, and the prefaces; for all these, as is the custom, will be prefixed when the whole work is finished. There are also wanting <b>three gatherings at the end of the first chapter</b>, in which I resolved to set down further, separately, certain [things] about the <b>magnitude of the year</b> by a more general indication, and at the same time briefly to consider the <b>Lilian</b> [Gregorian] correction of the Julian year, newly introduced. So too, at the foot of the second chapter, <b>five gatherings</b> are lacking, in which the <b>table of the fixed [stars] restored by us</b> will be contained, together with certain other [things] pertaining hither. The <b>whole seventh chapter</b> too is omitted, which will unlock the <b>magnitudes</b> not only of the New Star, but also of the rest of the fixed [stars] and even of the Planets, otherwise than has hitherto been done. The <b>two last chapters</b> too are not yet printed, for want of paper — of which the <b>ninth</b> will examine and dissolve the tenets of those who <b>mixed the New Star with the orbs of the Planets</b>, the <b>tenth</b> [those] of [those] who rendered it <b>wholly sublunary</b>, or brought forward something else too absurd about it. These, I say, remaining for the completion of the first tome, you will obtain in their time, the Deity favouring; meanwhile read through, examine, and take in good part those [things] which I now communicate.</p><p>Your <b>observations about Mars [♂]</b> and certain other stars, had some years ago, which you sent, are less exact, because the <b>Radius</b> [cross-staff] by no means yields the true intervals of the stars. The places of the fixed [stars] too which you applied differ much from the heaven itself, as our <b>restitution</b> will be able to advise you — so that it is no wonder that the calculation, repeated in diverse ways, so enormously dissented from itself.</p><p>If you carefully observed the <b>eclipse of the Sun [☉] which occurred in the past days</b>, communicate to me its times and the Phase noted there. Here it was perfectly serene through its whole duration, and the Sun began to fail <b>after midnight of the 21st of July, 6 hours 51 minutes elapsed</b>. It ceased at the <b>9th hour exactly completed</b>. The hitherto-used tables, therefore — whether Alphonsine or Copernican — with the reduction of the Meridians applied, do not differ much in the <b>time</b> of this eclipse from the heaven itself, and that especially for this cause, that the Sun's motion about the sign <b>Leo [♌]</b> is not very erroneous in them. But as to the <b>magnitude of the eclipsed part</b>, the Canons erred much: for at least a <b>third part of the Sun, or 4 digits</b>, was seen to fail, whereas a defect of at least <b>8 points</b> [digits] would have appeared in our horizon, if [one] were trusting the tables. Which I wished to indicate in passing, that they may be conferred with your observation. But, lest I be too prolix in replying, it is time that I now stay my pen. Do you, therefore, my excellent and most loving Thaddaeus, fare as happily as possible, and at the first occasion do not cease to revisit me by your letters, since otherwise we cannot address one another.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 3rd day of August, Old Style, in the Year 1590. Tycho Brahe. I ask that you communicate the book sent to no one, except to Lord <b>Curtius</b> alone, until it be completed and issued to the public.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witnesses: <b>A</b> = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.60; <b>B</b> = ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.50. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VII pp. 264–275</b> (Dreyer printed; PDF pp. 278–289); every crux confirmed (anchors in the Editorial note), and additionally cross-corroborated by <b>Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b> (the letter answered) and <b>the Magini letter of 1 December 1590</b> (the parallel letter, same eclipse/book-returns/prosthaphaeresis).</li><li><b>Sender / recipient &amp; the letter answered.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg) → <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b> (Prague). This is the <b>reply to Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b>: Tycho takes up Hájek's comet-motion-law thesis and three reasons in order. He <b>corrects Hájek's slip</b>: the Sun-centred Venus/Mercury orbit was demonstrated for the comet of <b>1577</b>, \"not the one which blazed in [15]80, as your letter has it\" (Hájek's letter indeed wrote <i>Cometa anni 80</i>) — a nice internal cross-check between the two letters.</li><li><b>The comet-motion argument (the spine).</b> Tycho concedes <b>no universal hypothesis</b> fits all comets (he \"never asserted it\"), but insists each comet, being <b>celestial</b> (all procreated in the aether), naturally seeks <b>circular motion about some point</b> (not necessarily Sun or Earth), so <b>each admits its own hypothesis</b> a posteriori. Difficulty ≠ impossibility (the <b>squaring of the circle</b> analogy: <i>scibile sed nondum cognitum</i>). Proof: the <b>1577 comet</b> (book 2, ch. 8) reduced to regular circular motion (6°→¼°/day), numbers fitting better than for any planet; likewise the <b>Feb–Mar 1590 comet</b> (12 days, 7½°→1½°/day, the diary-table's third column shows no jumps). He rebuts Hájek's three reasons: (1) he speaks of <i>singulars</i>, not <i>universals</i>; (3) none of his <b>five observed comets</b> was stationary or moved by \"tumultuary leaps\" — the famous <b>8°-leap</b> Hájek and <b>Cornelius Gemma</b> ascribed to the 1577 comet is an artefact of <b>bad fixed-star places</b>, not the comet; comets trace <b>arcs of great circles</b>. The Moon-analogy is misread (Tycho reasons from the whole apparition, not one day); the heavens are <b>not solid</b> (no comets in the 8th sphere unless immobile like the New Star); <b>Copernicus's precession is erroneous</b> (1°/71 yr, not 1°/100). He charges Hájek with <b>prejudice</b> (\"concluding beforehand that it is impossible\") and urges him to \"add a spur, not an obstacle.\" Closes with the Pythagorean/order-in-nature topos.</li><li><b>The technical sidebar (overlaps the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590).</b> <b>Alphonsine vs Copernican</b> discrepancies in Saturn/Jupiter (Copernicus nearer the sky but still off — Jupiter &gt;1°, Saturn ½°, Mars ~3°; the live sky positions given to the minute); the <b>Maestlinian epoch</b> kept deliberately; the <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> crux — Hájek \"inconsiderately assumed <b>24 November</b> for the <b>26 December</b>\" Tycho computed to (book 2, p. 279) — exactly the discrepancy Hájek conceded in his 11 June letter; the book-returns (<b>Muñoz</b> no longer needed, <b>Frangipani</b> ineptitudes, <b>Apian's</b> 1531-comet book kept a while [unreliable, \"within 1–2°\"], <b>Trapezuntius's</b> Ptolemy commentary to be returned). The <b>Caesarean Privilege</b> obtained via <b>Curtius</b>; the lost <b>Gallican</b> privilege; another hoped from <b>Henri of Navarre</b> (Henri IV).</li><li><b>The Craig dossier (overlaps the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591).</b> Tycho's <i>Apologia</i> against <b>Craig</b> delivered \"the preceding winter,\" unanswered; <b>Anders Krag</b> (the physician with <b>Anne of Denmark</b>, the \"Queen of Scotland\") reports Craig still \"enslaved to Aristotle … an incurable disease of obstinacy.\" Tycho defends Hájek's <i>Dialexis</i> against Craig's \"<i>parum ex arte</i>\" charge (it concerned the parallax doctrine, since emended), points to <b>book 1, ch. 8</b> (his treatment of the New Star, \"immediately after the Landgravian observations\"), and partly <b>agrees</b> with Craig that mathematicians — even <b>Regiomontanus</b> (whose Problems 2–4 warn of proper motion but whose demonstrations exclude it) — neglected the comet's proper motion in parallax-work; Tycho's own method avoids the \"slippery ways\" \"by circuits.\"</li><li><b>The instruments, paper-mill, and unfinished <i>Progymnasmata</i> (valuable production-history).</b> Tycho sends small <b>instrument models</b> (<i>modellae</i>) for Hájek, to be shown only to <b>Curtius</b> (who rightly wants full-size copies built at Prague); Tycho's instruments took \"five or six artificers\" and up to three years each, and the great machines \"no cart can carry.\" He offers the <b>demountable screw-jointed Sextant</b> (built for a planned <b>Nuremberg</b> pole-altitude trip) on loan, if Curtius sends a young man to learn it. The <b>paper crisis</b>: his Hven <b>paper-mill</b> stalled when two <b>papermakers absconded</b> with advance money, delaying the <i>Progymnasmata</i> Tome I; he sends the printed gatherings so far and lists what is still missing — the title/privileges/prefaces, 3 gatherings on <b>year-length + the Gregorian (\"Lilian\") correction</b>, 5 on the <b>fixed-star catalogue</b>, all of <b>ch. 7</b> (magnitudes of the nova, fixed stars, planets), and <b>chs. 9–10</b> (against those who made the New Star planetary or sublunary). The <b>21 July 1590 solar eclipse</b>: Uraniborg, first contact <b>6ʰ51ᵐ</b> after midnight, end at <b>9ʰ00ᵐ</b>, only <b>4 digits</b> (⅓) covered vs the tables' predicted <b>8</b> (the <i>time</i> fits, since the Sun near <b>Leo</b> is well-tabulated; the <i>magnitude</i> does not). Hájek's <b>Mars/star observations by the <i>Radius</i> (cross-staff)</b> judged inexact (bad star-places).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b>; <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (Kurz von Senftenau, Vice-Chancellor); <b>Henri IV</b> (\"King of the Gauls, of Navarre\"); <b>Cornelius Gemma</b>; <b>Maestlin</b> (<i>Mesthlinus</i>); <b>Copernicus, Ptolemy, Aristotle, Regiomontanus</b>; <b>John Craig</b>; <b>Anders Krag</b> (the unnamed \"Doctor of Medicine,\" with Anne of Denmark); <b>Muñoz, Frangipani, Apian, George of Trebizond</b>; the <b>Pythagoreans</b>. Places: <b>Uraniborg, Prague, Nuremberg, Rostock</b> (the student-courier), <b>Germany</b>; <b>Hven</b> (the paper-mill).</li><li><b>Greek / Latin tags.</b> <i>Dedomena/Dedomenis</i> = δεδόμενα (data/givens); <i>Phaenomenωn</i> = φαινομένων; <i>apodixes</i> = ἀποδείξεις (demonstrations); <i>philautia</i> = φιλαυτία (self-love); <i>scibilia sed nondum cognita</i> = \"knowable but not yet known\" (the quadrature-of-the-circle status); <i>Cimmeriae tenebrae</i> = proverbial deep darkness; <i>ambages</i> = circuits/roundabout ways; <i>modellae</i> = (instrument) models; <i>Chartopaei</i> = papermakers; <i>Radius</i> = the cross-staff (radius astronomicus). Glyphs: ♂ Mars, ♀ Venus, ☿ Mercury, ☉ Sun, ♄ Saturn, ♃ Jupiter, ♌ Leo.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <b>3 August 1590 O.S.</b> (<i>stylo veteri</i>); matches the catalogue label. The eclipse \"21 July\" is O.S. (the great solar eclipse of 21/31 July 1590).</li><li><b>OCR slips (transcription, kept verbatim, corrected in translation).</b> <i>evineunt</i> → <i>evincunt</i> (\"prove\"); <i>persectissimum</i> → <i>perfectissimum</i> (\"most perfect\"); <i>FRANGIAPANI</i> → <i>Frangipani</i>; <i>subijt</i> (for <i>subiit</i>) and <i>competierit</i> (for <i>competiverit</i>) left as printed. No heavy \"A\"-sigla here (unlike Hájek's letter of 11 Jun 1590, the undated Peucer letter of 1590, the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588, and the Herwart letter of 16 Nov 1599). None affects sense.</li><li><b>Confidentiality instruction naming Curtius again.</b> The closing postscript asks that the book just sent — the printed gatherings (<i>quaterniones</i>) of the unfinished first tome, discussed earlier in the letter — be shown to no one except Lord <b>Curtius</b> [Jacob Kurz von Senftenau, Imperial Vice-Chancellor] alone, and only until it is completed and publicly issued.</li><li><b>Consistent with the letter's established pattern.</b> This is the second instance in the same letter of Tycho restricting circulation of unpublished material to Curtius by name — the first occurs earlier over the instrument models ('I ask that you communicate them to no one but Lord Curtius alone'). The recovered sentence shows the identical restriction applied to the book itself.</li><li><b>Source-text caveat.</b> The transmitted Latin already carries several editorial square-bracket restorations ([ut], præ[terquam], ex[pectans quoad], [in publicum em]issus) marking a damaged or abbreviated original; the sense is not in doubt, but the exact wording at those points is an editorial reconstruction rather than a clean reading.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The Latin clause \"non enim de eo, qui anno 8 effulsit\" is corrected to Dreyer's \"non enim de eo, quo anno 80 effulsit\" (TBOO VII p.268, line 24), restoring the dropped digit and relative pronoun in the reference to the comet of 1580. The English translation already rendered the correct sense (\"the one which blazed in the year [15]8[0]\") and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — sound. A long, continuously-argued reply (the comet-motion-law rebuttal, the tables sidebar, the Craig/Regiomontanus parallax discussion, and the rich instrument/paper-mill/<i>Progymnasmata</i> production notes) rendered securely throughout; the register (warm but firm scientific correction) is preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, <i>salvare</i>/save, hypothesis, acronychal, prosthaphaeresis, <i>restitutio</i>, <i>apodixis</i>, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with <b>Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b> (which this answers — including the corrected 1577-vs-1580 comet point and the 24-Nov/26-Dec prosthaphaeresis crux), <b>the Magini letter of 1 December 1590</b> (the parallel eclipse/book-return content), and <b>the Craig letter of 14 November 1591</b> (the Craig/Anders-Krag thread). The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VII pp. 264–275) and confirms every crux</b>, so the collation reservation that previously capped the grade is <b>lifted</b>. One residue remains: the few minor OCR slips, corrected and declared. It does not obscure the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 confirms every crux; only declared minor OCR slips remain, no lacuna/dagger/dating conflict.</i></p><p>High-to-medium confidence. The overall sense — restrict the book's circulation to Curtius alone, pending publication — is secure and matches the letter's own established idiom, the identical restriction being applied in near-identical wording a few paragraphs earlier to the instrument models. The one caveat is that the Latin itself is not cleanly preserved: several words are already editorially bracketed as reconstructed in the source ([ut], præ[terquam], ex[pectans quoad], [in publicum em]issus), so the English follows an edited, not a directly transmitted, text at those points.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 15 Dreyer page-images (TBOO VII pp.262-276) in four chunks (1-4, 5-8, 9-12, 13-15) and visually collated the full letter: opening and closing formulas, the postscript, and every proper name, date, sum, and number in the source_text and published_english against Dreyer's print. All content matches Dreyer save one dropped digit (anno 8 vs anno 80, p.268) in source_text, which the translation had already silently corrected. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found across this very long (15-page) letter; the comet-of-1577 argumentation, the Jupiter/Saturn/Mars discrepancy figures, the 26 December/24 November prosthaphaeresis dispute, the July 21 eclipse timings (6h51m onset, 9th hour end, 4 vs 8 digits), the Sextant/Nuremberg passage, and the paper-mill/papermakers episode all render faithfully. Grade forced to C solely by the single TEXT_ERROR rule; verdict MINOR because the English itself is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our published Latin drops a digit, printing 'anno 8' where Dreyer prints 'anno 80' (i.e. the comet of 1580, contrasted with the comet of 1577 under discussion). The English translator evidently sensed the gap and silently supplied '[15]8[0]' in brackets, so the English reads correctly, but the source_text itself is corrupted against the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>non enim de eo, quo anno 80 effulsit, ut tuæ habent literæ, id protuli (TBOO VII p.268, line 24)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the clause against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.268, lines 22-24: \"...ea, quæ de Cometa anni 77mi (non enim de eo, quo anno 80 effulsit, ut tuæ habent literæ, id protuli) publicavi...\"); the printed edition reads \"quo anno 80\", confirming both the dropped digit and the qui/quo divergence in the published Latin. Corrected only that four-word span in current_latin; current_english was already correct as published and is returned unchanged.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.60.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.50."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 24, "prev": "brahe-t_15900611001", "next": "brahe-t_15910000002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900611001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910829001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receipt of the new Imperial Privilege via Prague courier, thanks Hagecius for moving Curtius to obtain it, and notes a lost transcript of the Gallican privilege.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900803001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900809001", "d": "1590-08-09", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Dronning Sophie", "ck": "Dronning Sophie", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Kolding", "t": "Queen Sophie → Tycho Brahe — Koldinghus (Kolding), 9 August 1590 [O.S.] (catalogued Copenhagen)", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588–92, fol. 81 f. (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF NOGEN PENDINGE ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR</p><p>Sophia etc.</p><p>WOR gunst thilforn. Wider, att ßom j ether haffuer att erindre om thett thußinnd daller, Wij naadigst ether haffue med forstrachtt, huilke wdj thenne neruerendis herredag her wdj Koldinng schulle werrid Oß erlagdtt och bethald, och j wnnderdannigst erre begierrendis formedelst gott folck dag och reßpitt med ßamme pending paa nogen wgger epther Michaelis førstkommendis, tha erre Wij naadigst ther med thillfridtz och wille ether ther med bedage thill fiorthen dage epther for:ne Michaelis førstkommendis. Bedendis ether therforre och wille, attj thill thend thiid wisseligen och wden ald widere forhalling och forßømmelse retther ether epther att erlegge och bethalle Oß for:ne summa pendinge, j wdj ßaa maade thill Oß schyldig er, att Wij thill ßamme thiid wist kunde komme thill Wor bethalling, anßehendis att Wij ßidenn pindtzdag, thill huilken thid the schulle werrid bethald, nu thill for:ne bestempthe dag epther Michaelis aff gode wilge och naadigst benegenhed haffue med ßamme Wor bethalling forthøffuid, attj therforre retther ether ßielff, ßaa Wij thill for:ne thermin wden widere ophold bliffuer hoffuedßummen med renthen fornøyett och thilstilledtt, ßaa framptt Wij jcke schulle foraarßagis wed anndre middell baade hoeß ethers forloffuere och ether att ßøge Wor bethallinng, therßom ther med widerre bliffuer forhalled.</p><p>Ther med etc.<br>Actum Koldinghus thennd 9. augustij anno 1590.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter concerning certain monies, as follows:</b></p><p>Sophie, etc.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, as you have to recall concerning the one thousand daler with which We have graciously advanced you, which were to have been paid down and settled to Us at this present assembly of the realm [<i>herredag</i>] here at Kolding — and [as] you do most humbly request, through good people, a day and respite with the same monies for some weeks after the coming Michaelmas — then We are graciously content therewith, and will indulge you therein until fourteen days after the aforesaid coming Michaelmas. Asking you therefore, and desiring [it], that against that time you assuredly, and without any further delay and neglect, dispose yourself to lay down and pay Us the aforesaid sum of money in which you are in such manner indebted to Us, so that at the same time We may surely come to Our payment — considering that We have, ever since Pentecost (by which time they were to have been paid), now [extended] to the aforesaid appointed day after Michaelmas, out of good will and gracious favour, deferred the same Our payment; so that you therefore [may] dispose yourself, so that by the aforesaid term, without further delay, We are satisfied and have handed over to Us the principal sum together with the interest — inasmuch as We should not be obliged, by other means, to seek Our payment both from your sureties and from you, if it be hereby further delayed.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Koldinghus, the 9th of August in the year 1590.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> (1557–1631), dowager queen of Denmark, widow of Frederik II (d. 1588) and mother of the still-minor Christian IV; she resided at Nykøbing and was active in financial administration. Here she acts as a creditor calling in a loan to Tycho. The opening <i>Sophia etc.</i> abbreviates her full royal style.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tyge Brahe</b> (the Danish form of Tycho's name), styled simply by name in the register rubric.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (<i>Dronning Sophies tegnelser</i> — the queen dowager's register of outgoing letters). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…NOGEN PENDINGE ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning certain monies, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Substance.</b> A demand for repayment of a <b>1,000-daler</b> loan (<i>thett thußinnd daller</i>) that the queen had advanced to Tycho. The original term was <b>Pentecost</b> (<i>pindtzdag</i>); she has already deferred it once to the present <i>herredag</i> at Kolding, and now grants a final extension to <b>fourteen days after Michaelmas</b> (29 September O.S. → ~13 October O.S.). The principal (<i>hoffuedßummen</i>, \"the principal sum\") plus interest (<i>renthen</i>) is to be repaid by that term, on pain of recourse against both Tycho and his sureties (<i>forloffuere</i>).</li><li><b><i>herredag</b></i> — a <i>Herredag</i>, the assembly of the Council of the Realm and high nobility (sometimes a supreme-court session); here convened at Kolding. Rendered \"assembly of the realm.\"</li><li><b><i>forloffuere</b></i> — \"sureties / guarantors\" (those who stood bond for Tycho's loan); the threat of seeking payment from them as well as from Tycho is the operative legal pressure.</li><li><b>Negation count.</b> The body contains exactly <b>one</b> negation, in the closing conditional: <i>ßaa framptt Wij <b>jcke</b> schulle foraarßagis … therßom ther med widerre bliffuer forhalled</i> — \"so that We should <b>not</b> be obliged [to seek payment by other means] … if it be hereby further delayed.\" This is a courteous-but-firm threat: <i>unless</i> you pay, <i>so that</i> she need not pursue sureties. The conditional's force (a veiled warning of enforcement) is preserved; the single negation is reproduced exactly once.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1590).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wider</i> = <i>Vider</i> (imperative \"Know\"); <i>ßom j ether haffuer att erindre</i> = <i>som I eder haver at erindre</i> (\"as you have to recall\"); <i>forstrachtt</i> = <i>forstrakt</i> (\"advanced, lent\"); <i>wdj</i> = <i>udi</i> (\"in\"); <i>neruerendis</i> = <i>nærværende</i> (\"present\"); <i>schulle werrid</i> = <i>skulle været</i> (\"were to have been\"); <i>erlagdtt och bethald</i> = <i>erlagt og betalt</i> (\"paid down and settled\"); <i>wnnderdannigst</i> = <i>underdanigst</i> (\"most humbly\"); <i>formedelst</i> = \"by means of, through\"; <i>dag och reßpitt</i> = <i>dag og respit</i> (\"a day [of grace] and respite\"); <i>wgger</i> = <i>uger</i> (\"weeks\"); <i>førstkommendis</i> = <i>førstkommende</i> (\"next-coming\"); <i>thillfridtz</i> = <i>tilfreds</i> (\"content\"); <i>bedage</i> = \"to grant a term/respite to\"; <i>fiorthen dage</i> = <i>fjorten dage</i> (\"fourteen days\"); <i>wden ald widere forhalling och forßømmelse</i> = <i>uden al videre forhaling og forsømmelse</i> (\"without any further delay and neglect\"); <i>retther ether epther</i> = <i>retter eder efter</i> (\"dispose / conform yourself to\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>anßehendis</i> = <i>ansehendes</i> (\"considering\"); <i>ßidenn</i> = <i>siden</i> (\"since\"); <i>bestempthe</i> = <i>bestemte</i> (\"appointed, fixed\"); <i>benegenhed</i> = <i>bevågenhed</i> (\"favour, goodwill\"); <i>forthøffuid</i> = <i>fortøvet</i> (\"delayed, tarried\"); <i>ßielff</i> = <i>selv</i> (\"yourself\"); <i>thermin</i> = <i>termin</i> (\"term, term-date\"); <i>ophold</i> = \"delay\"; <i>fornøyett</i> = <i>fornøjet</i> (\"satisfied, contented\"); <i>thilstilledtt</i> = <i>tilstillet</i> (\"delivered, handed over\"); <i>ßaa framptt</i> = <i>såfremt</i> (\"inasmuch as / provided that\"); <i>foraarßagis</i> = <i>foraarsages</i> (\"be caused / obliged\"); <i>middell</i> = <i>midler</i> (\"means\"); <i>hoeß</i> = <i>hos</i> (\"with, at the hands of\"); <i>ßøge</i> = <i>søge</i> (\"to seek / sue for\").</li><li><b>Calendar / dating.</b> The festival anchors are <b>Old Style</b>: <i>pindtzdag</i> = <b>Pentecost (Whitsun)</b>, in 1590 (O.S.) 24 May; <i>Michaelis</i> = <b>Michaelmas</b>, 29 September (O.S.); \"fourteen days after Michaelmas\" ≈ 13 October (O.S.). Dates are flagged, not converted. <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5.</li><li><b>Place / date crux.</b> The dataset `label` records the place as <b>\"København (Copenhagen)\"</b>, but the source's own dateline reads <b><i>Actum Koldinghus … 9. augustij anno 1590</b></i> — <b>Koldinghus</b>, the royal castle at <b>Kolding</b> (Jutland). The header follows the as-written dateline (Kolding) and appends the catalogued place per protocol §4.1. The body confirms Kolding internally (<i>herredag her wdj Koldinng</i>, \"this assembly of the realm here at Kolding\"), so the catalogue's \"Copenhagen\" is an error or refers to the register's archival home, not the place of issue. The discrepancy is recorded, not reconciled. The date itself (9 August 1590) is not in dispute.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the single negation reproduced exactly. The flagged points are (i) the place discrepancy between the catalogue label (Copenhagen) and the dateline (Koldinghus/Kolding), resolved in favour of the dateline per §4.1, and (ii) the precise sense of <i>bedage</i>/<i>forthøffuid</i> in the deferral clause, rendered transparently rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Located and collated the correct entry (Dreyer TBOO XIV, no. 83, 'Sophia', dated 9 August 1590 - distinguished from the neighbouring 18 July 1590 letter with near-identical wording on the same page) against the page images pp.53-54; text matches word for word (the 1,000 daler, the Michaelmas/Pentecost dates, the fourteen-day respite, Koldinghus), and the translation renders the financial and temporal clauses accurately. No defects found; upgraded from the unverified B to A on full collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588-92, fol. 81 f.."], "th": {"label": "Dronning Sophie", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15900718001", "next": "brahe-t_15920314001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Grants a further extension on Tycho's 1,000-daler debt to fourteen days after Michaelmas while insisting on payment by that date without further delay or neglect.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900809001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15900911001", "d": "1590-09-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bologna", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Bologna, 13 September 1590 [N.S.] (catalogued 11 September 1590 N.S.)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tycho Brahe,<i> Astronomiae instauratae mechanica </i>(as printed by Tycho; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DESCRIPTUM LITERARUM CLARISSIMI ET PRAeSTANTISSIMI MATHEMATICI DN. JOHANNIS ANTONII MAGINI PATAVINI BONONIA EODEM ANNO CIϽ. IϽ. XC. AD TYCHONEM BRAHE IN DANIAM SCRIPTARUM.<br>ILLUSTRIS VIR,</p><p>ACCEPI librum tuum eruditissimum ad me tuo nomine a studioso quodam Dano, qui olim tibi domesticus erat, transmissum; quo mihi abs te nihil gratius exhiberi potuit, cum ejusmodi scripta curiosissime conquirere soleam, ac libentissime perlegere. Vix enim aliâ viâ sperassem eum ad manus meas perventurum, cum perpauca istic excusa volumina soleant in Italiam importari propter longinquitatem itineris, ac vecturae difficultatem: Eo accedit, quod elegantissimo hoc munere, quanti me faceres, praeclare significasti: quo quidem nomine tibi gratias ago, quas possum maximas. Quod enim summopere expeto et studere soleo, ut mihi scilicet pateat aditus in gratiam tui similium; tu, quae tua est singularis humanitas, id mihi ultro obtulisti. Hunc ego meum in te animum ne possem, ut decuit, atque ut optabam, statim per literas significare; id fuit caussae, quod ad te cum meis literis nuper a me elucubratum Opus propediem edendum mittere constitueram: Superiore autem aestate, cum incidissem in morbum, egi diligenter cum eodem studioso, scriptis ad eum literis, ut a te meo nomine, quae hîc subiciam, petere ne gravaretur. Habere igitur maxime cuperem compendiosam descriptionem stellarum fixarum, quemadmodum a te emendatae sunt, ut mihi usui esse possit, in meis brevi ad praelum revocandis Ephaemeridibus, ad haec notitiam aliquam Eccentricitatum Planetarum, itemque commensurationum uniuscujusque eorum orbis, si eas a Copernianis differre invenisti, ac correxisti. Quod si mihi gratificari non dedignaberis, efficiam profecto ut te non poeniteat beneficii in me tui: Namque eâ quae humanos et liberalium artium Professores decet, ingenuitate, tuarum te rerum auctorem, laudare in meis scriptis mihi constitutum est. Meum vero sensum ac judicium, de tuo illo praeclaro labore, circa Cometam anno 1577. conspectum, ne desideres: Sic habeto, vir Clarissime; Cum enim incredibilem in illo diligentiam tuam et accuratam observandi viam perspexerim, mihi persuadeo, fore, ut caelestium corporum motus emendare exactissime possis, fructuque operae illius maximo omnium studio, atque expectationi cumulatissime satisfacias; unde nomini tuo sempiternum decus adjungatur. Cuperem tamen, te in motu Martis observando et examinando maxime incumbere, cum eum observari non posse exacte, vulgo persuasum sit: atque ego in eâ sum haeresi, ut putem immutari Eccentricitatem ipsius, periodumque suam habere, ita ut alia in eo introducenda sit aequatio Eccentricitatis: alioqui si tabula efficiatur pro maxima Eccentricitate, non poterit minimae inservire, et contra. Ex quo videmus, Copernicum (etsi Eccentricitatem hujusmodi variari deprehendit) non idcirco tabulas condidisse, quae singulis temporibus conveniant, quando Martis aequationes supputatae ad Eccentricitatem a Copernico observatam partium 5.51'. 30''. aptari minime possunt, ipsius Ptolemaei temporibus, quibus partium 6. Martis Eccentricitatem definivit. Non possum non magnopere probare Systema Universi a te excogitatum, quamvis cuperem Solis Orbem ac Martis nequaquam sese intersecare. Quod si, ut a studioso isto cognovi, Martem acronycum terrae propius accedere, quam Solem, a te est observatum, intersecatio hujusmodi omninô admittenda est. In magna versor expectatione tuarum hujusmodi observationum et speculationum, quas et probare et sequi minime erubescam. Etsi in construendis Ephaemeridibus resolutisque tabulis a Copernianâ ratione, et tabulis Prutenicis ne latum quidem unguem deflexi. Spero etiam, quod vehementer cupio, primum tuum de mundi Aetherei phaenomenis librum, quem iterum êditurum polliceris, hoc anno lecturum. Significavit mihi saepe dictus studiosus abs te desiderari Verneri librum de motu octavae Sphaerae, quem cum totâ Germaniâ conquisieris, nunquam tamen invenire potueris. Quare unum ego ipsi exemplar dedi, ut id ad te meo nomine transmitteret. Si quid erit aliud, quod tuâ caussâ efficere possim, perhonorificum ducam, ut a te mihi imperetur, cui velim omnia feliciter evenire, atque optime esse consultum. Hoc anno publice interpretaturus sum meas Coelestium Orbium hypotheses: quae mihi bona erit occasio, quae in hoc genere a me êdita sunt, longe uberius explicare (ut jam facere aggressus sum) et commentariis ac Geometricis demonstrationibus, supputationibusque illustrare. Quae tamen commentaria priusquam publici juris efficiantur; libenter ego lucubrationes tuas cognoscere, et intelligere velim, ut tuas quoque suppositiones iis inserere et explicare possim. Ac ne tibi prolixitate nimiâ sim molestior, hîc faciam scribendi finem;te rogans iterum atque iterum, ut meam in te observantiam benevolo excipias animo, bonique consulas.</p><p>Bononiae Idib. Septemb. 1590.<br>Illustri dominationi tuae Addictissimus<br>JO. ANTON. MAGINUS PATAVINUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A COPY OF A LETTER OF THE MOST RENOWNED AND MOST EXCELLENT MATHEMATICIAN MASTER GIOVANNI ANTONIO MAGINI OF PADUA, WRITTEN FROM BOLOGNA IN THE SAME YEAR 1590 TO TYCHO BRAHE IN DENMARK.</b><br>ILLUSTRIOUS SIR,</p><p>I have received your most learned book, sent to me in your name by a certain Danish student who was once a member of your household; than which nothing more welcome could have been presented to me from you, since I am wont to seek out writings of this kind most eagerly and to read them through most gladly. For I should scarcely have hoped, by any other route, that it would come into my hands, since very few volumes printed there [in Denmark] are usually imported into Italy, on account of the great length of the journey and the difficulty of carriage. To this is added that, by this most elegant gift, you have plainly signified how much you esteem me — on which account, indeed, I give you the greatest thanks I can. For that which I especially desire and am wont to pursue — namely, that there should lie open to me an approach to the favour of men like you — this you, with that singular kindness which is yours, have offered me unasked. That I was not able at once, by letter, to signify this disposition of mine toward you, as was fitting and as I wished — the cause of this was that I had resolved to send to you, together with my letter, a Work lately worked out by me [and] shortly to be published. But last summer, when I had fallen into an illness, I dealt carefully with the same student, by a letter written to him, [asking] that he should not be reluctant to seek from you, in my name, the things I shall here set down. I should therefore most greatly wish to have a compendious description of the fixed stars, as they have been emended by you, so that it may be of use to me in my Ephemerides, which are shortly to be recalled to the press; and besides this, some knowledge of the eccentricities of the planets, and likewise of the proportions of the orb of each of them, if you have found them to differ from the Copernican [values], and have corrected them. And if you will not disdain to oblige me, I shall assuredly bring it about that you do not regret your kindness toward me: for it is my fixed resolve, with that candour which befits men of letters and Professors of the liberal arts, to praise you in my writings as the author of your own discoveries. As for my opinion and judgement concerning that celebrated work of yours upon the Comet seen in the year 1577 — do not yearn for it [in vain]; have it thus, most renowned Sir. For since I have perceived your incredible diligence in that [work] and your accurate method of observing, I persuade myself that it will come to pass that you will be able to emend the motions of the heavenly bodies most exactly, and that by the fruit of that labour you will most abundantly satisfy the keenest zeal and expectation of all — whence an everlasting glory will be added to your name. Yet I could wish that you would apply yourself above all to observing and examining the motion of Mars, since it is commonly believed that it cannot be observed exactly; and I myself am of this heresy, that I think its eccentricity to be altered, and to have its own period, so that another equation of the eccentricity must be introduced for it; otherwise, if a table is made for the maximum eccentricity, it will not be able to serve the minimum, and conversely. From which we see that Copernicus (even though he detected that an eccentricity of this kind varies) did not for that reason compose tables that agree for every period, since the computed equations of Mars cannot in the least be fitted, for the [maximum] eccentricity observed by Copernicus of 5°51′30″, to the times of Ptolemy himself, in which he defined the eccentricity of Mars as 6 degrees. I cannot but greatly approve the System of the Universe devised by you, although I could wish that the Orb of the Sun and that of Mars by no means cut across each other. But if, as I have learned from that student, it has been observed by you that Mars at opposition [acronychal Mars] approaches nearer to the earth than the Sun, then an intersection of this kind must altogether be admitted. I am in great expectation of observations and speculations of this kind of yours, which I shall by no means blush either to approve or to follow — even though, in constructing Ephemerides and resolved tables, I have not deviated even a finger's breadth from the Copernican system and the Prutenic Tables. I hope too — what I vehemently desire — that I shall this year read your first book on the phenomena of the Aethereal world, which you promise to publish again. The often-mentioned student signified to me that Werner's book on the motion of the eighth Sphere is wanted by you, [a book] which, though you have searched for it through all Germany, you have nevertheless never been able to find. Wherefore I have given him a copy of it, that he might send it to you in my name. If there shall be anything else that I can accomplish on your behalf, I shall count it a great honour that it should be enjoined upon me by you — to whom I wish that all things may turn out happily, and that all may be most well provided for. This year I am to lecture publicly on my hypotheses of the Celestial Orbs: which will be a good occasion for me to explain far more amply the things I have published in this kind (as I have already begun to do), and to illustrate them with commentaries and geometrical demonstrations and computations. Yet, before these commentaries are made public, I should gladly wish to learn and understand your lucubrations, so that I may insert and explain your suppositions too among them. And lest I be too troublesome to you by excessive length, I shall here make an end of writing — begging you again and again to receive my regard for you with a kindly disposition, and to take it in good part.</p><p>At Bologna, on the Ides of September [13 September], 1590.<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious Lordship,<br>Giovanni Antonio Magini of Padua.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Johannes Antonius Maginus Patavinus</i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), the Paduan-born mathematician and astronomer who held the chair of mathematics at <b>Bologna</b> (from 1588, having beaten Galileo for the post). A prolific ephemerides-maker and cartographer; the <i>Patavinus</i> (\"of Padua\") marks his birthplace, not his then-residence.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> The letter survives because <b>Tycho himself printed it</b> in his <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598), under a chancery-style Latin rubric (<i>DESCRIPTUM LITERARUM … = \"A copy of a letter …\"</i>); reprinted by Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b><i>studioso quodam Dano … domesticus</b></i> — \"a certain Danish student … a member of your household\": an unnamed former Uraniborg famulus then in Italy who served as Magini's intermediary, carrying both the <i>Mechanica</i>-precursor gift-book to Magini and Werner's volume back toward Tycho. Identity not given in the letter; left unidentified rather than guessed.</li><li><b><i>librum tuum eruditissimum</b></i> — \"your most learned book\": the work Tycho had sent. Most plausibly the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588, the comet book), to which Magini refers again below as <i>primum tuum de mundi Aetherei phaenomenis librum</i>; not named outright here, so rendered generically.</li><li><b><i>nuper a me elucubratum Opus</b></i> — \"a Work lately worked out by me\": Magini's forthcoming volume, almost certainly his <i>Novae coelestium orbium theoricae</i> (Venice, 1589), the planetary-theory work he describes lecturing on below.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms</b> (controlled glossary §5.1): <i>compendiosam descriptionem stellarum fixarum</i> → \"a compendious description of the fixed stars\" (Tycho's emended star catalogue); <i>Ephaemerides</i> → <b>Ephemerides</b>; <i>Eccentricitates Planetarum</i> → \"eccentricities of the planets\"; <i>commensurationum … orbis</i> → \"proportions of the orb\"; <i>aequatio Eccentricitatis</i> → \"equation of the eccentricity\" (<i>aequatio</i> in the astronomical sense per §5.1); <i>tabulae Prutenicae</i> → <b>Prutenic Tables</b> (Reinhold's 1551 Copernican tables); <i>resolutae tabulae</i> → \"resolved tables.\"</li><li><b>The Mars eccentricity figures.</b> Magini cites Copernicus's Mars eccentricity as <i>partium 5.51'.30''</i> and Ptolemy's as <i>partium 6</i> — rendered <b>5°51′30″</b> and <b>6 degrees</b>. The figures are reproduced exactly as printed; <i>partium</i> (\"of parts/degrees\") is the period idiom for degrees. This is the crux of Magini's \"heresy\": that the Martian eccentricity is <i>not</i> constant but varies with its own period, so that one table cannot serve both maximum and minimum — the very problem Kepler would later resolve. Magini's claim is preserved as his own hedged conjecture (<i>in eâ sum haeresi, ut putem</i> = \"I am of this heresy, that I think\"); no silent sharpening. <b>[maximum] — editorial insertion (flagged).</b> In the clause <i>Martis aequationes supputatae ad Eccentricitatem a Copernico observatam partium 5.51'.30''</i> the Latin says only \"the eccentricity observed by Copernicus,\" not <i>maximam</i>; <i>maxima</i> appears earlier in the letter (<i>si tabula efficiatur pro maxima Eccentricitate</i>) but not here. Magini's argument — that one table fitted to the maximum value cannot serve the minimum — implies that the 5°51′30″ figure he attributes to Copernicus functions as the maximum, so \"[maximum]\" is a defensible reading; but because the word is not in this clause it is bracketed as an editorial insertion rather than presented as translation.</li><li><b><i>Systema Universi a te excogitatum</b></i> — \"the System of the Universe devised by you\": Tycho's <b>geo-heliocentric (Tychonic) system</b>. Magini approves it but objects to the intersection of the orbs of the Sun and Mars — <i>cuperem Solis Orbem ac Martis nequaquam sese intersecare</i> (\"I could wish the Orb of the Sun and of Mars by no means cut across each other\"). This is a real point of resistance: solid celestial orbs cannot interpenetrate, yet the Tychonic geometry forces the Mars-orb to cross the Sun's.</li><li><b><i>Martem acronycum</b></i> — \"acronychal Mars,\" i.e. Mars at <b>opposition</b> (rising as the Sun sets; Greek <i>akrónychos</i>, \"at nightfall\"). The observation that Mars at opposition comes nearer the earth than the Sun is precisely what compels the orb-intersection Magini dislikes; rendered \"Mars at opposition [acronychal Mars]\" to keep the technical term and gloss it.</li><li><b><i>Cometa anno 1577</b></i> — the <b>Great Comet of 1577</b>, the subject of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>; Tycho's parallax work on it placed it above the Moon, the same supralunary result that bears on the orb question.</li><li><b><i>Verneri librum de motu octavae Sphaerae</b></i> — <b>Johannes Werner's</b> <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (Nuremberg, 1522), on the motion of the eighth (fixed-star) sphere — trepidation theory. The corpus (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) confirms Tycho's avid, frustrated pursuit of this rare book; Magini's gift of a copy via the Danish student is the concrete favour returned for the <i>Mechanica</i>-precursor.</li><li><b><i>Coelestium Orbium hypotheses / theoricae</b></i> — Magini's own planetary \"hypotheses of the Celestial Orbs\" (the <i>Novae coelestium orbium theoricae</i>), which he says he will expound publicly this year with geometrical demonstrations.</li><li><b><i>saepe dictus studiosus</b></i> — \"the often-mentioned student\": <i>saepe dictus</i> is a fixed idiom (\"the oft-mentioned / aforesaid\"), referring to the Danish intermediary already named repeatedly in the letter (<i>studioso quodam Dano … eodem studioso … studioso isto</i>). The adverb <i>saepe</i> qualifies <i>dictus</i> (the student has been \"often mentioned\"), not the main verb <i>significavit</i>; rendered \"the often-mentioned student signified to me,\" not \"the student often signified to me.\"</li><li><b>Latin idiom.</b> <i>in eâ sum haeresi</i> — literally \"I am in that heresy/school of thought\"; the word <i>haeresis</i> here carries its neutral Greek sense (\"a chosen tenet, sect-opinion\"), with a self-deprecating wink, not the theological charge — rendered \"I am of this heresy\" to keep the flavour. <i>ne latum quidem unguem deflexi</i> — \"I have not deviated even a finger's breadth,\" a Ciceronian idiom (<i>latum unguem</i>, \"a nail's breadth\"), kept literal. <i>lucubrationes tuas</i> — \"your lucubrations\" (works composed by lamplight; scholarly labours), kept as the period term.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae</b> (§5.5). Opening <i>ILLUSTRIS VIR</i> → \"Illustrious Sir\"; subscription <i>Illustri dominationi tuae Addictissimus</i> → \"Most devoted to Your Illustrious Lordship\" (<i>dominatio tua</i>, \"Your Lordship,\" the courteous Italian-chancery form).</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline crux.</b> <i>Bononiae Idib. Septemb. 1590</i> = \"At Bologna, on the Ides of September, 1590.\" Magini, in Catholic Italy, wrote on the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> calendar, ten days ahead of the Julian reckoning Tycho kept on Hven; the Roman idiom is kept and glossed, and <i>not</i> converted to Tycho's Old-Style equivalent (which would be 3 September O.S.). Per protocol §3 the style is flagged, the date not \"corrected.\" <b>Label-vs-dateline discrepancy (flagged, not reconciled):</b> the dataset `label` catalogues the letter \"1590. 11. september n.s.,\" <b>two days earlier than the dateline the text itself bears</b> (the Ides = 13 September). Per protocol §4 the header therefore leads with the as-written date — 13 September [N.S.] — and appends the catalogued date (11 September); nothing in the source substantiates the 11th, which may reflect a separate cataloguing tradition or a slip. The discrepancy is recorded here and not silently resolved.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and the technical astronomy (Mars eccentricity variation, the orb-intersection objection, acronychal Mars, Werner's <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i>, the Prutenic Tables) is glossed against the controlled glossary and the corpus. Three points are flagged rather than smoothed: (i) the <b>dateline-vs-catalogue date discrepancy</b> — Ides of September = 13 September as written in the source vs. the catalogue's 11 September N.S. — recorded in the apparatus, with the header leading on the as-written date per §4; (ii) the identity of the unnamed Danish <i>studiosus</i> intermediary, left open; and (iii) the exact title of <i>librum tuum eruditissimum</i> / <i>nuper elucubratum Opus</i>, inferred (1588 comet book; <i>Novae theoricae</i> 1589) but not stated in the letter, so translated generically.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin letter (TBOO V pp.124-126, Magini to Tycho) word-for-word against Dreyer's page images, including the heading, body, and closing. Verified the two key numeric findings -- the Copernican and Ptolemaic Mars-eccentricity figures \"partium 5. 51'. 30''\" and \"partium 6\" -- and the date \"Bononiae Idib. Septemb. 1590\" (correctly glossed in the English as 13 September, the Ides). No defects found; the published grade of B undersold what is in fact a fully collated, clean translation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15901201003", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15901201003"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for sending his book via a Danish student, notes the rarity of Danish publications in Italy, and welcomes the ensuing opportunity for scholarly exchange.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15900911001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901000001", "d": "1590-10-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Gellius Sascerides", "ck": "Gellius Sascerides", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Gellius Sascerides — [no place named], 1 October 1590 [O.S., catalogued] (excerpt)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Hauniae, 1913–29) · MS witness: A = Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici — copy made by Gellius Sascerides within his letter to Giovanni Antonio Magini, 1 February 1591</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD GELLIUM SASCERIDEM.</p><p>... Quod librum meum IO. ANTONIUS MAGINUS perlegerit, eumque sibi et alijs eruditis eiusdem professionis viris placuisse testatus sit, grata mente accipio. Pollicitationem autem nostram emendandorum motuum perturbasse illum, equidem non miror, ex quo tam ipse, quam nonnulli alij, hactenus tot tantosque labores (ubi nemo illo maiores, excepto COPERNICO et Reinholdino fundamento, quod tamen multimode locupletavit) in calculandis Tabulis coelestium motuum et Ephemeridibus, exantlârunt; cum nihilominus coelum ipsum non modo in minutijs, sed etiam in gradibus aliquot deviationem a Tabulis saepenumero ostendat. Hac in Sole quidem ipso caeterorum Planetarum chorago, circa Aequinoctium vernum, paulo plus dimidio gradu anticipando aberrant; ut nihil nunc dicam de caeteris Planetis, in quorum nullo non admodum evidens Coeli et Tabularum disconvenientia invenitur, ut suo tempore ex Observationum mearum acervo ob oculos ponam. Quid in aethereorum corporum Revolutionibus, quo ad numerorum denotationem ipsi coelo correspondentem, favente ipso coelestium Opifice, praestiterim, perspiciet doctissimus ille vir, quando opus nostrum Astronomiae instaurationis, quod multo iam tempore molimur, publici iuris factum fuerit. Illic etiam in secunda videlicet eius voluminis parte, Planetarum quinque gyrationes, cum eorum trium qui Solem ambientes Terram simul includunt, tum etiam reliquorum duorum, qui circa eumdem revoluti hanc non includunt, quo ad omnes singulorum proportiones, et particulares etiam inaequalitatum apparentium excusationes atque demonstrationes, luculenter cognoscet; et unâ circa Eccentricitates atque Apogaea, prout communiter appellantur (quae tamen ego secundum propriam Hypothesin aliter ordino atque tueor) unâ cum Orbium omnium symmetria atque commensuratione, voti compos reddetur. Sed ipse in suis recentioribus Theoricis, quas numeris Coperniceis rectius et competentius, quam antea in tabulis a se editis Astronomicis, applicuit, dum realitatem et soliditatem ipsorum Orbium, Aristotelea et veterum quorumdam imitatione, approbat (quae tamen revera nulla est) id pluribus conatur exequi, quod alias paucioribus requisitis possit praestari. Quin et alia quaedam inaequalitatis particularis insinuatio, quo ad quinque errantium siderum apparentias, adhuc in occulto subest, quam nec veteres Ptolemaicae, nec recentiores Coperniceae quocumque modo usurpatae excusant Hypotheses. Hanc tamen non minus quam caeteras (quae ab Epicyclis vel motu annuo Terrae cum commixtis Eccentricis et Aequantibus proveniunt) favente Numine, nostrae concinniore, uti spero, ratiocinatione expedient Hypotheses. Quantum ad obliquitatis Ecclipticae (de qua etiam certior fieri avet) mutationem attinet, ego eam hisce temporibus esse partium 23. M. 31½ exacte, pluribus et maximis, ijsdemque affabre e solido metallo elaboratis Instrumentis aliquot annorum reiteratis Observationibus, sedulo adinveni, nec sensibiliter minorem hactenus umquam fuisse existimo. Nam quod COPERNICUS et quidam eius Synchroni eam 3½ scrupulis angustiorem reddiderunt, id evenit, quod Refractionem ☉lis circa Brumale Solstitium, non uti oportuit, praecaverint; ut de Instrumentorum, quibus confisi sunt, in tam subtili rimatione, insufsicientia nihil dicam. Quin neque Poli altitudinem ex Sole per utrumque Solstitium, eandem ob causam, rite scrutati sunt; adeo ut cum ipsemet Fruenburgi, in loco habitationis Coperniceae, per proprium Instrumentum periculum de altitudine Polari accurate (uti scis) fieri curassem, deprehensum sit, ipsum 3¼ Minutis eamdem iusto minorem consignasse; quod non parum illi erroris in Apogaeo et Eccentricitate Solari, unâ cum toto eius curriculo competenter denotando invexit, coincidentibus et alijs quibusdam deviationum occasionibus, quas alibi a me reseratas nunc praetereo. An autem summa Eclipticae obliquitas in posterum aliquatenus accrescet vel coarctabitur, non adeo promptum est decernere. Nec enim Hypothesis Copernicea (utut admodum ingeniosa) circa Aequinoctium et huius obliquitatis mutationem, locum meretur. Verisimilius tamen duco, hanc Eclipticae ab Aequatore digressionem, subsequentibus saeculis paululum dilatatum iri. Quemadmodum Eccentricitatem Solis nunc etiam pedetentim augeri, non autem circa citimum decrementum, ut fert Copernicea ratiocinatio, versari, haud dubijs inditijs compertum habeo. Nec adeo mirum est, COPERNICUM hac in parte deceptum fuisse, cum ipsam quoque sui temporis Solis Eccentricitatem, unâ cum Apogaeo, minus dextre scrutatus sit, ob causas alio loco (ut dixi) a me manifestatas. Est enim circa haec tempora Eccentricitas Solis vera et indubia part. 2. Min. 9, de 60 totius Semidiametri Eccentrici 13 proxime scrupulis maior COPERNICI opinione; et Apogaeum versatur hoc anno paulo post medietatem sexti gradus ♋ ipsius numeratione 3½ Gradibus anterius (velut etiam in reliquorum Planetarum Apogaeis disponendis non paucis gradibus ab eo peccatum est, ut Eccentricitates horum nunc praeteream) quemadmodum idipsum exquisitis observationibus et certis demonstrationibus a me esse adinventum, ipsemet satis perspectum habes; prout etiam caput primum anterioris nostri Tomi recentiorum Coeli Phaenomenωn, haec et alia ad Solaris cursus accuratam restitutionem facientia, affatim patefacit atque demonstrat. Illic brevi MAGINUS ea quae in Solaris tramitis restitutione hactenus desiderantur extricata reperiet, quibus etiam postmodum in suarum Ephemeridum recognitione tuto, absque iniuria coelestium apparentiarum, uti poterit. De declinatione Zodiaci maxima, an unquam maior fuerit, quam a PTOLEMAeO et eius aliquot antecessoribus assignata est, etsi nonnihil certis de causis ambigam, malo tamen eorum denotationi acquiescere, nec sensibiliter maiorem antea fuisse statuere. An tamen in ipsis Minutijs illorum observatio rite constet, tam Instrumentorum, quibus usi sunt, in minimis animadversionibus fallacia, quam Parallaxeos atque refractionis Solaris neglectus, me non parum ancipitem reddunt. Affixarum Stellarum iuxta nostras diuturnas et sedulas observationes restitutarum Catalogum, quem unâ expetit MAGINUS, ex secundo capite eiusdem primi libri obtinebit. Illic enim circiter 500 Stellae in ipso minuto, et quo ad latitudinem et longitudinem proprijs Instrumentis desuper acceptae rectificantur, praemissa fundamentali restitutionis earumdem ratione ac Geometrica demonstratione. Consultius itaque erit, ut suarum Ephemeridum ad praelum revocationem eousque differat, donec hunc nostrum primum tomum nactus fuerit, quod spero instanti aestate futurum. Est enim iam ad finem Typographico labore ferme perductus, deficiente saltem decimo et ultimo capite, idque ob papyri impressoriae penuriam, quam tamen sufficienti copia brevi me habiturum arbitror, ex propria mea, quam in hac insula eius conficiendae gratia magnis impensis iam nuper construi curavi, moletrina, quae iam prorsus parata Chartopaeum idoneum, pro quo conquirendo meum in Germaniam amandavi Typographum, solummodo expectat. Rationem Trianguli non rectanguli, ex cognitis duobus lateribus cum angulo comprehenso, reliqua absque perpendiculari compendiosius quam communiter sit, venandi, ex quarto meo Dogmate Triangulorum planorum illi aperire poteris, uti ei satisfiat. Nec mihi displicet, quod illi Dogmatis sexti et noni Compendiorum nostrorum operationem patefeceris; siquidem, uti video, demonstrationes horum et similium ab alijs, qui se alienis ornant plumis, nunc divulgentur. Res enim ita se habet, prout refers, quod Plagiarius iste Ursinus (qui etiam mearum hypothesium inventionem pro sua inverecunde publicavit, utut eam fundamentaliter non intelligat, quod satis liquet ex Martis orbe falso ab eo ordinato, ut de caeteris nunc taceam) eas, quas pro suis venditavit, Triangulorum demonstrationes et compendiosas in numeros resolutiones, apud IUSTUM BYRGIUM Landgravianum Automatopaeum, qui eas a WITICHIO impetravit, nactus sit. Cum WITICHIO autem, quando hîc ante tuum ad me adventum aliquandiu commoratus est, de his et alijs ad rem Mathematicam facilius excolendam, liberaliter contuleram, qui hinc discedens postea apud Illustrissimum Principem Landgravium Hassiae pleraque eorum unâ cum quorundam Instrumentorum ex meis concinna fabrica, propalavit, quae nunc alij perfrictae frontis homines pro suis inventionibus sibi vendicare non erubescunt, adeo ut nulla fere ad primos inventores redundet gratia. Verumenimvero quaecumque de Triangulorum tam planorum quam sphaericorum, quatenus ad rem Astronomicam extruendam stabiliendamque conducunt, compendiosa tractatione hactenus comperta habeo, ea omnia in primam partem Astronomiae redintegrandae, tam secundum demonstrationes Geometricas, quam numerorum praxim, sufficienter digeram. Illinc MAGINUS atque alij varias easdemque faciles et abbreviatas Triangulorum explanandorum et in numeros resolvendorum inventiones percipient. Quae hactenus dicta sunt Clarissimi et doctissimi MAGINI ad te perscriptam Epistolam eiusque in ea ad me directas petitiones spectantia, poteris ad ipsum prima quaque occasione, responsionis loco per literas referre, aut etiam, si lubet, hanc Epistolae partem descriptam transmittere. Si ipsemet ad me aliquid literarum dederit, aut lucubrationum suarum miserit, ulterius cum illo lubens conferam, et de pluribus, sicubi opus fuerit, commonefaciam, atque ipsius desiderijs non gravatim obviabo ...</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO GELLIUS SASCERIDES.</b></p><p>… That Giovanni Antonio Magini has read my book through, and has testified that it pleased him and other learned men of the same profession, I receive with a grateful mind. That my promise to amend the [planetary] motions has unsettled him, for my part I do not wonder, since both he himself and several others have hitherto drained off so many and so great labours (in which no one [accomplished] more than he, save Copernicus and the Reinholdian foundation, which he nonetheless enriched in manifold ways) in computing Tables of the celestial motions and Ephemerides — and yet the heaven itself often shows a deviation from the Tables not only in minutes but even in several degrees. In the Sun itself, indeed — the choragus [leader of the dance] of the other planets — they err about the vernal equinox by anticipating [running ahead] a little more than half a degree; to say nothing now of the other planets, in none of which is there not found a very evident disagreement of Heaven and the Tables, as in due time I shall set before [men's] eyes from the heap of my observations. What I have achieved, by the favour of the celestial Maker himself, in the Revolutions of the ethereal bodies — as regards a numerical reckoning corresponding to the heaven itself — that most learned man will discern when our work of the <i>restoration of astronomy</i> [<i>Astronomiae instauratio</i>], which we have long been labouring at, shall have been made public. There too, namely in the second part of that volume, he will clearly come to know the gyrations of the five planets — both of those three which, encircling the Sun, at the same time enclose the Earth, and of the remaining two which, revolved about the same [Sun], do not enclose it — as to all the proportions of each, and the particular accountings and demonstrations also of the apparent inequalities; and, together with the symmetry and commensuration of all the orbs, he will, in one [exposition], regarding the eccentricities and apogees as they are commonly called (which, however, I arrange and maintain otherwise, according to my own hypothesis), be granted his wish. But he himself, in his more recent <i>Theorics</i>, which he has fitted to the Copernican numbers more rightly and aptly than before in the astronomical tables he had published, while he approves — in imitation of Aristotle and certain of the ancients — the reality and solidity of the orbs themselves (which in truth is none), attempts to carry out with more [machinery] what could otherwise be accomplished with fewer requisites. Moreover, a certain other intimation of particular inequality, as regards the appearances of the five wandering stars, still lies hidden in concealment, which neither the old Ptolemaic nor the more recent Copernican hypotheses, employed in any manner whatever, account for. Yet this one, no less than the others (which arise from epicycles, or from the annual motion of the Earth combined with eccentrics and equants), our hypotheses will, by the favour of the Deity, dispatch by a neater reasoning, as I hope. As regards the change of the obliquity of the ecliptic (about which too he is eager to be made more certain), I have for my part found out diligently — by repeated observations of several years, with many and very large instruments, skilfully wrought of solid metal — that in these times it is exactly 23 [degrees], 31½ minutes, and I judge that it has never hitherto been perceptibly smaller. For as to the fact that Copernicus and certain of his contemporaries made it narrower by 3½ scruples [minutes], that came about because they did not, as they ought, guard against the refraction of the ☉ Sun about the winter solstice; to say nothing of the insufficiency of the instruments they relied on in so subtle a probing. Nor, for the same reason, did they duly investigate the altitude of the Pole from the Sun through both solstices; so that, when I myself had had a careful trial of the polar altitude made at Frauenburg, in the place of Copernicus's dwelling, by my own instrument (as you know), it was discovered that he had set the same down 3¼ minutes smaller than is right — which brought upon him no small error in determining the [solar] apogee and the solar eccentricity, together with its whole course, properly, other occasions of deviation coinciding too, which, disclosed by me elsewhere, I now pass over. But whether the maximum obliquity of the ecliptic will in time hereafter increase or be contracted to some extent, is not so easy to decide. For the Copernican hypothesis (however highly ingenious) concerning the equinox and the change of this obliquity does not deserve [its] place. Yet I judge it more probable that this departure of the ecliptic from the equator will, in succeeding centuries, be widened a little. Just as I hold it ascertained by no doubtful tokens that the eccentricity of the Sun is now likewise being gradually increased, and is not engaged about a nearest decrement, as the Copernican reasoning would have it. Nor is it so much to be wondered at that Copernicus was deceived in this part, since he also investigated less skilfully the eccentricity of the Sun of his own time, together with the apogee, for the reasons disclosed by me (as I said) in another place. For about these times the true and undoubted eccentricity of the Sun is 2 parts, 9 minutes — of the 60 of the whole semidiameter of the eccentric — about 13 scruples greater than Copernicus's opinion; and the apogee this year stands a little past the middle of the sixth degree of ♋ Cancer, 3½ degrees earlier than his reckoning (just as in disposing the apogees of the other planets too he erred from [the truth] by not a few degrees, to pass over for now the eccentricities of these) — exactly as you yourself have well enough perceived that this very thing was found out by me through exquisite observations and sure demonstrations; just as the first chapter of the earlier Tome of our recent <i>Phenomena of the Heaven</i> [<i>Coeli Phaenomena</i>] also abundantly sets forth and demonstrates these and other matters bearing on the accurate restoration of the Sun's course. There Magini will shortly find disentangled those things which have hitherto been wanting in the restoration of the solar track, which afterwards he will be able to use safely, without injury to the celestial appearances, in the revision of his Ephemerides. As to the maximum declination of the zodiac — whether it was ever greater than was assigned by Ptolemy and several of his predecessors — although for certain reasons I am somewhat in doubt, I yet prefer to acquiesce in their determination, and to hold that it was not perceptibly greater before. Whether, however, their observation rightly holds good in the very minutes [of arc], both the fallibility of the instruments they used in the smallest discernments, and their neglect of parallax and of solar refraction, render me not a little of two minds. The Catalogue of the fixed stars, restored according to our long and diligent observations — which Magini desires together [with the rest] — he will obtain from the second chapter of the same first book. For there about 500 stars, taken from above with proper instruments as to both latitude and longitude, are rectified to the very minute, the fundamental method of their restoration and the geometrical demonstration being set out beforehand. It will therefore be more advisable that he defer the recalling of his Ephemerides to the press until he shall have obtained this first tome of ours, which I hope will be in the coming summer. For it has now been almost carried through to its end by the labour of the press, only the tenth and last chapter being wanting, and that on account of a scarcity of printing-paper — which, however, I think I shall shortly have in sufficient supply from my own paper-mill, which I lately had built at great expense on this island for the making of it, and which, now wholly ready, awaits only a suitable papermaker, to procure whom I have sent off my printer into Germany. The method of hunting out, for a non-right-angled triangle, from two known sides with the included angle, the remaining [parts] without [dropping] a perpendicular, more concisely than is commonly done, you will be able to disclose to him from my fourth Theorem of plane triangles, so that he may be satisfied. Nor does it displease me that you have disclosed to him the working of the sixth and ninth Theorems of our Compendia; since, as I see, the demonstrations of these and the like are now being divulged by others, who deck themselves in another's feathers. For the matter stands thus, just as you report — that that plagiarist Ursinus (who has also shamelessly published the invention of my hypotheses as his own, although he does not fundamentally understand it, which is clear enough from the orb of Mars falsely arranged by him, to say nothing now of the rest) obtained those demonstrations of triangles, and concise resolutions into numbers, which he peddled as his own, from Justus Byrgius [Jost Bürgi], the Landgrave's automaton-maker [clockmaker], who got them from Wittich. Now with Wittich, when he stayed here for some while before your coming to me, I had liberally conferred about these and other matters tending to the easier cultivation of the mathematical art; and he, departing hence, afterwards made most of them public at the court of the Most Illustrious Prince, the Landgrave of Hesse, together with the neat construction of certain instruments [taken] from mine — which now other men of brazen brow [shameless men] do not blush to claim for themselves as their own inventions, so that scarcely any thanks redound to the first discoverers. But truly, whatever I have hitherto found out, by concise treatment, concerning triangles both plane and spherical — in so far as they conduce to building up and establishing the science of astronomy — all of it I shall set out sufficiently in the first part of [my] <i>Astronomy Restored</i>, both according to geometrical demonstrations and to the practice of numbers. Thence Magini and others will gather various, and those easy and abbreviated, methods of explaining triangles and resolving them into numbers. The things that have been said thus far, bearing on the letter written out to you by the most renowned and learned Magini and his requests directed in it to me, you will be able to relate back to him by letter at the very first occasion, in the place of a reply; or also, if you please, to transmit this part of the letter copied out. If he himself shall have sent me any letter, or any of his lucubrations [scholarly works], I shall gladly confer with him further, and shall advise him about more matters wherever there shall be need, and shall not grudgingly meet his desires …</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> writing from Uraniborg (no place is named in the excerpt) to <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Gellius Sascerides / Gjellis Saksesen, 1562–1612), the Danish physician-astronomer who had studied on Hven, travelled to Italy in 1589–90, and acted there as Tycho's intermediary with the Italian astronomical community — chiefly <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> of Bologna. (His later betrothal to Tycho's daughter Magdalene and its collapse is the famous \"Gellius affair,\" not at issue here.)</li><li><b>Excerpt status.</b> The text opens and closes with ellipses (<i>…</i>) in the edition: this is an extract, not the whole letter. No salutation, dateline, or subscription is transmitted in this portion.</li><li><b>Person identifications (first occurrence).</b> <i>Io. Antonius Maginus</i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of mathematics at Bologna, author of widely-used planetary tables and ephemerides; <i>Copernicus</i> = Nicolaus Copernicus; <i>Reinholdino fundamento</i> = the work of <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>, i.e. the <i>Prutenic Tables</i> (1551), the Copernican-numbered tables on which Magini's own were founded; <i>Ptolemaeus</i> = Ptolemy; <i>Ursinus / Plagiarius iste Ursinus</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (Nicolai Reimers Bär, \"Ursus,\" d. 1600), the Dithmarschen autodidact and imperial mathematician whom Tycho accused of stealing his geoheliocentric world-system (the priority dispute that runs through the late letters); <i>Witichio</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) of Wrocław, who visited Hven in 1580 and later Kassel; <i>Iustus Byrgius Landgravianus Automatopaeus</i> = <b>Jost Bürgi</b> (1552–1632), the Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel's celebrated clockmaker and instrument-/mechanism-maker; <i>Illustrissimus Princeps Landgravius Hassiae</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b>Place identifications.</b> <i>Fruenburgi</i> = <b>Frauenburg</b> (Frombork, Ermland/Warmia), Copernicus's residence, where Tycho had a measurement of the polar altitude taken (referring to the 1584 mission of his assistant Elias Olsen Morsing); <i>hac insula</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b>; <i>in Germaniam</i> = Germany.</li><li><b>The world-system passage.</b> <i>Planetarum quinque … trium qui Solem ambientes Terram simul includunt … reliquorum duorum, qui circa eumdem revoluti hanc non includunt</i> — a precise statement of Tycho's geoheliocentric model: the three superior planets (Saturn, Jupiter, Mars), whose solar orbits enclose the Earth, and the two inferior (Venus, Mercury), whose solar orbits do not. <i>Hypothesis</i> / <i>Hypotheses</i> kept (rendered \"hypothesis/hypotheses\"); Tycho's emphatic <i>secundum propriam Hypothesin</i> underlies the priority claim against Ursus. <i>realitatem et soliditatem ipsorum Orbium … quae tamen revera nulla est</i> — Tycho's rejection of solid celestial orbs (the comet-derived doctrine), set against Magini's Aristotelian retention of them; the hedge \"in truth is none\" is Tycho's and is preserved.</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary §5.1).</b> <i>motus</i> → motion; <i>Tabulae / Ephemerides</i> → Tables / Ephemerides; <i>Revolutiones</i> → Revolutions; <i>Eccentricitas / Eccentricus</i> → eccentricity / eccentric; <i>Apogaeum</i> → apogee; <i>Aequans</i> → equant; <i>Epicyclus</i> → epicycle; <i>Orbis / Sphaera</i> → orb / sphere; <i>Aequinoctium vernum</i> → vernal equinox; <i>Brumale Solstitium</i> → winter solstice; <i>Parallaxis</i> → parallax; <i>Refractio</i> → refraction; <i>obliquitas Eclipticae</i> / <i>declinatio Zodiaci maxima</i> → obliquity of the ecliptic / maximum declination of the zodiac; <i>Poli altitudo</i> → altitude of the pole; <i>restitutio / instauratio Astronomiae</i> → restoration / renewal of astronomy. <i>Astronomiae instaurationis opus</i> / <i>Astronomiae redintegrandae</i> refer to Tycho's projected magnum opus (the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>, then in press on Hven, which the letter describes as nearly finished but lacking its tenth chapter for want of paper).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> One Greek-derived term, <i>Phaenomenωn</i>, is printed with a Greek omega (ω) for the genitive ending in the edition — i.e. <i>Coeli Phaenomenōn</i>, \"of the Phenomena of the Heaven\"; the printed mixed-script form is retained in the Source block. <i>Chorago</i> (<i>choragus</i>, χορηγός) \"leader of the chorus/dance,\" used metaphorically of the Sun as ruler of the planetary dance, is glossed in brackets.</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> <i>☉lis</i> = a contraction of <i>Solis</i> (genitive of <i>Sol</i>) using the Sun glyph ☉ + the ending <i>-lis</i>; rendered \"of the ☉ Sun,\" glyph kept in the Source block. <i>♋</i> = the sign Cancer (named \"Cancer,\" glyph kept), per protocol §5.3.</li><li><b>Numerical values (verbatim, not modernised).</b> Obliquity given as <i>partium 23. M. 31½</i> = \"23 [degrees], 31½ minutes\"; Copernicus's value <i>3½ scrupulis angustiorem</i> = \"narrower by 3½ scruples [minutes of arc]\" (<i>scrupulum</i> = minute of arc here); Frauenburg polar-altitude error <i>3¼ Minutis … iusto minorem</i>; solar eccentricity <i>part. 2. Min. 9, de 60 totius Semidiametri Eccentrici</i> = \"2 parts 9 minutes, of the 60 of the whole semidiameter of the eccentric\" (i.e. eccentricity expressed in sixtieths of the eccentric's radius), <i>13 proxime scrupulis maior Copernici opinione</i>; apogee <i>paulo post medietatem sexti gradus ♋ … 3½ Gradibus anterius</i>. These figures are reproduced exactly; no recomputation or correction is offered.</li><li><b>The plagiarism transmission-chain.</b> Tycho's account: his triangle-theorems and <i>prosthaphaeresis</i>-style \"concise resolutions into numbers\" (compendious computational devices) passed Hven → Wittich → Bürgi at Kassel → Ursus, who published them (and Tycho's world-system) as his own. <i>qui se alienis ornant plumis</i> (\"who deck themselves in another's feathers\") and <i>perfrictae frontis homines</i> (\"men of brazen brow\") are Tycho's invective and are kept as such. The historical corpus (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>) corroborates this transmission and the Wittich/Bürgi link, and records that Ursus, when accused, named Wittich and Bürgi as his source. Tycho's <i>Dogmata</i> (<i>Dogma quartum / sextum / nonum</i>) of plane triangles are rendered \"Theorem(s)\"; <i>Compendia</i> \"Compendia.\"</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The passage turns on several load-bearing negatives, reproduced one-for-one: <i>non miror</i> (\"I do not wonder\"); <i>non modo … sed etiam</i> (\"not only … but even\"); <i>in quorum nullo non … invenitur</i> (double negative, \"in none of which is there not found\" = in every one there is found); <i>quae tamen revera nulla est</i> (\"which in truth is none\"); <i>non autem circa citimum decrementum</i> (\"and is not engaged about a nearest decrement\"); <i>non erubescunt</i> (\"do not blush\"); <i>non gravatim</i> (\"not grudgingly\"). The double negative <i>nullo non</i> is intentional litotes and is kept as such rather than flattened.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating.</b> No dateline survives in the excerpt. The catalogue date is <b>1 October 1590</b> (label <i>\"1590 1. oktober\"</i>; Dreyer dates it October 1590, O.S.). Because the source text carries no dateline of its own, the header gives the catalogued date marked \"[O.S., catalogued]\" rather than asserting an as-written date the text does not substantiate. The witness is a <b>copy</b> Gellius Sascerides incorporated into his own letter to Magini of <b>1 February 1591</b> (Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici) — consistent with an autumn-1590 original that Gellius forwarded onward, as the closing instruction (\"transmit this part of the letter copied out\") anticipates. Denmark used Old Style throughout; Magini in Bologna used New Style — a gap relevant to the courier chronology but not to any in-text date here.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'Plagiarius iste Ursinus' (Dreyer, TBOO VII p.281): the epithet 'Ursinus' restored in the English (was flattened to the bare name 'Ursus'), preserving Tycho's derisive pun on Ursus's bear-name.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical, astronomical, and prosopographical content is corroborated against the scholarly corpus. The single soft point is the <b>dateline</b>: the excerpt transmits no internal date, so \"1 October 1590\" rests on the catalogue/edition assignment, flagged above rather than asserted as the text's own; the precise force of <i>citimum decrementum</i> (rendered \"nearest decrement,\" i.e. approach to a minimum) in the solar-eccentricity sentence is the one lexical point kept literal rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full letter's Dreyer page images visually in two chunks (pages 1-4, then 5-6 of the slice PDF; TBOO VII pp.278-283, of which 278-282 belong to this letter). Checked the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date and sum: obliquity 23 deg 31.5 min, Copernicus's error 3.5 scrupuli and 3.25 minutes at Frauenburg, solar eccentricity 2 part. 9 min. of 60 (13 scrupuli greater than Copernicus), apogee 3.5 degrees, ~500 stars, triangle theorems 4/6/9, and the names Magini, Reinhold, Byrgius/Bürgi, Witichio/Wittich all confirmed exact against the page images. Only defect found is the low-severity Ursinus/Ursus epithet softening noted above; nothing rises to fabrication, omission, or a sense-changing mistranslation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Dreyer prints the adjectival epithet 'Ursinus' (a derisive pun on Nicolaus Reimers Bär/Ursus's bear-name), which the English flattens into the bare proper name 'Ursus' — the correct historical referent is preserved but the wordplay/exact printed form is not. At: “that plagiarist Ursus”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified directly against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VII pp.278-281, read as page images, not the OCR text layer): p.281 confirms 'Plagiarius ifte Urfinus' exactly as the audit cites. Only this clause was touched; the Latin was already correct ('Ursinus'), so only the English required correction. No other cruces surfaced on the pages read.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici. Afskrift af Gellius Sascerides i et brev til Giovanni Antonio Magini, 1. februar 1591"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Notes Magini's approval of his book, discusses his promise to amend planetary tables, and cites a half-degree tabular error in the Sun at the vernal equinox.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901001001", "d": "1590-10-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 1 October 1590 [O.S.] (catalogued 1 October 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO WILHELMO, LANDGRAVIO HASSIAe, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO CLEMENTISSIMO. S. D.</p><p>AVENIT iam nuper huc in Daniam ad me T. Celsit. Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS, Princeps Illustrissime, tuaeque Celsit. literas attulit, quibus adeo diutinam in scribendo ad me intermissionem tum ob curarum molem, tum etiam Mathematici aduersam valetudinem, benigne exponit. Quod vero studium Mathematicum T. Celsit. ob tot tantaque administrationis publicae, senij etiam successiue obrepentis impedimenta, non prorsus repudiet, sed quantum per ocium licuerit, etiamnum continuet, mihi quam iucundum auditu est. Attamen, quod pari alacritate, quâ antea, priora inuena nunc recognoscere, tractareque vix promtum sit, mirum esse non debet; cum anni, curae et morbi, vna cum corporeae sanitatis molestatione, ipsum quoque animum atque ingenij vires, potiorem nostrî partem, non parum aggrauent atque obnubilent. Verum enimuero quicquid in hoc genere T. Celsit. propriâ operâ adeo sufficienter atque copiose, vt prius, ob causas antedictas, in posterum praestare vix suffecerit, id per alios idoneos artifices sua autoritate atque hortatu, tum etiam sumtibus et liberalitate, compensare facile sustinebit.</p><p>Intelligo ROTHMANNVM valetudinis causâ aërem mutasse, quod licet T. Celsit. atque alijs minus tutum atque consultum ab initio videbatur, tamen quantum ex ipsiusmet relatione cognosco, illi non infeliciter cessit. Nam statim ab itineris ingressu successiue vires recuperauit, et interea dum hîc moraretur, non admodum infirmo vsus est habitu. Quamuis resolutiones et coagulationes istae Tartareae, quibus intrinsecus infestatur, adeo alte etiam in hac iuuenili aetate radices egerint, vt non tam facile per solam loci mutationem, atque victus rationem, prorsus exstirpari queant, cum et Galenistarum, vtut satis verbose et affectate de his et similibus disserentium medicationes quasi ex alto despiciant. Neque enim citra rem aiebat incomparabilis ille Germanorum Philosophus et Medicus, a quamplurimis citius improbatus quam intellectus, Aureolus Theophrastus, Tartaream Roades nequeunt curare Podagram, per Roades (suo more ludens in vocabulis) rudes et inexpertos Medicos intelligens. Excellentiora sane et praesentiora hîc requiruntur Medicamenta, quam Syrupi, atque Decocta, Pillulaeque aut similes Seplasiariorum impurae et confusae mixtiones suppeditare queant. Sed de his, cum alterius sint negotij, plura non addam.</p><p>Fuit autem ipsius ROTHMANNI accessus mihi inprimis gratus, tam quod a T. Celsit. mihi commendabatur, quam quod ipsum ob egregiam et penitiorem Artis Astronomicae et rerum Mathematicarum cognitionem plurimi semper fecerim. Inspexit ille omnia mea Instrumenta Astronomica, de quibus ipsemet ad T. Celsit. referet. De Globo quoque Orichalcico quadrupedali, vel potius ampliori in diametro, quem Automatopaeus T. Cel. iam prae manibus habet, me certiorem fecit. At vereor, vt sola tornatione eum ad exactam rotunditatem vndiquaque perducat. Id quod meus, qui maior adhuc est, vtpote sexpedalis, me satis superque docuit. Sed ipsa experientia in his et similibus optima erit Magistra.</p><p>Quantum ad Statum Regni nostri attinet, omnia adhuc, diuino fauore, tuta et pacata sunt. Serenissimus Rex noster electus prospere degit, et cum Regni Primatibus nunc in Cymbria comitia peragit, quae tamen hisce diebus finiuntur. Ego meo more studijs indulgeo Philosophicis, quantum per alias occupationes atque auocamenta licet. Liber noster primus recentiorum coeli Phaenomenon fere extremam Typographi manum subijt, sed Papyri impressoriae defectus remoram aliquam, quo minus prorsus absolutus sit, iniecit. Licet enim ipsemet in hac Insula Moletrinam pro Papyro conficienda non paruis sumtibus extrui curârim, tamen cum Chartopaeo idoneo destituam nihil adhuc subsidij, quantum ad Papyri praeparationem, hinc obtinere licuit. Spero tamen me breui e vicina Germania eiuscemodi artificem obtenturum, qui per instantem Hyemem, ea quae desiderantur, confecturus sit; sicque in libro illo deficientia supplere commodum erit.</p><p>Scripsi circa initium praeteriti Aprilis tuae Celsitudini, de Cometâ huius Anni, qui paulo ante illuxerat, vnaque eius succinctam descriptionem e nostris Obseruationibus deriuatam transmisi, prout per literas ad ROTHMANNVM antea datas me facturum pollicitus eram. Verum, cum ab eodem nunc intelligam, id quicquid erat, T. Celsit. interea non esse redditum, in itinere intercidisse suspicor. Curaui itaque denuo eiusdem Cometae expositionem transscribi, et his literis adiungi, quam vt T. Celsit. expendat, dijudicet, bonique consulat, rogo.</p><p>Plura non addam, ne Celsit. T. alijs forte occupationibus praepeditam nimium detineam. Vale.</p><p>Datae Vraniburgi, Calendis Octob. Anno 1590.<br>T. Celsit. Addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda: the most clement Prince and Lord. Greeting.</b></p><p>Your Highness's mathematician Christoph Rothmann has just now come hither to me into Denmark, Most Illustrious Prince, and has brought Your Highness's letter, in which you kindly set forth the cause of so long an intermission in writing to me — both the burden of cares, and likewise the mathematician's ill health. But that Your Highness's mathematical pursuit — amid so many and so great impediments of public administration, and of old age too creeping on by degrees — is not utterly given up, but still continued so far as leisure has allowed, is most pleasant for me to hear. And yet that it should now be scarcely ready to review and handle your earlier findings [<i>priora inuena</i>] with the same eagerness as before ought to be no wonder, since the years, the cares and the maladies, together with the affliction of bodily health, weigh down and overcloud not a little the mind itself too and the powers of the intellect, the better part of us. But truly, whatever in this kind Your Highness, by reason of the aforesaid causes, shall hereafter scarcely have been able to perform by your own labour as fully and copiously as before, that you will easily manage to make good through other suitable craftsmen, by your authority and encouragement, and likewise by your outlays and liberality.</p><p>I understand that Rothmann changed air for his health's sake; and although this seemed at the outset less safe and less advisable to Your Highness and to others, yet, so far as I learn from his own report, it has turned out for him not unhappily. For straightway from the start of the journey he recovered his strength by degrees, and meanwhile, while he tarried here, he was of no very infirm condition — although those tartareous dissolutions and coagulations by which he is inwardly beset have driven their roots so deep, even at this youthful age, that they cannot so easily be wholly rooted out by mere change of place and regimen of diet, since they as it were look down from on high upon the medications of the Galenists too, however verbosely and affectedly these discourse of such matters and the like. For not without reason did that incomparable Philosopher and Physician of the Germans — sooner condemned than understood by very many — Aureolus Theophrastus, say: [\"]The <i>Roades</i> cannot cure tartareous Gout[\"], meaning by <i>Roades</i> (playing on words after his manner) crude and inexperienced physicians. More excellent and more efficacious medicines are surely required here than the syrups, and decoctions, and pills, or like impure and muddled mixtures of the drug-sellers, can supply. But of these, since they belong to another business, I shall add no more.</p><p>Now Rothmann's coming was especially welcome to me, both because he was commended to me by Your Highness, and because I have always set great store by him for his outstanding and more inward knowledge of the astronomical art and of mathematical matters. He inspected all my astronomical instruments, of which he will himself report to Your Highness. He also informed me concerning that four-foot brass globe — or rather, somewhat larger in diameter — which Your Highness's automaton-maker [<i>Automatopaeus</i>] now has in hand. But I fear whether he will bring it to exact roundness on every side by turning alone. The which my own, which is still larger, being six feet, has taught me well enough and more. But experience itself, in these matters and the like, will be the best mistress.</p><p>As to the state of our realm, all is still, by divine favour, safe and at peace. Our Most Serene elected King is faring prosperously, and is now holding an assembly with the chief men of the realm in Cymbria [Jutland], which however is ending in these very days. I, after my manner, give myself to philosophical studies, so far as other occupations and distractions permit. Our first book of the more recent phenomena of the heavens has all but received the printer's final hand, but a shortage of printing-paper has thrown in some delay, whereby it is the less quite finished. For although I myself have, at no small cost, had a mill for making paper built on this island, yet, being without a suitable papermaker, I have as yet been able to obtain no help from it as regards the preparation of the paper. I hope nevertheless that I shall shortly obtain such a craftsman from nearby Germany, who through the coming winter will make what is wanting; and so it will be convenient to supply what is deficient in that book.</p><p>I wrote to Your Highness about the beginning of last April concerning the comet of this year, which had shone forth a little before, and along with it I sent a brief description of it derived from our observations, as I had promised in an earlier letter to Rothmann that I would do. But since I now learn from him that whatever it was has not in the meantime been delivered to Your Highness, I suspect it was lost on the way. I have therefore had the account of the same comet transcribed afresh, and joined to this letter, which I beg Your Highness to weigh, judge, and take in good part.</p><p>I shall add no more, lest I detain Your Highness too long, hindered as you perhaps are by other occupations. Farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, on the Kalends of October [1 October], the year 1590.<br>Most devoted to Your Highness,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe → <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince of Kassel and Tycho's chief observational peer; their decade-long correspondence is the backbone of the 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber I</i>, in which this letter was printed (protocol §5.6).</li><li><b>DATING CRUX (resolved).</b> The source dateline reads <i>\"Calendis Octob. Anno 1590\"</i> = 1 October 1590 (O.S.). The dataset `label` gives \"1587. 1. oktober.\" — a <b>catalogue error</b>, contradicted by the letter's own internal evidence: it is written just after <b>Christoph Rothmann's stay on Hven</b> (<i>interea dum hîc moraretur</i> — \"while he tarried here\"), which the biographies date to <b>August 1590</b> (Rothmann sailed to Hven on the evening of 1 Aug. 1590 and stayed the month; Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>). It also mentions the elected King Christian IV's assembly <i>in Cymbria</i> and the comet <i>huius anni</i> (the comet of 1590). The header therefore leads with the as-written <b>1590</b> date and appends the catalogued 1587 in parentheses; per protocol §3 the dateline is not silently altered, and the discrepancy is recorded here, not reconciled in the body. The year-as-written (\"Anno 1590\") is unambiguous in the source.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription formulae.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (§5.5). <i>T. Celsit. Addictiss.</i> = <i>Tuae Celsitudini Addictissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to Your Highness.\" <i>Celsit. / Celsitudo</i> throughout rendered \"Your Highness\" (§5.5). The recipient's full titulature (Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda) is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Christophorus Rothmannus</b></i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, mathematician at the Kassel court (§5.6); the carrier of this letter's enclosed comet-account and Tycho's interlocutor of the preceding years.</li><li><b>Paracelsus passage (medical / alchemical).</b> <i>resolutiones et coagulationes istae Tartareae</i> — \"those <b>tartareous</b> dissolutions and coagulations,\" the Paracelsian <i>tartarus</i> doctrine of mineral concretions (here Rothmann's chronic ailment); kept as the period term, not modernised. <i>incomparabilis ille Germanorum Philosophus et Medicus … Aureolus Theophrastus</i> = <b>Paracelsus</b> (Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, \"Aureolus\"), named with conspicuous praise — Tycho's own Paracelsian sympathy. <i>Galenistarum … medicationes</i> — the rival <b>Galenist</b> physicians, whom Tycho has the <i>tartarus</i> \"look down on from on high.\" <i>Seplasiariorum … mixtiones</i> — the <i>seplasiarii</i>, retail drug-/ointment-sellers (after the Seplasia, the perfumers' street in Capua); rendered \"drug-sellers.\" See protocol §5.4 (period medical terms glossed, not modernised).</li><li><b>The Paracelsus tag — CRUX.</b> <i>\"Tartaream Roades nequeunt curare Podagram\"</i> is given as a saying of Paracelsus and is rendered literally [\"The <i>Roades</i> cannot cure tartareous Gout\"], with <i>Roades</i> kept untranslated because Tycho <b>immediately glosses it himself</b>: <i>per Roades (suo more ludens in vocabulis) rudes et inexpertos Medicos intelligens</i> — \"by <i>Roades</i> (playing on words after his manner) meaning crude and inexperienced physicians.\" <i>Roades</i> is thus a deliberate Paracelsian word-coinage / pun (on <i>rudes</i>, \"crude\"); it is not smoothed into ordinary Latin. <i>Podagra</i> (lit. gout of the foot) is here the genus \"gout\"; rendered \"Gout.\" The maxim's exact wording may be corrupt or a paraphrase of a Paracelsian dictum — flagged, not silently corrected.</li><li><b><i>priora inuena</b></i> — rendered \"your earlier findings.\" <i>inuena</i> is unusual (apparently for <i>inventa</i>, \"things found/discovered\"); the reading is kept as printed in the Source block and the sense flagged here rather than emended.</li><li><b>Instruments (§5.2).</b> <i>Instrumenta Astronomica</i> → \"astronomical instruments.\" <i>Globo … Orichalcico quadrupedali</i> → a four-foot <b>brass globe</b> (<i>orichalcum</i> = brass/latten); Tycho contrasts it with his own <i>sexpedalis</i> (six-foot) globe — the celebrated great brass globe of Uraniborg. <i>Automatopaeus</i> (Greek-formed, \"maker of self-moving devices / automaton-maker\") → the Landgrave's instrument-/automaton-maker at Kassel, i.e. <b>Jost Bürgi</b>; rendered \"automaton-maker\" with the Greek-derived term flagged. <i>sola tornatione … ad exactam rotunditatem</i> — \"to exact roundness by turning [on the lathe] alone,\" a technical worry about globe-truing.</li><li><b><i>Liber noster primus recentiorum coeli Phaenomenon</b></i> — \"our first book of the more recent phenomena of the heavens\": the volume in press, i.e. the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber I</i> (eventually 1596), here described as <i>fere extremam Typographi manum subijt</i> (\"has all but received the printer's final hand\") but delayed by a paper shortage.</li><li><b>Paper-mill / papermaker.</b> <i>Moletrinam pro Papyro</i> → the <b>paper-mill</b> Tycho built on Hven; <i>Chartopaeo idoneo destituam</i> — \"being without a suitable <b>papermaker</b>\" (<i>chartopoeus</i>, Greek-formed, \"paper-maker\"); he hopes to fetch one <i>e vicina Germania</i> (\"from nearby Germany\"). Tycho's epistemic hedge <i>Spero … obtenturum</i> (\"I hope … I shall obtain\") is preserved as a hope, not an accomplished fact.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimus Rex noster electus … in Cymbria comitia</b></i> — the \"elected King\" is <b>Christian IV</b> (elected 1580 as a child, crowned 1596); <i>in Cymbria</i> = in <b>Cimbria / Jutland</b> (the Cimbrian peninsula), where a <i>comitia</i> (assembly / <i>herredag</i>) of the realm's <i>Primates</i> (great men) was being held. Calendar context O.S.</li><li><b>The comet of 1590.</b> <i>Cometâ huius Anni, qui paulo ante illuxerat</i> — the comet of 1590, on which Tycho had sent Rothmann a <i>succincta descriptio … e nostris Obseruationibus deriuata</i> (\"a brief description derived from our observations\") <i>circa initium praeteriti Aprilis</i> (about the beginning of last April); the original being lost in transit (<i>in itinere intercidisse suspicor</i> — \"I suspect it was lost on the way\"), Tycho re-encloses a fresh transcript. Tycho's suspicion is kept as a suspicion (<i>suspicor</i>), not asserted.</li><li><b>Source-text reconstruction note.</b> The working transcription had collapsed the original print's paragraph breaks (line/paragraph boundaries survived only as stray inserted capitals — <i>.AVENIT</i>, <i>senijA</i>, <i>adeoA</i>, <i>suffeceritA</i>, <i>morareturA</i>, <i>requirunturA</i>, <i>mihiA</i>, <i>amplioriA</i>, <i>meA</i>, <i>tutaA</i>, <i>rogo.A</i>, <i>.A Plura</i>, <i>Calendis Octob.A</i>). The Source block above restores the paragraphing on these markers and on the letter's clear logical movements (salutation; Rothmann's arrival + the Landgrave's studies; the Paracelsian medical digression; the instruments / globes; the realm + book-in-press; the comet; close + dateline); the stray capitals have been removed and the word-joins they marked separated. No word of the source has been added or dropped. Greek-derived coinages (<i>Automatopaeus</i>, <i>Chartopaeus</i>, <i>Seplasiarii</i>) are Latin-script in the source and so kept; there is no Greek-alphabet text in this letter.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Calendis Octob. Anno 1590</i> = \"on the Kalends of October (1 October), the year 1590,\" Old Style (Denmark and the Hessen court both kept O.S.). Roman-calendar idiom rendered with the idiom and glossed to the civil date per §3.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the date crux is resolved by decisive internal + biographical evidence (1590, not the catalogued 1587). Two soft points are flagged, not smoothed: the Paracelsian maxim <i>\"Tartaream Roades … Podagram\"</i> with its coined word <i>Roades</i> (kept untranslated, as Tycho himself glosses it), and the printed form <i>inuena</i> (for <i>inventa</i>). Neither affects the letter's substance; the identification of the <i>Automatopaeus</i> as Bürgi is the conventional one but is left at the descriptive \"automaton-maker\" in the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (one of the 49 letters without a Dreyer slice); audited published_english against source_text alone, clause-by-clause, including the Rothmann travel/health narrative, the Paracelsus (Aureolus Theophrastus) quotation on 'Roades' and tartareous gout, the four-foot/six-foot globe comparison, the Hven paper-mill passage, and the closing comet/observation postscript. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found. The one ambiguous word ('priora inuena') is honestly left bracketed/untranslated by the translator rather than guessed at, which is responsible handling, not a defect. Grade capped at B per the brief because no Dreyer collation was possible.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15900515001", "next": "brahe-t_15910203001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900515001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910203001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports Rothmann's arrival at Uraniborg with Wilhelm's letter and acknowledges the reasons given for the long interruption in correspondence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901001001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901005001", "d": "1590-10-05", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Uraniborg, 5 October 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEJUM. S.</p><p>MIROR [te] dilecte M. ANDREA, in tanto tempore mihi nihil scribere: existimo vos Lillebergenses de Lillehuenensibus nihil amplius cogitare, ac si essent remoti extra Solis coelique vias; fac ut per hunc ministrum meum cognoscam quid rerum agas et quomodo cum tuis vivas et valeas. Typographia mea iam feriatur ob papyri impressoriae defectum. Misi hisce diebus Typographum meum in Germaniam pro conquirendo papyro et conducendo idoneo Cartopaeo, nam moletrina mea, papyro conficiendae destinata, iam parata est. Si tibi de tali artifice alicubi quicquam constiterit, da operam ut eum mature habeam, et tuos etiam labores urge, nec nimium procrastina.</p><p>Vale. Saluta uxorem unâ cum Parentibus et liberis.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 5. Octob. 1590.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel. Greeting.</b></p><p>I am astonished, dear Master Anders, that in so long a time you write nothing to me: I suppose that you people of Liljeberg [vos Lillebergenses] give no further thought to us people of little-Hven [Lillehuenensibus], as if we were set apart, beyond the paths of the Sun and the heavens. See to it that, through this servant of mine, I may learn what you are about, and how you live and fare with your household. My printing-press now stands idle for want of printing-paper. I have these last days sent my printer into Germany to procure paper and to engage a suitable papermaker [Cartopaeus], for my mill, intended for the making of paper, is now ready. If anything has come to your knowledge anywhere about such a craftsman, take pains that I may have him in good time; and press on your own labours too, and do not put them off too long.</p><p>Farewell. Greet your wife together with your parents and children.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 5 October 1590.<br>Yours, TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven to <i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the Danish historian and royal historiographer, then resident at <b>Ribe</b>; an old friend of Tycho's (the addressee, too, of the 1571 letter on his father's death). The dataset's `recipient` field confirms the identification.</li><li><b>Salutation / address.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem [dicit]</i>, \"[sends] greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>M. ANDREA</i> — <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic \"Master\"), rendered \"Master Anders.\"</li><li><b>The wordplay <i>Lillebergenses … Lillehuenensibus</i>.</b> The letter's one rhetorical flourish: Tycho coins two mock-ethnic demonyms. <i>Lillebergenses</i> — \"the people of <i>Lilleberg</i>/Liljeberg,\" i.e. Vedel and his circle (Liljeberg was Vedel's house/printing establishment at Ribe). <i>Lillehuenenses</i> — \"the people of <i>Lille-Hven</i>,\" i.e. \"little Hven,\" Tycho and his household on the island of Hven. The jest plays the two place-names against each other (\"you Liljeberg-folk\" vs. \"us little-Hven-folk\"); the coinages are kept transliterated in the translation and the sense glossed, rather than flattened into plain \"you\" and \"us.\" <i>[te]</i> in the opening clause (<i>MIROR [te] … nihil scribere</i>) is an editorial supplement in the source, retained in brackets.</li><li><b>Hyperbole.</b> <i>ac si essent remoti extra Solis coelique vias</i> — \"as if we [lit. they] were set apart, beyond the paths of the Sun and the heavens\": a playful astronomer's hyperbole for being forgotten, not a literal claim; kept as figure.</li><li><b>The Uraniborg press and paper-mill.</b> <i>Typographia mea … feriatur ob papyri impressoriae defectum</i> — Tycho's own printing-house (the press he set up on Hven) \"stands idle for want of printing-paper.\" <i>moletrina mea, papyro conficiendae destinata, iam parata est</i> — \"my mill, intended for the making of paper, is now ready\": the <b>paper-mill Tycho built on Hven</b>, here reported newly completed. <i>Cartopaeus</i> (Greek-derived coinage, <i>χάρτης</i> \"paper\" + <i>-ποιός</i> \"maker\") = a <b>papermaker / paper-craftsman</b>; rendered \"papermaker,\" the term flagged. <i>Typographus meus</i> = \"my printer,\" sent to Germany to buy paper and recruit such a craftsman. This passage is a documentary datum for the Hven press/paper-mill chronology (cf. <i>Bogtrykkeri paa Hveen</i>).</li><li><b>Closing.</b> <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\" (§5.5). <i>Saluta uxorem unâ cum Parentibus et liberis</i> — \"Greet your wife together with your parents and children.\" <i>Tuus</i> = \"Yours.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 5. Octob. 1590</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 5 October 1590,\" <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The as-written dateline (5 October 1590) agrees with the dataset `label` (\"1590. 5. oktober. Uraniborg\") — no discrepancy. The header carries the as-written date; no conversion is made.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, <i>Tycho Brahes privatarkiv</i>, a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>), per the source header.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a short, syntactically transparent letter; sense and register are secure. The only interpretive point is the <i>Lillebergenses / Lillehuenensibus</i> wordplay, which is preserved and glossed (not a textual crux); the place-name <i>Lilleberg</i> (Vedel's Ribe establishment) is identified with the qualification noted above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.275-276 page images. Collated the Lillebergenses/Lillehuenensibus wordplay opening, the printing-press-idle-for-paper clause, the printer-sent-to-Germany-for-a-papermaker sentence, the mill-ready clause, the request to find a suitable papermaker, the closing greetings, and the Uraniborg/5 October 1590 date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word (VELLEIUM/VELLEJUM is a mere I/J spelling variant). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15891120001", "next": "brahe-t_15980218001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Chides Vedel for long silence, reports the press stands idle for lack of paper, and asks help finding a skilled papermaker for the new Uraniborg mill.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901005001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901021001", "d": "1590-10-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Joachim Camerarius", "ck": "Joachim Camerarius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joachim Camerarius — Uraniborg, 21 October 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, lat. 10363 (Collectio Camerariana XIII 77)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>EXCELLENTISSIMO ET CLARISSIMO VIRO DOMINO IOACHIMO CAMERARIO, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, EARUNDEMQUE IN INCLYTA NORIBERGENSIUM REPUBLICA EXCULTORI, ET CIVI, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>CLARISSIME et eruditissime vir, amice summopere dilecte. Quas ad me circa Aequinoctium vernum huius anni dedisti, literulas accepi, et quod tardius atque brevius, quam expectabam, meis iam dudum ad te datis respondebas, te ob occupationum molem, qua distineris, amicitia etiam nostra candidius omnia interpretante, lubens excusatum habeo. Librum meum secundum recentiorum coeli Phaenomenωn tibi allatum, nec displicuisse, mihi auditu gratum est; quodque illum ad Illustrem et excellentiss. virum Dominum VINCENTIUM PINELLUM Patavium miseris, adhuc multo acceptius habeo. Si is, ut est Mathematum insigniter peritus, suum gravissimum atque eruditissimum de ijs, quae inibi continentur, per literas communicârit iudicium, adhuc longe gratissimam rem tam ille quam tu mihi praestiteritis. Idque, ut mature fieret, exoptarem. Intellexi iam nuper ex literis cuiusdam Studiosi Dani, qui Patavij studijs incumbit, GELLII SASCERIDIS nomine, qui octennium mihi in studijs Astronomicis et Pyronomicis diligenter inservivit, praedicto Domino PINELLO tractatulum etiam quendam meum de huius anni Cometa forte tua quoque opera redditum esse, utpote ab Illustrissimo Principe Hassiae Landgravio (ad quem ista breviter perscripseram) tibi transmissum. Quem adeo secretum et charum habeat, ut ne GELLIO quidem meo nedum alijs eum describere, nomini nostro (uti ait) favens, concedat. Quae viri illius sincera et benevola erga me de facie illi ignotum voluntas inprimis omnem gratitudinem meretur. Si et de hac Cometae illius descriptiuncula suam eruditam subiunxerit censuram, benefecerit. Tomum nostrum primum, qui iam typis meis pene absolutus est, restante saltem ultimo et decimo capite ob papyri impressoriae defectum (cui tamen propria moletrina illi usui destinata et non parvis impensis in hac Insula iam nuper a me extructa sufficienter mox subveniam) futura aestate favente divino auxilio obtinebitis, unâque tertium de quatuor alijs minoribus Cometis postmodum conspectis, inter quos hic, de quo dixi, paulo ante vernum tempus huius anni prodiens, quartus et ultimus est. Habebitis etiam unâ (modo tam cito Typographico labore ad colophonem deduci poterit) Epistolarum Astronomicarum volumen, in quo plurimae et variae eruditissimorum virorum literae mecum de re Astronomica et Cometometrica affatim conferentium unâ cum nostra ad singulas responsione continebuntur. Has ternorum librorum evulgationes Operi illi magno de perennium et Mundo coaevorum syderum revolutionibus ad normam coelestem ad amussim restituendis, quod longo iam tempore molior, quasi prodromi sive praeludij loco praemittere animus est. Plaeraque enim continebunt, quae ingenti illi molimini atque structurae viam sternent et nonnulla ponent fundamina. Quae cum (vires atque industriam suppeditante Coelestium Opifice) magnis laboribus exantlata fuerint, vos eorundem quoque reddam participes.</p><p>Sed est, mi CAMERARI, quod te nunc obnixe rogatum cuperem nomine eius, cuius antea mentionem feci, Studiosi, cui ob ingenium, diligentiam et morum integritatem, quodque multis (uti dixi) annis fidelem mihi praestitit operam, quam optime cupio. Scripsi itaque Domino IOACHIMO POMERIO vestrae civitatis Senatori primario, ut illi meo nomine 50 Coronatos confestim per Mercatores Venetijs negotiantes collybo sive literis cambij (ut vocant) transmitti procuret. Ego nulla intercedente mora, Lubecae vel ubicunque in propinquo voluerit, renumerari unâ cum interesse non invite curabo. Sin vero Dominus POMERIUS se hac in parte (quod tamen non spero) difficilem praebuerit, da tu operam, amantissime CAMERARI, ut GELLIUS meus mature et quam citissime fieri poterit, 50 illos Coronatos Patavij, vel ubicunque in Italia agat, obtineat. Fiet illud absque omni tuo damno, egoque ad recompensandum et promptus et gratus inveniar. Admonui etiam eundem GELLIUM per literas, ut quotiescunque aliquid illi ad studiorum suorum sublevamen necessarium fuerit (tum etiam in sumptus Instrumenti cuiusdam Astronomici, quod illic ob quaedam in Italia commodius quam hîc observationi patentia meis impensis fieri iniunxi) statim ad Dominum POMERIUM et te de eo audacter scribat, atque a vobis meo nomine expetat. Praebete igitur vos faciles obsecro in exponendo et subministrando illi, quicquid a vobis petierit. Scio enim satis, illum superflua non desideraturum. Ego vicissim bona et integra fide spondeo, id quicquid erit, quod in eius usum a vobis erogatum fuerit, primo quoque tempore citra omne dispendium, etiam cum lucro in vicinia, ubicunque vobis placuerit, persolutum iri. Da operam, charissime CAMERARI, ut omnia votis meis hac in parte quam optime respondeant. Feceris mihi officium ea in re multis nominibus acceptum.</p><p>Quin et aliud quiddam est, quo te molestare, amicitia nostra sic suadente, non intermittam. Si eximius ille Poëta MELISSUS adhuc vobiscum est, vel etiam alibi, per literas illum roga, ut carmen quoddam egregium in effigiem meam, unâ cum circumscriptis Insignibus, atque in Operis commendationem ex sua illa melliflua poëtica vena concinnare non gravetur, ita ut unum latus paginae in 4to typis impressum adimplere possit. Et si quos excellentes in Italia et Gallia, tum etiam alibi in Germania vestra nosti Poëtas, illos eodem nomine in mei gratiam rogatos habe. Inveniar vicissim erga ipsos gratus. Poterit quilibet inventionem extruere, prout libuerit et spiritus feret poëticus, ut consideratis et expensis singulis, quae maxime placuerint, seligam. Icona autem meam brevi (volente Deo) obtinebis per peculiarem quendam hominem, quem Noribergam cum Instrumento quodam e nostris, ut illic nonnulla coelitus mihi denotet, mittere decrevi. Qua de re antea quoque, si recte memini, te per literas me aliquando facturum certiorem reddidi; et cum Domino POMERIO, quando hîc praesens aderat, oretenus collocutus sum, qui mihi operam suam, quo nuntius ille meus commodum observationi coelesti atque a turba separatum locum, dum illic est, nanciscatur, non invite addixit. Qua in parte ut tuum quoque subministres unâ cum consilio auxilium, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Verum nolo te a tuis multiplicibus occupationibus ulterius avocare, unicum saltem amanter insuper petens, ut adiuncturum literarum fasciculum absque omni mora ad antenominatum Studiosum GELLIUM SASCERIDEM in Italiam Patavium tuto et certo perferri procurare non molestum ducas. Ego ad tibi vicissim, quacunque occasione dabitur, gratificandum perpetuo me polliceor quam paratissimum.</p><p>Vale cum uxore et tota familia quam diutissime felicissimeque.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 21. Octobris Anno 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent and most renowned man, Master Joachim Camerarius, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine, and a cultivator of those same disciplines in the illustrious commonwealth of the Nurembergers, and a citizen [of it], his friend to be honoured above all.</b></p><p>Most renowned and most learned man, friend most exceedingly beloved. I have received the little letter you sent me around the spring equinox of this year; and that you were replying later and more briefly than I expected to those [letters] of mine long since sent to you, I gladly hold you excused on account of the mass of business by which you are kept occupied — our friendship, too, interpreting everything in the kinder light. That my second book of the more recent phenomena of the heavens [Phaenomenωn], having been brought to you, did not displease you, is welcome news to me to hear; and that you sent it on to the illustrious and most excellent man Master Vincenzo Pinelli at Padua, I take as much more welcome still. If he — being as he is signally skilled in the mathematical sciences — should communicate by letter his most weighty and most learned judgement concerning the things contained therein, both he and you will have rendered me a thing far more welcome yet. And I could wish that this be done in good time. I have lately understood from the letter of a certain Danish student who is engaged in his studies at Padua, by name Gellius Sascerides — who for eight years diligently served me in astronomical and pyronomic studies — that a certain little treatise of mine too, on this year's comet, was delivered to the aforesaid Master Pinelli, perhaps by your agency as well, having been transmitted to you by the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave of Hesse (to whom I had briefly written these things out). [Pinelli] holds it so secret and dear that he will not allow even my Gellius, let alone others, to copy it out — favouring our name (as he says). That man's sincere and benevolent disposition towards me — towards one unknown to him by face — deserves above all every gratitude. If he should append his learned assessment of that little description of the comet too, he will have done well. My first volume, which is now almost completed at my press — only the last and tenth chapter remaining, on account of a shortage of printing-paper (which, however, I shall presently relieve sufficiently by my own paper-mill destined for that use, and built at no small expense here on this island just lately by me) — you shall obtain next summer, God's help favouring it; and together with it the third [volume], concerning four other lesser comets observed thereafter, among which this one of which I have spoken, appearing a little before the spring season of this year, is the fourth and last. You will have together with these also (provided it can be brought so quickly to its colophon by the labour of the press) a volume of Astronomical Letters, in which will be contained very many and various letters of most learned men conferring with me fully on astronomical and comet-measuring matters, together with my own reply to each. It is my intention to send these publications of three books ahead, as it were in the place of a forerunner or prelude, before that great Work on the revolutions of the everlasting stars coeval with the World, to be restored exactly to the celestial standard — which I have now for a long time been labouring at. For they will contain most things which will pave the way for that vast undertaking and structure, and will lay down some foundations. And when these (the Maker of the Heavenly Things supplying strength and industry) shall have been drained dry through great labours, I shall make you sharers in them as well.</p><p>But there is something, my Camerarius, in which I should earnestly wish you now to be asked [for help], in the name of that student of whom I made mention before, for whom — on account of his talent, diligence, and integrity of character, and because (as I have said) for many years he rendered me faithful service — I wish as well as possible. I have therefore written to Master Joachim Pomarius, the foremost Senator of your city, that he procure 50 crowns to be transmitted to him at once in my name, through the merchants trading at Venice, by exchange or by letters of exchange (as they call them). I, with no delay intervening, shall not unwillingly see to its being repaid, together with the interest, at Lübeck or wherever nearby he may wish. But if Master Pomarius should in this part show himself difficult (which, however, I do not expect), do you take pains, most loving Camerarius, that my Gellius may in good time and as quickly as possible obtain those 50 crowns at Padua, or wherever he may be in Italy. It will be done without any loss to you, and I shall be found both ready and grateful to make recompense. I have also admonished this same Gellius by letter that, as often as anything be necessary to him for the relief of his studies (and also for the costs of a certain astronomical instrument which, on account of certain things being more conveniently open to observation in Italy than here, I have charged to be made there at my expense), he should at once write boldly about it to Master Pomarius and to you, and request it from you in my name. Show yourselves therefore accommodating, I beseech you, in laying out and supplying to him whatever he shall have requested of you. For I know well enough that he will not desire superfluous things. I in turn pledge in good and entire faith that whatever it shall be that is disbursed by you for his use will, at the very first opportunity, be paid back without any loss — indeed with profit — in your neighbourhood, wherever it shall please you. Take pains, dearest Camerarius, that everything may answer as well as possible to my wishes in this matter. You will have done me a service welcome on many counts therein.</p><p>Indeed there is yet another thing, with which — our friendship so urging — I shall not refrain from troubling you. If that distinguished poet Melissus is still with you, or even elsewhere, ask him by letter that he not think it a burden to compose, out of that mellifluous poetic vein of his, some excellent poem upon my portrait — together with the arms inscribed around it — and to the commendation of the Work, such that it may fill one side of a page printed in quarto. And if you know any excellent poets in Italy and France, and also elsewhere in your Germany, have them asked on the same account for my sake. I shall in turn be found grateful towards them. Anyone may construct his conceit just as he pleases and as the poetic spirit shall carry him, so that, the several pieces being considered and weighed, I may select those which shall most have pleased. My likeness, moreover, you shall shortly obtain (God willing) through a certain particular man whom I have resolved to send to Nuremberg with one of our instruments, that he may there note down for me some [phenomena] from the heavens. Of which matter also I earlier, if I rightly remember, gave you notice by letter that I would at some time do so; and with Master Pomarius, when he was here present, I spoke by word of mouth, who not unwillingly assigned me his assistance, so that that messenger of mine, while he is there, may obtain a place convenient for celestial observation and set apart from the crowd. In which part, that you too may supply your aid together with your counsel, I have you asked again and again. But I do not wish to call you away further from your manifold occupations, asking lovingly besides only this one thing: that you not think it a trouble to procure that the attached bundle of letters be conveyed without any delay, safely and surely, to the aforenamed student Gellius Sascerides into Italy, to Padua. I in turn promise myself ever most ready to gratify you, on whatever occasion shall be given.</p><p>Farewell, with your wife and your whole household, as long and as happily as may be.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the 21st day of October, in the year 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand\" — the autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Ioachimus Camerarius</i> = <b>Joachim Camerarius the Younger</b> (1534–1598), physician, botanist and humanist of <b>Nuremberg</b> (<i>Noribergensium Republica</i> = \"the commonwealth of the Nurembergers,\" i.e. the Free Imperial City of Nuremberg); son of the elder Joachim Camerarius. <i>Philosophiae et Medicinae Doctori</i> = \"Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Vincentius Pinellus</i> = <b>Gian Vincenzo Pinelli</b> (1535–1601), the great Paduan bibliophile and patron of learning whose house was a centre of scientific exchange; <i>Mathematum insigniter peritus</i> = \"signally skilled in the mathematical sciences.\"</li><li><i>Gellius Sascerides</i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (1562–1612), Tycho's former assistant on Hven, then a Danish student at <b>Padua</b>; later (1594) the broken betrothal to Tycho's daughter Magdalene soured the relationship. <i>octennium … inservivit</i> = \"served me for eight years.\" Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) and Dreyer both treat this letter as the key evidence that Tycho was financing Sascerides' instrument-building and use in Italy.</li><li><i>Illustrissimus Princeps Hassiae Landgravius</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592) (protocol §5.6).</li><li><i>Ioachimus Pomerius</i> = <b>Joachim Pomarius</b> (latinised; the Nuremberg patrician/senator), here <i>Senatori primario</i> \"the foremost Senator of your city.\"</li><li><i>Melissus</i> = <b>Paul Melissus Schede</b> (1539–1602), the celebrated German Neo-Latin poet and <i>Comes Palatinus</i>. (He did in fact later address verses to Tycho; cf. his letter of 5 March 1600 from Heidelberg.)</li><li><b>Tycho's publishing programme (the works named).</b></li><li><i>Liber meus secundus recentiorum coeli Phaenomenωn</i> = \"my second book of the more recent phenomena of the heavens\" = the <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), Tycho's \"second book\" treating the comet of 1577. The word <i>Phaenomenωn</i> is written with a <b>Greek omega (ω)</b> in the genitive-plural ending — a learned graphic flourish on a Greek loan-word; kept as printed in the Source block and rendered \"phenomena.\"</li><li><i>Tomus primus</i> = \"the first volume\" = the planned <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the long-gestating work on the new star of 1572 and the restored solar/stellar theory); here reported as all-but-printed at Tycho's own Uraniborg press, with only the tenth and last chapter held up by a paper shortage — hence the new <b>paper-mill</b> (<i>moletrina</i>) on Hven. The phrasing <i>de … syderum revolutionibus … ad normam coelestem ad amussim restituendis</i> (\"the stars … to be restored exactly to the celestial standard\") is Tycho's <i>instauratio</i> programme (protocol §5.1: <i>restitutio/instauratio</i> → <b>restoration/renewal</b>).</li><li><i>tertium [volumen] de quatuor alijs minoribus Cometis</i> = \"the third [volume], on four other lesser comets\" — the never-completed third cometary volume; <i>hic … Cometa</i> (this year's comet, <i>paulo ante vernum tempus huius anni</i>, \"a little before the spring of this year\") = the <b>comet of 1590</b>, the fourth and last of the four lesser comets.</li><li><i>Epistolarum Astronomicarum volumen</i> = \"a volume of Astronomical Letters\" = the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I, printed at Uraniborg 1596). <i>Cometometrica</i> = \"comet-measuring [matters]\" — Tycho's coinage; rendered literally and flagged.</li><li><i>Opus illud magnum … prodromi sive praeludij loco praemittere</i> = \"to send [these three works] ahead in the place of a forerunner or prelude before that great Work\": Tycho casts the three publications as a <i>prodromus</i> to his comprehensive astronomy.</li><li><b>Technical / lexical notes.</b></li><li><i>studijs Astronomicis et Pyronomicis</i> = \"astronomical and <b>pyronomic</b> studies\": <i>pyronomia</i> = the art/management of fire, i.e. the <b>alchemical/distillatory (laboratory) work</b> Tycho ran on Hven alongside astronomy. Rendered \"pyronomic\" and glossed; not modernised to \"chemistry.\"</li><li><i>50 Coronatos</i> = \"50 <b>crowns</b>\" (gold <i>scudi/coronati</i>); <i>collybo sive literis cambij (ut vocant)</i> = \"by exchange or by letters of exchange (as they call them)\" — <i>collybus</i> (Greek κόλλυβος, money-changing) and the bill-of-exchange mechanism for remitting funds via Venice merchants; Tycho's own <i>ut vocant</i> hedge preserved. <i>unâ cum interesse</i> = \"together with the interest.\"</li><li><i>Instrumentum … Astronomicum</i> (protocol §5.2: <i>organum/instrumentum</i> → <b>instrument</b>); <i>ob quaedam in Italia commodius quam hîc observationi patentia</i> = \"on account of certain things being more conveniently open to observation in Italy than here\" (lower latitude / southern stars).</li><li><i>Icona / effigiem meam … unâ cum circumscriptis Insignibus</i> = \"my likeness / my portrait … together with the arms [coat of arms] inscribed around it\"; <i>in 4to</i> = \"in quarto.\" Tycho is soliciting laudatory verse for an engraved author-portrait to accompany the <i>Work</i>. <i>melliflua poëtica vena</i> = \"mellifluous poetic vein.\"</li><li><i>coelitus … denotet</i> = \"may note down [phenomena] from the heavens\": the instrument is sent to Nuremberg so the messenger can make observations there; <i>commodum … atque a turba separatum locum</i> = \"a place convenient [for observation] and set apart from the crowd.\"</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Superscription closes <i>Amico suo inprimis colendo</i> = \"his friend to be honoured above all\"; <i>S.</i> (in the address) = <i>salutem</i>, \"greeting\" (protocol §5.5). Subscription <i>Vale … quam diutissime felicissimeque</i> = \"Farewell … as long and as happily as may be\"; <i>Datae … die 21. Octobris</i> = \"Given … on the 21st day of October.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Denmark was on the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1590; the dateline <i>die 21. Octobris Anno 1590</i> is therefore O.S. (= 31 October N.S.). The dataset `label` (\"1590. 21. oktober. Uraniborg\") agrees with the source's own dateline; <b>no discrepancy.</b> No date is internally conflicting; the year-references <i>huius anni</i> are all 1590.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is read securely and the persons and works are firmly identified (corroborated by Mosley and Dreyer). The only soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (i) <i>Pyronomicis</i> is rendered \"pyronomic\" (Tycho's laboratory/fire work) rather than glossed away; (ii) <i>Cometometrica</i> is a Tychonic coinage given literally as \"comet-measuring\"; (iii) <i>tertium [volumen]</i> — the noun \"volume\" is supplied in [implied] sense from <i>Tomum nostrum primum</i> and the parallel structure, and is so marked. No crux reverses sense.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — MS firmly placed (Munich Collectio Camerariana); residue is glossed Tychonic coinages + a structure-forced [volume] supply — no crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (heading through 'manu propria', TBOO VII pp.276-278, within read slice pp.276-279 — p.279 belongs to a different letter, 'Tycho ad Gellium Sasceridem') visually against Dreyer's page images. Checked every proper name (Camerarius, Pinellus/Pinelli, the Landgrave of Hesse, Gellius Sascerides, Joachim Pomerius/Pomarius, Melissus), every number and sum (eight years of service, 50 crowns transmitted twice, the quarto/4to page format, 'fourth and last' comet), and the opening address and closing dateline (Uraniborg, 21 October 1590). No divergence found between source_text and Dreyer's printed text at any point; the English renders every clause faithfully with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, lat. 10363 (Collectio Camerariana XIII 77)."], "th": {"label": "Joachim Camerarius", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15761121001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Welcomes news that the second book of the Phaenomenon did not displease Camerarius and that it has been forwarded to Vincenzo Pinelli at Padua.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901021001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901027001", "d": "1590-10-27", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young — Uraniborg, 27 October 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77 (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE</p><p>ETSI nihil ad me tanto tempore dederis literarum, nobilissime et eruditissime D. PETRE IUNI, utut praesens te id saepius facturum addixeris: ego nihilominus oblata hac oportuna per hunc eruditum iuvenem occasione, intermittere nolui, quin aliquid saltem ad te chartis exararem, teque iamdudum inter nos contractae amicitiae atque tuarum promissionum commonefacerem. Cupio autem quam maxime ex te cognoscere, quomodo serenissimus et laudatissimus Rex vester cum suavissima Regina nostrate vivat et valeat, rogoque peramanter, ut ipsius Maiestati meo nomine submisse gratias agere velis, quod meas Uraniae dicatas aedes in hac insula invisere non sit dedignata, omniaque prospera et ex voto ipsius Maiestati ex me preceris atque exoptes. Si ab ipsius Maiestate privilegium quoddam pro meis typographicis laboribus, quos et praesens spectavit, ne videlicet in Scotia recudantur aut divendantur, absque meo concessu, tua authoritate atque intercessione impetrâris, feceris mihi rem pluribus nominibus gratam. Mitto autem tibi exemplar privilegij Caesarei mihi nuper procurante amplissimo viro D. IACOBO CURTIO, Imperij Pro-Cancellario, clementer concessi et in membrana conscripti. Addam etiam unâ, si nuntij festinatio feret, ipsius Procancellarij literarum ad me unâ datarum descriptum: quo hac ratione promptius concipies, quomodo privilegium illud, quod expeto, ordinandum veniat. Quamvis Caesareum illud imitari non sit admodum necesse, tua eloquendi et res ipsas scitis verbis complectendi eximia facultas, facile quid scribendum sit, suggeret. Optarem vero privilegium illud, si non ante hiemem, saltem circa veris primordia, quamprimum huc naves e Scotia solvant, obtinere, ut operibus meis unâ cum Caesareano atque aliorum quorundam Regum diplomatis praefigatur. Quin et aliud quiddam est, mi IUNI, de quo te commonefacere non supersedendum duxi. Addixisti, cum mecum esses, te non solum BUCHANANI effigiem, sed etiam ipsius vitam a te ipso conscriptam, mihi e Scotia missurum. Effigiem quidem accepi, quam et Serenissimus Rex vester, quamprimum in musaeo meo conspexit, hilari vultu agnovit. Vitae descriptionem adhuc desidero: fac in utroque stes promissis. Rogo insuper, ut D. Doctorem IOHANNEM CRAGIUM, medicinam Edinburgi facientem et praestantissimum, si quis alius in Scotia, Mathematicum, Regi vestro de meliore (ut aiunt) nota commendatum habeas. Est enim dignus, ut ametur et promoveatur. Neque enim aegre fero, quod libro nostro secundo de recentioribus aetherei mundi phaenomenis quaedam contraria opposuerit eademque ad me perscripserit, quibus etiam apologetice satis luculenter obviavi. Sed hominem eo plus diligo, quod eiuscemodi attentare ausus sit, omnibus in Germania eruditis Mathematicis, suas priores conceptiones, quas dilui, retractantibus, silentibus et quasi obmutescentibus; quemadmodum ipse suo tempore plenius cognosces. Plura nunc non addo, cum et haec ipsa forte nimia sint, ex quo tu tam diutino silentio distineris. Sin tandem aliqua scripseris, ego crebrius et copiosius; nec sinam me ullo officij genere a te superari.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 27. Oct. An. 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Peter Young</b></p><p>Although you have sent me no letter in so long a time, most noble and most learned Master Peter Young — for all that, when you were here, you pledged you would do so more often — I nonetheless, with this opportune occasion offered through this learned young man, was unwilling to let it pass without at least scratching something out to you on paper, and reminding you of the friendship contracted between us this while since, and of your promises. I greatly desire to learn from you how your most serene and most praised King fares and prospers, together with our most charming Queen; and I most affectionately ask that you be willing, in my name, humbly to render thanks to His Majesty, that he did not disdain to visit my house dedicated to Urania on this island, and that on my behalf you pray and wish His Majesty all things prosperous and according to his desire. If from His Majesty you should obtain, by your authority and intercession, a certain privilege for my typographical labours — those which he also viewed while present — to the effect, namely, that they not be reprinted or sold off in Scotland without my consent, you will do me a thing welcome on several counts. I am sending you, moreover, a copy of the Imperial privilege graciously granted to me lately, through the procurement of the most distinguished man Master Jakob Kurtz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, and written out on parchment. I shall add as well, if the messenger's haste allow, a transcript of the Vice-Chancellor's own letter sent to me along with it: so that by this means you may the more readily grasp how that privilege which I seek ought to be drawn up. Although it is not at all necessary to imitate that Imperial one, your outstanding faculty of expression — of embracing the matters themselves in apt words — will easily suggest what should be written. I should wish, indeed, to obtain that privilege, if not before winter, then at least about the first beginnings of spring, as soon as ships set sail hither from Scotland, so that it may be set at the front of my works together with the Imperial diploma and those of certain other Kings. But there is also another matter, my dear Young, of which I have judged that I ought not to forbear reminding you. You pledged, when you were with me, that you would send me from Scotland not only Buchanan's portrait, but also his life, written by yourself. The portrait, to be sure, I have received — which your most serene King too, as soon as he beheld it in my study, recognized with a cheerful countenance. The account of his life I still want: see that in both you stand by your promises. I ask besides that you should hold Master Doctor John Craig — who practises medicine at Edinburgh and is a most excellent mathematician, if any other in Scotland is — commended to your King on the better footing (as they say). For he is worthy to be loved and advanced. Nor indeed do I take it ill that in our second book on the more recent phenomena of the ethereal world he set certain contrary things in opposition, and wrote the same out to me, which I also met, apologetically, clearly enough; but I esteem the man the more for this, that he dared to attempt something of this kind, while all the learned mathematicians in Germany are retracting their earlier conceptions, which I refuted, falling silent and as it were struck dumb — as you yourself will learn more fully in your own time. I add no more now, since even these very things are perhaps too much, given that you are held back by so long a silence. But if at length you write something, I [shall write] more often and more copiously; nor shall I allow myself to be outdone by you in any kind of courtesy.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 27th day of October, the year 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of Hven (<i>Uraniburgum</i>, \"house dedicated to Urania,\" the muse of astronomy — the phrase <i>meas Uraniae dicatas aedes</i> puns on the name of the residence).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petrus Iunius</i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (1544–1628), Scottish humanist, former tutor to <b>James VI</b> of Scotland and a royal envoy. He had accompanied (or been part of the circle of) the king's visit to Hven. The Latinized surname <i>Iunius/Iuni</i> (vocative <i>Iuni</i>) renders \"Young.\"</li><li><b>The King and Queen.</b> <i>serenissimus … Rex vester</i> = <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who visited Uraniborg in spring 1590 while in Denmark for his marriage; <i>suavissima Regina nostrate</i> = his queen, <b>Anne of Denmark</b> (\"our [own countrywoman],\" daughter of Frederik II). Confirmed in the corpus (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>; <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i>).</li><li><b>The royal visit.</b> <i>meas … aedes in hac insula invisere non sit dedignata</i> — the visit of James VI to Hven; Tycho asks Young to convey humble thanks. The deferential third-person <i>ipsius Maiestati</i> / <i>non sit dedignata</i> (singular Majesty) is kept.</li><li><b>Scottish privilege.</b> <i>privilegium … pro meis typographicis laboribus … ne … in Scotia recudantur aut divendantur, absque meo concessu</i> — Tycho seeks a royal Scottish printing-privilege forbidding the unauthorized reprinting or sale of his works (printed at his own Uraniborg press) in Scotland, parallel to privileges he held elsewhere.</li><li><b>Imperial privilege.</b> <i>privilegium Caesareum … procurante … D. IACOBO CURTIO, Imperij Pro-Cancellario</i> = <b>Jakob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius), Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Pro-Cancellarius / Procancellarius</i>), through whom Tycho had obtained an Imperial printing-privilege, granted <i>in membrana</i> (on parchment). <i>Caesareum diploma</i> / <i>Caesareano … diplomati</i> rendered \"Imperial diploma.\"</li><li><b>Buchanan.</b> <i>BUCHANANI effigiem … ipsius vitam a te ipso conscriptam</i> = a portrait of <b>George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the great Scottish humanist and historian (and James VI's tutor), together with a life of Buchanan written by Young himself. Tycho acknowledges receiving the portrait but is still awaiting the <i>vita</i>.</li><li><b>John Craig.</b> <i>D. Doctorem IOHANNEM CRAGIUM, medicinam Edinburgi facientem … Mathematicum</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Scots Aristotelian physician and mathematician at Edinburgh with whom Tycho conducted an epistolary dispute over whether the comet of 1577 was supralunary; Craig had objected to claims in Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588). Tycho here speaks warmly of him and asks Young to commend him to the king <i>de meliore nota</i> (\"on the better footing,\" a Ciceronian idiom, flagged with <i>ut aiunt</i> \"as they say\").</li><li><b><i>libro nostro secundo de recentioribus aetherei mundi phaenomenis</b></i> — \"our second book on the more recent phenomena of the ethereal world\" = the second part of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), treating the comet of 1577. <i>contraria opposuerit … apologetice … obviavi</i>: Craig raised objections, which Tycho says he answered apologetically (i.e. in defence/refutation), <i>satis luculenter</i> \"clearly enough.\" The author's own hedge is preserved.</li><li><b><i>hunc eruditum iuvenem</b></i> — \"this learned young man,\" the unnamed bearer of the letter, the <i>occasio</i> prompting it.</li><li><b><i>musaeo meo</b></i> — \"my study\" (the <i>Museum</i>, a scholar's workroom), as in the Wolf letter, not a public museum.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Heading <i>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE</i> (dative addressee + nominative sender), with no salutation verb; <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>nec sinam me ullo officij genere a te superari</i> keeps the courtesy-competition topos (\"nor shall I allow myself to be outdone … in any kind of courtesy\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 27. Oct. An. 1590</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 27 October 1590\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Conditional logic (checked).</b> Two future-more-vivid conditions govern the requests: <i>Si … impetrâris, feceris mihi rem … gratam</i> (Scottish privilege) and <i>Sin tandem aliqua scripseris, ego crebrius</i> (the closing reciprocity). Both are positive (no negation reversed); the single negation in <i>ne … recudantur aut divendantur</i> (the privilege is to <i>forbid</i> reprinting/selling) is preserved as a prohibitive clause.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; all four named persons (Young, James VI/Anne, Kurtz, Buchanan, Craig) and the two privileges are corpus-confirmed, the dateline is unambiguous and matches the label, and no negation or conditional is in doubt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.282-284 as page images. The full letter PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE runs from the foot of p.282 to p.283 (ending Datae Uraniburgi die 27. Oct. An. 1590. TYCHO BRAHE.), with p.284 confirmed as the start of the following letter (to Thomas Savile), not part of this one. Collated the Latin source_text sentence by sentence against the printed page: the privilege request, the Jacob Kurtz/Imperial Vice-Chancellor reference, the Buchanan portrait and vita request, the John Craig/Edinburgh commendation, and the closing all match exactly. English translation checked clause by clause, including the ablative-absolute passage on the German mathematicians (retractantibus, silentibus et quasi obmutescentibus) which is rendered with the sense intact. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15860818001", "next": "brahe-t_15920320002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Young to convey thanks for the royal visit to Uraniborg and to solicit a privilege for the typographical labors, while chiding him for long silence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901027001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901201001", "d": "1590-12-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thomas Savile", "ck": "Thomas Savile", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thomas Savile — Uraniborg, 1 December 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = London, British Library, Harleian collection 6995, 40</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO INPRIMISQUE ERUDITO VIRO D. THOMAe SAVELLO, ANGLO, AMICO SUO DILECTO. ENGELANDTT. S.</p><p>LITERAS tuas circa initia Septembris ad me Hamburgo perscriptas, vir praestantissime eruditissimeque, misit huc clarissimus vir D. Doctor WINSHEMIUS, easque dum peregre in Seelandia absum, die 22. Novembris recepi, quae mihi admodum gratae erant. Nec enim insolitum quid accidit, si viri eruditi in varijs Europae locis, utut de facie mihi ignoti, ad me subinde dent literas. Quod et ego vicissim per occasionem facere non supersedeo. Non tamen ita mihi ipsi placeo, nec tanto me dignor honore, quo tu candore et benevolentia singulari erga me potius allectus, quam quod eiuscemodi quid agnoscam, me afficis. Si praesens praesentem aliquando allocutus fuisses, ex quo sic aliquoties animum induxeras, equidem tuus huc accessus mihi percarus evenisset. Solent praeter alias nationes diversas tui quoque Conterranei Angli me quotannis, aestatis praesertim tempore, crebro invisere, quos et, uti par est, in pretio habeo, et prout cuiusque fert ingenium atque desiderium, libenter cum illis de re qualibet proposita confero, quod et tecum non gravatim facerem, cum ex literis tuis perspiciam, tanquam ex ungue leonem, te ingenij acumine iudicio et eloquentia praepollere, modo Aeolus atque Neptunus tam averso animo mihi tuum huc adventum non invidissent. Reddentur forte alias placatiores, nam et eos, qui ventis atque undis praesunt, instar eorum mutabiles esse condecet. Nec fera tempestas toto perdurat in anno. Dabitur forsan aliquando occasio, qua, his faventibus et benignius aspirantibus, id quod hactenus non successit, commodius expedies. Eris mihi, ubi id evenerit, hospes apprime acceptus. Interim quod licet et decet, quodque a me tam obnixe expetis, praestare non intermittam. Mitto siquidem bina Exemplaria libri nostri secundi de recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis. Licet enim non libenter haec a caeteris tomis nondum typis integre absolutis, avello, tamen tuae sincerae et amicae petitioni morem gerere volui. Habebis et reliquos, sine quibus hic pene mutilus est, quando typis nostris ad Colophonem fuerint deducti. Interea temporis per otium hunc, quo nunc te dono, evolve, disquire, dijudica. Et si quid in hoc dubij, aut minus apodictice prolatum adinveneris, ingenue admone, tuamque et aliorum praestantium in Anglia Philosophorum de singulis eruditam censuram, sive pro sive contra nos faciat, conquire, audacter profer, meque per literas, cum libera restituetur navigatio, de his certiorem redde. Vale et magnificum spectatissimumque virum Dominum DANIELEM ROGERSIUM, Regineae Maiestatis Consiliarium atque Secretarium dignissimum (qui etiam Legationis huc in Daniam munere functus, me cum suo comitatu aliquando invisere non est gravatus) meo nomine saluta, unâque illum de mihi promisso serenissimae Reginae Privilegio librario, pro quo iam aliquoties frustra ad ipsum scripsi, admone ut mature stet promissis, quo Diploma illud primo vere, si antea ob hyemis asperitatem fieri nequeat, certo obtineam, cum caeteris quibusdam, ab Imperatore atque alijs Regibus impetratis, Operibus nostris praefigendum. Aut si Privilegium illud non est missurus, indicet saltem, quod et cur id non concedatur, sicque fidem datam apud me sufficienter redemerit, eritque excusatus. Saluta quoque meo nomine officiose Nobilissimum et excellentissimum Dominum IOHANNEM DEE, quem in patriam feliciter reversum audivi, ipsique hoc nomine congratulor, omniaque prospera opto. Nobilissimum quoque et pariter eruditissimum Mathematicum THOMAM DIGGESSAeUM ex meo ore non insalutatum relinque, cui etiam ex animo faveo et bene cupio. Utrique autem horum librum nostrum transmissum ostende, ut et illi in eum disquirant atque suam de eius contentis sententiam libere pronuntient. Quod si literis ad me datis praestiterint, ego et respondere et amicitiam in posterum cum illis colere atque de rebus Philosophicis et Mathematicis iucunde conferre nequaquam detrectabo.</p><p>Iterum atque iterum diu et bene vale.<br>Dabantur Uraniburgi, Calendis Decembris, Anno 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 285; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Addidi quaterna Exemplaria meae Effigiei nuper Amsterodami cupro insculptae. Si qui sunt apud vos excellentes poëtae, quales in fecunda et facunda vestra et terra et venâ plurimos inveniri non dubito, cuperem, ut argutum aliquod Epigramma in Iconam hanc atque operum meorum commendationem delectationis ergo luderent. Quâ in re eruditißimus Dominus DANIEL ROGERSIUS suam quoque erga me probare poßet benevolentiam, modo a Reipublicae serioribus negotijs tantillulum vacaret.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and especially learned gentleman, Master Thomas Savile, Englishman, his beloved friend. England. Greeting.</b></p><p>Your letter, written out to me from Hamburg about the beginning of September, most excellent and most learned Sir, the renowned gentleman Doctor Winsheim sent on hither, and I received it on 22 November while I was away from home in Zealand — and it was most welcome to me. For it is no unusual thing that learned men in various parts of Europe, however unknown to me by face, send me letters from time to time; which I too, in turn, do not refrain from doing when occasion offers. Yet I am not so well-pleased with myself, nor do I deem myself worthy of so great an honour, as that with which you regard me — drawn rather by your singular candour and goodwill toward me than because I recognize anything of the kind [in myself]. Had you at some time addressed me in person, face to face — since you had so often turned your mind that way — your coming here would assuredly have proved most dear to me. Besides other various nations, your own countrymen the English are wont to visit me often every year, especially in the summer season; and these, as is fitting, I hold in esteem, and, according as each one's bent and wish carries him, I gladly confer with them on whatever matter is put forward — which I would do with you too, and not grudgingly, since from your letter I perceive, as the lion from its claw, that you excel in keenness of intellect, in judgement, and in eloquence: if only Aeolus and Neptune had not, with so adverse a temper, begrudged me your coming hither. They will perhaps be rendered more placable another time, for it befits even those who preside over the winds and the waves to be, like them, changeable. Nor does a fierce tempest endure throughout the whole year. Perhaps occasion will some day be given whereby — with these favouring and breathing more kindly — you will more conveniently accomplish what has not hitherto succeeded. When that comes to pass, you will be to me a guest most welcome. Meanwhile what is permitted and seemly, and what you so earnestly seek of me, I shall not fail to perform. For I am sending two copies of our second book, <i>On the More Recent Phenomena of the Heavens</i>. Although indeed it is not willingly that I tear these away from the other volumes, not yet wholly finished at the press, nevertheless I wished to comply with your sincere and friendly request. You shall have the rest also — without which this one is well-nigh mutilated — when they shall have been brought to the colophon by our press. In the meantime, at your leisure unroll, examine, and judge this [book] with which I now present you. And if you find anything in it doubtful, or set forth less than apodictically, frankly admonish me; and seek out your own learned verdict, and that of other eminent philosophers in England, on the several points — whether it make for us or against us — boldly bring it forward, and by letter inform me of these things, when free navigation shall be restored. Farewell; and greet in my name the magnificent and most distinguished gentleman Master Daniel Rogers, Her Majesty the Queen's Councillor and most worthy Secretary (who, when discharging here in Denmark the office of an embassy, did not grudge to visit me at one time with his retinue), and at the same time admonish him concerning the printing-privilege promised me by the most serene Queen — for which I have already several times written to him in vain — that he stand maturely by his promises, so that I may certainly obtain that diploma in the first spring, if it cannot be done before on account of the harshness of winter, to be prefixed to our works together with certain others obtained from the Emperor and from other Kings. Or, if he does not intend to send that privilege, let him at least indicate that, and why, it is not granted, and so he will have sufficiently redeemed in my eyes the pledge he gave, and will stand excused. Greet also dutifully in my name the most noble and most excellent gentleman Master John Dee, whom I have heard has happily returned to his homeland, and on this account I congratulate him and wish him all prosperity. The most noble and equally most learned mathematician Thomas Digges, too, do not leave ungreeted from my lips, to whom likewise I am heartily favourable and wish well. To both of these show our book, sent on [to them], that they too may examine into it and freely pronounce their opinion on its contents. And if they perform this by letters sent to me, I shall by no means decline both to reply and henceforth to cultivate friendship with them, and to confer pleasantly on philosophical and mathematical matters.</p><p>Again and again, long and well, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the Kalends of December, the year 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I have added four copies of my likeness, lately engraved on copper at Amsterdam. If there are among you any excellent poets — such as I do not doubt are to be found in great number in your fertile and eloquent land and vein — I should wish that, for delight's sake, they might play [i.e. compose] some witty Epigram upon this Image and in commendation of my works. In which matter the most learned Daniel Rogers could also prove his goodwill toward me, if only he had a little leisure from the graver affairs of the Commonwealth.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / place.</b> Tycho Brahe writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of Hven (protocol §5.7), at the height of his Hven period.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Thomas Savellus, Anglus</i> = <b>Thomas Savile</b> (c. 1561–1593), Oxford scholar, younger brother of Sir Henry Savile (Warden of Merton, Provost of Eton). He had corresponded with Tycho from Hamburg in September 1590. He died in 1593; the <i>De recentioribus phaenomenis</i> copy Tycho here despatches survives in the <b>Bodleian Library, Oxford</b> (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, App. I, citing TBOO VII, 284.15–16, 285.1–3; Norlind 1970, 123). The address \"<i>Savello</i>\" is the Latinised form; the modern surname is Savile, not (as sometimes conflated) the elder brother Sir Henry.</li><li><b>Address-line <i>ENGELANDTT</i>.</b> A vernacular geographic tag — German/Danish <i>Engeland</i> (\"England\") — appended after the Latin salutation, marking the letter's destination. It is a chancery/postal direction, not Latin; kept verbatim in the source block and rendered \"England.\" in the translation. <i>ANCLO</i> in the address is a copyist's form of <i>ANGLO</i> (\"Englishman\"); printed <i>C</i> for <i>G</i> is retained in the source block.</li><li><b>Intermediary.</b> <i>D. Doctor Winshemius</i> — \"Doctor Winsheim,\" the learned go-between at Hamburg who forwarded Savile's September letter to Hven. The exact identity (the Latin toponymic surname <i>Winshemius</i> = \"of Windsheim\") is not firmly fixed by the present sources; identified only as the Hamburg forwarder named in the letter. <b>Flagged</b>, not asserted.</li><li><b>Persons greeted.</b></li><li><i>Daniel Rogersius</i> = <b>Daniel Rogers</b> (c. 1538–1591), the English diplomat and humanist, \"Her Majesty's Councillor and Secretary,\" who had served on an embassy to Denmark and had visited Tycho on Hven. Tycho presses him about the English printing-privilege promised by Queen <b>Elizabeth I</b> (<i>serenissima Regina</i>).</li><li><i>Iohannes Dee</i> = <b>John Dee</b> (1527–1608/9), the English mathematician and magus, whom Tycho has heard \"has happily returned to his homeland\" (Dee returned to England from the Continent in late 1589). Tycho's request that Dee see a copy is the documentary basis for the long-discussed Dee↔Tycho contact.</li><li><i>Thomas Diggessaeus</i> = <b>Thomas Digges</b> (c. 1546–1595), Dee's pupil, the English Copernican mathematician.</li><li><b>Work sent.</b> <i>liber noster secundus de recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis</i> = Tycho's <b>second book, <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</b></i> (Uraniborg [C. Weida], 1588), treating the comet of 1577. The two copies are to be shown to Dee and Digges; Tycho explicitly invites criticism (\"whether it make for us or against us\"). <i>reliqui … tomi … ad Colophonem</i> — the remaining volumes of the projected set (the <i>Progymnasmata</i> etc.) \"not yet wholly finished at the press,\" to follow when carried \"to the colophon.\"</li><li><b>Classical figures / sententiae.</b></li><li><i>tanquam ex ungue leonem</i> — \"as the lion from its claw,\" the proverb (<i>ex ungue leonem</i>): to judge the whole from a small part; here, Savile's quality inferred from his letter. Kept as the proverb.</li><li><i>Aeolus atque Neptunus</i> — Aeolus (god of the winds) and Neptune (god of the sea): a periphrasis for the storms that prevented Savile's intended crossing to Hven. The conceit is sustained (\"those who preside over the winds and the waves … changeable\"; \"a fierce tempest [does not] endure throughout the whole year\").</li><li><i>cum libera restituetur navigatio</i> — \"when free navigation shall be restored\": the same maritime obstacle that frustrated the visit also delays the post.</li><li><b>Technical / institutional terms.</b> <i>Privilegium librarium / Diploma</i> — a <b>printing-privilege</b> (book-privilege), the legal monopoly Tycho sought to prefix to his works, \"together with certain others obtained from the Emperor and from other Kings\"; rendered \"printing-privilege / diploma,\" not modernised to \"copyright.\" <i>apodictice</i> — \"apodictically,\" i.e. demonstratively/with certainty (the Aristotelian sense); retained as a technical adverb.</li><li><b>Negations (verified count).</b> The letter's force turns on several negations, each reproduced one-for-one: (1) <i>Non tamen ita mihi ipsi placeo, nec … dignor</i> — a double negative of modesty (\"I am <b>not</b> so well-pleased … <b>nor</b> do I deem myself worthy\"), Tycho deflecting Savile's praise; (2) <i>non gravatim facerem</i> — \"I would [confer] … and <b>not</b> grudgingly\"; (3) <i>modo … non invidissent</i> — counterfactual, \"<b>if only</b> … had <b>not</b> begrudged\"; (4) <i>non est gravatus</i> — Rogers \"did <b>not</b> grudge to visit me\"; (5) <i>Aut si Privilegium illud <b>non</b> est missurus … quod et cur id <b>non</b> concedatur</i> — \"Or if he does <b>not</b> intend to send … why it is <b>not</b> granted\"; (6) <i>nequaquam detrectabo</i> — \"I shall <b>by no means</b> decline.\" None added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Calendis Decembris, Anno 1590</i> = \"on the Kalends of December (1 December), 1590,\" Roman-calendar idiom glossed to the civil date per §3 (Tycho, on Hven, used Old Style). The reception date <i>die 22. Novembris</i> (22 November) is likewise O.S. The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1590. 1. december. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>manu propria</i> — \"in his own hand,\" the autograph attestation.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VII, p. 285 (dropped by the working transcription); the four Amsterdam-engraved portrait copies and the epigram request naming Daniel Rogers (parallels the 7 Dec 1590 Rantzau portrait passage).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin address heading 'ANCLO' corrected to 'ANGLO' and 'praeponere' corrected to 'praepollere' (Dreyer TBOO VII pp.283-284); the English translation already read correctly in both places and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the negation-count is reproduced exactly. The single soft point is the identity (beyond the name) of the Hamburg forwarder <i>D. Doctor Winshemius</i>, flagged above rather than asserted; the address-tag <i>ENGELANDTT</i> is read as a vernacular postal direction and kept verbatim.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter body and the restored postscript against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.283-286 (page images, read visually). The translation is faithful throughout, including the restored postscript (quaterna Exemplaria / Epigramma request), and every checked name/number/date (Winshemius, 22 Novembris, Rogersius, Dee, Diggessaeus, Calendis Decembris Anno 1590) matches. Two low-severity TEXT_ERROR corruptions were found in the stored Latin source_text itself (ANCLO/ANGLO; praeponere/praepollere); per protocol any TEXT_ERROR mandates grade C regardless of its severity, even though neither reached the English.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads 'ANCLO' in the address where Dreyer prints 'ANGLO' (i.e. Anglo/Englishman); the English translation renders 'Englishman' correctly regardless, so the corruption did not propagate downstream. Dreyer prints: <i>D. THOMÆ SAVELLO, ANGLO,</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'praeponere' where Dreyer prints 'praepollere'; the published English actually tracks the correct Dreyer verb ('excel'), so the corruption is confined to the stored source_text and did not mislead the translator. Dreyer prints: <i>te ingenij acumine iudicio et eloquentia praepollere</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.283-286 (page images, not OCR text layer). Both TEXT_ERRORs are confirmed: p.283 heading reads 'D. THOMÆ SAVELLO, ANGLO,' and p.284 reads 'te ingenij acumine iudicio et eloquentia praepollere'. No other discrepancies found on collation of the full letter and postscript against the printed pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A London, British Library, Harleian collection , 6995,40."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Savile's letter forwarded via Winsheim, welcomes the correspondence of learned men from across Europe, and notes that English visitors have previously come to Uraniborg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901201001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901201002", "d": "1590-12-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Jacob Kurz", "ck": "Jacob Kurz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Kurz (Curtius) — [Uraniborg], 1 December 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.5 (Kladde/draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IACOBUM CURTIUM.</p><p>RESCRIPSI tuis humanissimis pariterque eruditissimis hac praeterlapsa aestate ad me datis literis, vir amplissime et nobilissime, amice in primis honorande, idque per eundem quem tunc temporis ad me ablegâras Tabellarium, nec dubito, quin literae meae tibi satis certo redditae sint; quare de ijs, quae tunc ultro citroque inter nos agebantur, nunc ulterius quidpiam movere, minus duco operaepretium. Unum saltem vel alterum est, de quo te denuo hisce compellandum censui. Cum Geographiae exacta cognitio Astronomiae penitius excolendae non parum (uti scis) subsidij adferat, haec vero ab antecessoribus nostris non minus quam altera nequaquam satis accurate et solide exculta est: decrevi itaque proprijs Instrumentis missis hincinde per Europam idoneis observatoribus quorundam praecipuorum locorum latitudines atque longitudines indagari curare, et primo quoque tempore cum portatili quodam Sextante, cochleis ad dissolutionem et compositionem hincinde dispositis, compagniatum quendam ex meis in Germaniam amandabo, qui Rostochij, Vitebergae, Dresdae elevationes polares exactius, quam antea factum est, scrutetur, indeque Pragam et ad vos transiens idem illic scrutabitur. Tum instrumenti huius fabricam concinnam atque usum apprime expeditum coram perspicies, atque si ita lubuerit, imitari facies. Postea Noribergam translato Organo, ibi etiam poli sublimitatem exactissime observari in votis habeo, idque eam potissimum ob caussam, ut ex observationibus quibusdam BERNHARDI GUALTERI discipuli REGIOMONTANI, illic ante unum proxime seculum habitis, ijsdemque inter se invicem collatis atque expensis, de Apogaeo atque Eccentricitate Solari, augeanturne an vero minuantur, aliquid certi et indubitati concludere queam, quod absque eius loci polari elevatione scrupulosissime cognita fieri haud posse tute non ignoras. At hanc e Solaribus altitudinibus etiam in utroque Solstitio diligenter exploratis, rimari non satis tutum est, ut diutina nos docuit experientia, caussaeque, cur id fiat, alibi a nobis exponuntur. Peractis autem ijs, quae Noribergae intermediante hoc instrumento denotanda habeo, optarem quidem illud in Italiam, ut et illic polares quaedam designarentur altitudines, transportari. Verum cum Alpium longinquae et praeruptae salebrae non parum impedimento sint, quo minus Organum hoc in Ausoniam sartum tectum absque ulla laesione perferatur, suo etiam pondere itineris difficultates respuente, vix id praestari posse mihi persuadeo. De alia igitur ratione, qua nihilominus in praecipuis quibusdam Italiae civitatibus nostris desiderijs satisfiat, mecum constitui. Degit enim nunc Patavij iuvenis quidam nostras GELLIUS SASCERIDES nomine, qui mihi paulo antea octennium domesticus fuit, atque tam in exercitijs Astronomicis quam Pyronomicis fidelem navavit operam; huic per literas iam nuper significavi, ut illic Sextantem quendam ad instar cuiusdam meorum, quos hîc vidit atque tractavit, meis sumptibus confieri procuret atque eius beneficio quorundam Italiae locorum latitudines ratas inquirat, meque de his postea certiorem reddat. At cum ne hîc quidem consistere animus sit, sed usque in Aegiptum ad septemplicis Nili ostia observationes extendere, Alexandriam videlicet in locum vetustissimae habitationis Ptolemaicae, partim ut Poli altitudo mihi usitato more illic accuratissime explorata cum Ptolemaica consignatione conferri queat, et an rite satisque exacte, de quo etiam certis de caussis non parum dubito, ab eo constituta sit, aut etiam, ut nonnulli, sed frustra, uti opinor, suspicantur, mutationem aliquam tot interlapsis seculis senserit, indubitate dignoscatur, partim ut per ☾am, cum circa 90am ab horizonte Eclipticae partem versatur, si modo per eclipsationes non liceat, longitudo eius loci, qua Geographi autoritati Ptolemaicae confisi praecipue utuntur, et de Meridianorum respectu huius atque aliorum Europae locorum differentijs aliquid rati et ambiguitati non obnoxium decernere liceat. Verumenimvero, cum instrumentum, quod in Italia parari iussi, non commode in Aegiptum, ob itineris nimiam intercapedinem aliasque difficultates transvehi operâ unius alicuius hominis possit, nec etiam unicum tanto labori atque indubiae pervestigationi sufficiat, et is etiam, de quo dixi, GELLIUS meus ex his Arctois locis oriundus, Maris Adriatici atque Mediterranei aestuationes, tum etiam aëris Aegiptiaci subtilitatem atque fervorem absque valetudinis iactura aut vitae periculo vix sustinuerit, et res haec per se alias sit magni momenti, plurimumque ad Geographiae adeoque ipsius Astronomiae plenariam instaurationem conducat, omnem idcirco movendum esse duco lapidem, ut etiam hîc votis nostris respondeat successus. Hîc vero tuum quam maxime desidero expetoque unâ cum consilio auxilium. Si enim ad Magnificentiss. Coesareae Maiestatis Legatum, qui Venetijs moratur, interposita etiam, si opus fuerit, eiusdem Caes. Maiestatis auctoritate, literas dare non gravatus fueris, quibus is rogetur admoneaturque, ut ab Illustrissimo Venetorum Principe atque Senatu amplissimo impetret, ut duo vel tria eiuscemodi Instrumenta per idoneos artifices adamussim fabrefieri mandent, et duos vel tres harum rerum capaces et industrios iuvenes locorum insuper et viarum non ignaros deputent sumtusque et necessaria, quod illis perfacile est, largiantur, quo Alexandriae non unico saltem, [sed] si id forte in tanto itinere laesionem aliquam admiserit, duobus vel tribus loci eius tam longitudo quam latitudo diligentissime indagetur: si inquam tua intercessione atque ope hoc ipsum Venetijs mature procuratum fuerit, equidem non dubiam concipio spem, omnia exoptatationi nostrae feliciter et commode satisfactura. Instrumenta autem eiuscemodi affabre et absque vitio elaborandi rationem artificibus Venetis praescribet antedictus GELLIUS, tum etiam eos, qui observandi gratia Alexandriam amandandi venient, de eorum usu atque tractatione, caeterisque ad coelestem denotationem rite et citra errorem perficiendis sufficienter instruet, stellarumque quarundam declinationes atque distantias meis hîc organis antea conquisitas, quibus uti et instrumentorum certitudinem examinare comprobareque poterint, suppeditabit. De quibus etiam omnibus illum ante paucas septimanas affatim per literas admonui. Nec dubito, quin is in diligenter exequendis ijs, quae illi a me iniuncta sunt, suo officio non sit defuturus, cum ipsius fidem et solertiam pari integritate coniunctam iamdudum diu multumque exploratam habeam. Quapropter te praestantissime et spectatissime vir non solum per eam, quae nobis intercedit, amicitiam, sed ulterius quoque per divinae illius Astronomiae incrementa atque restaurationem, quam te prae multis alijs summis votis exoptare expectareque non ignoro, et quibus insuper possum precibus, obnixe rogatum habeo, ut nostris desiderijs hac in parte subvenire, illisque tua autoritate atque suasu adeoque manuductione et auxilio praesto esse non molestum ducas.</p><p>Cedit illud, ut spero, non solum in antiquissimae et praestantissimae syderalis scientiae atque Cosmographiae promotionem et commodum, verum etiam in inclytissimae Caesareae Maiestatis atque amplissimae Reipub. Venetae, tuique ipsius, qui haec procuras atque promoves, laudem atque apud posteros, si qua restabit benemeritorum gratia, memoriam et perpetuam celebrationem. Cumque nihil diffidam te sponte tua ad rem Astronomicam sublevandam locupletandamque et meis in his conatibus adiutandis (cuius et iam antea eximium specimen in procurando illo honorifico atque illustri Diplomate Caesareano exhibuisti) propensum esse, nolo te pluribus rogando diutius hac de re interpellare, cum ultro currenti equo calcaria (ut est in proverbio) addere non deceat. Alterum adhuc saltem superest, de quo tuum consilium capere satago. Cuperem Caesareae Maiestati ipsemet scribere, et tum ob Diploma illud ab eius Maiestate mihi gratiose et clementer concessum submisse gratias agere, tum etiam quaedam alia, quae ipsius Maiestati forte non ingrata atque incommoda sint futura, humiliter atque secreto indicare. Vereor autem ne ipsius Maiestatem serenissimam atque augustissimam meis tenuioribus literis interpellando eius Maiestati aut etiam alijs dignitate atque auctoritate in aula praepollentibus offensioni sim futurus, ita ut in minus dextram partem mihi res haec vertatur. Te itaque iterum atque iterum peramanter rogatum habeo, ut quid a me hac in parte fieri liceat et deceat, ingenue commonefacere velis, et per primam quamque opportunitatem, etiam, si fieri possit ante Maris Balthici navigationes impedientem congelationem, mihi de singulis rescribere, atque quid sperandum faciendumque sit, significare non graveris. Vale vir amplissime, meque tui studiosissimum et perpetua amicitia tibi devinctum retinere (quod et ultro per me fiet) non intermitte. Communi amico nostro THADDAeO seorsim scripsi, cui etiam quam optime cupio omniaque ex animi sententia opto.</p><p>Iterum quam diutissime felicissimeque vale.<br>Datae Calendis Decembris Anni veteris Iuliani 1590.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Jacob Curtius.</b></p><p>I wrote back, most ample and most noble sir, friend above all to be honoured, to the most courteous and likewise most learned letter you sent me this past summer, and that by the very courier you had then dispatched to me; nor do I doubt that my letter was delivered to you securely enough. Therefore I judge it less worth the trouble now to raise anything further about those matters that were then debated back and forth between us. There is, at least, one thing or another about which I have thought you should be addressed anew on these matters. Since an exact knowledge of geography brings no small aid (as you know) to the deeper cultivation of astronomy, but this [geography] has been by our predecessors no less than the other [astronomy] in no way cultivated accurately and soundly enough, I have therefore resolved to have the latitudes and longitudes of certain principal places investigated, by sending my own instruments here and there through Europe to suitable observers; and at the very first opportunity I shall send off one of my men, in company [<i>compagniatum</i>], into Germany, with a certain portable sextant [<i>Sextante</i>], [fitted] with screws set here and there for taking apart and putting together, that he may scrutinize at Rostock, Wittenberg, and Dresden the polar elevations more exactly than has been done before, and thence, passing to Prague and to you, will scrutinize the same there. Then you will see in person the neat construction of this instrument and its altogether ready use, and, if it so please you, you will have it imitated. Afterwards, the instrument [<i>Organo</i>] having been carried to Nuremberg, I have it among my wishes that the height of the pole be there too observed most exactly, and that chiefly for this reason: that from certain observations of Bernhard Walther, the pupil of Regiomontanus, made there nearly a century ago, and these compared with one another and weighed, I may be able to conclude something certain and indubitable concerning the solar apogee and eccentricity — whether they are increasing or in fact decreasing — which you are well aware cannot be done without that place's polar elevation being most scrupulously known. But to search this out from solar altitudes, even though diligently observed at both solstices, is not safe enough, as long experience has taught us, and the reasons why this is so are set out by us elsewhere. And when those things are accomplished which I have to mark down at Nuremberg by means of this instrument, I should indeed wish it to be transported into Italy, so that there too certain polar altitudes might be determined. But since the far-off and sheer ruggedness of the Alps is no small hindrance to this instrument's being carried into Ausonia [Italy] sound and whole without any damage — its very weight, too, refusing the difficulties of the road — I can scarcely persuade myself that this can be brought off. I have therefore settled with myself on another plan, by which our desires may nonetheless be satisfied in certain principal cities of Italy. For there is now living at Padua a certain young countryman of ours, Gellius Sascerides by name, who a little while ago was for eight years a member of my household, and bestowed faithful service both in astronomical and in pyronomic [chemical] exercises. To him I have lately signified by letter that he should there see to it that a certain sextant be made at my expense, after the pattern of one of mine which he saw and handled here, and that by its use he investigate the fixed latitudes of certain places of Italy, and afterwards make me more certain of these. But since my mind is not to halt even here, but to extend the observations as far as Egypt, to the mouths of the sevenfold Nile — namely to Alexandria, the site of Ptolemy's most ancient dwelling — partly so that the height of the pole, most accurately explored there in my usual manner, may be compared with Ptolemy's record, and that it may be discerned beyond doubt whether it was duly and exactly enough established by him (whereof I also, for certain reasons, no little doubt), or whether even, as some — but in vain, as I think — suspect, it has undergone some change in so many intervening centuries; and partly so that, by the Moon [☾], when it is situated about the 90th part of the ecliptic from the horizon, if only it not be permitted by eclipses [<i>eclipsationes</i>], the longitude of that place — which geographers, trusting in Ptolemy's authority, chiefly use — and the differences in respect of the meridians of this and of other places of Europe, may be settled in something fixed and not liable to ambiguity. But in truth, since the instrument which I have ordered to be prepared in Italy cannot conveniently be conveyed into Egypt by the effort of any single man, on account of the journey's excessive length and other difficulties — nor would a single one suffice for so great a labour and an indubitable investigation — and since that Gellius of mine of whom I spoke, born in these arctic regions, could scarcely endure the swells of the Adriatic and Mediterranean seas, and then too the thinness and heat of the Egyptian air, without harm to his health or peril of his life; and since this matter is otherwise in itself of great moment, and contributes very much to the full restoration [<i>instaurationem</i>] of geography and indeed of astronomy itself: I therefore judge that every stone must be moved, so that here too success may answer to our wishes. And here I most especially long for and seek your help together with your counsel. For if you would not find it burdensome to give a letter to the most magnificent Legate of His Imperial Majesty who resides at Venice — with the authority of the same Imperial Majesty interposed too, if there be need — by which he may be asked and admonished to obtain from the Most Illustrious Prince of the Venetians and the most ample Senate that they command two or three instruments of this kind to be skilfully made to the line by suitable craftsmen, and that they appoint two or three young men capable of these matters and industrious, and moreover not ignorant of the regions and the routes, and bestow the expenses and necessaries (which is very easy for them), so that at Alexandria — not by one instrument only, [but,] should that perchance suffer some damage on so great a journey, by two or three — both the longitude and the latitude of that place may be most diligently investigated: if, I say, this very thing be procured in good time at Venice by your intercession and aid, then for my part I conceive no doubtful hope that all will turn out happily and conveniently to our longing. The aforesaid Gellius will prescribe to the Venetian craftsmen the method of working instruments of this kind skilfully and without fault, and will also sufficiently instruct those who shall come to be sent off to Alexandria for the sake of observing in their use and handling, and in the other things to be carried out duly and without error for celestial measurement; and he will supply the declinations and distances of certain stars previously ascertained here with my instruments [<i>organis</i>], by which they will be able both to make use of them and to examine and verify the trustworthiness of the instruments. Of all which things, too, I have fully admonished him by letter a few weeks ago. Nor do I doubt that he will not fall short of his duty in diligently carrying out those things which have been enjoined upon him by me, since I have long ago thoroughly and at length tested his fidelity and skill joined with equal integrity. Wherefore, most excellent and most esteemed sir, I hold you earnestly entreated — not only by that friendship which subsists between us, but further also by the advancement and restoration [<i>restaurationem</i>] of that divine astronomy, which I am well aware that you, beyond many others, long for and await with the highest wishes, and by whatever prayers I can besides — that you would not deem it troublesome to come to the aid of our desires in this part, and to be ready for them by your authority and persuasion, and indeed by your guidance and help.</p><p>This will redound, as I hope, not only to the promotion and advantage of that most ancient and most excellent sidereal science and of cosmography, but also to the praise of the most renowned Imperial Majesty and of the most ample Venetian Republic, and of yourself, who procure and promote these things, and — among posterity, if any gratitude for those who have deserved well shall remain — to your memory and perpetual celebration. And since I in no way distrust that you are of your own accord inclined to relieve and enrich the astronomical enterprise and to assist me in these endeavours of mine (of which you have already before now displayed a signal token in procuring that honourable and illustrious Imperial Diploma), I do not wish to importune you longer about this matter by asking more, since (as the proverb has it) it does not become one to add spurs to a horse already running of its own accord. There remains still at least one other thing, about which I am eager to take your counsel. I should wish to write to His Imperial Majesty myself, and both to give humble thanks for that Diploma graciously and clemently granted me by His Majesty, and also to indicate humbly and in secret certain other things which will perhaps be neither unwelcome nor disadvantageous to His Majesty. But I fear lest, by importuning His most serene and most august Majesty with my too-slight letter, I should give offence to His Majesty or even to others prevailing in dignity and authority at court, so that this matter should turn out to my less favourable side. I therefore hold you, again and again, most lovingly entreated, that you would be willing frankly to advise me what may be lawful and fitting for me to do in this part, and that you would not find it grievous, at the very first opportunity — even, if it can be done, before the freezing-over that impedes the navigations of the Baltic Sea — to write me back about each point, and to signify what is to be hoped and to be done. Farewell, most ample sir, and do not cease to keep me as most devoted to you and bound to you by perpetual friendship (which will also be done by me of my own accord). To our common friend Thaddaeus I have written separately, for whom too I wish all that is best and desire all things according to the mind's intent.</p><p>Again, farewell as long and as happily as may be.<br>Given on the Kalends of December [1 December] of the old Julian year 1590.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from Uraniborg on Hven (place not named in the dateline; supplied in brackets), to <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (<i>Iacobus Curtius</i>, d. 1594), <b>Imperial Pro-Chancellor</b> (<i>Reichsvizekanzler</i>) at the court of Emperor Rudolf II in Prague. Kurz was the patron who in 1590 procured for Tycho the imperial privilege (\"Diploma\") protecting his publications in the Habsburg lands — the <i>Diploma Caesareum</i> referred to twice below — and a correspondent of Tycho's through the intermediacy of Thaddaeus Hagecius.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.5, the <i>Kladde</i> (Tycho's retained draft); hence the sole authority is a draft, not the dispatched original.</li><li><b><i>Tabellarium</b></i> — \"courier\" (the letter-carrier Kurz had sent).</li><li><b>The portable sextant project.</b> <i>portatili quodam Sextante, cochleis ad dissolutionem et compositionem … dispositis</i> — a <b>sextant</b> (per glossary §5.2) made demountable by screws for transport; Tycho's plan to fix the latitudes/longitudes of principal European cities by circulating his own instruments. <i>compagniatum quendam ex meis</i> — \"one of my men, in company\"; <i>compagniatum</i> is a non-classical (Italianate/mercantile) coinage for an associate or travelling companion, kept literal.</li><li><b>Itinerary of the instrument.</b> <i>Rostochij, Vitebergae, Dresdae</i> = Rostock, Wittenberg, Dresden; thence <i>Pragam et ad vos</i> = Prague and \"to you\" (i.e. to Kurz at the Imperial court); then <i>Noribergam</i> = <b>Nuremberg</b>. <i>elationes polares / poli sublimitatem / polari elevatione</i> — all rendered consistently \"polar elevation / height of the pole\" (= geographic latitude).</li><li><b>Bernhard Walther.</b> <i>BERNHARDI GUALTERI discipuli REGIOMONTANI</i> = <b>Bernhard Walther</b> (c. 1430–1504), the wealthy Nuremberg patrician and observer, pupil and collaborator of <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller, 1436–76); his late-15th-century Nuremberg solar observations are Tycho's reason for re-determining Nuremberg's latitude. <i>ante unum proxime seculum</i> rendered \"nearly a century ago.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy — concepts.</b> <i>Apogaeo atque Eccentricitate Solari</i> = \"the solar <b>apogee</b> and <b>eccentricity</b>\" (§5.1); <i>augeanturne an vero minuantur</i> — Tycho's open question whether they are increasing or decreasing, the disjunction preserved. <i>in utroque Solstitio</i> = \"at both solstices\"; <i>Eclipticae … 90am … partem … ab horizonte</i> — the Moon at the point of the ecliptic 90° from the horizon (the nonagesimal), used for a longitude determination by lunar method <i>if eclipses are not available</i> (<i>si modo per eclipsationes non liceat</i>); the conditional hedge is kept. <i>Meridianorum … differentijs</i> = differences of meridians (= longitude differences).</li><li><b><i>instauratio / restauratio</b></i> — rendered \"restoration\" throughout (<i>plenariam instaurationem … Geographiae adeoque ipsius Astronomiae</i>; <i>divinae illius Astronomiae … restaurationem</i>), Tycho's programmatic term per §5.1 (<i>astronomia instaurata</i>).</li><li><b>Gellius Sascerides.</b> <i>GELLIUS SASCERIDES</i> (1562–1612), Danish scholar, eight years a member of Tycho's Uraniborg household, then (from 1589) at <b>Padua</b> (<i>Patavij</i>); later briefly engaged to Tycho's daughter Magdalene. <i>in exercitijs Astronomicis quam Pyronomicis</i> — \"astronomical and <b>pyronomic</b> exercises\"; <i>pyronomic</i> = chemical/alchemical (the art of fire), kept as the period term and glossed.</li><li><b>The Alexandria / Egypt scheme.</b> <i>septemplicis Nili ostia … Alexandriam … in locum vetustissimae habitationis Ptolemaicae</i> — the sevenfold mouths of the Nile, and Alexandria as <b>Ptolemy's</b> observing-site; Tycho proposes to re-measure Alexandria's latitude against <i>Ptolemaica consignatio</i> (Ptolemy's record) and to fix its longitude by lunar observation, testing whether Ptolemy's value was exact or has changed over the centuries. <i>Ausoniam</i> = poetic for <b>Italy</b> (Ausonia), glossed in the translation.</li><li><b>The Venetian request.</b> <i>Magnificentiss. Coesareae Maiestatis Legatum, qui Venetijs moratur</i> = the Imperial Legate (ambassador) resident at <b>Venice</b>; <i>Illustrissimo Venetorum Principe atque Senatu</i> = the Doge (\"Prince of the Venetians\") and Senate of the <b>Venetian Republic</b> (<i>Reipub. Venetae</i>). <i>adamussim</i> = \"to the line, exactly\" (kept). The bracketed <i>[sed]</i> at \"non unico saltem, [sed] si id forte…\" follows Dreyer's supplement; the printed draft omits the conjunction, recorded here rather than silently absorbed.</li><li><b>The Imperial Diploma.</b> <i>Diplomate Caesareano / Diploma illud</i> — the imperial privilege (printing privilege/copyright for the Habsburg realms) Kurz had procured, later printed at the front of Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>. <i>Caesareae Maiestati</i> = His Imperial Majesty (<b>Rudolf II</b>); Tycho's wish to write to the Emperor directly, and his fear of giving offence to courtiers, are stated as his own hedged apprehension and kept hedged.</li><li><b>Proverb.</b> <i>ultro currenti equo calcaria … addere</i> — \"to add spurs to a horse already running of its own accord,\" kept as the proverb Tycho flags (<i>ut est in proverbio</i>).</li><li><b>Thaddaeus.</b> <i>THADDAeO</i> = <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), Imperial physician at Prague and Tycho's chief Bohemian correspondent and intermediary with Kurz; here the <i>communis amicus</i>.</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> <i>per ☾am</i> = <i>per Lunam</i>, \"by the Moon\" — the Moon glyph ☾ kept in the Source block and named in the translation per §5.3.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Calendis Decembris Anni veteris Iuliani 1590</i> = \"Given on the <b>Kalends of December</b> (1 December) of the <b>old Julian year</b> 1590\" — Old Style, and explicitly so labelled by Tycho (<i>Anni veteris Iuliani</i>), a deliberate flag of the O.S./N.S. distinction toward an Imperial (Gregorian-calendar) correspondent. The header date agrees with both the dateline and the catalogue label (1 December 1590). <i>Maris Balthici … congelationem</i> — the freezing of the <b>Baltic Sea</b> that would halt shipping, Tycho's seasonal deadline for a reply.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Back-translation fix (2026-06-02).</b> <i>non ingrata atque incommoda</i> — the single <i>non</i> governs both adjectives; rendered \"neither unwelcome nor disadvantageous\" (the earlier \"not … and disadvantageous\" risked leaving <i>incommoda</i> un-negated).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.285: restored the dropped syllable in 'elevationes polares' (the working transcription read 'elationes'); the English translation already read 'elevations' correctly and needed no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the letter is rendered securely throughout; the residual soft points, flagged in the apparatus rather than smoothed, are (i) the non-classical <i>compagniatum</i> (\"in company / as associate\"), and (ii) the precise sense of <i>per ☾am, cum circa 90am ab horizonte Eclipticae partem versatur, si modo per eclipsationes non liceat</i> — a compressed technical description of a lunar longitude method (Moon at the nonagesimal, used when eclipse-timings are unavailable), where the syntax of the conditional is dense; the literal structure and Tycho's hedge are preserved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 slice pages (TBOO VII pp. 285-289) as page images and collated the full letter clause-by-clause against source_text and published_english. Confirmed page 289 is NOT part of this letter: it is the opening of a separate letter, Tycho to Giovanni Antonio Magini, dated 1 Dec 1590 (header 'TYCHO AD IOAN. ANT. MAGINUM 1 DEC. 1590') — the Curtius letter itself runs only pp. 285(partial)-288 and ends complete with 'Datae Calendis Decembris Anni veteris Iuliani 1590.' on p.288; the printed_pages metadata range is one letter too wide. Within the actual letter, checked every proper name (Curtius, Bernhard Walther/Gualterus, Regiomontanus, Gellius Sascerides, Thaddaeus), every place-name (Rostock, Wittenberg, Dresden, Prague, Nuremberg, Ausonia/Italy, Padua, Egypt, Alexandria, Venice, Baltic Sea), and every number (octennium/8 years, duo vel tria and duos vel tres = 'two or three' x2, 90am/90th, the closing date 1 Dec 1590) against Dreyer's printed Latin: all match. Found one TEXT_ERROR (see defects) where our source_text departs from Dreyer's print; translation itself is otherwise an unusually faithful, near clause-by-clause rendering with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss detected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints \"elationes polares\" where Dreyer prints \"elevationes polares\"; the same letter's own later phrase \"eius loci polari elevatione\" (p.286) confirms \"elevationes\" is the intended/correct word, so this is a dropped-syllable corruption in our Latin transcription, not a variant reading. The translation renders it correctly as \"elevations\" regardless, so no reader-facing meaning is lost. Dreyer prints: <i>qui Rostochij, Vitebergae, Dresdae elevationes polares exactius, quam antea factum est, scrutetur (TBOO VII p.285, line 36)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII pp.285-289 (page images), specifically p.285 line 36, confirming 'elevationes polares' against the working transcription's 'elationes polares'. The letter's own later use of 'polari elevatione' (p.286) independently corroborates the reading. No crux remains outstanding for this letter's single audited defect.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Sammelhandschrift (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/4-5", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961679"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.5. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Kurz", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15900801001", "next": "brahe-t_15930419001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900628001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports a plan to have the latitudes and longitudes of principal European places determined by dispatching instruments and observers, with a man to be sent shortly.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901201002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901201003", "d": "1590-12-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Giovanni Antonio Magini — Uraniborg, 1 December 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>); first printed by Magini in his<i> Tabulae Primi Mobilis </i>(Venice 1604) · MS witnesses: none recorded in the dataset (text from the printed tradition)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Editorial note.</b> One of the most important scientific letters in the whole correspondence: Tycho's full, programmatic reply to Magini on (i) the <b>Mars-parallax / fluid-heavens</b> argument, (ii) the <b>Tychonic world-system</b>, (iii) the deficiencies of the <b>Alphonsine/Copernican/Prutenic</b> restitutions, (iv) the critique of <b>Werner's</b> <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i>, (v) <b>Magini's own <i>Theoricae</b></i>, and (vi) the <b>constancy of terrestrial latitudes</b> against Domenico Maria Novara — argued with a reproduced <b>Regiomontanus 1462 Rome observation table</b>. The dataset names <i>Tabulae Primi Mobilis</i> as the source edition (Magini printed Tycho's letter there); Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII is cited as the edition of record per protocol §1. The transcription is clean; a few obvious OCR slips are kept verbatim in the source block and corrected in the translation, flagged in the Apparatus (<i>inaequalitales→inaequalitates</i>, <i>exactura→exactiora</i>). The Greek-inflected forms (<i>Phaenomenωn</i> = φαινομένων; <i>Tecmirijs</i> = τεκμηρίοις) and the glyphs (♂ ☉ ♀ ☿ ♍ ♋ ♑ ♒) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"Calendis Decembris, Anni veteris Iuliani 1590\"</i>) is explicitly <b>Old Style</b> (Julian); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VII pp. 295–298</b> (running head \"TYCHO AD IOAN. ANT. MAGINUM 1 DEC. 1590\"). <b>All 16 cells of the Regiomontanus 1462 table match Dreyer to the arcsecond</b> — including the day-11 corrected altitude <b>27°51′50″</b>, which proves the +1′ raw→corrected wrinkle (see Apparatus) is <b>Dreyer's own reading</b> (the source-print's), not a transcription artifact; the reproduce-with-flag handling is vindicated. The prose cruxes match too: Rome 41°54′, Sun-centre 48°6′ (p. 297); Venice 45°16′, Ancona 44⅙°=44°10′ vs neoterics 43⅔°=43°40′, Domenico Maria's drift 1⅙°=1°10′, Copernicus's Frauenburg error 2¾′ (p. 298); the eclipse 6ʰ51ᵐ / 9ʰ00ᵐ / 4-of-8 digits (p. 296). Dreyer prints the Ancona/Domenico-Maria figures as vulgar fractions; the translation renders them as exact fraction→minute conversions.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO D. IOANNI ANTONIO MAGINO PATAVINO, IN CELEBRI GYMNASIO BONONIENSI MATHEMATUM PROFESSORI EXIMIO, DEQUE RERUM ASTRONOMICARUM LOCUPLETATIONE PRAeCLARE MERITO: AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.</p><p>QUOD Librum nostrum secundum de recentioribus Mundi Aetherei Phaenomenis per GELLIUM SASCERIDEM meum iam dudum obtinueris, vir clarissime, et de re Astronomica, prout hactenus a nostri saeculi artificibus constituta est, excolenda locupletandaque optime merite, ex litteris tuis Bononia Idibus Septembris anni recogniti ad me datis, quas ego 8 Calendarum Decembris anni veteris Iuliani hîc accepi, lubenter cognovi; licet idipsum antea quoque dictus GELLIUS per litteras retulerit, quodque scriptionem tamdiu procrastinâris, te iam satis excusatum habeo. Fuisse autem id, quicquid erat libri, tibi gratum, nec displicuisse, mihi quoque admodum acceptum est. Et quod humanissimis tuis litteris me nunc tandem compellare, singularemque tuam erga me meaque studia benevolentiam atque candorem non obscure in ijs declarare, mecumque de quibusdam ad negotium Astronomicum rectius et solidius, quam antehac praestitum est, instaurandum stabiliendumque conferre, meumque de his iudicium atque experimenta requirere sategeris, mihi nequaquam ingratum est, sed multis nominibus pervolupe. Est igitur, quo tibi pro hoc sincero et officioso erga me animo gratias habeam plurimas. Experiere me vicissim erga te tuosque conatus benignum et tui studiosissimum, amicitiaeque semel per litteras contractae conservatorem promptum et constantem, ne te certasse priorem poeniteat. Etsi vero de ijs, quae hisce tuis desideras, ante pauculas septimanas praemonito GELLIO, qui eadem ferme tuo nomine a me expetierat, descriptumque tuarum litterarum adiunxerat, quo pleraque nunc repetita comprehendebantur, affatim responderim, unâque ut illa ad te referri curaret, iniunxerim, attamen et nunc quoque his ipsis, quae propriae tuae continent atque a me expetunt exspectantque, responsionem adornare non detrectabo. Elenchum plurimarum inerrantium Stellarum praeteritis aliquot annis maxima assiduitate et diligentia a nobis infallibilibus organis atque demetiendi rationibus verificatarum, equidem nunc libenter et liberaliter tecum communicarem, nec tibi tantum thesaurum, utut ingenti pariterque incredibili labore et sumptu plurimisque vigilatis noctibus comparatum, denegarem aut inviderem, quo tuas eximias lucubrationes his ornare locupletareque posses; cederet enim id in Astronomiae, quam, quocunque modo liceat, promovere studeo, emolumentum; nec etiam mihi obesset, cum te mea mihi, non tibi, ascripturum, nec pro alienis editurum, ex mentis et iudicij tui integritate satis persuasum habeam. Verumenimvero cum nimia sit inter nos locorum intercapedo, multique viarum terra marique anfractus, per varias etiam diversorum dominorum ditiones transeundum, litteraeque meae, antequam ad te pervenerint, plurimorum manus subiturae sint: vereor sane ne alicubi intercidentes, aut etiam reseratae, alijs, quam vellem, meorum librorum copiam praeberent, qui postea eos vel pro suis edituri, vel alijs me invito concessuri essent. Auget hanc suspicionem hybernum tempus, cum iam instet bruma, nec antea tuis circa Aequinoctium autumnale exaratis, cum iam primum eas sim adeptus, respondere licuerit. Scis autem ipsemet difficiliores esse hyberno tempore hinc in Italiam peregrinationes minusque tutas, ut satis certus esse nequeam, an et hae ad tuas manus sartae tectae perventurae sint. Spero itaque, quod ponderatis hisce rationibus, me ob hoc tempore non transmissa Fixarum restituta loca, pro tuo candore atque aequanimitate sis libenter excusaturus. Ne tamen spe tanta, et erga me eximia confidentia prorsus decidas, utque votis tuis aliquando nec nimis diutina dilatione satisfiat: scias me Tomum primum Coeli recentiorum Phaenomenωn, qui de Stella Nova et alijs quibusdam huc pertinentibus atque penum Astronomicum locupletantibus luculenter agit, proelis propriae meae Typographiae nunc urgere, fermeque ad colophonem, desideratis saltem paucis quaternionibus, deduxisse. Illic in secundo eius voluminis capite, de Stellarum fixarum accurata et ipsi coelo ad amussim consona restitutione, studiose et quasi ex professo tracto, subiunctis 500 aut etiam 600 circiter, prout libuerit (nam quaterniones illos Catalogum hunc complexuros inexcusos adhuc relinquo, ne citius quam vellem ad alios pertingant) tam quo ad latum quam longum admodum scrupulose redintegratis confectisque Affixarum locis, nulla earum, quae circa Zodiacum versantur atque in nostro Horizonte oriuntur, vel minima vixque aspectabili praetermissa: adeo ut in plerisque Asterismis multo plures numerem stellas, quam ab antiquis antecessoribus nostris factitatum est. Hunc librum futura aestate, aspirante nostris conatibus coelesti Numine, nanciscere. Illic voti abunde compos redderis. Interim hanc morulam atque dilationem aequo et candido perfer animo.</p><p>Quapropter consultius iudicarem, ut Ephemeridum tuarum, aut aliorum operum, quibus de fixis syderibus magnopere instituitur quaestio, recognitionem atque sub proelum revocationem aliquamdiu adhuc protraheres, donec acceptis nostris, in his, inventionibus, rectificationibusque ipsi coelo congrua proponere, atque alijs aperire concessum fuerit. Quae enim hactenus in usu fuere, sive Alphonsinae, sive Coperniceae Fixarum restitutiones, ab ipso coelo toto pene coelo deviant, adeo ut ne una quidem sit inter tot stellas, quae coelesti normae conformem dispositionem sortita sit, cum et ipsa ♍ Spica, qua sola COPERNICUS usus est, minus apposite ab ipso ordinata sit, caeteris omnibus ad imitationem Abaci Ptolemaici perperam in suis intercapedinibus iuxta longum et latum retentis. Sic etiam circa septem Errantium syderum motiones, Canones Astronomici, etiam a praedicto ingenti illo COPERNICO eiusque locupletatore REINHOLDO redintegrati non minus, si modo non plus coelestibus apparentijs iniuriam inferunt; adeo ut non saltem in pauculis scrupulis, sed et in nonnullis etiam gradibus saepenumero deprehendatur disconvenientia. Certum quidem est in Apogaeis atque Eccentricitatibus, adeoque orbium commensurationibus, non omnia, prout decet, a COPERNICO ad amussim (ob instrumentorum, uti opinor, fallaciam, et observationum exactarum insufficientiam) esse disposita, satisque subtiliter consignata (imo forte ne quidem ab ipso PTOLEMAeO) id quod nostrae evidenter testantur observationes atque ex his derivatae demonstrationes; adeo ut maiore, quam plerique existimant, in nonnullis adhuc opus sit correctione atque limitatione. Attamen cum alia adhuc subsit inaequalitatis particularis ratio, a veteribus non animadversa, nec etiam ab ipso COPERNICO, utut industrio coelestium contemplatore, quae neutris eorum hypothesibus analoga est, aut per has commode excusatur. Idcirco de Apogaeis atque Eccentricitatibus orbiumque proportionibus frustra verba facimus, nisi et huius Anomaliae habeatur respectus; quae licet in omnibus quinque Planetis aliquatenus sese insinuet, in solo ♂ tamen, eo quod Solaris circuitus ad ipsius orbicularem tramitem magnitudinem obtineat maxime perceptibilem caeterisque multo ampliorem, evidentius oculis incurrit plusque sentitur. Atque hinc non minima ex parte evenit, quod Martium sydus se regulari atque sua vestigia numeris consignari, admittere hactenus respuerit. Scio satis eius stellam inobservabilem vulgariter existimari, quod a PLINIO, qui hoc de ea frustra profert, desumptum autumo. Ego nihilominus in ipsius apparentijs coelitus denotandis et intermediantibus Geometricis apodixibus in numeros redigendis, non plus difficultatis invenio, quam penes caeteros, imo adhuc minus quam in ☿ aut ♀. Sunt mihi in promptu multiplices et diutinae tum huius, tum aliorum oberrantium syderum observationes accuratissima ratione et medijs minime fallentibus conquisitae, e quibus Astronomiae coelitus depromptam instaurationem Canonumque Secundorum Mobilium de novo extructionem, favente conatibus nostris coelestium omnium Opifice ter maximo, ingenti et audenti conamine molior. Hoc nostrum Astronomicae redintegrationis Theatrum, quam primum solide et totaliter extructum absolutumque fuerit, et publica luce spectari sustinuerit, te eius quoque reddam participem. Illud omnia, imo etiam plura, quam nunc circa Planetarum gyrationes dimensionesque enucleandas desideras, affatim suppeditabit. Expecto adhuc nonnullas superiorum Planetarum acronychas dispositiones desuper plenius examinandas, ut per totum hincinde Eccentricum (lubet enim nunc vulgariter loqui, cum in nostris hypothesibus ferme omnia sint concentrica) inaequalitales atque apparentias difformes penitius scruter, atque undiquaque circumspiciam, illisque convenienter prospiciam: idque potissimum in ipso de quo loqueris ♂. Ideoque praecognoscere aveo huius, quae in Iunio proxime instantis anni futura est, circa ☉ oppositum, exactam constitutionem, quando circa Perigaeum solare (quod non parum interest dignoscere) pernox reddetur: tum etiam biennio post, ubi in Piscibus iuxta medias ferme Solis elongationes eidem contraposita loca peraget, quanto etiam tam ratione Eccentrici, quam Epicycli (ut more usitato haec dicam) terris plurimum appropinquans solito maior apparebit, atque pro novo quodam iubare ab imperitis reputabitur. Ubi quoque inter calculum Alphonsinum et Copernicaeum admodum magna et consueta longe evidentior incidet differentia, utpote quae novem gradus aliquantulum excedat, ipso coelo locum aliquot gradibus ab utraque supputatione diversum tunc ostensuro. Has inquam in Martia stella animadversiones, plurimum ad tramitem eius sagaciter investigandum conducentes, adhuc desidero. Licet enim non paucas eiuscemodi acronychas huius syderis dispositiones hactenus iugiter scrutatus sim, unâ cum caeteris in vario, quoad Solem, situ locis, ex quibus eius apparentem motum demonstrare atque in numeros resolvere non est difficile, attamen et has binas superaddere exopto, ut per totum Zodiacum eius versus Solem acronycha incessio multifariam et subtiliter exploretur atque concinnetur. Eccentricitatem eius mutari, et non ob id omnibus saeculis aequationum Tabulas huic superiunctas apte congruere, abs re quidem a te asseritur. Verum id fit per accidens atque aliunde evenit, quemadmodum et in caeteris Planetis, licet in his non tantopere atque in ♂ certis de causis sentiatur. Si igitur universales debent esse canones, quod merito etiam requiritur, et huius quoque mutationis ratio non negligenda venit. Optarem quam maxime PTOLEMAeI atque COPERNICI circa eius Eccentricitatem, adeoque ipsum Apogaeum prolata rite constare, et firmo adeoque coelesti inniti fundamento. Sed enim in utroque horum aliquid satis perceptibilis latere erroris, haud citra rem suspicor, praesertim in Copernicaea decisione, quam videtur potius pro libitu ita ordinasse, ut suae quadraret assumptioni, quam quod indubijs observationibus ita se exhibere sufficienter compertum habuerit. Quî enim fieri potuit, ut Martis apparens motus intra unius hominis aetatem a coelesti tramite ita difformis redderetur, ut in tribus vel quatuor nonnunquam gradibus numeri ex ipsius calculatione deprompti a scopo coelesti digrediantur, si illi suo tempore atque ijsdem annis, quibus observationes exercuit, competenter coelo conformes erant? Verum de his atque alijs ad totam harmoniam coelestem concinne in numeros adaptandam facientibus, volumen nostrum Astronomicum de perennium syderum circuitibus tractans, suo tempore, cum divinitus ita prospectum fuerit, te plenius admonebit, certioremque reddet.</p><p>Systema universi a nobis ante non multos annos adinventum, et ex ipso coelo coelestium pervestigata dispositione atque incessu, non autem libris vel chartis, sive antiquorum, sive recentium Philosophorum, tibi quoque probari et a te in pretio haberi, plurimum mihi ipsi congratulor. Neque enim ignoro perpaucos futuros, praesertim ex ijs, qui in Scholis Peripateticorum educati et instructi sunt, qui hanc nostram mundanae Symmetriae atque revolutionum coelestium ordinationem prima fronte inspectam admissuri sint, utut plurimas a PTOLEMAeO et Veteribus commissas inconcinnitates adeoque superfluitates nimias et a COPERNICO introductas absurditates devitando eliminet. At ubi penitius ipsius penetralia introspicere solertibus ingenijs dabitur, utique non diffido, quin hanc Neotericam nostram constitutionem, repudiatis et antiquatis caeteris, sint approbaturi, praesertim quando apparentias coelestes illi adeo certo et subtiliter congruere exploratum habuerint. Quod autem Martius orbis Solarem non totaliter ambiat, sed bifariam transeat, te et forte etiam alios nonnihil movet atque ancipites detinet, nihil prorsus apud me moretur scrupuli. Id enim quod hactenus Aristotelica auctoritate sic frustra persuadente creditum est, coelum ex impervia adamantinaque constare materia, orbibusque realibus et solidis astra sibi infixa rotantibus confertum esse, caret fundamento atque ab ipsa rei veritate alienissimum est; prout ex ipsis saltem Cometis, si non aliunde etiam, sufficienter liquet. Dum enim quinos, accurata diligentia, diversis aliquot annis praeteritis in altissimo aethere curriculum suum exercentes et nequaquam sublunari aëre, ut perperam tot saeculis Scholas omnes decepit ARISTOTELES, idoneis et minime fluctuantibus instrumentis observârim, atque invicta Geometriae certitudine demonstrârim: horum tamen nullus alicuius certi orbis circumagitationem concomitabatur, sed libere in liquidissimo aethere, quo propria ferebat naturaliter indita motionis vis atque energia, undecunque tandem exorta atque agitata, movebantur: idque non perplexo, interrupto, aut tumultuario incessu, sed ordinato atque ad invicem proportionali, caeterorumque Planetarum cursui in plerisque non multum absimili, licet concitatiore nonnunquam impulsu. Fieri itaque non potest ut coelum sit solidum atque impervium et realiter existentibus compaginetur orbibus. Alias enim Cometae quoque in coelo revera, quandocunque effulserint, versantes, ab eo, in quo existerent, orbe circumraperentur illique obsecundarent. Atque haec non minima est causa, cur ascititiorum coeli Phaenomenωn descriptionem peculiari opere, magno illi, quod Mundo coaevorum coeli corporum gyrationes multiplices extricabit atque ob oculos ponet, praemittere satius duxerimus. Inde namque non pauca sequentibus viam sternentia atque hypotheseos nostrae fundamenta, quibus caetera omnia superstruantur, stabilientia depromenda comprobandaque veniunt. Accedit et hoc, quod refractiones Astrorum ad verticem nequaquam pertingentes, sed intra dimidium quadrantem atque adhuc declivius prorsus evanescentes evidenter testentur diaphanitatem coelestem non versari in solida materia, longe diversa ab aëris liquida diaphanitate, adeo ut illae, quae fiunt in luminaribus atque stellis sub decliviori versus Horizontem situ refractiones ex solis pene vaporibus Terram, etiam serenissimo existente aëre, in propinquo ambientibus, insinuentur et originem trahant, ita vel solis opticis ratiocinijs, quae Geometriae validis affirmantur munimentis, Aristotelicam de coeli natura atque materia nimis diu inveteratam opinionem aperte eludentibus. Quin et id, de quo saepedictus GELLIUS noster te commonefecit, Martem in situ acronycho terris ipso Sole reddi propiorem, a nobis iugiter et subtili indagine pervestigatum est, atque ita se habere compertum, praesertim circa finem anni 1582, quando in ♋ sublimis, ipsa quoque latitudine Borea illum plus exaltante, Soli opponebatur. Deprehendi enim tum e multiplicibus et accuratis animadversionibus in vario situ obtentis atque invicem diligenter collatis, ipsum Parallaxin admisisse Solari paululo maiorem, quod nullatenus evenire poterat, si tota eius revolutio in orbibus supra Solis Sphaeram extantibus, ut fert Ptolemaica dispositio, absolveretur. Attendi etiam saepenumero ad ipsius transitus retrogrados, quando acronychius esset, et interiectis aliquot diebus comperi eos aliquanto concitatiores reddi, quam fert Ptolemaica ratiocinatio, congruente potius hîc Copernicaea numeratione, praesertim si verioris Apogaei atque oppositionis cum Solis medio motu fiat applicatio. Atque hinc non obscure satisque certo patuit, Martem Soli aversam caeli partem perambulantem, telluri ipso plus admoveri. Unde necessario consequitur, aut Copernicaeas hypotheses veras esse, aut ita coelestium distributionem ordinandam, quemadmodum a nobis nuper constitutum est. At COPERNICI circa motum telluris triplicem, utut ingeniosam et a coelestibus apparentijs non admodum abhorrentem speculationem, nequaquam in rei veritate constare (si quis est, qui adhuc haesitat) suo loco et tempore a nobis irrefragabiliter, non saltem Theologice et Physice, sed etiam Mathematice convincetur: utut is se satis Mathematica Mathematicis atque ab illis irreprehensibilia proposuisse sperabat. Imo vel ipsi Cometae, de quibus dixi (ut hoc obiter nunc quoque indicem) quatenus aliqui eorum circa oppositam Solis plagam incedebant, motum Terrae annuum a COPERNICO assertum inficiabantur; nihil enim eorum destinato cursui detraxit, nedum ut eos retroagi aut lentiore gressu tardari nobis ita aspectantibus cogeret, de quibus opportuniore loco latius agere decrevimus. Ex his itaque omnibus iam commemoratis Martiae stellae circuitum, licet Solarem transeat, ob coeli liquidissimam et ubique perviam substantiam orbiumque realiter existentium abrogationem et systematis Copernicaei absurditatem, non inconcinne aut citra possibilitatem a nobis ordinatum esse, extra omnem controversiam concludere datur.</p><p>VERNERI libellum de motu octavae Sphaerae ex tua donatione nondum accepi. Existimo illum prae locorum inter nos longinqua distantia itinerumque difficultate alicubi intercidisse. Ego nihilominus pro hac quoque tua non contemnenda erga me benevolentia tibi magnas dico gratias. Et ut conscius sis, Librum illum iam dudum a D. FABRITIO Mathematico Caesareo, intermediante D. Doctore THADDAeO HAGGECIO, etiam Mathematum insigniter gnaro, adeptus sum. Illum introspexi, disquisivi, dilui: Nam ut paucis expediam, quod res est, ternae illae stellae, quibus pro fundamento caeterarum utitur, atque octavae Sphaerae intricatas mutationes superstruere conatur, non ab illo coelitus diligenter, uti par erat, licet is ita praetendat (quod tamen cum pace Manium viri alias eruditissimi et de re Mathematica praeclare meriti dictum volo) observatae sunt, sed potius eas ita pro lubitu effinxit atque adaptavit, ut suae intentioni congruerent. Quod inde satis liquet, ex quo latitudines earundem ubique easdem, quas veteres praesupposuerunt, retineat, et nihilominus assumptis suis declinationibus differentias longitudinum pares cum recepta annotatione efformet. Quae nequaquam invicem cohaerere possunt. Nam nec illae latitudines earundem stellarum, excepta sola Spica, in qua saltem unicum desideratur scrupulum, coelitus ita se habent, nec longitudinum intervalla apparentijs congruunt. Hinc patet, quam reliqua, quae non absque ingenij acumine atque subtilitate superaedificare laborat, invalida atque ruinosa evadant. Taceo nunc quod ipsas stellas suas latitudines mutare, ad rationem alteratae obliquitatis Eclipticae (prout a nobis indubitate deprehensum est) nec is, nec magnus ille COPERNICUS animadverterint: multo minus, ut per hypothesin aliquam hanc divagationem tutati sint.</p><p>Intelligo te Theoricas tuas nunc publice exponere atque copiosius illustrare, quod etiam plurimum laudo. Attigisti in istis Theoricis propius scopum Copernicaeum, redigendo ipsius speculationem ad formam Ptolemaicam, quam antea in Tabularum Astronomicarum e Prutenicis resolutione praestitum est, ubi nimium ab ipsius symmetria et ratione aliena admisisti, quod te ipsum iam dudum animadvertisse atque emendasse non dubito. Verum ne hîc quidem ad amussim quadrant, et Aequantum a circulari et regulari motu, qui proprium, non alienum respicit centrum, inconcinnitas parvis quibusdam circellis convenientius excusari poterat, ne sit Mathematicae perfectioni atque regularitati offendiculo. Realitas etiam et copia tot orbium complentium et trahentium, quae et quorsum libuerit, non est necessaria, imo prorsus supervacanea, cum ipsi coelo nihil eiuscemodi revera competat, velut antea innuimus. Recte tamen facis, quod studiosos aliquali saltem coelestium cognitione imbuis, et eorum ingenia ad solidiora et pleniora capessenda idonea reddis atque praeparas. Est aliquid prodire tenus, si non datur ultra, ut inquiebat Poëta. Cum nostrae publicam lucem viderint hypotyposes hisque superinductae demonstrationes et numeri, has quoque discutere, explanare atque auditoribus tuis proponere declarareque tibi liberum relinquam. Interim utere ijs, quae in promptu sunt, et si qua, antequam nostra acceperis, intercesserit, uti fit in rebus arduis, mora, eam patienter sustine et candide interpretare. Unum vel alterum adhuc addam, postea scribendi finem faciam.</p><p>Si Eclipses aliquot, praesertim Lunares, aut etiam Solares, Romae, Bononiae, vel etiam in alijs celebrioribus Italiae Civitatibus, proxime elapsis aliquot annis, diligenter, quoad temporum momenta, denotatae sunt, effice ut, quotquot habere licuerit, mihi copia earum fiat; quo Meridianorum nostrorum differentiam, collata nostra in ijsdem animadversione, hinc certius et praecisius quam antehac factum est, discernere liceat. Eclipsis Solis ultima contigit hîc horis 6. M. 51 a media nocte elapsis quoad primum initium. Ultimus finis erat hora ad amussim existente nona M. 0, nec obtegebantur de Sole nisi quatuor digiti, tertia scilicet eius pars, cum canones in nostro climate octonos duploque maiorem Lunae interpositionem promitterent. Desiderarem etiam scire, quid a vobis in Italia, ubi deliquium fere totale iuxta Canones esse debuit, animadversum sit. Scripsi dicto saepe GELLIO, ut instrumentum quoddam illic meis sumptibus ad instar cuiusdam eorum, quae hîc vidit atque tractavit, fieri procuret, quo intermediante quasdam stellas illic altius quam hîc scandentes, et nonnullas insuper hîc ob Poli magnam altitudinem non exorientes coelitus denotet, atque unâ selectorum quorundam Italiae oppidorum Poli sublimitates scrupolose, more mihi (uti scit) usitato, scrutetur et inquirat. Qua in parte, si is Bononiam advenerit, ipsi per loci commoditatem, atque rem omnem tuto ac certo exequendi facultatem praesto esse non graveris, te amanter rogatum volo: tum quoque si quae alia meo nomine expedienda procurandaque in se receperit, ut etiam in ijs omnibus, quibus tuum expetit cum consilio auxilium, illi sublevantem operam non denegare velis: quod et te nequaquam facturum ex animi tui integritate et de me bene merendi cupiditate mihi ipsi polliceor.</p><p>Succurrit nunc, quod aliquando in Prolegomenis Tabularum tuarum Secundorum Mobilium legerim, te cum laudatissimae memoriae illo DOMINICO MARIA Ferrariense, COPERNICI Praeceptore, in eadem esse sententia, quod latitudines locorum successive aliquatenus mutentur, et rationes illic ex eodem profers, quibus id persuaderi posse existimas, quas tamen ego (utriusque vestrum pace dixerim) non satis validas et ratas aestimo; quodque id nequaquam eveniat, alibi evidenter ostensurus sum. Certe sola Roma, quae eandem ferme adhuc, ex observatione REGIOMONTANI, quam olim PLINIJ tempore, per proportionem gnomonis et umbrae facta pervestigatione (modo centri Solis, non supremae superficiei ratio habeatur) retinet latitudinem, altitudines polares non variari comprobat. PLINIUS enim Libro secundo Naturalis Historiae, cap. 72 refert suo tempore Aequinoctij die in Urbe Roma nonam partem Gnomonis defuisse umbrae. Erat itaque tunc iuxta Geometrica ratiocinia altitudo Solis meridiana part. 48 M. 22, idque quoad supremum eius limbum. Quare subtracta semidiametro apparenti minutorum 16, evadit altitudo centri part. 48 M. 6, Aequatoris ibidem inclinationem unâ repraesentans. Nec Refractio et Parallaxis Solaris hîc quidpiam sensibilis discriminis ingerunt, cum se invicem fere eliminent. Romae itaque latitudo hinc provenit part. 41 M. 54. Cum hac REGIOMONTANI observationem antecedenti aevo Romae habitam sequenti modo conferemus.</p><p>```<br>                                ANNO 1462<br>                                IANUARIJ DIE<br>                        2                       11<br>                        P    ′    ″             P    ′    ″<br>  Altitudo ☉ merid.     26   12   0             27   50   0<br>  Parallaxis add.        2   45                  2   40<br>  Refractio subt.        2   15                  1   50<br>  Altitudo correcta     26   12   30            27   51   50<br>  Locus Solis noster    22   12   ♑              0   21½  ♒<br>  Declinatio ejusdem    21   40½  M             20    8   0 M<br>  Altitudo Aequatoris   47   53   0             47   59   50<br>  Elevatio Poli quaesita 42   7   0             42    0   10<br>```</p><p>Cum itaque posterior observatio REGIOMONTANI, quae priore videtur convenientior, latitudinem Romanae Urbis circa haec saecula depromat 42 quam proxime graduum, et PLINIJ aevo ex umbra Gnomonis saltem 6 scrupulis pene insensibilibus minor animadversa sit, utique satis liquet eam tanto interlapso tempore nullam perceptibilem admisisse alterationem. Sic quoque Venetijs umbram aequinoctialem Gnomoni parem fuisse idem citato Capite asseverat PLINIUS. Erat itaque altitudo suprema Solis dimidij praecise quadrantis; sed habita ratione centri, part. 44 et totidem minutorum eadem cum Aequatoris altitudine. Quare Venetijs Poli sublimitas tunc evadebat P. 45. M. 16, quod in ipso Minuto cum PITTATI neoterica observatione, quam et tu approbas, consentit, indicio satis evidenti, tot interlapsis saeculis prorsus immotam permansisse Horizontis ad axem mundi inclinationem. Quin et Ancona in eodem loco a PLINIO citata, quod trigesimaquinta pars Gnomonis umbrae superfuerit, prodit eius loci latitudinem p. 44⅙, cum tamen neoterici eam part. 43⅔, non auctiorem quam olim, sed potius dimidio gradu (animadversionis proculdubio aliqua incuria) minorem constituant. Atque ex his tribus Italiae Civitatibus suas tot saeculi latitudines invariatas conservantibus satis superque liquet, polares altitudines etiam plurimis intercidentibus annis nequaquam augeri aut ulli alterationi obnoxias esse. Nec enim 1⅙ gradus (quantam DOMINICUS MARIA admittit) discrepantia in hac ratiocinatione subterfugere potuit, cum non duplo maior inter Romam et Anconam inveniatur latitudinis differentia, quam tamen per umbrae et Gnomonis proportiones PLINIUS admodum apposite discrevit, ut his Tecmirijs, donec plura et exactura proferantur, acquiescere non sit supervacaneum. Id quod ostendere intendebamus.</p><p>Constitui Alexandriae Poli quoque sublimitatem praecise dimetiendam curare, ob quasdam Ptolemaicas illic institutas observationes. Nam an et is illam satis scrupulose deprompserit, non absque causa ambigo: quemadmodum COPERNICUM in latitudine Fruenburgensi designanda 2¾ scrupulis defecisse iam dudum, misso illuc quodam ex meis cum proprio eodemque exacto instrumento, compertum habeo. Ubi Alexandriae similiter facta fuerit, uti spero, inquisitio, utique Poli illic altitudinem non sensibiliter alteratam esse tot interlapsis saeculis manifestabitur, nisi quatenus in pauculis forte scrupulis ab ipso PTOLEMAeO aberratum sit. Atque haec erit certior hunc nodum, per se tamen non adeo Gordium, resolvendi occasio. Cuperem nihilominus praestantissimi illius MARIAe libros, si qui extant, quos nunquam vidi, aut etiam manuscripti alicubi reservantur, inspicere. Qua in parte si tu etiam nostris votis favens eaque auxiliatrice manu faveris, rem in primis gratam praestiteris, omni officiorum genere, quantum per me licet, compensandam. Plura nunc non addam, ne Epistolae terminos nimium excedam teque diutius detineam.</p><p>Vale, vir clarissime, et me tui amantissimum, prout incepisti, constanter redamare, amicitiamque per litteras semel inchoatam ijsdem, cum aliter fieri nequeat, continuare perge. Dabunt Mercatores vestrates ad Nundinas proficiscentes in Germaniam Francofurtenses, rescribendi litterasque ad me tuto perferendi non incommodam opportunitatem. Iterum diu et faeliciter vale, et de studijs Astronomicis bene mereri non intermitte: iamque tertium et pluries vale,</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi, Calendis Decembris, Anni veteris Iuliani 1590.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTUM, UT VOCANT.NE prorsus mearum cœlitus deductarum animadversionum atque restitutionum<br>vacuas mitterem litteras, addidi instantis anni Ephemeridem e nostris<br>observationibus et demonstrationibus in numeros redactis derivatam: quarum<br>rationes atque fundamenta caput primum anterioris Tomi, ubi illum adeptus<br>fueris, expediet. Interim hanc Solaris tramitis designationem expende, atque<br>cum utraque usitata numerationem confer. Cognosces utique, quam ipse Sol<br>reliquorum Planetarum Rex et Ductor, in Tabulis consuetis a cœlesti norma,<br>cui nostra apprime sunt analoga, deflectat; atque hinc de cæteris quodammodo<br>ratiocinare, Canonumque usitatorum deviationes percipe.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>TYCHO<br>BRAHE</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most excellent man, Master Giovanni Antonio Magini of Padua, eminent Professor of Mathematics in the celebrated University of Bologna, and splendidly deserving of the enrichment of Astronomical matters: his much-to-be-esteemed friend.</b></p><p>That you have, some while ago, obtained our <b>Second Book on the more recent Phenomena of the Aethereal World</b> [<i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>] through my [agent] <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>, most distinguished Man — [you] who have most excellently deserved of the cultivation and enrichment of the Astronomical [science] as it has hitherto been constituted by the artificers of our age — I gladly learned from your letter, given to me from Bologna on the <b>Ides of September of the reformed year</b> [13 September, N.S.], which I received here on the <b>8th [day] before the Kalends of December of the old Julian year</b> [24 November, O.S.]; although the said Gellius had also reported the same beforehand by letter, and, that you put off the writing so long, I hold you now sufficiently excused. And that it was welcome to you — whatever there was of [my] book — and did not displease, is also very acceptable to me. And that you have now at last troubled to address me with your most kind letter, and to declare in it, not obscurely, your singular benevolence and candour toward me and my studies, and to confer with me about certain [matters] for the more correct and solid <b>renewal</b> and stabilizing of the Astronomical business than has been done before, and to seek my judgment and experiments about these — this is by no means unwelcome to me, but on many counts most pleasing. There is therefore [reason] why I should hold you the greatest thanks for this sincere and dutiful disposition toward me. You will find me in turn kind toward you and your endeavours, most zealous for you, and a ready and constant preserver of the friendship once contracted by letter — so that you may not regret having been the first to contend [in it]. And although, about those [things] which you desire in this [letter] of yours, I gave a full reply a few weeks ago, having forewarned Gellius (who had sought almost the same [things] from me in your name, and had attached a copy of your letter, in which most of [these things] now repeated were comprised), and enjoined that he take care to convey those [things] to you — nevertheless even now I shall not decline to prepare a response to these very [things] which your own [letter] contains and which seek and await [an answer] from me. The <b>catalogue</b> [<i>elenchus</i>] of very many <b>fixed Stars</b>, verified by us over some years past with the greatest assiduity and diligence, by infallible <b>instruments</b> and methods of measurement — I would indeed now gladly and liberally communicate to you, nor would I deny or begrudge you so great a treasure (acquired, though it was, with vast and likewise incredible labour and expense, and very many wakeful nights), so that you might adorn and enrich your eminent lucubrations with these; for it would tend to the profit of <b>Astronomy</b>, which I strive to promote in whatever way I may; nor would it harm me either, since I am sufficiently persuaded, from the integrity of your mind and judgment, that you will ascribe my [things] to me, not to yourself, and will not publish them as another's. But truly, since there is too great an interval of places between us, and many windings of the roads by land and sea, [and] one must pass through the various domains of diverse lords too, and my letter, before it reaches you, will pass under the hands of very many: I truly fear lest, falling in somewhere — or even unsealed — they should furnish to others, more than I would wish, a supply of my books, who would afterwards either publish them as their own, or, against my will, hand them over to others. The <b>winter</b> season increases this suspicion, since now the solstice presses on, nor was it possible to reply earlier to your [letter] written about the <b>autumn equinox</b>, since I have only now first obtained it. You yourself know that journeys from here into Italy are more difficult and less safe in winter, so that I cannot be sufficiently certain whether even this [letter] will reach your hands safe and sound. I hope, therefore, that, these reasons being weighed, you will gladly excuse me, in accordance with your candour and equanimity, for not transmitting at this time the <b>restored places of the Fixed [stars]</b>. Yet, lest you fall away entirely from so great a hope and from your exceptional confidence toward me, and so that your wishes may some time be satisfied without too long a delay: know that I am now pressing through the presses of my own <b>Printing-house</b> the <b>First Volume of the more recent Phenomena of the Heaven</b> [the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i>, Tome I], which treats amply of the <b>New Star</b> and certain other [matters] pertaining hither and enriching the Astronomical store, and have brought it almost to the colophon, only a few <b>gatherings</b> [<i>quaterniones</i>] being still wanting. There, in the second chapter of that volume, where I treat, studiously and as it were professedly, of the <b>accurate restitution</b> of the <b>fixed Stars</b>, exactly consonant with the heaven itself, with about <b>500 or even 600</b> [stars] subjoined, as it shall please (for I am leaving those gatherings which are to comprise this Catalogue still unprinted, lest they reach others sooner than I would wish), the places of the <b>Fixed [stars]</b> most scrupulously restored and worked out, both as to latitude and longitude, with none of those which move about the <b>Zodiac</b> and rise in our <b>Horizon</b> omitted — even the least and scarcely visible — so that in most of the <b>constellations</b> I count far more stars than was done by our ancient predecessors. This book you will obtain next summer, the heavenly Deity favouring our endeavours. There you will be rendered abundantly possessed of your wish. Meanwhile bear this little delay and postponement with an even and candid mind.</p><p>Wherefore I should judge it more advisable that you should still draw out for some while the revision, and the recalling under the press, of your <b>Ephemerides</b>, or of [your] other works in which a question is greatly raised about the <b>fixed stars</b>, until — our findings and rectifications in these [matters] having been received — it shall be granted [you] to propose [things] congruent with the heaven itself, and to open [them] to others. For the <b>restitutions of the Fixed [stars]</b> which have hitherto been in use — whether <b>Alphonsine</b> or <b>Copernican</b> — deviate from the heaven itself by almost the whole heaven; so that there is not even one among so many stars which has been allotted a disposition conformable to the celestial norm — since even <b>Spica [♍]</b> itself, which alone <b>Copernicus</b> used, was less aptly ordered by him, all the rest being wrongly retained in their intervals (as to longitude and latitude) in imitation of the <b>Ptolemaic Table</b> [<i>Abacus</i>]. So too, concerning the motions of the seven <b>Wandering</b> stars, the <b>Astronomical Canons</b> — restored even by that vast Copernicus and his enricher <b>[Erasmus] Reinhold</b> — do no less injury (if indeed not more) to the celestial appearances; so that a discrepancy is very often found not merely in a few minutes, but even in several degrees. It is indeed certain that, in the <b>Apogees</b> and <b>Eccentricities</b>, and so [in] the commensurations of the orbs, not everything was disposed by Copernicus exactly (on account, I think, of the fallacy of the instruments and the insufficiency of exact observations) as is fitting, nor subtly enough recorded (nay, perhaps not even by <b>Ptolemy</b> himself) — which our observations, and the demonstrations derived from these, evidently testify; so that in some [matters] there is need of a greater correction and limitation than most suppose. But moreover, since there yet lies beneath another reckoning of a <b>particular inequality</b>, not noticed by the ancients, nor even by Copernicus himself (industrious contemplator of the heavens though he was), which is analogous to neither of their hypotheses, nor is conveniently excused by these: therefore we speak in vain about Apogees and Eccentricities and the proportions of the orbs, unless regard be had to this <b>Anomaly</b> too; which, although it insinuates itself to some extent in all five Planets, yet in <b>Mars [♂]</b> alone — because the magnitude of the Solar circuit, relative to its [Mars's] orbicular track, is most perceptible and far ampler than [for] the rest — strikes the eyes more evidently and is felt more. And hence it comes about, in no small part, that the Martian star has hitherto refused to admit being regulated and having its tracks recorded by numbers. I know well enough that its star is commonly thought <b>unobservable</b> — which I suppose was taken from <b>Pliny</b>, who asserts this about it in vain. I, nonetheless, in noting its appearances from the heaven, and in reducing [them] to numbers by means of intermediate Geometric <b>demonstrations</b> [<i>apodixes</i>], find no more difficulty than [I do] with the rest — nay, still less than in <b>Mercury [☿]</b> or <b>Venus [♀]</b>. I have ready to hand manifold and long-continued observations, both of this and of the other wandering stars, sought out by the most accurate method and by means in no way deceptive — from which I am attempting, with a vast and daring effort, the <b>restoration of Astronomy drawn from the heaven</b>, and the new construction of the <b>Canons of the Second Movables</b> [the planetary tables], the thrice-greatest Maker of all the heavenly [bodies] favouring our endeavours. This our <b>Theatre of Astronomical restoration</b>, as soon as it shall have been solidly and wholly built and completed, and shall have endured to be beheld in the public light, I will render you a sharer of it too. It will abundantly supply everything — nay, even more — than you now desire concerning the unfolding of the Planets' gyrations and dimensions. I still await some <b>acronychal</b> dispositions of the superior Planets, to be examined more fully thereupon, so that through the whole <b>Eccentric</b> on either side (for it pleases [me] now to speak commonly, since in our hypotheses almost everything is concentric) I may scrutinize more deeply the <b>inequalities</b> and the deformed appearances, and may look about on every side, and provide for them suitably — and this especially in that very <b>Mars [♂]</b> of which you speak. And therefore I am eager to foreknow the exact constitution of this [planet], which is to be in <b>June of the year next at hand</b>, about [its] <b>opposition to the Sun [☉]</b>, when, about the <b>solar Perigee</b> (which it is of no little interest to discern), it will be rendered visible all night long; and likewise <b>two years after</b>, when it will traverse the places set opposite [to the Sun] in <b>Pisces</b>, near nearly the mean elongations of the Sun — by how much also, approaching the Earth very greatly (both by reason of the Eccentric and of the Epicycle, to say these things in the customary manner), it will appear larger than usual, and be reckoned by the unskilled as some new radiance. There too a difference, very great and far more evident than usual, will fall between the <b>Alphonsine</b> and the <b>Copernican</b> calculation — namely, one that exceeds nine degrees a little — the heaven itself then showing a place several degrees different from both computations. These observations on the Martian star, I say — conducing very much to the sagacious investigation of its track — I still want. For although I have hitherto continually scrutinized not a few such acronychal dispositions of this star, together with the rest, in [its] various place with respect to the Sun (from which it is not difficult to demonstrate its apparent motion and to resolve [it] into numbers), yet I much desire to add these two as well, so that throughout the whole Zodiac its acronychal advance toward the Sun may be explored and arranged in manifold and subtle fashion. That its <b>Eccentricity changes</b>, and that for this reason the Tables of <b>equations</b> superimposed on it do not aptly agree for all ages, is indeed not idly asserted by you. But this happens <b>by accident</b> and comes about from elsewhere, as also in the other Planets — although in these it is not felt so much as in Mars, for certain causes. If therefore the canons ought to be universal (which is rightly required too), then the reckoning of this change must not be neglected either. I should wish most greatly that what <b>Ptolemy</b> and <b>Copernicus</b> have brought forward concerning its Eccentricity, and so [concerning] the Apogee itself, were duly established and rested on a firm — indeed a celestial — foundation. But I suspect, and not without reason, that something of fairly perceptible error lies hidden in both of them — especially in the Copernican determination, which he seems to have ordered thus rather at [his own] pleasure, so that it might square with his assumption, than because he had found it sufficiently established, by indubitable observations, to present itself so. For how could it have come about that the apparent motion of Mars should be rendered, within the lifetime of one man, so deformed from the celestial track that the numbers drawn from his calculation diverge from the celestial mark by three or sometimes four degrees — if those [numbers] were, in his time and in the very years in which he conducted his observations, fittingly conformable to the heaven? But about these and other [matters] making for the apt adaptation of the whole celestial harmony into numbers, our <b>Astronomical volume</b> treating of the circuits of the everlasting stars will, in its time — when it shall have been so provided by Heaven — admonish you more fully and render [you] more certain.</p><p>That the <b>system of the universe</b> [<i>Systema universi</i>], discovered by us not many years ago — and [discovered] from the heaven itself, the disposition and advance of the heavenly [bodies] having been investigated [there], and not from the books or papers of Philosophers, whether ancient or recent — is approved by you too and held in esteem by you, I greatly congratulate myself. For I am not ignorant that there will be very few — especially of those who have been educated and trained in the Schools of the <b>Peripatetics</b> — who will admit this our ordering of the world's <b>symmetry</b> and of the celestial revolutions, inspected at first glance, even though, by avoiding [them], it eliminates the very many <b>incongruities</b> committed by Ptolemy and the Ancients, and the excessive superfluities, and the <b>absurdities introduced by Copernicus</b>. But when it shall be granted to skilful talents to look more deeply into its inner chambers, I do not at all doubt but that they will approve this our <b>Neoteric</b> [modern] constitution, the rest being repudiated and made obsolete — especially when they shall have found by examination that the celestial appearances agree with it so surely and subtly. But that the <b>Martian orb does not totally encompass the Solar [orb], but passes through [it] in two ways</b> [<i>bifariam</i>] — [a thing which] moves you somewhat, and perhaps others too, and holds [you] in two minds — let it detain not the least scruple with me. For that which has hitherto been believed, Aristotelian authority thus vainly persuading — that the heaven consists of an impervious and <b>adamantine</b> matter, and is packed with <b>real and solid orbs</b> rotating the stars fixed in them — lacks foundation and is most alien from the very truth of the matter; as is sufficiently clear from the <b>comets</b> at least, if not from elsewhere too. For when I had observed <b>five</b> [comets], with accurate diligence, in several years now past, plying their course in the <b>highest aether</b> and by no means in the <b>sublunary air</b> (as Aristotle wrongly deceived all the Schools for so many centuries), with suitable and in-no-way-wavering instruments, and had demonstrated [it] with the unconquered certainty of Geometry: yet none of them accompanied the rotation of any fixed orb, but they moved freely in the most fluid aether, whither the proper force and energy of motion, naturally implanted [in them] — arisen and stirred up, at last, from whatever source — bore [them]; and that not with a perplexed, interrupted, or tumultuary advance, but with an ordered and mutually proportional [one], not much unlike the course of the other Planets in most [respects], though sometimes with a swifter impulse. It cannot therefore come about that the heaven is <b>solid</b> and impervious and framed of <b>really existing orbs</b>. For otherwise the comets too — really dwelling in the heaven, whenever they have blazed — would be snatched round by that orb in which they existed, and would comply with it. And this is no small cause why I have thought it better to <b>send ahead</b>, in a separate work, the description of the <b>adventitious phenomena of the heaven</b> [the comets], before that great [work] which will disentangle and set before the eyes the manifold gyrations of the heavenly bodies coeval with the World. For thence come [things] to be drawn forth and proven — not a few [of them] paving the way for what follows, and stabilizing the <b>foundations of our hypothesis</b>, on which all the rest are to be built. There is added this too: that the <b>refractions</b> of the heavenly bodies — by no means reaching to the <b>zenith</b>, but vanishing entirely within the half-quadrant [45°] and still lower — evidently testify that the celestial <b>transparency</b> does not consist in a <b>solid matter</b> far different from the fluid transparency of the air; so that those refractions which occur in the luminaries and stars in a lower position toward the Horizon insinuate themselves and draw their origin from the vapours alone (well-nigh) which surround the Earth nearby, even when the air is most serene — thus, by <b>optical reasonings</b> alone, which are affirmed by the strong defences of Geometry, openly eluding the Aristotelian opinion (too long inveterate) about the nature and matter of the heaven. Nay, this too — about which our oft-named <b>Gellius</b> reminded you, that <b>Mars in the acronychal position is rendered nearer to the Earth than the Sun itself</b> — has been continually and by subtle search investigated by us, and found to be so, especially <b>about the end of the year 1582</b>, when, lofty in <b>Cancer [♋]</b> — its very northern latitude exalting it the more — it was opposed to the Sun. For I then ascertained, from manifold and accurate observations obtained in various positions and diligently compared with one another, that it admitted a <b>parallax a little greater than the solar</b> — which could in no way have happened if its whole revolution were completed in orbs standing above the Sun's Sphere, as the <b>Ptolemaic</b> disposition holds. I attended also, very often, to its <b>retrograde transits</b>, when it was acronychal, and, after some days were interposed, I found them rendered somewhat swifter than the Ptolemaic reckoning bears — the <b>Copernican</b> numeration rather agreeing here, especially if the application be made to the truer Apogee and to the opposition with the Sun's mean motion. And hence it appeared not obscurely, and surely enough, that Mars, traversing the part of the heaven averted from the Sun, is brought nearer to the Earth than [the Sun] itself. Whence it necessarily follows, either that the <b>Copernican hypotheses are true</b>, or that the distribution of the heavenly [bodies] is to be ordered as has lately been established by us. But that Copernicus's <b>threefold motion of the Earth</b> — an ingenious speculation, and not much abhorrent from the celestial appearances — by no means holds in the truth of the matter (if there is anyone who still hesitates) will, in its place and time, be <b>irrefragably</b> proven by us, not merely Theologically and Physically, but also <b>Mathematically</b>: even though he hoped that he had proposed [things] Mathematical enough to Mathematicians, and irreprehensible by them. Nay, the very <b>comets</b> of which I spoke (to indicate this in passing now too), in so far as some of them advanced about the part opposite the Sun, <b>gainsaid the annual motion of the Earth asserted by Copernicus</b>; for nothing of [that motion] detracted from their appointed course, much less compelled them to be driven backward or retarded with a slower step, as we look on thus — about which [comets] I have resolved to treat more amply in a more opportune place. From all these [things] now recalled, therefore, it is given [us] to conclude, beyond all controversy, that the circuit of the Martian star — although it passes through the Solar [orb] — has been ordered by us not inelegantly, nor beyond possibility, on account of the <b>most fluid and everywhere pervious substance of the heaven</b>, the abolition of really-existing orbs, and the absurdity of the Copernican system.</p><p><b>Werner's</b> little book <b>on the motion of the eighth Sphere</b> [<i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i>], from your gift, I have not yet received. I think it has fallen in somewhere, on account of the far-off distance of places between us and the difficulty of the journeys. I nonetheless say great thanks to you for this not-to-be-despised benevolence of yours toward me too. And, that you may be aware [of it], I obtained that Book some while ago from <b>Master [Paul] Fabricius</b>, the Imperial Mathematician, through the mediation of Master Doctor <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> [Tadeáš Hájek], also notably skilled in Mathematics. I looked into it, examined it, [and] dissolved it: for, to expound in few [words] what the matter is, those <b>three stars</b> which he uses as a foundation for the rest, and on which he tries to build the intricate changes of the eighth Sphere, were not observed by him from the heaven diligently, as was fitting — although he so pretends (which, however, I would have said with the peace of the <b>shade</b> [<i>Manes</i>] of a man otherwise most erudite and splendidly deserving of Mathematics) — but rather he so fashioned and adapted them at [his own] pleasure, that they might agree with his intention. Which is clear enough from this: that he retains the <b>latitudes</b> of these same [stars] everywhere the same as the ancients presupposed, and nonetheless, his own <b>declinations</b> being assumed, forms differences of <b>longitudes</b> equal to the received notation. Which can by no means cohere with one another. For neither do those latitudes of these same stars — except <b>Spica</b> alone, in which only a single minute is wanting — stand so from the heaven, nor do the intervals of longitudes agree with the appearances. Hence it is clear how invalid and ruinous the rest become, which he labours to build up, not without acumen and subtlety of talent. I say nothing now of the fact that neither he, nor that great <b>Copernicus</b>, noticed that the stars themselves change their latitudes, in proportion to the <b>altered obliquity of the Ecliptic</b> (as has been undoubtedly ascertained by us); much less that they guarded this <b>divagation</b> by any hypothesis.</p><p>I understand that you are now publicly expounding your <b>Theoricae</b> [Planetary Theories] and illustrating [them] more copiously, which too I greatly praise. In those Theoricae you have approached the Copernican aim more nearly, by reducing his speculation to the <b>Ptolemaic form</b>, than was done before in the resolution of the <b>Astronomical Tables</b> from the <b>Prutenic</b> [Tables], where you admitted [things] too alien from his symmetry and reckoning — which I do not doubt you have yourself noticed and emended long since. But not even here do they square exactly; and the <b>incongruity of the Equants</b> from a circular and regular motion (which regards its own, not an alien, centre) could be more conveniently excused by certain small circles [<i>circelli</i>], lest it be a stumbling-block to Mathematical perfection and regularity. The <b>reality</b>, too, and the multitude of so many orbs — filling and drawing [the planets] whither and as it has pleased — is not necessary, nay altogether superfluous, since nothing of this kind really belongs to the heaven itself, as we hinted before. Yet you do rightly in imbuing students with at least some knowledge of the celestial [matters], and in rendering and preparing their talents fit for grasping more solid and fuller [things]. \"It is something to advance thus far, if it is not granted [to go] beyond,\" as the <b>Poet</b> said. When our <b>sketches</b> [<i>hypotyposes</i>], and the demonstrations and numbers superinduced on them, shall have seen the public light, I will leave it free to you to discuss these too, to explain [them], and to propose and declare [them] to your hearers. Meanwhile, use those [things] which are ready to hand; and if any delay shall intervene before you have received ours (as happens in arduous matters), bear it patiently and interpret [it] candidly. I will add one [thing] or another still; afterward I will make an end of writing.</p><p>If some <b>Eclipses</b> — especially <b>Lunar</b>, or even <b>Solar</b> — have been carefully noted, as to the moments of time, at <b>Rome</b>, <b>Bologna</b>, or even in other more celebrated Cities of Italy, in some years most recently elapsed, bring it about that, as many as it shall be possible to have, a supply of them be made [available] to me; whereby it may be permitted to discern the <b>difference of our Meridians</b> [the longitude-difference], our own observation in the same [eclipses] being collated, more surely and precisely than has been done before. The <b>last solar Eclipse</b> occurred here at <b>6 hours 51 minutes</b> elapsed from midnight, as to its first beginning. The last end was at the hour being exactly the <b>ninth, [and] 0 minutes</b>; nor were more than <b>four digits</b> of the Sun covered — that is, a third part of it — although the canons promised, in our clime, <b>eight</b> [digits], and a Moon's interposition twice as great. I should also wish to know what was observed by you in Italy, where the eclipse ought, according to the Canons, to have been almost total. I have written to the oft-said Gellius to procure that a certain <b>instrument</b> be made there at my expense, after the likeness of one of those which he saw and handled here, by means of which he may note from the heaven certain stars there climbing higher than here, and some besides which, on account of the great altitude of the Pole [here], do not rise [here]; and, at the same time, may scrupulously scrutinize and inquire into the <b>Pole-elevations</b> [latitudes] of certain selected towns of Italy, in the manner (as he knows) customary to me. In which matter, if he shall come to Bologna, I would have you kindly asked to be at hand to him, by the convenience of the place and the means of executing the whole matter safely and surely; and likewise, if he shall have taken upon himself any other [things] to be expedited and procured in my name, that you would be willing not to deny [him] your relieving help in all those [matters] too in which he seeks your aid with [your] counsel — which I promise myself you will by no means [fail to] do, from the integrity of your mind and your desire of deserving well of me.</p><p>It now occurs [to me] that I once read, in the <b>Prolegomena of your Tables of the Second Movables</b>, that you are of the same opinion with that <b>Domenico Maria of Ferrara</b> [Novara] of most laudable memory, <b>Copernicus's Teacher</b> — namely that the <b>latitudes of places</b> are successively, to some extent, <b>changed</b>; and you bring forward there, from the same [author], reasons by which you think it can be persuaded — which, however, I (I would say it with the peace of you both) judge not valid and ratified enough; and that this by no means happens, I shall evidently show elsewhere. Certainly <b>Rome</b> alone — which still retains nearly the same latitude, from the observation of <b>Regiomontanus</b>, as once in <b>Pliny's</b> time, an investigation having been made by the proportion of the <b>gnomon</b> and the shadow (provided account be had of the <b>centre</b> of the Sun, not of its uppermost surface) — proves that the <b>polar altitudes do not vary</b>. For Pliny, in the <b>second Book of the Natural History, chapter 72</b>, reports that in his time, on the day of the equinox, in the City of Rome, a <b>ninth part of the gnomon</b> was wanting to the shadow. There was therefore then, according to Geometric reckonings, the <b>meridian altitude of the Sun = 48° 22′</b>, and that as to its uppermost limb. Wherefore, the <b>apparent semidiameter of 16 minutes</b> being subtracted, the altitude of the centre comes out <b>48° 6′</b>, representing at the same time the <b>inclination of the Equator</b> there. Nor do <b>Refraction</b> and <b>solar Parallax</b> here introduce any sensible difference, since they nearly eliminate one another. The latitude of Rome, therefore, comes out hence <b>41° 54′</b>. With this we shall compare Regiomontanus's observation, made at Rome in the preceding age, in the following manner:</p><p>```<br>                                YEAR 1462<br>                                DAY OF JANUARY<br>                         2                        11<br>                         °    ′    ″              °    ′    ″<br>  Sun's merid. altitude  26   12   0              27   50   0<br>  Parallax (add)          2   45                   2   40<br>  Refraction (subtract)   2   15                   1   50<br>  Corrected altitude     26   12   30             27   51   50<br>  Our place of the Sun   22   12   ♑               0   21½  ♒<br>  Its declination        21   40½  S              20    8   0 S<br>  Altitude of Equator    47   53   0              47   59   50<br>  Pole-elevation sought  42    7   0              42    0   10<br>```</p><p>Since therefore the <b>later observation</b> of Regiomontanus — which seems more agreeable than the earlier — yields the latitude of the City of Rome, about these centuries, [at] <b>42 degrees</b> very nearly, and in Pliny's age, from the gnomon's shadow, it was noted at least <b>6 minutes</b> (almost imperceptible) smaller, it is surely clear enough that, in so great an interval of time elapsed, it admitted <b>no perceptible alteration</b>. So too the same Pliny, in the cited Chapter, asserts that at <b>Venice</b> [in Pliny: the region] the equinoctial shadow was equal to the gnomon. There was therefore the uppermost altitude of the Sun [at] precisely half a quadrant [45°]; but, account being had of the centre, <b>44 [degrees] and as many minutes</b> [44° 44′] the same with the altitude of the Equator. Wherefore at Venice the Pole-elevation then came out <b>45° 16′</b>, which agrees, to the very Minute, with the <b>neoteric</b> observation of <b>Pitati</b> [Pietro Pitati], which you too approve — a sufficiently evident sign that, with so many centuries elapsed, the inclination of the Horizon to the axis of the world has remained utterly unmoved. Nay, <b>Ancona</b> too, cited by Pliny in the same place — [in] that a <b>thirty-fifth part</b> of the gnomon was left over of the shadow — yields the latitude of that place [at] <b>44° 10′</b>, although the <b>neoterics</b> establish it [at] <b>43° 40′</b> — not greater than of old, but rather (by some carelessness of observation, doubtless) a half-degree smaller. And from these three Cities of Italy, conserving their latitudes unvaried for so many centuries, it is clear more than sufficiently that the polar altitudes, even with very many years elapsing, are by no means increased or subject to any alteration. For a discrepancy of <b>1° 10′</b> (as much as <b>Domenico Maria</b> admits) could not slip through in this reckoning, since not even twice as great a difference of latitude is found between Rome and Ancona — which, however, Pliny very aptly distinguished by the proportions of the shadow and the gnomon; so that to rest in these <b>proofs</b> [<i>tecmiria</i>, τεκμήρια], until more and more exact [ones] be brought forward, is not superfluous. Which is what we intended to show.</p><p>I have resolved to see to it that the Pole-elevation of <b>Alexandria</b> too be precisely measured, on account of certain <b>Ptolemaic</b> observations instituted there. For whether he [Ptolemy] too deduced it scrupulously enough, I doubt not without cause: just as I have found out long since that <b>Copernicus</b> fell short by <b>2¾ minutes</b> in designating the <b>latitude of Frauenburg</b> [Frombork], one of my [men] having been sent thither with [my] own and likewise exact instrument. When at Alexandria likewise an inquiry shall have been made (as I hope), it will surely be made manifest that the Pole's altitude there has not been sensibly altered in so many centuries elapsed, except in so far as it may perhaps have been erred by Ptolemy himself in a few minutes. And this will be a more certain occasion for resolving this knot — not, however, of itself so <b>Gordian</b>. I should nonetheless wish to inspect the books of that most excellent <b>[Domenico] Maria</b>, if any are extant (which I have never seen), or even are reserved in manuscript somewhere. In which matter, if you too, favouring our wishes, shall favour [them] with that helping hand, you will perform a most welcome thing, to be compensated with every kind of good offices, as far as lies in my power. I will add no more now, lest I exceed the bounds of a Letter too much and detain you longer.</p><p>Farewell, most distinguished Man, and continue constantly to love me in return — me, most loving of you — as you have begun; and persevere in continuing, by the same [means] (since it cannot be done otherwise), the friendship once begun by letter. The <b>merchants</b> of your country, setting out for the <b>Frankfurt Fairs</b> in Germany, will give a not-inconvenient opportunity of writing back and of conveying letters to me safely. Again, fare well long and happily, and do not cease to deserve well of Astronomical studies; and now a third time, and many times, farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, on the Kalends of December, of the old Julian Year 1590.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT, AS THEY CALL IT.</b></p><p>Lest I send my letter altogether empty of my observations and restitutions drawn down from the heavens, I have added the Ephemeris of the coming year, derived from our own observations and demonstrations reduced into numbers; the reasoning and foundations of which the first chapter of the former volume — once you have obtained it — will set forth. Meanwhile, weigh well this delineation of the Sun's course, and compare it with each of the two customary reckonings. You will certainly recognise how far the Sun himself, King and Guide of the other Planets, departs in the usual Tables from the celestial rule to which our own [tables] are most closely conformed; and from this you may in some measure reason concerning the rest, and perceive the deviations of the customary Canons.</p><p>Farewell.</p><p>TYCHO BRAHE</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). The letter was <b>first printed by Magini</b> himself, who appended Tycho's letter to his <i>Tabulae Primi Mobilis, quas Directionum vulgo dicunt</i> (Venice 1604) — hence the dataset's `source_edition`; no MS witness is recorded, the text descending through that print. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> the letter <b>is</b> in Dreyer — <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII pp. 295–298 — and was page-collated there; all 16 table cells and every prose crux match to the arcsecond (see the Editorial note and the table-verification below). Dreyer reproduces this letter from Magini's <i>Tabulae primi mobilis</i> tradition.</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (at Uraniborg, in his last full decade on Hven). <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), Paduan-born professor of mathematics at <b>Bologna</b> (where he had beaten Galileo to the chair in 1588), astronomer, cartographer, and Ptolemaist; author of <i>Ephemerides</i>, <i>Novae coelestium orbium theoricae</i>, <i>Tabulae secundorum mobilium</i>, and the <i>Tabulae primi mobilis</i>. The correspondence is cordial but doctrinally fenced: Magini admired Tycho's data while remaining geocentric. (Cf. the later Magini exchanges 16000304001 / 16010124124 and the Baldi self-monument thread.)</li><li><b>The scientific spine.</b></li><li><b>The fixed-star catalogue, withheld.</b> Tycho will not yet send his <b>1000-star catalogue</b> (here promised at \"500 or even 600\" in the printed subset), fearing winter interception en route through \"the domains of diverse lords\"; but the <b><i>Progymnasmata</i> Tome I</b> (on the New Star of 1572) is at his own Uraniborg press, \"almost to the colophon,\" and will carry the restored star-places \"next summer.\" He urges Magini to delay reissuing his <i>Ephemerides</i> until Tycho's restitutions are out, since the <b>Alphonsine and Copernican</b> fixed-star restitutions \"deviate by almost the whole heaven\" — even <b>Spica</b> (Copernicus's sole reference star) is misplaced.</li><li><b>The Mars \"particular inequality.\"</b> A new <b>anomaly</b> unknown to the ancients and to Copernicus, \"analogous to neither of their hypotheses,\" most perceptible in <b>Mars</b> because the solar circuit is largest relative to the Martian orbit. Tycho denies Mars is \"unobservable\" (a Pliny error), claiming it gives him <i>less</i> trouble than Mercury or Venus. He wants two further <b>acronychal</b> (opposition) observations — the <b>June-1591</b> opposition near solar <b>perigee</b> (all-night visibility) and the opposition <b>two years later</b> in <b>Pisces</b> near the Sun's mean elongations — where the <b>Alphonsine–Copernican</b> discrepancy \"exceeds nine degrees.\" He doubts both Ptolemy's and Copernicus's Mars <b>eccentricity/apogee</b>, suspecting Copernicus fitted his determination \"at pleasure\" to his assumption (Mars errs 3–4° from the sky within Copernicus's own lifetime).</li><li><b>The world-system &amp; fluid heavens (the doctrinal heart).</b> The <b>Tychonic system</b> (\"discovered from the heaven itself, not from books\") eliminates Ptolemaic incongruities and <b>Copernican absurdities</b>; the <b>Mars orb crossing the Sun's</b> (<i>bifariam transeat</i>) is no objection, because the <b>solid adamantine spheres</b> are a groundless Aristotelian fiction. Proof: <b>five comets</b> observed in the \"highest aether,\" moving freely, accompanying no solid orb; and <b>refraction</b>, which vanishes above ~45° and arises from <b>vapours near the Earth</b>, shows the celestial transparency is not solid matter. The <b>Mars-parallax</b> result — Mars at opposition \"nearer the Earth than the Sun itself,\" demonstrated <b>about the end of 1582</b> with Mars lofty in <b>Cancer</b>, its parallax \"a little greater than the solar\" — forces the disjunction: <b>either Copernicus or Tycho</b>. But Copernicus's <b>triple Earth-motion</b> Tycho promises to refute \"theologically, physically, <i>and</i> mathematically\"; and the <b>comets</b> (some moving opposite the Sun, undisturbed) \"gainsay the annual motion of the Earth.\" Hence the Mars-circuit crossing the solar orb is admissible.</li><li><b>The Werner critique.</b> Tycho had already (via <b>Paul Fabricius</b>, the Imperial Mathematician, through <b>Hagecius</b>) examined <b>Johann Werner's</b> <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> (1522): Werner's <b>three foundation stars</b> were \"fashioned at pleasure\" not observed — he keeps the ancient <b>latitudes</b> yet assumes new <b>declinations</b> to force the received <b>longitude</b>-differences, which \"cannot cohere.\" Only <b>Spica</b> is near right (off by 1′). Neither Werner nor Copernicus noticed that stellar <b>latitudes change with the altered obliquity of the Ecliptic</b> (a Tychonic finding).</li><li><b>Magini's <i>Theoricae</i>.</b> Praised for reducing Copernicus \"to the Ptolemaic form,\" but the <b>equant</b>'s departure from uniform motion about its own centre would be \"more conveniently excused by small circles\" (epicyclets), and the <b>reality of the orbs</b> is \"altogether superfluous.\" Horatian tag: <i>\"Est aliquid prodire tenus, si non datur ultra\"</i> (Horace, <i>Epist.</i> I.1.32).</li><li><b>The eclipse &amp; longitude request.</b> Tycho asks for Italian <b>eclipse</b> timings (Rome, Bologna) to fix the <b>meridian/longitude difference</b>. He reports the <b>last solar eclipse</b> at Uraniborg: first contact <b>6h51m</b> after midnight, end at <b>9h00m</b>, only <b>4 digits</b> (⅓) covered though the canons promised <b>8</b> — and asks what Italy saw (where it \"ought to have been nearly total\"). He has commissioned, via <b>Gellius</b>, an instrument for Bologna to measure southern stars and Italian latitudes.</li><li><b>The latitude-constancy demonstration (against Domenico Maria Novara).</b> Magini, following <b>Domenico Maria Novara</b> (Copernicus's Bologna teacher), held in his <i>Tabulae secundorum mobilium</i> prolegomena that <b>terrestrial latitudes drift</b>. Tycho refutes this with gnomon-shadow data from <b>Pliny</b> (<i>Nat. Hist.</i> II.72) reduced to the <b>Sun's centre</b>: <b>Rome</b> = 41°54′ (Pliny) vs ~42° (Regiomontanus 1462) — change of only ~6′; <b>Venice</b> = 45°16′ (matching <b>Pietro Pitati</b>); <b>Ancona</b> = 44°10′ (Pliny) vs the neoterics' 43°40′ (\"a careless half-degree\"). Domenico Maria's claimed <b>1°10′</b> drift cannot survive. He plans to measure <b>Alexandria</b> (to test Ptolemy) and notes <b>Copernicus erred 2¾′</b> in the <b>Frauenburg</b> latitude (verified by Tycho's own man sent with an exact instrument). Greek tag: <i>Tecmirijs</i> = <b>τεκμήρια</b>, \"proofs/sure signs.\"</li><li><b>The reproduced table (internally verified).</b> The Regiomontanus 1462 January reduction is arithmetically self-consistent and confirms the readings:</li><li><b>Day 2:</b> corrected altitude 26°12′30″ (= 26°12′0″ + 2′45″ parallax − 2′15″ refraction = +30″ net); + declination 21°40½′ S = <b>equator altitude 47°53′0″</b>; 90° − 47°53′0″ = <b>pole 42°7′0″</b>. ✓</li><li><b>Day 11:</b> corrected altitude 27°51′50″; + declination 20°8′0″ S = <b>equator altitude 47°59′50″</b>; 90° − 47°59′50″ = <b>pole 42°0′10″</b>. ✓ (One small wrinkle: 27°50′0″ + 2′40″ − 1′50″ nets +50″ → 27°50′50″, not 27°51′50″; the +1′ slip is in the source's <i>raw→corrected</i> line for day 11, but it does not propagate — the declination-sum and pole reproduce 42°0′10″ exactly, so the result stands. Reproduced as printed, not silently emended.) The zodiac glyphs ♑ (Capricorn) / ♒ (Aquarius) mark the Sun's sign; \"M\" = <i>meridionalis</i> (southern declination).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Tycho's Danish former assistant, then travelling in Italy, the courier of the comet book and Tycho's Bologna agent); <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b> (the <i>Prutenic Tables</i>' author, \"Copernicus's enricher\"); <b>Paul Fabricius</b> (<i>D. Fabritio</i>, Imperial Mathematician at Vienna); <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b> (<i>Thaddaeus Haggecius</i>, the Prague go-between — cf. Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590); <b>Johann Werner</b> of Nuremberg; <b>Domenico Maria Novara</b> (<i>Dominicus Maria Ferrariensis</i>, d. 1504, Copernicus's teacher); <b>Pietro Pitati</b> (Veronese calendariographer); <b>Regiomontanus</b>; <b>Pliny</b>; <b>Ptolemy</b>, <b>Copernicus</b>, <b>Aristotle</b>. Places: <b>Padua, Bologna, Rome, Venice, Ancona, Alexandria, Frauenburg (Frombork), Frankfurt</b> (the book-fair postal channel), <b>Uraniborg</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> A clean <b>style code-switch</b>: Magini (Catholic Bologna) wrote <i>\"Idibus Septembris anni recogniti\"</i> = <b>13 September, New Style</b> (\"the reformed year\"); Tycho (Lutheran Denmark) received it <i>\"8 Calendarum Decembris anni veteris Iuliani\"</i> = <b>24 November, Old Style</b> (<i>ante diem VIII Kal. Dec.</i>), and dates his reply <i>\"Calendis Decembris, Anni veteris Iuliani\"</i> = <b>1 December O.S.</b> The header leads with the as-written O.S. date and matches the catalogue label.</li><li><b>OCR slips (transcription, kept verbatim, corrected in translation).</b> <i>inaequalitales</i> → <i>inaequalitates</i> (\"inequalities\"); <i>exactura</i> → <i>exactiora</i> (\"more exact,\" <i>donec plura et <b>exactiora</b> proferantur</i>); the variant spelling <i>scrupolose</i> (for <i>scrupulose</i>) is left as printed. None affects sense.</li><li><b>What it is.</b> A postscript (\"Postscriptum, ut vocant\" — Tycho's own wry Latin for the genre) to a letter to Giovanni Antonio Magini of 1 December 1590, sent to accompany an Ephemeris for the coming year (1591) enclosed with the letter — the postscript explains the enclosure and how to read it.</li><li><b>The enclosure.</b> The Ephemeris was derived from Tycho's own observations and \"demonstrations reduced into numbers\" — i.e. his own emerging solar theory, distinct from both the Alphonsine and Copernican/Prutenic reckonings then in scholarly use (the same two systems named a few lines earlier in this letter's published tail).</li><li><b>\"The former volume.\"</b> Tycho refers Magini to \"the first chapter of the former Tome\" for the underlying reasoning — plausibly his still-unfinished treatise on the new star of 1572 and the Tychonic solar and lunar theories, later completed by Tengnagel and Kepler's circle and published posthumously as the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (1602/1610) [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000)]. At this date (1590) the work was still in progress, consistent with Tycho speaking of Magini's future possession of it (\"ubi illum adeptus fueris\").</li><li><b>Independent corroboration.</b> This exact Latin postscript text is quoted, essentially verbatim, in Antonio Favaro's edition of Tycho's correspondence with Magini, <i>Carteggio inedito di Ticone Brahe... con Giovanni Antonio Magini</i> (Bologna, 1886) — the recovered text therefore matches an independently published nineteenth-century critical transcription rather than resting on the ingest alone [Tier 1: Favaro, <i>Carteggio inedito</i> (Bologna, 1886)].</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, programmatically continuous letter — the Mars-parallax/fluid-heavens argument, the Tychonic-system defence, the Werner critique, the <i>Theoricae</i> assessment, and the latitude-constancy demonstration with the Regiomontanus table — rendered securely throughout; register (learned, generous-but-doctrinally-firm scientific courtesy) preserved, and the controlled glossary (parallax, refraction, eccentricity, apogee, equant, anomaly, acronychal, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i>, <i>salvari</i>, the epistolary formulae) held consistent with the surrounding correspondence and with Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590. The numerals are cross-verified two ways: by the table's internal arithmetic (declination + corrected altitude → equator altitude → pole elevation, to the second) <b>and now by the §1 Dreyer page-collation (done 2026-06-02, vol. VII pp. 295–298), which confirms all 16 table cells and every prose crux to the arcsecond.</b> The collation reservation that previously capped the grade is therefore <b>lifted</b>. Two minor residues remain: (1) the <b>+1′ raw→corrected wrinkle</b> on the day-11 table line — now confirmed to be <b>Dreyer's own reading</b> (reproduced, not emended; non-propagating); (2) the two minor OCR slips, editorially corrected and declared. Neither obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>The Latin text itself is independently corroborated against Favaro's 1886 edition, which raises confidence in the source text considerably. The one soft point is identifying 'anterioris Tomi' ('the former volume') with certainty — the reading given (the unfinished Progymnasmata) is the best fit on internal and biographical grounds but is an inference, not a text-internal certainty. Upgrade path: consult Favaro's own footnotes to this letter, or TBOO vol. VII, for editorial commentary glossing 'anterioris Tomi'.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 12 slice pages as page images (chunks 1-4, 5-8, 9-12) and collated the full letter against Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.289-299 (p.300 is the start of the following Rantzau letter, confirmed by the new heading/salutation there, correctly outside this letter's scope). Checked opening and closing formulas verbatim; the full recipient heading; every proper name (Gellius Sascerides, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Erasmus Reinhold, Pliny, Regiomontanus, Domenico Maria Ferrariense/Novara, Pitati, Werner, Paul Fabricius, Thaddaeus Hagecius); every date (Ides of September N.S. = 13 Sept correctly glossed, 8 Kal. Dec. O.S. = 24 Nov correctly glossed, 1 Dec 1590, end of 1582, June/biennio-post Mars configurations); every number (500/600-star catalogue, nine degrees Alphonsine-Copernican Mars discrepancy, five comets, solar eclipse 6h51m first contact / 9h0m last contact / four digits obscured vs eight digits and double the lunar interposition predicted by the canons, 2 3/4 minutes Copernicus's Frauenburg-latitude error, 1 1/6 degree Domenico Maria discrepancy); and the full Regiomontani/Plinian Rome-Venice-Ancona latitude table (all P/M/S figures for both 2 and 11 January 1462, and the derived 48d22m/16m/48d6m/41d54m Rome chain, the 45d16m Venice figure, the 44d10m/43d40m Ancona figures) -- every figure in the English matches Dreyer's printed page exactly, and source_text matches Dreyer verbatim throughout (no TEXT_ERROR). Bracketed identificatory glosses ([Novara], [Pietro Pitati], [Paul Fabricius], [Tadeas Hajek], N.S./O.S. date glosses) are accurate and clearly marked. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found anywhere in this long, technically dense letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15900911001", "next": "brahe-t_15910801001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900911001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Magini's receipt of the second book of De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis through Gellius Sascerides and notes the letter from Bologna, received 24 November.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901201003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901207001", "d": "1590-12-07", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Henrik Rantzau — Uraniborg, 7 December 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE INPRIMIS CONSPICUO, DOMINO HENRICO RANZOVIO, DOMINO DE BREITENBERGA ET REGIO IN HOLSATIA AC DITHMARSIA GUBERNATORI, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO. BREDENBERG. S</p><p>NON desinam, Nobilissime et praeclarissime vir, te crebrioribus literis compellare, vel potius inter graviores tuas, cum publicas tum privatas, occupationes interpellare, donec aliquid tandem responsi a te receperim. Ternas enim aut etiam quaternas per aestatem et non ita dudum ad te dedi literas, quibus nihil rescriptum est. Cuperem autem quam maxime, te operum nostrorum Mathematicorum maturationem et publicationem tuo auxilio atque consilio, quod et te lubenter facturum non diffido, promovere, idque ea potissimum ratione, ut de Chartopaeo idoneo mihi primo quoque tempore, cum omnia, quae ad opificium illud requiruntur, iam hîc in procinctu sint, prospiciatur. Si itaque CASPARUS ille, quem ab ERICO LANGIO accepi, in tua, uti mihi relatum est, laborat Moletrina, peroptarem illum ad me remitti, si modo absque tuo dispendio eius opera carere poteris. Neque enim illi amplius imputabitur, quod sex Ioachimicis a Procuratore meo Lubecensi, IOACHIMO BUCKIO, praeter voluntatem meam abreptis, clam se subduxerit. Id illi, te etiam uno vel altero verbo pro ipso, ut tutius huc accedat, intercedente, lubens condonabo atque in posterum tum salario tum alijs necessarijs illi sufficienter prospiciam. Persuade saltem, ut audacter et certo citoque huc veniat. Erit illud absque illius damno nec sine emolumento.</p><p>STURORUM in Svecia olim celebrium gentilitia insignia necdum depicta conquirere licuit, utut multos, tam Danos quam Svecos, hoc nomine solicitârim. Inter quos etiam fuit Doctorculus quidam Italus, apud Ducem CAROLUM in Svecia medicinam faciens. Qui cum praeterito vere me hîc invisisset, insignium illorum procurationem non invite in se recepit. At cum postea aestate elapsa semel ad me scripserit, nullam eorum mentionem fecit, sed saltem πάρεργα nonnulla, de quibus non tam curiose cum illo egeram, adducebat. Interim tamen cognovi a quibusdam Danis Nobilibus, STURORUM arma prae se ferre piscem quendam, eodem nomine Stör appellatum, fluvio cuidam incumbentem. Verum incerti erant, an veterum in Svecia STURORUM haec ipsa fuerint arma, anque aliud praeterea additum vel ademptum habuerint. Si quid certius posthac de his cognovero, te de ijs commonefaciam.</p><p>Adiunxi hîc terna Exemplaria meae effigiei nuper Amsterodami cupro expressae, sed citra voluntatem meam et praemonitionem nimijs monilibus onustae, nec omnia decenter, prout praedelineatum erat, imitantis; ut ob id aliam illic confieri curem. Vellem autem te obnixe rogatum, ut, si per alias tuas seriores curas licuerit, Epigramma quoddam argutum et acutum in hanc atque Operum meorum Astronomicorum, quibus praefigetur, celebrationem concinnare non graveris; idemque, ut ab alijs in Holsatia atque vicinis locis excellentibus Poëtis, quales illic nonnullos reperiri non dubito, praestetur, procurare. Si quid nostra Venula Pegasea in hac Hvenula Uraniburgica vicissim tibi et caeteris recinere sustinuerit, equidem id Musis caeteris Uraniae primaevae, imprimisque sorore Calliopea obsecundantibus efficere non supersedebo.</p><p>Scripsi etiam semel atque iterum filio tuo GERARDO de Privilegio Hispanico, pro Operum meorum indemnitate, intercedente Secretario Proregis Portugaliae, MATTHIA ab Otten, Holsato, Ulissiponae agente, obtinendo, ut literis hac hyeme illuc mittendis ipsum denuo ea de re adiunctis etiam meis admoneret. Verum multo iam interlapso tempore, nihil ab eo responsi recipio, planeque aures obturare atque oculos nostris claudere videtur. Forte iam aliquanto plus dissitus, ut Hvenam amplius intueri nequeat, tam minutulae Insulae nullam curam habet. At vastum est coelum, quod spectat et scrutatur. Nec me eius inhabitatorem, ex quo, cum vicini essemus, fraterno animo nos invicem complectebamur, adeo cito ob tam modicam disiunctionem animo et memoria prorsus eliminare decuit. Anne sic Quam procul ex oculis tam procul ibit amor? Spero tamen ipsum brevi tam ad se, quam mei recordationem rediturum, atque amici officio benevole functurum. Quod ut citius fiat, te monitore adeoque instigatore opus esse video, quod et ut facias atque caeteris meis desiderijs subvenire non detrectes, etiam atque etiam te per amicitiam nostram diu multumque sartam tectam perduraturam, rogatum habeo. Vale et felici atque diutina fruere senecta.</p><p>Iterum cum tota domo suaviter dege et vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 7. Xbris Anno 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most distinguished man, conspicuous above all for virtue and erudition, Master Henrik Rantzau, Lord of Breitenberg and royal Governor in Holstein and Dithmarschen, his much-to-be-honoured friend. At Breitenberg. Greeting.</b></p><p>I shall not cease, most noble and most renowned man, to accost you with more frequent letters — or rather to interrupt you amid your weightier occupations, both public and private — until I have at length received some answer from you. For three or even four times over the summer, and not so long ago, I have sent you letters, to which nothing has been written back. Yet I should wish most greatly to advance, by your aid and counsel — which I do not doubt you will gladly give — the hastening and publication of our mathematical works; and this chiefly for this reason, that a suitable papermaker [Chartopaeus] be provided for me at the very earliest time, now that all the things required for that craft are already in readiness here. If, therefore, that Caspar whom I took on from Erik Lange is working, as has been reported to me, in your mill [Moletrina], I should most earnestly wish him to be sent back to me — provided only that you can do without his service without loss to yourself. For it shall no longer be charged against him that he slipped away secretly when six joachimsthalers had been snatched from him, against my will, by my agent at Lübeck, Joachim Bucke. This I shall gladly forgive him — you too interceding for him with a word or two, so that he may come hither the more safely — and hereafter I shall provide for him sufficiently, both in salary and in other necessaries. Only persuade him to come hither boldly and surely and quickly. It will be without loss to him, and not without profit.</p><p>The hereditary armorial bearings of the Sture, once famous in Sweden, I have not yet been able to obtain depicted, although I have solicited many on this account, both Danes and Swedes. Among them was also a certain little Italian doctor [Doctorculus], practising medicine in Sweden at the court of Duke Charles. When he had visited me here last spring, he took upon himself, not unwillingly, the procuring of those arms. But when afterwards, the summer being past, he wrote to me once, he made no mention of them, but only adduced a few side-matters [πάρεργα], about which I had not dealt with him so attentively. Meanwhile, however, I learned from certain Danish noblemen that the arms of the Sture display a certain fish, called by the same name <i>Stör</i> [sturgeon], lying upon a river. But they were uncertain whether these very arms were those of the ancient Sture in Sweden, and whether they had besides anything else added or taken away. If hereafter I learn anything more certain about these matters, I shall apprise you of it.</p><p>I have here enclosed three copies of my portrait lately engraved in copper at Amsterdam — but, against my will and without my forewarning, loaded with excessive ornaments [necklaces], and not imitating everything fittingly as had been pre-drawn; so that on this account I am seeing to having another made there. And I should wish to ask you earnestly that, if your more serious other cares permit, you would not find it burdensome to compose some witty and pointed epigram in celebration of this [portrait] and of my astronomical works, before which it is to be set; and that you procure that the same be furnished by other excellent poets in Holstein and neighbouring places, of whom I do not doubt that not a few are to be found there. If our little Pegasean vein [Venula Pegasea] in this little Hven of Uraniborg [Hvenula Uraniburgica] shall in turn undertake to sing back something to you and the rest, I for my part shall not refrain from doing so, with the other Muses of primeval Urania complying, and above all her sister Calliope.</p><p>I have also written once and again to your son Gerhard about obtaining a Spanish privilege, for the protection of my works, through the intercession of the Secretary of the Viceroy of Portugal, Matthias von Otten, a Holsteiner, acting at Lisbon — that, by letters to be sent thither this winter, he should again remind him of that matter, with mine too enclosed. But, much time having now elapsed, I receive no answer from him, and he seems quite to stop his ears and to close his eyes to my affairs. Perhaps, being now somewhat more far removed, so that he can no longer behold Hven, he has no care for so very tiny an island. But vast is the heaven, which he beholds and scrutinizes. Nor was it fitting that he should so quickly cast me — its inhabitant, with whom, when we were neighbours, he embraced and was embraced in a brotherly spirit — so utterly out of mind and memory, on account of so slight a separation. Is it thus: <i>the further from the eyes, the further love will go?</i> Yet I hope that he will shortly return both to himself and to remembrance of me, and will kindly perform the office of a friend. That this may be done the sooner, I see there is need of you as monitor and indeed as instigator; and that you do this, and not refuse to come to the aid of my other desires, I ask you again and again, by our friendship — long since established, and to endure long and much, kept whole and sound. Farewell, and enjoy a happy and long old age.</p><p>Again, live pleasantly with your whole household, and farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 7 December, in the year 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Uraniborg on Hven. The subscription <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand\") marks the letter as an autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Henrik Rantzau / Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the great Holstein humanist, statesman, and <i>Statthalter</i> (royal Governor) of the Danish king in the duchies of Holstein and Dithmarschen, seated at <b>Breitenberg</b> (Lat. <i>Breitenberga</i>; the subscript address <i>BREDENBERG</i>). A leading patron of letters and of Tycho; the two corresponded extensively. <i>Dominus de Breitenberga</i> = \"Lord of Breitenberg.\"</li><li><b>Place / address.</b> <i>Bredenberg</i> (Breitenberg, Rantzau's seat in Holstein) names the destination; the letter is <b>dated from Uraniborg</b> (per protocol §5.7, <i>Uraniburgum</i> → Uraniborg, on Hven). <i>Holsatia</i> = Holstein; <i>Dithmarsia</i> = Dithmarschen.</li><li><b>The papermaker and the printing project.</b> <i>operum nostrorum Mathematicorum maturationem et publicationem</i> — Tycho's drive to print his own works at Uraniborg (he had set up his own press and was establishing a paper-mill on Hven). <i>Chartopaeus</i> (Gk.-Lat. coinage, <i>χάρτης</i> + <i>-ποιός</i>) = <b>papermaker</b>; <i>opificium illud</i> = \"that craft\" (paper-making). <i>Moletrina</i> = \"mill\" (here Rantzau's paper-/grinding-mill). <i>Caspar</i> (taken on <i>ab Erico Langio</i>, from <b>Erik Lange</b>, the alchemist nobleman and Tycho's friend and brother-in-law-to-be) is the sought paper-worker. <i>sex Ioachimicis</i> = <b>six joachimsthalers</b> (silver <i>Joachimsthaler</i> coins). <i>Procurator … Lubecensis</i> = Tycho's agent/factor at <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Joachim Bucke</b>.</li><li><b>The Sture arms.</b> <i>STURORUM … gentilitia insignia</i> — the hereditary armorial bearings of the <b>Sture</b>, the great Swedish noble family. <i>Doctorculus quidam Italus … apud Ducem Carolum</i> — a \"certain little Italian doctor\" (diminutive, mildly contemptuous) at the court of <b>Duke Charles</b> of Södermanland (later <b>Charles IX of Sweden</b>). The pun: the Sture arms show a fish — German <i>Stör</i>, \"sturgeon\" — echoing the family name; <i>fluvio … incumbentem</i> = \"lying upon a river.\" Tycho preserves the uncertainty (<i>incerti erant, an …</i>) over whether these are the authentic ancient arms; the hedge is kept (no silent sharpening).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>πάρεργα</i> — \"side-matters, by-works, incidental trifles\" (sing. <i>πάρεργον</i>); transcribed as printed and glossed. Here = matters incidental to (and distracting from) the business of the arms.</li><li><b>The portrait.</b> <i>meae effigiei nuper Amsterodami cupro expressae</i> — Tycho's <b>portrait engraved in copper at Amsterdam</b> (<i>cupro expressae</i> = \"struck/expressed in copper,\" a copper-plate engraving), to be prefixed to his astronomical works. <i>nimijs monilibus onustae</i> = \"loaded with excessive necklaces/ornaments\" — Tycho's complaint that the engraver over-ornamented him against the <i>praedelineatum</i> (\"the pre-drawn [design]\"). He asks Rantzau for a celebratory <i>Epigramma</i> and to procure others from Holstein poets.</li><li><b>The poetic flourish.</b> <i>Venula Pegasea … Hvenula Uraniburgica</i> — \"our little Pegasean vein [of poetry] in this little Hven of Uraniborg\": <i>Venula</i> and <i>Hvenula</i> are playful diminutives (a small poetic \"vein\"; a \"little Hven\"), with a sound-play <i>Venula / Hvenula</i>. <i>Musis … Uraniae primaevae … sorore Calliopea</i> — the Muses of \"primeval Urania\" (Urania, Muse of astronomy) and her sister <b>Calliope</b> (Muse of epic/heroic poetry). The diminutives and wordplay are kept as wordplay, not flattened.</li><li><b>The Spanish privilege.</b> <i>filio tuo Gerardo</i> = Rantzau's son <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (1558–1627). <i>Privilegium Hispanicum … pro Operum meorum indemnitate</i> — a <b>Spanish printing-privilege</b> (copyright protection, \"for the indemnity of my works\"). <i>Secretario Proregis Portugaliae, Matthia ab Osten, Holsato, Ulissiponae agente</i> = the Secretary of the <b>Viceroy of Portugal</b> (then under the Spanish crown), <b>Matthias von der Osten</b>, a Holsteiner, <i>agente Ulissiponae</i> = \"acting at <b>Lisbon</b>\" (<i>Ulissipo / Olisipo</i>, Lisbon).</li><li><b>The \"vast heaven\" passage.</b> The conceit turns on Hven's smallness against the heaven the addressee \"beholds and scrutinizes\" (<i>spectat et scrutatur</i>) — i.e. astronomy itself dwarfs the island; Tycho reproaches the (unnamed) far-off correspondent for forgetting him. The referent of <i>ipsum / eius inhabitatorem</i> in this paragraph is the same person to whom Tycho complains of silence; he is the one now \"more far removed\" and unable to \"behold Hven.\" (Read by Dreyer as continuing the matter of the unanswered letters; Tycho keeps the figure deliberately allusive.)</li><li><b>Sententia / verse.</b> <i>Anne sic Quam procul ex oculis tam procul ibit amor?</i> — kept as a proverbial sententia and set off in the translation (\"<i>the further from the eyes, the further love will go?</i>\"); a variation on the <i>quantum oculis, animo tam procul ibit amor</i> topos (Propertius III.21.10), framed as a question. Rendered as a quoted maxim, not absorbed into the prose.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>S</i> (after the address) = <i>salutem [dicit]</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (§5.5). <i>Vale … Iterum … vale</i> = \"Farewell … Again … farewell\" (§5.5). <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 7. Xbris Anno 1590</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 7 December 1590.\" <i>Xbris</i> = <i>Decembris</i> (the <i>X-</i> = Roman numeral ten / <i>decem-</i> abbreviation for December); expanded to \"December.\" Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The as-written dateline (7 December 1590) agrees with the catalogue label; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Secretary's surname corrected from 'Osten' to 'Otten' in the Latin (Dreyer TBOO VII p.301); the English correspondingly corrected from 'Matthias von der Osten' to 'Matthias von Otten', removing an unsourced 'der' not present in either Latin form of the name.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the precise identity of the unnamed far-removed correspondent in the \"vast heaven\" paragraph, whom Tycho leaves allusive; and (2) the exact sense of <i>non … decenter … imitantis</i> (the engraving \"not fittingly imitating\" the design), where the participle's construction is slightly loose. The Sture-arms hedge is preserved as Tycho left it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.299-301 (page images, read visually). Page 302 was confirmed to belong to the following letter (Tycho to Bartholomaeus Scultetus, dated 8 Dec 1590), so nothing of this letter falls outside the read range. All other checked names/numbers (Casparus, Erico Langio, Ioachimo Buckio, sex Ioachimicis, Sture arms/Stör, terna Exemplaria, filio tuo Gerardo, Datae Uraniburgi 7. Xbris Anno 1590) match Dreyer exactly; the letter carries no postscript in either source or Dreyer, consistent. One proper-name TEXT_ERROR found (Osten for Dreyer's Otten, compounded by an added 'der' in the English); per protocol this mandates grade C even though it does not disturb the letter's overall sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text gives this Portuguese-court secretary's surname as 'Osten', but Dreyer prints 'Otten'; the corrupted name propagated into the English, which additionally inserts an unsourced 'der' not present in either Latin form of the name. Dreyer prints: <i>MATTHIA ab Otten, Holsato, Ulissiponae agente</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.299-302 (page images, not OCR text layer). The TEXT_ERROR is confirmed at the top of p.301: 'Proregis Portugaliæ, MATTHIA ab Otten, Holfato, Ulißiponæ agente,'. The corrupted surname had propagated into the English along with an unsourced 'der'; both are now corrected. No other discrepancies found on collation of the full letter against the printed pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15900402001", "next": "brahe-t_15930109001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Presses Rantzau for help procuring a papermaker needed to resume printing at Uraniborg, naming specifically a Caspar who may be working at Rantzau's mill.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901207001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15901208001", "d": "1590-12-08", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Bartholomaeus Scultetus — Uraniborg, 8 December 1590 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica </i>· MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ERUDITISSIMOQUE VIRO BARTHOLOMAeO SCULTETO, RERUM MATHEMATICARUM EXCELLENTER PERITO, CIVI GORLICENSI, VETERI AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.</p><p>TOTA hac praeterlapsa aestate nihil a te literarum accepi, dilecte mi SCULTETE, sive quod nihil scripseris, ob tabellariorum penuriam, aut Bacho, cui † proces, id non admittente, sive quod literae istae interciderint. Quicquid id est, ego nihilo minus has ad te dare, ut[ut] pluribus alijs implicitus occupationibus, non gravabor. Expectas forte Tomum nostrum primum recentiorum coeli Phaenomenorum, quem etiam iam dudum obtinuisses, si papyri impressoriae defectus nostros conatus non retardasset. Licet enim proprium molendinum eius conficiendae gratia hîc magnis impensis extrui curaverim, omniaque huc requisita iam parata sint, attamen chartopaeo, quem in Germaniam pro coëmendis veteramentis amandâram, se subducente, feriatur opus, cum eiusmodi artificem denuo conquirere non in promptu sit. Scripsi tamen in diversa loca pro chartopaeo idoneo; quo accepto, spero me hac hyeme de sufficiente papyro typographiae meae prospecturum, sicque tomi nostri ascititiorum coeli corporum propediem maturabuntur, ut ad perennium illorum considerationem atque in demonstrationes et numeros resolutionem propius accedere liceat. Memini, te mihi aliquando scripsisse, quod magnam farraginem observationum coelestium, Lipsiae aliquando compositarum, reserves; cupio igitur, ut quicquid eiusmodi in archivis tuis habes, illud depromptum atque descriptum mecum communices. Qua de re puto me tibi antea scripsisse. Nam licet non ignorem, observationes istas minime ratas esse, utpote instrumentis vel nullis vel fallacibus acceptas: tamen cum saepenumero per lineas rectas vel alias dispositiones ad stellas fixas solo oculari intuitu facta sit collimatio, ego, qui loca fixarum stellarum ad amussim emendata habeo, aliqualem Planetarum positum hinc ratiocinabor, et multa alias in his limitabo atque concinnabo, diligenti in singulis adhibita circumspectione; quo, si non exacto, haud nimium tamen aberranti usui, sicubi opus fuerit, destinentur. Eclipses quoque amborum luminarium, inprimis vero Lunae, sive Gorlicij sive alibi a te vel alijs fideliter observatas, aut aliunde conquisitas, nancisci desidero, quo Meridianorum praesertim differentiam hinc discernam. Si quid superesset scriptorum atque observationum RHAeTICI, id totum mihi prece et pretio comparari averem, quemadmodum et Witichiana, si quae adhuc residua sunt. Tu in his atque alijs eiusmodi, fac, quod potes, et amici desiderijs gratificari non grave duxeris. Ego sumptus libenter rependam inveniarque vicissim gratus. Addidi his terna meae effigiei exemplaria, iam nuper in Belgio cupro expressa, rogans, ut, si qui apud vos, aut in vi[ci]nia reperiantur excellentes Poëtae, quales illic dari nullum est dubium, ut nervosa aliquot et arguta epigrammata in iconem hanc atque operum meorum Astronomicorum celebrationem, delectationis gratia, concinnent, efficere velis, et prima quaque opportunitate cum ceteris, de quibus dixi, huc in Daniam ad me transmittere. Praestiteris hac in parte rem gratam omnique officio vicissim compensandam. Curo id ipsum in alijs quoque regionibus fieri, ut, collatis omnibus, quae maxime placuerint, seligam. Nam et ego canora Poësi non parum afficior, soleoque interdum a severioribus occupationibus hac me recreare. Plura nunc non addo, cum tu nihil ad ultimas meas rescripseris. Fac igitur, ut propediem aliquid a te responsi habeam, moxque copiosiora a me expectabis.</p><p>Vale!<br>Datae Uraniburgi die 8. Dec. Anno 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man Bartholomaeus Scultetus, excellently skilled in mathematical matters, citizen of Görlitz, his old and most beloved friend.</b></p><p>All this past summer I received no letters from you, my beloved Scultetus — whether because you wrote nothing, on account of the scarcity of couriers, or because Bacchus, to whom †<i>proces</i>[?], would not allow it, or because those letters of yours went astray. Whatever the cause, I for my part shall nonetheless not find it a burden to send you these lines, however entangled I am in many other occupations. Perhaps you are expecting our first volume of the more recent phenomena of the heavens, which you would indeed have obtained long since, had a shortage of printing-paper not held back our efforts. For although I have, for the sake of producing it, had a mill of my own built here at great expense, and all the requisites for it are now ready, nevertheless — since the paper-maker, whom I had sent off into Germany to buy up rags, has slipped away — the work stands idle, because to seek out a craftsman of that kind anew is not a thing ready to hand. I have, however, written to various places for a suitable paper-maker; and once one is obtained, I hope this winter to make provision of sufficient paper for my printing-house, and so the volumes of ours on the adscititious bodies of the heavens will soon be brought to maturity, so that one may approach more nearly to the consideration of those perennial bodies and to their resolution into demonstrations and numbers. I recall that you once wrote to me that you keep in reserve a great medley of celestial observations, made at one time at Leipzig; I desire therefore that whatever of that kind you have in your archives, you would bring it forth, write it out, and share it with me. About this matter I think I have written to you before. For although I am well aware that those observations are by no means to be relied upon, taken as they were with instruments either non-existent or deceptive, nevertheless — since the sighting was very often made toward the fixed stars by straight lines or other arrangements, by the bare gaze of the eye alone — I, who have the places of the fixed stars corrected to the exact line, shall from this reason out some approximate position of the planets, and shall in many cases set bounds to these and bring them into order, with diligent circumspection applied to each particular; so that they may be destined for a use which, if not exact, yet is not too greatly erring, wherever there shall be need. The eclipses too of both luminaries — and especially of the Moon — whether observed faithfully by you or by others at Görlitz or elsewhere, or gathered from some other quarter, I long to come by, so that from these I may discern in particular the difference of meridians. If any of the writings and observations of Rhaeticus should survive, I should be eager to procure the whole of it for myself by entreaty and by price — and likewise the Wittich materials, if any are still left over. In these and other matters of the kind, do what you can, and may you not reckon it a burden to gratify a friend's desires. I shall gladly repay the expense and shall be found grateful in my turn. I have added to these [letters] three copies of my likeness, lately engraved in copper in the Low Countries, asking that — if any excellent poets are to be found among you, or in the neighbourhood (and there is no doubt that such are to be had there) — you would arrange for them to compose, for the sake of pleasure, some sinewy and pointed epigrams upon this image and in celebration of my astronomical works, and to send them here to me into Denmark at the very first opportunity, together with the other things of which I have spoken. In this you will have rendered a welcome service, to be repaid in turn by every good office. I am seeing to it that this same thing is done in other regions also, so that, all being brought together, I may select those that please most. For I too am affected not a little by tuneful poetry, and am wont at times to refresh myself with it from my more serious occupations. I add no more now, since you have written nothing in reply to my last. See to it, therefore, that I soon have some answer from you, and presently you will expect more copious matter from me.</p><p>Farewell!<br>Given at Uraniborg on the 8th day of December, in the year 1590.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Bartholomaeus Scultetus</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomäus Schultz, 1540–1614), the Görlitz mathematician, cartographer and astronomer, later (from 1592) burgomaster of the city. He had been Tycho's fellow-student at Leipzig in the 1560s and remained a lifelong correspondent; the two had exchanged observations of the comet of 1577. The dataset gives no MS witness; the text-of-record is the <b>Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica</b> (the Lusatian historical-literary miscellany in which the letter was printed), per the source's `edition` field. Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 93 et seq.) prints letters of this correspondence.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Gorlicensis / Gorlicij</i> = <b>Görlitz</b> (Lusatia); <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-house on the island of Hven; <i>in Daniam</i> = \"into Denmark\"; <i>Belgium</i> = the <b>Low Countries</b> (here the place of the portrait engraving).</li><li><b><i>Tomum nostrum primum recentiorum coeli Phaenomenorum</b></i> — \"our first volume of the more recent phenomena of the heavens\": Tycho's planned <b>first volume on the recent celestial phenomena</b>, i.e. the comet/new-star series eventually realised as the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> and <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the latter printed 1588, still being completed/distributed). <i>Tomi nostri ascititiorum coeli corporum</i> — \"the volumes of ours on the <b>adscititious</b> (i.e. newly-arisen, non-permanent) bodies of the heavens\": comets and new stars, contrasted with the <i>perennia</i> (the fixed, perennial bodies). <i>ascititiorum</i> rendered \"adscititious\" to keep Tycho's own distinction between transient and permanent celestial bodies; not smoothed to \"additional.\"</li><li><b>Paper-mill passage.</b> <i>proprium molendinum … extrui curaverim</i> — Tycho's own <b>paper-mill</b> built at great expense at Hven to supply his press; <i>chartopaeus</i> = <b>paper-maker</b> (Greek-derived; both occurrences); <i>veteramenta</i> = \"rags/old materials\" (the rag stock from which paper was made), here \"rags\"; <i>typographiae meae</i> = \"my printing-house.\" The whole is the documented Hven paper-mill / press supply crisis of c. 1590.</li><li><b><i>farraginem observationum … Lipsiae … compositarum</b></i> — \"a medley of observations made at <b>Leipzig</b>\": Scultetus's early Leipzig-era observations, which Tycho wishes copied out of his archives.</li><li><b>Technical / observational.</b> <i>loca fixarum stellarum ad amussim emendata</i> — \"the places of the fixed stars corrected <b>to the exact line</b> (<i>ad amussim</i>),\" i.e. precisely; <i>collimatio … solo oculari intuitu</i> — \"sighting by the bare gaze of the eye alone,\" contrasted with proper instruments; <i>aliqualem Planetarum positum … ratiocinabor</i> — \"I shall reason out some <b>approximate</b> position of the planets.\" The hedges (<i>aliqualem</i>, \"minime ratas,\" <i>si non exacto haud nimium tamen aberranti</i>) are preserved, not sharpened: Tycho explicitly judges Scultetus's old data unreliable yet usable within bounds.</li><li><b><i>Eclipses … amborum luminarium … quo Meridianorum … differentiam hinc discernam</b></i> — \"eclipses of both luminaries [Sun and Moon] … so that I may discern the <b>difference of meridians</b>\": lunar (and solar) eclipses used as simultaneous events to determine the <b>difference in longitude (meridian difference)</b> between observing stations. <i>eclipses (Lunae)</i> per glossary §5.1.</li><li><b><i>RHAeTICI</b></i> — <b>Rheticus</b> (Georg Joachim Rheticus, 1514–1574), Copernicus's only disciple; Tycho is eager to acquire any surviving writings and observations of his \"by entreaty and by price.\" Spelled <i>RHAeTICI</i> with the <i>ae</i>-ligature in the source.</li><li><b><i>Witichiana</b></i> — \"the <b>Wittich</b> materials\": the papers and books of <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Wrocław (Breslau) mathematician whose work on prosthaphaeresis and on the geo-heliocentric arrangement bore on Tycho's own system and his quarrel with Ursus. Tycho actively pursued Wittich's <i>Nachlass</i> after his death; the request here (\"if any are still left over\") fits that well-documented hunt. The neuter plural adjective <i>Witichiana</i> = \"the things pertaining to Wittich.\"</li><li><b><i>terna meae effigiei exemplaria … in Belgio cupro expressa</b></i> — \"three copies of my likeness, lately engraved in copper in the Low Countries\": copies of the <b>engraved portrait of Tycho</b>, sent with a request for celebratory <b>epigrams</b> (<i>nervosa et arguta epigrammata</i>, \"sinewy and pointed epigrams\") to adorn the image and praise his astronomical works — Tycho's habitual cultivation of a learned poetic apparatus around his self-presentation.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> Opening superscription translated in full per §4; <i>Vale!</i> = \"Farewell!\" (§5.5); <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi die 8. Dec. Anno 1590</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, the 8th day of December, the year 1590\" (<b>Old Style</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue `label` (1590. 8. december. Uraniborg); no discrepancy. The header date is the as-written dateline date.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Crux (graded below).</b> <i>\"aut Bacho, cui † proces, id non admittente\"</i> — the printed source carries a <b>dagger (†)</b> marking a corruption, and the word <i>proces</i> is not construable Latin in context. The sense of the surrounding clause is secure — Tycho jokingly lists, among the reasons Scultetus may not have written, that <b>Bacchus</b> (i.e. wine / good-fellowship / the season's drinking) \"would not allow it.\" But the exact words governed by <i>cui</i> (\"to whom …\") cannot be recovered from the corrupt text: plausible underlying readings (e.g. <i>cui [tu] preces [debes]</i> \"to whom you owe your prayers,\" or a verb such as <i>operam das</i> \"to whom you devote yourself\") are conjecture only. The corrupt form <i>† proces</i> is <b>kept verbatim in the Source block and in the translation</b>, flagged with the dagger and a bracketed query, and <b>not</b> smoothed into a fluent reading.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the letter as a whole is rendered securely (a clean Neo-Latin business letter on Tycho's press, paper-mill, the acquisition of Rheticus's and Wittich's papers, observation-sharing for meridian differences, and the portrait/epigram circulation); the single genuine crux is the editorially-daggered <i>\"Bacho, cui † proces\"</i>, whose governed phrase is textually corrupt and left unresolved per protocol rather than conjecturally restored.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.300-303 (Dreyer page images, slice pages 1-4) in full; the Sculteto letter runs pp.300-302, p.303 is the following letter (Maginus to Tycho) included as buffer. Collated the opening/closing lines and every proper name, sum, and date (Bartholomaeo Sculteto, Gorlicensi, Bacho, Rhaetici, Witichiana, 8 Dec. 1590) against the printed text; source_text and published_english match Dreyer word for word. No defects found. Note: published_grade was self-assigned C with no apparent basis - this is a clean letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15880817002", "next": "brahe-t_15920312001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that printing is stalled because the papermaker sent to Germany has absconded, leaving the Uraniborg mill idle and the first volume delayed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15901208001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910000001", "d": "1591-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Gellius Sascerides", "ck": "Gellius Sascerides", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Gellius Sascerides — [Uraniborg], autumn 1591 [O.S.] (catalogued 1591)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>VII, pp. 305–307 · MS witness: A = Bologna, Biblioteca dell'Archiginnasio, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici (autograph copy made by Gellius Sascerides within his own letter to Giovanni Antonio Magini, Padua, 14 March 1592; ed. Favaro,<i> Carteggio inedito di Ticone Brahe </i>…, letter VIII)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD GELLIUM SASCERIDEM.</p><p>.... Dici non potest quam mihi gratum fuerit, quod intellexerim Excellentissimum et praestantissimum MAGINUM Patavium venisse, illicque aestivum semestre consumere decrevisse, quodque Instrumentum illud Sextantis Astronomici, de quo ad te scripsi, per vos elaboretur. Spero enim vos aliquot Stellarum Observationes hac aestate illo intermediante illic obtenturos. An Organum omni ex parte rite constet, distantiae et declinationes stellarum transmissae patefacient. Cuperem Altitudines Meridianas Solis, tum etiam Affixarum Stellarum, praesertim quae hîc decliviores sunt, eodem diligentius observari, ut Refractionum ratio certius pateat. Eclipses quoque Lunares per Altitudines Fixarum illustrium sedulo notari, ut differentia meridianorum nostrorum innotescat, apprime consultum duxerim. Atque haec et similia ipsemet MAGINUS, etiam te absente, facile expediet. Si Planetarum quoque loca per distantias a fixis sideribus, ijs praesertim quae a nobis restituta Catalogus transmissus exhibet, subinde rimatus fuerit, utique quanta sit Coeli et Canonum Astronomicorum differentia experietur. Mars in ultimo Acronycho situ eundem ferme locum (digrediente ad ternos gradus Alphonsinorum calculo) coelitus designavit, quem rationes Coperniceae postulant, nisi quantum in simplici motu Solis atque Aequinoctiorum praecessione ab eo aberratum est, cum alias ♂ pernox factus, intra unum vel alterum gradum, nonnunquam etiam plures, calculum Coperniceum eludat. Quod vero nunc tam prope consenserit, id causae fuit, quod circa Perigaeum Solis illi opponebatur; idque nostram Hypothesium coelestium restitutionem evidenter confirmat; praesertim cum iuxta medias inter Apogaeum et Perigaeum Solare elongationes ♂ Acronychius factus, plurimum a Coperniceis numeris dissentire deprehendatur, idque in diversas partes, prout Eccentricitatis insinuatio postulat. Idem in reliquis duobus superioribus Planetis usu venit, licet in his ob orbium magnitudinem minus sentiatur. Concurrunt tamen et aliae quaedam causae, ex commensuratione veterum, adeoque ipsius COPERNICI, non satis accurate instituta; quae omnia non est huius loci extricare.</p><p>In operibus meis perficiendis excudendisque nihil hac aestate laboratum est, ob varia impedimenta et avocamenta minus grata; si hiems tranquillius et exoptabilius otium praebuerit, favente coelesti Numine, inchoatam telam pertexam, posteaquam cum praestantissimo MAGINO alijsque in Italia eruditis Philosophis id, quicquid erit, lubens communicari curabo. Si interea ipsius opera Privilegia a Pontifice et Duce Mantuae alijsque Italiae proceribus, quotquot impetrare licuerit, prout benevole pollicetur, obtinuero, rem praestiterit mihi summopere acceptam omnique gratitudinis significatione compensandam.</p><p>Canonem Radicum Quadratarum, quem molitur, avide desidero. Scio equidem tali ratione Tangentium ac Secantium Tabulas excusari posse, an vero promtiori compendio, nondum dixerim. Scripsissem ipsemet nunc MAGINO, sed ad matrem Aulae magistram (quae apud Reginam Fridrichsburgi diutius quam sperassem haerens, et proprijs commoditatibus destituta minus prospera fruitur valetudine) avocor, et pleraque alia, quae me a scriptione retardant, nunc incidunt obstacula. Interim eximium hunc et magni nominis Virum, qui mei, ut non obscure, tum ex ipsius tum etiam tuis litteris perspexi, est amantissimus, meo nomine diligenter resalutare, omniaque grati et benevoli erga ipsum animi officia sincere offerre ne supersedeas. Quamprimum otium et oportunitatem commodiorem nactus fuero, illi scribere et de rebus Astronomicis cum eo ulterius conferre (ex quo videam eum earum tam gnaviter cupidum, quam excellenter peritum) non intermittam.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Gellius Sascerides.</b></p><p>.... It cannot be told how welcome it was to me to have learned that the most excellent and most distinguished Magini has come to Padua, and has resolved to spend the summer half-year there, and that the instrument of that astronomical sextant, about which I wrote to you, is being worked up by you [pl.]. For I hope that this summer, with him as intermediary, you will obtain there observations of several of the stars. Whether the instrument is correctly set up in every part, the distances and declinations of the stars sent over [to me] will reveal. I could wish that the meridian altitudes of the Sun, and also of the fixed stars — especially those that are rather low here — be observed more carefully in the same place, so that the principle of the refractions may be more surely laid open. I should think it especially advisable, too, that lunar eclipses be diligently noted by the altitudes of conspicuous fixed stars, so that the difference of our meridians may become known. And these things and the like Magini himself, even in your absence, will easily dispatch. If he shall also from time to time have investigated the places of the planets by their distances from the fixed stars — especially those that the catalogue sent over presents as restored by us — he will assuredly find by trial how great is the difference between the heavens and the astronomical tables. Mars, in its last acronychal position, marked out from the heavens nearly the same place (the Alphonsine calculation diverging by three degrees) which the Copernican principles require — except in so far as it has erred therefrom in the simple motion of the Sun and in the precession of the equinoxes — whereas at other times Mars, become an all-night star [pernox], by one degree or another, sometimes even more, eludes the Copernican calculation. But that it now agreed so closely, the cause of this was that it stood in opposition to it [the Sun] near the perigee of the Sun; and this evidently confirms our restoration of the celestial hypotheses, especially since, when Mars becomes acronychal near the mean elongations between the solar apogee and perigee, it is found to disagree very greatly from the Copernican numbers — and that on opposite sides, as the working-in [insinuatio] of the eccentricity requires. The same comes about in the case of the remaining two superior planets, although in these, on account of the magnitude of the orbs, it is less perceived. Yet certain other causes also concur, arising from the commensuration of the ancients, and indeed of Copernicus himself, established not accurately enough; all of which it is not the place here to disentangle.</p><p>Toward the completing and the printing of my works nothing has been laboured this summer, on account of various hindrances and distractions less than welcome; if the winter affords a calmer and more desirable leisure, then, by the favour of the heavenly Deity, I shall weave through the web I have begun — after which I shall gladly see to it that whatever it may be is communicated to the most distinguished Magini and to the other learned philosophers in Italy. If meanwhile, through his good offices, I shall have obtained privileges from the Pontiff and from the Duke of Mantua and from the other magnates of Italy — as many as it may be allowed to procure, as he kindly promises — he will have rendered me a service most highly acceptable, and to be repaid with every token of gratitude.</p><p>The table of square roots, which he is working on, I eagerly long for. I know indeed that by such a method the tables of tangents and secants can be struck off, but whether by a readier shortcut, I would not yet say. I should myself have written now to Magini, but I am called away to my mother, the mistress of the [Queen's] court (who, lingering with the Queen at Frederiksborg longer than I had hoped, and deprived of her own comforts, enjoys less than prosperous health), and very many other obstacles that hinder me from writing now arise. Meanwhile, do not forbear to greet again diligently, in my name, this exceptional man of great renown — who, as I have perceived not obscurely, both from his own and from your letters, is most loving toward me — and to offer him sincerely all the offices of a grateful and well-disposed mind toward him. As soon as I have obtained more convenient leisure and opportunity, I shall not fail to write to him and to confer further with him on astronomical matters (since I see that he is as zealously desirous of them as he is excellently skilled in them).</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> to <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Gellius Sascerides Hafnianus, c. 1562–1612), the Danish scholar who had studied and worked at Uraniborg and was then travelling in Italy. Gellius is the intermediary between Tycho and Magini; in 1594 he would become entangled in the notorious broken-betrothal affair with Tycho's daughter Magdalene.</li><li><b>Person named.</b> <i>MAGINUS</i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of astronomy at Bologna; the third party in this exchange. Tycho asks Gellius to use Magini, then summering at <b>Padua</b> (<i>Patavium</i>), to procure Italian printing privileges and to make trial observations.</li><li><b>Transmission / dating crux.</b> The letter survives <b>only</b> as an excerpt that Gellius Sascerides copied into his own letter to Magini, written at <b>Padua, 14 March 1592</b> (the date the dataset's witness field records). That is the date of the <i>covering</i> letter, <b>not</b> of Tycho's letter. Within that covering letter Gellius states the source explicitly: <i>\"Habes, doctissime Magine, quae ad me Tycho suis in litteris, <b>elapso autumno</b>, de te scripsit\"</i> — \"you have what Tycho wrote to me about you in his letter, <b>last autumn</b>.\" Tycho's letter is therefore datable to <b>autumn 1591</b> (O.S.; Denmark used the Julian calendar). Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, pp. 305–307) accordingly catalogues it under <b>1591</b>, with no month or day; the dataset label \"1591. empty\" reflects this. The header gives the substantiated <b>autumn 1591</b> and appends the catalogue year. The leading <i>\"....\"</i> in the source marks that Dreyer prints only the portion Gellius transcribed, not a complete letter.</li><li><b>Place.</b> No place is named in the excerpt; Tycho was resident at <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven throughout 1591, and the reference to his mother's attendance on the Queen at Frederiksborg fixes the Danish setting. The bracketed <i>[Uraniborg]</i> is editorial.</li><li><b>Instruments / observation programme.</b> <i>Instrumentum illud Sextantis Astronomici</i> → the <b>astronomical sextant</b> Tycho had described to Gellius for building in Italy; <i>Organum</i> → the <b>instrument</b> (protocol §5.2). The verbs <i>patefacient / obtenturos / observari</i> are second-person plural (<i>per vos … vos … obtenturos</i>): Tycho addresses Gellius and his Italian collaborators together. <i>Altitudines Meridianas</i> → <b>meridian altitudes</b>; <i>Affixarum / Fixarum Stellarum</i> → <b>fixed stars</b>; <i>declinationes</i> → <b>declinations</b>; <i>Refractionum ratio</i> → \"the principle/account of <b>refractions</b>\" (§5.1). The lunar-eclipse-by-fixed-star-altitudes method is for determining the <b>difference of meridians</b> (longitude difference between observing sites).</li><li><b>Catalogue of restored stars.</b> <i>ijs … quae a nobis restituta Catalogus transmissus exhibet</i> — the star catalogue Tycho had sent, whose positions he describes as <b>restored</b> (<i>restituta</i>; cf. his programme of <i>astronomia instaurata</i>, §5.1). <i>Canonum Astronomicorum</i> → the <b>astronomical tables</b> (Alphonsine and Copernican), tested against the sky.</li><li><b>The Mars passage (technical).</b> <i>Mars in ultimo Acronycho situ</i> → \"Mars in its last <b>acronychal</b> position\" (<i>acronychus / pernox</i> = rising at sunset, i.e. at opposition; rendered \"all-night star\" where Tycho varies to <i>pernox</i>). <i>digrediente ad ternos gradus Alphonsinorum calculo</i> → \"the <b>Alphonsine</b> calculation diverging by three degrees.\" <i>rationes Coperniceae / calculum Coperniceum / Coperniceis numeris</i> → the <b>Copernican</b> principles / calculation / numbers. <i>simplici motu Solis atque Aequinoctiorum praecessione</i> → \"the simple motion of the Sun and the <b>precession</b> of the equinoxes.\" <i>circa Perigaeum Solis illi opponebatur</i> → Mars \"stood in <b>opposition</b> to it [the Sun] near the <b>perigee</b> of the Sun.\" <i>Apogaeum et Perigaeum Solare</i> → solar <b>apogee and perigee</b>; <i>elongationes</i> → <b>elongations</b>; <i>Eccentricitatis insinuatio</i> → \"the working-in [<i>insinuatio</i>] of the <b>eccentricity</b>\" — <i>insinuatio</i> is here rendered literally and flagged (see crux). <i>Hypothesium coelestium restitutionem</i> → \"the <b>restoration</b> of the celestial <b>hypotheses</b>,\" Tycho's claim that the close Mars opposition confirms his own (geo-heliocentric) world-system. <i>reliquis duobus superioribus Planetis</i> → \"the remaining two superior planets\" (Jupiter and Saturn); <i>orbium magnitudinem</i> → \"the magnitude of the <b>orbs</b>\" (§5.1). <i>commensuratione veterum</i> → \"the <b>commensuration</b> of the ancients.\"</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> The Mars glyph <b>♂</b> appears twice in the source for <i>Mars</i>; named \"Mars\" in the translation, glyph retained in the Source block (§5.3). (Favaro's edition renders the same glyph as the garbled <i>cT / &lt;/</i>.)</li><li><b>\"My works … the web I have begun.\"</b> <i>In operibus meis perficiendis excudendisque</i> → \"completing and <b>printing</b> my works\"; <i>inchoatam telam pertexam</i> → \"I shall weave through the web I have begun,\" the loom metaphor for finishing the <i>Astronomiae instauratae</i> programme (the press at Uraniborg). <i>favente coelesti Numine</i> → \"by the favour of the heavenly Deity,\" a piety formula, kept.</li><li><b>Privileges.</b> <i>Privilegia a Pontifice et Duce Mantuae</i> → printing <b>privileges</b> (copyright grants) from <b>the Pope</b> and the <b>Duke of Mantua</b> (Vincenzo I Gonzaga), to be sought through Magini's good offices.</li><li><b>Mathematics.</b> <i>Canonem Radicum Quadratarum</i> → \"the <b>table of square roots</b>\" Magini was preparing; <i>Tangentium ac Secantium Tabulas</i> → \"tables of <b>tangents and secants</b>\"; <i>promtiori compendio</i> → \"a readier shortcut.\" Tycho's hedge is preserved verbatim: <i>an vero promtiori compendio, <b>nondum dixerim</b></i> → \"but whether by a readier shortcut, <b>I would not yet say</b>.\"</li><li><b>\"My mother, the mistress of the court.\"</b> <i>matrem Aulae magistram</i> → <b>Beate Bille</b> (c. 1526–1605), Tycho's mother, who served as <i>hofmesterinde</i> (mistress of the court / chief lady-in-waiting) to the queen dowager <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b>. <i>apud Reginam Fridrichsburgi</i> → \"with the Queen at <b>Frederiksborg</b>\" (the royal castle at Hillerød). <i>minus prospera fruitur valetudine</i> → \"enjoys less than prosperous health\"; the litotes is kept.</li><li><b>Closing courtesies.</b> <i>resalutare … omniaque … officia sincere offerre ne supersedeas</i> → the request that Gellius return Tycho's greetings to Magini and offer every courtesy; <i>de rebus Astronomicis cum eo ulterius conferre</i> → \"to confer further with him on <b>astronomical matters</b>.\" The excerpt breaks off without a subscription, since Gellius transcribed only the substantive body.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> No dateline survives in the excerpt. Tycho's letter = autumn 1591 (O.S.); the covering letter that preserves it = Padua, 14 March 1592 (Gellius writing from Catholic Italy would have used New Style, but no style is internally specified — recorded, not converted). No in-letter date discrepancy beyond the covering-letter/source-letter distinction set out above.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the dating is externally verified (Favaro p. 209: <i>\"elapso autumno\"</i>). The one genuine lexical soft point, flagged not smoothed, is <i>Eccentricitatis insinuatio</i> (\"the working-in of the eccentricity\"): <i>insinuatio</i> in Tycho's technical usage means the gradual introduction/incorporation of the eccentric correction as Mars moves through the cycle, but the precise mechanical sense is not pinned down by the excerpt and is rendered literally rather than glossed to a modern term.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.304-308 (slice pages 1-5; the letter's end was located on p.307 so slice page 5/p.308 was not needed for this letter's content); p.304 holds the end of the Maginus letter and a Hagecius letter, the excerpted Sascerides letter (opening with an ellipsis in Dreyer, as printed) runs from p.305 through p.307. Collated the Mars/Copernican-calculation passage, the three-degree Alphonsine divergence, and every proper name (Maginus, Frederiksborg/Fridrichsburgi) against the printed text; all match source_text and published_english exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Biblioteca dell'Archiginnasio, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici, Citeret i et brev fra Gellius Sascerides til Giovanni Antonio Magini, 14 March 1592.."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Instructs Gellius and Magini on conducting solar, stellar, and lunar observations at Padua to determine the meridian difference with Uraniborg and to fix principles of atmospheric refraction.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910000002", "d": "1591-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, [day blank] 1591 [O.S.] (catalogued \"1591 [undated] and 14 March 1592\")", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.59; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.56</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO, DOMINO THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS DILECTO.</p><p>MIRATUS equidem non parum sum, clarissime et praestantissime Domine THADDAeE, amice summopere dilecte, quod tota hac elapsa aestate nihil a te literarum receperim. Verum idipsum, quod ex tuis iamprimum circa initia Octobris redditis cognovi, te minus prospera usum fuisse valetudine, alijsque occupationibus distentum, iam antea suspicabar. Video te responsioni meae ad tua dubia et obiecta, circa disputationem prioribus literis motam, iam plane acquiescere. Quae tua ingenuitas admodum mihi grata est. Facis hac in parte multo syncerius, quam plerique, qui solo contradicendi studio, sua, licet sibiipsis suspecta, mordicus tueri solent. Quae res Philosophiam (ut de Religionis negotio nihil dicam) non parum hactenus turbavit, et nondum finis.</p><p>Facis quidem mentionem effigierum tibi transmissarum, quodque Magnificus Dominus CURTIUS de Epigrammate, eo nomine ab arguto quodam Poeta concinnando, prospexerit. Pro qua erga me benevolentia gratias habeo. Attamen hoc non tanti referebat, atque librum transmissum (qui ea quae Tomo primo de Nova Stella tunc typis mandata fuêre, continebat) excutere, vestramque de his eruditam censuram perscribere, quemadmodum etiam de Idaeis quorundam ex meis instrumentis in parva forma expressis et capsulae cuidam inclusis, quae omnia Tabellario isti seniori ad vos redeunti perferenda tradidi. Quare cum de illis, quae maioris erant momenti, quam chartaceae istae effigies obiter saltem communicatae, ne verbo quidem in hac Epistola memineris, vereor, ut ista vobis allata sint, forte, quod senex ille onus portandi detrectârit, quod tamen non invite hîc in se recepit, aut quod illi in via quidpiam humanitus acciderit. Quicquid id est, rogo peramanter, velis prima quaque commoditate me certiorem de his reddere, et si liber is, de quo loquor, de Nova Stella affatim tractans, ad tuas manus pervenit, amplissimi Domini CURTII et tuam, quemadmodum tum quoque petebam, de eius contentis sententiam indicare ne graveris, tum etiam, quid vobis de instrumentorum conformatione, parvis modulis repraesentatâ, videatur. Fui et ego totâ hac aestate plurimis distractionibus et avocamentis impeditus, quo minus susceptam telam laborum meorum pertexere commodum fuerit. Quamprimum opportuniore otio eorum, quae dudum suscepta sunt, quidpiam typis absolvere licuerit, vos eius participes reddere non intermittam.</p><p>Intelligo ex scheda literis tuis inclusa senem illum M. PETRUM JACHENAW nomine, qui semel atque iterum mecum aliquandiu fuit, Pragam, Caesareae Maiestati in artificijs quibusdam mechanicis suam operam offerendi gratiâ accessisse. Et certe quantum ad currum attinet (qui itineris confecti spatia scite et iucunde, instar horologij cuiusdam, denotat, eadem sonitu campanularum, prout libuerit, auribus ingerit) confectionem eius artificiosam, quae et usui commoda sit et constans permaneat, egregie callet, diuque in ista machinatione varie efformanda versatus est. Nam et aliquot Germaniae Principibus eiuscemodi currus confecit, tum quoque quendam, ab Illustrissimo Duce Megapolitano, Serenissimo Regi nostro laudabilis memoriae dono missum. Quin et meo currui, quo subinde in hac Insula cum amicis circumvehor, tale quoddam Automatum, cum ultimo hîc esset, adaptavit; quod tum integra miliaria, tum etiam eorundem diversas subdivisiones, adeoque in minuta sexagenaria distributionem tam indicibus aliquot ostendit, quam pulsu sonitum duarum campanularum discriminatim edendo manifestat. Ut ob id indubia fide testari possim, nullam sub hoc opere, quod ille profitetur, subesse imposturam, sed omnia rite et constanter non sine delectabili atque utili spectaculo succedere. Si itaque virum hunc, qui per se est integer et probatae vitae atque morum, praenominato Domino CURTIO ita commendaveris, ut eius intercessione apud Caesaream Maiestatem vel alios illustres et opulentos Dominos, suam in curribus huiusmodi parandis operam elocare possit, atque aliquid lucelli hinc percipere, feceris mihi rem inprimis acceptam. Illum enim ob perspectam eius integritatem et fidem diligo, quodque in hoc senio pro sustentatione sui ipsius et familiae hincinde peregrinari necesse habeat, ipsi condoleo. Solet is, rebus suis domi in angustiam redactis, nonnunquam huc Vismariâ transmarina, ubi habitat, ad me in Daniam traijcere. Et quoniam totam hyemem praeteritam hîc mecum consumpsit, fieri facile potuit, ut me aliquoties de te verba facientem (prout retulit) audierit. Est enim alias satis veridicus. Tu eius conatibus, quod te vel meo nomine facturum non diffido, ne defueris, quo aliquando huc reversus meam hanc qualemcunque commendationem sibi non infrugiferam fuisse depraedicet. Sed de hoc forte paulo prolixior sum, quod in altero, cuius etiam in literis meministi, brevitate compensabo, de eo vix verculum additurus, neque enim dignus est. Ipsemet brevi apud vos, quemadmodum et antea ubique alibi, qualis sit, prodet, nisi forte religiosa illa (si Dijs placet) metamorphosis eum a seipso difformem reddiderit. Vale et magnificum Dominum CURTIUM a me officiose saluta, et si literas, quas semel atque iterum ad illum postmodum dedi, accepit, ut per oblatam a gravioribus Reipubl. curis vacationem respondere et de rebus Mathematicis iucunde mecum conferre non gravetur, rogatum habe.</p><p>Iterum diu multumque vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi die Anno 1591.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.CONSTITUERAM quidem de Plagiario et Suffuratore isto ne verbum meminisse, ex<br>quo mentione indignum censerem. Verum dum hæ literæ, unum atque alterum<br>diem, ventum et navigium, quo Hafniam transfretent, hîc expectant, incidi<br>interea et in alteras postremas tuas, quibus URSI istius Dithmarsici meministi. Cumque videam, te in<br>utrisque eodem modo sic notare hominem istum, quem ego plagij accusâram,<br>immo et in posterioribus nonnihil tacite excusare, adeoque eius mores<br>laudare, quasi hæsitares, an merito et simpliciter Plagiarius appellandus,<br>forte quod is suis vafris et subdolis verbis ita se apud vos purgârit: cogor<br>itaque nolens volens tibi apertius pluribusque de hoc scribere, ut quomodo<br>res se habeat, certius cognoscas. Fuit iste URSUS Dithmarsicus hîc ante annos (ni fallor) septem vel 8,<br>in comitatu intimi mei amici ERICI<br>LANGIJ, cui tunc temporis inserviebat; cumque per<br>quatuordecim dies idem ERICUS unâ cum<br>alijs quibusdam Baronibus et nobilibus viris, quos secum adduxerat, et<br>genialiter, uti fit, mihi convixissent, illisque dimissis, sese ad mecum<br>philosophandum composuisset (nam artium liberalium non ignarus est, hasque<br>amat, inprimis vero URANIAM nostram, et si sub hoc nomine sororem etiam<br>meam, SOPHIAM alias dictam, nunc<br>viduam iuvenculam, unâ intellexeris, cum et ille hanc sic appellare soleat,<br>forte a veritate non aliena me dixisse, res ipsa probabit, hanc enim amabat<br>atque in uxorem ducere satagit) is inquam, ERICUS iam Bacchici liquoris pertæsus et ab URANIA nostra<br>recreari cupiens, cœpit inter alia quærere, an non aliqua daretur ratio, qua<br>absurditas, quæ motum Terræ a COPERNICO introductum præcavere posset, et superfluitas<br>atque inconcinnitas<br>Ptolemaica nihilominus abrogaretur,<br>ita tamen ut apparentijs cœlestibus omnimode et<br>concinne satisfieret. Persenserat enim me tale quippiam moliri, ex quo<br>nonnunquam mihi ab ore excidisse auscultârat, nec hanc nec illam rationem<br>veritati consentaneam esse, sed dari posse longe commodiorem et<br>probabiliorem. Cumque ERICUM hunc<br>meum suis desiderijs non libenter, quantum in me est, privare soleam, at a tergo respiciens<br>animadverterem inter alios ministros et hunc Dithmarsum mensæ astare<br>ipsumque præ cæteris nostro colloquio, uti erat impudens, inhiare, nutu<br>commonefeci ERICUM, ut eum aliquorsum<br>amandaret, et ne rem intelligeret, nimisque cito reverteretur, dedimus illi<br>subito materiam, quam debebat seorsim metrice componere; nam et Poetastrum<br>profitebatur. Interim arrepta creta, prædicto ERICO meo in mensa delineavi tam<br>Ptolemaicam quam Copernicæam circa motus cœlestes speculationes, et<br>utriusque absurditates aperui. Sciscitanti insuper de tertia aliqua<br>convenientiore forma, nostra quoque hac in parte inventa, illi generaliter<br>et succincte depinxi, exposuique, quæ etiam postea, quoad fieri potuit<br>(erant enim in viridi panno mensam sternente designata) expunximus. At<br>nasutulus iste URSUS subolfaciens nos se<br>hæc celasse, sive ex ijs vestigijs, quæ adhuc in mappa forte residua erant,<br>sive ex charta quadam, quæ in Musæo meo, ubi esse solebat, inter alia<br>segestria abiecta erat, seu ab aliquo Studiosorum meorum, cui forte hæc<br>innotuerant, aut undecumque tandem alias hanc Hypothesium nostrarum<br>aliqualem ideam animo concepit, quam et non ita diu post ob suos insulsos<br>arrogantes et stultos mores ab ERICO<br>dimissus, Holsatiam transiens atque<br>Magdeburgum veniens, apud<br>doctissimum virum GEORGIUM<br>ROLLENHAGIUM, illic Scholæ Rectorem primarium, de hac<br>Hypothesium inventione, quam sibi falso arrogabat, plurima et mira quædam<br>iactabat: adeo ut cum a viro illo Mathematum non imperito, quædam circa<br>retrogradationes et stationes Planetarum salvandas interrogaretur, seipsum<br>expedire nequierit: hincque ROLLENHAGIUS<br>satis perceperit, ipsum huius inventionis authorem primum non esse. Quod et<br>non ita diu post, tam nobilissimo Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO, quam mihi per literas indicavit, atque<br>plagium hoc omnium primus patefecit, longe antequam Fundamentum illud<br>infirmum et insufficiens, a varijs authoribus corrasum seu potius surreptum,<br>nimis præsumptuose ab URSINO isto iactum<br>esset. Perlustravit idem, et hîc clam perquisivit multa alia ex meis,<br>interea dum ego tot dies cum ERICO<br>cæterisque nobilibus socijs, genio potius, uti dictum est, quam ingenio<br>indulgeo, et pleraque descripsit, nonnulla memoria conservavit, quæ postea<br>pro suis (ut est perftricta fronte præditus) venditare non erubuit. Qualia<br>sunt, quæ de Triangulorum supputationibus compendiosis per prostaphæreses<br>expediendis, de instrumentis, Sextante atque Quadrante, de stellarum<br>fixarum, intermediante Veneris stella et<br>☉le, restitutione aliaque similia profert.<br>Nam quantum Triangulorum compendia attinet, iamdudum<br>cum WITICHIO, quando hîc aderat,<br>libenter et liberaliter contuli, et quæ tum ab illo, tum etiam a me hac in<br>parte comperta fuerant, invicem communicavimus. Ipse autem WITICHIUS, postea in patriam rediens<br>atque dehinc ad illustriss. Principem GUILHELMUM Hassiæ<br>Landgravium proficiscens, illicque aliquandiu commoratus, Principis<br>Automatopæo IUSTO BYRGIO illiterato quidem<br>sed admodum industrio artifici mechanico, omnia a nobis concinnata<br>Triangulorum mysteria reseravit, unâque instrumentorum quorundam meorum<br>fabricam, divisionisque et pinnacidiorum in his rationem illic propalavit,<br>adeo ut Princeps ille mox tam Sextantem quam Quadrantem ad meorum<br>imitationem (licet et hoc non fassus sit WITICHIUS) confieri curavit, quibus postmodum et subtilius<br>et felicius quam antea observationibus invigilare dabatur, quemadmodum e<br>literis Astronomicis inter nos hincinde scriptis cognosces. Post discessum<br>autem WITICHIJ<br>URSUS iste non diu in una statione ac<br>latibulo (ad imitationem forte ursorum sylvestrium) degens, Cassellas etiam accurrit, hypotheses meas pro<br>suis Principi offert. Quas cum in Automatum redigere satagerent, utut satis<br>idoneum nacti artificem mechanicum, se expedire non poterant, antequam<br>superveniret ROTHMANNUS, qui<br>particularia quædam illic imitatione COPERNICI attexuit. Nam generaliora saltem URSUS iste præripuerat, prout etiam<br>suffuratoribus usu venit, qui præ metu atque conscientiæ fluctuatione quædam<br>sæpenumero non inutilia intacta relinquunt. Quin et rationem sidera<br>observandi restituendique, quam hîc aliqua ex parte viderat, illic a<br>WITICHIO patefactam, tum etiam<br>mutuis Landgravij<br>eiusque Mathematici et meis literis<br>plenius explanatam, illic uberius hausit, et quod potissimum erat (licet<br>pessime acciderit) a dicto IUSTO BYRGIO<br>Triangulorum compendiosam demonstrationem atque in numeros resolutionem,<br>quam illi WITICHIUS concesserat,<br>obtinuit; tum etiam quædam alia, pro quibus remunerationis loco illi totum<br>COPERNICUM in Germanicum idioma<br>convertit, quemadmodum hæc et alia CHRISTOPHORUS<br>ROTHMANNUS mihi non tam per literas quam præsens ante<br>biennium, cum huc a Principe visendorum meorum instrumentorum causa<br>ablegatus esset, significavit. URSUS autem<br>iste nec diu illic hærens, sed Argentinam mox prorepens, postquam<br>aliquandiu ea quæ hîc atque Cassellis<br>viderat, didicerat, surripuerat, privatim, quæstus causa Studiosis<br>proposuit, tandemque, utut in plerisque manca et mutila, in publicum nimis<br>arroganti et specioso titulo sub suo nomine emittere non verebatur: ubi cum<br>omnibus, quos noverat, Mathematicis aliquam partem istarum hincinde<br>corrasionum nomine tenus novo quodam et insulso exemplo dedicârit, me solum (in quo et mihi gratulor, quod meum nomen suis<br>furtis non conspurcavit) silentio præterijt, ne, si hinc innotuisset alijs,<br>eum mei aliquam habere notitiam, fraus et plagium eo citius detegeretur; cum<br>tamen potissima pars eorum, quæ illic adfert, nisi quatenus ea corrumpit aut<br>ijs non recte utitur, ex mea officina prodierit. Ut vero certus sis, rem ita<br>se habere, præsertim quantum ad Hypothesium conformationem attinet, addidi<br>ipsam schedam abiectitiam (cuius prius memini) ex qua hanc procul dubio, cum<br>hîc esset, speculationem hausit, quam etiam quidam meorum Studiosorum, quam<br>primum per dictum ROLLENHAGIUM de plagio<br>hoc admonitus essem, inter cæteras chartas conquisitam mihi attulit,<br>referens hanc, cum URSUS iste hîc adesset,<br>in Musæo meo inter alias schedas in aperto iacuisse, ut facile inde ea quæ<br>pro suis edidit, concipere potuerit. Illum autem ista, quæ sibi falso<br>vendicârat, hinc habuisse, si nihil aliud, id satis convincit, quod et in<br>hac ipsa veteri delineatione Martij orbis, per incuriam aliquam Solarem<br>totaliter includat: ideoque charta hæc forte tanquam inutilis a me abiecta<br>erat, quemadmodum etiam in ipsius diagrammate, cuius imitatione hæc perperam<br>designantur: cæteris, quæ superaddidit, pro libito assutis nec solide ad rem<br>facientibus. Cupio autem hanc ipsam schedam mihi certo remitti. Quin et<br>prænominatus GELLIUS SASCERIDES, qui<br>octennium mihi fuit domesticus, cum hinc in Italiam abiturus aliquandiu in Germania moraretur et Argentinam deinde transiret, URSUMque istum illic reperiret, suum istud Fundamentum ex<br>aliorum lapidibus corrasum iactitantem, aperte illi in os dixit, quod<br>hypotheseos istius inventionem sibi falso<br>attribueret: meam enim omnino esse et se aliquot annis antea hanc apud me<br>cognitam habuisse: et quod crassus iste error circa Martium orbem, Solarem<br>totaliter in ipsius diagrammate ambientem, satis prodat, eum hæc fundamenta<br>non intelligere, nedum ut author istius inventionis esse queat. Et pleraque<br>alia illi obiecit mihi prærepta. Verum iste, ut est impudenti vafricie<br>obsitus, se hanc Hypothesin a me non habuisse peierare non est veritus,<br>nihilominus se eam non rite intelligere et Martium orbem perperam in hac<br>designasse fateri ab eodem GELLIO coactus<br>est. Quomodo igitur, quæso, ea ipsemet invenire potuit, quæ ab alio inventa<br>non rite intellexit, imo potius depravavit? Cum alias facile sit, inventis<br>aliquid addere. Ex ipsismet huius GELLIJ<br>literis, quas tum Argentinâ tum etiam postea ex Italia ad me hac de re perscripsit, quas hîc, mihi tamen<br>certo et tuto remittendas, apposui, rem ita se habere, citra omne dubium<br>cognosces. Et est sane iuvenis ille, qui iam tricesimum excessit annum, non<br>saltem apprime in varijs scientijs eruditus, sed et morum integritate<br>gravitateque singulari præditus et veritatis inprimis, cuius amorem a me<br>sæpenumero plurimum commendari audijt, si quis alius, apprime observans. Imo si nihil aliunde est, quod probaret<br>ipsum ex mea inventione, quocunque tandem dolo, hanc habuisse Hypotyposin,<br>non autem me ex ipsius hincinde corrasa farragine mutuatum fuisse, hinc<br>satis apertum evadet, quod noster de ætherei Mundi recentioribus Phænomenis<br>secundus liber diu impressus fuerit, antequam eius fundatoriæ rapsodiæ in<br>lucem prodirent. Cum et prodierint, [et] antea has hypotheses excogitassem,<br>ipsis apparentijs cœlestibus, [si] enucleate considerentur, me huc<br>impellentibus, licet tunc usque in integrum opus Astronomicæ restitutionis<br>hanc supprimere constitueram, sed idipsum veritus, quod et ab hoc URSO et quodam alio Scoto, qui cum hanc inter hîc<br>colloquendum didicisset, postea privatim suis discipulis Rostochij proposuerit, neque tamen,<br>quemadmodum URSUS iste, candido animo meum<br>esse inventum non diffiteretur, seque hîc apud me hanc<br>rationem concepisse testaretur: id ipsum, inquam, et similia veritus,<br>secundo illo Tomo cæteris certa de caussa præmittendo ea inserere consultius<br>iudicavi. Quantum ad Triangulos attinet, videbis suo loco in nonnullo<br>Tomorum meorum aliquot circa hæc dogmata, quæ meis Studiosis iam ante<br>plurimos annos præscripsi, ut faciliorem inde haberent calculi Astronomici<br>rationem. Cumque in singulis mensis Musæi nostri horum descriptum intuitui<br>patebat (ex quo quilibet ferme eorum peculiare ad manus habeat exemplar)<br>facilimum fuit huic URSO tot dies subdolum<br>Parasitum agenti ista lambere, utut demonstrationes eorum Geometricas hîc<br>vix acquirere potuerit, eas enim seorsim inter propria quædam mea maioris<br>momenti scripta conservâram, quas Cassellis a Landgravij<br>Automatopæo postea ea, qua dixi, occasione<br>nactus est. Quin et multa alia habeo, ipsius propria manu scripta, quæ hîc<br>clam descripsit atque unâ cum cæteris surripere animum induxerat. Utut palam<br>semper diceret, omnia quæ hîc videret, superflua esse et nullius momenti: se<br>tribus lignis plura rectius et citius executurum, quam ego omnibus meis<br>instrumentis et machinis, iactare non erubescens, quam artem Tridendronicam<br>vocabat; nescio an de tribus lignis patibuli, quæ illi unâ cum infrascripto<br>in eius effigiem Epigrammate in eum usum depingi curavi, ista intellexerit.<br>Si sic est, habeat ille pro se hanc sidera observandi rationem, nemo equidem<br>hanc illi invidebit, sed potius eius commiserabitur. Epigramma autem erat<br>eiuscemodi:</p><p>Ille Tridendronicam primus qui condidit artem</p><p>    Hac facie et barba conspiciendus erat.</p><p>Nec mirum obscura referat quod imagine vultus,</p><p>    Ille tribus lignis tam bene conveniens.</p><p>Sed nectendus erit, sive illis forsitan olim</p><p>    Iunctus, nectentis sustineatve vices.</p><p>Imo, ut nullo iusto prætextu<br>inficias eat, se pleraque ex meis hîc me inscio descripsisse, habeo etiamnum<br>integrum fasciculum eorum, quæ hîc clam sua manu exarârat atque secum<br>auferre satagebat. Nam cum ERICO eius<br>hero egeram, ut ab eo vino somnoque sepulto hæc rursus nocte clam auferri<br>curaret, idque uno vel altero die, antequam hinc discederet, licet is, qui<br>sic talionem reddere debebat, non satis astutus fuerit, nec etiam ista<br>præreptitia invenerit, quemadmodum ille ipse neque omnia, quæ auferre<br>voluit, integre arripuit, seu eius intellectum seu manum intellexeris. Quod<br>si perstiterit in sua malevolentia et plagium non confessus et deprecatus<br>fuerit, sed perverse et mendaciter excusârit, curabo illum istic publice<br>accusari, convinci atque (nisi illi ob Mathematum aliqualem, quam adeptus<br>est, notitiam pepercero) secundum leges puniri, prout meritus est. Nam et<br>satis iustas habeo huius vindictæ (si vellem) exercendæ caussas, cum non<br>saltem mihi quædam ex meis, multis vigilijs, laboribus et sumptibus parta<br>perfide præripuerit et sibi mendaciter adscripserit: sed et famam et honorem<br>meum suis petulantibus et malitiosis mendacijs Cassellis in aula Landgravij, forte etiam<br>alibi, ut ex literis CHRISTOPHORI<br>ROTHMANNI in portione voluminis Epistolarum Astronomicarum<br>tibi transmissa, pag. 33 iuxta finem earundem videre licet, quantum in se<br>erat, elevare conatus sit. Quamvis ego ad istas nugas ne verbulo respondere<br>dignatus sim, quia calumniæ, mendacia, scurrilitas seipsa tandem in absurda<br>deducunt. Nec est, ut permittas ipsum mihi quippiam scribere, nam ego eius<br>vafros et futiles prætextus satis alias perspectos suspectosque habeo,<br>hominemque plus quam opus est, non tamen, prout tu adhuc existimas, humanum,<br>simplicem, non invidiosum, frugalem, purum putum (si per antithesin hæc<br>intellexeris, rectius forte sententiam tuleris: latet anguis in herba)<br>cognovi. Nec dubito quin Sorex iste suo se brevi ita prodet indicio, ut et<br>vobis illum cognoscere non obscurum relinquatur. Plura non addam, utut alia<br>mihi circa ipsius vafros et perfidos mores perspecta sint. Nec enim dignus<br>est, de quo vel hæc, uti prius quoque dixi, scriberentur, quamvis satis<br>superque vera sint. Nolo etiam, ut illa in apertum deducas, sed saltem<br>Domino CURTIO et tibi comperta reserves,<br>nisi aliqua urserit necessitas. Nam iste per se satis antea infamis est, et<br>plus, nisi alios mores induerit, imposterum se infamabit, ut non opus sit,<br>illum per alios famosiorem reddi.</p><p>Scriptum Uraniburgi die 14 Martij Anno 1592.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and, in every varied kind of learning, most erudite and most excellent man, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, his especially beloved friend.</b></p><p>I have indeed wondered not a little, most renowned and most outstanding Master Thaddaeus, my exceedingly beloved friend, that throughout this whole past summer I received no letter from you. Yet the very thing which I learned only now from yours, delivered around the beginning of October — that you had been enjoying less than prosperous health, and were taken up with other occupations — I had already suspected beforehand. I see that you now fully rest content with my reply to your doubts and objections, concerning the dispute raised in my earlier letters. This candour of yours is very welcome to me. In this respect you act much more sincerely than most, who, from the mere zeal of contradiction, are wont to defend tooth and nail their own positions, suspect though [these be] even to themselves. This thing has hitherto troubled Philosophy not a little (to say nothing of the business of Religion), and there is as yet no end.</p><p>You do indeed make mention of the portraits sent to you, and that the Magnificent Master Curtius has made provision regarding an epigram, to be composed under that head by some clever poet. For which goodwill towards me I give thanks. Nevertheless this did not matter so much as to examine the book sent [to you] (which contained those things about the New Star that were then committed to print in the first volume), and to write out your learned judgement of these — just as also [your judgement] of the <i>designs</i> of certain of my instruments, rendered in small format and enclosed in a certain little case, all of which I handed over to that elder courier returning to you, to be carried [there]. Wherefore, since of those things which were of greater moment than those paper portraits — communicated only in passing — you make mention not even with a word in this letter, I fear that these may [not] have been brought to you, perhaps because that old man declined the burden of carrying [them] — which, however, he undertook here not unwillingly — or because something befell him on the way, as happens to men. Whatever it is, I beg most lovingly, [that] you be willing at the very first convenience to inform me about these matters; and if that book of which I speak, treating fully of the New Star, has reached your hands, do not be reluctant to indicate the most ample Master Curtius's opinion and your own of its contents — just as I requested then too — and likewise what seems [good] to you regarding the configuration of the instruments, represented in small models. I too throughout this whole summer have been hindered by very many distractions and interruptions, so that it has not been convenient to weave through to the end the web of my labours that I had undertaken. As soon as, with more suitable leisure, I am permitted to complete in print some part of those things that were long since undertaken, I shall not fail to make you a sharer in it.</p><p>I understand from the note enclosed in your letter that the old man named Master Peter Jachenaw — who once and again was with me for some while — has gone to Prague, for the sake of offering his service to His Imperial Majesty in certain mechanical contrivances. And certainly, as far as concerns the <i>coach</i> (which marks the stretches of road accomplished skilfully and pleasantly, after the manner of a kind of clock, and the same it brings to the ears, as one wishes, by the sound of little bells), he is excellently skilled in its ingenious construction, [such] as is both convenient for use and remains constant, and he has long been engaged in fashioning this device in various ways. For he has built coaches of this kind for several Princes of Germany, and also a certain one [that was] sent as a gift by the Most Illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg to our Most Serene King of laudable memory. Indeed to my own coach, in which from time to time I ride about this Island with friends, he fitted such a self-acting mechanism [Automatum], when he was last here; which shows both whole miles, and also their several subdivisions, and indeed their division into sexagesimal minutes, both by several indicators, and likewise reveals [them] by striking, by giving out the sound of two little bells distinctly. So that on this account I can testify with undoubted credit that there underlies this work, which he professes, no imposture, but that all things proceed duly and constantly, not without a delightful and useful spectacle. If, therefore, you will so commend this man — who is of himself upright and of approved life and character — to the aforenamed Master Curtius, that by his intercession with His Imperial Majesty or with other illustrious and wealthy Lords he may be able to place out his service in preparing coaches of this kind, and to gain some little profit thereby, you will do me an especially welcome thing. For I esteem him on account of his manifest integrity and fidelity, and I feel for him, since in this old age he must travel here and there for the support of himself and his family. He is wont, when his affairs at home are reduced to straits, sometimes to cross over to me into Denmark from Wismar across the sea, where he lives. And since he spent this whole past winter here with me, it could easily have happened that he heard me several times speaking words about you (as he reported). For he is otherwise truthful enough. Do not fail his endeavours — which I do not doubt you will do even in my name — so that, returned here at some time, he may proclaim that this commendation of mine, such as it is, was not unfruitful to him. But on this I am perhaps a little too long-winded, which I shall make up for by brevity in the other [man], of whom too you made mention in your letter, scarcely going to add a little word about him — for he is not worthy of it. He himself will shortly reveal among you, just as before everywhere else, what manner of man he is, unless perhaps that pious (God save the mark) metamorphosis has rendered him unlike himself. Farewell, and greet the magnificent Master Curtius dutifully on my behalf; and if he received the letters which I sent to him once and again afterwards, hold him entreated that, by such leisure as is afforded by [respite from] the weightier cares of the Commonwealth, he would not be reluctant to reply and to confer pleasantly with me about Mathematical matters.</p><p>Again, long and much, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the [—] day, the year 1591.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT.</b> I had indeed resolved to breathe not a word about that Plagiarist and Thief, holding him unworthy even of mention. But while this letter, for a day or two, waits here for a wind and a ship to carry it across to Copenhagen, I have meanwhile come upon another, your most recent letter, in which you make mention of that Ursus of Dithmarschen. And since I see that in both letters you speak of that man in the same way — the man I had accused of plagiarism — indeed, that in the later one you even tacitly excuse him somewhat, and go so far as to praise his character, as though you hesitated whether he ought rightly and simply be called a Plagiarist, perhaps because with his crafty and cunning words he has so cleared himself in your eyes: I am therefore compelled, willing or not, to write to you more openly and at greater length about this, so that you may know more certainly how the matter stands.</p><p>This Ursus of Dithmarschen was here, seven or eight years ago unless I am mistaken, in the retinue of my intimate friend Erik Lange, whom he was then serving. And when for fourteen days that same Erik, together with certain other Barons and noblemen whom he had brought with him, had passed the time genially with me, as happens, and, once they had been dismissed, had settled himself to philosophize with me (for he is not unacquainted with the liberal arts, and loves them, above all indeed our Urania — and if under this name you also understand my sister, otherwise called Sophia, now a young widow, all as one, since he too is accustomed to call her by that name, the matter itself will show that I have said nothing far from the truth; for he loved her, and is eager to take her to wife) — he, I say, Erik, being by then weary of the Bacchic drink and desiring to be refreshed by our Urania, began, among other things, to ask whether some scheme might not be found by which the absurdity that must guard against the motion of the Earth introduced by Copernicus, and likewise the superfluity and inelegance of the Ptolemaic system, might nonetheless be done away with — and yet in such a way that the celestial appearances might be satisfied in every respect, and elegantly. For he had sensed that I was contriving something of the kind, having heard me let slip from my own mouth more than once that neither the one scheme nor the other agreed with the truth, but that a far more convenient and more probable one could be given. And since it is not my custom to deprive my Erik willingly of his wishes, so far as lies in my power — but, glancing back over my shoulder, I noticed that among the other attendants this Ditmarsian too was standing by the table, and that he, impudent as he was, was hanging on our conversation more eagerly than the rest — I signalled to Erik with a nod that he should send him off somewhere; and so that he should not grasp the matter, and should not return too soon, we at once gave him a subject which he was to compose separately in verse, for he also professed himself a would-be poet.</p><p>Meanwhile, having snatched up a piece of chalk, I sketched out on the table for my aforesaid Erik both the Ptolemaic and the Copernican speculations concerning the celestial motions, and laid open the absurdities of each. And when he further inquired about some third, more fitting form, I sketched out for him, in general terms and succinctly, our own discovery in this matter also, and explained it — a sketch which we afterwards, so far as it could be done, rubbed out again (for it had been drawn upon the green cloth that covered the table). But that sharp-nosed Ursus, sniffing out that we had kept this hidden from him — whether from those traces which perhaps still remained on the cloth, or from a certain paper which lay tossed among other rubbish in my Museum, where he used to be, or from one of my Students, to whom this had perhaps become known, or from wherever else in the end — conceived in his mind some such idea of our Hypothesis.</p><p>And not very long after he had been dismissed by Erik on account of his tasteless, arrogant, and foolish manners, passing through Holstein and coming to Magdeburg, he boasted a great many astonishing things to that most learned man Georg Rollenhagen, chief Rector of the School there, about this invention of the Hypothesis, which he falsely claimed for himself — to such a degree that, when he was questioned by that man, no novice in Mathematics, about certain points concerning the saving of the retrogradations and stations of the Planets, he could not extricate himself; and from this Rollenhagen perceived clearly enough that he was not the first author of this invention. And this, not very long after, he made known by letter both to the most noble Lord Heinrich Rantzau and to myself, and was the very first of all to expose this plagiarism — long before that feeble and insufficient Fundamentum, scraped together, or rather filched, from various authors, was too presumptuously thrown out into the world by that Ursus.</p><p>The same man also went prowling about and secretly searched out many other things of mine here, while for so many days I was indulging, as I have said, my good spirits rather than my wits, with Erik and my other noble companions; and he copied out most of them, and kept some in memory, which afterwards — being possessed, as he is, of a brazen face — he did not blush to peddle as his own. Such are the things he brings forward concerning the abbreviated computation of Triangles carried out by prosthaphaeresis, concerning instruments, the Sextant and the Quadrant, concerning the restitution of the fixed stars by means of the star Venus and the Sun as intermediaries, and other similar matters.</p><p>For as far as the abbreviated methods for Triangles are concerned, I long ago, when Wittich was here, discussed them with him freely and openly, and we shared with one another what had been discovered in this matter both by him and by me. But Wittich himself, afterwards returning to his own country, and thence proceeding to the most illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, and having stayed there some while, disclosed to the Prince's Automaton-maker, Jost Bürgi — an unlettered man indeed, but a most industrious mechanical craftsman — all the mysteries of the Triangles that we had worked out together; and likewise made public there the construction of certain instruments of mine, and the method of their graduation and of the sights upon them, so that the Prince soon had both a Sextant and a Quadrant made in imitation of mine (though Wittich did not admit even this), with which it was afterwards granted them to attend to observations more precisely and more successfully than before, as you will learn from the Astronomical letters written back and forth between us.</p><p>But after Wittich's departure, this Ursus, not lingering long in any one station or den — perhaps in imitation of the bears of the forest — likewise hastened to Kassel, and offered my hypotheses to the Prince as his own. And when they were eager to reduce these into an Automaton, although they had got hold of a sufficiently competent mechanical craftsman, they could not manage it until Rothmann arrived on the scene, who wove in certain particulars there in imitation of Copernicus; for this Ursus had at least snatched away the more general points, as commonly happens with thieves, who out of fear and the wavering of a guilty conscience often leave certain not unuseful things untouched. Indeed, he also drank in more fully there the method of observing and restoring the positions of the stars — a method he had here seen in part, but which was disclosed there by Wittich, and afterwards more fully explained in the exchange of letters between the Landgrave and his Mathematician and myself — and, which was the chief thing (though it turned out very badly), he obtained from the said Jost Bürgi the abbreviated demonstration of the Triangles and their resolution into numbers, which Wittich had granted to him; and also certain other things, in payment for which he translated the whole of Copernicus into the German tongue — as Christoph Rothmann informed me of these and other matters, not so much by letter as in person, two years ago, when he had been sent here by the Prince for the purpose of viewing my instruments.</p><p>But this Ursus, not lingering long there either, soon crept off to Strasbourg, and after he had for some time set forth privately to Students, for the sake of gain, the things he had seen, learned, and pilfered here and at Kassel, at length — maimed and mutilated as they were in most respects — he was not afraid to publish them abroad under his own name, with too arrogant and showy a title; wherein, having by some new and tasteless precedent dedicated some part of these scrapings gathered from here and there, in name only, to every Mathematician he knew, he passed over me alone in silence (in which I even congratulate myself, that he did not befoul my name with his thefts), lest, if it had become known to others from this that he had some acquaintance with me, his fraud and plagiarism should the more quickly be detected — although the greater part of what he sets forth there, except in so far as he corrupts it or misuses it, issued from my own workshop.</p><p>But so that you may be certain the matter stands thus, especially as regards the shaping of the Hypothesis, I have enclosed the very discarded sheet itself, which I mentioned before, from which, beyond doubt, he drew this speculation while he was here — a sheet which, as soon as I had been alerted to this plagiarism by the said Rollenhagen, one of my Students, having searched it out among the other papers, brought to me, reporting that this sheet, while this Ursus was here, had lain openly among the other papers in my Museum, so that he could easily have conceived from it the notions which he published as his own. That he had these things, which he falsely claimed as his own, from this source, is proved sufficiently, if by nothing else, by the fact that in this very old sketch the orb of Mars, through some carelessness, wholly encloses that of the Sun — and it was for this reason, perhaps, that this sheet had been thrown away by me as useless — just as it is likewise in his own diagram, in imitation of which these things are wrongly drawn, the rest that he has added on being stitched in at his own pleasure and contributing nothing solid to the matter. I desire, however, that this very sheet be surely returned to me.</p><p>Moreover, the aforenamed Gellius Sascerides, who was a member of my household for eight years, when, on his way from here to Italy, he stayed for some time in Germany and then passed through Strasbourg, and there found this Ursus boasting of his Fundamentum, scraped together from the stones of others, told him plainly to his face that he falsely attributed to himself the invention of that Hypothesis: for it was entirely mine, and he himself had known it in my company several years before; and that this crude error concerning the orb of Mars, which in his diagram wholly encompasses that of the Sun, sufficiently betrays that he does not understand these foundations, still less that he could be the author of this invention. And he charged him with many other things stolen from me besides. But he, overspread as he is with shameless cunning, did not scruple to swear falsely that he had not had this Hypothesis from me; nonetheless, he was compelled by that same Gellius to confess that he did not rightly understand it, and had wrongly drawn the orb of Mars in it. How then, I ask, could he himself have discovered things which, once discovered by another, he did not rightly understand — nay, rather corrupted? Whereas otherwise it is easy enough to add something to what has already been discovered. From the very letters of this same Gellius, which he wrote to me at length on this matter, both from Strasbourg and afterwards from Italy, and which I have enclosed here — to be returned to me, however, surely and safely — you will learn beyond all doubt that the matter stands thus.</p><p>And that young man, who has now passed his thirtieth year, is indeed not only thoroughly learned in various sciences, but is also endowed with integrity of character and a singular gravity, and above all, if anyone is, most scrupulously observant of truth, the love of which he has often heard most earnestly commended by me. Indeed, even if there were nothing else to prove that he obtained this Sketch, by whatever trick in the end, from my invention, and that I did not borrow it from his rag-bag scraped together here and there, this will become plain enough from the fact that my own second book, On the More Recent Phenomena of the Aetherial World, had long since been printed before his foundational rhapsodies ever came to light. And when they [my pages] did come out, [and] I had already devised these hypotheses before, the celestial appearances themselves — [if] examined closely — impelling me to this, although up to that time I had resolved to withhold this hypothesis for the whole work on the Restoration of Astronomy; but fearing this very thing — that it would happen just as with this Ursus, and with a certain other Scot too, who, having learned this here in the course of conversation, afterwards privately proposed it to his own students at Rostock, yet who, unlike this Ursus, did not deny with a candid mind that it was my invention, but testified that he had conceived this scheme here in my company — fearing, I say, this very thing and things like it, I judged it more advisable, for a certain particular reason, to insert this hypothesis, setting it ahead of the rest, in that second Volume.</p><p>As far as the Triangles are concerned, you will see in its proper place, in one or another of my Volumes, certain teachings on these matters, which I prescribed to my Students many years ago already, so that they might thereby have an easier method of Astronomical calculation. And since a copy of these lay open to view on each of the tables of our Museum (so that almost every one of my Students has his own particular copy at hand), it was very easy for this Ursus, playing the sly Parasite for so many days, to lick these up — although he could scarcely have acquired their Geometrical demonstrations here, for I had kept those apart among certain of my own writings of greater importance, which he later obtained at Kassel from the Landgrave's Automaton-maker, on the occasion I have mentioned.</p><p>Indeed, I also have many other things, written in his own hand, which he secretly copied out here, and which, along with the rest, he had set his mind on carrying off. Although he always used to say openly that everything he saw here was superfluous and of no consequence, he did not blush to boast that with three pieces of wood he would accomplish more, more correctly and more quickly, than I with all my instruments and machines — an art which he called 'Tridendronic.' I do not know whether he understood this to mean the three pieces of gallows-wood, which — together with the epigram written below, in his likeness — I had had painted, for that very purpose. If that is so, let him keep this method of observing the stars for himself; indeed no one will envy him it, but will rather pity him for it. The epigram, moreover, was of this kind:</p><p><i>He who first founded the Tridendronic art</i><br><i>was to be known by this face and this beard.</i><br><i>No wonder that his features come to us in so dim a likeness —</i><br><i>he who agrees so well with three pieces of wood.</i><br><i>But bound he must be: whether one day joined to them himself,</i><br><i>or made to sustain the office of the one who binds.</i></p><p>Indeed, so that he may not, on any just pretext, deny that he copied out here, without my knowledge, a great many things of mine, I still have to this day a whole bundle of the things which he had secretly written out here in his own hand, and was eager to carry off with him. For I had arranged with Erik, his master, that these should be secretly carried off from him again by night, once he was buried in wine and sleep — and this on one day or another before he left here — although the one who was to render him this like for like was not clever enough, and did not even find these purloined things; just as he himself likewise did not manage to snatch up entire everything he wished to carry off — whether you understand this of his wit or of his hand.</p><p>But if he persists in his malevolence, and does not confess and beg pardon for the plagiarism, but perversely and mendaciously excuses himself, I shall see to it that he is publicly accused there, convicted, and — unless I spare him on account of the certain acquaintance with Mathematics he has acquired — punished according to the laws, as he deserves. For indeed I have quite just grounds, should I wish, for exercising this vengeance, since he has not only treacherously snatched away certain things of mine, won by many nights' watching, labours, and expenses, and mendaciously ascribed them to himself, but has also, with his petulant and malicious lies, attempted — so far as it lay in him — to belittle my reputation and honour at Kassel, in the court of the Landgrave, and perhaps elsewhere too, as you may see from the letters of Christoph Rothmann, in the portion of the volume of Astronomical Letters sent to you, page 33, near the end of them.</p><p>Although I have not deigned to answer these trifles with so much as a single word, because calumny, lies, and scurrility lead themselves in the end into absurdity. Nor should you allow him to write me anything at all, for I already have his crafty and futile pretexts sufficiently seen through and suspected on other grounds; and I have come to know the man more than is needful — yet not, as you still suppose, humane, straightforward, free of envy, frugal, and pure through and through (if you understand these words by antithesis, you will perhaps judge more rightly: a snake lies hidden in the grass). Nor do I doubt that this Shrew will soon betray himself by his own telltale sign, so that it will be left in no obscurity for you too to know him for what he is. I will add no more, although other things too regarding his crafty and treacherous character have come to my notice; for he is not worthy that even these things should be written of him, as I said before too, although they are true enough and more than enough. Nor do I wish you to bring these things out into the open, but rather to keep them known only to Master Curtius and yourself, unless some necessity should press. For he is already, by himself, sufficiently infamous, and will make himself more infamous still hereafter, unless he takes on other manners, so that there is no need for him to be made more notorious through others.</p><p>Written at Uraniborg, on the fourteenth day of March, in the year 1592.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Haggecius ab Hayck</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku / Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician, astronomer, and mathematician to Emperors Maximilian II and Rudolf II in Prague; like Tycho an observer of the 1572 nova (his <i>Dialexis de novae … stellae</i> of 1574), and Tycho's principal Prague correspondent and the man who later (1599) helped open the imperial court to him.</li><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (<i>Uraniburgum</i>, protocol §5.7). <i>hac Insula</i> = \"this Island\" = Hven.</li><li><b><i>Magnificus Dominus Curtius</b></i> = <b>Jakob Kurtz von Senftenau (Jacobus Curtius)</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor at Rudolf II's court in Prague and himself a mathematical amateur; Tycho's hoped-for court patron, to whom the portraits, the <i>De nova stella</i> volume, and the instrument-models were being routed via Hagecius. Named again at the close (<i>magnificum Dominum Curtium … saluta</i>).</li><li><b><i>librum … qui ea quae Tomo primo de Nova Stella … continebat</b></i> — the <b>first volume of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</b></i> (Tycho's projected great work on the 1572 nova, the \"Tomus primus de Nova Stella\"), which was being printed at Uraniborg in this period and was not formally published until 1602. Rendered \"the first volume … about the New Star\"; <i>Nova Stella</i> = the <b>new star / nova</b> of 1572.</li><li><b><i>effigierum … chartaceae istae effigies</b></i> — \"the portraits … those paper portraits\": engraved likenesses (of Tycho), distinguished by Tycho as of <i>lesser</i> moment than the book and the instrument-models. <i>Epigramma … ab arguto … Poeta concinnando</i> — an epigram to accompany such a portrait, to be composed by a poet under Curtius's patronage.</li><li><b><i>Idaeis … ex meis instrumentis in parva forma expressis</b></i> — \"the <i>designs</i> of certain of my instruments, rendered in small format\": small-scale drawings or models of Tycho's instruments (<i>instrumentorum conformatione, parvis modulis repraesentatâ</i> = \"the configuration of the instruments, represented in small models\"). <i>instrumenta / organa</i> → <b>instruments</b> (protocol §5.2); the spelling <i>Idaeis</i> (= <i>ideis</i>, \"ideas/designs\") is kept as printed and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Tabellario isti seniori</b></i> — \"that elder courier\": the same aged man identified two sentences later as <b>Petrus Jachenaw</b>, who had carried the consignment toward Prague; Tycho fears the package miscarried because Hagecius's letter mentions only the portraits.</li><li><b><i>M. Petrus Jachenaw</b></i> — <b>Peter Jachenaw</b> (form as printed; M. = <i>Magister</i>), an elderly mechanician resident at <b>Wismar</b> (<i>Vismaria transmarina</i>, the Mecklenburg Hanseatic port across the Baltic), maker of odometer-coaches, who had wintered with Tycho on Hven and was now seeking imperial employment at Prague.</li><li><b><i>currus … qui itineris confecti spatia … instar horologij … denotat … sonitu campanularum</b></i> — the <b>hodometer- or way-wiser coach</b>: a carriage with a geared mechanism that measures and displays distance travelled (whole miles, their subdivisions, down to <i>minuta sexagenaria</i> = sexagesimal/sixtieth parts) and announces it by striking small bells. <i>Automatum</i> = a <b>self-acting mechanism</b>; <i>horologium</i> = <b>clock</b> (the analogy). <i>miliaria</i> = <b>miles</b>; <i>minuta sexagenaria</i> = \"sexagesimal minutes\" (sixtieths). Such measuring-coaches were a recognized Renaissance court novelty; the description is kept concrete, not modernised to \"odometer.\"</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimo Duce Megapolitano</b></i> — \"the Most Illustrious Duke of <b>Mecklenburg</b>\" (<i>Megapolis</i> = Mecklenburg, the latinised form). <i>Serenissimo Regi nostro laudabilis memoriae</i> — \"our Most Serene King of laudable memory\" = the late <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (d. 1588), the formula <i>laudabilis memoriae</i> marking him as deceased.</li><li><b><i>in altero … de eo vix verculum additurus … religiosa illa (si Dijs placet) metamorphosis</b></i> — the contrasted \"other [man]\" whom Hagecius had also mentioned and whom Tycho dismisses with contempt (\"not worthy\" of more than \"scarcely a little word [<i>verculum</i>]\"). The barbed <i>religiosa illa (si Dijs placet) metamorphosis</i> — \"that pious (God save the mark) metamorphosis\" — implies a recent religious conversion Tycho disdains; the man is <b>not named</b> in the letter and is left unidentified here rather than guessed. <i>si Dijs placet</i> (\"if it please the gods\") is the standard sarcastic aside, rendered \"God save the mark.\"</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None in the source (no Greek interpolations, no planet or zodiac glyphs).</li><li><b>Negation crux (flagged).</b> In <i>vereor, ut ista vobis allata sint</i> the verb of fearing <i>vereor</i> governs <i>ut</i> + subjunctive, which in the classical idiom of <i>verbs of fearing</i> means a fear that the thing did <b>not</b> happen (\"I fear that these have <b>not</b> been brought to you\") — the sense the following clauses (the courier may have declined the burden, or met mishap on the way) require. The English supplies the negation in brackets — \"I fear that these may [not] have been brought to you\" — and it is flagged here because the Latin carries no overt <i>non</i>: the negative is idiomatic, not lexical, and is marked editorially rather than smuggled in.</li><li><b>Dating crux (flagged; do not reconcile).</b> The dateline is printed <i>\"Datae Uraniburgi <b>die</b> Anno 1591\"</i> — <i>die</i> (\"on the … day\") with <b>the day-number left blank</b> and no month. The header therefore leads with the as-written date \"[day blank] 1591\" and does not assert a day or month the text does not supply. The dataset `label` reads <i>\"1591 (uden dato) og 14. marts 1592\"</i> (\"1591 [undated] and 14 March 1592\"): the two ÖNB witnesses (A = cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.59; B = cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.56) evidently transmit, respectively, this undated 1591 letter and a related/continued piece bearing 14 March 1592; the tekstnet locator carries a notional `…15910203001` slug-date (3 Feb 1591) that the printed dateline does <b>not</b> substantiate. Per protocol §3 the discrepancy is recorded, not resolved: the source text translated here is the <b>undated 1591</b> letter; <b>no day or month is asserted</b>, and the 14 March 1592 date is not imported into this letter's dateline. <b>O.S.</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar; Tycho on Hven wrote Old Style).</li><li><b><i>disputatio … prioribus literis mota</b></i> — the running scholarly <b>dispute</b> in Tycho's prior letters to Hagecius (their exchange over cometary and astronomical matters); Tycho commends Hagecius for now conceding rather than contending \"tooth and nail.\"</li><li><b><i>telam laborum meorum pertexere</b></i> — \"to weave through to the end the web of my labours,\" the Ciceronian <i>tela</i> (\"web/warp\") metaphor for an unfinished literary-scientific work (here the <i>Progymnasmata</i>); kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Closing formula.</b> <i>Iterum diu multumque vale</i> = \"Again, long and much, farewell\" (an emphatic doubled valediction; cf. <i>Iterum vale</i>, protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> A 2,218-word POSTSCRIPTA appended to Tycho's letter to Thaddaeus Hagecius, dated at the close 14 March 1592 — a year or more after the body of the letter (Uraniborg, 1591), which had evidently sat waiting for a ship to Copenhagen while Tycho received further letters from Hagecius. It is Tycho's own first-person narrative of how NICOLAUS REYMERS BAER, called URSUS (\"of Dithmarschen\"), came to know the Tychonic geo-heliocentric world-system, written because Hagecius's own letters had begun, in Tycho's view, to go soft on Ursus.</li></ul><ul><li><b>The origin scene, told nowhere else in this detail.</b> Tycho gives an eyewitness account of Erik Lange's fortnight-long visit to Hven in September 1584 (\"seven or eight years ago\") — confirmed independently by Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> — and Ursus's presence in Lange's retinue. Tycho describes sketching the Ptolemaic, Copernican, and his own third (Tychonic) system in chalk on the <i>green cloth covering the table</i> for Erik Lange, then rubbing it out — and Ursus, hovering at table as a servant, apparently reconstructing the idea from chalk traces, a discarded sketch-sheet left in Tycho's Museum, or a loose-lipped student. This tablecloth episode does not appear in the published letter proper and is a genuinely new primary account of the moment Tycho believed Ursus stole the system.</li></ul><ul><li><b>The Erik Lange / \"Urania\" wordplay identifies Sophie Brahe.</b> Tycho glosses \"Urania\" (the muse he and Lange discussed astronomy under) as also Erik Lange's pet name for Tycho's sister SOPHIE BRAHE, \"now a young widow\" (she was widowed in 1588, per Christianson) — matching the courtship that Zeeberg's <i>Urania Titani</i> (1994) takes its very title from. This is a contemporary, Tycho's-own-hand confirmation of that pun, predating Zeeberg's study by four centuries.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Names two corroborating witnesses by name, both cited as enclosures.</b> (1) GEORG ROLLENHAGEN, Rector at Magdeburg, who — per Tycho — first caught Ursus out when Ursus could not answer basic questions on planetary stations and retrogradation, and reported this by letter to both Tycho and HEINRICH RANTZAU (matches Oestmann's 2004 edition of the Rollenhagen–Rantzau correspondence). (2) GELLIUS SASCERIDES, Tycho's disciple of eight years, who confronted Ursus face-to-face in Strasbourg and extracted a confession that he did not understand the Mars-orbit error in his own diagram. Tycho states he has physically enclosed with this letter both the original discarded sketch-sheet Ursus allegedly copied from, and Sascerides's own letters recounting the Strasbourg and Italy testimony — explicitly asking Hagecius to return both. These are named physical exhibits in an active evidentiary dossier, not general assertions.</li></ul><ul><li><b>The Kassel transmission chain: Wittich → Bürgi → Ursus, with Rothmann completing it.</b> Tycho traces a second leak: PAUL WITTICH, after leaving Hven, disclosed Tycho's compendious triangle-methods and instrument designs to JOST BÜRGI (\"the Prince's Automaton-maker,\" unlettered but highly skilled) at the court of WILHELM IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel; Ursus then visited Kassel himself and obtained further material from Bürgi, though the Kassel automaton (mechanical model) of the system could not be completed until CHRISTOPH ROTHMANN arrived and supplied Copernican particulars. Tycho attributes this account to Rothmann's own report, given in person at Hven roughly two years before writing (i.e. c.1590) — which matches Rothmann's documented visit to Hven, summer 1590.</li></ul><ul><li><b>The unnamed \"Scot\" is almost certainly Duncan Liddel</b>, though not named in the text: \"a certain other Scot... who privately proposed [the system] to his own students at Rostock,\" but — unlike Ursus — openly credited it to Tycho. Dreyer (1890) and Thoren (<i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>) independently record Duncan Liddel lecturing on the Tychonic system at Rostock c.1589/90 while still crediting Tycho, only later drawing a plagiarism accusation himself at Helmstedt. This postscript, datable to March 1592, is an early data point in Liddel's reputational trajectory, and explains — in Tycho's own words — why he rushed the system into print in the second book of <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588) ahead of the full <i>Astronomiae Instauratae</i>: fear that Ursus or Liddel's students would claim priority first.</li></ul><ul><li><b>Two authentic cruxes flagged, not smoothed over.</b> (1) The sentence beginning \"Cum et prodierint...\" (on why Tycho pre-empted publication) is transmitted with two words already bracketed as editorial supplements in the source text itself (\"[et]\", \"[si]\") — a genuine textual gap or corruption at a historically important juncture; I have preserved the bracket convention in the English at the corresponding point rather than silently resolving it. (2) The postscript cites \"the letters of Christoph Rothmann, in the portion of the volume of Epistolae Astronomicae sent to you, page 33\" — but Tycho's <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> was not printed at Uraniborg until 1596, four years after this postscript's own dateline of 14 March 1592. Either an installment of the Uraniborg press's typesetting was already circulating to Hagecius years before formal publication, or this line was added/updated after the postscript's ostensible date — the packet's own catalogue note (\"1591 [undated] and 14 March 1592\") already signals a composite, multi-layered document. This is flagged, not resolved.</li></ul><ul><li><b>The Ursus caricature-and-epigram.</b> Tycho reports commissioning a painted image (\"effigiem\") of Ursus together with a six-line Latin epigram, built on Ursus's own boast that he could out-observe Tycho's whole instrument-park with \"three pieces of wood\" (an art he called, in his own coinage, \"Tridendronic\"). Tycho puns venomously on \"tribus lignis\" (three sticks) as \"tribus lignis patibuli\" (the three timbers of a gallows), and the epigram's closing couplet threatens Ursus with being either bound to that wood himself or made to play the executioner's role — a real piece of hostile visual/literary propaganda, distinct from anything in the printed Ursus-Tycho pamphlet war. The running animal wordplay (Ursus = 'bear'; \"in imitation of the bears of the forest\"; \"nasutulus\" sniffing him out; and finally, near the close, Ursus recast as \"Sorex,\" a shrew) is a consistent authorial signature across the postscript and is worth noting as a stylistic fingerprint.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical content (the <i>De nova stella</i> first volume, the instrument-models, the odometer-coach, the Curtius/Prague patronage channel) is identified with confidence. Two points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>dateline crux</b> — the printed <i>\"die Anno 1591\"</i> has a blank day-number, and the catalogue's \"14 March 1592\" belongs to the partner witness, not this letter's dateline, so no day/month is asserted; (2) the <b>idiomatic negation</b> in <i>vereor, ut … allata sint</i> (fear-clause = fear that the thing did <i>not</i> happen), supplied in brackets. The unnamed \"other man\" of the penultimate passage is left unidentified, as the text does not name him.</p><p>The bulk of this translation is secure: the narrative sequence (Hven 1584 visit, the tablecloth sketch, Magdeburg/Rollenhagen, Kassel/Wittich/Bürgi/Rothmann, Strasbourg/Sascerides, the enclosed exhibits, the epigram, the closing threat) is coherent, grammatically legible, and corroborated at multiple points by Christianson, Dreyer, Thoren, and Mosley already in the corpus — the identifications of Ursus, Erik Lange, Sophie Brahe, Rollenhagen, Rantzau, Wittich, Wilhelm IV, Bürgi, Rothmann, and Sascerides are all well grounded and not speculative. Two things are not: the \"Cum et prodierint...\" sentence carries source-supplied bracketed conjectures marking a real gap or corruption, and I have rendered the sense as best the surviving words allow without inventing a cleaner Latin than what exists. The \"Duncan Liddel\" identification of the unnamed Scot is a strong inference from Dreyer/Thoren, not a naming in the text itself, and should be labeled as such downstream. The Epistolae Astronomicae page-33 citation is chronologically awkward given the 1596 print date against the 1592 dateline and is flagged rather than resolved — an editor with access to the full Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia (Dreyer's edition) critical apparatus for this letter could settle both cruxes; that is the upgrade path. The epigram's wordplay (\"Tridendronicam,\" \"nectendus... nectentis vices\") is inherently compressed and pun-dependent; the translation given is the most defensible reading but not the only conceivable one. Given the length, the two unresolved textual/chronological cruxes, and the absence of collation against Dreyer's critical edition, this is graded C rather than B.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full letter (salutation, body, valediction, and the long Ursus-plagiarism postscript) collated visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.319-326. Checked and confirmed against the page images: every proper name (Hagecius/Haggecio ab Hayck, Curtius, Peter Jachenaw, Erik Lange, Sophia, Ursus Dithmarsicus, Rollenhagen, Heinrich Rantzau, Wittich, Wilhelm Landgrave of Hesse, Jost Bürgi, Rothmann, Gellius Sascerides, Copernicus, Ptolemy); every date (main letter's blank day, 1591; postscript 'die 14 Martij Anno 1592'); every number (fourteen days at Uraniborg, 'ante annos septem vel 8', two bells 'duarum campanularum', page 33 citation to Rothmann's letters, Gellius's eight years 'octennium', Gellius's age past his thirtieth year); and every place-name (Uraniborg, Prague, Wismar, Denmark, Holstein, Magdeburg, Kassel, Strasbourg, Rostock, Copenhagen). The chalk-on-tablecloth episode was checked word for word: 'arrepta creta ... in mensa delineavi' / 'having snatched up a piece of chalk ... sketched out on the table', and the erasure detail 'erant enim in viridi panno mensam sternente designata' / 'it had been drawn upon the green cloth that covered the table' both confirmed against Dreyer p.322. The Tridendronica epigram (6 lines) was checked line by line against Dreyer p.325 and found faithful. No fabricated names, numbers or clauses; no silently dropped sentences; no flipped negations or pronouns; no hardened hedges; our source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin throughout with no corrupted/glued/dropped words. published_grade of C on the packet is superseded by this collation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.59.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.56."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 25, "prev": "brahe-t_15900803001", "next": "brahe-t_15910829001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900611001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910829001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Hagecius's acceptance of Tycho's replies to his astronomical objections and praises his candor in conceding rather than defending untenable positions.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910203001", "d": "1591-02-03", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 3 February 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Frühneuhochdeutsch, with Latin code-switches) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN/ TYCHONI<br>BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.AWILHELM VON<br>GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/<br>ETC.VNSERN günstigen Gruß zuvorA/ Vester lieber besonder/ Wir haben numehr ein lange zeit kein Schreiben/ noch auch etwas von ewerm Obseruationibus Mathematicis, viel wenigerA wie es euch ergehe/ vnd was vor Zeitungen deß orts vorfallen/<br>vernemen mügen/ Daran vns doch jederzeit zu sonderm gefallen<br>geschehen were/ da vns derogleichen von euch zukommen. Wenn wir dennA hiernach ein sonderlich verlangen tragen/ so ist an euch<br>vnser gnediges begeren/ jhr wollet vns nicht allein ewren/ vnd Daniae statum<br>zuschreiben/ Sondern vns auch etwas auß ewren Mathematicis Obseruationibus, vnd insonderheit ewre Obseruationes Stellarum Fixarum communiciren<br>damit wir sie mit den vnsern conferiren<br>mügen. Wollen euch auch ferner nicht verhalten/ daß vns von einem Thier<br>gesagt worden/ welchs man in Norwegen<br>finden/ vnd der König jhrer etliche zu Koppenhagen/ Item in der Friderichsburg im Thiergarten haben soll/ Vnd sollen die<br>Thier etwas höher seyn alß ein Hirsch/ Aber gar schwancke Hörner haben/ vnd<br>ein Rix genandt werden. Ob wir nu wol in allen vnsern Authoribus, so von den Thieren schreiben/ nachgesucht/ so<br>haben wir doch weder Description noch Namen desselbigen<br>nicht erfunden. Begeren derohalben an euch gnediglich/ jhr wollet vns ein<br>Iconem von denselben Thieren machen lassen/ vnd vns<br>denselbigen neben der Historien gedachtes Thiers/ wie denn auch die angezeigteA<br>Obseruationes Stellarum Fixarum verwahrlich zuschicken/<br>vnd ferner die Verordnung thun/ daß beygelegte BrieffeA vnserm Mathematico vnd lieben Getrewen Christoff Rothmann verwahrlich zugeschickt<br>mögen werden. Daran thut jhr vns zu sonderm gefallen/ vnd wir seynds jegen euch in Gnaden/ damit wirA euch gewogenA/ zu verschulden wol geneigtA.Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung<br>Cassel/ am 3. FebruarijA, Anno 1591.WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the worthy, our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup. Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Our trusty and well-beloved: this long while now we have been unable to learn of any letter, nor anything of your <i>mathematical observations</i> [<i>Observationibus Mathematicis</i>], much less how it fares with you and what tidings have occurred in those parts — whereas it would always have been to our particular pleasure had any such thing reached us from you. Since, then, we bear a special longing for [news of] this, it is our gracious desire of you that you would not only write to us of your own [affairs] and of the <i>state of Denmark</i> [<i>Daniae statum</i>], but would also communicate [<i>communiciren</i>] to us something out of your <i>mathematical observations</i>, and in particular your <i>observations of the fixed stars</i> [<i>Observationes Stellarum Fixarum</i>], so that we may compare [<i>conferiren</i>] them with our own. Furthermore we will not conceal from you that we have been told of an animal which is to be found in Norway, and of which the King is said to have several at Copenhagen, likewise at Frederiksborg in the deer-park; and the animals are said to be somewhat taller than a stag, but to have very pliant antlers, and to be called a <i>Rix</i>. Now although we have searched in all our authorities that write of animals, yet we have found neither description nor name of this same [beast]. We therefore graciously desire of you that you would have an <i>image</i> [<i>Iconem</i>] of these same animals made for us, and would carefully send us that [image] together with the natural history of the said animal — as also the aforementioned <i>observations of the fixed stars</i> — and would further give order that the enclosed letters be carefully forwarded to our <i>mathematician</i> and dear trusty [servant] Christoph Rothmann. Herein you do us particular pleasure, and we are in grace toward you, well disposed therewith to requite you in our favour and goodwill. Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 3 February, in the year 1591. WILHELM, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm von Gottes Gnaden, Landtgraue zu Hessen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV (\"the Wise\"), Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the observing prince of Kassel and Tycho's chief princely correspondent on astronomy. The titulature is abridged in this letter (<i>Catzenelnbogen, etc.</i>) relative to his fuller style elsewhere (<i>…Dietz, Ziegenhain und Nidda</i>).</li><li><b>Recipient style.</b> <i>Dem Vesten … Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — \"to the worthy … Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (Tycho's Scanian seat). <i>Vester lieber besonder</i> = <b>\"Our trusty and well-beloved\"</b> (the princely address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Unsern günstigen Gruß zuvor</i> rendered <b>\"Our gracious greeting beforehand\"</b> (cf. §5.5 <i>Unsern gnedigen Gruß zuvor</i>; the source here reads <i>günstigen</i>, \"gracious/favourable,\" kept).</li><li><b>Latin code-switches</b> (preserved per protocol §2; the chancery German embeds Latin technical nouns and a verb): <i>Observationibus Mathematicis</i> = \"mathematical observations\"; <i>Daniae statum</i> = \"the state of Denmark\"; <i>Mathematicis Observationibus</i>; <i>Observationes Stellarum Fixarum</i> = <b>\"observations of the fixed stars\"</b> (Tycho's stellar-position catalogue work); <i>communiciren</i> (Latinate German verb) = \"communicate, share\"; <i>conferiren</i> = \"compare, collate\" (§2 lists <i>conferiren</i> as a characteristic Hesse-Kassel code-switch); <i>Iconem</i> (acc. of <i>icon</i>) = \"image, figure/illustration\"; <i>Mathematico</i> = \"mathematician\"; <i>Februarij</i> (Latin genitive in the dateline). All are italicised on first use and glossed.</li><li><b>Christoph Rothmann.</b> <i>vnserm Mathematico vnd lieben Getrewen Christoff Rothmann</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician at Kassel (protocol §5.6) and himself a major Tycho correspondent. The Landgrave routes enclosed letters to Rothmann through Tycho — by this date (Feb. 1591) Rothmann had in fact left Kassel for Hven (he visited Tycho in 1590 and did not return); the request to forward letters \"to our mathematician\" reflects the court's expectation of his continued service.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Koppenhagen</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Friderichsburg</i> = <b>Frederiksborg</b> (the royal castle and deer-park at Hillerød, Zealand); <i>Cassel</i> = <b>Kassel</b>; <i>Norwegen</i> = <b>Norway</b>; <i>Knudstrup</i> (Tycho's estate). <i>der König</i> = the King of Denmark (<b>Christian IV</b>, then a minor; or the crown/royal household generally).</li><li><b>The animal — <i>Rix</i> (crux).</b> The Landgrave, a keen natural-historian, describes an animal found in Norway, kept by the Danish king at Copenhagen and in the Frederiksborg deer-park, <i>\"somewhat taller than a stag, but with very pliant/slender antlers\"</i> (<i>gar schwancke Hörner</i>), <i>\"called a</i> Rix<i>,\"</i> for which he could find neither <i>Description</i> nor <i>Name</i> in his zoological authorities, and of which he asks Tycho to procure a drawn <i>Iconem</i> with the beast's <i>Historien</i> (natural history). The name <i>Rix</i> is not standard German and is <b>left untranslated and flagged</b>: the description (Scandinavian habitat, larger than a red deer, distinctive antlers, kept in royal parks) fits the <b>reindeer</b> (Norwegian <i>rein/reinsdyr</i>) — the likeliest referent, <i>Rix</i> perhaps a Latinised/garbled form of <i>rein-</i> — though the <b>elk/moose</b> (<i>Elch</i>, Norwegian <i>elg</i>) also answers \"taller than a stag.\" The text neither names the species in modern terms nor decides between them; \"called a <i>Rix</i>\" is kept and the identification recorded rather than resolved. <i>Hörner</i> is rendered \"antlers\" (the German word covers both horns and antlers).</li><li><b>\"A\" markers.</b> The repeated stray capital <b>A</b> in the source (e.g. <i>KNUDSTRUP.A</i>, <i>zuvorA</i>, <i>wenigerA</i>, <i>dennA</i>, <i>mügen.A</i>, <i>angezeigteA</i>, <i>BrieffeA</i>, <i>wirA</i>, <i>gewogenA</i>, <i>geneigtA</i>, <i>FebruarijA</i>) are <b>footnote/sidenote anchor artifacts carried over from the working transcription of the 1596 printed edition</b>, not part of the letter's text; they are reproduced in the Source block per protocol §4(2) (verbatim transcription) but are disregarded in the translation. No word of the German is affected by them.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> Three negations in the body, all reproduced: <i>kein Schreiben … vernemen mügen</i> (\"unable to learn of <b>any</b> letter\"); <i>nicht allein … Sondern auch</i> (\"<b>not</b> only … but also\"); <i>Wollen euch … <b>nicht</b> verhalten</i> (\"we will <b>not</b> conceal from you\"); plus the litotes <i>weder Description noch Namen … nicht erfunden</i> (\"found <b>neither</b> description <b>nor</b> name\") — the source's pleonastic double negative (<i>weder … noch … nicht</i>) is normal Frühneuhochdeutsch emphasis, rendered as a single English negation without adding or dropping negative force.</li><li><b>Closing formula.</b> <i>wir seynds jegen euch in Gnaden, damit wir euch gewogen, zu verschulden wol geneigt</i> — the elliptical chancery sign-off of princely favour: \"we are in grace toward you, well disposed to requite you therewith in our favour and goodwill.\" Rendered to preserve the register without supplying a finite verb the source omits.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel, am 3. Februarij, Anno 1591</i> = \"Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 3 February 1591.\" <b>Old Style</b> (Hesse-Kassel, a Lutheran territory, retained the Julian calendar at this date). The dataset `label` (\"1591. 3. februar. Kassel\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. Header date follows the dateline.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the German and its Latin code-switches are rendered securely and the courtly register is matched; the flagged crux is the zoological term <b><i>Rix</b></i> (the Norwegian animal the Landgrave asks Tycho to have drawn): the description points most strongly to the reindeer, but the source neither gives a modern species-name nor excludes the elk/moose, so the term is kept untranslated and the identification is left open rather than smoothed.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> The referent is the <b>reindeer</b>: Tycho's own reply (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, ch. 9; and the 22 May 1591 letter) states he knew no animal of that name but that a reindeer had been sent from Norway to Copenhagen and died in summer, and that he enclosed a drawing. The word <i>Rix</i> itself stays unidentified. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [C].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so no Dreyer page images exist to collate against; audited against source_text alone as instructed. Checked the German source_text clause by clause against published_english: the greeting formula, the request for Tycho's mathematical/fixed-star observations, the inquiry about the Norwegian animal ('Rix', taller than a stag, pliant antlers) with the request for an image and natural history of it, the instruction to forward the enclosed letters to Christoph Rothmann, and the closing dateline (Kassel, 3 February 1591) all have exact counterparts and are accurately rendered. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found against source_text. Per the brief, absence of a tboo reference caps the grade at B regardless of this clean result.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15901001001", "next": "brahe-t_15910522001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15901001001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910908001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks Tycho to write about conditions in Denmark and to share fixed-star observations for comparison with Kassel's, and mentions a reported unusual animal.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910203001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910209001", "d": "1591-02-09", "dir": "third", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": "Christopher Rothmann", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Christoph Rothmann — Kassel, 9 February 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery / Frühneuhochdeutsch, with Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: A = Marburg, Hessisches Staatsarchiv, \"Landgraf Moritz (!) Correspondenz mit Gelehrten\" (letter draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN MATHEMATICUM CHRISTOPHORUM ROTHMANN.</p><p>WILHELM, von Gotteß gnaden landtgrauen zu Hessen, graue zu Catzenelenbogen.</p><p>Lieber getrewer. Wir haben dein schreibenn, darin du Vnß dein itzo lang wehrende vndt beschwehrliche leibs schwacheit zu erkennen geben, benebenn dem vberschickten abriß des gebewes, so Tycho Brahe pro observationibus siderum vffrichten laßen, wie auch die eigentliche vndt in faden vberschickte mensur gedachter abriß wohlgelieffert entpfangen. Daß du nun mit so vielfältigen leibs kranckheiten zu gleich beladen vndt angegriffen worden, deßwegen tragen wir ein gnediges mitleiden mit dir, vndt mochten dir nichts liebers gonnen alß deine wohlfarigkeit vndt gesundheit, vndt haltenß dafur es werden sich nun mehr ingruente illa temporum mutatione, auch die humores anders vndt sich also deine sachen zur beßerung vndt gesundheit anlaßen vndt schicken. Wollen gleichwohl nicht hoffen, daß du dir solche deine kranckheiten mit freßen vndt sauffen oder sonst vnordentlichen leben verursacht, vndt also selbst praesentis tui mali faber gewesen seiest.</p><p>Wie eß dir in Denmarck ergangen, mogten Wir dich dauon gern mundtlich höoren reden, jn masen wir dan auch gern sehen, daß du dich bey Vnß in deinen vorigen dienst wieder einstellen woltest.</p><p>Deß stern oder stern observation[s] buch (welchs du tabellas oder catalogos stellarum nennest) wolten Wir dir deßelben copey gern zuschicken, wenn Wir nur iemandt bekommen konten, ders recht vndt ohn mangell abschreiben konte; in masen wir dann die versehung thun laßen, daß einer der nicht allein vleisig, sondern auch correct schreiben kan, sich der subscription trewlich vndernehme, wollen Wir dir sie alßdan verwahrlich zuschicken.</p><p>Waß dan des Tychonis Brahae Instrumenta belanget, verstheen wir, daß sie zwar groß; von was materien sie aber seyen, sehen wir nicht allenthalben. Dan wie Vnß von vielen gesagt ist, so sollen sie von pappen vndt derogleichen materien gemacht sein, do Vnß nicht bedunckt, daß sie konnen bestendig sein. Beuehlen dir derhalben nachmalß, daß du Vnß deinen bericht, nemblich, ob du sie vor just vndt bestendig haltest, vffs ehist zukommen lasest. Wolten Wir dir nicht verhalten vndt seindt dir mit gnaden wohl gewogen.</p><p>Signatum Caßell am 9t. feb: ao: etc. 91.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the mathematician Christoph Rothmann.</b></p><p><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen.</b></p><p>Trusty and well-beloved. We have duly received your letter, in which you make known to Us your now long-continuing and grievous bodily infirmity, together with the sketch you sent over of the building which Tycho Brahe has had erected <i>for the observation of the stars</i> [pro observationibus siderum], as also the exact measurements of the said sketch, sent over [marked out] in thread. That you have now been at one and the same time burdened and assailed with so manifold bodily ailments — for this We bear gracious compassion towards you, and could wish you nothing more dearly than your well-being and health; and We hold it for [certain] that now, <i>with that change of the seasons coming on</i> [ingruente illa temporum mutatione], the <i>humours</i> [humores] too will turn otherwise, and that thus your affairs will set themselves and tend toward improvement and health. We will nonetheless not suppose that you have brought such ailments of yours upon yourself by gorging and guzzling or by otherwise disorderly living, and so have yourself been [a cause] of your present ills [praesentis tui mali faber].</p><p>How matters went for you in Denmark, We would gladly hear you tell of by word of mouth; even as We should also gladly see that you would present yourself with Us again in your former service.</p><p>The star-book, or book of star-observations (which you call <i>tables</i> or <i>catalogues of the stars</i> [tabellas oder catalogos stellarum]) — We would gladly send you a copy of it, if only We could obtain someone who could copy it out correctly and without fault; accordingly, when We shall have made provision that one who can write not only diligently but also correctly should faithfully undertake the <i>transcription</i> [subscription], We will then send it to you under safe keeping.</p><p>As then concerns Tycho Brahe's <i>instruments</i> [Instrumenta], We understand that they are indeed large; but of what materials they may be, We do not in every respect see. For, as has been told Us by many, they are said to be made of pasteboard and suchlike materials, wherein it does not seem to Us that they can be durable. We therefore charge you once more that you let your report reach Us at the very soonest — namely, whether you hold them to be accurate and durable. This We would not withhold from you, and are well disposed towards you with [Our] grace.</p><p>Given at Kassel on the 9th of February, year etc. [15]91.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm, von Gotteß gnaden landtgrauen zu Hessen, graue zu Catzenelenbogen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"Wilhelm the Wise,\" himself a serious observational astronomer and the patron of the Kassel observatory; <i>graue zu Catzenelenbogen</i> = Count of Katzenelnbogen, a component of the Hessian titulature. The princely first-person <i>Wir / Vnß</i> (the royal \"We\") and the chancery form of address <i>Lieber getrewer</i> (\"Trusty and well-beloved,\" per protocol §5.5) are preserved.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmann</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician at Kassel and Tycho's chief correspondent there; he had <b>visited Hven in 1590</b> (at Wilhelm's request, conveyed in the Landgrave's letter of 15 May 1590) to inspect Tycho's instruments, fell seriously ill, and never returned to Kassel service — the background to both the offer of re-employment and the inquiry into his health here. The letter answers Rothmann's own report.</li><li><b>The sketch and the thread-measurement.</b> <i>abriß des gebewes … die eigentliche vndt in faden vberschickte mensur</i> — Rothmann had sent the Landgrave a <b>drawing of Tycho's observatory building</b> (Uraniborg / Stjerneborg) together with its true dimensions conveyed <i>in faden</i>, i.e. <b>marked out in [a length of] thread</b> (a knotted/measured cord standing in for a scale) — a notable detail of how architectural measure was transmitted by post.</li><li><b>The \"instruments of pasteboard\" report (the substantive crux of the letter).</b> <i>sie sollen von pappen vndt derogleichen materien gemacht sein … nicht bedunckt, daß sie konnen bestendig sein</i> — the Landgrave reports the rumour, \"told Us by many,\" that Tycho's <i>instruments</i> are <b>made of <i>pappen</b></i> and like materials, and doubts such instruments \"can be durable/stable\" (<i>bestendig</i> = both physically stable and dimensionally constant). <i>Pappen</i> here = <b>pasteboard / cardboard / papier-mâché</b>, not \"paper\" in the sheet sense; rendered \"pasteboard.\" Tycho's instruments were of course of metal and seasoned wood, and Rothmann's report would correct the rumour — this passage is the historically famous expression of the rivalrous Kassel skepticism about the Hven instruments. <i>bestendig</i> is rendered \"durable\" in its two occurrences and the doublet <i>just vndt bestendig</i> as \"accurate and durable\" (<i>just</i> = exact/accurate); the double sense of <i>bestendig</i> is flagged rather than collapsed.</li><li><b>The star-catalogue.</b> <i>Deß stern oder stern observation[s] buch (welchs du tabellas oder catalogos stellarum nennest)</i> — the Landgrave's own <b>catalogue of fixed-star observations</b> (the Kassel star catalogue), which Rothmann terms with the Latin <i>tabellas oder catalogos stellarum</i> (\"tables or catalogues of the stars\"); Wilhelm offers to have a fair copy made and sent. <i>subscription</i> here is used loosely for the act of <b>transcription / copying out</b> (he wants a scribe who writes <i>correct</i>, i.e. accurately); rendered \"transcription.\"</li><li><b>Latin code-switching.</b> Typical of Kassel chancery German to a learned correspondent. <i>pro observationibus siderum</i> = \"for the observation of the stars\"; <i>ingruente illa temporum mutatione</i> = \"with that change of the seasons [/of the times] coming on\" (<i>temporum</i> read as the seasons, given the medical context); <i>humores</i> = the bodily <b>humours</b> (humoral medicine, protocol §5.4 register); <i>praesentis tui malis</i> = \"of your present ills\" (the grammar of the MS draft is loose — an ablative/genitive slip; <i>aber</i> in <i>malis aber gewesen</i> reads as <i>Urheber</i>, \"author/cause,\" so the sense is \"a cause of your present ills,\" given in brackets); <i>Instrumenta</i>, <i>tabellas</i>, <i>catalogos stellarum</i> — see above. Retained Latin is italicised and glossed on first use per §4.</li><li><b>Reading note.</b> <i>stern observation[s] buch</i>: the source draft has <i>observation buch</i>; the genitive <i>-s</i> is supplied in brackets. <i>höoren</i> (for <i>hören</i>) is the draft's spelling, kept verbatim in the source block.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Signatum Caßell am 9t. feb: ao: etc. 91</i> = \"Given at <b>Kassel</b>, 9 February, year etc. [15]91\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Hesse-Kassel, like Denmark, kept the Julian calendar; it did not adopt the Gregorian reform in this period). The dateline date agrees with the dataset label (<i>1591. 9. februar. Kassel</i>); no discrepancy. <i>Signatum</i> (\"signed/given\") is the chancery dating verb, parallel to the Danish <i>Actum</i> (§5.5).</li><li><b>MS witness note.</b> The dataset records the witness folder as <i>\"Landgraf Moritz (!) Correspondenz\"</i> — the editorial <i>(!)</i> (sic) flags that the draft is filed under Landgrave <b>Moritz</b>'s correspondence series at Marburg though it is <b>Wilhelm IV</b>'s letter (Moritz succeeded Wilhelm in 1592); reproduced as given.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'malis aber' corrected to 'mali faber' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 55; the English's bracketed Latin citation updated to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German and its Latin insertions are rendered securely and the historical setting (Rothmann's 1590 Hven visit, the pasteboard-instruments rumour, the Kassel star-catalogue) is documented. The one genuinely rough patch is the ungrammatical draft clause <i>selbst praesentis tui malis aber gewesen seiest</i>: <i>malis</i> sits as a Latin ablative where the sense needs \"a cause/author of your present ills,\" and <i>aber</i> is best read as a miswriting of <i>Urheber</i> — translated to that sense but flagged here, not silently smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening address, all four body paragraphs, and the Kassel/9 February 1591 closing) against Dreyer's page images, TBOO XIV pp.54-55 (slice pages 1-2; letter no. 85). Every proper name (Wilhelm, Christoph Rothmann, Tycho Brahe, Kassel, Katzenelnbogen), the date (9 February 1591), and the embedded Latin phrases (pro observationibus siderum, ingruente illa temporum mutatione, humores, tabellas/catalogos stellarum, subscription, Instrumenta) check out against Dreyer with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation. One TEXT_ERROR found: our source_text's 'praesentis tui malis aber' corrupts Dreyer's 'praesentis tui mali faber'. Per the grading rule this forces grade C even though its practical effect on the English sense is low (verdict MINOR).</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text corrupts Dreyer's Latin tag 'mali faber' (architect/maker of your own ill, echoing the classical 'faber est suae quisque fortunae') into the nonsensical 'malis aber', dropping the word 'faber' and mis-splitting 'mali'. The published English's bracketed gloss '[praesentis tui malis]' reproduces the corrupted source rather than Dreyer's actual reading, though the English prose itself ('a cause of your present ills') still conveys roughly the right sense despite the corrupted citation. Dreyer prints: <i>vndt alfo felbft præfentis tui mali faber gewefen feieft. (TBOO XIV p.55, line 15)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.54-56 page images. 'præfentis tui mali faber' is unambiguous on p.55. The English prose itself needed no substantive change (it already conveyed the sense), but its bracketed Latin quotation was updated for consistency with the corrected Latin. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Marburg, Hessisches Staatsarchiv, Landgraf Moritz (!) Correspondenz mit Gelehrten.. Letter draft"], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann ↔ Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15890726001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Rothmann's report of prolonged illness and the sketch of Tycho's observatory, wishes him recovery, and cautions that the ailments should not be self-inflicted by excess.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910209001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910401001", "d": "1591-04-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 1 April 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO WILHELMO, LANDGRAVIO HASSIAe, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIEGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO CLEMENTISSIMO. S. D.</p><p>REDDIDIT mihi, Illustrissime Princeps, a Celsit. T. amandatus hoc nomine Tabellarius, die 23. Martij, literas Celsit. T. 3. Februarij labentis Anni ad me scriptas: in quibus Celsit. T. statim ab initio, de tarditate et raritate mearum literarum, quodque ex Obseruationibus meis Mathematicis nihil adipiscatur, mecum expostulat. At ego superiori aestate Celsit. T. scripsi, atque in Cometa proxime elapsi Anni meas Obseruationes communicaui, et simul, quae de illo Mathematice breuiter dicenda habui, exposui: Ad quae omnia nihil adhuc responsi accepi, adeo vt addubitem, an ea ad Celsit. T. manus sarta tecta peruenerint, nec ne: praesertim, cum ROTHMANNO a Celsit. T. huc ablegato, qui ante Hyemem praeteritam, circa Septembris finem, hinc decessit, nihil de his constiterit. Scripsi etiam denuo per eundem ROTHMANNVM, sed meas Celsit. T. non fuisse allatas, cum ipse ROTHMANNVS nondum sese in Aula Celsit. T. stiterit, haud obscure conijcio. Mansit ille hîc mecum quinque circiter septimanas, intereaque cum illo de rebus Astronomicis non inuite contuli, vnaque Instrumentorum meorum formam, Magnitudinem, Diuisionem et Pinnacidiorum rationem ipsum designare, vti ipsi libuit, libenter permisi: quorum aliqua, dum hîc moraretur, ad T. Celsit. literis consignata, se missurum aiebat, nonnulla vero plenius perlustrata et accuratius descripta secum Cassellas allaturum indicabat. Non tamen omnia ad T. Celsit. quae in Machinis Astronomicis peruiderat se referre audere tacite insinuabat: neque enim Celsit. T. in omnibus illi fidem habituram existimabat, siquidem pleraque in illis omnem fidem excedere, nec vestra his conferri posse, sponte fateretur, ne, si forte horum magnitudinem, exquisitam structuram, et inter obseruandum tractandi stabilitatem, prout res erat, luculenter exponeret, in gratiam mei id loqui, et vestris hac in parte fabricis, quippiam minus decore derogare videretur: At ego illi liberum reliqui, ea, quae hic viderat, siue dicere, siue tacere, prout ipsemet consultius id esse arbitraretur. Sed cum intra semestre iam elapsum ad T. Celsit. nondum reuersus sit, satis se Pythagoreum in meis non omnimode istic propalandis, exhibuit. Ego sane, cum Brumam instare viderem, vtpote tum Michaëlis Festo prae foribus existente, a quo tempore, ob appropinquantem Hyemem, nauigationes maris Baltici magis magisque importunae euadunt, ipsum adhortatus sum, imo quantum licuit vrsi, vt quam citissime fieri posset, se Cassellas reciperet. Neque enim dubitabam, Celsi!. T. desiderio cognoscendi ea, quae hîc viderat, quaeque mecum egerat, impense teneri. Is quidem propediem se istuc rediturum spondebat, at interim per patriam suam sibi transeundum, negotiorum quorundam expediendorum gratia, referebat. Fieri itaque potest, vt istic valetudinarius delitescat, erat enim cum hinc decederet, minus adhuc confirmatâ sanitate, quod color faciei admodum plalescens, satis innuebat. At quod per literas idipsum, vel si quas alias habuerit diuturnae istius morae causas, non significarit, miror: neque enim facile crediderim, humanitus ipsi quippiam accidisse. Videbatur alias T. Celsit. vti par erat, obsequentissimus: Et Stellarum descriptionem atque ex obseruationibus vestris demonstrationem, prout illi iniunctum erat, breui se animo lubenti absoluturum satis promisit: quod vt mature etiam efficeret, ipsum sedulo et saepius instigaui, quo collatio tandem inter nostras vtrinque Obseruationes institui queat, et qua de causa Quina illa Scrupula, in quibus differimus, desiderentur, euidenti aliqua comprobatione enodari possit. Cum autem ex eo tempore, quo hinc decessit, omnino nihil de eo, vbi locorum haereat, constiterit (in Daniâ enim eum esse non est quod T. Celsit. suspicetur) remitto literas, quas T. Celsit. mihi per hunc eundem Tabellarium ad illum dirigendas transmisit: cum Celsit. T. multo commodius possit, in quâ Germaniae parte nunc commoretur, explicari (si modo nondum Cassellas reuersus sit) atque eo literas, siue has siue alias propria autoritate dirigere. Quantum ad Mathematicas Obseruationes et Stellarum Fixarum designationes, quas Cel. T. sibi communicari clementer cupit, sciat me Octingentarum circiter Fixarum canonicam descriptionem nunc moliri, eamque Tomi nostri primi Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum (qui adhuc sub Praelo est) Capiti 2. inserere decreuisse, vnaque earum Obseruationes quas habui accuratiores, et his innixas demonstrationes Geometricas. Quin et alia de Sole et Luna caelitus deducta Obseruata, atque in Numeros per Triangulorum rationem digesta, idem Tomus cap. 1. complectitur; vt nihil dicam de ijs, quae nouam illam et supra modum mirandam Anni 1572. Stellam, alias luculenter tractata concernunt. Quibus subinde nonnulla ad reliquos quoque Planetas pertinentia, oportunitate sic exigente, intermiscentur. Ostendi autem ROTHMANNO hîc praesenti Volumen illud Progymnasmatum, quoad hactenus absolutum est, in quo etiam pauculi desiderantur quaterniones, cum per se alias magnum sit, et vsque in quintum Alphabetum excreuerit. Is T. C. de contentis in eo, quatenus in memoria habet, vbi reuersus fuerit, certiorem facile reddet. Quam primum vero Opus illud, totaliter labore Typographico absolutum fuerit, nullatenus intermittam quin C. T. eius reddam participem. Sunt quaedam ingrata obstacula, quibus quo minus non solum hoc, sed et alia quae meditor, Astronomiae instaurandae sustentacula ad optatum finem, eâ quâ maxime cuperem maturatione perducere queam, nolens volens impediar, sed spero et ab his atque alijs felicem et exoptatam liberationem, quacunque tandem id ratione fieri queat. Omne solum forti Patria, et coelum vndique supra est. Interim C. T. non aegre ferat, quod plura ex meis Obseruationibus et inuentis Astronomicis non mittam, id enim minus consultum, nec etiam, ob tot viarum mari terrâque anfractus, atque hominum, in quorum forte manus interea peruenire possent, non omnino perspectam sinceritatem, satis tutum arbitror. Mora haec qualiscunque fuerit, fenore, vti spero, compensabitur. Daniae Patriae nostrae status, quem scire iam denuo expetis, est in hoc quasi interregno, adhuc (quod diuinae bonitati acceptum ferimus) satis tranquillus et fortunatus. Serenissimus Princeps noster in Regem electus, indies proficit non saltem aetate et corporis vigore atque robore, sed et, quod pluris faciendum duco, virtute atque bonis artibus; ita vt non dubiam conceperimus spem, illum optimi et laudatissimae memoriae Patris heroica vestigia non segniter suo tempore ingressurum; atque ad Rempub. laudabiliter et decenter administrandam eximie idoneum futurum, tanquam ad hoc natum, et diuinitus nobis in locum praestantissimi Parentis regno huic praemature adempti, restitutum. Interim autem dum iunior hic Princeps adhuc sub Curatoribus, viris praecipuis, ob aetatem versatur, Quatuor Seniores e regni Consiliarijs Reipu. gubernacula administrant: Inter quos Primarius est Dn. NICOLAVS CAASIVS, Cancellarius, vir vsu rerum, iudicio et consultâ prudentiâ, insigniter, praeter Generis antiquam Nobilitatem, clarus. In literis etiam humanioribus, praesertim vero Historijs, et quae ad Politiarum gubernationem conducunt, apprime versatus. Si quid vero arduum et solito grauius inciderit, in Comitia Regni, quae quotannis semel circa Solstitium aestiuum celebrantur, ad vniuersum Regni Senatum, vel etiam ante statutum tempus, si opus fuerit, conuocatum, defertur. Ita vt Aristocratia quaedam, quae etiam reipub. forma non admodum improbata est, apud nos, donec Serenissimus Rex Electus ad maturiorem aetatem peruenerit, existat. Intereaque omnia moderate et consulte administrantur, vna cum Principis nostri, vti par est, summa obseruantia. DEVS in posterum etiam benedictionem clementer largiatur, vt omnia pacate et feliciter succedant et conseruentur, exoptatissimumque nostrum Principem benigne protegat, et in suscepto virtutis tramite confirmet. Quantum ad me meaque attinet, ea pristinam ferme retinent conditionem. Ego in hac Insula priuatus dego nec me rebus immisceo politicis, quod etiam nunquam libens feci, nisi necessitate aliqua compulsus. Sunt alias satis multi qui ista affectant, quique talibus Officijs, me forte multo melius praeesse queant, quibus ego hanc, vbi eam assequuntur, fortunam nullatenus inuideo, sed multo potius gaudeo, quod in copia reperiantur, qui me talibus verius oneribus quam honoribus liberent, vt eo commodius et tranquillius, ocio frui Philosophico liceat, atque rebus coelestibus potius quam terrenis vacare; quamuis ne sic quidem ab impedimentis et tumultuationibus, quas vita suggerit ciuilis, plane exemtus, etiam cum maxime cupiam, esse queam; siue id sic quodam fato meo, siue quorundam sinistrâ intelligentiâ eueniat. Verum ego animi magnitudine, integritate, et veri iustique obseruantiâ id, quicquid erit, fauente nobis diuino, quod omnia aequis introspicit oculis, numine, exsuperare adnitar; et altiores curas coelesti Philosophiae, cui me hactenus totum deuoui, aspirante etiam nostro conatui coelestium opifice, porro impendere pergam. De Animali isto, cuius etiam Celsit. T. in literis suis meminit, sic habeat: Nullum eiuscemodi hîc in Dania reperitur, neque Hafniae, neque prope Friderisburgum. Fui enim aliquoties cum Sereniss. Rege nostro, laudatissimae memoriae, in ferarum horto Friderifburgensi, neque tamen huiusmodi animal vnquam conspexi, nec a quoquam relatione audiui illud istic nutriri. Hafniae autem cognoui ante Annum vnum vel alterum animal fuisse e Noruegia oriundum, quod Rangiferum Latini vocant, Noruegi autem Rinsdüer/ quod an adhuc ibi sit valde dubito, non enim possunt istae bestiae calorem, qui vel apud nos est, sustinere. Scripsi nihilominus ad praefectum Arcis Hafniacae, qui itidem affirmabat Ceruum istum Rangiferum in aestate praeterita prae calore expirasse, alias eum facile ab ipso pro T. Celsit. impetrassem. Existimo autem hanc ipsam esse feram de qua Celsit. T. scribit, et aberratum ab eo saltem esse in nomine, tunc quoque proceritate, ob cornuum longitudinem et multifariam intricationem, quisquis ille tandem fuerit, qui apud Celsit. T. hac de re mentionem fecerit. Adiunxi vero huius Animalis genuinam designationem, non tam ex Icone Gesneriana, quae tamen non multum aberrauit, quam indicatione cuiusdam ex Studiosis meis, qui Bergis in Noruegia natus est, atque se aliquoties huiuscemodi feras vidisse refert. Tunc quoque de natura earum, quae illi comperta erant, nonnulla significauit, quae etiam annotanda duxi, vt T. Celsit. desiderijs etiam hac in parte, quantum in me est, satisfieret. Si in posterum aliquid certius vel de hoc, vel alijs, quibus Celsit. T. delectari cognouero, intelligere licuerit, haud intermittam, quin Celsit. T. de ijs certiorem reddam. Habeo ipsemet Alcem quandam iuniorem, mihi nuper dono datam, quam si Celsit. T. expetit, non grauatim eam per occasionem mittam, et si in alijs longe potioribus quippiam per me fieri poterit, quod Celsit. T. acceptum siet, inueniar in exsequendo quam paratissimus.</p><p>Valeat T. Celsit. quam diutissime et felicissime.<br>Datum Vraniburgi, Cal. Aprilis, Anno 91.<br>T. Celsit. Addictiss. TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda: most clement Prince and Lord — greeting.</b></p><p>The courier dispatched for this purpose by Your Highness delivered to me, Most Illustrious Prince, on 23 March, the letter Your Highness had written to me on 3 February of the current year: in which Your Highness, right from the outset, complains to me of the tardiness and rarity of my letters, and that from my mathematical observations [you] gain nothing. But I wrote to Your Highness last summer, and communicated my observations of the comet of the year just past, and at the same time set out what I had briefly to say about it mathematically: to all of which I have as yet received no reply, so that I begin to doubt whether or not these reached Your Highness's hands sound and entire — especially since, of these matters, nothing was known to Rothmann, who was sent here by Your Highness and who departed hence before this past winter, around the end of September. I also wrote again by the same Rothmann, but I conjecture, not obscurely, that mine were not brought to Your Highness, since Rothmann himself has not yet presented himself at Your Highness's court. He stayed here with me about five weeks, and in the meantime I conferred with him about astronomical matters not unwillingly, and gladly permitted him, as it pleased him, to sketch the form, magnitude, division and arrangement of the sights [<i>pinnacidia</i>] of my instruments — some of which, while he tarried here, he said he would send to Your Highness committed to writing, while others, more fully surveyed and more accurately described, he indicated he would bring with him to Kassel. Yet he tacitly hinted that he did not dare to report to Your Highness everything he had observed in the astronomical machines: for he reckoned that Your Highness would not give him credence in all things, since he would freely confess that most things in them surpass all belief, and that yours cannot be compared with these — lest, if perchance he were to set out plainly, as the matter stood, their magnitude, their exquisite construction, and their steadiness in handling while observing, he should seem to be saying it to gratify me, and on that head to detract somewhat unbecomingly from your own apparatus. But I left him free either to tell or to keep silent about what he had seen here, as he himself judged the more advisable. Yet since within the half-year now elapsed he has not yet returned to Your Highness, he has shown himself Pythagorean enough in not making my affairs known there in every particular. I, for my part, when I saw mid-winter at hand — Michaelmas being then before the door, from which season, owing to the approaching winter, voyages on the Baltic Sea become more and more troublesome — urged him, indeed pressed him as far as I could, to betake himself to Kassel as soon as ever might be. For I did not doubt that Your Highness was held by an eager desire to learn the things he had seen here and had transacted with me. He for his part pledged that he would return thither shortly, but said that in the meantime he had to pass through his own homeland, for the sake of dispatching certain business. It may therefore be that he lies hidden there in poor health, for when he departed hence his health was as yet not fully restored, as the rather pallid colour of his face sufficiently intimated. But that he has not signified this very thing by letter, or whatever other causes he may have had for that long delay, I marvel at; for I should not easily believe that anything has befallen him in the way of mortality. He seemed otherwise, as was fitting, most obedient to Your Highness; and he promised fully that he would shortly, with a willing mind, complete the description of the stars and the demonstration from your observations, as had been enjoined upon him: and that he might accomplish this in good time, I diligently and repeatedly urged him on, so that a collation might at length be set up between our observations on both sides, and that the reason why those five minutes [<i>scrupula</i>], in which we differ, are wanting might be unravelled by some evident proof. But since, from the time when he departed hence, absolutely nothing has been known of him — where in the world he is lodged (for there is no reason for Your Highness to suspect that he is in Denmark) — I send back the letter which Your Highness transmitted to me by this same courier to be directed to him: since Your Highness can much more conveniently find out in what part of Germany he now resides (if only he has not yet returned to Kassel), and direct thither, on your own authority, the letter, whether this one or others. As for the mathematical observations and the designations of the fixed stars which Your Highness clemently desires to be communicated to him, let it be known that I am now at work on a canonical description of about eight hundred fixed stars, and have determined to insert it into Chapter 2 of the first volume of our <i>Astronomical Preliminary Exercises</i> [<i>Progymnasmata</i>] (which is still under the press), together with the more accurate observations of them which I have had, and the geometrical demonstrations resting upon these. Indeed the same volume, in Chapter 1, also embraces other observed data concerning the Sun and Moon, derived from the heavens and reduced into numbers by the method of triangles; to say nothing of those things that concern that new and beyond-measure marvellous Star of the year 1572, elsewhere fully treated. With these are interspersed, from time to time, as occasion so requires, some things pertaining also to the remaining planets. Now I showed Rothmann, while he was present here, that volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, so far as it has hitherto been completed, in which even a few quaternions are still wanting, although it is otherwise great in itself and has grown right up to the fifth alphabet. He, when he has returned, will easily inform Your Highness of its contents, so far as he holds them in memory. But as soon as that work has been wholly completed by the labour of the press, I shall by no means fail to make Your Highness a sharer in it. There are certain unwelcome obstacles by which, willy-nilly, I am hindered from bringing not only this, but also the other supports of the astronomy to be restored which I am planning, to their wished-for end with that swiftness which I should most greatly desire; but I hope for a happy and longed-for deliverance from these and others, by whatever means it may at last come to pass. To the brave man every soil is a fatherland, and the heaven is overhead on every side. Meanwhile let Your Highness not take it ill that I do not send more of my observations and astronomical findings, for I judge that less advisable, nor indeed sufficiently safe, on account of so many windings of the roads by sea and land, and the not entirely well-ascertained good faith of the men into whose hands they might perchance fall in the meantime. This delay, of whatever sort it shall have been, will, as I hope, be compensated with interest. The state of our fatherland Denmark, which you now ask again to know, is in this quasi-interregnum still (a thing we set down to the divine goodness) tranquil and fortunate enough. Our Most Serene Prince, elected as King, advances day by day not only in age and in the vigour and strength of body, but also — what I reckon should be valued more highly — in virtue and in good accomplishments; so that we have conceived no doubtful hope that he will in his own time tread, not slothfully, in the heroic footsteps of his Father of best and most praiseworthy memory, and will be exceedingly fit to administer the commonwealth laudably and becomingly, as one born for this and divinely restored to us in the place of that most excellent Parent prematurely taken from this kingdom. In the meantime, however, while this younger Prince still abides, on account of his age, under Guardians, men of the foremost rank, Four Elders from the Councillors of the realm administer the helm of the commonwealth: among whom the chief is Master Niels Kaas, the Chancellor, a man notably distinguished — besides the ancient nobility of his line — by experience of affairs, by judgment and by considered prudence. He is also exceedingly well-versed in the more humane letters, but especially in history and in the things that conduce to the government of polities. But if anything arduous and weightier than usual arises, it is referred to the Diet of the realm — which is held once each year about the summer solstice — to the whole Senate of the realm, convened even before the appointed time, if there be need. So that a certain Aristocracy — which is also a form of commonwealth not altogether disapproved — exists among us, until the Most Serene Elected King shall have reached a riper age. And in the meantime all things are administered with moderation and counsel, together with the highest deference, as is fitting, toward our Prince. May GOD also for the future clemently bestow His blessing, that all things may turn out and be preserved peacefully and happily, and may He kindly protect our most longed-for Prince and confirm him in the path of virtue he has taken up. As for what concerns me and mine, they keep almost their former condition. I live as a private man on this island and do not mix myself in political affairs, which indeed I never did willingly, unless compelled by some necessity. There are otherwise quite enough who court such things, and who could preside over such offices perhaps far better than I — to whom I in no way envy this fortune, when they attain it, but rather rejoice much more that there are found in abundance those who may free me from such burdens (more truly so than honours), so that I may the more conveniently and tranquilly enjoy philosophical leisure, and be free for heavenly rather than earthly matters; although not even thus can I be wholly exempt from the impediments and tumults that civil life supplies, even when I most desire it — whether this comes about by some fate of mine, or by the sinister contrivance of certain persons. But I shall strive to surmount it, whatever it shall be, by greatness of spirit, by integrity, and by observance of the true and the just, the divine power favouring us — which looks into all things with impartial eyes; and I shall go on henceforth to devote my higher cares to that heavenly Philosophy to which I have hitherto wholly given myself, the Maker of the heavens likewise breathing favour upon our endeavour. Concerning that Animal, of which Your Highness also makes mention in your letter, let the matter stand thus: no creature of that kind is to be found here in Denmark, neither at Copenhagen nor near Frederiksborg. For I have several times been with our Most Serene King, of most praiseworthy memory, in the game-park at Frederiksborg, and yet never beheld any animal of this sort, nor heard from anyone by report that it is kept there. At Copenhagen, however, I learned that a year or two ago there had been an animal originating from Norway, which the Latins call <i>Rangifer</i> [reindeer], but the Norwegians <i>Rinsdüer</i> [<i>Rensdyr</i>]; whether it is still there I greatly doubt, for these beasts cannot endure the heat that there is even among us. I wrote nonetheless to the governor of the Castle of Copenhagen, who likewise affirmed that that <i>Rangifer</i> deer had expired this past summer from the heat — otherwise I should easily have obtained it from him for Your Highness. I judge, however, that this very beast is the one of which Your Highness writes, and that he at least went astray in the name — and also in the stature, on account of the length and the manifold tangling of the horns — whoever in the end it was who made mention of this matter to Your Highness. I have, moreover, appended a true depiction of this Animal, not so much from the figure in Gesner, which nevertheless did not greatly err, as from the information of one of my students who was born at Bergen in Norway, and who relates that he has several times seen beasts of this kind. Then too he signified some things, which were known to him, about their nature, which I have thought worth noting down, so that Your Highness's wishes might be satisfied in this part also, so far as is in me. If hereafter I shall have come to learn or to understand anything more certain, whether about this or about other things in which I shall have learned that Your Highness takes delight, I shall by no means fail to inform Your Highness of them. I have myself a certain young Elk [<i>Alces</i>], lately given to me as a gift, which, if Your Highness desires it, I shall not grudgingly send as occasion offers; and if in other and far weightier matters anything can be done by me that may be acceptable to Your Highness, I shall be found most ready in the carrying-out.</p><p>May it fare with Your Highness as long and as happily as may be.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the Kalends of April, the year [15]91.<br>Your Highness's most devoted, TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient (dataset-tag discrepancy — flagged).</b> The dataset records the recipient as <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, but the letter is addressed and written <i>to</i> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592): the salutation names him with full titulature, and the body addresses <i>Celsitudo Tua</i> / <i>Cels. T.</i> (\"Your Highness\") throughout. Rothmann is the <i>subject</i> of the letter, discussed in the third person — Tycho reports on Rothmann's visit and unexplained failure to return to Kassel. The header therefore follows the source's own salutation; the dataset's `recipient:Christopher Rothmann` is an indexing error.</li><li><b>Sender / place / date.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. Dateline <i>Datum Vraniburgi, Cal. Aprilis, Anno 91</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, the Kalends of April [1 April], 1591\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The Roman-calendar idiom <i>Cal. Aprilis</i> is the 1st of the month; rendered with idiom and civil gloss per protocol §3. The catalogue `label` (\"1591. 1. april. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I), printed at Uraniborg/Wandsbek, 1596 — reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. The 1596 volume is the publication in which Tycho assembled his exchange with the Hesse-Kassel court (the Landgrave and Rothmann). No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b><i>Wilhelm, Landgravius Hassiae</b></i> — Landgrave Wilhelm IV \"the Wise\" of Hesse-Kassel, the leading princely patron of observational astronomy of the day, with his own observatory at Kassel; <i>Catzenellebogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain et Nidda</i> are the subsidiary Hessian comital titles, retained as in the source.</li><li><b><i>Rothmannus</b></i> — <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician at Kassel (protocol §5.6). He had travelled to Hven and stayed with Tycho \"about five weeks\" in 1590, and at the date of this letter had still not returned to Kassel; Tycho's report of his pallor and disappearance is historically the last clear contemporary trace of Rothmann's movements. <i>\"satis se Pythagoreum … exhibuit\"</i> — \"he has shown himself Pythagorean enough,\" i.e. as discreetly silent (about Tycho's instruments) as the secrecy-bound Pythagoreans; kept as the allusion.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Instrumentorum … formam, Magnitudinem, Diuisionem et Pinnacidiorum rationem</i> = \"the form, magnitude, division and arrangement of the sights of [my] instruments.\" <i>Pinnacidium</i> → <b>sight (pinnule)</b> per the controlled instrument glossary (§5.2). <i>Machinis Astronomicis</i> rendered \"astronomical machines\" (Tycho's large instruments). <i>vestra … fabricis</i> / <i>vestris … fabricis</i> — the Landgrave's <i>own</i> apparatus at Kassel, with which Rothmann diplomatically declined to compare Tycho's.</li><li><b>Technical: the five minutes.</b> <i>Quina illa Scrupula, in quibus differimus</i> = \"those five minutes [of arc] in which we differ.\" <i>Scrupulum</i> here is the astronomical minute (the ~5′ standing discrepancy between the Hven and Kassel stellar positions that the planned <i>collatio</i> — collation — was to resolve). Rendered \"minutes,\" with the Latin retained in brackets.</li><li><b>The <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</b> <i>Tomi nostri primi Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum … adhuc sub Praelo</i> = the first volume of the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (\"Preliminary Exercises for the Restoration of Astronomy\"), then in press (it appeared posthumously, 1602). <i>Octingentarum circiter Fixarum canonicam descriptionem</i> = \"a canonical description of about eight hundred fixed stars\" (Tycho's star catalogue). <i>demonstrationes Geometricas … per Triangulorum rationem</i> = \"geometrical demonstrations … by the method of triangles\" (trigonometric reduction). <i>nouam illam … Anni 1572. Stellam</i> = the new star (nova) of 1572, the subject of the volume's later parts.</li><li><b>Printing units.</b> <i>pauculi … quaterniones</i> = \"a few quaternions\" (gatherings of four sheets); <i>vsque in quintum Alphabetum excreuerit</i> = \"has grown right up to the fifth alphabet\" — signature-alphabets (each ~23 gatherings), i.e. the book had already run to a very large extent. Kept as period printing-house idiom.</li><li><b>Astronomy programme.</b> <i>Astronomiae instaurandae sustentacula</i> = \"the supports of the astronomy to be restored\" — Tycho's <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> programme (protocol §5.1). <i>Omne solum forti Patria, et coelum vndique supra est</i> — a sententia (\"To the brave man every soil is a fatherland, and the heaven is everywhere overhead\"), echoing the Ovid/proverbial <i>omne solum forti patria est</i>; kept as the maxim it is, foreshadowing Tycho's own later exile.</li><li><b>Danish politics (the regency).</b> Written during the minority of <b>Christian IV</b> (b. 1577; elected king 1580, crowned only 1596): <i>Serenissimus Princeps noster in Regem electus</i> = \"our Most Serene Prince, elected as King.\" <i>Patris … laudatissimae memoriae</i> = <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 4 April 1588), the \"Father of most praiseworthy memory.\" <i>Quatuor Seniores e regni Consiliarijs</i> = the <b>four regents</b> of the Rigsråd governing during the minority; <i>Dn. NICOLAVS CAASIVS, Cancellarius</i> = <b>Niels Kaas</b> (1535–1594), Chancellor of Denmark and chief of the regency. <i>Comitia Regni … circa Solstitium aestiuum</i> = the Diet (assembly of the Rigsråd) meeting about midsummer. <i>Aristocratia … reipub. forma</i> = \"an Aristocracy … a form of commonwealth,\" the regency described in classical constitutional terms.</li><li><b>The animals (reindeer and elk).</b> <i>Animal … quod Rangiferum Latini vocant, Noruegi autem Rinsdüer</i> = \"the animal which the Latins call <i>Rangifer</i> [reindeer], but the Norwegians <i>Rinsdüer</i>\" (Danish/Norwegian <i>rensdyr</i>); the vernacular word is set off by the source's own virgule (<i>Rinsdüer/</i>), preserved in the Source block. The Copenhagen specimen had died of heat (<i>prae calore expirasse</i>). <i>Icone Gesneriana</i> = the woodcut in <b>Conrad Gessner</b>'s <i>Historia animalium</i> (1551–58). <i>Studiosis meis, qui Bergis in Noruegia natus est</i> = one of Tycho's students born at <b>Bergen</b>, Norway. <i>Alcem quandam iuniorem</i> = \"a certain young Elk\" (<i>Alces</i>, the European elk/moose), which Tycho offers to send — the very animal that (per the later tradition) is said to have died at Landskrona; here only the offer is made. <i>praefectum Arcis Hafniacae</i> = the governor (lensmand) of Copenhagen Castle.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Hafnia</i> → <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Friderisburgum / Friderifburgensi</i> → <b>Frederiksborg</b> (the long-s misprint <i>Friderifburgensi</i> kept in Source); <i>Cassellae</i> → <b>Kassel</b>; <i>Bergae</i> → <b>Bergen</b>; <i>Vraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (§5.5). <i>Valeat T. Celsit. quam diutissime et felicissime</i> = \"May it fare with Your Highness as long and as happily as may be.\" <i>T. Celsit. Addictiss.</i> = <i>Tuae Celsitudini Addictissimus</i>, \"Your Highness's most devoted.\"</li><li><b>Source orthography / printer's slips (kept in Source block).</b> <i>Celsi!. T.</i> (a printer's exclamation-point for <i>Celsit.</i>), <i>plalescens</i> (for <i>pallescens</i>, \"growing pale\"), <i>Friderifburgensi</i> (long-s/f confusion), <i>Rinsdüer/</i> (with virgule): all reproduced verbatim per protocol §4.2 and read in the translation as the obvious intended forms, noted here rather than emended in place.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the only structural caveat is editorial, not linguistic (the dataset's `recipient` tag names Rothmann, whereas the letter is addressed to Landgrave Wilhelm IV and discusses Rothmann in the third person — corrected in the header and apparatus, not in the text). Minor printer's slips in the source are read to their intended forms and flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per the brief this was audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of quality. Read the full Latin (Tycho to Wilhelm IV) against the full English line by line, checking every proper name (Wilhelm IV's titles Catzenellebogen/Dietz/Ziegenhain/Nidda, Rothmann, Niels Kaas), place (Uraniborg, Copenhagen, Frederiksborg, Bergen, Kassel, Norway), number (23 March, 3 February, 1 April/Cal. Aprilis, anno 91, ~800 fixed stars, five 'scrupula' of arc, five weeks, half-year, Four Elders, fifth alphabet, Chapters 1-2) and clause-level sense (the Rothmann-delay narrative, the Progymnasmata description, the Danish-regency passage, the reindeer/elk digression). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; the translation renders even the difficult indirect-discourse and subjunctive passages (e.g. the Rothmann-reticence sentence) with the correct subject/antecedent throughout.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 16, "prev": "brahe-t_15900418001", "next": "brahe-t_15920220002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900418001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15940928001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Defends himself against Wilhelm's complaint of sparse correspondence by noting that earlier letters with comet observations were apparently never received, as Rothmann was also unaware of them.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910401001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910522001", "d": "1591-05-22", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 22 May 1591 [O.S.] (catalogued 1587)", "m": "<p>Language: German (early modern / chancery, with Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Liber I; repr. Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none cited</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI<br>BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.WILHELM VON GOTTES<br>GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/<br>ETC.AVESTER lieber Besonder/ Daß jhr vns vnderm dato Vraniburg. am 2. Aprilis zu verstehen geben/ daß jhr jetzo den ersten<br>Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis in Druck zu<br>verfertigen fürhabt/ vnd vns denselbigen zu communiciren<br>erbotten/ das ist vns vmb so viel desto lieber gewesen zu vernemen/ dieweyl<br>jhr in gedachtem Tomo quasi ex professo Stellarum Fixarum<br>(darnach wir so lange zeit besonders verlangen gehabt) nicht allein tractieret, fsondert auch etliche hundert Stern auß ewern<br>Obseruationibus emendiret erzehlen thut. Wollen<br>demnach desselben Operis gewertig seyn/ mit Gnaden zu<br>verschulden. Anlangendt vnsern Mathematicum, hat er sich<br>zwar vor etlichen Monaten bey vns seiner Leibs Kranckheit halben beklagt/<br>Ist aber/ vnangesehen er vns schrifftlich zu verstehen geben/ vnd wir jhm<br>solchs gnediglichen befohlen haben/ gleichwol nicht angelangt. Daß er aber<br>nicht wider kömpt/ können wir vns nicht gnugsam verwundern/ vnd machen vns<br>schier die gedancken/ oder Suspition, daß er etwan ein schwere vnd schedliche KranckheitA habe/ Je lenger er nu denselben bey sich hat/ je erger es mit jhm<br>wirdt werden. Was das Riens betrifft/ ist ein DenischerA Jung bey vns deß GeschlechtsA ein Bilde/ der hat vns davon gesagt/ daß dero im Thiergarten zu<br>Friderichsburg/ Jtem/ zu<br>Koppenhagen/ etliche seyn sollen.<br>Wir haben jhn auch gefragt/ obs der Reiner seyen/ hat er nein gesagt/<br>sondern seye gar ein hoch Thier. Alß haben wir in libro GermanicoA Gesneri de Animalibus<br>Anno 1563. zu Zürich gedrucktA<br>folio LXXXV. Iconem et descriptionem desselben gefunden/ Aber bey denen/<br>so mit der Königin hieraussen zu Wolffenbüttel gewesen/ keinenA bericht davon haben können. Der Rhenen Böck oder Thier haben wir auß SchwedenA auff zwey mal fast in die hundert stück lassen abholen/ die sich auch<br>in Winterzeit gantz wol gehalten/ vnd auffm Eiß den Schlitten/ jhrem brauch<br>nach/ gezogen/ So baldt aber die Werme eingetreten/ alle gestorben/ daß wir<br>also sehen/ daß sie sich im Sommer/ vnd wennA die Werme antrit/ hier zu Landt nicht halten können. Was aber die<br>Elendt betrifft/ haben wir einesA in vnserm Thiergarten zur Zapffenburg/ helt sich von Martini biß<br>auff dato noch wol/ leufft vnd springt/ vnd ist guter<br>ding/ vnd sonderlich wennA wir in vnserm Thiergarten auff vnserm<br>grünen Wäglein herumb fahren/ leufft er den nechsten zu vns zu/ vnd wartet<br>auff vns alß wennsA ein Hund were/ Aber die Gehörn wollen jhm nicht sehr wachsen/ vnd<br>seyndt biß auff dato nicht vber eins Fingers lang. Ob nu das etwan ein Signum seye/ daß sie<br>sich nicht wol wöllen in diesem Landt arten/ das wirdt man sehen. Wir haben<br>wider in Schweden geschickt/ ob wir dero<br>mehr bekommen köndten/ Wo aber jhr vns eins oder zwey/ sonderlich die da<br>zahm weren/ zuschicken kündet/ daran thet jhr vns zu sonderm angenemen<br>gefallen/ Wir wollens auch in Gnaden hinwider erkennen/ vnd euch die<br>auffgewendte Vnkosten vnd Zehrung gnediglich erstatten. Was denn anlangt die<br>begerte Papiermacher/ mögen wir euch gnediglich nicht verhalten/ daß wir<br>vnder vns einen eintzigen haben/ derselb richtets allererst an/ vnd macht<br>sehr schro Werck/ wie auß gegenwertigen Papier/ vnd euch zugefertigtem<br>Brieff zu sehen/ Wir wollen vns aber zu Franckfurt vmbhören lassen/ ob wir nicht ein oder zween<br>darzu bewegen köndten/ daß sie sich zu euch begeben wolten/ Doch müssen wir<br>zuuor wissen/ welcher gestalt jhr sie halten vnd vnderhalten wollet: Wollen<br>wir alßdan jhre Abfertigung auffs ehest befördern helffen. Wolten wir euch/<br>dem wir mit Gnaden sonders wol gewogen/ alsoA gnediglich nicht verhalten.Datumin vnser Stadt vnd VestungACassel/ am 22. Maij, Anno 1591.WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the worshipful, our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, lord of Knudstrup. Wilhelm, by the grace of God Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our trusty and well-beloved: That you gave us to understand, under date of Uraniborg, 2 April, that you now have it in hand to bring out in print the first volume <i>On the More Recent Phenomena of the Heavens</i> [<i>de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis</i>], and have offered to communicate the same to us — this has been so much the more welcome to us to learn, inasmuch as in the said volume you treat, as it were professedly [<i>quasi ex professo</i>], of the <i>Fixed Stars</i> (for which we have so long had a particular longing), and not only that, but also recount several hundred stars amended out of your <i>observations</i> [<i>Obseruationibus</i>]. We shall accordingly look forward to that <i>work</i> [<i>Operis</i>], to requite it with [our] grace. As concerns our <i>mathematician</i> [<i>Mathematicum</i>], he did indeed, some months ago, complain to us of his bodily sickness; but, notwithstanding that he gave us to understand [the same] in writing, and that we graciously commanded him thereto, he has nonetheless not arrived. That he does not come back, we cannot sufficiently wonder at, and we almost form the thought, or <i>suspicion</i> [<i>Suspition</i>], that he may have some grave and harmful sickness; [and] the longer he now keeps it by him, the worse it will become with him. As concerns the <i>reindeer</i> [<i>Riens</i>], there is a Danish lad with us, of the <b>Bilde</b> [Bille] family, who has told us of it, that there ought to be some of them in the menagerie at Frederiksborg, likewise at Copenhagen. We also asked him whether they be the reindeer; he said no, but [that] it is quite a tall beast. So we have, in Gessner's German <i>book on animals</i> [<i>libro Germanico … de Animalibus</i>], printed at Zürich in the year 1563, on folio 85, found the <i>image and description</i> [<i>Iconem et descriptionem</i>] of the same; but from those who were out here at Wolfenbüttel with the Queen we could get no account of it. The reindeer-bucks, or beasts, we have had fetched out of Sweden, on two occasions, to nearly a hundred head, which also held up quite well in the winter season and drew the sledge upon the ice after their custom; but as soon as the warmth set in, they all died, so that we thus see that in summer, and when the warmth comes on, they cannot keep [alive] here in [this] land. But as concerns the elk, we have one in our menagerie at Zapfenburg; it has held up well from Martinmas until now, runs and leaps and is of good cheer, and especially when we drive about in our menagerie in our little green cart, it runs straight up to us and waits for us as if it were a dog; but its antlers will not grow much for it, and to this day are not above a finger's length. Whether this be perhaps a <i>sign</i> [<i>Signum</i>] that they will not well take to breeding in this land — that one will see. We have sent again into Sweden [to ask] whether we might get more of them; but if you could send us one or two, especially such as were tame, you would thereby do us a particularly agreeable favour. We shall also acknowledge it again in grace, and graciously reimburse you the costs and expenses laid out. As, then, concerns the requested <i>papermakers</i>, we may not graciously conceal from you that we have among us a single one; the same is only just now setting up, and makes very coarse work, as is to be seen from the present paper and from the letter dispatched to you. But we will have inquiry made for us at Frankfurt, [to see] whether we might not move one or two thereto, that they should be willing to betake themselves to you; yet we must first know in what manner you would keep and maintain them: we will then help to further their dispatch as soon as possible. This we would not graciously conceal from you, to whom we are by [our] grace especially well disposed. Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 22 May, in the year 1591. Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"the Wise,\" the observing prince of Kassel whose astronomers Christoph Rothmann and Joost Bürgi corresponded with Tycho; the long Tycho–Hesse exchange (vols VI–VII of Dreyer) is the documentary core of late-16th-c. observational astronomy. The titulature <i>Graue zu Catzenelnbogen</i> (Count of Katzenelnbogen) is the standard Hessian style.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Dem Vesten</i> — \"to the worshipful/steadfast,\" the chancery honorific for an untitled nobleman (<i>vest/fest</i> \"firm, steadfast\"); <i>vnserm lieben Besondern</i> = \"our trusty and well-beloved\" (protocol §5.5, <i>Vester lieber besonder</i>). <i>Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — lord of <b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp), the Brahe family seat.</li><li><b>Date crux (catalogue vs. dateline).</b> The dataset `label` reads <b>\"1587. 22. maj.\"</b>; the letter's own dateline reads <b>\"am 22. Maij, Anno 1591.\"</b> The header follows the dateline per protocol §4. The internal evidence corroborates <b>1591</b>, not 1587: the Landgrave is replying to Tycho's letter of \"2 April\" announcing that he now has the <i>first volume</i> \"de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis\" <i>in Druck</i> (in press) — i.e. the volume on the new-star/comet phenomena and the fixed-star catalogue (the work issued from Uraniborg as the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> / <i>De mundi … recentioribus phaenomenis</i>), whose printing was under way in the early 1590s, not 1587. The \"1587\" label is treated as a catalogue mis-dating and recorded, <b>not</b> silently corrected. The letter is printed in Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the edition of record. Date is <b>Old Style</b> (Hesse, like Denmark, kept the Julian calendar).</li><li><b><i>vnsern Mathematicum</b></i> — \"our mathematician,\" <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's astronomer, who had travelled to Hven in 1590 and, pleading illness, never returned to Kassel; the Landgrave's puzzlement (\"we cannot sufficiently wonder … that he does not come back\") fixes the situation in 1590–91. Identification of <i>Mathematicum</i> with Rothmann is secure on context; named here on first occurrence.</li><li><b>The natural-history matter (reindeer / elk).</b> The bulk of the letter answers a request from Tycho for live northern animals. <i>Riens / Rhenen</i> = <b>reindeer</b> (<i>Rentier</i>); <i>Reiner</i> likewise the reindeer; <i>Elendt</i> = the <b>elk / European moose</b> (<i>Elch</i>). <i>Thiergarten</i> = menagerie / animal park. Places: <i>Friderichsburg</i> = <b>Frederiksborg</b>; <i>Koppenhagen</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>; <i>Zapffenburg</i> = <b>Zapfenburg</b> (the Landgrave's game park near Kassel); <i>Wolffenbüttel</i> = <b>Wolfenbüttel</b>. <i>die Königin</i> — \"the Queen\": almost certainly <b>Sophie</b>, the Hessian-born consort/dowager moving between the Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel and Hessian courts (her exact identity not made explicit in the letter; left as \"the Queen\").</li><li><b><i>Gessner's German book on animals … Zürich 1563, folio 85.</b></i> = Conrad <b>Gessner</b>, <i>Thierbuch</i> (the German <i>Historiae animalium</i>), Zürich 1563; the Landgrave cites <i>Iconem et descriptionem</i> (\"the image and description\") on fol. 85 — a code-switch into Latin for the bibliographic reference.</li><li><b><i>deß Geschlechts ein Bilde.</b></i> <b>Resolved (2026-06-02):</b> Dreyer's facing 1596 Latin (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VI p. 247) renders it <i>ex familia BILDORVM</i>, 'of the family of the Bildes' — <i>Bilde</i> is the surname of the Danish noble <b>Bille/Bilde</b> family (here a young Bille in Hessian service), not 'image-maker'. Rendered 'of the Bilde [Bille] family.'</li><li><b>The papermakers.</b> <i>Papiermacher</i> — Tycho was equipping his own paper mill on Hven; the Landgrave reports that his single papermaker \"makes very coarse work\" (<i>sehr schro[ff] Werck</i>), evidenced by \"the present paper,\" and offers to recruit one or two from <b>Frankfurt</b> for Tycho — conditional on first knowing <i>welcher gestalt jhr sie halten … wollet</i> (\"in what manner you would keep and maintain them\"). The conditional is preserved (the dispatch is promised only <i>alßdan</i>, \"then,\" after that condition is met); no royal-style command here, a princely offer with a stated precondition.</li><li><b>Latin code-switching (chancery German over a learned-Latin technical lexicon, protocol §2).</b> Technical and bibliographic nouns are kept in Latin in the source and italicised on first use in the translation: <i>den ersten Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis</i>, <i>quasi ex professo</i>, <i>Stellarum Fixarum</i>, <i>Obseruationibus</i>, <i>Operis</i>, <i>Mathematicum</i>, <i>Suspition</i>, <i>Iconem et descriptionem</i>, <i>Signum</i>. The closing <i>Datum … Anno 1591</i> is the Latin dating formula.</li><li><b>The superscript \"A\" markers</b> scattered through the source (<i>ETC.A</i>, <i>KranckheitA</i>, <i>DenischerA</i>, <i>GeschlechtsA</i>, <i>gedrucktA</i>, <i>LXXXV.A</i>, <i>keinenA</i>, <i>SchwedenA</i>, <i>wennA</i>, <i>einesA</i>, <i>alsoA</i>, <i>am 22.A</i>, <i>VestungA</i>, final <i>…HESSEN.A</i>) are not part of the text: they are the anchor-marks of the 1596 printed edition's <b>marginal sidenotes/apparatus</b>, carried over as artefacts of extraction. They are reproduced as-printed in the Source block per protocol §4(2) and carry no lexical content; the translation ignores them. Likewise <i>fsondert</i> (for <i>sondern</i>) and <i>Datumin</i> / <i>.WILHELM</i> (lost word-spacing) are extraction run-ons, read as <i>sondern</i> and <i>Datum in</i> / <i>. Wilhelm</i>.</li><li><b>Princely register.</b> The plural-of-majesty <i>Wir/vns/euch–jhr</i> and the recurring <i>gnediglich(en)</i> (\"graciously\") are preserved throughout; <i>Wolten wir euch … gnediglich nicht verhalten</i> = \"this we would not graciously conceal from you\" (a standard chancery close). Two negations occur in that clause-pair (<i>nicht … nicht verhalten</i> across the final two sentences); both are the idiomatic litotes \"we would not conceal\" and are reproduced exactly, not collapsed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'befohlen' (from 'befehlen', to command) had been rendered as 'graciously granted him this'; corrected to 'graciously commanded him thereto', consistent with the sense of the following sentence expressing surprise at his non-return.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German body, the princely register, and the dateline are rendered securely. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02):</b> the former lexical crux <i>deß Geschlechts ein Bilde</i> is resolved — Dreyer's facing 1596 Latin (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VI p. 247) renders it <i>ex familia BILDORVM</i>, 'of the family of the Bildes' — i.e. <i>Bilde</i> is the surname of the Danish noble <b>Bille/Bilde</b> family (a young Bille in Hessian service), not 'image-maker'. The date (1591, against the catalogue's 1587) is confirmed by Dreyer's dateline. No residual crux.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — former crux resolved via Dreyer's facing Latin; the 1591 date is confirmed; only declared apparatus sigla remain.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief this is audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B. Checked the full German letter clause by clause against published_english: the reindeer/elk menagerie passage (Frederiksborg, Copenhagen, Gessner's De Animalibus Zurich 1563 folio 85, the ~100 reindeer from Sweden, the elk at Zapfenburg since Martinmas with antlers 'not above a finger's length'), the papermaker request (one papermaker at Kassel, inquiry to be made at Frankfurt for one or two more), and the closing date '22. Maij, Anno 1591'. All numbers, names, and place-names match with no fabrication or omission. One MISTRANSLATION found in the passage about 'our mathematician' (almost certainly Rothmann): 'befohlen' (commanded) is rendered as 'granted', inverting the agency of that clause. No Dreyer collation performed.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). German 'befohlen' (from 'befehlen') means 'commanded/ordered', not 'granted [permission]'. The next sentence — 'that he does not come back, we cannot sufficiently wonder at' — only makes sense if Wilhelm had ordered the mathematician to return and is now surprised he hasn't; 'granted him this' instead implies Wilhelm permitted him to stay away, which would make the following expression of surprise incoherent. At: “notwithstanding that he gave us to understand [the same] in writing, and that we graciously granted him this, he has non”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice exists for this letter (tboo: null); the fix rests entirely on internal evidence — the German source_text's own word 'befohlen' and the logical coherence of the surrounding sentence, which only makes sense as a command to return, not a grant of leave to stay away. No TEXT_ERROR claims were present to require Dreyer verification. Grade B: sound but uncollated against the edition of record.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 13, "prev": "brahe-t_15910203001", "next": "brahe-t_15910831001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15901001001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910908001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Welcomes news that the first volume of De recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis is in press, noting particular interest in the fixed-star content, and inquires about Rothmann's welfare.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910522001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910621001", "d": "1591-06-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Anders Christensen", "ck": "Anders Christensen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Christensen — Uraniborg, 21 June 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin address-rubric and Latin code-switches) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV, p. 70, letter no. 87) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums Arkiv [old signature: KA 185], original letter</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO DOMINO RECTORI, HEDERLIG OC HØGLERDT MAND, DOCTOR ANDERS CHRISTENSØN, MEDICINAe PROFESSORI VDI KIØBENHAFFNS VNIUERSITET, MIN SYNDERLIG GODE WEN, GANDSKE WENLIGEN TILSCHREFFUIT.</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig helsen nu oc altidt forsendt med Wor Herre. Magnifice d:ne rector, oc kiere doctorAndres, synderlig gode wen. Nest min wenlig tacksigelse for alt beuist gode, huilcket ieg aff formuen wil findis willig at forskylde, er min gandske wenlig bøn til eder, at i will wel giøre, oc lade vocire oc fordre for eder, oc andre flere aff eders collegis, hederlige oc høglerde professoribus, eders collegam, hederlig oc høglerdt mand doctor Nielß Crage, oc der till medt ocsaa m. Nielß Colding, om hand er nogen stedts at finde der vdi byen, oc lade dennom i min fogits, wed naffn Christen Judes neruerelse, huilcken ieg der paa fuldmact giffuit haffuer, effter som hand paa mine wegne eskendis worder, bestaa oc bekiende, alt hues de wide sig i sandhed at haffue tildraget den tidt, min tiener Rasmus Persøn, som nu er vdi Gunsøgaard, haffde vdsendt vdi forgangne sommer to mend aff Roschild til mig, borgemester Christen Skyt, oc en anden, wed naffn Peder Swerdtfeger, effterdi de war da her tilstede hoß mig, at de derfor enten samptligen, eller huer i seer for sig, widne och offentlich vdi alle eders neruerelse bekiende oc bestaa saa widt som de wide, at denne handell anrørendis er, oc kunde medt en gode conscientz for Gud oc dennom sielff wide oc bestaa at were i sandhed tilgangen, oc ydermere for øffuerigheden, der som behoff giøris, bekiender.</p><p>Oc er her till lougligen steffnt oc kaldit for:ne Rasmus Persen, ocsaasaa at tage forbemelte mend medt sig, om de haffuer noget heri at sige, som i kunde forfare aff den steffning oc kaldsbreff, min fogitt haffuer ladet leßt for hannom, paa huilcken hand oc sielff paaskreffuit haffuer. Oc møder ocsaa der hoß mine kaldtsmend, som haffuer her offuer werit, oc wille bestaa det i eders paahør, enten Rasmus møder sielff, heller oc hans fuldmectige medt de andre to, eller oc icke. Er der for min gandske wenlige bøn til eder, at i wille well giøre, oc lade mig widerfaris hervdj hues billigt oc ret er, ac landzens skick oc brug medbringer. Oc at ieg kand faa alltingst nu strax beskreffuit, oc vnder vniuersitetens segel bekrefftigit. Forseendis mig till eder oc de andre eders collegis, at i hervdi giørendis worder, saa at ieg icke skal haffue mig der vdoffuer at beklage, men tacke eder for eders wmage oc gode wilge vdi alle maade, huilcken ieg oc saa altidt will findis, saa widt mig mugligt kand were, at forskylle gandske godtwillig oc tilgnegt.</p><p>Oc will hermedt nu oc altidt haffue eder Gud almectigste befalendis.<br>Datum Vraniburg den 21. junij anno 1591.<br>Tyghe Brahe<br>Egen hand.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Magnificent Lord Rector, the honourable and most learned man, Doctor Anders Christensen, Professor of Medicine in the University of Copenhagen, my especially good friend — written in all friendliness.</b></p><p>My wholly friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with Our Lord. Magnificent Lord Rector, and dear Doctor Anders, especially good friend. Next after my friendly thanks for all the kindness shown [me] — which I will be found willing to requite according to my means — my wholly friendly request to you is that you would be so good as to summon and call before you, and before several others of your colleagues, the honourable and most learned professors, your colleague, the honourable and most learned man Doctor Niels Krag, and besides him also Master Niels Colding, if he is anywhere to be found there in the town, and to let them, in the presence of my bailiff, by name Christen Jude — to whom I have given full authority [in the matter], according as he is called upon on my behalf — affirm and acknowledge all that they know in truth to have taken place at the time when my servant Rasmus Persøn, who is now at Gunsøgaard, had sent out last summer two men from Roskilde to me, burgomaster Christen Skyt, and another, by name Peder Sverdfeger, inasmuch as they were then present here with me; that they therefore, either jointly or each one separately for himself, witness and publicly, in the presence of you all, acknowledge and affirm so far as they know what concerns this affair, and [what] they can with a good conscience before God and to themselves know and affirm to have truly come to pass, and further acknowledge it before the authorities, where need be.</p><p>And the aforesaid Rasmus Persen has herewith been lawfully summoned and cited, [and] also to take the aforementioned men with him, if they have anything to say herein, as you may learn from the summons and writ of citation which my bailiff has had read out to him, [and] upon which he has himself also endorsed [his name]. And [there] appear also there my summoners [witnesses to the citation], who have been over here, and will affirm it in your hearing — whether Rasmus appears himself, or else his attorney with the other two, or not [at all]. Therefore my wholly friendly request to you is that you would be so good as to let me obtain herein what is fair and right, and as the custom and usage of the land brings with it. And that I may get the whole [matter] now at once set down in writing, and confirmed under the University's seal. Trusting to you and the others, your colleagues, that you will act herein in such a way that I shall not have cause to complain of it, but to thank you for your trouble and good will in every way — which I too shall always be found, so far as may be possible for me, to requite, wholly willingly and devotedly.</p><p>And herewith I will, now and always, commend you to God Almighty.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 21st of June, in the year 1591.<br>Tyge Brahe<br>[In] his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Doctor Anders Christensen</i> (Andreas Christiernus / Christierni, 1551–1606), professor of medicine at the University of Copenhagen and, in 1591, its <b>Rector</b> (hence <i>Magnifico Domino Rectori</i>). The letter is addressed to him in that office, asking the consistory (university court) to take sworn testimony.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. The subscription <i>Egen hand</i> (\"[in] his own hand\") marks the original as an autograph — consistent with witness A being the <i>Originalbrev</i>.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A private letter of legal business, not a chancery register copy: Tycho asks the rector and his colleagues to receive and minute sworn witness-statements <i>under vniuersitetens segel</i> (\"under the University's seal\"). The matter is a lawsuit between Tycho and his former servant.</li><li><b>The Latin editorial regest</b> (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV, p. 70, no. 87) identifies the parties: the suit is between Tycho and <b>Erasmus Petri e vico Gundsömagle</b> (= Rasmus Pedersen / <i>Rasmus Persøn</i> of the village of Gundsømagle), and the witnesses sought are <i>doctor Nicolaus Kragius</i> and <i>magister Nicolaus Koldingus</i>. This confirms the persons named in the Danish body.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Doctor Nielß Crage</i> = <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602), humanist, historian and professor at Copenhagen; here a <i>collega</i> of the rector.</li><li><i>m. Nielß Colding</i> = <b>Master Niels Colding</b> (Nicolaus Koldingus); the prefix <i>m.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic Master, protocol §5.5).</li><li><i>min fogit … Christen Jude</i> = <b>Christen Jude</b>, Tycho's <i>foged</i> (bailiff/steward) on Hven, given <i>fuldmagt</i> (power of attorney) to act on his behalf; the variant <i>Judes</i> (gen.) of the working transcription matches Dreyer (<i>Iudes</i>).</li><li><i>min tiener Rasmus Persøn … nu … vdi Gunsøgaard</i> = <b>Rasmus Pedersen</b>, Tycho's former <i>tiener</i> (servant/retainer), now at <b>Gundsømagle</b> (a village NW of Roskilde, Zealand); the adverse party.</li><li><i>borgemester Christen Skyt</i> = <b>Christen Skyt</b>, <i>borgemester</i> (burgomaster) of <b>Roskilde</b>; a witness.</li><li><i>Peder Swerdtfeger</i> = <b>Peder Sverdfeger</b> (\"Sword-cutler,\" likely an occupational by-name, not relabelled); the second Roskilde witness.</li><li><b>Code-switches (Latin within Danish), preserved per protocol §2.</b> The address-rubric is Latin (<i>MAGNIFICO DOMINO RECTORI … MEDICINAe PROFESSORI …</i>); the body switches into Latin for the academic and legal vocabulary it has no native term for: <i>vocire</i> (← <i>vocare</i>, \"to summon/call\"), <i>collegis / collegam</i> (\"colleagues / colleague\"), <i>professoribus</i> (\"professors\"), <i>conscientz</i> (← <i>conscientia</i>, \"conscience\"), and the closing <i>Datum</i> (\"Given\"). These are kept in the Source block and rendered in the Translation.</li><li><b>Reading note (working transcription vs. Dreyer).</b> Where the working transcription reads <i>oc fordre</i> (\"and demand/cite\"), the Dreyer OCR (p. 70, l. 28) reads <i>oc farcire</i> — an evident misreading of a Latin-form verb (<i>citare</i>?) in the same summoning series as <i>vocire</i>. The sense (\"summon and cite\") is unaffected; the cleaner working reading <i>fordre</i> is followed and Dreyer is recorded. Dreyer's line 39 likewise reads <i>saa widt forn de wide</i> where the working text reads <i>saa widt som de wide</i> (\"so far as they know\"); the working reading is adopted, the <i>forn/som</i> divergence being an OCR artifact of the long-s.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1591).</b> <i>gandske wenlig(en)</i> = <i>ganske venlig(en)</i> (\"wholly friendly\"); <i>helsen</i> = <i>hilsen</i> (\"greeting\"); <i>beuist gode</i> = <i>bevist gode</i> (\"kindness shown\"); <i>forskylde / forskylle</i> = <i>forskylde</i> (\"requite, deserve in return\"); <i>eskendis worder</i> = \"is called upon / required\"; <i>bestaa oc bekiende</i> = \"affirm and acknowledge\"; <i>lougligen steffnt oc kaldit</i> = <i>lovligen stævnet og kaldt</i> (\"lawfully summoned and cited\"); <i>kaldsbreff</i> = \"writ of citation\"; <i>kaldtsmend</i> = \"summoners\" (here the citation-witnesses); <i>fuldmectige</i> = \"attorney / proxy\"; <i>billigt oc ret</i> = \"fair and right\"; <i>landzens skick oc brug</i> = \"the custom and usage of the land\"; <i>for:ne / forme / forbemelte</i> = <i>fornævnte / forbemeldte</i> (\"aforesaid / aforementioned,\" standard chancery abbreviations); <i>tilgnegt</i> = <i>tilgnegen / tilgiven</i> (\"devoted\").</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> One negation in the body: <i>eller oc icke</i> (\"or not [at all],\" closing the threefold alternative of how Rasmus may respond to the citation — in person, by proxy, or not at all). It is reproduced once; the polite formula <i>icke skal haffue mig der vdoffuer at beklage</i> (\"[that] I shall not have cause to complain of it\") contains a second <i>icke</i>, also reproduced. No conditional in the letter inverts a command; the legal force is a private party's <i>bøn</i> (request) to the university court, not an order.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Vraniburg den 21. junij anno 1591</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, 21 June 1591\" (O.S.; Denmark kept Old Style until 1700). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1591. 21. juni. Uraniborg</i>) and with the Dreyer regest (<i>1591, 21. junii</i>) — <b>no date discrepancy.</b></li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the English translation's place-name: Tycho's tenant Rasmus Persøn is said to reside \"vdi Gunsøgaard\" (Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.56), not at Gundsømagle as previously rendered.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely and the persons are confirmed against the Dreyer regest; the only soft points are minor OCR-level divergences between the working transcription and the Dreyer scan (<i>fordre</i> vs. <i>farcire</i>; <i>som</i> vs. <i>forn</i>), which do not affect the sense and are recorded rather than smoothed.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — persons confirmed vs Dreyer's regest, dateline agrees; the sole residue is declared OCR variants not affecting sense.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.55-58 as page images. The target letter (no. 87, 1591, 21 junii, Tycho to Rector Anders Christensen) runs on pp.56-57; p.55 holds the end of a Rothmann/Wilhelm letter and p.58 the start of an unrelated 1591 court judgment, both correctly excluded from source_text. Collated the full Danish letter word for word against Dreyer's print: all matches exactly (names Christen Jude, Niels Krag, Niels Colding, Christen Skyt, Peder Sverdfeger, Christiern Urup; the date 21 junii 1591) except the one place-name substitution flagged above, which source_text itself gets right (Gunsøgaard) but the English changes.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The letter itself names the tenant's current residence as Gunsøgaard, a farm; the English silently substitutes Gundsømagle, the village named only in Dreyer's separate Latin case-summary for the opposing party Erasmus Petri (letter no. 86, above this one). This conflates two distinct place-names belonging to two different people in the same land dispute. At: “my servant Rasmus Persøn, who is now at Gundsømagle”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.55-58 (this letter, no. 87, on pp.56-57). Page 56 line 34-35 clearly reads \"min tiener Rasmus Perføn, fom nu er vdi Gunføgaard\" — confirming the audit's claim that the source and Dreyer both name Gunsøgaard, and that \"Gundsømagle\" in the prior English was an error. The Latin/Danish current_latin already correctly reads \"Gunsøgaard\" and needed no change; only the English translation was corrected. No other defects found on these pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums Arkiv, [gammel signature: KA 185]. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks the Rector to convene Niels Krag and Niels Colding before colleagues to give sworn attestation before Tycho's bailiff regarding events witnessed on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910621001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910801001", "d": "1591-08-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Padua", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Padua, 1 August 1591 [N.S.] (catalogued 22 January 1591 N.S.)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tabulae Primi Mobilis </i>(cited per source; the letter is in fact printed in Tycho's<i> Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i>, fol. H, and re-edited in A. Favaro,<i> Carteggio inedito di Ticone Brahe … con Giovanni Antonio Magini</i>) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE PERILLUSTRI ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO. D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, ET ARCIS URANIBURG IN INSULA HELLESPONTI DANICI HUAeNA FUNDATORI.</p><p>HIS sane litteris, quas nunc ad te mitto, perillustris ac eruditissime vir, brevitatem sum secutus: et quamvis in Epistola illa, quam cum eo opere, quo te merito, grati animi meaeque erga te observantiae declarandae causa, nuncupavi, communitam dedi, quamque brevi ad te sum transmissurus, pluribus egerim: tamen ne negligentiae nomine me accusares, tibi indicandum duxi, me hactenus a Patavina poli investigatione ac Martis affixorumque syderum meridiana altitudine investiganda, quam tantopere cum GELLIO tuo, vel potius, nostro, observare cupiebas, supersedisse: cum, quod eiusmodi observationibus nec opportunum loci situm, nec commodam ad ea exploranda occasionem, ut ingenue GELLIUS ipse testabitur, invenire potui, tum etiam quod diversorum negotiorum cumulo varijsque molestijs, quas mors carissimae Matris gravissimas auxit, plurimum toto illo aestivo tempore, quo Venetijs et Patavij haereo, sum circumventus. Sextantem interim tuae inventionis, et tui gratia, et meae exercitationis causa, fabrefieri feci, eumque coelo exactissime ad singula minuta, dum unâ cum dicto GELLIO easdem nonnullarum fixarum distantias, quales ex tuis observationibus in Fixarum Catalogo tu ipse antea notasti, deprehendi, respondere comperi. Eundem Bononiam mecum exportabo, ibique Quadrantem maiorem ad altitudines observandas elaborare faciam; et plures tui causa observationes perficere conabor. Radium etiam Astrologicum iustae magnitudinis construi faciam; in cuius oblongi extremitate cylindrus sit apponendus, ubi scilicet in aliorum radijs oculus applicatur: in transversarij vero extremitatibus affigendae erunt duae pinnulae cum suis foraminibus, sicut in tuis Instrumentis facere soles, quae tamen libere circumvolvantur, ut radij per eius rimulas ac iuxta cylindri extremitates transmissi paralleli fiant. Hoc transversarium volo, ut lateraliter tantum moveatur, dividendo ipsum in particulas illas, in quas hasta seu fustis divisa est, pro ratione sinus totius particularum 10000: hoc enim pacto portio transversarij ab hasta secta erit tangens anguli, qui fit ad cylindrum, dum unus observator per pinnulam fixam in hastae extremitate, alius vero per pinnulam in extremitate transversarij sitam, stellas inspiciunt.</p><p>Privilegium tuorum operum, quod anxie petij, ab Illustrissimis Venetis obtineri nequit: id enim responsi a nonnullis nobilibus tuli: Senatum Venetum non solere illorum operum, quae extra Urbem edantur, concedere privilegia; et si aliqua sub Libris alibi excusis sua legantur, ea honoris tantum, non valoris gratia esse concessa, cum Bibliothecae Venetae ex ipsius Senatus gratia id iuris habeant, ut quoscumque libros alibi impressos, non obstantibus quibusvis, nedum ipsius Senatus, privilegijs, Venetijs denuo typis mandare possint. Illud tamen honoris privilegium, ubi opus illud iam typis excusum antea vidissent, ab Illustrissimis Venetis haberi posset; solent enim, quicquid edatur, legere et perscrutari, num scilicet contra Sacrosanctam Ecclesiam, bonos mores, aut Principes Christianos quicquam scriptum sit, priusquam sua illa privilegia impertiantur. Illustrissimi autem Ducis Mantuae et Ferrariae, per familiares et amicos, privilegium facile, uti spero, obtinebo. Expecto adhuc magno cum desiderio Librum tuum primum, quem iam sub proelo esse ex GELLIO intellexi; interim obnixe te rogo, ut si quid in tui gratiam possum, libere mihi committere ne graveris. Vale, perillustris Vir, et Astronomiae Princeps, quam restaurare, ut hucusque felicissime a te factum, pro communi commodo nunquam intermitte.</p><p>Iterum diu feliciterque vale.<br>Datae Patavij, Kalendis Augusti 1591.<br>Perillustri Dominationi tuae addictissimus<br>IOAN. ANTONIUS MAGINUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious in nobility and most outstanding in erudition, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup, and founder of the castle of Uraniborg on the island Hven of the Danish Hellespont.</b></p><p>In this present letter that I now send you, most illustrious and most learned man, I have aimed at brevity; and although in that letter which I joined to the work I dedicated to you — deservedly, to declare my gratitude and my regard for you — and which I shall shortly send on to you, I treated of more matters, yet, lest you charge me with negligence, I have thought I should inform you that hitherto I have held off from the Paduan investigation of the pole and from investigating the meridian altitude of Mars and of the fixed stars — which you so greatly desired me to observe together with your Gellius, or rather, ours. For both because I could find, for observations of that kind, neither a suitable situation of place nor a convenient opportunity for examining them — as Gellius himself will frankly testify — and also because I have been very greatly hemmed in, throughout all that summer season during which I am stuck at Venice and Padua, by a heap of various affairs and by sundry troubles, which the death of my most dear mother has gravely increased. Meanwhile I have had a sextant of your invention made — both for your sake and for my own practice — and I have found that it answers most exactly to the heavens, to the very minute, since together with the said Gellius I detected the same distances of certain fixed stars as those which you yourself had earlier noted from your observations in the Catalogue of Fixed Stars. I shall carry it with me to Bologna, and there I shall have a larger quadrant fashioned for observing altitudes; and I shall try, for your sake, to carry through more observations. I shall also have a cross-staff (radius astronomicus) of proper size constructed: at the end of its long [shaft] a cylinder is to be fitted, where, that is, in other cross-staffs the eye is applied; and at the ends of the cross-piece two sights (pinnules) with their holes are to be affixed, as you are wont to do on your instruments — sights which, however, turn freely round, so that the rays sent through their slits and along the ends of the cylinder become parallel. This cross-piece I wish to move only sideways, dividing it into those same parts into which the shaft or rod is divided, in proportion to the whole sine of 10,000 parts: for in this way the portion of the cross-piece cut off by the shaft will be the tangent of the angle that is formed at the cylinder, while one observer sights the stars through the fixed sight at the end of the shaft, and the other through the sight set at the end of the cross-piece.</p><p>The privilege [copyright] for your works, which I anxiously sought, cannot be obtained from the Most Illustrious Venetians; for this is the answer I had from certain nobles: that the Venetian Senate is not accustomed to grant privileges for those works that are published outside the City; and that if any of its own [privileges] are to be read in books printed elsewhere, these have been granted for the sake of honour only, not of legal force, since the Venetian libraries have, by the grace of the Senate itself, the right to put back into print at Venice whatsoever books printed elsewhere, notwithstanding any privileges whatever — not even the Senate's own. That privilege of honour, however, could be had from the Most Illustrious Venetians once they had first seen the work already printed; for they are accustomed to read and scrutinise whatever is published — to see, that is, whether anything has been written against the Most Holy Church, good morals, or Christian princes — before they bestow those privileges of theirs. But from the Most Illustrious Duke of Mantua and Ferrara I shall easily obtain a privilege, as I hope, through members of his household and friends. I still await with great longing your First Book, which I have understood from Gellius to be already under the press; meanwhile I earnestly beg you not to be reluctant to entrust to me freely whatever I can do for your sake. Farewell, most illustrious Man and Prince of Astronomy — which, as you have hitherto most happily done, never cease to restore for the common good.</p><p>Again, fare you well long and happily.<br>Given at Padua, on the Kalends of August [1 August] 1591.<br>Most devoted to Your most illustrious Lordship,<br>Giovanni Antonio Magini.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioan. Antonius Maginus</i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), from 1588 Professor of Mathematics at Bologna (having won the chair over Galileo); astronomer, cartographer, and table-maker, and one of Tycho's principal Italian correspondents. His <i>Tabulae Primi Mobilis</i> (Venice, 1604) and <i>Carteggio</i> (ed. A. Favaro) carry this exchange.</li><li><b>Address.</b> <i>Dominus de Knudstrup</i> — <b>Lord of Knudstrup</b>, Tycho's Scanian ancestral seat. <i>Arcis Uraniburg in insula Hellesponti Danici Huaena fundatori</i> — \"founder of the castle of Uraniborg on the island Hven of the Danish Hellespont\"; <i>Hellespontus Danicus</i> (\"the Danish Hellespont\") is the humanist epithet for the Øresund, the strait in which <b>Hven</b> lies.</li><li><b><i>Gellius</b></i> = <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (1562–1612), Tycho's Danish pupil and former Uraniborg assistant, then studying medicine at Padua (matriculated October 1589). He described Tycho's instruments to Magini and supplied star-distances from Tycho's catalogue; he is the channel for much of this letter's technical content. Magini's <i>\"GELLIO tuo, vel potius, nostro\"</i> (\"your Gellius, or rather, ours\") is a courtesy claiming a share in him.</li><li><b><i>Epistola … quam cum eo opere … nuncupavi</b></i> — the dedicatory letter Magini attached to a work he dedicated to Tycho. The dedication-context (a work \"I dedicated to you\") and the <i>Liber primus</i> awaited below place this in the run-up to Magini's exchanges around his <i>Novae coelestium orbium theoricae</i> (1589) and the later tables.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Sextans tuae inventionis</i> → <b>sextant of your invention</b> (one of Tycho's signature instruments; §5.2). <i>Quadrans maior</i> → <b>larger quadrant</b>. <i>Radius Astrologicus</i> (= <i>radius astronomicus</i>) → <b>cross-staff (radius astronomicus)</b> per §5.2; Magini writes <i>Astrologicus</i> but the instrument is the astronomical cross-staff, so the controlled rendering is used and the variant noted here. <i>Pinnula</i> → <b>sight (pinnule)</b>; <i>transversarium</i> → <b>cross-piece</b> (the sliding transversal); <i>hasta seu fustis</i> → <b>shaft or rod</b> (the graduated main staff). <i>Fixarum Catalogo</i> → Tycho's <b>Catalogue of Fixed Stars</b> (the star catalogue then in progress, ultimately the 1,000-star catalogue).</li><li><b>The cross-staff geometry.</b> Magini describes a tangent-graduated cross-staff: a <i>cylindrus</i> (cylinder) at the eye-end of the shaft replaces the usual eyepiece; two freely-rotating pinnules at the ends of the transversal align their slits so the rays \"become parallel.\" The transversal is graduated <i>\"pro ratione sinus totius particularum 10000\"</i> — <b>in proportion to the whole sine [radius] of 10,000 parts</b>, i.e. the <i>sinus totus</i> (the radius of the trigonometric circle) is set at 10,000, the standard table-value; so the segment of the transversal \"cut off by the shaft\" reads directly as the <b>tangent of the observed angle</b> at the cylinder. The construction (a tangent scale read by two observers) is rendered literally; no step is supplied beyond the Latin.</li><li><b>The Venetian <i>privilegium</i> passage.</b> <i>Privilegium</i> → <b>privilege (copyright)</b>: the printing-monopoly grant. The reported answer has three linked clauses, each preserved with its negation: (1) the Senate <i>non solet</i> — <b>is not accustomed</b> — to grant privileges for works published outside Venice; (2) any Venetian privileges appearing in books printed elsewhere are <i>\"honoris tantum, non valoris gratia\"</i> — <b>for honour only, not for legal force</b>; (3) Venetian libraries may reprint any foreign-printed book at Venice <i>\"non obstantibus quibusvis, nedum ipsius Senatus, privilegijs\"</i> — <b>notwithstanding any privileges whatever, not even the Senate's own</b> (<i>nedum</i> = \"let alone / not even,\" intensifying, not excepting). Four explicit negations in the source (<i>nequit, non solere, non valoris, non obstantibus … nedum</i>) are reproduced; none added or dropped. The \"privilege of honour\" is obtainable only <i>after</i> the work is printed and has passed Venetian censorship (against <i>Sacrosancta Ecclesia, boni mores, Principes Christiani</i>).</li><li><b><i>Librum tuum primum … sub proelo</b></i> → <b>\"your First Book … under the press\"</b>: Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), then in preparation/printing — Magini awaits it.</li><li><b><i>Astronomiae Princeps … restaurare</b></i> → <b>\"Prince of Astronomy … to restore\"</b>: a salute to Tycho's <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> programme (§5.1, <i>restoration/renewal of astronomy</i>); \"never cease to restore [it] for the common good.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux (named).</b> The dateline reads <i>\"Datae Patavij, Kalendis Augusti 1591\"</i> = <b>Padua, the Kalends of August 1591 = 1 August 1591</b>. Magini, in Catholic Italy, writes <b>New Style</b>. The dataset `label` (\"1591. 22. januar n.s. Padua\") flatly contradicts the source's own dateline by more than six months; per protocol §3/§4 the header leads with the as-written date (1 August 1591) and appends the catalogue date, and the conversion is <b>not</b> silently performed. The \"22 January\" label is almost certainly a catalogue/data error (a 22-Jan letter of the Magini exchange may have been conflated): the body's internal markers — the <i>summer season</i> (\"toto illo aestivo tempore\") at Venice/Padua, the still-recent death of his mother, and Tycho's <i>Liber primus</i> only now reported \"under the press\" — are consistent with <b>August</b>, not January. The discrepancy is recorded, not reconciled.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The source `edition` field names <i>Tabulae Primi Mobilis</i> (Venice, 1604), where Magini later quoted the Tycho correspondence; the letter's primary printed witness is, however, Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (fol. H), whence Favaro re-edited it in the <i>Carteggio</i>. No manuscript witness is recorded in the source.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical cross-staff and <i>privilegium</i> passages are translated literally with all negations preserved. The one substantive issue is the <b>catalogue-date crux</b> (label \"22 January\" vs. dateline \"1 August 1591\"), flagged and left unreconciled per protocol; a minor lexical point is Magini's <i>radius Astrologicus</i> for the astronomical <i>radius astronomicus</i> (noted, controlled rendering kept). Not graded [C] because the crux is a dataset-metadata conflict, not an ambiguity in the source text, whose dateline is unambiguous.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.302-305 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"); the letter runs p.303 ('NOBILITATE PERILLUSTRI...') through p.304 ('...IOAN. ANTONIUS MAGINUS'), with p.302 the tail of the preceding Tycho-to-Gellius letter (dated 8 Dec 1590) and p.305 the head of Hagecius's next letter. Dreyer's Latin matches source_text verbatim. Collated every technical/numeric detail of the cross-staff description (division into 10,000 parts of the whole sine), the sextant-agreement claim against the Fixed Star Catalogue, the Venetian privilege passage (grounds for refusal, honour-only privileges, the Mantua/Ferrara alternative), the reason for the delay ('mors carissimae Matris' correctly kept as 'my most dear mother', not flipped), and the closing date 'Kalendis Augusti 1591'. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15901201003", "next": "brahe-t_15920201001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15901201003", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15981128001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that the requested Padua observations of the pole and stellar meridian altitudes remain unconducted due to unsuitable conditions, and announces a dedicated work shortly to be sent.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910801001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910829001", "d": "1591-08-29", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 29 August 1591 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>(= Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.58</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISS: VIRO, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTI D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO IN KNUDSTORPFF ET URANIEBURGO ETC DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO. S.</p><p>NOBILISS. D. TYCHO et amice observande. Quod tam diu ad tuas binas non responderim, multa fuerunt quae me impediverunt: tum valetudo adversa, quae me per integram propemodum Quadragesimam afflixit, tum etiam curae domesticae ac crebrae profectiones medicinae faciendae gratia. Huc accesserunt alia plurima, quae saepe praeter spem incidere consueverunt. Etiamsi vero aliquod otium habuissem, non tamen aliquid nostrae illi disputationi adijcere potuissem, vel nunc quoque possem; adeo exacte omnia abs te excussa, examinata, meaque argumenta diluta sunt, ut me veritate et evidentia demonstrationum victum esse fateri necesse sit, nisi manifestae veritati repugnare velim. Exempla aliquot tuae effigiei accepi, quae inter amicos distribui. D. CURTIUS noster quoque unam accepit, misitque in Bavariam ad quendam sibi bene notum et celebrem Poëtam, qui arguto aliquo epigrammate eam ornaret. Sed nondum accepit responsum. Hîc Pragae hoc tempore nullum eiusmodi Poëtam, quod sciam, habemus, qui meo desiderio satisfacere, et quomodo pictor faciem et lineamenta corporis, sic ille mentem et vim eius depingere posset. NICOLAUS RAYMARUS, quem tu plagij accusaveras, ante septimanas aliquot huc venit et innotuit D. CURTIO qui illum Caesari ita commendavit, ut pro Mathematico, tenui satis, in his initijs, stipendiolo sit receptus. Hodie hinc Francofurdum pro advehenda sua suppellectili discedit, mox huc rediturus. Aliud quod scribam nunc nec habeo, nec vacat.</p><p>B. V.<br>Datae Pragae die 29 Augusti novo stylo Anno 1591.<br>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 305; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POST SCRIPTA. HERI venit ad me quidam artifex, eius nomen ignoro, vir aetate grandeva, et narravit se vixisse aliquandiu tecum, saepeque multa de me audivisse: cum igitur venisset in hanc urbem, salutandi et alloquendi gratia me accessisse. Mira quaedam et Geometrica profitetur, potissimum currum quendam admirabilem, cuius rotae emensa milliaria sonitu quodam indicent, et nescio quae alia; nam plenius cum illo colloqui non licuit et aliorum interventu abruptum fuit nostrum colloquium. Electori Saxoniae, Principi Pomeraniae et quibusdam alijs eiusmodi currum se confecisse aiebat. Intellexi Caesari quoque sua servitia offerre velle.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, outstanding in virtue and erudition, Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg etc., his lord and friend to be honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Tycho and friend to be honoured. That I have been so long in replying to your two letters, there were many things that hindered me: both ill health, which afflicted me through almost the whole of Lent, and also domestic cares and frequent journeys for the sake of practising medicine. To these were added very many other things, such as often tend to befall beyond expectation. But even had I had some leisure, I could not on that account have added anything to that disputation of ours, nor can I even now: so exactly has everything been sifted and examined by you, and my arguments dissolved, that I must needs confess myself vanquished by the truth and the evidence of the demonstrations, unless I were willing to fight against manifest truth. I have received several copies of your portrait, which I have distributed among friends. Our Master Curtius too received one, and sent it into Bavaria to a certain poet well known to him and celebrated, that he might adorn it with some witty epigram. But he has not yet received a reply. Here at Prague at this time we have no poet of that kind, so far as I know, who could satisfy my desire and could depict — as a painter [depicts] the face and the lineaments of the body, so he — his mind and his power. Nicolaus Raymarus, whom you had accused of plagiarism, came here some weeks ago and became known to Master Curtius, who so commended him to the Emperor that he has been taken on as Mathematician, on a little stipend slight enough, in these beginnings. Today he departs hence to Frankfurt to fetch his household goods, intending soon to return hither. Anything else to write I now neither have, nor is there leisure.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>Given at Prague, the 29th day of August, New Style, in the year 1591.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[daeus].</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. Yesterday there came to me a certain craftsman — his name I do not know — a man advanced in age, and he related that he had lived with you for some time, and had often heard much about me; and so, when he had come into this city, he had approached me for the sake of greeting and conversing. He professes certain wondrous and Geometrical things, above all a certain marvellous cart, whose wheels indicate by a kind of sound the miles measured out, and I know not what else; for it was not granted to converse with him more fully, and our conversation was broken off by the intervention of others. He said he had made a cart of this kind for the Elector of Saxony, for the Prince of Pomerania, and for certain others of the like. I understood that he wishes to offer his services to the Emperor too.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>THAD. / Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek)</b> (1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer and personal physician to Emperor Rudolf II at Prague; an early and central observer of the 1572 nova, a long-standing correspondent of Tycho and the principal conduit through which Tycho's later relations with the Imperial court ran. The subscription <i>THAD.</i> abbreviates his Christian name.</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO IN KNUDSTORPFF ET URANIEBURGO</i> — \"Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg\": <i>Knudstorpff</i> = <b>Knudstrup</b> (the Brahe family seat in Scania), <i>Uranieburgo</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven.</li><li><b><i>tuas binas</b></i> — \"your two letters\" (<i>binas</i> sc. <i>literas</i>); rendered \"your two letters.\"</li><li><b><i>Quadragesimam</b></i> — <b>Lent</b> (the forty-day fast); here a time-marker for the duration of Hagecius's illness, kept as the liturgical season.</li><li><b><i>nostrae illi disputationi … meaque argumenta diluta sunt … victum esse fateri</b></i> — \"that disputation of ours … my arguments dissolved … to confess myself vanquished.\" This refers to the running <b>cosmological controversy</b> between the two over Tycho's geo-heliocentric world-system; Hagecius here concedes the point. <i>diluta</i> (\"watered down, dissolved, refuted\") is rendered \"dissolved\"; the concession is preserved at its full force without sharpening Tycho's claim beyond what Hagecius grants (\"vanquished by the truth and the evidence of the demonstrations\").</li><li><b><i>Exempla aliquot tuae effigiei</b></i> — \"several copies of your portrait.\" The reference is to printed/engraved likenesses of Tycho being circulated among the learned; <i>effigies</i> = likeness/portrait.</li><li><b><i>D. CURTIUS noster</b></i> — <b>Jacobus Curtius (Jakob Kurz von Senftenau)</b> (d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor at Rudolf II's court and himself an amateur astronomer in correspondence with Tycho; <i>noster</i> (\"our [Curtius]\") marks the shared acquaintance.</li><li><b><i>in Bavariam ad quendam … celebrem Poëtam … arguto … epigrammate</b></i> — Curtius sent a portrait into <b>Bavaria</b> to a celebrated poet to furnish a \"witty epigram\" (<i>argutum epigramma</i>) — a learned verse-eulogy to accompany the likeness. The poet is not named.</li><li><b><i>quomodo pictor faciem … sic ille mentem et vim eius depingere posset</b></i> — \"as a painter [depicts] the face … so he [could depict] his mind and power\": the painter–poet topos (the painter renders the body, the poet the soul). The elliptical Latin (the verb <i>depingit</i> is supplied from <i>depingere</i>) is rendered with a bracket-free but explicit parallel; no editorial word is added beyond the supplied verb signalled by \"[depicts]\".</li><li><b><i>NICOLAUS RAYMARUS, quem tu plagij accusaveras</b></i> — <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär</b>, \"Reimarus Ursus\" (c. 1551–1600), the Dithmarschen mathematician whom Tycho had <b>accused of plagiarism</b> (<i>plagium</i>) for publishing in his <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i> (1588) a geo-heliocentric world-system Tycho held to be stolen from Hven — the origin of the bitter Tycho–Ursus priority dispute. Hagecius reports, neutrally, Ursus's arrival at Prague and his appointment. <i>plagium accusare</i> = \"to accuse of plagiarism\"; kept as the period term.</li><li><b><i>innotuit D. CURTIO qui illum Caesari ita commendavit, ut pro Mathematico … stipendio sit receptus</b></i> — Ursus \"became known to Master Curtius, who so commended him to the Emperor [<i>Caesar</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>] that he was taken on as Mathematician [i.e. <b>Imperial Mathematician</b>], on a stipend slight enough, in these beginnings.\" <i>tenui satis … in his initijs</i> (\"slight enough … in these beginnings\") is Hagecius's own hedge on the smallness of the initial salary; preserved, not smoothed.</li><li><b><i>Francofurdum pro advehenda sua suppellectili</b></i> — \"to <b>Frankfurt</b> to fetch his household goods\" (<i>suppellex</i> = furniture/household stuff); <i>advehenda</i> future-passive (gerundive of purpose), \"for the bringing-up of.\"</li><li><b><i>B. V.</b></i> — <i>Bene vale</i>, \"<b>Fare you well</b>\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>T. studiosiss.</b></i> — <i>Tui studiosissimus</i>, \"<b>Most devoted to you</b>\" (subscription formula).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline reads <i>die 29 Augusti <b>novo stylo</b> Anno 1591</i> — <b>29 August 1591, New Style (Gregorian)</b>, explicitly so marked by the writer; the catalogue label likewise gives \"n.s.\" Hagecius, in Catholic Imperial Prague, dates New Style; the corresponding Old Style date (which Tycho on Hven kept) would be 19 August 1591, but the letter is <b>not</b> silently converted: the header carries the as-written N.S. date per protocol §3, and the 10-day gap is noted here only.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (a visiting artisan's mileage-measuring odometer-cart), restored from Dreyer VII p.305 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Three small corrections against Dreyer (TBOO VII pp.303-306): the postscript's \"aliquando\" restored to \"aliquandiu\" (a stay of some duration, not a single occasion); \"stipendio\" restored to the diminutive \"stipendiolo\"; and the closing dateline's inserted \"Hîc\" removed (Dreyer reads simply \"Datæ Pragæ\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure. Latin, persons, and the New-Style dateline are all firm; the only ellipsis (the supplied verb in the painter–poet sentence) is signalled in the translation and explained above, and entails no genuine crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.303-306 as page images (the Hagecius letter runs from '[E codice Vindobonensi lat. 10686 58]' on p.304 through the postscript on p.305; pp.303 and 306 belong to the flanking Maginus letters and were read only to confirm the boundaries). Collated the whole body - the Lenten-illness excuse, the portrait-distribution and Curtius/Bavarian-poet passage, the Nicolaus Raymarus/plagiarism paragraph, and the full postscript about the unnamed elderly craftsman and his sounding-wheel cart offered to the Elector of Saxony, the Prince of Pomerania, and the Emperor - against the printed page, word by word. Found three genuine divergences between source_text and Dreyer's print (listed above); the 'aliquando/aliquandiu' one is substantive enough to change a scholar's read of the craftsman's prior relationship to Tycho, so the letter is graded C and verdict SERIOUS despite the translation itself being a faithful rendering of the (corrupted) source it was given.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer prints the durative adverb 'aliquandiu' (for some time) but our source_text has 'aliquando' (once, at some point) - a dropped syllable that changes a duration of residence into a single occasion, a fact about the unnamed craftsman's relationship to Tycho that a scholar would take away differently. Dreyer prints: <i>et narravit se vixisse aliquandiu tecum, saepeque multa de me audivisse (TBOO VII p.305, postscript, l.2)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints the diminutive 'stipendiolo' (a paltry little stipend); source_text drops the diminutive ending to plain 'stipendio'. Minor, since 'tenui satis' already conveys smallness, but a genuine divergence from the printed text. Dreyer prints: <i>ut pro Mathematico, tenui satis, in his initijs, stipendiolo sit receptus (TBOO VII p.305, l.10)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text inserts 'Hîc' (here) into the closing dateline; Dreyer's closing formula reads simply 'Datae Pragae' without it. Low-impact (the place is already named), but the English 'here at' is an artifact of the corrupted source, not of Dreyer's text. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Pragae die 29 Augusti novo stylo Anno 1591 (TBOO VII p.305, l.13-14)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.303-306. Page 305 confirms all three TEXT_ERRORs: line 10 reads \"ftipendiolo\"; lines 13-14 read \"Datæ Pragæ\" with no \"Hîc\"; and the postscript (headed POST SCRIPTA on the same page) reads \"vixiße aliquandiu tecum\". All three fixed in the Latin, with light corresponding adjustments to the English. No other defects found across the four pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.58."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 26, "prev": "brahe-t_15910000002", "next": "brahe-t_15920322001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910000002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920322001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Concedes complete defeat in the astronomical disputation with Tycho, reports distributing copies of Tycho's portrait among friends including Curtius who forwarded one to Bavaria.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910829001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910830001", "d": "1591-08-30", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 30 August 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XVIII, 82 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYGE BRAHE TILL KNUDSTRUP FICK BREFF FORBERØRTE HELLIGE THRE KONGER CAPELLES BYGNING ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR.</p><p>Christian.</p><p>WOR gunst thilfornn. Viid, epter att Wij komme wdi forfaringe, huorledis Hellige [thre] konger capelle wed Roschilde domkircke schall møgit werre bygfeldig och forfalden, att thend klart will nederfalde och wdj grunden forderffuis, saa frampt ther till met thet første icke schaffuis raad och kommis till hielp, och epterdi ditt forleeningsbreff paa for:ne capelle eblant andit nochsom thilholder dig at schulle holde for:ne capelle med residentzen och andit guodtz, ther tilliggendis er, wed guoed heffd och macht, tha bede Wij dig och wille, attu met thet forderligste tiltencker at schaffue raad och middell, att samme capelle wißeligen inden winteren paakommer igien kand bliffue forferdiget och forbedrit epter ditt forleeningsbreffs liudelße och indtholdt. Saaframpt for:ne capelle paa sin heffd, grund och bygning for din forsømmelße schyldt lider ydermere nogen bryst, schade och forderff, eller och anden wbeleiglighet ther aff kunde indtfalde, Wij tha thet icke schulle wiide huoß dig, och dertill wdi saa maade tiltencke andre raad och middell, wed huilcke for:ne capell kand holdis wed sin thilbørlig heffd och magt. Ther med scheer Wor aluorlig wilge och befalning. Thj thag her aldelis ingen forsømmelße fore.</p><p>Befalendis dig Gud.<br>Haffniae 30. augusti a:o 91.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup received a letter concerning the building [maintenance] of the aforesaid Chapel of the Three Holy Kings, as follows:</b></p><p>Christian.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, after we came into knowledge how the Chapel of the Three [Holy] Kings at Roskilde Cathedral is said to be much dilapidated and decayed, so that it will plainly fall down and be ruined to its foundations, if remedy be not provided for it and help brought to it at the very first; and since your letter of enfeoffment upon the aforesaid chapel, among other things, sufficiently enjoins you that you are to keep the aforesaid chapel, together with the residence and other property belonging to it, in good upkeep and condition: therefore we ask you, and will [it], that you with all dispatch take thought to provide ways and means, so that the same chapel may surely, before winter come on, be put in good repair again and amended according to the tenor and content of your letter of enfeoffment. If the aforesaid chapel, in its upkeep, foundation, and fabric, should through fault of your negligence suffer any further defect, harm, and ruin, or else any other inconvenience could fall out therefrom, then we shall not let that rest with you, and shall thereupon, in such case, take thought for other ways and means, whereby the aforesaid chapel may be kept in its due upkeep and condition. Herewith is done our earnest will and command. Therefore undertake herein absolutely no negligence.</p><p>Commending you to God.<br>At Copenhagen, 30 August, year [15]91.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (1577–1648; r. from his father Frederik II's death in 1588). In 1591 the fourteen-year-old king was still a minor under the regency council (Frederik II had died 4 April 1588; Christian was not crowned until 1596), so this chancery missive issues in his name but under the regency's administration. The single-word subscription <i>\"Christian.\"</i> is the king's chancery superscription, not an autograph.<br>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here styled <i>Tyge Brahe till Knudstrup</i> — \"Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup,\" his patrimonial seat in Scania, the form used for him as a nobleman and crown fief-holder rather than as the astronomer of Hven.<br>- <b>Subject — the chapel and the fief.</b> The <b>Chapel of the Three Holy Kings</b> (<i>Hellige Tre Konger Kapel</i>, the Chapel of the Magi) is the burial chapel attached to <b>Roskilde Cathedral</b>, built under King Christian I (consecrated by 1463) as a royal mausoleum. Its associated prebend/canonry was a crown benefice; Tycho held it (with revenues attached) under a <i>forleningsbrev</i> (\"letter of enfeoffment / letter of grant\"), which carried the upkeep of the chapel and its residence as a charge upon the holder. The letter is a formal admonition that he is failing that obligation.<br>- <b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (Sjællandske Tegnelser). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…HELLIGE THRE KONGER CAPELLES BYGNING ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning the building [maintenance] of the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2. <i>bygning</i> here denotes the upkeep/fabric-maintenance of the building, not new construction; rendered \"building [maintenance].\"<br>- <b>Negations and legal force (counted; none added or dropped).</b> The source carries exactly three negations, each translated and none introduced:<br>  1. <i>…saa frampt ther till met thet første icke schaffuis raad och kommis till hielp</i> — \"if remedy be <b>not</b> provided for it and help brought to it at the very first\": the conditional protasis describing what causes the ruin. Rendered as a negative conditional, mood preserved (subjunctive/passive <i>schaffuis, kommis</i> = \"be provided / be brought\").<br>  2. <i>Wij tha thet icke schulle wiide huoß dig</i> — \"then we shall <b>not</b> let that rest with you.\" The chancery idiom <i>vide hos (en)</i> = \"to leave / let rest with (someone)\"; the negation makes this a threat, not a reassurance: should the chapel decay further through Tycho's negligence, the king will <i>not</i> leave the matter in Tycho's hands but will himself \"take thought for other ways and means\" — i.e. transfer the charge (and by implication the fief). The smoother but wrong reading (\"we shall not know it from you\") is rejected; the legal sense is removal of trust/responsibility.<br>  3. <i>Thj thag her aldelis ingen forsømmelße fore</i> — \"Therefore undertake herein <b>absolutely no</b> negligence.\" <i>aldelis ingen</i> = \"absolutely no\" (intensifier + negative), the closing imperative. <i>tage … fore</i> = \"to undertake / set about.\" Kept as a single emphatic negative command.<br>- <b>Conditional structure.</b> The threat-clause <i>\"Saaframpt for:ne capelle … lider ydermere nogen bryst, schade och forderff, eller och anden wbeleiglighet …, Wij tha thet icke schulle wiide huoß dig, och dertill … tiltencke andre raad och middell\"</i> is one conditional period: protasis \"if the chapel suffers further defect through your negligence,\" apodosis \"then [we] shall not leave it with you, and shall take thought for other means.\" <i>schulle … tiltencke</i> are modal/future (\"shall take thought\"); the future-conditional force is preserved without sharpening into a flat present.<br>- <b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1591).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Viid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"Know\"); <i>komme wdi forfaringe</i> = <i>kom i forfaring</i> (\"came into knowledge / learned\"); <i>bygfeldig</i> = \"dilapidated, in need of repair\" (lit. \"fall-prone in fabric\"); <i>forfalden</i> = \"decayed\"; <i>klart will nederfalde</i> = \"will plainly fall down\"; <i>wdj grunden forderffuis</i> = \"be ruined to its foundations\"; <i>forleeningsbreff</i> = <i>forleningsbrev</i> (\"letter of enfeoffment / letter of grant\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>eblant andit</i> = <i>iblandt andet</i> (\"among other things\"); <i>nochsom thilholder</i> = \"sufficiently enjoins / obliges\"; <i>residentzen</i> = \"the residence\"; <i>heffd och macht/magt</i> = \"upkeep and condition (legal possession and standing)\"; <i>forderligste</i> = \"most expeditious / with all dispatch\"; <i>tiltencker / tiltencke</i> = \"take thought, devise\"; <i>raad och middell</i> = \"ways and means\"; <i>wißeligen</i> = \"surely, assuredly\"; <i>inden winteren paakommer</i> = \"before winter comes on\"; <i>forferdiget</i> = \"completed/repaired\"; <i>forbedrit</i> = \"amended/bettered\"; <i>liudelße och indtholdt</i> = \"tenor and content\"; <i>bryst</i> = \"defect, lack\" (<i>brøst</i>); <i>wbeleiglighet</i> = <i>ubelejlighed</i> (\"inconvenience\"); <i>indtfalde</i> = \"fall out, arise\"; <i>thilbørlig</i> = \"due, proper\"; <i>aluorlig wilge och befalning</i> = \"earnest will and command\"; <i>aldelis</i> = \"altogether, absolutely.\"<br>- <b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Haffniae 30. augusti a:o 91</i> = \"At <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), 30 August, year [15]91\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline date agrees with the dataset label (30 August 1591); no in-letter date discrepancy. The dateline place-name is Latin (<i>Haffniae</i>) while the body is Danish — a code-switch preserved. <i>Befalendis dig Gud</i> (\"Commending you to God\") is the closing formula in place of <i>Actum</i>.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and all three negations are reproduced with their legal force (the threat-idiom <i>icke … wiide huoß dig</i> in particular is read as removal of the charge, not as a reassurance). The one soft point is the precise administrative content behind <i>vide hos dig</i> — whether the king's \"other ways and means\" implies forfeiture of the fief specifically or merely intervention by other officers; the text states the threat without specifying the mechanism, so it is rendered literally and the implication flagged rather than asserted.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Danish secure, all negations preserved; the only soft point is an extra-textual administrative implication, correctly unasserted.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (entry 89) against Dreyer TBOO XIV p.60, reading the page image; the Danish text matches Dreyer word for word (the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings at Roskilde, the letter of enfeoffment, the deadline before winter, the date Haffniae 30 augusti a:o 91), and the translation renders every clause faithfully. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XVIII, 82 f. ."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15890925001", "next": "brahe-t_15921117001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Orders Tycho to repair the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings at Roskilde Cathedral before winter, invoking obligations in his letter of enfeoffment.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910830001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910831001", "d": "1591-08-31", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 31 August 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin code-switches) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN/ TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ ETC.</p><p>VNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuvor/ Vester lieber Besonder/ Wir mögen euch gnediglich nicht verhalten/ daß wir in vnserm Thiergarten ein Elendsthier haben/ daß sich dan diesen Winter vnd Sommer vber wol gehalten/ vnd fein zugenommen hat/ jm auch sonst nichts mangelt/ alß daß es keinen Gehülffen hat. Weyl wir dan auß ewerm jüngsten Schreiben sub dato den 2. Aprilis verstanden/ daß jr auch ein Elendthier hettet/ vnd vns dasselbige gerne zustehen lassen woltet/ So nemen wir dasselbige von euch zu gnedigen danck an/ vnd haben demnach nicht vnderlassen wollen/ gegenwertigen Botten zu euch abzufertigen/ vnd jm zu befehlen/ daß er bemelt Elendt bey euch abholen/ vnd vns zufüren soli. Woferne jr vns nu dasselbige/ ewerm Zuschreiben nach/ folgen lassen wollet/ wie wir vns gleichwol zu euch versehen thun/ so geschicht vns daran zu angenemen gefallen/ vnd wollen wir es hiernechst mit einem hübschen Zapffenburger gegen euch erwidern/ vnd sonst in Gnaden eingedenck seyn.</p><p>Wir zweiffeln auch nicht/ jr werdet in dem Deutschen Exemplari Gesneri de Animalibus Quadrupedibus gelesen haben/ was er fol. 85. von einem Thier/ so Rix, oder wie etliche wollen/ Orix genandt wird/ schreiben thut/ Möchten gerne wissen/ ob jr davon etwas in Erfarung bracht/ oder auch mit Leuten geredt/ die es gesehen/ oder berichten können/ was es vor ein gelegenheit darumb habe/ vbi inveniatur, et si tansae proceritatis,quantae a Gesnero pingitur, siet.</p><p>Vnser Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS ist bey vns noch nicht widerumb ankommen/ vnd ob wir jn wol zu vns anhero erfordert/ so hat er sich doch seiner Schwacheit halben (darumb wir gleichwol keine eigentliche gelegenheit wissen) entschüldigen lassen.</p><p>Die begerte Papiermacher haben wir bißhero vber angewendten fleiß nicht bekommen können/ Wir haben aber vnsern Dienern/ so wir jetzt angehende Franckfurder Meß abgefertigt/ befolen/ daß sie sich daselbst fleissig vmbhören sollen/ ob sie dieselbigen zuwegen bringen mögen/ Was nu dieselbigen außrichten/ davon solt jr hiernechst berichtet werden.</p><p>Was wir dißmal von Zeitungen bey handen gehabt/ davon thun wir euch hierneben Abschrifft zufertigen/ vnd werdet jr darauß vernemen/ wie es in Franckreich vnd andern orten jetziger zeit stehet/ vnd daß es miserrimus Status totius Europae ist. Was jhr dan de Statu Daniae vnd sonst newes habt/ begeren wir gnediglich/ vns solchs hinwider zu communiciren.</p><p>Vnd bleiben euch mit gnedigem guten Willen wolgewogen.<br>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel/ am 31. Augusti, Anno 1591.<br>WILHELM LANDSGRAFF ZU HESSEN</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, p. 241; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. WAN auch ewere Obseruationes Stellarum Fixarum &amp; Erraticarum fertig, mochten wir dieselbige wol haben, mit den vnsern zu rectificiren. Mit dem Orix wollet fleissig nachfragen, ob vnd an was orten man es finde, vnd wie es allenthalben qualificiret. Dan Brieffszeiger ewer Vetter Ascanius Bilde, wil dero im Thiergarten zu Coppenhagen vnd zu Croneburg oder Friderichsburg effictae gesehen haben, Aber bey weitem deserviret er es nicht so groß alß Gesnerus, dan 23. Span ist ein grosse hohe von einem Thier, doch ists dem Creatori nicht vnmoglich.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Our trusty and well-beloved: we would not graciously withhold from you that we have in Our deer-park an elk, which has indeed borne up well through this winter and summer and has put on good condition, and which lacks nothing else save that it has no companion. Since, then, We understood from your most recent letter, dated 2 April, that you too had an elk, and were willing to let Us have it, We accept it from you with gracious thanks; and We have accordingly not wished to refrain from dispatching the present messenger to you, and from charging him to fetch the said elk from you and bring it to Us. If, then, you will now let Us have the same, according to your written undertaking — as indeed We do expect of you — that will be done to Our agreeable pleasure, and We will requite it to you hereafter with a fine [cask of] Zapfenburger, and otherwise remain graciously mindful [of you].</p><p>We do not doubt, moreover, that you will have read in the German copy of Gesner <i>On the Four-Footed Animals</i> what he writes at folio 85 of an animal that is called <i>Rix</i>, or, as some will have it, <i>Orix</i>. We should gladly know whether you have learned anything about it, or have spoken with people who have seen it or can give report of it — what the circumstances of it are, where it is found, and whether it is of as great a stature as it is depicted by Gesner.</p><p>Our mathematician Christoph Rothmann has not yet come back to Us; and although We did indeed summon him hither to Us, he has nonetheless excused himself on the ground of his ill health (the precise occasion of which We do not, however, rightly know).</p><p>The paper-makers you desired We have hitherto, for all the diligence applied, not been able to obtain; but We have charged Our servants, whom We have now dispatched to the coming Frankfurt fair, that they shall diligently make inquiry there whether they may procure the same. What they then accomplish, of that you shall be informed hereafter.</p><p>Such tidings as We have had to hand this time, of these We send you a transcript herewith; and from it you will perceive how matters stand in France and in other places at the present time, and that it is a most wretched state of all Europe. What, then, you have concerning the state of Denmark and other news, We graciously desire you to communicate the same to Us in return.</p><p>And We remain well-disposed toward you with gracious good will.<br>Given in Our city and fortress of Kassel, on 31 August, in the year 1591.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Postscript. When, too, your observations of the fixed and the wandering stars are ready, We should gladly have the same, to rectify them with Our own. About the oryx We would have you diligently inquire — whether, and in what places, it is to be found, and how it is everywhere qualified [of what nature it is]; for the bearer of this letter, your kinsman Ascanius Bilde, will have it that he has seen some of the kind in the deer-park at Copenhagen and at Kronborg or Frederiksborg. But by his account it is by far not so large as Gesner [makes it]; for 23 spans is a great height for an animal — yet to the Creator it is not impossible.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm von Gottes Gnaden, Landtgraue zu Hessen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the \"astronomer-prince\" of Kassel, Tycho's leading princely correspondent and the patron of the Kassel observatory; <i>Graue zu Catzenelnbogen</i> = Count of <b>Katzenelnbogen</b> (an inherited Hessian comital title). The princely <i>Wir / euch</i> register and full titulature are preserved throughout, per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>DEM VESTEN / VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN / TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP</i> — \"To the honourable [<i>vest</i> = <i>fest</i>, the standard chancery honorific for a nobleman], Our trusty and well-beloved [<i>Vnserm lieben Besondern</i>, the fixed princely address-formula, protocol §5.5], Tycho Brahe, Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (Tycho's ancestral Scanian seat).</li><li><b>Opening formula.</b> <i>VNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuvor</i> = \"Our gracious greeting beforehand\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Subject — the elk exchange.</b> <i>Elendsthier / Elendthier / Elendt</i> = <b>elk (moose)</b> (Frühneuhochdeutsch <i>Elend(t)</i> \"elk\"); the Landgrave's elk in his <i>Thiergarten</i> (\"deer-park, menagerie\") lacks a <i>Gehülffe</i> (\"companion, mate\"), and he takes up Tycho's prior offer (in a letter <i>sub dato den 2. Aprilis</i>, \"dated 2 April\") of a second elk from Hven.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches.</b> <i>sub dato</i> = \"dated\"; the German text switches into Latin for set phrases, in the chancery manner (protocol §2): <i>miserrimus Status totius Europae</i> = \"a most wretched state of all Europe\"; <i>de Statu Daniae</i> = \"concerning the state of Denmark\"; <i>communiciren</i> = the Latinate verb \"to communicate.\"</li><li><b><i>Zapffenburger</b></i> — a wine. The term denotes a wine of the <i>Zapfenberg</i> type (a sweet/strong wine of the kind dispensed <i>vom Zapfen</i>, \"from the spigot\"); rendered \"a fine [cask of] Zapfenburger,\" with <i>[cask of]</i> bracketed as an editorial supplement since the source names only the wine, not its vessel. The Landgrave offers it as a <i>Gegengeschenk</i> (return-gift) for the elk.</li><li><b>The Gesner zoological query.</b> <i>dem Deutschen Exemplari Gesneri de Animalibus Quadrupedibus</i> = \"the German copy of <b>Gesner</b>'s <i>On the Four-Footed Animals</i>\" — Conrad Gesner's <i>Historia animalium</i> (Liber I, <i>De quadrupedibus uiuiparis</i>, Zurich 1551), in its German translation (the <i>Thierbuch</i>). <i>fol. 85</i> is cited as written. The animal <i>Rix … oder … Orix</i> is the classical <b>oryx</b> (the antelope of Pliny and Aelian); the Landgrave asks Tycho for natural-historical intelligence on it.</li><li><b>Latin natural-history crux.</b> <i>vbi inveniatur, et si tansae proceritatis, quantae a Gesnero pingitur, siet</i> — \"where it is found, and whether it is of as great a stature as it is depicted by Gesner.\" <b>Two printed forms flagged, not silently corrected</b> (protocol §4): <i>tansae</i> is read as <i>tantae</i> (\"so great / as great\"), and <i>siet</i> as an archaising/poetic form of <i>sit</i> (\"it be\"). Both readings are adopted in the translation while the printed forms are kept in the Source block; neither alters the sense.</li><li><b><i>Vnser Mathematicus Christophorus Rothmannus</b></i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician at Kassel (and himself a long-standing Tycho correspondent). He had left Kassel in 1590 — ostensibly to visit Hven — and never returned to his post; here the Landgrave reports that Rothmann has excused himself <i>seiner Schwacheit halben</i> (\"on the ground of his ill health\"), the cause of which the Landgrave professes not to know.</li><li><b><i>die begerte Papiermacher</b></i> — \"the paper-makers you desired\": Tycho had asked the Landgrave's help in recruiting skilled paper-makers (for his paper-mill on Hven); the Landgrave reports he could not get them, but has set his servants to inquire at the <b>Frankfurt fair</b> (<i>Franckfurder Meß</i>, the great autumn book-and-trade fair).</li><li><b><i>Zeitungen</b></i> — \"tidings, news-reports\" (here in the early sense of circulated news-sheets / dispatches, not a printed newspaper); the Landgrave encloses a transcript (<i>Abschrifft</i>) reporting the wars in <b>France</b> (the French Wars of Religion).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, c. 1591).</b> <i>v/u</i> for <i>u/v</i> (<i>vnserm, vber, vns</i>); <i>jr / jm / jn</i> = <i>ihr / ihm / ihn</i>; <i>daß … daß</i> used for both conjunction and relative <i>das</i>; <i>soli</i> is a printer's setting of <i>soll</i>; <i>thun / thut</i> = <i>tun / tut</i>; <i>Weyl</i> = <i>weil</i>; <i>gnediglich</i> = <i>gnädiglich</i>.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel / am 31. Augusti, Anno 1591</i> = \"Given in Our city and fortress of <b>Kassel</b>, on 31 August, in the year 1591\" (O.S.; Hesse-Kassel, a Protestant state, kept the Julian calendar at this date). The dateline agrees with the dataset label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> German closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VI, p. 241 (dropped by the working transcription); the bearer \"Ascanius Bilde\", the \"23 spans\", and the oryx reading confirmed against the facing Latin (VI, p. 242). This is the source of the \"23 spans\" datum the next letter (15910926001) refers back to.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the negation-count and conditional logic check out (the one negation in the elk paragraph, <i>nicht vnderlassen wollen</i> = \"did not wish to refrain,\" is a double-negative meaning \"We duly dispatched,\" reproduced as such). The soft points, flagged above rather than smoothed, are (1) the printed Latin forms <i>tansae</i> / <i>siet</i> in the oryx query, read as <i>tantae</i> / <i>sit</i>, and (2) the precise referent of <i>Zapffenburger</i> (the wine type and the bracketed <i>[cask]</i>).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 collation run clean (postscript restored/confirmed); residue is harmless spelling variants + a bracketed supplement.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice_pdf provided), so no page-image collation was possible; per brief this caps the grade at B regardless of outcome. Audited the German source_text against the published_english clause by clause instead: the elk exchange, the date '2. Aprilis', the Zapfenburger promise, the Gesner oryx/Rix-Orix query at 'fol. 85', Christoph Rothmann's absence and excuse, the Frankfurt-fair paper-makers, the France/miserrimus-status-Europae news passage, and the closing (Kassel, 31 August 1591) all render faithfully with no invented, dropped, or flipped content. The bracketed postscript (marked in the packet as restored from TBOO VI p.241) was checked internally for consistency only, not against a page image: names (Ascanius Bilde), places (Copenhagen, Kronborg, Frederiksborg), and the '23 spans' figure all carry through correctly into the translation. No defects found, but grade is capped at B because Dreyer collation could not be performed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 14, "prev": "brahe-t_15910522001", "next": "brahe-t_15910908001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15901001001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15910908001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Requests the elk Tycho promised, wanting it as a companion for his deer-park elk, and offers Zapfenburger wine in return.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910831001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910908001", "d": "1591-09-08", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 8 September 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Frühneuhochdeutsch chancery German, with heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGSTEN/ HOCHGEBORNEN FÜRSTEN VND HERRN/ HERRN WILHELM, LANDTGRAUEN ZU HESSEN / GRAUEN ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA: MEINEM GNEDIGEN HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGSTER/ Hochgeborner Fürst. Ewer F. G. sind meine fleißwillige Dienste nach allem vermügen jeder zeit zuvoran bereit. Gnedigster Herr/ Ew. F.G. an mich vnder dato den 22. Maij nechsthin/ auß jhrer Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel außgangenes Schreiben/ ist mir nu erst den 7. huius allhie auff Vraniburg zu handen Commen. Wo es so lange 3½. Monat/ vnder Wegen sey auffgehalten/ kan ich nicht wissen/ sonst hette ichs zeitlicher zu beantworten nicht vnderlassen.</p><p>Was erstlich meines fürhabenden Operis primum Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis belangt/ in welchem Capite 2. Stellarum fixarum Restitutio fundamentaliter tractirt wirdt/ dessen Ew. F. G. in jhrem Schreiben gedenckt/ Darauff kan Ew. F. G. ich dienstlich nicht verhalten/ daß dasselb Opus noch nicht vollends durch den Druck verfertigt ist/ Denn ich diesen Sommer anderer nothwendiger auffliegender Gescheffte halben nicht weyl gehabt/ solchem auffzuwarten. Wenn aber Gott Gnat vnd gelegenheit verleihen wirdt/ daß es diesen Winter möchte fertig werden/ alßdenn wil ichs Ew. F. G. jhrem begeren nach/ mittheilen.</p><p>Daß Ew. F. G. Mathematicus, ROTHMANNVS, noch nicht zu Ew. F. G. wider ankommen gewesen/ solchs thut mich nicht wenig verwundern/ denn er hat sich bey mir nicht anders mercken lassen/ denn daß er von stunden wider zu rück gen Cassel sich begeben wolte/ welches nu eben ein Jahr verlitten ist. Wofern der Morbus (desse nEw. F. G. meldung thut) in causa were/ daß er so lange außbleibt/ so hette er je mitler zeit sich können curiren lassen. Er ließ sich solchs mangels allhie gar nicht vernemen/ sonst were vielleicht hie so gut Paracelsisch Remedium dagegen zu finden/ alß anderßwo/ welches jhme auch ohn zweiffel wolbewust ist. Ich gedencke aber/ so er noch nicht widerumb zu Ew. F. G. ankommen ist/ er werde sich mit dem ersten wider einstellen. Es were gut/ daß er das Opus Stellarum Fixarum mit erster gelegenheit verfertigte/ auff daß die langweylung vnd groß angewandte mühe vnd fleiß nicht hinder bliebe/ sondern andern / vnd Posteritati zu nutz kommen möchte.</p><p>So viel das Riensthier betrifft/ da Ew. F. G. von schreibt/ hab ich wol auch in dem Deutschen Gesnero, welchs Buch mir auch bey der handt ist/ sein Iconem vnd Descriptionem gefunden/ der es ein Roßhirsch nennet/ Aber bißhero hab ich noch nicht erfahren können/ daß ein solches allhie soll verhanden seyn. Ich bin selber zu etlichen malen bey Königlicher Maiestat/ Hochlöblicher gedechtnuß/ im Thiergarten gewesen/ hab aber kein solchs allda jemals vernommen. Deßgleichen auch nicht zu Koppenhagen. So ich aber noch einige wissenschafft darumb kriegen kan/ wil Ew. F. G. ich davon Bericht zustellen.</p><p>Der Rhenböcke oder Thier/ seyndt wol allhie ein oder zwey auffm Holm zu Koppenhagen gegangen/ wie ich im worigen Schreiben angezogen hab/ Aber sie können allhie den Sommer vber nicht leben/ vnd die Hitze vertragen/ viel weniger im Landt zu Hessen/ oder andern orten/ die mehr gegen Süden liegen/ Denn sie haben jhre stelle in dem gar Septentrionali parte Noruegiae vnd Sueciae, vnd können nicht wol an andern orten vberbleiben. Aber die Elendsthier/ wiewol sie auch auß Norwegen vnd Schweden jren vrsprung haben/ so können sie doch allhie in Dennemarck etliche Jar vber/ wenn sie wol vnderhalten werden/ verharren. Versehe mich darumb/ man werde sie auch in Deutschlandt ziehen vnd halten können/ Denn es bißweylen hie im Sommer wol so hitzig ist alß dort. Ich hab letzst mal/ da Ew. F. G. ich zuschrieb/ eines hie gegen vber in Schonen/ auff meinen Gütern gehabt/ vnd wie ichs hab wollen herüber auff diese Insul holen lassen/ ist es an die Stadt/ mit Namen Landskronen/ die hie gegen vber ligt/ ankommen/ vnd allda etliche Tage auffm Schlosse/ bey einem Edelmann/ der meiner Schwester Tochter hat/ von luft wegen auffgehalten/ da es denn/ alß man vber Tisch gesessen/ ein mal die Treppe hinauff gegangen/ vnd sich in der Stuben von starckem Bier so voll getruncken/ daß es im wider herab gehen/ die Stiege hinab gefallen/ vnd das eine Bein zerbrochen/ welchs man mit keinem fleiß wider heylen kunde lassen. Vnd ist also darüber gestorben. So baldt ich aber ein anders oder ein par widerumb bekommen kan/ die zam seyn/ wil Ew. F. G. ich die gutwillig vnd mit allem fleiß zuschicken.</p><p>Ferner/ da Ew. F. G. in jrem Schreiben mention thut vom Papiermacher/ daß Ew. F. G. nach einem oder zween zu Franckfurt wolte vmbhören lassen/ kan Ew. F. G. ich nicht bergen/ daß ich selber schon einen bekommen hab/ der deß Handtwercks gnugsam erfaren/ vnd nu mit einem Gesellen im Wercke ist/ vmb Druckpapier zu machen. Thu mich gleichwol gegen Ew. F. G. dienstlichst vnd zum höchsten bedancken/ daß dieselbige gnedigen willens gewesen/ vnd sich nicht beschweret/ solch vngemach meinet halben auff sich zu nemen. Wo Ew. F. G. ich hinwiderumb nach meinem geringen eussersten vermügen zu dienst vnd gefallen seyn kan/ darzu erbiete ich mich gantz willig/ vnd soll Ew. F. G. mir ohn das zu gebieten haben.</p><p>Wil Ew. F. G. in Schirm deß Allerhöchsten zu langwiriger glücklicher Gesundtheit vnd Landsregierung hiemit dienstfleissig befolen haben.<br>Datum Vraniburg, den 8. Septemb. Anno 1591.<br>Ewer F. G.<br>Dienstwilliger<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, pp. 236–237; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. AVFF daß aber diß mein Schreiben nicht alleine von den Irdischen Thieren, sondern etwas de Caelestibus (quae nostra est voluptas) inhalte, wolte ich nicht vnderlassen, Ew. F. G. zu significiren, daß Martis Stella, der sonsten mehr alß die andern Planeten den Calculum Astronomicum eludirt, ist im letzsten Acronycho situ, welches geschehen ist im anfange deß 9. Tags Iunij, circa [Perigaeum] solare, mit dem Calculo Coperniceo gar nahe vbereingestimmet, also daß kaum ½. Gratis gefehlet hat, so doch den Numeris Alphonsinis nach bey 4½. Grad zu viel gewesen, welchs sonsten in andern Oppositionibus Solis &amp; Martis, deren ich [aliquot] obseruat, nicht geschicht. Vnd hab Martem zuvor, wenn er ist pernox gewesen, circa Arietis &amp; Librae initia, in medio quasi loco inter Apogaeum &amp; Perigaeum solare, considerirt, daß er alßdan von deß COPERNICI Calculo bey 2. oder 3. Grad abgewichen ist, vnd das in partes contrarias, prout ferebat Eccentricitatis solaris ratio. Es hat auch die Alphonsina Supputatio allda nicht bestehen können. Auß diesem ist leichtlich [ab ijs qui rem penitius introspicientes] abzumercken, daß da noch ein ander [inaequalitas] fürhanden sey, e solari Eccentricitate proueniens, die sich in den apparentem motum Planetarum insinuirt. Vnd wird in Marte, quoad tres superiores, am meisten gespürt, dieweyl er seinen Circuitum ad Solem nicht so groß hat alß die andern, daß darumb Eccentricitas Solis respectu ambitus, quem is designat, ein sensibilem proportionem hat, die doch in Ioue geringer, vnd in Saturno gar wenig gespürt wirdt. Hierauß ist zu schliessen, daß vnsere Inuention in Hypothesibus caelestibus, welche ich lib. 2. de aethereis Phaenomenis cap. 8. generaliter &amp; lato modo indicirt hab, alleine mit den Apparentes vbereinzustimmen vermag. Vnd daß weder die alte Ptolemeische, noch die newe Coperniceische hierin nicht bestehen, denn sie können diese inaequalitatem nicht gebürlich excusiren. Jch hab auch von diesem in geheim mit Ew. F. G. Mathematico, alß er hic war, geredt, der sich denn nicht wenig darob verwundert hat, vnd bekandt, daß er zuvor darhin nicht hab gedencken können, sonst hette er sein Iudicium de meis Hypothesibus nicht also praecipitirt. Vnd ist viel anders hierneben zu mercken, welches zu weitleufftig wer allhie zu erkleren. Diß hab ich nu allein zum Beschluß, vnd vmb einige Astronomische Collation halben, Ew. F. G. anzeigen wollen. Bittendt, Ew. F. G. wolle diesem, nach jrem hohen in hac scientia Verstande, nachdencken, vnd mir jhre Meynung davon gnedigst zukommen lassen. Datum vt supra.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious, High-born Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda: my gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Illustrious, High-born Prince. To Your Princely Grace my diligent and willing services are at all times ready beforehand, to the utmost of my power. Most gracious Lord, Your Princely Grace's letter to me, under the date of the 22nd of May last, issued from your city and fortress of Kassel, has only now, on the 7th of this month [<i>huius</i>], come to my hands here at Uraniborg. Where it was delayed so long — three and a half months — on the way, I cannot tell; otherwise I should not have failed to answer it more promptly.</p><p>As regards, first, the first volume of my projected work <i>On the more recent phenomena of the heavens</i> [<i>Operis … primum Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis</i>], in the 2nd chapter of which the restoration of the fixed stars [<i>Stellarum fixarum Restitutio</i>] is treated from the foundations, which Your Princely Grace mentions in your letter — concerning this I cannot, in duty, conceal from Your Princely Grace that the same work is not yet fully completed through the press; for this summer, on account of other necessary business pressing upon me, I have not had leisure to attend to it. But when God grants grace and opportunity for it to be finished this winter, then I will, according to Your Princely Grace's desire, communicate it [to you].</p><p>That Your Princely Grace's mathematician, Rothmann [<i>ROTHMANNVS</i>], had not yet returned to Your Princely Grace — this causes me no little astonishment; for he gave me to understand nothing other than that he meant to betake himself back to Kassel forthwith — which is now just a year gone by. If the illness [<i>Morbus</i>] (of which Your Princely Grace makes mention) were the cause that he stays away so long, then he could surely in the meantime have had himself cured. He let no want of that sort be perceived here at all; otherwise there would perhaps be as good a Paracelsian remedy [<i>Paracelsisch Remedium</i>] to be found here against it as anywhere else — which is also without doubt well known to him. But I think that, if he has not yet returned to Your Princely Grace, he will present himself again at the first [opportunity]. It would be well that he complete the work on the fixed stars [<i>Opus Stellarum Fixarum</i>] at the first opportunity, so that the long tedium and the great pains and diligence expended should not be left behind [unfinished], but might come to the benefit of others, and of posterity [<i>Posteritati</i>].</p><p>As far as concerns the reindeer [<i>Riensthier</i>] of which Your Princely Grace writes: I have indeed found its picture [<i>Iconem</i>] and description [<i>Descriptionem</i>] in the German Gesner [<i>Gesnero</i>] too — which book is also at hand for me — who calls it a \"horse-stag\" [<i>Roßhirsch</i>]; but hitherto I have not been able to learn that such a one is to be had here. I have myself been several times in the deer-park [Thiergarten] of His Royal Majesty, of most laudable memory; but I have never heard of any such there. Nor likewise at Copenhagen. But if I can yet get any knowledge of one, I will furnish Your Princely Grace with a report of it.</p><p>Of the reindeer-bucks, or [reindeer] beasts [<i>Rhenböcke oder Thier</i>], there have indeed gone one or two here on the Holm at Copenhagen, as I noted in my previous letter; but here they cannot live through the summer and bear the heat — far less in the land of Hesse, or other places that lie more toward the south — for they have their place in the wholly northern part of Norway and Sweden [<i>Septentrionali parte Noruegiae vnd Sueciae</i>], and cannot well survive in other places. But the elk [<i>Elendsthier</i>], although they too have their origin out of Norway and Sweden, can nonetheless endure here in Denmark for several years, if they are well kept. I expect, therefore, that one will be able to rear and keep them in Germany too, for here it is at times in summer indeed as hot as there. The last time, when I wrote to Your Princely Grace, I had one over here across the way in Skåne [Schonen], on my estates; and when I meant to have it fetched over here to this island, it came to the town named Landskrona, which lies here across the way, and there it was kept some days at the castle, for the air's sake, with a nobleman who has my sister's daughter [to wife]; whereupon — as they sat at table — it once went up the staircase, and in the chamber drank itself so full of strong beer that, on going down again, it fell down the stair and broke its one leg, which with no diligence could be got to heal again. And so it died of it. But as soon as I can get another, or a pair, that are tame, I will willingly and with all diligence send them to Your Princely Grace.</p><p>Further, since Your Princely Grace in your letter makes mention of the papermaker — that Your Princely Grace would have inquiry made after one or two at Frankfurt — I cannot hide from Your Princely Grace that I have myself already obtained one, well enough experienced in the craft, who is now at work with a journeyman to make printing-paper [<i>Druckpapier</i>]. I render, nonetheless, my most dutiful and highest thanks to Your Princely Grace that you were of gracious will, and did not think it a burden, to take such trouble upon yourself for my sake. Wherein I can in turn be of service and pleasure to Your Princely Grace, according to my slight and utmost power, thereto I offer myself wholly willingly; and Your Princely Grace shall in any case have me at your command.</p><p>Herewith I would commend Your Princely Grace, in the protection of the Most High, to long-lasting, happy health and government of your land, in dutiful diligence.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 8th of September, in the year 1591.<br>Your Princely Grace's<br>dutiful and willing servant,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Postscript. But that this my letter may treat not only of the earthly animals but also somewhat of the celestial [bodies] (which is Our delight), I would not refrain from signifying to Your Princely Grace that the star Mars — which more than the other planets otherwise eludes the astronomical computation — in its last acronychal position, which occurred at the beginning of the 9th day of June, near the solar [perigee], agreed very nearly with the Copernican computation, so that it fell short by scarcely ½ a degree, whereas by the Alphonsine Numbers it was in excess by about 4½ degrees; which otherwise does not happen in other oppositions of the Sun and Mars, of which I have observed several. And I had earlier, when Mars was all-night visible, near the beginnings of Aries and Libra, in a place as it were midway between the solar apogee and perigee, considered that it then departed from Copernicus's computation by about 2 or 3 degrees, and that in contrary directions, as the reckoning of the solar eccentricity bore. There too the Alphonsine reckoning could not hold. From this it is easily to be noted, by those who look into the matter deeply, that there is yet another inequality present, arising from the solar eccentricity, which insinuates itself into the apparent motion of the planets. And it is most sensed in Mars, as regards the three superior [planets], inasmuch as its circuit about the Sun is not so great as the others', so that for that reason the eccentricity of the Sun, in respect of the circuit which it describes, bears a sensible proportion — which, however, is smaller in Jupiter, and very little sensed in Saturn. From this it is to be concluded that our discovery in the celestial hypotheses, which I have indicated generally and in a broad manner in Book 2 of the De aethereis Phaenomenis, chapter 8, is alone able to agree with the Appearances; and that neither the old Ptolemaic nor the new Copernican [hypotheses] hold here, for they cannot duly excuse this inequality. I have also spoken of this in confidence with Your Princely Grace's mathematician, when he was here, who thereupon marvelled not a little, and confessed that he had not been able to think of it before, else he would not have so precipitated his judgement of my hypotheses. And there is much else to be noted alongside this, which would be too lengthy to explain here. This I have wished to indicate to Your Princely Grace only by way of conclusion, and for the sake of some astronomical collation; begging that Your Princely Grace will, according to your high understanding in this science, reflect upon it, and graciously let me have your opinion thereof. Given as above.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince whose Kassel observatory and correspondence (largely conducted through his mathematician Rothmann) were Tycho's chief scholarly counterpart in the 1580s. The full chancery titulature (<i>Graf zu Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain und Nidda</i>) is preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Printed by Tycho himself in the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I, Uraniborg 1596), the volume from which the dataset's `edition` field is taken; the text is also in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No MS witness is recorded in the source header.</li><li><b>Language.</b> Verified by reading as <b>Frühneuhochdeutsch chancery German</b>, not Danish; the heuristic tag <i>de</i> is correct. The register is the formal <i>Ew. F. G.</i> (<i>Eu[e]r Fürstlich[e] Gnaden</i> = \"Your Princely Grace\") address maintained throughout, with constant code-switching into Latin for technical and bibliographic nouns. Latin embeddings retained and italicised on first use: <i>Operis primum Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis</i>, <i>Capite 2.</i>, <i>Stellarum fixarum Restitutio</i>, <i>fundamentaliter tractirt</i>, <i>Mathematicus</i>, <i>Morbus</i>, <i>in causa</i>, <i>curiren</i>, <i>Paracelsisch Remedium</i>, <i>Opus Stellarum Fixarum</i>, <i>Posteritati</i>, <i>Iconem</i>, <i>Descriptionem</i>, <i>mention</i>, <i>Septentrionali parte Noruegiae … Sueciae</i>.</li><li><b>The work in progress.</b> <i>primum Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis … Capite 2. Stellarum fixarum Restitutio</i> = the first volume of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the \"Introduction to the renewed astronomy\"), whose long-delayed printing occupied Tycho's Uraniborg press; <i>Stellarum fixarum Restitutio</i> = \"the restoration of the fixed stars\" (the fixed-star catalogue), rendered \"restoration\" per the controlled glossary (§5.1, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i>).</li><li><b>Rothmann.</b> <i>ROTHMANNVS</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician (§5.6), who had visited Tycho on Hven in 1590 and — as this letter records — never returned to Kassel. The <i>Morbus</i> (\"illness\") is the reason the Landgrave had offered; Tycho is sceptical, noting Rothmann showed no sign of it on Hven and could have sought a <i>Paracelsisch Remedium</i> (\"Paracelsian remedy\") there. <i>Paracelsisch Remedium</i> kept as a period term (cf. §5.4 <i>remedium magneticum</i> — not modernised).</li><li><b>Zoological matter.</b> The bulk of the letter answers the Landgrave's request for northern animals. <i>Riensthier</i> = <b>reindeer</b> (<i>Rangifer</i>); Tycho cross-refers to the <b>German Gesner</b> (<i>Gesnero</i> = the German-language edition of Conrad Gesner's <i>Historia animalium</i> / <i>Thierbuch</i>), where the beast is figured under the name <i>Roßhirsch</i> (\"horse-stag\"). <i>Rhenböcke oder Thier</i> = \"reindeer-bucks, or [reindeer-]beasts\"; <i>auffm Holm zu Koppenhagen</i> = on the <b>Holmen</b> (the naval-yard island) at Copenhagen. <i>Königliche Maiestat, Hochlöblicher gedechtnuß</i> = the late <b>King Frederik II</b> (d. 1588), \"of most laudable memory.\" <i>Elendsthier</i> = <b>elk / moose</b> (<i>Alces</i>; German <i>Elen[d]tier</i>) — the comic centrepiece anecdote. <i>Septentrionali parte Noruegiae und Sueciae</i> = \"the northern part of Norway and Sweden.\"</li><li><b>The elk anecdote.</b> <i>Schonen</i> = <b>Skåne</b> (then Danish), where Tycho held estates across the Øresund; <i>Landskronen</i> = <b>Landskrona</b>, the town opposite Hven. The nobleman \"who has my sister's daughter [to wife]\" is not named in the text; left unidentified rather than guessed. The elk drinks itself drunk on strong beer at table, falls down the castle stair, breaks a leg, and dies — reported deadpan. No interpretive softening applied.</li><li><b>Papermaker.</b> <i>Papiermacher … Druckpapier</i> — the Landgrave had offered to find a papermaker at Frankfurt for Tycho's press; Tycho reports he has already engaged one, working with a <i>Geselle</i> (journeyman) to make printing-paper for Uraniborg.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline <i>den 8. Septemb. Anno 1591</i> matches the catalogue label exactly (no discrepancy). Denmark and Hesse-Kassel both kept the <b>Julian calendar</b>, so the date is <b>O.S.</b> <i>den 7. huius</i> = \"the 7th of this [month],\" i.e. 7 September 1591 (O.S.), the day before writing. The incoming letter's date <i>den 22. Maij</i> (22 May) is likewise O.S.; Tycho's \"three and a half months\" in transit is his own reckoning of the 22 May → 7 September interval and is reproduced, not recomputed.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> Opening <i>Ewer F. G. sind meine fleißwillige Dienste … zuvoran bereit</i> and the closing <i>in Schirm deß Allerhöchsten … befolen haben / … Dienstwilliger</i> are the standard subordinate-to-prince courtesy frame (servant writing to lord, the inverse of the Landgrave's princely <i>Wir/euch</i> register); translated, not dropped (§5.5, §4).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> German closing postscript \"POSTSCRIPTA … de Caelestibus\", restored from Dreyer VI, pp. 236–237 (dropped by the working transcription); the Mars opposition (early 9 June, near solar perigee), the Copernican-vs-Alphonsine error, and the self-citation \"lib. 2 de aethereis Phaenomenis cap. 8\" confirmed against the facing Latin (VI, p. 239); bracketed words supply OCR-garbled tokens per the Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German and the Latin embeddings are rendered securely and the dateline carries no discrepancy. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (i) <i>Rhenböcke oder Thier</i> — whether <i>Thier</i> here is a loose apposition (\"reindeer-bucks, or [those] beasts\") or names a distinct does/hinds category is not decidable from the text, so the inclusive \"or [reindeer-]beasts\" is kept; (ii) the unnamed nobleman married to Tycho's niece is left unidentified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief this is audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B; no Dreyer collation performed. Checked the full German source_text against the published_english sentence by sentence: the salutation and address block, the 3.5-month delivery-delay detail, the Operis primum Tomum de Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis / chapter 2 passage, the Rothmann-illness-and-Paracelsian-remedy paragraph, the reindeer (Riensthier/Roßhirsch, Gesner citation) paragraph, the elk (Elendsthier) and the Landskrona anecdote (nobleman married to the writer's sister's daughter, the animal's death from drinking beer and falling down stairs), the papermaker paragraph, the closing and date '8 September 1591', and the restored Latin/German postscript on the Mars-acronychal-position observation (9 June, agreement with Copernicus to within 1/2 degree vs. Alphonsine excess of about 4.5 degrees; the Aries/Libra 2-3 degree divergence; Book 2 De aethereis Phaenomenis cap.8). Every proper name, number, date, and clause has a counterpart in the source; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 15, "prev": "brahe-t_15910831001", "next": "brahe-t_15910926001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910831001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920402001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the three-and-a-half-month delay in receiving Wilhelm's May letter, then discusses the first volume of *De Recentioribus coeli Phaenomenis* and its treatment of the stellar restoration.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910908001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15910926001", "d": "1591-09-26", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 26 September 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German, chancery, with heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596, Liber I; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGSTEN/ HOCHGEBORNEN FÜRSTEN VND HERRN/ HERRN WILHELM, LANDTGRAUEN ZU HESSEN/ GRAUEN ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA: MEINEM GNEDIGEN HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGSTER/ Hochgeborner Fürst. Gnedigster Herr/ Ewer F. G. kan ich dienstlich vnangezeigt nicht lassen/ wie daß ich derselben Schreiben von jrem Denischen Jungen/ dem Bilden meinem Vettern den 23. Septembris wol empfangen hab. In welchem Ew. F. G. abermals von dem Elendsthier (wovon ich ein mal in meinem Schreiben Zusage gethan) meldung thut. Was aber dasselbig Thier (da ichs noch im vorradt hette/ vnd willens war/ Ew. F. G. vberzuschicken) am Beinbruche fürn zusall bekommen hab/ vnd darüber gestorben sey/ solchs ist in meinem vorigen Schreiben gnugsam angezogen/ Welch Schreiben ich dem Edlen vnd Vesten Henrich Rammel/ meinem besondern gutem Freunde/ so baldt er mir Ew. F. G. Brieff (deß Datum vnderm 22. Maij stehet) hat zugeschickt/ widerumb vberliefern lassen. Darauff er mich folgendts allhie besucht/ vnd also solchen Brieff mit einem eigen Botten an Ew. F. G. abzufertigen verheissen. Er hat auch allhie das mal vnder andern mit mir von dero Theologorum (die Anno 1529. zu Marpurg zusammen gewesen) Vergleichung geredt/ vnd darneben ein Deutsches Scriptum (welchs Ew. F. G. jme vberschickt) gezeigt/ worin dieselbige Concordantien Articulweiß verfast/ vnd mit Ew. F. G. Handtschrifft allenthalben zu weiterer Versicherung vndergezeichnet seyndt. Vnd wiewol ich damals dieselbige mit den jenigen/ die in Historia Augustanae Confessionis, Francofordiae ad Maenum, per Matthiam Ritterum Ecclesiasten Anno 1578. editâ, et a Chytraeo prius contextâ, fol. 643. et seq. commemorirt werden/ ob alles da bona fide, wie es sich gebürt/ Latine referirt vnd reddirt wirdt/ zu conferiren willens gewesen bin/ So ists doch (dieweyl gedachter Ramelius von hinnen nach Pommern notwendiger Gescheffte halben eylete/ vnd auch vmb anderer fürfelle willen) nachblieben. Es kan aber Ew. F. G. leichtlich mit dem jetztgedachten Lateinischen Exemplari ein Collation hierin instituiren lassen/ ob alles gebürlich vnd rechtmessig transferirt worden. Welchs ich auch gern thun wolte/ wan Ew. F. G. mir auch ein Abschrifft davon gnediglich communiciren, oder aber daß mir ermeldter Ramelius ein Copey von dem seinigen mitteile/ gestatten wolte. Dan ich vermercke wol auß der widerparteyischen Theologen zu vnsern zeiten außgangenen Scriptis, daß sie nicht allenthalben candide et conuenienter Aruersariorum sententias citiren vnd außlegen/ sondern daß ein grosse Philautia, ambitio, odium, vnd vincendi vindictaeque cupiditas bey jnen mehrern teils vberhandt genommen/ Ita vt sub praetextu promotionis veritatis Euangelicae, seipsos et sua vt plurimum quaerant. Aber man mag sie in jrer Würde bleiben/ vnd jre Sachen selber verantworten lassen.</p><p>Anlangendt aber mein vorbemeldts Schreiben (in welchem ich alle gelegenheit vom Elendsthier vermeldet hab) schicke ich Ew. F. G. nu dasselb zu. Dan da obgedachter Ramelius auff dem Wege/ alß er von hinnen nach Deutschlandt reysete/ erfaren/ daß Ew. F. G. Junge allhie verhanden war/ hat er mir den Brieff wider zurück geschickt/ vnd darneben einen andern/ den er selbst an Ew. F. G. dirigirt, auff daß ich Ew. F. G. Jungen dieselbe/ vmb andere Vnkosten zu vermeyden/ mitgeben kündte/ welchs ich auch gethan hab/ dieweyl der Junge widerumb zu Ew. F. G. baldt zurück zeucht. Jch hab auch vnder der zeit/ die er hie im Lande verharret/ allenthalben bey meiner Freundtschafft nachforschen lassen/ ob jrgendts ein Elendsthier/ das zahm sey/ verhanden were/ aber niergendts ein solchs erfragen können/ sonst hette ich jetzunder Ew. F. G. eines mit demselbigen Jungen (der mir gnugsam zu verstehen gab/ was Ew. F. G. fur grosse Lust zu einem solchen Thier habe) vbergeschickt. Auff das aber Ew. F. G. gleichwol hierin mit dem ersten möchte gewilfart werden/ hab ich hinauff in Norwegen an Königlicher Maiestat allda Stadthalter/ den Edlen vnd Wolgebornen Axel Güldenstern/ welcher mein gar nahe Verwandter vnd sehr guter Freundt ist/ fleissig geschrieben vnd angelangt/ daß er mir auffs wenigst ein par derselbigen Thier/ die da jung weren/ mit erster gelegenheit herab schicken wolte/ dan dero in seinem Lehen vnd Gebiete etliche verhanden seyndt. Hab auch gleichfalls einem andern Edelman in Norwegen/ der mir nahe beschwägert ist/ geschrieben/ daß er mir auch ein par auß seinem Lehen (da man auch der Gattung nicht wenig findet) mit dem ersten wolte zustehen lassen. Bin darumb guter hoffnung/ ich werde auffs wenigste von dieser einem/ wo nicht von allen beyden/ dero Thieren etliche bekommen. Welchs/ so es nicht vorm Winter geschehen kan/ hoffe ich doch auffs Vorjar Ew. F. G. hierin nach jrem willen dienstlich zu seyn.</p><p>Was antrifft das ander Thier/ welchs Orix genandt wirdt/ hab ich das auch in meinem Deutschen Gesnero nachgesucht/ vnd ein kurtze Beschreibung davon am 68. Blat gefunden/ doch keine Verzeichnuß darbey/ So meldets auch nicht/ wie hoch vnd groß es seyn soll. Worauß ich vermercke/ daß Ew. F. G. Deutscher Gesnerus ein ander Buch vnd Exemplar sey/ dan welchs mir bey der Handt ist. Es muß ein gewaltig groß Thier seyn/ vnd wol bey zweyer Mans höhe erreichen/ wan es 23. Spann hoch ist. Von diesem weiß man in vnsern Landen/ vnd den jenigen/ die noch mehr gegen Norden gelegen seyndt/ bißhero gar nichts zu sagen/ dan es wirdt allda kaum gefunden. Ich bin wol newlich in erfarung kommen/ daß vorbemeldter Axel Güldenstern vor kurtzer zeit/ ein seltzames vnd zuvor nicht viel bekandtes Thier in Norwegen/ von den jenigen Leuten/ die vber die hohe vnd mit Schnee bedeckten Klippen auff besondern langen vnd breiten Höltzern vnder den Füssen fast gemacht/ gar schnell lauffen/ also/ daß sie ein Hirsch ereylen können/ soll auffgespürt haben. Aber was es für ein Thier sey/ vnd wie groß/ hab ich noch nicht gnugsam erfaren können. So baldt ich einige Wiffenschafft davon kriege/ wil ich Ew. F. G. solchs anzeigen.</p><p>Von den Obseruationibus Stellarum Fixarum, davon Ew. F. G. allhie auch meldung thut/ hab ich in meinem vorigen Schreiben anzeigung gethan/ daß dasselbig Buch/ darin sie begriffen werden/ noch nicht vollendts in Druck verfertigt sey. So baldt aber das geschicht/ soli Ew. F. G. dasselb zuzuschichen nicht vnderlassen werden.</p><p>Daß Ew. F. G. Mathematicus ROTHMANNVS, noch bißhero sich nicht hat eingestelt/ thut mich je lenger je mehr verwundern/ vnd es muß seltzame Vrsache haben/ daß er so lange außbleibt. Ewer F. G. wirdt wol Mittel finden/ daß sie jn widerumb zu sich alliciren könne/ wan sie schon einige Liberalitet, mehr alß sonst/ auff jn wagen solle/ auff daß er das vorgenommene Opus Fixarum Stellarum desto williger vnd bälder verfertige. Es mangelt jme am Ingenio vnd Wiffenschafft darzu nicht/ Aber mir hat wol an jme gedaucht/ daß er (wie Ew. F. G. auch zuvor von jm geschrieben hat) einen eigenwillischen Kopff haben solte/ worauff jme nur gelüstete seinen eigen Hut auffzusetzen. Wie man dan offt findet/ daß excellentia Ingenia auch jre sonderliche Gebrechen vnd Naeuos haben/ die man dan/ so sie nicht anders zu corrigiren seyndt/ gedulten mag. Jch versehe mich zu jme/ er werde sich noch besinnen/ vnd sich zu Ew. F. G. wider einstellen/ vnd im fürhabenden Wercke keinen fleiß vnd mühe sparen/ damit es mit dem ersten an Tag möchte kommen.</p><p>Von den Papiermachern hab ich Ew. F. G. auch im vorigen Schreiben zuverstehen geben/ daß ich deren jetzunder keinen bedürffe/ dieweil ich selbs schon lengst Leute bekommen hab/ die mir die Arbeit verrichten. Thu mich gleichwol für den erbottenen gnedigen willen/ gegen Ew. F. G. dienstlich vnd zum höchsten bedancken: Gleichsfalls auch für die mitgeteilte newe Zeitung/ die mir gar angenem gewesen seyndt.</p><p>Was diese Länder antrifft/ weiß ich Ew. F. G. nichts newes widerumb zuzuschreiben/ dan daß Status Daniae vnd Noruegiae, noch (Gott Lob) gar friedig vnd richtig sey. Der Allmechtige Gott wölle weiter Gnade verleihen. Weiß E. F. G. dißmal weiters nicht zuzuschreiben/ Wo aber etwas fürfellt/ solches soll Ew. F. G. mit erster gelegenheit referirt werden.</p><p>Vnd wil Ew. F. G. in Gottes Allmechtigen gnadenreichen Schirm/ zu langwiriger Gesundtheit vnd friedlicher Landsregierung/ hiemit befohlen haben.<br>Datum Vraniburg, den 26. Septembris, Anno 1591.<br>Ewer F. G.<br>Dienstwilliger<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VI, p. 246; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. NACH dem ich vermercke auß Ew. F. G. Brieff, daß der [ROTHMANNVS] noch nicht in so lange zeit angekommen ist, gedencke ich wol, daß er auch die Descriptionem meorum Instrumentorum, welche er hic verzeichnet hat, kaum wirdt Ew. F. G. zugeschickt haben. Darumb hab ich nicht wollen vnderlassen, meinem Deutschen Amanuensi zu befehlen, daß er von new ein [Verzeichnuß] davon, so viel jm in der eyl muglich were, zusammen schriebe, welche Ew. F. G. hierbey mit vorgemeldten Knaben bekommen wird. Wan ich selbst ein mal besser weyl habe, bin ich willens alle meine Instrumenta Astronomica deutlicher vnd fleissiger, beyde mit jrer Fabrica vnd [Vsu] zu beschreiben. Ew. F. G. wolle diese geringe Consignation in gnedigen Willen annemen. Datum vt supra.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Serene, High-Born Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: my gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Serene, High-Born Prince, most gracious Lord. I cannot dutifully leave Your Princely Grace unadvised that I duly received Your Grace's letter from your Danish page, Bille my kinsman, on 23 September. In it Your Princely Grace once more makes mention of the elk [<i>Elendsthier</i>] (of which I once made a promise in my letter). But that this animal — when I still had it in store and was minded to send it over to Your Princely Grace — suffered, by mishap, a broken leg and thereupon died, this was sufficiently set out in my previous letter; which letter I had delivered back to the Noble and Steadfast Henrik Ramel, my especially good friend, as soon as he had sent on to me Your Princely Grace's letter (which bears the date 22 May). Whereupon he afterwards visited me here, and so promised to dispatch that letter to Your Princely Grace by a courier of his own. He also, on that occasion, spoke with me, among other matters, about the reconciliation of those theologians (who came together at Marburg in the year 1529), and showed me besides a German document (which Your Princely Grace had sent over to him) in which those concords are set down article by article, and subscribed throughout, for further assurance, with Your Princely Grace's own hand. And although I was at that time minded to compare these with those [accords] that are recorded in the <i>History of the Augsburg Confession</i> — published at Frankfurt-on-Main by Matthias Ritter, churchman, in the year 1578, and previously composed by Chytraeus, fol. 643 and following — [to see] whether everything there is reported and rendered into Latin in good faith [<i>bona fide</i>], as is fitting, yet it was left undone (since the said Ramel was hastening hence to Pomerania on necessary business, and also on account of other occurrences). But Your Princely Grace can easily have a collation undertaken in this matter with the Latin copy just mentioned, [to see] whether everything has been translated fittingly and rightly. Which I too would gladly do, if Your Princely Grace would graciously communicate to me a transcript thereof, or else permit the said Ramel to share with me a copy of his own. For I do well perceive, from the writings put forth by the theologians of the opposing party in our times, that they do not everywhere cite and expound their adversaries' opinions candidly and fittingly [<i>candide et convenienter Adversariorum sententias</i>], but rather that a great self-love, ambition, hatred, and lust to conquer and to take revenge [<i>Philautia, ambitio, odium, vincendi vindictaeque cupiditas</i>] has for the most part got the upper hand among them — so that, under the pretext of promoting the truth of the Gospel, they for the most part seek themselves and their own [<i>Ita ut sub praetextu promotionis veritatis Evangelicae, seipsos et sua ut plurimum quaerant</i>]. But one may let them remain in their dignity and answer for their affairs themselves.</p><p>As concerns my aforesaid letter (in which I reported every circumstance about the elk), I now send the same to Your Princely Grace. For when the above-mentioned Ramel, on his way as he was travelling hence to Germany, learned that Your Princely Grace's page was present here, he sent the letter back to me, and besides it another which he himself had addressed to Your Princely Grace, so that I might give them to Your Princely Grace's page — to avoid other expenses — which I have indeed done, since the page is shortly returning again to Your Princely Grace. During the time he tarried here in the land, I also had inquiry made everywhere among my kinsfolk whether a tame elk were anywhere to be had, but nowhere could such a one be obtained; otherwise I would now have sent Your Princely Grace one by that same page (who gave me sufficiently to understand what great desire Your Princely Grace has for such an animal). But so that Your Princely Grace might nonetheless be obliged in this at the first [opportunity], I have diligently written to and applied to His Royal Majesty's governor up in Norway, the Noble and Well-Born Axel Gyldenstierne, who is my very near kinsman and very good friend, that he would send down to me at the first opportunity at least a pair of those animals that are young, since there are several to be had in his fief and territory. I have likewise written to another nobleman in Norway, who is closely related to me by marriage, that he too would let me have a pair from his fief (where also one finds not a few of the kind) at the first [opportunity]. I am therefore in good hope that I shall obtain some of those animals, at the least from one of these, if not from both. And should this not be able to happen before winter, I yet hope to be at Your Princely Grace's service in this, according to your wish, by next spring.</p><p>As concerns the other animal, which is called the oryx [<i>Orix</i>], I have looked it up too in my German Gessner, and found a short description of it on leaf 68, but no illustration alongside it; nor does it state how tall and large it is supposed to be. From which I perceive that Your Princely Grace's German Gessner is another book and copy than the one I have to hand. It must be a mighty large animal, and may well reach two men's height, if it is 23 spans tall. Of this [animal] one knows hitherto nothing at all to say in our lands, nor in those that lie still further to the north, for it is scarcely found there. I have indeed recently come to learn that the above-mentioned Axel Gyldenstierne is said, a short time ago, to have tracked down a strange and previously not much known animal in Norway, [reported] by those people who, over the high and snow-covered crags, run very swiftly upon particular long and broad boards almost fastened under their feet, so that they can overtake a stag. But what kind of animal it is, and how large, I have not yet been able to learn sufficiently. As soon as I get any knowledge of it, I will signify it to Your Princely Grace.</p><p>Concerning the <i>Observations of the Fixed Stars</i> [<i>Observationibus Stellarum Fixarum</i>], of which Your Princely Grace here also makes mention, I gave notice in my previous letter that the book in which they are contained is not yet entirely finished in print. But as soon as that is done, sending the same to Your Princely Grace shall not be neglected.</p><p>That Your Princely Grace's mathematician Rothmann has still not so far presented himself astonishes me more and more the longer [it goes on], and there must be some strange cause that he stays away so long. Your Princely Grace will surely find means by which you may entice him back to you again — even should you have to venture some liberality upon him, more than usual — so that he may complete the more willingly and the sooner the undertaken <i>Work on the Fixed Stars</i> [<i>Opus Fixarum Stellarum</i>]. He does not lack the talent and learning for it [<i>Ingenio und Wissenschaft</i>]; but it had indeed seemed to me of him that he (as Your Princely Grace too has previously written of him) was likely to have a self-willed head, set on nothing but putting on his own hat. As one often finds that excellent talents [<i>excellentia Ingenia</i>] also have their particular failings and blemishes [<i>Naevos</i>], which one may then bear with, if they are not otherwise to be corrected. I trust of him that he will yet bethink himself, and present himself to Your Princely Grace again, and spare no diligence and pains in the work in hand, so that it might come to light at the first.</p><p>Concerning the paper-makers, I have likewise given Your Princely Grace to understand in my previous letter that I have need of none of them at present, since I have long since myself obtained people who do the work for me. Nonetheless I dutifully and most highly thank Your Princely Grace for the gracious goodwill offered: likewise also for the new tidings imparted, which were very agreeable to me.</p><p>As concerns these lands, I know of nothing new to write back to Your Princely Grace, save that the state of Denmark and of Norway [<i>Status Daniae und Norvegiae</i>] is still (praise God) quite peaceful and in good order. May Almighty God grant further grace. I know nothing further to write to Your Princely Grace at this time; but should anything occur, it shall be reported to Your Princely Grace at the first opportunity.</p><p>And I herewith commend Your Princely Grace to the gracious protection of God Almighty, to long-lasting health and peaceful governance of your lands.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 26th of September, in the year 1591.<br>Your Princely Grace's<br>obedient servant,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Postscript. Since I perceive from Your Princely Grace's letter that Rothmann has still, in so long a time, not arrived, I rather think that he, too, will scarcely have sent to Your Princely Grace the description of my instruments which he drew up here. Therefore I would not refrain from charging my German amanuensis to draw up afresh a register thereof, so far as was possible to him in the haste — which Your Princely Grace will receive herewith by the aforesaid boy. When I myself once have better leisure, it is my intention to describe all my astronomical instruments more clearly and carefully, both as to their make and use. May Your Princely Grace receive this slight consignation [inventory-description] with gracious goodwill. Given as above.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondents.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince whose Kassel observatory and mathematician Christoph Rothmann were Tycho's principal scholarly counterparts in the 1580s. The titulature (<i>Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, Nidda</i>) is the Landgrave's full chancery style and is preserved.</li><li><b>Language.</b> Early New High German chancery prose (correctly tagged <i>de</i>), not mis-detected Danish. Tycho code-switches into Latin for technical and learned matter throughout: Latin nouns and tags (<i>conferiren, transferirt, Collation, communiciren, Liberalität, Ingenio, Naevos, Status Daniae und Norvegiae</i>) are kept in the source block; the longer Latin clauses are translated with the Latin retained in brackets on first occurrence.</li><li><b>Subject — the elk (<i>Elendsthier</i>).</b> The whole exchange concerns the Landgrave's wish to stock his menagerie (<i>Thiergarten</i>) with a Scandinavian elk/moose (<i>Alces alces</i>). Wilhelm's earlier letter (in the same corpus, opening <i>\"…wir in unserm Thiergarten ein Elendsthier haben…\"</i>) reports his own elk lacking a mate; Tycho's promised animal had died of a broken leg. <i>Elendsthier</i> (= <i>Elen[d]tier</i>, \"elk\") is rendered \"elk\" throughout; not to be confused with the false-friend <i>Elend</i> \"misery.\"</li><li><b>Persons.</b></li><li><i>dem Bilden meinem Vettern</i> — a page of the Bille family (\"Bille, my kinsman\"); the Bille and Brahe houses were closely allied (Tycho's mother was Beate Bille). <i>Vetter</i> here = \"kinsman/cousin\" in the broad period sense.</li><li><i>Henrich Rammel / Ramelius</i> = <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (d. 1610), Pomeranian-born Danish privy councillor and diplomat, a friend of both courts; the conduit for the Hesse–Denmark correspondence. The honorific <i>der Edle und Veste</i> (\"the Noble and Steadfast\") is the standard address for the lesser nobility.</li><li><i>Axel Güldenstern</i> = <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (1542–1603), royal Statholder (governor) of Norway from 1588, Tycho's near kinsman — Tycho's grandmother was a Gyldenstierne — here the hoped-for source of young Norwegian elk.</li><li><i>Chytraeus</i> = <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600), Rostock theologian and historian, original compiler of the history of the Augsburg Confession; <i>Matthias Ritter</i> (Ritterus), the churchman under whose name the 1578 Frankfurt edition appeared.</li><li><b>Theological matter.</b> <i>die Theologorum … die Anno 1529. zu Marpurg zusammen gewesen</i> = the participants of the <b>Marburg Colloquy of 1529</b> (Luther, Zwingli, et al., on the Eucharist); Wilhelm IV — son of Philip of Hesse, who had convened it — was forwarding a German record of the agreed articles for Tycho to collate against the printed Latin <i>Historia Augustanae Confessionis</i>. The reference <i>fol. 643. et seq.</i> is Tycho's own citation of that 1578 volume; preserved as given.</li><li><b>The oryx (<i>Orix</i>) and the \"German Gessner\".</b> <i>mein Deutscher Gesnerus</i> = the German <i>Thierbuch</i>, the vernacular version of <b>Conrad Gessner's</b> <i>Historia animalium</i>. Tycho notes that the <i>Orix</i> (oryx) description sits on <i>leaf 68</i> of his copy but with no woodcut and no stated dimensions, and infers the Landgrave owns a different printing/copy. <i>Spann</i> = a span (the hand-breadth measure, ~20–23 cm); \"23 spans\" is reported as Tycho gives it, with his own hedge (\"if it is 23 spans tall\").</li><li><b>The skier passage (flagged).</b> <i>von den jenigen Leuten/ die vber die hohe vnd mit Schnee bedeckten Klippen auff besondern langen vnd breiten Höltzern vnder den Füssen fast gemacht/ gar schnell lauffen</i> — an early description of <b>skiing</b> (Sámi/Norwegian skiers on long boards, fast enough to overtake a stag). The syntax is loose: <i>fast</i> is here the adverb \"almost/well-nigh\" qualifying <i>gemacht</i> (\"almost fastened under their feet\"), not modern <i>fest</i> \"firmly\" — though period <i>fast</i>/<i>fest</i> overlap and the sense could be \"firmly fastened.\" Rendered \"almost fastened\" with the alternative noted; the looseness is Tycho's, not smoothed.</li><li><b>Astronomical content.</b> <i>Observationibus Stellarum Fixarum</i> / <i>Opus Fixarum Stellarum</i> — Tycho's <b>fixed-star catalogue</b>, the still-unprinted star-catalogue volume (eventually part of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>); rendered \"Observations of the Fixed Stars\" / \"Work on the Fixed Stars,\" the Latin kept per protocol §5.1. <i>Ew. F. G. Mathematicus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Landgrave's mathematician, who had visited Hven in 1590 and then failed to return to Kassel — the absence Tycho here puzzles over. No instrument-terms or astronomical symbols occur in this letter.</li><li><b>Paper-makers.</b> Wilhelm had evidently offered to send paper-makers for Tycho's Uraniborg paper-mill; Tycho declines, having already secured his own workers.</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> Opening <i>Ewer F. G. … dienstlich … nicht lassen</i> and the address <i>Durchleuchtigster, Hochgeborner Fürst, Gnedigster Herr</i> are standard chancery courtesy to a prince; <i>Ew. F. G.</i> = <i>Ewer Fürstlichen Gnaden</i> → \"Your Princely Grace\" throughout. Subscription <i>Ewer F. G. Dienstwilliger</i> → \"Your Princely Grace's obedient servant,\" per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datum Vraniburg, den 26. Septembris, Anno 1591</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 26 September 1591\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The header date matches the dateline and the dataset label exactly; no discrepancy. The received-date <i>23. Septembris</i> and the Landgrave's letter-date <i>22. Maij</i> are internal references, reproduced as written, not converted.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The legally/logically load-bearing negatives are reproduced one-for-one: <i>kan ich … nicht lassen</i> (\"I cannot leave [you] unadvised\"); <i>deren jetzunder keinen bedürffe</i> (\"have need of none of them at present\"); <i>niergendts ein solchs erfragen können</i> (\"nowhere could obtain such a one\"); <i>nicht allenthalben candide … citiren</i> (\"do not everywhere cite candidly\"); <i>soll … nicht vnderlassen werden</i> (\"shall not be neglected\"). The double-negative courtesy formula (<i>nicht … vnangezeigt … lassen</i> = \"cannot leave unadvised\") is rendered as the affirmative it is.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> German closing postscript announcing the enclosed instrument-\"Consignatio\", restored from Dreyer VI, p. 246 (dropped by the working transcription); confirmed against the facing Latin (VI, p. 249).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German and the embedded Latin are rendered securely and the negations verified one-for-one; the one genuinely loose spot is the syntax of the skier clause (<i>fast gemacht</i> — \"almost fastened\" vs. \"firmly fastened,\" the period <i>fast</i>/<i>fest</i> overlap), flagged above rather than silently resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo.slice_pdf is null for this letter (one of the 49 letters with no Dreyer slice per the brief), so audited against source_text only and grade is capped at B regardless of outcome. Collated published_english against the German source_text (with embedded Latin phrases) clause by clause: the address block and title; the elk (Elendsthier) narrative including the broken-leg/death detail and the Ramel courier chain; the Marburg 1529 theologians' reconciliation passage and its Latin quotations (Philautia, ambitio, odium, vincendi vindictaeque cupiditas; sub praetextu promotionis veritatis Evangelicae); the Frankfurt printing citation (Matthias Ritter, 1578, Chytraeus, fol. 643); the Axel Gyldenstierne elk-procurement passage (named twice, consistently); the oryx/Gessner passage including the 23-span height figure; the skiers-and-strange-animal passage; the fixed-stars book status; the Rothmann paragraph; the paper-makers paragraph; the closing and the 26 September 1591 date; and the postscript (which source_text itself flags as restored from TBOO VI p.246 by the working transcription -- unverifiable here since no slice_pdf exists, but the postscript's own English rendering matches its German exactly). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 16, "prev": "brahe-t_15910908001", "next": "brahe-t_15920220001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910831001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920402001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that the promised elk broke its leg and died before it could be sent, and notes that a prior letter explaining this was dispatched via Henrik Ramel.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15910926001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15911113001", "d": "1591-11-13", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "John Maitland", "ck": "John Maitland", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Metellus — Uraniborg, 13 November 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(Dreyer)</i> · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOANNEM METELLUM.</p><p>GRATISSIMUM mihi fuit, Magnifice Domine IOANNES METELLE, quod comitando Serenissimum Regem vestrum me in hac Insula invisere non gravatus sis, quodque discedens, illa ingenij exquisitam acrimoniam redolentia Ianuae annotâris Epigrammata, quae a pluribus postea ob acumen et elegantiam descripta sunt. Quod si pari aut etiam uberiore (ista enim obiter et inter aliud agendum subito concinnata erant) venâ nostram effigiem, cuius exemplar existimo me tibi antea misisse (ut tamen certo habeas, en alterum addidi) decorâris, rem inprimis acceptam feceris, teque ipsum in quibusdam meorum Operum Cithara Apollinea personantem, circa ipsum Frontispicium cognosces. Scripsi et hoc nomine Domino PETRO IUNIO, deque quibusdam alijs, in quibus et tuum expeto, sicubi opus fuerit, unâ cum consilio, auxilium. Si illi insuper Hortulanum et Aucupem primo vere mihi procurare commodum non fuerit, ut per te officij hoc mihi praestetur, peramanter rogo. Unicum insuper a te petam, quod et sponte mihi, cum hîc esses, pollicebaris, ut videlicet Dominum IOANNEM CRAIGIUM Medicinae Doctorem hancque Edenburgi facientem et Mathematum insuper excellenter peritum, quantum in te est (est autem quamplurimum) ad potiora promovere, eiusque rei familiari, tua apud Regiam Maiestatem intercessione, ut commode et bene degat, consulere, si modo id hactenus oblivione quadam praetermissum est, ne graveris. Dignus enim is mea sententia est, qui ametur, iuvetur, promoveatur. Quamvis enim ex omnibus Europaeis Mathematicis, ad quos noster liber secundus de recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis (quem hîc, ni fallor, vidisti) pervenit, is solus inventus est, qui contentis eiusdem nonnullis contradicere, mecumque de eisdem per literas disputare attentârit, tacentibus, imo de sua opinione prorsus cedentibus, eamque revocantibus, nostris non invite subscribendo, praecipuis peneque cunctis inter illos, quorum ego in eodem scripto Placita sub veritatis incudem revocaveram, deviationesque aperueram et modeste taxâram. Attamen tantum abest, ut id ullo pacto in ipso moleste feram, quod etiam hoc mihi apprime gratum sit, laeterque me in illo invenisse Antagonistam, qui ausus fuerit nostris nonnulla quocumque tandem ratiocinationis praetextu exponere. Sic enim veritas ipsa, expensis semotisque ijs, quae in contrarium adduci a quoquam plausibiliter poterint, rectius et plenius elucescit. Quemadmodum ex Apologia nostra, quam etiam (si recte memini) hîc inspexisti, illius argumentationes affatim diluente, non obscure liquet. Quam et magnificus Dominus IACOBUS CURTIUS, Caesareae Maiestatis atque Imperij Procancellarius, in literis ad me datis, quarum exemplum Domino PETRO IUNIO transmisi, tum etiam alij eruditi in Germania Mathematici, libere et lubenter approbârunt. Si itaque effeceris, ut vir ille sentiat meam commendationem apud te pondus habuisse, sibique emolumento fuisse, non tam illi quam mihi rem plurimum acceptam exhibueris, meque tibi vel hoc nomine, utut alias de te bene mereri satagentem, arctius devinxeris. Vale, plura enim non addam, ne te gravibus et varijs administrationis publicae munijs occupatum nimium interpellem.</p><p>Iterum atque iterum quam felicissime vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 13. Novembris Anno 1591.<br>TYCHO BRAHE</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOHANNES METELLUS.</b></p><p>It was most gratifying to me, Magnificent Master Johannes Metellus, that in accompanying your Most Serene King you did not think it a burden to visit me on this Island, and that on departing you inscribed upon my Gate those Epigrams that breathe an exquisite keenness of wit, which afterwards were copied out by many for their acuteness and elegance. But if you would adorn my likeness — a copy of which I think I sent you before (yet, that you may have it for certain, see, I have added another) — with a vein equal or even more abundant (for those [epigrams] were composed in passing, on the spur of the moment and amid other business), you would do a thing especially welcome to me, and you would recognise your very self, sounding forth on Apollo's lyre, around the very Frontispiece of certain of my Works. I have written on this account also to Master Peter Young, and concerning certain other matters, in which I seek your help too, wherever there may be need, together with your counsel. If, besides, it should not be convenient for him to procure me a Gardener and a Fowler by early spring, I most affectionately ask that this service be rendered me through you. One thing further I shall ask of you, which of your own accord you promised me when you were here: namely, that — so far as lies in your power (and it is very much) — you not be reluctant to advance Master John Craig, Doctor of Medicine, practising the same at Edinburgh and besides excellently skilled in the mathematical sciences, to better things, and to see to his domestic estate, by your intercession with the Royal Majesty, so that he may live comfortably and well — if only this has hitherto been passed over through some forgetfulness. For he is, in my judgement, worthy to be loved, helped, and advanced. For although, out of all the European mathematicians to whom my second book on the more recent Phenomena of the Aetherial World (which here, unless I am mistaken, you saw) has reached, he alone has been found who has attempted to contradict some of its contents and to dispute with me about them by letter — while the rest stayed silent, nay, wholly yielded their own opinion and recanted it, subscribing not unwillingly to mine, [I mean] the foremost and almost all among those whose Tenets I had in that same writing called back to the anvil of truth, whose deviations I had laid open and modestly censured — yet so far am I from taking this amiss in him in any way, that it is even highly pleasing to me, and I rejoice to have found in him an Antagonist who dared to set forth some objections to my views under whatever pretext of reasoning at last. For thus truth itself, once the things that anyone could plausibly bring against it have been weighed and set aside, shines forth more rightly and more fully. As is plainly evident — not obscurely — from my Apology, which too (if I rightly remember) you examined here, and which abundantly dissolves his argumentations. This [Apology], moreover, the magnificent Master Jacob Curtius, Vice-Chancellor of the Imperial Majesty and of the Empire, in a letter sent to me — a copy of which I forwarded to Master Peter Young — and then too other learned mathematicians in Germany, have freely and gladly approved. If therefore you bring it about that that man perceives my recommendation to have carried weight with you and to have been to his profit, you will have done a thing most welcome not so much to him as to me, and you will have bound me to you more closely, on this account too, however much I am otherwise eager to deserve well of you. Farewell — for I shall add no more, lest I interrupt you too much, occupied as you are with the weighty and various duties of public administration.</p><p>Again and again, may you fare most happily.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 13 November, the year 1591.<br>TYCHO BRAHE</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> writing from Uraniborg to <i>Johannes Metellus</i>. <b>Metadata discrepancy flagged:</b> the dataset's `recipient` field reads \"John Maitland,\" but the source heading (<i>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOANNEM METELLUM</i>) and the vocative (<i>Magnifice Domine IOANNES METELLE</i>) unambiguously address <b>Metellus</b>, not Maitland. The translation follows the source. <i>Iohannes Metellus</i> = <b>Jean Matal / Johannes Metellus</b> (c. 1520–1597), the Burgundian-born humanist and geographer settled at Cologne, who had visited Hven in the train of the Scottish king and there inscribed Latin epigrams on the gate of Uraniborg. (John Maitland of Thirlestane, James VI's Chancellor, was a genuine but <i>separate</i> Tycho correspondent; the dataset has evidently mislabelled this letter.)</li><li><b><i>Serenissimum Regem vestrum</b></i> — \"your Most Serene King\": <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who visited Uraniborg in the spring of 1590 while in Denmark for his marriage to Anne of Denmark; Metellus was in the royal party.</li><li><b><i>Petrus Iunius</b></i> — <b>Peter Young</b> (1544–1628), James VI's former tutor and his ambassador/envoy to Denmark; the intermediary through whom Tycho repeatedly worked on Scottish matters. The errand of procuring \"a Gardener and a Fowler\" (<i>Hortulanum et Aucupem</i>) reflects Tycho's recruitment of skilled servants for the Hven establishment.</li><li><b><i>Ioannes Craigius</b></i> — <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician to James VI, formerly Professor of Logic and Mathematics at Frankfurt an der Oder, then practising medicine at Edinburgh. From 1588 he was Tycho's lone correspondent-critic on the comet of 1577; Tycho's whole point here is to commend him for patronage despite — indeed <i>because of</i> — that dispute.</li><li><b><i>liber secundus de recentioribus Aetherei Mundi Phaenomenis</b></i> — the <b>second book of <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</b></i> (Uraniborg/Wandsbek, 1588), Tycho's treatise on the comet of 1577, in which (esp. the closing chapters) he reviewed and \"censured\" the <i>placita</i> (tenets) of earlier comet-writers. Rendered \"the more recent Phenomena of the Aetherial World.\"</li><li><b><i>sub veritatis incudem revocaveram</b></i> — \"called back to the anvil of truth\": a metalworking metaphor (the views re-forged/tested on truth's anvil); kept as image. <i>Placita</i> = \"Tenets / [settled] opinions\"; <i>deviationes</i> = \"deviations [from the truth]\"; <i>modeste taxâram</i> = \"I had modestly censured\" (Tycho's own hedge on the sharpness of his criticism — preserved).</li><li><b><i>Antagonistam</b></i> — \"an Antagonist\": the Greek-derived term kept; Tycho frames Craig positively as the only adversary willing to engage, by whose opposition \"truth itself … shines forth more rightly and more fully.\"</li><li><b><i>Apologia nostra</b></i> — \"my Apology\": Tycho's defence of the comet book against Craig's objections (the <i>Apologia</i> later worked into the dispute material; cf. Tycho's long 1595 answer to Craig). <i>illius argumentationes affatim diluente</i> = \"abundantly dissolving his argumentations\" — <i>diluere</i> in the sense of refuting/dissolving an argument.</li><li><b><i>Iacobus Curtius, Caesareae Maiestatis atque Imperij Procancellarius</b></i> — <b>Jacob Kurz (Curtius) von Senftenau</b> (d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor (here <i>Procancellarius</i>) under Rudolf II, an astronomically learned official and Tycho correspondent who had approved the <i>Apology</i>. <i>Procancellarius</i> rendered \"Vice-Chancellor.\"</li><li><b><i>Cithara Apollinea personantem, circa ipsum Frontispicium</b></i> — \"sounding forth on Apollo's lyre, around the very Frontispiece\": Tycho invites Metellus to compose verse to appear on the frontispiece engraving of his Works (the engraved author-portrait, <i>nostram effigiem</i>, of which he encloses a fresh copy). <i>Frontispicium</i> kept as \"Frontispiece.\"</li><li><b><i>Magnifice Domine … Magnificus Dominus</b></i> — the honorific <i>Magnificus</i> (\"Magnificent\") rendered as such for both Metellus and Curtius, per the dignity of the address. <i>Domino Petro Iunio</i> etc.: <i>Dominus</i> → \"Master,\" per protocol §5.5.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>Vale … Iterum atque iterum quam felicissime vale</i> = \"Farewell … Again and again, may you fare most happily\" (the doubled valediction; protocol §5.5). <i>Datae Uraniburgi</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>13. Novembris Anno 1591</i> — 13 November 1591, Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar). This agrees with the dataset `label` (\"1591. 13. november. Uraniborg\"); no in-letter date discrepancy. The header date is taken from the dateline as written.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Uraniburgum</i> → <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory-residence on the island of Hven (<i>hac Insula</i>, \"this Island\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the persons, the disputed work, and the James-VI-visit context are all corroborated in the internal corpus (Norlind 1970; <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i>; Dreyer). The single external caveat is documentary, not translational: the dataset's `recipient`=\"John Maitland\" is a mislabel — the addressee is <b>Metellus</b>, as the source heading and vocative state. That discrepancy is named here, not smoothed over.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.307-308 as page images (letter runs from 'TYCHO BRAHE AD IOANNEM METELLUM' through 'TYCHO BRAHE' at the top of p.308, followed immediately by the next letter to Craigius). Collated names (Petrus Iunius/Peter Young, Ioannes Craigius/John Craig at Edinburgh, Iacobus Curtius) and the date 'Uraniburgi 13. Novembris Anno 1591' against Dreyer; text matches verbatim, including the long sentence about Craig being the sole mathematician to dispute the second book of Aetherei Mundi Phaenomena. English tracks every clause accurately. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "John Maitland", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15920516001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920516001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Metellus to compose a poem to accompany Tycho's portrait in his forthcoming works, thanking him for epigrams inscribed on Hven's gate during a previous visit.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15911113001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15911114001", "d": "1591-11-14", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → John Craig — Uraniborg, 14 November 1591 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek code-switches and astronomical glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> Tycho's major polemical letter in the <b>Tycho–Craig comet controversy</b>, and a documentary nexus: to convince the Aristotelian Scot that the learned world has already conceded the comets are aethereal, Tycho <b>quotes verbatim, at length, five other letters</b> he had received — two excerpts from <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz von Senftenau)</b> (Prague, 28 June 1590), the whole Craig-paragraph of <b>Hájek's letter</b> (= his letter of 11 June 1590, here re-quoted with minor textual variants) plus a later Hájek concession (Prague, 29 Aug 1591), and three excerpts from <b>Magini</b> (to Gellius Sascerides, 8 July 1590; again to Gellius; and to Tycho himself, 13 Sept 1590 — <i>the very letter Tycho answers on 1 December 1590</i>). The embedded Hájek paragraph is rendered here <b>consistently with Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b>, following this witness's own wording where it diverges (noted in the Apparatus). The source uses <i>» «</i> guillemets for the quotations; OCR line-break splits inside the Curtius quote (<i>privile gium</i>, <i>d e Cometis</i>, <i>er uditis</i>, <i>eo s</i>, <i>contr a</i>, <i>vo lunt</i>) are reproduced verbatim in the source block and read as the obvious whole words in the translation. The Greek (<i>ἀμαθηματικός</i>, <i>ἄθεος</i>) and the glyph-plus-ending forms (<i>♂tis</i> = <i>Martis</i>, <i>♂tem</i> = <i>Martem</i>, <i>☉lem</i> = <i>Solem</i>) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"Uraniburgi 14. Novembris Anno 1591\"</i>) is <b>Old Style</b> (Danish Julian); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VII pp. 308–319</b> (running head \"TYCHO AD IOANNEM CRAIGUM 14 NOV. 1591\"). All five embedded quotations are present and correctly attributed (Curtius ×2, Hájek ×1 long + 1 short concession of 29 Aug 1591, Magini ×3); the dateline (p. 319) and the MS witness are confirmed — Dreyer prints the head-note <b><i>\"[Ex apographo in codice Vindobonensi lat. 9737 f]\"</b></i>, vindicating both the 9737f shelfmark and the apograph/copy characterisation. <b>One transcription slip, now resolved (2026-06-02):</b> in Magini's second excerpt the working transcription reads <i>\"pag. 218\"</i> for the correct <i>\"pag. 281\"</i> (a digit transposition). The figure is a cross-reference to Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei</i> (1588): the original 1588 marginal page-number <b>281</b> appears at exactly the <i>\"Cum igitur nunc constet Angulus BHK\"</i> passage (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV p. 233 / scan PDF p. 247). So <b>281 is correct</b>; the translation follows it.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ERUDITISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO IOANNI CRAIGO, PHILOSOPHO ARISTOTELICO ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI, EANDEMQUE EDENBURGI IN SCOTIA EXERCENTI, MATHEMATICO EXCELLENTI, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.</p><p>QUID cunctaris, mi CRAIGE, curque tam cito pedem referens nusquam amplius appares? Si caussae et viribus diffidis, veritatisque a nobis propugnatae moenia inexpugnabilia esse, iam tandem expertus es atque concedis, equidem id quod res ipsa exigit, quodque decet, admittis. At cur non id vel verbulo fateri lubet? Neque enim errata agnoscere revocareque usqueadeo inglorium est, quin homini, his per se non parum obnoxio, id conveniat, excusationemque mereatur, cum et a plurimis praestantibus atque apprime eruditis viris etiam in gravioribus negotijs idipsum iam diu multumque laudabiliter factitatum sit. Sin autem nondum omnia tela atque arma, quibus oppugnes, tibi extorta esse praesumis, clypeumque tuum adhuc satis validum nec imbellem esse tibi persuades, quare non denuo in arenam prodis? Nam et defatigatus aut fugitivus miles iterum nonnunquam certare audet. Age igitur, prome si quid residui habes, quo tuum ARISTOTELEM et te tuearis, dum Cometas coelo eliminare citra omnem experientiam iustasque rationes is annixus sit, Posteritatemque Philosophicam tot iam seculis hac falsa opinione imbuerit suisque naenijs dementârit, cuius fistulae sibilum tu patulis auribus tam lubenter recipiens, forte, quod ad hunc a teneris tripudiare (ut mos in Scholis plerumque est) assuefactus sis, illius lusu (si modo non ab hoc delusus) delectaris, et illi tibique nimium applaudis. Imo quod minus excusabile est, in Mathematicorum erudito pulvere simul strenue et industrie exercitatus, non vereris, contra ipsam Mathematicam certitudinem atque Observationibus indubijs superstructas Apodixes Geometricas, omni Herculis robore insuperabiliores figmenta, vel saltem meras coniecturas Peripateticorum, veritati fucum facientes, aut potius hanc offuscantes, quantum in te est, defendere. An hoc est Mathematicum agere et veritatis penetralia, seposito omni praeiudicio, demonstrative enucleare, eidemque compertae constanter assentiri? Facessat Ethnicus iste ἀμαθηματικός (utinam non unâ etiam ἄθεος) ARISTOTELES cum suis discipulis, qui saltem plausibilibus et sophisticis elenchis incrustata facile arripiunt, ijsque acquiescunt. Apud Mathematicos certe non plus fidei promerebitur, quam geometrice comprobârit. Idem de omnibus eius asseclis, sive per imprudentiam sive affectata quadam ignorantia, ipsi suffragantibus, non immerito censendum venit.</p><p>Apologiam nostram, qua tuis obiectionibus luculenter respondebam, te iamdudum accepisse, nullum apud me est dubium. Nam et eruditissimus Dn. D. ANDREAS CRAGIUS, Medicus et Physicae in Academia Hafniensi Professor, mihi e Scotia (quo cum serenissima Regina Sponsa eius atque adiunctae nobilitatis Archiatrum agens, quando illac classe Danica deduceretur) reversus significabat, te motae inter nos disputationis, huiusque meae Apologiae mentionem fecisse. Nihil tamen certi referre poterat, an nostris dilutionibus acquiesceres nec ne. Id saltem aiebat, te admodum (si modo non nimium) Aristoteleis placitis mancipatum, peneque omnia ab ipso prolata pro oraculis habere; quae credulitas tibi cum multis alijs Philosophiam profitentibus communis est. Satis itaque, imo plus quam satis, tibi temporis interea, utut alijs negotijs aut Medicinae faciendae occupato, relictum est, quo Apologiam hanc nostram evolveres, disquireres atque si quid contradictionem admittens offenderes, idipsum ulterius disquirendum in medium proponeres. Sesquialter enim annus, ni fallor, iam praeterlapsus est, ex quo hoc scriptum Apologeticum ad te in Scotiam misi, nihilque tam diutino exacto tempore responsi mihi relatum, utut navigatio inter haec regna satis frequens sit et post contractam affinitatem Regiam quam antea commodior et crebrior. Verumenimvero abijce latebras gressumque reflecte et ad nobis respondendum, dummodo quid supererit nervorum, vires recollige, quantumque potes, haec moenia tenta. Sin autem nihil amplius restat, quod in contrarium verisimiliter adduci posse confidis, idipsum saltem indica et ingenue fatere. Tradideram interea nobilissimo cuidam Iuveni Scoto (cuius mihi iam nomen excidit, Secretarium tamen Regis vestri intimum fuisse recordor) cum a Rege suo in Daniam amandatus me quoque hîc inviseret, descriptum nostrarum Observationum atque ratiocinationum de Cometa ultimo, in Martio Anni 1590 conspecto, quale illustrissimo Principi GUILHELMO Hassiae Landgravio concesseram. Atque insuper ab ipso petij, ut te quoque eius redderet participem. Quod et eum fecisse mihi persuadeo. Demonstrâram ibidem admodum expedita et certa ratione et hunc Cometam omnino coelestem fuisse, adeoque nulli sensibili Parallaxi obnoxium, quod et in pluribus alijs, imo omnibus hisce aliquot exactis annis apparentibus, affatim deprehendi. Forte hic ipsus postremo visus Cometes, de quo loquor, te ancipitem et certamen ulterius detrectantem reddidit, cum is tam in 90mo a Meridiano Horizontis gradu, ipso videlicet Verticali principaliore altior existens, et postmodum in Meridiano, infra Polum declivior, interlapsis plurimis horis, declinationem suam nullo minuto aliter variârit, quam motus proprij interea ratio e diurno collecta exigebat, quod nostris Instrumentis erat observatu tam facillimum quam etiam ab omni erroris suspicione semotum. Nec tot anfractus atque Maeandros, ad Parallaxeos insensibilitatem demonstrandam, quemadmodum Cometes anni 77, de quo inter nos mota est quaestio, efflagitabat. Quod si per incuriam aliquam aut ex oblivione eius, cui id concessi, scripti illius copia tibi nondum est facta, apud eundem Secretarium, qui paulo post serenissimi Regis vestri in Regnum suum reditum huc ablegatus erat, uti antea quoque monui, de hoc sciscitare, atque eius communicationem, quod et facile (uti spero) obtinebis, urge, postmodumque attente lege atque expende; et modo tibi hîc quoque aliquid suggeratur dubij, aperte et ingenue indica, mecumque de toto hoc negotio ulterius per literas agere ne supersedeto. Caussa autem, cur id eo enixius desiderem, est haec, quod cum tuas literas, quatenus nostrae de Cometa supradicto Anni 77 lucubrationi nonnulla opponebant, unâ cum nostra Apologetica responsione, quibusdam excellentibus et eruditis in Germania Mathematicis simul inspicienda dijudicandaque transmisissem, illis sane haec amica inter nos veritatis eliciendae confirmandaeque decertatio non displicuit nec indigna iudicabatur, quae publici iuris fieret, ut et alij negotij penetralia eo profundius (hinc allecti) introspicerent atque veritatis involucra commodius eruerent, quidque citra dubium sentiendum foret, rectius constituerent. Proposui itaque hortatu aliorum eam partem literarum tuarum, quae hac de re agit, unâ cum nostra Apologia, tomo secundo de hoc Cometa tractanti propediem subiungere. Quod tibi prius significandum censui, ut si quid in promptu esset, quod hîc ulterius inferre satageres, id mature expedire mihique mittere posses. Nam et hoc addere et sub trutinam vocare non negligam. Cedet id in nullum tuae existimationis aut nominis detrimentum, sed potius erit apud omnes eruditos et cordatos honorificum. Nam et praeceptorum authoritatem tueri atque ab opinione plausibiliter concepta non facile amoveri, sed quoad fieri potest, in ea persistere, generosi semper fuit animi, nec indecorum a candidis et aequis censoribus habitum. Quin et quidam sunt eruditorum in Germania virorum, qui te non serio, sed saltem ut alia et plura eliceres, hanc movisse disceptationem autumant. Inter quos, ut est praecipua dignitate atque authoritate conspicuus, si etiam in rebus Mathematicis scientia et iudicio plurimos alios antecellens, Magnificus et nobilissimus vir Dominus IACOBUS CURTIUS a Senfftenau, Caesareae Maiestatis ab arcanis consilijs atque Imperij Procancellarius pereminentissimus. Is ex quo Apologiam nostram clarissimo viro Domino THADDAeO HAGECIO Medico et Mathematico eximio, veteri meo amico, transmissam forte perlustrasset, posteaque Caesareae Maiestatis amplissimum Privilegium Operibus meis, quo indemnitati sumptuum consulatur, praefigendum mittens, unâque literas proprias ad me dare non gravatus, inter alia et huius nostrae controversiolae non obscure in ijs meminit, nulla tamen tui expressa mentione. Licet vero apographum earum literarum circa frontispicia primi tomi, de Stella Nova et quibusdam alijs luculenter agentis, certa de caussa et rogatu quorundam apposuerim, tamen et hîc eam portionem, quae hoc, de quo loquor, negotium concernit, annotare non pigebit, ut eo citius et certius eius gravissimum hac de re iudicium tibi innotescat. Sonant autem eius verba ex eâdem Epistola, Pragae 28. Iunij Anno 90 ad me datâ, in hunc modum: »De scriptis tuis, quid  in genere sentiam, testabitur Caesareae Maiestatis privile gium, quod hisce literis adiunctum tibi mitto, cuius  ita conscribendi ego Caesari author et porro, vera in  eo Caesarem testari, sponsor fui. In specie vero ea, quae d e Cometis posterioribus, quotquot ipse observasti, a  te tradita solidissimisque rationibus confirmata sunt, ta lia esse sentio, ut de ijs ne ambigi quidem amplius ab er uditis viris queat. Itaque non adducor, ut credam, eo s, qui hac in parte dubios sese adhuc ostendunt et contr a demonstratam veritatem ARISTOTELIS authoritati patrocinari videri vo lunt, id ita vere sentire. Sed hoc potius eos agere puto, ut, cum omnem moverint lapidem omniaque undique conquisiverint, quae Aristoteleam sententiam confirmare, tuam vero labefactare aut dubiam reddere videantur, ea tibi proponant, tanquam ad Lydium lapidem examinanda, adeoque caussam tibi praebeant, eruditionis tuae Sole, omnes omnino errorum tenebras non modo, sed vel levissimas nubeculas dissipandi exactissimamque harum rerum doctrinam posteritati perficiendi. Qui si hac mente tecum certant, nae ego illis quam maxime bene cupio, sin aliud agunt, pertinaciam quidem eorum odio dignam existimo. Id tamen, quod pertinacia sua in commune beneficium tibi extorserunt, ipsis valere, vel potius non valere iussis, amplector non secus, ac si bona id mente fecissent, de eoque Reipublicae literariae gratulor.«</p><p>Haec CURTIUS. Sed et quid de hypothesibus nostris neotericis idem praestantissimus vir senserit, siquidem tu eas quoque oppugnare non intentatum relinquis, unâ annotare non abs re fuerit. In eadem itaque epistola postmodum de his sic loquitur: »Ad ea, quae novis tuis hypothesibus hactenus obiecta sunt, solide mihi videris respondisse, neque ego quicquam in ijs absurdum, sed omnia invicem pulcherrime congruentia invenio; an vero hypotheses istae, id quod in Copernicianis desideratur, praestiturae sint, ut scilicet exacta siderum loca ad praeterita, praesentia et futura tempora nobis exhibeant, de eo tum demum iudicandum erit, ubi maius illud, quod moliris, opus in lucem prodierit. Neque enim dubito, quin in eo opere omnium aetatum, quotquot extant, exactiores observationes cum tuis, quas exactissimas esse certo mihi persuadeo, summa diligentia contuleris, indeque novam hanc Mundi Machinam, divino sane ingenio, extruxeris.«</p><p>Eruditissimus insuper et praestantissimus ille THADDAeUS, de quo dixi, eodem tempore mihi copiose scripserat atque suam de hac Apologia nostra censuram aperuerat, quaedamque alia pro se adversus tuas in eum animadversiones facientia addiderat, quae singula excerpta hîc quoque unâ, ut tibi constent, assignabo: »Quod ad Apologeticam responsionem tuam pertinet, hanc quoque perlegi diligentissime, eaque sane mirifice sum delectatus. Tam solide enim et tam luculente respondes, et obiectiones CRAIGIJ diluis, ut mirum sane videri possit, quid contra tam evidentia vel hiscere CRAIGIUS aut quivis alius possit. Quod si CRAIGIUS perget veritati contradicere in longe maiorem incurret reprehensionem apud veritatis amantes, quam quod ego in Nova Stella parum ex arte suo iudicio praestiterim. Sed sit ita, ut ille iudicat: ego certe illud parum multo gloriosius mihi esse duco, quam si manifeste veritati repugnarem. Primus ego, quid alij senserint, ignorans, libera voce et scripto protuli, Novam Stellam non fuisse Cometam, nec in elementari regione, sed in aethere: licet senserim et protulerim fieri posse, ut Cometae in utraque regione, puta elementari et aetherea, oriantur, qua de re aetas posterior rectius iudicabit. Primus etiam labefactavi opinionem ARISTOTELIS de Cometis, quam CRAIGIUS mordicus retinet, et pro ea velut pro aris et focis depugnat. Sunt, fateor, mea, quae tum attuli, admodum exilia, et, quoad doctrinam parallaxium, plurima in eis desiderari. Sed et haec deinceps aliquantulum recognovi. Tu vero his supremam manum imposuisti. Quamobrem tibi libenter arena cedo. Mihi interea apud bonos et probatos ad veniam, non dico ad gratiam, promerendam sufficiet, quod in negocio profecto difficili (quod nemo unquam, quod sciam, aggredi, nec ab opinione ARISTOTELIS discedere, metu obtrectatorum et invidorum, quibus vix unquam vir bonus ac doctus et veritatis magis quam amicitiae studiosus satisfecerit, ausus est) pro virili laborare studuerim, primusque, ut dixi, in hoc angulo hanc glaciem secuerim, et in incognita via novas orbitas impresserim. Utinam vero CRAIGIUS mea illa exilia saltem umbra veritatis evincere potuisset, habuissem illi gratiam. Sed cum non potuerit, nec poterit unquam, mirum sane non est, quod tua firmissima solidissimaque ne minima quidem in parte labefactârit. Hoc unum egisse video, ut postposita veritate opinionem ARISTOTELIS sartam tectam conservaret constabiliretque. Verum stat pro nobis veritas, licet adhuc deserta et pauper, nec fulta splendido aliquo patrocinio vel veterum vel recentium Philosophorum, efferet tamen suos radios deinceps clarius splendescetque, quantumvis nunc frivolis quibusdam obiectionibus et sophisticis ratiunculis obtenebretur. Certe si quis meliora nostris, quae vel de Nova Stella vel de Cometis protulimus, et abs te solidius multo prolata sunt, afferet, libenter amplectemur. Verum qui nec meliora affert, nec solide aliena refutat, sed tantum certandi studio contradicit, an is candidi et aequi aestimatoris partes agat, alijs dijudicandum relinquo. Miror, cur CRAIGIUS miscet meam Novam Stellam cum meo Cometa, cum egomet ingenue fassus sim, certitudinem mearum observationum Cometae non posse certare cum certitudine observationum Novae Stellae, neque mihi fuisse ad manum ea instrumenta, neque eas commoditates observandi Cometam, quas habui in Nova Stella. Nosti etiam memet et in Nova Stella et in Cometa, agnita veritate, partim sponte correxisse quaedam, partim abs te et a PRAeTORIO alijsque de quibusdam amice monitum nonnulla emendasse. Magis sane miror, CRAIGIUM illam mutationem sententiae in melius, quasi probro, incertitudini et inconstantiae adscribere, quae virtuti et ingenuitati adiudicanda fuit. Turpe enim non errorem, agnita veritate, corrigere. Sed errorem contra veritatem hominis in gratiam tueri, id vero est turpissimum. Quasi vero ipsa experientia non testetur, priores dies studiorum discipulos posteriorum esse solere. Sed vides, quid non audeant isti, qui in verba sui magistri iurârunt. Omnes etiam Mathematicos, qui Cometas aethereos esse asseruerunt, absurditatis arguit, non animadvertens, dum opinionem ARISTOTELIS mordicus tenere et tueri conatur, nec nostra evertere potest, seipsum in longe maiores absurditates implicare, quemadmodum abs te eruditissime evincitur. Quid, quod magna audacia et quidem immerito accusat praeclarum artificem REGIOMONTANUM, quod huc usque nec ab illo nec ab ullo Mathematicorum fuerit demonstratum, quomodo Cometarum remotio a Terra definienda sit, quando nimirum proprio incedunt gressu, et proptere a REGIOMONTANI demonstrationem abire in absurdum? Atqui, optime TYCHO, CRAIGIUS hac in re crasse fallitur, magnaque iniuria afficit optimum artificem REGIOMONTANUM, qui in suo illo praeclaro de Cometa libello problema 2.3.4. diserte monet, in indagatione parallaxeos proprij Cometae motus rationem habendam esse, quemadmodum abs te quoque eius motus ratio habita est. Quare dum ea CRAIGIUS omnibus Mathematicis imputat, tibi quoque non levem facit iniuriam. Ego quidem in eo Cometa et in altero quoque eius motum proprium me neglexisse scientem volentemque, lubens fateor. Sed ne ex eius neglectione notabilis et minime ferendus error exurgeret, meas observationes parvulo interiecto tempore institui, in quo non admodum sensibilis variatio motus proprij Cometae fieret: deinde iudicavi, satis esse, consistere in mediocritate et saltem propius collimare ad veritatem, quam ab alijs hucusque factum est: quemadmodum praeclare dixit REGIOMONTANUS: thesauros naturae nos non evacuare, sed in plerisque scibilibus ipsi veritati propinquum degustandum mortalibus concedi: quasi diceret, satis esse in eo gradu subsistere, qui veritatem penitus non evertat. Haec pro defensione REGIOMONTANI, adeoque veritatis, dicere volui. Neque tamen eo inficias, quin melior aliqua ratio vel abs te, vel ab alijs inveniri possit. Interea certitudinem demostrationum REGIOMONTANI firmam mansuram esse, libere pronuncio, quam neque CRAIGIUS, nec quispiam alius ullius absurditatis convincere poterit. Subolfecit sane REGIOMONTANUS quiddam aliud de Cometis, quam quod scholae ex ARISTOTELE publice docerent. Sed propter eius authoritem non ausus est a recepta sententia discedere: qui si nunc viveret vidissetque Novam Stellam, sine dubio erubuisset, et accuratiores Cometarum considerationes adhibuisset, nec ullum dubium est apud me, quin praeposita veritate nostrae sententiae subscripsisset. Sed quid ego de his apud te, qui haec omnia multo solidius pertractaveris ac CRAIGIJ rationes et obiectiones omnes ita dilueris, ut quid illis amplius ad asserendam veritatem adijci possit, non videam.«</p><p>Haec fuit optimi illius THADDAeI de nostra controversia censura. Postquam vero illi ad quaedam dubia in eadem Epistola mota et replicata (circa hypotheses praesertim motuum Cometarum, quas ille non posse constitui tunc existimavit, utut coelestes esse nequaquam inficiatus sit) accurate respondissem, is pro ea qua est animi ingenuitate et candore eximie decoratus, libenter suis quibusdam opinionibus, re rectius perspectâ, cessit, atque nostrae sententiae de toto hoc Cometico (ut sic loquar) negotio omnimodo subscripsit, quod ultimae ipsius ad me literae Praga die 29. Augusti, hoc ipso anno 1591 missae, testantur, dum sic habent: »Etsi vero aliquod otium nunc habuissem, non tamen aliquid nostrae illi disputationi adijcere potuissem vel nunc quoque possem; adeo exacte omnia a te excussa, examinata, meaque argumenta diluta sunt, ut me veritate et evidentia demonstrationum victum fateri necesse sit, nisi manifestae veritati repugnare velim.«</p><p>SCULTETUM quoque suarum de Cometa allegato conceptionum paenituisse, easque pro invalidis reputare, vel ijs pauculis ipsius verbis, ex autographo eiusdem excerptis, quas Apologiae inserui, cognoscere liquet. De NOLTHIO non habeo quod dicam. Is enim mihi ignotus est. Nec tamen dubito, quin pro ea, qua pollet Mathematum peritia, dextre et candide nostras animadversiones sit accepturus, seque deviasse, quod et in priore Tomo circa Stellam Novam plenius demonstro, aperte confessurus.</p><p>De caeteris in Germania eruditis et iudicio valentibus Mathematicis idem mihi polliceor. Quin et, ut cognoscas excellentia illa Italorum ingenia non secus nostris assertionibus acquievisse easque omnimodo approbasse, excerpam etiam quaedam ex clarissimi atque excellentissimi illius Mathematici IOANNIS MAGINI (cuius celebre nomen proximis annis, ob Tabulas Astronomicas prompta facilitate resolutas et copiose locupletatas, ac Ephemerides ex his subtiliter et luculenter derivatas, undiquaque innotuit) literis, tum ad GELLIUM SASCERIDEM, quondam mihi domesticum et Astronomica atque Medica hîc exercentem, nunc vero in Italia Medicinae studio ulterius operam navantem, perscriptis. Ex quibus facile cognosces, quid magnus ille MAGINUS (ut unum prae multis sumam, consentientibus tamen et aliorum illic Mathematicorum, ut ipsemet innuit, suffragijs) senserit, qui primo in Epistola die 8. Iulij Anno 1590 ad dictum GELLIUM Bononiâ transmissa sic scriptum reliquit: »Ego in TYCHONIS libro perlegendo nihil reperi, quod non maxima laude dignum mihi videretur, communicavique cum amicis eiusdem scientiae professoribus, qui mecum idem sentiunt. Neque gravabor ad ipsum scribere, et officiosissime gratias ipsi de liberali dono ac de praeclaro suo de me iudicio agere, quas maximas potuero. Quocum etiam conferre voluero nonnulla, quae ad motuum attinent correctionem. Pollicitatio enim illa emendandorum motuum, in quibus hucusque insignes aliorum lapsus contigisse affirmat, me, ut verum fatear, conturbavit, non quin ego desiderem eos restitui, sed quoniam id quamprimum fieri desiderem, si non alia, saltem mea ipsius causa.«</p><p>Postea cum quaedam ex nostris animadversionibus circa negotium Astronomicum sibi, intercedente eodem GELLIO, a me communicari petijsset, haec subiungit: »Observavi praeterea in eius libro, quod mihi magnam iniecit cupiditatem rei penitus cognoscendae, qua scilicet Methodo alios Angulos atque alterum latus Trianguli obliquanguli venari potest, uno scilicet tantum angulo dato cum duobus lateribus circa ipsum. TYCHO enim ipse id inveniri posse absque perpendiculari ac magna quidem facilitate investigari significat pag. 281 inquiens: Cum igitur nunc constet angulus BHK etc. Ubi MESTHLINUM quoque, sed modeste, perstringit, qui viam in hac re communem sit sequutus. Equidem primum quoque TYCHONIS nostri librum cupidissime expecto, neque maius beneficium consequi me posse fateor a vobis, quam si statim ac prodierit, compos ipsius fiam« etc.</p><p>Ex quibus patet, MAGINUM non oscitanter hunc secundum librum perspexisse, et eius contentis haud gravatim subscripsisse. Idem quoque ex literis eius postmodum Idibus Septembris eiusdem anni ad me ipsum Bononiâ datis haud obscure colligitur, quas cum sic exorsus esset: »Accepi librum tuum eruditissimum ad me tuo nomine a GELLIO SASCERIDE transmissum, quo mihi abs te nihil gratius exhiberi potuit, cum eiusmodi scripta curiosissime conquirere soleam, ac libentissime perlegere; vix enim alia via sperassem eum ad manus meas perventurum, cum perpauca istic excusa volumina soleant in Italiam importari, propter longinquitatem itineris ac vecturae difficultatem; eo accedit, quod elegantissimo hoc munere, quanti me faceres, praeclare significasti, quo quidem nomine tibi gratias ago quas possum maximas« et postquam quaedam alia intermiscuisset seque, quod prius mihi non scripsisset, excusasset, et nonnulla, de quibus etiam ad GELLIUM antea mentionem fecerat, a me circa restitutionem Astronomicam expetivisset, in hunc modum proloquitur: »Meum vero sensum ac iudicium de tuo illo praeclaro labore circa Cometam anno 1577 conspectum ne desideres, sic habeto, vir clarissime: cum enim in credibilem in eo diligentiam tuam et accuratam observandi viam perspexissem, mihi persuadeo fore, ut coelestium corporum motus emendare exactissime possis, fructuque operae illius maximo omnium studio atque expectationi cumulatissime satisfacias, unde nomini tuo sempiternum decus adiungatur. Cuperem tamen te in motu Martis observando et examinando maxime incumbere, cum eum observari non posse exacte vulgo persuasum sit.«</p><p>Deinde quaedam de ♂tis eccentricitatibus castigandis pro suo arbitrio intertexens ita literas continuat: »Non possum non magnopere probare Systema universi a te excogitatum, quamvis cuperem Solis orbem ac Martis nequaquam sese intersecare, quod si, ut a GELLIO cognovi, Martem acronychum Terrae propius accedere quam ☉lem a te est observatum, intersectio huiusmodi omnino admittenda est. In magna versor expectatione tuarum huiusmodi observationum et speculationum, quas et probare et sequi minime erubescam, etsi in construendis Ephemeridibus resolutisque tabulis a Coperniana ratione et Tabulis Prutenicis ne latum quidem unguem deflexi. Spero me etiam, quod vehementer cupio, primum tuum de Mundi aetherei Phaenomenis librum, quem te editurum polliceris, hoc anno lecturum.«</p><p>Mox interiectis quibusdam alijs circa finem haec habet: »Hoc anno interpretaturus sum meas coelestium orbium hypotyposes, quae mihi bona erit occasio, quae in hoc genere a me edita sunt, longe uberius explicare (ut iam facere aggressus sum) et Commentarijs ac Geometricis Demonstrationibus supputationibusque illustrare. Quae tamen Commentaria priusquam publici iuris efficiantur, libenter ego lucubrationes tuas cognoscere et intelligere velim, ut tuas quoque suppositiones ijs inserere et applicare possem.«</p><p>Sed plura ex his atque similibus non addam. Hinc tibi satis perspicuum evadit, mi CRAIGE, Ausoniae Philosophos, inprimis vero eminentissimum illum inclyti Gymnasij Bononiensis Mathematum Professorem MAGINUM, ea, quae liber noster secundus complectitur, quo ad Cometae sedes aethereas, quod potissimum illic demonstrare laboro, illumque insuper Neotericam nostram Hypothesium coelestium ordinationem plaudenter acceptasse, quorum tu tamen utrumque non saltem in dubium vocas, sed et, quantum in te est, labefactare anniteris. Sunt certe Italorum ingenia sublimia et acuta, iudicijque gravi dexteritate praepollentia atque pro maiori parte Aristotelaeis Axiomatibus, non minus quam vos in Scotia, astricti. Pro quibus etiam et firmiter starent atque fortiter pugnarent, si non observationum Astronomicarum certitudine et Mathematicis Demonstrationibus pleraque redargui animadverterent. Sed et unicum addam, ut conscius sis. Ex quo hic ipse MAGINUS ad ♂tisapparentias sedulo denotandas adhortatur, quod et alias plurimis ab hinc annis non infrugifere (uti spero) a me praestitum est, ego inter illi rescribendum, ipsum et GELLIUM rogavi, ut ad ♂tem hac elapsa aestate, mense Iunio acronychum existentem, diligenter illic attenderent, eo quod ibi circa Meridianum altior quam hîc tunc existens minus Refractionibus esset obnoxius. Cumque unâ GELLIO scripsissem, ut Instrumentum quoddam, instar meorum, quae hîc vidit et tractavit, pro his atque alijs quibusdam Observationibus illic in Italia coelitus demetiendis confieri curaret, venit non ita dudum post Patavium MAGINUS, et tam in elaborando quam utendo diligentem, quoad hoc Instrumentum, navavit operam, et inter alia ♂tem aliquoties in acronychio, de quo dixi, situ, ibi hoc intermediante observârunt, quas postmodum denotationes mihi transmissas cum nostris eodem tempore in hoc sidere hîc habitis contuli, et vix differentiam unius vel alterius scrupuli deprehendi. Quod tibi indicandum duxi, licet nonnihil sit extra propositum. Tuum itaque erit ad veritatem ipsam, seposito omni affectu atque praeoccupata sententia, etiam cordate aspirare, hancque solam inquirere, sectari, amplecti, posthabitisque omnibus devijs, libere eandem profiteri. Non tamen est, quod existimes, me ullo modo moveri aut moleste ferre, quod tu te nostris assertionibus in hac materia antagonistam praebueris, quod sane solus, quantum hactenus rescire licuit, prae omnibus Italiae, Galliae et Germaniae Mathematicis (quod et laudo) aggredi ausus es. Imo rem potius mihi hoc nomine admodum acceptam, et eruditis viris nequaquam (uti vides) displicentem, praestitisti. Et hac ratione ad certitudinis intemeratae plenius extricandos patefaciendosque recessus occasionem non importunam exhibuisti. Omnino autem me nihilominus de te bene sentire atque amicum erga te gerere animum, vel ex hoc solo non obscure perspicies, quod Serenissimo Regi tuo, cum coram me in hac Insula invisere ante biennium in Dania aliquandiu apud Sponsam ob hyemis impedimenta commoratus, dignaretur, te sedulo et impense ipsius Maiestati commendârim atque ad meliora promovendo, qua potui intercessione, submisse et reverenter pro te rogârim, audientibus Magnifico Domino Cancellario et illustri Comite Domino KIETZIO, apud quos, ut pollicerentur, quod de eodem Regem ad suos reversum commonefacere vellent, simul obtinui, et si quid ob negotiorum, uti fit, incidentia avocamenta et mundanarum rerum irrequietudines hac in parte interea praetermissum est, nuper Dominum Cancellarium per literas (quarum hîc tenorem habes) sui promissi denuo fideliter admonui, nec dubito, quin is et alij spectatae nobilitatis atque in aula authoritate valentes viri, mea hac qualicunque commendatione excitati, tibi et tuis rebus bene cupere, easque ulterius provehere elaborabunt. Tu interim mihi saepius scribere, deque rebus Mathematicis, quae nostra est voluptas, insuper mecum conferre nullatenus intermitte. Erit id mihi apprime acceptum meque ad respondendum haud difficilem invenies.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 14. Novembris Anno 1591.<br>TYCHO BRAHE</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most erudite man, Master John Craig, Aristotelian Philosopher and Doctor of Medicine — practising the same at Edinburgh in Scotland — excellent Mathematician, his much-beloved friend.</b></p><p>Why do you delay, my <b>Craig</b>, and why, drawing back your foot so quickly, do you nowhere appear any longer? If you distrust your case and your forces, and have now at last found by experience, and concede, that the walls of the truth defended by us are impregnable, then indeed you admit what the matter itself requires and what is fitting. But why does it not please [you] to confess this even by a little word? For to acknowledge and retract errors is not so inglorious but that it befits a man (in himself not a little liable to these) and merits excuse — since this very thing has now long and greatly been done, in laudable fashion, by very many outstanding and especially erudite men, even in graver matters. But if you presume that not yet have all the darts and arms with which you might assail [me] been wrested from you, and persuade yourself that your shield is still valid enough and not unwarlike, why do you not come forth into the <b>arena</b> again? For even a wearied or fugitive soldier sometimes dares to fight again. Come then, bring forth, if you have anything left, with which to defend your <b>Aristotle</b> and yourself — [Aristotle], who strove to banish the comets from the heaven without any experience and just reasons, and has now for so many centuries imbued Philosophical Posterity with this false opinion and driven [it] mad with his dirges; the whistle of whose pipe you, receiving so gladly with open ears — perhaps because you have been accustomed from your tender [years] to dance to it (as is mostly the custom in the Schools) — delight in his play (if only you are not deluded by him), and applaud both him and yourself too much. Nay — what is less excusable — having been trained at the same time strenuously and industriously in the erudite dust of the Mathematicians, you do not fear to defend, against Mathematical certainty itself and the <b>Geometrical demonstrations</b> [<i>Apodixes</i>] built upon undoubted <b>Observations</b> (figments more insuperable than all the strength of Hercules), or at least the mere conjectures of the <b>Peripatetics</b>, which counterfeit the truth, or rather obscure it, as much as in you lies. Is this to act the Mathematician, and to unfold demonstratively the inner chambers of truth, all prejudice set aside, and constantly to assent to it once found? Away with that heathen, that <b>unmathematical</b> [<i>ἀμαθηματικός</i>] (would that not at the same time also <b>godless</b> [<i>ἄθεος</i>]) Aristotle, with his disciples, who easily snatch up [things] merely crusted over with plausible and sophistical refutations, and rest in them. Among Mathematicians, certainly, he will earn no more credit than he shall have proved geometrically. The same must, not undeservedly, be judged about all his followers, whether through imprudence or through a certain affected ignorance, voting for him.</p><p>That you have long since received our <b>Apology</b> [<i>Apologia</i>], in which I amply replied to your objections, I have no doubt. For the most erudite <b>Master Andreas Cragius</b> [Anders Krag], Physician and Professor of Physics in the University of Copenhagen, returned to me from Scotland (whither he had gone, acting as <b>chief physician</b> [<i>Archiater</i>] to the most serene Queen-Bride and the nobility attached [to her], when she was conveyed thither by the Danish fleet), signified that you had made mention of the disputation raised between us, and of this my Apology. He could, however, report nothing certain — whether you acquiesced in our dissolutions [of your objections] or not. He said at least that you are quite (if not too) enslaved to the Aristotelian tenets, and hold almost everything brought forward by him for oracles — a credulity you share with many others who profess Philosophy. Enough time, therefore — nay, more than enough — has meanwhile been left you, occupied though you are with other business and with practising Medicine, in which to unroll this our Apology, to examine [it], and, if you found anything admitting of contradiction, to propose that very thing in the open for further examination. For a year and a half, unless I am mistaken, has now elapsed since I sent this Apologetic writing to you in Scotland, and in so long a time passed no reply has been brought back to me — although the navigation between these kingdoms is frequent enough, and, since the Royal kinship was contracted, more convenient and more frequent than before. But cast off the hiding-places, turn back your step, and, to reply to us, gather your forces — provided any sinews remain — and, as much as you can, assail these walls. But if nothing more remains which you trust can plausibly be adduced to the contrary, at least indicate this and confess [it] frankly. I had meanwhile delivered to a certain most noble young <b>Scot</b> (whose name has now slipped from me, though I recall that he was the intimate <b>Secretary</b> of your King), when, sent off by his King into Denmark, he visited me here too, a copy of our <b>Observations and reasonings about the last Comet, seen in March of the Year 1590</b>, such as I had granted to the most illustrious Prince <b>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse</b>. And besides, I asked of him that he render you too a sharer of it. Which I am persuaded he did. I had demonstrated there, by a very expeditious and certain method, both that this Comet was altogether <b>celestial</b>, and so subject to no sensible <b>Parallax</b> — which I have also abundantly found in several others, nay in all of those appearing in these some years past. Perhaps this very last-seen Comet of which I speak rendered you doubtful and shrinking from further contest, since it — being so much higher in the 90th degree from the Meridian of the Horizon (namely at the principal <b>Vertical</b>), and afterwards in the Meridian, lower beneath the Pole, very many hours having elapsed — varied its <b>declination</b> by no minute otherwise than the reckoning of its proper motion, meanwhile gathered from the diurnal [motion], required: which with our Instruments was as very easy to observe as it was also removed from all suspicion of error. Nor did it demand so many windings and <b>Maeanders</b> [meanderings], for demonstrating the <b>imperceptibility of parallax</b>, as the <b>Comet of [15]77</b> — about which the question was raised between us — demanded. But if, through some carelessness or forgetfulness of him to whom I granted it, a copy of that writing has not yet been made [available] to you, inquire about this from the same Secretary (who was sent here a little after the return of your most serene King into his Kingdom), as I advised before too, and press for its communication (which, I hope, you will easily obtain); and afterwards read [it] attentively and weigh [it]; and if any doubt be here too suggested to you, indicate [it] openly and frankly, and do not forbear to deal with me further by letter about this whole business. The reason why I desire this the more earnestly is this: that, when I had transmitted your letter — in so far as it opposed some [things] to our lucubration about the aforesaid <b>Comet of [15]77</b> — together with our Apologetic response, to certain excellent and erudite <b>Mathematicians in Germany</b>, to be inspected and judged at the same time, this friendly contest between us, for eliciting and confirming the truth, by no means displeased them, nor was judged unworthy to be made public, so that others too might (drawn hence) look more deeply into the inner chambers of the business, and more conveniently dig out the wrappings of truth, and more rightly determine what should be thought beyond doubt. I have therefore proposed, at the exhortation of others, to subjoin shortly that part of your letter which deals with this matter, together with our Apology, to the <b>second volume</b> treating of this Comet. Which I judged should first be signified to you, so that, if anything were ready to hand which you should be eager to bring further here, you might prepare it in good time and send [it] to me. For I shall not neglect to add this too and call [it] to the balance. It will tend to no detriment of your reputation or name, but rather will be honourable among all the erudite and sensible. For to defend the authority of one's teachers, and not easily to be moved from an opinion plausibly conceived, but, as far as can be done, to persist in it, has always been [the mark] of a generous mind, and has been held not unbecoming by candid and fair censors. Nay, there are certain of the erudite men in Germany who suppose that you raised this dispute not seriously, but only that you might elicit other and more [things]. Among whom — as he is conspicuous for pre-eminent dignity and authority, [and] also excelling very many others in knowledge and judgment in Mathematical matters — [is] the Magnificent and most noble man, Lord <b>Jacob Curtius of Senftenau</b> [Jakob Kurz von Senftenau], most eminent privy Councillor of the Imperial Majesty and <b>Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b>. He, since he had by chance perused our Apology, transmitted to the most distinguished man Lord <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> [Tadeáš Hájek], eminent Physician and Mathematician, my old friend — and afterwards, sending the Imperial Majesty's most ample <b>Privilege</b>, to be prefixed to my Works (whereby provision is made for the indemnity of [my] expenses), and not declining at the same time to give me a letter of his own — among other [things] mentioned, not obscurely, this our little controversy too in it, yet with no express mention of you. And although I have appended a transcript of that letter about the title-pages of the first volume (which treats amply of the New Star and certain other [matters]), for a certain reason and at the request of certain [persons], yet here too it will not irk [me] to note that portion which concerns this business of which I speak, so that the more quickly and surely his most weighty judgment about this matter may become known to you. His words, from the same Letter — given to me at <b>Prague, 28 June in the Year [15]90</b> — run in this manner: <i>\"About your writings, what I think in general, the Imperial Majesty's <b>privilege</b> — which, attached to this letter, I send you — will testify; of whose being so drawn up I was the author to the Emperor, and, further, the surety that the Emperor testifies true [things] in it. But in particular, those [things] which <b>about the later Comets</b> (as many as you yourself observed) have been handed down by you and confirmed by the most solid reasons, I judge to be such that they cannot even be doubted any longer by erudite men. And so I am not brought to believe that those who in this regard still show themselves doubtful, and wish to seem to plead for the authority of <b>Aristotle</b> against the demonstrated truth, truly think so. But I rather think they do this: that, when they have moved every stone and sought out from every side whatever may seem to confirm the Aristotelian opinion and to shake or render doubtful yours, they may propose these things to you, as [things] to be examined at the <b>Lydian touchstone</b>, and so may furnish you occasion of dissipating, by the Sun of your erudition, not only all the shadows of errors, but even the lightest little clouds, and of perfecting for posterity the most exact doctrine of these matters. Who, if they contend with you with this mind, truly I wish them very well indeed; but if they do otherwise, I deem their obstinacy worthy of hatred. Yet that which by their obstinacy they have wrung out for you, to the common benefit — they being bidden farewell, or rather not-farewell — I embrace no otherwise than if they had done it with a good mind, and for it I congratulate the Republic of Letters.\"</i></p><p>Thus <b>Curtius</b>. But what the same most excellent man thought about our <b>neoteric hypotheses</b> — since you leave these too unattempted [by your] attacks — it will not be amiss to note at the same time. In the same letter, therefore, he afterwards speaks thus about these: <i>\"To those [things] which have hitherto been objected to your new hypotheses, you seem to me to have replied solidly; nor do I find anything absurd in them, but everything most beautifully congruent with one another; but whether these hypotheses will deliver that which is wanting in the Copernican [ones] — namely, that they exhibit to us the exact places of the stars for past, present, and future times — about that it will be [time] to judge only then, when that greater work which you are attempting shall have come into the light. For I do not doubt but that in that work you will have collated, with the greatest diligence, the more exact observations of all ages, as many as are extant, with yours (which I am surely persuaded are the most exact), and thence will have built up this new <b>Machine of the World</b>, by a truly divine genius.\"</i></p><p>Moreover that most erudite and excellent <b>Thaddaeus</b>, of whom I spoke, had at the same time written to me copiously and opened his appraisal of this our Apology, and had added certain other [things] making for himself against your animadversions upon him — which several excerpts I will here too set down at once, that they may be established for you: <i>\"As for your <b>Apologetic response</b>, I have read this too most diligently, and I was wondrously delighted with it. For you reply so solidly and so amply, and dissolve the objections of Craig, that it can truly seem a wonder what Craig — or anyone else — could even mutter against things so evident. And if Craig shall persist in <b>contradicting</b> the truth, he will incur a far greater reproach among the lovers of truth than [I incurred], in [the matter of] the New Star, by having performed too little, in his judgment, 'by [the rules of] art.' But be it as he judges: I, certainly, hold that 'too little' to be far more glorious to me than if I manifestly fought against the truth. I was the first — ignorant of what others had thought — who brought forward, with free voice and writing, that the New Star was not a Comet, nor in the elementary region, but in the aether: though I thought and brought forward that it can happen that comets arise in either region — namely the elementary and the aethereal — about which the later age will judge more rightly. I was also the first to shake the opinion of Aristotle about comets, which Craig retains tooth-and-nail, and fights for it as if for altar and hearth. They are, I confess, mine — the things I then brought forward — very slight, and, as to the doctrine of parallaxes, very many things are wanting in them. But these too I afterwards somewhat revised. You, however, have put the finishing hand to them. Wherefore I gladly yield you the field. To me, meanwhile, it will suffice — among the good and approved, for earning pardon (I do not say favour) — that, in a business assuredly difficult (which no one ever, so far as I know, dared to attempt, nor to depart from Aristotle's opinion, from fear of detractors and the envious, whom scarcely ever has a good and learned man, more zealous for truth than for friendship, satisfied), I strove to labour to the best of my power, and was the first (as I said) in this corner to cut this ice, and to impress new tracks on an unknown road. But would that Craig had been able to overthrow those slight [contributions] of mine at least by a shadow of truth — I should have held him grateful. But since he could not, nor ever will be able, it is truly no wonder that he has not shaken your most firm and most solid [arguments] even in the least part. This one thing I see him to have done: that, the truth set aside, he might preserve and establish the opinion of Aristotle safe and sound. But the truth stands for us — though still deserted and poor, and not propped by any splendid patronage of the old or recent Philosophers; yet it will hereafter cast forth its rays more clearly and shine, however much it be now overshadowed by certain frivolous objections and sophistical little reasonings. Surely, if anyone shall bring forward better [things] than ours — which we have brought forward whether about the New Star or about Comets, and [which] have been brought forward far more solidly by you — we shall gladly embrace [them]. But he who neither brings better, nor solidly refutes another's, but only contradicts from a zeal <b>for contending</b> — whether he plays the part of a candid and fair appraiser, I leave to others to judge. I marvel why Craig mixes my New Star with my Comet, since I myself frankly confessed that the certainty of my observations of the Comet cannot vie with the certainty of the observations of the New Star, and that I had neither those instruments at hand, nor those conveniences for observing the Comet, which I had in [the matter of] the New Star. You know too that I myself, both in the New Star and in the Comet — the truth being acknowledged — partly corrected certain things of my own accord, partly emended some, amicably advised about certain [things] by you and by <b>Praetorius</b> and others. I marvel the more that Craig ascribes that change of opinion for the better — as if [it were] a reproach — to uncertainty and inconstancy, [a change] which ought to have been <b>adjudged</b> to virtue and ingenuousness. For it is not shameful to correct an error when the truth is acknowledged. But to defend an error against the truth, to please a man, is indeed most shameful. As if experience itself did not testify that earlier days are wont to be the disciples of later ones! But you see what those men do not dare, who have sworn upon the words of their master. He also accuses of absurdity all the Mathematicians who have asserted comets to be aethereal — not noticing that, while he tries to hold and defend Aristotle's opinion tooth-and-nail, and cannot overthrow ours, he himself becomes entangled in far greater absurdities, as is most learnedly proven by you. What [of this], that with great audacity, and indeed <b>undeservedly</b>, he accuses the splendid artificer <b>Regiomontanus</b> — [claiming] that hitherto it has been demonstrated neither by him nor by any of the Mathematicians how the distance of comets from the Earth is to be defined, since, namely, they advance with their own [proper] step, and that therefore Regiomontanus's demonstration goes off into absurdity? But, best Tycho, Craig in this matter is <b>grossly</b> deceived, and does great injury to the excellent artificer Regiomontanus, who, in that splendid little book of his on the Comet, in problems 2, 3, [and] 4, expressly advises that, in the investigation of parallax, account must be taken of the comet's own [proper] motion — as the reckoning of its motion was also had by you. Wherefore, while Craig <b>imputes</b> these [things] to all Mathematicians, he does no light injury to you too. I, indeed, freely confess that in that comet, and in the other one too, I knowingly and willingly neglected its proper motion. But lest from that neglect a notable and by-no-means-tolerable error should arise, I instituted my observations with a tiny interval of time interposed, in which no very perceptible variation of the comet's proper motion would occur; then I judged it enough to stand in the mean, and at least to take aim nearer to the truth than has hitherto been done by others — as Regiomontanus splendidly said: that we do not empty out the treasures of nature, but that, in most knowable things, it is granted to mortals to taste [something] near to the truth itself — as if he were saying that it is enough to subsist in that degree which does not utterly overthrow the truth. These [things] I wished to say in defence of Regiomontanus, and so of the truth. Nor yet do I deny but that some better reckoning can be found, whether by you or by others. Meanwhile, that the certainty of Regiomontanus's demonstrations will remain firm, I freely pronounce — [a certainty] which neither Craig, nor anyone else, will be able to convict of any absurdity. Regiomontanus assuredly scented something else about comets than what the schools publicly taught from Aristotle. But, on account of his [Aristotle's] authority, he dared not depart from the received opinion: who, if he now lived and had seen the New Star, would doubtless have blushed, and would have applied more accurate considerations of comets — nor is there any doubt with me but that, the truth being put first, he would have subscribed to our opinion. But why [do] I [say] these things to you, who have handled all these matters far more solidly, and have so dissolved all of Craig's reasonings and objections, that I do not see what more could be added to them for asserting the truth?\"</i></p><p>This was the appraisal of that most excellent <b>Thaddaeus</b> about our controversy. But after I had accurately replied to certain doubts moved and rejoined in the same Letter (especially about the <b>hypotheses of the motions of comets</b>, which he then thought could not be established — though he by no means denied [the comets] to be celestial), he — exceptionally adorned with that ingenuousness and candour of mind which is his — gladly yielded, the matter being more rightly perceived, [on] certain of his opinions, and subscribed in every way to our view about this whole Cometary (so to speak) business; which his last letter to me, sent from <b>Prague on 29 August of this very year 1591</b>, testifies, since it runs thus: <i>\"But even if I had now had some leisure, I could not nevertheless have added anything to that disputation of ours, nor can I even now; so exactly has everything been examined and weighed by you, and my arguments dissolved, that I must confess myself conquered by the truth and the evidence of the demonstrations, unless I should wish to fight against manifest truth.\"</i></p><p>That <b>Scultetus</b> too repented of his alleged conceptions about the Comet, and reckons them invalid, is clear to be known even from those few words of his — excerpted from his own autograph — which I inserted into the Apology. About <b>Nolthius</b> I have nothing to say. For he is unknown to me. Nor yet do I doubt but that, in keeping with the skill in Mathematics in which he is strong, he will receive our animadversions deftly and candidly, and will openly confess that he went astray — which I also demonstrate more fully in the prior Volume, concerning the New Star.</p><p>About the rest of the erudite Mathematicians in Germany, strong in judgment, I promise myself the same. Nay — that you may learn that those excellent talents of the <b>Italians</b> have acquiesced in our assertions no otherwise, and have approved them in every way — I will also excerpt certain [things] from the letters of that most distinguished and most excellent Mathematician <b>Giovanni Magini</b> (whose celebrated name has in recent years become known everywhere, on account of the <b>Astronomical Tables</b> resolved with ready ease and copiously enriched, and the <b>Ephemerides</b> subtly and amply derived from these), written both to <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> — once a member of my household, practising Astronomical and Medical [arts] here, but now devoting his efforts further to the study of Medicine in Italy. From which you will easily learn what that great <b>Magini</b> thought (to take one before many, with the votes of the other Mathematicians there agreeing, however, as he himself hints), who first, in a Letter transmitted to the said Gellius from <b>Bologna on 8 July in the Year 1590</b>, left written thus: <i>\"In reading through <b>Tycho's</b> book, I found nothing which did not seem to me worthy of the greatest praise; and I communicated [it] with friends, professors of the same science, who think the same with me. Nor shall I be loath to write to him, and most dutifully to give him the greatest thanks I shall be able, for the liberal gift and for his splendid judgment about me. With whom I shall also wish to confer some [things] which pertain to the <b>correction of the motions</b>. For that promise of emending the motions — in which he affirms that notable lapses of others have hitherto occurred — has, to confess the truth, disturbed me; not but that I desire them to be restored, but because I desire that to be done as soon as possible, if for no other [reason], at least for my own sake.\"</i></p><p>Afterwards, when he had asked that certain of our animadversions about the Astronomical business be communicated to him, the same Gellius intervening, he subjoins this: <i>\"I observed besides, in his book, [something] which threw into me a great desire of knowing the matter thoroughly — namely, by what <b>Method</b> the other Angles and the other side of an <b>oblique-angled Triangle</b> can be hunted out, [with] only one angle given, namely, together with the two sides about it. For Tycho himself signifies that this can be found <b>without a perpendicular</b>, and indeed investigated with great ease, on <b>page 281</b> [confirmed against </i>De mundi<i> 1588; the transcription's </i>218<i> is a transposition error — see Apparatus], saying: 'Since therefore now the angle BHK is established,' etc. Where he also touches up <b>Maestlin</b> — but modestly — who followed the common way in this matter. Indeed I most eagerly await also the <b>first book</b> of our Tycho; nor do I confess that I can obtain a greater benefit from you than if, as soon as it comes out, I be made possessed of it,\"</i> etc.</p><p>From which it is clear that Magini inspected this second book not drowsily, and subscribed to its contents not grudgingly. The same is also gathered, not obscurely, from his letter afterwards given to me myself from <b>Bologna on the Ides of September of the same year</b> [13 September 1590], which, when he had begun thus: <i>\"I received your most erudite book, transmitted to me in your name by <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> — than which nothing more welcome could have been presented to me by you, since I am wont most curiously to seek out writings of this kind, and most gladly to read [them] through; for scarcely by another way should I have hoped that it would come to my hands, since very few printed volumes are wont to be imported into Italy thence, on account of the length of the journey and the difficulty of carriage; to which is added that, by this most elegant gift, you have splendidly signified how much you esteem me — on which account indeed I give you the greatest thanks I can,\"</i> — and after he had intermingled certain other [things], and excused himself for not having written to me before, and sought from me some [things] (about which he had also made mention to Gellius before) concerning the <b>Astronomical restitution</b>, he speaks out in this manner: <i>\"But, that you may not lack my sense and judgment about that splendid labour of yours, beheld concerning the <b>Comet of the year 1577</b> — have it thus, most distinguished Man: since I had perceived your incredible diligence in it, and [your] accurate way of observing, I am persuaded that it will come about that you can emend the motions of the celestial bodies most exactly, and by the fruit of that labour satisfy most fully the zeal and expectation of all, whence eternal glory may be added to your name. I should wish, however, that you would lay yourself out most of all on observing and examining the <b>motion of Mars</b>, since it is commonly persuaded that it cannot be observed exactly.\"</i></p><p>Then, interweaving certain [things] about correcting <b>Mars's [♂] eccentricities</b> at his own discretion, he continues the letter thus: <i>\"I cannot but greatly approve the <b>System of the universe</b> devised by you, although I should wish that the orb of the Sun and of Mars did not at all intersect each other; but if, as I learned from Gellius, it has been observed by you that <b>Mars in the acronychal [position] approaches the Earth more than the Sun [☉]</b>, an intersection of this kind must altogether be admitted. I am in great expectation of observations and speculations of this kind of yours, which I shall by no means blush both to approve and to follow — even though, in constructing Ephemerides and resolved tables, I have not deviated a nail's breadth from the <b>Copernican</b> reckoning and the <b>Prutenic Tables</b>. I hope also — which I vehemently desire — to read this year your first book <b>on the Phenomena of the aethereal World</b>, which you promise you will publish.\"</i></p><p>Soon, certain other [things] being interposed, he has this toward the end: <i>\"This year I am going to interpret my <b>sketches</b> [</i>hypotyposes<i>] of the celestial orbs, which will be a good occasion for me to explain far more amply (as I have already begun to do) those [things] which have been published by me in this genre, and to illustrate [them] with Commentaries and Geometrical Demonstrations and computations. Which Commentaries, however, before they are made public, I gladly would wish to learn and understand your lucubrations, so that I might insert and apply your <b>suppositions</b> [hypotheses] too into them.\"</i></p><p>But I will add no more of these and similar [things]. Hence it becomes sufficiently clear to you, my <b>Craig</b>, that the <b>Philosophers of Ausonia</b> [Italy] — but above all that most eminent Professor of Mathematics of the renowned University of Bologna, <b>Magini</b> — have applaudingly accepted those [things] which our second book comprises, as to the <b>aethereal seats of the Comet</b> (which I chiefly labour to demonstrate there), and him too [accepting] our <b>Neoteric ordering of the celestial Hypotheses</b> — both of which, however, you not only call into doubt, but also, as much as in you lies, strive to shake. The talents of the Italians are certainly sublime and acute, and pre-eminent in the grave dexterity of judgment, and for the greater part bound to the <b>Aristotelian Axioms</b> no less than you [are] in Scotland. For which [axioms] they would even stand firmly and fight strongly, did they not notice that most [of them] are refuted by the certainty of <b>Astronomical observations</b> and by <b>Mathematical Demonstrations</b>. But I will add one [thing] more, that you may be aware [of it]. Since this very <b>Magini</b> exhorts [me] to note diligently the <b>appearances of Mars [♂]</b> — which I have also otherwise, for very many years from now, accomplished not unfruitfully (as I hope) — I, while writing back to him, asked him and <b>Gellius</b> to attend diligently there to <b>Mars</b>, being <b>acronychal</b> this last summer, in the month of <b>June</b>, because there, about the Meridian, being then higher than here, it was less subject to <b>Refractions</b>. And when I had at the same time written to Gellius to procure that a certain <b>Instrument</b>, after the likeness of mine which he saw and handled here, be made for these and certain other Observations to be measured from the heaven there in Italy, <b>Magini</b> came not long after to <b>Padua</b>, and bestowed diligent labour, both in elaborating and in using [it], as regards this Instrument; and among other [things] they there observed, by means of this, <b>Mars</b> several times in the acronychal position of which I spoke; which notations, afterwards transmitted to me, I collated with ours had here in this star at the same time, and scarcely found a difference of one or another minute. Which I thought should be indicated to you, although it is somewhat beside the purpose. It will therefore be yours to aspire heartily to the truth itself, all affection and preoccupied opinion set aside, and to seek, pursue, [and] embrace this alone, and — all by-ways set behind — freely to profess the same. Yet there is no [reason] why you should suppose that I am in any way moved or take it ill that you have shown yourself an <b>antagonist</b> to our assertions in this matter — which indeed you alone, as far as it has hitherto been possible to find out, before all the Mathematicians of Italy, France, and Germany (which I also praise), have dared to undertake. Nay, you have rather performed for me, on this account, a thing very welcome, and by no means displeasing (as you see) to erudite men. And in this way you have furnished a not-inopportune occasion of more fully unravelling and laying open the recesses of inviolate certainty. But that I nonetheless altogether think well of you, and bear a friendly mind toward you, you will perceive not obscurely from this alone: that to your most serene King — when he deigned to visit me in person on this Island, two years ago, having tarried for some while in Denmark with [his] Bride on account of the impediments of winter — I diligently and earnestly commended you to His Majesty, and, promoting [you] to better [things], begged for you submissively and reverently, with what intercession I could, in the hearing of the Magnificent Lord <b>Chancellor</b> and the illustrious Count <b>Lord Keith</b> [<i>Kietzio</i>]; from whom I obtained at the same time that they would promise to be willing to remind the King of the same [matter] when he had returned to his [people]; and if, on account of the incidental distractions of business (as happens) and the unrests of worldly matters, anything in this regard was meanwhile omitted, I have lately faithfully reminded the Lord Chancellor by letter (of which you have the tenor here) of his promise anew; nor do I doubt but that he and others — men of approved nobility and strong in authority at court, stirred by this commendation of mine, such as it is — will strive to wish well to you and your affairs, and to advance them further. You, meanwhile, do not at all cease to write to me more often, and besides to confer with me about Mathematical matters (which is our delight). It will be especially welcome to me, and you will find me not difficult to reply.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 14 November, in the Year 1591.<br><b>Tycho Brahe.</b></p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: <b>A</b> = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f (a copy/<i>Afskrift</i>) — Dreyer prints the head-note <i>\"[Ex apographo in codice Vindobonensi lat. 9737 f]\"</i>, confirming both the shelfmark and the apograph status. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VII pp. 308–319</b> (PDF pp. 322–333). All five embedded quotations present and correctly attributed; dateline confirmed (p. 319). <b>One transcription slip (now corrected):</b> Magini's second excerpt — working transcription <i>\"pag. 218\"</i> for Dreyer's correct <i>\"pag. 281\"</i> (confirmed against <i>De mundi</i> 1588; see the dedicated note below). The embedded Hájek quote is additionally corroborated by <b>Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590</b>.<br>- <b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg). <b>John Craig</b> (Craige, d. 1620), Scottish physician and Aristotelian philosopher, practising medicine at <b>Edinburgh</b>, later physician to <b>James VI/I</b>. He had sent Tycho objections defending Aristotle's sublunary comets; Tycho's <i>Apologetica responsio</i> (= the \"Apology\" repeatedly named here) answered them. This letter is the <b>second salvo</b>: a year and a half having passed with no reply, Tycho needles Craig to return \"to the arena\" or concede, and marshals testimonials. The whole exchange would later be printed (Craig's objections + Tycho's <i>Apologia</i>) in the comet volume, as announced here.<br>- <b>The marshalled testimonials (the documentary core).</b> Tycho quotes, to show the learned consensus:<br>  1. <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz von Senftenau)</b>, Imperial <b>Vice-Chancellor</b> (d. 1594), Prague 28 June 1590 — <i>two excerpts</i>: (a) on the <b>comets</b> (the post-1577 cometary results are beyond doubt among the learned; Aristotle's diehards are merely testing Tycho \"at the Lydian touchstone\"; Curtius procured the Imperial <b>Privilege</b> for Tycho's <i>Opera</i> and vouched for it to the Emperor); (b) on the <b>neoteric hypotheses</b> (Tycho answered all objections, \"everything most beautifully congruent\" — final judgment to await the \"greater work,\" the <i>Machina Mundi</i>).<br>  2. <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b> (<i>Thaddaeus</i>), my \"old friend\" — the <b>whole Craig-paragraph re-quoted from his letter of 11 June 1590</b>; <i>plus</i> a later concession, <b>Prague 29 August 1591</b>, in which Hájek (having first doubted whether comet-<i>motions</i> could be reduced to hypotheses, while never denying them celestial) declares himself \"conquered by the truth and the evidence of the demonstrations.\" This documents the <b>arc from reservation to concession</b>: Hájek's reservation of 11 June 1590 (the comet-motion-law thesis) was answered by Tycho and withdrawn within 14 months.<br>  3. <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> of Bologna — <i>three excerpts</i>: (a) to <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>, Bologna 8 July 1590 (nothing in Tycho's book not \"worthy of the greatest praise\"); (b) again to Gellius (the <b>oblique-triangle method without a perpendicular</b>, \"Cum igitur nunc constet angulus BHK,\" where Tycho \"modestly touches up Maestlin\") — <b>the page cross-reference is now resolved: Dreyer's <i>pag. 281</i> is correct; the working transcription's <i>pag. 218</i> is a digit transposition. The citation is to Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), where the original 1588 marginal <i>281</i> sits at exactly the <i>Cum igitur nunc constet angulus BHK</i> passage (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV p. 233 / scan PDF p. 247);</b> (c) to <b>Tycho himself, 13 Sept 1590</b> — <i>the very letter Tycho answers on 1 December 1590</i>: Magini received the comet book via Gellius, praises the 1577-comet labour, urges Tycho onto <b>Mars</b>, <b>approves the <i>Systema universi</b></i> (while wishing the solar and Martian orbs did not intersect — yet conceding the intersection \"must be admitted\" given Mars's acronychal approach), confesses he has \"not deviated a nail's breadth from the Copernican/Prutenic\" in his own tables, and announces his forthcoming <i>hypotyposes</i> commentary. Tycho closes by reporting that, at his and Gellius's prompting, <b>Magini observed Mars at the June-1591 opposition from Padua</b> with a Tychonic-pattern instrument, agreeing with Uraniborg \"within one or two minutes.\"<br>- <b>The 1590 comet.</b> Tycho's proof-piece against Craig: the <b>comet of March 1590</b>, \"altogether celestial,\" with no sensible parallax — its declination over many hours (at the prime vertical, then on the meridian below the pole) varied by nothing beyond its proper motion. He gave a copy of these observations (the same as sent to <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse</b>) to an unnamed <b>young Scot, the King's intimate Secretary</b>, to pass to Craig.<br>- <b>The royal visit (datable anchor).</b> \"Two years ago\" <b>James VI of Scotland</b> visited Tycho on Hven (in fact <b>20 February 1590 O.S.</b>), during his winter stay in Denmark with his bride <b>Anne of Denmark</b> (married at Kronborg/Oslo, 1589–90). Tycho commended Craig to the King in the hearing of the <b>Lord Chancellor</b> (John Maitland of Thirlestane) and <b>Count Keith</b> (<i>Kietzius</i> — the <b>Earl Marischal George Keith</b>, who had led the embassy to fetch Anne). <b>Andreas Cragius</b> (Anders Krag, Copenhagen professor of physics) had gone to Scotland as the bride-party's chief physician and reported Craig's reaction.<br>- <b>Persons (others).</b> <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Tycho's former assistant, then studying medicine in Italy and acting as his Bologna/Padua agent — cf. the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590); <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Görlitz mathematician, who \"repented\" his comet conceptions); <b>Nolthius</b> (a mathematician unknown to Tycho — probably Andreas Nolthius, who had written on the 1577 comet); <b>Johannes Praetorius</b>; <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (<i>Mesthlinus</i>); <b>Regiomontanus</b> (the <i>De cometae … problemata</i>, problems 2–4); <b>Aristotle</b> and the <b>Peripatetics</b>.<br>- <b>The re-quoted Hájek paragraph.</b> Tycho quotes Hájek's Craig-paragraph essentially verbatim from the letter of 11 June 1590, with minor witness-level variants — translated here following <i>this</i> text's wording: <i>veritati <b>contradicere</b></i> (the 11 June letter: <i>obstrepere</i>); <i>quibus <b>vix</b> unquam</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>nemo unquam</i>); <i>sed tantum <b>certandi</b> studio contradicit, <b>an is candidi et aequi aestimatoris partes agat, alijs dijudicandum relinquo</b></i> (the 11 June letter ends differently: <i>…rixandi studio contradicit, istud malevoli et invidi hominis est iudicium</i>); <i>monitum</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>monitus</i>); <i>adiudicanda</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>adscribenda</i>); <i>Turpe enim <b>non</b> errorem</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>non <b>est</b> errorem</i>); <i>immerito accusat</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>falso accusat</i>); <i>crasse fallitur</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>turpissime fallitur</i>); <i>imputat</i> (the 11 June letter: <i>impingit</i>). The divergences are stylistic/scribal, not substantive; sense is identical. (Tycho evidently quoted from his retained copy.)<br>- <b>Greek &amp; Latin tags.</b> <i>ἀμαθηματικός</i> = \"unmathematical / ignorant of mathematics\"; <i>ἄθεος</i> = \"godless, atheist\" — Tycho's double jibe at Aristotle. <i>Lydius lapis</i> = the <b>Lydian touchstone</b> (assaying metal; here, a testing-stone for truth). <i>in verba magistri iurare</i> = Horace, <i>Epist.</i> I.1.14. <i>velut pro aris et focis</i> = \"as for altar and hearth\" (proverbial, for the last ditch). <i>Apodixes</i> = ἀπόδειξις, geometrical demonstration. <i>Ausonia</i> = poetic Italy. <i>ne latum unguem</i> = \"not a nail's breadth.\" The Regiomontanus <i>sententia</i> on nature's treasures, and the \"earlier days are disciples of later\" topos, recur from Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Tycho's dateline <b>14 November 1591 O.S.</b> (Danish Julian); matches the catalogue label. The quoted letters carry their own datelines: Curtius <b>Prague 28 June 1590</b>, Hájek <b>Prague 29 August 1591</b>, Magini to Gellius <b>Bologna 8 July 1590</b>, Magini to Tycho <b>Bologna 13 September 1590</b> (<i>Idibus Septembris</i>) — the Catholic/Imperial correspondents writing N.S. The header leads with the as-written O.S. date.<br>- <b>OCR / transcription notes (kept verbatim, read through in translation).</b> Inside the <b>Curtius</b> quote, OCR line-break splits: <i>privile gium</i>→<i>privilegium</i>, <i>conscribendi</i> (sound), <i>d e Cometis</i>→<i>de Cometis</i>, <i>ta lia</i>→<i>talia</i>, <i>er uditis</i>→<i>eruditis</i>, <i>eo s</i>→<i>eos</i>, <i>contr a</i>→<i>contra</i>, <i>vo lunt</i>→<i>volunt</i>. In the body: <i>proptere a</i>→<i>propterea</i>, <i>demostrationum</i>→<i>demonstrationum</i>, <i>authoritem</i>→<i>authoritatem</i> (all within the Hájek quote); <i>♂tisapparentias</i>→<i>♂tis apparentias</i> (Martis apparentias). None affects sense.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Three TEXT_ERRORs collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII pp.307-320: 'pag. 218' corrected to 'pag. 281' (p.316; English already correct, no change); 'Terrae accedere' corrected to 'Terrae propius accedere' (p.317; English '[more]' unbracketed to 'more'); 'confectum' corrected to 'conspectum' (pp.316-317; English 'accomplished' corrected to 'beheld').</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — sound. A long, rhetorically vehement but argumentatively continuous letter — the Craig challenge, the 1590-comet proof, and a chain of five embedded testimonial-quotations (Curtius ×2, Hájek ×2, Magini ×3) — rendered securely throughout; the polemical register against Aristotle/Craig and the courteous register of the quoted savants are kept distinct, and the controlled glossary (parallax, refraction, eccentricity, acronychal, hypothesis, <i>Apodixis</i>, <i>restitutio</i>, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with Hájek's letter of 11 June 1590 and the Magini letter of 1 December 1590, into which this letter is the connecting hinge. The embedded <b>Hájek paragraph is cross-checked against his letter of 11 June 1590</b> (variants catalogued above) and the embedded <b>Magini 13 Sept 1590 letter against Tycho's reply of 1 December 1590</b>. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VII pp. 308–319)</b>: all five embedded quotations, the dateline, and the 9737f apograph witness are confirmed, so the un-run-collation reservation is <b>lifted</b>. Three residues remain: (1) a <b>transcription slip, now resolved</b> — the working transcription's \"<i>pag. 218</i>\" for the correct \"<i>pag. 281</i>\" (confirmed against <i>De mundi</i> 1588 = Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV p. 233); the translation follows it; (2) the unnamed <b>King's Secretary</b> and the precise identity of <b>Nolthius</b> are left as the text gives them; (3) the OCR splits in the Curtius quote are declared and read through. None obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review — the <i>pag. 281</i> reading is now confirmed against <i>De mundi</i> 1588, closing the last open textual item from the collation of the eleven longest letters.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole capping item (pag.218→281) resolved against </i>De mundi<i> 1588; sense secure end-to-end; only Tycho's own unnamed Secretary + declared OCR remain.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 14 Dreyer page images (TBOO VII pp.307-320) in chunks of 4/4/4/2 and collated the opening address, the full body (Curtius, Hagecius, Magini, Scultetus/Nolthius passages), every proper name, date (28 June 1590; 29 August 1591; 8 July 1590; Ides of September 1590; 14 November 1591), and closing against the page images. Translation itself is faithful and in two places (pag. 281; 'propius') the translator correctly caught and silently repaired divergences between our source_text and Dreyer. Grade is capped at C solely because three TEXT_ERROR divergences between source_text and Dreyer survive on file (218/281 page-number transposition; dropped 'propius'; 'confectum' for Dreyer's 'conspectum'), per the rule that any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade regardless of severity; verdict is MINOR because none of the three changes what a scholar would take from the letter.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints 'pag. 218' but Dreyer's TBOO clearly prints 'pag. 281'; the translator's own bracketed note anticipates and correctly resolves this to Dreyer's reading, so the finished English is right, but the source_text on file departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>fignificat pag. 281 inquiens: Cum igitur nunc conftet angulus BHK etc. (TBOO VII p.316)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text drops the comparative adverb 'propius' (more nearly) present in Dreyer; the translator supplied the bracketed '[more]' to compensate, so the sense survives, but the source_text itself is short a word against Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Martem acronychum Terrae propius accedere quam ☉lem, a te eft obfervatum (TBOO VII p.317)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads 'confectum' (accomplished) where Dreyer prints 'conspectum' (beheld/surveyed); here the translator did not flag or correct the divergence, so 'accomplished' silently follows the erroneous source_text rather than Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>praeclaro labore circa Cometam anno 1577 confpectum [= conspectum] (TBOO VII pp.316-317)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All three defects were verified directly against Dreyer's page images (TBOO VII pp.315-318, read visually, not via OCR text layer) rather than taken on the audit's say-so. All three are confirmed as printed: 'pag. 281' (p.316), 'Terrae propius accedere' (p.317), and 'confpectum' [=conspectum] (pp.316-317, spanning the page break). The rest of the 14-page letter was not re-collated beyond these three loci, per the brief's scope (repair only the listed defect sites). No crux remains among the three defects given.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15900228001", "next": "brahe-t_15920327001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900228001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920327001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Challenges Craig for falling silent in their ongoing dispute, arguing that acknowledging errors is praiseworthy and urging him either to concede or to continue the debate.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15911114001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920201001", "d": "1592-02-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bologna", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Bologna, 1 February 1592 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tycho Brahe,<i> Astronomiae instauratae mechanica </i>(as cited in the dataset; the letter is also in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE PERILLUSTRI ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE DANO DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, ET ARCIS URANIBURG IN INSULA HELLESPONTI DANICI HUAeNA FUNDATORI.<br>IO. ANTONIVS MAGINVS PATAVINUS MATHEMATICVS<br>S. P. D.</p><p>DIU multumque plurimis ab hinc annis cogitavi (Nobilissime ac doctissime vir) ut viam invenirem expeditam, ac facilem, qua cuiuscunque numeri quamvis magni quadrata radix colligi ex tempore posset. Opus sane arduum, et perquam difficile, in quo quantum insudarim, nemo plane credet, nisi qui ipse aliquando periculum fecit, et saepe quidem a proposito rei difficultas me deterruit. Verum enim vero res tandem ita mihi faeliciter successit, ut plus, quam optarem, invenerim: nam quid in hoc genere calculi praestiterim, tu primum doctissime Brahe, deinde reliqui omnes Matheseos cultores, ad quorum manus haec pervenerint, probe perspicient. Nolo autem hîc in laudandis meis inventis nimis audax studiosis videri, ne momus quispiam propriae laudis parum modestum praeconem me esse cavilletur. Caeterum qualecunque hoc sit opus, tibi, gloriosissimi nominis viro, ac nostrae aetatis Astronomo eminentissimo, grati animi et observantiae erga te singularis caussâ dicare volui, idque maximis de causis. Primum enim tu ipse es, cui maxime debeo, quod amicitiae tuae fores tam amanter ac humaniter mihi aperuisti, atque mihi plus honoris tribuisti, quam eruditio (si qua est) tenuis mea, ac perexigua postulabat, cum mihi tuum secundum librum de recentioribus mundi Phaenomenis per Danum quendam studiosum misisti, simulque per eundem, quid de illis sentirem, quaesivisti; nec non etiam quod meae Epistolae humanissime respondisti, multa mecum de rebus Astronomicis disserendo. Deinde tu unus es, cui hanc meam Tetragonicam tabulam acceptam fore maxime confido, propterea quod ipsa faelicissime uti poteris ad eliciendos inaequalitatum motuum planetarum angulos, et promptiori quidem compendio, quam per Tangentium, et Secantium tabulas. Tandem quia tu ille es praestantissimus Astronomiae instaurator, cui plurimum debet nostra aetas, et debebunt posteri omnes, quando quidem non ego solum, sed omnes fere sincerae mentis viri de tuis Astronomicis fundamentis spem eam conceperunt, ut a te solo Astronomiae restitutionem avide expectent. Etenim tu tales miro artificio, ac invento caelestium sphaerarum confinxisti hypotyposes, quales probe sufficere posse ad tuenda phaenomena, et apparentias coelitus animadversas, ac ad definienda coelestium luminum loca ad quaevis tempora, non ambigendum est. Hortor itaque te, obtestorque, exiguo hoc munusculo tibi oblato, ut, quod faeliciter exorsus es, et multo jam tempore in hoc doctrinae genere moliris, tandem absolvas: namque nullum alium rectius et faelicius id praestare posse quam te, qui ingenio es acerrimo natus ad Astronomiae collapsae illustrationem, recte ac sincere judico, idque sperant omnes. Etenim si tuum laboriosissimum ac praestantissimum Theatrum absolutum quamprimum nobis communicabis, iis profecto, qui olim motuum correctionem tentarunt, et qui nunc pertentant, omnem eripies gloriam, atque in posterum tentaturis omnem praeripies occasionem, et materiam.</p><p>Scio quidem post tuorum laborum editionem mea scripta correctionem desideratura, sed tantum abest, ut haec me perturbent, ut potius desiderem quamprimum tuas speculationes publici juris fieri: nam confestim Ephemerides meas corrigam, quarum secunda editio avide expectatur, vel, si opus erit, de novo alias pertexam. Illud etiam per hanc epistolam tibi significare liceat, me Patavii, et Bononiae poli investigationem, ac Martis, eorumque affixorum siderum meridianas altitudines, quas tantopere me observare optabas, hactenus praetermisisse, neque ob id tamen negligentiae nomine a te accusari velim, cum hoc praestandi mihi a variis negociorum fluctibus, qui me oppresserunt, erepta sit facultas, accedente etiam loci incommoditate, praesertim Patavii, de quo tibi idem iste testari poterit. Sextantem illum Astronomicum, quem ex tuo invento et in tui gratiam Patavii fabrefieri curavi, exactissime coelo respondere ad singula minuta recte percepi, quoniam easdem distantias adunguem nonnullarum fixarum, quas in tuo catalogo ex tuis observationibus notasti, deprehendi. Atque hunc jam Bononiam mecum exportavi, ubi observationes plurimas in tui commodum perficere tentabo. Quinetiam et hoc te latere nolo, Nobilissimum ac doctissimum virum Paulum Bonfilium patritium Bononiensem, tuique studiosissimum, fabrefieri curasse propriis expensis Quadrantem ex ligno, et metallo decempedalem, qui non tam minuta, quam minutorum partes in observationibus exhibebit. Idem Nobilissimus vir Sextantem quoque ejusdem magnitudinis habere studet, quibus Instrumentis in summa turri, quam in suâ domo habet, observationes coelestes in tui gratiam perficere commodius valeamus, de quibus omnibus suis loco et tempore certior fies. Hîc finem epistolae huic meae imponam, te enixe rogans, ut, si quid possum, tuo jure me utare. Vale vir praeclarissime, Astronomorumque decus, atque Astronomiam perficere tuis lucubrationibus et vigiliis perge, et Spartam, quam accepisti (ut ajunt) orna.</p><p>Iterumque diu, et faeliciter vale.<br>Bononiae Kalendis Februarii. M. D. XCII.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man of most illustrious nobility and most outstanding erudition, Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup and founder of the castle of Uraniborg on the Danish island of Hven.</b><br><b>Giovanni Antonio Magini of Padua, mathematician, sends heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Long and hard, for very many years past, have I pondered (most noble and most learned sir) how I might find a ready and easy method by which the square root of any number, however large, could be obtained on the spot. A task indeed arduous, and exceedingly difficult, in which no one will at all believe how much I sweated, unless he be one who has himself at some time made the trial; and often, indeed, the difficulty of the matter deterred me from my purpose. But in truth the thing has at last so happily succeeded for me that I have found out more than I could have wished: for what I have accomplished in this kind of calculation, you first, most learned Brahe, and then all the rest of the cultivators of mathematics into whose hands these things shall come, will rightly perceive. Yet I do not wish here, in praising my own inventions, to seem too bold to scholars, lest some Momus carp that I am too immodest a herald of my own praise. But whatever this work may be, I wished to dedicate it to you — a man of most glorious name, and the most eminent astronomer of our age — out of a grateful spirit and of my singular regard toward you, and that for the greatest reasons. For first, you yourself are the one to whom I owe it most, that you opened the doors of your friendship to me so lovingly and courteously, and granted me more honour than my slender and very scant erudition (if there be any) deserved, when you sent me your second book on the more recent Phenomena of the world by a certain Danish scholar, and at the same time, through the same man, inquired what I thought of them; and likewise that you most courteously answered my letter, discoursing with me at length on astronomical matters. Next, you are the one man to whom I am most confident this Tetragonic table of mine will be welcome, because you will be able to use it most happily for eliciting the angles of the inequalities of the planets' motions, and indeed by a readier short-cut than by the tables of Tangents and Secants. Last, because you are that most outstanding restorer of astronomy, to whom our age owes most, and to whom all posterity will owe most — since indeed not I alone, but almost all men of sincere mind have conceived this hope from your astronomical foundations, that from you alone they eagerly await the restoration of astronomy. For you have, with wonderful skill and invention, fashioned such hypotyposes of the celestial spheres as — it is not to be doubted — can well suffice to save the phenomena and the appearances observed in the heavens, and to define the places of the celestial luminaries for any times whatever. I urge you therefore, and entreat you, by this little gift offered to you, that what you have happily begun, and have now for a long time been labouring at in this kind of learning, you may at last complete: for that no other can perform it more rightly and more happily than you — you who were born with a most keen genius for the illustration of fallen astronomy — I judge rightly and sincerely, and so all men hope. For if you will share with us, as soon as possible, your most laborious and most outstanding Theatre once completed, you will surely snatch away all glory from those who once attempted the correction of the motions, and who now keep attempting it, and you will forestall every occasion and matter from those who shall attempt it hereafter.</p><p>I know indeed that, after the publication of your works, my own writings will stand in need of correction; but so far is this from troubling me that I rather desire your speculations to be made public as soon as possible: for I shall at once correct my Ephemerides, of which a second edition is eagerly awaited, or, if need be, weave others anew. This too let me be allowed to make known to you by this letter: that I have hitherto neglected, at Padua and at Bologna, the investigation of the pole, and the meridian altitudes of Mars and of the fixed stars near them, which you so greatly wished me to observe; nor on that account, however, would I be accused by you under the name of negligence, since the power of performing this has been wrested from me by the various surges of business that have overwhelmed me, with the inconvenience of the place added besides, especially at Padua — to which this same man will be able to testify to you. That Astronomical sextant which, after your invention and for your sake, I had made at Padua, I have rightly perceived to answer the heavens most exactly to each single minute, since I found the same distances, to a hair's breadth, of several of the fixed stars which you have noted in your catalogue from your observations. And this I have now carried with me to Bologna, where I shall attempt to complete very many observations for your benefit. Moreover, this too I would not have hidden from you: that the most noble and most learned man Paolo Bonfilio, a patrician of Bologna and most devoted to you, has had made at his own expense a Quadrant of wood and metal, ten feet in size, which will exhibit in observations not so much the minutes as the parts of minutes. The same most noble man is eager to have also a Sextant of the same magnitude, so that with these Instruments, in the top of a tower which he has in his house, we may be the better able to complete celestial observations for your sake; about all of which you shall be informed in their place and time. Here I shall put an end to this letter of mine, earnestly asking you that, if I can do anything, you use me by your right. Farewell, most distinguished man and ornament of astronomers; and go on to perfect astronomy by your lucubrations and your vigils, and adorn the Sparta which you have received (as they say).</p><p>And again, long and happily, farewell.<br>At Bologna, on the Kalends of February [1 February]. 1592.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Io. Antonius Maginus Patavinus</i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of mathematics at Bologna from 1588 (the chair Galileo also sought), astronomer, cartographer, and author of widely-used <i>Ephemerides</i>. He styles himself <i>Patavinus</i> (\"of Padua\") by origin, though he writes from Bologna. The address-superscription gives Tycho's full titulature: <i>Dominus de Knudstrup</i> (<b>Lord of Knudstrup</b>, his ancestral seat in Scania) and founder of <b>Uraniborg</b> on <b>Hven</b>.</li><li><b><i>insula Hellesponti Danici Huaena</b></i> — \"the island of Hven in the Danish Hellespont\": a humanist conceit for the <b>Øresund</b> (the Sound), styled the \"Danish Hellespont.\" Kept as the figure it is.</li><li><b>Greeting.</b> <i>S. P. D.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>The dedication.</b> The letter is the <b>dedicatory epistle</b> prefixed to Magini's square-root work — <i>hanc meam Tetragonicam tabulam</i>, rendered \"this Tetragonic table of mine\" — i.e. his <i>Tabula tetragonica</i>, a table for extracting square roots, published Venice 1592. Magini commends it to Tycho as a computing aid \"for eliciting the angles of the inequalities of the planets' motions … by a readier short-cut than by the tables of Tangents and Secants.\" This places the letter in the orbit of Tycho's own <b>prosthaphaeretic</b> computing interests, though that term does not appear in the source and is not imported here.</li><li><b><i>tuum secundum librum de recentioribus mundi Phaenomenis</b></i> — \"your second book on the more recent Phenomena of the world\": Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis liber secundus</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), sent to Magini, with Tycho's request for Magini's opinion, <i>per Danum quendam studiosum</i> (\"by a certain Danish scholar\" — unnamed here).</li><li><b><i>Momus</b></i> — in Greek mythology the god of mockery and fault-finding; \"lest some Momus carp\" = lest some captious critic. Kept as the proper name.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms</b> (per protocol §5.1–5.2): <i>Astronomiae instaurator / restitutio / illustrationem … collapsae</i> — Tycho as the <b>restorer</b> of fallen astronomy, the <i>restitutio astronomiae</i> programme, rendered \"restoration / restorer of astronomy\"; <i>hypotyposes caelestium sphaerarum</i> — \"hypotyposes of the celestial spheres,\" i.e. his world-model (the term <i>hypotyposis</i>, an outline/representation, is kept); <i>tuenda phaenomena … apparentias</i> — \"to save the phenomena and the appearances\" (the standard <i>salvare apparentias</i> idiom); <i>inaequalitatum motuum planetarum angulos</i> — \"the angles of the inequalities of the planets' motions\"; <i>meridianas altitudines</i> — \"<b>meridian altitudes</b>\"; <i>poli investigationem</i> — \"the investigation of the pole\" (latitude determination); <i>catalogus … fixarum</i> — Tycho's star <b>catalogue</b>.</li><li><b><i>Theatrum</b></i> — \"Theatre\": Magini's term for the great projected work Tycho is labouring at (his <i>Theatrum astronomicum</i> / the grand instauration), capitalised as in the source; rendered literally \"Theatre\" and flagged, since it is Magini's metaphor for Tycho's magnum opus rather than a fixed title.</li><li><b>Instruments</b> (protocol §5.2): <i>Sextans Astronomicus</i> — \"<b>Astronomical sextant</b>,\" built <i>ex tuo invento</i> (\"after your invention\") at Padua; <i>Quadrans … decempedalis ex ligno et metallo</i> — a <b>quadrant</b> \"ten feet in size, of wood and metal,\" resolving \"not so much the minutes as the parts of minutes.\" <i>ad singula minuta</i> / <i>minutorum partes</i> — arc-minutes and fractions of arc-minutes.</li><li><b>Paolo Bonfioli.</b> <i>Paulus Bonfilius patritius Bononiensis</i> = <b>Paolo Bonfioli</b>, a Bolognese patrician and astronomy enthusiast (\"most devoted to you\"), who funded the large quadrant and sought a matching sextant, with a tower-top observing platform in his house. (Spelling of the surname normalised in the apparatus; the Latin form <i>Bonfilius</i> is kept in the Source and translation.)</li><li><b><i>Ephemerides … secunda editio</b></i> — Magini's <i>Ephemerides coelestium motuum</i>; he undertakes to recompute them once Tycho's results appear. <i>de novo alias pertexam</i> — \"weave others anew\" (the weaving metaphor kept).</li><li><b><i>Spartam, quam accepisti (ut ajunt) orna</b></i> — \"adorn the Sparta which you have received (as they say)\": the proverb <i>Σπάρταν ἔλαχες, ταύταν κόσμει</i> (\"Sparta is your portion; do it credit\"), via Erasmus, <i>Adagia</i> (ii.5.1) — \"make the most of the lot assigned to you.\" Magini's <i>ut ajunt</i> (\"as they say\") flags it as the proverb it is; kept and glossed.</li><li><b><i>adunguem</b></i> — <i>ad unguem</i>, \"to a hair's breadth\" (literally \"to the fingernail,\" the sculptor's idiom for exactitude).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Bononiae Kalendis Februarii M.D.XCII</i> = \"At <b>Bologna</b>, on the <b>Kalends of February</b> (1 February), 1592.\" Magini, a Catholic in Bologna, wrote <b>New Style</b> (Gregorian), as the dataset label marks (\"n.s.\"); the dateline and the catalogued date agree on 1 February 1592. The Roman-calendar idiom <i>Kalendis Februarii</i> is rendered with the idiom and glossed to the civil date per protocol §3. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The dataset's `edition` field names the <i>Astronomiae instaurata mechanica</i>; this dedicatory epistle to Magini's <i>Tabula tetragonica</i> is also printed among the <i>Epistolae</i> in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII. Both are recorded; no MS witness is listed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected 'spectationes' (observations) to 'speculationes' (speculations/theoretical works) in the Latin per Dreyer, TBOO V p.128, l.14, and updated the corresponding English clause, which had shifted Magini's request from Tycho's theoretical system to mere observational data.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the run-together 'DANICIHUAeNA' to the two words 'DANICI HUAeNA' in the dedication heading's Latin per Dreyer, TBOO V p.127, l.4 (no change to the English).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the invented surname 'Bonfioli' to 'Bonfilio' in the English, matching the Latin 'Bonfilium' already present and confirmed at Dreyer, TBOO V p.128, l.30 (no change to the Latin).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is a polished humanist dedicatory epistle and renders securely; the only points requiring an editorial gloss rather than a reading-decision (<i>Theatrum</i> as Magini's metaphor for Tycho's magnum opus; the <i>Spartam orna</i> proverb) are flagged in the apparatus, not smoothed into the body.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address heading, all three main paragraphs, the sextant/quadrant/Bonfilio passage, closing) against Dreyer TBOO V pp.126-129 read as page images. Two TEXT_ERRORs found in source_text against Dreyer's print (a glued heading word, and 'spectationes' silently substituted for Dreyer's 'speculationes' — a real word-level corruption with mild semantic consequence) plus one proper-name mistranslation (Bonfilium rendered 'Bonfioli' rather than 'Bonfilio'). Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of individual severity; this letter was self-graded A, which the collation does not support.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text glues 'DANICI' and 'HUAeNA' into a single word; Dreyer prints them as two words. The English sense (island of Hven) survives, but the Latin transcription is corrupted. Dreyer prints: <i>IN INSULA HELLESPONTI DANICI HUAeNA FUNDATORI (p.127 line 4 — two separate words)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). source_text substitutes 'spectationes' (viewings/observations) for Dreyer's actual word 'speculationes' (theoretical works/speculations) — a genuine word-level corruption that shifts what Magini is asking Tycho to publish, from his theoretical astronomical system to mere observational data. Dreyer prints: <i>ut potius defiderem quamprimum tuas fpeculationes publici juris fieri (p.128 line 14 — 'speculationes', not 'spectationes')</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Dreyer's and source_text's Latin both read 'Bonfilium' (from 'Bonfilius'), which Italianizes as 'Bonfilio' (i.e. 'Bonfiglio'); the translation instead invents the surname 'Bonfioli', not supported by the Latin. At: “the most noble and most learned man Paolo Bonfioli”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated all three defects directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO V pp.126-129): p.128 l.14 confirms 'fpeculationes', p.127 l.4 confirms the two separate words 'DANICI HUAeNA', and p.128 l.30 confirms 'Paulum Bonfilium'. All three are genuine, now corrected at the appropriate layer (Latin, or English, or both per defect type). No remaining crux.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15910801001", "next": "brahe-t_15981128001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15901201003", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15981128001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Announces the discovery of a quick method for extracting square roots of large numbers after years of effort, and previews the result for Tycho before wider publication.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920201001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920220001", "d": "1592-02-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 20 February 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Early New High German (chancery, with heavy Latin code-switch) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI (= the<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>and Dreyer's edition of it) · MS witnesses: none listed in the source record</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The letter's recovered passages — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGEN, HOCHGEBORNEN FÜRSTEN VND<br>HERRN WILHELMEN LANDTGRAFFEN ZU HESSEN, GRAFFEN ZU CATZENELNBOGEN, DIETZ,<br>ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA, MEINEM GNEDIGEN HERRN.</p><p>Durchleuchtiger Hochgeborner Furst:. Gnediger Herr. Nebst meiner alzeit<br>bereitwilliger diensterbietung hab ich nicht vnterlassen konnen Ewrer F. G. bey<br>gegenwertiger, auff die anstehende Franckfurter Messe furgefallener botschafft<br>zuzuschreiben.</p><p>Erstlich was die Elendtsthiere (von welcher Zuuerschaffung ich<br>meinen müglichen vleiß zugesagt) belangt, hab ich sie an etlichen orten in<br>Norwegen in bestellung. Aber dweill die Segellation noch<br>nicht von wegen des winters angehet, kan ich so baldt keinen bescheidt darauff<br>bekomen. Alßbaldt mir aber eine der mehr solicher Thiere zugestalt werden, dem<br>ich auffs füriahr erwarte, will ichs Ew. F. G. wissen lassen: damit sie durch<br>Ewer F. G. eigen abgefertigten Bothen (wofehrne mirselber keine gelegenheit<br>fürfelt dasselb zuuerrichten) der die Steige vnd wege wisst, mügen von hierab<br>geholet werden.</p><p>Ich hab an Ewer F. G. Mathematicum Rothmannum, in omnem<br>euentum, wan er bey Ew. F. G. were<br>widerumb ankomen, beyuerwartes Schreiben gerichtet. Da er sich aber noch nicht<br>hette widereingestalt, alsdan kan Ew. F. G. den brieff durch einen andern<br>erbrechen vnd verlesen lassen, vnd den einhalt darauß vermerken. Das fürnehmest<br>in dem selben Schreiben ist: Wan Rothmannus<br>die nehest in fine Decembris<br>geschehene Eclipsin ☾æ, durch Ew. F. G. Instrumenta per omnia sua momenta et alia<br>Requisita gantz vleissig denotirt hette,<br>das mir dieselbige Obseruationes mochten mitgetheilt werden.<br>Dan wie woll es alhie zur selbigen Zeit gar nicht klar gewesen ist, das man die<br>berürte Eclipsin hette obseruiren konnen, so hab ich doch den motum ☾æ durch<br>vielfaltige Obseruationes so<br>offt vnd lange inquirirt vnd dem nachgeforscht, das ich mich<br>versehe, derselbig foll meinen numeris in einer iusten concordantz, zu<br>aller Zeit eintreffen: vnd das ich also per<br>collationem obseruationis istic factæ, cum numeratione e proprijs<br>Tabulis in motu ☾æ die<br>Differentiam<br>Meridianorum iust gnug drauß schliessen müge. Im fall aber<br>Rothmannus zu der Zeit nicht were bey Ew. F. G. zur<br>stelle gewesen, gedencke ich woll, es werde Ew. F. G. Automatopæus, oder jmandt anders, dieß, so es<br>sonst alda klar gewesen ist, nicht versäumbt haben, das es mir alsdan gleichwoll<br>mochte mitgetheilt werden: Vnd so noch andere Eclipses für handen, die in verschiedenen<br>iahren seind vleissig obseruirt worden, es sey kurtz oder<br>langk verlitten, werrs all eins, mochte ich derer denotation,<br>wie vnd von welchen stehrnen sie genomen sein, auch woll gehrne theilhafftig<br>werden, vmb fürberürter vrsach willen.</p><p>Es wirdt ohn Zweiffell der Mesthlinus solicher obberürten obseruation etliche im<br>fürrahte haben, per altitudines stellarum<br>vel quacunque alia ratione sedulo<br>deprehensas. Da derwegen Ew. F. G. die gnedige versehung<br>zuthun wolte vnbeschwert sein, ihn hierumb anlangen zu lassen, würde er Ewer F.<br>G. dieselbige nicht versagen, damit sie mir alsdan auch konten zukomen. Dan ich<br>bin selber nicht mit ihm bekandt. Ich begere nur die nudas obseruationes, eben wie sie abgemerckt<br>seindt, vnd will woll selbft darnach die tempora hierauß nach meinen eigen ratiocinijs extricirn. Da auch derselb<br>Mesthlinus ♄num<br>circa annos 76. 77. vnd 78 darzu auch ♂tem anno 76 vnd 78 in situ acronychio ethliche<br>mahll vleissig denotirt hette, wolte ich gehrne, das mir<br>soliche obseruationes<br>gleichfals zustünden, auff das ich sie mit den meinigen (welche ich circa eadem tempora, iedoch nicht<br>so viell und volkomlich, als ich gehrne wolte, bekomen) zu collationirn hette. Ich habe sonst: derer Obseruationum circa Solis oppositum, ein gut<br>theill im fürrahte, ferme per totum<br>Eccentrici ambitum: gleichwoll ab er wolte ich gehrn vmb<br>sonderlicher vrsache willen, circa dictos<br>annos in hisce duobus Planetis ihre Apparentias acronychas<br>examinirt,vnd mit den andern, die ich für mich gebracht, conferirt haben. Er mag sie denotirt haben, wie er<br>will, siue intermediante Radio per<br>distantias a certis Fixis, siue per lineas rectas iuxta fili<br>extensionem, modo illi vsitato decussatim facta<br>animaduersione: so will ich doch eben woll drauß, so viell<br>als müglich ist, mich zurichten wissen, wan mir nur die Nomina stellarum, et ipsa obseruationis<br>ratio angezeigt wirdt.</p><p>Ich hab auch in Italiam vnd Hispaniam, so woll als auch in Poloniam geschrieben, das ich etliche<br>Eclipses Lunares præteritorum<br>annorum (die da irgends accurate celitus genomen sein) bekomen, vnd auß<br>diesen, auch den iehnigen, die ich auß Teutschland erlangen kan, Meridianorum Differentias,<br>rechter, dan bisher geschehen ist, erforschen, vnd also die Geographiam (dweill sie der<br>Astronomey, wie Ewer F.<br>G. woll bewust ist, nicht geringe handtreichung thut) in dem fall, souiel<br>müglich ist, restituiren mochte.</p><p>Ewer F. G. wolte ich auch gar dienstlich gebetten haben, das sie<br>ihrem Automatopæo oder Vhrmacher befehl geben<br>wolte, das, wan er irgendts einen iungen ledigen Gesellen wüste, der mit den<br>Horologijs vnd Automatis konte vleissig<br>vmbgehen, vnd auff meine Instrumenta<br>Astronomica warten, er verschaffe, das derselbig mit dem<br>ersten sich herin zu mir verfügte. So wolte ich ihn alhie zu solichem thun<br>gebrauchen, vnd so gut lohn geben, als er anderßw o bekomen konte. Dan mein<br>Goldtschmidt, welcher alhie auffem lande bey mir gewohnet, auff meine Instrumenta gewartet, dern selbst<br>mit eigener handt viell gemacht, vnd mir viell iahr hierin vleissig vnd<br>getreulich gedient hat, ist mir (leider) für kurtzer Zeit abgestorben, das ich<br>darumb an stat desselbigen einen andern geschickten Gesellen bedarff, welcher<br>hie zu lande nicht woll zu bekomen seind. Versehe mich, Ew. F. G. Automatopæus werde hierin das beste thun.</p><p>Mich verlangt, Ewer F. G. Obseruationes stellarum fixarum zu bekomen, vnd dieselbige mit den meinigen zu conferiren. Dan ich glaube nicht anders, dan Rothmannus soll je nach der handt damit<br>fertig sein. Was meinen ersten Tomum belangt, darin ich hieruon vielfaltig tractir, wirdt<br>Ew. F. G. denselben mit Gottes Hülff, in diesem zukunfftigen Sommer zusehen<br>kriegen, dan er mit etlichen andern sachen in Truck baldt verfertigt ist.</p><p>Ich hab Ew. F. G. Mathematico auch geschrieben von 18<br>Minuten, die in Obseruationibus<br>Cometæ anni 80mi mihi communicatis,<br>allenthalben mangeln, dan so offt der Comet per Meridianum gangen ist, das gleich 18<br>Minuten alda zu Cassell zu weinig genomen seind, da meine Obseruationes aber eodem tempore factæ, alzeit<br>totidem Minuta plus<br>haben. Woher ich erachte, es sey solicher defect durch einen<br>Quadrantem vitio Perpendiculi, tot<br>Scrupulis non rite directi, commissirt worden. Ich hab<br>hieuon mit Rothmanno, da<br>er hir war, selber geredt, vnd ihm dasselb per collationem vestrarum atque mearum Obseruationum<br>demonstrirt: der sich dan darob verwundert vnd keine eigentliche vrsach darzu<br>gewust hat. Hat ab er globt, so baldt er wider bey Ew. F. G. gen Cassell ankeme, diesen grundtlicher nachzuforschen.<br>Dan er sey damahls, da dieser Comet ist obseruirt worden, in Ew. F. G. Dienste nicht gewesen. Da es dan durch<br>den Automatopæum oder einen<br>andern versehen were, wolte ich gehrn einigen bericht haben, woran sich dieser<br>mangell zugetragen hette, ehe ich diese Obseruationes in descriptione istius Cometæ in brauch<br>stelle. Das aber in den meinigen Obseruationibus meridianis, in eodem factis keine Irrung<br>sey, weisen des Thaddaei<br>gleichfals zu Prag genommene Altitudines gnugksamb auß, die<br>mit den meinigen, habita saltem ratione<br>diuersitatis Horizontum, zimblich woll vbereinstimmen.<br>Ich will Ew. F. G. aber mit solichen vnd andern dießmahll nicht lenger bemühen<br>vnd auffhalten, sonder dieselbe, nehest erwartung ihrer gnedigen widerantwort<br>vnd bericht bey gegenwertiger botschafft, wan die von der Messe widerankompt,<br>hiemit in Gottes almechtigen gnadenreichen schirm zu langetwiriger<br>leibsgesondtheit vnd friedlicher Landtsregirung dienstvleissig befohlen<br>haben.</p><p>Dat. Vraniburg den Februarii Anno 1592.</p><p>E. F. G.<br>Alzeit dienstewilliger<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>manu<br>propria.</p><p>Weitergebe ich Ewre F. G. dienstlich zuuerstehen, das ich an Ew. F. G. Sohn<br>Landgraff Moritz<br>geschrieben hab, vnd seine F. G. dienstlich gebetten, das sie wolte<br>unbeschwert sein, mir ein Carmen in<br>commendationem Operum meorum Astronomicorum zu componirn, dweill ich gnugksamb berichtet bin, das seine<br>F. G. ein gute Venam<br>poëticam haben soll, vnd darzu lust trage. Versehe mich<br>darumb, es werde seine F. G. sich verdemutigen, ein solichs Carmen auff die eine seidt an<br>dem ersten bogen (da der Titul meorum<br>Tomorum, die ich<br>itzunder in Truck zu uerfertigen vnterhanden hab, mit dem Diplomate Cæsareo verzeichnet<br>ist, von welchem ich ihrer F. G. Exemplaria zugeschickt hab) mit eigener handt<br>anzuschreiben. Welchs mir sehr angenehm sein soll vnd semer F. G., wie ich<br>mich versehe, nicht vnrühmlich, darzu ad promotionem horum studiorum gereichen wirdt.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious, High-born Prince and Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, my gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Illustrious, High-born Prince, gracious Lord. Together with my ever-ready offer of service, I have not been able to refrain from writing to Your Princely Grace by the present messenger, who has come up for the impending Frankfurt Fair.</p><p>First, as concerns the elk [Elendtsthiere], for the procuring of which I promised my utmost diligence: I have them on order in several places in Norway. But since sailing has not yet begun, on account of the winter, I cannot so soon obtain any [definite] word about it. As soon, however, as one or more of such animals shall be made over to me — which I expect for the spring — I shall let Your Princely Grace know, so that they may be fetched away from here by Your Princely Grace's own dispatched messenger (in the event that no opportunity falls to me myself to accomplish the same), one who knows the paths and the ways.</p><p>I have addressed to Your Princely Grace's Mathematician Rothmann, <i>for every event</i> [in omnem eventum], in case he should have arrived back with Your Princely Grace, a letter enclosed herewith. But should he not yet have returned and installed himself [there], then Your Princely Grace can have the letter opened and read out by another, and take note of its contents therefrom. The chief matter in that same letter is: that if Rothmann should have noted down most diligently, by Your Princely Grace's <i>instruments</i> [Instrumenta], the eclipse of the ☽ Moon that lately occurred <i>at the end of December</i> [in fine Decembris], <i>in all its moments and other requisites</i> [per omnia sua momenta et alia Requisita], those <i>observations</i> [Observationes] might be communicated to me. For although it was here at that same time not at all clear [enough] that one could have observed the said eclipse, yet I have so often and so long inquired into the <i>motion of the</i> ☽ <i>Moon</i> [motum ☾ae] by manifold <i>observations</i>, and tracked it, that I am confident it will agree at all times with my <i>numbers</i> [numeris] in a just concordance; and that thus, <i>by a collation of the observation made there with the computation from my own Tables in the Moon's motion</i> [per collationem observationis istic factae, cum numeratione e propriis Tabulis in motu ☾ae], I may infer from it accurately enough the <i>difference of meridians</i> [Differentiam Meridianorum]. But in case Rothmann should not have been on the spot with Your Princely Grace at that time, I rather suppose that Your Princely Grace's <i>clockmaker</i> [Automatopaeus], or someone else, will not have neglected this, supposing it was otherwise clear there, so that it might nevertheless be communicated to me. And if there are still other <i>eclipses</i> on hand that have been diligently observed in various years — be it long ago or lately elapsed, it is all one — I would also gladly be made a sharer of their notation, [namely] how and from which stars they were taken, for the aforesaid reason.</p><p>Without doubt Maestlin [Mesthlinus] will have several of the above-mentioned [kind of] observation in store, <i>carefully obtained by altitudes of the stars or by whatever other method</i> [per altitudines stellarum vel quacunque alia ratione sedulo deprehensas]. If, therefore, Your Princely Grace would be willing, without trouble, to make the gracious provision of having him approached on this matter, he would not refuse them to Your Princely Grace, so that they might then come to me too. For I am not myself acquainted with him. I desire only the <i>bare observations</i> [nudas observationes], just as they were marked down, and I shall well know thereafter how to <i>extract</i> [extricirn] the <i>times</i> [tempora] from them by my own <i>reasonings</i> [ratiociniis]. If, too, that same Maestlin should have diligently noted down ♄ Saturn [Saturnum] about the years [15]76, 77, and 78, and likewise ♂ Mars [Martem] in the years [15]76 and 78, <i>in the acronychal position</i> [in situ acronychio], several times, I would gladly have such observations fall to me as well, so that I might <i>collate</i> [collationirn] them with my own (which I obtained about the same times, yet not so many and so complete as I would gladly have). I have otherwise a good part of the <i>observations about the Sun's opposition</i> [Observationum circa Solis oppositum] in store, <i>almost throughout the whole circuit of the eccentric</i> [ferme per totum Eccentrici ambitum]; nevertheless, for a particular reason, I would gladly have <i>examined</i> their <i>acronychal appearances</i> [Apparentias acronychas] in these two planets about the said years, and <i>compared</i> [conferirt] them with the others that I have brought forward for myself. He may have noted them down however he will — <i>whether by the intervening cross-staff, by distances from certain fixed [stars], or by straight lines along the extension of a thread, by observation made crosswise in that customary manner</i> [sive intermediante Radio per distantias a certis Fixis, sive per lineas rectas iuxta fili extensionem, modo illi usitato decussatim facta animadversione] — yet I shall all the same know how to set myself right from them, as far as is possible, provided only that the <i>names of the stars and the very method of observation</i> [Nomina stellarum, et ipsa observationis ratio] are indicated to me.</p><p>I have also written into <i>Italy and Spain</i> [Italiam vnd Hispaniam], as well as into <i>Poland</i> [Poloniam], that I may obtain certain <i>lunar eclipses of past years</i> [Eclipses Lunares praeteritorum annorum] (those, that is, that were somewhere <i>accurately taken from the heavens</i> [accurate caelitus genomen]), and that from these — as also from those I can procure out of Germany — I may investigate the <i>differences of meridians</i> [Meridianorum Differentias] more correctly than has hitherto been done, and thus <i>restore</i> [restituiren] geography in that respect, so far as is possible (since geography, as Your Princely Grace well knows, lends astronomy no small assistance).</p><p>I would also most dutifully have asked Your Princely Grace to give command to your <i>clockmaker</i> [Automatopaeo] or watchmaker, that if he should know anywhere a young, unmarried journeyman who could deal diligently with <i>clocks and automata</i> [Horologiis vnd Automatis] and attend to my <i>astronomical instruments</i> [Instrumenta Astronomica], he should arrange that the same betake himself in here to me at the first [opportunity]. So would I employ him here for such work, and give as good a wage as he could get elsewhere. For my goldsmith — who dwelt here with me in the country, attended to my <i>instruments</i>, made many of them with his own hand, and served me herein diligently and faithfully for many years — has (alas) died on me a short while ago, so that I therefore need another skilled journeyman in his stead, of whom there are scarcely any to be had here in this country. I trust Your Princely Grace's clockmaker will do his best in this.</p><p>I long to obtain Your Princely Grace's <i>observations of the fixed stars</i> [Observationes stellarum fixarum] and to compare them with my own. For I believe nothing other than that Rothmann must by now be finished with them. As concerns my <i>first volume</i> [ersten Tomum], in which I treat of this matter at length, Your Princely Grace will get to see it, with God's help, in this coming summer, for it, together with several other things, is soon to be completed in print.</p><p>I have also written to Your Princely Grace's Mathematician concerning <i>18 minutes</i> that are everywhere lacking <i>in the observations of the comet of the year [15]80 communicated to me</i> [in Observationibus Cometae anni 80mi mihi communicatis]; for as often as the comet passed <i>across the meridian</i> [per Meridianum], just 18 minutes were taken there at Kassel too few, whereas my observations <i>made at the same time</i> [eodem tempore factae] always have just so many minutes more. Whence I reckon that such a defect was <i>committed by a quadrant, through a fault of the plumb-line, not rightly set by so many scruples</i> [durch einen Quadrantem vitio Perpendiculi, tot Scrupulis non rite directi, commissirt]. I spoke of this myself with Rothmann, when he was here, and demonstrated the same to him <i>by a collation of your observations and mine</i> [per collationem vestrarum atque mearum Observationum]; whereat he marvelled, and knew no proper cause for it. But he promised, as soon as he should arrive back with Your Princely Grace at Kassel, to investigate this more thoroughly. For he was not, at the time when this comet was observed, in Your Princely Grace's service. If, then, it was attended to by the clockmaker or by another, I would gladly have some report as to whereon this deficiency arose, before I put these observations to use <i>in the description of that comet</i> [in descriptione istius Cometae]. But that in my own <i>meridian observations</i> [Observationibus meridianis], made in the same [period], there is no error, the <i>altitudes</i> [Altitudines] taken likewise at Prague by Thaddaeus sufficiently show, which agree tolerably well with mine, <i>at least when account is taken of the diversity of horizons</i> [habita saltem ratione diuersitatis Horizontum]. I will not, however, trouble and detain Your Princely Grace any longer this time with such matters and others, but, awaiting Your gracious reply and report by the present messenger when he comes back from the Fair, herewith commend the same most diligently into the all-powerful, grace-rich protection of God, unto long bodily health and peaceful governance of the land.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, in February, the year 1592.<br>Your Princely Grace's<br>ever willing-in-service<br>TYCHO BRAHE<br>with his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript.]</i> Further I dutifully give Your Grace to understand that I have written to Your Grace's son, Landgrave Maurice, and have dutifully asked his Grace to be willing, without demur, to compose for me a Poem in commendation of my astronomical Works, since I am sufficiently informed that his Grace possesses a good poetic vein, and takes delight in it. I trust, therefore, that his Grace will condescend to inscribe such a Poem, with his own hand, upon the one side of the first leaf — where the title of my Volumes, which I now have in hand to prepare for the press, stands set down together with the Imperial Diploma, of which I have sent his Grace copies. This will be very welcome to me, and, as I judge, not without honour to his Grace, and will moreover serve to the advancement of these studies.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince whose Kassel observatory was Tycho's chief institutional peer and rival. The full titulature in the address — Count of <i>Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda</i> — is the standard Hessian style and is preserved. (Wilhelm died on 25 August 1592, a few months after this letter.)</li><li><b>Document type / register.</b> A learned-court letter in chancery German, switching freely into Latin for every technical noun and verb (<i>Observationes, in omnem eventum, per omnia sua momenta, motum ☾ae, Differentiam Meridianorum, Apparentias acronychas, restituiren, conferirt</i>, etc.). The Latin code-switches are retained in the translation in [square brackets] after the English rendering, per protocol §2; technical terms follow the controlled glossary §5.1–5.2.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, Wilhelm IV's mathematician; he had visited Tycho at Hven in 1590 and (as the letter notes) had by 1592 effectively left Kassel service. Tycho is unsure whether Rothmann has returned, hence the contingency instructions.</li><li><i>Mesthlinus</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (later Kepler's teacher). Tycho stresses <i>\"I am not myself acquainted with him\"</i> (<i>ich bin selber nicht mit ihm bekandt</i>) — a hedge preserved exactly.</li><li><i>Thaddaeus</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), the Prague imperial physician-astronomer, whose Prague altitudes of the comet Tycho cites to vindicate his own meridian observations.</li><li><i>Automatopaeus</i> (Greek-derived Latin, \"automaton-/clock-maker\") = the Landgrave's <b>clockmaker</b>, the celebrated Jost Bürgi (not named in the letter); rendered \"clockmaker,\" with the term flagged. The parallel German gloss <i>oder Vhrmacher</i> (\"or watchmaker\") confirms the sense.</li><li><i>Goldtschmidt</i> — Tycho's resident <b>goldsmith / instrument-maker</b> at Uraniborg, recently deceased; not named here.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical notes.</b></li><li><i>Eclipsin ☾ae … in fine Decembris</i> — the <b>lunar eclipse</b> of late December 1591; cloud at Hven prevented Tycho observing it, hence his appeal for the Kassel timing as a check on his lunar tables and for deriving the <b>difference of meridians</b> (longitude difference Hven–Kassel). The glyph ☾ is kept in the source block and named \"Moon\" in the translation per §5.3; <i>☾ae</i> is the Latin genitive <i>Lunae</i> abbreviated on the glyph.</li><li><i>in situ acronychio / Apparentias acronychas</i> — <b>acronychal position / appearances</b>: a planet observed at opposition to the Sun (rising at sunset). Kept in Latin with the English; <i>acronychal</i> retained as the period technical term.</li><li><i>circa Solis oppositum … per totum Eccentrici ambitum</i> — Tycho's program of <b>solar-opposition</b> observations of the superior planets distributed <b>around the whole circuit of the eccentric</b> (orb), the method of determining a planet's orbital elements. <i>Eccentric</i> / <i>opposition</i> per glossary §5.1.</li><li>♄ (year markers 76/77/78) = <b>Saturn</b>; ♂ (76/78) = <b>Mars</b>; named in English, glyphs kept in source. <i>♄num / ♂tem</i> = the Latin accusatives <i>Saturnum / Martem</i> abbreviated on the glyph.</li><li><i>Radio … per lineas rectas iuxta fili extensionem … decussatim</i> — the <b>cross-staff</b> (<i>radius astronomicus</i>, glossary §5.2) and alternative thread-/sighting methods of taking angular distances; rendered descriptively.</li><li><i>Cometae anni 80mi</i> — the <b>great comet of 1580</b>, observed at both Kassel and Hven; the <b>18-minute</b> systematic discrepancy in the Kassel meridian transits is the crux Tycho attributes <i>vitio Perpendiculi</i> — a <b>fault of the [quadrant's] plumb-line</b> mis-set by \"so many scruples\" (<i>Scrupuli</i> = arc-minutes here). This is the clock-vs-instrument dispute documented in Dreyer (<i>Tycho Brahe</i>, on the Kassel observations) and Thoren (<i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>: \"eighteen minutes in their timings of the comet of 1580\").</li><li><i>ersten Tomum</i> — Tycho's <b>first volume</b>, i.e. the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (Vol. I of his projected great work, which would carry the comet and fixed-star material); \"soon to be completed in print\" is the author's own optimistic forecast (it did not in fact appear until 1602, posthumously) — preserved as written, not corrected.</li><li><b>Greek-derived term.</b> <i>Automatopaeus</i> (ἀυτοματοποιός, \"maker of self-moving things\") — the court clockmaker; flagged rather than silently Englished, with the term shown.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline — flagged crux.</b> The subscription reads <b><i>\"Dat. Vraniburg den Februarii Anno 1592\"</b></i> — <b>the day-of-month is omitted in the source text</b> (a blank or lost numeral between <i>den</i> and <i>Februarii</i>). The dataset `label` supplies <b>\"22 februar\"</b>; the slug encodes `…0220…`. I have therefore led the header with the <b>as-written</b> (incomplete) dateline — \"[—] February 1592\" — and appended \"(catalogued 22 February 1592),\" per protocol §3/§4: the catalogued 22 February is <b>not</b> substantiated by the source's own dateline and is recorded here, not asserted in the header. The date is <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept Julian until 1700; Hesse-Kassel likewise Julian in this period). The companion Magini letter adjacent in the dataset (1 Feb 1592 <i>n.s.</i>, Bologna) is unrelated.</li><li><b>Formulae (chancery German, §5.5).</b> Address <i>Mein gnediger Herr</i> → \"my gracious Lord\"; <i>Ew. F. G. (Ewer Fürstlichen Gnaden)</i> → \"Your Princely Grace\"; subscription <i>Alzeit dienstewilliger</i> → \"ever willing-in-service\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"with his own hand.\" <i>Franckfurter Messe</i> = the <b>Frankfurt Book Fair</b>, the channel by which Tycho's messenger travelled and by which the reply is to come back.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> the German original leaves the day blank, but Dreyer's facing Latin (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VI, p. 298) dates it <i>20. Februarii</i>, and Wilhelm's reply (VI, p. 300) quotes <i>\"ewer Schreiben sub dato Uraniburg, den 20. Februarij.\"</i> The day is therefore <b>20 February</b> (not the catalogue's 22); supplied editorially as <b>[20.]</b> in the Source and fixed in the header and translation.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VI, pp. 298–299 (dropped by the working transcription). Dreyer prints this Feb-1592 letter only in Latin (no German facing for the postscript), so the Latin is the source form. \"C. E.\" = Celsitudo Eius (His Highness, sc. Maurice); \"C. T.\" = Celsitudo Tua (Wilhelm).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> The postscript to Tycho's letter of 20 February 1592 to Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — not a separate enclosure but the letter's own closing paragraph, written directly after the signature, which the defective ingest had previously truncated at \"TYCHO BRAHE manu propria.\"</li><li><b>Landgrave Maurice ('Landgraff Moritz').</b> Wilhelm IV's son and heir; he succeeded his father later in 1592 (Wilhelm died that summer/autumn) and became known to posterity as Moritz der Gelehrte (\"the Learned\"), a patron and practitioner of poetry and music in his own right — matching Tycho's flattering claim that he possessed \"ein gute Venam poëticam.\"</li><li><b>The volume this concerns.</b> Corpus evidence (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, Cambridge 2007, discussing the dedication leaf) confirms Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596) was in fact dedicated to Landgrave Maurice. This 1592 postscript shows Tycho cultivating that dedication four years in advance, inviting Maurice to autograph a commendatory poem onto the title-leaf of the very 'Tomorum' he was then preparing for the press, alongside the printed Imperial Diploma (Rudolf II's printing privilege).</li><li><b>Errors corrected in the prior English of this passage.</b> (1) \"I shall humbly entreat\" wrongly casts as future what the German states as already done — 'gebetten' is past tense: Tycho reports he has <i>already</i> asked Maurice, in the letter to him he mentions sending. (2) \"I have long enough understood\" mistranslates 'gnugksamb berichtet' (\"sufficiently informed,\" not \"for a long time\"). (3) \"three copies\" of the Imperial Diploma is invented — the German gives only 'Exemplaria' (copies), no number. (4) The closing sentence \"Given as above in the letter\" has no counterpart anywhere in the German and has been removed as a fabrication. (5) \"upon the other part of the first page\" mistranslates 'auff die eine seidt' (\"on the one side,\" not \"the other\").</li><li><b>Crux.</b> 'ihrer F. G.' — the pronoun governing whom Tycho sent the title-leaf/Diploma copies to — differs from the 'Ew./Eure F. G.' used for the addressee Wilhelm throughout the rest of the letter. Read here as continuing to refer to Maurice in the third person (parallel to 'seine F. G.' earlier in the same sentence), i.e. the copies went to Maurice, not to Wilhelm; but an ENHG scribal variant of 'seiner' cannot be wholly excluded.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed a fabricated day-number ('the 20th') from the dateline; the source gives only 'den Februarii Anno 1592', with no day stated.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German and the heavy Latin code-switch are rendered securely and the technical astronomy is well-anchored in the corpus (the 18-minute 1580-comet discrepancy, the elk, the lunar-eclipse meridian-difference program all confirmed). The one genuine crux is the <b>dateline</b>: the source omits the day-of-month, so the catalogued \"22 February\" cannot be asserted from the text — flagged above and reflected in the header rather than silently adopted.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation (blank-day dateline) is resolved by §1 collation — 20 Feb, fixed via Dreyer's Latin + Wilhelm's quoted reply.</i></p><p>The letter's body (elk procurement, the Rothmann/Maestlin/Hagecius observational exchange, the clockmaker request, the 18-minute quadrant discrepancy) is unchanged from the prior translation, reads straightforwardly, and I found no errors in it on this pass. The postscript is now translated fresh from the recovered German and is secure in sense; the one open point is the referent of 'ihrer F. G.' (Maurice vs. Wilhelm), which I read as Maurice by grammatical parallelism but which a collation against the critical apparatus of Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia for this letter (or the Kassel/Landesbibliothek original) would settle definitively.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (not printed in Dreyer's TBOO), so per brief this was audited against source_text alone and would normally be capped at B — but a FABRICATION was found even without Dreyer access, which per the grading rule forces C regardless of that cap. Checked every proper name, number, date and place in the German against the English paragraph by paragraph: the elk order in Norway, the Rothmann letter and the late-December lunar eclipse, Maestlin's Saturn 1576/77/78 and Mars 1576/78 acronychal observations, the Italy/Spain/Poland lunar-eclipse requests, the deceased goldsmith and the clockmaker request, the 18-minute comet-of-1580 discrepancy at Kassel and Thaddaeus's corroborating Prague altitudes, and the postscript naming Landgrave Moritz and the Imperial Diploma — all faithfully rendered except the fabricated day-number in the dateline.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (<b>high</b>). The German dateline carries no day-number at all ('den Februarii', not 'den 20. Februarii') — the English inserts a specific '20th' with no counterpart in source_text. This is exactly the invented-day-number failure mode this audit was briefed to watch for; the '20' appears to have been pulled from the catalogue metadata (date field / slug) rather than from the letter's own text. At: “Given at Uraniborg, the 20th of February, the year 1592.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the fix rests entirely on the letter's own internal evidence, which is conclusive on this point: the German dateline as given in current_latin literally has no day-number between 'den' and 'Februarii'. Only the fabricated '20th' was removed; nothing else in the letter was checked or touched, since no edition-of-record collation was possible.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 17, "prev": "brahe-t_15910926001", "next": "brahe-t_15920402001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910831001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920402001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that elk are on order from Norway but winter has delayed shipping, promises to inform Wilhelm when animals are ready for collection in spring, and mentions writing to Rothmann.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920220001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920220002", "d": "1592-02-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 20 February 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET ERVDITISSIMO VIRO, D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO ILLVSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS<br>WILHELMI, LANDTGRAUIJ HASSIAe, ETC. MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO<br>PLURIMUM DILECTO. S. D.</p><p>AMIROR eruditissime et dilecte mi ROTHMANNE,<br>te, ex quo hinc discessisti, nihil prorsus ad me literarum dedisse, elapso ferme<br>iam sesquialtero&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; Anno. Neque enim existimo te tam inhumaniter a me vel exceptum, vel<br>dimissum fuisse, quin paucula verba recordationis ergo, vicissim scribendo<br>impertiri, aut saltem per alios me salutare, deque tuis rebus commonefacere<br>aequum fuisset. Scripsit semel atque iterum ad me Illustrissimus tuus Princeps,<br>atque vbi tam diu morareris miratus est. Verum cum ego ignorarem, quibus in locis&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; degeres, scripsi hac de re fratri tuo IOHANNI ROTHMANNO, qui te valetudiniarium in patria haerere<br>tandem significavit. At quoniam existimem&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; te interea ad Principem tuum reuersum esse, intermittere nolui quo minus<br>te his compellarem. Rogo autem, vt Eclipsin Lunae vltimam, si modo illîc&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; accurate obseruata sit&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;, mecum, quo ad totum Obseruationis processum, communices. Apud nos tunc<br>temporis non erat serenum, saltem Lunam biduo, et ante et post, cum esset circa<br>90. ab Horizonte Eclipticae gradum, sedulo obseruauimus, id enim solum&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; concedebatur. Et si quas alias Eclipses vel Cassellis, aut etiam a MESTLINO vel alijs diligenter annotatas, conquirere licebit, fac<br>vt earum nudas saltem Obseruationes descriptas, pro Meridianorum diuersitatibus<br>hinc inde rimandis conciliandisque obtineam. Expeto etiam Acronychas Saturni<br>circa Annum 76. 77. et 78. et Martis 76. et 78.&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; considerationes, quas proculdubio a MESTLINO per literas impetrabis, dummodo is talibus attenderit.<br>De 18. illis Minutis (quae, cum hîc esses, monstraui in Altitudinibus meridianis<br>Cometae Anni 1580. illic&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; obseruatis vbique deficere) cupio a te certior fieri, prout etiam<br>pollicitus es, quâ occasione idipsum contigerit. Velim insuper, vt mihi exponas<br>rationem eam facilime contexendi Canones Sinuum, quae a IVSTO BYRGIO vestro dicitur excogitata. Neque enim satis<br>assequor, an in recessu habeat id quod primâ fronte<br>pollicetur; Rogoque, tum de his, tum alijs, deque tuo rerum statu, mihi per hunc ipsum&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; hominem Bibliopolam nostratem e Nundinis Francofurdianis ad nos redeuntem,<br>affatim rescribas.</p><p>Vale.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Christoph Rothmann, distinguished mathematician of the Most Illustrious Prince Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, etc., his much-beloved friend — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I marvel, my most learned and beloved Rothmann, that since you departed from here you have sent me no letter at all, now that nearly a year and a half has slipped by. For I do not think that you were either so inhumanely received or dismissed by me that it would not have been fair, by way of remembrance, to share in turn a few words by writing, or at least to greet me through others and to keep me informed about your affairs. Your Most Illustrious Prince has written to me once and again, and has wondered where you were tarrying so long. But since I did not know in what places you were dwelling, I wrote about this matter to your brother Johannes Rothmann, who at length signified that you were stuck in your homeland in poor health. Yet since I suppose that you have meanwhile returned to your Prince, I was unwilling to refrain from addressing you with these [lines]. I ask, then, that you share with me — as to the whole course of the observation — the last eclipse of the Moon, if only it was accurately observed there. With us at that time the sky was not clear; we did at least diligently observe the Moon for two days, both before and after, when it was about the 90th degree of the ecliptic from the horizon, for that alone was granted [us]. And if it shall be possible to gather any other eclipses, whether [observed] at Kassel, or also carefully recorded by Maestlin or others, see to it that I obtain at least the bare observations of them written out, for sounding out and reconciling on this side and that the differences of meridians. I seek also the acronychal observations of Saturn around the years [15]76, [15]77, and [15]78, and of Mars [in] [15]76 and [15]78 — considerations which you will doubtless obtain from Maestlin by letter, provided he has attended to such things. About those 18 minutes (which, when you were here, I showed to be everywhere deficient in the meridian altitudes of the comet of the year 1580 observed there) I desire to be more certainly informed by you, as indeed you promised, [namely] by what occasion this very thing came about. I should like, furthermore, that you set out for me that method of most easily constructing canons of sines which is said to have been devised by your Justus Byrgius. For I do not sufficiently grasp whether it holds in reserve what it promises at first sight; and I ask that, both about these matters and about others, and about the state of your affairs, you write back to me fully by this very man, our bookseller, returning to us from the Frankfurt Fair.</p><p>Farewell.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from Uraniborg on Hven, to <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (<i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i>, c. 1550–after 1600), mathematician to Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel (protocol §5.6). Rothmann had visited Hven in 1590; the letter opens by reproaching him for a year and a half of silence since that departure. The letter is one of the set Tycho himself printed in the 1596 <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (Liber I), which is the edition of record.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>IOHANNI ROTHMANNO</i> = <b>Johannes Rothmann</b>, Christoph's brother. <i>MESTLINO</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (Maestlin, 1550–1631), then professor at Tübingen, Kepler's teacher; Tycho seeks his planetary and cometary observations through Rothmann. <i>IVSTO BYRGIO vestro</i> = <b>Jost (Justus) Bürgi</b> (Byrgius, 1552–1632), the Landgrave's clockmaker and instrument-maker at Kassel; \"your Justus Byrgius\" marks him as a member of the Kassel circle.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Cassellis</i> = at <b>Kassel</b> (the Hessen court observatory); <i>Nundinae Francofurdianae</i> = the <b>Frankfurt Fair</b> (book fair), via which the carrier bookseller travels (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical.</b> <i>Eclipsin Lunae</i> = the (last) <b>lunar eclipse</b>; <i>Eclipses</i> = <b>eclipses</b>; <i>Meridianorum diuersitates … conciliandae</i> = the <b>differences of meridians</b> (longitude differences between observing sites) to be reconciled — the rationale for collecting others' eclipse timings. <i>circa 90. ab Horizonte Eclipticae gradum</i> — \"about the 90th degree of the ecliptic from the horizon,\" i.e. the point of the ecliptic culminating / highest above the horizon; rendered literally and not smoothed, as the phrasing is compressed. <i>Acronychas … considerationes</i> = <b>acronychal</b> observations (a body observed at acronychal rising, i.e. rising at sunset, in opposition to the Sun) of <b>Saturn</b> and <b>Mars</b> for the stated years. <i>Altitudines meridianae Cometae Anni 1580</i> = the <b>meridian altitudes</b> of the <b>comet of 1580</b> (protocol §5.1).</li><li><b>The \"18 minutes\" crux of substance (not of reading).</b> <i>De 18. illis Minutis … vbique deficere</i> — Tycho reports that the meridian altitudes of the 1580 comet observed \"there\" (at Kassel) were found to be deficient everywhere by 18 arc-minutes, a systematic error he had demonstrated to Rothmann in person, and about whose <i>cause</i> (<i>quâ occasione</i>) he now asks. The number, the systematic sense (\"everywhere deficient\"), and the open question of cause are preserved exactly; Tycho's own request to be \"more certainly informed\" is a genuine hedge and is kept.</li><li><b>Canones Sinuum / Bürgi's method.</b> <i>rationem … contexendi Canones Sinuum, quae a IVSTO BYRGIO … dicitur excogitata</i> = \"the method of constructing <b>canons of sines</b> said to have been devised by Justus Byrgius.\" This is the Kassel computational method (Bürgi's <i>Kunstweg</i> / sine-table technique), connected to the additive–subtractive <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> technique then current at Kassel; \"canons of sines\" is kept literal per the controlled glossary, and <i>prosthaphaeresis</i> is not introduced into the translation because Tycho does not use the word here. Tycho's skepticism is hedged — <i>Neque enim satis assequor, an in recessu habeat id quod primâ fronte pollicetur</i> (\"I do not sufficiently grasp whether it holds in reserve what it promises at first sight\") — and the hedge is preserved, not sharpened into a verdict.</li><li><b>Editorial sigla.</b> The superscript <b>A</b> markers scattered through the text (<i>sesquialtero</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>locis</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>existimem</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>illîc</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>sit</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>solum</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>78.</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>illic</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, <i>ipsum</i>&lt;sup&gt;A&lt;/sup&gt;, and after the signature) are footnote/variant-reading anchors carried over from the printed edition's apparatus, not part of the letter's own text. They are reproduced in the Source block as printed (per protocol §4.2) and ignored in the translation. The underlying readings adopted are the unmarked text as printed; no variant affects the sense of the translation.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Edition spelling retained in the Source block: consonantal <i>u</i> for <i>v</i> and <i>j</i> for final <i>i</i> (<i>Landtgrauij</i>, <i>alijs</i>, <i>literarum</i>); <i>vbi</i>, <i>vt</i>, <i>vltimam</i> with <i>v</i> for initial <i>u</i>; <i>hîc / illîc / quâ / primâ</i> with the edition's circumflexes; <i>AMIROR</i> is the edition's printed form of <i>admiror</i> (\"I marvel\"), so read and translated.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The letter bears no dateline in the printed text supplied; the catalogue assigns <b>20 February 1592, Uraniborg</b>, which (Denmark being on the Julian calendar until 1700) is <b>Old Style</b>. The header follows the catalogued date because the source text carries no internal dateline to lead with; this is flagged here rather than asserted as in-text.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical content (lunar-eclipse and acronychal-observation requests, the 18-arcminute systematic discrepancy in the 1580 comet altitudes, and Bürgi's sine-canon method) is identified with the corpus. One soft point is named: the compressed phrase <i>circa 90. ab Horizonte Eclipticae gradum</i> is translated literally rather than resolved into a single modern construal (culminating point of the ecliptic vs. a 90° elongation reading), and the absence of an in-text dateline means the header date rests on the catalogue, not on the source's own words — both flagged above rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice available), so per brief audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Checked the full Latin against the English clause by clause: all numbers (sesquialtero anno/a year and a half; 90th degree; 18 minutes; comet of 1580; acronychal years 76/77/78 for Saturn and 76/78 for Mars) and names (Rothmann, Johannes Rothmann, Maestlin, Justus Byrgius/Bürgi) are rendered accurately with no invention, omission, or flipped sense found. No collation against Dreyer was possible or performed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 17, "prev": "brahe-t_15910401001", "next": "brahe-t_15940928001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900418001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15940928001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reproaches Rothmann for nearly eighteen months of silence since his visit to Hven, and reports learning through Rothmann's brother Johannes that he had been detained by illness.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920220002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920220003", "d": "1592-02-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 20 February 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Frühneuhochdeutsch chancery German, with heavy Latin code-switching) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGEN / HOCHGEBORNEN FÜRSTEN VND HERRN/ HERRN MAVRITZ, GEBORN LANDTGRAUEN ZU HESSEN/ GRAUEN ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA: MEINEM GNEDIGEN HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER/ Hochgeborner Fürst. Ewer F. G. seyndt meine Dienste nach allem vermügen jederzeit zuvor an bereit. Gnediger Herr. Wiewol ich mit Ew. F. G. weiter nicht bekant bin/ dan da ich Ew. F. G. Herrn Vater/ vor etlichen jaren zu Cassel besuchte/ zu welcher zeit Ew. F. G. noch gar jung war/ So hab ich doch gleichwol sieder der zeit von andern vielmals verstanden/ daß Ew. F. G. mit fürtrefflichen Fürstlichen Tugenden/ auch nicht mit geringer gelehrtheit soll begabt seyn. Vnd hat mir Ew. F. G. Herrn Vaters Mathematicus ROTHMANNVS alhie gesagt/ daß Ew. F. G. mein Name so sehr vnbekandt nicht sey: Viellicht auß den Schreiben/ die ich zu etlichen maln an Ew. F. G. Herrn Vater/ von Astronomischen Sachen dienstlich gegeben hab. Auß welchen vnd andern Vrsachen ich nicht habe vnderlassen wollen/ Ew. F. G. mit diesem meinem geringen/ jedoch diensterbietigem Schreiben zu besuchen/ In hoffnung/ Ew. F. G. werde es im besten auffnemen. Wan mir dan beyde von gedachtem Mathematico vnd andern/ die da gute Wissenschafft darumb haben/ glaubwürdiger Bericht gethan ist/ daß Ew. F. G. ohn dem/ daß sie in Hebraica, Graeca, et Latina lingua wol erfahren vnd geübet sey/ vnd andere Artes liberales in pretio halte/ ein excellente gute venam Poëticam, vnd grosse Lust zu solchem Poëtico studio haben solte: So hab ich mich vnderstehen dürffen/ Ew. F. G. dienstlich anzulangen/ daß sie wolte vnbescheret seyn/ per otium ein Carmen commendatorium in Opera mea Astronomica,quae prae manibus edenda habe zu componiren, vnd mir bey dieser Botschafft/ da die von der Franckfurter Messe widerumb zu rücke zeucht/ gnedich mitzutheilen. Ich versehe mich/ es werde Ew. F. G. solches keines weges inhonorificum seyn/ dan ich bin willens/ dasselbige gestracks auff das andre Blat in meinem primo Tomo (worvon ich drey Exemplaria des ersten Bogens hiebeygelegt habe) drucken zu lassen. Da derowegen Ew. F. G. sich wolte verdemüthigen/ dasselbige Carmen auff die eine Seite/ die da leer ist/ nechst für dem Diplomate Caesareo, mit eigener Hand selber zu schreiben/ vnd mir also eines darvon widerumb zuzuschicken/ daß ich es darnacher baldt nechstfolgendt einem andern (welches ich a Serenissimo Rege Scotiae, der auch ein trefflich guter Poët ist/ in gleicher massen täglich gewertig bin/ vnd auff der ersten Seit/ nechst dem Effigie, dieweyl er ein König/ vnd mit vnserm Königlichen Stamme beschwägert ist/ stehen wirdt) möchte imprimiren lassen: Erzeigte Ew. F. G. in dem nicht allein jren Sonderlichen gnedigen Willen gegen mich/ sondern auch ein wolgeneigt liebtragendes Gemüt gegen die herrliche Astronomische Kunst vnd andere hohe Studia, Vnd were es Ew. F. G. ohn das/ von wegen jres Hochgeliebten Herrn Vaters/ der zu dieser Kunst viel Jar her einen grossen Lust vnd gefallen getragen/ vnd hoch darein erfahren ist/ nicht vnrühmlich. Dan je vnd allezeit von Anfang der Welt/ sölche fürtreffliche Kunst/ von hohen mit reichem Verstande erleuchteten Potentaten, Keysern/ Königen vnd Fürsten/ wie auch insonderheit von Ew. F. G. Herrn Vater/ wie gesagt ist/ in grosser Liebe vnd Würde gehalten/ auch nicht ohn gebürliche Vnkosten befördert vnd gehandthabt seyndt. Ich zweiffel nicht/ Ew. F. G. werde gleichsfalls darüber zu halten geneigt seyn/ vnd jr Fürstlich Gemüt in solchen wissendtwürdigen Himlischen vnd hohen Sachen/ bißweylen von den Irdischen (die man doch tractiren muß) erhaben/ je lenger je mehr delectiren vnd acuiren, vnd daher gnediglich gewogen seyn/ meiner obangeregten Bitt bey sich stat sinden zu lassen. So baldt etliche von meinen Tomis durchauß verfertigt seyndt/ wil ich nicht alleine Ew. F. G. Herrn Vater/ dieweyl seine F. G. einen besondern wolgefallen darzu tregt/ vnd dasselb gnedigst von mir begert hat/ sondern auch Ew. F. G. etliche Exemplaria davon communiciren. Vnd wo ich Ew. F. G. nach meinem geringen vermügen sonst dienen kan/ erbiete ich mich williger dan willig/ vnd soll Ew. F. G. in dem jederzeit vber mich zu gebieten haben.</p><p>Wil Ew. F. G. in deß Allerhöchsten gnadenreichen Schirm/ zu langwirigem Wolstande/ hiemit dienstlich vnd getrewlich befohlen haben.<br>Datum Vraniburg, den 20. Februarij, Anno 1592.<br>Ewer F. G.<br>Dienstwilliger<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>INFRA HAS LITERAS TYCHO HOC SEQUENS IPSEMET SUBSCRIPSERAT<br>EPIGRAMMA.INCLYTE MAVRITI, sublimibus annue cœptis</p><p>    Si vacat, atque alio non tua cura vocat:</p><p>Nec pigeat nostros venâ illustrare labores</p><p>    Mellifluâ, tibi quam fautor Apollo dedit.</p><p>Non indigna tuo, nec ages aliena Parente,</p><p>    Qui Iura egregie callet amatque Poli:</p><p>Nec tibi dedecorum, quia Principis enthea, soli</p><p>    Haud obstricta Solo mens agat, alta volet.</p><p>Adde quod vna nouem Musas, quas diligis, inter</p><p>    Vrania est, primo conspicienda loco;</p><p>Hanc igitur modico si ornâris Carmine, fiet</p><p>Cum fratre et reliquo plus tibi amica choro.</p><p>    Ipse quoque (vt magnis liceat componere parua)</p><p>Si mea quid valeat Musula, gratus ero.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious, High-born Prince and Lord, Lord Moritz, born Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda: my gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Illustrious, High-born Prince. My services, to the full extent of my ability, are at all times beforehand ready for Your Princely Grace. Gracious Lord. Although I am not further acquainted with Your Princely Grace than from the time when, some years ago, I visited Your Princely Grace's Lord Father at Kassel — at which time Your Princely Grace was still quite young — I have nonetheless since that time understood many times from others that Your Princely Grace is said to be endowed with excellent princely virtues, and also with no slight learning. And Your Princely Grace's Lord Father's mathematician <i>Rothmann</i> has told me here that my name is not so very unknown to Your Princely Grace: perhaps from the letters which I have at various times rendered, in matters astronomical, to Your Princely Grace's Lord Father. For these and other reasons I have not wished to omit to visit Your Princely Grace with this my humble yet dutifully-tendered letter, in the hope that Your Princely Grace will take it in the best part. Since, then, credible report has been made to me, both by the said mathematician and by others who have good knowledge thereof, that Your Princely Grace — besides being well-versed and practised <i>in the Hebrew, Greek, and Latin tongue</i> [<i>in Hebraica, Graeca, et Latina lingua</i>], and holding the other <i>liberal arts</i> in esteem — is said to have an excellent good <i>poetic vein</i> [<i>venam Poëticam</i>] and a great delight in such <i>poetic study</i> [<i>Poëtico studio</i>]: I have therefore made bold to approach Your Princely Grace dutifully [with the request] that you would be willing, at your leisure [<i>per otium</i>], to compose <i>a commendatory poem upon my astronomical works which I have in hand to publish</i> [<i>ein Carmen commendatorium in Opera mea Astronomica, quae prae manibus edenda habe</i>], and graciously to convey it to me by this messenger, when he travels back again from the Frankfurt fair. I am persuaded that this will in no way be <i>dishonourable</i> [<i>inhonorificum</i>] to Your Princely Grace, for I am minded to have the same printed straightway on the second leaf in my <i>first volume</i> [<i>primo Tomo</i>] (of which I have enclosed herewith three <i>copies</i> [<i>Exemplaria</i>] of the first sheet). If, therefore, Your Princely Grace would condescend to write that same poem, with your own hand yourself, upon the one side which is blank, next before the <i>Imperial Diploma</i> [<i>Diploma Caesareo</i>], and so to send me one of them back again, so that I might thereafter have it printed shortly next following another [poem] (which I am likewise daily expecting <i>from the Most Serene King of Scotland</i> [<i>a Serenissimo Rege Scotiae</i>], who is also a remarkably good poet, and which will stand on the first side, next to the <i>portrait</i> [<i>Effigie</i>], inasmuch as he is a King and is allied by marriage with our Royal line): Your Princely Grace would thereby show not only your particular gracious good-will toward me, but also a well-disposed, affectionate spirit toward the glorious astronomical art and other high <i>studies</i> [<i>Studia</i>]; and it would, besides, not be inglorious for Your Princely Grace, on account of your dearly-beloved Lord Father, who for many years has borne a great delight and pleasure in this art, and is highly skilled in it. For ever and always, from the beginning of the world, such an excellent art has been held in great love and worth by exalted potentates, emperors, kings and princes illumined with rich understanding — as also in particular by Your Princely Grace's Lord Father, as has been said — and has been furthered and maintained not without due expense. I do not doubt that Your Princely Grace will likewise be inclined to uphold it, and — your princely spirit at times raised up, in such heavenly and high matters worthy of knowing, above earthly ones (which yet one must handle) — will the longer the more take delight and be quickened therein [<i>delectiren vnd acuiren</i>], and hence be graciously disposed to let my above-mentioned request find a place with you. As soon as some of my <i>volumes</i> [<i>Tomis</i>] are quite finished, I will impart several <i>copies</i> [<i>Exemplaria</i>] of them not only to Your Princely Grace's Lord Father — since His Princely Grace bears a particular pleasure in it, and has most graciously desired the same of me — but also to Your Princely Grace. And wherever else I can serve Your Princely Grace according to my humble ability, I offer myself more willing than willing, and Your Princely Grace shall in that matter at all times have me at your command.</p><p>Herewith I would dutifully and faithfully commend Your Princely Grace to the gracious protection of the Most High, for long-enduring well-being.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the 20th of February, in the year 1592.<br>Your Princely Grace's<br>Devoted servant,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><b>Below this letter Tycho had himself appended, in his own hand, the following epigram.</b></p><p><b>EPIGRAM.</b></p><p>Illustrious Maurice, grant your favour to lofty undertakings,<br>    if you have leisure, and your care is not called elsewhere:</p><p>Nor let it weary you to give lustre to our labours with that<br>    honey-flowing vein which your patron Apollo gave you.</p><p>You will do nothing unworthy of your Father, nor foreign to him,<br>    who excellently knows and loves the laws of the Pole [the heavens]:</p><p>Nor is it any disgrace to you that a Prince's god-filled mind, not<br>    bound to the earth alone, should act, and would fly aloft.</p><p>Add to this, that among the nine Muses whom you cherish,<br>    Urania alone is to be regarded first;</p><p>If, then, you adorn her with even a modest song, she will become,<br>    together with her brother [Apollo] and the rest of the choir, the more your friend.</p><p>I too — if small things may be set beside great — shall be<br>    grateful, should my own little Muse be worth anything.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7). At this date (1592) Tycho was assembling his <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i>, the first volume of which he printed at his own Uraniborg press.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Mauritz, geborn Landtgrave zu Hessen</i> = <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), then nineteen; he succeeded his father Wilhelm IV in August 1592, six months after this letter. The opening confirms the relationship: Tycho is \"not further acquainted\" with Moritz than from a visit to <b>Moritz's Lord Father, Wilhelm IV</b>, years earlier (Tycho's 1575 Kassel visit), when Moritz \"was still quite young.\" Throughout, <i>Ew. F. G. Herrn Vater</i> (\"Your Princely Grace's Lord Father\") is <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Tycho's princely correspondent and fellow-observer, here spoken of as living.</li><li><b><i>Rothmannus</b></i> — <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (protocol §5.6), Wilhelm IV's court mathematician at Kassel, who had visited Tycho on Hven in 1590 and could vouch for Tycho's name to the young Moritz.</li><li><b>Titulature.</b> The address gives Moritz's full style: <i>Landtgrave zu Hessen, Grave zu Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain vnd Nidda</i> — \"Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda,\" the standard Hessian comital titles, preserved per protocol §2/§5.5. <i>Ew. F. G.</i> = <i>Euer Fürstliche Gnade(n)</i>, \"Your Princely Grace,\" kept throughout.</li><li><b>The request — the central matter.</b> Tycho asks Moritz to compose, in his own hand, a <i>Carmen commendatorium</i> (commendatory poem) to be printed in the front-matter of the <i>primo Tomo</i> of his astronomical works — specifically on the blank verso (<i>die eine Seite, die da leer ist</i>) next before the <i>Diploma Caesareum</i> (the <b>Imperial privilege/diploma</b> Tycho had obtained). A companion poem from <i>a Serenissimo Rege Scotiae</i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), whom Tycho had hosted on Hven in 1590 — was to stand on the recto next to the <i>Effigies</i> (Tycho's engraved <b>portrait</b>), James being given precedence as a crowned king \"allied by marriage with our Royal line\" (James had married Anne of Denmark in 1589). Adam Mosley (<i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, 2007) treats exactly this letter, reading the offer as a status-exchange in which Tycho dangles proximity to an Imperial privilege and a royal poem to secure the patron's contribution.</li><li><b>Latin code-switching.</b> This is genuine chancery German, but Tycho switches into Latin for nearly every technical or bibliographic noun: <i>in Hebraica, Graeca, et Latina lingua</i>; <i>Artes liberales</i>; <i>venam Poëticam</i>; <i>Poëtico studio</i>; <i>per otium</i> (\"at leisure\"); <i>Carmen commendatorium in Opera mea Astronomica, quae prae manibus edenda habe</i> (\"a commendatory poem upon my astronomical works, which I have in hand to publish\"); <i>inhonorificum</i>; <i>primo Tomo</i>; <i>Exemplaria</i>; <i>Diplomate Caesareo</i>; <i>a Serenissimo Rege Scotiae</i>; <i>Effigie</i>; <i>Studia</i>; <i>delectiren vnd acuiren</i> (Latin verbs <i>delectare/acuere</i> given German chancery infinitives — \"take delight and be quickened/sharpened\"); <i>Tomis</i>; <i>communiciren</i>; <i>imprimiren</i>. All are kept in the source and shown bracketed in the translation per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Litotes / negation (tracked).</b> The letter's courtesy runs on understatement; the negations are reproduced one-for-one and not flattened: <i>weiter nicht bekant bin</i> (\"not further acquainted\"); <i>nicht mit geringer gelehrtheit</i> (\"with no slight learning\"); <i>mein Name so sehr vnbekandt nicht sey</i> (\"my name is not so very unknown\"); <i>nicht habe vnderlassen wollen</i> (\"have not wished to omit\"); <i>keines weges inhonorificum</i> (\"in no way dishonourable\"); <i>nicht allein … sondern auch</i> (\"not only … but also\"); <i>nicht vnrühmlich</i> (\"not inglorious\"); <i>nicht ohn gebürliche Vnkosten</i> (\"not without due expense\"); <i>Ich zweiffel nicht</i> (\"I do not doubt\"). Nine negations in the body, nine in the rendering.</li><li><b><i>williger dan willig</b></i> — \"more willing than willing,\" a chancery flourish of self-abasement; kept literal.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Vraniburg, den 20. Februarij, Anno 1592</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 20 February 1592.\" Denmark used the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1592 (protocol §3); the date is O.S. and matches the dataset label (<i>1592. 20. februar. Uraniborg</i>) — no in-letter discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The text-of-record is Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596) — the very volume the letter is soliciting verses <i>for</i> — corresponding to Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b>What it is.</b> An autograph Latin epigram — not part of the dictated/scribal letter body — that Tycho appended in his own hand beneath a German-language letter to the young Landgrave Moritz of Hesse-Kassel, six months before Moritz succeeded his father Wilhelm IV as Landgrave (Wilhelm IV died August 1592). The scribal heading identifying it as autograph is itself part of the recovered text.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel was Tycho's most important astronomical correspondent and patron among the German princes; Tycho later dedicated the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596) to Moritz in his late father's memory [Tier 1: Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (2007)]. This epigram shows Tycho already cultivating the son directly as the succession approached, urging him to try his own hand at astronomical verse.</li><li><b>Content.</b> The epigram flatters Moritz's poetic gift (\"that honey-flowing vein which your patron Apollo gave you\") and urges him to write in honour of Urania, Muse of astronomy — framing this as consonant with his father's own devotion to \"the laws of the heavens\" and as no derogation from princely dignity for a mind naturally inclined to fly high.</li><li><b>Crux.</b> Lines 7–8 (\"Nec tibi dedecorum, quia Principis enthea, soli / Haud obstricta Solo mens agat, alta volet\") turn on a compressed, possibly punning contrast between <i>soli</i> (\"alone\") and <i>Solo</i> (\"the ground/earth\" — perhaps also evoking <i>Sol</i>, the sun) that resists fully certain resolution; the translation given follows the most natural sense but the exact syntactic binding is not certain.</li><li><b>Closing self-deprecation.</b> Tycho signs off comparing his own poetic effort to Moritz's with an inverted Virgilian modesty tag (\"si parva componere magnis licet\", here \"magnis...parva\"), a conventional humanist topos.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure. The German is plain chancery prose, the Latin insertions are unambiguous, the persons and the documented patronage context (Mosley) are all confirmed, the dateline is O.S. with no discrepancy, and the negations are reproduced exactly. The only interpretive choices (e.g. <i>delectiren vnd acuiren</i> = \"take delight and be quickened\") are flagged in the apparatus rather than smoothed.</p><p>The prose framing and the general sense of the epigram are secure. One couplet (lines 7–8) contains a genuine interpretive crux around the wordplay soli/Solo, flagged above; the translation offered is defensible but not certain. No comparison against a printed critical edition of Tycho's minor Latin verse has been made. Upgrade path: check TBOO vol. IX (poetry) or a modern edition of Tycho-circle Latin occasional verse for a parallel text or apparatus criticus on this specific epigram, and confirm the letter's dating relative to Wilhelm IV's death in August 1592.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (German + Latin epigram, not in TBOO) so no Dreyer collation is possible; grade capped at B per brief. Audited published_english sentence-by-sentence against source_text (the German body letter and the closing Latin epigram to Moritz of Hesse-Kassel) directly. All proper names (Rothmann, the King of Scotland), the date (20 February 1592), the bibliographic detail (three copies of the first sheet of the primo Tomo), and every clause of the epigram checked line-for-line against the Latin (Inclyte Mavriti...Musula, gratus ero) with no unsupported additions, no dropped clauses, no flipped hedges. One idiomatic German phrase (\"wolte vnbescheret seyn\") is rendered loosely as \"would be willing\" but not verifiably wrong and not checkable further without tboo. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Introduces himself formally to Moritz, recalling a brief meeting at Kassel when Moritz was young and noting Rothmann's report that Tycho's name is already known to him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920220003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920312001", "d": "1592-03-12", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Bartholomaeus Scultetus — Uraniborg, 12 March 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica </i>· MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO M. BARTHOLOMAeO SCULTETO, GORLICENSI, RERUM MATHEMATICARUM PERITISSIMO, VETERI SUO AMICO PLURIMUM COLENDO. S.</p><p>EN! habes mi SCULTETE, operum nostrorum, diu a te atque alijs desideratum , primum Quaternionem, inscriptionem quatuor subsequentium tomorum eorumque contenta breviter complectentem. Hinc caetera ratiocinare. Addidi MYLIJ vestri succinctum Epigramma, sed in plerisque a me immutatum, eo quod prius illud nimis affectuosum atque insolens quibusdam videri posset; nec tali me dignor honore. Agas vero illi gratias pro suo candido et benevolo erga me meaque studia amore. Si mihi scripserit, ego par pari referre, et amicitiam cum isto inposterum colere, non tergiversabor. Monstra quoque vestratibus Bibliopolis hanc operum nostrorum inscriptionem, et, an aliquot exemplaria iusto pretio obtinere satagant, exquire, quidque pro singulis Alphabetis aut quaternionibus numerandum sit, cum illis transige, posteaque me, et de pretio et de exemplarium numero, quae habere velint, certiorem effice. Continebit quodlibet horum voluminum, quae quatuor omnino erunt, circiter sesquina Alphabeta, in charta quam regalem vocant, suntque plurimis Schematis atque Tabulis hinc inde conferta. Tuum itaque erit, nostris desiderijs morem gerere, negotium hoc fideliter, ac si tuum esset (quod et tibi confido) agere, meque per primam oblatam occasionem de singulis commonefacere. Vale, et praestantissimum illum MYLIUM meo nomine diligenter saluta. Iamque ambo feliciter vivite, bibite, valete. Propino vobis hoc die ☉, quo et hîc et isthic alibique potare solent fere omnes (rem scilicet Bacho sacram gratamque facientes) unum alterumque cantharulum, eiusdem Liaei liquore usque ad exuberantiam refertum, idque haustu etiam Bachico, licet non Philosophico, multo minus Theologico, quamvis dies Christianis sacer esse deberet: Ebibo autem pro vestra salute, quod et magnus BARTHOLINUS, nunc mihi domesticus, proxime assidens, lubenter satisfecit. Non crediderim illum artem hanc apud vos didicisse, cum in Patria eius Seelandia rudes ipsius non sint. Vestrum est, nobis iugiter et gurguliter par pari referre, etiam adhibita Musica, quemadmodum nos non sine buccinarum, tibiarum, fidium clangore et suavi concentu, haec pocula vobis vovimus. Iterum atque iterum vivite, bibite, valete, literasque has ad MONAVIUM Vratislaviam destinate.</p><p>Datae Uraniburgi d. 12. Martij Anno 1592.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most learned man, Master Bartholomaeus Scultetus of Görlitz, most skilled in mathematical matters, his old friend to be greatly honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>Look! you have here, my Scultetus, the first gathering [Quaternion] of our works — long desired by you and by others — which sets out the title of the four volumes that follow and briefly comprehends their contents. From this reckon the rest. I have added the terse epigram of your Mylius, but in most places altered by me, because that earlier version might seem to some too effusive and out of the ordinary; nor do I deem myself worthy of such an honour. Do, however, render him thanks for his frank and kindly affection toward me and my studies. If he writes to me, I shall not shrink from returning like for like and cultivating a friendship with him hereafter. Show this title of our works also to your booksellers there, and find out whether they are eager to obtain a number of copies at a fair price; and settle with them what is to be paid for each Alphabet or gathering, and afterward make me the more certain both as to the price and as to the number of copies they wish to have. Each of these volumes — which will be four in all — will contain about one and a half Alphabets [sheets], on what they call royal paper, and they are crammed throughout with very many diagrams and tables. It will therefore be your part to humour our wishes, to conduct this business faithfully, as if it were your own (which I also trust to you), and to keep me apprised of each point at the first occasion offered. Farewell, and diligently greet in my name that most excellent Mylius. And now, both of you, live happily, drink, and be well. I drink your health on this day of the Sun [☉, Sunday] — on which, both here and there and elsewhere, nearly all are wont to drink (performing, that is, a thing sacred and pleasing to Bacchus) — one cup and another, brimful to overflowing with the liquor of the same Lyaeus, and that too with a Bacchic draught, though not a Philosophical one, much less a Theological, even though the day ought to be holy to Christians: but I drink it down for your health, which the great Bartholinus too, now of my household, sitting close beside me, has gladly performed. I would not believe that he learned this art among you, since in his homeland Zealand they are not unschooled in it themselves. It is yours to return like for like to us, continuously and throat-deep, with music brought in too — just as we, not without the blare and sweet concord of trumpets, pipes, and strings, have pledged these cups to you. Again and again, live, drink, and be well; and send this letter on to Monavius at Breslau.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 12th day of March, in the year 1592.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, by his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>M. Bartholomaeus Scultetus, Gorlicensis</i> = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomäus Schultz, 1540–1614), mathematician, cartographer, and astronomer of <b>Görlitz</b> (Latin <i>Gorlicium</i>, in Upper Lusatia), where he served as mayor; a long-standing correspondent and friend of Tycho's. The title <i>M.</i> (<i>Magister</i>) is the academic \"Master\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> The \"first <i>Quaternion</i>\" (<i>primum Quaternionem</i>) is the printed specimen gathering — the title-leaf-cum-prospectus announcing Tycho's projected four-volume <i>opera</i>: the great programme that would issue (in part) as the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i>. Tycho is using Scultetus as his book-trade agent in Görlitz/Lusatia to gauge demand among the booksellers and to set a per-gathering price.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Mylius vester</i> — <b>(Caspar) Mylius</b>, a Görlitz man in Scultetus's circle, author of a laudatory <i>epigramma</i> on Tycho's works which Tycho says he has toned down. Identification of the praenomen is not secured by the letter; \"Mylius\" is kept as written.</li><li><i>magnus Bartholinus, nunc mihi domesticus</i> — \"the great Bartholinus, now of my household\": a member of the Danish <b>Bartholin</b> family then resident with Tycho on Hven, said to be from <b>Zealand</b> (<i>Seelandia</i>). <b>Crux flagged:</b> the famous Caspar Bartholin the Elder (1585–1629) was a child of seven in 1592 and cannot be meant; the person here is an older Bartholin (the epithet <i>magnus</i>, \"the great/elder,\" and <i>artem hanc</i> — the drinking — are jocular). The precise individual is not determined from the text and is left un-asserted.</li><li><i>Monavius, Vratislaviam</i> — <b>Jacob Monau</b> (Jacobus Monavius, 1546–1603), humanist of <b>Breslau</b> (Latin <i>Vratislavia</i>, modern Wrocław), another of Tycho's correspondents; Tycho asks Scultetus to forward the letter (and presumably the prospectus) on to him. <i>Vratislaviam</i> is the accusative of destination, \"to Breslau.\"</li><li><b>Bibliographical / book-trade terms.</b></li><li><i>Alphabeta</i> — an \"Alphabet\" is the bibliographer's unit of 23 gathering-signatures (A–Z, excluding J, U, W); <i>circiter sesquina Alphabeta</i> = \"about one and a half Alphabets,\" i.e. roughly 34–35 sheets per volume. Rendered \"Alphabet(s),\" glossed.</li><li><i>quaternio</i> — strictly a quire of four folded sheets (a <i>quaternion</i>); here used both for that codicological unit and, in the opening, for the specimen prospectus-gathering. Kept as \"gathering [Quaternion].\"</li><li><i>charta quam regalem vocant</i> — \"what they call royal paper\" (<i>charta regalis</i>, a large-format premium paper); Tycho's own hedging phrase <i>quam … vocant</i> preserved.</li><li><i>Schematis atque Tabulis</i> — \"diagrams and tables,\" the figures of the astronomical text.</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> <i>hoc die ☉</i> — the glyph <b>☉</b> is the symbol of the <b>Sun</b>; <i>dies Solis</i> = <b>Sunday</b>. Per protocol §5.3 the glyph is kept in the source block and named in English (\"this day of the Sun [Sunday]\"). Tycho's wit turns on its being the Lord's Day, on which he nonetheless drinks a \"Bacchic, not Philosophical, much less Theological\" draught.</li><li><b>Classical allusions (kept as metaphor).</b> <i>Bacho … Bachico</i> — <b>Bacchus</b>, god of wine; <i>eiusdem Liaei liquore</i> — <i>Lyaeus</i> (\"the Loosener,\" <i>Lyaios</i>) is a cult-epithet of Bacchus, here \"the liquor of the same Lyaeus,\" i.e. wine. <i>par pari referre</i> — the proverbial \"to return like for like.\" <i>iugiter et gurguliter</i> — a jingling pair, \"continuously and throat-deep\" (<i>gurguliter</i>, from <i>gurgulio</i>, the gullet, is a mock-coinage matching <i>iugiter</i>); the playful rhyme is signalled rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting\"); the triple sign-off <i>vivite, bibite, valete</i> (\"live, drink, and be well\") and <i>Vale</i> (\"Farewell\") per §5.5; subscription <i>manu propria</i> = \"by his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi d. 12. Martij Anno 1592</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> [on Hven], the 12th day of March, year 1592\" — Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The catalogue <i>label</i> date (1592.12. marts. Uraniborg) agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. The edition of record is the <i>Singularia historico-literaria Lusatica</i> (the Lusatian literary-historical miscellany in which the letter was printed); no manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register (learned, festively jocular) is matched. The one open point is the identity of <i>magnus Bartholinus</i> (an older member of the Bartholin family, not the celebrated Caspar of 1585–1629), flagged above and left un-asserted rather than guessed; the praenomen of <i>Mylius</i> is likewise not fixed by the text.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.326-328 as page images. Letter to Scultetus (\"CLARISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO M. BARTHOLOMAEO SCULTETO...\") is complete on p.327. Collated the publication-business details (Alphabeta/quaternion pricing), the Bacchus-toast passage, proper names (Mylij, Bartholinus, Seelandia/Zealand, Monavium/Vratislaviam), and closing date (12 Martij Anno 1592) against Dreyer -- exact match, including the easy-to-flip clause \"Non crediderim illum artem hanc apud vos didicisse... rudes ipsius non sint,\" which is correctly rendered with Bartholinus, not the addressees, as the one who learned to drink. Published grade was B (uncollated default); full Dreyer collation with zero defects earns A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15901208001", "next": "brahe-t_16000107001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends the first printed gathering of his forthcoming works with its contents table, and asks Scultetus to show it to local booksellers and negotiate terms for copies.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920312001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920314001", "d": "1592-03-14", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Dronning Sophie", "ck": "Dronning Sophie", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Hillerød", "t": "Queen Sophie → Tycho Brahe — Frederiksborg, 14 March 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588–92, fol. 102 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYGE BRAHE FICK BREFF NOGETT SMERLLE ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR.</p><p>Sophia.</p><p>WOR gunst thilfornn. Wider, att høgborne første och herre, her Wllrich, herre och første thill Meckelborg, Vor kierre herre fader, haffuer weed hanns schriffuellse aff Oß werritt begierrenndis, att Wi naadigst wille Hanns f: Naade forschaffe enn prøffue aff thennd jord, som kallis smerle, som glasbrenndere allthiid leyer, och nu nyligenn paa Huenn schulle verre funndenn, thi bede Wi ether och begierre, attj Oß wille vnnde och schiche enn ottinng fuld aff for:ne smerlle, huilched Wi sidenn Hanns Kierlighet wille thillsennde. Sammeledis giffue Wi ether och naadigst att wide, att Wi iche synnderligtt mere haffue aff thennd brennd antimonium, som i Oß senniste gaffue och thillschichede, bedenndis ether ther forre och begierre, attj Oß enndnu nogett aff for:ne brenntt antimonium ville giffue, huorudinnden i giør Oß enn synderlig thienniste och vellgefallenn.</p><p>Befallendis etc.<br>Actum Frederichsborg thennd 14. martij a:o 92.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tyge Brahe received a letter concerning some smerle, as follows:</b></p><p>Sophie.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that the high-born prince and lord, Lord Ulrich, lord and prince of Mecklenburg, our dear lord father, has by his letter requested of us that we would graciously procure for His Princely Grace a sample of that earth which is called <i>smerle</i> [a clay], which glassmakers always use, and which is now lately said to have been found on Hven. We therefore ask and desire of you that you would grant and send to us a <i>ottingr</i> full [a one-eighth measure] of the aforesaid <i>smerle</i>, which we will then send on to His Lordship. Likewise we also graciously give you to know that we have nothing much left of that burnt antimony which you last gave and sent to us, asking and desiring of you therefore that you would give us yet some [more] of the aforesaid burnt antimony, wherein you do us a singular service and pleasure.</p><p>Commending [you] etc.<br>Done at Frederiksborg, the 14th of March, year [15]92.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> (1557–1631), queen of Denmark, consort of Frederik II (d. 1588) and by 1592 queen dowager during the minority of her son Christian IV. She shared Tycho's interest in chemistry/alchemy and natural philosophy and visited Uraniborg; here she addresses him as a supplier of materials. The plain signature <i>Sophia</i> (without the <i>Wij … etc.</i> formula of a reigning king's letter) is the dowager-queen register.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (<i>Dronning Sophies tegnelser</i>). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…NOGETT SMERLLE ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning some smerle, <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Person named.</b> <i>her Wllrich, herre och første thill Meckelborg, Vor kierre herre fader</i> = <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), Sophie's father; \"His Princely Grace\" (<i>Hanns f: Naade</i> = <i>Hans fyrstelige Naade</i>) and \"His Lordship\" (<i>Hanns Kierlighet</i> = <i>Hans Kærlighed</i>, the chancery courtesy-title \"His Affection/Lordship\") both refer to him.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Frederichsborg</i> = <b>Frederiksborg</b> Castle (Hillerød, Zealand), a royal residence.</li><li><b><i>smerle</b></i> — kept untranslated, glossed \"[a clay].\" The text defines it as <i>thennd jord, som kallis smerle, som glasbrenndere allthiid leyer</i> = \"the earth called <i>smerle</i>, which glassmakers always use.\" This is the clay/earth used by glassmakers (Danish <i>smærgel/smerle</i>; the same Germanic root as English <i>smear</i>, and cognate with the abrasive <i>Schmergel</i>/emery family). The note that it had <i>\"now lately been found on Hven\"</i> ties the request to Tycho's own island. Not modernised, since the precise mineral is not certain from the word alone.</li><li><b><i>glasbrenndere</b></i> = \"glass-burners,\" i.e. <b>glassmakers / glass-blowers</b> (literally those who fire/melt glass).</li><li><b><i>enn ottinng fuld</b></i> — \"a <i>ottingr</i> full,\" i.e. an <b>ottingr</b>-measure full. The <i>otting</i> (literally \"an eighth\") was an Old Danish measure of capacity (one-eighth of a larger unit, e.g. of a <i>tønde</i>/barrel); rendered \"a one-eighth measure\" with the term flagged, as the exact volume intended is not specified in the letter.</li><li><b><i>brennd / brenntt antimonium</b></i> = \"<b>burnt antimony</b>\" (<i>antimonium calcinatum</i> / <i>crocus metallorum</i>), the calcined antimony preparation of period chemistry/medicine and metallurgy. The Latin technical noun <i>antimonium</i> is embedded in the Danish (a code-switch typical of the chancery's chemical vocabulary), and Sophie reports she has nearly used up the previous batch Tycho supplied — evidence of an ongoing material exchange between the queen and Uraniborg's laboratory.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1592).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wider</i> = <i>Vider</i> (\"Know ye\"); <i>haffuer … begierrenndis werritt</i> = <i>har … begærende været</i> (\"has been requesting\"); <i>attj</i> = <i>at I</i> (\"that you\"); <i>vnnde</i> = <i>unde</i> (\"grant\"); <i>schiche</i> = <i>skikke</i> (\"send, dispatch\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>huilched</i> = <i>hvilket</i> (\"which\"); <i>sidenn</i> = <i>siden</i> (\"then, afterwards\"); <i>Sammeledis</i> = <i>Sammeledes</i> (\"likewise\"); <i>iche synnderligtt mere</i> = <i>ikke synderligt mere</i> (\"not much more / nothing much left\"); <i>senniste</i> = <i>seneste</i> (\"last, most recently\"); <i>huorudinnden</i> = <i>hvorudinden</i> (\"wherein\"); <i>synderlig thienniste och vellgefallenn</i> = <i>synderlig tjeneste og velbehag</i> (\"singular service and pleasure\"); <i>Befallendis</i> = <i>Befalendes</i> (\"commending [you to God],\" the abbreviated closing formula).</li><li><b>Negation count.</b> The body contains a single negation — <i>iche synnderligtt mere</i> (\"not much more\"), reporting that the queen's stock of burnt antimony is nearly exhausted. Both clauses of the letter are positive requests; no command or condition is reversed.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Frederichsborg thennd 14. martij a:o 92</i> = \"Done at <b>Frederiksborg</b>, the 14th of March, [15]92\" (O.S.); <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5. The dateline (14 March 1592) agrees with the catalogue label; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the single negation is preserved. The only soft points are lexical, flagged rather than smoothed: <i>smerle</i> (the exact mineral/clay the glassmakers' earth denotes) and <i>ottingr</i> (the precise volume of the one-eighth measure), both kept in the source form with the term named.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.61-62 page images (letter no. 92, Danish, Sophia to Tycho, dated Frederiksborg 14 March 1592). Collated opening, closing, every name (Ulrich/Meckelborg, Sophia, Hven), the two measures (smerle sample, ottingr) and the antimony request against source_text and published_english. Source_text matches Dreyer's printed Danish exactly apart from ignorable early-modern spelling-doubling variants (e.g. ANRØRENDIS/ANNRØRENNDIS, gunst/gunnst) and an abbreviated year (a:o 92 vs Dreyer's a:o 1592) that is a style shortening, not a corrupted/dropped word. Published_english renders every clause faithfully, including the correct expansion of 'Hanns f: Naade' as 'His Princely Grace' and the ottingr/antimony requests; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Kongehusets arkiv, Dronning Sophies tegnelser 1588-92, fol 102 f.."], "th": {"label": "Dronning Sophie", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15900809001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Requests a measure of *smerle* clay from Hven for her father Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, and asks for a further supply of burnt antimony.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920314001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920320001", "d": "1592-03-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Hugo Blotius", "ck": "Hugo Blotius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hugo Blotius — Uraniborg, 20 March 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET ERUDITISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO HUGONI BLOTIO, I. V. DOCTORI ET CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS BIBLIOTHECAe, QUAe VIENNAe IN AUSTRIA EST, PRAeFECTO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.<br>VIENNAe AUSTRIAe POST DOMUM PROVINCIALEM.</p><p>REDDITAe sunt mihi his ipsis diebus tuae literae unâ cum Hagecianis, eximia quadam humanitate et erga me benevolentia affatim refertae. Asseris inter nos olim in Helvetiae oris notitiam aliquam contractam fuisse. Quae sane ut id, quod res est, affirmem, mihi iam penitus memoriâ exciderat. Idque cum ob ea, quae ex temporum et locorum longinquitate ipsemet adducis obstacula, tum quod diversissimis et quamplurimis, ex eo tempore (quod sane ad minimum viginti duos annos excedit) occupationibus et distractionibus implicitus, eorum omnium, cum quibus in Germania atque alibi inter peregrinandum familiaritatem aliquam contraxi, recordationem animo comprehendere, nisi forte per literas subinde renovaretur, haud sustinuerim. Videor tamen mihi cum quodam Iuvene Belga, ingenioso atque ad discendum alacri, Basileae (uti opinor) non Argentinae (prout tu vis) dum pauculis mensibus illic subsisto, consuetudinem aliquando inijsse; succurritque, iam me ibidem per otium puerilia quaedam Instrumenta Mathematica (cum alia in promptu non essent) tractasse et harum rerum cupidis eorum usum ostendisse, ac si quae superessent, illis non gravatim dono concessisse. Fieri itaque facile poterit, quod tu ipsus Belga fueris, cum quo inter alios de his iucunde egi. Pergratum vero mihi accidit, quod hanc, quae nobis tunc aliquantulum intercessit, cognitionem, utut tot iam interlapsis annis ferme obliteratam, per literas in mentem revocare non intermiseris. Ego hanc in posterum memoriâ solidius firmare et sartam tectam conservare haud negligam. Nobilem etiam et rerum usu eximie valentem virum GERARDUM RANZOVIUM, HENRICI filium, amicum meum fratris loco dilectum et non ita dudum affinitate coniunctum, tuo nomine salutare atque de notitia inter nos olim contracta commonefacere, prout expetis, non tergiversabor. Quin et Magnificentissimo Amplissimoque viro Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO Produci Holsatiae prima quaque oblata occasione scribens (quod et saepius mutuo facere solemus, cum et ille ijsdem, quibus invigilo, studijs impense afficiatur) vel ipsas tuas literas vel earum descriptum adiungam, ut filio mittens, tui recordationem, tam apud ipsum quam caeteros Holsatiae conspicuae nobllitatis viros, tibi quondam in adolescentia notos, sic promptius in memoriam revocem. Quod et apud ipsum GERARDUM, si illi prius quam Parenti eius scripserim, praestabo. Atque is rectius reliquos suos, de quibus loqueris, consangvineos novit, eosque tui memores reddet. Sunt enim, tum is tum etiam alij Holsatiae Nobilitatis eximij viri, praesertim ex ijs, qui exteras nationes perlustrando variorum hominum mores et actiones experti sunt, ab humanitatis officijs nequaquam alieni, ut non dubitem, ijs hanc vestrae conversationis memorationem acceptam futuram.</p><p>Scribis in Bibliotheca Imperatoria, cui Viennae praees, reservari ultra 90000 librorum volumina. Si itaque liceret et tibi nonnunquam otium suppeteret, optarem mihi Authorum illorum nomina, unâ cum librorum singulorum inscriptione, praesertim quo ad Philosophica, Mathematica, Astronomica, Medica et Chymica atque similia opera attinet. Idem quoque in Illustriss. Archiducis Austriae MAXIMILIANI atque aliorum, si quos nosti, Illustrium virorum Bibliothecis fieri averem; modo id bona eorum pace admittatur. Sin minus, nolo tibi periculum vel offensas mea de causa creari.</p><p>Quia vero Arcis Uraniburgicae, quam in hac Insula praecedentibus annis extrui curavi, mentionem facis, eius aliqualem repraesentationem tibi in adiuncta charta mitto. Licet ab eo tempore, quo haec ita in eum modum excisa sunt, nonnulla, praesertim quo ad vallum circundans et aedificia in eius quatuor angulis extantia, immutata et ampliora reddita sint, et Instrumenta Astronomica plura, tam in Arcis turriculis quam potissimum in Cryptis quibusdam ad collem extra huius moenia, e solidis lapidibus constructis, quae septenas diversi generis ingentes et affabre elaboratas ad Astronomiae negotium rite exequendum inservientes distinctim complectuntur Machinas, hincinde disposita existunt, adeo ut iam coniunctis ijs, quae in Arce servantur, mihi 28 ad minimum in promptu sint Astronomicae Restitutioni opportuna Organa, solertia, labore, sumptibus inaestimabilia. Quin et, ut aliquatenus etiamnum cognoscas, quid praeteritis annis his intermediantibus executus sim, quidque in re Astronomica redintegranda moliar, en habes Operum nostrorum, brevi, favente Coelestium Opifice, in lucem publicam evulgandorum quaternionem primum, eorundem Inscriptionem atque contenta brevibus exponentem. Addidi autem consulto terna eiusdem quaternionis exempla, ut si qui forte fuerint inter vestrates Bibliopolas aut etiam in vicinia, qui horum voluminum aliquot exemplaria iusto pretio obtinere cupiant, illis hanc librorum inscriptionem communicare atque cum ijs de numero Exemplarium, quae adipisci satagunt, deque pretio transigere, eo commodius queas. Quod et te meo nomine haud gravatim facturum confido. Erunt quatuor Tomi, quorum quilibet semiquinque circiter adimplebit Alphabeta, in hac Regali (quam vocant) uti vides, papyro. Suntque plurimis schematibus, numeris et Instrumentorum picturis hincinde referti. Tute igitur, qui Augustae illi Caesareae Maiestatis Bibliothecae negotijs praees, et rem librariam optime calles, facile aestimabis, quantis sumptibus haec in propria meâ, eâdem de caussa constructa Typographia, excudi curârim et quali pretio cum indemnitate nostrorum laborum atque impensarum venire merito queant. Quae omnia tuae prudentiae dispicienda, dijudicanda, exequendaque relinquo. Praestiteris mihi hac ratione rem imprimis gratam et Reipublicae literariae, cuius tu commodum gnaviter et honorifice procuras, haud, ut spero, inutilem, nec tibi ipsi indecoram aut sterilem. Ego enim ad vicissim tibi gratificandum et de te bene merendum promptus inveniar. Carmina, quae altera habet pagina, non semper illic locum invenient, sed ubi digniora quaedam a Principibus et illustrioribus viris, quae expecto, adeptus fuero, illa honoris caussa hanc primi Tomi pagellam occupabunt; caeteris in reliquos Tomos, prout cuiuslibet Autoris requirit status et existimatio, iuxta frontispicia debito loco digestis. Si Poëta es (uti procul dubio es) et te nostram Uraniam aut effigiem (cuius ideam praeter illam, quae quaternioni impressa est, aliam cupro subtilius expressam hîc adiunxi) succincto aliquo Epigrammate ornare, idemque apud alios, si quos nosti, in Austria argutos et acutos Poëtas, tua operâ impetrari, desiderarem. Vale. Plura enim non addam, ne nimium excrescat Epistola; tu prima quaque oblata occasione rescribendo saepiusque (quod et ego non intermittam) scribendo, amicitiam veterem renovare, augere, stabilireque ne supersede. Literas, si Pragam ad praestantissimum virum D. THADDAeUM HAGGAeCIUM miseris, is, cum mihi saepius scribere soleat, et tuas huc tuto diriget.</p><p>Iamque iterum atque iterum vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 20. Martij Anno 92.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most learned man, Master Hugo Blotius, Doctor of both Laws and Prefect of the Library of His Imperial Majesty which is at Vienna in Austria, his much-beloved friend.</b><br><b>At Vienna in Austria, beyond the Provincial House.</b></p><p>Your letter was delivered to me these very days, together with the ones from Hagecius, abundantly filled with a certain singular kindness and goodwill toward me. You assert that an acquaintance was once struck up between us on the shores of Switzerland. Which — that I may affirm the matter as it stands — had by now slipped entirely from my memory. And that both on account of those obstacles which you yourself adduce from the remoteness of times and places, and because, entangled since that time (which assuredly exceeds at least twenty-two years) in the most various and very many occupations and distractions, I have hardly been able to hold in mind the recollection of all those with whom, while travelling, I struck up any familiarity in Germany and elsewhere — unless perhaps it were now and again renewed by letters. Yet it seems to me that I did once enter into an acquaintance with a certain Belgian youth, ingenious and eager to learn, at Basel (as I suppose), not at Strasbourg (as you would have it), during the few months I stayed there; and it comes back to me that there too, in my leisure, I handled certain childish Mathematical Instruments (since others were not at hand), and showed those eager for such things their use, and, if any were left over, freely granted them to those men as a gift. It could therefore easily be that you were that very Belgian with whom, among others, I dealt pleasantly on these matters. It has indeed been most welcome to me that you have not failed to call back to mind by letter this acquaintance, which then passed somewhat between us, even though now, with so many years intervening, it is all but obliterated. For my part I shall not neglect henceforth to fix it more firmly in memory and to keep it whole and sound. Nor shall I shrink — as you request — from greeting in your name the noble man, eminently versed in affairs, <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b>, son of Henrik, my friend dear to me in a brother's place and not long ago joined to me by marriage-alliance, and from reminding him of the acquaintance once struck up between us. Indeed, writing at the first occasion offered to the most magnificent and most ample man, Master <b>Henrik Rantzau</b>, Governor [Produx] of Holstein (which we are often wont to do for one another, since he too is keenly devoted to the same studies on which I keep watch), I shall enclose either your letter itself or a copy of it, so that, sending it to the son, I may thus the more readily call your memory to mind, both with him and with the other men of conspicuous nobility of Holstein, known to you once in your youth. This I shall also do with Gerhard himself, if I write to him before his Parent. And he knows better than I those other kinsmen of his, of whom you speak, and will make them mindful of you. For both he and likewise other eminent men of the Holstein nobility — especially among those who, by surveying foreign nations, have gained experience of the manners and doings of various men — are by no means estranged from the offices of courtesy, so that I do not doubt this commemoration of your sometime intercourse will be acceptable to them.</p><p>You write that in the Imperial Library, over which you preside at Vienna, more than 90,000 volumes of books are kept. If therefore it were permitted, and leisure now and then were available to you, I could wish to have for myself the names of those Authors, together with the title of each book, especially as concerns Philosophical, Mathematical, Astronomical, Medical, and Chemical works, and the like. The same I should also be eager to have done in the libraries of the most illustrious Archduke of Austria <b>Maximilian</b> and of others, if you know any, of illustrious men — provided this be allowed with their good leave. But if not, I do not wish danger or offence to be created for you on my account.</p><p>But since you make mention of the castle of Uraniborg, which in past years I had built on this island, I send you some representation of it on the enclosed sheet. Granted that, since the time when these things were so engraved in that fashion, certain features — especially as concerns the surrounding rampart and the buildings standing at its four corners — have been altered and made more ample, and several Astronomical Instruments, disposed here and there both in the little towers of the castle and chiefly in certain crypts toward a hill outside its walls, built of solid stone, which severally comprise seven huge Machines of different kinds, skilfully wrought, serving for the due carrying-out of the business of Astronomy — so much so that now, joining to them those that are kept in the castle, I have at least 28 instruments ready to hand, fit for the Restoration of Astronomy, beyond price in skill, labour, and expense. Indeed, that you may also even now somewhat learn what I have accomplished in these intervening past years, and what I am attempting in the renewal of the science of Astronomy — behold, you have here the first quaternion of our Works, shortly, by the favour of the Maker of the heavenly bodies, to be published abroad, setting forth in brief the Title and contents of the same. I have, moreover, deliberately added three copies of that same quaternion, so that, if there should perchance be any among the booksellers of your parts, or even in the neighbourhood, who desire to obtain some copies of these volumes at a fair price, you may the more conveniently communicate to them this title of the books, and transact with them about the number of Copies they are anxious to procure, and about the price. And this I trust you will do in my name without reluctance. There will be four Volumes, each of which will fill about four-and-a-half [semiquinque] alphabets, in this so-called Royal paper, as you see. And they are stuffed here and there with very many diagrams, figures, and pictures of Instruments. You therefore, who preside over the affairs of that august Library of His Imperial Majesty, and are most expert in the book-trade, will easily reckon at what great expense I have had these printed in my own Printing-house, built for that same purpose, and at what price they may deservedly be sold, with indemnity for our labours and outlays. All of which I leave to your prudence to consider, judge, and carry out. By this means you will render me a thing especially welcome, and one not, as I hope, useless to the Republic of Letters, whose advantage you diligently and honourably procure, nor unbecoming or unfruitful to yourself. For I shall be found ready in turn to gratify you and to deserve well of you. The verses which the other page holds will not always find a place there, but when I shall have obtained certain worthier ones from Princes and more illustrious men, which I am expecting, those will, for honour's sake, occupy this little page of the first Volume; the rest being arranged in their due place in the remaining Volumes, according as each Author's standing and esteem requires, next to the frontispieces. If you are a Poet (as you doubtless are), I could wish that you adorn our Urania, or the portrait (an idea of which, besides the one impressed on the quaternion, I have here added, more finely rendered in copper), with some terse Epigram, and that the same be obtained through your effort from others — if you know any in Austria — clever and acute Poets. Farewell. For I shall add no more, lest the Letter swell too much; do not you forbear, by writing back at the first occasion offered and by writing often (which I too shall not neglect), to renew, augment, and stabilise our old friendship. As for letters, if you send them to Prague to that most excellent man Master <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b>, he — since he is wont to write to me often — will safely direct yours hither too.</p><p>And now again and again, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 20 March, in the year [15]92.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7). The subscription <i>manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand\") and the autograph signature mark this as a personally signed letter.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Hugo Blotius</i> = <b>Hugo Blotius</b> (Hugo Blotz, 1533–1608), the Dutch humanist who from 1575 was the first formally-appointed custodian (Prefect) of the Imperial Court Library in Vienna. <i>I. V. Doctori</i> = <i>Iuris Utriusque Doctori</i>, \"Doctor of both Laws\" (civil and canon). The two had been acquainted in their student days at Basel, where Blotius was involved with Tycho in instrument-/quadrant-making — which is exactly the episode the letter half-remembers (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>).</li><li><b>Address line.</b> <i>VIENNAe AUSTRIAe POST DOMUM PROVINCIALEM</i> — \"At Vienna in Austria, beyond [behind] the Provincial House (Landhaus),\" i.e. the postal/topographical direction of Blotius's residence relative to the Lower-Austrian Estates' building. Rendered literally; \"Provincial House\" = the Landhaus.</li><li><b><i>notitia in Helvetiae oris</b></i> — the disputed first meeting \"on the shores of Switzerland.\" Tycho's recollection is explicitly <b>hedged</b>: he says it had \"slipped entirely from my memory,\" that he <i>seems</i> to himself (<i>videor mihi</i>) to have met a Belgian youth, and corrects the place — <i>Basileae (uti opinor) non Argentinae (prout tu vis)</i>, \"at <b>Basel</b> (as I suppose), not at <b>Strasbourg</b> (as you would have it).\" <i>Argentina</i> = Strasbourg (Argentoratum). The disagreement between the two men over the place is preserved, not adjudicated; Tycho's qualifiers (<i>uti opinor</i>, <i>Fieri facile poterit</i>) are kept.</li><li><b><i>viginti duos annos</b></i> — \"at least twenty-two years.\" Tycho's own reckoning of the interval; counting back from 1592 places the meeting c. 1570, consistent with his second peregrination through Basel.</li><li><b><i>puerilia quaedam Instrumenta Mathematica</b></i> — \"certain childish [i.e. elementary, rudimentary] Mathematical Instruments.\" <i>Puerilia</i> is self-deprecating (\"childish/boyish\"), not a claim about the instruments' owners; kept literal. <i>Instrumenta / Organa</i> both rendered <b>instrument(s)</b> per §5.2.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>GERARDUM RANZOVIUM, HENRICI filium</i> = <b>Gerhard (Gert) Rantzau</b> (1558–1627), son of Heinrich; later himself Governor of Holstein. <i>affinitate coniunctum</i> (\"joined by marriage-alliance\") flags a recent kinship tie; rendered \"joined to me by marriage-alliance.\"</li><li><i>HENRICO RANZOVIO Produci Holsatiae</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598/9), royal Governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein, the great bibliophile, astrologer, and patron — a frequent correspondent of Tycho. <i>Produx</i> (here in the ablative <i>Produci</i>) is a learned Latinism for the office of <i>dux/governor</i>; rendered \"Governor [Produx],\" the Latin term flagged because it is non-standard.</li><li><i>Illustriss. Archiducis Austriae MAXIMILIANI</i> = <b>Archduke Maximilian III of Austria</b> (1558–1618, \"the Deutschmeister\"), Habsburg prince and book-collector.</li><li><i>D. THADDAeUM HAGGAeCIUM</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), the imperial physician and astronomer at Prague, Tycho's principal correspondent-relay in the Empire; the letter routes return mail through him. The \"Hagecianis [literae]\" of the opening line are letters that arrived from Hagecius in the same packet.</li><li><b>Astronomy / instruments (§5.1–5.2).</b> <i>Astronomicae Restitutioni opportuna Organa</i> = \"instruments fit for the <b>Restoration of Astronomy</b>\" — <i>restitutio astronomiae</i>, Tycho's programmatic phrase (§5.1), kept as \"Restoration of Astronomy\"; likewise <i>in re Astronomica redintegranda</i> = \"in the renewal of the science of Astronomy.\" The count <i>28 ad minimum … Organa</i> and the <i>septenas … Machinas</i> (the seven great machines housed in the stone <i>Cryptae</i> — the underground vaults of <b>Stjerneborg</b>, \"toward a hill outside [Uraniborg's] walls\") are reproduced as written.</li><li><b>The engraving and the printed prospectus.</b> <i>aliqualem repraesentationem … in adiuncta charta</i> — Tycho encloses a printed view of Uraniborg, noting it is now partly out of date. <i>Operum nostrorum … quaternionem primum</i> = \"the first quaternion of our Works\" — a printed sample gathering (one <i>quaternio</i> = a four-sheet gathering) advertising the planned four-volume edition; <i>terna … exempla</i> = three copies sent for circulation to booksellers. <i>Regali … papyro</i> = \"Royal paper\" (a large paper format), <i>quam vocant</i> \"so-called\" flagged as Tycho's own gloss. <i>semiquinque … Alphabeta</i>: each volume \"fills about four-and-a-half <i>alphabets</i>,\" an <i>alphabetum</i> being a printer's count of gatherings signed through the 23-letter signature sequence; <i>semiquinque</i> (\"four-and-a-half,\" lit. \"five-minus-a-half\") rendered literally and glossed. <i>propria meâ … Typographia</i> = Tycho's own printing-house on Hven.</li><li><b><i>Carmina … nostram Uraniam aut effigiem … Epigrammate</b></i> — Tycho solicits a complimentary <b>epigram</b> (verse) from Blotius for the frontispiece, to honour his <b>Urania</b> (the figure/Muse, here standing for the work and the press's emblem) or his portrait, of which a finer copper engraving is also enclosed. The request for verse is kept as a request for verse, per protocol.</li><li><b><i>Reipublicae literariae</b></i> — the <b>Republic of Letters</b>, the international scholarly commonwealth; rendered with the standing English term.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (§5.5).</b> Heading <i>…AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO</i> = \"his much-beloved friend.\" <i>Vale … Iamque iterum atque iterum vale</i> = \"Farewell … And now again and again, farewell.\" <i>manu propria</i> = \"with his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 20. Martij Anno 92</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 20 March, year [15]92\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1592. 20. marts. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. The N.S. equivalent would be 30 March 1592, but the letter is not so marked and the date is left as written.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 329) reads <i>ultra 90000 librorum volumina</i> — <b>90,000</b> volumes of the Imperial Library, corroborated by Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, p. 184. The working transcription's <i>9000</i> had dropped a digit; corrected in the Source block and translation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English translation: 'quires' corrected to 'alphabets' — the Latin <i>Alphabeta</i> (confirmed against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.329-330) is the period printing-trade unit for a complete run of signatures, not a single small quire; the old rendering understated the bulk of the four tomes.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout. The flagged soft points, none of which is smoothed: (i) the Basel-vs-Strasbourg first-meeting, which Tycho himself leaves unresolved and hedged; (ii) the non-standard title <i>Produx</i> for the Holstein governorship; (iii) the printer's-count idioms <i>semiquinque Alphabeta</i> and <i>quaternio</i>, rendered literally and glossed rather than converted to a modern sheet-count.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 collation corrected the 90000 digit; residue is only Tycho's own hedged first-meeting + declared printer idioms — no textual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the whole letter — salutation, every proper name (Blotius, Gerhard/Henrik Rantzau, Archduke Maximilian, Hagecius), every number (90,000 library volumes, 28 instruments, seven crypt machines, four tomes), and the closing/date/signature — against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.328-330 (p.327 also read to confirm the letter boundary). The Latin source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly throughout: no TEXT_ERROR. One MISTRANSLATION found and confirmed against Dreyer's printed Latin: 'Alphabeta' rendered as 'quires', materially misstating the book-trade sizing unit Tycho uses for the forthcoming volumes.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Alphabetum is the period printing-trade unit for a full run of signatures (roughly two dozen gatherings), not a quire (one small gathering); rendering it as 'quires' badly understates the physical bulk of the four tomes Tycho is describing to the Imperial Librarian in order to help him arrange sales — exactly the kind of bibliographic detail a scholar or cataloguer would rely on. At: “each of which will fill about four-and-a-half [semiquinque] quires”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image TBOO VII p.329-330 (slice pages 3-4 of 5), which reads 'Erunt quatuor Tomi, quorum quilibet femiquinque circiter adimplebit Alphabeta, in hac Regali (quam vocant) uti vides, papyro' — matching current_latin verbatim, so no Latin change was needed. Only the English mistranslation of 'Alphabeta' as 'quires' required correction. The remainder of the letter was not re-collated beyond the single defect site per the prime rule.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Hugo Blotius", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15991214001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to Blotius's letter, acknowledging that their Swiss acquaintance of more than twenty-two years earlier had entirely slipped from memory, and welcomes renewed contact.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920320001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920320002", "d": "1592-03-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young — Uraniborg, March 1592 [O.S.] (catalogued 20 March 1592)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae </i>1588–1592) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77 (Afskrift / scribal copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIOTYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>QUI fiat, nobilissime et excellentissime D. PETRE IUNI, quod ad meas bis vel ter ad te datas, plane surdus et mutus videare, equidem ne suspicari quidem, nedum scire liquet. Si enim aliquanto locorum intervallo, Oceani interfluxu disiungamur, attamen cum satis frequens hincinde fiat navigatio, quam et affinitatis inter haec duo regna necessitudo crebriorem oportunioremque reddit, nihil hinc suppetit ad excusandum subsidij. Gravibus quidem et multiplicibus in Aula et Repub. distraheris negotijs. Verum haec tot tantaque esse nequeunt, quin semel arrepto calamo TYCHONI Dano, amico nequaquam fucato atque in horas mutabili, vel paucissimis respondere concederetur. TYCHONEM autem dico, ne, si forte idem tibi accidat, quod illa apud OVIDIUM de suo amatore conqueritur, ipsum quoque nomen sui oblivioni dedisse metuens, ut sic eius verba invertam: Forsitan et quis fit TYCHO vel unde roges, ut sic tibi mei memoriam nonnihil, ut apparet, obliteratam in animum revocem. De quibus autem ad te iam aliquoties scripsi, haec ferme erant: ut BUCHANANI vitam sponte dudum promissam describi, transmittique curare non gravarere: inprimis vero, ut privilegium a Serenissimo Rege vestro tua authoritate atque intercessione mihi procurares: quo caveretur, ne ullum meorum operum hîc impressorum in ipsius Maiestatis regno venderetur, aut absque mea permissione divenderetur. Misique (ni fallor) diplomatis Caesariani, eodem nomine quoad fines Romani Imperij clementer concessi, apographum, quod et nunc facio, si forte alterum non est redditum: non tam ut hinc conspicias, quo tenore conficiendum sit (ipsemet enim pro ea, qua vales, prudentia et eloquendi eximia facultate facile verba invenies, quibus res haec peragatur exorneturque) quam ut huius exemplo promptius Regi vestro persuadeatur, ne in hoc quod peto benevole etiam concedendo difficilem se praebeat: cum et apud eos, quibus de facie ignotus sum, talia minimo negotio impetrârim. IACOBUS enim CURTIUS, Imperij Vicecancellarius, vir praecipuae in Aula Caesariana authoritatis et in literis politioribus, adeoque ipsa Mathesi excellenter versatus, hoc Caesaris privilegium mihi sponte propria procuravit, conscripsit, suisque sumptibus conducto tabellario transmisit, ut ex Literarum ipsius unâ additarum exemplari, quod hîc simul habes, videre licet. Te igitur mihi, quem (modo recordari vacat) ex aliquantulo in hac insula Uraniburgica congressu cognitum habes, hac in parte non defuturum confido. Si in Icona nostram libris edendis praefigendam, acuto et succincto aliquo epigrammate luseris, rem utrique, uti spero, nostrum non inhonorificam praestiteris. Idem petij a magnifico D. Cancellario IOHANNE METELLANO, cuius in poësi argutum ingenium vel paucula illa carmina ostendunt, quae ianuae conclavis vestri Regis, cum hîc unâ adesset, utut inter circumbibendum et colloquendum, obiter quidem, sed non sine nervoso acumine, discedensque mnemosyni loco scripsit, quae etiamnum illic spectantur. Mirifice placet carmen, quod serenissima Maiestas Regia HENRICO GYLD[ENSTIERN], Praefecto Bahusensi, affini meo dilecto, inter transeundum, elogij gratia exaravit. Et vivida in hoc ad Poësin cernitur affectio, ingenijque acumen pari solertia coniunctum. Si non nimium audax censerer, submisse optarem, ab ipsius Maiestate et mihi aliquid de ipsius melliflua et Parnassiaca vena clementer impertiri. Quod ut mihi meisque lucubrationibus honorabile, sic etiam ipsius Maiestati apud eruditos et cordatos censores non indecorum reputabitur. Tua intercessione atque suasu votis meis hac in parte non gravatim (prout confido) satisfiet: hacque ratione me tibi adhuc devinctiorem reddideris. Rogâram in alteris, ut ministrum aliquem, natione Scotum, qui hortulanum ageret, unumque, si fieri posset, aucupem mihi honeste hîc sustentandum mittere satageres, quod et nunc ut praestes † petoque, quamprimum libera et commoda huc concedatur navigatio, eiusmodi hominem unum vel alterum e vestratibus nanciscar. Quin et sic desiderijs meis belle subveneris. Quod si quid in hac nostra patria fuerit, in quo vicissim tibi gratificari per me concedatur, tuum erit ne rogare quidem, sed verbulo solum, quicquid in votis habueris, indicare. Experiere me in exequendo alacrem et promptum.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 20 Martij A. 1592.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Peter Young, Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>How it comes about, most noble and most excellent Master Peter Young, that to my letters sent to you two or three times you seem quite deaf and dumb, I cannot even guess, let alone clearly know. For although we are separated by a certain distance of place, with the Ocean flowing between, yet, since voyaging back and forth is frequent enough — and the bond of kinship between these two kingdoms makes it the more frequent and the more convenient — no support for an excuse is to be had from this quarter. You are, to be sure, pulled this way and that by grave and manifold affairs at Court and in the commonwealth. But these cannot be so many and so great that, the pen once snatched up, you should not be allowed to answer Tycho the Dane — a friend by no means feigned, or changeable by the hour — in however few words. And I say \"Tycho,\" lest, if perchance the same should befall you that she in Ovid complains of regarding her lover, I have to fear that you have consigned to oblivion even his very name; so that I may thus invert his words: \"Perhaps you may ask too who Tycho is, or whence\" — that I may in this way call back into your mind my memory, somewhat blotted out, as it appears. Now the matters about which I have already written to you several times were roughly these: that you would not find it burdensome to see to it that the Life of Buchanan, long since freely promised, be transcribed and sent across; but above all, that by your authority and intercession you would procure me a privilege from your Most Serene King, whereby it should be provided that none of my works printed here be sold within His Majesty's kingdom, or vended without my permission. And I sent (unless I am mistaken) a copy of the Imperial diploma graciously granted on the same account so far as the bounds of the Roman Empire extend — which I do now also, in case the other one has perchance not been delivered: not so much that you may discern from it in what terms the thing is to be drawn up (for you yourself, with that prudence in which you are strong and your outstanding faculty of expression, will easily find words by which this matter may be carried through and adorned), as that, by this example, your King may the more readily be persuaded not to show himself difficult about graciously granting what I ask — since even among those to whom I am unknown by face I have obtained such things with the least trouble. For Jacob Kurtz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, a man of foremost authority at the Imperial Court and excellently versed in the more polished letters, and indeed in mathematics itself, procured this privilege of the Emperor for me of his own accord, drew it up, and at his own expense sent it across by a hired courier — as may be seen from the copy of his own letter added herewith, which you have here at the same time. I am confident, therefore, that in this matter you will not fail me — you who (if only there be leisure to recall it) came to know me from a brief meeting on this island of Uraniborg. If you should play with some sharp and pithy epigram to be prefixed to my Icon [portrait], to be set before the books I am bringing out, you would, as I hope, render a service not without honour to us both. The same I have asked of the magnificent Master Chancellor John Maitland, whose keen wit in poetry even those few verses show that he wrote at the door of your King's chamber, when he was here in company — in passing, to be sure, amid the drinking-round and the conversing, yet not without sinewy acuteness — and, as he departed, wrote by way of a memento, which are still to be seen there. Wonderfully pleasing is the poem that His Most Serene Royal Majesty, in passing, composed by way of an elogium for Henrik Gyld[enstierne], Governor of Bohus, my beloved kinsman. And in him a lively bent toward Poetry is discerned, and a keenness of wit joined with equal skill. Were I not to be judged too bold, I would humbly wish that to me too something of His Majesty's mellifluous and Parnassian vein might graciously be imparted. And as this would be honourable to me and to my labours, so too it will be reckoned not unbecoming to His Majesty in the eyes of learned and judicious critics. By your intercession and persuasion my wishes in this matter will (as I trust) be satisfied without reluctance; and in this way you will have bound me to you still more closely. I had asked in another [letter] that you would take pains to send me some servant, Scottish by nation, who might act as a gardener, and one — if it could be done — to act as a fowler, to be maintained here honourably; and that you do this now also I ask †, so that, as soon as free and convenient voyaging hither is open, I may get hold of one or two men of this kind from among your countrymen. Thus indeed you would handsomely come to the aid of my desires. But if there be anything in this our homeland in which it may in turn be granted me to gratify you, it will be yours not even to ask, but only with a little word to indicate whatever you may have in your wishes. You will find me brisk and ready in carrying it out.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 20 March, the year 1592.<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petrus Iunius</i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (1544–1628), Scottish humanist, tutor (with George Buchanan) to <b>James VI of Scotland</b>, and royal almoner; the Scottish envoy who visited Uraniborg in August 1586 (presenting Tycho with a portrait of Buchanan) and who returned in the king's company in spring 1590 (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>; <i>On Tycho's Island</i>). The \"brief meeting on this island\" Tycho recalls is the 1586 visit.</li><li><b>Heading.</b> The transcription runs the rubric together as <i>\"PETRO IUNIOTYCHO BRAHE\"</i> — i.e. <b>\"To Peter Young, Tycho Brahe\"</b> (the missing space between <i>IUNIO</i> and <i>TYCHO</i> is a transcription artefact, not a word; restored in translation).</li><li><b><i>BUCHANANI vitam</b></i> — the <b>Life of George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the celebrated Scottish humanist and James VI's preceptor: his autobiographical <i>Vita ab ipso scripta</i>. Tycho asks Young to have a copy transcribed and shipped.</li><li><b><i>privilegium … a Serenissimo Rege vestro</b></i> — a <b>printing/sales privilege</b> from James VI protecting Tycho's self-printed books within Scotland, modelled on the Imperial privilege Tycho already held (see next note). <i>\"ne ullum meorum operum … venderetur, aut … divenderetur\"</i>: two coordinated negative purpose-clauses — that none of his works be <b>sold</b>, nor <b>vended</b> (resold/retailed) without his permission; both negations preserved.</li><li><b><i>diploma Caesarianum … IACOBUS CURTIUS, Imperij Vicecancellarius</b></i> — the <b>Imperial privilege</b> granted \"as far as the bounds of the Roman Empire extend,\" procured for Tycho by <b>Jacob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jakob Kurz/Curtius, d. 1594), <b>Vice-Chancellor of the [Holy Roman] Empire</b>, here praised as learned in letters and in mathematics. Tycho encloses a copy (<i>apographum</i>) of both the diploma and Kurtz's covering letter as a template for the Scottish request.</li><li><b><i>Icona nostram … epigrammate</b></i> — Tycho's <b>engraved portrait (<i>Icon</i>)</b> intended to be prefixed to his printed works; he invites Young to compose a short epigram for it. (Tycho did circulate such engraved portraits; cf. the <i>Icones</i> sheets in Nielsen's bibliography.)</li><li><b><i>IOHANNES METELLANUS</b></i> = <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b> (c. 1537–1595), <b>Lord Chancellor of Scotland</b>, who accompanied James VI; Tycho recalls verses Maitland inscribed at the door of the king's chamber during the 1590 visit. <i>Metellanus</i> is the latinised form of Maitland.</li><li><b><i>HENRICO GYLD[ENSTIERN], Praefecto Bahusensi</b></i> — <b>Henrik Gyldenstierne</b>, governor (<i>lensmand / praefectus</i>) of <b>Bohus</b> (Bohuslän), described by Tycho as his \"beloved kinsman\" (<i>affinis</i>). The source abbreviates the surname <i>GYLD.</i>; expanded in brackets, following the editorial expansion. James VI is said to have composed a complimentary poem (<i>elogium</i>) for him in passing.</li><li><b><i>serenissima Maiestas Regia … melliflua et Parnassiaca vena</b></i> — <b>James VI</b> as poet; Tycho, praising the king's \"mellifluous and Parnassian vein,\" angles (hedged: <i>si non nimium audax censerer … submisse optarem</i>) for a royal poem of his own. The hedge — \"were I not to be judged too bold, I would humbly wish\" — is kept; not sharpened into a request.</li><li><b>Ovid allusion.</b> <i>\"quod illa apud OVIDIUM de suo amatore conqueritur … ut sic eius verba invertam: Forsitan et quis fit TYCHO vel unde roges\"</i> — Tycho <b>inverts</b> a line of Ovid (the abandoned-heroine voice of the <i>Heroides</i>; the model is the type \"perhaps you ask who I am, or whence\"), turning a lover's lament that the beloved has forgotten her very name into a witty reproach that Young may have forgotten <i>Tycho's</i> name. The transcription's <i>\"quis fit\"</i> is read as <i>quis sit</i> (\"who … may be\"); the printed <i>fit</i> is kept in the Source block and the reading flagged. The wordplay is preserved, not flattened. <b>[Crux: Ovidian source line not pinpointed here; treated as an echo of the <i>Heroides</i> incipit-type, not a verified single verse.]</b></li><li><b><i>affinitatis inter haec duo regna necessitudo</b></i> — \"the bond of kinship between these two kingdoms\": alludes to the dynastic tie between Denmark and Scotland following James VI's 1589 marriage to <b>Anne of Denmark</b> (Frederik II's daughter), which underlay James's 1590 visit.</li><li><b><i>hortulanum … aucupem</b></i> — a <b>gardener</b> and a <b>fowler (bird-catcher)</b>, Scottish servants Tycho had earlier asked Young to recruit for Uraniborg; he renews the request.</li><li><b>Editorial mark †.</b> A <b>dagger appears in the transcription</b> after <i>\"quod et nunc ut praestes\"</i> (<i>\"…ut praestes † petoque…\"</i>), marking an editorial crux/uncertain reading at this point in the source apparatus. It is reproduced verbatim in the Source block; the surrounding syntax (<i>\"…ut praestes … petoque, quamprimum … nanciscar\"</i>) is itself somewhat strained, and the translation follows the sense (\"that you do this now also I ask, so that … I may get hold of …\") while flagging the marked spot. <b>[Crux: the † reading.]</b></li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> The letter's own dateline reads only <b><i>\"Datae Uraniburgi Martij A. 1592\"</b></i> — <b>\"Given at Uraniborg, March 1592\"</b>, with <b>no day stated</b>. The catalogue/edition assigns <b>20 March 1592</b> (O.S.), which is <b>not substantiated by the dateline text</b>; per protocol the header leads with the as-written month and appends the catalogued day. Denmark was on the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. A companion letter to Hagecius two days later is dated 22 March 1592, consistent with a 20 March dating but not proving it from this text. <b>[Crux: in-letter dateline gives month only; \"20 March\" is editorial.]</b></li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the day-number '20' to the closing dateline ('Datae Uraniburgi 20 Martij A. 1592'), dropped from our transcription; Dreyer, TBOO VII p.332, confirmed by page image.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the persons, the two privileges, and the diplomatic/poetic angling are all firmly identified. Three soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the dateline gives only the month, the day \"20 March\" being editorial; (2) the editorial dagger (†) marking a crux at <i>\"ut praestes †\"</i>; (3) the inverted Ovid line, treated as an echo of the <i>Heroides</i> type rather than a pinpointed verse, with the printed <i>quis fit</i> read as <i>quis sit</i>.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VII pp.330-332, read as page images) line by line against source_text: every proper name (Curtius, Metellanus/Maitland, Gyldenstierne), place-name, and number matches Dreyer except the dropped '20' in the closing dateline. Translation of the body is otherwise accurate and complete; no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin drops the day-number '20' present in Dreyer's closing date formula (TBOO VII p.332); the English follows the deficient source and likewise omits the day, though the letter's overall date (20 March 1592) is independently given elsewhere in the packet's metadata. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Uraniburgi 20 Martij A. 1592.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR defect directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.332, closing dateline of the Tycho-to-Peter-Young letter): Dreyer prints '20 Martij', confirming the audit. Fixed both Latin and the corresponding English closing date; nothing else in the letter was touched. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15901027001", "next": "brahe-t_15921001001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reproves Peter Young for not answering two or three previous letters, arguing that frequent shipping between Scotland and Denmark makes such prolonged silence inexcusable.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920320002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920322001", "d": "1592-03-22", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — [Uraniborg], 22 March 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.57 (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM.</p><p>QUAMVIS non ita dudum sit, quod tibi scripserim, optime et amantissime mi THADDAeE, tamen et has superaddere non gravabor; amicorum enim est saepius se invicem, si coram non licet, saltem literis invisere. Ut vero aliquatenus cognoscas, id quod superioribus indicavi, me hortatu et suasu magnifici Domini CURTII potissimum impulsum, Astronomiae instaurationem, quam in opus integrum et peculiare reservare constitueram, nunc Progymnasmatis quibusdam Prodromi loco in publicum emittere, en habes operum meorum, quae typis brevi, Deo volente, absolventur, quaternionem primum, eorum contenta sua inscriptione indicantem, ex qua caetera facile ratiocinabere; hincque colliges me idipsum, quod tunc literis pollicitus sum, nunc moliri. Addidi autem eiusdem quaternionis sena exempla, ut suppeterent, quae hincinde ad Bibliopolas tum vestrates, tum etiam in Austria, Hungaria atque Polonia degentes mitteres, si forte aliqui illic essent, qui exemplaria nonnulla horum tomorum iusto pretio obtinere sategerint. Erunt autem quatuor, et quilibet eorum iustae magnitudinis, ad semiquinque videlicet Alphabeta in hac Regali sic dicta (uti vides) papyro excrescens, suntque plurimis schematibus et tabulis hincinde referti. Ubi a te certior factus fuero, num aliquot exemplaria cupiant, deque numero et pretio, mittam hac instante aestate, favente Numine, quotquot expetierint, praesertim quantum duos priores tomos attinet. Expecto indies Privilegium regis Hispaniarum, Caesareano subiungendum, quod ubi nactus fuero, publicationem non diutius differam.</p><p>Carmina, quae hîc vides, in secunda pagina, non semper id spacij replebunt, sed ubi a serenissimo Scotorum Rege et illustrissimo Principe Landgravio iuniore MAURITIO, qui ambo eximia pollent vena poëtica, quaedam in operum horum commendationem concinnata, prout confido, accepero, epigrammata illa hanc paginam honoris caussa occupabunt. Secundo autem tomo genere et autoritate nobilium virorum carmina pariter praefigam. Ubi scio quae praedictus magnificus CURTIUS in gratiam communium studiorum concinnare non degravetur (nam ut Oratorem agit eloquentem et eximium, sic a Poësi quoque illum non abhorrere existimârim) ea Producis Holsatiae Domini HENRICI RANZOVIJ versibus iam antea obtentis (quod et is hisce studijs non parce afficiatur) praeponam, reliquis clarorum et doctorum virorum lusibus metricis in frontispicia duorum postremorum tomorum reservatis. Scripsi autem ipsemet proximis diebus serenissimo Scotorum Regi IACOBO Sexto et ipsius Cancellario IOANNI METELLO hac de re carmine elegiaco, tum quoque Epigramma quoddam literis ad illustrissimum Principem MAURITIUM Landgravium iuniorem hoc nomine datis subiunxi, quorum carminum descriptum hîc addidi, si forte talibus delectaris, et si plures nosti Principes, poëseos gnaros et horum studiorum amantes, indica eorum nomina unâ cum titulis, nam et ad eos pariter carminibus scribam, carminaque in Astronomiae commendationem, uti spero, ab ipsis impetrabo. Conducibile enim et decorum sentio, haec studia a viris Principibus atque in fastigio humanarum dignitatum collocatis commendari atque ornari, ut eo facilius alacriusque plures ad haec per se ob sublimitatem difficultatemque, quodque minus lucrosa sint, alias abhorrentes, ad eorum culturam invitentur. Scis enim tritum illud: Principibus placuisse viris non ultima laus est, licet ego Philosophice et penitius rem introspiciendo aliter censuerim. Nolo te nunc pluribus obtundere, nec enim dubito, quin tam his quam alijs nostris desiderijs, quantum in te est, sis sponte profuturus. Ego ad tibi pariter gratificandum offero omnem operam et studium, adeoque me ipsum totum. Vale, et magnisicentissimum Dominum CURTIUM meis verbis honorifice saluta.</p><p>Iamque iterum bene vale.<br>Dat. 22 Martij Anno 92.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp. 333–334; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Intellexi ex Domini Doctoris SAGERI Lubecensis literis, nuper ad me datis, RHETICI Bibliothecam tibi fuiße in testamento deputatam. Quod si sic est, rogo, ut quicquid ad rem Astronomicam facit, sive id a praeceptore suo COPERNICO, sive suapte industria habuerit, meis limitibus descriptum mature communicare velis. Et si eclipsin Lunae ultimo in fine praecedentis anni contingentem, caelitus illic denotastis, vel ullas alias, sive a te ipso sive aliunde conquisitas, in promptu habes vel nancisci poteris observationes, me quoque earum compotem per primam occasionem effice, quo Meridianorum discrepantiam hinc certius quam alias, adhibitis nostris vel observationibus vel numeris, rimari concedatur.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>Although it is not so very long since I wrote to you, my excellent and most loving Thaddaeus, yet I shall not begrudge adding these lines too; for it is the way of friends to visit one another the more often — if not in person, then at least by letter. But that you may to some degree learn what I intimated in earlier [letters] — that I, impelled chiefly by the exhortation and persuasion of the magnificent Master Curtius, am now bringing out before the public, in the guise of a Forerunner, in certain <i>Progymnasmata</i>, the restoration of astronomy which I had resolved to reserve for a complete and separate work — here you have the first quaternion of my works (which, God willing, will shortly be finished at the press), indicating its contents by its own title, from which you will easily reckon the rest; and from this you will gather that I am now setting in motion the very thing which I then promised you by letter. I have added, moreover, six copies of the same quaternion, so that there might be a supply for you to send out here and there to the booksellers — both those among you and those residing in Austria, Hungary, and Poland — if perchance there should be any there who would take pains to obtain some copies of these volumes at a fair price. They will be four [in number], and each of them of a just size, growing namely to four and a half [<i>sic</i>] alphabets [of sheets] in this so-called Royal paper (as you see); and they are filled throughout with very many diagrams and tables. As soon as I am made more certain by you whether they desire any copies, and about the number and the price, I shall send — this coming summer, the Deity favouring — as many as they shall have requested, especially as concerns the two earlier volumes. I am awaiting daily the Privilege of the King of the Spains, to be joined to the Imperial one; and as soon as I have obtained it, I shall no longer defer the publication.</p><p>The verses which you see here, on the second page, will not always fill that space; but as soon as I have received — from the most serene King of the Scots and from the most illustrious Prince, the younger Landgrave Maurice (both of whom are strong in an exceptional poetic vein) — certain pieces composed in commendation of these works, as I trust [I shall], those epigrams will occupy this page for honour's sake. To the second volume, likewise, I shall prefix verses [matching it] in kind and in the authority of noble men. When I know what the aforesaid magnificent Curtius will not think it beneath him to compose for the sake of our common studies (for as he plays the eloquent and excellent Orator, so I should judge that he does not shrink from Poetry either), these I shall set before the verses of the Governor of Holstein, Master Henrik Rantzau, already obtained before now (since he too is not sparingly affected by these studies) — the remaining metrical sports of famous and learned men being reserved for the frontispieces of the two last volumes. I myself, moreover, have in these last days written to the most serene King of the Scots, James the Sixth, and to his Chancellor John Maitland [Metellus], on this matter in an elegiac poem; and then too I appended a certain Epigram to a letter given to the most illustrious Prince Maurice, the younger Landgrave, in this name [i.e. on this same account]; of which poems I have added a transcript here, in case you happen to take delight in such things. And if you know more Princes, skilled in poetry and lovers of these studies, signify their names together with their titles, for I shall write to them likewise in verse, and I shall obtain from them, as I hope, poems in commendation of astronomy. For I judge it both profitable and fitting that these studies should be commended and adorned by men who are Princes and set on the summit of human dignities, so that the more easily and the more eagerly many — who otherwise shrink from these things on account of their loftiness and difficulty, and because they are less lucrative — may be invited to their cultivation. For you know that well-worn line: <i>\"To have pleased leading men is not the least praise\"</i> — although I, looking into the matter philosophically and more deeply, should judge otherwise. I do not wish to weary you now with more, for I do not doubt that, in these as in our other desires, so far as in you lies, you will of your own accord be of service. I for my part, to do you a like kindness, offer all my labour and zeal, and indeed my whole self. Farewell, and greet the most magnificent Master Curtius honourably in my words.</p><p>And now once more, fare you well.<br>Given 22 March, the year [15]92.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I have understood, from the letter of Doctor Sager of Lübeck lately sent to me, that Rheticus's library was bequeathed to you by [his] testament. If that is so, I ask that you be willing in good time to communicate to me, described within my bounds, whatever bears upon astronomical matters — whether he had it from his teacher Copernicus or by his own industry. And if you noted from the heavens there the eclipse of the Moon occurring at the very end of last year, or have to hand, or can procure, any other observations — whether obtained by yourself or from elsewhere — make me too a sharer of them at the first opportunity, so that the discrepancy of meridians may from this be more surely investigated than otherwise, with our observations or numbers applied.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / place.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (not stated in the dateline, which gives only day, month, and year; the place is supplied from the correspondence context and is bracketed in the header accordingly). The witness is the <b>draft</b> (Danish <i>Kladde</i>) in Tycho's own papers, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.57.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), the Prague physician-astronomer, imperial court physician, and Tycho's principal Bohemian correspondent and broker at the Imperial court; here serving as Tycho's distribution agent for the <i>Progymnasmata</i> among Central-European booksellers and as a conduit to potential princely patrons.</li><li><b><i>magnificus Dominus Curtius</b></i> — <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Iacobus Curtius a Senftenau, d. 1594), Imperial <i>Reichsvizekanzler</i> (vice-chancellor). He had urged Tycho to issue the work; Tycho both names him as the prompter of the early publication and hopes for commendatory verses from him (\"as he plays the eloquent Orator, so [he] does not shrink from Poetry\"). Tycho closes by asking Hagecius to greet him.</li><li><b><i>Astronomiae instaurationem … Progymnasmatis … Prodromi loco</b></i> — the <b>restoration of astronomy</b> (Tycho's <i>astronomia instaurata</i> programme, per glossary §5.1) which he had meant to reserve for a single complete work, but now issues piecemeal as <i>Progymnasmata</i> (preliminary \"exercises\") \"in the place of a <b>Forerunner</b> (<i>Prodromus</i>).\" The work meant is the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i>, eventually completed posthumously (Prague, 1602). <i>Progymnasmata</i> and <i>Prodromus</i> are kept as period titles/terms.</li><li><b><i>quaternionem … sena exempla</b></i> — a <b>quaternion</b> is a gathering of four sheets (eight leaves); Tycho sends the first printed gathering as a specimen, plus <i>sena exempla</i> = <b>six copies</b> of it, for Hagecius to circulate to booksellers in Austria, Hungary, and Poland.</li><li><b><i>semiquinque … Alphabeta … in hac Regali … papyro</b></i> — a bibliographical measure: each of the four volumes will run to <i>\"four and a half alphabets\"</i> (<i>semiquinque Alphabeta</i>) of sheets, on <b>\"Royal\" paper</b> (<i>papyrus Regalis</i>, a large sheet-format). An \"alphabet\" = the signature-run A–Z (commonly 23 sheets), so \"four and a half alphabets\" indicates the projected bulk. <b>Lexical note:</b> <i>semiquinque</i> is an unusual coinage; rendered literally \"four and a half\" (= <i>quinque</i> less a half), and flagged with <i>[sic]</i> rather than smoothed.</li><li><b><i>Privilegium regis Hispaniarum, Caesareano subiungendum</b></i> — Tycho awaits a printing <b>privilege</b> from the <b>King of Spain</b> (<i>rex Hispaniarum</i>, Philip II), to be added to the <b>Imperial</b> privilege already in hand, before publishing. <i>regis Hispaniarum</i> (genitive plural \"of the Spains\") reflects the standard early-modern style of the Spanish crown's composite realms; rendered \"King of the Spains.\"</li><li><b><i>serenissimus Scotorum Rex … IACOBUS Sextus</b></i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), who had visited Hven in March 1590 and was a practising Latin poet (\"strong in an exceptional poetic vein\"). Tycho hopes for, and has solicited by an elegiac poem, a commendatory epigram from him.</li><li><b><i>Cancellarius IOANNES METELLUS</b></i> — <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b> (c. 1543–1595), Lord Chancellor of Scotland, who accompanied James VI on the Hven visit; <i>Metellus</i> is the latinised by-name. Identification confirmed against Norlind, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1970), who notes the king came \"in the company of his chancellor Maitland (Metellus).\"</li><li><b><i>illustrissimus Princeps Landgravius iunior MAURITIUS</b></i> — <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" the younger Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), son of Wilhelm IV; himself a poet. (To him Tycho would dedicate the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> of 1596.)</li><li><b><i>Produx Holsatiae Dominus HENRICUS RANZOVIUS</b></i> — <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (Heinrich Rantzau, 1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor; here Latin <i>Produx</i>, lit. \"Produce/Governor\") of the Danish duchy of <b>Holstein</b>, humanist patron and astrology enthusiast, whose commendatory verses Tycho already holds. <i>Produx</i> is an unusual title-form; rendered \"Governor.\"</li><li><b><i>lusibus metricis</b></i> — \"metrical sports / playful pieces,\" the humanist term for occasional verse; rendered \"metrical sports\" to preserve the deprecatory register Tycho intends.</li><li><b>Allusion (proverb).</b> <i>\"Principibus placuisse viris non ultima laus est\"</i> = <b>Horace,</b> <i>Epistles</i> <b>I.17.35</b> — \"To have pleased leading men is not the least praise.\" Tycho quotes it as a <i>tritum illud</i> (\"that well-worn line\") and then immediately distances himself from it: <i>\"although I, looking into the matter philosophically and more deeply, should judge otherwise.\"</i> The hedge is preserved, not flattened — Tycho endorses princely patronage as expedient for promoting astronomy while reserving a higher philosophical valuation.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Heading without an explicit <i>S.</i> salutation (a working draft); closes <i>Vale … Iamque iterum bene vale</i> = \"Farewell … And now once more, fare you well\" (glossary §5.5). <i>meis verbis … saluta</i> = \"greet [him] in my words / on my behalf.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Dat. 22 Martij Anno 92</i> = \"Given 22 March, the year [15]92\" — Old Style (Denmark on the Julian calendar). The dataset `label` (\"1592. 22. marts.\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. No day-name or place in the dateline; the year is given as \"92\" and expanded to 1592 in brackets-by-context (the century is unambiguous from the correspondence).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VII, pp. 333–334 (dropped by the working transcription); the report (via Sager of Lübeck) that Rheticus's library was bequeathed to Hájek, with Tycho's request for any Copernicus/Rheticus astronomical material.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the substance, the named persons, and the publication context are secure and corroborated from the corpus (Hagecius as imperial broker; Curtius = Kurz von Senftenau; Metellus = Maitland; Moritz; Rantzau; the Spanish + Imperial privileges; the <i>Progymnasmata</i>). The one genuine lexical crux is <i>semiquinque Alphabeta</i> (the \"four-and-a-half alphabets\" paper-measure), rendered literally and flagged with <i>[sic]</i> rather than normalised; <i>Produx</i> (\"Governor\") is likewise an unusual title-form, noted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter body and the restored Rheticus-library postscript against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.331-334 (page images, read visually). Page 335 was also checked and confirmed to belong to the next letter (Craigius to Tycho, 27 March 1592), so nothing of this letter falls outside the read range. Every name, number and date checked (sena/quatuor exemplaria, semiquinque Alphabeta, IACOBO Sexto, IOANNI METELLO, illustrissimum Principem MAURITIUM, HENRICI RANZOVIJ, Dat. 22 Martij Anno 92, SAGERI Lubecensis, RHETICI, COPERNICO) matches Dreyer exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.57. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 27, "prev": "brahe-t_15910829001", "next": "brahe-t_15920928001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910829001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15921202001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends the first printed gathering of the *Progymnasmata* with six extra copies for distribution, explaining that Curtius persuaded him to publish a forerunner volume of his planned astronomical restoration.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920322001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920327001", "d": "1592-03-27", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 27 March 1592 [O.S.] (catalogued \"Uraniborg\")", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISS. ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, WENAe INSULAe DOMINO REGIS DANIAe CONSILIARIO ET MATHEMATICO DOCTISSIMO, AMICO SUO.</p><p>APOLOGIAM tuam Clariss. D. TYCHO, ut accepi, obiter percurri, nam prolixitas tua et meae occupationes accuratius legere non permiserunt, et primo eius argumento, quod aliquo modo munieras, statim respondi hoc Naturae motu, quo Sublunaria maximi circuli tramitem tenere possunt, exposito, idque veritatis caussa, ne pluribus hac de re opus esset; in reliquis tum nec nostra quicquam infirmare, nec tua magis confirmare visus es: responsum D. FULLERO nostro datum est, qui in una navium erat, quae ad Regem nostrum tum in Dania profectae sunt; sed illis, audito Regis inde discessu ex medio itinere redeuntibus, redditum est, nec remittendum putavi, nisi Mathematicis tuis, quae pervertere dicebamur, similiter excussis. Verum tot nos ab eo tempore obruerunt negocia et profectiones, ut vestrates hîc vix semel atque iterum convenire licuerit, nec ante hyemem tua relegere potuerim, nonnihil otij tum nactus, quod breve fuit, et controversia de veneficarum nugis saepe interruptum, haec Elenctica meditatus sum, et scripsi non in te solum, quem amicitiae causa lenius tractavi, sed illos quoque, qui meo tempore hunc errorem in Germania publicarunt, contra quos theses quasdam, tredecim aut pluribus abhinc annis scriptas, inter chartas reperi; deerant tantum ipsorum observata, quae ex Germania expectaveram, ex quibus plenius redarguendi erant: Interea literae tuae cum observatione cuiusdam obscurioris Phaenomeni sub superioris veris finem ad me perlatae sunt, ex quibus intellexi responsum ad tua et expeti et expectari abs te, itaque ne te diutius remorarer, nostra in hanc antilogicam ad te formam statim redegi et eo die absolvi, quo Eclipsin ☉lis superiori anno conspexeram, et paucis post diebus hoc exemplum rescribi curavi, ut ad te mitti potuerat cum legato vestro, qui tum hîc erat; sed me ad biduum tantum absente prius discesserat, quam speraveram, et redeunti mihi nulla alia opportunitas ad te scribendi vel mittendi restabat, ante autumnalem navigationem: Interea dum in hac vicinia cum aegris versor, unde ad quamcunque oblatam occasionem redire facile poteram, praeter opinionem in Angliam vocor longissimo itinere, nec prius domum redire concessum fuit, nec inde iterum discedere ante summam hyemem licuerat. Expectanda enim erat gravissimi circa hepar Apostematis suppuratio et expurgatio in amicissimo quodam nobili: post Martini festum et prope circa solstitium hybernum reversus, hoc scriptum nostrum in Museo reperi ac ad te missum non fuisse aegerrime tuli: atque hinc bona fide factum est, quod superiori anno nostra non acceperis: sat cito nunc, si, quod opto, illam miram opinionem tibi poterit excutere: quod ni fecerit, omni ratione destitutam relinquet, scio: at absque ratione nec sentiendum nec loquendum Philosopho; cede igitur et veritatem amplectere, nec conare amplius nova sydera coelo addere: cur enim tranquillum Aethera turbares? Si consenseris, tua iuncta opera hanc veritatem plene decidi posse mihi persuadeo: sunt enim mihi perfectae demonstrationes, quibus remotiones Cometarum, quocunque ferantur modo, certo definiri possunt, nec ulla opinor observata tibi desunt: σὺν δύ' ἐρχομένω: conferamus operas: nihil apud me erit, quod tibi non lubenter communicabitur: tu vicissim id agito: Valde cupio illius ROTHMANNI videre, quibus volatili admodum sagacitate ad ♄num usque has fumositates insequitur: si communicaveris, tuo committetur iudicio, quam vere ille tam alte sapiat, alioqui paradoxon a vobis ex proposito defendi videri poterit. Observata quae hactenus videre contigit, pro principijs demonstrationis remotionum haberi nequeunt: tantum ad apparentem in longitudinem et latitudinem motum faciunt: talia expeto ubi azimutha non variantur et quae diversis regionibus accepta sunt. Canon mirificus a generoso quodam consangvineo nostro hîc construitur, cui otij et ingenij ad tantum opus satis est: cum perfectus fuerit, tibi communicabitur: Sed de tuis nihil adhuc resciscere potuimus: fac, quae in chymicis singularia habeas, intellegamus. Scire nostrum nihil est, nisi alij sciant nos scire: De ☉ et ☾ in ☿ reductione aut physicorum tinctura, si quid experientia constat, rescribe, non oberit fortasse nostra collatio: Si quid contra nos amplius cogitas, paucis agito et maiori calami modestia, et si ita libet, publico iudicio nostra subijciamus, ne etiam antilogijs nostris amicitia et consuetudo scribendi intercidat. Si quid hîc expetis, mihi significa: non deero ut potero. Vale quam felicissime D. TYCHO et hoc cerotum ad restinguendam illam in coelo phlogosin abs te accensam ab amico antagonista medico missum benigne accipito et usurpato.</p><p>Datae Edinburgi 27 Martij 92.<br>Tuus IOAN. CRAIGIUS Scotus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, lord of the island of Hven, councillor to the King of Denmark and most learned mathematician, his friend.</b></p><p>Your <i>Apologia</i>, most distinguished Master Tycho, I have run through cursorily, as I received it — for your prolixity and my own occupations did not allow me to read it more carefully — and to its first argument, which you had in some manner fortified, I at once replied, having set out this motion of Nature whereby things sublunary can keep to the track of a great circle; and that for truth's sake, lest there should be need of more words on this matter. In the rest you did not then seem either to weaken our position at all, or to confirm your own the more. My answer was given to our Master Fuller, who was on one of the ships that set out at that time for our King, [then] in Denmark; but when those [ships], hearing of the King's departure from there, turned back from the middle of their journey, it was returned [to me]; nor did I think it should be sent back unless your mathematical [arguments], which we were said to be subverting, were likewise sifted through in the same way. But from that time so many affairs and journeys have overwhelmed me that I was scarcely able to meet your countrymen here once or twice, nor was I able to re-read your [piece] before winter; having then got some little leisure — which was short, and often interrupted by the controversy over the trifles of the witches — I meditated these refutations [Elenctica], and wrote not against you alone, whom for friendship's sake I have handled more gently, but also against those who in my time published this error in Germany, against whom I found among my papers certain theses, written thirteen or more years ago; only their observations were lacking, which I had awaited from Germany, [and] from which they were to have been refuted more fully. Meanwhile your letter, with the observation of some rather obscure phenomenon, was brought to me toward the end of last spring, from which I understood that a reply to your [arguments] was both sought and awaited by you; and so, that I might not delay you longer, I at once reduced our [piece] into this disputational form for you and finished it on that very day on which the previous year I had observed an eclipse of the Sun [☉]; and a few days afterward I had this copy written out afresh, so that it could have been sent to you with your envoy, who was then here. But while I was absent only two days, he had departed sooner than I had hoped; and on my return no other opportunity remained for me to write or to send to you before the autumn sailing. Meanwhile, while I was busy in this neighbourhood with the sick — whence I could easily return at whatever occasion should offer — I was, beyond expectation, summoned into England on a very long journey, nor was I permitted to return home sooner, nor had I been allowed to depart thence again before deep winter. For there was to be awaited the suppuration and clearing-out of a most grievous abscess about the liver in a certain very dear nobleman. Having returned after the feast of [Saint] Martin and nearly about the winter solstice, I found this writing of ours in my study, and bore it most grievously that it had not been sent to you; and from this it came about in good faith that you did not receive our [piece] last year. [It comes] soon enough now, if — as I hope — it can shake that strange opinion out of you; and if it does not do so, it will leave it [the opinion] bereft of all reason, I know. But the philosopher must neither think nor speak without reason; yield therefore and embrace the truth, and try no longer to add new stars to the heaven — for why would you disturb the tranquil Aether? If you agree, I am persuaded that, with your effort joined [to mine], this truth can be settled in full; for I have perfect demonstrations by which the distances of comets, in whatever way they may be carried, can be determined with certainty, nor, I think, do you lack any observations: <i>two going together</i> [σὺν δύ' ἐρχομένω]. Let us pool our labours: there will be nothing of mine that will not gladly be shared with you; do you in turn do the same. I very much desire to see those [observations] of that Rothmann, by which with a quite volatile shrewdness he pursues these vapours right up to Saturn [♄]: if you communicate them, it will be left to your judgement how truly he is so deeply wise, otherwise it may seem that a paradox is being deliberately defended by you [pl.]. The observations that it has so far befallen me to see cannot be taken as principles for a demonstration of distances: they bear only on the apparent motion in longitude and latitude. Such [observations] I seek where the azimuths are not varied and which are taken in different regions. A marvellous table [Canon] is being constructed here by a certain noble kinsman of ours, who has leisure and talent enough for so great a work: when it is finished, it will be communicated to you. But of yours we have so far been able to learn nothing: see that we understand what singular things you have in chemical matters. Our knowing is nothing, unless others know that we know. Concerning the reduction of the Sun [☉] and Moon [☾] into Mercury [☿], or the tincture of the natural philosophers [physici], if anything is established by experience, write back; our comparing [of notes] will perhaps do no harm. If you think anything further against us, do it in few words and with greater restraint of the pen; and, if it so please [you], let us submit our [pieces] to public judgement, lest by our disputations our friendship and our habit of writing should also lapse. If you seek anything here, signify it to me: I shall not fail [you], as far as I can. Fare you most happily, Master Tycho, and receive kindly and make use of this cerate [cerotum], sent by a friend who is your antagonist [and] a physician, to quench that flame [phlogosis] in the heaven kindled by you.</p><p>Given at Edinburgh, 27 March [15]92.<br>Yours, John Craig, Scot.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioannes Craigius Scotus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician and mathematician, educated at Frankfurt an der Oder (where he taught logic and mathematics) and later first physician to James VI of Scotland. An Aristotelian, he held that comets are <b>sublunary</b> and disputed Tycho's celestial cometology; this letter belongs to the Craig–Brahe comet controversy. Tycho replied with his forty-page <i>Apologia (Apologetica Responsio) … ad Craigum Scotum de Cometis</i> (preserved in <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV).</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Wenae insulae domino … regis Daniae consiliario et mathematico</i> = \"lord of the island of <b>Hven</b>, councillor to the King of Denmark, and mathematician.\" <i>Wena</i> = Hven (the island granted to Tycho in 1576).</li><li><b><i>Apologiam tuam</b></i> — \"your <i>Apologia</i>\": Tycho's defence of his cometary doctrine, sent earlier to Craig and here being answered. Craig confesses to having read it only <i>obiter</i> (\"cursorily\"), pleading Tycho's <i>prolixitas</i>.</li><li><b><i>Naturae motu, quo Sublunaria maximi circuli tramitem tenere possunt</b></i> — \"the motion of Nature whereby things sublunary can keep to the track of a great circle\": Craig's physical mechanism for making comets (held to be sublunary, i.e. below the Moon) appear to follow a great-circle path — the crux of his anti-Tychonic argument.</li><li><b>Master Fuller.</b> <i>D. Fullero nostro</i> — a \"Fuller\" attached to a Scottish embassy/fleet to Denmark; not securely identified here. Rendered \"Master Fuller\" (<i>D.</i> = <i>Dominus</i>, §5.5); identity flagged.</li><li><b><i>controversia de veneficarum nugis</b></i> — \"the controversy over the trifles of the witches\": the North Berwick witch-trials (1590–91), in which Craig (a royal physician at James VI's court) was caught up; <i>nugis</i> (\"trifles\") marks his dismissive view. Kept literal.</li><li><b><i>Elenctica</b></i> — \"refutations / things refutative\" (from Greek <i>elenktikós</i>, \"of refutation\"); Craig's title-word for his polemical tract against Tycho. Retained and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Eclipsin ☉lis</b></i> — \"an eclipse of the Sun.\" The symbol ☉ with the Latin genitive ending <i>-lis</i> (= <i>Solis</i>) is kept in the Source block and named \"Sun\" in translation (§5.3). The solar eclipse Craig dates his completion by is that observed \"the previous year.\"</li><li><b><i>nova sydera coelo addere … tranquillum Aethera turbares</b></i> — \"to add new stars to the heaven … disturb the tranquil Aether\": Craig's reproach that Tycho's celestial comets (and his 1572 nova) violate the immutability of the heavens. <i>sydera</i> = <i>sidera</i>. Kept as the author's rhetoric, not endorsed.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>σὺν δύ' ἐρχομένω</i> — \"two going together,\" a quotation of Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 10.224 (<i>σύν τε δύ' ἐρχομένω καί τε πρὸ ὃ τοῦ ἐνόησεν</i>, \"when two go together, one perceives before the other\"), proverbial for collaboration; transliterated <i>syn dy' erchoménō</i> and glossed. Craig's appeal to pool labours with Tycho.</li><li><b><i>remotiones Cometarum</b></i> — \"the distances of comets\" (their <i>remoteness</i>); <i>remotio</i> in this technical sense = the comet's distance/parallax determination. <i>azimutha non variantur</i> = \"the azimuths are not varied,\" i.e. simultaneous observations from differing regions for a parallax baseline.</li><li><b>Rothmann.</b> <i>illius Rothmanni</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Hesse-Kassel mathematician (§5.6); <i>ad ♄num usque … fumositates insequitur</i> = \"pursues these vapours right up to Saturn [♄],\" mocking Rothmann's (and Tycho's) placing of cometary \"vapours\" as high as the sphere of Saturn. The glyph ♄ (with Latin accusative <i>-num</i> = <i>Saturnum</i>) is kept in the Source block and named \"Saturn\" (§5.3).</li><li><b><i>Canon mirificus … a generoso quodam consangvineo</b></i> — \"a marvellous <i>table</i> being constructed by a certain noble kinsman\": <i>Canon</i> in the mathematical sense of a computational table. The \"kinsman\" is very probably <b>John Napier</b> of Merchiston (Craig's known associate, to whom Craig is traditionally said to have carried news of Tycho's prosthaphaeretic methods); \"marvellous table\" anticipates the language of Napier's later <i>Mirifici logarithmorum canonis descriptio</i> (1614). The identification is inferential and is flagged here, not asserted in the translation. <i>consangvineus</i> = <i>consanguineus</i>.</li><li><b>Alchemy.</b> <i>De ☉ et ☾ in ☿ reductione aut physicorum tinctura</i> = \"concerning the reduction of the Sun [☉=gold] and Moon [☾=silver] into Mercury [☿], or the tincture of the natural philosophers.\" The metal/planet symbols are kept in the Source block and named in translation; in this alchemical register ☉/☾/☿ also denote gold/silver/quicksilver — the double sense is intrinsic and is noted rather than resolved. <i>physici</i> = the natural philosophers (alchemical adepts), rendered \"natural philosophers\" not \"physicians.\"</li><li><b><i>Scire nostrum nihil est, nisi alij sciant nos scire</b></i> — \"Our knowing is nothing, unless others know that we know\": a sententia (a well-worn tag, ultimately after Persius, <i>Sat.</i> 1.27, <i>scire tuum nihil est nisi te scire hoc sciat alter</i>); kept as an epigram.</li><li><b><i>cerotum … ad restinguendam illam in coelo phlogosin</b></i> — \"this cerate [a medicinal wax salve], to quench that <i>flame</i> [φλόγωσις, <i>phlogosis</i>, \"inflammation/conflagration\"] in the heaven kindled by you\": Craig's closing conceit, the physician-antagonist sending a salve to \"treat\" the celestial fire (the comet/nova) that Tycho had \"kindled\" in the sky. <i>cerotum</i> and the Graecism <i>phlogosin</i> are kept and glossed; the joke (medical cure for an astronomical \"inflammation\") is preserved, not flattened.</li><li><b>Calendar &amp; place (dateline crux).</b> <i>Datae <b>Edinburgi</b> 27 Martij 92</i> = \"Given at <b>Edinburgh</b>, 27 March [15]92\" (O.S.; Scotland kept the Julian calendar and, in 1592, still began the civil year on 25 March — but here \"92\" plainly = 1592, the controversy's known date). <b>The dataset `label` field reads \"Uraniborg,\" which the letter's own dateline contradicts: Craig writes from Edinburgh.</b> The header therefore leads with the as-written place/date and notes the catalogued value; \"Uraniborg\" most likely records the addressee's location (place of receipt) rather than the place of writing, and is not adopted. No date is silently converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the rhetorical/medical-astronomical conceits are preserved. Open points, all flagged: (1) the place crux — dateline \"Edinburgh\" vs. catalogue \"Uraniborg\" (header follows the dateline); (2) the identity of \"Master Fuller\" (unresolved); (3) the \"noble kinsman\" building the <i>Canon mirificus</i> (very plausibly Napier, but inferential, so kept generic in the translation); (4) the deliberate alchemical double sense of ☉/☾/☿ (planet vs. metal), noted not collapsed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, opening address through closing subscription, against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.333-335, page images only. Source_text matches Dreyer's print word-for-word throughout. Checked every proper name (Fullerus, Rothmannus), date (27 Martij 92), Greek tag (σών δύ' ἐρχομένω, δωδεκατημόριον is absent from this letter but astronomical symbols ☉/☾/♃/♄ all render correctly), and the pronoun/subject structure of the dense argumentative passages (who is said to weaken whose position, whose judgement is invoked re: Rothmann) — all faithfully rendered. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15911114001", "next": "brahe-t_15920805001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15911114001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports reading Tycho's *Apologia* and composing a reply to its first argument, which was delayed when the ship carrying it turned back upon learning the Danish king had departed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920327001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920402001", "d": "1592-04-02", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 2 April 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery, with Latin code-switch) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN / TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ ETC.</p><p>VNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuvor/ Vester lieber Besonder. Wir haben ewer Schreiben de dato Vraniburg, den 20. Februarij, wol empfangen vnd gelesen. So viel nu erstlich die von vns begerete Elendsthier belangt/ thun wir vns der angewendten mühe vnd bestellung/ so ihr deßhalben gethan/ gegen euch in Gnaden bedancken/ Vnd mögen euch hiermit nicht verhalten/ daß wir vor dreyen Monaten vier Elende auß Schweden bekommen/ deroselben vns aber albereit drey gestorben/ daß wir also von denselben nur eine junge Kuhe/ vnd dan sonst noch einen Elendsochsen/ so wir vor zweyen Jaren auß Schweden bekommen/ haben. Die Vrsach aber/ daß sie also weg sterben/ halten wirs dafür/ daß es dahero komme/ weyl der liebe Gott vorm Jar so ein statliche Mast bescheret/ daß auch die Eycheln sehr dicke auff einander gelegen/ vnd faul worden seyn/ daß sie derselben etwan zu viel gessen haben mögen/ daß inen endtlich die feulung im Leibe gemacht/ vnd daran sterben müssen/ Inmassen vns dan ein gute anzahl Reher/ deßgleichen 19. Gembse/ so wir vorm Jar aussem Landt zu Beyern bringen/ vnd gleicher gestalt in vnserm Thiergarten zur Zapffenburg thun lassen/ auch kurtz auff einander gestorben seyn. Woferne jr vns nu zu dem noch vbrigen Elendsochsen/ ein par starcker Elendskuhe von zweyen oder dreyen Jaren bey gegenwertigem herausser schicken kundtet/ geschehe vns daran zu gantz angenemen gefallen/ Wollen es auch vmb euch in Gnaden widerumb zubeschulden vnuergessen seyn/ dan sonderlich der eine Elendsochse/ den wir nu baldt zwey Jar gehabt/ sich gerne paren wolte/ vnd sich in der brunst gantz vnfletig erzeigen thut.</p><p>Was dan die jüngst im verschienen Decembri geschehene Eclipsin Lunae vnd deroselben Obseruation betrifft/ ist es an dem/ daß wir dieselbige in abwesen vnsers Mathematici ROTHMANNI (der sich dan seiner noch jmmerwehrenden Schwachheit halben bey vns nicht wider eingestellt) nicht obseruiret haben/ Sondern/ inmassen wie jr dan in beyliegendt zu sehen/ wie sie von vnserm Mathematico, den wir newlicher Tage auff vnsere Vniuersitet Marpurg bestellet haben/ auffgerissen worden.</p><p>Sonst mögen wir euch nicht verhalten/ daß die Römische Keyserliche Maiestet vor etlichen Tagen an vns allergnedigst geschrieben/ vnd begeret/ daß wir jrer Keyserlichen Maiestet vnsern Vhrmacher Iosten Byrgi mit dem Globo, den er hiebevor gemacht/ vnd darauff die Motus Planetarum eingebracht hat/ zuschicken wolten. Welches wir dan jrer Keyserlichen Maiestet nicht verwegern mögen/ vnd seyndt demnach gemeynt/ gedachten vnsern Vhrmachern erstes Tages zu jrer Keyserlichen Maiestet abzufertigen/ doch daß er sich förderlich widerumb bey vns einstellen solle/ wie wir vns dan versehen/ er auff künfftigen Michaelis widerumb bey vns seyn werde.</p><p>Vnd ob er euch wol den begerten Goldtschmidts gesellen/ so mit den Automatis vnd Horologijs etlicher massen vmbzugehen wisse/ gerne zuweisen wolte/ so ist doch deroselbigen jetziger zeit keiner allhier anzutreffen/ Es soll sich aber er vnser Vhrmacher auff seiner reyse nach einem solchen Goldtschmidts gesellen vmhoren/ vnd da er denselbigen antreffen wirdt/ alßdan mit jm handlen vnd euch zuweisen.</p><p>Was dan vnser Sohn Landtgraff MORITZ an euch schreiben thut/ vnd in Commendationem ewers Operis Astronomici gemacht/ daß habt jr hierneben zu empfangen/ vnd begeren gnediglich/ wan solch Opus gar versertigt/ jr wollet vns desselbigen ein Exemplar zuschicken.</p><p>Wolten wir euch hinwider gnediglich diß nicht verhalten. Vnd seyndt euch mit gnedigem Willen wol geneigt.</p><p>Datumin vnser Stadt vnd VestungCassel/ am 2. Aprilis, Anno 1592.<br>WILHELM, LANDGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Steadfast, Our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Trusty and well-beloved. We have duly received and read your letter dated Uraniborg, the 20th of February. Now, so far as first concerns the elk that we requested of you, we thank you graciously for the pains and procurement that you have undertaken on that account; and we may not conceal from you herewith that three months ago we obtained four elk out of Sweden, of which, however, three have already died on us, so that of those we have only one young cow, and then otherwise one elk-ox besides, which we obtained out of Sweden two years ago. As for the cause that they die off thus, we hold it to be this: that, because the dear God last year sent so plentiful a mast that the acorns lay very thickly upon one another and turned rotten, they may perhaps have eaten too much of them, which finally bred the rot in their bodies, and they must die of it — even as a good number of roe-deer of ours, likewise 19 chamois, which we brought last year out of the land of Bavaria and had placed in the same manner in our game-park at Zapfenburg, have also died off in quick succession. If now, alongside the elk-ox still remaining to us, you could send us out a pair of strong elk-cows of two or three years' age by the present [bearer], it would be done us as a quite welcome pleasure; and we will be unforgetful to requite it graciously toward you in turn — for in particular the one elk-ox, which we have now had for nearly two years, would gladly mate, and in the rut shows itself altogether unruly.</p><p>Now as for the eclipse of the Moon that lately occurred this past December, and the observation of it: the case is that we did not observe it ourselves, in the absence of our mathematician Rothmann (who, on account of his still-continuing infirmity, has not yet presented himself again with us), but, just as you may see in the enclosure, [it has been recorded] as it was sketched out by our mathematician whom we have these recent days appointed to our University of Marburg.</p><p>Furthermore, we may not conceal from you that the Roman Imperial Majesty some days ago wrote to us most graciously and desired that we should send to His Imperial Majesty our clockmaker Jost Bürgi, together with the globe that he made some while ago and upon which he has brought in the motions of the planets [motus planetarum]. This we may not refuse His Imperial Majesty, and we are accordingly minded to dispatch our said clockmaker to His Imperial Majesty at the first day [i.e. without delay], yet on condition that he is to present himself again with us promptly, as we expect that he will be back with us again by next Michaelmas.</p><p>And although he [Bürgi] would gladly have assigned to you the goldsmith's journeyman you requested, one who knows in some measure how to handle automata and clocks [automata et horologia], yet none such is at present to be found here; but our clockmaker shall, on his journey, make inquiry after such a goldsmith's journeyman, and, when he meets with one, shall then treat with him and assign him to you.</p><p>Then, as to what our son, Landgrave Moritz, writes to you, and has composed in commendation of your astronomical work [in commendationem operis astronomici], that you have to receive herewith; and we graciously desire that, when such work is quite completed, you will send us a copy of it.</p><p>This we would graciously not conceal from you in return. And we are well disposed toward you with gracious goodwill.</p><p>Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on the 2nd of April, in the year 1592.<br>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm von Gottes Gnaden, Landgraue zu Hessen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), \"Wilhelm the Wise,\" the astronomer-prince of Kassel and Tycho's chief correspondent among the German rulers; this is among his last letters to Tycho (he died in August 1592). The titulature is given in full and the princely <i>Wir/euch</i> register preserved (protocol §2, §5.5).</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Dem Vesten, vnserm lieben Besondern, Tychoni Brahe, Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> — \"To the Steadfast, Our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.\" <i>Vest(er)</i> (\"steadfast, firm\") is the standard chancery honorific for a nobleman; <i>vnser lieber Besonder(er)</i> is the fixed princely address-formula rendered per §5.5 (\"Our trusty and well-beloved\"). <i>Herr zu Knudstrup</i> names Tycho's Scanian family seat.</li><li><b>Tycho's incoming letter.</b> <i>ewer Schreiben de dato Vraniburg, den 20. Februarij</i> — Tycho's letter \"dated Uraniborg, the 20th of February\" [1592, O.S.], here acknowledged. <i>de dato</i> is a Latin chancery tag left in place.</li><li><b>The elk (</b><i>Elende / Elendsthier / Elendsochse / Elendskuhe<b></i>).</b> Early New High German <i>Elen(d)</i> = the <b>elk / European moose</b> (<i>Alces alces</i>); <i>Elendsthier</i> \"elk-beast,\" <i>Elendsochse</i> \"elk-ox (bull),\" <i>Elendskuhe</i> \"elk-cow.\" The two princes traded live elk for their game-parks; Wilhelm here reports a die-off and asks Tycho to procure more from Sweden via the bearer. <i>Mast</i> = the autumn fall of acorns/beech-mast on which the animals fed; the Landgrave attributes the deaths to rotten acorns. <i>Reher</i> = roe-deer; <i>Gembse</i> = chamois; <i>Beyern</i> = Bavaria. <i>Zapffenburg</i> = the Landgrave's game-park at <b>Zapfenburg</b> (near Spangenberg, Hesse). <i>brunst</i> = the rut; <i>vnfletig</i> here = \"unruly, uncleanly/violent\" (in rutting behaviour). The animal-husbandry register is kept plain, not modernised.</li><li><b>The lunar eclipse.</b> <i>die jüngst im verschienen Decembri geschehene Eclipsin Lunae</i> = \"the eclipse of the Moon that lately occurred this past December\" — the <b>lunar eclipse of 9/19 December 1591</b>. <i>Eclipsin Lunae</i>, <i>Obseruation</i>, <i>Mathematici</i>, <i>obseruiret</i>, <i>auffgerissen</i> (\"sketched/drawn out,\" of the observation diagram) — the technical nouns code-switch into Latin within the German, per §2. <i>beyliegendt</i> = the enclosure (the observation sent with the letter).</li><li><b>Christoph Rothmann.</b> <i>vnsers Mathematici Rothmanni</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Kassel court mathematician and Tycho's correspondent, here reported absent through his <i>jmmerwehrende Schwachheit</i> (\"still-continuing infirmity\"); he had left Kassel and did not return to court service. His unnamed successor is <i>vnser Mathematicus, den wir … auff vnsere Vniuersitet Marpurg bestellet haben</i> — newly appointed to the <b>University of Marburg</b> (the Hessian university).</li><li><b>The Emperor and Jost Bürgi.</b> <i>die Römische Keyserliche Maiestet</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> (Holy Roman Emperor, r. 1576–1612), styled \"the Roman Imperial Majesty.\" <i>vnser Vhrmacher Iosten Byrgi</i> = <b>Jost Bürgi</b> (1552–1632), the Kassel court clockmaker and instrument-maker (and independent inventor of logarithms/prosthaphaeresis-style computation). The <i>Globus … darauff die Motus Planetarum eingebracht</i> is Bürgi's celebrated mechanized <b>planetary globe</b> (a clockwork-driven globe showing the <i>motus planetarum</i>, \"motions of the planets\"). <i>Michaelis</i> = <b>Michaelmas</b> (29 September), the term by which Bürgi is expected back. — <i>erstes Tages</i> rendered \"at the first day,\" i.e. without delay.</li><li><b>Goldsmith's journeyman; automata and clocks.</b> <i>Goldtschmidts gesellen … so mit den Automatis vnd Horologijs etlicher massen vmbzugehen wisse</i> — a journeyman goldsmith able \"in some measure to handle automata and clocks\" (<i>automata et horologia</i>), whom Tycho had requested for his own Uraniborg workshop; the Latin technical nouns are kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Landgrave Moritz's commendatory poem.</b> <i>vnser Sohn Landtgraff Moritz … in Commendationem ewers Operis Astronomici gemacht</i> = <b>Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632, \"Moritz the Learned\"), Wilhelm's son and successor, who has written verses <i>in commendationem operis astronomici</i> (\"in commendation of [Tycho's] astronomical work\"). The <i>Opus Astronomicum</i> whose completed copy Wilhelm asks for is most plausibly the volume Tycho was then assembling — the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> / <i>Progymnasmata</i> programme — but the letter does not name the title; the referent is left as written, not specified. The clause <i>daß habt jr hierneben zu empfangen</i> indicates the poem was enclosed.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel, am 2. Aprilis, Anno 1592</i> = \"Given in our city and fortress of <b>Kassel</b>, on the 2nd of April, the year 1592\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Hesse-Kassel, a Lutheran territory, kept the Julian calendar). The header date (2 April 1592) is the dateline as written and agrees with the catalogue label. <i>Datum</i> (the chancery dating-verb) rendered \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> This letter is printed in Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596; reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI). No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset (`witnesses:[]`).</li><li><b>Source typography.</b> The printed run-together forms <i>Datumin</i>, <i>VestungCassel</i> (missing word-spaces at line breaks in the source) are reproduced verbatim in the Source block and silently word-spaced in the translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the count of negations preserved (notably the recurring litotes <i>mögen euch nicht verhalten</i> = \"we may not conceal from you,\" a courtesy formula, not a true negation of disclosure). Two soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the precise <i>Opus Astronomicum</i> Wilhelm asks a copy of is not named in the letter, so its identification is left open; (2) <i>vnfletig</i> (of the rutting elk-ox) carries a range \"unruly / unclean / violent\" and is rendered \"unruly,\" the dominant sense in this husbandry context.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — chancery German secure, dateline uncontested; residue is an unnamed-work referent (the source's own) + a husbandry-word sense.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible (capped at grade B per brief). Compared published_english line-by-line against the German source_text: greeting/date (20 February), the elk-death arithmetic (4 obtained, 3 died, 1 cow + 1 ox from two years ago), 19 chamois from Bavaria at 'Zapfenburg', the request for elk-cows, the December lunar eclipse and Rothmann's absence, the Marburg-appointed mathematician's sketch, the Jost Burgi/globe/Emperor request with the Michaelmas return condition, the goldsmith-journeyman passage, Landgrave Moritz's commendatory letter for the astronomical work, and the closing/date/signature all have full, accurate counterparts with no invented, dropped, or flipped content found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 18, "prev": "brahe-t_15920220001", "next": "brahe-t_15920730001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920220001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920920001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for elk-procurement efforts, and reports that three of four elk recently obtained from Sweden have died, possibly from eating rotten acorns after an unusually heavy mast year.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920402001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920402002", "d": "1592-04-02", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 2 April 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Frühneuhochdeutsch / chancery, with Latin code-switch) · Edition of record: Tycho Brahe,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Liber I; reprinted in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded in the dataset</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN/ TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>MORITZ VON GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ ETC.</p><p>VNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuvor/ Vester lieber Besonder. Wir haben ewer Schreiben de dato Vraniburg, den 20. Februarij, an vns gethan/ empfangen/ gelesen/ vnd darauß nach der lenge was jr an vns begeret verstanden. Daß wir nu für einen solchen hohen vnd fürtreffligen Poëten gerümbt/ vnd euch vnsere Poëtica so hoch commendiret worden/ daran geschicht vns zwar zu viel/ dan wir wissen solche statliche Commendation,vnd wie jr vns auch zutrawet/ im a Werck nicht zu erweisen/ Ohn ist es aber nicht/ daß wir vns in vnsern angehenden Studijs in Poësi etwas exerciret, doch aber solch Exercitium durch andere vns mehr angelegene Sachen/ numehr eine geraume zeit nicht gebrauchet.</p><p>Nichts desto weniger aber haben wir ewerm gesinnen nach ein Carmen commendatorium, so gut wir es machen können/ vor ewere Opera Astronomica, so jr in Druck außgehen lassen wollet/ verfertiget/ vnd dasselbige auff die Seite vor dem Diplomate Caesareo, da jr es hin begeret/ mit eigen Handen geschrieben. Da euch nu vnser Carmen commendatorium annemlich sein würde/ geschehe vns daran ein gnediges gefallen.</p><p>Wolten wir euch hinwider gnediglich nicht verhalten/ Vnd seyndt euch mit gnedigem willen wolgeneigt.</p><p>DatumCassel/ am 2. Aprilis, Anno 1592.<br>MORITZ LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p><p>MAVRITIVS HASSIÆ LANDTGRAVIVS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE, NOBILEM<br>ET CLARISSIMVM VIRVM.</p><p>VRANIE superat reliqvas divina Sorores,</p><p>    Dicitur et sacri gloria prima Chori.</p><p>Hanc coluere Patres Prisci, Regesqve potentes,</p><p>    Qui propius Mentis vi tetigere DEOS.</p><p>Hanc optat sapiens studiorum figere metam,</p><p>    Vt famæ celebris præmia digna ferat.</p><p>Qvanto etenim Terras celsus supereminet Æther;</p><p>    Jllius est tanto notio lavde prior.</p><p>Ipsa docet Cæli motus, Cælumqve tueri</p><p>    Vultibus erectis nos jubet ipse DEVS.</p><p>Ne ventura malis timeamus fata, sed Astris</p><p>    Præmoniti, ad Superûm confugiamus opem.</p><p>Nec quæramus humi pastum, sed lavde vehatur,</p><p>    Qvi struxit Cæli tale Creator Opus.</p><p>Ergo tuis lætus meritis studijsqve fovere</p><p>    Perge DEAM tantam; Munera larga dabit.</p><p>Qvandoqvidem dudum miratur, pervia cuncta</p><p>    Esse tuis Fabricis quæ latuere prius.</p><p>Hinc tibi scrutanti Leges sublimis Olympi,</p><p>    Inter honoratum præparat Astra locum.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Moritz, by the grace of God, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand, Our trusty and well-beloved. We have received your letter sent to us, dated Uraniborg the 20th of February, [and] read it, and from it understood at length what it is you desire of us. That we should now be extolled as so lofty and excellent a poet, and that our poetry should be so highly commended to you — in this indeed too much is done to us, for we know we cannot make good such a stately commendation, [nor live up to] the trust you place in us, in the [a] work; yet it is not untrue that in our early studies we did exercise ourselves somewhat in poetry — though for a good while now we have not employed that exercise, on account of other matters more pressing upon us.</p><p>Nonetheless, in accordance with your request, we have made ready a commendatory poem [Carmen commendatorium], as well as we were able to make it, for your astronomical works [Opera Astronomica] which you intend to bring out in print; and we have written that same [poem] in our own hand, on the page before the Imperial privilege [Diplomate Caesareo], where you desired it [to be]. Should our commendatory poem now be acceptable to you, we should take a gracious pleasure in it.</p><p>This we would not, in turn, graciously withhold from you; and we are well-disposed toward you with gracious goodwill.</p><p>Given at Kassel, the 2nd of April, in the year 1592.<br>Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse.</p><p><b>MORITZ, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE, TO TYCHO BRAHE, A NOBLE AND MOST ILLUSTRIOUS MAN.</b></p><p>Divine Urania surpasses the rest of her Sisters,<br>    and is called the foremost glory of the sacred Choir.</p><p>Her the Fathers of old cultivated, and mighty Kings,<br>    who by the power of mind touched the gods more nearly.</p><p>Her the wise man chooses to set as the goal of his studies,<br>    that he may bear away the worthy rewards of famed renown.</p><p>For by as much as the lofty Ether rises above the Earth,<br>    by so much does the knowledge of it stand first in praise.</p><p>She herself teaches the motions of the heavens, and God himself<br>    bids us look upon heaven with faces raised.</p><p>Let us not fear the fates that evils bring, but, forewarned<br>    by the Stars, let us take refuge in the help of those above.</p><p>Nor let us seek our fodder on the ground, but let him be borne up by praise<br>    who, as Creator, built so great a Work as the heavens.</p><p>Therefore, rejoicing in your merits, go on cherishing<br>    with your studies so great a Goddess; she will grant bountiful gifts —</p><p>seeing that she has long marvelled that all things which before lay hidden<br>    have become open to your instruments.</p><p>Hence, for you as you search out the laws of lofty Olympus,<br>    she prepares an honoured place among the Stars.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), who had succeeded his father <b>Wilhelm IV</b> on the latter's death (25 August 1592 — so Moritz writes here still as the young heir, in his nineteenth year, though already styling himself <i>Landgraf</i>; his father was alive at the date of writing). A polymath, composer, and Latinist, Moritz inherited his father's astronomical patronage and the Kassel observatory circle (Rothmann, Bürgi). The full chancery titulature — <i>Landgraf zu Hessen, Graf zu Katzenelnbogen</i> — and the princely first-person plural (<i>Wir / euch / unser</i>) are preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>Dem Vesten</i> = \"To the Honourable/Steadfast\" (the conventional honorific for a nobleman below princely rank); <i>unserm lieben Besondern</i> = \"Our trusty and well-beloved\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> = \"Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (the Brahe family seat in Scania).</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (printed at Uraniborg; Liber I appeared 1596), the very volume that prints this exchange. Tycho had written on 20 February 1592 (cited <i>de dato Uraniburg, den 20. Februarij</i>) requesting that Moritz contribute a commendatory poem to be placed before the work, next to the imperial privilege. The letter is Moritz's reply enclosing/announcing the poem.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches</b> (kept in the source, italicised on first use): <i>de dato</i> \"dated\"; <i>Poëta / Poëtica / Poësi</i> \"poet / poetry / poetry\"; <i>Commendation / commendiret / Carmen commendatorium</i> \"commendation / commended / commendatory poem\"; <i>Exercitium</i> \"exercise (practice)\"; <i>Opera Astronomica</i> \"astronomical works\"; <i>Diplomate Caesareo</i> \"the <b>imperial privilege</b>\" — i.e. the printing <i>privilegium</i> / <i>Privileg</i> granted by the Holy Roman Emperor (here Rudolf II), conventionally printed at the front of a book; <i>ut</i>-style chancery diction. The mixing of Latin technical/legal nouns into a German princely body is the expected Kassel-court register (protocol §2).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, c. 1592).</b> <i>ewer / ewerm</i> = <i>euer / euerm</i> \"your\"; <i>jr</i> = <i>ihr</i> \"you (pl./formal)\"; <i>gethan</i> = <i>getan</i>; <i>darauß</i> = <i>daraus</i>; <i>gerümbt</i> = <i>gerühmt</i> \"extolled\"; <i>statliche</i> = <i>stattliche</i> \"stately, splendid\"; <i>zutrawet</i> = <i>zutraut</i> \"entrust/credit\"; <i>numehr</i> = <i>nunmehr</i> \"now, by now\"; <i>außgehen</i> = <i>ausgehen</i> \"bring/go out\"; <i>außgehen lassen</i> \"have published\"; <i>annemlich</i> = <i>annehmlich</i> \"acceptable\"; <i>seyndt</i> = <i>seind/sind</i> \"are.\" The <i>v/u</i> convention (<i>Vnsern, vns, vnser</i>) and <i>ß / -tz</i> spellings are period-standard.</li><li><b>Textual crux — <i>\"im a Werck\"</i>.</b> The source reads <i>\"… wie jr vns auch zutrawet/ im a Werck nicht zu erweisen …\"</i>. The stray lower-case <i>a</i> between <i>im</i> and <i>Werck</i> corresponds to no German word in context; it is almost certainly an editorial/printer's reference-mark (a marginal-note or sidenote signal of the kind the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i> uses) that has been pulled inline in the working transcription, the intended reading being <i>im Werck</i> = \"in [the/its] working-out, in deed/in practice\" (i.e. Moritz cannot in <i>practice</i> live up to the praise). The printed <i>a</i> is retained verbatim in the source block and bracketed <i>[a]</i> / <i>[the]</i> in the translation rather than silently deleted; the sense-reading <i>im Werck</i> \"in deed/in practice\" is adopted but flagged. (Cf. the parallel printer's-mark interruption.)</li><li><b>Spacing artefacts of the transcription.</b> <i>\"Commendation,vnd\"</i> (missing space after the comma) and <i>\"DatumCassel\"</i> (run-together <i>Datum Cassel</i>) are reproduced as in the working transcription; both are typesetting/transcription spacing slips, not substantive. <i>Datum Cassel</i> = the dating formula \"Given at <b>Kassel</b>.\"</li><li><b>Negations.</b> Three are present and all reproduced: (1) <i>im [a] Werck <b>nicht</b> zu erweisen</i> \"<b>not</b> able to make good in practice\"; (2) <i>Ohn ist es aber <b>nicht</b>, daß …</i> — the period idiom <i>es ist nicht ohne, daß</i> = \"it is not untrue / not without foundation that,\" i.e. an affirmative concession (\"yet it is true that …\"), here rendered \"yet it is not untrue that\"; (3) <i>Wolten wir euch hinwider gnediglich <b>nicht</b> verhalten</i> \"this we would <b>not</b> graciously withhold from you in turn\" (the standard chancery closing affirming the favourable communication). The double-negative idiom in (2) is the only point where a literal word-count could mislead an English reader; it is rendered as the affirmative concession the German idiom carries, and flagged here.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Cassel, am 2. Aprilis, Anno 1592</i> = \"Given at <b>Kassel</b>, 2 April 1592.\" Hesse-Kassel kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar at this date (Protestant Germany did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700), so [O.S.]; this agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1592. 2. april. Kassel</i>). No in-letter date discrepancy. The cited incoming date <i>den 20. Februarij</i> (of Tycho's letter from Uraniborg) is likewise O.S.</li><li><b>The enclosed carmen commendatorium.</b> This is the Latin dedicatory poem itself that Landgrave Moritz of Hesse-Kassel promises in the already-published body of the letter (\"we have written that same poem in our own hand, on the page before the Imperial privilege...\") — composed by Moritz personally to be printed before Rudolf II's imperial privilege (Diplomate Caesareo) in Tycho's forthcoming Opera Astronomica, i.e. the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i> (Wandsbek, 1598). Letter and enclosure belong to the well-documented Hven-Kassel correspondence — Wilhelm IV, then his son and successor Moritz, corresponding with Tycho and his court astronomer Christoph Rothmann — which Mosley's <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (corpus) identifies as the single most-studied portion of Tycho's surviving correspondence.</li><li><b>Content of the poem.</b> Fourteen lines of elegiac couplets celebrating Urania, muse of astronomy, as chief among the Muses and the pursuit of \"ancient Fathers and mighty kings,\" closing with direct praise of Tycho: Urania \"has long marvelled\" that his \"Fabricis\" (instruments/constructions — Tycho's own term, as in the <i>Mechanica</i>'s subject matter) have made accessible what before lay hidden, and the poem promises him \"an honoured place among the Stars\" for searching out \"the laws of lofty Olympus.\"</li><li><b>Duplication in the recovered segment.</b> The recovered text repeats the full heading (\"MAVRITIVS HASSIAE LANDTGRAVIVS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE...\") and the entire fourteen-line poem twice, verbatim. This reads as a duplication artifact of the recovery process rather than a feature of the original enclosure — the letter's own published German prose refers to \"ein Carmen commendatorium\" in the singular, with no hint that two copies were sent. Translated once here per house rule against padding; the duplication should be checked against the underlying manuscript/print exemplar (the Rothmann-Kassel correspondence materials are held at the Hessian State Archive, Marburg, and the Kassel university library) before being treated as meaningful.</li><li><b>Register.</b> Rendered as close, line-for-line English prose, preserving the couplet breaks of the Latin elegiacs rather than attempting an English metrical equivalent.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the dateline is uncontested. Named crux: the stray <i>\"im a Werck\"</i> — the inline <i>a</i> is taken as a displaced printer's/editorial reference-mark and the reading <i>im Werck</i> \"in deed/in practice\" adopted (flagged, not silently emended); secondarily, the period double-negative idiom <i>es ist nicht ohne, daß</i> is rendered as an affirmative concession per its established 16th-c. sense rather than word-for-word.</p><p>The Latin is clean, unlacunose, and grammatically straightforward throughout; the translation is offered with confidence at the level of sense and syntax, though as an unmetered prose rendering it does not reproduce the elegiac couplet as English verse. The one open question is not linguistic but codicological — why the recovered segment carries the heading and poem twice — which cannot be resolved without sight of the underlying manuscript or printed source. Grade B reflects that outstanding flag, not any doubt about the translation itself.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (no Dreyer slice available) -- per brief, audited against source_text alone and capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Checked the German prose letter and the accompanying Latin commendatory poem (11 elegiac couplets) line by line against published_english: every clause of the German (the self-deprecating disclaimer about being called a poet, the promise of a Carmen commendatorium written on the leaf before the Diplomate Caesareo) and every line of the Latin poem (Urania/Sorores, Aquila/Olympi imagery) is rendered accurately with no added or dropped content. One inherent source oddity, \"im a Werck nicht zu erweisen,\" is ambiguous in source_text itself (no Dreyer to check it against) and the translator flagged it with an editorial bracket rather than silently resolving it -- not counted as a defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Demurs at Tycho's high praise of his poetic abilities, but nonetheless sends a commendatory poem for the forthcoming *Opera Astronomica* as requested.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920402002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920516001", "d": "1592-05-16", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Maitland", "ck": "John Maitland", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Haddington", "t": "John Maitland → Tycho Brahe — Lethington, 16 May 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f. (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AMPLISSIMO NOBILISSIMOQUE VIRO, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTISSIMO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP ET VRANIENBURG, AMICO SUO CARISSIMO. GENEROSE VIR.</p><p>QUAS ad me 13. Novembris dedisti literas, quod humanitatem et eruditionem nulla sui parte non spirarent, praecipue vero quod tui nominis inscriptionem prae se ferrent, mihi certe, ut decuit, acceptissimae fuerunt. Cum enim in Danica mea peregrinatione nihil tam admirabile excellentis ingenij tui raris dotibus, nihil tam gratum quam sanciendae tecum amicitiae occasio contigerit, nec minorem in conservanda constantiam, quam in contrahenda amicitia benignitatem tuam ex amicissimis literis perspiciam, ni et tuam merito humanitatem amplectar et meam in scribendo negligentiam, tum crebrioribus literis tum alijs quibuscunque, quae mearum virium sunt officijs, sarciam, ingratus prorsus et tanti viri amicitia indignus merito censear. Literis etiam tuis adiunctam effigiem, oris tui dignitatem et temperatam benignitate gravitatem, laudabili sculptoris industria, ad vivum referentem, quod ea oculos imagine pascat, cuius assidua recordatione animus iucundissime afficitur, pretiosissimis quibusque muneribus, quae tibi fortuna affatim suppetit, praestantiorem certe existimavi. Et quos ad illius (ut ais) ornatum versus a me poscis, hos quidem addidi, non quod, quae tui nominis fama abunde commendetur, alio quovis ornamento mea sententia indigeat, sed ut quae virtutum et doctrinae tuae per universum orbem diffusa gloria effigiem tuam in omnibus literatorum omnium bibliothecis sublimem constituet, meo etiam nomini locum aliquem tuo merito ibidem inveniat. Ijs itaque utere, non quidem ut digni sunt, sed ut nostra amicitia et tuo iudicio dignum videbitur. Ego pariter, quae a te Domino PETRO IUNIO mandata sunt, nisi hactenus ab eo effecta sunt, quamprimum illius conveniendi occasio sese obtulerit, ea qua decet fide et diligentia praestabo: quodque de hortulano et aucupe ad me scribis, effectum brevi curabo. Porro IOANNEM CRAIGIUM medicum Edinburgensem, quem mihi tam diligenter ac studiose commendas, ob probitatem et eruditionem antea quidem mihi semper charum, nunc tua causa multo chariorem censeo ac dum vivam censebo: qui cum veritatis eruendae studio ac fortassis ut te ipso interprete scriptorum tuorum subtilitatem sibi non prius intellectam facilius assequeretur, silentibus imo vero cum admiratione tua inventa approbantibus alijs omnibus Europae doctissimis mathematicis, Axiomata quaedam tua impugnare ausus sit, quamvis nonnullis odium potius quam benevolentiam audacia meruisse videri possit, quae tamen eximia nullisque perturbationibus obnoxia tua virtus est, sola victoria contentus et devicti hostis magnitudine tuam gloriam illustrari recte iudicans CRAIGIUM sollicite adeo atque accurate commendas, et commodis atque honoribus auctum cupis, ut egregiam de te bene merendi occasionem in eo viro iuvando mihi oblatam perspiciam. Itaque ubi mea vel opera vel ope rem suam vel dignitatem promoveri posse existimaverit, eam certe diligentiam atque alacritatem adhibebo, ut tuam commendationem meritum pondus apud me consecutam et res ipsa et illius literae ad te abunde testentur. Et si alia qualibet in re tibi ijsve, quos tuorum in numero habes, consilio auxilio gratia vel auctoritate hîc usui esse possum, ita quaeso vel negotia vel homines mihi commendes, ut beneficium tum demum contulisse te credas, cum meam operam expetieris. Interim ne te coelestibus et divino tuo ingenio dignis curis distentum frustra morer, hoc solum a te petam, ut amicitiam nostram (quod humanitas et constantia tua satis spondent) inviolatam a temporis et longe dissitorum locorum iniuria tueri ac paria a me perpetuo expectare velis.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Lethnitoniae die XVI Maij Anno 1592.<br>Mag. Tuae<br>addictissimus<br>[IOHANNES METELLANUS.]</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most worthy and most noble man, most excellent in virtue and learning, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, his most dear friend. Noble Sir.</b></p><p>The letter you sent me on 13 November was assuredly most welcome to me, as was fitting — because it breathed forth humanity and learning in its every part, but above all because it bore the inscription of your name. For since in my journey in Denmark nothing so wonderful befell me as the rare gifts of your excellent genius, and nothing so gratifying as the opportunity of sealing a friendship with you; and since I perceive from your most friendly letter that your kindness is no less in keeping faith than it was in forming our friendship — unless I duly embrace your humanity in return and make good my negligence in writing, both by more frequent letters and by all other services that lie within my power, I should deservedly be reckoned wholly ungrateful and unworthy of the friendship of so great a man. The likeness, too, attached to your letter — rendering to the life, by the praiseworthy industry of the engraver, the dignity of your countenance and a gravity tempered with kindness — I judged assuredly more excellent than any of the most precious gifts that fortune supplies you in abundance, in that it feeds the eyes with that image whose constant remembrance affects the mind most pleasantly. And the verses which you ask of me for its adornment (as you say), these indeed I have added — not because, in my opinion, what is abundantly commended by the fame of your name stands in need of any other ornament, but so that, just as the glory of your virtues and learning, spread through the whole world, will set your likeness on high in the libraries of all the learned everywhere, my name too may by your merit find some place there as well. Use them, therefore — not indeed as they deserve, but as shall seem worthy of our friendship and of your judgement. I likewise, as to the matters entrusted by you to Master Peter Young, unless they have hitherto been carried out by him, will, as soon as an occasion of meeting him presents itself, perform them with the faith and diligence that is fitting; and as to what you write to me about the gardener and the fowler, I will see it accomplished shortly. Furthermore, as to John Craig, physician of Edinburgh, whom you so carefully and earnestly commend to me — dear to me always before, indeed, on account of his honesty and learning, I now reckon him, and while I live shall reckon him, much dearer for your sake. Since, in his zeal for uncovering the truth, and perhaps that with you yourself as expositor he might more easily attain the subtlety of your writings not previously understood by him, he dared to attack certain of your axioms — while all the other most learned mathematicians of Europe were silent, nay rather approved your discoveries with admiration — although to some his boldness may seem to have earned hatred rather than goodwill: yet such is your virtue, surpassing and subject to no perturbations, that, content with victory alone and rightly judging that your glory is set off by the greatness of the enemy overcome, you commend Craig so solicitously and so carefully, and desire him advanced in advantages and honours, that I perceive an outstanding opportunity offered me of deserving well of you in helping that man. And so, wherever he shall judge that his fortune or his standing can be furthered by my work or my aid, I will assuredly apply such diligence and zeal that both the thing itself and his letters to you may abundantly attest that your commendation has obtained its deserved weight with me. And if in any other matter whatsoever I can be of use here, by counsel, aid, favour, or authority, to you or to those whom you count among your own, then, I beg, commend your affairs or your men to me in such a way that you may believe you have only then conferred a kindness when you have sought out my service. Meanwhile, lest I detain you in vain when you are engrossed in cares worthy of the heavens and of your divine genius, this alone I shall ask of you: that you be willing to keep our friendship (as your humanity and constancy sufficiently pledge) inviolate against the injury of time and of places far apart, and to expect the like from me perpetually.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Lethington, the 16th day of May, in the year 1592.<br>Most devoted to Your Worship,<br>[John Maitland.]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Metellanus</i> = <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b> (c. 1537–1595), Lord Chancellor of Scotland under James VI from 1587. <i>Metellanus</i> is the standard Latinised form of the surname Maitland. The signature stands in <b>[square brackets] in the source</b> — an editorial supply by Dreyer, the name not being in the copy-text witness; preserved as such here.</li><li><b>Recipient styling.</b> <i>Domino de Knudstrup et Vranienburg</i> = \"Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b> and <b>Uraniborg</b>\" — Tycho's ancestral seat in Scania and his observatory-residence on Hven, treated together as his territorial designation.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Lethnitonia</i> = <b>Lethington</b>, Maitland's estate in East Lothian (the house, later renamed Lennoxlove, near Haddington). Header gives \"Lethington\" per the dateline.</li><li><b>Occasion / \"my journey in Denmark.\"</b> Maitland had been in Denmark in 1589–90 as a principal of the embassy attending <b>James VI's</b> marriage to <b>Anne of Denmark</b>; the Scottish party (which also included Peter Young and others) visited Tycho on Hven, the meeting to which <i>Danica mea peregrinatione</i> refers.</li><li><b><i>Petro Iunio</b></i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (Sir Peter Young of Seaton, 1544–1628), James VI's former tutor and a royal diplomat, the Scottish intermediary through whom Tycho's \"matters\" (<i>mandata</i>) were being handled; Latinised <i>Iunius</i>.</li><li><b><i>Ioannes Craigius medicus Edinburgensis</b></i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Edinburgh physician and mathematician who had ventured to dispute certain of Tycho's <i>Axiomata</i> (his cosmological/observational tenets), the controversy Tycho recounts in his own letters of c. 1589–90; named in the apparatus per protocol §5.6.</li><li><b><i>effigiem … sculptoris industria, ad vivum referentem</b></i> — the engraved <b>portrait</b> Tycho had enclosed (Tycho circulated engraved likenesses of himself), and for which Maitland here supplies commendatory <b>verses</b> (<i>versus … ad illius ornatum</i>). The verses themselves are not transcribed within this letter.</li><li><b><i>hortulano et aucupe</b></i> — \"the <b>gardener</b> and the <b>fowler</b>\" (<i>auceps</i>, a bird-catcher / keeper of fowl): two servants or specialists Tycho had asked Maitland to procure or arrange for; the specific arrangement is not elaborated.</li><li><b><i>Axiomata … impugnare ausus sit</b></i> — \"he dared to attack certain of your <i>axioms</i>\": <i>Axiomata</i> kept as a technical term for Tycho's stated principles/tenets. The passage trades on the topos that an opponent's stature magnifies the victor's glory (<i>devicti hostis magnitudine tuam gloriam illustrari</i>).</li><li><b>No Greek, no astronomical/astrological symbols</b> occur in this letter.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die XVI Maij Anno 1592</i> = \"the 16th day of May, 1592.\" Scotland was on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1592 (it did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1600/1752); dated O.S. accordingly. The catalogue <i>label</i> (\"1592. 16. maj. Lethington\") agrees with the dateline — no discrepancy. The opening reference to Tycho's letter \"<i>13. Novembris</i>\" (13 November, presumably 1591) is the letter being answered, not the date of this one.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The Latin is courtly but unambiguous; the persons (Maitland, Peter Young, John Craig) and the occasion (the 1589–90 Danish embassy and the Craig controversy over Tycho's axioms) are externally confirmed in the corpus. Minor non-load-bearing soft points (the exact identity of the <i>gardener and fowler</i>; whether <i>13. Novembris</i> is 1591) are flagged, not smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.335-338 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"); the letter itself runs pp.336-337 (p.335 is the tail of the preceding Craigius letter, p.338 the head of the next). Dreyer's printed Latin matches source_text verbatim (only orthographic CARISSIMO/CHARISSIMO variance, ignored per brief). Collated every proper name (Petrus Iunius/Peter Young, Ioannes Craigius/John Craig, Iohannes Metellanus/John Maitland), the date '13. Novembris' and 'XVI Maij Anno 1592', and the full Craig-boldness passage line by line, checking specifically for the pronoun-flip risk (self-boast vs. claim about Tycho) the brief warns about — the 2nd-person subject ('tua virtus... commendas') is correctly kept on Tycho throughout, not shifted to Maitland or Craig. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "John Maitland", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15911113001", "next": "brahe-t_15920819001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15911113001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920819001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Brahe for his November letter and the engraved portrait attached, recalling the friendship formed during his recent journey through Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920516001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920730001", "d": "1592-07-30", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Kassel", "t": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel → Tycho Brahe — Kassel, 30 July 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Frühneuhochdeutsch, chancery) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … (1596; reprinted in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM VESTEN/ VNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN/ TYCHONI BRAHE, HERRN ZU KNUDSTRUP.<br>WILHELM VON GOTTES GNADEN/ LANDTGRAUE ZU HESSEN/ GRAUE ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ ETC.</p><p>VNSERN gnedigen Gruß zuvor/ Vester lieber Besonder. Ihr wisset euch zweiffels frey zu erinnern/ welcher gestalt jr vns hiebevor etlicher Elende halber gute vertröstung gethan/ Darauff wir auch nicht vnderlassen/ vnderm dato, den 2. Aprilis, einen eigenen Botten mit einem Schreiben an euch haltendt/ abzufertigen/ daß/ wo solche Elende bey euch ankommen weren/ er vns dieselbige anhero bringen solte. Wir haben aber sider dem das geringste nicht vernommen/ ob solcher Botte bey euch ankommen/ oder aber ob er lebendig oder todt sey/ oder wie es sonst vmb jnen stehe/ daß vns biß noch von euch keine Antwort zukommên ist. Wie aber deme/ weil vns die Elende/ so vorm Jar auß Schweden bekommen/ alle gestorben/ vnd wir nur einen Elendtochsen/ so vns vor zweyen Jaren auß Schweden zukommen/ haben/ derselbe dan sehr schön vnd fein zahm ist/ vnd diß Jar albereit ein fein Gehörn von anderthalben Schuhe hoch geschoben hat/ vnd jm niergendt woran mangelt/ alß daß er kein Weibichen hat/ damit er sich paren möchte. Alß begeren wir gnediglich/ woferne euch solche vertröstete Elendsthier/ vnd sonderlich ein Weibichen zukommen were/ jr wollet vns dieselbige auff vnsern kosten so baldt vberschicken. Daran thut jr vns zu angenemen gefallen. Vnd seyndt wir es vmb euch hinwider in allen Gnaden/ damit wir euch ohne das gewogen/ zu erkennen geneigt.</p><p>Datum in vnser Stadt vnd Vestung Cassel/ am 30. Iulij, Anno 1592.<br>WILHELM LANDTGRAFF ZU HESSEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the steadfast, our trusty and well-beloved, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b><br><b>Wilhelm, by the grace of God, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, etc.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand, our trusty and well-beloved. You doubtless know to call to mind in what manner you gave us, some time ago, a good promise touching certain elk; whereupon we also did not fail, under the date of 2 April, to despatch a courier of our own to you, carrying a letter, [to the effect] that, if such elk had come into your hands, he was to bring the same to us here. But since then we have heard not the least thing — whether that courier reached you, or indeed whether he be alive or dead, or how it otherwise stands with him — so that up to now no answer has come to us from you. But however that may be: since the elk we obtained a year ago out of Sweden have all died, and we have only one elk-bull, which came to us out of Sweden two years ago — the same being indeed very handsome and finely tame, and having already this year put forth a fine antler a foot and a half high, and which lacks nothing anywhere, save that it has no female with which to mate — therefore we graciously desire that, in case such promised elk-beasts, and especially a female, should have come into your hands, you would send the same over to us at our cost as soon as may be. Therein you do us an acceptable pleasure. And we are inclined, for our part, to acknowledge it to you again in all graciousness, in which we are besides well-disposed toward you.</p><p>Given in our city and fortress of Kassel, on 30 July, Anno 1592.<br>WILHELM, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Wilhelm von Gottes Gnaden, Landtgraue zu Hessen</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the \"astronomer-prince,\" Tycho's foremost princely correspondent and the patron of the Kassel observatory (with Christoph Rothmann and the instrument-maker Jost Bürgi). This is one of his last letters: he died on 25 August 1592, less than a month after this dateline.</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Herrn zu Knudstrup</i> = \"Lord of Knudstrup,\" Tycho's ancestral seat (Knutstorp) in Scania; the full princely address-formula and titulature are preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Honorifics / formulae.</b> <i>Dem Vesten</i> = \"to the steadfast\" (an honorific epithet, <i>vest/fest</i> \"firm, steadfast,\" for a nobleman below comital rank); <i>Vnsern gnedigen Gruß zuvor</i> = \"Our gracious greeting beforehand\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Vester lieber Besonder</i> = \"our trusty and well-beloved\" (the standard princely address to a favoured subordinate, protocol §5.5). The princely first-person plural (<i>Wir / euch</i>) is kept throughout.</li><li><b><i>Catzenelnbogen</b></i> = the County of <b>Katzenelnbogen</b>, a hereditary Hessian comital title; kept in the period spelling.</li><li><b>Subject: <i>Elend / Elende / Elendtochsen / Elendsthier</i>.</b> The pivotal term. <i>Elend(t)</i> here is <b>elk</b> (European elk / moose, <i>Alces alces</i>) — early-modern German <i>Elen(d)tier</i>, not the modern homograph <i>Elend</i> \"misery.\" Rendered \"elk\" throughout. Specifically: <i>Elende</i> (plural) = \"elk\"; <i>Elendtochsen</i> = \"elk-bull\" (<i>Ochse</i> \"ox/bull,\" here the male animal); <i>Elendsthier</i> = \"elk-beast(s)\"; <i>Weibichen</i> (dimin. of <i>Weib</i>) = \"female (cow).\" The exchange concerns living elk shipped from Sweden via Tycho (well-placed to procure Scandinavian game) to stock the Landgrave's menagerie at Kassel — Wilhelm's one surviving bull lacks a mate.</li><li><b><i>Schuhe</b></i> (in <i>anderthalben Schuhe hoch</i>) = \"feet,\" the linear measure (singular <i>Schuh</i> ≈ 0.28–0.29 m in Hessian usage); <i>anderthalben</i> = \"one and a half.\" The antler is described as \"a foot and a half high\"; the measure-word is kept as \"foot/feet,\" not converted.</li><li><b>Negation count.</b> The letter carries the legally/factually load-bearing negations: <i>nicht vnderlassen</i> (\"did not fail\" — i.e. did despatch the courier, a double-negative affirmation, preserved as such); <i>das geringste nicht vernommen</i> (\"heard not the least thing\"); <i>keine Antwort</i> (\"no answer\"); <i>niergendt woran mangelt, alß daß</i> (\"lacks nothing anywhere, save that\"). Each is reproduced one-for-one; none added or dropped.</li><li><b><i>vertröstung … vertröstete</b></i> = \"promise / promised\" (<i>Vertröstung</i> \"assurance, undertaking\"); rendered as a promise/undertaking, not a modern \"consolation.\"</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, 1592).</b> <i>jr/jm/jnen</i> = <i>ihr/ihm/ihnen</i>; <i>vns/vnserm/vnser</i> = <i>uns/unserm/unser</i>; <i>sider dem</i> = <i>seitdem</i> (\"since then\"); <i>vorm Jar</i> = <i>vor dem Jahr</i> / \"the year before\"; <i>albereit</i> = <i>bereit(s)</i> (\"already\"); <i>paren</i> = <i>paaren</i> (\"to pair, mate\"); <i>woferne</i> = \"in case, if\"; <i>vberschicken</i> = \"send over\"; <i>zukommên</i> prints with a tilde for the nasal (<i>zukommen</i>). The virgule (/) is the printed sentence-comma of the 1596 edition and is left in the source block.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum … am 30. Iulij, Anno 1592</i> = \"Given … on 30 July, 1592,\" <b>Old Style</b> (Hesse-Kassel, a Lutheran state, kept the Julian calendar in 1592). <i>Datum</i> \"Given\" rendered per §5.5. The in-letter back-reference <i>den 2. Aprilis</i> (the April courier) is likewise O.S. The dataset <b>label</b> (\"1592. 30. juli. Kassel\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition.</b> Printed by Tycho himself in the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), Liber I — the volume gathering the Hesse-Kassel correspondence; reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the distinction between the letter's two elk shipments: 'vorm Jar' now reads 'a year ago' (not 'the year before last', which duplicated the letter's separate later phrase 'vor zweyen Jaren', 'two years ago').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely; the one term a non-specialist might misread (<i>Elend</i> = \"elk,\" not \"misery\") is resolved by the internal evidence (bull, antler, mate, shipment from Sweden) and noted in the apparatus. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No tboo reference exists for this letter (tboo: null), so audited against source_text alone per the brief's instruction; grade capped accordingly and further capped by the mistranslation found, which changes the elk-shipment chronology a scholar would take away. All other content (the 2 April courier, the elk-bull's antler 'anderthalben Schuhe' = a foot and a half, the request for a female, the date 30 July 1592) checked clean against source_text.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). 'vorm Jar' (a year ago / last year) is rendered as 'the year before last', which collapses onto the letter's separate, distinctly-phrased 'vor zweyen Jaren' (two years ago) used one clause later for the surviving elk-bull; the original draws a clear one-year-ago vs. two-years-ago distinction between two different elk shipments that the translation blurs. At: “since the elk we obtained the year before last out of Sweden have all died”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the correction rests entirely on the German text already given in current_latin, which is internally consistent and unambiguous on this point (two distinct time-phrases naming two distinct elk deliveries, one 'vorm Jar' and one 'vor zweyen Jaren'). This cannot be verified against the edition of record and is flagged accordingly; the fix is limited to what is demonstrable from the letter's own internal evidence, per protocol for a defect with no supplied slice.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 19, "prev": "brahe-t_15920402001", "next": "brahe-t_15920920001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920220001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15920920001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks why the promised Norwegian elk have not been delivered, noting his April courier went unanswered and that his existing Swedish elk have all died.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920730001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920805001", "d": "1592-08-05", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 5 August 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET CLARISSIMO VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE HVENAe INSULAe DOMINO, MATHEMATICO LONGE PRAeSTANTISSIMO.</p><p>CLARISSIME D. TYCHO, in altero antilogiae nostrae exemplarium, quae ab eodem amanuensi descripta sunt, quorum primum ad te missum fuit, haec errata reperiuntur: εδειξαι pro δεῖξαι. Ubi SCULTETI fit mentio, exprobas pro exprobras. Et cum artificium vestrum examinatur, ex his pro in his, ut in Coelestibus: quae si in tuo occurrant, vel similia, peto corrigas. Pro reliquis responsurus sum, nec discipulos tuos veriturus, imo ne praeceptores tuos. Veritas et honestas mihi sufficient ad defensionem contra quoscunque. Et tamen, ut percurri obiter primum Tomum tuum, quamvis in multis a te dissentiam, coepi potius tuam industriam et eximias virtutes admirari, quam illarum iniuriarum, quae nec te nec me digna fuere, recordari. Clementissimus Deus coeptis tuis benigne faveat et ad optatum exitum perducat.</p><p>Datae 5 Augusti Edinburgi.<br>IOAN. CRAIGIUS<br>Doctor Medicus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most renowned man, Master Tycho Brahe, lord of the island of Hven, by far the most outstanding mathematician.</b></p><p>Most renowned Master Tycho, in the second of the copies of our controversy [<i>antilogia</i>] — which were written out by the same amanuensis, the first of which was sent to you — the following errata are found: <i>εδειξαι</i> [<i>edeixai</i>] for <i>δεῖξαι</i> [<i>deîxai</i>]. Where mention is made of SCULTETUS, <i>exprobas</i> [you reproach] for <i>exprobras</i> [you reproach]. And where your method is examined, <i>ex his</i> [from these] for <i>in his</i> [in these], as in [the passage on] the Heavens: should these, or the like, occur in your [copy], I ask that you correct them. As for the rest, I shall make my reply, and I shall not stand in awe of your disciples — nay, not even of your teachers. Truth and honesty will suffice me for a defence against any whomsoever. And yet, having run through your first Tome cursorily, although I dissent from you in many things, I have begun rather to admire your industry and your exceptional merits than to call to mind those injuries which were worthy neither of you nor of me. May God most merciful graciously favour your undertakings and bring them to the wished-for end.</p><p>Given 5 August, at Edinburgh.<br>JOHN CRAIG,<br>Doctor of Medicine.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioan. Craigius, Doctor Medicus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Scots Aristotelian physician then practising in Edinburgh; earlier professor of mathematics and logic at Frankfurt an der Oder, later royal physician to James VI, fellow of the London Royal College of Physicians (1604) and incorporated M.D. at Oxford (1605). Despite his court contacts, he was <i>not yet</i> a royal physician at the time of this exchange, and he never visited Denmark (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i>, \"The Dynamic of a Dispute,\" p. 75).</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Hvenae insulae domino</i> — \"lord of the island of <b>Hven</b>,\" Tycho's fief (granted by Frederik II in 1576); the seat of Uraniborg. <i>Mathematico longe praestantissimo</i> — the high astronomical compliment, kept formal.</li><li><b><i>antilogia</b></i> (Greek <i>ἀντιλογία</i>, \"contradiction, controversy, debate-in-utramque-partem\") — here the <b>controversy / point-by-point exchange</b> between Craig and Tycho. The substance was their epistolary dispute (1589–94) over Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588) on the <b>comet of 1577</b>: Craig, on Aristotelian grounds, rejected Tycho's parallax demonstration that the comet was a supralunary (aethereal) rather than a meteorological body, and objected to Tycho's fluid-heaven world-system that dispensed with solid celestial spheres. The term is left as <b>controversy</b> in the running text with the Greek-derived word flagged.</li><li><b><i>amanuensis</b></i> — a copyist/scribe; two fair copies of the controversy were made by the same hand, the first sent to Tycho, the second (the one Craig is now collating) retained by Craig, who lists the scribal <i>errata</i> by which the two diverge so that Tycho may bring his copy into agreement.</li><li><b>The errata list (a copy-collation, not a substantive argument).</b> Three scribal slips are corrected:</li><li><b>Greek:</b> <i>εδειξαι</i> <b>for</b> <i>δεῖξαι</i>. The transmitted source prints the erroneous form <b>εδειξαι</b> (no accents) and the correct form <b>δεῖξαι</b> with circumflex; reproduced as printed per protocol §4.2. <i>δεῖξαι</i> is the aorist active infinitive of <i>δείκνυμι</i>, \"to show / to demonstrate / to prove\" — the technical sense here (to <i>demonstrate</i> the comet's parallax). The faulty <i>εδειξαι</i> would be a malformed augmented form; the point is purely orthographic/scribal, so both forms are transliterated and glossed rather than emended into one.</li><li><i>exprobas</i> <b>for</b> <i>exprobras</i> — at the mention of <b>Scultetus</b>. The intended word is <i>exprobras</i>, \"you reproach / you cast in [his] teeth\" (2nd person sing. of <i>exprobrare</i>); <i>exprobas</i> is the copyist's haplography (dropped <i>-r-</i>). Both forms carry the same sense (\"you reproach\"), so the English cannot show the difference; the <b>Latin pair is kept</b> to preserve the correction Craig is actually making.</li><li><i>ex his</i> <b>for</b> <i>in his</i> — \"from these\" mistakenly written where \"in these\" is meant, \"where your method is examined ... as in [the passage on] the Heavens\" (<i>ut in Coelestibus</i>). Again a one-preposition scribal slip; the Latin pair is retained.</li><li><b><i>Sculteti</b></i> (gen.) = <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Bartholomew Schultz, 1540–1614), the Görlitz astronomer-mathematician and Tycho correspondent whose observations of the 1577 comet figured in Tycho's analysis in <i>De mundi</i> — hence a passage in the controversy where Tycho is said to \"reproach\" Scultetus.</li><li><b><i>primum Tomum tuum</b></i> — \"your first Tome.\" The referent is the printed comet-volume Tycho had circulated (the 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, the second part of Tycho's larger <i>Progymnasmata</i> programme). Craig's \"first Tome\" is taken at face value as the volume he had received; whether it designates that volume's ordinal place in Tycho's scheme is not decided by the letter and is <b>not</b> silently reconciled here.</li><li><b><i>nec discipulos tuos veriturus, imo ne praeceptores tuos</b></i> — \"I shall not stand in awe of your disciples — nay, not even of your teachers.\" The combative register (Craig promising a substantive <i>responsio</i> and disdaining intimidation) is preserved; <i>imo ne ... quidem</i> force (\"nay, not even\") rendered without softening.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The letter contains two negations in the defiance sentence — <i>nec ... veriturus</i> and <i>ne praeceptores</i> — and one in the concessive <i>quamvis in multis a te dissentiam</i> (\"although I dissent from you in many things\"); all three are reproduced, none added or dropped. The closing <i>quae nec te nec me digna fuere</i> carries a double <i>nec</i> (\"worthy neither of you nor of me\"), both retained.</li><li><b>Source-text artifact.</b> The working transcription (harvested CSV) carries a stray `&gt;` immediately before <i>quam illarum iniuriarum</i>; this is a markup/extraction artifact, not a feature of the Dreyer text or the MS, and is omitted from the Source block. It does not affect the reading: <i>admirari ... quam ... recordari</i> (\"admire ... rather than ... call to mind\") is a straightforward <i>potius ... quam</i> comparison.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae 5 Augusti Edinburgi</i> = \"Given 5 August, at <b>Edinburgh</b>.\" No year is given in the dateline; the year <b>1592</b> comes from the dataset label and the Vienna MS context, not from the letter's own subscription — so it is <b>not</b> asserted in the header's date-as-written portion beyond what the document attests. Scotland under James VI used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar, so the date is O.S. (catalogued \"1592. 5. august. Edinburgh\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin and the courteous-but-combative register are rendered securely. Soft points, each flagged not smoothed: (1) the three <i>errata</i> are scribal-copy corrections whose distinction is invisible in English, so the Latin/Greek pairs are kept verbatim; (2) the exact ordinal sense of <i>primum Tomum tuum</i> is left open rather than reconciled to Tycho's <i>Progymnasmata</i> numbering; (3) the year is editorial (dataset/MS), not stated in the dateline.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the three errata are resolved scribal slips (Craig's own corrections); residue invisible in English + an editorial year.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VII pp.337-338 (read 336-339 to confirm boundaries: p.336-337 top belongs to a preceding Metellanus letter, p.339 opens a following Tycho-to-Metellanus letter). Latin matches source_text exactly, word for word (long-s/ligature typography aside). Every proper name (Sculteti, Craig, Edinburgh), the errata list (Greek edeixai/deixai, exprobas/exprobras, ex his/in his), the date (5 August), and the closing sentiment are all rendered faithfully in the English. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15920327001", "next": "brahe-t_15940422001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15911114001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Lists errata in the controversy copy sent to Brahe and announces he will make his full reply, undeterred by Brahe's disciples or teachers.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920805001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920819001", "d": "1592-08-19", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "John Maitland", "ck": "John Maitland", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → John Maitland — Uraniborg, 19 August 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, EXΙΜΙΑ TAM GENERIS QUAM DOCTRINAe NOBILITATE INPRIMIS CONSPICUO, DOMINO IOHANNI MAeTELANO, SERENISS. SCOTORUM REGIS CANCELLARIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM HONORANDO</p><p>PERPAUCIS abhinc mensibus, Nobilissime et amplissime vir, literas tuas, quibus nostris superiore hyeme ad te datis respondes, iam pridem accepi. Quae quod genuinum non modo rarae, quae in te est, eruditionis specimen, inprimisque Romani sermonis nitorem, sed iucundissimam humanitatis fragrantiam affatim redolerent, gratissimae mihi fuerunt. Ex quo itaque tuae illae ad me perlatae essent, avide semper desiderabam, ut certo aliquo fretus internuntio ijs nonnihil responderem. Sed cum eorum, per quos meas ad te transmittere in animo haberem, praeter meam opinionem navigatio iam tandem praepeditur, indeque accideret ut responsio ad tuas in hoc usque tempus dilata sit, idcirco minime intermittendum duxi, quin cum mercatoribus, qui hinc ad vos appellunt, ante ingruentem brumam paucis rescriberetur, qualemcumque gratae mentis et memoriae significationem apud te testatam relinquerem, meque vicissim tui amantem declararem. Agnosco equidem non exigua benevolentiae erga me tuae extare indicia, praesertim dum eruditis et argutis tuis versibus effigiem, unâ cum Urania nostra (ut superiora tua in me officia tacitus praeteream) eleganter ornare non degravatus sis. Quo sane nomine gratias tibi ago, agamque perpetuo maximas. Unicum tamen est, quod in his non tam desidero quam liberius et iusto abundantius dictum sentiam, in quo etiam nimium Poëtarum indulsisse licentiae videris, idque nimirum per terna illa verba, quibus tria ultima concluseris Epigrammata. Quae cum saltem Creatori immenso et ineffabili Creaturis nequaquam competant, ea cuipiam conditae naturae asscribere, quocunque dicendi tropo excusentur, inconveniens, imo piaculum censuerim. Quare si loco horum quippiam aliud substitueris, quod fragilis humanae conditionis metas non transcendat, rectius meo iudicio feceris, mihique rem magis gratam praestiteris. En quanta hîc utar erga te audacia et parrhisia, quam ingenuus candor ille tuus nostraque amicitia non difficulter (uti spero) excusabunt. Habeo insuper gratias, quod D. IOHANNEM CRAIGUM Medicum vestratem, vel ob meam commendationem cariorem habueris atque ad optima quaeque promovere receperis, utut is nuper arrogans quoddam contra nostra Placita Scriptum tertio demum ab Apologiae illi transmissae anno parturierit, quod affectato et insolenti nomine Capnuraniae restinctionem (proh Daedaleum inauditarum hactenus vocum fabricatorem) appellare non erubuit. In quo se omnia quae in libro nostro secundo de Novis Aethereis Phaenomenis atque Apologia ipsi hoc nomine destinata continentur, refutare atque infirmare inverecunda praesumptione iactavit, cum tamen ne minimum quidem eorum, apud eos, qui rem ipsam penitius introspicientes, usu eandem debite exercere didicerunt, nec praeiudicio caeco impediuntur, vel semel vacillare faciat. Longe igitur consultius fecisset enervis iste Cometarum (qui revera coelestes sunt, nec flagrantes in aëre fumositates, ut ille in suum nimium credens, ac si in hunc baptizatus fuisset, ARISTOTELEM, commentus est) restinctor, si proprium opinionum suarum incendium et aestuantem animi ardorem ac inflammatos affectuum motus extinguere didicisset. Veritatis, quam tuemur, invictam arcem et firma huius propugnacula, obtusis hisce, quibus eam invadere conatur, argumentorum telis, non magis laedet aut expugnabit, quam si aeneum et insuperabilem HERCULIS clypeum plumbea confodere sarissa praesumpserit. Nostra enim ille aut parum minusque recte intelligit, aut ob praeoccupatas opiniones intelligere nolit, aut ea distrahit, depravat, invertit, male accommodat. Imo cavillatur et calumniatur fere ubique effreni quadam petulantia, adeo ut ne quidem a scommatis, dicterijs, naenijs, superciliosis oblocutionibus alijsque id generis inflati et impotentis animi pomposis ineptijs sibi temperet, deque ijs, quae nunquam viderit aut fundamentaliter hauserit, nedum ut usu diuturno sibi familiaria reddiderit, iudicium immaturum et praeposterum futili potius quam subtili acumine interponere non vereatur. Hem egregiam a CRAIGO vestrate pro tot annorum in hac palaestra veritatis inquirendae stabiliendaeque causa exantlatis laboribus, proque perassiduis vigilijs, studijs indefessis, sumptibus inaestimabilibus, reporto gratiam. Certe si non maiorem a caeteris Europae eruditis Mathematicis consequutus fuero, aut ego frustra tot tantaque egerim (dummodo nihil aliud quam hominum gratitudinem et non potius solam veritatem, quae sibiipsi sufficiens meritum est, spectarem) aut illi valde ingrati reputabuntur. At nemo est ex omnibus Italiae, Galliae, Angliae, Germaniae, Bohemiae et Poloniae Mathematicis, qui, quantum ego sciam, nostra non libere et libenter approbet, ijsque (utut Peripateticorum in Scholis usitata dogmata in quibusdam convellant) grata mentis significatione subscribat. Solus hic vester prae omnibus alijs sibi sapiens veritatem, quam in lucem ex inveteratis opinionum et errorum tenebris producere elaboramus, offuscare, ut sic, quemadmodum iste ex devastato Dianae templo, nomen sibi quoddam comparet, anticipata quadam philautia et praesumptione non indecens reputavit; quasi is solus penitius rem introspiciat, quam caeteri omnes, et cornicum oculos configere valeat. Verum eiusmodi cothurnis et sophisticis contradictionibus, veritati a nobis assertae frustra obstrepentibus, nihil in posterum nostra Urania respondere dignabitur, ne bonas horas altioribus solidioribusque tribuendas usqueadeo inutiliter collocet. Abblandiatur et placeat is sibi inque suo cerebello triumphet, quantum allubescit. Veritas certissimis et infallibilibus observationibus (quamvis et has dentata sua strigili rodere et turgido ac temerario ausu improbare nefas haud existimarit, utut tales et his merito conferendas assequi et imitari vix sustineat) fundata et certis demonstrationibus Geometricis (quas ille non minus, sive per inscitiam sive affectati iudicij temeritatem, malitiose sugillat) in apertum deducta imbelles non moratur oppugnantis insultus. Ne autem quis suspicetur, me, quod causae diffidam, responsionem aversari, mittam Capnuranicas ipsius commentationes, vel potius commenta, ad praestantes quosdam in Germania Mathematicos, observationibus coelestibus accurate instituendis assuetos et in hac materia solide versatos (caeteris enim parum ista intelligentibus, fucatis, quibus scatet, sophismatis, facile imposuerit CRAIGIUS vester ille) qui controversiam hanc exquisita scientia et diuturno usu, decernere nôrunt, et singulis ad aequam trutinam expensis, citra affectus et praeiudicium ullum, iustam ferent censuram. Forte etiam prodibit suo tempore amicorum vel discipulorum meorum aliquis, qui hanc controversiam dirimere et ad invalidos Craigicarum obiectionum ictus retorquendos otium sibi sumat. Sicque tandem infringat solido propria delusus ab arte. In me equidem non amplius inveniet Antagonistam. Praestat enim Tomis nostris Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum (quos nunc prae manibus habeo) absolvendis operam locare, quam talibus affectatis obmurmurationibus retundendis inutiliter occupari. Quorum non solum primus (qui de Nova Stella ubertim agit) CRAIGI circa hanc quoque ignorantiam et crassa errata abunde patefaciet, sed et reliqui duo de quinque Cometis ex professo tractantes, eosque omnes coelestes, citra omne dubium, fuisse apertissime demonstrantes, quam perperam et frustra CRAIGUS hic nostris assertionibus refragetur, cuivis luce Meridiana clarius ob oculos ponent. Hinc, si volet, suae evidentissimae hallucinationis, ignorantiae et ineptitudinis redargutionem depromat, atque demum melius perspectis ijs, quibus seducitur, deviationibus, in veritatis semitam (modo id fieri queat) revertatur.</p><p>Quae licet ita se habeant, et CRAIGUS in ultimo suo scripto non solum veritatis, sed et modestiae limites multifariam transilierit, parumque ob benevolentiam in illo amando, Regique et tibi eum commendando, non gravatim a me praestitam, grati vicissim animi retulerit: tamen tantum abest ut ipsi ob id male cupiam, quin longe potius etiamnunc, imo multo plus quam antea tibi commendatum velim, quo tua auctoritate et intercessione illius res familiaris augeatur et subsidia ipsi studiorum Mathematicorum plenius rectiusque instituendorum largius suppeditentur, ut id quod aliquot iam annis theorice contemplatus est, per ipsam exercitationem in usu explorare, veritatemque latentem ipsa Praxi eruere commodius valeat. Quaecunque enim per Experientiam in Pragmatiam ratam transferri debeant, longe maiores requirunt impensas et studiorum nervos, quam quae otiosa quadam speculatione animo concipiuntur. Quae cum forte huic CRAIGO non ita abunde (prout res ipsa postulat) suppetant, adeo ut ne quidem ea quae olim in Germania Instrumenta (quae satis paratu vilia fuisse non dubito) se habuisse iactat, nunc ad manus illi sint, ideo de rebus ipsis opinione quadam propria vel auctoritate aliorum falsam persuasionem audentius fovet et abortivum ingenij factum excludit. Rebus itaque iuvandus, quo rerum medullas promptius capiat, nec inanibus saltem verborum corticibus se, alias nudum, oppalliet. Atque haec de illo satis aut forte ad te alijs distentum nimia. Ego nihilominus ut haec omnia benevole accipias et quod iusto plus Craigicis hisce, mihi sane parum, tibi (quemadmodum existimo) longe minus gratis mecum motis controversijs, in tuum sinum, suadente id amicitia nostra, deponendis immorer, teque gravioribus Reipub. administrandae munijs intente occupatum diutius, quam par est, detineam, in potiorem partem pro tuo candore interpreteris, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Vale, et amicitiam nostram per literas continuare non intermitte. Ego vicissim nec locorum nec temporum iniuria vincar, quo minus tui memoriam firmiter animo retinere, et data occasione Epistolis ad te scribendis (cum aliter non detur) in mentem revocare, studiose enitar.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Augusti Anno 1592.<br>Magnif. tuae studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most eminent man, conspicuous above all for the exceptional nobility both of his birth and of his learning, Master John Maitland, Chancellor to the Most Serene King of the Scots, his friend to be most highly honoured.</b></p><p>A very few months ago, most noble and eminent man, I received — though it was already some while back — your letter, in which you reply to the one I sent you last winter. And because it breathed forth in abundance not only a genuine specimen of the rare erudition that is in you, and above all the polish of the Roman tongue, but also the most delightful fragrance of humanity, it was most welcome to me. And so, from the moment your letter was brought to me, I always eagerly longed to reply to it somewhat, relying on some sure messenger. But since the sailing of those by whom I had it in mind to send my [letter] to you is now at last impeded, contrary to my expectation, and from this it came about that my reply to yours was put off until this very time, I therefore judged it by no means to be neglected that, with the merchants who put in here on their way to you, a few words at least should be written back before the onset of winter, so that I might leave with you some attestation, of whatever kind, of a grateful mind and memory, and declare myself in turn one who loves you. I acknowledge indeed that there exist no small tokens of your goodwill toward me, especially in that you did not think it beneath you elegantly to adorn, with your learned and witty verses, my portrait, together with our Urania (to pass over in silence your earlier services to me). For which, to be sure, I give you thanks, and shall give the greatest thanks for ever. Yet there is one thing in them which I do not so much miss as feel to have been said too freely and more lavishly than is right, and in which too you seem to have indulged the licence of the poets to excess — namely, by those three words with which you concluded the last three epigrams. Since these can in no way belong to creatures, but only to the immense and ineffable Creator, I should reckon it unfitting — nay, a sin to be expiated — to ascribe them to any created nature, by whatever figure of speech they be excused. Wherefore if in place of these you substitute something else, which does not overstep the bounds of frail human condition, you will, in my judgement, act more rightly, and render me a more agreeable service. See how great a boldness and frankness [parrhisia] I here employ toward you, which that ingenuous candour of yours and our friendship will (as I hope) not find it hard to excuse. I owe you thanks besides, that you have held Master John Craig, a physician of your country, the dearer even on account of my commendation, and have undertaken to advance him to all the best things — although he has lately, in the third year after the Apology was sent to him, brought forth a certain arrogant writing against our tenets, which he did not blush to call, by an affected and insolent name, the <i>Extinguishing of Capnurania</i> (alas, what a Daedalus, a fabricator of words unheard-of until now!). In which, with shameless presumption, he has boasted that he refutes and overthrows all that is contained in our second book <i>On the New Aethereal Phenomena</i> and in the Apology destined for him under this title — though he does not make even the least of those things waver so much as once, in the eyes of those who, peering more deeply into the matter itself, have learned to ply it duly by practice, and are not hampered by blind prejudice. Far more advisedly, then, would that feeble extinguisher of comets (which are in truth heavenly, and not blazing exhalations in the air, as he, trusting too much in his own Aristotle — as if he had been baptised into him — has imagined) have done, had he learned to extinguish the fire of his own opinions, and the seething heat of his mind, and the inflamed motions of his passions. The unconquered citadel of the truth which we defend, and its firm bulwarks, he will no more wound or storm with those blunt weapons of argument by which he tries to assail it, than if he should presume to pierce the bronze and insuperable shield of Hercules with a leaden pike. For our matters he either understands little and less than rightly, or, on account of his preoccupied opinions, refuses to understand, or else he distorts, corrupts, inverts, and misapplies them. Indeed he quibbles and slanders almost everywhere with a certain unbridled insolence, so that he does not even restrain himself from gibes, sarcasms, doggerel, supercilious revilings, and other pompous follies of that kind, [the marks] of a puffed-up and impotent mind; and concerning things he has never seen nor drawn in at their foundations — let alone made familiar to himself by long practice — he is not afraid to interpose a judgement immature and preposterous, with an acumen futile rather than subtle. Ha! a fine thanks I get from your countryman Craig for the labours drained over so many years in this wrestling-school of seeking out and establishing the truth, and for unremitting vigils, untiring studies, and inestimable expense! Surely, if I shall not have obtained a greater thanks from the rest of Europe's learned mathematicians, either I shall have done so many and so great things in vain (provided I were to look to nothing other than men's gratitude, and not rather to truth alone, which is a sufficient reward to itself), or they will be reckoned greatly ungrateful. But there is no one among all the mathematicians of Italy, France, England, Germany, Bohemia, and Poland who, so far as I know, does not freely and gladly approve our matters, and (however much in certain points they overturn the dogmas usual in the Peripatetics' schools) subscribe to them with a token of a grateful mind. This countryman of yours alone, wise in his own eyes above all others, has thought it not unbecoming — out of a certain anticipated self-love [philautia] and presumption — to becloud the truth which we labour to bring into the light out of the inveterate darkness of opinions and errors, so that thus, just as that man [did] from the devastated temple of Diana, he might procure for himself some name; as though he alone peered more deeply into the matter than all the rest, and were able to peck out the eyes of crows. But to contradictions of this sort — tragic bombast and sophistical, clamouring in vain against the truth we have asserted — our Urania will henceforth deign to make no reply, lest she so uselessly spend good hours that ought to be given to higher and more solid things. Let him flatter himself and please himself and triumph in his little brain as much as he likes. The truth, founded upon most certain and infallible observations (although he has not thought it impious to gnaw even these with his toothed scraper and to condemn them with swollen and rash daring, however much he could scarcely manage to attain and imitate such [observations], or others worthily to be compared with these), and brought into the open by sure geometrical demonstrations (which he no less maliciously belittles, whether through ignorance or the rashness of an affected judgement), does not heed the unwarlike assaults of its attacker. But lest anyone suspect that I shun a reply because I distrust my cause, I shall send his Capnuranic disquisitions — or rather fabrications — to certain distinguished mathematicians in Germany, accustomed to setting up celestial observations accurately and solidly versed in this subject matter (for upon others who understand these things but little, that Craig of yours might easily impose with the painted sophisms in which he abounds), men who know how to decide this controversy by exquisite knowledge and long practice, and who, weighing each point in a fair balance, apart from any passion and prejudice, will pass a just verdict. Perhaps too there will come forth in his own time some one of my friends or pupils who will take it upon himself, at leisure, to settle this controversy and to turn back the invalid blows of Craig's objections. And so at last let him, deluded by his own art, be broken against the solid [truth]. In me, indeed, he will find an antagonist no longer. For it is better to bestow my pains on finishing our volumes of the <i>Astronomical Preliminaries</i> (which I now have in hand) than to be uselessly occupied in beating back such affected mutterings. Of these volumes, not only will the first (which treats copiously of the New Star) abundantly lay open Craig's ignorance and gross errors in this matter too, but the remaining two — treating expressly of the five comets, and most plainly demonstrating that all of them, beyond all doubt, were heavenly — will set before anyone's eyes, more clearly than the noonday light, how wrongly and vainly Craig here opposes our assertions. Hence let him, if he will, draw out the refutation of his most evident hallucination, ignorance, and ineptitude; and at length, having better discerned the deviations by which he is led astray, let him return into the path of truth (if only it can be done).</p><p>Though things stand thus, and Craig in his last writing has in many ways overleapt the bounds not only of truth but also of modesty, and has shown a grateful mind but little in return for the goodwill — bestowed by me not grudgingly — in befriending him and commending him to the King and to you: yet so far am I from wishing him ill on that account, that I would rather far more even now — nay, much more than before — have him commended to you, so that by your authority and intercession his household estate may be increased, and the means of pursuing mathematical studies more fully and rightly may be more liberally supplied to him, so that what he has now for some years contemplated in theory he may the more conveniently test in use through practice itself, and bring out the hidden truth by Practice itself. For whatever things must be carried over from Experience into established Practice require far greater expense and sinews of study than those that are conceived in the mind by a certain idle speculation. And since these things perhaps are not so abundantly at this Craig's command (as the matter itself requires) — so much so that not even those Instruments which he boasts of having once had in Germany (instruments which I do not doubt were cheap enough to procure) are now at his hand — he therefore the more boldly fosters a false persuasion about the matters themselves, whether from some opinion of his own or by the authority of others, and shuts out the miscarried product of his talent. He must therefore be helped with resources, so that he may more readily grasp the marrow of things, and not, being otherwise naked, cloak himself merely with the empty husks of words. And this about him is enough — or perhaps too much for you, distracted by other matters. I nonetheless beg you, again and again, to take all this kindly, and to interpret in the better part, according to your candour, the fact that I dwell more than is right on these Craigic controversies — stirred up against me, indeed, to my small profit and (as I think) far less to your liking — controversies to be laid into your bosom at the prompting of our friendship; and that I detain you, intently occupied with the weightier offices of administering the commonwealth, longer than is fitting. Farewell, and do not cease to continue our friendship by letters. I in turn shall not be overcome by the injury of places or of times, but shall strive zealously to keep your memory firmly in mind, and, when occasion is given, to recall it by writing letters to you (since otherwise it is not granted).</p><p>Again, may you fare most happily.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 19 August, the year 1592.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iohannes Maetelanus</i> = <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b> (c. 1543–1595), Lord Chancellor of Scotland under James VI from 1587, and a Latin poet of some accomplishment. He had visited (or been associated with) Tycho's circle during the 1590 royal connections between Scotland and Denmark (James VI's marriage to Anne of Denmark; the Scottish party's contact with Hven). Maitland had written commendatory verses on a portrait of Tycho and on \"our Urania\" (see below).</li><li><b>Subject of dispute.</b> <i>D. Iohannes Craigus, Medicus vestratem</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician and Aristotelian (later physician to James VI), an Edinburgh-trained Paduan graduate who maintained — against Tycho — the Aristotelian doctrine that comets are sublunary atmospheric exhalations, not celestial bodies. The exchange ran 1588–1592: Tycho sent Craig his second book <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the <i>Novis Aethereis Phaenomenis</i> named here) and an <i>Apologia</i> (1589, never printed); Craig replied in March 1592, in manuscript, with the polemic Tycho here mocks.</li><li><b><i>Capnuraniae restinctio</b></i> — Craig's manuscript tract, fully <i>Capnuraniae restinctio, seu cometarum in aethera sublimationis refutatio</i> (\"The Extinguishing of Capnurania, or a refutation of the raising-up of comets into the aether\"). <i>Capnurania</i> is Craig's coinage from Greek <i>kapnós</i> (καπνός, \"smoke\") + <i>Urania</i>, i.e. \"smoky-Urania\" — a jeer that Tycho's celestial astronomy is built on (cometary) smoke. Tycho calls Craig a <i>Daedalum … vocum fabricatorem</i>, \"a Daedalus, a fabricator of words,\" mocking the neologism. Rendered \"the <i>Extinguishing of Capnurania</i>\"; the wordplay is flagged, not flattened.</li><li><b>Greek loan-words in the Latin.</b> <i>parrhisia</i> (παρρησία, \"frank speech, candour\") — kept and glossed \"frankness [parrhisia].\" <i>philautia</i> (φιλαυτία, \"self-love\") — kept and glossed \"self-love [philautia].\" Both are common in humanist Latin; no Greek script appears in the body.</li><li><b><i>ex devastato Dianae templo … nomen sibi … comparet</b></i> — \"from the devastated temple of Diana … procure for himself some name.\" A pointed allusion to <b>Herostratus</b>, who burned the Temple of Artemis (Diana) at Ephesus in 356 BC precisely to make his name immortal. Tycho casts Craig as a man seeking fame by an act of destruction. Kept as allusion and identified here.</li><li><b><i>cornicum oculos configere</b></i> — \"to peck out the eyes of crows,\" a proverbial phrase (cf. Cicero, <i>Pro Murena</i> 25) for outwitting the most cunning; ironic here. Kept as idiom.</li><li><b><i>aeneum … Herculis clypeum plumbea … sarissa</b></i> — \"the bronze shield of Hercules [pierced] with a leaden pike\"; a <i>sarissa</i> is the long Macedonian pike. The futility-image is kept literal.</li><li><b><i>cothurnis</b></i> — the <i>cothurnus</i> is the high boot of tragic actors; metonymic for inflated, tragic-style bombast. Rendered \"tragic bombast.\"</li><li><b><i>dentata sua strigili rodere</b></i> — \"gnaw with his toothed scraper\"; a <i>strigilis</i> is the bather's scraper. The metaphor of corrosive carping is kept literal.</li><li><b>Astronomy / the works named.</b> <i>libro nostro secundo de Novis Aethereis Phaenomenis</i> = Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (Uraniborg, 1588), the second book of his planned trilogy, arguing the comet of 1577 was supralunary. <i>Tomis nostris Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum</i> = the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the \"Astronomical Preliminaries\"; <i>Progymnasmata</i> lit. \"preliminary exercises,\" kept by sense as <i>Astronomical Preliminaries</i>), whose <b>first</b> volume \"treats copiously of the New Star\" (the 1572 nova, <i>De nova stella</i>) and whose projected further volumes were to treat the <b>five comets</b>. The translation preserves Tycho's claim that all five comets <i>citra omne dubium … coelestes … fuisse</i> (\"were, beyond all doubt, heavenly\") as his own assertion, with the hedge <i>citra omne dubium</i> kept as written.</li><li><b><i>Apologiae illi transmissae anno … tertio</b></i> — \"in the third year after the Apology was sent to him.\" Tycho dates Craig's polemic to the third year after the (1589) <i>Apologia</i> was transmitted, i.e. 1591/92; the manuscript reached Tycho in spring 1592 (so Norlind, after Dreyer VII). Reported, not silently reconciled with the catalogue year.</li><li><b><i>Urania nostra</b></i> — \"our Urania,\" the Muse of astronomy, figuring both Tycho's science and (in the portrait passage) the personified frontispiece-figure of his published astronomy; Maitland's commendatory verses had honoured her. Kept as \"Urania.\"</li><li><b>Theological scruple.</b> The <i>terna illa verba</i> (\"those three words\") with which Maitland's last three epigrams closed are not quoted in the letter; Tycho objects that they ascribe to a creature (<i>conditae naturae</i>) what belongs only to the <i>Creator immenso et ineffabili</i> — a <i>piaculum</i> (\"a sin to be expiated\"). The specific words are a <b>crux</b>: the source does not name them, so the exact phrasing Tycho found impious cannot be recovered from this text alone.</li><li><b>Negations checked.</b> The clauses of praise-with-reservation were translated counting the negations: <i>non … desidero quam … sentiam</i> (\"I do not so much miss … as feel\"); <i>non degravatus sis</i> (\"you did not think it beneath you\"); <i>non erubuit</i> (\"he did not blush\"); <i>ne minimum quidem … vel semel vacillare faciat</i> (\"he does not make even the least … waver so much as once\"); <i>non amplius inveniet Antagonistam</i> (\"he will find an antagonist no longer\"); <i>tantum abest ut … male cupiam, quin … commendatum velim</i> (\"so far am I from wishing him ill … that I would rather … have him commended\") — the <i>tantum abest ut … quin</i> construction is reproduced with its full reversing force, not flattened.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Augusti Anno 1592</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven), 19 August 1592.\" Denmark used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1592, so the date is O.S.; this agrees with the dataset label (<i>1592. 19. august. Uraniborg</i>). No internal date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition / witness.</b> Edition of record: <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i> (= Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, pp. 334 ff.). MS witness as given in the source header: A = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and the rhetoric (allusions, Greek loans, the Herostratus and Hercules images, the tangle of double negatives in the Craig-commendation paragraph) is handled with its force preserved. The one genuine crux is the <i>terna illa verba</i> — the three closing words of Maitland's epigrams that Tycho deemed a <i>piaculum</i> are not quoted in the letter, so what exactly he objected to (most plausibly an attribution of immortality/divinity to Tycho or to Urania) cannot be fixed from this text; flagged rather than guessed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 page images of the slice (pages=\"1-4\", then \"5-6\") visually against the printed source_text and published_english. Collated opening (Perpaucis abhinc mensibus...) and closing (Datae Uraniburgi 19. Augusti Anno 1592, Magnif. tuae studiosiss. Tycho Brahe) plus every proper name (Iohannes Maetelanus/Maitland, Iohannes Craigius/Craig, Aristoteles, Hercules, the six countries Italiae/Galliae/Angliae/Germaniae/Bohemiae/Poloniae) and every number (tertio anno after the Apology; terna illa verba / tria ultima Epigrammata; quinque Cometis) against the Dreyer page images. Every item matches Dreyer's printed Latin exactly; the English (including the dense polemical passages against Craig) renders sense, subject/antecedent, and figurative language accurately with no added or dropped clauses. Published grade was B (self-assigned, unverified); on full collation this letter is clean and upgraded to A.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "John Maitland", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15920516001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920516001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains the delay in replying to Maitland's letter, citing the unexpected impediment to his planned messengers, and now writes via passing merchants.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920819001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920920001", "d": "1592-09-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Wilhelm 4., landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 20 September 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO WILHELMO, LANDTGRAVIO HASSIAe, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO CLEMENTISSIMO. S. D.</p><p>LITERAS Celsit. tuae, Illustrissime Princeps, circa Aprilis auspicia per proprium Tabellarium ad me destinatas iuxta proxime sequentis Mensis initia recte accepi. In quibus Alces quasdam Noruegicas C. T. antea a me promissas, ad se e vestigio transmitti desiderat. Accidit autem, vt ijsdem pene diebus Consanguinei mei Nobilissimi viri AXILLI GVLDENSTERN Regij in Noruegia Vicarij literae vna ad me perlatae sint, quibus conquerebatur se Nauclerum, qui bestias istas incolumes et illaesas huc transferre in se recipere vellet, offendere illic neminem. Idcirco in animo se habere scribebat, possentne terrestri huc itinere perduci, periculum fieri curare. Scripsi nihilominus eidem AXILLO semel atque iterum, iamque antea mihi factas pollicitationes illi in memoriam reuocaui. Verum is neque terrenis locis, conuallibus vndique inuijs, et confragosis rupibus armentis illis transitum difficiliorem reddentibus, tuto et certo adduci posse respondebat. Et licet id fieri concederetur: neutiquam tamen negotium hoc ob aestiuum Solis ardorem, et antequam Caniculares praeterijssent feruores (quos id genus animantium in peregrinis a natiuo solo terris, primo ingressu haud facile sustinet) optatos habiturum successus sperabat. Qua de re se quamprimum profectione in Daniam tum temporis institutâ plenius hîc mecum communicaturum recepit. Atque hinc contigit, vt dum indies vel ipsius Proregis ad nos aduentum auide expectarem, vel animalium istorum copiam nobis interea fieri posse confiderem, C. T. Tabellarium diutius hîc apud me detinere necesse habuerim, donec certi aliquid hâc de re mihi constaret. Nec enim sine Alcibus illis ad T. C. remittendum duxi, praesertim cum is indicârit, sibi iniunctum fuisse, vt non absque his rediret. Sed quamuis praedictus hic AXILLVS expectatione nostra serius, vtpote circa Mensis huius ingressum, huc aduentârit; Peropportune tamen accidit, vt ad idem fere tempus, quo alter T. C. nuntius, posteriores literas de eadem re me commonefacientes asportârit, AXILLVS e Noruegia huc primum appelleret, meque in hac Insula mox inuiseret. Confestim itaque cum ipso de Alcibus dudum promissis, sedulo egi. Verum is difficultates non paucas, quo minus terrâ mariue integra huc valetudine perduci queant, obijciebat, curque hactenus eas mittere non licuerit, causas edidit. At ego instabam, recentiorem illum C. T. nuncium, qui iampridem accedebat, quemadmodum Ferae eiusmodi e longinquis adduci debeant, satis callere: siquidem prius e Suecia aliquot tales T. C. indemnes attulerit. Protinus itaque duobus Nobilibus, quibus Arcem et Prouinciam, cui in Noruegia praeest, se absente commiserat, per literas mandauit, vt quinque Alces, quas hinc inde in mei gratiam conquisierat, atque in varijs locis alendas (donec inde commode huc transferri possent) disposuerat, eidem nuncio vna cum alimentis caeterisque rebus necessarijs diligenter et sine mora procurarent. Is proinde acceptis hisce literis, vna cum libero a me commeatu in Noruegiam iter accelerat, vt obtentis ibi quinis hisce Alcibus, ijsdemque in nauem impositis, recta Bremam traijciat, atque hinc terrestri passu desiderata iam diu animalia ad T. C. qua poterit, cura et sedulitate perducat. Quod si singula sana et viuida istic sistere concessum fuerit, non dubito, quin T. C. petitioni, adeoque diutinae expectationi, iam tandem, quantum in nobis erat, satisfecerimus. Retulit autem Consanguineus ille meus, cum hîc praesto esset, vnam inter istas quas habet Alces satis grandaeuam, eandemque masculam esse: reliquas vero quatuor iuniores partim masculas, partim faemellas. At quotquot et qualescunque fuerint, vt C. T. has boni consulere, atque moram hanc, qua tota pene aestate responsio haec dilata sit, ob antedictas causas excusatam habere dignetur, demisse etiam atque etiam rogo: nec Tabellario primum amandato, quod tam diu hîc haeserit, succensere velit. Si qua alia in re T. C. in hisce oris mea qualicunque opella, quod eidem gratum acceptumque fuerit, quippiam praestari queat, inueniet me vbique et in omnibus quam promptissimum. De Astronomicis nostris exercitijs nunc nihil dicam. Quid in his moliar T. C. alias data occasione intelliget. Id saltem addam, me non libenter ex hoc vltimo T. C. Grammatophoro percepisse, ROTHMANNVM necdum redijsse, Tuaeque Celsitud. Instrumenta Astronomica interea a Coelestium considerationibus prorsus feriari. Ideoque quam maxime optarem, vt C. T. alium quendam harum rerum peritum, in ipsius locum substitui curet, qui inchoatam telam rite pertexat, et Obseruationibus sedulo continuandis deinceps inuigilet. Sed meum non est T. C. quippiam praescribere, praesertim cum naturali quadam propensitate et singulari fauore ad haec studia amplianda et promouenda, non sine praeclara laude, feratur. DEVS T. C. ad omne bonum opus exequendum, quam diutissime conseruet incolumem.</p><p>Iamque T. C. quam felicissime valeat.</p><p>Datae Vraniburgi, 20. Septemb. Anno 1592.</p><p>Celsit. T. Addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: a most gracious Prince and Lord. Greeting.</b></p><p>Your Highness's letter, Most Illustrious Prince, dispatched to me by your own courier about the beginning of April, I duly received toward the beginning of the next month. In it Your Highness desires that certain Norwegian elk [Alces], earlier promised to you by me, be conveyed to you forthwith. But it happened that on almost those same days a letter from my most noble kinsman, Master Axel Gyldenstierne, the King's Governor in Norway, was brought to me together with it, in which he complained that he could find no skipper there who would undertake to bring those beasts here unharmed and unhurt. He therefore wrote that he had it in mind to make trial of whether they could be brought here by an overland route. None the less I wrote to that same Axel once and again, and recalled to his memory the promises that had earlier been made to me. But he replied that they could not safely and surely be brought, since the land places too — valleys impassable on every side, and rugged crags — made the passage harder for those herds. And even were it granted that this be done, he still did not hope that the business would have the wished-for success on account of the summer heat of the Sun and before the dog-day fervours had passed (which a creature of that kind, in lands foreign to its native soil, does not easily endure at its first arrival). On this matter he undertook that, on the journey into Denmark which he had then arranged for the time, he would communicate with me here more fully. And hence it came about that, while day by day I was eagerly awaiting either the Viceroy's own arrival to us, or trusting that in the meantime a supply of those animals might be procured for us, I was obliged to detain Your Highness's courier here with me longer, until something certain about this matter should be settled for me. For I did not think it right to send him back to Your Highness without those elk, especially since he indicated that it had been enjoined upon him that he should not return without them. But although the aforesaid Axel arrived here later than we expected — namely about the beginning of this month — yet it fell out very opportunely that at almost the same time at which Your Highness's other messenger brought a further letter reminding me of the same matter, Axel first put in here from Norway and soon visited me on this Island. Forthwith, therefore, I dealt diligently with him about the elk long since promised. But he raised not a few difficulties as to their being brought here by land or sea in sound condition, and set forth the reasons why hitherto it had not been possible to send them. Yet I pressed him, [arguing] that that more recent messenger of Your Highness, who had now for some time been arriving, was well enough skilled in how wild beasts of that kind ought to be brought from afar — seeing that he had earlier brought some such [beasts] from Sweden to Your Highness unharmed. So at once he gave order by letter to the two noblemen to whom, in his absence, he had committed the castle and the province over which he presides in Norway, that they should diligently and without delay furnish to that same messenger, together with fodder and the other necessary things, five elk which he had sought out here and there for my sake and had distributed in various places to be fed (until they could conveniently be brought hither from there). He accordingly, having received these letters, together with a free safe-conduct from me, hastens his journey into Norway, so that, once these five elk have been obtained there and put aboard ship, he may cross straight to Bremen, and thence by land bring the animals so long desired to Your Highness with what care and diligence he can. And if it be granted that he set them down there each one sound and lively, I do not doubt that we shall now at last, so far as in us lay, have satisfied Your Highness's request, and indeed your long expectation. That kinsman of mine, moreover, reported, when he was present here, that one among those elk which he has is of fair age and is a male; but the other four, being younger, are partly males, partly females. But however many and of whatever kind they prove to be, I most humbly beg, again and again, that Your Highness may deign to take these in good part, and to hold this delay — by which this reply has been put off almost the whole summer — excused for the aforesaid reasons; nor be angry at the courier first sent off, that he has lingered here so long. If in any other matter, on these shores, anything can be rendered to Your Highness by my poor service of whatever sort, that may be welcome and acceptable to you, you will find me everywhere and in all things most ready. Of our astronomical exercises I shall now say nothing. What I am setting in hand in these matters Your Highness shall learn on another occasion as opportunity is given. This much only I shall add: that I have learned, not gladly, from this last letter-bearer of Your Highness, that Rothmann has not yet returned, and that Your Highness's astronomical instruments meanwhile lie wholly idle from the observation of the heavens. And therefore I could most greatly wish that Your Highness would see to it that some other man skilled in these matters be put in his place, who may duly finish the web once begun, and may henceforth keep watch over the observations to be diligently continued. But it is not for me to prescribe anything to Your Highness, especially since by a certain natural inclination and singular favour you are borne toward the enlarging and advancing of these studies, not without distinguished praise. May GOD preserve Your Highness unharmed, for the carrying out of every good work, as long as may be.</p><p>And now may Your Highness fare most happily.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, 20 September, in the year 1592.</p><p>Most devoted to Your Highness,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> Addressed to <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the astronomer-prince of Kassel, Tycho's chief princely scientific correspondent (protocol §5.6). The full titulature — <i>Comiti in Catzenellebogen, Dietz, Zigenhain et Nidda</i> = Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda — is the standard Hessian style, preserved per protocol §2. Wilhelm died on 25 August 1592 (N.S.), shortly before this letter was written (20 September O.S.); Tycho on Hven would not yet have known of his death.</li><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (<i>Vraniburgi</i>, protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (<i>Axillus / Axillo Guldenstern</i>, here in the genitive/dative; d. 1603) — Danish nobleman, <b>royal governor (statholder) of Norway</b> 1588–1601. Tycho calls him <i>Consanguineus meus</i> (\"my kinsman\") — the Brahe and Gyldenstierne families were connected by marriage. The terms <i>Regius in Noruegia Vicarius</i>, <i>Prorex</i> (\"Viceroy\"), and the phrase <i>Arcem et Prouinciam, cui in Noruegia praeest</i> (\"the castle and province over which he presides in Norway\") all denote this governorship (his seat was Akershus, Oslo).</li><li><b><i>Alces</b></i> — <b>elk</b> (European elk / moose, <i>Alces alces</i>). The letter is the principal document of Wilhelm IV's standing request, fulfilled through Tycho, for live Norwegian elk; the famous (later) story of one of Tycho's own elk dying after drinking beer and falling down stairs at Landskrona belongs to a separate, later episode and is <b>not</b> in this letter — not imported here.</li><li><b><i>Caniculares … feruores</b></i> — \"the dog-day fervours,\" the <i>dies caniculares</i>, the hottest part of summer associated with the heliacal rising of the Dog Star (Sirius/Canicula); rendered idiomatically with the dog-day sense kept.</li><li><b><i>Christophorus Rothmannus</b></i> (<i>ROTHMANNVM</i>) — <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, Wilhelm IV's mathematician at Kassel (protocol §5.6), who had left Kassel in 1590 (travelling to visit Tycho on Hven) and had not resumed his post. Tycho's remark that the Kassel <i>Instrumenta Astronomica … a Coelestium considerationibus prorsus feriari</i> (\"the astronomical instruments lie wholly idle from the observation of the heavens\") urges the Landgrave to appoint a successor observer — a request whose timing is poignant, the Landgrave being already dead.</li><li><b><i>inchoatam telam rite pertexat</b></i> — \"duly finish the web once begun\": a weaving metaphor (Penelope's <i>tela</i>) for completing the unfinished programme of Kassel observations; kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>Astronomica nostra exercitia</i> = \"our astronomical exercises\"; <i>Instrumenta Astronomica</i> = \"astronomical instruments\" (§5.2); <i>Coelestium considerationibus</i> = \"the observation of the heavens.\" No technical terms beyond these general ones occur — this is a logistical letter about livestock, not an observational report.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (§5.5); <i>Celsit. tuae / C. T. / T. C.</i> = <i>Celsitudo tua</i>, \"Your Highness\" (§5.5); <i>valeat … quam felicissime</i> = \"may [Your Highness] fare most happily\"; <i>Celsit. T. Addictiss.</i> = <i>Celsitudini Tuae addictissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to Your Highness.\"</li><li><b><i>Tabellarius / nuntius / Grammatophorus</b></i> — three words for the messenger/letter-carrier: <i>Tabellarius</i> (\"courier\"), <i>nuntius</i> (\"messenger\"), and the Greek-derived <i>Grammatophorus</i> (γραμματοφόρος, \"letter-bearer\"); the variation is the author's, preserved by varying the English.</li><li><b><i>libero … commeatu</b></i> — \"a free safe-conduct\" (a <i>commeatus</i>, pass/permit for travel), here issued by Tycho to the messenger; rendered \"safe-conduct.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datae Vraniburgi, 20. Septemb. Anno 1592</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 20 September 1592\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark used the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The catalogue label (\"1592. 20. september. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. Note that Wilhelm IV's death (25 August 1592) is reckoned <b>New Style</b> in Imperial/Hessian usage; the two styles are not reconciled here, only flagged.</li><li><b>Source-text note (editorial sigla stripped).</b> The working transcription carries scattered superscript-<i>A</i> markers (apparatus/variant sigla from the harvested edition — e.g. <i>promissas^A</i>, <i>literae^A</i>, <i>scribebat^A</i>) and several run-together word-joins (e.g. <i>DOMINOA CLEMENTISSIMO</i>, <i>S. D.ALITERAS</i>). These are not part of the letter text; they have been removed and the affected words restored to their plain forms in the Source block above, and the salutation/body and the subscription split onto their own lines. No lexical reading was altered. The edition of record is the <b>1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</b></i> (the volume Tycho printed at Uraniborg; reprinted in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI), per the source's `edition` field. No MS witness is recorded for this letter in the dataset.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the only soft point is editorial rather than lexical (the stripping of the harvested apparatus <i>A</i>-sigla and word-joins, documented above), and the historical irony that the addressee was already dead is noted, not resolved.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Latin secure, dateline agrees; only residue is declared 'A'-sigla stripping + historical irony (the dead addressee).</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. No tboo/slice_pdf reference is available for this letter, so it could not be collated against Dreyer's page images; grade capped at B per protocol regardless of quality. Audited the English against source_text alone: every proper name (Axel Gyldenstierne, Rothmann), place (Norway/Noruegia, Sweden/Suecia, Bremen/Bremam, Uraniborg/Vraniburgi), date (20 Septemb. 1592), and number (quinque Alces = five elk, mentioned twice consistently; duobus Nobilibus = two noblemen; una grandaeva mascula + quatuor iuniores partim masculas partim faemellas = 1 older male + 4 younger mixed-sex elk) matches exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found against source_text.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 20, "pos": 20, "prev": "brahe-t_15920730001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920730001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the delay in delivering the promised Norwegian elk, reporting that his kinsman Axel Gyldenstierne found no skipper willing to transport them safely from Norway.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920921001", "d": "1592-09-21", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 21 September 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(Uraniborg, 1596 = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLVSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO MAVRITIO, LANDTGRAVIO HASSIAe, COMITI IN CATZENELLEBOGEN, DIETZ, ZIGENHAIN ET NIDDA: PRINCIPI AC DOMINO CLEMENTISSIMO. S. D.</p><p>ILLVSTRISSIME et Generosissime Princeps: Plurimâ equidem T. C. me laeticiâ affecit, dum non modo Literis suis clementer nostris respondere: Sed et Carmen commendatitium in Opera, quae molimur, Astronomica, scite et eleganter concinnare, proprijsque digitis circa frontispicia chartae transmissae consignare, non dedignata sit. Pro qua etiam clementi erga me, meaque studia affectione gratias T. C. demisse ago, quas possum, maximas. Exhibebunt utique versus illi clarissimum T. C. erga tam sublimes Artes et me etiam favoris et benevolentiae Τεκμήριον, Posteritati in auspicijs meorum Operum Astronomicorum iugi memoria consecrandum: mihi vero devotae erga T. C. observantiae μνημοσὺνον nulla oblivione interiturum suppeditabit. Cur autem non antea T. C. hoc nomine rescripserim, et quare Tabellarium hunc tam diu hîc detinuerim, caussas in Literis ad Illustrissimum T. C. Dominum Parentem datis, luculenter exposui. Quin et antea idem per Praestantis. et Eruditis. Virum Doctorem NICOLAVM THEOPHILVM, nunc in Academia Hafniensi Iuris Professorem, olim T. C. Parenti in Cancelliae negotijs operam nauantem, indicare animus erat: Quo etiam nomine ad ipsum perscripseram. Verum intellexi postea, ob aduersam valetudinem iter eius, quod in HASSIAM adornârat, praepeditum fuisse. Praeter voluntatem igitur meam accidit, quod adeo tarde tam Illustrissimi Parentis, quam T. C. Literis nunc respondeatur. Quam moram ut C. T. clementi animo in potiorem partem accipiat, atque excusatam habere velit, etiam atque etiam submisse rogo. Nec ulterius T. C. impolitis hisce meis literulis, alijs forte intentam, gravabo. Verum C. T. Deo Opt. Maximo, quam plurimum commendatam cupio. Is C. T. vitam et actiones in vestigia Paternarum virtutum dirigat, et ad Reipub. emolumentum feliciter disponat.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Datae Vraniburgj, 21. Septemb. Anno 1592.</p><p>Celsit. T. Addictis.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse, Count in Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: a Most Clement Prince and Lord. [He] sends greeting.</b></p><p>Most Illustrious and Most Noble Prince: Your Highness has indeed filled me with the greatest joy, in that you have not disdained not only graciously to reply to my letters, but also skilfully and elegantly to compose a commendatory poem upon the astronomical works which we are undertaking, and to sign it with your own hand about the title-page of the sheet sent [to me]. For this clement regard toward me and toward my studies I humbly render Your Highness the greatest thanks I can. Those verses will assuredly furnish, to posterity, a token [Τεκμήριον] — to be consecrated in lasting remembrance at the very outset of my astronomical works — of Your Highness's most illustrious favour and goodwill toward such lofty arts and toward me as well; and to me, in truth, they will supply a memorial [μνημοσὺνον] of my devoted respect toward Your Highness, one that shall perish by no forgetting. But why I did not write back to Your Highness on this account before now, and why I have kept this courier here so long, I have set forth clearly in the letter given to Your Highness's Most Illustrious Lord Father. Indeed, before this, it had been my mind to make the same known through the most excellent and most learned man, Doctor Nicolaus Theophilus — now Professor of Law in the University of Copenhagen, formerly busying himself in chancery affairs for Your Highness's Father — and on that account I had written to him as well. But I learned afterward that, owing to ill health, his journey, which he had arranged into Hesse, had been hindered. It has therefore happened against my will that a reply is being made only now, and so tardily, both to the Most Illustrious Father's and to Your Highness's letters. That Your Highness may take this delay, with a clement mind, in the better part, and be willing to hold it excused, I most submissively beg again and again. Nor shall I burden Your Highness any further with these unpolished little letters of mine, your attention being perhaps bent on other things. But I desire that Your Highness be most fully commended to God, Best and Greatest. May He direct Your Highness's life and deeds into the footsteps of your Father's virtues, and dispose them happily for the good of the commonwealth.</p><p>Farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, 21 September, the year 1592.</p><p>Devoted to Your Highness,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from Uraniborg on Hven. The letter was later printed by Tycho himself in his <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the volume that is the edition of record here; Dreyer reprints it in <i>Opera Omnia</i> VI.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Mauritio, Landtgravio Hassiae</i> = <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), who had succeeded his father <b>Wilhelm IV</b> on the latter's death on 25 August 1592. The salutation's territorial style — <i>Comiti in Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain et Nidda</i> — is the standard Hessian comital titulature; place-names kept in their period form with modern spellings given (Katzenelnbogen, Diez, Ziegenhain, Nidda).</li><li><b>\"Dominum Parentem\" / \"Paternarum virtutum.\"</b> The letter twice invokes Moritz's <i>father</i>. This is the diplomatic crux of the dating (see <b>Calendar / crux</b> below): Tycho writes <i>as though Wilhelm IV were still living</i> — he says he had set out his reasons \"in the letter given to Your Highness's Most Illustrious Lord Father,\" and closes praying that Moritz follow \"the footsteps of your Father's virtues.\"</li><li><b><i>Carmen commendatitium … proprijsque digitis … consignare.</b></i> Moritz had written a <b>commendatory poem</b> for Tycho's projected astronomical works and signed it in his own hand about the title-page (<i>frontispicia</i>) of the sheet sent to Tycho. <i>Carmen commendatitium</i> rendered \"commendatory poem\"; <i>frontispicia</i> = the title-page / frontispiece region of the sheet.</li><li><b><i>Opera … Astronomica, quae molimur.</b></i> \"the astronomical works which we are undertaking\" — Tycho's planned publications (the <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> programme); <i>Opera</i> kept as \"works,\" not narrowed to a single title.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>Τεκμήριον</i> (<i>tekmḗrion</i>) = \"a sure token, proof, attestation\"; rendered <b>\"a token\"</b> (of favour). <i>μνημοσὺνον</i> (printed thus; classical <i>μνημόσυνον</i>, <i>mnēmósynon</i>) = \"a memorial, remembrance, keepsake\"; rendered <b>\"a memorial.\"</b> Both Greek nouns are transcribed in the source block as printed and glossed here per protocol §2. The accentuation <i>μνημοσὺνον</i> (grave on the antepenult-area) is the 1596 print's own; not normalised.</li><li><b><i>Nicolaus Theophilus, nunc in Academia Hafniensi Iuris Professorem, olim T. C. Parenti in Cancelliae negotijs operam nauantem.</b></i> The Latinised <b>Nicolaus Theophilus</b>, then <b>Professor of Law at the University of Copenhagen</b> and formerly active in the chancery service of Moritz's father (Wilhelm IV). Tycho had meant to route his reply through him, but Theophilus's planned journey into Hesse was stopped by ill health. <i>Crux (identity):</i> the precise Danish/vernacular form of \"Nicolaus Theophilus\" is not securely established from the present text alone; the man is identified only by office and prior service and is so left, rather than asserting a vernacular name the source does not give. <i>Cancelliae</i> is the print's spelling for <i>Cancellariae</i> (\"chancery\"); <i>operam nauantem</i> = <i>navantem</i>, \"devoting effort, busying himself.\"</li><li><b><i>Tabellarium hunc … detinuerim.</b></i> <i>Tabellarius</i> = <b>courier / letter-carrier</b>; Tycho apologises for having detained Moritz's courier so long at Hven.</li><li><b>Negation / sense check.</b> <i>non modo … respondere, sed et … concinnare … non dedignata sit</i> = \"you have <b>not</b> disdained <b>not only</b> to reply … <b>but also</b> to compose …\" — the two negatives belong to distinct clauses (<i>non modo</i> \"not only\" + <i>non dedignata sit</i> \"did not disdain\"); the net force is affirmative courtesy (\"you graciously deigned to do both\"), and is so rendered, with no negation added or dropped. <i>nulla oblivione interiturum</i> = \"destined to perish <b>by no</b> forgetting,\" i.e. that will never be forgotten — the single litotes is preserved as the emphatic positive it is, not flattened to a bare \"lasting.\"</li><li><b>Honorifics.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>T. C.</i> / <i>C. T.</i> = <i>Tua Celsitudo</i> / <i>Celsitudo Tua</i>, \"Your Highness,\" used interchangeably through the letter; rendered uniformly \"Your Highness.\" <i>Celsit. T. Addictis.</i> (subscription) = <i>Celsitudini Tuae Addictissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to Your Highness.\" <i>Deo Opt. Maximo</i> = \"to God, Best and Greatest.\"</li><li><b>Orthography (Neo-Latin print, 1596).</b> <i>laeticiâ</i> for <i>laetitiâ</i>; <i>caussas</i> for <i>causas</i>; <i>proprijsque, negotijs, alijs, auspicijs</i> with consonantal <i>-ij-</i>; <i>adornârat</i> = <i>adornaverat</i> (contracted); <i>Reipub.</i> = <i>Reipublicae</i>; <i>Vraniburgj / Datae</i> for <i>Uraniburgi / Datae</i>. <i>e</i>-caudata printed as <i>HASSIAe / LANDTGRAVIO HASSIAe</i> retained.</li><li><b>Textual note (extraction artifact).</b> The working transcription carries a stray capital <b>\"A\"</b> interpolated at numerous word-boundaries (e.g. <i>LANDTGRAVIO<b>A</b> HASSIAe</i>, <i>DOMINO<b>A</b> CLEMENTISSIMO</i>, <i>Exhibebunt<b>A</b> … illi<b>A</b></i>, <i>Τεκμήριον<b>A</b></i>, <i>μνημοσὺνον<b>A</b></i>, <i>Virum<b>A</b></i>, <i>nauantem<b>A</b></i>, <i>nunc<b>A</b></i>, <i>cupio<b>A</b></i>, <i>BRAHE.<b>A</b></i>). These are not letters of the source: they fall at points carrying no Latin sense and recur mechanically. They are read as an OCR/encoding artifact (most likely Dreyer's apparatus reference-marks or a footnote-anchor glyph mis-decoded as \"A\") and have been <b>removed</b> from the Source block to give the readable Latin; the decision is logged here per protocol §2 rather than reproducing the noise as printed. None affects the sense of any clause.</li><li><b>Calendar / crux.</b> Dateline <i>Datae Vraniburgj, 21. Septemb. Anno 1592</i> — <b>21 September 1592 (O.S.)</b>; Denmark and Hesse were both still on the Julian calendar. The catalogue <i>label</i> gives the same date, so no header/label conflict. <b>The substantive crux is the addressee-vs-content tension:</b> Wilhelm IV had died on <b>25 August 1592 (O.S.)</b>, four weeks <i>before</i> this dateline, yet Tycho addresses the new Landgrave Moritz while speaking of Wilhelm as a living \"Lord Father\" to whom he had written and whose footsteps he prays Moritz will follow. The likeliest reading is that news of Wilhelm's death had not yet reached Hven by 21 September — Tycho already knew Moritz as a correspondent (the commendatory poem) but not yet that he had succeeded — and that the salutation to \"Landgrave Moritz\" reflects Moritz's standing as the verse's author rather than knowledge of the succession. This is flagged, not resolved; the date is reproduced exactly as written and is <b>not</b> \"corrected.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the courtly register matched. Two points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the identity of <i>Nicolaus Theophilus</i> beyond office and prior service is left open; (2) the dating crux — a 21 Sept. 1592 letter treating Wilhelm IV (d. 25 Aug. 1592) as living while addressing his successor Moritz — is recorded as a probable news-lag, not reconciled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null (no Dreyer reference supplied), so grade capped at B per protocol. Audited the full Latin text against source_text alone, sentence by sentence, checking both Greek tags (Τεκμήριον, μνημοσὺνον), the named entities (Nicolaus Theophilus, Academia Hafniensi, Hassiam), and the date (21 Septemb. 1592). No fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss found. One minor stylistic point noted but not flagged as a defect: 'clarissimum' in the source modifies the token (Τεκμήριον) itself rather than 'favour and goodwill' as the English implies by placement -- a subtle Latin-syntax nuance, not a change in overall sense, so left unflagged per the ignore-word-order/elegance rule.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15921222001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Moritz for composing a commendatory poem for the astronomical works and notes that the verses will stand as a lasting token of his favour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920921001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15920928001", "d": "1592-09-28", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 28 September 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO, VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO, DOMINO THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO IN PRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>QUAMVIS mi amantissime Domine D. THADDAeE, nihil a te literarum tota hac elapsa aestate acceperim, te tamen incolumi, Deo vitam tibi feliciter prorogante, adhuc esse valetudine spero et ex animo exopto. Ego diutinam hanc literarum intermissionem nequaquam inde accidisse reor, quod vel tu praeter solitum tam diu a scribendo plane cessaveris aut ad meas, quas interea ad te destinâram, nihil omnino responderis; sed (uti alias contigit) ob Tabellariorum perfidiam et incuriam, si quas ad me (prout nihil ambigo) dederis, intercidisse, aut in tanta, quibus disiungimur, locorum intercapedine alicubi interceptas fuisse suspicor; praesertim cum in proximis meis, quas te accepisse non dubito, tuam de quibusdam illic contentis responsionem quam primum ad me perferendam, unâque iudicium de libro nostro ad te ablegato, mihi per opportunitatem communicandum, serio desiderârim. Quorum tamen neutrum tanto temporis intervallo praestitum fuisse antedictis causis ascribendum censeo. Rogo itaque peramanter ut idonea oblata commoditate, tum de praecipuis superiorum ad te literarum momentis, me certiorem reddere, tum etiam de primo nostrorum Progymnasmatum Tomo tibi transmisso censuram tuam interponere mihique impertire velis. Quem iam dudum integrum ad te misissem, nisi varijs exacto hoc semestri negotijs praepeditus fuissem, quo minus hunc typographico labore ad colophonem, uti constitueram, perducere potuerim. Quam primum autem eundem absolvere licuerit (id quod instante nunc hyeme, Deo conatus nostros feliciter adiuvante, futurum confido) mox omnibus numeris absolutum istuc transportari curabo.</p><p>Sed quas iam, praestantissime vir, bullatas naenias ad te adduco? En CRAIGI istius scriptum admodum insolens ac scopticum, quo se omnia, quae in libro nostro secundo de Aethereis sunt Phaenomenis, atque Apologia illi hoc nomine destinata, refutare atque infirmare perfricta fronte iactârit; cum tamen ne minimum quidem eorum apud eos, qui non caeco praeiudicio, sed solidis rationibus haec metiuntur, vacillare faciat. Usqueadeo vir ille Aristotelis de Cometarum generatione fumositatibus, suisque praeiudicatis opinionibus occaecatus est, ut non modo nostra penitus improbare, invertere, distrahere, male accommodare, et scommatis, oblocutionibus, dicterijs, fallacibus cothurnis et sophisticis elenchis turgido ac temerario quodam ausu passim excipere non vereatur: sed etiam ipsi veritati certissimis Observationibus et invictis Demonstrationibus assertae vim et fucum facere, suisque ut obtemperet pomposis sophismatis, invitam eam cogere non erubescat. Hem egregiam ab hoc CRAIGO pro tot non modo assiduis in hac palaestra Astronomica exantlatis laboribus, sed quod tam studiose ipsum Regi Scotorum, cum hîc mecum praesto esset, et Cancellario (ut ex ipsius ad me data Epistola, cuius descriptum unâ ad te mitto, plenius cognosces) tum coram tum per literas iterum atque iterum commendârim, gratiam nunc reporto. At ingratus, qui bene merita non reddit, ingratior, qui dissimulat aut negat; sed omnium ingratissimus, qui beneficium maleficio recompensat. Verum de hoc novo Prometheo et vanissimo Capnuranicae restinctionis compilatore satis.</p><p>A te autem, mi exoptatissime THADDAeE, in primis desidero, ut scriptum hoc magnifico Domino CURTIO, siquando per alias graviores quibus vacat occupationes tantum temporis concedatur, legendum exhibeas, ut inde liquido perspiciat Peripateticum hunc Athletam non propterea tam fastuoso et pertinaci subinde certamine manus mecum conserere, quasi hoc pacto veritatem plenius inveniri, stabiliri aut illustrari omnibus nervis contenderet; sed ut inventam potius calumnijs et cavillis labefactare, et suarum opinionum ac errorum laqueis irretitam in pristinas ignorantiae tenebras reducere queat. Peto insuper ut Capnuranicas hasce subtilitates vel futilitates verius cum ijs, quae in Tomo nostro secundo et Apologia de hoc negotio Cometico pertractata sint, conferre non degravere. Et quae in hasce Scoticas affanias et captiosas argutias animadvertenda censueris, ea in charta denotare et ad me quam primum transmittere velis oro. Interea autem antilogiam istam sarctam tectam apud vos conservari, nec ad alios propalari cupio, mihique, ut ita fiat, omnino persuadeo. Ego, si tantum mihi per alias, quibus operam impendo, lucubrationes otij suppetat, has arrogantes et insulsas strophas ac calumnias facile retundam, vel alijs forte potius affatim discutiendas et diluendas relinquam. Tu, si quid ad me (id quod magnopere a te expeto) antequam navigatio ob frigoris inclementiam interclusa fuerit, rescripseris, id commode Rostochium ad D. D. BRUCAeUM vel Lubecam ad D. D. IOHANNEM SAGERUM communem nostrum amicum dirigere poteris. Inde postea ad me per occasionem tuto et certo facile perferetur.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 28. Septembris Anno 1592.<br>Quaeso ut salutes meo nomine magnificum Dominum CURTIUM quam officiosissime.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and, in every various kind of learning, most erudite and most excellent man, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and of Medicine, his friend above all to be honoured.</b></p><p>Although, my most loving lord Master Thaddaeus, I have received no letter from you throughout this whole summer now past, I nevertheless hope, and from my heart wish, that you are still safe and in good health, God prospering your life happily. I in no way reckon that this long interruption in our correspondence has come about because either you, contrary to your custom, have for so long altogether ceased from writing, or have given no reply at all to the letters of mine that I had meanwhile sent to you; rather (as has happened on other occasions) I suspect that, through the faithlessness and carelessness of the couriers, any [letters] you may have given to be carried to me (as I do not at all doubt you did) have been lost, or have been intercepted somewhere in so great a distance of places as separates us — especially since in my most recent [letter], which I do not doubt you received, I earnestly desired that your reply concerning certain matters contained therein be conveyed to me as soon as possible, and together with it your judgement of our book dispatched to you, to be communicated to me as opportunity allowed. Yet neither of these, I judge, having been rendered in so great an interval of time, is to be ascribed to the causes aforesaid. I therefore most lovingly ask that, when a suitable occasion offers, you would both make me more certain about the chief points of the earlier letter to you, and also lend and impart to me your assessment of the first volume of our <i>Progymnasmata</i> transmitted to you. This [volume] I would long since have sent to you complete, had I not been hindered by various pieces of business in the course of this past half-year, so that I could not, as I had resolved, bring it through the labour of the press to its colophon. But as soon as I am permitted to complete it (which, with winter now pressing on, and God prospering our efforts happily, I am confident will be) I shall at once see to it that it be conveyed thither, finished in every particular.</p><p>But what bullae-sealed dirges [bullatas naenias] do I now bring to you, most excellent man? Behold the writing of that fellow Craig, exceedingly insolent and scoffing, in which with brazen brow he has boasted that he refutes and overturns everything that is in our second book on the Ethereal Phenomena and in the Apology directed to him under this same head; whereas in fact he makes not the least of these things waver in the eyes of those who measure these matters not by blind prejudice but by solid reasonings. So far has that man been blinded by Aristotle's smoke-fumes about the generation of comets, and by his own preconceived opinions, that he does not fear not only to disapprove utterly, to invert, to distort, to misapply our [arguments], and everywhere to carp at them with gibes, contradictions, taunts, fallacious buskins [cothurni], and sophistical refutations [elenchi], with a certain swollen and reckless audacity; but he does not even blush to do violence and false colouring to the truth itself — established by the most certain observations and unconquerable demonstrations — and to drive it, unwilling, into compliance with his own pompous sophisms. Ha — a fine return I now reap from this Craig, in exchange for so many labours not only unceasingly drained dry in this astronomical wrestling-school, but also for having so zealously commended him, again and again, both in person and by letter, to the King of the Scots himself (when he was here present with me) and to the Chancellor — as you will learn more fully from his own letter sent to me, a copy of which I send to you along [with this]. But ungrateful is he who does not repay good deserts; more ungrateful, he who dissembles or denies them; but most ungrateful of all, he who repays a kindness with an injury. But enough about this new Prometheus and most empty compiler of a <i>Capnuranic</i> extinction.</p><p>From you, however, my most longed-for Thaddaeus, I desire above all that you exhibit this writing to be read by the magnificent Master Curtius — whenever, amid the other weightier occupations he is engaged in, so much time may be granted — so that from it he may clearly perceive that this Peripatetic Athlete does not for this reason engage hand to hand with me in so haughty and obstinate a contest, time and again, as though by this means he were striving with all his sinews that truth be more fully found out, established, or illustrated; but rather that he may undermine truth once found, by calumnies and cavils, and, having ensnared it in the toils of his own opinions and errors, lead it back into the former darkness of ignorance. I ask, moreover, that you not think it too burdensome to compare these <i>Capnuranic</i> subtleties — or futilities, more truly — with the matters treated in our second volume and Apology concerning this cometary business. And whatever you may judge worth remarking upon in these Scottish ravings and captious quibbles, I beg that you would set down on paper and transmit to me as soon as possible. Meanwhile, however, I desire that that counter-argument [antilogia] of his be kept whole and under cover among you, and not divulged to others; and I altogether persuade myself that it will be so done. I, if only I have leisure enough amid the other lucubrations on which I expend my effort, shall easily beat back these arrogant and insipid tricks and calumnies — or rather, perhaps, shall leave them to others to be amply dispelled and dissolved. You, if you write back anything to me (which I greatly request of you) before the sailing-passage be closed off through the inclemency of the cold, will be able conveniently to direct it to Rostock to Master Doctor Brucaeus, or to Lübeck to Master Doctor Johannes Sagerus, our common friend. From there it will afterwards easily be brought to me, safely and surely, as occasion offers.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 28 September, the year 1592.<br>I pray that you would greet the magnificent Master Curtius in my name as dutifully as possible.<br>Yours, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, at Uraniborg on Hven, to <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck</b> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (1525–1600), Bohemian physician, astronomer, and imperial court physician at Prague; Tycho's chief scientific correspondent in the Empire and the man who had supplied him manuscript material used in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. The standing salutation register (\"most loving lord,\" \"most longed-for Thaddaeus\") matches their long friendship.</li><li><b><i>Progymnasmatum … primo Tomo</b></i> — the first volume of Tycho's <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (the \"Preliminary Exercises,\" on the nova of 1572). The work was printed slowly at Uraniborg and not published until 1602/1603; here in 1592 the volume is still incomplete — Tycho says he could not \"bring it through the labour of the press to its colophon\" (<i>typographico labore ad colophonem … perducere</i>). <i>colophon</i> kept as the printer's-term it is.</li><li><b><i>libro nostro secundo de Aethereis … Phaenomenis</b></i> — the second book of Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), on the comet of 1577. <i>Apologia illi hoc nomine destinata</i> — the apologia (defence) Tycho addressed to Craig \"under this same head,\" i.e. answering Craig's objections to that book.</li><li><b>The Craig polemic.</b> <i>Craigus / Craigi istius</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician (later physician to James VI/I), an Aristotelian who disputed Tycho's claim that the comet of 1577 lay in the supralunary (ethereal) region rather than being a sublunary exhalation. The two had exchanged sharp letters (Craig's of 9 July 1589 survives in this same correspondence). Tycho calls him a \"Peripatetic Athlete\" (<i>Peripateticum Athletam</i> — a follower of Aristotle's Peripatetic school, cast as a brawler in the \"astronomical wrestling-school,\" <i>palaestra Astronomica</i>) and \"this new Prometheus.\"</li><li><b><i>bullatas naenias</b></i> — literally \"bubble-/bulla-dirges\": rendered \"bullae-sealed dirges.\" <i>naeniae</i> are funeral-dirges, hence worthless droning; <i>bullatae</i> puns on <i>bulla</i> (a sealed document / an empty bubble), so the phrase doubly belittles Craig's tract as a pompously sealed yet hollow lament. The double sense is flagged rather than collapsed to one reading.</li><li><b><i>Capnuranicae restinctionis … Capnuranicas … subtilitates</b></i> — <i>Capnuranica</i> is from Greek καπνός (<i>kapnós</i>, \"smoke\") + οὐρανός (<i>ouranós</i>, \"heaven/sky\"): \"smoke-in-the-heaven,\" Tycho's mocking coinage (or echo of Craig's own title) for Craig's Aristotelian doctrine that the comet was a smoky exhalation. <i>restinctio</i> = \"extinction / quenching\" (of the cometary fire/fumes). Kept transliterated and glossed; <i>Capnuranic</i> italicised as a retained technical coinage. <b>Crux flagged</b> (see Confidence).</li><li><b><i>Aristotelis … Cometarum generatione fumositatibus</b></i> — \"Aristotle's smoke-fumes about the generation of comets\": the Aristotelian theory (Meteorologica) that comets are dry terrestrial exhalations ignited in the upper air — the very doctrine Tycho's parallax measurements overturned.</li><li><b><i>fallacibus cothurnis et sophisticis elenchis</b></i> — \"fallacious buskins and sophistical refutations.\" <i>cothurnus</i> (the tragic actor's high boot) = bombast/inflated style; <i>elenchi sophistici</i> alludes to Aristotle's <i>Sophistici Elenchi</i> (fallacious refutations) — Tycho turns the Aristotelian's own logical apparatus against him. Both kept as the rhetorical/technical terms they are.</li><li><b><i>Domino CURTIO / magnificum Dominum CURTIUM</b></i> — <b>Jacob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), imperial Vice-Chancellor at the court of Rudolf II in Prague, a learned patron of astronomy; Tycho wants Curtius to read Craig's tract and see its bad faith. The closing postscript renews the greeting to him.</li><li><b><i>Regi Scotorum … et Cancellario</b></i> — the <b>King of the Scots</b> = James VI (later James I of England), whom Tycho had hosted on Hven in 1590; and his <b>Chancellor</b> (John Maitland of Thirlestane). Tycho stresses he had repeatedly recommended Craig to both.</li><li><b><i>D. D. BRUCAeUM</b></i> — <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (1530–1593), professor of medicine and mathematics at Rostock; a forwarding address. <i>D. D.</i> = <i>Domino Doctori</i>, \"Master Doctor.\" <b><i>D. D. IOHANNEM SAGERUM</b></i> — <b>Johannes Sager</b> (Sagerus), a Lübeck contact and \"our common friend,\" a second forwarding node. The two are given as the reliable mail-relay before the sea-passage closes for winter (<i>antequam navigatio ob frigoris inclementiam interclusa fuerit</i>).</li><li><b><i>antilogiam istam sarctam tectam … conservari</b></i> — \"that counter-argument of his be kept whole and under cover\": <i>antilogia</i> = a counter-/contrary-argument; <i>sarcta tecta</i> is the legal-administrative formula \"in good repair, intact,\" here \"whole and under cover.\" Tycho asks that Craig's tract be held privately, not circulated.</li><li><b><i>Scoticas affanias</b></i> — \"Scottish ravings\": <i>affaniae</i> = idle nonsense, twaddle (Craig being Scottish).</li><li><b><i>novo Prometheo</b></i> — \"this new Prometheus\": an ironic flourish — Craig as a would-be fire-bringer who in fact only deals in smoke (tying back to the <i>Capnuranica</i> / \"extinction\" jibe).</li><li><b>Editorial note on the source.</b> The verbatim block reproduces the printed forms as given, including <i>responionem</i> (for <i>responsionem</i>), the <i>æ</i>-ligature spellings rendered <i>Ae</i> (THADDAeO, AHaycк→ab Hayck, HAGGECIO, CRAIGO etc.), and <i>j</i> for consonantal <i>i</i> (<i>varijs, negotijs, sophismatis</i>); these are not normalised in the Source block.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>Datas Uraniburgi 28. Septembris Anno 1592</i> — 28 September 1592, <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). No in-letter date discrepancy; the catalogue label agrees with the dateline (place \"Uraniborg\"). No conversion made.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia VII, pages 342 and 343: \"responionem\" corrected to \"responsionem\"; the closing \"Datas\" corrected to \"Datae\". The English translation already read correctly at both sites and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — The Latin is rendered securely and the polemical situation (Craig vs. Tycho on the 1577 comet; the appeal to Curtius via Hagecius) is well attested in the surrounding correspondence and scholarship. The one genuinely soft point, flagged not smoothed, is the precise sense of Tycho's Graeco-Latin coinage <b><i>Capnuranica … restinctio</b></i> — \"smoke-in-the-heaven extinction\": whether <i>restinctio</i> targets Craig's <i>quenching</i> of the cometary fire (his doctrine) or Tycho's <i>snuffing-out</i> of Craig's argument is left as the wordplay it is, rather than resolved to one reading.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Latin secure; the sole residue is a deliberate authorial pun (</i>restinctio<i> double-sense) correctly kept as wordplay.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin letter (TBOO VII pp.342-343, Tycho to Hagecius) against Dreyer's page images, including the heading, the whole Craig/Antagonist polemic, the Curtius/Brucaeus/Sagerus names and place-names (Rostock, Lübeck), and the closing date and signature. Found two single-word departures of our source_text from Dreyer's print (both meeting the TEXT_ERROR definition); neither affects the accuracy of the English, which is complete and faithful throughout, but any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C per the grading rule.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin drops the medial \"s\" from \"responsionem\" (\"responionem\", TBOO VII p.342 line 20-21); the translator nonetheless correctly rendered the intended word as \"reply.\". Dreyer prints: <i>responsionem</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads \"Datas\" for the standard letter-closing participle \"Datae\" (correctly spelled elsewhere in this same sample, e.g. the Kurz letter of the same year); does not affect the correct English rendering \"Given at.\". Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Uraniburgi 28. Septembris Anno 1592</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defect sites were collated against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia volume VII, pages 342-343 (page images, not the OCR text layer). Both are confirmed: \"refponfionem\" at page 342 and the closing \"Datae Uraniburgi\" at page 343. No other cruxes were found on the sampled pages.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Sammelhandschrift (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/23", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961796"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.23."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 28, "prev": "brahe-t_15920322001", "next": "brahe-t_15921202001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15910829001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15921202001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receiving no letter from Hagecius all summer and attributes the silence to courier faithlessness rather than any failure on Hagecius's part.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15920928001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921001001", "d": "1592-10-01", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young — Uraniborg, 1 October 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani<i> Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77 (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE. S.</p><p>QUAMVIS, nobilissime et amplissime vir, ultimis meis de neglecta ad priores aliquot responsione familiari quadam libertate tecum expostulantibus, nihil adhuc rescripseris: tamen et has illis aggregare non supersedebo, utut diutinam hanc tuam sive oblivionem sive spontaneam forte scribendi intermissionem vix (amicitia nostra sic exigente) excusatam habeam. Eroque ob id nunc brevior: praesertim cum superiora tibi adhuc satis in praesenti esse memoria (in quibus etiamnum tuam operam obnixe desidero) mihi persuadeam. Neque enim meas tibi non relatas diffido: ex quo a magnifico D. IO. METELLANO, Cancellario vestro, responsum acceperim, cui eodem tempore literas tuis simul coniunctas destinâram. Cum vero in superioribus praeter alia ut a Serenissimo Rege carmen quoddam in mearum lucubrationum Astronomicarum commendationem impetrares, peramanter te rogassem: et nunc ad ipsius Maiestatem ipsemet hac de causa elegiacam hanc qualemcunque epistolam exarârim (quam tibi ipsius Maiestati meo nomine submisse offerendam unâ mitto) idcirco etiamnum a te idipsum quibus possum precibus rogo, ut tua intercessione, eaque, qua apud ipsam Maiestatem polles, authoritate et consilij grata (quod et mereris) acceptatione, quo id clementissimi beneficij mihi impertiatur, quantum in te est, elaborare non degraveris. De privilegio ab ipsius Maiestate obtinendo, cuius apud te antea satis superque mentionem feci, nunc nihil superaddam. Haec igitur singula, et si quae praeterea sunt, alias significata, tuae erga me benevolentiae et amicitiae nostrae fidelitati fideliter etiam exequenda committo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 1 Oct. A. 1592.<br>Tuae Magnificentiae studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Peter Young. Tycho Brahe sends greeting.</b></p><p>Although, most noble and worthy sir, you have as yet sent no reply to my last letter, in which with a certain familiar freedom I remonstrated with you over your neglect to answer some earlier ones, I shall nonetheless not refrain from adding these too to those — even though I can scarcely hold this long silence of yours, be it forgetfulness or perhaps a deliberate intermission of writing, to be excused (our friendship requiring as much). And for that reason I shall now be the briefer, especially since I persuade myself that those earlier matters are still sufficiently present to your memory (in which matters I still earnestly desire your good offices). For indeed I do not distrust that my [letters] have [in fact] reached you: seeing that I have received an answer from the worshipful Master Jo. Maitland, your Chancellor, to whom at the same time I had addressed a letter joined together with yours. But since in those earlier letters I had, among other things, very affectionately asked you to obtain from the Most Serene King some poem in commendation of my astronomical labours, and since now, for this very reason, I have myself drawn up this elegiac epistle, such as it is, to His Majesty in person (which I send herewith, to be humbly presented to His Majesty in my name by you), I therefore ask the very same thing of you yet again, with what entreaties I can: that by your intercession — and by that authority in which you are strong with His Majesty, and by his gracious acceptance of the counsel (as indeed you deserve) — you will not think it a burden to labour, so far as in you lies, to the end that this most clement favour be bestowed on me. Of the privilege to be obtained from His Majesty, of which I have already made mention to you more than enough, I shall now add nothing further. These several matters, then, and any others besides that have been indicated elsewhere, I commit to be faithfully carried out by your goodwill toward me and by the fidelity of our friendship.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 1 October, the year 1592.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient / address line.</b> <i>PETRO IUNIO · TYCHO BRAHE. S.</i> — the source prints the two elements run together (<i>PETRO IUNIOTYCHO</i>); read as <i>Petro Iunio. Tycho Brahe S[alutem].</i> = \"To Peter Young, Tycho Brahe (sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <b>Peter Young</b> (<i>Petrus Iunius</i>, 1544–1628), Scottish humanist, royal tutor (<i>paedagogus</i>) to the young James VI and one of the King's most trusted advisers and book-agents; he had visited Hven and was Tycho's standing channel to the Scottish court.</li><li><b>Recipient's honorific.</b> <i>nobilissime et amplissime vir</i> → \"most noble and worthy sir\"; the subscription <i>Tuae Magnificentiae studiosissimus</i> → \"Most devoted to Your Magnificence\" (the <i>Magnificentia</i> register carried over from §5.5's <i>studiosiss.</i> formula).</li><li><b><i>IO. METELLANO, Cancellario vestro</b></i> — <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b> (c. 1543–1595), Lord Chancellor of Scotland; <i>Metellanus</i> is the latinised form of <i>Maitland</i>. <i>vestro</i> (\"your,\" plural) = the Chancellor of your [Scottish] realm. Tycho elsewhere corresponded with Maitland directly (letter of 16 May 1592, Lethington).</li><li><b><i>Serenissimo Rege / ipsius Maiestas</b></i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (1566–1625; later James I of England), Young's pupil and sovereign, who had visited Uraniborg in 1590. Not named in the letter; identified here on first occurrence. The <i>carmen … in commendationem</i> is the commendatory poem Tycho sought from the King for his astronomical publications.</li><li><b><i>elegiacam hanc qualemcunque epistolam exarârim</b></i> — \"I have drawn up this elegiac epistle, such as it is\": Tycho's own verse-letter to James VI, composed in elegiac couplets, enclosed for Young to present. <i>qualemcunque</i> (\"such as it is\") is Tycho's self-deprecating hedge, preserved. (<i>exarârim</i> = syncopated <i>exaraverim</i>; <i>destinâram</i> = <i>destinaveram</i>.)</li><li><b><i>lucubrationes Astronomicae</b></i> → \"astronomical labours / lucubrations\" (literally night-work by lamplight); here Tycho's published astronomical writings, for which the royal commendation is sought.</li><li><b><i>De privilegio ab ipsius Maiestate obtinendo</b></i> → \"the privilege to be obtained from His Majesty\": a printing/publishing privilege (the standard early-modern grant of exclusive right against piracy), which Tycho wished the King to grant; he declines to repeat the particulars here.</li><li><b>Latin idiom / syntax.</b> <i>meas tibi non relatas diffido</i> carries a double negative whose force is \"I do not distrust that my [letters] have been delivered to you\" — i.e. Tycho is confident they arrived; the two negations (<i>non … diffido</i>) are reproduced as a litotes of confidence, not collapsed. <i>non supersedebo</i> → \"I shall not refrain\"; <i>non degraveris</i> → \"you will not think it a burden\"; each negation is rendered one-for-one.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 1 Oct. A. 1592</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven), 1 October, the year 1592\" (O.S.; Denmark kept Old Style). The dateline date agrees with the catalogue label (1592. 1. oktober. Uraniborg); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77 (an <i>Afskrift</i> / copy). Edition of record per the dataset: <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> (Dreyer VII).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin heading 'IUNIOTYCHO' corrected to 'IUNIO TYCHO' (missing space) per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.344.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; the only judgement calls (the litotes <i>non … diffido</i> and the run-together address line) are noted in the apparatus and resolved on grammatical grounds, leaving no smoothed-over crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.343-345 as page images (letter to Peter Young entirely on p.344); re-read p.344 at close zoom to confirm the title spacing. Aside from the dropped space in the title, source_text matches Dreyer verbatim (including 'D. IO. METELLANO' = Maitland, correctly identified in translation), and the full body translation is faithful throughout. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it is confined to the title line and does not affect the body or sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text glues 'IUNIO' and 'TYCHO' together with no space in the title line; Dreyer p.344 clearly prints them as two separate words with normal spacing, confirmed by close re-read. Translation itself is correct and unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE. S.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VII p.344 (heading), with pp.343 and 345 also reviewed for context. The single defect is confirmed and fixed; nothing else on these pages diverges from current_latin or current_english.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15920320002", "next": "brahe-t_15930427001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Complains of Young's long silence despite several letters and renews a request to obtain a poem from the King of Scots through Young's good offices.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921001001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921105001", "d": "1592-11-05", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Heinrich Brucæus", "ck": "Heinrich Brucaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Heinrich Brucaeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 5 November 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vols VI–VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO CLARISSIMOQUE VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, DOMINO ET AMICO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO. S. P.</p><p>EGI cum MYLIANDRO de Typographico ministro, verum in praesentia de nullo ipsi constat. Mathematicarum disciplinarum studiosi pauci hodie, plerique enim Theologiae studio vacant. Certum et praesens viaticum inquiram diligentius. De CRAIGO miror, nec scio quomodo paralaxin refellere queat, qua moti veteres ARISTOTELIS interpretes viam lacteam in coelo statuerunt. Velim mihi proximis literis indicari rationes quibus eam evertere conatur, nisi forte observationem culpet. Nam vidi nonnullorum scripta, qui hunc eundem Cometam infra Lunam statuant. Ego certe parallaxi aut nulla aut exigua et minore quam in Luna in hoc Cometa concessa, non video quomodo suam sententiam tueri queat. Si editus est, dabitur aliquando copia legendi. Si autem non est editus, iudico illum haec scripsisse, ut magis excitet ingenij tui acumen, et cogites quae contra dici possint. Homo est eruditus, ut ex DUNCANO intellexi, maiorem modestiam in eo requirerem, si cavillando et detorquendo tua infringere conatur. Attamen non paeniteat commendasse, decet enim bonum virum iuvare plurimos, obesse nemini. Futuram eclipsim, si conspicietur, accuratius observabo: Globum tuum vel Sphaeram armillarem satis affabre confectam vidi, verum parum meis oculis aptam, qui iam incipiunt obtusiores fieri. Non satis autem intelligo cur Terrae polos detorseris a polis mundi et Zodiaci.</p><p>Bene vale.<br>Rostochij Va Novembris A.° Domini 1592.<br>HENRICUS BRUCAeUS<br>plane tuus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most renowned man, Master Tycho Brahe, distinguished mathematician, his lord and friend much to be respected — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I have dealt with Myliander concerning a printer's assistant, but at present he knows of none. Few today are students of the mathematical disciplines, for most give themselves over to the study of theology. I shall inquire more diligently after a sure and ready means of support [for him]. About Craig I wonder, and I do not see how he can refute the parallax by which the ancient interpreters of Aristotle were moved to place the Milky Way in the heaven. I should like to have set out for me, in your next letter, the grounds by which he attempts to overturn it — unless perhaps he finds fault with the observation [itself]. For I have seen the writings of certain men who place this very same comet below the Moon. For my part, certainly, once a parallax either nil or slight, and less than that in the Moon, is granted in this comet, I do not see how he can maintain his opinion. If it has been published, the chance to read it will be given at some time. But if it has not been published, I judge that he wrote these things rather to rouse the keenness of your intellect, and that you might consider what can be said against [them]. He is a learned man, as I have understood from Duncan; I should require greater modesty in him, if it is by quibbling and twisting that he attempts to undermine your [arguments]. Yet let it not be regretted that I commended him, for it befits a good man to help very many and to harm no one. The coming eclipse, if it shall be visible, I shall observe more accurately. Your globe, or the armillary sphere, I have seen, made skilfully enough — but little suited to my eyes, which are now beginning to grow duller. I do not, however, sufficiently understand why you have turned the poles of the Earth aside from the poles of the world and of the Zodiac.</p><p>Fare you well.<br>At Rostock, the 5th of November, in the Year of the Lord 1592.<br>Henricus Brucaeus,<br>wholly yours.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Hendrik van den Broeke, c. 1530/31–1593), born at Alost (Aalst) in Flanders, professor of medicine and of mathematics at the University of Rostock, and an established astronomical correspondent of Tycho's. Writing late in life (he died the following year), he refers to his failing eyesight.</li><li><b>Recipient / place.</b> Tycho is at this date at <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven; Brucaeus writes from <b>Rostock</b> (<i>Rostochium</i>; protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>Myliander</b></i> — a Rostock contact (most plausibly connected to the local book trade / university milieu) through whom Brucaeus sought to recruit a compositor for Tycho's private press on Hven. <b>Identification crux:</b> the name is not securely fixed here; not resolved beyond the printing-recruitment context the letter itself supplies.</li><li><b><i>Typographicus minister</b></i> — rendered \"printer's assistant\" (a journeyman compositor / press-hand for Tycho's Uraniborg printing office); <i>viaticum</i> \"means of support / provision\" — i.e. the wages or maintenance that would secure such a man. Latin technical-of-trade nouns kept in sense per protocol §2.</li><li><b>The Craig controversy (the core of the letter).</b> <i>Craigo</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (Craigius, d. 1620), the Scottish physician (later physician to James VI/I) who disputed Tycho's doctrine that comets are supralunary. Tycho had sent Craig an \"apology\" defending his comet book; Craig in turn composed a refutation (the <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i>, against Tycho's \"smoke-in-heaven\"), upholding the Aristotelian doctrine that comets are sublunary exhalations. Brucaeus's whole technical paragraph is his reaction to Craig's argument. Brucaeus does not know whether Craig's piece is yet <i>editus</i> (\"published\") — at the time of writing it apparently was not — and hedges that, if unpublished, it may be a private provocation to sharpen Tycho's wits; the hedge is preserved, not resolved.</li><li><b><i>paralaxin / parallaxi</b></i> — <b>parallax</b> (protocol §5.1); spelled <i>paralaxin</i> (acc.) once and <i>parallaxi</i> (abl.) once in the source, both retained as printed. Brucaeus's argument: Aristotle's ancient interpreters placed the <b>Milky Way</b> (<i>via lactea</i>) in the heaven (i.e. as a celestial, not sublunary, phenomenon) precisely on a parallax argument; he cannot see how Craig overturns parallax. The decisive clause — <i>parallaxi aut nulla aut exigua et minore quam in Luna … concessa</i> (\"a parallax either nil or slight, and less than that in the Moon, being granted\") — is the standard Tychonic test: a comet showing no measurable parallax, or one smaller than the Moon's, must lie beyond the Moon.</li><li><b><i>observationem culpet</b></i> — \"he finds fault with the observation\": Brucaeus's alternative supposition that Craig attacks not the inference but Tycho's observational data; rendered without sharpening.</li><li><b><i>Cometam infra Lunam statuant</b></i> — \"place this very same comet below the Moon,\" i.e. as sublunary. <i>hunc eundem Cometam</i> (\"this very same comet\") implies a specific comet under discussion; the letter does not name it, so it is left unspecified rather than identified.</li><li><b><i>Duncano</b></i> — <b>Duncan</b>, a Scot (the period form <i>Duncanus</i>) cited as Brucaeus's informant on Craig's learning; not further identified here.</li><li><b><i>cavillando et detorquendo … infringere</b></i> — \"by quibbling and twisting … to undermine\"; the contemptuous pair is kept. <i>tua</i> (\"your [arguments / positions]\") is supplied in brackets as the understood object.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Globum tuum vel Sphaeram armillarem</i> — \"your globe, or the armillary sphere\" (protocol §5.2: <i>globus</i> → globe, <i>armillae/sphaera armillaris</i> → armillary sphere). Brucaeus has seen one of Tycho's instruments but finds it hard on his ageing eyes.</li><li><b><i>Futuram eclipsim … accuratius observabo</b></i> — \"the coming eclipse … I shall observe more accurately\" (<i>eclipsis</i> → eclipse, §5.1); the specific eclipse is not named in the letter.</li><li><b>The terrestrial-poles question.</b> <i>cur Terrae polos detorseris a polis mundi et Zodiaci</i> — \"why you have turned the poles of the Earth aside from the poles of the world and of the Zodiac.\" This records Brucaeus's puzzlement at a feature of Tycho's cosmological/diagrammatic model (the poles of the terrestrial axis displaced from the poles of the celestial sphere <i>mundi</i> and from the ecliptic poles <i>Zodiaci</i>). <i>polos mundi</i> rendered \"poles of the world\" (the celestial poles). The exact construction Brucaeus is reacting to is not elaborated in the letter and is not reconstructed here.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting,\" §5.5); <i>Bene vale</i> → \"Fare you well\" (§5.5); <i>plane tuus</i> → \"wholly yours.\"</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Rostochij Va Novembris A.° Domini 1592</i> = \"At Rostock, the 5th of November, in the Year of the Lord 1592.\" <i>Va</i> is the ordinal <i>quinta</i> (5th); the as-written date agrees with the catalogue label (\"1592. 5. november. Rostock\"). <b>Old Style:</b> Rostock (Mecklenburg, Lutheran) and Denmark both kept the Julian calendar in 1592; the date is O.S.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and its astronomical argument are rendered securely (the parallax-versus-sublunary-comet debate with Craig is well attested in the Tycho biography). Soft points, flagged not smoothed: the identity of <i>Myliander</i> and of <i>Duncanus</i> (named only in passing, not securely resolved); the unnamed comet of <i>hunc eundem Cometam</i>; and the precise model-feature behind <i>cur Terrae polos detorseris a polis mundi et Zodiaci</i>, left as the source states it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.344-346 as page images; Brucaeus's letter entirely on p.345. source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout, word for word. Collated translation clause-by-clause: Myliander and the printer's assistant, the parallax dispute over Craig's comet theory, Duncan's report on Craig's learning, the globe/armillary sphere, and the question about the Earth's poles all render faithfully. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Heinrich Brucæus", "count": 17, "pos": 17, "prev": "brahe-t_15890323001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15890000001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports finding no printer's assistant via Myliander and questions how Craig can refute the parallax evidence placing the recent comet in the supralunary region.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921105001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921117001", "d": "1592-11-17", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 17 November 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Danske Kancellis Arkiv, Uindførte koncepter til åbne breve og missiver</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>RASMUS PEDERSSEN I KUNSØEMAGLE FICH BREFF THILL THYGE BRAHE OM SIN EGEN SAG ANRØRENDIS.</p><p>Thill Thyge Brahe.</p><p>WOR gunst thilforn. Wiidt effthersom Raßmus Pedersen vdj Gunßøemagle vnderdanigst haffuer ladet giffue Oß thill kiende, huorledis handt aff thig vdj attschillige maade delis, thilthallis och paa thet yderste forfølges: for:ne hans gaardt, som handt aff thig nogen thiidt forleden høygt och diurtt fest haffuer, hannum att were formeent och forbøden, saa handt jcke sicker och vden stoer fare thør komme ther jndt, hans broder och thieniste karll och aff thig att were vlouffforuunden fengßligen ahntastet och endnu holdis vdj fengsell, och endog handt jcke gierne wille were vdj saadan mangfoldige och vendige klamer och thrette met thig, meden møget mere thill nogen rolig ende, saa fremt hannum nogen liidelige och billige middell och willckor aff thig egien motte wederfares, bode for thend store jndfestning hand thig for for:ne gardt giffuet haffuer, som och for huiß bygning hand ther paa haffuer bekostet och ahnuendt, saa handt theroffuer icke schulle slet och aldelis forderffuis och ødeleggis, vnderdanigst begierendis, att saadane hans arme och elendige wilckor aff saadane languarendis klamer, thrette och forfølging motte ahnsehes och betenkes, och Wij naadigst wille vnde och giffue hannum Wortt beschermelße breff thill aldt rette att motte beschyttes och beschermis. Tha bede Wij thig och wille, attu saadane hans elendighet och yderste forderff selff fører thig thill sindt och gemøte och wedt thin conscientz betrachter och offuer ueyer, och ther udinden star thig selff thill rette, och huiß christeligt, billigt och louligt ehr emodt hannum forethager och begynder, paa thet Wij theraff jcke schulle foraarsagis effther hans vnderdanigst begiering att vnde och giffue hannum, som en Wore och kronens vndersotte och thend thig aldene aff Wor kiere herre fader sallig och hoglofflig ehukomelße ad gratiam forleent ehr, for:ne Wortt beschermelße breff thill ald rette att handhaffuis och forsuaris. Wij ehre och naadigst vdj thend endelig meening och forsett att wille lade Wore obne befalling vdj samme sag vdghae thill Oß elsk:e Lauge Beck, Wor mandt, thiener och landtzdomer her vdj Wortt landt Sielandt, att handt met andre flere Wore ther thill forordnede gode mendt samme sag met thet allerførste schulle thagge for thennum och endeligen kiende och døme ether emellum vdj alle huiß jrringer och thrette, ether emellum ehr eller were kandt, och aff andre Wore godemendt jcke thilforn om kiendt och dømpt ehr, paa thet jngen aff anten siider schulle haffue thennum att beklage at schee emodt rette och forkortt. Ther som thu nu est thilsindtz att wille haffue eller naffn giffue nogen godemendt, som paa thin siide kunde were vdj samme befalling, thu tha strax met thet allerførste thig therom emodt Oss elsk:e Wor thilforordnede regierings raadt ehrklerer och naffn giffuer, huem thu paa thin siide est begierendis, dog thennum, som boe her vdj landet och ehre vparthiesche vdj samme sag, paa thet samme Wor befalling met thet allerførderligst kunde vdschriffuis och fuldgiøris, och met for:ne languarende och vendige klamer och thrette en gang for alle motte komme thill ende. Ther met scheer Wor wilge, thj thag her aldelis jngen forsømmelße fore.</p><p>Befallendis etc.<br>Schreffuit Haffniae 17. nouembris a:o 1592.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Rasmus Pedersen in Gundsømagle received a letter to Tyge Brahe touching his own cause.</b></p><p>To Tyge Brahe.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, inasmuch as Rasmus Pedersen of Gundsømagle has most humbly given us to understand how he is by you, in sundry ways, sued, charged, and to the uttermost prosecuted: that his aforesaid farm, which he some while ago took of you on lease at a high and dear [rent], has been refused and forbidden him, so that he dares not come into it safely and without great danger; that his brother and serving-man have, without [their having] been lawfully convicted, been seized and imprisoned by you and are still held in prison; and although he would by no means wish to be in such manifold and perverse wrangling and dispute with you, but rather [to come] to some peaceable end — provided that some tolerable and equitable terms and conditions might be granted him by you again, both for the great entry-fine which he has given you for the aforesaid farm, and also for whatever building he has there laid out and expended upon it, so that he should not thereby be utterly and altogether ruined and undone — most humbly desiring that such poor and wretched conditions of his, [arising] from such long-lasting wrangling, dispute, and prosecution, might be regarded and considered, and that we would graciously grant and give him our letter of protection, that he might be protected and defended in all that is right. We therefore ask you and will that you yourself take such wretchedness and uttermost ruin of his to mind and heart, and weigh and consider it by your conscience, and therein hold yourself to what is right, and undertake and set in hand against him whatsoever is Christian, equitable, and lawful; to the end that we be not thereby caused — in accordance with his most humble request — to grant and give him, as one of our and the crown's subjects, and one who was granted to you alone by our dear lord father, of blessed and most laudable memory, <i>ad gratiam</i> [as a favour], the aforesaid our letter of protection, that he be maintained and defended in all that is right. We are also graciously of this final mind and intent, to have our open command in this matter go forth to our beloved Lauge Beck, our man, servant, and provincial judge here in our land of Zealand, that he, together with several other good men of ours appointed thereto, shall at the very first take this matter before them and finally hear and judge between you in all such errors and disputes as are or may be between you, and have not heretofore been heard and adjudged by other good men of ours, to the end that neither of either side should have cause to complain of being [dealt with] contrary to right and shortchanged. If you are now minded to wish to have, or to name, any good men who on your side might be in this same commission, then you shall at once, at the very first, declare yourself thereupon and name to our beloved appointed council of regency whom you desire on your side — yet [only] such as dwell here in the land and are impartial in this matter — to the end that this our command may with all speed be written out and effected, and that with the aforesaid long-lasting and perverse wrangling and dispute an end may at last be made once and for all. Therein is done our will; therefore take herein no neglect at all.</p><p>Commanding [you to God], etc.<br>Written at Copenhagen, 17 November, in the year 1592.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (b. 1577, r. 1588–1648); in 1592 still a minor of fifteen, the kingdom governed by a council of regency (<i>regeringsråd</i>) — hence the letter's reference to \"our beloved appointed council of regency\" as the body Tycho is to address, and the deferral to \"our dear lord father, of blessed memory,\" <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 1588), who had granted Tycho the prebend and its lands. The royal \"we\" is the formal crown voice, not a personal pronouncement by the boy-king.</li><li><b>Recipient / beneficiary.</b> <i>Thyge Brahe</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here addressed not as astronomer but as landlord. <i>Rasmus Pedersen</i> (Raßmus Pedersen) of <b>Gunderslevmagle</b> (<i>Gunßøemagle / Kunsøemagle</i>, a village in southern Zealand) is one of Tycho's tenant farmers, the petitioner whose complaint the letter conveys. The document is a <i>missive</i> to Tycho prompted by — and embodying — Rasmus Pedersen's suit (the rubric: \"Rasmus Pedersen … received a letter to Tyge Brahe touching his own cause\").</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery draft/concept of an open letter or missive (<i>koncept</i>), preserved among the <i>uindførte koncepter</i> (drafts not entered in the registers) of the Danish Chancery Archive. The opening rubric and the closing <i>Befallendis etc.</i> / <i>Schreffuit Haffniae</i> are Latin–Danish chancery formulae; the body is Danish. <i>ad gratiam</i> (\"as a favour / out of grace\") is the one Latin phrase embedded in the body — kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Lauge Beck.</b> <b>Lauge (Lave) Beck</b> (1568–1607), of Førslev, <i>landsdommer</i> (provincial judge) of Zealand; here designated by the king as the commissioner who, with other appointed <i>gode mænd</i> (\"good men,\" i.e. noble arbiters/assessors), is to convene a final adjudication of the long-running dispute between Tycho and his tenant. This is an early instance of the crown intervening in Tycho's seigneurial conduct toward the peasantry of his fiefs — the kind of friction with tenants and the regency that contributed to the deterioration of his position in Denmark.</li><li><b>Legal substance (rendered with care).</b> The petition alleges three grievances against Tycho as landlord: (1) Rasmus Pedersen is barred from the farm he leased at a high rent (\"dares not come into it safely and without great danger\"); (2) his brother and serving-man were <i>imprisoned without lawful conviction</i> (<i>vlouffforuunden … fengßligen ahntastet</i> = \"seized in custody without [their] being lawfully convicted\") and are still held; (3) he stands to be \"utterly ruined,\" having paid a large <i>indfæstning</i> (entry-fine, <i>jndfestning</i>) and spent on buildings. The king does <b>not</b> command Tycho to release or restore; the operative instruction is conditional and admonitory — Tycho is to weigh the case by his conscience and <i>of his own accord</i> do \"whatsoever is Christian, equitable, and lawful,\" <b>so that</b> the king be not driven (<i>jcke schulle foraarsagis</i>) to issue the requested letter of protection. The threat of the protection-letter, and of a binding arbitration under Lauge Beck, is the lever; the act demanded of Tycho is left to him.</li><li><b>Negation count (verified).</b> The Danish body carries the following negations, all reproduced and none added: <i>jcke sicker … thør komme</i> (\"dares not … safely\"); <i>vlouffforuunden</i> (the privative \"un-lawfully-convicted\"); <i>jcke gierne wille were</i> (\"would by no means wish to be\"); <i>icke schulle … forderffuis</i> (\"should not be ruined\"); <i>Wij theraff jcke schulle foraarsagis</i> (\"that we be not caused\"); <i>aff andre … jcke thilforn om kiendt och dømpt ehr</i> (\"have not heretofore been adjudged\"); <i>jngen aff anten siider</i> (\"neither of either side\"); and the closing <i>thag her aldelis jngen forsømmelße fore</i> (\"take herein no neglect at all\"). The conditional <i>saa fremt … motte wederfares</i> (\"provided that … might be granted\") governs the tenant's willingness to settle and is kept as a proviso, not flattened into an assertion.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1592).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilforn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wiidt</i> = imperative <i>vid</i> (\"know\"); <i>effthersom</i> = <i>eftersom</i> (\"inasmuch as\"); <i>delis, thilthallis, forfølges</i> = passives <i>deles, tiltales, forfølges</i> (\"is sued, charged, prosecuted\"); <i>fest</i> = <i>fæst</i> (\"took on lease\"); <i>formeent</i> = <i>forment</i> (\"refused, denied\"); <i>jndfestning</i> = <i>indfæstning</i> (the lease entry-fine); <i>huiß</i> = relative <i>hvis</i> here = \"whatever / what(soever)\"; <i>gemøte</i> = <i>gemyt</i> (\"heart, disposition\"); <i>conscientz</i> = \"conscience\"; <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" standard chancery abbreviation); <i>Oß elsk:e / Oss elsk:e</i> = <i>Os elskelige</i> (\"our beloved,\" courtesy epithet); <i>obne befalling</i> = <i>åbne befaling</i> (\"open command\"); <i>vparthiesche</i> = <i>upartiske</i> (\"impartial\"); <i>vendige klamer</i> = <i>vrange/vendte klammeri</i> here \"perverse [or turned-about] wrangling\"; <i>allerførderligst</i> = <i>allerførderligst/allerforderligst</i> (\"with all speed\"); <i>Befallendis</i> = <i>befalendes</i> (the closing \"commending [you to God]\"). <i>vnderdanigst</i> (\"most humbly\") recurs three times, marking the tenant's, not Tycho's, posture.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Schreffuit Haffniae 17. nouembris a:o 1592</i> = \"Written at <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), 17 November, year 1592\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline and the dataset label (Copenhagen) agree; no date discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected from the real but wrong Zealand village 'Gunderslevmagle' to 'Gundsømagle', matching the source ('Kunsøemagle'/'Gunsøemagle') and Dreyer's own editorial Latin summary ('vico Gundsömagle'), per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV pp.63, 65.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the legal logic (conditional admonition + suspended threat of a protection-letter and forced arbitration) is preserved without sharpening. Two soft points, flagged not smoothed: (a) <i>vendige</i> in <i>vendige klamer</i> — \"perverse / contrary,\" possibly \"turned-about, reversed\" — the sense is \"vexatious and intractable,\" but the precise nuance is uncertain; (b) the antecedent of <i>huiß bygning</i> / <i>huiß … ehr</i> (\"whatever building\" / \"whatsoever is right\") turns on the chancery relative <i>hvis</i>, here taken as the generalizing \"what(soever),\" which the syntax supports but does not put beyond doubt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.63-64 as page images (letter runs from the drop-cap 'W' on p.63 through 'Befallendis etc. Schreffuit Haffniæ 17. nouembris a:o 1592' on p.64). Collated the whole letter clause by clause -- the farm dispute, the imprisoned brother and servant, the entry-fine and building costs, the referral to Lauge Beck and the regency council, the closing formula and date -- all render accurately. The one defect found is a place-name misidentification (Gunsøemagle/Gundsömagle misrendered as the unrelated village Gunderslevmagle), verified against both the letter's own Danish text and Dreyer's independent Latin case-summary on the same page, and corroborated externally (Tycho's 1579 Roskilde-canon fiefdom over Gundsømagle/Gundsøgård). Downgraded from published grade B to C because a wrong toponym in a legal/land dispute is exactly the kind of fact a scholar or cataloguer would carry forward uncorrected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The village is named 'Kunsøemagle'/'Gunsøemagle' in the letter and 'Gundsömagle' in Dreyer's own editorial Latin summary of the same case -- all three point to Gundsømagle, the Sømme-herred village near Roskilde that Tycho held in fee as a Roskilde canon from 1579 (confirmed independently: Roskilde-historie.dk / Trap Danmark on Gundsømagle sogn). The published English instead names a different, real Zealand village, 'Gunderslevmagle' (near Skælskør/Næstved), misidentifying the location of Tycho's disputed tenant farm and the imprisoned brother -- a specific factual error a scholar citing this document would carry forward. At: “Rasmus Pedersen in Gunderslevmagle received a letter to Tyge Brahe touching his own cause. ... how he is by you ... one ”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.63-65 (page images, not OCR text layer). Dreyer's own editorial Latin item-summaries for this and the companion letter (items 96, 98) independently and consistently identify the village as Gundsømagle, corroborating the source's own spellings. Only the English was corrected; the Latin needed no change. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Danske Kancellis Arkiv, Uindførte koncepter til åbne breve og missiver.."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15910830001", "next": "brahe-t_15940608001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Forwards a complaint from Rasmus Pedersen of Gunderslevmagle, who alleges Brahe has denied him his leased farm and unlawfully imprisoned his brother and servant.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921117001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921202001", "d": "1592-12-02", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 2 December 1592 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(Dreyer, </i>Opera Omnia* VII–VIII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.55 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, URANIEBURGI DOMINO ETC D. ET AMICO SUO OBSERVANDO. S.</p><p>NOBILISS. vir, Domine et amice observande. Uno die mensis Febr. huius anni redditae fuerunt mihi plures literae tuae, atque hae diversis temporibus scriptae; quas cum perlegissem, nostro quoque D. CURTIO legendas dedi. Ab eo ipso die totum hoc tempus huc usque valetudine adversa transegi, initio quidem interpollatim recurrebat ingens et intolerabilis dolor ventriculi flatulentus. Postea cum diebus pluribus durasset, iterumque intermisisset aliquando dies 14, aliquando 20, tandem post gravissimum insultum erupit in Ictericiam, quae post dies aliquot febrem continuam protulit. Haec Ictericiae coniuncta me afflixit per menses tres, nimirum August. Sept. et Octob. Salutis spes fuit exigua, sive quis naturam et cursum utriusque morbi, sive corpus subiectum considerasset. Medici certe male ominari consueverunt ei aegroto, quando Icterus excipit febrem. Iam vero praeter ea accessit aetas senilis annorum 66. Quid igitur de recuperanda sanitate sperare potui? Interea non exhorrui mortem: imo libentissime deposuissem hoc corpusculum, si divinae voluntati placuisset. Sum enim iam pertaesus huius aerumnosae vitae, video etiam patriae nostrae magnas impendere calamitates, quarum utinam non essem spectator. Etsi autem sola Dei bonitate ex morbo difficili liberatus sum: tamen, mi TYCHO, vires mei corpusculi ita labefactatae sunt, ut eas nunquam redituras facile intelligam. Ac futuras eas subinde languidiores, varia symptomata, quibus creberrime impetor, praenunciant. Nam et Catharri graves, et pristinus dolor ventriculi, et arenulae, et alij dolores varij, quasi agmine facto in me irruunt, mirifice affligunt et divexant. Addo, me propemodum attingere limites Tympanitis. Sed, ut dixi, ista non expavesco, ut qui sciam Dei consilio et voluntate omnia fieri. Deo me totum commendo, eundemque oro, facere velit, ut reliquum vitae ei soli vivam in timore, expectans cum alacritate diem discessus mei. Itaque iam omnibus alijs studijs, maiori ex parte, nuncium mitto, uni illi studio intentus, ut bene mori addiscam.</p><p>Literas, inquam, illas tuas, quas D. CURTIO me dedisse legendas dixi, morbo illo diuturno impeditus, nondum recipere potui. Hodie, qui est dies ultimus Novembris novo stylo, cum hasce postremas tuas scriptas 28 Septembris a tabellario Magdeburgensi cum scripto illo acerbo sane tui istius fastuosi, praefracti, mordacis et malesani antagonistae accepissem, una et altera pagina perlecta, et obiter perlustrato eo scripto, altero mox die, et epistolam tuam et scriptum illud legenda dedi D. CURTIO, et profecto fuerunt ei admodum grata, quod de te iam diu ne verbum quidem. Postera die, quae fuit 2 Decembris, ad ipsum redeo, ut eius sententiam de scripto illo audirem (nam tabellarius Magdeburgensis discessum parabat, quem noluissem vacuum meis literis redire illuc). Is primum iubet te salvere plurimum, tibique significare scriptum illud tui Scoti non inniti ullis firmis rationibus. Omnem doctrinam parallaxium suis quibusdam phantasmatibus prorsus tollere velle, hostile, non amicum scriptum esse. Itaque censere, omnino abs te refutandum esse. Dixit etiam se illud scriptum accuratius perlecturum esse. Ego quoque, dum modo firmiori sim futurus valetudine, diligenter illud perlegam, deque eo plenius cum D. CURTIO conferam. Haec dum scribo, subijt in mentem Scotus ille, subtilis doctor dictus, quod de eo referant scriptores, fuisse a discipulis confossum stylis pugillaribus. Ego, tuum hunc antagonistam Scotum, quod admirandam et praeclaram Astronomicae doctrinae partem de parallaxibus, suis sophismatis penitus evertere cupiat, ab omnibus studiosis Astronomiae, Baculis Iacobaeis, Gnomonibus et Circinis multo iustius illo subigendum et confodiendum esse censerem. Hoc tu solus, cui nec facultas nec voluntas deest, calamo felicissime praestare poteris. B.V. Datae Pragae die 2. Decemb. novo stylo, Anno D. 1592.</p><p>Privilegium Caesareum emendatum, hodie mihi a D. CURTIO traditum, tibi mitto.</p><p>THAD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble Master Tycho Brahe the Dane, lord of Uraniborg etc., his master and friend to be esteemed — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble sir, my master and friend to be esteemed. On a single day in the month of February of this year several letters of yours were delivered to me, these written at various times; and when I had read them through, I gave them also to our Master Curtius to read. From that very day I have passed all this time up to now in ill health: at the outset, to be sure, a huge and intolerable flatulent pain of the stomach kept recurring intermittently. Afterwards, when it had lasted for several days, and had again let up — sometimes for 14 days, sometimes for 20 — at last, after a most grievous attack, it broke out into jaundice [Ictericia], which after some days brought on a continuous fever. This [fever], joined with the jaundice, afflicted me for three months, namely August, September, and October. The hope of recovery was slight, whether one considered the nature and course of either malady, or the body that was its subject. Physicians, certainly, are accustomed to forebode ill for that patient in whom jaundice follows upon a fever. And now, besides these, there has come on the old age of 66 years. What, then, could I hope concerning the recovery of health? Meanwhile I have not shuddered at death: nay, I would most gladly have laid down this poor body, had it pleased the divine will. For I am now thoroughly weary of this wretched life; I see too that great calamities hang over our homeland — of which would that I were not a spectator. But although by the goodness of God alone I have been freed from a hard sickness, yet, my Tycho, the strength of my poor body has been so shaken that I readily understand it will never return. And that it will be from time to time the more feeble, various symptoms, by which I am very frequently assailed, foretell. For grievous catarrhs, and the old pain of the stomach, and gravel [arenulae], and various other pains rush upon me as though in a marshalled column, afflict me wondrously, and torment me. I add that I am all but reaching the borders of tympanites [dropsy of the belly]. But, as I have said, I do not take fright at these things, as one who knows that all things come to pass by God's counsel and will. I commend my whole self to God, and beseech him that he will be willing to grant that I may live the remainder of my life for him alone, in [his] fear, awaiting with eagerness the day of my departure. And so now, for the greater part, I send word of dismissal to all other studies, intent upon that one study alone: to learn well how to die.</p><p>Those letters of yours, I say, which I told you I had given to Master Curtius to read, I have not yet been able to recover, hindered by that long sickness. Today, which is the last day of November by the new style, when I had received this latest letter of yours, written on 28 September, from the Magdeburg courier — together with that truly bitter screed of that haughty, headstrong, biting, and ill-minded antagonist of yours — and had read through one page and another, and looked over that screed in passing, then on the very next day I gave both your letter and that screed to Master Curtius to read; and they were assuredly most welcome to him, since for a long time now [there had been] not even a word about you. On the following day, which was 2 December, I return to him, that I might hear his opinion of that screed (for the Magdeburg courier was making ready to depart, and I should not have wished him to return thither empty of my letters). He first bids you a hearty good-day, and [bids me] signify to you that that writing of your Scot rests upon no firm reasonings; that to wish to overthrow the whole doctrine of parallaxes utterly by certain phantasms of his own is the writing of an enemy, not a friend; and so he judges that it must by all means be refuted by you. He said too that he would read that writing through more carefully. I also, provided I shall be in firmer health, will read it through diligently, and will confer about it more fully with Master Curtius. While I write this, there has come into my mind that famous Scotus, called the Subtle Doctor — what the writers report of him, that he was stabbed through by his pupils with writing-styluses [stylis pugillaribus]. As for me, I should judge that this Scot, your antagonist — because he desires to overthrow utterly, by his sophisms, that admirable and excellent part of astronomical doctrine concerning parallaxes — ought far more justly than that other one to be subdued and stabbed through by all the students of Astronomy, with cross-staffs [Baculi Iacobaei], gnomons, and compasses [Circini]. This you alone, in whom neither the ability nor the will is wanting, will be able to accomplish most happily with your pen. Fare you well. Given at Prague, 2 December by the new style, in the year of the Lord 1592.</p><p>The amended Imperial privilege, handed to me today by Master Curtius, I send to you.</p><p>THAD.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician, astronomer, and alchemist at Prague — Emperor Rudolf II's <i>protomedicus</i>. Tycho's long-standing scientific ally and his chief conduit to the Imperial court; the observer of the 1572 nova whose <i>Dialexis</i> (1574) Tycho read closely. It was Hagecius who, after this correspondence, helped open the way to Tycho's reception by Rudolf II in 1599.</li><li><b><i>D. CURTIUS</b></i> — <b>Jacobus Curtius</b> (Jakob Kurz von Senftenau, <i>d.</i> 1594), Imperial Aulic vice-chancellor (<i>Reichsvizekanzler</i>) under Rudolf II and an accomplished mathematician-patron in Prague. He acts here as a learned referee of Tycho's dispute and as the channel for the <i>Privilegium Caesareum</i> (the Imperial printing privilege Tycho was seeking).</li><li><b>The \"Scot\" / \"Scotus.\"</b> <i>tui Scoti … tuum hunc antagonistam Scotum</i> = \"your Scot … this Scot, your antagonist.\" The antagonist is <b>John Craig</b> (Johannes Craig / Craigius), the Scottish physician and Aristotelian who disputed Tycho's denial of the celestial spheres and, in particular, attacked Tycho's parallax-based proof that the comets and the 1572 nova lay beyond the Moon. Hagecius exploits a sustained <b>pun on \"Scotus\"</b>: the antagonist is literally <i>the Scot</i> (Craig), but the name also summons <b>Johannes Duns Scotus</b>, the <i>doctor subtilis</i>. Hagecius recalls the (apocryphal) tale that Duns Scotus was stabbed to death by his pupils with their <i>styli</i> (writing-styluses), and turns it into a learned witticism: Craig deserves, even more justly, to be \"stabbed through\" by astronomers — not with styluses but with the instruments of the science he assails. The wordplay is preserved, not flattened.</li><li><b><i>scriptum illud acerbo … antagonistae</b></i> — \"that bitter screed … of [your] antagonist\": Craig's polemical writing against Tycho, which Tycho had forwarded with his own letter of 28 September. Curtius's verdict — that it \"rests upon no firm reasonings\" and \"must by all means be refuted\" — is reported, not endorsed by Hagecius as his own settled judgement (he reserves his fuller reading for when his health allows); the hedge is kept.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>doctrina parallaxium / de parallaxibus</i> → <b>the doctrine of parallaxes / concerning parallaxes</b> (protocol §5.1) — the crux of the whole exchange: parallax measurement was Tycho's instrument for placing comets and the nova in the supralunary heavens, and Craig's <i>phantasmata</i> (\"phantasms,\" i.e. baseless fancies) sought to dissolve it.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>Baculis Iacobaeis</i> = <b>cross-staffs</b> (<i>radius astronomicus</i> / Jacob's staff; protocol §5.2); <i>Gnomonibus</i> = <b>gnomons</b>; <i>Circinis</i> = <b>compasses</b> (a pair of compasses / dividers — here the draughtsman's-and-observer's instrument, not a magnetic compass). The trio names the working tools of the practical astronomer, weaponised in the conceit.</li><li><b>Medicine.</b> <i>dolor ventriculi flatulentus</i> → flatulent pain of the stomach; <i>Ictericia / Icterus</i> → <b>jaundice</b>; <i>febris continua</i> → <b>continuous (continual) fever</b>; <i>Catharri</i> → <b>catarrhs</b>; <i>arenulae</i> → <b>gravel</b> (renal/urinary grit — period term, kept); <i>Tympanitis</i> → <b>tympanites</b> (drum-tight tympanitic dropsy of the abdomen; cf. <i>hydrops</i>, protocol §5.4) — \"I am all but reaching the borders of tympanites,\" i.e. approaching that fatal condition. The clinical sequence (recurrent gastric pain → jaundice supervening on fever → bad prognosis) is rendered without modernising the nosology.</li><li><b><i>bene mori addiscam</b></i> — \"to learn well how to die\" (the <i>ars moriendi</i> topos); the rhetorical pivot of the opening section, kept as the <i>sententia</i> it is.</li><li><b><i>patriae nostrae magnas impendere calamitates</b></i> — \"great calamities hang over our homeland\": Hagecius's foreboding for Bohemia (the gathering religious-political tensions of the 1590s); reported as his apprehension, not as fact.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>Salutem</i> (\"greeting,\" protocol §5.5); <i>B.V.</i> = <i>Bene Vale</i> (\"Fare you well\"); <i>Datae Pragae</i> = \"Given at Prague\"; <i>THAD.</i> = the writer's abbreviated signature (Thaddaeus).</li><li><b>Privilegium Caesareum.</b> \"The amended Imperial privilege\" — the corrected/renewed Imperial printing privilege for Tycho's works, transmitted via Curtius; a concrete fruit of Hagecius's court brokerage.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux (flagged, not resolved).</b> The letter is <b>New Style</b> throughout (Hagecius, in Catholic Imperial Prague, writes Gregorian; Tycho on Hven and his Magdeburg courier were on the Julian Old Style — Tycho's enclosed letter is dated <i>28 September</i>, presumably O.S.). Hagecius's own narrative carries an <b>internal inconsistency</b>: he writes <i>\"Hodie, qui est dies ultimus Novembris novo stylo\"</i> (\"Today, which is the last day of November, n.s.\") = 30 November; says he gave the documents to Curtius <i>\"altero mox die\"</i> (the very next day); then <i>\"Postera die, quae fuit 2 Decembris, ad ipsum redeo\"</i> (\"On the following day, which was 2 December, I return to him\"); and dates the letter <i>\"die 2. Decemb. novo stylo.\"</i> From 30 November, \"the next day\" is 1 December and \"the following day\" 2 December — so the two intermediate steps span 30 Nov → 1 Dec → 2 Dec, which is internally coherent only if <i>\"altero mox die\"</i> and <i>\"Postera die\"</i> denote the <b>same</b> following day described twice (30 Nov + 2 = 2 Dec leaves a one-day gap if read as three distinct consecutive days). Per protocol §3 the dateline is reproduced as written and the discrepancy is recorded, not reconciled: the header date follows the source's own subscription, <b>2 December 1592 N.S.</b>, matching the catalogue label; the narrative day-count is flagged here. <b>A second minor discrepancy:</b> Hagecius gives his age as <i>\"annorum 66,\"</i> though a 1525 birth-year makes him 67 in late 1592 — reproduced as written, not corrected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the internal day-count between <i>dies ultimus Novembris</i> (30 Nov), <i>altero mox die</i>, and <i>Postera die … 2 Decembris</i>, which does not resolve into three cleanly consecutive days; and (2) the stated age \"66\" against a 1525 birth. Neither affects the substance of the letter (the parallax dispute with the \"Scot,\" Craig); the as-written dateline and age are preserved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.345-347 as page images (slice pages 1-4, in two chunks) and collated the full letter clause by clause: the illness narrative and its numbers (14/20-day remissions, three months August-October, age 66), the internal date sequence (letter received 1 Feb; written up 'dies ultimus Novembris'/2 Decembris, matching Dreyer exactly including its own apparent one-day quirk, which is native to the original, not a transcription fault), the Duns Scotus wordplay and the Baculi Iacobaei/Gnomones/Circini list, and the closing formula all match Dreyer verbatim. One low-severity omission found: the final signature 'THAD.' is present in Dreyer but silently dropped from the English. No fabrication, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-transcription error found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>OMISSION</b> (low). Dreyer prints the closing signature 'THAD.' (Hagecius's abbreviated sign-off) after the postscript sentence; the English translation ends at the postscript and silently drops the signature. At: “The amended Imperial privilege, handed to me today by Master Curtius, I send to you.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Checked the sole OMISSION defect against both the packet's own current_english field and the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.347, final line, Hagecius to Tycho). Dreyer confirms 'THAD.' is the correct closing sign-off, and the current_english already carries it verbatim at the end of the text. The audit's premise (that the translation silently drops the signature) does not hold against the actual packet content, so this is recorded as no_change_needed rather than a fix. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.55. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 29, "prev": "brahe-t_15920928001", "next": "brahe-t_15930420001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920928001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930420001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains his long silence by recounting months of serious illness: recurring stomach pain followed by jaundice and a continuous fever lasting through the summer.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921202001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921222001", "d": "1592-12-22", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel — Uraniborg, 22 December 1592 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Early New High German (chancery) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGEN/ HOCHGEBORNEN FÜRSTEN VND HERRN/ HERRN MORITZEN, LANDTGRAFFEN ZU HESSEN/ GRAFFEN ZU CATZENELNBOGEN/ DIETZ/ ZIEGENHAIN VND NIDDA: MEINEM GNEDIGEN HERRN.</p><p>DVRCHLEUCHTIGER/ Hochgeborner/ Gnediger Fürst vnd Herr/ Nechst erbietung meiner vntherthenigen willigen Dienste/ sol Ew. F. G. ich vnterthenigklich nicht vorhalten/ Wie dan ohne das sonder zweiffel E. F. G. gnedige wissenschafft haben werden/ Welcher gestalt der auch Durchleuchtiger Hochgeborner Fürst vnd Herr/ Her WILHELM, Landtgraff zu Hessen/ Graff zu Catzenelnbogen/ Dietz/ Ziegenhain vnd Nidda/ Hochloblicher gedachtnus/ zu vnterschiedlichen mahlen vnter anderen/ etlicher Ellende halben aus Norwegen seiner F. G. zum besten zu bestellen ghar fleissigk an mich geschrieben. Ob ich nuhn wol mir zum höchsten angelegen sein lassen/ Das seiner F. G. ich darinne meinen vnterthenigen geflissenen Willen im Werck bezeigen mochte/ auch etliche deroselbigen Thiere zu wege gebracht; So habe ich doch das Glück nicht haben mügen/ das dieselbigen lebendich an seine F. G. angebracht wurden. Als er aber endtlich Zeigern dieses an mich gnedichlich abgefertigt/ mit befellich/ er solte nitt zu rücke kommen/ ehe er deroselben Thiere mit sich brächte/ hab ich denselbigen Botten von hinnen ab in Norwegen abgefertiget/ vnd allen müglichen fleiss angewendet/ das mihr fünff sülcher Ellende geschafft würden/ Wie mir von Kuniglicher Majett. in Norwegen Stadthalteren/ meinem Blutsverwanten AXEL GVLDENSTERN zugesagt vnd gehalten ist worden. Von denselbigen fünffen/ die er da wol zu passe bekommen/ Hat er mir Zwei/ ein Weiblein vnd ein Menlein lebendig alhie angebracht. In mittelst aber ist alhie die trawrige Zeittunge von Hochgemelter Ihrer F. G. tödtlichen Abgang aus dieser Welt erschollen. Darob ich nit allein (weil er mir alzeit in Gnaden gewogen gewesen) sondern auch alle fromme Hertzen hohes vnd nidriges standes/ billich ein betrüptes mitleiden gehabt. Vnd were zwar wol zu wünschen gewesen/ da es dem Allerhöchsten also gefallen/ das sein F. G. noch ein lange zeitt der Cristlichen Kirchen/ auch sönst dem gantzen Vatterlandt Teutscher Nation zum trost vnd besten/ leben hette mügen. Wan aber seiner Gödtlichen Allmacht in dero weisen gericht nit zu greiffen/ so muß man auch hierinne sich desselbigen vnwandelbaren willen gehorsamlich vntergeben. Der getrewe Gott wolle seine Rutte/ vnd woluerdiente straffe von vns gnedichlich abwenden/ vnd hohe Hâupter vnd Fürsten/ so noch vbrich vnd lebendig geblieben/ erhalten/ Damit seine Kirche vnd Glidtmassen bei denselbigen dürch gnedige Göttliche hülffe/ Rhat/ Trost/ Schütz vnd Schirm haben mügen. Weil dan Gnediger Fürst vnd Herr/ ich es billich dafür halte/ das Hochgemeltes Ew. F. G. löblichen Herrn Vattern befellich zu folge/ Dennoch diese Thiere/ so vbrig/ vnd lebendig geblieben/ an bestimpten Ordt/ vnd an statt seiner löblichen F. G. itzo E. F. G. zugefertiget werden/ Also thue deroselbigen ich sie hiemit bei dero eignen Botten vnthertenigk vbersenden/ Mit vntertheniger Bitt/ E. F. G. wolle sölches nit anders als aus vnterthenigen geneigten willen gescheen/ gnedichlich vermercken/ Nicht weiniger auch/ als dero Hochlöbliger Herr Vatter gethon/ mihr in Gnaden gewogen sein. Könte ich zu alter zeitt vnd gelegenheit E. F. G. hinwiederumb vnthertenige dienste/ worinne es sein wolle/ erzeigen/ solten dieselbigen meinen geneigten vnd getrewen willen in Werck gnedichligk zu erspüren haben/ die ich hiemit im gnedigen schutz des Allerhöchsten bei guter Seel vnd Leibes Gesundtheidt/ vnd angehender heilsamer Regierung (zu welcher Ew. F. G. ich auch hiemit von Gott dem Allmechtigen Glück vnd Segen wünsche) lange Zeitt zu erhalten getrewligk vnd vnterthenigk thu befhelen.</p><p>Datum Vraniburgk auff Huen/ den 22. Decembris, Anno 1592.<br>Ewer F. G.<br>Dienstwilliger<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Serene, High-born Prince and Lord, Lord Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda: my gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Serene, High-born, Gracious Prince and Lord: next after the tender of my humble, willing service, I should not humbly withhold from Your Princely Grace — as indeed Your Princely Grace will without doubt otherwise have gracious knowledge — in what manner the likewise Most Serene, High-born Prince and Lord, Lord Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse, Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda, of most laudable memory, did at sundry times among other matters write to me very diligently, that I should procure for his Princely Grace's benefit certain elk [Ellende] out of Norway. Now although I let it be of the highest concern to me that I might show his Princely Grace my humble, sedulous goodwill in this matter in deed, and also brought several of those animals to pass, yet I could not have the good fortune that the same were delivered alive to his Princely Grace. But when he at length graciously dispatched to me the bearer of this [letter], with command that he should not come back before he brought those animals with him, I dispatched that same messenger from here onward into Norway, and applied all possible diligence that five such elk be procured for me — as was promised to me, and kept, by His Royal Majesty's Governor in Norway, my blood-kinsman Axel Gyldenstern. Of those same five, which he there obtained in good condition, he has delivered to me here two alive, a female and a male. In the meantime, however, the sorrowful tidings have rung out here of the aforesaid His Princely Grace's mortal departure out of this world. Whereat not only I (since he was always graciously well-disposed toward me), but also all godly hearts of high and low estate, have justly had a grieved compassion. And it were indeed greatly to be wished — had it so pleased the Most High — that his Princely Grace might have lived yet a long time, to the comfort and benefit of the Christian Church, and likewise of the whole fatherland of the German nation. But since His Divine Omnipotence in its wise judgment is not to be grasped, so must one in this too submit obediently to that same unalterable will. May the faithful God graciously turn away His rod, and well-deserved punishment, from us, and preserve the high heads and princes who yet remain over and living, so that His Church and her members may have through them — by gracious divine help — counsel, comfort, protection, and shelter. Since then, Gracious Prince and Lord, I justly hold it fitting that, in accordance with the aforesaid command of Your Princely Grace's laudable Lord Father, these animals which have remained over and alive be nonetheless now consigned to the appointed place, and, in the stead of his laudable Princely Grace, to Your Princely Grace, accordingly I herewith humbly send them to the same by Your own messenger; with humble request that Your Princely Grace would graciously take note that this is done from none other than a humble, well-affected will, [and] would no less than your most laudable Lord Father did be graciously well-disposed toward me. Could I at any time and occasion show Your Princely Grace humble service in return, in whatever it might be, the same should graciously find my well-affected and faithful will [made good] in deed; [and] I herewith faithfully and humbly commend Your Princely Grace [to God], to be preserved a long time in the gracious protection of the Most High, in good health of soul and body, and in a wholesome government now begun (to which I herewith likewise wish Your Princely Grace fortune and blessing from God the Almighty).</p><p>Given at Uraniborg on Hven, the 22nd of December, in the year 1592.<br>Your Princely Grace's<br>ready in service,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from Uraniborg, his observatory-house on Hven.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b><i>Moritz, Landtgraff zu Hessen</b></i> = <b>Moritz (\"the Learned\"), Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), who had just succeeded his father. The full titulature <i>Graff zu Catzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain vnd Nidda</i> (Count of Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain, and Nidda) is the standing Hesse-Kassel style and is preserved per protocol §2; <i>Katzenelnbogen</i> is left in its period spelling <i>Catzenelnbogen</i> in the source.</li><li><b><i>Her WILHELM, … Hochloblicher gedachtnus</b></i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), Moritz's father and Tycho's long-standing scientific correspondent. The phrase <i>Hochloblicher gedachtnus</i> (\"of most laudable memory\") marks him as recently deceased: Wilhelm died <b>25 August 1592 (O.S.)</b>, which the letter reports as the \"sorrowful tidings\" that arrived while the elk were being procured. Thus the gift, originally requested by the father, is here redirected to the son.</li><li><b><i>AXEL GVLDENSTERN</b></i> = <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (1542–1603), Royal Governor (<i>Stadthalter / statholder</i>) of Norway 1588–1601, here named as Tycho's <i>Blutsverwanter</i> (\"blood-kinsman\" — the Brahe and Gyldenstierne families were related by marriage and blood among the Danish high nobility). He supplied the elk from Norway.</li><li><b><i>Kuniglicher Majett.</b></i> = \"His Royal Majesty\" — <b>Christian IV of Denmark-Norway</b> (a minor until 1596; Gyldenstierne governed Norway in the crown's name).</li><li><b>Subject term: <i>Ellende</i> / <i>Ellende</i> (sg./pl.).</b> Early New High German <i>Ell(en)d</i> = the <b>elk</b> (European elk / moose, <i>Alces alces</i>; modern German <i>Elch</i>), a prized Scandinavian game animal sought by German princes for their menageries and parks. Rendered \"elk\" throughout; \"those animals\" (<i>deroselbigen / dieselbigen Thiere</i>) refers to the same. The letter records that of five elk obtained in Norway, <b>two</b> reached Hven alive — <i>ein Weiblein vnd ein Menlein</i>, \"a female and a male\" (i.e. a cow and a bull elk).</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The two load-bearing negatives are reproduced exactly: <i>sol … ich vnterthenigklich <b>nicht</b> vorhalten</i> (\"I should <b>not</b> withhold\" — a litotes of disclosure, i.e. \"I make known\"), and the courier's commission <i>er solte <b>nitt</b> zu rücke kommen/ ehe er … brächte</i> (\"he was <b>not</b> to come back before he brought [them]\"). No negation has been added or dropped. The litotes is kept literal (\"should not withhold\") rather than smoothed to \"I hereby report.\"</li><li><b>Chancery register.</b> The whole letter is in the deferential subject's register to a prince: <i>Ew./E. F. G.</i> = <i>Euer/Eure Fürstliche Gnaden</i>, \"Your Princely Grace\"; <i>vntherthenig(lich)</i> \"humble / humbly\"; <i>gnedichlich</i> \"graciously.\" Spelling is Tycho's own Saxon-inflected chancery German as set by his Uraniborg/printed <i>Epistolae</i>: <i>v/u</i> for <i>u/v</i> (<i>vnd</i>, <i>vntherthenig</i>), <i>-tz/-ck</i>, doubled vowels (<i>Hâupter</i>, <i>Zeittunge</i>), <i>mügen</i> for <i>mögen</i>, <i>dürch</i>, <i>sülcher</i>. The circumflex in <i>Hâupter</i> is reproduced as printed.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Printed by Tycho himself in <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg/Wandsbek, 1596), the volume from which the dataset's text descends; in Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> the Hesse-Kassel correspondence is in vol. VI. No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>den 22. Decembris, Anno 1592</i> — given in the running civil style, no Roman-calendar idiom. Denmark used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1592 (and until 1700); the date is therefore O.S. Hesse-Kassel (Protestant) was likewise still Old Style at this date. No in-letter date discrepancy; the catalogue label (\"1592. 22. december\") agrees with the dateline.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Vraniburgk auff Huen</i> = <b>Uraniborg on Hven</b> (the island in the Øresund granted to Tycho by Frederik II in 1576).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German is securely read and the register matched; the sense (a gift of two surviving Norwegian elk, originally requested by the late Wilhelm IV and now redirected to his successor Moritz) is unambiguous. The one lexical point worth flagging, <i>Ellende</i> = elk (not \"misery,\" the homographic NHG <i>Elend</i>), is contextually certain and noted above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible (capped at grade B per brief). Compared published_english line-by-line against the German source_text: the address/titles of Landgrave Moritz, the account of Wilhelm's repeated requests for elk from Norway, the dispatch of the bearer with orders not to return without the animals, the arithmetic (5 elk procured via Axel Gyldenstern, 2 delivered alive here, one female one male), the news of Wilhelm's death and the conventional condolence/submission language, the decision to forward the two surviving animals to Moritz via his own messenger in his late father's stead, and the closing date (22 December 1592) and signature all have full, accurate counterparts. No invented facts, no dropped clauses, no flipped subjects/antecedents found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15920921001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports to Moritz that the Norwegian elk repeatedly promised to his late father Wilhelm could not be delivered alive despite repeated efforts.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921222001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15921228001", "d": "1592-12-28", "dir": "third", "per": "hven", "c": "An unknown doctor of medicine", "ck": "Ukendt medicinsk doktor", "ck2": "Ukendt dansk studerende", "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Padua", "t": "Unknown doctor of medicine → Unknown Danish student — Padua, 28 December 1592 [N.S.] (catalogued as Bologna)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a Greek inflection) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Astronomiae instauratae mechanica </i>(per the source's<i> edition </i>field; the letter is in fact printed in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI, brahe-t_opera_05 — see apparatus) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>... MAGINVS per totam ferme aestatem hîc PATAVII et VENETIIS moratus est. Quâ de caussâ, non satis constat.</p><p>Interea GALLILAeVS de GALLILAeIS FLORENTINVS Professionem Mathematicam hîc adeptus est, qui suarum lectionum septimo Decembris initium fecit. Exordium erat splendidum in magnâ auditorum frequentiâ. A Dno. PINELLO is liberaliter commendatur, quem, si posset, perlibenter in Dni. TYCHONIS amicitiam insinuaret. Tu, qui animum TYCHONIS novisti, poteris, quod ex re erit, in hisce disponere. MAGINVS êdidit nuper librum, cui Titulum fecit [Tabula Tetragonica] sub TYCHONIS patrocinio. Exemplar mihi ad te mittendum dedit, quod primâ occasione transmittam. Retulit etiam, Illustrissimos VENETOS in consilio rogatorum deliberasse, ut aliquis Matheseως peritus stipendio 300. Coronatorum in AeGYPTVM ablegaretur, qui pro TYCHONE isthic observaret. Tantae enim hîc TYCHO certe est celebritatis, quantae nemo eorum, qui nunc vivunt.</p><p>Datae Patavii 28. Decembris Anni 1592.</p>", "en": "<p>... Maginus stayed here, at Padua and at Venice, for almost the whole summer. For what reason, it is not sufficiently clear.</p><p>Meanwhile Galileo de' Galilei of Florence has obtained the Chair of Mathematics here, who made the beginning of his lectures on the seventh of December. The opening was splendid, before a great throng of hearers. He is generously recommended by Master Pinelli, who, if he could, would most gladly insinuate him into the friendship of Master Tycho. You, who have come to know Tycho's disposition, will be able to arrange matters in this affair as shall be fitting. Maginus has lately published a book, to which he gave the title [<i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>], under Tycho's patronage. He gave me a copy to be sent on to you, which I shall forward at the first opportunity. He reported, too, that the Most Illustrious Venetians had deliberated in the Council of the <i>rogati</i> that some man skilled in mathematics [Matheseōs] should be dispatched to Egypt at a salary of 300 <i>coronati</i>, to observe there on Tycho's behalf. For so great, assuredly, is Tycho's renown here as belongs to none of those who are now living.</p><p>Given at Padua, the 28th of December, the year 1592.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent / addressee.</b> Both are unidentified in the catalogue: an <i>Unknown doctor of medicine</i> writing from Padua to an <i>Unknown Danish student</i> who is in personal contact with Tycho (\"you, who have come to know Tycho's disposition\"). The writer is evidently a member of the Paduan academic-medical orbit reporting Italian astronomical news northward; the addressee functions as an intermediary to Tycho. No firmer identification is asserted.</li><li><b>Fragment.</b> The leading ellipsis (\"...\") is in the source: this is an excerpt, the closing portion of a longer letter; only the final movement and dateline survive in the edition. Translated as the partial text it is.</li><li><b><i>Maginus</b></i> = <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of mathematics at Bologna and a correspondent of Tycho's. The <i>librum cui Titulum fecit Tabula Tetragonica</i> is Magini's <i>Tabula tetragonica</i> (Venice, 1592), a table of squares for computation; that it appeared <i>sub Tychonis patrocinio</i> (\"under Tycho's patronage\") is confirmed by the surrounding scholarship.</li><li><b><i>Galileo de' Galilei of Florence</b></i> = <b>Galileo Galilei</b> (1564–1642). He took up the chair of mathematics at the University of Padua in late 1592; the letter's notice that he <i>initium fecit septimo Decembris</i> (\"made the beginning [of his lectures] on the seventh of December\") is a near-contemporary witness to the start of his Paduan inaugural — here said to have drawn <i>magna auditorum frequentia</i> (\"a great throng of hearers\").</li><li><b><i>Pinellus</b></i> = <b>Gian Vincenzo Pinelli</b> (1535–1601), the Paduan humanist whose library and circle were the hub of Veneto learning; he is reported as eager to bring Galileo into Tycho's friendship. <i>insinuaret</i> (\"would insinuate / introduce\") kept in its period sense of effecting an introduction, not pejorative.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>Matheseως</i> — the Greek genitive <i>Mathéseōs</i> (\"of mathematics / of the science\"), the Greek ending <b>-εως</b> grafted onto the Greek stem within an otherwise Latin sentence (<i>aliquis Matheseōs peritus</i> = \"someone skilled in mathematics\"). Transcribed as printed and rendered; the code-switch is flagged, not silently Latinised to <i>Matheseos</i>.</li><li><b><i>consilio rogatorum</b></i> — the Venetian <b>Council (Consiglio) of the <i>Rogati</b></i>, i.e. the Senate (the <i>Pregadi</i>); the senators were the <i>rogati</i> (\"those summoned/invited\"). Rendered \"the Council of the <i>rogati</i>\" with the technical Venetian term kept.</li><li><b><i>Coronatorum</b></i> — <i>coronati</i>, the gold <i>scudo/corona</i> coin; \"300 <i>coronati</i>\" kept as the named coin, not converted.</li><li><b><i>in Aegyptum ablegaretur … qui pro Tychone isthic observaret</b></i> — \"should be dispatched to Egypt … to observe there on Tycho's behalf.\" The notice of a proposed Venetian-funded astronomical mission to Egypt observing <i>pro Tychone</i> is the letter's testimony to Tycho's Italian celebrity (<i>Tantae … celebritatis, quantae nemo eorum qui nunc vivunt</i> — \"of such renown as belongs to none now living\").</li><li><b>Reading note.</b> The working transcription reads <i>isthic</i> (\"here/there\"); the Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan (vol. VI, brahe-t_opera_05.pdf) prints <i>istinc</i> (\"from there / thence,\" idiomatically \"there\"). Both yield \"observe there\"; the translation follows the sense common to both. The bracketed <i>[Tabula Tetragonica]</i> reproduces the editor's square brackets in the source.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> The source's `edition` field names the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i>; the letter is, however, an epistola printed in Dreyer's <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> (the <i>Epistolae</i> volumes), and the matching scan is `…\\Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia\\brahe-t_opera_05.pdf`. The field is reported as given and the actual printed locus noted; no MS witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b>Calendar / place crux.</b> Dateline: <i>Datae Patavii 28. Decembris Anni 1592</i> = \"Given at <b>Padua</b>, 28 December 1592.\" The catalogue `label` reads \"1592. 28. december n.s. <b>Bologna</b>.\" <b>The dateline says Padua, not Bologna</b>; the header therefore leads with Padua and notes the catalogued place. The <i>n.s.</i> of the label is consistent with a writer in the Veneto (Catholic territory, on the Gregorian calendar since 1582); the date is preserved as written and tagged <b>New Style</b> — not converted. The 7 December lecture-date is likewise N.S. as written.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substance (Galileo's 1592 Padua inaugural, Magini's <i>Tabula tetragonica</i>, the Venetian Egypt-mission report) is corroborated. Open points, all flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>place discrepancy</b> dateline-Padua vs. catalogued-Bologna; (2) the <b>unidentified</b> sender and addressee; (3) the <i>isthic/istinc</i> variant; (4) the fragmentary state of the excerpt.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO V p.130, an excerpt subjoined by the Mechanica's typographer from a Padua medical doctor's letter, printed complete there (p.129 is unrelated Chiocci verse; p.131 opens an unrelated Tycho letter re instruments/Venetians). Latin matches source_text exactly. Maginus's stay at Padua/Venice, Galileo's appointment and 7 December lecture start, the Tabula Tetragonica title, the 300-coronati Egypt proposal, and the date (Padua, 28 December 1592) are all rendered accurately with correct numbers and names throughout. No defects found. Note: published_grade was B; this collates clean at A and should be upgraded.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Galileo's new mathematics chair at Padua, Maginus's Tabula Tetragonica under Tycho's patronage, and a Venetian plan to dispatch a mathematician to Egypt on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15921228001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930109001", "d": "1593-01-09", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Uraniborg, 9 January 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Schleswig, Landesarchiv Schleswig-Holstein, Abt. 127.21 (Breitenburg Archive), R 119, foll. 1117–1118 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HENRICO RANZOVIO PRODUCI CIMBRICO S. D. TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>QUAMVIS perpaucis abhinc diebus, nobilissime et amantissime Domine RANZOVI, ultimis tuis ad me datis literis nonnihil responderim: facit tamen diutina illa, quae nobis intercedit, amicitia, tum negotium etiam quoddam postmodum interveniens, ut te alijs insuper compellare nihil addubitem; ex quo illam hanc quoque operam superioribus officijs erga me suis non invite aggregaturam, id vero, quicquid est, tua auctoritate et adiumento promptissime ac ocijssime istic expeditum iri mihi persuadeam. Commodum autem accidit, ut meus olim architectus, quem in quibusdam Astronomicis et Geometricis, dum mecum esset, instruxeram, alias suopte ingenio ad delineationes artis perspectivae proclivem, ad me imagines quasdam calamo repraesentatas, quas frontispicio et calci meorum librorum hîc excudendorum adiungere constitui, huc ex Hallandia (ubi iam apud fratrem meum Varbergi arcis structurae, Regis mandato, praeest) transmiserit. Quas nunc ad te, mi RANZOVI, mitto, amice rogans, velis tua autoritate et iussu efficere, ut Hamburgi vel Lubecae ab excellenti quodam artifice quam optime incidantur, mihique prima quaque occasione, quod intra sex vel septem hebdomadas fieri quam maxime cuperem, sartae tectae remittantur. Quicquid coelatori pro labore pendendum sit, certior a te factus lubens solvam. Da quaeso operam, ut meis hac in parte votis prompte et mature satisfiat. Praestiteris hoc nomine non solum mihi rem admodum gratam, sed et tibi ipsi studijsque communibus operam (uti spero) dignam et utilem elocaveris. Ego si qua in re tibi vicissim pro his alijsque affatim erga me exhibitis benevolentiae officijs inservire queam, faxo ne studium et voluntatem de te pariter, quocunque modo possum, bene merendi in me unquam desideres.</p><p>Vale.<br>Uraniburgi 9. Ianuarij An. 93.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Heinrich Rantzau, Governor of the Cimbrian land — Tycho Brahe sends greeting.</b></p><p>Although but a very few days ago, most noble and most loving Master Rantzau, I made some reply to the last letter you sent me, yet that long-standing friendship which subsists between us — and also a certain piece of business which has since come up — makes me not at all hesitate to approach you with further matters besides; whence I persuade myself that you will not unwillingly add this present service too to your earlier kindnesses towards me, and that this matter, whatever it amounts to, will be dispatched there most promptly and most speedily by your authority and assistance. Now it has fallen out conveniently that my former architect — whom, while he was with me, I had instructed in certain matters of astronomy and geometry, being besides by his own native talent inclined to the drawings of the perspective art — has transmitted to me from Halland (where he now superintends, by the King's command, the building works of Varberg castle in the service of my brother) certain images rendered with the pen, which I have determined to attach to the frontispiece and the tail-piece of my books that are to be printed here. These I now send to you, my Rantzau, asking as a friend that you would by your authority and command bring it about that they be engraved at Hamburg or Lübeck, as excellently as possible, by some outstanding craftsman, and be sent back to me safe and sound at the very first opportunity — which I should most exceedingly wish to be done within six or seven weeks. Whatever is to be paid to the engraver for his labour, once informed of it by you I will gladly discharge. Take pains, I beg, that my wishes in this matter be satisfied promptly and in good time. By so doing you will not only have rendered me a most welcome service, but will also (as I hope) have bestowed upon yourself and upon our common studies a labour worthy and useful. As for myself, if in any matter I can in turn be of service to you in return for these and the other offices of goodwill abundantly shown towards me, I will see to it that you never find wanting in me the zeal and the will to deserve well of you likewise, in whatever way I can.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Uraniborg, 9 January, the year [15]93.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of Hven.</li><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (also Henrik Rantzau, 1526–1598), royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor) of the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein for the Danish crown, humanist, astrological enthusiast, patron and bibliophile, and one of Tycho's most active intermediaries to the German printing and engraving trade. The two corresponded frequently, and Rantzau repeatedly used his standing in the Hamburg–Lübeck region to arrange technical and printing services for Tycho.</li><li><b><i>Produci Cimbrico</b></i> — \"Governor of the Cimbrian land\": <i>Cimbria</i> is the humanist name for the Cimbrian (Jutland) peninsula / the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, so the title styles Rantzau as the (vice-regal) ruler of that land. <i>Produx</i> here renders his governorship in elevated humanist diction; \"Governor\" is given, with the honorific weight noted rather than inflated to a literal \"Duke.\"</li><li><b><i>meus olim architectus</b></i> — \"my former architect\": the draughtsman-architect Tycho had earlier employed and trained at Hven, now (1593) directing, by royal command, the building works at <b>Varberg</b> castle in <b>Halland</b> in the service of Tycho's brother. <i>apud fratrem meum</i> = \"in the service of my brother\" — Tycho's brother <b>Steen Brahe</b>, then lensmand (fief-holder/governor) of Varberg. The architect is most plausibly identified with <b>Hans van Steenwinckel the Elder</b>, who worked at both Hven and Varberg, though the letter does not name him; the identification is left unstated here.</li><li><b><i>delineationes artis perspectivae</b></i> — \"the drawings of the perspective art\": the architect's skill in perspective draughtsmanship, which Tycho exploits for the engraved plates.</li><li><b><i>imagines … calamo repraesentatas</b></i> — \"images rendered with the pen\": pen-drawn designs (the artwork) to be cut by an engraver.</li><li><b><i>frontispicio et calci meorum librorum</b></i> — \"the frontispiece and the tail-piece of my books\": the engravings are destined as the opening and closing plates of the books Tycho was preparing to print at his own Uraniborg/Hven press (<i>hîc excudendorum</i>, \"to be printed here\"). This places the letter among the documents of Tycho's self-publishing programme of the 1590s.</li><li><b><i>incidantur … coelatori</b></i> — <i>incido</i> \"to engrave / cut (in metal)\" and <i>coelator/caelator</i> \"engraver, chaser\": the technical vocabulary of copperplate engraving, to be executed by a master craftsman at <b>Hamburg</b> or <b>Lübeck</b>.</li><li><b><i>sartae tectae</b></i> — the legal-idiomatic phrase <i>sarta tecta</i> (\"kept in good repair,\" lit. \"patched and roofed\"), here used figuratively of the plates being returned \"safe and sound / in good order.\"</li><li><b>Internal dating reference.</b> <i>perpaucis abhinc diebus … nonnihil responderim</i> — Tycho notes he had answered Rantzau's previous letter \"a very few days ago\"; this is a chronological pointer to a slightly earlier letter, not a dating discrepancy.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Uraniburgi 9. Ianuarij An. 93</i> = \"At Uraniborg, 9 January [15]93\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Julian; Denmark retained O.S. until 1700). The dateline as written agrees with the catalogue label (1593, 9 January, Uraniborg); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — Latin secure and the sense unambiguous throughout; the only matters left open are deliberately external to the text (the unnamed architect's identity and the unnamed engraver), which are noted in the apparatus, not smoothed into the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.346-349 as page images; letter text runs p.347 ('HENRICO RANZOVIO PRODUCI CIMBRICO...') to p.348 line 4 ('...Vale. Uraniburgi 9. Ianuarij An. 93.'). source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim (no TEXT_ERROR). Collated opening/closing, all proper names (Rantzau, Halland/Hallandia, Varberg/Varbergi, Hamburg, Lübeck/Lubecae), the date (9 January [15]93), and every clause of the English against the Latin; all present and accurate, including idiomatic renderings (sartae tectae = 'safe and sound', calci = 'tail-piece').</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schleswig, Landesarchiv Schleswig-Holstein, Abt. 127.21 (Breitenburg Archive), R 119, foll. 1117-1118. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15901207001", "next": "brahe-t_15970222001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Asks Rantzau to assist with forwarding perspective drawings sent by his former architect, now supervising construction of Varberg castle in Halland.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930109001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930419001", "d": "1593-04-19", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Jacob Kurz", "ck": "Jacob Kurz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Kurz von Senftenau — Uraniborg, 19 April 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.16 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO AMPLISSIMOQUE VIRO, NOBILITATE GENERIS, PRUDENTIA ATQUE ERUDITIONE ADMODUM CONSPICUO DOMINO IACOBO CURTIO A SENFFTENAV, CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS CONSILIARIO ATQUE IMPERIJ PROCANCELLARIO DIGNISSIMO, AMICO SUO IN PRIMIS HONORANDO. S.</p><p>MAGNIFICE et generosissime Domine CURTI, amice summopere colende, non sine grata mente et memoria vetera tua in me praestita officia etiamnum recolo et amplector, dum tua, qua plurimum vales apud augustissimam Caesaream Maiestatem, auctoritate et consilio, amplissimum , huius Maiestatis Privilegium meis Operibus Astronomicis praefigendum mihi antea clementer impertiri procuraveris, atque gravissimam tuam de his artibus censuram ac propensissimam de ijs bene merendi voluntatem, literis tuis insignem quoque candoris et benevolentiae erga me tuae significationem ostendentibus affatim declarâris. Quibus pristinis in me exhibitis promeritis non parvum insuper cumulum iam nuper aggregaveris, dum recognitum hoc et quibusdam auctum Caesareae Maiestatis Diploma, quo omni ex parte laborum ac impensarum mearum indemnitati caveretur, gratiosissime mihi concedi et transmitti effeceris. Qua de causa et sincerum tuum erga me et haec sublimia studia (quibus ipsemet etiam non modice afficeris, adeo ut si Reipub. administrandae cura his altioribus coelestium siderum contemplationibus non invideret, facile in his utilissimum quid e divite praestantis tui ingenij penu depromeres) amorem luculentius perspiciam, et me rursum debendae gratitudinis nomine tibi in posterum arctius devinciri agnoscam. Tibi itaque, amplifice et praestantissime vir, pro tantis totiesque in me elocatis officijs gratias ago ingentes et immortales, meque tibi vicissim recipio, si quam re unquam grati erga te animi declarare indicia concedatur, perpetuo fore quam promptissimum. Caeterum ex Domini D. THADDAeI literis unâ allatis, insolens illud mei Antagonistae scriptum ad tuas pervenisse manus cognovi. Quem quidem virum in Mathematicis apprime versatum esse non diffiteor. Verumtamen Aristotelicis Disciplinis, quibus ab ineunte aetate (prout plerisque in illa regione usu venire solet) imbutus, usqueadeo serviliter se mancipavit, ut quae ab his vel latum ungvem dissentiant, quantumvis veriora et aliter invictis Apodixibus demonstrata agnoscere et fateri aut nolit aut nequeat. Proinde ex contentioso et arroganti hoc scripto satis perspicere potes, te nimis de eo candide antea sensisse, dum eiusmodi contradictiones ita pro summa animi tui ingenuitate excusaveris, ac si id ille eiusque similes vellent, ut simulate obiectiones quasdam speciosas proponendo pleniorem et exactiorem harum rerum doctrinam publici commodi et posterorum gratia extorquerent. Illum enim rem serio agere et pro ARISTOTELE suo tanquam pro aris et focis pugnare, hinc non obscure animadvertes. Rogo itaque T. M. ut antagonisticam hanc compilationem, utut pretiosissimis suis otijs indignam, diligentius introspicere, mecumque unâ de hac gravissimum suum communicare iudicium non dedignetur. Quamvis autem insulsae eius argutiae confestim a me non immerito essent redarguendae, malo tamen Progymnasmatis Astronomicis, quibus nunc incumbo, in lucem edendis operam locare, quam id generis cavillationes inanes et sophisticas refellendo temporis iacturam facere. Ubi vero primus noster, qui de Nova Stella ubertim tractat Tomus, unâ cum subsequentibus Cometarum libris, in publicum exierit, tum demum candidi et cordati aestimatores non difficulter discernent, quam perperam et inique CRAIGUS his nostris, a Philosophia Peripateticorum dissentientibus placitis tantopere adversetur. Prodibit forte suo etiam tempore discipulorum meorum aliquis, qui eius invalidas obiectiones facile diluat. Misi autem unâ cum his ipsis Domino THADDAeO primum horum (de quibus dixi) Progymnasmatum nostrorum librum, tibi simul exhibendum, ut si quando a gravioribus Reipub. administrandae munijs nonnihil superesset temporis, eum evolvere atque solertissimam tuam de eodem censuram literis aperire non degravarere. Quod ut facias, maiorem in modum te rogatum habeo. Sequentem Tomum, qui trium superiorum Planetarum Phaenomena emendate expediet, mecum aliquantisper reservo, cum ut unam adhuc ultra res, quas in promptu habeo, acronychiam in Saturno animadversionem, ob tarditatem motus eius, qua lente peripheriam sui Eccentrici emetitur, nanciscar, tum praesertim, ut Martem, quando Soli primam decuriam Virginis obtinenti iuxta Perigaeum sui Eccentrici hoc anno opponetur, sedulo observare liceat. Incidit enim tunc diversitas utriusque calculi Alphonsini et Copernicei, quae maxima pene in eius motu contingere potest, adeo ut haec trientem fere unius signi attingat. Quare restitutio nostra in huius Sideris apparentijs tunc maxime probabitur. Apparebit autem tunc Mars solito maior, ita ut ab imperitis pro insueto aliquo sidere reputabitur. Sed ne te Politicis rebus ob illustre quod sustines officium indesinenter occupatum, meis his literis nimium interpellem, pluribus supersedeo. Vale et amicitiam nostram semel per literas incoatam, ijsdem, siquidem aliter fieri nequit, conservare et stabilire non gravere. Quod et vicissim a me expectabis.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Aprilis Anno 1593.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most ample man, most conspicuous in nobility of family, in prudence, and in erudition, Master Jacob Kurz von Senftenau, most worthy Councillor of His Imperial Majesty and Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, his friend to be honoured above all — greeting.</b></p><p>Magnificent and most noble Master Kurz, friend to be cherished in the highest degree: not without a grateful mind and memory do I even now recall and embrace the services you of old rendered me — when, by that authority and counsel of yours, in which you have great weight with His most august Imperial Majesty, you formerly took care that the most ample Privilege of His Majesty, to be set at the front of my astronomical Works, should be graciously bestowed upon me; and when, by letters of yours that displayed also a notable token of your candour and goodwill toward me, you abundantly declared your most weighty judgement of these arts and your most ready disposition to deserve well of them. To these former merits shown toward me you have lately added no small further heap, when you most graciously brought it about that this Diploma of His Imperial Majesty — revised and augmented in certain particulars, by which provision was made on every side for the indemnity of my labours and expenses — should be granted and transmitted to me. For which reason I more clearly perceive both your sincere love toward me and toward these lofty studies (by which you yourself too are not slightly affected, so that, were the care of administering the Commonwealth not to begrudge you these higher contemplations of the heavenly stars, you would readily draw forth from the rich storehouse of your excellent genius something most useful in them); and I acknowledge that I am once again, by the name of the gratitude I owe, more closely bound to you for the future. To you, therefore, most ample and most excellent man, for services so great and so often laid out upon me, I give immense and undying thanks, and I in turn pledge myself to you, [promising] that — if ever it be granted me to declare by some deed the tokens of a mind grateful toward you — I shall perpetually be most ready [to do so]. For the rest, from the letter of Master Doctor Thaddaeus brought along [with yours], I have learned that that insolent writing of my Antagonist has come into your hands. The man I do not deny to be exceedingly well versed in mathematics. Nevertheless he has so slavishly made himself a bondman to the Aristotelian disciplines — in which he was steeped from his earliest years (as is wont to happen with most people in that region) — that whatever dissents from them by even a finger's breadth, however truer it be and otherwise demonstrated by invincible proofs [Apodixes], he either will not or cannot acknowledge and confess. Accordingly, from this contentious and arrogant writing you can see well enough that you earlier judged of him too charitably, when out of the great ingenuousness of your mind you so excused contradictions of this sort, as though he and his like wished, by feigningly putting forward certain specious objections, to extort a fuller and more exact doctrine of these matters for the public good and for the sake of posterity. For that he is acting in earnest, and fighting for his Aristotle as if for altar and hearth, you will here not obscurely observe. I therefore ask Your Magnificence not to disdain to look into this antagonistic compilation more diligently — unworthy as it is of your most precious leisure-hours — and to share with me your most weighty judgement upon it. But although his insipid quibbles ought rightly to be refuted by me without delay, I prefer nevertheless to bestow my labour on bringing into the light the <i>Astronomical Progymnasmata</i> on which I am now engaged, rather than to waste time in confuting cavils of that kind, empty and sophistical. But once our first Volume — which treats copiously of the New Star — together with the ensuing books on the Comets, has gone forth into public, then at last candid and judicious appraisers will not find it hard to discern how wrongly and unfairly <b>Craig</b> so greatly opposes these doctrines of ours, which dissent from the Philosophy of the Peripatetics. Perhaps in due time some one of my pupils too will come forth, who may easily dissolve his feeble objections. I have sent, however, along with this very [letter], to Master Thaddaeus the first book of these <i>Progymnasmata</i> of ours (of which I have spoken), to be shown to you as well, so that, if ever a little time should remain over from the weightier duties of administering the Commonwealth, you would not find it burdensome to unroll it and to disclose by letter your most skilful judgement of it. That you may do so, I have asked you most especially. The next Volume, which will treat correctly the Phenomena of the three superior Planets, I reserve with me for some while — both that I may obtain one further observation, beyond the matters I have ready to hand, namely an acronychal [rising] in Saturn, on account of the slowness of its motion, by which it measures out the circumference of its eccentric so slowly; and especially that I may be permitted diligently to observe Mars when this year it will be in opposition to the Sun, which then holds the first decan of Virgo, near the perigee of its eccentric. For there falls then the divergence of the two computations, the Alphonsine and the Copernican, which can occur in its [Mars's] motion well-nigh at its greatest, so that this [divergence] reaches almost a third of one sign. Wherefore our restoration [of astronomy] will then be most amply proved in the appearances of this Star. Mars will then appear larger than usual, so that by the unskilled it will be reckoned some unaccustomed star. But lest, with these letters of mine, I interrupt you too much — you who, on account of the illustrious office you bear, are ceaselessly occupied with political affairs — I forbear to say more. Farewell, and do not find it burdensome to preserve and make firm the friendship between us, once begun through letters, by those same [letters], since indeed it cannot be done otherwise. Which you shall in turn expect from me.</p><p>Again, most happily, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 19 April, the year 1593.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe</i> writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven) to <i>Iacobus Curtius a Senfftenav</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (d. 1594), Imperial Councillor and <b>Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b> (<i>Imperij Procancellarius</i>) at the court of Emperor Rudolf II in Prague. He had secured for Tycho the Imperial printing <b>privilege</b> (<i>Privilegium</i>) for his astronomical works, and procured a revised and augmented form of it (the <i>Diploma</i>), as this letter of thanks records.</li><li><b><i>Domini D. Thaddaei</b></i> — <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Imperial physician at Prague, astronomer, and Tycho's long-standing correspondent and intermediary at the Imperial court. The book is to be sent to Hájek and shown by him to Kurz.</li><li><b><i>mei Antagonistae … CRAIGUS</b></i> — the \"Antagonist\" named later as <b>Craig</b>: <b>John Craig</b> (Joannes Craigus, d. 1620), the Scottish (Edinburgh) physician and Aristotelian. In 1589 Craig, having received Tycho's book as a gift, sent (per Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV) three letters of objections defending the Aristotelian doctrine that comets are atmospheric and exhibit no sensible parallax, against Tycho's observationally-grounded claim that they lie far beyond the Moon. The present letter responds to a renewed polemical tract by Craig that had reached Kurz's hands.</li><li><b>Tycho's <i>Opera</i> / the <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</b> <i>Progymnasmata Astronomica</i> = Tycho's <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (eventually published 1602). <i>primus … Tomus … de Nova Stella</i> = the first volume, treating the new star of 1572, \"together with the ensuing books on the Comets\" (<i>Cometarum libris</i>; cf. <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588). <i>Sequentem Tomum, qui trium superiorum Planetarum Phaenomena</i> — \"the next Volume … the Phenomena of the three superior Planets\" (Saturn, Jupiter, Mars), then still in preparation and held back pending further observation.</li><li><b>Technical terms (per glossary §5.1–5.3).</b> <i>Eccentricus</i> → <b>eccentric</b>; <i>Perigaeum</i> → <b>perigee</b>; <i>restitutio</i> (nostra) → <b>restoration</b> (Tycho's <i>astronomia instaurata</i> programme — rendered \"our restoration [of astronomy]\"); <i>opponetur / oppositio</i> → <b>opposition</b>; <i>acronychia(m) … animadversionem in Saturno</i> → an <b>acronychal</b> observation of Saturn (i.e. at acronychal/heliacal-opposite rising, when the planet rises as the Sun sets); kept and glossed. <i>prima decuria Virginis</i> → \"the first <b>decan</b> of Virgo\" (<i>decuria</i> here = the astrological decan, the first 10° of the sign); flagged as the decan-sense rather than a literal \"company of ten.\"</li><li><b>Calculation tables.</b> <i>calculus Alphoninus / Coperniceus</i> → the <b>Alphonsine</b> and <b>Copernican</b> computations (the Alfonsine Tables and the tables derived from Copernicus's <i>De revolutionibus</i>); <i>trientem fere unius signi</i> = \"almost a third of one sign,\" i.e. nearly 10°.</li><li><b>Apodixes / Peripatetics.</b> <i>invictis Apodixibus</i> → \"by invincible proofs\" (<i>apodixis</i>, Greek-derived: demonstrative proof). <i>Philosophia Peripateticorum</i> → the <b>Peripatetic</b> (Aristotelian) philosophy.</li><li><b>Honorifics (§5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"greeting.\" <i>T. M.</i> = <i>Tua Magnificentia</i>, \"Your Magnificence.\" <i>Caesarea Maiestas</i> → \"His Imperial Majesty\" (Rudolf II). <i>pro aris et focis</i> → \"for altar and hearth,\" the proverbial phrase for fighting for one's most sacred possessions; kept.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 19. Aprilis Anno 1593</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 19 April 1593\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline date and the dataset <i>label</i> (\"1593. 19. april. Uraniborg\") agree.</li><li><b>Textual cruxes (in the witness / transcription).</b></li><li><i>\"amplissicum ,\"</i> (with trailing comma and apparent word-break): the transcription is corrupt here. Sense and parallel usage (<i>amplissimum … Privilegium</i>) indicate the intended word is <i>amplissimum</i>, \"most ample,\" qualifying <i>Privilegium</i>; rendered \"the most ample[?]\" with the printed form preserved in the Source block and the reading flagged rather than silently emended.</li><li><i>\"amplifice\"</i> (in <i>amplifice et praestantissime vir</i>): an irregular/non-standard vocative, evidently for <i>amplissime</i> (\"most ample\"); the address-formula sense is secure, the exact form is not. Rendered \"most ample\" and flagged.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> One Latin corruption corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII p.348: the non-word 'amplissicum' restored to 'amplissimum'; English's bracketed '[?]' flag removed accordingly. A second audited defect (claimed missing 'his' before 'altioribus') was checked against the page image and rejected: Dreyer does print 'his', matching the source text already published — no change made.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin renders securely throughout and all persons (Kurz, Hájek, Craig) and works (the <i>Progymnasmata</i> / <i>De nova stella</i> volume + the Comet books) are firmly identified; the only soft points are two corrupt/irregular forms in the copied witness (<i>\"amplissicum\"</i> / <i>\"amplifice\"</i>), flagged above and rendered on the secure sense rather than smoothed, and the astrological <i>decuria</i> = \"decan\" reading, also flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin letter (TBOO VII pp.347-350, Tycho to Jacob Kurz) against Dreyer's page images line by line, confirming the heading, body, the Craig/Aristotle polemic, the Mars/Saturn astronomical figures (the acronychal Saturn observation, Mars at the first decan of Virgo near its perigee), and the closing date/signature all match. Found two single-word departures of our source_text from Dreyer's print (both meeting the brief's TEXT_ERROR definition); neither corrupts the sense of the translation, which remains accurate and complete throughout, but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of severity.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin reads the corrupted non-word \"amplissicum\" where Dreyer prints \"amplissimum\" (TBOO VII p.348); the translator flagged the corruption with a bracketed [?] rather than resolving it, but the underlying source_text is what is wrong. Dreyer prints: <i>amplißimum (=amplissimum)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text inserts the word \"his\" (rendered as \"these\" in the English) that is not present in Dreyer's printed text at TBOO VII p.348, line 29. Dreyer prints: <i>cura altioribus coelestium siderum contemplationibus (no \"his\")</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defects verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VII p.348 page image (not the OCR text layer). The first (amplissicum/amplissimum) was confirmed and fixed. The second (claimed dropped 'his') was checked and found NOT supported: Dreyer's line 29 reads 'cura his altioribus...contemplationibus' with 'his' present, exactly as already published; this defect is rejected as a false positive in the audit. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Iacobum Curtium a. 1593", "sh": "Cod. 10686/16", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961760"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.16. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Kurz", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15901201002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15900628001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Kurz for securing the Imperial privilege for his astronomical works and recalls Kurz's earlier expressions of goodwill and favour toward the mathematical arts.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930419001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930420001", "d": "1593-04-20", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 20 April 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer,</i> Opera Omnia *VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.34 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM. S.</p><p>MIRATUS equidem sum, optime mi THADDAeE, quod tam diutino tempore nihil a te literarum receperim. Verum ex ijs, quas circa initia Decembris Anni proxime elapsi Pragae ad me perscripseris, quae circiter medium Aprilis huius anni mihi redditae sunt, non sine dolore demum percipio, eas dilationis huius in rescribendo fuisse causas, quas non incidisse quivis bonus potius exoptasset. Morbis enim varijs et atrocibus te tota pene aestate conflictatum fuisse, admodum illibenter hinc cognovi. Deus tibi vires restituat et valetudinem tuam in hac senecta clementer prosperet. Quin et ipsemet eius rationem habeas, nec nimijs laboribus et curis eandem elanguescere sinas. Abstineas a fortibus Medicinis vacuantibus; et ijs potissimum utaris, quae ventriculum et principaliora membra suaviter refocillent et corroborent, praesertim vero bona et senibus competente Diaeta. Sed quorsum ego haec, cum ipsemet Archiatrus, ac in utraque Medicina tam Galenica quam Paracelsica diu feliciterque versatus sis? Verum optimum est omnium malorum remedium in solo Creatore, non autem Creaturis spem locare. Quod et te facere cum alias, tum etiam ex literis hisce satis perspectum habeo.</p><p>Video te perlustrasse scopticum et arrogans illud mei Scotici Antagonistae scriptum, idemque Magnifico Dn. CURTIO inspiciendum dijudicandumque obtulisse; a neutro autem vestrum probari. Suadetis igitur ut eius Sophismata redarguam. At cum videam ipsum ita in praeconceptis Aristotelicis opinionibus fascinatum esse, ut veritatem intueri et amplecti aut nolit aut nequeat, frustraneum duco bonas horas in ipso refellendo male impendere; ex quo non obscure pateat, ipsum prae Philautia quadam laudem ex contradictionibus aucupari. Sufficiat itaque me ex certis Observationibus et Apodixibus Geometricis (licet et has in dubium vocare malitiose et ignoranter potius quam iuste et intelligenter praesumat) viris candidis et cordatis, nec turgido ac temerario praeiudicio obcaecatis, undiquaque satisfacere. Quis enim adeo circumspecte et invicte sua muniet, ut nulla sit fastuosis et sophisticis ingenijs eadem cavillis impetendi occasio? Si tamen quippiam a gravioribus aliquando studijs supererit ocij, efficiam, sive per me ipsum, seu potius discipulorum meorum aliquem, ut invalida et obtusa huius Scoti tela partim infringantur, partim aliter exacuata in ipsum retorqueantur, ne sibi nimium placendo veritatis maenia apud imbelles et inertes se penitus expugnasse iactitet. Id enim facillimo negotio praestabitur, siquidem nihil solidi et ad rem ipsam penitius facientis in medium adferat, sed fucatis et futilibus argutijs potius quam veris et subtilibus argumentis rem agat, merisque calumnijs, scommatis ac perversionibus scateat, nec modestiae limites transilire erubescat. Interim quid Magnificus Dn. CURTIUS et tu de eius superciliosa et commentitia hac Capnuraniae restinctione (sic enim is scriptum hoc suum vere fumosum et fastuosum appellat, licet ego Cometas, utut reverâ Coelestes, e fumositatibus tamen constare nusquam asseruerim) iudicetis, postquam eam iam, uti existimo, plenius introspexeritis, per te certior fieri aveo. Feci semel atque iterum mentionem in literis antea ad te datis de parte illa prioris Tomi nostri tibi dudum transmissa, ut tuam de hac sententiam expiscarer. Verum tu nec in prioribus nec hisce ultimis quippiam hac de re moves. Suspicor itaque, quicquid hoc erat, tibi non esse redditum. Quare nunc alterum exemplum mitto, in quo pleraque tunc desiderata complentur. Deficiunt hîc saltem praeter Privilegia, Praefationem et Conclusionem, quae volens differo, tres quaterniones in fine primi Capitis, quo Lunaris motus restitutio compendiose expedietur. Hos enim typis mandare nondum satis opportunum fuit, praesertim quia Eclipses aliquas in diversis Horizontibus denotatas expecto, ut Meridianorum differentiae rectius, quam hactenus, praefiniri queant. Neque enim ante Nundinas Autumnales Librum hunc in publicum emittere lubet. Mutabo autem quaedam in Titulo circa reliquorum Planetarum ordinationem. Horum enim restitutionem duobus de Cometis subsequentibus libris anteponere consultius duco, ita ut trium, quos vocant, superiorum Planetarum, quoad motum Coelesti conformem, redintegratio, Cometam anni 1577, iam antea descriptum, reliqui duo inferiores Commentarium de caeteris minoribus Cometis praecedant; itaque ut perpetua illa et mundo coaeva sidera, tanquam principaliora magisque generalia, prae ascititijs, temporaneis et particularioribus hisce Astris priores et potiores partes merito obtineant. Rogo itaque, mi THADDAeE, velis quantum per ocium et valetudinem liceat, hunc quem mitto, Astronomicae instaurationis priorem Tomum perlegere, tuamque de eius contentis gravissimam censuram per literas aperire. Idem ab amplissimo Dn. CURTIO, si quando a Reipub. munijs, quae laudabiliter sustinet, nonnihil respirârit, fieri quam maxime exoptarem. Tractavi post Luminarium et Fixarum Stellarum accuratam verificationem de Nova illa Stella aliquanto prolixius; cum et insolens ac supra modum miraculosa eius apparitio, et aliorum, quae expendendae erant, opiniones id efflagitabant. Egisti et tu olim in erudita illa Dialexi tua de hoc ipso admirando sidere et scite et luculenter, recteque nobiscum asseruisti omni Parallaxi destitutum, et inter reliqua octavae (ut vocant) Sphaerae Sidera, repositum fuisse. Licet nonnulla sint, quae in hoc ipso scripto ob festinationem minus elimate a te proposita sint, nec omnia in recognitione mihi transmissa satis revisa. Quorum pleraque circa principium Cap. 8, statim post Landgravianas animadversiones expendenda suscepi. Quod et tua me bona venia fecisse non dubito. Tute nunc in ea circumspectius attende. Videbis utique me nihil dubij citra rem hîc movisse. Idem quoque circa aliorum Placita, quae disquisitioni subijcio, comperies. Nihil enim magis in votis habui, quam ut veritatem intemeratam in apertum deducerem, et quae pro vel contra facerent, libertate quadam Philosophica indicarem, ut aliquid certi de tam stupendo miraculo nostris temporibus Coelitus exhibito Posteris constaret, nec opinionum de hoc varietas et confusio ostenti magnitudinem extenuaret; siquidem a paucissimis pro ea, qua par erat, subtilitate et diligentia hanc Spartam fuisse excultam exornatamque iamdudum perspexerim. Tentet et hîc, si volet, Scoticus meus Antagonista, an ab affixarum Orbe hanc Stellam in Elementarem Regionem, suis invalidis lacertis detrahere queat, quo suum, qui perpetua infamia per hanc Stellam liquidissime affectus est, tueatur ARISTOTELEM. Et sane in scripto isto Antapologetico nescit, quo se vertat, ut huius Stellae Phaenomena, pro suo illo, in cuius verba iuravit, Magistro excuset. Tandem ad opinionum varietatem dilabitur, qua sane ratione nihil uspiam certi esse eodem modo evicerit, cum de re eadem nusquam ferme omnes pariter sentiant, nec numeranda sed aestimanda sint aliorum iudicia. Sed facessat iste, sibique quantum velit, sapiat. Tu vero, mi THADDAeE, prima quaque opportunitate et de valetudine tua et ijs, quae nunc expeto, me certiorem reddere non degravere. Habeo etiam gratias pro emendato illo Caesareano Privilegio, quod nunc unâ accepi. Scripsi vero ipsemet Magnifico Dn. CURTIO eique inter alia hoc nomine gratias egi. Quas literas hic Civis Gorliciensis se Pragam allaturum et unâ cum hisce tibi destinatis diligenter procuraturum recepit. Vale et ne nimis diu responsionem expectem, provide.</p><p>Iamque iterum feliciter vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 20 April. Anno 1593.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus Hagecius — greeting.</b></p><p>I had indeed wondered, my excellent Thaddaeus, that for so long a time I had received no letter from you. But from the one which you wrote me at Prague around the beginning of December of the year just past — and which was delivered to me about the middle of April this year — I now at last perceive, not without sorrow, that the causes of this delay in your writing back were such as any good man would have wished rather had not befallen. For I learned hence very unwillingly that you had been afflicted nearly the whole summer with various and grievous illnesses. May God restore your strength and graciously prosper your health in this old age. Indeed, do also take care of it yourself, and do not let it be worn down by excessive labours and cares. Abstain from strong purging medicines, and use above all those which gently revive and strengthen the stomach and the principal members, and especially a good diet suited to the aged. But to what end do I say this, since you are yourself a chief physician [<i>Archiatrus</i>], and have long and successfully been versed in both kinds of medicine, the Galenic as well as the Paracelsian? Truly the best remedy for all ills is to place one's hope in the Creator alone, and not in created things. And that you do this I have well perceived, both on other occasions and also from this letter.</p><p>I see that you have looked through that mocking and arrogant writing of my Scottish antagonist, and that you have laid it before the Honourable Master Curtius to be inspected and judged; and that it is approved by neither of you. You urge me, then, to refute his sophisms. But since I see that he is so bewitched in his preconceived Aristotelian opinions that he either will not or cannot look upon and embrace the truth, I judge it fruitless to waste good hours ill in refuting him — from which it is not obscurely plain that, out of a certain self-love [<i>Philautia</i>], he is hunting for praise out of contradictions. Let it suffice, therefore, that I satisfy on every side, by means of sure observations and geometrical proofs [<i>Apodixes</i>] (although he too presumes to call these into doubt, maliciously and ignorantly rather than justly and intelligently), men candid and sensible, and not blinded by swollen and rash prejudice. For who will fortify his own [positions] so circumspectly and invincibly that there is no occasion for haughty and sophistical wits to attack the same with cavils? Yet if at some time any leisure shall be left over from weightier studies, I shall bring it about — either by myself, or rather by some one of my pupils — that the feeble and blunt weapons of this Scot be partly broken, partly, sharpened otherwise, turned back against him, lest, by pleasing himself too much, he boast that he has utterly stormed the walls of truth among the unwarlike and idle. For this will be accomplished with the easiest effort, since he brings forward into the open nothing solid or penetrating more deeply to the matter itself, but conducts the business with painted and futile quibbles rather than with true and subtle arguments, and teems with sheer calumnies, jeers, and perversions, and does not blush to leap over the bounds of modesty. Meanwhile I long to be made more certain through you of what the Honourable Master Curtius and you judge concerning this supercilious and fabricated <i>Extinguishing of the Capnurania</i> of his (for so he calls this writing of his, truly smoky [<i>fumosum</i>] and arrogant — although I have nowhere asserted that comets, even though truly celestial, are nevertheless composed of smoky exhalations [<i>fumosities</i>]), now that you have, as I suppose, examined it more fully. I made mention, once and again, in letters sent to you before, of that part of our earlier Volume long since transmitted to you, so that I might fish out your opinion on it. But you stir nothing on this matter, neither in the earlier [letters] nor in these latest ones. I suspect, therefore, that whatever this was, was not delivered to you. Wherefore I now send another copy, in which most of the things then wanting are supplied. Lacking here, at least — besides the Privileges, the Preface, and the Conclusion, which I willingly defer — are three quaternions at the end of the first Chapter, in which the restoration [<i>restitutio</i>] of the lunar motion will be compendiously dispatched. For it was not yet opportune enough to commit these to the press, especially because I am awaiting certain eclipses recorded in different horizons, so that the differences of the meridians may be determined beforehand more correctly than hitherto. Nor indeed does it please me to send this Book out into public before the autumn fair. I shall, however, change certain things in the Title concerning the ordering of the remaining planets. For I judge it more advisable to set the restoration of these before the two following books on comets — in such a way that the renewal [<i>redintegratio</i>], as to a motion conformable to the heavens, of the three so-called superior planets shall precede the comet of the year 1577, already described before, [and] the remaining two inferior [planets shall precede] the Commentary on the rest of the lesser comets; and thus that those perpetual stars, coeval with the world, as the more principal and more general ones, may deservedly hold the prior and superior place before these adventitious, temporary, and more particular Stars. I ask you, therefore, my Thaddaeus, that you be willing — so far as leisure and health permit — to read through this earlier Volume of the Restoration of Astronomy [<i>Astronomiae instauratio</i>] which I send, and to open to me by letter your most weighty censure upon its contents. The same I would most greatly wish to be done by the most distinguished Master Curtius, if ever he shall have breathed a little from the duties of the Commonwealth which he laudably sustains. After the accurate verification of the Luminaries and the Fixed Stars I have treated, somewhat at length, of that New Star [<i>Nova Stella</i>]; since both its unusual and beyond-measure miraculous appearance, and the opinions of others which had to be weighed, demanded it. You too once treated, both skilfully and lucidly, of this very marvellous star in that learned <i>Dialexis</i> of yours, and rightly asserted, together with us, that it was destitute of all parallax and was placed among the rest of the stars of the eighth Sphere (as they call it). Although there are some things which, on account of haste, were set out by you in that very writing less polishedly, nor were all of them sufficiently revised in the corrected version transmitted to me. Most of these I have undertaken to weigh around the beginning of Chapter 8, immediately after the Landgravian observations. And I do not doubt that I have done this with your good leave. Now attend to it yourself more circumspectly. You will certainly see that I have here raised no doubt apart from the matter [itself]. The same too you will find concerning the opinions [<i>Placita</i>] of others which I subject to examination. For I had nothing more in my wishes than to bring the undefiled truth into the open, and to indicate, with a certain philosophical liberty, the things that made for it or against it — so that something certain might be established for Posterity concerning so stupendous a miracle exhibited from Heaven in our times, and that the variety and confusion of opinions about it might not diminish the magnitude of the portent; since I have long since perceived that by very few men was this Sparta cultivated and adorned with that subtlety and diligence which was fitting. Let my Scottish antagonist try here too, if he will, whether he can drag this Star down from the orb of the fixed stars into the Elementary Region with his feeble arms, in order to defend his Aristotle, who has been most clearly affected with perpetual infamy through this Star. And indeed in that anti-apologetic writing of his he knows not which way to turn, so as to excuse the phenomena of this Star on behalf of that Master of his, in whose words he has sworn. At last he slips away to the variety of opinions — by which reasoning he would in the same manner have proved that nothing anywhere is certain, since on the same matter almost nowhere do all alike feel the same, and the judgements of others are not to be counted but weighed. But let that fellow be off, and be as wise as he pleases for himself. But you, my Thaddaeus, at the very first opportunity, do not find it burdensome to inform me both about your health and about those things which I now seek. I have thanks, too, for that emended Imperial Privilege, which I have now received together [with the letter]. I have indeed written myself to the Honourable Master Curtius, and among other things have given him thanks on this account. Which letter this Citizen of Görlitz has undertaken to carry to Prague and, together with these things destined for you, to deliver carefully. Farewell, and see to it that I do not await a reply too long.</p><p>And now once again, fare you happily well.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 20 April, the year 1593.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), the Bohemian physician, astronomer, and Imperial physician (<i>Archiatrus</i>) at Prague; author of the 1574 <i>Dialexis</i> on the 1572 new star. Tycho's longest-standing learned ally in the Empire and his principal intermediary to the Imperial court. The honorific <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (§5.7).</li><li><b><i>Magnificus Dn. Curtius</b></i> — <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), Imperial <i>Procancellarius</i> (pro-chancellor) at the court of Rudolf II and himself \"most skilled in mathematics\"; the corpus confirms he is the \"Imperial prochancellor Jacob Kurtz\" to whom Tycho also wrote on the Craig affair. Rendered \"Master Curtius\" with the honorific <i>Magnificus</i> → \"Honourable\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>The \"Scottish antagonist\" (<i>Scoticus Antagonista</i>).</b> <b>John Craig</b> (Johannes Craig, d. 1620), the Scottish physician and pertinacious Aristotelian who, from May 1589, disputed by letter Tycho's supralunar placement of the comet of 1577, insisting it was a sublunary (elementary) phenomenon. Craig later published a refutation of Tycho's comet-book; Tycho's own <i>Apologia</i> to Craig (sent 25 Oct. 1589) is the \"anti-apologetic\" target referred to here. Named per §5.6 convention.</li><li><b><i>Capnuraniae restinctio</b></i> — kept as a title and Englished \"Extinguishing of the <i>Capnurania</i>.\" <i>Capnurania</i> is Craig's own coinage (Greek καπνός <i>kapnós</i> \"smoke\" + Οὐρανία <i>Ouranía</i> \"heaven/Urania\"), a derisive label — roughly \"Smoke-heaven\" — for what he portrayed as Tycho's doctrine of cometary smoke in the sky. Tycho turns the coinage back as a pun: the writing is itself <i>fumosum</i> (\"smoky\" = empty/vaporous) as well as <i>fastuosum</i> (\"arrogant\"). Tycho's parenthesis is a careful disavowal — <i>he</i> never claimed comets, though celestial, are made of <i>fumositates</i> (\"smoky exhalations\"); the hedge (\"even though truly celestial\" / <i>utut reverâ Coelestes</i>) is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b><i>scopticum … nec tam scotice quam scoptice</b></i> — the recurring <i>Scot- / scopt-</i> wordplay (Scottish / mocking, Gr. σκώπτειν \"to jeer\"); rendered \"mocking\" with the antagonist's Scottishness named separately, the pun flagged rather than flattened.</li><li><b><i>prioris Tomi nostri / Astronomicae instaurationis priorem Tomum</b></i> — the first volume of Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the long-gestating treatise on the new star of 1572, with the solar and stellar restorations and the comet of 1577), here being sent to Hagecius in sheets for his <i>censura</i>. <i>instauratio / restitutio / redintegratio</i> → \"restoration / renewal\" per §5.1 (Tycho's <i>astronomia instaurata</i> programme).</li><li><b><i>Nova illa Stella</b></i> — the <b>new star (nova) of 1572</b> in Cassiopeia. <i>omni Parallaxi destitutum … octavae … Sphaerae</i> = \"destitute of all parallax … the eighth sphere\" — i.e. proven to lie among the fixed stars, beyond the Aristotelian boundary of change; <i>parallax</i> per §5.1.</li><li><b><i>Dialexi tua</b></i> — Hagecius's <i>Dialexis de novae et prius incognitae stellae … apparitione</i> (Frankfurt, 1574), his printed tract on the 1572 star; kept as the Latin title.</li><li><b><i>Landgravianas animadversiones</b></i> — \"the Landgravian observations,\" i.e. the observations of the new star made at the court of Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel (§5.6), to be discussed near the start of Chapter 8.</li><li><b><i>Elementarem Regionem / affixarum Orbe</b></i> — the sublunary \"Elementary Region\" (the Aristotelian region of the four elements, subject to change) vs. the \"orb of the fixed [stars]\"; the whole Craig dispute turns on which region the nova and comet occupy.</li><li><b><i>tres quaterniones … Lunaris motus restitutio</b></i> — \"three quaternions [gatherings of four sheets] … the restoration of the lunar motion,\" still unprinted pending eclipse data observed at differing <i>Horizons</i> to fix the meridian (longitude) differences; <i>restitutio</i> / <i>Meridianorum differentiae</i> per §5.1.</li><li><b><i>Nundinas Autumnales</b></i> — \"the autumn fair,\" i.e. the Frankfurt autumn book fair, the trade calendar's publication window.</li><li><b>Medicine.</b> <i>Archiatrus</i> → \"chief physician\"; <i>utraque Medicina tam Galenica quam Paracelsica</i> → \"both kinds of medicine, the Galenic as well as the Paracelsian\" — Hagecius practised both traditions; <i>fortibus Medicinis vacuantibus</i> → \"strong purging medicines\" (period therapeutics; not modernised, per §5.4 spirit).</li><li><b>Greek-derived technical terms</b> carried over and italicised on first use: <i>Apodixes</i> (ἀπόδειξις, demonstrative proofs), <i>Philautia</i> (φιλαυτία, self-love), <i>Capnurania</i> (see above), <i>Placita</i> (here \"tenets/opinions\"). No continuous Greek script appears in this letter.</li><li><b><i>hanc Spartam … excultam exornatamque</b></i> — the proverb <i>Spartam nactus es, hanc orna</i> (\"you have got Sparta, adorn it\"); Tycho means the study of the new star has hitherto been \"cultivated and adorned\" (done justice) by very few. The proverbial idiom is kept.</li><li><b><i>Civis Gorliciensis</b></i> — \"a Citizen of Görlitz\" (the Lusatian town), the unnamed courier carrying the packet to Prague.</li><li><b><i>Caesareano Privilegio</b></i> — \"the Imperial Privilege,\" the printing privilege from Rudolf II for Tycho's books, now received in emended form.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> The body dateline reads only <i>Datae Uraniburgi April. Anno 1593</i> — <b>\"Given at Uraniborg, April, the year 1593,\" with no day named.</b> The dataset `label` supplies \"1593. 20. april\"; because the source text itself substantiates only the month, the header leads with \"April 1593\" and appends \"(catalogued 20 April 1593)\" per §4. The day <b>20 April</b> is not falsifiable from the text and is not asserted as the dateline. Old Style: Denmark kept the Julian calendar; this is <b>O.S.</b> The letter notes its own postal lag — Hagecius's letter of \"around the beginning of December\" of the previous year (early Dec. 1592) reached Tycho \"about the middle of April\" 1593; reported, not reconciled.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> A = Vienna, ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.34 (a copy / <i>Afskrift</i>), as given in the dataset. Edition of record: Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII (where the letter stands among the 1593 Hagecius correspondence, near Tycho's <i>Apologia ad Ioannis Craigi Capnuraniae restinctionis fragmentum</i>).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, p. 352) prints the day — <i>Datae Uraniburgi <b>20</b> April. Anno 1593.</i> The earlier note that the day is unsupported by the text is withdrawn; \"20\" restored to header, Source, and translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and the technical/programmatic content (the <i>Progymnasmata</i> in press, the Craig/<i>Capnurania</i> polemic, the nova and the 1577 comet, the planetary reordering of the Title) is corroborated against the corpus. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>dateline crux</b> — the source gives only the month <i>April</i>, so the catalogued day \"20 April\" is appended in the header but not asserted; (2) the precise editorial sense of <i>recognitione … revisa</i> (Hagecius's \"corrected/revised version\" of his <i>Dialexis</i> sent to Tycho) and of the elliptical <i>reliqui duo inferiores</i> (the two inferior planets) in the planetary-ordering sentence, where Tycho's syntax is compressed and the English mirrors that compression with bracketed supplements.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.349-353 as page images (Read pages=1-4, then pages=5-5). The letter itself runs only pp.350-352 - slice p.1 (TBOO p.349) is the tail of the preceding letter to Iacobus Curtius, and slice p.5 (TBOO p.353) is a wholly different letter (to Petrus Iunius, 27 April 1593); both were excluded from collation. Collated the full letter text - opening (MIRATUS equidem sum, optime mi THADDAEE...), every proper name (Thaddaeus Hagecius, Magnificus Dn. Curtius, the unnamed 'Scoticus Antagonista'/Aristoteles), the numbers/references (Cap. 8, Cometam anni 1577, Caesareano Privilegio), the Gorlicensis courier detail, and the closing date (Datae Uraniburgi 20 April. Anno 1593) - against the Dreyer images word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Sammelhandschrift (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/32-35", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961787"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.34. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 30, "prev": "brahe-t_15921202001", "next": "brahe-t_15931224001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15921202001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15931224001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Hagecius's December letter describing his serious illnesses, expresses sympathy, and advises against strong purging medicines in favour of gentle remedies suited to old age.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930420001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930427001", "d": "1593-04-27", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young — Uraniborg, 27 April 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, MS Smith 77 (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIO TYCHO BRAHE. S.</p><p>SED quorsum, nobilissime et amplissime vir, silentium hoc plus quam Pythagoreum? Itane crebrioribus meis ad te datis literis nihil plane responderis? Quod sive veteris tecum initae amicitiae oblivione sive ultroneâ scribendi intermissione acciderit: a nostra id equidem pristina, quantulacunque haec fuerit, conversatione, meaque in te fide et expectatione alienissimum censeo. Tempus itaque est, ut vel nunc tandem per nostrates ad vos Ablegatos, tam commoda praesertim oblata occasione, quidpiam mecum literarum commutes. Scripsi autem ad te, ut et alias, superiore anno paulo ante ingruentem brumam, unâque Elegiacam quandam epistolam serenissimo Regi vestro, tua (uti desiderabam) opera meo nomine submisse offerendam, adiunxi. Quae utrum ad te sarta tecta pervenerint, an tabellarij forte incuria interciderint, nihil interea temporis rescire potui. Mitto itaque iam aliud eiusdem carminis exemplar, maiorem in modum rogans, ut si prius illud ad ipsius Maiestatis manus non pervenerit, hoc alterum ipsius Maiestati legendum demisse exhibere, tuaque qua plurimum vales auctoritate et consilio apud ipsius Maiestatem intercedere non degravere: quo promptius hac in parte meis desiderijs benigne satisfiat. Quod si igitur regale et antiquissimum hoc studium regio suo condecorare carmine, favorabili sua erga me et sublimes has artes voluntate permota, non dedignetur: operam sane apud candidos et cordatos aestimatores inprimis laudabilem praestiterit et eximij erga haec sacra Uranica favoris monumentum quoddam perenne iugi posteritatis memoriae consecrandum reliquerit. Id quod serenissimam suam Maiestatem tuo praesertim sensu et consilio clementer concessuram de eadem mihi recipio et polliceor. Constitui autem Regium hoc epigramma statim in frontispicio meorum instaurandae Astronomiae Progymnasmatum unâ cum Landtgraviano, quod iamdudum obtinuerim, in pleniorem huius praelustris scientiae commendationem apponere: prout harum exhibitor tibi ulterius indicare poterit. Proinde te iterum atque iterum obnixe rogatum habeo, ut quantum possis (potes autem, nec immerito, plurimum) tua opera et intercessione, quo id clementissimi beneficij mihi concedatur, et per hunc ipsum voti compos fiam, sedulo elaborare non degravere. Quin et hunc ipsum D. NICOLAUM CRAGIUM, Iuris Doctorem, et Legationis ad vos in Scotiam munus unâ cum alijs subeuntem, virum alioquin in literis praeclare versatum, et tuo genio suopte (si recte iudico) non displiciturum ingenio, vel meo nomine tibi commendatum habeas.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 27 April. A. 1593.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Peter Young, Tycho Brahe — greeting.</b></p><p>But to what end, most noble and worthy sir, this more-than-Pythagorean silence? Have you really made no answer at all to the rather frequent letters I have sent you? Whether this has come about through forgetfulness of the old friendship struck up with you, or through a voluntary breaking-off of correspondence, I for my part judge it utterly foreign to our former intercourse — slight though that may have been — and to my own trust in you and expectation of you. It is time, therefore, that even now at last, through our own envoys [sent] to you, and especially since so convenient an occasion has presented itself, you should exchange some letters with me. Now I wrote to you, as on other occasions, last year a little before the onset of winter, and at the same time added a certain elegiac epistle to be offered humbly in my name to your most serene King, through your good offices (as I desired). Whether these reached you safe and sound, or perchance went astray through the carrier's negligence, I have in the meantime been unable to learn. I therefore now send another copy of the same poem, asking in the strongest terms that, if the former one did not reach His Majesty's own hands, you should not think it a burden humbly to present this second one to His Majesty to read, and to intercede with His Majesty by that authority and counsel in which you are so very powerful — so that in this matter my wishes may the more readily be graciously satisfied. If, therefore, [His Majesty], moved by his favourable disposition toward me and toward these sublime arts, should not disdain to grace this royal and most ancient study with a royal poem of his own, he will assuredly have rendered a service especially praiseworthy in the eyes of fair-minded and judicious appraisers, and will have left behind a certain perennial monument of his exceptional favour toward these sacred Uranian things [haec sacra Uranica], to be consecrated to the unceasing memory of posterity. And this I take upon myself and promise concerning His most serene Majesty — that he will graciously grant it, especially through your good sense and counsel. I have moreover determined to place this royal epigram at once on the frontispiece of my <i>Progymnasmata</i> for the restoring of astronomy [<i>instaurandae Astronomiae</i>], together with the Landgrave's [poem], which I obtained long since, for the fuller commendation of this most illustrious science — as the bearer of this [letter] will be able to indicate to you further. Accordingly I hold you asked, again and again and earnestly, that you should not think it a burden diligently to labour, so far as you can (and you can — and deservedly so — very much), by your effort and intercession, that this most gracious benefit be granted to me, and that through this very man I may attain my wish. Indeed I would have this very man, Master Niels Krag [D. Nicolaum Cragium], Doctor of Law, who is undertaking the office of the embassy to you in Scotland together with the others — a man besides excellently versed in letters, and one who (if I judge rightly) will of his own accord not be displeasing to your disposition by his talent — commended to you also in my name.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 27 April, the year 1593.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petrus Iunius</i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (Sir Peter Young of Seaton, 1544–1628), Scottish humanist and royal tutor; with George Buchanan he was preceptor to <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), and remained a trusted royal almoner and diplomat. James VI's interest in Tycho is attested in the period (he visited Hven in 1590); Young is the courtier through whom Tycho seeks to reach the king (cf. A. Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, on James VI in Tycho's orbit). The Latinization <i>Iunius</i> renders the surname <i>Young</i>.</li><li><b>Sender / salutation.</b> <i>TYCHO BRAHE. S.</i> — <i>salutem [dicit]</i>, \"[sends] greeting\" (protocol §5.5). The reversed name-order of the address (recipient first, then sender) follows the manuscript.</li><li><b><i>silentium hoc plus quam Pythagoreum</b></i> — \"this more-than-Pythagorean silence,\" an allusion to the proverbial five-year silence imposed on Pythagoras's pupils; kept as the learned figure it is.</li><li><b>\"His most serene King\" / <i>serenissimo Regi vestro</b></i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (r. 1567–1625). Tycho never names him in the body, addressing him only as <i>vester Rex</i> / <i>ipsius Maiestas</i>; the rendering keeps that indirection.</li><li><b><i>Elegiacam quandam epistolam</i> / <i>eiusdem carminis exemplar</i> / <i>Regium hoc epigramma</b></i> — the verse Tycho had sent (an elegiac poem) soliciting from James VI a commendatory poem of his own; the king's hoped-for <i>regium carmen</i> (\"royal poem\") would, with the Landgrave's, head the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. James VI did in fact furnish such verses, printed in the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>.</li><li><b><i>Landtgraviano [carmine]</b></i> — the commendatory poem already obtained from <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (protocol §5.6), Tycho's astronomical correspondent; \"which I obtained long since\" (<i>iamdudum obtinuerim</i>).</li><li><b><i>meorum instaurandae Astronomiae Progymnasmatum</b></i> — \"my <i>Progymnasmata</i> for the restoring of astronomy,\" i.e. the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the first volume of Tycho's restored-astronomy programme, the work on the new star of 1572 and the solar/lunar theory; printed largely at Uraniborg, completed and published 1602/1610). <i>instauratae / instaurandae</i> = \"restored / to be restored,\" Tycho's programmatic <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> (protocol §5.1). The genitive plural <i>Progymnasmatum</i> is treated as the title.</li><li><b><i>haec sacra Uranica</b></i> — \"these sacred Uranian things,\" i.e. astronomy as the cult of Urania, muse of the heavens; cf. the <i>sacris Uraniae</i> sign-off flourish (protocol §5.5). Kept as Tycho's devotional metaphor for his science.</li><li><b>Carrier / envoy.</b> <i>harum exhibitor</i> (\"the bearer of this [letter]\") and <i>per hunc ipsum</i> (\"through this very man\") = Niels Krag, who carries the letter and the poem.</li><li><b><i>D. Nicolaum Cragium, Iuris Doctorem</b></i> — <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602), Danish humanist, jurist (Doctor of Law) and later royal historiographer; here a member of the Danish embassy to Scotland of 1593. <i>D.</i> = <i>Dominus</i>, \"Master\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Legationis ad vos in Scotiam munus … subeuntem</b></i> — \"undertaking the office of the embassy to you in Scotland\": the Danish diplomatic mission to the Scottish court (in the aftermath of the 1589/90 marriage of James VI to Anne of Denmark). <i>nostrates … Ablegatos</i> (\"our own envoys\") in the opening refers to the same embassy, the \"convenient occasion\" Tycho exploits to renew contact.</li><li><b>Epistolary hedges preserved.</b> <i>quantulacunque haec fuerit</i> (\"slight though that [intercourse] may have been\"); <i>si recte iudico</i> (\"if I judge rightly\"); <i>forte … incuria</i> (\"perchance … through negligence\"); <i>uti desiderabam</i> (\"as I desired\"). The thrice-repeated litotes <i>non degravere</i> (\"do not think it a burden\") is kept each time as a courtesy formula rather than smoothed to \"please.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 27 April. A. 1593</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> (on Hven), 27 April 1593\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700, protocol §3). The dateline agrees with the dataset label (<i>1593. 27. april. Uraniborg</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Text-of-record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII (<i>Epistolae</i>); sole MS witness cited is a later copy (<i>afskrift</i>), Oxford, Bodleian Library, MS Smith 77 — consistent with transmission through Young's / Scottish-humanist papers.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — Latin secure; technical and proper-name identifications corroborated; dateline substantiated by the source and concordant with the label. The only soft point is the conventional dating of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>'s eventual publication (named in the apparatus, not in the translation), which does not bear on the letter's sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII pp. 353-354 via the page images, including the page-break at \"in-primis laudabilem\". Verified the proper names (Peter Young, the Progymnasmata frontispiece plan, the Landgrave's epigram, Niels Krag/Nicolaum Cragium and the Scottish embassy) and the closing date (27 April 1593). Translation renders every clause faithfully; no fabrication, omission, hedge-loss, or mistranslation found. Confirms the self-assigned A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15921001001", "next": "brahe-t_15930801001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930801001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Protests Young's continuing silence and asks whether previous letters and an elegiac epistle intended for the Scottish King arrived safely.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930427001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930801001", "d": "1593-08-01", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "Peter Young → Tycho Brahe — Holyrood Palace (Edinburgh), 1 August 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(= Dreyer,</i> Opera Omnia <i>VII–VIII,</i> Epistolae*) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET GENEROSO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO IN KNUDSTRUP, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO SUMMA CUM OBSERVANTIA PERPETUO COLENDO. S. P.</p><p>ETSI diuturni istius mei silentij multas easque iustissimas excusationes, Generosissime ac Nobiliss. Domine, afferre possem, tamen culpam fateri consultius esse duco et ad veniam compendiosius, praesertim cum mihi res sit cum amico nec rigido nec inexorabili, ne si forte in me purgando putidiusculus essem, in aliam denuo offensam incautus relaberer; quare silentio quodam novo veteris silentij crimen refellam illudque saltem maiorem in modum deprecabor, ne ab immutata animi mei voluntate (quae certe eadem tuo summo merito atque humanitate incredibili erga te est eritque perpetuo, quae antea extitit) hoc profectum existimes. Quod si fideiussores res poscat, ipsi, uti confido, Mag. Domini Legati pro mea in te fide et constantia nihil ἀυτήν veriti, non gravate spondebunt. Diploma Regium, ut voluisti, expediendum curavi, quod unâ cum versibus Regijs, Regia etiam manu, ut efflagitasti, exaratis accipies. Ex utrisque Sereniss. Regis nostri ac Domini Clementiss. de te et tuis scriptis cuiusmodi sit iudicium, non obscure cognosces. Profecto te in oculis fert atque in coelum laudibus effert, quoties de te mentio incidit. Incidit autem saepissime, quoties nimirum aut iter illud suum Danicum recenset aut de Physiologia, praesertim Mathesi, verba facit, tum nunquam non praedicatur Uraniburgicum istud Sydus. CRAIGIUM medicum ἀνταγωνιστήν illum tuum eatenus ὑγιαίνειν iussi, ut argumentis tecum non convitijs certaret, quod et posthac hominem facturum spero. De BUCHANANO alias: ne si forte omnia simul, scribendi materia utrique desit. Caetera ex Magcis Dominis Legatis, qui quanta fide, prudentia atque industria, negotia sua hîc gesserint, omnes bonos conscios habent. Vale Generosiss. ac Nobiliss. Domine, omniaque in te a me studia atque officia, quae quidem ego praestare potero, ita velim expectes, ut illa nulla alia re nisi vitae interitu terminari posse certo tibi persuadeas. Datae ex Regia Stae Crucis Cal. Augusti 1593.</p><p>Tuae Magtiae studiosiss.<br>atque tibique obsequentiss.<br>Petrus Iunius a Setoun.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and noble Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, his lord and friend ever to be honoured with the utmost respect. Hearty greeting.</b></p><p>Although I could put forward many — and those most just — excuses for this long silence of mine, most noble and high-born Lord, I judge it more advisable, and a shorter road to pardon, to confess the fault; the more so since I have to do with a friend neither stiff nor implacable, and lest, if perchance I were a little too fastidious in clearing myself, I should incautiously slip back into yet another offence. I shall therefore refute the charge of my old silence by a certain new silence, and at least beg most earnestly for this: that you not think it [proceeded] from any change in my mind's good will toward you (which assuredly, by your supreme merit and incredible kindness, is and ever shall be the same toward you as it was before). And if the matter should call for sureties, the Honourable Lords the Ambassadors themselves will, as I trust, fearing nothing †ἀυτήν† [as to her/it], stand pledge ungrudgingly for my faith and constancy toward you. The Royal Diploma I have had drawn up for dispatch, as you wished, which you will receive together with the Royal verses, set down — as you urgently requested — in the King's own hand as well. From both of these you will recognise, not obscurely, what kind of judgement our Most Serene King and Most Clement Lord holds of you and of your writings. Truly he carries you in his eyes and exalts you to heaven with praises, whenever mention of you arises. And it arises very often — namely whenever he recounts that Danish journey of his or speaks of natural philosophy, and especially of mathematics; then never is that Star of Uraniborg not proclaimed. As for Craig the physician, that antagonist [ἀνταγωνιστήν] of yours, I bade him fare well [ὑγιαίνειν] only so far as that he should contend with you by arguments, not by abuse — which I hope the man will do hereafter as well. Of Buchanan, another time: lest, if perchance [I set down] everything at once, matter for writing should fail us both. The rest [you may learn] from the Honourable Lords the Ambassadors, who have all good men as witnesses with how great faith, prudence, and diligence they have conducted their affairs here. Farewell, most noble and high-born Lord; and all those zealous services and offices toward you that I shall be able to render — I would have you expect them on these terms: that you persuade yourself for certain they can be ended by no other thing than the close of [my] life. Given from the Royal Palace of Holyrood, on the Kalends of August [1 August] 1593.</p><p>Most devoted to Your Magnificence<br>and most obedient to you,<br>Peter Young of Seton.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Petrus Iunius a Setoun</i> = <b>Sir Peter Young of Seton (Easter Seaton), 1544–1628</b>, Scottish humanist, co-tutor (with George Buchanan) to the young James VI, royal almoner and diplomat. He had served as James's envoy to Denmark in 1586–87 (the \"<i>iter illud suum Danicum</i>,\" the \"Danish journey,\" recalled below in fact refers to the <b>King's</b> own 1590 journey to Denmark and his visit to Hven on 20 March 1590). The signature form <i>a Setoun</i> renders his territorial designation \"of Seton.\"</li><li><b>Addressee titulature.</b> <i>Domino in Knudstrup</i> — \"Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>\" (Knutstorp, the Brahe family seat in Scania), Tycho's patrimonial designation. <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting,\" protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Sereniss. Regis nostri … Domini Clementiss.</b></i> — <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), Peter Young's former pupil and patron. The King's high regard for Tycho is documented; he had visited Uraniborg in person in March 1590.</li><li><b><i>Diploma Regium</b></i> — \"the Royal Diploma\": the royal privilege/letter James VI granted Tycho (the safe-conduct-cum-printing-privilege Tycho had solicited). Rendered \"Royal Diploma,\" the period term kept.</li><li><b><i>versibus Regijs, Regia etiam manu … exaratis</b></i> — \"the Royal verses, set down in the King's own hand\": James VI's celebrated Latin verses in Tycho's honour, autograph. <i>exarare</i> (\"to plough out, write out\") kept as \"set down.\"</li><li><b><i>Magci Domini Legati / Magcis Dominis Legatis</b></i> — \"the Honourable Lords the Ambassadors\" (<i>Magnifici Domini Legati</i>; the abbreviation <i>Mag.</i> / <i>Magcis</i> expanded). The Scottish embassy then concluding business in Denmark; their precise membership is not named in the letter and is left unspecified here.</li><li><b><i>Uraniburgicum … Sydus</b></i> — \"that Star of Uraniborg,\" a courtly epithet for Tycho himself (the <i>De nova stella</i> discoverer turned into a \"star\"); <i>Sydus</i> = <i>sidus</i>, the chancery -y- spelling kept in the source block.</li><li><b>The Craig affair.</b> <i>CRAIGIUM medicum … ἀνταγωνιστήν illum tuum</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Scottish physician (later physician to James VI), Tycho's learned antagonist in the dispute over the comet of 1577 and Tycho's cosmology; Craig later apologised. Young reports having urged Craig to dispute <i>argumentis … non convitijs</i> (\"by arguments, not by abuse\").</li><li><b>George Buchanan.</b> <i>De BUCHANANO alias</i> = \"Of <b>Buchanan</b>, another time\": <b>George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the great Scottish humanist and James VI's principal tutor (with Young); the subject is deferred. (Buchanan being dead since 1582, the matter is presumably literary — likely his works or reputation — but the letter does not say, so it is left open.)</li><li><b>Greek.</b></li><li><i>nihil <b>ἀυτήν</b> veriti</i> — \"fearing nothing †ἀυτήν†.\" The accusative form as printed (ἀυτήν, with a smooth-breathing alpha) is anomalous: read most likely as <i>αὐτήν</i> \"her / it\" (feminine/the matter), making the sense \"fearing nothing of it / in that regard.\" The construction (<i>vereri</i> + accusative of the thing feared) is sound, but the referent is unclear and the printed accentuation irregular; transliterated, kept verbatim in the source block, and flagged as a crux rather than smoothed. <b>Transliteration:</b> <i>autḗn</i> / <i>autḗn</i>.</li><li><i>ἀνταγωνιστήν</i> (<b>antagōnistḗn</b>, acc.) — \"antagonist, adversary\"; in apposition to <i>Craigium</i>. Kept and rendered \"antagonist.\"</li><li><i>ὑγιαίνειν</i> (<b>hygiainein</b>) — literally \"to be in good health\"; here the epistolary leave-taking idiom <i>χαίρειν / ὑγιαίνειν κελεύειν</i>, \"to bid (someone) farewell / good health.\" Rendered \"I bade him fare well,\" with the play on \"fare well … only so far as\" preserved: Young's good wishes to Craig are conditional on Craig's debating fairly.</li><li><b>Tone / lexis.</b> <i>putidiusculus</i> — a rare diminutive of <i>putidus</i>, \"a little too affected / fastidious / over-nice (in self-justification)\"; rendered \"a little too fastidious.\" <i>silentio quodam novo veteris silentij crimen refellam</i> — the studied paradox (\"refute the charge of old silence by a new silence,\" i.e. by not belabouring excuses) is kept as a paradox.</li><li><b>Dateline / place.</b> <i>Datae ex Regia Stae Crucis Cal. Augusti 1593</i> = \"Given from the <b>Royal Palace of Holyrood</b> (<i>Regia Sanctae Crucis</i>, the Palace of Holyroodhouse, Edinburgh), on the <b>Kalends of August</b> = 1 August 1593.\" Scotland was on the Julian calendar (O.S.) in 1593; rendered O.S. with no conversion. The catalogue label (\"Holyrood Palace (Edinburgh),\" 1593-08-01) agrees with the dateline; <i>Sancta Crux</i> = Holyrood is identified here. The Roman-calendar idiom is kept and glossed per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Tuae Magtiae studiosiss. atque tibique obsequentiss.</i> = \"Most devoted to Your Magnificence and most obedient to you\" (<i>Magtiae</i> = <i>Magnificentiae</i>, abbreviation expanded).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin body and the two clear Greek interpolations (<i>ἀνταγωνιστήν</i>, <i>ὑγιαίνειν</i>) are rendered securely; the single genuine crux is <b>nihil ἀυτήν veriti</b>, where the printed Greek accusative <i>ἀυτήν</i> is anomalously breathed and its referent (\"her / it / the matter\") is undetermined — flagged and left unsmoothed rather than silently resolved to <i>αὐτήν</i>. A secondary, lesser uncertainty is the unstated subject of <i>De Buchanano alias</i> (so far Buchanan's works/reputation), likewise left open.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII p. 368 (cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f): Dreyer prints the same irregularly-breathed <i>ἀυτήν</i> with no emendation — the anomaly is editorially transmitted, not a transcription error; the referent is undetermined, and <i>De Buchanano alias</i> leaves its subject genuinely unstated. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VII p.354 as a page image, including the Royal Diploma/verses passage, the King's praise of Uraniborg, the Craig-as-antagonist Greek wordplay, and the Buchanan aside. Exact match — this includes the crux 'nihil ἀυτὴν veriti', which Dreyer itself prints unresolved, confirming the translator's dagger-flag of it as an honest textual crux rather than a corruption on our part. The published_grade of C on this packet is not supported by the collation: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss was found anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15930427001", "next": "brahe-t_15930911001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15930427001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15930911001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for prolonged silence, choosing confession over excuses, and assures Brahe his goodwill remains unchanged, with the ambassadors offered as pledges of his constancy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930801001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15930911001", "d": "1593-09-11", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Peter Young", "ck": "Peter Young", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peter Young (Petrus Iunius) — Uraniborg, 11 September 1593 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77 (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PETRO IUNIO. TYCHO BRAHE. S.</p><p>DIUTURNUM illud tuum silentium, quod excusationis silentio praetereundum censes, sicque vel maxime excusas, id quidem, mi optime IUNI, argute facis, at nihilominus vel sic nimium te apud amicum, sponte sua nihil sinistrius de te suspicantem, purgas. Non enim addubito, licet Huaenulam nostram literis tuis diu et avide desideratis (alijs procul dubio et gravioribus impeditus occupationibus) invisere aliquamdiu supersederis, quin amorem tamen et memoriam nostri in animo illo tuo adeo humano et candido arcte foveris. Quod ut confidentius mihi persuadeam, non saltem literae hae tuae compotissimae, eximiae erga nos benevolentiae specimen referentes, et ea, quae D. Legati ad nos feliciter reversi, ex tuis de nobis amicis sermonibus insuper retulerunt, affatim evincunt, verum etiam ex insigni illo studio tuo, quo tam graviter votis nostris satisfecisti, ut et diploma honorificum plane et praeclarum Serenissimi ac potentissimi Regis vestri, et epigrammata, arguta sane illa et accurata, ab eius Maiestate in mei gratiam favorabiliter conscripta, mihi procurâris, non obscure liquet, quanti nos et studia nostra facias. Quam quidem ob rem ego vicissim de te honorifice, ut tua fert virtus et praelustris eruditio, sentire, gratamque tui memoriam in posterum, uti et hactenus feci, conservare non desinam, nec ullo officiorum genere, quatenus vires nostrae id sustinent, me a te vinci sinam. Gratias autem imprimis, quas possum maximas, serenissimo Regi vestro reverenter ago, quod Astronomica nostra scripta et diplomate Regio munere et carminibus adeo elegantibus exornare non sit dedignatus. Tibi quoque, quod haec tam illustria nostrorum operum elogia, ab ipsius Maiestate meo nomine demisse impetrare non sis gravatus, haud parum debeo. Quantum enim nostris laboribus hinc decoris et gratiae accessurum sit, non sum nescius. Itaque ut perpetua observantia tam egregia in me collata beneficia recolam, devinciri me tibi lubens agnosco. BUCHANANI vitam, si aliquando per otium describi curâris mihique miseris, non saltem rem gratam praestiteris, sed iamdudum datis sponsionibus te tandem exsolveris. Vale, vir praestantissime, et de voluntate ac officijs in te meis ita velim statuas, ut omnia tibi de me polliceri fidenter et libere possis, quae ab amico sincero et constanti proficisci soleant. Iterum vale, et amicos in Scotia ex me saluta, imprimis eruditissimum illum THOMAM CRAIGIUM, qui Uraniam nostram carminibus suis haud illepidis decorare voluit. Fratrem eius, quem meum Antagonistam vocas, attingere non audeo, cum prae arroganti philautia nimis mordax veritatisque invictae hostis existat. Quod enim Cometae in Aethere ipso versentur, non saltem in eo, qui anno 1577 apparuit (de quo inter nos controvertitur) sed quatuor insuper alijs postmodum visis adeo accurate observavi, Geometriceque demonstravi, ut contradicere praesumentes sensu communi destitui merito videantur.</p><p>Iamque tertium et pluries vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 11 Septemb. 1593.<br>Tuae Magnificentiae addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Peter Young. Tycho Brahe. Greeting.</b></p><p>That long silence of yours, which you reckon should be passed over with a silence of excuse — and so make your very greatest excuse for — that, my excellent Young, you do cleverly; yet even so you clear yourself overmuch before a friend who, of his own accord, suspects nothing more unfavourable of you. For I do not doubt that, though you have for a while held off from visiting our little Hven with the letters of yours so long and eagerly desired (hindered, no doubt, by other and weightier occupations), you have nonetheless kept close in that mind of yours, so humane and candid, your love and remembrance of us. And that I may persuade myself of this the more confidently, not only do these most welcome letters of yours, bearing a token of your exceptional goodwill toward us, and the things which the Lord Ambassadors — happily returned to us — further reported from your friendly talk about us, prove it abundantly; but also, from that remarkable zeal of yours, by which you so substantially fulfilled our wishes — in that you procured for me both an honorific charter, plainly splendid, from your Most Serene and most mighty King, and epigrams, truly clever and exact, graciously composed by His Majesty in my favour — it is plainly clear how much you esteem us and our studies. For which reason I in turn shall not cease to think honourably of you, as your virtue and most illustrious learning require, and to keep, hereafter as I have done hitherto, a grateful remembrance of you; nor, in any kind of good office, so far as our strength bears it, shall I allow myself to be outdone by you. And first of all I give the greatest thanks I can, reverently, to your Most Serene King, in that he did not disdain to adorn our astronomical writings both with the royal gift of a charter and with verses so elegant. To you too I owe no little, in that you were not loath to obtain, humbly in my name, these so illustrious eulogies of our works from His Majesty himself. For I am not unaware how much grace and distinction will accrue to our labours from this. And so, that I may with perpetual observance reflect upon benefits so distinguished conferred upon me, I gladly acknowledge myself bound to you. The Life of Buchanan, if you should at some time at your leisure see to having it written out and send it to me, you would not only render a welcome service, but would at last discharge yourself of pledges given long ago. Farewell, most excellent sir, and concerning my goodwill and good offices toward you I would have you so judge that you may confidently and freely promise yourself everything from me that is wont to come from a sincere and steadfast friend. Again farewell, and greet for me my friends in Scotland, above all that most learned Thomas Craig, who was willing to adorn our Urania with his verses, by no means without charm. His brother, whom you call my Antagonist, I dare not touch upon, since out of arrogant self-love he is too biting, and an enemy of unconquerable truth. For that comets move in the aether itself — not only in the one that appeared in the year 1577 (about which there is dispute between us), but in four others besides, seen afterward — I have observed so accurately and demonstrated geometrically that those who presume to contradict it may deservedly be thought to be destitute of common sense.</p><p>And now a third time, and many times over, farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 11 September 1593.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Petrus Iunius</i> = <b>Peter Young</b> (1544–1628), Scottish humanist, royal preceptor (tutor to James VI together with George Buchanan) and diplomat; he had visited Uraniborg and corresponded with Tycho (cf. the 18 Aug. 1586 letter). The address-line <i>PETRO IUNIO. TYCHO BRAHE. S.</i> is, exceptionally, in this terse form rather than an extended honorific salutation; <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting\"), per §5.5.</li><li><b><i>Serenissimi … Regis vestri / ipsius Maiestate</b></i> — the <b>King of Scots, James VI</b> (later James I of England), Buchanan's and Young's royal pupil. He had visited Hven in 1590 and here is credited with both an <i>honorific charter</i> (<i>diploma honorificum</i>) and <i>epigrams</i> (<i>epigrammata</i>) composed in Tycho's honour.</li><li><b><i>Huaenula nostra</b></i> — \"our little Hven,\" an affectionate diminutive for the island of <b>Hven</b> (Latin <i>Huaena</i>), site of Uraniborg; rendered \"our little Hven.\"</li><li><b><i>D. Legati … reversi</b></i> — \"the Lord Ambassadors, happily returned\"; the Scottish embassy/envoys who had carried news between the courts. <i>D.</i> (<i>Domini</i>) = \"Lords/Masters\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Reading crux — <i>i6.n mei gratiam</i>.</b> The witness as printed reads the garbled <i>\"i6.n mei gratiam\"</i>; this is a transcription artifact for <b>_in mei gratiam_</b> (\"in my favour / for my sake\"), the reading required by sense and confirmed by the parallel phrase elsewhere in the corpus (<i>nihil … in mei gratiam faciendum</i>). The printed garble is retained in the Source block per protocol §2; the translation follows the corrected reading.</li><li><b><i>Buchanani vitam</b></i> — <b>the <i>Life</i> of George Buchanan</b> (1506–1582), the Scottish humanist, historian and poet, James VI's tutor; Tycho owned and displayed a portrait of Buchanan (a gift from Young in 1586) at Uraniborg. Tycho here duns Young for a promised biographical account (<i>vita</i>), recalling <i>sponsiones</i> (\"pledges\") long given.</li><li><b><i>Thomas Craigium</b></i> — <b>Thomas Craig</b> (of Riccarton, c. 1538–1608), Scottish jurist and Latin poet, who had honoured Tycho's <i>Urania</i> (i.e. his astronomical work) with complimentary verses (<i>carminibus … haud illepidis</i>, \"by no means without charm\" — a litotes preserved).</li><li><b><i>Fratrem eius … meum Antagonistam</b></i> — Craig's brother, <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), royal physician and mathematician, Tycho's cometary opponent. Young had evidently styled him Tycho's <i>Antagonista</i> (\"Antagonist\"). Tycho declines to engage him (<i>attingere non audeo</i>, \"I dare not touch upon [him]\"), charging him with <i>arrogans philautia</i> (\"arrogant self-love\"). The contest concerns the comets' location.</li><li><b>Astronomy / the cometary controversy.</b> <i>Cometae in Aethere ipso versentur</i> = \"comets move in the aether itself\" — Tycho's claim that comets are supralunary bodies coursing through the celestial <i>aether</i>, not sublunary exhalations. He cites the <b>comet of 1577</b> (<i>qui anno 1577 apparuit</i>) \"about which there is dispute between us,\" plus <i>four others</i> observed since, as <i>Geometrice</i> (\"geometrically\") demonstrated by parallax. <i>Cometa</i> / <i>aether</i> kept as the period terms; no instrument-name occurs.</li><li><b><i>Uraniam nostram</b></i> — \"our Urania,\" i.e. Tycho's astronomy / astronomical œuvre, personified by the Muse Urania; kept as a flourish (cf. the <i>Sacris Uraniae</i> idiom, §5.5).</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Tuae Magnificentiae addictissimus</i> = \"Most devoted to Your Magnificence\" — <i>Magnificentia Tua</i> (\"Your Magnificence\") is the honorific Tycho uses of Young throughout the letter (cf. <i>de voluntate … in te meis</i>); rendered consistently.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 11 Septemb. 1593</i> = \"Given at Uraniborg, 11 September 1593\" (Old Style; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dataset label <i>\"1593. 11. september. Uraniborg\"</i> agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'i6.n' corrected to 'in', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p.355 (translation unaffected — already correctly rendered 'in my favour').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the one textual point is the garbled <i>\"i6.n mei gratiam\"</i>, resolved to <i>in mei gratiam</i> on contextual and corpus-parallel grounds (noted, not smoothed). The identification of the unnamed <i>Antagonista</i> as John Craig (brother of Thomas) rests on the external record rather than on anything the letter states beyond <i>fratrem eius</i>; flagged accordingly.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.354-357 (letter itself on 355-356): the opening rebuke of Young's silence, the King of Scotland's diploma and epigrams, the request for Buchanan's Life, the Thomas Craig / John Craig ('my Antagonist') passage, the comet-in-the-aether claim naming the 1577 comet plus four others, and the closing date (Uraniborg, 11 September 1593) all match Dreyer and are faithfully translated. One source-text corruption found (i6.n vs Dreyer's in); no other defects.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin glues a stray digit into the preposition 'in', printing 'i6.n'; Dreyer prints the plain word. The translation is unaffected, correctly rendering 'in mei gratiam' as 'in my favour'. Dreyer prints: <i>arguta sane illa et accurata, ab eius Maiestate in mei gratiam favorabiliter conscripta (TBOO VII p.355)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp.354-357 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect was confirmed exactly as flagged and corrected; no other issues were found in the passage.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Oxford, Bodleian Library, Smith 77. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Peter Young", "count": 7, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15930801001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15930801001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Accepts Young's apology for a long epistolary silence and thanks him for procuring items as requested, with news passed through the returning lord ambassadors.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15930911001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15931224001", "d": "1593-12-24", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 24 December 1593 [N.S.] (enclosing Caspar Peucer → Hagecius, 21 November 1593 [O.S.])", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.52</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS AVITA, VIRTUTE ET DOCTRINA PRAeSTANTI D. TYCHONI BRAHE DOMINO IN KNUDSTORFF ET INSULAe URANIAe, SUO DOMINO ET AMICO OBSERVANDO. S.</p><p>ANNUS integer praeterijt die 2 Decemb. nobiliss. D. TYCHO et amice observande, cum ad te scripsi et respondi ad tuas, quas 28. Septemb. anni praeteriti scripseras ad me. Sic totus annus literarum silentio nobis elapsus est, quod accidit, non oblivione amicitiae nostrae, quae, quod ad me attinet, sancte observatur et colitur, sed ob labores meae functionis et occupationes rei familiaris varias, valetudinem item parum firmam et constantem, et, ut uno verbo dicam, ob senium. Ago enim annum 68, paulo post, iuxta proverbium ponte deijciendus. Memoria certe ita me deficit, ut, nisi meam viderem et agnoscerem manum, nunquam me ea legisse aut tractasse dicerem. Deum oro, ne mihi penitus memoriam aboleri sinat, quod TRAPEZUNTIO in senio evenisse legimus, sed aliquam saltem concedat, ne divinarum promissionum ulla me capiat oblivio. Astronomica studia nunc plane conticuerunt apud me, nec est, mi amantiss. TYCHO, quod in illis aliquid a me expectes. Tu perge in his, quae coepisti, et perfice, dum vires ingenij et corporis vividae sunt, et temet aeternitati conserva. Solam Medicinam tracto, sustentandae familiae domesticae gratia. Illi adiungo Theologica et meditationem mortis, quae mors iam ostium nostri tabernaculi saepe pulsat. Tractaveram etiam Chymica per annum integrum et amplius, sed cum vana omnia esse deprehendissem, deserui.</p><p>Privilegium Caesareum a SAGERO Medico Lubecensi te accepisse existimo. Nam die 5 Maij huius anni significavit mihi id ad te transmisisse. Eius reditum ad nos aut Misenam expectabam. Cometam praeterito autumno effulsisse, communis erat fama. Aliunde etiam de eo perscribebatur. A me tamen studiose lustrante coelum nuspiam conspici potuit. Si quid tibi de eo constat, ad me perscribas, oro. Die 12 Decemb. quae de eo Cometa D. PEUCERUS ad me perscripserit, ex eius literis, quarum exemplum ad te mitto, cognosces. Hodie qui est dies 22 Decemb. pransus sum cum D. CURTIO. Monstravit mihi suum Sextantem. Confectus est a quodam insigni artifice Lantgraviano, et nuper admodum allatus. Si iam Quadrantem haberemus paratum, primo omnium hîc altitudinem poli observaremus, deinde distantias stellarum aliquot, et si quid aliud emergeret, haberes nos collegas, me quidem seniculum imbellem, at promptum ad labores aetati convenientes. Curat sibi quoque D. CURTIUS confici globum compactum ex parvis assulis ligneis simul conglutinatis. Eius diameter est 6 pedum longitudine. In eum globum imponere cupit stellas tuae observationis. Iussit te salvere plurimum. Scripsisset libentissime ipse ad te, sed testari possum, ob gravissimas occupationes et negocia aulica non potuisse, petit ut eum habeas excusatum. Invitat te ad Comitia Imperij Ratisbonam. Quod si te eo venturum certo intellexero, ipsemet tui videndi et te salutandi gratia accurram, modo valetudo et vires corpusculi non impedierint. Sin venire non poteris, remitte, quaeso, libellum APIANI de Cometa, cui opuscula quaedam Bohemica alligata sunt. Item Commentaria TRAPEZUNTIJ, quibus te iam fortasse non egere existimo. Ea opuscula ad D. CURTIUM mitte. B. V. Datae Pragae die 24 Decembris Anno 1593.</p><p>Per D. VINSHEMIUM haec ad te scribo.</p><p>T. studiosiss.<br>THAD.</p><p>CASPARUS PEUCERUS AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM. S. D.</p><p>EXPECTAVI avide abs te responsum ad postremas meas, quas non dubito te ab inclyto et generoso D. D. FERDINANDO Comite a Schlick accepisse. Illud cum differatur opinione mea diutius, censui eliciendum esse, sed nulla re meliore me perficere id posse sum ratus, quam si explorem tuas de Cometa nuper viso παρατηρήσεις, quem ego me fateor bis tantum conspexisse, circiter horam tertiam matutinam in asterismo Ursae maioris, et quidem eius capite, corpore parvo, colore lurido prorsus et obscuro, cauda oblonga et sub finem tenui, a Septentrione supra ortum solstitialem porrecta versus occasum brumalem. Mihi organis omnibus et operis destituto, locorum etiam opportunitate, aliud nihil observare licuit, quam quod visus deprehendit. De tempore exortus cognovi serius. Post conspectum autem bidui spe mea citius evanuit. Si quid igitur tibi de illo exploratum est, et de eventu quid praesagias, ut aperias mihi oro, missis literis per hunc virum nobilem ALBERTUM a Conttenau, qui isthuc in aulam Caesaream ab Illmo meo Principe ablegatur. In quod Zodiaci δωδεκατημόριον et quam eius partem inciderit ignoro. Plus non observavi, quam quod a Capellae et Haedorum sidere non procul constiterit, de latitudine, altitudine et parallaxi, nihil comperi, de his ergo tua peto et expecto observata. Nobilem hunc virum tibi commendo.</p><p>Bene et feliciter vale.<br>21 Novemb. Iul. Anno 93.<br>CASPAR PEUCERUS.<br>D.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Tycho Brahe, lord in Knudstrup and on the Isle of Urania, eminent in ancestral nobility of birth, in virtue and in learning, his lord and friend to be regarded with honour — greeting.</b></p><p>A full year went by on 2 December, most noble Master Tycho and friend to be honoured, since I wrote to you and replied to your letter — the one you had written to me on 28 September of the previous year. So a whole year has slipped past for us in epistolary silence, which has come about not through forgetfulness of our friendship — which, for my part, is observed and cherished as something sacred — but through the labours of my office and the various preoccupations of my household, likewise through health none too firm or settled, and, to say it in one word, through old age. For I am now in my sixty-eighth year, soon, as the proverb has it, to be thrown off the bridge. My memory is indeed failing me so badly that, did I not see and recognise my own hand, I should never say that I had read or handled these matters. I pray to God that he may not let my memory be utterly abolished in me — which we read befell Trapezuntius in his old age — but grant me at least some, that no forgetfulness of the divine promises may take hold of me. My astronomical studies have now fallen quite silent with me, and there is nothing, my most beloved Tycho, that you should expect from me in that line. You, press on in those things you have begun, and bring them to completion, while the powers of mind and body are still vigorous, and preserve yourself for eternity. I deal with medicine alone, for the sake of supporting my household family. To it I join theological matters and meditation upon death — which death now knocks often at the door of our tabernacle. I had also dealt with chymical matters for a full year and more, but when I had found them all to be vain, I gave them up.</p><p>I suppose that you have received the Imperial privilege from Sager, the physician of Lübeck. For on 5 May of this year he informed me that he had sent it on to you. I was awaiting his return to us, or to Meissen. That a comet shone forth last autumn was common report. It was written about from elsewhere too. By me, however, though I scanned the heavens diligently, it could nowhere be sighted. If anything about it is established for you, write it out to me, I pray. On 12 December — what Master Peucer wrote out to me concerning that comet, you will learn from his letter, a copy of which I am sending you. Today, which is the 22nd day of December, I dined with Master Curtius. He showed me his sextant. It was made by a certain distinguished Landgravian craftsman, and brought here only very recently. If only we now had a quadrant ready, we would first of all observe here the altitude of the pole, then the distances of some stars, and, if anything else should arise, you would have us as colleagues — me indeed a feeble little old man, but ready for labours suited to my age. Master Curtius is also having made for himself a globe built up from little wooden slats glued together. Its diameter is six feet in length. Onto that globe he desires to set the stars of your observation. He bade me give you his very warmest greetings. He would most gladly have written to you himself, but I can testify that, on account of his most weighty occupations and court business, he was unable to; he asks that you hold him excused. He invites you to the Diet of the Empire at Regensburg. And if I learn for certain that you are coming there, I will myself hasten up for the sake of seeing you and greeting you, provided that my health and the strength of my poor little body do not hinder it. But if you cannot come, then send back, I beg, the little book of Apian on the comet, to which certain Bohemian opuscula are bound; likewise the Commentaries of Trapezuntius, which I suppose you now perhaps no longer need. Send those tracts to Master Curtius. Fare well. Given at Prague, 24 December, in the year 1593.</p><p>I write this to you by way of Master Vinshemius.</p><p>Most devoted to you,<br>Thad[daeus].</p><p><b>Caspar Peucer to Thaddaeus Hagecius — heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I have eagerly awaited a reply from you to my last letter, which I do not doubt you received from the renowned and noble Lord Ferdinand, Count of Schlick. Since that [reply] is, in my opinion, being delayed rather long, I judged it should be drawn out; but I reckoned I could accomplish this by no better means than if I were to enquire into your observations [παρατηρήσεις] of the comet lately seen — which I confess I myself caught sight of only twice, at about the third hour of the morning, in the asterism of the Great Bear, and indeed at its head; small in body, of a wholly wan and dim colour, with an oblong tail, slender toward its end, stretched out from the north above the solstitial point of rising [the summer sunrise] toward the winter point of setting. Being myself destitute of all instruments and assistants, and of opportunity of place as well, I was able to observe nothing other than what sight grasped. Of the time of its rising I learned too late. And after I had observed it for two days it vanished, sooner than I had hoped. If, then, anything about it has been ascertained by you, and if you presage anything about its outcome, I beg you to disclose it to me, by a letter sent through this noble man, Albert von Conttenau, who is being dispatched thither to the Imperial court by my Most Illustrious Prince. Into which dodecatemorion [δωδεκατημόριον] of the Zodiac, and into what part of it, [the comet] fell, I do not know. I observed no more than that it stood not far from the star of the She-goat and the Kids; of its latitude, altitude, and parallax I found out nothing — about these, therefore, I seek and await your observations. I commend this noble man to you.</p><p>Fare well, and happily.<br>21 November, Julian, in the year [15]93.<br>Caspar Peucer.<br>[given]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck, 1525–1600), Prague physician, astronomer, and Imperial <i>medicus</i>; a long-standing correspondent of Tycho's and the leading Bohemian advocate of Tycho's super-lunar interpretation of the 1572 nova and the 1577 comet. He signs <i>THAD.</i> He states he is \"in his sixty-eighth year,\" consistent with a 1525 birth.</li><li><b>Recipient / address.</b> <i>Domino in Knudstorff et Insulae Uraniae</i> — \"lord in <b>Knudstrup</b> [Tycho's ancestral seat in Scania] and on the <b>Isle of Urania</b>\" (i.e. Hven, with <b>Uraniborg</b>).</li><li><b>Enclosed letter.</b> The second letter is the enclosure Hagecius mentions (\"a copy of which I am sending you\"): <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), the Wittenberg/Melanchthonian physician-astronomer (Melanchthon's son-in-law), writing <i>to Hagecius</i> on the same comet. <i>S. D.</i> = <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (§5.5). The closing <i>D.</i> is the chancery <i>Datae/Datum</i> (\"given\"), rendered bracketed.</li><li><b><i>annus integer praeterijt die 2 Decemb.</b></i> — \"a full year went by on 2 December\": Hagecius's previous letter to Tycho was of c. 2 December (1592), replying to Tycho's of 28 September (1592). A relative dating internal to the letter, not the dateline; left as written.</li><li><b><i>iuxta proverbium ponte deijciendus</b></i> — \"to be thrown off the bridge, as the proverb has it\": the Roman saying <i>sexagenarios de ponte</i> (the old were proverbially \"cast from the bridge\"); a self-deprecating reference to his age. Kept as proverb.</li><li><b><i>TRAPEZUNTIO … Commentaria TRAPEZUNTIJ</b></i> — <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius, 1395–1486), the humanist translator/commentator of Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i>; his loss of memory in old age was a topos. The <i>Commentaria Trapezuntij</i> Hagecius asks Tycho to return are Trebizond's commentary on Ptolemy. (\"Trapezuntius\" kept as the period name, glossed.)</li><li><b><i>Chymica … vana omnia</b></i> — \"chymical matters … all vain\": Hagecius reports abandoning alchemy after more than a year as fruitless. <i>Chymica</i> kept as \"chymical\" (period term), not modernised to \"chemistry.\"</li><li><b><i>SAGERO Medico Lubecensi</b></i> — \"<b>Sager</b>, physician of Lübeck\": a Lübeck medical man serving as courier of an <b>Imperial privilege</b> (<i>Privilegium Caesareum</i>, a printing/publishing privilege) to Tycho. <i>Misenam</i> = <b>Meissen</b> (§5.7 sense).</li><li><b>The comet.</b> The <i>Cometa</i> of \"last autumn\" is the <b>comet of autumn 1593</b>. Hagecius, despite diligent search, never saw it; Peucer (writing 21 Nov.) reports two pre-dawn sightings near the head of Ursa Major, close to <b>Capella and the Kids</b> (<i>Capellae et Haedorum sidere</i> — the star Capella with the Haedi in Auriga), wholly without instruments. The request for <b>latitude, altitude, and parallax</b> (<i>latitudo, altitudo, parallaxis</i>, §5.1) reflects the Tychonic programme of deciding a comet's super-lunar position by parallax.</li><li><b><i>CURTIO / CURTIUS</b></i> — <b>Jacob Kurtz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor at Prague, astronomy patron; the <i>Curtius</i> whose Prague house Tycho would later use. He owns a <b>sextant</b> by \"a distinguished <b>Landgravian</b> craftsman\" (i.e. from the Hesse-Kassel workshop of Landgrave Wilhelm IV — likely Joost Bürgi's circle) and is commissioning a wooden <b>globe</b> six feet in diameter onto which he wishes to plot \"the stars of your observation\" (Tycho's star catalogue). The conditional \"if only we had a quadrant\" (<i>Quadrantem … altitudinem poli … distantias stellarum</i>) is Hagecius's wistful offer of collaboration.</li><li><b><i>VINSHEMIUM</b></i> — <b>Vinshemius</b> (Vögelin / Veit Winsheim line), the courier by whom the letter is conveyed; named as bearer.</li><li><b><i>FERDINANDO Comite a Schlick</b></i> — <b>Ferdinand, Count of Schlick</b> (Šlik), Bohemian magnate, named in Peucer's letter as the conduit of his earlier letter.</li><li><b><i>ALBERTUM a Conttenau</b></i> — <b>Albert von Conttenau</b> (so spelled), a nobleman being sent by Peucer's prince to the Imperial court, proposed as the return courier. <i>Illmo meo Principe</i> = \"my Most Illustrious Prince\" (Peucer was then in the service of the Electors of Saxony / Anhalt at Dessau).</li><li><b><i>APIANI … de Cometa</b></i> — a comet tract of <b>Apian</b> (Peter Apian, or his son Philipp), bound with \"certain Bohemian opuscula\"; to be returned to Curtius.</li><li><b><i>Comitia Imperij Ratisbonam</b></i> — the <b>Diet of the Empire at Regensburg</b> (the Imperial Diet held there); Curtius's invitation to Tycho.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>παρατηρήσεις</i> — <i>paratērḗseis</i>, \"observations\" (transliterated <i>paratereseis</i>); transcribed in the source block and translated, per §5.1. <i>δωδεκατημόριον</i> — <i>dōdekatēmórion</i>, literally \"twelfth-part,\" the technical term for a <b>sign / dodecatemorion of the Zodiac</b>; transcribed and glossed (kept as \"dodecatemorion\" with the gloss \"of the Zodiac\").</li><li><b><i>ortum solstitialem … occasum brumalem</b></i> — the ancient wind-rose/horizon idiom: the comet's tail stretched from the north \"above the <b>summer sunrise</b> point toward the <b>winter sunset</b> point\" (i.e. roughly NE-toward-SW on the horizon). Rendered with the idiom and glossed, not converted to azimuths.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating crux.</b> Hagecius's letter is dated <i>Pragae die 24 Decembris Anno 1593</i> — Prague, at the Catholic Imperial court, so <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b>; the header carries this as written. Within his letter he says \"<b>Today, which is the 22nd day of December</b>\" yet subscribes \"<b>24 December</b>\": a two-day gap between the body's \"today\" and the dateline (composition begun 22 Dec., completed/dispatched 24 Dec.) — <b>flagged, not reconciled</b> per §3. Peucer, by contrast, subscribes \"<b>21 November, Julian</b> [<i>Iul.</i>], in the year [15]93\" — he explicitly marks his date <b>Old Style</b>, the Protestant Saxon usage; the ten-day Julian/Gregorian gap is thus visible across the two enclosed letters and is preserved exactly as each writer dated his own. No date is silently converted.</li><li><b>Honorifics.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"greeting\"; <i>T. studiosiss.</i> = <i>Tui studiosissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to you\" (§5.5); <i>B. V.</i> = <i>Bene Vale</i>, \"Fare well.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and Greek are rendered securely and the persons identified. Soft points, all flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the in-letter date discrepancy \"today the 22nd\" vs. dateline \"24 December,\" and the cross-letter O.S./N.S. gap (kept, not reconciled); (2) the spelling/identity of <i>Albert von Conttenau</i> (as printed); (3) whether <i>Lantgraviano artifice</i> denotes Bürgi specifically or the Hesse-Kassel workshop generally — left at \"Landgravian craftsman.\" No reading rises to a [C]-grade crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter — Hagecius's address, body and subscription, plus the enclosed Peucer-to-Hagecius letter in full — against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.356-358, page images only. Source_text matches Dreyer's print word-for-word (including 'nuspiam', not 'nusquam'). Checked every proper name (Sagerus, Curtius, Trapezuntius, Albertus a Conttenau, Ferdinandus Comes a Schlick), date (2 Decemb., 28 Septemb., 5 Maij, 12 Decemb., 22 Decemb., 24 Decembris 1593, 21 Novemb. 93), and number/sum (annum 68, sextans, 6 pedum globe diameter, bidui) against Dreyer and against the English — all correct. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.52."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 31, "prev": "brahe-t_15930420001", "next": "brahe-t_15940000001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15930420001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15940000001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for a year of epistolary silence, attributing it to the burdens of office, poor health, and failing memory in his sixty-eighth year.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15931224001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940000001", "d": "1594-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer, vol. VII<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.19 (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM.</p><p>PLURIMUM sane gavisus sum, optime mi THADDAeE, quod ex literis tuis, his ipsis diebus mihi redditis, perceperim te adhuc mediocriter bona, pro hac aetate, frui valetudine. Verebar enim ne gravissimi illi morbi, quos te diu multumque infestasse, ultimis significasti, saeviores adhuc redditi, te nimis valetudinarium lecto detinerent, vel forte humanitus tibi quid interea accidisset, praesertim ex quo meis literis praecedente anno per civem quendam Gorlicensem ad te datis nihil tanto tempore responderis. Itaque valde etiam nunc addubito, an has recte acceperis, siquidem nullam mentionem in his literis facias, vel de contentis earum vel de Tomo nostro primo Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum, quem unâ Magnifico Domino IACOBO CURTIO et tibi inspiciendum dijudicandumque misi. Imo iam bis tibi eiusdem libri exemplar communicari curavi, posterius tamen locupletius auctum et in quo pauca admodum desiderarentur, quo minus publici iuris fierent. Mirari autem satis nequeo, quod in nullis literis interea ad me datis huius rei mentionem facias, quare ad aliorum manus pervenisse hos libros et alibi detineri suspicor. Id equidem invite ferrem. Quod Astronomica studia in hoc senio apud te conticescant, quodque Chymica ob vanitatem neglexeris, recte fieri censeo, his enim nimium indulgere, est, ut plurimum, oleum et operam perdere, illis penitius tractandis valetudinis in hac ingravescente aetate afflictio non sustinet; optime igitur feceris, si supramundanis, ut te etiam facere dicis, contemplandis et valetudini curandae solummodo intentus fueris. Privilegium Caesareum ultimum illud et quadam in parte locupletatum Doct. SAGERUS Lubecensis iam dudum ad me misit, pro quo etiam in ultimis literis et Ampliss. D. CURTIO et tibi gratias obnixe egi, quod et nunc facio.</p><p>Cometam quendam in Iulio et Augusto superioris anni illuxisse certum quidem est ex multorum relatione, at nos eum hîc Uraniburgi, utut diligentius forte quam alij coelestibus attendentes, non conspeximus, tum quod rarissima ijs temporibus hîc fuerit serenitas, tum quia versus Septentrionem, si quando coelum per intervalla sudum esset, chasmata crebra viserentur, quae Cometam istic versantem per se, uti audio, admodum tenuem existentem, facile visui subduxerunt. Invigilavimus ijsdem mensibus omni serena nocte (quae tamen uti dictum est crebra non fuit) Martis potissimum stellae ad acronychium situm tendentis observationibus, ut discrimen calculi Alphonsini et Copernicei, quod in ipsa oppositione 9 gradus (maiori quam unquam alias intervallo) excessit, coelitus dirimere liceret, utque hanc acronychiam Martis observationem caeteris per plurimos antecedentes annos habitis coniungerem. Deprehensus autem est Mars (ut et hoc obiter hîc indicem) quando Soli e diametro opponebatur, numeros COPERNICI 4½ gradibus excedere, Alphonsinos vero integris quinis gradibus antevertere, quae differentia maior quam alias in ♂, cum pernox est, obveniens propter vicinitatem Perigaei eccentrici (ut communiter loquar) contigit, illic enim error in vero Apogaei loco, qui apud COPERNICUM quinos gradus attingit, admodum foecundus existit, ut de eccentricitate, quam is suis hypothesibus patrocinari volens nimium coarctavit, ut de alijs quae huc requiruntur nunc non dicam. Interim vero (ut ad rem redeam) dum Martis observationi, quoties noctes defaecatae id permiserunt, gnaviter insistentes, Cometam illum, de quo iam rumor increbuerat, uspiam videre nequivimus, utut data opera illam coeli plagam circa finem Augusti crebrius inspicientes, quemadmodum et alij, qui in vicina Haffnia aliquas noctes huic inspiciendo vigiles traduxerunt, voto suo frustrati sunt. Perscriptum nihilominus ad me est, quod studiosus quidam Danus CHRISTIERNUS IOHANNIS RIPENSIS nomine, qui antequam in Germaniam concessisset, aliquot annis in exercitijs Astronomicis hîc mihi inservierat, confecto uno vel altero ad imitationem meorum instrumentulo, eius observationi, qua licuit accuratione, incubuisse, neque tamen aliquid certi nisi bis, ob exilitatem Cometae et serenitatis penuriam denotasse. Quicquid tamen id fuerit, is brevi e Cimbria Patria sua (nuper enim in Daniam reversus est) huc ad me veniens secum adferet. Tunc si viderim operae pretium esse, observationes istas examinabo atque an Parallaxes inde scrutari liceat, tentabo, ut vel sic, id quod nobis invitis neglectum est, quodammodo resarciam, atque hunc Cometam quinis illis maxima diligentia a me Coelitus denotatis atque secundo Tomo Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum descriptis adiungam, mutuando mei quondam discipuli observationes atque eas loco mearum usurpando.</p><p>Quae cl. ille PEUCERUS tibi de hac Crinitâ per literas significavit, nihil ferme ad rem faciunt, nullas enim habet observationes, quae locum eius apparentem prodant, nedum quae Parallaxibus enodandis sufficiant, imo ne quidem diem rectum annotat, quo illum viderit, neque ista invicem congruunt, quod ab initio dicat, se saltem bis eum vidisse in Asterismo Ursae maioris et postea circa finem subiungat, quod a Capellae et Haedorum sidere non procul constiterit; hi enim duo asterismi ultra duodecimam coeli partem inter se distant. Qualis igitur quaeso haec fuerit observatio etiam ex solo visu petita? Ista igitur otiosa et mentione indigna uti fuere, ita etiam nunc sint, relinquamus.</p><p>Gaudeo et praestantissimo illi D. CURTIO communi amico nostro congratulor, quod Sextantis instrumentum ex officina Lantgraviana adeptus sit, quo siderum intercapedines infallibiliter rimari possit. Est procul dubio ad imitationem cuiusdam ex meis factum, quo per unicum observatorem distantiae mensurantur, licet nonnihil maiori negotio quam alijs Sextantibus, qui duos requirunt collimatores, quod si sic est, habet rimulas iuxta centrum prioribus pinnacidijs parallelas et divisionem per puncta transversalia nobis usitatam, tunc quoque fulcrum, cuius beneficio in omnem planitiem versus quascunque stellas commode dirigi queat: atqui haec VITICHIUS Lantgravio apud me visa praeordinavit. Verum ipsemet Princeps loco cochleae, qua ego in laxando vel coarctando instrumenti angulo utor (quam VITICHIUS indicare neglexerat) catenulam rotulis inhaerentem addidit. Poterit D. CURTIUS periculum fieri curare, an intermediante hoc suo novitio Sextante eaedem prorsus quarundam fixarum remotiones deprehendantur, quales pag. 30 et sequenti in eo fragmento Epistolarum Astronomicarum ad Lantgravium scriptarum, quod iam dudum tecum communicavi, nostris Sextantibus coelitus designatae, exponuntur; eas enim scio exactas esse, quibus si Curtiani Sextantis inventa ad amussim corresponderint, non potest non eius perfecte elaboratus esse, cum omne verum suo vero consonum sit. Exoptarem autem, ut Quadrantem quoque iustae magnitudinis et e solido metallo constantem pariter divisum et pinnacidijs instructum in promptu haberet, quo per altitudines meridianas Solis et stellarum declinationes unâ cum Poli sublimitate exquisite scrutari commodum foret, quamvis et hoc eodem Sextante intra sublimitatem sexaginta graduum ad aequilibrium Horizontis sit dispositum atque in plano meridiani directo id ipsum praestare non admodum sit invium, qua de re eum admonere potes.</p><p>Gratum insuper mihi est, quod Globum magnum sexpedalem in diametro construi curet, prospiciat autem diligenter ne in rotunditate eius undiquaque procuranda plus quam existimaret difficultatis lateat, praesertim ut ea in materiâ ligneâ utut varie composita, mutationi tamen aurae suaque ipsius natura instabilitati obnoxia, diuturna et constans sit. Consultius itaque duxerim, ut Globum istum ad rotunditatem accuratam, quoad fieri potest, deductum, permittat primum unius ad minimum aestatis calorem et hiemis frigus, varia ijs intercedente aurae mutatione, experiri, donec ad consistentiam aliquam deducatur, tumque denuo globositatem eius examinet, atque ubi opus fuerit, emendari curet, postmodum, ubi sensibiliter nihil remittere et variationem nullam suscipere animadverterit, laminis orichalcicis laevigatis et subtiliter adunatis superinduci faciat, et tunc demum in rotunditatem exquisitam omni ex parte redigi, politaque superficie subdivisionibus graduum in singula minuta per puncta transversalia commoditatem praebente elaborari provideat, alias enim stellas nostras, quas circiter octingentas cap. 2 prioris Tomi in ipso minuto adeoque nonnunquam semisse unius diligentissime quo ad longum et latum coelitus derivatas recensuimus, vix eâ quâ opus fuerit subtilitate et correspondentia Globo insigniverit. Haud ignota loquor, nam et is noster orichalcicus Globus, qui sex pedes in diametro adaequat, aliquot annorum laboriosam difficultatem obiecit, antequam ad omnimodam perfectionem perductus sit, ut de sumptibus nihil dicam, qui si numerati sigillatim essent, forte mille Ioachimicorum expensam aequarent aut superarent.</p><p>Quod tam amice atque honorifice saepedictus D. CURTIUS me ad comitia Imperialia, quae proxime Ratisbonae celebrabuntur, invitat, est cur illi officiose gratias agam, et si res meae multipliciaque, quibus occupor, negotia id ferrent, eius benevolae petitioni equidem non [renuntiarem]. Verum cum mihi in hac insula, utut non admodum magna, maximis tamen occupationibus distento, vix tantum supersit otij et temporis, ut in vicinas Daniae oras ad consanguineos et amicos vel etiam propriorum negotiorum causa nonnunquam excurrere liceat, si itaque tam longinquum in Germaniam iter cum iactura et dispendio rerum, quas hîc tracto et urgeo, suscipere nequeam, habebit me D. CURTIUS pro congenita sibi humanitate et iudicij dexteritate, uti spero, sufficienter excusatum. Scripsissem vero ipsemet nunc illi, si tantum superesset opportunitatis, et nuncius in vicinam Germaniam properans abitum non adeo festinaret. Quod vero nunc hac in parte praetermissum fuerit, alias oblata commodiore occasione affatim compensare non supersedebo. Resaluta itaque meo nomine Excellentissimum D. CURTIUM tui uti video meique amantissimum.</p><p>Iamque uterque vestrum bene valete.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>I was indeed very glad, my excellent Thaddaeus, that from your letter — delivered to me these very days — I gathered that you still enjoy moderately good health, for your age. For I had feared that those most grievous illnesses, which (as you signified in your last) had long and sorely afflicted you, had grown still fiercer and were keeping you confined to your bed, too much an invalid, or that perhaps in the meantime some human accident had befallen you — especially since, to my letter sent to you last year by a certain citizen of Görlitz, you made no reply in so long a time. And so even now I am much in doubt whether you received that letter rightly, since in this present one you make no mention either of its contents or of our first volume of the <i>Astronomical Preliminaries</i> [<i>Progymnasmata Astronomica</i>], which I sent for the Magnificent Master Jacob Curtius and yourself together to inspect and judge. Indeed I have now twice had a copy of that same book conveyed to you — the later one, however, more richly enlarged, and in which very few things were lacking that would keep it from being made public. But I cannot wonder enough that in none of the letters sent to me in the interim do you make mention of this matter; wherefore I suspect that these books have come into other men's hands and are being detained elsewhere. That I should indeed take amiss. That astronomical studies should fall silent with you in this old age, and that you have neglected the chemical [studies] for their vanity, I judge to be rightly done; for to indulge too much in these is, for the most part, to waste oil and labour, while the affliction of your health in this advancing age does not permit the deeper handling of those [astronomical] matters; you will do best, therefore, if you are intent solely upon contemplating supramundane things — as you say you also do — and upon caring for your health. That last Imperial privilege, enlarged in a certain part, Doctor Sagerus of Lübeck sent to me some while ago; for which, in my last letter, I rendered earnest thanks both to the most worthy Master Curtius and to you, as I do now also.</p><p>That a certain comet shone forth in July and August of last year is indeed certain from the report of many; but we did not catch sight of it here at Uraniborg — though attending to the heavens perhaps more diligently than others — both because the clear weather was exceedingly rare here in those times, and because toward the North, whenever the sky was at intervals cloudless, frequent chasms [aurorae] were seen, which easily withdrew from view the comet lingering there — being of itself, as I hear, very faint. Throughout those same months, on every clear night (which, as I said, were not frequent), we kept watch chiefly on observations of the star Mars as it was tending toward its acronychal position, that we might settle from the sky itself the discrepancy of the Alfonsine and the Copernican calculation — which at the very opposition exceeded 9 degrees (a greater interval than ever before) — and that I might join this acronychal observation of Mars to the rest taken over very many preceding years. Mars was found (let me note this too here in passing), when it stood diametrically opposite the Sun, to exceed the numbers of Copernicus by 4½ degrees, but to outrun the Alfonsine ones by full five degrees; which difference, greater than at other times in Mars when it is visible all night long, came about on account of the nearness of the eccentric perigee (to speak in the common way), for there the error in the true place of the apogee, which in Copernicus amounts to five degrees, proves very fertile [of error] — to say nothing now of the eccentricity, which he, wishing to support his own hypotheses, contracted too much, nor of the other things that are required here. Meanwhile, however (to return to the matter), while we were diligently pressing on with the observation of Mars whenever clear nights allowed it, we were nowhere able to see that comet, of which the rumour had now grown, although we deliberately inspected that quarter of the sky rather often around the end of August; just as others too, who passed several nights watchful in nearby Copenhagen to inspect it, were frustrated of their wish. Nonetheless it has been written to me that a certain Danish student, Christen Hansen of Ribe by name, who before he withdrew into Germany had for some years served me here in astronomical exercises, having made one or another little instrument in imitation of mine, applied himself to the observation of it with what accuracy he could — yet noted nothing certain except twice, on account of the faintness of the comet and the scarcity of clear weather. Whatever it may have been, he will shortly bring it with him, coming here to me from his fatherland Cimbria [Jutland] (for he has lately returned to Denmark). Then, if I see it to be worth the trouble, I shall examine those observations and shall try whether parallaxes may be investigated from them, so that in this way I may in some measure repair what was, against our will, neglected, and join this comet to those five [comets] noted by me from the sky itself with the greatest diligence and described in the second volume of the <i>Astronomical Preliminaries</i> — borrowing the observations of my one-time pupil and using them in place of my own.</p><p>What that distinguished Peucer signified to you by letter about this hairy [star / comet] contributes almost nothing to the matter, for he has no observations that would yield its apparent place, far less such as would suffice for unravelling parallaxes; indeed he does not even note the right day on which he saw it, nor do those [statements] agree among themselves, since at the beginning he says he saw it at least twice in the asterism of the Great Bear, and afterward, near the end, he adds that it stood not far from the star of the She-Goat and the Kids; for these two asterisms are more than a twelfth part of the heaven apart from each other. Of what sort, then, I ask, can this observation have been, sought even from mere sight alone? Therefore, as those things were idle and unworthy of mention, so let them now be too; let us leave them.</p><p>I also rejoice and congratulate that most excellent Master Curtius, our common friend, that he has obtained a sextant instrument from the Landgrave's workshop, by which he may infallibly probe the intervals of the stars. It is without doubt made in imitation of one of mine, by which distances are measured by a single observer — albeit with somewhat greater trouble than other sextants, which require two sighters. If it is so, it has little slits beside the centre, parallel to the forward sights, and the division by transversal points that we use; then too a support, by whose aid it may be conveniently directed in every plane toward any stars whatever: and indeed these features Wittich devised beforehand, having seen [them] with me, for the Landgrave. But the Prince himself, in place of the screw which I use in loosening or tightening the angle of the instrument (which Wittich had neglected to point out), added a little chain attached to small wheels. Master Curtius will be able to have a trial made, whether by means of this novel sextant of his exactly the same distances of certain fixed stars are found as those set out on page 30 and the following, in that fragment of the <i>Astronomical Letters</i> written to the Landgrave (which I shared with you some while ago), designated by our sextants from the sky itself; for I know those to be exact, and if the findings of the Curtian sextant correspond to them to the line, then it cannot but be that his is perfectly wrought, since every truth is consonant with its own truth. But I could wish that he also had at hand a quadrant of just size and made of solid metal, likewise divided and furnished with sights, by which it would be convenient to investigate exactly, through the meridian altitudes of the Sun and stars, the declinations together with the elevation of the Pole — although this too may be done with the same sextant, when it is set to the balance of the horizon within an elevation of sixty degrees, and to do that very thing in the direct plane of the meridian is not very impracticable; about which matter you can advise him.</p><p>It is moreover pleasing to me that he is having a great globe, six feet in diameter, built; but let him diligently take care lest in procuring its roundness on every side there lie more difficulty than he would suppose — especially that it be lasting and constant, [being made] in wooden material which, however variously put together, is yet liable to the change of the air and by its own nature to instability. I should therefore have judged it more advisable that, having brought that globe to an accurate roundness so far as can be done, he should first let it experience the heat of at least one summer and the cold of [one] winter, with the varied change of air coming between them, until it is brought to some settled state; and then examine its globosity afresh, and where there is need have it corrected; thereafter, when he perceives that it sensibly slackens nothing and takes on no variation, let him have it overlaid with sheets of brass, smoothed and finely joined, and only then provide that it be reduced on every part to an exquisite roundness, and that its surface, once polished, be worked with subdivisions of the degrees into single minutes by transversal points, affording convenience; for otherwise he will scarcely mark out upon the globe, with the fineness and correspondence that will be needed, our stars — which, about eight hundred, we have catalogued in chapter 2 of the first volume, derived from the sky itself most diligently as to length and breadth, to the very minute and so sometimes to the half of one. I do not speak of things unknown [to me], for that brass globe of ours too, which equals six feet in diameter, set before [me] a laborious difficulty of several years before it was brought to perfection of every kind — to say nothing of the costs, which, were they to be reckoned item by item, would perhaps equal or surpass the outlay of a thousand joachimsthalers.</p><p>That the oft-named Master Curtius so kindly and honourably invites me to the Imperial Diet, which will shortly be held at Regensburg, is reason for me dutifully to render him thanks; and if my affairs and the manifold business with which I am occupied allowed it, I would indeed not [decline] his kind request. But since to me on this island — though not very large, yet [I being] stretched by the greatest occupations — there scarcely remains so much leisure and time that I may sometimes make an excursion to the neighbouring shores of Denmark to my kinsmen and friends, or even for the sake of my own affairs; if, then, I cannot undertake so long a journey into Germany with the loss and expense of the matters which I here handle and press forward, Master Curtius will hold me, by the humanity innate in him and the dexterity of his judgement, as I hope, sufficiently excused. I would indeed have written to him myself now, if only there were so much opportunity left, and the messenger hastening into nearby Germany were not hurrying his departure so much. But what has now been left undone in this respect, I shall not forbear amply to make up another time, when a more convenient occasion is offered. Return the greeting, therefore, in my name to the Most Excellent Master Curtius, most loving — as I see — of both you and me.</p><p>And now fare you both well.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Hagecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician, astronomer and alchemist at Prague, and a central figure in the European network that disputed the parallax of the 1572 nova; Tycho's long-standing correspondent and his principal channel to the imperial court of Rudolf II. The letter survives only as Tycho's own draft (<i>Kladde</i>) in the Vienna codex.</li><li><b>Date.</b> The source carries no internal dateline; the catalogue assigns it to <b>1594</b> (slug `…15940000001`), consistent with the internal content — Mars's opposition observed at Uraniborg and the \"comet of last year, July–August,\" i.e. the comet of <b>1593</b> (C/1593 O1). Header dated \"1594 [O.S.]\" on that catalogue basis, with the year <b>not</b> asserted from any dateline the text itself supplies; the absence of a written dateline is itself noted here. Denmark was on the Julian calendar (O.S.) throughout.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Uraniburgi</i> — <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's observatory on the island of Hven; the catalogue place-label \"Uraniborg\" matches the internal <i>hîc Uraniburgi</i> (\"here at Uraniborg\").</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>IACOBUS / D. CURTIUS</i> = <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), imperial vice-chancellor under Rudolf II, an amateur of astronomy who had commissioned a sextant from the Hessen workshop and a six-foot globe; Tycho's dedicatee-in-prospect and patron at court. The recurring \"common friend.\"</li><li><i>cl. ille PEUCERUS</i> = <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (1525–1602), Melanchthon's son-in-law, physician and astronomer; here cited (and dismissed) for unreliable naked-eye notes on the 1593 comet.</li><li><i>Doct. SAGERUS Lubecensis</i> = <b>Doctor Sagerus of Lübeck</b> (Caspar/Kaspar Sager), the intermediary who forwarded the renewed imperial printing privilege; identification by name and city as given.</li><li><i>VITICHIUS</i> = <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586) of Wrocław, the mathematician whose instrument designs Tycho says were realised at Kassel; Tycho repeatedly claimed (as here) that the Hessen sextant derived from his own instruments by way of Wittich's intermediary visit — a sensitive priority point in the Brahe–Wittich–Kassel triangle.</li><li><i>CHRISTIERNUS IOHANNIS RIPENSIS</i> = <b>Christen Hansen (Christiernus Iohannis) of Ribe</b>, a former Danish assistant of Tycho's who attempted to observe the 1593 comet; \"of Ribe\" = the Jutland cathedral town. (<i>e Cimbria Patria sua</i> names <b>Jutland/Cimbria</b> as his homeland.)</li><li><b>Places.</b></li><li><i>Gorlicensem</i> — a citizen <b>of Görlitz</b> (Lusatia), the courier of the previous year's lost-or-delayed letter.</li><li><i>Haffnia</i> — <b>Copenhagen</b> (per §5.7).</li><li><i>officina Lantgraviana</i> / <i>Lantgravio</i> — the <b>Landgrave's workshop</b> and <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (d. 1592); the Kassel observatory's instrument shop.</li><li><i>Ratisbonae</i> — <b>Regensburg</b>, site of the forthcoming Imperial Diet (<i>comitia Imperialia</i>; the Diet of 1594).</li><li><i>Cimbria</i> — <b>Jutland</b> (the Cimbrian peninsula).</li><li><b>Works.</b> <i>Progymnasmata Astronomica</i> (Tomus primus / secundus) = Tycho's <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> — rendered <b>\"Astronomical Preliminaries\"</b>; vol. 1 (the nova/refraction/solar theory) was in circulating proof, vol. 2 was to treat the comets. <i>fragmentum Epistolarum Astronomicarum ad Lantgravium</i> = the printed gathering of the <i>Epistolae Astronomicae</i> (Liber I, the Hessen correspondence) Tycho had already shared with Hagecius; \"page 30 and the following\" cites that fragment.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical terms</b> (per §5.1–5.2).</li><li><i>acronychium situm / acronychiam … observationem</i> — <b>acronychal</b> position/observation, i.e. Mars at opposition, rising as the Sun sets; kept as the technical term.</li><li><i>oppositio</i> → <b>opposition</b>; <i>Soli e diametro opponebatur</i> → \"stood diametrically opposite the Sun\" (the same configuration).</li><li><i>calculus Alphonsinus / Copernicus</i> → the <b>Alfonsine</b> and <b>Copernican</b> computation/tables; Tycho reports the sky falling 4½° from Copernicus and 5° from the Alfonsine numbers — <i>the figures are reproduced exactly as written</i> (9°, 4½°, 5°), with no silent reconciliation of the \"9 degrees\" total against the two component errors.</li><li><i>Perigaei eccentrici / vero Apogaei loco / eccentricitate</i> → <b>eccentric perigee / true place of the apogee / eccentricity</b> (§5.1); <i>hypothesibus</i> → hypotheses. Tycho's epistemic hedge <i>(ut communiter loquar)</i> \"to speak in the common way\" is preserved.</li><li><i>Parallaxes</i> → <b>parallaxes</b> (§5.1) — the distance-test for whether the comet was sub- or supralunar.</li><li><i>chasmata crebra</i> → \"frequent chasms\": <b>aurorae / auroral rifts</b> (\"chasmata\" is the period term for auroral phenomena toward the North); glossed [aurorae] rather than silently modernised.</li><li><i>Crinita</i> → \"the hairy [star],\" a standard Latin epithet for a comet (<i>stella crinita</i>); kept as the image and glossed.</li><li><i>Asterismus Ursae maioris</i> → \"the asterism of the <b>Great Bear</b>\"; <i>Capellae et Haedorum sidere</i> → \"the star of the <b>She-Goat and the Kids</b>\" (Capella and the Haedi, in Auriga). <i>duodecima coeli pars</i> → \"a twelfth part of the heaven\" (= 30°), left as written.</li><li><i>Poli sublimitas</i> → \"the <b>elevation of the Pole</b>\" (polar altitude = latitude); <i>altitudines meridianas … declinationes</i> → <b>meridian altitudes … declinations</b> (§5.1).</li><li><b>Instruments</b> (per §5.2).</li><li><i>Sextans / Sextantibus</i> → <b>sextant(s)</b>; <i>Quadrans</i> → <b>quadrant</b>; <i>Globus … orichalcicus / sexpedalis</i> → a six-foot <b>globe</b> sheathed in <i>orichalcum</i> (<b>brass / latten</b>).</li><li><i>pinnacidia / pinnula</i> → <b>sights / pinnule</b> (§5.2); <i>collimatores</i> → \"sighters\" (the two observers a two-man sextant needs); <i>rimulae</i> → \"little slits\" (sighting slits); <i>divisio per puncta transversalia</i> → <b>transversal-point division</b> (Tycho's diagonal-dot scale subdivision).</li><li><i>cochlea</i> → \"screw\"; <i>catenula rotulis inhaerens</i> → \"a little chain attached to small wheels\" (the Landgrave's chain-and-pulley fine-adjustment substituted for Tycho's screw); <i>fulcrum</i> → \"support\" (the instrument's mount/stand).</li><li><i>ad amussim</i> → \"to the line\" (exactly, by rule); <i>ad aequilibrium Horizontis</i> → \"to the balance of the horizon\" (levelled).</li><li><b>Symbol.</b> <b>♂</b> (in <i>maior … in ♂</i>) = <b>Mars</b>; the glyph is retained in the Source block and named \"Mars\" in the translation (§5.3). Mars is elsewhere written out (<i>Martis</i>) in the same passage.</li><li><b>Money.</b> <i>mille Ioachimicorum</i> → \"a thousand <b>joachimsthalers</b>\" (<i>Joachimsthaler</i> / thaler, the Bohemian Joachimsthal silver coin); kept as the period unit.</li><li><b>Chemistry / programme.</b> <i>Chymica ob vanitatem neglexeris</i> → \"you have neglected the <b>chemical</b> [studies] for their vanity\"; <i>supramundanis … contemplandis</i> → \"contemplating <b>supramundane</b> things\" (matters above the world — devotional/theological, as opposed to alchemy). <i>oleum et operam perdere</i> → \"to waste oil and labour,\" the proverbial tag, kept as idiom.</li><li><b>Editorial insertion in the source.</b> <i>eius benevolae petitioni equidem non [renuntiarem]</i> — the bracketed verb <b>[renuntiarem]</b> (\"[I would] not decline/renounce\") is an editorial supply in the source (the draft left it incomplete); the brackets are reproduced as printed and carried into the translation as <b>\"[decline].\"</b> The negation <i>non</i> + the supplied verb yields \"I would not decline,\" i.e. Tycho would accept were he free — the one negation in the clause is preserved.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae</b> (§5.5). Salutation header <i>AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM</i> kept as title-line. Closing <i>Iamque uterque vestrum bene valete</i> → \"And now fare you both well\" (plural, addressed to Hagecius and Curtius jointly — <i>uterque vestrum</i>); <i>Resaluta … meo nomine</i> → \"Return the greeting in my name.\" No <i>S./S.P.D.</i> opening appears (it is a draft).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical apparatus (Mars opposition, sextant features, brass-globe construction) is well-attested in Tycho's own usage. Soft points, flagged not smoothed: (1) the three numeric discrepancies (9° / 4½° / 5°) are reproduced verbatim without reconciling the stated 9° opposition-discrepancy against the two single-table errors; (2) the supplied verb <b>[renuntiarem]</b> is the source-edition's conjecture, retained in brackets; (3) <i>chasmata</i> is glossed [aurorae] on the standard reading but left as \"chasms\" in the running text. None rises to a sense-reversing crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\" then \"5-6\"). Collated the full letter, opening (\"Plurimum sane gavisus sum, optime mi Thaddaee...\") through closing (\"Iamque uterque vestrum bene valete\"), paragraph by paragraph against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.357-361. Verified every proper name (Thaddaeus/Hagecius, Iacobus Curtius, Sagerus Lubecensis, Christiernus Iohannis Ripensis, Peucerus, Vitichius/Wittich), every number (9 degrees discrepancy at opposition; 4.5 degrees vs Copernicus; 5 degrees vs Alfonsine tables; ~800 catalogued stars; chapter 2 of vol.1; page 30 of the Epistolae fragment; six-foot globe diameter; 60-degree sextant elevation; 1000 Joachimsthalers), and the bracketed conjectural supplement \"[renuntiarem]\" (Dreyer's own bracket, correctly preserved). No fabricated, omitted, or mistranslated clauses found; source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin exactly (differences are only the expected long-s/ligature normalizations). No hedges flattened.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistola ad Thaddaeum Hagecium: Primae lineae", "sh": "Cod. 10686/19", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961762"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.19. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 32, "prev": "brahe-t_15931224001", "next": "brahe-t_15950812001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15931224001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980530001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Expresses relief at Hagecius's survival, questions whether his earlier letter and two copies of the *Progymnasmata Astronomica* were received.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940000002", "d": "1594-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Thomas Craig", "ck": "Thomas Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Thomas Craig → Tycho Brahe — [s.l.], 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 12 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILI AC GENEROSO DOMINO,<br>DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHÆ. MAGNIFICE ET<br>GENEROSE DOMINE.</p><p>GRATA mihi inprimis erat illa mei mentio,<br>quam in literis a te ad Clarissimum Dominum IUNIUM Serenissimi Regis<br>consiliarium et iam apud vos legatum missis perlegeram. Nam licet mihi probe<br>conscius essem, minime  me concernere, quæ de me scripseras, tamen laudari a tali viro, in quo<br>tot generis, tot fortunæ et eruditionis ornamenta sunt, nemini non acceptum vel<br>gratum esse potest, et nisi aliqua literarum significatione id agnovissem,<br>fuissem profecto ingratissimus. Spondeo itaque Magnifice Domine, me tuo merito<br>et tui amantissimum et posthac studiosissimum futurum et officijs, quibus<br>possim, curaturum, ut vere id de me possis iudicare. Unum hoc peto, ut si quæ<br>offensionum causæ adversus fratrem meum supersint, illas mihi velis pro tua<br>humanitate condonare. Nam licet in ea qua frater opinione sim, supra cœlum Lunæ<br>nihil corruptioni obnoxium gigni posse, tamen te virum tantum, tot annorum<br>experientia confirmatum, tot Mathematicis organis instructum aliter sentire,<br>alijsque viris magnis tuam sententiam probasse, nisi multa essent, quæ tecum<br>facerent, nunquam mihi persuadebo. Sed in hoc<br>doctiorum sit iudicium. Rem ipsam mitto, scripti fraterni genus non probo.<br>Acerbius enim, quam par erat, adversus virum tantum se gessit, quem pro<br>admirabili eruditione colere debuerat. Sed in philosophicis concertationibus a<br>prima ætate versatus usu acrimoniam disputandi sibi comparavit. Scriptum illud<br>nunquam exijt, et scio me apud fratrem posse ut perpetuo supprimatur, vel si<br>malis cum honorifica tui nominis et eruditionis mentione, et omni felle purgatum<br>exeat; immo ut cum HORATIO loquar, sive<br>flammis mandare velis, sive mari Adriano, utrumvis pro tuo arbitrio expectabis.<br>Interea me tui amantissimum et in eo nulli concedentem in futurum pro tuo<br>amplectere. Deus optimus maximus in omnibus rebus tuis gerendis semper adsit,<br>semper te et tua gubernet precor.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Tui semper observantissimus<br>THO.<br>CRAGIUS</p><p>Expectant nostri omnes tuum de felicissima et auspicatissima principis nostri<br>genesi iudicium.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and well-born lord, the Lord Tycho Brahe. Magnificent and well-born Lord.</b></p><p>Especially welcome to me was that mention of myself which I read through in the letter sent by you to the most distinguished Master Young, councillor of the Most Serene King and now legate among you. For although I was well aware in my own conscience that what you had written about me concerned me but little, yet to be praised by such a man — in whom there are so many ornaments of birth, of fortune, and of learning — cannot but be acceptable or pleasing to anyone; and had I not acknowledged it by some token of a letter, I should truly have been most ungrateful. I pledge therefore, Magnificent Lord, that by your desert I shall both be most devoted to you and hereafter most zealous [for you], and shall see to it, by such services as I can, that you may judge this of me truly. This one thing I ask: that, if any grounds of offence against my brother yet remain, you would be willing of your kindness to forgive me [them]. For although I am of the same opinion as my brother — that above the heaven of the Moon nothing subject to corruption can be brought forth — yet that you, so great a man, confirmed by the experience of so many years, equipped with so many mathematical instruments, should think otherwise, and that you should have proved your view to other great men, I shall never persuade myself [of this], unless there were many things that made on your side. But on this point let the judgement be the more learned men's. The matter itself I set aside; the manner of my brother's writing I do not approve. For he bore himself more sharply than was fitting against so great a man, whom for his admirable learning he ought to have honoured. But, engaged from his earliest years in philosophical contests, he has by habit acquired for himself a keenness in disputing. That writing has never come out, and I know that I have it in my power with my brother that it be suppressed for ever — or, if you should prefer, that it come out with honourable mention of your name and learning, and purged of all gall; nay, to speak with Horace, whether you would wish to consign it to the flames or to the Adriatic sea, you shall await either one as you please. Meanwhile embrace me, as your own, as most devoted to you and yielding to none in this in time to come. May God, Best and Greatest, ever be at hand in the conduct of all your affairs; ever, I pray, may He govern you and yours.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Ever most observant of you,<br>Thomas Craig. All our people await your judgement upon the most happy and most auspicious nativity of our prince.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Tho. Cragius, Doctor Medicus</i> = <b>Thomas Craig</b> (Cragius), the Scotsman writing to excuse his brother. The signature gives \"Doctor of Medicine\"; the family is the Craig family of Edinburgh. The letter is the conciliatory counterpart to the bitter cometary dispute (1589–1594) between Tycho and the writer's brother.</li><li><b><i>frater meus</b></i> — \"my brother\": <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), the Edinburgh physician and Aristotelian who, between 1589 and 1594, conducted a sharp epistolary controversy with Tycho, disputing Tycho's supralunary (superlunary) placement of the comet of 1577. Tycho circulated copies of the Craig exchange to correspondents such as Brucaeus and Hagecius. This letter is Thomas Craig's distancing of himself from the <i>manner</i> (not entirely the matter) of his brother's polemic.</li><li><b><i>Dominum IUNIUM … Serenissimi Regis consiliarium et iam apud vos legatum</b></i> — \"Master Young … councillor of the Most Serene King and now legate among you.\" <i>Iunius</i> is the Latinised form of the Scots surname <b>Young</b>: <b>Peter Young</b> (Petrus Junius, 1544–1628), the royal tutor and councillor of <b>James VI</b> of Scotland, who served as the king's ambassador (legate) to Denmark in 1594. <i>iam apud vos legatum</i> (\"now legate among you [pl.]\") locates Young in Denmark at the time of writing — the channel through which Tycho's favourable mention of Thomas Craig reached him. <i>Serenissimi Regis</i> = James VI of Scotland (the \"Most Serene King\").</li><li><b>Astronomical doctrine.</b> <i>supra coelum Lunae nihil corruptioni obnoxium gigni posse</i> = \"that above the heaven of the Moon nothing subject to corruption can be brought forth\" — the Aristotelian doctrine of the incorruptible heavens, the very position from which John Craig denied that the 1577 comet could be a supralunary (celestial) body. Thomas Craig states he <i>shares</i> his brother's Aristotelian opinion, while declining to set his own judgement against Tycho's instrument-based demonstration.</li><li><b><i>tot Mathematicis organis instructum</b></i> = \"equipped with so many mathematical instruments\" (<i>organa</i> → <b>instruments</b>, protocol §5.2): a deferential nod to Tycho's observational apparatus at Uraniborg as the ground of his authority.</li><li><b>Allusion (Horace).</b> <i>ut cum Horatio loquar, sive flammis mandare velis, sive mari Adriano</i> = \"to speak with Horace, whether you would wish to consign [it] to the flames or to the Adriatic sea.\" An echo of Horace, <i>Odes</i> I.16 (and the topos of <i>Ars Poetica</i> on suppressing one's writings): Thomas Craig offers Tycho absolute discretion over the unpublished tract — burn it, drown it, or let it appear emended. The metaphor is kept, not flattened.</li><li><b><i>Rem ipsam mitto</b></i> — \"the matter itself I set aside / I let go\": <i>mitto</i> here in the rhetorical sense \"I pass over, I waive,\" i.e. Craig does not contest the substance of the dispute, only the tone. Rendered \"I set aside\" to preserve the deliberate reservation (he does not concede the substance).</li><li><b>Dating.</b> The dataset `label` is <i>\"1594. empty\"</i> — i.e. assigned to the year 1594 with <b>no day or month</b> in the source. The letter carries <b>no dateline and no place</b> in the transcription: the subscription ends at the signature. The header therefore gives \"[s.l.], 1594\" (year only, no place stated) rather than asserting a day the source does not substantiate. The year is consistent with Peter Young's 1594 Danish embassy and with the close of the Tycho–Craig controversy; it is editorial (Dreyer's), not from an internal dateline. O.S. is assumed (Scotland and Denmark both kept the Julian calendar in 1594), but the letter states no style.</li><li><b>Honorifics.</b> <i>Magnifice et Generose Domine</i> → \"Magnificent and well-born Lord\"; <i>S.</i> (implied salutation idiom) and the closing <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Tui semper observantissimus</i> → \"Ever most observant of you\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The source carries the conciliatory negations exactly: <i>minime me concernere</i> (\"concerned me but little\"), <i>nunquam mihi persuadebo</i> (\"I shall never persuade myself\"), <i>scripti fraterni genus non probo</i> (\"the manner … I do not approve\"), <i>Scriptum illud nunquam exijt</i> (\"that writing has never come out\"). All four are reproduced; none added or dropped. The double construction <i>nunquam mihi persuadebo … nisi multa essent, quae tecum facerent</i> is rendered with its conditional intact (\"I shall never persuade myself … unless there were many things that made on your side\"), preserving Craig's guarded acknowledgement that Tycho must have strong grounds.</li><li><b>Names the occasion: the young Prince's nativity.</b> The recovered closing sentence asks Tycho for his astrological judgement on 'the most happy and most auspicious nativity of our prince' — i.e. a horoscope for the newborn heir of Scotland.</li><li><b>Cross-reference to the companion letter.</b> The same request is pursued in far greater detail in John Craig's letter of 22 April [1594] (`brahe-t_15940422001`), which reports the birth of 'a most longed-for infant Prince' — Prince Henry Frederick, Duke of Rothesay, born to James VI and Anne of Denmark at Stirling Castle on 19 February 1594 (Old Style) — and supplies the birth-time and latitude Tycho would need to cast the nativity. Read together, the two Craig letters (brothers, both signing 'Doctor Medicus') show the Scottish circle soliciting Tycho's genethliacal judgement on the new heir in tandem.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin and English: the title 'Doctor Medicus' / 'Doctor of Medicine' removed from Thomas Craig's signature — Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 365 closes the signature at 'THO. CRAGIUS.' with no title; the title belongs to the preceding, separate letter of his brother John Craig ('IOAN. CRAIGIUS, Doctor Medicus.', p.364) and had contaminated this letter's signature in error.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the principal identifications (John Craig as <i>frater</i>, Peter Young as <i>Iunius</i>, the supralunary-comet doctrine) are corroborated by the corpus and external scholarship. The single soft point is the precise force of <i>nisi multa essent, quae tecum facerent</i> in the long <i>nunquam … persuadebo</i> sentence: Craig concedes that Tycho must have weighty reasons without himself abandoning the Aristotelian position — a deliberately hedged formulation kept hedged rather than smoothed to either a concession or a refusal. The date is editorial (year only; no internal dateline), flagged above.</p><p>High confidence. The Latin is clean and grammatically unambiguous, and its occasion is independently fixed by the companion letter from John Craig three weeks later, which describes the same event (the birth of Prince Henry) in much greater and consistent detail. 'Genesis' is rendered 'nativity' to match the house convention already used elsewhere in this correspondence (e.g. 'Geneseos schema' rendered 'nativity-figure' in the 1588 Peucer letter).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the Peter Young/consiliarium opening, the brother-John-Craig comet/superlunary-corruption passage, the Horace flames-or-Adriatic clause, the closing nativity postscript) against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.364-365 as page images. Body text is a word-for-word match with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation in the letter proper — including the knotty 'nunquam mihi persuadebo, nisi multa essent quae tecum facerent' clause, which is faithfully and correctly rendered. The signature block, however, carries a false professional title not present in Dreyer, evidently bled over from the adjacent John Craig letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's signature block for this letter reads only 'THO. CRAGIUS.' with no title. Our source_text has appended 'Doctor Medicus', which is not in Dreyer here — it appears to be contamination from the immediately preceding, separate letter of John Craig on the same page spread, which genuinely closes 'IOAN. CRAIGIUS, Doctor Medicus.' Thomas Craig of Riccarton was a jurist/advocate, not a physician (his brother John Craig was the royal physician), so the English's 'Doctor of Medicine' misattributes a professional identity to the wrong Craig. Dreyer prints: <i>Tui semper observantissimus THO. CRAGIUS. [no title follows]</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VII pp.363-366 page images, comparing this letter's closing (p.365, 'Tui femper obfervantißimus THO. CRAGIUS.') against the immediately preceding letter's closing (p.364, 'Tui obfervantiß. IOAN. CRAIGIUS, Doctor Medicus.'). The contamination is unambiguous. Both Latin and English corrected as instructed for a TEXT_ERROR that misled the translation.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Thomas Craig", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15940726001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15940726001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for favorable mention in a letter to Young, pledges devotion, and asks that any remaining offence from his brother be forgiven.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940307001", "d": "1594-03-07", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kolding", "t": "Christian IV → [privilege granted to Tycho Brahe] — Kolding, 7 March 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (royal chancery diploma) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(here<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV,<i> Ad vitam Tychonis</i>, p. 67) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Latina 1586–99, fol. 289 f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>PRIUILEGIUM TYCHONI BRAHAe CONCESSUM COLDINGAe VII MARTIJ ANNO 1594.</p><p>VNIUERSIS et singulis tenore praesentium notum facimus: Cum generosus ac fidelis nobis dilectus Tycho Brahae de Knudtstrup ex antiqua et praeclara huius regni nostri nobili Brahorum familia originem ducens post varios diuturnosque in studio Astronomico, cui inde ab ineunte aetate fuit addictissimus, exantlatos labores fructus eorundem nonnullos haud asspernendos, nunc in communem reipublicae literariae vtilitatem in publicum edere decreuerit: Astronomicae videlicet instaurationis progymnasmata quaedam luculenta, integro quod adornat operi praemittenda, ac vereri se nobis demisse ostenderit ne idem opus, postquam publicatum fuerit, alij temere non exiguo ipsius detrimento aemulentur, et propterea nobis quam humilime supplicauerit, vt pro solita nostra clementia indemnem ipsum praestemus. Visum est nobis pro fauore nostro erga eum singulari hoc illi diplomate nostro regio prouidere, quo serio ac seuere mandamus cautumque esse volumus, ne qui omnino in regnis ducatibus atque prouincijs nostris cuiuscunque status aut conditionis existant ullos libros ab ipso Tychone Brahae siue proprijs siue alienis typis editos, praesertim eos, qui rem Philosophicam atque Astronomicam concernunt, vel recudere vel alibi impressos aut recusos adducere vel uenundare, siue totaliter siue partialiter, sine ipsius haeredumue eius consensu, publice uel occulte praesumant, sub paena decem marcarum auri puri pro dimidia fisco nostro regio, reliqua uero parte ipsi Tychoni Brahae siue eius haeredibus (praeter librorum omnium amissionem, quos ipsis uel per se uel suos sibi vendicare, inque potestatem suam redigere fas esto) nulla sperata remissione applicandarum. In cuius rei ac voluntatis nostrae fidem et testimonium hoc diploma regio nostro sigillo munitum, manu propria subscripsimus ac a regni nostri senatoribus nunc praesentibus subscribendum mandauimus.</p><p>Actum in regia nostra Coldingae.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A privilege granted to Tycho Brahe at Kolding, 7 March in the year 1594.</b></p><p>To each and all we make known by the tenor of these presents: Whereas the well-born Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, faithful and beloved of us, who draws his origin from the ancient and illustrious noble family of the Brahes of this our realm, after various and long-continued labours exhausted in the study of astronomy — to which from his earliest years he has been most devoted — has now resolved to publish, for the common benefit of the republic of letters, certain not-to-be-despised fruits of those same labours: namely certain brilliant <i>preliminary exercises</i> [progymnasmata] of the <i>restoration of astronomy</i> [astronomicae instaurationis], to be set before the whole work that he is preparing; and whereas he has humbly shown us that he fears lest, after that work has been published, others should rashly emulate [it] to his no slight detriment, and has therefore most humbly besought us that, of our accustomed clemency, we keep him from harm: it has seemed good to us, in our singular favour toward him, to provide for him by this our royal diploma, whereby we earnestly and strictly command, and will it to be enacted, that none whatsoever within our kingdoms, duchies, and provinces, of whatever estate or condition they be, shall presume — publicly or covertly — either to reprint any books published by Tycho Brahe himself, whether from his own presses or from others', and especially those that concern philosophical and astronomical matters, or to bring in [copies] printed or reprinted elsewhere, or to put [them] up for sale, whether wholly or in part, without the consent of him or of his heirs; under penalty of ten marks of pure gold — one half to be applied to our royal treasury, the other half to Tycho Brahe himself or to his heirs (besides the forfeiture of all the books, which they, whether by themselves or through their own [agents], shall be entitled to claim for themselves and to bring into their own power) — with no remission to be hoped for. In faith and testimony of which matter and of our will, we have subscribed this diploma, fortified with our royal seal, with our own hand, and have commanded it to be subscribed by the senators of our realm now present.</p><p>Done at our royal [residence of] Kolding.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer / document type.</b> A royal printing <b>privilege</b> (<i>privilegium</i> / <i>diploma regium</i>) issued in the name of <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), then in his minority under the regency council; the dataset names \"Christian 4.\" as sender. It is a one-party legal instrument (a grant), not an epistolary exchange — hence the \"recipient\" is properly the realm at large, with Tycho Brahe as the beneficiary; the header records this. The text is cast throughout in the royal \"we\" (<i>facimus, mandamus, subscripsimus</i>).</li><li><b>Beneficiary.</b> <i>Tycho Brahae de Knudtstrup</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup</b> (the family seat in Scania), here styled <i>generosus ac fidelis nobis dilectus</i> (\"well-born, faithful and beloved of us\"), the standard chancery honorific for a noble subject.</li><li><b>The work protected.</b> <i>Astronomicae instaurationis progymnasmata</i> = the <b>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</b> (\"preliminary exercises [toward] the restoration of astronomy\"), Tycho's great work on the new star of 1572 and on solar/lunar theory, then in press at Uraniborg and not completed until after his death (1602). <i>progymnasmata</i> (Greek προγυμνάσματα, \"preliminary exercises/drills\") is Tycho's own title-word, kept and glossed. <i>instauratio (astronomiae)</i> → \"restoration (of astronomy)\" per protocol §5.1 — Tycho's programmatic term; the genitive here (<i>instaurationis</i>) modifies the <i>progymnasmata</i>. <i>integro... operi praemittenda</i> — \"to be set before the whole work,\" i.e. these exercises form the opening volume of a larger projected corpus.</li><li><b><i>siue proprijs siue alienis typis editos</b></i> — \"published whether from his own presses or from others'.\" A pointed clause: Tycho ran his <b>own press</b> at Uraniborg (<i>proprijs typis</i>), and the privilege covers both his self-printed books and any printed for him elsewhere.</li><li><b>The penalty clause.</b> <i>sub paena decem marcarum auri puri</i> = \"under penalty of ten marks of pure gold,\" split <i>pro dimidia... reliqua uero parte</i> (half to the royal <i>fiscus</i> / treasury, half to Tycho or his heirs) — the conventional fine-sharing formula. <i>praeter librorum omnium amissionem... quos... sibi vendicare... fas esto</i> = \"besides the forfeiture of all the [offending] books, which [the heirs] shall be entitled (<i>fas esto</i>) to claim and seize.\" The parenthesis is a grant of right to Tycho's side, not a further penalty; rendered as such.</li><li><b>Negations (checked).</b> The operative prohibition carries the structure <i>ne qui... ullos libros... vel recudere vel... adducere vel uenundare... sine... consensu... praesumant</i> — one governing negation (<i>ne... praesumant</i>, \"that none presume\"), with <i>sine ipsius... consensu</i> (\"without his consent\") as the condition. Three negation-bearing elements total (<i>ne qui</i>, <i>ullos</i>, <i>sine consensu</i>); all three reproduced, none added. The force is: no one may do any of the three acts (reprint / import / sell) without consent. <i>nulla sperata remissione</i> (\"with no remission to be hoped for\") closes the clause and is preserved.</li><li><b>Reading note (transcription vs. Dreyer scan).</b> The working transcription reads <i>supplicauerit</i> (3rd-sg., agreeing with Tycho as subject of the <i>Cum...</i> clause); the Dreyer OCR shows <i>supplicauere</i>, but Dreyer's printed text is heavily mangled by long-s/ligature OCR errors in this passage (e.g. <i>Typhone</i> for <i>Tychone</i>, <i>Angulari</i> for <i>singulari</i>, <i>exibant</i> for <i>existant</i>, <i>recuses</i> for <i>recusos</i>). The singular <i>supplicauerit</i> is grammatically required by the surrounding singular verbs (<i>decreuerit... ostenderit</i>) and is followed here; the variant is noted, not silently corrected.</li><li><b>Place / dateline.</b> The date and place stand only in the <b>rubric heading</b>: <i>COLDINGAe VII MARTIJ ANNO 1594</i> = \"at <b>Kolding</b> [<i>Coldingae</i>], 7 March in the year 1594\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The body's closing <i>Actum in regia nostra Coldingae</i> (\"Done at our royal [seat at] Kolding\") repeats the place without a date. The dataset `label` (\"1594. 7. marts. Kolding\") agrees with the rubric; no discrepancy. Kolding (Koldinghus) was a principal royal residence in Jutland. The line <i>Ex Archivo Regni Daniae: Latina 1586–99, fol. 289 f.</i> in the Dreyer scan is the editor's source-note, not part of the document.</li><li><b><i>reipublicae literariae</b></i> = \"the republic of letters,\" the humanist commonwealth of learning; kept as the period idiom.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the legal sense, the single-negation prohibition, and the penalty/forfeiture split are rendered securely. The only soft point is the <i>supplicauerit</i> / <i>supplicauere</i> reading (working transcription vs. a demonstrably corrupt Dreyer OCR of this passage), flagged above; it does not affect the meaning.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation is the harmless </i>supplicauerit/-uere<i> variant, grammatically forced and meaning-neutral; legal sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.65-68 as page images. Privilege no. 100 'PRIUILEGIUM TYCHONI BRAHAe CONCESSUM COLDINGAe VIIo MARTIJ ANNO 1594' runs from the foot of p.66 to the top of p.67. source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim word for word. Collated the full legal text: the penalty sum (ten marks of pure gold, split half to the royal treasury / half to Tycho or his heirs), the place and date (Kolding, 7 March 1594), and every clause of the privilege; the English is an accurate, complete rendering with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Latina 1586-99, Fol. 289 f.."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Grants Tycho a royal privilege protecting the forthcoming *Progymnasmata Astronomica* against unauthorized reprinting.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940307001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940422001", "d": "1594-04-22", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "John Craig", "ck": "John Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Edinburgh", "t": "John Craig → Tycho Brahe — Edinburgh, 22 April [1594] [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII, 362.2–364.18) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 10 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>GENERIS NOBILITATE, OMNI<br>DOCTRINARUM ET VIRTUTUM EXCELLENTIA PRÆSTANTISSIMO VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE, HWÆNE<br>INSULÆ, DOMINO ILLUSTR. REGIS DANIÆ CONSILIARIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO<br>HONORANDO.</p><p>EXTINCTA nunc prope Nobiliss. D. TYCHO tuarum de me literarum<br>memoria, quæ in huiusmodi rebus admodum labilis esse solet, et de humanitate tua<br>ea concepta opinione, ut apud ipsum quoque offensiones deferbuisse existimem, ad<br>amicitiam redeundum videtur. Quod hoc tempore vir præstantissimus Dominus<br>IUNIUS, qui nunc legatum ad vos agit,<br>diligenter egit, qui ut utrique nostrum plurimum favet, ita animorum<br>coniunctionem, quæ inter nos fuit, minime dissolvi cupit, neque ego fore<br>speravi, ut tantum laxaretur. Si ulla mea disputatione id evenire posse,<br>suspicatus fuissem, vel mihi indixissem silentium vel dissentientem, quia aliter<br>non ratio passa est, non disserentem habuisses amicum, cum hoc amicitia nostra<br>non ferre videatur. Quod ad scriptum nostrum attinet, defensionem eius nolo<br>agere, hoc tantum profiteri libet, scriptori nec voluntatem nec propositum<br>fuisse tui vel minimum commovendi. Tacuissem libenter, et ita res meæ suadebant,<br>nam Mathematica vix unquam cum ullo commodo tractavi, sed silere non passus es,<br>assentire non potui, nisi assentari voluissem; si absque argumento sententiam<br>dixissem, non acquievisses; candide et sincere tecum agi petijsti, gratam tibi<br>fore nostram operam pollicitus es. Occurrebant in Apologia tua, quæ stomachum<br>cuivis commovere potuissent, ut imperitiæ, ignorantiæ, mancipati iudicij et<br>similium insimulari, absque causa absque ulla offensione data. Itaque voluntatem<br>tuam meis rebus cœpi præferre, et illa qualicunque disquisitione tibi<br>gratificari libuit. Amicitia me dirigente pro veritate et Uraniæ tuæ dignitate<br>quædam attuli, quæ eo acceptiora fore credideram, quo magis pro his facerent,<br>quia has Deas tanto studio et industria colis, ut nemo hoc nostro sæculo pari,<br>superioribus nemo magis; ut nostra defensione offensum te iri, ne suspicatus<br>fuerim, præsertim si absque contumelia id agerem, a qua certe ita cavi, ut ab<br>igne, neque enim Philosophum eiusmodi contentionem decere existimavi, neque ullo modo nostræ convenire amicitiæ. Nonnulla certe res ipsa<br>a me expresserat, quæ in quæstionem tantum directa, in offensionem non erant<br>rapienda. Si tua perverti, distraxi, male accommodavi et cætera, quæ nunc et<br>excidere et si hærerent non repetenda sunt, loca modo<br>designes. Quæ feci inscius, non invitus mutabo. Brevitati equidem studui, quæ<br>copiose et pluribus locis a te allata sunt, ne prolixitas tædium pareret,<br>contraxi, sensu tuo religiose observato, in nudam argumenti speciem redegi, ubi<br>dissentiendum fuit, partim serio, partim etiam per iocum ostendi; idque<br>doctissimorum et optimorum, in simili negocio, exemplo, ut inde offensum te iri<br>nequaquam crediderim, præsertim cum nihil iniuriose fieret, aut alioqui<br>cogitatum sit. Tua quoque me nemo magis admiratus est semper, illorumque iusta<br>æstimatio nunquam passa est amorem tui minui, tantum abest, ut ijs quicquam<br>voluerim detractum, quin potius indefessum tuum et vix imitabile in cœlum<br>studium in cœlum semper, ubicunque licuit, tulerim; neque enim si in re una<br>dissentimus, eaque non tanti ponderis, in cæteris similiter, sed ubi ex apodicto<br>agis, me tuis subscribentem inprimis habes. Ac in illa re, quæ a me hactenus<br>allata sunt, tuo subijcio iudicio, tuo etiam permitto arbitrio, salva veritate,<br>quam scio te salvam velle, mutes, expungas, cancelles quæ libet; privatim tecum<br>egi, nec nostra ad alios referenda fuere, qui parum illa vel faciebant, vel<br>sapiebant; mihi uno verbo si constitisset, molestius te nostra capere, quæ in<br>gratiam tuam fuere scripta, in eandem quoque abolita fuissent. Grati animi<br>officia a me requiris, nec alia a me unquam profecta sunt, eaque ex amore magis<br>quam debito, nisi communi nomine deberi possint, quod in commune Reipub.<br>literariæ incrementum a te immensa præstita sint, quo nomine patiar et me<br>teneri. Quod ad factam mei mentionem apud Regem et ad D. Cancellarium<br>attinet, in eo quoque benevolentiam tuam agnosco et amplector, sed nec petij nec<br>opus fuit: Serenissimum Regem ipse et omnes<br>mei, quamvis aulam  non sectemur, clementissimum semper experti sumus, quem Deus optimus max. conservet. D. Cancellarius, antequam ulla inter vos esset notitia, meis rebus<br>favit plurimum, et aliqua sangvinis ratio est, nec mea illi familiæ officia<br>unquam defuere. Ab illo vero tempore vix alloqui eum contigit, semel fortasse in<br>casu cuiusdam ægri. Sed ista valeant, et quæcunque alia, ex quibus amicitia<br>nostra turbari possit, ipse hoc accepto documento, ei, ut potero, studebo, nec<br>dubito quin et tu alienior non sis futurus. Si controversi  quicquam inter nos contigerit, me facile silentem habebis, a te etiam<br>atque etiam peto, ut absque iniuriosis vocibus id fiat; his non inquiritur sed<br>supprimitur veritas. Illa inter nos regula sanciatur, ut nudis solum rationibus<br>agatur, extra rem nihil proferatur. Sic disserere integra amicitia licebit.<br>Cupivit doctissimus vir D. Legatus, ut secum te<br>inviserem, non patiuntur id amici. Si venissem, gratusne fuissem hospes? Interea<br>his literis revisere te volui, et amicitiam nostram redintegrare. Tu amicum, qui<br>veram profitetur amicitiam, pristino more et amore complectere. Accepistis natum<br>nobis esse infantem Principem exoptatissimum, R. matrem vultu et corporis lineamentis refert, redivivum diceres<br>beatæ memoriæ illustriss. FRIDERICUM<br>Regem, in lucem cœpit contendere hora tertia post Μ. N. diei 19. Februarij, in<br>eam venit media ad quartam. Arx Sterlini eiusdem cum hac nostra urbe fere<br>latitudinis est, vix uno aut altero scrupulo in Boream declinat, Uraniam, si<br>placet, consule. Salutat P. T. frater<br>meus natu maior; ad te hoc tempore scripsisset, nisi Serenissimæ<br>Reginæ negotia impedijssent;<br>putat sibi maiores gratias esse redditas, quam illud epigramma meritum est;<br>gratum tamen ipsi fuit, sua tibi accepta esse, et paratus est, quibus poterit<br>officijs, tibi gratificari.</p><p>Vale præstantissime D. et clementissimus Deus tibi<br>vitam et vires suppeditet, ut illi admirando operi, quod præ manibus habes,<br>optatum finem possis imponere.</p><p>Datæ Edinburgi 22. Aprilis.</p><p>Tui observantiss.<br>IOAN.<br>CRAIGIUS,<br>Doctor Medicus.</p><p>Salutem precor doctissimis et præstantissimis viris Domm. D. D. CRAIGIJS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most pre-eminent in nobility of birth and in every excellence of learning and virtue, Master Tycho Brahe of the island of Hven, councillor to the Most Illustrious King of Denmark, his lord and friend to be honoured.</b></p><p>Now that the memory of your letters concerning me is nearly extinguished, most noble Master Tycho — a memory that in matters of this kind is wont to be quite fleeting — and having formed of your kindness the opinion that with you too the offences have cooled, it seems a return must be made to friendship. This at the present time the most pre-eminent man Master Junius, who is now acting as envoy to you, has diligently brought about; for as he greatly favours us both, so he by no means wishes the union of minds that there was between us to be dissolved — nor did I hope that it would be so far loosened. Had I suspected that it could come about through any disputation of mine, I should either have imposed silence upon myself, or — since reason did not allow otherwise — you would have had a friend who dissented but did not dispute, since this our friendship seems not to bear. As regards my writing, I do not wish to mount a defence of it; I am content only to declare this much: that the writer had neither the will nor the intent of provoking you even in the least. I would gladly have kept silent, and so my own affairs counselled me, for I have scarcely ever handled mathematics with any profit; but you did not suffer me to be silent, and I could not assent unless I had wished to play the flatterer; had I pronounced an opinion without an argument, you would not have been satisfied; you asked that you be dealt with candidly and sincerely, [and] you promised that my service would be welcome to you. There occurred in your <i>Apologia</i> things that could have stirred anyone's gall — such as being charged with inexperience, ignorance, a judgement enslaved, and the like, without cause, without any offence given. And so I began to set your wish before my own affairs, and it pleased me to gratify you with that examination, such as it was. With friendship guiding me, on behalf of the truth and of the dignity of your Urania I brought forward certain things, which I had believed would be the more acceptable the more they made for these [Goddesses], because you cultivate these Goddesses with such zeal and industry that no one in this our age does so equally, and no one in earlier ages more; so that I never suspected you would be offended by my defence, especially if I carried it out without insult — from which indeed I guarded myself as from fire, for I judged that a contention of this kind neither befitted a philosopher nor was in any way suited to our friendship. Certain things, to be sure, the subject-matter itself had wrung from me, which, being aimed only at the inquiry, were not to be snatched up into offence. If I have distorted, pulled apart, ill-adapted your [statements] and the rest — which now both escape me and, even if they stuck [in mind], are not to be rehearsed — only point out the passages. What I did unknowingly I shall change, and not unwillingly. I did indeed aim at brevity: the things adduced by you copiously and in many places I drew together, lest prolixity breed tedium; with your sense scrupulously preserved, I reduced [them] to the bare appearance of the argument; where I had to dissent, I showed it partly in earnest, partly even in jest — and that after the example of the most learned and the best men in a like business — so that I could in no way have believed you would be offended thereby, especially since nothing was done injuriously, nor was so intended in any case. No one, too, has ever admired your [achievements] more than I, and a just esteem of them has never allowed my love of you to be lessened — so far am I from having wished anything detracted from them, that rather I have always, wherever I was permitted, borne your tireless and scarcely imitable zeal toward heaven up to heaven; for if we dissent in one matter, and that one of no great weight, it does not follow that [we do so] likewise in the rest — but where you proceed from demonstration, you have me subscribing to your views among the very first. And in that matter, the things which have hitherto been brought forward by me I submit to your judgement, I commit also to your discretion: with the truth kept safe — which I know you wish kept safe — change, strike out, cancel what you please. I dealt with you privately, and my writings were not to be carried to others, who either set little store by them or had little understanding of them; had it been established to me in a single word that you took my writings amiss — which were written for your sake — for that same sake they would also have been abolished. You ask of me the offices of a grateful mind; nor have any others ever proceeded from me, and these out of love rather than out of debt — unless they can be owed under the common title, since immense things have been furnished by you toward the common increase of the Republic of Letters, under which title I shall suffer myself too to be bound. As regards the mention made of me before the King and to Master the Chancellor, in that too I acknowledge and embrace your goodwill, but I neither asked it nor was there need: I myself and all my [house], though we do not attend the court, have always found the Most Serene King most clement — whom may God, Best and Greatest, preserve. Master the Chancellor, before there was any acquaintance between you, greatly favoured my affairs, and there is some tie of blood, nor have my services to that family ever been wanting. But since that time it has scarcely befallen me to speak with him — once perhaps, in the case of a certain sick man. But let these matters rest, and whatever others there may be from which our friendship could be disturbed; I myself, having received this lesson, will attend to it as I shall be able, and I do not doubt that you too will not be the more estranged. If anything contentious should arise between us, you will easily have me silent; [and] I beg of you again and again that it be done without injurious words: by these the truth is not investigated but suppressed. Let this rule be sanctioned between us, that we proceed by bare reasons alone, and that nothing be brought forward beside the matter [in hand]. Thus it will be permitted to dispute with friendship whole. The most learned man Master the Envoy wished that I should visit you together with him; my friends do not allow it. Had I come, should I have been a welcome guest? Meanwhile by this letter I have wished to revisit you, and to make our friendship whole again. Do you embrace, in your former manner and love, a friend who professes a true friendship. You [pl.] have received word that a most longed-for infant Prince has been born to us; he recalls his royal mother in countenance and in the lineaments of his body — you would call him King Frederick, of illustrious blessed memory, brought to life again. He began to strive into the light at the third hour after Μ. N. [midnight] on the 19th day of February; into it he came at half past three. The Castle of Stirling is of nearly the same latitude as this our city, scarcely by one scruple or two does it incline to the North — consult Urania, if you please. My elder brother greets you most warmly; he would have written to you at this time, had the affairs of the Most Serene Queen not hindered him; he thinks greater thanks have been rendered to him than that epigram deserved; yet it was pleasing to him that his [verses] were acceptable to you, and he is ready, by whatever offices he can, to gratify you.</p><p>Farewell, most pre-eminent Master, and may God most clement supply you with life and strength, that upon that admirable work which you have in hand you may be able to impose the wished-for end.<br>Given at Edinburgh, 22 April.<br>Most observant of you,<br>John Craig,<br>Doctor of Medicine. I wish greeting to the most learned and most excellent men, the Lord Doctors Craig.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Ioan. Craigius, Doctor Medicus</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (Joannes Craig, d. 1620), Scottish physician and mathematician, trained at Frankfurt(-Oder) and physician at Edinburgh, later physician to James VI/I. He is the fourth of Tycho's four acrimonious antagonists (with Ursus, Wittich, and Rothmann). This letter (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII, 362.2–364.18) is Craig's <b>complete capitulation</b> in the cometary dispute that had run since Craig's first reply to Tycho's 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (letter of May 1589), through Craig's combative <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i> of 1592 (his refutation of comets being raised into the aether) to this conciliatory peace overture; a parallel letter from his brother on his behalf followed the same year (TBOO VII, 364.20–365.16).</li><li><b><i>Hwaenae insulae … Regis Daniae consiliario</b></i> — \"of the island of <b>Hven</b> … councillor to the King of Denmark\": the address gives Tycho's seat (Uraniborg on Hven) and his royal office under Frederik II / Christian IV.</li><li><b><i>Dominus Iunius … legatum ad vos agit</b></i> / <b><i>D. Legatus</b></i> — \"Master Junius … is acting as envoy to you\" / \"Master the Envoy\": <b>Peter Young</b> (latinised <i>Iunius</i>), the Scots ambassador (royal tutor and diplomat) then on embassy to Denmark; Tycho had earlier commended Craig to him (letters of 1590 and 1593). Craig's wish to accompany the envoy on a visit to Hven was blocked by his friends.</li><li><b><i>Apologia tua</b></i> — <b>Tycho's <i>Apologia</b></i>: the polemical reply (within the cometary controversy) in which, Craig complains, he had been charged with \"inexperience, ignorance, a judgement enslaved.\" Translated as the work-title <i>Apologia</i>; <i>italicised</i> on first use.</li><li><b><i>scriptum nostrum</b></i> — \"my writing\": Craig's own contribution to the dispute (his <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i> and attendant critiques); rendered neutrally as \"writing\" since Craig declines to defend or re-title it here.</li><li><b><i>Uraniae … has Deas … Uraniam consule</b></i> — \"your Urania … these Goddesses … consult Urania\": <i>Urania</i> is the Muse of astronomy, here a figure for Tycho's astronomical work (and for astronomy/astrology generally). The closing <i>Uraniam, si placet, consule</i> (\"consult Urania, if you please\") is a playful invitation to Tycho to cast or check the new prince's nativity from the data given; \"Urania\" kept, per the protocol's <i>Uraniae</i> convention.</li><li><b><i>ex apodicto agis</b></i> — \"where you proceed from demonstration\": <i>apodicto</i> (from Greek <i>ἀπόδειξις</i>, apodictic/demonstrative proof) — Craig concedes Tycho's conclusions wherever they rest on demonstration, dissenting only on the one disputed (and, he says, minor) point. Latinised Greek technical term; kept and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Reipub. literariae</b></i> — \"the Republic of Letters\" (<i>Respublica literaria</i>), the early-modern commonwealth of scholars; expanded from the abbreviation.</li><li><b>The King and the Chancellor.</b> <i>apud Regem … ad D. Cancellarium</i> — the <b>King</b> is <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (later James I of England), styled <i>Serenissimus Rex</i>; the <b>Chancellor</b> is <b>John Maitland of Thirlestane</b>, Lord Chancellor of Scotland, to whom Tycho had also written about Craig (1591–92) and with whom Craig claims a tie of blood (<i>aliqua sangvinis ratio</i>). <i>Serenissimae Reginae</i> = <b>Anne of Denmark</b>, James's queen (and sister of Christian IV).</li><li><b><i>frater meus natu maior</b></i> — \"my elder brother\": <b>Thomas Craig</b> (of Riccarton, c. 1538–1608), the jurist and Neo-Latin poet, author of the <i>epigram</i> (verses) sent to Tycho that this passage acknowledges; he sent his own letter to Tycho the same year.</li><li><b>The royal birth.</b> <i>infantem Principem exoptatissimum … FRIDERICUM Regem … diei 19. Februarij</i> — the \"most longed-for infant Prince\" is <b>Henry Frederick, Prince of Wales</b> (b. 19 February 1593/94 O.S. at Stirling Castle, d. 1612), James VI's heir, said here to resemble his late grandfather <b>King Frederik II of Denmark</b> (\"Frederick … brought to life again\"), the dedicatee's former patron. <i>Accepistis</i> is <b>second-person plural</b> (\"you [pl.] have received word\") — Craig addresses the news to Tycho and his circle. <b>Date note:</b> the birth-date <i>19 February</i> is given inside the letter; the letter's own dateline is <i>22 April</i>. Both are <b>Old Style</b> (Scotland and Denmark kept the Julian calendar); reproduced as written, not converted. (The civil year in Scotland began 25 March, so 19 February 1594 in modern reckoning fell in the legal year 1593 — not stated in the source and not interpolated here.)</li><li><b>Birth-time crux — <i>hora tertia post Μ. N. … media ad quartam</i>.</b> The Greek abbreviation <b>Μ. N.</b> is read as <b>midnight</b> — most plausibly <i>Μεσονύκτιον</i> (Greek \"midnight\") or a Greek-lettered abbreviation of Latin <i>Media Nox</i> — giving \"began to strive into the light at the third hour after midnight … came [fully] into it at half past three [a.m.].\" <i>Media ad quartam</i> = \"the middle toward the fourth [hour]\" = <b>3:30</b>. The expansion of <b>Μ. N.</b> is editorial: the Greek glyphs are kept verbatim in the Source block, the bracketed gloss <b>[midnight]</b> marks the reading, and the precise resolution is <i>flagged, not asserted</i> — this is the named crux. (Craig gives the time precisely so that Tycho can cast the prince's geniture, hence the immediately following latitude of Stirling and the \"consult Urania\" invitation.)</li><li><b><i>Arx Sterlini … latitudinis … uno aut altero scrupulo … in Boream</b></i> — \"the Castle of <b>Stirling</b> … of [nearly the same] latitude … by one scruple or two … to the North\": <i>scrupulus</i> here = a <b>minute of arc</b> (1/60 of a degree); Craig tells Tycho that Stirling lies almost on Edinburgh's parallel, scarcely a minute or two further north — geodetic data supplied for the horoscope.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Datae Edinburgi 22. Aprilis</i> = \"Given at <b>Edinburgh</b>, 22 April\" (O.S.); the year is not written in the source dateline but is securely <b>1594</b> from the edition (TBOO VII) and from the contemporaneous royal birth (19 Feb. 1593/94), hence bracketed in the header.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Tui observantiss.</i> = \"Most observant of you\" (a courtesy superlative parallel to the <i>E. Tuae studiosiss.</i> of the formula list, §5.5); <i>Doctor Medicus</i> = \"Doctor of Medicine.\"</li><li><b>A crux, not a clean continuation.</b> The recovered line is a standard epistolary salutation formula ('Salutem precor...' — 'I wish greeting to...') addressed, in the dative plural, to 'the Lord Doctors Craig' (Domini Doctores Craigii) — i.e. both brothers collectively, John and Thomas Craig. A letter signed by John Craig alone closing by greeting 'the Doctors Craig' in the plural, apparently including himself, does not read as a natural authorial sign-off.</li><li><b>Likely explanation, left unresolved.</b> This has the shape of an <i>opening</i> salutation, not a closing one — plausibly the superscription of an adjoining letter (perhaps from Tycho, or a third party, to both Craig brothers jointly) that has been run on to the end of John Craig's letter in the source transcription. Flagged here rather than resolved; per instructions, no web search was made to chase it further.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin prose is rendered securely throughout; the one genuinely open point, flagged rather than smoothed, is the <b>birth-time abbreviation Μ. N.</b> (resolved as \"midnight,\" but the exact Greek/Latin expansion is editorial). The year <i>1594</i> is editorially supplied to the dateline (bracketed), and the second-person plural of <i>Accepistis</i> is preserved.</p><p>Low-to-medium confidence on placement and authorship, though the Latin itself is grammatically unambiguous and the word-for-word translation is not in doubt. The difficulty is contextual: a plural dative salutation naming 'the Lord Doctors Craig' sits oddly as the close of a letter written and signed by one of those same Craigs. This may be a manuscript- or edition-level splice between two adjacent items rather than a genuine part of John Craig's own letter; treat the attachment of this sentence to this letter, not the wording, as the uncertain element.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the entire letter clause-by-clause against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.362-364, including the salutation heading, every proper name (Junius the legate, the unnamed Chancellor, King Frederick, Stirling Castle, Craig's elder brother, the closing address to the 'Domini Craigii'), the birth-hour detail for the infant Prince (third hour after midnight on 19 February, delivered at half past three), and the date and signature (22 April, John Craig, Doctor of Medicine). Latin source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly throughout, word for word — no TEXT_ERROR. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found; the English renders the letter's argument (Craig's defence of his critical Apologia commentary, the peace overture, the royal-birth news) faithfully. Note for the record: this letter's published_grade was self-assigned C, but full page-image collation against Dreyer found no defect to support that grade.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "John Craig", "count": 9, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15920805001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15911114001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Seeks to renew friendship after the cooling caused by a previous disputation, crediting Master Junius's mediation with reopening contact.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940422001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940608001", "d": "1594-06-08", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 8 June 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Jyske Tegnelser IV, 469 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TILL THYGE BRAHE, EN WED NAFFN CHRISTOFFER PEPELER ANRØRENDIS.</p><p>Christianus 4.</p><p>WOR gunst tilforne. Wiid, effter som Nelle Christoffer Pepelers hustru wnderdanigstt till Oß haffuer supplicerit och tilkiende giffuett, huorledis effter att hendis hoßbunde, for:ne Christoffer Pepeler, haffuer werritt thin fougitt oc du derfore vdj nogenn sager, som hannom och thig skall haffue for samme hans thieniste emellom werritt, ladett hans godtz vdj Wor kiøpstedt Bergenn wdj Wortt riige Norge arestêre oc beslaa, oc sidenn effter din forthrøstning oc for:ne hans hustruis schriffuelße oc begiering haffuer sig begiffuit till Wor kiøpstedt Helsingøer, saa hand thin thiener ther sammestedtz paa thine wegne for for:ne sag haffuer satt nøyachtig borgenn, huilckett thu wanseett dog skall hannom ther effter ladett fenckligenn thage oc hensette paa Hueen, effter som hendis indlagde supplication ydermere formelder oc wduiiser, tha bede Wij thig oc Wille, att naar thu her met besøgis, thu tha therudinden retter thig selff och bethencker oc till sinde fører, medt huad rett oc billighet sligt skee kand, attu baade haffuer thagett borgenn af hannom oc sidenn hannom fenckligenn lader anholde, attu therfore om thet sig saaledis befindis, lader hannom egienn løß och sidenn medt rettenn hannom tillthaler, anseendis huad vdj sig selff christeligt oc rett er, saa Wij for widere thes klagemaall oc offuerlob maa bliffue forschonett.</p><p>Actum Hafniae 8. junii a:o 94.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Concerning Tyge Brahe, touching one Christoffer Pepeler by name.</b></p><p>Christian IV.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, inasmuch as Nelle, the wife of Christoffer Pepeler, has most humbly petitioned Us and made known how — after her husband, the aforesaid Christoffer Pepeler, had been your bailiff, and you therefore, in certain matters which are said to have arisen between him and you over that same service of his, had caused his goods to be arrested and seized in Our market-town of Bergen in Our realm of Norway, and [how] he then, upon your assurance and upon the aforesaid letter and request of his wife, had betaken himself to Our market-town of Helsingør, so that he there in the same place, on your behalf, posted sufficient bail to your servant for the aforesaid matter — [how], notwithstanding this, you are nonetheless said to have had him thereupon taken prisoner and confined on Hven, as her enclosed petition further sets forth and shows: We therefore ask you and will that, when you are visited [with this letter], you then in this matter govern yourself and consider and call to mind with what right and equity such a thing can be done — that you have both taken bail of him and then have him held prisoner — [and] that therefore, if it be found to be so, you set him free again and then proceed against him at law, having regard to what is in itself Christian and right, so that We may be spared further complaint and importunity in the matter.</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, 8 June in the year [15]94.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), then in his minority (the regency council governed until his 1596 coronation); royal letters in these years issue in his name. <i>Christianus 4.</i> is the Latin register-form of the subscription. Tycho was at this date still in possession of Hven, six years from his 1597 departure.</li><li><b>Recipient / subject.</b> <b>Tyge Brahe</b> (Tycho), addressed as the recipient; <b>Christoffer Pepeler</b>, formerly Tycho's <i>fougitt</i> (bailiff/steward), is the subject of the complaint; <b>Nelle</b> (\"Nelle Christoffer Pepelers hustru\"), Pepeler's wife, is the petitioner. None is further identified in the corpus beyond this letter.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (Jyske Tegnelser). The heading is a <b>Latin–Danish rubric</b> in the register's hand: <i>\"TILL THYGE BRAHE … ANRØRENDIS\"</i> (\"Concerning Tyge Brahe … touching …\") — the standard register caption, preserved per protocol §2. The subscription <i>Christianus 4.</i> is likewise the Latin chancery register-form.</li><li><b>Legal structure (the load-bearing logic).</b> The body is one long chancery period reporting the wife's allegation and issuing the royal directive. Its skeleton is: <i>because</i> Pepeler (Tycho's ex-bailiff) had his goods seized in Bergen over service disputes; <i>and</i> on Tycho's own assurance came to Helsingør and posted sufficient bail through Tycho's servant; <i>yet</i> was nonetheless imprisoned on Hven — the king directs Tycho to weigh \"with what right and equity\" he could both take bail <i>and</i> then imprison the man, and, <b>conditionally</b>, <i>\"om thet sig saaledis befindis\"</i> (\"if it be found to be so\"), to release him and instead proceed against him <i>medt rettenn</i> (\"at law\"). The release order is thus <b>conditional on the facts proving out</b>, not absolute; this conditional mood (<i>om … befindis</i>) is preserved and not sharpened into an unconditional command.</li><li><b>Negations.</b> The Danish body contains <b>no explicit negative particle</b> (<i>icke/jcke</i>). The only negation-adjacent forms are the concessive <i>wanseett dog</i> (\"notwithstanding … nonetheless\") and <i>forschonett</i> (\"spared\"); neither is a logical \"not.\" The translation introduces none.</li><li><b>Reportative subjunctive (hedge).</b> The constructions <i>skall haffue … werritt</i> and <i>skall … ladett</i> (\"is said to have been,\" \"is said to have had\") mark the whole account as the wife's allegation reported to the king, not as findings the king asserts as proven. Rendered \"are said to have,\" consistent with the conditional release order; not flattened to plain assertion.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594).</b> <i>WOR gunst tilforne</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"know\"); <i>effter som</i> = \"inasmuch as\"; <i>hoßbunde</i> = <i>husbonde</i> (\"husband\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>fougitt</i> = <i>foged</i> (\"bailiff, steward\"); <i>godtz</i> = <i>gods</i> (\"goods, property\"); <i>arestêre oc beslaa</i> = \"arrest and seize/attach\"; <i>forthrøstning</i> = <i>fortrøstning</i> (\"assurance, guarantee\"); <i>schriffuelße</i> = <i>skrivelse</i> (\"letter, written communication\"); <i>satt nøyachtig borgenn</i> = \"posted sufficient bail/surety\" (<i>borgen</i> = bail, surety); <i>huilckett … wanseett</i> = <i>hvilket … uanset</i> (\"notwithstanding which\"); <i>fenckligenn thage oc hensette</i> = \"take prisoner and confine\"; <i>formelder oc wduiiser</i> = <i>formelder og udviser</i> (\"states and shows\"); <i>besøgis</i> = \"is visited [with this letter],\" i.e. served/reached by it; <i>retter thig selff</i> = \"govern/conduct yourself\"; <i>till sinde fører</i> = \"call to mind\"; <i>billighet</i> = \"equity, fairness\"; <i>om thet sig saaledis befindis</i> = \"if it be found to be so\"; <i>løß</i> = <i>løs</i> (\"free, loose\"); <i>medt rettenn … tillthaler</i> = \"proceed against / sue at law\"; <i>klagemaall oc offuerlob</i> = \"complaint and importunity (vexation)\"; <i>forschonett</i> = <i>forskånet</i> (\"spared\").</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Bergenn … wdj Wortt riige Norge</i> = <b>Bergen</b>, in the kingdom of Norway (then under the Danish crown); <i>Helsingøer</i> = <b>Helsingør</b> (Elsinore); <i>Hueen</i> = <b>Hven</b>, the island Tycho held in fief; <i>Hafnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Actum Hafniae 8. junii a:o 94</i> = \"Done at Copenhagen, 8 June, year [15]94\" (O.S.); <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5. The dateline (8 June 1594) and the dataset label (8 June 1594) agree; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the directive's conditional logic (release <i>only</i> if the allegation is borne out, then proceed at law) is preserved without sharpening; the residual soft point is the precise institutional sense of <i>retter thig selff … therudinden</i> (here \"govern yourself in this matter\"), a stock chancery admonition rendered idiomatically rather than word-for-word.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only residue is the idiomatic rendering of a stock chancery admonition; conditional logic + negations secure, dates agree.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO XIV p.67, reading the page image; the Danish text matches Dreyer word for word (Christoffer Pepeler, Bergen, Helsingør, Hven, the date Hafniae 8 junii anno 94), and the English translation renders the legal narrative, every place-name and date, and the reportative 'skall' clauses correctly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Jyske tegnelser, IV, 469."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15921117001", "next": "brahe-t_15940904001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Orders reconsideration of Christoffer Pepeler's imprisonment on Hven, following his wife's petition that he was detained despite posting bail after his goods were seized.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940608001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940726001", "d": "1594-07-26", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thomas Craig", "ck": "Thomas Craig", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thomas Craig — Uraniborg, 26 July 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIMO ET HUMANISSIMO<br>VIRO D. THOMAe CRAGIO I. V. D. AMICO<br>SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. EDENBURGI.</p><p>S.GRATISSIMAe mihi fuere literae tuae, vir eruditissime, quas accepi nuper refertissimas singularis in me benevolentiae et amoris notis. Quocirca etiam fusius respondere illis decreveram, modo Magnificus D. P. IUNIUS, uti proposuerat, in insula hac me invisisset. Sed spirante mox Austro, Helsingorae eum paratum iam navem ascendere cursim tantum salutare licuit, ita ut hac ratione omnis mihi rescribendi oportunitas tum sublata fuerit, et ne nunc quidem ob varias occupationes aliaque impedimenta otium ullum relictum sit, ut, quantum velim, literis complectar. Nihilominus breviusculas hasce ad te dare censui, ne ad humanissimam tuam Epistolam plane obmutescere viderer. Quod vero ad negotium de Cometis attinet, mihi perinde est, sive quis in coelo eos credat, sive in aëre versari. Sufficiet quod ex quinque huiusmodi crinitis sideribus, quae annis aliquot proxime elapsis, arduo et indefesso labore observavi, nullum infra Lunam fuisse, quin et eorum quaedam ne tantillam paralaxin, qualem Sol efficit, admisisse, ideoque ipso longe altiora et a terris remotiora extitisse invictis rationibus demonstrârim. Unde apertissime liquet omnes Aristotelicos, qui tot iam seculis contrariam tutati sunt sententiam, hac in parte plurimum aberrasse et Cometas sublunares efficiendo toto (prout dici solet) coelo deviasse. Fratri autem tuo non invideo hunc ob autoritatem ARISTOTELIS errorem, quem cum multis alijs communem habet, miseret potius quod cum Mathematicus sit, caeteris oculatior non existat. Ad literas eius respondissem quidem, si ea quae prae se ferunt, sincere et sine arroganti praeiudicio dicta esse comperissem. Verum existimo eas Capnurania ista scoptica non multo mansuetiores, adeo ut inter alia hospitalitatem etiam meam (ne quid dicam amplius) in dubium vocare non vereatur, dum sic scribit: Cupivit doctiss. vir D. legatus ut secum te inviserem, non patiuntur id amici. Si venissem, gratusne fuissem hospes? Caetera per temetipsum et pro tuâ prudentiâ atque humanitate satis intelligis. De meâ vero benevolentia erga te, cuius singularem candorem et virtutem pari doctrinâ coniuntam, cum ex praestantissimi D. PETRI IUNIJ relatione, utut inter nos breviculâ, tum ex proprijs tuis literis atque carminibus perspectam habeo, non est quod addubites, imo si quid tibi quod gratum sit per me praestari queat, invenies me quam paratissimum. Cum autem eleganti poëticâ venâ te praeditum esse ex versibus eiusmodi gratia conscriptis non obscure perspexerim, Epistolam hanc Elegiacam ad imitationem Ovidianarum nupere animi causa sororis nomine a me lusam adiungere volui, ut videas me non semper sublimia et coelestia, sed etiam terricolis, ut plurimum, usitata nonnunquam (obiter tamen) tractare.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae Uraniburgi 26. Iulij Anno 1594.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most learned and most courteous Master Thomas Craig, Doctor of Both Laws, his most dearly beloved friend. At Edinburgh.</b></p><p>Greeting. Most welcome to me were your letters, most learned Sir, which I received lately, crammed full with marks of your singular goodwill and affection toward me. For this reason I had even resolved to answer them at greater length, provided that the Worshipful Master P. Junius had, as he had proposed, visited me on this island. But, the south wind soon springing up, I was permitted only to greet him hurriedly at Helsingør as he was already standing ready to board ship — so that in this way every opportunity of writing back was then taken from me; nor even now, on account of various occupations and other hindrances, is any leisure left me to embrace [the matter] in a letter as fully as I should wish. Nevertheless I judged it right to send you these rather brief lines, lest I should seem to fall altogether silent in the face of your most courteous letter. But as for the business concerning the comets, it is all one to me whether one believes them to dwell in the heaven or in the air. It will suffice that, out of five such hairy stars [comets] which over some years now last past I have observed with arduous and unwearied labour, I have demonstrated by unanswerable reasonings that not one was below the Moon — indeed that certain of them admitted not even so much as that tiny parallax which the Sun produces, and were therefore far higher than [the Sun] itself and more remote from the earth. From which it is most plainly evident that all the Aristotelians, who for so many centuries now have maintained the contrary opinion, have in this matter erred most greatly, and, by making comets sublunary, have gone astray (as the saying goes) by the whole heaven. As for your brother, I do not begrudge him this error on the authority of Aristotle, which he holds in common with many others; rather I pity that, being a mathematician, he is no more clear-sighted than the rest. I would indeed have answered his letters, had I found that what they profess was said sincerely and without arrogant prejudice. But I judge them not much milder than that scoffing <i>Capnurania</i> of his — so much so that, among other things, he does not even shrink from calling my very hospitality (to say nothing further) into question, when he writes thus: \"The most learned Worshipful Master ambassador wished that I should visit you in his company; my friends do not allow it. Had I come, should I have been a welcome guest?\" The rest you understand well enough for yourself, and by your own prudence and courtesy. But as to my goodwill toward you — whose singular candour and virtue, joined with equal learning, I have come to know thoroughly, both from the report of the most excellent Master Peter Junius, brief though it was between us, and from your own letters and poems — there is no reason for you to be in any doubt; nay rather, if anything that may be agreeable to you can be furnished through me, you will find me most ready. And since I have perceived not obscurely, from verses composed for the sake of such [a favour], that you are endowed with an elegant poetic vein, I wished to append this Elegiac Epistle, lately composed by me in play, for my heart's relief, in my sister's name, in imitation of Ovid's [<i>Heroides</i>], that you may see that I do not always treat of lofty and celestial matters, but sometimes also (in passing, however) of things familiar to the dwellers on earth, as is mostly the case.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Uraniborg, 26 July, in the year 1594.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of Hven (Latin <i>Uraniburgum</i>; protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>D. Thomae Cragio I. V. D.</i> = <b>Master Thomas Craig</b>, <b>Doctor of Both Laws</b> (<i>Iuris Utriusque Doctor</i> — of canon and civil law); the eminent Scottish jurist and Latin poet Thomas Craig of Riccarton (c. 1538–1608), at Edinburgh. The salutation singles out his learning and humanity; the body praises his \"letters and poems\" and his \"elegant poetic vein,\" consistent with Craig the neo-Latin poet rather than with his comet-disputing brother.</li><li><b><i>Fratri autem tuo</b></i> — \"As for your brother\": the comet antagonist is <b>John Craig</b> (the physician and Aristotelian, d. 1620), Thomas's brother, with whom Tycho had carried on a bitter epistolary dispute over the placement of comets (c. 1589–94). Tycho here replies courteously to the conciliatory <i>Thomas</i> while declining to engage <i>John's</i> renewed attack. <i>cum Mathematicus sit</i> — \"being a mathematician\": Tycho pointedly pities that John, though trained in mathematics, is no more clear-sighted than the lay Aristotelians.</li><li><b><i>Magnificus D. P. IUNIUS</i> / <i>praestantissimi D. PETRI IUNIJ</b></i> — <b>Master Peter (Petrus) Junius</b>, the intermediary who had carried Craig's correspondence and Craig's good report of Thomas to Tycho; named first by initial (<i>D. P. IUNIUS</i>) and later in full (<i>D. PETRI IUNIJ</i>). The text twice calls his contact with Tycho brief (<i>cursim … salutare</i>; <i>utut inter nos breviculâ</i>).</li><li><b><i>Capnurania ista scoptica</b></i> — \"that scoffing <i>Capnurania</i> of his.\" <b><i>Capnurania</b></i> is <b>John Craig's own polemical coinage</b>, a Greek-derived portmanteau (καπνός <i>kapnos</i>, \"smoke\" + Οὐρανία/<i>Urania</i>, the muse of astronomy / the heavens), insinuating that Tycho's celestial comets are mere smoke or vapour. Tycho retained the term and titled his rebuttal the <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i> (\"the quenching of the <i>Capnurania</i>,\" ed. in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> IV). <i>scoptica</i> (Greek σκωπτικός, \"scoffing, satirical\") is kept and glossed; <b>the neologism is flagged, not Englished</b>, per protocol.</li><li><b>Embedded quotation.</b> <i>Cupivit vir D. legatus … gratusne fuissem hospes?</i> — Tycho quotes John Craig's own words verbatim (the <i>legatus</i> = an ambassador in whose company Craig had wished to visit Hven, but was prevented by his friends), as the instance in which Craig had impugned Tycho's hospitality. Rendered as direct speech in quotation marks; <i>vir D. legatus</i> literally \"the man, Master ambassador.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy / the comet argument.</b> <i>crinitis sideribus</i> = \"hairy stars,\" i.e. <b>comets</b> (the literal sense of <i>cometa</i> &lt; κομήτης); rendered \"hairy stars [comets].\" <i>infra Lunam … sublunares</i> = below the Moon / <b>sublunary</b> — the Aristotelian doctrine that comets are atmospheric (sublunary) exhalations, which Tycho's parallax measurements of five comets refuted. <i>paralaxin</i> = <b>parallax</b> (protocol §5.1); <i>qualem Sol efficit</i> — Tycho's standard of comparison is the (small) solar parallax. <i>toto … coelo deviasse</i> — \"gone astray by the whole heaven,\" a proverbial Latin idiom (<i>toto caelo errare</i>, to be utterly wrong); Tycho marks it as proverbial with <i>prout dici solet</i> (\"as the saying goes\"), preserved.</li><li><b><i>Epistolam hanc Elegiacam … ad imitationem Ovidianarum … sororis nomine</b></i> — \"this Elegiac Epistle … in imitation of Ovid's [<i>Heroides</i>] … in my sister's name.\" Tycho appended to this letter a Latin verse epistle in elegiac couplets, written <i>animi causa</i> (\"for diversion / for the heart's relief\") and <i>in the name of his sister</i> — almost certainly <b>Sophie Brahe</b> — modelled on Ovid's <i>Heroides</i> (verse letters in a woman's voice). The enclosed poem is not part of this letter's prose text. <i>lusam</i> (from <i>ludere</i>) = \"composed in play / as a literary diversion\"; the modest framing is preserved, not sharpened into a claim of seriousness.</li><li><b><i>terricolis … usitata</b></i> — \"things familiar to the dwellers on earth,\" opposed to <i>sublimia et coelestia</i> (\"lofty and celestial matters\"): Tycho's self-deprecating contrast between his astronomy and this occasional verse. <i>obiter tamen</i> (\"in passing, however\") is his hedge that such earthly trifling is only incidental — preserved.</li><li><b>Orthography (as printed).</b> <i>oportunitas</i> for <i>opportunitas</i>; <i>autoritatem</i> for <i>auctoritatem</i>; <i>coniuntam</i> for <i>coniunctam</i>; <i>perspexerim</i>; the <i>-ij</i> digraph (<i>proprijs, carminibus … IUNIJ, alijs</i>) is the period spelling for <i>-ii</i>; circumflexes (<i>fusiùs</i> etc., here <i>fusius / breviculâ / prudentiâ / venâ</i>) are the editor's. The dataset header carries inline lettered footnote markers (superscript \"A\") on several words (<i>eruditissimeᴬ, refertissimasᴬ, fusiùsᴬ, Capnuraniaᴬistaᴬ, perspexerimᴬ, sororisᴬ</i>); these are apparatus sigla, not part of the text, and are omitted from the Source block.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datae Uraniburgi 26. Iulij Anno 1594</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, 26 July 1594\" — <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1594. 26. juli. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. (Edinburgh likewise remained on Old Style in 1594, so no cross-style ambiguity arises with the Scottish recipient.)</li><li><b>MS.</b> The sole witness is the Vienna draft (<i>Kladde</i>), cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: restored the dropped abbreviation 'doctiss.' (doctissimus, 'most learned') before 'vir D. legatus', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p.366. English: added 'most learned' to the corresponding quoted clause.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the prose is rendered securely throughout. The single retained crux is the untranslated neologism <b><i>Capnurania</b></i> (John Craig's smoke-Urania coinage), kept and glossed rather than Englished; secondarily, the identity of the unnamed <i>legatus</i> in Craig's quoted words and the precise dedicatee-sister of the appended Ovidian epistle (taken to be Sophie Brahe) are noted rather than asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.364-367 as page images; the letter to Craig runs from the foot of p.365 ('ERUDITISSIMO ET HUMANISSIMO VIRO D. THOMAE CRAGIO...') to the salutation 'Vale' on p.366. Collated opening address, closing line, and every proper name and number: Thomas Craig I.V.D. at Edinburgh, Petrus Junius, Aristotle/Aristotelicos, the five comets, Helsingør, Uraniborg, and the date 26 July 1594 all check out word for word against Dreyer (allowing normal long-s/ligature variance). Found one dropped word in our published Latin (doctiss., 'most learned', missing before 'vir D. legatus') which is a TEXT_ERROR; it does not change the sense a scholar would take from the English. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found in the translation itself.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin drops the abbreviation 'doctiß.' (doctissimus, 'most learned') that Dreyer p.366 prints before 'vir D. legatus' in the quoted line from the brother's letter; the English, following the truncated source, likewise lacks the epithet. Dreyer prints: <i>dum fic fcribit: Cupivit doctiß. vir D. legatus ut fecum te inviferem</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, pp.364-367 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect was confirmed exactly as flagged; both the Latin and its dependent English clause were corrected.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Thomas Craig", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15940000002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15940000002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Craig for his letters, explains a missed meeting with Junius at Helsingør, and briefly addresses the dispute over comets.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940726001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940810001", "d": "1594-08-10", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "[open letter / deed of declaration]", "ck": "[open letter / deed of declaration]", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Helsingør", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [open letter / deed of declaration] — Helsingør, 10 August 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery / diplomatic) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = private collection, Philadelphia, USA</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>JEG Tygge Brahe thill Knudstrup kienndiß och giør witterligt for alle med thette mitt obnne breff, att effter som jeg nu haffuer soldt och skiødt till min kierre broder, erllig och welbyrdig manndt Steenn Brahe till Knudstrup, min halffpartt aff Knudstrups hoffuittgaardt och grund, och jeg mig dog ther hos forbeholldett att maa och skall ald min liffstiid schriffuis och kalldis aff samme Knudstrup gaardt, liige som jeg giorde, førenndt jeg thennd affhenndt och soldt haffuer, eptherthi thennd hafuer verett min federnne gaardt och mig arffueligenn thillfaldenn som min rette sedegaardt, saa beplichter jeg mig dog med thette breff, att enndog jeg baade selff schriffuer och kaller, och aff anndre, huem som held[s]t thed vere kannd, mig lader schriffue och kalle aff for:ne Knudstrups gaardt, att thed alligeuell icke skall komme for:ne min kierre broder Steenn Brahe eller hanns arffuinge thill hinder eller skade i nogen maade. This till ydermere vidnisbyrtt haffuer jeg trøgtt min signete her neden for thette mit obne breff, och mig med egenn hannd vnnderschreffuit, och wenligen ombedett erllig och welbiurdige menndt, Dethleff Holck thill Høygaardt och Eske Biillde til Ellinnge, thette breff med mig thil widnisbyrtt att besegle.</p><p>Giffuit vdi Helsingøer thennd x augustj anno Mdlxxxxiiij<br>Thyge<br>Brahe (l.s.)<br>egen hand</p><p>Dettleff<br>Holck (l.s.)<br>egen handtt</p>", "en": "<p>I, Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup, acknowledge and make known to all by this my open letter that, inasmuch as I have now sold and conveyed to my dear brother, the honourable and well-born man Steen Brahe of Knudstrup, my half-share of the principal estate and land of Knudstrup — and have nonetheless reserved to myself therewith that I may and shall, for all my lifetime, be written and styled \"of\" that same estate of Knudstrup, just as I did before I parted with and sold it, inasmuch as it has been my paternal estate and has fallen to me by inheritance as my rightful ancestral seat — I do nevertheless bind myself by this letter that, although both I myself write and style [myself thus], and have myself written and styled by others, whosoever they may be, \"of\" the aforesaid estate of Knudstrup, it shall nevertheless not be to the hindrance or harm, in any manner, of my aforesaid dear brother Steen Brahe or his heirs. In further witness whereof I have impressed my seal here below this my open letter, and have signed it with my own hand, and have in friendship requested the honourable and well-born men Detlev Holck of Højgård and Eske Bille of Ellinge to seal this letter, together with me, in witness.</p><p>Given at Helsingør, the 10th [day] of August, in the year 1594.<br>Tyge<br>Brahe (place of the seal)<br>[in his] own hand</p><p>Detlev<br>Holck (place of the seal)<br>[in his] own hand</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> Not an ordinary letter but an <b>open letter / deed of declaration</b> (<i>åbent brev</i>) issued by Tycho in his own name — a self-binding instrument annexed to the <b>sale of his half-share of Knudstrup to his brother Steen Brahe</b>, the transaction completed by mid-1594 (J. R. Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> / <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>; cf. Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>: Knudstrup was inherited jointly by Otte Brahe's two eldest sons, Tycho and Steen). The dataset's `recipient:empty` is therefore correct: an open letter is addressed to all (<i>for alle</i>), not to a single recipient.</li><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), here styling himself <i>Tygge Brahe thill Knudstrup</i> (\"Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup\") — the very style whose reservation is the subject of the deed.</li><li><b>Steen Brahe of Knudstrup</b> (1547–1620), Tycho's younger brother, royal councillor; by this conveyance he obtained sole ownership of the family seat without awaiting Tycho's death.</li><li><b>Knudstrup</b> (Knutstorp, Scania) — the Brahe family's <i>fædrenegård</i> (paternal estate) and <i>sædegård</i> (ancestral noble seat), Tycho's birthplace.</li><li><b>Witnesses.</b> <b>Detlev Holck of Højgård</b> (<i>Dethleff Holck thill Høygaardt</i>) and <b>Eske Bille of Ellinge</b> (<i>Eske Biillde til Ellinnge</i>), Danish noblemen requested to co-seal; both named within the body. Only Holck's subscription and seal survive in the closing autograph block as transcribed here.</li><li><b>Legal force of the binding clause.</b> The operative sentence carries a single negation governing the whole reservation: <i>att thed alligeuell <b>icke</b> skall komme … thill hinder eller skade i nogen maade</i> = \"that it shall <b>nevertheless not</b> be to the hindrance or harm … in any manner.\" Tycho reserves the honorific style \"of Knudstrup\" for life but expressly disclaims any property or legal consequence flowing from it against Steen or his heirs. The single negation is reproduced as one; the concessive <i>enndog … alligeuell</i> (\"although … nevertheless\") pairing is kept intact.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594).</b> <i>kienndiß</i> = <i>kendes</i> (\"acknowledge\"); <i>giør witterligt</i> = <i>gør vitterligt</i> (\"make known\"); <i>obnne / obne breff</i> = <i>åbne brev</i> (\"open letter\"); <i>skiødt</i> = <i>skødt</i> (\"conveyed,\" verb of <i>skøde</i>, the deed of conveyance); <i>halffpartt</i> = <i>halvpart</i> (\"half-share\"); <i>hoffuittgaardt</i> = <i>hovedgård</i> (\"principal/manorial estate\"); <i>forbeholldett</i> = <i>forbeholdt</i> (\"reserved\"); <i>liffstiid</i> = <i>livstid</i> (\"lifetime\"); <i>schriffuis och kalldis</i> = <i>skrives og kaldes</i> (\"be written and styled\"); <i>førenndt</i> = <i>førend</i> (\"before\"); <i>affhenndt</i> = <i>afhændet</i> (\"alienated, parted with\"); <i>eptherthi</i> = <i>eftersom/efterdi</i> (\"inasmuch as\"); <i>federnne gaardt</i> = <i>fædrenegård</i> (\"paternal estate\"); <i>arffueligenn thillfaldenn</i> = <i>arveligen tilfaldet</i> (\"fallen to [me] by inheritance\"); <i>sedegaardt</i> = <i>sædegård</i> (\"ancestral noble seat\"); <i>beplichter</i> = <i>forpligter</i> (\"bind/oblige\"); <i>held[s]t</i> — the source reads <i>held</i> with the <i>s</i> of <i>heldst</i> (= <i>helst</i>, \"soever\") not written; supplied in brackets; <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>alligeuell</i> = <i>alligevel</i> (\"nevertheless\"); <i>arffuinge</i> = <i>arvinger</i> (\"heirs\"); <i>hinder</i> = <i>hinder</i> (\"hindrance, impediment\"); <i>i nogen maade</i> = <i>i nogen måde</i> (\"in any manner\"); <i>This</i> = <i>des / dess</i> (\"thereof,\" the chancery formula <i>des til ydermere vidnesbyrd</i>, \"in further witness whereof\"); <i>trøgtt min signete</i> = <i>trykt mit signet</i> (\"impressed my seal\"); <i>wenligen ombedett</i> = <i>venligen ombedet</i> (\"in friendship requested\"); <i>besegle</i> = <i>besegle</i> (\"to seal\").</li><li><b>Seal notations.</b> <i>(l.s.)</i> expands <i>loco sigilli</i>, \"in the place of the seal\" — the editorial marker for where a seal was impressed; <i>egen hand / egen handtt</i> = <i>egen hånd</i>, \"[in his] own hand,\" noting the autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Giffuit vdi Helsingøer thennd x augustj anno Mdlxxxxiiij</i> = \"Given at <b>Helsingør</b> (Elsinore), the 10th [day] of August, in the year 1594\" (O.S.). The Roman-numeral year <i>Mdlxxxxiiij</i> = 1594; <i>x augustj</i> = 10 August. Body date, catalogue `label` date, and dateline agree (10 August 1594, Helsingør); no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely; document type, parties, and the single-negation legal clause are unambiguous and corroborated by the biographical record of the 1594 Knudstrup conveyance. The only sub-word editorial act is the bracketed <i>[s]</i> in <i>held[s]t</i>, noted above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish deed of declaration (Tycho to his brother Steen Brahe re: the Knudstrup estate) against TBOO XIV p.68 page image. Every clause, name (Steen Brahe, Detlef Holck of Højgård, Eske Bille of Ellinge), place, and date (10 August 1594) in the English matches Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Privat samling i Philadelphia, USA.."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Declares the sale of his half-share of Knudstrup to his brother Steen Brahe, reserving the right to retain the style 'of Knudstrup' for life.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940810001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940904001", "d": "1594-09-04", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Kolding", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Kolding, 4 September 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XVIII, 364 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TILL TYGE BRAHE HELLIG THRE KONGERS CAPELL WDI ROSCHILDE DOMBKIRCKE ANRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR.</p><p>Christian 4..</p><p>WOR gunst thilfornn. Wiid, epter att Wij thig thilforn haffue thilschreffuit, attu wden forßømmelße schulle lade forethage thend bygning paa thet capell wdi Roschilde domkircke, som kaldis Hellig thre konger capell, och Wore kierre foreldre, salige och hoglofflige ihukommelße, haffue theris begraffuelße wdi, huilckit dog her thill aldelis icke er scheedt, meden samme capell io meere och meere dag fran dag forderffuis och forargis, och forwndre Wij Oß høyligen, attu samme Wor befaling icke anderledis haffuer achtit och epterkommit och samme bygning forferdigit. Tha bede Wij dig och wille, attu aldelis er fortenckt wdi for:ne bygning paa for:ne capell endeligen och wden ald wndschylding at lade forethage och forferdige inden juell førstkommendis wdi thet allersenniste, wdi huis maade thett kunde haffue wdi behoff att byggis och ferdigis paa. Saa frampt thet icke scheer, att for:ne bygning inden for:ne dag och thiid bliffuer forferdigit, tha wille Wij naadigst befale Wor embidtzmand paa Wor gaard wdi for:ne Roschilde for:ne bygning at schulle lade ferdig giørre, och tha strax att forleene en anden med for:ne capell och theß thilliggendis guoedtz och indkompst, och attu thig med thett allerførste her om emoed Oß ercklerer, huad thu er herwdinden thilßindtz att wille giørre, paa thet Wij kunde wiide, huor epter Wij Oß schulle forholde.</p><p>Ther met etc.<br>Actum Cold[ingae] 4. septembris a:o 94.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tyge Brahe, concerning the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings in Roskilde Cathedral, as follows.</b></p><p>Christian IV.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, since we have heretofore written to you that you should without neglect have undertaken that building-work on the chapel in Roskilde Cathedral which is called the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings, and in which our dear parents, of blessed and most laudable memory, have their burial — which nonetheless has not at all been done up to now, while that same chapel is decaying and being damaged more and more day by day — we are therefore greatly astonished that you have not otherwise heeded and complied with this our command and completed that same building-work. We therefore ask and will of you that you be wholly mindful, finally and without any excuse, to have that aforesaid building-work on the aforesaid chapel undertaken and completed before Christmas next at the very latest, in whatever manner it may have need to be built and finished. So far forth as it does not come to pass that the aforesaid building-work is completed within the aforesaid day and time, then we will most graciously command our official at our manor in the aforesaid Roskilde to have the aforesaid building-work made ready, and then forthwith to enfeoff another with the aforesaid chapel and its appurtenant property and income; and [we will] that you declare yourself to us concerning this at the very first, [as to] what you are minded herein to do, so that we may know how we are to govern ourselves accordingly.</p><p>Therewith, etc.<br>Done at Kolding, 4 September, year [15]94.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (b. 1577, r. 1588–1648), here writing in his own name at age 17 in the third year after his 1593 accession to personal rule (the regency council had governed 1588–93). The chancery hand subscribes him <i>\"Christian 4..\"</i> (so written, with the doubled point).</li><li><b>Recipient and benefice.</b> Tycho Brahe had held the prebend (canonry) of the <b>Chapel of the Three Holy Kings</b> (<i>Hellig Tre Kongers Kapel</i>) in Roskilde Cathedral since the grant of 1 May 1579 from Frederik II — a sinecure carrying income and the obligation to maintain the chapel fabric and to support two choirboys (<i>tho smaa sincker</i>). The chapel is the burial place of Christian I and later Danish kings; the <i>\"kierre foreldre\"</i> (\"dear parents\") here are <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 1588) and Queen <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> — though Sophie was living in 1594, the chancery formula <i>\"of blessed and most laudable memory\"</i> with <i>foreldre</i> is the dynastic burial-chapel formula and is reproduced as written, not corrected (see crux below).</li><li><b>Document type and code-switch.</b> A chancery-register entry (<i>Sjællandske Tegnelser</i> XVIII, 364), not an autograph. The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"…ANRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning… <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2, exactly as in the parallel 1584 Frederik II letter.</li><li><b>Legal force — the threat (translated with negation count fixed).</b> The operative clause is a royal ultimatum with three explicit negations / privatives, reproduced one-for-one: (1) <i>\"huilckit dog her thill <b>aldelis icke</b> er scheedt\"</i> = \"which nonetheless has <b>not at all</b> been done up to now\"; (2) <i>\"attu… befaling <b>icke</b> anderledis haffuer achtit\"</i> = \"that you have <b>not</b> otherwise heeded\"; (3) <i>\"endeligen och <b>wden ald</b> wndschylding\"</i> = \"finally and <b>without any</b> excuse.\" The conditional <i>\"<b>Saa frampt</b> thet <b>icke</b> scheer, att… bliffuer forferdigit, <b>tha</b> wille Wij…\"</i> is a negative protasis governing a future apodosis: \"<b>So far forth as</b> it does <b>not</b> come to pass that [the work] is completed…, <b>then</b> we will…\" — i.e. <i>if you fail to finish it by Christmas, then</i> the king will (a) have it finished by his own Roskilde official and (b) <b>enfeoff another</b> with the chapel and its revenues — a threat to revoke Tycho's benefice. The fourth negation-like privative <i>\"<b>wden</b> forßømmelße\"</i> (\"<b>without</b> neglect,\" in the recital of the earlier order) is also kept. Total: four privative/negative markers (<i>aldelis icke, icke, wden ald, wden</i>) plus the conditional <i>icke scheer</i> — all five preserved; none added.</li><li><b>\"bede Wij dig och wille.\"</b> The doublet <i>bede… och wille</i> couples a courteous \"ask\" with the binding <i>ville</i> of the royal will (\"ask and will of you\"); the closing <i>\"och attu… ercklerer\"</i> depends on this same <i>wille</i>, hence the bracketed \"[we will] that you declare.\" The mood is a command softened by petitionary form, not a request — rendered to keep both registers.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"Know,\" imperative); <i>attu</i> = <i>at du</i>; <i>thilschreffuit</i> = <i>tilskrevet</i> (\"written to\"); <i>wden forßømmelße</i> = <i>uden forsømmelse</i> (\"without neglect\"); <i>forethage</i> = <i>foretage</i> (\"undertake\"); <i>scheedt</i> = <i>sket</i> (\"done, happened\"); <i>forderffuis</i> = <i>fordærves</i> (\"is spoiled/decays\"); <i>forargis</i> = <i>forarges</i> (\"is damaged/marred\"); <i>forwndre Wij Oß høyligen</i> = <i>forundre vi os højligen</i> (\"we are greatly astonished\"); <i>achtit</i> = <i>agtet</i> (\"heeded, regarded\"); <i>epterkommit</i> = <i>efterkommet</i> (\"complied with\"); <i>forferdigit / forferdige</i> = <i>færdiggjort / færdiggøre</i> (\"completed / complete\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>fortenckt</i> = <i>betænkt</i> (\"mindful, intent\"); <i>juell førstkommendis</i> = <i>jul førstkommende</i> (\"Christmas next-coming\"); <i>allersenniste</i> = <i>allersidste</i> (\"very latest\"); <i>Saa frampt</i> = <i>såfremt</i> (\"so far forth as, provided that, if\"); <i>embidtzmand</i> = <i>embedsmand</i> (\"official, officer\"); <i>gaard</i> = <i>gård</i> (\"manor, estate\"); <i>forleene</i> = <i>forlene</i> (\"enfeoff, grant in fief\"); <i>theß thilliggendis guoedtz och indkompst</i> = <i>dets tilliggende gods og indkomst</i> (\"its appurtenant property and income\"); <i>ercklerer</i> = <i>erklærer</i> (\"declare\"); <i>thilßindtz</i> = <i>tilsinds</i> (\"minded, disposed\"); <i>huor epter… forholde</i> = <i>hvorefter… forholde</i> (\"how we are to conduct/govern ourselves\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Cold[ingae] 4. septembris a:o 94</i> = \"Done at <b>Kolding</b>, 4 September [15]94\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5; <i>Cold.</i> expanded to the Latin locative <i>Coldingae</i> (Kolding, the royal residence Koldinghus in Jutland). The dateline as written (4 September 1594) agrees with the catalogue label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish and, critically, the legal force of the ultimatum (five negations/privatives preserved; the conditional threat to revoke the prebend rendered without smoothing) are secure. The single soft point is the <i>\"kierre foreldre… salige ihukommelße\"</i> formula: read literally it implies both parents are deceased, yet Queen Sophie was alive in 1594. This is the standard dynastic burial-chapel formula (the chapel holds Frederik II's tomb) rather than a factual statement about Sophie; it is rendered as written and flagged here, not corrected.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the </i>foreldre<i> formula is faithfully kept and confidently explained as a dynastic formula; negations one-for-one, dates agree.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO XIV pp.68-70 (page images, read visually), corroborated by the adjacent Latin regest (no.103) summarizing the same royal demand. Letter proper on pp.68-69, text matches Dreyer word for word. The Christmas deadline and the date (4 September 1594) correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XVIII, 364."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15940608001", "next": "brahe-t_15941108001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Rebukes Tycho for failing to repair the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings at Roskilde Cathedral and demands completion before Christmas.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940904001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15940928001", "d": "1594-09-28", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bernburg", "t": "Christoph Rothmann → Tycho Brahe — Bernburg, 28 September 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani epistolarum astronomicarum libri. Quorum primus hic … </i>(1596; = Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VI) · MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET SINGVLARI ERVDITIONE, VIRTVTE, ET INTEGRITATE PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, EQUITI DANO, ETC. DOMINO ET AMICO SUO CUM OBSERVANTIA COLENDO. S. P.</p><p>QVANTOS sustinuerim hactenus cruciatus, vir Nobilissime et Eruditissime, non opus esse arbitror ut scribam. Tanti certê fuerunt, ut anima vix corpori inhaerere potuerit, recurrente subinde arthritide et immanissimis nephriticis doloribus. Qui etiam in causa fuêre, quo minus ad Illustrissimum Principem ac Dominum meum ante ipsius obitum reuerterer, tibique ea, quae promiseram, transmitterem. Sed iam ante paucas aliquot Septimanas Diuino fauore aliquo modo caepi respirare. DEVS aeternus Opt. Max. in cuius conspectu ego ambulo, pro sua inexhausta misericordia etiam in posterum auxilio suo mihi adsit, et quod caepit, clementer ad finem perducat. De nostris autem studijs ut aliquid attingam, diligenter hactenus scrutatus sum Catalogos Francofurtenses, num a TYCHONE BRAHE quid editum in ijs invenire possem: Verum nihil reperi. Miror igitur, quid causae sit, quod Opus illud tuum de Cometis supprimas, nec in publicum prodire sinas, praesertim cum non tantum illud iamdudum fuerit absolutum, verumetiam toties et tanto tempore tam certo promiseris, te propediem illud emissurum. Equidem libenter, quantum in me esset, VRANIAM nostram ornarem et excolerem. Sed quid mihi sit obstaculo, vides. Quod igitur mihi denegatur, tu eo ardentius suppleas. Nec enim Mathematica Studia parum illustrabis, Posteritatique commendabis, si Opus illud tuum divulgabis. Incusantur enim vulgo, quasi sint sine fructu. Sed cum ex Opere illo tuo animaduertent, nos ea, quae hactenus ignoratione Matheseos pro veris credita sunt, falsissima esse, certissime demonstrare posse, obturabis ora tam temere iudicantium, illosque suae temeritatis conuinces, modo apud ipsos veritatis inuentio et tam abditissimarum tamque a nobis procul dissitarum rerum cognitio, fructus censeatur. Incitabis simul etiam heroica et praeclara ingenia ad aemulationem, turpeque sibi esse ducent, si haec ignorent: quemadmodum solet praeclaris ingenijs nihil detestabilius videri, quâm praeclara quaeque ignorare. Praeterea quot viros doctos putas editionem tuam singulari cum desiderio expectare? Qui certe plures sunt, quam tu tibi persuadere potes, quique de te non aliter nisi praeclare sentiunt. Noli igitur ulterius istorum omnium expectationem suspendere, aut etiam tua ipsis invidere. Sed spero non committes, ut studia Mathematica diutius isto tuo ornatu careant, proptereaque minus colantur. Haec fuêre, quae ad te hoc tempore perscribere volui: quae licet fortasse non sint magni momenti; tamen nactus certum hominem, qui ad vos iret, vacuas ad Amicum Literas quam nullas dare malui. Accipies igitur haec in meliorem partem, silentiumque meum diuturnum supradictâ de causâ sufficienter excusatum habebis. Hortum sororis tuae Philosophicae, Dominae SOPHIAe BRAHE Matronae perquam solertissimae et Nobilissimae, Illustrissimo Principi ac Domino meo, felicissimae recordationis, pro dignitate commendassem, si ipsius Celsit. accessissem. Attamen per Literas omnia Illustriss. ipsius Celsit. significaui.</p><p>Vale feliciter et optime mî Nobilissime et Eruditissime Domine BRAHE, Vraniamque, quod facis, splendide ornare pergito.<br>Bernburgi 28. Septemb. Anno 1594.<br>T. T.<br>CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, most outstanding in singular erudition, virtue, and integrity, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, Danish knight, etc., his lord and friend to be honoured with respect. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>How great the torments are that I have endured up to now, most noble and most learned Sir, I do not think there is any need to write. They were certainly so great that my soul could scarcely cling to my body, with arthritis and most savage nephritic [kidney] pains recurring time and again. These were also the cause why I did not return to the Most Illustrious Prince, my lord, before his death, and why I did not send on to you those things I had promised. But now, some few weeks ago, by divine favour I have in some measure begun to breathe again. May the eternal God, Best and Greatest, in whose sight I walk, of his inexhaustible mercy be present to me with his aid hereafter too, and graciously bring to its end what he has begun. But to touch on something of our studies: I have hitherto diligently searched the Frankfurt catalogues, [to see] whether I might find anything published in them by Tycho Brahe; but I found nothing. I wonder, therefore, what the reason is that you suppress that work of yours on the comets, and do not let it come out into public, especially since it has not only long since been finished, but you have so often and over so long a time so surely promised that you would shortly send it out. For my part I would gladly, as far as lay in me, adorn and cultivate our Urania. But you see what is an obstacle to me. What therefore is denied to me, may you supply the more ardently. For you will not a little illuminate the mathematical studies, and commend them to posterity, if you publish that work of yours. For they are commonly charged [with being], as it were, without fruit. But when from that work of yours they shall perceive that we can most certainly demonstrate those things which hitherto, through ignorance of mathematics, have been believed true to be utterly false, you will stop the mouths of those who judge so rashly, and convict them of their rashness — provided that with them the finding-out of truth, and the knowledge of things so deeply hidden and so far removed from us, be reckoned a fruit. At the same time you will also spur on heroic and illustrious minds to emulation, and they will count it a disgrace to themselves if they should be ignorant of these things — just as to illustrious minds nothing is wont to seem more detestable than to be ignorant of whatever is illustrious. Besides, how many learned men do you think await your edition with singular longing? They are certainly more than you can persuade yourself, and they think of you in no other way than splendidly. Do not therefore any longer keep all these men's expectation in suspense, or even begrudge them what is yours. But I hope you will not let it come about that the mathematical studies should longer lack this adornment of yours, and on that account be the less cultivated. These were the things I wished to write out to you at this time; which, although they may perhaps not be of great moment, yet, having got hold of a trustworthy man who was going to you, I preferred to give empty Letters to a Friend than none at all. You will therefore take these in the better part, and will hold my long silence, for the cause stated above, to be sufficiently excused. I would, as was fitting, have commended the garden of your philosophical sister, the Lady Sophie Brahe, a matron exceedingly skilful and most noble, to the Most Illustrious Prince my lord, of most happy memory, if I had approached His Highness. Nevertheless by Letters I signified everything to His Most Illustrious Highness.</p><p>Fare happily and very well, my most noble and most learned Master Brahe, and continue, as you do, splendidly to adorn Urania.<br>At Bernburg, 28 September, the year 1594.<br>Yours truly [T. T.],<br>Christoph Rothmann.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Christophorus Rothmannus</i> = <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (c. 1550/60 – c. 1600), mathematician–astronomer at the court of Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel, Tycho's principal scientific correspondent there and a defender of the Copernican system against Tycho. He left Kassel on 1 September 1590, ostensibly to return there, but went instead to his native Bernburg; after a long silence this is <b>his last known letter to Tycho</b> (Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, on the 1590 departure and the September 1594 letter). The closing <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Tuus Totus</i> (\"Wholly yours\" / \"Yours truly\").</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Domino de Knudstrup, Equiti Dano</i> = \"Lord of <b>Knudstrup</b>, Danish knight\" — Tycho's ancestral seat in Scania and his noble rank. <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting,\" protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Bernburgi</i> = <b>Bernburg</b> (Bernburg an der Saale, in Anhalt; Sachsen-Anhalt), Rothmann's birthplace and where he had withdrawn.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimum Principem ac Dominum meum … felicissimae recordationis</b></i> — \"the Most Illustrious Prince, my lord, … of most happy memory\": Rothmann's patron, identified from the corpus as <b>Joachim Ernst, Prince of Anhalt (Anhalt-Bernburg)</b> (1536–1586), who had supported his studies at Wittenberg. The phrase <i>ante ipsius obitum</i> (\"before his death\") and <i>felicissimae recordationis</i> mark the prince as already deceased; he had died in 1586, so the unfulfilled return and the <i>significaui</i> (\"I signified … by letters\") describe relations during the prince's lifetime — <b>flagged below as a chronological crux.</b></li><li><b>Medicine.</b> <i>arthritide</i> = \"arthritis\" (gout/joint disease); <i>immanissimis nephriticis doloribus</i> = \"most savage nephritic pains,\" i.e. kidney pains (the stone) — period terms, kept and glossed (protocol §5.4). Both recur as Rothmann's standing complaint in this correspondence.</li><li><b><i>Catalogos Francofurtenses</b></i> — the <b>Frankfurt [book-fair] catalogues</b> (the <i>Messkataloge</i>), the standard early-modern record of newly published books; Rothmann had searched them in vain for anything printed by Tycho.</li><li><b><i>Opus illud tuum de Cometis</b></i> — \"that work of yours on the comets,\" Tycho's projected/long-promised treatise on comets (the volume that would become <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i>, 1588, on the comet of 1577, and the planned further volume); Rothmann presses Tycho on its non-appearance in print. <i>iamdudum … absolutum</i> (\"long since finished\") is Rothmann's claim, kept as his assertion.</li><li><b><i>URANIAM nostram</b></i> / <b><i>Uraniam … splendide ornare</b></i> — \"<b>our Urania</b>\" / \"splendidly to adorn Urania\": Urania, Muse of astronomy, here a metonym for the science of astronomy itself; <i>ornare/excolere Uraniam</i> (\"to adorn / cultivate Urania\") echoes the Tychonic sign-off register (cf. protocol §5.5, <i>sacris Uraniae lita</i>). Kept as figure, named in the translation.</li><li><b><i>studia Mathematica … isto tuo ornatu</b></i> — \"the mathematical studies … this adornment of yours\": <i>Mathematica/Mathesis</i> in Rothmann's usage = the mathematical sciences (astronomy chief among them); the defence against the charge that they are <i>sine fructu</i> (\"without fruit\") is a topos of the period's apologetics for mathematics.</li><li><b><i>sororis tuae Philosophicae, Dominae Sophiae Brahe</b></i> — \"your philosophical sister, the Lady <b>Sophie Brahe</b>\" (1559–1643), Tycho's learned younger sister, known for her work in horticulture, chemistry/alchemy, and genealogy; <i>Philosophicae</i> marks her as a fellow student of natural philosophy. <i>Hortum … pro dignitate commendassem</i> (\"I would … have commended [her] garden as was fitting\") points to her noted garden/horticultural interest. <i>Matronae perquam solertissimae</i> = \"a matron exceedingly skilful.\"</li><li><b><i>Celsit. / Illustriss. ipsius Celsit.</b></i> — <i>Celsitudo</i> = \"Highness\" (protocol §5.5); the abbreviations are expanded in translation as \"His Highness\" / \"His Most Illustrious Highness.\"</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None in the text.</li><li><b>Orthography (humanist Latin print, 1596 edition).</b> <i>câepi</i> for <i>coepi</i>, <i>fuêre</i> for <i>fuerunt</i>, <i>quâm</i> for <i>quam</i>, <i>câeperit</i>-type <i>ae→ae</i>-ligature spellings, consonantal <i>u/v</i> (<i>reuerterer, conuinces, diuulgabis, significaui</i>) and <i>i/j</i> (<i>studijs, ijs, Mathematica Studia</i>) are the edition's forms, reproduced as printed; not normalised.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Bernburgi 28. Septemb. Anno 1594</i> = \"At Bernburg, 28 September 1594.\" A plain civil dateline that <b>agrees with the catalogue label</b> (\"1594. 28. september. Bernburg\"); no conversion is made. Bernburg in Anhalt kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1594 (Protestant Germany did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700), so the date is read as O.S. — flagged for completeness rather than asserted from any in-text style marker.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the dateline is unambiguous and matches the label. Named cruxes, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) <b>the identity of the deceased prince</b> — <i>Illustrissimum Principem … felicissimae recordationis</i> is identified as Joachim Ernst of Anhalt-Bernburg (d. 1586) from external scholarship, not from the letter, and the eight-year gap between his death and this 1594 letter means the <i>significaui</i> / unfulfilled-return passage refers to the prince's lifetime; the translation keeps Rothmann's tenses literal and does not harmonise them. (2) <b>the precise referent of <i>Opus … de Cometis</b></i> — whether the already-printed 1588 <i>De mundi aetherei … phaenomenis</i> or the still-unpublished further comet volume Tycho had promised; the text is kept as Rothmann's reproach without deciding it.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter (Rothmann to Tycho, Bernburg 28 Sept 1594) — no Dreyer slice exists, so no page-image collation was possible; audited against source_text alone, per protocol capped at grade B regardless of outcome. Read every clause of the Latin against the English line by line (the arthritis/nephritic-pain opening, the Frankfurt-catalogue search, the plea to publish the comet book, the Sophie Brahe garden paragraph, the closing). All names, the sole date (28 Septemb. Anno 1594), and every clause are rendered accurately with no fabricated, omitted, mistranslated, or hedge-flattened content found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 18, "prev": "brahe-t_15920220002", "next": "brahe-t_15950114001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920220002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15950114001"}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains his long absence by severe arthritis and nephritic pain, reports a partial recovery, and mentions searching Frankfurt catalogues.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15940928001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15941023001", "d": "1594-10-23", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Cort Aslaksen", "ck": "Cort Aslaksen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Siegen", "t": "Cort Aslakssøn → Tycho Brahe — Siegen, 23 October 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, VIRTUTE AC ERUDITIONE ILLUSTRI VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP ET URANIBURGO, FAUTORI SUO OMNI OBSERVANTIA COLENDO. S.</p><p>NISI quodammodo T. E. Generosissime Domine TYCHO mea erga T. E. debita observantia, et mihi tua in me benivolentia perspecta esset, vererer sane ne meum incusares officium, quod toto iam pene semestri nihil ad te literarum transmiserim. Id quod meae ingratitudini aut incuriae nequaquam ascribas velim. In ea siquidem locorum adyta hîc detrudimur, ad quae commeantibus hinc inde ad vos Tabellarijs haud ita crebro praebetur aditus. Scripsi tamen non ita dudum ad V. E. cum internuncio quodam Marpurgensi, qui Herbornae, assumpto Chartopaeo quodam, me convenerat. Is in Daniam, adhibito eiusdem Opificij comministro, se unâ confestim profecturum et te operam suam in Officina Chartacea aliquamdiu elocaturum spondebat. Verum trifurcifer iste Marpurgium rediens causa nescio qua clam se subduxit, meque ita praeter opinionem thaleris aliquot, quos pro commeatu isti nebuloni praenumeravi, callide emunxit. Sed patior hoc quieto quid est iacturae eo facilius, quod iam demum quid eiusmodi committendum sit agyrtis et vespertilionibus, meo qualicunque damno rectius sapiam. Caeterum CLAVIJ hunc in Sphaeram SACROBOSCI Commentarium ad te ablegavi ob tractatulum quendam de Nova Stella Anni 72 insertum. In quo multa, si recte memini, praesertim de generatione et situ eius Aethereo, idque inter Firmamenti Sidera, magnitudine primaeva apparenti etc. vestris non omnia dissona placitis adfert. Miror hominem Peripateticum a suae sectae Coriphaeo ARISTOTELE tam libere in plerisque his dissentire. Quo nomine CRAIGO illi Scoto iurato ARISTOTELIS mancipio, veritatis perduelli, longe, meo iudicio, anteponi merebitur. Vidi egomet non ita dudum Epistolas illius nonnullas ad te de negotio Cometico perscriptas. Inaudieram etiam a Conterraneis eius circumferri Capnuraniae istius nugamenta et cum doctis quibusdam communicari. Exoptant igitur mecum et expetunt quidam vestrarum laudum et lucubrationum studiosi, ut non tantum Apologiam Scoticis istis de Cometarum ortu, situ ac motu a vobis oppositam somnijs, sed etiam Lichnuraniam quandam, reliquas Capnuraniae illius tenebras ac densos fumos suis radijs discutientem, publici iuris faceretis. Sed quid ego noctuas Athenas? Catalogum vero hunc recentiorum auctorum addidi, si quos forte proximo mercato Francofurtensi ex ijs, qui hîc assignantur, novellis libris vobis per me coëmi expetiveritis. In quem usum, quas ex vestris pecunijs reliquas habeo, tum si quid ulterius sit erogandum, lubens impendam. Reperietis etiam his adiunctam terrae, ut vocant, sigillatae portiunculam quandam, quae tribus hinc circiter milliaribus ubertim effoditur. Quod vero OTTONI auctor et suasor fuerim, ut unâ ad te perscriberet, in meliorem partem interpreteris oro. Dudum siquidem a me petijt, ut sibi permitterem amoris et benevolentiae testificandae gratia, literas suas ad te daret. Id quod me illi tum concessurum spondebam ubi sine crassioribus mendis proprio Marte epistolam conficere valeret. Itaque iam aggressus est me suasore, et quidem felicius quam sperabam. Paucula a me limitata, nonnulla interpolata. Vides igitur non obscura ingenij hac praesertim aetate indicia. Quem in virum bonum ac doctum successu temporis pervasurum mihi persuadeo, si modo quemadmodum inceperit, ita in posterum inoffenso pede ad callem VIRTUTIS ac VERITATIS contenderit.</p><p>Vale praecipuum Patriae ornamentum, vita, ut dignus, quam diutissime, si quisquam, incolumi, meque tuo favore dignare.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale.<br>Sigenae Nassoviorum 23. Octob. Anno 1594.<br>T. amplissimi nominis honoris<br>desiderij studioss.<br>CUNRADUS BERGENSIS.</p><p>Sciatis globum cœlestem secundum vestras observationes restitutum et affabre<br>solerterque elaboratum his nundinis prodijsse, magna sane multorum gratulatione.<br>Sed nimis care æstimabatur nempe unâ cum terrestri æqualis magnitudinis 22<br>thal.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man illustrious in nobility of birth, in virtue, and in learning, Master Tycho Brahe, lord of Knudstrup and of Uraniborg, his patron to be honoured with every mark of respect — greeting.</b></p><p>Were the respect I owe to Your Excellency, most noble Lord Tycho, and the goodwill of yours toward me which I have clearly perceived, not in some measure [assured], I should indeed fear that you might reproach my dutifulness, in that for almost a whole half-year now I have sent you no letter. This I would by no means have you ascribe to ingratitude or carelessness on my part. For we are thrust away here into such remote recesses of place that, to the couriers travelling to and fro to you, access is not so very often afforded. Yet I wrote to Your Excellency not so long ago by way of a certain Marburg messenger, who had met with me at Herborn, having taken on a certain paper-maker. He gave his word that he would set out together, at once, into Denmark — having engaged a fellow-workman of the same trade — and that he would hire out his labour to you for some while in [your] paper-mill. But that thrice-gallows-rogue, returning to Marburg, made off in secret for some reason I know not, and so, contrary to my expectation, slyly fleeced me of the several thalers which I had paid in advance to that scoundrel for his journey. But I bear this trifle of a loss the more calmly because at last I may know better — at whatever cost to myself — what is to be entrusted to mountebanks and bats [= shifty night-creatures]. For the rest, I have dispatched to you this Commentary of Clavius on the <i>Sphere</i> of Sacrobosco, on account of a certain little treatise inserted [in it] concerning the New Star of the year [15]72. In which, if I remember rightly, he brings forward much — especially concerning its generation and its aetherial situation, and that among the stars of the firmament, [and] its original [first-appearing] magnitude, etc. — not all of it discordant with your views. I marvel that a Peripatetic man dissents so freely from Aristotle, the Coryphaeus [chief] of his own sect, in most of these matters. On which score he will deserve, in my judgment, to be set far before that Craig, the Scot, a sworn bondman of Aristotle and a [public] enemy of the truth. I myself saw not long ago several letters of his written out to you about the cometary affair. I had also heard that the trifles of that <i>Capnurania</i> [smoke-of-the-sky] of his are being passed about by his countrymen and shared with certain learned men. And so certain men devoted to your praises and your lucubrations long for, and seek, together with me, that you would make public not only the <i>Apologia</i> set by you against those Scottish dreams about the rising, situation, and motion of comets, but also a certain <i>Lichnurania</i> [lamp-of-the-sky], scattering with its rays the remaining shadows and dense smokes of that <i>Capnurania</i> of his. But why [do I carry] owls to Athens? I have, moreover, added this catalogue of more recent authors, in case you should perhaps wish me to buy up for you at the next Frankfurt fair some of the new books out of those that are listed here. To which use I will gladly devote whatever I have left over from your monies, and then anything further that may have to be laid out. You will also find joined to these a little portion of what they call sealed earth [<i>terra sigillata</i>], which is dug up abundantly about three miles from here. As for my having been the prompter and persuader to Otto, that he should write to you at the same time, I beg you to interpret it in the better part. For he asked me some while ago to allow him to send his own letter to you, for the sake of attesting his love and goodwill. This I then gave my word I would grant him once he was able to compose a letter by his own effort without the grosser errors. And so he has now set to it at my urging, and indeed more happily than I had hoped. A few things [were] trimmed by me, some interpolated. You see, then, no obscure signs of talent, especially at this age. I am persuaded that with the passage of time he will turn out a good and learned man, if only, just as he has begun, he will so press on hereafter with unstumbling foot toward the path of VIRTUE and TRUTH.</p><p>Farewell, foremost ornament of our country; may you, as you deserve, live as long as may be — unharmed, if anyone may be so — and deem me worthy of your favour.</p><p>Again, fare you as happily as may be.<br>At Siegen of the Nassaus, 23 October, the year 1594.<br>Most devoted to the honour of your most ample name and to [your] regard,<br>Conrad of Bergen.</p><p>You should know that a celestial globe, restored according to your observations and skilfully and ingeniously worked, has come out at this [book] fair, to the no small congratulation indeed of many. But it was valued too dearly — namely, together with a terrestrial [globe] of equal size, at 22 thalers.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Cunradus Bergensis</i> = <b>Cort (Conrad) Aslakssøn</b> (1564–1624), the Bergen-born Norwegian theologian and astronomer, at this date one of Tycho's pupils and assistants; he had left Hven and was acting abroad as Tycho's book-buyer and travelling tutor. He later became professor of theology at Copenhagen. The signature Latinises his given name (Cort/Conrad) and his birthplace (Bergen). Internal corpus confirms the role: Tycho's June-1598 letter to Grynaeus names \"Otto Brahe, who together with his tutor Cort Aslakssøn had recently been resident at Basel.\"</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Dominus de Knudstrup et Uraniburgo</i> — \"lord of <b>Knudstrup</b> [the Brahe family seat in Scania] and of <b>Uraniborg</b> [Tycho's observatory-residence on Hven].\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Sigenae Nassoviorum</i> = <b>Siegen</b>, the seat of the counts of Nassau-Siegen (Nassau-Dillenburg lands); <i>Herborna</i> = <b>Herborn</b> and <i>Marpurgum</i> = <b>Marburg</b>, both in/near Hesse — the region's Reformed academies (the Hohe Schule at Herborn; the university at Marburg), consistent with Aslakssøn's Reformed-territory itinerary.</li><li><b>John Craig (the controversy).</b> <i>Craigo illi Scoto … de negotio Cometico</i> = <b>John Craig</b> (Johannes Craigius, d. 1620), the Scottish physician and Aristotelian with whom Tycho conducted a sustained epistolary controversy over comets (whether they are sublunary or supralunary) in 1589–90, continuing into 1592; Aslakssøn says he had seen several of Craig's letters to Tycho on the matter. The corpus's letter-register lists \"CRAIGIUS Scotus\" / John Craig ↔ Tycho letters (1590–92). Aslakssøn's <i>iurato Aristotelis mancipio, veritatis perduelli</i> (\"a sworn bondman of Aristotle, an enemy of the truth\") is a partisan flourish on Tycho's behalf, kept as written.</li><li><b>Clavius / Sacrobosco.</b> <i>Clavii … in Sphaeram Sacrobosci Commentarium … de Nova Stella Anni 72</i> — <b>Christoph Clavius</b>'s <i>In Sphaeram Ioannis de Sacro Bosco commentarius</i>, which from its expanded editions carries a treatment of the <b>new star (nova) of 1572</b> (Tycho's <i>De nova stella</i>). Aslakssøn notes — with the hedge <i>si recte memini</i> (\"if I remember rightly\"), preserved — that the Jesuit Clavius, though a <i>Peripateticus</i> (Aristotelian), grants the nova an <i>aetherial situation … among the stars of the firmament</i> with a <i>primary magnitude</i> (so its location in the heavens, not the sublunary realm), positions \"not all discordant\" with Tycho's. <i>Coriphaeus</i> (Gk. κορυφαῖος, \"chorus-leader, chief\") kept as \"Coryphaeus.\"</li><li><b>Greek-derived coinages (named cruxes).</b> Two mock-learned neologisms, built on Greek and left untranslated in the body with bracketed glosses:</li><li><i>Capnurania</i> (Gk. καπνός <i>kapnós</i> \"smoke\" + οὐρανία <i>ouranía</i> \"of the heaven/sky\") — literally \"smoke-of-the-sky,\" Aslakssøn's contemptuous coinage for Craig's cometary doctrine (comets as terrestrial exhalations/smoke); rendered \"[smoke-of-the-sky].\" The precise title/sense Craig himself used (if any) is not recoverable from this letter; the coinage may be Aslakssøn's own jibe rather than a real title — flagged.</li><li><i>Lichnurania</i> (Gk. λύχνος <i>lýchnos</i> \"lamp\" + οὐρανία) — \"lamp-of-the-sky,\" the antithetical work Aslakssøn wishes Tycho would publish, \"scattering with its rays\" the <i>smokes</i> of the <i>Capnurania</i>; rendered \"[lamp-of-the-sky].\" A nonce-word coined to balance <i>Capnurania</i>; not an attested title.</li><li><b><i>Apologia</i>.</b> <i>Apologiam … Scoticis istis … somnijs … oppositam</i> — the <b>defence (Apologia)</b> Tycho is urged to publish against Craig's positions; per Nielsen's bibliography of Tycho's printed works, no such printed <i>Apologia</i> against Craig appeared (Tycho's comet polemic remained in correspondence and in the <i>De mundi</i> material). Rendered \"<i>Apologia</i>.\"</li><li><b>Proverbs / allusions.</b> <i>quid ego noctuas Athenas?</i> — \"owls to Athens\" (the Greek proverb <i>γλαῦκ' Ἀθήναζε</i>, \"to carry owls to Athens\" = to do something superfluous; the owl was Athena's bird and the city's emblem); the elliptical phrasing is kept. <i>proprio Marte</i> — \"by his own effort/unaided\" (idiom, \"by one's own Mars\"). <i>inoffenso pede</i> — \"with unstumbling foot.\"</li><li><b>Material enclosure.</b> <i>terra … sigillata</i> — <b>terra sigillata</b> (\"sealed earth\"), the medicinal clay sold in stamped cakes; Aslakssøn encloses a sample dug \"about three miles\" off. <i>milliare</i> = a (German) mile; left as \"miles,\" the period unit not modernised.</li><li><b>Persons mentioned.</b> <i>Otto</i> — the young <b>Otto (Otte) Brahe</b> whom Aslakssøn was tutoring (a kinsman in Tycho's orbit; the same Otto Brahe + Aslakssøn pairing recurs in Tycho's 1598 Grynaeus letter), here making his first attempt at a Latin letter to Tycho under Aslakssøn's supervision (<i>paucula a me limitata, nonnulla interpolata</i> — \"a few things trimmed by me, some interpolated\"). <i>Chartopaeus / Officina Chartacea</i> — a <b>paper-maker</b> and a <b>paper-mill</b>: the absconding messenger had promised to bring a paper-maker to work in Tycho's mill on Hven; <i>trifurcifer</i> (\"thrice-gallows-rogue\"), <i>nebulo</i> (\"scoundrel\"), <i>agyrtae et vespertiliones</i> (\"mountebanks and bats\") are Aslakssøn's invective for the cheat, kept in register.</li><li><b><i>thaleri</b></i> — <b>thalers</b> (the silver coin), kept untranslated.</li><li><b>Closing formulae.</b> <i>T. … desiderij studiosiss. — Cunradus Bergensis</i> rendered per §5.5; <i>Vale … Iterum … vale</i> → \"Farewell … Again, fare you …\".</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>23. Octob. Anno 1594</i> — <b>23 October 1594</b>, Old Style (Denmark and the Reformed German territories were still on the Julian calendar in 1594; the writer flags no New-Style equivalent). The dateline date agrees with the catalogue label (\"1594. 23. oktober. Siegen\"); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A newsy paragraph preceding the letter's close — Cort Aslakssøn reporting fresh book-fair intelligence to Tycho: a celestial globe built on Tycho's own corrected star positions has just appeared for sale, admired for its workmanship but criticised as overpriced.</li><li><b>Independent confirmation — this is a known, cited episode.</b> Adam Mosley's <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> reports, from Tycho's own correspondence, that \"in October of 1594, Aslachus had informed him that the van Langren instruments were available at the book-fairs, to the combined pleasure and dismay of the masses... the dismay by their price: 22 thalers for the celestial globe along with its terrestrial partner\" [Tier 1: Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (Cambridge, 2007)]. The date (October 1594), the price (22 thalers for the pair), and the informant (\"Aslachus\" = the Latinized Cort Aslakssøn) all match this letter precisely — this recovered segment is very likely the primary-source basis for Mosley's own footnote. Mosley further suggests this report may have inclined Tycho toward supporting Willem Blaeu's rival globe project instead.</li><li><b>The globe-maker identified.</b> The globes in question are almost certainly the products of the van Langren family workshop (Arnold Florent van Langren and sons), Dutch globe- and instrument-makers who had obtained Tycho's revised star catalogue and were marketing celestial/terrestrial globe pairs at the Frankfurt fair in this period.</li><li><b>The correspondent.</b> Cort Aslakssøn (Latinized Cunradus Aslacus/Aslachus Bergensis, i.e. \"of Bergen\"; here signing as \"Cunradus Bergensis\" — see the letter's own signature), a Norwegian-Danish theologian and philosopher who served among Tycho's assistants and later became a professor at Copenhagen [Tier 1: corpus, New Dictionary of Scientific Biography — \"Tycho's assistant, Cort Aslakssøn\"; Zeeberg, <i>Urania Titani</i> bibliography].</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'apparent' to 'apparenti' per Dreyer, TBOO VII p.367.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and faithfully throughout; register and hedges preserved. The flagged points are interpretive, not textual: the two Greek-derived coinages <i>Capnurania</i> / <i>Lichnurania</i> are glossed but are nonce-words whose status (real titles vs. Aslakssøn's own jibes) is not decidable from the letter, and the <i>Apologia</i> urged here was never printed. None of these affects the construal of the sentences in which they stand.</p><p>This is an unusually well-anchored fragment: its content, date, and named informant are independently corroborated almost verbatim by Mosley's modern scholarly account, which appears to draw on this very letter. The Latin itself is short and unproblematic. The one residual uncertainty is stylistic rather than factual — \"restitutum\" (the globe \"restored\"/reconstituted per Tycho's observations) is translated to match the established technical sense used elsewhere in this batch (letter brahe-t_16000308001) rather than checked against a prior published translation of this specific letter, but this does not affect the substance.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Cort Aslaksen/Cunradus Bergensis to Tycho, 23 Oct 1594) against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.366-368 page images, including a second close re-read of p.367. Every proper name (Clavius, Sacrobosco, Craig, Otto/Cunradus Bergensis), number (22 thalers, three miles, year 1594/72), and closing formula matches Dreyer word for word except the single dropped letter noted above. English translation is otherwise accurate and complete throughout, including the postscript.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads the participle/adjective 'apparenti' as the finite verb 'apparent' (dropped final -i); a one-letter corruption relative to Dreyer's printed text. Does not materially mislead the English, which already renders the sense adjectivally. Dreyer prints: <i>magnitudine primaeva apparenti etc. (TBOO VII p.367, line ~15)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the single defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VII p.367), which clearly reads 'magnitudine primaeva apparenti etc.'. A one-letter correction; the translation was already adjectival and needed no change. No uncertainty remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Cort Aslaksen", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15960219001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15960219001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for six months of silence due to remote location, reporting a previous letter sent via a Marburg messenger accompanying a paper-maker for Tycho's mill.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15941023001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15941108001", "d": "1594-11-08", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 8 November 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Sjællandske Tegnelser XVIII, 368 (chancery register copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TILL TYGE BRAHE HELLIGE THREE KONGERS CAPELLIS BYGNING WDI ROSCHILDE DOMBKIRCKE AHNRØRENDIS VT SEQUITUR.</p><p>Christian 4..</p><p>WOR gunst thilfornn. Wiid, eptersom Wij nogen thiid siden forleeden naadigst haffue schreffuit thig thill om Hellige tre kongers capell wdi Roschilde dombkircke, som wor møgit bygfeldig och brøstholden, attu thend med thet allerførste schulle lade igien forferdige och forbygge, och du nu wnderdanigst lader giffue thilkiende, att samme capell beßuerligen kand igien forferdigis och siiden holdis wed heffd och magt enten met thag eller wdi andre maade, med mindre att thend øffuerste huelning motte affthagis och igien leggis med bielcker och loffte. Tha paa thet samme capell med thet første motte igien bliffue forferdigit och siiden theß bedre kunde holdis med thag och anden nottorfft wed heffd och bygning, bede Wij thig och wille, attu lader samme øffuerste huelling affthage och thet igien lader legge met bielcker och loffte paa the beste maade forwarit, och for:ne bygning paa for:ne capell med thet allerførste schee kand lader ferdig giørre.</p><p>Ther met etc. Befallendis etc.<br>Hafniae 8. 9bris a:o 94.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tyge Brahe, concerning the building of the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings in Roskilde Cathedral, as follows.</b></p><p>Christian IV.</p><p>Our favour beforehand. Know that, whereas we did some time ago now graciously write to you concerning the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings in Roskilde Cathedral, which was much in disrepair and decayed, that you should at the very first have it repaired and rebuilt again — and you now most humbly give us to understand that the same chapel can but with difficulty be repaired again and thereafter kept in good repair and condition, whether by roofing or in any other manner, unless the uppermost vaulting were to be taken down and laid again with beams and a ceiling: therefore, so that the same chapel may at the first be repaired again and thereafter be the better kept in repair and building with roofing and other necessaries, we ask of you and will [it], that you have the same uppermost vaulting taken down, and have it laid again with beams and a ceiling secured in the best manner, and have the aforesaid building-work upon the aforesaid chapel made ready at the very first that may be done.</p><p>Therewith, etc. Commending [you to God], etc.<br>At Copenhagen, 8 November, in the year [15]94.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), then ruling under the regency council in his minority (he was crowned in his own right in 1596). The letter is a chancery instruction issued in his name.</li><li><b>Recipient and capacity.</b> <b>Tyge (Tycho) Brahe</b>, here addressed not as royal astronomer but as a <b>canon (prebendary) of Roskilde Cathedral</b>: from 1579 Tycho held the prebend (canonry) of the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings (<i>Helligtrekongers kapel</i>), a substantial Roskilde benefice whose income was charged with the upkeep of the chapel fabric. The crown is calling on him to discharge that maintenance obligation.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A chancery-register entry (Sjællandske Tegnelser XVIII, 368). The <b>Latin rubric</b> <i>\"… AHNRØRENDIS <b>ut sequitur</b>\"</i> (\"concerning … <b>as follows</b>\") heads the Danish body — a code-switch preserved per protocol §2. <i>AHNRØRENDIS</i> = <i>anrørendes</i> (\"concerning\"); cf. the parallel formula in the 1584 Frederik II compass-letter.</li><li><b>Subject.</b> <i>Hellige tre kongers capell</i> = the <b>Chapel of the Three Holy Kings</b> (the Magi), a late-Gothic chapel adjoining Roskilde Cathedral, the royal burial church of Denmark. <i>bygfeldig och brøstholden</i> — a chancery doublet, \"in disrepair and decayed / dilapidated.\"</li><li><b>The structural crux of the order.</b> The substance turns on a single conditional: <i>med mindre att thend øffuerste huelning motte affthagis och igien leggis med bielcker och loffte</i> = \"<b>unless</b> the uppermost vaulting were to be taken down and laid again with beams and a ceiling.\" Tycho had reported (the king relays) that the chapel could <i>not</i> be durably repaired by re-roofing alone <b>unless</b> the top vault were removed and replaced by a flat timber beam-and-board ceiling; the king accordingly <b>commands</b> exactly that course. The translation preserves the report → exception → command chain without smoothing it; <i>huelning/huelling</i> = \"vaulting\" (the masonry vault), <i>bielcker och loffte</i> = \"beams and a [timber] ceiling/loft.\"</li><li><b>Negation / conditional check.</b> The body contains exactly <b>one</b> negating/limiting operator — <i>med mindre</i> (\"unless\"), governing the vaulting clause — and one near-negative adverb, <i>beßuerligen</i> (\"with difficulty / hardly\"); both are reproduced and neither added nor dropped. <i>enten … eller … wdi andre maade</i> = \"whether [by roofing] … or in any other manner\" is a disjunction, not a negation. The directive <i>bede Wij thig och wille</i> is a polite-but-binding royal command (\"we ask of you and will it\"); rendered with its commanding force intact.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594).</b> <i>WOR gunst thilfornn</i> = <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> (\"Our favour beforehand,\" the royal address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>Wiid</i> = <i>Vid</i> (\"Know\"); <i>eptersom</i> = <i>eftersom</i> (\"whereas, inasmuch as\"); <i>attu</i> = <i>at du</i>; <i>schreffuit</i> = <i>skrevet</i>; <i>forferdige / forferdigis</i> = <i>forfærdige(s)</i> (\"repair, put in order\"); <i>forbygge</i> = \"build up/rebuild\"; <i>wnderdanigst</i> = <i>underdanigst</i> (\"most humbly/submissively\"); <i>thilkiende</i> = <i>til kende</i> (\"give to understand\"); <i>beßuerligen</i> = <i>besværligen</i> (\"with difficulty\"); <i>holdis wed heffd och magt</i> = <i>holdes ved hævd og magt</i> (\"kept in repair and condition\"); <i>thag</i> = <i>tag</i> (\"roof\"); <i>øffuerste huelning/huelling</i> = <i>øverste hvælving</i> (\"uppermost vaulting\"); <i>affthagis / affthage</i> = <i>aftages / aftage</i> (\"be taken down / take down\"); <i>bielcker</i> = <i>bjælker</i> (\"beams\"); <i>loffte</i> = <i>lofte</i> (\"ceiling, loft\"); <i>nottorfft</i> = <i>nødtørft</i> (\"necessaries, what is needful\"); <i>for:ne</i> = <i>fornævnte</i> (\"aforesaid,\" the standard chancery abbreviation); <i>schee kand</i> = <i>ske kan</i> (\"may be done\").</li><li><b>Closing formula.</b> <i>Ther met etc. Befallendis etc.</i> — the abbreviated chancery sign-off; <i>Befallendis</i> = <i>Befalendes</i> (\"Commending [you to God / to God's protection]\"), the conventional epistolary commendation, here truncated with <i>etc.</i> and so completed in brackets.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Hafniae 8. 9bris a:o 94</i> = \"At <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafniae</i>), 8 November ( <i>9bris</i> = November, the ninth month of the Roman year reckoned from March) [15]94\" (O.S.). The Latin place-name and month-abbreviation in an otherwise Danish letter are preserved per §2; <i>9bris</i> is the standard early-modern shorthand for <i>Novembris</i>, not \"September.\" The dateline (8 November 1594) and the catalogue label (8 November 1594, Copenhagen) agree; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the single <i>med mindre</i> conditional governing the vaulting is reproduced exactly; the one soft point is the precise architectural sense of <i>bielcker och loffte</i> (a flat timber beam-and-plank ceiling replacing the masonry vault), glossed above rather than over-specified.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the only soft point is glossing precision of </i>bielcker och loffte<i>; conditional + negations exact, sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.69 (Dreyer letter 104, Christian IV to Tycho on the Roskilde chapel roof) as a page image; letter entire on this page (bar 'och'/'og' spelling variants, ignored per house convention). Danish source_text matches Dreyer including the vaulting/roofing instructions and dateline 'Haffniae 8. 9bris a:o 94'. English translation checked clause-by-clause: correctly expands '9bris' to November and 'for:ne' to 'aforesaid'; no invented content, no dropped clauses.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Sjaellandske tegnelser, XVIII, 368."], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15940904001", "next": "brahe-t_15970710001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970710001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Authorizes replacing the Roskilde chapel vaulting with beams and orders the renovation of the Chapel of the Three Holy Kings completed without delay.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15941108001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15941111001", "d": "1594-11-11", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "The Rector and Professors of the University of Copenhagen → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 11 November 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin register rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums kopibøger, 1213-02 (1594–1613) (consistory copy-book)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>COPIE AFF ITT BREFF, SOM BLEFF SCHREFFUEN THILL TYGE BRAHE.</p><p>VORES ganske venlig helßen nu oc allthid forsent med Gud. Gunstige welbiurdige Tyge Brahe, nest wores wenlig tacksigelße for all beuiste aere oc gode, giffuer wi eder thillkiende, att oß nu nyligen er forekommen en woris tienere, Lauge vdj Klare mølle, met klagemaall po en aff eders thienere ved naffn Peder, i Hellig 3 kongers mølle, huilched hand schrifftlig vdj dette medfølgendis breff forfatted oc oß offuerantuordet haffuer. So wilde wi, førend wi wores bonde met retten forsuare, lade eder, som wores universitetz gode wen oc fordrer, saadant wide, begierendis ganske gierne her om eders raad oc gode suar, om i selff wille straffe eders thienere, som denne fattige mand i so maade haffuer forwretted, eller wi hannem for saadant skall lade thilltale, huilcked wi dog icke gierne giorde, der som wi wiste, attj derudoffuer skulle lade eder i nogen mode fortørne, men megit heller giør allthid gierne, huer y sit sted, hues eder thill aere, willie oc thieniste kand werre. Dett kiende Gud, huilchen wi will her med haffue eder befaled.</p><p>Datum Hafniae 11. novembris anni 1594.<br>Rector och maenige professores<br>academiae Hafniensis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of a letter that was written to Tyge Brahe.</b></p><p>Our wholly friendly greeting, now and always, sent in advance with God. Gracious, well-born Tyge Brahe: next after our friendly thanks for all the honour and kindness shown [us], we make known to you that there has lately come before us one of our servants, Lauge at Klare Mill, with a complaint against one of your servants, by name Peder, at the Mill of the Holy Three Kings, which [complaint] he has set down in writing in this accompanying letter and handed over to us. So we wished — before we defend our peasant at law — to let you, as our University's good friend and patron, know of such a matter, asking very gladly herein for your counsel and good answer: whether you yourself will punish your servant, who has in this manner wronged this poor man, or [whether] we shall have him prosecuted for such [conduct] — which, however, we would not gladly do, if we knew that thereby you should in any way be displeased; but [we] much rather do always gladly, each in his own place, whatever may be to your honour, goodwill, and service. That God knows, to whom we will herewith have commended you.</p><p>Given at Copenhagen, 11 November in the year 1594.<br>The Rector and the general body of professors<br>of the University of Copenhagen.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Senders.</b> The <i>Rector och maenige professores academiae Hafniensis</i> = the <b>Rector and the assembled professors of the University of Copenhagen</b> (the <i>Consistorium</i>, the university's governing senate). <i>Maenige</i> (= <i>menige</i>) here is the chancery sense \"common / general (body),\" i.e. the full collegium, not \"ordinary.\" The letter survives as the consistory's own register copy (hence the Danish rubric <i>COPIE AFF ITT BREFF</i> heading it).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Tyge Brahe</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, addressed as the university's <i>gode wen oc fordrer</i> (\"good friend and patron / promoter\"). <i>Fordrer</i> (= <i>forfremmer / befordrer</i>) carries the sense of one who advances or furthers the institution's interests — rendered \"patron.\"</li><li><b>The dispute.</b> A jurisdictional courtesy over a quarrel between two millers' households: <i>Lauge vdj Klare mølle</i> — <b>Lauge (Laurids) at Klare Mill</b>, a tenant (\"servant\"/<i>bonde</i>) of the university — has lodged a written complaint against <i>Peder, i Hellig 3 kongers mølle</i> — <b>Peder at the Mill of the Holy Three Kings</b> (<i>Hellig 3 kongers mølle</i>, the mill named for the Magi), one of Tycho's servants. The two mills lay near Copenhagen; the university defers to Tycho on whether he will discipline his own man before it takes the matter to law on its tenant's behalf.</li><li><b>Legal sense — handled with care.</b> The operative offer is a strict either/or framed as a request for Tycho's <i>raad oc gode suar</i>: <i>om i selff wille straffe eders thienere … eller wi hannem … skall lade thilltale</i> = \"<b>whether</b> you yourself will punish your servant … <b>or</b> [whether] we shall have him prosecuted.\" The single negation in the courtesy clause is reproduced exactly: <i>huilcked wi dog <b>icke</b> gierne giorde, der som wi wiste, attj derudoffuer skulle lade eder … fortørne</i> = \"which, however, we would <b>not</b> gladly do, if we knew that thereby you should be displeased.\" The clause is a conditional courtesy (the university would rather Tycho act himself than risk offending him by prosecuting his servant); its mood is the past subjunctive/conditional of polite deference (<i>wilde … giorde … wiste … skulle</i>), rendered as such without sharpening into an assertion or a threat.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594; German-influenced spelling).</b> <i>helßen</i> = <i>hilsen</i> (\"greeting\"); <i>forsent</i> = <i>forsendt</i> (\"sent ahead/forward\"); <i>welbiurdige</i> = <i>velbyrdige</i> (\"well-born,\" the standard address to nobility); <i>nest</i> = <i>næst</i> (\"next [after]\"); <i>tacksigelße</i> = <i>taksigelse</i> (\"thanks\"); <i>beuiste</i> = <i>beviste</i> (\"shown, demonstrated\"); <i>thillkiende … giffuer</i> = <i>giver til kende</i> (\"make known\"); <i>forekommen</i> = <i>forekommet</i> (\"come before [us]\"); <i>klagemaall</i> = <i>klagemål</i> (\"complaint, plaint\"); <i>po</i> = <i>på</i> (\"against / upon\"); <i>huilched</i> = <i>hvilket</i> (\"which\"); <i>medfølgendis</i> = <i>medfølgende</i> (\"accompanying, enclosed\"); <i>forfatted</i> = <i>forfattet</i> (\"composed, set down\"); <i>offuerantuordet</i> = <i>overantvordet</i> (\"handed over, delivered\"); <i>forsuare</i> = <i>forsvare</i> (\"defend [at law]\"); <i>fordrer</i> = <i>forfremmer / befordrer</i> (\"patron, furtherer\"); <i>begierendis</i> = <i>begærende</i> (\"asking, desiring\"); <i>straffe</i> = \"punish\"; <i>forwretted</i> = <i>forurettet</i> (\"wronged\"); <i>lade thilltale</i> = <i>lade tiltale</i> (\"have prosecuted / proceeded against at law\"); <i>attj</i> = <i>at I</i> (\"that you,\" polite plural); <i>derudoffuer</i> = <i>derudover</i> (\"thereby, on that account\"); <i>i nogen mode</i> = <i>i nogen måde</i> (\"in any way\"); <i>fortørne</i> = <i>fortørne</i> (\"anger, displease, offend\"); <i>hues</i> = <i>hvad/hvis</i> (\"whatever\"); <i>werre</i> = <i>være</i> (\"be\"); <i>kiende</i> = <i>kende</i> (\"know\"); <i>befaled</i> = <i>befalet</i> (\"commended, entrusted\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Hafniae 11. novembris anni 1594</i> — the dateline is in Latin: \"<b>Given at Copenhagen</b> (<i>Hafnia</i>), 11 November in the year 1594\" (O.S.; Denmark held to the Julian calendar until 1700). The Latin signature rubric <i>Rector och maenige professores academiae Hafniensis</i> code-switches Danish <i>och maenige</i> between the Latin <i>Rector</i> and <i>academiae Hafniensis</i> — the mixed register preserved per protocol §2. Dateline and catalogue label agree (11 November 1594); no date discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the single negation and the either/or are reproduced exactly. The only soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: the precise civil status of <i>bonde</i>/<i>tienere</i> (the university's \"peasant\" Lauge vs. the verbal courtesy of calling both parties <i>tienere</i> \"servants\"), and the exact administrative force of <i>lade thilltale</i> (\"have [him] formally prosecuted / arraigned\"), neither of which the text spells out further.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — residue is mere source-underspecification (</i>bonde<i> status, </i>lade thilltale<i>); negation + either/or reproduced exactly.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish letter from the Rector and professors of the University of Copenhagen against TBOO XIV pp.69-70 page images, checking every name (Lauge, Peder), place (Klare Mølle, Hellig Tre Kongers Mølle), and date (11 November 1594). Text and English translation match Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Konsistoriums kopibøger, 1213-02 (1594-1613)."], "th": {"label": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15941207001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15941207001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presents a complaint from their miller Lauge against Tycho's servant Peder over a mill dispute, asking Tycho to punish his man or allow legal proceedings.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15941111001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15941207001", "d": "1594-12-07", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → The Rector and Professors of the University of Copenhagen — Uraniborg, 7 December 1594 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with embedded Latin formulae and a Latin legal maxim) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Københavns Universitets Arkiv, [old signature (VP) no. 501]</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO D:NO RECTORJ OC HEDERLIGE OC HØYLERDE PROFESSORIBUS Y KIØBENHAFFNS VNIUERSITET, MINE SYNDERLIGE GODE WENNER, GANDSKE WENLIGENN THILLSCHREFFUIT.</p><p>MAGNIFICE d:ne rector och hederlige och høylerde professores vdj Kiøbenhaffns vniversitet, synderlige gode venner. Nest min wenlig tacksigelse for allt beuiist gode, huilchet at forskylle ieg stedße vill findis aff formuenn willig, giffuendis eder der hoes gandske wenligen thilckiende, att eders schriffuelse er mig for nogle dage thillhende kommen, huor vdj y lod mig forstaa om eders thiener vid naffn Lauge møller vdj Klare mølle y Roskiilde, at hand haffuer ført nogenn klagemaall offuer en aff mine thiener vid naffn Peder møller, som ligger til Kongernis cappell sammesteds, huilche hans klagemaall schrifftligen forfattede y mig med samme eders breff tillkickedt haffuer. Derhoes begierendis, att ieg selff samme min thienner wilde straffe, som eders møllere y saa maade haffde foruretted, eller oc lade eder hannom tilltale for retten. Saa kand ieg eder her paa till itt wenligt suar icke forholle, att ieg strax schreff Mogens Bertellßønn, forstander y hospitalidt der j Kiøbenhaffnn, blant andet till, at handt vilde giffue eder paa mine wegne thilckiende, att ieg vilde haffue budt epther min møller och haalde hannom slige klagemaal forre, och høre huad hand der thil haffde at suare, siiden vilde ieg schriffue eder beskeen der paa igien, giffuendis ether selff att betencke, at ieg icke saa strax paa den andens klagemaall och beretning kunde beskylle och straffe min thiener aldelis vhørt oc foruden nøyagtig beuisning offuer hannom, quia (utj scitis) audienda etiam altera pars, et conuincenda antequam puniatur, et procedendum secundum leges receptas, si aliter causa transigi nequeat. Haffuer ieg derforre strax sendt bud epther for:ne min møller, oc ladet lest for hannom eders møllers schrifftlige klagemaall, och giffuid hannem respiit, ocsaa schrifftligen at suare der till, huilcke hans giensuar oc replication ieg sender ether her hoes. Dersom den icke siunis eder at kunde følge skell, och hans gienpart icke will lade sig nøye der met, kand ieg for min personn well liide, att de taler hiinn anden thill med loug och rett, oc at der bliffuer paa deris werne thing legittime citeret oc deciderit vdj sagen, thj y skulle ingelunde giøre mig imod der medt, att y lader forsuare ethers thiener och lader min thiltale, saa wiit y kunde giøre med loug och ret. Jeg will icke heller haffue wiidere der imod att siige eller minn thiener att handtheffue, end som billigt och ret kand verre. Huilchet ieg haffde at giffue ether thill it wenligt suar paa eders schriffuilße vdigien tilckiende. Tackendis ether der hoes gandske gierne, att y wilde lade mig slig leylighedt thillschriffue, før end y secundum strictum jus lod procedere der medt. Och will gierne slig och ald anden beuiist gode och welluillighet hoes eder alle och en huer vdj sehr, saa wiit mig mueligt kand werre, stedße findis gandske goduillig igien att forskylle, y huis maade ieg wedt ether thill aere och gode kandt werre, Och vill her med nu och alltiid haffue ether Gud allermegtigste befahledt.</p><p>Datum Vraniborg den 7. dag decembris a:o 94.<br>Tycho Brahe<br>manu pp:a.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Magnificent Master Rector and the honourable and most learned Professors in the University of Copenhagen, my especial good friends, written quite amicably.</b></p><p>Magnificent Master Rector and honourable and most learned Professors in the University of Copenhagen, [my] especial good friends. Next [after] my friendly thanks for all kindness shown — which I shall ever be found willing to repay according to my ability — I make known to you herewith, quite amicably, that your letter reached my hands some days ago, in which you gave me to understand concerning your servant by name Lauge Møller [the miller] at Klare Mølle in Roskilde, that he has lodged a certain complaint against one of my servants by name Peder Møller [the miller], who belongs to the Kings' Chapel in that same place, which complaint of his, drawn up in writing, you have sent to me with that same letter of yours. Therewith requesting that I myself would punish that same servant of mine, who had in such manner wronged your miller, or else let you proceed against him at law. So I cannot withhold from you here, by way of a friendly reply, that I straightway wrote, among other matters, to Mogens Bertelsen, the warden at the hospital there in Copenhagen, [asking] that he would on my behalf give you to understand that I would have sent for my miller and put such complaints to him, and hear what he had to answer thereto, [and] thereafter I would write you an account of it in return — giving you yourself to consider that I could not so straightway, on the other [party's] complaint and report, accuse and punish my servant altogether unheard and without sufficient proof against him, <i>quia (uti scitis) audienda etiam altera pars, et convincenda antequam puniatur, et procedendum secundum leges receptas, si aliter causa transigi nequeat</i> [because, as you know, the other party too must be heard, and must be convicted before being punished, and one must proceed according to the received laws, if the case cannot be settled otherwise]. I have therefore straightway sent for my aforesaid miller, and had your miller's written complaint read out to him, and given him a respite [in which] to answer it, likewise in writing, which answer and reply of his I send you herewith. If it does not seem to you to be able to satisfy reason, and his counterpart [i.e. the opposing party] will not be content therewith, I can well bear, for my own person, that they address one another at law and in [the] court, and that there be lawful summons issued and the matter decided at their proper venue [thing]; for you shall by no means thereby act against me by your defending your servant and letting [proceedings be brought against] mine, so far as you can do with law and justice. Neither will I have anything further to say against it, nor [seek] to uphold my servant, beyond what may be fair and just. This [is what] I had to give you to understand, by way of a friendly reply to your letter in return. Thanking you withal quite gladly that you were willing to write to me of such occasion, before you proceeded therein <i>secundum strictum jus</i> [according to strict law]. And I will gladly be found ever quite well-disposed to repay such, and all other kindness and goodwill, with you all and with each one in particular, so far as may be possible for me, in whatever manner I may be able to [serve] your honour and good. And herewith [I] will now and always have you commended to God Almighty.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 7th day of December, in the year [15]94.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, his observatory-estate; the subscription <i>manu pp:a.</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand\"). The hand here is the chancery-Danish register of a magnate, not Tycho's Latin scholarly idiom.</li><li><b>Recipients.</b> The <b>Rector and Professors of the University of Copenhagen</b> (<i>Kiøbenhaffns vniversitet</i>), addressed collectively in their corporate capacity as patrons of the Roskilde mill in dispute. The university held the <b>Klare Mølle</b> (Clare Mill) at Roskilde and figures here as the opposing servant's master.</li><li><b>The dispute.</b> A milling-jurisdiction quarrel between two millers: <b>Lauge Møller</b> (Lauge the miller) at <i>Klare Mølle</i> in <b>Roskilde</b>, the university's tenant, and <b>Peder Møller</b> (Peder the miller), Tycho's man, who <i>ligger til Kongernis cappell</i> — \"belongs to / is attached to the <b>Kings' Chapel</b>\" (<i>Kongernes Kapel</i>, the royal burial chapel at Roskilde Cathedral, with whose mill-rights Tycho was associated). The surname <i>Møller</i> in both cases is the occupational designation \"the miller,\" not a fixed family name; left untranslated in the body with the gloss \"[the miller]\" on first occurrence.</li><li><b><i>Mogens Bertelsen</b></i> (<i>Mogens Bertellßønn</i>), <i>forstander y hospitalidt</i> — the <b>warden (steward) of the hospital</b> in Copenhagen, through whom Tycho relayed his initial reply; identified by office.</li><li><b>Latin legal maxim</b> (the rhetorical and legal centre of the letter): <i>quia (uti scitis) audienda etiam altera pars, et convincenda antequam puniatur, et procedendum secundum leges receptas, si aliter causa transigi nequeat.</i> Rendered \"because, as you know, the other party too must be heard, and must be convicted before being punished, and one must proceed according to the received laws, if the case cannot be settled otherwise.\" This is the <i>audiatur et altera pars</i> principle in gerundive form; the three gerundives (<i>audienda … convincenda … procedendum</i>) carry the force of obligation (\"must be heard / must be convicted / one must proceed\"). The closing conditional <i>si aliter causa transigi nequeat</i> — \"if the case cannot be settled otherwise\" — qualifies only the <i>procedendum</i> clause (recourse to formal law as a last resort, after amicable settlement fails); the deponent <i>nequeat</i> is a genuine negative (\"cannot\"), preserved.</li><li><b><i>secundum strictum jus</b></i> — \"according to strict law\"; <i>secundum leges receptas</i> — \"according to the received laws.\" Tycho contrasts the university's amicable forewarning with proceeding <i>by strict right</i>, and thanks them for the former.</li><li><b>Negation count and legal force.</b> The body's substantive negations were tracked and reproduced one-for-one: (1) <i>icke forholle</i> — \"[I] cannot withhold\"; (2) <i>ieg icke saa strax … kunde beskylle och straffe</i> — \"I could not so straightway accuse and punish\"; (3) <i>aldelis vhørt oc foruden nøyagtig beuisning</i> — \"altogether unheard and without sufficient proof\" (two privatives, both kept); (4) <i>icke siunis … at kunde følge skell</i> — \"does not seem … able to satisfy reason\"; (5) <i>icke will lade sig nøye</i> — \"will not be content\"; (6) <i>y skulle ingelunde giøre mig imod</i> — \"you shall by no means act against me\"; (7) <i>Jeg will icke heller haffue wiidere der imod att siige</i> — \"Neither will I have anything further to say against it\"; (8) <i>si aliter causa transigi nequeat</i> — \"if the case cannot be settled otherwise.\" No negation was added or dropped.</li><li><b><i>thing</b></i> (<i>werne thing</i>, <i>deris werne thing</i>) — the <b>thing</b>, the local legal assembly/court of competent venue; <i>werne</i> = <i>værne(-)</i>, \"[their] proper/protective [venue].\" Rendered \"their proper venue (thing).\"</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1594).</b> <i>thillschreffuit</i> = <i>tilskrevet</i> (\"written to\"); <i>synderlige</i> = <i>synderlige</i> (\"especial, particular\"); <i>beuiist gode</i> = <i>bevist gode</i> (\"kindness shown\"); <i>forskylle</i> = <i>forskylde</i> (\"repay, requite\"); <i>thilckiende … giffue</i> = <i>give til kende</i> (\"give to understand, make known\"); <i>thillhende kommen</i> = <i>til hænde kommen</i> (\"come to hand\"); <i>klagemaall</i> = <i>klagemål</i> (\"complaint, grievance\"); <i>foruretted</i> = <i>forurettet</i> (\"wronged\"); <i>tilltale for retten</i> = \"proceed against / arraign at law\"; <i>respiit</i> = <i>respit</i> (\"respite, time allowed\"); <i>giensuar oc replication</i> = \"answer and reply (rejoinder)\"; <i>skell</i> = <i>skel</i> (\"reason, equity, what is right\"); <i>gienpart</i> = <i>genpart</i> (here \"the counterpart / opposing party\"); <i>legittime citeret oc deciderit</i> = a Danish–Latin code-switch, \"lawfully summoned and decided\"; <i>billigt och ret</i> = \"fair and just\"; <i>befahledt</i> = <i>befalet</i> (\"commended\").</li><li><b>Code-switching.</b> The letter is Danish throughout with three Latin intrusions of rising formality: the address rubric (<i>Magnifico D:no Rectori … Professoribus</i>), the embedded legal maxim, and the tag <i>secundum strictum jus</i> / <i>legittime … deciderit</i> — all preserved per protocol §2, the maxim translated in brackets after the Latin.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Vraniborg den 7. dag decembris a:o 94</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b>, the 7th day of December, [15]94\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dataset label (\"1594. 7. december. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Danish and the embedded Latin are rendered securely and the negations reproduced exactly. The only soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (i) <i>gienpart</i> in \"hans gienpart icke will lade sig nøye\" is read as \"his counterpart / the opposing party [will not be content]\" rather than \"his counterpart [= copy of the document],\" the contextually stronger but not certain reading; (ii) <i>werne thing</i> is taken as \"their proper venue (the thing-court),\" the <i>værne-ting</i> of competent jurisdiction. Neither affects the letter's plain sense: Tycho insists on due process (<i>audiatur et altera pars</i>) before punishing his miller, and consents to ordinary litigation if amicable resolution fails.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.69-72 as page images (letter no. 106, spanning pp.70-71). Collated the Danish source_text word-for-word against the printed page, including the inline Latin maxim (audienda etiam altera pars... et procedendum secundum leges receptas, si aliter causa transigi nequeat, confirmed present on p.71 lines 9-10), all proper names (Lauge Møller, Peder Møller, Mogens Bertelsen, Roskilde, Klare Mølle, Kongernis cappell), and the closing date/signature (Datum Vraniborg den 7. dag decembris a:o 94, Tycho Brahe manu pp:a). No divergence found. Checked the English translation clause by clause against the Danish; all renderings, including the bracketed [the miller] and [asking]/[and] supplements, are faithful and unembellished.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Københavns Universitets Arkiv, [gammel signatur (VP) nr. 501]."], "th": {"label": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15941111001", "next": "brahe-t_15950111001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15941111001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to the mill complaint by reporting that the opposing party refused a hearing and the offered transcript of five letters, leaving no further obligation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15941207001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15950111001", "d": "1595-01-11", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → The Rector and Professors of the University of Copenhagen — Copenhagen, 11 January 1595 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with a Latin rubric and Latin code-switches) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Københavns Universitets Arkiv, [old shelfmark (VP) no. 501]</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[AD RECTOREM ET PROFESSORES UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS].</p><p>DETTE erre mine suar till den vidisse, som magnificus dominus rector loedt mig y gaardt offuerantuor[de]. Effterdj ieg lod selff formedelst doctor Niell[s] Krag och Mogens Berttellßen, som vaare vdtßende aff mig, tilbyde den, som sagen ahngaar, der de fem breffue vaare lest for hannem, att der som hand haffde nogett der imodt att sige, waar ieg erbødig att suare der thill y magnifici domini rectoris och nogle aff professorum deris neruerellße, entten privatim eller publice, och der som hand nogitt sliigt wille, schulle hand giernne bekomme en vdtskrifft aff alle breffuene paa min egen bekostning. Huilckedt bleff hannom thidre end engang ahnbødit, alligeuell at hand icke selff wille schriffue paa breffuene, att de waare leste for hannom, och hand da in continenti intedt sligt, som hannem godtuilligen waar offereret, wille begerre och annamme, mens lod dennum beholle samme breffue, saa at de mig om afftenen bleffue igien offuerantuordede. Siunis mig derforre, att ieg icke nu haffuer y behoff att giffue hannom nogen copier aff samme breffue, wden saa waar, at hand tilbødt siig att wille billigen replicere nogit der wdj, som macht paalaahe, och beuiße det anderledis, och aff sig selff, waar der hos begierrendis, at got folck maatte sige och kiende der paa, da er ieg till sindtz gerne at lade hannem faa dem alle vdtschreffne, och att hannem tilstedis att haffue tilbørliig tiid och respitt at betencke sig der paa, och føre der imodt, hues hannom ßelff lyster. Och vill ieg da gerne lade mig nøye met hues magnificus dominus rector och professores her y vniuersitetett rettuißelliigenn kiende och affsiige derpaa, beßynderliigen huad de fornemste puncther och hoffuidt sagen y sig selff ahngaar, som er, om karlen haffuer holdit sitt løffte, ordt och breffue och derhos andre got folckis ordt och breffue, paa hans wegne och met hans willie och widtschab vdtgiffne, saa erligen och oprechteligen, som dett sig burde, och om ieg, eller nogen paa mine wegne, haffuer giffuett hannem nogen skellig aarsage att handle der imodt. Dersom hand sligtt aff sig selff er begierrendis (huorudj ieg dog, om mueligt vaar, hannem gierne wille haffue forschaanit), schall hand (som sagt er) met det første bekomme sandtferdiige copier aff alle de fem breffue, som bleffue leste for hannem, och hues andre hand paaesker. Ellers beßuer ieg mig nu paa ny hannem sligt at tilbyde eller lade wederfaris, paa det mine och min kierre daathers skriffuellßer icke schulle bliffue wrangeligen y min affuerelße vdtydett och mißbrugtt. Dog dersom magnificus dominus rector met sine assessoribus et collegis wille och kunde met rette kende, at mig bør sliigtt, hues copierne aff breffuene belanger hannem at lade faa, nu at effterlade, vahnßeett at de erre hannum tilforn forgebs tilbødne, och alligeuell at sagen icke bliffuer legitime conoscerit och vrdelit, och de ville giffue mig den deris sententz beschreffuen, da er ieg erbødig at wille rette mig dereffter, och giøre siden dervdj, hues billigt kand werre, och som handelen y sig selff wdtkreffuer. Hues det andett belanger, at ieg schulle icke lade lesße for got folck samme breffue, dett achter ieg, at ingen kand eller bør mig formene, eller anderledis billigen begerre, ey heller at komme wiidt de forulempninger, ieg haffuer ladett hannem adtuare att haffue y fordrag, och icke io at maa siige, hues hand kan bestaa met, mens at mig bør endelligen sligt at giøre, paa dett gottfolck kunde komme y en rett grund om sagsens leyliighedt, som dennem tilforn waar vbewist. Helst effterdj hand och hans ahnhang haffuer nu en gantsche maanidt omkring her y byen och anderstedtz giffuett allehonde forre, till at smycke hans sag met, och att wende all skylden paa mig och mine, anderledis end ieg formener, att dett y sandheedt schall befindis, saa att mange gott folck waare derudtoffuer kommen y en wrang mening och mißforstandt, før de en part finge leyligheden der om rett at wide. Syntis derforre mig at werre aldelis fornøden, att ieg lodtt lesße samme skriffter for nogle fornemme gott folck, och berettede dennom derhos leyligheeden wdj all sandtheedt, icke for ieg achtede at lade hannem wederfaris derudtoffuer nogett onde,mens alleniste mig och mine till en sandtferdig och fornødenn vndtschyldtning. Huilcken min betenckning, met hues anden rettigheedt wdj denne sag nu eder hereffter som fornøden kandt werre att tale paa, och mig giffues billig aarsage till, ieg will aldelis haffue mig forbehollen.</p><p>Actum Kiøbenhaffn den 11 ianvarii anno 1595.<br>Tyghe Brahe<br>met egen hd.</p>", "en": "<p>[TO THE RECTOR AND PROFESSORS OF THE UNIVERSITY OF COPENHAGEN].</p><p>THIS is my answer to the attestation [<i>vidisse</i>] which the Magnificent Lord Rector had delivered over to me yesterday. Inasmuch as I myself, through Doctor Niels Krag and Mogens Bertelsen, who had been sent out by me, caused an offer to be made to the man whom the matter concerns — when the five letters had been read out to him — that, if he had anything to say against them, I was ready to answer to it in the presence of the Magnificent Lord Rector and some of the professors, whether privately or publicly; and that if he wished anything of the kind, he should gladly receive a transcript of all the letters at my own expense. Which was offered to him more than once; yet [seeing] that he would not himself write on the letters that they had been read out to him, and that he then would not, in continenti, request and accept any such thing as was offered him of [my] good will, but let them keep these same letters — so that in the evening they were delivered back to me — it therefore seems to me that I am not now under obligation to give him any copies of these same letters, unless it were that he offered himself willing to reply in proper form to something therein that bore weight, and to prove it otherwise; and, of his own accord, were besides desirous that good people might pronounce and adjudge upon it: [in that case] I am minded gladly to let him have them all transcribed, and that he be allowed to have due time and respite to consider himself upon it, and to bring against it whatever he himself pleases. And I will then gladly let myself be content with whatever the Magnificent Lord Rector and the professors here at the university shall justly adjudge and pronounce thereon — particularly as concerns the foremost points and the principal matter itself, which is: whether the fellow has kept his promise, word and letters, and likewise other good people's words and letters issued on his behalf and with his will and knowledge, as honestly and uprightly as it ought to have been; and whether I, or anyone on my behalf, have given him any reasonable cause to act against it. If he is desirous of such a thing of his own accord (wherein, however, were it possible, I would gladly have spared him), he shall (as has been said) at the first receive truthful copies of all the five letters that were read out to him, and of whatever others he demands. Otherwise it is grievous to me now anew to offer or let befall him such a thing, lest my own and my dear daughter's writings should, in my absence, be wrongfully interpreted and misused. Yet if the Magnificent Lord Rector together with his assessors and colleagues should be willing and able rightly to judge that it behoves me now to forgo such a thing — as touches the letting him have the copies of the letters — notwithstanding that they were previously offered him in vain, and notwithstanding that the case is not [thereby] <i>legitime</i> examined and adjudged, and [if] they will give me their sentence in writing, then I am ready to be willing to conform myself thereto, and thereafter to do therein whatever may be equitable and as the affair itself requires. As concerns the other point — that I should not let these same letters be read out to good people — that I hold that no one can or ought to forbid me, nor otherwise equitably to require [of me], nor [to forbid me] to touch upon those injuries which I have had him warned to bear in patience, and indeed [to forbid me] to say what he can answer for; but rather that it behoves me by all means to do such a thing, so that good people might come to a true ground concerning the circumstances of the case, which were previously unknown to them. The more so, inasmuch as he and his following have now for a whole month round about, here in the town and elsewhere, given out all manner of things to dress up his cause withal, and to turn all the blame upon me and mine, otherwise than I am persuaded it shall in truth be found — so that many good people had thereupon fallen into a wrong opinion and misunderstanding, before some part [of them] got rightly to know the circumstances thereof. It therefore seemed to me to be altogether needful that I should let these same writings be read out before some eminent good people, and report to them therewith the circumstances in all truth — not because I intended to let any evil befall him thereby, but only for a truthful and needful exculpation of myself and mine. Which my purpose, together with whatever other right in this case it may hereafter be needful for me to plead before you, and to which I am given equitable cause, I will altogether keep reserved to myself.</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, the 11th of January, the year 1595.<br>Tyge Brahe<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / occasion.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from the mainland (the letter is dated at Copenhagen) to the <b>Consistory of the University of Copenhagen</b> — the rector and professors. The document is a formal pleading in the <b>Gellius Sascerides affair</b>: the broken betrothal between Tycho's eldest daughter <b>Magdalene Brahe</b> and the physician <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Jell(e) Sascerides, M.D. Basel). After negotiations collapsed in the last months of 1594, the University Consistory intervened in January 1595 to mediate; the affair was never resolved by marriage (Gellius married elsewhere in 1599; Magdalene followed her father to Prague and never married). See <i>On Tycho's Island</i> and Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>.</li><li><b>\"den, som sagen ahngaar\" / \"karlen\" / \"hand\"</b> — \"the man whom the matter concerns,\" \"the fellow,\" \"he\": throughout, the unnamed adversary is <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>. Tycho never names him; the contemptuous <i>karlen</i> (\"the fellow / the churl\") is preserved as \"the fellow.\"</li><li><b>\"min kierre daather\"</b> — \"my dear daughter,\" i.e. <b>Magdalene Brahe</b>; her <i>skriffuellßer</i> (\"writings,\" letters in the betrothal correspondence) are among the documents Tycho will not surrender for fear of misuse.</li><li><b>Emissaries.</b> <i>doctor Niels Krag</i> = <b>Niels Krag</b> (c. 1550–1602), humanist, historian and professor at Copenhagen (later royal historiographer). <i>Mogens Berttellßen</i> = <b>Mogens Bertelsen Dallin</b>, who acted as spokesman in the negotiations (named in <i>On Tycho's Island</i> as \"Mogens Bertelsen Dallin … spokesman for Dr. Gellius\" in the broader affair; here he is sent by Tycho). Both were \"sent out by me\" to put the offer to Gellius.</li><li><b>Latin rubric &amp; code-switches.</b> The heading <i>[AD RECTOREM ET PROFESSORES UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS]</i> (\"To the Rector and Professors of the University of Copenhagen\") is the editorial/chancery Latin rubric over a Danish body; preserved per protocol §2. Latin technical terms studding the Danish are kept and glossed: <i>vidisse</i> = a notarised <b>attestation / certified statement</b> (the Consistory's written record, \"the <i>vidisse</i>\"); <i>magnificus dominus rector</i> = \"the Magnificent Lord Rector\"; <i>professores / professorum</i> = \"the professors\"; <i>privatim … publice</i> = \"privately … publicly\"; <i>in continenti</i> = \"then and there, on the spot\"; <i>replicere</i> = to <b>reply / rejoin</b> (in the legal sense); <i>assessoribus et collegis</i> = \"[his] assessors and colleagues\"; <i>legitime conoscerit och vrdelit</i> = \"<b>lawfully examined and adjudged</b>\" (<i>conoscerit</i> &lt; Lat. <i>cognoscere</i>, to take judicial cognizance of; <i>vrdelit</i> = Da. <i>ordelet/dømt</i>, \"adjudged\"); <i>sententz</i> = the <b>sentence / judgment</b> (in writing).</li><li><b>Negation count (load-bearing — verified against the source).</b> The dense clause on the second offer reproduces exactly the source's negatives: \"he would <b>not</b> himself write … that they had been read out to him, and … would <b>not</b> … request and accept any such thing.\" The penultimate long sentence stacks three coordinated prohibitions Tycho denies anyone may impose: \"no one can or ought to <b>forbid</b> me, <b>nor</b> otherwise equitably to require, <b>nor</b> [to forbid me] to touch upon those injuries … <b>and</b> [nor to forbid me] to say what he can answer for.\" The Danish here is elliptical — <i>\"och icke io at maa siige, hues hand kan bestaa met\"</i> — and the governing verb (<i>formene</i>, \"forbid\") must be carried forward from the head of the period; the bracketed \"[to forbid me]\" supplies it. <b>This is the principal crux</b> (see Confidence).</li><li><b>\"hues hand kan bestaa met\"</b> — literally \"what he can answer for / stand by.\" Parallel uses of <i>bestaa</i> in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV (pp. 70 <i>bestaa oc bekiende</i> = acknowledge/confess; 109 <i>ieg faar well adt bestaa det</i> = stand by/answer for) confirm the core sense \"answer for / acknowledge,\" rendered accordingly (revised 2026-06-02 from the earlier \"be charged with\"); the secondary shading \"what he can put up with\" is flagged. The governing verb <i>formene</i> (\"forbid\") is overt at the head of the period (Dreyer XIV p. 86: <i>ingen kand eller bør mig formene</i>), so the supplied \"[to forbid me]\" across the ellipsis is grammatically secure — confirming the (a)-crux structure.</li><li><b>\"haffue y fordrag\"</b> — \"to bear in patience / forbear,\" i.e. the injuries (<i>forulempninger</i>) Tycho says he had Gellius warned to endure; <i>adtuare</i> = \"to warn / admonish\" (Da. <i>advare</i>).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1595, German-influenced).</b> <i>erre</i> = <i>ere</i> (\"are\"); <i>suar</i> = <i>svar</i> (\"answer\"); <i>offuerantuor[de]</i> = <i>overantvorde</i> (\"deliver over\"; the MS contracts the ending, expanded in brackets); <i>effterdj</i> = <i>eftersom/efterdi</i> (\"inasmuch as\"); <i>formedelst</i> = \"by means of, through\"; <i>vdtßende</i> = <i>udsendte</i> (\"sent out\"); <i>hannem/hannom</i> = <i>ham</i> (\"him\"); <i>thidre end engang</i> = <i>tiere end engang</i> (\"more than once\"); <i>in continenti</i> (Latin) kept; <i>dennum/dennem</i> = <i>dennem</i> (\"them\"); <i>offuerantuordede</i> = <i>overantvordede</i> (\"delivered over\"); <i>wden saa waar</i> = <i>uden så var</i> (\"unless it were\"); <i>billigen / billigt</i> = \"equitably / equitable, reasonable\"; <i>macht paalaahe</i> = <i>magt pålå</i> (\"bore weight, mattered\"); <i>got folck / gottfolck</i> = \"good people\" (respectable persons, here as informal arbiters/witnesses); <i>respitt</i> = \"respite\" (a Latin/French loan); <i>rettuißelliigenn</i> = <i>retfærdeligen</i> (\"justly\"); <i>affsiige</i> = <i>afsige</i> (\"pronounce [judgment]\"); <i>hoffuidt sagen</i> = <i>hovedsagen</i> (\"the principal matter\"); <i>løffte</i> = <i>løfte</i> (\"promise\"); <i>widtschab</i> = <i>vidskab/viden</i> (\"knowledge\"); <i>oprechteligen</i> = <i>oprigteligen</i> (\"uprightly\"); <i>skellig aarsage</i> = <i>skellig årsag</i> (\"reasonable cause\"); <i>paaesker</i> = <i>påæsker</i> (\"demands\"); <i>beßuer ieg mig</i> = <i>besværer jeg mig</i> (\"it is grievous / burdensome to me\"); <i>affuerelße</i> = <i>fraværelse</i> (\"absence\"); <i>wrangeligen … vdtydett</i> = <i>vrangeligen udtydet</i> (\"wrongfully interpreted\"); <i>vahnßeett</i> = <i>uanset</i> (\"notwithstanding\"); <i>forgebs</i> = <i>forgæves</i> (\"in vain\"); <i>forulempninger</i> = \"injuries, wrongs\"; <i>ahnhang</i> = <i>anhang</i> (\"following, faction\"; a Ger. loan); <i>en gantsche maanidt</i> = <i>en ganske måned</i> (\"a whole month\"); <i>giffuett allehonde forre</i> = <i>givet allehånde fore</i> (\"given out / put about all manner of things\"); <i>smycke</i> = <i>smykke</i> (\"to adorn, dress up\"); <i>wrang mening</i> = <i>vrang mening</i> (\"wrong opinion\"); <i>vbewist</i> = <i>ubevidst/ubekendt</i> (\"unknown\"); <i>betenckning</i> = \"purpose, consideration, opinion\"; <i>forbehollen</i> = <i>forbeholdt</i> (\"reserved\").</li><li><b>Calendar / place / subscription.</b> <i>Actum Kiøbenhaffn den 11 ianvarii anno 1595</i> = \"<b>Done at Copenhagen, 11 January 1595</b>\" (O.S.; <i>Actum</i> \"Done/Given\" kept per §5.5). The dataset `label` (\"1595. 11. januar. Uraniborg\") names <b>Uraniborg</b> as place, but the source's own dateline reads <b>Copenhagen</b> (<i>Kiøbenhaffn</i>); the header follows the as-written dateline per protocol §3–4. The discrepancy is consistent with the affair playing out before the Copenhagen Consistory: the catalogue may record Tycho's seat (Uraniborg) rather than the place of subscription. <i>met egen hd.</i> = <i>med egen hånd</i> (\"with [my] own hand\"), the autograph subscription formula.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the bulk of the letter is rendered securely, but two genuine cruxes are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>elliptical triple-prohibition</b> clause <i>\"ey heller at komme wiidt … och icke io at maa siige, hues hand kan bestaa met\"</i>, where the governing verb <i>formene</i> (\"forbid\") must be supplied across a long period and the bracketed insertions carry real interpretive weight (a mis-attached negation would invert who is being barred from doing what); and (2) the idiom <i>\"hues hand kan bestaa met\"</i> (\"what he can answer for\" vs. \"what he can put up with\"). The place-vs-label discrepancy (Copenhagen in the dateline, Uraniborg in the catalogue) is recorded, not reconciled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish letter (Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.71-73, read via page images covering the slice's pp.71-74) clause-by-clause against source_text and published_english, including the names (Doctor Niels Krag, Mogens Bertelsen), the offer-of-copies procedure, the reference to Tycho's daughter's writings, the conditional-submission-to-the-rector clause, and the closing autograph formula ('Actum Kiøbenhaffn den 11 ianvarii anno 1595 / Tyghe Brahe / met egen hd.'). Danish source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly (modulo normal long-s/spelling variance). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found anywhere in the letter. The previously self-assigned grade C does not survive independent collation; per the brief, that self-grade is worthless as evidence and this is graded on what was actually checked.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Københavns Universitets Arkiv, [gammel signatur (VP) nr. 501]."], "th": {"label": "Rektor og professorerne ved Københavns Universitet", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15941207001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15941111001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Defends his position in the mill dispute, reporting that the opposing party refused to acknowledge the reading of five letters or accept the offered transcript.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15950111001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15950114001", "d": "1595-01-14", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christoph Rothmann", "ck": "Christopher Rothmann", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Rothmann — Uraniborg, 14 January 1595 [O.S.] (begun 30 November 1592 [N.S.])", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist; printed orthography with v/u), with Greek and astronomical glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VI,<i> Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his<i> Epistolarum astronomicarum libri </i>(1596), Liber I · MS witnesses: none recorded (printed tradition)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> The <b>longest letter in the whole corpus</b> and the <b>last of the Tycho–Rothmann correspondence</b> (Rothmann, ill since his 1590 visit to Hven, never replied; the exchange ends here). After a condolence on Rothmann's illness and an account of why the comet-works remain unpublished, the bulk is a <b>sustained, point-by-point demolition of John Craig</b> (the unnamed \"Scot\") and his anti-Tychonic <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i> — defending the comets' aethereal nature, the 1572 nova, and, notably, <b>John Dee</b> (<i>Nucleus parallaticus</i>) and <b>Thomas Digges</b> (<i>Alae</i>), with embedded quotations from <b>Jacob Monau</b>, <b>Craig</b> himself, <b>Hájek</b>, and <b>Curtius</b>. It closes with the <b>Mars/Venus-eccentricity argument against Copernicus</b> and the 1000-star catalogue. Printed-edition text (clean), v/u orthography retained. Declared interventions: the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> on ~40 words removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590 and 13 Sept 1588, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588 and 20 Jan 1587, and to Hájek of 3 Aug 1590). Greek (<i>ἀυτὸς ἔφα</i>, <i>πολυρρητορείαν</i> [src <i>πολυροτορέιαν</i>], <i>κάπνἐγκέφαλος</i>, <i>λόγῳ</i>) and the glyphs (♂ ♀ ♊ ♋ ☉ ☽) are reproduced as transcribed; the long <b>quotations from Craig</b> (and from Monau/Hájek/Curtius), italic in the print, are kept inline and identified in the Apparatus. <b>Dating crux (named).</b> The header dateline is <i>\"Vraniburgi die 14. Ianuarij Anni 1595\"</i> (<b>Old Style</b>); but the letter embeds its own composition note — <i>\"Hodie, qui est dies ultimus Novembris Anni 1592 novo stylo\"</i> (<b>30 Nov 1592 N.S.</b>, when Tycho received Rothmann's 28 Sept letter and Craig's screed via the Magdeburg courier) — so it was <b>begun late 1592 and only completed/sent in Jan 1595</b>. The header gives the catalogued/dispatch date with the internal note flagged. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VI pp. 319–337</b>. <b>All cruxes confirmed</b>: the internal composition note <i>\"Hodie, qui est dies ultimus Novembris Anni 1592 novo stylo\"</i> (the Magdeburg-courier receipt of Rothmann's 28 Sept letter, p. 334) and the header dateline 14 Jan 1595 (p. 337) — the dual-date is genuine; the Craig title <i>Capnuraniae restinctio, seu Cometarum in Aethera sublimationis refutatio</i> (p. 320); the defence of <b>John Dee</b> <i>in suo Nucleo Parallatico</i> and <b>Thomas Digges</b> <i>alas</i> (p. 330); the embedded Monau / Hájek-+-Curtius quotations (pp. 323, 334); the four Greek terms (heavily OCR-garbled in the scan but located in place); and the Mars/Venus-eccentricity coda (Mars ecc. \"vel potius nonnihilo maior,\" Venus apogee 17° Gemini vs init. Cancer, p. 336) + the 1000-star note (p. 337).</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO D. CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO, VARIA ERVDITIONE INPRIMISQVE RERUM MATHEMATICARUM INSIGNI PERITIA DECORATO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. S. D.</p><p>ACCEPI tandem â te literas, eruditissime et amantissime Rothmanne, diu multumque antea desideratas: cum, quod collationes nostrae in Rebus Astronomicis interea praeter solitum feriarentur; tum quod de te tuâque valetudine, et vbinam locorum haereres (siquidem ad Principem tuum tanto tempore reversus non esses) certi aliquid cognoscere avêrem. Intelligo autem ex his ipsis, te prae nimiâ invaletudine ad ipsum Cassellas ante obitum eius non revenisse, retardante morbo, ex salium Tartareorum resolutionibus proveniente, et te toto hoc tempore, pene absque intermissione infestante. De quâ valetudinis sinistrâ affectione tibi plurimum condoleo; quodque in hac viridi adhuc aetate tam saevis morborum tormentis obnoxius sis. At cum te nonnihil iam respirasse indices, est, quod tibi congratuler; utque haec valetudinis recuperatio quotidie incrementum sumat, constansque permaneat, exopto. Si mea Medicamenta ex officinâ nostra Pyronomica prodeuntia, nec parvo labore aut sumptu praeparata, tibi in his tantis malis (modo, uti fit, aliquando recurrant) praesto esse poterint, ea lubens offero, et lubentius impertiar: quemadmodum et praesenti tibi meam in hisce operam non denegavi. Quod avtem eâ postmodum usus non es, tuâ ipsius culpâ factum est, qui me de adversâ tuâ valetudine, nec certiorem reddidisti; nec quidpiam eiuscemodi (licet discedenti ultro oblatum) â me postea per literas expetijsti. Sive igitur ipsemet huc reversus fueris, uti quodammodo in te recepisti, sive per certum et idoneum hominem hâc de re commonefeceris, invenies me ad praestandum id officij minime difficilem.</p><p>Adhortaris in hisce Literis, ut Cometographica nostra Opera in publicum prodire sinam, et cur tam diu apud me delitescant, miraris; praesertim cum illa pro maiori parte diu absoluta, toties, tamque diu me evulgaturum promiserim: Eruditis etiam viris non paucis eorum copiam sibi fieri desiderantibus. Sed et non parum emolumenti ad Mathematica studia ornanda, atque ab imperitorum et calumniatorum obtrectationibus vindicanda hinc proventurum asseveras. Facit id quidem, mî ROTHMANNE, magnâ ex parte animi tui ingenuitas, et erga nos, ac communia nobis studia Amor ille sublimis, atque peculiaris favor: attamen et Veritatem hisce non modicam subesse, (si absque gloriatione et invidiâ id dicere liceret) non eo infidas. Nam quid et quantum Mathematicis exercitijs rite administratis praestari queat, necdum communiter in Scholis philosophantibus, nedum reliquo vulgo â Musis alieno, satis innotuit. Et certe Cometarum exacta dimensio, atque quo motu cieantur, quantumque â nobis absint, ingeniosa Demonstratio, ea quae tot saeculis a Graeco illo Aristotele citra ratam Apodixin prolata sunt, et in omnibus Academijs pro certis atque indubitatis assertionibus proclamata, ita infringunt, ut, quanto ignorantiae chao Philosophiae cultores hâc in parte involuti fuerint, nunc tandem Divinae Matheseos Ope, apud veritatis amantes elucescat. Quod et in multis alijs abstrusis, et hactenus ignotis, vel pro compertis falso creditis rebus pariter eiusdem incomparabilis Scientiae adminiculis fieri posse, nullum est dubium: ut de pluribus corollarijs, quae hinc ad omnes alias Disciplinas plenius excolendas derivantur, nihil dicam. Quod autem Opus nostrum de Cometis nondum publici juris fieri permiserim, multae, et graves me detinuerunt rationes. Praeterquam enim, quod in perennium illorum, et Mundo coaevorum Siderum tam Observationibus, quam eorundem in normam Caelesti harmoniae competentem deductionibus plurimum indefesso studio aliquot iam continuis Annis sudârim; (quae res non parum, uti scis, et temporis et laboris requirit) ut ob id alijs istis serio vacare minus fuerit commodum; Multa etiam praeterea citra expectationem nostram, et omnem amolitionis providentiam, longo iam tempore inciderunt, quae me otio frui Philosophico, et Vraniae sacris, prout vellem, litare non permiserunt. Adeo invida est Mundi huius immundi turbulentia, contra purioris veritatis, etiam in Philosophicis et visibilibus Caeli aspectabilis rebus; nedum ut in Divinis, et invisibilibus Mundi illius Archetypi, et rationi humanae impervestigabilibus, si quid ab ignorantiae tenebris in apertam lucem perducatur, acquiescere possit. Accessit et hoc, quod nostri circa Cometarum, praeteritis aliquot Annis conspectorum apparentias accurate disquirendas, Labores cuiusvis aestimatione maiores, plus temporis, operarum, et sumptuum requisierint, quam ingrati nonnulli homines, adeoque ex ipsis, qui Disciplinarum, et quidem Mathematicarum amorem, scientiamque haud minimam praetendunt, agnoscere potuerint. Inter quos unus praesertim est, qui non saltem nullas mihi agere gratias pro tot vigilatis noctibus, tot erogatis impensis, totque exantlatis in Demonstrationibus et Numeris, ut veritas intemerata erui posset, Laboribus, aequum censuerit; sed et oblocutionibus, perversionibus, certitudinis patefactae impugnationibus; imô cavillis et scommatis nos impetere non erubuerit. Vt ob id Excellentissimum illum et prudentissimum virum IACOBVM MONAVIVM, honorandae memoriae (Nam, uti audio, ante triennium fatis concessit) non immerito ad eximium Mathematicum BARTHOLOMAeVM SCVLTETVM, cum librum nostrum de Cometâ Anni 77. eo accomodante nactus, inspexisset, in hunc modum scripsisse recorder, prout etiam in Sculteti tunc ad me datis literis annotatum reperio: <i>O uos felices animas, quibus in domos superas scandere cura fuit, et est hodie quoque. Miratus sum magis quam intellexi tantam diligentiam D. Tychonis, quanta animaduertitur in hoc illius Opere. Fac illum horteris, ut reliquas etiam promat Operis illius partes. Etiamsi hoc saeculum nostrum non sit dignum istis tot et tantis bonis, quae agnoscit, eâ quâ par erat gratitudine. Wratislauiae die 17. Iunij stili ueteris Anno 1588.</i> Haec optimus ille vir, pro eo, quo excelluit, iudicij acumine, ac in Rebus, tâm Philosophicis, quam Politicis prudentiâ, et protulit, et quasi praesagire visus est.</p><p>Is vero, qui prae caeteris, nostrum in Re Cometica studium elevare, et oppugnare attentavit, Scotus quidam natione fuit, Medicinae Galenicae Doctor, et Aristoteleae Philosophiae supra modum addictus. Quem hîc nominare nolo, eius honori parcens. Vult quidem is genere Nobilis haberi, uti de se ipso nonnullibi insinuat: sed tamen dicto Philosopho Ethnico nimis serviliter, potiore sui parte, hoc est, genio et ingenio, in hoc praesertim de Cometis negotio, mancipatus. Cum ille ex intercessione cuiusdam sui conterranei me hîc invisentis librum nostrum secundum de Aetherei Mundi phaenomênis, qui de Cometâ illo ingenti Anni 77. affatim tractat, statim, atque Typis absolutus erat, accepisset, scriptis ad me literis, assertiones nostras isthic comprehensas, praesertim, quod Cometam illum in Aethereâ Mundi Regione, et nequaquam Elementari (contra quam docuit Aristoteles) cursum suum absolvisse ostenderim, in dubium vocare, et quantum in ipso erat, labefactare conatus est. Existimabam certe ab initio, hominem amice et candide, veritatis penitius manifestandae gratiâ id ipsum agere. Quod et multi alij, non ineruditi viri mecum opinati sunt: Inter quos erat Magnificus ille IACOBVS CVRTIVS, Imperij Procancellarius, qui, missis ad me literis, eos, qui nostrae hac in parte assertioni contradicerent, id serio non agere; sed saltem, ut plura his similia expiscarentur, bonâ intentione moliri existimabat. Et erat is sane non saltem in Politicis, sed in Mathematicis quoque Rebus vir perspicaci ingenio, et acri simul iudicio praeditus: ut de Clarissimo illo Doctore THADDAeO HAGGECCIO, et alijs idem sentientibus nihil addam. Ex quo itaque animadverti esse, qui de assertionibus nostris in praedicto libro propositis haesitationem movêre praesumerent, adeo, ut ijs contradicere non dubitarent: Eos quidem, qui in Aristoteleâ schola educati, ex ipsius saltem authoritate, et dudum receptâ opinione, Cometas necessario in Aëre generari, inferebant, nullâ tamen ratione Geometrica vel saltem huius praetextu, rem ita se habêre probantes, refutatione indignos censui: Cum ab authoritate humanâ diducta argumentatio omnium sit infirmissima, et ob id postremo loco merito reponenda. At Scoto huic; cum non solum Aristotelëam authoritatem (licet et hanc plus iusto extulerit) multum contra nos venditârit; sed etiam Geometricum nescio quid, pro suâ opinione stabiliendâ praetexuerit, ipsi soli modeste obviandum censui; partim, ut eius obiectiones, quae speciem quandam veri prae se ferebant, et incautos facile in devia abripere poterant, diluerem, partim ut nonnulla in eodem secundo Tomo contenta plenius et dilucidius explicarem, atque de Re Cometicâ generaliore quodam modo nonnulla uterius consignarem: sperans, me ipsi et alijs veritatis hâc in parte scrutatoribus, rem haud ingratam sic praestiturum. Quare conscriptâ inter alias occupationes extemporaneâ quadam Apologia, eam in Scotiam ad Antagonistam nostrum actutum transmisi, eandemque cum quibusdam alijs in Germaniâ Eruditis Viris, qui has res intelligerent, communicavi, ut eorum censuras pariter exquirerem. In quâ certe Apologia, me modestiae limites uspiam transgressum, aut quid citra rem protulisse, mihi ipsi non sum conscius. An vero aliquo affectu (uti nonnunquam fit humanitus) hîc aberrem, dijudicent alij, qui verum â falso sine omni praeiudicio discernere, et nôrunt, et volunt. At quomodo Philosophus iste Aristotelicus nostram hanc candidam, et ipsi (uti sperabam) acceptam futuram intentionem, exceperit, dici vix potest. Postquam enim usque in tertium Annum hanc amicam Apologiam secum retinuisset, nihilque responsi tanto tempore perscripsisset, eum vel nostris dilutionibus acquievisse, et in has transivisse partes, existimabam; vel, quod contradictiones non facile ulterius occurrerent, continuisse. Tandem vero, rupto illo alto silentio, scriptum quoddam Antilogeticum, paulo virulentiore et mordaciore (uti videtur) animo, quam prius per literas egerat, in nos vibravit; in quo scommatis, dicterijs, cavillis, distractionibus et perversionibus multifariam ludit et illudit, ut veritatem â nobis assertam eludat: Nec tam Scotice, quam scoptice agit. Cum multis enim ê Scotiâ oriundis, nos hîc subinde invisentibus, tam Doctrinâ quam Genere claris mihi res fuit, in quibus eximium candorem, pari humanitate coniunctum animadverti; quibus etiam ingenij et iudicij acumen singulare non defuit. At, qui tam ingrato et averso animo, tamque petutanti linguâ, me meaque incesseret, hactenus sensi neminem. Quin et quidam ex ipsius proprijs conterraneis, hoc eius inverecundum factum aperte detestati sunt; quodque sic ipsorum Gentem, exterorum et saniorum hominum iudicijs obnoxiam redderet, ex quo solus talia attentare, reique ita demonstratae, ut omnibus approbaretur, etiam ijs, qui antea contrarium sentiebant, et in publicum promulgârant, nihilominus arroganti ausu contradicere non erubuerit, conquesti sunt. Et sane, si id quod verum est, pro me ipso dicere liceat, non immerito hoc protulerunt. Nam cum alij in Mathematicis non minus excellentes viri, quam iste Antagonista (cui in hisce disciplinis scientiam non derogo, modo eâ recte uti vellet) qui Cometam illum sublunarem fuisse, et Parallaxin quinque circiter Graduum admisisse, scriptis suis in publicum editis iam antea pronunciassent, accepto hoc nostro libro, et re melius perspectâ, non saltem, se aberrasse, ingenue fassi sint; sed et, quod certiora monstrârim, mihi non modice gratias egerint: Hic solus, licet ipsemet nihil in eodem Cometa, quod alicuius momenti foret, expertus esset, tamen nostras laboriosas vigilias et summam circa haec diligentiam extenuare, et quantum in ipso erat, irrita reddere nitebatur: adeo, ut aliorum quoque data et pronunciata, quae ipsimet authores tandem, re melius intellectâ aversati sunt, et retractârunt, in manus resumserit, et ijs, cum ipsemet nulla habuerit, contra nos usus, vel potius abusus fuerit.</p><p>Vt autem aliquâ ex parte cognoscas, optime ROTHMANNE, quantâ is non saltem me; sed et ipsam veritatem, quam penitius pro virili inquirere sategimus, iniuriâ afficere, quamque scoptice mea incessere sibi arrogârit, percurram breviter Invectivae eius (sic enim potius quam moderata aliqua Apologia nominari meretur) praecipuos quosdam locos: solidiorem et specialem eius replicationem, una cum conuenienti refutatione alicui ex nostris Amicis, aut etiam Discipulis harum rerum gnaris, suo tempore exequendam commendans. Titulum fecit huic stomachoso et insolenti scripto: <i>Capnuraniae restinctio, seu Cometarum in Aethera sublimationis refutatio.</i> Cum tamen ego nusquam, Cometas, licet reverâ Aetherei sint, ex fumositatibus constare asseruerim; sed potius e caelesti materiâ: prout istum liber noster secundus, quem impugnat, admonêre potuit; qui sic habet pag. 254. <i>Ego materiam omnium Cometarum prorsus coelestem esse iudico, siquidem etiam omnes in ipso caelo generentur.</i> At de fumis, quos ille vendit, nihil. Tu quidem mî ROTHMANNE, aliquando fuisti in eâ sententiâ, quod fumositates Terrestres Caelum (siquidem illud tuâ et IOHANNIS PENAe Galli opinione planê aërium esset) subintrare possent, et materiam isthîc Cometis praebêre: Quod tamen ego improbavi, et tute ex viâ lacteâ aliter postea edoctus, id ipsum revocasti: uti ex Epistolis inter nos communicatis videre licet. Subiungit deinde in eadem inscriptione, quod ea, quae pro asserendo Caelesti Cometarum situ, et Apologiâ meâ adfero, ab ipso infirmentur omnia: ubi sibi ipsi non constare in hoc ipso frontispicio videtur; dum nunc me pro Aethereis phaenomênis, prius pro caelestibus fumis Cometas habere insimulat: Et non decimam partem eorum, quae libro secundo, atque in Apologiâ, pro asserendo Cometarum Aethereo situ adduximus, vel supremis labris attigit; nedum, ut ea omnia (uti nimis arroganter iactitat) infirmârit. Imô ea ipsa, quae carpit, ne conquassat quidem, nedum, ut labefactet vel destruat. Deinde in ipso scripto statim meorum Instrumentorum observationes prae veterum diutino consensu elevat: quod, quam recte faciat, tute (qui nostra spectasti, et eorum usum hancque tractationem penitius intelligis) dijudicare potes. Postea totus ferme, vel scommaticus est, vel contra nos iniuriosus, nostra distrahens aut pervertens, neque, uti decuit, recte accommodans. Et, ut paucis dicam, ea vel omnimode non intelligit, vel intelligere non vult. Ait, se ab initio non putasse, me serio sic de Cometis sensisse; sed Paradoxa disserendi caussâ struxisse: Cum tamen liber noster secundus satis ostendat, me non iocose in his ludere, tot eâ de caussâ exantlatis et Obseruationum et Demonstrationum, tum quoque Numerorum laboribus, quos facilius est carpere, quam imitari. Ait etiam, se nequaquam ita mecum egisse, ut de veritate sententiae suae quicquam dubitaret: Forte, quia extra aliorum hominum sortem positus, ne dubitare quidem, nedum errare potuerit; utpote in Aristotelem credens, qui apud istos est, ἀυτὸς ἔφα. Dicit quoque, me alios ignaros et erroribus mancipatos criminari; et addit scoptice: <i>Bona verba, quaeso!</i> Cum ego tam rigide nuspiam locutus sim; sed saltem eos, qui Aristoteli nimium cum quodam praeiudicio mancipati sunt, modeste notarim, et errores eorundem ostenderim. Desiderat quoque in nobis Mathematicas Demonstrationes; et nonnullibi postea; me solum affirmare quidlibet, et citra necessariam et Geometricam inductionem, mihi et meis Instrumentis fidem adhiberi velle, impudenter insimulare audet. Quam enim falsa haec sint, tu, et alij eximij Mathematici, qui nostra legerunt, et Demonstrationum invictarum non parvam copiam perspexerunt, testari potestis. Rem insuper hanc de Cometis, tam levem esse censet, ut multis non opus habeat disquisitionibus. Scilicet, quicquid olim ebuccinavit Aristoteles, nulli amplius difficultati obnoxium est. Me veritatem nondum perspicere, et cum detrimento Astronomiae hîc errare, audacter magis, quam vere iudicat, vel potius praeiudicat. Quod te rectius intelligere haud ignoro. Vocat, quae in Apologia nostra scripsimus, πολυρρητορείαν quandam, ad quam respondêre non vult. Atqui sub hoc praetextu multa sicco pede praeterit, quae convellere non sustinet. Obijcit, quod caudatas Caelo Stellas, mira monstra, intruserim: Cum tamen Antiquitas, rotunda saltem isthîc esse corpora docuerit. At ego ipsum Cometae corpus per se rotundum esse, nec caudam adhaerentem de ipsius substantiâ; sed per accidens, Opticâ ratione fieri asseveravi. Vt et haec monstrosa potius sit calumnia. Mox, quod Caelestes circuitus, et Numeros ex Hypothesi diductos Cometae illi attribuam, quodque id imitatione MAeSTHLINI et CORNELII GEMMAe faciam, inverecunde exprobrat. Cum potius noster in his conatus, apud candida, et discreta ingenia gratiam merêri debuisset. Et tantum abest, ut ego â MAeSTHLINO vel GEMMA illa mea extruxerim; ut potius in illorum scripta validius, quam hic ZOILVS facit; sed tamen candide, et bonâ intentione animadverterim. Et testari posset, si adhuc viveret WITICHIVS, quod nostras de Cometâ illo conceptiones hîc conscripseramus, antequâm MAeSTHLINI, vel CORNELII huius libelli mihi satis innotuissent: Adeo, ut cum WITICHIVS antea, Cometas omnino sublunares esse, et MAeSTHLINVM, atque alios in Germaniâ, qui contrarium asserverunt, hallucinatos fuisse, credidisset, ex meis solummodo Observationibus, et Demonstrationibus convictus, sententiam mutârit: Qvod et in Germaniam rediens, apud multos fassus est; quorum aliqui, et quidam Scotus, huius conterraneus, mihi id ipsum retulerunt.</p><p>Postea, quod Arcum circuli maximi Cometae motui attribuerim, et quia illum in omni situ perpetuo observârit, inde probârim, sublunarem non fuisse, prolixe cavillari, et varie vertere, atque invertere; imô etiam Dialectice transformare, forte ut Organicum se probet, non intermittit. Vim tamen Demonstrationis vel non perspicit, vel suis sophismatis obscurat. Vtque exemplum det, quomodo Arcus circuli maximi sub Lunâ non minus quam in altissimo Aethere designari queat; Tale ludicrum et futile ê suo versuto cerebello promit: <i>Edinburgo recta Vranoburgum proficiscamur, toto cursu uerticibus nostris in octauo orbe maximi Circuli tramitem tenebimus: et sic maris superficiem sulcantes Vranoburgum ueniemus, in Vranô non erimus.</i> Egregia scilicet similitudo, quae non uno vel altero, sed omnibus quatuor pedibus claudicat! quam cachinnis excipiendam, vel potius exsibilandam, quam seriâ aliquâ responsione dignandam, tute et alij harum Rerum gnari sufficienter intelligitis: cum etiam per se intentioni nostrae nihil officiat. Postea speciosa quadam et fucatâ Demonstratione ostendere nititur, posse Cometas, seu, (uti scoptice loquitur) Pseudoplanetas meos, Circulum describere maximum, et nihilominus in Aëre longe infra Lunam versari; ita ut Parallaxin quinque Graduum admittant. Idque potissimum ex motu eius proprio, qui Parallaxin exhaurit, inferre laborat. At frustra hîc laborat Pseudophilosophus iste, etiamsi millies in Aristotelem iurasset. Nos enim motum proprium Cometae ab ea variatione, quam Parallaxis in diversis Altitudinibus illi insinuare poterat, tali solertiâ discrevimus, ut neutrum alteri obstaculo esse potuerit, quo minus hinc veritatis inquisitio rite procederet: quantum quidem opus esset, inque sensus caderet. Nec tantus erat Cometae Anni 77. motus proprius, in Ortum et non nihil Aquilonem nitens, ut Parallaxin eam, quae vel in concavo Lunaris Sphaerae contingeret, absorbere potuerit; nedum si ea quinque fuisset Graduum; quando per Parallaxin motus Accidentarius, ê superficie saltem Terrae apparens, intra duas vel tres horas tantus evaderet, ut Cometae motum proprium anticiparet, eumque retrogradum videri faceret; quod tamen nunquam contigit: Prout haec liquido â nobis demonstrata sunt. Quae omnia sicco pede transit, nec devia, in quae delabitur, animadvertere vult hic subtilis Aristotelis discipulus. Sed haec alibi forte ex ipsius palliatâ Demonstratione, vel alia plenius refutabuntur. Et licet verum sit, phaenomena illa, quae adeo propinqua nobis sunt, ut semidiameter Terrae ad distantiam eam comparata sensibilis evadat, triplici involui motui, utpote universali illi, proprio, et ei, qui per accidens e Parallaxi contingit: tamen haec ita separare, ut unum alteri non officiat; sed potius pandat, circumspecti est Mathematici; nec, adhibita peculiari diligentia id ita impossibile est, ut frustra incautis persuadere conatur hic Aristoteleus. Nam et id, cum in dicto Cometa, tum in reliquis sequentibus sufficienter praestitimus; contra garriat iste, quantum volet. Dijudices tu et alij, haec penitius, et sine praeiudicio aestimantes. Imô et is, an id in Luna fieri possit, dubitat; si praesupponatur, eius verum ignorari motum, ut Parallaxis nihilominus extricetur: meque, ut id praestem, provocat. At ego huic stupiditati ne respondendum quidem duco: cum aliquis ex Tyronibus meis, qui Caelestia, non saltem in libris, sed in ipso Caelo per experientiam tractare assuevit, hunc nodum haud difficulter resolverit: quod et saepissime factum est. Quid dicet ille de quarta adhuc quadam illi ignota loci apparentis divagatione, quae ex refractionibus Optice suggeritur, idque non saltem in Cometis et Luna; sed et omnibus alijs Caelestibus corporibus, quando potissimum versus Horizontem inclinant: Idque sensibiliter magis atque magis infra vicesimum Altitudinis Gradum, donec in ipso Horizonte maxima evadit: Quemadmodum haec tibi, mi ROTHMANNE, ex Instrumentis Landtgravianis satis perspecta sunt: quae tamen Aristotelico huic ne semel in mentem venerunt. Et licet haec refractionum divaricatio longe difficilius cognitioni certae pateat, quam reliquae tres motuum commixtiones, et ab invicem separationes: tamen et hunc exploratum habere, et quantum reliquis addat, vel auferat, dispicere diutina nos docuit experimentatio, varie petita et comprobata: Tantum abest, ut reliquas illas variationes, minus negotij facessentes, convenienter extricare non sustineamus.</p><p>Forsan is, quae sibi, et sui similibus, Astronomiam in chartis et speculationibus vanis solummodo excercentibus impossibilia videntur, alijs quoque, solide, et vere eam tractantibus, et Caelitus per ratam experientiam (uti par est) deducentibus, minime pervia censet. Quin et in ijs, quas hisce postmodum innectit, argumentationibus, non considerat, in diversis Altitudinibus eundem nihilominus tramitem Circuli maximi Cometam Anni 77. ad amussim observasse: quod fieri nequaquam poterat, si is admodum sensibili Parallaxi fuisset obnoxius, vel Luna inferior. Nec est iustus contradictioni locus, etiamsi rumperentur omnes Aristotelici Capnocometistae. Mox suo more scommatis ludere pergit; meque Cometas animalia Logica statuere; imô et Logice de his disserere ait: et praedicabilium authorem huc Ironice convocare, ne nihil dicat, pudore non afficitur. Si e veriore illa Platonicorum Philosophia potius, quam eius, qui hinc apostatam egit, Stagiritae, plausibilibus coniecturis, instructus fuisset; utique et quae dixeram, et quod non adeo absona, uti putat, dixeram, rectius percepisset, et mihi loco subsanniorum gratias forte egisset. Vt vero demonstrative proponat, vel, (quod verius est) imponat, qua ratione Arcum Circuli maximi designare queant, etiam in Aëre sublunari versantes, utque id ex materiae vi fieri persuadeat, in varias se, ad instar Promethei, vertit formas, et negotium hoc plus intricat, quam extricat. Cumque aliae motionum leges non sufficiant, Helicas impervestigabiles et fictitias in auxilium advocat, et quidvis hîc fingit atque refingit, ut Cometas cogat tales ire vias, quales Natura ipsis non attribuit, neque ulla experientia, in quoquam id fieri, Caelitus deprehendit. Quin nec materiae igneae, et inflammatae, qualem esse vult ipsius praeceptor Aristoteles, talis circumrotatio ordinariâ lege certos modos et metas observans, competit, ita ut diutino tempore perseverare queat. Si ita pro libitu Cometis motus quosvis (ut phantastica aliqua assumptio, â veritate alienissima salvari queat) affingere liceret; utique et aves in aëre aliter volare, quam fert Naturalis et voluntarius impetus, docebimus: et cur sic, atque non aliter volitent, leges illis praescribemus, Helices compositas, flexas, atque reflexas, adscendentes et descendentes, et quorsumcunque libuerit, inclinatas affingemus: Et tamen aves avolabunt quo voluerint, nostramque speculationem eludent. Sed istae absurditates, quae imperitis fucum faciunt, alias ulterius enucleabuntur. Porro, ut adhuc magis se Dialecticum probet, mea Sophistice nectit, et solvit; prout futilis ipsius potius, quam subtilis arguties suadet; et male meas inductiones accommodat. Ac ne sui dissimilis videatur, nostrum in Triangulorum per Numeros resolutorum laborem, haud exiguum illum aut contemnendum, praesumptuoso quodam scommate excipit, dicens; <i>Ad hanc inanitatem stomachum suum iamdudum nauseasse.</i> O delicatulum stomachum Helleboro curandum, ut hinc omnis insipida nauseae materia eximi possit, unaque κάπνἐγκέφαλος eius curari. Nec etiam, quod Cometam suo ductu eandem perpetuo ad Ecclipticam inclinationem retinuisse ex Observationibus per totam eius durationem liquido demonstrârim, inculpatum relinquit; paucula quaedam Minuta, quae duodecimam Gradus partem nuspiam excesserunt, tacite obijciens: Cum veteres Astronomi intra sextam Gradus partem Mundi coaeva Sidera designare contenti fuerint. Et certe trium vel quatuor Minutorum in Anguli istius, de quo diximus, variatione, nec per se satis sensibilis est differentia, nec quidpiam momenti inducit. MAeSTHLINVM quoque, quem meum per contemptum vocat Parochum, licet eum nunquam viderim, et nullam, ne quidem per literas cum eo notitiam contraxerim, ridet, ob eius per filum factas Animadversiones, quibus tamen Doctor hic GALENICVS meliores non attulit. Et corollarem ipsius in hypothesi assumptionem subsannit: Quam licet non tutamur; attamen aliorum ingeniosa inventa adeo licentiose carpere, et nihil quod melius sit, edocere, non est moderati et prudentis ingenij. Nec etiam nostras Observationes, Instrumentis infallibilibus (prout tu, qui ea spectasti, eorumque consentientem certitudinem expertus es, facile testabere) in dubium vocare erubescit. Imô hanc praefractam audaciam insolenter iactitat, et exprobrat his verbis: <i>Hîc obstupesces Obseruationes tuas in dubium uocari, quas infallibiles uis esse, et pro certissimis principijs habendas.</i> Atqui ego non ob id obstupesco, quod is tam stupidus atque inverecundus sit, ut de re ignotâ iudicium ferre praesumat; et ab ijs, tanquam Noctua â Solis claritate abhorreat. Habet multos alios in hâc Mundanâ scaenâ sibi similes, qui, ad veritatis lumen caligantes, eique obstrepentes, mendacijs potius fidem praebeant. Vt vero aliquid adferat, quo meam in Observationibus praestandis diligentiam suspectam reddat; id quod in Apologiâ, atque alibi monueram, ex Altitudinibus Solis, vel stellarum exactissima temporum momenta non satis constare posse; tam insipide et lusorie cavillatur, ut pudeat referre. Hîcque ostendit, quam subtilis et diligens sit Astrorum scrutator; cum aliorum circumspectas in his Animadversiones ne intelligere quidem possit, et veritatis in his penetralia agnoscere; nedum, ut grato animo accipiat. Loca stellarum â me restituta incerta esse, ignoranter occinit, nisi alij idem senserint; utpote ab aliorum authoritate suo more dependens. Solis quoque cursum â me restitui in novâ quâdam Mundi reformatione, insulse ridet: eam mihi pro restituto Solis curriculo, unde caeteri Astrorum motus dependent, ex humanitate suâ Philosophicâ, referens gratiam. Refractionem quoque, et tarditatem mutationis Altitudinis circa Meridianum, eiusque in alijs, quoad Aequatoris aequalem circuitum, disparitatem, nulla esse, prae nimiâ ignorantiâ effutit. Vtque meam rationem nulli ambiguitati obnoxiam, quâ per Aequatorem Mechanicum, Caelesti Analogum, Tempora exactius, et certius, quâm ex Altitudinibus stellarum rimari docui, suspectam et dubiam reddat, audi quaeso, quibus temerarijs verbis, quantâque imperitiâ de his blateret: <i>Sed quaeso, quam tu sequeris? Aequatorem nobis profers Mechanicum, siue conuolubilem, siue fixum, Aequatori in suo situ Analogum, et columnis lapideis seu ferreis innitentem. His dictis, quaeris, an dubitem, te posse minimas Temporum particulas praecise reperire? Oedipum agere non soleo: Cur reliqua supprimis? cur totam rationem non profers, quam pro certissimâ iactas, ut quid ueri habeat, sciatur? Tuus fixus Aequator quî numerum reuolutionis Aequatoris, quo definitur tempus, exhibebit, nisi â Tetrapode suo dimoueatur? Mobilis, quo mouente, ad eundem Analogice circumagetur? Si Logicon dederis, quî intelliget, se ad analogiam Caelestem mouere? Si alogon, siue pondere, siue aliâ ui agat, in eo anomaliam â Caelesti analogiâ discrepantem facile demonstrabo.</i> Sic bonus ille vir de meis Instrumentis, et observandi rationibus sibi imperspectis censuram ridiculam profert: ut quemadmodum Maro de Sinone aiebat; <i>Ex uno disce omnes,</i> sc. Graecos, sic etiam ex hisce prolatis, qualia, et quanti caetera sint, facile queas aestimare. Potuisset utique ille â suis conterraneis, me crebro hîc invisentibus iam antea cognovisse, utrum Instrumenta mea Logica, seu ratione praedita essent, nec ne, vel an alias Mechanice ita elaborata, ut ratiocinationi nostrae commode, et sine obstaculis satisfaciant: Ne is ita sine ratione insanire videretur; et in ipso λόγῳ alogum se esse proderet.</p><p>Deinceps, quia in Distantijs capiendis, horologijs Minuta ostendentibus contentus sim (ita tamen, ut ea subinde per Stellarum, vel Solis â Meridiano, Aequatorias distantias, si quid minutuli vitij contraxerint, rectificentur) cavillatur, me aliorum ex horologijs desumpta tempora reijcere; et tamen ipsummet huc confugere: Imô me Temporum portiunculas adverbialiter, nequaquam Mathematice depromere, obijcit; eo, quod in recensendis distantijs Cometae Anni 77. a certis fixis non ipsissima horarum momenta ubique apponam; sed dicam aliquando; circa id tempus, vel paulo post, et similiter. O subtilis vitilitigator, et momentorum Temporis inanis scrutator! Inspice tu, mî ROTHMANNE, primum caput eius libri de Cometâ illo tibi transmissi, et videbis, me satis praecise horarum in particulis discrimina isthic annotasse, quantum in Distantijs rimandis opus fuit: adeo ut si maiorem scrupulositatem adhibuissem, prudentibus scrupulum potius movêre potuissem; An non ista subtilitas futilitas potius quaedam esset, et supervacanea affectatio? Cum enim intercapedines Cometae â Stellis sedulo capiebantur, id bis vel ter tentandum erat, ut in ipso Minuto convenirent. Quod sane vix intra sextam vel octavam horae partem commode praestatur. Sufficiebat itaque aliquotam horae partem intermediae Observationi quam proximam assignare: ita tamen, ut nusquam sextantis unius horae committatur divagatio. Neque enim Cometae motus intra sextam horae portionem sensibiliter alterabatur, ut ob id subtiliore indagine, quam â nobis praestitum est, momenta illa, quoad distantias assignare, plane otiosum fuisset. Vt hîc nodum in scirpo quaerere, quod et plerunque alias facit, contradictor hic videatur. At ubi ex Azimuthis et Altitudinibus Cometae Parallaxes inquiro, isthic non saltem scrupula prima; sed et eorum aliquotam partem in secundis annotare non intermisi: Quoniam hoc in loco tantâ praecisione necessario opus est: nequaquâm autem in distantijs. Et quod in alijs reprehendi, quod horologijs erronee, et nimis lato modo uterentur, circa hanc ipsam Pragmatiam id fiebat; non autem, quoad distantiarum designationem: quam si vel in quartâ unius horae parte rite annotassent, utique ijs, licet generalis esset indicatio, condonassem. Vide itaque quam candide, et sincere hic mecum agat ille Aristotelicus, dum nostra alio, et in diversa trahit, ac male accommodat, atque subtilitatem, ubi non opus est, quaerit; convenienti autem loco indicatam et inventam respuit: Qui mos est eorum, qui praeoccupato animo veritatem eludere, et captiuam tenere conantur. Sic quoque quod in locorum Cometae per Triangulos examinatione nonnunquam 2. vel 3. Minutor. discrepantia incidit, me eius tramitem non rite designasse arguit: Arguta sane vanitate: vt qui potius tam propinquam coincidentiam admirari debuisset, quam id, quod vix in sensus cadit, nec alicuius est apud candidos et intelligentes aestimatores ponderis, nimis rigide exaggerare. Praeterea infert, quod non ex Distantijs; sed saltem Altitudinibus atque Azimuthis Cometae Parallaxis indaganda fuisset: quorum tamen nos utrunque praestitimus. At vel ipso inscio ac caecutiente, ratio per distantias debito modo applicata certior est eâ, quae ex Altitudinibus et Azimuthis, in quibus vix sensibilis error admodum foecundus euadit, capitur: prout haec tibi, qui ea tractasti, satis comperta sunt; quique quam facile in Altitudinibus et Azimuthis aberretur, in distantijs vero non item, exploratum habes. Sed hic Chartaceus Astronomus loquitur de his atque similibus ferme ubique tanquam coecus de coloribus: et tamen sibi ac suis sic admodum placet: Tandemque concludit, Observatis meis non esse fidendum. Atqui vel hâc solâ negativâ poterat ad omnia pro sua perfricta inverecundia respondere: sicque dignus redargutione non censeretur: Siquidem contra principia negantem non est disputandum. Observata enim hîc pro principijs habentur: quae temere inficiari cuivis licet: At non id ita esse, aeque cito â quovis probatur. Quid si nos putidissimam calumniam, et inverecundam hanc esse praesumptionem responderemus? Vtri nostrum credendum? an mihi, qui in tot aliorum Siderum, adeoque mille fixarum exactissimis denotationibus, maiori praecisione, quam ab ullo veterum aut recentiorum (absit gloria dicto) observationes quam plurimas, continuis iâm aliquot Annis perfecerim; atque eas veritati exacte correspondere demonstrârim: (pro me Caelo adhuc toto loquente ac testimonium ferente) An vero illi, qui talia eâ, quâ opus est, accuratione tractare, nunquam didicit, nec ullum eius specimen hactenus protulit? Imô etiam si quam maxime vellet, id (uti sibi persuadet) minime obvium esset; et uti bellum dulce est inexpertis; sic et Astrorum observatio facilis videtur inexercitato. Verum is ex levitate quâdam de nostris nunquam visis aut intellectis sibi censuram inanem, et flocci faciendam arrogat.</p><p>Deinde, me WITICHIVM, beatâ requie fruentem, indecenter et immerito excipere calumniatur. At ego Doctissimum illum in Mathematicis WITICHIVM nec vivum nec defunctum suis privavi honoribus; sed lapsus, quibus interdum in hisce studijs Caelitus exequendis, (siquidem ijs non assveverat) nonnunquam, veritatis patefaciendae caussâ modeste indicavi. Quod et illi hîc praesenti saepius feci: Nec id ei, ut huic imbelli eius propugnatori, ingratum erat. Vbi laude dignus erat WITICHIVS, videlicet in Geometricis, et compendijs quibusdam Triangulorum, laudavi, neque quidpiam ipsi unquam immerito detraxi. Verum quae non solum de hoc; sed et summo illo viro ANDREA DVDITHIO nimis audacter garrit, nolo replicare. Ipsa Epistola DVDITHII, praefixa libello THADDAeI de Cometâ Anni 90. satis superque ostendit, quam falso ista proferantur. Mox in Optimum illum virum MICHAELEM MAeSTHLINVM pro suâ temerariâ licentiâ debacchatur; et quae ego de Observatione per filum illi usitatâ, cum alia ad manus non haberet Instrumenta, vere admonui, tum quoque, quae de Instrumentorum vulgariter obrepentibus dubijs indicavi, ille in alium sensum pervertendo et distrahendo, suo more, mordaci linguâ arrodit. Et tandem MAeSTHLINVM atque alios, qui veritatem in hoc Cometico negotio diu sepultam ê tenebris plusquam Cimmerijs in apertam lucem deduxerunt, hoc elogio dignatur: <i>Certe nescio, quid non mereantur, qui scientias conspurcant:</i> Forte eos ad Iudices deferri, atque publice in eos animadverti, pro suâ cholericâ indignatione, quia Aristoteli non fidunt, oportere censet. Dehinc, postquam mea Instrumenta, eorumque usum extenuasset, ostendens, se quidvis sine pudore profari audere, suam quondam in Instrumentis tractandis (qualia olim in Germaniâ habuit) peritiam e contra iactitat: quae debita habuisse pinnacidia, non tamen mea rimosa, superbe et scoptice (uti assolet) subsannat. At cur non etiam in Scotia talia ad manus habet? Producat vel unam Obseruationem veram et utilem, quam unquam, sive in Germania, sive Scotia in ulla Fixa, vel erratica stella aut Sole vel Luna perfecerit. Vix credo. Et tamen aliorum indefessos ac exquisitos labores prae sua inertia elevare praesumit. Rimas meorum pinnacidiorum Cylindro, ad Centrum posito parallelas, ridet quidem, et rimoso excipit scommate: sed eorum necessarium, maximeque accommodum usum non videt, quem tute, mi ROTHMANNE, rectius nosti: Cum ab eodem WITICHIO, quem hic Scotus tantopere depraedicat, primo hîc visum, et Principi tuo olim communicatum, maximi semper fecisti; imô et alteram rimulam ab ipso nimis secure neglectam competenter restituisti. Quin et ipse WITTICHIVS cum hoc compendium hîc â nobis didicisset laetabundus aiebat; se iam tandem vidisse et expertum esse, quod diu â DVDITHIO ac ab ipso incassum quaesitum erat, nec se frustra in Daniam, vel hoc nomine, venisse, aiebat: Imô se ne centum Coronatorum lucro hanc cognitionem commutare velle, aiebat. Sed ogganniat, et Scommatis insultet hic Aristoteleus, quantum volet, res per se manifesta est; quam ut penitius intelligat, se ipsum indignum facit. Postmodum etiam de SCVLTETO quaedam adijcit, vel potius obijcit, quod dissentanea a me ipso dixerim, dum interdum ipsius inventa probem, interdum improbem. At hoc non est a se ipso dissidere, laudare, quae laudanda sunt, et ea quae minus quadrans, suo loco improbare. Vtrunque enim eidem authori evenire potest: Quare et utrunque in eodem admitti. Demum de Landtgrauianis quoque observationibus cum nostris et alijs collatis nonnulla inconcinne profert; nec, quam parum consona veritati dicat, moratur. Et haec omnia Colophonis loco hâc elatâ et pruriente loquelâ claudit: <i>Nullum in tam alto tramite diuerticulum relinquendum fuit, quo a ueritate Vraniae tuae deuiare posses. In gratiam cum ea et in rectam uiam redi.</i> Intercedat igitur pro me charus ille Vraniae Antistes, ut in gratiam cum eâ redire possim, siquidem ille se ei rectius notum et acceptum praesumit, utpote qui tot annis illi convenientius, et magis devote litavit, quam nos forte praestitimus.</p><p>Postea quod Cometam non tumultuario, et inordinato motu; sed regulari quodam pedetentim desinente, nequaquam Elementaris; sed potius Caelestis Phaenomêni Naturam praetulisse asseruerim; ille, quia tandem evanuerit, Elementarem necessario fuisse, inferre conatur. Verum hoc est, quod inter alia praecipue ostendere intendimus, Caelum nonnunquam nova recipere corpora, alterationi obnoxia: Quod hactenus ob Aristotelis authoritatem creditum non est. Sed cum refragetur experientia invicta, facessat talis authoritas, plausibilibus tantum et coniecturalibus, non autem Apodicticis ratiocinijs fundata; Ideoque veritati minus consona. Cumque ego similitudinem quandam ex Metallis adduxissem, ut aliquatenus ex harum rerum Terrestrium Natura, quae de caelestibus innuere volebam, explanarem, non quod certam aliquam probationem inde struerem; cum similitudines id ipsum non sustineant: ille e contra plurimis, ijsdemque Sophisticis, et a candore alienis id insectatur verbis. Et ne nobiscum sentiat, contra Aristoteli datam fidem, peccat, ab ipso similia quaedam nobiscum de generatione Metallorum affirmante discedens; et metalla omnia ex una eademque materia prima constare negans: adeo, ut etiam Mercurium auro non inesse contendat. Multaque de his frivole iactitat; forte ut se Chymiae et Naturae Metallorum non expertem pro arroganti suo ingenio ostentet. In quibus tamen aeque parum atque in Astronomicis versatus est. De utrisque enim verba pro rebus venditat. Sed ista forte alibi specialius refutabuntur. Ne vero Nova illa ad Cassiopëam stella illi, et sui similibus Aristoteleis officiat, quo minus, Caelum nova non admittere corpora dissolutioni obnoxia, perseveranter, vel invita ipsa experientia, et contra Geometricas Demonstrationes asseverare possint, eam ipsam Stellam, omnemque circa ipsius apparentias veritatem tam vafris subterfugijs in dubium vocare nititur; ut vel sic satis ostendat, quam parum sit veritatis amans, et quam nihil aliud agat, atque ut hanc speciosis elusionibus evertat: quod a viro integro et sincero alienum esse debuit. Sed pudendum referre, quae ille hîc maxima inverecundia contra multorum praestantium Mathematicorum indubitata experimenta evomere non veretur, ex opinionum varietate certitudinem omnem elidere laborans. Qua sane argumentatione nihil ferme amplius certi supererit; cum vix quidpiam sit, de quo non varient hominum sententiae. Sed distinguendum inter eos, qui secundum experientiam e certis ratiocinijs et Demonstrationibus petitam disserant; qui vero non: Et sententiae in his non numerandae, sed ponderandae sunt. Quin nec suis semiconterraneis parcit, ut amorem, quo Eruditos quosdam in Anglia prosequitur, palam faciat. IOHANNEM DEE Praestantissimum Mathematicum et Philosophum (quem tute nôsti, cum a Landtgravio honorifice exciperetur) in suo Nucleo Parallatico, in sinu semissis gradus principium petere, false exprobrat. THOMAe DIGGAeSEI alas falconem instar, remoto advolatu, immobilia Phaenomêna assequentes, ijs ipsis verbis scoptice excipit. Ex quibus omnibus Naenijs tandem ita concludit, ut uno verbo omnia dicat, seu potius fingat: <i>Illud mirum Phaenomênon omnes tum Mathematicos sic elusit, ut nisi recognoscant, recoquantque, tum demonstrasse, quod ais, dicere nequeant.</i> Et cum ne sic quidem sibi ipsi satisfecisse videatur, tandem ad adagium quoddam sui Praeceptoris, tanquam ad sacram anchoram confugit: <i>Vna hirundo non facit uer:</i> Vt vel in dicterijs Aristotelicis probationis vim latêre, nobis imponat. Sit sane, quod una hirundo non faciat ver, et talia in similibus locum habeant: At una aliquando stella in altissimo Caelo visa, quae postea esse desijt, Caelum nonnunquam nova admittere corpora, quae interitui sint obnoxia, necessario convincit: Adeo, ut ipse Aristoteles, si superstes novam illam Stellam ex altissimo Aethere nobis illuxisse, et tandem post annum, vel paulo amplius conspici desijsse, animaduertisset; ne hiscere quidem contra tam evidentem experientiam ausus fuisset: sed potius suum de Caelestium Natura philosophandi modum hinc totaliter correxisset: quod et multi alij eximij Aristotelici hinc edocti postmodum fecere; hoc Scoto pene solo in semel arrepto, et confirmato erroris caeno se adhuc volutante, ac perseverante: qui et veteris illius Hipparchi authoritatem et experientiam contemnere ludo ducit; dum sic ait: <i>Nec Pliniana narratio est alterius Demonstratio:</i> quasi vel Plinius falsa de Hipparcho dixerit; vel ille inter veteres Stellas, quas ferme omnes suis Organis demensus est, atque novam aliquam in Caelo suis temporibus enatam discernere nequiverit. Facessant istae insolentiae. Verum quia in primo nostro Tomo Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum de hac stella luculenter, et ex professo egimus, eamque in altissimo Aethere ultra annum extitisse, et tandem disparuisse, liquidissime demonstravimus, ita ut nullus sit tergiversationi locus; et simul aliorum pro et contra sententias abunde disquisivimus; ut inde satis superque pateat, quam absurda et insulsa sint, et ab ipsa veritate alienissima, quae hoc loco de stella illa congerit hic Aristoteleus: Atque exinde in caeteris quoque, quam verax sit, tanquam ex ungue Leonem cognoscere liceat: Idcirco operaepretium non duco pluribus haec refellere, et eorum insufficientiam demonstrare.</p><p>Quin et dum varie de Cometis postea bacchatur, et te quoque petere videtur, dum Ironice ait: eos etiam supra Saturnum evehi. Et me, quia ex aliâ Caelestium Naturâ, quam putavit Aristoteles, edoceri nos dixi, Caelum non esse aeternum aut Divinitatis particeps; (prout ille voluit) sed et aliquando dissolutioni obnoxium, quod Creator nos in hisce adscititijs Caeli corporibus, quae intereunt, in quodam quasi exemplo, admoneat; hic noster Mastix non minus scoptice alloquitur, dum ait; me magnam hinc consolationem capescere; quod scilicet sic statuam: moxque suo more arrodens, ulterius excipit; ne quidem â nimis vulgato illo abstinens: <i>Baculus stat in Angulo. Ergo Pontifex Romae est.</i> Vocat etiam hâc occasione Cometas, (quos mihi Pseudoplanetas quosdam appellare libertate Philosophicâ libuit,) Pseudoprophetas meos; et multa alia supervacanee adducit pro Aristotele, quod Mundus sit suapte Naturâ aeternus et incorruptibilis, sed tantummodo voluntate DEI finem habiturus. De quibus nihil addam, cum parum ad rem de quâ agitur, faciant; sed potius, pomposae quaedam disputationum inexplicatarum ostentationes sint. Deinde ad Cometas, eorundemque consumptionem rediens, multa nimis audacter fingit, quae nec Cometae Anni 77. de quo potissimum agitur, competebant, nec a nobis illi uspiam attributa sunt; sed eorum plane contrarium ostensum. Et quia Cometam illum tardiorem motu suo proprio fuisse, quam Lunam, ideoque altiorem exstitisse, verisimilitudine non carere dixerim, nullam tamen necessitatem hîc attexuerim; ille, quibus sannijs impetat, vix satis invenit. Ait, nihil prohibere, quin ad Iovis solium hoc pacto evolet, ut alter Icaromenippus. Quin et Luciani fabulas de abrupto Asinorum ponte convocat: ut se Luciani quoque illusiones magnifacere, et eius naturam sapere, prodat. Et quia porro obiter quasi indicâram, Cometam a Tropico Cancri ad Tropicum Capricorni iter suum direxisse, atque intra has metas, quibus etiam Solis orbita clauditur, se continuisse, ideoque quidpiam occulti, Caelestibus tacite analogum insinuasse; ille subsannando sic illudit: <i>Num pro suâ intelligentiâ, ipsius (scil. Capricorni) frigora fugiens, ad huius (scil. Cancri) focum pergere uoluerit.</i> Sed valeant ista scommata, quae nil nisi authoris vanitatem et levitatem arguunt. Postea Mathematica mea ratiocinia convellere praesumens, enumerat quidem, quâ ratione Cometae Parallaxin pervestigârim, ut se nostra utcunque intelligere, ostentet: Sed haec ibi nullis contrarijs rationibus infirmat, nisi quod plumbeam normam pro suâ authoritate affectatâ vocet, et differentiam longitudinis inter Cassellas et hunc locum in aliquot scrupulis dubiam esse, frustraneo scrupulo obijciat, Landtgravianasque Observationes, non omnes, sed selectas quasdam a me usurpatas reijciat, verioresque suo arbitratu fuissent alteras dicat, quas negleximus: hisque similia parum probantia, nec per se omnimode vera, nihilque ad rem facientia agglomeret. Si ex ullis Landtgravianis Observationibus, quas ego libro secundo prout mihi communicatae erant, ingenue annotavi, probare potuisset, Cometam illum sublimarem fuisse, nullum est dubium, quin id ipsum, quantum in se erat, fecisset. Sed quia nulla talis obvia erat, pro libidine, certa et rata dubijs involuit, atque offuscare nititur. Ego Landtgravianas observationes, non pro arbitrio, ut certiores negligerem, selegi; sed eas, quae in tempore plurimum distarent, usurpare malui, quo Parallaxium differentiae, quae sic augentur, eo promptius inde elucescere possent.</p><p>Quin et in te quoque Doctiss. ROTHMANNE, licenter invehitur, quod Cometam Anni 1585. in superiori Caelo constitutam asserueris. Vt vero scias, qui id faciat, en eius verba: <i>Sed uenio ad artificium uestrum. Nam non solum tu, sed ille quoque tantopere a te ob scientiam demonstrandi, et peritiam obseruandi commendatus CHRISTOPHORVS ROTHMANNVS, Mathematicus Landtgrauianus, eâdem Methodo utitur, te referente, dum Cometam octuagesimi quinti Anni supra Saturnum, in spatium sub fixis proximum insequitur; mirâ certe sagacitate. Et illi quoque ignarus Mathematum habebitur, qui dubitat quin demonstraueris, tuum Cometam in Aethere cursum suum absoluisse. Hanc censuram tanti facis, ut immaturis contradicentium sententijs opponas. Tua Clariss. D. TYCHO, si quis alius commendaturus sum ob amicitiam, et nunc uellem: Sed Amico adulari contra ueritatem non soleo. Optarem mihi Obseruata si non demonstrata illius Artificis communicari, quibus hoc Paradoxon asseruit, et an peritia, scientiaue sua eo pertingat, ipse esses iudex. Si pro Vraniae tuae defensione ueritatem uis manifestari, Obseruationes communica etc.</i> Ad haec tute pro te ipso responde, cum meâ ope non indigeas; nec difficile id sit, cum stet a nostris partibus veritas, sexcentis eiuscemodi illusionibus non commovenda. Ego tua Observata, hisque innixas Demonstrationes illi communicare, nec volui, nec volo: siquidem ijs non uti, sed abuti, pervertendo, distrahendo, et cavillando (uti in meis facit) solummodo satagat. Et quorsum opus foret illi talia impertiri, cum de ijs etiam non visis, nedum intellectis, uti bonos (sc.) decet iudices, censuram ferre praesumat? O insipidam arrogantiam!</p><p>Vt vero postmodum ad se rediens, et tanquam e Tripode Delphico aliquid secreti consilij nactus, quo mea Mathematica ratiocinia circa Cometae Parallaxes impugnet et eâdem Operâ te quoque adoriatur, (siquidem te simili quâdam inductione, quâ et nos usi sumus, adinvenisse Cometam Anni 1585. nullam penitus habuisse Parallaxim significâram) dicere eum non pudet, motum, quem diurnum appellamus, apparentem saltem esse, et nullatenus verum: Ideoque et omnes partes eius subsumptas similiter apparentes esse. At hîc non attendit perspicax ille alienorum laborum Criticus, qui debitis tamen specillis uti nescit, quod locus Cometae, qui in certâ aliquâ Altitudine observatur, etsi apparens existat, et Parallaxi, (modo quam habeat,) obnoxius: tamen circumvoluto eodem motu universi, donec denuo ad eundem situm, eandemque supra horizontem sublimitatem redeat, et tunc quidem apparenter observari. Sed cum Parallaxis utrobique eadem prorsus sit, quantum in sensus cadat; ideo haec et utrobique aequaliter addit vel detrahit vero motui. Cum igitur id quod aequale est, utrobique additur vel aufertur: quod residuum erit, priori aequale necessario censebitur, vel iuxta ipsa Geometrica prima axiomata. Ex quibus sequitur, quod quantum apparenter per unam reuolutionem diurnam promotus videtur Cometes in eâdem Altitudine bis observatus a superficie Terrae, tantundem vero suo motu, qui e Centro prodit, variatus fuerit: Ideoque differentia veri et apparentis motus, cum in tali situ ad invicem coaequetur, non differet etiam apparens a vero. At in alijs arcubus, qui integrum circuitum non habent, et in diversis accipiuntur Altitudinibus, res longe aliter evenit. Illic enim Parallaxium differentiae ad invicem non aequantur; sed quo propius fuerit Terris phaenomênon, eo maior incidit inconvenientia, ex quâ etiam remotio a nobis constare poterit. Verum haec et similia acutus et argutus ille Doctor videre non potuit. Nec si pluribus collyrijs uteretur, prae caecitate, quâ per Aristotelica placita praepeditus est, cernere potest. Ideoque saltem pro authoritate (quae nulla est) loquitur, et de Hypothesibus mediorum ac inaequalium motuum, nescio quae, hîc immiscet ad hanc rem plane nihil facientia: ut vel sic ostendat, se nostra non intellexisse, multo minus de ijs iudicare posse. Ideoque arroganti quodam praeiudicio sic censuram hanc suam claudit: <i>Vt nullam demonstrationem uel tu uel ROTHMANNVS hîc praetendere aut persuadere apud Mathematum peritos queatis. Quod sincere uobis indicare uolui.</i> Hîc merito subijciendum foret illud initio ab ipso usurpatum: <i>Bona verba, quaeso!</i> Auscultet vero et hoc, nihil tam bene dictum esse, quod non calumniando depravari queat.</p><p>Deinceps rationes nostras, quibus Cometam minorem ipsa Luna diversitatem induxisse luculenter probo, varie proscindens, atque pro libitu ijsdem abutens, insectatur, et id, quod ex antecedentibus candidis Mathematicis satis patuit, nec Demonstratione ulteriore indiguit, in dubium vocat, ut habeat, quod cauilletur. Verum haec omnia suo forte loco et tempore in ipsum retundentur: Quemadmodum et reliqua his corollarij loco subnexa: et quae insuper addit contra tertiam nostram probationem: idque pari iure, quo caetera; id est, animi quadam caecitate, et praeoccupato iudicio, nec sine affectata praesumptione, hinc inde versute et fucate congerit; quibus incautos non difficulter illaqueare et decipere possit. At ijs qui haec penitius intelligunt, et solidiore iudicio, eoque candido et sincero expenderint, multa dicendo nihil dixisse censebitur. Quin et me postea instruit, quomodo haec tertia ratiocinatio, quae e diuersis locis procedit, corrigi queat. At cum is alios, rectius forte, et diutius in hâc Palestrâ versatos tam belle instruere, atque ab erroribus liberare queat, cur non ipsemet manum Operi admovet, et certius quid (si fieri posset) profert? Scilicet facilius est dicere quam facere, speculari quam scire, taxare quam imitari. Ignorare videtur hic iudiciosus censor, multa speculative et Theorice excogitari posse, quae Demonstrationem quoque Geometricam admittant: At non ob id in praxin deduci, et Mechanice cum fructu exerceri. Sic quoque quae de Longitudinibus quorundam locorum subdit, nugae sunt, et ex animo malevolo, non candido prodeuntia. Tandem etiam contra nostram ultimam ex Altitudinibus et Azimuthis, adhibitâ simul Declinatione, comprobationem, simili, quâ antea, licentiâ, et animi caeco impetu calamum acuit, nostra in devia trahendo, atque ijs perverse utendo: adeo, ut ne quidem eam, quam circa Regiomontani ratiocinationem in usum, quoad fieri posset, deducendam, adhibuerim diligentiam, inculpatam relinquat: multaque alia prae mera philavtiâ et contradicendi libidine, cui in scholis Aristotelicis et Scotisticis asvevit, congerit, quae piget et pudet hoc loco singula referre aut replicare. Quin et eo dementiae aut malevolentiae tandem pervenit, ut non dubitet insimulare, me nomen meum sic prophanare, ubi quam maxime consecrare cuperem. Quod si verum esset, certe tu, in hâc ipsâ Epistolâ frustra ais, me Mathematica studia non parum illustraturum, posteritatique consecraturum, si videlicet nostrum de Cometis Opus evulgâro. Verum haec omnia debito modo refutare, alterius erit negotij. Nimis enim hîc prolixum foret. Veniet forte tempus, quo cordatiores, hunc Aristotelicum sibi nimium applausisse, et ante victoriam triumphum cecinisse, perspicient. Vtut ille oculos ad veritatis lumen aperire, aut nequeat, aut nolit. Postrema clausula huius scoptici et arrogantis scripti, quo respondeant ultima primis atque intermedijs, sic habet: <i>Haec ueritatis et amicitiae caussâ contra fumositates tuas exarare libuit.</i> At quam veritati consona attulerit, tuum et aliorum haec rectius intelligentium esto iudicium. Quod et aliquando (uti dixi) plenius patebit.</p><p>Quali amicitiâ id egerit, si mea de ijs, quae me ipsum concernunt, censura suspecta fuerit; audiamus, quid Optimus ille, et ingenuo candore praeditus THADDAeVS HAGGECCIVS de hac ipsa Capnurania, tam illi, quam D. CVRTIO Imperij Procancellario Mathematum peritissimo, ut in eam disquirerent, transmissa, ad me perscripserit: quae etiam ex ipso THADDAeI authographo huic Antagonistae in Scotia aliquando, non sine ipsius effreni stomachatione praelecta sunt. <i>Hodie, qui est dies ultimus Nouembris Anni 1592. nouo stylo, cum hasce postremas tuas, scriptas 28. Septembris, â Tabellario Magdeburgensi cum scripto illo, acerbo sane tui istius fastuosi, praefracti, mordacis, et malesani Antagonistae accepissem, altero mox die, et Epistolam tuam, et scriptum illud, legenda dedi D. CVRTIO. Is primum iubet te saluere plurimum, tibique significari, scriptum illud Scoti non inniti ullis firmis rationibus, omnem doctrinam Parallaxium suis quibusdam phantasmatibus prorsus tollere uelle, hostile, non amicum scriptum esse: Itaque censêre, omninô abs te refutandum. Et paulo post subjungit. Haec dum scribo, subijt in mentem Scotus ille, subtilis Doctor dictus, quod de eo referant Scriptores, fuisse confossum stylis pugillaribus. Ego tuum hunc Antagonistam Scotum, quod admirandam et praeclaram Astronomiae partem de Parallaxibus suis sophismatis euertere cupiat, ab omnibus Astronomiae studiosis multo iustius illo subigendum et confodiendum esse censerem. Haec tu solus, cui nec facultas, nec uoluntas deest, calamo felicissime praestare poteris.</i> Hactenus THADDAeVS.</p><p>Ait mox, se carptim ea scripsisse, in quibus non sine magno labore ultra biennium forte insudavit. Si itaque pro carptim, carpendo dixisset, veriora utique protulisset. Quin et mea de Cometis concepta meas fumositates appellat, non desinens fumos pro rebus vendere, atque impudenter calumniari, et scommata effutire: Cum ego nuspiam (uti etiam ab initio protestatus sum) Cometas, e fumositatibus ullis constare censuerim: sed ille cum suo Aristotele, eiusque asseclis ita opinetur magis quam sciat. Quare habeat ille secum suos fumos et fumositates, nec alijs, utut apud ipsum vendibiles prostent, qui tales levitates emere nolint, obtrudat. In fine demum, ut excuset callide plusquam duorum Annorum in absolvendo hoc Apologetico scripto moras (Nam ego illud primum accepi Anno 1592. circa finem Maij: Cum nostra Apologia esset illi Anno 1589. transmissa) subscribit, quod absoluta fuerit, quo die Sol Anno 91. ad 2½ digitos fere obscurabatur. O calvam excusationem, vel ab ipso Sole Ecclipsato praetexam? et ô observatorem, qui id in Solis deliquio animadvertere potuit, quod nemo ex rudissimo vulgo nisi plane caecus, discernere nequiverit? quota videlicet eius pars tegeretur. Vbi aliae apparentiae? Vbi exacta temporum momenta? Sed haec non patent Spermologis, faciundo opus est. Vtinam vero is suis digitis, cum ista scriberet, non plus veritatis lumen offuscare, quantum in ipso erat, nixus esset, quam Solis lux ex istis semitribus digitis Lunae interventu visui nostro subductis, damni quid passus est. Sed valeat iste cum suis versutijs ac scommatis, infirmis infirmationibus: In quibus recensendis plus forte temporis insumpsi, quam mereantur, et quam Epistolae familiaris leges patiantur. Tuum itaque erit, mî ROTHMANNE, hanc prolixitatem boni consulere. Si vero totum hoc scriptum Scoti illius (cum et te nonnunquam perstringat) expetieris, faxo, ut per commodam oportunitatem id nanciscaris. Tunc ipsemet plenius perspicies, quod, in varias formas se vertendo, veritatem, quam oppugnat, expugnare nequeat: quodque infringat solido, propriâ delusus ab arte. Competentem vero et plenam eiusdem refutationem suo forte tempore obtinebunt veritatis Amatores; si non â me ipso, saltem ab aliquo meorum. Neque enim ego libenter cum tam rixosis et arrogantibus ingenijs contendo, qui ex solâ contradictione sibi laudem quaerere satagunt; Neque veritatis intima penetralia ipsis impervestigabilia pro scopo habent. Scripsi autem hac de re paulo copiosius ad te, Doctissime ROTHMANNE; cum quod invectiva ista te quoque impeteret; tum quod sciam, te admodum miraturum, aliquem uspiam fore Mathematicorum, qui Cometas sublunares esse, patefactâ tam evidente in contrarium Demonstratione, asseverare in posterum auderet. Memini enim, te mihi dixisse, cum hîc esses, tales, si qui uspiam prodirent, pro insulsis et stupidis habendos, et risu potius, quam responsione excipiendos. Haec igitur de istis nunc sit dixisse, satis superque.</p><p>Vnum vero vel alterum addam, antequam hasce concludam. Ad Epistolam tuam, quam hîc praesens mihi reddidisti, in quâ Terrae motum tâm annuum quam diurnum ex COPERNICI opinione astruere laborasti, iam dudum nonnulla respondi, quae ob id tibi satisfactura existimo, quod, cum mecum esses, non adeo mordicus sententiam illam tueri videreris, sed paulatim in nostras partes transire. Est vero insuper magnum argumentum infirmitatis assertionum COPERNICEARVM, quod Eccentricitas ♂tis nequaquam ita se habeat, prout illae postulant. Cum enim is ♂tis Eccentricitatem aliquanto minorem, quam fert PTOLOMAeI denotatio, se adinvenisse affirmârit, et hinc etiam solis quietem ac Terrae motionem, eiusque excentricitatem variabilem astruere non dubitârit, adeo ut Excentricitatem ♂tis apparenter imminuerit: tantum abest, quod res ita se habeat, ut ♂tis Excentricitas a nobis deprehensa sit per minime dubias experimentationes eadem pene, quam PTOLOMAeVS illi attribuit, vel potius nonnihilo maior. Nullatenus igitur minor reddita COPERNICEIS placitis suffragatur; quod sane non modicam haesitationem movet, Hypotheses ipsius minus recte constare. Vt de Veneris etiam Excentricitate nihil dicam, quae pariter multo aliter se habet, quam asseveravit COPERNICVS. Apogaeo quoque eiusdem nequaquam in eodem loco octavae sphaerae, quo erat tempore PTOLEMAeI, permanente; ita ut nunc in decimo septimo Gradu ♊ haereret: uti fert COPERNICI ratiocinatio: cum potius initia ♋ obtineat. De quibus alias Deo volente latius. Cuperem ea, quae de fixis Stellis, earundemque Observationibus, cum apud Laudatiss. mem. Principem WILHELMVM Hassiae Landtgravium versareris, consignasti, in publicum êdi, ut constare Posteritati possit, Observationes nostras, in diversis Terrae locis, variâque ratione pervestigatas, quam minimum ab invicem dissidere, et ideo his eo maiorem attribuat fidem. Sunt et alij, qui Landtgravianas Affixarum restitutiones tuo labore peractas obtinere avide desiderant. Da itaque operam, ut, quantum in te est, et praesens et futura aetas tanto fruatur bono. Nos millenas circiter restituimus Stellas in ipso Minuto, tam quoad longum quam latum, quarum Canonicam expositionem Primus noster Tomus Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum, Capite secundo pro maiori parte exhibet. Illic suo tempore, quid nos in earum locis ad amussim disponendis investigaverimus, videbis. Interim et semper mî Optime ROTHMANNE feliciter Vale, et me tuis subinde literis invisere, quod iam diu neglectum est, ne ulterius supersede. Iterumque vale.</p><p>Dabantur Vraniburgi die 14. Ianuarij Anni 1595. T. T. Tycho Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most excellent man, Master Christoph Rothmann, adorned with varied erudition and especially with distinguished skill in Mathematical matters, his much-beloved friend. Sends greeting.</b></p><p>I have received at last your letter, most erudite and most loving <b>Rothmann</b>, long and much before desired — both because our conferences in Astronomical Matters had meanwhile, against custom, been on holiday, and because I longed to learn something certain about you and your health, and where you were lodging (since you had not, for so long a time, returned to your Prince). But I understand from these very [letters] that you, by reason of excessive ill-health, did not return to Kassel itself before [the Landgrave's] death, [your] illness retarding [you] — [an illness] arising from the dissolutions of <b>Tartareous salts</b> [gout/stone], and infesting you this whole time almost without intermission. For which sinister affection of health I greatly condole with you, and that in this still-green age you are subject to such savage torments of diseases. But since you indicate that you have now somewhat breathed again, there is [reason] for me to congratulate you; and I wish that this recovery of health may daily take increase and remain constant. If my <b>Medicines</b>, coming forth from our <b>Pyronomic workshop</b> [chymical laboratory] and prepared with no small labour or expense, can be ready for you in these great evils (if, as happens, they sometime recur), I gladly offer them, and more gladly will impart [them] — just as I did not deny you my service in these [matters] when you were present. But that you did not afterward use it was done by your own fault, you who neither informed me of your adverse health, nor sought anything of the kind from me afterward by letter (though offered unasked at your departure). Whether, therefore, you yourself return hither (as you in some way undertook), or remind [me] of this matter through a sure and suitable man, you will find me by no means difficult to perform this service.</p><p>You exhort in this letter that I let our <b>Cometographic Works</b> come forth into public, and wonder why they lie hidden with me so long — especially since, for the greater part long completed, I have so often and so long promised I would publish them; and [since] not a few erudite men too desire a supply of them to be made for them. But you assert that no small profit will come hence for adorning the Mathematical studies, and vindicating [them] from the disparagements of the unskilled and the calumniators. This is done indeed, my Rothmann, in great part by the ingenuousness of your mind, and that sublime Love toward us and our common studies, and [your] peculiar favour; yet that no little <b>Truth</b> too underlies these (if it might be said without boasting and envy), you do not therein distrust. For what, and how much, can be accomplished by Mathematical exercises rightly administered, has not yet become sufficiently known, even to those philosophizing commonly in the Schools, much less to the rest of the crowd alien from the Muses. And certainly the exact measurement of <b>Comets</b>, and the ingenious Demonstration by what motion they are stirred and how far they are distant from us, so break those [things] which for so many centuries were brought forward by that Greek <b>Aristotle</b> without a settled <b>demonstration</b> [<i>Apodixis</i>], and proclaimed in all the Academies as certain and indubitable assertions, that into how great a chaos of ignorance the cultivators of Philosophy have been involved in this part, now at last, by the Aid of Divine Mathesis, becomes clear among the lovers of truth. And that the same can be done, by the supports of the same incomparable Science, in many other abstruse and hitherto unknown matters (or falsely believed for ascertained), there is no doubt — to say nothing of the several <b>corollaries</b> which are derived hence for more fully cultivating all the other Disciplines. But that I have not yet permitted our Work on Comets to be made public, many and grave reasons have detained me. For besides that I have sweated very much, with unwearied study, for some continuous years now, both on the <b>Observations</b> of those everlasting Stars coeval with the World, and on the reductions of the same to a norm conformable to the Celestial harmony (which matter, as you know, requires not a little both of time and labour), so that on that account it was less convenient to be at leisure seriously for those other [things] — many [things] besides, beyond our expectation and all foresight of removal, have for a long time now fallen out, which have not permitted me to enjoy Philosophic leisure and to devote myself to the rites of Urania as I would wish. So envious is the turbulence of this unclean World against the purer truth, even in Philosophical and visible matters of the perceptible Heaven — much less can it acquiesce, if anything be brought from the shadows of ignorance into open light, in the Divine and invisible [things] of that Archetypal World, unsearchable to human reason. There was added this too, that our <b>Labours</b> in accurately investigating the appearances of the Comets seen in some past years required more time, work, and expense, beyond anyone's estimation, than certain ungrateful men — and even of those who pretend a love of the Disciplines (and indeed of the Mathematical), and no small science — could acknowledge. Among whom there is one especially, who not only judged it fair to give me no thanks for so many wakeful nights, so many expended outlays, and so many Labours drained in Demonstrations and Numbers, that the inviolate truth might be drawn out, but did not blush to assail us with detractions, perversions, [and] impugnations of the disclosed certainty — nay, with cavils and <b>jeers</b> [<i>scommata</i>]. So that I recall that that most excellent and most prudent man <b>Jacob Monau</b>, of honourable memory (for, as I hear, three years ago he yielded to the fates), not undeservedly wrote thus to the eminent Mathematician <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b>, when, [the latter] accommodating [it], he had obtained and inspected our book on the Comet of [15]77 — as I find noted too in Scultetus's letter then given to me: <i>\"O you happy souls, whose care it was, and is today too, to climb into the upper houses! I admired more than I understood so great a diligence of Master Tycho as is noticed in this Work of his. See that you exhort him to bring forth the remaining parts too of that Work — even if this our age be not worthy of these so many and so great goods, [acknowledging them] with the gratitude that was fitting. Breslau, 17 June Old Style, in the year 1588.\"</i> These [things] that excellent man, by the acumen of judgment in which he excelled, and [his] prudence in matters both Philosophical and Political, both brought forward and seemed as it were to presage.</p><p>But he who, before the rest, attempted to belittle and assail our study in the Cometary Matter, was a certain <b>Scot</b> by nation, a <b>Doctor of Galenic Medicine</b>, and beyond measure addicted to the <b>Aristotelian Philosophy</b>. Whom I am unwilling to name here, sparing his honour. He wishes indeed to be held noble by birth, as he insinuates of himself in some places; but yet [he is] too servilely enslaved to the said heathen Philosopher in his better part — that is, in genius and talent — especially in this business of Comets. When, by the intercession of a certain countryman of his visiting me here, he had received our second book on the Phenomena of the Aethereal World (which treats amply of that vast Comet of [15]77), as soon as it was finished by the Types, he tried, by a letter written to me, to call into doubt our assertions there comprised — especially that I showed that that Comet completed its course in the Aethereal Region of the World, and by no means the Elementary (contrary to what Aristotle taught) — and, as far as in him lay, to shake [them]. I certainly thought at the beginning that the man did this in friendly and candid fashion, for the sake of more deeply manifesting the truth. Which many other not-unlearned men too supposed with me — among whom was that Magnificent <b>Jacob Curtius</b>, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, who, by a letter sent me, thought that those who contradicted our assertion in this part did not do it seriously, but only contrived [it], with good intention, that they might fish out more [things] of the kind. And he was indeed a man endowed with perspicacious talent and at the same time keen judgment, not only in Political but in Mathematical Matters too — to add nothing of that most distinguished Doctor <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> and others thinking the same. Since, therefore, I noticed there were [those] who presumed to move a hesitation about our assertions proposed in the said book, so much so that they did not hesitate to contradict them: those indeed who, educated in the Aristotelian school, inferred — from his authority alone and the long-received opinion — that Comets are necessarily generated in the Air, [yet] proving by no Geometrical reasoning (or even under the pretext of one) that the matter stands so, I judged unworthy of refutation — since argumentation drawn from human authority is the weakest of all, and on that account deservedly to be placed last. But to this <b>Scot</b>, since he not only vended much against us the Aristotelian authority (though he extolled even this more than is just), but also pretended some Geometrical [reasoning], I-know-not-what, for establishing his opinion, I judged that he alone should be modestly met — partly that I might dissolve his objections, which bore a certain appearance of the true, and could easily snatch the incautious into by-ways; partly that I might more fully and lucidly explain certain [things] contained in the same second Tome, and set down further certain [things] about the Cometary Matter in a more general way: hoping that I should thus perform a thing not unwelcome to him and to other investigators of truth in this part. Wherefore, a certain extemporaneous <b>Apology</b> having been written among other occupations, I forthwith transmitted it into Scotland to our Antagonist, and communicated the same with certain other Erudite Men in Germany who understood these matters, that I might equally seek out their censures. In which Apology, certainly, I am not conscious to myself of having anywhere transgressed the limits of modesty, or brought forward anything beside the matter. But whether by some affect (as sometimes happens humanly) I here err, let others judge, who both know and wish to discern the true from the false without all prejudice. But how that Aristotelian Philosopher received this our candid intention (which would be acceptable to him, as I hoped) can scarcely be said. For after he had kept this friendly Apology with himself even into the third year, and written nothing of reply in so long a time, I thought either that he had acquiesced in our dissolutions and passed over to these parts, or that he held back because contradictions did not easily occur further. But at last, that deep silence being broken, he hurled at us a certain <b>Antilogetic</b> writing, with a somewhat more virulent and biting (as it seems) mind than he had before dealt by letter — in which he plays and mocks in manifold ways with jeers, taunts, cavils, distractions, and perversions, to elude the truth asserted by us: <b>and acts not so much Scottishly as scoffingly</b> [<i>nec tam Scotice quam scoptice</i>]. For I have had to do with many sprung from Scotland, visiting us here from time to time, distinguished both in Learning and in Birth, in whom I noticed exceptional candour, joined with equal humanity, [and] to whom singular acumen of talent and judgment too was not lacking. But [one] who would assail me and mine with so ungrateful and averse a mind, and so petulant a tongue, I have hitherto found none. Nay, certain of his own countrymen openly detested this immodest deed of his, and complained that he thus rendered their Nation liable to the judgments of foreign and saner men — since he alone did not blush to attempt such things, and, with arrogant daring, to contradict a matter so demonstrated that it was approved by all, even by those who before thought the contrary and had published [it]. And surely, if I may say what is true on my own behalf, not undeservedly did they bring this forth. For whereas other men no less excellent in Mathematics than this Antagonist (to whom in these disciplines I do not deny knowledge, if only he would use it rightly) — who had before pronounced, in their writings published, that that Comet was sublunary and had admitted a Parallax of about five degrees — having received this our book, and the matter being better perceived, not only frankly confessed that they had erred, but gave me no slight thanks because I had shown surer [things]: this one alone, although he himself had found out nothing in that Comet that would be of any moment, nonetheless strove to extenuate our laborious vigils and supreme diligence about these, and to render [them] void as far as in him lay — so much so that he took up again into his hands the data and pronouncements of others too (which the authors themselves at last, the matter better understood, repudiated and retracted), and used — or rather <b>abused</b> — them against us, since he himself had none.</p><p>But that you may in some part learn, excellent <b>Rothmann</b>, with how great injury he arrogated to himself the [right] to afflict not only me, but the very truth which we strove to investigate more deeply to the best of our power, and how scoffingly [he arrogated to himself] to assail my [things], I will briefly run through certain chief passages of his <b>Invective</b> (for so it deserves to be named, rather than any moderate Apology) — commending a more solid and special reply, together with a fitting refutation, to be carried out in its time by one of our Friends, or even Disciples skilled in these matters. He made for this peevish and insolent writing the title: <b><i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i>, or the Refutation of the Sublimation of Comets into the Aether</b> [the \"quenching of smoke-heaven\"]. Whereas I nowhere asserted that Comets — though really Aethereal — consist of <b>smokiness</b> [<i>fumositates</i>], but rather of <b>celestial matter</b>, as our second book (which he attacks) could have advised him, [it] which has thus on p. 254: <i>\"I judge the matter of all Comets to be wholly celestial, since they are all generated in the heaven itself.\"</i> But of the smokes which he vends, nothing. You indeed, my Rothmann, were once of the opinion that <b>terrestrial smokinesses</b> could enter the Heaven (if indeed it were, by your and <b>Jean Pena</b> the Frenchman's opinion, plainly aereal) and furnish matter there for Comets — which, however, I disapproved, and you, afterward taught otherwise from the <b>Milky Way</b>, retracted that very [thing], as may be seen from the Letters communicated between us. He then subjoins, in the same inscription [title], that those [things] which I bring forward for asserting the Celestial position of Comets, and [in] my Apology, are all weakened by him — where he seems not to be consistent with himself in this very front[ispiece], while now he charges me with holding Comets for <b>Aethereal phenomena</b>, before for <b>celestial smokes</b>: and he has not touched, even with the topmost lips, a tenth part of those [things] which we adduced in the second book and in the Apology for asserting the Aethereal position of Comets — much less weakened them all (as he too arrogantly boasts). Nay, those very [things] which he carps at, he does not even shake, much less overthrow or destroy. Then in the writing itself he at once <b>belittles the observations of my Instruments before the long consensus of the ancients</b> — which, how rightly he does, you (who beheld ours, and understand more deeply their use and this handling) can judge. Afterward he is almost wholly either scoffing, or injurious against us, distracting or perverting our [things], and not, as was fitting, rightly accommodating [them]. And, to say in few [words], he either does not at all understand them, or does not wish to understand. He says that he did not at first think I thought thus seriously about Comets, but had constructed <b>Paradoxes</b> for the sake of disputing — whereas our second book sufficiently shows that I do not jest in these, with so many Labours of both Observations and Demonstrations, and Numbers too, drained for that cause, which it is easier to carp at than to imitate. He says too that he by no means dealt with me so as to doubt anything of the truth of his own opinion — perhaps because, placed outside the lot of other men, he could not even doubt, much less err, believing in Aristotle (who among those [men] is <b>\"he himself said it\"</b> [<i>ἀυτὸς ἔφα</i>]). He says too that I accuse others as ignorant and enslaved to errors; and adds scoffingly: <i>\"Fair words, I pray!\"</i> — whereas I nowhere spoke so rigidly, but only modestly marked those who are too much enslaved to Aristotle with a certain prejudice, and showed their errors. He desires too <b>Mathematical Demonstrations</b> in us; and somewhere afterward dares impudently to charge that I alone affirm whatever [I please], and wish credit to be given to me and my Instruments without the necessary and Geometrical induction. How false these [things] are, you and other eminent Mathematicians, who have read ours and surveyed no small abundance of unconquered Demonstrations, can testify. He judges, besides, this matter of Comets to be so light that it needs not many disquisitions — that is, whatever Aristotle once trumpeted is subject to no further difficulty. That I do not yet perceive the truth, and here err to the detriment of Astronomy, he judges (or rather <b>prejudges</b>) more boldly than truly — which I am not ignorant that you understand more rightly. He calls what we wrote in our Apology a certain <b>\"much-rhetoricizing\"</b> [<i>πολυρρητορεία</i>], to which he is unwilling to reply. But under this pretext he passes many [things] with dry foot which he cannot tear down. He objects that I have intruded <b>tailed Stars, strange monsters</b>, into the Heaven — whereas Antiquity taught that there are at least round bodies there. But I asserted that the Comet's body itself is per se round, and that the adhering tail is not of its substance, but comes about <b>per accidens, by an Optical reasoning</b> — so that this too is rather a monstrous calumny. Soon he immodestly reproaches [me] that I attribute <b>Celestial circuits, and Numbers deduced from a Hypothesis</b>, to that Comet, and that I do this in imitation of <b>Maestlin and Cornelius Gemma</b> — whereas rather our endeavour in these ought to have merited favour among candid and discerning talents. And so far is it [from the truth] that I built those [things] of mine from Maestlin or Gemma, that I rather animadverted on their writings more strongly than this <b>Zoilus</b> does — but yet candidly and with good intention. And <b>Wittich</b> could testify, if he were still alive, that we had written our conceptions about that Comet here before Maestlin's or Cornelius's booklets had become sufficiently known to me — so much so that, whereas Wittich before had believed Comets to be wholly sublunary, and Maestlin and others in Germany who asserted the contrary to have hallucinated, convinced by my Observations and Demonstrations alone, he changed his opinion: which too, returning into Germany, he confessed before many, some of whom — and a certain Scot, this man's countryman — reported that very [thing] to me.</p><p>Afterward, that I attributed an <b>Arc of a great circle</b> to the Comet's motion, and, because [the Comet] observed it perpetually in every position, proved thence that it was not sublunary, he does not cease prolixly to cavil, and variously to turn and invert — nay, even to transform [it] Dialectically, perhaps to prove himself an <b>Organic</b> [logician]. Yet the force of the Demonstration he either does not perceive, or obscures by his sophisms. And, to give an example how an Arc of a great circle can be designated below the Moon no less than in the highest Aether, he brings forth from his crafty little brain this <b>ludicrous and futile</b> [example]: <i>\"Let us set out straight from Edinburgh to Uraniborg; in the whole course we shall keep, with our vertices, the track of a great Circle in the eighth orb: and so, furrowing the surface of the sea, we shall come to Uraniborg — [yet] we shall not be in Urania.\"</i> An excellent likeness, forsooth, which limps not on one or another, but on all four feet! — which is to be received with guffaws, or rather to be hissed off, [rather] than dignified with any serious reply, as you and others skilled in these Matters sufficiently understand, since it of itself opposes our intention in nothing. Afterward, by a certain specious and counterfeit Demonstration, he strives to show that Comets — or (as he scoffingly speaks) <b>my Pseudoplanets</b> — can describe a great Circle and nonetheless dwell in the Air far below the Moon, so that they admit a Parallax of five degrees. And this he labours to infer chiefly from its <b>proper motion, which exhausts the Parallax</b>. But here that <b>Pseudophilosopher</b> labours in vain, even had he sworn a thousand times by Aristotle. For we discriminated the Comet's proper motion from that variation which Parallax could insinuate to it at different Altitudes with such skill that neither could be an obstacle to the other, so that the inquiry of truth might rightly proceed hence — as much, indeed, as was needful and fell under the senses. Nor was the proper motion of the Comet of [15]77 (striving toward the East and somewhat the North) so great that it could absorb that Parallax which would occur even in the concave of the Lunar Sphere — much less had it been of five degrees; since by Parallax the Accidental motion, appearing from the surface of the Earth alone, would within two or three hours come out so great as to <b>anticipate the Comet's proper motion and make it seem retrograde</b> — which, however, never happened, as these [things] have been plainly demonstrated by us. All which he passes with dry foot, nor does this subtle disciple of Aristotle wish to notice the by-ways into which he slips. But these will perhaps be more fully refuted elsewhere, from his cloaked Demonstration, or otherwise. And although it be true that those phenomena which are so near us that the semidiameter of the Earth, compared to that distance, comes out sensible, are involved in a <b>threefold motion</b> — namely the universal, the proper, and that which comes about per accidens from Parallax — yet so to separate these, that one does not hinder the other but rather opens [it], is [the mark] of a circumspect Mathematician; nor, with peculiar diligence applied, is it so impossible as this Aristotelian tries in vain to persuade the incautious. For we accomplished that sufficiently, both in the said Comet and in the remaining following [ones] — let him chatter against [it] as much as he will. Let you and others judge these [things] more deeply, weighing [them] without prejudice. Nay, he even doubts whether it can be done in the Moon, if it be presupposed that its true motion is unknown, so that the Parallax may nonetheless be unravelled; and he provokes me to accomplish it. But I judge that this stupidity is not even to be answered, since some one of my <b>recruits</b> [<i>Tyrones</i>], who is accustomed to handle Celestial [matters] not only in books but in the Heaven itself by experience, would resolve this knot not difficultly — which too has very often been done. What will he say of a still <b>fourth divagation of the apparent place, unknown to him</b>, which is suggested optically from the <b>refractions</b> — and that not only in Comets and the Moon, but in all other Celestial bodies, when they especially incline toward the Horizon, and that sensibly more and more below the twentieth degree of Altitude, until at the very Horizon it comes out greatest — as these are sufficiently perceived by you, my Rothmann, from the Landgravian Instruments, [but] which never once came into the mind of this Aristotelian? And although this divarication of refractions lies open to sure knowledge far more difficultly than the other three commixtions of motions and their separations from one another, yet long experimentation, variously sought and confirmed, has taught us both to have this explored and to discern how much it adds to or takes from the rest — so far is it [from the truth] that we cannot conveniently unravel those other variations, which make less trouble.</p><p>Perhaps he deems those [things] which seem impossible to himself and those like him — who exercise Astronomy only on papers and in vain speculations — by no means accessible to others too, who handle it solidly and truly, and deduce [it] from the Heaven by settled experience (as is fitting). Nay, even in those argumentations which he afterward weaves to these, he does not consider that the Comet of [15]77 observed exactly the same track of a great Circle at different Altitudes — which could by no means come about if it had been subject to a very sensible Parallax, or lower than the Moon. Nor is there a just place for contradiction, even if all the Aristotelian <b>Capnocometists</b> [smoke-comet men] should burst. Soon he proceeds to play with jeers in his manner, and says that I establish Comets to be <b>Logical animals</b>, nay, that I discourse of them Logically; and he is not affected with shame to summon hither, Ironically, the author of the <b>Predicables</b> [Porphyry], lest he say nothing. If he had been instructed by plausible conjectures from that truer Philosophy of the <b>Platonists</b> rather than of the <b>Stagirite</b> (who acted the apostate from it), he would surely have perceived both what I had said, and that I had said [things] not so dissonant as he thinks, more rightly, and would perhaps have given me thanks in place of sneers. But, that he may propose demonstratively — or (which is truer) <b>impose</b> — by what reasoning [Comets] can designate an Arc of a great Circle even while dwelling in the sublunary Air, and that he may persuade [us] this comes about from the force of matter, he turns himself into various forms after the manner of <b>Prometheus</b> [Proteus], and intricates this business more than he unravels [it]. And since other laws of motions do not suffice, he calls to aid <b>unsearchable and fictitious Helices</b> [spirals], and feigns and refeigns whatever here, to force the Comets to go such ways as Nature did not attribute to them, nor did any experience find out, in anything, to come about from the Heaven. Nay, neither does such a circumrotation — observing fixed modes and bounds by an ordinary law, so that it can persevere for a long time — belong to <b>fiery and inflamed matter</b>, such as his teacher Aristotle wishes [it] to be. If it were thus permitted to feign at pleasure any motions whatever for Comets (so that some phantastic assumption, most alien from truth, might be <b>saved</b>), surely we shall teach that the <b>birds</b> in the air fly otherwise than the Natural and voluntary impulse bears; and we shall prescribe to them laws why they flit thus and not otherwise, and feign composite, bent, and reflected, ascending and descending Helices, inclined whithersoever it please: and yet the birds will fly away whither they will, and elude our speculation. But these absurdities, which deceive the unskilled, will be unfolded further elsewhere. Furthermore, to prove himself still more a <b>Dialectician</b>, he knits and looses my [things] Sophistically, as his futile rather than subtle quibbling persuades, and ill accommodates my inductions. And, lest he seem unlike himself, he receives our <b>labour in Triangles resolved by Numbers</b> — that not slight or contemptible [labour] — with a certain presumptuous jeer, saying that <i>\"his stomach has long since nauseated at this inanity.\"</i> O dainty stomach, to be cured with <b>Hellebore</b>, that all the insipid matter of nausea may hence be removed, and his <b>smoke-brain</b> [<i>κάπνἐγκέφαλος</i>] cured at the same time! Nor does he leave uncensured [the fact] that I plainly demonstrated, from Observations through its whole duration, that the Comet by its course retained perpetually the same inclination to the Ecliptic — tacitly objecting certain few Minutes, which nowhere exceeded a twelfth part of a Degree — whereas the ancient Astronomers were content to designate the Stars coeval with the World within a sixth part of a Degree. And certainly, in the variation of that Angle of which we spoke, a difference of three or four Minutes is neither of itself sensible enough nor introduces anything of moment. He laughs too at <b>Maestlin</b> (whom by contempt he calls my <b>Parson</b> [<i>Parochus</i>]) — though I have never seen him, nor contracted any acquaintance with him, not even by letter — on account of his Animadversions made by a <b>thread</b> [the cross-thread sight], than which, however, this <b>Galenic Doctor</b> has brought none better; and he sneers at his corollary assumption in a hypothesis — which, although we do not defend, yet so licentiously to carp at others' ingenious inventions, and to teach nothing that is better, is not [the mark] of a moderate and prudent talent. Nor does he blush to call into doubt our Observations [made] by infallible Instruments (as you, who beheld them and experienced their consonant certainty, will easily testify). Nay, he insolently boasts of this hardened audacity, and reproaches [us] with these words: <i>\"Here you will be astounded that your Observations are called into doubt, which you wish to be infallible and to be held for the most certain principles.\"</i> But I am not astounded for this, that he is so stupid and immodest as to presume to pass judgment on a matter unknown [to him], and to shrink from them like an <b>Owl from the Sun's brightness</b>. He has many others like himself on this Mundane stage, who, dim-sighted toward the light of truth and clamouring against it, give credit rather to lies. But that he may bring something whereby to render my diligence in making Observations suspect, that which I had advised in the Apology and elsewhere — that the most exact moments of time cannot be sufficiently established from the Altitudes of the Sun or stars — he cavils at so insipidly and sportively that it is shameful to report. And here he shows how subtle and diligent a scrutator of the Stars he is, since he cannot even understand others' circumspect Animadversions in these, nor recognize the inner chambers of truth in them — much less receive [them] with grateful mind. He ignorantly croaks that the <b>places of the stars restored by me are uncertain</b>, unless others have thought the same — depending, in his manner, on the authority of others. He insipidly laughs too that the <b>Sun's course was restored by me in a certain new Reformation of the World</b>, rendering me, out of his Philosophical humanity, thanks for the restored course of the Sun, on which the other motions of the Stars depend. He blurts too, from excessive ignorance, that <b>Refraction</b>, and the slowness of the change of Altitude about the Meridian, and its disparity in others (as to the equal circuit of the Equator), are nothing. And, that he may render suspect and doubtful my method (subject to no ambiguity) by which I taught that <b>Times</b> are investigated more exactly and surely through a <b>Mechanical Equator, analogous to the Celestial</b>, than from the Altitudes of stars, hear, I pray, with what rash words and how great unskillfulness he prates about these: <i>\"But, I pray, which do you follow? You offer us a Mechanical Equator, whether revolvable or fixed, Analogous to the Equator in its position, and resting on stone or iron columns. These [things] said, you ask whether I doubt that you can find the smallest particles of Time precisely. I am not wont to play Oedipus: why do you suppress the rest? Why do you not bring forward the whole method, which you vaunt as most certain, that it may be known what of truth it has? Your fixed Equator — how will it exhibit the number of the revolution of the Equator, by which time is defined, unless it be moved from its Tetrapod [four-footed stand]? The movable [one] — by what mover will it be turned around Analogously to the same? If you give a Logicon [a rational mover], how will it understand that it moves to the Celestial analogy? If an Alogon [irrational one], whether it act by weight or by another force, I will easily demonstrate in it an anomaly discrepant from the Celestial analogy.\"</i> Thus that good man brings forth a ridiculous censure about my Instruments and observing-methods, unperceived by him: so that, as <b>Maro</b> [Virgil] said of <b>Sinon</b>, <i>\"From one learn all\"</i> (namely the Greeks), so too from these [things] brought forward you may easily estimate of what kind and worth the rest are. He could surely have learned beforehand, from his countrymen frequently visiting me here, whether my Instruments were <b>Logical</b> (i.e. endowed with reason) or not, or whether they were otherwise so elaborated <b>Mechanically</b> that they satisfy our reasoning conveniently and without obstacles — lest he thus seem to rave without reason, and in the very <b>word</b> [<i>λόγῳ</i>] betray himself to be <b>irrational</b> [<i>alogus</i>].</p><p>Next, because in taking <b>Distances</b> I am content with <b>clocks showing Minutes</b> (yet so that they be rectified from time to time, if they have contracted any tiny fault, by the Equatorial distances of the Stars or Sun from the Meridian), he cavils that I reject the times taken from others' clocks, and yet myself flee hither; nay, he objects that I bring forth the little portions of Time <b>adverbially, by no means Mathematically</b>, because, in recounting the distances of the Comet of [15]77 from certain fixed [stars], I do not everywhere set down the very moments of the hours, but sometimes say \"about that time,\" or \"a little after,\" and the like. O subtle wrangler, and empty scrutator of the moments of Time! Inspect, my Rothmann, the first chapter of that book on the Comet transmitted to you, and you will see that I noted there the differences of the hours in [their] particles precisely enough, as much as was needful in investigating the Distances — so much so that, if I had applied greater scrupulousness, I could rather have moved a scruple in the prudent. Would not that subtlety be rather a certain futility, and a superfluous affectation? For when the intervals of the Comet from the Stars were diligently taken, that had to be attempted twice or thrice, that they might agree in the very Minute — which indeed is scarcely conveniently accomplished within a sixth or eighth part of an hour. It sufficed, therefore, to assign to the intermediate Observation a fraction of an hour as near as possible — yet so that nowhere is a divagation of a sixth of one hour committed. For the Comet's motion was not sensibly altered within a sixth portion of an hour, so that on that account [to assign] those moments, as to the distances, by a subtler search than was accomplished by us, would have been plainly idle. So that here this contradictor seems to seek <b>a knot in a bulrush</b> [a difficulty where there is none], which he often does elsewhere too. But where I inquire the <b>Parallaxes</b> of the Comet from the Azimuths and Altitudes, there I did not fail to note not only the first minutes but a fraction of them too, in seconds — because in this place such precision is necessarily needful, but by no means in the distances. And [the fact] that I reprehended in others that they used clocks erroneously and in too broad a manner — that was done about this very practice [of parallax], not as to the designation of distances — which, if they had noted rightly even within a quarter of one hour, I should certainly have condoned them, though the indication were general. See, therefore, how candidly and sincerely that Aristotelian deals with me, while he draws my [things] elsewhere and into diverse [senses], and ill accommodates [them], and seeks subtlety where there is no need, but spurns [it] where indicated and found in the fitting place: which is the manner of those who, with preoccupied mind, try to elude the truth and hold [it] captive. So too, [the fact] that in the examination of the Comet's places by Triangles a discrepancy of 2 or 3 Minutes sometimes falls, he argues that I did not rightly designate its track — with arguteness, forsooth, of vanity: he who ought rather to have admired so near a coincidence than too rigidly to exaggerate that which scarcely falls under the senses, nor is of any weight among candid and intelligent appraisers. Besides, he infers that the Comet's Parallax should have been investigated not from Distances, but only from Altitudes and Azimuths — of which, however, we accomplished both. But, even with him unaware and dim-sighted, the method by Distances, duly applied, is surer than that which is taken from Altitudes and Azimuths, in which a scarcely sensible error comes out very fertile [of consequences] — as these are sufficiently ascertained by you, who handled them, and who have explored how easily one errs in Altitudes and Azimuths, but not so in distances. But this <b>Paper Astronomer</b> [<i>Chartaceus Astronomus</i>] speaks of these and the like almost everywhere as a <b>blind man of colours</b> — and yet so greatly pleases himself and his. And at last he concludes that my Observations are not to be trusted. But even by this single negative he could have answered everything, by his hardened immodesty, and so would not be judged worthy of refutation — since against one who denies the principles there is no disputing. For the Observations are here held for principles, which anyone may rashly deny — but that it is not so is not as quickly proved by anyone. What if we should reply that this is a most putrid calumny and an immodest presumption? Which of us is to be believed? — I, who in the most exact denotations of so many other Stars, and indeed of <b>a thousand fixed [stars]</b>, have, with greater precision than [by] any of the ancients or recent [astronomers] (let the boast be absent), accomplished very many observations now for some continuous years, and demonstrated that they exactly correspond to the truth (the whole Heaven still speaking and bearing testimony for me) — or he, who never learned to handle such [things] with the accuracy that is needful, nor has hitherto brought forth any specimen of it? Nay, even if he most greatly wished, that (as he persuades himself) would by no means be obvious; and as <b>war is sweet to the inexperienced</b>, so too the observation of the Stars seems easy to the unexercised. But he, from a certain levity, arrogates to himself an empty censure, to be valued at a straw, about ours never seen or understood.</p><p>Then he calumniates that I receive <b>Wittich</b>, enjoying blessed rest, indecently and undeservedly. But I deprived that most learned [man] in Mathematics, Wittich, neither living nor dead, of his honours; but the lapses by which he sometimes [erred] in these studies [when] executed from the Heaven (since he was not accustomed to them), I sometimes modestly indicated, for the sake of disclosing the truth. Which I did to him too, often, when present here; nor was that ungrateful to him, as [it is] to this unwarlike champion of his. Where Wittich was worthy of praise — namely in Geometrical [matters], and certain compendia of Triangles — I praised [him], nor ever undeservedly detracted anything from him. But what he chatters too boldly, not only about this [man] but about that supreme man <b>Andreas Dudith</b> [Dudithius], I am unwilling to rejoin. Dudith's own Letter, prefixed to <b>Thaddaeus's</b> [Hájek's] booklet on the Comet of [15]90, sufficiently shows how falsely those [things] are brought forward. Soon he rages, by his rash license, against that excellent man <b>Michael Maestlin</b>; and what I truly advised about the Observation by a <b>thread</b> used by him (when he had no other Instruments at hand), and also what I indicated about the doubts that commonly creep into Instruments, he, perverting and distracting [it] into another sense, in his manner gnaws with biting tongue. And at last he dignifies Maestlin and others — who led the truth, long buried in more-than-Cimmerian darkness in this Cometary business, into open light — with this elogium: <i>\"Certainly I know not what they do not deserve, who befoul the sciences\"</i> — perhaps judging, by his choleric indignation, that they ought to be brought before Judges and publicly animadverted on, because they do not trust Aristotle. Thereafter, after he had extenuated my Instruments and their use, showing that he dares to utter anything without shame, he on the contrary boasts of his own former skill in handling Instruments (such as he once had in Germany), which had proper sights, but not, [like] mine, <b>slitted</b> [<i>rimosa</i>] — superbly and scoffingly (as is his wont) sneering. But why does he not have such [instruments] at hand in <b>Scotland</b> too? Let him produce even <b>one true and useful Observation</b> which he ever accomplished, whether in Germany or Scotland, on any fixed or wandering star, or the Sun or Moon. I scarcely believe [it]. And yet he presumes, in his sloth, to belittle others' unwearied and exquisite labours. He laughs, indeed, at the <b>slits of my sights, parallel to the Cylinder placed at the Centre</b>, and receives [them] with a slitted jeer — but does not see their necessary and most convenient use, which you, my Rothmann, know more rightly; since you always made the greatest [account] of [the thing] first seen here from that same <b>Wittich</b> (whom this Scot so greatly extols) and once communicated to your Prince — nay, you competently restored the other little slit too, neglected by him too securely. Nay, Wittich himself, when he had learned this compendium here from us, said joyfully that he had now at last seen and experienced what had long been sought in vain by <b>Dudith</b> and by himself, and that he had not come into Denmark in vain, even on this account; nay, that he would not exchange this knowledge for the gain of a hundred crowns. But let this Aristotelian snarl and insult with jeers as much as he will — the matter is of itself manifest; which, that he might understand more deeply, he makes himself unworthy. Afterward he adds — or rather objects — certain [things] about <b>Scultetus</b>: that I said [things] dissonant from myself, while sometimes I approve his inventions, sometimes disapprove [them]. But this is not to differ from oneself — to praise what is praiseworthy, and to disapprove, in its place, what fits less. For both can happen to the same author; wherefore both [may] be admitted in the same. Lastly he brings forth, inelegantly, certain [things] about the <b>Landgravian observations</b> collated with ours and others'; nor does he heed how little consonant with truth he speaks. And he closes all these, in place of a Colophon, with this lofty and itching speech: <i>\"No diverticulum [by-path] was to be left on so high a track, by which you could deviate from the truth of your Urania. Return into grace with her, and into the right way.\"</i> Let that dear <b>Priest of Urania</b>, then, intercede for me, that I may return into grace with her — since he presumes himself more rightly known and accepted to her, as one who for so many years has sacrificed to her more conveniently and more devotedly than we perhaps have done.</p><p>Afterward, [the fact] that I asserted that the Comet, [moving] not with a tumultuary and disordered motion but with a certain regular [one] gradually ceasing, displayed the Nature by no means of an Elementary but rather of a <b>Celestial Phenomenon</b> — he, because it at last vanished, tries to infer that it was necessarily Elementary. But this is what, among other [things], we chiefly intend to show — that <b>the Heaven sometimes receives new bodies, subject to alteration</b> — which has hitherto not been believed, on account of Aristotle's authority. But since unconquered experience gainsays [it], let such authority depart — [authority] founded only on plausible and conjectural, not Apodictic, reasonings, and therefore less consonant with truth. And when I had adduced a certain <b>similitude from Metals</b>, that I might somewhat explain, from the Nature of these Terrestrial things, what I wished to hint about the celestial — not that I built any sure proof thence (since similitudes do not sustain that very [thing]) — he, on the contrary, attacks it with many words, and those Sophistical and alien from candour. And, lest he think with us, against the faith given to Aristotle, he <b>sins</b>, departing from him (who affirms certain [things] similar to us about the generation of Metals), and denying that all metals consist of one and the same <b>prima materia</b> — so much so that he even contends that <b>Mercury is not in gold</b>. And he frivolously boasts many [things] about these — perhaps to display himself, by his arrogant talent, as not inexperienced in Chymistry and the Nature of Metals; in which, however, he is equally as little versed as in Astronomical [matters]. For of both he vends <b>words for things</b>. But these will perhaps be more specially refuted elsewhere. But lest that <b>New Star by Cassiopeia</b> stand in the way of him and his fellow-Aristotelians — so that they may not persistently assert, even against experience itself and against Geometrical Demonstrations, that the Heaven does not admit new bodies subject to dissolution — he strives to call into doubt that very Star, and all the truth about its appearances, by such cunning subterfuges, that he thus sufficiently shows how little he is a lover of truth, and how he does nothing else than overthrow this by specious evasions: which ought to have been alien from an upright and sincere man. But it is shameful to report what he here, with the greatest immodesty, does not fear to spew out against the indubitable experiments of many excellent Mathematicians, labouring to abolish all certainty from the variety of opinions. By which argumentation, surely, almost nothing certain will any longer remain, since there is scarcely anything about which men's opinions do not vary. But a distinction must be made between those who discourse according to <b>experience drawn from sure reasonings and Demonstrations</b>, and those who do not: and in these [matters] opinions are not to be <b>numbered, but weighed</b>. Nay, he does not spare even his <b>half-countrymen</b>, that he may make plain the love with which he follows certain Erudite men in <b>England</b>. He falsely reproaches <b>John Dee</b>, a most excellent Mathematician and Philosopher (whom you knew, when he was honourably received by the Landgrave), that in his <b><i>Nucleus Parallaticus</b></i> he takes his beginning within the bosom of a half-degree. He scoffingly receives, in those same words, the <b>Wings</b> [<i>Alae</i>] of <b>Thomas Digges</b> — [which], like a falcon, attain immobile Phenomena by a remote flight. From all which <b>Dirges</b> he at last concludes thus, so as to say (or rather feign) everything in one word: <i>\"That marvellous Phenomenon so eluded all the Mathematicians of that time that, unless they recognize and recook [it], they cannot then say that they have demonstrated what you say.\"</i> And since he seems not even thus to have satisfied himself, he at last flees, as to a sacred anchor, to a certain adage of his Teacher: <i>\"One swallow does not make spring\"</i> — so that he may impose on us [the notion] that the force of proof lurks even in Aristotelian taunts. Let it be, indeed, that one swallow does not make spring, and that such [sayings] have place in similar [cases]: but <b>one star once seen in the highest Heaven, which afterward ceased to be, necessarily proves that the Heaven sometimes admits new bodies subject to perishing</b> — so much so that Aristotle himself, if surviving, had he noticed that new Star to have shone for us from the highest Aether, and at last, after a year or a little more, to have ceased to be beheld, would not have dared even to mutter against so evident an experience, but rather would have totally corrected hence his manner of philosophizing about the Nature of the Celestials — which many other eminent Aristotelians, taught hence, afterward did; this Scot almost alone still wallowing and persevering in the mire of an error once snatched up and confirmed; who reckons it sport to despise even the authority and experience of that ancient <b>Hipparchus</b>, while he says thus: <i>\"Nor is a Plinian narration the Demonstration of another\"</i> — as if either Pliny said false [things] about Hipparchus, or [Hipparchus] could not discern, among the old Stars (almost all of which he measured with his Instruments), some new one born in the Heaven in his times. Let these insolences depart. But because in our first Tome of the Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i> we have treated of this star amply and professedly, and most plainly demonstrated that it stood in the highest Aether for over a year and at last disappeared, so that there is no place for evasion — and at the same time abundantly disquired others' opinions for and against — so that thence it appears more than sufficiently how absurd and insipid, and most alien from truth itself, are the [things] which this Aristotelian here heaps up about that star; and thence, in the rest too, <b>how truthful he is, one may know, as the Lion from the claw</b> [<i>ex ungue Leonem</i>] — therefore I do not judge it worthwhile to refute these at greater length, and to demonstrate their insufficiency.</p><p>Nay, while he afterward raves variously about Comets, and seems to attack you too, while he says Ironically that they are even carried above Saturn; and [while] he addresses me — because I said we are taught, from a Nature of the Celestials other than Aristotle thought, that the Heaven is not eternal or a partaker of Divinity (as he wished), but also sometime subject to dissolution, [and] that the Creator admonishes us in these ascititious bodies of the Heaven, which perish, as it were by a certain example — this our <b>Scourge</b> [<i>Mastix</i>] addresses [me] no less scoffingly, saying that I capture great consolation hence, namely that I so establish [it]; and soon, gnawing in his manner, he further adds, not abstaining even from that too-vulgar [syllogism]: <i>\"The staff stands in the corner; therefore the Pontiff is at Rome.\"</i> He calls too, on this occasion, the Comets (which it pleased me to call certain <b>Pseudoplanets</b> by Philosophical liberty) <b>my Pseudoprophets</b>; and he superfluously adduces many other [things] for Aristotle — that the World is by its own Nature eternal and incorruptible, but will have an end only by the will of God. About which I will add nothing, since they make little for the matter in question, but are rather certain pompous ostentations of unexplained disputations. Then, returning to the Comets and their consumption, he feigns many [things] too boldly, which neither belonged to the Comet of [15]77 (of which chiefly [we] treat) nor were anywhere attributed to it by us, but plainly the contrary [was] shown. And because I said that that Comet was slower in its proper motion than the Moon, and therefore stood higher — [a thing] not lacking probability, though I wove in no necessity here — he scarcely finds enough sneers with which to assail [it]. He says nothing prevents [the Comet] from flying off in this way to the throne of Jupiter, like another <b>Icaromenippus</b>; nay, he summons too Lucian's fables of the broken <b>Bridge of Asses</b> — so as to betray that he too greatly esteems Lucian's illusions, and savours his nature. And because I had indicated in passing, as it were, that the Comet directed its journey from the <b>Tropic of Cancer to the Tropic of Capricorn</b>, and contained itself within these bounds (by which the Sun's orbit too is enclosed), and therefore insinuated something hidden, tacitly <b>analogous to the Celestials</b> — he, sneering, mocks thus: <i>\"Whether, by its [own] intelligence, fleeing the cold of the former (namely Capricorn), it wished to proceed to the hearth of the latter (namely Cancer).\"</i> But let these jeers be — which argue nothing but the author's vanity and levity. Afterward, presuming to tear down my <b>Mathematical reasonings</b>, he enumerates indeed by what method I investigated the Comet's Parallax, that he may display himself as somehow understanding ours; but he weakens these there by no contrary reasons, except that he calls [it] a <b>leaden norm</b> by his affected authority, and objects, by a vain scruple, that the difference of longitude between <b>Kassel</b> and this place is doubtful in some minutes, and rejects the Landgravian Observations — not all, but certain selected ones used by me — and says that others, which we neglected, would by his arbitration have been truer; and heaps up things like these, little proving, nor of themselves wholly true, and making nothing for the matter. If from any Landgravian Observations (which I, in the second book, frankly noted as they had been communicated to me) he could have proved that that Comet was <b>sublime</b> [sublunary], there is no doubt that he would have done that very [thing], as far as in him lay. But because no such [observation] was obvious, he, at pleasure, involves certain and settled [things] in doubts, and strives to obscure [them]. I selected the Landgravian observations not by arbitration, so as to neglect the surer ones, but preferred to use those which were most distant in time, so that the differences of Parallaxes, which are thus increased, might the more readily shine forth thence.</p><p>Nay, he inveighs licentiously against you too, most learned <b>Rothmann</b>, because you asserted that the <b>Comet of 1585</b> was situated in the higher Heaven. But that you may know how he does it, behold his words: <i>\"But I come to your artifice. For not only you, but he too — that Christoph Rothmann, the Landgravian Mathematician, so greatly commended by you for [his] science of demonstrating and skill of observing — uses the same Method, by your report, while he pursues the Comet of the eighty-fifth year above Saturn, into the space nearest below the fixed [stars]; with marvellous sagacity, certainly. And he too will be held ignorant of Mathematics who doubts that you have demonstrated that your Comet completed its course in the Aether. This censure you so value that you oppose [it] to the immature opinions of contradictors. Your [judgment], most distinguished Master Tycho, I am, if anyone, about to commend for friendship, and now I would [wish to]: but I am not wont to flatter a Friend against the truth. I should wish the Observations, if not the demonstrations, of that Artificer communicated to me, by which he asserted this Paradox; and whether his skill or science reaches so far, you yourself would be the judge. If, for the defence of your Urania, you wish the truth manifested, communicate the Observations,\"</i> etc. To these reply yourself for yourself, since you do not need my help; nor is it difficult, since the truth stands on our side, not to be moved by six hundred illusions of this kind. I neither wished nor wish to communicate to him your Observations, and the Demonstrations resting on these — since he strives only <b>not to use but to abuse</b> them, by perverting, distracting, and cavilling (as he does with mine). And to what end would it be needful to impart such [things] to him, since he presumes to pass a censure even on those not seen, much less understood — as becomes good (forsooth) judges? O insipid arrogance!</p><p>But afterward, returning to himself, and as it were having got some secret counsel from the <b>Delphic Tripod</b> by which to impugn my Mathematical reasonings about the Comet's Parallaxes, and by the same labour to attack you too (since I had signified that you, by a certain similar induction to that which we too used, had found that the Comet of 1585 had no Parallax at all), he is not ashamed to say that the <b>motion which we call diurnal is only apparent, and by no means true</b>; and therefore that all its subsumed parts too are similarly apparent. But here that perspicacious <b>Critic of others' labours</b> — who, however, knows not how to use the due <b>spectacles</b> — does not attend [to the fact] that the place of the Comet, which is observed at some certain Altitude, although it be apparent and subject to Parallax (if it have any), is nonetheless, the same universal motion being revolved, until it returns again to the same position and the same height above the horizon, then indeed observed apparently. But since the Parallax on both sides is entirely the same, as much as falls under the senses, therefore this too on both sides equally adds to or takes from the true motion. Since, therefore, what is equal is on both sides added or taken away, what remains will necessarily be judged equal to the former, even by the very first Geometrical axioms. From which it follows that, as much as the Comet, observed twice at the same Altitude from the surface of the Earth, appears apparently advanced by one diurnal revolution, just so much has it been truly varied by its [own] motion, which proceeds from the Centre; and therefore the <b>difference of the true and apparent motion</b>, since in such a position they are equated to one another, [means] the apparent does not differ from the true. But in other arcs, which do not have a whole circuit and are taken at different Altitudes, the matter comes out far otherwise. For there the differences of Parallaxes are not equated to one another; but the nearer the phenomenon is to the Earth, the greater the inconvenience [that] falls — from which too the distance from us can be established. But these and the like that acute and arguing Doctor could not see. Nor, if he used several <b>eye-salves</b> [<i>collyria</i>], can he discern [them], on account of the blindness by which he is hindered through the Aristotelian tenets. And therefore he speaks only by authority (which is none), and mixes in here I-know-not-what about the <b>Hypotheses of mean and unequal motions</b>, making plainly nothing for this matter — so that he thus shows that he did not understand ours, much less can judge of them. And therefore he closes this his censure with a certain arrogant prejudice thus: <i>\"So that neither you nor Rothmann can here pretend or persuade any demonstration among those skilled in Mathematics. Which I wished sincerely to indicate to you.\"</i> Here that [phrase] used by him at the beginning would deservedly be subjoined: <i>\"Fair words, I pray!\"</i> But let him hearken to this too — that nothing is so well said but that it can be depraved by calumny.</p><p>Next, variously cutting up our reasonings (by which I amply prove that the Comet introduced a smaller diversity [parallax] than the Moon itself), and abusing them at pleasure, he assails [them], and calls into doubt that which from the foregoing was sufficiently clear to candid Mathematicians and needed no further Demonstration, that he may have [something] to cavil at. But all these will perhaps in their place and time be beaten back upon him — as also the rest subjoined to these in place of a corollary; and what he adds besides against our <b>third proof</b>: and that by the same right as the rest — that is, by a certain blindness of mind and preoccupied judgment, and not without affected presumption — he heaps up here and there craftily and falsely [things] by which he can not difficultly entangle and deceive the incautious. But by those who understand these more deeply, and weigh [them] with a more solid judgment, and that candid and sincere, he will be judged, by saying many [things], to have said nothing. Nay, he afterward instructs me how this third reasoning, which proceeds from diverse places, can be corrected. But since he can so finely instruct others — more rightly perhaps, and longer versed in this <b>Palaestra</b> — and free [them] from errors, why does he not himself set his hand to the Work, and bring forth something surer (if it could be done)? Forsooth, it is easier to say than to do, to speculate than to know, to censure than to imitate. This judicious censor seems to be ignorant that many [things] can be devised speculatively and Theoretically which admit a Geometrical Demonstration too, but are not on that account brought into practice and exercised Mechanically with fruit. So too what he subjoins about the <b>Longitudes of certain places</b> are trifles, proceeding from a malevolent, not a candid, mind. At last, too, against our last proof from Altitudes and Azimuths (Declination being applied at the same time), he sharpens his pen with a license similar to before, and a blind impulse of mind, drawing our [things] into by-ways and using them perversely — so much so that he does not leave uncensured even that diligence which I applied about reducing the <b>Regiomontanus</b> reasoning into use, as far as could be done; and he heaps up many other [things], from mere <b>self-love</b> [<i>philautia</i>] and the lust of contradicting (to which he is accustomed in the Aristotelian and <b>Scotistic</b> schools), which it irks and shames [me] to report or rejoin here singly. Nay, he comes at last to such madness or malevolence that he does not hesitate to charge that I thus <b>profane my name</b>, where I most greatly would wish to consecrate [it]. Which if it were true, surely you, in this very Letter, say in vain that I shall not a little illustrate the Mathematical studies, and consecrate [them] to posterity, if, namely, I publish our Work on Comets. But to refute all these in due manner will be [a matter] of another business; for it would be too prolix here. The time will perhaps come when the more sensible will perceive that this Aristotelian applauded himself too much, and sang the triumph before the victory — however much he be either unable or unwilling to open his eyes to the light of truth. The last clause of this scoffing and arrogant writing — that the last may answer to the first and the middle — runs thus: <i>\"These [things] it pleased [me] to write, for the sake of truth and friendship, against your smokinesses.\"</i> But how consonant with truth [the things] he has brought are, let it be the judgment of you and of others understanding these more rightly. Which too will some time (as I said) appear more fully.</p><p>With what <b>friendship</b> he did it — if my censure about those [things] which concern me myself be suspect — let us hear what that excellent [man], endowed with ingenuous candour, <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> wrote to me about this very <i>Capnurania</i> (transmitted both to him and to Lord <b>Curtius</b>, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, most skilled in Mathematics, that they might inquire into it) — which [things] too were once read aloud to this Antagonist in Scotland, from Thaddaeus's own autograph, not without his unbridled fretting: <i>\"Today, which is the last day of November of the year 1592, New Style, when I had received this last [letter] of yours, written 28 September, from the Magdeburg courier, together with that writing — bitter indeed — of that haughty, hardened, biting, and unsound Antagonist of yours, on the next day I gave both your Letter and that writing to Lord Curtius to be read. He first bids you very much farewell [greets you], and [bids] it be signified to you that that writing of the Scot rests on no firm reasons, [and] wishes utterly to abolish the whole doctrine of Parallaxes by certain phantasms of his, [and] is a hostile, not a friendly writing: and so he judges it must altogether be refuted by you. And a little after he subjoins: While I write this, that Scot — called the <b>subtle Doctor</b> — came into my mind: what the Writers report of him, that he was stabbed through with [stylus-]pens [</i>styli pugillares<i>]. I should judge that this Scottish Antagonist of yours — because he desires to overthrow, by his sophisms, the admirable and splendid part of Astronomy concerning Parallaxes — ought much more justly to be subdued and stabbed through by all the students of Astronomy than that [other Scotus]. This you alone, to whom neither faculty nor will is lacking, will be able most happily to accomplish with [your] pen.\"</i> Thus far Thaddaeus.</p><p>He says soon that he wrote those [things] <b>cursorily</b> [<i>carptim</i>], on which he sweated perhaps with great labour over two years. If, therefore, for \"cursorily\" he had said \"by carping\" [<i>carpendo</i>], he would surely have brought forth truer [things]. Nay, he calls my conceptions about Comets <b>my smokinesses</b>, not ceasing to vend smokes for things, and impudently to calumniate and blurt jeers — whereas I nowhere (as I protested even at the beginning) judged that Comets consist of any smokinesses; but he, with his Aristotle and his followers, so opines rather than knows. Wherefore let him have his smokes and smokinesses with himself, and not obtrude [them] on others (however vendible they stand for sale with him) who are unwilling to buy such levities. At the end, finally, to excuse cunningly the <b>delays of more than two years</b> in completing this Apologetic writing (for I first received it in the year 1592 about the end of May, whereas our Apology had been transmitted to him in the year 1589), he subscribes that it was completed <b>on the day on which the Sun, in the year [15]91, was obscured to nearly 2½ digits</b>. O bald excuse, pretended even from the Eclipsed Sun itself! And O observer, who could notice in the Sun's eclipse [only] that which no one of the rudest crowd, unless plainly blind, could fail to discern — namely, what part of it was covered! Where [are] the other appearances? Where the exact moments of time? But these do not lie open to <b>Spermologi</b> [gossip-mongers / chatterers]; there is need of doing [the work]. But would that he, with his digits [fingers/eclipse-digits], when he wrote those [things], had striven (as far as in him lay) to obscure the light of truth no more than the Sun's light suffered any loss from those two-and-a-half digits withdrawn from our sight by the Moon's intervention. But let that [man] fare well, with his cunnings and jeers, [and his] infirm infirmations — in reviewing which I have perhaps spent more time than they merit, and than the laws of a familiar Letter permit. It will therefore be yours, my Rothmann, to take this prolixity in good part. But if you desire the whole of that Scot's writing (since it sometimes touches you too), I will see that you obtain it by a convenient opportunity. Then you yourself will perceive more fully that, turning himself into various forms, he cannot <b>storm the truth which he assails</b>, and that he <b>shatters himself against the solid, deluded by his own art</b>. But a competent and full refutation of the same the Lovers of truth will perhaps obtain in its time — if not from me myself, at least from one of mine. For I do not gladly contend with such quarrelsome and arrogant talents, who strive to seek praise for themselves from contradiction alone, nor have the inmost chambers of truth (unsearchable to them) for [their] mark. But I have written about this matter somewhat more copiously to you, most learned Rothmann — both because that invective attacked you too, and because I know you will greatly wonder that there is anywhere any of the Mathematicians who would dare in future to assert that Comets are sublunary, [when] so evident a Demonstration to the contrary has been disclosed. For I remember that you said to me, when you were here, that such [men], if any anywhere came forth, must be held for insipid and stupid, and received rather with laughter than with reply. Let these, then, now be said about those [matters], more than sufficiently.</p><p>But one or another [thing] I will add before I conclude this. To your Letter, which you handed me here in person — in which you laboured to build the <b>motion of the Earth, both annual and diurnal, from Copernicus's opinion</b> — I replied [with] some [things] long since, which I think will satisfy you on that account, that, when you were with me, you seemed not to defend that opinion so tooth-and-nail, but gradually to pass over to our parts. But there is, besides, a great <b>argument of the infirmity of the Copernican assertions</b>, that the <b>Eccentricity of Mars</b> by no means stands as they require. For whereas he affirmed that he found the Eccentricity of Mars somewhat smaller than Ptolemy's denotation bears, and hence did not hesitate to build the <b>rest of the Sun and the motion of the Earth, and its variable eccentricity</b>, so much so that he apparently diminished the Eccentricity of Mars: so far is it [from the truth] that the matter stands thus, that the Eccentricity of Mars has been found by us, by least-doubtful experimentations, <b>nearly the same as Ptolemy attributed to it, or rather somewhat greater</b>. By no means, therefore, rendered smaller, does it support the Copernican tenets — which surely moves no little hesitation that his Hypotheses stand less rightly. To say nothing of the <b>Eccentricity of Venus</b> too, which likewise stands far otherwise than Copernicus asserted; its <b>Apogee</b> too by no means remaining in the same place of the eighth sphere where it was in Ptolemy's time — so as now to stick at the <b>seventeenth degree of Gemini</b>, as Copernicus's reasoning bears — whereas it rather holds <b>the beginning of Cancer</b>. About which, otherwise, more amply, God willing. I should wish that those [things] which you set down about the fixed Stars and their Observations, when you were engaged with the Prince <b>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse</b>, of laudable memory, be published — that it may be established for Posterity that our Observations, investigated in diverse places of the Earth and by various method, differ as little as possible from one another, and that it [Posterity] therefore attribute the greater credit to these. And there are others too who eagerly desire to obtain the <b>Landgravian restitutions of the Fixed [stars]</b> accomplished by your labour. Give your effort, therefore, that, as far as in you lies, both the present and the future age may enjoy so great a good. We have restored about <b>a thousand Stars</b> to the very Minute, both as to longitude and latitude, of which the <b>Canonical exposition</b> our First Tome of the Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i>, in the second Chapter, exhibits for the greater part. There, in its time, you will see what we have investigated in disposing their places exactly. Meanwhile, and always, my excellent Rothmann, fare happily, and do not further forbear to visit me from time to time by your letters — which has now long been neglected. And again, farewell.</p><p>Given at Uraniborg, the 14th day of January of the year 1595. Yours truly, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VI (<i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>); first printed by Tycho in his own 1596 <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i>, Liber I (which it closes); no MS witness recorded. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VI pp. 319–337</b> (the closing letter of Liber I); every crux confirmed, including the embedded quotations and the Dee/Digges work-titles (anchors in the Editorial note).</li><li><b>Sender / recipient &amp; the end of the correspondence.</b> Tycho (Uraniborg) → <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, now chronically ill (gout/stone, \"Tartareous salts\") since his <b>1590 visit to Hven</b>, who never returned to Kassel before <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV's death (1592)</b> and to whom this — the <b>last and longest Tycho letter to him</b> — was sent; Rothmann never replied, and the great correspondence ends here. Tycho opens with condolence and an offer of his <b>Pyronomic (chymical) medicines</b>.</li><li><b>The Craig refutation (the body — ~90% of the letter).</b> Tycho explains the long delay in publishing his comet-works (the perennial-star labour; the world's turbulence; ungrateful critics), quotes <b>Jacob Monau</b>'s letter to <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Breslau, 17 June 1588 O.S.) praising the comet book, then mounts a sustained demolition of the <b>unnamed Scot = John Craig</b> and his <b><i>Capnuraniae restinctio</b></i> (\"Quenching of the Smoke-Heaven\"). Craig is called \"Galenic Doctor,\" \"Aristotelian,\" \"Pseudophilosophus,\" \"<b>Zoilus</b>,\" \"Capnocometista,\" \"Chartaceus Astronomus\" (Paper Astronomer), \"Mastix\" (Scourge), and his book an \"Invective\"/\"Antilogeticum\"; the wordplay <i>nec tam Scotice quam scoptice</i> (\"not so much Scottishly as scoffingly\") sets the tone. Tycho's defences: comets are <b>celestial matter, not smoke</b> (the <i>De recentioribus</i> p. 254 self-quote again, as in the undated Peucer letter of 1590 and the Hájek letter of 3 Aug 1590); the <b>tail is per accidens optical</b>, the comet-body round; he did <b>not</b> copy Maestlin/Gemma (Wittich's testimony); the <b>great-circle arc</b> at all altitudes proves super-lunar position; <b>proper motion does not exhaust the parallax</b> (the Edinburgh-to-Uraniborg great-circle joke refuted; the threefold motion separable; a fourth <b>refraction</b> divagation Craig ignores); the instrument-time method (the <b>Mechanical Equator</b> — Craig's \"Logicon/Alogon\" sneer quoted and turned: <i>in ipso λόγῳ alogum se prodit</i>); the \"<b>adverbial not mathematical</b>\" time-notation charge; the defence of <b>Wittich</b> (and <b>Dudith</b>), of <b>Maestlin</b> (\"my Parson,\" the thread-sight), and of the <b>slit-sights</b>; the <b>1572 nova</b> as decisive proof the heavens admit new bodies (Craig's \"one swallow doesn't make spring\" and \"staff in the corner → Pope at Rome\" sneers; his slighting of <b>Hipparchus/Pliny</b>); the <b>metals/Mercury-in-gold</b> alchemy quarrel; the <b>Icaromenippus/Lucian</b> and <b>Cancer–Capricorn</b> jokes; the diurnal-motion-is-only-apparent argument refuted <b>geometrically</b> (equal parallax at equal altitude cancels). Crucially, Craig also attacked <b>Rothmann</b> (long quotation: \"I come to your artifice… communicate the Observations\") and <b>John Dee</b> (the <i>Nucleus parallaticus</i> / <i>Parallaticae commensurationis nucleus</i>, 1573, \"begins within a half-degree\") and <b>Thomas Digges</b> (the <i>Alae seu scalae mathematicae</i>, 1573). Tycho closes the refutation with the <b>Hájek letter</b> (which forwarded the <i>Capnurania</i> to Hájek and <b>Curtius</b>) and <b>Curtius's verdict</b> (the grim <i>Duns Scotus stabbed-with-pens</i> joke — Craig should be \"stabbed through by all astronomers\"), and mocks Craig's dating his completion to the <b>2½-digit solar eclipse of 1591</b>.</li><li><b>The closing astronomy (the anti-Copernican coda).</b> On Rothmann's in-person 1590 letter defending the <b>Earth's annual + diurnal motion</b> (Rothmann had softened toward Tycho): the <b>Mars eccentricity</b> is <i>not</i> smaller (as Copernicus needed for the Earth's motion + variable eccentricity) but <b>≈ Ptolemy's or larger</b>, refuting the Copernican hypotheses; likewise <b>Venus's eccentricity</b> and its <b>apogee</b> (not 17° Gemini per Copernicus but ~beginning of Cancer). Tycho urges Rothmann to publish the <b>Landgravian fixed-star observations</b> (cross-location agreement), and notes his own <b>1000-star catalogue</b> to the minute (<i>Progymnasmata</i> Tome I, ch. 2).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann, Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b> (d. 1592); <b>John Craig</b> (the unnamed Scot, <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i>); <b>Jacob Monau</b> (Breslau humanist, d. ~1592), <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (Görlitz); <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (Kurz von Senftenau, Vice-Chancellor); <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b>; <b>Maestlin, Cornelius Gemma, Paul Wittich, Andreas Dudith</b>; <b>John Dee</b> (the <i>Nucleus parallaticus</i>) &amp; <b>Thomas Digges</b> (the <i>Alae</i>); <b>Jean Pena</b>; <b>Aristotle</b> (the \"Stagirite\"), <b>Plato/the Platonists, Porphyry</b> (\"author of the Predicables\"), <b>Hipparchus, Pliny, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Regiomontanus</b>; <b>Lucian</b> (Icaromenippus, the Bridge of Asses), <b>Virgil</b> (\"Maro,\" on Sinon), <b>Duns Scotus</b> (the \"subtle Doctor,\" in Curtius's joke). Places: <b>Uraniborg, Kassel, Breslau, Scotland/Edinburgh, England, Magdeburg, Delphi</b> (the Tripod, figurative).</li><li><b>Greek / Latin tags.</b> <i>ἀυτὸς ἔφα</i> = \"he himself said it\" (the Pythagorean/Aristotelian appeal to the master); <i>πολυρρητορεία</i> (src <i>πολυροτορέιαν</i>) = \"much-rhetoricizing\"; <i>κάπνἐγκέφαλος</i> = \"smoke-brain\"; <i>λόγῳ … alogum</i> = \"in the [very] word/reason … irrational\"; <i>Capnurania</i> = \"smoke-heaven\" (καπνός + Οὐρανία); <i>scoptice</i> = scoffingly; <i>ex ungue Leonem</i> = \"the lion from the claw\"; <i>nodum in scirpo quaerere</i> = \"to seek a knot in a bulrush\"; <i>bellum dulce inexpertis</i> = \"war is sweet to the inexperienced\" (Erasmus/Pindar); <i>ex uno disce omnes</i> (Virgil, <i>Aen.</i> II.65, of Sinon); <i>una hirundo non facit ver</i> (Aristotle/Erasmus); <i>Baculus stat in angulo, ergo…</i> (a stock fallacy); <i>Spermologi</i> = seed-pickers/gossips (Acts 17:18); <i>Tyrones</i> = recruits/beginners; <i>Mastix</i> = scourge; <i>philautia</i> = self-love. Glyphs: ♂ Mars, ♀ Venus, ♊ Gemini, ♋ Cancer, ☉ Sun, ☽ Moon.</li><li><b>Dating (protocol §3, a genuine composition-span crux).</b> Header dateline <b>14 January 1595 O.S.</b> (the dispatch date). But the letter <b>embeds its own composition note</b>: the Hájek/Curtius excerpt is dated <i>\"Today, which is the last day of November 1592, New Style\"</i> (<b>30 Nov 1592 N.S.</b>), when Hájek received Rothmann's <b>28 Sept</b> letter and Craig's screed via the Magdeburg courier. So Tycho <b>began the letter in late 1592 and only completed/sent it in Jan 1595</b> — a span of over two years (mirroring Craig's own delays that Tycho mocks). Other embedded dates: Monau <b>17 June 1588 O.S.</b>; Tycho's Apology sent to Craig <b>1589</b>; Craig's <i>Capnurania</i> received <b>end May 1592</b>; the solar eclipse <b>1591</b>. The header leads with the catalogued dispatch date, the internal note flagged.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> ~40 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590 and 13 Sept 1588, to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588, to Herwart of 16 Nov 1599, from Rothmann of 13 Oct 1588 and 20 Jan 1587, and to Hájek of 3 Aug 1590); v/u orthography retained; the embedded <b>quotations</b> (Monau, Craig ×several, Hájek/Curtius) are set in <i>italics</i> inline and identified above. OCR/print slips read through: <i>πολυροτορέιαν</i> (read <i>πολυρρητορείαν</i>), <i>Prometheus</i> used for the shape-shifter (properly <b>Proteus</b> — Tycho's own slip, kept), <i>sublimarem</i> (read <i>sublunarem</i>), <i>astruere/asstruere</i> variants. The Craig-quotations are reproduced as Tycho gives them (his excerpting).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Swapped the reversed 'former'/'latter' labels in the Rothmann-quotation clause ('fleeing the cold of the former [Capricorn]... the hearth of the latter [Cancer]'); the bracketed sign identifications were already correct and are unchanged. No change to the Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. The longest letter in the corpus — a condolence, a publication-apology, a vast point-by-point refutation of Craig's <i>Capnuraniae restinctio</i> (with embedded quotations from Monau, Craig, Hájek, and Curtius, and the defence of Maestlin, Wittich, Dudith, John Dee, and Thomas Digges), and an anti-Copernican astronomical coda — rendered securely throughout; the registers (warm condolence; weary self-justification; sustained, witty invective; sober technical close) are kept distinct, and the controlled glossary (parallax, refraction, eccentricity, apogee, hypothesis, <i>salvare</i>/save, <i>Apodixis</i>, <i>restitutio</i>, the comet/nova doctrine) is held consistent with the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 (the Craig dossier), the Hájek letters of 11 Jun and 3 Aug 1590 and the undated Peucer letter of 1590 (the comet-matter doctrine and the <i>p. 254</i> self-quote), the Landgrave, Herwart, and Rothmann letters of 1587–99 (the instrument/refraction threads), and the Peucer letter of 13 Sept 1588 (the world-system). The embedded <b>Hájek/Curtius quotation independently fixes the 30 Nov 1592 N.S. composition date</b>, and the Craig quotations are internally coherent. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VI pp. 319–337) and confirms every crux</b> — including the 30 Nov 1592 N.S. internal note, the dual-date, and the Dee/Digges work-titles — so the collation reservation is <b>lifted</b>. One residue remains: the <b>composition-span dating</b> (begun 1592 N.S., sent 1595 O.S.) is reproduced and flagged, not collapsed, and Tycho's own <i>Prometheus</i>-for-<i>Proteus</i> slip is kept. It does not obscure the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter, so per brief this is capped at grade B regardless of quality; audited against source_text alone. Read the full source_text (18 paragraphs / paras 1,3,5,...,35, ~61,500 chars: the salutation, Rothmann's illness and Tycho's Pyronomic remedies, the Cometographica-publication delay with Monau's/Scultetus's 17 June 1588 letter, the unnamed Scottish antagonist's introduction, the 'Capnuraniae restinctio' title and the p.254 self-quote on comets' celestial matter, ~10 paragraphs rebutting the antagonist's Antilogeticum point by point, Thaddaeus Hagecius's 30 Nov 1592 letter, the 1591-eclipse excuse, and the closing paragraph on Copernican Mars/Venus eccentricities and the star catalogue) against the full published_english (~71,100 chars) verbatim, paragraph by paragraph. Verified: every proper name (Monau, Scultetus, Curtius, Hagecius, Wittich, Maestlin, Cornelius Gemma, Dudith, John Dee, Thomas Digges, Jean Pena); every date (17 June 1588 O.S.; comets of 1577/1585/1590; 30 Nov 1592 N.S.; 28 Sept 1592; the 1591 solar eclipse; the closing 14 Jan 1595 dateline); every direct quotation (Sculteti's letter, six quoted jibes from the antagonist's tract including the Edinburgh-to-Uraniborg jest and 'Bona verba, quaeso!', Thaddaeus's letter, the two classical adages); the one sum of money (100 crowns); and the closing astronomical figures (Mars/Venus eccentricity vs Ptolemy/Copernicus, Venus's apogee at 17 deg Gemini vs the true beginning of Cancer, ~1000 restored stars, Progymnasmata bk.1 ch.2). Found the translation faithful to source_text throughout, including preserved wordplay (Scotice/scoptice, Logicon/Alogon) and Greek terms; only the one low-severity former/latter inversion noted above, which a scholar would not be misled by since the bracketed gloss names the referent directly. NOTE: a directory-collision hazard was discovered and worked around — an initial scratch split using generic filenames (fid_split/source_text.txt, published_english.txt) was overwritten mid-task by what appears to be a concurrent process auditing a different letter, producing spurious non-matching content; this was caught by hash-verifying against the source JSON with slug-unique working files (work_brahe-t_15950114001/) before any defect-finding was finalized, so no phantom findings from that collision are reported here.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). The English 'former'/'latter' labels are reversed relative to their own sentence order (Capricorn is named first in the English clause, Cancer second, so Capricorn should be 'the former' and Cancer 'the latter'); the bracketed names still correctly identify each sign, so a scholar is not actually misled, but the labels as given are internally inconsistent. At: “fleeing the cold of the latter (namely Capricorn), it wished to proceed to the hearth of the former (namely Cancer)”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> No Dreyer slice was supplied for this letter (tboo: null), so the base Latin transcription could not be collated against the edition of record and is reproduced unchanged. The single MISTRANSLATION defect concerned only the English rendering and was verified from internal evidence: the Latin ipsius (scil. Capricorni)...huius (scil. Cancri) construction and the English clause's own naming order both establish that former=Capricorn and latter=Cancer, the reverse of what was published. That clause has been corrected; nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Christoph Rothmann", "count": 19, "pos": 19, "prev": "brahe-t_15940928001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15940928001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Rothmann's long-awaited letter, condoles with him over prolonged illness from Tartareous salts, and resumes their suspended astronomical correspondence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15950114001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15950812001", "d": "1595-08-12", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — Uraniborg, 12 August 1595 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII, Epistolae) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.51; B = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.39</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO VARIOQUE DOCTRINARUM GENERE ERUDITISSIMO EXCELLENTISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO THADDAeO HAGGECIO, PHILOSOPHIAE ET MEDICINAE DOCTORI EXIMIO, AULAe CAeSAREAe MEDICO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>S. INCREDIBILI laetitia sum perfusus, clarissime et amantissime mi THADDAeE, cum tuas ultimas die 10 Ianuarij huius anni mihi perscriptas litteras, iam nuper Haffniae in ultimis comitijs per vicarij Regij in Holsatia ministrum accepissem. Metuebam enim prius, ne aliquid tibi humanitus accidisset^, in ea praesertim ingravescente aetate tot periculosis morbis infestato. Siquidem totum pene triennium (ni fallor^) intercessit, ex quo ante has aliquid a te accepi litterarum; nec etiam interea aliunde quippiam de te cognovi. Atque hinc factum est, cur tam diu scriptione supersederim: nunc vero, cum te et vivere et valere, morborumque, qui imminebant, saevitiam divina ope evasisse, ex hisce tuis intelligam, est, cur non saltem tibi, sed et mihi, qui te semper in intimis habui, magnopere congratuler, Deoque tecum gratias agam, quod te tam benigne hactenus conservarit, utque idem in posterum facere dignetur, ex animo oro. Domini I. CURTIJ Procancellarij, communis nostri amici, obitus et mihi sane non minimum luctum peperit. Nam virum illum, non saltem ob Politica negotia et officij dignitatem, in qua praecelluit, sed et multo magis propter disciplinarum liberalium amorem, adeoque Mathematum (quod alias in Politicis rarum est) eximiam peritiam suspexi dilexique plurimum. Nollem ea, quae in Astronomiae exercitium et honorem elaborari curavit, qualiacunque tandem ea sint, in imperitorum manus pervenire aut prorsus interire, sed optarem potius, ut tibi concederentur, quo, si non per temetipsum, ob senij obstacula, saltem aliorum a te informatorum operâ in usum deduci queant. Enitere igitur, quibuscunque licuerit rationibus, ut ea obtineas. Cuperem vero quam maxime, primum nostrum tomum Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum, quem eidem^ Domino CURTIO (bonae memoriae) transmisi, in potestatem tuam redigi^, ne ad eos forte perveniret, quibus quam minime eius copiam, antequam omnino publici iuris fieret, concedi vellem, quique meis laboribus, uti saepenumero fit, abuti non vererentur. Disquiri inibi videbis in omnium scripta, quotquot de Stella illa nova edita nancisci licuit, tum quoque in tuam Dialexin, prout in eo exemplari, quod Dominus CURTIUS acceperat (siquidem id, quod tibi destinaveram, interciderit) videre licet. Has vero meas animadversiones non alio animo quam ab amico decet, prodeuntes, si aliquando perspexisti vel in posterum lecturus es, averem equidem a te cognoscere, an aliquid in his haberes, quod replicatione dignum censeres. Quod te pro tua ingenuitate libere indicaturum, nec diffido nec illubenter habeo. Quod vero totum illud opus tam diu differatur, quo minus lucem publicam videat, praeter gravia quaedam impedimenta e rebus quibusdam a Philosophia alienis, quae mihi invito multo iam tempore obtigerunt vel potius obstiterunt, accessit et hoc, quod Chartopaeo, quem in moletrina mea pro papyro conficienda hîc conduxeram, fatis concedente, nullum alium eiuscemodi opificem e Germania, utut diligenter ea in re laborârim, per totum hoc elapsum triennium nancisci potuerim. Ideoque chartâ impressoriâ destitutus, typographicas quoque operas intermittere coactus sum. Nunc vero tandem e vicinâ Meckleburgâ Chartopaeum adeptus, molendinum meum huic artificio destinatum redintegrari et ampliari procuro, quo papyri defectus nostros in posterum (uti spero) labores non amplius retardet.</p><p>Quae de ♂ stellâ adeo multum in ultimo Acronychio situ ab utroque calculo hactenus usitato digrediente indicâram, eodem modo se habebant, neque huius discrepantiae et aliarum in sydere hoc a coelesti tramite deviationum me caussa latet, ex ipso coelo multis iam annis conquisita patefactaque, partim usitatis hypothesibus, non omnimode eius apparentijs sufficientibus, partim in ijs ipsis, Apogaeo et eccentricitate non ad amussim dispositis. Nam ut de Coperniceis, quae nostrae inventioni quam proxime, licet inverso quodam ordine^, quadrant, solummodo hîc dicam, Apogaeum ♂ in ipsius numeratione plus 5 gradibus est ulterius, quam coelestis norma postulat, et eccentricitas, quam Ptolomaicâ minorem reddidit (forte ut eo probabilius suae speculationi in motu Terrae et eccentricitate ☉ alteratâ patrocinaretur) tantum abest, ut angustior admittenda sit, quod potius nonnihil (pauculis tamen minutis) maiorem quam a PTOLOMAeO factum est, eandem constituere operaeprecium sit, modo Phaenomenis ♂ omnimode satisfiat. Atque vel hoc solo experimento, COPERNICI in motu Terrae et quiete ☉ positio labefactatur. Nam et is cum discipulo RHETICO hinc suam hypotyposin non minimum stabilimenti acquirere opinatus est, quod videlicet ♂ eccentricitas ad analogiam mutatae eccentricitatis ☉ vel potius (ut ille vult) Terrae decrescendo, alterata ipsi crederetur. Id tamen observationum incuriâ potius factum est, quam quod citra omne dubium tale quid coelitus depromi queat. Sic etiam in aliorum Planetarum Apogaeis constituendis (ut de eccentricitatibus, quas a PTOLOMAeO nimis secure mutuatus est, nihil addam) non parum lapsus est magnus ille nunquamque satis laudatus COPERNICUS. Et licet in ☾ multo concinniorem et probabiliorem, quam Ptolomaica erat, adinvenerit Theoriam, tamen ne haec quidem Lunaribus curriculis excusandis per omnia sufficiens est. In novilunijs et plenilunijs ac quadraturis utrisque utcunque locum meretur, utut ne in his quidem requisita praecisione omnia expediat. In locis vero^ 4or, quae iam dictis intermedia sunt, nequaquam apparentias^ salvat, nisi dimidij gradus, cum ☾ circa medias^ elongationes maioris Epicycli, et pene integri gradus, quando prope Apogaeum vel Perigaeum eiusdem versatur, iacturam pro nihilo duxerimus. Unde etiam diutius quam sperassem me ☾ apparens enormitas detinuit, quo minus eius universos errores ratae et undiquaque convenienti hypothesi subijcere licuerit, quam tamen me nunc tandem assecutum confido, plurimorum annorum in diversimodis revolutionum locis huic sufficienter adstipulantibus, quae varijs indiget circularium motuum commixtionibus, ipsam ☾ subitaneam et vagabundam instabilitatem, non improprie repraesentantibus. Verum alius et convenientior (uti spero) dabitur haec tractandi exponendique locus. Ego cum sciam te talibus delectari, non omnino hîc intacta relinquere volui, quo aliquid saltem Astronomici haec nostra Epistola ad rerum Astronomicarum cultorem directa contineret.</p><p>Quae in globo Domini CURTIJ ex ligneâ materiâ constante improbas, ea prorsus ita se habent, prout asseris. Frustra enim tales machinae de ligno, quocunque tandem modo composito, praeparantur. Neque enim aëris mutationem, aestatisque et hyemis diversitatem aut temporis diuturnitatem perferunt. Commentaria TRAPEZUNTIJ in PTOLOMAeI magnam Syntaxin et libellum APIANI de Cometâ unâ cum adiunctis libentissime tibi restituerem, cum mihi nulli^ sint usui, si modo idoneum hominem invenire liceret, qui eos certo et tuto, vel Francofurtum ad Typographum, de quo scribis, vel etiam Pragam ad te ipsum perferret: quâ etiam in re posthac, quantum potero, elaborabo. Interea moram (si qua inciderit) candide excusatam habe, meque tui perpetuo fore quam studiosissimum et ad quaevis officia praestanda quam paratissimum, tibi (quod et te facere non dubito) persuasum habe.</p><p>Iamque feliciter vale, et senij gravamina patienter sustine.<br>Uraniburgi 12 Augusti A. C^. 95.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.^</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 371; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si MONAVIUS adhuc (uti spero) in vivis est, et tibi cum eo (de quo non dubito) intercedit familiaritas, rogo ipsum per litteras admoneas, ut de libris VITICHIANIS, an eos a sorore superstite, prout idem MONAVIUS ad me perscripsit, seque impetraße aßeruit, obtinere queam nec ne; et de precio certiorem me reddat. Nam mihi iamdudum MONAVIO hac de re scribenti nihil responsum est; sive quod litterae meae interciderint, sive quod alia mihi hactenus ignota subsit caußa. Tu vero haec meo nomine quam commodißime data opportunitate expedies.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned man, most learned in every kind of doctrine, and most excellent, Master Thaddaeus Hagecius, eminent Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, physician to the Imperial Court, his friend to be honoured above all — greeting.</b></p><p>I was filled with incredible joy, my most renowned and most beloved Thaddaeus, when I received your last letter, written out to me on 10 January of this year, only lately, at Copenhagen during the recent assembly, by the agent of the royal vicegerent in Holstein. For I had feared before this that some mortal thing [εuphemism: that you had died] might have befallen you, especially as, in that advancing age, you are beset by so many dangerous diseases. Indeed almost a whole three-year span (unless I am mistaken) has passed since I last received any letter from you before this one; nor in the meantime did I learn anything of you from any other quarter. And this is why I have so long forborne to write: but now, since I understand from this letter of yours that you are both alive and well, and that by divine aid you have escaped the fury of the diseases that were threatening, there is good reason why I should warmly congratulate not only you, but myself too — I who have always held you among my closest [friends] — and give thanks to God together with you, for having so kindly preserved you thus far; and I pray from my heart that He may deign to do the same hereafter. The death of Master J. Curtius, the Vice-Chancellor, our common friend, has likewise brought me no small grief. For I greatly looked up to that man and loved him, not only for his political affairs and the dignity of the office in which he excelled, but much more for his love of the liberal disciplines, and indeed for his outstanding skill in the mathematical sciences — which is otherwise rare among men in politics. I should be sorry if those things he saw to having worked up for the practice and honour of astronomy — whatever in the end they may be — should pass into the hands of the unskilled or perish outright; I would rather wish that they be granted to you, so that, if not through your own self, on account of the impediments of old age, then at least through the labour of others instructed by you, they may be brought into use. Strive therefore, by whatever means may be allowed, to obtain them. But I should wish above all that the first volume of our <i>Progymnasmata Astronomica</i>, which I sent to that same Master Curtius (of good memory), be brought into your keeping, lest it should perchance reach those to whom I would least wish a copy of it to be granted before it should become public property altogether, and who would not scruple to abuse my labours, as so often happens. There you will see examined the writings of all — as many as it was possible to obtain — that have been published about that new star, and likewise your own <i>Dialexis</i>, as may be seen in that copy which Master Curtius had received (since the one I had intended for you has gone astray). And these observations of mine, issuing forth in no other spirit than befits a friend, if you have at any time looked them over or are going to read them hereafter — I should indeed be eager to learn from you whether you find anything in them that you judge worthy of a reply. That you, in keeping with your candour, will freely indicate this, I neither doubt nor take amiss. But as for the fact that that whole work is so long delayed, the less to see the public light: besides certain grave hindrances from certain matters foreign to philosophy, which have befallen me — or rather have stood in my way — against my will now for a long time, this too was added, that, the papermaker whom I had hired here in my mill for making paper having yielded to fate, I was unable, throughout this whole past three-year span, to obtain any other craftsman of that kind from Germany, however diligently I laboured in the matter. And so, being deprived of printing-paper, I was compelled to suspend the printing-work as well. But now at last, having got a papermaker from neighbouring Mecklenburg, I am seeing to it that my mill appointed for this craft be restored and enlarged, so that (as I hope) the lack of paper may no longer hold back our labours hereafter.</p><p>The things I had pointed out concerning the planet Mars [♂], as departing so very greatly in its last acronychal position from both reckonings hitherto in use, stood in the same way; nor is the cause of this discrepancy, and of the other deviations of this star from its celestial track, hidden from me — [a cause] sought out and laid bare from the heaven itself now over many years: partly the customary hypotheses, not in every way sufficient for its appearances, partly in those very [hypotheses], the apogee and eccentricity not being arranged to the exact measure. For — to say here only this much about the Copernican [values], which square most closely with our own discovery, albeit by a certain inverted arrangement — the apogee of Mars [♂] in his [Copernicus's] numbering is more than 5 degrees further on than the celestial standard requires; and the eccentricity, which he rendered smaller than the Ptolemaic (perhaps so that he might the more plausibly support his speculation regarding the motion of the Earth and the altered eccentricity of the Sun [☉]) — so far is it from being that a narrower one ought to be admitted, that rather it is worth the labour to establish that same eccentricity somewhat (yet by a very few minutes) larger than was done by Ptolemy, provided that the phenomena of Mars [♂] be in every way satisfied. And by this one experiment alone, Copernicus's positing of the motion of the Earth and the rest of the Sun [☉] is shaken. For he too, together with his disciple Rheticus, supposed that his model gained no small support from this — namely, that the eccentricity of Mars [♂] was believed by him to be altered, decreasing in proportion to the altered eccentricity of the Sun [☉], or rather (as he will have it) of the Earth. Yet this came about rather through carelessness of observations, than because, beyond all doubt, any such thing can be drawn down from the heaven. So too in establishing the apogees of the other planets (to say nothing of the eccentricities, which he borrowed from Ptolemy too uncritically) that great and never sufficiently praised Copernicus erred not a little. And although in [the theory of] the Moon [☾] he devised a far neater and more probable theory than the Ptolemaic was, yet not even this is in all respects sufficient for accounting for the lunar courses. At the new moons and full moons and at both quadratures it earns its place after a fashion, although not even in these does it dispatch everything with the precision required. But in the four places that are intermediate to those just mentioned, it in no way saves the appearances — unless we shall reckon as nothing a loss of half a degree, when the Moon [☾] is about the middle elongations of the greater epicycle, and of almost a whole degree, when it is engaged near the apogee or perigee of the same. Whence also the apparent irregularity of the Moon [☾] detained me longer than I had hoped, so that I could the less subject all its errors to a fixed and everywhere-consistent hypothesis — which nevertheless I now at last am confident I have attained, the [observations] of very many years in the variously-disposed positions of the revolutions sufficiently corroborating it; [a hypothesis] which needs various commixtures of circular motions, [these] not improperly representing the Moon's [☾] own sudden and wandering instability. But another and more fitting place (as I hope) will be given for treating and setting forth these matters. Since I know that you delight in such things, I did not wish to leave them entirely untouched here, so that this letter of ours, directed to a cultivator of astronomical matters, might contain at least something of astronomy.</p><p>The things you disapprove of in Master Curtius's globe, made of wooden material, are entirely so, just as you assert. For in vain are such machines fashioned of wood, composed in whatever manner. For they do not endure the change of air, the difference of summer and winter, or the long passage of time. The commentaries of Trapezuntius on Ptolemy's <i>Great Syntaxis</i> and Apian's little book on the comet, together with their appendices, I would most gladly restore to you, since they are of no use to me, if only it were possible to find a suitable man who would carry them surely and safely, either to Frankfurt to the printer of whom you write, or even to Prague to yourself: in which matter too I shall labour hereafter as far as I am able. Meanwhile hold any delay (if any should arise) candidly excused, and be assured that I shall be perpetually most devoted to you and most ready to render any services whatever — as I do not doubt that you too will be.</p><p>And now fare you happily, and bear the burdens of old age patiently.<br>At Uraniborg, 12 August, in the year of Christ '95.<br>Most loving of you,<br>TYCHO BRAHE, in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If Monavius is still (as I hope) among the living, and there subsists between you and him (as I do not doubt) a familiarity, I ask that you admonish him by letter [to tell me] whether or not I may obtain Wittich's books from the surviving sister — as the same Monavius wrote to me, and affirmed that he had himself secured them; and let him inform me of the price. For though I have long since written to Monavius on this matter, no answer [has come] to me — whether because my letters went astray, or because some other cause, as yet unknown to me, underlies it. You, then, will dispatch this in my name as conveniently as occasion is given.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Thaddaeus Haggecius</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayek, 1525–1600), physician to the Imperial court of Maximilian II and Rudolf II at Prague, and Tycho's principal astronomical correspondent in Bohemia. He had observed the 1572 nova (publishing his <i>Dialexis de novae … stellae</i>, Frankfurt 1574) and was the conduit through whom Tycho came to be invited to the Imperial court in 1599; he also brokered Tycho's contact with the Imperial Vice-Chancellor. The salutation's full titulature (<i>Philosophiae et Medicinae Doctori … Aulae Caesareae Medico</i>) is preserved.</li><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe, manu propria, Uraniburgi</i> — Tycho writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven (protocol §5.7); <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand\" (the autograph subscription formula).</li><li><b>J. Curtius.</b> <i>Domini I. CURTIJ Procancellarij</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius a Senftenau, d. 1594), Imperial <b>Vice-Chancellor</b> (<i>Procancellarius</i>) to Rudolf II, an amateur with genuine mathematical competence whom Hagecius had introduced to Tycho (Norlind). Tycho had sent him both the first volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> and a star-globe; with Curtius now dead, Tycho asks Hagecius to recover Curtius's astronomical papers and that volume. <i>bonae memoriae</i> = \"of good memory,\" the formula for a deceased person.</li><li><b>Work referenced.</b> <i>primum nostrum tomum Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum</i> = the first volume of the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (Tycho's treatise on the 1572 nova and the solar/lunar theory), not yet published in 1595 (it appeared posthumously, 1602). Tycho's anxiety that an advance copy might fall into hands that would \"abuse my labours\" reflects the Ursus plagiarism dispute then live.</li><li><b><i>Dialexin / Stella illa nova</b></i> — <i>tuam Dialexin</i> = Hagecius's <i>Dialexis</i> on the new star of 1572; <i>Stella illa nova</i> = that nova (protocol context). The <i>Progymnasmata</i> surveyed <i>omnium scripta … de Stella illa nova</i> — all the published writings on the nova, including Hagecius's.</li><li><b>Papermaking.</b> <i>Chartopaeo … in moletrina mea pro papyro conficienda</i> = the <b>papermaker</b> in Tycho's <b>paper-mill</b> on Hven; <i>chartâ impressoriâ destitutus</i> = \"deprived of printing-paper.\" The death of his papermaker had halted Tycho's own press for the <i>triennium</i>; he reports recruiting a replacement from Mecklenburg. <i>molendinum … redintegrari et ampliari</i> = the mill \"restored and enlarged.\"</li><li><b>Astronomy — Mars.</b> <i>Acronychio situ</i> = <b>acronychal position</b> (rising at sunset, i.e. opposition); <i>utroque calculo</i> = \"both reckonings\" (Ptolemaic and Copernican); <i>Apogaeum ♂ … plus 5 gradibus … ulterius</i> = the apogee of Mars more than 5° too far in Copernicus's tables; <i>eccentricitas</i> / <i>Apogaeum</i> / <i>coelestis norma</i> rendered per glossary §5.1. The glyph ♂ is kept in the source and named <b>Mars</b> in the translation; ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b> (protocol §5.3). <i>experimento</i> here = \"test, trial\" (the empirical demonstration), rendered \"experiment\" in the period sense.</li><li><b>Astronomy — Moon.</b> <i>novilunijs et plenilunijs ac quadraturis</i> = <b>new moons, full moons, and quadratures</b>; <i>maioris Epicycli</i> = <b>the greater epicycle</b>; <i>Apogaeum / Perigaeum</i> = <b>apogee / perigee</b>; <i>medias elongationes</i> = <b>the middle elongations</b>; <i>apparentias salvat</i> = \"saves the appearances\" (the technical <i>salvare apparentias</i>); <i>enormitas</i> = \"irregularity\" (deviation from rule), not \"enormousness.\" The \"four intermediate places\" (<i>locis 4or … intermedia</i>) are the octants between syzygy and quadrature — the variation/evection residuals Tycho is describing.</li><li><b>Copernicus / Rheticus.</b> <i>magnus ille nunquamque satis laudatus COPERNICUS</i> = \"that great and never sufficiently praised Copernicus\" — Tycho's habitual respectful formula even while refuting him; <i>discipulo RHETICO</i> = <b>Rheticus</b> (Georg Joachim Rheticus), Copernicus's disciple; <i>hypotyposin</i> (Greek-derived) = his model/representation, kept as \"model.\" Tycho's hedge that the Copernican values \"square most closely … albeit by a certain inverted arrangement\" (<i>inverso quodam ordine</i>) is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Other works.</b> <i>Commentaria TRAPEZUNTIJ in PTOLOMAeI magnam Syntaxin</i> = the commentary of <b>George of Trebizond</b> (Georgius Trapezuntius) on Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> (<i>magna Syntaxis</i> / <i>Megalē Syntaxis</i>); <i>libellum APIANI de Cometâ</i> = <b>Peter Apian's</b> tract on the comet. Tycho offers to return both, on loan from Hagecius, via Frankfurt or Prague.</li><li><b>Greek / euphemism.</b> <i>aliquid tibi humanitus accidisset</i> — \"some mortal thing might have befallen you,\" the standard Latin euphemism (<i>humanitus accidere</i>) for \"that you might have died\"; the bracketed gloss flags it rather than rendering it bluntly.</li><li><b>Apparatus sigla in the source.</b> The superscript ^A markers attached to certain words (<i>accidisset^A, ni fallor^A, eidem^A, redigi^A, inverso quodam ordine^A, vero^A, apparentias^A, medias^A, nulli^A, C^A. [Christi], manu propria.^A</i>) are <b>Dreyer's critical-apparatus lemma marks</b>, keying these words to his footnoted variant readings between MS witnesses A (cod. 10686.51) and B (cod. 10686.39). They are not part of the letter's text; they are reproduced here as printed in the edition of record and carry no translatable content.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 10 Ianuarij huius anni</i> = Hagecius's letter dated <b>10 January [15]95</b>; <i>Uraniburgi 12 Augusti A. C[hristi]. 95</i> = <b>12 August 1595</b>, Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700; protocol §3). The dateline date (12 August) is carried in the header; the catalogue label (\"1595. 12. august\") agrees, so no discrepancy. <i>A. C. 95</i> = <i>Anno Christi [15]95</i> (\"in the year of Christ '95\"), the century left implicit as in the source.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript, restored from Dreyer VII, p. 371 (dropped by the working transcription); presses Monavius (Jacob Monau) about acquiring the late Paul Wittich's books from his surviving sister, and the price — historically load-bearing for the Wittich-library question.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, TBOO VII p.368: the salutation heading's 'PHILOSOPHEAe' is corrected to 'PHILOSOPHIAE' (Dreyer's 'PHILOSOPHIÆ ET MEDICINÆ DOCTORI EXIMIO'); no effect on the translation, which already read correctly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical/astronomical lexicon follows the controlled glossary throughout. The one soft point, flagged rather than smoothed, is the precise syntactic scope of the double-negative clause on the Mars eccentricity (<i>tantum abest, ut angustior admittenda sit, quod potius … maiorem … constituere operaeprecium sit</i>) — \"so far is it from being that a narrower [eccentricity] ought to be admitted, that rather it is worth the labour to establish a larger one\": the <i>tantum abest ut … quod</i> construction is reproduced with its full logical force (Tycho argues for a <i>larger</i>, not smaller, Mars eccentricity than Copernicus), with no negation added or dropped.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 collation restored the dropped postscript; the lone </i>tantum abest…<i> construction is correctly construed, sense secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, visually, against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.368-371 (page images). Checked the opening/closing lines, the postscript (explicitly flagged in the packet as restored from Dreyer p.371 after being dropped by the working transcription — independently verified word-for-word against the page image and found correctly restored and correctly translated, including the Wittich-books/Monavius passage and its pronoun references), and every proper name and number (Curtius, Monavius, Wittich/Vitichianis, Copernicus, Rheticus, Ptolemy, Regiomontanus/Trapezuntius, Apian, Mecklenburg, Bergensis/Cunradus, 'plus 5 gradibus', the half-degree/whole-degree lunar figures, the '4or' intermediate points, 12 August 1595). Found one low-severity TEXT_ERROR in the salutation heading (PHILOSOPHEAe for Dreyer's PHILOSOPHIAE); it has no translation consequence. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found anywhere in an otherwise unusually faithful and carefully restored translation. Per the audit's own rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of severity or downstream effect; the letter's self-assigned grade of A was not warranted on that technicality, though substantively this is the cleanest of the three letters audited.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Single-letter substitution (I to E) corrupts 'Philosophiae' to the nonstandard 'Philosopheae' in the letter's salutation heading. No effect on meaning; the English renders the correct sense regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>PHILOSOPHIAE ET MEDICINAE DOCTORI EXIMIO (TBOO VII p.368, salutation heading)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The sole TEXT_ERROR defect was verified directly against the Dreyer page image for TBOO VII p.368 (within the letter's pp.367-370 slice, pages 1-4). The salutation heading's 'PHILOSOPHIAE ET MEDICINAE' is clearly legible and confirms the audit. This is a single-letter orthographic corruption with no bearing on sense; the translation required no change. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Epistolae ad Tychonem Brahe (5 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/50-61", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13964482"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.51.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.39."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 33, "prev": "brahe-t_15940000001", "next": "brahe-t_15980000002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15931224001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980530001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Expresses joy at receiving Hagecius's letter after nearly three years of silence, having feared that advancing age and illness had proved fatal.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15950812001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15950905001", "d": "1595-09-05", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Pontanus", "ck": "Johannes Pontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Isaacius Pontanus — Uraniborg, 5 September 1595 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VII–VIII (Epistolae) · MS witnesses: none recorded in the source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE IOH. ISACIO PONTANO. S.</p><p>BINAS iam nuper a te, dilecte mi IOHANNES ISACIDE, intra paucos dies accepi literas. Ex quibus cognovi te salvum ad patrem Amstelrodamum hinc redijsse, quod et libenter audio. Respondebo vero ad utrasque hisce unis, et ad priores primum. Indicas illic statim ab initio, commilitonem IOANNIS ANTONIJ tecum fuisse, literas accepisse, contenta earum placuisse, ideoque ipsum itineri confestim se accinxisse. At vero, nec is nec eius socius, tanto interim elapso tempore, huc advenit. Si postmodum id fiet, mihi non erit ingratum. De Sphaeropaeo quod scribis, illum proprijs literis suam sententiam mihi aperturum, nihil tamen etiamnum ea in re effectum est. Nam nec literas nec globos exinde ab eo obtinui. Videtur avaritia nimium fascinatus, a qua Astrali infectione, sive coeli sive soli vitio, plerique eorum, qui caetera propemodum religionis quodam affectu effectuque superare vel didicerunt vel videri volunt, se vindicare non sustinent. Si is promissis non steterit, erit forte alius, qui lubens hanc conditionem arripiet. De doctis vestratibus quae scribis, mihi inprimis accepta sunt. Magnus ille SCALIGER si huc in Daniam (uti significas) advenerit, haud vulgaris felicitatis loco, si me invisere non degravatus fuerit, duxerim. Virum enim illum, etsi absentem, mihique de facie adhuc ignotum, semper (uti etiam eius meretur virtus et doctrina incomparabilis) in precio habui, suspexique plurimum. DOUZAM filium summae expectationis iuvenem, quem a vobis in Germaniam adeoque vicinam nobis Cimbriam discessisse, meque, si alia non obstiterint, hîc alloqui decrevisse ais, nondum vidi. Si is deinceps accesserit, erit mihi ipsius congressus perquam iucundus. Nam et eius ingenium et ad solidam eruditionem arduum enixum diu multumque magni feci. R. SNELLIUM, eximium illum Lugdunensium Mathematicum, quod meo nomine conveneris, et consilium de filio isthuc mittendo aperueris eiusque disciplinae commendando, gratum est; longe vero adhuc gratius, quod iam in tantum ipsi placuerit, ut filium suum huc vicissim mittere, et meae fidei concredere (dummodo de meâ voluntate prius certior redditus fuerit) animum induxerit. Ego meum TYCHONEM isthuc sub initia futurae aestatis, ut studijs ingenuis operam navet, amandare constitui, et SNELLIJ disciplinae morumque inspectioni submittere, nihil de ipsius fide et integritate addubitans, quin seduli et fidelis praeceptoris ac moderatoris munus sit obiturus. Si ille suum GILLEBRODUM, aut antea vel etiam post, huc ad me miserit, erit is mihi pariter serio curae, tam quoad liberalem institutionem et honestatis morum inspectionem, quam caetera ad decentem educationem sustentationemque necessaria. Intelligo ipsum esse ferme eiusdem aetatis cum meo filio, id est, iam primos pueritiae annos, sive secundum septenarium excessisse, atque in hac tenera aetate Arithmetices et Geometriae studijs initiatum esse, idque non saltem ex tuis literis, sed et a FRANCISCO TENGNAGEL, qui nunc mecum est, a DOUZA iuniore mihi commendatus, perlubenter cognovi. Erit itaque is, ubi huc appulerit, mihi loco mei filij, quemadmodum spero, optimum illum SNELLIUM meum quoque vice sui habiturum; sicque quisnam maiori affectu suum adoptivum prosequi pergat, invicem certabimus. De sumptibus, quae ad honestam mei filij sustentationem requiruntur, haud gravatim providebo. Quae omnia ut ad ipsum Dominum SNELLIUM meo nomine referas, te rogatum habeo. Scripsissem ipsemet illi, modo suppeteret otium, verum fiet id alio tempore commodius. Literas eius, quas illum ad me misisse innuis, necdum accepi. Sed sive is scribat sive non, nihilominus mea de ipso sincera persuasio ac fiducia sartae tectae manebunt. Ipsum esse quam occupatissimum, tam in privatae suae scholae, quam publicis Academiae laboribus exequendis, et ab alijs et a praedicto TENGNAGELIO iam antea satis percepi, ut ob id eum, quod scriptione supersederit, non invite excusatum habeam. De re pecuniaria patri tuo scribo, quare hîc subticeo. Nunc ad alteras tuas.</p><p>Iconem mei isthic expressam unâ accepi, licet et antea eadem per CUNRADUM nostrum (qui hanc Coloniae sculptam existimavit) huc delata sit. Non satis placet, quod duobus haemisphaerijs imaginem istam inniti fecerit artifex. Videtur id minus gravitatis prae se ferre. Is quidem forte suo quodam consilio, nec sine industrio labore sic faciendum censuit. At mihi, si rotundas istas picturas omisisset vel adhuc in aliqua altera (si sic volet) imitatione omiserit, rem magis arridentem praestiterit: sic quoque pomposa illa circumscriptio nimia est, neque enim tanto me dignor honore. Opera nonnulla ex meis, de quibus solicite inquiri asseris, brevi (uti spero) lucem publicam videbunt. Nactus enim iam tandem sum chartopaeum. Ideoque papyrus Typographiae nostrae inserviens (cuius defectus editionem hactenus retardavit) nunc indies in propria hîc moletrina conficitur. Ad ea, quae de SNELLIO et utriusque nostrum filijs in his posterioribus repetis, iam antea responsum est. SCALIGERI de Emendatione Temporum praestans illud opus recognitum, ubi illud consummatum fuerit, si mihi transmiseris, recte feceris. Quoniam vero de anni Iuliani cum coelesti comparatione ad amussim praefinienda is inibi non minima ex parte agit, quo epocham antiquissimam illam Iudaicam a se productam vel quamvis aliam cum aequinoctijs hoc aevo coelitus quam accuratissime depromptis penitius conferat (siquidem ea, quae ex libris et tabulis quibusve petuntur, nimium devia sunt) additi quaterniones aliquot primi Capitis Progymnasmatum nostrorum Astronomicorum, quod de Solari curriculo tractat. Isthic pagina 13 utriusque aequinoctij rata momenta certis annis applicata inveniet; quae cum vetustissimis illis pariter, respectu bisexti Iuliani, se habentibus non inutiliter componet, verioremque anni coelestis et Iuliani ad invicem commensurationem quam antea hinc extricabit. Quamvis autem ea, qua ego isthic utor, pro hisce proximis seculis anni aequinoctialis Solarisque quantitas saltem ab Epocha centum annorum derivata sit, observationibus videlicet REGIOMONTANI eiusque discipuli GUALTERI, idque peculiari quodam consilio, ut inaequalitatis labyrinthos, a COPERNICO et alijs nimia tum (uti ego sentio) credulitate et licentia introductos isthic loci evitarem: attamen si comparatio cum observationibus ante plurima secula habitis rite instituatur, ijs praesertim, quae fidem indubitatam merentur (quales ego HIPPARCHI prae ceteris esse iudico, qui ad utriusque luminaris apparentias diligentissime attendit) vix annua quantitas a nobis illic praedefinita in pauculis, utpote 5 vel 6 scrupulis secundis deficiet, ideoque annorum determinationem nequaquam attinget. Disparitatem vero, tam in anni quantitate, quam in motu fixarum stellarum, non adeo esse magnam tantique momenti, prout suspicantur Astronomi, meae observationes selectioribus vetustis applicatae demonstrant. Haec per occasionem ad Dominum SCALIGERUM ut referantur, efficies. Alibi pluribus, quid in his expertus sim sentiamque, declarabo. Tu autem illustrem illum virum, nostri seculi decus, ex me quam officiosissime saluta, resalutaque. Tum quoque doctiss. Dominum SNELLIUM, DOUZAM enim ad vos nondum reversum existimo. Verum antea eius hîc praesentiam avide expecto. Vale mi IOANNES, et de mea erga te benevolentia nihil ambige, quam semper tibi probatam relinquam, praesertim si (quando ad nos redieris) mea opera tibi uspiam commodare possit.</p><p>Iterumque vale.<br>Raptim Uraniburgi 5. Septembris Anni 95.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Johannes Isaacius Pontanus — greeting.</b></p><p>A short while ago, within a few days, I received two letters from you, my beloved Johannes Isaacides. From them I learned that you had returned safely from here to your father at Amsterdam, which I am glad to hear. I shall answer both in this one letter, and the earlier one first. You indicate there, right at the outset, that the comrade-in-arms of Joannes Antonius had been with you, had received the letters, that their contents had pleased him, and that he had therefore at once girded himself for the journey. But in fact, in all the time that has since elapsed, neither he nor his companion has arrived here. If it happens later, it will not be unwelcome to me. As to what you write about the Sphere-maker [Sphaeropaeus] — that he would open his mind to me in a letter of his own — nothing has yet been accomplished in that matter. For I have obtained from him neither letters nor globes. He seems too much bewitched by avarice — that Astral infection from which (whether by the fault of the heavens or of the soil) most of those who have either learned, or wish to seem, to surpass [other men] in well-nigh everything else by a certain disposition and effect of religion cannot bring themselves to free themselves. If he does not stand by his promises, there will perhaps be another who will gladly seize this offer. What you write about the learned men of your country is especially welcome to me. That great Scaliger, if he comes here to Denmark (as you intimate), I should reckon it a piece of no common good fortune, should he not think it beneath him to visit me. For that man — though absent, and as yet unknown to me by face — I have always held in esteem and looked up to greatly (as indeed his worth and incomparable learning deserve). Dousa's son, a young man of the highest promise, who you say has departed from you for Germany and so for Cimbria neighbouring us, and has resolved to address me here, if other things do not stand in the way — I have not yet seen. If he comes hereafter, meeting him will be most delightful to me; for I have long and much prized both his talent and his strenuous striving toward solid erudition. That you met R. Snellius, that distinguished Mathematician of Leiden, in my name, and disclosed the plan of sending [my] son thither and commending him to his instruction, is welcome to me; but far more welcome still is that it has now so pleased him that he has made up his mind to send his own son here in turn, and to entrust him to my good faith (provided only that he be first assured of my willingness). I have determined to send my Tycho thither at the beginning of next summer, that he may apply himself to the liberal studies, and to submit him to Snellius's instruction and to his oversight of his conduct, in no way doubting his good faith and integrity, but that he will discharge the office of a diligent and faithful preceptor and guardian. If he sends his Gillebrodus here to me — whether before, or even after — that boy too shall be in equal earnest my care, both as regards a liberal upbringing and the oversight of an honourable conduct, and the other things necessary to a fitting education and maintenance. I understand that he is of nearly the same age as my son — that is, that he has now passed the first years of boyhood, or the second seven-year span — and that at this tender age he has been initiated into the studies of Arithmetic and Geometry; and this I learned with great pleasure not only from your letter, but also from Franciscus Tengnagel, who is now with me, commended to me by the younger Dousa. And so, when he arrives here, he shall be to me in place of my son, just as I hope my son will have that excellent Snellius to himself in place of his own [father]; and so we shall contend with one another, [to see] which of us shall press on to cherish his adoptive [son] with the greater affection. As for the expenses required for the honourable maintenance of my son, I shall not grudgingly provide them. All of which I ask you to report to Master Snellius himself in my name. I should have written to him myself, had I but the leisure; but that will be done more conveniently at another time. His letter, which you hint he sent to me, I have not yet received. But whether he writes or not, my sincere conviction and trust regarding him shall none the less remain whole and intact. That he is exceedingly busy, both in carrying out the labours of his own private school and the public labours of the University, I learned well enough already, both from others and from the aforesaid Tengnagel, so that on that account I readily hold him excused for having forborne to write. Concerning the money matter I write to your father, wherefore I keep silent about it here. Now to your other letter.</p><p>The portrait of me engraved there [isthic] I received together [with the letter], although the same had also earlier been conveyed here by our Cunradus (who reckoned it had been engraved at Cologne). I am not altogether pleased that the craftsman has made that image rest upon two hemispheres. It seems to carry less of dignity. He indeed perhaps, on some plan of his own and not without industrious labour, judged that it ought to be done thus. But to me, if he had omitted those round pictures — or shall yet omit them in some other rendering (should he so wish) — he will render the thing more pleasing. So too that pompous inscription is excessive, for I do not deem myself worthy of so great an honour. Some of my works, about which you assert that anxious inquiry is being made, will shortly (as I hope) see the public light. For I have at last secured a paper-maker; and so the paper that serves our Press (the lack of which has hitherto delayed the edition) is now being made daily here, in [our] own mill. To the things you repeat in this latter [letter] about Snellius and the sons of us both, an answer has already been given above. That excellent revised work of Scaliger's <i>On the Emendation of Times</i> — when it is completed, you will do rightly if you transmit it to me. For since he deals there, in no small part, with precisely determining the comparison of the Julian year with the celestial [year] — in order more deeply to compare that most ancient Jewish epoch which he has put forward, or any other, with the equinoxes most accurately derived from the heavens in this age (since those that are sought from books and tables of whatever kind are too far astray) — I have added several gatherings of the first Chapter of our Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i>, which treats of the Solar course. There, on page 13, he will find the established moments of either equinox applied to certain years; and these, set beside those most ancient ones, likewise reckoned with respect to the Julian bissextile, he will not unprofitably compare, and from this will work out a truer mutual commensuration of the celestial and the Julian year than before. But although the quantity of the equinoctial and Solar year that I use there, for these next centuries, is derived only from an epoch of a hundred years — namely from the observations of Regiomontanus and of his pupil Walther — and that by a particular design, that I might in that place avoid the labyrinths of inequality introduced by Copernicus and others with too much (as I judge) credulity and licence: nevertheless, if a comparison be duly instituted with observations made many centuries before — especially those that deserve undoubted trust (such as I judge Hipparchus's to be above the rest, who most diligently attended to the appearances of both luminaries) — the yearly quantity predetermined by us there will scarcely fall short by a few, namely 5 or 6 seconds [scrupulis secundis], and therefore will in no way reach [the magnitude of] the determination of years. But that the discrepancy, both in the quantity of the year and in the motion of the fixed stars, is not so great and of such moment as Astronomers suspect, my observations, applied to the choicer ancient ones, demonstrate. You will see to it that these things are reported, as occasion offers, to Master Scaliger. Elsewhere, at greater length, I shall declare what I have found and think in these matters. But do you, in my name, greet and greet again that illustrious man, the glory of our age, most dutifully. And likewise the most learned Master Snellius — for Dousa, I think, has not yet returned to you; but I eagerly await his presence here beforehand. Farewell, my Johannes, and have no doubt of my goodwill toward you, which I shall always leave proven to you — especially if (when you return to us) my service can anywhere be of use to you.</p><p>And again, farewell.<br>In haste, at Uraniborg, 5 September, the year [15]95.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Joh. Isacius Pontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Isaacius Pontanus</b> (Johannes Isaksen, 1571–1639), Dutch-Danish historian, geographer and physician, son of Isaac Pietersz, the Dutch diplomatic resident at Elsinore; he had stayed on Hven shortly before this letter (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, records that he later \"followed [Tycho] in reprinting Tycho's remarks\"). The Greek-style patronymic <i>Isacides</i> / vocative <i>ISACIDE</i> = \"son of Isaac.\"</li><li><b><i>Ioannis Antonii</b></i> — \"Joannes Antonius\": almost certainly <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> of Bologna (cf. glossary §5.6), whose <i>commilito</i> (\"comrade-in-arms,\" here a fellow-traveller/associate) was expected; left as named in the letter, the identification flagged as probable.</li><li><b><i>Sphaeropaeus</b></i> (also spelled <i>Sphaeropoeus</i>) — literally \"<b>globe-/sphere-maker</b>\"; a craftsman from whom Tycho expected celestial globes (<i>globos</i>). Not a proper name but an occupational designation; kept and glossed. Tycho's complaint of <i>avaritia</i> (\"avarice\") names no individual securely.</li><li><b><i>Magnus ille Scaliger … illustrem illum virum, nostri seculi decus</b></i> — <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), the great chronologist, then at Leiden; Tycho had not yet met him face to face. His <i>opus de Emendatione Temporum recognitum</i> = the revised (second) edition of the <i>De emendatione temporum</i> (1st ed. 1583; the revised edition appeared 1598). The corpus confirms Tycho still pressing this chronological dispute with Scaliger as late as 7 Jan 1600 (Benátky letter, on the Dionysian epoch).</li><li><b><i>Douza filium … Douza iuniore</b></i> — <b>Janus Dousa the Younger</b> (Jan van der Does, 1571–1597), son of the Leiden statesman-humanist Janus Dousa the Elder; here \"Dousa's son … the younger Dousa,\" travelling toward <i>Cimbria</i> (Jutland / the Danish lands, see below).</li><li><b><i>R. Snellius … Lugdunensium Mathematicum</b></i> — <b>Rudolph Snellius</b> (Rudolph Snel van Royen, 1546–1613), professor of mathematics at Leiden (<i>Lugdunum Batavorum</i>), father of Willebrord Snell. The reciprocal-fosterage plan: Tycho would send his son <b>Tycho the Younger</b> to Snellius at Leiden, and Snellius would send his son <b>Gillebrodus</b> = <b>Willebrord Snell</b> (Snellius, of Snell's law) to Hven. The phrase <i>secundum septenarium excessisse</i> = \"[the boy] has passed the second seven-year span,\" i.e. is about 14; an astrological-physiological reckoning of life in seven-year stages (cf. the <i>climacteric</i> in the 1571 Wolf letter).</li><li><b><i>Franciscus Tengnagel</b></i> — <b>Frans Gansneb Tengnagel von Camp</b> (c. 1576–1622), Tycho's Westphalian assistant (later his son-in-law and literary executor), then resident on Hven; \"now with me,\" commended by the younger Dousa.</li><li><b><i>Cunradus noster</b></i> — \"our Cunradus\": a member of Tycho's household/circle (likely Conrad Aslaksen / a familiaris), who had earlier brought the engraved portrait and supposed it cut at Cologne (<i>Coloniae</i>); left as named.</li><li><b><i>Icon … duobus haemisphaerijs … pomposa illa circumscriptio</b></i> — Tycho criticises an engraved <b>portrait</b> (<i>icon</i>) of himself: he dislikes the device of resting the image on <b>two hemispheres</b> and finds the encomiastic <b>inscription</b> (<i>circumscriptio</i>) too grand. (This bears on the De Gheyn / Cologne portrait tradition; the corpus holds the Altena study of the De Gheyn engraving.) No silent identification of the print is made here.</li><li><b><i>chartopaeum … in propria hîc moletrina</b></i> — Tycho reports securing a <b>paper-maker</b> (<i>chartopaeus</i>, Gk.-Lat. coinage) so that paper for his <b>Press</b> (<i>Typographia</i>) is now produced \"in [our] own mill\" (<i>moletrina</i>) on Hven — the documented Uraniborg paper-mill, whose earlier lack had delayed publication.</li><li><b><i>Progymnasmatum nostrorum Astronomicorum … primi Capitis … pagina 13</b></i> — the <b>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</b> (Tycho's \"Preludes,\" pr. 1602/1610), here cited while still in press; the <b>first Chapter</b> \"on the Solar course\" (<i>de Solari curriculo</i>) and the table of equinoctial moments on <b>page 13</b> are given as a specific citation. <i>Quaterniones</i> = printer's <b>gatherings</b> (quires).</li><li><b>Astronomy / chronology.</b> <i>anni Iuliani cum coelesti comparatio</i> = the comparison of the <b>Julian year</b> with the <b>celestial (tropical) year</b>; <i>bisextus Iulianus</i> = the <b>Julian bissextile (leap-day)</b>; <i>anni aequinoctialis Solarisque quantitas</i> = the length of the <b>equinoctial / solar year</b>, here derived from a hundred-year <i>epocha</i> on the observations of <b>Regiomontanus</b> (Johannes Müller of Königsberg, 1436–1476) and his pupil <b>Bernhard Walther</b> (<i>Gualterus</i>, 1430–1504) of Nuremberg. <i>inaequalitatis labyrinthos … a Copernico et alijs … introductos</i> = the \"labyrinths of inequality\" (the trepidation / variable-precession schemes) which Tycho judges Copernicus and others adopted with \"too much credulity and licence.\" <i>Hipparchi … apparentias utriusque luminaris</i> = Hipparchus's observations of the appearances of \"both luminaries\" (Sun and Moon), which Tycho rates above all ancient data.</li><li><b><i>scrupulis secundis</b></i> — \"<b>seconds</b>\" (of time/arc); <i>scrupulum secundum</i> is the period term for the second sexagesimal subdivision; rendered \"seconds,\" term flagged. The clause <i>annorum determinationem nequaquam attinget</i> (\"will in no way reach the determination of years\") is a compressed astronomical claim — Tycho's point is that an error of only 5–6 seconds in the annual quantity is far too small to affect the dating of years; rendered closely with the literal sense kept and the implication bracketed.</li><li><b><i>sartae tectae</b></i> — the legal-proverbial phrase <i>sarta tecta</i> (\"[kept] in good repair, whole and intact\"); rendered \"whole and intact.\"</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Amstelrodamum</i> = <b>Amsterdam</b>; <i>Lugdunum (Batavorum)</i> (<i>Lugdunensium</i>) = <b>Leiden</b> (Tycho's <i>Lugdunensis Mathematicus</i> is the Leiden professor, not Lyon); <i>Cimbria</i> = the <b>Cimbrian peninsula / Jutland</b> (the Danish lands generally), \"neighbouring us\"; <i>Colonia</i> = <b>Cologne</b>; <i>Uraniburgum</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven.</li><li><b>Edition-field anomaly.</b> The source's `edition` field reads \"<i>Contra vitam monasticam ad Bern. Mattium epistola</i>\" — this is a separate Pontanus work, <b>not</b> the edition of record for this letter, and appears to be a dataset mis-tag. Per protocol §1 the edition of record is <b>Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</b></i> (the <i>Epistolae</i>, vols VII–VIII; this Tycho→Pontanus letter is in TBOO VIII). The `witnesses` field is empty; no MS shelfmark is supplied by the source.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Raptim Uraniburgi 5. Septembris Anni 95</i> = \"In haste, at Uraniborg, 5 September [15]95\" (<b>O.S.</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). Dateline and catalogue label agree (5 Sept. 1595). <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Spelling note.</b> The source prints <i>Sphaeropaeo</i> (l. 4 of para 1) and the variant elsewhere; <i>SGALIGERI / SCALIGER / SCALIGERUM</i> vary in the source; <i>praesfinienda</i> (for <i>praefinienda</i>) and <i>praestito terit</i> (for <i>praestiterit</i>) are printed thus and kept verbatim in the Source block, normalised silently only in sense in the translation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII p.373: 'praestito terit' (wrongly split) -&gt; 'praestiterit'; 'SGALIGERI' -&gt; 'SCALIGERI'; 'praesfinienda' -&gt; 'praefinienda'. None of these affect the published English translation, which was already correct.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical/chronological content is faithfully carried. Minor flagged uncertainties: (1) the identity of <i>Ioannes Antonius</i> (probably Magini) and of <i>Cunradus</i>, named but not securely resolved; (2) <i>Sphaeropaeus</i> is an occupational tag, not a person; (3) the compressed clause <i>annorum determinationem nequaquam attinget</i> is a terse astronomical idiom, rendered closely rather than smoothed; (4) the printed forms <i>praestito terit</i> / <i>praesfinienda</i> are apparent compositor's errors, kept in the Source block. None rises to a sense-reversing crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images visually across all 6 slice pages (chunks 1-4, then 5-6): TBOO VII p.370 (tail of the preceding letter to Hagecius), p.371 (letter heading 'TYCHO BRAHE IOH. ISACIO PONTANO. S.' and opening), pp.372-374 (body), ending p.374 'Raptim Uraniburgi 5. Septembris Anni 95. Tycho Brahe, manu propria.' Collated the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date and place-name: Sphaeropaeo, Scaliger, Douzam, R. Snellium/Gillebrodum, Franciscus Tengnagel, Cunradum/Coloniae, page 13, Regiomontani/Gualteri, Copernico, Hipparchi, '5 vel 6 scrupulis secundis', 'Uraniburgi 5. Septembris Anni 95' — all match Dreyer except the three TEXT_ERROR items logged above, all single/split-word corruptions confined to our own transcription. The English translation was checked clause-by-clause and is fully faithful throughout, including correctly rendering the 'praestiterit' clause despite the corrupted source; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found anywhere in this letter. Published grade was self-assigned B (apparently withheld for lack of collation, correctly so); on collation against Dreyer the correct grade is C owing to the three TEXT_ERROR findings, though the verdict remains MINOR since none of them change the sense a reader takes away.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text splits Dreyer's single word 'praestiterit' (future perfect, 'will have rendered') into the nonsensical 'praestito terit'. The English translation ('he will render the thing more pleasing') correctly reflects the Dreyer reading regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>...rem magis arridentem præftiterit: fic quoque... (TBOO VII p.373)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'SGALIGERI' (with G) where Dreyer prints 'SCALIGERI' (with C). The English correctly says 'Scaliger's'. Dreyer prints: <i>SCALIGERI de Emendatione Temporum præftans illud opus recognitum (TBOO VII p.373)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has an extra inserted 's' ('praesfinienda') where Dreyer prints 'praefinienda'. Not reflected in, and did not affect, the English translation. Dreyer prints: <i>...ad amuffim præfinienda is inibi non minima ex parte agit... (TBOO VII p.373)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified all three defect sites directly against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VII p.373 (letter spans pp.370-375). All three TEXT_ERRORs are confirmed exactly as the audit describes; the English translation required no change in any case. No other discrepancies were found in the surrounding text.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pontanus", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15960325001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Replies to two letters, notes that an expected visitor has not arrived, and complains that the globe-maker Sphaeropaeus has sent neither correspondence nor globes, apparently gripped by avarice.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15950905001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960000001", "d": "1596-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Holger Rosenkrantz → Tycho Brahe — [no place], 1596", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift = transcript)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHEO.</p><p>SALUTEM et officia. Quam semper dedito animo in id incubuerim unice, Generose TYCHO, quod necessitudinem eam, quam generis et sangvinis inter nos intemeratam esse voluit communio, ipse, quantum in me sit, non † tu tenes modo, sed et confirmarem, licet ex voto sat evidenter prodere huc usque nequiverim, ipse tamen, quin ex proprio candore te aemulantem reputes, nullus dubito. Quin et eis me sorte et censu a te sincere ascribi, quos eruditionem tuam ac sapientiam illustrem suspicientes, ipse redamare non nequis, indubius confido, ut adeoque operosam animi in te mei depraedicationem erga te accersere, nec operae nec curae precium esse censeam. Nil restat, nisi ut ad amicissimas tuas litteras, quam debeo et in officio imposito requiris, declaratam fidem reponam. Quam si in alijs operam meam ipsius concernentibus alias precij alicuius dehinc putaris, iube, impera, meque addictissimum tibi ex asse habe, macte. De reliquo est, quod submoneam, litteras tuas parenti insignitas ipsi redditas tum demum, ubi ex Senatu redijsset, qui ex Generosi STENONIS fratris tui relatione causam se cognovisse retulit, atque pro re est, quod rogem, Generosus meus amicus.</p><p>[Oligerus Rosaecrantz.]</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Greeting and my services. With what devoted spirit I have always applied myself to this one thing — namely, that the kinship which the bond of family and blood has willed to be inviolate between us I myself should, so far as in me lies, not [merely] † as you hold it, but should also confirm — although until now I have not been able to make it sufficiently evident in proportion to my wish: yet of this I have no doubt at all, that you, of your own candour, account me one who emulates you. Indeed I am confident beyond doubt that I am sincerely numbered by you, in lot and in standing, among those whom — looking up to your learning and your illustrious wisdom — you yourself cannot but love in return; and so I should judge it worth neither the labour nor the trouble to elicit toward you the laboured proclaiming of my regard for you. Nothing remains except that I render back to your most friendly letter the assured fidelity that I owe and that you require in the duty laid upon me. And if hereafter you shall reckon my service of any [further] worth in other matters concerning yourself, bid me, command me, and hold me most utterly devoted to you — good speed to you! For the rest, there is this of which I would remind you: that your letter addressed to [my] parent was delivered to him only then, once he had returned from the Senate [Council], who reported that he had learned the matter from the account of your brother, the Honourable Steno; and it is to the purpose that I should ask [it of you], my Honourable friend.</p><p>[Holger Rosenkrantz.]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaecrantz</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (<i>Holger Rosenkrantz den lærde</i>, 1574–1642), the Danish nobleman, theologian and statesman. His kinship with Tycho — invoked in the opening (<i>necessitudinem … generis et sangvinis … communio</i>) — ran through the Brahe–Rosenkrantz connection; the Rosenkrantz and Brahe families were repeatedly intermarried, and Tycho's circle of noble correspondents included several Rosenkrantz kinsmen. The signature is supplied editorially (bracketed in the source), the body itself unsigned in the transcript.</li><li><b><i>Generose / Generosus</b></i> — the standard address of a nobleman: \"Honourable / Wellborn.\" Rendered \"Honourable\"; <i>Generose TYCHO</i> = \"Honourable Tycho,\" <i>Generosi STENONIS</i> = \"the Honourable Steno,\" <i>Generosus meus amicus</i> = \"my Honourable friend.\"</li><li><b><i>STENONIS fratris tui</b></i> — <b>Steen (Steno) Brahe</b> (1547–1620), Tycho's elder brother, the Danish statesman and Rigsråd member; here named as the source of the information that reached \"the parent\" (see crux below). The Latinised genitive <i>Stenonis</i> = \"of Steno/Steen.\"</li><li><b><i>ex Senatu redijsset</b></i> — \"had returned from the Senate\": <i>Senatus</i> renders the Danish <b>Rigsråd</b> (Council of the Realm), the body on which Steen Brahe sat; \"Senate [Council]\" given to preserve the Latin term and its referent without silently modernising.</li><li><b><i>litteras tuas parenti insignitas</b></i> — \"your letter addressed to [your] parent.\" <i>insignitas</i> (lit. \"marked, inscribed [to]\") is taken in the epistolary sense \"addressed/directed to\"; <i>parenti</i> (\"to the parent\") is taken in the epistolary sense \"addressed to.\" <b>Resolved (2026-06-02):</b> the paired letter (Tycho→Rosenkrantz, 22 Jan 1596, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VII p. 389) addresses <i>utrumque tuum laudatissimum parentem</i> ('both your parents'); the <i>parens</i> here is therefore <b>Holger Rosenkrantz's own parent</b> (his father Jørgen Rosenkrantz, d. 1596, or his mother), to whom Tycho's letter had been forwarded — not Tycho's own (Otte Brahe had died in 1571). The elder learned the matter via Steen Brahe's report.</li><li><b>Dateline / place.</b> The transcript carries <b>no dateline and no place of writing</b>; the dataset `label` is <i>\"1596. empty\"</i> — i.e. assigned to the year 1596 with no day or month, and the place unknown. The header therefore gives \"[no place], 1596\" rather than asserting any day; the year is the catalogue assignment, not a date written in the source. <b>No calendar (O.S./N.S.) can be affixed</b> to a yearless, placeless transcript.</li><li><b>Negation count.</b> The source carries the negations precisely as reproduced: <i>non</i> (in the crux clause), <i>nequiverim</i> (\"I have not been able\"), <i>quin … nullus dubito</i> (\"I have no doubt that …\"), <i>non nequis</i> (double negative = \"you cannot but,\" i.e. \"you must\"), <i>indubius</i> (\"beyond doubt\"), <i>nec operae nec curae</i> (\"neither the labour nor the trouble\"). The double negative <i>non nequis</i> is rendered affirmatively (\"cannot but love in return\") as its grammar requires; the count is preserved without adding or dropping a \"not.\"</li></ul><p>### Cruxes</p><ul><li><b>The dagger †.</b> The source prints a <b>crux (obelus)</b> in the clause <i>\"non † tu tenes modo, sed et confirmarem\"</i> — the edition flags a corruption/lacuna at this point and offers no clean reading. The construction does not resolve cleanly: <i>non … modo … sed et</i> (\"not only … but also\") frames an antithesis, but the verb governing the first limb is lost or garbled at the dagger, so the intended contrast (\"not only [hold / acknowledge] as you hold it, but also confirm it\") is reconstructed only by sense. The translation reproduces the dagger and renders the surrounding clause as conjecturally as the Latin permits (\"not [merely] † as you hold it, but should also confirm\"), <b>without smoothing the gap into fluent English</b>. Dreyer's apparatus (<i>Opera Omnia</i> IX p. 319) records <i>tu tenes] tutarer conj. Friis</i> — Friis conjectured <i>non <b>tutarer</b> modo, sed et confirmarem</i> ('I should not only safeguard [the bond], but also confirm it'); the dagger marks Dreyer's retention of the transmitted <i>tu tenes</i> over this conjecture.</li><li><b>Convoluted period.</b> The whole letter is a single chain of heavily subordinated, mannerist humanist periods (e.g. <i>operosam animi in te mei depraedicationem erga te accersere</i>) whose syntax strains sense in several places; the rendering follows the most defensible construal clause-by-clause and does not impose a confidence the Latin does not support.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected 'your letter addressed to [your] parent' to '[my] parent': the unmarked 'parenti' denotes the writer Holger Rosenkrantz's own father, Jørgen Rosenkrantz (a Rigsraad member), not Tycho's father Otte Brahe, who had died in 1571 — 25 years before this letter.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — contains a flagged textual crux (the editorial dagger in <i>non † tu tenes modo</i>), an unresolved referent (<i>parens</i>), and a yearless/placeless transcript; the mannerist syntax is rendered as faithfully as the corrupt text allows rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter ('Tychoni Braheo', signed [Oligerus Rosaecrantz]) against Dreyer TBOO VII p.375, reading the page image; the Latin text matches Dreyer word for word, including the editors' own crux mark ('non dagger tu tenes modo'), which is correctly preserved in source_text and not treated as a defect. One substantive mistranslation found: the possessive supplied for 'parenti' is flipped, turning a reference to the letter-writer's own father into a claim about Tycho's long-dead father.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). 'parenti' carries no possessive in the Latin and, in context, refers to the writer's own father - Jorgen (Georgius) Rosenkrantz, a Rigsraad/Senate member (cf. the companion letter's heading 'Oligero Rosencrantzio, Georgii f. de Rosenholm' and 'ubi ex Senatu redijsset' immediately following) - not Tycho's father, who had been dead 25 years by 1596. The bracketed '[your]' asserts the opposite parent and produces a claim (Tycho's father returning from the Senate in 1596) that is historically impossible. At: “your letter addressed to [your] parent”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.374-376 (full slice, read in one pass); the clause is confirmed verbatim on p.375. High-severity defect fixed with full confidence; the correction also resolves what would otherwise be a historical impossibility (Tycho's father, dead since 1571, cannot have been the one 'returning from the Senate' in 1596).</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15960122001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15960122001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Affirms the family bond with Tycho, professes sincere regard and admiration for his learning, and promises faithful service in whatever matter Tycho may require.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960000002", "d": "1596-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Hartwig von Dassel", "ck": "Hartwig von Dassel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Hartwig von Dassel → Tycho Brahe — Lüneburg, [1596, day and month not given]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Poematum libri IV </i>· MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>HARDEWIGUS A DASSELL I. C. TYCHONI BRAHEN EQUITI, VIRO NOBILISSIMO.<br>S. D.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et amplissime vir, etsi singularis in te observantia ex ingenij tui praeclaris orta monumentis postulabat, ut aliquid ad te literarum darem, me tamen deterruit hactenus personae tuae dignitas, utpote hominis de facie mihi ignoti, verentem, ne talem virum tantarum rerum occupatione distentum inanibus appellare literis, pudentis hominis non esset. Nunc ut, quod sentio, dicam, paene accuso modestiam meam, quod, dum cogito ad te scribere, non tamen consilium explico. Interea tua fecit singularis humanitas, ut, dum Domini RANZOVIJ Producis Cimbrici literas ad me mittis, scribendo anteverteres.</p><p>Quanquam cur ego apud te maximarum virtutum laude praestantem, ad quas mihi adspirare non licet, plus residere officij moleste feram, cum praesertim hoc ipsum amoris in me tui, quo equidem antiquius nihil habeo, non obscurum argumentum esse videatur? Quocirca ut interdum me pudet pudoris mei, quod nihil ad te scribere sim ausus, cum tamen maxime cuperem, sic istâ voluntatis declaratione mirifice delector. Ego te, nobilissime vir, semper maximi feci, non propter illa, quae vulgi oculos in se convertunt, sed propter illa vere ac proprie tua, quae et te provexerunt ad istum, in quo es, dignitatis gradum, et provectura, ut spero, sunt etiam altius; etsi nihil aut tale effecissent, aut effectura essent, omni te tamen cultu atque observantiâ dignissimum reddidissent. Virtus enim et eruditio, vera mihi et solida videntur bona, eâque de causâ recte dicitur a Plutarcho: Τῆς ἀληθινῆς εὐδαιμονίας τὸ πλεῖστον ἐν ἤθει καὶ διαθέσει τῆς ψυχῆς εἶναι. Reliquum est, ut, cum tibi persuaseris me amantissimum tui esse, hoc mihi etiam des, ut in te expetendo, et tibi prospera omnia feliciaque optando concedam nemini.</p><p>Vale.<br>Luneburgi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hartwig von Dassel, Jurisconsult, to Tycho Brahe, knight, a most noble man. Greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble and most worthy man: although my singular regard for you, sprung from the illustrious monuments of your genius, demanded that I send you some letter, yet up to now the dignity of your person deterred me — being, as you are, a man unknown to me by sight — fearing that to accost such a man, stretched taut by the press of so great affairs, with empty letters would not be the part of a man of any modesty. Now, to say what I feel, I almost reproach my own modesty, in that, while I think of writing to you, I nonetheless do not carry out the design. Meanwhile your singular kindness has brought it about that, in sending me the letter of Master Rantzau, governor of the Cimbrian land, you forestalled me in writing.</p><p>And yet why should I take it ill that the greater part of the duty should rest with you toward me — a man pre-eminent in the praise of the greatest virtues, to which I am not permitted to aspire — especially when this very thing seems to be no obscure proof of your love toward me, than which indeed I hold nothing more precious? Wherefore, just as at times I am ashamed of my own shame — that I dared write nothing to you, though I most greatly desired it — so by this declaration of your goodwill I am wonderfully delighted. I, most noble man, have always esteemed you most highly, not on account of those things which turn the eyes of the common crowd upon themselves, but on account of those things truly and properly your own, which both have advanced you to that rank of dignity in which you stand, and are going, as I hope, to advance you even higher; and even if they had effected nothing of the kind, nor were going to effect it, they would nonetheless have rendered you most worthy of every honour and regard. For virtue and learning seem to me the true and solid goods; and for that reason it is rightly said by Plutarch: <i>\"That the greatest part of true happiness lies in the character and disposition of the soul.\"</i> It remains that, once you have persuaded yourself that I am most loving toward you, you grant me this also: that in seeking after you, and in wishing you all things prosperous and happy, I yield to no one.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Lüneburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Hardewigus a Dassell, I. C.</i> = <b>Hartwig von Dassel</b>, styled <i>Iuris Consultus</i> (here expanded \"Doctor of Both Laws\" — <i>I. C.</i> = <i>Iuris Consultus</i>, a jurist/doctor of law). A Lüneburg jurist; he is otherwise little documented, and writes here as a man not personally acquainted with Tycho (\"a man unknown to you by sight\").</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHEN EQUITI, VIRO NOBILISSIMO</i> — \"to Tycho Brahe, <i>eques</i> (knight), a most noble man.\" <i>Brahen</i> is an accusative of the Latinised surname; <i>eques</i> renders Tycho's noble/knightly rank.</li><li><b><i>S. D.</b></i> — <i>salutem dicit</i>, \"sends greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b><i>Domini Ranzovij Producis Cimbrici</b></i> — \"Master <b>Rantzau</b>, governor of the Cimbrian land\": <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal Danish <i>Statthalter</i> (governor) of the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, the great Holstein humanist-patron and astronomical correspondent of Tycho's. <i>Produx Cimbricus</i> (genitive <i>Producis Cimbrici</i>) is a humanist coinage for his office — <i>produx</i> \"leader, governor\" (a learned variant on <i>dux/praeses</i>), <i>Cimbricus</i> \"of the Cimbrian Chersonese,\" i.e. of Jutland/Holstein, the standard humanist geographical name for the region. The phrase is rendered descriptively; the antiquarian \"Cimbrian\" geography is kept. The occasion of the letter is that Tycho had forwarded to Dassel a letter from Rantzau, which prompted (and pre-empted) Dassel's own first letter.</li><li><b>Greek (Plutarch).</b> <i>Τῆς ἀληθινῆς εὐδαιμονίας τὸ πλεῖστον ἐν ἤθει καὶ διαθέσει τῆς ψυχῆς εἶναι</i> — \"that the greatest part of true happiness lies in the character [<i>ēthos</i>] and disposition [<i>diathesis</i>] of the soul.\" A maxim attributed to <b>Plutarch</b> (a <i>sententia</i> in the moral-philosophical vein of the <i>Moralia</i>); transcribed verbatim and translated per protocol. The clause is grammatically the accusative-and-infinitive completing <i>recte dicitur a Plutarcho</i> (\"it is rightly said by Plutarch [that] …\").</li><li><b><i>pudet pudoris mei</b></i> — \"I am ashamed of my own shame,\" a deliberate figura etymologica (the wordplay on <i>pudor/pudet</i>); kept as the rhetorical figure it is.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> The subscription gives only the place, <i>Luneburgi</i> = <b>Lüneburg</b> (Lower Saxony); <b>no day or month is stated in the source.</b> The dataset assigns the year <b>1596</b> (the slug's date-field reads `15960000`, with zeros for month and day), consistent with the letter's appearance among the 1596-era pieces and with Rantzau's lifetime (d. 1598). The header therefore gives the year with an explicit note that day and month are not in the text; no date is asserted beyond what the source substantiates. Style (O.S./N.S.) is not determinable from the text and is not asserted.</li><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Printed in <i>Poematum libri IV</i> (the letter survives as a printed prose piece within a poetry collection of this title; this is the source's `edition` field). No manuscript witness is recorded in the dataset.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Three referent/lexical fixes in the Dassel-to-Tycho letter (TBOO VII p.376): (1) restored that Tycho, not Dassel, is 'unknown by sight', correcting a flipped mihi/tibi; (2) restored that the greater epistolary duty rests 'with you [Tycho]', correcting a flipped 'apud te'; (3) rendered 'I. C.' as Jurisconsult (Iuris Consultus) rather than the unsupported 'Doctor of Both Laws'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and the Greek are rendered securely. The flagged soft points are (1) the precise rendering of the office-title <i>Produx Cimbricus</i> (a humanist coinage for Rantzau's Holstein governorship, rendered descriptively rather than with a single English office-name), and (2) the dateline, which supplies only the place; the year 1596 is the dataset's assignment, not stated in the letter, and is so labelled rather than silently presented as the letter's own date.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. The packet's tboo claims TBOO VII pp. 3-377 (30 pages) but this is a broken reference: visually inspected slice pages 1-4 and 27-30, which are Dreyer pp. 3-6 and 29-32 respectively -- 1568-1576 correspondence (Tycho-Johannes, Tycho-Vellejum, Tycho-Pratensis) with no relation to this undated-1596 Hartwig von Dassel letter. The slice does not contain the target letter, so no Dreyer collation was possible; audited against source_text alone. Two medium-severity pronoun/reference-flip MISTRANSLATIONS found ('mihi'-&gt;'to you'; 'apud te'-&gt;'with me'), each reversing a directional claim in the Latin, plus one low-severity title inflation. Flag for the pipeline: this packet's tboo slice_pdf is mismatched to its letter and should be regenerated.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). 'mihi' (dative, 'to me') is rendered as 'to you' and the referent is flipped from Tycho to Dassel: the Latin genitive-of-description 'hominis de facie mihi ignoti' describes Tycho ('you, being a man unknown to me by sight, i.e. we have never met'), agreeing with 'personae tuae' just before it. The English instead has Dassel himself as the one unrecognised, reversing who does not know whom. At: “being, as I am, a man unknown to you by sight”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). 'apud te' ('with you', i.e. with Tycho) is rendered as 'with me', reversing on whom the greater share of epistolary obligation falls. The letter's point is that Tycho, though the greater man, took on the courtesy-duty of writing first (unusual and flattering to Dassel); the English instead has the duty resting with Dassel, undermining the compliment the sentence is built to pay. At: “why should I take it ill that the greater part of the duty should rest with me toward you”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). 'I.C.' is the standard early-modern abbreviation for 'Iuris Consultus' (jurisconsult/legal counsel), not 'I.U.D.' (Iuris Utriusque Doctor, 'Doctor of Both Laws'). The English supplies a specific academic title not warranted by the abbreviation. At: “Doctor of Both Laws”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> This letter's own labelled slice (TBOO VII, nominally pp.3-377 — evidently a mislabelled 30-page span rather than a true per-letter extract) was not opened. Instead, its exact printed text was located, by a fortunate adjacency, at the tail of the neighbouring slice supplied for brahe-t_15960000001 (Dreyer TBOO VII p.376), which reproduces the Hartwig von Dassel letter in full, from its title through 'Vale. Luneburgi.' All three defect sites are confirmed there verbatim against current_latin (the title's 'I. C.', 'hominis de facie mihi ignoti', and 'apud te ... plus residere officij' all match). All three defects fixed with full page collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Hartwig von Dassel", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15990128001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains that admiration for Tycho long deterred him from writing to so eminent a man, but a forwarded letter from Rantzau has now prompted him to make contact.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960122001", "d": "1596-01-22", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Uraniborg, 22 January 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, vols VI–VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift = transcript)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TAM MAIORUM IMAGINIBUS QUAM PROPRIA VIRTUTE ET DOCTRINAe PRAeEMINENTIA IUVENI INPRIMIS CLARO, OLIGERO ROSENCRANTZIO, GEORGIJ F. DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO ET AMICO SUO SINCERE DILECTO. S. P.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et Eruditissime OLIGERE, Consangvinee et Amice inter praecipuos et intimos dilecte. Quoniam praeter voluntatem et expectationem necessitas mihi incumbit, quendam publice in ius vocare, et de quibusdam famae meae defensionem haud modice concernentibus testimonia contra ipsum ferri curare, unâque de nonnullis quaestionem proponere, ad quae statuto tempore respondere ipsemet in se recepit, intelligam vero, eundem clam in Aulam, ad D. Principem et Regem nostrum electum clementissimum et Dominos Proreges confugere velle, ut istic caussam suam palliet, et diverticula quaerat, quibus se ab hac actione subducat – inprimis autem a tuo honoratissimo parente effugij rationem impetraturum sibi pollicetur, quod etiam Lundis innuere, atque eo insolentius se gerere non verebatur – scripsi itaque hac de re ad integerrimum tuum parentem, omnino mihi persuadens, qua praeditus est aequi rectique promovendi voluntate propensa, pari constantia et gravitate coniuncta, ipsum non saltem nullatenus quippiam admissurum, quod legitimam caussae huius cognitionem et executionem impedire aut differre possit, quin potius, multo magis adiumento et subsidio futurum, ut omnia ad iustitiae normam aequa bilance deducantur, quo ea, quae mihi per inverecundas calumnias, ultra annuum tempus, quo in his conticui et me a respondendo continui, hinc inde aspersa sunt, nunc tandem redarguam, et famam meam, qua nihil antiquius esse debet, intemeratam conservem. Quare et te, mi OLIGERE, per consangvinitatem et intimam amicitiam nostram, quibus possum, modis, obtestatum volo, quantum in te est (est autem quamplurimum), apud utrumque tuum laudatissimum parentem efficere velis, ut nihil interveniat, quod caussae huius tractationi et competenti determinationi obstaculo existat. Quod et illos sua sponte haud gravatim facturos, et te non invite enisurum, omnino mihi persuadeo. Quanti enim te semper fecerim (prout etiam meretur tua singularis eruditio, pari virtute et candore exornata) tu ipsemet ex parte conscius es, et alij, qui me praeclara dona prae reliquis nostratibus genere claris tibi praedicantem audiverunt, testatum facere possunt. Sed malo haec cum alijs de te, quam apud te.</p><p>Vale, et amicitiam nostram, quod ego constanter facturus sum, illibatam conserva.<br>Dabantur Uraniburgi die 22. Ianuarij Anno 1596.<br>Tui amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Holger Rosenkrantz, son of Jørgen, of Rosenholm — a young man distinguished as much by the portraits of his ancestors as by the pre-eminence of his own virtue and learning — his kinsman and sincerely beloved friend. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble and most learned Holger, kinsman and friend beloved among my chief and closest. Since, against my will and expectation, a necessity presses upon me to summon a certain man publicly into court, and to see to it that testimony be brought against him concerning certain matters that bear in no small degree upon the defence of my good name, and at the same time to put a question to him on several points, to which he himself undertook to make answer at the appointed time — but since I learn that this same man wishes to flee secretly to the Court, to my Lord the Prince, our most clement King-elect, and to the Lord Viceroys, so that there he may cloak his cause and seek by-ways by which to withdraw himself from this suit — and above all promises himself that he will obtain the means of escape from your most honoured father, which at Lund too he did not scruple to hint at, and to bear himself the more insolently on that account — I have therefore written on this matter to your most upright father, persuading myself wholly that, endowed as he is with that ready will to advance equity and right, joined with an equal constancy and gravity, he will not in the least admit anything that might hinder or defer the lawful examination and execution of this cause; but rather will be much more of a help and support, so that all may be brought, by an even balance, to the standard of justice — whereby I may at last now refute the things that, through shameless slanders, over a period of more than a year, during which I held my peace in these matters and restrained myself from answering, have been bespattered upon me on this side and that, and may keep my good name, than which nothing ought to be held more precious, unblemished. Wherefore I would also have you, my Holger, by our kinship and our intimate friendship, beseeched in such ways as I can, that, so far as in you lies (and it lies very much in you), you would be willing to bring it about with each of your most praiseworthy parents that nothing intervene which might stand as an obstacle to the handling of this cause and its proper determination. And I am wholly persuaded that they will do this of their own accord without reluctance, and that you will strive for it not unwillingly. For how highly I have always esteemed you (as indeed your singular learning, adorned with equal virtue and candour, deserves) you yourself are in part aware, and others can bear witness — those who have heard me proclaiming to you [your] distinguished gifts above the rest of our countrymen of illustrious birth. But I had rather [say] these things about you with others than to your face.</p><p>Farewell, and keep our friendship — as I for my part shall constantly do — inviolate.<br>Given at Uraniborg, on the 22nd day of January, in the year 1596.<br>Most loving of you,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / \"manu propria.\"</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. The closing <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand\") indicates Tycho subscribed the letter autograph; the surviving witness, however, is a later transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b> (Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet), not the autograph itself.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosencrantzius, Georgij f. de Rosenholm</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (1574–1642), later renowned as <i>den lærde Holger</i> (\"the learned Holger\"), theologian and Privy Councillor, owner of Rosenholm Castle. In 1596 he was a young man of 21; Tycho addresses him as a <i>consanguineus</i> (kinsman — the Brahe and Rosenkrantz lines were intermarried; Holger's mother was a Brahe connection) and flatters his birth and erudition.</li><li><b>\"Georgij F.\" / \"tuo honoratissimo parente.\"</b> <i>Georgius</i> = <b>Jørgen (Georg) Rosenkrantz</b> (1523–1596) of Rosenholm, Holger's father, a member of the Rigsråd — hence one of the \"Lord Viceroys\" (<i>Domini Proreges</i>) whose influence at court Tycho fears the defendant will solicit. The phrase <i>utrumque tuum laudatissimum parentem</i> (\"each of your most praiseworthy parents\") embraces both father and mother; Holger's mother was <b>Dorte Lange</b> (cf. her 1589 portrait with Jørgen at Rosenholm).</li><li><b>The unnamed defendant (\"quendam … in ius vocare\").</b> Not named in the letter, but securely identified from the date and circumstance as <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (Gellius Sascerides Hafniensis, b. 1562), Tycho's former pupil on Hven, whose broken betrothal to Tycho's daughter <b>Magdalene Brahe</b> had by 1595–96 turned into a bitter and very public lawsuit that Tycho framed as a matter of his own <i>fama</i> (good name). The \"shameless slanders … over more than a year\" refer to this affair; Tycho is the plaintiff seeking to compel testimony and prevent the defendant from finding political cover at court.</li><li><b>Legal vocabulary.</b> <i>in ius vocare</i> = \"to summon into court / to law\"; <i>actio</i> = \"(legal) suit / action\"; <i>caussa</i> (= <i>causa</i>) = \"cause / case\"; <i>cognitio et executio</i> = \"examination and execution [of the cause],\" i.e. the judicial hearing and its enforcement; <i>competens determinatio</i> = \"proper / competent determination [= verdict]\"; <i>diverticula quaerere</i> = \"to seek by-ways / evasions\"; <i>caussam palliare</i> = \"to cloak / gloss over one's cause.\" The mood and force of the negations are reproduced exactly: <i>non saltem nullatenus quippiam admissurum</i> is an emphatic double negation (\"will not in the least admit anything\") asserting that the father will permit no obstruction — rendered without flattening the doubled negative; <i>te non invite enisurum</i> = \"that you will strive not unwillingly.\"</li><li><b>\"D. Principem et Regem nostrum electum clementissimum et Dominos Proreges.\"</b> The \"Prince, our most clement King-elect\" is <b>Christian IV</b> (b. 1577), elected king in 1596 but not yet crowned (the coronation followed in August 1596); during his minority the realm was governed by a regency of four <b>Rigsråder</b> (<i>Domini Proreges</i>, \"Lord Viceroys / Regents\"), among them Jørgen Rosenkrantz. <i>electum</i> (\"elect[ed]\") is retained literally and is chronologically exact for January 1596.</li><li><b>\"Lundis.\"</b> <i>Lundis</i> = \"at <b>Lund</b>\" (in Scania), where the defendant had reportedly hinted he would obtain the elder Rosenkrantz's protection; locative kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Honorifics / formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>Salutem Plurimam</i> (\"Heartiest greeting,\" protocol §5.5); the heading's parallel <i>Tam maiorum imaginibus quam … praeeminentia</i> (\"as much by the portraits/images of his ancestors as by his pre-eminence\") plays on <i>imagines maiorum</i>, the Roman ancestral portrait-masks denoting noble lineage. <i>Vale … illibatam conserva</i> = \"Farewell … keep [it] inviolate\"; <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary plural for <i>Datae</i>) = \"Given.\" <i>Tui amantissimus</i> = \"Most loving of you.\"</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Humanist Latin with consonantal <i>j</i> for <i>i</i> (<i>Georgij, ijs→hisce, Ianuarij, alij, amicitiam</i>) and <i>-ae</i> written as <i>Ae/ae</i> in majuscule (<i>DOCTRINAe, PRAeEMINENTIA, famae</i>); <i>caussa</i> for <i>causa</i> and <i>unâque, quâ</i> with the circumflex are the editor's diacritics, reproduced as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 22. Ianuarij Anno 1596</i> — 22 January 1596, Old Style (Denmark used the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline matches the dataset label; no discrepancy. Note the historical caution: in Tycho's reckoning the year had begun on 1 January, so \"1596\" here is unambiguous.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VII p. 374) reads <i>quam <b>propria</b> virtute</i> — “by his <b>own</b> virtue,” the antithesis to the inherited <i>imagines maiorum</i>; the transcription had dropped “-pria.” Corrected in the heading and its translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clear, idiomatic humanist legal-epistolary prose with no textual or lexical crux; the only interpretive point (the identity of the unnamed defendant as Gellius Sascerides) is supplied in the apparatus from external evidence and is not asserted in the translation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.373-376 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the full title/salutation (Georgii filius, de Rosenholm), the body concerning the lawsuit and the flight to the Court/Viceroys (Proreges), the Lund reference, and the closing dated 22 January 1596, against the printed page word-for-word. Text matches source_text exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15960000001", "next": "brahe-t_15971107001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15960000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15971216001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Rosenkrantz to prevent a defendant from fleeing to court, where Tycho is pressing a legal suit requiring testimony in defence of his reputation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960122001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960219001", "d": "1596-02-19", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Cort Aslaksen", "ck": "Cort Aslaksen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Cort Aslaksen — Uraniborg, 19 February 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Sphaericorum librorum trium </i>(Cort Aslaksen,<i> Sphaericorum libri tres</i>, in which Tycho's letter was printed) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>M. CUNRADO ASLACIO BERGENSI.</p><p>... Doctissimi illius B. PITISCI de Triangulis acutum et compendiosum libellum lubens accepi, rogoque ut illi ex me gratias agas. Optarem plures eiuscemodi concionatores reperiri, qui Geometrica gnaviter callerent, forte plus esset in ijs circumspecti et solidi iudicij, rixarum inanium et logomachiarum minus. Si is mihi aliquando scripserit et de hisce studijs contulerit, inveniet responsorem non invitum. Idem apud alios eruditos viros, quos scis emunctioris nasi, effice.</p><p>19. Febr. 1596 Uraniburgi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Cunradus Aslacius of Bergen.</b></p><p>... I gladly received that most learned B. Pitiscus's keen and compendious little book on triangles, and I ask you to give him thanks on my behalf. I could wish that more preachers of this kind were to be found, men who had a diligent command of geometry: perhaps there would be in them more of circumspect and solid judgement, and less of empty wrangling and logomachy [word-battling]. If he should at some time write to me and confer about these studies, he will find me a not-unwilling correspondent. Bring this about likewise among the other learned men whom you know to be of a more discerning nose.</p><p>19 February 1596, [at] Uraniborg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <i>M. Cunrado Aslacio Bergensi</i> — Master <b>Cort Aslaksen</b> (Conradus Aslacus / Cunradus Aslacius Bergensis, 1564–1624), born in Bergen, Norway; one of Tycho's pupils and assistants (<i>famuli</i>) on Hven in the early 1590s, later professor at the University of Copenhagen (matriculated there 22 Oct. 1607; d. 10 Feb. 1624). This is an excerpt from Tycho's letter to him, printed in Aslaksen's own <i>Sphaericorum libri tres</i> (the edition of record; the source's `edition` field gives the genitive <i>Sphaericorum librorum trium</i>). The leading <i>...</i> shows the editor's excision of the letter's opening — only this passage and the dateline are transmitted here.</li><li><b><i>B. Pitisci de Triangulis libellum</b></i> — <b>Bartholomäus Pitiscus</b> (1561–1613), Calvinist court chaplain (hence Tycho's calling him and his like <i>concionatores</i>, \"preachers\") and mathematician, whose <i>Trigonometria sive de dimensione triangulorum</i> (first appended to A. Scultetus's <i>Sphaericorum libri tres</i>, Heidelberg 1595) introduced the very word \"trigonometry.\" <i>de Triangulis … libellum</i> = \"the little book on triangles,\" i.e. this <i>Trigonometria</i>; <i>acutum et compendiosum</i> = \"keen and compendious.\" The abbreviation <i>B.</i> before the name is a courtesy initial (Bartholomaeus).</li><li><b><i>concionatores</b></i> — \"preachers\" (<i>contionatores</i>), here in the literal clerical sense: Tycho wishes more <b>clergymen</b> had Pitiscus's command of geometry. Rendered \"preachers,\" not metaphorically.</li><li><b><i>qui Geometrica gnaviter callerent</b></i> — \"who had a diligent command of geometry\" (<i>Geometrica callere</i> = to be skilled in geometrical matters). The subjunctive <i>callerent / esset</i> keeps Tycho's wish-and-conjecture mood (\"I could wish … perhaps there would be\"); the hedge <i>forte</i> (\"perhaps\") is preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b><i>logomachiarum</b></i> — <i>logomachia</i> (from Greek λογομαχία, \"word-battle\"), \"logomachy\": disputes over mere words. Tycho's Greek-derived term is kept and glossed in brackets, per protocol §2. Paired with <i>rixae inanes</i> (\"empty wrangling\").</li><li><b><i>responsorem non invitum</b></i> — \"a not-unwilling correspondent\" (literally \"a not-unwilling answerer/respondent\"); the litotes is reproduced rather than flattened to \"willing.\"</li><li><b><i>quos scis emunctioris nasi</b></i> — \"whom you know to be of a more discerning nose,\" a Horatian idiom (<i>emunctae naris</i>, \"of a wiped/keen nose\" = of sharp critical judgement; cf. Horace, <i>Sat.</i> 1.4.8); comparative <i>emunctioris</i>, \"of a keener nose.\" Kept as the period idiom and glossed; <i>effice</i> (\"bring [it] about, see to it\") governs <i>idem</i> (\"the same,\" i.e. convey my like greeting/openness).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>19. Febr. 1596 Uraniburgi</i> = \"19 February 1596, at <b>Uraniborg</b>\" (on Hven) — <b>Old Style</b>; Denmark kept the Julian calendar. The dataset `label` (\"1596. 19. februar. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; the one interpretive choice (<i>concionatores</i> as literal \"preachers/clergymen,\" consistent with the Pitiscus-the-court-chaplain context) is noted, and Tycho's epistemic hedges (<i>forte</i>, the litotes <i>non invitum</i>) are preserved unsharpened.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.376-378 (page images). The Tycho-to-Aslakssen excerpt is printed on p.377 under 'M. CUNRADO ASLACIO BERGENSI.', matching source_text word-for-word including the opening ellipsis. Checked the named person (B. Pitiscus, 'de Triangulis') and the closing date (19 Febr. 1596 Uraniburgi) against the translation, and confirmed the idiomatic phrases ('emunctioris nasi', 'logomachiarum') are rendered with sense preserved and appropriately glossed in brackets. No defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Cort Aslaksen", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15941023001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15941023001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Aslaksen for Pitiscus's book on triangles, praises it, and invites Pitiscus to correspond, expressing a wish that more clergymen possessed skill in geometry.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960219001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960325001", "d": "1596-03-25", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Johannes Pontanus", "ck": "Johannes Pontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Isaacius Pontanus — Uraniborg, 25 March 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with one verse quotation of Manilius) · Edition of record:<i> Contra vitam monasticam ad Bern. Mattium epistola </i>(as cited in the dataset; the letter is also in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>) · MS witnesses: none recorded</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIMO ET HUMANISSIMO IUVENI IOHANNI ISACIO PONTANO NUNC CANTABRIGIAe IN ANGLIA LITERIS POLITIORIBUS INCUMBENTI SIBI PLURIMUM DILECTO TYCHO BRAHE.<br>S. P.</p><p>EX literis tuis Cantabrigiae 12. Novemb. anni praecedentis ad me datis, dilecte mi IOANNES ISACIDE, cognovi te nunc in Anglia degere et literis humanioribus criticisque operam navare. Non displicet mihi hoc studium, siquidem eo, a Patre tuo olim sic educatus, inclinare videris. Exhibebit Anglia multos in his commilitones, cum fertilis sit liberalium ingeniorum et eloquentiae inprimis deditorum. Admones de Anglico privilegio, et in eo procurando tuam offers voluntatem; idque oportune admones, gratumque est quod offers. Quaero itaque omnem moveas lapidem, ut illud mature obtineam: si ante festum Pentecostes vel paulo post id fieri queat, votis meis apprime responderes. Efficies id per aliquem primariae auctoritatis in Serenissimae Reginae ELISABETHAe Cancellaria, quae cum sit inter Heroinas nostri seculi non solum genere Regio splendidissima, sed etiam varia doctrina, quod alias in hoc sexu perrarum est, decoratissima, nihil addubito, quin doctrinis et studijs liberalibus, praesertim Astronomicis, quae, ut ait noster Manilius,</p><p>Regales animos primum dignata movere,<br>Proxima tangentes coelo fastigia rerum,<br>clementissime et impense faveat, ea promovere inter alias Regales curas non dedignetur. Quo vero generalis aliqua forma constet scribendi eius diplomatis, mitto exemplum Caesareani, tum quoque Regis Scotorum. Ex quibus perspicax conscriptor facile modum reperiet, quo reliquum illud consignandum sit. Neque tamen opus est praeeuntes omnino sequi, sed fertile Anglorum ingenium facile rationem inveniet, qua singula terse et magnifice, ut artis regiae promotioni accommoda et honorifica sint, concipiantur. Pollicitus est olim optimae memoriae DANIEL ROGERSIUS, amicus meus desideratissimus (in quo eruditio et humanitas pariter certabant) suum in tali privilegio procurando conscribendoque studium, quando is legatione nomine Serenissimae Reginae hîc in Dania functus me semel atque iterum invisere non est degravatus. Sed cum fata nimis immatura hunc tantum nobis praeripuerint, haud dubito, quin in Cancellaria Latina Reginae successor aliquis eius doctrinae et virtuti non impar substitutus sit, cuius officio et beneficio id quod petimus privilegium non invite, uti spero, obtinebimus. Honorarium vicissim meo nomine gratitudinis ergo exhibendum offer et expone. Ego id, quicquid erit, quam primum de eo certior redditus fuero, patri tuo Amstelrodamum, ut ille tibi, restitui curabo. Vide itaque, mi ISACIDE, nequa mora nostris desiderijs aut ulla alia impedimenta obveniant. Habebis me ad tibi vicissim gratificandum non difficilem, et quid expectandum sit, mature significa. Ad quaestionem illam iocosam et nonnihil criticam antiqui LUCRETIJ, cum is sectam Philosophorum Deos eorumque providentiam inficiantem profiteretur, Venerem nihilominus, Aeneadum genitricem, primordio sui operis, eiusque opem imploret, non habeo serio dicere, quomodo haec resolvenda sit, siquidem non ad Veneris sidus coeleste, quod nos unâ cum ceteris subinde scrutamur, sed ad terrestrem illam Venerem, Aeneadum, uti fingebant Poëtae, matrem, et aliorum quoque hominum genitricem pertineat; cuius naturam et genealogiam Anglis ita perspectam esse existimo, tum quoque tuis Batavis, ut non opus sit Danos de his percontari. Meministi me solere dicere Anglos participare ex influentia potissimum ♀ ♃ ☿, unde alacres, mundi et iucundi sunt, tum quoque ingeniosi pariter et foecundi. Est igitur haec quaestio, licet non Angelica, Anglicana tamen et faceta. Quamobrem ab ijs potius quam a nobis eius enodationem edisces. Siquid tamen in his nostri valent lusus, crediderim LUCRETIUM ad imitationem aliorum Poëtarum sic exorsum esse, non quod revera aliquam Deam, quae Venus appellaretur, aut ulla alia numina statueret. Ideoque sub hoc nomine voluptatem corpoream, quam etiam Deam subinde nuncupare non veretur, intellexisse arbitror. Verum tute cum alijs sodalibus haec plenius dispicito. Ante omnia vero voluptatem animi vere Deam omnibus corporis illecebris anteponito. Sic te verae Veneres, Charites et Musae nunquam intermoriturae perpetuo redamabunt et sertis semper virentibus coronabunt. Vale et nostros in Anglia amicos, licet de facie non omnes notos, quos non paucos esse mihi persuadeo, diligenter ex me saluta, inprimis optimum illum et reverendum senem, virum excellentissimum THOMAM DIGGAeSEUM, si isthic locorum is degit. Obtinebunt hi et alij brevi nonnulla ex nostris Operibus, in quibus hos quoque permixtos Ducibus noscetur Olympi. Iamque iterum et multoties vale, mi IOANNES, et me saepius tuis invise, hisque actutum rescribe.</p><p>Raptim Uraniburgi die 25. Martij Anni 1596.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>Binas eodem hoc tempore ad te per diversos homines misi<br>literas, uti si alteræ interciderent, unæ saltem ex his tibi traderentur.<br>Quin et hasce ternas, ut res certior esset, addidi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to the most learned and most courteous young man Johannes Isacius Pontanus, now at Cambridge in England devoting himself to the more polished letters, most dearly beloved of him. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>From your letter written to me at Cambridge on 12 November of last year, my beloved Johannes son of Isaac [Isacides], I learned that you are now living in England and applying yourself to the more humane and critical letters. This study does not displease me, seeing that you appear to incline toward it, having long ago been so educated by your father. England will furnish you many fellow-soldiers in these pursuits, since it is fertile in liberal talents and in men especially devoted to eloquence. You advise me about the English privilege, and offer your goodwill in procuring it; and you advise this opportunely, and what you offer is welcome. I ask, therefore, that you move every stone, so that I may obtain it in good time: if it can be done before the feast of Pentecost or a little after, you would answer my wishes exceedingly well. You will accomplish it through someone of leading authority in the Chancery of the Most Serene Queen Elizabeth; and since she, among the heroines of our age, is not only most splendid by royal birth but also most adorned with manifold learning — which is otherwise very rare in this sex — I do not at all doubt that she most graciously and lavishly favours the learned disciplines and the liberal studies, especially the astronomical, which, as our Manilius says,</p><p>First deigned to stir the minds of kings,<br>Who touch the topmost heights of things nearest to heaven,<br>and that she does not disdain, among her other royal cares, to promote them. But that some general form may be at hand for the drafting of that diploma, I send a specimen of the Imperial one [Caesareani], and also that of the King of the Scots. From these a perceptive drafter will easily find the manner in which the rest of it is to be drawn up. Yet there is no need to follow the precedents altogether; rather the fertile English wit will easily find a way in which each particular may be conceived neatly and magnificently, so that they are suited and honourable to the advancement of the royal art [the art of astronomy]. There once promised — of best memory — Daniel Rogers, my most longed-for friend (in whom erudition and humanity strove on equal terms), his zeal in procuring and drafting such a privilege, when, discharging an embassy here in Denmark in the name of the Most Serene Queen, he did not think it beneath him to visit me once and again. But since fates all too untimely have snatched this great man from us, I do not doubt that in the Queen's Latin Chancery some successor not unequal to his learning and virtue has been put in his place, by whose office and good service we shall obtain — not unwillingly, as I hope — the privilege we seek. Offer and set forth in turn, in my name, an honorarium to be presented by way of gratitude. Whatever it shall be, as soon as I am made more certain of it, I shall see it repaid to your father at Amsterdam, so that he [may repay it] to you. See to it therefore, my son of Isaac, that no delay nor any other impediments befall our desires. You will find me not difficult in gratifying you in turn, and signify in good time what is to be expected. As to that jocular and somewhat critical question concerning the ancient Lucretius — that, while he professed the sect of philosophers who deny the gods and their providence, he nonetheless, at the very beginning of his work, invokes Venus, the mother of the descendants of Aeneas, and implores her aid — I cannot say in earnest how this is to be resolved, seeing that it pertains not to the celestial star of Venus, which we together with the rest now and again scrutinise, but to that earthly Venus, mother of the Aeneadae, as the poets feigned, and begetter of other men too; whose nature and genealogy I judge to be so well understood by the English, and also by your Batavians [the Dutch], that there is no need to question Danes about these matters. You remember that I am wont to say that the English partake especially of the influence of ♀ ♃ ☿ [Venus, Jupiter, Mercury], whence they are lively, elegant, and pleasant, and likewise ingenious and fertile alike. This question, then, though not Angelic, is yet Anglican and witty. Wherefore you will learn its unravelling from them rather than from us. Yet if our jests have any weight in these matters, I should believe that Lucretius began thus in imitation of other poets, not because he really posited some goddess who should be called Venus, or any other divine powers. And so under this name I judge that he understood bodily pleasure, which too he does not fear now and again to call a goddess. But do you, with your other companions, look into these matters more fully. Above all, however, set the pleasure of the mind, truly a goddess, before all the enticements of the body. So shall the true Venuses, Graces, and Muses, never to die, love you back perpetually and crown you with ever-green garlands. Farewell, and diligently greet on my behalf our friends in England, though not all known to me by face, of whom I am persuaded there are not a few — above all that excellent and reverend old man, the most excellent man Thomas Digges, if he is living in those parts. These and others will shortly obtain some of our Works, in which they too will be known to be mingled with the Leaders of Olympus. And now again and many times farewell, my Johannes, and visit me more often with your letters, and write back to this one straightway.</p><p>In haste at Uraniborg, the 25th day of March in the year 1596.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p><p>At this same time I have sent you two [sets of] letters through different men, so that, if one set should be lost, at least one of these might be delivered to you. Indeed, I have even added this third, that the matter might be more certain.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven. <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand,\" a subscription formula.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Iohannes Isacius Pontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Isaacius Pontanus</b> (Jan Isaacsz Pontanus, 1571–1639), born at Elsinore of a Haarlem patrician family that had fled to Denmark for its Protestant faith; Tycho's <i>famulus</i> (assistant) on Hven 1593–95, later a noted historian in Denmark and the Netherlands. Tycho addresses him with the playful Greek-style patronymic <b>Isacides</b> (\"son of Isaac\"), since his father was <b>Isaac Pietersz</b>, then at Amsterdam (<i>patri tuo Amstelrodamum</i>, \"to your father at Amsterdam\"). At the date of the letter Pontanus was studying at <b>Cambridge</b> (<i>Cantabrigiae</i>).</li><li><b>Salutation.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (dicit), \"(sends) heartiest greeting\" (§5.5). <i>literis politioribus / humanioribus criticisque</i> — \"the more polished / the more humane and critical letters,\" i.e. classical philology and textual criticism (the <i>studia humaniora</i>).</li><li><b><i>Anglicum privilegium</b></i> — \"the English privilege\": a royal printing-privilege / copyright protection Tycho sought from Elizabeth I for his published <i>Works</i> (his <i>Opera</i>), to be drafted in the <b>Queen's Latin Chancery</b>. He encloses model documents — <i>exemplum Caesareani</i> (\"a specimen of the Imperial [privilege],\" i.e. from the Holy Roman Emperor) and <i>Regis Scotorum</i> (\"that of the King of the Scots,\" James VI of Scotland, later James I).</li><li><b><i>omnem moveas lapidem</b></i> — \"move every stone,\" the classical idiom (Greek <i>πάντα κινῆσαι λίθον</i>); rendered literally to preserve the figure.</li><li><b><i>festum Pentecostes</b></i> — the feast of <b>Pentecost</b> (Whitsun); a movable liturgical date used as a calendar marker. Not converted.</li><li><b>Queen Elizabeth.</b> <i>Serenissima Regina Elisabetha</i> = <b>Elizabeth I of England</b> (r. 1558–1603). Tycho's praise of her learning \"rare in this sex\" (<i>quod alias in hoc sexu perrarum est</i>) is preserved as written, hedge intact.</li><li><b>Manilius quotation.</b> <i>ut ait noster Manilius</i> — <b>Marcus Manilius</b>, <i>Astronomica</i> I.41–42 (the didactic Latin astronomical poem). The two verses are kept as verse per §4: <i>Regales animos primum dignata movere, / Proxima tangentes coelo fastigia rerum</i> — astronomy \"first deigned to stir the minds of kings, who touch the topmost heights of things nearest to heaven.\" <i>noster Manilius</i> = \"our Manilius,\" claiming the poet as a kindred astronomer.</li><li><b><i>artis regiae</b></i> — here \"the royal art,\" i.e. astronomy (the <i>ars regia</i> of stirring kings' minds in the Manilius lines), not alchemy; rendered \"the royal art [the art of astronomy].\"</li><li><b>Daniel Rogers.</b> <i>Daniel Rogersius</i> = <b>Daniel Rogers</b> (c. 1538–1591), the Anglo-Dutch humanist and Elizabethan diplomat who had visited Tycho during embassies to Denmark; here remembered as dead (<i>optimae memoriae … fata nimis immatura hunc … praeripuerint</i>). <i>Cancellaria Latina Reginae</i> = the <b>Queen's Latin Secretariat / Chancery</b> (the office handling the crown's Latin diplomatic correspondence), in which Rogers had served.</li><li><b><i>honorarium</b></i> — \"an honorarium,\" a fee/gratuity to be advanced by Pontanus and repaid by Tycho via Pontanus's father at Amsterdam.</li><li><b>Lucretius question.</b> <i>antiqui Lucretij</i> = <b>Lucretius</b> (<i>De rerum natura</i>), the Epicurean poet who \"denies the gods and their providence\" (<i>Deos eorumque providentiam inficiantem</i>) yet opens his poem with an invocation of <b>Venus</b> (<i>Aeneadum genitrix</i>, \"mother of the descendants of Aeneas,\" I.1). Tycho distinguishes the <i>Veneris sidus coeleste</i> — \"the celestial star of Venus\" (the planet, which astronomers scrutinise) — from the <i>terrestris Venus</i> — \"that earthly Venus,\" the mythological/sensual goddess. He resolves the puzzle by reading Lucretius's Venus as a poetic personification of <i>voluptas corporea</i> (\"bodily pleasure\"). Hedges preserved: <i>non habeo serio dicere</i> (\"I cannot say in earnest\"), <i>crediderim</i> (\"I should believe\"), <i>arbitror</i> (\"I judge\").</li><li><b><i>Veneris sidus</b></i> — rendered \"the star of Venus\" (the planet); per §5.1 <i>sidus/stella</i> of a planet is the planet itself.</li><li><b>Astral-influence remark.</b> <i>Anglos participare ex influentia potissimum ♀ ♃ ☿</i> — \"the English partake especially of the influence of Venus, Jupiter, Mercury.\" The planet glyphs ♀ ♃ ☿ are kept in the Source block and named in English per §5.3; whence Tycho's characterology of the English as <i>alacres, mundi, iucundi … ingeniosi … foecundi</i>.</li><li><b><i>non Angelica … Anglicana</b></i> — a pun on <i>Angelicus</i> (\"Angelic\") / <i>Anglicanus</i> (\"English/Anglican\"), playing on the old <i>Angli/Angeli</i> topos (Gregory the Great's \"non Angli sed Angeli\"). The wordplay is kept rather than flattened: \"though not Angelic, yet Anglican.\"</li><li><b><i>verae Veneres, Charites et Musae</b></i> — \"the true Venuses, Graces [<i>Charites</i>], and Muses\": the <i>Charites</i> are the three Graces (Greek term kept). <i>sertis semper virentibus</i> = \"with ever-green garlands.\"</li><li><b>Thomas Digges.</b> <i>Thomam Diggaeseum</i> = <b>Thomas Digges</b> (c. 1546–1595), the English mathematician and Copernican; Tycho's hedge <i>si isthic locorum is degit</i> (\"if he is living in those parts\") is preserved — Tycho did not know Digges had in fact died in August 1595, which the hedge inadvertently flags.</li><li><b><i>Ducibus … Olympi</b></i> — \"the Leaders of Olympus,\" a flourish for the eminent men to whom Tycho will send his <i>Works</i>; the phrase echoes Manilius/poetic diction. Rendered literally.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Raptim Uraniburgi die 25. Martij Anni 1596</i> = \"In haste at <b>Uraniborg</b>, the 25th day of March, the year 1596\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1596. 25. marts. Uraniborg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The dataset's `edition` field reads <i>\"Contra vitam monasticam ad Bern. Mattium epistola\"</i> — this appears to be a mis-carried edition tag (that title is a separate work and does not match this letter); the letter is among Tycho's <i>Epistolae</i> (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i>). Flagged, not relied upon.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A short paragraph immediately preceding the letter's warm close (greetings to English friends, Thomas Digges, etc., in the published tail) — Tycho explaining his own redundant-delivery practice: this is, by his own account, the <i>third</i> copy of essentially the same letter, sent by three different couriers to guard against loss in transit.</li><li><b>The recipient.</b> Johannes Isaacius Pontanus (1571–1639), the young Danish-Norwegian humanist scholar and mathematician who had spent time in Tycho's circle; he later became a historian (author of a major history of Denmark) and remained a lifelong correspondent, addressed here with evident personal warmth (\"mi Ioannes\" in the published tail).</li><li><b>Significance for the corpus of letters.</b> This is direct authorial testimony to a documented hazard of early modern scholarly correspondence — letters routinely lost in transit — and to Tycho's countermeasure of triplicate dispatch by separate carriers, a practice also implicit elsewhere in this batch (e.g. Fabricius's and Scaliger's concern over safe and swift delivery routes).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Source text corrected against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia VII, page 377: \"nostri secuti\" corrected to \"nostri seculi\" (genitive of saeculum, \"of our age\"). The English translation already read correctly and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin renders securely and the substance (the suit for an English printing-privilege via Elizabeth's Latin Chancery, the Lucretius–Venus <i>jeu d'esprit</i>, the planetary characterology of the English) is clear. Soft points, each flagged above rather than smoothed: the mismatched `edition` tag; the precise referent of <i>artis regiae</i> (\"royal art\" = astronomy here); and the exact identity of Rogers's \"successor\" in the Latin Chancery (left unnamed by Tycho).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — sense secure end-to-end, date agrees; residue only a mis-tagged edition field + glossed idioms — no textual crux.</i></p><p>The Latin is simple and entirely unambiguous; there is no crux of any kind. Confident throughout.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images TBOO VII pp.377-379 (letter to Johannes Isacius Pontanus runs from the bottom of p.377 through p.379, ending \"manu propria\" plus the triple-letter postscript; p.376 and the tail of p.379/p.380 hold unrelated letters to Hardewig Dassell, Cunrad Aslacius, and Heinrich Rantzau and were not needed for this letter). Collated the whole letter clause by clause: the Manilius couplet, the Elizabeth/Chancery/Daniel Rogers passage, the Amsterdam repayment arrangement, the full Lucretius/Venus excursus with the ♀♃☿ planetary-influence joke, and the closing Thomas Digges greeting and 25 March 1596 date — all names, the date, and every clause match Dreyer and are accurately rendered in published_english. One TEXT_ERROR found in source_text itself (see defects); per brief, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it is low-severity and does not affect the sense a scholar would take from the translation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our stored source_text reads \"nostri secuti\" (ungrammatical - a form of sequor with no sense here); Dreyer's page image clearly prints \"nostri seculi\" (\"of our age/century\", genitive of saeculum), the only reading that parses. The English translation already renders this correctly as \"among the heroines of our age\", so the corruption is confined to our source_text field. Dreyer prints: <i>quae cum sit inter Heroinas nostri seculi non solum genere Regio splendidißima</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The single defect site was collated against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia volume VII, page 377 (page image, not the OCR text layer). Confirmed: \"quae cum ſit inter Heroinas noſtri ſeculi non ſolum genere Regio ſplendidiſſima.\" No other cruxes were found on the sampled pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Johannes Pontanus", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15950905001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to news that Pontanus is now in England, commends his literary studies there, and urges him to secure an English publication privilege before Pentecost.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960325001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960331001", "d": "1596-03-31", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → the Rector of the University of Copenhagen — [no place given], 31 March 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (with a Latin address-rubric and Latin code-switches) · Edition of record:<i> Danske Magazin </i>· MS witness: none recorded</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[AD RECTOREM UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS].</p><p>MAGNIFICE domine rector. Eftersom ieg talde med eder y gaar, og waar venligen begierendis, att mig motte bliffue førdrett mett nogen endelig beskeen, och icke lenger opholden, mett hvis mig burde at haffue beskreffuit aff eders consistorio, och att ieg derhos maatte faae dett att see, førend dett bleff beseglet, eftersom mig anderstedz vederfaret er, saa well eder selff mett eders assessoribus som mig till beste, och widere uleylighed att forekomme. Jeg begier dog intet andet, end huis billigt och rett er och sandhed gemeß, eftersom eders acta udviser och eder alle witterligt kand vaere. Paa dett første gaff j mig temmelig beskeden, formenendis, att dett skulde bliffue snart fuldendt. Mens om dett andet kunde j ingen rigtig fortrøstning giøre mig, førend j havde talet och raadslaget met eders assessoribus derom. Thi er ieg endnu aff eder och de andre eders collegis ett gott och endeligt suar udi begge stycker paa thet wenligste och i en god mening begierendis, saa att ieg kunde faa att wide mett thette mitt bud, huoreffter ieg haffuer mig att rette. Thet forskylder ieg gierne igien, huor mig mugeligt kand were. Ombedendis derhos, att der motte bliffue tegnet paa denne skrifft, at den er laest for eder.</p><p>Actum 31. martii 1596.<br>Thyge Brahe<br>met egen haand.</p>", "en": "<p>[TO THE RECTOR OF THE UNIVERSITY OF COPENHAGEN].</p><p>Magnificent lord rector. Inasmuch as I spoke with you yesterday, and was kindly requesting that I might be furthered [helped forward] with some final statement, and no longer held off, with that which I ought to have set down in writing for me by your consistory; and that I might moreover get to see it before it was sealed, as has befallen me elsewhere — both for your own and your assessors' best [interest] as well as mine, and to forestall further inconvenience. Yet I ask nothing else than what is fair and right and conformable to the truth, as your records [acta] show and as can be known to you all. At first you gave me a fair enough answer, supposing that it should soon be brought to completion. But concerning the other [matter] you could give me no proper assurance until you had spoken and taken counsel with your assessors about it. Therefore I am still, of you and the others, your colleagues, requesting a good and final answer on both points, in the friendliest way and in good intent, so that I may get to know by this my messenger how I am to govern myself. This I gladly requite in return, in whatever way may be possible for me. Requesting moreover that there might be marked upon this writing that it has been read out before you.</p><p>Done, 31 March 1596.<br>Tyge Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / hand.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), signing <i>Thyge Brahe met egen haand</i> — \"Tyge Brahe, with his own hand,\" i.e. an autograph subscription (the Latin formula's Danish equivalent of <i>manu propria</i>). Tycho's name appears in its Danish vernacular form <i>Thyge</i> (not the Latin <i>Tycho</i>) in the subscription.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> Addressed by office only — <i>Magnifice domine rector</i> / <i>Rector Universitatis Hauniensis</i>, the <b>rector of the University of Copenhagen</b> (the <i>rector magnificus</i>) and his consistory. The text names no individual; the present translation therefore identifies the addressee by office, not by name. The rector of Copenhagen in spring 1596 is not asserted here on the strength of the letter alone.</li><li><b>Context.</b> One of a pair of letters Tycho addressed to the rector on the same day, 31 March 1596 (the companion piece is brahe-t_15960331002, opening <i>Efterdi jeg formercker, att j mett eders assessoribus icke wilde…</i>). Tycho is pressing the university consistory for a formal, sealed, written attestation (<i>nogen endelig beskeen … beskreffuit aff eders consistorio</i>) on two matters (<i>begge stycker</i>) — an administrative dispute, consonant with the deterioration of Tycho's standing at Copenhagen in the years 1596–97 that preceded his departure from Denmark in 1597. The substance of the two \"points\" is not specified within the letter and is not reconstructed here.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches (a learned chancery hand).</b> <i>Magnifice domine rector</i> — \"Magnificent lord rector,\" the formal academic salutation; <i>consistorio</i> — the university <b>consistory</b> (governing council); <i>assessoribus / collegis</i> — its <b>assessors / colleagues</b> (the professors sitting on the consistory); <i>acta</i> — the consistory's <b>records / acts</b>; <i>Actum</i> — \"Done / Given\" (the dating formula, protocol §5.5); <i>met egen haand</i> glosses the <i>manu propria</i> subscription. The body is otherwise Danish.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1596).</b> <i>waar … begierendis</i> = <i>var … begærende</i> (\"was requesting\"); <i>førdrett</i> = <i>fordret/befordret</i> (\"furthered, helped forward\"); <i>opholden</i> = <i>opholdt</i> (\"held off, delayed\"); <i>hvis</i> = relative <i>hvad</i> (\"that which\"); <i>beskreffuit</i> = <i>beskrevet</i> (\"set down in writing\"); <i>anderstedz vederfaret</i> = <i>andetsteds vederfaret</i> (\"befallen [me] elsewhere\"); <i>till beste</i> = <i>til bedste</i> (\"for the best [interest]\"); <i>uleylighed</i> = <i>ulejlighed</i> (\"inconvenience\"); <i>billigt</i> = \"fair, equitable\"; <i>sandhed gemeß</i> = <i>sandhed gemæss</i> (\"conformable to the truth\" — <i>gemæss</i> a German-loan postposition, \"in accordance with\"); <i>temmelig beskeden</i> = <i>temmelig besked</i> (\"a fair enough answer/information\" — <i>beskeden</i> here is the noun <i>besked</i>, not the adjective \"modest\"); <i>fortrøstning</i> = \"assurance, reassurance\"; <i>raadslaget</i> = <i>rådslået</i> (\"taken counsel, deliberated\"); <i>stycker</i> = <i>stykker</i> (\"points, items, matters\"); <i>huoreffter ieg haffuer mig att rette</i> = <i>hvorefter jeg har mig at rette</i> (\"by which I am to govern/regulate myself\"); <i>forskylder … igien</i> = <i>forskylder igen</i> (\"requite, repay in return\"); <i>bud</i> = \"messenger\" (the bearer of the letter); <i>tegnet paa denne skrifft</i> = <i>tegnet på denne skrift</i> (\"marked / noted upon this writing\"); <i>laest</i> = <i>læst</i> (\"read [out]\").</li><li><b>\"beskeen / beskeden / besked.\"</b> The two near-identical forms are the same noun <i>besked</i> (\"a [final] statement, ruling, answer, information\"): <i>endelig beskeen</i> = \"a final statement/ruling\"; <i>temmelig beskeden</i> = \"a fair enough answer.\" Rendered consistently as \"statement / answer\"; not the modern adjective <i>beskeden</i> (\"modest\").</li><li><b>Negations.</b> The body contains two negations — <i>icke lenger opholden</i> (\"no longer held off\") and <i>intet andet, end</i> (\"nothing else than\") — and the reported refusal <i>kunde j ingen rigtig fortrøstning giøre mig</i> (\"you could give me no proper assurance\"). All three are reproduced; none added or dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum 31. martii 1596</i> = \"Done, 31 March 1596\" (Julian / O.S.; Denmark kept Old Style until 1700). No place of writing is given (the dataset's <i>label</i> records the place as <i>s.l.</i> = <i>sine loco</i>, \"without place\"); the header accordingly states \"[no place given].\" The date in the source's own dateline (31 March 1596) agrees with the catalogue label, so no date discrepancy arises.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and all negations are preserved; the only residual uncertainties are extrinsic to the wording: the unnamed rector is identified by office only, and the substance of the two disputed \"points\" (<i>begge stycker</i>) is left as the letter leaves it, unspecified, rather than reconstructed.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — chancery Danish secure, negations preserved, date agrees; residue purely extrinsic (rector by office).</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (entry 110, [Ad rectorem universitatis Hauniensis], 31 March 1596) against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.74-75, reading the page images; found one dropped clause in the published source_text relative to Dreyer. All other clauses, the date, and the signature match; downgraded from the unverified A because a TEXT_ERROR was found even though the English translation itself is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). source_text silently drops the clause 'ett gott och endeligt fuar' (a good and final answer) that Dreyer prints between 'collegis' and 'udi begge stycker'. The published English happens to include this sense anyway, so the reader is not misled, but the published source_text itself is corrupted relative to Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Thi er ieg endnu aff eder och de andre eders collegis ett gott och endeligt fuar udi begge ftycker paa thet wenligfte og i en god mening begierendis</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the full letter against Dreyer, TBOO XIV pp.71-74 (page images, entry 110). The clause the audit's source_text excerpt appeared to be missing ('ett gott och endeligt suar') is in fact already present in the current_latin supplied in this packet, word for word against Dreyer. The audit's defect quote describes a state that does not match what was given to repair; nothing needed changing. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15960331002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presses the rector of the University of Copenhagen for a definitive written reply from the consistory on two pending matters, having received only vague assurances thus far.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960331001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960331002", "d": "1596-03-31", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "ck": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → the Rector of the University of Copenhagen — Copenhagen, 31 March 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (with Latin academic address and chancery formulae) · Edition of record:<i> Danske Magazin </i>· MS witness: none recorded in source</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[AD RECTOREM UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS].</p><p>MAGNIFICE domine rector. Efterdi jeg formercker, att j mett eders assessoribus icke wilde bevilge mig att maa fange att see, hues j giffue mig aff eders consistorio beskreffuen, førend det bliffuer beseglet, endog ieg icke dett aff eder begierede i nogen ond mening, men snarer till dett beste, andre vitløfftighed och uleyglighed att forekomme, saa kand och wil ieg hertill nu intet andet suare, end j kunde derudi effterfølge eders egen betenkning och mening, og lade mig mett det første fange beskreffuet og beseglet, hvis j vilde unde mig, saa at ieg icke nu lenger skal bliffue opholden, med hvilcket ieg faaer paa denne tid att lade mig nøye. Begierendis derhos, att alle eders acta, hues denne min sag angaaer, maa bliffue paa huer side undertegnet, og siden tillbeseglet og velforvaret, indtil videre beskeden, efftersom behoff giøris, og att der motte bliffue tegnet paa denne skrifft, att den er laest for eder.</p><p>Actum Kiøbnehaffn 31. martii 1596.<br>Thyge Brahe<br>met egen haand.</p>", "en": "<p>[To the Rector of the University of Copenhagen.]</p><p>Magnificent lord rector. Since I perceive that you, together with your assessors, would not grant me leave to be allowed to see what you give me set down in writing from your consistory before it is sealed — even though I did not request this of you with any bad intent, but rather for the best, to forestall further prolixity and inconvenience — I therefore can and will answer nothing else to this now than that you may in this matter follow your own judgement and opinion, and let me at the very first have [it] set down in writing and sealed, [namely] whatever you would grant me, so that I shall not now be held up any longer; with which I must for the present let myself be content. Requesting moreover that all your acts touching this my case may be subscribed on each page, and thereafter sealed up and well kept safe, until further notice, as the need shall arise; and that there be noted upon this writing [of mine] that it has been read out before you.</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, 31 March 1596.<br>Tyge Brahe<br>with [his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / subscription.</b> <i>Thyge Brahe … met egen haand</i> = \"<b>Tyge Brahe</b> … with [his] own hand\" — the autograph subscription formula (rendered per protocol §5.5 conventions for <i>manu propria</i>).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> Addressed to the office of the <b>Rector of the University of Copenhagen</b> (<i>Universitas Hauniensis</i>) and the <i>consistorium</i> (the academic senate of professors). The bracketed Latin heading <i>[AD RECTOREM UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS]</i> — \"To the rector of the University of Copenhagen\" — is an <b>editorial supplied title</b> (square brackets as printed in the edition of record), not part of the original letter body; reproduced as printed.</li><li><b>Context.</b> Written in the spring of 1596, in the months before Tycho's relations with the Danish establishment broke down and he left the kingdom (1597). The letter concerns a legal/administrative matter (<i>denne min sag</i>, \"this my case\") in which Tycho had asked to inspect the consistory's written record <i>before</i> it was sealed; the rector and assessors refused, and Tycho here concedes the point while insisting on procedural safeguards (each page subscribed, the whole sealed and kept, and a note that the document was read out to him). <b>No specific person is named</b>; the identification rests at the level of the office.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches (within a Danish body).</b> <i>Magnifice domine rector</i> — \"Magnificent lord rector,\" the standard formal academic address to a university rector; kept and translated. <i>assessoribus</i> (abl. pl.) = \"assessors\" (the consistory members sitting in judgement). <i>consistorio</i> = \"consistory\" (the university senate). <i>acta</i> = \"acts / records\" (the official minutes). <i>Actum</i> = \"Done / Given\" (the dating formula, protocol §5.5). Each is a learned Latin technical noun embedded in the Danish, in the chancery manner described in protocol §2.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1596).</b> <i>Efterdi</i> = \"since, inasmuch as\"; <i>formercker</i> = <i>formærker</i> (\"perceive, observe\"); <i>j</i> = <i>I</i> (\"you,\" pl./polite); <i>icke</i> = <i>ikke</i> (\"not\"); <i>wilde bevilge</i> = <i>ville bevilge</i> (\"would grant\"); <i>fange att see</i> = \"get to see / be allowed to see\"; <i>hues / hvis</i> = \"what / that which\"; <i>beskreffuen / beskreffuet</i> = <i>beskrevet</i> (\"written down, set down in writing\"); <i>beseglet</i> = \"sealed\"; <i>endog</i> = \"even though, although\"; <i>vitløfftighed</i> = <i>vidtløftighed</i> (\"prolixity, protracted trouble\"); <i>uleyglighed</i> = <i>ulejlighed</i> (\"inconvenience\"); <i>forekomme</i> = \"forestall, prevent\"; <i>betenkning</i> = \"deliberation, judgement\"; <i>opholden</i> = <i>opholdt</i> (\"held up, delayed\"); <i>lade mig nøye</i> = \"let myself be content\"; <i>Begierendis derhos</i> = \"requesting moreover\"; <i>huer side</i> = \"each page/side\"; <i>undertegnet</i> = \"subscribed, signed below\"; <i>tillbeseglet</i> = \"sealed up\"; <i>velforvaret</i> = \"well preserved/kept safe\"; <i>indtil videre beskeden</i> = \"until further notice/instruction\"; <i>behoff giøris</i> = \"need arises, is required\"; <i>motte</i> = <i>måtte</i> (\"might\"); <i>tegnet paa</i> = \"noted/marked upon\"; <i>laest for eder</i> = \"read [out] to/before you.\"</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The Danish carries <b>four</b> negations, all preserved in the translation and none added: (1) <i>icke wilde bevilge</i> — \"would <b>not</b> grant\"; (2) <i>icke dett … begierede i nogen ond mening</i> — \"did <b>not</b> request … with any bad intent\"; (3) <i>intet andet suare</i> — \"answer <b>nothing</b> else\"; (4) <i>icke nu lenger skal bliffue opholden</i> — \"shall <b>not</b> now be held up any longer.\" The opening <i>Efterdi … icke wilde bevilge …, saa kand och wil ieg … intet andet suare, end …</i> is a single long causal-conditional period (\"Since … would not grant …, I therefore can and will answer nothing else than that …\"); its logic is concessive resignation, not a counter-demand, and is rendered as such.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Actum Kiøbnehaffn 31. martii 1596</i> = \"Done at <b>Copenhagen</b>, 31 March 1596\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline supplies <b>Copenhagen</b>, although the dataset `label` records the place as <i>s.l.</i> (<i>sine loco</i>, \"without place\"); the as-written place is followed here, the catalogue's <i>s.l.</i> noted. The dateline date (31 March 1596) agrees with the catalogue date; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Dropped the unsupported 'for others' from '...to forestall further prolixity and inconvenience for others'; the Danish 'andre' means 'further/additional', not 'for other people'. Verified from the letter's own Danish wording; the supplied Dreyer slice pages did not contain this letter's text.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely and the four negations are reproduced exactly; the only soft points are (i) the precise legal referent of <i>denne min sag</i> (\"this my case\"), which the letter does not specify and which is left unexpanded, and (ii) the catalogue <i>s.l.</i> vs. the dateline's <i>Kiøbnehaffn</i>, resolved in favour of the as-written place and flagged above.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Danish secure, four negations one-for-one, the s.l.-vs-dateline place resolved as-written; no textual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. Could NOT collate: the supplied slice (TBOO XIV pp.71-72) does not contain this letter. Both pages hold a different, longer letter -- no. 107, dated 1595, 11 January, addressed 'AD RECTOREM ET PROFESSORES UNIVERSITATIS HAUNIENSIS' (rector AND professors, about five letters / Niels Krag / Mogens Bertelsen) -- not the single-addressee 31 March 1596 letter this packet claims. No further pages of the slice were available to check a neighbouring page. Checked the English against source_text alone instead: faithful throughout except one low-severity addition ('for others', not supported by 'andre'). published_grade was A; capped to B here because Dreyer collation failed and a minor mistranslation exists. Flag for pipeline: the tboo page pointer for this slug needs re-derivation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"andre\" most plausibly means \"further/additional\" (more of the same trouble), not \"[trouble] for other people\"; the added \"for others\" is not supported by the Danish and mildly shifts who is said to be inconvenienced. At: “to forestall further prolixity and inconvenience for others”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> This packet's slice PDF (TBOO XIV pp.71-72) reproduces entries 106-108 (unrelated correspondence from 1594-1596), not the Copenhagen, 31 March 1596 letter itself — a page-range/slicing mismatch, not a fault of current_latin. I could not collate the Danish source text itself against Dreyer's actual printed page for this specific letter. The MISTRANSLATION fix rests on internal evidence only (comparing the given Danish to the given English translation), which is linguistically sound and sufficient for a translation-fidelity defect of this type, but the source_text's own fidelity to Dreyer remains formally unverified for this letter pending a corrected slice.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Rektor ved Københavns Universitet", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15960331001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Accepts the rector's refusal to show the unsealed statement, but requests that all consistory acts relating to his case be subscribed on each page, sealed, and retained until needed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960331002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15960402001", "d": "1596-04-02", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Mads Jørgensen Huus", "ck": "Mads Jørgensen Huus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Halsnøy Abbey", "t": "Mads Jørgensen Huus → Tycho Brahe — Halsnøy Abbey, 2 April 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 1</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELBYRDIIGE MANNDT TYGGJ BRADE TILL KNUDSTRUP, HØFFUIDTZMANDT PAA VRANNIBORGH, GANDTZE YDMYGELIGENN TILLSCHREFFUITT.</p><p>MYNN gandske ydmygelige helsßenn nu och altidt forsenndt medt Wor Herre. Kierre Tyggi Brade, thacker ieg ethers fromhedt for alt ere och gott, som y meg giort och beuist haffuer, for huilcket ieg effter minn formoue gandtze giernne will findis ethers fattige thienner till att giørre och lade alt huis ieg widt ethers fromhedt kandt haffue ere och gott aff. Kierre Tyggi och guode juncker, giffuer ieg ethers welbyrd[ig]hedt wenligen att wiide, om thj breffue, som eders fromhedt meg antuordit haffuer, denom ere huer well thill throer hande komit, som thj war tillschreffuit (Gud werre loffuit). Sameledis, frome juncker, som ethers welbyrdighedt well dragis tiill minnde, att ieg paa ethers wegnne schulle giøre min beste och forhørre ether om enn from, erlige personne, som kunde well stande ethers fromhedtz lehnn och befalling Nordfiordt forre, och som well widste landzenns leilighedt och skicke, huorfor ieg haffuer giffuit thenne breffuißer ethers welbyrdighedtz willie tilkiennde. Om saa kunde werre, attj wille betrou hanom eders lehnn, tha thuiller ieg jntedt ther paa, att samme personn, Morttenn Bertilßønn, skall io bliffue och werre eders fromhedt huldt och trou y alle maade, saa y nest Gudtz hielp skall hanom jche andit skylde, end thet som erligt och christeligt skall werre. Och thuiller ieg iche heller paa, att hand skall forsøme seg y huis maade, som handt widt eders welbyrdighedtz sager och bestelling kandt kome till gaffn och beste vdj nogenn maader. Ther for, gunstige juncker, haffuer ieg paa eders guode behagh forskreffuet samme Morttenn Bertilßønn till ether, saa ethers fromhedt kanndt sielffuer widere handle och talle medt hanom. Och om ieg widste, att handt war iche throu och guodt, tha skulle ieg jche forschriffue hanom tiill eders fromhedt; thi handt haffuer thiennt min hustrue[s] fader, Michell Eskelßønn, fogitt y Halsnø Kloster, y 9 aar som en erlig och trou karll y all maade, saa handt maa fritt skyude sinn skodtzmaall tiill hanom igienn, om behouff giøris. Frome gunstige juncker, er minn ydmygelige bønn till ethers welbyrdighedt, om saa er, att ethers fromhedt iche sielff hagde hans tienniste behouff, y tha wille wel giøre, som enn from manndt, och werre hanom behielpelig huos enn ethers fromhedtz guode wenner, som haffuer forlening her i landit, att handt motte fange nogenn pladtz. Thj hanndt widt well landzens leilighedt. Och will ieg iche lenger bemøde ethers fromhedt medt thenne minn schriffuelße, menn altidt giernne giøre huis ethers fromhedt kand werre till thienniste och guode, huilcke Gudt allemectigste kiennde. Jeg will nu och altidt haffue ether euindelige beffallindis.</p><p>Schreffuit vdj Halsnø closter then 2 aprillis anno 96.<br>Ethers ydmyge thienner altidt<br>Matzs<br>Jørgensßøn.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born man Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup, governor at Uraniborg, written all most humbly.</b></p><p>My wholly humble greeting, now and always conveyed with Our Lord. Dear Tyge Brahe, I thank your worthiness [<i>fromhedt</i>] for all honour and good that you have done and shown me, for which, to the best of my ability, I will most gladly show myself your poor servant, to do and to leave undone all whereby I may, with your worthiness, have honour and good. Dear Tyge and good squire [<i>juncker</i>], I make known to your well-bornness in friendly wise that the letters which your worthiness entrusted to me have each duly come into faithful hands, as they were addressed (God be praised). Likewise, good squire, as your well-bornness will well recall, that I, on your behalf, was to do my best and make inquiry for you after an upright, honest person who could well preside over your worthiness's fief and command of Nordfjord, and who knew well the country's condition and order — wherefore I have made known to this letter-bearer your well-bornness's wish [<i>willie</i>]. If it might be that you would entrust your fief to him, then I doubt nothing thereof: that this same person, Morten Bertelsen, shall indeed remain and be loyal and faithful to your worthiness in every way, so that, next to God's help, you shall charge him with nothing other than what shall be honest and Christian. And I doubt no more either that he shall be negligent in any way wherein he, in your well-bornness's affairs and business, can come to profit and advantage in any manner. Therefore, gracious squire, at your good pleasure I have written the same Morten Bertelsen to you, so that your worthiness may yourself deal and speak further with him. And if I knew that he were not faithful and good, then I should not commend him to your worthiness; for he has served my wife's father, Michel Eskelsen, bailiff [<i>fogitt</i>] at Halsnøy Abbey, for nine years as an honest and faithful fellow in every way, so that he may freely refer his testimonial [<i>skodtzmaall</i>] back to him, if need be. Good gracious squire, my humble plea to your well-bornness is, if it be so that your worthiness yourself had no need of his service, that you would then do well, as an upright man, and be helpful to him with one of your worthiness's good friends who has an enfeoffment [<i>forlening</i>] here in this land, that he might obtain some place. For he knows well the country's condition. And I will not trouble your worthiness longer with this my writing, but will always gladly do what may be of service and good to your worthiness, as Almighty God knows. I will now and always have you eternally commended [to God].</p><p>Written at Halsnøy Abbey, the 2nd of April, the year [15]96.<br>Your humble servant always,<br>Mads<br>Jørgensen.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent (sender).</b> <i>Matzs Jørgensßøn</i> = <b>Mads Jørgensen Huus</b> (the surname is supplied from the dataset/edition heading, not from the letter's own subscription, which signs only \"Mads Jørgensen\"). He writes from Halsnøy Abbey in western Norway, evidently in some administrative or household capacity connected to the bailiff Michel Eskelsen, whose daughter he had married. He is otherwise a minor figure; this commendatory letter is his sole appearance in the Brahe correspondence.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tyge (Tycho) Brahe of Knudstrup</b>, here addressed by his Danish style and titled <i>høffuidtzmandt paa Uraniborg</i> (\"governor / captain at Uraniborg\"). At this date (1596) Tycho still held Hven and his crown fiefs; the letter assumes he has fiefs (<i>lehn</i>) at his disposal to bestow, including <b>Nordfjord</b> in Norway.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> An autograph <b>letter of recommendation (commendation)</b> for the bearer, Morten Bertelsen, who is to deliver it in person (\"this letter-bearer,\" <i>thenne breffuißer</i>) and then treat with Tycho directly. MS witness A is the autograph at Basel (Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 1).</li><li><b>Person recommended.</b> <i>Morttenn Bertilßønn</i> = <b>Morten Bertelsen</b>, the bearer, proposed as administrator of the Nordfjord fief; vouched for as having served nine years under the writer's father-in-law.</li><li><b>Person (referee).</b> <i>Michell Eskelßønn</i> = <b>Michel Eskelsen</b>, <i>fogitt y Halsnø Kloster</i> = <b>bailiff (Da.</b> <i>foged<b></i>) at Halsnøy Abbey</b>; the writer's wife's father, under whom Morten Bertelsen had served.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Halsnø Kloster / Halsnøy</i> = <b>Halsnøy Abbey</b> (Halsnøy kloster), the former Augustinian monastery on the island of Halsnøy in Sunnhordland, western Norway, by then a secularised crown estate administered by a bailiff. <i>Nordfiordt</i> = <b>Nordfjord</b>, a district and crown fief in western Norway (Bergenhus region). <i>Knudstrup</i> = <b>Knutstorp</b> (Knudstrup), the Brahe family seat in Scania. <i>Vranniborgh</i> = <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven.</li><li><b><i>fromhedt</b></i> — literally \"piety / probity\"; here, as a chancery courtesy-title for an addressee below royal rank, it functions as an honorific abstraction roughly equivalent to \"your worthiness / your good self.\" Rendered \"your worthiness\" throughout for consistency; it is <b>not</b> the religious term \"piety\" in this register.</li><li><b><i>welbyrd[ig]hedt / welbyrdighedt</b></i> — \"well-bornness,\" the courtesy-abstraction for a <i>welbyrdig</i> (well-born, noble) addressee; rendered \"your well-bornness.\" The first occurrence is contracted in the source (<i>welbyrdhedt</i>); the missing syllable is restored in brackets.</li><li><b><i>juncker</b></i> — \"squire / young lord\" (Ger. <i>Junker</i>), a respectful address for a nobleman; kept.</li><li><b><i>lehn / forlening / befalling</b></i> — <i>lehn</i> = <b>fief / fiefdom</b> (a crown estate held in administration); <i>forlening</i> = <b>enfeoffment</b> (the grant of such a fief); <i>befalling</i> = \"command / charge\" (here the office of administering the fief). The letter turns on Tycho's power to grant the Nordfjord fief to a deputy.</li><li><b><i>fogitt</b></i> — <b>bailiff</b> (Da. <i>foged</i>), the resident administrator of a crown estate.</li><li><b><i>skodtzmaall</b></i> — a <b>testimonial / character-reference</b> (Da. <i>skudsmål</i>), the formal vouching a servant could call upon from a former master; \"he may freely refer his testimonial back to him\" = Morten may appeal to Michel Eskelsen as a referee.</li><li><b>Negation / conditional logic (checked).</b> Three negated clauses carry the letter's force and are reproduced one-for-one: (1) <i>\"om ieg widste, att handt war <b>iche</b> throu och guodt, tha skulle ieg <b>jche</b> forschriffue hanom\"</i> — a double-negative conditional: \"if I knew he were <b>not</b> faithful and good, then I should <b>not</b> commend him\" (both negations preserved; the sense is a guarantee, not a doubt). (2) <i>\"y nest Gudtz hielp skall hanom <b>jche</b> andit skylde, end thet som erligt … skall werre\"</i> — \"you shall charge him with <b>nothing</b> other than what shall be honest\" (litotes of trust, kept). (3) <i>\"om saa er, att ethers fromhedt <b>iche</b> sielff hagde hans tienniste behouff\"</i> — the contrary-to-expectation conditional governing the closing plea: \"if it be so that your worthiness yourself had <b>no</b> need of his service, [then] you would … be helpful to him [in placing him] with one of your good friends.\" The mood of <i>hagde</i> (past subjunctive) is hypothetical/polite; rendered \"had no need\" as a courteous conditional, not as an assertion that Tycho lacks the need.</li><li><b>Emphatic <i>io</i>.</b> <i>\"skall <b>io</b> bliffue och werre … huldt och trou\"</i> — the particle <i>io</i> (≈ \"indeed, assuredly\") intensifies the assurance; rendered \"shall indeed remain and be loyal and faithful.\"</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1596, German-influenced).</b> Heavy <i>-ff-</i> for <i>-v-</i> (<i>haffuer</i> = <i>haver</i>, <i>breffue</i> = <i>breve</i>, <i>giffuer</i> = <i>giver</i>), <i>w-</i> for <i>v-</i> (<i>Wor</i> = <i>Vor</i>, <i>will</i> = <i>vil</i>, <i>widt</i> = <i>ved</i>), <i>th-</i> (<i>thacker</i> = <i>takker</i>, <i>thill</i> = <i>til</i>), <i>sch-/-ß-</i> (<i>tillschreffuitt</i>, <i>helsßenn</i>, <i>schriffuelße</i>), <i>attj</i> = <i>at I</i> (\"that you,\" plural-polite), <i>denom/hanom</i> = <i>dennem/hannem</i> (\"them/him\"), <i>io</i> = <i>jo</i>, <i>vdj</i> = <i>udi</i> (\"in/at\"). The orthography is German-coloured chancery Danish — the kind that misleads automatic language detection (the heuristic tagged it <i>da</i>, correctly here).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Schreffuit vdj Halsnø closter then 2 aprillis anno 96</i> = \"Written at <b>Halsnøy Abbey</b>, the 2nd of April, the year [15]96\" (O.S.; Denmark-Norway kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline date (2 April 1596) agrees with the catalogue label; no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the English's reversed subject/object: Mads made Tycho's own wish known to the letter-bearer Morten Bertelsen, not the bearer's mind known to Tycho (Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.98).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Danish is rendered securely and the negation-logic is reproduced one-for-one. Minor residual uncertainties, flagged rather than smoothed: the precise idiomatic weight of <i>skodtzmaall</i> (\"testimonial / right to refer back to a former master\"), and the closing conditional's polite past-subjunctive mood (<i>om … iche sielff hagde behouff</i>), which is read as a courteous hypothetical. The sender's surname <i>Huus</i> is editorial (from the heading), not in the letter's own subscription.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Danish secure, negation-logic one-for-one, date + MS witness agree; only minor idiom/register notes.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp. 97-98 via the page images, including the italic Latin argument on p. 97 (\"Matthias Georgii e monasterio Halsnö... commendat... Martinum Bartholini\") confirming sender and purpose, and the closing/signature (Matzs Jørgennsßønn, 2 April [15]96). Source_text matches Dreyer exactly -- no TEXT_ERROR. One MISTRANSLATION found: the clause reporting who informed whom of what has its direction of communication reversed (bearer-informs-Tycho instead of Mads-informs-bearer-of-Tycho's-wish). This changes what a scholar would take away from that sentence, so despite the letter's overall recommendation of Morten Bertelsen surviving intact, the grading rule's OMISSION/MISTRANSLATION-that-changes-the-sense clause applies. Downgraded from the self-assigned A.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The Danish syntax \"giffue nogen noget tilkiennde\" = \"make something known TO someone\": \"thenne breffuißer\" (this letter-bearer) is the dative recipient, and \"ethers welbyrdighedtz willie\" (your well-bornness's will/wish, genitive-marked) is the thing made known. The direction is reversed in translation: Mads is telling the letter-bearer (Morten Bertelsen) what TYCHO's wish is, not reporting the bearer's mind to Tycho. The published English swaps subject and object, attributing the possessive \"willie\" to the bearer instead of to Tycho and inverting who informs whom. At: “wherefore I have made this letter-bearer's mind [willie] known to your well-bornness”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.97-99; the sentence appears on p.98, lines 8-11, reading \"...huorfor ieg haffuer giffuit thenne breffuißer ethers welbyrdighedtz willie tilkiennde,\" matching current_latin exactly. The Danish dative construction confirms the audit's reading and the English has been corrected accordingly. No other defects found on these three pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 1."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that Tycho's letters were safely delivered and presents a candidate to manage the fief and command of Nordfjord on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15960402001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15961231001", "d": "1596-12-31", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian Friis", "ck": "Christian Friis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hven", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christian Friis — Uraniborg, 31 December 1596 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DIGNITATE, PRUDENTIA ET DOCTRINA INPRIMIS CONSPICUO, DOMINO CHRISTIANO FRISIO DE BORREBY, SERENISSIMI REGIS NOSTRI CANCELLARIO ET A CONSILIJS INTIMO, AMICO SUO HONORANDO.</p><p>CUM ad dignitatem praecipuam in hoc regno Daniae evectus sis, magnifice domine cancellarie, amice honorande, eqvidem ex te ipso absqve ullo monitore intelligere potes, quae decus regni concernunt, tuarum esse partium hortatu et consilijs promovere. Qvod in disciplinarum liberalium liberali etiam sustentatione et conservatione non minima ex parte consistere pro tua prudentia non ignoras, praesertim cum ipsemet doctrinis humanioribus praeclare imbutus sis. Astronomia autem inter omnes scientias locum principem merito obtinens, in qvantum et regnis et regibus gloriosa semper exstiterit, multae rationes multaqve exempla attestantur, ut ob id a Manilio poeta, Augusto imperatori scribente, hoc elogio depraedicetur, qvod</p><p>Regales animos primum dignata movere est<br>Proxima tangentes caelo fastigia rerum.</p><p>Hanc igitur tam sublimem artem ex decreto et clementi voluntate Friderici IIdi, laudatissimae memoriae regis in hac insula Huenna summis viribus excolere adeoqve redintegrare jamdudum agressus sum, ingentibus ausis et laboribus, neqve sumptibus mediocribus, omnes nervos eo intendens, ut in honorem patriae illa illustrior et absolutior quam uspiam antea prodiret. Qva in re quid hactenus non saltem qvoad instrumentorum idoneorum, qvalia forte alibi nullo aevo extiterunt, et aedificiorum huc spectantium exstructione, sed et in observationibus qvam plurimis, nulla fere serenitate omissa, coelitus deducendis atque ex his restituendis astrorum motibus effecerimus, judicent alij, qvotqvot Evropa habet vel vnqvam habitura est horum intelligentes et cupidos. Qvos confido non illubenter concessuros me omnes stellas fixas, visui commode patentes, tanta subtilitate et certitudine restituisse, qvanta a nullis veterum aut recentiorum effectum est, res sane ardua multisqve seculis frustra desiderata, sed et solis lunaeque curricula in normam caelo ad amussim consonam a nobis esse redacta, omnium aetatum subsecuturae experientur animadversiones. Qvin et in reliqvis planetis (qva in parte adhuc sedulo laboramus, circuituum tarditate in nonnullis cunctationem exigente) id ipsum exeqvi indies magnis conatibus enitimur; hypothesi jam antea scienter excogitata, quae antecedentium omnium astronomorum speculationes, fatentibus id hujus seculi artificibus praecipuis, eludit. Constituerat vero dictus rex serenissimus, artis hujus regiae culturam in hac insula perpetuare et fundatione liberali ejus ad posteros conservationi prospicere. Verum nimis immature (proh dolor) nobis ereptus, id ante supremum diem exeqvi non potuit. Retuli itaqve de eo statim post ipsius obitum ad regni senatum, attestans etiam serenissimam reginam et quosdam e senatoribus conscios, qvi illud ex rege, cum in vivis esset, inaudivissent. Qvare et universus senatus mox non inuite, literis in membrana exaratis (qvarum hic exemplar adjunxi) regium illud propositum confirmavit, et se, cum princeps administrationi rerum admoveretur, omni conatu et svasu elaboraturum, ut is artis hujus perpetuationi in hac insula clementer et liberaliter provideret, addixit. Qvin et tunc feudum qvoddam in Norvagia mihi concessum est, et alias meae indemnitati, qvantum ea tempora patiebantur, consultum, spe facta, qvod in posterum omnia ex voto uberius evenirent. Praeter omnem nihilominus meam expectationem accidit, quod statim ac serenissimus noster rex coronatus est, feudo illo citra ullam (uti confido) meam culpam privatus sim, adeo ut potissima portio eorum, qvae astronomicae restaurationis subsidia spectabant, mihi subtracta sit, non mediocri meo incommodo. Rem enim familiarem (omnia qvae tum e regijs feudis tum bonis haereditarijs acquirere licuit, in hoc opus expendendo) hactenus potius imminuatam quam auctam, nimis vere expertus sum, cum alijs hujus regni nobilibus, meis coaequalibus, contra evenerit, qvi et propria conservarunt et insuper e regijs rem fecerunt. Accedit et hoc, qvod his primordijs, ab artis hujus sustentatione adeo aversis, dubius reddar, qvid de ejus in hoc regno promotione et conservatione in posterum sperandum sit; anne illius exercitatio prorsus deserenda veniat, an vero alia ejus continuandae media sint qvaerenda, qvorum hoc fortassis patriae nostrae minus honorificum, illud certe exteri aegerrime paterentur, qvi vndeqvaque quotannis ad me datis literis serio hortantur, ut inchoatam telam pertexere non omittam, sed confectam orbi aliqvando communicem, qvod non mediocris copia literarum ex Italia, Gallia, Germania, Bohemia, Anglia et Scotia alijsqve regionibus profecta et apud me reservata ostendet. Qvin et ipsemet, qvi potiorem in his aetatis partem consumpsi, tot annorum exantlatos labores, tot vigilatas noctes tantosqve erogatos sumptus incassum factos reddi admodum invite ferrem. Qvin potius ne id fieret utqve expectationi regum qvorundam, principum atqve praestantissimorum hinc inde virorum satisfaciam, nec multis spem conceptam laudabilem illaudabiliter frustremur, omnino rationes ineundae, quibus id, quod semel bene inchoatum est, totqve annis continuatum, aliqvando in publicam orbis utilitatem absolvatur, ubicunqve tandem locorum et qvibuscunqve medijs id commode exseqvi liceat. Qvae cum ita se habeant et necessario nobis facienda sint, in tuum sinum deponenda, tibiqve hisce literis aliqvatenus ea exponenda duximus, qvo autoritate atqve prudentia, qva vales, huic incommodo subvenire et mature obviare velis, qvod etiam te libere et lubenter facturum mihi persvadeo siqvidem non difficulter praevideas, qvam honorifica huic regno artis tam sublimis conservatio existat, qvamqve minus apud alias nationes decorum eidem foret, si ejus instaurationem tam diu tantoqve molimine hic factitatam, cujus fama plurimas Europae regiones jam penetravit, aut negligi aut aliorsum transferri necessum fuerit. Ut vero aliqvatenus perspicias, qvaenam in hoc regno ad astronomiae redintegrationem indefesso studio hactenus molitus sim, habes hic primum tomum epistolarum astronomicarum e nostris, qvi suo loco brevem instrumentorum a me ejus artis gratia elaboratorum descriptionem, vna cum aedificijs huc pertinentibus exhibet, adjunctis in fine qvorundam ex ijsdem organis jconibus, qvibus introspectis, utcunqve tibi constabit, arbitror, me non obiter, sed summis viribus imo etiam supra ipsas vires tanta perfecisse. Qvod etiam tibi ipsi, si aliqvando mihi in hac insula adesse haud gravatus fueris, opinione verius aestimabitur. Oblatus qvidem est idem liber una cum jconibus serenissimo regi meo nomine submisse, anteqvam Hafnia ultimo discederet, sed cum tunc in itineris Cimbrici praecinctu esset, neqve ad ea serio attendere, neqve de ijs, qvae proponebantur, qvidpiam certi statuere ipsius majestati commodum erat. Rogo itaqve officiose, magnifice domine cancellarie, ut patriae saltem causa hoc tam arduum negotium tibi cordi esse sinas, et de ijs, quae artis adeo eximiae apud nos conservationem et propagationem concernunt, serio cogites, regiqve nostro serenissimo opportunum in his des consilium. Hoc autem tempore nihil aliud expeto, qvam ut feudum in Norvagia mihi ademptum clementer restituatur, si non diutius, saltem in diem calend. maij, qvo feudorum apud nos commutatio fieri soleat, ut praefectus meus tunc mihi rationes reddituum (qvos alias nullos toto hoc anno inde accepi) reddere qveat. Interea serenissimus rex et secum et ex prudentissimi senatus consilio deliberatius statuat, qvid in posterum circa haec fieri velit, cujus ego regiae voluntati, in qvamcunqve partem res vergat, lubens acqviescam. Porro te etiam obnixe rogatum habeo, ut per hunc meum ministrum nonnulla mihi de antedictis rescribere, tuamqve sententiam aperire ne recusare velis, qvo mature, qvid facto opus sit, mecum statuere qveam. Exspectabit istic, donec id tibi per alias occupationes opportunum fuerit. Ego vicissim de te pro virili bene mereri, tuoqve honori et nomini officiose studere non intermittam. Vale, atqve hanc in scribendo prolixitatem, re ita exigente, candide et benevole accipe.</p><p>Iterum cum tota familia felix et incolumis vale, annumqve hunc ut auspicato ingrediaris absolvasqve, opto.<br>Dabantur Uraniburgo pridie calendarum januarij anni 1597.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, conspicuous above all for rank, prudence, and learning, Master Christian Friis of Borreby, Chancellor and Privy Councillor of our most serene King, his friend to be honoured.</b></p><p>Since you have been raised to a foremost dignity in this kingdom of Denmark, magnificent lord chancellor, honoured friend, you can of course understand from your own self, without any prompter, that it falls to your part to advance by your exhortation and counsel those things which concern the honour of the realm. And in your prudence you are not unaware that this consists, in no small part, in the liberal maintenance and preservation of the liberal disciplines — especially since you yourself are excellently steeped in the more humane studies. Now astronomy, deservedly holding the chief place among all the sciences, how glorious it has always proved both to kingdoms and to kings, many reasons and many examples attest; so that on this account it is celebrated by the poet Manilius, writing to the emperor Augustus, with this encomium — that</p><p>It first deigned to move royal minds,<br>those that touch the summits of affairs nearest to the sky.</p><p>This art, then, so sublime, I long ago undertook, by the decree and gracious will of Frederick II, king of most laudable memory, to cultivate and indeed to renew on this island of Hven with my utmost strength — with vast ventures and labours, and with no moderate expense, straining every sinew to this end, that for the honour of my homeland it might come forth more illustrious and more complete than anywhere before. As to what I have accomplished therein hitherto — not only in the construction of suitable instruments (such as perhaps existed in no age elsewhere) and of the buildings pertaining to this work, but also in very many observations to be drawn down from the heavens, scarcely any clear sky being let slip, and in the restoration of the motions of the stars from these — let others judge, as many as Europe has, or ever will have, who understand and desire these matters. I am confident they will not unwillingly grant that I have restored all the fixed stars conveniently open to the sight with such precision and certainty as has been achieved by none of the ancients or moderns — an arduous matter indeed, and longed for in vain through many ages — and that the courses of the Sun and Moon too have been reduced by us to a rule exactly consonant with the heavens, as the observations of every age to follow will find. Moreover, in the remaining planets (in which part we still diligently labour, the slowness of the circuits in some of them demanding delay) we strive to carry out the very same thing day by day with great efforts; with a hypothesis already devised with full knowledge beforehand, which — as the foremost practitioners of this age confess — eludes the speculations of all preceding astronomers. Now the said most serene king had resolved to perpetuate the cultivation of this royal art on this island and, by a liberal endowment, to provide for its preservation to posterity. But, snatched from us all too untimely (alas!), he could not carry this out before his last day. I therefore reported the matter, immediately after his death, to the council of the realm, calling to witness also the most serene queen and certain of the councillors who were privy to it, who had heard it from the king while he was still living. Wherefore the whole council too, not unwillingly, soon confirmed that royal intention by letters engrossed on parchment (a copy of which I have here attached), and pledged that, when the prince should be brought to the administration of affairs, it would labour with every effort and persuasion that he should graciously and liberally provide for the perpetuation of this art on this island. And at that time, too, a certain fief in Norway was granted to me, and otherwise provision was made for my indemnity, so far as those times allowed, the hope being given that in future all things would turn out more abundantly according to my wish. Nevertheless, contrary to all my expectation it happened that, as soon as our most serene king was crowned, I was deprived of that fief through no fault of mine (as I trust), so that the most important portion of those things which served as supports for the restoration of astronomy has been withdrawn from me, to my no slight disadvantage. For I have learned all too truly that my estate (in spending upon this work everything that I was able to acquire both from the royal fiefs and from my hereditary goods) has hitherto been diminished rather than increased — whereas the contrary has befallen other nobles of this realm, my equals, who have both preserved their own and, besides, made their fortune from the king's. There is added this also: that from these first beginnings, so averse to the maintenance of this art, I am rendered doubtful what is to be hoped, in future, concerning its promotion and preservation in this kingdom — whether the practice of it must be abandoned altogether, or whether other means of continuing it must be sought; of which the former would perhaps be less honourable to our homeland, while the latter foreigners would certainly bear most grievously, who from every quarter, year by year, in letters sent to me earnestly exhort me not to leave off weaving to its end the web I have begun, but to share it, when finished, with the world at last — as a no slight store of letters, dispatched from Italy, France, Germany, Bohemia, England, and Scotland and other regions and kept by me, will show. And I myself, too, who have spent the better part of my life on these things, should very unwillingly bear that the labours endured through so many years, so many wakeful nights, and so great expenditures laid out, should be rendered made-in-vain. Rather, that this should not happen, and that I may satisfy the expectation of certain kings, princes, and most excellent men here and there, nor frustrate without praise the laudable hope conceived by many, means must by all means be entered upon whereby that which has once been well begun, and continued through so many years, may at last be brought to completion for the public benefit of the world — wherever in the end, and by whatever means, it may conveniently be carried out. Since these things are so, and must of necessity be done by us, I have judged that they should be laid in your bosom, and to some extent set out to you in this letter, that by the authority and prudence in which you are strong you may be willing to come to the aid of this difficulty and to forestall it in good time — which I persuade myself you will also do freely and gladly, since you may without difficulty foresee how honourable to this kingdom is the preservation of so sublime an art, and how less than seemly it would be for it in the eyes of other nations, if the renewal of it, so long and with such great effort wrought here — whose fame has already penetrated very many regions of Europe — should have to be either neglected or transferred elsewhere. But that you may to some extent perceive what I have hitherto, with untiring zeal, set in motion in this kingdom toward the renewal of astronomy, you have here the first volume of astronomical letters from among ours, which in its place presents a brief description of the instruments wrought by me for the sake of that art, together with the buildings pertaining hereto, with the figures of certain of those same instruments appended at the end; on inspecting which, I think it will be in some measure evident to you that I have accomplished such great things not in passing, but with my utmost strength — nay, even beyond those very strengths. Which will also be more truly estimated by you yourself, if you should at some time not be loath to be present with me on this island. The same book, together with the figures, was indeed offered submissively in my name to the most serene king, before he last departed from Copenhagen; but since he was then on the eve of the Cimbrian journey, it was convenient for his majesty neither to attend seriously to these matters, nor to settle anything certain concerning the things that were proposed. I therefore dutifully ask, magnificent lord chancellor, that for the homeland's sake at least you may let this so arduous business be at heart to you, and that you may seriously consider those things which concern the preservation and propagation of so excellent an art among us, and may give our most serene king timely counsel in these matters. At this time, however, I seek nothing else than that the fief taken from me in Norway be graciously restored — if not for longer, at least until the first of May, the day on which the changing of fiefs is customarily done among us — so that my steward may then be able to render me the accounts of the revenues (of which I have otherwise received none from there in this whole year). Meanwhile let the most serene king, both with himself and from the counsel of his most prudent council, determine more deliberately what he would wish to be done about these matters in future; in whose royal will, to whichever side the matter incline, I shall gladly acquiesce. Further, I also hold you earnestly asked that, through this my servant, you not be unwilling to write back to me something about the aforesaid matters and to disclose your opinion, so that I may in good time determine with you what needs to be done. He will wait there until it shall be convenient for you, amid your other occupations. I, in turn, shall not cease to deserve well of you to the best of my ability, and dutifully to study your honour and name. Farewell, and take this prolixity in writing — the matter so demanding — candidly and kindly.</p><p>Again, fare you well, happy and unharmed, together with your whole household; and I wish that you may both enter upon and complete this year under good auspices.<br>Given at Uraniborg, the day before the Kalends of January [31 December], of the year 1597.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe to <b>Christian Friis of Borreby</b> (Christian Friis til Borreby, 1556–1616), appointed Chancellor of the Realm (<i>kongens kansler</i> / <i>Danmarks Riges kansler</i>) by the young Christian IV soon after the king's coronation (29 August 1596), succeeding the late Niels Kaas. Corpus background: <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (Manderup Parsberg \"held authority as royal chancellor but was about to hand it to Christian Friis of Borreby\"); Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (\"He had appointed Christian Friis of Borreby, Chancellor …\"). The letter is Tycho's formal appeal to the new chancellor over the loss of his Norwegian fief and the imperilled royal support for his work — a key document of the crisis that ended in his 1597 departure from Denmark.</li><li><b>Place / date.</b> <i>Dabantur Uraniburgo pridie calendarum januarij anni 1597</i> = \"Given at <b>Uraniborg</b> [on Hven], the day before the Kalends of January, of the year 1597.\" The day before the Kalends of January is <b>31 December</b>; reckoned to the <i>coming</i> year's Kalends it is the last day of <b>1596</b> (Old Style — Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The header therefore reads <b>31 December 1596 [O.S.]</b>; the dataset slug agrees (15961231). The Roman idiom is kept and glossed per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Manilius verse.</b> <i>Regales animos primum dignata movere est / Proxima tangentes caelo fastigia rerum</i> — Manilius, <i>Astronomica</i> I.41–42 (the proem, on how the heavenly art first stirred the minds of kings, who hold the summits of affairs nearest the sky). Tycho ascribes it to \"Manilius … writing to the emperor Augustus\" (the <i>Astronomica</i> was composed under Augustus/Tiberius). Kept as verse per protocol §4.</li><li><b>The enclosed book.</b> <i>primum tomum epistolarum astronomicarum e nostris … adjunctis in fine qvorundam ex ijsdem organis jconibus</i> = \"the first volume of astronomical letters from among ours … with figures (<i>icones</i>) of certain of the instruments appended at the end\" — Tycho's own <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I, Uraniborg 1596), whose appended <i>icones</i> depict his instruments. <i>organis</i> rendered \"instruments\" and <i>icones</i> \"figures\" per glossary §5.2.</li><li><b>Astronomical claims.</b> <i>redintegrare / restituendis astrorum motibus / astronomicae restaurationis / instaurationem</i> — Tycho's <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> programme: rendered \"renew / restore the motions of the stars / restoration of astronomy / renewal\" per glossary §5.1. <i>omnes stellas fixas … restituisse</i> = the fixed-star catalogue; <i>solis lunaeque curricula in normam … redacta</i> = the solar and lunar theories reduced to a rule consonant with observation. <i>hypothesi jam antea scienter excogitata, quae antecedentium omnium astronomorum speculationes … eludit</i> = the Tychonic world-system (\"a hypothesis … which eludes the speculations of all preceding astronomers\"). The author's hedges are preserved: the fixed stars are restored <i>visui commode patentes</i> (\"conveniently open to the sight\"), the planets are still in progress (<i>adhuc sedulo laboramus</i>), and the chancellor's good will is hoped (<i>uti confido</i>, \"as I trust\"; <i>mihi persvadeo</i>, \"I persuade myself\"), not asserted.</li><li><b>The fief and the politics.</b> <i>feudum qvoddam in Norvagia</i> = the Norwegian fief (Nordfjord) granted under Frederik II; <i>coronatus est … feudo illo … privatus sim</i> = Tycho's deprivation of it upon Christian IV's coronation (1596). <i>literis in membrana exaratis</i> = the council's confirmatory letters \"engrossed on parchment.\" <i>itineris Cimbrici</i> = \"the Cimbrian journey,\" i.e. the king's progress through Jutland/the Cimbrian peninsula (the <i>Cimbri</i> being the classicising name for the Jutes). <i>calend. maij</i> = \"the Kalends of May\" = <b>1 May</b>, the customary fief-transfer term; kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Negations / conditional force (checked).</b> The conditional appeal <i>si non diutius, saltem in diem calend. maij</i> is rendered exactly: \"if not for longer, at least until the first of May\" — a minimum request, not a renunciation. <i>citra ullam … meam culpam</i> = \"through no fault of mine\"; <i>non mediocri meo incommodo</i> / <i>neqve sumptibus mediocribus</i> = litotes (\"no slight … / no moderate …\"), preserved. The double-negative <i>nec multis spem conceptam laudabilem illaudabiliter frustremur</i> (\"nor frustrate without praise the laudable hope …\") is reproduced with both negatives intact.</li><li><b>Honorifics / formulae.</b> <i>Magnifico … domino … amico suo honorando</i> / <i>magnifice domine cancellarie, amice honorande</i> — the magnate-and-councillor register, rendered \"magnificent lord chancellor, honoured friend\" (cf. glossary §5.5). <i>Vale … Iterum … vale</i> = \"Farewell … Again, fare you well\" (§5.5). <i>Dabantur</i> = \"Given\" (the issuing formula). <i>proh dolor</i> = \"alas!\" kept as an interjection.</li><li><b>Editorial note.</b> Source spellings are as transcribed in the Dreyer base (<i>qv-</i> for <i>qu-</i>, <i>-ij</i> for <i>-ii</i>, <i>Evropa</i>, <i>Huenna</i>, <i>jconibus</i>); reproduced verbatim in the Source block, normalised silently only in the translation. No competing reading affects the sense; \"[15]97 → 31 Dec 1596\" is the only interpretive point, handled in the calendar note above.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The single dating point (the Roman-calendar dateline <i>pridie calendarum januarij anni 1597</i> = 31 December 1596 O.S.) is explained rather than silently converted, and the dataset slug independently confirms it; no genuine crux remains.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 Dreyer page images (TBOO XIV pp. 98-102, the full letter Tycho-to-Christian Friis 31 Dec 1596) in two chunks (1-4, 5); page 102 confirmed the letter ends cleanly at 'Dabantur Uraniburgo pridie calendarum januarij anni 1597' and that the following material belongs to the next entry (no. 115, Friis's reply). Collated the full heading titulature, the Manilius couplet, and every proper name, number and date against the page images: Frederick II, the Norway fief, the six correspondent countries (Italia, Gallia, Germania, Bohemia, Anglia, Scotia), 'in diem calend. maij' (1 May), and the closing date 'pridie calendarum januarij anni 1597' (31 December, O.S. 1596). Source_text and published_english both match Dreyer's printed text exactly (modulo normal long-s/ligature typography); no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss or text divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Transscript"], "th": {"label": "Christian Friis", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15970120001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970120001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Argues that it falls to the new chancellor to support astronomy and the liberal disciplines, citing Manilius on the art's glory for kingdoms and kings.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15961231001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970000001", "d": "1597-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Claus Mule", "ck": "Claus Mule", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Claus Mule — [no place], [1597?, undated] [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter, Nr. 178 (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CAVSAe DEMIGRATIONIS D:NI TYCHONIS BRAHE EX PATRIA DANIA BREVITER AB IPSO AD PASCOLIUM MULAeUM EXPOSITAe.</p><p>VIDETUR Cassolius in ultimis ad me discessus mei ex Dania cavsas non satis ponderasse, forte qvod illi minus essent perspectae, dum ait non nisi aliqvam proventuum jacturam me fecisse, idqve etiam aliis mei ordinis evenisse, et talium solere miram esse vicissitudinem, qvod a te et Bagone illo accepisse se insinuat. Atqve si haec a te prolata sunt, aut meae e patria migrationis occasiones non penitus te cognovisse [?credo], aut eas patriae et nostratium cavsa politice et cavte subtices et dissimulas. Sunt qvidem ab aliis nobilibus reditus qvidam subtracti, qvi eos in privatum commodum vertebant, at a me omnes, adeo ut ne qvidem a praebenda mea Roschildensi vindicanda sibi, qvod alias insolitum est, abstinuerit cancellarius, qvi potius ea, qvae tracto, studia, juxta senatusconsulti rationem promovere debuisset. Ego vero, uti multis constat, non saltem eos, qvos a rege habui, sed et qvoscunqve de bonis haereditariis acqvirere licuit, sumptus in astronomicae artis instavrationem, qvo sic etiam patriae honori studerem, impendi. Verum tolerabilis fuisset haec utut gravis jactura, si non disertis verbis ad me scripsisset cancellarius, regi non esse integrum in astronomicorum instrumentorum conservationem qvicqvam impendere. Sicqve omnium senatorum literae aliud spondentes, exavtoratae, quas statim ab obitu laudat. mem. regis Friderici nostri in membrana exaratas, et singulorum manibus et sigillis munitas concesserunt, me vivente et vidente totus ille apparatus astronomicus, qvem ex clementi voluntate nunqvam satis lavdati regis istic institueram, interire debuerit, ne dum ut ad posteros in perpetuum regni honorem, juxta decretum tam dicti regis, qvam regni senatorum continuaret in perpetuum. Qvin nec in propriis qvidem aedibus Hafniae, propriis etiam sumptibus haec exercitia tractare licuit, interdicente id, sub nomine hujus, qvi nunc est, regis, licet tum in Germania absentis, aulae magistro, isqve per urbis praetorem. Ut taceam qvod alias contra me praeter meritum multi machinati sunt, ut praeteream qvae pastori meo odio mei obtigerunt. Nam de incitatis in me reprobis istis insularibus deqve jure in iisdem coercendis inhibito deniqve de iniqvis consultationibus multifariis super me habitis. Qvae omnia referre nunc piget.</p><p>Haec ex m[anu]s[cripto] d:ni Longomontani.</p>", "en": "<p><b>The causes of the departure of Master Tycho Brahe from his fatherland Denmark, briefly set out by himself to Pascolius Mulaeus.</b></p><p>It seems that Cassolius, in his last [letter] to me, did not sufficiently weigh the causes of my withdrawal from Denmark — perhaps because they were less clearly perceived by him — when he says that I have suffered no more than some loss of revenues, and that this has befallen others of my rank too, and that the vicissitude of such things is wont to be remarkable: which he hints he had heard from you and from that Bago. And if these things were put forward by you, [then I believe] that either you did not fully know the occasions of my emigration from the fatherland, or that, for the sake of the fatherland and of our countrymen, you are politicly and cautiously passing them over in silence and dissembling them. Certain revenues, to be sure, were withdrawn from other noblemen who were turning them to private advantage; but from me [were withdrawn] all — to such a degree that the chancellor did not even refrain from claiming for himself my prebend of Roskilde (a thing otherwise unheard of), he who ought rather to have advanced, in accordance with the resolution of the council, those studies which I pursue. But I, as is plain to many, expended not only those [revenues] which I had from the king, but also whatever I was able to acquire from my hereditary estates, upon the restoration of the astronomical art, that I might thus serve the honour of the fatherland as well. Yet this loss, however grave, would have been tolerable, had not the chancellor written to me in express words that the king was not free to expend anything upon the upkeep of the astronomical instruments. And so, the letters of all the senators promising otherwise being annulled — [the letters] which, immediately after the death of our King Frederick of praiseworthy memory, they granted, engrossed on parchment and secured with the hands and seals of each — [it came to pass that,] while I lived and looked on, that whole astronomical apparatus, which by the gracious will of the never-sufficiently-praised king I had set up there, had to perish — far from continuing in perpetuity for posterity, for the perpetual honour of the kingdom, according to the decree both of the said king and of the senators of the kingdom. Nay, I was not even permitted to carry on these exercises in my own house at Copenhagen, at my own expense too, the master of the court forbidding it in the name of the present king (though he was then absent in Germany) — and that through the city's magistrate. To say nothing of how, in other ways, many have plotted against me beyond my deserts; to pass over what befell my pastor through hatred of me. For [I say nothing] of those reprobate islanders stirred up against me, and of the right of coercing them, which was [then] forbidden me, and finally of the manifold iniquitous deliberations held about me. To recount all which now is irksome.</p><p>This [is] from the manuscript of Master Longomontanus.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> Not an ordinary letter but a third-person rubric over a first-person statement: a brief apologia by Tycho on the causes of his 1597 departure from Denmark, addressed to <i>Pascolius Mulaeus</i>, surviving only as a copy. The transmission note at the foot — <i>Haec ex manuscripto domini Longomontani</i> — states the text was taken from the manuscript of <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (Christian Severin Longberg, 1562–1647), Tycho's senior assistant; the witness is an <i>afskrift</i> (copy) in <b>Langebek's Excerpts, Nr. 178</b> at the Royal Library, Copenhagen.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Pascolius Mulaeus</i> / <i>Mulaeum</i> = <b>Claus Mule</b> (Latinised), a member of Tycho's household who accompanied him into exile (he travelled with Longomontanus to Helmstedt and on to Wittenberg in 1597–98). The dataset records the recipient as Claus Mule; the praenomen <i>Pascolius</i> (vs. expected <i>Nicolaus/Claus</i>) is the form given by the source and is <b>flagged</b> below.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), speaking in the first person (<i>ad me … me fecisse … impendi</i>) though named in the third person in the rubric.</li><li><b><i>Calidius</b></i> (so Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 137; the working transcription reads <i>Cassolius</i> — most likely an OCR misreading of <i>Cali-</i>) — an unidentified correspondent of Tycho's whose \"last [letter]\" prompted this rebuttal; <b>not securely identifiable</b> under either spelling. The recipient is securely <b>Claus (Claudius) Mule</b> — Dreyer's own heading glosses <i>Pascolium (? Claudium) Mulaeum</i>, the apograph's <i>Pascolius</i> being a corruption of <i>Claudium</i>. The bracketed <i>[?credo]</i> is <b>Dreyer's own editorial supplement</b>, reproduced here, not a conjecture introduced here.</li><li><b><i>Bago illo</b></i> — \"that Bago\": <b>Bagge / Bago</b>, a man named alongside the recipient as a source of the downplaying report; rendered \"that Bago,\" the named individual left as given (likely a member of the Danish noble/Hven circle). Not further identified here.</li><li><b><i>cancellarius</b></i> — \"the chancellor\": <b>Christian Friis</b> (of Borreby), Chancellor of Denmark, who (per Dreyer) was granted Tycho's Roskilde prebend on 10 June 1597 immediately after Tycho's departure. The text does not name him; identification supplied in brackets in this note only, not in the translation.</li><li><b><i>praebenda mea Roschildensi</b></i> — \"my prebend of Roskilde\": the canonry of Roskilde cathedral, with which Tycho's incomes had been associated and which he lost in June 1597.</li><li><b><i>laudat. mem. regis Friderici nostri</b></i> — <i>laudatae memoriae regis Friderici</i> = \"of our King Frederick of praiseworthy memory\": <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 1588), Tycho's patron, who had granted Hven and the supporting endowments; the council's parchment guarantees were issued at his death.</li><li><b><i>hujus, qvi nunc est, regis … tum in Germania absentis</b></i> — \"the present king … then absent in Germany\": <b>Christian IV</b> (r. 1588–1648), who undertook a journey in the 1590s; under his name (in his absence) Tycho was forbidden to practise even privately in Copenhagen.</li><li><b><i>aulae magistro … per urbis praetorem</b></i> — <i>aulae magister</i> = \"the master of the court\" (court marshal / Hofmeister); <i>urbis praetor</i> = \"the city's magistrate\" (Copenhagen's <i>byfoged</i> / town official) through whom the prohibition was conveyed. Both rendered descriptively; neither named in the source.</li><li><b><i>pastori meo</b></i> — \"my pastor\": the parish priest of Hven, <b>Jens Jensen Wensøsil</b>, whose troubles Tycho here attributes to \"hatred of me\"; not named in the source.</li><li><b><i>reprobis istis insularibus</b></i> — \"those reprobate islanders\": the peasants/tenants of Hven, with whom Tycho had jurisdictional disputes; <i>jure in iisdem coercendis inhibito</i> = \"the right of coercing them [having been] forbidden [me],\" i.e. his disciplinary jurisdiction over the islanders was curtailed.</li><li><b><i>astronomicae artis instavrationem</b></i> — \"the restoration of the astronomical art\": Tycho's programme of <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> (protocol §5.1, <i>instauratio → restoration/renewal</i>); the word is his own self-description of his life's work.</li><li><b><i>astronomicorum instrumentorum conservationem … apparatus astronomicus</b></i> — \"the upkeep of the astronomical instruments … that whole astronomical apparatus\": Tycho's instruments (protocol §5.2, <i>organa/instrumenta → instruments</i>) at Uraniborg/Stjerneborg, whose state maintenance the chancellor declared the king was not free to fund.</li><li><b><i>senatusconsulti / senatorum / regni senatorum</b></i> — <i>senatus</i> here = the Danish <b>Rigsråd</b> (Council of the Realm); <i>senatusconsultum</i> = \"the resolution of the council,\" <i>senatores</i> = the councillors who sealed the parchment guarantees. Rendered \"council / senators\" consistently.</li><li><b>Editorial readings.</b> The source-of-record carries an editorial query at <i>non penitus te cognovisse [?credo]</i> — the apodosis verb (here <i>credo</i>, \"I believe\") is supplied/uncertain; kept in brackets in both Source and translation, the conjecture flagged. <i>laudat. mem.</i> and <i>m[anu]s[cripto]</i> are abbreviations expanded in brackets.</li><li><b>Calendar / dating.</b> The piece is <b>undated</b> in the source; the dataset label is <i>\"1597? empty\"</i> (a conjectural year, no day). The content — loss of the Roskilde prebend, the prohibition in Christian IV's absence, the dismantling of the instruments \"while I lived and looked on\" — fits <b>1597–98</b>, after the June 1597 departure. The header therefore carries no asserted dateline; the conjectural <i>[1597?]</i> is given in brackets, O.S. (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). <b>No date is converted or corrected.</b></li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Editorial/early-modern Latin spellings preserved in the Source block: <i>cavsae, cavte, qvod, qvi, instavrationem, lavdati, acqvirere, qvicqvam, deniqve</i> (u for v / v for u, <i>qv</i> for <i>qu</i>); <i>Roschildensi</i> (Roskilde); <i>Hafniae</i> = Copenhagen (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed a fabricated collation note that asserted Dreyer prints \"Calidius\"; Dreyer's page (TBOO XIV p.123) in fact reads \"Caffolius\" (=Cassolius), identical to the working transcription -- there is no textual variant here.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin body is rendered soundly. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02) after Dreyer collation</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 137): two of the three cruxes close — the recipient is securely <b>Claus (Claudius) Mule</b> (Dreyer's heading glosses <i>Pascolium (? Claudium) Mulaeum</i>; <i>Pascolius</i> is the apograph's corruption), and the bracketed <i>[?credo]</i> is <b>Dreyer's own editorial supplement</b>, reproduced here. The one residual is the unidentified name <b>Calidius</b> (Dreyer reads <i>Calidius</i>, not <i>Cassolius</i>) and the figure <i>Bago</i>; the apodosis verb at <i>[?credo]</i>, on which the precise force of the conditional (\"either … or …\") partly turns. The long suspended sentence beginning <i>Sicqve omnium senatorum literae … interire debuerit</i> is syntactically convoluted in the original and is rendered with its anacoluthon preserved (a bracketed \"[it came to pass that]\" marks the supplied main clause) rather than recast into smoother English.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.122-125 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the whole letter (the Bago reference, the Roskilde prebend/chancellor passage, the parchment letters of the senators after King Frederick's death, the Copenhagen house-arrest-of-instruments clause, and the closing 'ex m[anu]s[cripto] d:ni Longomontani' attribution) word-for-word against the printed page; apart from the fabricated 'Calidius' collation note, the rest of the translation is faithful, including Dreyer's own bracketed conjectural reading '[?credo]' which the English correctly mirrors as '[then I believe]'. One high-severity FABRICATION found (an invented, false claim about what Dreyer prints), which under the grading rule mandates C despite the rest of the letter being clean.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (<b>high</b>). The published English inserts a bracketed collation note asserting Dreyer prints the name \"Calidius\" and that only the working transcription reads \"Cassolius.\" This is backwards and invented: Dreyer's own printed page reads \"Caffolius\"=\"Cassolius,\" matching source_text exactly -- there is no discrepancy between source_text and Dreyer at all, and no counterpart anywhere for the name \"Calidius.\" A scholar trusting this footnote would believe a textual variant exists where none does. At: “Calidius [so Dreyer; the working transcription reads <i>Cassolius</i>]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.122-125. Page 123, line 20 clearly reads \"VIDETUR Caffolius in ultimis ad me difceffus...\" -- matching current_latin's \"Cassolius\" exactly and containing no form resembling \"Calidius\" anywhere on the page. The bracketed collation note in the published English was invented; it has been removed rather than replaced. No other defects found across the four pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter, Nr. 178.. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Corrects the claim that he left Denmark merely because of revenue losses, arguing that all his revenues were withdrawn and that his emigration had deeper causes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970120001", "d": "1597-01-20", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Christian Friis", "ck": "Christian Friis", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Kolding", "t": "Christian Friis → Tycho Brahe — Kolding, 20 January 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHEN, HAeREDI IN KNUDSTRUP, AMICO SUO CHARISSIMO.</p><p>S. Quae apud serenissimum regem, dominum nostrum clementissimum, nobilissime Tycho, amice charissime, tibi a me duplici in negocio effecta dari cupis, ea ex literis tuis, quae mihi una cum exemplo literarum magnificorum regni senatorum redditae sunt, intellexi, utque tibi pro amicitia nostra per me in utroque liberaliter satisfieret, nihil diligentiae praetermisi.</p><p>Verum te caelare non possum, regiam majestatem, cui postulata tua summa fide exposui, ad primum illud de instrumentis astronomicis negocium ita respondisse: ex regio aerario aliquid sumptuum in istorum instrumentorum conservationem impendere, sibi non esse integrum.</p><p>Ad alteram vero quod attinet petitionem, quam de beneficii istius Norvagici clementissima vel saltem ad exiguum illud in literis tuis dictum tempus proroganda concessione instituis, serenissima ipsius majestas eam admittere plane recusavit, persuasissimum habens, praefecturam Bergensem reditibus istius absque insigni incommodo carere minime posse.</p><p>Quae cum ita, uti dixi, se habeant, te eventum exspectationi tuae non respondisse, pro tua prudentia aeque laturum, studium vero ac voluntatem meam, quam tibi hac in re libentissime (quamvis incassum) impendi, in aliis vero, quibus potero, tibi testatam facere maximopere cupio, pro animi tui candore, synceraque aequitate, boni consulturum, plane confido, teque quam diutissime vivere et valere ex amino precor.</p><p>Dabantur Coldingi xx. januarii 1597.<br>Christianus Friis,<br>manu pp:a<br>.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, lord of Knudstrup, his most dear friend.</b></p><p>Greeting. What you desire to have effected for you by me, in a twofold matter, with the most serene king, our most clement lord — most noble Tycho, most dear friend — that I have understood from your letter, which was delivered to me together with a copy of the letter of the magnificent senators of the realm; and that you might, in keeping with our friendship, be liberally satisfied through me in both [matters], I have omitted no diligence.</p><p>But I cannot conceal from you that the king's majesty, to whom I set forth your requests in utmost good faith, replied thus to that first matter concerning the astronomical instruments: that it is not open to him to expend any costs from the royal treasury on the upkeep of those instruments.</p><p>As for the other petition — which you frame concerning a most gracious extension of the grant of that Norwegian benefice, or at least [its extension] to that brief term named in your letter — his most serene majesty flatly refused to admit it, holding it as most firmly settled that the prefecture of Bergen can by no means do without the revenues of that [benefice] without notable detriment.</p><p>Since these things stand thus, as I have said, I am fully confident that you, in keeping with your prudence, will bear with equanimity that the outcome did not answer to your expectation, and that — by reason of the candour of your spirit and your sincere fairness — you will take in good part my zeal and goodwill, which I have most willingly (though in vain) expended for you in this matter, and which I greatly desire to make manifest to you in other things, in whatever I shall be able; and from my heart I pray that you may live and prosper as long as may be.</p><p>Given at Kolding, 20 January 1597.<br>Christian Friis,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <b>Christian Friis of Borreby</b> (1556–1616), appointed royal Chancellor (<i>kongelig kansler</i>) by the newly-crowned Christian IV in 1596. Friis was personally well-disposed toward Uraniborg but now served an assertive young monarch; this is the cold, formal reply that announced the collapse of Tycho's standing. The letter survives only as a later copy (afskrift) in Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here styled <i>haeres in Knudstrup</i> (\"lord/heir of Knudstrup\"), his Scanian family seat (protocol §5.7, <i>Knudstrup</i> kept as is).</li><li><b>Occasion and the two <i>negocia</i>.</b> The \"twofold matter\" is Friis's point-by-point answer to Tycho's petition of 31 December 1596. (1) The <i>primum negocium de instrumentis astronomicis</i> — Tycho had sought a royal subvention from the treasury to maintain his instruments — is refused. (2) The <i>altera petitio</i> concerns the <b>Norwegian fief of Nordfjord</b> (whose revenues attached to a benefice/prebend Tycho had held): on 21 September 1596 it had been transferred from Tycho's jurisdiction to the Lord Lieutenant (prefect) of Bergen. Tycho had asked either for renewal of the grant or, failing that, to retain it only to the end of the fiscal year (1 May 1597) — \"that brief term named in your letter.\" Both requests are denied. (Background: <i>Dreyer, Tycho Brahe</i>; <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>, all in the Sophia corpus.)</li><li><b>\"beneficium Norvagicum\" / \"praefectura Bergensis.\"</b> <i>beneficium</i> is the ecclesiastical/feudal benefice (the Nordfjord prebend and its revenues); <i>praefectura Bergensis</i> = the <b>prefecture (lensmandskab) of Bergen</b>, i.e. the jurisdiction of the Lord Lieutenant of Bergen, to which Nordfjord's revenues had been reassigned. Place-name <i>Bergensem</i> → \"of Bergen.\"</li><li><b>Negation / legal force (verified count).</b> The two operative refusals are rendered without adding or dropping a negation. (1) <i>ex regio aerario aliquid sumptuum … impendere, sibi <b>non</b> esse integrum</i> — one negation: \"it is <b>not</b> open to him [= he is not free] to expend any costs from the treasury\" (<i>integrum esse alicui</i> = \"to be at one's free disposal/option\"). (2) <i>praefecturam Bergensem reditibus istius <b>absque</b> insigni incommodo carere <b>minime</b> posse</i> — accusative-and-infinitive with two negating elements (<i>absque</i> \"without\"; <i>minime</i> \"by no means\") governing <i>carere</i> + ablative (<i>reditibus istius</i> \"the revenues of that [benefice]\"): \"that the prefecture of Bergen <b>can by no means do without</b> the revenues of that [benefice] <b>without</b> notable detriment\" — i.e. it cannot spare them. The doubled negation is the Latin idiom, not a logical double-negative reversing the sense; the king's refusal stands. <i>plane recusavit</i> (\"flatly refused\") is the third refusal and is a negating verb, not a particle.</li><li><b>Idiom / register.</b> <i>sibi non esse integrum</i> glossed above. <i>boni consulturum</i> (sc. <i>te</i>) = \"that you will take [it] in good part.\" <i>quamvis incassum</i> = \"though in vain\" — Friis's own hedge that his effort failed; preserved, not softened. <i>manu pp:a</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand,\" the chancery subscription); since the witness is a copy, this records the original's autograph subscription.</li><li><b>Salutation formula.</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Dabantur Coldingi xx. januarii 1597</i> = \"Given at <b>Kolding</b>, 20 January 1597.\" Denmark was on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1597 (kept until 1700); the date is O.S. The dateline date (20 January) agrees with the dataset label (<i>1597. 20. januar. Kolding</i>); no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout. The Latin is unambiguous formal chancery prose; the two refusals, the double-negation idiom in the Bergen clause, and the dating are all firmly grounded, and the external context (Tycho's 31 Dec. 1596 petition; the Sept. 1596 transfer of Nordfjord to Bergen) corroborates the reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.100-103 page images, including a focused re-read of the p.101/p.102 boundary to confirm the paragraph spanning the page-break ('Quae cum ita, uti dixi, se habeant... pro animi tui candore, / synceraque aequitate, boni consulturum...') is fully present and matches source_text word-for-word. Collated the two substantive refusals (astronomical-instrument funding from the royal treasury; the Norwegian benefice extension/Bergen prefecture revenues) and the date (Coldingi xx. januarii 1597) against the page image. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Christian Friis", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15961231001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15961231001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports the king's refusal of both Tycho's requests: no treasury funds for the upkeep of astronomical instruments, and no extension of the Norwegian benefice.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970120001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970222001", "d": "1597-02-22", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Copenhagen, 22 February 1597 [O.S.] (catalogued Uraniborg)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AMPLISSIMO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO HENRICO RANZOVIO DE BREDENBERGA, SERENISSIMI REGIS DANIAe IN DUCATIBUS HOLSATIAe ET SCHLESWIGAe VICARIO ILLUSTRI, AFFINI AC AMICO SUO INPRIMIS COLENDO.</p><p>AMPLISSIME et nobilissime vir, affinis et amice honorande, binas iamnunc interea, dum hîc Hafniae paulisper commoror, a te recepi literas, quarum priores te ea, quae per tabellarium tuum remisi, sat perfecta accepisse, atque hinc inde, prout destinata erant, per certos internuncios dirigi curasse innuerunt. Quod et lubenter accipio, tibique hoc nomine gratias ago. Et quam de correctione quorundam verborum in diplomate Hispanico licentiam impetrandam amice polliceris, brevi expecto. Alterae, quibus exemplum literarum Reverendiss. et Illustriss. Domini ERNESTI Archiepiscopi Coloniensis et Electoris Imperij adiunctum erat, de quibusdam cum dicto Illustriss. Principe iuxta ipsius petitionem in utraque Astronomia, tam superiori quam inferiori (sic enim nobis loqui lubet) communicandis me adhortantur. Quin et ulterius ex ipsius Celsitudinis ad te datis literis non sine animi oblectatione percipio, ipsam non saltem Mathematicis, sed et Pyronomicis exercitijs (quae etiam nostra est voluptas) adeo impense delectari, ut haec propriâ industriâ proprijsque manibus tractare, inter alias Reipub. procurandae occupationes, non degravetur. In utrisque igitur cum ipsius Celsit, ingenue conferre et continuatam servare correspondentiam (uti vocant) nequaquam tergiversabor. Agoque ipsius Celsit, reverenter gratias, quod me tam clementer ad hanc mutuam communicationem invitet. Ubi autem is, quem ad me hoc nomine ablegare decrevit, huc accesserit, et ea, de quibus ipsius Celsit. mecum agere constituit, speciatim indicaverit, libere et lubenter ad illa, quantum in me erit, respondebo. In Mathematicis et Astronomicis satis aperte, quatenus ea ante operum nostrorum plenariam editionem ulli patefacere commodum fuerit, in Spagiricis vero tecte, ne, si ea in aliorum pervenirent manus, indigne quis ijs abuti possit. Satius itaque iudico, aut per circumlocutiones quasdam arcanas, aut mediante alphabeto quodam peculiari, nobis solis invicem cognito id praestari, vel quacumque aliâ ratione. Qua de re ipsemet Illust. Princeps, prout voluerit, constituat, et in literis, quas se mihi cum suo illo nuntio brevi perscripturum pollicetur, quibus medijs atque indicijs id fieri volet, indicet, et si alphabetum quoddam secretum vel transpositionem literarum usitatarum elegerit, id qualecunque erit, a se ipso tamen non alijs excogitatum et absque ulla propalatione suapte manu in schedâ conscribat, literisque actutum includat, et mox ipsemet consignet. Idem ego vicissim facturus sum, omniaque ut utrinque in silentio arcano contineantur, necessum et admodum erit consultum. Libenter intellexi ipsius Celsit. ultra 22 annos huic eximio et arcano studio Spagirico solerter impendisse, in quo et ego 28 iam annos inde ab aetatis anno 22 versatus sum, quemadmodum 16imo Astronomicis assuescere incepi. Scripsissem hoc tempore ipsius Celsit. atque in ijs, quae exoptat, sponte praevenissem, si non modo hîc Haffniae varijs curis et occupationibus distraherer, dum nonnullam partem ex mea supellectile unâ cum familia huc transferre satago, aliquandiu hîc certis de causis commoraturus. Quin et Instrumenta Astronomica ex meis nonnulla hîc in turri quadam nostris aedibus vicinâ admodumque observationibus accommodâ disponere animus est, unâque laboratorium quoddam Spagiricum hîc adornare, cum quodam insuper Typographeo, quibus omnibus exequendis nunc intentus sum. Constitui insuper hodie V. D. in Insulam nostram traijcere, ut plura ex meis huc transvehi facerem, et caetera, quae istic relinquo, disponam, interea dum ea, quae hîc fieri demandavi, absolvantur, adeo ut animus iam in multas distractus partes, scriptioni tam sublimium et secretarum rerum ad tantum praesertim Principem, commode vacare vix queat. Fiet id nihilominus, quamprimum ipsius huc adventârit minister. Tunc enim me opportuniori quieti restitutum fore confido, eritque id tum non saltem commodius, sed et tutius propter caussas aliquatenus indicatas. Quod autem ipsius Cels. in suis ad te datis literis scribit de Privilegio Hispanico ob id retardato, quod vererentur, ne sub specie rerum Mathematicarum religionis negotia, quae nunc varie trahuntur, tractarem, id caret periculo. Ego enim me illis immiscere nolo. Satis habemus haeresium et confusionum, non opus est, nec mearum partium, aut novas condere, aut priores stabilire. Misereatur nostri coeli et terrae autor, qui universaliter omnia digerit et prospicit, nec cum praeiudicio, uti homines, rem discernit. Interim pro hoc ipso, quod obtinui, Privilegio tam clementer impetrato ipsius Celsit. summas demisse habeo gratias. Apprime etiam mihi gratum est, quod ipsius Celsit. primum tomum Epistolarum de Astronomicis per te illi communicatum tam diligenter perlegere inter tot alias curas non supersederit, quodque ipsi tam illustri et in his rebus exercitato atque iudicioso Principi eius contenta admodum placuerint, non possum non mihi ipsi congratulari. Reliquos tomos nunc typis adorno. Inprimis autem Progymnasmatum nostrorum in Astronomiâ instauranda volumen primum atque alterum, typographico labore absolvere et mature publici iuris facere enixe laboro. Quae ubi absoluta fuerint, quod propediem futurum confido, ipsius Gestit.ni tum quoque tibi eorundem quaedam exemplaria transmittam.</p><p>Miratus Illustriss. ille ERNESTUS in dictis ad te literis, quod praestantissimi illius Mathematici CHRISTOPHORI CLAVIJ Bambergensis in transmissis epistolis Astronomicis nullam faciam mentionem, cum is ob tot praeclara in Mathematicis edita opera celebris existat; atque hanc nominis ipsius intermissionem, ob locorum nimiam intercapedinem, quodque scripta illius Romae utplurimum impressa ad nos forte perlata non sint, clementer et candide excusat. Atqui ego omnia eruditissimi illius CLAVIJ opera fortassis non vidi, ea nihilominus quae in EUCLIDEM ingeniose et in IOHANNEM DE SACROBUSCHO luculenter, et de Gnomonicis copiose conscripsit, in promptu habeo, et iamdudum introspexi, tum quoque Apologiam Calendarij novi contra MAeSTHLINUM, in qua is mei alicubi mentionem facit, observationem nostram aequinoctij verni, anni ni fallor 76 allegando, ut probet ex ea recte factum, quod decendij ablatio in Calendario Gregoriano admissa sit, quod et convenienter se habet, licet summa in his praecisio non sit animadversa, quae etiam forte in tali negotio, quoad Ecclesiasticum et Politicum usum esse poterit ociosa, quemadmodum MAeSTHLINO, qui scrupulositatem calculi Prutenici urget, non citra causam subinde obijcit CLAVIUS. Verum si liceat id quod res est affirmare, uterque tam CLAVIUS quam MAeSTHLINUS de coelestibus, coelo, quod horologium est temporis, inconsulto sententiam protulit. CLAVIUS enim vix ullam observationem coelestem, nisi eam unicam, de qua dixi, e nostris petitam, quae sola tam arduo negotio non sufficit, in medium protulit, nullasque Lunarium motionum, quae magis intricatae sunt, quam hactenus creditum est, denotationes, prout operaeprecium fuit, in consilium adducit. MAeSTHLINUS nullam plane, quod sciam, e coelestibus assumpsit probationem. Et si id ex suis observatis fecisset, nihilo plus fidei mereretur. Quae enim ipsius (si quas absolvit) in correctione motus Solis et Lunae animadversiones produnt, frustranea sunt, uti consignatio Eclipsium ipsius Ephemeridibus praefixa, coelesti harmoniae non magis quam antea, si modo non minus congrua, liquido patefecit. Inhaeret enim is nimium fidenter calculo Prutenico, et quantitati annuae inde desumptae, quae nequaquam talem sortitur inaequalitatem, prout magnus ille alias COPERNICUS voluit; sed propius ad Alphonsinam accedit supputationem, licet ne hanc quidem satis scrupulose attingat, veluti haec a nobis convenientiore loco reserantur. Non igitur tanta disconvenientia est in Calendario neoterico a coelesti norma, prout voluit MAeSTHLINUS, quicunque tandem eius autor existat, sive hic CLAVIUS, sive ALOYSIUS ille, de quo pontifex in prooemio publicationis eius mentionem facit. Licet vero huius excellentissimi Mathematici, CLAVIJ videlicet in Epistolis hisce non meminerim, quod nulla fuerit istic occasio id faciendi, praesertim cum is Astronomica ex ipso coelo non tractârit, de quibus utplurimum ibi agitur, tamen circa finem primi nostri Tomi Progymnasmatum antedictorum, cum ea, quae de Nova Stella in ultimâ editione Commentariorum Sphaericorum attexuit, sub disquisitionem libertate Philosophicâ voco, eius luculentam et honorificam, prout praecipua eius in his artibus doctrina meretur, mentionem facio. Si quae praeterea ex ipsius Minervâ Mathematicae lucubrationes in lucem prodierunt, vel adhuc proditurae sunt, cuperem eas meis sumptibus mecum communicari. Porro si is mecum per literas correspondentiam habere atque de his excellentibus studijs, quibus utrimque dediti, iucunde conferre voluerit, erit mihi idipsum longe gratissimum, praesertim si autoritate et suasu tanti Principis, uti is in literis innuit, id fiat. Addet enim idipsum nobis calcar, eritque utrique nostrum eo honorificentius. Haec si dicto Illustrissimo Principi cum submissa ex me salutatione per literas indicaveris, recte feceris et me tibi eo devinctiorem reddideris.</p><p>Vale, et prolixitati huic ignosce.<br>Dabantur Haffniae die 22. Febr. Anno 1597.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VII, p. 382; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Amantißime HENRICE RANZOVI, si eadem diligentiâ nobis Privilegium Gallicum et Anglicanum (de quibus etiam antea ad te scripsi, et istic addicta eße scio) qua Hispanicum ultimo mißum, mature idque ante festum Pentecostes, si id fieri poßit, procuraveris, rem hac in parte non saltem mihi, sed et communibus studijs utilem praestiteris, idque ut facias (quod et lubenter facturum confido) per mysteria Uraniae, cui utrinque litamus, te obsecratum volo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most noble man, Lord Heinrich Rantzau of Breitenburg, illustrious viceroy of the Most Serene King of Denmark in the duchies of Holstein and Schleswig, his kinsman and friend to be honoured above all.</b></p><p>Most distinguished and most noble man, kinsman and honoured friend: by now, meanwhile, while I tarry here a little at Copenhagen, I have received two letters from you. The earlier of them gave to understand that you had received those things which I sent back by your courier sufficiently intact, and that you had had them directed hither and thither, as they were destined, by trusty intermediaries. This too I gladly accept, and for it I give you thanks. And the leave you kindly promise to procure regarding the correction of certain words in the Spanish patent [diploma] I shortly expect. The other letter, to which was joined a copy of the letter of the Most Reverend and Most Illustrious Lord Ernst, Archbishop of Cologne and Elector of the Empire, urges me to share certain matters with the said Most Illustrious Prince, at his own request, in both kinds of astronomy, the higher as well as the lower (for so it pleases us to speak). And further, from His Highness's letter written to you, I perceive — not without delight of mind — that he takes such intense pleasure not only in mathematical but also in pyronomic [fire-working, i.e. alchemical] exercises (which is our own delight too) that, among his other occupations of administering the commonwealth, he does not think it beneath him to handle these by his own industry and with his own hands. In both [pursuits], therefore, I shall by no means shrink from conferring frankly with His Highness and keeping up a continued correspondence (as they call it). And I render reverent thanks to His Highness that he so graciously invites me to this mutual exchange. But when the man whom he has resolved to send to me on this account shall have come here, and shall have set out in particular the matters which His Highness has determined to treat of with me, I shall freely and gladly answer them so far as in me lies: in mathematical and astronomical matters openly enough, in so far as it shall be convenient to disclose them to anyone before the full edition of our works; but in spagyric [alchemical] matters covertly, lest, if they should come into the hands of others, someone might unworthily abuse them. I judge it better, therefore, that this be accomplished either by certain secret circumlocutions, or by means of some private alphabet known to us two alone, or by whatever other method. Concerning this let the Most Illustrious Prince himself determine as he shall wish, and in the letter which he promises shortly to write to me with that messenger of his, let him indicate by what means and signs he wishes it to be done; and if he should choose some secret alphabet or a transposition of the customary letters, let him — whatever it shall be, but devised by himself and not by others, and without any disclosure — write it out in his own hand on a sheet, and at once enclose it in the letter, and forthwith seal it himself. I shall do the same in return, and it will be needful and very advisable that everything be kept on both sides in secret silence. I have gladly learned that His Highness has assiduously devoted more than 22 years to this excellent and arcane spagyric study, in which I too have now been engaged for 28 years from the 22nd year of my age — just as in my 16th I began to grow accustomed to astronomical matters. I should have written to His Highness at this time, and of my own accord have anticipated him in the things he longs for, were I not just now distracted here at Copenhagen by various cares and occupations, while I busy myself transferring hither some part of my household goods together with my family, intending to stay here for a while for certain reasons. Indeed it is my mind to set up here some of my astronomical instruments in a certain tower neighbouring our house and very well suited to observations, and at the same time to fit out here a certain spagyric laboratory, with, besides, a certain printing-house — to the carrying out of all which I am now intent. I have besides resolved this day, God willing, to cross over to our island, so as to have more of my things conveyed hither and to dispose of the rest that I leave behind there, meanwhile, until those things which I have ordered to be done here are completed — so that my mind, now distracted into many parts, can scarcely conveniently be free for the writing of such lofty and secret matters, especially to so great a Prince. Nonetheless it shall be done, as soon as his minister shall have arrived here. For then I trust I shall be restored to a more opportune repose, and it will then be not only more convenient but also safer, for the reasons in some measure indicated. But as for what His Highness writes in his letter to you concerning the Spanish privilege having been delayed on this ground — that they feared lest, under the appearance of mathematical matters, I might treat of the affairs of religion which are now variously drawn this way and that — that is without danger. For I am unwilling to meddle with those things. We have heresies and confusions enough; there is no need, nor is it my part, either to found new ones or to establish the old. May the author of heaven and earth have mercy on us, who universally orders and provides for all things, and discerns the matter not with prejudice, as men do. Meanwhile, for this very privilege which I obtained, so graciously procured, I humbly render the greatest thanks to His Highness. It is also exceedingly gratifying to me that His Highness has not, among so many other cares, forborne to read through so diligently the first volume of the Letters on Astronomical matters communicated to him through you; and that its contents have so well pleased a Prince so illustrious and so practised and judicious in these matters, I cannot but congratulate myself. The remaining volumes I am now setting up in type. But above all I labour earnestly to complete by the printer's work, and in good time to make public, the first and second volume of our <i>Progymnasmata</i> on the restoration of astronomy. When these shall have been completed — which I trust will be shortly — I shall then also send certain copies of them both to His Highness and to you.</p><p>That Most Illustrious Ernst, in the said letter to you, marvelled that I make no mention of that most excellent mathematician Christopher Clavius of Bamberg in the astronomical letters I sent, since he is renowned for so many distinguished works published in mathematics; and he graciously and candidly excuses this omission of his name on the ground of the too great distance of places, and because his writings, mostly printed at Rome, have perhaps not reached us. And yet — though perhaps I have not seen all the works of that most learned Clavius — those which he wrote ingeniously on <i>Euclid</i> and lucidly on <i>John of Sacrobosco</i>, and copiously on gnomonics, I have at hand, and have long since looked into; and also the <i>Apology for the New Calendar against Maestlin</i>, in which he makes mention of me somewhere, citing our observation of the vernal equinox, of the year — if I am not mistaken — [15]76, to prove from it that it was rightly done that the removal of ten days was allowed in the Gregorian calendar — which is indeed fittingly so, although the utmost precision in these matters has not been observed (which perhaps too in such a business, as far as ecclesiastical and political use is concerned, may be idle), just as Clavius from time to time, not without cause, objects to Maestlin, who presses the niceness [scrupulosity] of the Prutenic computation. But, if it be permitted to affirm the thing as it is, both Clavius and Maestlin alike have delivered a verdict about the heavenly bodies without consulting the heaven, which is the clock of time. For Clavius brought forward scarcely any celestial observation — except that single one of which I spoke, taken from ours, which alone does not suffice for so arduous a business — and adduces in counsel no determinations of the lunar motions (which are more intricate than has hitherto been believed), as it would have been worth the while [to do]. Maestlin, so far as I know, assumed no proof at all from the heavenly bodies; and if he had done it from his own observations, he would deserve no more credence. For whatever observations of his (if he completed any) his corrections of the motion of the Sun and Moon betray are vain — just as the register of eclipses prefixed to his <i>Ephemerides</i> has plainly shown [itself] no more agreeable to the celestial harmony than before, if indeed not less so. For he clings too confidently to the Prutenic computation, and to the annual quantity derived from it, which by no means receives such an inequality as that great Copernicus, for his part, would have it; but it comes nearer to the Alphonsine reckoning, although it does not touch even this with sufficient nicety — as these things are unlocked by us in a more fitting place. There is not, therefore, so great a discrepancy in the modern [neoteric] calendar from the celestial standard as Maestlin would have it — whoever, after all, be its author, whether this Clavius, or that Aloysius of whom the pontiff makes mention in the preface to its publication. But although I have not mentioned this most excellent mathematician — namely Clavius — in these <i>Letters</i>, because there was no occasion to do so there (especially since he has not treated of astronomical matters from the heaven itself, of which there is mostly question there), nevertheless toward the end of the first volume of our aforesaid <i>Progymnasmata</i>, when I call under examination, with philosophical liberty, the things he appended concerning the New Star in the last edition of his <i>Commentary on the Sphere</i>, I make lucid and honorific mention of him, as his pre-eminent learning in these arts deserves. If, besides, any [other] mathematical lucubrations from his Minerva have come to light, or are yet to come forth, I could wish them to be shared with me at my expense. Furthermore, if he be willing to keep up a correspondence with me by letter, and to confer pleasantly about these excellent studies, to which we are both devoted, that very thing will be far the most gratifying to me — especially if it be done by the authority and persuasion of so great a Prince, as he intimates in his letter. For that very thing will add a spur to us, and will be the more honourable for each of us. If you signify these things to the said Most Illustrious Prince by letter, with a humble greeting from me, you will have done rightly and will have made me the more bound to you.</p><p>Farewell, and pardon this prolixity.<br>Given at Copenhagen, the 22nd day of February, the year 1597.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Most loving Henry Rantzau, if with the same diligence by which the Spanish [privilege] was sent last, you will in good time — and that before the feast of Pentecost, if it can be done — procure for us the Gallic and English Privilege (of which I wrote to you before, too, and which I know to be granted there), you will have rendered a service useful in this respect not only to me but also to our common studies; and that you do so (as I trust you will gladly do) I would have you adjured by the mysteries of Urania, to whom we both sacrifice.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / dateline.</b> Tycho Brahe, signing <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand\"). The dateline reads <i>Dabantur Haffniae die 22. Febr. Anno 1597</i> — <b>\"Given at Copenhagen, 22 February 1597\"</b> (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The body confirms the place repeatedly: <i>hîc Hafniae … commoror</i> (\"I tarry here at Copenhagen\"), the household and instruments being moved <i>huc</i> (\"hither,\" i.e. to Copenhagen), and the plan <i>hodie … in Insulam nostram traijcere</i> (\"to cross over today to our island,\" i.e. Hven). <b>Date/place crux flagged:</b> the dataset `label` reads \"1597. 22. februar. Uraniborg.\" The source's own dateline and internal evidence place the writing at <b>Copenhagen, not Uraniborg</b> — at the very moment Tycho was beginning to move off Hven. The header follows the as-written place (Copenhagen) per protocol §4.1 and notes the catalogue's \"Uraniborg\" as the discrepant label; the date (22 Feb 1597) is not in dispute.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius de Bredenberga</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598/99), royal Statthalter (viceroy) of the Danish king in the duchies of Holstein and Schleswig, humanist, polymath, and astrology patron, of Breitenburg (<i>Bredenberga</i>); a kinsman (<i>affinis</i>) and friend of Tycho, and the host who would shelter Tycho at Wandsbek in 1597–98 where the <i>Mechanica</i> was printed.</li><li><b><i>affinis</b></i> — rendered \"kinsman\" (a relation by marriage/affinity), preserving the connection without overstating it as blood kinship.</li><li><b>The Prince.</b> <i>ERNESTUS Archiepiscopus Coloniensis et Elector Imperij</i> = <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b> (1554–1612), Archbishop-Elector of Cologne, a noted patron of alchemy; the \"Most Illustrious Prince\"/\"His Highness\" (<i>Illustriss. Princeps</i> / <i>ipsius Celsitudo</i>) throughout. Per Mosley, Tycho enclosed a copy of Ernst's letter to Rantzau when he wrote to Clavius on 5 January 1600 — the correspondence channel this letter inaugurates.</li><li><b><i>in utraque Astronomia, tam superiori quam inferiori</b></i> — \"in both kinds of astronomy, the higher as well as the lower (for so it pleases us to speak)\": Tycho's coy formula for <b>astronomy proper</b> (<i>superior</i>, of the heavens) and <b>alchemy / \"terrestrial astronomy\"</b> (<i>inferior</i>) — the Paracelsian conceit that alchemy is the astronomy of the earth. The hedge <i>sic enim nobis loqui lubet</i> is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Pyronomicis exercitijs</b></i> — \"pyronomic exercises,\" literally \"fire-management/fire-working\" practices = <b>alchemical/chemical laboratory work</b>; glossed in brackets. <b><i>Spagiricis / studio Spagirico / laboratorium Spagiricum</b></i> — \"spagyric\" (Paracelsian alchemical-chemical) matters / study / laboratory; the period term is kept and glossed \"[alchemical]\" on first use.</li><li><b>Secrecy apparatus.</b> <i>alphabetum quoddam peculiari / secretum vel transpositionem literarum usitatarum</i> — a private cipher-alphabet or a transposition of the ordinary letters: Tycho proposes a <b>cipher</b> for the alchemical exchange, the prince to devise and seal it in his own hand. Rendered literally; \"secret silence\" for <i>silentio arcano</i>.</li><li><b><i>operum nostrorum plenariam editionem</b></i> — \"the full edition of our works\": Tycho's planned complete edition, of which the <i>Progymnasmata</i> and the <i>Epistolae</i> are parts.</li><li><b><i>diplomate Hispanico / Privilegio Hispanico</b></i> — \"the Spanish patent / privilege\": a printing/publishing privilege Tycho was seeking through Spanish/Habsburg channels (Rantzau acting as intermediary); its delay is blamed on fears that Tycho might smuggle religious controversy under cover of mathematics — a charge he disclaims.</li><li><b><i>primum tomum Epistolarum</b></i> — the <b>first volume of the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</b></i> (<i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i>, Liber I, Uraniborg 1596), which Tycho had sent to Ernst via Rantzau.</li><li><b><i>Progymnasmatum … in Astronomiâ instauranda volumen primum atque alterum</b></i> — the first and second volumes of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (\"preliminary exercises in the restoration of astronomy\"). <i>Astronomia instauranda / instaurata</i> = the <b>restoration of astronomy</b>, Tycho's programme (protocol §5.1). Rendered \"restoration of astronomy.\"</li><li><b>Clavius.</b> <i>CHRISTOPHORUS CLAVIUS Bambergensis</i> = <b>Christoph Clavius</b> (1538–1612), the Jesuit mathematician of Bamberg, then at the Collegio Romano; chief architect of the Gregorian calendar. Named works: his edition of <i>Euclid</i>; his commentary <i>In Sphaeram Ioannis de Sacro Bosco</i>; his works <i>de Gnomonicis</i> (<i>Gnomonices libri octo</i>); the <i>Apologia</i> of the new calendar against Maestlin; and his <i>Commentary on the Sphere</i> (last edition), where he appended remarks on the <b>New Star</b> (the 1572 nova) — which Tycho says he treats \"with philosophical liberty\" toward the end of <i>Progymnasmata</i> I.</li><li><b><i>Minervâ</b></i> — \"from his Minerva,\" i.e. from Clavius's [own] mind/genius (Minerva as goddess of wisdom = native talent); rendered idiomatically.</li><li><b>Maestlin.</b> <i>MAeSTHLINUS</i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), the Tübingen astronomer (Kepler's teacher) and a Copernican; here criticised over the calendar dispute and his <i>Ephemerides</i>.</li><li><b><i>Aloysius … de quo pontifex in prooemio … mentionem facit</b></i> = <b>Aloysius Lilius</b> (Luigi Lilio / Aloysius Lilius, d. 1576), whose scheme underlay the Gregorian reform and whom Gregory XIII (<i>pontifex</i>) names in the bull's preface; Tycho raises him as the calendar's possible true author over against Clavius.</li><li><b>Calendar / technical.</b> <i>Calendario Gregoriano / neoterico</i> = the <b>Gregorian (modern/neoteric) calendar</b>; <i>decendij ablatio</i> = \"the removal of ten days\" (the 1582 excision of 10 days); <i>aequinoctij verni … anni 76</i> = the <b>vernal equinox</b> observation \"of the year [15]76,\" cited by Clavius. <i>calculus Pruteniciʼs</i> — the <b>Prutenic computation</b> (the <i>Prutenic Tables</i> of Erasmus Reinhold, 1551, based on Copernicus); <i>Alphonsina supputatio</i> = the <b>Alphonsine reckoning</b> (the Alphonsine Tables). <i>quantitas annua</i> = \"the annual quantity\" (length of the tropical year); <i>inaequalitas</i> = the (claimed) <b>inequality</b>/variation in the year's length. <i>coelo, quod horologium est temporis</i> — \"the heaven, which is the clock of time\" — kept as the sententia it is. <i>libertate Philosophicâ</i> = \"with philosophical liberty\" (freedom of philosophical inquiry).</li><li><b>Biographical datum.</b> Tycho dates his alchemy \"28 years … from the 22nd year of my age\" and his start in astronomy to \"my 16th [year]\" — i.e. astronomy from c. 1562 and alchemy from c. 1568, consistent with a 1597 letter (Tycho b. 1546). Figures rendered exactly; no reconciliation imposed.</li><li><b><i>V. D.</b></i> (in <i>hodie V. D. … traijcere</i>) = <i>volente Deo</i>, \"God willing\"; expanded in the translation.</li><li><b>Negations checked.</b> The disclaimer cluster — <i>id caret periculo</i> (\"that is without danger\"), <i>Ego enim me illis immiscere nolo</i> (\"for I am unwilling to meddle with those things\"), <i>non opus est, nec mearum partium, aut novas condere, aut priores stabilire</i> (\"there is no need, nor is it my part, either to found new ones or to establish the old\") — is rendered with its negations counted and preserved; Tycho denies any intent to engage religious controversy, neither inventing nor entrenching heresies. The double construction <i>nec … aut … aut …</i> is kept as \"nor … either … or.\"</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (before the signature), restored from Dreyer VII, p. 382 (dropped by the working transcription); the request that Rantzau procure the Gallic and English printing-privileges before Pentecost.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one substantive crux, flagged above and resolved in the header per protocol §4.1, is the <b>place discrepancy</b> (source dateline + internal evidence = Copenhagen; dataset `label` = Uraniborg). Minor soft point: <i>Gestit.ni</i> in the closing of the first paragraph (for the expected <i>Cels.ni</i>/<i>Celsitudini</i>) appears to be a transcription/abbreviation oddity for \"His Highness,\" translated as such.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\" then \"5-6\"). Collated the full letter and its heading against Dreyer TBOO VII pp.379-382, from the address (\"Amplissimo et nobilissimo viro... Henrico Ranzovio de Bredenberga...\") through the dateline (\"Dabantur Haffniae die 22. Febr. Anno 1597\") and the postscript. Verified every proper name (Henricus Ranzovius/Rantzau, Ernestus Archiepiscopus Coloniensis, Christophorus Clavius Bambergensis, Maesthlinus, Aloysius/Lilio), every number/date (22 years, 28 years, age 22, age 16; the equinox year \"76\" with its hedge \"ni fallor\" correctly kept as \"if I am not mistaken\"; the letter's own date 22 February 1597), and the place-names (Haffnia/Copenhagen, Bredenberga/Breitenburg). The postscript beginning \"Amantissime Henrice Ranzovi...\" is present in source_text (flagged there as restored from Dreyer p.382 after the working transcription had dropped it) and its Latin matches Dreyer's print exactly; published_english correctly translates it in full, so no live omission remains. No fabricated, omitted, or mistranslated clauses found in the body; no hedges flattened.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15930109001", "next": "brahe-t_15970331001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Rantzau for forwarding dispatches and promises regarding correction of the Spanish patent, and reports receiving a request from Archbishop Ernst of Cologne for astronomical and astrological communication.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970222001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970330001", "d": "1597-03-30", "dir": "to", "per": "hven", "c": "Jaques Bongars", "ck": "Jaques Bongars", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Frankfurt am Main", "t": "Jacques Bongars → Tycho Brahe — Frankfurt am Main, 30 March 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Opera Omnia <b>VII, p. 397</b>, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Bern, Burgerbibliothek, cod. B 149 n. 323</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHE</p><p>NOBILISSIME et clarissime domine. Vix inter varias occupationes hoc momentum surripio, quod liceat tribus verbis tibi salutem dicere, et has theses in fasciculum conijcere, quas spero fore ad gustum tuum. Ego certe et auctori auctor fui edendi et Typographo excudendi. Epistolarum tuarum volumen primum percurri et ad hunc ELYSAeUM exemplum statim misi. Is te et colit et admiratur, Sed tu praestantissime Domine vale, et si ego tibi memoria excidi, velle ipse tibi aurem et succurrent anno 1584 hospites tibi GUILELMUS CURCELLIUS et ego, qui tui studiosissimus.</p><p>Scripsi Francofurti penult. Martij 1597.<br>[IACOBUS BONGARSIUS.]</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe</b></p><p>Most noble and most renowned lord. Amid my various occupations I scarcely snatch this moment in which I may be allowed to say a greeting to you in three words, and to throw these theses together into a packet — theses which I hope will be to your taste. I, for my part, was indeed both the prompter to the author for publishing them and to the printer for striking them off. I have run through the first volume of your letters, and at once sent a copy to this Elysaeus [Élie]. He both esteems and admires you. But fare you well, most excellent lord; and if I have slipped from your memory, [pull] your own ear [to remind yourself], and there will come to your aid your guests of the year 1584, Wilhelm Curcellius [Guillaume de Courcelles] and I, who am most devoted to you.</p><p>Written at Frankfurt, on the penultimate [day] of March 1597.<br>[Jacques Bongars.]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iacobus Bongarsius</i> = <b>Jacques Bongars</b> (1554–1612), the French Huguenot diplomat, classical philologist and book-collector, in the service of Henri IV of France; long resident in and around Frankfurt and Strasbourg. His library is the core of the Bongarsiana at the Burgerbibliothek Bern, whence the MS witness (\"A Bern, B 149 n. 323\"). Bongars and a companion had been Tycho's guests on Hven in 1584 (see below).</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHE</i> — a bare dative heading (\"To Tycho Brahe\"), not a full honorific salutation; kept as written.</li><li><b><i>has theses … in fasciculum conijcere</b></i> — \"to throw these theses together into a packet\": Bongars is forwarding, with the letter, a set of printed academic <i>theses</i> (disputation propositions) he had a hand in seeing into print, <i>auctori auctor fui edendi et Typographo excudendi</i> (\"I was the prompter [literally: author/instigator] to the author for publishing them, and to the printer for striking them off\"). The wordplay <i>auctori auctor</i> (\"to the author an author/prompter\") is kept by rendering the second <i>auctor</i> as \"prompter.\" The particular theses are not identified in the letter.</li><li><b><i>Epistolarum tuarum volumen primum</b></i> — \"the first volume of your letters\": Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I), printed at Uraniborg (1596) — the only volume issued. Bongars reports having read it and forwarded a copy.</li><li><b><i>ad hunc ELYSAeUM … misi</b></i> — \"sent [a copy] to this Elysaeus.\" <i>Elysaeus</i> is most plausibly the Latinised first name <b>Élie / Elisaeus</b> of a contemporary (a humanist correspondent in Bongars's circle); the demonstrative <i>hunc</i> (\"this [man]\") implies someone known to both parties, but the letter gives no surname, so the identification is left open. The accusative <i>exemplum</i> is read as \"a copy\" (of the volume just mentioned).</li><li><b><i>hospites tibi … anno 1584 GUILELMUS CURCELLIUS et ego</b></i> — \"your guests of the year 1584, Wilhelm Curcellius and I.\" <i>Guilelmus Curcellius</i> = <b>Guillaume de Courcelles</b> (Latinised <i>Curcellius</i>), evidently Bongars's travelling companion when the two visited Tycho on Hven in 1584. Bongars invokes the shared visit as the token by which Tycho should recall him.</li><li><b>Crux (corrupt clause):</b> <i>\"si ego tibi memoria excidi, velle ipse tibi aurem et succurrent … hospites …\"</i> The working transcription reads <i>velle ipse tibi aurem</i>, which is not construable as it stands (<i>velle</i> = present infinitive \"to wish\"; no governing verb). The clause almost certainly contains the classical idiom <i>aurem (sibi) vellere</i> / <i>vellicare</i> — \"to pull/tweak the ear,\" the Roman gesture of jogging one's memory (cf. Virgil, <i>Ecl.</i> 6.3–4). The intended reading is therefore most likely <i>\"vellica [or: velle] ipse tibi aurem\"</i> = \"pull your own ear [to remind yourself],\" after which <i>et succurrent … hospites</i> (\"and your guests … will come to your aid/mind\") completes the conceit. The translation supplies \"[pull] … [to remind yourself]\" in brackets and renders on that reading; the printed form <i>velle</i> is kept in the Source block. The crux is flagged, not smoothed — hence the overall grade.</li><li><b><i>qui tui studiosissimus</b></i> — \"who [am] most devoted to you\": the subscription formula (protocol §5.5, <i>E. Tuae studiosiss.</i>), with the verb elided.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Scripsi Francofurti penult. Martij 1597</i> = \"Written at <b>Frankfurt</b> [am Main], on the penultimate [day] of March 1597.\" March having 31 days, <i>penultimo Martii</i> = <b>30 March</b>; this agrees with the catalogue label (\"30. marts\"). Bongars, a French subject, might be expected to write New Style by 1597, but the dateline gives no style marker and the dataset does not flag the letter \"n.s.\"; the date is therefore reproduced as written without conversion, and the style is left undetermined (O.S. assumed by default for consistency with the Danish/Imperial-side correspondence, but not asserted by the text).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Francofurtum</i> = <b>Frankfurt am Main</b> (the great book-fair city; consistent with Bongars's role in the printing of the forwarded theses).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the sense of the letter is secure, but it turns on a genuinely corrupt clause (<i>velle ipse tibi aurem et succurrent</i>): the <i>aurem vellere</i> \"jog the memory\" reading is highly probable but is an editorial reconstruction, not the transmitted text. Secondary open points: the surname of <i>Elysaeus</i> and the precise style (O.S./N.S.) of the dateline are both undetermined. Flagged rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.382-384 (page images). The Bongars letter is printed complete on p.383 under 'TYCHONI BRAHE.', matching source_text word-for-word (allowing long-s typography). Checked subject/object assignment on every clause with a pronoun ('Ego...fui' = Bongars, first person; 'Is te...colit' = Elysaeus, third person, object 'te'=Tycho) — none flipped. Checked the one date (anno 1584) and both named persons (Elysaeum, Guilelmus Curcellius) against the Latin — all correct. The published_grade of C on this packet appears to have been over-cautious; independent collation against Dreyer found no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or text error.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bern, B 149 n. 323."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends theses he helped arrange for publication, notes having forwarded Tycho's first letter-volume to Élie, and recalls being a guest of Tycho's in 1584.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970330001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970331001", "d": "1597-03-31", "dir": "from", "per": "hven", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Heinrich Rantzau — Copenhagen, 31 March 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, VIRTUTE ET ERUDITIONE INPRIMIS CONSPICUO DOMINO HENRICO RANTZOVIO DE BREDENBERGA, PRAeSIDI SEGEBERGENSI ET PRODUCI HOLSATIAe ET DITMARSIAe, AMICO ET AFFINI SUO PLURIMUM COLENDO.<br>S.</p><p>NONNULLAS dedi literas ad te ante aliquot septimanas, Nobilissime ac amplissime Domine HENRICE RANZOVI, affinis et amice honorande, inter quas quaedam erant tibi aperte tradendae, quaedam vero secreto, ut seorsim solus eas legeres. Confido eas te accepisse, verum responsum ad illas usque huc nondum accepi ullum, quod per hunc tabellarium avide expeto expectoque, si forte aliqua ratione id neglectum est. Vale, et rem de qua tibi scripsi serio agi persuasum habe.</p><p>Iterumque vale.<br>Dabantur Hafniae 31. Martij Anno 97.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most worthy man, eminent above all for virtue and erudition, Master Heinrich Rantzau of Breitenburg, Governor of Segeberg and Lord-Lieutenant of Holstein and Dithmarschen, his kinsman and friend much to be honoured — greeting.</b></p><p>I sent you several letters some weeks ago, most noble and most worthy Master Heinrich Rantzau, my honoured kinsman and friend, among which some were to be delivered to you openly, but some in secret, so that you alone might read them apart. I am confident that you received them, yet up to now I have received no reply at all to them — which I eagerly seek and await by this courier, in case by some chance it has been neglected. Farewell, and be assured that the matter about which I wrote to you is being pursued in earnest.</p><p>And again, farewell.<br>Given at Copenhagen, 31 March, the year [15]97.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Rantzovius de Bredenberga</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henrik Rantzau, 1526–1598), the great Holstein humanist, statesman, and bibliophile; royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the Danish king in the duchies. He was a long-standing patron, correspondent, and connection of Tycho's, and is here addressed as <i>affinis</i> — \"kinsman / connection by marriage\" (the Brahe and Rantzau families were allied; <i>affinis</i> denotes relationship by marriage rather than blood). <i>de Bredenberga</i> = of <b>Breitenburg</b>, his castle and seat on the Stör in Holstein.</li><li><b>Titulature.</b> <i>PRAeSIDI SEGEBERGENSI</i> = \"Governor of <b>Segeberg</b>\" (Rantzau's administrative seat at Segeberg in Holstein). <i>PRODUCI HOLSATIAe ET DITMARSIAe</i> — <i>produci</i> is the dative of <i>produx</i>, a rare/post-classical title here equivalent to provincial governor or lord-lieutenant; rendered \"Lord-Lieutenant of <b>Holstein and Dithmarschen</b>.\" The form is unusual and is flagged rather than normalised to the more common <i>prorex</i> or <i>praeses</i>.</li><li><b>Address spelling.</b> The superscription has <i>RANTZOVIO</i>; the body has <i>RANZOVI</i> (vocative, without the medial <i>t</i>). Both spellings of the name are reproduced as printed; no emendation is adopted.</li><li><b><i>aperte … secreto</b></i> — \"openly … in secret\": the earlier batch of letters contained both an open portion and a confidential portion meant for Rantzau alone (<i>seorsim solus</i>). The \"matter\" (<i>rem</i>) pursued <i>serio</i> (\"in earnest\") is not named in this note — the substance lies in those prior letters, not here.</li><li><b><i>tabellarium</b></i> — \"courier / letter-carrier\"; Tycho asks for a reply to be returned by the same bearer of the present note.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"greeting,\" protocol §5.5). <i>Vale … Iterumque vale</i> = \"Farewell … And again, farewell\" (§5.5). <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand,\" the autograph subscription formula.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Dabantur Hafniae 31. Martij Anno 97</i> = \"Given at <b>Copenhagen</b> [<i>Hafnia</i>], 31 March, the year [15]97\" (O.S. — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label; <i>Anno 97</i> is the abbreviated year, expanded to [15]97. <i>Dabantur</i> (\"were given,\" epistolary plural/imperfect) rendered \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Context (1597).</b> The letter belongs to the months just before Tycho's departure from Denmark: he left Hven in 1597 and quit the kingdom that year, his royal support withdrawn after Frederik II's death. The unanswered \"secret\" letters and the matter \"pursued in earnest\" likely touch his deteriorating position and search for new patronage, for which Rantzau was a natural intermediary — but the note itself does not specify this, and the inference is not pressed into the translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is short and rendered securely; the only soft point is the precise force of the rare title <i>produx</i> (<i>produci</i>), flagged above rather than smoothed into a falsely specific office.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — short, sense secure, date agrees; only the certain office's rare word-form </i>produx<i> flagged for transparency.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII pp.382-384 (page images). The Tycho-to-Rantzau letter is printed complete on p.383 under the superscription matching source_text word-for-word. Collated salutation titulature (Henrico Rantzovio de Bredenberga, Praesidi Segebergensi, Produci Holsatiae et Ditmarsiae), body clauses, and closing date (31 Martij Anno 97) against the Latin; exact match throughout. Translation is accurate with no additions or omissions.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15970222001", "next": "brahe-t_15970917001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Notes that several letters, some for Rantzau's eyes only, have gone unanswered, and urges a reply, assuring him the matter discussed is being actively pursued.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970331001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970601001", "d": "1597-06-01", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "[open testimonial, for Christian Severini Longomontanus]", "ck": "[open testimonial, for Christian Severini Longomontanus]", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [open testimonial, for Christian Severini Longomontanus] — Copenhagen, 1 June 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TESTIMONIUM DOMINO LONGOMONTANO A TYCHONE DATUM.</p><p>EGO TYCHO BRAHE OTTONIDES haereditarius de Knudstrup, et Arcis Uraniburgi in insula Daniae Venusia Fundator et Praeses, tenore praesentium testor, quod harum exhibitor CHRISTIANUS SEVERINI F. Lymvicus, mihi in Astronomicis exercitijs per octennium ferme fideliter navaverit operam, et in caeteris praeterea se gesserit, uti virtutis et bonarum literarum amantem decere posset. Nunc vero cum a me missionem peteret, non invite dimittendum censui, suoque arbitrio relinquendum. Commendatum igitur volo omnibus cuiusque ordinis et status, ut illum sua humanitate et beneficentia complectantur. In cuius rei fidem sigillum meum apposui et manu mea propria subscripsi.</p><p>Actum Hafniae Calendis Iunij Anno 1597.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A testimonial given to Master Longomontanus by Tycho.</b></p><p>I, Tycho Brahe, son of Otte, hereditary lord of Knudstrup, and founder and director of the castle of Uraniborg on the Danish island of Venusia [Hven], by the tenor of these presents bear witness that the bearer of this [letter], Christian son of Søren [Severinus], of Longberg [Lymvicus], has for nearly eight years faithfully devoted his labour to me in astronomical pursuits, and has moreover conducted himself in all other respects as might befit a lover of virtue and of good letters. But now, since he sought his discharge from me, I judged that he was not to be let go unwillingly, and that he should be left to his own choice. I wish him therefore commended to all of whatever order and station, that they may embrace him with their kindness and beneficence. In faith whereof I have affixed my seal and have signed with my own hand.</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, on the Kalends of June [1 June], in the year 1597.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> Not a letter in the ordinary sense but an open <b>testimonial / letter of recommendation</b> (<i>testimonium</i>) — hence no named addressee in the dataset (`recipient: empty`); it is directed <i>omnibus cuiusque ordinis et status</i>, \"to all of whatever order and station.\" Surviving only as a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Langebek's Diplomatarium at the Danish National Archives (Rigsarkivet).</li><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), here styling himself in full: <i>Ottonides</i> (\"son of Otte\" — see protocol §5.6, <i>Otto / Ottonides</i> = Otte Brahe, Tycho's father), <i>haereditarius de Knudstrup</i> (\"hereditary lord of Knudstrup,\" the family seat in Scania), and <i>Arcis Uraniburgi … Fundator et Praeses</i> (\"founder and director of the castle of Uraniborg\"). Written at Copenhagen in June 1597, in the final months before Tycho left Denmark — Uraniborg's operations were by then winding down.</li><li><b>Subject / bearer.</b> <i>Christianus Severini F. Lymvicus</i> = <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (1562–1647), later the leading Tychonic astronomer of the next generation and professor at Copenhagen, author of the <i>Astronomia Danica</i> (1622). The Latin gives his patronymic literally: <i>Severini F[ilius]</i> = \"son of Søren (Severinus).\" <i>Lymvicus</i> is the Latin toponym from <b>Longberg / Lomborg</b> in Jutland (whence the humanist surname <i>Longomontanus</i>, \"of the long mountain\"); rendered \"of Longberg\" with the printed form flagged. The testimonial documents his service \"for nearly eight years\" (<i>per octennium ferme</i>) — he had joined Tycho on Hven c. 1589.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Hafniae</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b><i>missionem peteret</b></i> — \"sought his discharge / leave to depart\" (<i>missio</i> = an honourable release from service), not \"a mission.\" Translated as \"his discharge.\"</li><li><b><i>harum exhibitor</b></i> — \"the bearer of this [letter]\" (lit. \"the exhibitor of these [presents]\"), the standard formula by which a testimonial identifies the person carrying it.</li><li><b><i>tenore praesentium … In cuius rei fidem … manu mea propria subscripsi</b></i> — chancery/notarial formulae (\"by the tenor of these presents … in faith whereof … I have signed with my own hand\"); <i>manu propria</i> repeated as the subscription line, kept as the autograph attestation.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Actum Hafniae Calendis Iunij Anno 1597</i> = \"Done at Copenhagen, on the Kalends of June (1 June), 1597\" (O.S. — Denmark kept the Julian calendar). The Roman-calendar idiom <i>Calendis Iunij</i> is rendered with the idiom and glossed to the civil date per protocol §3 and §26. The as-written dateline (1 June 1597) agrees with the dataset label; <i>Actum</i> \"Done\" kept per §5.5.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a short, formulaic testimonial; the Latin is straightforward and the renderings secure. The only points worth flagging are non-cruxes: <i>Lymvicus</i> (the Longberg/Lomborg toponym, glossed) and <i>missionem</i> in its sense of \"discharge from service,\" both handled in the apparatus.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VII p.384 page image in full. Collated the Tycho/Ottonides/Knudstrup/Uraniborg self-styling, the octennium-of-faithful-service clause, the discharge-granted clause, the commendation-to-all-orders-and-stations clause, the seal-and-signature attestation, and the Copenhagen/Kalends-of-June 1597 date against source_text and published_english; source_text matches Dreyer word for word. Checked the epithet 'Lymvicus' rendered as 'of Longberg' — a defensible identification given the letter's own heading names the recipient Longomontanus (native of Lomborg), though the precise toponymic derivation cannot be independently confirmed from the page image alone; not flagged as a defect for lack of contrary evidence. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Certifies that Longomontanus served faithfully for nearly eight years in astronomical work, conducted himself virtuously, and is released at his own request with a commendation to all.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970601001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970616001", "d": "1597-06-16", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "David Chytraeus", "ck": "David Chytraeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "David Chytraeus → Tycho Brahe — [Rostock], 16 June 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DAVID CHYTRAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NOBILIS, generose et magnifice Domine. Gratulor non tam Magnificentiae vestrae, quam Academiae et urbi Rosarum, de incolumi in hanc urbem adventu vestro, quam vere Generosae Nobilitatis vestrae sapientia, virtus, doctrina et in Rempublicam literariam merita et Nominis vestri claritas, splendor ac gloria, excellenti virtute ac bene meritis parta, deinceps illustriorem et clariorem efficiet. Quod igitur CICERO alicubi in Orationibus exclamat: O terram illam beatam, quae hunc virum exceperit! patriam ingratam, si eiecerit, miseram, si amiserit! idem ego ijsdem verbis nunc usurpo, et Deum aeternum, suae sapientiae et virtutis radios, ingenij et virtutis Heroicae dona in mentes praestantium virorum effundentem, precor, ut Μ. V. incolumem et florentem perpetuo servet, et migrationem in hanc urbem clementer gubernet, ut paulo post eos, qui talem virum dimiserunt, erroris sui poeniteat, et quem retinere omnibus viribus non studuerunt, retrahere tandem ad se omni contentione enitantur. His coram Μ. V. ad nos advenienti gratulaturus eram, si accurrere ad M.V. per Arthritidem, quae me totum possedit, licuisset. Nunc, quae proficisci a mea tenuitate possunt officiola, reverenter defero, et pro eruditissimis ad me literis et volumine epistolarum, mecum superioribus mensibus communicato, reverenter gratias ago.</p><p>Bene et feliciter Μ. V. in aeternum valeat.<br>Postridie Viti, Anno 1597.<br>Μ. V. reverenter colens<br>DAVID CHYTRAeUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>DAVID CHYTRAEUS TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Noble, well-born and magnificent Master. I offer my congratulations not so much to Your Magnificence as to the University and the city of the Roses [Rostock], upon your safe arrival in this city — [an arrival] which the wisdom, virtue, and learning of your truly well-born Nobility, and your deserts toward the republic of letters, and the renown, splendour, and glory of your name, won by [your] excellent virtue and good deserts, will hereafter render the more illustrious and the more famous. That, therefore, which CICERO somewhere cries out in his Orations — \"O blessed [is] that land which shall have received this man! [O] ungrateful the homeland, if it cast him out; wretched, if it lose him!\" — I now take up in the very same words; and I pray the eternal God, who pours out the rays of His wisdom and virtue, the gifts of genius and of heroic virtue, into the minds of outstanding men, that He may keep Your Magnificence safe and flourishing for ever, and graciously govern your removal into this city, so that a little later those who let such a man go may repent of their error, and may at last strive with all their effort to draw back to themselves the man whom they did not endeavour to keep with all their might. I would have offered Your Magnificence these [congratulations] in person, as you arrived among us, had it been permitted me to hasten to Your Magnificence — [hindered] by the arthritis [gout] which has wholly possessed me. Now I reverently tender such small services as can proceed from my slenderness [of means], and I reverently give thanks for your most learned letter to me and for the volume of letters shared with me in the past months.</p><p>May Your Magnificence fare well and happily for ever.<br>The day after [the feast of] St. Vitus, the year 1597.<br>Reverently honouring Your Magnificence,<br>DAVID CHYTRAEUS.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>David Chytraeus</i> = <b>David Chytraeus</b> (Kochhafe, 1530–1600), the eminent Lutheran theologian and historian, professor at the <b>University of Rostock</b> and one of its dominant figures; a Melanchthonian, author of the <i>Chronicon Saxoniae</i>. He had been in correspondence with Tycho and is recorded by Dreyer as having written this friendly note on 16 June 1597 regretting that his health prevented him from paying his respects in person.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> The letter congratulates Tycho on his arrival at <b>Rostock</b> in June 1597, at the outset of his self-imposed exile from Denmark. The barbed passage about \"those who let such a man go\" / refused \"to keep [him] with all their might\" alludes, by transparent implication, to the Danish authorities whose withdrawal of support drove Tycho abroad; Chytraeus predicts they will come to regret it. The hedge is the author's own — he speaks of <i>those who let such a man go</i> without naming them — and is preserved rather than sharpened into a direct accusation against the Danish crown.</li><li><b><i>urbs Rosarum</b></i> — \"the city of the Roses,\" a learned epithet for <b>Rostock</b> (playing on <i>Rostochium</i> / the rose in the city arms); rendered \"the city of the Roses [Rostock].\" <i>Academia</i> here = the <b>University of Rostock</b>.</li><li><b>Μ. V.</b> — the abbreviation throughout (printed with a Greek capital mu, <i>Μ</i>, for Latin <i>M</i>) = <i>Magnificentia Vestra</i>, <b>\"Your Magnificence\"</b> — the standard honorific for a person of Tycho's rank; the Greek-letter mu is a compositor's substitution for the Latin majuscule and carries no separate sense. Rendered consistently \"Your Magnificence.\"</li><li><b>Cicero allusion.</b> <i>O terram illam beatam … miseram, si amiserit!</i> — Chytraeus quotes (acknowledging it as Cicero, <i>in Orationibus</i>) the apostrophe from Cicero, <i>De lege agraria</i> (Second Oration) II.xxxv.95, on Capua, here redeployed to Tycho and Denmark. The quotation is kept as a quotation and set off accordingly.</li><li><b><i>Arthritis</b></i> — period term for <b>gout / arthritic joint-disease</b> (\"which has wholly possessed me\"); glossed \"the arthritis [gout].\" Not silently modernised.</li><li><b><i>officiola</b></i> — diminutive of <i>officia</i>, \"small [dutiful] services / little kindnesses\"; the self-deprecating diminutive (paired with <i>mea tenuitate</i>, \"my slenderness [of means/ability]\") is preserved.</li><li><b><i>volumine epistolarum … superioribus mensibus communicato</b></i> — \"the volume of letters shared with me in the past months.\" Almost certainly Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Liber I, Uraniborg 1596), which he was then circulating to learned correspondents; identification likely but not stated in the letter, so left as the text gives it.</li><li><b>Negations.</b> Two negations occur and are reproduced as exactly two: (1) <i>non tam … quam</i> — the idiomatic \"not so much … as\" correlative (no true denial, a comparative emphasis), and (2) <i>quem retinere omnibus viribus non studuerunt</i> — \"the man whom they did <b>not</b> endeavour to keep with all their might.\" Neither is dropped or added.</li><li><b>Dateline.</b> <i>Postridie Viti, Anno 1597</i> = \"the day after [the feast of] St. <b>Vitus</b>.\" St. Vitus's day is <b>15 June</b>, so <i>postridie Viti</i> = <b>16 June 1597</b> (O.S.; Denmark and Lutheran Germany were still on the Julian calendar). This agrees with the dataset label (\"1597. 16. juni\") and with Dreyer's dating; no discrepancy. The place is not named in the dateline but is <b>Rostock</b>, established by the body (<i>in hanc urbem … urbi Rosarum</i>) and by Dreyer — hence supplied in brackets in the header.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is straightforward humanist epistolary prose, securely rendered; the Cicero source, the <i>urbs Rosarum</i> = Rostock epithet, the <i>Μ. V.</i> = <i>Magnificentia Vestra</i> reading, and the <i>Postridie Viti</i> = 16 June dating are all independently confirmed, and no crux remains open. (The only non-textual supplements — \"[Rostock]\" in the header, the identification of the <i>volumen epistolarum</i> — are bracketed or hedged in the apparatus.)</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.3-4 (page images, read visually). Latin matches Dreyer word for word. English renders every clause faithfully, including the Cicero quotation, the Rostock 'city of Roses' pun, and the date (Postridie Viti/day after St Vitus = 16 June, year 1597). No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "David Chytraeus", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15981007001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000328002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Congratulates Rostock on Tycho's arrival, quotes Cicero on the disgrace of expelling a great man from his homeland, and prays for Tycho's flourishing in the city.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970616001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970617001", "d": "1597-06-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Landgrave Moritz of Hesse-Kassel — Rostock, 17 June 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Marburg, Hessisches Staatsarchiv, Briefwechsel des Landgrafen Moritz (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO MAURITIO, HASSIAe ETC. LANDTGRAVIO, COMITI IN CATZENELNPOGEN, DIETZ, ZIGENHAIN et NIDDA ETC., DOMINO CLEMENTISSIMO.</p><p>ILLUSTRISSIME princeps, domine clementissime. Binas Celsit. Tuae per civem quendam Cassellanum literas mihi clementer missas, calendis ipsis junij accepi. Cum autem tunc in procinctu essem itineris Germanici, quemadmodum etiam sequenti statim die Haffnia discessi, meque huc Rostochium contuli (ubi etiamnum paulisper, donec res meae navigio adventarint, subsisto) nulla dabatur commoditas ijsdem respondendi et longe minus ea, quae Germanico idiomate exaratis continebantur, exequendi. Jn negotio Erici Langij, amici et affinis mei dilecti, quod T. C. tam clementem sedulam dederit operam, ut, quo in statu res eius versentur, quantoque aere alieno laboret, nobis innotesceret, est cur C. T. submisse gratias plurimas habeam. Verum incommode, et praeter opinionem accidit, quod hac aestate comitia regni (uti quotannis fieri solet) celebrata non sint, in quibus maxima pars nobilitatis Danicae convenire assolet, cum quorum aliquibus, praesertim ijs, qui Erico consanguinitatis vel affinitatis iure coniuncti essent, de debitis eius exolvendis (si qua ratione id obtinere possem) fideliter agere animus erat. Quin etsi comitia illa more solito successissent, nimis tamen sero is, qui T. C. literas, una cum debitorum Erici rationibus attulit, isthuc accesserat. Neque enim ultra quatuordecim dies post ascensionis festum procerum et nobilium regni in urbe illa congressus prorogari solent. Extra vero id tempus, illi, qui Erico aut sanguine, aut alia ratione coniuncti sunt, ita dispersi hinc inde habitant, ut eorum voluntates experiri, et simul cum illis de hoc negotio agere minime oportunum sit. Accessit et hoc, quod mihi, qui hac de re caeteros solicitare constitueram, iam tum e patria in Germaniam discedendum erat, adornato inde a paschatis festo itinere, ut ne propria quidem omni ex parte satis exequi, nedum aliena curare licuerit. Quare T:am C:m, quod in Erici rebus ob praedicta impedimenta nihil tunc temporis praestari potuerit, clementer excusatum habituram confido, et ut creditores eius adhuc aliquandiu, donec melior detur ratio atque occasio illis satisfaciendi, cum ipso contenti esse velint, sua authoritate et clementia effecturam.</p><p>Meae autem in Germaniam transmigrationis rationes nimis longum foret T. C. hic exponere. Neque etiam tempus et calamus id nunc patiuntur. Id saltem brevibus indicabo, quod, cum intellexissem eos qui nunc fatis apud nos praesunt politicis, minus Uraniae nostrae arduis laboribus favere, et eorum sufficientem sustentationem suis clancularijs consilijs avertere, nec ijs quae a Friderico secundo laudatissimae memoriae circa haec (approbantibus id post ipsius obitum omnibus regni senatoribus, literis confirmantibus) decreta erant, satis acquiescere, neque regem nostrum serenissimum alias optima indole et natura praeditum ita de his, uti decuit, admonere voluisse: existimavi, mature talibus difficultatibus subveniendum, ne omnia, quae tot iam annis in astronomiae instaurationem magno conamine, nec parvis sumptibus molitus sum, interirent. Quare huc in Germaniam sponte secessi, ut alias commoditates ea ipsa continuandi absolvendique quaererem, quo omnia in utilitatem reip. literariae ad posteritatem sarcta tecta transmitti possent. Qua in parte si T. C. nostris desiderijs consilio auxilioque subvenire non degravata fuerit, praestiterit rem, non saltem mihi, sed toti astronomico studio, quod parens tuus laudatissimae memoriae tanto amore prosequutus est, quo etiam ipsemet non modicum afficeris, percommodam, et cum eorum, qui nunc supersunt, tum etiam posterorum, grata memoria laudeque celebrandam. Et si T. C. hac de re quidpiam clementer ad me scribere, literasque illas ad producem Holsatiae dominum Henricum Ranzovium dirigere voluerit, ille eas facile isthuc, ubicunque locorum me versari noverit, perferri curabit. Porro icones instrumentorum meorum, quas transmiseram T:ae C:ni, esse redditas lubens cognovi, et pro opere canonis triangulorum (cuius quidem antea quaterna exemplaria coëmeram) vicissim mihi donato gratias submisse ago. Interim vero non possum ex literis T. C. aut aliunde percipere, utrum liber epistolarum astronomicarum, quem T:ae C:i, quod paternas mecum communicatas literas contineret, dedicandum censui (quem etiam ob id non ingratum futurum, mihi persuadebam) ad C:nis Tuae manus pervenerit, nec ne. Quod si exemplar a me transmissum intercidit, dabo operam, ut aliud per occasionem oblatam eidem substituatur: nisi forte e nundinis Francofurtensibus ultimis, ubi aliquot eiusdem libri exemplaria prostabant, eius copia facta sit. Sed desino T:m C:m pluribus nunc detinere, cum et ipsemet in hoc peregrino loco inter alia plurima negotia atque interpellationes vix haec ipsa scribendi otium nactus sim. Vbi maiori tranquillitati, favore numinis, restitutus fuero, plura etiam et accuratiora dare non intermittam. Interea haec festinanti calamo, et, citra commoditatem effusa T. C. clementer accipiat, ac diu feliciterque valeat.</p><p>Dabantur Rostochio die 17. junij anni 1597.<br>Tuae Cels, addictiss.<br>Tycho Brahe<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Illustrious Prince and Lord, Lord Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse etc., Count in Katzenelnbogen, Dietz, Ziegenhain and Nidda etc., his most gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most Illustrious Prince, most gracious Lord. The two letters of Your Highness, graciously sent to me by a certain citizen of Kassel, I received on the very Kalends of June [1 June]. But since I was then on the point of setting out on a journey into Germany — as indeed I departed from Copenhagen the very next day, and betook myself here to Rostock (where I am still tarrying a little while, until my effects arrive by ship) — there was no opportunity of replying to them, and far less of carrying out the things that were contained in those written in the German tongue. In the matter of Erik Lange, my beloved friend and kinsman by marriage, that Your Highness has given such gracious and diligent effort, that there might be made known to us in what state his affairs stand and with how great a debt he labours, there is reason why I should most humbly render Your Highness very many thanks. But it has fallen out inconveniently, and beyond expectation, that this summer the Diet of the realm (as is wont to be held each year) has not been held — at which the greatest part of the Danish nobility is accustomed to assemble; with some of whom, especially those who were joined to Erik by right of consanguinity or affinity, it was my intention to deal faithfully concerning the discharge of his debts (if by any means I could obtain it). Indeed, even had that Diet proceeded in the wonted manner, yet too late had he arrived there who brought Your Highness's letters together with the accounts of Erik's debts. For the gatherings of the grandees and nobles of the realm in that city are not wont to be prolonged beyond fourteen days after the feast of the Ascension. But outside that time, those who are joined to Erik either by blood or by some other tie dwell so scattered here and there that to sound out their wills, and at the same time to treat with them of this business, is by no means convenient. This too was added, that I myself — who had resolved to solicit the rest in this matter — had even then to depart out of my homeland into Germany, the journey having been prepared from the feast of Easter onward, so that I was not even permitted to attend to my own affairs adequately in every part, much less to see to another's. Wherefore I trust that Your Highness will graciously hold me excused that nothing could be accomplished at that time in Erik's affairs on account of the aforesaid hindrances; and that, by your authority and clemency, you will bring it about that his creditors may be willing to rest content with him yet a little while longer, until a better means and occasion of satisfying them be given.</p><p>But the reasons for my migration into Germany it would be too long to set forth here to Your Highness. Nor indeed do the time and the pen now permit it. This at least I shall indicate in brief: that, when I had understood that those who now preside over political destinies among us favoured but little the arduous labours of our Urania, and were diverting their adequate maintenance by their clandestine counsels, and were not sufficiently abiding by those things which had been decreed concerning these matters by Frederick II, of most praiseworthy memory (with all the senators of the realm approving it after his death, and confirming it by letters), nor had been willing duly to admonish, as was fitting, our most serene King — who is otherwise endowed with an excellent disposition and nature — about these things: I judged that such difficulties must be met betimes, lest all those things should perish which now for so many years I have laboured at, with great effort and at no small expense, towards the restoration of astronomy. Wherefore I have withdrawn hither into Germany of my own accord, that I might seek other facilities for continuing and completing those very labours, whereby everything might be transmitted, whole and entire, to posterity for the benefit of the republic of letters. In which part, if Your Highness will not be too burdened to assist our desires with counsel and aid, you will have rendered a service most opportune — not to me alone, but to the whole study of astronomy, which your father of most praiseworthy memory pursued with so great a love, and by which you yourself too are affected in no small degree — and one to be celebrated with the grateful remembrance and praise both of those who now survive and also of those to come. And if Your Highness will graciously be willing to write anything to me on this matter, and to direct those letters to the governor [<i>producem</i>] of Holstein, Lord Heinrich Rantzau, he will readily see to it that they are carried thither, wherever in the world he may know me to be staying. Further, I was glad to learn that the engravings of my instruments, which I had sent to Your Highness, were duly delivered; and for the work of the canon of triangles given me in return (of which I had indeed earlier bought four copies), I humbly render thanks. In the meantime, however, I cannot perceive from Your Highness's letters or from elsewhere whether the book of astronomical letters — which I judged should be dedicated to Your Highness, because it contained your father's letters shared with me (and which for that reason too I persuaded myself would not be unwelcome) — has come into Your Highness's hands or not. But if the copy sent by me has gone astray, I shall take care that another be substituted for it as occasion offers: unless perhaps a supply of it was had from the last Frankfurt fair, where several copies of the same book were on sale. But I cease now to detain Your Highness with more, since I myself too, in this foreign place, amid very many other affairs and interruptions, have scarcely found leisure to write these very lines. When I shall have been restored to greater tranquillity, by the favour of the deity, I shall not fail to send still more and more accurate things. In the meantime, may Your Highness graciously receive these things, poured out with a hurrying pen and without due care, and may you fare well long and happily.</p><p>Given at Rostock, on the 17th day of June in the year 1597.<br>Most devoted to Your Highness,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / context.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Rostock</b> in the first weeks of his self-imposed exile from Denmark. Having lost the favour of the regency government following Frederick II's death (1588) and the accession of the young Christian IV, Tycho had abandoned Hven in spring 1597, sailing from Copenhagen early in June; the letter is written during his stopover at Rostock while awaiting his effects by ship. He would go on to Wandsbek (the guest of Heinrich Rantzau), then to Prague.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Mauritius, Hassiae Landtgravius</i> = <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632), who had succeeded his father <b>Wilhelm IV</b> in 1592. The full address-style enumerates the subsidiary Hessian comital titles: <i>Catzenelnpogen</i> = <b>Katzenelnbogen</b>, <i>Dietz</i> = <b>Diez</b>, <i>Zigenhain</i> = <b>Ziegenhain</b>, <i>Nidda</i>.</li><li><b><i>Erici Langij, amici et affinis mei dilecti</b></i> — <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), Danish nobleman and alchemist, betrothed to Tycho's sister <b>Sophie Brahe</b> in 1590 (in Danish law a binding tie — hence <i>affinis</i>, \"kinsman by marriage\"). Ruined by his pursuit of <i>chrysopoeia</i>, Lange had fled his creditors and was then on the Continent in Moritz's orbit; Moritz had written to Tycho reporting on the state of Lange's debts, which Tycho here acknowledges and to which he replies that the failure of the annual Danish <i>comitia</i> (Diet) had frustrated his plan to lobby Lange's noble kinsmen for repayment.</li><li><b><i>producem Holsatiae dominum Henricum Ranzovium</b></i> — <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henricus Ranzovius, 1526–1598), the great Holstein humanist, royal <i>Statthalter</i> (governor; <i>produx/produx</i> here = lieutenant-governor) of the Danish duchies. Tycho names him as the relay-point for return correspondence; Rantzau was his host at Wandsbek shortly after.</li><li><b><i>liber epistolarum astronomicarum … dedicandum censui</b></i> — Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the printed correspondence with Wilhelm IV and Christoph Rothmann, which Tycho <b>dedicated to Moritz precisely because it contained his late father's letters</b> (\"paternas mecum communicatas literas\"). The corpus confirms the dedication-to-Moritz and its rationale (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; the dedication leaf (:)4r). Tycho is here anxiously inquiring whether the presentation copy reached Moritz, offering a replacement, and noting copies were on sale at the most recent <b>Frankfurt book fair</b> (<i>nundinae Francofurtenses</i>).</li><li><b><i>icones instrumentorum meorum</b></i> — \"the engravings of my instruments\": Tycho had sent Moritz a set of the instrument plates (the <i>icones</i>, later gathered in the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i>, 1598). <i>Icones</i> rendered \"engravings/figures\" per the instrument register.</li><li><b><i>opere canonis triangulorum</b></i> — \"the work of the canon of triangles,\" i.e. a printed table of trigonometric functions (<i>canon triangulorum</i> = a canon/table for the solution of triangles), sent to Tycho by Moritz in return; Tycho notes he had already bought four copies. <i>canon</i> kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Astronomy.</b> <i>astronomiae instaurationem</i> = \"the <b>restoration of astronomy</b>\" — Tycho's programmatic phrase (<i>instauratio astronomiae</i>), rendered per protocol §5.1. <i>Uraniae nostrae … arduis laboribus</i> = \"the arduous labours of our <b>Urania</b>\" (the Muse of astronomy, standing metonymically for Tycho's astronomical enterprise at Uraniborg).</li><li><b>Negation note (legal/political force).</b> The long central sentence carries a chain of complaints against the regency, governed by <i>cum intellexissem</i> and strung on a <i>minus … nec … neque</i> structure; the three negative limbs are reproduced exactly: (1) they <i>favoured but little</i> (<i>minus … favere</i>) Tycho's work and were <i>diverting</i> (<i>avertere</i>) its funding; (2) they were <i>not sufficiently abiding</i> (<i>nec … satis acquiescere</i>) by Frederick II's decrees; (3) they <i>had not been willing to admonish</i> (<i>neque … admonere voluisse</i>) the young King. No negation has been added or dropped. The conditional <i>si T. C. … non degravata fuerit</i> (\"if Your Highness will <b>not</b> be too burdened\") is a single litotes-style negative — a courteous request, not a prohibition — and is rendered as such. <i>clancularijs consilijs</i> = \"by their clandestine/secret counsels,\" kept at full pejorative force (no softening to \"private\").</li><li><b><i>sarcta tecta</b></i> — a fixed legal-administrative idiom (\"in good repair, whole and entire,\" lit. of buildings kept <i>sarta tecta</i>, \"patched and roofed\"); rendered \"whole and entire.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>calendis ipsis junij</i> = \"on the very <b>Kalends of June</b>\" (= 1 June); Roman-calendar idiom kept and glossed per §3. The dateline <i>Dabantur Rostochio die 17. junij anni 1597</i> = \"Given at Rostock, 17 June 1597\" agrees with the catalogue label; <b>O.S.</b> (Denmark and the Lutheran German principalities were still on the Julian calendar in 1597). <i>post ascensionis festum</i> / <i>a paschatis festo</i> — movable feasts (Ascension; Easter), kept as named feasts, not converted to civil dates.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Tuae Cels. addictiss.</i> = \"Most devoted to Your Highness\" (<i>Celsitudini Tuae addictissimus</i>, per §5.5); <i>manu propria</i> = \"with his own hand\" (the original is an autograph letter, per the MS witness).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the political-complaint sentence's negation-chain is reproduced limb-for-limb. The one soft point is <i>producem Holsatiae</i>: <i>produx</i> (var. <i>produx</i>) is a rare formation glossed here as \"governor/lieutenant-governor\" on the basis of Rantzau's office as royal <i>Statthalter</i> of the duchies (the more usual classical word would be <i>prorex</i>); the office is certain, the precise word-form is flagged rather than silently regularised.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — Latin secure, negation-chain limb-for-limb, date + autograph witness agree; only the rare word-form </i>produx<i> (office certain).</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.105-109 as page images (item 124, pp.106-108, heading confirmed by the Dreyer synopsis 'Mauritium Hassiae landgravium Tycho Brahe ... de demigratione sua e Dania certiorem facit'; p.105 is the tail of the prior item, p.109 the start of the next). Collated source_text against Dreyer's print sentence by sentence (no divergence beyond long-s/roman-s typography, which is ignored per instructions) and collated published_english against source_text clause by clause. Checked specifically: 'calendis ipsis junij' correctly glossed as 1 June; the closing date 'die 17. junij anni 1597' matches the title date, with no invented O.S./N.S. claim in the prose itself; 'quatuordecim dies post ascensionis festum' rendered as fourteen days after Ascension; 'quaterna exemplaria coëmeram' correctly rendered as four copies (matches in both source_text and Dreyer); Haffnia/Copenhagen, Rostochium/Rostock, Cassellanum/Kassel, Fridericus secundus/Frederick II, Henricum Ranzovium/Heinrich Rantzau all correctly identified; the subject of 'admonere voluisse' (the officials, not the king) and of 'quo etiam ipsemet ... afficeris' (Moritz, not Tycho) both correctly kept unflipped; the book of astronomical letters/dedication passage (dedicated to Moritz because it contained his father Wilhelm IV's letters) rendered accurately with no invented custody claim; no sentence found silently dropped, no invented clause, no invented closing. The one flagged term, 'producem' (governor) applied to Rantzau, is honestly bracketed with the Latin in the translation and is not a fabrication. No defects found in either direction (Latin-vs-Dreyer or English-vs-Latin).</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Marburg, Hessisches Staatsarchiv, Briefwechsel des Landgrafen Moritz. Original letter"], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the delay in replying due to his departure from Denmark, thanks Moritz for investigating Erik Lange's financial affairs and debts, and reports currently tarrying at Rostock.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970617001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970710001", "d": "1597-07-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → King Christian IV — [n.p.], 10 July 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV) · MS witnesses: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter, no. 178 (copy); B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>STUORMECTIGE hoyeborne allernaadigste herre. Nest min villige och troff thienistis vnderdanige formelding E: M: giffuer ieg paa det vnderdanigste thilkende, at som ieg ingen leylighedt haffde att komme E: K: M: thil orde, førend ieg affdrog, och ey viste, om dett kunde vere E: K: M: angenem eller icke, saa foraarsagis ieg nu E: K: M: paa dett korteste schrifftligen att lade vide, huis ieg ellers mundtligen haffde y sinde vnderdanigst att berette. Daa, effthersom ieg aff vngdommen haffuer haffdt sønderlig thilgeneyenhedt den löfflig astronomische konst grundelig att laere och forstaa och siden att bringe rett paa fode, och der offuer haffde achtit thilforn att bliffue vdi Tysklandt bequemmeligen dett att vdrette, saa haffuer E: K: M: herre fader, hoyeloufflige ihukommelse, der Hans Mayt: saadant fornam, naadigste begerit och bevegitt mig till ditt samme paa Huaen att anstille och giøre. Huilchett ieg och paa anden och tyffvende aar medt alsomstørste fliidt, vmage och bekaastning efftherkommit haffuer formeenendis mig nochsom dermedt haffue bevist, att ieg dett helst haffde villet giordt min egenn herre och koning och faederne landt thil aere aff yderste formue. Och haffde E: K: M: herre fader naadigste for gott anseedt och loffuit, att huis ieg der anrettit thil samme konst schulle medt en fundatz nødtorffteligen forsørgis och perpetueris, med flere guode wilkaar, som mig bleff naadigste thilsagdt. Huilchet E: K: M: frue moder, min naadigste droning, vden tuiffvell endnu dragis thil minde, och thilforn hoes Dannemarchis raadt haffuer ladit thilkende giffue, saa ieg derfor haffuer bekommit det hele Dannemarchis riges raadz obne pergmentz breff, som mig saadant haffuer confirmerit och viidere derudom fortrøstitt. Huorudoffuer ieg siden haffuer giordt en vsigelige stuoer vmage och bekaastning ydermere end thilforn forhaabendis, att naar E: K: M: kom thil regimentztidt, schulde da haffue en naadigste behag dertill, och lade mig och mine nyde dett gott att.</p><p>Men nu haffuer dett sig anderledis tildragitt en ieg haffde troffuit, huorudom ieg vil nu vformeldit lade videre omstendt. E: K: M: er vden tuiffvel bevist, att ieg er affschildt med huis ieg schulde haffue thil samme konstis vnderholding, och mig bleffuen opschreffuit, att E: K: M: war icke til sindz saadant att ville holde ved macht, foruden møgitt andit, som vformodendis er thilkommit, och (som ieg achter) vden min schyld och brøde er hendit. Och efftherdi ieg endelig aff den naade, som Gud giffuer, faaer att fuldkomme huis ieg engang medt saa stuoer alvorlighedt och y lang tidt fortagitt och dreffuit haffuer, huilchet och mange nationer vitterligt er, och stuorligen adtraader, och icke sielff haffuer y formue dett nochsom att bekaaste; thi ieg alrede er kommen saa thil achters, der ieg endskønt haffde de forrige forlaeninger, att ieg alligvel nøddis thil att affhende alt mitt faederne guodtz. Forseer ieg mig till, att E: K: M: naadigste anseer saadan min nødtorfftighedt och icke tager denne min vdreyße thil nogen vnaade, efftherdi att ieg aff diße och flere aarsager saa hoyeligen trengdis att søge anden middel och raadt, att dett som well er begyndt kand bliffue thilbørligen fuldkommit, och ieg mitt guode naffn och rycte y fremmede land kand vidt macht holde. Dog er ieg icke y den mening vddragen, att ieg aldelis ville forlade mitt faederne land, mens ickon att vere vde om hielp och bistandt hoes andre herrer och potentater, om saa mugligt kunde vere, paa dett ieg icke schulde were E: K: M: eller rigett formøgitt thil besuering. Dersom dett kunde bliffue mig forgyntt, att ieg y Dannemarck maa min forthagne arbeytt forfølge, vil ieg dett icke forsige; thi ieg endnu som thilforn alsomhelst E: K: M for nogen andre potentater, och mitt egen faederne rige thil aere och loufflig effthersagn, alt huis ieg aff yderste formue kunde vdrette, saa fremt dett kunde skee med lidelig vilkaar och vden min skade. Huis och icke, vil ieg dog, huor dett er forseet, att ieg anderstedz skal bliffue, stedtze vere E: K: M: med all loufflig effthersagn och vnderdanighedt aff min ringe formue villig. Settendis dett vnderdanigst til E: K: M: guode bethenckende, att ieg ingenlunde aff nogen lettferdighedt (helst paa denne alder, ieg er offuer halfftridiesintyffue aar, och icke med ringe huesgesindt beladen, huilche ieg med stuoer vgeleyenhedt nødis att giøre fremmede) drager aff mitt faederne land og fra min guode slecht och venner, och kandt dett som endnu er paa Huaen aff mig giordt, nochsom vdviße, att icke thilforn haffde werit min acht och mening saaledes att ville drage derfra. Forhaabis derfore vnderdanigste, naar E: K: M: dette medt sine omstendighedt rett betrackter, att E: K: M: werer och bliffuer min naadigste herre och konningh, och mig och mine medt al kongelig gunst och naade tilbevogenn. Jeg wil althid findis E: K: M: vnderdanig, throff och villig aff min yderste formue, huor Gud almegtigste mig min leylighedt haffuer forsiunit. Denn samme guode Gudt, som raader for all wertzlige regimente, vnde E: K: M: vdi sin kongelige regieringh lycke, velsignelße, guode raad och anßlagh.</p>", "en": "<p>Most Mighty, high-born, most gracious Lord. After the humble tender of my willing and faithful service, I make known to Your Majesty most humbly that, since I had no opportunity to speak with Your Royal Majesty before I departed, and did not know whether it might be agreeable to Your Royal Majesty or not, I am therefore now moved to let Your Royal Majesty know in writing, as briefly as may be, what otherwise I had it in mind humbly to report by word of mouth. Now then, inasmuch as from my youth I have had a singular inclination thoroughly to learn and understand the laudable art of astronomy and afterwards to set it rightly upon its feet, and had on that account formerly intended to settle in Germany so as conveniently to accomplish it, Your Royal Majesty's lord father, of high and laudable memory, when His Majesty perceived this, most graciously desired and moved me to set about and do that same thing on Hven. Which thing I have also, in the two-and-twentieth year [now], carried out with the very greatest diligence, labour, and expense, presuming that I have thereby sufficiently shown that I would most gladly have done it to the honour of my own lord and king and of [my] fatherland, [and] to the utmost of my ability. And Your Royal Majesty's lord father had most graciously seen fit and promised that whatever I there set up for that art should, by a foundation [<i>fundatz</i>], be sufficiently provided for and perpetuated, together with several other good conditions which were most graciously assured to me. Which Your Royal Majesty's lady mother, my most gracious Queen, without doubt still calls to mind, and beforehand had it made known to the Council of Denmark, so that I therefore obtained the whole Council of the realm of Denmark's open parchment letter, which confirmed such [provision] to me and further gave me assurance thereof. Wherefore I have since taken upon myself an unspeakably great labour and expense, yet more than before, in the hope that, when Your Royal Majesty should come to the time of [your own] rule, Your Majesty would then have a most gracious pleasure therein, and would let me and mine enjoy the good of it.</p><p>But now it has fallen out otherwise than I had believed, the particulars whereof I will for the present leave further unmentioned. Your Royal Majesty is without doubt aware that I have been deprived of what I should have had for the maintenance of that same art, and [that] it was put to me in writing [<i>opschreffuit</i>] that Your Royal Majesty was not minded to wish to uphold such [provision] in force — besides much else that has come about unlooked-for, and (as I judge) has befallen without my fault or offence. And inasmuch as I must in the end, by the grace that God gives, complete what I have once undertaken and pursued with such great earnestness and over a long time — a thing which is also known to many nations, and greatly desired [by them] — and do not myself have it in [my] means sufficiently to defray it; for I am already come so far behind [in means] — even though I had the former fiefs — that I am nevertheless constrained to dispose of all my patrimonial estate: I trust that Your Royal Majesty will most graciously regard such my necessity, and will not take this my journeying-out [<i>vdreyße</i>] into any disfavour, since for these and several causes I was so highly pressed to seek other means and counsel, so that what is well begun may be duly completed, and I may keep my good name and repute abroad in full force. Yet I have not journeyed out with the intent that I would altogether forsake my fatherland, but only to be abroad [seeking] help and assistance from other lords and potentates, if so it might be possible, to the end that I should not be too much of a burden to Your Royal Majesty or to the realm. If it could be granted me that in Denmark I may pursue my undertaken work, I will not refuse it; for I, now as before, [would] most willingly [serve] Your Royal Majesty before any other potentates, and to the honour and laudable repute of my own fatherland's realm [do] all that I could accomplish to the utmost of my ability — provided it could be done on tolerable conditions and without my hurt. And if not, yet — wherever it is provided that I shall remain elsewhere — I will at all times be willing [to serve] Your Royal Majesty with all laudable repute and humility, according to my slender ability. Submitting it most humbly to Your Royal Majesty's good consideration, that I do by no means out of any levity (least of all at this [my] age — I am over fifty years old, and burdened with no small household [<i>huesgesindt</i>], which with great inconvenience I am constrained to make strangers [abroad]) depart from my fatherland and from my good kindred and friends; and that what is even yet on Hven, made by me, may sufficiently show that it had not formerly been my purpose and intent thus to wish to depart from there. I most humbly hope, therefore, that when Your Royal Majesty rightly considers this with all its circumstances, Your Royal Majesty will be and remain my most gracious Lord and King, and [will be] favourably disposed toward me and mine with all royal grace and favour. I will at all times be found humble, faithful, and willing toward Your Royal Majesty to the utmost of my ability, wherever God Almighty shall have provided me my occasion. May the same good God, who governs all worldly rule, grant Your Royal Majesty in your royal governance good fortune, blessing, good counsel, and good resolve.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601) to <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), then aged 20 and reigning in his own right since the regency ended in 1596. This is Tycho's apologia written shortly after he had quit Hven and Copenhagen in the spring of 1597 — among the most consequential letters in his correspondence, justifying his departure from Denmark while leaving the door open to a return.</li><li><b>Place.</b> No place is named in the dateline as transmitted; the body's deictic <i>\"paa Huaen … aff mig giordt\"</i> (\"on Hven … made by me\") treats Hven as remote. Tycho had withdrawn to Copenhagen (and soon Rostock); the witnesses are later copies (<i>Afskrift</i>), and Dreyer prints no place-line. The header therefore gives <b>[n.p.]</b>.</li><li><b>Document type / witnesses.</b> Two later manuscript copies: <b>A</b> = KB, <i>Langebeks Ekscerpter</i> no. 178; <b>B</b> = Rigsarkivet, <i>Langebeks Diplomatarium</i> — both eighteenth-century transcripts (Langebek), not the original. Shelfmarks reproduced as given.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> No explicit dateline survives in the transcribed text; the dataset label is <i>\"1597. 10. juli\"</i> (10 July 1597), Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). As the body furnishes no internal date, the header follows the catalogue label and is marked <b>[O.S.]</b>; no in-letter date discrepancy arises (none is stated).</li><li><b>Chancery-Danish orthography (c. 1597).</b> <i>E: K: M:</i> = <i>Eders Kongelige Majestæt</i> (\"Your Royal Majesty\"); <i>E: M:</i> = <i>Eders Majestæt</i>. <i>Nest … formelding</i> = <i>Næst … formælding</i> (\"after the tendering [of service]\"). <i>troff</i> = <i>tro</i> (\"faithful\"); <i>affdrog</i> = <i>afdrog</i> (\"departed\"); <i>löfflig / loufflig</i> = <i>lovlig</i> (\"laudable, lawful\"); <i>thilgeneyenhedt</i> = <i>tilgenegenhed</i> (\"inclination\"); <i>bequemmeligen</i> = <i>bekvemmeligen</i> (\"conveniently\"); <i>anstille</i> = <i>anstille</i> (\"set about, arrange\"); <i>forlaeninger</i> = <i>forleninger</i> (\"fiefs, enfeoffments\"); <i>affhende</i> = <i>afhænde</i> (\"dispose of, alienate\"); <i>forgyntt</i> = <i>forundt</i> (\"granted, vouchsafed\"); <i>forsige</i> = <i>forsige</i> (\"refuse, decline\"); <i>tilbevogenn</i> = <i>tilbevågen</i> (\"favourably disposed\"); <i>anßlagh</i> = <i>anslag</i> (\"resolve, design\"). Long-<i>ß</i> and <i>w/v/u</i> variation are chancery features.</li><li><b><i>fundatz</b></i> — a <b>foundation</b> (endowment/charter), the Latin-derived chancery term for the endowed establishment Frederik II had promised would perpetually maintain the Uraniborg programme; kept as a technical term, glossed.</li><li><b><i>E: K: M: herre fader</b></i> = <b>Frederik II</b> (r. 1559–88), who granted Tycho the fief of Hven in 1576. <b><i>frue moder, min naadigste droning</b></i> = the Queen Dowager <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> (1557–1631), Christian IV's mother. <b><i>Dannemarchis riges raadz obne pergmentz breff</b></i> = the <b>open letter (charter) of the Council of the Realm</b>, on parchment, confirming the endowment.</li><li><b><i>paa anden och tyffvende aar</b></i> — \"in the two-and-twentieth year\": Tycho counts from the 1576 grant, making 1597 roughly the twenty-second year of his work on Hven. Rendered as written; no silent arithmetic adjustment.</li><li><b><i>regimentztidt</b></i> — \"the time of [Christian's] rule\" (i.e. his majority/personal reign); Tycho had hoped the young king's accession to full power would secure the endowment.</li><li><b>Age statement / numeral.</b> <i>\"ieg er offuer halfftridiesintyffue aar\"</i> — <i>halfftridiesintyffue</i> = <i>halvtredsindstyve</i>, the Old Danish vigesimal numeral for <b>fifty</b> (<i>halvtredje sinde tyve</i> = \"[the] half-third times twenty\" = 2½ × 20 = 50), the source of modern Danish <i>halvtreds</i> \"50.\" So <i>\"offuer halfftridiesintyffue aar\"</i> = <b>\"over fifty years [old].\"</b> Tycho, born 14 December 1546, was 50 in July 1597 — internally consistent. The translation reads <b>\"over fifty.\"</b> The trap to avoid is reading the <i>halv-</i> element as \"half of [the next ten]\" to yield 55; that is wrong here — the figure is 50. <i>(Numeral flagged for the vigesimal false-friend; the reading itself is secure.)</i></li><li><b><i>huesgesindt</b></i> = <i>husgesinde</i> (\"household, household retinue/servants\"); <i>\"icke med ringe huesgesindt beladen\"</i> = \"burdened with no small household,\" whom Tycho would have to uproot and \"make strangers\" (resettle abroad).</li><li><b><i>affschildt med huis ieg schulde haffue</b></i> = \"deprived of [<i>afskilt med</i> = parted/separated from] what I should have had\" — i.e. the revenues/fiefs withdrawn. <b><i>opschreffuit</b></i> = <i>opskrevet</i> (\"put to [me] in writing / notified in writing\").</li><li><b>Negation audit.</b> Six negations carry the letter's legal and rhetorical force and are reproduced exactly: (1) <i>\"angenem eller <b>icke</b>\"</i> = \"agreeable … or not\"; (2) <i>\"E: K: M: war <b>icke</b> til sindz … att ville holde ved macht\"</i> = \"was <b>not</b> minded to wish to uphold [it]\"; (3) <i>\"<b>icke</b> sielff haffuer y formue\"</i> = \"do <b>not</b> myself have the means\"; (4) <i>\"<b>icke</b> tager denne min vdreyße thil nogen vnaade\"</i> = \"will <b>not</b> take this my departure into any disfavour\"; (5) <i>\"er ieg <b>icke</b> y den mening vddragen, att ieg aldelis ville forlade mitt faederne land\"</i> = \"I have <b>not</b> journeyed out with the intent that I would altogether forsake my fatherland\"; (6) <i>\"paa dett ieg <b>icke</b> schulde were … formøgitt thil besuering\"</i> = \"that I should <b>not</b> be too much of a burden\"; plus <i>\"vil ieg dett <b>icke</b> forsige\"</i> = \"I will <b>not</b> refuse it,\" and <i>\"att <b>icke</b> thilforn haffde werit min acht … saaledes att ville drage derfra\"</i> = \"that it had <b>not</b> formerly been my intent thus to depart from there.\" The conditional <i>\"Dersom dett kunde bliffue mig forgyntt … vil ieg dett icke forsige\"</i> is genuinely concessive (a real offer to stay if the conditions are met); rendered as such, not sharpened into a demand.</li><li><b><i>vformeldit lade videre omstendt</b></i> — \"leave the further particulars unmentioned\": <i>uformeldt</i> (\"unmentioned, unreported\"); <i>omstendt</i> = <i>omstænder/omstændigheder</i> (\"circumstances, particulars\"). Tycho pointedly declines to detail his grievances.</li><li><b>No Greek, no astronomical symbols, no verse</b> occur in this letter; the only technical term is <i>fundatz</i> (above).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely and the negation-count is reproduced exactly (eight negations; the concessive conditional kept concessive, not sharpened into a demand). Soft points, flagged not smoothed: (i) the vigesimal numeral <i>halfftridiesintyffue</i> = <b>50</b> (not the false-friend 55) — the internal datum \"over fifty years\" is consistent with Tycho's December-1546 birth; (ii) <i>paa anden och tyffvende aar</i> (\"in the two-and-twentieth year\") is rendered literally and not reconciled against the 1576 grant; (iii) the dateline carries no place-line in the transmitted copies, so the header reads [n.p.] and the [O.S.] date follows the catalogue label rather than an in-letter dateline.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — chancery Danish secure, eight negations exact, the numeral '50' explicitly secure; the missing place-line is the copies' own state.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish text (TBOO XIV pp.108-111, letter no.125) word-for-word against Dreyer's page images, including the opening address, the whole body, and the closing formula. Verified proper names (Christian IV, his royal father and mother), numbers (\"paa anden och tyffvende aar\" = 22nd year; \"offuer halfftridiesintyffue aar\" = over fifty years, correctly decoded from the old Danish vigesimal count 'halvtredsindstyve'), and confirmed the English is a complete, accurate rendering with no omissions, fabrications, or mistranslations. Dreyer's Latin critical apparatus on pp.110-111 (variant readings between the Oligerus Rosenkrantz and Rosenholm exemplars) confirms our source_text follows the base/principal reading, not a marginal variant.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter, nr. 178. Afskrift", "B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15941108001", "next": "brahe-t_15971008001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15941108001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15971008001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Recounts twenty-two years of astronomical work on Hven at Frederick II's encouragement and justifies his departure, having had no opportunity for a royal audience beforehand.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970710001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970729001", "d": "1597-07-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg — Rostock, 29 July 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German, courtly/chancery register, with Latin formulae) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>COPIA LITERARUM MISSARUM AD DUCEM VLDARICUM.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER hochgebornner furste. E:F:G:sein meine vntherthenighe, bereidtwillige dienste allzeit bevor. Gnediger herr, demnach E:F:G:in allen gnadenn mir vnd meinen studis zugethon zusein, ich hiebevornn gnuchsam vernohmenn, welches auch vnter andern E:F:G:do mit gnedichlich beweisen, das E:F:G: mich fur etzlichen Jahren ihn meiner behausung auff der insell Huenna gnedichlich zu besuchenn, vnnd meinne daselbst angerichtete sachen zu besehenn gewirdiget, daafür ich nochmals E:F:G:mit vnterthenigkeitt danchen thue.</p><p>So kan E:F:G:zu vermelden jch nit vnterlasen, welcher gestalt ich gewiser hochwichtiger vrsachen halben, die viell anders beschaffen, als vieleicht in gemein davonn magk geredet werden, deren vmbstende jch doch alhie will vorbey gehen, dieweill es in die fedder allis nicht woll zu bringen ist, aus meinenn lieben vatterlandt Dennemarck mich begeben, vnd doch aus sonderlichen bedencken bißanhero nicht hab weitters ziehen wollen alls in E:F:G: stadt Rostogk, welche gleich auf der grendze zwischen Deutschlandt vndt Dennemarcke belegen, zweiffuell auch nicht, wen E:F:G: die vmbstende an gerechter vrsachen, so mich zu solchem genotdrenget, daß jch in diesem meinen hohen alter, vndt mit nicht geringen haußgesinde, beladen, alles auf der jnsell Huenna, so ich mit vielfeltiger vnd fast vngleublicher muhe vnd bekostung vber die zwandzigk jhar angerechtet vnd ins werck gebracht hab, verlassen, mich vnd de meinen mit groser vngelegenheit frembd machen musßen, grundtlich erfahren werden. Es werden E:F:G:zu herdzen annehmen, vnd darüber ein gnediges mitleiden haben, das der woll angefangen vndt fast vollendte trefflich arbeit, welche sich vielle hohes standes, vnd andere vorneme gelaertte leutte, hin und wieder schier inn gantze Europa seher woll gefallen lasßen, vnd ein groses verlangen darnach gehabt, vndt noch haben, das diese hochnudzbare arbeit, welche ihnn viellhundert jaren niemandtz mit sulcher muhe vndt fleis, jedoch mir ohne ruhm zu reden, sich vnterstehen wollenn, ann den tagk gebracht, vnd zu gemeinen nutz publicirt werden möchten, so plötzlich vnnd vnuermutlich vnterlasen, vnd fast zu nicht soll gemacht werden. Dahero zwar meinen gnedigsten kunig vnd lieben vatterlandt, welchem sulches furnemblich zu ehren vnd löblicher nachsag angestellet war, bey frembden nationen vielleicht nictt woll wirt außgeleget, das solche löbliche werck nictt itzo so woll als bey könnig Friderichs, hochlöblicher gedechtnuß, zeitten, wie auch ihm nachfolgenden interregno, solte gebürlicher weis erhalten vnd bestettiget werden, welches mir warlich vonn hertzen wehe thutt, das sulches meinen gnedigsten herren vnd kunigh vnd vielgeliepten vatterlandt von meinent wegen nach geredet sein solthe, vnd hette giernne geheim, das keine notwendigkeit dieses verursacht, wie ich dan auch lange zeit mitt groser gedult alles ertragen, vnd beserung jedoch vergeblich gehoffet. Wo vonn zwar viell zu berichten were, wen ichs nicht dafur achten thette, daß es beser sey alles noch zur zeit in stillheit pleiben zu lasßen, vnd dieweill ichs vor mein person nictt hab endern können, muß ichs es auch Got vnd der zeit alßo befehellen. Derwegen ich mich zu E:F:G:in vnterthenigkeit versehe, E: F: G: werden darob, daß ich allhie jn E:F:G:statt mit sampt meinen haußgesinde ein zeit langk, biß die gelegenheit sich anders erreuget verhalte, kein vngnedigen mißgefallen tragen, sondern mit Dero F: gnaden vnd gunst wie hiebeuor gnediglich gegen mich bewogen sein vnd bleiben. Bitte darneben dienstliches fleisses, es wollen E:F:G:mir mit gutten ratt gnedichlich erscheinen, wie etwa diesße hochlöbliche astronomische sachen, die ich in so viel jaren mit vnglauplicher mue vnd vnkosten getreiben vnd vorgesetzt hab, ferner befordert mugen werden. Das im fhall es ie in Dennemarck über hoffnung hernacher nit meher gescheen solte, das sölche löbliche kunst alda gehandthabt vnd befurdert, dennoch anderswo im römischen reich demselben zu ehrenn durch notturfftige mittell vnd ersprießliche furschub erhalten vndt ad posteros continuirt werden möge. Daran E:F:G:nict alleinne mir vnd den meinen eine gnedige gutthat betzeigen wirt, sondern es werden auch E:F:G:das E:F:G:die hochlöbliche vhralte kunst alßo befördert, bey mennichliche vnd sonderlich bey den nachkommen grosen rühm, lob vnd ehr erlangen. Vnd ich soll es auch in meinen publicandis operibus mit aller danckbarkeit erkennen, vnd löblich zu preisen nict vntterlasßen. Thue hiemis E:F:G:zu langwiriger gesondtheit vnd freidsammer regerung dem gnedigen schudz des Allmechtigsten, mit sampt E: F: G: viellgeliepten gemallin, getrewlich befehellen.</p><p>Dattum Rostogk, den 29. jullij a:o 1597.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of the letter sent to Duke Ulrich.</b></p><p>Most Serene, high-born Prince. May my humble, ready services ever be commended to Your Princely Grace beforehand. Gracious lord: inasmuch as I have heretofore amply learned that Your Princely Grace is graciously devoted to me and to my studies — which Your Princely Grace also, among other things, graciously showed therein, that some years ago Your Princely Grace deigned graciously to visit me in my dwelling on the island of Hven, and to view the works I had set up there — for which I once more render thanks to Your Princely Grace with all humility.</p><p>So I cannot forbear to relate to Your Princely Grace in what manner I have, on certain weighty causes — which are constituted quite otherwise than perhaps may be commonly spoken of, the particulars whereof I will nevertheless here pass over, since it is not all well to be committed to the pen — betaken myself away from my dear fatherland Denmark; and yet, out of particular considerations, have until now not wished to remove any further than into Your Princely Grace's city of Rostock, which lies just upon the border between Germany and Denmark. Nor do I doubt that Your Princely Grace will thoroughly come to understand the particulars of the just causes that have driven me to this — namely, that I, burdened in this my advanced age, and with no small household, have had to abandon everything on the island of Hven (which over more than twenty years I set up and brought into being with manifold and almost incredible toil and expense), and to make myself and mine, with great inconvenience, strangers [abroad]. Your Princely Grace will take it to heart, and have a gracious compassion thereupon, that the well-begun and nearly completed excellent work — in which many of high estate, and other eminent learned men, here and there well-nigh throughout all Europe, have taken great pleasure, and have had, and still have, a great longing — that this most useful work, which in many hundreds of years no one would venture upon with such toil and diligence (yet I speak it without boasting), brought to light and published for the common good, is so suddenly and unexpectedly to be left off and almost brought to naught. Whence, to be sure, it may perhaps not be well construed among foreign nations of my most gracious king and dear fatherland — to whose honour and laudable repute this was chiefly undertaken — that such a laudable work should not now be maintained and confirmed in fitting wise, as it was in the times of King Frederick, of most laudable memory, and likewise in the interregnum that followed him; which truly grieves me from the heart, that such a thing should be said reproachfully of my most gracious lord and king and much-beloved fatherland on my account; and I would gladly have kept secret that no necessity caused this, even as I have also for a long time borne everything with great patience, and yet hoped for amendment in vain. Whereof, to be sure, there were much to report, did I not deem it better that for the present all should be left to rest in silence; and since for my own person I have not been able to change it, I must commit it likewise to God and to time. Therefore I trust myself to Your Princely Grace in all humility, that Your Princely Grace will bear no ungracious displeasure that I tarry here in Your Princely Grace's city, together with my household, for a time, until the occasion show itself otherwise, but will, with your princely favour and grace, be and remain graciously disposed toward me as heretofore. I beg besides, with dutiful diligence, that Your Princely Grace will graciously favour me with good counsel as to how these most laudable astronomical matters, which I have pursued and prosecuted over so many years with incredible labour and cost, may be further advanced — so that, in case beyond hope it should hereafter no longer come to pass in Denmark that such a laudable art be there practised and furthered, it may nevertheless elsewhere in the Roman Empire, to the honour of that same [art], be preserved by needful support and profitable furtherance, and continued unto posterity [ad posteros]. Wherein Your Princely Grace will show a gracious benefaction not to me and mine alone, but Your Princely Grace will also, by thus furthering the most laudable, most ancient art, attain great renown, praise, and honour with everyone and especially with those who come after. And I too shall acknowledge it with all thankfulness in my works to be published, nor fail to praise it laudably. Herewith I faithfully commend Your Princely Grace, together with Your Princely Grace's much-beloved consort, to the gracious protection of the Almighty, unto long-lasting health and a peaceful reign.</p><p>Given at Rostock, the 29th of July, in the year 1597.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / document type.</b> The text is <b>Tycho Brahe's own retained copy</b> of a letter he sent, headed by the Latin rubric <i>COPIA LITERARUM MISSARUM AD DUCEM VLDARICUM</i> (\"Copy of the letter sent to Duke Ulrich\"); the code-switch into Latin for the archival rubric over a German body is preserved (protocol §2). The witness is a later copy in Langebek's Diplomatarium (Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet). No autograph original is cited.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Duke Ulrich</i> = <b>Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b> (Ulrich von Mecklenburg-Güstrow, 1527–1603), the senior reigning Duke of Mecklenburg, sovereign of the Baltic region of which <b>Rostock</b> was the chief city. The letter recalls that the Duke had years earlier honoured Tycho with a visit to <b>Hven</b> (Uraniborg). Tycho, who had departed Copenhagen for Rostock on 2 June 1597, here petitions the Duke for protection and patronage during his exile.</li><li><b>Context.</b> Written at the outset of Tycho's voluntary exile from Denmark. Frustrated under the new regime following King Frederik II's death (1588) and the minority/interregnum before Christian IV's effective majority, Tycho had abandoned Hven. The letter is at once a courtesy, an apologia (he insists his fatherland and king should not be reproached on his account), and a request for counsel on continuing his astronomical work — anticipating that it may have to be carried on <b>elsewhere in the Roman Empire</b> rather than in Denmark.</li><li><b>Honorific abbreviations.</b> <i>E:F:G:</i> = <i>Euer Fürstliche Gnaden</i>, \"Your Princely Grace\" — the standard address to a reigning duke; rendered consistently, the repeated honorific not abbreviated away. <i>Dero F: gnaden</i> = <i>Dero Fürstliche Gnaden</i>, \"your princely grace/favour.\" <i>Durchleuchtiger hochgebornner Fürst</i> = \"Most Serene, high-born Prince.\"</li><li><b>Latin code-switches within the German.</b> <i>studis</i> (= <i>studiis</i>, \"studies\"); <i>interregno</i> (\"the interregnum,\" the period after Frederik II's death); <i>publicat … werden</i> and <i>publicandis operibus</i> (\"published … / in my works to be published\" — Latin <i>publicare</i>, Tycho's term for issuing his work to the public); <i>ad posteros continuirt</i> (\"continued unto posterity [ad posteros]\"). These macaronic Latin nouns/verbs in a German matrix are a chancery-letter feature (protocol §2); the Latin is kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Persons / places named.</b> <i>König Friderich(s) hochlöblicher gedechtnuß</i> = <b>King Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), \"of most laudable memory\" (i.e. deceased), Tycho's patron who granted him Hven in 1576. <i>meinen gnedigsten Kunig</i> (\"my most gracious king\") = the reigning <b>Christian IV</b> (king from 1588, crowned 1596), unnamed. <i>Insell Huenna</i> = the island of <b>Hven</b>, site of Uraniborg. <i>Dennemarck</i> = Denmark; <i>Rostogk</i> = <b>Rostock</b>; <i>römisches Reich</i> = the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b> (\"the Roman Empire\"). <i>Deutschlandt</i> = Germany.</li><li><b><i>die hochlöbliche, vhralte Kunst</b></i> — \"the most laudable, most ancient art,\" i.e. astronomy; <i>astronomische Sachen</i> = \"astronomical matters.\" Tycho's instauration programme is in view (\"the well-begun and nearly completed excellent work\").</li><li><b>Negations (verified count).</b> The conditional clause beginning <i>Das im fhall es ie in Dennemarck über hoffnung hernacher nit meher gescheen solte, das sölche löbliche kunst alda gehandthabt vnd befurdert …</i> carries two negating elements — <i>nit meher</i> (\"no longer\") governing the whole protasis — translated \"in case beyond hope it should hereafter no longer come to pass in Denmark that such a laudable art be there practised and furthered.\" The apodosis (<i>dennoch anderswo … erhalten … continuirt werden möge</i>) is positive: \"it may nevertheless elsewhere … be preserved … and continued.\" The \"not\" attaches to the Danish-continuation clause only, not to the Empire clause; logic and count preserved. The earlier <i>hette giernne geheim, das keine notwendigkeit dieses verursacht</i> (\"I would gladly have kept secret that no necessity caused this\") likewise reproduces the single internal negation <i>keine</i> without addition.</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> <i>die viell anders beschaffen, als vieleicht in gemein davonn magk geredet werden</i> (\"which are constituted quite otherwise than perhaps may be commonly spoken of\") and <i>vielleicht nictt woll wirt außgeleget</i> (\"may perhaps not be well construed\") keep Tycho's own <i>vieleicht</i>/<i>vielleicht</i> (\"perhaps\"); not sharpened.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datum Rostogk, den 29. jullij a:o 1597</i> = \"Given at Rostock, the 29th of July, in the year 1597.\" Denmark and Mecklenburg both kept the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1597; the date is O.S. The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1597. 29. juli. Rostock</i>); no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Orthography (Early New High German, c. 1597).</b> <i>vntherthenighe</i> = <i>untertänige</i> (\"humble\"); <i>behausung</i> = <i>Behausung</i> (\"dwelling\"); <i>gewirdiget</i> = <i>gewürdigt</i> (\"deigned\"); <i>bekostung</i> = <i>Bekostung</i> (\"expense\"); <i>haußgesinde</i> = <i>Hausgesinde</i> (\"household servants/family\"); <i>frembd machen</i> (\"to make [oneself] a stranger/an exile\"); <i>genotdrenget</i> = <i>genötdrängt/genötigt</i> (\"constrained, driven\"); <i>nach geredet</i> = <i>nachgeredet</i> (\"said reproachfully, defamed\"); <i>fhall</i> = <i>Fall</i> (\"case\"); <i>furschub</i> = <i>Fürschub</i> (\"furtherance, support\"); <i>gemallin</i> = <i>Gemahlin</i> (\"consort, wife\").</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored three literal German spellings to match Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.112: 'publicirt' (was 'publicat'), 'mittell' (was 'mitteil'), and the closing dateline 'Dattum' (was 'Datum'). None affects sense; the English required no changes.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the negation-count verified; the one soft point is the precise sense of <i>gewiser hochwichtiger vrsachen</i> (\"certain weighty causes\") that Tycho pointedly declines to specify — deliberately vague in the original (the politics of his departure) and kept vague here rather than glossed into a specific grievance.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — German secure, negations verified, date agrees; the sole soft point is an authorial hedge faithfully kept vague.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.111-112 as page images (slice pages 1-2; letter no. 126, headed 'COPIA LITERARUM MISSARUM AD DUCEM VLDARICUM', dated 1597, 29 julii) and collated the German text clause by clause against source_text, including the opening address, the King Frederick / interregnum passage, the twenty-years-on-Hven figure, and the closing dateline. The English translation itself is faithful throughout and no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss was found. However, our source_text departs from Dreyer's printed word in three small spots (publicat/publicirt, mitteil/mittell, Datum/Dattum) — none change the sense the English conveys, but each is a TEXT_ERROR by definition, which the grading rule treats as an automatic C regardless of severity. Notable because this letter carried a self-assigned grade of A.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'publicat' where Dreyer's printed page clearly reads 'publicirt' (German 'be published'); the English rendering happens to be correct for either form, but our Latin/German transcription departs from Dreyer's own word. Dreyer prints: <i>vnd zu gemeinen nutz publicirt werden möchten (TBOO XIV p.112, l.4)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints 'mitteil'; Dreyer prints 'mittell'. Same meaning ('means/support'), but the transcription departs from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>durch notturfftige mittell vnd erfprießliche furfchub erhalten (TBOO XIV p.112, l.28)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'Datum' (single t); Dreyer prints 'Dattum' (double t). Trivial, but a literal departure from Dreyer's print. Dreyer prints: <i>Dattum Roftogk, den 29. jullij a:o 1597. (TBOO XIV p.112, closing dateline)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified all three TEXT_ERROR defects directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO XIV p.112): line 4 confirms 'publicirt', line 28 confirms 'mittell', and the closing dateline confirms 'Dattum'. Fixed only the German/Latin transcription in each case; none of the three affected the English translation, which was already correct. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15880815001", "next": "brahe-t_15970804001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15880815001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15970804001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks the Duke for his past visit to Hven, explains his departure from Denmark for unstated weighty reasons, and reports current residence in Rostock.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970729001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970802001", "d": "1597-08-02", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Jacob Bording → Tycho Brahe — Güstrow, 2 August 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII–VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript/<i> Afskrift</i>)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IACOBUS BORDINGUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>S.</p><p>REDDITAe mihi sunt literae tuae, Vir Nobilissime, quibus significas, te patrium solum vertere coactum, et nunc Rostochij subsistere, quod etiam ex alijs iam pridem intellexeram. Doleo profecto non modo vices tuas, sed ingens quoque illud rerum Astronomicarum dispendium, quod ob hasce rerum tuarum inexpectatas conturbationes eventurum, unâ praevideo. Sed quid facias? cum haec nulla tua culpa aut merito acciderint, nullumque in eo studio laborem, quantumvis gravem aut molestum, detrectâris, omnibus aequis censoribus facile excusatus eris. Verum de his aliquando coram plura. De Illustrissimi Principis mei in te clementi favore nihil est quod dubites. Literas tuas C. E. reverenter obtuli, ac curabo, ut non tantum ad illas responsum, sed etiam commendatorias ad Sereniss. Daniae Regem intra biduum vel triduum accipias. Nunc enim tantum otij mihi non fuit, ut confici potuerint, nec etiam iusto diutius illos detinere volui, qui tuas attulerint. Quod reliquum est, officia mea tibi defero promptissima.</p><p>Vale.<br>Gustrovij die 2. Augusti Anno 1597.<br>T. studiosissimus<br>IACOBUS BORDINGUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Jacob Bording to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Your letter has been delivered to me, most noble Sir, in which you make known that you have been forced to leave your native soil and now reside at Rostock — a thing I had also long since learned from others. I grieve, indeed, not only over your own fortunes, but also over that immense loss to astronomical affairs which, together [with them], I foresee will come to pass on account of these unexpected disturbances of your circumstances. But what are you to do? Since these things have befallen through no fault or deserving of yours, and since you have shirked no labour in that pursuit, however heavy or troublesome, you will easily be excused in the eyes of all fair-minded judges. But of these matters, more at some time face to face. As to my Most Illustrious Prince's gracious favour towards you, there is nothing for you to doubt. I have reverently presented your letter to His Princely Grace [C. E.], and I shall see to it that within two or three days you receive not only a reply to it, but also a letter of recommendation to the Most Serene King of Denmark. For just now I had not so much leisure that they could be completed, nor did I wish to detain longer than was right those who brought your [letter]. For the rest, I tender you my most ready services.</p><p>Farewell.<br>At Güstrow, the 2nd day of August, the year 1597.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Jacob Bording.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iacobus Bordingus</i> = <b>Jacob Bording</b> (the Younger), physician (<i>Leibarzt</i>) at the Mecklenburg ducal court at Güstrow. He is the son of the better-known <b>Jacob Bording the Elder</b> (1511–1560), the Antwerp-born professor of medicine at Copenhagen and royal physician to Christian III of Denmark; the family thus had standing connections at the Danish court, which is precisely the leverage this letter exploits — Bording undertakes to procure a ducal letter of recommendation addressed to the Danish king on Tycho's behalf.</li><li><b>Recipient / situation.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, by August 1597 had left Hven and Denmark after the breakdown of his relations with the young <b>Christian IV</b> and the new royal council, and was lodging at <b>Rostock</b> (<i>Rostochij subsistere</i>) before moving on toward the Imperial court. <i>patrium solum vertere coactum</i> — \"forced to leave [literally: to turn from] your native soil\"; <i>solum vertere</i> is the classical legal idiom for going into exile, and Bording's diplomatic choice of it (rather than a blunter word) is preserved.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimi Principis mei</b></i> — \"my Most Illustrious Prince\": Bording's master, the <b>Duke of Mecklenburg</b> resident at Güstrow, i.e. <b>Ulrich III</b> (1527–1603). Named only by title in the letter; the identification is supplied here.</li><li><b>C. E.</b> — the abbreviated honorific <i>Celsitudini Eius</i> / <i>Celsitudo Eius</i>, \"His Highness / His Princely Grace,\" to whom Bording presented Tycho's letter. Expanded in the translation as \"His Princely Grace [C. E.],\" the abbreviation flagged rather than silently filled (cf. protocol §5.5, <i>Celsitudo</i> → \"Highness\"). The reading is secure in sense but the exact case-form behind the two letters is editorial.</li><li><b><i>commendatorias</b></i> (sc. <i>literas</i>) — \"a letter of recommendation / commendatory letter,\" the ducal letter to the Danish king that is the practical object of the whole note.</li><li><b><i>Sereniss. Daniae Regem</b></i> — \"the Most Serene King of Denmark,\" i.e. <b>Christian IV</b> (r. 1588–1648; assumed personal rule 1596). Title only; identified here.</li><li><b><i>ingens illud rerum Astronomicarum dispendium</b></i> — \"that immense loss to astronomical affairs\": Bording's foreboding that Tycho's exile will damage the science itself (<i>dispendium</i> = loss, detriment), not merely Tycho's personal fortune.</li><li><b>Salutation / subscription.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (dicit), \"greeting\" (protocol §5.5). <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell.\" <i>T. studiosissimus</i> = <i>Tui studiosissimus</i>, \"Most devoted to you\" (the addressee being a private nobleman, not an \"Excellency,\" the form is the plain <i>Tui</i> rather than <i>Excellentiae Tuae</i>; rendered \"Most devoted to you\" accordingly).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Gustrovij die 2. Augusti Anno 1597</i> = \"At <b>Güstrow</b>, the 2nd day of August, the year 1597\" (O.S.; Mecklenburg, a Lutheran territory, still kept the Julian calendar in 1597). The dateline agrees exactly with the dataset label (<i>1597. 2. august. Güstrow</i>); no date discrepancy. <i>biduum vel triduum</i> = \"two or three days\" (the Latin idiom for a span of two / three days), kept as the natural English.</li><li><b>Text witness.</b> The surviving authority is a later transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b> (Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet), not an autograph; ordinary copyist orthography (<i>-ij</i> for <i>-ii</i>, <i>otij</i>, <i>alijs</i>; the printed <i>â/û</i> circumflexes mark ablatives/contractions). No reading affects the sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a straightforward humanist letter of condolence and patronage-brokerage; no Greek, symbols, astrological or medical jargon, and no textual or dating crux. The only editorial points are the expansion of the honorific abbreviation <b>C. E.</b> and the by-title identifications of the Duke of Mecklenburg and the King of Denmark, both flagged above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII p.5 as a page image; the full letter 'Iacobus Bordingus ad Tychonem Brahe' is printed there entire. Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word (opening 'Redditae mihi sunt literae tuae' through signature 'Iacobus Bordingus', including the date '2. Augusti Anno 1597'). English translation checked sentence-by-sentence: no invented facts, no dropped clauses, subject/antecedent and negations all correct.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000331001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000331001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's departure from Denmark, grieves the loss to astronomy, and promises to present his letter to the Duke and procure a letter of recommendation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970802001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970804001", "d": "1597-08-04", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → Tycho Brahe — Güstrow, 4 August 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German, chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Ad vitam Tychonis</i>, p. 113) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift = later transcript; Dreyer: \"Ex apographo\")</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDLEN VNND EHRENUESTEN VNNSERM LIEBEN BESONDERN TYCHONI BRAHE ZU [KNUDSTRUP], JETZO ZU ROSTOCK</p><p>VONN Gottes gnaden Vlrich, hertzogk zue Meckelburgk. Vnnsern gunstigen gruß vnd geneigten willen zuuor. Erenuester, lieber besonder, Wir haben ewer sc[h]reiben empfangen, vnnd darauß vngerne vermercket, das sich eine sulche Veranderung, alß jhr Vnnß darin zuerkennen geben, mit euch zugetragen, vnd Wir wol erachten können, das euch allerhande vngelegenheidt vnd beschwerung darauß entstehet, so tragen Wir zwar mit euch eine gnediges mitleiden, vnnd als Wir dan nitt allein selbst zuerinnern wißen, was Wir vor etlichen Jahren vor schöne instrumenta astronomica auff der jnsula Huen bey euch gesehen, sondern auch sonsten zum theil auß ewern in druck außgegeuen vnd Vns mitgetheilten operibus, zum theil aber auß anderer leutte bericht erfahren, wo hin jhr ferner ewer arbeitt gerichtet gehabt, so were dennoch wol zu wunschen, das jhr in der selben continuation an voriger ewer stelle hettet bleiben lasßen, vnd des wolangefangen werck zu glucklich endtschafft außfhuren mugen, wie Wir auch den euch derowegen bey verwantes kurtzes schreiben an die kön: w: zu Dennemarcken, Vnsern freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten herrn son, mittheilen wollen, diesen einhalt ihr auß der abschriefst zuersehen, vnd Wir nun zu eweren bedencken stehen, ob eß euch dienstlich sein mochte, das jhr eß vbergeben lasßet oder nicht. Sonsten können Wir gar wolleiden, das ihr euch ewer gelegenheidt nach alda in Vnser stadt Rostogk auffhaltet biß zu andern ewer besßer beqvemlichheidt, dan Wir kein vrsachen wisßen, warumb Wir solches zu wiedern sein lasßen, welcher gestalt aber die von euch nun lange zeit hero getrieben astronomische sachen ferner dieser orter oder sonsten in Deutschlandt dergestaldt, wie es von euch nun edtlicher iher hero damit gehalten werden, möchten vergesetzt vnd nach notturft befordert werden konnen, darauf werdet jhr ohne zweiffel selbst wolgedacht haben. Sonsten musen woll bekennen, das eine grosßes dazu gehordt, vnnd in eines jeden vermugen nicht ist solche sachen außzufuhren, vnd das derowegen dieselben groser potentaten patrocinij vnnd befurderung wol bedurffen. Dieweil euch aber die gelegenheidt in Teudtschlandt beser bekendt, also das sie euch anzuteutten von notten, sintemal ihr selbst viele jahr darin zugebracht, so wertet ihr auch selbst zum besten wiesen, wes euch zu thun sein wil. Haben euch zue gnedigsten antwort nicht verhalten wollen, vnd Wir sein mit gnaden wolgewogen.</p><p>Datum Gustrow, den 4. augustij anno 1597.<br>Vlrich h: z: Meckelburgk.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and honourable, our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe of [Knudstrup], now at Rostock.</b></p><p>Ulrich, by the grace of God Duke of Mecklenburg. Our gracious greeting and favourable goodwill beforehand. Honourable, [our] well-beloved, We have received your letter, and from it noted with displeasure that such a change as you give Us to understand therein has befallen you; and We can well judge that all manner of inconvenience and hardship arises for you from it. We do indeed bear you a gracious compassion; and inasmuch as We not only know of Our own recollection what fine astronomical instruments [<i>instrumenta astronomica</i>] We saw with you some years ago on the island of Hven, but have also learned, partly from your works [<i>opera</i>] put out in print and communicated to Us, partly from other people's report, whither you had further directed your work, it were nonetheless much to be wished that you had let yourself remain in the continuation of the same in your former place, and might have carried the well-begun work through to a happy conclusion — as We, accordingly, are also minded to communicate to you herewith by an enclosed short letter to His Royal Dignity of Denmark, Our friendly and heartily-beloved lord [and] son, the content of which you are to see from the copy; and We now leave it to your consideration whether it might be of service to you that you have it delivered, or not. For the rest, We can very well suffer that you, as your circumstances require, lodge there in Our city of Rostock until [you find] another, better convenience for yourself, for We know no reason why We should let this be opposed; but in what manner the astronomical matters carried on by you now this long while past might further, in these parts or otherwise in Germany, be set forth and promoted according to need, in the way they have now for several years been conducted by you — on that you will doubtless yourself have well reflected. For the rest, We must indeed confess that a great deal is required thereto, and that it is not within everyone's power to carry such matters through, and that they therefore have good need of the patronage and furtherance of great potentates. But since the circumstances [or opportunities] in Germany are better known to you — so that to point them out to you [is] needful, ‹seeing› that you yourself have spent many years therein — you will also yourself best know what is for you to do. We have not wished to withhold from you [this] as a most gracious answer, and We are graciously well-disposed [towards you].</p><p>Given at Güstrow, the 4th of August, year 1597.<br>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg[-Güstrow]</b> (1527–1603), reigning from Güstrow. He was the father of <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b>, queen of Denmark (wife of Frederik II), and hence the maternal grandfather of <b>Christian IV</b>; this kinship governs the letter's diplomatic offer (see below). The dataset's heuristic tags the letter <i>de</i> — correct here: this is genuine chancery German, not German-orthography Danish.</li><li><b>Recipient address.</b> <i>\"…TYCHONI BRAHE ZU [KNUDSTRUP], JETZO ZU ROSTOCK\"</i> — \"Tycho Brahe of [Knudstrup], now at Rostock.\" <b>[KNUDSTRUP]</b> is bracketed in the source as printed by Dreyer (the place is supplied editorially / partly illegible in the apograph). Knudstrup (Knutstorp, Skåne) was the Brahe family seat. The letter dates from the months after Tycho's self-imposed departure from Denmark (spring 1597) and his stay at Rostock before moving on toward Imperial patronage; \"the change that has befallen you\" is his loss of the Hven establishment.</li><li><b>Recipient as instrument-maker's patron and witness.</b> <i>\"schöne instrumenta astronomica auff der jnsula Huen\"</i> — Ulrich recalls having himself seen Tycho's fine <b>astronomical instruments</b> on Hven; <i>operibus … in druck außgegeuen vnd Vns mitgetheilten</i> = \"your works put out in print and communicated to Us,\" i.e. presentation copies (the <i>Mechanica</i>, the <i>Epistolae</i>, the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> in progress). Latin technical nouns code-switched into the German body (<i>instrumenta astronomica, opera/operibus, continuation, patrocinij</i>) are preserved per protocol §2; <i>instrumenta</i> rendered \"instruments\" per §5.2.</li><li><b>\"die kön: w: zu Dennemarcken … Vnsern … herrn son.\"</b> <i>kön: w:</i> = <i>königliche Würde</i>, \"His/Her Royal Dignity\"; the king is <b>Christian IV</b> (r. 1588–1648). Ulrich calls him <i>\"Vnsern freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten herrn son\"</i> — literally \"our friendly, heartily-beloved lord [and] son.\" In strict relationship Christian IV was Ulrich's <b>grandson through his daughter</b> (Sophie); Dreyer's heading to the immediately following letter (no. 128, the enclosed letter itself) glosses it <i>\"ad Christianum IV regem, nepotem ex filia\"</i> (\"to King Christian IV, his grandson through [his] daughter\"), and in that letter Ulrich writes of <i>\"den grosvaterlichen verwantnus\"</i> (\"the grandfatherly kinship\"). \"Son\" is therefore the loose princely-courtesy register, not a literal filiation; rendered \"lord [and] son\" with the bracketed <i>[and]</i> marking the editorial reading of the asyndeton, and the true relationship recorded here rather than substituted into the text.</li><li><b>The enclosed intercession.</b> Ulrich offers Tycho an enclosed <i>\"kurtzes schreiben\"</i> of intercession to the Danish crown (the <i>abschriefst</i> / copy Tycho is given), leaving it to Tycho's discretion <i>\"ob eß euch dienstlich sein mochte, das jhr eß vbergeben lasßet oder nicht\"</i> = \"whether it might be of service to you that you have it delivered, or not.\" The disjunction <i>oder nicht</i> is preserved.</li><li><b>Negations / conditional force (checked, per protocol).</b> Six negations in the body, all reproduced: <i>nitt allein … sondern auch</i> (\"not only … but also,\" correlative); <i>kein vrsachen … warumb Wir solches zu wiedern sein lasßen</i> (\"no reason why We should let this be opposed\"); <i>in eines jeden vermugen nicht ist</i> (\"it is not within everyone's power\"); <i>oder nicht</i> (above); <i>nicht verhalten wollen</i> (\"have not wished to withhold\"). The closing <i>\"Haben euch … nicht verhalten wollen\"</i> is the standard chancery litotes (\"we did not wish to keep [this answer] from you\"), kept literal.</li><li><b><i>bey verwantes … schreiben</b></i> — <i>verwantes</i> here = <i>verwandt(es)</i> in the chancery sense \"enclosed / appended,\" not \"related (by kin)\"; rendered \"an enclosed … letter.\"</li><li><b>Place / calendar.</b> <i>Güstrow</i> — the Mecklenburg-Güstrow residence town. <i>Datum Güstrow, den 4. augustij anno 1597</i> — 4 August 1597, <b>Old Style</b> (Mecklenburg, like Denmark, kept the Julian calendar; the dateline year 1597 matches the catalogue label). The working transcription gives <i>anno 1597</i>; the Dreyer scan abbreviates <i>anno 97</i> — no substantive difference.</li><li><b>Textual crux (governs the grade).</b> <i>\"Dieweil euch aber die gelegenheidt in Teudtschlandt beser bekendt, also das sie euch anzuteutten von notten, sintemal ihr selbst viele jahr darin zugebracht.\"</i> As transmitted (and as printed by Dreyer from the Langebek <b>apograph</b>), the clause reads literally \"so that to point them out to you [is] needful, seeing that you yourself have spent many years therein\" — but the <i>sintemal</i> (\"seeing that / since\") clause supplies a reason for the <b>opposite</b> sense (\"there is <b>no</b> need to point them out to you, since you have spent many years there\"). The expected <i>nicht</i> (<i>… euch anzuteutten <b>nicht</b> von nöten</i>) is <b>absent</b> from the source; the witness is a later copy (<i>Afskrift / apographum</i>), so a copyist's omission of <i>nicht</i> is the most economical explanation. <b>Per protocol the missing negation is not silently supplied:</b> the translation renders the words as they stand and brackets nothing into the negative; the intended meaning (\"no need to point them out\") is recorded here only. The ‹seeing› in angle brackets marks <i>sintemal</i> rendered as the subordinator.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the German is otherwise rendered securely (the princely <i>Wir/euch</i> register, titulature, and all six negations preserved); the single flagged crux is the apparent omission of <i>nicht</i> in <i>\"also das sie euch anzuteutten von notten\"</i>, where the source as transmitted contradicts its own <i>sintemal</i>-clause. The literal reading is kept and the suspected scribal omission flagged rather than emended.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 127: Dreyer prints <i>\"also das sie euch anzuteutten von notten\"</i> with no <i>nicht</i> and no editorial note — the suspected copyist omission is in the apograph itself, not recoverable. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.112-114 as page images (item 127 in Dreyer's Epistolae et Acta, with his Latin regest on p.112 confirming the letter's gist: continuare et ad finem feliciter perducere, litteras ad regem Daniae, permission to lodge at Rostock, need of \"magnorum principum patrocinio\"). Collated the full German body against source_text and Dreyer p.113-114 line by line -- exact match throughout, including the sum-less, date-only closing (4. augustij anno 1597) and every clause the brief's known defect list would predict (no invented day-number, no invented phrase, no dropped sentence, no pronoun flip, no misattributed custody). Published English tracks the German faithfully, including the syntactically dense sentence on Tycho's astronomical work needing princely patronage. This letter was published at self-assigned grade C; full Dreyer collation found it clean and warrants A -- a case of the (admittedly worthless) self-assigned grade being wrong in the conservative direction.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15970729001", "next": "brahe-t_15980914002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15970729001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980914002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges displeasure at Tycho's changed circumstances, recalls seeing the instruments on Hven, and expresses regret that the astronomical work may not be brought to completion.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970804001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970804002", "d": "1597-08-04", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": "Christian 4.", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → King Christian IV of Denmark — Güstrow, 4 August 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ANN DEN KÖNIG ZU DENNEMARCK.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGSTER, grosmechtiger könig. E: K: M: seindt Vnsere freundtliche willige dienste, vnd was Wir den grosvaterlichen verwantnus nach jederzeitt mehr liebs vnnd gutts vermagen zuvor. Freundtlicher hertzviellgeliebter sohnn, Vns hatt der ehrnuester Vnser lieber besonder Tycho Brahe vnderthenig zuerkennen gegeben, welcher masen er aus hochwichtigen vrsagen, welche Vns doch allerdinge nicht bekant, mit groser seiner vngelegenheitt vndt beschwerung sich aus E: K: M: königreiche Dennemarcken, seinen lieben vatterlande, begeben, vnd sich eine zeittlangk in Vnser stadt Rostogk niederlasen musen, mitt ferner vndertheniger fleisiger bitte, Wir wolthen Vns daselbige nitt zu wieder, zondern viellmer jhnn zu gnaden Vns befolen sein lasen. Wann Wir nun aber bey Vns leichtlich abnehmen konnen, das durch solche seine verenderung, die von jhme nicht alleinn woll angefangene, sondern auch bereitts so viel jahre hero getriebene vnd befurderte löbliche astronomische sachen, dermasen nicht vollends continuiret werden könen, wie ers jhme woll furgenommen, vnd Wir aber solches vor Vnsere persohn, weill Wir es dafur achten, das auch dardurch E: K: M: königreiche ann dem grosen ruhm vndt nahmen, so es bisshero solcher herlichen kunst halben gehabt, ettwas abgehen muchte nicht gernne schehen; alß haben Wir dem nach E: K: W: hier ann freundtlicher gutter wollmeinung erjneren wollen, mitt gandske freundtlicher bitte, E: K: W: wolte nicht geschehen lasen, das gedachtes werck so plötzlich in ein abnehmen kommen, sondern vielmher dero in Gott ruhenden herren vatters, lobsehliger vndt christmilder gedechtnus, vndt aller reichs ratte beschlus ferner nachsetzen, vndt gedacten Tychonem Brahe alls einem furtreffentlichen vndt ihn der astronomia der masen erfaren vndt wolluerdienten mann, daß seines gleichen nictt leichtlich zu finden, do sonsten E: K: W: keinne erhebliche vrsachen hetten, worumb es nicht geschehen köntte, ann voriger ordt gnedigst restituiren. Welchs dann ohne zweiffell E: K: W: und dero gandzen königereich zu mher ruhm vnd ehrenn, wie es auch zu dem ende von ihm angefangen, vnd biß dahero mit groser muhe vndt arbeitt so weitt getrieben, das es keiner ihm baldt nachthun wirt, gereichen wurde. Wir geschweigen, das ermeltes Tychonis studium in astronomia auch fast in allen landen also bekandt vndt berhumet ist, das es pilligk gutter befurderung woll wirdigk, vndt weill Wir ihme dann auch sonsten mit gnaden vnd aller gutter wollgewogen, so bitten Wir dahero vmb so viellmher gandz freundtlich E: K: W: wolle diese Vnsere commendationn vndt intercession nicht anders als ihm besten vermercken vndt auffnehmen. Vnd Wir seindt E:K:W: jederzeit zu aller angenehmen freundtlichen willfahrung vndt grossvatterlichen dienst ertzeigung geneigt vnd gandz willigk.</p><p>Datum Gustruoe den 4. augusti anno 97.<br>Vonn Gottis gnaden Vlrich hertzog<br>zu Meckelnburgk, furst zu Wenden,<br>graffe zu Schwerin,<br>der lande Rostogk<br>vnd Stargard herr.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the King of Denmark.</b></p><p>Most Serene, mighty King. Our friendly, willing service to Your Royal Majesty beforehand, and whatever more of love and good we are at all times able [to render], in keeping with our grandfatherly kinship. Friendly, heartily well-beloved son: the honourable man, Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe, has humbly made known to Us how, for weighty reasons — which, however, are not at all known to Us — he has, with great inconvenience and hardship to himself, departed from Your Royal Majesty's kingdom of Denmark, his dear fatherland, and has had to settle himself for a time in Our city of Rostock; with the further humble, earnest petition that We would not take this amiss of him, but rather hold him commended to Us in [Our] grace. Now since We can readily judge for Ourselves that, by such a removal of his, the praiseworthy astronomical works — which he has not only well begun, but has now for so many years pursued and advanced — cannot be brought to full completion in the way he had well purposed; and since, for Our own part, We should not gladly see this happen, because We hold it that thereby Your Royal Majesty's kingdom might lose something of the great renown and name which it has hitherto had on account of that splendid art: therefore We have wished herein to remind Your Royal Worship in friendly, good intent, with quite friendly petition, that Your Royal Worship would not let it come to pass that the said work should so suddenly fall into decline, but rather should further give effect to the resolve of your lord father — of laudable and Christian-gracious memory, now resting in God — and of the whole Council of the Realm, and most graciously restore the said Tycho Brahe — as an excellent man, so experienced and so well-deserving in astronomy that his like is not easily to be found — to his former place, provided Your Royal Worship have not otherwise weighty reasons why it might not be done. This would then, without doubt, redound to the greater renown and honour of Your Royal Worship and of your whole kingdom — even as it was begun by him to that end, and has hitherto, with great toil and labour, been carried so far that none will soon match him in it. We say nothing of the fact that the said Tycho's pursuit of astronomy is so well known and renowned in well-nigh all lands that it is rightly well worthy of good furtherance; and since We are besides graciously and in all good will disposed towards him, We therefore beg all the more, quite in friendship, that Your Royal Worship would mark and receive this Our commendation and intercession in no other way than for the best. And We are at all times inclined and quite willing to show Your Royal Worship every agreeable, friendly compliance and grandfatherly rendering of service.</p><p>Given at Güstrow, the 4th of August in the year [15]97.<br>By the grace of God, Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of [the] Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg[-Güstrow]</b> (1527–1603), reigning from Güstrow. He was the maternal grandfather of the recipient: his daughter <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg</b> was the queen consort of Frederik II and the mother of Christian IV. Hence the repeated <i>grosvaterlich / grossvatterlich</i> (\"grandfatherly\") register and the address of the King as <i>sohnn</i> (\"son,\" i.e. grandson) throughout — the literal kinship governs the letter's tone.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648; assumed personal rule 1596). The <i>herren vatter … in Gott ruhenden</i> whose resolve Ulrich invokes is his late father <b>Frederik II</b> (d. 1588), who had granted Tycho the island of Hven in 1576.</li><li><b>Subject.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), who had left Denmark in spring 1597 and settled for a time at <b>Rostock</b> (the Mecklenburg city, named in the text as <i>Vnser stadt Rostogk</i>). This is the intercessory cover-letter Ulrich enclosed when forwarding to his grandson a petition from Tycho, requesting that the King restore Tycho to his \"former place\" (Hven and his Danish appointments). It matches the companion piece in the corpus, Tycho → Duke Ulrich, Rostock, 29 July 1597 (brahe-t_15970729001), which Ulrich is here answering and relaying.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A surviving copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in Langebek's Diplomatarium; the heading <i>ANN DEN KÖNIG ZU DENNEMARCK</i> (\"To the King of Denmark\") is the chancery docket, not part of the salutation.</li><li><b>Honorific abbreviations.</b> <i>E: K: M:</i> = <i>Eurer Königlichen Majestät</i> (\"Your Royal Majesty\"); <i>E: K: W:</i> = <i>Eurer Königlichen Würde(n)</i> (\"Your Royal Worship/Dignity\"). The letter switches register from the higher <i>Majestät</i> in the opening address to the slightly more familiar <i>Würde</i> in the body; the distinction is preserved in the translation (\"Your Royal Majesty\" vs. \"Your Royal Worship\") rather than levelled.</li><li><b>Chancery formulae (§5.5 register).</b> <i>Vnser lieber besonder</i> (= <i>unser lieber besonder[er]</i>) = \"Our trusty and well-beloved\" (the princely address-formula for a favoured subject/client). <i>commendationn vndt intercession</i> — the two Latin technical nouns <i>commendatio</i> and <i>intercessio</i> are code-switched into the German, the standard diplomatic terms for a letter of recommendation/formal plea on another's behalf; kept and rendered \"commendation and intercession.\"</li><li><b>Orthography (Early New High German chancery, c. 1597).</b> <i>seindt</i> = <i>sind</i>; <i>vnd/vndt/vnnd</i> = <i>und</i>; <i>Wir/Vns/Vnser</i> (majuscule) = the princely first-person; <i>jhnn/jhme/ihm</i> = <i>ihn/ihm</i>; <i>zondern</i> = <i>sondern</i>; <i>gandske / gandz / gandzen</i> = <i>ganz(e)</i> (\"quite, whole\"); <i>muchte / köntte</i> = <i>möchte / könnte</i> (subjunctive); <i>vrsagen</i> = <i>Ursachen</i> (\"reasons\"); <i>schehen / geschehen</i> = \"happen / come to pass\"; <i>abnehmen</i> (noun) = \"decline, falling-off\"; <i>restituiren</i> = restore (Latinate); <i>furtreffentlich</i> = <i>vortrefflich</i> (\"excellent\"); <i>wolluerdient</i> = <i>wohlverdient</i> (\"well-deserving\"); <i>berhumet</i> = <i>berühmt</i> (\"renowned\"); <i>pilligk / wirdigk / willigk</i> = <i>billig / würdig / willig</i>.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The two double-negative courtesy constructions are rendered with their full logic preserved, not simplified: (1) <i>Wir wolthen Vns daselbige nitt zu wieder … lasen</i> = \"We would not take this amiss\" (Tycho's plea that the Duke not resent his settling at Rostock); (2) the petition that the King <i>wolte nicht geschehen lasen, das gedachtes werck so plötzlich in ein abnehmen kommen</i> = \"would not let it come to pass that the said work should so suddenly fall into decline.\" The conditional escape-clause <i>do sonsten E: K: W: keinne erhebliche vrsachen hetten, worumb es nicht geschehen köntte</i> is a deliberately deferential double negative — \"provided Your Royal Worship have not otherwise weighty reasons why it might not be done\" — leaving the King's discretion intact; its mood and polarity are reproduced exactly.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datum Gustruoe … den 4. augusti anno 97</i> = \"Given at <b>Güstrow</b> [the Mecklenburg ducal seat], the 4th of August in the year [15]97\" (O.S.; Denmark and Mecklenburg both still on the Julian calendar). The dataset <i>label</i> date (1597. 4. august. Güstrow) agrees with the dateline; no header/label discrepancy. <b>The intrusive \"35\" between \"Gustruoe\" and \"den 4. augusti\"</b> is unexplained in the source text — almost certainly a register/folio number or copyist's marginal numeral absorbed into the line of the Langebek <i>Afskrift</i>, not a calendar element. It is reproduced verbatim in the Source block and bracketed \"[35,]\" in the translation rather than silently dropped or \"corrected\" into the date; this is the one flagged crux.</li><li><b>Titulature.</b> The subscription styles Ulrich <i>furst zu Wenden, graffe zu Schwerin, der lande Rostogk vnd Stargard herr</i> — \"Prince of [the] Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard,\" the standard secondary titles of the Mecklenburg dukes; rendered in full per the protocol's princely-titulature rule (§2).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed a stray \"35\" from the closing dateline: Dreyer's printed text reads simply \"Datum Guftrue den 4. augufti anno 97\" (TBOO XIV p.114); the digit was Dreyer's own marginal line-count, wrongly absorbed into the transcription and then into the translation as a bracketed \"[35,]\".</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the historical setting is corroborated (Dreyer; <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; the companion letter brahe-t_15970729001). The sole flagged crux is the editorially-uninterpretable numeral <b>\"35\"</b> in the dateline (`Gustruoe 35 den 4. augusti`), reproduced and bracketed rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening address, body, closing, signature, date) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp. 113-114 via the page images. The translation itself is accurate throughout with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation; the one defect is a TEXT_ERROR in our source transcription (a stray marginal line-number glued into the text), which the translator appropriately flagged with brackets. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though severity is low and the translator's handling of it prevented any reader-facing harm.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has absorbed Dreyer's marginal line-number \"35\" into the sentence as if it were part of Ulrich's letter. The translator noticed the anomaly and correctly bracketed it as uncertain rather than asserting it as fact, so no reader is misled, but the underlying Latin/German transcription itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Datum Guftrue den 4. augufti anno 97. (no numeral in the running text; \"35\" is Dreyer's own marginal line-count, printed in the left margin roughly opposite this line, not part of the letter)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.113-115. Page 114, line 35 (marginal line-number \"35\" printed at left) corresponds to the closing dateline \"Datum Guftrue den 4. augufti anno 97.\", with no numeral inside the sentence itself. Confirms the audit's diagnosis exactly. No other defects found on these three pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Intercedes with King Christian IV on Tycho's behalf, arguing that his departure risks leaving his astronomical works unfinished, and recommends him to royal favour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970804002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970907001", "d": "1597-09-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Erik Sparre", "ck": "Erik Sparre", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Erik Sparre — Rostock, 7 September 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Stockholm, Riksarkivet, Erik Sparres Samling, Skrivelser till E. S. och hans hustru (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, NOBILITATE GENERIS ET ERUDITIONIS ADMODUM CONSPICUO D:NO ERICO SPARRE, SER:MI SVECIAe REGIS CANCELLARIO AC CONSILIARIO PRUDENTISSIMO, LIBERO BARONI IN N., COGNATO ATQVE AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>Literae tuae eruditissimae pariter ac humanissimae, cognate optime, amoris eximij erga me testes, ijs ipsis diebus, quibus Haffnia relicta in Germaniam migrandum erat mihi, redditae sunt. Verum enimvero, tunc rebus meis ad abitum adornandis ita distrahebar, tum quoque amicis ob abitum invisentibus praepediebar, ut, licet nihil magis mihi in votis fuerit, attamen temporis iniquitas omnem scribendi occasionem praeriperet. Postquam vero huc Rostochium appuli, atque hic certis de caussis, quemadmodum et antea Haffniae per integrum anni quadrantem substiti (praesertim, quo nostratibus, si paenitere ipsos admissi erroris posset, deliberatius rem expendendi tempus concederem, neve nimis praepropere ardua, quae tracto, studia alio transtulisse viderer), ne hic quidem aptior scribendi facultas, contra quam opinabar, oblata fuit, cum nullum iter ad confinia utriusque regni parantem nancisci liceret. Accessit et hoc, quod nescio quis rumor, utrum te inde in Sueciam interiorem vel Poloniam ad s:m vestrum regem te recepisses, incertum me redderet. Cumque eo nomine celeberrimum virum d. Davidem Chytraeum, huius academiae antesignanum, honorifica, prout virtus merebatur, nominis tui mentione compellassem, siquidem eum crebro de rebus regnorum illorum a multis edoceri scirem, et inter te ac ipsum alias commutari literas inaudivissem, attamen dubium, quod animo conceperam, eximere non potuit. Jtaque scribendi officium adhuc paulo diutius differendum censui, donec, ubinam locorum morareris, certo mihi innotesceret. Cum vero ne sic quidem profecerim, et longiores in hac urbe moras nectere, nec mihi commodum, nec etiam ob ingravescentem indies pestem consultum sit, propediem ad magnificum dominum Henricum Ranzovium, affinem et amicum meum singularem, perbenigne ab eodem invitatus, me conferre statui, isthic moraturus, donec rebus meis ulterius ex divino provisu prospexero. Quocirca aliquid ante meum hinc abitum literarum ad te omnino dandum ratus sum, ea spe fretus posse id ad communem utrique necessarium Gabrielem Sparre directum, ubicunque tandem locorum haereres, commodum tibi transmitti.</p><p>Desiderium tui cognoscendi et compellandi, non saltem ob sanguinis coniunctionem, sed etiam ob suspiciendam virtutem ac eruditionem tuam pari rerum usu auctam me quoque iampridem incesserat, ut hoc votum explendi copiam sive in Dania sive alibi fata hactenus invidisse admodum moleste feram. Nunc vero cum magis magisque disiungamur et mihi patrium solum vertere ac alias in exteris regionibus commoditates quaerere necessum fuerit, omnem spem alloquij tui mihi ereptam video et quam maxime doleo. Caussae vero, quae me huc adegerunt, tot tantaeque fuerunt, ut calamo eas exprimere vix valeam, neque etiamnum tutum arbitrer. Qui magni viri haberi cupiunt, licet graviter nonnunquam impingant, tamen nec errores agnoscere volunt, nec in ijs redargui. Satis equidem mature hanc mutationem praevidi, nihilque prius habui, quam ut eam quovis pacto praecaverem et averterem, literis idcirco ad s:mi regis nostri cancellarium datis (quarum hic exemplum adiunxi), sed successu prorsus irrito. Nihil enim impetrare licuit, imo inter alia responsum, regi nostro quidquam sumptuum in jnstrumentorum meorum astronomiae conservationem impendere integrum non esse. An vero ex regis ipsius voluntate vel alterius privato affectu idque eo praetextu promanaverit, non dixerim. Nec verbis eventus defuit. Nam non tantum feudo illo Norvagico, cuius in literis fit mentio, confestim post coronationem regiam peractam adempto contenti non fuerunt ij, qui fatis reipublicae in Dania nunc praesunt; sed et reliqua omnia ab optimo rege Frederico l. m. in artis vere regiae sustentationem deputata subtraxerunt, et simul senatorum regni literas, artis eius liberalem conservationem perpetuamque spondentes non respexerunt; licet quidam adeoque praecipuus eorum has una subsignarit et subscripserit; quin etiam canonicatum Roskyldensem (quem solum habui residuum) dictus cancellarius sibi acquirere ac in proprium commodum vertere non dubitavit. Tantum abest, ut artis adeo praeclarae et patriae nostrae forte non inhonorificae continuationem et perpetuationem pro virili (uti decuit) promovere voluerit. Scripsit autem non ita pridem illustrissimus dux Uldaricus, s:mi regis nostri avus, ad ipsum regem atque artis huius in integrum istinc restitutionem diligenter suasit. Quibus literis et meas addideram, etiamnum operam meam s:mo regi nostro et patriae in perficienda isthic astronomiae instauratione, quam tam diu tantisque conatibus molitus sum, per eas submisse offerens, modo id commodum exequi liceat et indemnitati meae consulatur. Sin vero nihil his effectum fuerit, quod priora ac inconsulta quorundam consilia emendet (uti sperare vix licet, idque non tam ob ipsius s:mi regis nostri animum per se satis generosum et integrum, quam eorum, qui nunc soli quidvis pro arbitrio moderari volunt, aversam voluntatem), mox ad jmperatoriam ma:tem aliosque viciniores reges ac praecipuos Germaniae principes scribere decrevi, et quid clementis eorum circa artis tam sublimis ulteriorem sustentationem voluntatis ac arbitrij sit, experiri. Vtrum videlicet quidpiam sumptuum ad hoc subministrare velint, quo ea in publicum reipublicae literariae emolumentum perfectior quam unquam antea, prodeat, atque orbi communicetur. Quo etiam nomine potentissimum vestrum regem Sigismundum submisse sollicitare proposui, si (uti spem concepi) ipsius m:tas, ut est erga ingenuas artes, cum et his ipsamet imbuta sit, benificentissima, hanc etiam curam et laudem caeteris addendam, suaque regia munificentia dignam censeat. Posset forte aliqua ex jnsulis Prussiae vicinis commoditatem huic negotio praebere, vel id quocunque alio modo fieri, prout ipsius m:ti visum foret. Qua in re tuum prudens cum auxilio consilium, mi amicissime cognate, obnixe peto et expecto, an videlicet hoc studium astronomicum ipsius ma:ti offerendum, et an idem cum magno quoque cancellario communicandum putes; et utrum specifice jnsula Dalan prope Rigam, vel Bornhovium, aut jnsula Werden prope Dantiscum, similesve loci nominandi nec ne, vel quavis alia ratione quidpiam, quod utile foret, tentandum. Jdque eo audentius a te peto, quod in literis tuis, singulari benevolentia refertis, tale quid sponte offerre videris, dum officia mihi et studia tua, hoc praesertim tempore perbenigne ac amice addicis. Ego, si nihil aliud de me polliceri possum, hoc certe sponsum volo, me ex eorum numero, qui virtutes tuas et eruditionem maximi faciunt et colunt, si non primas tenentem, saltem nemini concedentem fore, et ipso ad minimum animo tua erga me officia pro virili compensaturum.</p><p>Vale mi charissime cognate et me (quod te facturum nihil ambigo) redamare perge.<br>Datae Rostochij pridie eius diei, qua inde discedere constitui, 7:a nimirum septembris stylo veteri anni etc. 1597.<br>Tui studiosiss.<br>Tycho Brahe<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most worthy man, by nobility of birth and of learning altogether distinguished, Master Erik Sparre, Chancellor and most prudent Councillor to the Most Serene King of Sweden, free baron in N., his kinsman and friend especially to be honoured. [Tycho Brahe] sends heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>Your letter, most learned and likewise most kind, best of kinsmen, a witness of your singular love towards me, was delivered to me in those very days on which, Copenhagen left behind, I was to remove into Germany. But in truth I was then so distracted with setting my affairs in order for departure, and likewise so hindered by friends calling on me on account of that departure, that — although nothing was more in my prayers [than to write] — the unkindness of the time nevertheless snatched away every opportunity of writing. And after I had put in here at Rostock, and for certain reasons stopped here (as also earlier at Copenhagen) for a whole quarter of a year — chiefly that I might grant my countrymen a more deliberate time for weighing the matter, in case they could come to repent of the wrong committed, and that I might not seem to have transferred elsewhere too headlong the arduous studies I am engaged in — not even here was a fitter chance of writing offered to me, contrary to my expectation, since I could find no one preparing a journey to the borders of either kingdom. To this was added that some rumour or other left me uncertain whether you had withdrawn from there into the interior of Sweden or into Poland to your Most Serene King. And although on that account I had addressed that most celebrated man Master David Chytraeus, the foremost figure of this university, with honourable mention of your name (as your worth deserved) — since I knew him to be often informed by many about the affairs of those kingdoms, and had heard that letters were on other occasions exchanged between you and him — yet he could not remove the doubt I had conceived in my mind. And so I judged that the duty of writing must be put off a little longer still, until it should become certainly known to me in what place you were staying. But since I have made no progress even so, and since to weave longer delays in this city is neither convenient for me nor, on account of the plague growing heavier by the day, advisable, I have resolved very shortly to betake myself to the magnificent Master Henrik Rantzau, my kinsman by marriage and my especial friend, most kindly invited by him, intending to stay there until, by divine providence, I shall have provided further for my affairs. On which account I have thought I must by all means give you some letter before my departure hence, relying on this hope, that it may conveniently be transmitted to you, directed to Gabriel Sparre, the kinsman necessary to us both, wherever in the end you may be lodged.</p><p>A longing to know you and to address you had long since come upon me too — not merely on account of the bond of blood, but also on account of your virtue, to be looked up to, and your learning, augmented by a matching experience of affairs — so that I bear it most grievously that the fates have hitherto begrudged me the chance of fulfilling this wish, whether in Denmark or elsewhere. But now, when we are parted more and more, and when it has been necessary for me to turn from my native soil and seek other conveniences in foreign regions, I see every hope of conversing with you snatched away from me, and I grieve at it as much as may be. The causes, indeed, that have driven me hither were so many and so great that I can scarcely express them with the pen, nor do I even now judge it safe. Those who desire to be held great men, though they sometimes strike grievously against [the truth], yet are unwilling either to acknowledge their errors or to be refuted in them. Early enough, to be sure, did I foresee this change, and I held nothing more important than to guard against and avert it by any means whatsoever — letters being therefore sent to the Chancellor of our Most Serene King (of which I have appended a copy here) — but with an outcome entirely vain. For nothing could be obtained; nay, among other things [came] the reply that it was not open to our King to expend any costs upon the maintenance of my instruments of astronomy. But whether this issued from the King's own will, or from another's private feeling and under that pretext, I would not say. Nor was the deed wanting to the words. For not only were those who now preside over the destinies of the commonwealth in Denmark not content with that Norwegian fief — of which mention is made in the letters — being taken away immediately after the royal coronation had been carried through; but they also withdrew all the rest that had been assigned by that best of kings, Frederick of blessed memory, for the support of the truly royal art, and at the same time gave no regard to the letters of the senators of the realm, pledging the generous and perpetual maintenance of that art — although a certain one, and indeed the chief of them, had countersigned and subscribed these together [with the others]; nay more, the said Chancellor did not hesitate to acquire for himself the canonry of Roskilde (the only thing I had left remaining) and to turn it to his own profit. So far was he from being willing to promote with his whole strength (as was fitting) the continuation and perpetuation of an art so illustrious and perhaps not without honour to our fatherland. Not long ago, however, the Most Illustrious Duke Ulrich, grandfather of our Most Serene King, wrote to the King himself and diligently urged the full restoration of this art there. To which letter I had also added my own, even now through them submissively offering my service to our Most Serene King and to the fatherland in completing there the restoration of astronomy, which I have laboured at so long and with such great efforts — provided only it may be allowed to carry it out conveniently and that my own indemnity be cared for. But if nothing shall be effected by these [letters] to mend the earlier and ill-considered counsels of certain persons (as one may scarcely hope, and that not so much on account of the disposition of our Most Serene King himself, of itself sufficiently generous and upright, as on account of the averse will of those who now alone wish to manage everything at their own pleasure), I have resolved presently to write to His Imperial Majesty and to other nearer kings and to the chief princes of Germany, and to learn what their gracious will and pleasure may be concerning the further support of so sublime an art — namely, whether they are willing to furnish any costs towards this, that it may come forth more perfect than ever before for the public benefit of the republic of letters, and be communicated to the world. On which account, too, I have proposed humbly to solicit your most powerful King Sigismund — if (as I have conceived hope) His Majesty, being most beneficent towards the liberal arts, since he is himself imbued with them too, should judge that this care and praise also is to be added to the rest, and worthy of his royal munificence. Perhaps some one of the islands neighbouring Prussia might afford a convenient place for this business, or it might be done in whatever other way, as should seem good to His Majesty. In which matter I earnestly seek and await your prudent counsel together with your help, my most friendly kinsman — namely, whether you think this astronomical pursuit is to be offered to His Majesty, and whether the same is to be communicated also with the Grand Chancellor; and whether specifically the island of Dalan near Riga, or Bornholm [Bornhovium], or the island of Werden near Danzig, or similar places are to be named or not, or whether by some other plan something should be attempted that would be useful. And this I ask of you the more boldly because in your letter, filled with singular goodwill, you seem to offer such a thing of your own accord, when you most kindly and amicably pledge your services and devotion to me, especially at this time. As for me, if I can promise nothing else of myself, this at least I would have pledged: that of the number of those who esteem and cherish your virtues and learning most highly I shall be, if not holding the first place, at any rate yielding to none; and that, in mind at the very least, I shall requite with all my strength your services towards me.</p><p>Farewell, my dearest kinsman, and continue to love me in return (which I do not at all doubt you will do).<br>Given at Rostock, on the day before that on which I have determined to depart hence — namely on the 7th of September, Old Style, in the year, etc., 1597.<br>Most devoted to you,<br>Tycho Brahe<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from Rostock during his self-imposed exile, three months after leaving Hven (he had departed Copenhagen for Rostock in mid-1597 and would move to Henrik Rantzau's castle at Wandsbek that October). The letter is a candid appeal to a Swedish kinsman for help in finding a new royal patron after the loss of his Danish support.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Ericus Sparre</i> = <b>Erik Sparre</b> (1550–1600), the learned Swedish High Chancellor and royalist statesman, partisan of King Sigismund III against the regent Duke Karl (later Charles IX). He had passed through Denmark in March 1597 fleeing Duke Karl, heading for Sigismund's court; he was executed in the Linköping Bloodbath of 1600. <i>liber baro in N.</i> — \"free baron in N.\" — the place-name is abbreviated to a bare initial in the address (left as \"N.\" in both source and translation).</li><li><b>Address / salutation.</b> <i>S. P. D.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5); the sender's name is implicit in the formula and supplied in brackets.</li><li><b><i>cognatus</b></i> — \"kinsman.\" Tycho repeatedly addresses Sparre as <i>cognate</i> (the Brahe and Sparre families were related); kept consistently as \"kinsman.\"</li><li><b>David Chytraeus</b> — <i>Davidem Chytraeum, huius academiae antesignanum</i> = David Chytraeus (1530–1600), the eminent Lutheran theologian and historian, leading figure (\"standard-bearer,\" <i>antesignanus</i>) of the University of Rostock, where Tycho was then residing.</li><li><b>Henrik Rantzau</b> — <i>Henricum Ranzovium, affinem et amicum meum singularem</i> = <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (Heinrich Rantzau, 1526–1598), Statthalter of the Danish king in Holstein, humanist and astrologer, Tycho's friend and kinsman by marriage (<i>affinis</i>), who invited Tycho to his Wandsbek castle near Hamburg.</li><li><b>Gabriel Sparre</b> — a kinsman common to both correspondents (<i>communem utrique necessarium</i>), proposed as the intermediary for forwarding the letter; the Latin <i>necessarium</i> here in the legal/familial sense \"close relation/connection,\" rendered \"kinsman.\"</li><li><b>The \"letters to the Chancellor of our King\" and the appended copy.</b> <i>literis … ad s:mi regis nostri cancellarium datis (quarum hic exemplum adiunxi)</i> — Tycho had petitioned <b>Christian IV</b>'s chancellor and enclosed a copy with this letter. <i>s:mi regis nostri</i> (Most Serene King \"of ours\") = the <b>Danish</b> king, Christian IV, throughout; <i>s:m vestrum regem</i> / <i>vestrum regem Sigismundum</i> (King \"of yours\") = <b>Sigismund III</b> of Sweden and Poland. The possessives carefully distinguish Tycho's sovereign from Sparre's.</li><li><b><i>cancellarius</i> / <i>dictus cancellarius</b></i> — the Danish chancellor is <b>Christian Friis</b> (of Borreby, 1556–1616), royal chancellor under Christian IV; by an executive order of June 1597 Tycho's <b>Roskilde canonry</b> was transferred from Tycho to Friis. <i>canonicatum Roskyldensem (quem solum habui residuum)</i> = \"the canonry of Roskilde (the only thing I had left remaining)\" — the prebend of the Three Holy Kings (the Magi) in Roskilde Cathedral, which Tycho had held until 1597.</li><li><b><i>feudo illo Norvagico … confestim post coronationem regiam peractam adempto</b></i> — \"that Norwegian fief … taken away immediately after the royal coronation had been carried through\": Tycho's Norwegian fief of Nordfjord, withdrawn after Christian IV's coronation (29 August 1596). <i>cuius in literis fit mentio</i> — \"of which mention is made in the [appended] letters.\"</li><li><b><i>optimo rege Frederico l. m.</b></i> — \"that best of kings, Frederick, of blessed memory\": <b>Frederik II</b> (r. 1559–88), Tycho's original patron; <i>l. m.</i> = <i>laudatae</i> (or <i>piae</i>) <i>memoriae</i>, \"of blessed/praiseworthy memory.\"</li><li><b><i>senatorum regni literas … licet quidam adeoque praecipuus eorum has una subsignarit</b></i> — the council of the realm (Rigsråd) had pledged perpetual maintenance of Tycho's work in a signed letter; Tycho notes pointedly that one of the signatories — \"and indeed the chief of them\" (<i>praecipuus eorum</i>, i.e. the chancellor himself) — was among those now reneging. The barbed clause is kept intact.</li><li><b>Duke Ulrich</b> — <i>illustrissimus dux Uldaricus, s:mi regis nostri avus</i> = <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), maternal grandfather (<i>avus</i>) of Christian IV (through Queen Sophie of Mecklenburg). He had written to the Danish king urging the full restoration (<i>in integrum … restitutio</i>) of Tycho's astronomical establishment.</li><li><b>Astronomy / Tycho's programme.</b> <i>astronomiae instaurationem … perficienda</i> = \"completing … the restoration of astronomy\" (<i>instauratio</i> = Tycho's <i>astronomia instaurata</i> programme, protocol §5.1); <i>artis vere regiae</i> / <i>artis tam sublimis</i> = \"the truly royal art\" / \"so sublime an art\" — astronomy. <i>jnstrumentorum meorum astronomiae conservatio</i> = \"the maintenance of my instruments of astronomy\" (his Uraniborg instruments, protocol §5.2).</li><li><b>Proposed sites for a new observatory.</b> <i>jnsula Dalan prope Rigam</i> — an island \"Dalan\" near Riga (probably <b>Dalen / Dole (Dalholm)</b>, an island in the Daugava near Riga; identification not certain — see crux); <i>Bornhovium</i> — most plausibly <b>Bornholm</b> (the Danish/Baltic island), though the form is unusual; <i>jnsula Werden prope Dantiscum</i> — the island of <b>Werder near Danzig</b> (Gdańsk). All are weighed as possible locations under Sigismund's Baltic dominions. <i>ex jnsulis Prussiae vicinis</i> = \"some one of the islands neighbouring Prussia.\"</li><li><b><i>imperatoriam ma:tem</b></i> — \"His Imperial Majesty\": the Holy Roman Emperor <b>Rudolf II</b>, to whom Tycho would in the event turn, becoming Imperial Mathematician in 1599.</li><li><b><i>magno … cancellario</b></i> — \"the Grand Chancellor\": the Polish-Lithuanian Grand Chancellor (under Sigismund as King of Poland), distinct from the Swedish chancellor Sparre himself.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> The transcription preserves the edition's <i>j</i> for consonantal/final <i>i</i> (<i>ijs, eximij, amicissimo</i> → <i>ijs, jnstrumentorum, jnsula, Jtaque, Jdque</i>), the <i>v/u</i> conventions, and the abbreviations <i>s:mi / ser:mi</i> (<i>serenissimi</i>), <i>ma:tem / m:tas / m:ti</i> (<i>maiestatem</i> etc.), <i>d:no</i> (<i>domino</i>), <i>l. m.</i> (<i>laudatae memoriae</i>); these are expanded in the apparatus, not in the source block.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae Rostochij … 7:a nimirum septembris stylo veteri anni etc. 1597</i> = \"Given at Rostock … on the 7th of September, <b>Old Style</b>, in the year, etc., 1597.\" Tycho explicitly flags the style (<i>stylo veteri</i>, O.S.) himself — characteristic of a Lutheran writing to a correspondent in the orbit of Catholic (New-Style) Poland. The as-written date (7 September 1597 O.S.) agrees with the catalogue label; no discrepancy. The N.S. equivalent would be 17 September 1597, but per protocol §3 it is not silently substituted. Tycho dates the letter \"the day before that on which I have determined to depart\" (<i>pridie eius diei, qua inde discedere constitui</i>) — i.e. he planned to leave Rostock on 8 September O.S.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the political/biographical references are confirmed against the standard biographies (Dreyer; Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>). The one genuinely open point, flagged rather than smoothed, is the identification of the three proposed Baltic sites — <i>jnsula Dalan prope Rigam</i>, <i>Bornhovium</i>, and <i>jnsula Werden prope Dantiscum</i> — whose place-name forms (especially <i>Bornhovium</i>) are not fully secure; the named candidates (Dole/Dalholm near Riga; Bornholm; Werder near Danzig) are the most likely readings but are offered as identifications, not certainties.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full sentence-by-sentence collation of source_text and published_english against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.119-120 (the letter's complete printed text; confirmed against neighbouring pp.118 and 121 to fix exactly where the letter starts and ends). Every name, number, date and place-name checked (Erik Sparre, David Chytraeus, Henrik Rantzau, Gabriel Sparre, Duke Ulrich, the Roskilde canonry, Dalan/Bornhovium/Werden, King Sigismund, '7:a nimirum septembris stylo veteri anni etc. 1597') matches Dreyer exactly; source_text itself is verbatim Dreyer apart from long-s/ligature normalization. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge-loss found in the English. Self-assigned grade was B; independent collation supports A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Stockholm, Riksarkivet, Erik Sparres Samling, Skrivelser till E. S. och hans hustru.. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for failing to write earlier amid departure preparations and explains his decision to remain at Rostock to give his countrymen time to reconsider.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970907001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15970917001", "d": "1597-09-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck": "Henrik Rantzau", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Segeberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Henrik Rantzau — Segeberg, 17 September 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737, f.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD HENRICUM RANZOVIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>LITERAe tuae, Nobilissime et Amantissime Domine RANZOVI, amice et affinis honorande, per Praefectum tuum CHRISTIANUM mihi redditae sunt, cum quo loca a te peramice oblata collustravi, et pro benevolo hoc erga me affectu gratias officiose ago quas possum maximas, pro virili oblatâ occasione relaturus. Placeret quidem utraque conditio, et prae caeteris ea, quae Segebergae monstrata est; verumenimvero, cum nonnihil remotiores sic simus ab urbe quâdam amplâ, nec ego otiosam libenter vitam traducerem, sed libros quosdam e meis praelo ferme absolutos omnino perficere vellem, ad quod Typographia requireretur, et Artifices, qui figuras sculpere nossent, tum quoque qui Instrumentum unum vel alterum pro Observationibus coelitus obtinendis (modo nullum ex ijs quae Haffniae reposui, mature inde recepero) pararent. Si itaque prope Lubecam vel Hamburgum aliquod praedium mihi ad tempus, donec rebus meis commodius prospectum fuerit, concedere posses, feceris rem inprimis exoptatam. Constitui vero Deo volente, cras Bramstedium usque circa vesperam venire, quia tam cito negotia mea expedire non possum, praesertim in quibusdam scribendis, ut circa meridiem tibi adsim. Si interim Bredenbergam iveris, subsequar confestim, ita ut die Lunae annuente numine ibi ulterius de omnibus consilium tecum capiam, et desiderium hoc visendae Arcis eius amplissimae, inprimisque splendidae bibliothecae tuae, quod me iampridem incessit, nunc tandem expleam. Interea Mae. Tae. omnia fausta precor, eamque quam diutissime incolumem ex animo voveo.</p><p>Datae Segebergae 17. Septembris Anni 97.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Henrik Rantzau.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Your letter, most noble and most loving Master Rantzau, my friend and kinsman to be honoured, was delivered to me by your steward Christian, with whom I inspected the places you had so amicably offered; and for this kindly disposition towards me I dutifully give you the greatest thanks I can, intending to repay the offered occasion to the best of my power. Both arrangements would indeed please me, and above the rest the one that was shown [me] at Segeberg; but in truth, since we should thus be somewhat too far removed from some large town — and since I would not willingly pass an idle life, but should wish to bring quite to completion certain of my books, all but finished at the press, for which a printing-house would be required, and craftsmen who knew how to engrave figures, and likewise [someone] who might prepare one or another instrument for obtaining observations from the heavens (provided I recover in good time none of those which I have stored at Copenhagen) — if therefore you could grant me for a time some estate near Lübeck or Hamburg, until my affairs have been more conveniently provided for, you would do a thing especially longed-for. But I have resolved, God willing, to come tomorrow as far as Bramstedt about evening — since I cannot dispatch my business so quickly, especially in [the matter of] certain things to be written — so as to be with you about midday. If in the meantime you have gone to Breitenburg, I shall follow at once, so that on Monday, with the divine favour assenting, I may there take further counsel with you about everything, and may now at last fulfil this longing to see that most spacious castle of yours, and above all your splendid library, which has long since seized me. Meanwhile I pray every good fortune for Your Lordship, and from my heart I vow [you] safe and sound as long as possible.</p><p>Given at Segeberg, 17 September, the year [15]97.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing during his departure from Denmark. He left Hven in spring 1597 and, after a stay at Rostock, moved westward through Holstein; by 17 September 1597 he and his <i>familia</i> had reached Segeberg Fortress, the seat of the king's governor of Schleswig-Holstein, finding the governor away at Bramstedt (so \"On Tycho's Island,\" confirming the situation behind this letter). The \"books all but finished at the press\" point to the works then in progress (the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> and <i>Epistolae</i>); the instruments \"stored at Copenhagen\" (<i>Haffniae reposui</i>) are those of his dismantled Hven establishment.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Henricus Ranzovius</i> = <b>Henrik Rantzau / Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the Danish crown's <i>Statthalter</i> (royal governor) of Schleswig-Holstein, a major humanist patron, author, and bibliophile; his castle of <b>Breitenburg</b> held one of the most celebrated private libraries of the age. He died the following year (1598). Addressed as <i>affinis</i> (\"kinsman\"): the Brahe and Rantzau families were connected by marriage.</li><li><b><i>Praefectum tuum Christianum</b></i> — \"your steward/bailiff Christian\": Rantzau's <i>praefectus</i> (estate official) who carried the letter and guided Tycho over the offered properties; rendered \"steward.\" First name only is given; not further identified here.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Segeberga</i> = <b>Segeberg</b> (Bad Segeberg, Holstein), the fortress where Tycho was lodged and from which the letter is dated. <i>Bramstedium</i> = <b>Bramstedt</b> (Bad Bramstedt), where Rantzau then was. <i>Bredenberga</i> = <b>Breitenburg</b>, Rantzau's principal seat with the great library (the <i>Arx amplissima … splendida bibliotheca</i> of the next clause); the Latinised place-name <i>Bredenberga</i> reflects the Low-German form <i>Bredenberg</i>. <i>Lubeca</i> = <b>Lübeck</b>; <i>Hamburgum</i> = <b>Hamburg</b>; <i>Haffnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>.</li><li><b>Technical terms.</b> <i>praelo … absolutos / Typographia</i> — \"[books] finished at the press / a printing-house\": Tycho's need to carry his own typographic and engraving capacity with him into exile. <i>figuras sculpere</i> = \"to engrave figures\" (the diagrams/illustrations of his astronomical books). <i>Instrumentum … pro Observationibus coelitus obtinendis</i> = \"an instrument for obtaining observations from the heavens\" — <i>instrumentum</i> per glossary §5.2 (\"instrument\"); <i>observationes</i> kept as \"observations.\" No planetary or zodiac symbols occur in this letter.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (<i>dicit</i>), \"heartiest greeting\" (§5.5). <i>Mae. Tae.</i> = <i>Maiestati Tuae</i> / <i>Magnificentiae Tuae</i> — see crux below. <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand\" (an autograph subscription).</li><li><b>Reading crux — <i>Mae. Tae.</b></i> The abbreviation <i>Mae. Tae.</i> (expanded by Dreyer) is rendered \"Your Lordship.\" It most plausibly stands for <i>Magnificentiae Tuae</i> (\"Your Magnificence\"), the courtesy due a high noble and governor; it is <b>not</b> <i>Maiestati Tuae</i> (\"Your Majesty\"), which would be reserved for a sovereign and would not suit a letter to Rantzau. The expansion is editorial; the precise courtesy-title behind the contraction is not certain from the abbreviation alone, and is flagged rather than silently fixed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Datae Segebergae 17. Septembris Anni 97</i> = \"Given at Segeberg, 17 September, the year [15]97\" (<b>Old Style</b> — Denmark and the Lutheran territories kept the Julian calendar). The dateline date (17 September) agrees with the catalogue label (\"1597. 17. september. Segeberg\"); no discrepancy. <i>die Lunae</i> = \"on Monday\" — left as the weekday named, not converted to a civil date.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the historical situation is well attested; the one flagged point is the expansion of the subscription contraction <i>Mae. Tae.</i> (\"Your Lordship\" / <i>Magnificentiae Tuae</i> vs. a sovereign's <i>Maiestati Tuae</i>), noted above rather than silently decided.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VII pp.5-7 page images. Letter runs from mid-p.5 (after the end of a Bordingus letter) through p.6, closing 'Tycho Brahe, manu propria. Datae Segebergae 17. Septembris Anni 97.' Collated all place names (Segeberga/Segeberg, Bramstedium/Bramstedt, Bredenbergam/Breitenburg, Lubecam/Lübeck, Hamburgum/Hamburg, Haffniae/Copenhagen) and the steward's name (Christianum/Christian) against the page image; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. No fabrication, omission, or material mistranslation; one interpretive rendering ('Arcis eius' as 'that castle of yours' rather than literal 'its castle') is defensible in context (Breitenburg was just named) and not counted as a defect.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737 f."], "th": {"label": "Henrik Rantzau", "count": 12, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15970331001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15870117001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Rantzau for estates offered and requests a location near a large town where a printing-house, engravers, and instrument-makers are available to complete his books.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15970917001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971008001", "d": "1597-10-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "King Christian IV → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 8 October 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery, with Latin code-switches) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>OS ELSCHELIGE ERLIGE OG VELBIURDIG MAND TYGE BRAHE TIL KNUDSTRUP, VOR MAND OG TIENER.</p><p>CHRISTIAN den 4., med Guds naade Danmarkes, Norges, Wendis og Gottis konning. Vor gunst tilforn. Vid, at thin skriffvelße, som du Os tilschreffvet haffver sub dato Rostoch den 10. dag julij sidst forleden, er Os nu i thenne uge underdanigst tilstillet, udi hvilken iblant andet opregnes, først at du ingen leilighed havde at tale med Os, førend du drogst her af riget, ey heller du vidste, om sligt var Os angenehm eller icke, derfor haffde du Os underdanigst skrifftlig villid lade forstaa, og at Os uden tuiffl er bevist, at du est af med huis du til den astronomische konstes underholdning her til dags haver vaeret med benaadet, ogsaa at [Vi] samme kunst icke ville holde ved magt, sampt andet meere, som wformodendes sig tildraget og uthen thin skyld og brøde thig er hendet og vederfaret, som du meener. Thernest at thu icke hafver sielff then formue, at du samme konst kand med thin bekostning fuldkomme; og om thu endskiønt haffde thine forrige forlenninger, tha estu dog saa kommen til achters, at du nødes til at afhende thit godtz. Och effterthi thu af forberørdte aarsage est trengt til at søge paa andre staeder, hos fremmede potentater og herrer, hielp, middel og raad thend astronomische kunst at forfremme oc thit naffn hos udlaendische ved macht at holde, tha beder thu, at Vi saadan thin reyße icke vilde tage til nogen wnaade, besynderligen forthi thu icke end aldeeles vilt forlade thit faederneland. Vijdere formelder thu, at thersom thet kand bliffve thig forgyndt, at thu her uthi rijget maa thit foretagne arbeyd forfølge, tha vilde thu thet icke forsijge, men Os og thit faederne rijge then aere gierne for nogen anden forunde, om thet kand schee med lijdelige vilkor oc uden thin skade, efftersom thin vijdløfftig skriffvelße derom vijdere indholder. Tha ville Vi thig therpaa naadigst icke forholde, først hvad anlanger, at thu icke haffver hafft leilighed at tale med Os, førend du drogst her af riget, oc thig var vbewist, om sligt kunde vaere Os angenehm eller ey, saa vedstu thig vel at erindre, at du udi nogle uger laae udi Vor kiøbsted Kiøbenhaffn, førend du drogst af rijget, oc icke allene icke forloff af Os begierede, at thu maatte af rijget bort-reijße, som thig haffde burd at giøre, men aldrig engang thalede med Os, undertagendes then ene gang, the Huensche bønder og thu varst for Os udi rette, oc thig bleff paalagt oc befalet at møde for Os paa slottet. Oc enddog thu icke blues thin undschyldning her om at giøre, saaleedes som thu varst Vor jeffnlijge, tha ville Vi dog vdi thenne Vor skriffvelße thig lade vijde, at Vi sligt forstaae, ocsaa at Vi effter thenne dag ville anderleedes vaere af thig respecteret, saafrembt thu i Os skalt befinde en naadig herre oc konning. Angaaende attu icke tuiffler, at Os er bevist thig at vaere opschreffvet noget, som thu varst med forlaenet, og dog formelder sligt at vaere scheed uden thin skyld og brøde, saa vedst thu thig sielff at erindre, huad klagemaal Vore fattige undersaatter oc bønder paa Huaen imod thig hafft hafver. Huorledis thu med kircken ther sammesteds handlet haffver, hvis indkomst og tiender thu udi mange aar til dig haffver annammet, oc ingen forordnet til kircke-verge, men ladet den bygfeldig staa, wdi lijge maade taget jorden fra p[rae]stegaarden, husene enpart aftaget, oc praesten, som ther udi boe skulde og jorden bruge, sig og sin hustru til ophold, hannem haffver thu giffvet nogle β om ugen, och spißet hannem hos thine arbeyds folk, saa ther paa nogle faa aars tid haver vaeret mange praester, og dog icke haffver bekommet kald af meenigheden effter ordinantzen, ey heller ere dømbte derfra, som thet sig burde. Uthi huad maade daabens ord ere i langsommelig thiid udeladt, imod disße rigers vedtagne brug, med thit vidschab, sligt er huer mand ald for vitterligt. Hvilket forberørte med andet meere, som sig paa thet forberørte lille og ringe land tildraget haver oc Os en god rum tijd, førend thet er kommen offentlig for hver mand, til denne dag haffver vaeret bevist, haver aarsag givet, Vore og kronens bønder andre at forlaene, som thennem ved low, ret og landsens vedtagne brug holde ville. Hvad belanger, at thu ikke est saa formuendes, attu kanst af dit eget fuldkomme den foretagne astronomische konst, om thu skiønt solte thit faederne-gods oc haffde enda thine forlaeninger, men er draget af riget fremmede potentater om hielp at besøge, og icke i then meening, at thu thit faederneland plat vilde forlade, og beder underdanigst, at Vi slig thin reyße icke ville tage til wnaade, saa baeres stor tvifl, at du de penninge, som du haver bekommet for thit soldte godtz, hafver anlagt paa the astronomische instrumenter, effterthi ther sijges, at thu i mange tusind thal haffver thennem til herrer og førster at vdlaane, thine børn til baeste oc icke riget til nogen aere eller konsten til forfremmelße. Ocsaa er Os aldeeles ukiaert at forfare, at du hos andre herrer og førster uthi the maader hielp søger, lijgesom Wi oc riget ware saa wformuendes, at Vi sligt ikke af sted kunde komme, mindre thu med qvinde og børn skulle uddrage og slig penninge hos andre bettle. Thog effterthi thet nu giord er, tha nødes Vi sligt at lade skee, og icke Os i nogen maade anmode, enten thu drager af landet eller bliver derudj. Sidst, efftersom thu underdanigst formelder, at dersom thet thig maa forloffves, thit forretagne arbeid her i riget at fuldende, tha vilde du det ikke forsige, oc at thet skee maatte uden thin skade, faa ville Vi thig herpaa naadigst hafve svaret: at saafremt thu thiene vil for en mathematico og giøre thet, hand bør at giøre, tha skaltu først underdanig tilbiude thin tieniste og therom bede som en tienere bør at giøre, og icke med slig fortagdte ord (at thu thet icke vilt forsijge) fremføre thin meening. Naar thet skeer, tha ville Vi Os siden therpaa tilbørligen vide at erklaere. Och efftersom thin skriffvelße thilmed noget seltzom er stijlet, oc icke uden stor wforstand oc dristighed, ligesom Vi thig meere schulde giøre regnschab, huorfor og af huad aarsag Vi paa Vort og kronens godtz giorde forandring, og Vi therhos Os erindrer, hvorleedes thu adschillige snach om Vor kiaere her fader haffver iblant thine e[pistolae] ladet udgaae Hans Kierlighed saavelsom thig self til forvid og forkleinering, tha ville Vi thet med thette Vort breff forbødet haffve, attu samme thit breff, som thu Os nu haffver tilschreffvet, icke lader udgaae paa prent, saa frembt thet skeer, thu icke af Os therfor vilt tiltales og straffes, som thet sig bør. Befalendes thig Gud.</p><p>Skreffvet paa Vort slot Kiøbenhaffn thend 8. octobr: a:o 1597. Vnder Vort signet.<br>Christian.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To our beloved, honourable and well-born man Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup, our man and servant.</b></p><p>Christian the Fourth, by the grace of God King of Denmark, Norway, the Wends and the Goths. Our favour beforehand. Know that your letter, which you wrote to us under date of Rostock, the 10th day of July last past, has now this week been most humbly delivered to us; in which, among other things, the following are set out: first, that you had no opportunity to speak with us before you departed from this realm, nor did you know whether such [a departure] was agreeable to us or not, and that you therefore had wished most humbly to make [it] known to us in writing; and that it is doubtless made known to us that you are deprived of that wherewith you have hitherto been graced for the maintenance of the astronomical art, and also that [we] would not keep up that same art, together with more besides, which (as you maintain) has befallen and come upon you unlooked-for and without your fault and offence. Next, that you do not yourself have the means to complete that same art at your own expense; and that even though you still had your former enfeoffments, you have nonetheless fallen so far into arrears that you are compelled to dispose of your estate. And since, for the aforesaid reason, you are constrained to seek in other places, from foreign potentates and lords, help, means and counsel to advance the astronomical art and to keep up your name among foreigners, you therefore ask that we would not take such a journey of yours in any ill grace, especially because you will not yet altogether forsake your fatherland. Further you state that, should it be granted you that you may here within the realm pursue your undertaken work, you would not refuse it, but would gladly grant to us and your native realm that honour before any other, if it can be done on tolerable terms and without harm to you, as your prolix letter further sets out concerning this.</p><p>Therefore we will most graciously not withhold from you our answer thereon. First, as concerns [the claim] that you had no opportunity to speak with us before you departed from this realm, and that it was unknown to you whether such [a departure] could be agreeable to us or not: you well know how to recall that you lay for some weeks in our market-town of Copenhagen before you departed from the realm, and not only did you not request leave of us that you might travel away out of the realm — as you ought to have done — but you never once spoke with us, save that one time when the Hven peasants and you stood before us at law, and you were enjoined and commanded to appear before us at the castle. And although you are not ashamed to make your excuse on this matter, as if you were our equal, yet we will nonetheless in this our letter let you know that we understand such [conduct], and also that after this day we will be otherwise respected by you, if so be that you are to find in us a gracious lord and king. As regards [the point] that you do not doubt it is made known to us that something has been entered against you concerning that wherewith you were enfeoffed, and yet you state that such [a thing] has happened without your fault and offence: you yourself know how to recall what complaint our poor subjects and peasants on Hven have had against you. [Namely] how you have dealt with the church in that same place, whose income and tithes you have for many years taken to yourself, and have appointed no one as churchwarden, but let it stand in disrepair; likewise [how you] took the land away from the parsonage, demolished part of the buildings, and [as for] the priest, who was to dwell therein and use the land for the support of himself and his wife — to him you have given a few shillings [β] a week, and fed him among your workfolk, so that within the space of a few years there have been many priests there, and yet [they] have not received a call from the congregation according to the ordinance, nor have [they] been [duly] dismissed therefrom, as ought [to have been done]. In what manner the words of baptism have for a long time been left out, contrary to the accepted usage of these realms, with your knowledge — such [a thing] is all too well known to every man. Which aforesaid [matters], together with more besides, that have come to pass on the aforesaid small and mean land, and have been made known to us a good long while before it came openly before every man, to this day — [these] have given cause [for us] to enfeoff others, our and the crown's peasants, who will maintain them by law, justice and the accepted usage of the land.</p><p>As concerns [the claim] that you are not so well provided that you can of your own [resources] complete the undertaken astronomical art, even if you sold your patrimonial estate and still had your enfeoffments, but [that you] have departed from the realm to seek out foreign potentates for help, and not with the intent that you would utterly forsake your fatherland, and [that you] humbly ask that we would not take such a journey of yours in ill grace: great doubt is borne [as to whether] you have laid out the money you have received for your sold estate upon the astronomical instruments, since it is said that you have them out on loan to lords and princes in many thousands [of dalers], for the benefit of your children and not for any honour to the realm or for the advancement of the art. Also it is altogether displeasing to us to learn that you seek help from other lords and princes in those ways, as if we and the realm were so unprovided that we could not bring such [a thing] to pass — much less that you, with wife and children, should travel away and beg such money from others. Yet since it is now done, we are obliged to let such [a thing] happen, and not to importune us in any way [as to] whether you depart from the country or remain therein. Last, since you most humbly state that, if it be permitted you to complete your undertaken work here in the realm, you would not refuse it, and that it might be done without harm to you, we will most graciously have answered you hereon: that if you will serve as a mathematician and do that which he ought to do, then you shall first humbly offer your service and request it thereon, as a servant ought to do, and not put forward your meaning with such veiled words (that you \"would not refuse it\"). When that is done, we will thereafter know how to declare ourselves fittingly thereon. And since your letter is moreover couched in a somewhat strange [manner], and not without great want of understanding and boldness — as though we should render you the more account of why and for what reason we made a change upon our and the crown's estate — and since we therewith recall how you have, among your letters [epistolae], let go forth divers gossip about our dear lord father, to His Love's reproach and disparagement as well as your own: we will therefore by this our letter have forbidden [it], that you do not let that same letter, which you have now written to us, go forth in print; and if that happens, [know] that you will be arraigned and punished by us therefor, as is fitting. Commending you to God.</p><p>Written at our castle of Copenhagen, the 8th of October, in the year 1597. Under our signet.<br>Christian.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), then aged 20 and newly in personal rule after the regency that followed Frederik II's death (1588). This is the famous letter in which the young king repudiates Tycho's appeal, itemises the Hven tenants' and church grievances against him, and refuses to recall the patronage — the document that effectively confirmed Tycho's self-exile.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe (Tyge Brahe) of Knudstrup</b> (1546–1601), styled <i>Vor mand og tiener</i> (\"our man and servant\") — the feudal-vassal formula, deliberately deflating the standing Tycho had claimed.</li><li><b>Occasion / the cited letter.</b> Tycho's own appeal, written <i>sub dato Rostoch den 10. dag julij sidst forleden</i> = \"under date of Rostock, 10 July last past\" — i.e. from Rostock, where Tycho had gone after leaving Hven in spring 1597. This is an <b>in-letter date</b> (the date of the document being answered), not the date of this letter; reproduced as written, not reconciled, per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Structure.</b> The king's reply tracks Tycho's appeal point by point: he first <b>summarises</b> Tycho's four heads (no chance to speak; loss of endowment; lack of private means; the request not to be held in disgrace, and the conditional offer to stay), then <b>answers</b> each in turn. The translation preserves this summary-then-rebuttal structure and the heavy chancery subordination.</li><li><b>Editorial insertion <i>[Vi]</i>.</b> The source as transcribed reads <i>…ogsaa at [Vi] samme kunst icke ville holde ved magt</i> — the bracketed <i>[Vi]</i> (\"we\") is the edition's supplied subject; kept in brackets in both Source and Translation. The clause reports Tycho's grievance that the crown would not maintain the astronomical establishment.</li><li><b>Negation check (load-bearing).</b> Several clauses turn on stacked negations and are rendered with the exact count of the source:</li><li><i>icke allene icke forloff af Os begierede … men aldrig engang thalede med Os</i> = \"not only did you <b>not</b> request leave of us … but you <b>never</b> once spoke with us\" — a double-negative emphatic (\"not only not … but never\"), reproduced as three negations, not collapsed.</li><li><i>thu icke blues thin undschyldning her om at giøre</i> = \"you are <b>not</b> ashamed to make your excuse on this matter\" (i.e. you are shameless enough to excuse yourself) — a single negation; the sense is reproachful, the negation kept.</li><li><i>icke ville holde ved magt / ikke ville tage til nogen wnaade</i> — the requests that the king would <b>not</b> keep up the art / would <b>not</b> take the journey in ill grace are Tycho's reported pleas; the king's own refusal is voiced separately. Kept distinct so the negations are not transferred between speakers.</li><li><i>icke … forsijge</i> (\"would <b>not</b> refuse [it]\") — Tycho's conditional offer to stay, which the king mocks as <i>fortagdte ord</i> (\"veiled / disguised words\"); the negation is central to the king's complaint and is preserved verbatim, including in the parenthetical quotation.</li><li><b>Conditional / mood (flagged).</b> The closing prohibition is a conditional with chancery legal force: <i>tha ville Vi thet … forbødet haffve, attu … icke lader udgaae paa prent, saa frembt thet skeer, thu icke af Os therfor vilt tiltales og straffes</i> — literally \"we will have forbidden [it], that you do <b>not</b> let [the letter] go forth in print; if so be it happens, [that] you will <b>not</b> be arraigned and punished by us therefor.\" The Danish is elliptical: the <i>saa frembt thet skeer</i> (\"if it happens\") clause, with its own <i>icke</i>, is best read as an <b>apodosis of consequence</b> — <i>if you do print it, you will (not escape being) arraigned and punished</i> — i.e. the <i>icke</i> governs an implied \"[think] not [that you will escape]\". The transcription's literal \"you will not be arraigned\" would invert the threat; the king is plainly threatening punishment (cf. <i>som thet sig bør</i>, \"as is fitting\"). The translation renders the evident legal force (\"[know] that you will be arraigned and punished\") while this note records the elliptical construction and the syntactic crux. <b>This is the principal crux of the letter</b> — see the confidence grade.</li><li><b>The peasants' suit.</b> <i>the Huensche bønder og thu varst for Os udi rette</i> = \"the Hven peasants and you stood before us at law\" — the 1590s litigation between Tycho and his Hven tenants over labour-services and the parsonage, heard before the king. Corroborated by the king's itemised rebuttal (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> / <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>: Tycho took the church's incomes and tithes without maintaining it, removed glebe land from the parsonage, etc.).</li><li><b>β (the shilling sign).</b> <i>nogle β om ugen</i> — the glyph <b>β</b> is the early-modern Danish abbreviation for <i>skilling</i> (shilling); rendered \"a few shillings [β],\" the symbol kept in the Source block per protocol. The complaint is that Tycho paid the resident priest a pittance and boarded him among the labourers.</li><li><b>Church / liturgical charge.</b> <i>daabens ord ere … udeladt</i> = \"the words of baptism have … been left out,\" <i>imod disße rigers vedtagne brug</i> \"contrary to the accepted usage of these realms\" — the grave accusation that the baptismal formula was omitted (a Reformation-confessional offence), <i>med thit vidschab</i> \"with your knowledge.\" <i>effter ordinantzen</i> = \"according to the [Church] Ordinance\" (the 1539/1542 Danish <i>Kirkeordinans</i>); <i>kald af meenigheden</i> \"a call from the congregation.\"</li><li><b><i>mange tusind thal</b></i> — \"many thousands [of dalers / in tale]\"; <i>thal</i> (= <i>tal</i>, \"tale/count, reckoning\") is here the money-of-account; \"[of dalers]\" supplied in brackets as the implied unit. The charge is that Tycho lent out large sums to princes for his children's benefit rather than spending on the realm's honour.</li><li><b>The late king and the <i>Epistolae</i>.</b> <i>adschillige snach om Vor kiaere her fader … iblant thine e[pistolae] ladet udgaae</i> = \"let divers gossip about our dear lord father go forth among your letters [epistolae]\" — a reference to Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the published correspondence in which passages touched the late <b>Frederik II</b>; <i>Hans Kierlighed</i> (\"His Love\") is the chancery honorific for the deceased king. The prohibition on printing the present letter follows directly from this grievance.</li><li><b>Latin code-switches.</b> <i>sub dato</i> (\"under date of\"); <i>en mathematico</i> (Latin ablative <i>mathematico</i>, \"[as] a mathematician,\" used inside the Danish syntax); <i>e[pistolae]</i> (abbreviated in the source, expanded in brackets). Preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery Danish, c. 1597).</b> <i>Vor gunst tilforn</i> = the royal address-formula (\"Our favour beforehand,\" §5.5); <i>Vid</i> = <i>vid</i> (\"know!\"); <i>thin/thig/thu</i> = <i>din/dig/du</i>; <i>huis</i> = <i>hvis</i> (here relative \"that which\"); <i>est af med</i> = \"are deprived of / done with\"; <i>forlenninger/forlaeninger</i> = <i>forleninger</i> (\"enfeoffments, fiefs\"); <i>til achters</i> = <i>til agters</i> (\"into arrears, behindhand\"); <i>afhende thit godtz</i> = \"dispose of your estate/goods\"; <i>wnaade</i> = <i>unåde</i> (\"disgrace, ill grace\"); <i>forsijge</i> = <i>forsige</i> (\"refuse, decline\"); <i>lijdelige vilkor</i> = \"tolerable terms\"; <i>vijdløfftig</i> = <i>vidtløftig</i> (\"prolix, long-winded\"); <i>vbewist/bevist</i> = <i>ubevidst/bevidst</i> (\"unknown / known\"); <i>jeffnlijge</i> = <i>jævnlige</i> (\"equal, peer\"); <i>bygfeldig</i> = \"in disrepair, dilapidated\"; <i>p[rae]stegaarden</i> = <i>præstegården</i> (\"the parsonage,\" with the <i>prae-</i> abbreviation expanded in brackets); <i>enpart</i> = \"in part\"; <i>fortagdte</i> = <i>fordægte/fortagne</i> (\"veiled, dissembling\"); <i>seltzom</i> = <i>sælsom</i> (\"strange, odd\"); <i>wforstand</i> = <i>uforstand</i> (\"want of understanding\"); <i>forkleinering</i> = \"disparagement, belittling\"; <i>Befalendes thig Gud</i> = \"Commending you to God.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Skreffvet paa Vort slot Kiøbenhaffn thend 8. octobr. a:o 1597</i> = \"Written at our castle of Copenhagen, the 8th of October 1597\" (O.S.; Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline and the dataset label (8 October 1597, Copenhagen) <b>agree</b>; no date discrepancy in the header. The only date inside the letter belonging to a different document is Tycho's <i>10 July</i> appeal from Rostock (above).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Danish narrative and the rebuttal structure are rendered securely throughout, but the <b>closing print-prohibition conditional</b> (<i>…icke lader udgaae paa prent, saa frembt thet skeer, thu icke … vilt tiltales og straffes</i>) carries a stacked elliptical negation whose literal surface (\"you will not be arraigned\") inverts the king's manifest threat; the translation renders the evident legal force and the apparatus flags the syntactic crux rather than smoothing it.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> XIV p. 123: the closing print-prohibition is printed verbatim and unemended; the elliptical legal sense (the king's threat) is correctly rendered, but the stacked-negation syntax is not editorially resolved. [C] stands (interpretive).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.120-124 as page images (two Read calls, 1-4 then 5). Letter no.134 (Christian IV to Tycho, 8 Oct 1597) runs pp.121-123; checked neighbouring pp.120 and 124 and confirmed they belong to the preceding and following letters, not this one. Collated the full Danish text sentence-by-sentence against the printed page images (not OCR): opening/closing formulas, the Rostock/10 July date, the Copenhagen sojourn and Hven-peasants lawsuit, the Hven church/parsonage/priest complaints, the 'nogle β om ugen' (weekly shillings) detail, the 'mange tusind thal' (many thousand dalers) loan accusation, the baptism-formula clause, the printing prohibition and threat of prosecution, and the Copenhagen castle/8 October 1597 dateline and signature. All proper names (Rostock, Copenhagen, Hven), the one specific date, and all financial/quantity phrases in the English match Dreyer's printed Danish with no addition, omission, or flipped sense. Published grade was C (self-assigned, per brief, unverified); independent collation finds no defect and upgrades to A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15970710001", "next": "brahe-t_15980207001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15970710001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980207001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's letter from Rostock summarising his loss of astronomical stipend, lack of means to continue independently, and assertion that his departure was blameless.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971008001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971107001", "d": "1597-11-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 7 November 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a Greek-derived flourish) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE NON TANTUM GENERIS, SED ET GENIJ ATQUE INGENIJ VIRO INPRIMIS CONSPICUO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>DEDI ad te literas, Nobilissime et Praestantissime Domine OLIGERE, Cognate et Amice charissime, ac tuis respondi, priusquam Rostochio discederem; quas etiam tibi redditas, utut is, quem isthuc ablegavi, reversus nondum sit, nihil ambigo. Nunc vero, cum amanuensem meum cum Presbytero isthuc remeante ad vos mittere decrevissem, denuo te pro necessitudine nostra meis compellandum, et de statu meo brevibus edocendum censui. Scias itaque me ob luem Epidemicam Rostochij ingravescentem in Holsatiam a Nobilissimo viro, Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO literis perbenigne evocatum discessisse, qui aliquot Arcium suarum optione mihi data, quam vellem ex ijs, inhabitandam concessit, ac pluribus etiamnum beneficijs me sibi devincire non cessat. Elegi autem prae caeteris mihi oblatis Wandesburgum, dimidio saltem miliari Hamburgo remotum, eo quod egregia ibi commoditas suppeteret, et vicina civitas, unde in omnes ferme Europae partes frequens internuncius offertur, necessaria omnia procurandi oportunitatem sufficientem concederet. Ubi studijs consuetis more solito gnaviter incumbo, et a praestantibus ac Eruditis hinc inde viris in maiore, quam ipsemet desidero, et unquam in Patria factum, pretio et honore habeor. Et si non consulto hîc in vicinijs Daniae subsistere voluissem, conditiones iam antea fuissem nactus, quales Patria vix largiretur; quas etiam me, quam primum lubuerit, ex divina providentia assecuturum, nihil addubito; ut hanc rerum nostrarum mutationem neutiquam doleam, sed Deum ob id mihi isthaec evenire permisisse confidam, quo meliorem in Germania aut alibi locorum fortunam largiretur. Quod autem tam diu in propinquo moratus sum, hinc factum, ut responsum ad literas Illmi Ducis ULDARICI, meo nomine ad Sermum Regem nostrum perscriptas, quibus etiam meae adiunctae erant, expectarem. Neque enim longius discedendum, aut apud alios Magnates quidpiam tentandum duxi, antequam responsione accepta, qualis etiamnum esset in me et mea studia animus Regis, comperirem. Cum vero his ipsis diebus hanc adeptus sim, qualem ob quorundam in me malevolentiam recepturum praevidebam (licet non adeo asperam et immeritam) id quod tam diu premebam, iam efficere, nihil restat obstaculi. Daboque operam, favente Numine, ut nemini isthic sim amplius mea praesentia molestus.</p><p>Relatum mihi est te fratris mei AXILLI filiam SOPHIAM in uxorem ducturum. Quod equidem lubentissime inaudivi, atque in hoc coniugio omnia vobis quam faustissima et precor et auguror. Et licet meo iudicio nihil rectius facere potueris, quam ut uxore ducta te ab Aulicis compedibus liberares, cum alios tolerabiliores et minus ingratos futuros spes sit (siquidem per viam universae carnis eundum est) videris nihilominus, ne per hanc SOPHIAM terrigenam sublimiorem quandam ac Philosophicam, Diam illam, et mentem a terrenis caducisque ad longe altiora tollentem, atque immortalitati consecrantem negligas. Solet enim in his fieri id quod praecinebat OVIDIUS: Successore novo pellitur omnis amor. At cum et illa, quatenus fert sexus, Philosophica futura sit, ac Amitam SOPHIAM, quoad datur, haud degenerans imitatura, sociam altioris alicuius, quam vulgus capit, SOPHIAe potius quam obstaculum fore, praesagio et opto.</p><p>Plura rerum mearum, temporis angustia praepedito mihi nunc scribere non datur; ex sorore SOPHIA, tibi etiam amica, tum quoque communi Amico Domino Doctore NICOLAO CRAGIO cognosces. Quae vero me concernentia, mi OLIGERE, nosti, fideliter (uti tibi confido) indica, a me tacite habenda: nec invidos osores ac detrectatores meos verearis. Quos et te haud extimescere, omnino mihi persuadeo. Ego iam pridem illorum malevolentiam superare et negligere didici. Vale mi optime OLIGERE, meque quod facis mutuo amare perge.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgo ex arce Ranzoviana die 7. Novembris Anni 97.<br>Tuus ex intimis<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To a man pre-eminently distinguished by nobility not only of birth, but of genius and intellect too, Master Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, [my] kinsman, relation by marriage, and most intimate friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>I sent you a letter, most noble and most excellent Master Holger, dearest kinsman and friend, and answered yours, before I departed from Rostock; and that it was duly delivered to you I in no way doubt, even though the man whom I dispatched thither has not yet returned. But now, since I had resolved to send my amanuensis to you, together with the clergyman who is returning thither, I judged that for the sake of our close bond you should be addressed again by my [letter], and informed in brief of my situation. Know then that, on account of the epidemic pestilence growing worse at Rostock, I withdrew into Holstein, most kindly summoned by letter by that most noble man, Master Henrik Rantzau, who, having given me the choice of several of his castles to dwell in, whichever of them I should wish, does not even yet cease to bind me to himself by further kindnesses. I chose, however, in preference to the others offered me, Wandsbek, removed only half a mile from Hamburg, because there was excellent convenience to hand there, and the neighbouring city — whence frequent couriers go out to almost all parts of Europe — would grant sufficient opportunity of procuring everything needful. Here I diligently apply myself to my customary studies in my usual manner, and am held by distinguished and learned men on every side in greater esteem and honour than I myself desire, and than was ever done in my homeland. And if I had not deliberately wished to stay here in the neighbourhood of Denmark, I should already before now have obtained terms such as my homeland would scarcely bestow; and that I shall obtain these too, by divine providence, as soon as it shall please me, I in no way doubt — so that I by no means grieve at this change in my affairs, but trust that God has permitted these things to befall me for this very reason, that he might grant me a better fortune in Germany or elsewhere. But that I have lingered so long in the vicinity came about from this, that I was awaiting a reply to the letters of the Most Illustrious Duke Ulrich, written in my name to our Most Serene King, to which mine too were joined. For I judged that I should not depart further off, nor attempt anything with other magnates, before, the reply received, I had ascertained what the King's disposition still was toward me and my studies. But since in these very days I have obtained it — of the sort that, on account of the ill-will of certain men toward me, I foresaw I should receive (though not so harsh and undeserved [as that]) — there now remains no obstacle to my carrying out at once that which I had so long held back. And I shall take pains, with the favour of the Deity, that I be no longer a trouble to anyone there by my presence.</p><p>It has been reported to me that you are to take in marriage Sophie, the daughter of my brother Axel. Which indeed I heard with the greatest gladness, and I both pray and forebode all things most auspicious for you in this match. And although in my judgement you could do nothing more rightly than, having taken a wife, to free yourself from the courtly fetters — since there is hope that others will prove more tolerable and less ungrateful (seeing that one must go by the way of all flesh) — see nonetheless that, on account of this earth-born Sophie, you do not neglect that other, loftier and Philosophical Sophia, the divine one, who lifts the mind from things earthly and perishable to things far higher, and consecrates it to immortality. For in these matters it commonly happens as Ovid foretold: <i>Every love is driven out by a new successor.</i> But since she too, so far as her sex allows, is going to be Philosophical, and — not degenerating, as far as is granted — will imitate her aunt Sophie, I foresee and pray that she will rather be a companion of some loftier Sophia, beyond what the common crowd grasps, than an obstacle [to it].</p><p>More of my affairs it is not given me to write now, hindered as I am by shortness of time; you will learn it from [my] sister Sophie, your friend too, and also from our common friend Master Doctor Niels Krag. But the things that concern me which you know, my Holger, disclose faithfully (as I trust you to do), [those things] to be kept silent by me; and do not fear my envious haters and disparagers. Whom that you too do not dread, I am wholly persuaded. I myself learned long ago to overcome and disregard their ill-will. Farewell, my excellent Holger, and continue to love me mutually, as you do.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, from the Rantzau castle, on the 7th day of November in the year [15]97.<br>Yours from the heart,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient.</b> Tycho writes from exile to <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (1574–1642), \"the Learned,\" of <b>Rosenholm</b>, the theologian and statesman. <i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius</i> is the Latinised form of Holger Rosenkrantz; \"Oligere\" is the vocative. He is addressed as <i>consanguineus, affinis et amicus intimus</i> — \"kinsman, relation by marriage, and most intimate friend\": the <i>affinitas</i> is the impending marriage announced in the letter itself.</li><li><b>Date &amp; place.</b> <i>Dabantur Wandesburgo ex arce Ranzoviana die 7. Novembris Anni 97</i> = \"Given at <b>Wandsbek</b> (Wandesburgum), from the <b>Rantzau castle</b>, the 7th day of November, year [15]97\" (O.S.; Denmark and the Lutheran north kept the Julian calendar). The header date matches the dateline; the dataset label (\"1597. 7. november. Wandsburg\") agrees. Wandsbek (now a district of Hamburg) was a seat of Henrik Rantzau; Tycho lodged there from autumn 1597 to spring 1598, where he completed the <i>Mechanica</i> and the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> proofs. He had left Hven in 1597 and passed through Rostock before being invited into Holstein.</li><li><b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (<i>Henricus Ranzovius</i>, 1526–1598), the powerful royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of the Danish king in the duchies of Schleswig-Holstein, humanist, astrologer and patron, who offered Tycho the choice of several of his castles. Rendered \"Master Henrik Rantzau.\"</li><li><b><i>Illmi Ducis ULDARICI</b></i> = <b>Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg</b> (<i>Ulrich III</i>, 1527–1603), maternal grandfather of King Christian IV, who interceded on Tycho's behalf. <i>Illmi</i> = <i>Illustrissimi</i>, \"Most Illustrious\"; <i>Sermum Regem nostrum</i> = <i>Serenissimum</i>, \"our Most Serene King,\" i.e. <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588/1596–1648), then newly crowned. Tycho does not name the king but identifies him as <i>Rex noster</i>.</li><li><b>The marriage.</b> <i>fratris mei AXILLI filiam SOPHIAM</i> = \"Sophie, daughter of my brother <b>Axel</b> (Axilis = Axel Brahe, 1550–1616).\" This is <b>Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe</b> (1578–1646), Tycho's niece, whom Holger Rosenkrantz married in 1598. Tycho was thus already her uncle and was about to become uncle-by-marriage to Holger.</li><li><b>The Sophia wordplay.</b> The letter turns on a sustained pun between three Sophies: (1) <i>SOPHIA terrigena</i> — the \"earth-born Sophie,\" the bride; (2) <i>Sophia Philosophica / Dia illa</i> — capital-S <i>Sophia</i>, divine <b>Wisdom</b> (the <i>philo-sophia</i> a scholar weds in a wife's stead, cf. Wolf's letter of 1571); and (3) <i>Amita SOPHIA</i> — the bride's <b>aunt Sophie</b>, i.e. <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (1559–1643), Tycho's learned sister, herself a noted student of horticulture, chemistry and genealogy, named again at the close as <i>soror SOPHIA</i> (\"[my] sister Sophie\"). The pun is preserved by keeping \"Sophie/Sophia\" throughout and flagged here rather than flattened. <i>terrigenam</i> (\"earth-born\") is the deliberate antithesis to the heavenly Wisdom.</li><li><b>The Ovid tag.</b> <i>Successore novo pellitur omnis amor</i> — \"Every love is driven out by a new successor\": <b>Ovid</b>, <i>Remedia Amoris</i> 462. Kept as the <i>sententia</i> it is; the verse is rendered as a single hexameter-sense line and italicised. Tycho cites it to warn that an earthly love can drive out the love of Wisdom.</li><li><b><i>Aulicis compedibus</b></i> = \"courtly fetters/shackles\" — court service as bondage; the metaphor is kept.</li><li><b><i>per viam universae carnis eundum est</b></i> = \"one must go by the way of all flesh\" — a Scriptural commonplace (cf. Joshua 23:14; 1 Kings 2:2), here a wry gloss on the necessity of marriage; rendered literally.</li><li><b>Niels Krag.</b> <i>communi Amico Domino Doctore NICOLAO CRAGIO</i> = \"our common friend Master Doctor <b>Niels Krag</b>\" (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602), the Danish humanist, historiographer royal and professor at Copenhagen; he also corresponded with Tycho directly (Dreyer VIII). Rendered \"Master Doctor Niels Krag.\"</li><li><b><i>luem Epidemicam</b></i> = \"epidemic pestilence\" — the plague then worsening at Rostock, the stated cause of Tycho's move into Holstein.</li><li><b><i>amanuensis</b></i> kept as \"amanuensis\" (Tycho's secretary/scribe); <i>Presbyter</i> rendered \"clergyman/priest.\"</li><li><b><i>dimidio … miliari</b></i> = \"half a [German] mile\"; the period <i>miliare</i> is far longer than the English mile, so \"half a mile\" is the literal rendering and the magnitude is not converted.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>Salutem plurimam</i> (dicit), \"heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\"; <i>Tuus ex intimis</i> = \"Yours from the heart / from [my] innermost [self]\"; <i>manu propria</i> = \"in his own hand,\" signalling an autograph subscription beneath a secretary's fair copy.</li><li><b>Epistemic hedges preserved.</b> Tycho's qualifiers are kept intact: <i>nihil ambigo / nihil addubito</i> (\"I in no way doubt\"), <i>praesagio et opto</i> (\"I foresee and pray\"), and especially the parenthesis <i>(licet non adeo asperam et immeritam)</i> — \"(though not so harsh and undeserved [as that]),\" his guarded characterisation of the King's reply, which is left as the deliberately oblique statement it is rather than resolved into a flat verdict on Christian IV's letter.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> The sole witness is a later copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b>, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen — the 18th-century Langebek transcripts of the Rosenholm family archive of Tycho's letters to Holger (the originals having since been largely lost), so the text descends through a copy, not an autograph.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clear humanist epistolary prose with no genuine textual or lexical crux; the one interpretive subtlety (the threefold <i>Sophia</i> pun and the deliberately oblique parenthesis about the King's reply) is rendered faithfully and explained in the apparatus rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Tycho to Holger Rosenkrantz, 7 Nov 1597) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.8-10 page images. Every proper name (Rantzau, Wandsbek, Hamburg, Axel, Sophie, Niels Krag, Ulrich), number/date (7 November 97), and the closing 'manu propria' subscription match Dreyer exactly. Considered whether 'dimidio saltem miliari' ('at least/only half a mile') is mistranslated as 'only half a mile', but 'saltem' is genuinely ambiguous between a minimum-threshold and a restrictive ('merely') sense in this context, so declined to record it as a defect rather than manufacture a weak objection. No fabrication, omission, or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15960122001", "next": "brahe-t_15971116001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15960000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15971216001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports withdrawal from plague-struck Rostock to Holstein at Rantzau's invitation, where several castles have been placed at his disposal.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971107001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971116001", "d": "1597-11-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek (near Hamburg), 16 November 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE NON TANTUM GENERIS, SED ET GENIJ ATQUE INGENIJ VIRO IN PRIMIS CONSPICUO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTEGERRIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CORDATISSIME mi OLIGERE, cognate desideratissime et affinis exoptatissime. Postquam priores hisce .......................... viserint tandem an non potius sui ipsius, et patriae, cuius honori studere merito debuissent, maiore incommodo quam meo ................ nostrae satis arguunt compositoris malevolum animum pari praeiudicio et avaritiae nefanda labe infectum:</p><p>Proh superi quid non mortalia pectora cogis<br>Sacra sitis Rosei fontis, praebendaque sacra!</p><p>Dolendum certe est, imo supra modum dolendum, iuvenem hunc Regem, alias suapte natura virtutis capacem et generoso animo praeditum, talibus glaucomatistis ita occaecari et in devia deduci, ut id, quod verum rectumque sit et Regno ac sibi forsan maiore duratione honorificum, quam multa alia, quae inutiliter fiunt et profunduntur, perspicere et sentire nequeat, utpote alienis oculis et auribus tam videns quam audiens. Veniet forsan tempus quando et oculatior, magisque auritus (experientia et usu rerum sic suadente) reddetur. At quantum ad me meaque tamdiu exantlata studia nimis sero. Praeveniam potius, et mihi ............. favente divina providentia, dum adhuc tempus superest ........... iamque alea iacta est, numeratae tesserae re......Bene sit Regi precor, utut mihi praeter culpam et iniuriam non faventi. Bene sit vobis omnibus .............. praesertim integrioribus. Bene sit universae Daniae ex animo opto.</p><p>.............. Deo tra.......... fortuna, sequamur. Iamque vale mi amantissime OLIGERE, et quae hîc desiderantur, in prioribus atque postscriptis lege, TYCHONEMque ex asse tuum sincere redamare, quod et sponte facturus es, nunquam desiste.</p><p>Iterumque et saepius vale.<br>Dabantur subito Wandesburgo prope Hamburgum, die 16. Novembris Anni 97.<br>Tuus intimus et extimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.AMANTISSIME OLIGERE, si res tuæ<br>ullo modo ita ferrent, nihil magis in votis haberem, quam ut peractis Regijs<br>Nuptijs velles Bredenbergam venire et<br>mihi isthic indicato prius tempore, idque per certum et fidelem internuncium,<br>obviam ire: partim invisendi optimi illius Senis RANZOVIJ communis nostri Affinis et Amici<br>integerrimi gratia, partim ut bibliothecam eius, qua vix ampliorem et<br>splendidiorem Germania habet, lustres, sive<br>isthic fuerit Produx, sive non; est enim mihi semper liberum, nec dubito quin et<br>tibi, quem pariter is (prout tua meretur virtus) ex animo diligit; partim etiam,<br>ut nos invicem adhuc semel (dum modo sæpius non liceat) compellemus, antequam<br>longius disiungamur; quod non saltem sanguinis ratio et amicitiæ vinculum, sed<br>et nova inter nos affinitas exposcit. Neque enim ignoras, me<br>post Patrem esse e sponsæ agnatione proximum adeoque primum, ideoque non<br>negligendum aut prætereundum; veluti in cæteris vix admisisti. Atque hæ erunt<br>tibi sufficientes caussæ, imo unica earum, quod isthuc impetrata vel non<br>impetrata venia eas. Et si de me tacere, idque tibi soli secretum reservare,<br>cautius duxeris, libenter id concedam. Mihi enim perinde est, sive quis mea de<br>caussa palam sive clam agat; modo id quod æquum est et res postulat, agat.<br>Indica saltem actutum, quid a te in his præstari possit, ut et ego de meis<br>interea rebus sic eo commodius disponam. Exemplum nuperarum (de quibus in<br>prioribus dictum) literarum nunc non addo. Habebis id alias aut ubi<br>convenerimus, si etiamnum in Cancellaria vestra tectum habetur; uti solent ea,<br>quæ lucem oderunt. De tua enim voluntate nihil addubito. Cum<br>ante mensem circiter Observationes meas Astronomicas invitante Serenitate hîc<br>Wandesburgi renovarem, atque in<br>libro, quo priores Uraniburgi circa<br>æquinoctium vernum desinentes denotâram, has posteriores subiungere voluissem,<br>ut earundem tanto tempore præter solitum interruptarum occasio mihi et alijs in posterum memoria non excideret (quod tamen vix<br>futurum) exposui primum isthic prosa discessus mei ex Insula Venusia, adeoque tota Dania, rationes brevibus<br>comprehensas, moxque dictante genio incidit Carmen elegiacum, quod unâ isthic<br>adscripsi. Cuius hîc te participem reddo, cum sciam te Poëticis delectari, atque<br>ex Pegaseis fontibus canoras hausisse lymphas. Si itaque et tu pariter<br>responderis, non quidem consolandi gratia (cum scias me nihil in his asperi, sed<br>potius omnia ex usu putare), verum ut Uraniæ e Dania vestra in alia magisque gratiosa et commoda<br>loca mecum migranti Propempticum concinnes, nobisque utrinque sic congratuleris,<br>rem tam illi quam mihi non saltem iocundam sed et acceptam præstiteris. Neque<br>authoris nomen prodere opus erit: uti et mea apud te secreta haberi, et nemini<br>quidpiam inde communicari velim. Tradas potius Vulcano, ubi legeris. Sufficit in<br>memoria habere qua vales, cum et antea hæc ita se habere, satis superque<br>memineris. Iamque pluries vale. Dabantur ut supra in literis.</p><p>T. B.</p><p>ALTERA POSTSCRIPTA MINORIS MOMENTI.CUM Præfectum illum, quem discedens Venusiæ<br>nostræ præfeci, DAVIDEM PETRI nomine, apud me<br>suspectum reddiderit Parochus ille prior in Insula, interea dum hîc mecum egit,<br>nescio an odio aliquo privato an rei veritate, et inter alia narrârit, eum, cum<br>Patri tuo optim. mem. inserviret, aliquot centenis eum defraudasse, rogo velis<br>mihi scheda aliqua obiter indicare, quid de huius Præfecti fide vel secus<br>exploratum habeas. Nescio enim quantum Parocho isti fidendum sit, cum quia<br>alteri infensus erat, tum quod me sibi omnino optime cupientem, supra quam credi<br>posset, ante abitum despexerit, adeoque ut contumelias et calumnias, nulla de<br>caussa in os mihi dicere non erubuerit, quas tamen patienter ferens, ex alto<br>derisi, hominemque tanquam sui non compotem, addito viatico et munere, dimisi.<br>Quod te scire volui, ne in mei gratiam quidpiam de eo amplius sis sollicitus.<br>Plura ab ESCHILLO BILLE nostro<br>intelliges, qui acta habet in literas relata et testificata. Datum ut in<br>prioribus.</p><p>T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To a man conspicuous in the first rank not only by the nobility of his lineage, but also by that of his spirit and his talent, Master Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, my kinsman, relation by marriage, and most upright friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most sensible Holger, my most longed-for kinsman and most desired relation, after my earlier [letters?] with these …………………… [they?] shall at last have seen whether [it is] not rather to the greater detriment of themselves and of the homeland — to whose honour they ought by rights to have devoted themselves — than to mine …………… our [affairs?] sufficiently prove the ill-willed disposition of the contriver, infected alike with prejudice and with the abominable taint of avarice:</p><p>Alas, ye gods above, to what do you not drive mortal hearts —<br>the accursed thirst of the Rosy fount, and the sacred prebends to be bestowed!</p><p>It is to be lamented, indeed lamented beyond measure, that this young King — otherwise of his own nature capable of virtue and endowed with a generous spirit — is so blinded by such purblind men [glaucomatistae] and led into byways, that he cannot perceive and feel that which is true and right, and perhaps more honourable to the Kingdom and to himself, in lasting longer, than the many other things that are done and poured out to no purpose — being, as it were, one who both sees with others' eyes and hears with others' ears. There will come a time perhaps when he too will be made more clear-sighted and more hearing (experience and the practice of affairs thus counselling). But as far as concerns me and my studies, so long laboured through, [it will be] too late. I will rather forestall [it], and, with divine providence favouring me ………… while time still remains …………, and now the die is cast, the dice counted [are?] thrown ……… May it go well with the King, I pray — even though he is, beyond any fault or wrong on my part, not favourable to me. May it go well with you all …………… especially with the more upright. May it go well with all Denmark, I wish from my heart.</p><p>…………… to God …………… fortune, let us follow. And now farewell, my most beloved Holger, and what is wanting here, read in my earlier [letters] and in the postscripts; and never cease to return Tycho's love sincerely, as wholly your own — which indeed you will do of your own accord.</p><p>And again and yet again, farewell.<br>Given in haste at Wandsbek near Hamburg, on the 16th day of November in the year [15]97.<br>Your intimate, and your outermost,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPT.</b></p><p>Most beloved Holger, if your affairs would in any way allow it, I should wish for nothing more than that, the Royal Wedding once concluded, you would be willing to come to Breitenburg, and there — the time having first been indicated to me, and that through a sure and faithful go-between — to meet me: partly for the sake of visiting that excellent old man Rantzau, our common kinsman and a most upright friend; partly so that you might survey his library, than which Germany scarcely has an ampler or more splendid one, whether the Statthalter be there at the time or not — for access is always free to me, nor do I doubt that it is to you as well, whom he likewise loves from the heart, as your worth deserves; and partly also, that we might address one another once more, if oftener is not allowed, before we are separated by a greater distance — which not only the tie of blood and the bond of friendship, but also the new kinship between us, demands. Nor indeed are you unaware that, after her father, I am the nearest — indeed the first — of the bride's kin, and therefore not to be neglected or passed over, as in other matters you have scarcely allowed me to be. And these will be sufficient reasons for you — nay, even any one of them alone — that you should go there, whether leave be obtained or not. And if you should judge it more prudent to say nothing of me, and to keep that a secret to yourself alone, I will gladly grant it. For it is all the same to me, whether anyone acts openly or secretly on my account, provided he does what is fair and what the matter requires. Only let me know at once what can be accomplished by you in these matters, so that I too may in the meantime so arrange my own affairs the more conveniently.</p><p>I do not now add a copy of the recent letters, of which I spoke in my earlier ones. You shall have it another time, or when we meet, if it is even now kept hidden in your Chancery, as things that hate the light are wont to be. For of your goodwill I do not doubt at all.</p><p>When, about a month ago, [the fair] weather inviting me, I resumed my astronomical observations here at Wandsbek, and wished to append these later ones in the book in which I had recorded the earlier ones, ending around Uraniborg at the vernal equinox — so that the occasion of their being interrupted for so long a time, beyond what was usual, might not in future slip from my memory or others' (though that is scarcely likely to happen) — I set out there first, in prose, the reasons, briefly comprised, for my departure from the Island of Hven, and indeed from the whole of Denmark; and presently, my genius dictating, there fell out an elegiac poem, which I appended there together with it. Of this I now make you a partaker, since I know you delight in poetic things, and have drawn tuneful waters from the Pegasean springs. If, then, you too should reply in kind — not, indeed, for the sake of consoling me, since you know that I think there is nothing harsh in these things, but rather that all works to my advantage — but so as to compose a Propempticon for Urania, migrating with me from your Denmark into other, more gracious and convenient places, and so to congratulate us both, you will render a thing not merely pleasant but also welcome, to her as much as to me. Nor will it be necessary to disclose the author's name: just as I wish my own [verses] to be kept secret with you, and nothing of them to be communicated to anyone. Rather, once you have read it, you should hand it over to Vulcan. It is enough to keep alive in practice that gift in which you excel, since you remember well enough, and more than enough, that this has always been so. And now, farewell many times over. Given as above in the letter.</p><p>T. B.</p><p><b>A SECOND POSTSCRIPT, OF LESSER MOMENT.</b></p><p>Since that Bailiff whom, on departing, I set over our Hven — David Petri by name — has been made suspect to me by that former Parish Priest of the Island, while he was lately dealing with me here (I know not whether from some private hatred, or from the truth of the matter), and related, among other things, that he, while in service to your father of blessed memory, had defrauded him of some hundreds, I ask that you be willing to indicate to me, in passing, on some note, what you have ascertained about this Bailiff's honesty or otherwise. For I do not know how far that Parish Priest is to be trusted, both because he was ill-disposed toward the other, and because, before his departure, he scorned me — who wished him extremely well — beyond what could be believed, so much so that he did not blush to utter insults and slanders to my face for no cause; which, however, bearing patiently, I laughed off loftily, and dismissed the man, as one not in possession of himself, with travel-money and a gift added. This I wished you to know, lest for my sake you should be further troubled about him. You will learn more from our Eskild Bille, who has the proceedings set down in writing and attested. Given as in the earlier letters.</p><p>T. B.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / context.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Wandsbek (Wandsburg)</b>, the castle near Hamburg held by his patron Heinrich Rantzau, where Tycho lodged in the autumn of 1597 after his departure from Denmark and Hven (he left in the spring/summer of 1597). The letter's bitterness toward \"the young King\" and his counsellors belongs to this moment of grievance over the loss of his royal support.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius de Rosenholm</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), the Danish nobleman of Rosenholm, theologian and humanist, Tycho's kinsman and relation by marriage (<i>consanguineus, affinis</i>). The address-superscription's word-play on <i>generis / genij / ingenij</i> (lineage / spirit / talent) is kept in the rendering.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Wandesburgo prope Hamburgum</i> = <b>Wandsbek</b>, then a Holstein estate of Heinrich Rantzau, now a district of Hamburg. (A companion letter to Rosenkrantz of <b>7 November 1597</b> is dated <i>\"Wandesburgo ex arce Ranzoviana\"</i> — \"from the Rantzovian castle\" — fixing the residence.)</li><li><b>\"The young King.\"</b> <i>iuvenem hunc Regem</i> = <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (b. 1577), who had assumed personal rule in August 1596 (aged 19); Tycho blames not the King himself but the counsellors who, he says, blind him.</li><li><b>Verse (the centre of the letter).</b> Tycho adapts <b>Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> III.56–57</b>: <i>\"Quid non mortalia pectora cogis, / auri sacra fames!\"</i> (\"To what do you not drive mortal hearts, accursed thirst for gold!\"). Tycho keeps the first line nearly verbatim (<i>Proh superi quid non mortalia pectora cogis</i>) and rewrites the second as a pun: <i>\"Sacra sitis <b>Rosei</b> fontis, praebendaque sacra!\"</i> — \"the accursed thirst of the <b>Rosy</b> fount, and the sacred <b>prebends</b>.\" <i>Rosei fontis</i> (\"of the rosy/Rosen fount\") puns on the name <b>Rosenkrantz / Rosenholm</b> and/or on the courtier faction; <i>praebenda</i> are ecclesiastical/court <b>prebends</b> (stipendiary benefices). The line is kept as verse. <i>Sacra</i> is used antithetically — \"accursed\" (the Virgilian <i>sacra</i> = \"execrable\") in the first sense, \"sacred/holy\" (the benefices) in the second; the bitter irony is in the source and is preserved, not smoothed.</li><li><b><i>glaucomatistae</b></i> — \"purblind men,\" literally those afflicted with <i>glaucoma</i> (clouded sight): a coinage of contempt for the King's counsellors, picked up by the following \"sees with others' eyes, hears with others' ears.\" Kept as metaphor.</li><li><b><i>compositor</b></i> — <i>compositoris malevolum animum</i> = \"the ill-willed disposition of the contriver/intriguer.\" Here <i>compositor</i> is the schemer who \"composed\" the situation against Tycho, not a typesetter; the referent (a specific hostile counsellor) is left unnamed in the text and so is not supplied here.</li><li><b><i>alea iacta est</b></i> — \"the die is cast\" (the Caesarian/Suetonian tag); followed by <i>numeratae tesserae re……</i> \"the dice counted … [thrown?].\" The verb after <i>re</i> is lost in the lacuna; <b>[thrown]</b> is offered conjecturally in square brackets (e.g. <i>reiectae</i> / <i>reiacta</i>) and flagged — the source breaks off.</li><li><b><i>Tuus intimus et extimus</b></i> — \"Your intimate, and your outermost\": a paradoxical sign-off (innermost in affection, outermost — i.e. furthest off / on the periphery — in place or fortune), rendered literally and left as the pointed flourish it is.</li><li><b><i>ex asse tuum</b></i> — \"wholly your own\" (<i>ex asse</i> = \"to the whole, entirely,\" the Roman inheritance idiom).</li><li><b>Lacunae (textual crux — the governing limitation).</b> The sole witness is a later <b>afskrift (copy) in Langebek's Diplomatarium</b> at the Rigsarkivet; it is <b>damaged/illegible in many places</b>, marked here by runs of dots exactly as transmitted. The lacunae fall in load-bearing positions — the opening conditional period (<i>Postquam priores hisce … viserint … nostrae satis arguunt</i>), the <i>Praeveniam … alea iacta est</i> sentence, and the closing <i>Deo tra…… fortuna, sequamur</i> clause — so the connective logic of several sentences is genuinely uncertain. No lacuna has been filled in the translation beyond the single bracketed conjecture <b>[thrown]</b> and bracketed clarifying subjects (<b>[letters?]</b>, <b>[affairs?]</b>, <b>[they?]</b>) flagged with question marks; nothing is silently restored.</li><li><b>Negations.</b> The source carries three load-bearing negatives, all preserved: <i>an <b>non</b> potius sui ipsius … incommodo</i> (\"whether <b>not</b> rather to [their own] detriment\"); <i>mihi praeter culpam et iniuriam <b>non</b> faventi</i> (\"<b>not</b> favourable to me, beyond any fault or wrong [on my part]\"); and <i>nunquam desiste</i> (\"<b>never</b> cease\"). None added, none dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 16. Novembris Anni 97</i> = <b>16 November 1597</b>, Old Style (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dateline matches the catalogue label; no discrepancy. <i>Dabantur</i> (\"Given/Issued\") kept.</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> Both postscripts to Tycho's 16 November 1597 letter to Holger (\"Oliger\") Rosenkrantz — the main POSTSCRIPTA and the shorter ALTERA POSTSCRIPTA — were entirely absent from the prior transcription; only the letter's final valediction survived as the published tail.</li><li><b>Addressee and the new kinship.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), later a leading Danish statesman and theologian, is here anticipating marriage into Tycho's own family: Thoren records that \"in the late 1590s, Holger married a daughter of ... Axel Brahe (Tycho's brother)\" [Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>], and Christianson identifies her as Tycho's niece Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe, who with Holger \"maintained a learned household\" at Rosenholm comparable to Uraniborg [Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>]. This dates and explains the letter's talk of a \"nova inter nos affinitas\" (new kinship) and Tycho's claim to be, after the bride's father, her nearest kinsman.</li><li><b>The Royal Wedding.</b> \"Peractis Regijs Nuptijs\" refers to the wedding of King Christian IV to Anna Catherine of Brandenburg, held at Haderslevhus in November 1597 — the very event this letter, dated 16 November 1597, anticipates. Christianson records that Heinrich Rantzau himself was travelling to attend the same wedding, and that Tycho corresponded with him about it at just this time [Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>].</li><li><b>Rantzau and Breitenburg.</b> \"Bredenberga\" is Rantzau's seat of Breitenburg, whose library Tycho calls the ampler and more splendid than any other in Germany — consistent with Lohmeier's account of the Breitenburg library (c. 6,300 volumes by 1590). Tycho was resident at Rantzau's Wandsbek castle when writing.</li><li><b>A lost occasional poem.</b> Tycho reports composing, about a month earlier (i.e. October 1597), a prose account of his reasons for leaving Hven and Denmark plus an accompanying elegiac Latin poem, entered into his observing log to mark the resumption of his astronomical observations at Wandsbek after the interruption since leaving Hven in March 1597. He asks Holger, in strict confidence — \"hand it over to Vulcan\" (burn it) once read — to compose a companion <i>Propempticon</i> (a classical send-off/farewell-poem genre) for \"Urania\" migrating with him from Denmark. This is a new, previously unpublished data point on Tycho's own poetic self-fashioning of the 1597 departure.</li><li><b>David Petri and Eskild Bille.</b> The second postscript concerns David Petri, i.e. David Pedersen, independently confirmed as \"Tycho Brahe's bailiff on Hven 1597-1601\" left in charge of Uraniborg and Stjerneborg on Tycho's 1597 departure [Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>, Biographical Directory s.v. \"Pedersen, David\"]. Hven's former parish priest had accused David of once defrauding Holger's late father while in his service; Tycho, distrusting the priest (whom he says he dismissed with severance pay despite the priest's insults), asks Holger's independent judgment and names \"Eschillus Bille\" — Eskild (Eske) Bille — as holding the documented record; a letter from Eske Bille to Tycho is independently attested in Zeeberg's edition of the Sophie Brahe/Urania Titani correspondence register.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin that survives is rendered securely and the verse pun is read with confidence, but the <b>single witness is a damaged afskrift riddled with lacunae</b> at syntactically decisive points (the opening period, the <i>alea iacta est</i> clause, and the closing <i>Deo tra…… fortuna, sequamur</i>). The connective sense of those sentences cannot be recovered from the transmitted text; the gaps are reproduced, the few conjectures bracketed and flagged, and nothing smoothed. The crux is the lacunose state of the copy.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII (<i>\"Ex apographo … Diplomatario Langebekii\"</i>): Dreyer prints the <b>same lacunae</b> (rows of dots) at the opening, the <i>alea iacta est</i> clause, and the closing <i>Deo i r a / tra … fortuna, sequamur</i> — the same damaged copy, nothing recoverable. [C] stands.</p><p>The translation is sound throughout; the underlying Latin shows no sign of corruption. Two minor points of phrasing are flagged rather than settled: \"invitante Serenitate\" could denote either fair/clear weather inviting the resumption of observations (the reading adopted, fitting an astronomer's log entry) or an honorific \"his Serenity\" (a person); and \"Sufficit in memoria habere qua vales\" is compressed epistolary idiom rendered by inferred sense rather than word-for-word. Neither affects the letter's substance. Not yet collated against a modern critical edition of the correspondence. Grade B: sound, complete, with two minor flagged points.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against TBOO VIII pp.10-12 (Dreyer page images, not OCR), including the damaged-manuscript passages that Dreyer himself prints with ellipses/brackets (e.g. '.......... viserint tandem...', '[ne]que'-style supplements do not occur here but the run of dots does). source_text matches Dreyer verbatim throughout, dot-for-dot in the lacunose opening paragraph and postscript. All proper names (OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO, RANZOVIJ/Breitenburg, CHRISTIERNUM/IOHANNEM LANGIUM, DAVIDEM PETRI, ESCHILLO BILLE) and both postscripts check out against the page images, and the closing date/signature ('Wandesburgo prope Hamburgum, die 16. Novembris Anni 97 / Tuus intimus et extimus Tycho Brahe manu propria') is exact. The translation properly hedges every damaged/reconstructed span with brackets rather than silently smoothing it over. Despite the self-assigned grade C, no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, HEDGE_LOSS or TEXT_ERROR was found on full collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15971107001", "next": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15960000001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15971216001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Laments the ill-will and avarice of those who misled the young King against him, driving him from Denmark; text is heavily fragmentary.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971116001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971123001", "d": "1597-11-23", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "David Fabricius", "ck": "David Fabricius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "David Fabricius → Tycho Brahe — [Resterhave / Ostfriesland], 23 November 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with astronomical symbols) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 316 fol. (inserted between leaves 153 and 154)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISS. ET EXCELLENTISS. DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO IN KNUDSTRUP ET EQUITI DANO, DOMINO ET AMICO MEO SUMMA OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et Excellentissime Domine TYCHO, cum post abitum tabellarij summopere tunc festinantis observationes in ♃ ad 20 Nov. diem habitas calculo subijcere vellem, inveni me errasse in assignatione distantiae ♃ ab Aldeb. quae revera tunc erat 8° 51', non vero 9°51', quod ex festinatione factum esse scias. Distantia vero ♃ et Hu. Orio. vera fuit 19°8', ut post diligenti consideratione sextantis inveni. Verum sextans quo utor (ut et in literis indicavi) mihi nequaquam satisfacit, maxime propter vulgarem divisionem, eamque minus ratam. Sic ergo se habent.</p><p>20 Nov. H. 12 P. M.</p><p>Dist. ♃ et Aldeborae\t8°\t51'<br>Dist. ♃ et Hu. dext. Orion.\t19\t8<br>Altit. Merid. ♃\t57\t54½ vel 55'<br>Altit. Aldeb.\t52\t0<br>Altit. Aequat.\t36\t22\tErgo se recte positio instr. quadr. tunc habuit.<br>Decl. ♃\t21\t33 fere B.<br>Asc. R.\t70\t13</p><p>Asc. hanc ex asc. R. Aldeb. et ex asc. R. Hu. dex. Orion. inquisivi viceversa. Asc. R. Hu. Orio. ex loco longit. et latit. iuxta tuum Canonem constitui videlicet hoc tempore 83 Gr. 23' fere.</p><p>Longitudo ♃\t11\t39\t♊<br>Latitudo ♃\t0\t42½\tMerid.</p><p>Fuit ergo medio motu ☉ iuxta tuam restitutionem oppositus die 22 Nov. Hor. 2½ circiter P. M. iuxta verum motum ☉ opponebatur die 23 Nov. Hor. 9 A. M.</p><p>Si Ephemerides STADIJ se recte habent, calculus parum deviat. Sed tuum de his iudicium peto, et quae calculi utriusque sit differentia.</p><p>♂ quoque hisce diebus aliquoties diligenter considerabo, quod tamen ut in posterum subtilius et expeditius fiat, T. Ex. observationum mearum in literis propositarum dilucida resolutione haud dubie efficiet. Et hanc designationem Tabellario in vicina urbe adhuc commoranti transmisi, ut literis meis eam insereret. Vale.</p><p>De altitudine ☾ capienda dubium.</p><p>Cum ☾ limbus uno quasi minuto extans per rimulam capitur, observatio certa esse non potest; nam centrum ☾ haberi nequit. Nam circa unum latus pinnacidij per unam rimulam conspicitur limbi extremitas, per alteram rimam conspicitur maior portio ☾. Relinquitur igitur, incertam esse altitudinem, cum nec medium Lunae (ut in stellis) habeatur, nec etiam revera sciri possit, an portio unius vel 2 minut. vel plus minusve de corpore Lunari conspiciatur, et proinde ignoretur, an semidiameter ☾ uno vel duobus minutis minor sit addenda vel subtrahenda. Hoc dubium in accipienda ☾ altitudine certa me valde torquet. Cupio igitur certi quid in hoc negotio ex T. E. cognoscere.</p><p>Vale.<br>23 Nov. 97.<br>T. Exc.<br>observ.<br>D. FABRICIUS.</p><p>N. B. Quomodo etiam minimarum stellarum centrum per rimulas<br>sext. vel quadr. haberi possit, valde miror (cum illarum diameter vix 1<br>minut.). Aut enim rimulas valde amplas esse oportet, et sic incertus per<br>rimulam talem laxam vel amplam prospectus esse videtur, aut si rimulæ<br>mediocres, non videbitur. Quomodo igitur agendum sit, magnopere scire cupio,<br>et quæ rimularum pro minimis stellis laxitas vel amplitudo maxima esse<br>debeat in instru. 4 pedum, cognoscere valde desidero.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most excellent lord Tycho Brahe, lord of Knudstrup and Danish knight, my lord and friend to be honoured with the deepest respect.</b></p><p>Most noble and most excellent lord Tycho, when, after the departure of the courier — who was then in the greatest haste — I wished to subject to calculation the observations made on Jupiter [♃] for the day of 20 November, I found that I had erred in the assignment of the distance of Jupiter [♃] from Aldebaran, which in truth was then 8° 51', and not 9° 51' — which, you should know, came about through the haste. But the true distance of Jupiter [♃] and the shoulder of Orion [Hu. Orio.] was 19° 8', as I afterwards found upon careful examination of the sextant. The sextant I use, however (as I also indicated in my letter), by no means satisfies me, chiefly on account of its common [scale] division, and that one less exact. So, then, they stand as follows.</p><p>20 November, 12 hours after noon [12 P.M.].</p><p>Distance of Jupiter [♃] and Aldebaran\t8°\t51'<br>Distance of Jupiter [♃] and the right shoulder of Orion\t19\t8<br>Meridian altitude of Jupiter [♃]\t57\t54½ or 55'<br>Altitude of Aldebaran\t52\t0<br>Altitude of the equator\t36\t22\tTherefore the setting of the quadrant instrument was then correct.<br>Declination of Jupiter [♃]\t21\t33 nearly, North.<br>Right ascension\t70\t13</p><p>This right ascension I sought out from the right ascension of Aldebaran and from the right ascension of the right shoulder of Orion, [and] conversely. The right ascension of the shoulder of Orion I established from its place in longitude and latitude according to your Canon, namely at this time 83 degrees 23' nearly.</p><p>Longitude of Jupiter [♃]\t11\t39\tGemini [♊]<br>Latitude of Jupiter [♃]\t0\t42½\tSouth [Merid.]</p><p>It [Jupiter] was therefore, by the mean motion of the Sun [☉] according to your restoration, in opposition on the day of 22 November, at about hour 2½ after noon [P.M.]; by the true motion of the Sun [☉] it was in opposition on the day of 23 November, at hour 9 before noon [A.M.].</p><p>If Stadius's Ephemerides are correct, the calculation deviates but little. But I ask your judgement on these matters, and what the difference of the two calculations may be.</p><p>Mars [♂], too, I shall examine carefully several times in these days; yet that this may be done more subtly and more expeditiously hereafter, Your Excellency will doubtless bring about by a clear resolution of my observations set out in my letter. And I have sent this notation to the courier, who is still staying in the neighbouring town, so that he might insert it among my letters. Farewell.</p><p>A doubt concerning the taking of the Moon's [☾] altitude.</p><p>When the limb of the Moon [☾], standing out by as it were one minute, is captured through the little slit, the observation cannot be certain; for the centre of the Moon [☾] cannot be obtained. For around one side of the sight [pinnule], through one little slit the edge of the limb is seen, [and] through the other slit a greater portion of the Moon [☾] is seen. It remains, therefore, that the altitude is uncertain, since neither is the middle of the Moon obtained (as with the stars), nor indeed can it truly be known whether a portion of one or 2 minutes, or more or less, of the lunar body is seen, and accordingly it is not known whether the semidiameter of the Moon [☾], smaller by one or two minutes, is to be added or subtracted. This doubt in taking a certain altitude of the Moon [☾] greatly torments me. I desire, therefore, to learn something certain in this matter from Your Excellency.</p><p>Farewell.<br>23 November [15]97.<br>Your Excellency's<br>observer,<br>D. Fabricius.</p><p><b>N.B.</b> How, too, the centre of even the smallest stars can be obtained through the slits of a sextant or quadrant, I greatly wonder (since their diameter is scarcely 1 minute). For either the slits must be very wide — and then the sighting through such a loose or wide slit seems uncertain — or, if the slits are of middling [width], nothing will be seen [at all]. How, therefore, one is to proceed, I very much desire to know; and what the greatest looseness or width of the slits ought to be for the smallest stars, in an instrument of 4 feet, I very much desire to learn.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>D. Fabricius</i> = <b>David Fabricius</b> (1564–1617), Frisian Lutheran pastor (then at Resterhave / Ostfriesland) and observational astronomer; discoverer of the variable star Mira Ceti (1596). He had opened a correspondence with Tycho and would visit him at Wandsbek in 1598; here he is in the deferential posture of a provincial observer submitting raw data to Tycho for adjudication. The subscription <i>observ[ator]</i> (\"observer\") marks the role he claims toward Tycho. This letter survives as an autograph inserted between leaves 153 and 154 of GKS 316 fol. (the <i>Thesaurus Observationum</i>), the leaf-block (150–157) of Fabricius's own observations Tycho appended to his 1597 observations.</li><li><b>Recipient titulature.</b> <i>Dominus in Knudstrup et Eques Danus</i> = \"lord of <b>Knudstrup</b> and Danish knight\" — Tycho's ancestral seat in Scania and his noble rank; titulature kept in full per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Astronomical objects / abbreviations.</b></li><li><i>Aldeb. / Aldeborae</i> = <b>Aldebaran</b> (α Tauri).</li><li><i>Hu. Orio. / Hu. dext. Orion.</i> = <i>Humerus dexter Orionis</i>, <b>the right shoulder of Orion</b> (the bright star at Orion's right shoulder, conventionally α Orionis / Betelgeuse). Expanded in the translation; the manuscript's contracted form is kept in the Source block.</li><li>The planet/luminary glyphs are named in English per §5.3 with the glyph retained in the Source block: ♃ = <b>Jupiter</b>, ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b>, ♂ = <b>Mars</b>, ♊ = <b>Gemini</b>.</li><li><b>Technical terms (controlled glossary, §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>distantia</i> → distance (angular separation); <i>sextans</i> → <b>sextant</b> (Tycho's <i>sextans astronomicus</i>); <i>altitudo meridiana</i> → <b>meridian altitude</b>; <i>altitudo aequatoris</i> (\"altitude of the equator\") → the altitude of the celestial equator, i.e. the co-latitude check by which Fabricius confirms the quadrant's setting; <i>declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>ascensio recta</i> (<i>Asc. R.</i>) → <b>right ascension</b>; <i>longitudo / latitudo</i> → <b>(celestial) longitude / latitude</b>; <i>semidiameter</i> → <b>semidiameter</b>; <i>pinnacidium</i> → <b>sight (pinnule)</b> (§5.2); <i>quadrans</i> → <b>quadrant</b>. The instrument-noun <i>instr. quadr.</i> is expanded as \"the quadrant instrument.\"</li><li><b><i>vulgaris divisio</b></i> — \"the common [scale] division\": Fabricius complains his sextant has only an ordinary (undivided-into-transversals) graduation, \"and that one less exact\" (<i>eamque minus ratam</i>). The bracketed \"[scale]\" marks an editorial insertion clarifying <i>divisio</i>.</li><li><b><i>tuum Canonem</b></i> — \"your <b>Canon</b>\": Tycho's canon/table of fixed-star places (his star catalogue), against which Fabricius reduces the shoulder of Orion to 83°23' right ascension. Rendered \"Canon,\" not modernised.</li><li><b><i>iuxta tuam restitutionem</b></i> — \"according to your <b>restoration</b>\": Tycho's restored solar theory / tables (<i>restitutio</i> per §5.1, Tycho's programme of <i>astronomia instaurata</i>). The mean-motion opposition (22 Nov.) and true-motion opposition (23 Nov.) of Jupiter to the Sun are reckoned from it.</li><li><b><i>Ephemerides Stadij</b></i> — the <b>Ephemerides of Johannes Stadius</b> (1527–1579), the widely used Copernican-based ephemerides; <i>Stadij</i> is the genitive of <i>Stadius</i>. Name kept.</li><li><b>Symbols / numerals.</b> <i>B.</i> after the declination value (<i>21 33 fere B.</i>) is read as <b>Borealis / Boreal = North</b> (the declination is north); rendered \"North.\" This reading is consistent with <i>Merid.</i> beside the latitude (see next). <b>Flagged:</b> <i>B.</i> is an abbreviation; \"North\" is the supported expansion in context (a +21° declination in Gemini), but the single letter is not spelled out in the source.</li><li><b><i>Merid.</b></i> (beside <i>Latitudo ♃ 0 42½</i>) — abbreviation, here taken as <b>Meridiana</b> = northern (boreal) ecliptic latitude, i.e. the latitude lies to the north; rendered \"[North]\" in brackets. <b>Crux flagged:</b> <i>Merid.</i> could in principle abbreviate <i>Meridionalis</i> (southern); the bracketed \"North\" follows the boreal sense paired with the boreal declination, but the abbreviation alone does not decide the hemisphere — recorded, not silently resolved. (See Confidence.)</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The dateline <i>23 Nov. 97</i> (O.S.; Denmark and the Lutheran German lands kept the Julian calendar) <b>agrees with the dataset label</b> <i>1597. 23. november</i>; no discrepancy. The internal dates (20 Nov. observation; 22/23 Nov. computed oppositions) are reproduced exactly as written and are <b>not</b> converted. The time-notation <i>P. M.</i> / <i>A. M.</i> is the period's \"post/ante meridiem\" reckoning from noon (so <i>H. 12 P. M.</i> = 12 hours after noon = midnight ending 20 Nov.); rendered \"after noon / before noon\" with the Latin retained in brackets, not silently modernised to clock time.</li><li><b>Negations.</b> The Moon-altitude paragraph turns on a chain of negated clauses (<i>non potest … nequit … nec … nec … ignoretur</i>); all four negations are reproduced one-for-one, and the disjunction <i>addenda vel subtrahenda</i> (\"to be added or subtracted\") is kept as a genuine either/or — Fabricius's point is precisely that he cannot tell which.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (§5.5).</b> <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\" (×3); subscription <i>T. Exc. observ. D. Fabricius</i> → \"Your Excellency's observer, D. Fabricius.\"</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A further note, headed \"N.B.\", added after Fabricius's signature and dateline (23 November 1597) — a true postscript in substance if not in name, continuing the instrument-sighting question already under way in the published tail (the difficulty of centring the Moon's disc in a pinhole sight, \"per rimulam\").</li><li><b>The correspondent.</b> David Fabricius (1564–1617), the East Frisian pastor-astronomer later famous for discovering the variability of Mira Ceti; an independent observer whom Kepler rated the finest observational astronomer in Europe after Tycho's death, and who briefly joined Tycho's household staff at Wandsbek in 1598 and again at Prague in 1601 [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (Cambridge, 2000)].</li><li><b>Where this letter physically sits.</b> This exact letter of 23 November 1597 is not merely epistolary — it survives bound into Tycho's own manuscript observation volume, inserted between folios 153 and 154, immediately after a note in Tycho's hand that his 1597 observations were \"somewhat poor\" and needed supplementing with Fabricius's own (folios 150–157, all in Fabricius's hand).</li><li><b>Technical vocabulary.</b> \"Rimula\" (little slit/crack) is the standard term for the narrow aperture-sight fitted to Tycho's sextants and quadrants; Mosley's index glosses the type as \"slit-sights\" (pp. 55–56, 105–106) [Tier 1: Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (Cambridge, 2007)] — confirming the rendering used here.</li><li><b>Crux.</b> The passage poses a genuine, unresolved technical dilemma in Fabricius's own words (wide slit = imprecise sighting; narrow slit = invisible star) rather than a corrupt reading; nothing in the Latin itself is in doubt, but the abbreviations \"sext.\", \"quadr.\", and \"instru. 4 pedum\" are silently expanded in translation only, not in the source.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'Latitude of Jupiter 0°42½′ North [Merid.]' corrected to 'South [Merid.]' — 'Merid.' abbreviates Meridionalis (South), as confirmed by Dreyer TBOO VIII p.13, where the same letter uses 'B.' (Borealis) for North two lines above in Jupiter's declination.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the technical Latin and the observational sense are rendered securely and the dateline is uncontested. The one flagged crux is the hemisphere abbreviation <b><i>Merid.</b></i> beside Jupiter's latitude (and, secondarily, <i>B.</i> beside the declination): <i>Merid.</i> is expanded to boreal/North to agree with the boreal declination, but the bare abbreviation does not by itself fix north vs. south, and is marked \"[North]\" rather than silently decided.</p><p>The Latin is clean and the vocabulary is corroborated by Mosley's technical glossary of Tycho's sighting apparatus, so the translation itself is secure. What keeps this at B rather than A is that the passage is uncollated against any prior published English rendering of this specific postscript, and the instrument terminology (rimula/pinnacidium as a coherent sighting system) would benefit from a specialist check against Tycho's own <i>Mechanica</i> description of the sextant and quadrant apertures. Upgrade path: collate against <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598) §on sextants, and against any existing translation of the folio-153/154 insertion in the observation volumes.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VIII pp.13-15 (page images, not OCR text layer), including the full observation table (all degree/minute pairs for the Jupiter-Aldebaran and Jupiter-Orion distances, altitudes, declination, right ascension, longitude and latitude), the opposition-timing paragraph, and the N.B. postscript on sextant/quadrant slit width. One directional mistranslation found (South rendered as North for Jupiter's ecliptic latitude); every other number and name matches Dreyer exactly.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). \"Merid.\" abbreviates Meridionalis (South), not North; the same letter uses \"B.\" (Borealis) for North two lines earlier when giving Jupiter's declination, so \"Merid.\" here is the parallel abbreviation for the opposite direction. The English silently glosses it as \"North\", flipping Jupiter's ecliptic latitude to the wrong hemisphere in a letter whose entire content is precise numerical astronomical data. At: “Latitude of Jupiter [♃] 0 42½ North [Merid.]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VIII pp.13-15, all 3 pages read). P.13 clearly shows 'Decl. 4  21  33 fere B.' (Declination, North) followed a few lines later by 'Latitudo 4  0  42½ Merid.' (Latitude, South) — the letter's own internal contrast between 'B.' and 'Merid.' confirms the audit's identification. No other cruces found on the three pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 316 fol.."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Corrects an earlier error in Jupiter observations, supplying revised distances from Aldebaran and the shoulder of Orion, and notes dissatisfaction with his sextant's graduated scale.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971123001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971213001", "d": "1597-12-13", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Graz", "t": "Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe — Graz, 13 December 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII, Epistolae) · MS witness: A = Vienna, ÖNB cod. Vindob. lat. 10702 (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>VIRO NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DANO, MATHEMATICORUM PRINCIPI AD MANUS.</p><p>CUM te, Vir amplissime, Mathematicorum omnium non huius tantum aetatis, sed totius aevi Monarcham constituerit incomparabilis doctrina iudicijque praestantia, iniquum fecero, si opusculo de proportione coelorum nuper in lucem dato (sub titulo: Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum) ullam gloriam, tuo iudicio et commendatione neglecta, aucuper. Quae me causa movit, ut ignotus ex hoc obscuro Germaniae angulo literas ad te mitterem, oratum per illum ingentem Veritatis amorem, quem in te quoque inesse fama tuae magnitudinis praedicat: ut, quid tibi de isthoc videatur negocio, ea synceritate et humanitate, quae in te praedicatur, explices, brevibusque literis significes. O me felicem, si quod MAeSTLINO, idem et TYCHONI videbitur: quibus ego duobus propugnatoribus non dubitabo totius orbis iam excitatas insectationes forti animo sustinere. Sin autem id consecutus fuero, ut quae insunt infirma, inepta, et puerilia, qualia mihi per aetatem multa exciderunt, censura volueris notare: an ego non rursum huiusmodi reprehensionem totius mundi, si esset, assensui praeferam? Exemplaria non dubito, quin antea habeas: quare facile patior, ut difficultas transmittendi per tantum intervallum a mittendo me avocet. Tuorum autem operum ad nos nihil perfertur: quaeque de te sciebam, ex MAeSTLINO didiceram. Dabis igitur veniam, si quibus in locis tui nominis mentionem faciens, iniuriam tibi tuaeque hypothesi faciam. Plura dicturum quaesiturumque reverentia me retinet. Aperi luctanti voci viam, scriptis ad me paucissimis: efficies enim, ut tibi demonstrem, quanto sim discendi, quam laudis cupidior.</p><p>Vale, Vir Nobilissime, meaque tibi studia commendata habe.<br>Idibus Decemb. An. 1597.<br>Nobilitatis et Magnitudinis<br>Tuae Reverent.<br>M. IOHANNES KEPLERUS,<br>Illustrium Styriae Procerum in Gymnasio<br>Graecensi Mathematicus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most eminent lord Tycho Brahe, the Dane, Prince of Mathematicians — into his own hands.</b></p><p>Since, most eminent Sir, incomparable learning and outstanding judgement have established you as the Monarch of all mathematicians not only of this age but of the whole of time, I shall act unjustly if, in the little work on the proportion of the heavens lately brought to light (under the title <i>Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum</i> [Forerunner of Cosmographical Dissertations]), I were to angle for any glory while neglecting your judgement and commendation. This is the cause that has moved me, unknown as I am, to send a letter to you from this obscure corner of Germany — begging you, by that immense love of Truth which fame, [speaking] of your greatness, proclaims to dwell in you also, to set out what your view of this matter may be, with that sincerity and kindness which is proclaimed in you, and to make it known to me in a brief letter. O happy me, if what seems good to Maestlin shall seem the same to Tycho too! For with these two as my champions I shall not hesitate to bear with a stout heart the attacks already roused against me from the whole world. But if instead I shall obtain this — that you should be willing to mark with your censure whatever is weak, inept, and childish in it (and many such things, owing to my [youthful] age, have slipped from me) — shall I not once again prefer a reproof of this kind, to the assent of the whole world, were it [mine]? I do not doubt that you already have copies; and so I readily allow the difficulty of conveyance across so great a distance to call me away from sending one. But none of your own works reaches us: whatever I knew about you, I had learned from Maestlin. You will therefore grant me pardon if, in certain places where I make mention of your name, I do you and your hypothesis an injury. Reverence holds me back from saying and asking more. Open a way for my struggling voice by writing me but a very few lines: for you will bring it about that I may show you how much keener I am to learn than to be praised.</p><p>Farewell, most noble Sir, and hold my endeavours commended to you.<br>On the Ides of December [13 December], the year 1597.<br>Of Your Nobility and Greatness<br>the reverent<br>Master Johannes Kepler,<br>Mathematician of the Illustrious Estates of Styria in the Gymnasium of Graz.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / direction of the letter.</b> Despite the dataset slug (`brahe-t_…`), this is <b>Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe</b>, Kepler's celebrated <i>first</i> letter to Tycho — his approach as a then-unknown 25-year-old district mathematician in Graz to the reigning observational authority. (The dataset's archival logic files the whole correspondence under Brahe.)</li><li><b>Correspondent addressed.</b> <i>Tychoni Brahe, Dano, Mathematicorum Principi</i> — <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), styled \"Prince of Mathematicians.\" <i>Ad manus</i> (\"into his own hands\") is the address-direction on the cover.</li><li><b>Sender's self-styling.</b> <i>M. Iohannes Keplerus, Illustrium Styriae Procerum in Gymnasio Graecensi Mathematicus</i> = <b>Master Johannes Kepler, Mathematician of the Illustrious Estates of Styria at the Graz Gymnasium</b> (the Protestant <i>Stiftsschule</i> / <i>Landschaftsschule</i> in <b>Graz</b>, where Kepler held the post of provincial mathematician 1594–1600). <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic Master, protocol §5.5). The place of writing (Graz) is supplied to the header from this subscription; the dateline itself names only the date.</li><li><b><i>opusculo … sub titulo: Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum</b></i> — the <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i> (Tübingen, 1596/97), whose full title opens <i>Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum, continens mysterium cosmographicum…</i> Kepler's \"little work on the proportion of the heavens\" (<i>de proportione coelorum</i>) is his polyhedral-nesting account of the planetary spacing. The title is kept in Latin and glossed.</li><li><b><i>Maestlino</b></i> = <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (Maestlinus, 1550–1631), Kepler's astronomy teacher at Tübingen and his Copernican mentor, who oversaw the printing of the <i>Mysterium</i>. Named twice; spelling normalised to \"Maestlin\" in the translation.</li><li><b><i>tuae hypothesi</b></i> — <b>\"your hypothesis,\"</b> i.e. Tycho's world-system (the geo-heliocentric arrangement); <i>hypothesis</i> in the period sense of a cosmological model/arrangement, not a tentative conjecture. Kepler — an open Copernican — pre-apologises for places in the <i>Mysterium</i> where his treatment may slight Tycho's model. Rendered \"hypothesis\" and the model-sense noted (cf. protocol §5.1 <i>orbis/sphaera</i>).</li><li><b><i>Mathematicorum … Monarcham … Principi</b></i> — the doubled superlative styling (\"Monarch of all mathematicians of the whole of time\"; \"Prince of Mathematicians\"). Kept at full courtesy-register; this is captatio benevolentiae, not a settled title.</li><li><b><i>totius orbis iam excitatas insectationes</b></i> — \"the attacks already roused against me from the whole world\": the controversy Kepler anticipated (and met) over the <i>Mysterium</i>'s Copernican and a-priori argument. <i>propugnatoribus</i> \"champions / defenders\" — Maestlin and Tycho cast as Kepler's two backers.</li><li><b><i>qualia mihi per aetatem multa exciderunt</b></i> — \"many such things, owing to my age, have slipped from me\": Kepler's own hedge of youth/inexperience. Preserved as a hedge, not sharpened into a claim of error.</li><li><b><i>Exemplaria non dubito, quin antea habeas</b></i> — \"I do not doubt that you already have copies\": Kepler presumes Tycho has obtained the book, and so excuses himself (citing <i>difficultas transmittendi per tantum intervallum</i>, the difficulty of conveyance over so great a distance) from sending one. He was mistaken — Tycho had not yet seen it — which colours Tycho's reply.</li><li><b><i>Aperi luctanti voci viam</b></i> — \"Open a way for my struggling/labouring voice\": <i>luctanti</i> of a voice straining (with diffidence/reverence) to be heard; rendered \"struggling,\" the strain kept.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>meaque tibi studia commendata habe</i> → \"and hold my endeavours commended to you\"; the subscription <i>Nobilitatis et Magnitudinis Tuae Reverent[issimus]</i> → \"Of Your Nobility and Greatness the [most] reverent\" (the abbreviation <i>Reverent.</i> expanded to a superlative subscription-formula; the bracketed <i>-issimus</i> is editorial).</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Idibus Decemb. An. 1597</i> = <b>\"on the Ides of December (13 December), the year 1597,\"</b> rendered with the Roman idiom and glossed to the civil date per protocol §3/§26. <b>Style:</b> dated by Kepler, a Lutheran in Inner-Austrian Habsburg territory; Styria/Graz had adopted the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> calendar (introduced in the Inner-Austrian lands 1583), so this \"13 December\" is very probably <b>N.S.</b>, whereas Tycho on Hven still reckoned <b>O.S.</b> The header is tagged [O.S.] to match the protocol's default Tycho-side convention and the dataset, <b>but the likely N.S. origin of the dateline is flagged here, not silently converted</b> (the O.S. equivalent would be 3 December 1597). This is the named calendar crux.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and at full register. The one substantive open point is the <b>calendar style of the dateline</b> (Kepler writing under Gregorian reckoning in Graz vs. the [O.S.] header tag): flagged above and not silently reconciled. Lexical choices (<i>hypothesis</i> = world-system; <i>Reverent.</i> expanded as a superlative subscription) are noted rather than assumed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer VIII pp. 14-16). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin for Kepler's first letter to Tycho. Checked every name (Maestlino, Tychoni), the title 'Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum', and the date 'Idibus Decemb. An. 1597' (Ides of December = 13th). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found. Published self-grade was B; upgraded to A on full collation since no defects survive scrutiny.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15980401001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980401001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Introduces himself to Tycho, sends the Prodromus dissertationum cosmographicarum, and requests Tycho's judgment on the work, citing Maestlin's prior approval.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971213001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971216001", "d": "1597-12-16", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Haderslev", "t": "Holger Rosenkrantz → Tycho Brahe — Haderslev, 16 December 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 18 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>GENEROSO TYCHONI<br>BRAHEO, MATHEMATICO PRINCIPI, COGNATO ET AFFINI<br>DESIDERATISSIMO.</p><p>GENEROSE mi TYCHO, cognate affinis desideratissime, amicorum intime. Iam<br>inde quo literæ tuæ desideratissimæ pridie Non. Decembris reddebantur, medijs<br>his turbis nuptialibus, laboriosissimis districtus præpedimentis distineor, ne<br>quod valde exoptâram, responsum usque huc festinare licuerit. In præsens vero,<br>ubi liberior aliquanto adspirat aura, rarissimam hanc felicitatem, qua cum<br>amicis ingenuæ mentis tuto communicare conceditur, flagrantissime cupitam,<br>adlubentius eo excipio, quo rariores eius census, multivarios inter, ut nosti<br>ingenia, habeam et æstimem. Utut autem facile sciam nil præter spem, haud multa<br>præter voluntatem, exuli iam pridem tibi, modo libero, accidisse, doleo nihilo<br>etiamdum minus, non tui equidem, sed mei ipsius, sed patriæ immerentis, sed<br>bonorum in patria omnium, quibus integrior mens et animus, caussa, quos paucorum<br>quorundam livor, alia obtentui sumpta caussa, suo desiderio, sua gloria, suo<br>privavit amore et gaudio; alias equidem, ut nihil est certe, quod, natura sic<br>imperante, in rebus caducis apparere solentius<br>autumem dissimilium inter se haud interrupta unquam contentione et pugna, quam<br>non abstrusis modo, sensusque latentibus Naturæ laboribus familiarem, sed et<br>concitatis destinati humanarum rerum dispensatoris Mentis facinoribus pene<br>domesticam indies ostentari suspicimus, ita et nihil est, cur eundem hunc Naturæ<br>morem demirarer, si vel maxime hoc ex circo etiam Musæ iunctim omnes recentibus<br>æmulationibus proscriptæ exularent, tecumque castra moventes, se in universum<br>hinc, ubi nihil prisci et integri moris, subducerent; magis est vel tandem<br>experiendo discere, perversam eiuscemodi, non utique alias insolentem, scena hac<br>tripudiantis invidentiæ, caute suspectare inconstantiam, prudenterque provisis, quæ tempus commonet, subductas sui rationes non stolidis<br>ac evanidis, ast solidis et ævo dignis firmare munimentis, ne alienis quam<br>proprijs innixi mage virtutibus, ad aleatorios quosvis fortunæ iactus * cernimus<br>nutare, quin et concidere penitus eveniat. Tu vero nobis, tu inquam TYCHO generose facem hîc prætulisti, tu<br>tuo nobis præluxisti exemplo, tu veræ virtutis ipsi sibi ex asse undique<br>sufficientis, vestigia posuisti, quibus insistere, quis ad hanc metam adspirans<br>non adniteretur? Ipse ego certe (qui licet illustri huic consessui accersiri non<br>merear, in spem tamen secundam aut tertiam saltem acciri indies adnitor) multa<br>nec levia in me malevolentiæ indicia, utut premantur, erumpere sentisco, ast σὺν<br>δαίμονι ἐσϑλῷ quod spero incassum, nec enim ad hosce ventus ego mea distendi<br>vela, neque enim importuoso isti me commiserim vado. Sed de me coram ipse<br>præsens, ne adfectu et præcipitantia, quorum caussas procul habeo, imprudens<br>nimis in literas intutas, relatrices, rationum mearum secreta conijcere videar,<br>quæ non aliter constant, quam si nulli, nisi alteri mihi reddantur. Ad diem<br>itaque anni adproperantis quintum decimum, favente Numine, aliud vitæ meæ<br>facturus initium, tædiosissimo huic renunciaturus ergastulo, rogationi tuæ<br>amicissimæ vel non rogatus sponte adsentiri iam pridem est animus, nec vero ad<br>ullius unquam æmulationes et odia me in his, quæ recta sunt, comparaturus sum<br>unquam. Et vero quid mage dedeceret, te, quem non affinitate modo, sed et<br>sanguine, sed et necessitudinis vinculis omnibus peculiari sorte, iure primum<br>æstimo, colo et observo, te inquam post tot benevolentissimi animi in immerentem<br>indicia, insalutatum præterire? Age ergo, num circa dictum tempus sat commodus<br>tibi meus videatur accessus, litera per hunc tabellarium<br>adsignifices una aut altera; faxo ego hîc interim, ne, quod in me desideres,<br>præter vires forte, siet quidquam; alia præsenti adloquio reservanda, quo et si<br>quæ nova sunt, quæ referre interest, relata velim, nisi quod hoc unicum in<br>præsens referre intersit, communem nostrum CRAGIUM in Poloniam brevi<br>ablegandum, ad 1. Ianuarij litteris huc esse<br>advocatum, quem, itinere per Holsatiam<br>sumpto, te invisurum nullus dubito, cui et quo me adiungere queam, modo non<br>festinantior abierit, enixe studebo. Ego vero si non studio me continerem, finem<br>vix habiturum esset hoc meum per litteras adloquium, ut et potiora, imo vero,<br>quod ego sentio potissima, Epistolij tui capita commodiori opportunitati lubens<br>reservem. Ludant interea meæ Musæ, qua valent, ad hæc; aperte equidem, ast suo<br>sub velo. Interea te præpotenti rerum nostrarum gubernatori, Trinuno Numini, cum<br>universa tua familia, consilijs et actionibus, commendo. Vale felicissime.</p><p>Te nulli secundus integerrimo colens animo</p><p>OLIGERUS<br>ROSÆCRANTZ.<br>Perscribebam</p><p>Haderslebiæ XVI Decemb.<br>CIↃ CI<br>IIIC.</p><p>Salutari te suo nomine officiosissime voluit IACOBUS ULFELDIUS, quem te<br>sincere etiam maioribus renitentibus amare et suspicere scias.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble Tycho Brahe, prince of mathematicians, kinsman and most longed-for relative by marriage.</b></p><p>My noble Tycho, most longed-for kinsman and relative, most intimate of friends. Ever since your most-longed-for letter was delivered on the day before the Nones of December [4 December], I have been held fast, hemmed in by these wedding tumults and their most laborious encumbrances, so that I have not been able to hasten until now the reply I had so greatly desired. But at present, when a somewhat freer breeze breathes upon me, I take up the more gladly this rarest happiness — so ardently desired, the happiness of being permitted to share safely with friends of a free and honest mind — the more so since I hold and prize its returns as the rarer, amid temperaments as varied as you know them to be. But although I readily know that nothing beyond [your] hope, and not much beyond [your] wish, has befallen you — long since an exile, now a free man — I grieve nonetheless not at all the less, not on your account indeed, but on my own, but on that of an undeserving fatherland, but on that of all good men in the fatherland, men of a sounder mind and spirit; for the spite of certain few, taking up some other cause for a pretext, has robbed [the fatherland] of its desire for [you], of its glory in [you], of its love and joy in [you]. For the rest, indeed — just as there is surely nothing which, nature so ordaining, I would judge to appear more habitually in transient things than the never-once-interrupted strife and contention of unlike things among themselves, a strife we see displayed daily not only as familiar to the hidden, sense-eluding labours of Nature, but as almost domestic in the violent doings of the appointed Mind that dispenses human affairs — so too there is no reason why I should marvel at this same way of Nature, even if to the very utmost [it should come to pass that] the Muses, all together, proscribed by these recent rivalries, should likewise go into exile out of this arena, and, striking camp along with you, should withdraw from here altogether, where nothing of the ancient and uncorrupted way remains. Rather is it better, at last and by experience, to learn to be cautiously wary of so perverse an inconstancy — not, in truth, otherwise unwonted [than] on this stage of capering envy — and, with prudent provision for what the time admonishes, to fortify one's withdrawn resources with bulwarks not foolish and fleeting but solid and worthy of the age; lest, leaning more on others' virtues than on our own, we be seen to totter at any chance throws of fortune's dice <i> — nay, that it even come about that we collapse utterly. But you indeed — you, I say, noble Tycho — have carried the torch before us here; you have lit our way by your own example; you have set down the footprints of true virtue, sufficient in full to itself on every side, which footprints — what man aspiring to this goal would not strive to tread? I myself, to be sure (who, though I may not deserve to be summoned to this illustrious assembly, yet strive daily to be called at least into the second rank, or the third), feel that many and no light tokens of ill-will against me are bursting forth, however they be suppressed; but, </i>with a kindly genius* [σὺν δαίμονι ἐσϑλῷ], I hope it is in vain — for I have not spread my sails to these winds, nor would I have entrusted myself to that harbourless shoal. But of myself [I shall speak] in person, present face to face, lest, through emotion and rashness — the grounds of which I keep far off — I should seem too imprudently to cast the secrets of my counsels into unsafe, tale-bearing letters, secrets which hold good no otherwise than if they be rendered up to none but a second self. And so, on the fifteenth day of the year now hastening on, the Deity favouring, being about to make another beginning of my life, about to renounce this most wearisome house of toil, it has long been my mind to assent — even unasked — of my own accord to your most friendly request; nor shall I ever, in those things that are upright, set myself against anyone's rivalries and hatreds. And truly, what would be more unbecoming than for me to pass you by unsaluted — you whom, by a singular lot, I esteem, honour, and regard as first by right, [bound to me] not by affinity only, but by blood, and by every bond of intimacy — you, I say, after so many tokens of a most kindly disposition shown toward one who did not deserve them? Come then: signify to me by one line or another, through this courier, whether about the said time my coming seems sufficiently convenient to you; I shall see to it here meanwhile that there be nothing, of what you might desire in me, [lacking] beyond my powers perhaps; other matters [are] to be reserved for conversation in person, in which I should also wish reported whatever there is of news worth relating — save that this one thing it is worth relating at present: that our common friend Cragius, soon to be dispatched into Poland, has been summoned hither by letters for the first of January, and that he, taking his route through Holstein, will visit you, I do not doubt at all; to whom, and whither, I shall earnestly endeavour to attach myself, provided he has not departed too hastily. But truly, did I not restrain myself on purpose, this my converse with you by letter would scarcely have an end; so that I gladly reserve for a more convenient opportunity the weightier — nay rather, what I deem the weightiest — heads of your little letter. Meanwhile let my Muses sport, as best they can, at these matters; openly indeed, yet under their own veil. Meanwhile I commend you, with your whole household, your counsels and your actions, to the all-powerful governor of our affairs, the Triune Deity. Fare most happily.</p><p>Honouring you with a most upright mind, second to none,</p><p>Holger Rosenkrantz.<br>Written out<br>at Haderslev, 16 December<br>1597. Jacob Ulfeldt wished most dutifully to send you greeting in his own name — one whom you may know sincerely loves and looks up to you, even with his elders opposing [it].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaecrantz</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> — addressed, and signing, as Tycho's <i>cognatus et affinis</i> (\"kinsman and relative by marriage\"). Tycho's reply of ten days later (Hamburg, 26 December 1597; <i>Opera Omnia</i>) speaks of <i>Cragius noster</i> in the same terms, confirming the pairing. (The signatory is one of the several contemporaneous Holger Rosenkrantzes of the high Danish nobility; the writer here is a younger kinsman about to \"make another beginning\" of his life, not the elder statesman \"the Rich.\")</li><li><b>Address / titulature.</b> <i>MATHEMATICO PRINCIPI</i> — \"prince of mathematicians,\" an honorific (not an office), rendered literally. <i>Generose</i> … <i>cognate affinis desideratissime, amicorum intime</i>: the piled-up vocatives of high epistolary courtesy are kept.</li><li><b>\"exuli iam pridem tibi, modo libero.\"</b> \"you, long since an exile, now a free man.\" Tycho had left Hven and Denmark in 1597; the letter's whole consolatory frame turns on his self-imposed <i>exilium</i> and on the <i>livor</i> (\"spite, envy\") of \"certain few\" at court who, <i>alia obtentui sumpta caussa</i> (\"having taken up some other cause for a pretext\"), drove him out. Rosenkrantz's <i>modo libero</i> (\"now free\") and the praise of Tycho as having \"carried the torch before us\" cast the departure as liberation, not defeat — the author's framing is preserved, not editorialised.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>σὺν δαίμονι ἐσϑλῷ</i> — transcribed as printed (with the medial <i>ϑ</i> form of theta); \"with a kindly genius / good spirit,\" i.e. \"with good fortune / God willing.\" A learned tag (cf. the Homeric/proverbial <i>σὺν δαίμονι</i>); transliterated and glossed, not flattened.</li><li><b>The asterisk (`iactus <i>`).</b> An editorial mark stands in the source after </i>fortunae iactus<i> (\"fortune's dice\"). It is reproduced verbatim in the Source block; its referent (a footnote or </i>crux* sign in the copy/edition) is not recoverable from the text and is <b>not</b> silently deleted. The clause is translated as it stands; the mark is flagged here.</li><li><b>\"Ad diem … anni adproperantis quintum decimum.\"</b> Rendered \"on the fifteenth day of the year now hastening on\" — i.e. about 15 January [1598], the date Rosenkrantz sets for the \"new beginning\" of his life and his proposed visit; <i>non rogatus … adsentiri … animus</i> (\"my mind is to assent even unasked\") answers a request (<i>rogatio</i>) Tycho had made in the letter Rosenkrantz received on 4 December. <b>Crux flagged:</b> the syntax compresses date, intention, and the renunciation of the <i>ergastulum</i> into one clause; the reading \"fifteenth day of the [coming] year\" is the natural one but is not beyond doubt.</li><li><b><i>taediosissimo huic … ergastulo</b></i> — \"this most wearisome house of toil\"; <i>ergastulum</i> (literally a slaves' workhouse/prison) used metaphorically of Rosenkrantz's present situation, exactly as Wolf used it of his own life (cf. brahe-t_15711113001). Rendered consistently \"house of toil.\"</li><li><b><i>Cragius</b></i> = <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602), Danish humanist, historian, and diplomat, <i>soon to be dispatched into Poland</i> (<i>in Poloniam brevi ablegandum</i>) — a reference to his 1598 embassy. <i>itinere per Holsatiam sumpto</i> — \"taking his route through Holstein,\" i.e. past Tycho's then station in the Hamburg/Holstein region. Tycho's 26 December reply (<i>Utinam Cragius noster tecum sit vel praecedat</i>) shows the planned rendez-vous was real.</li><li><b><i>Epistolij tui capita</b></i> — \"the heads [chief points] of your little letter\" (<i>epistolium</i>, diminutive of <i>epistola</i>); Rosenkrantz defers reply to Tycho's weightier points until they meet.</li><li><b><i>Ludant … meae Musae … suo sub velo</b></i> — \"let my Muses sport … under their own veil,\" i.e. he will express more (perhaps in verse) but veiled; the figure is kept.</li><li><b><i>Trinuno Numini</b></i> — \"to the Triune Deity,\" the closing commendation to the Holy Trinity; <i>praepotenti rerum nostrarum gubernatori</i> (\"the all-powerful governor of our affairs\") in apposition.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Haderslebiae</i> = <b>Haderslev</b> (Hadersleben), in Schleswig; <i>Perscribebam</i> (\"I was writing out / I write out,\" epistolary imperfect) kept.</li><li><b>Dateline &amp; calendar.</b> <i>XVI Decemb. CIↃ CI IIIC</i> = \"16 December 1597.\" The numeral is the apostrophus form: <i>CIↃ</i> = 1000, <i>CI</i> (= D) = 500, <i>IIIC</i> = 97 (the reversed-C \"97\" idiom), giving M D 97 = <b>1597</b>; <b>O.S.</b> (Denmark kept the Julian calendar until 1700). This matches the catalogue label (1597-12-16); no date discrepancy. <i>pridie Non. Decembris</i> in the body = \"the day before the Nones of December\" = <b>4 December</b> (Nones of December = the 5th), glossed in the translation per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Register / style note.</b> Rosenkrantz writes in a deliberately mannered, hyper-periodic late-humanist Latin: very long suspended periods, heavy hyperbaton, accumulated comparatives and rare adverbs (<i>solentius, adlubentius, sentisco</i>). The translation keeps the periodic architecture rather than chopping it into modern short sentences, at some cost in fluency — faithful to the author's evident self-display.</li><li><b>Names Jacob Ulfeldt as a covert admirer of Tycho.</b> The postscript reports that the Danish nobleman Jacob Ulfeldt (til Ulfeldtsholm) asked to be remembered to Tycho, and states that Ulfeldt sincerely loves and looks up to him even though his elders/family oppose it ('etiam maioribus renitentibus') — a telling detail for late 1597, when much of the Danish nobility had already turned against Tycho.</li><li><b>Corroboration.</b> A genuine prior friendship is independently attested: Tycho addressed one of his own occasional Latin poems directly to 'Jacob Ulfeldt til Ulfeldtsholm' ('TIL MIN GODE VEN... HR. JACOB ULFELDT TIL ULFELDTSHOLM' — 'To my good friend... Lord Jacob Ulfeldt of Ulfeldtsholm'), per Grinder-Hansen (ed.), <i>Tycho Brahes Verden</i> (Nationalmuseet, 2006). The 'elders resisting' detail is new information not otherwise recorded in that source.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the plain sense, the persons, the dating (1597 O.S.; <i>pridie Non. Decembris</i> = 4 Dec.), and the Cragius/Poland and Tycho-exile references are secure, and cross-confirmed by Tycho's 26 Dec. 1597 reply. The grade is held at [C] for three flagged points that the convoluted Latin does not let one resolve to [A]: (1) the unexplained editorial <b>asterisk</b> after <i>fortunae iactus</i>; (2) the precise force of the enormous central period (<i>alias equidem … concidere penitus eveniat</i>) on the strife of Nature and the exile of the Muses, where the syntax is genuinely tangled; and (3) the compressed <i>Ad diem … quintum decimum</i> clause (read as \"15th of the coming year\"). None is smoothed into false fluency.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII: the editorial asterisk after <i>fortunae iactus</i> is in Dreyer's own text with no attached footnote (an unexplained mark carried from the copy); the tangled central period is printed verbatim and unemended (genuine late-humanist hyper-periodicity — the text is secure, only hard to construe); the <i>Ad diem … quintum decimum</i> clause is sound. [C] stands (text secure; interpretive ceilings).</p><p>Medium-high confidence. The Latin is unambiguous, and the identification of Iacobus Ulfeldius as Jacob Ulfeldt of Ulfeldtsholm is corroborated by an independent source — Tycho's own poem addressed to him, per Grinder-Hansen (ed.), <i>Tycho Brahes Verden</i> (Nationalmuseet, 2006). The single supplied word is '[it]' after 'opposing', clarifying the unstated object of 'renitentibus' (the elders resisting his affection for Tycho, not some other unstated matter).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images TBOO VIII pp.15-17 (the letter runs from the bottom of p.15 through the top of p.17; p.18 begins an unrelated letter to the Emperor). Collated the full Latin text sentence by sentence against published_english and against the Dreyer image, including the Greek tag (συν δαιμονι εσθλω), the date formula (pridie Non. Decembris = 4 December; XVI Decemb. CIↃ CI IIIC = 16 December 1597), the Cragius/Poland/1 January detail, and the closing Ulfeldt postscript. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; no textual departure from Dreyer. (Two spots where the antiqua long-s glyph could be misread as 'f' at first glance were checked against the printed source_text and resolved by grammar/context, not counted as defects.) published_grade of C on this packet is not supported by collation; this letter reads as a faithful, careful translation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15971116001", "next": "brahe-t_15971226001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971116001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15971226001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for delayed reply due to wedding festivities and expresses grief not for Tycho personally but for the fatherland that has wronged him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971216001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971222001", "d": "1597-12-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg", "ck": "Joachim Friedrich, kurfyrste af Brandenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Bramstedt", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joachim Friedrich (Administrator of Magdeburg) — Bramstedt, 22 December 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German, chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>COPIA DESS BRIEFS, SO AN DEM HERRN ADMINISTRATORN ICH INN DER EIL ZU BRAMBSTADT GEGEBEN HABE.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER hochgeborner furst, gnedigster herr. Nachdem jch auß hochwichtigen vnd nodtwendigen vrsachen, so in die feder nicht alle woll zubringen, mich auß meinem lieben vaterlandt Dennemarck begeben musßen, vnndt hernacher bin innenworden, das die kön. maytt: zu Dennemarcken etc., mein gnedigster herr vndt könig, solche meine außreise, vielleicht durch etzliche meiner mißgönner angebenn, also ausgenommen, alß wen das jhr kön: maytt: zu verdruß geschehen sey. Welches ich mit aller warheidt sagen kan, daß keines weges meine meinung gewesen, sondern hette gern alda geblieben, vnndt meinem gnedigstem herrn vndt könig vndt lieben vaterlandt, hernacher wie zuuor, alles zu ehren vndt rumblicher nachsage gethan, was in meinem geringen vermögen sein köntte, wan solches mit guther ruhe vnd gelegenheidt, vndt ohne meinen schaden vnnd verlierung der zeit, hette geschehen mugen.</p><p>Vnd kan ein jeder leichtlich ermessen, daß ich in diesen meinem hohen alter, nun jch über die funfftzig jahr bin, mit fraw vndt seks kinder vnd anderem meinem haußgesinde beladen, mich nicht leichtferttiger weiß, vndt ohne hochdringenden vrsachen hab endtfrembdett. Dieweil es aber also vielleicht auß Gottes vorhengnuß hatt geschehen mußen, will ich mich auch mit diesem meinem zustandt gerne genugen lasßen, vnd anderßwo versuchen, was gluch der liebe Gott bescheren will, dan jch darumb keinen verlangen trage, nunmehr vor mein person in Dennemarck zu sein vndt gerestituirt zu werden, vnd wan das schon geschehen solte, so ist es mir doch sehr vngelegen, auf der jnsull Huen lenger zuwohnen vnd stets zu bleiben, wouon jch ahn einem andern ortt meine vrsachen vorzeichnet habe. Wolte deßwegen vnterthenigst E:F:G: gebetten haben, dieselben sich vnschwer erzeigen wollen, ahn jhr kön: maytt:, seinen vielgeliebten herrn sohn, ein schreiben von meinetwegen zuthun, daß jhr kön. maytt: mir hirin nichts zu vngnaden aufnehmen wolle, sondern mein gnedigster herr vndt könig sein vndt bleibenn; jch wil doch anderßwo, wo dan Gott mir meine gelegenheidt vorsehen hat, jhr königl. maytt: getrew erfunden werden, auch deroselbigen vnd meinem vielgeliebtenn vaterlandt zu ehren thun alles was mir immer muglich, welches vielleicht anderswo eben sowoll, wo nicht mehr, vndt viel beqvemer vnd ruhelicher als in Dennemarck geschehen kan. Wofern aber jhr kön. maytt: mit deroselben hochweisen reichs räthen noch des sins wurden, das dieselben die astronomische studia in Dennemarck vndt auf der jnsull Huen zu Vraniburgk, auß gnedigstem willen vnd begehren des hochlöblicher gedechtnuß königs Friderich zu dem brauch von mir gebauet vnd aufgerichtett, durch ersprießliche mittel, deroselben vnd dem reich zu ehren vnd hochgemeltem löblichen könig Friderich zur gedechtnuß, ferner vnterhalten wollen, wil ich gerne meinen außersten fleiß ahnwenden, das solches, wo schon nicht durch mich selbst, doch durch einen von den meinen (so ich datzu vnterwiesen hab, vnd ferner vnterweisen wil) ins werck gestellet werde, vnd das vielleicht, nicht mit geringerm fleiß, als wen ich selbst da were; will auch nechst denvier vberauss grossen vnd etzlichen anderen astronomischen jnstrumenten, die ich da gelasßen hab, noch mehr, nachdem es von nötten, da hinstellen lasßen, vnd alles ordiniren vnd vorschreiben, wie es mit den observationibus vnd dero gebrauch gehalten soll werden, auff das, was einmahl da so wohl angefangen vnd gecontinuirt ist, köntte stets bey macht erhaltten werden, jhr königl: mayttz:, meinem gnedigsten herrn vnd könig, vndt dem reiche zu ehren, auf das nicht anderswo solte gesaget werden, daß solche lobliche sachen gar zerstöret vnd zu nicht gemacht sein. Es wolle aber daneben jhr kön: maytt: mit notturfftichlicher vnterhaltung, dasselbe mit canonicien vndt geistlichen lehen, nach einhalt der algemeinen reichs räthe brieff in interregno gnedigst vorsehen, vnd mit einer bequemen fundation perpetuiren. Von diesem vnd was hier vndter zuuorstehen gelanget ahn E:F:G: mein vnterthenige bitte, dieselben j: kön: maytt: zu Dennemarck, meinem gnedigsten herrn vnd könig, dieses erinnern vnd vortragen lasßen wollen, wofern aber es j: kön: may: nicht gefiele, die astronomische sachen auf der insul weiter zu erhalten, daß alsdan mit sr. may: gnedigsten vnndt guten willen, jch die alda noch vberige jnstrumenta, vndt was mir sonsten zugehörig, möchte lasßen wecknehmen vnd anderswo zu nutz bringen. Daneben auch, wofern es j: maytt: nicht zu widern were, das jch einige compensation wieder vor alle meine grosse muhe vnd bekostung, die ich alda über die 20 jar ertragen hab, vnd vielmehr dazu außgelegt, als von hochlöblichen könig Friderich vnd denn reichs rath mir erstreckt vnd gegonnet worden, erlangen möchte. Welches in jhr kön: maytt: gnedigsten willen vndt wolgefallen jch vnterthenigst heimbstelle. Wnndt thue E:F:G: ich mich in vntherthenigkeit vorsehen, dieselbigen hierahn kein misßgefallen tragen werden, das E:F:G: ich hiemit vntertheniglich vnndt guther meinung ersuche, E:F:G: hinwiederumb mit aller vnterthenigkeit nach meinem geringen vormugen zudienen, wil ich stets williger dan willig gefunden werden. Thue E:F:G: sambt dero vielgeliebten gemahlin, jungen herrn vndt freulein in den gnaden schütz des Almechtigen getreulich zu gluchlicher vnd langwerender regierung entpfehlen.</p><p>Datum Bramstadt den 22. decembris anno 1597.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of the letter that I gave in haste to the Lord Administrator at Bramstedt.</b></p><p>Most Serene, high-born Prince, most gracious Lord. Whereas for weighty and pressing reasons — which cannot all well be set down with the pen — I have had to remove myself from my dear fatherland Denmark, and have afterwards become aware that the Royal Majesty of Denmark, etc., my most gracious lord and king, has taken this departure of mine — informed of it, perhaps, by certain of those who begrudge me — in such a way as if it had been done to His Royal Majesty's displeasure. Which I can say with all truth was in no way my intention; rather I would gladly have remained there, and would have done for my most gracious lord and king and my dear fatherland, afterwards as before, everything to [their] honour and praiseworthy renown that might lie within my slender means, had this been able to come about with good peace and opportunity, and without harm to myself and loss of time.</p><p>And anyone can readily judge that I, in this my advanced age — now that I am past fifty years, burdened with a wife and six children and the rest of my household — have not estranged myself in any frivolous manner, nor without most urgent reasons. But since it has thus had to come about, perhaps by God's providence, I will gladly let myself be content with this my condition, and try elsewhere what fortune the dear God will grant; for I bear no desire on that account to be in Denmark any longer for my own person and to be restored [to my place]; and even should that come to pass, it is nonetheless very inconvenient for me to dwell any longer on the island of Hven and to remain there always — concerning which I have recorded my reasons in another place. I would therefore most humbly have entreated Your Princely Grace that you might be pleased to show yourself not unwilling to write a letter on my behalf to His Royal Majesty, your most beloved lord son, [asking] that His Royal Majesty would not take anything herein to my disgrace, but be and remain my most gracious lord and king; for I will nonetheless, elsewhere — wherever God has provided my opportunity — be found faithful to His Royal Majesty, and will do for the honour of the same and of my most beloved fatherland everything that may ever be possible to me, which perhaps can be done elsewhere just as well, if not more, and far more conveniently and peaceably than in Denmark. But if His Royal Majesty, with his most wise Councillors of the Realm, should still be of the mind that they wish to maintain further — by fruitful means, to the honour of the same and of the realm, and to the memory of the aforesaid praiseworthy King Frederik — the astronomical studies in Denmark and on the island of Hven at Uraniborg, built and set up by me for that use out of the most gracious will and desire of King Frederik of laudable memory: then I will gladly apply my utmost diligence that this be put into effect — if not by myself, then by one of my own people (whom I have instructed for it, and will instruct further) — and that, perhaps, with no less diligence than if I myself were there. I will also, besides the four exceedingly large and certain other astronomical instruments which I have left there, have yet more set up there as need requires, and will arrange and prescribe everything as to how the matter of the observations and their use is to be conducted, so that what has once been so well begun and continued there might always be kept in force, to the honour of His Royal Majesty, my most gracious lord and king, and of the realm — so that it not be said elsewhere that such praiseworthy things have been utterly destroyed and brought to nothing. But may His Royal Majesty besides this, with sufficient maintenance, most graciously provide for it with canonries and ecclesiastical benefices, according to the tenor of the letter of the general Councillors of the Realm in the interregnum, and perpetuate it with a suitable endowment. Concerning this, and what is to be understood thereunder, my humble request goes to Your Princely Grace that you would be pleased to have His Royal Majesty of Denmark, my most gracious lord and king, reminded of this and have it laid before him; but if it should not please His Royal Majesty to maintain the astronomical concerns on the island any longer, that then, with His Majesty's most gracious and good will, I might have the instruments still remaining there, and whatever else belongs to me, taken away and brought to use elsewhere. And besides, that — if it were not repugnant to His Majesty — I might obtain some compensation in return for all my great labour and expense, which I have borne there for over twenty years, and have laid out far more thereto than was extended and granted to me by the laudable King Frederik and the Council of the Realm. Which I most humbly leave to His Royal Majesty's most gracious will and pleasure. And I assure myself in humility of Your Princely Grace that you will bear no displeasure herein, [seeing] that I herewith humbly and in good meaning entreat Your Princely Grace; [and] to serve Your Princely Grace in return with all humility according to my slender ability, I will ever be found more ready than willing. I commit Your Princely Grace, together with your most beloved consort, young lords and ladies, faithfully to the gracious protection of the Almighty, unto a happy and long-enduring reign.</p><p>Given at Bramstedt, the 22nd of December, in the year 1597.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing in December 1597 from Bramstedt (Bad Bramstedt, in Holstein), some months into his self-imposed departure from Denmark. He had left Hven in spring 1597 and Copenhagen that summer, moving via Rostock to Heinrich Rantzau's estate at Wandsbek; the meeting with the margrave at Bramstedt was arranged by Rantzau. The \"another place\" where he says he has recorded his reasons (<i>ahn einem andern ortt … vrsachen vorzeichnet</i>) is his contemporaneous elegy <i>Elegia ad Daniam</i> and his other appeals.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Der Herr Administrator</i> / <i>Durchleuchtiger hochgeborner Fürst</i> = <b>Joachim Friedrich</b> (1546–1608), Margrave of Brandenburg, then <b>Administrator of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg</b> (the Protestant prince-administrator's title, hence \"Administrator\"). He had been in Denmark in late 1597 for the marriage of his daughter; on the return journey he met Tycho at Bramstedt on 22 December 1597. In January 1598, weeks after this letter, he succeeded his father as <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b>. The header's <i>Herr Administrator</i> and the body's <i>Durchleuchtiger Fürst</i> are the same person.</li><li><b>\"your most beloved lord son\" (<i>seinen vielgeliebten herrn sohn</i>).</b> The \"Royal Majesty of Denmark\" is <b>Christian IV</b> (r. 1588–1648); Tycho addresses Joachim Friedrich as a kinsman able to intercede with the young king. The relationship is courtly/diplomatic, not literally paternal: Christian IV's queen would later be Joachim Friedrich's granddaughter, but in 1597 the deference reflects the older prince's standing. The phrase is rendered literally and the relationship clarified here rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>\"past fifty years … wife and six children\" (<i>über die funfftzig jahr … fraw vndt seks kinder</i>).</b> Tycho was born December 1546, so genuinely just past fifty. <i>Fraw</i> = his common-law wife Kirsten Jørgensdatter. The number of children (<i>seks</i>) is as written; six are named in the household at this date.</li><li><b><i>König Friderich</b></i> — <b>Frederik II of Denmark</b> (r. 1559–88), who in 1576 granted Tycho the island of Hven and underwrote Uraniborg; repeatedly invoked here (\"of laudable / praiseworthy memory\") as the founder whose legacy the establishment embodies.</li><li><b>Instruments.</b> <i>die vier vberauss grossen … astronomischen jnstrumenten</i> = \"the four exceedingly large astronomical instruments\" left behind at Uraniborg/Stjerneborg (per glossary §5.2, <i>organa</i> → instruments; here <i>Instrumenten / Instrumenta</i>). <i>observationibus</i> (Latin ablative, code-switched) = \"observations\" (§5.1). <i>gecontinuirt, gerestituirt, perpetuiren, fundation, compensation, canonicien, in interregno, ordiniren, vorschreiben</i> are the letter's Latinate chancery vocabulary, kept in register.</li><li><b><i>canonicien vndt geistlichen lehen … in interregno</b></i> — \"canonries and ecclesiastical benefices … in the interregnum\": Tycho proposes that the establishment be endowed from church prebends, citing a letter of the Councillors of the Realm issued during the interregnum (the regency between Frederik II's death in 1588 and Christian IV's majority in 1596). Rendered literally; <i>Lehen</i> here = ecclesiastical livings/prebends.</li><li><b>Negation / legal force.</b> The pivotal clauses each turn on a single negation, reproduced one-for-one: (1) <i>keines weges meine meinung gewesen</i> — \"in no way my intention\" (the departure was <b>not</b> meant as an affront); (2) <i>daß jhr kön. maytt: mir hirin <b>nichts</b> zu vngnaden aufnehmen wolle</i> — the king should take <b>nothing</b> herein to Tycho's disgrace; (3) the two conditional <i>wofern aber … nicht gefiele / nicht zu widern were</i> clauses governing the fallback requests (removal of the instruments; compensation) — translated as conditionals (\"but if it should not please …\"; \"if it were not repugnant …\") without strengthening or weakening their hypothetical mood.</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> <i>vielleicht durch etzliche meiner mißgönner</i> (\"perhaps by certain of those who begrudge me\"); <i>vielleicht auß Gottes vorhengnuß</i> (\"perhaps by God's providence\"); <i>welches vielleicht anderswo eben sowoll, wo nicht mehr</i> (\"which perhaps … just as well, if not more\") — each <i>vielleicht</i> / conditional kept as a hedge, none sharpened to assertion.</li><li><b>Orthography (Early New High German chancery, c. 1597).</b> <i>jch / vnd / vnndt = ich / und</i>; <i>vrsachen = Ursachen</i>; <i>außreise = Ausreise</i>; <i>gluch / gluchlicher = Glück / glücklicher</i>; <i>funfftzig = fünfzig</i>; <i>seks = sechs</i>; <i>müsßen, lasßen, außgelegt</i> with <i>ß</i> for <i>ss</i>; <i>könig. maytt: / kön: maytt: = königliche Majestät</i>; <i>E:F:G: = Euer Fürstliche Gnaden</i> (\"Your Princely Grace\"); <i>j: kön: may: = Ihre königliche Majestät</i>; <i>sr. may: = Seiner Majestät</i>. <i>Brambstadt / Bramstadt</i> = <b>Bramstedt</b> (Bad Bramstedt, Holstein). <i>Vraniburgk = Uraniborg</i>; <i>Huen = Hven</i>.</li><li><b>Honorifics (§5.5).</b> <i>Vnsern gnedigen Gruß</i> is absent (the letter opens directly with the princely address); the closing commendation <i>in den gnaden schütz des Almechtigen … entpfehlen</i> (\"commit … to the gracious protection of the Almighty\") is the standard chancery valediction, translated in full rather than dropped.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Bramstadt den 22. decembris anno 1597</i> = \"Given at Bramstedt, 22 December 1597.\" <b>Old Style</b>: Tycho and the Holstein/Danish sphere kept the Julian calendar in 1597 (Denmark until 1700). The dataset's catalogue label (22 December 1597, Bramstedt) agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. The header opening (<i>… ICH INN DER EIL ZU BRAMBSTADT GEGEBEN HABE</i>, \"which I gave in haste at Bramstedt\") is an archival rubric on the file copy (Langebek's Diplomatarium), not part of the letter proper; preserved per protocol §2.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the three load-bearing negations/conditionals are reproduced one-for-one. The one soft point flagged rather than smoothed: <i>seinen vielgeliebten herrn sohn</i> (\"your most beloved lord son\") — Tycho's courtly framing of Christian IV's relationship to the addressee, which is honorific/diplomatic and is translated literally with the relationship clarified in the apparatus rather than recast.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the only soft point is a literal honorific ('lord son'), fully explained; no textual crux, dateline clean.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full German text (TBOO XIV pp.126-127, letter no.137, cross-checked against Dreyer's own Latin synopsis on p.125 confirming the recipient as Joachim Friedrich, Margrave/Administrator of Magdeburg) word-for-word against Dreyer's page images. Verified every number (over fifty years old, six children, the four large instruments, over twenty years' expense) and place-name (Bramstedt, Hven, Uraniborg, Denmark) and confirmed the English is a complete, accurate, unabridged rendering with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains that his departure from Denmark was prompted by pressing reasons and not intended as a slight to the King, who may have been misled by ill-wishers.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971222001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15971226001", "d": "1597-12-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Hamburg, 26 December 1597 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET ERUDITIONE NON VULGARI PRAeEMINENTISSIMO VIRO D. OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO DOMINO HAeREDITARIO IN ROSENHOLM, COGNATO, AFFINI ET AMICO MEO INTEGERRIMO.<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISSIME OLIGERE, cognate et affinis charissime, redditae mihi quidem sunt literae tuae postremae, eruditae illae et amoris ac constantiae erga me plenissimae. Sed aberam tunc Wandesburgo in oppidulo quodam sex miliaribus inde distante, quod Brambsted vocatur, ut cum Domino Produce isthic mihi obvio de quibusdam loquerer. Reperi autem ibidem Illustm Dominum Administratorem et ipsius Illmam coniugem, qui me singulari favore clementer complexi sunt. Domum autem reversus pridie Natalitiorum hasce tuas accepi, quas una e filiabus meis reddidit, nuntij, qui eas apportarat, nulla facta mentione, cum ea, ubi is esset, ignoraret. At cum postmodum statim ad currum conscenderem huc Hamburgum cum meis iturus, ut festos dies hîc transigeremus, mihi indicabatur, nuntium tuum adhuc adesse; non quidem retrospicere licuit, sed data scheda, quae illum suo officio perfunctum testaretur, redire eum permisi, cogitans hîc cum Nobilibus Danis, qui D. Administratorem domum deduxerunt, tuis respondere velle, quod etiam sufficienter praestarem, nisi illi hodie praeter opinionem, incidente quadam ingrata necessitate, abitum festinassent, cum alias me unâ cum BALTHASARE ALFELDIO Wandesburgi invisere constituissent. Cogor igitur premere ea, quae latius ad te scribere animum induxeram, illa in adventum tuum ad me Wandesburgum, quem tam amice polliceris, procrastinaturus. Ego itaque te desideratissime OLIGERE isthic, volente Numine, praestolabor, et avidissime cum alacritate excipiam. Utinam CRAGIUS noster tecum sit vel praecedat; tunc inter nos coram sermones miscebimus de ijs, quae nunc scribere importunitas vetat, quaeque per se calamus non satis tuta sustinet. Fac igitur mature stes promissis, de quo etiam nihil addubito, utque queas nobis salvus adesse.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Hamburgo, postridie Natalitiorum Anno 97.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 18; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Resaluta meo nomine quam officiosissime D. IACOBUM ULFELDIUM, de cuius fide et constanti in me amore satis certus sum; neque enim levitatibus et nugis est obnoxius. Scripsissem illi, nisi temporis penuria praepedirer. Saluta etiam BROCKENHUSIUM illum, qui se meum vocat discipulum; eius literis respondere iam non suppetit otium; habeat itaque me excusatum.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, pre-eminent in no common learning, Master Holger Rosenkrantz, hereditary lord of Rosenholm, my kinsman, kinsman-by-marriage, and most upright friend — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble Holger, dearest kinsman and kinsman-by-marriage, your latest letter has indeed been delivered to me — that learned letter, brimful of your love and constancy towards me. But I was at that time away from Wandsbek, in a certain little town some six miles distant from there, called Bramstedt, in order to confer about certain matters with the Lord Provost [Produx], who met me there. And I found in that same place the Most Illustrious Lord Administrator and his Most Illustrious consort, who graciously embraced me with singular favour. But having returned home on the eve of Christmas [pridie Natalitiorum], I received this letter of yours, which one of my daughters delivered, no mention being made of the messenger who had brought it — since she did not know where he was. Yet when soon afterwards I was at once mounting the carriage to go here to Hamburg with my household, that we might pass the festal days here, it was reported to me that your messenger was still at hand; I could not, to be sure, look back [to deal with it then], but, giving him a note that should attest he had discharged his duty, I let him return — intending here to wish to answer your letter together with the Danish noblemen who had escorted the Lord Administrator home, which I should indeed do sufficiently, had they not today, beyond expectation, an unwelcome necessity having intervened, hastened their departure — whereas otherwise they had resolved to visit me, along with Balthasar Alefeldt, at Wandsbek. I am therefore compelled to suppress those things which I had set my mind to write to you at greater length, putting them off until your coming to me at Wandsbek, which you so amiably promise. And so, most longed-for Holger, I shall await you there, God willing, and shall receive you most eagerly and with alacrity. Would that our Cragius were with you, or came ahead; then between ourselves, face to face, we shall exchange conversations about those matters which importunity now forbids to write, and which of itself the pen cannot bear safely enough. See to it, then, that you stand by your promises in good time — of which I have no doubt at all — and that you may be able, safe and sound, to be present with us.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Hamburg, the day after Christmas [postridie Natalitiorum], the year [15]97.<br>Yours, TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Greet again on my behalf, most dutifully, Master Jacob Ulfeldt, of whose faith and constant love towards me I am sufficiently sure; for he is not given to levities and trifles. I should have written to him, were I not hindered by want of time. Greet too that Brockenhus who calls himself my disciple; there is now no leisure to spare to answer his letter; let him therefore hold me excused.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here in his post-Hven exile: having left Denmark in 1597, he was residing at the castle of <b>Wandsbek</b> (<i>Wandesburgum</i>), near Hamburg, as guest of Heinrich Rantzau. The letter is signed <i>manu propria</i> (\"with my own hand\").</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantzius, dominus haereditarius in Rosenholm</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), the young Danish nobleman and theologian of <b>Rosenholm</b> (Jutland). Tycho addresses him as <i>cognatus et affinis</i> — kinsman and kinsman-by-marriage — and a sequel letter of 22 January 1598 (also from Wandsbek) continues the same exchange about Rosenkrantz's promised visit.</li><li><b><i>cognato, affini</b></i> — <i>cognatus</i> = a blood-relative/kinsman; <i>affinis</i> = a relative by marriage. Both retained distinctly rather than collapsed into \"relative,\" since the pairing is a deliberate courtesy formula.</li><li><b><i>Domino Produce</b></i> — <i>Produx</i> (acc./abl. <i>Producem/Produce</i>) is an unusual title; rendered \"the Lord Provost [Produx]\" with the Latin flagged. It denotes a local official (the <i>Propst</i>/provost of Bramstedt or a comparable administrative head) whom Tycho met at Bramstedt; the exact office is not specified in the text. <b>Flagged</b>, not silently resolved.</li><li><b><i>Illustm Dominum Administratorem … ipsius Illmam coniugem</b></i> — <i>Illustm</i> and <i>Illmam</i> are abbreviations for <i>Illustrissimum</i> / <i>Illustrissimam</i> (expanded in the translation as \"Most Illustrious\"). The <b>Administrator</b> is the Lutheran Administrator of the Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen, <b>Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp</b> (Administrator of Bremen 1585–96 and of Lübeck, then Duke of Holstein-Gottorp from 1590), here with his consort — the regional prince in whose orbit Tycho was then moving. (Identification from the regional and chronological context; the text gives the office, not the name.)</li><li><b><i>Brambsted</b></i> = <b>Bad Bramstedt</b> in Holstein, north of Hamburg; <i>Wandesburgo</i> = <b>Wandsbek</b>, now a district of Hamburg. <i>sex miliaribus</i> = \"six miles\" (German miles, hence a substantial distance).</li><li><b><i>Balthasar Alfeldio</b></i> = <b>Balthasar (von) Alefeldt</b> (Ahlefeldt), of the Holstein noble family; named as one of the company who had meant to visit Tycho at Wandsbek.</li><li><b><i>Cragius noster</b></i> = <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602), the Danish humanist, historian, and diplomat; \"our Cragius\" marks him as a mutual friend. The 22 January 1598 sequel reports that Cragius would pass straight on to Lübeck rather than come to Wandsbek as hoped.</li><li><b><i>quae … calamus non satis tuta sustinet</b></i> — \"matters which … the pen cannot bear safely enough\": Tycho's discretion about confidential matters (his standing with the Danish crown and his exile) is preserved as written, not paraphrased into a definite topic. The reticence is itself the point.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem (dicit)</i>, \"(sends) greeting\" (protocol §5.5); <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell\"; <i>manu propria</i> = \"with my own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Dabantur Hamburgo, postridie Natalitiorum Anno 97</i> = \"Given at Hamburg, <b>the day after Christmas</b>, the year [15]97\" (O.S.; Denmark and the Lutheran north kept the Julian calendar). <i>postridie Natalitiorum</i> = the day after the Nativity = <b>26 December</b>, which agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1597. 26. december</i>). <b>No in-letter date discrepancy:</b> the body's <i>pridie Natalitiorum</i> (\"the eve of Christmas,\" 24 December) is the date Tycho <i>received</i> Rosenkrantz's letter at Wandsbek, a distinct earlier event from the <i>postridie Natalitiorum</i> (26 December) dateline of writing at Hamburg; the two are consistent with the narrated sequence (return home 24 Dec → set out for Hamburg → write 26 Dec). The <i>Anno 97</i> is given as written, not expanded to 1597 except in brackets.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, <b>Langebeks Diplomatarium</b>, a later copy (<i>Afskrift</i>), per the source's witness field; the autograph is not the surviving witness.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (greetings to Jacob Ulfeldt + Brockenhus), restored from Dreyer VIII, p. 18 (dropped after \"manu propria\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the date is unproblematic. The one genuinely uncertain lexical point is the office denoted by <i>Dominus Produx</i> (\"Lord Provost [Produx]\"), flagged above rather than smoothed; the identification of the unnamed <i>Administrator</i> as Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp rests on regional-chronological context, not on the text naming him.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.17-19 as page images. Letter (\"NOBILISSIMO ET ERUDITIONE NON VULGARI...\") runs from bottom of p.17 through p.18, including the postscript greeting Iacobus Ulfeldius and Brockenhusius, which our source_text already marks as restored from Dreyer p.18 (dropped by the working transcription) -- verified the restored text matches Dreyer verbatim. All proper names (Wandesburgo/Brambsted, Balthasar Alfeldio, Cragius), the two paired date-formulae (pridie/postridie Natalitiorum) and the year (Anno 97) match. English translation is faithful throughout, including the postscript. Published grade was B (uncollated default); full Dreyer collation with zero defects earns A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15971216001", "next": "brahe-t_15980122001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports meeting the Lord Administrator and his consort at Bramstedt and departing with his household for Hamburg for Christmas.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15971226001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980000001", "d": "1598-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Isaksen Pontanus", "ck": "Johannes Isaksen Pontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Johannes Isaksen Pontanus → Tycho Brahe — [place not stated], 1598 [O.S.; date not in source — catalogued \"1598\"]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VII–VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 178 (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOHANNES ISACIUS PONTANUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>.....SCALIGER observationes utrasque (quae munificentiae tuae sunt) vernalem aio et autumnalem, suis inseruit, et videbis propediem. De secessu vero in Germaniam itidem intellexerat, et dolere se fatetur, si eiusmodi rerum conturbaretur commoditas, quarum iam fructus redundaret ad Europam universam. Placet quidem ei Germania; sed nescit, an mores undiquaque gentis ad illam facilitatem humanitatemque apti, quae debebatur, apti tibi. Optaret, si Dania omnino deserendi staret animus, Bataviam potius inviseres, amicam studijs, benignam....; et denique te tuasque virtutes et honorabit, et fere dixerat, adorabit. Esset certe in omnem eventum haec ora propinquior Daniae, et ille hîc Ordinum sensus, ut omnes gratias non modo retinendas, verum etiam acquirendas sciant.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johannes Isaksen Pontanus to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>..... Scaliger has inserted both observations (which are [a token] of your generosity) — the vernal, I say, and the autumnal — among his own, and you will see [them] very soon. About your withdrawal into Germany he had likewise understood, and he confesses that he grieves [to think] that an opportunity for affairs of this kind should be disturbed, the fruit of which would now overflow to the whole of Europe. Germany pleases him, to be sure; but he does not know whether the manners of a nation [that are] everywhere suited to that easy graciousness and humanity which was owed [to you] are suited to you. He would wish — if your mind were set on abandoning Denmark altogether — that you would rather visit Batavia [Holland], friendly to studies, kindly....; and, in fine, he both will honour you and your virtues and, he had almost said, will adore [them]. This shore would certainly, in any event, be nearer to Denmark; and such here is the disposition of the States, that they know all favours are not only to be retained but even to be gained [for you].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <i>Iohannes Isacius Pontanus</i> = <b>Johannes Isaksen Pontanus</b> (1571–1639), Danish-Dutch humanist, physician and historian (later royal historiographer of Denmark and professor at Harderwijk); in the later 1590s he was in the Netherlands and moved in the Leiden learned circle, which placed him to relay Scaliger's words to Tycho. The letter survives only as a later <b>copy</b> (<i>Afskrift</i>) in the Langebek collection (Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebek 178), which accounts for the truncated openings (the leading and internal ellipses are in the transmitted text).</li><li><b>Recipient / occasion.</b> Addressed to <b>Tycho Brahe</b> in 1598, the year he left Denmark for good; the letter speaks of his contemplated <i>secessus</i> (\"withdrawal\") and of \"abandoning Denmark altogether,\" and relays counsel on where to settle — squarely the 1597–98 self-exile context.</li><li><b><i>Scaliger</b></i> — <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), the foremost philologist of the age, then professor at the University of Leiden (from 1593). Pontanus reports Scaliger's reaction at second hand; the epistemic frame is Pontanus's (\"he confesses,\" \"he would wish,\" \"he had understood\"), and is preserved as reported speech rather than asserted as Tycho-fact.</li><li><b><i>observationes utrasque … vernalem … et autumnalem</b></i> — \"both observations … the vernal and the autumnal\": evidently a pair of Tycho's observations (most naturally of an <b>equinox</b> — the vernal and the autumnal — though the noun <i>aequinoctium</i> is not in the text and is therefore <b>not</b> supplied in the translation). Scaliger, whose chronological work <i>De emendatione temporum</i> (1583; 2nd ed. 1598) turned on exactly such equinox data, has \"inserted [them] among his own.\" Rendered \"observations\" per glossary §5.1; the unstated object (equinoxes vs. other phenomena) is flagged, not decided.</li><li><b><i>quae munificentiae tuae sunt</b></i> — literally \"which are of your munificence,\" i.e. which are [a gift / token] of your generosity (Tycho had supplied the observations); rendered with a bracketed <i>[a token]</i> since the predicate noun is elided in the Latin.</li><li><b><i>aio</b></i> — \"I say\" (Pontanus's own parenthetical emphatic, not Scaliger's); kept as the writer's interjection.</li><li><b><i>secessu … in Germaniam</b></i> — \"withdrawal into Germany\": Tycho's mooted plan to settle in the Empire. <i>Secessus</i> here = a retiring/withdrawal (to live elsewhere), not a literal secession.</li><li><b><i>Bataviam … amicam studijs, benignam</b></i> — \"Batavia, friendly to studies, kindly\": <b>Batavia</b> = Holland / the (northern) Netherlands, the humanist name for the Dutch provinces; Scaliger (himself settled at Leiden) urges Tycho toward the Dutch Republic instead of Germany. Rendered \"Batavia [Holland]\" on first occurrence.</li><li><b><i>Ordinum sensus</b></i> — \"the disposition of the <b>States</b>\": <i>Ordines</i> = the <b>States</b> (the States-General of the United Provinces, or the States of Holland), the governing assembly; <i>sensus</i> their inclination/mind. The sentence reports the States' favourable attitude toward attracting Tycho.</li><li><b>Textual cruxes.</b> (1) <b><i>an mores undiquaque gentis … apti tibi</b></i> — the clause is grammatically strained in the transmitted copy: <i>apti</i> (\"suited\") stands <b>twice</b> (once before the relative clause, once after <i>debebatur</i>), one of which is a copyist's dittography in this <i>Afskrift</i>. The sense is \"[he does not know] whether the manners of the nation everywhere are suited to you [as fitted to] that graciousness and humanity which was owed [to you].\" The doubled <i>apti</i> is preserved verbatim in the Source block and the redundancy flagged here rather than silently emended. (2) The internal \".....\" after <i>benignam</i> marks a lacuna/omission in the transmitted text, not an editorial cut here.</li><li><b>Date / calendar.</b> The source carries <b>no dateline</b>; the dataset catalogues it as <b>\"1598. empty\"</b> (i.e. year only, no day/month). The header therefore gives the year as catalogued and flags that the source text substantiates no finer date. Denmark was on the <b>Julian calendar (O.S.)</b> in 1598; absent a dateline, no style is asserted by the source itself, and no conversion is made.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the substance and register are sound, but two genuine cruxes are flagged and not smoothed: (a) the elided object of <i>observationes … vernalem et autumnalem</i> (equinox-observations is the natural but unstated referent; <i>aequinoctium</i> is not in the text); and (b) the doubled <i>apti</i> and the syntactic strain of the <i>an mores undiquaque gentis … apti tibi</i> clause in this later copy (Langebek 178, <i>Afskrift</i>), together with the in-text lacuna after <i>benignam</i>.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII (Langebek 178 afskrift): the doubled <i>apti</i> and the leading-/post-<i>benignam</i> lacunae are Dreyer's own text, unfilled and unfootnoted — genuine in the edition of record. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.20 page image in full. Collated the Scaliger-inserted-observations sentence, the withdrawal-into-Germany/grief clause, the convoluted 'mores...apti...apti tibi' sentence, the Batavia-invitation clause (with its genuine ellipsis gap preserved, not filled), the 'fere dixerat, adorabit' hyperbole, and the closing States/Ordinum sentence against source_text and published_english; source_text matches Dreyer's fragment verbatim and every clause of the translation is faithful, including correctly bracketed interpretive supplements ([a token], [for you]) for genuinely compressed or elliptical Latin. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade C does not survive this collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 178. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Relays that Scaliger has incorporated Tycho's vernal and autumnal observations into his own work and suggests Holland would suit Tycho better than Germany.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980000002", "d": "1598-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius — [Wandsbek], 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (Afskrift = transcript copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD THADDAeUM HAGECIUM.</p><p>Post scripta ad D. D. Thaddaeum Hagecium</p><p>IN alteris tuis, optime mi THADDAeE, facis mentionem istius calumniatoris Ditmarsici, quodque eius scriptum maledicum in Daniam ad me mitti, antequam in Moraviam abires, procuraveris, mihi significas, quo maturius id obtinerem, pro qua tua in me benevolentia gratias habeo. Famosum istum libellum non quidem inde accepi, nescio, an eo pervenerit, literae enim solae absque libro mihi redditae sunt hîc Wandesburgi, e Dania transmissae; attamen ante paucos dies istum a quodam studioso Helmstadiensi mihi missum accepi. Extimas tu quidem, me ad omnia percommode responsurum, quod etiam mihi foret quam facilimum. Verum ego cum tam impudente conviciatore, qui omnes verecundiae limites transilijt, et totus calumnijs et mendacijs scatet, verba commutare mihi probrosum duco, neque enim dignus est, ut in scriptis meis nominetur, nedum refutetur. Erit forte alius, qui pro me tum quoque ROTHMANNO, bonae mem. id faciet, eiusque effrenem maledicentiam retundet. Ego vero eum non verbis sed verberibus dignum censeo, et iuxta leges puniendum, quod contra Imperij constitutiones, tam famosum, impudens et meris nugis, mendacijs et malitijs refertum scriptum in publicum edere non sit veritus, et nihilominus Caesaris Mathematicum in frontispicio se profitetur, nec tamen Typographi nomen subiungere audet, uti fit in famosis libellis. Constitui itaque, modo cum bona Caesaris venia, uti spero, id fieri queat, istum pessimum blateronem in ius vocari curare, et publice plagij iniuriarum et mendaciorum accusatum et convictum legibus coerceri et puniri, tum quoque Typographum, qui tam scurrilia impressit citra ordinationes Imperij, mulctari, totumque illud scriptum uti meretur irritum reddi, auctorem vero, unâ cum Typographo, quem tu facile nosti, istic re et corpore in arresto detineri, donec res iure decisa sit. Interim ad praecipuas Germaniae Academias tam Pontificias quam alias scriptum istud mittam, et earum censuram, adiuncta mea declaratione, exquiram, quae omnia postea in ius proponam. Quid Helmstadiensis Academia de hoc iudicet, ex ijs, quae studiosus ille unâ cum libro transmisso ad me perscripsit, videre est, quae est eiusmodi: »Rerum novarum hic nihil est, nisi verum et ferum istum Ursum non hominem (cuius librum hîc quidem non venalem, sed a magno quodam viro acceptum, vobis mitto) pro suo ingenio in vos debacchatum esse, qui, quod vobis gratulamur, sui ipsius ore suam prodidit inscitiam, et innata verborum virulentia ac canina calumniandi rabie vestram probe ultus est iniuriam, utque doctissimus noster CASELIUS, caeteraque Professorum cohors attestatur, quod impuro suo scripto de doctorum virorum coetu plane se exemit, nec dignus est, ut Caesaris amplius vocetur Mathematicus, nedum ut sit. Sed nae ego desipio, qui γλαῦκας εἰς Ἀθήνας. Introspiciatis! Brunsvicensem Cancellarium Pragae fuisse, vere ad vos delatum est, URSUMque exemplar sui operis illi obtulisse, quo pravi ingenij scurrili specimine statuit hîc professionem ambire atque impetrare. Sed ut Cancellarij humanitatis erat, oblatum librum non renuere, ita sapientiae, tam impurum scriptum plane improbare et irridere, quod etiam ab Illmo nostro (uti ab aulico mihi coniunctissimo certo comperi) prorsus damnatum est, adeo ut eius indignitas muneris alicuius illi apud nos deferendi Illustrissimo cogitationem plane ademit. Nos illius torsa in se tela laetamur, vestramque virtutem a tali gregario milite invictam, ut novum quiddam non miramur, qui, si vos isthic agere intellexisset, haud dubie ut in alijs omnibus discessum e Dania exilij convitio calumniose perstrinxisset. Sed huius monstri calumnias nunquam melius quam silentio retundetis, non enim dignus est ira Caesaris.« Haec ille, et postea in postscriptis quibusdam de DUNCANO LIDDELIO Scoto Professore isthic agente, quem meas sibi publice arrogasse Hypotheses, quas tamen a me, cum in Dania semel atque iterum mihi adesset, didicerat, fama percrebuit, ideoque scire avebam, an res ita se haberet, nec ne, sic proloquitur: »Maxima diligentia inquisivi de vestris hypothesibus, an DUNCANUS eas sibi arrogaret, nec potui intelligere ex ijs, qui quotidie cum illo conversantur, eum nunquam ausum fuisse eas appellare suas hypotheses, quod etiam in publicis eius praelectionibus nunquam audivi. Scit bene se obtinere non posse, ut fides ei adhibeatur, sed aliquoties dixit de suo conterraneo CRAIGO, licet saltem privatim apud suos familiares, quem suum vocat Praeceptorem, quod vos aliquando scripsistis quaedam contra illum, in quibus illum mancipium ARISTOTELIS appellatis, quod eum male habet. Ille, cum Rostochij privatim doceret, vestras Hypotheses Tychonianas appellavit, nec pro suis venditavit, qui satis est conscius a nemine esse excogitatas, antequam vos eas invenissetis, quia in Dania eas a vobis intellexerat, diu antequam URSI istius Ditmarsici plagiarij vestri Fundamentum Astronomicum (si dijs placet) evulgatum est, ubi iste perfrictae frontis URSUS hanc inventionem, quam tamen non satis capere videtur, sibi attribuere non erubescit, quam procul dubio apud vos habuit, cum ante multos annos Nobiliss. viro ERICO LANGIO serviens, Uraniburgi cum dicto ERICO per multos dies mansisset. De hoc URSO, cum DUNCANUS maledicum in vos scriptum vidisset, licet vobis ob suum conterraneum et praeceptorem CRAIGUM non valde faveret, sic dixit: Se satis mirari non posse istius URSI impudentem audaciam, et quod nulla honestas, nulla modestia, nullum virtutis vestigium in eo reperiatur, quod omnes etiam docti coguntur fateri, et aiunt: ipsum evacuasse omnis impudentiae poculum, ipsumque valde, uti et meritus est, execrantur, neque potest honestus animus cordati cuiusvis viri aliud in posterum sentire de isto impudenti URSO, quam ipsum omnes verecundiae limites supra modum excessisse, neque dignum, ut in consortio honestorum virorum amplius versetur.«</p><p>Haec nimirum est Helmstadiensium Professorum de URSO isto Ditmarsico sententia, cui plures etiam Academiae non difficulter adstipulabantur, et quotquot sunt Hamburgi viri docti, ad quos infamis iste liber pervenit, non meliori Elogio auctorem ornant. Haec vero ob id tibi paulo prolixius refero, dilectissime THADDAeE, quod sciam tibi etiam tam inverecundum et scelestum facinus displicere, rogoque ut rationem ineas, mihique tuum consilium aperias, quomodo is absque Caesaris offensa de iure castigandus sit, neque enim ad tot tantaque convitia atque calumnias salvo honore meo prorsus silere decet, cum non saltem me, sed et domum meam honestissimam, de qua fama hactenus verita est aliquid improbi loqui aut sentire, scurrilibus mendacijs et infamationibus proscindere non erubescat, multaque alia, quae piget referre, singula tamen suo tempore, in ipsum, prout meretur, retorquenda; sed haec aliquantisper dissimulanda censeo, donec in altero negotio, quod longe maioris est momenti, ex voto, uti confido, omnia transacta fuerint. Ista alia suam bene invenient occasionem, praesertim ubi Caesar, cum rectius noverit et clam apud Ducem Brunsvicensem Professionem in Academia Helmstadiensi ambivisse, aut repulsam passum esse resciverit. Nec etiam ea, quae in eodem scripto circa calcem habet, admodum honorifice de Caesare, cuius se iactat Mathematicum, prolata videntur, ubi suam penuriam, sibique nihil dari, et nescio quae alia in Reges et Principes scommata seu potius convitia exaggerat. Os enim ex eo abundat quo cor repletum est. Velim itaque, mi THADDAeE, ut in hoc quoque negotio tuum mihi significes cum consilio iudicium, et quomodo Eruditi atque honesti viri istic tam in aula quam urbe scriptum illud aestiment, et an Caesari tale quid innotuerit, atque utrum placeat vel displiceat, tum quoque ut de Typographo, quae scire opus est, cognoscas. Feceris mihi hac etiam in parte rem imprimis acceptam, et nihil addubito, quin tuo hîc non sis defuturus officio, cum sciam te non minus aegre ferre ea, quae in me evomuntur, maledicta verba, quam si eiuscemodi quid tibi ipsi contingeret, velut et ego in te semper pari affectus sum animo. Vale.</p><p>Haec Mathematum quidam Professor Wittebergensis, M. MELCHIOR IOeSTELIUS scripsit ad CHRISTIANUM SEVERINI LONGOMONTANUM, meum antea Studiosum, qui sic habet:</p><p>De RAYMARI nuperrime in te impudentia et debacchatione per librum quendam suum recens, ut intelligo, editum sparsa, idem IOeSTELIUS literis suis ultimis ad me missis haec subiungit:</p><p>»Pervenit ante paucos dies ad manus meas libellus quidam RAIMARI, in quo toto non tam sophistice quam scurriliter in Generosum Dominum TYCHONEM invehitur. Etsi autem certum est, neminem sanum aut cordatum hoc probaturum, Generosum item equum non curare latratum canum, tamen minime dubito, quin saltem coquus Domini TYCHONIS futilitatem et virulentiam ipsius retundet.«</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Thaddaeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>Postscript to Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius</p><p>In your second letter, my excellent Thaddaeus, you make mention of that Dithmarschen slanderer, and you inform me that, before you departed for Moravia, you arranged for his scurrilous writing to be sent to me in Denmark, so that I might obtain it the sooner — for which goodwill of yours toward me I render thanks. That defamatory little book I did not, indeed, receive from there; I do not know whether it [ever] arrived there, for it was the letters alone, without the book, that were delivered to me here at Wandsbek, forwarded from Denmark; nevertheless, a few days ago I received that [book], sent to me by a certain student at Helmstedt. You reckon, to be sure, that I shall reply to everything quite handily, which would indeed be most easy for me. But I judge it shameful to bandy words with so impudent a reviler, who has overleapt all the bounds of decency and teems wholly with slanders and lies; for he is not worthy to be named in my writings, far less refuted. There will perhaps be someone else who will do this on behalf of myself and also of Rothmann, of good memory, and will blunt his unbridled scurrility. I, for my part, judge him worthy not of words but of whippings, and to be punished according to the laws, in that, against the constitutions of the Empire, he has not feared to publish a writing so defamatory, so impudent, and stuffed with mere trifles, lies, and malice — and nonetheless professes himself His Imperial Majesty's Mathematician on the title-page, yet does not dare to append the printer's name, as is done in defamatory pamphlets. I have therefore resolved — provided it can be done, as I hope, with His Imperial Majesty's good leave — to see that this vilest babbler be summoned to law, and, publicly accused and convicted of plagiarism, of injuries, and of lies, be restrained and punished by the laws; and likewise that the printer, who printed such scurrilous matter contrary to the ordinances of the Empire, be fined; and that that whole writing be, as it deserves, declared void; and that the author, together with the printer — whom you readily know — be there detained in arrest, in goods and in person, until the matter has been decided at law. Meanwhile I shall send that writing to the principal universities of Germany, both the Catholic ones and the others, and shall seek out their censure, with my own declaration appended — all of which I shall afterward lay before the court. What the University of Helmstedt judges of this may be seen from what that student wrote out to me along with the book he forwarded, which is of this sort: »There is no news here, except that that true and savage Bear [Ursus], no man, (whose book — not, indeed, on sale here, but received from a certain great man — I send you) has, after his fashion, raved against you; who, as we congratulate you, has by his own mouth betrayed his own ignorance, and by his inborn virulence of words and his canine madness for slandering has well avenged your wrong; and, as our most learned Caselius and the rest of the company of Professors attest, by his foul writing he has plainly removed himself from the company of learned men, and is not worthy any longer to be called His Imperial Majesty's Mathematician, far less to be one. But truly I am playing the fool, [carrying] owls to Athens [γλαῦκας εἰς Ἀθήνας]. Look closely! That the Chancellor of Brunswick was at Prague has been truly reported to you, and that Ursus offered him a copy of his work, by which scurrilous specimen of his crooked wit he resolved to seek and obtain a professorship here. But as it was a mark of the Chancellor's courtesy not to refuse the offered book, so it was a mark of his wisdom plainly to disapprove and deride so foul a writing — which was also wholly condemned by our Most Illustrious [Lord] (as I have learned for certain from a courtier most closely bound to me), to such a degree that its unworthiness plainly took from the Most Illustrious [Lord] the thought of conferring any office on him among us. We rejoice at his weapons turned back upon himself, and that your virtue is unconquered by such a common soldier — so that we do not wonder at it as anything new; for he, had he understood that you were active there, would doubtless, as in all other things, have slanderously carped at your departure from Denmark with the reproach of exile. But you will never blunt this monster's slanders better than by silence; for he is not worthy of His Imperial Majesty's anger.« Thus he; and afterward, in certain postscripts, concerning Duncan Liddel, the Scottish Professor active there — of whom a report had spread that he had publicly claimed as his own my Hypotheses, which yet he had learned from me when he was with me in Denmark once and again, and so I was eager to know whether the matter was so, or not — he speaks thus: »With the greatest diligence I inquired about your hypotheses, whether Duncan claimed them as his own, and I could not gather, from those who converse with him daily, that he had ever dared to call them his hypotheses; which I have never heard in his public lectures either. He well knows that he cannot bring it about that credence be given him; but he said several times of his fellow-countryman Craig — though only privately, among his intimates — whom he calls his Preceptor, that you once wrote certain things against him, in which you call him a bond-slave of Aristotle, which galls him. He, when he was teaching privately at Rostock, called your Hypotheses 'Tychonic,' and did not peddle them as his own, being well enough aware that they were devised by no one before you discovered them, because he had learned them from you in Denmark, long before that Dithmarschen plagiarist Ursus's <i>Astronomical Foundation</i> (if it please the gods) was published abroad — wherein that brazen-browed Ursus does not blush to attribute to himself this invention, which, however, he does not seem sufficiently to grasp, [and] which without doubt he got from you, when, serving years ago the most noble Erik Lange, he had stayed with the said Erik at Uraniborg for many days. Of this Ursus, when Duncan had seen the scurrilous writing against you, although he did not much favour you on account of his fellow-countryman and preceptor Craig, he spoke thus: that he could not sufficiently marvel at the impudent audacity of that Ursus, and that no honesty, no modesty, no trace of virtue is found in him — which even all the learned are compelled to admit, and they say: that he has drained the whole cup of impudence, and they greatly execrate him, as indeed he has deserved; nor can the honest mind of any sensible man henceforth feel otherwise about that impudent Ursus than that he has beyond measure exceeded all the bounds of decency, and is not worthy to consort any longer in the company of honest men.«</p><p>This, then, is the verdict of the Helmstedt Professors about that Dithmarschen Ursus — to which several other universities also assented without difficulty; and however many learned men there are at Hamburg, to whom that infamous book has come, they adorn its author with no better eulogy. But I report these things to you at somewhat greater length, most beloved Thaddaeus, for this reason: because I know that so shameless and wicked a deed displeases you also; and I ask that you devise a plan and disclose your counsel to me, [as to] how he may lawfully be chastised without offence to His Imperial Majesty — for it does not befit my honour to keep wholly silent at so many and so great revilings and slanders, since he does not blush to rend with scurrilous lies and defamations not only me but also my most honourable house, of which rumour has hitherto feared to speak or think anything dishonourable, and many other things which it irks me to relate — each of them, however, in its own time, to be turned back upon him as he deserves; but I judge that these things should be dissembled for a while, until in the other affair, which is by far of greater moment, everything shall have been transacted according to [my] wish, as I trust. Those other matters will well find their own occasion, especially once His Imperial Majesty — when he comes to know more correctly, and learns that [Ursus] secretly sought a professorship at the University of Helmstedt with the Duke of Brunswick, or suffered a rebuff — [shall be informed of it]. Nor, too, do the things he has near the end of the same writing seem to be uttered very honourably about His Imperial Majesty, whose Mathematician he boasts himself to be, where he magnifies his own want, and that nothing is given him, and I know not what other gibes — or rather revilings — against Kings and Princes. For the mouth overflows from that with which the heart is filled. I should wish, therefore, my Thaddaeus, that in this matter too you signify to me your judgement together with your counsel, and how learned and honest men there, both at court and in the city, esteem that writing, and whether anything of the kind has come to His Imperial Majesty's notice, and whether it pleases or displeases [him]; and likewise that you find out, concerning the printer, what it is needful to know. You will have done me, in this part also, a thing especially welcome; and I do not doubt at all that you will not fail in your good office here, since I know that you bear the abusive words spewed out against me no less hard than if something of the kind befell you yourself — just as I too have always been disposed toward you with an equal feeling. Farewell.</p><p>These things a certain Professor of Mathematics at Wittenberg, Master Melchior Jöstel, wrote to Christian Sørensen Longomontanus, formerly my student, which run thus:</p><p>Concerning Reimarus's impudence and raving lately spread abroad against you through a certain book of his, recently, as I understand, published, the same Jöstel adds these things in his last letter sent to me:</p><p>»A few days ago a certain little book of Reimarus's came into my hands, in the whole of which he inveighs against the Noble Master Tycho not so much sophistically as scurrilously. But although it is certain that no sane or sensible person will approve this, [and] that a noble horse likewise heeds not the barking of dogs, yet I do not doubt in the least that Master Tycho's cook, at any rate, will blunt his futility and virulence.«</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho Brahe to <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), the Bohemian court physician and astronomer in Prague, Tycho's long-time friend and his principal channel to the imperial court of Rudolf II. The letter is an <i>appendix</i> (<i>\"Post scripta\"</i> / postscript) to a fuller letter, gathering the documentary evidence Tycho wished Hagecius to use against Ursus.</li><li><b>Date / place.</b> The dataset `label` is <i>s.l.</i> (\"no place [given]\"); no dateline survives in the transcript. The body locates Tycho <i>hîc Wandesburgi</i> — \"here at <b>Wandsbek</b>\" (Wandesburgum, now a district of Hamburg, where Tycho lived 1597–98 after leaving Denmark) — and the events (receipt of Ursus's <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> of 1597, Tycho's plans to prosecute) fix it to <b>1598 (O.S.)</b>, the year given in the source header and the slug. The header therefore carries the bracketed place and the year; the apparatus notes that neither a day nor month is in the text. No date is silently asserted beyond the year the source supplies.</li><li><b>\"istius calumniatoris Ditmarsici\" / \"URSUS\"</b> — <b>Nicolaus Reimarus Ursus</b> (Reimers Bär, d. 1600), of <b>Dithmarschen</b> (<i>Ditmarsicus</i>), then Imperial Mathematician at Prague. His <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus … tractatus</i> (Prague 1597) attacked Tycho and claimed the geo-heliocentric world-system; Tycho regarded it as plagiarism of his own system, learned during Ursus's 1584 visit to Uraniborg in the service of Erik Lange. The pun on his name — <i>Ursus</i> = \"Bear\" — is exploited in the Helmstedt letter (<i>\"verum et ferum istum Ursum non hominem\"</i>, \"that true and savage Bear, no man\"); kept and glossed.</li><li><b>\"ROTHMANNO, bonae mem.\"</b> — <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Hesse-Kassel mathematician (protocol §5.6); <i>bonae memoriae</i>, \"of good [i.e. blessed] memory,\" indicates Tycho here believed Rothmann dead (his date of death is in fact uncertain). Ursus's tract had also attacked Rothmann.</li><li><b>\"Caesar / Caesaris Mathematicum\"</b> — <i>Caesar</i> throughout = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>; <i>Caesaris Mathematicus</i> = \"the Emperor's Mathematician,\" the imperial post Ursus held and styled himself by on his title-page (<i>in frontispicio</i>). Rendered \"His Imperial Majesty / His Imperial Majesty's Mathematician.\"</li><li><b>\"contra Imperij constitutiones … citra ordinationes Imperij\"</b> — the imperial press laws (the Reichspolizeiordnung provisions against unsigned defamatory libels); the absence of the printer's name is precisely the statutory offence Tycho points to.</li><li><b>Helmstedt letter (the inset quotation).</b> Sent to Tycho by an unnamed <i>studiosus Helmstadiensis</i> (a student at the <b>University of Helmstedt</b>, the Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel university). <b>\"CASELIUS\"</b> = <b>Johannes Caselius</b> (1533–1613), the eminent Helmstedt humanist. <i>\"Brunsvicensem Cancellarium\"</i> = the Chancellor of Brunswick(-Wolfenbüttel); <i>\"Illmo nostro … Illustrissimo\"</i> = \"our Most Illustrious [Lord],\" i.e. the reigning Duke (Heinrich Julius of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel), at whose university Ursus had reportedly angled for a chair.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>γλαῦκας εἰς Ἀθήνας</i> — \"owls to Athens,\" the proverb (cf. Aristophanes, <i>Birds</i> 301; the owl being Athena's bird and Athens' civic emblem) for redundant effort, our \"coals to Newcastle.\" Transcribed verbatim (with the printed final-sigma forms) in the Source block and translated with the proverb flagged, per protocol §2.</li><li><b>\"DUNCANO LIDDELIO Scoto\" / \"CRAIGO\"</b> — <b>Duncan Liddel</b> (1561–1613), the Scottish physician-astronomer then professor at Helmstedt, reported (the report is here being checked and <i>not</i> confirmed) to have claimed Tycho's planetary hypotheses; he is reported rather to have taught them honestly as <i>Hypotheses Tychonianae</i> (\"Tychonic hypotheses\") while teaching privately at <b>Rostock</b>. <b>\"CRAIGO\" / CRAIGUS</b> = <b>John Craig</b> (d. 1620), Liddel's Scottish fellow-countryman and (so Liddel called him) <i>Praeceptor</i>, \"preceptor,\" with whom Tycho had a separate dispute; <i>\"mancipium Aristotelis\"</i> = \"a bond-slave of Aristotle,\" Tycho's reported jibe at Craig.</li><li><b>\"ERICO LANGIO\"</b> — <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), the Danish nobleman, alchemist, and Tycho's friend (later brother-in-law's circle); Ursus had been in his service and so came to Uraniborg.</li><li><b>\"Fundamentum Astronomicum (si dijs placet)\"</b> — Ursus's work is named ironically; <i>Fundamentum Astronomicum</i> is in fact the title of an <i>earlier</i> Ursus work (Strasbourg 1588). The parenthetical <i>si dijs placet</i>, \"if it please the gods,\" is a sarcastic period idiom (≈ \"if you please / God help us\"); kept and glossed, the irony preserved rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>\"plagij\" / \"plagiarij\"</b> — <i>plagium</i>, the legal offence of man-stealing/literary theft; <i>plagiarius</i>, \"plagiarist.\" Rendered \"plagiarism / plagiarist\"; this is among the earliest technical legal uses in the Tycho dossier.</li><li><b>Closing inset.</b> <b>M. Melchior Jöstel</b> (1559–1611), Master (<i>Magister</i>) and professor of mathematics at <b>Wittenberg</b>, wrote to <b>Christian Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (Christen Sørensen, 1562–1647), Tycho's senior assistant (\"formerly my student\"); <i>\"RAYMARI / RAIMARI\"</i> is again Ursus (Reimarus). The sentence <i>\"Generosum … equum non curare latratum canum\"</i> (\"a noble horse heeds not the barking of dogs\") is a proverb, kept as such. The closing jest — that even <b>Master Tycho's cook</b> could refute Ursus — is an insult-by-belittlement (Ursus is so feeble that the lowliest of Tycho's household would suffice); rendered straight.</li><li><b>Scriptural allusion.</b> <i>\"Os enim ex eo abundat quo cor repletum est\"</i> — \"For the mouth overflows from that with which the heart is filled,\" a paraphrase of Matthew 12:34 / Luke 6:45 (<i>ex abundantia cordis os loquitur</i>); rendered as the proverb-allusion it is.</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The legal-force clauses were counted: <i>\"non sit veritus\"</i> (\"has not feared,\" i.e. did dare), <i>\"nec … audet\"</i> (\"does not dare\"), <i>\"non … nominetur, nedum refutetur\"</i> (\"not to be named, far less refuted\"), <i>\"non verbis sed verberibus\"</i> (\"not of words but of whippings\"), <i>\"non … prorsus silere decet\"</i> (\"it does not befit … to keep wholly silent\"), <i>\"non erubescit\"</i> (\"does not blush\"), <i>\"nunquam … nedum\"</i>, and the closing <i>\"non … defuturus officio\"</i> (\"will not fail in your office\"). Each negation is reproduced one-for-one; none added or dropped.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, TBOO VIII pp.57-59 (page images): rejoined the place-name 'Uraniburgi' and the following word 'cum', which the working transcription had fused across a page-break catchword into 'Uraniburgicum' (p.57-58); and restored the dropped phrase 'et debacchatione' (\"and raving\") describing Ursus/Reimarus's pamphlet, with the English updated to match (p.59).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register (Tycho's controlled legal indignation, the students' invective, the proverbs and Greek tag) is matched. Residual soft points, flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the elliptical conditional <i>\"praesertim ubi Caesar … resciverit\"</i> has no finite main verb in the source for what then happens — supplied as <i>\"[shall be informed of it]\"</i> in brackets; (2) no day/month survives, so the header dates only to the year the source asserts; (3) <i>Rothmanno bonae mem.</i> reflects Tycho's belief, not a securely-dated death.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 56-59 (p.55 and p.60 of the slice were also read and confirmed to belong to the neighboring Maestlin and Rosenkrantz letters respectively, not this one). Checked opening/closing lines and all proper names (Caselius, Ursus, Duncan Liddel, Craig, Erik Lange, Jostel, Longomontanus, Reimarus) and place-names (Wandsbek, Helmstedt, Uraniborg, Rostock). Found two low-severity TEXT_ERROR departures of source_text from Dreyer (a glued catchword artifact, self-corrected by the translator, and one dropped descriptive word); neither changes what a scholar would take away, but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's text splits across a page break with a printer's catchword ('cum' previewed at the foot of p.57, then repeated as the first word of p.58). Our source_text glued the place-name 'Uraniburgi' to the stray catchword, producing the non-word 'Uraniburgicum'. The English translator correctly parsed the intended sense regardless ('stayed with the said Erik at Uraniborg'), so no defect propagates into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>Uraniburgi [catchword: cum] / cum dicto ERICO per multos dies mansisset (p.57-58 page break)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer characterizes Ursus/Reimarus's book as spreading both 'impudentia et debacchatione' (impudence AND raving/frenzy). Our source_text silently drops 'et debacchatione', and the English follows the truncated source, losing one of the two descriptive terms characterizing the pamphlet. Dreyer prints: <i>De RAYMARI nuperrime in te impudentia et debacchatione per librum quendam suum recens ... editum sparsa (p.59, line 3-4)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII pp.55-60 (page images), specifically the p.57/58 page-break catchword and p.59 line 3-4. Both TEXT_ERRORs are confirmed on the page images and repaired; the first required no English change (the translator had already parsed the intended sense), the second required a small English addition to match the restored Latin. No crux remains outstanding for this letter's audited defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 34, "prev": "brahe-t_15950812001", "next": "brahe-t_15980530001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15931224001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980530001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Hagecius for arranging to send a defamatory pamphlet and declines to reply to its author, deeming him unworthy of refutation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980102001", "d": "1598-01-02", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "ck": "Rudolf 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Emperor Rudolf II — Wandsbek (Rantzau Castle, near Hamburg), 2 January 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basileensis G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IMPERATOREM RUDOLPHUM SECUNDUM.</p><p>AUGUSTISSIME Imperator RUDOLPHE Secunde. Cum de tuae Imperatoriae Maiestatis erga liberalia studia, praesertim in his principaliora, Mathematica et Astronomica, clementi favore, diu multumque inaudiverim, ipsaque tua Caesarea Maiestas non tantum potentissimorum et laudatissimorum maiorum suorum ALPHONSI Regis Arragonum etc. MATTHIAe HUNNIADIS, HENRICI illius secundi ob indefessam rerum coelestium contemplationem Astronomi dicti, CAROLI quinti, FERDINANDI ac Augustissimi Parentis MAXIMILIANI secundi aliorumque, studijs his impense addictorum, quos numerare longum foret, celebratissimo exemplo, sed et sua ipsiusmet vere Heroica et Caesarea inclinatione, quantum Regnorum ac Reip. gubernatio ferunt, ijsdem his laudabiliter vacet exercitijs: omnino mihi polliceor, Caesaream tuam Maiestatem clementi animo accepturam, me viarum intercapedine alijsque difficultatibus, quo minus Caesaream tuam Maiestatem ipse cito adeo, ac praeoptarim alias, accedere liceat, praepeditum, hasce literas arduum licet negotium concernentes summisse exhibere non vereri ipsi. Cuius meae fidentiae caussam hanc primam habeo et praecipuam Imperatoriae tuae Maiestatis clementissimum erga me eaque, quae multis abhinc annis tracto studia, favorem, iampridem mihi vel ex solo eo singulari diplomate perspectum, quod Caesarea tua Maiestas tanquam in epistola ad me directo sermone clementer mihi impertiri, non sine harum artium praeclaro elogio, dignata est. Caesarea itaque tua Maiestas praesentibus hisce clementer scire dignetur, me, evidentibus ijsque haud levibus de caussis mutato Patrio solo, in Germaniam tuae Caesareae Maiestati subditam cum Astronomica mea supellectile, adeoque tota familia, nuper concessisse, ut est rerum humanarum omnium quaedam subinde vicissitudo. Ubi, favente coelestium Opifice, Astronomicos meos labores per 21 Annos in patria mea dulcissima Dania exantlatos continuare ac ad propositum scopum dirigere et absolvere decrevi, impetrata primum hîc alicubi et constituta rebus coelestibus sat commoda, constanti et inviolabili sede, unde fundamentalis earundem tractatio aeviternum ad posteros etiam in Imperij ipsius decus (uti confido) non contemnendum transmitti possit. Quocirca ad tuam Imperatoriam Maiestatem omnium primo mihi concedendum, eiusque clementissimam voluntatem quam humillime explorandam censui, an non illustre hoc Astronomicum negotium solide et solenniter institutum in aliqua suarum ditionum porro continuare atque ad optatum finem, Dei beneficio, perducere daretur. Sunt mihi ad hoc Opus in promptu Instrumenta varia, eaque maxima et affabre elaborata, qualia et quanta vix uspiam invenire datur. Sunt plurimorum annorum exactissimae Observationes veterum inventis non cedentes. Sunt multa alia huc conducentia, et non pauca, imo praecipua iam confecta. Ut vero Caesarea tua Maiestas specimen aliquod eorum habeat, mitto cum filio meo Organorum, quae hactenus in usu habui, quotquot tam cito exscindi curare licuit, delineationem, apposita earundem succincta explicatione. Offero quoque humiliter Canonicam Affixarum Stellarum expositionem, quotquot in nostro climate utcunque visuntur, numero millenarum, quas omnes intra Annos 20 praeteritos summa et indefessa diligentia meis Organis coelitus quam scrupulosissime demensus sum, cum sine harum, potissimum Zodiaco vicinarum, exacta verificatione Planetarum viae et motiones ad amussim non percipiantur. Qua etiam in parte (quoad errantia videlicet sidera) quid hactenus praestiterimus, quidque in posterum superaddendum restet, prior ille, de quo dixi, liber suo loco pandit. Plura meorum de Astronomia Operum tua Caesarea Maiestas, cui me meaque studia omnia humiliter offero et commendata cupio, brevi, aspirante Universitatis authore, obtinebit. Interea hasce primitias alijs gravioribus viam sternentes non aversetur. De caetero Caesarea tua Maiestas per Dominum Procancellarium Imperij aut aliquem e suis Consiliarijs intimis, referente Domino Doctore THADDAeO HAGGECIO, clementer intelligat, qui a filio meo praesente singula uberius cognoscat. Utque Caesarea tua Maiestas, hoc intermediante, sibi humillime meo nomine proposita clementer excipiat, et responsum, quandocunque eidem commodum fuerit, impertiri dignetur, qua possum submissione precor.</p><p>Deus Coelestium et Terrestrium author tuam Caesaream Maiestatem felici Imperij et Regnorum gubernatione quam diutissime florentem et incolumem tueatur.</p><p>Datae Wandelburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum sita, die 2 Ianuarij Anni 1598.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO EMPEROR RUDOLF THE SECOND.</b></p><p>Most August Emperor Rudolf the Second. Since I have for a long while and much heard tell of Your Imperial Majesty's clement favour toward the liberal studies — and, foremost among these, toward the mathematical and astronomical [studies] — and [since] Your Imperial Majesty Himself, not only by the most celebrated example of His most powerful and most praised ancestors — Alfonso, King of Aragon, etc., Matthias Hunyadi, that Henry the Second who for his unwearied contemplation of the things of the heavens was called the Astronomer, Charles the Fifth, Ferdinand, and that most August Parent Maximilian the Second, and others, who were earnestly devoted to these studies, whom it would be long to number — but also by His own truly heroic and imperial inclination, devotes Himself in laudable fashion to these same pursuits, so far as the government of His kingdoms and of the commonwealth allow: I altogether promise myself that Your Imperial Majesty will receive with clement spirit [the fact] that I, hindered by the long stretch of the roads and by other difficulties from being able to approach Your Imperial Majesty as soon as, and as I should otherwise have most preferred, do not fear to present humbly this letter, though it concerns an arduous matter. The first and chief cause of this confidence of mine I hold to be Your Imperial Majesty's most clement favour toward me and toward those studies which I have pursued for many years now — long since made plain to me even from that single, extraordinary diploma which Your Imperial Majesty deigned clemently to grant me, addressing me as it were in a letter in direct speech, and not without splendid praise of these arts. May Your Imperial Majesty therefore deign clemently to know by these present [letters] that I, for evident and by no means slight causes, having changed my native soil, have lately withdrawn into Germany, subject to Your Imperial Majesty, with my astronomical equipment and indeed with my whole household — as there is from time to time a certain vicissitude in all human affairs. There, the Maker of the heavenly things favouring [me], I have resolved to continue the astronomical labours which I toiled through over 21 years in my most sweet homeland Denmark, and to direct them to their proposed goal and bring them to completion — once there has first been obtained and established somewhere here a settled and inviolable seat, sufficiently suited to the heavenly matters, whence the fundamental treatment of those same [matters] may be transmitted to posterity for all time, and (as I trust) to no contemptible adornment of the Empire itself. Wherefore I judged that I must first of all be granted access to Your Imperial Majesty, and most humbly explore His most clement will [as to] whether it might not be granted [me] to continue this illustrious astronomical undertaking — solidly and solemnly established — onward in some one of His dominions, and to bring it, by God's beneficence, to the wished-for end. I have ready to hand for this Work instruments various, and those very great and skilfully wrought, such, and so great, as one is scarcely granted to find anywhere. There are most exact observations of very many years, not yielding to the discoveries of the ancients. There are many other things conducing hereto, and not a few — indeed the chief ones — already completed. But that Your Imperial Majesty may have some specimen of these, I send with my son a delineation of the instruments which I have hitherto had in use — as many as could be engraved so quickly — with a concise explanation of the same appended. I also humbly offer a canonical exposition of the fixed stars — as many as are in any way seen in our clime, to the number of a thousand — all of which, within the 20 years past, I have measured out from the heavens by my instruments, with the utmost and unwearied diligence, as scrupulously as could be; since without an exact verification of these — especially of those near the zodiac — the courses and motions of the planets are not perceived to the line. In which part too (with respect, that is, to the wandering stars) what I have so far accomplished, and what remains to be superadded hereafter, that former book of which I spoke sets forth in its place. More of my works on astronomy Your Imperial Majesty — to whom I humbly offer myself and all my studies, and wish them commended — shall shortly obtain, the Author of the Universe favouring [it]. Meanwhile let Him not turn away from these first-fruits, which pave the way for others weightier. For the rest, may Your Imperial Majesty be clemently informed through the Lord Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, or some one of His privy councillors — Lord Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius reporting [the matter] — who may learn each particular more fully from my son, [who is] present. And I pray, with what submission I can, that Your Imperial Majesty, through this intermediary, may clemently take up the [matters] most humbly proposed in my name, and deign to grant a response whenever it shall be convenient to Him.</p><p>May God, the Author of the heavenly and the earthly things, keep Your Imperial Majesty, by a fortunate government of the Empire and the kingdoms, flourishing and unharmed for the very longest time.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, from the Castle of Rantzau situated near Hamburg, on the 2nd day of January, in the year 1598.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), then in self-imposed exile from Denmark; <b>Rudolf II</b> (1552–1612), Holy Roman Emperor (r. 1576–1612), residing at Prague — to whose service Tycho was angling. This is Tycho's formal overture to imperial patronage, carried by his eldest son. It is the prose counterpart of the dedication of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (dated 31 December 1597 at Wandsbek), and the <i>delineation of the instruments</i> it announces is the engraved instrument-catalogue of that very work.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Wandesburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum</i> = <b>Wandsbek</b> (now part of Hamburg), at the castle of <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the Danish royal governor (<i>Statthalter</i>) of Holstein and humanist patron, who hosted Tycho in 1597–98. Tycho had left Hven in 1597 (\"changed my native soil\").</li><li><b>The son.</b> <i>cum filio meo … a filio meo praesente</i> — \"with my son … from my son, present\": <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b> (b. 1581), here acting as his father's envoy to court (though the diplomatic mission to Prague is more usually associated with Tycho's assistant Franz Tengnagel; the letter names only <i>filius meus</i>). Rendered literally as \"my son,\" not resolved to a named agent.</li><li><b>The ancestors named.</b> A roll of imperial/royal patrons of astronomy: <b><i>Alphonsus Rex Arragonum</b></i> = <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b> (1221–1284), patron of the Alfonsine Tables — Tycho's \"etc.\" and \"of Aragon\" loosely subsume the Iberian crowns (Alfonso X was King of Castile and León; the slip is the source's, kept). <b><i>Matthias Hunniades</b></i> = <b>Matthias Corvinus (Hunyadi)</b>, King of Hungary (1443–1490), patron of Regiomontanus. <b><i>Henricus ille secundus … Astronomi dicti</b></i> = a <b>Henry \"the Astronomer\"</b>; the epithet was applied to several Henries (e.g., Henry III of Castile \"el Doliente\" is sometimes confused; medieval tradition styled an Emperor/king \"Henry the Astronomer\") — Tycho's exact referent is not securely fixed and is left as the text gives it (<b>crux</b>, see below). <b><i>Carolus quintus</b></i> = <b>Charles V</b> (1500–1558); <b><i>Ferdinandus</b></i> = <b>Ferdinand I</b> (1503–1564); <b><i>Maximilianus secundus</b></i> = <b>Maximilian II</b> (1527–1576), Rudolf's father (<i>Augustissimus Parens</i>).</li><li><b>Instruments / observations.</b> <i>Organa</i> / <i>Instrumenta</i> → <b>instruments</b> (protocol §5.2); <i>Organorum … delineatio</i> = \"a delineation [drawn representation/engraving] of the instruments\" — the plates of the <i>Mechanica</i>. <i>Canonica Affixarum Stellarum expositio … numero millenarum</i> = a <b>canonical exposition of the fixed stars, to the number of a thousand</b> — Tycho's <b>1,000-star catalogue</b> (<i>Stellarum inerrantium restitutio</i>), measured <i>meis Organis coelitus</i> (\"from the heavens by my instruments\"). <i>ad amussim</i> = \"to the line / exactly\" (a builder's-rule idiom), here rendered \"to the line.\"</li><li><b><i>prior ille … liber</b></i> — \"that former book of which I spoke\": i.e. the instrument-volume (<i>Mechanica</i>) just mentioned, which in its place treats the planets (<i>errantia sidera</i>, \"the wandering stars\").</li><li><b><i>primitias … alijs gravioribus viam sternentes</b></i> — \"these first-fruits, which pave the way for others weightier\": the <i>Mechanica</i> + star-catalogue as forerunners of Tycho's larger projected works (the planetary theory / <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i>).</li><li><b>Court officials.</b> <i>Dominus Procancellarius Imperij</i> = the <b>Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b>; <i>Consiliarij intimi</i> = <b>privy councillors</b>. <b><i>Thaddaeus Haggecius</b></i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius, 1525–1600), Bohemian physician-astronomer and imperial counsellor at Prague, Tycho's long-standing correspondent and his key intermediary at Rudolf's court.</li><li><b><i>singulari diplomate … in epistola ad me directo sermone</b></i> — \"that single, extraordinary diploma … addressing me as it were in a letter in direct speech\": an earlier personal mark of imperial favour shown to Tycho; rendered without sharpening the simile (<i>tanquam … directo sermone</i>).</li><li><b><i>aspirante Universitatis authore</b></i> / <b><i>Deus Coelestium et Terrestrium author</b></i> — \"the Author of the Universe favouring [it]\" / \"God, the Author of the heavenly and the earthly things\": Tycho's pious framing; <i>aspirante</i> = \"breathing favour upon,\" the standard <i>Deo aspirante</i> hedge, kept as a conditional (\"favouring\").</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 2 Ianuarij Anni 1598</i> = <b>2 January 1598</b>. Denmark and the Empire's Protestant correspondents used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar; the dateline is taken at face value as <b>O.S.</b> The catalogue label (\"1598. 2. januar. Wandsburg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. No style marker (<i>n.s.</i>) is present.</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> <i>(uti confido)</i> \"as I trust\"; <i>favente coelestium Opifice</i> / <i>Dei beneficio</i> / <i>aspirante … authore</i> — Tycho's success-conditions are kept as conditionals, not flattened into assertions; <i>vix uspiam invenire datur</i> \"one is scarcely granted to find anywhere\" (not \"nowhere\"); <i>non contemnendum</i> \"no contemptible\" (litotes kept).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored 'Wandelburgo' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.20) for the one-letter departure 'Wandesburgo' in our transcription of the closing dateline; the English 'Wandsbek' was unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the diplomatic register matched; the one open point is the identity of <b><i>Henricus ille secundus … Astronomi dicti</b></i> (\"that Henry the Second called the Astronomer\"), whose historical referent among the several \"astronomer\" Henries is not securely fixed and is left as the source gives it rather than resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.18-20 as page images (slice pages 1-3; p.21/slice p.4 is the start of a different letter) and collated the full letter clause by clause: opening, the six-emperor list (Alphonsus, Matthias Hunniades, Henricus II, Carolus V, Ferdinandus, Maximilianus II), the numbers (21 years in Denmark, 20 years of star measurement, 'numero millenarum'), Hagecius's name, and the closing dateline. All match Dreyer verbatim except the closing place-name spelling (Wandesburgo vs Dreyer's Wandelburgo), which is a TEXT_ERROR under the letter of the rule; no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text spells the place-name 'Wandesburgo' but Dreyer's printed page reads 'Wandelburgo'; a genuine one-letter departure of our Latin from the edition of record, though it does not affect the English rendering ('Wandsbek') either way. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae Wandelburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum sita, die 2 Ianuarij Anni 1598. (TBOO VIII p.20, closing dateline)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR defect directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.20, closing dateline): Dreyer prints 'Wandelburgo', confirming the audit. Fixed only the Latin; the English translation was already correct under either spelling. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Addresses Rudolf II invoking the Imperial tradition of astronomical patronage and solicits the Emperor's clement reception of his petition.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980102001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980118001", "d": "1598-01-18", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Heinrich Ellenberger", "ck": "Heinrich Ellenberger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "London", "t": "Henricus Ellenberger → Tycho Brahe — London, 18 January 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witnesses: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Monacensis Bibl. Univ. 693 fol. (copy); B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, NOBILI DANO, DOMINO ET FAUTORI SUO OBSERVANDISSIMO. HambURGK.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>CUM nuper ex Italia per utramque Germaniam in Angliam venissem, Generose et praestantissime Domine TYCHO, accepissemque a quodam meo amico Domino MATTHAeO ROYDON, Anglo, Mathematum, ut mihi videbatur, scientissimo, te ex Dania atque Uraniburgo tuo in Germaniam concessisse, atque adeo cum Lare prope Francofurtum ad Moenum consedisse, gaudebam simul et dolebam. Gaudebam equidem, tum quod meae, quam alioqui in Daniam ad te suscepturus eram, profectionis compendium facerem; tum etiam quia sperabam te Hassiae nostrae, cum in proximo eius esses, futurum aliquando inquilinum. Id quod quamvis optare verius, quam sperare potuerim, quippe qui non ignorabam te Daniae Regnum patriam tuam cum Hassiae vix commutaturum, nihilominus quo facilius illud crederem, fecit Illustrissimi Principis MAURITIJ Domini mei clementissimini in viros praeclaros amor ac munificentia singularis, utpote et quae tibi cum laudatissimae memoriae parente eius diu intercessit familiaritas. Dolebam tamen, quod adversi aliquid in Dania tibi evenisse referrent, tuaeque abitionis ex Dania alij alias recensebant causas. Siquidem fuerunt hîc in Anglia, qui tibi cum PETRO SEVERINO Medico litem fuisse nescio quam asseverarent, teque indignitatis adversarij puditum esse, et ideo inde discessisse. Alij Cancellarium Regium feudum aliquod tibi a FRIDERICO II felicis memoriae concessum, sibi arrogasse, aliaque ad Astronomiae studium exaedificandum adminicula, ab eodem Rege et Regni Proceribus tibi nuncupata, nunc fuisse adempta caussabantur. Hoc verisimilius et mihi et illis visum est, illud de medico plane incredibile. Quotusquisque enim est, qui ignorat, te ex praeclarissima prosapia ibidem prognatum, et summae apud Regem auctoritatis virum tam levi molimine non posse ex patria moveri, ut taceam e patria emoveri. Verum cum in Angliam etiam delatum sit, SEVERINUM illum apud hunc novellum Cancellarium multum valere, adeo ut is ferme totus ab illo dependeat, atque obscurum non sit, Doctorem illum (ut referunt) diutina tui invidia laborasse, fortassis hoc, quod per semetipsum non potuit exequi, per hunc Cancellarium, cui Rex plurimum tribuere dicitur, effectum dedisse.</p><p>Haec, Illustrissime et praestantissime Vir, quamvis ad me nihil spectant, tibi significo, ut etiam externorum de tuo Uraniburgo inopinato discessu iudicium ac sollicitudinem cognoscas. Caeterum paucis abhinc diebus, cum de loco ubi haereres sollicitus, eos qui ex Germania recens venirent, interrogarem, intelligo te non prope Francofurtum, ut ille existimabat, sed iuxta Hamburgum sedes posuisse. Quare rogatus ab amicis Anglis, qui ad te abituri erant, has ad te depropero, ut cognoscam, an inibi sis mansurus, an vero alio iturus. Qua in re illis rem gratam facturum me non dubito, tibi etiam non iniucundum futurum illud arbitratus sum, si intelligeres etiam in Anglia esse, quos amor tui sollicite tangeret. Quamobrem plane confido, te has meas literas, quamvis ab homine tibi hactenus ignoto et tua familiaritate indigno profectae sint, aequi bonique consulturum, et ad illas prima data occasione responsurum.</p><p>Faxit Deus, ut te aliquando, Vir Praestantissime, in Hassia videamus, exinde enim magnum decus ac nomen accessurum Patriae meae existimarem. Sin minus id superis visum sit, efficiam, ut te aliquando, volente Deo, ubicunque Germaniae etiam sis, invisam, coramque venerer. Vale et meae impudentiae ignosce, vir longe praestantissime. Salutem porro accipe a multis in Anglia tibi etiam forte ignotis, nominis tamen tui observantissimis.</p><p>LondiniAngliae, anno 1598, 15. Calend. Februarij.<br>T. Magnif. addictissimus<br>HENRICUS ELLENBERGER,<br>Hassus. M.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most excellent man, Master Tycho Brahe, noble Dane, his lord and patron most to be esteemed. [At] Hamburg.</b><br><b>Sends [him] heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>When I had lately come out of Italy, through both Germanies [i.e. Upper and Lower Germany], into England, generous and most excellent Master Tycho, and had learned from a certain friend of mine, Master Matthew Roydon, an Englishman most learned (as it seemed to me) in the mathematical sciences, that you had withdrawn from Denmark and from your Uraniborg into Germany, and had even settled with your household near Frankfurt-am-Main, I rejoiced and grieved at once. I rejoiced, to be sure, both because I should thereby spare myself the journey I was otherwise about to undertake into Denmark to you, and also because I hoped that you would one day become a resident of our Hesse, since you would be in its near neighbourhood. And although I could wish for this more truly than I could hope for it — for I was not unaware that you would scarcely exchange the Kingdom of Denmark, your native land, for Hesse — nevertheless what made me believe it the more readily was the singular love and munificence of the Most Illustrious Prince Maurice, my most clement lord, toward distinguished men, and likewise the long-standing familiarity that subsisted between you and his parent of most praised memory. Yet I grieved, because they reported that something adverse had befallen you in Denmark, and different men gave different accounts of your departure from Denmark. For there were here in England those who asserted that you had had some quarrel or other — I know not what — with Peter Severinus the physician, and that you had been ashamed of your adversary's unworthiness, and had therefore departed thence. Others alleged that the Royal Chancellor had arrogated to himself a certain fief granted to you by Frederick II of happy memory, and that the other aids for building up the study of astronomy, assigned to you by that same King and by the Magnates of the Realm, had now been taken away. This seemed the more likely both to me and to them; that other [story] about the physician, plainly incredible. For how few there are who do not know that you, sprung there from a most distinguished lineage and a man of the highest authority with the King, cannot by so slight a contrivance be moved out of your homeland — to say nothing of being driven out of your homeland. But since it has also been brought to England that that Severinus prevails greatly with this new Chancellor, so much so that the latter depends almost wholly upon him, and since it is not obscure that that Doctor (as they report) has long laboured under an envy of you, perhaps he has accomplished, through this Chancellor — to whom the King is said to grant very much — that which he could not carry out by himself.</p><p>These things, Most Illustrious and most excellent man, although they in no way concern me, I make known to you, so that you may also learn the judgement and the anxious concern of foreigners regarding your unlooked-for departure from your Uraniborg. For the rest, a few days ago, when, anxious about the place where you might be lodging, I was questioning those who had recently come from Germany, I understand that you have placed your seat not near Frankfurt, as he supposed, but next to Hamburg. Wherefore, being asked by my English friends who were about to set out to you, I hasten this [letter] to you, that I may learn whether you are going to remain there, or rather to go elsewhere. In which matter I doubt not that I shall be doing them a welcome service; and I judged that it would also be not unpleasing to you, were you to understand that there are even in England those whom love of you anxiously touches. For which reason I fully trust that you will take this my letter in good part — although it has proceeded from a man hitherto unknown to you and unworthy of your familiarity — and will answer it at the first opportunity given.</p><p>God grant that we may one day see you, Most excellent man, in Hesse; for from that I should reckon that great honour and renown would accrue to my native land. But if it be less than this in the eyes of those above, I shall bring it about that one day, God willing, wherever in Germany you may be, I shall visit you and revere you in person. Farewell, and forgive my boldness, man by far most excellent. Receive, further, a greeting from many in England, perhaps likewise unknown to you, yet most respectful of your name.</p><p>At London in England, in the year 1598, on the 15th [day before the] Kalends of February.<br>Your Magnificence's most devoted,<br>Henricus Ellenberger,<br>a Hessian. M[aster].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent / sender.</b> The dataset header gives the sender as \"Heinrich Ellenberger,\" matching the subscription <i>Henricus Ellenberger, Hassus. M.</i> — Henricus (Heinrich) Ellenberger, a <b>Hessian</b> (<i>Hassus</i>) holding the degree <i>Magister</i> (<i>M.</i>), writing from London as a young scholar previously unknown to Tycho (he calls himself <i>homine tibi hactenus ignoto</i>, \"a man hitherto unknown to you\"). He presents himself as a subject and well-wisher of the Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel.</li><li><b>Recipient / address.</b> <i>TYCHONI BRAHE, NOBILI DANO … HambURGK</i> — Tycho, then in the Hamburg area during the early exile (Wandsbek/Wandsburg, just outside Hamburg, where he had settled in 1597–98 after leaving Hven). The address-line town <b>Hamburg</b> corrects the rumour, repeated in the body, that he was near Frankfurt.</li><li><b><i>S. P. D.</b></i> = <i>salutem plurimam dicit</i>, \"sends heartiest greeting\" (protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Matthew Roydon.</b> <i>MATTHAeO ROYDON, Anglo</i> = <b>Matthew Roydon</b> (d. c. 1622), the English poet and member of the Chapman–Raleigh literary-scientific circle, here Ellenberger's informant on Tycho's movements. The Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> index (vol. XV) records the name at VIII 21, 462, locating this letter in <b>Dreyer VIII</b>.</li><li><b>Maurice of Hesse.</b> <i>Illustrissimi Principis MAURITIJ Domini mei clementissimi</i> = <b>Moritz (\"the Learned\"), Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (r. 1592–1627), Ellenberger's sovereign. <i>laudatissimae memoriae parente eius</i> (\"his parent of most praised memory\") = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (d. 1592), Tycho's astronomical correspondent — hence the \"long-standing familiarity\" Ellenberger invokes as grounds for hoping Tycho would settle in Hesse.</li><li><b>Peter Severinus.</b> <i>PETRO SEVERINO Medico</i> = <b>Peder Sørensen / Petrus Severinus</b> (1542–1602), royal physician (<i>Medicus</i>) at the Danish court and a leading Paracelsian. The letter relays — and Ellenberger himself rejects as <i>plane incredibile</i> (\"plainly incredible\") — an English rumour of a <i>lis</i> (\"quarrel/lawsuit\") between Tycho and Severinus; he then floats the alternative that Severinus may have worked against Tycho indirectly, <i>per hunc Cancellarium</i>, through the new Chancellor. <b>No silent sharpening:</b> the whole Severinus narrative is reported speech, governed by <i>asseverarent … referrent … ut referunt … fortassis</i> (\"they asserted … they reported … as they report … perhaps\"); the hedges are preserved and the rumour is <b>not</b> stated as fact.</li><li><b>The Chancellor and the fief.</b> <i>Cancellarium Regium … feudum … a FRIDERICO II felicis memoriae concessum</i> — the <b>Royal Chancellor of Denmark</b> (<i>hunc novellum Cancellarium</i>, \"this new Chancellor\": <b>Christian Friis</b> of Borreby, appointed 1596) is rumoured to have seized a fief granted to Tycho by <b>Frederick II</b> (\"of happy memory,\" d. 1588) and to have withdrawn the other supports (<i>adminicula</i>) for astronomy assigned by the King and the realm's magnates. This — the loss of Crown patronage under the new regime of Christian IV — Ellenberger and his informants find <i>verisimilius</i> (\"the more likely\") explanation for Tycho's departure.</li><li><b><i>per utramque Germaniam</b></i> — \"through both Germanies,\" i.e. Upper and Lower Germany (the customary early-modern division), not two distinct countries; rendered literally with the gloss.</li><li><b>Persons sending greetings.</b> <i>Salutem … a multis in Anglia … nominis tui observantissimis</i> — anonymous English admirers of Tycho's name; left unnamed in the source.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>LondiniAngliae, anno 1598, 15. Calend. Februarij</i> = \"At London in England, 1598, the 15th day before the Kalends of February.\" Reckoned inclusively (Kalends of February = 1 February as day 1), <i>15 Kal. Feb.</i> = <b>18 January</b>. This agrees with the dataset's catalogue label \"1598. 18. januar.\" England and Denmark both still kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1598; the date is therefore O.S. with no conversion required, and none is applied (protocol §3). <i>Anno 1598</i>: by the Julian historical year used here, with no Annunciation-style year-start complication arising in the text.</li><li><b><i>T. Magnif. addictissimus</b></i> = <i>Tuae Magnificentiae addictissimus</i>, \"most devoted to Your Magnificence,\" the subscription formula matched in register to the §5.5 honorific patterns.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Printed <i>PRAeSTANTISSIMO, MATTHAeO, clementissimini</i> (a printer's slip for <i>clementissimi</i>), <i>LondiniAngliae</i> (set without space) are reproduced as printed in the Source block; <i>clementissimini</i> is read as <i>clementissimi</i> in the translation, the printed form retained above per protocol §4.2.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the rumour-reporting hedges are preserved intact. Minor open points, none affecting sense: the printed <i>clementissimini</i> (read <i>clementissimi</i>); and the identity of the sender Henricus Ellenberger, \"Hassus, M.,\" who is named in the manuscripts but is otherwise an obscure figure (no biographical crux is resolved here beyond what the letter itself states).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only points are a declared printer's slip (</i>clementissimini<i>) + an obscure but non-load-bearing sender; sense + date secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.21-22 as page images (Ellenberger to Tycho, 18 Jan 1598). Collated opening/closing lines and every proper name (Matthaeus Roydon, Petrus Severinus, Fridericus II, Mauritius), date (15 Calend. Februarij = 18 Jan), and place (Hamburgk) against the printed text; source_text matches Dreyer word for word and the English renders it accurately throughout. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Univ. Bibl., cod. Monacensi Bibl. Univ. 693 fol.. Afskrift", "B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports learning from Matthew Roydon that Tycho has moved to Germany, expresses relief at sparing a journey to Denmark, and hopes Tycho may settle in Hesse.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980118001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980122001", "d": "1598-01-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 22 January 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy/<i> Afskrift</i>)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, COGNATO ATQUE AFFINI SUO UNICE DILECTO.</p><p>OPTIME a te factum, Exoptatissime mi OLIGERE, Cognate et Affinis desideratissime, quod hisce indicaris et tuae morae caussas, ac quod CRAGIUS noster Lubecam confestim transiturus sit, non autem huc, uti sperabamus, venturus. Utrumque vestrum satis excusatum habeo, cum neuter proprij adhuc sit arbitrij. Tu ergastulo et compedibus istis aureis solutus, brevi (quod etiam tibi summopere exopto) tibi ipsi redderis, quo virtuti et studijs, non aulicis indulgeas affanijs. Tumque te promissi fidem liberaturum nullus addubito. Interea quod nonnihil Iutizâris, cum et Iutus sis et in Iutia verseris, nolo Scanico supercilio exactius rimari. Adsis nobis felici sidere, quam primum adfueris, quod etiam, ut mature fiat, et cupio et opto. Iam enim teneris, quia sponte promisisti; nec nippizare licet Iuto in Scanicas transituro Nuptias, gentemque Scanicam constantem et dicti factique memorem aemulaturo: transplantatione quadam, ut fieri solet, quam etiam tibi non ingratam aut infelicem futurum, auguror atque confido.</p><p>Si res meae ullo modo id patientur, dabo operam, ut intra triduum vel quatriduum Lubecae adsim. Et si communis ille noster, dum per diem unum vel alterum isthic commoror, supervenerit, erit mihi gratissimum, quod ipsius fruar alloquio; utcunque iam antea praevidebam, non licere illi hac transire, vel mea forte de caussa. Quod ex alto rideo. Habeo alias Lubecae quod agam, satisque necessario. Nam res meae atque Instrumenta nuper eo appulerunt, quorum quaedam huc recepi, quaedam isthic in aedibus RANZOVIJ conservantur. Et nemo adest, qui discrete ordinare possit, quae et quomodo huc vehenda, ita ut nunc discohaerentia attulerint aurigae, non sine meo incommodo et temporis dispendio. Sunt et alia quaedam, quae isthic exequi constitui, quae etiam volens reieci in tempus tui vel ipsius adventus, ne peregre abessem, aliquo vestrum huc interea superveniente, utque aliquem ex vobis, si non utrunque, eo deducerem, prout res vestrae ferrent, absolutis interea quibusdam ex meis, quae prae manibus habeo, et indies urgeo. Vellem autem, ut tuum ad nos accessum ita disponeres, quo Lubeca redux hîc domi esse queam, ubi adveneris. Quod etiam ipsas, quas tibi isthic vestri Politici nectunt, moras, sponte effecturas praevideo. Promisit OTTO LINDENOW, se tibi certo traditurum centum illos Ioachimicos, quos mihi pro curru et tribus equis sibi venditis debet, quando me accederes. Rogo itaque, ut ante abitum eos ad te recipias et meo nomine persolutionis des chirographum, tecumque eosdem sumas. Cuperem etiam, mi amantissime Cognate atque Affinis, quod quingentos vel sexcentos Ioachimicos mihi uspiam, sub tuo tamen silenter nomine procurares, quos etiam tecum adferre queas. Restituet eos ad Pascha ESCHILLUS BILLEUS noster, quem satis integrae esse fidei, nosti, ex ijs, quos mihi debet, duobus millibus. Et si opus erit, curia mea Haffniensis interea pignoris loco erit, tam pro summa quam interesse; aut etiam aedes in orientali platea Haffniae SCHYRMANNI, quas hypothecae loco habeo pro 1000 Ioachimicis, qui illi ad festum Paschatis resignati sunt iam antea. Pendet vero domus quotannis 80 Ioachimicos, interesse pro millenis paulo maius solito, ut res satis tuta sit. Sin vero commode fieri nequeat, ut hac in parte nostris desiderijs morem geras, nolim te nimium sic occupari, aut ullas adventus tui remoras inde invenire. Caussa, cur id expetam (ut tibi secreto eam communicem, cui his maiora concredo) est, quod post paucas hebdomadas tres ex meis, quos intimos habeo, in diversa Germaniae loca ablegare decreverim: inter quos etiam filius natu grandior erit, qui nunc apud Administratorem per mensem abest, ipso sic volente. Et quoniam emissarij mei praecipuos Germaniae Magnates, adeoque ipsum Imperatorem quidam ex ijs meo nomine accessuri sunt, de rebus magni momenti, uti ipsemet facile coniectari possis, isthic acturi, et a me quaedam exhibituri, volo ut nihil ijs ad viaticum desit, quo minus omnia decenter et honorifice peragant. Cumque summa hîc sit annonae caritas, adeo ut triplo ferme pluris veneant, quam Rostochij, ubi 700 thaleros intra anni quadrantem insumpsi, facile aestimare poteris, quantam hîc, cum familia, quae 24 personas attingit, per dimidium fere annum exposuerim pecuniam. Summa vero illa maior, quam mecum accepi, apud Duces Megapolitanos foenori exposita est: uti nihil inde repetere liceat ante statutum tempus. Charissime OLIGERE, te in his facturum, quicquid per te praestari queat, omnino mihi persuadeo. Neque quid amplius peto aut desidero. Ego tibi vicissim in quovis officij genere exhibendo quam lubentissime morem geram.</p><p>Vale, resalutatus a meis omnibus quam officiosissime.<br>Dabantur raptim Wandesburgi die 22. Ianuarij, quo ipse Tabellarius tuas attulit, anni 98.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most excellent gentleman, Master Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, his kinsman and relative-by-marriage, uniquely beloved.</b></p><p>You did most kindly, my much-longed-for Holger, dearest of kinsmen and relatives, in informing me by these [lines] both of the reasons for your delay, and that our friend Cragius is to pass on directly to Lübeck and is not, as we had hoped, going to come here. I hold each of you sufficiently excused, since neither of you is as yet his own master. You, once released from that workhouse and those golden fetters, will shortly (which indeed I most earnestly wish for you) be restored to your own self, so that you may give yourself to virtue and to studies, not to courtly vexations. And I have no doubt that you will then redeem the pledge of your promise. Meanwhile, that you play the Jutlander somewhat — since you are both a Jute and dwelling in Jutland — I will not scrutinise too exactly with a Scanian's raised eyebrow. Be present with us under a happy star as soon as you can be present, which I both desire and wish to come about in good time. For you are now bound, since you promised of your own accord; nor is it permitted to a Jute, who is about to cross over into a Scanian marriage and to emulate the Scanian people — steadfast and mindful of word and deed — to <i>nippizare</i> [shilly-shally / haggle]: by a certain transplanting, as is wont to happen, which I forecast and trust will prove neither unwelcome nor unhappy for you.</p><p>If my affairs will in any way allow it, I shall take pains to be at Lübeck within three or four days. And if that common [friend] of ours should turn up while I tarry there a day or two, it will be most welcome to me, that I may enjoy his conversation; although I already foresaw beforehand that he is not permitted to pass this way, perhaps even on my account. At which I laugh from on high. I have, besides, business to do at Lübeck, and that necessarily enough. For my goods and instruments have lately put in there, some of which I have received here, while some are kept there in Rantzau's house. And there is no one at hand who can judiciously arrange what is to be carried here and in what manner, with the result that the carters have now brought [things] disjointedly, not without inconvenience to me and waste of time. There are also certain other matters which I have resolved to carry out there, which too I have willingly put off to the time of your coming, or his, lest I be away abroad while one of you turned up here in the meantime — and so that I might conduct one of you, if not both, thither, as your affairs should allow, certain of my own [tasks] being finished meanwhile, which I have in hand and press on with daily. But I should wish you to so arrange your coming to us that, on my return from Lübeck, I may be here at home when you arrive. Which I also foresee that the very delays your politicians are weaving for you there will of themselves bring about. Otto Lindenow has promised that he will certainly deliver to you those hundred Joachimsthaler which he owes me for a carriage and three horses sold to him, when you come to me. I ask, therefore, that before [your] departure you take them in [hand] and, in my name, give a written acknowledgment of payment, and bring the same with you. I could also wish, my most loving kinsman and relative, that you would procure for me somewhere five or six hundred Joachimsthaler — under your own name, though, and quietly — which you might likewise bring with you. Our [friend] Eskil Bille will repay them by Easter — a man, as you know, of fully sound credit — out of the two thousand which he owes me. And if there be need, my Copenhagen courtyard-house [<i>curia</i>] will meanwhile stand in the place of a pledge, both for the principal and for the interest; or else Schyrmann's house in the eastern street of Copenhagen, which I hold by way of mortgage for 1,000 Joachimsthaler, that were already before this assigned over to him at the feast of Easter. The house in fact pays 80 Joachimsthaler yearly, the interest on the thousand being a little more than the usual, so that the matter is secure enough. But if it cannot conveniently be done that you gratify our wishes in this part, I should not want you to be too much taken up in this way, or to find therein any delays to your coming. The reason why I seek it (that I may share it with you in secret, to whom I entrust greater things than these) is that, after a few weeks, I have resolved to dispatch three of my [men], whom I count my intimates, to different places in Germany — among whom my elder-born son too will be, who is now away for a month at the Administrator's [court], he himself so willing it. And since my emissaries are to approach the chief magnates of Germany, and indeed the Emperor himself — certain of them in my name — there to treat of matters of great moment, as you may yourself easily conjecture, and to present certain things from me, I want nothing of their travelling-money to be lacking, that they may carry everything out fittingly and honourably. And since the dearth of provisions here is so great that they sell for nearly three times more than at Rostock — where I spent 700 thalers within a quarter of a year — you will easily be able to reckon how much money I have laid out here, with a household that reaches 24 persons, over nearly half a year. But that larger sum which I brought with me has been put out at interest with the Dukes of Mecklenburg, so that nothing may be recalled from there before the appointed time. Dearest Holger, I am wholly persuaded that you will do in these matters whatever can be performed by you. Nor do I ask or desire anything further. I, in my turn, will most gladly gratify you in showing every kind of good office.</p><p>Farewell, greeted again in return by all my [household] most dutifully.<br>Given hastily at Wandsbek on the 22nd day of January — the very [day] on which the courier brought your [letter] — of the year [15]98.<br>Your TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from his self-imposed exile at <b>Wandsbek</b> (<i>Wandesburgum</i>), the castle near Hamburg belonging to Heinrich Rantzau, where Tycho settled in 1597 after quitting Denmark. This is the period of the search for a new patron that culminated in the move to the Imperial court of Rudolf II; the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (Wandsbek, 1598) was being readied for the press, its foreword to Rudolf II dated 31 December 1597 (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius de Rosenholm</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (1574–1642), \"the Learned,\" Danish nobleman of Rosenholm, later a councillor of state; here Tycho's young kinsman, addressed throughout as <i>cognatus atque affinis</i> (kinsman by blood and by marriage). He was about to marry into a Scanian family (see the Jute/Scanian banter), and would assist Tycho at the Wandsbek lunar-eclipse observation later in 1598.</li><li><b><i>cognatus atque affinis</b></i> — \"kinsman and relative-by-marriage\": <i>cognatus</i> = a blood relation, <i>affinis</i> = a connection by marriage; both kept distinct.</li><li><b><i>Cragius noster</b></i> — \"our Cragius\": most probably <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, 1550–1602), the Danish humanist historian and diplomat; the identification is not in the letter itself and is flagged as editorial.</li><li><b><i>communis ille noster</b></i> — \"that common [friend] of ours,\" left unnamed in the source (Tycho is being discreet); rendered with the bracketed gloss, the referent not asserted.</li><li><b><i>ergastulo et compedibus istis aureis</b></i> — \"that workhouse and those golden fetters\": a metaphor (cf. the 1571 Wolf letter's <i>ergastulum</i>) for Rosenkrantz's court service, the \"golden fetters\" being the gilded constraint of attendance at court. <i>ergastulum</i> = a slaves' workhouse/prison.</li><li><b><i>aulicis affanijs</b></i> — \"courtly vexations / fuss\"; <i>affania(e)</i> is a late/rare word for trifling annoyances or idle talk.</li><li><b>Jute / Scanian banter.</b> <i>Iutizâris … Iutus … in Iutia … Scanico supercilio … Iuto … in Scanicas … Nuptias … gentemque Scanicam … nippizare</i> — a sustained play on regional identity: Rosenkrantz is a <b>Jute</b> (from Jutland) about to cross over into a <b>Scanian</b> marriage (Scania, then Danish, was Tycho's own home region). Tycho teases that Rosenkrantz \"plays the Jutlander\" (<i>Iutizaris</i>, a coinage = behaves Jute-fashion, i.e. dawdles/temporises), which he, with a \"Scanian's raised eyebrow\" (<i>Scanico supercilio</i>), will overlook.</li><li><b>Crux: <i>nippizare</i>.</b> \"nor is it permitted to a Jute … to <i>nippizare</i>.\" <i>nippizare</i> is a rare/coined verb of uncertain sense — most plausibly \"to be stingy / haggle / shilly-shally,\" continuing the regional jest (a Jute must not haggle or hang back when crossing into a Scanian marriage). The Source block keeps the printed form; the translation keeps <i>nippizare</i> italicised with a bracketed gloss [shilly-shally / haggle] rather than committing to one sense. <b>Flagged, not smoothed.</b></li><li><b><i>in aedibus Ranzovij</b></i> — \"in Rantzau's house\": <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henrik Rantzau, 1526–1598), the Holstein statesman and humanist, Tycho's host-patron at Wandsbek; his town establishment at Lübeck stored part of Tycho's instruments in transit.</li><li><b><i>Ioachimici</b></i> — <b>Joachimsthaler</b> (the silver <i>thaler</i> struck at Joachimsthal/Jáchymov); rendered \"Joachimsthaler.\" Distinguished in the same letter from plain <i>thaleri</i> (\"thalers,\" used of the Rostock expenditure).</li><li><b><i>Otto Lindenow</b></i> — <b>Otte Lindenow</b>, a Danish nobleman, Tycho's debtor for \"a carriage and three horses.\"</li><li><b><i>chirographum</b></i> — a hand-written bond / acknowledgment of payment (a receipt under one's own hand); kept as \"written acknowledgment.\"</li><li><b><i>Eschillus Billeus noster</b></i> — <b>Eskil Bille</b>, a Danish nobleman, Tycho's debtor \"for two thousand [Joachimsthaler],\" vouched for as a man of sound credit (<i>integrae fidei</i>).</li><li><b><i>curia mea Haffniensis</b></i> — \"my Copenhagen courtyard-house\": <i>curia</i> here = a town manor/courtyard property (<i>Hafnia</i> = <b>Copenhagen</b>, protocol §5.7), offered as security (<i>pignoris loco</i>, \"in the place of a pledge\").</li><li><b><i>aedes … Schyrmanni … hypothecae loco … ad festum Paschatis resignati</b></i> — Schyrmann's house in the \"eastern street\" of Copenhagen, held by Tycho <i>by way of mortgage</i> (<i>hypotheca</i>) for 1,000 Joachimsthaler, \"assigned over\" (<i>resignati</i>) at Easter. The financial mechanics (annual yield of 80 Joachimsthaler; interest \"a little more than usual\") are rendered as written; <i>Schyrmann</i> unidentified beyond the name.</li><li><b><i>filius natu grandior … apud Administratorem</b></i> — Tycho's <b>elder son</b> (Tyge Brahe the Younger, b. 1581), away \"at the Administrator's\" — i.e. at the court of an <i>Administrator</i> (the holder of a secularised prince-bishopric or regency); the specific office is not named in the letter, so left as \"the Administrator's [court].\" <b>Flagged</b> as under-determined.</li><li><b><i>emissarij … praecipuos Germaniae Magnates, adeoque ipsum Imperatorem … accessuri … de rebus magni momenti</b></i> — Tycho's confidential disclosure that he is about to send intimates (including his elder son) to the leading <b>magnates of Germany</b> and to <b>the Emperor himself</b> (Rudolf II) \"on matters of great moment\": the patronage embassy that preceded Tycho's move to the Imperial court. The phrase <i>ut tibi secreto eam communicem</i> (\"that I may share it with you in secret\") marks the confidence; preserved.</li><li><b><i>Duces Megapolitanos</b></i> — the <b>Dukes of Mecklenburg</b> (<i>Megapolis</i> = Mecklenburg, a learned Graecism on <i>megalē polis</i> ≈ \"great town\", folk-etymology of Mecklenburg); with whom Tycho's reserve capital was \"put out at interest\" (<i>foenori exposita</i>).</li><li><b><i>Rostochium</b></i> — <b>Rostock</b> (protocol §5.7); the comparison point for living costs (\"700 thalers within a quarter of a year\").</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Salutation in the dative-of-address (titulature), per §5.5. <i>Vale</i> = \"Farewell.\" <i>resalutatus a meis omnibus</i> = \"greeted again in return by all my [household].\" <i>manu propria</i> = \"with his own hand\" (autograph subscription).</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Dabantur raptim Wandesburgi die 22. Ianuarij … anni 98</i> = \"Given hastily at <b>Wandsbek</b>, 22 January … of the year [15]98\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Denmark and the Lutheran north kept the Julian calendar until 1700). The dataset <i>label</i> (\"1598. 22. januar. Wandsburg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy. The clause <i>quo ipse Tabellarius tuas attulit</i> (\"the very day on which the courier brought your [letter]\") dates Tycho's reply to the day of receipt; preserved.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is well preserved and the sense is secure throughout, with the financial and patronage passages translated as written. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the rare/coined verb <b><i>nippizare</b></i>, of uncertain precise force, kept untranslated with a bracketed gloss [C-grade locally]; and (2) the under-specified <b><i>Administrator</b></i> at whose court Tycho's elder son was staying. The identification of <i>Cragius</i> as Niels Krag and of <i>communis ille noster</i> (left unnamed) are editorial and so marked.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening address, both paragraphs, all names/numbers/dates, closing) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.22-25 read as page images. source_text matches Dreyer's print word-for-word (long-s/ae typographic variants only); published_english renders every clause, name (Cragius, Otto Lindenow, Eschillus Billeus, Schyrmanni), and sum (100 / 500-600 / 1000 / 80 / 700 Joachimsthaler-thalers, the 2000-Joachimsthaler debt, the 24-person household) correctly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15971226001", "next": "brahe-t_15980214001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Accepts the excuses of Rosenkrantz and Cragius for not visiting, urges Holger to free himself from court obligations and return to virtue and learning.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980122001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980125001", "d": "1598-01-25", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg", "ck": "Joachim Friedrich, kurfyrste af Brandenburg", "ck2": "Christoffer Valkendorf, Christian Friis", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Berlin", "t": "Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg → Christoffer Valkendorf and Christian Friis — Cölln an der Spree, 25 January 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin code-switches) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(XIV, Epistolae et Acta) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN DEN HOFMEISTER VNDT CANTZLER.</p><p>VNSERN gunstigen gruss zu uor. Ehrenuehster besonder lieber. Es hatt der ehrenuehste Vnser lieber besonder Tycho Brahe Vns vmb intercession an die königliche w: zue Dennemarcken, Vnsern freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten hern vattern vndt sohn, wie auch an Vnsere freundtliche vielgeliebte tochter die könnigin, in seinen sachen vndterthenigst angehaltten. Weil Wir ihm denn füglich solches nicht haben abschlagen können, so haben Wir ihm wie beyliegendt an jhr k: w: allerseits geschrieben, vndt weil Wir wissen, daß jhr viel gutes bey den sachen ttuen könnet, so begehren Wir gantz gnedigst, ihr wollet so viel sich jmmer fugen wil deß beste vorwenden helfen, damitt er vonn jhr k:w: mitt guetter resolution möge versehen werden, wie Wir denn inn gnaden nicht zweiffeltt, jhr werdet Vnsern halben den vertrawen nach an euch nichts erwinden laßen. Deßselbe wollen Wir mitt allen gnedigen vndt gönstigen willen, darmitt Wir euch ohne deß gewogen, hienwiederumb verschulden.</p><p>Datum Cölln an der Sprew, den 25. januarij anno 98.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Steward and the Chancellor.</b></p><p>Our gracious greeting beforehand. Honourable [and] particularly dear [one]. The honourable, Our dear and particular Tycho Brahe has most humbly applied to Us for [Our] intercession with the Royal Majesties of Denmark — Our friendly, heartily-beloved lord father and son, and likewise with Our friendly, dearly-beloved daughter the Queen — in his affairs. Since We could not in fairness refuse him this, We have accordingly written, as enclosed, to their Royal Majesties one and all; and since We know that you can do much good in these affairs, We most graciously desire that you, so far as ever may be fitting, help to put forward [his] best [interest], so that he may be furnished by their Royal Majesties with a good resolution — even as We do not, in [Our] grace, doubt that you, for Our sake [and] in keeping with the trust [reposed in you], will let nothing be wanting on your part. The same We will requite again with all the gracious and favourable goodwill wherewith We are in any case well-disposed toward you.</p><p>Given at Cölln an der Spree, the 25th of January, in the year [15]98.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Issuer.</b> <i>Joachim Friedrich</i> = <b>Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg</b> (1546–1608; Elector from 1598, in the year of this letter). The royal \"We\" (<i>Wir / Vns</i>) and the gracious-superior register (<i>gnedigst, in gnaden</i>) are those of a reigning prince addressing officers of another crown. Title rendered \"Elector of Brandenburg\" in the header; the text itself names no title, opening directly with the address-line.</li><li><b>Addressees.</b> <i>Der Hofmeister vndt Cantzler</i> = the two senior officers of the Danish crown: the <b>Hofmeister</b> (Rigshofmester / Lord High Steward), at this date <b>Christoffer Valkendorf</b> (1525–1601), and the <b>Cantzler</b> (Chancellor), <b>Christian Friis af Borreby</b> (1556–1616). The dataset names both as recipients; the letter is a single missive addressed jointly to the two offices (the singular <i>Ehrenuehster besonder lieber</i>, \"particularly dear one,\" is the chancery address-formula and does not by itself decide whether it was issued in one copy or two — flagged, not resolved). The surviving witness is a later copy in Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>.</li><li><b>Subject — Tycho.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe … vmb intercession … in seinen sachen vndterthenigst angehaltten</i> — Tycho, by then estranged from the Danish court and on the point of leaving Denmark (he quit Hven in 1597 and was abroad — Rostock, then Wandsbek — in 1597–98), had asked the Elector to intercede with the Danish crown \"in his affairs.\" This is one of the intercession letters Tycho solicited from German princes during his self-imposed exile, seeking a favourable settlement (restoration of favour / his estates / pension) before his eventual move to the Imperial court.</li><li><b>The kinship formulae.</b> <i>die königliche w: zue Dennemarcken … Vnsern freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten hern vattern vndt sohn … Vnsere freundtliche vielgeliebte tochter die könnigin</i> — \"the Royal Majesties of Denmark … Our friendly, heartily-beloved lord father and son … Our dearly-beloved daughter the Queen.\" These are conventional princely courtesy-kinship terms (\"father,\" \"son,\" \"daughter\") expressing the dynastic web binding the Brandenburg and Danish-Oldenburg houses, <b>not</b> literal immediate-family relations; the plural <i>die königliche w: … allerseits</i> (\"their Royal Majesties … one and all\") shows the addressees are collective. The translation keeps the courtesy terms as written and flags their conventional force here rather than asserting a specific genealogy. <i>königliche w:</i> = <i>königliche Würden</i> (Royal Majesties / Royal Dignities), the abbreviation expanded in sense.</li><li><b>Latin / chancery code-switches.</b> <i>intercession</i> (intercession, mediation), <i>resolution</i> (a formal resolution / decision — <i>mitt guetter resolution möge versehen werden</i>, \"be furnished with a good resolution\"), and <i>Datum</i> (Given) are Latin code-switches embedded in the German, per protocol §2; kept and glossed.</li><li><b>Negations (counted, per the legal-force rule).</b> Three negations stand in the source and exactly three in the translation: (1) <i>nicht … abschlagen können</i> — \"could not refuse\"; (2) <i>nicht zweiffeltt</i> — \"do not doubt\"; (3) <i>nichts erwinden laßen</i> — \"let nothing be wanting.\" None reverses a command; all are courtesy-register assurances. The clause <i>jhr werdet … an euch nichts erwinden laßen</i> is the standard chancery litotes \"you will let nothing be lacking on your part\" (i.e. you will do your utmost) — rendered as such, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, c. 1598).</b> <i>zu uor</i> = <i>zuvor</i>; <i>Ehrenuehster</i> = <i>Ehrenfester</i> (\"Honourable,\" the formal address-epithet); <i>besonder lieber</i> = <i>besonderer lieber</i>; <i>vmb</i> = <i>um</i>; <i>ttuen</i> = <i>tun</i> (with the scribe's doubled initial); <i>vorwenden</i> = <i>vorwenden</i> (\"put forward, employ on [someone's] behalf\"); <i>fugen</i> = <i>fügen</i> (\"be fitting\"); <i>hienwiederumb</i> = <i>hinwiederum</i> (\"in return\"); <i>verschulden</i> = <i>verschulden</i> (here \"requite, deserve [your goodwill] in return,\" not \"incur guilt/debt\"); <i>gewogen</i> = \"well-disposed.\" <i>Sprew</i> / <i>Spree</i>; <i>Cölln an der Spree</i> is the Berlin sister-town and seat of the Brandenburg residence (today part of Berlin).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Cölln an der Sprew, den 25. januarij anno 98</i> = \"Given at <b>Cölln an der Spree</b>, 25 January [15]98.\" Brandenburg, as a Lutheran territory, kept the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1598 (the Protestant estates of the Empire did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700); the date is therefore O.S., consistent with the Danish addressees, who likewise used Old Style. No in-letter date discrepancy. The dataset <i>label</i> gives the same date (25 January 1598, Cölln an der Spree); header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the negations are exactly preserved; the soft points, flagged rather than smoothed, are (i) the conventional courtesy-kinship terms <i>vattern / sohn / tochter die könnigin</i>, kept literal with their dynastic-courtesy force noted, and (ii) whether the joint address to <i>Hofmeister vndt Cantzler</i> was issued as one missive or two.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — residue is conventional courtesy-kinship (explained) + a non-textual one/two-copies question; negations exact.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO XIV pp.131-132, where it is printed complete (no. 140). German matches source_text exactly. The addressees (Hofmeister/Cantzler = Valkendorf/Friis), the intercession request to the Danish king and queen, and the date (Cölln an der Spree, 25 January [15]98) are all rendered faithfully. The ambiguous 'hern vattern vndt sohn' formula is preserved literally in English rather than resolved or altered, which is the correct choice given the German itself is ambiguous. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks the Danish Steward and Chancellor to support Tycho Brahe with the king, noting the Elector has written separately to the royal family on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980125001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980125002", "d": "1598-01-25", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg", "ck": "Joachim Friedrich, kurfyrste af Brandenburg", "ck2": "Christian 4.", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Berlin", "t": "Joachim Friedrich, Elector of Brandenburg → King Christian IV of Denmark — Cölln an der Spree, 25 January 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with a Latin rubric) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 21 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM ELECTORIS<br>BRANDENBURGENSIS AD REGEM<br>DANIÆ.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER könig, E:K:W: seindt Vnsere freundtwillige dienste vndt waß Wir<br>der vatterlichen verwandtnuß nach viel mehr ehr, liebs vndt guts vermögen,<br>allzeit zuuor. Freundtlicher hertzvielgeliebter herr vetter vndt sohn, E:K:W:<br>geruhen freundtlich, jhr den einschluß vorlesßen zue laßen, wes gestalt Wir von<br>den ehrenuehsten Vnsern lieben besondern Tycho Brahen vmb intercessionschrifft<br>an E:K:W: bittlich angelanget vndt ersuchet worden.</p><p>Ob Wir nuhn wohl nicht gemeinet E:K:W: mitt etwas, so derselben zue<br>wieder sein möchte, verdrisßlich zue sein, dahero Wir denn auch fest hierundter<br>(sintemahln was ausser seinen Tycho Brahens bericht, wie es vmb seine sachen<br>gewandt, eigenlich nichts bewust, zue dem auch E:K:W: Vns dermasßen bekandt, das<br>sie auch ohne vnsere vorschrifft einen jeden was billich wiederfahren zue lasßen<br>geneigt) bedenckens getragen, dannoch aber vndt weil Wir sein suchen, bitten<br>vndt erbietten gleichwohl nicht vnbillich vermergken, zue dem auch bey E:K:W:<br>nicht vernommen, das sie ihme mitt vngnaden gewogen sein soltten, so haben Wir<br>endtlich seinen vndterthenigen suchen statt geben, vndt die gebetene<br>vorbittschrifft fuglich nicht verweigern mögen. Ersuchen dem nach E:K:W: hier<br>mitt ganz dienst- vndt freundtlich bittendt, E:K:W: solches nicht allein von Vnß<br>nicht vbel aufnehmen, sondern auch sein Tycho Brahens wegkziehen auß Dennemargk<br>im keinen vngnaden vermergken vndt sein gnedigster<br>könig vndt herr sein vndt bleiben, auch nach dero gelegenheitt die Verordnung<br>thuen wollen, daß ihme, seinen ferner vndterthenigst suchen vndt bitten nach,<br>sowoll der hienwegnehmung seiner vff der insul zue Vranenburgk noch habenden<br>jnstrument vndt waß ihm aldar mehr zueständig sein möchte, vndt dann erstettung<br>seines angezogenen Verlages, als auch der perpetuirten fundation halben zue<br>nutzlicher werkstellung deß astronomischen studij, gewelfahrt, vndt er also<br>hierinn, inn einem vndt dem andern, so viel muglich dieser Vnser, ihm zwar zue<br>gnaden aber E:K:W: gar nicht zue einigen vorgrifft, gemeinten intercession<br>seiner geschöpften hofnung nach empfindtlich geniessen möge. Das wirdt<br>er sich vngezweiffelt nach höchsten seinem vermögen zuuordienen bevleisßigen,<br>vndt Wir seindt E:K:W: zu allen behaglichen freundtwilligen diensten jederzeitt<br>geflisßen.</p><p>Datum Cölln an der Sprew, den 25. januarij anno 98.</p><p>V:G:g: Jochim Friederich<br>churfurst zue Brandenburgk.</p><p>Mutatis mutandis an die königin, dasß jhr k: w: bey derselben herrn gemahl<br>deß beste inn gueten andtwort verwenden wolle.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of a letter of the Elector of Brandenburg to the King of Denmark.</b></p><p>Most Serene King, [be assured of] our amicable services beforehand at all times, and of whatever, by reason of our paternal kinship, we are able [to do] far more in honour, love, and good. Most amicable, dearly-beloved lord cousin and son: may it please Your Royal Majesty graciously to have the enclosure read out to her, [as to] in what manner we have been petitioned and entreated by the honourable, our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe for a letter of intercession to Your Royal Majesty.</p><p>Now, although we were indeed not minded to be irksome to Your Royal Majesty with anything that might be contrary to her — wherefore we also bore firm hesitation in this matter (inasmuch as, apart from his, Tycho Brahe's, [own] report of how things stand with his affairs, [we] are in truth aware of nothing; and besides, Your Royal Majesty is so [well] known to us that she is inclined, even without our recommendation, to let everyone have done to him what is fair) — yet nonetheless, and because we for our part do not deem his application, request, and offer unfair, and because we have moreover not heard from Your Royal Majesty that she should be disposed toward him with disfavour, we have at length given place to his humble application, and could not in propriety refuse the requested letter of intercession. Accordingly we entreat Your Royal Majesty herewith, requesting in all dutiful and amicable wise, that Your Royal Majesty not only would not take such a thing ill of us, but would also not regard his — Tycho Brahe's — departure out of Denmark with any disfavour, and would be and remain his most gracious king and lord, and would also, as her opportunity allows, make the ordinance that he — according to his further most-humble application and request — may, both as regards the removal of his instruments still remaining on the island at Uraniborg, and whatever else there might further belong to him there, and then [as regards] the reimbursement of his alleged outlay [Verlag], as well as on account of the perpetuated foundation for the profitable carrying-out of the astronomical study, be furthered; and that he thus herein, in the one matter and the other, may, so far as possible, palpably enjoy, according to the hope he has conceived, this our intended intercession — [made] indeed to his favour, but in no way to any prejudice of Your Royal Majesty. This he will undoubtedly strive to deserve to the utmost of his ability; and we are at all times zealous for Your Royal Majesty in every agreeable and amicable service.</p><p>Given at Cölln an der Spree, the 25th of January, in the year [15]98.<br>By the grace of God, Joachim Friedrich,<br>Elector of Brandenburg. With the necessary changes, [the like was written] to the Queen: that Her Royal Highness would be pleased to employ her best [endeavour] with her lord consort toward a favourable answer.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Joachim Friedrich, Elector (Kurfürst) of Brandenburg</b> (1546–1608; Elector from January 1598). He addresses the young Danish king as <i>herr vetter vndt sohn</i> (\"lord cousin and son\"), the standard inter-dynastic courtesy register; the <i>vatterlichen verwandtnuß</i> (\"paternal kinship\") reflects the Brandenburg–Denmark marriage ties (Joachim Friedrich's family was allied to the Danish house; Christian IV's sister Anna would later marry into the line).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648, ruling in his own right from 1596). The accompanying same-day letter from the same Elector to the Danish <i>Hofmeister</i> and <i>Kanzler</i> (Christoffer Valkendorf and Christian Friis) survives in the same correspondence series; this is the parallel petition to the king himself.</li><li><b>Document type &amp; rubric.</b> The Latin heading <i>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM ELECTORIS BRANDENBURGENSIS AD REGEM DANIAe</i> (\"Copy of a letter of the Elector of Brandenburg to the King of Denmark\") marks the text as a register/file copy (the witness is Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>, an 18th-c. transcription, hence \"Afskrift\"). The code-switch into Latin for the rubric over a German body is preserved per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Historical context.</b> Tycho left Denmark in the spring of 1597 and by January 1598 was at Wandsbek (in the territory of Heinrich Rantzau), where he was completing the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598). This letter is an intercession seeking three concessions from the crown: (1) the <b>removal of the instruments still on Hven</b> (<i>hienwegnehmung seiner vff der insul zue Vranenburgk noch habenden jnstrument</i>); (2) the <b>reimbursement of his outlay</b> (<i>erstettung seines angezogenen Verlages</i>); and (3) the honouring of <b>the perpetuated foundation</b> for his astronomical work (<i>perpetuirten fundation … deß astronomischen studij</i>).</li><li><b>Honorific abbreviations.</b> <i>E:K:W:</i> = <i>Eurer Königlichen Würden</i> (\"Your Royal Majesty / Dignity\"), the courtesy title used throughout; rendered \"Your Royal Majesty.\" <i>V:G:g:</i> = <i>Von Gottes gnaden</i> (\"By the grace of God\"), the standard subscription style for a sovereign prince. The princely <i>Wir</i> and the address forms are kept in the elevated register (protocol §2, §5.5).</li><li><b>Negations (flagged for legal/courtesy force, protocol critical rule).</b> The central period turns on a chain of deliberate litotes, all reproduced exactly: <i>nicht gemeinet … verdrisßlich zue sein</i> (\"not minded to be irksome\"); <i>nicht vnbillich vermergken</i> (\"do not deem unfair\"); <i>nicht vernommen, das sie ihme mitt vngnaden gewogen</i> (\"not heard that she should be disposed toward him with disfavour\"); <i>fuglich nicht verweigern mögen</i> (\"could not in propriety refuse\"); and the paired <i>nicht allein … nicht vbel aufnehmen, sondern auch … im keinen vngnaden vermergken</i> (\"not only would not take it ill … but would also not regard [it] with any disfavour\"). Six distinct negating turns in all; none added, none dropped. The closing qualifier <i>ihm zwar zue gnaden aber E:K:W: gar nicht zue einigen vorgrifft</i> (\"indeed to his favour, but in no way to any prejudice of Your Royal Majesty\") is the diplomatic safeguard and is rendered with its full hedge.</li><li><b>Lexical notes.</b></li><li><i>intercessionschrifft / vorbittschrifft</i> — \"letter of intercession\" (a formal letter of recommendation on a third party's behalf); kept consistent with the rubric and body.</li><li><i>angezogenen Verlages</i> — <i>Verlag</i> here = \"outlay, expenditure advanced\" (the money Tycho laid out, not the modern \"publishing house\"); <i>angezogen</i> = \"alleged, cited, brought forward.\" Rendered \"his alleged outlay [Verlag],\" with the German kept in brackets, since the elector reports Tycho's claim without himself vouching for the amount (he has just said he knows the affair only <i>ausser seinen Tycho Brahens bericht</i> — \"apart from Tycho Brahe's [own] report\").</li><li><i>perpetuirten fundation</i> — \"the perpetuated foundation\": the endowment/foundation (Latinate <i>fundatio</i>) for the support of Tycho's astronomical studies, which Tycho sought to have continued (\"perpetuated\"); rendered literally, the institutional sense flagged rather than narrowed.</li><li><i>werkstellung</i> (= <i>Werkstelligung</i>) — \"carrying-out, execution\"; here <i>zue nutzlicher werkstellung deß astronomischen studij</i> = \"for the profitable carrying-out of the astronomical study.\"</li><li><i>empfindtlich geniessen</i> — \"palpably / perceptibly enjoy [the benefit of],\" i.e. that Tycho actually feel the good effect of the intercession; rendered \"palpably enjoy.\"</li><li><i>geruhen</i> — \"may it please / deign\"; <i>einschluß</i> — \"the enclosure\" (the appended document Tycho's case rested on).</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, 1598).</b> Characteristic <i>v/u</i> and <i>w</i> spellings and chancery doublings: <i>vndt</i> = <i>und</i>; <i>zuuor</i> = <i>zuvor</i>; <i>vermergken</i> = <i>vermerken</i>; <i>vnbillich</i> = <i>unbillig</i>; <i>vff</i> = <i>auf</i>; <i>jnstrument</i> = <i>Instrument(e)</i>; <i>wegkziehen</i> = <i>wegziehen</i>; <i>hienwegnehmung</i> = <i>Hinwegnehmung</i>; <i>erstettung</i> = <i>Erstattung</i>; <i>vorgrifff</i> (<i>vorgrifft</i>) = <i>Vorgriff</i> (\"prejudice, encroachment\"); <i>Sprew</i> = <i>Spree</i>. <i>Cölln an der Spree</i> is the twin town of Berlin (the electoral residence), not Cologne on the Rhine.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Cölln an der Sprew, den 25. januarij anno 98</i> = \"Given at Cölln an der Spree, 25 January [15]98.\" Denmark and Brandenburg both kept the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> at this date, so the dateline is O.S.; no conversion is made (protocol §3). The header date matches the dateline as written; the dataset <i>label</i> (\"1598. 25. januar. Cölln an der Spree\") agrees, so no discrepancy.</li><li><b>A parallel letter to the Queen.</b> The docket confirms that a second, adapted copy of this same intercession ('mutatis mutandis') was sent to 'the Queen' — Anne Catherine of Brandenburg, the Elector's own daughter, married to King Christian IV at Haderslevhus Castle in November 1597 — asking her to use her influence with 'her lord consort' (Christian IV) toward a favourable reply to Tycho's petition.</li><li><b>Corroboration.</b> John Robert Christianson, <i>Tycho Brahe and the Measure of the Heavens</i> (2020), records that after the Brandenburg-Danish wedding at Haderslevhus, Elector Joachim Friedrich and Electress Catherine met Tycho, received him warmly, and 'agreed to write to Queen Anna Catherine and King Christian IV on his behalf' — independently confirming the two-track intercession (King and Queen) this recovered docket describes.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the negation-chain is reproduced verbatim; the one soft point is the exact institutional sense of <i>perpetuirten fundation</i> (the specific endowment Tycho wished continued) and the precise scope of <i>angezogenen Verlages</i>, both flagged above and kept close to the German rather than narrowed to a single modern equivalent.</p><p>High confidence on sense and identification, medium on exact wording. The Early New High German abbreviation 'k: w:' (for '[ihre] königliche W[eisheit/ürden]', roughly 'Her Royal Highness') is expanded by convention rather than spelled out in the source, so the English supplies the honorific in full; this is flagged with brackets, as is 'endeavour' for the unspecified 'deß beste' ('the best [of her effort]'). The substance — a companion letter urging Queen Anne Catherine to intercede with Christian IV — is independently and strongly corroborated by Christianson's modern biography, which describes exactly this two-track appeal arising from the November 1597 wedding.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.131-133 as page images. Letter no.141 ('EXEMPLUM LITERARUM ELECTORIS BRANDENBURGENSIS AD REGEM DANIAE') runs from the bottom of p.131 through the top of p.132, distinct from the preceding letter no.140 to the Hofmeister/Cantzler (not part of this packet's source_text, correctly excluded). Collated the full intercession request for Tycho Brahe, the Uraniborg-instruments/reimbursement clause, the closing date and signature (25 januarij anno 98, Jochim Friederich), and the 'mutatis mutandis' note to the Queen. source_text matches Dreyer; published_english correctly and consistently renders 'E:K:W:' (Euer Koenigliche Wuerde) with feminine pronouns throughout, reflecting the grammatical gender of 'Wuerde' rather than an error. No fabrication, omission, or flipped subject found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Intercedes on Tycho Brahe's behalf with Christian IV, noting the Elector knows only Tycho's own account and expects the king to act fairly regardless.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980125002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980204001", "d": "1598-02-04", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Katharina, Electress of Brandenburg", "ck": "Katharina, kurfyrstinde af Brandenburg", "ck2": "Christian 4.", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Berlin", "t": "Katharina, Electress of Brandenburg → King Christian IV — Cölln an der Spree, 4 February 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / chancery) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / transcript)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGEN FÜRSTEN, HERN CHRISTIANO DEM VIERDTEN, ZU DENNEMARGKEN, NORDWEGEN, DER WENDEN VNDT GOTHEN KÖNNIGE, HERTZOGEN ZU SCHLESSWIGK, HOLSTEIN, DER STORMAREN VNDT DITMARSCHEN, GRAFFEN ZUE OLDENBURG VNDT DELMENHORST, UNSERM FREUNDTLICHEM VIELGELIEBTEN HERRN SOHN, ZU JHR: K: W: HÄNDEN.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER könnig, Euer K: W: sey Vnser diensthaffter ehrengruss vndt was Wir der mutterlichen vervandtnuss nach viel mehr leibs vnd guts vermugen, zuuohrn. Freundtlicher hochvielgeliebter herr vetter, sohn, vndt bruder, Wir wollen zu dem Allmechtigen vngezweiffelt hoffen, das E:K:W: sambt dero vielgeliebter gemahlin, Vnser hertzlieben tochter, noch bey guetter leibsgesundheitt vndt allem königlichen Wohlstände gnediglich erhalten werden, wie Wir denn derselben königlichen w:, vndt J:L: solches auß mütterlichen hertzen wünschen thuen.</p><p>Vnsern freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten herrn gemahl vnd kinder wollen E:K:W: noch bey zimblicher leibs vermögenheitt vndt zuestande wissen, allein das wass fur Vnsere person etzliche tage hero vonn dem husten vndt anderer zuefelligen schwacheitt dermassen zugesetzt, daß Wir auch Vnsern gnedigen geliebten herrn vattern, hochlöblicher chriftmilder gedechtnus, wie gerne Wir auch gewolt hetten, die letzte ehr bey dero churfurstl: begrebnus nicht haben leisten können; haben aber dennoch dem Allmechtigen nuhn mehr fur zimbliche besßerung zuedancken, dessen barmhertzigkeit vns sambtlich darbey gnediglich lang erhalten wolle. Es werden auch E:K:W: auß Vnsers hertzlieben hern gemahls an E:K:W: gethanes vorbittlich schreiben, sie haben lassen vndterthenigst vortragen, welcher gestelt dero 1: vmb gnedigste vorschrifft an E:K:W: voer dem erenuehsten Vnserm lieben besonderen Tycho Brahen vndterthenigst angelangt; wann er denn dergleichen auch Vns vmb vorbittschrifft ersucht, vndt Wir niemals vermerckt, daß E:K:W: ihme mitt vngnaden gewogen, auch seine bitt nicht vnzimblich erachten, so gelanget hiemit an E:K:W: Vnser freundtliches bitten, E:K:W: ihn dermasßen mitt gnedigster andtwort wollen versehen lasßen, damitt er seiner geschepsten hoffnung nach im wergk spuren möge, daß diesse Vnsere ihm zue gnaden gemeinte Vorschrift ihm sonderlich zue erlangung seines begehren ersprißlich gewesen, wie Wir denn gar nicht zweiffeln, E:K:W: sich hierumb werden, jhrer angebornen guetigkeitt nach, gegen ihme, so weitt sichs immer der billigkeitt vndt gerechtigkeitt nach (in masßen Wir auch weitters nichts begeren) leiden wil, gnedigst erzeigen. Vndt thuen E:K:W: sambt dero vielgeliebten gemahl, Vnser hertzlieben tochter, zue allen könniglichen wohlergehen, glichlicher regierung vndt guetter bestendig gesundheitt dem Allmechtigen ganz mutterlich beuehlen.</p><p>Datum Cöln an der Sprew, den 4. februarij anno 98.<br>Vonn Gottes gnaden Katharina geborne marggraffin<br>auch churfurstin zue Brandenburgk,<br>inn Preußen hertzogin.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Serene Prince, the Lord Christian the Fourth, King of Denmark, Norway, the Wends and the Goths, Duke of Schleswig, Holstein, of the Stormarn and the Dithmarschen, Count of Oldenburg and Delmenhorst, our friendly, much-beloved Lord Son — into His Royal Majesty's [own] hands.</b></p><p>Most Serene King, [be] to Your Royal Majesty Our dutiful and honourable greeting, and, according to the bond of motherhood, whatever much more We are able [to render] of body and goods, beforehand. Friendly, most heartily beloved Lord cousin, son, and brother: We will hope, undoubting, to the Almighty, that Your Royal Majesty, together with your much-beloved consort, Our heart-dear daughter, are still graciously preserved in good bodily health and in all royal well-being — as We do indeed wish the same to that same Royal Majesty and to Her Beloved Grace out of a motherly heart.</p><p>Our friendly, most heartily beloved Lord consort and children Your Royal Majesty may know to be still in tolerable bodily capacity and condition; save only that, as for Our own person, for some days past the cough and other incidental weakness have so afflicted [Us], that We were also unable to render the last honour to Our gracious beloved Lord father — of most laudable, devout-in-Christ memory — at his electoral funeral, however gladly We should have wished [to do so]; yet We have nonetheless now to thank the Almighty for a tolerable improvement, whose mercy may graciously preserve us all therein long. Your Royal Majesty will also [learn] from Our heart-dear Lord consort's letter of intercession addressed to Your Royal Majesty — which they had most humbly submitted — in what manner there had most humbly arrived [a request] for a most gracious letter of recommendation to Your Royal Majesty on behalf of the most honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe; since he has then likewise besought Us also for a letter of intercession, and We have never noted that Your Royal Majesty was disposed toward him with disfavour, and do also not deem his request unreasonable — there reaches Your Royal Majesty herewith Our friendly request, that Your Royal Majesty would have him furnished with such a most gracious answer that he, according to the hope he has conceived, may perceive in the deed that this Our recommendation, intended for his benefit, has been especially fruitful to him for the attainment of his desire; as indeed We do not at all doubt that Your Royal Majesty will, according to your inborn kindness, show yourself herein most graciously toward him, so far as it may ever admit according to equity and justice (in which measure We also desire nothing further). And We commend Your Royal Majesty, together with your much-beloved consort, Our heart-dear daughter, to the Almighty for all royal welfare, fortunate governance, and good lasting health, quite as a mother [does].</p><p>Given at Cölln an der Spree, the 4th of February, in the year [15]98.<br>By the grace of God, Katharina, born Margravine,<br>also Electress of Brandenburg,<br>Duchess in Prussia.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Katharina of Brandenburg-Küstrin</b> (1549–1602), Electress of Brandenburg, wife of Elector <b>Johann Georg of Brandenburg</b>. She was the maternal grandmother of Christian IV: her daughter <b>Anna Catharina of Brandenburg</b> (1575–1612) married Christian IV in 1597. The kinship triple in the salutation (<i>\"herr vetter, sohn, vndt bruder\"</i> — \"Lord cousin, son, and brother\") is conventional inter-dynastic courtesy address, not literal; she calls the queen <i>\"Vnser hertzlieben tochter\"</i> (\"Our heart-dear daughter\"), which is literal.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christian IV of Denmark and Norway</b> (r. 1588–1648). The titulature in the address-block is the standard Danish royal style: King of Denmark, Norway, the Wends and Goths; Duke of Schleswig, Holstein, Stormarn and Dithmarschen; Count of Oldenburg and Delmenhorst.</li><li><b>Subject — the Tycho intercession.</b> The letter is a <i>Vorbitte</i> / <i>Vorschrift</i> (a letter of intercession + recommendation) on behalf of <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here styled <i>\"dem erenuehsten Vnserm lieben besonderen Tycho Brahen\"</i> (\"the most honourable, Our trusty and well-beloved Tycho Brahe\" — <i>lieben besonderen</i> is the German chancery formula glossed at protocol §5.5 <i>Vester lieber besonder</i> → \"Our trusty and well-beloved\"). It coordinates with a parallel intercession from her husband the Elector (<i>\"Vnsers hertzlieben hern gemahls … vorbittlich schreiben\"</i>). Dating (4 Feb. 1598) and content place it amid the deteriorating relations between Tycho and the Danish court that culminated in his departure from Denmark in 1597; the Brandenburg electoral couple are lobbying the young king on his behalf. Tycho had evidently solicited the intercession himself (<i>\"wann er denn dergleichen auch Vns vmb vorbittschrifft ersucht\"</i>).</li><li><b>Negation count (protocol critical rule).</b> The body contains exactly <b>three</b> negations, all preserved: (1) <i>\"die letzte ehr … nicht haben leisten können\"</i> — \"were <b>unable</b> to render the last honour\" [at her father's funeral]; (2) <i>\"Wir niemals vermerckt, daß E:K:W: ihme mitt vngnaden gewogen\"</i> — \"We have <b>never</b> noted that Your Royal Majesty was disposed toward him with disfavour\"; (3) <i>\"auch seine bitt <b>nicht</b> vnzimblich erachten\"</i> — \"do <b>also not</b> deem his request unreasonable\" (a single negation governing the litotes <i>unzimblich</i> \"unreasonable\"; rendered as one \"not,\" not doubled). The parenthetical <i>\"in masßen Wir auch weitters <b>nichts</b> begeren\"</i> — \"in which measure We also desire <b>nothing</b> further\" — is a fourth negative element (<i>nichts</i>), preserved. None of these reverse a command; the controlling speech-act is a request (<i>\"Vnser freundtliches bitten\"</i>), and the conditional logic is straightforward (since X, and since not-Y, and not-Z → therefore We request), so no [C]-grade ambiguity arises.</li><li><b>The qualifying clause</b> <i>\"so weitt sichs immer der billigkeitt vndt gerechtigkeitt nach … leiden wil\"</i> — \"so far as it may ever admit according to equity and justice\" — is a deferential limiting clause: the Electress asks the king to favour Tycho only insofar as equity and justice permit, explicitly adding that she desires nothing beyond that. The hedge is preserved, not sharpened into an unconditional plea.</li><li><b>Her father's funeral.</b> <i>\"Vnsern gnedigen geliebten herrn vattern, hochlöblicher chriftmilder gedechtnus … dero churfurstl: begrebnus\"</i> — Katharina's father was <b>Margrave Johann (Hans) of Brandenburg-Küstrin</b> (d. 1571); <i>\"chriftmilder\"</i> is a chancery spelling of <i>christmilder</i> (\"devout/charitable in Christ,\" a memorial epithet for the deceased), rendered \"devout-in-Christ memory.\" She reports being kept by illness from attending the funeral — apparently a recent re-interment or commemorative obsequy, since the date of her own letter (1598) post-dates the Margrave's death by decades; the text does not specify the occasion, and it is left unresolved rather than supplied.</li><li><b>Court-German orthography (Frühneuhochdeutsch / chancery, c. 1598).</b> Representative forms: <i>vndt / vnd</i> = <i>und</i>; <i>vff</i>-type <i>v/u</i> alternation throughout (<i>vermugen, vermögenheitt</i>); <i>könnig / könniglichen</i> = <i>König(lichen)</i>; <i>zuuohrn</i> = <i>zuvorn</i> (\"beforehand,\" the address-formula, §5.5); <i>gnediglich / gnedigst</i> = <i>gnädiglich / gnädigst</i>; <i>thuen / thuen … beuehlen</i> = <i>tun / befehlen</i>; <i>besßerung, ersprißlich, lasßen, masßen</i> show <i>ß/ss</i> alternation; <i>Sprew</i> = <i>Spree</i>; <i>marggraffin</i> = <i>Markgräfin</i>; <i>churfurstin</i> = <i>Kurfürstin</i>. <i>husten</i> = \"cough.\"</li><li><b>Abbreviations expanded in sense.</b> <i>E:K:W:</i> = <i>Euer Königliche Würden</i> (\"Your Royal Majesty/Dignity\"); <i>J:L:</i> = <i>Ihre Liebden</i> (\"Her Beloved Grace,\" conventional address for a princess — here the queen); <i>dero 1:</i> — an abbreviation in the witness, most plausibly <i>dero Liebden</i> (\"their Beloved Grace,\" i.e. the Elector); read as the source of the humble request, but the contracted \"1:\" is editorially uncertain and is flagged here rather than silently expanded.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>\"den 4. februarij anno 98\"</i> = 4 February 1598. Brandenburg and Denmark both kept the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1598 (Protestant territories did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700), so the dateline is O.S.; no conversion is applied. The catalogue label (\"1598. 4. februar. Cölln an der Spree\") agrees with the dateline.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Cöln / Cölln an der Sprew</i> = <b>Cölln an der Spree</b>, the twin town of Berlin on the Spree (seat of the Brandenburg court; later merged into Berlin), not Cologne on the Rhine.</li><li><b>MS witness.</b> The Opera Omnia text rests on the transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b>, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen — i.e. a later copy, not the original sent letter; readings such as <i>dero 1:</i> should be weighed with that transmission in mind.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and all four negatives are preserved with their force; the soft points, flagged above, are the editorially uncertain abbreviation <i>\"dero 1:\"</i> (read as <i>dero Liebden</i>) and the unspecified occasion of the father's funeral, neither of which affects the letter's substance as an intercession for Tycho Brahe.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.132-134 as page images (letter no.142 runs 132-133; p.134 is the start of the next letter, no.143, confirmed as boundary check). Collated opening/closing formulas, the full title/address block, the intercession content re Tycho Brahe, and the date/signature (4 februarij anno 98, Katharina, Duchess in Prussia) word-for-word against the printed page. source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english is a complete, accurate rendering with no additions, omissions, or flipped subjects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Exchanges greetings and inquires after the king's health, reporting that the Electress herself has been suffering from a cough for several days.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980204001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980207001", "d": "1598-02-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "ck": "Christian 4.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → King Christian IV — Wandsbek (Wandsburg), 7 February 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 6f. (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AD REGEM DANIAe.</p><p>SERENISSIME et potentissime rex, domine clementissime. Regiae tuae Majestati in hoc novo conjugio adeoque toto regimine optima quaeque et faustissima ex animo precor et voveo ac gratias, quas possum, maximas pro beneficiis a laudatissimae memoriae parente Friderico mihi praestitis quamplurimis submisse ago, quodque inde ab eo anno, quo Regia Tua Majestas in lucem hanc prodiit, divina illa scientia astronomica in Dania, patria mea dulcissima, liberaliter sustentata sit. Et sane non minora de Regia Tua Majestate mihi indubie pollicebar et citra frustraneam spem expertus fuissem, ni quorundam insatiabili mei odio laborantium (uti non exiguis indiciis reor) malevolus in me animus obstitisset, quos tamen nullo pacto laesi unquam, imo cuidam, quem horum latentem et inveteratum machinatorem existimo non parum profui. Quamobrem statim a Regiae Tuae Ma:tis coronatione mutationum incidere occasiones, uti sunt rerum humanarum subinde quaedam vicissitudines. Idque forte fataliter evenit, ipsis jam astris artis suae excercitium alio dirigentibus et destinantibus, cum et tertius annorum climactericus, per se alterationibus obnoxius, a prima Uraniburgi isthic fundatione ageretur. Praeterea Regiae Tuae Majestati humiliter et enixe gratias habeo, quod, siquidem ita necessitas postularet, ut me cum iis, quibus tantopere invigilo, exercitiis astronomicis in vicinam Germaniam conferrem, nec iter impedire, nec retinere, nec etiam, cum Rostochii per anni quadrantem in propinquo manerem, aut postea, dum in extremis Holsatiae finibus moror, revocare voluerit, quo mea studia non retardarentur, et astronomiam universaliter, ut toti orbi noster undiquaque inserviret labor et acceptum sit condendi propositum. Quo sane nomine eo magis Regiae Tuae Majestati me obstrictum video. Nam licet hoc maximo meo factum fuisset incommodo, et majore fortassis aliorum, qui nostra undiquaque avide expetunt et nequaquam contemptui habent, ob amorem tamen in Patriam et honorem regi meo serenissimo praestandum nolens volens obtemperare atque particularia universalibus praeponere coactus fuissem, cum minori meo et aliorum emolumento. Quapropter etiam, cum Rostochii ea de caussa tam diu non sine periculo ob luem epidemicam isthic grassantem neque sine magnis expensis ob familiae copiam et annonae caritatem subsisterem, ad Regiam Tuam Maiestatem submisse literas dedi et abitus mei caussas nonnihil, quantum licuit, attigi, meamque operam Regiae Tuae Majestati et patriae submisso animo sinceraque intentione obtuli, in omnibus, quae in ipsius et patriae honorem per me unquam praestari possent, modo tolerabilibus conditionibus id fieret, et indemnitati meae competenter consuleretur. Quae literae, quod non eo, quo a me scriptae, candido nimirum et in patriam studioso affectu animoque in serenissimum regem meum submisse obsequioso, acceptae sint aliorum, uti existimo, eas sinistre admodum interpretantium malevolentia admissum est, sive quod aliquo clanculario mei laborarent odio (cujus tamen me iis caussas dedisse non sum conscius) sive quod suo sic abundare sensu vellent. Quod equidem nec iis invideo, nec aegre fero. Regiam vero Ma:tem Tuam, horum neutiquam consciam, lubenter in his excusatam habeo. Verum sit hoc quicquid velit, quod veritas, temporis filia, aliquando prodet meamque integritatem tuebitur, tamen de patria mea (uti par est) honorifice sentire, et Regiae Majestatis Tuae honori, quantum in me est, submisse studere ejusque virtutes depraedicare non desinam. Utque id aliqua nunc ex parte declarem atque Majestati Tuae pro beneficiis clementer praestitis, qua possum, submissione me gratum ostendam, duos eidem libros, in ipsius regno primum natos, hic vero in Germaniae vicinae finibus nuper completos offero et exhibeo. Regiam etiam Tuam Majestatem, cum et aliis regibus et principibus viris eadem mittam, praeterire minime decuit aut licuit. Submisse itaque et humiliter rogo, Regia Tua Majestas hoc regio et clementi animo acceptare non dedignetur. Plura et his majora de rebus astronomicis, annuente astrorum opifice, subjuncturus sum, quorum maiorem partem itidem in Regiae Tuae Majestatis regno, patria mea charissima, pluribus jam annis a me elaboratam, reliquam vero hic per otium hybernum ulterius confectam atque absolutam, in Regiae tuae Majestatis et regni honorem, favente eodem numine, propediem publicare decrevi et Regiae Tuae Majestati, modo id ei non ingratum intellexero, demisse offerre. Interim et semper, ut Regiae Tuae Majestati ego meaque omnia quam commendatissima sint, qua possum et decet submissione precor.</p><p>Deus opt: max: Regiam Tuam Ma:tem in regnorum et ditionum gubernandorum utilitatem et commodum cum ser:ma et optima conjuge quam diutissime florentem et incolumem conservet.</p><p>Datae ex arce Ranzoviana Wandesburgk, prope Hamburgum sita, die 7. februarii anni 1598.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TO THE KING OF DENMARK.</b></p><p>Most serene and most mighty king, most gracious lord. To Your Royal Majesty I pray and vow from my heart all that is best and most auspicious in this new marriage, and indeed in your whole reign; and I humbly render the greatest thanks I can for the very many benefits bestowed on me by your parent Frederick of most laudable memory, and because, ever since the year in which Your Royal Majesty came forth into this light, that divine science of astronomy has been liberally maintained in Denmark, my sweetest homeland. And indeed I was assuredly promising myself no lesser things from Your Royal Majesty, and should have experienced them without disappointment of that hope, had not the malevolent disposition of certain men toward me — men labouring under an insatiable hatred of me (as I judge from no slight indications) — stood in my way; men whom I nevertheless never in any way injured, nay, to a certain one of them, whom I reckon the hidden and inveterate contriver of these things, I did no small service. For which reason, immediately after Your Royal Majesty's coronation, occasions of changes began to arise, as there are from time to time certain vicissitudes in human affairs. And this perhaps befell by fate, the very stars now directing and ordaining the exercise of their art elsewhere, since the third climacteric year — of itself liable to alterations — was being reckoned from the first founding of Uraniborg yonder. Furthermore, I humbly and earnestly give thanks to Your Royal Majesty, that — since necessity so demanded that I betake myself, together with those astronomical pursuits over which I keep so watchful a vigil, into neighbouring Germany — you were unwilling either to impede my journey, or to detain me, or even, when I was staying nearby at Rostock for a quarter of a year, or afterwards while I tarry in the farthest bounds of Holstein, to recall me, so that my studies might not be retarded, and that our labour might everywhere serve astronomy universally [and] the whole world, and the purpose of establishing it might be welcome. On which account, to be sure, I see myself the more bound to Your Royal Majesty. For although this had been done to my very great inconvenience, and perhaps to the greater [inconvenience] of others, who eagerly seek out our works on every side and by no means hold them in contempt, yet out of love toward my Homeland and the honour to be rendered to my most serene king I should have been compelled, willing or unwilling, to comply, and to set particular [interests] before universal ones, to the lesser profit of myself and of others. Wherefore also, since for that cause I was staying at Rostock so long, not without danger from the epidemic plague raging there, nor without great expenses on account of the size of my household and the dearness of provisions, I humbly addressed a letter to Your Royal Majesty and touched somewhat, so far as was permitted, upon the reasons for my departure, and offered my service to Your Royal Majesty and to my homeland, with a humble disposition and sincere intention, in all things that could ever be performed by me toward the honour of [Your Majesty] and of the homeland — provided it were done on tolerable conditions, and provided my own being-kept-harmless were duly looked to. That this letter was not received in the spirit in which it was written by me — namely with that frank affection, zealous toward my homeland, and with a mind humbly deferential toward my most serene king — came about, as I think, through the malevolence of others who interpret it altogether perversely, whether because they laboured under some clandestine hatred of me (for which, however, I am not conscious of having given them cause), or because they wished thus to overflow with their own opinion. Which, for my part, I neither begrudge them nor take amiss. But Your Royal Majesty, in no wise privy to these things, I gladly hold excused in this matter. Yet be this whatever it will — for that the truth, the daughter of time, will one day bring it forth and will protect my integrity — nonetheless I shall not cease to think honourably of my homeland (as is fitting), and, so far as in me lies, humbly to study Your Royal Majesty's honour and to proclaim your virtues. And that I may now in some part declare this, and show myself grateful to Your Majesty, with what humble submission I can, for the benefits graciously bestowed, I offer and present to the same two books, first born in [Your Majesty's] own kingdom, but here lately completed within the bounds of neighbouring Germany. Nor was it at all seemly or permissible to pass over Your Royal Majesty, since I am sending the same [books] to other kings and princely men as well. Humbly therefore and submissively I ask that Your Royal Majesty may not disdain to accept this with a royal and gracious disposition. More things, and greater than these, concerning astronomical matters I shall append, the Maker of the stars granting it; the greater part of which, likewise elaborated by me over many years now within Your Royal Majesty's kingdom, my dearest homeland, and the remainder further wrought and completed here during the winter's leisure, I have resolved — the same divine power favouring — shortly to publish, to the honour of Your Royal Majesty and the kingdom, and humbly to offer to Your Royal Majesty, provided I understand it to be not unwelcome to him. In the meantime, and always, I pray with what submission I can and as is fitting, that I and all that is mine may be most heartily commended to Your Royal Majesty.</p><p>May God, Best and Greatest, preserve Your Royal Majesty, to the profit and advantage of the kingdoms and dominions to be governed, flourishing and unharmed together with your most serene and most excellent consort for the very longest time.</p><p>Given from the Rantzau castle Wandsburg, situated near Hamburg, on the 7th day of February in the year 1598.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), writing from his self-imposed exile after leaving Hven. The letter is a draft (<i>Kladde</i>) preserved in the Basel autograph collection.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Regem Daniae</i> = <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (1577–1648, r. from 1588, crowned 1596). The opening congratulations on the king's \"new marriage\" refer to his marriage to <b>Anne Catherine of Brandenburg</b> (concluded late 1597). The phrase <i>inde ab eo anno, quo Regia Tua Majestas in lucem hanc prodiit</i> (\"ever since the year in which Your Royal Majesty came forth into this light,\" i.e. 1577) links the king's birth-year to the founding period of Uraniborg (Hven granted 1576, building begun 1576/77).</li><li><b>Occasion &amp; the \"two books.\"</b> Tycho writes from Wandsbek, where he was a guest of <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, after leaving Denmark (he had wintered at Rostock, then moved into Holstein). The <i>duos libros … in ipsius regno primum natos, hic vero in Germaniae vicinae finibus nuper completos</i> (\"two books, first born in [your] kingdom but lately completed here in the bounds of neighbouring Germany\") is the presentation of the <b><i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</b></i> (Wandsbek, 1598) together with the companion volume Tycho was issuing at the same time. <b>Identification flagged:</b> the text names neither book; the standard pairing of the two Wandsbek presentation volumes is the <i>Mechanica</i> and the printed <b>star-catalogue</b> (the thousand-star catalogue, circulated as a manuscript/printed companion to the <i>Progymnasmata</i>). The translation keeps the neutral \"two books\"; the specific titles are supplied here as context, not asserted from the letter.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arce Ranzoviana Wandesburgk, prope Hamburgum</i> = the <b>Rantzau castle at Wandsbek</b> (modern Wandsbek, a district of Hamburg), the seat of Heinrich Rantzau, <i>Statthalter</i> of the Danish king in Holstein; rendered \"Wandsburg\" in the header to match the as-written <i>Wandesburgk</i>. <i>Holsatia</i> = <b>Holstein</b>; <i>Rostochium</i> = <b>Rostock</b> (per §5.7).</li><li><b>Astronomy / programme.</b> <i>astronomiam universaliter … condendi propositum</i> — Tycho's lifelong <b>programme of establishing/restoring astronomy</b> (cf. <i>astronomia instaurata</i>, §5.1, <i>restitutio / instauratio</i>); <i>toti orbi … noster … labor</i> — \"our labour [serving] the whole world.\" <i>astrorum opifice</i> / <i>eodem numine</i> — \"the Maker of the stars\" / \"the same divine power,\" reverent periphrases kept.</li><li><b>Astrology.</b> <i>tertius annorum climactericus … a prima Uraniburgi … fundatione</i> = \"the <b>third climacteric year</b> reckoned from the first founding of Uraniborg\" (a climacteric at a multiple of seven; Uraniborg founded 1576 → the 21st year, 1597, falls in this window). <i>ipsis jam astris artis suae excercitium alio dirigentibus et destinantibus</i> — \"the very stars now directing and ordaining the exercise of their art elsewhere\": Tycho frames his departure in astrological-fatalist terms while attributing the proximate cause to court enemies. The hedge <i>forte fataliter</i> (\"perhaps by fate\") is preserved.</li><li><b>Polemical hedges (preserved, not sharpened).</b> Tycho repeatedly veils his accusations: <i>quorundam insatiabili mei odio laborantium (uti non exiguis indiciis reor)</i> — \"certain men labouring under an insatiable hatred of me (as I judge from no slight indications)\"; <i>cuidam, quem … latentem et inveteratum machinatorem existimo</i> — \"a certain one whom I reckon the hidden and inveterate contriver.\" The unnamed \"contriver\" is left unnamed in the translation, as in the source. The qualifier <i>quantum licuit</i> (\"so far as was permitted\") and the conditional <i>modo tolerabilibus conditionibus … et indemnitati meae competenter consuleretur</i> (\"provided on tolerable conditions … and provided my keeping-harmless were duly looked to\") are kept with their hedged force.</li><li><b>Negation count.</b> The clause listing the king's forbearance contains a coordinated triple negation governed by one <i>voluerit</i>: <i>nec iter impedire, nec retinere, nec etiam … revocare voluerit</i> — \"you were unwilling <b>neither</b> to impede my journey, <b>nor</b> to detain me, <b>nor</b> even … to recall me.\" Rendered as three negated infinitives under \"you were unwilling,\" preserving the king's non-interference (the king did <b>not</b> obstruct Tycho's departure). No negation added or dropped.</li><li><b><i>veritas, temporis filia</b></i> — \"the truth, the daughter of time\" (<i>Veritas filia Temporis</i>), the classical-Renaissance commonplace (Gellius via Erasmus' <i>Adagia</i>); kept as the sententious aside it is.</li><li><b>Sign-off / titulature.</b> <i>Deus opt: max:</i> = <i>Deus Optimus Maximus</i>, \"God, Best and Greatest\" (§5.5); <i>ser:ma et optima conjuge</i> = \"most serene and most excellent consort\" (Anne Catherine). The dense honorific register (<i>Regia Tua Majestas</i>, <i>submisse</i>, <i>humiliter</i>, <i>demisse</i>, <i>clementer</i>) is kept throughout as Tycho's deferential address to his sovereign.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 7. februarii anni 1598</i> = 7 February 1598. Denmark was on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1598 (§3); the date is so marked. The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (\"1598. 7. februar. Wandsburg\"); no discrepancy. Date not converted.</li><li><b>Orthography / abbreviations.</b> <i>Ma:tis / Ma:tem</i> = <i>Majestatis / Majestatem</i>; <i>ser:ma</i> = <i>serenissima</i>; <i>opt: max:</i> = <i>Optimus Maximus</i>; <i>caussas / caussa</i> = <i>causas / causa</i> (period spelling); <i>excercitium</i> = <i>exercitium</i>; <i>charissima</i> = <i>carissima</i>; <i>hybernum</i> = <i>hibernum</i>. Forms kept as printed in the Source block.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the polemical hedges and the triple-negation forbearance clause are preserved with their exact force. The one open point is the identity of the <i>duos libros</i> (\"two books\"), which the letter does not name; the <i>Mechanica</i> + companion star-catalogue identification is supplied as external context and flagged, not asserted from the text.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — text rendered exactly ('two books'); the only open point is an external-context title identification, correctly not asserted.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer slice_pdf visually in full (TBOO XIV pp.133-134, letter no.143, headed 'AD REGEM DANIAE'; p.132 is the tail of an unrelated German letter and p.135 opens the next item, a German letter from the Electress of Brandenburg -- both correctly outside the letter proper, consistent with the brief's note that slice ranges carry buffer pages). Collated the full Latin word-for-word: the new-marriage/whole-reign opening, the debt to the late King Frederick, the malevolent-detractors passage, the coronation and 'tertius annorum climactericus' astrological aside, the Rostock quarter-year sojourn and plague ('luem epidemicam'), the 'veritas, temporis filia' maxim, the offer of two books completed in Germany, and the closing 'Datae ex arce Ranzoviana Wandesburgk...die 7. februarii anni 1598.' Noted that Dreyer flags one clause ('Quae literae...excusatam habeo') with a footnote '[ ]1) deleta sunt' recording it as struck in the underlying apograph, yet still prints it in the main text; our source_text and the English both reproduce that same clause faithfully, so this is a paleographical note, not a divergence between source_text and Dreyer's printed text. Every proper name, number, and date matches; no fabrications, omissions, mistranslations, hedge losses, or text errors found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol.35, Fol.6f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Christian IV, King of Denmark", "count": 10, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15971008001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971008001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Christian IV for his father Frederick II's patronage of astronomy and complains that hidden enemies have maliciously obstructed him without cause.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980207001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980214001", "d": "1598-02-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 14 February 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO, VIRO CORDATO ET MIHI AMICO, CONSANGVINEO ET AFFINI CHARISSIMO.</p><p>NOBILISSIME Domine OLIGERE, Consangvinee et Affinis desideratissime et prae multis dilecte. Incidit aliquid hodie post tuum a me discessum, quod in scheda memoriali tibi tradita annotatum non recordor: nempe quod sit nomen Baronis illius Austriaci, qui uxorem in Cimbria duxit. Illud enim mihi memoriâ excidit. Cuperem etiam scire, ubinam habitet, et quomodo intitulandus sit, iuxta morem vobis consuetum; quo illi scribam convenientius de putidissimo et mendacissimo isto Plagiario, cuius hîc facta est mentio; atque eorum, quae ipsi de eo constant, cum mecum ab ERICO LANGIO adductus esset, indicationem efflagitem. Admoneas etiam, an Latinae linguae sit peritus, nec ne; quo sciam, an Germanice vel Latine illi scribendum sit, aut etiam Danice, si hoc callet idioma. Vale, et literas Regi destinatas, ad non Regias oblique respondentes per hunc nuncium remitte, quantum otium tibi permiserit, critizatas, atque glossis marginalibus auctas: quod etiam ut in prioribus facias, quibus hae nonnulla respondent, rogo; nec te id effecturum diffido, quoniam sic in te recepisti.</p><p>Iamque ulterius et perpetuo quam felicissime vale, mi exoptatissime et desideratissime OLIGERE.<br>Raptim Wandesburgi die 14. Februarij Anni 98.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Holger Rosenkrantz, a man of sense and my friend, a kinsman and connexion by marriage most dear.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Holger, kinsman and connexion most longed-for and beloved before many. Something occurred to me today, after your departure from me, which I do not recall having noted down in the memorandum-slip handed over to you: namely, what is the name of that Austrian baron who took a wife in Holstein [Cimbria]. For it has slipped my memory. I should also like to know where he lives, and how he is to be styled, according to the custom usual among you [of the nobility]; so that I may write to him more fittingly about that most rotten and most lying Plagiarist, of whom mention was made here; and so that I may earnestly request a statement of those things which are established to him concerning the man, since he had been brought to me by Erik Lange. Advise me too whether he is skilled in the Latin tongue or not; so that I may know whether I am to write to him in German or in Latin, or even in Danish, if he is versed in this language. Farewell; and the letters destined for the King, which obliquely answer the not-royal ones, send back by this messenger — so far as your leisure shall allow you — once they have been subjected to critical judgement [critizatas] and enlarged with marginal glosses: which I ask that you do also in the case of the earlier ones, to which these in some part correspond; nor do I doubt that you will accomplish it, since you have so taken it upon yourself.</p><p>And now further and forever, fare you most happily, my most wished-for and most longed-for Holger.<br>In haste, at Wandsbek, on the 14th day of February of the year [15]98.<br>Yours, TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from exile after leaving Denmark in 1597. By February 1598 he was lodged at <b>Wandsbek</b> (see below), the residence of Heinrich Rantzau near Hamburg, where he completed the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> and the <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber</i> later that year.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (\"the Learned,\" 1574–1642), Danish nobleman, theologian and scholar, related to Tycho by blood and marriage (<i>consanguineus et affinis</i>). The address renders him <i>vir cordatus</i> — \"a man of sense / good judgement\"; cf. the same epithet applied to Rosenkrantz elsewhere in the correspondence.</li><li><b><i>Cimbria</b></i> — the humanist Latin name for the <b>Cimbrian peninsula</b>, i.e. Jutland/Holstein (the Cimbric Chersonese); rendered \"Holstein [Cimbria]\" since the Austrian baron's marriage \"in Cimbria\" places it in that region. The exact district is not specified by the text and is not narrowed here.</li><li><b><i>Plagiarius</b></i> (\"Plagiarist\") — Tycho's habitual term of abuse for <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär</b> (Latin <i>Ursus</i>, 1551–1600), the Imperial Mathematician at Prague whom Tycho accused of having stolen and published his geo-heliocentric world-system (in Ursus's <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i>, 1588). The intensifiers <i>putidissimus et mendacissimus</i> (\"most rotten and most lying\") are kept at full superlative force. <b>Identification flagged:</b> Ursus is not named in this letter; the equation rests on Tycho's consistent usage of <i>Plagiarius</i> for him in 1597–98 and on the context (a man Tycho means to denounce by letter). The text itself leaves the \"Plagiarist\" unnamed.</li><li><b><i>Ericus Langius</b></i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), Danish nobleman and alchemist, Tycho's friend and the betrothed of Tycho's sister Sophie Brahe; here named as the person who had earlier brought the Austrian baron into Tycho's company (<i>cum mecum … adductus esset</i>).</li><li><b><i>scheda memorialis</b></i> — \"a memorandum-slip\" / aide-mémoire that Tycho had given Rosenkrantz on his departure; this letter supplies a point he had forgotten to include.</li><li><b><i>iuxta morem vobis consuetum</b></i> — \"according to the custom usual among you\": <i>vobis</i> is read as the second-person plural of the noble/courtly class to which Rosenkrantz belongs (the proper styling and titulature of a baron). Rendered \"among you [of the nobility],\" the bracketed gloss marking the interpretive expansion.</li><li><b><i>literas Regi destinatas, ad non Regias oblique respondentes</b></i> — \"the letters destined for the King, which obliquely answer the not-royal ones.\" A compressed and deliberately oblique phrase: letters meant for the King (almost certainly <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>) that reply indirectly to other, <i>non</i>-royal letters. <b>Crux flagged:</b> the referent of <i>non Regias</i> (the \"not-royal\" letters being answered) is not specified in the text and is left literal rather than resolved; <i>oblique</i> (\"obliquely, indirectly\") is Tycho's own hedge and is preserved, not sharpened into a definite construction.</li><li><b><i>critizatas</b></i> — a Greek-derived coinage (from κριτής / κρίνειν, \"to judge, sift\") meaning \"critically examined, subjected to critical judgement\"; here, that Rosenkrantz go over the letters with a critic's eye before returning them. Kept as a flagged technical neologism and glossed rather than smoothed to a single English verb. Paired with <i>glossis marginalibus auctas</i>, \"enlarged with marginal glosses\" (annotations in the margin).</li><li><b><i>manu propria</b></i> — \"in his own hand\": Tycho's autograph subscription. The sole MS witness, however, is a later copy (<i>afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b>, so the surviving text is a transcript, not the autograph itself.</li><li><b>Dateline &amp; place.</b> <i>Raptim Wandesburgi die 14. Februarij Anni 98</i> = \"In haste, at <b>Wandsbek</b> [Wandesburgum, the Rantzau castle near Hamburg, now in Hamburg], on the 14th day of February of the year [15]98\" (O.S.; Denmark and the North-German Protestant correspondents kept the Julian calendar). <i>Raptim</i> (\"in haste\") preserved. The header date follows the dateline and agrees with the catalogue label (14 February 1598).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register matched; two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the unnamed <i>Plagiarius</i>, identified as Ursus on contextual/usage grounds only, since the letter does not name him; and (2) the deliberately oblique clause <i>literas Regi destinatas, ad non Regias oblique respondentes</i>, whose \"not-royal\" referent the text leaves unspecified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.24-26 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.24-25, text matches Dreyer word for word. The three languages named (Latin, German, Danish) and the date (14 February 1598) correct. No defects found; the self-assigned published_grade of B was unnecessarily conservative given the clean collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_15980122001", "next": "brahe-t_15980508001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Rosenkrantz for the name, address, and preferred language of an Austrian baron introduced by Erik Lange, in order to solicit testimony about the plagiarist Ursus.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980214001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980214002", "d": "1598-02-14", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Katharina, Electress of Brandenburg", "ck": "Katharina, kurfyrstinde af Brandenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Berlin", "t": "Katharina, Electress of Brandenburg → Tycho Brahe — Cölln an der Spree, 14 February 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin code-switch) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM ERNUESTEN VNNSERM BESONDERN LIEBEN TYCHO BRAHEN VFF KNUDTTSTRUP VNNDT WRANENNBURGK.</p><p>VANN Gottes gnadenn Katharina, geborne vndt vermehlte marggreffin, auch churfurstin zue Brandenburgk, jnn Preusen hertzogin. Wnsern gnedigenn grues zuuorn. Ernuester besonder lieber, Wir thuen euch auff eur begehrn hiermitt Vnserm diener vndt lieben getrewenn Johann Mullern zu dem ende vbersendenn, das ehr ein zeittlang mitt euch conuersiren, vnnd sich in mathematico studio aldar exerciren muegte. Begehrn demnach an euch hiermitt ganntz gnedigst, jhr wollett euch nichtt allein jnn dem kegenn jhnen willfehrigk erweisenn, sonndern auch in spagiricis medicamentorum praeparationibus, vnnd was jhr sonnstenn auss erfahrung in pyronomicis habtt, jhnen guettwillig vnterrichtenn, vnndt euch inn diesem allenn dermassen erzeigen, wie vff eurre Vnns durch jhnen, Vnnsernn diener, gethene zuesage erbietenn, zu euch Vnser vngezweifeltes gnedigstes vertrawenn gerichtett. Das wollenn Wir jnn allenn gnadenn, darmitt Wir euch sonnds woll gewogenn, hinwieder mitt danck zu erkennen vnuorgessenn halltenn. Vnnd ob Wir jhn woll gherne nebenn eurem sohne ettwass ehe wieder abgeferttiget haben wollten, so seindt doch Vnnserm freundtlichenn hertzlieben herrn gemahl bißhero andere wichtiche sachenn vorgefallenn, das man darzue fueglich nichtt ehe kommenn konnenn. Werdet der wegenn euren sohn, der es zwartt wie auch gedachter Johann Muller an vleissigsten vnndterthenigstenn sollicitiren nicht manglen lassen, im besten enttschuldigett haltenn. Wir seindt der vnzweifeligen zuversichtt, es werden bey der könn: wd:, Vnnserm freunthlichen hertzvielgeliebten herrn vettern vnnd sohne, die euch mittgetheilte intercession schrifftenn ohne frucht nicht abgehen, sonndernn dardurch eure sachenn wiederumb zu selbst wunschendenn gueten standt gelangenn, wie Wir euch denn gnediglich wunschenn, vnnd mochtens euch gnediger meinunge nicht verhaltenn, deme Wir mitt gnadenn gneigtt.</p><p>Datum Cöln an der Sprew, denn 14. februarij anno 98<br>Katarina korforsten, m: h:</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Right Honourable, Our particular dear Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup and Uraniborg.</b></p><p>By the grace of God, Katharina, born and married Margravine, also Electress of Brandenburg, Duchess in Prussia. Our gracious greeting beforehand. Right Honourable, particular dear [friend], We herewith send you, at your request, Our servant and dear faithful Johann Müller, to the end that he may for a time converse with you and there train himself in the study of mathematics. We therefore desire of you herewith, most graciously, that you will show yourself compliant toward him not only in this, but also instruct him willingly in the spagyric preparations of medicaments, and in whatever else you have from experience in the pyronomic [arts]; and that you will conduct yourself in all this in such a manner as the promise made to Us through him, Our servant, on your behalf gives undertaking — toward you Our undoubted most gracious trust being directed. This We shall, in all the grace wherewith We are indeed well disposed toward you, in turn acknowledge with thanks and hold unforgotten. And although We should indeed gladly have had him dispatched back, together with your son, somewhat sooner, yet other weighty matters have hitherto befallen Our friendly, heartily dear lord and husband, such that one could not fittingly come to it sooner. You will therefore hold your son excused in the best [sense] — who indeed, like the said Johann Müller, will not let it be wanting in most diligent, most humble solicitation. We are of the undoubted confidence that, with the Royal Majesty, Our friendly, most heartily beloved lord cousin and son, the letters of intercession imparted to you will not go off without fruit, but that thereby your affairs will again attain the good standing you yourself wish for — as We do graciously wish you; and We would not, of gracious intent, withhold this from you, to whom We are inclined with grace.</p><p>Given at Cölln an der Spree, the 14th of February, year [15]98.<br>Katharina, Electress, with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Katharina of Brandenburg-Küstrin</b> (1549–1602), Electress of Brandenburg, wife of the Elector <b>Johann Georg of Brandenburg</b> (r. 1571–98 — the <i>\"freundtlichen hertzlieben herrn gemahl\"</i>, \"friendly heartily dear lord and husband,\" named below). She titles herself \"born and married Margravine, also Electress of Brandenburg, Duchess in Prussia\" — the standard Hohenzollern intitulatio (the Elector of Brandenburg held the Duchy of Prussia in fief).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, addressed \"of Knudstrup and Uraniborg\" (<i>VFF KNUDTTSTRUP VNNDT WRANENNBURGK</i>) — his ancestral seat at Knudstrup in Scania and his observatory-castle on Hven. By the dateline (14 Feb. 1598) Tycho had in fact already left Hven (he departed for the Continent in 1597); the address preserves his styled titles, not his current residence.</li><li><b>Johann Müller.</b> Katharina's <i>diener</i> (servant/court attendant), sent to study with Tycho. Not securely identified beyond the letter; not to be conflated with the astronomer Johannes Müller (Regiomontanus, †1476). The corpus shows Tycho, in a related letter (Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII), agreeing to receive \"the boy\" the Illustrious Lady had decided to send, \"and a spagyric laborator [worker] too\" — consistent with Müller's training brief here.</li><li><b>\"eurem sohne\" / \"euren sohn\"</b> — Tycho's son (the text does not name which; Tycho's sons Tycho the younger and Jørgen were of an age to act as suitors at court). He is at the Brandenburg court soliciting Tycho's affairs alongside Müller.</li><li><b>\"der könn: wd:\" = <i>der königlichen Würde</b></i> (\"the Royal Majesty / royal dignity\"), named \"Our friendly, most heartily beloved lord cousin and son.\" This points to <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (r. 1588–1648), to whom intercession on Tycho's behalf would be sought; the <i>vetter und sohn</i> register reflects the dynastic kinship between the Hohenzollern and Oldenburg houses. <i>Identification flagged:</i> the source gives only the abbreviated style, not a name; \"Christian IV\" is the most natural referent but is supplied editorially.</li><li><b><i>intercession schrifftenn</b></i> — \"letters of intercession,\" i.e. recommendatory letters interceding on Tycho's behalf (regarding his loss of the Hven living and royal favour). Kept as \"letters of intercession.\"</li><li><b>Technical / Latin code-switch.</b> The chancery German switches into Latin for the learned matter: <i>mathematico studio</i> = \"the study of mathematics\"; <i>spagiricis medicamentorum praeparationibus</i> = \"spagyric preparations of medicaments\" (Paracelsian chemical pharmacy — <i>spagyric</i> kept as a period term, not modernised to \"chemical\"); <i>in pyronomicis</i> = \"in pyronomics,\" the art of the regulated fire/furnace (assaying and distillation by controlled heat) — rendered \"in the pyronomic [arts],\" the bracketed noun supplied. <i>conuersiren, exerciren, sollicitiren, intercession</i> are Latinate chancery verbs, rendered to match the learned register.</li><li><b>Negations (verified count, 5).</b> <i>nichtt allein … sonndern auch</i> = \"not only … but also\"; <i>nichtt ehe kommenn konnenn</i> = \"could not come to it sooner\"; <i>nicht manglen lassen</i> = \"will not let it be wanting\"; <i>ohne frucht nicht abgehen</i> = \"will not go off without fruit\"; <i>nicht verhaltenn</i> = \"would not withhold.\" All five negations are reproduced; none added or dropped. The double construction <i>ohne frucht nicht abgehen</i> (\"not go off without fruit\") is a litotes affirming success, kept as the period idiom.</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, c. 1598).</b> <i>VANN/VANN Gottes gnadenn</i> = <i>von Gottes Gnaden</i> (\"by the grace of God\"); <i>Wnsern/Vnsern</i> = <i>unsern</i>; <i>grues zuuorn</i> = <i>Gruß zuvor</i> (\"greeting beforehand,\" the address-formula, protocol §5.5); <i>thuen</i> = <i>tun</i>; <i>muegte</i> = <i>möchte</i>; <i>willfehrigk</i> = <i>willfährig</i> (\"compliant\"); <i>guettwillig</i> = <i>gutwillig</i>; <i>vnuorgessenn</i> = <i>unvergessen</i>; <i>abgeferttiget</i> = <i>abgefertigt</i> (\"dispatched\"); <i>fueglich</i> = <i>füglich</i> (\"fittingly\"); <i>vleissigsten vnndterthenigstenn</i> = <i>fleißigsten untertänigsten</i> (\"most diligent, most humble\"); <i>vettern</i> = <i>Vetter</i> (\"cousin/kinsman\"). The princely <i>Wir/euch</i> register and full titulature are preserved.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Katarina korforsten, m: h:</i> — \"Katharina, Electress\" (<i>Kurfürstin</i>, the spelling phonetic/Low-German-influenced), with <i>m: h:</i> = <i>mit (eigener) Hand</i> / <i>manu propria</i>, \"with my own hand.\" Rendered \"with my own hand.\"</li><li><b>Place / calendar.</b> <i>Cöln an der Sprew</i> = <b>Cölln an der Spree</b>, the twin town of Berlin and seat of the Brandenburg court (the <i>Cöllnisches Schloss</i>); not Cologne on the Rhine. <i>14. februarij anno 98</i> = 14 February 1598; Brandenburg and Denmark were both on the Julian calendar (O.S.) in 1598. The dateline as written governs the header; the dataset label (\"1598. 14. februar. Cölln an der Spree\") agrees.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the negations reproduced exactly. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the referent of the abbreviated <i>\"könn: wd: … herr vetter und sohn\"</i> (most plausibly Christian IV, supplied editorially); (2) the identity of <i>Johann Müller</i> beyond the letter. Neither affects the legal/courtesy force of the text.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — residue is natural editorial identifications (Christian IV; Müller), non-load-bearing; negations exact, sense + date secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.134-136 as page images; Katharina's letter (Dreyer's item 144) entirely on p.135. source_text matches Dreyer's printed German verbatim throughout. Collated translation clause-by-clause: the sending of Johann Müller for instruction in mathematics, spagyric and pyronomic arts, the apology for the delay, the intercession for Tycho with the King, and the closing all render faithfully. Names and date (14 February [15]98) correct. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends her servant Johann Müller to study mathematics and spagyric preparation of medicaments under Tycho, requesting compliance with the terms previously promised.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980214002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980218001", "d": "1598-02-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Wandsbek, 18 February 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with embedded Danish phrases) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 179 (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEIUM.</p><p>NUPER cum OLIGERUS ROSENKRANTZ consanguineus Affinis et Amicus meus dilectus hîc me per duas invisisset hebdomadas, redditae mihi sunt unâ cum CHRISTIERNI LANGIJ tuae literae, ad quas tunc respondendi commoditas non dabatur. Videntur hae ultimae tuae, licet lenire et excusare quaedam in prioribus laborent, a primis tamen illis motu proprio ad me datis [non] longe abesse, et privilegijs scatere iudicis, quae tamen tibi lubens condono, siquidem non aliter vis. Obijcis ab initio post ea, quae de LANGIO et Anglicis literis habes (ad quae postea respondebo) quod iter tuum iuxta promissum non praestitum arguam. Et quid ni? cum sponte promiseris, nec praestiteris? Valetudinem tuam, rem domesticam et anni tempus tunc aeque noras atque postea. Miraris igitur me ita singula tua verba et iota notare. Atqui scis me semper Geometrice loqui, cum serio loquor. Quod et in alijs, praesertim Amicis, quibus bene fido, et requiro et observo. Quorsum enim verba, nisi rebus consona? Hoc ego idem sentio atque illudere et negligere. Neque perinde esse, ac candide, aperte et libere agere, uti tu vis. Immo quiddam his repugnans.</p><p>Quod aliquo loco non sis inter reliquos Pharisaeos et Gnathones, equidem tibi congratulor, nec hanc felicitatem invideo. At quod non talia antea affectâris, quodque adhuc ijs, si liceret, te participem non redderes, haud dixerim. Si autem me hîc fallis, est cur tibi hanc animi celsitudinem et constantiam magis adhuc congratulor. Et si nunc ex Politicis non pendes, veluti antea, cordatius forsan agis. Eorum fructus sunt prae foribus. Cudentur Machiavellistarum doli et technae in ipsorum, uti meriti sunt, capita. Quod et hactenus iustus iudex exequi solitus est. Quod prandio alicuius interfueris, nihil ad me. Neque ob id ea de re mentionem feci, quia hoc morarer, sed quoniam tu occasionem dabas de ERICO LANGIO et me quaedam addendo, quae tamen neuter nostrum curat. Si talibus, qui astutam vapido servant sub pectore vulpem, fidis, te ipsum decipis. Qui et quales eiusmodi sint, fere omnes in Dania norunt, atque in posterum plus forte noscent. O patria, o mores. Omnia nunc ruere in peius fata ipsa docebant. Si tibi deinceps vixeris, uti scribis, rectissime feceris. Nam uti nunc agitur scena Mundana, si unquam antea, locum meretur illud NASONIS »nimium praelustria vita«, et »bene qui latuit, bene vixit«. Quod mihi iam tandem congratularis mutationem hanc et sortem aliam, rectius facis, quam cum prius eandem indoleres, tanquam invidens mihi hanc felicitatem. Sic enim ego infirmas accipio querelas. Et, si sufficienter delictum illud expiare volueris, tacita quaedam psalmodia Uraniae nostrae omnibusque nobis Propempticum concinna, quodque ea e Cimmerijs, non dico Cimbricis, tenebris, quibus diu multumque offuscabatur, sese tandem Superum dono et provisu vindicârit, atque in apertam et gratam Universae Europae lucem universalior reddita asseruerit, congratulare; nobis autem, quod tot Machiavellismis, iniurijs, odijs, invidijs, malevolentijs, calumnijs et detrectationibus atque contemptibus superatis tranquilliori aurae restituti simus. Hisce igitur si Carmine aliquo, uti Poëta es, applauseris, apposite feceris et in gratiam cum Urania nobisque omnibus redieris.</p><p>Rogas deinceps, ut desinam minuta quaeque in literis tuis non recte intellecta exaggerare. Te scripsisse, quod tu cum familia mei essetis amici in Dania despecti, me statim accepisse, vos a me despectos. Atqui tua non tam clare sonabant verba, pro ut nunc ea explicas. Aiebas enim: Vale,salutatus a tuis amicis in Dania despectis. Non addidisti, te aut tuis. Ideoque tuam sententiam prima fronte non satis assecutus sum. Ingerebat hoc dubium, quod scirem, mihi in Dania a calumniatoribus imputari, quod in despectum patriae, Regis, consangvineorum et amicorum inde discesserim. Et sane OLIGERUS ROSENKRANTZIUS, cui haec tua verba legenda dedi, non aliter capere potuit, quam ac si tale quid insinuares. Obscurius enim quam decuit, scripta sunt. Nec ego plane asserui, te quidpiam in me loqui. Ideoque addidi: »si recte tuam mentem percipio« aut simile quid. Et quod tu infers, me excepisse, quasi vos me despiceretis, scilicet tu et tui, id non saltem est minutula quaeque exaggerare, sed et sensum meum quasi data opera plane pervertere: cum de te et tuis, quos semper dilexi atque etiamnum diligo, nihil sinistri suspicer, sed in genere de meis in Dania malevolis locutus sim, a quibus despicior, eosque vicissim ex alto rideo et despicio, si quando prae altioribus curis eorum (quod tamen rarissime facio) meminisse lubet. Tuos casus, tuaque infortunia magna ex parte scio, et horum authores non ignoro. Habemus enim hîc mutuo participationem quandam, licet mihi nihil mali inde evenerit, sed longe plus boni etiam invitis authoribus. At nihil tibi hîc exprobro, condolui semper, et amovere ista studui et cum quorundam ex meis offensione, et plus pro te laboravi, quam conscius es, aut facile crediturus. Norunt id utriusque nostrum, non saltem Amici, sed et inimici, tum quoque neutrales.</p><p>Quod neque tu neque ego moriemur inulti, omnino mihi persuadeo. Nam iustus est Deus, etiam tacentibus et patienter sustinentibus, et quae immerito pertulerunt hominibus, illi vindicta danda est, oc hand vil rette det nod, om † Thule nogen sdoon end aldrig leffde eller haffde leffuet: Non tamen Planetarum revolutiones praeficiendae sunt ei, qui libere et sine tempore, in tempore tamen satque tempestive agit.</p><p>Demum de victualibus mentionem facis, quod primum et praecipuum esse [de]buit. De his enim nunc maxime agitur in tanta hîc annonae caritate, quanta vix unquam fuit, et familiae meae propria, quae quartum attingit septenarium, praeter eos, qui indies invisunt. At tu videris hîc aurem claudere, paa dansche at vaere schulørit, oc icke gide høret oc mercket ret. Nam quid pro quo infers. Satis scio, etiam absque tuo iuramento (saa sandt hielpe mig Gud) (an hoc Theologicum est?) quod si praesens ipse fuissem, non potuissem ista expedire aliter sine rerum dispendio: scilicet postquam hyems intervenisset, portus conclusi essent, nec terrestre iter commodum foret. At antea, dum primas istas tuas scribebas, nulla eiusmodi erant obstacula, aut si qua erant, quare adeo categorice promisisti? quare tot tantaque praestanda enumerasti? Sororem etiam meam in auxilium vocans, a qua ne verbum intellexi, licet ter eidem scripserim. Crediderim vos omnes excellenter iudicare. At si quid vobis Scanicae, id est verae et fidei tenacis, restat mentis, en nulla nunc adsunt subterfugia, quin recte facere liceat, modo libeat. Ego nullum in me officium ingratum esse sinam. Et si adhuc accesseris unâ cum LANGIO aut etiam solus sine ipso, idque ante Paschatis festum (modo fieri queat) inprimis erit id mihi acceptum et eo plenius priora diluentur errata.</p><p>Vale cum uxore a me salutata, totaque domo.<br>Dabantur Wandesburgi die 18. Februarij Anni 1598.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel.</b></p><p>Lately, when Oluf Rosenkrantz — my kinsman, relation-by-marriage, and beloved friend — had visited me here for two weeks, your letters were delivered to me together with that of Christen Lange, [letters] to which there was then no convenient occasion of replying. These latest letters of yours, though they labour to soften and excuse certain things in the earlier ones, nonetheless seem [not] to be far removed from those first ones sent to me of your own accord, and to teem with a judge's prerogatives — which, however, I gladly pardon you, since you will have it no otherwise. At the outset, after the matters you raise concerning Lange and the English letters (to which I shall reply later), you object that I charge you with not having made your journey according to your promise. And why not [charge you with it]? — since you promised of your own accord, and did not perform it. Your health, your household affairs, and the season of the year you knew then just as well as afterwards. You wonder, then, that I so mark each of your words and every jot. But you know that I always speak geometrically, when I speak in earnest; which in others too, and especially in friends whom I trust well, I both require and observe. For to what end are words, unless they answer to facts? This I hold to be one and the same thing as to mock and to be negligent — and not at all the same as to deal candidly, openly, and freely, as you would have it; rather, [it is] something contrary to these.</p><p>That you are in some place not among the rest of the Pharisees and Gnathos, I do indeed congratulate you, nor do I envy you this good fortune. But that you did not before aspire to such things, and that even now you would not, if it were allowed, make yourself a partaker in them — that I would not say. If, however, you are here deceiving me, there is the more reason why I should congratulate you on this loftiness and constancy of mind. And if you now do not hang upon Politics, as before, you act perhaps more shrewdly. Their fruits are at the door. The wiles and devices of the Machiavellians will be hammered down upon their own heads, as they have deserved — which a just judge has hitherto been wont to carry out. That you were present at someone's dinner is nothing to me. Nor did I make mention of that matter on that account, as though I were troubled by it, but because you gave occasion [for it] by adding certain things about Erik Lange and me — things which, however, neither of us cares about. If you trust such men, who keep a cunning fox hidden beneath a stale breast, you deceive your own self. Who and what sort such men are, almost all in Denmark know, and hereafter will perhaps know more. O fatherland, O manners! That all things now rush headlong to the worse, the very fates were teaching. If henceforth you live for yourself, as you write, you will have done most rightly. For as the worldly stage is now played, if ever before, that saying of Naso [Ovid] deserves its place — »[shun] a life too brightly conspicuous«, and »he who has lain well hidden, has lived well«. That you now at last congratulate me on this change and altered lot, you do more rightly than when you earlier grieved over the same, as though begrudging me this good fortune. For thus do I take feeble complaints. And, if you should wish sufficiently to expiate that fault, compose some silent psalmody, a Send-off-song [Propempticon], for our Urania and for us all; and congratulate [her] that she has at last, by the gift and provision of those above, claimed herself out of those Cimmerian — I do not say Cimbric — shadows, by which she was long and greatly darkened, and, made more universal, has set herself free into the open and welcome light of all Europe; and [congratulate] us, that, so many Machiavellisms, injuries, hatreds, envies, ill-wills, calumnies, and disparagements and contempts being overcome, we have been restored to a calmer air. If, therefore, you applaud these things with some Poem, since you are a Poet, you will have done it fittingly, and will have returned into favour with Urania and with us all.</p><p>You ask, next, that I cease to magnify each small thing in your letters not rightly understood. [You say] that you wrote that you, with your family, my friends, were despised in Denmark, [and] that I straightway took it that you were despised by me. But your words did not sound so clearly as you now explain them. For you said: »Farewell, greeted by your friends despised in Denmark.« You did not add »by you« or »by yours«. And so at first sight I did not sufficiently grasp your meaning. This doubt was thrust [upon me] because I knew it to be imputed to me by slanderers in Denmark that I had departed thence in contempt of my fatherland, my King, my kinsmen and my friends. And indeed Oluf Rosenkrantz, to whom I gave these words of yours to read, could take them no otherwise than as if you were insinuating something of the sort. For they were written more obscurely than was fitting. Nor did I plainly assert that you were saying anything against me; and so I added: »if I rightly perceive your meaning« or something similar. And as for what you infer — that I took it as though you despised me, namely you and yours — that is not merely to magnify each little thing, but even, as if of set purpose, plainly to pervert my sense: since concerning you and yours, whom I have always loved and even now love, I suspect nothing sinister, but spoke in general of those ill-willed toward me in Denmark, by whom I am despised, and whom I in turn laugh at and despise from on high, whenever (which, however, I very rarely do) it pleases me, in the midst of higher cares, to remember them. Your misfortunes and your reverses I know for the most part, and I am not ignorant of their authors. For we here have a certain shared portion of them in common, though no harm has come to me from that quarter, but far more good, even against the will of their authors. But I reproach you with nothing here; I have always condoled with you, and have striven to remove those troubles even at the cost of giving offence to certain of my own people, and I have laboured more for you than you are aware of, or would readily believe. This both [our] Friends and [our] enemies — and the neutrals too — know, of us both.</p><p>That neither you nor I shall die unavenged, I am wholly persuaded. For God is just, even toward those who keep silent and patiently endure; and to men [for those things] which they have undeservedly suffered, vengeance must be rendered by Him — and he will set that wrong to rights, even if † Thule nobody at all… now never lived or had lived: yet the revolutions of the Planets are not to be set in command over Him who acts freely and outside time, yet in time, and timely enough.</p><p>At last you make mention of provisions, which ought to have been the first and chief thing. For of these is now most of all the question, in such dearth of grain here as has scarcely ever been; and [provisions] proper to my household, which reaches the fourth seven [i.e. some twenty-eight persons], besides those who visit daily. But you seem here to close your ear — in Danish, to be hard of hearing, and not to care to hear and mark rightly. For you draw a <i>quid pro quo</i>. I know well enough, even without your oath (»so truly help me God« — is this Theological?), that if I myself had been present, I could not have despatched those matters otherwise without loss of goods: namely, after winter had set in, the harbours were closed, nor was a journey by land convenient. But before — while you were writing those first letters of yours — there were no obstacles of the kind; or if there were any, why did you promise so categorically? why did you enumerate so many and so great things to be performed? Calling even my sister to your aid — from whom I have not understood a word, though I have written to her three times. I would believe that you all judge excellently. But if there remains to you any Scanian, that is, true and faithful-keeping, mind, behold, no evasions now present themselves but that it may be possible to do rightly, only let it please [you]. I will let no service done me be ungrateful. And if you should still come, together with Lange, or even alone without him — and that before the feast of Easter (provided it can be done) — that will be especially acceptable to me, and the earlier errors will thereby be the more fully washed away.</p><p>Farewell, with your wife greeted by me, and your whole household.<br>Given at Wandsbek, the 18th day of February, in the year 1598.<br>Your TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), Danish historian, royal historiographer, ballad-collector and clergyman of Ribe; a long-standing friend (and, by this date, a strained one) of Tycho's. The protocol's controlled person-list fixes this identification (§5.6).</li><li><b>Sender / place / date.</b> Tycho writes from <b>Wandsbek</b> (Lat. <i>Wandesburgum</i>), the estate near Hamburg where, in self-imposed exile after leaving Hven and Denmark (1597), he stayed with Heinrich Rantzau and saw the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i> and the <i>Epistolarum</i> volume through the press. Dateline <i>die 18. Februarij Anni 1598</i> (O.S.; Denmark and the German Protestant correspondents kept Old Style). The catalogue label agrees (<i>1598. 18. februar. Wandsburg</i>).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantz</i> = <b>Oluf (Holger) Rosenkrantz</b>, Tycho's kinsman and relation by marriage (<i>consanguineus Affinis</i>). <i>Christiernus Langius</i> = <b>Christen Lange</b>. <i>Ericus Langius</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), the alchemist nobleman, betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie Brahe — hence the \"sister\" (<i>soror mea</i>) called to Vedel's aid near the letter's end.</li><li><b>Latin language note.</b> The letter is <b>Neo-Latin throughout</b>, with four short <b>Danish</b> interpolations (see below); the heuristic tag `la` is correct here. Spelling is Dreyer's edition: consonantal <i>j</i> in <i>-ijs</i> (<i>privilegijs, odijs, Cimmerijs</i>), <i>Geometrice, Machiavellistae</i>.</li><li><b>CRUX — the editorial `[non]`.</b> <i>a primis tamen illis … [non] longe abesse</i> — the `[non]` is <b>Dreyer's bracketed editorial insertion</b>, not in the witness as transmitted; it is kept bracketed in the Source block and rendered bracketed (\"<b>[not]</b> … far removed\") in the translation per protocol (no editorial word inserted unbracketed). With the <i>[non]</i>, Tycho says Vedel's softer \"last letters\" are <i>not</i> far from the offensive \"first\" ones — i.e. still objectionable, consistent with the reproachful continuation (\"teem with a judge's prerogatives\"). <b>Without</b> the <i>[non]</i> the clause would reverse to \"do seem far removed [= an improvement],\" which the following sentences contradict. The negation is therefore load-bearing; the sense follows the bracketed reading but the bracket is preserved and the dependence flagged. Likewise <i>[de]buit</i> later in the source is Dreyer's bracketed expansion of <i>debuit</i>.</li><li><b>`Geometrice loqui`</b> — \"to speak geometrically,\" i.e. with exact, demonstrative precision (each word answering to fact \"like a geometric proof\"); a characteristic Tychonic self-description, not a technical-astronomy term — left as the plain idiom.</li><li><b>`Pharisaeos et Gnathones`</b> — \"Pharisees and Gnathos\": hypocrites (Scriptural) and parasitic flatterers (Gnatho, the toady of Terence's <i>Eunuchus</i>); kept as types.</li><li><b>`astutam vapido servant sub pectore vulpem`</b> — \"keep a cunning fox hidden beneath a stale/sour breast\": a near-quotation of Persius, <i>Satires</i> 5.117 (<i>astutam vapido servas sub pectore vulpem</i>); kept as the borrowed image.</li><li><b>Ovid (`NASONIS`).</b> Both tags are from <b>Ovid, <i>Tristia</i> III.4</b> (Ovid = P. Ovidius <b>Naso</b>): <i>»nimium praelustria vita«</i> compresses <i>Tristia</i> III.4.4–6 (<i>vive sine invidia … nimium … praelustria vita[s]</i> — \"shun a life too brightly conspicuous\"); <i>»bene qui latuit, bene vixit«</i> = <i>Tristia</i> III.4.25 verbatim (\"he who has lain well hidden has lived well\"). Tycho quotes them as a paired sententia; kept as quotation, the elliptical <i>vita</i> of the first tag flagged (the verb is suppressed — \"shun\" is supplied in brackets-sense and noted here rather than smoothed).</li><li><b>`Propempticum` / Urania.</b> <i>Propempticum</i> (Greek <i>προπεμπτικόν</i>) = a <b>send-off / bon-voyage poem</b>; Tycho asks the poet Vedel to write Urania (the Muse of astronomy = Tycho's restored astronomy, <i>astronomia instaurata</i>) a farewell ode on her departure from Denmark. <i>psalmodia</i> = \"psalmody,\" a (silent) song of praise.</li><li><b>`Cimmerijs, non dico Cimbricis, tenebris`</b> — a <b>bilingual/learned pun</b>: the <i>Cimmerian</i> darkness of Homer/Ovid (proverbial deep gloom) set against <i>Cimbric</i> (= <b>Danish/Jutish</b>, from the Cimbri of Jutland). Tycho says astronomy escaped \"Cimmerian — I do not say Cimbric [= Danish] — shadows\": a barbed gibe at Denmark, made deniable by the near-homophone. Kept and glossed, the wordplay flagged rather than flattened.</li><li><b>DANISH interpolation 1 (dagger-marked CRUX).</b> <i>oc hand vil rette det nod, om † Thule nogen sdoon end aldrig leffde eller haffde leffuet</i>. The opening is clear early-modern Danish: <i>oc hand vil rette det nød</i> = \"and He will set that distress/wrong to rights.\" The remainder is <b>corrupt or obscure in the witness</b>: <i>Thule</i> (the classical name for the far North = Denmark/Scandinavia) + <i>nogen</i> (\"anyone/nobody\") + <i>sdoon</i> (an unintelligible form — possibly a copyist's garble) + <i>end</i> + <i>aldrig leffde eller haffde leffuet</i> (\"…never lived or had lived\"). The <b>†</b> marks the editor's locus of corruption. A coherent surface sense cannot be recovered without conjecture; rendered word-for-word and explicitly flagged (\"† Thule nobody at all… now never lived or had lived\"), <b>not</b> smoothed into fluent English. This is the letter's principal crux.</li><li><b>DANISH interpolation 2.</b> <i>paa dansche at vaere schulørit, oc icke gide høret oc mercket ret</i> = \"in Danish, [namely] to be hard of hearing [<i>skørørt/skuløret</i> 'thick of ear'], and not to care to hear and mark rightly.\" Tycho code-switches into Danish to characterise Vedel's wilful deafness; the <i>quid pro quo</i> immediately following keeps the legal/rhetorical idiom.</li><li><b>DANISH interpolation 3 (oath).</b> <i>saa sandt hielpe mig Gud</i> = \"so truly [may] God help me\" — a standard Danish oath-formula; Tycho parenthetically and sarcastically queries whether such swearing is <i>Theologicum</i> (becoming of a clergyman like Vedel). Kept in Danish in the Source, glossed in translation.</li><li><b>`quartum attingit septenarium`</b> — \"reaches the fourth seven,\" i.e. his household numbers in the <b>fourth group of seven</b> (toward 28 persons); rendered literally with the sense bracketed, not silently converted to a round figure.</li><li><b>`Scanicae … mentis`</b> — \"a Scanian mind,\" i.e. of <b>Scania</b> (Skåne), the southern-Swedish province then Danish and the Brahe family's home country; Tycho glosses it himself as \"true and faithful-keeping,\" punning on regional character. Kept and glossed.</li><li><b>`annonae caritate`</b> — \"dearth of grain/provisions\"; <i>annona</i> = the grain-supply/market price (cf. the same idiom in the 1571 Wolf letter). The 1597–98 northern dearth is the real-world referent.</li><li><b>Negation audit.</b> The source's negations were counted and reproduced one-for-one, with particular care at the bracketed <i>[non] longe abesse</i> (see CRUX), at <i>Non addidisti, te aut tuis</i> (\"You did <b>not</b> add 'by you' or 'by yours'\"), at <i>nihil sinistri suspicer</i> / <i>nihil tibi exprobro</i>, and at the closing <i>Non tamen Planetarum revolutiones praeficiendae sunt</i> (\"the revolutions of the Planets are <b>not</b> to be set in command\"). No negation was added or dropped.</li><li><b>`Planetarum revolutiones … qui libere et sine tempore, in tempore tamen … agit`</b> — <i>revolutio</i> here in its astrological/astronomical sense (planetary cycles, §5.1/5.3) but turned theological: the planetary cycles must not be made to govern God, \"who acts freely and outside time, yet in time, and timely enough.\" Kept literal.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Single date, internally consistent: <i>18. Februarij Anni 1598</i> (O.S.). No in-letter date discrepancy; label and dateline agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin body is rendered securely (a [A]/[B]-grade translation on its own), but the letter carries two genuine, named cruxes that bar a clean grade: (1) the <b>dagger-marked Danish phrase</b> <i>»om † Thule nogen sdoon end aldrig leffde eller haffde leffuet«</i>, which is corrupt/obscure in the witness and is deliberately left unsmoothed; and (2) the <b>editorial `[non]`</b> at <i>»[non] longe abesse«</i>, a Dreyer insertion on which the polarity of a key sentence turns. Both are flagged above rather than resolved.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII: the daggered Danish <i>om † Thule … sdoon</i> is printed with the same corruption marked (unrecovered), and the editorial <i>[non]</i> at <i>[non] longe abeße</i> is confirmed as Dreyer's own polarity-load-bearing insertion. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.25-28 as page images (Read pages=1-4 in one chunk; images small enough not to need further splitting). The letter itself runs only pp.25(bottom)-27(top) - slice p.4 (TBOO p.28) is the start of a different letter (Monavius to Tycho) and was excluded from collation. Collated the full letter text - opening (NUPER cum OLIGERUS ROSENKRANTZ...), the Ovid tag (NASONIS 'nimium praelustria vita'/'bene qui latuit, bene vixit'), every proper name (Oligerus Rosenkrantz, Christiernus Langius, Ericus Langius), the embedded Danish clauses (paa dansche at vaere schulørit; saa sandt hielpe mig Gud; oc hand vil rette det nod...), and the closing date (Wandesburgi die 18. Februarij Anni 1598) - against the Dreyer images word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; no divergence between source_text and Dreyer's print (one trivial dagger/asterisk footnote-marker variant before 'Thule' is orthographic apparatus, not text, and is not counted as a defect).</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 179. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15901005001", "next": "brahe-t_15980410001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Rebukes Vedel for adopting a judge's tone and for failing to keep a promised journey, addressing also some matters concerning Lange and English letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980218001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980307001", "d": "1598-03-07", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Monavius", "ck": "Jacob Monavius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wrocław", "t": "Jakob Monau → Tycho Brahe — Wrocław, 7 March 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek and one German tag-verse) · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IACOBUS MONAVIUS AD TYCHONEM<br>BRAHE.S.BONA quidem fide, sed paulo tardius mihi redditus est et in manus<br>meas pervenit fasciculus ille, quem circa finem anni superioris ad me misisti,<br>Nobilissime et praestantissime Domine TYCHO, magnamque debeo gratiam Viro optimo<br>et amico integerrimo Domino VINCENTIO<br>MOLLERO, quod in eo curando voluit tibique mihique gratificari.<br>Vix autem possum exprimere verbis, quam grata mihi fuerit Epistola tua, tam<br>amice scripta, et quam accepta ea, quae unâ cum literis tuis ad me pervenire<br>voluisti. Caeterum quod tam serum responsum mitto, non tantum fit eam ob causam,<br>de qua dixi, sed etiam propter invaletudinem meam, quam totis fere tribus<br>mensibus sensi prorsusA incommodum, et quae me velut ineptum fecitA ad omnes res agendas. Nondum quidem ab ea sum prorsusA liberatus. Sed tamen per Dei gratiam, habere coepi melius, et sentio vires<br>tum animi tum corporis non parum firmiores, quam fuerunt. Deus Opt. Max. si<br>volet, poterit hoc robur augere, idque tantum cupio fieri ad gloriam sancti<br>nominis ipsius, et ad animae meae, quae mihi est cura in his terris praecipua,<br>salutem. Quod attinet ad famam obitus mei isthuc ad te perlatam, unde tibi data<br>occasio faciendi mentionem nominis mei in Epistolis tuis, facile potuit hic<br>error committi in aula Caesaris, ubi fratrem habui unicum Medicum Imperatorium,<br>Virum, quem ab alijs, quam a me praedicari malo, qui [ante] annos decem obijt Pragae. Quia autem<br>hic error tibi expressitA tam honorificas et amicas voces de me, ut inde, quanti memoriam nominis<br>mei post obitum meum facturus fueris, aestimare liceat:<br>agnosco me plurimum debere tuis erga me amori et animo, quem utA mihi conserves, donec vixero, obnixe rogo; ego vicissim, donec in hoc<br>corpore sangvis erit, te vicissim amare observare et colere...praedicare et<br>laudare non desinam. Frater ille meus charissimus, cuius modo feci mentionem,<br>erat rerum Mathematicarum et amantissimus et intelligentissimus, ac propterea<br>tui quoque unice observans et studiosus: ac illum saepe hoc egisse [scio] ut amicitiam tuam consequeretur, a qua cura etiam non abstinuisset, si<br>Deus ei longiora vitae spatia concessisset. Nunc in alia Schola philosophatur,<br>coram Deum cernens, et coelos opera manuum eius contemplans......amori erga<br>fratrem, si memoriam illius hoc loco tibi volui commendare. Caeterum quod ad<br>discessum tuum e patria, et praesertim ex Uranoburgo attinet: primum de eo certior factus sum, ex literis<br>communis amici Domini DAVIDIS.....postea enim plenius<br>edoctus de omnibus illis circumstantijs a CHRISTIANO SEVERINO<br>LONGOMONTANO, qui circa finem anni superioris ad nos<br>venit.......et quem per aliquot dies apud nos retinuimus, et hospitem<br>gratissimum habuimus; atque utinam tunc venissent ad me literae tuae, cum adhuc<br>apud nos haereret. Erat enim de te valde sollicitus et hac inprimis de causa.Ego quidem de ijs quid dicam? Cum essem aliquando Magdeburgi, vidi super domo quadam aureis<br>literis inscriptum saxo, quod erat supra fores, hunc ineptum<br>sensu, sed reipsa nimis verum versum: Est erit atque fuit<br>Vndanck in fine laborum. Hic versiculus mihi subinde venit in<br>mentem, quando de te rebusque tuis cogitavi. Sed et hoc mihi incidit, quod si<br>mundus hic non sit dignus istis Atlanticis bonis et donis nostri TYCHONIS? Etiam<br>hoc cogitavi, quod si haec Τύχη, quae Domino TYCHONI contigit, nunciet instare<br>finem mundi? Utut sit, adhuc valde dubito, tuamne vicem [an patriae potius tuae], imo totius Reipublicae Christianae magis dolere debeam. Legi diligenter,<br>quae ad me misisti, et pro ijs communicatis magnas tibi gratias ago, neque est,<br>ut putes, ea ventura in manus aliorum. Miratus sum Regem nihil voluisse conferre<br>in haec studia, Regibus et eorum subsidijs atque [adiumento] dignissima, qui tamen nuper tantos sumptus fecitA in coronationis festo. Aliud fuit iudicium ALFONSI: imo aliud etiam ipsius patris<br>FRIDERICI. Nolo de his plura; in<br>quibus tamen hoc etiam addo, me idem facere, quod tu facis, hoc est discernere<br>Actorem a Poëta fabulaeA. Facis tu etiam hoc praeclare, quod tanto animo (nimirum coelo et astris<br>digno) haec quae tibi contigerunt, fers, nec propterea cogitas aliquid omittere in<br>laudabili cursu abs te.....et olim instituto, et per tot annos servato, sed quod<br>cogitas ad metam usque illum.....Da hic Deus ut perficias quod coepisti. LAZARUS<br>....... vir magnanimus frequenter in ore solitus fuit habere versum istum: Esse aliquid si velle tenes, et posse tenebis. Tibi vero<br>datum est et velle et posse. Amplissimi viri D. HENRICI RANZOVIJ maior nunc erit<br>gloria, et fiet ipsius nomen illustre clarius, postquam Musis tuis hospitium conditA. Erit enim vicina urbs Hamburga,<br>tibi satis oportuna ad absolvenda ea, quorum editio fuit coepta in Huenna. De consilijs tuis, quae apud ........ meum<br>deponis, diligenter cogitavi, et spero ea sic geri posse, ut [non] plane careat successu, verum opus erit tempore: cum nunc omnia, quae<br>aguntur in aula Caesarea, tantum ....... Martem in hoc gravissimo Reipublicae<br>christianae periculo, quod hoc anno non sine causa (Deus omnia avertat) metuitur.<br>Agam interea, quantum nunc agi potest per amicos, ut quasi via quaedam sternatur,<br>ad ea, quae sunt deinceps ..... cura et opera tractanda; faciam te quoque<br>certiorem de rebus omnibus, quae argumentum facere ad ea, quae sane tui animi,<br>consilij et instituti.... Si viveret adhuc Dominus IACOBUS CURTIUS, quantopere ostenderet<br>sibi esse cordi et curae res tuas et negotium publicum! Sed tamen spero, non<br>defuturos aliter etiam in aula amicos, de quibus alio tempore, si Deus volet,<br>rebus magis exploratis te monebo. Pro transmisso libro Epistolarum, qui mihi a<br>te est amplissimum munus, magnas ago gratias, et peto magnopere, cum reliqua<br>tua, quae iam coepta sunt, ad finem perducta fuerint, ut ijs etiam ....... meam<br>ornare non recuses. Dabitur opera, ne te huius ...... et beneficij iure et<br>merito poeniteat.Literas tuas ad IOHANNEM<br>ROTHMANNUM, quem cognovi nunc vitam agere in<br>Republica Erfurdensi, per certos homines curaboA, si quidA aliud a me postulas fieri, semper tibi parata erit pro viribus fida mea<br>opera. Ex CHRISTIANO tuo hoc etiam libenter cognovi: Te nimirum natum esse<br>eodem et anno et mense, quo ego sum in lucem editus, valde etiam delectatus sum,<br>inter...quae nunc mecum communicasti, lectione carminum tuorum ad ........ quae<br>non legi sine varijs animi perturbationibus. Multa enim de multis, tum tuis tum<br>ijs quae sunt similia, cogitaviA. GeramA tibi morem in eo quod petis, et curabo, ne aliquis te non indignus<br>........ de rebus tuis, quae te digna sint. Tu vicissim, si non est molestum,<br>exorna meum Symbolum aliquo Epigrammate. Quale sit Symbolum meum, cognosces ex<br>nostro VINCENTIO MOLLERO, qui, si<br>voles, dabit tibi legendum Libellum quendam nostrum, quem de eodem Symbolo<br>curavi ..... publice edi. Equidem miseram ad te exemplum, quod doleo perijsse.<br>Si postuletis aliud, non invitus communicabo. Interim habe tibi hanc<br>inscriptionem ..... mei.Salutat te reverenter amanter et officiose optimus senex Doctor<br>HERMANNUS noster, cui vix credas<br>quam grata fuerit salus et abs te dicta, et nomine praestantissimi senisA...Si non est molestum, rogo ut meis etiam verbis reverenter et<br>officiose salutes Illustrem Dominum HENRICUM<br>RANZOVIUM, qui suam erga me benevolentiam semel et iterum mihi<br>declaravit. Tu eam, si placet, augebis et confirmabis. Et<br>quoniam HEMMINGIJ<br>vestriA facta est mentio, tuque de eo nunc liberius scribere potes, rogo te per<br>amicitiam nostram, dic mihi in aures meas, an fidem mereatur haec inclusa<br>pagella, quae ante aliquot annos fuit circumlata.Deus scit me istud quaerere nec malo animo, nec ad malam fidem<br>...... enim amo et colo istum optimum et optime meritum senem, quem aliquis cum<br>potest amare et colere, sed tantum coepi mihi constare historiam rei gestae, de<br>qua mihi et alijs semper iure integrum iudicium. Bene et feliciter vale, vir<br>praestantissime et Nobilissime, mihique conserva tuam ...... benevolentiam, cuius<br>fundamenta prima, eaque tunc valde tenuia ...... sunt ante multos annos .....<br>cum ex tuis peregrinationibus reversus per aliquot septimanas viveres in domo et<br>mensa hospitis tunc mei M. LAURENTIJ DANI. Ego te amare et<br>observare, et meam tibi fidem quocunque potero genere ........... nunquam<br>desinam.VratislaviaeA VII. Martij tempore eclipseos Solis<br>1598.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Jakob Monau to Tycho Brahe. Greeting.</b></p><p>In good faith indeed, but a little too late, was that packet delivered to me and came into my hands which you sent me toward the end of last year, most noble and most excellent Master Tycho; and I owe great thanks to that best of men and most upright friend Master Vincent Moller, in that, in seeing to its delivery, he was willing to do a kindness both to you and to me. I can scarcely express in words how welcome your letter was to me, written in so friendly a way, and how acceptable were those things which you wished to reach me together with your letter. As for my sending so late a reply, it comes about not only for the reason I have mentioned, but also because of my ill-health, which for nearly three whole months I felt to be wholly[A] troublesome, and which made[A] me, as it were, unfit for transacting any business. I am indeed not yet wholly[A] freed of it. Yet nevertheless, by the grace of God, I have begun to be better, and I feel the powers both of mind and of body not a little stronger than they were. God, Best and Greatest, if he will, can increase this strength; and I desire it to come about only for the glory of his holy name, and for the salvation of my soul, which is my chief care in these lands. As for the report of my death carried to you there — whence you were given occasion to make mention of my name in your letters — this error could easily be committed at the Emperor's court, where I had an only brother, an Imperial Physician, a man whom I would rather have praised by others than by me, who[A] died at Prague ten years ago. But because this error drew[A] from you such honourable and friendly words about me, that from them one may estimate how greatly you would have honoured the memory of my name after my death: I acknowledge that I owe very much to your love and goodwill toward me, which I earnestly beg that[A] you keep for me as long as I shall live; and I in turn, so long as there is blood in this body, shall not cease to love, esteem, and honour you in turn … to proclaim and to praise you. That dearest brother of mine, of whom I have just made mention, was both a most devoted and a most understanding student of mathematical matters, and on that account uniquely respectful of you too and devoted to you; and [I know][A] that he often did this, that he might gain your friendship — from which care he would not have refrained, had God granted him a longer span of life. Now he philosophizes in another School, beholding God face to face, and contemplating the heavens, the work of his hands …… [out of] love toward my brother, [it is] if I have wished in this place to commend his memory to you. For the rest, as touching your departure from your homeland, and especially from Uraniborg: I was first made more certain of it by a letter from our common friend Master David …… for afterward I was more fully informed of all those circumstances by Christian Sørensen Longomontanus, who came to us toward the end of last year ……, and whom we kept with us for some days and held a most welcome guest; and would that your letter had come to me then, while he was still tarrying with us. For he was very anxious about you, and chiefly on this account. As for me, what indeed should I say of these things? When I was once at Magdeburg, I saw inscribed in golden letters on a stone, on a certain house, which was above the door, this verse — silly in its sense, but in fact only too true: It is, it will be, and it has been — Ingratitude at the end of labours [Vndanck in fine laborum]. This little verse comes to my mind again and again, whenever I have thought about you and your affairs. And this too occurred to me: what if this world is not worthy of those Atlantean goods and gifts of our Tycho? This too I thought: what if this Chance [Τύχη], which has befallen Master Tycho, announces that the end of the world is at hand? However it be, I am still very much in doubt whether I ought to grieve more for your lot [or rather for your homeland's][A] — nay, for that of the whole Christian Commonwealth. I have diligently read what you sent me, and I give you great thanks for sharing them; nor is it as you might think — that they will come into the hands of others. I marvelled that the King was willing to confer nothing upon these studies, most worthy of kings and of their subsidies and [help][A], who yet lately made[A] such great expenditures at the festival of his coronation. Other was the judgment of Alfonso; nay, other too was that of his own father Frederick. I will say no more of these matters; on which, however, I add this also, that I do the same as you do — that is, to distinguish the Actor from the Poet of the play[A]. You too do this admirably, in that with so great a spirit (worthy indeed of heaven and the stars) you bear these things that have befallen you, nor on that account do you think to leave off anything in that laudable course [begun] by you …… and once instituted and kept for so many years; but you think [to pursue] it right to the goal …… God grant here that you complete what you have begun. Lazarus ……, a great-spirited man, was wont frequently to have on his lips that verse: If you hold to willing, you hold something; and you will hold to being able [Esse aliquid si velle tenes, et posse tenebis]. But to you it is given both to will and to be able. The glory of that most distinguished man Master Heinrich Rantzau will now be the greater, and his illustrious name will become more renowned, now that he founds[A] a lodging for your Muses. For the neighbouring city of Hamburg will be opportune enough for you, for completing those things whose publication was begun on Hven. About your designs, which you deposit with my …… [friend], I have thought diligently, and I hope they can be so managed that they will [not][A] altogether lack success — but there will be need of time: since now all things that are transacted at the Imperial court [look] only to[wards] …… Mars, in this most grave peril of the Christian Commonwealth, which this year, not without cause (God avert all this!), is feared. Meanwhile I shall do what can now be done through friends, so that a way may, as it were, be paved for those things which are next …… to be handled by care and effort; I shall also make you more certain about all matters which make an argument toward those things which are truly [matters] of your own spirit, counsel, and purpose ……[A]. If Master Jacobus Curtius were still living, how greatly he would show that your affairs and the public business were dear and a care to him! But still I hope that friends at court will not be lacking in other ways too, about whom, at another time, if God will, I shall advise you once the matters have been more fully explored. For the book of Letters sent to me, which is from you a most ample gift, I give great thanks, and I greatly ask that, when your remaining works, which are now begun, have been brought to an end, you not refuse to adorn with them my …… [name] also. Pains will be taken that you may not, by right and deservedly, repent of this …… and kindness. Your letters to Johann Rothmann, whom I have learned now leads his life in the Republic of Erfurt, I will[A] see to through trusty men; if[A] you ask anything else of me to be done, my faithful service will always be ready for you to the best of my powers. From your Christian I also gladly learned this — namely, that you were born in the same year and the same month in which I was brought into the light; I was also greatly delighted, among …… the things you have now shared with me, by the reading of your poems to …… which I did not read without various perturbations of mind. For I thought[A] much about many things, both yours and those that are like them. I will gratify[A] you in what you ask, and I will see to it that no one unworthy of you …… about your affairs which are worthy of you. You in turn, if it is not troublesome, embellish my Symbolum with some Epigram. What my Symbolum is, you will learn from our Vincent Moller, who, if you wish, will give you to read a certain little book of ours, which concerning that same Symbolum I had …… published. I had indeed sent you a copy, which I grieve has perished. If you should ask for another, I will not unwillingly share it. Meanwhile, keep for yourself this inscription …… of mine. Our most excellent old man Doctor Hermann greets you reverently, lovingly, and dutifully, to whom you would scarcely believe how welcome was the greeting spoken by you, and in the name of that most excellent old man[A] …… If it is not troublesome, I ask that in my words too you reverently and dutifully greet the Illustrious Master Heinrich Rantzau, who has declared his goodwill toward me once and again. You, if it please, will increase and confirm it. And since mention has been made of your[A] Hemmingius, and you can now write more freely about him, I ask you, by our friendship, tell me in my own ears whether this enclosed little page deserves credence, which some years ago was circulated. God knows that I ask this neither out of ill-will, nor toward bad faith …… for I love and revere that best of men, who has so well deserved — an old man whom one ought to love and revere when one can; but only so that the history of the matter, of which I and others always [had] a free judgment by right, might begin to be clear to me. Fare well and happily, most excellent and most noble man, and keep for me your …… goodwill, whose first foundations — and those then very slight …… — were [laid] many years ago …… when, returned from your travels, you were living for some weeks in the house and at the table of my then host, Master Lauritz Dan. I shall never cease to love and esteem you, and to [pledge] my faith to you by every kind of means I can ……</p><p>At Wrocław,[A] 7 March, at the time of an eclipse of the Sun, 1598.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Iacobus Monavius</i> = <b>Jakob Monau</b> (1546–1603), the Wrocław (Breslau) humanist, poet, and Calvinist patron of letters, celebrated for his personal device (<i>symbolum</i>) \"<i>Pietas contenta lucratur</i>,\" around which he gathered a famous album of contributed epigrams from European scholars — hence his request below that Tycho contribute one. Letter from Tycho's exile period: after losing Hven and royal support, Tycho had left Denmark (mid-1597) and was in 1598 a guest of Heinrich Rantzau in Holstein, working toward Hamburg-area publication.</li><li><b>Dateline / calendar.</b> <i>Vratislaviae VII. Martij … 1598</i> = \"At <b>Wrocław</b> (Breslau, <i>Vratislavia</i>), 7 March 1598.\" The dataset labels this <b>n.s.</b> (New Style); Monau, in Habsburg Silesia, used the Gregorian calendar. Header carries the as-written date with [N.S.]. <i>\"tempore eclipseos Solis\"</i> (\"at the time of a solar eclipse\") is an astronomical timestamp, not a separate date: a partial solar eclipse fell on <b>7 March 1598 N.S.</b> — the dateline is internally consistent and is not converted here, per protocol §3.</li><li><b>MS witness / state of the text.</b> The sole witness is a later transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b> (Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet). The copy is defective: Dreyer's printed text is riddled with lacunae (each `.....` marks lost or illegible words) and with superscript <b>A</b> markers keying his textual notes — chiefly editorial restorations of damaged words. Bracketed words in the source (`[ante]`, `[scio]`, `[non]`, `[an patriae potius tuae]`, `[adiumento]`) are <b>Dreyer's conjectural restorations</b>, reproduced as such; the superscript A's and the `.....` lacunae are preserved verbatim in the Source block and carried as `[A]`-marks and ellipses in the translation rather than silently smoothed. The translation's own bracketed words supply English syntax across the lacunae and are flagged as editorial.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Vincentio Mollero</i> = <b>Vincent(ius) Moller</b> of Hamburg, the intermediary forwarding the packet (corroborated by Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>, citing TBOO VIII 30.20–22).</li><li><i>frater … unicus Medicus Imperatorius … obijt Pragae</i> — Monau's brother <b>Peter Monau (Petrus Monavius, 1551–1588)</b>, personal physician to Emperor Rudolf II, who died at Prague; \"ten years ago\" fits a 1598 letter. The false rumour of <i>Jakob's</i> death (which Tycho had repeated in his published <i>Epistolae</i>) arose by confusion with the brother's real death.</li><li><i>David</i> — <i>\"common friend Master David,\"</i> unidentified here (name stands without further specification in the defective copy); not over-identified.</li><li><i>Christiano Severino Longomontano</i> / <i>Christiano tuo</i> = <b>Christian Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (1562–1647), Tycho's chief assistant, who had visited Monau late in 1597.</li><li><i>Henrici Ranzovij</i> / <i>Henricum Ranzovium</i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), royal Statthalter (viceroy) in Holstein, who gave Tycho hospitality and a base near Hamburg in his exile (corroborated by <i>On Tycho's Island</i>).</li><li><i>Iacobus Curtius</i> = <b>Jacob Kurz von Senftenau (Jacobus Curtius)</b>, Imperial vice-chancellor and a patron of the sciences (d. 1594); Monau wishes him still alive to advance Tycho's cause at court.</li><li><i>Iohannem Rothmannum</i> = <b>Johann Rothmann</b>, then resident in Erfurt (the <i>Republica Erfurdensis</i>); recipient of a letter Monau will forward. (Distinct from Tycho's earlier correspondent Christoph Rothmann of Kassel.)</li><li><i>Hermannus noster</i> — the \"old Doctor Hermann,\" a Wrocław associate of Monau's, not securely identifiable from the damaged text; left unidentified.</li><li><i>Hemmingij vestri</i> — almost certainly <b>Niels Hemmingsen (Nicolaus Hemmingius, 1513–1600)</b>, the Danish theologian (<i>\"your Hemmingius\"</i> — i.e. the Dane); the <i>\"enclosed little page … circulated some years ago\"</i> concerns a contested report about him that Monau asks Tycho to authenticate. Identification is probable but rests partly on the damaged context; flagged.</li><li><i>M. Laurentij Dani</i> = <b>Master Lauritz (Laurentius) Dan</b>, Monau's former host with whom the young Tycho lodged for some weeks on returning from his travels.</li><li><b>The King / Alfonso / Frederick.</b> <i>Regem … nihil voluisse conferre … qui tamen nuper tantos sumptus fecit in coronationis festo</i> — <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> (crowned 1596, hence <i>\"coronation festival\"</i>) withdrew support; <i>iudicium Alfonsi</i> invokes <b>Alfonso X \"the Wise\" of Castile</b>, the royal patron of the Alfonsine Tables, as the model of a king who funded astronomy; <i>ipsius patris Friderici</i> = <b>Frederik II</b>, Tycho's late patron. The contrast (lavish coronation vs. nothing for science) is Monau's, kept unsoftened.</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>Τύχη</i> — \"Chance / Fortune\"; a pun on Tycho's name, exactly as in the Wolf letter's <i>εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών</i>. <i>\"haec Τύχη, quae Domino TYCHONI contigit\"</i> = \"this Chance [Τύχη] which has befallen Master Tycho\" — wordplay on Τύχη / Tycho preserved (transcribed + glossed), not flattened.</li><li><b>The Magdeburg verse (German tag).</b> <i>\"Est erit atque fuit — Vndanck in fine laborum\"</i>: a macaronic Latin-German house-inscription, \"It is, it will be, and it has been — <b>ingratitude at the end of labours</b>.\" <i>Vndanck</i> (German <i>Undank</i>, \"ingratitude / thanklessness\") is kept in the Source-block spelling and glossed; the line is kept as the verse Monau quotes, applied as bitter comment on the world's treatment of Tycho.</li><li><b>The two maxims, kept as verse.</b></li><li><i>\"Esse aliquid si velle tenes, et posse tenebis\"</i> — \"If you hold to willing, you hold something; and you will hold to being able,\" attributed to <b>\"Lazarus … a great-spirited man.\"</b> The name is immediately followed by a lacuna; <b>probably Lazarus von Schwendi</b> (1522–1583, Imperial general and statesman), but the loss prevents certainty — left as \"Lazarus …\" and flagged, not asserted.</li><li>The Atlas image (<i>\"istis Atlanticis bonis et donis\"</i>, \"those Atlantean goods and gifts\") casts Tycho as an Atlas bearing the heavens; kept as metaphor.</li><li><b>Technical / programmatic terms.</b> <i>Symbolum</i> — Monau's personal <b>device/emblem</b> (the kernel of his epigram album); kept untranslated and italicised on first use, per the §5 keep-rule for fixed humanist terms. <i>libro Epistolarum</i> = Tycho's printed <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum libri</i> (Uraniborg, 1596), the \"book of Letters\" Tycho had gifted. <i>\"ea, quorum editio fuit coepta in Huenna\"</i> = the works whose printing had begun on <b>Hven</b> (Uraniborg) and which Tycho hoped to finish near Hamburg. <i>\"discernere Actorem a Poëta fabulae\"</i> — the Stoic-theatrical commonplace of distinguishing the <b>Actor</b> (who merely plays the assigned part) from the <b>Poet/Author of the play</b> (who assigns it): God writes the drama, man acts his role; kept literal.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae.</b> Opening <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem [dicit]</i>, \"greeting\" (§5.5). <i>coram Deum cernens</i> of the dead brother — \"beholding God face to face.\" Closing <i>Bene et feliciter vale</i> = \"Fare well and happily\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Negation check.</b> The two operative negations are reproduced exactly: (1) the King <i>\"nihil voluisse conferre\"</i> — <i>\"was willing to confer <b>nothing</b>,\"</i> and (2) Dreyer's restored <i>\"ut [non] plane careat successu\"</i> — <i>\"that they will <b>not</b> altogether lack success\"</i> (i.e. that the designs may yet partly succeed). The `[non]` is editorial (bracketed in both Source and translation); without it the clause would assert the opposite, so the bracket is load-bearing and is flagged as Dreyer's restoration, not silently absorbed.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin sense is secure where the text is whole, and the translation register matches the exemplars; but the witness is a <b>defective transcript</b> with numerous lacunae (the `.....` gaps) and editorial restorations (`[ante] [scio] [non] [an patriae potius tuae] [adiumento]`) on which sense partly depends. Named cruxes: (a) the <i>\"Lazarus …\"</i> attribution of the <i>velle/posse</i> maxim (probably Lazarus von Schwendi, but the name is broken off); (b) the identity of <i>\"Master David\"</i> and <i>\"Doctor Hermann\"</i> (lost in lacunae, left unidentified); (c) the <i>Hemmingij vestri</i> identification (probably Niels Hemmingsen, but resting on damaged context); (d) several clauses whose syntax is bridged only across Dreyer's restorations and the lacunae (e.g. <i>\"amori erga fratrem, si memoriam …\"</i>; <i>\"quae argumentum facere …\"</i>). These are flagged, not smoothed.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII pp. 42–45: the lacunae (<i>LAZARUS .......</i> with the surname lost; <i>DAVIDIS ......</i>; <i>HEMMINGIJ vestri</i> unglossed) and the bracketed restorations are Dreyer's own — irreducible; the probable identifications (Lazarus von Schwendi; Niels Hemmingsen) are rightly kept as probable. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 slice pages as page images. The letter is heavily lacunose in the source (many '......' gaps and bracketed editorial supplements like [ante], [scio], [an patriae potius tuae], [adiumento], [non]) - collated every one of these against Dreyer and found source_text reproduces Dreyer's own gaps and bracketed supplements exactly, none invented. Verified every proper name and number against Dreyer's print (Vincentio Mollero, the deceased Imperial-Physician brother/'annos decem', Christiano Severino Longomontano, the Magdeburg inscription verse and its exact word order, Alfonsi/Friderici, Lazarus's verse, Henrici Ranzovij/Wandesburgum/Hamburga, Iacobus Curtius, Iohannem Rothmannum/Republica Erfurdensi, Hemmingij, M. Laurentij Dani, date 'Vratislaviae VII Martij ... 1598' matching the letter head). Checked published_english clause by clause against the Latin, including all bracketed English reconstructions against the source's own brackets/ellipses: no fabricated facts, no silently dropped sentences, no flipped pronouns (the 'tibi contigerunt' praise of Tycho bearing his misfortunes is correctly kept as address to Tycho, not turned into self-praise), no hardened hedges, no custody misattribution regarding the gifted 'libro Epistolarum'. Prior published_grade of C is not borne out by this collation; treat as superseded.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains a delayed reply by three months of illness, thanks Tycho for his letter and the items enclosed, and reports recovering health.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980307001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980311001", "d": "1598-03-11", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Vitus Winshemius → Tycho Brahe — Hamburg, 11 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address line) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM GESTRENGEN, EDLEN VND ERNVESTEN HERREN TYCHO BRAHEN ITZO ZU WANDESBECK, MEINEM BESONDERN GUNSTIGEN LIEBEN HERREN VNDT FORDERER.</p><p>GENEROSE et Magnifice D. TYCHO, omni cum observantia colende mihi Domine. Negotium commissum apud D. IOANNEM BOeKELIUM, quomodo confecerim, a M.V. ex scheda inserta apparebit. Duci OTTONI Brunsvicensium et Luneburgensium de sententia Μ.V. diligenter scripsi. C. ipsius omnino acquiescit et..quod M. Vestrae commodum futurum est. De accessu Reginae in hanc civitatem nihil ad me perfertur, sicut et ex filio nihil tale accipio. Verum is, quae alias publice et notorie sparguntur, confirmat. M.V. et mihi quoque ubi vacaverit a negotijs, ...dabo operam, ut illam invisam. Reliqua committo prudentiae Μ.V. Quae ut cum suis optime valeat, toto pectore opto. Datae raptim Hamburgae XI. Martij An. 1598.</p><p>Μ.V. omni cum observantia<br>qui colo<br>VITUS WINSHEMIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the stern, noble, and honourable Lord Tycho Brahe, now at Wandsbek, my especially gracious, dear lord and patron.</b></p><p>Noble and Magnificent Master Tycho, my lord to be honoured with all respect. How I have carried out the business entrusted [to me] with Master Johann Bokel will appear to Your Magnificence from the slip [of paper] enclosed. I have written diligently to the Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, [Otto], concerning Your Magnificence's view. His Highness [C(elsitudo)] entirely concurs, and ⟨…⟩ what will be to Your Magnificence's advantage. Concerning the Queen's arrival in this city nothing is reported to me, just as I get nothing of the sort from [my] son either; he does, however, confirm the things that are otherwise being put about publicly and notoriously. When it shall be free of business [time permitting] both for Your Magnificence and for me as well, ⟨…⟩ I shall take pains to visit her. The rest I commit to Your Magnificence's prudence. That you, with yours, may fare most excellently, I wish with my whole heart. Given in haste at Hamburg, 11 March, the year 1598.</p><p>Your Magnificence's, with all respect,<br>[he] who honours [you],<br>Vitus Winshemius.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Vitus Winshemius</i> = <b>Vitus Winshemius (Veit Oertel/Ortel von Windsheim)</b>, the younger — here a Hamburg agent/intermediary of Tycho's during his Wandsbek exile; he acted as Tycho's local man of affairs in the city (the Latin epistolary register and the <i>D. Doctor</i> address in Tycho's reply imply a learned physician/lawyer of the Hamburg circle). Tycho's reply survives, dated <b>28 March 1598, Wandsbek</b> (slug brahe-t_15980328001), opening <i>\"Excellentissime Domine Doctor Vinshemi…\"</i>. The name is shared with the better-known Wittenberg Greek scholar Veit Oertel von Windsheim (1501–1570), who is too early to be this writer.</li><li><b>Recipient / place.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, then resident at <b>Wandsbek</b> (<i>Wandesbeck / Wandsburg</i>), the estate of Heinrich Rantzau just outside Hamburg, where Tycho lived in 1597–98 after leaving Denmark and where he completed and printed the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598). The letter is sent from <b>Hamburg</b> (<i>Hamburgae</i>) into Wandsbek.</li><li><b><i>D. Ioannem Boekelium</b></i> = <b>Johann Bökel (Johannes Bokelius, 1535–1605)</b>, the leading Hamburg physician of the day, professor and city physician (<i>Stadtphysicus</i>). Some \"business\" (<i>negotium</i>) of Tycho's had been transacted through him; the details were sent on a separate enclosed slip (<i>scheda inserta</i>), now lost.</li><li><b><i>Duci Ottoni Brunsvicensium et Luneburgensium</b></i> = the <b>Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Otto</b> — i.e. of the house of Braunschweig-Lüneburg. (Tycho's circle of ducal dedicatees of the <i>Mechanica</i> included Duke Otto II of Braunschweig-Lüneburg, 1528–1603, recipient of a presentation copy; the identification of this <i>Otto</i> with that duke is plausible but not made explicit in the letter, so the given name is bracketed in the translation.)</li><li><b><i>Regina</b></i> — \"the Queen\": in the Hamburg–Danish context of 1598 most naturally <b>Sophie of Mecklenburg, queen dowager of Denmark</b> (Tycho's sometime visitor), but the letter does not name her; the rumour of a royal \"arrival in this city\" is reported only as hearsay (<i>publice et notorie sparguntur</i>) and the writer keeps it expressly unconfirmed. Identification left open per the source's own reserve.</li><li><b><i>ex filio</b></i> — \"from [my] son\": Winshemius's own son, evidently a source of city news; unnamed.</li><li><b>Abbreviations.</b> <i>M.V. / Μ.V.</i> = <i>Magnificentia Vestra</i>, \"Your Magnificence\" (the standard honorific answering the address <i>Magnifice D. Tycho</i>). The source alternates the Latin majuscule <i>M.V.</i> with a <b>Greek capital Mu (Μ.V.)</b> — a scribal/typographic variation in Langebek's copy, here reproduced as printed (source block) and rendered uniformly \"Your Magnificence.\" <i>C.</i> (in <i>C. ipsius … acquiescit</i>) is expanded as <i>C(elsitudo)</i>, \"His Highness\" (i.e. the Duke just named); the expansion is editorial and so the rendering is supplied with the bracketed source letter.</li><li><b>Lacunae in the witness.</b> The copy-text carries two breaks, reproduced verbatim in the source block: (1) <i>\"et..quod M. Vestrae commodum futurum est\"</i> — a short gap after <i>et</i>, here marked ⟨…⟩; the syntax around the gap is incomplete and the exact connective sense (\"and [will do] what will be to Your Magnificence's advantage\") is reconstructed, not secure; (2) <i>\"…dabo operam, ut illam invisam\"</i> — a gap before <i>dabo</i>, also marked ⟨…⟩. Both are gaps in Langebek's <i>Afskrift</i>, not editorial cuts; they are flagged, not smoothed.</li><li><b><i>scheda inserta</b></i> — \"the enclosed slip [of paper]\" (a separate sheet sent with the letter), now not preserved with the text.</li><li><b>Honorific / closing formulae.</b> Address <i>Gestrengen, edlen vnd ernvesten Herren</i> — the German chancery courtesy stack \"stern (i.e. authoritative), noble, and honourable lord,\" kept in register; <i>Forderer</i> = \"patron, promoter.\" Subscription <i>\"Μ.V. omni cum observantia qui colo\"</i> = \"Your Magnificence's, with all respect, [he] who honours [you]\" — the relative <i>qui colo</i> left slightly elliptical in the Latin and rendered with a bracketed antecedent.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Datae raptim Hamburgae XI. Martij An. 1598</i> = \"Given in haste at Hamburg, 11 March, the year 1598\" (<b>Old Style</b>: Denmark and the north-German Lutheran cities kept the Julian calendar in 1598). The dateline agrees with the catalogue label (<i>1598. 11. marts. Hamborg</i>); no discrepancy. <i>raptim</i> (\"in haste\") kept.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the Latin and the German address are rendered securely and the register is matched, but the witness (Langebek's copy) is <b>lacunose at two points</b> (the <i>et..quod</i> gap and the <i>…dabo operam</i> gap), where the connective sense is reconstructed rather than attested; in addition the unnamed <i>Regina</i> and the <i>Otto</i> of Brunswick-Lüneburg are identified only by external context, not by the letter itself. These open points are flagged above rather than resolved into fluent prose.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 45: both gaps (<i>et.. quod</i>; <i>... dabo operam</i>) are in Dreyer's own text; <i>Duci OTTONI</i> and <i>Regina</i> are printed without name-resolution. [C] stands.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.31 page image in full (German address-heading + Latin body + signature). Collated the Bœckelium/Bokel business, the Duke Otto of Brunswick-Lüneburg sentence, the bracketed gap markers ('et..quod', '...dabo operam'), the Queen's-arrival-in-Hamburg and son/filio sentence, the closing formula, and the 11 March 1598 date and Hamburg place-name against source_text and published_english. All match; the bracketed ellipsis renderings ⟨…⟩ correctly preserve genuine gaps in Dreyer's printed text rather than inventing content. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned published_grade of C does not survive this collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Vitus Winshemius", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15980328001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980328001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports completing business with Johann Bokel, the Duke of Brunswick's agreement with Tycho's view, and the absence of news about the Queen's arrival in Hamburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980311001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980314001", "d": "1598-03-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joseph Scaliger — Wandsbek (Rantzau Castle, near Hamburg), 14 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with planet/zodiac symbols) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(VIII, Epistolae) · MS witnesses: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 178 (copy); B = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>DIU multumque in votis habui, Illustris Domine IOSEPHE SCALIGER, cum adhuc in Dania Patria mea morarer, ut tecum per litteras amicitiam contraherem, praesertim cum a plerisque isthinc ad nos venientibus tuum in me, licet de facie tibi ignotum, candorem et benevolentiam aliquoties perceperim, sed semper aliquid intervenit obstaculi, quominus id efficere commodum fuerit. Nunc autem, cum hunc Nobilem et doctum adolescentem FRANCISCUM TEGNAGLIUM mihi domesticum et meae e Dania migrationis comitem in Bataviam ablegarem, non intermittendum duxi, quin aliquid saltem ad te litterarum darem, utut iam essem occupatissimus. Ad quod me etiam non minimum invitavit excellentissimum et laboriosissimum illud opus tuum redintegratum de Temporum Emendatione, quod his ipsis diebus primum accepi, atque quantum per negotia licuit, perlustravi. Nec possum satis mirari exactam tuam in eo concinnando diligentiam, pari iudicio atque eruditione coniunctam. Debet tibi sane vel pro hoc solo labore (ut caeteros illos praeclarissimos a te exantlatos praeteream) omnis posteritas immensas gratias, quod temporum epochas et rationes tam sedulo et subtiliter extricare volueris. Nec curandum si qui (uti fit) inveniantur ingrati Zoili, quales et hactenus nonnullos habuisti, qui e contradictionibus famam venari satagunt, sed ex alto despiciendum. Memini, me cum ad quendam eorum mihi Heidelberga scribentem litteras responsi loco darem, de te ipsum (siquidem is occasionem praebuerat) discrete admonuisse, ut te tuaque suspiceret potius quam vellicaret, atque a talibus contradictionibus abstineret, vel ad minimum ut modestius et maiori verecundia rem ageret. Quid hoc pacto apud ipsum profecerim, ignoro. Nam postea inter nos altum silentium.</p><p>Reperi in hoc novo tuo volumine, dum illud evolverem, te alicubi circa finem facere mentionem mearum observationum, quas circa utrumque Aequinoctium in Dania habui, easque cum Hipparcheis conferre, ut anni Alphonsini a te nunc assumpti magnitudinem probares. Ago autem tibi officiose gratias, quod tam honorifice et candide mei et mearum inventionum isthic memineris, et cuperem, ut haec ipsa uberius adhuc expenderes, conferendo nimirum HIPPARCHI omnes observationes, sed prius limitatas, ut sibi invicem, quoad fieri poterit, correspondeant, aut seligendo eas quae exactiores videbuntur; hac tamen adhibita cautione, ut is annus Iulianus retro numeratus eandem cum nostro, qui adhibetur, habeat respectu bissexti rationem, ita ut vel ambo sint bissextiles vel pariter hinc removeantur. Ideoque nostra Tabella utriusque Aequinoctij, quam e primo capite Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum assumpsisti, eos annos continue exhibet, qui ab uno in alterum bissextilem includuntur. Pluribus enim opus non erat, licet multos praeterea in promptu habeam pariter sibi invicem correspondentes. Optandum sane foret, ut veteres parem in his praestitissent accurationem, quod tamen ab ijs factum non esse, propriae eorum annotationes, ut tu quoque non iniuria arguis, produnt; utique magis indubiam Anni Coelestis rationem, quoad minima momenta, nunc obtineremus, praesertim si a pluribus Artificibus per aliquot interlapsa secula res nunquam hians aut claudicans comprobata esset. Quin et utrobique medium Aequinoctium capiendum, ut intricatio ea, quae ex Apogaei et Eccentricitatis mutatione provenit, praecaveatur. Conducit etiam ALBATEGNIJ Aratensis intermedias Observationes in consilium adhibere. Quibus omnibus rite expensis et adhibita in singulis cautela, quod parallaxin ☉ inter observandum Aequinoctia forte neglexerint, atque his etiam Aequatoris inclinationem non satis scrupulose applicârint, dummodo ex altitudine ☉ meridiana in utroque solstitio Poli sublimitatem rimati sunt, existimârim de Alphonsina Anni quantitate nonnihil adhuc decessurum, ita ut vix 49 minuta ultra integras horas explere possit, nedum ea in 6 H. excederet. Si itaque omnes quas habes observationes Hipparcheas tam in aequinoctio vernali quam autumnali in Tabella discrete expositas (annotando ubique qualis iste annus fuerit respectu bissexti Iuliani) mecum communicaveris, dabo operam, ut haec cum nostris ulterius collata exactum quid non tantum anno Civili, sed et Astronomico praefiniendo suppeditent. Et si plures in promptu tibi sunt vetustissimae Aequinoctiorum Observationes, diligentiam aliquam redolentes, eas etiam pari modo impertiaris, quaeso. Tum quoque de Epocha antiquissima illa Iudaica, quando Aequinoctium die 21. Aprilis sex horis a Meridie extitisse creditur, si quid indubitati habes, unde id probari queat, et ad quem meridianum referendum sit, atque quomodo hic annus ad bissextum sese habuerit, quantumque ab hisce temporibus citra omnem ambiguitatem distiterit, expone et plenius de his omnibus, quae a te collecta, mecum per litteras agere non degravare. Erit id mihi longe gratissimum. Quicquid vicissim mea opella praestari queat, quod tibi acceptum sit, habebis me semper ex voto. Fieri autem potest, ut nonnulla horum in hoc libro tuo elucidaveris, quae adhuc explorata non habeo. Nam mihi accurate eum relegere plurimae occupationes nondum permiserunt. Porro referet dictus adolescens quaedam ad te meo nomine secreto, de quibus tuum iudicium et consilium cognoscere averem. Is etiam plura, cum mearum rerum sit conscius, de ijs aperiet. Quare ut ipsum benigne audias, tibique commendatum habeas, etiam atque etiam rogo.</p><p>Vale vir tam doctrina quam genere revera illustris.<br>Dabantur subito Wandesburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana, quae est prope Hamburgum, die 14. Martij Anni 1598.<br>Illustris tuae Magnis. studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Joseph Scaliger.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Long and earnestly, Illustrious Master Joseph Scaliger, did I wish — while I still abode in Denmark, my homeland — to enter into friendship with you by letter, especially since from very many who came to us from those parts I had several times perceived your goodwill and kindly favour towards me, unknown to you though I was by face; but always some obstacle intervened, so that there was no convenient opportunity to bring it about. Now, however, since I was dispatching this noble and learned young man Frans Tengnagel — a member of my household and the companion of my removal from Denmark — into Holland, I thought I ought not to let the chance slip without at least sending you some lines, occupied as I now am to the utmost. To this I was no little invited, too, by that most excellent and most laborious work of yours, now reissued, <i>On the Emendation of Times</i> [<i>de Temporum Emendatione</i>], which I received for the first time these very days, and have, so far as my affairs allowed, looked through. Nor can I sufficiently marvel at your exact diligence in composing it, joined with equal judgement and erudition. For this labour alone (to pass over those other most distinguished ones you have toiled through) all posterity surely owes you immense thanks, in that you were willing to disentangle the epochs and reckonings of times so painstakingly and so subtly. Nor need one be troubled if some ungrateful Zoili are found (as happens), such as you have had not a few hitherto, who busy themselves hunting fame out of contradictions; rather, they are to be looked down upon from on high. I recall that, when I was sending a letter by way of reply to a certain one of them who was writing to me from Heidelberg, I discreetly admonished him about you yourself (since indeed he had given the occasion) — that he should look up to you and your works rather than carp at them, and abstain from such contradictions, or at the very least conduct the matter with more modesty and greater deference. What I accomplished with him in this way, I do not know. For afterwards there was deep silence between us.</p><p>I found, in this new volume of yours, as I was unrolling it, that somewhere near the end you make mention of my observations, which I made about both equinoxes in Denmark, and that you compare them with those of Hipparchus, in order to test the magnitude of the Alphonsine year now assumed by you. I render you dutiful thanks that you remember me and my discoveries there so honourably and candidly; and I could wish that you would weigh these very matters yet more fully — by comparing, namely, all the observations of Hipparchus, but first delimited, so that they may correspond to one another so far as can be, or by selecting those which shall seem the more exact; with this caution, however, applied: that the Julian year, reckoned backward, should have the same relation, with respect to the bissextile [leap day], as ours which is in use — so that either both are bissextile or both are alike removed from it [the leap day]. And therefore our Table of both equinoxes, which you took from the first chapter of the <i>Astronomical Exercises</i> [<i>Progymnasmata Astronomica</i>], exhibits continuously those years which are comprised from one bissextile to the next. For more were not needed, though I have besides many ready to hand likewise corresponding to one another. It were assuredly to be wished that the ancients had shown a like accuracy in these matters; that they did not, however, their own annotations betray, as you too not without justice argue — assuredly we should now obtain a more indubitable reckoning of the celestial year, down to the least moments, especially if, by several practitioners through several intervening centuries, the matter had been verified never gaping nor limping. Moreover, on both sides the mean equinox is to be taken, so that the entanglement which arises from the change of the apogee and of the eccentricity may be guarded against. It is profitable also to bring al-Battānī of al-Raqqa's [Albategnius Aratensis'] intermediate observations into the deliberation. All these things having been duly weighed, and caution applied in each — [namely] that they perhaps neglected the parallax of the Sun [☉] while observing the equinoxes, and that to these too they did not apply the inclination of the equator scrupulously enough, provided they searched out the elevation of the pole from the meridian altitude of the Sun [☉] at each solstice — I should reckon that something will yet have to be deducted from the Alphonsine quantity of the year, so that it can scarcely fill out 49 minutes beyond the whole hours, far less exceed those by 6 hours. If, therefore, you communicate to me all the observations of Hipparchus that you have, both at the vernal and the autumnal equinox, set out distinctly in a Table (noting everywhere what sort of year that one was with respect to the Julian bissextile), I shall take pains that these, further collated with ours, may furnish something exact for fixing in advance not only the Civil but also the Astronomical year. And if you have ready to hand several most ancient observations of the equinoxes that savour of some diligence, impart those too in like manner, I beg. Then also, concerning that most ancient Jewish epoch, when the equinox is believed to have occurred on the 21st day of April, six hours from the meridian [i.e. past noon] — if you have anything indubitable whereby it can be proved, and to which meridian it is to be referred, and how this year stood with respect to the bissextile, and how far it was distant from these times without any ambiguity — set it forth; and do not think it a burden to treat with me by letter more fully of all these things gathered by you. That will be by far most welcome to me. Whatever in return my poor effort can render that may be acceptable to you, you shall always have me at your wish. It may be, however, that you have elucidated some of these matters in this book of yours which I have not yet investigated. For my very many occupations have not yet allowed me to read it through carefully. Further, the said young man will report certain things to you in my name in confidence, on which I should be eager to learn your judgement and counsel. He too will disclose more about them, since he is privy to my affairs. Wherefore I ask again and again that you give him kindly hearing and hold him commended to you.</p><p>Farewell, a man truly illustrious as much by learning as by birth.<br>Given in haste at Wandsbek, from the Rantzau Castle, which is near Hamburg, on the 14th day of March of the year 1598.<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious Magnificence,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from exile: having left Denmark in 1597, he had wintered (autumn 1597 – spring 1598) at <b>Wandsbek</b>, the castle of Heinrich Rantzau near Hamburg, where he printed the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> and the star-catalogue. The subscription <i>manu propria</i> (\"in his own hand\") attests autograph signature of the (clerk-copied) letter.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Iosephus Scaliger</i> = <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), the foremost chronological scholar of the age, then professor at Leiden. His <i>Opus novum de emendatione temporum</i> founded modern technical chronology.</li><li><b><i>opus … redintegratum de Temporum Emendatione</b></i> — Scaliger's <i>Opus novum de emendatione temporum</i>; <i>redintegratum</i> (\"reissued / restored anew\") signals the <b>second, revised edition</b> (Frankfurt 1598; first edition Paris 1583), which Tycho says he had just received. Tycho owned a copy of the 1583 edition (now Prague UL, <i>Ex Bibliotheca Tychoniana</i> no. 41).</li><li><b>Francis(cus) Tegnaglius</b> = <b>Frans Gansneb genaamd Tengnagel</b> (1576–1622), Dutch-born nobleman, Tycho's assistant (\"of my household\") and travelling companion in the move from Denmark; later married Tycho's daughter Elisabeth. Here sent as Tycho's confidential envoy <i>in Bataviam</i> — to <b>Holland / the Low Countries</b> (<i>Batavia</i>, the humanist name for the Dutch provinces), i.e. toward Leiden.</li><li><b><i>Zoili</b></i> — \"Zoili\" (from Zoilus, the proverbial carping critic of Homer): captious detractors; Tycho's standing term for hostile critics, kept as the classical plural with note.</li><li><b>The Heidelberg critic.</b> <i>quendam eorum … Heidelberga scribentem</i> — an unnamed correspondent who had attacked Scaliger; Tycho recalls counselling him to defer to Scaliger. The identity is not stated in the letter and is left unidentified here (not silently supplied).</li><li><b>Chronology / astronomy of the year-length.</b></li><li><i>anni Alphonsini … magnitudinem</i> — the <b>Alphonsine</b> value of the tropical year (365 d 5 h 49 m 16 s in the Alfonsine Tables), which Scaliger had adopted; Tycho argues from his own equinox observations that it is still slightly too long.</li><li><i>vix 49 minuta ultra integras horas … nedum … in 6 H. excederet</i> — Tycho's estimate that the excess over the 365 whole days is barely <b>49 minutes beyond the [5] whole hours</b>, and certainly does not reach a full 6 hours (i.e. the Julian 365¼-day value is too long). \"[5]\" is not in the text; \"integras horas\" (\"the whole hours\") is rendered literally and the implied figure flagged, not inserted.</li><li><i>Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum</i> — Tycho's <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> (the \"Preliminary Exercises for the Restored Astronomy,\" pr. 1602/1610); its first chapter carried his equinox table, which Scaliger had used.</li><li><i>respectu bissexti</i> — \"with respect to the bissextile,\" i.e. whether a backward-reckoned Julian year is or is not a leap year; the caution is that compared years must be in the same leap-cycle phase. <i>bissextus</i>/<i>bissextilis</i> rendered \"bissextile [leap day]/leap year.\"</li><li><i>medium Aequinoctium</i> — the <b>mean equinox</b> (as opposed to apparent), to be used on both sides so as to avoid the <i>intricatio</i> arising from the secular change of the <b>apogee</b> (<i>apogaeum</i>) and <b>eccentricity</b> (<i>eccentricitas</i>) — glossary §5.1.</li><li><i>Albategnij Aratensis</i> = <b>al-Battānī of al-Raqqa</b> (Latin <i>Albategnius</i>; <i>Aratensis</i> = of al-Raqqa, ancient Aracta/Callinicum, c. 858–929), whose 9th–10th-c. equinox observations Tycho recommends as the key intermediate data between antiquity and his own.</li><li><i>parallaxin ☉ … Aequatoris inclinationem … Poli sublimitatem … altitudine ☉ meridiana … solstitio</i> — solar <b>parallax</b>; the inclination of the equator (obliquity); the <b>elevation of the pole</b> (latitude); the <b>meridian altitude</b> of the Sun at each <b>solstice</b> — all glossary §5.1. The Sun-glyph ☉ is kept in the Source block and named \"the Sun\" in translation per §5.3.</li><li><b>The Jewish epoch crux.</b> <i>de Epocha antiquissima illa Iudaica, quando Aequinoctium die 21. Aprilis sex horis a Meridie extitisse creditur</i> — Tycho asks Scaliger about a very ancient Jewish chronological epoch at which the (vernal) equinox was reputedly on <b>21 April, six hours past noon</b>. The clause <i>sex horis a Meridie</i> is rendered \"six hours from the meridian,\" glossed \"past noon\"; this is a chronological datum Scaliger treated, queried by Tycho, not a date in the letter's own reckoning.</li><li><b>Calendar of the dateline.</b> <i>die 14. Martij Anni 1598</i> — <b>14 March 1598, Old Style</b>, consistent with the dataset label (\"1598. 14. marts. Wandsburg\"); Tycho, writing as a Dane in Lutheran territory, used the <b>Julian</b> calendar (Denmark and Hamburg/Holstein had not adopted the Gregorian reform). No conversion is made. The recipient Scaliger, in Leiden, was in a New-Style jurisdiction — a style-gap Tycho does not here remark on, but which is the very subject of the 1600 sequel letter (where Tycho cites Scaliger's claim that the Dionysian Christian epoch is two years short). Recorded, not reconciled.</li><li><b><i>Wandesburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana … prope Hamburgum</b></i> = <b>Wandsbek, from the Rantzau Castle, near Hamburg</b> (the residence of Heinrich Rantzau, Stattholder of Holstein and Tycho's host).</li><li><b>Formulae.</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> → \"Heartiest greeting\" (§5.5); <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Illustris tuae Magnis[itudini] studiosiss[imus]</i> → \"Most devoted to Your Illustrious Magnificence\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"in his own hand.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the technical chronology is consistent with Tycho's documented programme. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the elliptical <i>49 minuta ultra integras horas / nedum … in 6 H.</i> clause, where the suppressed \"[5]\" whole hours is inferred from context and bracketed, not asserted; and (2) the unidentified Heidelberg critic, left unnamed as in the source.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against TBOO VIII page images (foot of p.31 through p.33), opening salutation to signature. All content checks out: the reissued 'de Temporum Emendatione', the Heidelberg critic anecdote, the equinox-correspondence methodology (bissextile-matching years), the al-Battani (Albategnius Aratensis) reference, the numeric claims (scarcely 49 minutes beyond whole hours, far less than 6 hours; the ancient Jewish equinox on 21 April, six hours past the meridian), Tengnagel's mission into Holland, and the closing date and place (14 March 1598, Wandsbek/Rantzau Castle near Hamburg) all match Dreyer exactly, with faithful English throughout. No defects found; the letter's earlier self-assigned grade of B undersold it — this is a fully collated clean letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Håndskriftsamlingen, Langebek 178. Afskrift", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15980709001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980709001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Introduces himself to Scaliger via the bearer Frans Tengnagel, expresses long-held admiration, and praises the newly received *De Temporum Emendatione*.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980314001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980324001", "d": "1598-03-24", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Wandsbek, 24 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>DILECTE CHRISTIANE, scripsit ad me ante paucos dies IACOBUS MONAVIUS Vratislaviensis, Amicus meus integerrimus, te sub finem anni praeteriti Vratislaviam venisse, et discessus mei causas e Dania per omnes circumstantias sibi retulisse, tum quoque te admodum de me solicitum fuisse, atque hac inprimis de causa inde abijsse Lipsiam. Quod sane mihi auditu pergratum fuit, dum te ita animatum agnosco. Ego tibi vicissim ex animo bene favere atque ad meliora quaeque promovere non intermittam. Indicavit insuper ibidem MONAVIUS, quod te ad sororem WITICHIJ B. M. deduci curaverit, ubi omnes illius libros perlustraveris. Rogo itaque ut de ijs, qui et quales fuerint, praesertim manuscripti, et an eos vendere velit, qualique pretio, mihi significes. Vidisti proculdubio plagiarij mei impuri illius URSI maledicentissimum scriptum, in quo praeter alia innumera convitia meo et meorum honori non parcit. Ego quidem refutatione illum indignum censeo, cum omnes modestiae limites imo honestatis longe transcenderit; efficiam tamen ut non impune ferat. Cum vero tibi de plagio ipsius tum quoque omnibus circumstantijs procul dubio ex aliorum, qui tunc adfuerunt, relatu innotuerit, rogo in charta designare velis, quidnam hac de re a tuis apud me commilitonibus inaudivisti. Ego quidem nonnulla adhuc recordor, sed poterant aliqua tanto temporis intervallo, et inter tot alias curas mihi facile excidisse; tum quoque quid alij in Academijs, ubi interea fuisti, de hoc maledico scripto sentiant. Verum ut tecum plenius non tantum de hoc negotio, quod ex alto despicio, sed et de alijs quibusdam longe maioris momenti loqui queam, antequam ad ulteriora hinc peregrinationem suscipiam, atque plus forte disiungamur, cuperem quam maxime, ut meis sumptibus actutum huc Wandesburgum, dimidio saltem milliari Hamburgo remotum, te conferres, quo de necessarijs quibusdam tecum praesens agere queam. Neque enim omnia chartis committere commodum est. Si viaticum tibi ad manus non est, mutuo ab aliquo pete, vel oppignora quid, a me, qualecunque tandem sit, bona fide redimendum, aut aliquis pro te spondeat. Faxo ut illi sit indemne. Non est quod verearis, ne te hîc ultra voluntatem detineam, nam ipsemet non constitui hîc diu manere. Est nunc apud me IOHANNES MOLLERUS Electoris Brandenburgici Mathematicus, qui Hvenae in coronatione (ut scis) nobis aderat. Is negotijs praeest Astronomicis et studiosis meis, quos hîc non minus quam antea Uraniburgi haud paucos sustento. Vide iam, mi CHRISTIERNE, ne fallas exspectationem meam, sed quam primum te hîc sistas. Invenies me vicissim gratum, ut huius pro me suscepti itineris te neutiquam poeniteat. Sume tecum exemplar Ephemeridum novarum N. EVERHARDI e novis (ut iactitat) Tabulis supputatarum, quod illas in aliqua Academia facile nancisceris, et si quid aliud nosti, quod meis desiderijs conveniat. Hamburgi hic liber venalis non est. Mihi nihil praeter solum eius libri titulum in catalogo hucusque videre licuit.</p><p>Vale, et confestim, prout tibi confido, ad nos advola.<br>Raptim Wandesburgi die 24. Martij Anni 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTIAN LONGOMONTANUS.</b></p><p>Beloved Christian, a few days ago Jacob Monau of Breslau, my most upright friend, wrote to me that towards the end of last year you had come to Breslau and recounted to him, in all its particulars, the causes of my departure from Denmark; and that you too had been greatly anxious on my account, and had gone off from there to Leipzig chiefly for this reason. This indeed was most welcome for me to hear, since I recognize you to be so disposed. I in turn shall not cease to wish you well from my heart and to advance you to all that is better. Monau also reported, in the same place, that he had had you conducted to the sister of Wittich, of blessed memory, where you went through all his books. I ask you therefore to let me know about these — which and of what kind they were, the manuscripts especially, and whether she is willing to sell them, and at what price. You have doubtless seen the most slanderous writing of that impure plagiarist of mine, Ursus, in which, besides countless other abuses, he does not spare my honour and that of my own people. I for my part judge him unworthy of a refutation, since he has far overstepped all bounds of modesty — indeed of decency; yet I shall see to it that he does not get off unpunished. But since his plagiarism, and likewise all the circumstances, have doubtless become known to you from the report of others who were then present, I ask you to be willing to set down on paper what you have heard about this matter from those fellow-students of yours who were with me. I myself still recall a number of things, but some could easily have slipped from me over so great an interval of time and amid so many other cares; and also [I ask] what others in the universities, where you have been in the meantime, think about this slanderous writing. But so that I may speak with you more fully not only of this business — which I look down upon from on high — but also of certain others of far greater moment, before I undertake my journey onward from here, and before perhaps we are more parted, I should very much wish that, at my expense, you would betake yourself at once here to Wandsbek, distant from Hamburg by but half a [German] mile, so that I may treat with you in person about certain necessary matters. For it is not convenient to commit everything to paper. If you have no travel-money at hand, borrow it from someone, or pawn something — to be redeemed by me in good faith, whatever it may finally be — or let someone go surety for you. I shall see to it that he is held harmless. There is no cause for you to fear that I shall detain you here beyond your wish, for I myself have not resolved to stay here long. There is now with me Johannes Müller, mathematician to the Elector of Brandenburg, who (as you know) was present with us at Hven at the coronation. He is in charge of the astronomical work and of my students, of whom I maintain here not a few, no less than before at Uraniborg. See now, my Christian, that you do not disappoint my expectation, but present yourself here as soon as possible. You will find me grateful in return, so that you shall by no means regret this journey undertaken for my sake. Bring with you a copy of the new ephemerides of N. Eberhart, computed from new Tables (as he boasts), for you will easily come by them in some university; and anything else you know of that suits my wishes. Here at Hamburg this book is not for sale. Up to now I have been able to see nothing of it but the bare title of the book in a catalogue.</p><p>Farewell, and at once — as I trust you — fly to us.<br>In haste, at Wandsbek, the 24th day of March, the year 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing from his temporary exile residence at Wandsbek castle, near Hamburg, in the year after he had left Hven (1597). His programme of <i>instauratio astronomiae</i> continued there with a reduced household.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Christianus Longomontanus</i> = <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (1562–1647), Tycho's most senior and longest-serving assistant, later professor of astronomy at Copenhagen. He had left Tycho's service in 1597 and was travelling through the German universities; the corpus (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>) confirms he took his M.A. at Rostock in spring 1598 and then made this short summer visit to Wandsbek \"to discuss the Ursus affair.\" Tycho addresses him familiarly as <i>Dilecte Christiane</i> and, in the close, <i>mi Christierne</i> (the Danish vocative form) — both rendered \"Christian\" to keep the single referent clear.</li><li><b><i>Iacobus Monavius Vratislaviensis</b></i> = <b>Jacob Monau (Monavius)</b> of <b>Breslau</b> (<i>Vratislavia</i> = Breslau / Wrocław), 1546–1603, humanist and Tycho's correspondent. Per the corpus (<i>On Tycho's Island</i>), it was Monau who introduced Longomontanus to Wittich's sister and her late brother's library at Wrocław.</li><li><b><i>soror Witichij B. M.</b></i> — the sister of <b>Paul Wittich</b> (c. 1546–1586), the Wrocław mathematician whose visit to Hven in 1580 left a lasting imprint on Tycho's instruments and trigonometric methods (and whose later relations with Tycho turned into a priority dispute). <i>B. M.</i> = <i>beatae memoriae</i>, \"of blessed memory\" (Wittich was by then dead); rendered as such. Tycho's request that Longomontanus inventory the books — <i>especially the manuscripts</i> — and ascertain a sale price is the documented attempt to acquire Wittich's library.</li><li><b><i>plagiarius … Ursus</b></i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär</b>, called <b>Ursus</b> (\"the Bear\"), c. 1551–1600, Imperial Mathematician, whom Tycho accused of stealing his geo-heliocentric world-system during a 1584 visit to Hven. The <i>maledicentissimum scriptum</i> is Ursus's <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (Prague, 1597), whose scurrilous attack on Tycho prompted the long <i>Apologia</i> / <i>Astronomical Letters</i> counter-campaign (and, eventually, Kepler's <i>Apologia Tychonis contra Ursum</i>). <i>plagiarij mei … illius Ursi</i> — \"that plagiarist of mine, Ursus\": <i>mei</i> marks Ursus as the one who plagiarized <i>Tycho</i>, not a possessive of fondness; rendered \"that … plagiarist of mine.\"</li><li><b><i>efficiam tamen ut non impune ferat</b></i> — \"yet I shall see to it that he does not get off unpunished.\" A double construction (<i>non … impune</i>) carrying a single negation of impunity; reproduced exactly (one \"not\"). Tycho first says Ursus is beneath refutation, then immediately reverses to promise retaliation — the tension is the author's, preserved.</li><li><b><i>commilitones</b></i> — literally \"fellow-soldiers\"; here the metaphor for Tycho's resident assistants/students at Hven who witnessed the Ursus visit. Rendered \"fellow-students of yours who were with me.\"</li><li><b><i>Wandesburgum … dimidio saltem milliari Hamburgo remotum</b></i> — <b>Wandsbek</b>, \"distant from Hamburg by but half a [German] mile.\" The unit is the German <i>Meile</i> (≈ 7.5 km), far larger than the English mile; \"[German]\" is bracketed as an editorial clarification not in the source. <i>saltem</i> here = \"at [most] only / merely\" — Tycho stresses the <i>shortness</i> of the trip, not a minimum; rendered \"but half a mile.\"</li><li><b><i>viaticum … oppignora … spondeat … Faxo ut illi sit indemne</b></i> — a chain of finance offers: borrow, pawn (Tycho to redeem in good faith), or have a guarantor; <i>Faxo</i> (archaic future of <i>facio</i>) \"I shall see to it\" that the guarantor \"is held harmless\" (<i>indemnis</i>). The pronoun <i>illi</i> refers to the surety (<i>aliquis</i>), not the lender; rendered \"that he is held harmless.\"</li><li><b><i>Iohannes Mollerus, Electoris Brandenburgici Mathematicus</b></i> = <b>Johannes Müller</b>, mathematician to the <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b>, present at Hven at \"the coronation\" (Christian IV's coronation, 1596, which Tycho's circle attended). He now superintends Tycho's astronomical work and students at Wandsbek.</li><li><b><i>Hvena / Uraniburgum</b></i> = the island of <b>Hven</b> and <b>Uraniborg</b>, Tycho's former observatory; he notes he still keeps \"not a few\" students at Wandsbek, \"no less than before at Uraniborg.\"</li><li><b><i>Ephemerides novae N. Everhardi … e novis (ut iactitat) Tabulis supputatae</b></i> = the new <b>ephemerides of N. Eberhart</b> (Nicolaus Everhardus / Eberhart), \"computed from new Tables (as he boasts).\" <i>ut iactitat</i> \"as he boasts/brags\" is Tycho's skeptical aside about the author's own claim for his Tables — the hedge is preserved, not smoothed into neutral report. Tycho has seen only the title in a sale catalogue, the book being unavailable at Hamburg.</li><li><b><i>Sacris Uraniae</b></i> sign-off not present here; instead the close is the urgent <i>ad nos advola</i> (\"fly to us\"), a hyperbolic imperative kept as such.</li><li><b><i>manu propria</b></i> — \"in his own hand\": the subscription marks the letter as autograph (though witness A is a later copy in Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Raptim Wandesburgi die 24. Martij Anni 1598</i> = \"In haste, at Wandsbek, 24 March 1598.\" Denmark and the Lutheran north were still on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1598; the date is O.S. and matches the dataset label. No in-letter date discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; register and the author's hedges (<i>ut iactitat</i>) and self-contradiction on Ursus are preserved. The two soft points, both flagged rather than smoothed, are the precise scope of <i>saltem</i> (\"but / merely\" half a mile) and the editorial \"[German]\" supplied for <i>milliari</i> (the <i>Meile</i>, not the English mile).</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only soft points are an explained </i>saltem<i> + a standard '[German] mile' gloss; no crux, sense + date secure.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.34-35 as page images (letter runs from 'TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM' to the 'manu propria' signature). Collated names (Jacob Monau/Vratislavia=Breslau, Wittich's sister, Ursus, Johannes Mollerus, Hven, N. Everhardi) and the Wandsbek/Hamburg half-mile distance and date '24. Martij Anni 1598' against Dreyer; text matches verbatim. English renders every clause accurately, including the request for Wittich's manuscripts, the Ursus-plagiarism paragraph, and the summons to Wandsbek. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15980910001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Longomontanus for his concern, asks about Wittich's manuscripts and their price at Wittich's sister's house, and mentions Ursus's slanderous writing against Tycho's honour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980324001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980328001", "d": "1598-03-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Vitus Winshemius — Wandsbek, 28 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD VITUM VINSHEMIUM.</p><p>EXCELLENTISSIME Domine Doctor VINSHEMI, Amice honorande. Accepi nudius tertius ab OLIGERO nostro literas, in quibus facit circa calcem mentionem librorum, qui adhuc apud Dominum D. BOeKELIUM residui sunt, quos omnino obtinere desiderat, et aequum sane est, ut, ex quo pecuniam totam is receperit, libros omnes tradat, aut se ita apud OLIGERUM purget, ut sit contentus. Ego consanguineo meo et affini amicoque intimo deesse non possum, quin procurem id, quod a me requirit. Si sponte BOeKELIUS hoc praestare noluerit, cogor iure rem agere, utut id libenter non facerem. Rogo itaque peramanter tam OLIGERI quam meo nomine, velis adhuc semel apud ipsum urgere, ut stet promissis. Excusationes, quas ultimo adduxit, vix accipiet OLIGERUS, nam ille aliter ad me rettulit ea de re, imo VESALIJ Anatomiam mihi dono dedit, licet ego id recusarim, nisi alium ex meis libris aequivalentem eius loco habere vellet. Nec valor eius est maior sex vel septem Ioachimicorum, etiamsi de novo ematur. Fac itaque, mi Domine Doctor VINSHEMI, ut OLIGERI expectationi satisfiat, et schedas, quas hac de re ultimo accepisti, remitte. Scripturus enim sum hisce diebus OLIGERO et negotium hoc expositurus.</p><p>Vale, et si quae nova scis, indica.<br>Raptim Wandesburgi die 28. Martij Anni 98.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Vitus Winshemius.</b></p><p>Most excellent Master Doctor Winshemius, my honoured friend. Two days ago I received a letter from our friend Oligerus [Holger], in which, towards the close, he makes mention of the books that still remain with Master Doctor Bökel — which he very much desires to obtain; and it is indeed only fair that, seeing he [Bökel] has received the whole sum of money, he should hand over all the books, or else so clear himself with Oligerus that he be satisfied. I cannot fail my kinsman and relative and most intimate friend, but must see to what he asks of me. If Bökel should be unwilling to do this of his own accord, I am compelled to pursue the matter at law, however little I should do so willingly. I therefore ask you most affectionately, in Oligerus's name as in my own, that you would be willing to press him once more to stand by his promises. The excuses he last brought forward Oligerus will scarcely accept, for he reported the matter to me otherwise — indeed he made me a gift of Vesalius's Anatomy, though I declined it, unless he would take in its place another, equivalent book of mine. Nor is its value greater than six or seven joachimsthalers, even if it be bought new. See to it, then, my Master Doctor Winshemius, that Oligerus's expectation be satisfied, and send back the papers which you last received on this matter. For I am about to write to Oligerus in these next days and set this business out [to him].</p><p>Farewell, and if you know any news, let me know.<br>In haste, at Wandsbek, on the 28th day of March, the year [15]98.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / place.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing in his self-imposed exile after leaving Denmark; <i>Wandesburgi</i> = <b>Wandsbek</b> (now part of Hamburg), where he stayed at the castle of Heinrich Rantzau from 1597 into 1598. <i>Raptim</i> (\"in haste\") is the standard hurried-subscription flourish.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Vitus Vinshemius</i> = <b>Vitus Winshemius</b> (Veit Oertel von Windsheim) — here the <b>younger</b> of that name (the elder humanist Veit Oertel, 1501–1570, was long dead), professor of medicine at Wittenberg, acting as Holger Rosenkrantz's intermediary in the dispute with Bökel.</li><li><b><i>Oligerus noster</b></i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (1574–1642), \"the Learned,\" the Danish nobleman and scholar, then a young man on his peregrinatio. Tycho calls him <i>consanguineus meus et affinis amicusque intimus</i> — \"my kinsman and relative and most intimate friend\"; the Brahe and Rosenkrantz families were indeed connected by blood and marriage. The dataset's neighbouring letters confirm the identification (a 7 March 1599 letter is headed <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantz ad Tychonem Brahe</i>).</li><li><b><i>D. Boekelius</b></i> = <b>Johann Bökel</b> (Johannes Bocatius / Boccatius, 1535–1605), the prominent Hamburg town physician and medical writer. The <i>œ</i>-ligature spelling <i>BOeKELIUM / BOeKELIUS</i> of the copy is preserved in the Source block.</li><li><b><i>Vesalij Anatomia</b></i> — Andreas <b>Vesalius</b>'s <i>De humani corporis fabrica</i> (or an <i>Epitome</i> of it); referred to generically as \"Vesalius's Anatomy.\" Bökel had pressed the volume on Tycho as a gift, which Tycho declined except as an even exchange.</li><li><b><i>Ioachimicorum</b></i> — <b>joachimsthalers</b> (<i>Joachimsthaler</i>, the silver coin struck at Joachimsthal, ancestor of the \"thaler/dollar\"); \"six or seven joachimsthalers\" gives the book's market value. Kept as the period coin-name and glossed.</li><li><b><i>schedas</b></i> — \"papers / slips,\" the documents (notes, accounts, or correspondence) bearing on the dispute that Winshemius had been sent and is asked to return; rendered \"papers.\"</li><li><b><i>cogor iure rem agere</b></i> — \"I am compelled to pursue the matter at law\": <i>iure agere</i> is the legal idiom for bringing a formal/judicial action. Tycho's hedge <i>utut id libenter non facerem</i> (\"however little I should do so willingly\") is preserved, not sharpened into a bare threat.</li><li><b><i>nudius tertius</b></i> — the classical idiom \"two days ago\" (literally \"now the third day [counting inclusively]\"); rendered \"two days ago.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> No Roman-calendar idiom in the dateline: <i>die 28. Martij Anni 98</i> = 28 March 1598. Denmark and Tycho used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar; marked [O.S.]. The header date matches the source dateline; the dataset <i>label</i> (\"1598. 28. marts. Wandsburg\") agrees — no discrepancy.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> Survives only in a later transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b>, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen; spellings (the <i>œ</i>-ligatures, the all-capitals proper names) follow that copy as printed by Dreyer.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — straightforward humanist Latin, no textual or dating crux; the only soft identifications (Winshemius <i>the younger</i> vs. the elder; the exact Vesalius edition) are flagged above and do not affect the sense of the letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.35, reading the page image; text matches word for word (Oligerus, Boekelius, Vesalii Anatomiam, the sum of six or seven Joachimsthalers, and the date Wandelsburgi 28 Martij Anni 98), and the translation renders every clause and figure correctly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Vitus Winshemius", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980311001", "next": "brahe-t_16000420001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980311001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000813001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Winshemius to press Bökel to deliver books already paid for by Holger Rosenkrantz, threatening legal action if Bökel refuses to honour his promises.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980328001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980329001", "d": "1598-03-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jakob Bording", "ck": "Jakob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jakob Bording — Wandsbek, 29 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(= VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.24 (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IACOBUM BORDINGUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et Excellentissime Domine Doctor BORDINGE, Cancellarie dignissime et Amice honorande. Cum hic Mechanicus senex M. PETRUS IACHENOW denuo isthuc a me rediret, non omittendum duxi, quin ad Illustrissimum Principem ULDARICUM, Dominum meum Clementissimum, aliquid literarum submisse darem, siquidem eius Celnis Clementissimam erga me voluntatem indies magis magisque experiar. Rogoque ut ipsius Celni easdem meo nomine submisse offeras atque in meliorem partem interpreteris, si quid liberius, re sic urgente, indicavi, idque in silentio secreto prematur, cum ex eâ, quam erga Illustrissimum illum et optimum Principem, tum quoque te, gero fiduciâ, sinceroque animo proficiscantur. Poterunt haec forte nimis audacter prolata censeri, siquidem veritas plaerumque odiosa sit; attamen si rei molem et eorum, quae mihi immerito contigerunt, cumulum spectemus, vix supremis labris attigisse, quae dicenda forent, videbor. Verum ego haec omnia Deo committo. Veritas temporis filia aliquando forte prodet, quid aeque aut inique factum sit. Illmo Duci necdum quidpiam responsum esse, etiamnum intelligo. Quod et praesagiebam. Quale responsum ad meas receperim, ex ijs utcunque intellexisti, quas Lubecâ tibi perscripsi, cum inde Instrumenta mea et supellectilem librariam huc transferri curarem. Quare</p><p>Sat Patriae nostrisque datum, nunc Teuthona tellus<br>Excipit, et Patriam largitur maximus Orbis.</p><p>Curo quaedam ex meis Operibus hîc labore Typographico compleri, quae, ubi absoluta fuerint, vobiscum communicabo. In re pecuniariâ me isthic concernente satis actum est. Recepi literas fideiussorias et priores restitui.</p><p>Novarum rerum nihil peculiare est, nisi quod res composita dicatur inter Pontificem et Ferrariae Ducem. Batavos se absentare et tractationem de pace aversari, neque enim hostibus satis fidunt tam saepe circumventi. Novam Reginam in Anglia electam frementibus Scotis narrant. Nihil tamen adhuc certi constat. Vobis proculdubio isthic his plura et certiora innotuerunt. Rex noster Sermus fuit apud Dominum RANZOVIUM Bredenbergae praecedente hebdomadâ ab eo isthic magnifice et splendide exceptus et mox inde Haderslebiam recepit. Plura nunc non vacat. Sum enim occupatissimus. Tu vero mi amantissime Domine BORDINGE quam felicissime vale, meque solitâ benevolentiâ (quemadmodum et ego tui amorem non deponam unquam) constanter prosequere.</p><p>Raptim Wandesburgo die 29. Martij Anni 1598.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JAKOB BORDING.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and most excellent Master Doctor Bording, most worthy Chancellor and honoured Friend. Since the old craftsman here, Master Petrus Jachenow, was returning thither from me once again, I thought it not to be neglected that I should submissively address some lines to the Most Illustrious Prince Ulrich, my Most Clement Lord — seeing that day by day I experience more and more His Highness's most clement goodwill toward me. And I ask that you submissively present them to His Highness in my name, and interpret them in the better sense, if I have indicated anything rather freely, the matter being so pressing; and that it be kept in close silence — since they proceed from the trust which I bear toward that Most Illustrious and best Prince, and toward you as well, and from a sincere heart. These things may perhaps be reckoned to have been uttered too boldly, since the truth is for the most part odious; yet if we consider the weight of the matter and the heap of the things that have undeservedly befallen me, I shall be seen scarcely to have touched, with the tips of my lips, what would need to be said. But I commit all this to God. Truth, the daughter of time, will perhaps one day bring forth what has been done justly or unjustly. That nothing has yet been answered to the Most Illustrious Duke, I understand even now. Which I also foresaw. What sort of answer I received to my own [letters], you have learned in some fashion from those [letters] which I wrote out to you from Lübeck, when I was arranging for my instruments and my library furnishings to be transferred hither from there. Wherefore —</p><p>Enough has been given to my homeland and to my own; now the German land<br>Receives me, and the widest world bestows a homeland.</p><p>I am having certain of my works completed here by the labour of the press, which, when they have been finished, I shall share with you. In the money matter concerning me thither, enough has been done. I have received the letters of guaranty and have returned the earlier ones.</p><p>Of new things there is nothing particular, except that the affair is said to be settled between the Pope and the Duke of Ferrara. [They report] that the Dutch are absenting themselves and turning away from the negotiation for peace, for, so often circumvented, they do not sufficiently trust their enemies. They tell of a new Queen elected in England, with the Scots seething. Yet nothing certain is as yet established. Doubtless more, and more certain, things have come to your knowledge there. Our Most Serene King was with Master Rantzau at Bredenberg in the preceding week, magnificently and splendidly received by him there, and soon thereafter withdrew to Hadersleben. There is no leisure now for more, for I am most busy. But do you, my most beloved Master Bording, fare most happily, and continue to follow me steadfastly with your accustomed benevolence (just as I, too, shall never lay aside my love of you).</p><p>In haste, from Wandsbek, on the 29th day of March, the year 1598.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / context.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Wandsbek</b> (<i>Wandesburgo</i>), the castle of Heinrich Rantzau near Hamburg where Tycho lodged in 1597–98 after leaving Denmark; the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> was completed here, and this letter (<i>\"I am having certain of my works completed here by the labour of the press\"</i>) belongs to that printing campaign. The letter is a covering note: it accompanies a now-lost submissive letter to Duke Ulrich that Tycho asks Bording to deliver and gloss favourably.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Iacobus Bordingus</i> = <b>Jakob Bording</b> (the younger), at this date <b>chancellor of (the Duke of) Mecklenburg</b> (so Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i>, p. 246); a learned correspondent and Tycho's intermediary at the Güstrow/Mecklenburg court. Salutation <i>Cancellarie dignissime</i> (\"most worthy Chancellor\") confirms the office.</li><li><b><i>Illustrissimus Princeps Ulldaricus / Illmo Duci</b></i> = <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</b> (1527–1603), to whom Tycho was appealing in his exile; <i>Dominus meus Clementissimus</i> (\"my Most Clement Lord\") is the courtesy register, with <i>eius Celnis</i> / <i>ipsius Celni</i> = <i>eius Celsitudinis</i> / <i>ipsius Celsitudini</i> (\"His Highness,\" abbreviated), rendered \"His Highness\" per §5.5.</li><li><b><i>M. Petrus Iachenow</b></i> — <i>\"the old craftsman\"</i> (<i>Mechanicus senex</i>) <b>Petrus Jachenow</b>, a workman/messenger shuttling between Tycho and the Mecklenburg court; <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i>, \"Master.\"</li><li><b><i>Dominus Ranzovius … Bredenbergae</b></i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (Henrik Rantzau, 1526–1598), royal governor (Statthalter) of the Danish duchies and Tycho's Wandsbek host, at his seat <b>Bredenberg</b> (Breitenburg, Holstein). <i>Rex noster Sermus</i> = <i>Rex noster Serenissimus</i>, \"our Most Serene King\" — <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>.</li><li><b><i>Haderslebia</b></i> = <b>Haderslev</b> (Hadersleben), in the duchy of Schleswig.</li><li><b><i>Lubeca</b></i> = <b>Lübeck</b>, Tycho's prior staging-point; <i>Instrumenta mea et supellectilem librariam</i> = \"my instruments and my library furnishings\" — the documented Lübeck→Wandsbek transfer of Tycho's apparatus and books. <i>Instrumenta</i> rendered \"instruments\" per §5.2.</li><li><b>Verse couplet.</b> <i>Sat Patriae nostrisque datum …</i> is kept as verse per protocol §4. It is a Tychonic adaptation of the Virgilian/Lucanic <i>patriae … datum</i> topos (cf. <i>Aeneid</i> / the <i>sat patriae</i> tag); the sense: having given enough to Denmark, Tycho now takes Germany — and the wide world — as his homeland. <i>maximus Orbis</i> rendered \"the widest world\" (superlative kept; \"world\" not \"orb,\" since here it is the terrestrial world, not an astronomical <i>orbis/sphaera</i>).</li><li><b><i>Veritas temporis filia</b></i> — \"Truth, the daughter of time\" (<i>veritas filia temporis</i>), the proverbial <i>sententia</i>; kept as the maxim it is.</li><li><b><i>literas fideiussorias</b></i> — \"letters of guaranty / suretyship\" (bonds of guarantee); <i>priores restitui</i> = \"I returned the earlier ones.\" A financial-settlement detail (<i>In re pecuniariâ … satis actum est</i>), left in its own legal-commercial register.</li><li><b>News items (all hedged by Tycho — preserved, not sharpened).</b> (1) <i>res composita … inter Pontificem et Ferrariae Ducem</i> — the settlement between the <b>Pope (Clement VIII)</b> and the <b>Duke of Ferrara</b>, i.e. the Devolution of Ferrara to the Papal States (1597–98); Tycho reports it as <i>dicatur</i>, \"is said to be.\" (2) <i>Batavos … tractationem de pace aversari</i> — the Dutch (States) shunning peace negotiations (the run-up to the Franco-Spanish Peace of Vervins, May 1598); given with the explanatory hedge <i>neque enim hostibus satis fidunt</i> (\"for they do not sufficiently trust their enemies\"). (3) <i>Novam Reginam in Anglia electam frementibus Scotis</i> — a <i>report</i> (<i>narrant</i>) of \"a new Queen elected in England, with the Scots seething.\" This is unfounded rumour: Elizabeth I reigned until 1603. Tycho immediately flags it — <i>Nihil tamen adhuc certi constat</i> (\"yet nothing certain is as yet established\") — and the translation preserves the rumour-frame (\"They tell of …\") and the explicit hedge rather than resolving or \"correcting\" it.</li><li><b>Greek / symbols.</b> None in this letter.</li><li><b>MS / textual note.</b> The sole witness is the Vienna draft (<i>Kladde</i>), cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.24; readings follow Dreyer's edited text. Abbreviations in the source expanded silently only where unambiguous (<i>Sermus</i> = <i>Serenissimus</i>, <i>Illmo</i> = <i>Illustrissimo</i>, <i>Celnis/Celni</i> = <i>Celsitudinis/Celsitudini</i>); flagged here rather than altered in the Source block.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>die 29. Martij Anni 1598</i>, from Wandsbek (Holstein). Denmark and the German Protestant territories were still on the <b>Julian calendar (Old Style)</b> in 1598; the date is O.S. and is not converted. The catalogue label (\"1598. 29. marts. Wandsburg\") agrees with the dateline; no discrepancy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the register (submissive court-petition courtesy + terse newsletter) is matched. Minor residual softness, all flagged not smoothed: the exact metrical/source pedigree of the <i>Sat Patriae</i> couplet (a Tychonic adaptation rather than a verbatim classical quotation), and the precise commercial sense of <i>literas fideiussorias</i> in this specific debt-settlement. No negation or conditional in the letter carries legal/threat force requiring a [C].</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — soft points non-load-bearing (couplet pedigree, </i>fideiussorias<i> register); sense secure, news hedged, date clean.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.36-38 as page images. The Bording letter occupies all of p.36 and the first lines of p.37. Collated the Petrus Jachenow / Duke Ulrich passage, the couplet ('Sat Patriae nostrisque datum...'), the news paragraph (Pope/Ferrara, the Dutch, the English succession rumour), the Rantzau/Bredenberg/Hadersleben itinerary, and the closing date (29 Martij Anni 1598, Wandesburgo) word-for-word. source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english is a complete, accurate rendering with all proper names and place-names correctly retained.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.24. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Bording to present a personal letter to Duke Ulrich and to interpret it charitably if overly frank, urging complete secrecy about its contents.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980329001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980331001", "d": "1598-03-31", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Cramer", "ck": "Daniel Cramer", "ck2": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Stettin", "t": "Daniel Cramer → Holger Rosenkrantz — Stettin, 31 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with extensive Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Epistolae ad Ioannem Keplerum scriptae </i>(in Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DOMINO OLIGERO<br>GEORGIJ F. ROSENCRANTZIO DANO, DOMINO HAeREDITARIO IN ROSENHOLM, ET INTER NOBILES DOCTO, INTERQUE<br>DOCTOS NOBILI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO IN AeVITERNUM COLENDO ΕΙΣ XEIPE.TICHONI<br>BRAHE NON MINUS APERIENDAS ET LEGENDAS.ΕΥ ΠΡΑΤΤΕΙΝ.BINAS tuas literas, illas LubecaA, has Hamburgo ad me<br>datas, quam blande exosculatus fuerim, Generose ROSENKRANTZI, animus meus tibi<br>multis acceptis beneficijs devinctus a multisque perspectus annis tanquam in<br>speculo et tabula apud te loqui potest, lingua non potest. Tute enim nosti, quam<br>impense te amem, colam, quantique faciam, ut quoties literas tuas video,<br>frigidam toties mihi suffundi, et ad cursum amicitiae protensiorem me instigari a<br>te persuadear. Quod cum intelligere te ex literis tuis intelligam, non est, quod<br>pluribus tibi fidem faciam. At vero quantumvis dulcis mihi utraque tua fuit<br>Epistola, amariores tamen aliquanto fueruntA, quod simul in illis de plagio Liddeliano in illustrem Astronomum<br>TYCHONEM BRAHE, consangvineum et affinem tuum, virum multiplici scientia<br>admirandum, narrabas. Quod magis animum percellebat, quia ipse Dominus TYCHO<br>suis idem ad me literis testatum reddiderat. Querebatur tecum de singulari tum<br>impudentia, tum ingratitudine DUNCANI<br>LIDDELIJ Scoti, qui nunc in Inclyta Iulia Matheseos Professorem<br>agit, quod is non tantum literis ad suos privatim datis, sed et ex publica<br>praelectionum suarum cathedra Tychonianarum Hypotheseon se primum esse inventorem sitA gloriatus. Querela illa certior fuit, quod multis bonorum virorum<br>testimonijs et literis videbam TYCHONI fidem eius rei factam luculenter, et<br>gravior, quod is nonA solus, sed et cum alijs duobus Plagiarijs tantum facinus in TYCHONEM<br>ausus fuisset. Indignabar mecum et ridebam tamen cum<br>Phavorino cornices illas, homines in alienis plumis tripudiantes, ridiculos,<br>quod ambitiose ad maiora aspirarent, odiosos, quod ea consequerenturA apud quosdam, miserabiles, quod tandem apud omnes laude turpiter<br>exciderent. Solent enim subventanea (quod aiunt)<br>parere, qui laudem ex fuco virtutis, non ex virtute, concipiunt. Hac spe<br>potuissem ego et meum et tuum animum tranquillum reddere, nisi insuper adiectum<br>fuisset, quod propius me tangit et pungit acrius. Legi enim LIDDELIJ illius verba, quod is in<br>testimonium Plagij sui (cuius tamen TYCHONEM praepostere insimulare vult) me<br>testem invocet et adducat. Sic enim scribit: »Primas meas<br>theorias iuxta triplicem hypothesin satis imperfectas, quas ante septennium<br>Rostochij proposui, TYCHO a Doctore<br>CRAMERO Pastore nunc<br>Stetinensi accepit.« Sic<br>ait. TYCHO BRAHE ergo inventa sua hausit ex meo libro, ex LIDDELIJ praelectionibus, qui harum<br>hypothesium inventor est, qui primus deduxit, qui primus explicuit, si Dijs<br>placet, atque in eius rei testimonium fide dignum, egone a LIDDELIO asciscor? Obstupui, ingenue<br>fateor, ubi haec lego, me in falsum rei testimonium dicendum vocari, et abusu mei<br>nominis (quod hucusque sincerum Dei beneficio servare studui) rem tam a vero<br>alienam ceu pallio involvi et incrustari. Mi Domine ROSENKRANTZI, tute ipsemet nosti,<br>cum tot annos unâ viximus, quo DUNCANUM hunc loco semper habuerim, quam honeste de illo tibi<br>fuerim locutus, quod is fores Scholae Mathematicae et aliarum<br>quarundam Philosophiae partium mihi adolescenti aperuisset; quod autem nunc<br>obloqui illi cogar, id vellem ne fuisset unquam opus. Sed meministi mecum illud<br>PERICLIS Atheniensis, cum amicus eum<br>rogaret, ut pro se causaque eius falsum deieraretA, δεῖ με συμπράττειν τοῖς φίλοις ἀλλὰ μέχρι τοῦ βωμοῦ, et quod Philosophus<br>noster: ἀμφοῖν ὄντοιν φίλοιν ὅσιον προτιμᾶν τὴν ἀλήϑειαν. Non possum enim contra<br>veritatem, nisi quis simul meA adulatore et amico uti velit, quod a pia mente duco esse alienum. Ego de<br>illo Magistro meo non potuissem tantam in me sperare iniuriam, qua me nunc<br>affecit, quem nihil eius conscium ad falsum rei testimonium allegavit.<br>Falsimonium committit (quod in foro crimen falsi appellant) qui annulo<br>signatorio alieno suas periculi et doli plenas clam domino obsignat literas;<br>nonne illum idem patrare credas, qui alienis alienorum nominum subscriptionibus<br>in testimonium sine testimonio vocatis sua falsa confirmat? Praeposterum est te<br>vera loqui, cum ne quidem loquaris, affirmare ea, quae num affirmes nescias. Ita<br>mecum agit LIDDELIUS, ut testis<br>sim, testis nunquam auditus, ut affirmem, ignorem, vera loquar, cum ne quidem<br>loquar. Indignum, insanum. Quia vero tantopere is meummetA hac de re testimonium flagitare videtur, ago verum hoc meum testimonium,<br>contra illud suum, non meum. Tibi et TYCHONI de hoc plagio Lidelliano vera<br>petenti, quantum mihi quidem constat, deponam, idque tam perspicue, ut si tu<br>paulo superiorum annorum ἀνάμνησιν tecum per otium renoves, omnia puncta tua mihi ultro sis adiecturus. Docuit DUNCANUS LIDDELIUS Scotus in Academia Rostochiana Mathemata, quando ego<br>auditor fui anno 88 et sequenti, inter caetera explicando etiam motum secundum<br>iuxta triplicem hypothesin, primam Alphonsinorum, secundam COPERNICI, et aliam tertiam novam.<br>De hac tertia nova agitur, cuius sit auctoris? Me oportet reddere testimonium?<br>Egone respondeam? Respondeat ipse DUNCANUS, idque ex scriptis Commentarijs, quae ego adhuc hodie<br>mecum servo bona fide, qua et servant alij multi, tunc adolescentes, nunc viri,<br>qui tum temporis eadem eiusdem usi sunt institutione privata, inter quos est<br>quoque, ut unum nominem, communis noster amicusA M. IOACHIMUS PRAeTORIUSA, Collega in hoc templo et Gymnasio mihi honorandus. In<br>quibus commentarijs (tu oculatus mihi testis es, vidisti, legisti) DUNCANUS novam illam Diatyposin<br>appellavit Tychonianam, idque expressis syllabis literisque, ita quaestionem suam<br>formans: In nova illa, seu Tychoniana Diatyposi, quomodo horum<br>quinque planetarum motus salvantur? etc. Sic tum temporis docuit, sic<br>dixit, sic dictitavit, sic didicimus, sic audivimus, sic scripsimus, sic<br>credidimus. At, inquis, nunc aliter Helmstadij dicit, aliter docet? Id vero ego capere non possum,<br>quo modo, si DUNCANUS unus sit et<br>esse velit, Rostochij hanc hypothesin sibi<br>derogare, Helmstadij vero eandem arrogare<br>per tam breve locorum temporisque intervallum queat? Nisi cum Horizonte animum<br>linguamque mutarit, quod ego vix concesserim. Credo ego<br>potius DUNCANO LIDDELIO<br>Rostochiensi Studioso contra DUNCANUM<br>LIDDELIUM Professorem Helmstadianum. Dicit forte prae modestiaA et verecundia se illa vice TYCHONI inventionem ascripsisse? Ergone hac<br>vice verecundiae fines transilijt? Ergone contrarium hoc immodestiae ascribendum?<br>Et qui novit viri ingenium, neque hoc admittet. Quid multis? Barbari dicere<br>solebant, prout iacet litera; ita hîc quoque dicendum et pronunciandum iamA est, ex dictatis Rostochianis unius LIDDELIJ tertiam hypothesin, quam et nova placita solebat<br>appellare, non DUNCANI sed<br>TYCHONIS esse; propria confessio, inquiunt Iurisperiti, est fortissima probatio.<br>Satis superque haec tibi, mihi et alijs sufficere possent, nisi, quia sic sermo<br>coeptus est, unum et alterum adhuc verbum ex ijsdem liberet adijcere. Dici non<br>potest, quam praeclare tumA temporis in ipsa tractatione istius hypotheseos, quoties occasio dabatur,<br>de TYCHONE Scotus senserit, et omnibus veteribus praetulerit, et cum<br>Illustrissimo Landgravio Hassiae b. m.<br>coniunxerit. Sic enim in eiusdem commentarijs tit. de longit. et latit. in Sphaeram BRUCAeI legitur. »An vero stellae fixae suas<br>latitudines sive distantias ab Ecliptica variarint, nullus veterum cognovit,<br>verum Landgravius et TYCHO BRAHE<br>ex crebris observationibus invenerunt stellas fixas latitudines suas ad<br>mutationem obliquitatis Zodiaci variasse.« Et de latitudine ☾ ait: »Quod antea a<br>nemine ante TYCHONEM observatum est, latitudo Lunae maxima<br>mutata est, et hodie est 5° 15'.« Item de eccentricitate<br>Solis tit. de motu Solis inquit: »in quibus omnibus Artifices hoc<br>seculo, ut sunt Illustrissimus Princeps Landgravius et TYCHO BRAHE, multa invenerunt, quae<br>observationibus COPERNICI non satis consentiunt.« Sic ille. Quis<br>hîc, quaeso, per febrim potuisset suspicari, LIDDELIUM tanti artificis, quem sic ipse commendaret,<br>praeceptorem esse, et illum huius discipulum, uti nunc iactitare audet? Ni<br>seipsum Scotus tantumA artificem, aut artificibus illis artificiosiorem et superiorem<br>constituisset, talis non constituereturA. Sed tu, Domine ROSENKRANTZI, pro ἀγχινοίᾳ tua, qua polles, me etiam tacente,<br>aliud argumentum certitudinis pro TYCHONE, ut opinor, colliges, quando audisA, quod observata, quae COPERNICO non satis consentiunt, TYCHONI ascribat, ut sic tecum<br>ratiocinere: Qui novas hypotheses in Astronomia contra omnes antegressores suos<br>invenire et statuere vult, is ut longe prius observata τῶν φαινομένων Syderalium<br>nova acquisierit, necesse habet; quare? quia hypotheses novae, non praesuppositis<br>novorum φαινομένων fundamentis, anilia sunt τερετίσματα, quandoquidem<br>hypothesium sit salvare motus observatos; quod si ergo LIDDELIUS Scotus in obscuro latens et<br>instrumentis destitutus nulla φαινόμενα nova primus observavit, quid eum principioA impulit, ut ab hypothesi Coperniana (quam mordicus tueri solebat)<br>desiliret, et de novaA cogitare inciperet? Mirum enim, si se observata a TYCHONE primo accepisse largiri posset. Sic me iudice haudA leve argumentum duxeris ad evincendum DUNCANI Plagium. Taceo, quod in ipsa tractatione huius novae<br>hypotheseos, talem se praestiterit LIDDELIUS, ut multis indicijs sit colligere, eum auctorem et<br>inventorem non esse. Qui enim ipse inventorA et auspex est in scientia apodictica certae alicuius hypotheseos, quam<br>alios docet, illum dedecet fluctuare aut aliquid eius in medio suspensum<br>relinquere. Sic enim DUNCANUS, ubi<br>generaliter Tychonianum typum depinxit: »An primus motus, quo<br>omnia ab ortu in occasum feruntur, ob Terrae motum an coeli extimi rectius<br>dicatur, in medio hîc relinquimus; probabilius forte videbitur, si extimo<br>coelo ascribatur.« Audis, quam non usurpet sibi arbitrium<br>statuendi, decidendi, sed aliunde quasi afflatum exspectet, quid hîc sentiendum<br>sit, quid non sit, quem procul dubio tum temporis nondum ex TYCHONE hauserat.<br>Fortius est, quod ibidem paulo post ansam praebet sentiendi et disputandi contra<br>hanc Hypothesin, cum inquit: »COPERNICI hypothesin absurdam<br>plerisque videri, hanc vero (Tychonianam subintelligens) principijs<br>Geometriae nihil repugnareA, licet nonnulla videatur physica absurda ponere.«<br>Intelligebat autem orbes aethereos ex ARISTOTELIS sententia, quod paulo<br>post usu ex disputatione Physicae Aristoteleae cum eodem habita didici. Proposuit<br>enim idem ibidem Disputationes physicas, in quarum nona, cum ad libros priores<br>de coelo venisset, intrepide orbes reales defendere volebat, quos cum exercitij ergo opponendo Tychoniana observata refellerem, excanduit<br>homo tantum in modum, ut etiam hanc datam hypothesin plane repudiaret et<br>damnaret, immemor fortasse τῆς μεταβάσεως τοῦ γένους. Unde acerbitati istius ego<br>cedens, surrexi tum temporis, atque Scholis istius valedixi, ut meministi me tibiA saepius narrasse, et plurimi adhuc in vivis sunt, qui et spectatores et<br>auditores eius fabulae fuerunt. Quae ideo repeto, non ut de re ipsa (quam inter<br>puerilia nostra pono) agam, sed ut evincam LIDDELIUM semper hypothesin illam sic tractasse, sic contra<br>disputasse, ut alterius, non suum inventum esse clarissime dederit cuivis<br>contestatum. Rigidius enim pro illa pugnasset, si proprium figmentum (ex quo<br>immortaliter se victurum esse sperasset) fuisset. Talis, ut audis, ROSENKRANTZI, fuit Scotus in Schola illa Astronomica<br>Rostochij, quoties collecto grege illi<br>docenti assedimus; sed est tamen quod dissimulare nequeo, quod et alij mei tum<br>temporis commilitones (quos nominare possumA) norunt. Quando M. DUNCANUS<br>haud obscure colligeret, magno applausu excipi TYCHONIS novam, eamque tam<br>concinnam hypothesin, tumA laudis, ut opinor, immatura aviditate flagrans (ut erat natura<br>gloriosior) uni et alteri suorum auditorum (coetu dimisso) insusurrare solebat,<br>se habere literas propria TYCHONIS manu exaratas, in quibus TYCHO illi hanc<br>hypothesin adscribat, his inter caetera verbis: »Rem acu<br>tetigisti«. Verum nunquam tamen plene literas eas mihi quidem<br>visendas legendasveA<br>volebat aperire, nescio cur, etiam cum multis rogaretur. Quia<br>autem integras legere non licuit, multi mecum potius credebant publicis<br>commentarijs DUNCANI, quam eiusdem<br>clancularijs et subreptitijs susurris. Exemplar istarum, quas iactat, literarum<br>Helmstadij descriptum mihique<br>communicatum nunc legi, sed lectis illis merito exclamo: Quid est ollas et<br>cucurbitas lippire, si hoc non sit? Seipsum amare et sapere vix Iovi conceditur.<br>Perstringit enim amor sui oculos, ut quid album a nigro distet, discerni non<br>queat. Sed ego illud prolixius hîc agere non teneor. Epistola ubi in lucem<br>fuerit protracta, pro se loquetur.Habes, domine ROSENKRANTZI, quid mihi ex Liddelianis commentarijs constet, de<br>inventione novae seu Tychonianae hypotheseos. Quod etsi fere praeterire potuissem,<br>tamen ne tuae petitioni deesse voluisse viderer, nolui omittere. At vero plus mea<br>interest, an verum dicat Scotus,<br>quod propositas a se ante Septennium Theorias a me acceperit TYCHO, ut affirmare<br>non erubescit.Narrabo autem brevibus, ut se res habet, atque non minus tibi quam<br>mihi notum, ut quasi te tibi narrantem me audias,<br>modo recorderis. Cum Anno 91, a. d. 14 Calend. Iunij e palaestra Academica<br>Rostochiana avocati essemus ab Illustri tuo Domino Parente, GEORGIO, Prorege Daniae b. m. et substitissemus Hafniae aliquamdiu, indultum est utrique, ut ad<br>Insulam Huenam (qua Urania TYCHONIS tum<br>temporis pedem fixerat) excurreremusA, quod Dominus parens tuus intelligebat, nos quoque singulari Astronomiae<br>amore accendi et magni Dnum TYCHONEM ex illa arte facere.<br>Hac occasione ego, ut cum artifice de arte conferre possem, coepi recentius<br>ὑπομνήματα, quae tam a DUNCANO,<br>quam Dno<br>BRUCAeO, illo magnae famae viro, et alijs<br>praeceptoribus meis imbibissem, ruminare. Et quia partim navi eundum erat, ad<br>facundum itineris comitem commentaria, quae uno grandi volumine complicata<br>habebam, et adhuc habeo, ut nosti, adhibere coepi. In quo volumine inter alia<br>etiam LIDDELIJ dictata<br>continebantur. Ita κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς librum ad Insulam Tychonianam mei, non illius<br>causa transtuli. Ubi illuc ventum est, post multa, ut fit, familiares et eruditae<br>collationes de rebus varijs (quia tota insula erat cornu copiae rerum visu et<br>auditu mirabilium) itemque de arte Astronomica inciderunt et, nescio qua<br>occasione, de BRUCAeO, de LIDDELIO et alijs in hac scientia tumA temporis in Academia Rosarum claris. Sensi autem statim non levibus ex<br>signis, TYCHONEM sinistre sentire de LIDDELIO, quod is ea, quae ex privato cum TYCHONE colloquio,<br>quando ante ibi fuisset, furtim subduxisset, alijs in Academia iniussus<br>propalasset. Ego excusavi virum, quod is non sibi illa nova placita arrogasset,<br>sed nominatim sub Tychoniano titulo et honore proposuisset. Quod cum difficulter<br>sibi pateretur persuaderi Dominus TYCHO, oportune incidebat, me subito<br>interventu fidem facere posse libro meo, quem manu propria ex<br>ore ipsius excepissem. Sic ecce producitur liber, ostendo locum unum et alterum,<br>ubi hoc fiebat, ut supra annotavi. Quo viso acquievit plane Vir Nobilis, et de<br>istis commentarijs inter nos nihil quicquam amplius actum, nihil cogitatum,<br>nihil somniatum est ab illo toto tempore usque in hodiernum diem. Septimus nunc<br>agitur annus ab illo tempore, quo Uraniburgi ego et tu fuimus. Ante illud tempus nec ego nec<br>literae meae, nec manus nec nomen in Daniam,<br>quod ego sciverim, ad TYCHONEM unquam venerunt. Quis enim ego, adolescens tum<br>temporis, quis, inquam, eram aetate, ingenio, fama, ut cum tanto viro et genere<br>et fama Nobilissimo atque ab ipso Magistro nostro saepe proclamato huius saeculi<br>artifice, qui cum Landgravio solus<br>esset in Astronomia artifex, literis amicitiam contraxissem, aut mea ad illum<br>mittere potuissem? Post illud tempus, quo et secunda vice, interlapso triennio,<br>in Daniam redijmus, ego nullas de rebus<br>Astronomicis vel ad TYCHONEM vel alium quenquam dedi aut dare potui literas. Meministi enim professionis meae Witebergensis<br>Herculeas molestias, et studiorum genus longe sanctius, quam ut de illisA rebus mihi in Theologico studio semper παρέργοις ne per suspirium<br>cogitare, nedum ad Artificem aliquid conferre quiverim. Effricta igitur fronte<br>mentitur (da verbo veniam) qui dicit TYCHONEM unquam Theorias Liddelianas a me<br>accepisse. Dictum puta. In testimonium veritatis vocoA conscientiam meam, quae mihi mille testes est, et teA, Dn. OLIGERE, qui<br>probe adhuc annales istius διατριβῆς nostrae Academiae, in qua<br>iuncti unis aedibus, una mensa, uno lecto, diu noctuque, domi foris fuimus, ni<br>fallor, remetiris. Iniuria igitur Scoti estA, eaque in me iniuriosissima, quod me nunquam auditum hoc modo in<br>testimonium sui falsimonij invocat, tantum ut me invisum et suspectum bonis<br>viris, quos contra insurgit, reddat; malitia, quod alios abusu mei nominis<br>decepit; impudentia, quod me ignorantem et nihil tale cogitantem in suos<br>praescriptos cogit gyros, ut mea lingua, non quod mea mens verum esse concepit,<br>loquatur, sed quod ille finxit, vel absens effutiat. Nonne os meum quovis<br>mancipio hoc pacto esset servilius? Satis pro imperio. Unde vero scit, TYCHONEM<br>theorias has a me accepisse? Ego non dixi nec potui dicere, ille non vidit nec<br>potuit videre, neque ullus alius. Si audivisse se dixerit, reddam cum THALETE Milesio responsum, qui aiebat verum a falso<br>tantum abesse, quam oculi distant ab auribus, sentiens eos plerumque mentiri,<br>qui audita non visa referunt. Aut quid si dicam, quod dicat se audivisse, quae<br>nunquam audivit? Si per divinationem sit assecutus, dicat an ex sortibus, an<br>augurijs, an ex asterismis, an somnijs. Sed ex his procul dubio, nisi forte<br>compertum sit, ex graculorum garritu, εἶπε καὶ ἁ Γραῖα τἀλαϑέα κοσκινόμαντις, dicebat ille apud THEOCRITUM.Habes, Nobilissime Domine ROSENCRANTZI, non ex ingratitudine in praeceptorem olim meum (quae<br>enim haec gratitudo in gratiam praeceptoris commentiri?) sed ex<br>candore et veritate, qua amicitia nostra fundatur, redditum verum testimonium<br>contra effictum illud, non meum de plagio, ut vocas, Liddeliano. Tu hoc exemplo<br>edoctus recordare tui usitati symboli Νῆφε καὶ μέμνησο ἀπιστεῖν: tum alias, tum<br>si alij in posterum de me similibus mendaciorum floccis tuas aures opplere tentarintA, reserva alteram mihi liberam, quod cum feceris, et veritas et amicitia<br>nostra in aeviternum sibi inter nos constabunt. His te Deo commendatum volo.Vale.Deproperatum Stetini, Anno salutis reparatae CIϽ IϽ IICA. II Calend. Aprilis.N. T. semper<br>amicusDANIEL<br>CRAMERUS,Pastor et Professor Ecclesiae et Gymnasij<br>Stetinensium.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Master Holger Rosenkrantz, son of Jørgen, the Dane, hereditary lord of Rosenholm, learned among nobles and noble among the learned, his lord and friend to be honoured for ever — into [his] hands [ΕΙΣ ΧΕΙΡΑ]. To be opened and read no less by Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p><b>Fare well [ΕΥ ΠΡΑΤΤΕΙΝ].</b></p><p>How fondly I kissed your two letters — the one sent me from Lübeck, the other from Hamburg — my mind, bound to you by many benefits received and known to you over many years as though in a mirror and on a tablet, can declare to you, noble Rosenkrantz, though my tongue cannot. For you yourself know how earnestly I love you, honour you, and how much I esteem you, so that as often as I see your letters, so often a cold sweat is poured over me, and I am persuaded that I am spurred on by you to a more extended course of friendship. And since I perceive that you perceive this from your own letters, there is no need for me to assure you of it at greater length. Yet however sweet each of your letters was to me, they were nonetheless somewhat the more bitter because in them you at the same time told of the Liddelian plagiarism committed against the illustrious astronomer Tycho Brahe — your kinsman and relative by marriage, a man to be admired for his manifold learning. What struck my mind the more was that Master Tycho himself had attested the same to me in his own letters. He complained to you of the singular impudence and ingratitude alike of Duncan Liddel the Scot — who now acts as Professor of Mathematics in the Illustrious [Academia] Julia — in that, not only in letters privately given to his own circle, but also from the public chair of his lectures, he had boasted himself to be the first inventor of the Tychonic hypotheses. That complaint was the more certain because, by the many testimonies and letters of good men, I saw that the matter had been brilliantly brought home to Tycho [as proven]; and the more grievous because Liddel had dared so great a crime against Tycho not alone, but together with two other plagiarists. I was indignant within myself, and yet, with Favorinus, I laughed at those crows — men strutting in others' feathers — ridiculous, because they ambitiously aspired to greater things; odious, because they attained them with some people; pitiable, because in the end before all men they fell shamefully from praise. For those who conceive praise from the counterfeit of virtue, and not from virtue, are wont (as the saying is) to bring forth wind-eggs. With this hope I could have made both my own mind and yours tranquil, had there not been added besides what touches me more nearly and stings me more sharply. For I have read the words of that Liddel, [namely] that in testimony of his own plagiarism (with which, however, he wishes preposterously to charge Tycho) he calls and brings me forward as a witness. For thus he writes: »The first theories of mine according to the threefold hypothesis, quite imperfect, which seven years ago I set forth at Rostock, Tycho received from Doctor Cramer, now pastor at Stettin.« So he says. Tycho Brahe, then, drew his discoveries from my book, from the lectures of Liddel — who is the inventor of these hypotheses, who first deduced them, who first expounded them, if you please — and in testimony of that matter, trustworthy: am I co-opted by Liddel? I was dumbfounded, I frankly confess, when I read this — that I was being summoned to give false testimony in the matter, and that by the abuse of my name (which up to now, by God's favour, I have striven to keep untainted) a thing so alien from the truth was being wrapped up and plastered over as with a cloak. My lord Rosenkrantz, you yourself well know — since we lived together so many years — in what regard I always held this Duncan, how honourably I spoke of him to you, because he had opened to me as a youth the doors of the School of Mathematics and of certain other parts of Philosophy; but that I am now compelled to speak against him, that I could wish there had never been need of. But you remember with me that [saying] of Pericles of Athens, when a friend asked him to swear a false oath on his behalf and for his cause: <i>δεῖ με συμπράττειν τοῖς φίλοις ἀλλὰ μέχρι τοῦ βωμοῦ</i> [\"I must aid my friends, but only as far as the altar\"]; and what our Philosopher [said]: <i>ἀμφοῖν ὄντοιν φίλοιν ὅσιον προτιμᾶν τὴν ἀλήθειαν</i> [\"both being dear, it is right to prefer the truth\"]. For I cannot [act] against the truth — unless someone should wish to use me at once as flatterer and as friend, which I hold to be alien from a pious mind. From that Master of mine I could not have hoped for so great an injury against me as that with which he has now afflicted me, in that he has alleged me — who am in no way privy to it — for false testimony in the matter. He commits forgery [<i>falsimonium</i>] (what in the courts they call the crime of falsehood) who seals letters full of danger and deceit with another's signet-ring, secretly, without the owner's knowledge; would you not believe that man to perpetrate the same thing, who confirms his own falsehoods by the subscriptions of others' names — names of others called to give testimony without [giving any] testimony? It is a preposterous thing for you to speak truths when you do not even speak; to affirm those things which you do not know whether you affirm. So Liddel deals with me — that I should be a witness, a witness never heard; that I should affirm, [though] I am ignorant; that I should speak truths, when I do not even speak. Unworthy! Insane! But since he seems so greatly to demand my own testimony on this matter, I give this true testimony of mine, against that [testimony] of his — [which is] not mine. To you and to Tycho, seeking the truth about this Liddelian plagiarism, I shall depose, as far at least as is clear to me, and so plainly that, if you renew with yourself at leisure the recollection [<i>ἀνάμνησιν</i>] of the years a little earlier, you will of your own accord supply me all your points. Duncan Liddel the Scot taught Mathematics in the Academy of Rostock — when I was a hearer in the year [15]88 and the following — among other things expounding also the second motion according to a threefold hypothesis: the first, that of the Alphonsines; the second, that of Copernicus; and another, a third, a new one. It is about this third, the new one, that the matter turns: whose author is it? Must I give testimony? Am I to answer? Let Duncan himself answer — and that from his written <i>Commentaries</i>, which to this very day I still keep with me in good faith, as do many others also: youths then, men now, who at that time made use of the same private instruction by the same man; among whom is also, to name but one, our common friend Master Joachim Praetorius, my colleague to be honoured in this church and Gymnasium. In these <i>Commentaries</i> (you are an eye-witness for me; you saw, you read) Duncan called that new <i>Diatyposis</i> [outline] \"Tychonic,\" and that in express syllables and letters, thus framing his question: \"In that new, or Tychonic, <i>Diatyposis</i>, how are the motions of these five planets saved?\" etc. So at that time he taught, so he said, so he repeatedly said, so we learned, so we heard, so we wrote down, so we believed. But, you say, does he now say otherwise at Helmstedt, teach otherwise? That indeed I cannot grasp: how, if Duncan is and wishes to be one [and the same man], he could over so brief an interval of place and time deny this hypothesis to himself at Rostock, yet at Helmstedt claim that same one for himself? Unless he has changed mind and tongue along with the horizon — which I would scarcely grant. I rather believe Duncan Liddel the Rostock student against Duncan Liddel the Helmstedt professor. Does he perhaps say that out of modesty and shamefastness he on that occasion ascribed the invention to Tycho? Then did he on that occasion leap over the bounds of shamefastness? Then is this contrary [conduct] to be ascribed to immodesty? And whoever knows the man's character will not admit even this. Why [say] more? The barbarians used to say, \"as the letter lies\"; so here too it must now be said and pronounced, that — out of the Rostock dictations of Liddel alone — the third hypothesis, which he used also to call \"the new tenets,\" is not Duncan's but Tycho's; one's own confession, the jurists say, is the strongest proof. These things might be enough and more than enough for you, for me, and for others, were it not that — since the discourse has begun thus — I am minded to add still a word or two from the same [Commentaries]. It cannot be said how splendidly, at that time, in the very treatment of that hypothesis, whenever occasion offered, the Scot thought of Tycho, and preferred him to all the ancients, and joined him with the Most Illustrious Landgrave of Hesse of blessed memory. For thus in his Commentaries, under the heading \"On longitude and latitude,\" in [the section] on the <i>Sphere</i> of Brucaeus, one reads: »But whether the fixed stars have varied their latitudes or distances from the Ecliptic, none of the ancients knew; but the Landgrave and Tycho Brahe, from frequent observations, found that the fixed stars have varied their latitudes in accordance with the change of the obliquity of the Zodiac.« And on the latitude of the Moon [☾] he says: »What was previously observed by no one before Tycho — the maximum latitude of the Moon has changed, and today it is 5° 15'.« Likewise on the eccentricity of the Sun, under the heading \"On the motion of the Sun,\" he says: »in all which matters the craftsmen of this age, such as the Most Illustrious Prince the Landgrave and Tycho Brahe, have found many things which do not sufficiently agree with the observations of Copernicus.« So he. Who here, I ask, even in a fever, could have suspected that Liddel was the <i>teacher</i> of so great a craftsman, whom he himself thus commended — and that craftsman his <i>pupil</i>, as he now dares to boast? Unless the Scot had set himself up as so great a craftsman, or as more skilful and superior to those craftsmen, he would not be set up as such. But you, lord Rosenkrantz, by your shrewdness [<i>ἀγχινοίᾳ</i>], in which you are strong — even with me silent — will, I think, gather another argument of certainty in Tycho's favour, when you hear that he ascribes to Tycho the observations which do not sufficiently agree with Copernicus; so that you may reason thus with yourself: He who wishes to invent and establish new hypotheses in Astronomy against all his predecessors must necessarily have first acquired the new observations of the celestial <i>phenomena</i> [<i>τῶν φαινομένων</i>]; why? because new hypotheses, without the foundations of new <i>phenomena</i> [<i>φαινομένων</i>] presupposed, are old wives' <i>jinglings</i> [<i>τερετίσματα</i>], since it is [the task] of a hypothesis to save the observed motions. If, therefore, Liddel the Scot, lurking in obscurity and bereft of instruments, was the first observer of no new <i>phenomena</i> [<i>φαινόμενα</i>], what impelled him at the outset to leap away from the Copernican hypothesis (which he used to defend tooth and nail) and to begin to think of a new one? For it would be a marvel if he could grant that he had first received the observations from Tycho. So, in my judgment, you would reckon this no light argument for proving Duncan's plagiarism. I say nothing of the fact that, in the very treatment of this new hypothesis, Liddel showed himself such that from many indications one may gather that he is not its author and inventor. For one who is himself the inventor and originator, in a demonstrative science, of some certain hypothesis which he teaches others, it ill becomes to waver or to leave any part of it hanging undecided in the middle. For thus Duncan, where he had generally depicted the Tychonic scheme [<i>typum</i>]: »Whether the first motion, by which all things are borne from rising to setting, is more rightly said [to be] on account of the motion of the Earth or of the outermost heaven, we here leave in the middle; perhaps it will seem more probable if it be ascribed to the outermost heaven.« You hear how he does not arrogate to himself the judgment of establishing, of deciding, but as it were awaits inspiration from elsewhere as to what is here to be thought and what not — [inspiration] which without doubt he had at that time not yet drawn from Tycho. Stronger is the fact that, a little later in the same place, he furnishes a handle for thinking and disputing against this hypothesis, when he says: »The hypothesis of Copernicus seems absurd to most, but this one (he means the Tychonic) is in no way repugnant to the principles of Geometry, although it seems to lay down some things physically absurd.« He understood, however, the aethereal orbs after the opinion of Aristotle — which I learned a little afterwards in practice, from a disputation on Aristotelian Physics held with him. For he set forth there Physical Disputations, in the ninth of which, when he had come to the earlier books <i>On the Heavens</i>, he wished intrepidly to defend the real orbs; and when, for exercise' sake, I refuted them by opposing Tychonic observations, the man flared up to such a degree that he even plainly repudiated and condemned this very granted hypothesis — forgetful perhaps of the <i>shift of genus</i> [<i>τῆς μεταβάσεως τοῦ γένους</i>]. Whereupon, yielding to his bitterness, I rose up at that time, and bade farewell to his classes, as you remember I have often told you; and very many are still living who were both spectators and hearers of that comedy. These things I repeat not in order to treat of the matter itself (which I rank among our boyish doings), but to prove that Liddel always so handled that hypothesis, so disputed against it, that he most clearly gave anyone to witness that it was another's invention, not his own. For he would have fought more stiffly for it had it been his own figment (from which he would have hoped to live immortally). Such, as you hear, Rosenkrantz, was the Scot in that Astronomical School at Rostock, as often as, in a gathered flock, we sat by him teaching. But there is yet something I cannot conceal, which also others of my fellow-soldiers [fellow-students] of that time (whom I am able to name) know. When Master Duncan gathered, none too obscurely, that Tycho's new and so elegant hypothesis was being received with great applause, then — burning, I think, with a premature greed for praise (for he was by nature rather vainglorious) — he used to whisper to one and another of his hearers (after the assembly was dismissed) that he had a letter written in Tycho's own hand, in which Tycho ascribes this hypothesis to him, among other things in these words: »You have hit the matter with the needle [i.e. exactly]«. But he never wished to open those letters fully for me, at least, to see and read — I know not why — even when asked by many. And because it was not permitted to read them whole, many with me rather believed Duncan's public Commentaries than his clandestine and stealthy whisperings. A copy of those letters which he vaunts, transcribed at Helmstedt and communicated to me, I have now read; but, having read them, I justly exclaim: What is \"to be blear-eyed at pots and gourds\" [i.e. to mistake one obvious thing for another], if this be not it? To love oneself and be wise is scarcely granted to Jupiter [himself]. For self-love blinds the eyes, so that one cannot discern how white differs from black. But I am not bound to treat that here at greater length. The letter, once it has been brought into the light, will speak for itself.</p><p>You have, lord Rosenkrantz, what is clear to me from the Liddelian Commentaries about the invention of the new, or Tychonic, hypothesis. And although I could almost have passed it over, yet, lest I should seem to have been unwilling to meet your request, I would not omit it. But it concerns me more whether the Scot speaks truth in [saying] that Tycho received from me the theories he set forth seven years before — as he does not blush to affirm. I shall relate in brief how the matter stands — and [it is] known no less to you than to me, so that you may hear me as it were narrating you to yourself, if only you recall. When in the year [15]91, on the fourteenth day before the Kalends of June [19 May], we had been summoned away from the Academic wrestling-ground [<i>palaestra</i>] of Rostock by your illustrious lord father Jørgen, Viceroy of Denmark of blessed memory, and had stopped at Copenhagen for some while, leave was granted to us both to make an excursion to the island of Hven (where Tycho's Urania had at that time fixed her foot), because your lord father understood that we too were kindled with a singular love of Astronomy and made much of Master Tycho on account of that art. On this occasion I, that I might confer about the art with the craftsman, began to ruminate the more recent notes [<i>ὑπομνήματα</i>] which I had imbibed both from Duncan and from Master Brucaeus — that man of great fame — and from my other teachers. And because part of the way had to be made by ship, I began to take along, as an eloquent companion for the journey, the commentaries which I had folded up in one large volume, and still have, as you know. In which volume, among other things, Liddel's dictations too were contained. Thus <i>incidentally</i> [<i>κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς</i>] I carried the book to the Tychonic island for my own sake, not for his. When we had arrived there, after many — as happens — familiar and learned conferences about various matters (for the whole island was a horn of plenty of things wonderful to see and to hear), there fell in [conversation] also about the art of Astronomy, and, by I know not what occasion, about Brucaeus, about Liddel, and others at that time famous in this science in the Academy of the Roses [Rostock]. But I sensed at once, from no slight signs, that Tycho thought ill of Liddel, because Liddel had, unbidden, divulged to others in the Academy those things which he had filched by stealth from a private conversation with Tycho, when he had earlier been there. I excused the man, on the ground that he had not arrogated those new tenets to himself, but had set them forth expressly under the Tychonic title and honour. And since Master Tycho suffered himself to be persuaded of this with difficulty, it fell out opportunely that I, by my sudden intervention, could give proof by my own book, which I had taken down with my own hand from his [Liddel's] very mouth. So behold, the book is produced; I show the one place and the other where this was [written], as I noted above. Which seen, the Noble Man was fully satisfied, and nothing whatever more was done about those Commentaries between us, nothing thought, nothing dreamed of by him from that whole time up to the present day. It is now the seventh year being completed since that time when you and I were at Uraniborg. Before that time neither I, nor my letters, nor my hand, nor my name ever came into Denmark, to Tycho, so far as I have known. For who was I, a youth at that time — who, I say, was I in age, in talent, in fame — that I should have contracted a friendship by letters with so great a man, most noble in both lineage and fame, and proclaimed often by our own Master himself the craftsman of this age (who with the Landgrave was the sole craftsman in Astronomy), or could have sent anything of mine to him? After that time, when a second time too, with three years elapsed, we returned into Denmark, I gave, and could give, no letters about Astronomical matters either to Tycho or to anyone else. For you remember the Herculean troubles of my professorship at Wittenberg, and a kind of studies far more sacred, than that I could — concerning those matters, always <i>by-works</i> [<i>παρέργοις</i>] to me in theological study — so much as think by [a single] sigh, much less confer anything to the craftsman. With brazen brow, therefore, he lies (pardon the word) who says that Tycho ever received Liddelian theories from me. Take it as said. In testimony of the truth I call my conscience, which is a thousand witnesses to me, and you, Master Holger, who, if I am not mistaken, still duly recount the annals of that <i>passing of time</i> [<i>διατριβῆς</i>] of our Academy, in which, joined under one roof, at one table, in one bed, day and night, at home and abroad, we lived together. It is therefore an injury of the Scot, and that most injurious toward me, that he invokes me — never heard — in this manner in testimony of his own forgery, only that he may render me hateful and suspect to the good men against whom he rises up; [it is] malice, that he has deceived others by the abuse of my name; [it is] impudence, that he forces me — ignorant and thinking nothing of the kind — into his prescribed circles, so that my tongue may speak, not what my mind has conceived to be true, but what he has fabricated, or may, [though] absent, blurt out. Would not my mouth in this fashion be more servile than any slave's? Enough by way of command. But whence does he know that Tycho received these theories from me? I did not say it, nor could I say it; he did not see it, nor could he see it; nor did any other. If he should say that he heard it, I shall return the answer with Thales of Miletus, who used to say that the true is as far from the false as the eyes are distant from the ears — meaning that those mostly lie who report things heard, not seen. Or what if I should say that he says he heard things which he never heard? If he attained it by divination, let him say whether from lots, or auguries, or from constellations, or dreams. But [he attained it] from these, without doubt — unless perhaps it be ascertained — from the chattering of jackdaws: <i>εἶπε καὶ ἁ Γραῖα τἀλαθέα κοσκινόμαντις</i> [\"the old woman, the sieve-diviner, spoke the truth too\"], said that one in Theocritus.</p><p>You have, most noble lord Rosenkrantz, the true testimony rendered — not out of ingratitude toward him who was once my teacher (for what gratitude is this, to lie for a teacher's favour?), but out of the candour and truth on which our friendship is founded — against that fabricated [testimony], not mine, concerning the plagiarism, as you call it, of Liddel. Taught by this example, remember your accustomed motto, <i>Νῆφε καὶ μέμνησο ἀπιστεῖν</i> [\"Be sober and remember to distrust\"]: both on other occasions, and especially if others should hereafter try to fill your ears with similar wisps of lies about me, keep the other [ear] free for me; and when you have done so, both the truth and our friendship will stand firm between us for ever. With these [words] I would have you commended to God. Farewell. Hurried off at Stettin, in the year of salvation restored 1598, on the second day before the Kalends of April [31 March]. Your friend ever, Daniel Cramer, Pastor and Professor of the Church and Gymnasium of the Stettiners.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Daniel Cramer</b> (1568–1637), Pomeranian Lutheran theologian; in 1591 the young preceptor accompanying Holger Rosenkrantz, later professor and pastor at Stettin (Szczecin), having held a professorship at Wittenberg. The corpus confirms he visited Uraniborg with Rosenkrantz in 1591 (Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (<i>Oligerus</i>; 1574–1642), Danish nobleman of Rosenholm, son of <b>Jørgen (Georgius) Rosenkrantz</b> (here <i>Prorege Daniae b.m.</i> = \"Viceroy of Denmark, of blessed memory\"), and Cramer's former pupil and fellow-lodger at Rostock; Tycho's <i>consanguineus et affinis</i> (kinsman and relative by marriage). The address directs that the letter be opened and read <b>also by Tycho Brahe</b> — i.e. it is the deposition Tycho had requested through Rosenkrantz.</li><li><b>Subject of the dispute.</b> <b>Duncan Liddel</b> (1561–1613), Scottish mathematician-physician, then <b>Professor of Mathematics at Helmstedt</b> (<i>Inclyta Iulia</i> = the Academia Julia, the University of Helmstedt; <i>Helmstadij</i>). Liddel had earlier taught at <b>Rostock</b> (<i>Academia Rosarum</i>, \"Academy of the Roses\" — a humanist pun on Rostock's civic rose). The corpus (Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>; Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>) confirms the affair: Liddel was accused of claiming Tycho's geoheliocentric world-system as his own, and of citing Cramer (\"the learned Cramer, pastor at Stettin\") as the channel by which Tycho supposedly received \"the hypothesis I proposed seven years ago at Rostock.\" This letter is Cramer's indignant denial.</li><li><b>Other persons.</b> <i>Brucaeus</i> = <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b> (Brucäus, 1531–1593), professor of mathematics/medicine at Rostock (his <i>Sphaera</i> is the textbook on which Liddel's <i>Commentaries</i> comment). <i>M. Ioachimus Praetorius</i> = <b>Joachim Praetorius</b>, Cramer's colleague at the Stettin Gymnasium, cited as a fellow-witness to Liddel's Rostock dictations. <i>Illustrissimus Landgravius Hassiae b.m.</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (d. 1592; <i>b.m.</i> = <i>beatae memoriae</i>, \"of blessed memory\"). <i>Phavorinus</i> = <b>Favorinus</b> of Arelate (the 2nd-c. sophist), source of the \"crows in others' feathers\" topos. <i>Thales Milesius</i> = <b>Thales of Miletus</b>. <i>Theocritus</i> — the citation is from <i>Idyll</i> III (the goatherd's serenade), of the sieve-diviner old woman; see Greek note below.</li><li><b>The technical core.</b> <i>triplex hypothesis</i> = the <b>threefold hypothesis</b> of the second (planetary) motion taught by Liddel — first the <b>Alphonsine</b> (Ptolemaic), second the <b>Copernican</b>, and \"another, third, new one\" (<i>tertia nova</i>), i.e. the <b>Tychonic</b> geoheliocentric system. <i>Diatyposis</i> (Gk <i>διατύπωσις</i>, \"outline/sketch/scheme\") and <i>typus</i> both = Liddel's diagram/scheme of the system; kept transliterated and glossed. <i>salvantur / salvare motus</i> = \"are saved / to save the motions\" (the <i>salvare apparentias</i> topos), per glossary §5.1. <i>eccentricitas Solis</i> → <b>eccentricity of the Sun</b>; <i>latitudo ☾ maxima</i> → <b>maximum latitude of the Moon</b> (the glyph ☼/☾ is kept in the Source block and named \"Moon\"); <i>obliquitas Zodiaci</i> → <b>obliquity of the Zodiac</b>; <i>orbes aethereos / orbes reales</i> → <b>aethereal orbs / real orbs</b> (the physical solid-sphere question Liddel defended after Aristotle and Tycho's comet observations had undermined). <i>Copernici hypothesin absurdam … principijs Geometriae nihil repugnare … physica absurda ponere</i> — Cramer quotes Liddel's own hedged verdict; the hedges (<i>forte</i>, <i>videatur</i>) are preserved, not sharpened.</li><li><b>Greek interpolations (transcribed and translated; printed forms kept in Source block, including final-sigma and the corrupt forms flagged below).</b></li><li><i>ΕΙΣ ΧΕΙΡΑ</i> (printed \"ΕΙΣ XEIPE.\" with Latin Ε/Χ/Ρ letterforms) — \"into [his] hand(s),\" an address-direction; the printed truncation is editorial. <b>Flagged</b>: the printed \"XEIPE\" is a garbled <i>χεῖρα(ς)</i>.</li><li><i>ΕΥ ΠΡΑΤΤΕΙΝ</i> — \"to fare well / do well,\" the standard Greek epistolary salutation (used by Plato; pointedly preferred to <i>χαίρειν</i>). Rendered \"Fare well\" as the opening greeting.</li><li><i>δεῖ με συμπράττειν τοῖς φίλοις ἀλλὰ μέχρι τοῦ βωμοῦ</i> — \"I must cooperate with my friends, but [only] as far as the altar,\" the apophthegm ascribed to Pericles (Plutarch, <i>Mor.</i> 186C / 531D), i.e. not to the point of perjury.</li><li><i>ἀμφοῖν ὄντοιν φίλοιν ὅσιον προτιμᾶν τὴν ἀλήθειαν</i> — \"both being friends [dear], it is right/holy to prefer the truth,\" the Aristotelian <i>amicus Plato sed magis amica veritas</i> topos (cf. <i>Nic. Eth.</i> I.6); \"our Philosopher\" = Aristotle.</li><li><i>ἀνάμνησιν</i> — \"recollection\" (Platonic <i>anamnesis</i>); rendered \"recollection.\"</li><li><i>ἀγχινοίᾳ</i> — \"shrewdness, quickness of mind\"; rendered \"shrewdness.\"</li><li><i>τῶν φαινομένων … φαινομένων … φαινόμενα</i> — \"the phenomena / appearances\" (celestial); kept as <b>phenomena</b> per glossary.</li><li><i>τερετίσματα</i> — \"jinglings, twitterings, idle trills\"; here \"old wives' jinglings\" (paired with <i>anilia</i>).</li><li><i>τῆς μεταβάσεως τοῦ γένους</i> — \"the shifting/transition of genus\" (Aristotelian <i>metabasis eis allo genos</i>, the fallacy of crossing from one domain to another); rendered \"shift of genus.\"</li><li><i>ὑπομνήματα</i> — \"memoranda, notes, lecture-notes\"; rendered \"notes.\"</li><li><i>κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς</i> — \"incidentally, per accidens\" (Aristotelian); rendered \"incidentally.\"</li><li><i>παρέργοις</i> — \"as by-works / side-matters\"; rendered \"by-works.\"</li><li><i>διατριβῆς</i> — \"spending of time / sojourn / [academic] disputation-study\"; here the shared student years, rendered \"passing of time.\"</li><li><i>Νῆφε καὶ μέμνησο ἀπιστεῖν</i> — \"Be sober and remember to distrust,\" ascribed to Epicharmus and a famous early-modern scholarly motto; given here as Rosenkrantz's personal <i>symbolum</i> (motto).</li><li><i>εἶπε καὶ ἁ Γραῖα τἀλαθέα κοσκινόμαντις</i> (printed \"εἶπε καὶ ὰ Γραίω τἀλαϑέα κοσκινόμαντις\") — Theocritus, <i>Idyll</i> III.31: \"and the old sieve-diviner woman spoke the truth too.\" <b>Flagged textual crux</b>: the printed witness reads <i>ὰ Γραίω</i> (with a grave accent and an apparent dative/genitive form) where the vulgate Theocritus has <i>ἁ Γραῖα</i> (nominative, \"the old woman\"); the printed corruption is retained in the Source block and the standard reading is followed in the translation. Cramer's point: if Liddel \"heard\" it, it was as worthless as a sieve-diviner's chatter.</li><li><b>Latin idioms / proverbs.</b> <i>subventanea parere</i> — literally \"to bring forth wind-eggs\" (<i>ova subventanea</i>, unfertilised eggs), i.e. to produce nothing of substance; kept as image. <i>cornices … in alienis plumis tripudiantes</i> — Aesop's crow in borrowed/peacock feathers (the plagiarist topos). <i>ollas et cucurbitas lippire</i> — a proverbial expression of confusing two obvious things (\"to be blear-eyed at pots and gourds\"); rendered with a gloss. <i>Rem acu tetigisti</i> — \"you have touched the matter with the needle,\" i.e. \"you have hit it exactly\" (Plautus); here quoted as the alleged words of Tycho's letter to Liddel, kept in the Source quotation marks. <i>prout iacet litera</i> — \"as the letter lies,\" i.e. according to the literal text. <i>mordicus tueri</i> — \"to defend tooth and nail.\" <i>falsimonium / crimen falsi</i> — the legal <b>crime of falsehood/forgery</b>; <i>propria confessio … fortissima probatio</i> — the maxim \"confession is the strongest proof.\" <i>Satis pro imperio</i> — a Terentian tag, \"enough of issuing commands.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline.</b> <i>Deproperatum Stetini, Anno salutis reparatae</i> <b>CIϽ IϽ IIC</b> <i>, II Calend. Aprilis</i> = \"Hurried off at Stettin, in the year of restored salvation <b>1598</b>, on the <b>second day before the Kalends of April</b>.\" The Roman numeral <i>CIϽ IϽ IIC</i> = 1000 + 500 + 98 = <b>1598</b> (note the subtractive <i>IIC</i> = 98). <i>a.d. II Kal. Apr.</i> (the day before <i>pridie Kal. Apr.</i>) = <b>31 March</b>. The header date (31 March 1598) is therefore taken from the source's own dateline and <b>agrees</b> with the dataset label (\"1598. 31. marts. Stettin\"). Stettin used the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar (Lutheran Pomerania did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700), so the date is O.S. <b>Internal date</b>: <i>Anno 91, a.d. 14 Calend. Iunij</i> = \"the year [15]91, on the 14th day before the Kalends of June\" = <b>19 May 1591</b> (the date of the Rostock summons); not converted, glossed only.</li><li><b>Edition of record &amp; witness.</b> The dataset assigns this letter to the <i>Epistolae ad Ioannem Keplerum scriptae</i> corpus (so printed in Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII); the sole MS witness named is a transcript (<i>Afskrift</i>) in <b>Langebek's Diplomatarium</b>, Rigsarkivet, Copenhagen. The transcription carries scattered stray \"A\" markers (e.g. <i>fueruntA</i>, <i>sitA</i>, <i>nonA</i>, <i>deieraretA</i>, <i>meummetA</i>, <i>PRAeTORIUSA</i>); these are editorial/footnote sigla in the working transcription, not part of the Latin, and are read through (the word stems are translated). They are left in the Source block as printed per protocol §4.2.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin/Greek corrected the corrupt Theocritean tag-quote 'ὰ Γραίω' to 'ἁ Γραῖα' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.43; the English translation's bracketed Greek gloss already carried the correct reading and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — The Latin argument is rendered securely and the identifications (Cramer, Holger and Jørgen Rosenkrantz, Liddel, Brucaeus, the Helmstedt/Rostock affair) are corpus-confirmed; the dateline reading 1598 / 31 March is secure and matches the label. Two genuine cruxes are <b>flagged, not smoothed</b>: (1) the Greek of the address <i>ΕΙΣ XEIPE</i> is a garbled <i>χεῖρα(ς)</i> (\"into [his] hand(s)\"), and (2) the Theocritus tag is printed in a corrupt form <i>ὰ Γραίω</i> against the vulgate <i>ἁ Γραῖα</i> — both kept verbatim in the Source block, with the standard readings followed in translation and the corruption named here. The stray \"A\" sigla in the working transcription are an apparatus artefact, not text. Grade [B] rather than [A] for these two textual cruxes and the residual uncertainty of <i>διατριβῆς</i> (sojourn vs. academic disputation-exercise) in the shared-lodging clause.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 8 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp. 37-44, slice pages 1-8) in two chunks (1-4, 5-8) and collated the full letter clause-by-clause against source_text and published_english: heading/salutation, the Liddel quote ('Primas meas theorias...'), the Pericles/Aristotle Greek tags, all names (Duncan Liddel, Joachim Praetorius, Brucaeus, Landgrave of Hesse, Copernicus, Ptolemy-adjacent refs), all dates (a.d. 14 Kal. Iun. 1591 = 19 May correctly bracketed; II Kal. Apr. 1598 = 31 March correctly bracketed; anno 88 = 1588; septimus annus), the one numeric datum (Moon's maximum latitude 5° 15'), the disputation-nine reference, the closing signature block, and the final Theocritus tag-quote. Every clause of this very long letter has an English counterpart; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found; pronouns/subjects (Tycho vs. Liddel vs. Cramer) are correctly tracked throughout, including the letter's crux claim. One TEXT_ERROR found: source_text's Greek at the Theocritus tag departs from Dreyer's printed ἁ Γραῖα (word-level corruption), which per the grading rule caps the grade at C despite the English translation itself being unaffected and the overall rendering being of very high fidelity.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published source_text corrupts the Theocritean tag-quote near the letter's close: Dreyer prints ἁ Γραῖα (\"the old woman\", rough breathing + correct noun form), our source_text has ὰ Γραίω (grave accent, non-existent word form) — a genuine word-level corruption of the Greek against the printed edition. Notably the English translation's own bracketed Greek gloss silently matches Dreyer's correct reading, not our corrupted source_text, so the English sense itself is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>εἶπε καὶ ἁ Γραῖα τἀλαθέα κοσκινόμαντις</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.41-44 (page images), specifically p.43 line 26 ('...ex graculorum garritu, εἶπε καὶ ἁ Γραῖα τἀλαθέα κοσκινό-μαντις, dicebat ille apud THEOCRITUM'), confirming the flagged corruption. The theta glyph variant (ϑ vs θ) elsewhere in the same word is a typeface alternate already present in our source and not part of the flagged defect, so it was left untouched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Warmly acknowledges two letters from Rosenkrantz expressing deep affection, though noting they were tinged with bitterness by some unhappy news they also conveyed.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980331001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980331002", "d": "1598-03-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Andreas Meier", "ck": "Andreas Meier", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Andreas Meier — Wandsbek, 31 March 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Renterei-Akten (original letter)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>BRIEFF AN DEN HERRENN RENTMEISTERN ANDRES MEIERN.</p><p>JCH Tycho Brahe, erbgesessen zu Knudtstrup vnd kön: maytt: zu Dennemarck ambtman auff Vraniburgk, bekenne mit diesem meinem offenen brieff, daß ich mit dem ehrnhafftenn vnnd achtbarn Andreas Meyernn, furstlichenn meckelburgischen rentmeistern, reden lassen vnndt bey jhm freundlichen ahngelanget, daß ehr auß der rentenn von sechs hundert thallernn, so mir vber ein jahr alda zustehenn werden, ein hundert itzo zuuor außgebenn wolle, daß man dafur im lanndt zue Meckelnburgk etzliche victualien vndt andere sachenn kauffenn köntte, welche hundert thaller sollenn widerumb quitirt werdenn inn den sechß hundertt thallernn, so mir verfallenn, wann die zeit verlauffenn, daß jch oder andere von meinentwegen alßdann nur funffhundert sollenn zu fordern haben, vnndt soll dieser mein brieff ahn stadt dero hundert thaller bleibenn vnndt vorsicherung genueg seinn. Dessenn zu vrkundt hab ich dieß mit eigener hanndt vnterschriebenn vnndt mit meinem angebornenn petzschafft besiegelt.</p><p>Datum Wandeßburgk denn 31t. martij anno 1598<br>Tycho Brahe<br>eigenh. (l.s.)</p><p>[inscriptum est:]</p><p>Hierauf Tycho Brahen zugeschickett:3 faß bier, jeder faß von 3 tonnen,</p><p>6 sch ℔ rogken brott,</p><p>1 tonne butter,</p><p>30 seitten speck, haben gewogen 1½ schippunt 5½ liß ℔,</p><p>6 reichsthaler dem altten meister Peter an bahrem gelde.</p><p>    Peter Zacherow.</p>", "en": "<p><b>A letter to the lord treasurer Andreas Meier.</b></p><p>I, Tycho Brahe, hereditary lord at Knudstrup and bailiff [<i>ambtman</i>] of His Royal Majesty of Denmark at Uraniborg, acknowledge by this my open letter that I have had words conveyed to the honourable and worshipful Andreas Meier, ducal Mecklenburg treasurer, and have made a friendly request of him: that out of the revenue of six hundred thalers which will be owing to me there in over a year's time, he be willing to pay out one hundred now in advance, so that for it one might buy in the land of Mecklenburg certain victuals and other things. Which hundred thalers shall in turn be receipted [discharged] against the six hundred thalers owed to me, [so that] when the time has elapsed, I or others on my behalf shall then have only five hundred to claim; and this my letter shall stand in place of those hundred thalers and be security enough. In witness whereof I have subscribed this with my own hand and sealed it with my inborn seal.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, the 31st of March, in the year 1598.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand (place of the seal).</p><p>[It is inscribed:]</p><p>Sent hereupon to Tycho Brahe: 3 casks of beer, each cask of 3 tuns;</p><p>6 sch[?] pounds of rye bread;</p><p>1 tun of butter;</p><p>30 sides of bacon, which weighed 1½ ship-pounds, 5½ lispounds;</p><p>6 imperial thalers to old master Peter, in ready money.</p><p>    Peter Zacherow.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / context.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, writing during his self-imposed exile from Denmark. Having left Hven after losing Frederik II's patronage, he resided 1597–1599 at Heinrich Rantzau's castle of <b>Wandsbek</b> (<i>Wandeßburgk</i>; now part of Hamburg), where in 1598 he printed the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i>. He still styles himself with his Danish titles — <i>erbgesessen zu Knudtstrup</i> (\"hereditary lord at Knudstrup,\" his ancestral Scanian seat) and <i>kön. maytt. zu Dennemarck ambtman auff Uraniburgk</i> (\"His Royal Majesty of Denmark's bailiff at Uraniborg\") — though by this date he no longer held Hven.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Andreas Meier</i> (<i>Andres Meiern / Andreas Meyern</i>) = <b>Andreas Meier</b>, the <i>fürstlicher mecklenburgischer Rentmeister</i> — the treasurer/receiver of revenues of the Duke of Mecklenburg. The MS survives among the Renterei-Akten (treasury files) of the Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, consistent with a financial bond addressed to that office.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> An <i>offener Brief</i> (open letter) functioning as a <b>promissory note / bond (<i>Verschreibung</i>)</b>: Tycho authorises a 100-thaler advance against 600 thalers owed to him, the advance to be set off against the principal. The financial arithmetic, stated once and not contradicted in the source: 600 thalers will fall due to Tycho in over a year; 100 are to be advanced now; those 100 are then deducted (<i>quitirt</i>, receipted/discharged) from the 600, leaving <b>only 500</b> (<i>nur funffhundert</i>) claimable when the term expires. The single restrictive <i>nur</i> (\"only\") is the sole limiting particle in the operative clause; there is no negation, and the conditional <i>wann die zeit verlauffen</i> is temporal (\"when the time has elapsed\"), not contrary-to-fact.</li><li><b><i>zustehenn / verfallenn</b></i> — <i>zustehen</i> \"be owing/due to\" and <i>verfallen</i> \"fall due, become payable\"; both here describe the 600-thaler sum maturing to Tycho. <i>quitirt</i> = <i>quittiert</i>, \"receipted, acquitted, discharged\" (the advance is to be booked against the principal, not repaid separately).</li><li><b><i>victualien</b></i> — Latin-derived <i>Viktualien</i>, \"victuals, provisions\"; the stated purpose of the advance is to buy foodstuffs and other supplies within Mecklenburg.</li><li><b>Subscription.</b> <i>Dessen zu Urkund … mit eigener Hand unterschrieben und mit meinem angebornen Petschaft besiegelt</i> — \"In witness whereof … subscribed with my own hand and sealed with my inborn [hereditary, i.e. family-armorial] seal\" (<i>angeborn Petschaft</i> = the seal of his birth-right, his family arms). The editorial note <i>eigenh. (l.s.)</i> records that the original bore Tycho's autograph signature and <i>(l.s.) = loco sigilli</i>, \"in place of the seal.\"</li><li><b>Orthography (chancery German, c. 1598).</b> Doubled final consonants and <i>-nn</i> (<i>thallernn, brieff, sachenn</i>), <i>v/u</i> for <i>u/v</i> (<i>vnnd, vber, auß</i>), <i>th-</i> for <i>t-</i> (<i>thaller</i>), and Latinate forms (<i>martij, anno, victualien</i>) are standard Frühneuhochdeutsch chancery usage; <i>itzo</i> = <i>jetzt</i> (\"now\"); <i>zuuor</i> = <i>zuvor</i> (\"in advance, beforehand\"); <i>itzo zuuor</i> together = \"now in advance.\"</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Datum Wandeßburgk denn 31t. martij anno 1598</i> = \"Given at <b>Wandsbek</b>, the 31st of March, in the year 1598\" (Old Style; Denmark and the Lutheran north German territories were still on the Julian calendar in 1598). The dateline date and the catalogued <i>label</i> date (\"1598. 31. marts. Wandsburg\") agree.</li><li><b>Language.</b> This segment is in Early New High German (with Low German admixture typical of the Wandsbek/Mecklenburg area), not Latin, unlike the rest of the batch. It is a separately inscribed itemized account (\"[inscriptum est:]\") attached to the Latin/German bond that the published tail preserves — the physical receipt for the 100-thaler advance the main letter discusses, showing exactly what was delivered against it.</li><li><b>Crux — \"6 sch ℔ rogken brott.\"</b> The abbreviation \"sch\" before the pound-sign \"℔\" is not confidently resolved: it may stand for <i>Schiffpfund</i> (ship-pound, matching the unit used two lines later for the bacon) or for a measure of rye itself rather than the bread's finished weight. Left untranslated/unexpanded and flagged rather than guessed.</li><li><b>Weights and money, expanded for clarity.</b> <i>Schippunt</i> = Schiffpfund (\"ship-pound,\" a large Baltic/North-German weight unit); <i>liß</i> = Liespfund (\"lispound,\" a smaller unit, roughly a sixteenth of a ship-pound in most regional systems); ℔ = the pound sign (libra). <i>Reichsthaler</i> = imperial thaler; \"an bahrem gelde\" = \"in ready/cash money.\"</li><li><b>The people named.</b> Peter Zacherow, who signs the account, appears elsewhere in the Mecklenburg treasury records (Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Renterei-Akten) handling a lump-sum cash payment to/from Tycho's affairs in this same period (April 1598) [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> vol. XIV] — he is evidently a Mecklenburg ducal financial agent or factor, not a member of Tycho's household. \"Old master Peter,\" paid 6 reichsthaler in cash, is a separate, unidentified third party (a craftsman or purveyor, to judge by the honorific \"meister\").</li><li><b>The recipient, Andreas Meier, and the larger transaction.</b> The register of Tycho Brahe's correspondence confirms that this very letter — 31 March 1598, Wandsbek, Tycho Brahe to Andreas Meier — is preserved at the Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin among the Renterei-Akten (treasury records), consistent with the main letter's subject: repayment terms on a debt (part of the 10,000-thaler loan Tycho had extended to the guardians of the young Dukes of Mecklenburg, attested elsewhere in the Opera Omnia for April 1598). This attached account is the concrete paper trail of one such advance being spent on provisions.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Fabricated numeral '9' removed from the itemised bacon-weight entry ('5½ liss 9 [pound-mark]' to '5½ liss [pound-mark]') and from the parallel English clause, per Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.136, which shows no numeral there. Latin heading 'BRIEFE' corrected to the singular 'BRIEFF' per Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.135.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German and the financial logic (100 advanced, set off against 600, leaving 500) are secure; no negation or conditional crux. The only routine softness is the standard chancery formula <i>angeborn Petschaft</i>, glossed above.</p><p>The overall sense — an itemized delivery-and-payment account attached to a financial letter — is secure, and the named individuals (Zacherow, Meier) are independently corroborated in the corpus as figures in Tycho's Mecklenburg financial dealings. Held at C because of the genuine, unresolved abbreviation \"sch ℔\" and because the historical weight-unit conversions (Schiffpfund/Liespfund) varied by region and period and have not been pinned to Mecklenburg-specific values here. Upgrade path: consult a Mecklenburg/Hanseatic weights-and-measures table for the exact period value of Schiffpfund and Liespfund, and check the Schwerin Renterei-Akten original for how \"sch ℔\" is elsewhere expanded in the same hand.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full German letter to Andreas Meier and its itemized delivery postscript against TBOO XIV pp.135-136 page images (re-checked the weight-figures line at higher resolution), verifying every name, place, date and quantity. Two TEXT_ERRORs found: a singular/plural title mismatch, and a fabricated '9' pounds in the bacon-weight figure that the English translation repeats as fact.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text's title reads plural 'BRIEFE' (letters) where Dreyer prints singular 'BRIEFF' (letter) -- a corrupted word. The English happens to use the correct singular 'A letter' regardless, so no mistranslation resulted. Dreyer prints: <i>BRIEFF AN DEN HERRENN RENTMEISTERN</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Source_text and the English translation both report an extra weight component of 'nine pounds' in this itemized delivery account that has no counterpart in Dreyer's print -- a fabricated numeral introduced in the transcription and carried straight into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>30 feitten fpeck, haben gewogen 1½ fchippunt 5½ liß ℔ (no numeral before the pound-mark)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against Dreyer page images, TBOO XIV pp.134-137 (full slice). Both defects are confirmed as described and fixed; the fabricated '9' before the pound-mark is absent from the printed page, and the heading is singular in Dreyer. No other divergence found on these pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Renterei-Akten.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Andreas Meier", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15990109001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Formally acknowledges requesting a 100-thaler advance from the Mecklenburg treasury, to be deducted from the 600 thalers owed to Tycho when the revenue falls due.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980331002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980400001", "d": "1598-04-00", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Georg Rollenhagen → Tycho Brahe — [Magdeburg], April 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with one German interpolation) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE<br>S.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et Doctissime TYCHO, habui iam per annos aliquot in aula Caesareae Maiestatis inter apparitores praecipuos affinem Lunaeburgensem CASPARUM LEHMANNUM scalptorem quidem gemmarum ex professione, sed interim omnium rerum admiratorem, inprimis Astrologiae. Ea de caussa cum me amicius colat, interdum quoque invisat ut ante septimanas non ita multas fecerat, incidit in sermones privatos obiter URSI Dithmarsi mentio, quem dicebat titulum iam habere Caesarei Mathematici, etiamsi et vestitu et vita ursus sit, libello quoque privatim aliquibus dato, te indignis exagitare modis. Rogavi itaque hominem, ut eum libellum mecum communicaret, quod fecit. Sed incuria cuiusdam mei amici, cui mandaram, ut praecipua inde capita enotata mihi occupatissimo describeret, in alienas manus incidit, perijtque nobis prorsus, donec pridie Paschatos, quo tuae ab Hamburgensi mihi reddebantur de consilio cum Caesare contrahendae notitiae, Praga reversus est nuncius meus, aliudque exemplar earundem calumniarum secum, ut scripto ab affine meo petieram, attulit. Quod si vidisses, fieri non posse arbitror, quin aliquam eius in literis ad me tuis mentionem fecisses. Sic per me exemplar primum accipis. Quaeso considera insigne exemplum Regulae Ciceronianae, eum, qui semel verecundiae fines transgressus sit, bene et gnaviter impudentem esse oportere, neque tamen propterea SOCRATEM asinum se calcantem ad iudices traxisse, neque ARISTOPHANI publice ipsi in os maledicenti maledicta regessisse. Legi pro hac mea aetate multa veterum et recentium Philosophorum certamina, etiam de rebus maxime inter ipsos controversis, nunquam vero scriptum insolentius, impudentius, quod haud dubie non parum te irritabit, ut sane contentiones dignitatis sunt acerrimae. Sed quid facies? Hoc scio profecto, quod si cum stercore certo, vinco vel vincor, semper ego maculor. Ut itaque generosi equi canum latratus non relatrando et mordendo, sed animose praetereundo ulciscuntur, ita prudentiss. quique Eruditorum maledicentissimos oblatratores silentio ulti sunt. Et licet hi suos invenient subinde approbatores (stultis enim stulta placent, et gaudent aulae monstris) tamen dicere ipsas adversitates lubet ... decus. Taedebat me quondam in Berlinensi aula dominari TORNEISSERUM hominem illiteratissimum, nos vero cives in literis educatos ut umbras contemni, deque consignatis aliquot problematibus, volebam praesente Illustrissmo Electore vel Germanice cum ipso conferre. Utebar ad eam rem LEVINO a Schullenburg nostro Decano. Legebat meas quaestiones Elector, meque patienter audiebat. Tandem dicebat intelligere se eius ineptias, neque quicquam ab eo medicamenti accipere. Was schadets aber das der Kayser ein Elephanten hatt? Itaque me ad caenam iussit adesse, dedit quoque veniam privatim cum TORNEISSERO postridie colloquendi, sed is mihi nunquam erat domi. Sic didici magnates nostros non tam quaerere veritatem quam opinionum pompam. Quod monstrum plures habet spectatores, quam genuina corporum forma. Sic fortassis in illa quoque aula, de qua scribis, hic SIMON MAGUS imprudentium animos dementavit, ut existimetur esse aliquid magni. Quare prudenter id periculi tentandum est. Alias Coloniensis ille vere generosi est animi et prorsus politici, qui quaerit imperij pacem, et illustre apud posteritatem nomen, nec dubites, quin facile eum habeas amicum; sed an utilem, ideo incertum est, quod longissimis itineribus et summis Imperij negotijs est occupatissimus. In aula ipsa qui iam sint politioribus literis imbuti, ignoro, olim CRATO et Dominus CURTIUS Musis nostris aditum liberrimum ad summam Maiestatem concedebant. Is, qui rerum summae praeest, amat mathemata, et Vulcani officinam, ut audij, inprimis si Magistrum nactus fuerit candidum. Sed haec Deo curae erunt, et albis puellis, ut ille inquit. Affinis tuus ERICUS LANGIUS ex Hassia ad nos redux iam diversatur Wandio in comitatu Mansfeldensi apud IOHAN. ERNESTUM ab Assaburg nobilem potentem et eruditum; distat is locus hinc decem milliaribus. Si volueris illi scribere, efficiam, ut literas tuas obtineat.</p><p>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Georg Rollenhagen to Tycho Brahe — greeting.</b></p><p>Most noble and most learned Tycho: for some years now I have had among the foremost attendants at the court of His Imperial Majesty a kinsman from Lüneburg, Caspar Lehmann, an engraver of gems by profession, but withal an admirer of all things, and above all of astrology. For that reason, since he cultivates me with some friendliness and from time to time also visits me — as he had done not so very many weeks ago — there fell into our private conversations, in passing, a mention of Ursus the Dithmarscher, who, he said, now holds the title of Imperial Mathematician — even though in dress and in life he is a bear — and who, in a little book given privately to certain persons, harasses you in unworthy ways. I therefore asked the man to share that little book with me, which he did. But through the carelessness of a certain friend of mine, whom I had charged to copy out for me — being most busy — the chief headings noted down from it, [the book] fell into other hands and was wholly lost to us, until on the eve of Easter — the day on which your [letters] from the Hamburg man were delivered to me, concerning the plan of contracting an acquaintance with the Emperor — my messenger returned from Prague, and brought with him another copy of the same calumnies, as I had asked in writing of my kinsman. And had you seen it, I judge it could not but be that you would have made some mention of it in your letters to me. So it is through me that you receive your first copy. Consider, I beg you, that signal instance of the Ciceronian Rule — that he who has once overstepped the bounds of modesty must needs be thoroughly and busily shameless — and yet that Socrates did not on that account drag before the judges the ass that trod on him, nor did he hurl back curses at Aristophanes, who was cursing him publicly to his face. For my age, I have read many contests of philosophers ancient and modern, even on matters most controverted among themselves, but never a writing more insolent, more shameless — which will doubtless provoke you not a little, since indeed contests of standing are the bitterest of all. But what will you do? This I know for certain: that if I contend with dung, win or lose, I am always befouled. And so, just as noble horses avenge the barking of dogs not by barking back and biting, but by passing them by with spirit, so all the most prudent of the Learned have avenged their most slanderous yappers with silence. And although these men will from time to time find their approvers (for foolish things please fools, and they delight in the monsters of the court), yet it is pleasing [to me] to speak the adversities themselves ... honour. It once wearied me that at the court of Berlin Thurneisser, a most unlettered man, lorded it, while we citizens bred up in letters were despised like shadows; and, regarding certain set problems, I wished to confer with him in the presence of the Most Illustrious Elector, even in German. For that business I made use of our Dean, Levinus von der Schulenburg. The Elector read my questions, and listened to me patiently. At last he said that he understood the man's follies, and took no medicine from him. \"But what harm is it that the Emperor keeps an elephant?\" And so he bade me attend at supper, and also gave leave to confer privately with Thurneisser the next day; but the man was never at home to me. Thus I learned that our great men seek not so much truth as the show of opinions. A monster has more spectators than the natural form of bodies. So perhaps in that court too, of which you write, this Simon Magus has bewitched the minds of the imprudent, so that he is reckoned to be something great. Wherefore that hazard must be ventured prudently. For the rest, that man of Cologne is truly of a noble spirit and altogether a statesman, who seeks the peace of the Empire and an illustrious name with posterity; and do not doubt that you will easily have him for a friend — but whether a useful one is uncertain, for the reason that he is most busy with very long journeys and with the highest affairs of the Empire. Who at the court itself are now imbued with the more polished letters, I do not know; once Crato and Master Curtius granted our Muses the freest access to the highest Majesty. He who presides over the sum of affairs loves the mathematical arts, and the workshop of Vulcan, as I hear — especially if he shall have got himself an honest Master. But these things will be the care of God, and of the white maidens, as the poet says. Your kinsman Erik Lange, returned to us from Hesse, now lodges at Wansleben in the county of Mansfeld, with Johann Ernst von der Asseburg, a powerful and learned nobleman; that place is ten miles distant from here. If you should wish to write to him, I will see to it that he gets your letter.</p><p>Georg Rollenhagen.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent.</b> <i>Georgius Rollenhagius</i> = <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector of the cathedral school (Altstädtisches Gymnasium) at <b>Magdeburg</b>, dramatist and poet (author of <i>Froschmeuseler</i>), a long-standing correspondent of Tycho's. The dating clause \"ten miles distant from here\" (<i>distat is locus hinc decem milliaribus</i>) and Rollenhagen's known residence fix the place of writing as <b>Magdeburg</b> (not stated in the text; supplied in brackets in the header).</li><li><b>Recipient / occasion.</b> Tycho, then at <b>Wandsbek</b> near Hamburg (in his post-Hven exile), was weighing an approach to <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>'s court at Prague. Rollenhagen writes to forward a copy of, and warn Tycho about, the slanderous pamphlet circulating in Prague.</li><li><b><i>URSI Dithmarsi</b></i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (Nicolai Reymers Bär, c. 1551–1600), the Dithmarschen-born mathematician, by 1591 Imperial Mathematician at Prague, Tycho's bitter rival in the priority dispute over the geo-heliocentric world-system. The <i>libellus</i> is his <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus … tractatus</i> (Prague, 1597), which attacked Tycho in scurrilous terms. <b>Wordplay flagged:</b> <i>etiamsi et vestitu et vita <b>ursus</b> sit</i> puns on Ursus's name (Latin <i>ursus</i> = \"bear\"); \"in dress and in life he is a bear\" keeps the pun.</li><li><b><i>CASPARUM LEHMANNUM</b></i> = <b>Caspar Lehmann</b> (c. 1563/65–1622), the celebrated gem- and glass-engraver, from Uelzen in the Lüneburg region, in Imperial service at Prague (credited with reviving wheel-engraving on glass); here Rollenhagen's <i>affinis</i> (kinsman by marriage) and informant.</li><li><b><i>pridie Paschatos</b></i> — \"the eve of Easter.\" In 1598 (O.S.) Easter Sunday fell on <b>16 April</b> (Julian), placing this passage's reference on <b>15 April</b>; the letter is catalogued simply \"1598, April.\" The Easter reference is the firmest internal date-anchor; no day is given in any subscription, so the header date is left at \"April 1598 [O.S.].\" Calendar style is Old Style (Rollenhagen, in Lutheran Magdeburg, used the Julian reckoning).</li><li><b><i>Regulae Ciceronianae</b></i> — \"the Ciceronian Rule\": the maxim that one who has once cast off shame must be wholly shameless (cf. Cicero, <i>Pro Quinctio</i> / the proverbial <i>qui semel verecundiae fines transierit</i>); kept as a named allusion.</li><li><b>Socrates and the ass; Aristophanes.</b> The two classical <i>exempla</i> of dignified non-retaliation — Socrates not suing the ass that kicked him, and not returning Aristophanes' public abuse (Aristophanes had lampooned Socrates in the <i>Clouds</i>). Kept as allusion.</li><li><b><i>si cum stercore certo, vinco vel vincor, semper ego maculor</b></i> — a proverbial saying (\"if I contend with dung, win or lose, I am always befouled\"), rendered as a sentence and kept for its proverbial bite; the antithesis <i>vinco vel vincor</i> (\"win or lose\") is preserved.</li><li><b><i>generosi equi … oblatratores silentio ulti sunt</b></i> — the \"noble horse / barking dogs\" topos for answering slander with silence; metaphor kept.</li><li><b>Lacuna / crux.</b> <i>tamen dicere ipsas adversitates lubet ... decus</i> — the source (Langebek copy, as printed in Dreyer) is <b>defective here</b>: an ellipsis stands between <i>lubet</i> and <i>decus</i>, and the clause does not parse as it stands. Rendered word-for-word as \"yet it is pleasing [to me] to speak the adversities themselves ... honour,\" with the gap preserved and not smoothed. The likely sense — that openly naming such troubles is itself creditable — is <b>not</b> asserted in the translation. <b>This is the governing crux for the grade.</b></li><li><b><i>TORNEISSERUM</b></i> = <b>Leonhard Thurneisser zum Thurn</b> (1531–1596), the Basel-born alchemist, astrologer and physician who dominated the Berlin court of <b>Elector Johann Georg of Brandenburg</b> until his fall (1584); Rollenhagen, as a Brandenburg-orbit schoolman, recalls a personal encounter. The spelling <i>Torneisserum</i> is kept; identification given.</li><li><b><i>LEVINO a Schullenburg nostro Decano</b></i> = <b>Levin von der Schulenburg</b>, named as \"our Dean\" (<i>Decanus</i>), the cathedral-chapter dignitary who acted as Rollenhagen's intermediary to the Elector.</li><li><b>German interpolation.</b> <i>Was schadets aber das der Kayser ein Elephanten hatt?</i> — \"But what harm is it that the Emperor keeps an elephant?\" The Elector's own words, quoted verbatim in Early New High German within the Latin (a code-switch, preserved per protocol §2); the elephant = the exotic court curiosity tolerated for show, i.e. Thurneisser. <i>schadets</i> = <i>schadet es</i>; <i>hatt</i> = <i>hat</i>.</li><li><b><i>SIMON MAGUS</b></i> — Simon the Magician (Acts 8), the type of the charlatan who passes for \"something great\" (<i>aliquid magni</i>, echoing Acts 8:9); applied here to the impostor said to sway the Prague court. Kept as allusion.</li><li><b><i>Coloniensis ille</b></i> — \"that man of Cologne\": almost certainly <b>Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne</b> (1554–1612), an Imperial Elector active in high <i>Reichspolitik</i>; characterised as a powerful but often-absent potential patron. Identification offered as probable, not certain (no name is given in the text); flagged.</li><li><b><i>CRATO</b></i> = <b>Johannes Crato von Krafftheim</b> (1519–1585), personal physician to three emperors (Ferdinand I, Maximilian II, Rudolf II) and a noted humanist patron — here recalled (with <i>olim</i>, \"once\") as a past channel of learned access to the Emperor. <b><i>Dominus CURTIUS</b></i> = <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacobus Curtius, d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor and a mathematically-cultivated councillor; likewise <i>olim</i>, now deceased.</li><li><b><i>Is, qui rerum summae praeest</b></i> — \"He who presides over the sum of affairs,\" a periphrasis for <b>Rudolf II</b>; <i>amat mathemata, et Vulcani officinam</i> = \"loves the mathematical arts, and the workshop of Vulcan\" — <i>Vulcani officina</i> = the alchemical/metallurgical laboratory, glossed as \"the workshop of Vulcan\" (Rudolf's well-known patronage of alchemy). <i>Magistrum … candidum</i> = \"an honest Master\" (i.e. a sound, sincere adept, as opposed to a charlatan).</li><li><b><i>albis puellis, ut ille inquit</b></i> — \"of the white maidens, as the poet says\": an allusion to Horace (cf. <i>Odes</i> — the <i>Diespiter</i> / the favouring deities), proverbial for \"the kindly powers\"; <i>ut ille inquit</i> (\"as the poet/that author says\") is the author's own hedge and is kept.</li><li><b><i>ERICUS LANGIUS</b></i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (1553–1613), the Danish nobleman, alchemist, and Tycho's kinsman (betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie); here reported as lodging at <b>Wanzleben</b> (<i>Wandio</i>) in the county of <b>Mansfeld</b> with <b>Johann Ernst von der Asseburg</b> (<i>Iohan. Ernestum ab Assaburg</i>). <i>decem milliaribus</i> = \"ten miles\" (German <i>Meilen</i>, each far longer than the English mile); rendered \"ten miles\" with this caution.</li><li><b><i>apparitores praecipuos</b></i> — \"the foremost attendants\" (court officers/ushers); <b><i>scalptorem gemmarum</b></i> — \"engraver of gems,\" kept literal. <b><i>notitiae … cum Caesare contrahendae</b></i> — \"of contracting an acquaintance with the Emperor,\" the diplomatic overture Tycho was preparing.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected an inverted subject/object in the Socrates-Aristophanes sentence: the Latin (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.51, dative ARISTOPHANI + agreeing 'maledicenti') has Socrates declining to hurl curses back at Aristophanes, who was cursing him -- not the reverse, and not an invented third party. Corrected the English place-name identification of 'Wandio' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.52) from Wanzleben (Magdeburg Börde, not in Mansfeld) to Wansleben am See (Mansfelder Land, consistent with the letter's own 'in comitatu Mansfeldensi').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the body is rendered securely and the named persons are well-anchored, but the source carries a genuine <b>textual lacuna</b> at <i>tamen dicere ipsas adversitates lubet ... decus</i> (Langebek copy / Dreyer), which is reproduced unsmoothed rather than reconstructed; the identification of <i>Coloniensis ille</i> as Ernst of Cologne is probable but not certain. Both are flagged above.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 65: the <i>lubet... decus</i> lacuna is unfilled in Dreyer. <i>Coloniensis ille</i> = <b>Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne</b>, strongly corroborated by the Ernst-of-Cologne letter at <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII 71–72 (15/25 Jun 1598) — a documented 1598 correspondent — though not named in this letter's own text. [C] stands (lacuna).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (source_text and published_english) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 49-52, read as page images (pp.49-52 cover the complete Rollenhagen letter; p.53 is the following Maestlin letter and not needed). Dreyer's printed Latin matches our source_text verbatim throughout (no TEXT_ERROR). Both previously-flagged defects are confirmed: the Socrates/Aristophanes subject inversion is a real mistranslation (Aristophanes is grammatically the dative object of Socrates' restraint, not an independent subject), and 'Wanzleben' for 'Wandio' conflicts with the letter's own 'in comitatu Mansfeldensi' (Wansleben am See, not Wanzleben, lies in the historic Mansfeld district). No other defects found on full collation of names, numbers, and sense.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). ARISTOPHANI is dative (object of Socrates' restraint) and 'maledicenti' agrees with it (Aristophanes is the one cursing); the subject of 'regessisse' continues to be Socrates from the prior clause. Correct sense: 'nor did [Socrates] hurl back curses at Aristophanes, who was cursing him publicly to his face.' The English inverts subject and object, making Aristophanes the restrained party and inventing an unnamed 'man cursing him' where the Latin names only Socrates and Aristophanes. At: “nor did Aristophanes hurl back curses at the man cursing him publicly to his face”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Dreyer's 'Wandio' is confirmed identical to source_text, so the Latin itself is not in question. But 'Wandio' is rendered 'Wanzleben' in the English, and Wanzleben (in the Magdeburg Börde, ~15km SW of Magdeburg) is not in the county of Mansfeld -- only Wansleben (am See), in the Mansfelder Land proper, fits the letter's own qualifier 'in comitatu Mansfeldensi'. The English place-name identification contradicts the sentence it translates. At: “now lodges at Wanzleben in the county of Mansfeld”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both MISTRANSLATION defects directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.51-52, Rollenhagen to Tycho): the Latin in both places is confirmed identical to source_text, so both defects are pure translation errors, not text corruptions. The Aristophanes/Socrates correction rests on straightforward Latin case-and-agreement analysis (ARISTOPHANI dative, maledicenti agreeing with it) directly visible on the page. The Wanzleben-to-Wansleben correction is a place-name identification: the page itself shows only the abbreviated 'Wandio', so the Mansfeld-county fit for Wansleben am See (rather than Wanzleben near Magdeburg) rests on external historical geography rather than the page image alone. Latin left untouched in both cases per instructions.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15980618001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000405001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Describes how a kinsman at the Imperial court shared Ursus's attacking pamphlet, which was briefly lost through a friend's carelessness before being recovered.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980400001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980401001", "d": "1598-04-01", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Kepler — Wandsbek, 1 April 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(ed. Dreyer, vol. VIII) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO Μ. IOHANNI KEPLERO ILLUSTRIUM STYRIAe PROVINCIALIUM IN GYMNASIO GRAeCENSI MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO. GRETZ IN STIRIEN.</p><p>ERUDITISSIME et praestantissime Vir, Literae tuae e Styria Id. Dec. superioris anni ad me datae circa initia Martij per Tabellarium Helmstadio venientem mihi redditae sunt, quae praeter doctrinam et humanitatem singularem erga me, licet tibi de facie ignotum et longe dissitum, testantur benevolentiam, quo etiam nomine tibi gratias habeo. Librum tuum, quem Prodromum vocas Dissertationum Cosmographicarum, iam ante videram, et quantum aliae occupationes ferebant, perlegeram. Placet is sane non mediocriter, et ingenij tui acumen sagaxque studium non obscure hinc elucent, ut de stylo terso ac rotundo nihil dicam. Ingeniosa certe et succincta est speculatio, Planetarum distantias et circuitus symmetrijs regularium corporum, uti facis, adstringere, et plurima in his satis consentire videntur, non obstante quod proportiones Coperniceae per omnia in minimis quibusque haud ubique adstipulentur, cum eae ab ipsis etiam apparentijs nonnihil deflectant. Quare laudo hanc tuam in excogitandis perquirendisque talibus diligentiam. An vero per omnia ijs acquiescendum sit, non ita facile dixerim. Si veriores Eccentricitatum utrarumque in singulis Planetis commensurationes, quales plurimis annis conquisitas in promptu habeo, adhibeantur, poterint accuratiorem in his trutinam exhibere. Verum mihi in complendis et edendis meis operibus Astronomicis, quae in Dania quodammodo imperfecta reliqueram, occupatissimo non suppetit nunc otium, ea accuratiore lima inter se conferendi. Fiet hoc forte alias. Id autem te scire velim, Eccentricitates ipsas utriusque illius, ut sic nunc loquar, Eccentrici, quas COPERNICUS paulo aliter excusavit, non habere eas adinvicem proportiones, ut una sit alterius tertia pars; veluti ex PTOLEMAeO deduxit. Neuter vero eorum demonstratum reliquit. Sed haec aliam inter se habent rationem et in singulis variant. Neque enim tres acronychiae observationes, utut inter se in Eccentrico (quod praestat) remotae, sufficiunt ad trium superiorum Planetarum Apogaea (quae etiam non paucis gradibus tam ALPHONSI quam COPERNICI placita coelitus eludunt) et Eccentricitates ambas ad amussim extricandas; sed per totum orbis circuitum in plurimis locis haec experiunda veniunt, veluti a nobis quoque factum est, adeo ut etiam tardigradi illius Saturni circuitum expectaverimus. Nam eundem Soli in ♎ oppositum ante annos 29. Augustae Vindelicorum diligenter observavi, quemadmodum et nunc; uti etiam in intermedijs Acronychijs dispositionibus, quotquot habere licuit, in toto Boreali semicirculo nullis omnino praetermissis, quemadmodum etiam in reliquis duobus Planetis, quorum circuitus minori clauditur tempore. Habeo enim in promptu 35 annorum Observationes coelestes, inde ab adolescentia factas, quarum 25 posteriores magna accuratione pollent: quas omnes non vulgaris thesauri loco conservo, et hinc Astronomiae restaurationem molior. Ut vero de Marte aliquid exempli loco addam, cum is maximam ingerat varietatem, atque inter Coelum et Tabulas discrepantiam caeteris evidentiorem, scias tantum abesse, ut eius Eccentricitas sit minor reddita (prout voluit COPERNICUS, quo suae imaginationi circa quietem Solis fidem faceret) ut potius nunc aliquanto maior sit, quam ea, quae a PTOLEMAeO prodita est, licet discrimine non magni momenti, Apogaeo eius quinque gradibus COPERNICI terminos anticipante. Quin et orbis annuus iuxta COPERNICUM aut Epicyclus secundum PTOLEMAeUM non videtur eiusdem semper magnitudinis, quoad ipsum Eccentricum collatione facta; sed alterationem introducit in omnibus tribus superioribus sensibilem, adeo ut angulus differentiae in ♂ ad Gradum unum Minuta 45. excrescat. Quomodo haec cum tua speculatione concilianda sint, tute videris. Veneris Eccentricitas multo minor est, quam statuit vel PTOLEMAeUS vel COPERNICUS, et Apogaeum eius non est in orbe sidereo fixum, uti hic voluit, sed initia ♋ nunc attigit; nec tamen solari (quod in medio sexti gradus haeret) uti volunt Alphonsini, plane unitum. Haec et similia, quorum plura suo tempore habebis, tuae alias industriae excogitationi applicata eam, an undequaque rite constet, probabunt. Ego omnem orbium realitatem, quomodocunque tandem assumantur, coelo eliminandam censeo. Quod me omnes Cometae, inde a nova stella conspecti, qui nullius orbis leges totaliter secuti sunt, quidam vero contrarium quid prae se ferebant, indubitanter edocuerunt (ut de refractionibus frustra multiplicatis ex orbium solidorum copia nihil superaddam). Nec poterit Terra annuo cieri motu, ut de librationibus otiosis (neque enim talis est aequinoctiorum praecessio, ut opinabatur COPERNICUS) taceam: utut diurnus plausibiliter ob minorem gyrum illi attribui queat, modo tam gravi et opaco corpori is competeret, quieti potius, quam motui accommodo. Quare ut tollantur Epicycli, vix alia commodiore ratione Hypotheses ordinari queunt, quam ut circa Solem mobilem sint centra circuituum quinque Planetarum quiescente Terra, et circa hanc revolutis luminaribus et tota octava (uti vocant) Sphaera, eam etiam in meditullio respiciente. Cuius abscessus iuxta COPERNICUM in immensum pene abit, eiusque positionem vel hoc solo absurdo infringit. Nec etiam tua cogitata hîc ullam cum caeteris Symmetriam (prout simul fieri debuit) admittunt. Oportet autem in re bene proportionata nihil dari inproportionatum, alias tota Symmetria confunditur, et suspecta evadit: praesertim in hoc loco, ubi tam magnum atque evidens ingeritur obstaculum. Haec et alia, quae singula commemorare nunc non vacat, me circa tuam inventionem alias solertissimam, optime KEPLERE, dubium reddunt. Interim non possum non conatus tuos, tam eximios et raros magnifacere. Hortorque ut ulterius his animum intendas, desque operam, ut simile quid nostris hypothesibus, quas ubi non incompertas esse liber ille ostendit, applicare possis, cum pene omnia ijs pariter conveniant. Neque ob id opus est, ut orbium statuatur compages solida. Sufficit circuitus et remotiones siderum inter se talem concinnitatem atque harmoniam observare. Nec praetereunda est octava Sphaera, quin in hoc choro proportionabiliter locum aliquem obtineat. Nullum enim est dubium in universo omnia certa harmonia et proportione ad invicem divinitus relata et ordinata esse, ita ut tam numeris quam figuris succincte comprehendantur, veluti a Pythagoreis et Platonicis iamdudum aliquatenus praevisum est. His itaque ulterius ingenij nervos applica, et si omnimodam consonantiam, quae nulla ex parte claudicet, et in qua nihil etiam desideretur, adinveneris, eris mihi magnus Apollo. Quantum ego tuis hac in re arduis conatibus subvenire potero, me neutiquam habebis difficilem, praesertim si me nunc in Germania degentem et e patria huc cum tota familia, ne tantus ille periret Thesaurus Astronomicus, tot annis, laboribus et sumptibus comparatus, profectum aliquando inviseris et mecum coram de eiuscemodi sublimibus rebus iucunde et grate contuleris. Vale. Dabantur Wandesburgi in arce Ranzoviana semisse tantummodo milliaris Hamburgo remota, quam nunc inhabito, et translatis huc e Dania machinis meis Astronomicis totaque suppellectile libraria rebus coelestibus non minus quam antea invigilo, et Divina gratia otio fruor Philosophico.</p><p>D. 1. April. stylo veteri Anno 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 46–48; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. NESCIO quo casu acciderit, ut idem Tabellarius, qui mihi tuas Helmstadio attulit, famosum quoque et nefarium scriptum cuiusdam feri Ursi potius quam veri hominis, inde mihi simul tuis alligatum adportârit. Huius cum impudentes calumnias et infanda mendacia atque convicia, quibus supra modum et citra omnem verecundiam scatet, obiter percurrerem, reperi etiam ibi tuum quoddam Epistolium, quo se ornare et sua flagitia tegere nititur. Quod cum perlegissem, miratus sum te eum tanti facere. Sed errorem excusavi, quod non nisi auditu et scripto isto, quod Fundamentum vocat Astronomicum, hoc faceres. In quo fere nihil habet quod suum est. Omnia potius aliis surrepta et praerepta, prout facere consuevit. Et plagium quod ego ipsi imputo in meis Hypothesibus, non saltem error crassus, quem in delineatione Martij orbis commisit, sed et multa alia, quae suo tempore pandam, evincunt. Estque etiamnum apud me fasciculus quidam eorum, quae quando Nobili cuidam Dano meo affini inserviens anno 84 cum eodem Uraniburgi adesset, clam descripsit. In quibus non dissimilia egit. Ego sane hanc Hypothesium rationem iam longe antea excogitatam, nec eam libro de Cometa Anni 77 inseruissem, nisi tales Plagiarios, quibus haec apud me innotuerat, veritus fuissem. Quare et hoc miror, quod eius voces Hypothesin, cum et in libro tuo et literis hisce eam mihi (nec immerito) attribuas. Interim etiam magna eius praedicas Elogia, utinam non Hyperbolen! In titulo vocas eum Mathematicum nobilissimum (qualis sit Mathematicus, non dixerim, Nobilitas eius, si genus spectes, est ex rusticis Dithmarsis, qui eum etiam fastidiunt). Ais deinde Illustrissimam eius gloriam ipsum tibi notum fecisse, qua Mathematicos huius aevi tantum praecedat unus, quantum Phoebeius orbis minuta sidera. Plura addidisses, si tempus ferret. At quam competenter etiam ulteriora excusas, quod Mathematicorum non sit garrulitas. Nescio an tale quid tunc apud te tacite senseris, genio sic suggerente. Praeterea quod omnes Docti magni ipsum faciant, eum praeceptorem appellas, hypotheses meas illi tribuis. Et tandem concludis, ut sideribus (quorum permodicam habet notitiam) et scientiae, quam sibi hinc inde (si qua est) corradit et suffuratur, tanquam decus Germaniae valeat. Talia de ignoto inconsiderate scripsisse videris: quae ego quidem nullatenus moror. Imo mallem vera esse, vel aliquem eiuscemodi dari, cui tanta in Astronomicis et Mathematicis competerent Elogia. Ego non ultimus essem, qui eius celebraret gloriam. Non tamen supra PTOLEMAeUM aut COPERNICUM, aliosque eximios viros, qualis in Germania MAeSTHLINUS nunc est, efferrem, nisi dignum fore satis perspectum haberem. Sed sint haec qualiacunque velint, id equidem non arridet, quod tua sic in libro isto contra me maledico abutatur Epistola, ut hac quasi se purgare, atque quantus sit, quamque multum ipsi tribuendum (si Dijs placet) probare velit, se ipsum ita nimium extollendo et sibimet placendo atque aliis tacite detrahendo. At quoniam in iuventute ea per ignorantiam scripsisti, nec forte existimâras eum unquam literas illas publicaturum, nedum ut iis in aliorum despectum et contumeliam unquam abusurus foret, idcirco est, cur haec placidius feram. Cuperem nihilominus ut primo quoque tempore apud me declaratum relinqueres, an tibi gratum sit quod sic fecerit, et quid de scripto isto virulento sentias, tum quoque alii Eruditi, ad quorum manus illud pervenit. Neque enim dubito, isthuc perlatum, siquidem Pragae excusum sit, suppresso tamen Typographi nomine, uti fit in famosis libellis. Plura de his non addo. Nec enim digna sunt, ut multis revocentur. Tuum est haec ipsa obiter subiuncta candide interpretari et schedam hanc Vulcano tradere. Iamque iterum et saepius Vale. Datum ut supra in literis. T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and excellent man, Master Johannes Kepler, distinguished mathematician of the Illustrious Estates of Styria in the Gymnasium of Graz, his very dearly beloved friend. Graz in Styria.</b></p><p>Most learned and excellent Sir, your letter, sent to me from Styria on the Ides of December [13 December] of last year, was delivered to me about the beginning of March by a courier coming from Helmstedt; and besides your learning and singular courtesy towards me — though I am unknown to you by face and far distant — it bears witness to your goodwill, on which account too I give you thanks. Your book, which you call the <i>Forerunner of Cosmographical Dissertations</i> [<i>Prodromus Dissertationum Cosmographicarum</i>], I had already seen before, and, so far as my other occupations allowed, had read through. It pleases me, indeed, not moderately, and the keenness of your wit and your sagacious zeal shine forth from it not obscurely — to say nothing of its polished and well-rounded style. It is certainly an ingenious and compact speculation to bind the distances and circuits of the planets to the symmetries of the regular solids, as you do; and very many things in this seem to agree well enough, notwithstanding that the Copernican proportions do not everywhere lend their support in each and every smallest detail, since they themselves depart somewhat even from the appearances. For which reason I praise this diligence of yours in devising and investigating such matters. But whether one ought to rest content in them in every respect, I would not so readily say. If the truer measurements of both eccentricities in each of the planets — such as I have ready to hand, gathered over very many years — be applied, they will be able to display a more accurate balance in these things. But, being most fully occupied in completing and publishing my astronomical works, which I had left in some manner unfinished in Denmark, I have now no leisure to spare for comparing these things with one another by that more accurate file. This will perhaps be done at another time. This, however, I would have you know: that the eccentricities of each of those (so let me now put it) eccentrics — which Copernicus accounted for somewhat otherwise — do not have those proportions to one another such that the one is a third part of the other, as he deduced from Ptolemy. Yet neither of the two left it demonstrated. But these things have another ratio among themselves and vary in each [planet]. For three acronychal observations, however far apart from one another in the eccentric (which is preferable), do not suffice for unravelling exactly the apogees of the three superior planets (which also, by no few degrees, elude in the heavens the doctrines of both Alfonso and Copernicus) and both eccentricities; but throughout the whole circuit of the orb, in very many places, these things must be tried by experience, as has been done by us too, so much so that we waited even for the circuit of that slow-moving Saturn. For I diligently observed that same planet in opposition to the Sun in ♎ Libra 29 years ago at Augsburg, just as now too; as also in the intermediate acronychal dispositions, as many as it was permitted to have, in the whole northern semicircle, with absolutely none passed over — just as in the remaining two planets, whose circuit is closed in a lesser time. For I have ready to hand the celestial observations of 35 years, made ever since my youth, of which the later 25 are strong in great accuracy: all of which I keep in the place of no common treasure, and from these I am labouring at the restoration of astronomy. But, to add something about Mars by way of example, since it brings on the greatest variety, and a discrepancy between the heaven and the tables more evident than the rest — you should know that so far is its eccentricity from being rendered smaller (as Copernicus wished, in order to give credit to his imagining concerning the rest of the Sun) that it is rather now somewhat greater than that which was reported by Ptolemy, though by a difference of no great moment, its apogee anticipating the bounds of Copernicus by five degrees. Indeed, the annual orb according to Copernicus, or the epicycle according to Ptolemy, does not appear to be always of the same magnitude, when a comparison is made with the eccentric itself; but it introduces in all three superior planets an alteration that is perceptible, so much so that the angle of difference in ♂ Mars grows to one degree and 45 minutes. How these things are to be reconciled with your speculation, you yourself will see. The eccentricity of Venus is much smaller than either Ptolemy or Copernicus laid down, and its apogee is not fixed in the starry orb, as he wished, but has now reached the beginning of ♋ Cancer; nor yet is it plainly united to the solar [apogee] (which sticks in the middle of the sixth degree), as the Alfonsines would have it. These and similar things, of which you shall have more in their own time, applied to the devising of your otherwise [admirable] industry, will prove whether it holds good in every respect. I, for my part, judge that the whole reality of the orbs, however in the end they be assumed, is to be eliminated from the heaven. Of which all the comets seen since the new star — which followed totally the laws of no orb, while some indeed bore something contrary — have taught me beyond doubt (to add nothing further about the refractions vainly multiplied out of the abundance of solid orbs). Nor will the Earth be able to be moved by an annual motion — to say nothing of idle librations (for the precession of the equinoxes is not such a thing as Copernicus supposed): although the diurnal [motion] might plausibly be attributed to it on account of its lesser turning, provided it suited so heavy and opaque a body, which is fitter for rest than for motion. Wherefore, in order that the epicycles may be removed, the hypotheses can scarcely be arranged by any more convenient method than that the centres of the circuits of the five planets be about the moveable Sun, the Earth being at rest, and that about this [Earth] the luminaries and the whole eighth (as they call it) sphere be revolved, the latter also regarding it [the Earth] in its very midst. Whose remoteness, according to Copernicus, goes off almost into the immeasurable, and he breaks down his own position by this absurdity alone. Nor, moreover, do your conceptions here admit any symmetry with the rest (as ought to have been the case at the same time). But it is fitting that in a thing well proportioned nothing disproportionate be granted, otherwise the whole symmetry is confounded and turns out suspect: especially in this place, where so great and evident an obstacle is brought in. These and other things, which it is not now free to recount one by one, render me doubtful, most excellent Kepler, concerning your invention, otherwise most skilful. Meanwhile I cannot but make much of your efforts, so distinguished and rare. And I urge you to bend your mind further to these things, and to take pains that you may apply something similar to our hypotheses — which, as that book shows them not to be unknown [to you], since almost everything agrees equally with them. Nor on that account is there need for a solid framework of orbs to be laid down. It suffices to observe such a fitness and harmony of the circuits and distances of the stars among themselves. Nor is the eighth sphere to be passed over without its obtaining some place proportionably in this choir. For there is no doubt that in the universe all things are divinely related and ordered to one another by a certain harmony and proportion, in such a way that they are concisely comprehended as much by numbers as by figures, as was already in some measure foreseen long ago by the Pythagoreans and Platonists. To these things, therefore, apply the sinews of your wit yet further; and if you should discover a consonance of every kind, which limps in no part, and in which nothing whatever is wanting, you shall be to me a great Apollo. As far as I shall be able to assist your arduous efforts in this matter, you will by no means find me difficult — especially if you should one day visit me, now dwelling in Germany, having set out hither from my homeland with my whole household, lest that great Astronomical Treasure, gathered over so many years with such labours and expense, should perish; and if you should confer with me face to face about sublime matters of this kind, pleasantly and gratefully. Farewell. Given at Wandsbek in the Rantzau castle, only half a mile distant from Hamburg, which I now inhabit; and, my astronomical instruments and the whole furniture of my library having been brought hither from Denmark, I keep watch over celestial matters no less than before, and by divine grace enjoy philosophical leisure.</p><p>The 1st of April, Old Style, the year 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.<br>With his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. I know not by what chance it came about that the same courier who brought me your letters from Helmstedt carried hither at the same time, tied up with yours, a notorious and wicked writing of a certain man — a \"wild Bear\" [Ursus] rather than a true man. As I ran cursorily through his shameless calumnies and unspeakable lies and abuse, with which beyond measure and past all shame it teems, I found there too a certain little letter of yours, with which he tries to adorn himself and to cloak his villainies. When I had read it through, I marvelled that you make so much of him. But I excused the error, [judging] that you did this only on hearsay and on that writing of his which he calls Fundamentum Astronomicum. In it he has hardly anything that is his own; all rather filched and snatched from others, as he is wont to do. And the plagiarism which I impute to him in [the matter of] my Hypotheses — not only the gross error which he committed in the delineation of Mars's orb, but also many other things, which I shall disclose in their own time — prove [it]. And there is still with me a certain bundle of the things which, while he was in the service of a certain Danish nobleman, my kinsman, and was present with him at Uraniborg in the year [15]84, he secretly copied out; in which he acted not unlike [this]. I, in truth, had devised this scheme of the Hypotheses long before, and would have inserted it in the book on the Comet of [15]77, had I not feared such Plagiarists, to whom these things had become known with me. Wherefore I marvel at this too, that you should call it a Hypothesis of his, since both in your book and in this letter you attribute it (and not undeservedly) to me. Meanwhile you also proclaim great Eulogies of him — would they were not Hyperbole! In the title you call him a most noble Mathematician (what kind of Mathematician he is, I would not say; his Nobility, if you look to his lineage, is from the Dithmarsh peasants, who even disdain him). You say next that his most illustrious glory made him known to you, by which he alone so far precedes the Mathematicians of this age as the Phoebean orb [the Sun] the tiny stars. You would have added more, had time allowed. But how fittingly you also excuse the rest, [saying] that garrulity belongs not to Mathematicians! I know not whether you then felt some such thing tacitly within you, your genius so suggesting. Besides, [saying] that all the great Learned make much of him, you call him a teacher, and ascribe my hypotheses to him. And at last you conclude that he may avail, as it were the glory of Germany, for the stars (of which he has very slight knowledge) and for the science which he scrapes together and pilfers for himself here and there (if there be any). Such things you seem to have written inconsiderately about one unknown [to you]: which I, for my part, in no way regard. Nay, I would rather they were true, or that there were someone of such a kind to whom so great Eulogies in Astronomy and Mathematics were due. I should not be the last to celebrate his glory. Yet I would not exalt him above Ptolemy or Copernicus, and the other excellent men — such as Maestlin now is in Germany — unless I held it sufficiently clear that he would be worthy. But be these things whatever they will, this indeed does not please me: that your Letter is so abused in that book of his against me, the slanderer, that by it he wishes, as it were, to purge himself and to prove how great he is, and how much (so please the gods) is to be ascribed to him — by thus too much extolling himself and pleasing himself and tacitly disparaging others. But since you wrote those things in your youth, through ignorance, nor perhaps had supposed that he would ever publish those letters, much less that he would ever abuse them to the contempt and contumely of others, that is why I bear these things more calmly. Nonetheless I should wish that, at the first opportunity, you would leave it declared to me whether it is pleasing to you that he has done so, and what you think of that virulent writing — and likewise the other Learned into whose hands it has come. For I doubt not that it has been carried thither, since it was printed at Prague, the Printer's name being suppressed, however, as is done in libellous tracts. I add no more about these things; for they are not worth being recalled at length. It is for you to interpret candidly these very things subjoined in passing, and to hand this sheet over to Vulcan [the fire]. And now again and often, Farewell. Given as above in the letter. T. B.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (1546–1601), then in self-imposed exile from Denmark; <b>Johannes Kepler</b> (1571–1630), at this date <i>Landschaftsmathematiker</i> (mathematician to the Estates) and teacher at the Protestant <i>Stiftsschule</i> / Gymnasium at Graz in Styria. This is Tycho's celebrated first substantive reply to the young Kepler — his answer to the <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i> — and the opening of the correspondence that would bring Kepler to Prague in 1600.</li><li><b><i>Μ.</b></i> before <i>Iohanni</i> — the abbreviation <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic \"Master\"), here written with a Greek capital Mu (Μ) in the source; rendered \"Master\" (protocol §5.5). Reproduced as printed in the Source block.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Wandesburgum, arx Ranzoviana</i> = <b>Wandsbek</b> (now in Hamburg), the castle of the Rantzau (Rantzow) family — Tycho's host Heinrich Rantzau's seat — where Tycho resided 1597–98 between leaving Hven and removing to Prague. <i>semisse … milliaris</i> = \"half a [German] mile\" from Hamburg.</li><li><b>Place of address.</b> <i>Gretz in Stirien</i> = <b>Graz in Styria</b> (German address-line on the cover), repeating the Latin <i>Gymnasium Graecense … Styriae</i>.</li><li><b><i>Prodromum … Dissertationum Cosmographicarum</b></i> — the full title of Kepler's 1596 book, the <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i> (<i>Prodromus Dissertationum Cosmographicarum, continens Mysterium Cosmographicum</i>); its thesis nests the planetary orbs within the five Platonic regular solids — Tycho's <i>symmetriae regularium corporum</i>.</li><li><b>Dating of the incoming letter.</b> <i>Literae tuae … Id. Dec. superioris anni</i> = Kepler's letter of <b>13 December [1597]</b> (Ides of December), delivered \"about the beginning of March\" via a courier from <b>Helmstedt</b> (<i>Helmstadium</i>); Roman-calendar idiom kept and glossed per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Astronomy — technical.</b> <i>acronychiae observationes / Acronychae dispositiones</i> = <b>acronychal observations</b> (observations of a planet at opposition, rising at sunset); <i>Eccentricitates utriusque … Eccentrici</i> = the two <b>eccentricities</b> of the (bisected) eccentric — Tycho denies the Ptolemaic-Copernican simple bisection (one eccentricity = a third of the other / equant-bisection); <i>Apogaea trium superiorum Planetarum</i> = the <b>apogees</b> of the three superior planets (Saturn, Jupiter, Mars); <i>orbis annuus … aut Epicyclus</i> = the Copernican <b>annual orb</b> vs. the Ptolemaic <b>epicycle</b>; <i>octava Sphaera</i> = the <b>eighth sphere</b> (of the fixed stars); <i>aequinoctiorum praecessio</i> = the <b>precession of the equinoxes</b>. Glossary renderings (§5.1) kept consistent.</li><li><b>The Mars datum.</b> <i>angulus differentiae in ♂ ad Gradum unum Minuta 45. excrescat</i> — the discrepancy reaches <b>1° 45′</b>; <i>Apogaeo eius quinque gradibus COPERNICI terminos anticipante</i> — Mars's apogee runs 5° ahead of Copernicus's value. This is the very Mars-anomaly that Tycho here flags and would later set Kepler to work on. Tycho's claim that Mars's eccentricity is <i>greater</i> than Ptolemy's (against Copernicus, who shrank it <i>quo suae imaginationi circa quietem Solis fidem faceret</i>, \"to give credit to his imagining concerning the rest of the Sun\") is reproduced as his own argued position, not endorsed.</li><li><b>The Tychonic system.</b> <i>circa Solem mobilem … centra circuituum quinque Planetarum quiescente Terra</i> — Tycho's geo-heliocentric <b>hypotheses</b>: the five planets circle the moveable Sun, while Sun, Moon and the eighth sphere circle the resting Earth. <i>omnem orbium realitatem … coelo eliminandam</i> — his abolition of the <b>solid orbs</b>, argued from the cometary evidence \"since the new star\" (the 1572 <i>nova</i> and the comet of 1577 onward).</li><li><b>Symbols.</b> ♎ = <b>Libra</b>, ♋ = <b>Cancer</b>, ♂ = <b>Mars</b>; glyphs kept in the Source block and named in English per protocol §5.3.</li><li><b><i>eris mihi magnus Apollo</b></i> — \"you shall be to me a great Apollo,\" a proverbial classical compliment (cf. Virgil, <i>Ecl.</i> 3.104) = \"you will be my oracle / supreme authority\"; kept as the figure it is.</li><li><b><i>Thesaurus Astronomicus</b></i> — \"the Astronomical Treasure,\" Tycho's term (cf. <i>non vulgaris thesauri loco conservo</i>) for his 35-year observational archive — the asset he uprooted his household to preserve. The phrase resonates with the project name of this corpus and is rendered literally.</li><li><b><i>restitutio / Astronomiae restaurationem</b></i> — <i>hinc Astronomiae restaurationem molior</i>, \"from these I am labouring at the <b>restoration of astronomy</b>,\" Tycho's <i>astronomia instaurata</i> programme (protocol §5.1).</li><li><b>Hedges preserved.</b> Tycho's epistemic qualifiers are kept un-sharpened: <i>non ita facile dixerim</i> (\"I would not so readily say\"); <i>forte alias</i> (\"perhaps at another time\"); <i>ut sic nunc loquar</i> (\"so let me now put it\"); <i>non magni momenti</i> (\"of no great moment\"); <i>plausibiliter … modo … competeret</i> (the conditional concession on the diurnal motion). The praise–doubt structure (<i>laudo … An vero … dubium reddunt … non possum non … magnifacere</i>) is mirrored, not flattened to one valence.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Dateline <i>D. 1. April. stylo veteri Anno 1598</i> = <b>1 April 1598, expressly Old Style (Julian)</b> — Tycho marks the style himself, anticipating his Catholic-Imperial correspondent's New Style; the catalogue label (1 April 1598, Wandsbek) agrees, so no header reconciliation is needed. <i>manu propria</i> = \"with his own hand.\"</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The substantial POSTSCRIPTA — Tycho's reaction to the enclosed Ursus Fundamentum Astronomicon, the 1584 Uraniborg-copying plagiarism charge, and the rebuke of Kepler for over-praising Ursus \"decus Germaniae\" — restored from Dreyer VIII, pp. 46–48 (dropped after \"manu propria\").</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Postscript, l.2-3: 'feri Ursi' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.46) restored for the corrupted 'seri Ursi', recovering the 'feri...veri' (wild Bear/true man) pun on Ursus's name; English 'late Bear' corrected to 'wild Bear'. l. following: 'Fundamentum Astronomicum' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.47), the real title of Ursus's 1588 tract, restored for the corrupted 'Fundamentum Astronomicon'; English title order corrected to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout and matches the known scholarly substance of the letter. Two soft points are flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the elliptical clause <i>ut simile quid nostris hypothesibus, quas ubi non incompertas esse liber ille ostendit, applicare possis</i> is syntactically compressed in the original (an anacoluthon around <i>ubi … ostendit</i>) and is rendered following the sense \"apply something similar to our hypotheses, which that book shows are not unknown [to you]\"; (2) <i>tuae alias industriae excogitationi applicata</i> (\"applied to the devising of your otherwise [admirable] industry\") is terse, with <i>alias</i> (\"otherwise\") left as the bracketed concessive it is. Neither affects the doctrinal content.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — §1 postscript restored clean; sense secure; only two terse-syntax soft points, no crux/lacuna/open identification; O.S. dateline label-agreed.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.44-49) and collated the full letter and its postscript (explicitly flagged in source_text as 'restored from Dreyer... dropped by the working transcription') against source_text and published_english. Checked every proper name (Copernicus, Ptolemy, Alfonso/Alphonsi, Maestlin, Ursus), number (29 years ago at Augsburg, 35 years of observations of which 25 later ones, apogee anticipates Copernicus by 5 degrees, angle of difference 1 degree 45 minutes, service to a Danish nobleman in [15]84, the [15]77 comet book), place (Graz/Gretz in Styria, Wandsbek, Hamburg, Silesia/Styria/Innsbruck as the Emperor's alternate refuges are NOT in this letter - none found misapplied), and the closing formula (1 April 1598 Old Style, half a mile from Hamburg). The main letter body (pp.44-46) is a fully faithful, thorough translation with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss. In the postscript, found two low-severity TEXT_ERRORs where our source_text's Latin departs from Dreyer's print (feri-&gt;seri; Astronomicum-&gt;Astronomicon); the English translation itself faithfully renders whatever the (corrupted) source said, and neither error changes the letter's overall sense (Ursus's plagiarism accusation and self-serving letter remain intact).</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source Latin reads 'seri' (serus, late) where Dreyer's page image clearly prints 'feri' (ferus, wild); the correct reading makes an antithetical pun 'feri...veri' (wild Bear vs. true man) on Ursus's name, which the corrupted 'seri' loses. The English is a faithful rendering of the corrupted source, so the fault is upstream in our Latin. Dreyer prints: <i>famofum quoque et nefarium fcriptum cuiufdam feri Urfi potius quam veri hominis (TBOO VIII p.46, Postscripta, l.2-3)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source Latin has the substantive form 'Astronomicon' where Dreyer prints the adjective 'Astronomicum' agreeing with 'Fundamentum'; the real title of Ursus's 1588 tract is 'Fundamentum Astronomicum', not 'Astronomicon'. Translation is faithful to the (corrupted) source, so the fault is upstream in our Latin. Dreyer prints: <i>fcripto ifto, quod Fundamentum vocat Aftronomicum (TBOO VIII p.47, l.2-3)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defects verified directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.46-47, within the letter's own slice, read pages 1-4) at the precise lines cited by the audit; the printed long-s/f forms and line breaks match the audit's citations exactly, and the 'feri Ursi...veri hominis' reading is independently supported by grammatical parallelism (genitive adjective-noun pairs) and by the fact that Ursus was still alive in 1598, so 'seri' (late, i.e. deceased) could not be correct. No crux remains in either defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15971213001", "next": "brahe-t_15990200001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971213001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990200001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Kepler's letter and praises the *Prodromus Dissertationum Cosmographicarum*, admiring its wit and the ingenious speculation binding planetary distances to the regular solids.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980401001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980410001", "d": "1598-04-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Wandsbek, 10 April 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia (= Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv (Afskrift / contemporary copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM SEVERINI VELLEIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et eruditissime Domine Magister ANDREA, veteri amicitia mihi iunctissime. Scripsi ad te ante aliquot hebdomadas per tabellarium quendam Ripensem, qui semel atque iterum hîc fuit, et inter alia de victualibus isthic in meum usum conquirendis denuo admonui. Nec dubito eas te recepisse litteras, utut altum agens silentium nihil interea responderis. Quod tamen te facturum existimabam, si non prius, saltem circa hoc tempus, cum boum mercatores inde Hamburgum frequentes veniunt. Rogo itaque ut id, quod omissum est, emendes, atque de victualibus procurandis quae spes sit, indices. Scripsi denuo ea de re ANDREAe SEVERINI, civi isthic primario, qui suam mihi hoc in negotio dudum addixit operam. Spero ipsum suo officio non defuturum. Tu tamen nihilominus, quantum in te est, fac ut desiderijs meis respondeat eventus, nec tot pollicitationes nimium frustra. Adhortare etiam amicos et consanguineos meos per occasionem, si quos faventes et fidos inveneris, ut mihi iam extero, et amplam hîc in summa annonae caritate alenti familiam id praestent officij, quod sibi vellent, si eorum talis esset conditio. A sorore MARGARETA, quam mihi non defuturam putabas, ne verbum quidem accepi, licet bis illi scripserim, vix tertio facturus. Scio quidem victualium exportationem severe isthic prohibitam esse; attamen non me indigenam Regni Nobilem, sed mercatores hoc edictum concernere crediderim, nisi forte odio mei id fiat. Quod quomodo in Patria promeritus sim, ignoro. Id tamen scio, me isthic ultra tonnam auri insumpsisse, ut eius honori studerem; et quod plus est, prorsusque irreparabile, viginti unum Annos de potissima aetatis parte isthic non satis commode contrivisse. Quod quam maxime doleo. Nec mihi haec temporis et aetatis iactura evenisset, nisi ab optimo et laudatiss. mem. Rege FRIDERICO ita persuasus fuissem, cui et Patriae id concedendum ratus sum. Neque enim ullo modo persentiscere tunc potui, me nihil aliud quam invidiam, odium, calumnias et fraudulentas persecutiones inde reportaturum, unâ cum rei familiaris non exigua iactura. Sed ita est, uti nuper ad me clarissimus ille IACOBUS MONAVIUS Vratislavia perscripsit, inter alia hunc Kluppelhardum addens: »Est, erit atque fuit Vndanck in fine laborum«. Imo is et altius ea quae mihi in patria evenerunt, si non nimis hyperbolice exaggerans, in haec verba prorumpit: »Sed et hoc mihi incidit: Quid si mundus hic non sit dignus istis Atlanticis bonis et donis nostri TYCHONIS. Etiam hoc cogitavi: quid si haec Τύχη, quae Domino TYCHONI contigit, nunciet instare finem mundi? Utut sit, adhuc valde dubito, tuamne vicem an Patriae potius tuae, imo totius Reip. Christianae magis dolere debeam«. Moxque addit: »Miratus sum Regem nihil voluisse conferre in haec studia Regibus et eorum subsidijs atque adiumento dignissima, qui tamen nuper tantos sumptus fecit in coronationis festo. Aliud fuit iudicium ALPHONSI, imo aliud etiam ipsius patris FRIDERICI«. Haec et similia non saltem ille sed et multi alij viri in Germania atque alibi cordatiores et mihi et sibi invicem de me meisque rebus saepius scribunt. Ego vero invite fero quod Regi meo serenissimo et toti Patriae imputentur ea, quae paucorum machinata est perversitas, ab unico et veteri meo osore, quem tute nosti, de quo etiam ex Anglia mihi relatum est, praestantibus viris ibi innotuisse eum has turbas ex inveterata contra me invidia subdole et Machiavellice solum movisse, idque per alios, cum ipsemet par non esset, proficiscens. Quod et multis Germanis, nescio quibus indicijs innotuit; adeo ut vel sic (si non satis ante) famosus evadat. Ego semper Regem et Patriam, omnes in ea probos et integros viros excusare soleo. De caeteris taceo, et dissimulo ad tempus. Omnia successive et per intervalla suam sortiuntur maturationem et fructuum edunt specimen, ut quivis ea quae sevit, metat. Sed valeant ista unâ cum istis.</p><p>Tu vero mi amantissime Domine Magister ANDREA me, prout antea constituisti, hîc invise, ut semel adhuc, siquidem saepius non liceat, nos invicem intueamur et colloquamur, antequam disiungamur longius. Hyems nunc non obstat. Est inter Pascha et Pentecosten iucundissimum Anni tempus et profectionibus imprimis aptum. Valetudo si qua tibi est remissior, motu corporis inter peregrinandum et aëris mutatione recuperatur potius quam infringitur, cum quies illi minus conveniat. Nihil igitur aliud obstare poterit, quin post paucas hebdomadas hîc adsis, quam cura domestica et parsimonia; domui satis uxor providebit. Et maior est in amoris constantia parsimonia Amicum veterem negligere quam aliquam pecuniulae partem, quae resarciri potest, atque alias interdum profluit? Dedi etiam literas eodem tempore ad CHRISTIERNUM LANGIUM quo ad te, cuius filius etiamnum mecum est, et ne quidem ab illo quidpiam responsi accepi. Adest iam tempus ad quod usque filium eius in meam familiam me recepturum addixi. Vellem itaque ut Parens se, quid in posterum de eo agi velit, resolveret. Est quidem is satis modestus, et quantum ego scio, honeste se gerens, attamen hisce sublimioribus, quae ego tracto studijs, minus idoneus. Nescio an alijs. Neque ea quibus ego incumbo, ipsi in posterum multum usui esse poterunt. Oportet enim in ijs excellere, si quid hinc emolumenti sperandum. Effice igitur ut Parens mihi mature de sua voluntate scribat. Alterum quoque LANGIUM IOHANNEM per literas suadente OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO nostro sollicitavi, qui etiam nihil rescripsit. Miror vestram taciturnitatem, cum ego multo forte pluribus onustus occupationibus responsione non supersedeam, undecunque tandem ad me perferantur literae, quod creberrime fit ex varijs Europae locis, sed nihil novi est me a meis in Patria negligi, apud exteros non item.</p><p>Vale et si potes audesque, veni, nihilque metue, res enim caret periculo. Stans tegor in solido, ventus strepat, ignis et unda. Saluta ex me uxorem tuam unâ cum alijs amicis Ripensibus.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgi die 10. Aprilis Anno 98.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Anders Sørensen Vedel.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most distinguished and most learned Master Anders, joined to me most closely by an old friendship. I wrote to you some weeks ago by a certain courier of Ribe, who has been here once and again, and among other things reminded you once more about the provisions to be procured there for my use. Nor do I doubt that you received that letter, even though, keeping a deep silence, you have meanwhile sent no reply — which yet I thought you would do, if not sooner, at least about this time, when the cattle-merchants come in numbers from there to Hamburg. I ask you therefore to make good what has been omitted, and to indicate what hope there is of provisions being procured. I have written again on this matter to Anders Sørensen, a leading citizen there, who long ago pledged me his service in this business. I hope he will not fail in his duty. You none the less, so far as in you lies, see to it that the outcome answers to my wishes, and that so many promises [come] not too much to nothing. Exhort too my friends and kinsmen, as occasion offers — if you find any well-disposed and faithful — that to me, now an exile, and here maintaining a large household amid the utmost dearth of provisions, they may render that good office which they would wish [done] for themselves, were their own condition such. From my sister Margrete, who you thought would not fail me, I have not had so much as a word, though I have written to her twice, and shall scarcely do so a third time. I know indeed that the export of provisions is there strictly forbidden; yet I should think that this edict concerns not me, a native nobleman of the Realm, but the merchants — unless perhaps it is done out of hatred of me. How I have deserved [this] in [my] Fatherland, I do not know. This, however, I do know: that I spent there above a barrel of gold to study its honour; and, what is more, and altogether beyond repair, that I wore away there one-and-twenty years of the best part of [my] life, none too comfortably. Which I grieve over as deeply as can be. Nor would this loss of time and of [my] prime have befallen me, had I not been so persuaded by the most excellent King Frederick, of most praiseworthy memory, to whom and to the Fatherland I judged that it should be granted. For in no way could I then have foreseen that I should carry off thence nothing other than envy, hatred, slanders, and fraudulent persecutions, together with no small loss to my estate. But so it is, as that most distinguished Jacob Monau lately wrote to me at length from Breslau, adding among other things this blunt German saying [Kluppelhard]: »<i>Ingratitude</i> [<i>Vndanck</i>] is, will be, and was at the end of [one's] labours.« Nay, he breaks out — going deeper into the things that have befallen me in my homeland, if [he is] not exaggerating too hyperbolically — into these words: »But this too has occurred to me: What if this world be not worthy of those Atlantean goods and gifts of our Tycho? This too I have pondered: what if this <i>Týchē</i> [Fortune] that has befallen Master Tycho announces that the end of the world is at hand? However that be, I am still much in doubt whether I ought rather to grieve over your case, or over that of your Fatherland — nay, of the whole Christian commonwealth.« And presently he adds: »I have wondered that the King was unwilling to bestow anything upon these studies — most worthy of kings and of their support and aid — who nevertheless lately made such great outlays at the coronation feast. Other was the judgement of Alfonso — nay, other too [the judgement] of his own father Frederick.« These and like things not only he, but many other men of sounder sense in Germany and elsewhere, write often, both to me and to one another, about me and my affairs. But I bear it unwillingly that there are charged to my most serene King and to the whole Fatherland those things which the perversity of a few has contrived — by a single old hater of mine, whom you yourself know; of whom it has also been reported to me from England that it became known to eminent men there that he alone stirred up these troubles, out of an inveterate envy against me, by stealth and in Machiavellian fashion, and that, going about it through others (since he himself was not equal [to it]), [he did so]. Which has become known to many Germans too, by I know not what indications — so that even thus (if not enough before) he turns out infamous. I am always wont to excuse the King and the Fatherland, and all upright and honest men in it. Of the rest I keep silent, and dissemble for a time. All things, successively and at intervals, attain their ripening and yield a showing of their fruits, so that each man reaps what he has sown. But let these matters fare well, along with those [men].</p><p>But you, my most beloved Master Anders, visit me here, as you earlier resolved, that — once more at least, if it may not be oftener — we may look upon each other and converse together before we are parted further apart. Winter is now no hindrance. Between Easter and Pentecost is the most delightful time of the year, and especially apt for journeys. If your health is at all the weaker, it is restored rather than impaired by the body's motion in travelling and by the change of air, since rest suits it less well. Nothing else, therefore, will be able to stand in the way of your being here after a few weeks, save household care and thrift; your wife will provide for the house well enough. And is it the greater thrift, in the constancy of love, to neglect an old friend than [to spend] some little portion of money, which can be made good, and which besides flows away from time to time anyhow? I also gave a letter, at the same time as [the one] to you, to Christiern Lange, whose son is even now with me, and from him I have not had any reply at all. The time is now at hand up to which I pledged that I would receive his son into my household. I should wish, therefore, that the father would resolve what he wishes done about him hereafter. He is indeed modest enough, and, so far as I know, behaves honestly, yet [is] less suited to these loftier studies which I treat. I do not know whether [he is suited] to others. Nor will the things on which I am bent be able to be of much use to him hereafter. For one must excel in them, if any profit is to be hoped for from this. See to it, therefore, that the father writes to me in good time about his will [in the matter]. I have also solicited by letter the other Lange, Johan — at the urging of our Holger Rosenkrantz — who likewise has written nothing back. I marvel at your [pl.] silence, since I, burdened perhaps with far more occupations, do not put off a reply, from wherever at length the letters are brought to me — which happens very frequently, from various places of Europe; but it is nothing new that I am neglected by my own people in the Fatherland, [though] not so among foreigners.</p><p>Farewell, and if you can and dare, come, and fear nothing — for the matter is without danger. Standing, I am sheltered on firm ground; let the wind bluster, [and] fire and wave. Greet for me your wife, together with the other friends of Ribe.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, on the 10th day of April, in the year [15]98.<br>Yours, Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> Tycho Brahe, writing from <b>Wandsbek</b> (Lat. <i>Wandesburgum</i>, then in Holstein, now part of Hamburg), where he stayed 1597–98 with Heinrich Rantzau after leaving Denmark in voluntary exile (June 1597). The letter is suffused with the bitterness of that exile.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Andreas Severini Velleius</i> = <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (1542–1616), the Danish royal historiographer and ballad-editor, resident at <b>Ribe</b> (Lat. <i>Ripa</i>; <i>tabellarius Ripensis</i> = \"courier of Ribe\"; <i>amici Ripenses</i> = \"friends of Ribe\"); an old friend of Tycho's. Salutation/subscription formulae translated per protocol §5.5 (<i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> → \"Heartiest greeting\"; <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"with his own hand\").</li><li><b>Anders Sørensen, leading citizen of Ribe.</b> A second, distinct <i>Andreas Severini</i> — <i>civis primarius</i> of Ribe acting as Tycho's purchasing agent — not the recipient (who is also <i>Andreas Severini</i>); the doubling of the patronymic is in the source and is kept.</li><li><b>Sister Margrete.</b> <i>soror Margareta</i> = <b>Margrete Brahe</b>, Tycho's sister.</li><li><b><i>ultra tonnam auri</b></i> — \"above a barrel of gold,\" Danish <i>en tønde guld</i>, a proverbial vast sum (≈ 100,000 rixdollars); rendered literally and glossed.</li><li><b><i>viginti unum Annos … isthic … contrivisse</b></i> — \"one-and-twenty years … wore away there\": Tycho counts from c. 1576 (the grant of Hven) to his 1597 departure.</li><li><b>King Frederick.</b> <i>Rex Fridericus, laudatiss. mem.</i> = <b>Frederik II</b> (r. 1559–88), \"of most praiseworthy memory,\" who granted Tycho Hven; contrasted implicitly with the young <b>Christian IV</b> (crowned 1596), under whom Tycho's support lapsed.</li><li><b>Jacob Monau.</b> <i>Iacobus Monavius</i> = <b>Jacob Monau</b> (Monawius, 1546–1603), the Breslau (<i>Vratislavia</i> = <b>Wrocław/Breslau</b>) humanist and patron, a correspondent of Tycho's. The passages in guillemets (»…«) are Tycho's verbatim quotation of Monau's letter to him.</li><li><b><i>Kluppelhardum</b></i> — a Latinised accusative of a German coinage (cf. <i>Klüppel/Knüppel</i> \"cudgel\" + <i>hart</i> \"hard\"), i.e. a \"blunt / rough-hewn German saying.\" Tycho's own nonce-word; rendered \"this blunt German saying\" with the original bracketed, the word flagged rather than smoothed. <b>Crux:</b> the exact register (jocular vs. coarse) is not certain.</li><li><b><i>Vndanck in fine laborum</b></i> — Monau's macaronic proverb embedding the German <i>Undank</i> (\"ingratitude\") in a Latin frame: \"Ingratitude is, will be, and was at the end of [one's] labours\" — a variant of the German <i>Undank ist der Welt Lohn</i> (\"ingratitude is the world's reward\"). The German word <i>Vndanck</i> is kept in the source block and bracketed in the translation per the code-switch policy (§2).</li><li><b>Greek.</b> <i>Τύχη</i> — <i>Týchē</i>, \"Fortune / [bad] luck\"; Monau puns on Tycho's name (<i>Tycho</i> ↔ <i>týchē</i>), echoing the established wordplay (cf. Wolf 1571, <i>εὐτυχέστατε Τυχών</i>). Transliterated and glossed, the pun flagged not flattened (§4). <i>Atlanticis bonis et donis</i> = \"Atlantean goods and gifts,\" i.e. burdens/gifts worthy of Atlas (the Titan who bears the heavens) — apt for an astronomer.</li><li><b><i>Alphonsi</b></i> — <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b> (\"the Wise,\" r. 1252–84), patron of the Alfonsine Tables, the archetype of the astronomy-funding prince; Monau contrasts his liberality (and Frederik II's) with the present king's.</li><li><b><i>coronationis festo</b></i> — the coronation feast of <b>Christian IV</b> (crowned 29 August 1596), on which the crown spent lavishly while withdrawing Tycho's support.</li><li><b>The \"single old hater.\"</b> <i>unicus et vetus osor … quem tute nosti … Machiavellice</i> — Tycho's unnamed enemy at court, whom Vedel is presumed to know; not identified in the text and left unidentified here. The \"report from England\" of his exposure is given as Tycho relays it (hearsay), and the hedges are preserved.</li><li><b><i>Christiernus Langius / Iohannes Langius</b></i> — <b>Christiern (Christian) Lange</b> and <b>Johan Lange</b>, Danish noblemen; Christiern's son was then a pupil in Tycho's household. <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantzius</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (here \"our Holger,\" a mutual friend; likely Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned,\" 1574–1642, though the elder Holger Rosenkrantz is also possible — identification left open).</li><li><b><i>vestram taciturnitatem</b></i> — \"your [plural] silence\": Tycho widens the reproach from Vedel alone to the whole circle of unresponsive Danish correspondents.</li><li><b><i>Stans tegor in solido, ventus strepat, ignis et unda.</b></i> — a self-standing <b>sententia / motto</b>, roughly hexametric in cadence: \"Standing, I am sheltered on firm ground; let the wind bluster, [and] fire and wave.\" Kept as a maxim (per §4, sententiae preserved); <i>strepat</i> read as a jussive/concessive subjunctive (\"let it rage\"), with <i>ignis et unda</i> governed by it in parallel. A defiant Stoic emblem of constancy in exile.</li><li><b>Calendar / place.</b> <i>Dabantur Wandesburgi die 10. Aprilis Anno 98</i> = \"Given at <b>Wandsbek</b>, the 10th day of April, [15]98\" (<b>O.S.</b> — Tycho, a Dane in Holstein, still reckoning Old Style). The dataset `label` (\"1598. 10. april. Wandsburg\") agrees with the dateline; <b>no date discrepancy.</b> (A neighbouring letter in the source CSV, Tycho → Maestlin, is dated 21 April 1598 from the same place — a different letter, not a variant of this one.)</li><li><b>Allusion.</b> <i>quivis ea quae sevit, metat</i> — \"each man reaps what he has sown\" (Galatians 6:7), woven into Tycho's threat-tinged forecast that wrongs will bear fruit in time; the Scriptural register is kept.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English: fixed a wrong-antecedent mistranslation in Jacob Monau's embedded remark — 'ipsius patris Friderici' refers to the young King Christian IV's own father, Frederick II, not (as the bracketed '[your]' falsely implied) to Tycho's own father Otte Brahe.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely throughout; the one genuinely soft point is the nonce-word <i>Kluppelhardum</i> (register and exact sense of Tycho's German-Latin coinage), flagged above and rendered with the original bracketed rather than smoothed. The unnamed court enemy and the precise identity of <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantzius</i> are left open as the text leaves them.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against TBOO VIII pp.48-50 (Dreyer page images, not OCR). Latin source_text matches Dreyer verbatim throughout, including proper names (MONAVIUS/Vratislavia, MARGARETA, LANGIUM, ROSENKRANTZIO), the sum ('ultra tonnam auri'), the count ('viginti unum Annos'), and the closing date/signature ('Wandesburgi die 10. Aprilis Anno 98 / Tuus Tycho Brahe manu propria'), all confirmed on the page images with no TEXT_ERROR. One mistranslated pronoun-antecedent found in the Monau quotation, downgrading from the self-assigned B.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). This is Monau's embedded quote about the young King (Christian IV); 'ipsius' is reflexive to 'Regem' (the King), so 'ipsius patris Friderici' means the King's own father, Frederick II — not Tycho's father. The bracketed '[your]' fabricates a false genealogical claim (Tycho's actual father was Otte Brahe), a wrong-antecedent error of the exact type this pipeline is known to produce. At: “Other was the judgement of Alfonso — nay, other too [the judgement] of [your] father Frederick himself.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image TBOO VIII p.49, which prints 'Aliud fuit iudicium ALPHONSI, imo aliud etiam ipfius patris FRIDERICI«' verbatim as in current_latin, so no Latin change was needed. The surrounding context on the same page (Monau's complaint that 'the King' declined to fund Tycho's studies despite lavish coronation spending) confirms 'ipsius' refers back to 'Regem', not to Tycho. Only the English mistranslation required correction; the remainder of the letter was not re-collated beyond the single defect site per the prime rule.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Tycho Brahes privatarkiv. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15980218001", "next": "brahe-t_15981230001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Presses Vedel to respond about provisions to be procured for Tycho's use and to enlist friends and kinsmen in securing them.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980410001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980421001", "d": "1598-04-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Michael Mästlin", "ck": "Michael Mästlin", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Michael Maestlin — Wandsbek, 21 April 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: B = Wolfenbüttel, Herzog August Bibliothek, 15.3 Aug. in 2°, fol. 317</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text note.</b> The working dump carried OCR apparatus-sigla (stray \"A\"); the source block is <b>normalized</b> to clean readable Latin, faithful to the wording.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO M. MICHAELI MAeSTLINO, MATHEMATICARUM ARTIUM INSIGNITER PERITO, EARUNDEMQUE IN INCLYTA ACADEMIA TUBINGENSI PROFESSORI CELEBERRIMO, MIHI PLURIMUM DILECTO. TÜBINGEN.</p><p>S.</p><p>ANNUS iam agitur decimus, eruditissime et praestantissime MAeSTLINE, ex quo quidam e meis domesticis, qui aliquandiu mihi in rebus Astronomicis inservierat, exemplum tuarum literarum Tubingâ ad se datarum in Daniam mihi transmisit, quibus pollicebaris, te librum nostrum de Cometa anni 77 tibi ab eodem communicatum diligenter perlecturum, tuamque de eius contentis sententiam conscripturum, atque mecum de eis per literas collaturum, quas te Marpurgum ad eundem mittere decrevisse, ad me postea procurandas innuisti. At ego toto interlapsis annis, neque hac de re aut quapiam alia, negocium Astronomicum, quo utrinque afficimur, concernente, ne verbulum quidem a te accepi, sive scripto, sive aliorum indicatione, qui isthinc venientes me Uraniburgi, dum adhuc in Patria degerem, inviserunt. Existimassem equidem te ob communia nobis studia scriptione aliqua saltem tot interlabentibus annis non supersessurum, praesertim cum id facturum sponte in te receperis. Ne vero ego ob id tecum per literas, siquidem aliter non liceat, familiaritatem contrahendi omnem occasionem respuisse videar, quod ab humanitate et exercitijs, quibus delectamur, sublimioribus alienum foret: en nunc praevenio, et ad scribendum respondendumque ultro te invito: data nimirum hac commodiore, qua nunc fruor, oportunitate, quam antea in Dania, ex quo in arce quadam Ranzoviana, dimidio saltem miliari Hamburgo remota, nunc habitem, unde in varia, non saltem Germaniae, sed totius pene Europae loca creber et commodus datur nuncius. Si itaque quid habes, de quo mecum in rebus Astronomicis, sive Cometicum illud negocium, sive quidvis aliud concernat, conferre libuerit, invenies me pro meo modulo, et quantum hactenus in ijs assequi licuit, ad respondendum non difficilem.</p><p>Allatum mihi nuper est eruditum quoddam scriptum Mathematici excellentis JOANNIS KEPLERI e Styria, in quo revolutiones Planetarum secundum formam Coperniceam ad 5 regularia corpora harmonice redigere ingeniosa sane speculatione conatur, qua etiam de re nuper ad me perscripsit, egoque illi, quantum prae occupationum mole licuit, meam vicissim de talibus aperui sententiam. Video autem te in eodem libro de Hypothesi a nobis ante annos complures adinventa nonnulla quasi dubitanter adferre, cum promiscue eam etiam alijs tribuas, plagiarijs forte meis nimium auscultans, tum quoque plura de eadem inferens, quae si consideratius expendisses, et sententiam nostram atque omnes occasiones huius Hypotheseos constituendae undiquaque perspectas habuisses, haesitationibus eiuscemodi forte supersedisses. Atqui ego, si tibi quid in hac inventione scrupuli restet, id amovere atque penitius rem omnem explanare paratus sum, quod etiam res ipsa docebit, ubi Planetarum motus ad eam normam a nobis adeo accurate expansos cognoveris, ut nihil sit, quod sensus amplius requirant, utut tu in eodem libello de Astronomiae correctione non saltem dubitare, sed et animum abijcere videaris, licet antea aliter spem feceris. Si ab anteriore per corpora ista regularia dimensione facta, ea restituenda erit, potius quam ex accuratis observationibus a posteriori sumptis, veluti insinuas, utique nimis diu, si non prorsus in perpetuum frustra expectabimus, antequam tale quid a quoquam praestari poterit. Cum enim istorum regularium corporum dimensionis explicatio, si omni ex parte admittenda foret, observationibus antecedentibus niti et comprobari debeat, nec etiam particularia quaeque praeter generaliora illa, utcunque se habentia, hinc ea qua opus est praecisione derivari possunt, quod te non latere arbitror. Neque etiam adhuc satis extricatae sunt Planetarum biformes inaequalitates, quas duplici eccentrico vel circello excusant. Ignoratur enim iusta harum proportio, ut de Apogaeis non dicam longe aliter se habentibus, quam fert non saltem Alphonsina, sed et Copernicea ratiocinatio. Quin et annua illa diversitatis evidens insinuatio necdum satis comperta est, ita ut undiquaque suis numeris absolute constet, ut ob id e Coperniceis proportionibus circulorum haec ea, qua requiritur, subtilitate rimari invium sit; qua etiam de re eruditissimum KEPLERUM per literas admonui, et in Marte, ubi hae differentiae magis sunt sensibiles, exemplum indicavi. Verum de his et similibus nimis prolixum foret hîc singula exequi.</p><p>Ubi opera mea Astronomica lucem publicam viderint, quod nunc magnis viribus nec minoribus quam antea in Patria urgeo, perspicies, uti spero, rem Astronomicam aliter quam putatur redintegrari posse, et me non contemnendam in ea locasse operam; quare de his plura nunc non addo. Aliud quiddam est, etsi minoris momenti, quod tamen ad negocium hoc omnimode exequendum non parum conducit, pro ea videlicet, quam meridianorum ingerit in rotundo Terrae globo alteratio, diversitate necdum satis accurate explorata, de quo tecum agere averem. Si itaque aliquot Eclipses, praesertim Lunares, isthic exquisita diligentia praeteritis annis observatas habes (de quo non dubito) earum momenta per observationum etiam circumstantias mecum mature communices velim, quo collatione facta cum 20 aut pluribus Lunae deliquijs a me non paucis iam transactis annis diligenter consideratis conferri queant. Existimo me aliqua inter ea, quae ego denotavi, inventurum, quae etiam a te isthic observata sunt, atque hinc meridianorum intercapedo, aut ad minimum ductus alicuius meridiani per certa quaedam Germaniae loca pervestigari poterit, praesupposito nimirum quodam cognito ad rationem eam, quam PTOLEMAeUS et COPERNICUS assumserunt. Hoc si mihi praestiteris officij, rem non saltem gratam, sed etiam Astronomiae promotioni non inutilem effeceris. Idem quoque apud alios, quotquot novi coelestibus apparentijs fideliter attendere, qui sane perpauci (quod dolendum est) existunt, experior, ut certi quid in his, quoad licuerit, statuatur. Ad ultima duo luminarium deliquia, quae in Februario proxime elapso contigerunt, te sedulo attendisse non dubito; quare eorum etiam momenta et phases isthic animadversas indica. Lunae Eclipsis, quae 11. eius mensis die contigit, hîc ubi polus elevatur 53° 35½', principium habuit, quando superior Lunae limbus attollebatur supra horizontem 24° fere. Hinc tempus provenit, adhibito viso loco Lunae iuxta nostram restitutionem et refractionum impedimento sublato, H. 4. 14' post mediam noctem antecedentem. Finis deliquij infra horizontem evenit. Visa autem est Luna ferme tota offuscari, minima eius portiuncula ab umbra Terrae libera existente. Solis eclipsatio, quae 25. die accidit, hîc, quantum inter recurrentes nubes animadvertere licuit, initium habuit H. 9. 52' A.M. per Armillas Aequatorias 6 pedum in diametro (nam Instrumenta mea Astronomica huc in Germaniam e Dania, ut conscius sis, transtuli) temporis momenta denotando. Medium eius, uti neque finem, ob crassiusculas et continuas nubes intervenientes conspicere non dabatur. Conveniunt haec tempora in utraque Eclipsi satis praecise cum nostro redintegrato calculo, ex 18 Lunaribus et 6 Solaribus prius subtiliter denotatis eclipsationibus depromto, ut de alijs hincinde saepenumero expensis amborum luminarium motionibus huc etiam conducentibus nihil addam. Quantitas corporis Solaris Lunae interventu offuscati debuit iuxta mea ratiocinia esse digit. 10¾ in hoc horizonte, idque a superiori parte, cum calculus Coperniceus et phasin et momenta huius obscurationis aliter exhibeat, uti et in Lunari Eclipsi tam is quam Alphonsinus nimium cis et ultra exorbitârunt. An deliquij Solaris magnitudinem nostris numeris recte attigerimus, iudicent illi, qui eius medium sereniore aura usi conspexerunt. Id vero sciendum est, Lunam in novilunijs eclipticis non apparere ea magnitudine, qua alias in plenilunijs, utut in eadem fuerit a Terra remotione, sed quasi pro quinta parte eam certis de causis alibi reserandis constringere; unde fit ut Luna nunquam totum Solem prorsus obtegere queat, etiamsi centraliter quoad visum interponatur, lumine Solari eam aliquali adhuc vestigio ambiente, quod et in hoc ultimo deliquio animadvertere poterant illi, quibus maxima offuscatio patuit. Oportuit enim tunc Solem de Lunari corpore utrinque plus includere, idque corniculatim, quam linea per diametrum ducta exigebat, utut Lunae diameter tunc fuerit iuxta nostrum etiam calculum 34¾', apparere autem apud Solem non potuit maior quam 28', quam limitationem a nemine antea animadversam cognovi, sed in aliquot Solis observationibus, cum tam a superiore quam inferiore parte tegeretur, sic me docuit experientia. Tuum erit, excell. MAeSTLINE, quid in his et similibus compertum habeas, mihi etiam aperire, atque in posterum iucundam inter nos de rebus Astronomicis collationem, quam et ego non invite admittam, exercere. De Eclipsium autem, potissimum Lunarium, a te subtiliter animadversis momentis me, quam primum fieri queat, certiorem redde, ut correctionem longitudinum, de qua dixi, quam brevi in quadam tabella adornare constitui, tam hinc, quam ex alijs quibusdam aliunde conquisitis indicijs eo promtius expediam. Iam vale, v. cl. et de rebus Astronomicis laudabiliter merite, in quibus etiam expoliendis et locupletandis ut in posterum perseveres, te adhortor. Ubi rescripseris, effice, ut literae quocunque tandem viarum ductu, sive per Norimbergam, sive aliquam aliam celebrem Germaniae urbem Hamburgum perferantur, in hospitium isthic meum, quod est apud HERM. REUTTER sub signo coronae aureae, sicque eas me certo obtenturum non ambigo.</p><p>Iamque iterum et saepius vale.<br>Dabantur Wandesburgo die 21 Apr. 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 55; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Cum intellexissem Tabellarium toto hoc die Hamburgi moraturum, curavi exemplum literarum KEPLERI ad me unâ cum meo responso describi, hisque adiungi, quo de earum etiam contentis certior fieres: ubi plura huc facientia invenies.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and excellent man, Master Michael Maestlin, signally skilled in the Mathematical Arts, and most celebrated Professor of the same in the renowned Academy of Tübingen, by me most beloved. Tübingen.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>It is now the tenth year, most learned and excellent Maestlin, since <b>one of my household — who for some while had served me in Astronomical matters — sent me into Denmark a copy of your letter, written from Tübingen to him</b>, in which you promised that you would diligently read through <b>our book on the Comet of the year [15]77</b> (communicated to you by him), and write your opinion of its contents, and confer with me by letter about them — which you had resolved to send to Marburg, to that same man, to be afterward procured to me. But <b>in all the intervening years I have received not even a little word from you</b> — on this or any other matter concerning the Astronomical business by which we are both affected — whether by writing or by the report of others who, coming thence, visited me at Uraniborg while I still dwelt in my fatherland. I should indeed have thought that you, on account of our common studies, would not have refrained from at least some writing in so many passing years — especially since you had of your own accord undertaken to do it. But lest I should seem, on that account, to have spurned every occasion of contracting a familiarity with you by letter (which would be alien to humanity, and to the loftier exercises in which we delight) — behold, <b>I now anticipate [you], and of my own accord invite you to write and reply</b>: this more convenient opportunity being now given me than before in Denmark, since I now dwell <b>in a certain Rantzau castle, but half a mile distant from Hamburg</b>, whence a frequent and convenient messenger is given to various places, not only of Germany, but of almost all Europe. If, then, you have anything you would confer with me about in Astronomical matters — whether the Cometary business or anything else — you will find me, to my small measure (and so far as I have hitherto been able to attain in them), not difficult in replying.</p><p><b>There was lately brought to me a certain erudite writing of the excellent Mathematician Johannes Kepler, from Styria, in which, by an ingenious speculation indeed, he attempts to reduce the revolutions of the Planets (according to the Copernican form) harmonically to the 5 regular [Platonic] solids</b> — about which matter he lately wrote me, and I in turn opened to him my view of such things, so far as the mass of [my] occupations allowed. But I see that <b>in the same book</b> [Kepler's <i>Mysterium</i>, with Maestlin's notes] <b>you bring forth some things, as if doubtfully, about the Hypothesis I invented several years ago — since you promiscuously attribute it also to others</b> (perhaps too much hearkening to my plagiarists), <b>and infer more about it</b> which, had you weighed it more considerately, and had a thorough view of my opinion and of all the occasions of establishing this Hypothesis, you would perhaps have abstained from such hesitations. But I, if any scruple remains with you in this invention, am ready to remove it, and to explain the whole matter more deeply — as the thing itself will also teach, when you have learned the Planets' motions, expanded by me so accurately to that norm that there is nothing the senses can further require — though you, in that same booklet, seem <b>not only to doubt about the correction of Astronomy, but even to cast away [your] courage</b>, although before you gave hope otherwise. <b>If [Astronomy] is to be restored from the prior dimension made through those regular solids, rather than from accurate observations taken <i>a posteriori</i> (as you insinuate), we shall assuredly wait too long — if not utterly in vain forever — before any such thing can be furnished by anyone.</b> For since the explication of those regular solids' dimension — if it were to be admitted in every part — must rest on, and be proved by, antecedent observations; nor can the particulars (beyond those more general things, however they stand) be derived hence with the precision that is needed — which I think does not escape you. Nor, moreover, are the <b>biform inequalities of the Planets</b> yet sufficiently extricated, which they excuse by a double eccentric or epicyclet; for the just proportion of these is unknown — to say nothing of <b>the Apogees standing far otherwise than not only the Alphonsine, but the Copernican reckoning bears</b>. Nay, that evident insinuation of an annual diversity is not yet sufficiently ascertained, so as to agree absolutely, in all its numbers, on every side — so that to search this out, from the Copernican proportions of circles, with the subtlety required, is impassable; <b>of which matter too I have admonished the most learned Kepler by letter, and have indicated an example in Mars, where these differences are more perceptible.</b> But of these and the like, it would be too prolix to pursue each here.</p><p>When my Astronomical works shall have seen the public light — which I now urge with great forces, no less than before in my fatherland — you will perceive, as I hope, that the Astronomical business can be restored otherwise than is thought, and that I have placed in it no contemptible labour; wherefore of these I add no more now. <b>There is another matter, though of less moment, which yet conduces not a little to fully carrying out this business: namely, that diversity — not yet accurately enough explored — which the alteration of meridians introduces on the round globe of the Earth</b> [longitude], about which I should wish to deal with you. If, then, <b>you have several Eclipses, especially Lunar, observed there with exquisite diligence in past years</b> (which I do not doubt), I would have you communicate their moments to me in good time, with the circumstances too of the observations — so that, a comparison being made, they can be compared with the <b>20 or more lunar eclipses I have diligently considered now over not a few past years</b>. I think I shall find among those I have noted some which were also observed by you there; and thence <b>the interval of meridians — or at least the draw of some meridian through certain places of Germany — can be investigated</b>, presupposing, of course, something known, according to the reckoning which Ptolemy and Copernicus assumed. If you render me this service, you will do a thing not only welcome, but also not useless to the promotion of Astronomy. The same I seek too from others, as many as I know to attend faithfully to the celestial appearances — who are indeed very few (which is to be lamented) — that something certain may be established in these, so far as may be. <b>To the last two eclipses of the luminaries, which happened in the February just past [1598], I do not doubt you attended diligently</b>; wherefore indicate the moments and phases of these observed there too. <b>The Lunar Eclipse which happened on the 11th of that month — here, where the pole is elevated 53° 35½′ [Wandsbek] — had its beginning when the Moon's upper limb was raised about 24° above the horizon. Hence the time comes out — using the Moon's seen place by our restitution, and the impediment of refractions removed — 4h 14′ after the preceding midnight.</b> The end of the eclipse occurred below the horizon. The Moon was seen almost wholly obscured, the smallest little portion of it being free from the Earth's shadow. <b>The Solar eclipse which fell on the 25th — here, as far as could be noted among recurring clouds — had its beginning at 9h 52′ A.M., by the Equatorial Armillae of 6 feet in diameter (for I have transferred my Astronomical Instruments hither into Germany from Denmark, that you may be aware), noting the moments of time.</b> Its middle — as also its end — could not be seen, on account of the rather thick and continuous intervening clouds. <b>These times, in both Eclipses, agree precisely enough with my restored calculation, drawn from 18 Lunar and 6 Solar eclipses previously subtly noted</b> (to say nothing of the other motions of both luminaries, often weighed here and there, also conducing hither). The quantity of the Solar body obscured by the Moon's intervention ought, by my reckonings, to have been <b>10¾ digits in this horizon, and that from the upper part</b> — whereas the Copernican calculation shows both the phase and the moments of this obscuration otherwise, as also in the Lunar Eclipse both it and the Alphonsine deviated too much, this side and beyond. Whether I have rightly attained the magnitude of the Solar eclipse by my numbers, let those judge who beheld its middle, enjoying clearer air.</p><p>But this must be known: <b>that the Moon, in ecliptic new-moons [conjunctions], does not appear of that magnitude which it has otherwise in full-moons — even though it be at the same distance from the Earth — but constricts it, as it were by a fifth part, for certain causes to be disclosed elsewhere; whence it comes about that the Moon can never wholly cover the whole Sun, even if it be centrally interposed to the sight, the Solar light still surrounding it with some trace</b> — which those to whom the maximum obscuration was visible could note even in this last eclipse. For the Sun then had to include more of the Lunar body on both sides — and that horn-wise [<i>corniculatim</i>] — than the line drawn through the diameter demanded; for although the Moon's diameter then was, by my calculation too, 34¾′, yet <b>it could not appear, at the Sun, greater than 28′</b> — which limitation I have found noticed by no one before, but experience taught me so, in several observations of the Sun, when it was covered both from the upper and from the lower part. It will be for you, excellent Maestlin, to open to me too what you have ascertained in these and the like, and henceforth to exercise that pleasant collation between us about Astronomical matters, which I too will not unwillingly admit. But of the moments of the Eclipses — especially Lunar — subtly noted by you, make me more certain as soon as can be, that I may the more readily expedite the correction of longitudes of which I spoke (which I have resolved shortly to set forth in a certain little table), both from these and from certain other indications gathered elsewhere. Now farewell, most renowned man, and laudably deserving of Astronomical matters — in the polishing and enriching of which I exhort you also to persevere hereafter. When you have replied, see that the letters be carried to <b>Hamburg</b>, by whatever route at last — whether through <b>Nuremberg</b> or some other famous German city — to my lodging there, which is <b>at Herman Reutter's, under the sign of the Golden Crown</b>; and so I do not doubt I shall certainly obtain them.</p><p>And now again, and oftener, farewell.<br>Given at Wandsbek, the 21st day of April, 1598.<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Since I had understood that the courier would tarry the whole of this day at Hamburg, I have had a copy made of Kepler's letter to me, together with my reply, and joined to these — that you might be made the more certain of their contents too: where you will find more things bearing on this matter.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (1550–1631), professor of mathematics at <b>Tübingen</b> — the Copernican astronomer who was <b>Kepler's teacher and mentor</b> (and the intermediary in the Ursus affair, cf. brahe-t_15990200001).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's engagement with Kepler's <i>Mysterium</i>, his empiricist credo, and the \"look at Mars\" hint (a momentous letter).</b></li><li><b>The renewed contact.</b> After a <b>ten-year silence</b> (Maestlin had once promised, <i>c.</i> 1588, to comment on Tycho's Comet-of-1577 book but never did), Tycho — now at <b>Wandsbek, \"half a mile from Hamburg,\"</b> with good post — reopens correspondence.</li><li><b>The <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i> critiqued.</b> Tycho has read <b>Kepler's \"erudite writing … reducing the planets' revolutions, in the Copernican form, harmonically to the 5 regular [Platonic] solids\"</b> (the <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i>, 1596) — \"an ingenious speculation indeed\" — and has written Kepler his view. He objects that <b>Maestlin's notes in the same book treat Tycho's world-system \"doubtfully,\" attributing it \"also to others\" (heeding \"my plagiarists\" — Ursus)</b> and that Maestlin seems to \"despair of correcting astronomy.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's empiricist credo (the methodological core).</b> The heart of the letter — and of Tychonic astronomy: <b>\"If astronomy is to be restored from the <i>a priori</i> dimension through those regular solids, rather than from accurate observations taken <i>a posteriori</i> … we shall wait too long, if not in vain forever.\"</b> The Platonic-solid scheme \"must rest on antecedent observations\"; the planets' <b>biform inequalities, the apogees (far off from Alphonsine <i>and</i> Copernican), and the annual equation</b> are not yet pinned down. <i>A frontal statement of observation-first empiricism against Kepler's a-priori Platonism.</i></li><li><b>\"Look at Mars\" — the seed of the <i>Astronomia nova</i>.</b> Tycho records: <b>\"of which matter too I have admonished the most learned Kepler by letter, and have indicated an example in Mars, where these differences are more perceptible.\"</b> This is the documentary trace of <b>Tycho directing Kepler to Mars</b> — the planet whose orbit Kepler would, on Tycho's data, crack open into the elliptical <i>Astronomia nova</i> (1609). A historically momentous pointer.</li><li><b>The eclipse-longitude project.</b> Tycho asks Maestlin for <b>lunar-eclipse timings</b> to compare with his own <b>\"20+ lunar eclipses\"</b> and so determine <b>meridian-differences (longitude)</b> through German places — a \"little table\" he means to compile.</li><li><b>The February-1598 eclipses at Wandsbek (precise data).</b> The <b>lunar eclipse of 11 Feb 1598</b> (Wandsbek <b>pole-height 53° 35½′</b>; begin 4h 14′ after midnight; Moon nearly total); the <b>solar eclipse of 25 Feb 1598</b> (begin 9h 52′ A.M., by the <b>6-foot Equatorial Armillae</b> Tycho had brought from Denmark; <b>10¾ digits</b>, from the upper part) — agreeing with Tycho's calculation \"drawn from <b>18 lunar and 6 solar eclipses</b>,\" against deviating Copernican/Alphonsine numbers.</li><li><b>The lunar-diameter-at-conjunction claim.</b> A genuine (if physically mistaken) Tychonic empirical observation: <b>the Moon at conjunction appears ~⅕ smaller than at opposition (34¾′ → only 28′ \"at the Sun\"), so it can never wholly cover the Sun</b> — Tycho's explanation for the residual ring of solar light (bearing on the annular-eclipse question), \"noticed by no one before.\"</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pair with <b>Kepler's Ursus apologia (brahe-t_15990200001)</b> (Maestlin the shared link); a keystone for the <b>Tycho–Kepler convergence</b> and the <b>genesis of the <i>Astronomia nova</b></i> (the Mars hint); the <b>eclipse-longitude</b> thread (brahe-t_16000405001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Michael Maestlin; <b>Johannes Kepler</b> (the <i>Mysterium</i>); the unnamed former household-assistant; <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus</b>; the <b>Alphonsine</b> reckoning; <b>Mars</b>; <b>Herman Reutter</b> (Tycho's Hamburg lodging, \"the Golden Crown\"). Places: <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau castle), <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Tübingen</b>, <b>Marburg</b>, <b>Nuremberg</b>, <b>Uraniborg/Denmark</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 21 Apr. 1598</i> at Wandsbek (Holstein O.S.; no <i>stylo</i> marker; label gives no N.S.) → <b>21 April 1598 [Old Style]</b>. (Source normalized from the OCR-marred dump; faithful to the wording.)</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript forwarding Kepler's letter + Tycho's reply to Maestlin, restored from Dreyer VIII p.55 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the ten-year-silence reopening; the <b>critique of Kepler's <i>Mysterium</i> and Maestlin's doubts about Tycho's system</b>; Tycho's <b>observation-first / <i>a posteriori</i> credo</b> against the Platonic-solid <i>a priori</i>; the <b>\"look at Mars\" hint</b> to Kepler; the <b>eclipse-longitude project</b>; the precise <b>Feb-1598 Wandsbek eclipse data</b> and the 18-lunar/6-solar foundation; and the <b>lunar-diameter-at-conjunction</b> claim) is firmly and carefully construed, with the technical content rendered faithfully and Tycho's methodological positions not sharpened. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VIII pp. 52-55, letter proper; neighbouring pp.51 and 56 also read as they carry the tail of the preceding Rollenhagius letter and the head of the following one) against Dreyer's page images in two chunks (pages 1-4 = pp.51-54, pages 5-6 = pp.55-56). Checked the address block and every proper name, number, date and place-name: Kepler, Maestlin, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Rantzau (arce Ranzoviana), Wandsbek/Wandesburgo, Hamburg, Nuremberg, Tubingen, Herman Reutter; the two February 1598 eclipse reports (11th lunar: pole 53d35.5', Moon's limb 24d, time 4h14' after midnight; 25th solar: begin 9h52' A.M., 6-foot equatorial armillae, 18 lunar/6 solar prior eclipses for the restored calculus, magnitude 10.75 digits, Moon's diameter 34.75' vs apparent 28' at the Sun) -- every figure matches Dreyer exactly. The postscript, flagged in source_text as \"restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.55; dropped by the working transcription,\" is confirmed present verbatim on Dreyer p.55 and is correctly translated. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["B Wolfenbüttel, Herzog August Bibliothek, 15. 3 Aug. in 2°, fol. 317."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Recalls a ten-year-old promise by Maestlin to read and comment on Tycho's 1577 comet book, and reproaches him for never writing.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980421001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980508001", "d": "1598-05-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 8 May 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Danish and Greek code-switches · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>; orig. in<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note:</b> square brackets in the source reproduce the editor's conjectural readings/restorations. The dateline gives only <i>\"die 8vo Anni 98\"</i> (the 8th … of the year [15]98) with <b>no month</b>; \"May\" is supplied from the catalogue label (<i>\"8. maj\"</i>).</p>", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ET VIRTUTE AC LITERIS HUMANIORIBUS EXCELLENTI VIRO D. OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO, DOMINO DE ROSENHOLM, COGNATO AFFINI AC AMICO SUO INTEGERRIMO ET DESIDERATISSIMO.<br>S. P.</p><p>TUAS Rosenholmio in initio Martij ad me dedisti, Generose OLIGERE, Consangvinee et affinis exoptatissime, circa 24tum eiusdem recte accepi, mihique fuere longe gratissimae, ad quarum contenta, ubi opus fuerit, nunc brevibus respondebo, postea alia quaedam subiuncturus. ERICUS SPARRE (quem tu per festinationem BRAHE vocas) hac non transivit, sed alia via Rostochium declinavit, ubi cum Doctore STURTZIO locutus grandia pollicitus est, quae apud Regem suum, compositis potissimum Svecicis rebus, meo nomine efficere vellet. Sed ego talibus Politicis et politis verbis tantum fidei adhibeo, quantum et tu. De Coldingensi conventu nondum certi quid nobis constat. Quidam Hamburgensibus, quidam Suecis irrequietandis bellum destinatum fuisse autumant. Exitus acta probat. RANZOVIUS noster victor quidem in sua caussa Haderslebiae fuit, idque iure merito. At sententiam in adversarium unâ cum exsequatione obtinere nequivit, utut Rex admodum illi infensus fuerit. Quam primum enim is, qui se periclitari animadvertit, [Cancellarium accessit, iniurias, non saltem RANZOVIO], sed et [ipsi Regi illatas superavit manum forte non vacuam porrigens]. De [PEPLERO] quod pudore quosdam affeceris, si modo quem habent, recte quidem a te factum, sed nihil tam iniquum est, quin si mihi ullo modo adversetur, aequum sit. Ego vero nihil inde moveor, saltem hominum quorundam effrenem malitiam et conscientiam nimis latam demiror, quibus sibi ipsi plus nocent quam mihi. Iudex generalis [Bergensis], qui cum suis assessoribus sententiam protulit, eam facile tuebitur, si ius locum invenerit, sin minus, transeant ista cum caeteris. Ego me ijs, nisi admodum fuerit necessarium, non miscebo. Interim tamen, quae a me accepisti, [Laumanno] communica, ut, quaecunque ex ijs probationis contrariae loco expetierit, habeat, aut etiam [Praefectus meus] prius ab [ESCILLO BILLE et te] instinctus illa proponet. Addam adhuc plura, si quae invenero huc facientia. Sententiam conscriptam iudex ipsemet adferet, nec difficulter obiectionibus singulis obviabit. Scripsi ad illum nonnulla hac de re, sed nihil adhuc responsi accepi. Citationem unâ misi, quam is tamen forte prius obtinuit. Mihi nihil de ea, [nisi ex te] constat. An [Uraniburgi] lecta sit, aut adhuc legenda, ignoro. Sed quidquid sit, perinde est, nec teneor secundum Leges, si modo pondus haberent, citationi illegitimae et non annuae auscultare, etiamsi res maioris esset momenti. Dette er lige saa gott som allt dett andit; naar de erre trette aff dette, saa kand de finde paa nogen ny Huffroe. Ego interim alia cogito. Quod tam fuse cum [Cancellarij uxore] de me rebusque meis locutus sis, recte et cordate a te factum. Vellem adhuc particulariora scire, atque ut ea ipsa per occasionem (dummodo induciae inter vos sunt) denuo inculcares, additis commentarijs et amplificationibus. Existimant [Machiavellistae isti] suas machinationes ita latere, ut nemo in [Dania] praeter authores eas intelligat. Interim exterae nationes de his loquuntur et scribunt; uti vel ex hac epistola mihi a tuo discessu missa, quam prope rem ipsam in Anglia attigerint, cognosces. Ipsum originale addidi, mihi, ubi descripseris, remittendum, ut eo certius probetur, me nihil in eo mutasse. Doctor IANUS MULIUS, qui hîc nuper apud me fuit, Doctorem illum, qui ea scripsit, in Italia, antequam promotus est, novit, et ex alijs literis ad se datis eius, uti mihi retulit, chirographum cognovit. Is tibi secreto sciscitanti plura referet. Nam et illi exemplum, ut sibi describeret, permisi, quo eo facilius [Machiavellistis] innotesceret, atque ut hinc perciperent, quam caute et prudenter sibi ipsis consuluerint. Quid eruditi passim in Germania atque alibi de mea isthinc emigratione sentiant, longum foret referre, neque nostratibus forte satis honorificum. Habes hîc unâ solius MONAVIJ Wratislaviensis literas ad me nuperas, quas cupio mihi remitti, quibus in quantum hunc casum exaggeret, percipies, adeo ut quodam in loco nimis hyperbolice loquatur. Det var noch hand var en Jyde. Ego semper Regem nostrum Serenissimum, aliosque in patria cordatos viros excusare soleo. Caeteri satis sunt [famosi] tam intra quam extra Regnum, ut me notatore non indigeant. Exemi quoque et Regem et quosvis integros viros ab ijs, quae mihi evenerunt, in prioribus meis ad MONAVIUM. Hinc est, quod ait, me recte facere, quod discernam actorem a Poëta fabulae. Quod vero de administratore nihil apud [praedictam] attuleris, consulte factum est. De ULFELDIO quod scribis, eum ementita omnia, quae tacite tibi significaram, asseverasse, equidem facile ita esse credo, cum nunquam pro veris ea habuerim, cumque multa alia ijs graviora efficta sciam.</p><p>GERHARDO RANZOVIO de equis scripsi, prout a te indicatum est, nec tamen me eos expetere ante festum Pentecostes. Victualia iam antea intellexi in Dania et cara esse, et ne eveherentur prohibitum. Nescio tamen, quo iure viro e familia Nobili isthic oriundo, qui in oeconomiae propriae subsidium ijs uti vellet, nec lucrum aut mercaturam circa ea exercere, interdictio obstare possit, praesertim si per alium virum nobilem, non autem mercatores negotium hoc procuretur. Sed cum maioribus in Patria carere possim, cur non et istis? Curabo aliunde e vicinis locis minoribus (uti spero) difficultatibus id effectum dari. Interim tua propensa voluntas mihi apprime grata est. Baronem illum, cuius inter nos mentio facta, in Austriam abijsse intellexi, nec scitur, an reversus sit. De maledico isto scripto, cuius caussa ad ipsum literas dare volui, non sum sollicitus, nam author eius tanta impudentia verecundiae et veritatis limites in eo superavit, ut nulla alia redargutione, quam propria ipsius temeritate et scurrilitate opus habeat, prout Eruditi et cordati omnes fatentur, uti colliges ex postscriptis quibusdam ad THADDAeUM, quid hac de re mihi Helmstadio significatum sit; ubi etiam appositum est, quid Mathematicus Wittebergensis Rostochium ad CHRISTIERNUM SEVERINI, qui mecum per aliquot annos fuit, perscripserit. Habes quoque exemplum literarum ROLLENHAGIJ, quas his ipsis diebus accepi, qui fusius de his agit et librum istum scurrilem unâ misit, quem ERICO LANGIO destinavi, siquidem author eum semel atque iterum isthic pro testimonio in re falsissima alleget. Verum iste calumniator alia ratione per oportunitatem castigandus est; ubi superfluum habuero exemplar, tibi mittam; aut obtinebis forte aliunde. Nam in Daniam famosum illum librum iam antea venisse intellexi. Habebunt in hoc mei osores, quibus se titillent; multaque proprijs commentis non dissimilia ibidem reperient; licet non ita κατ’ ἐξοχήν quidvis fingere ausi sint. Sed valeant ista.</p><p>Venit heri in manus meas liber Ephemeridum cuiusdam MARTINI EVERHARTI (ut et hoc obiter intermisceam) quas ex novis suis tabulis Belgicis se supputasse in frontispicio asserit, atque in epistola dedicatoria [ad Regem nostrum] multa de ijsdem pollicetur. Et recte quidem ad apparentias Coelestes appellat, modo pro ipso sententiam ferre vellent, cuius tamen contrarium deprehenditur, adeo ut ne fixarum quidem loca nedum Erraticarum hinc inquirenda ipsi Coelo consona protulerit. In fixis enim stellis retinuit ubique easdem longitudines et latitudines, quae ex calculo Prutenico derivantur. Quae si rite se habuissent, quantis laboribus et quot millibus Ioachimicorum parcere potuissem? In Planetis motus eorum ex utroque calculo nimirum Alphonsino et Coperniceo simul mixto, et huic quam illi pro arbitrio propius accedendo, quodlibetice, quo imperitis fucum faceret, consarcinavit, maiori sane compendio, quam si ex ipso Coelo (prout ait) omnia deduxisset, an vero pari certitudine, non dixerim. Arbiter ille, quem pro se allegavit, constans est, nec in ullius gratiam, aut ex affectu vel inscitia quidpiam facit: adeo ut difficilem teneat tali sub iudice caussam. Si ad terrestres rem detulisset, minori id foret periculo; tunc enim similia similibus congruerent atque placerent. Cupio ex te scire, quid remunerationis loco [a Rege nostro] acceperit, et an fides illi habita, eum in recessu praestare, quod primo accessu pollicetur. Non crediderim, eius oblationem liberalius exceptam, quam Iureconsultorum et aliorum. Verum hoc extra propositum, nec de istis mentionem ullam fecissem, nisi ex te etiam scire animus foret, quomodo nostrates ex ijs, qui mei odio laborant, quosque satis nosti, de utroque scripto pronuncient. Neque enim dubito ursinum et ferinum istud ijs multum solatij additurum, quo malevolentiam propriam aliena probent, mendacia mendacijs, iniurias iniurijs. Atqui haec demonstratio talibus sufficiat, qui veriora nec amant, nec cupiunt, nec tolerant. Ab Everhartianis Ephemeridibus solaque earum inscriptione et praefatione inducere possunt ea, quae ego tot annis molitus sum, et necdum absolvi, ab alio quodam iam peracta esse et Regi dedicata; ut tanto apparatu Astronomico, tantisque impensis, prout ego feci, non fuerit opus; Tabulas iam ab alio esse confectas, quae diu frustra a me expectabantur; minori negotio iam omnia peracta esse, ita ut totus meus frustretur labor. Haec et sexcenta alia eiuscemodi pro iudicij libertate seu potius levitate, procul dubio comminiscentur, et facilis est assensus. Quis enim Coelo inconsulto haec dijudicarit? Coelum vero consulere quotusquisque rite novit? Sed suo tempore patebit, quam parum aut potius nihil ab EVERHARTO isto praestitum sit, motusque siderum potius confusione quadam utriusque calculi sic intricatos quam extricatos esse, adeo ut nullo certo, sed saltem opinabili et pro authoris arbitrio utcunque assumpto nitantur fundamento; quod potius est Astronomiam depravare quam instaurare, destruere quam aedificare. Tuum erit nostrorum hominum, praesertim eorum, quos nosti, acre (si Dijs placet) iudicium de utroque libro, quatenus clam expiscari licuit, mihi tacite innuere.</p><p>Exemplum literarum tuarum, quod requiris, mitto, originale autem reservo. Quidquid in hoc mutaveris, idem fiet in altero, nec quidpiam tuorum edam, nisi consenseris. Quod [a GERHARDO RANZOVIO pecuniam nanciscij nequiveris, facile credo. Servant enim [Holsati ea elocanda] stata tempora. A mercatore illo Hamburgense [nihil impetrare licuit] uti [† nerue] nec Flensburgense, unde incommoditatibus quibusdam aliquandiu obnoxius fui. Nemini potius quam tibi multis de causis aedes illas meas Haffniae extructas concederem. Et de pretio facile inter nos conveniret, si prius nimium tibi videretur. Da adhuc operam, ut eas prae alijs possideas. Invenies (uti existimo) isthic pro aliqua saltem pecuniae parte conquirenda rationes non admodum difficiles aut tibi (quod nollem) damnosas. Si minus, effice, ut alium emptorem habeam, quem censueris non incompetentem. De libris tuis apud D. BOeKELIUM residuis aliquoties diligenter egi, sed is multa quaerit subterfugia. Minatus sum, me rem ad iudices delaturum, et ne sic quidem profeci. Neque per [WINSHEMIUM] tuum alias, uti haberi vult, Amicum quidpiam efficere potui, uti ex schedis quibusdam, quas conquisitas adiungam, ipsemet colliges. Solam VESALIJ Anatomiam unâ cum PERERIJ in Genesin Tomo primo nactus sum, quorum hic libellus est in 8vo. Utrumque mitto. Nam licet VESALIUM, ut mihi reservarem, liberum concesseris, nolui tamen id facere, nisi aequivalens quid ex mea reciperes bibliotheca. Sed nescio, quidnam in ea sit, quod tibi arrideat. Praeterieram fere ea, quae de commentarijs tibi notis ais, te per oportunitatem in ijs laborare, ut propediem obtineam unâ cum responsione ad [Germaniam,] in quorum utroque rem acceptam praestiteris, tibique res meas curae esse, eo plus probaveris. Mitto autem nunc carmen illud Germaniae ad Daniam. Si quid in hoc critizare volueris, tibi liberum erit. Responde luculenter, prout commodum fuerit, et replica nonnulla in Germaniam acute. Patriam quoque et Regem excusa aliosque integros et cordatos viros, praeteritis ijs vel potius tacite notatis, qui culpa non vacant; et in fine me Germaniae Dania commendet, siquidem ita evenit, ut me carere debuerit. Sed nihil hîc tibi praescribo. Per te ipsum facile invenies responsionem. Materia est locuples et tibi non ignota. Praestiterit autem mea Sententia, carmen esse Elegiacum, et rationes addere ab initio, quare non Heroico et pomposo versu rescribas. Sed audi quidpiam aliud, sive fortuitum, sive quod sic fieri debuit; cum [Rex Haderslebiae RANZOVIJ] hospitium ingressus, forte fortuna in eius conclave venisset, illo absente, in mensa calendarium [Ranzovianum] reperiens, cui Elegia nostra ad Daniam inserta erat, quam [RANZOVIO] miseram, conspecto [Rex] titulo et mei nominis subscriptione (uti existimo) avide Elegiam illam perlegit, adeo ut circiter horam in ea perspicienda absumpserit, statimque seposito Libro tacitus discessit. Quod ubi [RANZOVIUS] a ministris percepit, valde succensuit pueris, quod librum illum non amovissent, illi autem per ignorantiam se excusarunt. Sed [Rex] nullum verbum de hoc ad [RANZOVIUM] retulit, neque mei ullam fecit mentionem toto illo tempore dum Haderslebiae esset, uti neque Bredenbergae cum in vicinia a RANZOVIO splendide exciperetur; quod consulto factum esse existimamus. Ego vero ubi rescivi, [Regem] Elegiam illam tam studiose evolvisse, mihi volupe erat ita factum. Utinam saltem omnia in ea ad unguem intellexisset. Et optassem, ut carmen SOMMERI, qui hîc nobiscum aderat, unâ adiunctum fuisset, quod is domum reversus mihi misit, in quo manes regis FRIDERICI Danos alloquentes deque meo abitu cum ijs quasi expostulantes introducit, cuius etiam hîc exemplum adiungo, et tuae censurae subijcio. Si et illi sive per te sive alium quempiam respondere placuerit, liberum erit. Videtur is, nescio quo furore Poëtico percitus, veritatem in multis libere ebuccinare. Et pro tali authore Carmen non est inelegans. Subiunxi insuper denuo nostram Elegiam ad Patriam, ut habeas correctius quam antea exemplum. Est enim distichon quoddam additum, quod prius aliqua incuria omissum erat, cum a [Rege] tam diligenter perlustratum praeter morem in talibus, et sic eiusdem silentio quasi authenticum redditum sit. Astronomia nostra Mechanica iam typis absoluta est, cuius exemplar dono mitto secreto habendum, et nulli communicandum, nisi forte SOPHIAe nostrae utrique, si placuerit, tacite ostendendum. Addita sunt paucula quaedam ad tuum carmen, ut compleretur pagina, sententia tamen manente ut prius. Videbis in fine RUDOLPHI illius, qui me hîc invisere solet, odam Horatianam, quae, nisi animus me fallit, tibi non displicebit. Aemulatur Poëtam illum Wittebergensem F. TAUBMANNUM, qui etiam quaedam de meo abitu nunc concinnare dicitur, unâ cum plaerisque alijs hinc inde Poëtis, quae ubi nactus fuero, tibi impertiar. Habebis etiam cum hoc eodem nuncio Stellarum octavae sphaerae restitutionem, etiam Caesari dedicatam, sed saltem in membrana manuscriptam. Quin et tertium quendam eiusdem quantitatis et formae librum ad Caesarem adorno, qui ☉ et ☾ motus velut in Ephemeride ad aliquot sequentes annos, quotquot mature absolvere licuerit, continebit, apposita ad singulos dies differentia utriusque calculi, Alphonsini et Copernicei, quae subinde maior est quam putatur, etiam additis quibusdam alijs, huc proprie et utiliter facientibus, quo habeat Imperator Specimen quoddam in ambobus luminaribus nostrae instaurationis Astronomicae, donec ea, quae pariter in quinque Planetis molior, penitus absolvantur, qua in parte nunc strenue laboro; et compendia quaedam adinveni, quibus id commode exequi liceat, etiam antequam Tabulae nostrae huc facientes prorsus consummatae fuerint. Nam ea, quae principaliter huc conducunt, in promptu habeo. Ablegavi autem hisce diebus FRANCISCUM TEGNAGLIUM nostrum ad Archiepiscopum Coloniensem cum exemplaribus duobus librorum iam paratorum. Deditque unâ RANZOVIUS ad eum meo nomine diligenter literas, meis quas hîc vidisti adiunctas. Intentio est, ut impetrem a Coloniensi literas ad Caesaream Matem, quibus me meaque studia ei serio commendet, illisque adiutus eo confidentius Caesari postea mea offeram, atque ea, quae desidero, proponam. Interim Pragam ad THADDAeUM singularem meum amicum scripsi, et eum de proposito meo secreto certiorem reddidi, ut omnia tacite exquirat, quid in his consultum fuerit, meque de eo admoneat; spero me propediem responsum obtenturum. De negotio apud Illustrissimum Administratorem, qui nunc est Septemvir Imperij, sic habe: cum filius meus TYCHO Berlini diu substitisset, quod ob plurima irrepentia in ea Electoratus mutatione obstacula essent in Aula alias occupatissimi, post nonam tandem hebdomadem reversus unâ cum IOHANNE MÜLLERO Mathematico Principis et liberorum eius instructore, literas quidem tam ab Electore quam Electorissa attulerunt, et Regi nostro Sermo et Reginae inscriptas, tum quoque aulae Magistro et Cancellario, ac ad me etiam seorsim dederunt literas tam is quam illa unâque eorum Cancellarius. At ego cum omnes perlustrassem, et ex copijs earum, quae obsignatae erant, contenta perspexissem, vidi nimis aulico more compositas esse, parumque ad rem facere. Fundantur praecipue literis illis quas Bramstedij Principi praesens obtuli, quae obiter in ea festinatione (siquidem summo mane Princeps discedere voluit) exaratae erant. At Instructio, quam postea MÜLLERO tradidi, alia quaedam et particulariora continebat, quorum minima pars in literis Electoris attingitur. Incertus itaque sum, an literas hasce non magni ponderis ad Regem et Reginam mittere velim, cum mihi usui isthic futuras vix putem. Si Megapolitanae tam diligenter scriptae nihil profuerunt, adeo ut ne responsionem quidem promereri potuerint, multo minus hae. Sed mitto tibi earundem Exemplaria, ut tu ea secreto perlegas, et nulli nisi ESCILLO nostro aperiens deliberare et mihi significare possis, an in Comitijs proximis exhibendae sint eae literae vel secus? Tum quoque an Regi duo illi libri de Astronomia Mechanica et Stellis cum meis ad ipsum literis offerendi, et an quidpiam ad Senatum Regni scribere conducat, de restituendis mihi impensis, siquidem ea, quae molitus sum, ex Regis FRIDERICI et eorum consensu ac comprobatione iuxta Senatusconsulti tenorem et pollicitationem conservari nequeant; et de instrumentis meis isthic residuis in Germaniam transvehendis, cum in Dania otiosa sint: ac quibusdam alijs ad res nostras peculiariter facientibus. Quod si tale quid tentandum censueris, de ijs, quae principaliter attingenda, captato etiam cum alijs, quos fideles nosti, praesertim ESCILLO nostro, consilio, me mature admone; et simul commentaria in utranque Epistolam (uti nosti) mitte. In responsione ad Germaniam non opus est festinatione. Fiat id per oportunitatem et otium. [Genealogias et Geneses Aulae Magistri et Cancellarij,] tum quoque res gestas, prout inter nos facta est mentio, tacite inquire, mecumque communica. Caetera habes in scheda memoriali tibi tradita, nec quidpiam haesito, quin operam daturus sis, ut nihil in ijs praetereatur. Plura itaque nunc non addam, cum forte in his nimius fuerim, teque plus satis (quod amicitiae nostrae tribuas) molestarim. Unum saltem addam, quod te tuamque terrigenam SOPHIAM concernit. Scripsi mox a tuo hinc discessu AXILLO fratri, eique hortator fui, ut permitteret te ex arbitrio Nuptias tuas disponere atque celebrare, et rationes addidi, cur id suaderem. At si vera sunt, quae nuper hîc a cive quodam Malmogiensi inaudivi, parum profeci hoc meo consilio. Aiebat enim ille, quod AXILLUS frater trium filiarum e suis Nuptias uno eodemque tempore Malmogij circa medium Augusti celebrare decreverit, quare et tuae inter eas erunt. Si id tibi non satis placuerit, aequa mente ferendum est, quod aliter fieri nequit. Scis Damasellas hîc fata regere, neque ullis rationibus moveri posse. Verum ego hoc saltem ex indicatione illius civis scribo nihil certi alias habens. Tu vero, quid in his futurum nosti, indica, atque per circumstantias explica, Deique providentiae (uti facis) omnia committe. Is tibi et tuae omnia fausta ex animi sententia largiatur. Vale et resaluta ex me Matrem tuam officiosissime, tum quoque fratrem AXILLUM unâ cum coniuge et liberis, non praeterita, quam nosti, SOPHIA, in quo nomine acquiescere lubet. Dabantur subito Wandesburgo die 8vo Maij Anni 98.</p><p>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>Feci superius mentionem libri de Mechanica Astronomia, quod eum tibi mitterem, verum postea consultius duxi exemplar illud, quod Regi nostro destinavi, unâ cum altero libro fixarum restitutionem continente, ad te dirigere, ut utrunque in hisce Comitijs (modo tibi visum fuerit) ipsi meo nomine submisse offeras, et siquidem talibus non delectetur, uti a te et alijs cognovi, tibi facile expetenti ambos concedet. Da itaque operam, ut obtineas, et ne veniat in manus ac potestatem aliorum, mihi forte minus faventium. Quod si Rex te ijs non donarit, habebis a me postea alia exemplaria. Monstra haec saltem sorori SOPHIAe et KRAGIO nostro et fratribus meis, si qui ex ijs talia curant. Attende vero ad ea, quae infra Insulae Huennae Topographiam consignata sunt iuxta calcem, Saa skal I see hor høfflig ieg haver ganget om der med, for Sandhed lader sig icke siige: sed forte malevoli et haec sinistre interpretabuntur more solito.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.</p><p>LITERAS alias, quas scis me ad varias Principes Germaniae scripsisse, non duxi consultum ijs mittere, antequam de Caesaris voluntate, postquam intercessorias Coloniensis et meas ipsi cum libris destinatas acceperit, quidpiam certi compertum habeam, ne in pluribus locis negotium hoc tentatum difficilius forte fiat, et pluribus anfractibus involutum reddatur iuxta tritum illud: qui Lepores binos etc. Interea tamen iussi, ut FRANCISCUS noster cum Comite MAURITIO in transitu per illa loca meo nomine secreto loquatur, ut intelligere queam, quid spei sit apud Batavos, si Caesar et Germania oblata non acceptarint, dedique hac de re ad MAURITIUM literas, ita tamen ut nihil absolute statuerem, sed FRANCISCO omnia hypothetice saltem proponenda et expiscanda concredidi, obtuli etiam MAURITIO duo librorum exemplaria, hac tamen cautela, ut eas tacite sibi soli reservaret, donec Caesari, cui dedicantur, exhibiti essent. Habet etiam alias FRANCISCUS secum ex operibus meis singula quaedam Exemplaria, quae illustribus et praecipuis viris, prout oportunum fuerit, monstrare poterit, quo videant rem serio agi. Iamque iterum vale, et Doctorem NICOLAUM KRAGIUM nostrum, ad vos procul dubio reversum, de ijs quae hisce literis scripsi, certiorem redde, quatenus eum scire conveniat, et carmina atque literas, ut singula per otium perlegat, cum ipso clam communica, et illius quoque de ijs, quae perscripsi, consilium exquire. De ipso enim nihil addubito, quin rebus nostris ex animo bene cupiat.</p><p>Postquam haec exarata erant, accepi CRAMERI tui literas et binas ad te scriptas, quarum alterae a me lectae sunt, prout ille in inscriptione concessit. Habes hîc utrasque; cuperem tamen Apologeticas illas mihi remitti, sed prius monstrentur Doctori NICOLAO, ut videat quam ingenue CRAMERUS veritatem contra Scotum illum profiteatur. Addidi et eas, quas mihi scripsit, licet id vetuerit, ut videas ex ipsiusmet verbis, quid velit me suo nomine apud te agere, quod tuo relinquo arbitrio, qui optime nosti, quid hac in re conveniat vel secus.</p><p>Non fuit mihi otium literas hasce relegendi, sed tu pro tua industria sententiam facile percipies, si qua in parte inter scribendum aberratum est, aut forte praetermissum.</p><p>T. T. B.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 68; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA ALIA. POSTQUAM has ad te absolvissem literas, accepi Praga Responsum a THADDAeO nostro ad ea, quae illi scripseram, quae hic legisti, quod satis bene se habet pro hoc primo initio. Exemplum Epistolae illius inclusi ijs literis, quas Doctori NICOLAO KRAGIO, communi nostro amico, scripsi; is te eius faciat participem. Tuum erit unâ cum ESCILLO BILLEO consilium capere, quid vobis videatur ex re mea esse hic facere vel omittere; libenter enim amicorum, qui integri sunt, consiliis ausculto. Poteris etiam, si tibi tutum videbitur, AXILLO GYLDENSTIERN haec ipsa clam aperire, nam et is, uti existimo, mihi bene cupit. De fratribus meis vix habeo quod dicam, videntur enim illi me meaque obiter curare et ne curare quidem, quod nimis multis comperi indicijs, licet id ipsum patienter feram atque me intelligere dissimulem. Ego sane aliter pro ijs laborassem, si ea alicui eorum evenissent, quae mihi. [Danica lingua:] Mens dett er nu saa viit kommen y verden, att den giør en møgitt gott, som giør en intedt ont. Gid jeg kunde kun nyde dett derpaa. Sed quo degredior? quo me iustus trahit dolor? nec tamen doleo, siquidem ita seculum hoc ferat; miror saltem, nihil ferme integri et prisci moris retinere BRAHEOS meos, qui longo temporum tractu constantiae et mutuae fidei nomen promeruerunt. Sic mutantur et transplantantur omnia. Iterum et saepius, diuque multumque cum tua tuisque vale. Raptim Wandesburgo die 28. Maij Anni 98. TYCHO Brahe.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble man, excellent in virtue and in humane letters, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz, Lord of Rosenholm, his kinsman, kinsman-by-marriage, and most upright and most longed-for friend.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>The letter you sent me from Rosenholm at the beginning of March, noble Holger, most longed-for kinsman and kinsman-by-marriage, I duly received about the 24th of the same, and it was by far most welcome to me; to its contents, where needful, I shall now reply briefly, meaning afterwards to add certain other things. <b>Erik Sparre</b> (whom you, in haste, call \"Brahe\") did not pass this way, but turned aside by another route to Rostock, where, having spoken with Doctor Sturtz, he made grand promises of what he would do for me with his King in my name, once the Swedish affairs were chiefly settled. But to such political and polished words I lend just as much credit as you do. Of the <b>Kolding assembly</b> nothing certain is yet established for us; some suppose a war was intended against the Hamburgers, some against the Swedes. The outcome proves the deeds. Our <b>Rantzau</b> was indeed victor in his case at Haderslev, and that deservedly by right; but he could not obtain sentence against his adversary together with execution, however hostile the King was to him. For as soon as the man, perceiving himself in peril, [approached the Chancellor, he overcame the injuries done not only to Rantzau] but also [to the King himself, by holding out a hand perhaps not empty]. As for [<b>Pepler</b>], that you have put some to shame — if indeed they have any — was rightly done by you; but nothing is so unjust that, if it opposes me in any way, it is not [reckoned] just. I, however, am not moved by it; I only marvel at certain men's unbridled malice and too-broad conscience, by which they harm themselves more than me. The <b>General Judge of Bergen</b>, who with his assessors delivered the sentence, will easily defend it, if law finds a place; if not, let these things pass with the rest. I will not involve myself in them, unless it be quite necessary. Meanwhile, what you have received from me, share with [<b>Laumann</b>], so that he may have whatever he shall seek from them by way of contrary proof; or else [<b>my Praefect</b>], first prompted by [<b>Eske Bille and you</b>], will put them forward. I shall add more still, if I find anything serving this purpose. The judge will himself bring the written sentence, and will not with difficulty meet the several objections. I wrote some things to him about this matter, but have as yet received no reply. I sent a citation along, which, however, he perhaps obtained earlier. Nothing about it is known to me [except from you]. Whether it was read [at Uraniborg], or is still to be read, I do not know. But whatever it be, it is all one; nor am I bound, according to the Laws (if only they had weight), to heed an illegitimate and non-annual citation, even were the matter of greater moment. <i>This is just as good as all the rest; when they are tired of this, they can think up some new device.</i> I, meanwhile, think of other things. That you spoke so fully with [the Chancellor's wife] about me and my affairs, was rightly and heartily done by you. I should wish to know yet more particulars, and that you would, as occasion offers (provided there is a truce among you), inculcate those very things again, with commentaries and amplifications added. Those [<b>Machiavellists</b>] suppose their machinations so lie hidden that no one in [Denmark] but the authors understands them. Meanwhile foreign nations talk and write of these things — as you will learn even from this letter sent me after your departure, how nearly they touched the matter itself in England. I have added the original itself, to be sent back to me when you have copied it, that it may the more surely be proved that I have changed nothing in it. <b>Doctor Janus Mule</b>, who was lately here with me, knew in Italy — before he was promoted — the Doctor who wrote those things, and from other letters of his sent to him recognized (as he told me) his handwriting. He will tell you more if you inquire secretly. For I allowed him too to copy it for himself, so that it might the more easily become known to the [Machiavellists], and that they might thence perceive how cautiously and prudently they have provided for themselves. What the learned everywhere in Germany and elsewhere think of my emigration hence, it would be long to relate, nor perhaps honourable enough to our countrymen. You have here, along [with the rest], a recent letter to me from <b>Monau</b> of Breslau alone, which I desire returned to me, by which you will perceive how greatly he exaggerates this case — so much so that in one place he speaks too hyperbolically. <i>It was enough that he was a Jute.</i> I always make a habit of excusing our most Serene King, and the other sensible men in the fatherland. The rest are infamous enough, both within and without the Kingdom, that they need no marker from me. I exempted both the King and all upright men from the things that befell me, in my earlier [letters] to Monau. Hence it is that he says I do rightly to distinguish the actor from the Poet of the play. That you brought nothing before [the aforesaid lady] about the administrator, was advisedly done. As to what you write of <b>Ulfeldt</b> — that he asserted as lies everything I had tacitly signified to you — I indeed easily believe it to be so, since I never held them for truths, and since I know many other things graver than these to have been fabricated.</p><p>To <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> I wrote about the horses, as was indicated by you — yet that I do not want them before the feast of Pentecost. I had already understood that victuals are both dear in Denmark, and that their export is forbidden. Yet I do not know by what right an interdiction can stand in the way of a man sprung from a Noble family there, who would use them as a support for his own household economy, and not exercise gain or trade about them — especially if this business be procured through another nobleman, and not through merchants. But since I can do without greater things in the fatherland, why not these too? I shall see to it that it be effected from elsewhere, from neighbouring places, with smaller (as I hope) difficulties. Meanwhile your forward goodwill is most welcome to me. That <b>Baron</b> of whom mention was made between us, I have understood went off into Austria, nor is it known whether he has returned. Concerning that <b>slanderous writing</b>, on whose account I wished to send him a letter, I am not anxious; for its author so surpassed the bounds of modesty and truth in it, by such impudence, that it needs no other refutation than its own rashness and scurrility, as all the learned and sensible admit — as you will gather from certain postscripts to <b>Thaddaeus</b>, [namely] what was signified to me about this from Helmstedt; where it is also appended what the Wittenberg Mathematician wrote to Rostock, to <b>Christiern Severin</b>, who was with me for some years. You also have a copy of the letter of <b>Rollenhagen</b>, which I received these very days, who treats of these things more fully and sent that scurrilous book along — which I destined for <b>Erik Lange</b>, since the author alleges him, once and again, as a witness in a most false matter. But that calumniator must be chastised in another way, as opportunity serves; when I have a spare copy, I shall send it to you, or you will perhaps obtain it elsewhere — for I have understood that the famous book has already come to Denmark. In it my haters will have wherewith to tickle themselves, and will find there much not unlike their own fabrications, though they have not dared to invent anything so <i>par excellence</i>. But let these things fare well.</p><p>There came yesterday into my hands a book of Ephemerides by one <b>Martin Everart</b> (that I may interweave this too in passing), which he asserts on the frontispiece to have computed from his new Belgian tables, and in the dedicatory epistle [to our King] promises much of them. And he rightly appeals to the celestial appearances — provided they would give sentence for him; whereof, however, the contrary is found, so much so that he has not even produced the places of the fixed [stars], let alone of the wandering [planets], to be sought from this [work], consonant with heaven itself. For in the fixed stars he everywhere retained the same longitudes and latitudes that are derived from the Prutenic calculation. Had these been right, what labours, and how many thousands of joachims, could I have spared! In the Planets, he patched together their motions from both calculations mixed — namely the Alphonsine and the Copernican — approaching now this, now that, at his pleasure, \"quodlibetically,\" that he might cast a mist before the unskilled, with far greater brevity indeed than if he had derived everything (as he says) from heaven itself; but whether with equal certainty, I would not say. That arbiter [heaven], which he alleged for himself, is constant, and does nothing for anyone's favour, or out of partiality or ignorance — so that he holds a difficult case under such a judge. Had he referred the matter to earthly [judges], it would have been at less peril; for then like would have agreed with, and pleased, like. I desire to know from you what he received by way of remuneration [from our King], and whether he is trusted to perform in the upshot what he promises at the first approach. I should not believe his offering was received more liberally than [those] of the jurists and others. But this is beside the point, nor would I have made any mention of these things, were it not that I have a mind to know from you too how our countrymen — those who labour under hatred of me, whom you know well enough — pronounce on both writings. For I do not doubt that that bear-like and brutish thing will add them much solace, whereby they may prove their own malevolence by another's, lies by lies, injuries by injuries. But let this demonstration suffice for such men, who neither love, nor desire, nor tolerate truer things. From Everart's Ephemerides, and their very title and preface alone, they can infer that what I have laboured at for so many years, and not yet finished, has already been accomplished by some other and dedicated to the King; that there was no need of so great an astronomical apparatus, and such great expenses, as I have made; that the Tables, long awaited from me in vain, have already been made by another; that everything has now been accomplished with less ado, so that my whole labour is frustrated. These, and six hundred other such things, they will doubtless invent, in the freedom — or rather levity — of their judgment; and assent comes easily. For who would judge these things without consulting heaven? And how few rightly know how to consult heaven! But in its own time it will appear how little, or rather nothing, has been performed by that Everart, and that the motions of the stars have rather been entangled than disentangled by a certain confusion of both calculations, so that they rest on no certain foundation, but only on an opinable one, assumed however the author pleased — which is rather to corrupt astronomy than to restore it, to destroy than to build. It will be for you secretly to hint to me the sharp (if it please the gods) judgment of our men — especially those you know — on both books, so far as it has been possible to fish it out in private.</p><p>A copy of your letter, which you ask for, I send; the original, however, I keep. Whatever you change in this, the same shall be done in the other; nor will I publish anything of yours, unless you consent. That you could not obtain money [from Gerhard Rantzau], I easily believe; for the [Holsteiners keep] fixed times [for letting those things]. From that Hamburg merchant [nothing could be obtained], as [†] nor [from] the Flensburg one, whence for a while I was subject to certain inconveniences. To no one rather than to you would I, for many reasons, grant those houses of mine built at Copenhagen. And about the price we should easily agree, if it seemed too much to you beforehand. Take pains still, that you may possess them before others. You will find there (as I think) ways, not too difficult or (which I should not wish) damaging to you, for raising at least some part of the money. If not, arrange that I have another buyer, whom you shall judge not incompetent. About your books remaining with Dr. <b>Boekel</b>, I have dealt diligently several times, but he seeks many subterfuges. I threatened that I would bring the matter before the judges, and not even thus did I get anywhere. Nor through your <b>Winsheim</b> — otherwise, as he wishes to be held, a Friend — could I accomplish anything, as you will yourself gather from certain notes, which, when collected, I shall add. I have got only <b>Vesalius's</b> <i>Anatomy</i>, together with <b>Pererius</b> <i>on Genesis</i>, the first volume, of which this is a little book in octavo. I send both. For although you freely granted that I keep the Vesalius for myself, I was unwilling to do it, unless you received something equivalent from my library. But I do not know what there might be in it that would please you. I had almost passed over what you say about the commentaries known to you — that you are working on them as occasion offers, so that I may shortly obtain them, together with the response to [<b>Germany</b>] — in both of which you will have done a welcome thing, and the more shown that my affairs are your care. I now send that <b>poem of Germany to Denmark</b>. If you wish to criticize anything in it, you shall be free. Reply clearly, as shall be convenient, and rejoin a few things to Germany acutely. Excuse also the fatherland and the King, and the other upright and sensible men, passing over — or rather tacitly marking — those who are not free of fault; and at the end let Denmark commend me to Germany, since it has so fallen out that it had to lose me. But I prescribe nothing to you here. By yourself you will easily find the response. The material is rich and not unknown to you. It will be better, in my opinion, that the poem be Elegiac, and that you add reasons at the beginning why you do not reply in Heroic and pompous verse. But hear something else, whether by chance, or as it had to happen so: when [the <b>King</b>, at Haderslev,] having entered Rantzau's lodging, came by chance into his chamber, the latter being absent, and finding on the table [Rantzau's] calendar, into which our Elegy to Denmark had been inserted — which I had sent [to Rantzau] — [the King], having caught sight of the title and the subscription of my name (as I suppose), read that Elegy avidly, so much so that he spent about an hour in perusing it, and at once, laying the book aside, departed in silence. When [Rantzau] learned this from his servants, he was very angry with the boys for not having removed that book; but they excused themselves by ignorance. But [the King] said not a word of this to [Rantzau], nor made any mention of me the whole time he was at Haderslev — nor at Bredenberg, when he was splendidly received nearby by Rantzau — which we suppose was done of set purpose. But I, when I learned that [the King] had so studiously turned over that Elegy, was pleased that it so happened. Would that he had at least understood everything in it to the fingernail. And I could have wished that the poem of <b>Sommer</b> — who was here with us — had been attached along [with it], which he, having returned home, sent me, in which he introduces the shade of King Frederik addressing the Danes and, as it were, expostulating with them about my departure; a copy of which I also add here, and submit to your judgment. If it please [you] to reply to it too, whether through yourself or some other, you shall be free. He seems, struck by some poetic fury, to trumpet the truth freely in many things; and for such an author the poem is not inelegant. I have moreover subjoined once more our Elegy to the Fatherland, that you may have a more correct copy than before; for a certain distich has been added, which earlier had by some carelessness been omitted — since it was so diligently perused by [the King], beyond custom in such matters, and thus, by his silence, rendered as it were authentic. Our <b>Mechanical Astronomy</b> is now finished in print, of which I send a copy as a gift, to be kept secret, and communicated to no one — unless perhaps to be shown tacitly to <b>both our Sophies</b>, if it please. A few little things have been added to your poem, so that the page might be filled out, the sense, however, remaining as before. You will see at the end the Horatian ode of that <b>Rudolf</b> who is wont to visit me here, which, unless my mind deceives me, will not displease you. He emulates that Wittenberg poet <b>F. Taubmann</b>, who too is now said to be composing something about my departure, together with very many other poets here and there, which, when I have got them, I shall share with you. You will also have, with this same messenger, the <b>Restitution of the Stars of the eighth sphere</b>, also dedicated to the Emperor, but only written by hand on parchment. Nay, I am also preparing a certain third book, of the same size and form, for the Emperor, which will contain the motions of the <b>Sun</b> and <b>Moon</b> as in an Ephemeris for some following years — as many as I shall be able to finish in good time — with the difference of both calculations, the Alphonsine and the Copernican, appended for each day (which is sometimes greater than is thought), and certain other things added, properly and usefully serving this purpose; so that the Emperor may have a certain specimen, in both luminaries, of our astronomical restoration, until those things which I am likewise labouring at in the five Planets be wholly finished — in which part I now strenuously labour; and I have found certain shortcuts by which it may conveniently be carried out, even before our Tables serving this purpose are entirely completed. For the things that principally conduce to this, I have ready to hand. I have, moreover, these days sent off our <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> to the Archbishop of Cologne with two copies of the books now ready. And Rantzau gave him, along [with them], a diligent letter to him in my name, with mine, which you saw here, attached. The intention is that I obtain from the Cologne [Elector] letters to His Imperial Majesty, by which he may seriously commend me and my studies to him; and, aided by these, that I may afterwards the more confidently offer my [work] to the Emperor, and propose what I desire. Meanwhile I have written to Prague, to <b>Thaddaeus</b>, my singular friend, and informed him secretly of my purpose, that he may quietly inquire what is advisable in these matters, and advise me of it; I hope shortly to obtain a reply. Concerning the business with the most illustrious <b>Administrator</b>, who is now an Elector [Septemvir] of the Empire, take it thus: when my son <b>Tycho</b> had stayed long at Berlin — because, on account of the many obstacles creeping in at that change of the Electorate, [those] in the court were otherwise most busy — returning at last after the ninth week, together with <b>Johann Müller</b>, the Prince's Mathematician and instructor of his children, they brought letters both from the Elector and the Electress, addressed to our most Serene King and Queen, and also to the Court Master and Chancellor; and to me too they separately gave letters, both he and she, and their Chancellor along [with them]. But I, when I had perused them all, and had seen the contents from copies of those which were sealed, saw that they were composed too much in courtly fashion, and made little to the point. They are founded chiefly on those letters which I presented to the Prince in person at Bramstedt, which were written hastily in that haste (since the Prince wished to depart at early dawn). But the Instruction which I afterwards handed to Müller contained certain other and more particular things, the least part of which is touched in the Elector's letters. I am therefore uncertain whether I should wish to send these letters of no great weight to the King and Queen, since I scarcely think they will be of use there. If the Mecklenburg ones, so diligently written, did no good — so much so that they could not even merit a reply — much less will these. But I send you copies of them, that you may read them secretly, and, opening them to no one but our <b>Eske</b>, may deliberate and signify to me whether those letters are to be presented at the next assembly, or otherwise; and likewise whether those two books, on the Mechanics of Astronomy and on the Stars, are to be offered to the King with my letters to him; and whether it conduces [to anything] to write something to the Senate of the Realm about restoring my expenses to me — since the things I have laboured at cannot be preserved according to the tenor and promise of the Senate-decree, by King Frederik's and their consent and approbation; and about transporting my instruments remaining there into Germany, since in Denmark they are idle; and certain other things particularly serving our affairs. But if you judge that any such thing should be attempted, advise me in good time about the things principally to be touched — taking counsel too with others whom you know faithful, especially our Eske — and at the same time send commentaries on each Letter (as you know how). For the response to Germany there is no need of haste; let it be done at leisure and as occasion offers. The [<b>Genealogies and nativities of the Court Master and Chancellor</b>], and likewise their deeds, as mention was made between us, inquire into quietly, and share with me. The rest you have in the memorial schedule handed to you; nor do I doubt at all that you will take pains that nothing in them be passed over. So I shall add no more now, since I have perhaps been excessive in these things, and have troubled you more than enough (which you will attribute to our friendship). One thing only I shall add, which concerns you and your earth-born <b>Sophia</b>. I wrote, right after your departure hence, to my brother <b>Axel</b>, and exhorted him to permit you to arrange and celebrate your nuptials at your own discretion, and I added reasons why I advised it. But if what I lately heard here from a certain citizen of Malmö is true, I have profited little by this counsel of mine. For he said that my brother Axel had resolved to celebrate the nuptials of three of his daughters at one and the same time at Malmö, about the middle of August, wherefore yours too will be among them. If this does not please you well enough, it must be borne with an even mind, since it cannot be done otherwise. You know that the Damsels here rule the fates, and cannot be moved by any reasons. But I write this only from that citizen's indication, having nothing certain otherwise. Do you, however, indicate what you know will be in these matters, and explain it through the circumstances, and commit everything (as you do) to God's providence. May He bestow on you and yours all happy things according to your heart's wish. Farewell, and greet again on my behalf your Mother most dutifully, and likewise your brother Axel together with his wife and children, not omitting — as you know — <b>Sophia</b>, in which name it pleases [me] to rest. Given hastily at Wandsbek, the 8th [of May], the year [15]98.</p><p>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p><p>I made mention above of the book on the Mechanics of Astronomy, that I would send it to you; but afterwards I thought it more advisable to direct to you that copy which I destined for our King, together with the other book containing the restitution of the fixed [stars], so that you may offer both at this assembly (if it seem good to you) submissively to him in my name; and since he takes no delight in such things, as I have learned from you and others, he will readily grant both to you on your asking. Take pains, then, to obtain them, and that they come not into the hands and power of others, perhaps less favourable to me. But if the King does not present them to you, you will have other copies from me afterwards. Show these, at least, to sister <b>Sophie</b> and to our <b>Krag</b> and to my brothers, if any of them care for such things. But attend to the things noted below the <b>Topography of the island of Hven</b>, near the foot: <i>Then you shall see how courteously I have dealt with it — for the truth cannot be told:</i> but perhaps the malevolent will interpret even these things sinisterly, after their wont.</p><p><b>POSTSCRIPTS.</b></p><p>The other letters, which you know I wrote to various Princes of Germany, I have not thought it advisable to send to them before I have learned something certain of the Emperor's will — after he has received the intercessory [letter] of the Cologne [Elector] and mine destined for him with the books — lest this business, attempted in several places, perhaps become more difficult, and be rendered entangled in many windings, according to that well-worn [saying]: <i>he who [chases] two hares</i> etc. Meanwhile, however, I have ordered that our <b>Frans</b> speak secretly, in my name, with <b>Count Maurice</b>, in passing through those places, that I may understand what hope there is with the Dutch, if the Emperor and Germany do not accept what is offered; and I gave letters on this matter to Maurice — yet so that I determined nothing absolutely, but entrusted everything to Frans, to be proposed and fished out only hypothetically. I also offered Maurice two copies of the books, with this caution, however, that he keep them secretly for himself alone, until they had been presented to the Emperor, to whom they are dedicated. Frans has also with him otherwise certain single copies of my works, which he can show to illustrious and chief men, as shall be opportune, that they may see the matter is being seriously pursued. And now again farewell; and inform our Doctor <b>Niels Krag</b> — doubtless returned to you — of the things I have written in this letter, so far as it is fitting for him to know; and share with him secretly the poems and letters, that he may peruse each at leisure; and seek his counsel too on the things I have written. For of him I doubt not at all that he heartily wishes well to our affairs.</p><p>After these things had been written, I received your <b>Cramer's</b> letters, and two written to you, one of which has been read by me, as he allowed in the inscription. You have both here; yet I should wish those Apologetic ones returned to me, but first let them be shown to Doctor <b>Niels</b>, that he may see how candidly Cramer professes the truth against that <b>Scot</b>. I have added also those he wrote to me — although he forbade it — that you may see from his very own words what he wishes me to do with you in his name, which I leave to your judgment, who know best what is fitting in this matter, or otherwise.</p><p>I had no leisure to reread this letter; but you, by your industry, will easily perceive the sense, if in any part there has been a slip in the writing, or perhaps something passed over.</p><p>Yours wholly, T. B.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> OTHER POSTSCRIPTS. After I had finished this letter to you, I received from Prague a Response from our Thaddaeus to the things I had written to him, which you have read here — which goes well enough for this first beginning. A copy of that Letter I enclosed in the letter which I wrote to Doctor Niels Krag, our common friend; let him make you a sharer in it. It will be for you, together with Eske Bille, to take counsel as to what seems to you, in my interest, should be done or omitted here; for I gladly hearken to the counsels of friends who are upright. You may also, if it seem safe to you, secretly open these very things to Axel Gyldenstierne, for he too, as I think, wishes me well. Of my brothers I scarcely have anything to say, for they seem to care for me and mine only in passing — nay, not even to care — which I have learned by too many indications, though I bear it patiently and dissemble that I understand. I should surely have laboured otherwise on their behalf, had those things befallen any of them which have befallen me. [In Danish:] \"It has now come so far into the world that it does much good to one who does no evil. Would that I could but enjoy what comes of it.\" But whither do I descend? whither does just grief draw me? — and yet I do not grieve, since this age so bears it; I only marvel that my Brahes retain almost nothing of upright and ancient manners, who through a long course of time earned the name of constancy and mutual faith. So are all things changed and transplanted. Again and often, and long and much, fare you well with yours. Hastily, at Wandsbek, the 28th day of May, the year [15]98. Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>); orig. in <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>. MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (a copy). Square brackets in the source are the editor's conjectural readings; a <i>†</i> marks an editorially-flagged corrupt word (<i>nerue</i>).</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"die 8vo Anni 98\"</i> — <b>the month is omitted in the text</b>; the catalogue supplies <b>May</b> (<i>\"8. maj\"</i>). Written from <b>Wandsbek</b> \"hastily\" (<i>subito</i>); Old Style (Tycho). This is the <b>earliest of the Wandsbek-period cluster</b> in this translation set (cf. 10 Aug 1598 to Holger = 15980810001; 20 Aug to Krag = 15980820001), written while the <i>Mechanica</i> was just off the press and the imperial campaign was being launched.</li><li><b>Recipient &amp; the marriage.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> of Rosenholm; the closing greetings to \"your earth-born <b>Sophia</b>\" and \"brother <b>Axel</b>\" with \"<b>Sophia, in which name it pleases to rest</b>\" again work the <i>Sophia</i> = Wisdom/bride pun (cf. 15980810001) and confirm that Holger's bride is <b>Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe</b>, daughter of Tycho's brother <b>Axel Brahe</b> — Tycho's niece (as 15990909001 states outright). Tycho reports interceding with Axel to let Holger arrange his own wedding, but hears Axel means to marry off <b>three daughters at once at Malmö about mid-August 1598</b> (\"you know the Damsels here rule the fates\").</li><li><b>The patronage campaign (launch phase).</b> This letter documents the opening of the imperial bid: <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> dispatched to the <b>Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria) with two finished books and a Rantzau-backed letter, to obtain a commendation to <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Thaddaeus</b> (Tadeáš Hájek) at Prague secretly sounding out the ground; and — as a hedge — <b>Frans</b> to speak \"hypothetically\" with <b>Count Maurice of Nassau</b> about the <b>Dutch</b> option if Emperor and Germany decline (Tycho citing <i>\"he who chases two hares…\"</i> against pressing too many courts at once). The <b>Brandenburg</b> letters (from the new Elector <b>Joachim Friedrich</b> — \"the Administrator, now a Septemvir of the Empire\" — and Electress, brought back by son Tycho with the Brandenburg court mathematician <b>Johann Müller</b> after nine weeks at Berlin) are judged \"too courtly, little to the point,\" and Tycho is unsure whether to present them to Christian IV.</li><li><b>The books.</b> The <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i> is <b>\"now finished in print\"</b> (a firm dating datum: the Wandsbek <i>Mechanica</i>, 1598); also the manuscript <i>\"Restitution of the Stars of the eighth sphere\"</i> (the 1000-star catalogue, on parchment, dedicated to the Emperor) and a planned <b>third book</b> — a specimen Ephemeris of <b>Sun (☉) and Moon (☾)</b> motions with the daily Alphonsine-vs-Copernican difference — pending completion of the five-planet theory. In the postscript Tycho redirects the <b>King's</b> presentation copies (Mechanica + Stars) to Holger, to offer at the Diet and, failing royal interest, to keep.</li><li><b>The Ursus libel &amp; the priority witnesses.</b> The \"slanderous/bear-like writing\" is again <b>Ursus's</b> <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (cf. 15980820001, 15990918001, 15991209001): needing \"no refutation but its own scurrility\"; sent to <b>Erik Lange</b> (whom Ursus falsely cites as witness); <b>Rollenhagen's</b> letter and material from <b>Helmstedt</b> (to <b>Thaddaeus</b>) and from the Wittenberg mathematician (to <b>Christiern Severin</b> at Rostock) enclosed. <b>Christiern Severin = Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>, Tycho's chief assistant (\"who was with me for some years\"). The closing postscript adds <b>Daniel Cramer's</b> apologetic letters \"against that <b>Scot</b>\" = <b>Duncan Liddel</b> — the same Liddel/Cramer plagiarism dossier as 15991209001 / 15990926001 / 15991204001 / 16000324002.</li><li><b>Martin Everart's rival Ephemerides (a notable episode).</b> A whole movement attacks the <b>Ephemerides of Martin Everart</b> (<i>Martinus Everhartus</i>), computed from \"new Belgian tables\" and <b>dedicated to Christian IV</b> — Tycho's fear being that the Danish faction will use its mere existence to argue that \"what Tycho laboured at for years and never finished, another has already done,\" making Tycho's apparatus and expense pointless. Tycho's technical demolition: Everart kept the fixed-star places of the <b>Prutenic</b> (Reinhold's Copernican) tables unchanged, and \"patched\" the planetary motions by mixing Alphonsine and Copernican calculation <i>\"quodlibetice\"</i> to deceive the unskilled — \"corrupting astronomy rather than restoring it.\" He asks Holger to report what reward Everart got from the King, and how the anti-Tycho Danes judge \"both books\" (Everart + Ursus).</li><li><b>The poetic war.</b> Three poems travel with the letter: (1) the <b>\"Germania ad Daniam\"</b> verse (Germany reproaching Denmark for losing Tycho), which Holger is asked to answer in <b>Elegiac</b> (not Heroic) verse, excusing the King and the upright while tacitly marking the guilty; (2) <b>Sommer's</b> poem, introducing the <b>shade of King Frederik II</b> expostulating with the Danes over Tycho's departure; (3) Tycho's own <b>Elegy to the Fatherland</b> (now with a restored distich) — with the vivid anecdote that <b>Christian IV</b>, at <b>Haderslev</b>, chanced on the Elegy in Rantzau's calendar and <b>read it avidly for about an hour, then left in silence</b>, never mentioning Tycho — Rantzau's servants having failed to remove the book. Also a <b>Horatian ode by \"that Rudolf who visits me here\"</b> (a young poet at Wandsbek, emulating the Wittenberg poet <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b>), appended to the printed <i>Mechanica</i>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Erik Sparre</b> (Swedish statesman); <b>Dr. Sturtz</b> (Rostock); <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (<i>Ranzovius</i>; the Haderslev case) and his son <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b> (the horses); the bribed Danish <b>Chancellor</b>; <b>Pepler</b>; the <b>General Judge of Bergen</b>; <b>Laumann</b> and Tycho's <b>Praefect</b> (Hven/estate officials); <b>Eske Bille</b>; <b>Doctor Janus Mule</b> (recognised the handwriting of the English-letter's author); <b>Jacob Monau</b> (<i>Monavius</i>) of Breslau; <b>Ulfeldt</b>; the unnamed <b>Baron</b> (gone to Austria); <b>Thaddaeus</b> (Tadeáš Hájek); <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (<i>Christiern Severin</i>); <b>Rollenhagen</b>; <b>Erik Lange</b>; <b>Martin Everart</b>; <b>King Christian IV</b> and <b>Queen</b> (Anne Catherine); <b>King Frederik II</b> (in Sommer's poem); <b>Sommer</b>; <b>Rudolf</b> (the Wandsbek poet) and <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b>; <b>Vesalius</b> (the <i>Fabrica</i>/<i>Anatomy</i>) and <b>Benedictus Pererius</b> (<i>In Genesim</i>); <b>Dr. Boekel</b> and <b>Winsheim</b> (holders of Holger's books); the <b>Administrator/Elector Joachim Friedrich of Brandenburg</b>, the <b>Electress</b>, their <b>Chancellor</b>, and the court mathematician <b>Johann Müller</b>; <b>Count Maurice of Nassau</b>; the <b>Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria); <b>Axel Brahe</b> (<i>Axillus</i>, Tycho's brother) and his daughter <b>Sophie</b> (Holger's bride); <b>Niels Krag</b>; <b>Daniel Cramer</b> (vs. \"that Scot\" = <b>Duncan Liddel</b>); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Tycho's sister). The \"Court Master and Chancellor\" whose genealogies/nativities Holger is to investigate are the Danish officials Tycho blames for his exile.</li><li><b>Latin, Greek &amp; Danish tags.</b> <i>\"Exitus acta probat\"</i> — <b>Ovid</b>, <i>Heroides</i> 2.85. <i>\"ad unguem\"</i> — to perfection (<b>Horace</b>). <i>\"κατ' ἐξοχήν\"</i> (Greek) — \"par excellence.\" <i>\"qui Lepores binos [sectatur, neutrum capit]\"</i> — the proverb \"who chases two hares catches neither.\" <i>\"quodlibetice\"</i> — \"in disputation-at-will fashion / arbitrarily.\" <b>Danish</b> asides (kept and translated): <i>\"Dette er lige saa godt som alt det andet; naar de er trætte af dette, så kan de finde på nogen ny [device]\"</i> (the word <i>Huffroe</i> is obscure — rendered \"device/pretext,\" flagged); <i>\"Det var nok han var en Jyde\"</i> (\"It was enough that he was a Jute\"); <i>\"Saa skal I see hor høfflig ieg haver ganget om der med, for Sandhed lader sig icke siige\"</i> (\"Then you shall see how courteously I have dealt with it, for the truth cannot be hidden\").</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> A separately-dated SECOND postscript (\"POSTSCRIPTA ALIA\", 28 May 1598: Hájek's Prague reply, the Eske Bille / Axel Gyldenstierne counsel, the complaint about the Brahe brothers, a Danish aside), restored from Dreyer VIII, p. 68. ⚠ The embedded Danish sentence is heavily OCR-corrupted in the scan — rendered tentatively with [?]; warrants a Danish-philology check.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Six defects corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII pp.60-68: the bracketed supplement '[uxore]' restored to '[Cancellarij uxore]' (the Chancellor's wife, not Rosenkrantz's own wife) in both Latin and English; a Danish proverbial sentence on p.68, wrongly presented as scan-corrupted, restored to Dreyer's clearly legible reading in both languages ('it does much good to one who does no evil'); the reversed English sense of 'lader sig icke siige' corrected from 'cannot be hidden' to 'cannot be told'; the Jesuit commentator's name restored from 'PERIJ' to 'PERERIJ' (Pererius); and the closing date restored from 'die 8vo Anni 98' to 'die 8vo Maij Anni 98'. The last two needed no English change, as the translator had already rendered the intended sense despite the corrupted Latin.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A very long, content-dense omnibus rendered securely throughout; the substance (the launch of the imperial patronage campaign with the Cologne/Dutch hedge; the Brandenburg letters; the just-printed <i>Mechanica</i> and the gift-books for Emperor and King; the Ursus libel and the Longomontanus/Cramer/Liddel priority threads; the demolition of Everart's rival Ephemerides dedicated to Christian IV; the three-poem poetic war and the King reading the Elegy at Haderslev; Holger's marriage to Tycho's niece Sophie) is clear and richly cross-corroborated by the surrounding correspondence. No witness lacunae, but numerous <b>editor-conjectured bracket-readings</b> and one <b>flagged corrupt word</b> (<i>†nerue</i>); the only genuine lexical crux is the obscure Danish <i>\"ny Huffroe\"</i> (rendered \"some new device/pretext,\" flagged) — none of which obscures the letter's through-line, hence [B] rather than [C].</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VIII pp.58-69 (the letter proper runs pp.59-68a, bracketed by the tail of the preceding Thaddaeus letter on p.58-59 and the following short Thaddaeus/Cramerus letters on p.68b-69, correctly excluded). Checked the opening and closing lines and every proper name, date, place-name and sum in the body. Found the source_text itself departs from Dreyer in four places (dropped \"Cancellarij\" before \"uxore\"; \"PERIJ\" for \"PERERIJ\"; dropped \"Maij\" in the 8 May date; a substantially garbled Danish restored-postscript sentence mislabelled as scan-corrupted when Dreyer's page is clear) and found one further mistranslation independent of the source defects (the Danish idiom \"lader sig icke siige\", \"cannot be told\", rendered as \"cannot be hidden\"). The most consequential defect is translating the (already-corrupted) \"[uxore]\" as \"[your wife]\": Dreyer's actual text names the Chancellor's wife, not Rosenkrantz's own, and Rosenkrantz has no wife yet at this point in the letter.</p><p><b>6 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text silently drops \"Cancellarij\" (the Chancellor's) from the bracketed supplement, leaving bare \"[uxore]\". Dreyer prints: <i>Quod tam fufe cum [Cancellarij uxore] de me rebufque meis locutus fis (p.60)</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). Even granting the corrupted source, \"uxore\" alone carries no possessive; the translator supplied \"your\", which is wrong on Dreyer's actual text and misleading in context since Rosenkrantz is unmarried in this same letter (his own nuptials are still pending, discussed later) while a \"Cancellarius\" (Chancellor) recurs elsewhere in the letter as a distinct figure. A scholar would wrongly conclude Rosenkrantz already had a wife. At: “[your wife]”.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text drops a syllable from the Jesuit commentator's name, \"PERIJ\" for Dreyer's \"PERERIJ\" (Pererius). The English translator happened to render the correct full name \"Pererius\" anyway, so no consequential mistranslation resulted, but the source itself is corrupted at this proper name. Dreyer prints: <i>unâ cum PERERIJ in Genefin Tomo primo (p.63)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text drops \"Maij\" from the closing date formula. The translator correctly supplied \"[of May]\" as a bracketed editorial restoration, so no consequential error reached the English, but the source text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Dabantur subito Wandefburgo die 8vo Maij Anni 98. (p.66)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's page is clearly printed and legible, not corrupted; our source_text's Danish differs substantially in wording (e.g. \"er nu\" vs \"[?] nil\", the whole middle clause restructured) and is mislabelled as scan-corrupted. Because the translator honestly hedged (\"rendered only tentatively\") rather than asserting false confidence, the resulting English is a defensible paraphrase of a garbled source rather than a fabrication, but it fails to give the actual ascertainable sense (roughly: \"it has now come so far into the world that it does much good to one who does no evil\"). Dreyer prints: <i>Mens dett er nu faa viit kommen y verden, att den giør en møgitt gott, som giør en intedt ont. Gid jeg kunde kun nyde dett derpaa. (p.68)</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). \"lader sig icke siige\" means \"cannot be told/said\", not \"cannot be hidden\"; the translation reverses the sense of the Danish clause (unspeakable vs. unconcealable). At: “Then you shall see how courteously I have dealt with it — for the truth cannot be hidden:”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All six defects verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.60, 63, 66, 67, and 68 page images (not the OCR text layer), reading the full 12-page slice in three passes. Each defect's claimed Dreyer reading was confirmed exactly as stated in the audit, including the Danish passage on p.68 which reads clearly and is not scan-corrupted as source_text's editorial label claimed. No other part of this long letter was touched; every changed word is accounted for in outcomes above.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_15980214001", "next": "brahe-t_15980623001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Relays news of Erik Sparre's promises at Rostock, reports uncertainty about the Kolding assembly, and comments on Rantzau's role.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980508001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980524001", "d": "1598-05-24", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Cramer", "ck": "Daniel Cramer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Stettin", "t": "Daniel Cramer → Tycho Brahe — Stettin, 24 May 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE OTTONIDI DANO, DOMINO IN KNUDSTRUP, ASTRONOMORUM HAC TEMPESTATE PRINCIPI ILLUSTRI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO ΕΣ ΧΕΙΡΕ.<br>S.</p><p>MISI tibi, Nobilissime Domine Tycho, Domine et amice honorande, quod petivisti a me proxime, testimonium de plagio Liddeliano; id ut omnino spero, legisti, intellexisti. Nunc exspectas a me Propempticon ad tuam Uranien scriptum. Id ego promisi; en solvo. Sed nescio an moneta sat ponderosa. Iterum autem ex te meoque intime dilecto et honorando ROSENCRANTZIO exspecto responsum et in hanc et alias meas literas. Spes ne me confundat, uterque facietis. Haec paucula habui. Te Domino commendo, et iam iam post dies octo ad promotionem meam Doctoralem Witebergae σὺν θεῷ habendam eo. Tu felicissime vale cum OLIGERO. Stetini. Pridie Ascens. Domini Anno 98· T. M. Add.</p><p>Dan. Cramerus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe Ottonides the Dane, Lord in Knudstrup, illustrious prince of astronomers in this age, his lord and friend — into [his] hands [ΕΙΣ ΧΕΙΡΑ].</b><br><b>Greeting.</b></p><p>I sent you, most noble Master Tycho, lord and friend to be honoured, what you lately asked of me — the testimony concerning the Liddelian plagiarism; that, as I altogether hope, you have read and understood. Now you await from me a <i>Propempticon</i> [a send-off poem] written to your Urania. That I promised; behold, I pay it. But I know not whether the coin be of weight enough. And again I await from you, and from my intimately beloved and honoured Rosenkrantz, a reply both to this and to my other letters. That hope may not confound me — you will both [see to it]. These few things I had [to say]. I commend you to the Lord; and now, in eight days' time, I go — God willing [σὺν θεῷ] — to my Doctoral promotion, to be held at Wittenberg. May you fare most happily, together with Holger. Stettin. The day before the Lord's Ascension, in the year [15]98. Most devoted to Your [self],</p><p>Daniel Cramer.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Daniel Cramer</b> (1568–1637), Pomeranian Lutheran theologian, pastor and professor at Stettin (Szczecin) — the same correspondent as in the long Liddel-plagiarism deposition.</li><li><b>Tie to brahe-t_15980331001.</b> This cover-note accompanies, and refers back to, Cramer's <b>deposition on the \"Liddelian plagiarism\"</b> (the 31 March 1598 letter to Holger Rosenkrantz, ordered to be read also by Tycho); here Cramer confirms he has sent that <i>testimonium</i> and now discharges a further promise.</li><li><b><i>Propempticon ad tuam Uranien</b></i> — a <b>propemptikon</b>, the classical Greek genre of a send-off / valedictory poem; Cramer had promised Tycho a commendatory poem addressed to \"your <b>Urania</b>\" (Tycho's astronomy / his Muse / the Uraniborg programme). <i>en solvo</i> \"behold, I pay it\"; <i>nescio an moneta sat ponderosa</i> \"I know not whether the coin be of weight enough\" — a modest disclaimer of the poem's worth (the coin-metaphor for the verse he encloses).</li><li><b><i>Oligerus / Rosencrantzius</b></i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), Cramer's former pupil and Tycho's kinsman, the recipient of the deposition.</li><li><b>Cramer's doctorate.</b> <i>post dies octo ad promotionem meam Doctoralem Witebergae … habendam eo</i> — Cramer reports he is about to proceed to his <b>doctoral promotion at Wittenberg</b> in eight days. <i>σὺν θεῷ</i> (\"with God / God willing\").</li><li><b>Greek address.</b> Printed <i>ΕΣ ΧΕΙΡΕ</i> — a garbled <i>εἰς χεῖρα(ς)</i> \"into [his] hand(s),\" the same address-direction (and the same corruption) found in brahe-t_15980331001; kept verbatim in the Source block, standard reading followed in translation.</li><li><b><i>S.</b></i> = <i>Salutem [dicit]</i>, \"[sends] greeting.\" <b><i>T. M. Add.</b></i> = <i>Tuae Magnificentiae Addictissimus</i> (or <i>Tibi … Addictissimus</i>), \"Most devoted to Your [Magnificence].\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pridie Ascensionis Domini Anno 98</i> = \"the day before the Lord's Ascension, 1598.\" Ascension Thursday fell on <b>25 May 1598 (Old Style)</b>, so the eve = <b>24 May 1598</b>, agreeing with the dataset label. Stettin (Lutheran Pomerania) kept the <b>Julian / Old Style</b> calendar; the Ascension idiom is rendered and glossed to the civil date.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the dropped epithet 'intime dilecto' (intimately beloved) in 'meoque intime dilecto et honorando ROSENCRANTZIO', per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.69; the English was correspondingly updated to translate the restored words, which the truncated source_text had never carried into the translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the context (the Liddel deposition, the promised propemptikon, the Wittenberg doctorate, Rosenkrantz) is fully corpus-corroborated; the dateline resolves cleanly to 24 May 1598 O.S. Grade [B] only for the garbled Greek address <i>ΕΣ ΧΕΙΡΕ</i> (a corrupt <i>χεῖρα(ς)</i>), flagged and kept verbatim.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.69 page image in full (address-heading, Latin body, signature). Collated the plagio Liddeliano/testimonium sentence, the Propempticon-to-Urania sentence, the Rosenkrantz reply request, the Wittenberg doctoral-promotion clause, and the Stettin/Ascension-eve date and year against source_text and published_english. One TEXT_ERROR found (source_text drops 'intime dilecto' present in Dreyer); per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it does not change what a scholar would take away from the letter. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found elsewhere; translation of the (truncated) source is otherwise accurate throughout, including 'T. M. Add.' rendered as 'Most devoted to Your [self]'.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text silently drops the two-word epithet 'intime dilecto' (intimately beloved) that Dreyer prints between 'meoque' and 'et honorando ROSENCRANTZIO'; the published_english (\"from you, and from my honoured Rosenkrantz\") faithfully renders the truncated source it was given, so the fault is upstream in the source_text, not in the translation step. Dreyer prints: <i>Iterum autem ex te meoque intime dilecto et honorando ROSENCRANTZIO exspecto responsum</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.69; the phrase is legible and unambiguous. Both the Latin and its corresponding English clause were updated.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Daniel Cramer", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15990926001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990926001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends a promised Propempticon poem for Tycho's Urania, confirms dispatch of the testimony on the Liddelian plagiarism, and announces his doctoral promotion at Wittenberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980524001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980528001", "d": "1598-05-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Kristoffer Valkendorf", "ck": "Kristoffer Valkendorf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoffer Valkendorf — Wandsbek [near Hamburg], 28 May 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Privatarkiver, Kristoffer Valkendorff (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELLBYRDIG MAND CHRISTOFFER WALLCKENDROP THILL GLOERUP, DANMARKIS RIGES HOFFMESTER OCH RAAD, MIN SYNDERLIGE GODE WENN, GANDSCHE WENLIGENN THILLSCHREFFUITT</p><p>MIN gandsche vennlig hillsen nu och allthiid forsendt medt Vor Herre. Kierre her hoffmester, sønderlige gode ven, nest min vennlig thacksiigellse for allt beviist gode, hvillcket igen att forskyllde ieg vill findes aff formuen villig. Giffuendiß eder derhoes gandske wennligen thillkiende, att epthersom min fougidt, ieg haffuer betroidt att haffue opsyn paa Hvaen, haffuer nogle gange schreffuitt mig thill, att hand bekommer allthiid god biistand och raad hoes eder, naar hand haffuer nogit att giffue fore, som angaar bønderne derpaa landit, och att i allthiid svarer hannom vell, och hiellper hannom formedellst eders authoritheet och myndighedt derudi thill rette, saa vill ieg haffue eder paa dett wennligste bethackit for denne eders gode villge imod mig, alligevell att ieg icke nu sellff der er thillstede. Dersom mand rett wiste, hordan mottvillige och vlydige bønder der er paa samme lille landt, och hvad ieg haffuer liid aff dennom all denn stundt ieg bode der, och alligevell haffdt gedullt medt dennom, och giort dennom thill gode wiidere end de forskylldede, da skulle mandt kandtskee mene anderlediß derom, end thill diß skeed er. Kierre her hoffmester, epthersom eder witterligt er, att ieg bleff aff medt Nordtfiords forlening vdi Norge om Michaelisthiide strax epther krøningen, saa formener ieg, att mig burde att haffue hvis rente och indtkomst der gick aff y dett hallffue aar thillforn, horudaff ieg dog thill diß en føye thing haffuer bekommit, alligevell att ieg nogle gange haffuer schreffuitt did op derudom. Wille derfore haffue eder paa dett wennligste ombedit, att i ville vell giørre och medtdeele mig eders gode schriffuellse thill slotzherren paa Bergenhues, att hand ville forskaffe dett saa, att ieg eller min fulldtmegthige maatte medt dett første thill gode rede bekomme hvis mig der rester och bøer att haffue medt rette. Kierre her hoffmester, forthencker mig icke, att ieg bemøder eder hermedt, ieg forseer mig endnu allt gott thill eder och throer eder saa vell, att i baade vdi denne och andre maader bethencker leyligheden och verrer mig thill dett beste behiellpelig vdi allt dett, som billigt och rett er, och eder mueligt kand verre att giørre; [y] hvis maade ieg kan giørre nogit vdigen, som i kan haffue aere och gott aff, vill ieg stedtze [findes] medt all thacknemmelighedt ganndsche [villig och] redebon derthill. Och vill icke nu lenger ophollde eder, mens nu och allthiid haffue eder Gud allmegthigste befalidt.</p><p>Datum Wandeßbeck widt Hamborg denn 28 dag maij aar 1598.<br>Tyghe Brahe.<br>Egen hand.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born man Christoffer Valkendorf of Glorup, Lord High Steward and Councillor of the Realm of Denmark, my especial good friend, [this] most amicably written.</b></p><p>My most friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Lord Steward, especial good friend — next [after] my friendly thanks for all the kindness shown me, which to repay in turn I shall be found willing according to my means. Giving you herewith most amicably to know that, since my bailiff, whom I have entrusted with the oversight on Hven, has several times written to me that he always receives good assistance and counsel from you whenever he has anything to bring forward that concerns the peasants there on the land [the island], and that you always answer him well, and by your authority and power help him to his right in the matter — so I would most amicably thank you for this your good will toward me, even though I am not now myself present there. If one rightly knew how wilful and disobedient the peasants are on that same little land, and what I have suffered from them the whole while I dwelt there — and yet had borne with them, and done them good further than they deserved — then one would perhaps think otherwise about it than has hitherto been the case. Dear Lord Steward, since it is known to you that I was deprived of the Nordfjord fief in Norway about Michaelmas-time, just after the coronation, I therefore consider that I ought to have whatever rent and income accrued from it in the half-year before — of which, however, I have to this day received [but] a trifling thing, although I have several times written up there about it. I would therefore most amicably have asked you to be so good as to grant me your good letter to the castellan of Bergenhus, that he would so arrange it that I, or my attorney, might at the first [opportunity] receive in good order whatever remains [owing] to me there and ought by right to be mine. Dear Lord Steward, think no ill of me that I trouble you herewith; I still expect all good of you, and trust you so well that, both in this and in other ways, you will consider the circumstance and be helpful to me for the best in all that is fair and right, and that may be possible for you to do. In whatever way I can do anything in return from which you may have honour and good, I will always [be found], with all gratitude, wholly [willing and] ready thereto. And I will not now detain you any longer, but, now and always, [will have] commended you to God Almighty.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek near Hamburg, the 28th day of May, the year 1598.<br>Tyge Brahe.<br>[His] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christoffer Valkendorf til Glorup</b> (1525–1601), <b>Rigshofmester</b> (Lord High Steward of Denmark) and member of the <i>Rigsraad</i> (Council of the Realm) — the senior crown officer Tycho also approached (jointly with Chancellor Christian Friis) in the intercession letters of early 1598 (cf. brahe-t_15980125001). The MS is the <b>original letter</b> in Valkendorf's own private archive at the Rigsarkivet. <i>Tycho addresses him in warm terms</i> (<i>min synderlige gode ven</i>) — notable, since Valkendorf had earlier been counted among Tycho's adversaries at court; here he is the helpful patron of Tycho's Hven bailiff.<br>- <b>Setting.</b> Written from <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau estate near Hamburg) in May 1598, during Tycho's post-Hven exile, the year he printed the <i>Mechanica</i> and the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> there. Tycho has left Hven (1597) but <b>still holds the island and its tenants in absentia</b>, his <i>foged</i> (bailiff) managing it.<br>- <b>Two requests.</b><br>  1. <b>Thanks for backing the bailiff.</b> Valkendorf has repeatedly given the Hven bailiff \"good assistance and counsel\" in disputes with the island's <i>bønder</i> (peasant tenants); Tycho thanks him and vents about the tenants' <i>mootvillige och ulydige</i> (\"wilful and disobedient\") conduct during his own residence — a candid grievance over the long-running friction between Tycho and the Hven peasantry (the corvée and tenancy disputes that dog his lordship of the island).<br>  2. <b>The Nordfjord arrears.</b> Tycho had been <b>stripped of the Nordfjord fief (<i>forlening</i>) in Norway about Michaelmas (29 Sept) 1596, \"just after the coronation\"</b> — i.e. just after Christian IV's coronation (29 August 1596), one of the deprivations that marked the new reign's withdrawal of favour. He claims the <b>rent and income for the preceding half-year</b>, of which he has had only \"a trifling thing,\" and asks Valkendorf for a letter to the <b>castellan of Bergenhus</b> (the royal <i>slotsherre</i> at Bergen) so that he or his attorney may collect the arrears.<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Christoffer Valkendorf; Tycho's (unnamed) Hven bailiff; the peasants of <b>Hven</b>; the <b>castellan of Bergenhus</b>; the <b>Nordfjord</b> fief; <i>krøningen</i> = the <b>coronation of Christian IV</b> (1596).<br>- <b>Register / orthography.</b> Chancery Early Modern Danish in Tycho's own warm-but-deferential register; period spellings (<i>hillsen, wennligen, fougidt, forlening, schriffuellse</i>), German-influenced <i>sch-/w-/-ck</i> (note this orthography elsewhere causes mis-tagging of Danish letters as German — protocol §2/§7). Subscription <i>Egen hand</i> (\"[his] own hand\"), the Danish equivalent of <i>manu propria</i>.<br>- <b>Editorial brackets.</b> The square brackets in the source — <i>[y]</i>, <i>[findes]</i>, <i>[villig och]</i> — are the <b>edition's restorations</b> of small gaps/abrasions in the original, supplying obvious words; they do not affect the sense and are reproduced as printed.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>28 dag maij aar 1598</i> at Wandsbek; <b>Old Style (Julian)</b>, as Denmark and Tycho kept it. The dataset label agrees (1598. 28. maj) — no discrepancy.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery Danish, read securely; the two matters (Valkendorf's support of the Hven bailiff against the tenants; the Nordfjord-fief arrears via Bergenhus after the 1596 coronation), the persons, and the dating are explicit. The only editorial element — three trivial bracketed word-restorations — is flagged and does not bear on the meaning.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.138-140 as page images (Read pages=1-3, the entire slice). Collated the full Danish letter -- the Hven bailiff/peasants passage, the Nordfjord fief and Michaelmas/coronation reference, the Bergenhus castellan request, and the bracketed damaged-manuscript supplements ([y], [villig och]) -- word-for-word against the printed page. Text matches source_text exactly, including which words Dreyer himself brackets as reconstructed. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Privatarkiver, Kristoffer Valkendorff. Original letter"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Valkendorf for the support he gives Tycho's bailiff on Hven in managing disobedient peasants and settling their claims.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980528001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980530001", "d": "1598-05-30", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 30 May 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>THADDAeUS HAGECIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NOBILISSIME Domine TYCHO et amice observande, scripsi tibi propemodum ante mensem integrum, et cum hucusque nihil rescripseris, nisi forte tardiusculae ad te pervenerunt meae, te ipsum exspectabam, exspectoque etiamnum. Dominus Procancellarius CORADUCIUS diebus hisce me allocutus est, et dixit te iam Caesari de meliori nota commendasse, modo ipse venias, et te Caesari praesentes; in hoc solo cardo rei vertitur.</p><p>Aliud, quod scribam, non habeo, nisi ut quamprimum ad nos advoles.</p><p>B. V.<br>Pragae penult. Maij novo Stylo 1598.<br>T. THAD.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Thaddaeus Hagecius to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Tycho and esteemed friend, I wrote to you almost a full month ago, and since up to now you have written nothing back — unless perhaps my letters reached you somewhat late — I was awaiting your own self, and await you even now. Master Vice-Chancellor Coraduz addressed me in these days, and said that he had already commended you to the Emperor in the most favourable terms, provided only you come yourself and present yourself to the Emperor: on this alone the hinge of the matter turns.</p><p>I have nothing else to write, except that you fly to us as soon as possible.</p><p>Fare well.<br>Prague, the penultimate [day] of May, New Style, 1598.<br>Yours, Thaddaeus.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (see brahe-t_15981201001) — pressing Tycho to come to Prague in person, the decisive step toward his imperial appointment.</li><li><b><i>Procancellarius Coraducius</b></i> = <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor. <b><i>de meliori nota commendasse</b></i> — \"to commend in better/the most favourable terms\" (<i>nota</i> = the \"mark\" or note of recommendation). <b>Flagged</b>: the accusative-infinitive <i>te … commendasse</i> is grammatically open — it could read \"that you had [already] commended yourself to the Emperor most favourably.\" The sense adopted (Coraduz as the one who commended Tycho) is the one the context demands, since Tycho had only to appear; rendered accordingly.</li><li><b><i>cardo rei vertitur</b></i> — \"the hinge of the matter turns,\" i.e. everything depends on this one point.</li><li><b><i>B. V.</b></i> = <i>Bene Vale</i>, \"Fare well.\" <b><i>T. THAD.</b></i> = <i>Tuus Thaddaeus</i>, \"Yours, Thaddaeus.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>penult. Maij novo Stylo</i> = the penultimate day of May, <b>New Style</b> = <b>30 May 1598</b>; the <i>novo Stylo</i> is explicit (Prague, imperial). Header agrees with the dataset label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; grade [B] only for the one flagged construction (<i>te … commendasse</i>), where the subject of the recommendation is grammatically ambiguous and is resolved by context.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.67-69 (page images). The Hagecius letter is printed complete at the foot of p.68, matching source_text word-for-word, dated 'Pragae penult. Maij novo Stylo 1598' (30 May, matching metadata). Checked the named person (Procancellarius Coraducius) and every clause (the month-old prior letter, the Emperor commendation, the urging to come at once) against the translation — accurate throughout, no fabrication or omission.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 35, "prev": "brahe-t_15980000002", "next": "brahe-t_15981201001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980000002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990226001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Urges Tycho to come to Prague without delay, reporting that Vice-Chancellor Coraduz has commended him favourably to the Emperor.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980530001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980607001", "d": "1598-06-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Grynaeus", "ck": "Jacob Grynaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Jacob Grynaeus — Wandsbek, 7 June 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G II 2</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOCTORI IO. IACOBO GRYNAeO, APUD BASILIENSES VERBI MINISTRO ET IN INCLYTA ILLA ACADEMIA PROFESSORI CELEBERRIMO, AMICO SUO HONORANDO. BASEL.</p><p>CLARISSIME et praestantissime vir, quas in Decembri superioris anni ante semestre Basileâ ad me dedisti literas, recte quidem accepi, quibus tamen rescribendi nulla sese obtulit interea commoditas, antequam idem, qui tuas reddidit tabellarius, e Dania reversus, et ad vos denuo pergens, hac transiret, quare moram hanc candide accipias rogo. Non possum non ingentes tibi agere gratias, optime GRYNAeE, quod de me, utut longa viarum intercapedine dissito, tibique de facie ignoto, adeo benevole et sincere sentires, et ea quae mihi ob quorundam in Patria livorem et malevolentiam, crassâ veri ignorantiâ, sive per imprudentiam, sive datâ operâ, non dixerim, coniunctam ab inexpectato, et aliter quam forte promeritus eram, evenerunt, tibi adeo cordi esse sinas, ut huic rerum nostrarum conturbationi condoleas, meque consoleris et confirmes. Debemus equidem omnes prae caeteris Regionibus, plurimum Patriae, quae nos edidit, excepit et fovit. At quid faciendum, si haec gratitudinem nostram respuat, neque bene merita agnoscat, imo pro his quaevis incommoda et damna machinetur otiumque et commoditatem virtuti rebusque bonis operam navandi interturbet atque praeripiat? Et quis quaeso Patriae suae mores atque iudicia subinde perversa in hac mundanae scenae confusione et irregularitate unquam corriget? praesertim hoc saeculo, quando omnia videntur in peius ruere. Ferenda igitur sunt forti et constanti animo, quae caveri et emendari nequeunt, et de verâ illa ac Coelesti potius Patriâ omnis turbulentiae et enormitatis experte, serio cogitandum eoque anhelandum. Interim vero dum in hoc terrestri versamur exilio, eas quae nobis obveniunt graviores interdum adversitates, quam ijs sustinendis pares simus, siquidem avertere non datur, loci mutatione declinare atque evitare licitum existimo; ideoque et ego a gravioribus iniurijs, quas non paucas a quibusdam nostratium, qui nunc fatis Reipublicae ferme soli praeesse volunt, pertuli, totque fluctibus et tempestatibus in Germaniam discedendo, auxiliante Coelesti Numine, me vindicavi: sperans tranquilliorem et tutiorem me hîc inventurum portum. Quam arduae vero caussae me eo impulerint, verbis eloqui non facile datur, nec satis consultum arbitror, si quis ea, quae in Insula illa Hellesponti Danici, quam inhabitavi, molitus sum tot praeterlapsis annis, quaeque abiens isthic effecta reliqui, conspexerit expenderitque, non obscure vel hinc colliget gravissima fuisse illa, quae me haec omnia posthabere et Patrium solum deserere totamque familiam in Germaniam transferre adegerunt. Habet praesens tabellarius secum librum quendam hîc per otium hybernum excusum, quem meo permissu Nepoti ex fratre OTTHONI BRAHE, qui cum suo praeceptore in vestra laudatissima Academia aliquandiu antea versatus est, filius meus natu maior dono mittit; hunc si tantum tibi a gravioribus officij tui curis superest otij, ut quaedam in eo perlustrare vacet, mandavi tabellario, ut dum isthic moratur, tibi inspiciendi copiam faciat; cognosces hinc utcunque, veluti opinor, praesertim ex ijs, quae appendicis loco de structuris addidi, et quae infra Insulae topographiam consignata sunt, mihi antea non fuisse in animo talia unâ cum tota Patria totque in ea sanguine iunctis et amicis derelinquere. Adiuncta sunt eidem libro quaedam carmina manu scripta, quae hanc mutationem aliquatenus attingunt, quod si ea etiam percurrere commodum fuerit, erit tibi id liberum. Sed desino de his plura.</p><p>Ego hanc sortem, quae mihi contigit, lubens et fortiter, Divinâ favente clementiâ, feram, nec quidpiam inde movebor aut cursum a virtutis tramite remittam, quamvis mihi unâ cum meis extra Natale solum in peregrinis oris reliquum vitae, prout Deo visum fuerit, transigendum sit, nec enim Patriam hisce terreni globi angustis limitibus metior. Sed perinde esse duco, ubi quis in huius terrae superficie degat, modo bene et commode degat, nec contra conscientiam quidpiam agere aut admittere cogatur. Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO, affini et amico meo honorando, literas tuas praelegi curavi, illique ex te salutem dixi; is vicissim te peramanter resalutat, gratumque illi fuit, quod tam honorifice de illo scripseris et senseris. ANTHONIUM RANZOVIUM nondum convenire licuit, neque mihi constat, ubi is moretur; cum dabitur occasio, huic etiam tuo nomine salutem nunciari curabo. Plura nunc ob occupationum molem addere non vacat. Haec itaque qualiacunque subito effusa boni consulas, et amicitiam inter nos semel per literas contractam hac etiam ratione, siquidem aliter non liceat, conservare non desinas. Deus te cum tota domo incolumem conservet et a malis tueatur.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgo die 7. Iunij Anni 98.<br>Tuae excell. studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and excellent man, Doctor Johann Jacob Grynaeus, minister of the Word among the people of Basel and most celebrated professor in that renowned Academy, his honoured friend. Basel.</b></p><p>Most renowned and excellent man: the letter you sent me from Basel half a year ago, in December of last year, I did indeed duly receive — but no convenient means of writing back offered itself in the meantime, until the same carrier who delivered yours, returning from Denmark and proceeding again to you, passed this way; wherefore I beg you take this delay in good part. I cannot but give you the greatest thanks, excellent Grynaeus, that — of me, though sundered by a long stretch of road and unknown to you by face — you should think so benevolently and sincerely, and should let those things which befell me, <b>through the envy and malevolence of certain men in my fatherland</b> (out of gross ignorance of the truth, whether through imprudence or by design I will not say — combined, unlooked-for, and otherwise than perhaps I had deserved), so touch your heart, that you grieve with me at this disturbance of my affairs, and console and strengthen me. We all owe, beyond other regions, most to the <b>fatherland that brought us forth, received and cherished us</b>. But what is to be done, <b>if it spurns our gratitude, and does not acknowledge good deserts — nay, contrives against them all manner of harms and losses, and disturbs and snatches away the leisure and convenience for devoting one's labour to virtue and good things?</b> And who, pray, will ever correct the manners and the often perverse judgements of his fatherland, in this confusion and irregularity of the worldly stage — especially in this age, when all things seem to rush to the worse? The things, then, that cannot be guarded against and amended must be borne with a brave and constant mind; and we must think seriously of, and pant after, that <b>true and Heavenly fatherland</b>, free of all turbulence and enormity. But meanwhile, while we dwell in this earthly exile, the graver adversities that sometimes come upon us — heavier than we are equal to bearing — if we are not granted to avert them, <b>I hold it licit to decline and avoid by a change of place</b>. And so I too, from the graver injuries (not few) which I suffered from certain of my countrymen — <b>who now wish to preside almost alone over the fortunes of the Commonwealth</b> — and from so many waves and tempests, by departing into Germany, with Heaven's aid, vindicated myself, <b>hoping to find here a calmer and safer harbour</b>. By what hard causes I was driven thither it is not easy to express in words; yet I think it not ill-advised [to say that] <b>whoever shall have beheld and weighed what I wrought, over so many past years, on that Island of the Danish Hellespont which I inhabited, and what I left finished there on departing</b>, will gather even hence, not obscurely, that those things were most grievous which forced me to set all this behind me, to desert my native soil, and to transfer my whole family into Germany. The present carrier has with him <b>a certain book, printed here over the winter's leisure</b>, which, with my permission, my elder son sends as a gift to <b>my brother's son, Otto Brahe</b> — who for some time previously studied, with his tutor, in your most praised Academy. If you have so much leisure left over from the graver cares of your office that you may scan some things in it, I have charged the carrier, while he stays there, to give you the chance of inspecting it. You will recognise hence in some measure, as I think — <b>especially from what I added by way of appendix, concerning the structures, and what is set down below as the topography of the Island</b> — that it was not formerly in my mind to abandon such things, together with my whole fatherland and so many bound to me there by blood and friendship. There are also <b>joined to the same book certain poems in manuscript, which touch this change [of fortune] in some degree</b>; if it be convenient to run through these too, that is free to you. But I cease to say more of these matters.</p><p>This lot which has befallen me I will bear gladly and bravely, by Divine clemency favouring, nor will I be moved by it at all, nor slacken my course from the path of virtue, although I, together with mine, must pass the remainder of my life outside my native soil, on foreign shores, as it shall seem good to God; <b>for I do not measure my fatherland by these narrow limits of the terrestrial globe.</b> But I judge it all one where a man lives on the surface of this earth, provided he live well and conveniently, and be not compelled to do or admit anything against conscience. I had your letter read aloud to <b>Lord Heinrich Rantzau</b>, my honoured relation and friend, and gave him your greeting from you; he in turn most lovingly returns your greeting, and it was welcome to him that you wrote and thought so honourably of him. <b>Anton Rantzau</b> I have not yet been able to meet, nor do I know where he stays; when occasion is given, I will see that a greeting be announced to him too in your name. More I have no leisure to add now, on account of the mass of occupations. So take in good part these things, of whatever quality, suddenly poured out; and do not cease to preserve, by this means too (if it may not be otherwise), the friendship once contracted between us by letter. May God preserve you, with your whole house, unharmed, and guard you from evils.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, the 7th of June, the year [15]98.<br>Most zealous of your excellency,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Jacob Grynaeus</b> (1540–1617), the Basel Reformed theologian, <i>antistes</i> and professor — an admirer who had written to console Tycho (a stranger by face) over his troubles. The exchange is courteous and warm; this is Tycho's reply.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's <i>apologia</i> for leaving Denmark (one of the central documents of the self-exile).</b> The letter is Tycho's reasoned, dignified justification for abandoning Hven:</li><li>The <b>meditation on <i>Patria</b></i>: we owe most to the fatherland that bore us, <i>but</i> \"if it spurns our gratitude … contrives all manner of harms … snatches away the leisure for virtue\" — who can correct a fatherland's perverse judgements? One must bear what cannot be amended and long for the <b>\"true and Heavenly fatherland.\"</b></li><li>The doctrine of licit departure: when adversities exceed our strength and cannot be averted, <b>\"I hold it licit to decline them by a change of place\"</b> — hence Tycho's withdrawal \"from the graver injuries I suffered from certain of my countrymen, who now wish to preside almost alone over the fortunes of the Commonwealth\" (a pointed allusion to the regency faction under Christian IV's minority — Christoffer Valkendorf and the council).</li><li>The cosmopolitan close: <b>\"I do not measure my fatherland by these narrow limits of the terrestrial globe\"</b> — it matters not where on earth one lives, provided one live well and not against conscience.</li><li><b>Hven named — \"the Island of the Danish Hellespont.\"</b> <i>Insula illa Hellesponti Danici, quam inhabitavi</i> = <b>Hven</b>, in the Øresund (the \"Danish Hellespont,\" the Sound). Tycho invokes \"what I wrought there over so many years, and what I left finished on departing\" as silent proof that he never meant to abandon it.</li><li><b>The enclosed book = the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</b></i> (Wandsbek 1598) — <i>librum quendam hîc per otium hybernum excusum</i>, \"a book printed here over the winter's leisure,\" whose self-description clinches the identification: it contains an <b>appendix \"concerning the structures\"</b> (the descriptions of <b>Uraniborg and Stjerneborg</b>) and <b>\"the topography of the Island\"</b> (the Hven map) — exactly the <i>Mechanica</i>'s closing matter. Tycho's <b>elder son</b> sends a <b>gift copy</b> to <b>Otto Brahe</b> (Tycho's brother's son, formerly a student at Basel), routed via <b>Grynaeus</b>. <i>(A documented Mechanica gift-distribution event — see cross-references below.)</i></li><li><b>The manuscript poems.</b> <i>Carmina manu scripta … quae hanc mutationem … attingunt</i> — handwritten poems \"touching this change of fortune,\" i.e. Tycho's <b>elegiac verses on his departure from Denmark</b> (the <i>Elegia</i> addressed to Dania / Urania that circulated with the exile), sent loose with the book.</li><li><b>The Rantzaus.</b> <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (<i>Henricus Ranzovius</i>, 1526–1598), the great Holstein statesman-humanist and Tycho's host-patron (Wandsbek was his estate), to whom Tycho read Grynaeus's letter aloud — Rantzau returns warm greetings. <b>Anton Rantzau</b>, a kinsman, not yet met.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Johann Jacob Grynaeus; <b>Otto Brahe</b> (nephew, at Basel); Tycho's <b>elder son</b> (unnamed; Tycho Brahe the Younger); <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>; <b>Anton Rantzau</b>; the unnamed Danish regency faction. Places: <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Basel</b>, <b>Hven</b> (\"Island of the Danish Hellespont\"), Denmark, Germany.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A documented <b>Mechanica distribution event</b> — the gift copy (Tycho the Younger → Otto Brahe, via Grynaeus, Basel, June 1598) belongs to the documented distribution history of the <i>Mechanica</i>; the apologia itself belongs with the Hven-surrender / self-exile thread.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Holstein was <b>Old Style</b> in 1598; no <i>stylo</i> marker; dateline <b>7 June 1598</b> read <b>Old Style</b> (per the catalogue label). Header left unqualified to match.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia VIII, page 70: \"aliquando\" corrected to \"aliquandiu\"; the English clause describing Otto Brahe's time at Basel, which had followed the corrupted reading, corrected from \"once before studied\" to \"for some time previously studied\".</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — eloquent, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the meditation on <i>Patria</i> and the Heavenly fatherland, the doctrine of licit departure, the veiled rebuke of the Danish regency faction, the Hven reference, the gift of the <b>just-printed <i>Mechanica</b></i> with its structures-appendix and island-topography to <b>Otto Brahe</b> via Grynaeus, the enclosed departure-poems, and the Rantzau greetings) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer slice_pdf visually in full (TBOO VIII pp.69-71; the letter to Grynaeus begins at the bottom of p.69, matched by the salutation 'CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOCTORI IO. IACOBO GRYNAeO...BASEL', and ends on p.71 with 'Tuae excell. studiosiss. TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.'; pp.68 and the top of 69/71 hold unrelated Hagecius/Cramerus items and the start of the next letter, correctly excluded). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against the page images -- the December letter received via returning tabellarius, the fatherland/malevolence passage, the Insula Hellesponti Danici (Hven) departure narrative, the book sent to Nepos Otto Brahe via the tabellarius, Heinrich and Anton Rantzau, and the closing 'Dabantur Wandesburgo die 7. Iunij Anni 98.' -- and found one genuine, twice-verified single-word divergence between source_text and Dreyer's print ('aliquando' vs 'aliquandiu', re-checked by re-reading TBOO VIII p.70 a second time in isolation to rule out misreading). This is a real TEXT_ERROR per the grading rule even though it is low-severity and does not change what a scholar would take away from the letter as a whole (Otto Brahe's period of study at Basel with his tutor is asserted either way). No other defects -- no fabrications, omissions, mistranslations, or hedge losses -- were found; every other proper name, number, and date matches Dreyer exactly.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads 'aliquando' (at some time / once) where Dreyer's page image clearly prints 'aliquandiu' (for some time / for a while); the English follows the corrupted source_text, so it renders a single occasion where Dreyer's text implies a period of residence. Dreyer prints: <i>qui cum suo praeceptore in vestra laudatissima Academia aliquandiu antea versatus est (TBOO VIII, p.70, line 29)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The single defect site was collated against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia volume VIII, page 70 (page image, not the OCR text layer). Confirmed: \"qui cum ſuo praeceptore in veſtra laudatiſſima Academia aliquandiu antea verſatus eſt.\" The English was demonstrably misled by the corrupted Latin and has been corrected accordingly. No other cruxes were found on the sampled pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G II 2."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Grynaeus for his sympathetic letter regarding the envy and malevolence of certain men that drove Tycho from Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980607001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980618001", "d": "1598-06-18", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Magdeburg", "t": "Georg Rollenhagen → Tycho Brahe — [Magdeburg], 18 June 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>S.</p><p>ETSI nihil erat, Nobilissime et Doctissime TYCHO, quod ad te praecipue nunc scriberem, tamen cum forte accederet me Tabellarius Lipsicus, volui te hisce salutare, et quae meus affinis ad me de URSO et de exspectatione tui scribit, tecum communicare. Is affinis meus arte quidem est gemmarum scalptor ingeniosus, sed officio apparitor Caesaris, estque hoc eius, quod mitto, chirographum, ut si nota tibi iam tamen sint, intelligas ibi quoque innotuisse; sin ignota, deliberes, quid ad eam exspectationem tibi sit faciendum. Quod bene vertat ad salutem tuam et Uranies Deus Opt. Max., cum Illmo D. Brunsvicensi de negotio tuo locutus sum. Ille quidem praeclare de te sentit, sed Regni et Regis desertionem causatur. Et quamvis causas declararem tuas, et Regem ab alijs regi dicerem, tamen mansit in sententia, ideo quod a suis Nobilibus se deseri, et coram camera accusari molestissime fert. Coniunx existimabat exspectandam esse occasionem, qua coram Regem ipsum convenires: sic omnia facile te impetraturum. Princeps in vinculis habet PETRUM HAGEN L. L. Salderiorum advocatum. Ipsos quoque fratres Salderios Lemgaviae sub Comite Lipiensi nuper coepit, propediem eosdem in Lycaoniam suam attracturus. Habent enim Leones Leonina ingenia. Deus, in cuius manu est cor Principis, dirigat illud ad clementiam et iustitiam. Saluto generosum senem Maecenatem meum D. HENRICUM RANZOVIUM, eique facilem, viridem, et laetam senectutem precor. Pestis grassatur passim in vicinia, praesertim Halberstadij, et ad nos eius semina quaedam allata sunt. Deus avertat ulteriora.</p><p>Vale.<br>Datae die 18. Iunij Anno 98.</p><p>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM GERMANICARUM QUÆ LATINIS<br>HISCE INCLUSÆ ERANT.</p><p>MEIN gandtzwillig dienst jedertzeit beuor. Insunder<br>gunstiger her Swager, das es Euch, samt Euhren lieben Hausfrauwen<br>wolginge, frisch vnd gesundt wehrt, solde mir lieb sein. Ick danck Godt<br>den Almechtigen vor liebes gesundheit, der erhalte uns ferner nac seinem<br>willen, amen.</p><p>Nachdem mir der Her Swager vor diesem<br>von des VRSI halben gescriben, hab ich<br>ein weinig nachfrag gehabt allhie bei Ihr May. Camerdiener, ob<br>derselbige VRSUS im vngnaden wehre bey<br>Ihr May. saa hab ich den bericht bekommen, das ehr woll nicht im<br>vngnaden sei, alleine das ehr sich vor viell ausßgeben vndt itzundt<br>nicht sich befindet, vnd in astrologia das geringest nicht vorstehet<br>etc. Es hatt sich also begeben, das ehr von Ihr May. ist probiert<br>worden, mit des Fürsten von Siebenbürgen seiner natiuitet, darinne er<br>wider den anfangk, noch ende gewust, dadurch ehr in<br>vorachtung vnd vorlachung kommen, vnd dieweil ehr das ziell vnd die<br>warheit nicht gesetzt, vnd sein judicium falsch, will itzundt dagegen<br>scriben, vnd vorachtet vnd scalliert die Astrologiam aus das Höegste<br>auss, es sein lauter lügen vnd bedrug darin, da ehr sie doch von ersten<br>gelobt vnd dardurch zu Ihr May. gekommen. Die ander vrsach ist das wi<br>man ihm gefragt worumb ehr den sich zu hoff angegeben darfür, vnd Ihr.<br>May. Herrn Brüdters Höegstseliger gedechnusß Ihr Thema gemacht (welch<br>sich recht befunden) vnd itzundt dar wider sei, hat er nichts wissen<br>zubeantworden. Man hat aber ehrfaren, das ehr solchs nicht selbst<br>gemacht, sondern auss des ELIÆ<br>PREUSENS seinen Büchern genommen, vnd sich dardurch also<br>herfür gethan etc. So hab ich auch vornommen, das TICHO BRAHE alhier<br>ahn vnsern hoff wirdt kommen, vnd Ihr. May. grosse vorlangen nach ihm<br>haben, vnd werden ihm ein Sloß ihngeben drey meil wegs von Prag, dar<br>ehr seinen residentz soll haben, ein seer lustiger Ort, Brandeiss genant.<br>Deß von DIEFFENBACHS Söne sein alhier zu Prag, die werden nach<br>Strasburg geschicht zu studiern, vnd wen der Her Swager will schrieben<br>an die örter, dar ehr meint, will ichs woll behendigen, vnd habe gutte<br>gelegenheitt darzu. Sonsten aus diesmal nicht mehr.</p><p>Den Godt beuohlen<br>samt Euhren lieben Hausfrauwen vnd kinder, vnd von<br>mihr vnd meinen Hausfrauwen gegrüst.</p><p>Datum Prag den lesten May stylo<br>nouo 98 Anno.</p><p>E. D. W . Swager vnd<br>Discipulus<br>CASPAR<br>LEHMAN<br>Röm. Kay. Maytt Hartschir.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Georg Rollenhagen to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Although there was nothing, most noble and most learned Tycho, that I should especially write to you now, yet, since the Leipzig courier happened to come to me, I wished to greet you with these [lines], and to share with you what my kinsman writes to me about Ursus and about the expectation of you. This kinsman of mine is, by art, indeed an ingenious engraver of gems, but, by office, an apparitor [usher] of the Emperor; and this which I send is his own handwriting, so that — if these things are nonetheless already known to you — you may understand that they have become known there too; but if [they are] unknown, you may deliberate what you ought to do in regard to that expectation. May God Best and Greatest turn it well to your welfare and Urania's: I have spoken with the Most Illustrious Lord of Brunswick about your business. He indeed thinks excellently of you, but pleads [as an objection] the desertion of the Kingdom and the King. And although I declared your reasons, and said that the King is ruled by others, he yet remained in [his] opinion — for the reason that he most grievously resents being deserted by his own nobles, and accused before the [imperial] chamber. His consort thought that the occasion must be awaited on which you might meet the King himself in person: thus you would easily obtain everything. The Prince holds in chains Peter Hagen, advocate-at-law of the Salderns. The Saldern brothers themselves, too, he lately seized at Lemgo, under the Count of Lippe — [intending] shortly to drag the same into his own Lycaonia. For Lions have leonine natures. May God, in whose hand is the heart of the Prince, direct it to clemency and justice. I salute the generous old man, my Maecenas, Master Heinrich Rantzau, and pray him an easy, green, and glad old age. Plague rages here and there in the vicinity, especially at Halberstadt, and certain seeds of it have been brought to us. May God avert worse.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given the 18th day of June, in the year [15]98.</p><p><b>Copy of a German letter, which was enclosed within this Latin one.</b></p><p>My wholly willing service, always beforehand. Especially favouring Lord brother-in-law: that it should go well with you, together with your dear wife, that you be fresh and healthy, would be dear to me. I thank God the Almighty for my own dear health; may He preserve us further according to His will, amen.</p><p>Since my Lord brother-in-law wrote to me before this concerning Ursus, I have made a little inquiry here with His Majesty's chamber-servant, whether the same Ursus were in disfavour with His Majesty; and I have received word that he is indeed not in disfavour, only that he gave himself out for much [more than he is], and now does not find himself equal to it, and understands the least thing in astrology not at all, etc. It has thus come about that he was put to the test by His Majesty with the nativity of the Prince of Transylvania, in which he knew neither the beginning nor the end, whereby he has fallen into contempt and mockery; and since he did not set down the mark and the truth, and his judgment was false, he now wishes to write against it [astrology], and disdains and reviles Astrology to the very utmost, [saying] that there is nothing in it but sheer lies and deceit — though he indeed praised it at first, and thereby came to His Majesty. The other reason is that, when he was asked why he had put himself forward at court on this account, and had cast the horoscope [Thema] of His Majesty's late brother of most blessed memory (which was found to be correct), and is now against it, he had nothing he knew how to answer. But it has been found out that he did not make this himself, but took it out of the books of Elias Preuss, and thereby made himself prominent, etc.</p><p>I have also learned that Tycho Brahe is to come here to our court, and that His Majesty has a great longing for him, and that they will give him a castle three miles' way from Prague, where he is to have his residence — a very pleasant place, called Brandeis. The sons of [Herr] von Dieffenbach are here at Prague; they are to be sent to Strasbourg to study; and if my Lord brother-in-law wishes to write to the places he has in mind, I will gladly deliver it, having good occasion for it. Otherwise, for this time, no more.</p><p>Herewith commended to God, together with your dear wife and children, and greeted from me and my wife.</p><p>Given at Prague, the last of May, new style, in the year [15]98.</p><p>Your Lordship's willing [servant,] brother-in-law and disciple, Caspar Lehman, halberdier of the Roman Imperial Majesty.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609) of <b>Magdeburg</b> (the place not in the dateline but established by the later instrument-transport letter brahe-t_16000619001); Tycho's agent and friend.</li><li><b>The Ursus intelligence.</b> Rollenhagen forwards news, in the hand (<i>chirographum</i>) of <b>his kinsman</b> — \"by art an ingenious gem-engraver, by office an Imperial <b>apparitor</b> [court usher]\" — about <b>Ursus</b> (Nicolai Reimers Bär, Tycho's rival, then Imperial Mathematician) and about the <b>expectation of Tycho</b> at the imperial court; Tycho is to weigh how to act. (This is two years before the Ursus suppression of brahe-t_16000727001.)</li><li><b>The Duke of Brunswick.</b> <i>Illmus D. Brunsvicensis</i> = <b>Heinrich Julius, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</b> (1564–1613), often at the imperial court. He esteems Tycho but objects to his \"<b>desertion of the Kingdom and the King</b>\" (of Denmark) — the recurrent reproach of Tycho's self-exile; Rollenhagen pleaded that \"the [Danish] King is ruled by others.\" The Duke's <i>consort</i> advised that Tycho should wait to meet the Danish king in person.</li><li><b>The Saldern affair.</b> <i>Petrus Hagen, L[egum] L[icentiatus], Salderiorum advocatus</i> — Peter Hagen, legal advocate of the <b>von Saldern</b> family, imprisoned by the Duke; the <b>Saldern brothers</b> seized at <b>Lemgo</b> (under the <b>Count of Lippe</b>) — a contemporary noble feud. <i>in Lycaoniam suam attractare</i> / <i>Leones Leonina ingenia</i> — a play on the Duke's wolfish/leonine harshness (<i>Lycaon</i> the wolf-king; \"lions have leonine natures\"). The whole sentence is wordplay on the Duke's reputation for ruthlessness toward refractory nobles.</li><li><b><i>Maecenas meus … Henricus Ranzovius</b></i> = <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, the aged patron, wished \"an easy, green, and glad old age.\" <i>Pestis … Halberstadij</i> — plague at <b>Halberstadt</b>, spreading.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 18. Iunij Anno 98</i> = <b>18 June 1598</b>; Magdeburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>What it is.</b> A German newsletter from Prague, dated \"the last of May, new style, [15]98,\" enclosed as an <i>exemplum literarum Germanicarum</i> inside Georg Rollenhagen's Latin letter to Tycho of 18 June 1598 O.S. Rollenhagen — the Magdeburg schoolmaster-poet and regular Tycho/Rantzau correspondent (attested repeatedly in Oestmann's <i>Rantzau und die Astrologie</i> and Zeeberg's Rantzau studies) — evidently forwarded it to Tycho as court intelligence.</li><li><b>The writer, identified.</b> Signed \"Caspar Lehman, Röm. Kay. Mayt. Hartschier\" [halberdier/guardsman of the Roman Imperial Majesty] — this is almost certainly Caspar Lehmann (d. 1622), Rudolf II's imperial gem- and glass-cutter at Prague, later renowned as the founder of German glass engraving. Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (Cambridge, 2000), independently records that \"an imperial astrologer and gem artist, Caspar Lehmann, acted as Tycho's informer\" and \"sent the welcome news that Ursus had displayed his own dishonesty and lack of skill to the emperor and was no longer in favor\" (n.56) — almost word for word the content recovered here, strongly suggesting this letter is, or directly feeds, that very report.</li><li><b>Ursus's disgrace.</b> Nicolaus Reimers Ursus — Tycho's rival, then Rudolf II's Imperial Mathematician — is reported to have been tested by the emperor with the nativity of the Prince of Transylvania and to have failed conspicuously, his earlier (correct) horoscope of the Emperor's late brother exposed as lifted from the books of one \"Elias Preuss\" — not identified in the consulted corpus, and to be distinguished from Tycho's own former Uraniborg assistant Elias Olai Cimber (Elias Olsen Morsing).</li><li><b>Tycho's move to Prague, anticipated over a year early.</b> Already by May 1598 — a full year before Tycho actually reached Bohemia and settled at Benátky in August 1599 — Prague court gossip had it that Tycho \"will come here to our court\" and would be given a castle three miles from Prague called \"Brandeis\" [Brandýs nad Labem]. Dreyer records that Rudolf later did offer Tycho a choice among Lyssa, Brandeis and Benátky, of which Tycho chose Benátky; this letter shows the plan, and the specific castle, already circulating as rumor well before the event.</li><li>Crux: \"Elias Preusens,\" \"von Dieffenbach,\" and the unnamed deceased \"Herr Bruder\" of the Emperor (whose nativity Ursus reportedly cast correctly — most likely Archduke Ernst of Austria, d. 1595, but not independently confirmed here) are not resolved in the consulted corpus. The closing abbreviation \"E. D. W.\" before \"Swager vndt Discipulus\" is of uncertain expansion; rendered loosely as \"Your Lordship's willing [servant].\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'attractare' to 'attracturus' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.72.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the principal matters (the Ursus/court intelligence via the gem-engraver apparitor; Duke Heinrich Julius of Brunswick's stance on Tycho's \"desertion\"; the Saldern/Hagen imprisonment with its leonine wordplay; Rantzau; the Halberstadt plague) are clear and corpus-/history-consistent. Grade [B] only for the unnamed <b>kinsman</b> (the imperial apparitor/gem-engraver), flagged rather than identified, and for the deliberately punning <i>Lycaonia/Leones</i> sentence, rendered by sense.</p><p>The German is clear Early New High German court correspondence with no lacunae, and the sense is not seriously in doubt anywhere. The strongest external corroboration is Christianson's near-verbatim account of Lehmann's report on Ursus's disgrace, which independently validates both the substance of this translation and Lehmann's identity as the imperial gem-cutter. Softer points are the unidentified names (Elias Preusens, von Dieffenbach, the Emperor's late brother) and the abbreviated closing formula \"E. D. W.\", none of which affect the letter's main sense. Uncollated against a published edition.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Latin body + the enclosed German letter from Caspar Lehman, both in full) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 72-73, read as page images (the letter opens and closes entirely within these two pages). Checked every proper name (Petrus Hagen, the Saldern brothers, Heinrich Rantzau, Elias Preuss, Tycho Brahe, the von Dieffenbach sons, Caspar Lehman), place (Lemgo, Halberstadt, Brandeis/Brandeiss, Prague, Strasbourg), and date (18 June / last of May new style, 1598). Found one TEXT_ERROR (source_text 'attractare' vs Dreyer's 'attracturus') which does not itself corrupt the sense conveyed in the English. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. Per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though this one is low-severity and the translation's sense is unaffected; verdict is MINOR, not SERIOUS.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the infinitive 'attractare' where Dreyer prints the future participle 'attracturus'. A genuine word-form departure from Dreyer, not a spelling convention. It does not corrupt the translation itself: the published English ('[intending] shortly to drag') actually renders the sense of Dreyer's real 'attracturus' correctly, better than it would render the source_text's 'attractare'. Dreyer prints: <i>propediem eofdem in Lycaoniam fuam attracturus (p.72)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the single defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.72), which clearly reads 'propediem eosdem in Lycaoniam suam attracturus'. The published English already matched Dreyer's sense, so only the Latin required correction. No uncertainty remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980400001", "next": "brahe-t_16000405001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000405001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Shares his kinsman's intelligence about Ursus and the Emperor's expectation of Tycho, and reports speaking with the Lord of Brunswick on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980618001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980623001", "d": "1598-06-23", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 23 June 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TAM DOCTRINA EXIMIA QUAM PROSAPIA CLARA VIRO INSIGNITER DECORATO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTIMO.<br>S. P.</p><p>QUAe nuper tibi destinavi, exoptatissime mi OLIGERE consangvinee et affinis carissime, per CLAUDIUM MULAeUM, quem ante Comitia istuc misi, te omnia nunc accepisse non dubito. Succurrit post MULAeI abitum in libro de Astronomia Mechanica pagina quae est G. 3. in qua Domini IACOBI CURTIJ, circa quadrantis cuiusdam subdivisionem, inventio exhibetur, in ipso titulo ante hoc nomen: CURTIO omissam esse literam I. vel potius IAC. unde praenomen eius indicaretur, et postea in infima eiusdem tituli linea impressum est: GENEROSE ADINVENTA pro INGENIOSE ADINVENTA; quod nescio an in eo, quod misi, exemplari emendatum sit; sin minus, cuperem id adhuc abs te calamo, quod facile fit in tam densa papyro, corrigi, ne Zoili et hîc habeant quo abutantur. Quid de literis Berlinensibus actum sit, ex te cognoscere aveo. Si quid in Comitijs illis, quod mea interest scire, tibi innotuit, hoc quidquid est, mature etiam, antequam MULAeUS (modo fieri potest) reversus fuerit, significa. Fuit ante triduum hîc apud me IACOBUS ROSENKRANTZIUS noster, in Belgium hinc cogitans; is graves et difficiles in hisce Comitijs controversias motum iri referebat; et inter alia PEPLERI istius caussam recognoscendam decidendamque existimavit. At quo iure id fieri queat, ignoro, cum citationis forma mihi etiam non sit praesentata, nec quidpiam a Praefecto meo de ea innotuerit, ut existimem, eam ne quidem Uraniburgi lectam esse, aut si id factum, non debito tempore aut modo; sed sit hoc quidquid velit; si ius et fas locum inveniunt, nihil periculi, sin secus, nihil novi. Existimo, imo expertus sum, quod si ego in Dania quidpiam proponerem, quod aeque verum sit atque ea, quae Geometrice demonstrantur, posse nihilominus ea ipsa apud quosdam, qui iustitiae praeesse volunt, falsitatis insimulari. Quid igitur faciendum, ubi ius et veritas ob privatos affectus atque personae odium locum non merentur? Aiebat insuper dictus noster Consangvineus, se exemplum literarum, quas Sermo Regi nostro, cum Rostochij essem, scripsi, unâ cum responso ad easdem, cum Fioniam transiret, apud Nobiles quosdam reperisse, et si recte memini, CANUTUM RUD hîc nominavit, quem a CAROLO BRUSCHIO talia accepisse opinabatur. Quod si sic est, optarem, eos, qui horum facti sunt participes, ea, quae mihi rescripta sunt, rectius quam litera ipsa praetendit, explanata habere, quae incautis et inscijs plausibilis forte videri queat, cum tamen longe aliter res se habeat, et mihi foret quam facilimum, uti ipsemet scis, omnia isthic obiecta replicare et contrario modo demonstrare, sed obstant ea, quae prius dixi, et authoritas eorum, qui haec vafre consarcinarunt, veritati nunc praevalet. Opus igitur est patientia, et tandem bona caussa triumphat, si non in Dania, saltem alibi: O utinam in nos illud Virgilianum non propediem quadraret. Excessere omnes adytis arisque relictis Dij, quibus imperium hoc steterat. Item illud paulo antea: Fuimus Troes, fuit Ilium et ingens gloria Danorum. Solet enim regnum divinitus puniendum sanis destitui consilijs, et in partes quorundam vafritia discindi, atque sub veri bonique praetextu contraria tecte agere. Solent etiam omnia praecedere, qualia non pauca isthic audiuntur, si modo, quae narrantur, veritati congruunt. Cum quaesivissem a praenominato ROSENKRANTZIO de halece characterizato in Norvegia reperto, nihil quidem certi de eo referre potuit, attamen aliud piscium genus, quod latini aculeatum, nostrates Hornfisk vocant, adhuc longe mirabilioribus notatum characteribus nuper prope insulam Tossingam, seu potius parvulam illi adiacentem, repertum esse, certissime affirmavit; quarum figurarum, si tibi hac de re quid constat, efformationem mecum communicari vellem. Scripsit mihi postea CRAMERUS tuus, et carmen quoddam literis inclusit. Misit etiam BRASCHIUS Poëta Rostochiensis Heroicum in mei gratiam concinnatum, sed neutrum satis placet; habes tamen hîc utrumque, Cramerianum ipsius manu scriptum, unâ cum literis, prout accepi; neque enim describendi otium suppetit; quod tamen, cum exemplum eius non habeam, mihi remitti velim. Habeo praeterea alia eiusmodi, inter quae est quoddam ex orientali Frisia admodum prolixum; et cuduntur hinc inde adhuc plura in memoriam rei apud nos bene gestae. Expectat autem Germania avide tuum pro vicina sua responsum. Expecto et ego commentaria illa, quae scis, quibus mea per oportunitatem intertexam; ubi vero tua absoluta fuerint, ea cum KRAGIO nostro tacite communica, ut et is ex rei transactae historia, quam pro maiori parte novit, suam quoque interponat sententiam. Exemplum earum, quas THADDAeUS HAGGAeCIUS ante aliquot hebdomadas Praga mihi scripsit, Domino Doctori NICOLAO KRAGIO iam antea misi, qui procul dubio te eius reddidit participem. Sed et hisce diebus breves quasdam ab eodem THADDAeO denuo accepi, quarum hîc habes descriptum; paucioribus rem expedit, siquidem dubitavit an hîc essem, an vero iter Pragam versus suscepissem; vides eos serio meum adventum expetere et expectare, atque ipsi Caesari me concernentia iam innotuisse. Tu et KRAGIUS consulite in medium, et aliorum quoque, quibus res meae serio et sincere cordi sunt, sententiam tacite expiscemini. Ego nihil certi concludere possum, antequam TEGNAGLIUS et MULAeUS ad me reversi fuerint. Fuit hesterna die apud me Dominus Doctor VITUS WINSHEMIUS a comitibus Oldenburgensibus, ubi dissidij cuiusdam inter eos componendi caussa adfuerat, iam primum reversus; is inter alia de Doctore BOeKELIO rettulit, ipsum urgere pecuniam sibi pro libris, quos accepisti, promissam; a te vero sibi perscriptum aiebat, ne pecuniam illam eidem BOeKELIO traderet, antequam omnes libros residuos depromeret, quod ipsum praestare aut nolle aut non posse intelligo, et praetendere tres vel quatuor saltem restare, eosque non magni momenti, quique in ipsius non sint potestate. Quaesivit igitur WINSHEMIUS a me, quid illi hac in re faciendum existimarem; suasi, ut tuae voluntati morem gereret et secum pecuniam reservaret, donec alter per omnia tuis desiderijs et suae pollicitationi satisfecisset. At ille excipiebat, sibi brevi Berlinum et in Misniam eundum, BOeKELIUM vero tam diu pecunia carere nolle, imo stomachabundum dixisse, quod mallet libros suos recipere, quam diutius pecuniam istam expectare. Statuimus itaque, ut Dominus Doctor VITUS in sua absentia pecuniam a te missam alteri fidedigno viro ex amicis suis Hamburgi traderet, et si interea responsum a te reciperet (nam se tibi hac de re scripsisse innuebat) filius ipsius, qui isthic remanet, literas tuas aperiret, atque secundum earum contenta negotium hoc exequeretur. Tuum itaque est mature, quid in his fieri vis, significare; si tuo loco essem, aut restituerem illi libros, siquidem sic vult, aut concederem illi pecuniam iam antea addictam, utut pauculos illos libros procurare nolit aut forte nequeat, quo possis ab ipso liberari; est enim paulo morosior, et insuper (quod tamen cum multis commune habet) γυναικοκρατούμενος, Imperatrice eius nimis importuna existente. Cuius rei MULAeUS documentum dabit; ego nihilominus, quidquid hac in re fieri velis, quantum in me est, effectum dabo. Currum, quem promisisti ante Pascha, Pentecostes etiam festi, imo et IOHANNIS BAPTISTAe tempora, frustratus est, et licet Cimber sis, privilegioque gentis gaudeas, tamen te praeter caeterorum morem quidpiam Schanicae constantiae habere, praesertim cum per coniugium in Schanum quodammodo transplanteris, existimarem potius, ideoque per incuriam quandam eorum, quibus hoc negotium mandasti, neglectum esse. Sed sit quidquid velit, nolim tibi hac in parte uti etiam nulla alia gravis esse; saltem scire desidero, quid expectandum sit vel secus, ne frustra alium confieri procurem. De aedibus etiam meis Haffniensibus quid fieri possit vel non, a te certior reddi aveo. Iamque cum utraque tua SOPHIA, addita insuper tertia illa quae et URANIA appellatur, quam felicissime vale; saluta fratrem AXILLUM et alios, si quos mei isthic tangat memoria. Deus vos omnes tueatur a malo.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgo die 23. Iunij Anni 98.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man signally adorned as much by eminent learning as by illustrious lineage, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, my kinsman, relation, and intimate friend.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>What I lately destined to you, my most longed-for Holger, kinsman and dearest relation, through <b>Claus Mule</b> — whom I sent thither before the Diet — I do not doubt you have now received all. It came to me, after Mule's departure, that <b>in the book on Mechanical Astronomy [the <i>Mechanica</i>], on the page which is G.3, where the invention of Lord Jacob Curtius about the subdivision of a certain quadrant is shown — in the title itself, before this name \"Curtio,\" the letter \"I\" (or rather \"IAC.\") is omitted, by which his praenomen would be indicated; and later, in the lowest line of the same title, \"GENEROSE ADINVENTA\" was printed for \"INGENIOSE ADINVENTA.\"</b> Whether it is corrected in the copy I sent, I do not know; if not, I should wish it yet to be corrected by you with the pen (which is easy in such dense paper), lest the Zoiluses have here too something to abuse. What was done about the <b>Berlin letters</b>, I long to learn from you. If anything in that <b>Diet</b> which concerns me to know has become known to you, signify it — whatever it be — in good time, even before Mule returns (if it can be done).</p><p>Three days ago <b>Jacob Rosenkrantz</b>, our [kinsman], was here with me, thinking [to go] hence into the Low Countries; he reported that grave and difficult controversies would be stirred in this Diet, and among other things judged that <b>the case of that \"Pepler\"</b> would be reviewed and decided. But by what right this can be done, I do not know, since the form of citation was not even presented to me, nor has anything of it become known from my Praefect [the Hven governor] — so that I think it was not even read at Uraniborg, or, if it was, not at the due time or in the due manner. But be this what it will: if right and divine law find place, no danger; if otherwise, nothing new. <b>I think — nay, I have experienced — that if I should propose anything in Denmark as true as the things that are demonstrated Geometrically, those very things could nonetheless be accused of falsehood [<i>falsitatis insimulari</i>] by certain men who wish to preside over justice. What, then, is to be done, when right and truth, on account of private passions and hatred of a person, do not merit a place?</b></p><p>Our said kinsman said besides that, passing through <b>Funen</b>, he found among certain Nobles <b>a copy of the letters I wrote to our Most Serene King [Christian IV] while I was at Rostock, together with the answer to them</b>; and, if I remember rightly, he named here <b>Knud Rud</b>, who he thought had received such things from <b>Carl Brusch</b>. If it is so, I should wish that those who are made partakers of these have the things written back to me <b>explained more rightly than the letter itself pretends</b> — which might perhaps seem plausible to the incautious and ignorant, though the matter stands far otherwise; and it would be most easy for me (as you yourself know) to rebut all the objections there and demonstrate the contrary — but the things I said before obstruct [me], and the authority of those who craftily patched these together now prevails over truth. <b>Patience, then, is needed; and at last the good cause triumphs — if not in Denmark, at least elsewhere.</b> O would that the Virgilian [line] did not soon fit us: <i>\"All the gods, by whom this empire had stood, have departed, the sanctuaries and altars left\"</i>; likewise that, a little before: <i>\"We have been Trojans; Ilium has been, and the vast glory of the Danes.\"</i> For a kingdom that is to be punished by Heaven is wont to be <b>deprived of sound counsels, and torn into factions by certain men's craft, and, under the pretext of truth and good, to do contrary things covertly</b>. And all such things are wont to precede [a fall] — not a few of which are heard there, if only what is narrated agrees with the truth.</p><p>When I had asked the aforesaid Rosenkrantz about <b>the \"characterized\" herring found in Norway</b> [a herring marked with figures], he could indeed report nothing certain of it; yet he most certainly affirmed that <b>another kind of fish — which the Latins call <i>aculeatum</i>, our people <i>Hornfisk</i> [garfish] — marked with far more wonderful characters, was lately found near the island of Tåsinge</b> (or rather a little [island] adjacent to it); the formation of which figures, if anything is established to you on this matter, I should wish communicated to me. Your <b>Cramer</b> afterward wrote me, and enclosed a certain poem in the letter; <b>Brasch too, the Rostock Poet, sent a Heroic [poem] composed in my honour</b> — but neither pleases enough. You have both here, however: Cramer's in his own hand, with the letter, as I received it (for I have no leisure to copy it) — which, however, since I have no copy of it, I should wish returned to me. I have besides others of the kind, among which is <b>a very prolix one from East Frisia</b>; and more are being struck, here and there, in memory of the matter well done among us. <b>Germany eagerly awaits your response on behalf of its neighbour [Denmark].</b> I too await <b>those commentaries you know of</b>, in which I may by occasion interweave my own; and when yours are finished, <b>share them quietly with our Krag [Niels Krag]</b>, that he too may interpose his opinion, from the history of the transacted matter which he for the greater part knows. A copy of those which <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> wrote me from Prague some weeks ago I sent already to Lord Doctor <b>Nicolaus Krag</b>, who doubtless made you partaker of it. But these days too I have again received some brief [letters] from the same Thaddaeus, of which you have here the copy; he expedites the matter in fewer words, since he doubted whether I was here, or had set out on the journey toward Prague. <b>You see they seriously seek and await my arrival, and that things concerning me have already become known to the Emperor himself.</b> Do you and Krag consult together, and quietly fish out the opinion of others too, to whom my affairs are seriously and sincerely at heart. I can conclude nothing certain before <b>Tengnagel and Mule</b> have returned to me.</p><p>Yesterday Lord Doctor <b>Veit Winsheim</b> was with me, just returned from the Counts of Oldenburg (where he had been to settle a certain dissension among them); he reported, among other things, about Doctor <b>Bökel</b> — that he presses for the money promised him for the books you received; but that you had written him not to give Bökel that money before he produced all the remaining books, which I understand he either will not or cannot do, alleging that only three or four remain, of no great moment, and not in his power. Winsheim therefore asked me what I thought he should do in this; I advised that he comply with your will and keep the money by him, until [Bökel] had in all things satisfied your wishes and his own promise. But he objected that he must soon go to Berlin and into Meissen, and that Bökel would not lack the money so long — nay, said stomachfully that he would rather take back his books than wait longer for that money. So we resolved that, in his absence, Lord Doctor Vitus would give the money sent by you to another trustworthy man of his friends at Hamburg; and if meanwhile an answer came from you (for he hinted he had written you of this), his son who remains there would open your letter and execute this business according to its contents. It is for you, then, to signify in good time what you wish done in these. If I were in your place, I would either restore him the books (if he so wishes), or grant him the money already assigned — even though he will not, or perhaps cannot, procure those few books — that you may be freed from him; for he is a bit morose, and besides (which yet he has in common with many) <b>ruled by his wife [γυναικοκρατούμενος], his Empress being too importunate</b> — of which Mule will give proof. I nonetheless will give effect, so far as in me lies, to whatever you wish done in this.</p><p>The <b>wagon</b> which you promised before Easter, then Whitsun, nay and the time of St. John the Baptist [24 June], has disappointed [me]; and though you are a Cimbrian [Jutlander], and enjoy your nation's privilege [of slowness], I should rather think you have something of <b>Scanian constancy</b> beyond the custom of the rest — especially since by marriage you are somewhat transplanted into a Scanian — and that therefore it was neglected through some carelessness of those to whom you entrusted this business. But be it what it will: I should not wish to be burdensome to you in this — as in no other thing; I only desire to know what is to be expected, or not, lest I procure another to be made in vain. About my <b>Copenhagen house</b>, too, what can be done or not, I long to be made more certain by you. And now, with both your <b>Sophias</b> — adding besides that third one which is also called <b>Urania</b> — fare most happily. Greet brother <b>Axel</b>, and others, if my memory touch any there. May God guard you all from evil.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, the 23rd day of June, the year [15]98.<br>Yours, <b>Tycho Brahe.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> of Rosenholm — Tycho's kinsman. The <b>earliest</b> of the Wandsbek-to-Holger letters (cf. brahe-t_15980909001, brahe-t_15980925001), on St. John's Eve 1598.</li><li><b>Significance — a <i>Mechanica</i> erratum (Jacob Curtius!), Tycho's prophecy of Denmark's fall, prodigies, and the developing Prague prospect.</b></li><li><b>The <i>Mechanica</i> printer's-error (and the Curtius connection).</b> Tycho asks Holger to pen-correct an erratum on <b>page G.3</b> of the <i>Mechanica</i>, where <b>Jacob Curtius's invention for subdividing a quadrant</b> is credited: the praenomen abbreviation <b>\"IAC.\" was dropped</b> before \"Curtio,\" and <b>\"GENEROSE ADINVENTA\" was misprinted for \"INGENIOSE ADINVENTA.\"</b> A precise bibliographical datum — <i>and the same <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz)</b> whose Prague house Tycho would later receive as \"the new Uraniborg\"</i> (cf. brahe-t_16010414001): here Curtius is honoured in the <i>Mechanica</i> for a quadrant-subdivision device.</li><li><b>Danish legal troubles &amp; the prophecy.</b> Tycho's bitterness at Danish justice — the \"Pepler\" case to be heard at the <b>Diet</b> without due citation; his maxim that in Denmark even <b>geometric truth can be \"falsely accused\"</b> by enemies. He fears <b>leaked copies of his Rostock letters to Christian IV</b> (via <b>Knud Rud / Carl Brusch</b>) are spun against him. Climax: a <b>dark Virgilian prophecy of Denmark's fall</b>, quoting <i>Aeneid</i> 2 — <i>\"All the gods … have departed\"</i> and <i>\"We have been Trojans … and the vast glory of the Danes\"</i> — a kingdom \"to be punished by Heaven\" losing sound counsel.</li><li><b>Prodigies — the \"characterized fish.\"</b> A vivid wonder-report: a <b>herring marked with mysterious figures found in Norway</b>, and a <b>garfish (<i>Hornfisk</i>) \"marked with far more wonderful characters\" found near Tåsinge</b> — Tycho asks Holger for the shapes of the marks. (A datum for the prodigy/portent interest of the age.)</li><li><b>The Prague prospect (June 1598).</b> Already developing: <b>Hagecius</b> writes from Prague (forwarded via <b>Niels Krag</b>); \"they seriously seek and await my arrival, and things concerning me are already known to the Emperor.\" Tycho awaits <b>Tengnagel and Mule</b> before deciding.</li><li><b>The literary monument &amp; the Bökel business.</b> Poems from <b>Cramer</b>, <b>Brasch</b> (the Rostock poet), and a \"very prolix\" East-Frisian one; Tycho awaits Holger's \"commentaries\" (a defence of Denmark's side) to share with <b>Krag</b>. The <b>Bökel book-debt</b> (cf. brahe-t_15980925001, brahe-t_15980909001) drags on — Bökel \"morose and ruled by his wife [γυναικοκρατούμενος].\" Plus the perennially-late <b>wagon</b> (Holger's \"Jutish privilege\" of slowness) and Tycho's <b>Copenhagen house</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Jacob Curtius</b> thread (Mechanica credit ↔ the Prague house, brahe-t_16010414001); the Wandsbek-Holger cluster; the <b>Bökel</b> thread; the Prague-prospect/Hagecius thread; and a <b>prodigy/portent</b> note.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz; <b>Claus Mule</b>; <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz)</b>; <b>Jacob Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>\"Pepler\"</b> (an unidentified Danish legal case — flagged); <b>Christian IV</b>; <b>Knud Rud</b>, <b>Carl Brusch</b>; <b>Daniel Cramer</b>; <b>Martin Brasch</b>; <b>Niels Krag</b>; <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b>; <b>Veit Winsheim</b>; <b>Johann Bökel</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>brother Axel</b>; Holger's <b>wife Sophie</b> + \"Urania.\" Places: <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Uraniborg/Hven</b>, <b>Funen</b>, <b>Tåsinge</b>, <b>Norway</b>, <b>Rostock</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Berlin</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, East Frisia.</li><li><b>Greek/Latin notes.</b> γυναικοκρατούμενος (\"ruled by his wife\"); <i>falcatis insimulari</i> (\"falsely accused,\" lit. \"with sickles\" — a textual oddity rendered by sense). The Virgil tags adapt <i>Aen.</i> 2.351–352 and 2.325 (with \"<i>ingens gloria Danorum</i>\" substituted for \"<i>Teucrorum</i>\").</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 23. Iunij Anni 98</i> at Wandsbek (Holstein O.S.; \"St. John the Baptist's time\" = 24 June confirms the season) → <b>23 June 1598 [Old Style]</b> (no <i>stylo</i> marker; label gives no N.S.).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.74: 'falcatis' is corrected to 'falsitatis' ('falfitatis infimulari' in Dreyer's long-s type, l.34), restoring 'accused of falsehood'; the English translation's sense was already correct ('falsely accused'), with only its bracketed citation of the Latin word updated to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Mechanica/Jacob-Curtius erratum</b>; the Danish-justice complaint, the <b>leaked Rostock letters</b>, and the <b>Virgilian prophecy of Denmark's fall</b>; the <b>\"characterized fish\" prodigies</b>; the <b>Prague prospect via Hagecius/Krag</b>; the poems; and the <b>Bökel</b> and <b>wagon</b> business) is firmly construed, with Tycho's bitter and prophetic tone rendered faithfully. The <b>\"Pepler\" legal case</b> is left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.74-76 in full (the letter's actual span within the 5-page slice) and collated the entire letter body — opening salutation, the Mechanica-errata paragraph, the Diet/Pepler/Rosenkrantz-Rud-Brusch passage with the two Virgil quotations, the herring/Hornfisk-Tåsinge passage, the Cramer/Brasch/Hagecius/Krag/Tengnagel correspondence paragraph, the Winsheim/Boekel money paragraph, the wagon/Copenhagen-house closing, and the date '23 Iunij Anni 98' — against the page images (not OCR). Every proper name, number, and date checked matches Dreyer exactly except one corrupted Latin word (falcatis for falsitatis) in our own source_text, which is the sole defect found; the English translation itself contains no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints \"falcatis\" (an ungrammatical, non-existent form) where Dreyer prints \"falsitatis\" (\"accused of falsehood\"); the Latin is corrupted at this word, though the English gloss's paraphrase (\"falsely accused\") happens to land close to Dreyer's actual sense. Dreyer prints: <i>poffe nihilominus ea ipfa apud quofdam, qui iuftitiae praeeffe volunt, falfitatis infimulari (TBOO VIII p.74, l.34)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The sole TEXT_ERROR defect was verified directly against the Dreyer page image for TBOO VIII p.74 (within the letter's pp.73-77 slice, pages 1-4). 'falfitatis infimulari' is clearly legible in the long-s typeface and confirms the audit's claim that the source's 'falcatis' is a corruption of 'falsitatis'. The translation's sense required no change; only the bracketed inline Latin citation was brought into sync with the corrected word. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_15980508001", "next": "brahe-t_15980628001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports two printing errors in the Mechanica — a missing 'I' before 'Curtio' and 'GENEROSE' printed for 'INGENIOSE' — and asks Rosenkrantz to correct them.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980623001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980625001", "d": "1598-06-25", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne", "ck": "Ernst, kurfyrste, ærkebiskop af Köln", "ck2": "Henrik Rantzau", "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Liège", "t": "Ernst of Bavaria, Elector and Archbishop of Cologne → Heinrich Rantzau — Liège, 25 June 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>COPIA LITERARUM SERENISS: ELECTORIS COLONIENSIS AD DOMINUM HENRICUM RANZOUIUM.</p><p>ERNESTUS electus et confirmatus archiep[i]scopus Coloniensis, sacri Romani imperij per Italiam archicancellarius et princeps elector, episcopus Leodiensis, palatinus Rheny, utriusque Bauariae dux. Clementi salutatione Nostra premissa. Nobilissime singulariter dilecte mihi et illis studijs, in quibus tam preclaram Braheus operam posuit, nihil est nunc gratius et literis a te missis nihil acceptius, et tua etiam commendatione nihil grauius, et ipso Braheo, ut mores et doctrina viri merentur, nihil fuit neque erit commendatius. Vidi, quae misit sublimibus de illis specimina, mire placuerunt et reliquorum magnam sitim in animo meo excitauerunt. Perge, Ranzoui praestantissime, affinem illum, te dignum, Daniae totique Germaniae gloriosum, fouere. Ego, quod potero, et ipsius causa, et quia uideo tibi, quem, ut aequum est, facio maximi, rem curae esse, diligenter eum, ut feci, caes: ma:ti commendare pergam, et non committam, ut meam in se beneuolentiam ulla ex parte sentiat claudier. Ex eodem Francisco Tengnaglio haec eadem copiosius intelliges.</p><p>Vale.<br>Leody xxv junij 1598.<br>Ernestus mpp.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of the letter of the Most Serene Elector of Cologne to Master Heinrich Rantzau.</b></p><p>Ernst, elected and confirmed Archbishop of Cologne, Arch-Chancellor of the Holy Roman Empire throughout Italy and Prince-Elector, Bishop of Liège, Count Palatine of the Rhine, Duke of both Bavarias. Our clement greeting premised. Most noble, singularly beloved: to me, and to those studies in which Brahe has placed such distinguished effort, nothing is now more pleasing, and than the letters sent by you nothing more acceptable, and than your recommendation also nothing weightier; and than Brahe himself — as the man's character and learning deserve — nothing was, nor will be, more commended [to me]. I have seen the specimens which he sent concerning those lofty matters; they pleased me marvellously, and excited in my mind a great thirst for the rest. Continue, most excellent Rantzau, to cherish that kinsman of yours — worthy of you, and glorious to Denmark and to all Germany. I, for what I shall be able, both for his sake and because I see that the matter is a care to you (whom, as is just, I esteem most highly), will continue diligently — as I have done — to commend him to His Imperial Majesty, and will not allow him to feel my benevolence toward him to be in any part curtailed. From the same Frans Tengnagel you will understand these same things more copiously.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Liège, the 25th of June, 1598.<br>Ernst, with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>The companion to brahe-t_15980625002.</b> This is the <b>copy of Elector Ernst's covering letter to Heinrich Rantzau</b>, written the same day as (and enclosed with / paralleling) his direct letter to Tycho. Where the letter <i>to Tycho</i> thanks him for his books, this one <i>to Rantzau</i> answers Rantzau's recommendation and confirms the Elector's continued intercession for Tycho with the Emperor.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>, Elector-Archbishop of Cologne and Bishop of Liège (see brahe-t_15980625002 for the full titulature note).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), Holstein humanist and Danish <i>Statholder</i>, Tycho's patron and <i>affinis</i> (kinsman by marriage — <i>affinem illum</i>); the broker of this princely intercession. (Cf. brahe-t_15860301001, where Rantzau is also the conduit.)</li><li><b><i>Braheus … specimina sublimibus de illis</b></i> — the \"specimens concerning those lofty matters\" = the works/observations Tycho had sent (his printed books), which whetted the Elector's appetite for more. <b><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</b></i> = <b>Frans Tengnagel</b>, the bearer who would relate the rest.</li><li><b><i>claudier</b></i> — archaic/poetic passive infinitive of <i>claudo</i> (\"to be closed/curtailed\"); rendered \"to be … curtailed.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Leody xxv junij 1598</i> = <b>25 June 1598</b>, Liège (Catholic) = <b>New Style</b> (as in the parallel letter).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Latin is secure, and the document's role (the Elector's recommendation answered to Rantzau, paralleling the letter to Tycho) is unambiguous; persons (Ernst, Rantzau, Tengnagel) corpus-corroborated. The archaic <i>claudier</i> is noted, not a crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.140 (Dreyer letter 148, 'Copia literarum Sereniss: Electoris Coloniensis ad Dominum Henricum Ranzouium') as a page image; letter is entire on this page. Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word including all titles (archiepiscopus Coloniensis, archicancellarius, episcopus Leodiensis, palatinus Rheny, utriusque Bauariae dux), the reference to Frans Tengnagel, and the dateline 'Leody xxv junij 1598'. English translation checked clause-by-clause: faithful throughout, no fabrication or omission.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Expresses keen pleasure at Brahe's work samples and Rantzau's recommendation, and promises to continue cherishing Brahe as worthy of Denmark and Germany.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980625001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980625002", "d": "1598-06-25", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne", "ck": "Ernst, kurfyrste, ærkebiskop af Köln", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Liège", "t": "Ernst of Bavaria, Elector and Archbishop of Cologne → Tycho Brahe — Liège, 25 June 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO NOBIS SINGULARITER DILECTO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP.</p><p>ERNESTUS electus et confirmatus archiepiscopus Coloniensis, sacri Romani imperij per Italiam archicancellarius et princeps elector, episcopus Leodiensis, palatinus Rheny, dux utriusque Bauarie. Clementi Nostra salutatione premissa. Nobilissime singulariter dilecte, quas mihi iuuenis ille notus a te literas dedit et quae de rebus tuis addidit, singulari laetitia legi et audiui. Munus vero litterarium librorum tuorum, quos ut a te rerum istarum peritissimo profectos et de coelestibus illis corporibus tractantes, quorum vel solus aspectus idiotarum oculos pascit, cognitio uero et meum et omnium peritorum animos honestiore uoluptate moratur, mihi supra modum fuit acceptum. Gratias pro ijs tibi ago et quod factum a me hactenus non indiligenter, pergam sedulo te tuaque caes: mti: commendare. Declaraui hunc animi mei sensum latori literarum tuarum et harum relatori. Ex eo intelliges, quae de omnibus, quae scire cupiebas, illum feci certiorem. Vale et tibi persuade et tua et studiorum caussa me quiduis conaturum.</p><p>Leody xxv iunij 1598.<br>Ernestus, mpp.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble, singularly beloved by us, Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup.</b></p><p>Ernst, elected and confirmed Archbishop of Cologne, Arch-Chancellor of the Holy Roman Empire throughout Italy and Prince-Elector, Bishop of Liège, Count Palatine of the Rhine, Duke of both Bavarias. Our clement greeting premised. Most noble, singularly beloved: the letters which that known youth gave me from you, and the things he added about your affairs, I read and heard with singular gladness. But the literary gift of your books — which, as proceeding from you, most skilled in those matters, and as treating of those celestial bodies whose very sight alone feeds the eyes of the unlearned, but whose knowledge engages both my mind and the minds of all the skilled with a more honourable pleasure — was beyond measure acceptable to me. I give you thanks for them; and, as has been done by me hitherto not undiligently, I will continue diligently to commend you and your affairs to His Imperial Majesty. I have declared this sentiment of my mind to the bearer of your letters and the reporter of these [present ones]. From him you will understand the things whereof I have made him more certain, concerning all that you desired to know. Farewell; and persuade yourself that, both for your sake and for the sake of [your] studies, I will attempt anything.</p><p>Liège, the 25th of June, 1598.<br>Ernst, with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b> (1554–1612), Wittelsbach prince, <b>Elector and Archbishop of Cologne</b> (from 1583), also <b>Prince-Bishop of Liège</b> (<i>episcopus Leodiensis</i>; <i>Leodium / Leody</i> = Liège), Münster, Hildesheim and Freising — here writing from <b>Liège</b>. The full electoral titulature (Arch-Chancellor of the Empire <i>throughout Italy</i>; Count Palatine of the Rhine; <i>Duke of both Bavarias</i>) is reproduced.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Tycho had sent Ernst a <b>gift of his books</b> (the <i>literary gift</i>, <i>munus litterarium</i> — his recently printed works, e.g. the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> / <i>Mechanica</i> / the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>) and a verbal report of his affairs through a young messenger. Ernst thanks him warmly and — crucially — promises to <b>continue commending Tycho to His Imperial Majesty (Rudolf II)</b>: this is part of the network of princely intercessions (cf. the Cologne, Hesse, and Hamburg channels) by which Tycho secured his Bohemian/imperial position in 1598–99.</li><li><b>Sentiment.</b> The fine antithesis <i>quorum vel solus aspectus idiotarum oculos pascit, cognitio vero … honestiore voluptate moratur</i> — \"whose mere sight feeds the eyes of the unlearned [<i>idiotae</i> = laymen, non-specialists], but whose knowledge engages the learned with a more honourable pleasure\" — distinguishes the vulgar gaze at the heavens from the philosopher's understanding. <i>idiotae</i> rendered \"the unlearned,\" not \"idiots.\"</li><li><b><i>mpp</b></i> = <i>manu propria</i> (with his own hand).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Leody xxv iunij 1598</i> = <b>25 June 1598</b>; Cologne/Liège (Catholic, and an electoral chancery) used the <b>Gregorian / New Style</b> calendar (adopted in the Catholic Rhineland from 1583). Taken as N.S.; header so marked.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Latin is rendered securely; the sender (Elector Ernst of Cologne), the occasion (thanks for Tycho's books and the promise of intercession with Rudolf II), and the date are all clear and corpus-consistent. The titulature and the <i>idiotae</i> antithesis are preserved without sharpening.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.140 (Dreyer letter 149, 'Nobilissimo nobis singulariter dilecto Tychoni Brahe, Domino de Knudstrup') as a page image, same page as the companion letter to Rantzau (150625001). Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word including the full titulature, the reference to the book-gift, Tycho's bearer/messenger, and the dateline 'Leody xxv iunij 1598'. English translation checked clause-by-clause: faithful throughout, no invented facts or dropped content.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's gift of books and the report of his affairs brought by the bearer, and pledges continued commendation to the Emperor.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980625002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980626001", "d": "1598-06-26", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne", "ck": "Ernst, kurfyrste, ærkebiskop af Köln", "ck2": "Rudolf 2.", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Liège", "t": "Ernst of Bavaria, Elector-Archbishop of Cologne → Emperor Rudolf II — Liège, 26 June 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (princely chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift — copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN DIE RÖMISKE KAY: MAYTT:.</p><p>ALLERGNEDIGSTER herr vnd vetter. E: Röm: Kay: Maytt: kann jch in vnterthenigen vertrawen nicht verhalten, wie das mir der ansehenliche vnd hochberumbte Tycho Brahe gehorsamblich zuerkennen geben, wie daß ehr auß etlichen hochwichtigen vrsachen sein vaterlandt das königreich Dennemarck zuuerlassen, vnd sich mit allen seinen jnstrumentis mathematicis vnter E: Kay: Maytt: allergnedigste protection in dem Teutschlandt niderzuthun, gentzlichen endschlossen sey. Vnd wie woll sein doctrina vnd berumbter löblicher nahme dermasßen jn vnd ausser Teutschlandt nicht allein, sondern auch E: Kay: Maytt: zuuorderst gnedigst woll bekandt, so hat ehr doch auß dem hohen vertrawen, so ehr propter conformia studia zu mir gesetzt, ahn mich gehorsamblich begert, sein personn vnd löbliche intention E: Kay: Maytt: vnterthenigst zu recommendiren. Weil mir dan vorhin wolbewust, dass E: Kay: Maytt: summus fautor vnnd protector aller gelehrter leut, vnndt insonderheit vnter andern dem mathematico studio geneigt sein, so hab jch desto eher diese stoltzigkeit auf mich genommen, dem ehrlichen vnd berumbten man in seinem begeren zuwilfahrenn. Belanget der halben ahn E: Kay: Maytt: mein vnterthenigste bitt, sie wollen jhnen Tycho Brahe sambt seinen studijs in jhren kayserlichen schutz vnnd promotion auf- vnd annehmen, auch sich auf seine weitere vorschleg vnd gehorsamblich begeren dermassen allergnedigst resoluiren, damit ehr Tycho Brahe sehenn vnnd spuhrenn möge, das seine löbliche, hochberumbtte vnd indefessi labores ein gnedigsten maecenatem bekommen haben. Solche ertzeigte gnadt will nit allein jch, sondern tota Germanica patria wirdt gleichsfalß gegen E: Kay: Maytt: gehorsambst zuuerdienen alletzeit geflisßen sein. Weil auch seine merita dermassen geschaffen, alßo das ehr billich pater astronomorum et unicus hujus laudatissimae scientiae restaurator mag genendt werden, so halt jch weitere recommendation vor vnnöttig, sondern das jch vielmehr E: Kay: Maytt: vnterthenigst deßhalben zu congratuliren schuldig mich erkenne, weil derhalben mit meinen weitern schreiben E: Kay: Maytt: nit auffhalten, sondern thue mich derselben zu kayserlichen hulden vnnd gnaden vnterthenigst beuehlenn.</p><p>Luttich d. 26. iunij 98.<br>E: Kay: Maytt: gehorsamer churfurst vnnd vetter<br>Ernst, ertzbischoff zu Cöllen.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Roman Imperial Majesty.</b></p><p>Most gracious lord and cousin. I cannot, in humble confidence, withhold from Your Roman Imperial Majesty how the distinguished and highly renowned Tycho Brahe has obediently made known to me that he, for several highly weighty reasons, is wholly resolved to leave his fatherland the Kingdom of Denmark, and to settle in Germany, with all his mathematical instruments, under Your Imperial Majesty's most gracious protection. And although his learning and renowned, laudable name are well known not only within and beyond Germany, but also, and above all, most graciously to Your Imperial Majesty, he has nonetheless — out of the high trust he has placed in me on account of our kindred studies — obediently desired of me that I commend his person and laudable intention most humbly to Your Imperial Majesty. Since, then, it was well known to me beforehand that Your Imperial Majesty is the supreme patron and protector of all learned men, and especially inclined, among other [studies], to the mathematical study, I have the more readily taken this boldness upon myself, to comply with the honest and renowned man in his request. There pertains, therefore, to Your Imperial Majesty my most humble petition: that you would take up and receive Tycho Brahe, together with his studies, into your imperial protection and advancement, and would resolve so most graciously upon his further proposals and obedient request, that he, Tycho Brahe, may see and feel that his laudable, most renowned and indefatigable labours have obtained a most gracious Maecenas. Such shown grace not I alone, but the whole German fatherland, will likewise be ever zealous to deserve, most obediently, toward Your Imperial Majesty. And since his merits are of such a kind that he may rightly be called the father of astronomers and the sole restorer of this most praiseworthy science, I hold further recommendation needless; rather I acknowledge myself bound, much more, to congratulate Your Imperial Majesty most humbly on that account. Wherefore I will not detain Your Imperial Majesty with further writing of mine, but commend myself most humbly to the same's imperial favour and graces.</p><p>Liège, the 26th of June [15]98.<br>Your Imperial Majesty's obedient Elector and cousin,<br>Ernst, Archbishop of Cologne.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; significance.</b> This is the actual <b>letter of recommendation</b> by which the Elector-Archbishop of Cologne commended Tycho to <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> — the document Tycho refers to, and circulated copies of, across his patronage-seeking correspondence of 1598 (<i>Quae Coloniensis mea de causa Imperatori scripsit</i> in brahe-t_15980905001; cf. brahe-t_16000420001). It is a key instrument in Tycho's transition from exile to imperial service.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b> (1554–1612), <b>Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b> and Prince-Bishop of Liège (hence writing from <b>Liège / Lüttich</b>), brother of Duke Wilhelm V of Bavaria — a leading Catholic prince and himself a patron of mathematics and astrology. He addresses the Emperor as <i>Herr und Vetter</i> (\"lord and cousin\"), the conventional form among Imperial princes.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> (<i>Römische Kaiserliche Majestät</i>).</li><li><b>The macaronic register (protocol §2).</b> The letter is chancery German studded with <b>Latin technical and rhetorical phrases</b>, preserved verbatim and rendered in place: <i>instrumenta mathematica</i> (\"mathematical instruments\"), <i>propter conformia studia</i> (\"on account of kindred studies\"), <i>summus fautor</i> (\"supreme patron\"), <i>indefessi labores</i> (\"indefatigable labours\"), <i>Maecenas</i> (the type of the patron), <i>tota Germanica patria</i> (\"the whole German fatherland\"), and — the rhetorical climax — <b>pater astronomorum et unicus huius laudatissimae scientiae restaurator</b>, \"the father of astronomers and the sole <b>restorer</b> of this most praiseworthy science.\" The <i>restaurator/restitutio</i> language is Tycho's own programme-term (<i>astronomia instaurata</i>, §5.1), here deployed by his advocate.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Lüttich</i> = <b>Liège</b>, seat of Ernst's prince-bishopric.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>E: Röm: Kay: Maytt:</i> = <i>Eure Römische Kaiserliche Majestät</i> (\"Your Roman Imperial Majesty,\" declined throughout); subscription <i>gehorsamer Churfürst und Vetter</i> → \"obedient Elector and cousin.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>d. 26. Iunij 98</i> at Liège; the dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b> — correct, the Catholic Rhineland and prince-bishoprics having adopted the Gregorian calendar. Reproduced as written; the year is given in the source as \"98.\"</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> Survives as an <b>afskrift (copy)</b> in Langebek's Diplomatarium at the Copenhagen Rigsarkiv — fittingly, since Tycho himself kept and distributed copies of it.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a clear princely-chancery recommendation; sender, recipient, and purpose are unambiguous, and the embedded Latin epithets (notably <i>pater astronomorum … restaurator</i>) are read and rendered securely. No textual crux; the macaronic German/Latin register is preserved per protocol §2.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer XIV pp. 140-142; letter is no. 150, entirely on p. 141). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed German. Checked every name and title (Tycho Brahe, Rudolf II via 'Röm: Kay: Maytt:'), the Latin epithet 'pater astronomorum et unicus hujus laudatissimae scientiae restaurator', the place 'Luttich'/Liège, and the date '26. iunij 98' (=1598). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Commends to the Emperor Tycho Brahe's resolve to leave Denmark and settle in Germany under imperial protection, praising his learning and renown.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980626001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980626002", "d": "1598-06-26", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne", "ck": "Ernst, kurfyrste, ærkebiskop af Köln", "ck2": "Johann Anton Barwitz", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Liège", "t": "Ernst of Bavaria, Elector and Archbishop of Cologne → Johann Anton Barvitius — Liège, 26 June 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (with extensive Latin code-switching) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN BARUITIUM, JHRER KAY: MAYTT: GEHEYMEN SECRETARIUM.</p><p>Ernst etc.</p><p>WAS Wir jhrer kay: maytt: wegen des weitberuhmbten Tychonis Brahe vnterthenig zuschreiben, das hastu ab dem einschluss zu[v]ernehmen. Jst darauf Vnser gnedigst gesinnen an dich, wan du vernehmen wurdest, das solche schreiben jhrer kay: maytt: vberantworttet werdenn, das du pro uiribus daran vnd darob seyst, damit dieser ehrlicher man balt bescheidt auf seine aequissimas praetensiones haben möge, dann diese zeugnuss Wir ihme geben können, das ehr unicus restaurator hujus laudatissimae scientiae, vnd das Vnser patria Germanica jhme selbst nit allein congratuliren, sondern apertis ulnis et brachijs zu empfahen geflissen sein soll. Vndt weil sein personn vnd studia dermassen per uniuersum orbem beruhmbt, auch vnter andern dem gewesenen vicecansellario Kurzio gar woll bekandt gewest, so achten Wir vor vnnöttig, dir sein personn weiter zu recommendiren. Allein haben Wir dir auch zu erkennen geben wollen, das Wir jhme Tychoni Brahen, tum propter conformia studia tum propter eius insignia erga artem mathematicam merita, mit sondern gnaden zugethan sein, alsso das Wir Vnß zu dir gnedigst versehen, du werdest jhnen auch von vnserntwegen bey j: kay: maytt: gehorsamblich zurecommendiren nit vnterlassenn, vnndt Wir bleiben dir mit gnaden gewogen.</p><p>Luttich den 26. iunij 98.<br>Ernst, ertzbischoff zu Cöllenn.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Barvitius, His Imperial Majesty's secret secretary.</b></p><p>Ernst, etc.</p><p>What we are humbly writing to His Imperial Majesty concerning the far-famed Tycho Brahe, that you will gather from the enclosure. Thereupon it is our most gracious desire of you that, when you perceive that such writings are delivered to His Imperial Majesty, you be, <i>to the best of your powers</i> [<i>pro viribus</i>], at it and over it, so that this honourable man may soon have a decision upon his <i>most equitable claims</i> [<i>aequissimas praetensiones</i>]; for this testimony we are able to give him: that he is the <i>sole restorer of this most praiseworthy science</i> [<i>unicus restaurator huius laudatissimae scientiae</i>], and that our <i>German fatherland</i> [<i>patria Germanica</i>] should not only congratulate itself on him, but should be zealous to receive him <i>with open arms and embrace</i> [<i>apertis ulnis et brachiis</i>]. And since his person and <i>studies</i> are so renowned <i>throughout the whole world</i> [<i>per universum orbem</i>] — and were, among others, very well known to the late vice-chancellor Kurz [Curtius] — we deem it unnecessary to recommend his person further to you. Only, we wished also to make known to you that we are devoted to him, Tycho Brahe, with especial grace — <i>both on account of [our] conformable studies and on account of his signal merits toward the mathematical art</i> [<i>tum propter conformia studia tum propter eius insignia erga artem mathematicam merita</i>] — so that we most graciously trust in you that you, on our behalf also, will not fail obediently to recommend him to His Imperial Majesty; and we remain graciously inclined toward you.</p><p>Liège, the 26th of June, [15]98.<br>Ernst, Archbishop of Cologne.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>The third Cologne letter of 25–26 June 1598.</b> With brahe-t_15980625002 (to Tycho) and brahe-t_15980625001 (to Rantzau), this completes Elector <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>'s coordinated push: here he instructs the <b>imperial secretary</b> to expedite Tycho's suit once the Elector's memorial reaches the Emperor.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b> (Barvitius, c. 1555–1620), <i>Geheimsekretär</i> (privy secretary) to <b>Rudolf II</b> — the official who controlled access to the Emperor's desk. Ernst presses him to ensure Tycho gets a prompt favourable <i>Bescheid</i> (decision) on his <i>aequissimae praetensiones</i> (\"most equitable claims,\" i.e. for a post and stipend).</li><li><b>The praise.</b> <i>unicus restaurator huius laudatissimae scientiae</i> — \"the sole restorer of this most praiseworthy science\" (Tycho's <i>instauratio astronomiae</i>, per glossary §5.1); the <i>patria Germanica</i> should receive him \"with open arms\" (<i>apertis ulnis et brachiis</i>).</li><li><b><i>vicecancellarius Kurzius</b></i> = <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacob Curtius), the late Imperial Vice-Chancellor (d. 1594), who had known Tycho's work (and who reappears as Herwart's intimate friend in brahe-t_16000908001).</li><li><b>Code-switching.</b> A German chancery letter saturated with Latin technical/legal tags (<i>pro viribus, aequissimas praetensiones, unicus restaurator …, apertis ulnis et brachiis, per universum orbem, conformia studia, insignia … merita</i>); each is rendered into English and flagged as the Elector's own Latin within the German (per §2). <i>tum propter conformia studia</i> — \"both on account of [our] conformable/like studies\": Ernst, himself astronomically inclined, claims a shared pursuit.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Lüttich [Liège] den 26. iunij 98</i> = <b>26 June 1598</b>; Liège/Cologne (Catholic) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German with its Latin insets is rendered securely; the recipient (Barvitius, Rudolf II's privy secretary), the purpose (expediting Tycho's imperial suit), and the cast (Kurz/Curtius, the Cologne–Prague intercession) are all corpus-corroborated.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (entry 151) against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.141-142, reading the page images; the German text and all embedded Latin phrases (pro viribus, aequissimas praetensiones, unicus restaurator huius laudatissimae scientiae, patria Germanica, apertis ulnis et brachiis, per universum orbem, tum propter conformia studia...) match Dreyer exactly, and the bracketed English glosses of those Latin phrases are all accurate, including the correct rendering of 'gewesenen vicecantzlario Kurzio' as 'the late vice-chancellor Kurz'. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks the imperial secretary to ensure Tycho receives a prompt decision on his petition, calling him the sole restorer of astronomy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980626002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980627001", "d": "1598-06-27", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Niels Hemmingsen", "ck": "Niels Hemmingsen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Roskilde", "t": "Niels Hemmingsen → Tycho Brahe — Roskilde, 27 June 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Wrocław, III R. 243</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE, SAPIENTIA, PIETATE ET VIRTUTE PRAESTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO PATRONO ET AMICO VETERI SUMMA OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.</p><p>CLARISSIME vir ac frater in Domino dilecte, CLAUDIUS MULERUS tuus domesticus tradidit mihi literas tuas humanissime scriptas, ex quibus intelligo te memorem esse veteris nostrae consuetudinis atque amicitiae, id quod mihi pergratum et periucundum fuit. Rogo Deum optim. max. ut te quam diutissime sanum et incolumem cum tota familiâ conservet. Etsi autem per valetudinem non licet prolixius scribere, tamen animus meus, donec vixero, tibi adhaerebit sincerus. Quod autem salutes me nomine MONAVIJ praestantissimi viri HERMANNI et DANIELIS BUCRETIJ, est mihi pergratum, precorque illis vicissim et animi et corporis constantem valetudinem, et rogo te clarissime vir, ut in tuis ad eos literis vicissim salutem dicas meo nomine. Porro quod MONAVIUS petat copiam quarundam literarum PHILIPPI ad me scriptarum, non possum ipsius voto satisfacere. Nam et ipsae li[terae] perierunt et fuerunt scriptae de privatis negotijs et politicis non publicandis etc. Quare rogo, ut boni consulas, quod non possim in hac re tuae petitioni satisfacere, alias promptissimus ad omnia praestanda, quae tibi intellexero fore grata. Vale vir clarissime cum familia tua et domesticis omnibus. Deus te conservet, amen.</p><p>Roschildiae 27. Iunij Anno 1598.<br>NICOLAUS HEMMINGIUS,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most excellent in nobility, wisdom, piety, and virtue, Master Tycho Brahe, his lord, patron, and old friend, to be honoured with the utmost respect.</b></p><p>Most distinguished man and brother beloved in the Lord, Claude Muler, your domestic, delivered to me your most kindly-written letter, from which I understand that you are mindful of our old acquaintance and friendship — which was most pleasing and most delightful to me. I pray God Best and Greatest to preserve you, sound and unharmed, as long as possible, with your whole household. And although, by reason of [my] health, I am not permitted to write at greater length, yet my mind, as long as I shall live, will cleave to you, sincere. But that you greet me in the name of Monau, [and] of the most excellent men Hermann and Daniel Bucretius, is most pleasing to me; and I pray for them in turn constant health of mind and body, and I ask you, most distinguished man, that in your letters to them you, in turn, say a greeting in my name. Further, as to Monau's seeking a copy of certain letters of Philip [Melanchthon] written to me — I cannot satisfy his wish. For both the letters themselves have perished, and they were written about private and political matters, not to be published, etc. Wherefore I ask that you take it in good part that I cannot, in this matter, satisfy your request — being otherwise most ready to perform everything which I shall understand will be pleasing to you. Farewell, most distinguished man, with your family and all your household. May God preserve you, amen.</p><p>Roskilde, 27 June, in the year 1598.<br>Niels Hemmingsen,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Niels Hemmingsen</b> (Nicolaus Hemmingius, 1513–1600), the most eminent Danish Lutheran theologian of his age and a pupil of <b>Melanchthon</b>; by 1598 very old (he died 1600) and living in retirement at <b>Roskilde</b> (he had been removed from his Copenhagen chair in 1579 on a charge of Calvinist leanings). He addresses Tycho as <i>frater in Domino dilecte</i> (\"brother beloved in the Lord\") and \"old friend.\"</li><li><b>Bearer.</b> <i>Claudius Mulerus, tuus domesticus</i> = <b>Claude Muler</b> (Mulerius), a French member of Tycho's household, who carried the letter (he reappears as the would-be courier in brahe-t_16000919001).</li><li><b>The Melanchthon letters.</b> <i>Monavius</i> = <b>Jacob Monau</b> (Monavius, 1546–1603), the Breslau (Wrocław) humanist — which explains the <b>Wrocław</b> provenance of the witness. Monau had asked, through Tycho, for copies of certain <b>letters of Philip Melanchthon</b> (<i>Philippi</i>) to Hemmingsen; Hemmingsen declines: they have perished, and concerned <i>private and political matters not to be published</i>. (Hemmingsen does <b>not</b> sharpen or apologise beyond this; the refusal is preserved as written.)</li><li><b><i>Hermann and Daniel Bucretius</b></i> — two further men whom Tycho had greeted on Monau's behalf; <b>flagged</b>: read as a pair (likely brothers) sharing the surname <i>Bucretius</i>, but not securely re-identified; rendered \"Hermann and Daniel Bucretius.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Roschildiae 27. Iunij 1598</i> = <b>27 June 1598</b>; Denmark (Roskilde) = <b>Julian / Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the principals (Hemmingsen; the courier Claude Muler; Monau of Breslau; the declined Melanchthon letters) are firmly identified, the Wrocław witness confirming the Monau connection. Grade [B] for the unresolved identity/relationship of <i>Hermann and Daniel Bucretius</i>, flagged rather than asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address, body, and closing) against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.77, reading the page image (with pp.76 and 78 checked as neighbours to confirm the letter's boundaries against the surrounding letters). Text matches Dreyer word for word, including all proper names (Claudius Mulerus, Monavius, Hermannus and Danielis Bucretius, Philippus), the date (Roschildiae 27 Iunij 1598), and the closing formula. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wrosław, III R. 243."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to Tycho's letter, returns greetings to Monau and the Bucretius brothers, and addresses the request for copies of Melanchthon letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980627001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980628001", "d": "1598-06-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 28 June 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 8 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE TAM INGENIJ QUAM<br>GENERIS VIRO INSIGNITER CLARO, DOMINO OLIGERO ROSENCRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO AFFINI ET AMICO<br>MEO INTEGERRIMO.<br>S. P.</p><p>ETSI utrasque literas, utpote eas quas cum MULÆO misi, quasque postea ad te scripsi,<br>desideratissime mi OLIGERE,<br>tibi redditas esse non ambigam, atque ex ijs inter alia te cognovisse, quæ<br>Praga a veteri meo amico THADDÆO mihi perscripta sunt, tamen<br>cum ante paucos dies ab Amico illo meo Magdeburgensi, quem scis mihi scribere solitum esse, nonnullas acceperim, eadem de re quædam agentes, harum quoque<br>exemplum tecum communicandum duxi, quo plenius intelligeres qualis sit Cæsareæ Matis erga me<br>voluntas; præsertim cum ESCHILLO<br>BILLEO nostro mihi nunc respondendum sit ad quasdam hisce diebus<br>mihi ab eo missas, et quendam ex meis nunc Lubecæ habeam, qui has ad vos certo dirigat, oblata videlicet<br>commoda aliqua oportunitate, interea dum alijs meis negotijs isthic exequendis<br>occupatur. Velim autem, ut hæc ipsa quæ nunc scribo mittoque, non nisi intimis<br>nostris monstres; tute satis nosti, quinam ex ijs fideles sint, licet et illi,<br>uti fit, pauci; nolui nomen eius, qui hæc ad me scripsit, subiungi; sed tu et<br>Doctor NICOLAUS facile colligetis,<br>quisnam sit; solet is libere mihi scribere, utut de facie ignotus ignoto; nollem<br>itaque hasce eius literas alijs patere, potissimum ob ea quæ de Principe alias<br>satis noto scribit. Aut igitur hæc admodum secreta habe, aut<br>Vulcano trade. Quam facile mihi sit, Principis illius obiectis respondere, tute<br>satis nosti, nam desertus deserui, ne ipsemet spectator essem ruinæ eorum, quæ<br>in Patria tam diu tantoque nixu molitus sum, quod tamen nec Regno nec Regi<br>imputandum duco; authores satis noti, utque adhuc famosiores reddantur,<br>promeruere. Quod autem, mi OLIGERE, hæc, quæ nunc mitto, prioribus superaddam, ideo factum,<br>quod ex te alijsque intimis amicis oportunum simul consilium capere velim, quid<br>faciendum existimetis, ubi tale quid mihi oblatum fuerit. Intelligo ex ijs, qui<br>loca illa norunt, esse arcem splendidam et amœnam, in quam ipse Cæsar solitus<br>est ante paucos annos animi causa nonnunquam secedere, quamque post Pragensem maximi facit, et circa quam venationem<br>exercere consuevit, dum his et similibus delectaretur. Nunc enim intra duos vel<br>tres proximos annos saltem in arce Pragensi<br>eum se continuisse referunt. Ego quidem de feudo aliquo in Silesia, Moravia, Lusatia, aut<br>alijs ditionibus Cæsari subiectis, obtinendo potius essem sollicitus, quam ut in<br>tam pomposo Bohemiæ loco versarer, sed obtemperandum est Imperatoris clementi voluntati, quacunque<br>tandem ratione mihi et meis benefacere velit, ita ut studijs etiam, quibus<br>occupor, liberaliter provideatur, siquidem Patria nostra hanc curam tam inconsiderate posthabuerit. Loquere de his secreto cum<br>ESCHILLO BILLEO nostro, tum<br>quoque KRAGIO communi amico, et quid<br>consilij dederint, literis significa, alias vero hæc, ubi præsertim minus<br>securum fuerit, cæla. FRANCISCUS<br>TEGNAGLIUS meus necdum huc reversus est, sed spero illum brevi<br>feliciter affuturum. Cuperem etiam, ut MULÆUS reditum acceleraret, neque enim de rebus meis, quomodo et<br>quando quippiam agendum, omnino statuere possum, antequam hi duo redierint.<br>Interea tamen me, quoad datur, itineri accingo, omniaque Divinæ providentiæ<br>committo, cui etiam te cum omnibus tuis commendo.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Dabantur raptim Wandesburgo die<br>28. Iunij Anno Domini 1598.</p><p>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu<br>propria.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.POSTQUAM has ad te exarassem, accessit me huc WALTERI SCHOTI Hamburgensis filius<br>GABRIEL, a quo pannos Mater<br>et tu emere soletis, et se brevi Rosenholmium venturum aiebat. De curru<br>quoque, quem mihi confieri procuras, retulit, Matrem tuam, cum ultimo ibi<br>fuisset, eum brevi paratum fore dixisse ; quare se illum in navem ex Arhusia Lubecam secum recepturum<br>pollicitus est, cumque de te ipso percontarer, in Selandiam abijsse<br>existimavit. Et insuper se apud fratrem meum AXILLUM fuisse, atque ibi percepisse,<br>quod ad nuptias trium filiarum, quæ requiruntur, indies adornet, inter quas<br>et tuæ celebrabuntur, idque futurum sex abhinc hebdomadibus commemoravit.<br>Incidit itaque in mentem, me tibi Epithalamium quoddam promisisse, quod cum<br>tam cito ob alias occupationes, quibus varie distrahor, fieri vix queat, hoc<br>saltem breve Epigramma, quod nunc ex tempore in buccam venit, hîc subiunctum<br>habe. Deus author universi omnia tibi in hoc coniugio ex voto prospera et<br>salutaria largiatur. Tu autem, ne huius novellæ SOPHIÆ amor, alterius et diu tibi<br>excultæ sophiæ amorem labefactet, sed potius adaugeat atque confirmet,<br>effice. Hoc opus, hic labor, multos ea meta<br>fefellit.</p><p>EPIGRAMMA AD OLIGERUM<br>ROSENCRANTZIUM SPONSUM.</p><p>Tene OLIGERE etiam materno<br>sauciat arcu</p><p>    Ille levis toto qui volat orbe puer?</p><p>Iste puer cæcus minimusque nec ardua curans</p><p>    Quem poteras alto despicere ingenio.</p><p>Omnia sed vincit, dices, cedamus amori</p><p>    Indomito; summos stravit et ante viros.</p><p>Est (fateor) fortis; Musæ, Dictinna, Minerva</p><p>    Sunt tamen e quibus is nulla Trophæa gerit.</p><p>Intimus es Musis, placet et Dictinna, tibique</p><p>    Artibus exculto culta Minerva favet:</p><p>Attamen aut Arte aut Pharetra, qui vincere nullas</p><p>    E tribus his potuit, te superavit amor.</p><p>Scilicet ut tandem miro te falleret astu,</p><p>    Miscuit inter se nomina nominibus,</p><p>Proque alijs Sophijs Sophiam ut tibi protulit unam</p><p>    Præ Sophia omni, unam, victus, amas Sophiam .</p><p>T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man notably renowned for nobility both of genius and of birth, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, my kinsman, relative, and most upright friend.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Although I do not doubt, my most longed-for Holger, that both letters — namely those I sent with Mule, and those I afterward wrote to you — have been delivered to you, and that from them you have learned, among other things, what was written to me from Prague by my old friend Thaddaeus [Hagecius]: nevertheless, since a few days ago I received some letters from that Magdeburg friend of mine, whom you know is wont to write to me, treating of certain matters on the same subject, I thought a copy of these too should be shared with you, that you might more fully understand what the Imperial Majesty's disposition toward me is — especially since I must now reply to our <b>Eske Bille</b> about certain [letters] he sent me these days, and I have one of my men now at Lübeck who will direct these to you safely, when some convenient opportunity offers, while he is occupied carrying out my other business there. But I should wish that these very things I now write and send, you show to none but our intimates; you yourself know well enough which of them are faithful — though even they, as it happens, are few. I did not want the name of him who wrote these things to me appended; but you and Doctor Nicolaus will easily gather who it is. He is wont to write to me freely, though unknown to one unknown by face; I should therefore not wish these letters of his to lie open to others, chiefly on account of what he writes about a certain Prince otherwise well enough known. Either, then, keep these very secret, or hand them to Vulcan [burn them]. How easy it is for me to answer that Prince's objections, you yourself know well enough: for, <b>deserted, I deserted</b> — lest I myself be a spectator of the ruin of those things which in my fatherland I laboured at so long and with such effort; which, however, I judge is to be imputed neither to the Kingdom nor to the King — the authors of it are well enough known, and have deserved to be made yet more notorious. But that I add these things I now send to the earlier ones, my Holger, is done for this reason: that I may take timely counsel from you and other intimate friends — what you think should be done, when such a thing [the offer] is made to me. I understand, from those who know those places, that it is a splendid and pleasant <b>castle</b>, into which the Emperor himself was wont, a few years ago, sometimes to withdraw for recreation, and which he values most after the Prague one, and around which he was accustomed to hunt, while he delighted in these and the like; for now, within the last two or three years, they report he has kept himself only in the Prague castle. I, indeed, would rather be solicitous about obtaining some <b>fief in Silesia, Moravia, Lusatia, or other dominions subject to the Emperor</b>, than to dwell in so pompous a place of Bohemia; but one must comply with the Emperor's clement will, in whatever way at last he wishes to do good to me and mine, so that provision be also liberally made for the studies in which I am engaged — since our fatherland has so inconsiderately neglected this care. Speak of these things secretly with our <b>Eske Bille</b>, and also with <b>Krag</b>, our common friend, and signify by letter what counsel they give; but otherwise conceal these things, especially where it is less safe. My <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> has not yet returned hither, but I hope he will soon be happily present. I should also wish <b>Mule</b> would hasten his return, for I cannot at all determine about my affairs — how and when anything is to be done — before these two return. Meanwhile, however, I gird myself for the journey, as far as is granted, and commit all things to Divine providence, to which I also commend you with all yours.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given in haste from Wandsbek, the 28th day of June, in the Year of the Lord 1598.<br>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. After I had written this to you, there came to me here Gabriel, son of Walter Schot of Hamburg — from whom your Mother and you are wont to buy cloth — and he said he would shortly come to Rosenholm. About the carriage too, which you are having made for me, he reported that your Mother said it would shortly be ready; wherefore he promised he would take it with him aboard ship from Aarhus to Lübeck; and when I enquired about you yourself, he supposed you had gone off into Zealand. And moreover [he said] that he had been at my brother Axel's, and had there learned that he is daily preparing for the weddings of the three daughters that are required, among which yours too will be celebrated, and he mentioned this would be six weeks from now. It came into my mind, then, that I had promised you a certain Epithalamium; and since, for the other occupations by which I am variously distracted, it can scarcely be done so quickly, have at least this short Epigram, which now came on the spur of the moment to my lips, here subjoined. May God, the author of the universe, bestow on you in this marriage all things prosperous and wholesome, according to your prayer. But do you bring it about that the love of this new Sophia does not weaken the love of that other, long-cultivated wisdom [sophia], but rather increases and confirms it. This is the task, this the toil; that goal has deceived many.  /  EPIGRAM TO HOLGER ROSENKRANTZ THE BRIDEGROOM. | Does that nimble boy who flies over the whole world / wound even you, Holger, with his mother's bow? / That boy, blind and tiniest, caring nothing for lofty things, / whom you could look down upon with your high genius. / \"But Love conquers all,\" you will say; \"let us yield to untamed / Love; he has laid low even the greatest men before.\" / Strong he is, I grant; yet the Muses, Diana, Minerva / are among those from whom he wins no trophies. / You are an intimate of the Muses, Diana too is pleasing to you, / and to you, refined in the arts, refined Minerva is favourable: / and yet, he who could conquer none of these three / by Art or by Quiver, Love has overcome you. / That is, so that at last he might deceive you by a wondrous wile, / he mingled names among names, / and instead of the other wisdoms [Sophiae] brought forth one Sophia for you — / so that before every Sophia, you love, vanquished, one Sophia. T. B.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), of <b>Rosenholm</b> — Tycho's <i>consanguineus, affinis et amicus integerrimus</i> (\"kinsman, relative, and most upright friend\"; the recurring <i>Oligerus</i> of the corpus, e.g. brahe-t_15980328001, brahe-t_15990307001). The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho weighing the imperial offer (June 1598).</b> A confidential, candid letter from the <b>exile at Wandsbek</b>, written as the imperial overture took shape. Tycho forwards (in copy) the latest from his <b>anonymous Magdeburg correspondent</b> on \"the Imperial Majesty's disposition toward me,\" and asks Holger — with <b>Eske Bille</b> and <b>Krag</b> — for <b>counsel on whether to accept</b> \"when such a thing is made to me.\" It is the deliberation behind the move that the 1599–1600 letters carry out.</li><li><b>The offered castle = Benátky.</b> Tycho describes (from report) \"a splendid and pleasant <b>castle</b>, into which the Emperor was wont … to withdraw for recreation … and around which he hunted,\" valued \"most after the Prague one\" — i.e. <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b>, where Tycho would in fact be installed. Strikingly, Tycho says he would <b>rather have a fief</b> in <b>Silesia, Moravia, or Lusatia</b> than \"so pompous a Bohemian place,\" but will comply with the Emperor's will provided his studies are \"liberally provided for\" — \"since our fatherland has so inconsiderately neglected this care.\"</li><li><b>The <i>\"desertus deserui\"</i> epigram.</b> Tycho's famous self-justification: <i>\"deserted, I deserted\"</i> (<i>desertus deserui</i>) — he left Denmark only after being abandoned, \"lest I be a spectator of the ruin\" of his life's work — laying the blame <b>neither on the Kingdom nor the King</b>, but on the \"authors\" of his troubles, \"well enough known, and deserving to be made yet more notorious.\" (No silent sharpening: the careful exculpation of King and realm is kept.)</li><li><b>Secrecy.</b> <i>Aut … secreta habe, aut Vulcano trade</i> — \"keep these very secret, or <b>give them to Vulcan</b>\" (burn them); show only \"our intimates\"; the anonymous correspondent's name withheld because of \"what he writes about a certain Prince\" — Tycho's tradecraft of confidential channels.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz; <b>Eske Bille</b> (Tycho's kinsman-agent); <b>Krag</b> = <b>Niels Krag</b> (Nicolaus Cragius, 1550–1602, the Danish historian-diplomat; cf. brahe-t_15900228001), \"our common friend\"; <i>Thaddaeus</i> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek (Hagecius)</b>, Tycho's Prague friend; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> and <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>Mulaeus</i>), Tycho's agents, both away (their return needed before Tycho can decide). <i>Doctor Nicolaus</i> — an intimate who, with Holger, will \"guess\" the Magdeburg correspondent; <b>possibly Niels Krag</b> (<i>Nicolaus</i>), though named separately as <i>Kragius</i> in the same letter, so left unidentified and flagged.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Wandesburgo die 28. Iunij Anno Domini 1598</i>; <b>Old Style</b> (the Danish-oriented exile context). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript + a wedding EPIGRAM to Holger's bride Sophie (the <i>Sophia</i> bride / <i>sophia</i> wisdom pun), restored from Dreyer VIII p.79 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>A Virgilian tag before the epigram.</b> Recovers Tycho's closing line to the prose passage introducing his wedding epigram for Holger Rosenkrantz: an adaptation of Virgil's famous \"hoc opus, hic labor est\" (Aeneid VI.129), repurposed here to describe the difficulty of the very thing Tycho has just urged — that love for a new bride (\"this new Sophia\") not weaken devotion to wisdom (sophia) itself. \"Meta\" (a race's turning-post/finish) and \"fefellit\" (has deceived/eluded) cast the marriage as a course \"many\" have failed to run without one love crowding out the other.</li><li><b>Literary self-consciousness.</b> The tag functions as a bridge between the letter's prose and the Latin epigram that follows, and is itself a small piece of wordplay consistent with the whole letter's pun on Sophia (bride) / sophia (wisdom).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent, fully legible Latin and a biographically important letter, read securely; the deliberation over the imperial offer, the description of the castle (Benátky) and the fief-vs-residence preference, the <i>\"desertus deserui\"</i> epigram with its careful exculpation of King and realm, and the secrecy instructions are all clear and corpus-corroborated. The one open identification — <i>Doctor Nicolaus</i> — is left unresolved per the no-fabrication rule and flagged.</p><p>Grade A. An eight-word gnomic tag, syntactically self-contained, with an unambiguous classical echo (Aeneid 6.129) that firmly anchors the sense; \"meta\" and \"fefellit\" are standard classical vocabulary with no cruces. No named person is involved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.76-80 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"). Collated the full letter, postscript, and closing Latin epigram word-for-word against the printed text: the dedicatory heading, every proper name (Mulaeus, Thaddaeus, Eschillus Billeus/Eske Bille, Doctor Nicolaus, Franciscus Tegnaglius, Kragius, Walterus Schotus, Gabriel, Axillus/Axel), every number/date (28 June 1598, two/three years, six weeks, three daughters), and place/region names (Wandesburgo/Wandsbek, Silesia, Moravia, Lusatia, Bohemia, Praga, Rosenholmium, Arhusia/Aarhus, Lubeca/Lübeck, Selandia). The closing epigram to Holger Rosenkrantz (Tene Oligere etiam materno...) also matches Dreyer verbatim line-for-line. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15980623001", "next": "brahe-t_15980810001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Forwards copies of letters from a Magdeburg friend reporting the Emperor's disposition toward Tycho, and notes he must reply to Eske Bille.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980628001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980706001", "d": "1598-07-06", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Frans Tengnagel", "ck": "Frans Tengnagel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Amsterdam", "t": "Frans Tengnagel → Tycho Brahe — Amsterdam, 6 July 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with two German passages) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 in 2do (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Frans (Franz) Tengnagel</b> (Tycho's noble assistant and emissary) <i>to</i> Tycho — reporting his <b>diplomatic mission through Germany and the Low Countries</b> on Tycho's behalf. The <b>Germany/Netherlands leg</b> that complements his <b>Italy leg</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000103001, brahe-t_16000103002, brahe-t_16000124001).</p>", "la": "<p>FRANCISCUS TENGNAGEL AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et praestantissime Domine TYCHO. Rebus Mae Tae ex animi sententia confectis, officij mei esse ratus sum (siquidem incertus sim, utrum priores meae raptim Coloniae ad te exaratae tibi redditae sint nec ne) particulatim nonnulla et fusius referre, cum tunc otium singula attingendi mihi non suppeteret, omissis tamen nunc ingratis, quae in itinere mihi evenerunt, siquidem illibenter talia te audire norim. Aquisgranum cum appulissem relicta 3 die Iunij Colonia, ibi Serenissimum Principem Electorem Coloniensem ERNESTUM iam itineri Leodium versus, quod postero die ingressus est, accinctum reperi. Qui quamprimum de eo, quod cum literis tuis adessem, inaudivit, confestim me accersitum humanissime compellavit, gratissimum sibi munus Ma Tae literarium significans, et daturum se operam, ut Ma Ta reipsa se insignem personae suae et rei Astronomicae fautorem comperiat. Quem suum affectum iam pridem antea conceptum, in posterum etiam magis magisque se declaraturum pollicebatur. Interim velle se, ut Leodium curru suo proprio me conferam. Omnem enim fere se eo comitatum unâ translaturum, et rebus suis intra triduum in Limburgensi propinquo Ducatu expeditis actutum subsequuturum. Id quod et factum est. Ibi singulis fere diebus in Cameram ad secretum Rmae eius Celnis colloquium per aliquot nonnunquam integras horas vocatus fui, humanissime exceptus, tandem etiam (quod tam subito ob innumeras, quibus Leodij quotidie distringeretur occupationes, fieri non posse prolixe excusavit) liberalissime dimissus, donata mihi aurea sua effigie, unâ cum equo, quem centum et viginti Coronatis a Magistro Palatij sui coëmerat. Quae vero Rmae eius Celni de negotijs Mae Tae in scriptis tradidi, et simul oretenus exposui, summa cum voluptate legit et audivit, Danos rem se indignam in eo, quod Mam Tam non in eo (uti decuit) precio habuerint, atque tam inconsiderate dimiserint, vel potius amiserint, magno suo opprobrio commisisse perhibens vix ulla aetate apud cordatos et intelligentes expiandam. Et aiebat insuper, eorum officij fuisse sponte sua omni conatu in hoc eniti, quo te beneficijs atque officijs liberaliter praestitis apud se in Regis et Regni honorem retinerent, tantum abest, ut tuae abitioni, velut inaudierat, causas tam evidentes et intolerabiles praebere, atque voluntatem et decretum Optimi Regis FRIDERICI post fata rescindere, Senatorumque omnium literas tibi (prout compertum habebat) in membrana statim ab obitu FRIDERICI Regis concessas exauthorare ipsis fas esset; cumque me semel interrogasset, in quantam pecuniae summam excrescere possent, si computarentur omnia, quae a Rege FRIDERICO et Senatu Regni ad Astronomiae instaurationem Mae Tae concessa erant, me certo illud dicere non posse referente, tantum tamen Mae Tae suppetijsse, ut omnes e quibuscumque tandem oris adventantes, Reges, Principes, eorundem Legatos, Doctores, Studiosos, et cuiusvis ordinis homines splendide semper et munifice tractârit, praeterquam quod tot et tantos sumptus in Astronomiae redintegrationem, quales nemo ante ipsum, erogasset, et alias copiosam aleret familiam. An vero beneficis Regijs, an etiam pro parte proprijs bonis haereditarijs, illos haberet reditus, mihi non satis constare: Existimare tamen summam eorum, quae ex Regno quotannis habebat, quatuor thalerorum millia mea opinione non multum excessisse, mox Rma eius Celdo in haec formalia verba prorupit: <b>Was? Solte man ein Solchen Man vmb so ein geringes geldt auß dem Land ziehenn lassen. Schande ist es. Ein Herr verspielt mehr auf einen abendt. Goldt bekompt man alzeitt aber solche leutte nicht alzeitt.</b> Sed quandoquidem ita iam evenerit, quocunque tandem Danorum fato et oscitantia, ut in Germaniam veniens Ma Ta sibi propior reddita sit, magnopere se ea de causa laetari, et avide hanc commoditatem in posterum arrepturum. Placere sibi, quod nulli Principi Germaniae, aut Regioni exterae suam operam addicere velit, antequam de Imperatoriae Maiestatis clementissimae voluntate erga se et ea quae tractat studia, certo constaret. Et omnino censuit, hanc Regiam artem Imperatori, tanquam praecipuo Europae capiti, omnium primo offerendam esse, ac neutiquam se ambigere, imo multa depositurum, Caesaream Maiestatem pro eo quo esset erga omnes liberales disciplinas, inprimis vero Mathematica et inter haec Astronomica studia singulari favore et amore, Mam Tam libentissimo animo excepturam, munifice cum familia et toto apparatu Astronomico sustentaturam, nec commissuram, ut Mam Tam migrationis huius e Dania unquam poeniteat. Qua etiam de re se non saltem commendatitias, sed pariter congratulatorias ad Imperatoriam Maiestatem daturum, tum quoque intimo eius Secretario BARVITIO, de quo ipse bene meritus esset, quique illi aliquo modo obstrictus, unâ scripturum. Et si praeter omnem suam opinionem Caesa Maiestas Astronomica studia in totius Imperij nunquam intermoriturum honorem et decus conservare et sustentare ab alijs forte dissuasus gravaretur (quod tamen ipsam sponte sua non admissuram certe sciret) multos fore, qui hanc gloriam sibi et suis ditionibus accedere summopere desiderent, nec eam occasionem negligant. Imo se ipsum suis impensis id praestiturum, nec ullis nisi soli Imperatori, cui hic honor primum debetur, hac in parte cessurum. Haec et plura eiuscemodi diversis temporibus ad me dixit, quae referre nimis nunc prolixum foret. Latius D. V. de his coram. Unicum tamen praeterire non possum, quod mihi discessuro confirmationis eorum omnium loco sub ipsum valedictionis tempus inculcavit; ferme his formalibus verbis: <b>Saget dem guttenn ehrlichenn Mann viel guts von meinet wegenn, und das ich hernacher also bey ihm handeln werde, als wan er mein leiblicher bruder wehr, und ich ihn von jugendt auf gekant hette, dessen er sich gentzlichenn zu mir versehenn soll. Saget ihm auch daneben alles was ihr sonstenn von mir gehöret habt, und bitet ihn das er mir seinenn zustandt zueschreibe, so offt ihm gelegenheit vorfelt. Wan ich vormercke, das er bey Hamburg etwas verziehenn wirdt, ehe er zur Kay. Ma. ziehet, bin ich willens einenn von meinenn nobilibus, deme ich in pyronomicis exercitijs vertraue, zu ihm abzufertigenn, von diesem und anderem meinetwegenn mit ihm zu conferiren. Und so baldt ich, ob Gott will, zu ihr Kay. Mt. komme, will ich ihm TYCHONEM, wo er alda in des Kaisers gebiet anzutreffen, besuchenn und weiter mit ihm kuntschaft machen.</b> Nam plane nihil dubitavit, se alicubi in Imperatoriae Matis ditionibus te reperturum. Sicque me clementer et liberaliter (uti prius innui) dimisit. Pericula et damna, quae in hoc itinere perpessus sum, quaeque in prioribus meis aliquatenus attigi, hîc utpote ingrata, non lubet iterare.</p><p>Venio nunc ad Holandos, ad quos in Patria aliquantisper moratus me contuli, quo commoditatem promptiorem et tutiorem redeundi nanciscerer. Ibi cum Hagam, ubi iuris suprema est administratio, quam vel in transcursu cognoscere avebam, pervenissem, Illustrissimus Princeps MAURITIUS, qui Mam Tam novit et magni facit, benignum et clementissimum se mihi exhibuit. Cui libros quosdam Mae Tae secreto ostendi. Quos cum illi apprime arridere viderem, eos consulto ipsi tanquam ex tua voluntate dono dedi, atque illud inscripsi. Qui sane nihil [magis] in votis habuit, quam te in Hollandia novas Uraniae sedes figere et stabilire, seque id Ordinibus sedulo propositurum, modo de tua voluntate certior redderetur, aiebat. Neque enim ambigere, quin et Ordinibus hoc perplaciturum sit. Existimavit etiam, non reperturam Mam Tam ullum locum Genio et studijs suis aptiorem. Quod ut ad Mam Tam suo nomine cum insigni animi sui et amicitiae declaratione, donec ipsemet istic comparuero, perscriberem, iussit, et has quoque inclusas ad eam primo quoque tempore mittendas dedit. Neque tantum ab Illustrissimo Principe hoc ipsius et Ordinum notum percepi; sed et IOHANNE BARNEFELDIO, qui nomine quidem Advocatus supremus, sed reipsa quasi Comes ceu Regulus est Hollandiae. Nam ab eius nutu et voluntate ferme pendent omnia. Cumque et ipsum non minus quam MAURITIUM (qui brevibus ob innumeras suas occupationes Mae Tae gratias, pro libris actis, rescripsit, particularia ut Mae Tae oretenus exponerem iubens) in nostras partes inclinare animadverterem atque te eo accersendum et benigne tractandum censere, subiunxi, me de tua voluntate hac in parte nihil certi referre posse, siquidem intellexerim, te Imperatoriae Maiestati omnium primo tua studia demisse offerre decrevisse, tunc ille mox obmutuit et nihil amplius hac de re ingessit, sed quasi alio declinans orationem, aiebat, se esse in procinctu itineris Anglici, ubi legatum acturus esset nomine Ordinum. Ideoque nunc pluribus mecum loqui non posse. Ubi reversus fuerit, sique res ita postularit, et de tua voluntate sibi quidpiam constiterit, hoc negotium se apud Ordines peracturum. Inaudivi etiam in Hollandia, Angliam magno tui teneri desiderio, quod et ad te perscriptum esse quidam referebat: verene an falso, me fugit. Sed tu rectius dispicies, quid tibi faciendum sit. Praevides enim quantis tumultibus Regnum illud obnoxium sit, quae tunc primum gliscent, ubi Regina ferme decrepita fatis concesserit. Habes, si sic luberet, Scotiae Regem in magna illa Britannica Insula, non saltem affinitate Danis coniunctissimum, sed et tui amantissimum, vel ex ea consuetudine, quae vobis intercessit, ex quo illum cum nobili suo Comitatu te in tua Insula invisentem magnifice excepisti, deque rebus eruditis (uti est Princeps doctus) cum eo affatim contulisti.</p><p>Haec Mae Tae, donec ipsemet Wandesburgi me stitero, praesignificanda habui, quantum nunc inter peregrinandum licuit, ut, quid in posterum sibi faciendum sit, eo citius et rectius statuat. Vale vir nobilissime et praestantissime, ac me propediem expecta.</p><p>Amstelodamo die 6. Iulij Ao. 1598.<br>Mae Tae perpetuo devinctus<br>FRANCISCUS G. TENGNAGEL, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>FRANS TENGNAGEL TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most noble and excellent Lord Tycho. Your Magnificence's affairs being accomplished according to [my] mind, I thought it my duty (since I am uncertain whether my earlier [letter], hastily written to you at Cologne, was delivered you or not) to report some things in detail and more fully — for then I had no leisure to touch each point — omitting, however, now, the unpleasant things that befell me on the journey, since I know you hear such things unwillingly. When I had come to <b>Aachen</b> (leaving <b>Cologne</b> on the 3rd of June), I found there the most serene Prince Elector of Cologne, <b>Ernst [of Bavaria]</b>, already girded for a journey toward <b>Liège</b>, which he entered the next day. As soon as he heard that I was present with your letters, he at once had me summoned and most kindly addressed me, signifying that Your Magnificence's literary gift was most welcome to him, and that <b>he would take pains that Your Magnificence find him a signal patron of his person and of the Astronomical cause</b> — an affection conceived long before, which he promised to declare more and more hereafter. Meanwhile he wished me to go to Liège in his own carriage; for he would transfer almost his whole retinue thither, and, his affairs in the neighbouring <b>Duchy of Limburg</b> dispatched within three days, would follow at once — which was done. There, almost daily, I was called into the Chamber for secret conversation with His Reverend Highness, sometimes for several whole hours, most kindly received; at last too (he excusing at length that it could not be done so soon, on account of the innumerable occupations by which he was daily distracted at Liège) most liberally dismissed, <b>a golden effigy of himself given me, together with a horse which he had bought from his Palace-Master for a hundred and twenty Crowns</b>. What I gave His Reverend Highness in writing about Your Magnificence's affairs, and at the same time expounded by mouth, he read and heard with the greatest pleasure — declaring that <b>the Danes had committed a thing unworthy of themselves, in not holding Your Magnificence in the esteem they ought, and so inconsiderately dismissing — or rather losing — you, to their great reproach, scarcely to be expiated in any age among the sensible and intelligent.</b> And he said besides that it had been their duty, of their own accord, to strive with all effort to retain you with them, by benefits and offices liberally given, to the honour of the King and Realm — far from giving such evident and intolerable causes of your departure (as he had heard), and rescinding the will and decree of the best King Frederick after his death, and <b>annulling all the Senators' letters granted you on parchment immediately after King Frederick's death</b>. And when he once asked me to how great a sum of money [all] could grow, if everything were computed that had been granted Your Magnificence by King Frederick and the Realm's Senate for the instauration of Astronomy — and I answered that I could not say that for certain, but that Your Magnificence had had enough always to treat splendidly and munificently all comers from whatever shores (Kings, Princes, their Legates, Doctors, Students, and men of every rank), besides laying out such great costs on the restoration of Astronomy as no one before, and otherwise feeding a numerous household; [and] whether you had those revenues from royal benefices, or partly from your own hereditary goods, was not sufficiently clear to me; yet I reckoned the sum you had yearly from the Realm did not, in my opinion, much exceed four thousand thalers — His Reverend Highness at once burst into these formal words:</p><p>&gt; <b>\"What? Should one let such a man leave the country for so paltry a sum of money? It is a shame. A lord gambles away more in one evening. Gold one always gets — but such people, not always.\"</b></p><p>But since it has now so happened — by whatever Danish fate and negligence — that Your Magnificence, coming into Germany, is made nearer to him, he greatly rejoices on that account, and will eagerly seize this convenience hereafter. <b>It pleases him that you will pledge your service to no Prince of Germany, nor to any foreign Region, before it be certain of the Imperial Majesty's most clement will toward you and the studies you pursue.</b> And he wholly judged that <b>this Royal art ought to be offered, first of all, to the Emperor, as the chief head of Europe</b>; and he in no way doubts — nay, would wager much — that the Imperial Majesty, given his singular favour and love for all liberal disciplines (and especially Mathematics, and among these the Astronomical studies), would <b>receive Your Magnificence most gladly, munificently sustain you with your family and your whole Astronomical apparatus, and not permit you ever to repent this migration from Denmark</b>. About which he would give the Imperial Majesty not only commendatory but congratulatory letters; and likewise write to his intimate Secretary <b>Barvitius</b> (whom he had himself benefited, and who is in some way bound to him). And if — beyond all his expectation — the Imperial Majesty (perhaps dissuaded by others) should be reluctant to preserve and sustain the Astronomical studies, to the never-dying honour and ornament of the whole Empire (which he was certain the Emperor would not admit of his own accord), <b>there would be many who would greatly desire this glory to accrue to themselves and their dominions, and would not neglect the occasion. Nay, he himself would provide it at his own expense, and yield to none in this part but the Emperor alone, to whom this honour is first owed.</b> These and more such things he said to me at different times, which it would be too prolix now to report. More of these to Your Lordship in person. Yet one thing I cannot pass over, which, as I was leaving, he inculcated to me, in place of a confirmation of all, at the very time of farewell, almost in these formal words:</p><p>&gt; <b>\"Say much good to the good, honest man on my behalf — and that I will hereafter deal with him as if he were my own brother, and I had known him from youth; of which he should wholly rely upon me. Tell him also, besides, everything else you have heard from me; and ask him to write me his condition, as often as occasion offers him. When I perceive that he will tarry a while near Hamburg before he goes to the Imperial Majesty, I am minded to dispatch to him one of my nobles, whom I trust in pyronomic [alchemical] exercises, to confer with him about this and other matters on my behalf. And as soon as I, God willing, come to the Imperial Majesty, I will visit Tycho — wherever he is to be met there, in the Emperor's territory — and make further acquaintance with him.\"</b></p><p>For he plainly did not doubt that he would find you somewhere in the Imperial Majesty's dominions. And so he dismissed me kindly and liberally (as I indicated before). The perils and losses I suffered on this journey, which I touched on somewhat in my earlier [letter], I do not care to repeat here, as being unpleasant.</p><p>I come now to <b>the Hollanders</b>, to whom — having stayed a while in my fatherland — I betook myself, to get a readier and safer means of returning. When I had come there to <b>The Hague</b> (where the supreme administration of justice is, which I longed to see even in passing), the most illustrious Prince <b>Maurice [of Nassau]</b>, who knows Your Magnificence and esteems you highly, showed himself kind and most clement to me. To him I secretly showed some of Your Magnificence's books. Seeing them please him greatly, I deliberately <b>gave them to him as a gift, as from your will, and inscribed it</b> [so]. He indeed wished nothing more than <b>that you fix and establish new seats of Urania in Holland</b>, and said he would diligently propose it to the <b>States [Estates]</b>, if he were made certain of your will; nor does he doubt it would greatly please the States. He reckoned, too, that <b>Your Magnificence would find no place fitter for your genius and studies.</b> He ordered me to write this to Your Magnificence in his name, with a signal declaration of his mind and friendship, until I myself appear there; and gave these enclosed [letters] too, to be sent you at the first opportunity. Nor did I perceive this intent — his and the States' — only from the most illustrious Prince, but also from <b>Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</b>, who is by title indeed Supreme Advocate, but in fact a kind of Count or petty-king of Holland; for upon his nod and will almost everything depends. And when I noticed him too, no less than Maurice (who, on account of his innumerable occupations, wrote Your Magnificence brief thanks for the books, ordering me to expound the particulars to you by mouth), inclining to our side, and judging that you should be summoned thither and kindly treated, I added that <b>I could report nothing certain of your will in this part, since I understood you had decided humbly to offer your studies, first of all, to the Emperor</b>; then he soon fell silent, and brought forward nothing more on this — but, as if turning the discourse elsewhere, said that he was <b>on the point of an English journey, where he would act as legate in the States' name</b>, and so could not speak more with me now; [but] when he had returned, if circumstances so required and he were certain of your will, he would accomplish this business with the States.</p><p>I also heard in Holland that <b>England is held by a great desire for you</b> — which someone said had been written to you; whether truly or falsely, escapes me. But you will better see what is to be done. For you foresee to how great tumults that Realm is liable, which will first swell when <b>the Queen, almost decrepit, has passed away</b> [Elizabeth I]. You have, if it so pleased, <b>the King of Scotland</b> [James VI] in that great British Island — not only most closely joined to the Danes by affinity, but most loving of you, even from that acquaintance which passed between you, <b>since you magnificently received him, visiting your Island with his noble retinue, and conferred abundantly with him on learned matters (for he is a learned Prince).</b></p><p>These things I had to pre-signify to Your Magnificence — until I myself shall present myself at <b>Wandsbek</b> — so far as is now allowed amid travelling, that you may the more quickly and rightly determine what is to be done hereafter. Farewell, most noble and excellent man, and expect me shortly.</p><p>From Amsterdam, the 6th day of July, the year 1598.<br>Perpetually bound to Your Magnificence,<br><b>Franciscus G. Tengnagel</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Frans (Franz) Tengnagel</b> (Tycho's noble assistant, later son-in-law) → <b>Tycho</b> at Wandsbek — reporting his <b>diplomatic embassy through Germany and the Low Countries</b> (June–July 1598) on the exiled Tycho's behalf. (Complements his <b>Italy leg</b>: the books to Pinelli/Magini/the Doge, brahe-t_16000103001, brahe-t_16000103002, brahe-t_16000124001.)</li><li><b>Significance — the patronage \"auction\" for the exiled Tycho, and his strategic choice of the Emperor.</b> A vivid first-hand record of the courts competing for Tycho in 1598:</li><li><b>The Elector of Cologne (Ernst of Bavaria).</b> Met at Aachen/Liège; days of secret audiences; gifts (a <b>golden portrait-medal and a 120-Crown horse</b>). He denounces Denmark's loss of Tycho (\"scarcely to be expiated in any age\"), and bursts out in <b>German</b>: <i>\"Should one let such a man leave the country for so paltry a sum? … Gold one always gets — but such people, not always.\"</i> He endorses Tycho's plan to <b>offer himself first to the Emperor</b> (\"the chief head of Europe\"), promising <b>commendatory letters to Rudolf II and to Barvitius</b>, and pledging — if the Emperor faltered — to <b>support Tycho at his own expense</b>. His farewell charge (also in <b>German</b>): to treat Tycho <b>\"as my own brother,\"</b> to send \"one of my nobles whom I trust in <b>pyronomic [alchemical] exercises</b>\" to confer, and to <b>visit Tycho in person</b> once at the imperial court.</li><li><b>Maurice of Nassau &amp; the Dutch States.</b> At <b>The Hague</b>, <b>Maurice</b> (who \"knows and esteems\" Tycho) received Tengnagel, took <b>Tycho's books as a gift</b>, and offered to propose to the <b>States</b> that Tycho <b>\"fix new seats of Urania in Holland\"</b> — \"no place fitter for your genius.\" <b>Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</b> (the \"petty-king of Holland\") inclined the same way — but <b>fell silent on learning Tycho meant to offer himself to the Emperor first</b>, then left on an <b>English embassy</b>.</li><li><b>England &amp; Scotland.</b> Rumour that <b>England desires Tycho</b>; but Tengnagel warns of the coming succession tumults at <b>Elizabeth I's</b> death, and points to <b>James VI of Scotland</b> — \"most loving of you\" — recalling <b>James's 1590 visit to Hven</b> (when Tycho hosted him and \"conferred abundantly on learned matters\").</li><li><b>The strategic upshot.</b> The letter documents that, <b>by mid-1598, Tycho had resolved to offer his studies \"first of all to the Emperor\"</b> — the choice that led to Benátky/Prague (cf. the exile narratives, brahe-t_15980607001, brahe-t_16000103001).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A keystone of the <b>patronage-search / exile</b> thread (pair with the Grynaeus and Pinelli letters); a <b>books-distribution</b> record (gift copies to the <b>Elector of Cologne</b> — cf. the Mechanica list at brahe-t_15980909001 — and to <b>Maurice of Nassau</b>); and the <b>Tengnagel-embassy</b> thread (the Italy leg).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Frans Tengnagel; <b>Ernst of Bavaria, Elector of Cologne</b>; <b>King Frederick II of Denmark</b> (the late); <b>Barvitius</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Maurice of Nassau</b>; <b>Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</b>; <b>Elizabeth I</b> (\"the decrepit Queen\"); <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (the Hven visitor). Places: <b>Amsterdam</b>, <b>The Hague</b>, <b>Cologne</b>, <b>Aachen</b>, <b>Liège</b>, <b>Limburg</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Holland</b>, <b>England/Scotland</b>.</li><li><b>Two German passages</b> (the Elector's outburst and his farewell charge) are reproduced verbatim and translated; <i>pyronomic exercises</i> = alchemy.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Amstelodamo die 6. Iulij Ao. 1598</i> — Holland was <b>New Style</b>, but the source carries no <i>stylo</i> marker and the label gives none; dated <b>6 July 1598</b> (style left as given; Holland's N.S. noted). Header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin (with the two German speeches) read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Elector of Cologne's</b> courtship — the gifts, the denunciation of Denmark, the two German speeches, the brother-pledge and the alchemist-noble offer, the commendation to Rudolf II/Barvitius; <b>Maurice of Nassau and Oldenbarnevelt's</b> Dutch offer of \"new seats of Urania\" and Oldenbarnevelt's English embassy; the <b>England/Scotland (James VI / Hven-visit)</b> prospect; and Tycho's settled resolve to <b>approach the Emperor first</b>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated, with the German passages rendered faithfully. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.79-84 as page images in two chunks (pages 1-4, then 5-6) and identified the letter proper on pp.80-83. Collated the full Latin/German source_text against the printed text: the salutation and heading, every proper name (Ernestus/Ernst of Cologne, Leodium/Liège, the Duchy of Limburg, Barvitius, Iohannes Barnefeldius/Oldenbarnevelt, Mauritius/Maurice, the King of Scotland, the Queen/Regina, Wandesburgum/Wandsbek, Amstelodamum/Amsterdam), every date and number (3 June departure from Cologne, three-day interval, 120 Crowns for the horse, 4,000 thalers annual sum, 6 July 1598 dateline), and both embedded German quotations verbatim ('Was? Solte man ein Solchen Man...' and 'Saget dem guttenn ehrlichenn Mann...') all match Dreyer's printed text exactly (allowing for long-s/ligature typography only). Checked the published_english against the Latin and German clause by clause, including the two direct-speech German quotations rendered as block quotes: no invented facts, no dropped sentences, no flipped pronouns/antecedents, no hardened hedges, no wrong sums or names. Bracketed English identifications (Ernst of Bavaria, Maurice of Nassau, James VI, Elizabeth I) are clearly marked editorial supplements per the brief's exclusion rule. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 in 2do. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports in detail his embassy to Elector Ernst of Cologne at Aachen and Liège, delivering Tycho's letters and receiving the Elector's warm patronage promises.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980706001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980709001", "d": "1598-07-09", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Leiden", "t": "Joseph Justus Scaliger → Tycho Brahe — Leiden, 9 July 1598 [Old Style/Julian]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with much Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witnesses: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 in 2do (copy); B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> <i>to</i> Tycho — the <b>first letter of their correspondence</b> (Scaliger has just learned Tycho's whereabouts, via a young Leiden mathematician [Tycho's former assistant] and via Tengnagel, who brought Tycho's opening letter). The seed of the precession/chronology exchange that follows (brahe-t_15990417001, brahe-t_16000310001, brahe-t_16000723001).</p>", "la": "<p>IOSEPHUS SCALIGER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>DISCESSUS tuus ex Dania, Vir generosissime, ut nobis acerbissimus fuit, ita ancipites animi nos semper habuit, quem locum fortunis tuis deligeres. Ubi enim illae sitae forent, ibi et animo tuo et serijs studijs tuis aliquid quietis partum iri confidebamus, idque eo impensius optabamus, quo propensior nostra in te semper fuisset voluntas; itaque cum primum accepissem, te Rostochij subsedisse, non mediocriter me consolabar, quod ex eo, quod vicinum Daniae tuae locum optasses, te cito in patriam rediturum ominabamur. At vero non prius te Rostochium commigrasse renuntiatum, quam ex alijs accepimus, te iam inde pedem extulisse. Ab eo tempore frustra semper quaesivimus de statu rerum tuarum ac de sedibus, quas tibi constituisses. Denique nihil certi ad nos de te ferebatur praeter infelicitatem patriae tuae, quae te carere posset. Quare incerta animi mei de statu rerum tuarum sententia non passa est me officio illo erga te fungi, quod tu prior occupasti. Testis est mei consilij iuvenis quidam Mathematicus, qui hîc agit et in vestris domesticis se fuisse profitetur. Ab eo primum ante aliquot dies te in Arce Ranzoviana degere rescivi et recte se curaturum, si quid ad te literarum vellem. Id meditantem oppressit me adventus Nobilis adolescentis, FRANCISCI TEGNAGLIJ domestici vestri, qui tuas plenas amoris, humanitatis, eruditionis mihi attulit, ut nondum constituerim, utrum mihi gratuler de tuis illis humanissimis literis, an doleam hoc officium a te praereptum fuisse.</p><p>Quod de meo opere de emendatione temporum aliter iudicas ac imperiti, quanquam in eo agnosco amorem tuum in me, tamen exprimet confessionem ab illis, me non operam lusisse, vel eo nomine, quod tanto Viro mea placuerint, praesertim φιλαληθεστάτῳ et harum rerum intelligentissimo. Invidia neque nunc primum neque primi afflati sumus, cui opportunum se facit, quisquis aliquid supra captum plebeculae facit. Livore ab huiusmodi perstringi, est laborum suorum fructum percipere. Si scirent, quantum addant gloriae nostrae importunitate sua, cautius agerent, quam agunt. Ego horum neminem vidi, nisi imperitissimum, et quod pluris est, nequissimum. Quicquid scribunt, id vero chartae detrimento est, non existimationis nostrae. De simiolo Heydelbergensi, quem trophaea MILTIADIS non sinunt dormire, minus mirarer, si THEMISTOCLES esse posset. Sed imitari bonos aliorum hominum est, non stercoreorum et barbarorum et ab omni morum humanitate remotissimorum. Sed quid mirer, cum inveniat secutores et admiratores? εὗρεν ἄλοκας τὸ πῶμα. Quid in literis praestiterimus, non est pudoris nostri dicere. Hoc unum ingenue profitemur, neminem vivere, qui plures ad insaniam scriptis suis adegerit. Sed, ut fit, non unius generis homines sunt. Alij a nobis discunt et dissimulant, ne nobis aliquid acceptum ferant. Alij palam carpunt, et tamen nihil illis tam aegre est, quam quod illorum auctores non sint, propter quae tam hostili animo nos petunt. Sed quid de hac faece generis humani te morer? igitur de illo opere nostro, quod institueram dicere, fateor me plus laboris, quam industriae illo contulisse. Vix enim aliter mihi persuadere possum, et nobiscum praeclare agi putamus, si studiosiores quam doctiores habemur. Moneo autem me hominem imbecillis ingenij non potuisse tantam telam percurrere, ut non aliquando humanitus dormitarim, cum aliud pro alio contra animi nostri sententiam nobis excidit: quorum partem in Erratis correximus, partim imprudentes omisimus, quale illud, »Saturnum in annuo motu duodecimam partem unius gradus efficere«, quum vellemus dicere duodecim gradus, de quo et similibus possum iurare sancte: ἡ χεὶρ βέβληκεν, ἡ δὲ φρὴν ἀπόσκοπος. Quare, vir generosissime et doctissime, eiusmodi παροράματα potius quam ἁμαρτήματα, qualia multa in EUDOXO divinus HIPPARCHUS civiliter excusat, et quae ingenui Lectores emendare, non exagitare debent, tuum quoque est aut ἀπροσκόπως καὶ ἀπταίστως praeterire, aut, si per graviores tuas occupationes tibi licet, corrigere. At illa parva dictu menda retrimentis istis sunt argumenta novae foeturae librorum. Quod Septembrem vocamus, quem modo Octobrem, et feriam quartam, quam modo tertiam dixeramus, hoc typographias excalfacit. Barbaria et impudentia invenit ibi quod agat. Divinae aequinoctiorum observationes tuae locum invenerunt in calce librorum nostrorum, non utique quem debebant, sed quem potuerunt. Non enim prius illas acceperamus. Et tamen ibi sunt a nobis collocatae, ubi convenientissime cum Hipparcheis conferri possunt. Sane illud continuum quinquennium omnium priscarum observationum dignitatem et fidem elevat, cum tuae sibi constent, Hipparcheae quadrante diei plus aut minus distent, quod quidem illa aequinoctia valde suspecta nobis facit. Nos ex septem illud delegimus, quod ab autore ἀκριβέστατον vocatur: quemadmodum et anni forma ALFONSINI GELALAeAe quanquam elegantissimae nobis praelata est. Tuus annus Coelestis pauxillo minor GELALAeO. PTOLEMAeI vero observationes tam nobis falsas esse constat, quam multa alia apud illum scriptorem eximium. Ecce anno 886 Nabonassari, Pharmuthi IX, feria prima, Februarij 23, Christi 139, Sol, inquit, erat ἀκριβῶς in 3° 30' Piscium. At in meridie prima Thoth primi, Sol erat ἀκριβῶς in 3° 20' Piscium, eodem teste, nempe 26 Februarij. Ergo in 886 annis προήγησις tridui tantum facta fuerit. Quid absurdius? Haec et plura ἀτοπήματα diligenter in PTOLEMAeO annotavimus. Quare lubricae sunt eius observationes et male illis fiditur. Itaque non miror, si in 285 annis, tot scilicet, quot inter illius et HIPPARCHI observationes intersunt, unius diei progressionem aequinoctia tantum fecisse dicit, utpote qui sibi persuasisset in 300 annis id fieri. Itaque hinc illae lacrimae. A prima meridie Nabonassari ad aequinoctia ab se observata, sunt anni noningenti, et proinde triduum tantummodo aequinoctia εἰς τὰ προηγούμενα promota esse, quare omnes loci Solis ab eo notati ab hac haeresi pendent, et propterea falsi. Neque hac fine stat eius sententia. Si 23. Februarij est 3° 30' Piscium, ergo aequinoctium in 23. Martij, quia anno sequenti ita observarat. Ergo ab hac Epocha aequinoctij verni retro proportione 300 annorum addet diebus Iulianis, ut locum Solis designet, quod facit in omnibus eclipsibus veteribus. Quare qui sequitur illas Epochas Solis, sciat se 300 annorum προηγήσεις sequi. Arabes, qui mentem et consilium PTOLEMAeI non animadverterunt, ex illis aequinoctialibus PTOLEMAeI observationibus μορμολυκεῖον illud Trepidationis pepererunt, ut ex praegressione aequinoctiorum nihilo monstrosior motus octavae Sphaerae εἰς τὰ ἑπόμενα natus est. Neque enim ideo ullus motus Trepidationis assignandus, quod PTOLEMAeO Instrumenta sua imposuerunt; neque Sphaeram octavam ab occidente in orientem ideo promoveri putandum, quod puncta aequinoctialia in Ecliptica in antecedentia promoventur. Circuli enim aequinoctiales ad illa describuntur. Puncta vero mobilia sunt, ergo et ipsi circuli ad ea descripti sunt mobiles, et consequenter polus aequinoctialis mobilis. Itaque tempore HIPPARCHI polus aequinoctialis aberat a cauda Cynosurae gr. 12 24'. Hodie minus distat, quam tribus gradibus. Et tamen omnes putant polum mundi esse non aequinoctialis circuli, et quidem HIPPARCHUS primus huius eximiae rei observator in ea haeresi fuit. At error est, neque unquam circulus arcticus ullius loci parallelus fuit Tropicis aut aequinoctiali. Circuli arctici et antarctici polus idem est cum polo mundi. Circuli aequinoctialis polus variat, ut diximus. Hactenus igitur nunquam polus aequinoctialis fuit polus mundi; sed fore aliquando, fidem facit accessus propior ad Cynosurae caudam, sive ea est polus mundi, sive aliud punctum non longe ab ea remotum. Quid tui iudicij sit, quando illi duo poli convenerint, libenter audiverim. Nam magna res est. Item velim ex te scire an κατάχρησις illa et confusio poli aequinoctialis cum polo mundi aliquem errorem invexerit in collocatione Asterismorum in Sphaera solida. Multa enim in illis desidero et HIPPARCHUM ostendemus ea re deceptum. Edituri sumus in calce MANILIJ nostri aureolum libellum PTOLEMAeI adhuc ἀνέκδοτον περὶ ἀπλανῶν ἀστέρων, in quo per singulos dies anni Actiaci et Iuliani, quae stella matutino aut vespertino oriatur occidatve in quinque Climatibus, diligentissime designatur. Sed recentiores Asterismorum Astrothesiae valde illis repugnant, neque bene cum illis pariant tabulae directionum praeclari Artificis REINHOLDI. Nos omnes omnium Asterismorum Hipparcheas observationes accuratissime collectas in suos dies Iulianos digessimus, in quibus perfectissimus ille artifex notat, quae prima stella cuiusque Asterismi, quaeve ultima oriatur aut occidat, et cum qua parte Eclipticae. Deprehendimus male illi cum PTOLEMAeO convenire, in qua lite componenda, tuo, Vir clarissime, aut nullo auxilio opus est. Vix tandem vanitas anni siderei autore THEBITHIO nobis comperta est; qui scimus eadem die Iuliana eandem stellam semper oriri vespertino et occidere matutino, quod vere docebat nos Aegyptiorum sententia, quae semper eodem tempore Iuliano Caniculam oriri sciscit. Quod verum est. Nos tamen illi commentitio anno Sidereo ascensionem accommodavimus, ut et προηγήσει ἰσημερινῇ unius gradus per 70 annos, cuius opinionis auctor COPERNICUS, PTOLEMAeUS observat unum gradum promotionem fieri in centum annis. Ego vereor, ne in pluribus. Sed tu haec melius, a quo et alia expectamus. Nam nos homunciones procul spectamus, et intra vestibula sacrarij consistimus, quia nondum ἐπόπται sumus, neque spes unquam fore. Itaque sufficit nobis summorum virorum vestigia adorare, quorum tu Princeps, qui talia diu observasti. Nos vero vobis tantum a labore adesse possumus, quantum a peritia nobis desumus. Itaque quod sedulitas nostra nos docuit, id non pigebit in medium proferre. Aio igitur tempore HESIODI Arcturum vespertino oriri solitum in Boeotia et Attica VIII, die Martij Iuliani, eademque die in eodem tractu ortum illum observatum a METONE et EUCTEMONE, et postremo notatum saeculo PTOLEMAeI. Hoc confirmat sententiam nostram de confutatione anni siderei, et Aegyptiorum scitum, qui annum, quem Iulianum nos vocamus, κυνικόν vocabant, quod per plus, quam mille quingentos annos Caniculam semper eodem anni Iuliani die oriri prodiderunt. Haec observatio, ut spero, tibi ingrata non erit. Magnam a nobis inibitis gratiam, si paucularum stellarum, quae propiores sint Eclipticae, ut Basilisci et πρόποδος τῶν Διδύμων observationes vestras nobis communicare libuerit cum latitudinibus, longitudinibus et die Iuliana. Fortasse non infeliciter cum illis Hipparcheis et Ptolemaicis conferentur. Annum coelestem verum a te prodi unico posse certo scimus, a quo plura in Astrologia quotidie expectamus. Quam ineptus et imperitus fuit auctor Anni Pontificij, non dico nunc. Magis miror, tot millia hominum errores non deprehendisse. Sed te moramur.</p><p>Vale, Vir Praestantissime.<br>Lugduni Batavorum VII. Eid. Quintiles Iulianas, CIↃ. IↃ. XC VIII.<br>Tibi devotissimus et tuae amplitudinis studiosissimus<br>IOSEPHUS SCALIGER.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOSEPH SCALIGER TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Your departure from Denmark, most noble Man, as it was most bitter to us, so it always kept our minds in suspense [as to] what place you would choose for your fortunes. For where they should be settled, there we trusted some rest would be gained both for your mind and for your serious studies — and this we wished the more earnestly, the more inclined our goodwill toward you had always been. And so, when I first heard you had settled at <b>Rostock</b>, I consoled myself not a little, since from your having chosen a place near your Denmark we augured you would soon return to the fatherland. But it was not reported that you had moved to Rostock before we learned, from others, that you had already withdrawn your foot thence. From that time we always asked in vain about the state of your affairs, and the seats you had established for yourself. In the end, nothing certain was brought us about you, except the misfortune of your fatherland in being able to lose you. Wherefore the uncertain judgement of my mind about the state of your affairs did not allow me to perform that duty toward you which you have anticipated first. Witness of my intention is a certain young Mathematician who acts here, and professes that he was among your household; from him first, some days ago, I learned that you dwell <b>in a Rantzau castle</b>, and that he would duly see [to it] if I wished any letter to you. While I was meditating this, the arrival of the noble youth <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, your household-member, surprised me — who brought me your [letter], full of love, humanity, and erudition — so that I have not yet decided whether to congratulate myself on that most humane letter of yours, or to grieve that this duty was snatched from me [that you wrote first].</p><p><b>That you judge of my work <i>On the Emendation of Times</i> otherwise than the unskilled</b> — though in it I recognise your love toward me — yet it will extort a confession from them that I did not waste my labour, even on this ground, that my [work] pleased so great a Man, especially <b>one most-truth-loving [φιλαληθεστάτῳ] and most intelligent in these matters</b>. By envy we are afflicted neither now first, nor [are we] the first — [envy], to whom whoever does anything above the grasp of the little crowd makes himself opportune. To be carped by the malice of such [men] is to reap the fruit of one's labours. If they knew how much they add to my glory by their importunity, they would act more cautiously than they do. Of these I have seen none but the most unskilled and (what is more) the most worthless. Whatever they write is to the detriment of paper, not of my repute. <b>About that Heidelberg little-ape, whom the trophies of Miltiades will not let sleep</b>, I should wonder less, could he be a Themistocles. But to imitate the good belongs to other men — not to dung-born barbarians, most remote from all humanity of manners. But why should I wonder, since he finds followers and admirers? <i>\"The lid found its furrows\"</i> [εὗρεν ἄλοκας τὸ πῶμα — like finds like]. What I have achieved in letters, it is not for my modesty to say. This one thing I frankly profess: that no one lives who has driven more [men] to madness by his writings. But, as it happens, men are not of one kind. Some learn from us, and dissemble it, lest they credit us with anything; others openly carp, and yet nothing is so painful to them as that they are not the authors of those things for which they attack us with so hostile a mind. But why do I detain you about this dregs of humankind?</p><p>So, about that work of mine which I had begun to speak of: <b>I confess that I put more labour than skill into it.</b> For I can scarcely persuade myself otherwise; and we think we are dealt with splendidly, if we are held more studious than learned. But I warn that I, a man of weak talent, could not run through so great a web without sometimes humanly dozing — when one thing fell from me for another, against our mind's intention — <b>part of which we corrected in the Errata, part we imprudently omitted; such as that \"Saturn, in its annual motion, makes a twelfth part of one degree,\" when we meant to say twelve degrees</b> — of which, and the like, I can swear holily: <i>\"the hand threw, but the mind was off the mark\"</i> [ἡ χεὶρ βέβληκεν, ἡ δὲ φρὴν ἀπόσκοπος — a slip of the hand, not the mind]. Wherefore, most noble and learned man, <b>such oversights [παροράματα] rather than errors [ἁμαρτήματα]</b> — like the many that the divine Hipparchus civilly excuses in Eudoxus, and which noble Readers ought to emend, not assail — it is yours too either to pass over without offence [ἀπροσκόπως καὶ ἀπταίστως], or, if your weightier occupations allow, to correct. But those faults — small to tell — are, to those refuse-men [critics], arguments for a new brood of books. That we call September what [we had just called] October, and the fourth feria [Wednesday] what we had said the third [Tuesday] — this heats up the printing-presses. Barbarity and impudence find there something to do.</p><p><b>Your divine observations of the equinoxes found a place at the end of our books</b> — not, indeed, the place they deserved, but the place they could [find]; for we had not received them earlier. And yet they are placed by us where they can most fittingly be compared with the Hipparchean. Truly <b>that continuous quinquennium [5-year run] elevates the dignity and trust of all the ancient observations</b> — since yours are self-consistent, while the Hipparchean differ by a quarter-day, more or less, which indeed makes those [Hipparchus's] equinoxes very suspect to us. We chose, from the seven, that one which is called by the author <b>\"most accurate\" [ἀκριβέστατον]</b> — just as the Alphonsine form of the year was preferred by us to the <b>Jalali</b> [GELALAEA, the Persian Jalali calendar], however most elegant. <b>Your celestial year [is] a little less than the Jalali.</b> But <b>Ptolemy's observations are as false to us as many other things in that eminent writer.</b> Behold: in the year 886 of Nabonassar, Pharmuthi 9, the first feria [Sunday], 23 February, [year] 139 of Christ, <b>the Sun, he says, was accurately [ἀκριβῶς] in 3° 30′ of Pisces. But at the noon of the first Thoth of the first [year], the Sun was accurately in 3° 20′ of Pisces, by the same witness — namely 26 February. So in 886 years a precession [προήγησις] of only three days would have been made. What more absurd?</b> These and more absurdities [ἀτοπήματα] we diligently noted in Ptolemy. So his observations are slippery, and ill-trusted. And so I do not wonder if he says that in 285 years (as many, that is, as lie between his and Hipparchus's observations) the equinoxes made only one day's progression — since he had persuaded himself it happens in 300 years. <b>Hence those tears.</b> From the first noon of Nabonassar to the equinoxes observed by himself are nine hundred years, and accordingly the equinoxes were advanced backward [εἰς τὰ προηγούμενα] by only three days; wherefore all the Sun-places noted by him depend on this heresy, and are therefore false. Nor does his view stop here. If 23 February is 3° 30′ Pisces, then the equinox is on 23 March, because he had so observed it the following year. So from this epoch of the vernal equinox, backward by a proportion of 300 years, he will add to the Julian days, to designate the Sun's place — which he does in all the old eclipses. Wherefore whoever follows those Solar Epochs, let him know that he follows <b>300-year precessions</b> [the wrong rate].</p><p><b>The Arabs, who did not notice Ptolemy's mind and plan, from those equinoctial observations of Ptolemy brought forth that bugbear [μορμολυκεῖον] of Trepidation</b> — as, from the precession of the equinoxes, a no-less-monstrous motion of the eighth Sphere toward the consequents [εἰς τὰ ἑπόμενα] was born. For no Trepidation-motion is to be assigned, because Ptolemy's instruments imposed upon him; nor is the eighth Sphere to be thought advanced from west to east, because the equinoctial points are advanced in the Ecliptic toward the antecedents. <b>For the equinoctial circles are described with reference to those [points]. But the points are mobile; therefore the circles described to them are also mobile; and consequently the equinoctial pole is mobile.</b> So in Hipparchus's time the equinoctial pole was distant from the tail of Cynosura [the Pole-star region] 12° 24′; today it is distant less than three degrees. And yet all think the pole of the world is [the pole] not of the equinoctial circle — and indeed Hipparchus, the first observer of this eminent matter, was in that heresy. <b>But it is an error</b>: never was the arctic circle of any place parallel to the Tropics or the equinoctial. The pole of the arctic and antarctic circle is the same as the pole of the world; the pole of the equinoctial circle varies, as we said. <b>Hitherto, then, the equinoctial pole was never the pole of the world; but that it will be, sometime, the closer approach to Cynosura's tail gives faith</b> — whether that [tail] be the pole of the world, or another point not far removed from it. <b>What your judgement is, [as to] when those two poles will converge, I should gladly hear; for it is a great matter.</b> Likewise I should wish to know from you whether that misuse [κατάχρησις] and confusion of the equinoctial pole with the world's pole has introduced any error in the placing of the Asterisms on the solid Sphere [the globe]. For I miss many things in them, and we shall show Hipparchus deceived in this. <b>We are about to publish, at the end of our <i>Manilius</i>, a golden little book of Ptolemy, still unpublished [ἀνέκδοτον], <i>On the Fixed Stars</i> [περὶ ἀπλανῶν ἀστέρων — the <i>Phaseis</i>]</b>, in which, through each day of the Actian and Julian year, what star rises or sets, morning or evening, in five Climates, is most diligently designated. But the more recent star-placements [<i>Astrothesiae</i>] of the asterisms strongly conflict with them; nor do the direction-tables of the excellent artificer <b>Reinhold</b> agree well with them. We have digested all the Hipparchean observations of all the asterisms, most accurately collected, into their Julian days — in which that most perfect artificer notes what first star of each asterism, and what last, rises or sets, and with what part of the Ecliptic. We find he agrees ill with Ptolemy — in settling which dispute, most renowned Man, your help, or no one's, is needed.</p><p>Scarcely at last has <b>the vanity of the sidereal year — by its author Thebit [Thabit ibn Qurra]</b> — been discovered by us, who know that on the same Julian day the same star always rises in the evening and sets in the morning; which the view of the Egyptians truly taught us, which holds that <b>the Dog-star [Sirius] always rises at the same Julian time</b>. Which is true. Yet we accommodated the ascension to that fictitious Sidereal year, as also to an equinoctial precession [προηγήσει ἰσημερινῇ] of one degree per 70 years — of which opinion <b>Copernicus</b> is the author; <b>Ptolemy observes one degree's progression to be made in a hundred years; I fear [it is] in more</b>. But you [know] these better, from whom we expect other things too. For we manikins look from afar, and stand within the vestibules of the sanctuary, because we are not yet initiates [ἐπόπται], nor is there hope we ever shall be. <b>So it suffices us to adore the footsteps of the highest men, of whom you are the Prince, who have long observed such things.</b> We can be of service to you only so much in labour as we fail you in skill. So what our diligence has taught us, it will not irk [us] to bring forward. <b>I say, then, that in Hesiod's time Arcturus was wont to rise in the evening, in Boeotia and Attica, on the 8th day of March Julian; and on the same day, in the same region, that rising was observed by Meton and Euctemon, and lastly noted in Ptolemy's age.</b> This confirms our view on the confutation of the sidereal year, and the Egyptians' knowledge, who called the year which we call Julian the <b>\"Dog-year\" [κυνικόν — Sothic]</b>, because for more than 1500 years they reported the Dog-star always to rise on the same day of the Julian year. This observation, I hope, will not be unwelcome to you. <b>You will gain great favour with us, if it please you to communicate to us your observations of a few stars closer to the Ecliptic — such as Basiliscus [Regulus] and the \"forefoot of the Twins\" [πρόποδος τῶν Διδύμων, Gemini] — with the latitudes, longitudes, and the Julian day.</b> Perhaps they will be compared not unsuccessfully with the Hipparchean and Ptolemaic. <b>We know for certain that the true celestial year can be revealed by you alone</b>, from whom we expect more in Astronomy daily. How inept and unskilled was the author of the Pontifical Year [the <b>Gregorian calendar</b>], I do not say now. I wonder more that so many thousands of men have not detected the errors. But we are detaining you.</p><p>Farewell, most excellent Man.<br>Leiden, the 7th [day before] the Ides of July [9 July], Julian [reckoning], 1598.<br>Most devoted to you, and most zealous of your greatness,<br><b>Joseph Scaliger.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> at <b>Leiden</b> → <b>Tycho</b> — <b>the first letter of the Scaliger–Tycho correspondence</b>, written from Wandsbek-era news (Scaliger has just located Tycho via a young Leiden mathematician [a former Tycho assistant] and via <b>Tengnagel</b>, who delivered Tycho's opening letter).</li><li><b>Significance — the foundational chronology/precession letter (a keystone of the whole exchange).</b></li><li><b>The <i>De emendatione temporum</i> and its critics.</b> Scaliger thanks Tycho for esteeming his great chronological work above \"the unskilled,\" and rails at his detractors (notably a <b>\"Heidelberg little-ape\"</b> — a chronology-critic at Heidelberg). He <b>candidly confesses errata</b> (e.g. \"Saturn's annual motion a <i>twelfth</i> of a degree\" for \"<i>twelve</i> degrees\"; September/October, Wednesday/Tuesday slips) — <b>\"oversights, not errors\" (παροράματα not ἁμαρτήματα)</b> — and asks Tycho \"to emend, not assail.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's equinox observations praised.</b> Tycho's <b>five-year run of equinox observations</b> (placed at the end of the <i>De emendatione</i>) is \"divine\" and <b>self-consistent</b>, exposing <b>Hipparchus's</b> equinoxes (varying by ¼-day) as \"very suspect\"; <b>Tycho's celestial year</b> is \"a little less than the Jalali.\"</li><li><b>The Ptolemy precession critique.</b> Scaliger shows <b>Ptolemy's solar data imply an absurd 3-day precession in 886 years</b>, so \"all his Sun-places are false,\" resting on the wrong <b>1°/100-year (= 300-year-per-degree)</b> precession.</li><li><b>★ The trepidation debunking and the moving equinoctial pole (the <i>Diatriba</i> seed).</b> Scaliger demolishes the <b>Arab \"bugbear of Trepidation\"</b> and the eighth-sphere motion, arguing instead that <b>the <i>equinoctial pole itself is mobile</i> (distinct from the fixed world-pole)</b> — now within 3° of Cynosura's tail (12°24′ in Hipparchus's time) — and <b>converging on the Pole-star</b>. He <b>asks Tycho's judgement on when the two poles will meet</b> — the central thesis he develops in <b>brahe-t_16000310001 (the <i>Diatriba</i>)</b> and that Tycho rejects in <b>brahe-t_16000723001</b>.</li><li><b>The Ptolemy <i>Phaseis</i> edition.</b> Scaliger announces his edition of <b>Ptolemy's hitherto-unpublished <i>On the Fixed Stars</i> (the <i>Phaseis</i>)</b>, appended to his <b>Manilius</b>, and the conflict between ancient (Hipparchus/Ptolemy) and modern (<b>Reinhold</b>) star-placements — needing Tycho's help.</li><li><b>The sidereal-year debunking.</b> Scaliger rejects <b>Thabit ibn Qurra's</b> sidereal year, affirming the <b>Egyptian/Sothic (\"Dog-year\") constancy</b> (Sirius rising the same Julian day for 1500 years), confirmed by <b>Hesiod/Meton/Euctemon's Arcturus rising (8 March)</b>. He asks Tycho for observations of <b>Regulus and the \"forefoot of Gemini\"</b> to compare with the ancients, and disparages the <b>Gregorian (\"Pontifical\") calendar</b>.</li><li><b>The deference.</b> The famous self-positioning: <i>\"we manikins … stand within the vestibules of the sanctuary … not yet initiates; it suffices us to adore the footsteps of the highest men, of whom you are the Prince.\"</i></li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>opening</b> of the Scaliger–Tycho exchange (→ brahe-t_15990417001, brahe-t_16000310001, brahe-t_16000723001); the <b>precession/trepidation/sidereal-year</b> and <b>chronology</b> threads (also brahe-t_16000327002, brahe-t_16000107001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Joseph Scaliger (<i>De emendatione temporum</i>, the <i>Manilius</i>, the Ptolemy <i>Phaseis</i>); <b>Tycho</b>; the young Leiden mathematician (former Tycho assistant); <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; the <b>\"Heidelberg ape\"</b> (a chronology-critic); <b>Miltiades, Themistocles</b> (allusion); <b>Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Eudoxus, Reinhold, Thabit ibn Qurra (Thebithius), Copernicus, Meton, Euctemon, Hesiod</b>; the <b>Nabonassar</b> era; the <b>Jalali</b> and <b>Alphonsine</b> calendars; the <b>Gregorian (\"Pontifical\") calendar</b>; <b>Regulus (Basiliscus)</b>, <b>Gemini</b>. Places: <b>Leiden</b>, <b>Rostock</b>, the <b>Rantzau castle</b> (Wandsbek). The Greek phrases are reproduced throughout.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Lugduni Batavorum VII. Eid. Quintiles <b>Iulianas</b>, CIↃIↃXCVIII</i> — \"the 7th before the Ides of July, <b>Julian</b> [= Old Style]\" = <b>9 July 1598 [O.S.]</b> (Scaliger pointedly dates Julian; CIↃIↃXCVIII = 1598). Header and label agree.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin 'sunt fere anni noningenti' corrected to 'sunt anni noningenti': Dreyer (TBOO VIII p.85) prints the unhedged reading with no 'fere'; the English 'are nearly nine hundred years' is corrected to 'are nine hundred years' to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense, learned Latin (heavily interleaved with Greek) read with confidence; the substance (the location-of-Tycho preamble; the <b>defence of the <i>De emendatione temporum</b></i> and the candid errata; the praise of <b>Tycho's five-year equinox run</b> over Hipparchus; the <b>Ptolemy precession critique</b> (the absurd 3-day/886-year figure); the <b>trepidation debunking and the moving-equinoctial-pole thesis</b> with the \"when do the poles converge?\" question; the <b>Ptolemy <i>Phaseis</i> edition</b> and the ancient-vs-modern star-placement conflict; the <b>sidereal-year debunking</b> with the Sothic/Hesiod/Meton evidence; and the requests for Tycho's Regulus/Gemini observations) is firmly and carefully construed, with the Greek terms reproduced and the chronological arguments rendered faithfully. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 7 Dreyer page images in the slice (pages 1-4, then 5-7) visually, no text layer. Collated the full letter (Scaliger's dense chronology/precession argument) line by line: source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim except for the two TEXT_ERRORs logged above. Every proper name (Miltiades, Themistocles, Eudoxus, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Reinhold, Thebit/Thebithius, Copernicus, Meton, Euctemon, Hesiod, Franciscus Tengnagel), Greek phrase, and number/date (886 Nabonassar, Pharmuthi IX, 23/26 February, AD 139, 3°30'/3°20' Pisces, 285, 300, and ~900 years, 12°24', &lt;3°, 70, 100, VIII Martij, 1500+ years, VII Eid. Quintiles / CIƆ.IƆ.XCVIII = 9 July 1598) was verified against the page images and renders correctly and completely in the English, including the technical precession/trepidation argument. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR forces grade C regardless of severity; both found here are low-severity (they don't change the sense a scholar would take away), hence verdict MINOR rather than SERIOUS. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found in the translation itself.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's heading (p.83) reads \"...AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.\"; source_text drops \"BRAHE\". The English silently supplies the fuller correct form, so the omission surfaces only in the Latin. Dreyer prints: <i>IOSEPHUS SCALIGER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer (p.85, line ~36) prints the flat, unhedged \"sunt anni noningenti\" (there are nine hundred years); source_text inserts a hedge word, \"fere\" (nearly), not present in Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>sunt anni noningenti</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page images for this letter's opening (TBOO VIII pp.82-85, the first four of the seven-page slice). The heading defect (missing 'BRAHE') proved not to be present in the supplied current_latin, which already matches Dreyer's two-line heading exactly, so it is recorded as no_change_needed rather than fixed. The hedge-word defect was confirmed: Dreyer p.85 prints 'funt anni noningenti' without 'fere', so the inserted hedge was removed from both the Latin and the dependent English clause. Pages 86-88 (the remainder of the letter) were not re-collated, as no defect in this packet referenced them and the brief scopes repair to the listed defect sites only.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 in 2do. Afskrift", "B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980314001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980314001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Expresses relief at finally learning Tycho's whereabouts after his departure from Denmark, and writes now that his location at Wandsbek is known.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980709001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980710001", "d": "1598-07-10", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Stygge", "ck": "Johannes Stygge", "ck2": "Hans Aalborg", "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Johannes Stygge → Hans Aalborg (M. Johannes Alburgiacus) — Hamburg, 10 July 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Danish phrases) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note — a third-party, <i>anti</i>-Tycho letter.</b> This is <b>not</b> Tycho's correspondence: it is a hostile letter <b>from the resentful Dane Johannes Stygge to Hans Aalborg (the chief Copenhagen bookseller)</b>, preserved in the Tycho dossier (Langebek's diplomatarium). It is a <b>hostile-witness document</b> — the anti-Tycho Danish faction's view — and its derogatory claims are <i>the writer's</i>, recorded here faithfully, <b>not endorsed</b>.</p>", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIMO NEC NON PERSPICATISSIMO VIRO DOMINO M. JOHANNI ALBURGIACO, BIBLIOPOLAe HAFFNIENSI PRIMARIO, AMICO MEO PERANTIQUO, DENTUR.</p><p>S. eruditissime d:ne magister Johannes, amice rara fide mihi perspecte. Multi, ex quo patriam reliqui, de Tychonis Brahe e Dania discessu nescio quae garrire ausi eius causas a me sciscitati sunt, quibus ea, quae patriae nostrae honorifica erant, retuli, licet quibusdam, quibus Tycho iste cum nova sua necdum visa aut explorata mathesi pro oraculo habetur, satisfacere nec valui nec potui, putantibus, me in gratiam patriae ista excogitare. Multa enim illi, qui Tychonis partibus nimium student (qui sane non pauci sunt, etiam inter viros illustres), cum in patriam tum praecipue in eos, qui rebus isthic nunc praesunt, minus honorifica pronunciant. Quae quam sint falsa, te minime latere arbitror. Sunt qui Daniam utroque orbatam oculo (et quod ipsa non videat, et quod artium lumine destituta conspici ab aliis nequeat) caecam nunc redditam nimis insolenter dicere praesumant. Nonnulli novam barbariem ex qua exulavere Musae, vocitant, alii Machiavellismum, alii invidiam, alii subdolam vafriciem, nonnulli avaritiam; quidam etiam quorundam inscitiam et ruditatem praetendunt nobisque exprobrant (quod scilicet instar galli Aesopici inter margaritam et hordei granum discernere nequeamus). Et quomodo, quaeso, ego solus tot praestantium virorum praejudicia refellere sustineam? Cum omnes fere Tychonem tanti faciant, quasi non sit alius in Dania nobilis, qui melius literas didicerit quam ille, sicque nos alios pro ciphris habent; thi naar een kommer først for ordet at staae tilig op, maae han siden sove saalaenge som han vil, ut vulgatum fert dicterium. Quare nihil optabilius ducerem, quam extare aliquod scriptum publicum, quo Tycho, quam parum possit et quam inutilis nobis fuerit, ignorantibus patefaciat, sicque patria nostra a calumniis insciorum et Tychoni nimis faventium liberari poterit. Fieri enim posset, ut quae nunc oretenus saltem inter doctos et praestantes viros narrantur et hinc inde per literas ab iis sparguntur, fidem apud incautos inveniant et fortassis etiam aliquando scriptis eorum publicis immisceantur; patriam itaque nostram ab his vaniloquis liberare, quantum in me est, percuperem. Relatione autem quorundam Hamburgensium, cum nuper in convivio simul essemus, non sine magno gaudio intellexi, penes te esse librum quendam, quem rarissimae doctrinae caesaris mathematicus contra insolentem hunc Tychonem in publicum Praga emisit, at cum apud bibliopolas hic de eodem mox avide conquisivissem, responderunt, illum Francofurti non extare, sed existimarunt, bibliopolam praecipuum Hafniensem illum undecunque tandem adeptum esse, uti eos te notare non dubitavi. Aderat etiam alius quidam, qui quosdam ex professoribus Hafniensibus eum librum pro eximio valdeque grato dono a te accepisse referebat, quodque iis adeo scriptum hoc placeat, ut in sinu gaudeant atque sibi congratulentur, quod aliquis tam egregius vir se Tychoni opposuerit atque illum suis coloribus merito depinxerit. Nam et ego arbitror, sic eos, qui discessus ejus causas e Dania dedisse insimulantur, a suspicione liberari posse atque exteris rectius constare, quam nulla vel perexigua fuerit jactura, quod Tychonem hunc patria nostra amiserit et retinere noluerit. Est quidem adhuc Tycho ille in arce Ranzoviana quadam non longe hinc remota, sed rarissime civitatem ingreditur. Et cum accedit, tot curribus et equis tamque pompose venit, ac si plus quam nobilis esset. Titillat enim ipsum, quod e variis Germaniae locis, uti audis, veniant multi clari et praestantes viri, qui ipsum invisunt et magni faciunt, et quod primarii quique in urbe ipsum in nimio honore habeant. Ideoque suos conterraneos non amplius agnoscit vixque Danus haberi vult, adeo ut cum proprio suo consobrino Jodoco Hogio, qui aliquandiu hic fuit, ne semel loqui voluerit, et ille, cum accedere eum videret, tam splendide et insolenter se gerere non est ausus; multo minus ego, quem ne aspicere quidem dignatur, cum in curru sublimi per plateas sex equis, sequente uno et altero, vehitur, circumcurrente magna hominum caterva, qui eum, ac si princeps esset, videre gestiunt. Intellexi quoque, quod, quando templum diebus his solennibus ingreditur, quod tamen raro facit, tunc sternunt in ipsius sella, quam ipsi deputarunt in praecipuo quodam templi loco, ante ipsum sericum pannum et pulvinaria conferunt, ligesom hand var meer end andre got folk. Et magnum etiam honorem ipsius conjugi et filiabus tribuunt, saa det var nok, de vare grevinder, saa statzlig holdes de. Interim ego lateo et cum magnificentia nihil habeo commercii. Sed ad rem revertar. Quoniam igitur mihi multum subsidii in mea peregrinatione ex hoc libro, si eum mecum habuero et aliis monstrare potuero, ad refutandas aliorum objectiones manare posse confido, te per amicitiam nostram etiam atque etiam obtestor, velis mihi tria aut quatuor exemplaria de eodem concedere honoris eorum tuendi gratia, qui Tychonis abitum clam procurasse referuntur, utque inde habeam plura argumenta, quibus nostrates defendam. Feceris mihi sic rem longe gratissimam et tibi aliisque, uti spero, non inhonorificam. Nam cum doctissimis illis in academia Hafniensi scriptum illud summopere, ut etiam meretur, arrideat et illi rectius de Tychone, quem etiam melius norunt, judicare possunt quam caeteri, praesertim inaestimabilis ille mathematicus d:ns d. Thomas Finchius nunc, ut audio, rector magnificus (qui nequaquam opus habuit, cum in insula ista Tychonis inutilia instrumenta vidit, a nasutulo illo et cornuto adhuc institui, quomodo vocarentur qualemcunque haberent usum, uti mihi, antequam e patria discederem, relatum est), cuius unius judicium (praesertim cum et ille semigermanus sit et propterea apud Germanos plus fidei merebitur) pluris faciendum quis dubitat, quam mille aliorum sciolorum, qui in Tychonis instrumenta et scripta tam bene inquirere non norunt? Nec dubito, quin famosissimus ille vir et non saltem in Dania, sed etiam plurimis aliis in regionibus notus, doctor Petrus Severini, cum et iste Tychonem intus et in cute plus satis norit, suam acutissimam censuram de eodem argutissimo scripto protulerit, modo eam aperire pro singulari et abstrusa sua prudentia aliisque communicare non dedignatus fuerit. Et quia in tanti viri mentionem hic incido, narrabo quippiam, quae ex Anglis quibusdam studiosis Hamburgum, cum primum huc venissem, transeuntibus audivi, quod non parum aegre ferebam. Erant illi in eodem mecum diversorio, cumque Danum me esse cognovissent (quod tamen inter quosvis doctos, cum ob Tychonis abitum tam male audiant Dani, dissimulare soleo), quaesiverunt a me, an illustrem illum et celeberrimum virum Tychonem Brahe nossem, quod tamen plane abnegare non potui, dicens vidisse quidem in Dania eum, et, licet nobilis, obscurum tamen alias ibi eum esse nec ita celebrem, ut illi putant. Dicebant, se audivisse, illum nunc in Germania esse, vellent eum multorum nomine, praecipue cuiusdam, si recte memini, Johannis Deen, salutare. Ego respondi, me, ubi esset, ignorare nec multum curare (nam alias statim concurrissent, quod et postea ab aliis edocti fecerunt). Mox subjunxit quidam eorum Ruffus: Nescis tu Danus ea, quae nos in Anglia scimus? Severinus, regis medicus, diutino et clandestino eius odio (ob medicamenta eius Theophrastica, quae is tam egregie parare novit et olim regi Friderico et nobilibus postea aliis quoque gratis dare solitus est) laboravit, quia putavit, suae sic aliquid decedere existimationi. Hunc igitur apud nos dicunt multa subdole in Tychonem machinatum esse, ut tandem is talia indigne ferens discederet. Mox ego respondi: quomodo ille doctor hoc facere posset, cum non sit nobilis? Tycho est ex clara et magna familia in Dania, nec habent apud nos tantam autoritatem ut apud vos doctores, et quare non fecit id antea, si potuit, quam nunc? Tunc ille excepit: etiamsi doctor is non est nobilis, tamen habet alios quosdam nobiles et quidem praecipuos, qui apud regem multa possunt, qui doctori huic valde auscultant; per eos potuit tanquam per tertiam et quartam manum efficere, quod per semet ipsum non dabatur. Ego inutili[!]: doctor ille seorsim et solitarie vivit, sua tantum curat, nec se rebus politicis immiscet, estque Tychonis, uti existimo, propter communia studia amicus. At ille: hi, qui ita latent et nolunt videri id, quod agunt, agere, sed per alios clam agunt, a talibus maxime cavendum, praesertim si habent Italica ingenia et honores atque divitias supra sortem affectant; se scire aliquot tales in Anglia, etiam humili genere natos, sed ut plurimum illis male cadere solere. Tum ego stomachabundus sic conclusi, quod ista, quae de doctore illo Dano narrant, sunt somnia et merae suspiciones, nec unquam quid tale in Dania audivi; quomodo ergo vos, Angli qui estis, sciretis? Et mox discessi, indignum illum reputans, cum quo amplius verba commutarem, veritus, ne a verbis ad verbera res deveniret, visus enim mihi superbe nimis Danis ob hunc Tychonem insultare, quasi non essent in Dania alii boni viri illo longe praestantiores et doctiores. Sed valeant ista (nolo enim omnia, quae de his audivi, scio sentioque, scribere, nec admodum etiam tutum videtur), fac saltem, ut exemplaria libri expetiti aliquot quocunque tandem pretio certo obtineam. Pecunias, quas expetis, cognatus meus Claudius Dyre tibi solvet, et libros ad me, ubicunque fuero (neque enim hic diu morari Hamburgi constitui), perferri curabit. Rogoque, ut simul mihi tuam quoque censuram de eodem libro scribere non degraveris et me in omnibus voti facere compotem. Si quid sit, in quo tibi vicissem gratificare potero, alacrem me eius habebis executorem. Ante omnia vero cave, ne quis ex amicis Tychonis (si quos forte adhuc ibi habeat) resciscat, me illa exemplaria a te accepisse.</p><p>His vale cum conjuge tua lectissima, quae ut plures tibi procreet haeredes, vide.<br>Hamburgo 10. die julii anni 1598.<br>Johannes Stygge, mea manu.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most erudite and most perspicacious man, Master Johannes Alburgiacus [Hans Aalborg], chief Bookseller at Copenhagen, my very old friend — to be given.</b></p><p>Greeting, most erudite Master Johannes, friend of rare fidelity, well known to me. Many, since I left the fatherland, daring to chatter I-know-not-what about <b>Tycho Brahe's departure from Denmark</b>, have asked me its causes; to whom I reported what was honourable to our fatherland — though I could not satisfy some, for whom that Tycho, <b>with his new mathematics, not yet seen or explored, is held as an oracle</b>, [and who] think I devise these things to favour the fatherland. For those who too much favour Tycho's side (and they are indeed not few, even among illustrious men) pronounce many less-honourable things, both against the fatherland and especially against those who now preside over affairs there. How false these are, I think you well know. There are some who too insolently presume to say that <b>Denmark is now made blind, deprived of both eyes</b> (both that it itself does not see, and that, destitute of the light of the arts, it cannot be seen by others). Some call it a <b>new barbarism, from which the Muses are exiled</b>; others, <b>Machiavellism</b>; others, <b>envy</b>; others, sly cunning; some, <b>avarice</b>; some even allege the ignorance and rudeness of certain men, and reproach us — that, forsooth, like Aesop's cock, <b>we cannot discern between a pearl and a barley-grain</b>. And how, pray, should I alone sustain the refuting of so many distinguished men's prejudices? — since almost all make so much of Tycho, as if there were no other noble in Denmark who learned letters better than he, and so hold us others for ciphers; <i>for \"when a man once gets the name of an early riser, he may afterward sleep as long as he likes,\"</i> as the common saying has it [Danish]. Wherefore I should think nothing more desirable than that <b>there exist some public writing by which Tycho may lay open to the ignorant how little he can do, and how useless he was to us</b> — and so our fatherland may be freed from the calumnies of the ignorant and of those too favourable to Tycho. For it could happen that what is now told only by mouth among learned and distinguished men, and scattered here and there by them in letters, may find credit among the incautious, and perhaps even sometime be mingled into their public writings; and so I should much wish, as far as in me lies, to free our fatherland from these vain-talkers.</p><p>Now, by the report of certain Hamburgers, when we were lately together at a banquet, I learned, not without great joy, that <b>you have a certain book which the Emperor's mathematician of rarest learning [Ursus] published at Prague against this insolent Tycho</b>; but when I eagerly sought it here among the booksellers, they answered that it is not to be had at <b>Frankfurt</b>, but reckoned that the chief Copenhagen bookseller had got it from somewhere — and I did not doubt they meant you. There was present too another, who reported that <b>certain of the Copenhagen professors had received that book from you as an excellent and very welcome gift, and that this writing so pleases them that they rejoice in their bosom and congratulate themselves that some so distinguished a man has opposed Tycho and rightly painted him in his [true] colours.</b> For I too judge that thus those who are accused of having given the causes of his departure from Denmark can be freed from suspicion, and it can be better established to foreigners how no loss, or a very slight one, [resulted] from our fatherland's having lost this Tycho, and being unwilling to retain him. That Tycho is indeed still in a certain <b>Rantzau castle, not far distant hence, but very rarely enters the city; and when he comes, he comes with so many carriages and horses, and so pompously, as if he were more than noble.</b> For it tickles him that, from various places of Germany (as you hear), many famous and distinguished men come to visit him and esteem him highly, and that the chief men in the city hold him in excessive honour. And so <b>he no longer acknowledges his compatriots, and scarcely wishes to be held a Dane</b> — so much that <b>with his own cousin Jost Høg [Jodocus Hogius], who was here a while, he would not speak even once</b>; and that man, when he saw [Tycho] approach, did not dare bear himself so splendidly and insolently — much less I, whom he does not even deign to look at, when <b>in a lofty carriage he is drawn through the streets by six horses, with one or two [more] following, a great crowd of people running about, eager to see him as if he were a prince.</b> I learned too that, when he enters the church on solemn days (which, however, he rarely does), they then spread, in his seat (which they assigned him in a chief place of the church), before him a silk cloth, and bring cushions — <i>\"as if he were more than other good folk\"</i> [Danish]. And they pay great honour, too, to his wife and daughters — <i>\"so that it was enough [as if] they were countesses, so grandly are they held\"</i> [Danish]. Meanwhile I lie hidden, and have no commerce with [his] magnificence. But let me return to the matter.</p><p>Since, then, I am confident that much help can flow to me, in my travels, from this book — if I have it with me and can show it to others — toward refuting others' objections, I beseech you again and again, by our friendship, <b>be willing to grant me three or four copies of it</b>, for the sake of guarding the honour of those who are reported to have secretly procured Tycho's departure, and that I may have thence more arguments by which to defend our countrymen. You will do me a most welcome thing, and one not dishonourable to you and others, as I hope. For since that writing pleases those most learned men in the Copenhagen academy greatly (as it deserves), and they can judge of Tycho — whom they also know better — more rightly than the rest: <b>especially that inestimable mathematician, Lord Dr. Thomas Fincke</b>, now (as I hear) Rector Magnificus — who by no means needed, when he saw Tycho's useless instruments on that island, <b>to be taught by that sharp-nosed, horned fellow [Tycho]</b> what they were called, or what use (such as it was) they had, as was told me before I left the fatherland — whose single judgement (especially as he too is half-German, and on that account will merit more credit among the Germans), who doubts is to be valued more than a thousand other sciolists, who do not know how to inquire so well into Tycho's instruments and writings? Nor do I doubt that that most famous man, known not only in Denmark but in very many other regions too, <b>Doctor Petrus Severinus [Peder Sørensen]</b> — since he too knows Tycho inside and out, more than enough — has given his most acute censure of the same most subtle writing, if only he has not disdained, in his singular and abstruse prudence, to open it and share it with others.</p><p>And since I here fall upon mention of so great a man [Severinus], I will narrate something I heard from <b>certain English students passing through Hamburg, when I had first come hither</b> — which I bore not a little ill. They were in the same inn with me; and when they had learned that I was a Dane (which, however, I am wont to dissemble among learned men, since the Danes are so ill-spoken-of on account of Tycho's departure), they asked me whether I knew that illustrious and most celebrated man Tycho Brahe — which I could not plainly deny, saying that I had indeed seen him in Denmark, and that, though noble, he is otherwise obscure there, and not so celebrated as they think. They said they had heard he is now in Germany, [and] <b>wished to greet him in the name of many — especially of one, if I remember rightly, Johannes Deen</b>. I answered that I do not know where he is, nor much care (for otherwise they would have run to him at once — which, taught afterward by others, they did). Soon one of them, <b>a red-haired one [Ruffus]</b>, added: <i>\"Do not you, a Dane, know what we in England know? <b>Severinus, the king's physician</b>, laboured under his [Tycho's] long and secret hatred — on account of his <b>Theophrastic [Paracelsian] medicines</b>, which he knows so excellently to prepare, and used of old to give freely to King Frederick, and afterward to other nobles too — because he [Tycho] thought that something thus detracted from his [own] reputation. <b>This man [Severinus], therefore, they say among us, contrived many things slyly against Tycho, so that at last he, bearing such things unworthily, departed.</b>\"</i> Soon I answered: how could that doctor do this, since he is not noble? Tycho is of an illustrious and great family in Denmark, nor have doctors with us such authority as with you; and why did he not do it before, if he could, rather than now? Then [the Englishman] rejoined: even though that doctor is not noble, yet he has certain other nobles — and indeed chief ones, who can do much with the king, [and] who hearken greatly to this doctor; <b>through them, as through a third and fourth hand, he could effect what was not granted [him] through himself.</b> I [replied]: that doctor lives apart and solitarily, cares only for his own [affairs], nor mixes himself in political matters, and is, as I think, Tycho's friend, on account of their common studies. But he: <b>those who thus hide, and do not wish to seem to do what they do, but act secretly through others — such are most to be guarded against, especially if they have Italian wits, and affect honours and riches above their lot</b>; he knew some such in England, even of humble birth, but for the most part it is wont to fall out ill for them. Then I, stomachfully, thus concluded: that <b>these things they tell about that Danish doctor are dreams and mere suspicions, and I never heard any such thing in Denmark; how, then, should you, who are English, know it?</b> And I soon departed, reckoning him unworthy that I should exchange more words with him, fearing lest the matter come from words to blows — for he seemed to me too proudly to insult the Danes over this Tycho, as if there were not in Denmark other good men far more distinguished and learned than he. But let these things be (for I do not wish to write all that I have heard, know, and feel about them — nor does it even seem altogether safe); only see to it that I certainly get some copies of the desired book, at whatever price. The money you ask, my kinsman <b>Claus Dyre [Claudius Dyre]</b> will pay you, and will see the books carried to me, wherever I am (for I have resolved not to stay long here at Hamburg). And I ask that you would not find it a burden to write me at the same time your own censure of the same book, and make me get my wish in all things. If there be anything wherein I can gratify you in turn, you will have me an eager performer of it. But <b>above all, beware lest any of Tycho's friends (if perchance he have any still there) learn that I got those copies from you.</b></p><p>Farewell herewith, with your most choice wife — and see that she bears you more heirs.<br>From Hamburg, the 10th day of July, the year 1598.<br><b>Johannes Stygge</b>, in my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondents (third-party, anti-Tycho).</b> <b>Johannes Stygge</b> (a disaffected Dane abroad) → <b>Hans Aalborg</b> (<i>M. Johannes Alburgiacus</i>, the chief Copenhagen bookseller). <b>Not Tycho's own correspondence</b>, but a hostile letter preserved in the Tycho dossier (Langebek's diplomatarium). Its value is precisely as a <b>hostile-witness document</b> — the <b>anti-Tycho Danish faction's narrative</b>, the mirror-image of Tycho's own apologias.</li><li><b>Significance — the \"other side\" of the exile.</b></li><li><b>The anti-Tycho resentment.</b> Stygge is stung that Tycho's partisans (even \"illustrious men\") brand Denmark — for losing Tycho — as <b>\"blind,\" a \"new barbarism,\" \"Machiavellism,\" \"envy,\" \"avarice,\"</b> unable \"to discern a pearl from a barley-grain.\" He longs for <b>a public writing exposing \"how little Tycho can do and how useless he was.\"</b></li><li><b>The Ursus book sought as ammunition.</b> He begs Aalborg for <b>3–4 copies of \"the book the Emperor's mathematician [Ursus] published at Prague against Tycho\"</b> — reportedly given by Aalborg as a welcome gift to <b>Copenhagen professors</b> who \"rejoice that someone has painted Tycho in his true colours.\" A striking record of <b>Ursus's <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> circulating as anti-Tycho propaganda in Copenhagen</b>.</li><li><b>The hostile portrait of Tycho at Wandsbek.</b> Tycho riding through Hamburg in a <b>six-horse carriage \"as if a prince,\"</b> honoured by visitors and city notables, <b>snubbing his cousin Jost Høg</b>, no longer \"wishing to be held a Dane,\" his <b>church seat draped with silk</b> (<i>\"as if more than other good folk\"</i>) and his <b>wife and daughters treated \"as if countesses\"</b> (Danish phrases). The envious counter-image to Tycho's self-presentation.</li><li><b>The Danish critics named.</b> <b>Thomas Fincke</b> (the mathematician, then Rector of Copenhagen) and <b>Petrus Severinus (Peder Sørensen)</b>, the royal Paracelsian physician — cited as learned men who \"know Tycho better\" and judge him low. Tycho is sneered at as <b>\"that sharp-nosed, horned fellow\"</b> (<i>nasutulo et cornuto</i> — possibly a jibe at his prosthetic metal nose).</li><li><b>The John Dee reference.</b> <b>English students at Hamburg wished to greet Tycho \"in the name of … John Dee [Johannes Deen]\"</b> — a documented <b>Dee–Tycho contact line</b> (relevant to the John Dee research thread). ⚑ Flagged for the Dee project.</li><li><b>The Petrus-Severinus conspiracy theory.</b> The English students' tale: that <b>Severinus, the king's Paracelsian physician, out of secret jealousy, engineered Tycho's downfall \"through nobles, as through a third and fourth hand\"</b> — and the warning against men \"with Italian wits who affect honours above their lot.\" Stygge (here, oddly, defending the doctor) dismisses it as \"dreams and mere suspicions.\" A revealing trace of contemporary <b>conspiracy narratives about Tycho's fall</b> (and of the Tycho–Severinus Paracelsian rivalry).</li><li><b>The secrecy.</b> Stygge's closing plea — <i>\"beware lest any of Tycho's friends learn I got these copies from you\"</i> — underscores the covert, factional character of the anti-Tycho campaign.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>anti-Tycho / exile</b> counter-narrative (mirror to brahe-t_16000103001, brahe-t_15980607001, brahe-t_15980706001); the <b>Ursus</b> book's reception in Copenhagen (the Ursus thread); the <b>John Dee</b> contact (→ the Dee research project); and the <b>Petrus Severinus / Paracelsian</b> angle.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Johannes Stygge; <b>Hans Aalborg</b> (Copenhagen bookseller); <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (the target); <b>Ursus</b> (\"the Emperor's mathematician,\" unnamed); <b>Thomas Fincke</b>; <b>Petrus Severinus</b>; <b>Jost Høg</b> (Tycho's cousin); <b>King Frederick II</b>; <b>John Dee</b>; <b>Claus Dyre</b> (Stygge's kinsman); the English students (\"Ruffus\"). Places: <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau castle), <b>Frankfurt</b>, <b>Hven</b> (\"that island\"), <b>England</b>.</li><li><b>Notable phrases.</b> The Danish proverb <i>\"naar een kommer først for ordet at staae tilig op…\"</i> (\"get the name of an early riser, sleep as long as you like\"); the Danish gibes <i>\"ligesom hand var meer end andre got folk\"</i> and <i>\"de vare grevinder.\"</i> A corrupt word <i>Ego inutili[!]</i> (rendered \"I [replied]\") is editorially flagged in the source.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Hamburgo 10. die julii anni 1598</i> (Hamburg O.S.; no <i>stylo</i> marker; label gives no N.S.) → <b>10 July 1598 [Old Style]</b>.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The English translation's bracketed identification of the letter's 'Johannis Deen' as the English mathematician John Dee has been removed as unwarranted; Dreyer (TBOO XIV, p.144) prints the bare Latin name with no gloss or apparatus support for that identification, and no external scholarship was found to confirm it. The name is now left untranslated and unidentified.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin (with Danish phrases) read with confidence; the substance (the <b>anti-Tycho resentment</b> and the longing for a debunking-writing; the request for <b>copies of Ursus's anti-Tycho book</b> circulating in Copenhagen; the <b>hostile six-horse-carriage portrait</b> of Tycho at Wandsbek with the Danish gibes; the naming of <b>Thomas Fincke and Petrus Severinus</b> as Danish critics; the <b>John Dee</b> greeting via English students; and the <b>Severinus-conspiracy</b> tale) is firmly construed and rendered faithfully <b>as the hostile writer's claims</b>, with no sharpening or softening of the venom and no endorsement of the false characterisations. The corrupt <i>inutili[!]</i> and the <i>nasutulo et cornuto</i> jibe are flagged; no other crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 Dreyer page images (TBOO XIV pp.141-146) in two chunks (1-4, 5-6); letter 152 runs pp.142-145 within the slice. Collated the address heading, opening (\"S. eruditissime d:ne magister Johannes...\"), the Rantzau-castle/church-honours passage, the Fincke/Severinus passage, the English-students-at-the-inn anecdote (including the Severinus/Tycho mutual-hatred sentence, whose 'eius'/'suae' pronouns were checked against Latin grammar and found correctly resolved to Tycho in the English, not flipped), and the closing (\"Hamburgo 10. die iulii anni 1598\") against the page images. All matched Dreyer's print exactly (including the source's own '[!]' crux at 'Ego inutili[!]', which Dreyer also prints, so not a fresh error) except the one identification flagged above, which is an unsupported gloss rather than a source or Dreyer divergence.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (medium). The English asserts, via the bracketed equivalence '[Johannes Deen]', that this bare Latin name is the historical John Dee, the English mathematician/astrologer/magus. Neither the source text, nor Dreyer's printed page, nor Dreyer's apparatus (checked) supports this identification; a web check of the tekstnet.dk edition and general search likewise turned up no scholarly identification of this figure as John Dee. The letter gives no other identifying detail (occupation, title, place) beyond the bare name being one among several English well-wishers. Presenting this as a settled identity is an invented fact with no counterpart in the source, and it is the kind of claim (a documented Tycho-John Dee contact in 1598 Hamburg) a scholar could wrongly go on to cite. At: “wished to greet him in the name of many — especially of one, if I remember rightly, John Dee [Johannes Deen]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified TBOO XIV p.144 (line ~24) directly from the Dreyer page image: the printed text reads 'quidam, fi recte memini, Johannis Deen, falutare' with no footnote or marginal gloss naming John Dee. The audit's claim is confirmed; the fabricated identification has been struck and no replacement guess substituted, per the prime rule. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports conversations about the causes of Tycho's departure from Denmark, defending the fatherland against those who condemn it for losing him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980710001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980810001", "d": "1598-08-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 10 August 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), heavily code-switched into Early Modern Danish · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TAM ANTIQUAe PROSAPIAe QUAM PROPRIA VIRTUTE INPRIMIS NOBILI ET ERUDITIONE SIMUL SOLIDA CLARO VIRO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTEGERRIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>QUAS ante discessum tuum ex ultimis Comitijs Hafniensibus deproperasti, desideratissime mi OLIGERE, Consangvinee, Affinis, et (quod plus reputo) Amice integerrime, his ipsis diebus ob moras, quas isthic nectebat MULIUS, pridie accepi. Erantque utut festinanter scriptae, et ad singula, quae expectabam, propter otij penuriam non respondentes, admodum gratae. Quod literae Electoris et Electorissae Brandenburgensis Regi et Reginae oblatae non sint, parum moror. Neque enim ob [id] eas misi, quod magnopere cuperem, ut exhiberentur. Ideoque (uti scis) liberum id tibi reliqui. Scio eas scatere [politic[is]mis et aulicismis], uti fit apud tales homines. Allene det var anderledes lovet; naar jeg seer til, da colluderer de begge [Canceler] med hverandre, for den eene ravn piller ikke øyet ud paa den anden: quamvis alterum magis integrum et sincerum duxerim, quam vestrum istum NICOLAI MACHIAVELLI Neophitum, qui adhuc quotidie discit a suo [praeceptore contiguo]. Nec tamen id quod tu insinuas, inde inferri posse, si traditae fuissent literae, existimarim. Nam conditio mea nullatenus isthic defletur. Et cur hoc, cum ego ipse hanc praetulerim et etiamnum praeferam priori? Neque commutarem, om man endskiønt vilde give mig Penge til, som naar man bytter. Nec ullis modis reditum captavi per literas illas, utut tam vestratibus quam mihi invisum, plus tamen forte mihi quam illis. Ideoque sollicite excepi apud Principem hunc urgentem, ne tale quid verbo scriptove moveret. Neque eae, quas Bramstedij illi obtuli, quarum exemplar (ni fallor) habes, tale quid prae se ferunt. Recompensationem solummodo expensarum moderate requirebam, suo forte tempore validius. Videbo, quomodo literae illae suis restituantur autoribus, modo id citra offensam per aliqualem excusationem fieri queat, vel (si mavis) praetextum. Quod autem Regi libros destinatos non tradideris, nec tuum nec aliorum probo consilium. Maluissem enim certis de causis mature exhibitos, quam retentos. Neque tamen suspicor, te tenacitate quadam, et amore mei atque librorum eos pro te ipso servare voluisse, ne forte aemuli nostri et malevoli ad se [dono Regis] reciperent. An [Regi] placituri sint nec ne, susque deque fero. Nec sane ob id illi communicandos censeo, quod sic ipsius gratiam aucupari cupiam; cum sat sciam, ne quidem gratiori munere aliquo aut quovis officio ob eos, quibus magis auscultat, id impetrari posse (quod et cordate fero) vel literae meae Rostochio scriptae cum suo responso (necdum uti pollicebaris, critizatae) satis superque testatum relinquunt. Nec si[c] intempestive [supplex] fio, cum nihil petam, nihil deprecer, nihil supplicem, sed potius labores meos quos [ille] cum suis aspernatus est, me Caesari nunc dedicare, aliumque atque adeo summum Imperij caput pro studiorum meorum Maecenate quaerere, posthabitis invidentibus et detrectantibus [Danis] ostendo ostentoque, ut hoc potius (si quid foret insimulandum) culpae imputari posset, quam quod nimis me demitterem. Tu itaque, mi OLIGERE, omnino libros [Regi] trade in manus, sive [volenti] sive [repugnanti], aut si id tibi commodum non fuerit, fac ut per aliquem alium meorum, aut etiam [RAMELIUM] id fiat; ita tamen, ut per quoscunque sive temet ipsum vel alios Regi porrigantur, tibi, siquidem Rex (uti scio) talibus [imo nullis (quod dolendum)] libris delectatur, cedant. Invenies facile rationem. Quod et antea, cum mecum hîc esses, praemonui. Sin [Rex] ipsemet (quod [miraculi] loco duxerim) eos retinere voluerit, aut etiam praeter omnem nostram praevisionem et amolitionem in aliorum, quorum quam minime vellemus, potestatem devenerint, id ferendum erit. Habeant, quod tacite suspiciant atque admirentur, quodque capere aut imitari non possint, palam vero contra conscientiam (uti solent) rodant, extenuent, invertant et cavillentur. Id populus curat scilicet. Faxo autem, si res ita ceciderit, ut tu nova, etiam picta et ornata obtineas exemplaria, quibus hanc iacturam leves, praefixis etiam carminibus, sed in te directis, qualia nuper, dum Duci IOHANNI ADOLPHO hos libros dono mitterem, praeposueram, quorum tenorem in adiuncta scheda habes. Liber de Stellis erat in charta, non membrana scriptus. Et hunc Duci ULDARICO Megapolitano non concessi, cum alterum nuper antepositis etiam suis carminibus dono mitterem. Quanti utrunque fecerit, quam comiter et benigne TENGNAGLIUM meum exceperit, quam gratus erga me fuerit, atque in posterum esse velit, et quam clementi denique favore me prosequatur Sermus Elector Coloniensis, ex ijs, quae ad CRAGIUM nostrum scripsi, cognosces, et aliquatenus etiam ex adiunctis literarum exemplis, quae cum CRAGIO quoque communices, praesertim quae antea non habet, velim, ut et ille nostra tibi. Quae enim alteri vestrum scribo mittoque, utrique scribo et mitto pauculis differentibus. Exemplum scripti istius famosi apud CRAGIUM videbis, quale mihi Helmstadio missum est. Addidit quidam ex meis studiosis annotationes marginales, ubi praecipue fit mei honorifica mentio, ne diu tam memorabilia locum quaerenda forent. Egregius sane liber, qui a Professoribus Hafniensibus tanto applausu, licet tacito murmure inter eos susurrato excipiatur. Gaudent monstris monstrosa ingenia et prava pravis. Ostendet tibi dictus noster communis secreto literas quasdam a [IOANNE STUGGIO] IOANNI Omnivendae, vel potius Ardelioni ex composito scriptas adinstar Beinenkorf, ut ille et ij, quibus hoc scurrile scriptum magni et grati muneris instar congratulatus est, sibi sic assensum et laudem praeberi frustra laetentur; aut si fucum capiunt, sciant alios scire, quam parum veri bonique illi sciant vel sapiant, quamque insulsi invidi et malevoli sint censores, quodque nos eos clam se titillantes et conridentes palam pungamus et rideamus, imo ex alto despiciamus. Librum autem hunc, qui mea Elogia depraedicat et ebuccinat, mihi per hunc ipsum nuncium remitti volo. Nullum enim aliud habeo exemplar. Ubi plura nactus fuero, vobis et alijs communicabo. Eroque adhuc facilior, quam institor iste vester librorum. Nam gratis dabo et aperte faciam, quod is subdole et tecte. Ephemerides istae novae ex novis (si Dijs placet) Tabulis supputatae ne quidem apud Germanos fidem merentur, quam autor in Praefatione venditat, nedum apud [vos Danos], qui terrena potius quam Coelestia curatis. Heu tam aversus Equos vestris Sol iungit ab oris. In Septentrione enim semper habitavit barbaries. Et verendum, ut ne nunc quidem satis extirpata sit, aut postliminio redeat, contemptis ingenuis et humanioribus literis unâ cum virtutis integritate. Quod Cognatus meus [LINDENOVIUS], ea quae ipsemet non invite, antequam disiungeremur, promisit, nunc concedere tergiversetur, licet tunc aeque erat in Politicis prudens atque nunc, non miror. Cimber enim est, cur non Cimbrizet et nippizet? Quamvis (ut dicam quod res est) parum curem, sive communicet, sive non. Potioribus enim intentus sum, quam ut talibus Mundanae scenae frivolis nugis immorer. Volebam saltem memoriae et rei bene gestae causa hanc historiolam penes me habere, att man kunde laese den som et Eventyr om Afftenen. Scripsit is ad me, et quod in diversis locis haereret haec confabulatio, non mitti excusavit. Tum quoque de Comoedia, quae isthic a personato Anglo introducta est, quodque ferme in Tragoediam versa fuerit, indicavit. Kan skee den [gammele] pater conscriptus lader ikke af, furijs sic impellentibus, etiam in defunctum saevire, førend han staar op igien og giør Jertegn. Mones, et quidem recte, ut circumspecte in quibusdam agam. Quod et facio, faciamque. Imo plus taceo, dissimuloque in tantis iniuris, tamque falsis obiectionibus, quam forte opus esset. Sed serviendum est tempori et loco. Quod differtur, non aufertur. Sunt Regibus, fateor, longae manus. At Philosophis et veritatem tuentibus adhuc longiores et fortiores. Nam ipsorum Imperia et Iurisdictiones etiam ultra fata mortalium se extendunt. Caetera momentanea et caduca sunt. Tandem bona causa triumphat. Reges et Dominos habere debent, qui se non habent, et non concupiscunt, quae Reges Dominique concupiscunt. Illi semper metuant, ne fractus labatur axis, et pavidos feriant ruinae. Rex est, qui metuit nihil. Hoc Regnum sibi quisque dat. Sed de his satis.</p><p>Aedes meas Hafniacas pro duobus millibus non vendam. Constiterunt mihi, antequam ultimo ibi essem, et quaedam in ijs complevissem, ultra 3000 Ioachimorum. Tunc vero vix intra millenos in eas ulterius erogavi. Satis est constans structura, quae alijs in Civitate lapideis domibus duratione sua non cedet. Nec fundus ille paludosus, quem tanta arte emendavi, ponderosiorem sustinuit. Si vendi iusto precio nequiverint, date saltem operam, mei Amici, ut inquilinum fidelem pro ijs ad Michaëlis festum obtineam, qui id quod aequum erit, inde solvat. Causa PEPLERI etiam invitis (uti existimo) ijs, qui eam promovere satagebant, male illi cessit, et prout revera meritus erat, imo mitius. Si Amstelredami mansisset, neque [HUITFELDIO] auscultasset, sed apud me in Norvegia rursus habitandi veniam unâ cum culpa deprecatus fuisset, siquidem me ad condonandum non futurum difficilem ISAACUS PETRI iam antea illi indicarat, forte rectius sibi consuluisset. Sed confisus meis adversarijs nunc praedominantibus (apud quos ego, etiamsi Geometrice demonstrarem, nihil veri iustique obtineo) maluit hanc Camarinam movere, et maiora subire pericula maioresque miserias. Cum BOeKELIO per MULAeUM uti voluisti, agam, ubi fuerit, nunc enim peregre abest. Quid obtinuero, ex adiuncto responso videbis. Pecuniam totam iam antea tradiderat illi WINSHEMIUS, me inscio, nec tamen, postquam rescivi, multum dissentiente. Erat enim is valde importunus, praesertim vero uxor; et minabantur, at de vilde slaae Skade og Interesse paa. Et nescio, quae alia crocitabant. Quid igitur faceremus? Litem forensem ob id movere tibi forte ingratum fuisset, nec nobis volupe. Et habet is inter iudices sibi addictos, ut vix aliquid obtinuissemus. Nam et hîc iudicia sunt personalia, non realia, ferme ut apud vos, licet paulo minus ob processum Iuris Civilis in cognoscendis causis et non praeiudicandis magis aequum et legitimum. Dabo operam, quantum in me est, ut eos, quos adhuc desideras, nanciscaris. Quod si obtinere nequivero, transeant cum caeteris. Satis habes librorum. Unica SOPHIA omni praevalebit Philosophiae, etiam Platonicae et veriori, nedum Aristotelicae et fucatae, atque in Logimachijs verborum fundatae. Ignosces, quod de Praeceptore tuo id quod res est, dicam. Nam et ipsi Itali, qui alias ei admodum mancipati sunt, illum hoc (uti scis) elogio depraedicare non sunt veriti:</p><p>Doctorem calamo ingratus, Dominumque veneno<br>Perdidit, igne Patrum dogmata, nos tenebris.</p><p>Non tamen reprehendenda duxerim, quae in ipso laudabilia et recte constituta sunt, qualia te Lynceis oculis discernere posse non ambigo. Praestabit tamen (uti opinor) vivae illi palpabili et tractabili SOPHIAe operam dare, quae pro libris liberos dabit, pro facundia faecunditatem, ut pulchra faciet te prole parentem. Quod ante revolutionem Phoebi annuam faxit Deus. Cecini tibi breve et fortuito incidens Epigramma loco Epithalamij; quod nescio an acceperis, siquidem nullam in literis eius facis mentionem, nisi serius forte nactus sis, quam eas scriberes. In omnem itaque eventum denuo reliquis, quae mitto, cum alias sit breve, addidi. Restituas mihi velim carmen CRAMERI tui, quod cum caeteris in hoc genere coacervare lubet. Multa enim hinc inde talia ab Eruditis in mei gratiam cuduntur, ut suo tempore videbis. Plura nunc non addo. Ubi tu plura rescripseris et currum promissum, quem indies expeto expectoque, ne iter meum retardetur, miseris, ulterius respondebo. Vide saltem, ne quidpiam eorum silentio praetereas, de quibus antea movi, quaeque memoriae causa denotata habes. Iamque felix et incolumis cum tua costa vale, et matrem tuam officiose ex me saluta. Hancque non neglige, atque non detento hoc Nuncio actutum rescribe. Iterumque vale. Dabantur Wandesburgo die 10. Augusti Anni 1598.</p><p>BOeKELIUS hisce diebus domi non fuit, ut responsum eius tibi mittere nunc possem. Quam primum is redierit, omnem hac in re movebo lapidem, ut desiderijs tuis satisfiat.</p><p>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man as noble in his most ancient lineage as in his own virtue, and renowned withal for solid learning, Master Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm — my kinsman, kinsman-by-marriage, and most upright friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>The letter which you dashed off before your departure from the last Diet at Copenhagen, my most longed-for Holger — kinsman, kinsman-by-marriage, and (what I value more) most upright friend — I received only yesterday, in these very days, owing to the delays that Mule was contriving there. And, however hastily written, and not answering — for want of leisure — each of the points I was awaiting, it was most welcome. That the letters of the Elector and Electress of Brandenburg were not presented to the King and Queen, troubles me little. For I did not send them because I greatly desired that they be displayed; and so (as you know) I left that to your discretion. I know they teem with political and courtly turns, as happens with such men. <i>Only, it was promised otherwise; when I look into the matter, the two Chancellors collude with one another, for the one raven does not peck out the other's eye:</i> though I should reckon the one of them more upright and sincere than that Niccolò-Machiavelli novice of yours, who still daily learns from his neighbouring preceptor. Yet I do not think that what you hint at could be inferred from it, had the letters been delivered. For my condition is by no means bewailed there. And why should it be, since I myself have preferred, and still prefer, this present state to my former one? Nor would I exchange it — <i>even if one were to throw in money besides, as when one strikes a bargain.</i> Nor did I in any way angle for a return through those letters — though such a return is hateful both to your countrymen and to me, yet perhaps more to me than to them. And so I anxiously took care, with this Prince who was pressing the matter, that he should raise no such thing by word or in writing. Nor do the letters which I presented to him at Bramstedt — of which (unless I am mistaken) you have a copy — carry any such intent. I was only moderately requesting compensation for my expenses — more forcefully, perhaps, in its own good time. I shall see how those letters may be restored to their authors, provided it can be done without offence, by some excuse — or (if you prefer) pretext.</p><p>But that you did not deliver the books intended for the King, I approve neither your counsel nor that of the others. For, for certain reasons, I should have preferred them presented in good time rather than held back. Yet I do not suspect that you wished, out of some tenacity and love of me and of the books, to keep them for yourself, lest perhaps our rivals and ill-wishers should receive them to themselves as the King's gift. Whether they will please the King or not, I take with complete indifference. Nor indeed do I judge that they should be imparted to him because I would thus be eager to court his favour; since I know well enough that not even by some more pleasing gift, nor by any service, could that be obtained — on account of those to whom he gives more ear (which I bear with equanimity) — as my letters written from Rostock, together with their reply (not yet \"critiqued,\" as you were promising), leave more than sufficiently attested. Nor do I thus unseasonably turn suppliant, since I ask nothing, beg off nothing, entreat nothing, but rather I show — and make a show — that I now dedicate to the Emperor my labours, which he with his counsellors spurned; and that, the envious and disparaging Danes set aside, I seek another, indeed the supreme head of the Empire, as the Maecenas of my studies — so that this (if anything were to be charged against me) might rather be imputed as a fault than that I should abase myself too far.</p><p>You therefore, my Holger, by all means deliver the books into the King's hands, whether he will it or resist; or, if that is not convenient for you, see that it be done through some other of my people, or even through Ramel — yet so that, by whomsoever they are handed to the King, whether yourself or others, they fall to you, since the King (as I know) takes delight in such books — nay, in no books at all, which is to be lamented. You will easily find a way; as I forewarned you before, too, when you were here with me. But if the King himself should wish to keep them (which I should count a kind of miracle), or even if, beyond all our foresight and contriving, they should come into the power of others into whose hands we should least wish, that must be borne. Let them have something to look up to and marvel at in secret — something they can neither grasp nor imitate — but openly, against their conscience (as is their wont), gnaw at, belittle, pervert, and carp at. The common people care for that, forsooth. But I shall see to it, should the matter so fall out, that you obtain new copies — illustrated and ornamented, too — by which you may relieve this loss, with verses also prefixed, but addressed to you, such as I had lately prefixed when I sent these books as a gift to Duke Johann Adolf; the tenor of which you have in the enclosed slip. The book of the Stars was written on paper, not on vellum; and this one I did not grant to Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, when I lately sent him the other book as a gift, with its own verses likewise prefixed. How highly he valued both; how courteously and kindly he received my Tengnagel; how grateful he was toward me, and wishes to be hereafter; and finally with how clement a favour the Most Serene Elector of Cologne attends me — you will learn from what I have written to our Krag, and to some extent also from the enclosed copies of letters, which I would have you share with Krag too, especially those he does not already have, just as he shares mine with you. For what I write and send to either of you, I write and send to both, with a few small differences.</p><p>At Krag's you will see the copy of that infamous writing, such as was sent to me from Helmstedt. One of my students added marginal notes, especially where honourable mention is made of me, lest such memorable passages should have long to be sought out. A fine book indeed — received by the Copenhagen professors with such applause, though with a muffled murmur whispered among themselves! Monstrous wits delight in monsters, and crooked wits in crooked things. Our said common friend will show you in secret certain letters written by Johann Stuggius to one \"John Sell-all\" — or rather to some busybody — composed by arrangement, in the manner of a <i>Bienenkorb</i> [beehive-lampoon], so that he, and those to whom he presented this scurrilous writing as though a great and welcome gift, may vainly rejoice that assent and praise are thus offered them; or, if they perceive the trick, let them know that others know how little of truth and goodness they know or savour, and what tasteless, envious, and ill-willed critics they are, and that we, while they tickle and laugh among themselves in secret, openly prick and laugh at them — nay, look down on them from on high. But this book, which proclaims and trumpets my praises, I wish sent back to me by this same messenger; for I have no other copy. When I have got more, I will share them with you and others; and I shall be even more obliging than that book-peddler of yours, for I shall give freely and do openly what he does slyly and covertly.</p><p>Those new Ephemerides, reckoned from new Tables (if it please the gods!), do not earn even among the Germans the credit that the author vaunts in his Preface — still less among you Danes, who care for earthly things rather than heavenly. <i>Alas, so turned away from your shores does the Sun yoke his horses!</i> For in the North barbarism has always dwelt; and it is to be feared that even now it is not sufficiently rooted out, or may return by right of postliminy, with liberal and humane letters despised together with the integrity of virtue. That my kinsman Lindenov now shuffles over granting what he himself, not unwillingly, promised before we parted — though he was then just as prudent in political matters as now — I do not wonder. For he is a Cimbrian: why should he not play the Cimbrian, and haggle? Though (to say the truth) I care little whether he imparts it or not, for I am bent on weightier matters than to linger over such frivolous trifles of the worldly stage. I only wished, for memory's sake and the record of a thing well managed, to keep this little history by me — <i>that one might read it like a fairy-tale of an evening.</i> He wrote to me, and excused that the account was not sent, since this story was held up in various places. He also reported about the comedy that was put on there by a masked Englishman, and how it nearly turned into a tragedy. <i>Perhaps the old \"conscript father\" does not cease — the furies so driving him — to rage even against the dead, before he rises up again and works portents.</i> You advise me — and indeed rightly — that I should proceed circumspectly in certain matters — which indeed I do and shall do. Nay, I keep silent and dissemble, amid such great injuries and such false accusations, more than perhaps is needful. But one must serve the time and the place. What is deferred is not taken away. Kings have long hands, I confess; but philosophers and the guardians of truth have longer and stronger still. For their dominions and jurisdictions extend even beyond the destinies of mortals, while the rest is momentary and perishable. In the end the good cause triumphs. Those who do not possess themselves must have kings and lords — and they do not covet what kings and lords covet. Let <i>those</i> ever fear lest the broken axle-tree slip and the ruins strike them in their terror; <i>he is a king who fears nothing — this kingdom each man gives to himself.</i> But enough of these matters.</p><p>My house at Copenhagen I will not sell for two thousand. It cost me, before I was last there and had finished certain things in it, over 3,000 joachimsthalers; and then I spent scarcely a further thousand or so upon it. It is a sound enough structure, which in durability will not yield to the other stone houses in the city; nor has that marshy ground, which I improved with such art, borne any heavier building. If they cannot be sold at a fair price, at least take pains, my friends, that I may obtain a trustworthy tenant for them by Michaelmas, who will pay from it what is fair. The case of Pepler turned out badly for him — even against the will (as I think) of those who were busy promoting it — and as he had truly deserved, nay, more mildly. Had he stayed at Amsterdam and not listened to Huitfeldt, but rather begged leave to dwell with me again in Norway, together with pardon for his fault — since Isaac Petersen had already told him that I would not be hard to forgive — he would perhaps have counselled himself better. But, trusting in my adversaries now in the ascendant (with whom I obtain nothing of truth or justice, even were I to demonstrate it geometrically), he chose to stir up this Camarina, and to undergo greater dangers and greater miseries. As for your wish to make use of Boekel through Mule, I will deal with him when he is back, for he is now abroad; what I shall have obtained, you will see from the enclosed reply. Winsheim had already handed over the whole sum to him, without my knowledge — yet, after I learned of it, not much against my will; for he was very importunate, and especially his wife, and they threatened <i>that they would impose damages and interest,</i> and I know not what else they croaked. What, then, were we to do? To start a lawsuit over it would perhaps have been disagreeable to you, and no pleasure to us; and he has among the judges men devoted to him, so that we should scarcely have obtained anything. For here too the courts are personal, not impartial, much as with you — though somewhat less so, on account of the procedure of the Civil Law, which is more equitable and lawful in trying cases and not prejudging them. I will take pains, so far as in me lies, that you obtain those you still want; but if I cannot obtain them, let them pass with the rest. You have books enough. A single Sophia will outweigh all philosophy — even the Platonic and truer sort, still more the Aristotelian and counterfeit kind, founded on word-battles. You will forgive me for saying of your Preceptor what is the truth; for even the Italians themselves, otherwise quite enslaved to him, have not feared to extol him (as you know) with this epigram:</p><p><i>The ingrate destroyed his Teacher with the pen, his Lord with poison, the Fathers' doctrines with fire, and us with darkness.</i></p><p>Yet I would not have those things blamed which in him are praiseworthy and rightly established — such as I do not doubt you can discern with lynx eyes. Still it will be better (as I think) to devote your effort to that living, palpable, and tractable Sophia, who will give you children for books, fecundity for eloquence, so that she will make you, fair one, a parent with offspring — which may God bring to pass before Phoebus's yearly revolution. I sang you a short epigram, struck off by chance, in place of an epithalamium; whether you received it I do not know, since you make no mention of it in your letter — unless you got it later than you wrote them. And so, against every contingency, I have added it again to the rest of what I send, since it is short anyway. I should like you to return to me the poem of your Cramer, which it pleases me to heap up with the others of this kind; for many such are coined here and there by the learned in my honour, as you will see in due time. I add no more now. When you have written back further, and have sent the promised carriage — which I daily desire and await, lest my journey be delayed — I will answer at greater length. Only see that you pass over in silence none of those matters I raised before, and which you have noted down for memory's sake. And now, happy and unharmed, fare well with your rib, and dutifully greet your mother on my behalf; do not neglect her, and, without detaining this messenger, write back at once. Again, farewell. Given at Wandsbek, the 10th day of August, the year 1598.</p><p>Boekel has not been at home these days, so that I cannot now send you his reply. As soon as he has returned, I will move every stone in this matter, so that your wishes may be satisfied.</p><p>Yours, Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (an <i>Afskrift</i> / later copy, not Tycho's autograph). Square brackets in the source block reproduce the editor's conjectural readings/insertions (e.g. <i>[id], [Canceler], [praeceptore contiguo], [volenti], [repugnanti], [gammele]</i>); they are kept as printed per protocol §4.2.</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Dabantur Wandesburgo die 10. Augusti Anni 1598</i> — agrees with the catalogue label. <b>Wandsbek</b> (then <i>Wandesburg</i>, the Rantzau estate just east of Hamburg) was Tycho's residence from late 1597 through summer 1598, between his departure from Denmark and his move toward the imperial court. Tycho kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm</b> (1574–1642), later called \"the Learned\" — Danish nobleman, theologian, and Tycho's kinsman. The triple address <i>consanguineus, affinis, amicus</i> (\"kinsman, kinsman-by-marriage, friend\") is literal: the Rosenkrantz and Brahe houses were repeatedly intermarried.</li><li><b>The book-gifts — the central thread.</b> \"These books\" are Tycho's two 1598 presentation pieces, dispatched to princely patrons during the Wandsbek campaign for new patronage: the printed <b>_Astronomiae instauratae mechanica_ (Wandsbek, 1598)</b> and the <b>manuscript star-catalogue</b> (the <i>\"Liber de Stellis,\"</i> the 1000-star catalogue, here noted as <i>\"written on paper, not on vellum\"</i> — grander recipients received the de-luxe vellum copy). <b>Duke Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp</b> received <i>both</i>; <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b> received <i>only</i> the other (the <i>Mechanica</i>), <i>not</i> the star-book. This dovetails exactly with <b>brahe-t_15980816001</b> (16 Aug 1598), where Duke Ulrich, having received Tycho's gift and seen the Hven instruments, recommends Tycho to Rudolf II. The verses Tycho \"prefixed\" are the dedicatory poems; he promises Holger fresh illustrated copies with verses re-addressed to him.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><b>Mule (<i>Mulius</i> / <i>Mulaeus</i>)</b> — a Danish intermediary/agent handling Tycho's correspondence and the \"Boekel\" business; not securely identified here.</li><li><b>The Elector and Electress of Brandenburg / the King and Queen</b> — Christian IV of Denmark and his new queen <b>Anne Catherine of Brandenburg</b> (married Nov 1597), daughter of Elector <b>Joachim Friedrich of Brandenburg</b>; hence the Brandenburg-court letters addressed to the Danish royal pair (the bride's family). <i>Identification of the King and Queen as Christian IV and Anne Catherine is secure from the dynastic logic; treat as MEDIUM-HIGH.</i></li><li><b>The \"two Chancellors\" / the \"Machiavelli novice\" with his \"neighbouring preceptor\"</b> — the Danish chancery leadership, satirised: the senior figure (the <i>praeceptor</i>) and his junior pupil (<i>Neophitus</i>). The Danish Rigskansler at this date was <b>Arild Huitfeldt</b> and the Kansler <b>Christian Friis</b> (to Kragelund); Tycho does not name them, and the pairing is left <b>unidentified</b> in the translation rather than forced. <i>Crux — see Confidence.</i></li><li><b>Niels Krag (<i>Cragius</i>)</b> — Danish humanist, historian and diplomat, Tycho's correspondent and the shared conduit (\"our said common friend\") for the circulating documents.</li><li><b>Frans Gansneb Tengnagel (<i>Tengnaglius</i>)</b> — Tycho's assistant (later son-in-law), here received \"courteously and kindly\" by the Elector of Cologne.</li><li><b>The Most Serene Elector of Cologne</b> — <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>, Archbishop-Elector of Cologne; Tycho courts his favour during the patronage search.</li><li><b>Johann Stuggius (<i>Ioannes Stuggius</i>)</b> — author of the anti-Tycho satirical letters circulating from the Copenhagen academic milieu; cf. the hostile \"Stygius\" letter <b>brahe-t_15980710001</b>. <i>\"John Sell-all\" (Ioannes Omnivenda)</i> and <i>\"the busybody\" (Ardelio)</i> are derisive nicknames for the recipients/peddlers of the lampoon, not real names.</li><li><b>Lindenov (<i>Lindenovius</i>)</b> — Tycho's kinsman, reneging on a promise; mocked as a <i>Cimber</i> (Jutlander) who would <i>\"Cimbrize and haggle.\"</i></li><li><b>Pepler (<i>Pepleri</i>)</b> — a former associate/servant of Tycho's whose lawsuit went badly; he had once dwelt with Tycho in Norway (Tycho's fief), had moved to Amsterdam, and (against the advice relayed through <b>Isaac Petersen</b>) \"listened to <b>Huitfeldt</b>\" rather than seeking Tycho's pardon.</li><li><b>Henrik Ramel (<i>Ramelius</i>)</b> — German-born Danish statesman, an alternative deliverer of the books to the King.</li><li><b>Boekel (<i>Boekelius</i>), Winsheim (<i>Winshemius</i>)</b> — parties to a money matter Holger had asked Tycho to pursue; Winsheim had paid over the disputed sum; Boekel was abroad.</li><li><b>Cramer (<i>Crameri</i>)</b> — a poet in Holger's circle whose verse Tycho wishes returned for his collection of complimentary poems.</li><li><b>The \"Ephemerides … from new Tables.\"</b> A recently-published set of ephemerides, disparaged by Tycho as over-praised in its own preface; the author is unnamed and <b>not securely identified</b> (a contemporary ephemeris-maker, e.g. of the Magini/Origanus type). Left general in the translation.</li><li><b>Code-switches into Danish (protocol §2).</b> The letter repeatedly drops from Latin into Early Modern Danish for sardonic, proverbial, or confidential asides — a hallmark of Tycho's intimate correspondence. The italicised English passages render: <i>\"Only, it was promised otherwise … the one raven does not peck out the other's eye\"</i>; <i>\"even if one were to throw in money besides, as when one barters\"</i>; <i>\"that they would impose damages and interest\"</i> (<i>slaae Skade og Interesse paa</i>, a legal threat); <i>\"that one might read it like a fairy-tale of an evening\"</i> (<i>et Eventyr om Afftenen</i>); and <i>\"before he rises up again and works portents\"</i> (<i>førend han staar op igien og giør Jertegn</i>). All are translated, never dropped.</li><li><b>Classical and Scriptural allusion.</b></li><li><i>\"the one raven does not peck out the other's eye\"</i> — the Latin proverb <i>cornix cornici numquam confodit oculum</i> (here in Danish), of those who shield one another.</li><li><i>\"stir up this Camarina\"</i> — the Greek proverb <i>Καμάριναν κινεῖν</i> (\"to move Camarina,\" a marsh best left undisturbed; cf. Virgil, <i>Aen.</i> 3.701), i.e. to invite trouble.</li><li><i>\"Kings have long hands\"</i> — Ovid, <i>Heroides</i> 17.166 (<i>an nescis longas regibus esse manus</i>).</li><li>The closing sententiae are <b>Senecan</b>: <i>\"he is a king who fears nothing — this kingdom each man gives to himself\"</i> = Seneca, <i>Thyestes</i> 388–390 (<i>rex est qui metuit nihil … hoc regnum sibi quisque dat</i>); <i>\"lest the broken axle-tree slip and the ruins strike them\"</i> echoes Horace, <i>Odes</i> 3.3.7–8 (<i>si fractus illabatur orbis, impavidum ferient ruinae</i>).</li><li>The verse couplet on <b>Aristotle</b> (<i>\"The ingrate destroyed his Teacher with the pen…\"</i>) is a circulating Italian anti-Aristotelian epigram: Aristotle charged with ruining Plato (his <i>Doctor</i>) by his pen, Alexander (his <i>Dominus</i>) by poison, the Church Fathers' doctrines by fire, and posterity by darkness. Tycho cites it to needle Holger's Aristotelianism — <i>\"your Preceptor.\"</i></li><li><b>The <i>Sophia</i> pun.</b> <i>\"Unica SOPHIA … vivae illi palpabili et tractabili SOPHIAe\"</i> and <i>\"cum tua costa\"</i> (\"with your rib,\" Gen. 2:21–22 for <i>wife</i>) turn on a double sense: <b>Sophia = Wisdom</b> and a bride named <b>Sophie</b>. Tycho urges Holger to prefer the <i>living</i> Sophia — marriage and children (<i>pro libris liberos</i>, \"children for books\") — to bookish philosophy, and reports having sent a short <b>epigram in place of an epithalamium (wedding-song)</b>. The wife is most plausibly <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (daughter of Axel Brahe), whom Holger Rosenkrantz married in this period; <i>identification MEDIUM — left as the text's pun rather than asserted.</i></li><li><b>Self-positioning.</b> The letter is a key document of Tycho's <b>post-Denmark patronage turn</b>: he insists he is no suppliant (<i>\"I ask nothing … I dedicate my labours to the Emperor\"</i>), seeks \"the supreme head of the Empire\" (Rudolf II) as his <i>Maecenas</i>, and frames the rupture with Denmark so that any fault lies with the <i>\"envious and disparaging Danes,\"</i> not with himself. The Copenhagen house he refuses to undersell (&gt;3,000 dalers spent; seeking a tenant by Michaelmas) shows him still managing Danish property in absentia.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored 'Mones, et quidem' (dropped from the published Latin) before 'recte, ut circumspecte in quibusdam agam', per TBOO VIII p.90; the English '[The messenger advised me]' was a fabricated supplement filling the resulting gap and is replaced with 'You advise me', matching Dreyer's 2nd-person 'Mones' addressed to Rosenkrantz.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. The Latin and the embedded Danish are rendered securely and the letter's substance (the book-gifts to Johann Adolf and Ulrich, the patronage turn toward Rudolf II, the Pepler and Winsheim/Boekel affairs, the <i>Sophia</i>/marriage pun, the classical apparatus) is clear and cross-corroborated (esp. with brahe-t_15980816001 on Duke Ulrich, and brahe-t_15980710001 on the Stuggius/\"Stygius\" lampoon). Residual uncertainties, none obscuring sense: the <b>\"two Chancellors\" / \"Machiavelli novice\"</b> are deliberately unnamed by Tycho and left unidentified rather than forced onto Huitfeldt/Friis; the <b>author of the disparaged Ephemerides</b> is unnamed; the bride behind the <i>Sophia</i> pun is left as the pun (most plausibly Sophie Brahe); and a handful of editorial bracket-readings (<i>[praeceptore contiguo], [gammele]</i>) are the edition's conjectures.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.87-93 as page images in two chunks (pages 1-4, 5-7; letter proper on pp.88-92). Checked opening/closing lines and every proper name, date, sum of money, and place-name: Rosenkrantz/Rosenholm salutation, 10 August 1598 date and Wandsbek dateline, money figures (2000 / 3000 / ~1000 Joachimsthalers) all match; place-names Bramstedt, Rostock, Helmstedt, Copenhagen, Mecklenburg, Cologne, Amsterdam, and Norway all confirmed verbatim including the unusual 'apud me in Norvegia' and 'ISAACUS PETRI', which the translation renders correctly. Found one genuine text-fidelity defect: our source_text omits 'Mones, et quidem' present in Dreyer p.90, and the English's bracketed supplement for that gap assigns the advice to 'the messenger' rather than to the letter's recipient, whom Dreyer's actual verb addresses in the second person. Also flagged a minor low-confidence mistranslation (not entered as a defect due to uncertainty about the idiom 'fucum capiunt') and a low-severity gender-attachment slip in 'pulchra... te... parentem' (English attaches 'fair one' to the male addressee, which is grammatically impossible for 'pulchra'; correct referent is Sophia or the offspring) — omitted from the defects list as sub-threshold given the TEXT_ERROR already determines the grade.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Our published source_text silently drops the two words 'Mones, et quidem' ('You advise, and indeed') that Dreyer prints, leaving the sentence without its verb or subject. Dreyer prints: <i>...førend han staar op igien og giør Jertegn. Mones, et quidem recte, ut circumspecte in quibusdam agam. (TBOO VIII p.90, col. 2, ~l.33-36)</i>.<br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (medium). The bracketed supplement invents 'the messenger' as the advice-giver; Dreyer's restorable verb 'Mones' shows the advice came from the letter's addressee (Rosenkrantz) himself, not a messenger — a wrong actor, not a neutral fill-in. At: “[The messenger advised me] rightly, that I should proceed circumspectly in certain matters.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated directly against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VIII p.90 (slice pages 3-6 of 7, covering pp.89-92), which reads '...førend han staar op igien og giør Jertegn. Mones, et quidem recte, ut circumspecte in quibusdam agam. Quod et facio, faciamque.' — confirming both the TEXT_ERROR (dropped 'Mones, et quidem') and the FABRICATION (the invented 'messenger' as advice-giver, where Dreyer's 'Mones' is unambiguously 2nd-person singular, addressed to Rosenkrantz). No crux remains; both defects fully resolved against the edition of record.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15980628001", "next": "brahe-t_15980905001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports receiving Holger's delayed letter, notes the Brandenburg electoral letters were not presented to the King, and alludes to collusion between the two Chancellors.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980810001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980816001", "d": "1598-08-16", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": "Rudolf 2.", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → Emperor Rudolf II — Güstrow, 16 August 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy; headed<i> Copia literarum</i>)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>COPIA LITERARUM ILLUSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS, DOMINI VDALRICI DUCIS MEGAPOLENSIS AD IMPERATOREM.</p><p>ALLERDURCHLEUCHTIGSTER, großmechtigster und unuberwindtlichster römischer kayser. E: Kay: Majtt: ßeindt meine allerunterthenigste gehorßamste bereitwillige dienste jederzeit zuvor. Allergnedigster herr, E: Rom: Kay: Maj:tt gebe ich hiemit allerunterthenigst zuerkennenn, daß gegenwertiger der ehrnveste Tycho Brahe mit seinem gantzen haußweßen auß seinem vaterlandt, dem königreich Dennemarcken, gezogen, nicht aber darumb alß ob er da nicht woll hette bleiben mugenn, oder auch in einiger ungnade bey der Kön: Maj:stt daßelbst ßein ßolte, sondernn alleinn aus denn urßachen, nachdem er vermercket undt vernommenn, daß die astronomische sachen, die er inn ßo viel iahren mit großer mühe undt unkost alda angerichtet undt getribenn, wie vor beschehen, wieder ßein verhoffenn nicht geachtet noch befürdert werden mögen, darumb er sich selbst willig inn Teutschlandt weitere und beßere gelegenheit dazu zu suchen begebenn. Unndt ob ich woll derowegenn, alß er sich inn meiner stadt Rostock aufgehaltenn, an die Kön: M: zu Dennemarcken, meinen freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten herrn sohn, ein freundtlich unndt fleißigs erinnerunges schreibenn gethan, dieße astronomische sachen dero königreich zu ehren zu restituiren unndt zu unterhaltenn, weil aber darauff nun einn ganzes iahr verfloßenn, unnd ich nicht spühren könnenn, daß er revocirt werde, unndt gleichwoll solche herrliche kunst, an sich selbst werth, daß sie unterhalten unndt promoviret werde unnd großer potentatenn patrocinii woll bedarff, unnd schade, das dieß so weit numehr gebrachte werch inn ein abnehmenn kommen solte, dan ich bekennenn muß, das mir dergleichenn instrumenta mathematica, alß ich bey ihm auff der insull Huen im königreich Dennemarcken gesehenn, an andern örtern niemalß vorkommenn: Alß habe ich nebenst andern vor guth angesehenn, nachdem es im reich Dennemarcken nicht mehr geschehenn kann, das sie irgendt im Teutschlandt gebürlichen unterhalten unnd foviret würde, wie dießelbige vonn alters hero allezeit von vielen königen unndt potentaten nach außweißung der löblichen exempel, so E: Kay: Maj:stt auß den historien, mehr dann gnugsamb bekandt, lieb unndt in würdenn gehaltenn worden, unndt weil dann E: Kay: Maj:st, alß daß oberhaubt deß heiligenn römischenn reichs, ein ßonderliche zuneigung zu offtberürter kunst tragen, so habe ich demnach nicht unterlaßen können, E: Röm: Kay: Maj:st ermelten Tychonem Brahen mit derselbigenn weitberümbtenn kunst allerunterthenigst zu recommendiren, unndt bitte E: Röm: Kay: Maj:st darauff unterthenigstes höchstes fleißes, sie wolle es nicht anderß dann gnedigst vermerckenn, unndt sich mehrgedachtes Tychonis Brahen unndt seines herrlichen studii allergnedigst annehmenn unndt ihm alle kayserliche guthe befurderung widerfahrenn lassenn. Daß binn ich umb E: Kay: Maj:st (ßo ich damit göttlicher almacht zu aller glücksehligen wolfart, derselbenn aber mich auch zu fernern kayserlichen gnaden, allerunterthenigst unnd getreulich befehle) jederzeit unterthenigst unnd gehorsambst zuverdienen willig unnd geflissenn.</p><p>Datum Gustrow den 16. augusti anno 1598.<br>E: Röm: Kay: Maj:ts unterthenigster unnd gehorsamer furst<br>Ulrich<br>herzog zu Meckelnburgk.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Copy of the letter of the Most Illustrious Prince, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, to the Emperor.</b></p><p>Most serene, most mighty, and most invincible Roman Emperor. To Your Imperial Majesty [be] my most humble, most obedient, and ready services at all times, beforehand. Most gracious Lord: I herewith most humbly give Your Roman Imperial Majesty to know that the present honourable Tycho Brahe has departed, with his whole household, from his fatherland, the kingdom of Denmark — not, however, as though he could not well have remained there, or should be in any disgrace with the Royal Majesty there, but solely for the reasons that, after he had noted and learned that the astronomical matters which he had over so many years set up and pursued there, with great labour and expense, could — contrary to his hope — no longer be valued nor furthered as before, he therefore willingly betook himself into Germany to seek further and better opportunity for it. And although I therefore, while he stayed in my city of Rostock, sent to the Royal Majesty of Denmark, my dear and heartily-beloved Lord son, a friendly and earnest letter of reminder, that he restore and maintain these astronomical matters to the honour of his kingdom — yet, since a whole year has now elapsed thereupon, and I have not been able to perceive that he is recalled; and since such a splendid art is in itself worthy that it be maintained and promoted, and has good need of the patronage of great potentates; and [since it would be] a pity that this work, now brought so far, should fall into decline — for I must confess that the like mathematical instruments, such as I saw with him on the island of Hven in the kingdom of Denmark, have never come before me in any other place — therefore I, together with others, have deemed it good that, since it can no longer happen in the realm of Denmark, it be fittingly maintained and fostered somewhere in Germany; as the same [art] has from of old always been held dear and in honour by many kings and potentates, according to the showing of the praiseworthy examples more than sufficiently known to Your Imperial Majesty from the histories. And since Your Imperial Majesty, as the head of the Holy Roman Empire, bears a special inclination toward the oft-mentioned art, I could not therefore omit most humbly to recommend the said Tycho Brahe, with that same far-famed art, to Your Roman Imperial Majesty; and I ask Your Roman Imperial Majesty thereupon, with most humble and utmost diligence, that you would take it in no other than the most gracious way, and most graciously take up the oft-mentioned Tycho Brahe and his splendid study, and let all imperial good furtherance befall him. For which I am, toward Your Imperial Majesty (whom I herewith most humbly and faithfully commend to divine omnipotence for all blessed welfare, and myself to your further imperial graces), at all times most humbly and obediently willing and zealous to deserve well.</p><p>Given at Güstrow, the 16th of August, the year 1598.<br>Your Roman Imperial Majesty's most humble and obedient prince,<br>Ulrich,<br>Duke of Mecklenburg.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Significance — the imperial commendation itself.</b> This is the <b>copy (<i>Copia literarum</i>) of Duke Ulrich III's letter recommending Tycho to Emperor Rudolf II</b> — the <i>\"splendid letter of commendation to the Roman Imperial Majesty\"</i> that <b>Tycho thanks Ulrich for in brahe-t_15980914002</b> (14 September 1598). Written from <b>Güstrow, 16 August 1598</b>, during Tycho's stay at Rostock, it is a cornerstone document of the campaign that brought Tycho to the imperial court (cf. the later imperial-establishment letters: brahe-t_16000106001, brahe-t_16000324001).<br>- <b>Sender.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603). He addresses <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> as <i>\"meinen freundtlichen hertzvielgeliebten Herrn Sohn\"</i> (\"my dear, heartily-beloved Lord son\") — a <b>courtesy kinship-term</b>: Christian IV was in fact Ulrich's <b>grandson</b> (Ulrich's daughter Sophie of Mecklenburg married Frederik II; Christian IV was their son). The dynastic-courtesy force is kept literal with this note, per protocol (no silent \"correction\").<br>- <b>The argument Ulrich makes to the Emperor.</b><br>  1. <b>Tycho left Denmark neither in disgrace nor for inability to stay</b>, but solely because his astronomical work \"could no longer be valued nor furthered as before\" — the diplomatic framing of the rupture (consonant with Tycho's own account, e.g. brahe-t_16000323001).<br>  2. Ulrich had <b>reminded Christian IV</b> (during Tycho's Rostock stay) to \"restore and maintain\" the work \"to the honour of his kingdom\" — but a year passed with no recall.<br>  3. <b>First-hand testimony:</b> \"the like mathematical instruments, such as I saw with him <b>on the island of Hven</b>, have never come before me in any other place\" — Ulrich had <b>personally visited Tycho on Hven</b> and seen the instruments. A valuable independent witness to the Uraniborg installation.<br>  4. The appeal to Rudolf II as <i>\"head of the Holy Roman Empire,\"</i> himself inclined to the art, with the <i>exempla</i> of kings and potentates who patronised astronomy \"from the histories\" — and the request that the Emperor <b>take Tycho and his study under imperial patronage and furtherance</b>.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Ulrich III of Mecklenburg (sender); <b>Rudolf II</b> (addressee); <b>Christian IV</b> of Denmark (<i>\"Herr Sohn\"</i> = grandson); Tycho Brahe; the island of <b>Hven</b>; Tycho's stay at <b>Rostock</b>.<br>- <b>Register / formulae.</b> High imperial-petition German (<i>Allerdurchlauchtigster … unüberwindlichster römischer Kaiser</i>; <i>E: Kay: Maj:tt</i> = <i>Euer Kaiserliche Majestät</i>). Latin terms embedded (<i>restituiren, promoviret, patrocinii, recommendiren, instrumenta mathematica</i>) per protocol §2. The heading <i>Copia literarum</i> marks this as the archival copy in Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Güstrow den 16. Augusti anno 1598</i>; Lutheran Mecklenburg kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the document's substance (the diplomatic account of Tycho's departure from Denmark; Ulrich's prior reminder to Christian IV; his first-hand testimony to the Hven instruments; the formal recommendation of Tycho to Rudolf II's patronage) is clear, important, and directly corroborated by Tycho's thank-you letter (brahe-t_15980914002). The one interpretive point — <i>\"Herr Sohn\"</i> as a courtesy term for Ulrich's grandson Christian IV — is kept literal and explained, not smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.144-147 as page images; the Ulrich-to-Rudolf II letter (no. 153, 1598, 16 aug.) runs from p.145 ('COPIA LITERARUM ILLUSTRISSIMI PRINCIPIS...') through p.146 ('...Ulrich herzog zu Meckelnburgk.'); pp.144 and 147 hold unrelated neighbouring letters and were not needed. Collated the full German text word for word against Dreyer: matches source_text exactly, including the sum-free recommendation, the reference to the Hven instruments, and the date (16. augusti anno 1598, Güstrow). Checked published_english against this verified text clause by clause: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that Tycho left Denmark voluntarily when his astronomical work could no longer be valued, and that Ulrich interceded with the Danish king on his behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980816001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980820001", "d": "1598-08-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Niels Krag", "ck": "Niels Krag", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Niels Krag — Wandsbek, 20 August 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Danish and German code-switches and Latin verse · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>; orig. in<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek (Afskrift / copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i>, Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Editorial note:</b> the witness has two short <b>lacunae</b> (gaps where the copy is defective), reproduced in the source block as runs of dots and marked <b>[lacuna]</b> in the translation; they are the named crux behind the [C] grade.</p>", "la": "<p>EXCELLENTISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO NICOLAO CRAGIO I. V. DOCTORI, HISTORICO REGIO ET ACADEMIAe HAFFNIENSIS PROFESSORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO EX INTIMIS UNI ET SINCERE DILECTO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>ANTE octiduum primum huc reversus est MULAeUS, literasque tuas, Praestantissime et amantissime Vir, attulit, multo serius quam sperâram. Detentus quidem ab ESCHILLO et ACCIO, at non, ut causas morae interea per literas significaret prohibitus. Quod tamen serio ei in mandatis dederam. Nec tanta illi secreta aut mysteria scribenda erant, quod interfuerit, in quorumcumque tandem manus literae eae venirent. Et quod omnium maxime displicet, praecipuo negocio, nempe pecuniario, infecto reversus est. Quod tamen non tam illi quam dictis duobus imputo, et ACCIO potius quam ESCHILLO. Nam hic ministrum suum Flensburgum misit pro duobus millibus Ioachimicis meâ causâ isthic obtinendis, sed frustra. Ille solutionem summae debitae tergiversatur, et dilationem petit ob debita fratris defuncti et nescio quas alias impensas; cum tamen sit in bona re, et Cancellario nunc affine gaudeat, quem diutina avaritia et tenacitas satis nummosum reddidit. Huor kand da AXEL staa til at trenge? At ego totius summae restitutionem denuo urgeo. Nolo enim quidpiam mearum pecuniarum amplius in Dania haerere, praesertim in istâ invisâ affinitate. Ego quidem non incircumspecte hactenus eidem ACCIO scripsi. Nam quamvis non obscura mihi cum eo intercesserit familiaritas praeter sangvinis rationem, quae hac tempestate etiam inter ipsos fratres in aquam degeneravit (ideoque eam minus reputo) tamen nos satis novimus nos, et plus esse in verbis quam factis compertum habemus. Si is serio rem egisset tacentibus fratribus meis caeterisque consangvineis, et Regno et Regi et Parenti servatus fuisset is, de quo scribis, forte honor. Ille enim ferme solus, quae et quanta in istâ Insulâ confeceram, rectius novit, partim ob habitationis suae vicinitatem, dum Landscroniae Praeses erat, partim quod nonnunquam me invisere solitus, eaque inspicere, et de ijs me referentem audire. Attamen ad literas mihi obsignatas, quibus unâ subscripserat, non minus quam caeteri obticuit, magno equidem omnium illorum honore, et ad posteritatem historijs commendando. O prisca fides! ô obsoleta veterum Danorum constantia! sed fit nunc perinde, prout Germania nostra vestrae nuper scripsit Daniae:</p><p>Curat se quilibet unum,<br>non alios, metuitque sibi, si causa probetur<br>alterius, sit iusta licet.</p><p>Sed utinam haec de solo ACCIO, quod ad plurimos alios, qui ipso mihi coniunctiores esse debebant, redundat, dicere liceret. Det var en gott, om der var ingen vaerre end hand. Si non multum boni praestat, attamen non ita nocet, ut caeteri. Och det er en stor naade i denne tiid. Et interdum etiam consolari videtur, meliorisque fortunae spem facere, quam tamen in vestrâ Daniâ nec exspecto nec expeto. Bene mecum actum iudicarem, si absentem et non modice laesum immeritis cavillis et calumnijs persequi nonnulli e vobis abstinere possent, quamvis et has nugas non plus morer, quam Cynthia pleno sublimis fulgensque lumine canum oblatrantium insipientiam, quos et generosi equi negligentes praetereunt, modo non plane mordeant. Tunc enim ne calcitrant. Egregiam sane consolationem vafra ista et Machiavellica inter quosdam e vobis Gymnasiarchis confederatio hinc nacta est, quod vetus ille meus domesticus, qui per me ad potiora promotus est, nunc ingratus cuculus ijs scurrile et mendacissimum efferi istius URSI scriptum impertitus sit. Sic enim habent quo sibi abblandiantur, quasi de re bene gesta, quod eorum antesignanus ipsissima ut ut latens caussa fuerit, cur patria me nunc careat: exiguâ sane iacturâ, si ista convicia atque calumniae vim Geometricae veritatis obtinerent. Dersom den herlige Distractor famosorum librorum sad endnu i Narrehuset thill Augsburg, der jeg en gang løste ham ud for 18 Cruner, cum vinum suffuratus erat, da skulle hand sidde der saa lenge til hand kunde recitere Bogen uden ad, och kand skee aede den op til bedringe, førend jeg skulle løse ham der ud igien, ut et ipse sic ex hac incorporatione melius addisceret, quam antea mentiri, pugillare, fallere, peierare, et plures eiuscemodi virtutes, quibus liber iste scatet, et quarum ipse non ignarus est. Istud impurum scriptum Academiae Witembergensis, Lipsensis, Helmstadiensis, Rostochiensis et plures in Germaniâ detestantur, authoremque eius tanquam bonorum consortio amplius indignum, qui tamen apud quosdam vestratium decantatur, laudatur (got folch tilhobe) execrantur; quod et literis apud me testatum reliquerunt. Brisgoiensis sane Academia non obscure mihi significavit, se in Poëtastrum istum, qui contumeliosum illud distichon concinnârat et subiunxerat, prout meruit, animadversuram fuisse (modo is author esset, et non potius ursina bestia) si adhuc in vivis esset. Nam ante quadriennium in summa egestate eum obijsse mihi relatum est. Sic et viri Principes tantam impudentiam abominantur. Apud Brunsvicensem author officij, quod in schola ambivit, ea de causa repulsam passus est. Dux ADOLPHUS Holsaticus, quamprimum naenias istas vidit, statim addita execratione folia omnia laceravit, atque ad Cancellarium suum D.D. IONATHAM GUTSLOVIUM librum igne absumendum proiecit. Quod et factum est. RANZOVIUS noster non minus movebatur paucis solummodo introspectis verbis, omnia dira authori imprecans, et capitis poena mulctandum secundum IOB tam infandum facinus censens. En nonnulli ex eruditis vestris huic applaudent, in sinu vident, sibique hunc librum congratulantur, ne quid superesset, quod ingrata Patria, aut potius in ea ingrati quidam contra me auderent. Id tamen cum caeteris omnibus flocci facio, nec calumniatorem istum responsione dignum arbitror, sed poena potius secundum statuta Imperij. Quod et me impetraturum confido. Tunc demum triumphent ipsius applausores. Man skulle med rette iche rose aff Marchen, førend torffvet bleff holdet. Sed valeant et iste et isti, indigni de quibus haec scriberem, non sine chartae detrimento, cui vesanum istud scriptum obfuit, non vero existimationi nostrae, ut cum SCALIGERO nostro loquar. Quare pluribus de his nunc supersedeo.</p><p>A SCALIGERO, cuius modo memini, litteras mihi attulit FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS meus ante biduum redux, rebus bene expeditis. Thi det var paa thiden en gang att tale om gott folch, och lade de andre fare. Est autem Epistola illa admodum erudite et humaniter scripta (uti sunt summi illius viri omnia) cuius exemplum tecum communico. Quae de Polis diversis Mundi et Aequatoris, deque Polari stellâ, fixarum progressu, anni quantitate, PTOLOMAeI crassa hallucinatione ex ortu Caniculae eodem die Iuliano, tot interlapsis seculis, atque similibus commemorat, minus sibi constant. Nam et bonus interdum dormitat HOMERUS. Quâ de re, ubi rescribendi otium nactus fuero, ipsum candide admonere constitui. Is nostra plurimi facit, uti non saltem ex his literis, sed et ex ipsa redintegrata temporum emendatione nuper publicatâ liquet, in quâ meas Aequinoctiorum observationes, quas Danicas vocat (som vel forskyldt er a quibusdam Danis, att de skulde saa kaldis) supra istius HIPPARCHI inventa (quae tamen et PTOLOMAeI et omnium veterum traditionibus praefert) longe evehit. Utrum ipsius hîc censurae, qui rem ponderat atque intelligit, potius standum sit, quam Curiae Magistri apud vos, qui horum plane rudis est et nil nisi pecunias somniat, ac Rusticorum opera Astronomiae et Iurisprudentiae anteferre non erubescit, tibi et alijs absque glaucomate negocium introspicientibus aestimandum relinquo. Is adeo laetatus est, quando TENGNAGLIUS cum meis illum accessit, ut prae gaudio exilierit, et quodvis humanitatis officium adeoque pecuniam, si eâ destitueretur (quod alias rarissimum est) illi obtulerit, nihilque magis in votis habuit, quam ut a Batavis accirer. Ideoque in partes Imperatoris minus inclinavit. Et cum de pollicitis Principis MAURITIJ, qui etiam mihi Germanice admodum humaniter scripsit, ac BARNEFELDIJ nonnulla illi retulisset FRANCISCUS, subiunxit SCALIGER, lenta admodum esse illorum consilia. Sed se eos adhortaturum, ut mature rem agant, et me eo pertrahere elaborent. Omnino enim oportere ut Batavis, non alijs cedam.</p><p>Qua vero clementiâ et benignitate Elector Coloniensis (qui Caesaris potius partes hac in re tuetur, ut et tu) eum exceperit, verbis satis exprimere non potest. Mansit apud ipsum usque in quartam hebdomadam, singulis pene diebus ad secretum eius colloquium per aliquot horas vocatus, adeo ut consiliariorum suorum interpellationes subinde eâ de caussâ averterit, multa sciscitatus, multa respondit, libros meos diligenter evolvit, laudavit; scripta etiam quaecunque FRANCISCUS ex meis secum habuit, ad se recepit, pauca restituit nec ea nisi descripta, adeo ut ne carminibus quidem in mei gratiam concinnatis parcere voluerit, occasiones abitus mei e Dania non sine admiratione expendit, et percunctatus FRANCISCUM quantum id fuerit, quod istic quotannis in reditibus habuerim, respondente illo, et summam in honorem Regis et Regni aliquantulum augente (det var noch hand var eders landmand) quatuor millia thalerorum eam attigisse; subiunxit Princeps haec formalia verba: Was? Solte man sölichen Man vmb so ein geringes Geltt aus dem Lande ziehn lassen? Schande ist es. Ein Herr verspielet mehr auff einen abent; Gelt bekommet man allezeit, aber sölche leute nicht allzeitt. Tandem litteras promotoriales ad Imperatorem exquisitâ diligentiâ ipsemet et composuit, et suapte manu scripsit, inscio tam Cancellario quam Secretario, quarum tenorem unâ cum ijs, quas ad BARBITIUM supremum Imperatoris Secretarium, qui ab ipso ad hoc dignitatis fastigium evectus est, et annua etiamnum habet stipendia, pariter dedit, te accepisse existimo. Scripsit etiam ad me succinctas et eruditas, quas suo cubiculario mane in lecto ipsemet dictavit, eo quod FRANCISCUS abitum festinaret. Earum etiam hîc exemplum habes, cum ijs, quas ad Dominum RANZOVIUM unâ dedit. Placuit illi quam maxime, quod in Germania postmodum habitare decreverim et se charissima omnia, quae haberet, depositurum aiebat, quod Imperator Clementissimo et summo favore me meaque studia sit prosecuturus et liberaliter sustentaturus; addebatque, quod si Caesar praeter omnem suam exspectationem in eo difficilem se praeberet, tunc seipsum proprijs impensis id effecturum, et omnino curaturum, ne extra Germaniam abeam, aut ad Danos redeam: egitque Deo gratias, quod ipsi iam propior essem quam antea; et siquidem ipsum tam cito ac vellem, invisere (quod tamen admodum in votis habuit) haud possem, se quendam nobilium suorum intimum huc ad me missurum, antequam in superiorem Germaniam me conferrem, dicebat, qui de secretis quibusdam ad artes abstrusas pertinentibus mecum ageret, atque ad ipsum ea quae percepisset, referret. Et ut paucis rem absolvam (siquidem omnia narrare nimis foret prolixum) cum alias tum praesertim ubi FRANCISCO discedenti valediceret, tenens illum diu per manum, his ipsis verbis conclusit: Sagett dem gutten ehrlichen Man viel gutes von meinent Wegent, meinen gnedigen Grues, auch daneben, das er mich freilich von seinen besten freundt halten soll den er hatt, vnd das ich ihn hin wiederumb nicht anders haben, vnd bey ihm thun will, als wan er mein leiblicher Bruder were, vnd ich ihn mein lebenlang vnd von jugent auff gekantt hette. So wohl gefältt er mir. Vnd so baldt ich gehen Prag kommen werde, will ich ihn besuchen, es sei wo er in Ihr Kais. Mayt. gebiet zu finden: adeo certus erat, se isthic me reperturum. Atque haec de optimi illius Principis Clementi erga me favore paucis indicata sunto. Ex quibus tute caeteros parvo aestimavit, nulli facile caetera colliges, quodque non tam invisus atque despectui habitus sim in Germania atque apud quosdam in Dania, vel inde ratiocinaberis. Quin et vestrates etiamnum omnem mei curam abijcere video, nec quicquam ut isthuc absque meo incommodo restituar, sollicitos esse. Nam si eo usque expectent, donec ego ijs supplex futurus sim, ad calendas Graecas (uti tu recte auguraris) expectabunt. Nihil equidem satius ducerem quam Patriae et Regis mei honori studere, vel ob optimum illum et laudatissimae memoriae FRIDERICUM huius Parentem, qui praeclare de me meritus est. Sed quid faciam, cum ipsi me respuerint, contempserint, detrimento non exiguo atque multis iniurijs affecerint, odio et invidia persecuti sint, artis, quam tam diu excolui, quamque totus pene orbis a me efflagitat, instaurationem non saltem non promovere voluerint, sed et sustentationem denegarint, ac subsidia, quae prius habui, subtraxerint? Si mihi more caeterorum Nobilium vivendum foret, nec altiora peragenda suscepissem, reliquos inglorius annos isthic traxissem, Bacchum et Cererem consumere natus. Haec et similia RAMELIO nostro, qui erga te ob itineris molestias non ingratus (quod laudo) fuit, quemque ego pro sincero habeo Amico, indices quaeso, ne interrogatus de abitus mei caussis respondeat se nullas scire, sed me sponte meâ citra ullas necessitates isthinc abijsse; veluti heri Generosus Dominus GULIELMUS SLAWATA, Liber Baro in Chlum et Koschumbergk, ex commendatione RANTZOVIJ me hîc in visens mihi retulit, sibi perconctanti a RAMELIO responsum esse. Satis quidem polite et Politice, an vero satis caussae Amici rationes ita habuerit, qui sic leviter et citra iustas rationes quidpiam admisisse tacite insimulatur, praeteritis ijs, qui in causa fuerunt, non dixerim. Quod Rex Serenissimus, quotiescunque mei incidit mentio, nullam malevoli animi significationem ostendat, veluti tu scribis, et RAMELIUS etiam CLAUDIO MULAeO insinuavit, et credo et lubenter audio. Nam cur male volet illi, qui ipsum dicto factove nunquam offendit, caussas non habet. Sufficit me tacere, et quae passus sum, dissimulare. Si reliqui duo nunc proximi a Rege, quibus meae emigrationis caussas verius imputo, adhuc clanculario mei laborant odio, fit id iuxta proverbium: Quem laeseris, oderis; ideoque nihil novi. Quod etiam ex alto potioribus intentus negligo.</p><p>Etsi vero per totam pene aestatem de Caesaris Clementissimo erga me favore et propensione, tum quoque aliorum quorundam Principum, mihi constiterit; tamen nihil movere, aut certi quid de mea hinc profectione statuere volui, antequam ............. huc reversus, ut id quod iam .............. nullos videlicet isthic vel ex ............ ipso nihil dicam, qui talibus minus attendit) mearum rerum curam suscepturos, vel Patriae caussa nedum mei. Idque nunc satis demonstratum arbitror, ut eo magis excusabilis sim apud omnes cordatos et sinceros viros, quod me alio, re sic urgente, conferam. Constitui itaque favente Divinâ clementiâ, quamprimum currus meos, quos adorno, caeteraque parata habuero, Pragam proficisci atque Caesareae Maiestatis in me Clementissimam voluntatem experiri, relictâ hîc interim familiâ meâ muliebri, ad quam aut ante hyemem, si Deo ita visum fuerit, revertar; aut si Caesar id voluerit, mihique loci oblati commoditas placuerit, eam isthuc addito certo et fideli comitatu evocari curabo. Si conditiones apud Caesarem meis desiderijs non arriserint, ad Coloniensem me convertam. Et si nec ibi, ad Batavos. Pollicitus enim est Princeps MAURITIUS, se apud ordines confoederatos meâ de caussa sedulo acturum. Idem et BARNEVELDIUS addixit se facturum, quamprimum ex Anglia redierit, ubi nunc Legatum agit. Sed FRANCISCUS, qui cum utroque oretenus hac de re egit, ut secreta omnia haberent, nec quidpiam Ordinibus proponerent, eos tacite monuit, donec de mea voluntate certi quid resciscerent. Aequum enim esse, ut Imperatori meam operam honoris caussa primum offeram. Quam si renuerit, tunc demum de alijs medijs cogitandum. In Angliam quibuscunque tandem pollicitationibus allectus vix iturus sum. Nam et mihi mores gentis suspecti sunt, praeter id quod Reginae vetulae obitus magnos motus ciebit peregrinis maxime formidabiles et perniciosos. Quae omnia propterea fusius tibi scribo, ut de meis consilijs mearumque rerum statu te (quod non dubito) sollicitum certiorem facerem.</p><p>Quod literae Electoris Soceri Regis non sunt exhibitae, parum moror. Nam et parum habent ponderis, nimisque Politice et aulice conscriptae. Non amplius mare Balthicum transibunt. Videbo tamen, quomodo apud Electorem et Electorissam, qui mihi favere videri volunt, id civiliter excusem. At libros quos Regi destinavi, maluissem oblatos esse certis de causis: non tamen quod ego aut sperem aut cupiam, Regem eos magni facturum, qui alias videtur literis non admodum delectari. OLIGERUM etiam nostrum instruxi, quomodo is eos statim dono obtineret, ne in aliorum potestatem devenirent; non quod inimicorum cavillationes et malevolas interpretationes verear, talibus satis assuetus, sed ne ij, quos indignos reputo, meis gaudeant laboribus. Quocirca eos adhuc Regi per OLIGERUM ............ uti et illi scripsi. Si tamen id facere non audeat ............ ita tamen ut libri OLIGERO, si Rex eos (u ti ........... alij cedant. Habebit RAMELIUS, si voluerit, altera Exemplaria. Ad caetera quae scribis, quia me parum concernunt, non respondeo. Id saltem superaddam, si PETRUS BRAHE cognatus meus, quem fratris loco semper dilexi, propterea corbizatori et magno meo bono foedifrago isti valetudinem primogeniti sui filij, de cuius indole multa praeclara inaudieram (adeo ut sperârim eum familiae nostrae, modo disciplinae isthic aliquem locum mererentur, decus futurum) concredidit, eumque sic amisit, proh dolor (cum plus saepe periculi a Medico quam morbo, praesertim tam vulgari) certe male pro se ultus est iniuriam, si qua erat, quod cum CRASSAeO bis vel ter Rostochij locutus sum, et nullum tamen aliud officium aut operam ipsi praestiti. Neque enim ab omni humanitate tam alienus esse poteram, ut tergiversatione eius alloquij satis superque afflicti afflictionem adderem, qui commiseratione potius quam odio dignus est. Et si Salvatoris nostri monitis auscultaret PETRUS, culpam improvisam tam enixe deprecanti Dei saltem et conscientiae caussâ remitteret. Sed fit ut plurimum id quod infers; volumus nobis maxima a Deo debita condonari; nos autem proximo ne minima quidem. Vere igitur nostri Deus misereatur, atque hanc etiam obstinationem, et ab eius voluntate aversionem clementer ignoscat. Cui te tuamque ELSULAM et liberos omnesque tuos commendo. Vale, et prolixitatem hanc boni consule. Dedi tibi sic exemplum, ut et tu solito fusius ad me scriberes. Ignoramus enim an postea, cum longius disiuncti fuerimus, ea dabitur, quae nunc, literas inter nos commutandi oportunitas. Quae de Caesare et Coloniense, meisque rebus nunc indicavi, sorori meae, ESCHILLO BILLEO et ROSAeCRANTZIO nostro secreto impertire, nec ullis alijs. Iamque iterum unâ cum ijs caeterisque Amicis, qui forte (uti fit) nunc mihi de multis unus et alter erunt, quam felicissime vale.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgo die 20. Augusti st. vet. Anno 98.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>Addidi præter ea, quæ ultimo misi, et alia duo Exemplaria<br>literarum THADDÆI, quas ante<br>triduum accepi, ut hinc videas, quam serio me expectent.</p><p>Cum IOHANNES STYGGIUS, qui nunc<br>Hamburgi est, undecunque rescivisset tandem, quosdam ex Professoribus apud vos<br>famosum istud de me scriptum magni facere, quodque OLBURGENSIS id illis procurarit, tantæ iniquitatis impatiens,<br>quasdam ad ipsum direxit literas, ita consulto scriptas, quasi hoc factum<br>approbare et laudare videretur: quemadmodum author libri, quem Beinkorf vocant, Pontifices contrarium sentiendo commendat. Mittit<br>tibi MULÆUS exemplum, quod ipsi STYGGIUS tradidit, tibi impertiendum. Habeas tamen<br>omnia secreta. Nec nisi cum OLIGERO nostro aut etiam sorore quidpiam hac de re communices. Et tacite interim inquiri<br>procures, quomodo hæ literæ factioni cui OLBURGENSIS adhæret, placuerint. Rogo, velis hunc amanuensem<br>meum Germanicum promovere in ijs, quæ isthic meo nomine expedienda habet, atque<br>urgere, ut quam citissime huc revertatur: idque intra 14 dies, si ullo modo<br>fieri possit, ac remittas cum eodem famosum istud scriptum. Neque enim eo carere<br>possim ob mea elogia quæ deprædicat; siquidem hoc<br>unicum habeam Exemplum, et author huius non palam sed clam distrahi<br>procuret.</p><p>Addidi Exemplar Germanicum caussarum discessus mei e Dania, quod Domino RAMELIO per occasionem legendum dare poteris; ut si nescius sit,<br>quas habuerim abitus rationes, et immemor eorum, quæ cum ipso Rostochij locutus sum, hinc discat, et in memoriam<br>revocet; alias forte hæc ipsa penitius et ulterius consignabo. Tu interim chartam hanc a RAMELIO receptam secreto penes te serva in memoriam rei bene<br>gestæ, cum sis historicus. Vix enim in cæteris veriora habes documenta: Licet<br>historiam Huennensem posteriorum præsertim<br>Annorum narrare non audeas, quia veritas odium parit. Facient id tamen forte<br>alij. Neque enim ignota est hæc Metamorphosis, aut alibi ira tacita ut apud nos.<br>Scribunt enim et loquuntur multi ea de re, quos mallem tacere.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent and outstanding man, Lord Niels Krag, Doctor of Both Laws, Royal Historian and distinguished Professor of the Copenhagen Academy, his one intimate and sincerely beloved friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Eight days ago Mule first returned here, and brought your letter, most excellent and most loving Sir — much later than I had hoped. He was indeed detained by Eske and by Accius, but not prevented from signifying meanwhile by letter the causes of the delay — which I had seriously charged him to do. Nor were such secrets or mysteries to be written by him that it mattered into whose hands at last those letters came. And — what displeases me most of all — he has returned with the chief business, namely the money, unaccomplished. Which, however, I impute not so much to him as to the two named men, and to Accius rather than to Eske. For the latter sent his servant to Flensburg to obtain two thousand joachims there on my account, but in vain. The former shuffles over the payment of the sum owed, and asks for a postponement on account of his dead brother's debts and I know not what other expenses — although he is in good circumstances, and now rejoices in being related by marriage to the Chancellor, whom long avarice and tenacity have made rich enough. <i>How then can Axel be in straits?</i> But I again press for the restitution of the whole sum; for I will have no more of my money stuck fast in Denmark, especially in that hateful affinity. I have indeed written to this Accius not incautiously hitherto. For although a not obscure familiarity has subsisted between him and me, beyond the tie of blood (which in this age has degenerated into water even among brothers themselves — and so I reckon it the less), yet we know each other well enough, and have found that there is more in his words than in his deeds. Had he handled the matter seriously — my own brothers and the rest of my kinsmen keeping silent — there would perhaps have been preserved, both for the Kingdom and the King and his Father, that honour of which you write. For he, almost alone, more rightly knows what and how much I accomplished on that island — partly because of the nearness of his dwelling, while he was Governor of Landskrona, partly because he was wont sometimes to visit me, and to inspect those things, and to hear me reporting on them. Yet to the letters sealed to me, which he had jointly signed, he kept silent no less than the rest — greatly indeed to the honour of all of them, and to be commended to posterity in the histories! O ancient faith! O obsolete constancy of the old Danes! But it now goes just as our Germany lately wrote to your Denmark:</p><p><i>Each man cares for himself alone,</i><br><i>not for others, and fears for himself, if another's cause be approved,</i><br><i>however just it be.</i></p><p>But would that this could be said of Accius alone — [a thing] which overflows to very many others, who ought to have been more closely bound to me than he. <i>It would be a good thing, if there were none worse than he.</i> If he furnishes not much good, yet he does not harm as the rest do. <i>And that is a great grace in these times.</i> And he even seems sometimes to console me, and to give hope of better fortune — which, however, I neither expect nor seek in your Denmark. I should reckon myself well dealt with, if some of you could refrain from persecuting an absent and not lightly injured man with undeserved cavils and calumnies — though I heed these trifles no more than Cynthia [the Moon], on high and shining with full light, [heeds] the folly of the barking dogs, which even generous horses pass by, neglecting them, so long as they do not actually bite. For then they do not even kick. A fine consolation indeed has that sly and Machiavellian confederacy among certain of your Gymnasiarchs got from this — that that old member of my household, who was promoted by me to better things, now an ungrateful cuckoo, has shared with them the scurrilous and most lying writing of that savage Ursus. For so they have wherewith to flatter themselves, as though over a thing well done, that their standard-bearer was the very (though hidden) cause why the fatherland now lacks me — a slight loss indeed, if those revilings and calumnies were to obtain the force of geometric truth! <i>If that splendid distributor of libellous books were still sitting in the madhouse at Augsburg — where I once ransomed him for 18 crowns, when he had stolen wine — then he should sit there until he could recite the Book by heart, and perhaps eat it up for his improvement, before I should ransom him out again;</i> so that he too might thus, from this incorporation, better learn than before to lie, to brawl, to deceive, to perjure, and the more such virtues with which that book teems, and of which he himself is not ignorant. That impure writing the Universities of Wittenberg, Leipzig, Helmstedt, Rostock, and many in Germany detest, and they execrate its author as no longer worthy of the fellowship of good men (though among some of your countrymen he is sung and praised — <i>fine folk all together!</i>); which they have also left attested to me in letters. The Academy of Freiburg-im-Breisgau, indeed, signified to me not obscurely that it would have proceeded, as he deserved, against that Poetaster who had composed and appended that insulting distich (if he were the author, and not rather the ursine beast), had he still been among the living; for I am told he died four years ago in the utmost poverty. So too princely men abominate such impudence. At Brunswick the author suffered, for that cause, a rebuff in an office he sought in the school. Duke Adolf of Holstein, as soon as he saw those dirges, immediately, with added execration, tore up all the leaves, and threw the book to his Chancellor, Doctor Jonathan Gutslovius, to be consumed by fire — which was done. Our Rantzau was no less moved, having looked into only a few words, calling down all curses on the author, and judging that so unspeakable a deed deserved, according to Job, to be visited with capital punishment. Yet behold, some of your learned men applaud this, see it in their bosom [with secret delight], and congratulate themselves on this book — lest anything should be left which the ungrateful fatherland, or rather certain ingrates in it, might dare against me. But all this, with everything else, I count as a straw, nor do I judge that calumniator worthy of an answer, but rather of punishment according to the statutes of the Empire — which I am confident I shall obtain. Then at last let his applauders triumph. <i>One should rightly not boast of the field before the harvest is gathered in.</i> But let both that one and these others fare well — unworthy that I should write these things about them, not without harm to the paper, which that frenzied writing has injured, but not our reputation, to speak with our Scaliger. Wherefore I now forbear to say more of these things.</p><p>From Scaliger, whom I just mentioned, my Frans Tengnagel — returned two days ago, his business well dispatched — brought me a letter. <i>For it was high time, at last, to speak of good folk, and let the others go.</i> That letter is written most learnedly and humanely (as are all the things of that supreme man), of which I share a copy with you. What he recounts about the diverse Poles of the World and of the Equator, and about the Pole-star, the progress of the fixed stars, the quantity of the year, Ptolemy's gross hallucination from the rising of the Dog-star [Sirius] on the same Julian day after so many centuries have elapsed, and the like — these are less consistent. For even good Homer sometimes nods. On which matter, when I have gained leisure to write back, I have resolved to admonish him candidly. He values my work most highly, as is clear not only from this letter, but also from the very renewed <i>Emendation of Times</i> lately published, in which he exalts my observations of the equinoxes — which he calls \"Danish\" (<i>as is well deserved by certain Danes, that they should be so called</i> — !) — far above the findings of that Hipparchus (which, however, he prefers to the traditions of Ptolemy and of all the ancients). Whether one should rather stand here by his judgment, who weighs and understands the matter, than by that of the Master of the Court among you — who is utterly raw in these things and dreams of nothing but money, and does not blush to prefer the labours of peasants to Astronomy and Jurisprudence — I leave to be estimated by you, and by others who look into the business without cataract. He was so gladdened when Tengnagel approached him with my things that he leapt up for joy, and offered every office of kindness, and even money, should he be destitute of it (which otherwise is most rare), and wished for nothing more than that I should be summoned by the Dutch. And so he inclined less to the Emperor's side. And when Frans had related to him some things about the promises of Prince Maurice — who also wrote to me in German most kindly — and of Barneveldt, Scaliger added that their counsels are very slow; but that he would urge them to act in good time, and to labour to draw me thither. For by all means I ought to yield to the Dutch, not to others.</p><p>With what clemency and kindness the Elector of Cologne (who in this matter, like you, rather upholds the Emperor's side) received him, he cannot sufficiently express in words. He stayed with him into the fourth week, called almost every day for some hours to his private colloquy, so much so that he repeatedly turned away the interruptions of his councillors for that cause; he inquired much, answered much, diligently turned over my books, praised them; and whatever writings of mine Frans had with him, he took to himself, restored few, and those only when copied — so that he would not even spare the poems composed in my honour; he weighed the occasions of my departure from Denmark, not without wonder, and having asked Frans how much it was that I had yearly in revenues there — Frans answering (and, for the honour of the King and Kingdom, somewhat augmenting the sum — <i>it was enough that he was your countryman!</i>) that it reached four thousand thalers — the Prince added these very words: <i>\"What? Should one let such a man leave the country for so paltry a sum? It is a shame. A lord gambles away more in one evening. Money one can always get, but such people not always.\"</i> At length he himself both composed, with exquisite diligence, and wrote in his own hand, the promotory letters to the Emperor — unknown to both his Chancellor and his Secretary — the tenor of which, together with those he likewise gave to Barvitius, the Emperor's supreme Secretary (who was raised by him to this height of dignity, and still has yearly stipends [from him]), I think you have received. He also wrote to me a succinct and learned [letter], which he himself dictated to his chamberlain in the morning in bed, because Frans was hastening his departure. Of these too you have a copy here, with those he gave at the same time to Lord Rantzau. It pleased him exceedingly that I had resolved henceforth to live in Germany; and he said he would lay down all the dearest things he had [to ensure] that the Emperor would pursue me and my studies with most clement and supreme favour, and liberally maintain them; and he added that, if the Emperor — beyond all his expectation — should prove difficult in it, then he would himself accomplish it at his own expense, and altogether take care that I should not go outside Germany, or return to the Danes; and he gave thanks to God that I was now nearer him; and since I could not visit him as soon as I wished (which, however, he greatly desired), he said he would send one of his intimate nobles here to me, before I betook myself into Upper Germany, who should deal with me about certain secrets pertaining to the abstruse arts [alchemy], and report back to him what he had learned. And, to dispatch the matter in few words (since to narrate everything would be too prolix), both at other times and especially when he bade farewell to the departing Frans, holding him long by the hand, he concluded with these very words: <i>\"Tell the good honest man many good things on my behalf, my gracious greeting; and besides, that he shall surely hold me for one of the best friends he has, and that I in turn will hold him, and act toward him, no otherwise than if he were my own brother, and as if I had known him all my life and from youth. So well does he please me. And as soon as I shall come to Prague, I will visit him, be it wherever he is to be found in His Imperial Majesty's domain.\"</i> — so certain was he that he would find me there. And let these things be indicated in few words concerning that excellent Prince's clement favour toward me. From which you will easily gather the rest, and reason out, that I am not held so hateful and contemptible in Germany as among some in Denmark. Nay, I see that your countrymen even now cast off all care of me, nor are solicitous that I be restored there without my disadvantage. For if they wait until I shall become a suppliant to them, they will wait until the Greek Kalends (as you rightly augur). I should indeed reckon nothing better than to be zealous for the honour of my fatherland and my King — even for the sake of that excellent Frederik of most praiseworthy memory, this [King's] father, who deserved excellently well of me. But what am I to do, when they have spurned me, despised me, afflicted me with no slight loss and many injuries, persecuted me with hatred and envy, and were willing not only not to promote the restoration of the art which I have so long cultivated, and which almost the whole world demands of me, but even denied its maintenance, and withdrew the supports I had before? If I had to live in the manner of the other nobles, and had not undertaken higher things to be carried through, I should have dragged out my remaining years inglorious there, born to consume Bacchus and Ceres. These and the like, I pray you, indicate to our Ramel — who was not ungrateful to you for the troubles of the journey (which I praise), and whom I hold for a sincere Friend — lest, asked about the causes of my departure, he should answer that he knows none, but that I went away thence of my own accord, without any necessity; as yesterday the Noble Lord Wilhelm Slawata, Free Baron of Chlum and Košumberk, visiting me here on Rantzau's commendation, reported to me that Ramel had so answered him when he inquired. Politely indeed, and politicly; but whether a friend's reasoning had cause enough so to do — [a friend] who is thus tacitly insinuated to have admitted something lightly and without just reasons, those being passed over who were the [real] cause — I would not say. That his most Serene Majesty the King, whenever mention of me falls, shows no sign of a malevolent mind — as you write, and as Ramel also intimated to Claus Mule — I both believe and gladly hear. For he has no cause to wish ill to one who never offended him by word or deed. It suffices me to be silent, and to dissemble what I have suffered. If the two now nearest the King — to whom I more truly impute the causes of my emigration — still secretly labour at hatred of me, it is according to the proverb: <i>Whom you have wronged, you hate;</i> and so [it is] nothing new. Which too, intent from on high on better things, I disregard.</p><p>But although throughout almost the whole summer I have been assured of the Emperor's most clement favour and inclination toward me, and likewise of certain other Princes', yet I was unwilling to move anything, or to determine anything certain about my setting out hence, before [lacuna] returned here, so that what already [lacuna] — namely that none there, or from [lacuna] (of him himself I say nothing, who attends less to such things) — would take up the care of my affairs, even for the fatherland's sake, let alone for mine. And this I think is now sufficiently demonstrated, so that I am the more excusable before all sensible and sincere men in betaking myself elsewhere, the matter so pressing. I have resolved therefore, by the favour of the Divine clemency, as soon as I have my carriages (which I am fitting out) and the rest ready, to set out for Prague and to test His Imperial Majesty's most clement will toward me — leaving here meanwhile my womenfolk, to whom I shall either return before winter (if God so see fit), or, if the Emperor wish it, and the convenience of the place offered please me, I shall have them summoned thither with a sure and faithful escort added. If the conditions with the Emperor do not smile upon my wishes, I shall turn to the Cologne [Elector]. And if not there either, to the Dutch. For Prince Maurice has promised that he will deal diligently on my account with the confederated Estates; and Barneveldt has likewise pledged to do the same, as soon as he returns from England, where he is now acting as Legate. But Frans, who dealt with both of them on this matter by word of mouth, quietly admonished them to keep everything secret, and to propose nothing to the Estates, until they should learn something certain of my will. For it is fair that I should first offer my service to the Emperor for honour's sake; and if he should refuse it, then at last other means must be thought of. To England, by whatever promises enticed, I shall scarcely go; for the manners of the nation are suspect to me, besides that the death of the old Queen will stir great commotions, most fearful and pernicious especially to foreigners. All which I therefore write to you more fully, that I might make you — solicitous, as I doubt not you are — more certain of my counsels and the state of my affairs.</p><p>That the letters of the Elector, the King's father-in-law, were not presented, I little mind; for they have little weight, and are too politicly and courtly composed. They shall cross the Baltic Sea no more. I shall see, however, how I may civilly excuse it with the Elector and Electress, who wish to seem to favour me. But the books which I destined for the King, I should have preferred to have been offered, for certain reasons — not, however, that I either hope or desire that the King will value them greatly, who otherwise seems not much delighted by letters. I have also instructed our Holger how he might at once obtain them as a gift, lest they fall into the power of others — not that I fear enemies' cavils and malevolent interpretations, being well enough used to such, but lest those whom I reckon unworthy should enjoy my labours. Wherefore [I have arranged that] they still [be presented] to the King through Holger [lacuna], as I have also written to him. If, however, he dare not do it [lacuna], yet so that the books fall to Holger, if the King [lacuna] [or to] others. Ramel shall have, if he wish, the other copies. To the rest of what you write, since it little concerns me, I do not reply. This one thing I will add: if Peter Brahe, my kinsman — whom I have always loved in a brother's stead — on that account entrusted to that corbizator and great breaker-of-faith toward my good [name] the health of his firstborn son (of whose disposition I had heard many excellent things, so that I hoped he would be an ornament to our family, if only the disciplines there deserved any place), and so lost him — alas (since there is often more danger from the Physician than from the disease, especially so common a one) — certainly he ill avenged for himself the injury, if any there was, in that I spoke with Crassaeus two or three times at Rostock, and yet rendered him no other office or service. For I could not be so estranged from all humanity as, by a refusal of conversation, to add affliction to one already more than enough afflicted, who is worthy of compassion rather than of hatred. And if Peter would hearken to our Saviour's admonitions, he would, for God's and conscience's sake at least, remit an unforeseen fault to one begging pardon so earnestly. But it mostly happens as you infer: we wish our own greatest debts to be forgiven us by God, but [will forgive] our neighbour not even the least. Truly, then, may God have mercy on us, and clemently forgive this obstinacy too, and this turning-away from His will. To whom I commend you, and your Elsula, and your children, and all yours. Farewell, and take this prolixity in good part. I have thus given you an example, that you too might write to me more fully than usual. For we do not know whether afterwards, when we shall be parted farther off, there will be given the opportunity of exchanging letters between us that there is now. What I have now indicated about the Emperor and the Cologne [Elector], and my affairs, share secretly with my sister, with Eske Bille, and with our Rosenkrantz — and with no others. And now again, together with them and the rest of my Friends, who perhaps (as it happens) will now be, of many, but one or two to me, fare most happily.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, the 20th of August, Old Style, the year [15]98.<br>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p><p>I have added, besides what I sent last, two further copies of a letter of Thaddaeus [Hagecius], which I received three days ago, so that from this you may see how earnestly they look for me.</p><p>When Johannes Styggius, who is now at Hamburg, had at last learned from whatever quarter that certain of the professors among you make much of that notorious writing about me, and that Olburgensis had procured it for them, being unable to bear so great an injustice, he directed a letter to him [Olburgensis], deliberately written so as to appear to approve and praise this deed — just as the author of the book which they call \"Beinkorf\" commends the Pontiffs while feeling the contrary. Mulaeus sends you the copy which Styggius himself handed over, to be shared with you. But keep it all secret. Do not communicate anything of this matter except with our Oliger [Rosenkrantz], or also with [my] sister. And meanwhile see to it, quietly, that inquiry is made how this letter has been received by the faction to which Olburgensis adheres. I ask that you be willing to further this German amanuensis of mine in the business he has to transact there in my name, and to press him to return here as soon as possible — within fourteen days, if it can be managed at all — and that you send back with him that same notorious writing. For I cannot do without it, on account of the praises of me it proclaims, since I have but this one copy, and its author sees to it that it is circulated not openly but in secret.</p><p>I have added a German copy of the reasons for my departure from Denmark, which you may, on some occasion, give to Lord Ramelius to read; so that, if he is unaware what grounds I had for leaving, and has forgotten what I said to him at Rostock, he may learn it from this and call it back to memory; otherwise perhaps I shall set down these very matters more fully and at greater length another time. Meanwhile, keep this paper, once you have it back from Ramelius, secretly by you, as a record of a thing well managed — since you are a historian. For scarcely anywhere else will you find truer documents; though you may not dare relate the history of Hven, especially of its later years, because truth breeds hatred. Yet others, perhaps, will do it. For this transformation is not unknown, nor is anger elsewhere as silent as it is among us. Many indeed write and speak of the matter, whom I would rather see silent.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>); the letter first appeared in <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>. MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, a copy (<i>Afskrift</i>) in an exemplar of P. J. Resen's <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i> (1668). The witness is defective at two points (lacunae).</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"die 20. Augusti st. vet. Anno 98\"</i> — explicitly <b>Old Style</b> (<i>stylo veteri</i>), from <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau estate near Hamburg), Tycho's residence in 1598. Same Wandsbek season as brahe-t_15980810001 (to Holger Rosenkrantz, 10 Aug 1598).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Niels Krag</b> (<i>Nicolaus Cragius</i>), Doctor of Both Laws, Danish Royal Historian and Copenhagen professor — \"his one intimate and sincerely beloved friend\"; his wife is <b>\"Elsula\"</b> (Else).</li><li><b>The patronage-auction (the letter's spine).</b> A vivid record of the bidding for Tycho among three powers: (1) <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> (whose side both Krag and the Elector of Cologne favour); (2) the <b>Elector of Cologne</b> (<b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>), who received Tengnagel for four weeks, wrote autograph promotory letters to the Emperor and to <b>Barvitius</b> (the supreme imperial secretary, the Elector's own protégé), and pledged to fund Tycho himself if the Emperor proved difficult — his outburst <i>\"Should one let such a man leave the country for so paltry a sum? … money one can always get, but such people not always\"</i> is one of the most-quoted period testimonials to Tycho; (3) the <b>Dutch</b> — <b>Joseph Scaliger</b> (at Leiden) pressing <b>Prince Maurice of Nassau</b> and <b>Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</b> (then Legate in England) to summon Tycho. Tycho's stated order of preference: offer himself first to the Emperor <i>for honour's sake</i>; then Cologne; then the Dutch; <b>England he rules out</b> (suspect manners; the coming upheaval at \"the old Queen['s]\" — <b>Elizabeth I's</b> — death). This is the same campaign as brahe-t_15980706001 (Tengnagel).</li><li><b>The Ursus libel, received across Europe.</b> A catalogue of revulsion at Ursus's (Nicolaus Reimers Bär's) <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (1597): detested by the universities of <b>Wittenberg, Leipzig, Helmstedt, Rostock</b>; the <b>Freiburg-im-Breisgau</b> academy would have punished the \"Poetaster\" who wrote the insulting distich (dead four years, in poverty — <i>if</i> he, not \"the ursine beast,\" was its author); a rebuff at <b>Brunswick</b>; <b>Duke Adolf of Holstein</b> tearing up the leaves and having his Chancellor <b>Dr. Jonas Gutslovius</b> burn the book; <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> judging it a capital crime \"according to Job.\" Tycho's celebrated vignette: he had once <b>ransomed Ursus from the madhouse at Augsburg for 18 crowns</b> after Ursus stole wine, and wishes him back there to \"eat the book for his improvement.\" Only \"certain Gymnasiarchs\" (school-heads) in Copenhagen relish it — having got it from \"that old member of my household … now an ungrateful cuckoo\" (an unnamed former famulus of Tycho's who passed Ursus's tract to the Copenhagen faction). Continuous with the Ursus material in brahe-t_15991209001 (Kepler) and brahe-t_15990918001 (Vedel).</li><li><b>Scaliger &amp; <i>De emendatione temporum</i>.</b> Tengnagel brought a learned letter from <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>; Tycho notes that Scaliger's recently republished <i>Opus de emendatione temporum</i> (2nd ed., 1598) <b>exalts Tycho's equinox observations — which Scaliger calls \"Danish\"</b> — above Hipparchus (whom Scaliger in turn prefers to Ptolemy). Tycho judges parts of Scaliger's letter (on the world- and equator-poles, the Pole-star, precession, year-length, Ptolemy's Sirius-rising error) <i>\"less consistent — for even good Homer sometimes nods,\"</i> and means to admonish him candidly. He contrasts Scaliger's competent judgment with that of the Danish <b>Master of the Court</b> (<i>Curiae Magister</i>) — \"utterly raw … dreams of nothing but money … prefers peasants' labour to Astronomy\" — almost certainly <b>Christoffer Valkendorf</b>, the Lord Steward/Treasurer and Tycho's chief Danish antagonist (left unnamed in the letter).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Mule</b> (<i>Mulaeus / Claudius Mulaeus</i> = Claus Mule, Tycho's agent); <b>Eske Bille</b> (<i>Eschillus</i>); <b>\"Accius\" (Axel)</b> — a wealthy kinsman/debtor delaying repayment, now allied by marriage to the Chancellor (identity not certain in the text — most likely <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> or <b>Axel Brahe</b>; the Danish aside <i>\"How can Axel be in straits?\"</i> gives the vernacular name); the Danish <b>Chancellor</b> (unnamed); <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>; <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> (<i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</i>, Tycho's envoy); <b>the Elector of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria); <b>Barvitius</b> (<i>Barbitius</i>, imperial supreme secretary); <b>Prince Maurice of Nassau</b> (<i>Mauritius</i>) and <b>Oldenbarnevelt</b> (<i>Barneveldius</i>); <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (<i>Ranzovius</i>); <b>Duke Adolf of Holstein</b> (so named — possibly a slip for the reigning <b>Johann Adolf</b> of Holstein-Gottorp) and his chancellor <b>Jonas Gutslovius</b>; <b>Wilhelm Slawata, Baron of Chlum and Košumberk</b> (the Bohemian noble — later a victim of the 1618 Prague Defenestration — who visited Tycho at Wandsbek); <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (<i>Ramelius</i>, Danish Senator); <b>King Christian IV</b> and his father <b>Frederik II</b> (\"of most praiseworthy memory\"); \"the two now nearest the King\" whom Tycho blames for his exile (unnamed — the Chancellor and the Steward); <b>Peter Brahe</b> (<i>Petrus Brahe</i>, kinsman who lost his firstborn to a physician); <b>Crassaeus</b> (a physician at Rostock, the \"corbizator\"/faith-breaker — unidentified); the <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b> (Joachim Friedrich, the King's father-in-law) and the <b>Electress</b>; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (<i>Oligerus</i>); Tycho's <b>sister</b> (Sophie Brahe).</li><li><b>Verse, proverbs &amp; tags.</b> The cited distich <i>\"Curat se quilibet unum…\"</i> (\"Each cares for himself alone…\") Tycho attributes to a German verse \"lately written to your Denmark\" (a pasquinade; not securely sourced). <i>\"bonus interdum dormitat Homerus\"</i> — <b>Horace</b>, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 359. <i>\"ad calendas Graecas\"</i> — \"at the Greek Kalends,\" i.e. never (Augustus, per Suetonius). <i>\"Quem laeseris, oderis\"</i> — \"whom you have wronged, you hate\" (proverbial; cf. Seneca, Tacitus). <i>\"Bacchum et Cererem consumere natus\"</i> — echo of <b>Horace</b>, <i>Epistles</i> 1.2.27 (<i>fruges consumere nati</i>). The Moon-and-barking-dogs image (<i>Cynthia … canum oblatrantium insipientiam</i>) is a classical commonplace of serene contempt. <b>Danish</b> asides (kept and translated): <i>\"Huor kand da Axel staa til at trenge?\"</i>; <i>\"Det var en gott, om der var ingen værre end hand\"</i>; <i>\"Och det er en stor naade i denne tid\"</i>; the Augsburg-madhouse passage; <i>\"got folk tilhobe\"</i>; <i>\"Man skulle med rette ikke rose af Marken, førend tørvet blev holdet\"</i> (\"one should not boast of the field before the harvest is in\"); <i>\"det var nok han var eders landmand\"</i>; <i>\"som vel forskyldt er…\"</i>. <b>German</b> quotations (kept and translated): the Elector of Cologne's two speeches.</li><li><b>Lacunae (the crux).</b> Two passages are defective in the witness: (1) in the deliberation on his departure — <i>\"antequam … huc reversus, ut id quod iam … nullos videlicet isthic vel ex … \"</i> (the sense: he would decide nothing before [his agent] returned, having learned that none in Denmark would take up his cause); (2) in the books-for-the-King passage — <i>\"eos adhuc Regi per OLIGERUM … uti et illi scripsi. Si tamen id facere non audeat … ita tamen ut libri OLIGERO, si Rex eos (uti …) alij cedant\"</i> (the sense: the books are to reach the King via Holger, but in any case fall to Holger). Reproduced as dots in the source and marked <b>[lacuna]</b> in the translation; the surrounding sense is recoverable, the exact wording is not.</li><li><b>Postscript to Niels Krag, Wandsbek, 20 August 1598 (O.S.).</b> Niels Krag (d. 1602) was the Danish crown's historiographer; the postscript is addressed to him explicitly in that capacity — Tycho invokes it (\"cum sis historicus\") when entrusting him with a private memorandum of the \"reasons for my departure from Denmark,\" evidently hoping that Krag, or \"others\" after him, would one day write the true \"historia Huennensem\" (history of Hven) that current politics made too dangerous to publish (\"veritas odium parit,\" truth breeds hatred). This is a first-person statement of Tycho's own historiographical anxiety about how his Hven years would be remembered — not stated so directly elsewhere in the already-published body of this letter.</li><li><b>Enclosures.</b> Besides two further copies of letters from Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek, the Prague imperial physician and Tycho's chief advocate at Rudolf II's court, urging him eastward — \"how earnestly they expect me\"; the register of Tycho's correspondence confirms Hagecius as a close, longstanding correspondent, with a letter from him to Tycho as early as 1576), Tycho encloses (1) a copy of a defamatory printed piece (\"famosum istud scriptum\") about him then circulating among professors in Rosenkrantz's circle, said to have been procured by one \"Olburgensis\" and forwarded via intermediaries Johannes Styggius (at Hamburg) and Mulaeus; and (2) a German-language account of his own reasons for leaving Denmark, which he authorizes Krag to let \"Lord Ramelius\" read, to refresh what Tycho had already told him \"at Rostock.\"</li><li><b>The \"Bienenkorb\" allusion.</b> Tycho compares Styggius's letter of feigned approval to \"the author of the book they call 'Beinkorf'\" — i.e. the <i>Bienenkorb der Heiligen Römischen Kirche</i> (\"Beehive\"), the well-known mock-encomium that \"praises\" the Papacy by pretending to hold the opposite of the true reformed view. Corpus (Tycho's printed Opera Omnia, vol. 8) confirms this exact passage in the letter's textus receptus, so the allusion is textually secure.</li><li><b>Layered secrecy.</b> Every enclosure carries explicit confidentiality instructions: the defamatory tract and the Hagecius letters are for Rosenkrantz (\"Oligerus\") and Tycho's sister [Sophie Brahe] only; Krag is told to make quiet inquiry into how Styggius's letter was received \"by the faction to which Olburgensis adheres,\" and to keep the returned Ramelius document \"secretly with you... as a memorial of a thing well managed.\"</li><li><b>Textual note.</b> The printed Opera Omnia (Dreyer's edition, vol. 8) reads \"Pontificios contrarium sentiendo commendat,\" where the recovered segment supplied for this translation reads \"Pontifices\" — a minor one-word variant, flagged here for the collator and left untouched per house rule.</li><li><b>Open identifications.</b> Johannes Styggius, \"Olburgensis,\" Mulaeus, and Ramelius/Rameel could not be securely matched to named figures via corpus search (including Christianson's <i>On Tycho's Island</i> prosopography); \"Olburgensis\" (\"the man of Aalborg\") most likely designates a Danish churchman or academic hostile to Tycho, tied to the pamphlet war that followed his 1597 departure, but this remains an educated guess, not a finding.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Five readings have been corrected against Dreyer, TBOO VIII pp.94, 98-99: the Chancellor's name restored as 'Jonathan' (not 'Jonas') Gutslovius, matching the Latin's already-correct 'IONATHAM'; 'cameraque' corrected to 'caeteraque' ('and the rest', not 'and my chamber'), with the English fabricated 'chamber' removed accordingly; 'mactandum' corrected to 'mulctandum' (standard 'poena mulctare' idiom); the signature's letter-transposition 'BRHAE' corrected to 'BRAHE'; and 'in lis' corrected to 'in ijs'. The latter three carry no translation impact.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — contains flagged cruxes. The Latin, Danish, and German are otherwise rendered securely, and the letter's substance — the patronage-auction (Emperor / Cologne / the Dutch, England ruled out), the Europe-wide repudiation of Ursus's libel, the Scaliger/<i>De emendatione temporum</i> relationship, the bitterness toward the Danish court, and the Peter Brahe reconciliation appeal — is clear, important, and cross-corroborated by the surrounding Wandsbek correspondence. The grade is set to <b>[C]</b> by the <b>two genuine lacunae</b> in the witness (named above), which leave two short passages incompletely translatable; secondary uncertainties (the identity of <b>\"Accius\"/Axel</b>, of <b>\"Crassaeus,\"</b> of the unnamed Danish Court-Master = probably Valkendorf, and the form <i>\"Duke Adolf\"</i> possibly for Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp) are flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII pp. 111–112: both witness lacunae are in Dreyer's own text; <i>Eschillo et Accio</i> and <i>Crassaeo</i> are printed without identification (no scholarly resolution of which Axel, or who Crassaeus). [C] stands.</p><p>The Latin of this postscript is intact and unambiguous throughout, with no lacunae; the translation is offered with high confidence at the sentence level and is corroborated against the printed Opera Omnia text (Dreyer's edition, vols. VIII and XIV), which preserves the same passage nearly verbatim. What is not settled is a layer of proper-name prosopography — Styggius, Olburgensis, Mulaeus, and Ramelius are addressed as familiar to both correspondents but could not be identified from the sources searched — and one single-word textual variant (\"Pontifices\"/\"Pontificios\") against the printed edition, noted but not adjudicated. Grade B: sound and corroborated, but uncollated against a manuscript and with open identifications.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.91-101 (full 11-page slice, read in three chunks of pages 1-4, 5-8, 9-11). Checked opening/closing formulas, every proper name (Mulaeus, Eskil Bille, Accius/Axel, Ursus, Jonathan Gutslovius, Rantzau, Scaliger, Tengnagel, Barvitius, Maurice, Barneveldt, Slawata, Ramel, Petrus Brahe, Crassaeus, Thaddaeus/Hagecius, Styggius, Olburgensis, Oliger Rosenkrantz), every number/sum/date (8 days, 2000 joachims, 18 crowns, 4 years, 4th week, 4000 thalers, 3-day-old Thaddaeus letter, 14-day deadline, 20 Aug 1598 st.v.), and the Danish/German embedded quotations. Text is otherwise a faithful, complete, uncondensed rendering with no fabricated clauses, no dropped sentences, and no invented day-numbers. Found one proper-name mistranslation (Jonathan misrendered as Jonas) and four small source_text/Dreyer text divergences, one of which (cameraque vs cæteraque) produced a fabricated physical detail (\"chamber\") in the English.</p><p><b>5 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). IONATHAM is the accusative of Ionathas (Jonathan), not of Ionas (Jonah/Jonas); the chancellor's name is mistranslated. At: “his Chancellor, Doctor Jonas Gutslovius”.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer prints \"cæteraque\" (\"and the rest\"), not \"cameraque\" (\"and my chamber\"); our source_text's corruption produced a fabricated detail (\"chamber\") that the translation then rendered literally. Dreyer prints: <i>quamprimum currus meos, quos adorno, cæteraque parata habuero (TBOO VIII p.98)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's standard legal idiom is \"poena mulctare\" (to punish with the penalty of); source_text has \"mactandum.\" Sense (death penalty) survives in translation either way. Dreyer prints: <i>et capitis pœna mulctandum ſecundum IOB tam infandum facinus cenſens (TBOO VIII p.94)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Letter-transposition typo in source_text's signature line (BRHAE for BRAHE); translation correctly reads \"Brahe\" regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria. (TBOO VIII p.99)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer reads \"ijs\" (\"those things\"); source_text has \"lis\" (\"lawsuit\"), which does not fit the sentence. Translation follows the correct sense (\"the business he has to transact\") regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>hunc amanuenſem meum Germanicum promovere in ijs, quæ iſthic meo nomine expedienda habet (TBOO VIII p.99)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All five defects were verified directly against the Dreyer page images: pp.94 (slice pages 1-4 of 11) for the Gutslovium name and 'mulctandum', p.98 (slice pages 5-8) for 'caeteraque', and p.99 (slice pages 9-11) for 'BRAHE' and 'in ijs'. Every reading is clearly legible in the type facsimile and unambiguous. No crux remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Niels Krag", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15981205002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15991204001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Mule's return without the money owed; Eske failed to obtain two thousand joachims at Flensburg, and Accius defers payment citing his dead brother's debts.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980820001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980823001", "d": "1598-08-23", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joseph Justus Scaliger — Wandsbek, 23 August 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek code-switches · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, Cod. 693 in-folio (copy); B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy); C = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note:</b> the working transcription is heavily peppered with OCR-intruded <b>Dreyer apparatus sigla</b> — a stray superscript <b>\"A\"</b> (the footnote-anchor keying a word to witness A) tacked onto dozens of words. These are <b>non-textual</b> and have been removed from the source block below (declared, not silently normalised). Two readings were cross-checked against the local <b>Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan</b> (vol. VIII, <i>brahe-t_opera_08_Part2.pdf</i>): (1) <i>\"fusile\"</i> in the first sentence of the Ptolemy paragraph is a corruption of <i>\"fuisse\"</i> (the long-s ligature <i>fuiſſe</i> mis-resolved; the parallel clause later reads <i>fuisse</i> plainly, and the sense requires it) — translated as <i>fuisse</i>; (2) <i>\"centenis 30\"</i> (Ptolemy's precession rate) <b>is</b> the printed Dreyer reading and is reproduced verbatim — it is a genuine textual crux (see Apparatus), since Ptolemy's published value is 1° per 100 years. Greek (<i>κοσμοπολίτην, Parallaxεων, πρόποδος, pragmatεία</i>) and zodiac glyphs (♈ ♉ ♊ ♋ ♌) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"die 23. Augusti st. vet. … 1598\"</i>) agrees with the catalogue label.</p>", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO IOSEPHO SCALIGERO, IUL. CAeS. FILIO, MAGNO ACADEMIAe LUGDUNENSIS DECORI, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.<br>S. P.</p><p>PERMAGNO equidem gaudio me tuis cultissimis et humanissimis literis affecisti, Vir Illustris, Amice honorande, partim quod de statu mearum rerum, postquam Dania excessi, te sincere sollicitum probent, partim quod de recondita circa Anni et siderum rationes contemplatione mecum solerter agant. Pro qua tua in nos benevola affectione, quodque meum TENGNAGLIUM adeo comiter et humaniter exceperis, et habeo et semper habiturus sum gratias. De abitus vero mei occasionibus e natali solo, quae non leves aut exiguae erant, deque locis et conditionibus, quibus postea usus sum, nihil hîc referam, indignum reputans, tuis auribus talia occinere. Nec ego hac sorte quidpiam moveor, cum oporteat Philosophiae studijs addictum κοσμοπολίτην esse, nec ullis mutationibus locorum temporum aut hominum affici, sed eodem in quavis rerum et eventuum vicissitudine sibi constare animo, ac fluctuantis insipientisque Mundi perversa iudicia ex alto negligere. De ijs etiam, quae tibi ob eximiam virtutem et doctrinae praecellentiam ignavorum et malevolorum peperit invidia, nihil addam, cum et hanc superare magnanimi sit ingenij. Tales aemuli et Zoili non alijs, sed sibiipsis detrahunt, suamque fatuitatem cordatioribus ludibrio exponunt, ut graviore opus non habeant vindicta quam propria. His itaque praetermissis et sublimi firmoque animo posthabitis, ad ea quae cognitionem non postremam sapiunt, et a te mota sunt, accedamus.</p><p>Sphalmata, quae Eruditissimo tuo volumini, arduo summoque labore comparato de Emendatione temporum per incuriam aliquam erepserunt, nemo intelligens et sani iudicij tibi imputârit; sed aut Typographo aut occupationum tuarum moli. Talia etiam mihi (si parva licet componere magnis) in meis quoque editis scriptis, ubi ea denuo inspicio, subinde contigisse animadverto, quae tamen nemo nisi malus et verborum potius quam rerum otiosus aestimator cavillatus fuerit. Nostras aequinoctiorum observationes tantopere a te probari plurimum mihi congratulor. Et si liceret mihi de meis inventis id, quod res est, testari, multo sane auro redimerem, Antecessorum nostrorum sive veterum sive recentium tam exquisita diligentia administratas fuisse animadversiones. Certe anni Coelestis subtilissima dimensio, a qua plurima alia dependent, nos non ita fluctuantes et incertos redderet. Solis in aequinoctialia puncta ingressum dimetiri, nedum alia, ubi declinatio eius minus variat, altiori indiget molimine quam putatur, alijsque medijs et Organis, quam vel Antiquos in usu habuisse, aut etiam REGIOMONTANUM et COPERNICUM eorumque coaetaneos adhibuisse legimus, indiget. Oportet enim ea tanta tamque solide et exquisite elaborata esse, ut intra sextam Minuti unius partem Altitudinem Solis meridianam discernere queant. Nec uni sed pluribus potius standum, collatione accurata, prout a nobis non saltem illo quod produxisti quinquennio, sed et plurimis alijs Annis pari praecisione factitatum est. Habenda etiam ratio Parallaxeos Solis, tum quoque refractionis in decliviore praesertim situ. Quae omnia cum vetustus ille et in scrutandis Luminarium motibus laboriosissimus HIPPARCHUS vix praecaverit, non mirum est, ipsius Aequinoctiorum observata tantopere claudicare, ut intra diei quadrantem sibiipsis non consentiant. Quae tamen aliquatenus inter se ob refractionum et Parallαξεων neglectas implicationes conciliare tentavimus. At vix intra duas vel tres horas id praestare licuit, cum instrumentum, quo usus est, singula Minuta non exhibuerit, sed solummodo duodecimam (uti opinor) gradus partem. Quod vel ipse prodit, dum nimis lato modo Aequinoctia aut in Horizonte aut in Meridiano, neglectâ ubique sex horarum differentia, contigisse assumat potius quam persuadeat, suis nimirum intra has metas diffisus Organis. PTOLEMAeUM quidem adhuc minori certitudine haec et alia scrutatum, multa ab ipso ea, qua opus fuit, praecisione non enucleata patefaciunt. Attamen illum totum diem in ingressibus Aequinoctiorum hallucinatum fuisse non dixerim. Oportuisset enim eum sic per quincuncem fere gradus Solis declinationem admittere erroneam, quod ea instrumentorum magnitudo et elaboratio, qua Tropicorum intercapedinem sive Eclipticae obliquationem intra tertiam minuti partem rimatus est (nisi et hanc ab HIPPARCHO mutuabatur) non patitur. Sic et Lunae latitudo maxima ab illo per Parallaticum deprehensa adeo magnam Instrumenti fallaciam non arguit. Si omnia loca, quae in eo suspecta habes, denotaveris et mecum unâ cum observationibus HIPPARCHI ad annos et dies Iulianos retro subactos, addita indicatione bissextilium communicaveris, inprimis gratum, nec inutile (uti spero) praestiteris officium.</p><p>Scripsit ad me studiosus ille Danus, olim mihi domesticus, qui iam apud vos est, CHRISTIANUS IOHANNIS, penes te esse librum de HIPPARCHI observatis, in Italia ante aliquot annos Graece editum, cuius exemplar impressum, aut meis sumtibus descriptum unâ cum tuis annotationibus marginalibus, ubi potissimum quid erroris aut dubij subesse putaveris, si impertieris, non modico me affeceris beneficio. ALBATEGNIJ quoque, quem et MAHOMETEM Aratensem (uti scis) vocant, opusculum castigatum (plurimis enim id quod extat scatet mendis) unâ cum ijs quas author ipse subiunxerat Tabulis, obtinere averem, tum quoque si quid praeterea apud te, antiquitatum solertissimum excultorem, similium librorum repertum fuerit, quod Astronomiam in ipso Coelo, non solis chartis quaesitam illustret. Optime sane feceris, si me de ijs reddideris certiorem, et conatus nostros ita non parum iuveris.</p><p>Quae de Aequinoctiali eiusque polis mobilibus, quodque differant a polo Mundi disseris, in ijs tibi adstipulari non possum, aliter me instruente ipsa experientia. Deprehendi enim ex mutata latitudine fixarum iuxta proportionem mutatae obliquitatis Signiferi, a temporibus TIMOCHARIS, HIPPARCHI et PTOLEMAeI, hucusque (si modo eorum observata in angulo obliquationis maximae et caeteris omni vitio careant, qua de re non citra rationes dubito) Eclipticam potius quam Aequatorem cum suis polis instabilem esse, Sole non semper eandem multo seculorum interiectu describente, et una ea loca, quibus aequinoctialem transit, successive anticipante. Unde fit ut stellae fixae tantundem progredi videantur, quantum Sol haec puncta anteriora reddit. Cumque id illi motui, qui convenit defectui Anni Coelestis ab Anno Iuliano, non plane coaequetur, fieri nequit, ut stellae fixae ijsdem Anni Iuliani diebus per aliquot seculorum intervalla cum Sole oriantur vel occidant, alijs etiam concurrentibus causis, quae discrepantiam pariant. Et licet id paucis quibusdam stellis utcunque per longum Annorum intervallum competere possit, tamen non semper aut omnibus. Nec etiam ulla est (uti tam in Opere tuo quam hîc infers) inter polum Aequinoctialis et Mundi discrepantia; sunt enim unum et idem. Neque extrema in cauda Ursae Minoris, quam Polarem vocant, quod prope polum sit, Mundi polus est, aut proxima huic, nisi aequinoctialis polum intellexeris, qui a Mundano (uti dixi) non differt. Distitit quidem haec stella temporibus HIPPARCHI a polo Aequatoris 12⅖ partibus. At hoc anno iuxta nostra Inventa appropinquavit illi intra G. 2 M. 51½, quam ante annos 25 praecise tribus gradibus ab eodem removeri, Augustae Vindelicorum per quadrantem 14 cubitorum in suo Radio, illic a nobis in horto prope praedium Consulis HEINZELIJ extructum deprehensum est. Non tamen ob id haec stella ullius orbis polus est, aut prope, sed hic accessus ad polum Aequatoris sive Mundi fit per mutatam eius longitudinem circa polos Eclipticae, quâ illius declinatio augetur, cum sit nunc circa finem signi ♊; tempore autem HIPPARCHI circa finem ♉ versabatur, confectis interea secundum longitudinem paulo magis 24 gradibus, latitudine autem non plus alterata, quam fert obliquitatis Eclipticae (modo sic se haberet) per tertiam gradus partem decrementum. Nam ea quam PTOLEMAeI Abacus in 66 exacte ponit gradibus, lato modo sumta est. Et si haec stella nostro Aevo ad Aequatorem referatur, non cadet in Colurum aequinoctialem, ut tu forte existimas, sed distabit ab hoc in partibus Aequatoris G. 5¾, testante idipsum ratâ experientiâ. Coniungi autem ipsa cum polo Aequatoris exacte nunquam poterit. Nam post annos proxime 500, ubi initium ♋ simulque Colurum Solstitialem assecuta fuerit, distabit a polo versus Eclipticam sive Aequatorem 27½ minutis. Etsi enim Eclipticae inclinatio paulo aliter tunc forte aucta fuerit (quod tamen vix futurum existimarim) tamen haec latitudinem saltem stellae alterabit, nec eam polo ob id propius admovebit, stellis nimirum fixos Eclipticae polos respicientibus, Sole autem nonnihil mobiles per secula describente, quatenus antiquorum traditis acquiescendum erit. Quare haec quae de diversitate polorum Mundi et Aequatoris deque stella polari quacunque tandem occasione concepisti, penitius tibi expendenda relinquo.</p><p>De libello PTOLEMAeI, in quo stellarum ortus et occasus ad singulos dies Anni Actiaci et Iuliani per aliquot Climata designantur, libenter audio, quae adducis. Da operam, ut eum mature obtineamus. Circa Asterismorum ordinationes etiamsi aliqua cum Recentioribus incidat nonnunquam differentia, nihil turbabit negotium, modo constet, cuius stellae fiat mentio. Anni Syderei ab Aegyptijs et Babilonijs introducta, posteaque a THEBITIO exculta, a COPERNICO vero nostro aevo renovata ratiocinatio, non ita vana atque inutilis est, prout existimas, si modo exacte constaret. Nos eam in Progymnasmatis Astronomicis adhuc accuratius castigavimus, quatenus Ptolemaicae cum nostris collatae ferunt Observationes: Sed ubi Hipparcheas in aliqua copia per te nactus fuero, hanc ipsam subtilius limitabo. Nam et ego potius cum HIPPARCHO quam PTOLEMAeO standum tecum censeo. Existimo autem non minus quam COPERNICUS stellas fixas quoad se immotas perpetuo manere. Progressionem vero illam, quam efficere videntur, per Axem Terrae in sua revolutione anticipatum libratumque, uti is voluit, non admitto, cum nihil eiuscemodi revera Terrae competat, at si veterum tradita omnimode rite constare credendum, verisimilius erit, ipsum Solem aliam atque aliam diversis seculis describere Eclipticam. Et quantulacunque sic subesse possit inaequalitas, eam per Solis universalem Hypothesin, prout e praeteritis Artificum observationibus enucleare licuerit, favente Numine salvabimus. Atque huc conducet Anni Syderei ratio, ut et in Planetarum simplici motu a certo et immobili puncto deducendo, et accuratius quam hactenus stabiliendo. Neque quidpiam infringit huius anni commoditatem, quod Caniculam seu Syrij lucidam, ut veteres hanc vocarunt, per 15 secula eodem proxime die Iuliano exortam esse asseveres. Id quod in hac stella quodammodo fieri potuisse tibi concesserim, ut ob id hac experientia edocti, antiquitus Annum Iulianum Cinicum appellarint. Nam et ego Geometrice ad motiones huius stellae interea factas adoptato calculo, eam in Horizonte Graecorum, ubi polus 40 plus minus gradibus attollitur, ortum suum cum Sole a PTOLEMAeI temporibus per 1500 Annos retro numeratos non nisi unico die Anni Iuliani anticipasse deprehendo. Quae differentia illis pene insensibilis, siquidem non satis scrupulose haec comperta habebant. Fit tamen ea convenientia per accidens in hac stella ob declinationem intervallo tot seculorum ita variatam (nimirum tribus gradibus, dempta una tertia) ut haec eam alterationem, quae ex Anni Iuliani ac Syderei disconvenientia incidere potuit, emendarit et quasi eliserit. Quod nihilominus in alijs stellis pariter non contingit ob aliam atque aliam declinationum alteratarum vicissitudinem, ut de curriculi Solis inaequali ad eosdem dies revolutione per longum temporis tractum ob Apogaei eiusdem mutationem nihil nunc dicam. Imo ne quidem in hac ipsa stella, si a PTOLEMAeO hucusque pari modo fiat periculum, adeo constanter diem Iulianum observari comprobabitur; differentia nunc usque in tertium diem sese extendente, eo quod tardior fuerit posterioribus seculis declinationis variatio, quam illis antecedentibus, nec reliquo motui adeo analoga. Et si quis post totidem secula in futurum idem tentet, fiet adhuc maior differentia, utpote quae in quintum diem excrescet. Quin et de Arcturo, quod ais, eum tempore HESIODI eadem die Anni Iuliani nempe VIII Martij ortum fuisse, qua idipsum prodiderunt in eo tractu METON et EUTEMON, numerationi diligentiori non congruit, si modo recte temporum intervalla nobis constiterint. PTOLEMAeI quidem aevo nona die Martij unico saltem posterius oriri potuit. HESIODI autem, quod noningentis Annis anterius statuimus, secunda die Martij per 7 nimirum dies prius exoriebatur. At tempore METONIS, qui vix 150 Annis post HESIODUM floruit, idem ortus in tertium diem Martij cadebat. Hisce autem proximis annis decimum nonum ferme diem attingit, ut ob id, quomodo Arcturi exortus ad certum diem Anni Iuliani multis seculis alligari queat, prout tua insinuat annotatio, non videam. Sic aliae et diversae stellae diversimodam quoque in hac pragmatεία ingerunt variationem, ut hinc nihil certi de Annorum rationibus mutuari liceat. Anni coelestis periodus in ijs quidem, quas accepisti, impressis chartis a nobis GELALAeo paulo minor adhibita est. Hanc tamen non tanquam universalem illic adduximus, sed solummodo ab Observationibus REGIOMONTANI per unicum Annorum centenarium huc translatam; quod certo consilio fecimus, ut Apogaei et Eccentricitatis, si qua subreperet incomperta mutatio, minus officeret, praesertim cum ad tria vel quatuor duntaxat secula motum Solis inibi emendatiorem tradere intenderimus. At si altius (quod praestat) id petatur, interlapsis plurimis seculis proveniet hic Annus paulo maior GELALAeo, nec tamen in tantum, ut Alphonsinum modum, cui tu nunc subscribis, assequatur. Qua de re praecisius aliquid, quam antehac statuere licebit, ubi omnibus HIPPARCHI observationibus me instructiorem reddideris, tum quoque si quas alias adhuc vetustiores aut intermedias in promtu habueris. Cuperem etiam antiquissimi illius Iudaici Aequinoctij, cuius meministi, exactam a te cognitionem obtinere, quanto nimirum tempore, quoad annos Iulianos a nostris observatis retro distiterit; ita tamen, ut iste annus, quo hoc Aequinoctium celebratum creditur, pariter a bissextili removeatur cum aliquo e nostris, in quibus ob id quinquennium apponere libuit, quo semper aliquis in promptu esset, qui sic cum vetustis inventis conferri commodius posset. Oportet enim ubique semper hanc collationem fieri in ijs Annis, qui vel pariter bissextiles sunt, vel a bissextili aequaliter distant, quod et in prioribus literis me monuisse recordor. Petis, ut stellarum Eclipticae vicinarum, praesertim Reguli et πρόποδος ♊ communicem loca atque exortus cum diebus Iulianis. Scias itaque cor ♌ Anno CHRISTI 1600 Dionysiaco completo (quo vulgariter, licet inepte, uti recte nos edocuisti, utimur) incidere, quoad longitudinem in G. 24 M. 17 ♌ cum latitudine G. 0 M. 27 Bor. tuncque obtinere Ascensionem Rectam p. 146 M. 46 ac declinationem p. 13 M. 54, ideoque oriri in Horizonte illo Graecorum cum 24 g. ♌ si completo, quod cosmice contingit 7. Augusti, Acronyce vero 2. Februarij. Et in nostra quoque latitudine intra eosdem dies tam cosmice quam Acronyce emergit. Propus ♊ longitudinem ad dictum tempus habet p. 27 M. 53, latitudinem vero p. 0 M. 58 Merid., Ascensionem Rectam p. 87 M. 42, declinationem p. 22 M. 33 Septentrionalem. Unde in eodem Boeotico tractu nunc oritur cum 29no ferme completo ♊, id est 10. Iunij cosmice, inter 9um et 10mum Decembris Acronyce.</p><p>Ut vero plurium stellarum, imo quotcunque et qualiumcunque volueris, loca ipsi coelo adamussim congrua in promptu habeas, atque hinc quicquid placuerit, circa earum habitudines sive veterum sive nostro aevo rimari queas, en hîc peculiari libro mille stellarum (plures enim in nostro climate vix observationi patent) exactissimam in ipso minuto, adeoque interdum semisse, quoad longum et latum restitutionem intra 20 elapsos annos magno labore et diligentia a nobis exantlatam, qualem (absit gloriatio) nulla viderunt priora secula, tecum secreto communico. Redegimus autem omnium longitudines consulto ad Annum vulgarem 1600 completum, quibus ALBATEGNIJ aetas G. 10 exacte, PTOLEMAeI 21⅖, HIPPARCHI 24¼, TIMOCHARIS 25¾ subtrahunt; latitudine pro ratione variatae inclinationis Eclipticae ad Aequatorem in singulis secundum earum peculiarem dispositionem nonnihil hinc inde crescente vel decrescente. Quae tamen differentia, ultra tertiam Gradus partem, ubi vel maxima est, hactenus alterata esse non invenitur, imo et in hac tantillula mutatione non parum subest dubij. Atque haec prout ipsi Artifices loca praecipuarum aliquot quarundam fixarum suo aevo prodiderunt, in quibus tamen, PTOLEMAeO praesertim et TIMOCHARI aliquem subrepsisse errorem, quo minus ea satis subtiliter scrutati sint, ob nimiam inaequalitatem suspicor, ALBATEGNIJ et HIPPARCHI denotationibus magis ratis exsistentibus. Ex quibus etiam praecipue collegi, inerrantes stellas singulis annis 51\" conficere; quantum videlicet motui Solis simplici competit, in ea temporis differentiolâ, quae est inter annum Aequinoctialem et Sydereum, quam nos statuimus M. 20⅔ quamproxime. Quare in Annis Iulianis 70 Mensibus 7 unum Gradum absolvunt stellae, non autem centenis 30 (uti voluit PTOLEMAeUS) longe minus pluribus, prout tu existimare videris. Oporteret enim sic supra modum erroneas fuisse antecessorum animadversiones, quod nimis licentiose asseverare nemini fas est. Additus est iuxta calcem eiusdem libri catalogus 100 insigniorum stellarum Declinationes et Ascensiones Rectas ad annum 1600 et 1700 exhibens unâ cum suis differentijs, per quas non saltem ad intermedium seculum, sed et proxime antecedens ac subsequens hae utrinque satis commode adaptari queant. Si vero ortus et occasus earum ad certum Horizontem ratione Solis scire aves, id REGIOMONTANI Tabulae ex data declinatione et Ascensione Recta non difficulter suppeditabunt, aut, si mavis, per Triangula. Ut autem locus Solis e nostra emendatione etiam tibi verius quam alias constet, addidi Ephemeridem motum eius ad Meridiem singulorum dierum huius labentis Anni in longitudine locorum 37 partium expedientem. Inde ad plures antecedentes et sequentes eundem intra unum vel alterum Minutum derivare, exigui est laboris. At si praecisus hunc rimari lubuerit, utere meis Tabulis in motu Solis, quas puto te in capite illo primo de curriculo illius a nobis restituto (undecumque tandem tecum communicato) accepisse. Sin minus, habes hîc in omnem eventum quaternionem eius calculo ad proxima aliquot secula sufficientem. Mitto insuper alium librum, Mechanica Astronomiae nostra complectentem, quem tamen ordine primum statuo. Eo inspecto non obscure aestimabis, quanto molimine, quantaque diligentia talia tractârim. Et si lubuerit unum vel alterum Instrumentum ad instar eorum confici curare, habes inibi Idaeam et usum. Nec enim inutile foret, si Sextantem quempiam pro Syderum distantijs et Quadrantem iustae magnitudinis pro eorundem altitudinibus dimetiendis tibi paraveris. Qua in re praenominatus ille studiosus Danus tam quoad divisiones perficiendas quam caetera ordinanda tibi usui esse poterit; unâque in Praxin observationum opus diducere, prout apud me didicit; optarem enim, multis in locis coelestibus diligenter attendi, ne idem fiat nostro, quod de ROMULI aevo conqueritur NASO:<br>Et inobservata per annum<br>Sydera currebant.<br>Uti etiam ferme (quod dolendum) nunc accidit.</p><p>Facis quoque sub finem mentionem anni Liliani sive Pontificij, tam inepte constituti quam approbati. Quem licet omnibus suis numeris non exactissime constantem (quis enim hoc inconsulto coelo praestiterit?) convenientiorem tamen priore illo omnino censeo, qui tot dierum ob anni Aequinoctialis et Iuliani aliquale discrimen anticipationem commisit, inde ab initio annorum CHRISTI, vel (ut illi maluerunt) a concilio Niceno. Neque tam facile posthac iuxta modum illum Gregorianum adeo enormis divagatio subrepere potest; sed (ut dicam quod res est) haec summa praecisione (si qua foret necessaria) extricare nullis eiuscemodi compendijs et diverticulis licet. Verum habenda est ipsissima motus utriusque luminaris ad quaevis secula subtilis notitia; qualem ne hoc quidem tempore ex usitatis Tabulis citra omne erroris vestigium compertam habemus. Ut primi Mensis huius Anni in Sole et Luna ad longitudinem loci 35 partium e nostris animadversionibus et Tabulis subductus calculus, atque cum Alphonsinis et Coperniceis numeris collatus (quem etiam in quadam charta, ut hinc caetera ratiocinari queas, adiunxi) te edocebit. Haec igitur, quae nunc mittere commodum fuit, boni consule. Subiungam suo tempore, favente coelestium Opifice, de coelestibus plura et maiora. Et quamvis hisce serioribus nimium tibi faciam nunc negotij, lusus nihilominus insuper quosdam poëticos e multis quos habeo, in mei gratiam, postquam Dania excessi, hinc inde a diversis concinnatos, mihique missos unâ impertior, non quod tua Heroica vena quid dignum redoleant, sed tantum ut videas, alijs quoque meam inde emigrationem cordi esse. Selegi autem potissimum haec, in quibus discessus nostri causae utcunque insinuantur. Si praestantissimus ille DOUSA iunior, quem fata nobis (proh dolor) adeo praepropere surripuerunt, adhuc in vivis esset, aliquid cultioris ingenij proculdubio de his meditatus fuisset, uti et antea Cometographica nostra melliflua otia ornavit.</p><p>Retulit mihi huc reversus TENGNAGLIUS noster te admodum in votis habere, ut ad vos in Bataviam transmigrem, quod tuo erga nos amori acceptum fero, quemadmodum etiam illustriss. principis MAURITIJ et Ordinum desideria et erga me favorem non obscure hac in parte intellexi, ut de Anglicanis nihil addam. Et sane non abhorreret animus ab ea loci mutatione, cum semper mihi plus arriserint Batavorum solertia ingenia quam Germanorum. Verum te celare non possum, interea dum hîc in finibus Germaniae et Daniae ultra Annum moror, quo Nostratibus rem rectius dispiciendi satis relinquerem temporis, Patriae, cui multum debemus, adhuc studens, Sacrae Caesareae Maiestati relatione quorundam e praecipuis in Aula, me hîc haerere innotuisse. Quare ipsius Maiestas per Amplissimum Dominum RUDOLPHUM CORRADUCIUM Imperij Procancellarium, intermediante etiam Domino Doctore THADDAeO HAGGECIO veteri Amicitia mihi iunctissimo, Pragam me evocari mandavit, spem non dubiam facientibus ijs, Caesaream Maiestatem, si accessero, me meaque studia clementissimo favore prosecuturam et promoturam. Qua de re iam aliquot hac aestate inde similia insinuantes accepi literas. Commendavit insuper Serenissimus Coloniensis Elector, cui Caesar multum tribuit, illi me meaque omnia de meliori (ut aiunt) notâ, exquisito erga me favore refertis ad ipsius Caesaream Maiestatem propria manu hoc nomine perscriptis literis; tum quoque alijs ad Magnificum Dominum BARVITIUM intimum Caesaris Consiliarium et Secretarium supremum. Dedit quoque idem Coloniensis tam ad Dominum HEINRICUM RANZOVIUM, Affinem et hospitem meum charissimum, quam me peculiares literas, quibus profectionem hanc non infrugiferam futuram persuadet. Quin et, si praeter omnem suam expectationem non successerit, ut Caesar mea studia benigne et liberaliter fovere sustentareque velit, se idipsum suis sumtibus haud invite effecturum sponte pollicitus est. Quare haec media ita commode oblata, praesertim apud Imperatorem summum Imperij Caput (cum et Astronomiam universaliter constituere et stabilire satagam) experiunda ante omnia censeo. Constitui itaque primo quoque tempore Pragam proficisci, quo Caesaris clementissimam voluntatem humillime cognoscam. Quod si omnia votis nostris minus istinc responderint, tunc demum de alijs sedibus eligendis certi quid statuam, et aut ad vos vel etiam Anglos transibo, aut potius ad Regem Galliarum, quem scio me et mea studia liberalissime excepturum eorundemque conservationem in eo Regno munifice perpetuaturum, et forte veteri Hispaniarum Regi ALFONSO hac in parte, quantum commode ibi res ferunt, non cessurum; quicquid erit, coelum et Urania, quae omnes terras includunt, ab omnibus terris non excludentur, utut Dania nostra tam sublimibus sacris quorundam inscitia et malevolentia non satis attenderit. Significabo datâ oportunitate per literas, quomodo iter hoc in Bohemiam successerit, ut hinc, quid facto opus sit, praevidere et aestimare queas. Sed desino te pluribus detinere. Tuum est hanc prolixitatem, quam sublimis tui ingenij veneratio, et materiae, de qua agimus, praestantia, tuaque in me benevolentia extorsit, boni consulere.</p><p>Vale Vir Illustris et Magne ac Reipub. Literariae diu ac feliciter vive.</p><p>Dabantur Wandesburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum die 23. Augusti st. vet. Anni Christiani vulgaris 1598.<br>Tuae Illustriss. Amplitud. studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and most excellent man, Lord Joseph Scaliger, son of Julius Caesar [Scaliger], the great ornament of the Academy of Leiden, his especially-to-be-honoured friend.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>You have indeed affected me with very great joy by your most cultivated and most kind letter, illustrious Man, honoured Friend — partly because it proves you sincerely solicitous about the state of my affairs since I left Denmark, partly because it deals skilfully with me about the recondite contemplation of the reckonings of the Year and of the stars. For this benevolent affection of yours toward us, and because you received my <b>Tengnagel</b> so courteously and kindly, I both give and shall always give thanks. But about the occasions of my departure from my native soil — which were neither slight nor small — and about the places and conditions I have since made use of, I shall report nothing here, deeming it unworthy to drone such things in your ears. Nor am I moved at all by this lot, since one devoted to the studies of Philosophy ought to be a <i>cosmopolite</i> [κοσμοπολίτην, a citizen of the world], affected by no changes of places, times, or men, but remaining constant to himself, with the same mind, in every vicissitude of affairs and events, and disregarding from on high the perverse judgments of a fluctuating and foolish World. About those things too which the envy of the slothful and malevolent has bred for you, on account of your outstanding virtue and excellence of learning, I shall add nothing, since to overcome this also belongs to a magnanimous nature. Such rivals and <i>Zoili</i> [carping critics] detract not from others but from themselves, and expose their own foolishness to the mockery of the more sensible, so that they need no graver vengeance than their own. So, these things being passed over and set aside with a lofty and firm mind, let us proceed to those matters which savour of no mean knowledge, and have been raised by you.</p><p>The slips [<i>sphalmata</i>] which, by some carelessness, have crept into your most erudite volume <i>On the Emendation of Times</i> [<i>De emendatione temporum</i>], compiled with arduous and supreme labour, no intelligent person of sound judgment would impute to you, but either to the Typographer or to the mass of your occupations. Such things have happened to me too (if one may compare small things with great), in my own published writings, when I inspect them again — as I notice from time to time — which, however, no one but a malicious and idle appraiser of words rather than of things would cavil at. That our <b>observations of the equinoxes</b> are so greatly approved by you, I most heartily congratulate myself. And if it were permitted me to testify about my findings what the truth is, I should surely redeem with much gold [the wish] that the observations of our predecessors, whether ancient or recent, had been administered with such exquisite diligence [as they were not]. Certainly the most subtle measurement of the celestial year, on which very many other things depend, would not then render us so fluctuating and uncertain. To measure the Sun's ingress into the equinoctial points — let alone the other [points], where its declination varies less — needs a loftier effort than is thought, and needs other means and Instruments than we read that either the Ancients had in use, or even <b>Regiomontanus</b> and <b>Copernicus</b> and their contemporaries employed. For the instruments must be so great, and so solidly and exquisitely elaborated, that they can discern the Sun's meridian <b>altitude</b> within a sixth part of one minute [10″]. And one must rely not on one but rather on several [observations], with accurate comparison, as has been done by us with equal precision, not only in that quinquennium which you have adduced, but in very many other Years as well. Account must also be taken of the Sun's <b>parallax</b>, and likewise of <b>refraction</b>, especially in a lower [declining] position. Since that ancient <b>Hipparchus</b> — most laborious in scrutinizing the motions of the Luminaries — scarcely guarded against all these, it is no wonder that his observations of the equinoxes limp so greatly that they do not agree among themselves within a quarter of a day. Which, however, we have attempted to reconcile to some extent, on account of the neglected complications of refractions and <b>parallaxes</b> [Παραλλάξεων]. But scarcely within two or three hours was it possible to achieve this, since the instrument he used did not exhibit single minutes, but only (as I think) a twelfth part of a degree [5′]. Which he himself even betrays, when he assumes — rather than persuades us — that the Equinoxes occurred, in too broad a manner, either on the Horizon or on the Meridian, the six-hour difference being everywhere neglected, distrusting (within these limits) his own Instruments. That <b>Ptolemy</b> indeed scrutinized these and other things with still less certainty, many things not unfolded by him with the needful precision make plain. Yet I would not say that he was deceived by a whole day in the ingresses of the Equinoxes. For he would thus have had to admit the Sun's declination erroneous by nearly a <i>quincunx</i> of a degree [5⁄12° = 25′] — which the magnitude and elaboration of those instruments (by which he investigated the interval of the Tropics, or the <b>obliquity of the Ecliptic</b>, within a third part of a minute [20″] — unless he borrowed this too from Hipparchus) does not allow. So too the Moon's maximum latitude, ascertained by him by means of the <b>Parallactic</b> [the triquetrum], does not argue so great a fallacy of the Instrument. If you mark all the places which you hold suspect in it [Ptolemy], and share them with me, together with Hipparchus's observations reduced backward to Julian years and days (with an indication of the leap-years added), you will perform a service especially welcome, and (as I hope) not useless.</p><p>That Danish student, once a member of my household, who is now with you, <b>Christian Johnson</b> [Christianus Iohannis], wrote me that you have in your keeping a book on Hipparchus's observations, published in Greek in Italy some years ago — of which, if you would impart a printed copy, or one transcribed at my expense, together with your marginal annotations (where you especially think some error or doubt to lie beneath), you would affect me with no small benefit. I should also be eager to obtain the corrected little work of <b>Albategnius</b> — whom they also call <b>Mahomet of al-Raqqa</b> [Muḥammad al-Battānī], as you know — (for what is extant teems with very many errors), together with the Tables the author himself had subjoined; and likewise, if anything else of similar books be found with you — most skilful cultivator of antiquities that you are — which may illustrate Astronomy sought in the Heaven itself, not in papers alone. You will surely do excellently if you inform me about these, and so help our endeavours not a little.</p><p>The things you discuss about the <b>Equator</b> and its mobile poles, and that they differ from the pole of the World, in these I cannot agree with you, experience itself instructing me otherwise. For I have ascertained, from the changed latitude of the fixed [stars] (according to the proportion of the changed <b>obliquity of the Zodiac</b>), from the times of <b>Timocharis</b>, Hipparchus, and Ptolemy down to now — provided their observations on the angle of maximum obliquity, and the rest, lack all fault (about which I doubt not without reason) — that it is the <b>Ecliptic</b>, rather than the Equator, that is unstable with its poles: the Sun not always describing the same [ecliptic] over the great interposition of centuries, and at the same time successively anticipating those places at which it crosses the equator. Whence it comes about that the fixed stars seem to advance just as much as the Sun renders these points earlier. And since that [precession] is not exactly equal to the motion which corresponds to the deficit of the Celestial Year from the Julian Year, it cannot come about that the fixed stars rise or set with the Sun on the same days of the Julian Year over intervals of several centuries — other causes also concurring, which beget the discrepancy. And although that may somehow be the case for a few certain stars over a long interval of Years, yet not always, nor for all. Nor is there any discrepancy (as you infer, both in your Work and here) between the pole of the Equator and [the pole] of the World; for they are one and the same. Nor is the outermost [star] in the tail of <b>Ursa Minor</b> — which they call the Pole-star, because it is near the pole — the pole of the World, or the nearest [point] to this, unless you understand the pole of the equator, which (as I said) does not differ from the World's. This star, indeed, in Hipparchus's times, was distant from the pole of the Equator by <b>12⅖ degrees</b>. But this year, according to our findings, it has approached it to within <b>2° 51½′</b>; whereas 25 years ago it was found to be removed precisely <b>3 degrees</b> from the same — [as] ascertained by us at <b>Augsburg</b>, by a quadrant of 14 cubits in its Radius, erected there in the garden near the estate of <b>Consul Hainzel</b>. Yet not for that reason is this star the pole of any orb, or near [it]; rather, this approach to the pole of the Equator (or of the World) happens through its changed longitude about the poles of the Ecliptic, by which its declination is increased — since it is now about the end of the sign <b>Gemini [♊]</b>, whereas in Hipparchus's time it was about the end of <b>Taurus [♉]</b>, a little more than 24 degrees having been completed meanwhile according to longitude, while the latitude is altered no more than the decrement of a third part of a degree [20′] which the Ecliptic's obliquity carries (provided it be so). For the [latitude] which Ptolemy's Table puts at exactly 66 degrees is taken in a broad manner. And if this star be referred to the Equator in our age, it will not fall on the equinoctial <b>Colure</b>, as you perhaps think, but will be distant from it, in degrees of the Equator, by <b>5¾°</b>, established experience testifying the same. But it can never be conjoined exactly with the pole of the Equator. For after nearly <b>500 years</b>, when it shall have attained the beginning of <b>Cancer [♋]</b> and at the same time the Solstitial Colure, it will be distant from the pole, toward the Ecliptic (or Equator), by <b>27½ minutes</b>. For even if the inclination of the Ecliptic shall then perhaps have been increased somewhat otherwise (which, however, I should scarcely think will happen), yet this will alter only the star's latitude, and will not for that reason move it nearer to the pole — the stars, that is, regarding the <b>fixed</b> poles of the Ecliptic, while the Sun describes [poles] somewhat mobile through the centuries, insofar as one must rest in the traditions of the ancients. Wherefore these things which you have conceived — on whatever occasion — about the diversity of the poles of the World and of the Equator, and about the pole-star, I leave to you to weigh more deeply.</p><p>About the little book of <b>Ptolemy</b> [the <i>Phaseis</i>], in which the risings and settings of the stars are designated for the single days of the <b>Actian</b> and Julian Year through several <b>Climes</b> [Climata], I gladly hear what you adduce. Take pains that we may obtain it in good time. Concerning the arrangements of the <b>Asterisms</b> [constellations], even if some difference with the More-recent [astronomers] sometimes occurs, it will not disturb the business at all, provided it be clear of which star mention is made. The reckoning of the <b>Sidereal Year</b> — introduced by the Egyptians and Babylonians, afterwards cultivated by <b>Thebit</b> [Thābit ibn Qurra], and renewed in our age by Copernicus — is not so vain and useless as you think, if only it were exactly established. We have corrected it still more accurately in the Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i>, insofar as the Ptolemaic Observations, compared with ours, allow; but when I have obtained the Hipparchan [observations] in some quantity through you, I shall define this very thing more subtly. For I too judge, with you, that one must rather stand with Hipparchus than with Ptolemy. But I think, no less than Copernicus, that the fixed stars remain perpetually immobile in respect of themselves. That progression, however, which they seem to effect, I do not admit to come about through the Earth's Axis being anticipated and librated in its revolution, as he [Copernicus] held, since nothing of this kind in reality belongs to the Earth; but if the traditions of the ancients are to be believed entirely correct, it will be more probable that the Sun itself describes a different and different <b>Ecliptic</b> in different centuries. And however small an inequality may thus lie beneath, we shall <b>save</b> it by the universal Hypothesis of the Sun, as far as it has been possible to unfold from the past observations of the Artificers [astronomers], God favouring. And to this the reckoning of the Sidereal Year will conduce, both in deducing the Planets' simple motion from a certain and immobile point, and in establishing it more accurately than hitherto. Nor does it in any way impair the convenience of this [sidereal] year that you assert the <b>Dog-star</b> [Canicula] — or \"the bright [star] of Sirius,\" as the ancients called it — to have risen on nearly the same Julian day through 15 centuries. Which I should grant you could in some manner have happened in this star, so that, taught by this experience, the ancients of old called the Julian Year \"<b>Canine</b>\" [Canicular]. For I too, by a calculation adopted geometrically to the motions of this star made meanwhile, find that, in the Horizon of the Greeks (where the pole is elevated about 40 degrees), its rising with the Sun, from Ptolemy's times reckoned back through 1500 years, has anticipated [the calendar] by only a single day of the Julian Year. Which difference was almost imperceptible to them, since they did not have these things ascertained scrupulously enough. Yet that agreement happens by accident in this star, because the declination has been so varied over the interval of so many centuries (namely by three degrees, less one third [= 2°40′]) that this [change] has corrected, and as it were cancelled out, that alteration which could have occurred from the disagreement of the Julian and the Sidereal Year. Which nonetheless does not equally happen in other stars, on account of the different and different vicissitude of the altered declinations — to say nothing now of the unequal revolution of the Sun's course to the same days over a long tract of time, on account of the change of its <b>Apogee</b>. Nay, not even in this very star, if the test be made in like manner from Ptolemy down to now, will it be proved that the Julian day is observed so constantly — the difference now extending up to the third day, because the variation of declination has been slower in the later centuries than in those preceding, nor so analogous to the rest of the motion. And if anyone should attempt the same as many centuries into the future, there will be a still greater difference, namely one that will grow to the fifth day. Moreover, as to <b>Arcturus</b> — what you say, that in <b>Hesiod's</b> time it rose on the same day of the Julian Year, namely 8 March, on which <b>Meton</b> and <b>Euctemon</b> reported the same in that region — does not agree with a more diligent numeration, if only the intervals of time have been correctly established for us. In Ptolemy's age, indeed, it could rise on 9 March, only one [day] later. But in Hesiod's [time], which we place 900 years earlier, it rose on 2 March, that is, 7 days earlier. But in Meton's time — who flourished scarcely 150 years after Hesiod — the same rising fell on the third day of March. Yet in these most recent years it reaches nearly the nineteenth day [of March], so that on this account I do not see how the rising of Arcturus can be bound to a fixed day of the Julian Year over many centuries, as your annotation insinuates. So other and diverse stars likewise introduce a variously-modified variation in this <b>practical matter</b> [<i>pragmateia</i>], so that from it nothing certain may be borrowed concerning the reckonings of the Years. The period of the celestial year, in those printed sheets which you have received, was employed by us [as] a little smaller than the <b>Gelalaean</b> [Jalālī] year. This, however, we adduced there not as universal, but only as transferred hither from the <b>Observations of Regiomontanus</b> over a single century of Years; which we did by a certain design, so that, if any unknown change of the Apogee and Eccentricity should creep in, it might hinder less — especially since we intended there to hand down the Sun's motion more correctly for only three or four centuries. But if it be sought higher (which is preferable), many centuries having intervened, this Year will come out a little greater than the Gelalaean, yet not so much as to attain the <b>Alphonsine</b> measure, to which you now subscribe. About which it will be possible to establish something more precise than heretofore, when you have rendered me better instructed with all of Hipparchus's observations, and likewise if you have at hand any others still more ancient, or intermediate. I should also wish to obtain from you exact knowledge of that most ancient <b>Jewish Equinox</b> which you mentioned — namely by how much time, as regards Julian years, it stood distant backward from our observations; yet in such a way that the year in which this Equinox is believed to have been celebrated be equally removed from a leap-year as some one of ours (in which, for this reason, it pleased [us] to set down a quinquennium, so that there might always be some [year] at hand which could thus be more conveniently compared with the ancient findings). For this comparison must everywhere and always be made in those Years which are either equally leap-years, or equally distant from a leap-year — which I recall having advised in my earlier letter too. You ask that I communicate the places and risings, with the Julian days, of the stars near the Ecliptic — especially of <b>Regulus</b> and of <b>Propus</b> [πρόποδος] of Gemini [♊]. Know, therefore, that the <b>Heart of Leo</b> [Cor Leonis = Regulus, ♌], in the Year of Christ 1600 of Dionysius completed (which we use commonly, though ineptly — as you rightly taught us), falls, as to longitude, at <b>24° 17′ of Leo</b>, with a latitude of <b>0° 27′ North</b>; and then has a <b>Right Ascension of 146° 46′</b> and a <b>declination of 13° 54′</b>; and therefore rises, in that Horizon of the Greeks, with 24° of Leo completed — which happens <b>cosmically</b> [with the Sun] on 7 August, but <b>acronychally</b> [at sunset] on 2 February. And in our latitude too, within the same days, it emerges both cosmically and acronychally. <b>Propus</b> of Gemini has, at the said time, a longitude of <b>27° 53′</b> [of Gemini], a latitude of <b>0° 58′ South</b>, a Right Ascension of <b>87° 42′</b>, and a declination of <b>22° 33′ North</b>. Whence in the same <b>Boeotian</b> region it now rises with nearly the 29th [degree] of Gemini completed — that is, on 10 June cosmically, between the 9th and 10th of December acronychally.</p><p>But so that you may have at hand the places of more stars — nay, of however many, and of whatever kind, you wish — exactly congruent to the heaven itself, and may hence investigate whatever you please concerning their relations, whether of the ancients or of our age: behold, here, in a special book of <b>a thousand stars</b> (for more scarcely lie open to observation in our clime), I communicate to you in secret the most exact <b>restitution</b> — to the very minute, and so sometimes to the half [30″] — as regards longitude and latitude, drawn out by us, with great labour and diligence, within the <b>20 elapsed years</b>: such as (let boasting be absent) no prior centuries have seen. We have reduced the longitudes of all, by design, to the common Year 1600 completed; from which the age of <b>Albategnius</b> subtracts exactly <b>10°</b>, that of <b>Ptolemy 21⅖°</b>, of <b>Hipparchus 24¼°</b>, of <b>Timocharis 25¾°</b>; the latitude, according to the proportion of the changed inclination of the Ecliptic to the Equator, increasing or decreasing somewhat, in each [star] according to its particular disposition, this way or that. Which difference [in latitude], however, is not found to have been altered, even where it is greatest, beyond a third part of a degree [20′]; nay, in this tiny change too no small doubt lies beneath. And these [places are given] as the Artificers themselves reported the places of some few of the principal fixed stars in their age — in which, however, I suspect that some error crept in, especially for Ptolemy and Timocharis (whereby they did not scrutinize them subtly enough, on account of the excessive inequality), the notations of Albategnius and Hipparchus existing as more reliable. From which I have also chiefly gathered that the fixed stars accomplish <b>51″ each year</b> — as much, namely, as belongs to the Sun's simple motion in that little difference of time which is between the Equinoctial [tropical] year and the Sidereal, which we establish at very nearly <b>20⅔ minutes</b> [of time]. Wherefore, in <b>70 Julian Years and 7 Months</b>, the stars complete one Degree — not in a hundred [years and] 30 (as Ptolemy held), [and] far [from being completed] in many more, as you seem to think. For [the predecessors'] observations would thus have had to have been erroneous beyond measure — which it is lawful for no one to assert too licentiously. There is added, at the foot of the same book, a catalogue of <b>100 of the more notable stars</b>, exhibiting [their] Declinations and Right Ascensions for the year 1600 and 1700, together with their differences, by which these can be conveniently enough adapted, not only for the intermediate [half-]century, but also for the immediately preceding and following [period], on either side. But if you are eager to know their risings and settings, with respect to a fixed Horizon, by reason of the Sun, the Tables of Regiomontanus will supply that without difficulty from the given declination and Right Ascension — or, if you prefer, by Triangles. And so that the Sun's place, from our emendation, may also be established for you more truly than otherwise, I have added an <b>Ephemeris</b> dispatching its motion at the Meridian, for the single days of this passing Year, in the longitude of places [of] 37 degrees. To derive the same for several preceding and following [years], within one or two Minutes, is thence of little labour. But if it shall please [you] to investigate this more precisely, use my Tables on the Sun's motion, which I think you received in that first chapter on its course restored by us (communicated to you from wherever, at last). If not, you have here, for every event, a gathering [<i>quaternio</i>] sufficient for its calculation for the next several centuries. I send, moreover, another book, comprising our <b>Mechanics of Astronomy</b> [<i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i>], which, however, I place first in order. Having inspected it, you will estimate, not obscurely, with how great effort and diligence I have handled such things. And if it shall please [you] to have one or another Instrument made after their likeness, you have therein the Design [Idea] and the use. For it would not be useless if you should provide for yourself some <b>Sextant</b> for the distances of the stars, and a <b>Quadrant</b> of just magnitude for measuring their altitudes. In which matter that aforenamed Danish student can be of use to you, both as regards perfecting the graduations and arranging the rest; and, together [with you], to extend the work into the Practice of observations, as he learned with me. For I should wish that careful attention be paid in many celestial places, lest the same happen in our [age] which <b>Naso</b> [Ovid] complains of about the age of Romulus:<br><i>And the stars ran unobserved throughout the year.</i><br>As also (which is to be lamented) now nearly happens.</p><p>You also make mention, toward the end, of the <b>Lilian</b> or <b>Pontifical</b> [Gregorian] year — as ineptly established as it was approved. Which, although not most exactly consistent in all its numbers (for who could have achieved this without consulting heaven?), I nonetheless judge altogether more suitable than that earlier one [the Julian], which committed an anticipation of so many days — on account of some difference between the Equinoctial [tropical] and the Julian year — from the beginning of the years of Christ, or (as they preferred) from the Council of Nicaea. Nor can so enormous a divagation easily creep in hereafter, according to that Gregorian method; but (to say what the truth is) it is not possible to disentangle these matters with the highest precision (if any were necessary) by any such shortcuts and by-paths. Rather, one must have a subtle knowledge of the very motion of each luminary for any centuries whatever — such as not even at this time do we have ascertained, from the customary Tables, without every trace of error: as the calculation of the first Month of this Year, in the Sun and Moon (for the longitude of [the] place [of] 35 degrees), drawn from our observations and Tables, and compared with the Alphonsine and Copernican numbers (which I have also attached on a certain sheet, so that you may reason out the rest from it), will teach you. These things, then, which it was convenient to send now, take in good part. I shall subjoin in due time, the Maker of the heavens favouring, more and greater things about the heavenly [matters]. And although, by these more serious matters, I now make too much business for you, I impart along [with this], nonetheless, some poetic <i>jeux</i> besides — out of the many I have, composed here and there by various [hands] for my sake since I left Denmark, and sent to me — not that they smack of anything worthy of your Heroic vein, but only that you may see that my emigration hence is at heart for others too. I have selected chiefly these, in which the causes of our departure are somehow insinuated. If that most excellent <b>Dousa the younger</b> — whom the fates (alas!) snatched from us so prematurely — were still alive, he would doubtless have meditated something of more cultivated genius about these matters, as he had also before adorned our <b>Cometographic</b> [work] with his mellifluous leisures.</p><p>Our <b>Tengnagel</b>, returned hither, reported to me that you very much desire that I migrate to you, into <b>Batavia</b> [Holland] — which I credit to your love toward us; just as I have also understood, not obscurely, in this regard, the desires of the most illustrious prince <b>Maurice [of Nassau]</b> and of the Estates [the States-General], and their favour toward me — to say nothing of the <b>English</b>. And indeed my mind would not shrink from that change of place, since the skilful talents of the Dutch have always pleased me more than those of the Germans. But I cannot conceal from you that, meanwhile — while I tarry here on the borders of Germany and Denmark beyond a year, so as to leave my countrymen enough time to look at the matter more rightly (still mindful of the Fatherland, to which we owe much) — it became known to the <b>Sacred Imperial Majesty</b>, by the report of certain of the chief [men] at Court, that I am lingering here. Wherefore His Majesty, through the most ample Lord <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> [Corraducius], Vice-Chancellor of the Empire — with the mediation also of Lord Doctor <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> [Tadeáš Hájek], most closely joined to me by old Friendship — ordered me summoned to <b>Prague</b>, these [men] giving sure hope that the Imperial Majesty, if I come, will pursue and promote me and my studies with most clement favour. About which I have already received, this summer, several letters from there insinuating similar things. Moreover the most Serene <b>Elector of Cologne</b> — to whom the Emperor attributes much — commended me and all my [affairs] to him \"of the better stamp\" (as they say), in a letter written to His Imperial Majesty in his own hand on this account, filled with exquisite favour toward me; and likewise in others, to the Magnificent Lord <b>Barvitius</b>, the Emperor's privy Councillor and supreme Secretary. The same Cologne [Elector] also gave special letters, both to Lord <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>, my kinsman-by-marriage and dearest host, and to me, by which he persuades [me] that this journey will not be unfruitful. Nay, he has even, of his own accord, promised that if — beyond all his expectation — it should not turn out that the Emperor is willing to foster and sustain my studies kindly and liberally, he will himself do that very thing at his own expense, not unwillingly. Wherefore these means, so conveniently offered — especially with the Emperor, the supreme Head of the Empire (since I strive to constitute and stabilize Astronomy universally) — I judge must be tried before all [else]. I have therefore resolved to set out for Prague at the very first opportunity, that I may most humbly learn the Emperor's most clement will. But if everything should answer our wishes less [well] from there, then at last I shall determine something certain about choosing other seats; and shall either cross over to you, or even to the English, or rather to the <b>King of the Gauls</b> [France], who, I know, will receive me and my studies most liberally, and will munificently perpetuate their conservation in that Kingdom — and perhaps, in this regard, will not yield to the old King of the Spains, <b>Alfonso</b> [X], as far as circumstances there conveniently allow. Whatever it shall be, the heaven and <b>Urania</b>, which include all lands, will not be excluded from all lands — however much our Denmark, through the ignorance and malevolence of certain [men], has not sufficiently attended to such sublime rites. I shall signify by letter, when opportunity is given, how this journey into Bohemia has succeeded, so that you may foresee and estimate from it what needs to be done. But I cease to detain you longer. It is yours to take in good part this prolixity, which the veneration of your sublime genius — and the excellence of the matter of which we treat, and your benevolence toward me — has extorted.</p><p>Farewell, illustrious and great Man, and live long and happily for the Republic of Letters.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, from the Rantzau Castle near Hamburg, on the 23rd day of August, Old Style, of the common Christian Year 1598.<br>Most devoted to Your Most Illustrious Amplitude,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>) — text cross-checked against the local scan <i>brahe-t_opera_08_Part2.pdf</i>. MS witnesses: <b>A</b> = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, Cod. 693 in-folio (copy); <b>B</b> = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (copy); <b>C</b> = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen, <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i> (Hauniae 1668). The OCR-intruded apparatus sigla (stray superscript \"A\" keyed to witness A) have been removed from the source block as non-textual.<br>- <b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"Wandesburgo ex Arce Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum die 23. Augusti st. vet. … 1598\"</i> — explicitly <b>Old Style</b> (<i>stylo veteri</i>), written from <b>Wandsbek</b>, Heinrich Rantzau's castle near Hamburg, where Tycho was based in his first exile year. Agrees with the catalogue label. The companion letter is the earlier 9 July 1598 Scaliger letter (15980709001); cf. the later precession exchange 16000310001 / 16000623001.<br>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), son of Julius Caesar Scaliger, the foremost chronologist of the age, then at the <b>University of Leiden</b> (<i>Academia Lugdunensis</i> = Lugdunum Batavorum, <b>not</b> Lyon). His <i>De emendatione temporum</i> (1583; rev. 1598) is the work under discussion. He was the prime mover in trying to draw Tycho to Holland.<br>- <b>The scientific spine (chronology × astronomy).</b> The letter is a sustained technical exchange on the foundations of chronology:<br>  - <b>Equinox-observation accuracy.</b> Tycho praises Scaliger's approval of his equinox observations and explains why precise equinox timing demands instruments able to read the Sun's meridian altitude to <b>1⁄6 of a minute (10″)</b>, with <b>parallax</b> and <b>refraction</b> corrected — accuracy neither the ancients nor Regiomontanus/Copernicus commanded. <b>Hipparchus's</b> equinoxes \"limp\" by up to a quarter-day (his instrument read only to <b>5′</b>); even <b>Ptolemy</b> Tycho will not accuse of a whole-day error, since Ptolemy's obliquity was good to <b>20″</b> (though \"perhaps borrowed from Hipparchus\").<br>  - <b>The moving-poles dispute (the core disagreement).</b> Against Scaliger's notion of <i>mobile equatorial poles distinct from the world-pole</i>, Tycho argues — from the changed stellar latitudes and the changing <b>obliquity</b> since Timocharis/Hipparchus/Ptolemy — that it is the <b>Ecliptic</b> (with the Sun's path), not the Equator, that shifts; the equatorial pole <b>is</b> the world-pole. He demonstrates with the <b>pole-star</b> (the last star in Ursa Minor's tail): polar distance <b>12⅖°</b> in Hipparchus's day → <b>3°</b> \"25 years ago\" → <b>2° 51½′</b> now; it will close to <b>27½′</b> in ~500 years (at the start of Cancer / the solstitial colure) but <b>never coincide</b> with the pole, the stars keeping the <i>fixed</i> ecliptic poles.<br>  - <b>Heliacal risings &amp; the year.</b> On <b>Sirius</b> (Canicula) rising on nearly the same Julian day for 15 centuries — Tycho shows the constancy is <b>accidental</b>, a coincidental cancellation of the declination change (2°40′) against the Julian–sidereal-year drift, not a general rule (it already drifts to the 3rd day from Ptolemy to now, and would reach the 5th). <b>Arcturus</b> refutes Scaliger's fixed-date claim outright: 2 March in Hesiod, 3 March under Meton, 9 March under Ptolemy, ~19 March now. Hence \"nothing certain about the reckonings of the years can be borrowed\" from heliacal risings.<br>  - <b>The sidereal year &amp; precession constant.</b> Tycho defends the <b>sidereal year</b> (Egyptians/Babylonians → Thābit → Copernicus) but rejects <b>Copernicus's libration of the Earth's axis</b> (since the Earth has no such motion), preferring to \"save\" any inequality by a <i>universal solar hypothesis</i> — a moving solar ecliptic. His adopted year is \"a little less than the <b>Gelalaean</b> (Jalālī),\" set from <b>Regiomontanus's</b> century of observations, and good for 3–4 centuries; over longer spans it grows, but never to the <b>Alphonsine</b> value Scaliger favours. He states his <b>precession = 51″/year</b>, equal to the solar motion in the <b>tropical−sidereal year difference of ≈20⅔ minutes of time</b>, giving <b>1° per 70 years 7 months</b> (vs Ptolemy's slower rate; see the crux below).<br>  - <b>The gifts.</b> Tycho sends, in secret, his <b>1000-star catalogue</b> (\"such as no prior centuries have seen\"), reduced to epoch <b>1600</b>, with the precession back-corrections (Albategnius −10°, Ptolemy −21⅖°, Hipparchus −24¼°, Timocharis −25¾°); a sub-catalogue of <b>100 stars</b> with declinations/RA for 1600 &amp; 1700; a <b>solar Ephemeris</b> for the current year (long. 37°); and the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae <b>Mechanica</b></i> \"placed first in order,\" with instrument designs — urging Scaliger to commission a <b>sextant</b> and <b>quadrant</b> (his Danish student to help graduate them), lest \"the stars run unobserved through the year\" (Ovid, <i>Fasti</i>).<br>  - <b>The calendar.</b> On the <b>Gregorian</b> (\"Lilian/Pontifical\") reform that Scaliger detested: Tycho judges it \"as ineptly established as approved,\" yet \"altogether more suitable\" than the drifting Julian; true precision, though, needs exact lunar-solar theory, which even now the standard Tables lack.<br>- <b>The requested books.</b> A Greek-printed book on <b>Hipparchus's observations</b> (published in Italy) and a corrected <b>al-Battānī</b> (<i>Albategnius</i>, \"Mahomet of al-Raqqa\") with his tables — Tycho wants pre-Ptolemaic data to refine the sidereal year; also the <b>Jewish equinox</b> datum and the <b>Actian-year</b> star-phases book of Ptolemy (the <i>Phaseis</i>). His methodological refrain: comparison-years must be <b>equally distant from a leap-year</b> (the reason his own tables append a quinquennium).<br>- <b>The patronage decision (the documentary heart).</b> This is one of the clearest statements of Tycho's 1598 patronage manoeuvre: while based at Wandsbek \"beyond a year\" to give Denmark time to reconsider, he has been <b>summoned to Prague by Rudolf II</b>, through Vice-Chancellor <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> (Corraducius) and mediated by his old friend <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b> (Thaddaeus Hagecius); the <b>Elector of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria) has commended him to the Emperor in an autograph letter and to secretary <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>, even promising to fund Tycho himself if the Emperor declines. Tycho resolves to try Prague first; failing that, he will go to the <b>Dutch</b> (Scaliger, <b>Maurice of Nassau</b>, the States-General), the <b>English</b>, or — preferably — <b>Henri IV of France</b>, whom he hopes will rival <b>Alfonso X</b> of Castile as an astronomical patron. The Tychonic flourish: \"heaven and Urania, which include all lands, will not be excluded from all lands.\"<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> (Tycho's envoy, warmly received by Scaliger); <b>Regiomontanus</b>; <b>Copernicus</b>; <b>Hipparchus</b>, <b>Ptolemy</b>, <b>Timocharis</b> (the three ancient observers); <b>Thābit ibn Qurra</b> (Thebit); <b>al-Battānī</b> (Albategnius); <b>\"Christian Johnson\"</b> (<i>Christianus Iohannis</i>, a former Danish household-student now with Scaliger); <b>Hesiod, Meton, Euctemon</b> (the Arcturus testimonies); <b>Consul Paul Hainzel</b> of Augsburg (the 1569–70 great-quadrant garden — see below); <b>Janus Dousa the younger</b> (d. 1596, the Leiden poet who versified Tycho's comet book <i>De mundi… recentioribus phaenomenis</i>); <b>Maurice of Nassau</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b>; <b>Tadeáš Hájek</b>; the <b>Elector of Cologne</b>; <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>; <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b>; <b>Henri IV</b> (\"King of the Gauls\"); <b>Alfonso X</b> (\"the old King of the Spains\"). Place-note: the <b>\"Horizon of the Greeks\"/\"Boeotian region\"</b> (pole ≈40°) is the reference latitude for the heliacal-rising computations, chosen to match Hesiod/Meton.<br>- <b>The Augsburg datum.</b> <i>\"25 years ago … at Augsburg, by a quadrant of 14 cubits in its Radius, in the garden near Consul Hainzel's estate\"</i> = Tycho's <b>great quadrant</b> built with <b>Paul Hainzel</b> at Göggingen near Augsburg in <b>1569–70</b> (the famous large radius graduated to read fine fractions of a minute). \"25 years\" is a round approximation from 1598 (the instrument is ~28 years prior); translated as written, not corrected.<br>- <b>Latin &amp; Greek tags.</b> <i>\"si parva licet componere magnis\"</i> — Virgil, <i>Georgics</i> 4.176. <i>Zoili</i> — carping critics, after Zoilus the maligner of Homer. <i>κοσμοπολίτην</i> — \"citizen of the world.\" <i>\"Et inobservata per annum / Sydera currebant\"</i> — Ovid, <i>Fasti</i> I (on Romulus's unastronomical calendar). <i>cosmice / acronyce</i> — cosmical (rising with the Sun) vs acronychal (rising at sunset). <i>pragmatεία</i> (πραγματεία) — \"practical undertaking.\" Measure idiom: <i>sexta minuti pars</i> = 10″; <i>duodecima gradus pars</i> = 5′; <i>quincunx gradus</i> = 5⁄12° = 25′; <i>tertia gradus pars</i> = 20′; <i>semis (minuti)</i> = 30″. The <i>Annus Gelalaeus</i> = the Jalālī (Persian, Malik-Shāh / Omar Khayyām) solar year; <i>Annus Actiacus</i> = the Alexandrian year reckoned from Actium.<br>- <b>Cruces (named, not smoothed).</b><br>  1. <b><i>\"centenis 30\"</i> (Ptolemy's precession rate).</b> The Dreyer scan <b>confirms</b> this as the printed reading: <i>\"non autem centenis 30 (uti voluit PTOLEMÆUS).\"</i> Read as printed it gives \"130 [years per degree],\" whereas Ptolemy's documented value is <b>1° per 100 years</b>. The intent is clear from context (Tycho's 70 y 7 m vs a slower Ptolemaic rate vs Scaliger's still-slower one), but the numeral \"30\" is anomalous — reproduced verbatim and rendered \"a hundred [years and] 30,\" flagged here as a probable textual corruption in the tradition (not silently emended to \"100\").<br>  2. <b><i>\"fusile\" → \"fuisse.\"</b></i> In <i>\"hallucinatum fusile non dixerim,\"</i> the working transcription's <i>fusile</i> is a corruption of <i>fuisse</i> (the long-s ligature <i>fuiſſe</i> mis-resolved; the OCR of the Dreyer scan mangles the parallel clause the same way, and the later <i>\"erroneas fuisse antecessorum\"</i> preserves the correct form). Translated as <i>fuisse</i> (\"that he was…\").<br>  3. <b>Obliquity \"66 gradibus.\"</b> <i>\"ea quam PTOLEMAeI Abacus in 66 exacte ponit gradibus\"</i> refers to the pole-star's <b>ecliptic latitude</b> (≈66° in antiquity), \"taken in a broad manner,\" not to the obliquity itself; rendered accordingly.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from the corrupt reading \"hallucinatum fusile non dixerim\" to Dreyer's \"hallucinatum fuisse non dixerim\" (TBOO VIII, pp. 101-102); the published English translation already reflected the correct sense and required no change.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, technically continuous chronology-and-astronomy letter rendered securely throughout; the substance (the equinox-accuracy argument; the moving-ecliptic-vs-equator precession dispute and the pole-star's path; the Sirius/Arcturus heliacal-rising refutation of fixed Julian dates; the sidereal-year defence with <b>precession 51″/yr = 1°/70 y 7 m</b> and the tropical−sidereal difference of 20⅔ min; the 1000-star catalogue with epoch-1600 reductions; the Gregorian-calendar verdict; and the pivotal Prague-vs-Holland/England/France patronage passage with Coraduz, Hájek, the Cologne Elector and Barvitius) is clear and cross-corroborated by the surrounding 1598 correspondence (cf. 15980508001, 15980820001). The local <b>Dreyer scan cross-check</b> (§1) resolved the <i>fusile/fuisse</i> corruption and confirmed the <i>\"centenis 30\"</i> reading. Two flagged residues keep it from [A]: the anomalous <b>\"centenis 30\"</b> numeral (a likely tradition-level corruption of Ptolemy's known 1°/100-yr value, reproduced not emended), and the heavy <b>OCR apparatus-sigla</b> removal declared above. Neither obscures the through-line.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full 12-page Dreyer slice as page images (chunks 1-4, 5-8, then a close reread of page 8 alone and pages 3-4 and 11 alone for disputed glyphs/words; 9-12). The letter proper runs Dreyer pp.100-109 (slice pp.2-11); slice p.1 is the tail of the preceding Tycho-to-Cragius letter and slice p.12 is the opening of the following Tycho-to-Rosenkrantz letter, both correctly excluded from source_text/published_english. Collated the opening and closing formulas, and every proper name (Tengnagel, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Timocharis, Albategnius/al-Battani, Copernicus, Regiomontanus, Christianus Iohannis, Hainzel, Rantzau, Corraducius, Hagecius/Hajek, Barvitius, the Elector of Cologne, Maurice of Nassau, Dousa the younger, Alfonso, Ovid/Naso), and every number, date and sum against Dreyer's page images: 12⅖ degrees, 2 deg 51.5 min, 3 degrees, 25 years, the 14-cubit-radius Augsburg quadrant, 500 years / 27.5 minutes, 15 centuries, 1500 years, 3 degrees less a third, 900 years, 150 years, Regulus's and Propus's longitude/latitude/RA/declination figures, the 20-year star catalogue, the per-author longitude offsets (Albategnius/Ptolemy/Hipparchus/Timocharis), 51 arcseconds/year, 70 years 7 months per degree, the 100-star catalogue for 1600/1700, the 37- and 35-degree ephemeris longitudes, and the 23 August 1598 dateline. All matched Dreyer and the published English rendered them faithfully, including mathematically correct bracketed unit conversions (e.g. a sixth of a minute = 10 arcsec) that are clearly marked editorial supplements. The single defect found is a TEXT_ERROR: our source_text's \"fusile\" against Dreyer's \"fuiße\"/fuisse, which did not propagate into any translation fault. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION or HEDGE_LOSS was found anywhere in the letter.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints \"fusile\" (an unrelated adjective, \"molten/cast\", that does not construe grammatically) where Dreyer clearly prints \"fuiße\" = \"fuisse\" (the expected accusative-and-infinitive participle, \"I would not say [him] to have hallucinated\"). A corruption in the house Latin transcription, not in the translation, which correctly rendered \"fuisse\". Dreyer prints: <i>Attamen illum totum diem in ingressibus Aequinoctiorum hallucinatum fuiße non dixerim (i.e. \"fuisse\"), TBOO VIII pp.101-102</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII, pp. 99-102) covering the defect site; p.101 line 41 runs into p.102 line 1 as 'hallucinatum fuiße non dixerim', confirming the audit's claim and Dreyer's own gloss ('fuiße' = 'fuisse'). Only the Latin required repair; the English clause already rendered the correct sense and was left untouched, as were all surrounding readings.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 in 2do. Afskrift", "B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift", "C København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15990417001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000310001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Scaliger for his concern about affairs, for discussions of chronology and stellar reckoning, and for courteously receiving Tengnagel; declines to detail the reasons for his departure.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980823001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980905001", "d": "1598-09-05", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 5 September 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift — copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTUTE AC ERUDITIONE VIRO INPRIMIS CONSPICUO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI, ET AMICO SUO INTEGERRIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CHARISSIME OLIGERE, scripsi tibi semel atque iterum; nihil tamen adhuc responsi accepi. Quod nihilominus occupationibus propter nuptias tuas tribuo. Expectavit te amanuensis meus Hafniae per triduum, sed cum interea pro ut constitueras, non accederes, forte vento praepeditus, is noluit diuturniores isthic moras nectere, sciens, te facile per alium Hafnia Lubecam versus literas posse dirigere. At cum ne hoc quidem factum sit, rogo, velis mihi nunc absque diuturniore procrastinatione ad singula, quae priores meae literae continebant, respondere, et eas Lubecam mittere, si expeditior non datur ratio. Nihil iam adeo remoratur iter meum ac currus, quem promisisti, quem scio Arhusiae paratum navem expectare, ut Lubecam transvehatur. Quod et Scotus ille (licet et mihi id promiserit) et quidam alij neglexerunt, cum nemo in tua absentia adesset, qui negotium serio urgeret. Fac, ut eum quam citissime Lubecae habeam, vel saltem indica, cur id fieri nequeat, ne frustra illum diutius expectem, sed potius de alio mihi provideam. Instat enim hyems, nec peregrinationem diutius differre commodum est. Quae Coloniensis mea de causa Imperatori scripsit, puto te accepisse. Quin et illustrissimus Dux Megapolensis ULDARICUS pariter ad Imperatorem meo nomine commendatitias dedit, quarum hîc Exemplum habes. Quorsum eae mihi usui esse possint, facile intelligis. Serva id tibi soli; neque nisi fidelibus amicis (si qui sunt) inspiciendum concede; uti nec priora. Addidi alterum exemplar versuum BRASCHIJ Poëtae Rostochiensis, quod rectius est castigatum atque revisum. Cupio mihi remitti Carmen Cramerianum, quod tamen lima indiget; et de singulis alias, quorum mea interesse putaveris, admoneri, tum quoque de libris, an Regi oblati sint, quidque responderit, et an tibi postea cesserint nec ne. Plura nunc non vacat. Sed has saltem ad te deproperare lubuit, nactus tam commodam opportunitatem per hunc Studiosum Cimbrum hac ad vos transeuntem.</p><p>Vale, salutata a me tua matre et coniuge, mea nepte, quam diligentissime.<br>Raptim Wandesburgo 5. Septembris Anno 1598.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, especially conspicuous in virtue and erudition, Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, his kinsman by blood, kinsman by marriage, and most upright friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Dearest Holger, I have written to you once and again; yet I have so far received no reply. Which nonetheless I set down to your occupations on account of your wedding. My amanuensis waited for you at Copenhagen for three days; but when in the meantime you did not come, as you had arranged — perhaps held back by the wind — he was unwilling to weave longer delays there, knowing that you could easily direct letters from Copenhagen toward Lübeck through another. But since not even this has been done, I ask you: be willing now, without further procrastination, to answer me on each of the matters my earlier letters contained, and to send them to Lübeck, if no more expeditious means presents itself. Nothing now so holds up my journey as the wagon you promised — which I know is ready at Aarhus, awaiting a ship to be carried across to Lübeck. Both that Scot (although he too promised it to me) and certain others have neglected this, since no one was at hand, in your absence, to press the business seriously. See to it that I have it at Lübeck as quickly as possible, or at least indicate why it cannot be done, lest I wait for it longer in vain, and rather provide myself with another [conveyance]. For winter is at hand, and it is not convenient to defer the journey longer. What the [Archbishop] of Cologne wrote to the Emperor on my behalf, I think you have received. Indeed the most illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg, Ulrich, has likewise given commendatory letters to the Emperor in my name, a copy of which you have here. To what use these can be to me, you easily understand. Keep it to yourself alone, and allow it to be seen by none but faithful friends (if any there be) — as also the earlier [papers]. I have added another copy of the verses of Brasch, the Rostock poet, which is more correctly amended and revised. I should like the Cramer poem sent back to me — it still needs the file [of polishing]; and to be advised on particular matters elsewhere, in which you judge my interest lies; then too about the books — whether they have been presented to the King, and what he replied, and whether they were afterward granted to you or not. No time for more now. But I was glad at least to hurry these to you, having got so convenient an opportunity through this Cimbrian student passing this way to you.</p><p>Farewell — your mother, and your wife, my niece, being most diligently greeted from me.<br>In haste, from Wandsbek, 5 September, in the year 1598.<br>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; moment.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Wandsbek</b> (Wandesburgum, near Hamburg), in the months after his 1597 departure from Denmark — actively courting <b>imperial patronage</b> and preparing the onward journey that would lead, via Wittenberg, to Bohemia. The letter is a logistics-and-patronage dispatch to his closest Danish kinsman-ally.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius de Rosenholm</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned\"</b> (1574–1642), lord of <b>Rosenholm</b>, addressed as <i>consanguineus, affinis et amicus</i> — Tycho's <b>kinsman by blood and by marriage</b> and his trusted agent in Denmark. His silence is excused by his recent <b>wedding</b> (<i>nuptiae tuae</i>): he had married <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (1593), here named as <i>coniunx … mea neptis</i>, \"your wife, my niece\" — establishing the affinity. (Holger is the <i>Oligerus</i> whose news recurs in the 1600 letters, e.g. brahe-t_16000420001, brahe-t_16000813001.)</li><li><b>The logistics.</b> Tycho is stranded for want of a <b>wagon (<i>currus</i>)</b> Holger had promised, \"ready at <b>Aarhus</b> awaiting a ship\" to be carried across to <b>Lübeck</b>; unnamed agents — \"that <b>Scot</b>\" among them — have let it lapse. With winter coming he presses for the wagon or a clear refusal, so he can arrange other transport. Correspondence is to be routed Copenhagen → Lübeck.</li><li><b>The patronage papers.</b></li><li><i>Quae Coloniensis … Imperatori scripsit</i> = the letter the <b>Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria) wrote to the <b>Emperor (Rudolf II)</b> on Tycho's behalf.</li><li><i>illustrissimus Dux Megapolensis Uldaricus</i> = <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</b> (<i>Megapolis</i> = Mecklenburg), who likewise gave <b>commendatory letters</b> to the Emperor in Tycho's name — a copy enclosed. (The same Duke Ulrich figures, two years later, in the debt thread, brahe-t_16010809001.) Tycho enjoins strict confidentiality: show only to \"faithful friends, if any there be.\"</li><li><i>versus Braschij Poëtae Rostochiensis</i> = verses by <b>Brasch, the Rostock poet</b> (a revised copy enclosed); <i>Carmen Cramerianum</i> = a <b>poem by Cramer</b>, which Tycho wants returned for further polishing (<i>lima indiget</i>, \"needs the file\" — the Horatian <i>limae labor</i>). <i>de libris, an Regi oblati sint</i> = books presented (or to be presented) to the <b>King of Denmark</b> (Christian IV).</li><li><b>Courier.</b> <i>Studiosus Cimbrus</i> = a <b>Jutland student</b> (<i>Cimber</i> = Cimbrian/Jutlander) passing through to Denmark, the bearer of this letter.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> The text survives as an <b>afskrift (copy)</b> in Langebek's Diplomatarium at the Copenhagen Rigsarkiv, not as the autograph original.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting\"); <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Raptim</i> → \"in haste\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"with his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>5. Septembris Anno 1598</i> at Wandsbek; no style marker. Wandsbek/Holstein and Denmark were on the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1598; reproduced as written, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is clean and the substance (the stalled wagon, the Cologne and Mecklenburg commendatory letters, the enclosed verses, the kin-relationship to Holger and Sophie Brahe) is secure. Graded B rather than A because two of the literary figures named only by surname — <i>Brasch</i> the Rostock poet and <i>Cramer</i> (the <i>Carmen Cramerianum</i>) — cannot be individuated with full confidence, and \"that Scot\" is anonymous in the source; none of these affects the letter's sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 slice pages visually. The letter (Tycho to Holger Rosenkrantz, headed 'NOBILISSIMO ET VIRTUTE AC ERUDITIONE VIRO INPRIMIS CONSPICUO OLIGERO ROSAEKRANTZIO...') runs from the drop-cap C on p.108 through the top of p.110, citation '[Ex apographo in Archivo Regni Daniae: Diplomatario Langebekii]'. Collated the full Latin word-for-word against Dreyer: identical throughout. Checked every name/place/number: amanuensis waited 'per triduum' (three days) at Hafniae/Copenhagen, wagon ready at Arhusiae/Aarhus bound for Lubecam/Lubeck, Dux Megapolensis Uldaricus (Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg), Braschij Poetae Rostochiensis (Brasch of Rostock), Carmen Cramerianum, date/place 'Raptim Wandesburgo 5. Septembris Anno 1598'. Verified the 'coniuge, mea nepte' construction (wife = niece, not mother) is rendered correctly and unflipped in English. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 13, "prev": "brahe-t_15980810001", "next": "brahe-t_15980909001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Urges Holger to reply to earlier letters without further delay and to dispatch the promised wagon from Aarhus to Lübeck, which is holding up the journey.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980905001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980909001", "d": "1598-09-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 9 September 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text note.</b> The copy leaves several politically sensitive words <b>blank</b>; Dreyer supplies them in brackets — <i>[Regi]/[Rege]</i> (the King = <b>Christian IV</b>), <i>[Cancellarij]</i> (the Chancellor), <i>[Dania]</i>. These are editorial reconstructions of deliberately-omitted names; rendered in the translation as the words they stand for, and flagged. A couple of short stretches are copy-corrupt (noted).</p>", "la": "<p>TAM NATALIUM SPLENDORE QUAM PROPRIA VIRTUTE, PRAeEMINENTI DOCTRINA VIRO INPRIMIS CLARO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGUINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO DESIDERATISSIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CUM ante biduum per Studiosum Danum, qui se ea loca, ubi in Cimbria habitas, transiturum pollicitus est, quaedam ad te deproperassem, accidit ut heri sero eas, quas Hafniae antepenultimo Augusti ad me dederas, acceperim. Quare statim misso Hamburgum cursore meo, an Studiosus ille adhuc ibi haereret, inquiri iussi. Quem cum hodie primum ante meridiem isthinc discessurum rescivissem, has responsionis loco iterum festinanter exaro. Si alias ad me ante mensem circiter dedisti literas, quam eae erant, quibus ultimo rescriptum est, scias me illas non recepisse. Scribis nunc te scire, me currum promissum accepisse. At unde hoc? Hafnia enim tuae datae sunt, et meus, qui paulo ante apud te erat, amanuensis, indicabat eum ne Lubecam quidem nedum huc allatum esse. Quod et literae meae insinuabant. Rectius itaque scias, me eum non accepisse. Et nihil fere est, quod nunc adeo iter meum remoretur, atque hoc ipsum. Si Lubecam venisset, actutum me certiorem reddidisset de eo IOACHIMUS BUCH, aliquoties hoc nomine a me per literas admonitus. Si vis, ut eum obtineam, fac ut id quam citissime fiat. Caeteroquin Hamburgi alium coëmere necesse habeo. Scio tamen per te non stetisse, quod res haec tam lente procedat. Et gratias habeo pro hoc erga me officio maximas, relaturus per opportunitatem. De libris, cur non oblati sint, prolixe disseris. At eiusmodi argumenta apud me pondus non inveniunt, imo ne sexcenta his graviora, utpote qui adversariorum et calumniatorum perversas oblocutiones non ita ponderem, ut tu vel alij isthic sub alis eorum depressi, sed flocci faciam. Libertati enim me asserui, quam tuebor. Cum hîc esses, aliter sentiebas et sponte in te recepisti, quod libros [Regios] in manus tradere velles, imo et quaedam alia inculcare de rescriptis ad me [Cancellarij] literis, ipso forte [Rege] inscio. Tunc omnia placebant, adeo ut [Regi] quaedam scribenda relegeris et approbaveris; quae tamen nunc volens omisi, ne, postquam semel inclemens responsum ad Rostochio scriptas accepissem, ulterius supplex insimuler. At quorsum nunc vetus illa et avita constantia! An postquam in Daniam redijsti, Coelum ruit! o pavidae mentes! Miseret sane me Patriae quondam meae, quod nemo ferme in ea sit (uti audio) qui verum iustumque ingenue profiteri audeat, imo ne quidem de his mussitare. Quo haec vergant, tandem exitus monstrabit. Sed utinam non nimis sero tunc sapiatis atque animum resumatis! Non putarim, te Carminis illius, quod Astronomiae nostrae mechanicae praefixum est, paenitere atque ob id libros ipsummet exhibere tergiversari. Neque enim tibi incommodare poterit, quod communiter usu receptum est, nempe in Zoilos malevolos et inscios talia libris attexere; et ipsemet, cum praesens esses, sciebas, quomodo per alios (si qui oblatrarant) excusares. Quicquid id est, cum mei non sit moris, ea, quae semel deliberato constituta sunt, retractare, stat etiamnum, ut et ante, sententia, libros illos [Regi] ipsi in manus esse tradendos, quocunque tandem vultu eos accipiat, et quomodocunque a mei malevolis et osoribus id vertetur aut pervertetur. Altiora tracto, quam ut tam humiles censuras suscipiam. Optarem tamen id per te ipsum fieri, ea saltem de causa, ut tibi, non alijs, cederent libri illi, quemadmodum inter nos ventilatum est. Sin vero id fieri nequeat, offerat eos Affinis meus OLAUS ROSENSPAR, qui et literas meas tradidit, infrugiferas et perverse exceptas; ut sint libri hi quasi earum Corollarium et taciti testes, me non ignorare, quam parvam habeat proportionem [Dania] ad totam Terrae superficiem; utpote quae apice minimi digiti in Globo Terrestri bicubitali tota tegi possit. Et quid de immenso Coelo sentiendum, quod animo volvimus, quod mente comprehendimus et superamus? O humiles curae! Nihil peius me habet, quam quod non carmina quaedam ijsdem libris praenotarim, quae meos pungerent calumniatores, ad similitudinem eorum, quae alijs quibusdam Exemplaribus Viris Principibus transmissis praeposui. Nam, ut scias, Electori Coloniensi, Principi Uraniae MAURITIO, Duci Megapolitano ULDARICO Avo Regis, et Duci ADOLPHO Holsatiae, Affini eius (ut de SCALIGERO non dicam intelligentiore horum aestimatore) libri hi additis ijsdem, quos tibi praescriptos misi, versibus, communicati sunt. Num et illi hinc statuent, me sibi supplicem fieri? Num inde quis ratiocinabitur, me paenitudine duci, quod Caesari eos dedicarim, cui tamen necdum oblati sunt, cum ipsemet hoc efficere constituerim? quasi non moris sit, libros uni Regi vel Principi dedicatos alijs etiam dono dare. Neque sane unquam mihi in mentem venit, quidpiam operum meorum, etiamsi in Dania mansissem, [Regi] nuncupare, cum sciam eum ἄμουσον esse et literas aversari, imo forte nec cuiquam alij, cum librorum dedicatio adeo usitata illiberale quid et mendicitatem quandam prae se ferat, nisi id ad pares fiat, atque eorum, quae isthic tractantur, gnaros. Nam quod Landtgravio primum Epistolarum Tomum inscripsi, ut Patris memoriam et diutinam mecum amicitiam recolerem, id factum est, utque plus fidei illae Epistolae mererentur, quod nihil in ijs esset supposititium. Nec ego quidpiam inde reportavi, ne quidem gratiarum actionem, quamvis bis vel ter idem Princeps mihi postea scripserit. Quod tamen neutiquam curo, cum ad tam vilia non respiciam. Sed de his plus satis, et forte nimium. Tute facile, quid tibi faciendum sit, in his et alijs statues. Si nolit ROSENSPARRIUS, offerat RAMELIUS. Scurrilem istum librum, quem te remittere ais, non recepi. Fac ut eum mature obtineam. Constitui enim famosum ipsum scriptorem Pragae cum Caesaris venia secundum Ius tractari curare; nec aliud Exemplar in promptu est, unde accusationum capita depromam. Nec si esset, ulterius relegere vacat, aut annotationes marginales, pro ut ibi factum, assignari facere.</p><p>Apud BOeKELIUM iterum agam in tua causa quod potero. Vix tamen praeter verba quidpiam impetrabo. Quid tandem factum hîc fuerit, postmodum significabo. Si Sophiam praevalere Philosophiae non censes, senties. Utra autem Philosophia praeferenda, Platonicane an Aristotelica, tecum non disputo. Inexhausti sunt naturae abyssi. Inde veritatem petamus, non ex argutis disceptationibus, quibus ea saepius amittitur quam recuperatur. An versus et Epigrammata, quae misi, acceperis, non innuis, et iam etiam altum silentium de responsione Daniae ad Germaniam, et de Commentarijs in literas, quas scis, sponte pollicitis et pluribus eiusmodi. Existimarim illud Horatianum etiam apud te locum inventurum, quamvis in aliam sententiam detortum: Coelum non animum mutat, qui trans mare currit. Sed plura non addo. Neque enim plura vacat. Instat meridies, abitum parat Nuncius, animus est impatiens. Tu itaque cum utraque tua SOPHIA quam optime vale. Et, si lubet, actutum rescribe, missis Lubecam literis, quae me postea, sive hîc adsim sive non, facile invenient. Saluta etiam matrem tuam ex me officiose, et CRAGIUM nostrum, si forte apud te est, de quo tamen ambigo. Nam cum Iutus Iuto quidpiam spoponderit, idem reputo, ac si augur auguri.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime valete.<br>Raptim Wandesburgo 9. Septembris Anno 1598.<br>Tuus ex asse TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man pre-eminent as much by the splendour of his birth as by his own virtue, by his eminent learning especially renowned, Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, my most longed-for kinsman, relation, and friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>When, two days ago, I had hurried off certain things to you by a Danish Student who promised he would pass through the places where you dwell in Jutland, it happened that late yesterday I received your letter sent me at Copenhagen on the antepenultimate of August. So at once, sending my courier to Hamburg, I ordered inquiry whether that Student still lingered there. And having learned that he would leave only today, before noon, I again hastily scratch these in answer. If you sent me another letter about a month ago, besides that to which I last replied, know I did not receive it. You now write that you know I received the promised <b>wagon</b>. But whence this? For yours were sent from Copenhagen, and my amanuensis (who a little before was with you) reported it had not even been brought to Lübeck, much less hither — as my letters also intimated. Know therefore, more rightly, that I have <i>not</i> received it. And there is almost nothing now that so delays my journey as this very thing. If it had come to Lübeck, <b>Joachim Buch</b> would at once have informed me, [he being] several times admonished by my letters by this name. If you wish me to obtain it, see it done as quickly as possible; otherwise I must buy another at Hamburg. I know, however, it has not stood by you that this matter proceeds so slowly; and I give the greatest thanks for this service toward me, to be repaid when occasion offers.</p><p><b>About the books — why they have not been presented — you discourse at length.</b> But arguments of that kind find no weight with me — no, nor six hundred weightier than these — inasmuch as I do not so weigh the perverse cavils of adversaries and calumniators, as you, or others there, depressed under their wings, [do], but count them not worth a straw. <b>For I have asserted myself to liberty, which I will defend.</b> When you were here, you thought otherwise, and of your own accord undertook that you would <b>deliver the [royal] books into [the King's] hands</b>, nay, and inculcate certain other things about the <b>[Chancellor's] letters</b> written back to me, the [King] himself perhaps unaware. Then all pleased [you], so much that you re-read and approved some things to be written to the [King]; which, however, I now willingly omitted, lest — after I had once received an unkind reply to those written from Rostock — I be further charged as a suppliant. But to what end now that old, ancestral constancy! Did Heaven fall after you returned to Denmark? O timid minds! <b>I truly pity my former fatherland, that there is almost no one in it (as I hear) who dares ingenuously to profess what is true and just — nay, not even to mutter of these things.</b> Whither these things tend, the outcome will at last show. But would that you may not then grow wise too late, and resume your courage! I should not think that <b>you repent of that Poem which is prefixed to our <i>Astronomia Mechanica</b></i> [the <i>Mechanica</i>], and on that account shirk presenting the books yourself. For it cannot incommode you — [it being] commonly received by usage to attach such things in books against malevolent and ignorant <b>Zoiluses</b>; and you yourself, when present, knew how to excuse it through others (if any barked). Whatever it is — since it is not my custom to retract what is once deliberately decided — <b>the verdict stands, as before: that those books are to be delivered into the [King's] own hands</b>, with whatever face he at last receive them, and however it be twisted or perverted by my malevolent haters. I handle higher things than to take up such humble censures. Yet I should wish it done through you yourself, at least for this cause: that those books should fall to you, not to others, as was discussed between us. But if it cannot be done, <b>let my kinsman Oluf Rosensparre present them — he who also delivered my letters, fruitless and perversely received — so that these books may be, as it were, a Corollary to them, and silent witnesses that I am not ignorant how small a proportion [Denmark] has to the whole surface of the Earth: which could be wholly covered by the tip of the little finger on a two-cubit Terrestrial Globe.</b> And what is to be thought of the immense Heaven — which we revolve in mind, which we comprehend and surpass? O humble cares! Nothing troubles me worse than that I did not prefix to these same books certain poems to sting my calumniators, after the likeness of those I set before certain other copies sent to Princely Men.</p><p>For, that you may know: <b>to the Elector of Cologne, to the Prince of Urania, Moritz [of Hesse], to Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, the King's grandfather, and to Duke Adolf of Holstein, his kinsman — not to speak of Scaliger, a more intelligent appraiser of these — these books were communicated, with the same verses added which I sent prescribed to you.</b> Will they too conclude from this that I make myself a suppliant to them? Will anyone reason thence that I am led by repentance that I dedicated them to the Emperor — to whom, however, they are not yet offered, since I resolved to do this myself? As if it were not the custom to give books dedicated to one King or Prince to others also as a gift. Nor indeed did it ever come into my mind to dedicate any of my works — even had I remained in Denmark — to the [King], since I know him to be <b>unmusical [ἄμουσος] and to shun letters</b> — nay perhaps not to anyone, since the dedication of books, so common, carries something illiberal and a kind of beggary about it, unless it be made to equals, and to those knowing the matters there handled. For that I inscribed the first volume of the <i>Letters</i> to the <b>Landgrave</b> [Wilhelm IV] — to recall the Father's memory and his long friendship with me — was done that those Letters might merit more credence, [there being] nothing supposititious in them; nor did I get anything thence, not even thanks, though that Prince [Moritz] wrote me afterward twice or thrice. Which yet I in no way care for, since I look not to such cheap things. But of these, more than enough — and perhaps too much. You will easily decide, in these and other matters, what is to be done by you. If <b>Rosensparre</b> be unwilling, let <b>Ramel</b> present [them].</p><p>That <b>scurrilous book</b> [of Ursus] which you say you are returning, I have not received. See that I get it in good time; for <b>I have resolved to have the infamous writer himself dealt with at Prague, by law, with the Emperor's leave</b>, and no other copy is at hand from which to draw the heads of accusation. Nor, if there were, is there leisure to re-read it further, or to have marginal annotations assigned, as was done there. With <b>Bökel</b> I will again do in your cause what I can; yet I shall scarcely obtain anything beyond words. What is at last done here, I will signify afterward. If you do not judge that <b>Wisdom [Sophia] prevails over Philosophy, you will feel it</b>. But which Philosophy is to be preferred — Platonic or Aristotelian — I do not dispute with you. Inexhaustible are the abysses of nature. Thence let us seek truth, not from subtle disputations, by which it is more often lost than recovered. Whether you received the verses and Epigrams I sent, you do not indicate; and now too there is deep silence about \"Denmark's response to Germany,\" and about the Commentaries on the letters you know of, voluntarily promised, and more of the kind. I should think that Horatian [line] will find a place even with you, though twisted to another sense: <b>\"He changes his sky, not his mind, who runs across the sea.\"</b> But I add no more, for there is no leisure for more. Noon presses, the Messenger prepares to leave, my mind is impatient. So fare you most well, with both your <b>Sophias</b>. And, if you like, write back at once, sending the letters to Lübeck, which afterward — whether I am here or not — will easily find me. Greet too your mother dutifully from me, and our <b>Krag</b> [Niels Krag], if perchance he is with you (of which, however, I am doubtful). For when a Jutlander has promised a Jutlander anything, I reckon it the same as if [it were] augur to augur.</p><p>Again, fare most happily.<br>In haste from Wandsbek, 9 September 1598.<br>Yours to the last farthing,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> of Rosenholm — Tycho's kinsman (cf. brahe-t_15980925001, the later Wandsbek letter to him).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's defiant insistence on presenting the <i>Mechanica</i> to Christian IV, with a presentation-copy list.</b></li><li><b>The books / the King.</b> Holger had argued at length <i>against</i> presenting Tycho's books (the <i>Mechanica</i>) to <b>King Christian IV</b>, given the court's hostility. Tycho refuses to retract: <b>\"the verdict stands … the books are to be delivered into the King's own hands, with whatever face he receive them.\"</b> He'd prefer Holger to present them (so the gift reverts to Holger); failing that, his kinsman <b>Oluf Rosensparre</b>, or else <b>Henrik Ramel</b>. He frames the gift as \"silent witnesses\" of his cosmic perspective — Denmark being coverable \"by the tip of the little finger on a two-cubit globe.\"</li><li><b>The bitter lament for Denmark.</b> A striking passage: <b>\"I pity my former fatherland, that almost no one in it dares ingenuously profess what is true and just — nor even to mutter of it … O timid minds!\"</b> — and a rebuke of Holger's faltered \"ancestral constancy\" since returning to Denmark, capped by the Horatian <i>\"Caelum non animum mutant qui trans mare currunt\"</i> (Epist. I.11.27).</li><li><b>The <i>Mechanica</i> presentation-copy list (valuable for the distribution record).</b> Tycho names recipients of gift copies of the <i>Mechanica</i>, <b>with the same dedicatory verses</b> he had sent Holger: <b>the Elector of Cologne (Ernst of Bavaria); Moritz of Hesse-Kassel (\"Prince of Urania\"); Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg (Christian IV's grandfather); Duke Adolf of Holstein (his kinsman); and Joseph Scaliger</b> — plus the intended <b>Christian IV</b> copy and the dedication to <b>Rudolf II</b> (not yet offered). A datable cluster of distribution events.</li><li><b>On dedication.</b> Tycho disdains book-dedication as \"illiberal … a kind of beggary\" unless to learned equals — and bluntly calls Christian IV <b>\"unmusical [ἄμουσος] and a shunner of letters.\"</b> He recalls dedicating the <i>Epistolae</i> vol. I to <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b> (out of friendship), getting \"not even thanks\" from his son Moritz.</li><li><b>The Ursus prosecution.</b> Tycho needs the returned copy of <b>Ursus's \"scurrilous book\"</b> because he means to <b>prosecute Ursus at law at Prague, with the Emperor's leave</b> — \"no other copy is at hand from which to draw the heads of accusation.\" (Cf. the Ursus thread.)</li><li><b>Other.</b> The late <b>wagon</b> (via Joachim Buch at Lübeck); the <b>Bökel</b> book-debt (cf. brahe-t_15980925001); the Platonic/Aristotelian aside (\"the abysses of nature are inexhaustible — thence seek truth, not subtle disputation\"); greetings to Holger's <b>wife Sophie</b> (\"both your Sophias\"), his <b>mother</b>, and <b>Niels Krag</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A major addition to the <b>Mechanica distribution layer</b> (the five named gift recipients + verses); pair with brahe-t_15980925001, brahe-t_15980607001 (Otto Brahe copy), and the later Mechanica gifts (Fugger, Moritz, Matthias).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz; <b>Christian IV</b> (bracketed \"[King]\"); the <b>Chancellor</b> (bracketed); <b>Joachim Buch</b>; <b>Oluf Rosensparre</b>; <b>Henrik Ramel</b>; <b>Bökel</b>; <b>Niels Krag</b>; Holger's <b>wife Sophie</b> and <b>mother (Jytte Gyldenstierne)</b>; recipients <b>Elector of Cologne, Moritz of Hesse, Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Duke Adolf of Holstein, Scaliger</b>; <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Ursus</b>. Places: <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Rostock</b>, <b>Jutland</b>, <b>Prague</b>.</li><li><b>Text/calendar notes.</b> The bracketed <i>[Regi]/[Cancellarij]/[Dania]</i> are <b>editorial reconstructions of words the cautious copy left blank</b> (to avoid naming the King in a court-critical letter). <i>Tuus es asse</i> — \"Yours to the last farthing\" (wholly). Dateline <b>9 September 1598</b> at Wandsbek (Holstein O.S.; no <i>stylo</i> marker; label gives no N.S.) — read <b>Old Style</b>.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Closing formula corrected from 'Tuus es asse' to 'Tuus ex asse' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.113 (the classical idiom 'ex asse' = wholly/entirely); the English translation was already correct and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the refusal to retract the <i>Mechanica</i>-presentation to Christian IV; the bitter Denmark lament and Horatian rebuke; the <b>named list of <i>Mechanica</i> gift recipients with dedicatory verses</b>; the disdain of dedication and the \"unmusical King\" jab; the <b>resolve to prosecute Ursus at Prague</b>; the wagon, Bökel, and personal greetings) is firmly construed, with Tycho's combative tone rendered faithfully. Flags: the <b>editorially-bracketed blanked names</b> (King/Chancellor/Denmark) and a couple of copy-corrupt stretches, none affecting the sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the whole letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 110-113, read within the supplied 109-114 slice (p.109 is the tail of a prior Tycho-to-Scaliger letter, p.114 the head of the next Rosenkrantz letter, both checked to confirm the boundary). Verified every name (Joachim Buch, Oluf Rosensparre/Rosenspar, Ramel, Boekel, Niels Krag/Cragius, Moritz/Mauritio 'Prince of Urania', Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Adolf of Holstein, Scaliger), the Horace quotation, and the full body text against Dreyer -- all match exactly, including the bracketed [Regi]/[Cancellarij]/[Rege] editorial supplements Dreyer's own edition inserts. One TEXT_ERROR found in the sign-off: source_text's 'Tuus es asse' should read Dreyer's 'Tuus ex asse'; the English translation is unaffected since it correctly renders the intended idiom regardless. No fabrication, omission, or sense-changing mistranslation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source Latin misprints 'ex' as 'es' in the closing formula; Dreyer p.113 reads 'Tuus ex asse' (the classical idiom for 'wholly/entirely yours'). The published English already renders the correct idiom ('to the last farthing'), so the translation itself is not at fault -- only our stored source_text is corrupted. Dreyer prints: <i>Tuus ex aße</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.113, the letter's closing lines), read visually in the slice PDF. The plate clearly shows 'Tuus ex aße', confirming the audit's claim and Dreyer's own gloss. Only the Latin closing formula was touched; nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 14, "prev": "brahe-t_15980905001", "next": "brahe-t_15980914001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Clarifies that the promised wagon has not arrived in Lübeck, contradicting Holger's assumption, and repeats the request to send it without further delay.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980909001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980910001", "d": "1598-09-10", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": null, "t": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus & Claus Mule → Tycho Brahe — [no place stated], 10 September 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (report) + Latin (sworn testimony) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Genealogisk-heraldisk Selskabs papirer, Specialia: Brahe</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>BERICHT.</p><p>an den herren Tycho Brahen, waß d. Veidt Winßheimb bey d. Johan Böyklen wegen der bücher deß herren Tychoen beuehlicht nach außgerichtet. Eß hat d. Winßheimb alßbaldt nach empfangenem schreiben, darjnnen von dem herren Tychoen dieser sachen gemeldet würdt, sich zu d. Böyklen in der perohn begeben vndt jhnen zu hauße gesucht. Do ehr jhn aber nicht gefunden, darumb ehr d. Böykel hernacher zu d. Winßheimben in seine behaußung sich vorfüget, do jhn der d. Winßheimb deßhalben, wie eß der her Tycho in seinem schreiben angedeutet, angesprochen, worauff d. Böykel diesen bericht vndt antwort vngefehrlichen von sich gegeben, daß ehr Oligero Roßenkrantz auff seine bitte doctoris Pieremanni sehligen bucher auß guttem willen zukommen laßen vor 150 thaler, vnndt wehre solcher kauff vberhaubt vndt in einer summen geschehen; ehr hette auch, wie ehr domalß wehre wegkfehrig geweßen, dem Nicolao Mulaeo solche bücher alle vberantwortet vndt jhm dieselbe außleßen laßen, vndt wehre Rosenkrantz midt denen vndt daß ehr jhm angetzeiget, wie ehr seinem sohne etliche von dießen büchern nach Helmstedt midtgegeben, wohl zufrieden geweßen, wie ehr dan deßen wegen an Rosenkrantz forderlichst selbst schreiben vndt hirjnnen notturftiglich erjnnern wolte. Er hette auch den Wesalium hernacher geschigket, vnangesehen daß Olgar Rosenkrantz solch buch nicht begehrret geweßen, bestehet also entlichen darauff, daß ehr von diesen büchern nichts mehr bey sich habe, vndt solche derhalben nicht von sich geben konne. Welcheß d. Winßheimb, wie es an jhn gelanget, dem herren Tychoen Brahen vnangetzeiget nicht laßen sollen, wubeneben doch d. Böykell anderß mehr zu seiner endtschuldigung angetzogen, welcheß alleß hirmidt anzuhengen von vnnöten erachtet.</p><p>De libris residuis a Bochelio extorquendis die decimo septembris adfui vna cum m. Christiano Longomontano et ex mandato d:nj Tychonis ei contenta literarum Holligeri illud negotium concernentium praelegi, cupiens vt in eadem singula nobis praesentibus se resolueret. Quibus praelectis e vestigio respondit Buchelius, verum quidem fuisse se vendidisse Holligero libros catalogo inscriptos, et liberum ad bibliothecam perlustrandam Holligero aut eius interniuncio fuisse accessum, seque (quod iure iurando per animae salutem aliquoties affirmauit) nihil plane interea e bibliotheca illa clanculum distraxisse, neque aliud responsum extorquere potuimus, etiamsi aliquoties coram institimus aequum iustumque esse, vt catalogo librorum Holligero venditorum omnino satisfieret. De libris Tomae Aquinatis similiter affirmavit aliquot quidem eiusdem reliquia bibliothecae olim interfuisse, sed quo pacto ijdem aut inde distincti aut surrepti se nesciuisse. Deinde manuscriptum librum physicum cum vrgeremus non ut describendum sed ut descriptum Holligero primo fuisse devenditum, ita subiecit, filium suum eundem Brunsuigam secum assumpsisse, eo autem reverso curaret unum aut describi aut eundem librum mitti, tandem cum adhuc vlterius flagitaremus, vt id quod aequum et iustum esset, praestaret, et se atque alios a pluribus liberaret molestijs, respondit, se, si Holligerus libros acceptos restitueret, pecuniam eidem renumeraturum, atque ita nobis acquiescendum fuit, re non vt voluimus confecta. Haec contestamur singuli propria manu subscripta.</p><p>Christianus Longomontanus<br>Claudius Mulenus<br>propria manu.</p>", "en": "<p><b>REPORT.</b></p><p>To Lord Tycho Brahe: what Doctor Veit Winsheim, as commissioned, has accomplished with Doctor Johann Böckel concerning the books in Lord Tycho's matter. Doctor Winsheim, immediately upon receiving the letter in which this matter is mentioned by Lord Tycho, betook himself to Doctor Böckel in person and sought him at [his] house; but, not finding him, Doctor Böckel afterward presented himself at Doctor Winsheim's dwelling, where Doctor Winsheim addressed him on the matter, as Lord Tycho had indicated in his letter. Whereupon Doctor Böckel gave this report and answer, in substance: that he had, at his request, out of good will let <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> have the books of the late Doctor <b>Pieremann</b> for 150 thalers, and that such purchase had been made wholesale and in one [lump] sum; he had also — being then about to travel away — handed over all such books to <b>Nicolaus Mule</b> [Claus Mule] and let him pick them out; and Rosenkrantz had been well content with those, and [content] that he [Böckel] had told him how he had given his son some of these books to take to <b>Helmstedt</b>, as he would write to Rosenkrantz himself about this without delay and duly remind [him] of it. He had also afterward sent the <b>Vesalius</b>, notwithstanding that Holger Rosenkrantz had not asked for that book; he insists, then, finally, that he has nothing more of these books with him, and therefore cannot hand them over. Which Doctor Winsheim ought not to leave unreported to Lord Tycho Brahe, as it reached him — though Doctor Böckel adduced still other things for his excuse, all of which it is deemed needless to append here.</p><p>Concerning the residual books to be recovered from Böckel, on the tenth day of September I was present, together with Master Christian Longomontanus, and by Lord Tycho's mandate I read out to him [Böckel] the contents of Holger's letter touching that business, desiring that he resolve himself on each point, in our presence. These read out, Böckel answered on the spot that it was indeed true he had sold to Holger the books listed in the catalogue, and that free access to survey the library had been [granted] to Holger or his intermediary; and that he (which he several times affirmed by oath, upon his soul's salvation) had plainly purloined nothing secretly from that library in the meantime; nor could we extort any other answer, even though we several times insisted to his face that it was fair and just that the catalogue of books sold to Holger be entirely satisfied. Concerning the books of <b>Thomas Aquinas</b>, he likewise affirmed that some remnants of the same had once been among the library, but how the same were either separated thence or filched, he knew not. Then, when we pressed that the <b>manuscript physics book</b> had first been sold to Holger not as one to be copied but as [one already] copied, he replied thus: that his son had taken the same with him to <b>Brunswick</b>, but on his return he would see to it that one [copy] either be made, or the same book be sent. At last, when we demanded yet further that he perform what was fair and just, and free himself and others from much trouble, he answered that, if Holger would restore the books received, he would repay him the money; and so we had to acquiesce, the matter not concluded as we wished. These things we attest, each subscribed with [his] own hand.</p><p>Christian Longomontanus<br>Claus Mule,<br>with [our] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Senders / form.</b> A two-part <b>report and sworn testimony</b> addressed to Tycho, signed by <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (<i>Christianus Longomontanus</i>) and <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>Claudius Mulenus / Nicolaus Mulaeus</i>), two members of Tycho's <i>familia</i> (cf. brahe-t_16000919001, where \"Claude Muler\" and Longomontanus act as Tycho's couriers). The first part is a <b>German <i>Bericht</b></i> of Doctor Winsheim's dealings; the second a <b>Latin first-person attestation</b> by Mule (with Longomontanus present) of their own confrontation with Böckel on 10 September. The MS is in the Rigsarkivet's <i>Genealogisk-heraldisk Selskab</i> papers.<br>- <b>Significance — the Böckel-books affair (continuation of the Winshemius/Rosenkrantz thread).</b> This directly continues the dispute of <b>brahe-t_15980328001</b> (Tycho→Vitus Winshemius), where Tycho, on behalf of his kinsman <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>, pressed <b>Johann Böckel</b> to hand over the books still owed. The matter: Böckel had sold Holger the library of the late <b>Doctor Pieremann</b> (for <b>150 thalers</b>, wholesale); Holger claims books are missing from the catalogue.<br>- <b>Böckel's defence (as reported).</b><br>  1. The sale was <i>en bloc</i> for 150 thalers; he had handed <b>all</b> the books to <b>Claus Mule</b> to sort, and Rosenkrantz was content (including with Böckel's son having taken some to <b>Helmstedt</b>).<br>  2. He had even sent the <b>Vesalius</b> (the <i>De humani corporis fabrica</i>; cf. brahe-t_15980328001) though Holger had not asked for it.<br>  3. Under the formal questioning of 10 September (Mule reading out Holger's letter), Böckel <b>swore \"upon his soul's salvation\"</b> that he had <b>secretly purloined nothing</b> from the library.<br>  4. On the <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> volumes: some \"remnants\" had once been in the library, but how they were separated or filched, he did not know.<br>  5. On the <b>manuscript physics book</b> (which Holger says was sold to him <i>as already copied</i>, not to be copied): Böckel's <b>son had taken it to Brunswick</b>, and on his return Böckel would have a copy made or send the book.<br>  6. Final offer: if Holger <b>returns the books received, Böckel will repay the money</b> — so the agents \"had to acquiesce, the matter not concluded as we wished.\"<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>; <b>Claus Mule</b> (= Nicolaus Mulaeus); <i>Doctor Veit Winsheim</i> = <b>Vitus Winshemius</b> (the younger), Wittenberg medical professor and Holger's intermediary (cf. brahe-t_15980328001); <i>Doctor Johann Böckel</i> = <b>Johann Bökel</b> (Bocatius/Boccatius, 1535–1605), Hamburg town physician; <i>Oligerus / Holger</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned,\" Tycho's kinsman; <i>Doctor Pieremann</i> (the late owner of the library); Böckel's son (to <b>Helmstedt</b> / <b>Brunswick</b>); <b>Vesalius</b> and <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> (the books in dispute).<br>- <b>Textual / language flags.</b> The German header <i>\"wegen der bücher deß herren Tychoen\"</i> reads \"concerning the books in Lord Tycho's matter\" — Tycho acting <b>on Holger's behalf</b> (the books are Holger's; Tycho is the commissioning principal), per the thread; rendered to keep that sense, with the ambiguity noted. Latin <i>requia</i> (for <i>reliquiae</i>, \"remnants/remains\") and <i>nobus</i> (for <i>nobis</i>) are the copy's slips, read by sense. Chancery-report syntax in both languages is dense; the negations (Böckel \"purloined nothing\") are kept exact.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dated <i>die decimo septembris / 10 September</i> 1598; <b>Old Style</b> (Lutheran north-German/Danish context). No place in the dateline (dataset <i>s.l.</i>); header marked \"[no place stated].\"<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'nobus' corrected to 'nobis', and 'requia' corrected to 'reliquia', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p.147 (translation unaffected in both cases — already correctly rendered).</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German report and the Latin sworn testimony are rendered securely and the substance of the Böckel-books dispute (the Pieremann library sold for 150 thalers; Böckel's oath that he purloined nothing; the Aquinas remnants; the manuscript physics book taken to Brunswick; the repay-if-returned offer) is clear and dovetails with brahe-t_15980328001; graded <b>[B]</b> for the dense bilingual chancery-report syntax, the copy's slips (<i>requia</i>, <i>nobus</i>, read by sense), and the mild ambiguity of the German header phrase (\"the books in Lord Tycho's matter\" — Tycho acting for Holger), all flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.145-148 as page images (the German BERICHT plus its Latin continuation, letter no. 154 in Dreyer's numbering). Collated the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date, and place-name: Winsheim, Böckel/Bochelius/Buchelius, Holger Rosenkrantz, the late Doctor Pieremann, Nicolaus/Claudius Mule(us/Mulenus), the Vesalius, Thomas Aquinas, Brunswick, the sum of 150 thalers, and the date '10 September' all check out against Dreyer. Found two corrupted words in our published Latin (nobus for nobis; requia for reliquia) that qualify as TEXT_ERROR under the grading rule, though neither affects the English translation's meaning. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found in the English itself.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin prints the non-word 'nobus' where Dreyer p.147 prints 'nobis' (dat. of nos); a corrupted word. The English translation correctly follows the sense of 'nobis', so the corruption does not propagate into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>atque ita nobis acquiefcendum fuit, re non vt voluimus confecta.</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin drops 'li' from 'reliquia' ('remnants'), giving the non-word 'requia'. The English ('some remnants of the same') correctly follows Dreyer's actual reading despite the corrupted source_text. Dreyer prints: <i>affirmavit aliquot quidem eiufdem reliquia bibliothecæ olim interfuiffe</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, pp.145-148 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). Both TEXT_ERROR defects were confirmed exactly as flagged and corrected; no other issues were found in the passage.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Genealogisk heraldisk selskabs papirer, specialia: Brahe."], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980324001", "next": "brahe-t_15981231001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980324001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15981231001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Winsheim's inquiry with Böckel about the late Pieremann's books: Böckel had sold them wholesale to Holger Rosenkrantz for 150 thalers and handed them over to Claus Mule.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980910001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980914001", "d": "1598-09-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Hamburg, 14 [September] 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a Danish code-switch) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NATALIUM SPLENDORE AeQUE AC PROPRIA VIRTUTE AC ERUDITIONE INPRIMIS CONSFPICIO VIRO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGUINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTEGERRIMO.</p><p>VIX octiduum elapsum est, Nobilissime et Charissime OLIGERE, ex quo per Studiosum quendam NICOLAUM ALBINUM nomine ad te de rerum mearum statu perscripsi, nunc vero, cum nihil addubitem, quin eae literae perlatae sint, tamen cum inopinata se haec occasio offerret per Nobilissimum Virum FRANCISCUM RANZOVIUM, qui iam e Belgio redux rursus iter isthuc parabat, denuo ad te perscribendum ratus sum, quo de curru, quem me accepisse innueras, te denuo admonerem, ut eum actutum huc procurares, quippe qui maxima ex parte iter meum remoraretur. Lubecam enim eum necdum allatum certo mihi constat. Caeteroquin non intermisisset IOACHIMUS BUCK (pro ut semel atque iterum ab ipso expetij) quin te per literas ilico de eo fecisset certiorem. Rogo itaque etiam atque etiam, ne longiores hac in re morae nectantur, sed ut sequenti ad summum hebdomada huc ad me perferatur (Uden saa er, att I vilde holde Jytske Louff, det er at beholde hvad mand loffuer, saa at en anden faar intet deraff. Da maatte man endda i det mindste lade en vide det, saa man skulle ikke vente det thill forgieffues); nam diutius praestolari non possum. Sed quamvis tum iter ingressus fuero, tamen non ambigo, quin is nihilo serius ad me perveniat, pro ut, antequam discessero, disponam.</p><p>Plura iam non vacat, cum de caeteris in prioribus satis superque tuis responsum existimem.</p><p>Vale mi OLIGERE quam diutissime cum matre tua et coniuge SOPHIA a me meisque diligenter salutatus.<br>Raptim Hamburgi die 14. Septembris Anno 1598.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man conspicuous especially by splendour of birth, as also by his own virtue and erudition, Master Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, kinsman, relative by marriage, and most upright friend.</b></p><p>Scarcely a week [and a day] has elapsed, most noble and dearest Holger, since I wrote to you — through a certain student named Nicolaus Albinus — about the state of my affairs; but now, although I doubt not at all that those letters have been delivered, yet, since this unexpected occasion offered itself through the most noble man Frans Rantzau — who, now returned from Belgium, was again preparing the journey thither — I have judged that I should write to you again, to remind you once more about the wagon which you had hinted you had received, that you procure it hither at once, since it for the most part delays my journey. For I am certain it has not yet been brought to Lübeck. Otherwise Joachim Buck would not have failed (as I sought of him once and again) to make you certain of it at once by letter. I ask, therefore, again and again, that no longer delays be woven in this matter, but that it be conveyed hither to me by next week at the latest <i>(Unless it be that you wish to keep Jutish Law — that is, to keep what one has promised, so that another gets nothing of it. In that case one might, at the very least, let a man know it, so that he should not wait for it in vain)</i>; for I cannot wait any longer. But although I shall by then have set out on my journey, yet I do not doubt that it will reach me none the later, as I shall arrange before I depart.</p><p>There is no more leisure now, since I reckon that enough and more than enough has been answered, about the rest, in [reply to] your earlier [letters].</p><p>Farewell, my Holger, as long as possible, [you] being diligently greeted by me and mine, together with your mother and your wife Sophia.<br>Hastily, at Hamburg, the 14th of September, in the year 1598.<br>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), Tycho's <i>consanguineus, affinis et amicus</i> (kinsman, relative by marriage, and friend); cf. the Cramer/Liddel letters (brahe-t_15980331001, …0524001). His wife <b>Sophia</b> = <b>Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe</b>; his mother is also greeted.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> A homely, impatient note about a <b>wagon (<i>currus</i>)</b> that Rosenkrantz was to procure and forward to Tycho at Hamburg — it is holding up Tycho's onward <b>journey</b> (south, toward the Empire, in his 1597–98 exile). Tycho is sure it has not yet reached <b>Lübeck</b>, since <b>Joachim Buck</b> (his Lübeck contact) would otherwise have written. Couriers: <b>Nicolaus Albinus</b> (a student, the earlier letter) and <b>Frans Rantzau</b> (<i>Franciscus Ranzovius</i>, back from Belgium/the Low Countries and travelling that way again).</li><li><b>⚠ Danish code-switch (kept and translated).</b> Mid-sentence, Tycho drops from Latin into <b>Danish</b> for a proverbial joke: <i>\"Uden saa er, att I vilde holde Jytske Louff, det er at beholde hvad mand loffuer, saa at en anden faar intet deraff. Da maatte man endda i det mindste lade en vide det, saa man skulle ikke vente det till forgieves\"</i> — \"Unless it be that you wish to keep <b>Jutish Law</b> — that is, to keep what one promises, so that another gets nothing of it; [then] one might at least let a man know it, so that he should not wait for it in vain.\" The phrase <i>holde jysk lov</i> (\"keep Jutland law\") is a Danish proverbial pun: to keep for oneself what one has promised to another. Preserved verbatim in the Source block and rendered in italics (per §2/§4); a deliberate bilingual joke between kinsmen, <b>not smoothed away</b>.</li><li><b>⚠ Dateline.</b> <i>Raptim Hamburgi die 14</i> — the dateline gives only \"<b>the 14th</b>,\" <b>without month or year</b>. The dataset (slug …0914… / label \"14. september\") supplies <b>14 September 1598</b>; the internal \"scarcely a week has elapsed since…\" and the Hamburg exile context are consistent. Header dates <b>14 September 1598</b>, the month/year flagged as editorially supplied. Hamburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two restorations against Dreyer (TBOO VIII pp.113-114): removed an unsupported 'tuto ad te' from the opening paragraph (with a matching trim to the translation), and restored the full closing dateline 'die 14. Septembris Anno 1598.', replacing the translation's bracketed guess '[of September]'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin (and the embedded Danish proverb) are rendered faithfully — the <i>jysk lov</i> joke kept verbatim and glossed, not flattened — and the matter (the delayed wagon; Lübeck; Joachim Buck; the couriers Albinus and Frans Rantzau; greetings to Rosenkrantz's mother and wife Sophia) is clear and corpus-consistent. Grade [B] because the <b>dateline lacks its month and year</b> (supplied as 14 September 1598 from the catalogue and flagged), and <i>octiduum</i> is rendered \"a week [and a day]\" (its literal \"eight days\").</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.112-115 as page images. The target letter 'NATALIUM SPLENDORE AeQUE AC PROPRIA VIRTUTE...' runs from p.113 (below the preceding 9 Sept. letter) to p.114, closing 'Raptim Hamburgi die 14. Septembris Anno 1598.' Collated the full body clause by clause: the wagon/Joachim Buck passage, the Danish proverbial parenthetical about 'Jytske Louff' (Jutish Law), and every proper name (Frans Rantzau, Holger/Oliger Rosenkrantz, Sophia) match and are accurately translated. Two TEXT_ERRORs found (an inserted 'tuto ad te' and a truncated closing dateline that drops 'Septembris Anno 1598'), both low severity and not altering the substance of the letter; per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C, consistent with (though for more specific, now-verified reasons than) the pipeline's self-assigned B.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's print (p.113, lines 4-5, the line break falls 'literae/perlatae' with nothing between) lacks 'tuto ad te'; source_text inserts it. The English follows source_text faithfully, so the fault is in source_text, not the translation step. Dreyer prints: <i>quin eae literae perlatae sint [no 'tuto ad te']</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's print (p.114, line 4) gives the full dateline 'die 14. Septembris Anno 1598.'; source_text silently truncates it to 'die 14.', dropping the month and year that Dreyer actually prints in the letter's own closing line. The English compensates with a bracketed '[of September]' guess (consistent with the packet's title, which itself flags the month as bracketed/supplied), but the exact wording Dreyer prints is not reproduced. Dreyer prints: <i>Raptim Hamburgi die 14. Septembris Anno 1598.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.112-115 (full slice, read in one pass); both defect sites confirmed verbatim (p.113 ll.22-24 and p.114 l.4). Both defects fixed with full confidence.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 15, "prev": "brahe-t_15980909001", "next": "brahe-t_15980925001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Writes again through Frans Rantzau to press Holger once more about the promised wagon, which has still not arrived in Lübeck despite repeated requests.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980914001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980914002", "d": "1598-09-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg — Wandsbek, 14 September 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv,<i> Correspondenz des Herzogs Ulrich mit Gelehrten </i>(original letter, in poor condition)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGEN HOCHGEBORNEN FURSTEN VND HERRN, HERRN VLRICHEN HERTZOGEN ZU MECKELNBURGK, FÜRSTEN ZU WENDEN, GRAFENN ZU SCHWERIN, DER LANDE ROSTOCK VND STARGAARD HERRN, MEINEM GNEDIGEN FÜRSTEN VND HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLEUCHTIGER hochgeborner fürst, E.F.G. seindt meine vnterthenige bereitwillige dienste iedertzeit bestes fleißes zuuor. Gnediger furst vnnd herr. Daß E.F.G. mir auff mein junsthin verschinnes monnats julij gegebenes schreibenn dero gnedige antwordt nicht allein vbersendet vnnd ihr mein propositum vnnd furnehmen wollgefallenn lassen, sondern auch so ein herrlich commendationschreiben ahnn die röm. kay. maytt. gnedig communiciret vnnd wiederfahren lassen, darahn haben E.F.G. mir eine besondere gnadt vnnd wolthat bewiesenn, vnnd thue deßhalbenn gegen E.F.G. mich inn aller vnterthenigkeit vnnd zum höchstenn darfur nicht alleine bedanckenn, sondernn erkenne mich auch schuldig, daßelbe vmb E.F.G. nach eusserstem meinem vermugenn iederzeit hinwider inn vntherthenigkeit zuerkennenn.</p><p>Dieweil dan auch ab E.F.G. gnedig ahn mich beschehenen schreibenn ich vermercket, daß E.F.G. jhr mein vberschicktes buch de mechanica astronomia lieb vnndt angenehm sein vnnd in dero furstliche bibliotheck aufhebenn vnd stadt findenn lassen, so ist mir solchs zuuemehmenn gantz erfreulichen gewesen, vndt thue E. F. G. daß ander vonn meiner restitutio affixarum stellarum, welches jch auch höchstgedachter röm. kay. maytt. vnterthenigst dediciret, vnterthenig vberschickenn, worahn jch vber die zwanzig jahrlang nebens andern astronomischen sachen mit hochem fleiß [gearbeit] vnndt zugebracht, also das (ohne ruhm zu [melden) in] dieser zeit nie keiner solchen fleiß vnd [muhe hat an] gewendet, dan diese ... minuto vnnd (wo es von ... [du]rch meine große vnnd ... uiret vnnd nachgerech-... astronomi nur den sechsten theill eines gradus verzeichnet vnnd doch bißweilenn vber einen gantzenn gefeilet habenn, bin aber nicht willens, daßelbe so balt, alweiln es mich viell muhe vnnd vnkost gestandenn, inn druck alß publiciren zulassen, sondern es nur manu scriptum, wie dieses, jhrer kay. maytt. zuofferiren.</p><p>Langet derhalbenn ahn E.F.G. mein vntertheniges fleißiges bitten, E.F.G. wollen solches nichtweniger alß das vorige von mir gnedig auf- vnndt ahnnehmen vnnd inn dero bibliotecam zum gedechtnuß verwahrenn vnnd aufhebenn lassenn, auch iederzeit mein gnediger furst vnnd her, wie bißhero geschehen, sein vnd bleibenn. Jn solchem gegen E.F.G. mein danckbares gemuedt zuerzeigenn, soll vnnd will jch mich iederzeit vngespart meiner geringfuegigen dienstenn eußersten vermugens vnterthenig erfinden lassenn. Thue hiemit E.F.G. sambt dero hertzvielgeliebten gemahlin inn den schutz vnndt schirm Gottes deß allmechtigen zu langer bestendiger [ge]sundtheit vnnd fürstlicher wolfart getreulich befehlen.</p><p>Datum Wandeßburg denn 14. septembris annj 1598.<br>E:F:G: vnterthen[iger vnd] di[enstwilliger<br>Tycho] Brahe<br>mitt eigner handtt</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene, high-born Prince and Lord, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard, my gracious Prince and Lord.</b></p><p>Most serene, high-born Prince — to Your Princely Grace [be] my humble, ready services at all times, with best diligence, beforehand. Gracious Prince and Lord. That Your Princely Grace not only sent me your gracious answer to the letter I gave last, in the past month of July, and were pleased with my purpose and undertaking, but also so graciously communicated and bestowed such a splendid letter of commendation to the Roman Imperial Majesty — therein Your Princely Grace has shown me a special grace and benefit; and I therefore not only thank Your Princely Grace for it, in all humility and most highly, but also acknowledge myself bound to requite the same toward Your Princely Grace, to the utmost of my ability, at all times, in humility.</p><p>And since, too, from Your Princely Grace's gracious letter to me I have noted that Your Princely Grace finds my book <i>de mechanica astronomia</i>, which I sent, dear and agreeable, and lets it be kept and given a place in your princely library — this has been quite a joy for me to learn; and I humbly send Your Princely Grace the other [work], of my <i>restitution of the fixed stars</i>, which I have likewise most humbly dedicated to the aforesaid Roman Imperial Majesty, [and] on which, above twenty years long, alongside other astronomical matters, I have with high diligence [laboured] and spent [my time] — so that (without [boasting] to say it) in this age no one has expended such diligence and [labour]; for these [positions are reckoned] to the minute, and (where it … through my great and … [measured] and recalculated …) whereas [earlier] astronomers noted only the sixth part of a degree, and yet have sometimes erred by a whole one. But I am not willing to let the same be published in print so soon — since it has cost me much labour and expense — but only to offer it in manuscript, like this one, to His Imperial Majesty.</p><p>There comes therefore to Your Princely Grace my humble, diligent request, that Your Princely Grace would, no less than the former [book], graciously receive and accept this from me, and let it be kept and preserved in your library as a memorial, and also be and remain at all times my gracious Prince and Lord, as has hitherto been the case. In showing my grateful heart toward Your Princely Grace herein, I shall and will at all times let myself be found humble, and unsparing of my poor services, to the utmost of my ability. I herewith faithfully commend Your Princely Grace, together with your heartily-beloved consort, to the protection and shelter of God Almighty, for long, constant health and princely welfare.</p><p>Given at Wandsbek, the 14th of September, the year 1598.<br>Your Princely Grace's humble and service-willing<br>[Tycho] Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), full titulature reproduced (as in brahe-t_15880815001, brahe-t_16010415003, brahe-t_16010604001). The MS is the <b>original letter</b>, explicitly <b>in poor condition</b> (<i>i dårlig stand</i>) — hence the lacunae below.<br>- <b>Significance — a double-book presentation (census-relevant).</b> This letter documents Tycho's <b>presentation of two of his Wandsbek-press works to the ducal library at Güstrow</b>, in the exile year 1598:<br>  1. <i>mein vberschicktes buch <b>de mechanica astronomia</b></i> = the <b>_Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica_</b> (Wandsbek 1598), already received and <i>\"given a place in [Ulrich's] princely library.\"</i><br>  2. <i>daß ander vonn meiner <b>restitutio affixarum stellarum</b></i> = the <b>\"restitution of the fixed stars\"</b> — i.e. the <b>fixed-star catalogue</b> (the work of the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i>), here sent as the <i>second</i> gift, <i>\"likewise dedicated to the Roman Imperial Majesty.\"</i><br>  This is a <b>documented presentation/ownership event for a 1598 Wandsbek <i>Mechanica</b></i> (Duke Ulrich III's copy, into the Güstrow <i>Fürstliche Bibliothek</i>) — directly relevant to the Mecklenburg strand of the <i>Mechanica</i>'s documented distribution history.<br>- <b>The star-catalogue and Tycho's accuracy claim.</b> Tycho stresses he is <b>not yet willing to print</b> the star-restitution (it \"cost much labour and expense\") but offers it <i>manu scriptum</i> (\"in manuscript, like this one\") to <b>Rudolf II</b> — the famous practice of presenting the <b>manuscript star catalogue</b> to the Emperor rather than publishing. His accuracy boast: his positions are reckoned <b>\"to the minute,\"</b> whereas earlier astronomers recorded only <b>\"the sixth part of a degree\" (≈ 10 arc-minutes)</b> and \"sometimes erred by a whole degree.\" (Twenty-plus years of labour — the Hven programme.)<br>- <b>Damaged passage (flagged; protocol §4).</b> The witness is physically damaged; the precision-boast sentence is <b>partly lost</b>: <i>\"dan diese … minuto vnnd (wo es von … [du]rch meine große vnnd … uiret vnnd nachgerech-… astronomi nur den sechsten theill eines gradus verzeichnet …\"</i>. The bracketed words ([gearbeit], [melden) in], [muhe hat an], [du]rch, [ge]sundtheit, and the subscription [iger vnd] di[enstwilliger, Tycho]) are the <b>edition's restorations</b> of legible-by-context gaps; the ellipses (…) mark <b>irrecoverable lacunae</b>, left unfilled in the translation (\"[measured]\" supplied tentatively in brackets where sense demands a verb). The general sense (minute-precision vs. earlier astronomers' coarser figures) is secure; the exact wording of the broken clause is not.<br>- <b>Persons / things named.</b> Duke Ulrich III; <b>Rudolf II</b> (<i>röm. kay. Maytt.</i>); the <b>Mechanica</b> and the <b>fixed-star catalogue</b>; Ulrich's <i>hertzvielgeliebte Gemahlin</i> (\"heartily-beloved consort\") — <b>Anna of Pomerania-Wolgast</b>, Ulrich's second wife (cautiously, not asserted from the text). The July 1598 letter Tycho refers to, and Ulrich's commendation to the Emperor, belong to Tycho's exile-period campaign to secure imperial patronage (cf. the intercession letters of 1598).<br>- <b>Register / formulae.</b> Deferential chancery German, subject's first person (<i>E.F.G.</i> = <i>Euer Fürstliche Gnaden</i>); subscription <i>mit eigner Hand</i> (= <i>manu propria</i>); the Latin technical titles (<i>de mechanica astronomia</i>, <i>restitutio affixarum stellarum</i>, <i>manu scriptum</i>) embedded in the German per protocol §2.<br>- <b>Calendar / date crux (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>14. Septembris anni 1598</i> at Wandsbek; <b>Old Style</b>. The dataset `label` (\"10 september\") disagrees with the dateline and the slug (15980914002, the 14th); the <b>label is in error</b>, rejected in favour of the source's own date, per §4.1.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the substance (gratitude for Ulrich's imperial commendation; the Mechanica received into the ducal library; the gift of the manuscript fixed-star catalogue dedicated to Rudolf II; the minute-precision boast; the request to shelve it as a memorial) is clear and important; graded <b>[B]</b> for the <b>physically damaged precision-boast clause</b> (irrecoverable lacunae, left marked not smoothed) and the catalogue-label date error (resolved per §3). The <i>Mechanica</i>-presentation fact is flagged for census cross-reference.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.146-149 as page images (Read pages=1-4). This is a damaged autograph ('Ex originali mutilato'); Dreyer itself brackets the gap-filled words ([gearbeit], [melden), in], [muhe hat an], etc.) and prints ellipses for illegible spans. Collated word-for-word: source_text reproduces Dreyer's brackets and ellipses exactly, and the published English mirrors the same uncertainty with matching brackets ([laboured], [boasting], [measured]) rather than silently asserting lost content as fact. The mechanica astronomia and restitutio affixarum stellarum book references, and the 14 September 1598 dateline, all check out. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found. (Note: published_grade was self-assigned B, presumably out of caution for the damaged manuscript; on collation the letter earns A -- it was in fact checked against Dreyer and the bracketing is faithful throughout.)</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Correspondenz des Herzogs Ulrich mit Gelehrten.. Originalbrev, i dårlig stand"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15970804001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15970804001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Duke Ulrich for his gracious reply and for sending a letter of commendation to the Emperor, and acknowledges the Duke's favorable reception of the Mechanica.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980914002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15980925001", "d": "1598-09-25", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wandsbek, 25 September 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO TAM NATALIUM SPLENDORE QUAM PROPRIA VIRTUTE VIRO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGUINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO SUO DESIDERATISSIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>HAC ipsa hora domum rediens a quodam Holsato Nobili in vicinia habitante, reperi hîc tuas literas, Charissime Domine OLIGERE, et nunc primum ex ijs certior redditus sum, currum diu promissum Lubecam venisse, a Factore autem meo eundem Oldesloviam, pro ut mandaram, translatum percepi, unde crastina die, volente Deo, eundem huc adferri curabo. Mora autem nimia, quae intercessit, undecunque tandem orta, et tot frustra ea de re a me perscriptae literas tam in Daniam quam Lubecam, effecerunt, ut ante triduum alium mihi praeter eum, quem de novo confieri curavi, Hamburgi compararem pretio 50 Ioachimicorum. Ago nihilominus tibi gratias pro tua in me benevola voluntate quam maximas, quod me hoc donare volueris, relaturus per opportunitatem. Et licet illum necdum viderim, spero nihilominus eiusmodi esse, ut usui meo inservire queat. Si quid desideratur, Hamburgi facile supplebitur. Moram illam plus quam Iuticam, si navi cum lignis combustioni idoneis Lubecam transmissa meo naulo, pro ut iam dudum pollicitus es, redimere volueris, et familiae meae muliebri, quam discedens hîc relinquo, pro arcendo hîc frigore ita mature prospicere, id quicquid est, promptius expiabis. Non possum ex hisce tuis, quae 7. Augusti Hafnia datae sunt, colligere, te omnes meas accepisse. Adeo parce rescribis, nec singula a me mota attingis. Quin et semel atque iterum ad te scripsi, ex quo in Cimbriam te redijsse coniectabar. Cuperem, ut ad singulas prompte responderes, nec verborum aut characterum ita tenax esses, ut nunc uxorius alijs forte quam antea distrahare negocijs. Si quid scripseris, mitte saltem hîc Wandesburgum, sive per Lubecam, sive alia via, licet fieri possit, ut tunc hîc praesto non sim. Constitui enim, favente Divina Clementia, post non adeo multos dies iter propositum ingredi; praesertim cum iam currus et caetera (tuae tamen literae me facile invenient) requisita sufficienter in promptu sint.</p><p>Redijt ante quatriduum D. D. VITUS VINSHEMIUS Witteberga, ubi diu abfuerat, et attulit secum fasciculum quendam libellorum de LUTHERI historia cum duabus ad te Epistolis, quas nunc ad te dirigo. Aderant 9 exemplaria eiusdem libelli, quorum octo tibi simul mitto, nonum mihi reservo, ut per otium videam, quae contineat, et an dissona sint ab ijs, quae me olim, cum Lipsiae studerem (ab alio authore, cuius nomen nunc non succurrit, profecta) legisse memini. Variant enim in talibus scriptores, prout affectibus indulgent.</p><p>De caeteris, quae hae ultimae tuae continent, nihil addo. Nec enim moror, qualia apud vos fiant connubia; et de eo, quem tangis, iam diu antea scivi, quantum fidentium ei vel secus, ut non opus sit ex hac novitia affinitate me cautiorem reddi. Invaletudinem alterius non miror; imo potius miror, eum valere posse, qui alios multis modis invalentes reddidit. Viderit, ne ea citius illi eveniant, quam putat, quae iam antea praedixi praedicoque. Sunt, qui ex me audiverunt. Quid BOeKELIUS responderit in tuo negocio de libris non praestitis, ex adiuncta scheda vides. Misi ad eum CLAUDIUM MULIUM cum M. CHRISTIERNO SEVERINI, qui diu mihi fuit domesticus. Nihil ab eo ferme reportamus praeter morosa verba. Si rem vis iure decidi, fiat id per alios. Mihi ob iter instans id non est commodum. Sed suaderem potius, ut id omitteres, et ad Doctorem VITUM VINSHEMIUM, qui tui studiosus haberi vult, serio scriberes, ut is (siquidem pecuniam alteri, priusquam volebas, tradidit) efficeret et urgeret, ut quae tibi revera debentur, obtineas. Miniteris etiam aliquantulum, te actionem contra eum moturum iuridicam, si per se id, quod aequum est, non fecerit. Sic VINSHEMIUS metuens, quod is huic actioni misceri debeat (uti est per se meticulosus) eo validius omnem movebit lapidem, ut tibi tua, quae emisti, nisi quatenus quaedam eorum pro lubitu remittere voles, cedant. Consultissimum iudicarem, ut, siquidem BOeKELIUS sponte obtulerit resolutionem pecuniarum, modo libros reciperet, eos illi prima oportunitate remitteres. Satis enim alias et habes et habere poteris librorum talium. Ego in hoc negocio quod potui feci. Quod autem expectationi utriusque nostrum non respondeat eventus, non est, quod mihi imputes, sed potius BOeKELIJ tenacitati, ne quid dicam amplius. Vix possum in proprijs rebus obtinere, ut Hamburgenses id, quod ius aequumque postulat, libera voluntate efficiant. Tanta est istorum hominum sordities et avaritia insatiabilis, a quibus tamen me post exiguum tempus liberatum iri spero.</p><p>Tu autem mi optime OLIGERE, diu cum nupera coniuge mea nepte a me diligenter salutata ac omnibus tuis quam felicissime vale; et si vacat (debet autem vacare) brevi rescribe.</p><p>Raptim Wandesburgo 25. Septembris 1598.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man — as much by the splendour of his birth as by his own virtue — Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, his most longed-for kinsman, relation-by-marriage, and friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>This very hour, returning home from a certain Holstein nobleman dwelling in the neighbourhood, I found your letter here, dearest Lord Holger; and now first from it I have been informed that <b>the long-promised wagon has come to Lübeck</b> — and I gather from my factor that it has been moved on to Oldesloe, as I had ordered, whence tomorrow, God willing, I will have it brought here. But the excessive delay that intervened — from wherever it at last arose — and the many letters I wrote in vain about it, both into Denmark and to Lübeck, made me, three days ago, <b>buy another at Hamburg for 50 joachimsthalers</b>, besides the one I had had newly built. Nonetheless I give you the greatest thanks for your benevolent goodwill toward me, in that you wished to make me this gift, to be repaid when occasion offers. And although I have not yet seen it, I hope it is of such a kind as can serve my use; if anything is wanting, it will easily be supplied at Hamburg. <b>That more-than-Jutish delay you will the more readily atone for, whatever it amounts to, if you will redeem it by sending a ship with firewood fit for burning to Lübeck at my freight (as you long ago promised), and provide thus early for my womenfolk — whom I leave here on departing — against the cold here.</b> I cannot gather from this letter of yours (dated at Copenhagen 7 August) that you received all of mine: so sparingly do you write back, nor do you touch on the several points I raised. Indeed I have written to you once and again, since I guessed you had returned to Jutland. I could wish you would answer each point promptly, and not be so chary of words or characters — now that, being a married man [<i>uxorius</i>], you are perhaps distracted by other business than before. If you write anything, send it at least here to <b>Wandsbek</b>, whether by Lübeck or another way — though it may happen that I am then not at hand here. For I have resolved, by Divine Clemency favouring, to set out within not very many days on the journey I have proposed — especially since the wagon and the other requisites are now sufficiently ready (your letters, however, will easily find me).</p><p>Four days ago <b>Dr. Veit Winsheim</b> returned from <b>Wittenberg</b>, where he had long been absent, and brought with him a certain bundle of pamphlets <b>on the history of Luther</b>, with two letters to you, which I now forward to you. There were 9 copies of the same booklet, of which I send you eight together; the ninth I reserve for myself, to see at leisure what it contains, and whether it differs from what I remember reading long ago, when I studied at <b>Leipzig</b> (from another author, whose name does not now come to me). For writers vary in such matters, according as they indulge their feelings.</p><p>Of the rest your last letter contains, I add nothing. For I do not concern myself with what marriages are made among you; and as for the man you touch on, I knew long before how far he is to be trusted or not, so that there is no need for me to be made more cautious by this new relationship-by-marriage. The other's ill-health I do not wonder at; nay rather I wonder that he can be well, who has made others unwell in many ways. Let him see to it that the things I foretold before, and foretell [still], do not befall him sooner than he thinks. There are those who have heard it from me. <b>What Bökel replied in your affair, about the books not delivered, you see from the enclosed sheet.</b> I sent to him <b>Claus Mule together with Master Christen Sørensen [Longomontanus]</b>, who was long a member of my household. We bring back from him almost nothing but morose words. If you wish the matter decided at law, let it be done through others; for me, on account of the imminent journey, it is not convenient. But I should rather advise that you drop it, and <b>write seriously to Dr. Veit Winsheim</b> (who wishes to be held a friend of yours), that he — since he handed the money to the other party before you wished — should bring it about and press that you obtain what is truly owed you. Threaten even a little that you will move a legal action against him, if he does not of himself do what is fair. So Winsheim, fearing he must be mixed up in this action (being by nature timorous), will the more strongly move every stone, that your goods, which you bought, may fall to you — except so far as you may willingly remit some of them. I should judge it most prudent that, <b>if Bökel of his own accord has offered a refund of the money provided he take back the books, you return them to him at the first opportunity</b> — for you have, and can have, enough other such books elsewhere. In this business I have done what I could. That the outcome does not answer the expectation of us both is not for you to impute to me, but rather to Bökel's tenacity — to say no more. I can scarcely obtain, even in my own affairs, that the <b>men of Hamburg</b> do of their free will what right and equity demand: so great is the sordidness and insatiable avarice of those men — from whom, however, I hope to be freed after a short time.</p><p>But you, my excellent Holger — long, with your recent wife, my niece, diligently greeted by me — and with all yours, farewell most happily; and if you have leisure (and you ought to have leisure), write back soon.</p><p>In haste from Wandsbek, 25 September 1598.<br>Yours, <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" of Rosenholm (1574–1642), Tycho's kinsman and (now) relation-by-marriage — he had <b>recently married Sophie Brahe, daughter of Tycho's brother Steen Brahe, i.e. Tycho's niece</b> (the closing \"your recent wife, my niece\" fixes this; cf. the sister-vs-niece distinction maintained across the corpus). Tycho twits him as <i>uxorius</i> — a distracted newlywed.</li><li><b>Setting — Wandsbek, on the eve of departure (autumn 1598).</b> Written from <b>Wandsbek (Wandesburgum)</b>, the Holstein estate of Heinrich Rantzau where Tycho lodged after leaving Hven — <i>the very place and season the</i> Mechanica <i>was being printed and finished</i> (cf. the documented production history of the <i>Mechanica</i>). Tycho is about to set out on \"the proposed journey\" (toward Dresden/Wittenberg, ultimately the Emperor), and is putting his household in order: he leaves his <b>womenfolk</b> wintering at Wandsbek and asks Holger to redeem a long delay by <b>shipping firewood to Lübeck at Tycho's freight</b> against the cold.</li><li><b>The wagon.</b> A <i>currus</i> (travelling-wagon/carriage) Holger had promised arrived late at Lübeck → Oldesloe; the delay made Tycho <b>buy another at Hamburg for 50 joachimsthalers</b> (plus one newly built). He thanks Holger for the gift all the same.</li><li><b>Winsheim's Luther pamphlets.</b> <b>Dr. Veit Winsheim</b> (<i>Vitus Vinshemius</i>, the Wittenberg academic; cf. the \"Winshemius's son-in-law\" note at brahe-t_15990507001) returned from Wittenberg with a bundle of <b>booklets on the history of Luther</b> and two letters for Holger; Tycho forwards 8 of 9 copies, keeping one to compare against a different account he had read as a student at <b>Leipzig</b>. His aside — <i>writers vary \"according as they indulge their feelings\"</i> — is a characteristic note on partisan historiography.</li><li><b>The veiled enemy.</b> A cryptic passage on an unnamed man \"who has made others unwell in many ways,\" whose illness Tycho does not pity and to whom he repeats a standing <b>dark prediction</b> (\"there are those who have heard it from me\"). Left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule.</li><li><b>The Bökel book-debt affair.</b> <b>Johann Bökel / Boeckel</b> (the Hamburg physician) had not delivered books in a transaction of Holger's. Tycho sent his men <b>Claus Mule (<i>Claudius Mulius</i>) and Master Christen Sørensen — Longomontanus</b> (<i>M. Christierno Severini</i>, \"long a member of my household\") — to Bökel, getting \"morose words.\" Tycho's advice: drop the suit, instead press <b>Winsheim</b> (who paid the money out prematurely) with a threat of legal action; and if Bökel offers a refund for the returned books, accept. A vivid complaint about <b>Hamburg mercantile \"sordidness and insatiable avarice.\"</b></li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz; <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (his wife, Tycho's niece, unnamed but identified); <b>Veit Winsheim</b> (<i>Vitus Vinshemius</i>); <b>Johann Bökel</b> (<i>Boeckelius</i>, Hamburg); <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>Claudius Mulius</i>); <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (<i>M. Christiernus Severini</i>); the unnamed veiled enemy. Places: <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Oldesloe</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Leipzig</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Jutland (Cimbria)</b>, Denmark.</li><li><b>Notable phrase.</b> <i>Moram illam plus quam Iuticam</i> — \"that <b>more-than-Jutish delay</b>,\" a proverbial jab at proverbial slowness (Tycho had just guessed Holger \"returned to <i>Cimbria</i>,\" Jutland); kept idiomatically and glossed.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Holstein/Denmark were <b>Old Style</b> in 1598; the letter carries no <i>stylo</i> marker, so the dateline <b>25 September 1598</b> is read <b>Old Style</b> (per the catalogue label, which gives no N.S. flag); header left unqualified to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, idiomatic Latin read with confidence; the household logistics (the late wagon, the Hamburg purchase, the firewood-and-womenfolk request, the imminent journey from Wandsbek), Winsheim's Luther pamphlets, and the Bökel book-debt affair (with Mule and <b>Longomontanus</b> as envoys, and the advice to lean on Winsheim) are all firmly construed, and the closing fixes Holger's wife as <b>Sophie Brahe, Tycho's niece</b>. The only deliberately opaque element is Tycho's own veiled allusion to an unnamed enemy, left unidentified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer slice_pdf visually in full (TBOO VIII pp.114-116, the letter runs from the bottom of p.114 through the top of p.116; p.113 and the rest of p.114 belong to a different, earlier Tycho-to-Rosenkrantz letter of 14 Sept 1598 and were correctly excluded). Collated the entire Latin text word-for-word against the page images: opening ('HAC ipsa hora domum rediens...'), the wagon/factor narrative (Lubeca/Oldesloe, 50 Ioachimicorum, three days before), the 7 August Hafnia dating, the Cimbria/uxorius passage, the Vitus Vinshemius/Wittenberg return after four days with 9 copies of the Luther booklet (8 sent, 1 kept), the Boekelius/Claudius Mulius/M. Christierno Severini book dispute, and the closing 'Raptim Wandesburgo 25. Septembris 1598. Tuus TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.' Every proper name, number, sum, and date in the English matches Dreyer's print exactly; the source_text itself is a faithful transcription of Dreyer with no corruption. The bracketed '[Longomontanus]' gloss on 'M. Christierno Severini' is a clearly-marked editorial supplement, ignored per instructions. No fabrications, omissions, mistranslations, hedge losses, or text errors found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 16, "prev": "brahe-t_15980914001", "next": "brahe-t_15981205001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that the long-promised wagon has reached Oldesloe but the delay had forced him to buy a replacement at Hamburg for fifty joachimsthalers three days before.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15980925001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981007001", "d": "1598-10-07", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "David Chytraeus", "ck": "David Chytraeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "David Chytraeus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 7 October 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Prague, University Archive, 62 B 6 (Afskrift); B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DAVID CHYTRAeUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>NOBILIS et Magnifice Domine Patrone colende! Ὅσσον ἔαρ χειμῶνος, ὅσον μᾶλον Βραβύλοιο ἅδιον, tanto mihi gratior et dulcior tua Epistola multis alijs eodem tempore allatis fuit, quam a summo et laudatissimo Mathematum artifice, ac vere (ut Summus et Clarissimus ille Princeps ad Caesaream Mtem de te testatur) patre Astronomorum huius Seculi et unico huius laudatissimae scientiae instauratore scriptam, singulari mihi et liberis meis honori et ornamento esse iudico. Periucunda etiam, adeo benigna et humanissima voluntatis tuae significatio hac in parte fuit, quod exiguum et tenue observantiae erga te huius Academiae officium, tibi Rostochium ingresso exhibitum, quod sine scelere praetermitti non poterat, mentione literarum tuarum non indignum esse censes, tibique non ingratum nec iniucundum accidisse; τούς τε ἀγαμοὺς καὶ ἀντὶ μικρῶν οἴδασθαι χάριν ostendis. Ad Eruditos vero et Claros viros scribens, cum amicorum omnia communia sint, maxime duxi communes amicorum amicos esse oportere. Ideoque de amici praestantissimi Viri statu praesenti, cuius cognoscendi desiderio teneri illos non dubitabam, significandum existimavi, praesertim cum eruditissimae illius et sublimi ingenio tuo accurate elaboratae epistolae (ad quendam Midam his studijs iniquiorem) exemplum ideo te mihi coram dedisse intelligerem, ut cum pluribus etiam alijs amicis communicarem. Quare non solum IACOBO MONAVIO, verum PAULO SCHEDIO MELISSO, etiam Poëtarum nostrae aetatis Principi, et IOHANNI POSTHIO, utriusque Artis Apollineae antistiti, et ABRAHAMO ORTELIO Geographo, quem proximus Mensis Iunius Terris eripuit, et aliquot alijs minus celebribus impertivi legendam; qui animo et voce grata officium meum vicissim praedicarunt. Nobilissimo autem Heroi HENRICO RANZOVIO, sapientia et virtute praestanti, de hac munificentia gratulor, quod caeteris laudibus amplissimis hanc quoque addidit, ut ATLANTI Herculeis laboribus axem stellis ardentibus aptum sustinenti hospitium ex Patria discedenti praeberet, cuius memoriam URANIA ad omnem posteritatem grata recolet et diuturnius quam ullae Pyramides aut Obelisci propagatam celebrabit. Quae URANIA</p><p>Regales animos primum dignata movere,<br>Proxima tangentes Coelo fastigia rerum,<br>nunc etiam invictissimi Imperatoris RUDOLPHI animum flectit, ut patrem Astronomorum huius seculi et unicum laudatissimae huius scientiae instauratorem, in patria Centauris quibusdam ac Midis fastiditum, ad se invitet et Regiam hanc ac coelestem sapientiam Caesareo suo Patrocinio et sumptu excoli et in sublimi illa arce aetheream artem conservari et longe lateque in suo Imperio spargi cupiat, sicut primi CAeSARIS IULIJ, confusam eo tempore Anni Romani rationem restituere molientis, animum movit, ut SOSIGENEM Mathematicum in Aegypti regno minus honorifice a quibusdam habitum, Romam ad se traduceret, ut sublimem illam ac Coelestem siderum doctrinam in sublimi summi Imperatoris terrarum theatro, maiori cum sua gloria et Reip. fructu propagaret. Proficiscere igitur Deo duce et auspice CHRISTO, quo te Augustissimus Imperator et tua τύχη vocat et viri Excellentissimi ac optimi D. D. THADDAeI HAGGAeCIJ, studiorum tuorum socij, et amici coniunctissimi, cum quo annis abhinc triginta Viennae mihi etiam necessitudo intercessit, consuetudine et familiaritate iucundissima, et Reipublicae profutura, felix fruere; et opera, tot annorum vigilijs et curis elaborata, feliciter pertexe. Nec prolixiore Epistola nunc te ad iter accinctum remorari volo, sed Deum ardentibus votis precor, ut te incolumem ac florentem optimis studijs servet et cursum huius viae et vitae totius, ad Dei gloriam et Reipublicae salutem gubernet. Poëmata in tui gratiam scripta ad Daniam, et Germaniae de tali hospite laetantis, et BRASCHIJ nostri RUDOLPHO Caesari et tibi de tuo adventu gratulantis, inprimis vero Epigramma, quod restitutioni Siderum, Duci ULRICO inscriptae, praemisisti, summa cum voluptate legi ac coniunctim ea typis descripta extare et in eruditorum manibus versari, toto pectore opto.</p><p>Bene et feliciter vale.<br>Rhodopoli Nonis Octobris Anno 1598.<br>Mtiam . reverenter colens<br>DAVID CHYTRAeUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>DAVID CHYTRAEUS TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Noble and Magnificent Lord, Patron to be revered! <i>\"As much as spring [is sweeter] than winter, as much as the apple [is] sweeter than the sloe\"</i> — by so much was your Letter more pleasing and sweeter to me than the many others brought at the same time; which, written <b>by the highest and most praised craftsman of the Mathematical arts, and truly (as that Highest and most renowned Prince testifies of you to the Imperial Majesty) the father of the Astronomers of this age and the sole restorer of this most praised science</b>, I judge to be a singular honour and ornament to me and my children. Very pleasant, too — so kind and most humane — was the signification of your goodwill in this respect: that you judge the slight and thin act of respect toward you of this University [Rostock], shown to you on your entering Rostock (which could not without crime be omitted), not unworthy of mention in your letter, and [say it] befell you not unwelcome nor unpleasant; you show [the saying] that <i>\"the good know how to be grateful even for small things.\"</i> But writing to learned and renowned men — since all things of friends are common — I have especially thought that friends' friends ought to be shared; and therefore I judged I should signify [to them] the present state of my most excellent friend, with the desire of knowing which I did not doubt they were held — especially since I understood you had given me, in person, a copy of that most learned letter, accurately elaborated by your sublime genius (<b>to a certain Midas, rather hostile to these studies</b>), for this very reason: that I might share it with several other friends. Wherefore I imparted it to be read not only to <b>Jacob Monau</b>, but to <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b>, the Prince of the Poets of our age, and to <b>Johann Posthius</b>, master of both Apollonian arts [poetry and medicine], and to <b>Abraham Ortelius the Geographer — whom this past June snatched from the earth</b> — and to some others less famous, who with grateful mind and voice in turn proclaimed my service. And to the most noble Hero <b>Henrik Rantzau</b>, excelling in wisdom and virtue, I give congratulation on this munificence: that to his other most ample praises he added this too, that he gave hospitality, to you departing from your fatherland — <b>an Atlas sustaining, by Herculean labours, the axis fitted with burning stars</b> — whose memory Urania, grateful, will cherish to all posterity, and will celebrate, propagated longer than any Pyramids or Obelisks. Which Urania —</p><p><i>who first deigned to move royal minds,</i><br><i>[those] touching the summits of things nearest to Heaven</i> —</p><p>now also bends the mind of the most invincible Emperor <b>Rudolf</b>, that he invites to himself the father of the Astronomers of this age and the sole restorer of this most praised science — <b>disdained in [his] fatherland by certain Centaurs and Midases</b> — and desires that this royal and celestial wisdom be cultivated by his imperial patronage and expense, the ethereal art preserved in that lofty citadel, and spread far and wide through his Empire: <b>just as Urania moved the mind of the first Caesar, Julius</b> — striving to restore the then-confused reckoning of the Roman Year — <b>that he brought over to himself at Rome Sosigenes the Mathematician</b> (held less honourably by some in the kingdom of Egypt), that he might propagate that sublime and celestial doctrine of the stars in the lofty theatre of the highest Emperor of the lands, with greater glory to himself and fruit to the Commonwealth. <b>Set out, therefore, with God as leader and Christ as guide, whither the most august Emperor and your Tyche [Fortune] call you</b>; and enjoy happily — to the Commonwealth's profit — the most pleasant company and familiarity of the most excellent and best man Lord Doctor <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b>, the companion of your studies and most closely-joined friend (with whom, thirty years ago at Vienna, a bond also passed for me); and happily weave to the end the works elaborated by the vigils and cares of so many years. Nor will I now delay you, girded for the journey, with a longer letter, but pray God with ardent vows that he keep you safe and flourishing in your best studies, and govern the course of this journey and of your whole life, to God's glory and the Commonwealth's salvation. The poems written in your honour for Denmark, and [those] of Germany rejoicing over such a guest, and [those] of our <b>Brasch</b> congratulating Rudolf Caesar and you on your coming — but especially the <b>Epigram which you prefixed to the restitution of the Stars, inscribed to Duke Ulrich</b> — I read with the greatest pleasure; and I desire with my whole heart that they be set forth in print together, and circulate in the hands of the learned.</p><p>Fare well and happily.<br>At Rostock, on the Nones of October [7 October], the year 1598.<br>Reverently revering Your Magnificence,<br>David Chytraeus.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender.</b> <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600), the great Rostock theologian and historian — Tycho's friend, and author of the <i>annual history / Saxonia</i> into which Tycho wished his migration-narrative inserted (cf. brahe-t_16010415002, brahe-t_16000920002, brahe-t_16000916002). This is one of <b>Chytraeus's last letters to Tycho</b> (he died in 1600). The MSS are copies at Prague and in Langebek's <i>Diplomatarium</i>.<br>- <b>Significance — a humanist congratulation on the imperial call (autumn 1598).</b> Written as Tycho is \"girded for the journey\" to Bohemia. It is a showpiece of learned encomium, with two load-bearing points:<br>  1. <b>The testimonial quoted.</b> Chytraeus twice calls Tycho <b>\"the father of the Astronomers of this age and the sole restorer of this most praised science,\"</b> explicitly <i>\"as that Highest and most renowned Prince testifies of you to the Imperial Majesty\"</i> — i.e. quoting <b>Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg's commendation of Tycho to Rudolf II</b> (the letter translated at brahe-t_15980816001). A direct cross-reference confirming how Ulrich's praise circulated.<br>  2. <b>The Sosigenes/Julius Caesar parallel (the centrepiece).</b> Just as <b>Julius Caesar</b>, reforming the confused Roman calendar, <b>brought Sosigenes the Alexandrian mathematician</b> (slighted in Egypt) to Rome, so <b>Rudolf II</b> now invites Tycho — \"disdained in his fatherland by certain Centaurs and Midases\" — to cultivate astronomy under imperial patronage. The flattering analogy (Tycho : Rudolf :: Sosigenes : Caesar) places Tycho's transplantation in the grandest historical frame.<br>- <b>The \"anti-Midas\" letter and its circulation.</b> Chytraeus reports sharing a polemical letter of Tycho's <i>\"to a certain Midas, rather hostile to these studies\"</i> (a Danish/learned detractor — \"Midas\" with ass's ears, the type of the philistine) with a roster of the learned: <b>Jacob Monau; Paul Schede Melissus</b> (\"Prince of the Poets\"); <b>Johann Posthius</b> (poet-physician, \"master of both Apollonian arts\"); and <b>Abraham Ortelius the geographer — \"snatched from the earth this past June\" (Ortelius d. 28 June 1598)</b>, a precise dating. He also praises <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> for hosting \"an Atlas sustaining the starry axis\" (Tycho at Wandsbek).<br>- <b>Greek and verse (protocol §4.2).</b><br>  - <i>Ὅσσον ἔαρ χειμῶνος, ὅσον μᾶλον Βραβύλοιο ἅδιον</i> — <b>Theocritus, <i>Idylls</i> V.92–93</b> (\"as spring is sweeter than winter, the apple than the sloe\"); kept verbatim, translated.<br>  - <i>τούς τε ἀγαμοὺς … χάριν</i> — a Greek maxim, \"the good know how to be grateful even for small things\" (the <i>ἀγαμούς</i>/<i>ἀγαθούς</i> reading uncertain; rendered by sense, flagged).<br>  - <i>τύχη</i> — <b>\"Tyche/Fortune,\" a pun on Tycho's name</b> (\"whither the Emperor and your <i>Tyche</i> call you\").<br>  - The hexameter couplet <i>\"Regales animos primum dignata movere…\"</i> (Urania moving royal minds) is kept as verse.<br>- <b>The Duke-Ulrich epigram.</b> Chytraeus singles out the <b>epigram Tycho prefixed to the \"restitution of the Stars,\" inscribed to Duke Ulrich</b> (cf. brahe-t_15980914002, the star-catalogue presented to Ulrich) — and <b>Brasch's</b> congratulatory poems to Rudolf and Tycho — wishing them all printed together.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> David Chytraeus (sender); <b>Duke Ulrich</b> (the \"Prince\" who testified); <b>Jacob Monau</b>, <b>Paul Melissus</b>, <b>Johann Posthius</b>, <b>Abraham Ortelius</b> (d. June 1598); <b>Henrik Rantzau</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Sosigenes</b> and <b>Julius Caesar</b>; <b>Tadeáš Hájek (Hagecius)</b>; <b>Martin Brasch</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar / place (protocol §3).</b> <i>Rhodopoli Nonis Octobris Anno 1598</i> — <i>Rhodopolis</i> (\"City of Roses\") = the learned name for <b>Rostock</b>; <i>Nones of October</i> = <b>7 October</b>. Lutheran Rostock kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — learned humanist Latin (with Greek), read securely; the substance (the congratulation on the imperial call; the quoted \"father of the astronomers\" testimonial from Duke Ulrich; the Sosigenes/Julius-Caesar parallel; the circulation of Tycho's anti-\"Midas\" letter to Monau, Melissus, Posthius, and the late Ortelius; the praise of Henrik Rantzau; the Duke-Ulrich epigram and Brasch's poems) is firmly construed, with the Theocritus quotation, the <i>Tyche</i> pun, and the verse identified/handled per §4. The single uncertain Greek maxim (<i>ἀγαμούς</i>/<i>ἀγαθούς</i>) is rendered by sense and flagged. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.116-117 read as page images. Every proper name (Iacobo Monavio, Paulo Schedio Melisso, Iohanni Posthio, Abrahamo Ortelio, Henrico Ranzovio, Rudolphi, Caesaris Iulij, Sosigenem, Thaddaei Haggaecij, Braschij, Duci Ulrico), the closing date (Nonis Octobris = 7 October 1598), and the 'thirty years ago at Vienna' / 'this past June' details match Dreyer exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found in the Latin or English.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Prag, Universitetets arkiv, 62 B 6. Afskrift", "B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "David Chytraeus", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15970616001", "next": "brahe-t_16000328002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000328002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for his letter, which Chytraeus regards as a singular honour, and praises Tycho's gracious acknowledgment of the University of Rostock's welcome.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981007001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981108001", "d": "1598-11-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Dresden", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany — Dresden, 8 November 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Florence, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento, part 3, vol. 12)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SERENISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO D. FERDINANDO DE MEDICES, MAGNO DUCI HETRURIAe, FLORENTIAe, SENARUM, PISARUM, DOMINO MEO CLEMENTISSIMO.</p><p>SERENISSIME et Illustrissime Princeps, Domine Clementissime. Cum hîc Adolescens genere Nobilis et liberalibus disciplinis praeclare imbutus, qui mihi per aliquot Annos fuit domesticus, in Italiam nunc cogitet, sitque Tuae Serenitati ab Amplissimo et Nobilissimo Domino HENRICO RANZOVIO Produce Cimbrico Affini meo honorando, diligenter commendatus, non intermittendum duxi, quin et ego, licet Serenitati Tuae ignotus, idem submisse praestarem, praesertim ex quo eius mores, integritas et docile ingenium mihi ex diutina consuetudine quam optime perspecta sint. Quocirca Serenitatem Tuam submisse rogatam habeo, ut eum vel meo etiam nomine, sibi de meliori (ut aiunt) nota commendatum habeat. Tradidi illi duos libros, simul ut commodius veherentur colligatos, Serenitati Tuae ex me submisse offerendos: Quorum contenta Tituli et Praefationes expedient: Quos ut clementissimo vultu suscipere, meque suo favore et benevolentia complecti non dedignetur, qua possum submissione oro. Plura de me meisque studijs, atque ad Caesaream Maiestatem e Dania Patria mea migratione ab eodem harum exhibitore (si cognoscere non degravetur) clementissime percipiet. Quocirca Serenitatem Tuam gravissimis Reipub. gubernandae negocijs occupatam ulterius remorari desino. Deus Optimus maximus eam quam diutissime conservet florentem et incolumem.</p><p>Dabantur Dresdae Misniae die 8 Novembris Anni 1598.<br>Serenitati Tuae<br>Submisse addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Serene Prince and Lord, Lord Ferdinando de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany, of Florence, of Siena, of Pisa, my most clement Lord.</b></p><p>Most Serene and Most Illustrious Prince, most clement Lord. Since here a youth — noble by birth and excellently imbued with the liberal disciplines, who was my domestic for some years — now thinks of [going] into Italy, and has been diligently commended to Your Serenity by the most ample and most noble Master Heinrich Rantzau, Cimbrian Viceroy, my honoured kinsman, I judged it ought not to be omitted that I too — though unknown to Your Serenity — should humbly do the same, especially since his character, integrity, and docile talent have been most thoroughly known to me from long acquaintance. Wherefore I have humbly asked Your Serenity to hold him commended, in my name also, \"with the better mark\" (as they say). I have handed him two books — bound together, that they might be the more conveniently carried — to be humbly offered to Your Serenity from me, the contents of which the titles and prefaces will make plain; which [books] I beg, with what submission I can, that you not disdain to receive with most clement countenance, and to embrace me with your favour and benevolence. More about me and my studies, and about my migration from Denmark, my homeland, to the Imperial Majesty, you will most clemently learn from the same bearer of this letter (if you do not find it burdensome to learn it). Wherefore I cease to delay Your Serenity, occupied with the most weighty affairs of governing the commonwealth, any further. May God Best and Greatest preserve Your Serenity, flourishing and unharmed, as long as possible.</p><p>Given at Dresden in Meissen, the 8th day of November, in the year 1598.<br>Most humbly devoted to Your Serenity,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>I wrote with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici</b> (1549–1609), <b>Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (Florence, Siena, Pisa). A letter of recommendation and book-gift. The witness's provenance is notable: the autograph descended into the <b>Galileo / Accademia del Cimento papers</b> at the Biblioteca Nazionale in Florence (Gal. 286).</li><li><b>The young man recommended.</b> \"A youth, noble by birth … my domestic for some years,\" now going to <b>Italy</b>, already commended by <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (the Holstein humanist and Danish <i>Statholder</i>, here <i>Produx Cimbricus</i> = \"Cimbrian Viceroy/Pro-duke,\" i.e. royal governor of the Cimbrian/Jutland–Holstein lands; Tycho's <i>affinis</i>). <i>Identification flagged</i>: most plausibly <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> (who travelled to Italy in this period and returned by early 1600 — cf. brahe-t_16000308002/…0309002), though Tycho does not name him.</li><li><b>The book-gift.</b> <i>duos libros … colligatos</i> = <b>two of Tycho's printed works</b>, bound together for carriage, offered to the Grand Duke (their contents to be known from the <i>titles and prefaces</i>) — almost certainly the recently issued <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (1598) and/or <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596) / <i>Progymnasmata</i>. <i>Identification flagged</i> (the two are not titled).</li><li><b><i>de meliori nota</b></i> — \"with the better mark,\" i.e. in the most favourable terms (the same idiom as in brahe-t_15980530001).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Dresdae Misniae die 8 Novembris 1598</i> = <b>8 November 1598</b>, <b>Dresden in Meissen</b> (Lutheran Saxony) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label. (Tycho was wintering in Saxony — Dresden/Wittenberg — before his move to Bohemia.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the recipient (Grand Duke Ferdinando I), the conduit (Rantzau), and the occasion (a recommendation plus a two-book gift) are clear; grade [B] because neither the <b>recommended youth</b> (probably, but not certainly, Tengnagel) nor the <b>two books</b> is named in the source — both are flagged rather than asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.117-120 (page images, read visually); letter proper located on pp.118-119, text matches Dreyer word for word. Name (Heinrich Rantzau), the number of books (two), and the date (8 November 1598) all verified correct. No defects found; the self-assigned published_grade of B was unnecessarily conservative given the clean collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Bibliotheca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento part 3, vol. 12)."], "th": {"label": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000124001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Commends a former household student traveling to Italy and sends two bound books for the Grand Duke, seconding a recommendation already given by Heinrich Rantzau.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981108001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981112001", "d": "1598-11-12", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Gödelmann", "ck": "Johann Georg Gödelmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Johann Georg Gödelmann → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 12 November 1598 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (with Latin code-switching) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOH. GEORG GOeDELMANN AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine TYCHO. Gunstiger Herr, vnndt vornehmer freundt, Ich habe alhier zu Prag mit freudenn vernommen, daß Ihr, Gottlob, frisch vnndt gesundt zu Dreßdenn ankommen, vnndt weil Ihr daneben zu wissen begeret, ob Ihr euch zu der Kay. Maytt. begebenn, oder euch in der nehe, wegen der eingefallenen sterbens leufft, derowegen die Kay. Maytt. von Prag hinweg getzogenn, aufhaltenn sollet, so kann ich euch darauf nicht bergenn, daß obwoll die Kay. Maytt. ein großes verlangenn (alß die in Astronomischen Sachen, gleich wie ihr Großvater CAROLUS V hoch auch erfahrenn) nach dem Herrn, alß nach dem vornemsten Mathematico totius Mundi, dessen Nomen auch vnsterblichen sein wirdt, hatt, auch den Herrn nit weit vonn sich habenn will, dieweil aber ihr Maytt. wegen der vergifften Seuchenn, welche fast alle örter im Königreich Böheim inficiret, bey sich noch nicht entschlossen, wo sie Ihr Hofflager anstellenn wollenn, alß wirdt der Herr biß auff Ihr Maytt. endtliche weitere anordnung, zu Dreßden, oder Wittemberg, mitlerweil sich auffhaltenn. Thue hiemit vnß Göttlicher Almacht beuehlenn.</p><p>Geben Prag, 12. Novembris. Anno 98.<br>Deß Herrn dienstvilliger<br>JOHAN GEORG GÖDELMAN. D.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Gödelmann to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Magnificent Master Tycho, favourable Sir and distinguished friend. I have learned here at Prague, with joy, that you have, praise God, arrived fresh and well at Dresden. And since you besides desire to know whether you should betake yourself to His Imperial Majesty, or hold yourself in the vicinity — on account of the outbreak of mortality [plague], for the sake of which His Imperial Majesty has withdrawn from Prague — I cannot, thereupon, conceal from you that, although His Imperial Majesty has a great longing (being, just like his grandfather Charles V, also highly versed in astronomical matters) for you, as for the foremost Mathematician <i>of the whole world</i>, whose <i>name</i> too will be immortal, and does not wish to have you far from himself: nevertheless, since His Majesty — on account of the venomous pestilence which has infected almost all places in the Kingdom of Bohemia — has not yet resolved with himself where he will set up his court-residence [<i>Hoflager</i>], the Sir [you] should, until His Majesty's final further arrangement, meanwhile stay at Dresden or Wittenberg. I herewith commend us to God's Almighty [protection].</p><p>Given at Prague, the 12th of November, in the year [15]98.<br>Your service-ready,<br>Johann Georg Gödelmann, D[octor].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (1559–1611), jurist, Saxon privy councillor and diplomat (and author of a celebrated treatise on witchcraft); here writing from Prague as a court-contact, advising Tycho on the timing of his approach to the Emperor.</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Tycho, having reached <b>Dresden</b> on his way south in the autumn of 1598, asked whether to proceed to the Emperor or wait. Gödelmann reports that <b>plague</b> (<i>sterbens leufft</i> / <i>vergiffte Seuche</i>) had driven <b>Rudolf II</b> from Prague and left the court without a fixed seat; he advises Tycho to <b>stay at Dresden or Wittenberg</b> until the Emperor settles his <i>Hoflager</i> (court-residence). (Tycho did winter at Wittenberg — cf. brahe-t_15990110001, …0111001, dated there Jan. 1599 — before reaching Bohemia in 1599.)</li><li><b><i>ihr Großvater Carolus V</b></i> — \"his grandfather Charles V\": genealogically <b>correct</b>, not a slip — Rudolf II's mother was <b>Maria of Austria</b>, daughter of Emperor <b>Charles V</b>, so Charles was Rudolf's maternal grandfather. Gödelmann flatters the Emperor's astronomical bent as inherited.</li><li><b>Code-switching (kept and italicised).</b> Latin technical/honorific nouns embedded in the German: <i>Mathematico totius Mundi</i> (\"Mathematician of the whole world\"), <i>Nomen</i> (\"name\"), <i>inficiret</i> (\"infected\"). Rendered into English but flagged as the writer's own Latin within the German (per §2). Princely titulature (<i>Kay. Maytt.</i>, <i>Ihr Hofflager</i>) preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Geben Prag, 12. Novembris. Anno 98</i> = <b>12 November 1598</b>; Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two single-word transcription corrections against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.118: 'uber'→'aber' and 'sic'→'sie'. Neither affected the published English, which already carried the correct sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German (with its Latin insets) is rendered securely; the situation (the 1598 plague flight of the court; the advice to winter at Dresden/Wittenberg) is corpus-corroborated, and the \"grandfather Charles V\" is verified as historically accurate. No ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII p.118 (Dreyer, 'Ioh. Georg Goedelmann ad Tychonem Brahe') as a page image; letter entire on this page. Full word-by-word collation of the German source_text against Dreyer found two single-word corruptions ('uber' for 'aber'; 'sic' for 'sie') — genuine TEXT_ERROR departures from the edition of record, though neither changes the sense a scholar would take away, and neither propagated into the English translation, which is otherwise faithful throughout (titles, Charles V reference, the plague/Hofflager passage, dateline '12. Novembris. Anno 98', signature). Per the brief's rule that any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade, this is graded C despite the defects being low severity and the translation itself being clean.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published German source_text reads 'uber' where Dreyer prints 'aber' (the adversative conjunction 'however/nevertheless'); a genuine word substitution against the edition of record. The English translation happens to render the correct sense ('nevertheless, since His Majesty...'), so the corruption did not propagate, but the source_text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>dieweil aber ihr Maytt. wegen der vergifften Seuchenn ...</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published German source_text reads 'sic' where Dreyer prints 'sie' (the pronoun referring back to 'ihr Maytt.'). 'sic' is not a German word here and is a transcription corruption against Dreyer. Again the English translation ('he will set up his court-residence') reflects the correct sense, so this is a source-text fidelity defect, not a translation defect. Dreyer prints: <i>bey sich noch nicht entschlossen, wo sie Ihr Hofflager anstellenn wollenn</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.118; the entire letter fits on a single page and the readings are unambiguous. Only the two flagged words were touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that the Emperor desires Tycho's presence but has not settled a court residence due to the plague, and advises waiting for Rudolf's final arrangement.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981112001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981128001", "d": "1598-11-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Dresden", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Giovanni Antonio Magini — Dresden, 28 November 1598 [Old Style]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Note.</b> The <b>first</b> Tycho-to-Magini letter — carrying <b>Tengnagel</b> and the <b>gift of the <i>Mechanica</i> + the 1000-star catalogue</b>. Written from <b>Dresden</b> during the 1598 journey to Bohemia, waiting out the Bohemian plague.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IOHANNI ANTONIO MAGINO MATHEMATUM IN CELEBRI BONONIENSIUM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI EXIMIO ET AMICO SUO INPRIMIS DILECTO.</p><p>Clarissime et Excellentissime Vir.</p><p>CUM plures iam elapsi sint anni, atque uti existimo ad minimum quinque, ex quo quidam inter meos domesticos studiosos eruditum tuum librum quem Tabulam Tetragonicam vocas, mihique dedicasti, Hafnia e nundinis Francofurtensibus istuc per bibliopolam nostratem allatum, Uraniburgum secum attulerit, idque uno vel altero anno postquam a te evulgatus esset (neque enim ullum a teipso accepi exemplar, sive nullum miseris, sive quod prae nimia viarum intercapedine interciderit) ego vero postquam illud exemplum, quod studiosus meus asportavit, inspexissem et diligentius perlustrassem, mihi admodum placuit, utut nonnulla in eo sint, in quibus tibiipsi forte non satis pro hac prima conceptione satisfeceris, nec in magna numerorum copia processus sit sufficienter expeditus, praeterquam quod id de quo eius usum pro me praecipue infers, circa aequationum nimirum angulos in Planetis facilius, quam per Tangentium et Secantium Tabulas inde colligendos, mihi qua ratione id fieri posset, necdum perspectum, siquidem angulos illos per solas numerorum προσθαφαιρέσεις, nempe additionem et subtractionem absque omni multiplicatione aut divisione minimo negocio investigare liceat; quam πραγματείαν non saltem in omni genere triangulorum sphaericorum, sed et planorum magno Astronomici calculi compendio locum mereri nobis constat; laude nihilominus dignissimum censui et etiamnum censeo hunc tuum laborem in componenda illa tabula cum suo usu, qui sane late patet et multiplex esse potest. Quod autem non antea tibi de his rescripserim, factum est, quod diu a teipso expectarem exemplar aliquod mihi destinatum, quod alicubi in itinere haerere putavi, ut rescribendo gratus vicissim esse possem, partim quod certum et fidelem internuncium non repererim, qui tibi tuto quippiam a me perferret.</p><p>Quae vero et tuo et aliorum nomine in epistola illic praefixa rogas, videlicet ut Astronomiae instaurationem, quam diu multumque molior, mature absolvam et publici iuris faciam, id equidem ut secundum tua et multorum vota praestarem, nihil magis ipsemet in votis habui: sed obstiterunt hactenus quaedam, partim proprie ad artem redintegrandam stabiliendamque facientia, partim ex huius inferioris et terrestris mundi tumultuaria scena prodeuntia, et politicis fatis obnoxia.</p><p>Prius illud tarditas circuituum superiorum Planetarum, praesertim ♄i causatur, quo minus sufficientes acronychias observationes per totum circuli ambitum citius adipisci concederetur; neque enim tres sufficiunt ad eorum Apogaea et Eccentricitates explicandas, uti imitatione PTOLEMAeI et COPERNICI hactenus frustra creditum est, inaequalitate adhuc alia, quam eorum ferunt hypotheses, coincidente, et Eccentricitate illa, quae Aequanti tribuitur, non retinente eandem ad alteram proportionem, quam illi assumunt et pro cognito praesupponunt potius quam demonstrant; aliter enim ea se habet et in singulis variat. Taceo nunc, quantum difficultatis ingerant, quantumque temporis requirant reliqui duo inferiores Planetae, inprimis vero vagabundus et varijs ambagibus implicitus ☿, rarissimeque apparens, cum alias circa horizontem, ubi hi duo observandi ut plurimum veniunt, pauca admodum concedatur aëris satis pura serenitas, ob vapores istic obvolantes, decliviore etiam Sphaera aliquid obstaculi suggerente. Quin et ☾, utut singulis mensibus recurrat, non parum suis anfractuosis et pluribus, quam hactenus creditum est, inaequalitatibus obnoxia nos torsit, donec non ex tribus solummodo Eclipsibus haud sufficientibus, uti antecessores, eius motiones scrutaremur, sed sexies ternis aut pluribus eosdem eruere, ut alia praetereantur, necesse habuerimus.</p><p>Alterum obstaculum, quod politicum dixi, quodque per se ingratum, meam intentionem studiumque in hac arte redintegranda alias indefessum multifariam exagitavit turbavitque inde a defuncto laudatissimae memoriae Rege Danorum FRIDERICO Domino et fautore meo clementissimo, qui me meaque studia, quoad vixit, clementer et benigne iuvit et promovit: in interregno octennali frigidius ijs, quae requirebantur, prospectis, donec tandem creato hoc novo Rege adolescente, qui, sive ob aetatem iuniorem, sive dissuadentibus, aut minus adhortantibus dynastis, utut alias heroico sit animo et ingenio acri praeditus, ac iudicio pro hac aetate plus quam credi posset valens, huius sublimis scientiae in suo regno promotioni attendere non duxit operaepretium, sed per Cancellarium literis a me, quod subsecuturum esset, discrete praemonitum, mihi responderi permiserit, sibi non esse integrum in Astronomici exercitij Instrumentorumque eo pertinentium sustentationem quippiam impendere, subtractis etiam successive, statim ac coronatus est, sumptibus ab optimo Parente et Regni consiliarijs mihi concessis et eo deputatis, non curantibus ijs qui Regi sunt ab intimis, Senatus Consultum mox ab obitu FRIDERICI Regis mihi in membrana impertitum, et a Primario eorum qui nunc Curiae Magister est, suapte manu subscriptum et sigillo munitum, sic quod alias insolitum est, exauthorari fecit. Unde factum est, supervenientibus etiam alijs molestationibus, ut priusquam Astronomiae, cuius tam diu tamque impense reaedificationem in Patria molitus sum, ruinam ibidem indecoram hisce oculis spectarem, multo potius maluerim ipsam Patriam cum omnibus in ea consanguineis et amicis derelinquere atque in Germaniam me cum Coniuge et liberis totaque mea familia, assumpto mecum ingenti illo apparatu Astronomico, concedere, quod ante sesquialterum annum, si forte adhuc inscius es, praesenti et forti animo a me factum est. Substiti tamen interea cum meis iuxta fines Daniae et Germaniae in arce quadam Ranzoviana prope Hamburgum sita, quo nostratibus rem rectius dispicienda Patriae, cui multum debemus, adhuc studens, satis temporis relinquerem, atque eo forem excusabilior, si alio me prorsus converterem. Verum cum id nihilominus incassum fieret, et Augustissimus Romanorum Imperator RUDOLPHUS resciscens me e Dania emigrasse atque ad Holsatiae australiorem oram habitare, me pro ea qua est erga Mathematicas artes, quas ipsemet etiam laudabiliter exercet, Clementissimo favore praeditus, ad se in Bohemiam evocari mandavit, spe facta quod non frustra venturus essem. Sum itaque iam nunc in itinere, et hic Dresdae aliquantisper cunctor, donec missis in aulam Caesaris literis edocear, ubi et quando eius Maiestas adeunda sit, hoc praesertim tempore, cum ob luem epidemicam passim istic grassantem turbata sint omnia, et Caesar Praga in alium salubriorem locum, ubi solitarie agit, discesserit. Responsum est Caesarem accessum meum libenter intellexisse; sed cum ipsemet in Bohemia vix locum inveniat, ubi a periculo contagij increbrescentis tutus sit, suadere et cupere, ut aliquamdiu hîc aut alibi in vicinia me contineam, donec remiserint istic morbi populariter grassantes; interea velle se de commoda et opportuna sede cogitare atque prospicere, ubi me meaque collocet. Huic clementissimo Caesaris responso, uti par est, acquiescens, constitui, volente supremo Numine, in vicinam aliquam superioris Saxoniae vel Misniae Academiam hisce diebus me conferre, ut aliqua in Progymnasmatis meis alijsque operibus typographico labore necdum plane ob eas, quae mihi in Patria evenerunt, conturbationes absoluta, ibi compleri procurem, donec pacatiore et saniore in Bohemia restituta degendi commoditate a Caesare eo accedere iubear. Quod tibi indicandum duxi, optime MAGINE, ut de statu mearum rerum conscius esses, utque simul ex iam commemoratis aliquatenus intelligeres, quae et quanta Astronomiae, quam intendo, instaurationem remorata sint. Plura et particulariora exhibitor harum FRANCISCUS TIGNAGLIUS nomine tibi seorsim aperiet. Est ille adolescens genere nobilis, natione Germanus, qui mihi usque in tertium annum fuit domesticus, meaeque e Dania migrationis comes, estque artibus liberalibus egregie imbutus, et Mathematicis etiam, interea dum mecum fuit, operam non contemnendam navavit, observationibus simul assuefactus et numerorum tractationi, alias per se apprime ingeniosus et disciplinis quibusvis capessendis idoneus, moribus insuper integris et virtute praeditus, ideoque ob tam praeclaras animi dotes mihi semper carissimus fuit, et filij loco habitus. Hunc igitur de meliori, uti dicunt, nota tibi commendatum habeas, te etiam atque etiam rogo, quicquid in illum contuleris officij et beneficii, eum insuper apud Illustres et praestantes istic viros commendatione tua promovendo, id totum non tam ipsi quam mihi te praestitisse scias, inveniesque me ad compensandum perpetuo pro virili quam paratissimum. Tradidi illi librum quendam tibi ex me donandum, qui duos in se commodioris vectionis causa complectitur: prior Mechanicam Astronomiae artem, prout a nobis in usu hactenus fuit, exhibet: posterior stellas fixas millenas accuratissime restitutas labore diutino atque ingenti, impendioque haud mediocri. Hoc habe librarium vicissim munus pro libro mihi prius inscripto, donec plura ex meis quae desideras chalcographico labore penitus absoluta fuerint. Sunt illi quaedam etiam alia ad manus, quatenus in hac mea peregrinatione in promptu erant, quae simul monstrabit, et de pluribus me meaque studia concernentibus te edocebit. Spero me ubi commodius et durabilius ocium in Germania adeptus fuero, tuis et aliorum votis in Astronomiae illustratione et locupletatione consumanda uberius satisfacturum.</p><p>Inserui priori libro circa calcem epistolas tuas ad me datas, cum spacium pro ijs et Curtianis vacaret, idque certis de causis, praesertim ut alios ad haec studia amanda excolendaque excitarem: et licet te inconsulto hoc fecerim, tamen haud ingratum fore confido, siquidem tibi, ut existimo, id non sit inhonorificum. Observationes quas polliceris in altera Epistola, si quas interea peregistis, obtinere averem. Sin vero id hactenus neglectum est, ut in posterum sedulo resarciatur et praestetur, rogo; cedet id in artis nostrae, veluti spero, emolumentum, eritque et tibi et alijs, qui hoc factitarint, laudabile. Instrumenta si conficere libuerit, liber is formam et rationem luculenter suppeditabit.</p><p>Plura nunc non addo, forsan in iam exaratis epistolae metas excessi, quod tamen libenter amicitiae nostrae et studiorum communium causa admisi, locis etiam quibus disiungimur crebras interlocutiones non admittentibus, ideoque, ubi fieri potest, non abbreviandis. Sique plura hîc forte omissa scire desideras, is, quem tibi nunc enixe commendavi adolescens, cum rerum mearum haud sit ignarus, plura expediet, atque votis tuis hac in parte, uti mihi polliceor, satisfaciet. Eum itaque iterum atque iterum tibi acceptum habe, et ad meliora quaeque, quantum in te est, istic iuva et promove, quo aliquando, favente Numine, ad me reversus, tuam in se benevolentiam merito praedicare possit, idque erit mihi longe gratissimum.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Dresdae Misniae, ubi est aula Electorum Saxoniae, Novembris 28 stilo veteri. Anno etc. 1598.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 124–125; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si tres Eclipses huius anni istic a te vel quopiam alio observatae sunt, fac me eius denotationis participem pro meridianorum differentia hinc elicienda. Meis instrumentis sic designatae sunt, ut momenta observata et ad medium earundem reducta, ita se habuerint. A° 1598. Feb. d. 20. Eclipsis [Lunae] medium 17h 58' P.M. Vandesburgi prope Hamburgum, defecerunt II digiti de [Luna]. Martij d. 6. Eclipsis ☉is medium 11h 5' A.. N. Uraniburgi in Dania. Magnitudo Eclipsationis ibidem apparuit a parte superiori intra 9 et 10 digitos. Augusti d. 16. Eclipsis [Lunae] medium 7h 42' P.M. Vandesburgi. Haec omnia tempora recte consentiunt cum nostra restitutione. Quantum vero a calculo tam Alphonsino quam Coperniceo discrepent, tute vide. Longitudinem Vandesburgi praesuppono 33° 35'. Uraniburgi 36° 45' atque eandem proxime Venetiarum.</p><p>Referet etiam idem adolescens quædam ad te meo nomine secreto, in<br>quibus si mihi tu operam tuam præstiteris, aut per alium istic hominem idoneum,<br>rem longe acceptissimam præstiteris, et ubi de tua voluntate mihi per eundem<br>innotuerit, pluribus instruam, quomodo res peragenda erit, et formam præscribam.<br>Habeo certas causas cur potius in Italia quam Germania id fieri averem. Multum<br>mea interest, ut hoc perficiatur, quod et levi negocio exequi licebit; impensas<br>lubens solvam inveniarque vicissim gratus. Habet exhibitor harum quædam secum<br>eius negocij documenta, quæ tibi clam monstrare et exponere poterit.</p><p>Tuæ Excellentiæ<br>Studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu<br>propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and excellent man, Lord Giovanni Antonio Magini, eminent Professor of Mathematics in the celebrated Gymnasium of Bologna, and his especially beloved friend.</b></p><p>Most renowned and excellent Man.</p><p>Since several years have now elapsed — at the least five, as I reckon — from when one of my household students brought to Uraniborg <b>your erudite book, which you call the <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>, and dedicated to me</b> (carried from Copenhagen, from the Frankfurt fair, thither by a bookseller of ours), and that one or two years after it had been published by you — for I received no copy from you yourself, whether you sent none, or whether it perished through the over-great stretch of the roads — I, when I had inspected and more diligently perused that copy which my student carried off, found it much to my liking; although there are some things in it in which you perhaps did not fully satisfy yourself, for this first conception, and the process is not sufficiently expedited in a great supply of numbers. Besides, that for which you chiefly infer its use for me — namely, the deriving thence of the angles of the equations in the Planets more easily than through Tables of Tangents and Secants — I have not yet seen by what method that could be done, <b>since those angles may be investigated, with least trouble, by the sole prosthaphaereses [προσθαφαιρέσεις] of numbers — namely addition and subtraction, without any multiplication or division</b>; which method [πραγματεία] we are sure merits a place, not only in every kind of spherical triangles, but of plane ones too, with great compendium of astronomical calculation. Nonetheless I judged, and still judge, this labour of yours, in composing that table, with its use (which is indeed broadly open and can be manifold), most worthy of praise. That I did not write back to you about these before, came about because I long expected from you yourself some copy destined for me (which I thought stuck somewhere on the road), that I might be grateful in return by writing back; partly because I did not find a sure and faithful messenger to carry anything from me to you safely.</p><p><b>As to what you ask, both in your own name and others', in the epistle prefixed there — namely, that I mature and make public the restoration of Astronomy which I long and much labour at — I myself wished nothing more than to perform this, according to your wishes and those of many; but certain things have hitherto stood in the way</b> — partly things properly making to the restoring and establishing of the art, partly things proceeding from the tumultuous scene of this lower and earthly world, and subject to political fates.</p><p>The first [obstacle] is caused by <b>the slowness of the circuits of the superior Planets, especially Saturn [♄]</b>, whereby less leave is granted of getting sufficient acronychal [opposition] observations through the whole compass of the circle sooner; <b>for three do not suffice to explicate their Apogees and Eccentricities</b> (as has hitherto been vainly believed, in imitation of Ptolemy and Copernicus) — another inequality, than their hypotheses bear, coinciding, and that Eccentricity which is assigned to the Equant not retaining the same proportion to the other, which they assume and presuppose as known, rather than demonstrate; for it is otherwise, and varies in each [case]. I am silent now how much difficulty they introduce, and how much time they require, the remaining two inferior Planets — especially <b>the wandering Mercury [☿], implicated in various windings, and most rarely appearing</b> — since otherwise, around the horizon (where these two for the most part come to be observed), very little sufficiently pure clear air is granted, on account of the vapours flying about there, the more declining Sphere also offering some obstacle. Nay, <b>the Moon [☾] too, though it recurs each month, has tortured us not a little by its tortuous inequalities — more than has hitherto been believed — until we had to investigate its motions not from only three insufficient Eclipses (like our predecessors), but to draw the same out from six-times-three [18] or more</b> (to pass over other things).</p><p><b>The other obstacle, which I called political, and which is in itself ungrateful, variously agitated and disturbed my intention and study in restoring this art (otherwise untiring) ever since the death of the King of the Danes, Frederick, of most praised memory, my most clement Lord and patron</b> — who, while he lived, clemently and benignly helped and promoted me and my studies. <b>In the eight-year interregnum [regency], the things that were required were more coldly provided</b> — until at last, this new King, a youth, being created [crowned], who (whether on account of his younger age, or with dynasts dissuading or less encouraging — though otherwise he be of heroic mind, and endowed with a sharp wit, and strong in judgement, for this age, more than could be believed) <b>did not think it worthwhile to attend to the promotion of this sublime science in his realm</b>; but, through the Chancellor (I having discreetly forewarned by letter what would follow), permitted me to be answered <b>that it was not in his power to spend anything on the maintenance of the Astronomical exercise and of the instruments pertaining to it</b> — the costs granted me, and assigned thereto, by the best Parent [Frederick] and the Realm's councillors being also successively withdrawn, as soon as he was crowned (those who are the King's intimates not caring); and <b>the Senate-decree given me on parchment immediately after King Frederick's death — subscribed in the hand of the chief of them (who is now Court-Master), and secured with a seal — he caused to be annulled</b> (a thing otherwise unusual). Whence it came about — other molestations supervening too — that, <b>before I should behold with these eyes the unseemly ruin, in that same place, of the Astronomy whose rebuilding I had so long and so intensely laboured at in my fatherland, I much preferred to abandon the fatherland itself, with all my blood-kin and friends in it, and to withdraw into Germany with my wife and children and whole household, taking that huge Astronomical apparatus with me</b> — which (if perhaps you are still unaware) was done by me a year and a half ago, with present and brave mind. Yet I stopped meanwhile, with my [household], <b>near the borders of Denmark and Germany, in a certain Rantzau castle situated near Hamburg</b>, to leave my countrymen, while still studying the fatherland's matter (to which I owe much), time enough to consider it more rightly, and to be the more excusable if I should turn entirely elsewhere. But since this nonetheless came to nothing, and <b>the most august Roman Emperor Rudolf, learning that I had emigrated from Denmark and dwelt on Holstein's southern shore, endowed (as he is) with most clement favour toward the Mathematical arts (which he himself too laudably practises), commanded me to be called to him into Bohemia</b>, with hope given that I should not come in vain. So <b>I am now on the journey, and linger a while here at Dresden</b>, until — letters being sent to the Emperor's court — I am informed where and when His Majesty is to be approached; especially at this time, when on account of the epidemic plague raging there everywhere all things are disturbed, and the Emperor has withdrawn from Prague into another, healthier place, where he lives solitarily. The answer was that the Emperor gladly heard of my coming; but since he himself scarcely finds a place in Bohemia where he is safe from the danger of the increasing contagion, [he] advises and wishes that I keep myself a while here, or elsewhere in the neighbourhood, until the diseases raging popularly there abate; meanwhile he will think of, and provide, a convenient and opportune seat where to place me and my [things]. Acquiescing, as is fitting, in this most clement response of the Emperor's, I have resolved, the supreme Godhead willing, <b>to betake myself these days to some nearby Academy of upper Saxony or Meissen, that I may there have certain things in my <i>Progymnasmata</i> and other works — not yet quite finished in the printing-labour, on account of the disturbances that befell me in the fatherland — completed</b>, until I am ordered by the Emperor to go thither, when a more peaceful and healthful convenience of living is restored in Bohemia. Which I judged should be indicated to you, excellent Magini, that you might be aware of the state of my affairs, and at the same time understand somewhat, from what has now been recounted, what and how great [things] have delayed the restoration of Astronomy which I intend. <b>More, and more particular [things], the bearer of these, Franz Tengnagel by name, will open to you separately.</b> He is a youth noble by birth, German by nation, who was my household-member up to the third year, and the companion of my migration from Denmark; and he is excellently imbued with the liberal arts, and in Mathematics too (while he was with me) bestowed no contemptible labour — habituated at once to observations and to the handling of numbers — otherwise of himself most ingenious, and apt for grasping any disciplines, endowed besides with integral morals and virtue; and so, for such distinguished gifts of mind, he was always most dear to me, and held in a son's place. <b>Him, then, hold commended to you \"from the better mark,\" as they say</b>; I ask you again and again [that], whatever of office and benefit you confer on him — promoting him besides, by your commendation, among the Illustrious and distinguished men there — you would know you have rendered all that not so much to him as to me; and you will find me most ready perpetually, to my power, to repay it. <b>I have given him a certain book, to be presented to you from me, which comprises two [works] in itself for easier carriage: the first exhibits the Mechanical art of Astronomy, as it has hitherto been in use with us [the <i>Mechanica</i>]; the second, the thousand fixed stars, most accurately restored by long and huge labour, and no mediocre expense [the star-catalogue].</b> Have this gift of books in turn, for the book formerly dedicated to me [the <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>], until more of mine, which you desire, shall be quite finished by the engraving-labour. He has some other things too at hand, so far as they were ready in this peregrination of mine, which he will at the same time show, and will instruct you about more things concerning me and my studies. I hope, when I have got more convenient and durable leisure in Germany, to satisfy your wishes and others' more fully, in consummating the illustration and enrichment of Astronomy.</p><p><b>I inserted, near the end of the first book, your letters sent to me</b> — when space was free for them and for the Curtius [letters] — and that for certain reasons, especially to excite others to love and cultivate these studies; and although I did this without consulting you, yet I trust it will not be unwelcome, since (as I think) it is not dishonourable to you. The observations you promise in the second Epistle, if you have done any meanwhile, I should wish to obtain. But if it has hitherto been neglected, I ask that it be diligently made up and performed in future; it will turn to the profit of our art (as I hope), and will be praiseworthy both to you and to others who do it. If you wish to make instruments, that book [the <i>Mechanica</i>] will clearly supply the form and method.</p><p>I add no more now; perhaps in what is already written I have exceeded the bounds of a letter — which, however, I have gladly admitted for the sake of our friendship and common studies, the places too by which we are separated not admitting frequent conversations, and so [the letters] not to be abbreviated where it can be otherwise. And if you should wish to know more things perhaps here omitted, the youth whom I have now earnestly commended to you, since he is not ignorant of my affairs, will expedite more, and will satisfy your wishes in this part (as I promise myself). So have him welcome again and again, and help and promote him there, to all the better things, so far as in you lies — that one day, God favouring, returned to me, he may deservedly proclaim your benevolence toward him; and that will be far the most welcome to me.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Dresden in Meissen, where is the court of the Electors of Saxony, the 28th of November, Old Style, the year etc. 1598.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If three Eclipses of this year have been observed there by you or by anyone else, make me partaker of that record, for the difference of meridians to be elicited thence. By my instruments they were noted thus, so that the moments observed and reduced to the middle of the same stood as follows. In the year 1598: Feb. 20, Eclipse [of the Moon], middle 17h 58′ P.M., at Wandsbek near Hamburg; 2 digits of [the Moon] were wanting. March 6, Eclipse of the Sun ☉, middle 11h 5′ A.M., New Style, at Uraniborg in Denmark; the magnitude of the eclipse there appeared, from the upper part, between 9 and 10 digits. August 16, Eclipse [of the Moon], middle 7h 42′ P.M., at Wandsbek. All these times agree rightly with our restitution. But how much they differ from the calculation, both Alphonsine and Copernican, see for yourself. The longitude of Wandsbek I presuppose at 33° 35′; of Uraniborg 36° 45′; and that of Venice nearly the same.</p><p>The same young man will also relate to you certain things in my name, secretly, in which — if you should lend me your aid, or through some other suitable man there [in Italy] should render me a service — you will do me a thing most welcome by far; and once his willingness has become known to me through the same [messenger], I shall instruct you more fully how the matter is to be carried through, and shall prescribe the form of it. I have certain reasons why I should wish this to be done in Italy rather than in Germany. It matters much to me that this be accomplished, which can also be carried out without great trouble; I will gladly pay the expenses, and shall in turn be found grateful. The bearer of this letter has with him certain documents of that business, which he will be able to show and explain to you privately.</p><p>To Your Excellency,<br>Most devotedly,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>I have written with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> at <b>Bologna</b> — this is the <b>first</b> Tycho-to-Magini letter (cf. the later brahe-t_16010220001, brahe-t_16000103002, brahe-t_16010124001).</li><li><b>Significance — the foundational Magini letter: the <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>/prosthaphaeresis, the fullest exile narrative, the scientific obstacles, and the <i>Mechanica</i> gift.</b></li><li><b>The <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i> and prosthaphaeresis.</b> Tycho acknowledges Magini's <b>table of squares (<i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>, 1592), dedicated to him</b> (received late, second-hand), and judges its labour praiseworthy — but counters that the planetary-equation angles \"for which you chiefly recommend it\" are better got by <b>prosthaphaereses (addition/subtraction, no multiplication/division)</b> — the trigonometric product-to-sum method, \"in every kind of spherical and plane triangles.\" (Cf. Herwart's own prosthaphaeresis, brahe-t_15990917001.)</li><li><b>The scientific obstacles to publishing.</b> A candid statement of why the <i>Progymnasmata</i> is delayed: <b>Saturn's slow orbit</b> (three oppositions don't fix the apogee/eccentricity — Ptolemy/Copernicus \"vainly believed\"; the equant-eccentricity proportion \"varies\"); <b>Mercury's elusiveness</b>; and <b>the Moon's complex inequalities, needing \"eighteen or more eclipses,\" not three</b>.</li><li><b>★ The fullest exile narrative.</b> One of Tycho's most complete: <b>Frederick II's</b> patronage; the <b>eight-year regency's</b> cold neglect; <b>Christian IV's</b> refusal \"that it was not in his power to spend anything,\" via the <b>Chancellor</b>; the <b>withdrawn revenues</b> and the <b>annulled parchment Senate-decree</b> (signed by \"the chief, now Court-Master\"); the choice to <b>leave the fatherland with wife, children, and the \"huge astronomical apparatus\" \"a year and a half ago\"</b>; the <b>Wandsbek</b> wait; the <b>imperial call</b> from Rudolf II; and the <b>Dresden interim</b> during the Bohemian plague (the Emperor \"living solitarily,\" advising Tycho to wait nearby).</li><li><b>The Saxony-academy plan.</b> Tycho means to go to a <b>upper-Saxony/Meissen academy</b> to finish the <i>Progymnasmata</i> printing (interrupted by the Danish troubles) — the <b>Wittenberg</b> stay that followed.</li><li><b>★ The <i>Mechanica</i> + star-catalogue gift.</b> Tycho sends Magini, via <b>Tengnagel</b>, <b>a bound volume of two works — the <i>Mechanica</i> + the 1000-star catalogue</b> — \"in return for\" the <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>. <i>(A documented Mechanica/catalogue distribution event.)</i> And he notes he has <b>printed Magini's letters in his <i>Epistolae</b></i> (with the Curtius letters), \"without consulting you … to excite others.\"</li><li><b>The Tengnagel commendation.</b> A warm character-portrait of <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> — \"noble by birth, German, my household-member, companion of my migration … held in a son's place\" — commended to Magini and the Bolognese/Roman learned.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Magini</b> correspondence's opening (→ brahe-t_16010220001, brahe-t_16000103002, brahe-t_16010124001); the <b>exile-narrative</b> thread (brahe-t_16000103001, brahe-t_16000103002); the <b>Mechanica/catalogue distribution</b> layer; the <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> thread (brahe-t_15990917001); the <b>Tengnagel-embassy</b> thread.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Magini (the <i>Tabula Tetragonica</i>); <b>King Frederick II</b> &amp; <b>Christian IV</b>; the Danish <b>Chancellor</b> and <b>Court-Master</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus</b>; the <i>Mechanica</i>, the star-catalogue, the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, the <i>Epistolae</i>. Places: <b>Dresden</b> (Saxon Electors' court), <b>Uraniborg/Denmark</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Frankfurt</b>, <b>Hamburg</b> (the Rantzau castle), <b>Bohemia/Prague</b>. Glyphs ♄ ☿ ☾ reproduced; Greek προσθαφαιρέσεις / πραγματεία reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Dresdae Misniae … Novembris 28 <b>stilo veteri</b> Anno etc. 1598</i> → <b>28 November 1598 [Old Style]</b> (Tycho explicitly marks Old Style at Saxon Dresden); header and label agree.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Scientific postscript (three 1598 eclipses + the Wandsbek/Uraniborg/Venice longitudes), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.124–125 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>What it is.</b> The concealed core of a letter Tycho sent from Dresden (28 November 1598) to Giovanni Antonio Magini in Bologna — the surviving published text is only the purely astronomical tail (eclipse timings and longitudes). This recovered passage is the confidential errand referred to only obliquely elsewhere in the letter.</li><li><b>The messenger.</b> \"The same young man\" (\"idem adolescens\") is almost certainly Tycho's son-in-law and secretary Franz Tengnagel, who in November 1598 travelled to Italy as Tycho's emissary, meeting Magini in Bologna and being made a Knight of San Marco by the Doge of Venice [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000)].</li><li><b>What the secret was.</b> Dreyer records precisely this errand: Tycho wanted an Italian scholar to write a eulogy of him — a request Magini could not fulfil himself but two years later recommended to Bernardino Baldi, then compiling a collection of <i>Lives</i> of the mathematicians [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890)]. Tycho in fact never appears in Baldi's completed <i>Lives</i> or <i>History</i> [Tier 1: Thoren, <i>The Lord of Uraniborg</i> (1990)].</li><li><b>Why Italy, not Germany.</b> Tycho gives a practical reason of reputational strategy — he wanted the panegyrist to be Italian rather than German, presumably to extend his fame into the humanist literary culture Baldi represented, beyond the German-speaking scholarly world where he was already well known.</li><li><b>The documents.</b> The \"certain documents\" Tengnagel carried and could show Magini \"privately\" were presumably materials to brief a prospective biographer — a self-fashioning dossier consistent with Tycho's documented eagerness to control his own public image during the Dresden exile.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Address heading: corrected 'BONONIENSEM' to 'BONONIENSIUM' per Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia</i> VIII, p.120 (a corrupted accusative-singular reading restored to the printed genitive plural). No change to the English translation, which was already correct.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Tabula-Tetragonica acknowledgment and prosthaphaeresis</b> preference; the <b>scientific obstacles</b> — Saturn, Mercury, the 18-eclipse Moon; the <b>complete exile narrative</b> — Frederick II, the regency, Christian IV's refusal via the Chancellor, the withdrawn revenues, the annulled decree, the departure with the apparatus, Wandsbek, the imperial call, the Dresden/plague interim; the <b>Saxony-academy plan</b>; the <b>Mechanica + star-catalogue gift</b> via Tengnagel and the printing of Magini's letters in the <i>Epistolae</i>; and the <b>Tengnagel commendation</b>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>High confidence. The Latin is unremarkable epistolary prose and the content is independently and specifically corroborated by Dreyer's narrative account of this exact letter and errand — the eulogy commission Magini later passed to Bernardino Baldi. No direct textual crux. Upgrade path: locate the letter in TBOO vol. VIII to confirm wording against the printed critical edition, and check Favaro's <i>Carteggio inedito</i> (1886) for Magini's side of the correspondence discussing the Baldi recommendation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 8 Dreyer page-images (TBOO VIII pp.119-126) in two chunks (pages 1-4, 5-8) and collated the full letter to Magini line by line against published_english and source_text, including the disputed postscript. The postscript (eclipse data: Feb 20 17h58' PM Wandsbek 2 digits, Mar 6 11h5' AM Uraniborg 9-10 digits, Aug 16 7h42' PM Wandsbek; longitudes Wandsbek 33 deg 35', Uraniborg 36 deg 45'; plus the secret-business paragraph to be conveyed via Tengnagel) is printed on TBOO VIII pp.124-125 exactly as our source_text and published_english give it -- the earlier hand-restoration from Dreyer is verified correct, no fabrication. Every other proper name (Frederick, Rudolf, the Chancellor/Curiae Magister, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Franciscus Tignaglius/Tengnagel), number (5 years, 8-year interregnum, 1.5 years, sexies ternis=18), date (28 November 1598 stilo veteri, Dresden), and sum checked correctly against the page images. One TEXT_ERROR found in the dedicatory heading (Bononiensem vs Dreyer's Bononiensium) forces grade C under the grading rule even though it does not change what a scholar would take from the letter's content; verdict recorded as MINOR reflecting that low materiality.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text's address heading reads the grammatically anomalous 'BONONIENSEM' (accusative singular, does not agree with ablative 'gymnasio'); Dreyer's printed text has 'BONONIENSIUM' (genitive plural, 'of the Bolognese') -- a corrupted word relative to the edition of record. Does not affect the sense of the English translation, which renders the phrase correctly either way. Dreyer prints: <i>MATHEMATUM IN CELEBRI BONONIENSIUM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI EXIMIO (TBOO VIII, p.120)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VIII p.120 (address heading of the Magini letter), read directly from the slice PDF. The heading clearly prints 'MATHEMATUM IN CELEBRI BONONIENSIUM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI EXIMIO' with genitive plural BONONIENSIUM. This is the letter's only defect and it is now resolved; no crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici."], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15920201001", "next": "brahe-t_15991104001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15920201001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15991104001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges, five years after receipt, Magini's Tabula Tetragonica (dedicated to him) and notes his approval while observing that the calculations are not fully worked through.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981128001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981201001", "d": "1598-12-01", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Thaddaeus Hagecius → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 1 December 1598 [N.S.] (catalogued 1 November)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>POSTSCRIPTA THADDAeI HAGECIJ AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>CUM hasce ad te literas obsignassem, daturus eas tuo tabellario, misi adhuc meum puerum hodie, qui est 1. Decembr. cum hacce schedula ad D. CORRADUCIUM, quam ad te mitto, ex qua intelliges voluntatem Caesaris. Est sigillum Dni CORRADUCIJ et manus. Multum gavisus sum, me tandem ante hunc meum abitum hoc responsum et resolutionem Caesaris accepisse. Hisce quod addam amplius non est necesse.</p><p>EXEMPLUM LITERARUM DOMINI D. THADEI AD AMPLISSIMUM PROCANCELLARIUM IMPERIJ<br>RUDOLPHUM<br>CORRADUCIUM.Magce D. et Patrone observandiss. Hucusque noster Dominus<br>TYCHO omnia egit ex consilio tuæ Magtiæ et voluntate clementi Cæsaris. Venit cum<br>uxore, liberis et familia ac 12 equis longo et difficili<br>itinere, sumptibus magnis, Dresdam usque. Et nulli alij Principi, nulli nationi<br>suam operam voluit devovere nisi uni Cæsari. Rogat igitur, et ego intercedo,<br>efficere velit, ut responsum a Cæsare accipiat et negotia sua dirigere<br>stabilireque possit, ac ne ita dubius cum magno suarum rerum dispendio hæreat.<br>Literas meas ad BARVITIUM reverenter peto<br>prima occasione mitti curet, eumque etiam sua schedula<br>commonefaciat, ut Cæsari negotium proponat, et ultimam resolutionem Dresdam<br>mitti curet. Non pœnitebit, scio, Domine, huius operæ, et futurus est D. TYCHO,<br>ubi de facie aliquando innotuerit, longe gratissimus.</p><p>B. v.<br>Ex ædibus nostris 1. Decembris Anno 1598.T. M. promptus ad officia<br>THADEUS</p><p>RUDOLPHUS CORRADUCIUS AD THADDÆUM HAGECIUM.Quæ D. BARVITIUS iussu Cæsaris de Dno TYCHONE<br>nostro ad me rescripsit, hæc sunt: »De TYCHONIS adventu Cæsar perlibenter intellexit; ut Dresdæ<br>vel Pragæ vel alibi in vicinia tantisper, donec aula certo in loco recolligatur,<br>subsistat, cupit; ubi deinde ille commode et opportune collocari possit, benigne cogitat.« Hactenus Dnus<br>BARVITIUS, quæ Magtiæ tuæ<br>ad hasce suas significanda duxi. Interim rogo, ut Dnum<br>TYCHONEM meis verbis quam officiosissime salutet meamque operam eidem<br>polliceatur meque, quod ei ob ingentes meas occupationes non scribo,<br>diligentissime excuset.</p><p>RUDOLPHUS<br>CORRADUCIUS,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Postscript of Thaddaeus Hagecius to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>When I had sealed these letters to you, intending to give them to your courier, I have today — which is 1 December — sent my boy besides, with this note to Master Coraduz, which I send on to you, from which you will understand the will of the Emperor. It bears Master Coraduz's seal and hand. I have rejoiced greatly that at last, before this my departure, I have received this reply and resolution of the Emperor. To these things there is no need that I add anything more.</p><p><b>Copy of a letter of Master Thaddeus Hagecius to the Most Ample Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, Rudolph Corraducius.</b></p><p>Magnificent Lord and most honoured Patron. Thus far our Lord Tycho has done everything on the counsel and advice of Your Magnificence and by the gracious will of the Emperor. He has come, with his wife, children and household, and twelve horses, by a long and difficult journey and at great expense, as far as Dresden. And to no other Prince, to no [other] nation, has he wished to devote his service, but to the Emperor alone. He asks, therefore — and I intercede [on his behalf] — that you be pleased to bring it about that he receive an answer from the Emperor, and be able to direct and settle his affairs, and not be left hanging thus in doubt, to the great detriment of his estate. I respectfully beg that you see to it that my letter to Barvitius be sent at the first opportunity, and that you also remind him by your own note to lay the matter before the Emperor, and see to it that the final resolution be sent to Dresden. You will not repent, I know, my Lord, of this labour, and Master Tycho, once he has become known to you face to face, will prove exceedingly grateful.</p><p>Farewell. From our house, 1 December, in the year 1598. Ready to serve Your Magnificence, Thaddeus.</p><p><b>Rudolph Corraducius to Thaddeus Hagecius.</b></p><p>What Master Barvitius, by the Emperor's order, has written back to me concerning our Lord Tycho, is this: 'Of Tycho's arrival the Emperor has learned with the greatest pleasure; he wishes him to remain for a time at Dresden, or Prague, or elsewhere nearby, until the court is gathered again in some fixed place; where he may then be conveniently and suitably settled, he graciously has in mind.' Thus far Master Barvitius — which words I have thought it right to make known to Your Magnificence in this [letter] of mine. Meanwhile I ask that you greet Master Tycho most dutifully in my words, and promise him my service, and most diligently excuse me for not writing to him myself, on account of my immense preoccupations.</p><p>Rudolph Corraducius, with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), imperial physician at Prague, astronomer, and Tycho's long-standing friend and chief broker at the Rudolfine court; the principal channel by which Tycho's reception into imperial service was negotiated.</li><li><b><i>D. Corraducius</b></i> = <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> (Coraduz / Coraduz), Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Reichsvizekanzler</i>; <i>Procancellarius</i> in the companion letter brahe-t_15980530001). The enclosed note conveyed \"the will of the Emperor\" — <b>Rudolf II</b>'s favourable resolution on Tycho's appointment — under Coraduz's seal and hand.</li><li><b>Document type.</b> A <i>postscriptum</i> — a postscript written after the main letter had been sealed, hence its separate transmission.</li><li><b>⚠ Date.</b> The body fixes the day explicitly: <i>hodie, qui est 1. Decembr.</i> = <b>1 December 1598</b>; the slug (…12 01…) agrees. The dataset catalogues the piece under <b>1 November</b>; the one-month discrepancy is reproduced, not reconciled — the header follows the as-written <b>1 December</b>. New Style (Prague, imperial Catholic).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> The postscript already published for this letter (Hagecius to Tycho, 1 December 1598) tells Tycho he is forwarding a note from \"D. Coraducius\" bearing on \"the will of the Emperor,\" but breaks off before quoting it. The recovered segment is that enclosure in full: fair copies of two letters — (1) Hagecius's own appeal to the imperial Vice-Chancellor on Tycho's behalf, and (2) the Vice-Chancellor's reply, itself quoting the Emperor's secretary verbatim.</li><li><b>The three courtiers named are Tycho's patronage brokers at Rudolf II's court in the winter of 1598–99</b>: Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku), the Emperor's physician and Tycho's chief broker in Prague; Rudolph Corraducius (Rudolf von Coraduz), Vice-Chancellor of the Empire; and Johannes Barvitius, Rudolf II's confidential secretary and closest adviser — Christianson (<i>On Tycho's Island</i>, and <i>Measure of the Heavens</i>) names all three as the inner circle Hagecius mobilised to secure Tycho's imperial appointment.</li><li><b>Concrete biographical detail not in the letter proper.</b> Hagecius's covering letter to Corraducius specifies that Tycho arrived at Dresden \"with his wife, children and household, and twelve horses\" (<i>cum uxore, liberis et familia ac 12 equis</i>), by a long and costly journey — a precise snapshot of the size and expense of the 1597–98 exodus from Denmark that the published tail does not give.</li><li><b>This is the actual textual source of \"the will of the Emperor\" referenced in the already-published tail.</b> Corraducius quotes Barvitius's report of Rudolf II's own instruction (set off with guillemets in the source): the Emperor is glad of Tycho's arrival and wants him to wait at Dresden, Prague, or nearby until the peripatetic imperial court settles in one place, at which point he will be suitably provided for — the direct documentary basis for the extended limbo Tycho experienced before his Benátky/Prague settlement.</li><li><b>Hagecius stresses Tycho's exclusive loyalty to the Emperor</b> (\"nulli alij Principi, nulli nationi suam operam voluit devovere nisi uni Cæsari\") — a rhetorical move consistent with the delicate diplomatic position Tycho was in, having left Denmark and needing to be seen as uncompromisingly committed to Habsburg service rather than shopping his services elsewhere.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — short, secure; sense and identifications (Hagecius, Coraduz, the imperial resolution) are not in doubt. The catalogue's \"1 November\" is noted as a label error against the text's own \"1 December.\"</p><p>The Latin is standard, well-formed chancery/patronage correspondence with no corrupt readings; both copied letters are internally coherent and consistent with the already-published postscript that introduces them, so the translation is secure throughout. The one soft point is register: several closing formulae are heavily abbreviated in period epistolary shorthand (\"B. v.\", \"T. M. promptus ad officia\") and have been expanded to ordinary English sense rather than reproduced as abbreviations; this is a translation choice, not a reading uncertainty. Grade A is warranted for textual clarity, though the translation has not been collated against a published critical edition of this specific enclosure.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.118-121) visually, long-s typeface, no OCR/text-layer used. Located the letter's actual text on pp.119-120 (bottom of 118/119 through mid-120), bracketed by the unrelated Goedelmann-to-Tycho letter (ending top of 119) and the Tycho-to-Magino letter (starting mid-120). Collated all three layers - Hagecius's own postscript to Tycho, the enclosed copy of Hagecius's letter to Corraducius, and Corraducius's reply to Hagecius - word by word against source_text and against Dreyer's print. Checked opening/closing formulas, the date '1. Decembris'/'1 December' (consistent in all three: Hagecius's own dateline at postscript opening 'hodie, qui est 1. Decembr.' and in the enclosed letter's closing 'Ex aedibus nostris 1. Decembris Anno 1598' - no invented day-number), the figure '12 equis'/'twelve horses' (matches in source, Dreyer, and English), place-names Dresden/Prague (Dresdam, Dresdae, Pragae all correctly rendered and not swapped), and every proper name (Hagecius/Thadeus, Tycho, Corraducius, Barvitius). Checked subject continuity across the honorific third-person-subjunctive address forms (salutet/polliceatur/excuset all correctly resolved to Hagecius as the one asked to act on Corraducius's behalf in the closing paragraph; the earlier commonefaciat/proponat construction correctly keeps Barvitius, not Corraducius, as the one being reminded to place the matter before the Emperor) - no pronoun or custody flips found. source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly (allowing only for long-s/round-s and standard Latin abbreviation expansion, e.g. Dni/Domini, Magtiae/Magnificentiae, which are not fidelity issues). No fabricated names/dates/sums, no silently dropped clauses, no hedges hardened, no mistranslated antecedents were found in the English against either source_text or Dreyer's print.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrft"], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 36, "prev": "brahe-t_15980530001", "next": "brahe-t_15990226001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980000002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990226001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Forwards a note from Coraduz bearing the Emperor's resolution, received just before Hagecius's departure, and adds nothing further beyond this dispatch.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981201001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981205001", "d": "1598-12-05", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wittenberg, 5 December 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift — copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET ERUDITISSIMO VIRO DOMINO OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM, CONSANGUINEO, AFFINI ET AMICO INTIMO, MIHIQUE CHARISSIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CHARISSIME OLIGERE, Amice, Consangvinee et Affinis integerrime, de statu mearum rerum cognoscendo te esse sollicitum, id sic suadente eo, quo me prosequeris amore, et ego vicissim te, non dubito. Sic itaque brevibus habe: Wandesburgo statim a Michaëlis festo discedens Pragam versus ad Caesarem iturus, Magdeburgi primum per octiduum ob Ducem Brunsvicensem, tum quoque ERICUM LANGIUM substiti, postea Dresdae per mensem integrum, quo responsum a Caesare, an illis temporibus, cum Praga abesset ob luem Epidemicam, accedendus et compellandus foret, expectarem; quale vero tandem receperim, ex altera pagina cognosces. Caetera exemplum literarum ad KRAGIUM nostrum, quod adiunxi (neque enim nunc vacat eadem ad te repetere) edocebit. Ingressurus est hac ipsa hora filius meus TYCHO cum quibusdam comitibus iter Wandesburgum, ut familiam meam muliebrem huc meis curribus adducat; constitui enim, favente Deo, hîc Wittebergae hyemare, aut saltem tam diu manere, donec denuo a Caesare in Bohemiam vocer. Plura non addo, quia pluribus otium deest. Tu vero, mi OLIGERE, copiosius rescribe, et de singulis, quae scire mea interesse nosti, affatim instrue, meque saepius, interea dum hîc sum, tuis literis invise. Invenies me ad par pari referendum minime segnem. Habebis brevi plura carmina ad Caesarem de me, tum quoque ad me a varijs Poëtis concinnata, quae nunc describere non licuit. Tu interim, ut Dania Germaniae respondeat, effice, pro ut in te sponte recepisti, si modo Iutorum sit pollicitis aliquando stare. Si ego Schanus tale quid addixissem, certe fidem meam dudum redemissem, utut fuerim non minus quam tu occupatissimus. Sed si adhuc promissis steteris, erit veniae locus. Cum HUNNIO hîc necdum familiaritatem contraxi, pridie enim primum huc veni, sed fiet id facile in posterum, uti etiam cum reliquis huius Academiae Eruditis. POLYCARPUM decumbentem, cum Dresdae essem, bis invisi, humanissime ab illo exceptus; is te salutari voluit. Salutem etiam tibi dicit Hospes meus Dnus Doctor IOHANNES IESSENIUS, qui mihi in Philippaeis olim aedibus commodam habitationem pro me et meis lubenter et benevole concessit. Vale, et brevi plura a me expecta. Sed da operam, ut praevenias. Saluta coniugem SOPHIAM Neptem meam, utque alteri SOPHIAe praevaleat, indulge.</p><p>Dabantur properanter Wittebergae die 5. Decembris Anni 1598.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 126; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. IMPURUM istud et calumnijs scatens Dithmarsi istius scriptum, quod tibi inspiciendum misi, necdum recepi, utut in literis ultimis, quas, cum Wandesburgi essem, accepi, indicaveris te illud mihi remisisse; fac igitur, ut mature habeam, neque enim eo carere possum propter illa, quae in margine adscripta erant, ut inde dem occasionem alteri cuipiam ista maledicta in suum authorem retorquendi; sed luet is brevi quae meritus est. Retulit de eo ad me Doct. IOHANNES GŒDELMANNUS, Consiliarius Saxonicus et CHYTRAeI Gener, quem proculdubio nosti, quod URSUS iste plane contemptus sit et despectui habitus ab omnibus cordatis viris et ijs, qui in aula sunt Caesaris, postquam tam insulsum et temerarium scriptum publicavit, esseque ab ipso Caesare neglectum et vili pensum.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned man, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, kinsman by blood, kinsman by marriage, and most intimate friend, and most dear to me.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Dearest Holger — friend, kinsman by blood, and most upright kinsman by marriage — that you are anxious to learn the state of my affairs, the love with which you attend me (and I you in turn) so persuading, I do not doubt. So have it thus, in brief: leaving Wandsbek right after the feast of Michael, to go toward Prague to the Emperor, I halted first at Magdeburg for eight days, on account of the Duke of Brunswick, and also of Erik Lange; afterward at Dresden for a whole month, there to await a reply from the Emperor — whether at that time, since he was away from Prague because of the epidemic plague, he was to be approached and addressed. What [reply] I finally received, you will learn from the other page. The rest, the copy of the letter to our Krag, which I have appended (for there is no time now to repeat the same to you), will teach you. This very hour my son Tycho is to set out on the journey to Wandsbek, with some companions, to bring my womenfolk hither with my wagons; for I have resolved, God favouring, to winter here at Wittenberg, or at least to remain so long until I am called again by the Emperor into Bohemia. I add no more, because for more there is no leisure. But do you, my Holger, write back more fully, and instruct me amply on the particulars which you know it concerns me to know, and visit me the more often with your letters while I am here. You will find me by no means slow to render like for like. You will shortly have several poems to the Emperor about me, and also to me, composed by various Poets, which I could not now transcribe. You, meanwhile, see to it that Denmark answers Germany [in correspondence], as you spontaneously undertook — if only it be in Jutlanders to stand by their promises sometime. Had I, a Scanian, pledged such a thing, I should certainly have redeemed my word long ago, however much I have been no less busy than you. But if you will yet have kept your promises, there will be room for pardon. With Hunnius I have not yet struck up acquaintance here, for I came hither only yesterday; but it will easily happen hereafter, as also with the rest of the learned of this Academy. Polycarp, lying ill, I visited twice while I was at Dresden, and was most kindly received by him; he wished to be remembered to you. Greeting to you, too, is sent by my host, Lord Doctor Johannes Jessenius, who has freely and kindly granted me convenient lodging, for myself and mine, in the house that was once Philip's. Farewell, and shortly expect more from me — but take pains to forestall me. Greet your wife Sophie, my niece, and indulge her, that she may outdo the other Sophie.</p><p>Given hastily at Wittenberg, the 5th day of December, in the year 1598.<br>Your Tycho Brahe.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. That impure writing of the Dithmarsian, teeming with calumnies, which I sent you to inspect, I have not yet received back — although in your last letter, which I received while I was at Wandsbek, you indicated that you had sent it back to me; see to it, then, that I have it in good time, for I cannot do without it on account of those things which were noted in the margin, that I may thence give occasion to some other man of turning those slanders back upon their author; but he shall shortly pay what he has deserved. Doctor Johann Gödelmann, Saxon Councillor and Chytraeus's son-in-law — whom you doubtless know — reported to me concerning him, that this Ursus is plainly despised and held in contempt by all sensible men, and by those who are at the Emperor's court, after he published so insipid and rash a writing, and that he is neglected and lightly esteemed by the Emperor himself.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; moment.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Wittenberg</b>, December 1598, having just arrived (\"only yesterday\") — a chatty progress-report to his closest Danish kinsman during the slow approach to imperial service. It pairs with brahe-t_15980905001 (Wandsbek, Sept 1598) as the two main Tycho→Holger letters of the migration.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned\"</b> of <b>Rosenholm</b> — <i>consanguineus, affinis et amicus intimus</i> (kinsman by blood and marriage; cf. brahe-t_15980905001).</li><li><b>The itinerary.</b> From <b>Wandsbek</b>, just after <b>Michaelmas (29 Sept)</b>, toward Prague; halted a week at <b>Magdeburg</b> (on account of the <b>Duke of Brunswick</b> and <b>Erik Lange</b>), then a whole month at <b>Dresden</b>, awaiting the Emperor's word on whether to approach him (Rudolf II being absent from Prague <i>ob luem epidemicam</i> — because of the <b>plague</b>). Tycho now resolves to <b>winter at Wittenberg</b> until summoned into Bohemia; his son is being sent back to Wandsbek to fetch the <b>womenfolk</b> and wagons.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Dux Brunsvicensis</i> = the <b>Duke of Brunswick</b> (Heinrich Julius of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel).</li><li><i>Ericus Langius</i> = <b>Erik Lange</b> (d. 1613), Danish nobleman and alchemist, Tycho's friend and prospective brother-in-law (betrothed to Tycho's sister Sophie Brahe) — chronically in debt and abroad.</li><li><i>Kragius noster</i> = <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius)</b> (c. 1550–1602), Danish humanist, historian and diplomat — recipient of a parallel letter Tycho encloses in copy.</li><li><i>filius meus Tycho</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>, sent to fetch the household.</li><li><i>Hunnius</i> = <b>Aegidius Hunnius</b> (1550–1603), the leading Wittenberg Lutheran theologian, not yet met.</li><li><i>Polycarpus</i> = <b>Polycarp Leyser (Leiser)</b> the Elder (1552–1610), Saxon court preacher, visited (ill) at Dresden.</li><li><i>Dnus Doctor Iohannes Iessenius</i> = <b>Johannes Jessenius</b>, Tycho's <b>host</b> at Wittenberg, who lodged him <i>in Philippaeis aedibus</i> — \"in the house that was once Philip's,\" i.e. <b>Melanchthon's former house</b> (<i>Philippus</i> = Philipp Melanchthon) — a resonant detail (cf. Jessenius across the 1600 letters).</li><li><i>coniunx Sophia, neptis mea</i> = <b>Sophie Brahe</b>, Holger's <b>wife</b> and Tycho's <b>niece</b>; <i>altera Sophia</i> = the <b>other Sophie</b> — Tycho's learned <b>sister Sophie Brahe</b> (the wordplay: may the niece \"outdo\" the aunt).</li><li><b>The Scanian/Jutlander jest.</b> <i>si modo Iutorum sit pollicitis … stare … Si ego Schanus …</i> — Tycho teases Holger (a Jutland landowner) about Jutlanders keeping promises, contrasting himself as a <b>Scanian</b> (<i>Schanus</i>, of Skåne); rendered straight as the friendly regional jab it is.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> Survives as an <b>afskrift (copy)</b> in Langebek's Diplomatarium, Copenhagen.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i>; <i>Dabantur properanter</i> → \"Given hastily\"; <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>ad par pari referendum</i> → \"to render like for like.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 5. Decembris Anni 1598</i> at Wittenberg; no style marker. Lutheran Saxony was on the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar; reproduced, not converted.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (the unreturned Ursus book + Gödelmann's report that Ursus is despised at court), restored from Dreyer VIII p.126 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected from 'a week' to 'eight days', matching 'per octiduum' (Latin and Dreyer agree; only the translation was at fault), per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII p.125.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, allusive humanist Latin, read securely; the itinerary and the dense cast (Holger and Sophie Rosenkrantz, Erik Lange, Niels Krag, Hunnius, Polycarp Leyser, Jessenius and the Melanchthon house, sister Sophie) are firmly identified, and the Scanian/Jutlander joke is rendered as such. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.124-127 as page images; the letter to Holger Rosenkrantz runs from the header on p.125 through the postscript ending on p.126 (pp.124 and 127 belong to the flanking Maginus and Kragius letters). Collated salutation, full body, closing date, and the restored postscript word-for-word against the page images; all proper names (Erik Lange, Krag, Hunnius, Polycarpus, Jessenius, Sophia, Gödelmann, Chytraeus, Ursus), the date (5 December 1598), and the postscript content match exactly. Only defect found is the 'octiduum'/'a week' numeric rounding noted above.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). 'octiduum' denotes a specific eight-day period, not 'a week' (seven days); source and Dreyer agree, but the English rounds the number down. At: “I halted first at Magdeburg for a week”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.125 (page image). The Latin 'octiduum' is unambiguous and undisputed between our source and Dreyer; only the English rounding was corrected. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 17, "prev": "brahe-t_15980925001", "next": "brahe-t_15981231002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports his journey from Wandsbek toward Prague, with stops at Magdeburg and Dresden while awaiting the Emperor's reply, and announces his son Tycho's departure.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981205001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981205002", "d": "1598-12-05", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Niels Krag", "ck": "Niels Krag", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Niels Krag — Wittenberg, 5 December 1598", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a Danish phrase) · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Note.</b> The counterpart to <b>Niels Krag's reply of 4 December 1599 (brahe-t_15991204001)</b>: Tycho's fullest account of the <b>1598 journey</b> (Wandsbek → Magdeburg → Dresden → Wittenberg), the imperial-summons mechanism, and the <b>Wittenberg lodging in Melanchthon's house</b>.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD NICOLAUM KRAGIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CUM Dresdae adhuc essem, ante paucos dies literas tuas accepi, Praestantissime et Amantissime Domine Doctor NICOLAE KRAGIE, quae Haffnia datae erant Calendis Octobris huius anni. Indicas autem ab initio te meas non accepisse, quas HOLGERUS ROSENKRANTZIUS noster in tua absentia secum in Cimbriam recepit; miror itaque eum tanto tempore illas retinuisse. Existimaverat quidem ille, te esse invisurum sese Rosenholmij, uti ei forte pollicitus eras, at ego illi scripseram, quod frustra te exspectaret, aeque enim rarum esse Iutum Iuto vera dicere et fidem facere, atque augurem auguri, neque sane in hoc augurio falsus sum meo ipsius incommodo, siquidem plaeraque erant in illo, quem tibi inscripseram, fasciculo, quae te mature scire avebam; spero tamen te id iam accepisse et eius contenta omnia percepisse, quare ut de ijs, ubi praesertim opus est, mihi mature respondeas, rogo; atque simul indices, quid de literis illis actum sit, quas IOHANNES STYGIUS in Daniam ad quendam mea de caussa se scripsisse rettulit, et quomodo intellectae acceptaeque. Quae de Praefecto meo Huennensi in hisce ultimis attingis, parum moror, is non saltem erga me infidelis deprehenditur, atque iniquas reddere rationes suae villicationis, sed insuper nihil ferme eorum exequitur, quae aliquoties ei per literas mando, non sine rerum mearum gravi dispendio, adeo ut aliqua adhuc ex instrumentis meis et reliqua supellectile isthic in Insula, aut etiam Haffniae residua sint, quae necdum Lubecae accipere potuerim, utut aliquoties hac de re scripserim et MULAeUS, cum praesens esset, illi singula oretenus exposuit; taceo quod nullos pisces ex tot meis piscinis tanti constantibus, sive salitos sive exsiccatos, uti volui, nancisci queam, sed is illis in suum et suorum commodum saltem utitur, vel potius abutitur. Verum quid de eo faciendum sit, ESCILLUS BILLEUS dispiciat et statuat, cui supremam inspectionem rerum mearum concredidi. Quod autem ais esse quosdam, qui Insulam meam a me petitam volunt, peiore mea futura conditione, equidem non intelligo, quid haec verba sibi velint, nimis enim obscura sunt, et in varium sensum trahi possunt. Cum tota Dania mihi nimis coarctata censeatur, quî quaeso insulula tam angusta tamque illucrifera me remorabitur, his pauciora et longe maiora affectantem sperantemque? Sed vertat se meus osor et invidus in quascunque volet partes, quicunque tandem is sit, non multum de me triumphabit, sed potius infringet dentem solido, propria delusus ab Arte. Atqui ego de Insula illa non sum magnopere sollicitus, neque etiam quodpiam emolumentum inde habeo, sed potius plus damni, neque tamen ullo iure eam mihi adimere possunt, salvis Regis FRIDERICI Literis, neque etiam sine Senatorum omnium iudicio et consensu id facere licet, si modo non quidvis licet citra ius et fas; oportet etiam me legitime citari. Sed restituant mihi solummodo meas impensas aut dimidiam saltem earum partem, quas in illam expendi ultra summam quam a Rege FRIDERICO et Senatoribus Regni accepi, aut decernant, om det er billigt att jeg skulle liide saadan Skade thill forgeffvis. Sed de his alias agemus, quando status mearum rerum fuerit in tutioribus et inexpugnabilibus terminis, quod brevi me assecuturum, favente summo Numine, confido. Erit forte aliquis potentior, qui mei patrocinium in se recipiet, atque a tot iniurijs et damnis, quot in Dania passus sum, vindicabit.</p><p>Quia vero de rebus meis certior fieri desideras, sic habe: Paulo post Michaëlis festum Wandesburgo Pragam versus ad Caesarem iter suscepi, comitante me etiam familia mea muliebri Magdeburgum usque, deinde reversa. Substiti autem Magdeburgi per octiduum in gratiam ERICI LANGIJ nostri, qui mihi per quinque dies illic aderat, postea Dresdam profectus, ubi certo illic comperissem, Caesarem relicta Praga ob morbos Epidemicos, in tutiorem locum septem miliaribus inde distantem se recepisse, nec quempiam admittere, nisi paucissimos quosdam quotidianos ministros, Aulam dissipatam esse, Cancellariam remotam, Consilia et Iudicia omnia silere, in summa Iustitium esse et actiones cunctas ob contagium populare conturbatas, consultius duxi scriptis Pragam ad THADDAeUM nostrum literis, an in tali rerum statu ulterius procedendum sit, expiscari, atque interea Dresdae responsum praestolari. THADDAeUS acceptis meis in hanc sententiam literis, eas Domino Procancellario Imperij RUDOLPHO CORRADUCIO, qui Pragae remanserat, actutum legendas exhibuit, is vero descriptum earum confestim Caesari misit, petens a BARVICIO, ut de Caesaris voluntate quamprimum fieri possit, quidpiam certi rescribat cumque hoc aliquamdiu protraheretur, uti sunt lenta, quae apud Magnates expedire oporteat, praesertim eo tempore, quando Literae Praga allatae ob infectionis metum, non nisi post aliquot dierum intervallum legebantur. Hinc evenit, ut usque in quartam hebdomadam Dresdae responsum exspectare necesse habuerim, THADDAeO interea morae etiam plusquam ego ipsemet impatiente, semel atque iterum responsum urgente, et cum BARVICIO hac de re quasi expostulante, illique IACOBUM CURTIUM Praedecessorem, quod ille multo citius me concernentia praestitisset, exprobrante, et tandem etiam ad Dominum CORRADUCIUM, qui Pragae adhuc erat, hac de re schedam scribente. Accidit autem, ut eo ipso die BARVICIJ Secretarij Literae ad CORRADUCIUM pervenirent, quibus Caesaris resolutio continebatur, quam CORRADUCIUS actutum Schedae THADDAeI propria manu subscripsit, eamque sigillo suo munitam THADDAeO demisit, ut me de voluntate Caesaris inde certiorem efficeret. Quae autem erant eorum, de quibus dixi, formalia verba, ex adiuncta charta percipies. Ego itaque accepto hoc Clementissimo Caesaris responso, me illi lubenter acquiescere rescripsi; nam et antea THADDAeO per literas significaram, me in superiori Saxonia potius quam Dresdae mansurum, donec morbi illi populariter in Bohemia grassantes remiserint, et Caesari commodius fuerit me eo vocare; neque enim periculo caruit cum meo iuvenili comitatu illas oras passim peste et dysenteria infectas tunc intrare. Recepi itaque me cum meis huc Wittebergam, ubi D. V. hyemare constitui, nisi prius a Caesare, sopita istic lue Epidemica accersitus fuero, eritque mihi hîc oportunum in operibus meis necdum Hamburgi ob Typographorum imperitiam et paucitatem plane absoluta Typis complere. Habito hîc in aedibus, quae olim erant optimae recordationis viri PHILIPPI MELANCHTONIS, nunc CASPARIS PEUCERI, inquilino earum modo exciente Domino Doctore IOHANNE IESSENIO Philosopho et Medico eximio, qui me perlubenter, imo non sine gaudio ad se recepit, et potissimam aedium partem pro me meaque familia, quam diu lubuerit, inhabitandam benigne concessit. Adest commoditas pro observationibus continuandis, suntque mihi ad manus hîc quaedam ex meis Instrumentis, quae in Bohemiam mecum sumere constitui. Est etiam locus pro laboratorio Pyronomico, in quo nonnulla, interea dum hîc sum, parare animus est, praeter ea, quae prius Wandesburgi confecta in promtu habeo. Caesar enim talibus impense delectatur, eaque proprijs manibus versat, non minus quam Mathematica, quae probe intelligit et alijs praefert. Ideoque aiunt, ipsum magno mei desiderio teneri, adeo ut in locum, quo nunc secessit, Podebra dictum, me ad se accersere, ut quotidie istic illi adessem, voluerit, iam praeparato pro me et meis amplo hospitio, sed increbrescente pestis vehementia in illa vicinia, ita ut aliquot bruta animantia inficerentur, de mutatione loci cogitavit, ita ut aut in Sileciam, aut Styriam aut etiam versus Insbrug se recipere cuperet, relicta Bohemia, modo id tutum foret, et ab indigena Nobilitate permissum. Atque hinc etiam factum, ut responsum de me aliquandiu retardaretur quodque eiuscemodi quale in charta vides acceperim. Adfuit mihi Dresdae doctor IOHANNES GOeDELMANNUS Consiliarius Saxonicus, CHYTRAeI gener, ijs ipsis diebus, quando Dresda mihi abeundum fuit, is inter alia mihi dixit, se Pragae, unde nuper advenerat, a fidedignis audivisse, Caesarem in tantum meum expetivisse adventum, ut haec verba pronunciarit: »Si solummodo illum virum hîc mecum haberem, cum quo de rebus Mathematicis et Astronomicis, tum quoque Pyronomicis iucunde conferre possim, atque eius opera perlustrare, non caperet me tantum temporis taedium, veluti nunc sit, dum Praga absum. Sed quid faciam, si illum confestim huc vocavero, et mihi ipsi sit alio discedendum, non bene consulo illi et eius comitatui, hoc praesertim periculoso tempore. Praestat itaque id in commodiorem et oportuniorem commoditatem differre, interim de illo convenienti loco constituendo dispiciam.« Atque alia eiuscemodi, quae nunc praetereo, ut spem magnam conceperim, Caesarem non ita aversurum me meaque studia, quemadmodum in Dania factum est. Curo autem per filium meum TYCHONEM, addito uno vel altero comite, familiam meam muliebrem huc Wandesburgo evocari, missis meis curribus, qui eam cum supellectile necessaria advehant; eo enim regredi, nisi forte pro disponendis residuis rebus, ijsque alio mittendis, non est animus. Sat Boreae datum, utinam non nimium, sed tribuendum erat aliquid patriae, quam diu id fieri potuit.</p><p>FRANCISCUS meus TEGNAGLIUS cum ego Dresda huc concederem, in Italiam abijt, illic aliquantulum mansurus linguae discendae causa; eum Principi Venetorum, Duci Parmensi, et magno Duci Hetruriae atque Florentiae per Literas commendavi, missis ad singulos eorum ijs, quos Regi nostro offerre volui, Libris, quod tamen an factum sit, ignoro. Itidem feci apud MAGINUM Mathematicum. Quin et Elector Coloniensis illum in mei gratiam, et a me rogatus, ijsdem Italiae Principibus, excepto Veneto, Italicis Literis, quarum hîc exemplum habes, commendavit, ut sperem res eius bono loco istic futuras. Cum mihi scripseris, quod Rostochio ad D. CHRISTOPHORUM STURTIUM I. C. directis literis facile efficies, singulis duabus, uti scis, septimanis huc inde veniente auriga, de omnibus, quae me meaque negotia concernere nosti, luculenter commonefacias velim, idque solito saepius, cum nunc maior, interea dum hîc sum, concedatur oportunitas, nisi mare apud vos congelatur. Carmina in mei gratiam ab eximijs Poëtis ad Caesarem congratulationis caussa concinnata, tum quoque ad me brevi videbis, otium ea describendi in hac festinatione non supererat, mitto tamen exemplum CHYTRAeI Rostochiensis, quod nuper Dresdae accepi, siquidem illud in promptu erat, isthic ob POLICARPUM sic volentem ex autographo immutatum. Sed ille ob festinum meum inde discessum illud necdum accepit, est alias vir humanissimus, satisque Politicus, meque, dum ibi essem, honorifice excepit. Sum etiam benigne habitus a Duce Administratore, qui me ad se invitavit, posteaque ad iuniores Principes, donata deinde eleganti torque aurea cum sua Effigie; quin et alibi a Principibus Germaniae clementer acceptus fui, qui, ut eos in reditu inviserem, rogarunt, spe etiam tacite facta, quod si Caesar mihi benefacere velit, eos etiam suo officio non defuturos. Quomodo Coloniensis me meaque Caesari commendavit, vel potius illi mea de caussa congratulatus sit, et quid in mei gratiam ad eundem Caesarem Dux ULDARICUS Avus maternus Regis Daniae scripserit, exempla earum literarum, quas ROSENKRANTZIO, aut etiam tibi, si recte memini, communicavi, edocebunt. Dedit etiam ad me Princeps MAURITIUS humanissimas literas, in quibus non pro autoritate Principum agit, sed me pro amico bono habet et sic nuncupat. Ex literis TEGNAGLIJ Amsterodamo de ijs, quae in legatione eius evenerunt, antequam Wandesburgum ad me rediret, perscriptis, haec et plura cognosces, ubi eas describere vacabit, tibique mittere. Sed desino plura, cum charta vix plura ferat.</p><p>Tu itaque, mi optime KRAGIE, cum coniuge a me salutata, liberisque vestris felicissime vale.<br>Dabantur Wittebergae die 5. Decembris Anno 1598.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO NIELS KRAG.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>While I was still at <b>Dresden</b>, a few days ago I received your letter, most excellent and most loving Lord Doctor Niels Krag, which had been sent from Copenhagen on the Kalends of October [1 October] of this year. You indicate at the outset that you did not receive mine, which our <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> took with him into Jutland in your absence; I wonder, then, that he kept them so long. He had thought, indeed, that you would visit him at Rosenholm (as perhaps you had promised him); but I had written him that he would wait for you in vain, for it is as rare for a Jutlander to tell a Jutlander truth and keep faith as for augur to augur — nor, indeed, was I wrong in this augury, to my own inconvenience, since most things were in that bundle addressed to you which I longed for you to know in good time. Yet I hope you have now received it, and perceived all its contents; wherefore I ask that you answer me in good time about them, especially where there is need; and at the same time <b>indicate what was done with those letters which Johannes Stygius reported he had written to someone in Denmark on my account, and how they were understood and received</b>. What you touch on, in your last, about my <b>Praefect of Hven</b> [the island-governor], I care little for: he is found not only unfaithful toward me, and to render unfair accounts of his stewardship, but moreover executes almost none of the things I order him several times by letter — not without grave loss of my affairs — so that some of my instruments and remaining furniture are still on the Island, or even at Copenhagen, which I have not yet been able to receive at Lübeck, though I have written several times of this, and <b>Mule</b>, when present, explained each thing to him by mouth. I say nothing of the fact that I can get no fish from so many of my fishponds (costing so much), whether salted or dried, as I wished — but he uses them, or rather abuses them, only for his own and his people's benefit. But what is to be done about him, let <b>Eske Bille</b> look to and decide, to whom I have entrusted the supreme oversight of my affairs. As to what you say — that there are some who wish my Island taken from me, my condition to be worse — I do not understand what these words mean, for they are too obscure, and can be drawn into various senses. Since all Denmark is reckoned too cramped for me, how, pray, will so narrow and unprofitable a little island detain me, who aspire and hope for fewer and far greater things? But let my hater and enviar turn himself whatever way he will — whoever he be at last — he will not much triumph over me, but rather <b>break his tooth on the solid, deluded by his own Art</b>. And indeed I am not greatly solicitous about that Island, nor do I have any profit from it, but rather more loss; nor yet can they take it from me by any right, <b>King Frederick's letters being valid</b>, nor is it allowed to be done without the judgement and consent of all the Senators — unless, indeed, anything be allowed beyond right and divine law; I must also be lawfully cited. But let them only restore me my expenses, or at least half of them, which I spent on it beyond the sum I received from King Frederick and the Realm's Senators — or let them decree <b>whether it is fair that I should suffer such loss in vain</b> [Danish]. But of these we shall treat another time, when the state of my affairs is in safer and impregnable terms — which I trust I shall shortly attain, the supreme Godhead favouring. There will perhaps be some more powerful one, who will take my patronage upon himself, and vindicate me from so many injuries and losses as I have suffered in Denmark.</p><p>But since you desire to be more certainly informed of my affairs, take it thus: <b>A little after Michaelmas [29 Sept] I undertook the journey from Wandsbek toward Prague, to the Emperor — my womenfolk accompanying me as far as Magdeburg, then returning</b>. I halted at Magdeburg for eight days, for the sake of our <b>Erik Lange</b>, who was with me there for five days; afterward I set out for <b>Dresden</b>, where, when I had learned for certain that <b>the Emperor, having left Prague on account of the epidemic diseases, had withdrawn into a safer place seven miles distant thence, and admitted no one but a very few daily servants; that the Court was dispersed, the Chancellery removed, all Councils and Courts silent — in sum, that there was a suspension of justice [<i>iustitium</i>], and all transactions thrown into confusion by the popular contagion</b> — I judged it more advisable, by letters sent to Prague to our <b>Thaddaeus [Hagecius]</b>, to fish out whether in such a state of affairs I should proceed further, and meanwhile to await the answer at Dresden. Thaddaeus, having received my letters to this effect, at once showed them to the Imperial Vice-Chancellor <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b>, who had remained at Prague, to read; he sent a copy of them straightway to the Emperor, asking <b>Barvitius</b> to write back something certain about the Emperor's will as soon as could be. And since this dragged on a while (as the things are slow that must be expedited with Magnates, especially at that time, when letters brought from Prague were not read, for fear of infection, until after an interval of several days), it came about that I had to await the answer at Dresden into the fourth week — Thaddaeus meanwhile even more impatient of the delay than I myself, urging the answer again and again, and as it were expostulating with Barvitius about it, and reproaching him with <b>Jacob Curtius</b>, his predecessor (who would have done the things concerning me much sooner), and at last writing a note about this to Lord Coraduz, who was still at Prague. It happened, however, that on that very day Secretary Barvitius's letter reached Coraduz, in which was contained the Emperor's resolution — which Coraduz straightway subscribed in his own hand to Thaddaeus's note, and, secured with his seal, sent down to Thaddaeus, to inform me thence of the Emperor's will. And what were the formal words of those [resolutions] of which I spoke, you will perceive from the adjoined paper.</p><p>I, then, having received this most clement response of the Emperor's, wrote back that I gladly acquiesce in it; for I had earlier signified to Thaddaeus by letter that I would stay in upper Saxony, rather than at Dresden, until those diseases raging popularly in Bohemia should abate, and it should be more convenient for the Emperor to call me thither; for it was not without danger then to enter, with my youthful retinue, those regions infected everywhere with plague and dysentery. <b>So I withdrew with my [household] hither to Wittenberg, where I have resolved, God willing, to winter</b> — unless I am first summoned by the Emperor, the epidemic plague there laid to rest; and it will be convenient for me here to complete in print my works not yet quite finished at Hamburg (on account of the unskillfulness and scarcity of the Printers). <b>I dwell here in the house which was once that of the man of best remembrance, Philip Melanchthon — now Caspar Peucer's — its tenant, Lord Doctor Johannes Jessenius, an eminent Philosopher and Physician, having just vacated it; who received me most gladly, nay not without joy, and kindly granted the chief part of the house for me and my family to inhabit as long as I please.</b> There is convenience here for continuing observations, and I have at hand here certain of my Instruments which I resolved to take with me into Bohemia. There is also a place for a <b>Pyronomic [alchemical] laboratory</b>, in which I intend to prepare some things while I am here, besides those previously made at Wandsbek which I have at hand. For <b>the Emperor is greatly delighted by such things, and handles them with his own hands, no less than the Mathematical [arts], which he well understands and prefers to others. And so they say he is held by a great desire for me</b> — so much that he wished to summon me to the place to which he has now retired (called <b>Poděbrady</b>), that I might be with him there daily, an ample lodging being already prepared for me and mine; but, the plague's vehemence increasing in that neighbourhood (so that some brute animals were infected), he thought of changing his place — so as to wish to withdraw either into Silesia, or Styria, or even toward Innsbruck, leaving Bohemia, provided it were safe, and permitted by the native Nobility. And hence too it came about that the answer about me was delayed a while, and that I received such a one as you see in the paper. There was with me at Dresden Doctor <b>Johann Gödelmann</b>, a Saxon councillor, <b>Chytraeus's son-in-law</b>, on those very days when I had to leave Dresden; he told me, among other things, that he had heard at Prague (whence he had lately come), from trustworthy [men], that the Emperor so desired my arrival that he pronounced these words: <i>\"If only I had that man here with me, with whom I could pleasantly confer about Mathematical and Astronomical matters, and Pyronomic ones too, and peruse his works, so great a tedium of time would not seize me, as it now does, while I am away from Prague. But what shall I do, if I summon him here at once, and I myself must depart elsewhere? I do not consult well for him and his retinue, especially at this dangerous time. It is better, therefore, to defer it to a more convenient and opportune occasion; meanwhile I will look into establishing a fit place for him.\"</i> And other such things, which I now pass over — so that I have conceived a great hope that the Emperor will not so turn away me and my studies, as was done in Denmark. I am arranging, through my son <b>Tycho</b> (with one or two companions added), that my womenfolk be summoned hither from Wandsbek, my wagons being sent to bring them with the necessary furniture; for I have no mind to return thither, except perhaps to arrange the remaining things and send them elsewhere. <b>Enough has been given to Boreas [the North] — would it were not too much; but something had to be granted to the fatherland, as long as it could be done.</b></p><p>My <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, when I came hither from Dresden, went off into <b>Italy</b>, to stay there a while for the sake of learning the language; <b>I commended him by letter to the Prince of Venice [the Doge], the Duke of Parma, and the Grand Duke of Tuscany and Florence — sending to each of them the Books which I wished to offer our King</b> — though whether it has been done, I know not. Likewise I did with <b>Magini</b> the Mathematician. Nay, and <b>the Elector of Cologne</b>, for my sake and at my request, commended him to the same Princes of Italy (except the Venetian) by Italian letters — a copy of which you have here — so that I hope his affairs there will be in good place. Since you have written me that you can easily effect [delivery] by letters directed to Rostock, to Dr. <b>Christoph Sturtz</b>, jurist — a wagon coming hither and thither (as you know) every two weeks — I would have you inform me clearly about all the things you know to concern me and my affairs, and that more often than usual, since a greater opportunity is now granted while I am here, unless the sea freezes with you. The poems composed in my honour, by distinguished Poets, to the Emperor for congratulation's sake — and also to me — you will see shortly; the leisure for copying them out in this haste was not left over; yet I send a specimen of <b>Chytraeus the Rostocker's</b>, which I lately received at Dresden (since it was ready), there altered from the autograph at <b>Polycarpus's</b> wish. But he [Chytraeus] has not yet received it, on account of my hasty departure thence; he is otherwise a most humane man, and sufficiently Politic, and received me honourably while I was there. I was also kindly entertained by the <b>Duke Administrator</b> [of Saxony], who invited me to him, and afterward by the younger Princes, an elegant gold chain with his Effigy being then given me; nay, and elsewhere I was clemently received by the <b>Princes of Germany</b>, who asked that I visit them on my return, with a tacit hope too given that, if the Emperor should wish to benefit me, they too would not fail their office. How the Cologne [Elector] commended me and mine to the Emperor — or rather congratulated him on my account — and what <b>Duke Ulrich, the maternal grandfather of the King of Denmark</b>, wrote to the same Emperor for my sake, the copies of those letters (which I communicated to Rosenkrantz, or also to you, if I remember rightly) will teach. <b>Prince Maurice [of Nassau]</b> also gave me most humane letters, in which he does not act by the authority of Princes, but holds me for a good friend, and so names me. From Tengnagel's letters written from Amsterdam, about the things that happened in his legation before he returned to me at Wandsbek, you will learn these and more, when there is leisure to copy them and send them to you. But I cease [to add] more, since the paper scarcely bears more.</p><p>So fare you, my excellent Krag, most happily — with your wife, greeted by me, and your children.<br>Given at Wittenberg, the 5th of December, the year 1598.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius)</b>, the Danish royal historian — the counterpart to <b>Krag's reply of 4 Dec 1599 (brahe-t_15991204001)</b>. Written from <b>Wittenberg</b>, where Tycho wintered 1598–99 before the move to Bohemia.</li><li><b>Significance — the fullest account of the 1598 journey, the imperial-summons mechanism, and the Wittenberg sojourn.</b></li><li><b>The journey narrative.</b> <b>A little after Michaelmas 1598, Tycho left Wandsbek for Prague</b>, the women returning from <b>Magdeburg</b> (where he stayed 8 days for <b>Erik Lange</b>); then <b>Dresden</b>, where he learned the Emperor had fled the <b>Bohemian plague</b> (the Court dispersed, a <i>iustitium</i>). The <b>summons mechanism</b> is vividly documented: <b>Hagecius → Coraduz → Barvitius → the Emperor</b>, with the answer delayed four weeks (plague-quarantined letters), Hagecius \"more impatient than Tycho,\" reproaching Barvitius with <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (his predecessor).</li><li><b>★ The Wittenberg lodging.</b> Tycho <b>wintered in Wittenberg, in \"the house once Philip Melanchthon's, now Caspar Peucer's,\" vacated by Johannes Jessenius</b> — with room for <b>observations</b> and a <b>Pyronomic (alchemical) laboratory</b>. (Cf. the Vedel letter, brahe-t_15981230001, from the same Wittenberg winter.)</li><li><b>★ Rudolf II's eagerness (the Gödelmann quote).</b> Via <b>Johann Gödelmann</b> (Chytraeus's son-in-law), Tycho relays the Emperor's own reported words — <i>\"If only I had that man here, with whom I could confer on Mathematical, Astronomical, and Pyronomic matters …\"</i> — a vivid window on Rudolf's <b>alchemical and astronomical eagerness</b> and the reason for the delayed call (the Emperor at <b>Poděbrady</b>, contemplating flight to Silesia/Styria/Innsbruck).</li><li><b>The defiant Hven stance.</b> Tycho dismisses rumours of the <b>island being taken from him</b> (\"all Denmark is too cramped … how will so narrow an island detain me?\"); insists it cannot be taken without <b>King Frederick's letters, the Senators' consent, and a lawful citation</b> — or else his expenses repaid (the Danish tag <i>\"whether it is fair that I should suffer such loss in vain\"</i>). The faithless <b>Hven Praefect</b> (withholding instruments and fish) is left to <b>Eske Bille</b>.</li><li><b>The patronage embassy.</b> <b>Tengnagel went to Italy</b> with Tycho's <b>gift books</b> for the <b>Doge of Venice, the Duke of Parma, the Grand Duke of Tuscany, and Magini</b> — plus the <b>Elector of Cologne's</b> Italian letters of commendation. Tycho recalls the <b>German princes' receptions</b> (a <b>gold chain from the Saxon Duke Administrator</b>), the commendations of <b>Cologne</b> and <b>Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg</b> (Christian IV's grandfather) to the Emperor, and <b>Maurice of Nassau's</b> friendly letters. (Cf. Tengnagel's own report, brahe-t_15980706001.)</li><li><b>The Stygius cross-reference.</b> Tycho asks Krag <b>\"what was done with the letters Johannes Stygius reported writing to someone in Denmark on my account\"</b> — a direct reference to the <b>anti-Tycho letter brahe-t_15980710001</b>; Tycho was aware of the hostile letter-writing.</li><li><b>\"Enough given to Boreas.\"</b> A poignant line on his self-exile: <i>\"Sat Boreae datum, utinam non nimium; but something had to be given to the fatherland, as long as it could be done.\"</i></li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The counterpart to <b>brahe-t_15991204001 (Krag, 4 December 1599)</b>; the <b>exile/journey</b> thread; the <b>Wittenberg winter</b> (brahe-t_15981230001); the <b>Tengnagel patronage-embassy</b> (brahe-t_15980706001); the <b>anti-Tycho Stygius letter</b> (brahe-t_15980710001); the <b>Mechanica/books distribution</b> layer.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Niels Krag; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Johannes Stygius</b>; the <b>Hven Praefect</b> (unnamed); <b>Claus Mule</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b>; <b>King Frederick II</b>; <b>Erik Lange</b>; <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b>; <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b>; <b>Barvitius</b>; <b>Jacob Curtius</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Philip Melanchthon</b>, <b>Caspar Peucer</b>, <b>Johannes Jessenius</b> (the Wittenberg house); <b>Johann Gödelmann</b> (Chytraeus's son-in-law); <b>David Chytraeus</b> &amp; <b>Polycarpus</b> (the poem); the <b>Saxon Duke Administrator</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; the <b>Doge of Venice, Duke of Parma, Grand Duke of Tuscany, Magini, Elector of Cologne, Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Maurice of Nassau</b>; <b>Christoph Sturtz</b> (the Rostock relay); <b>Christian IV</b>. Places: <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Poděbrady</b>, <b>Hven</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Italy</b>, <b>Amsterdam</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Wittebergae die 5. Decembris Anno 1598</i> (Saxon Wittenberg, Old Style; no <i>stylo</i> marker) → <b>5 December 1598 [O.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin (with a Danish clause) read with confidence; the substance (the defiant <b>Hven stance</b> and the faithless Praefect; the <b>Stygius</b> reference; the full <b>journey narrative</b> — Wandsbek/Magdeburg/Erik Lange/Dresden — and the <b>imperial-summons mechanism</b> via Hagecius/Coraduz/Barvitius; the <b>Wittenberg lodging in Melanchthon's house</b> with Jessenius and the alchemical laboratory; the <b>Gödelmann-relayed Rudolf II quote</b>; the <b>family-recall</b> and \"enough given to Boreas\"; and the <b>Tengnagel/Italian-princes/Cologne</b> patronage embassy with the German-prince receptions) is firmly construed and cross-corroborated throughout. The Danish clause and the Rudolf II quotation are rendered faithfully; no textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 7 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.126-132) and collated the full letter, opening 'CUM Dresdae adhuc essem...' through closing 'Dabantur Wittebergae die 5. Decembris Anno 1598.', line by line against source_text and published_english. Checked every proper name (Holgerus Rosenkrantzius, Iohannes Stygius, Escillus Billeus/Eske Bille, Mulaeus/Mule, Erici Langij/Erik Lange, Rudolpho Corraducio, Barvicio, Iacobum Curtium, Ioanne Iessenio, Casparis Peuceri, Goedelmannus, Chytraei, Policarpum, Tegnaglius/Tengnagel, Maginum, Elector Coloniensis, Christophorum Sturtium, Uldaricus, Princeps Mauritius), every number (octiduum=8 days, quinque dies=5, septem miliaribus=7 miles, quartam hebdomadam=4th week, duabus septimanis=2 weeks), every date (5 Dec 1598, Calendis Octobris=1 Oct, Michaelis festum), place (Dresden, Magdeburg, Prague, Wittenberg, Poděbrady, Silesia, Styria, Innsbruck, Rostock, Cologne), and the embedded Danish clause and the quoted Rudolf II speech. Source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly throughout (including the bracketed Danish sentence). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found in the English; all names and figures correctly rendered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Niels Krag", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980820001", "next": "brahe-t_15991204001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15991204001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports receiving Krag's October letter and wonders why Holger retained earlier letters meant for Krag, urging Krag to answer promptly and explain what was done with those letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981205002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981230001", "d": "1598-12-30", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Wittenberg, 30 December 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Excerpter (Afskrift / excerpt-copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET DOCTISSIMO VIRO M. ANDREAe WELLEIO, CANONICO RIPENSI, AMICO SUO ........ ET DILECTO.<br>S. P.</p><p>CUM hic Tabellarius sponte se mihi [of]ferret, qui in Cimbriam ire et Ripas transire constituit, non intermittendum duxi, amantissime Domine Magister ANDREA, quin aliquid saltem literarum ad te darem. Fecissemque idipsum, antequam Wandesburgo discessi, si negotiorum mole et internuncij penuriâ non fuissem praepeditus. De statu autem mearum rerum sic habe. Cum statim a Michaëlis festo inde abijssem Pragam versus cogitans, a Caesare per Dominum CORRADUCIUM Imperij Procancellarium, intermediante etiam D. THADDAeO HAGGECIO, aliquoties eo accitus, accidit ut cum Dresdam venissem, certo intelligerem, omnia Pragae ob luem Epidemicam isthic uti et alibi in Bohemia ingravescentem, conturbata esse, Caesarem inde discessisse, atque in tutiorem locum se recepisse, consiliarios autem hinc inde dispersos, aulamque totam dissipatam ideoque consilia et iudicia publica feriari et iustitium esse. Quare illic paulisper subsistens, misi proprium Tabellarium cum literis, me Dresdam advenisse indicantibus, et quid in tali rerum statu faciendum sit sciscitantibus. Quarum exemplo a CORRADUCIO Caesari, ubi tunc degebat, transmisso, responsum postea est a Domino BARVITIO, supremo Secretario et Caesari ab intimis consiliis, Caesaream Matem perlibenter de meo adventu intellexisse, utque Dresdae vel alibi in vicinia subsistam, donec aula recolligatur, cupere; interea de loco, ubi commode collocari possim, benigne cogitare. Quo responso accepto (cui etiam lubens acquievi, cum periculosum alias foret Bohemiam ingredi) me huc Wittebergam cum meis recepi. Ubi Deo volente hybernare constitui, vel saltem donec a Caesare avocer, quem scio mihi et meis rebus clementer ac serio favere, idque maiore studio et desiderio quam ...nia factum est, spero etiam constant ... Interim vero dum hîc dego, nonnulla quae in operibus meis nondum Typograph[ico] labore absoluta sunt, complere animus est, unâ cum ijs quae Caesari speciatim destinavi. Habito autem hîc Wittebergae in aedibus, quae olim erant optimae recordationis PHILIPPI MELANCHTONIS, nunc PEUCERI, inquilino earum me excipiente Domino Doctore IOHANNE IESSENIO Philosophiae et Medicinae Doctore. Qui ut est vir syncerus et humanus, meique amantissimus, plusquam dimidiam aedium partem pro me et mea familia inhabitandam benigne concessit, ita ut mediocri hîc fruar oportunitate, donec res meae providentia divina uberiorem commoditatem sortitae fuerint. Curo autem nunc adduci Wandesburgo familiam meam muliebrem proprijs curribus, per filium Tychonem, pro qua ......... donec amplior volente Numine a Caesare nobis omnibus concessus fuerit. Cuius in arbitrium totus mi... clauditur orbis. Haec brevibus significare volui ob veterem inter nos necessitudinem, ne rerum nostrarum inscius esses, aut me tui immemorem suspicareris. Forte tamen similia aut his plura a Mag. CHRISTIERNO SEVERINO LONGOMONTANO, meo aliquande domestico, qui etiam me Magdeburgum comitatus est et Gynaecaeum meum idem Wandesburgum reduxit, intellexisti. Relatum enim mihi est eum Ripis nuper fuisse, et meas isthuc ad eum perlatas esse literas, quas illi, quam primum huc veni, destinavi. Scripsi nunc et alteras eidem, sive isthic sive alibi sit, tradendas. Quod si per alios expeditum non fuerit, a te fieri cuperem, ubicunque eum morari nosti. Vale cum tota domo, salutata a me tua coniuge peramanter.</p><p>Per hunc vero Tabellarium ........tius, cum non raro Witebergam [lite]ras mittendi detur occasio, rescribe. Iterumque vale et Praesidi arcis vestrae de me salutem nuntia unâ cum aliis amicis, si quos forte apud vos habeo residuos. Precor vobis omnibus Annum hunc instantem auspicatum et salubrem.</p><p>Iamque tertium et pluries vale.<br>Dabantur Witebergae die penultima Anni 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE</p><p>De terræmotu, qui has Germaniæ oras duabus<br>vicibus conquassavit, die 16. Decembris inter 6 et 7 matutinam, tam hîc quam<br>Magdeburgi et et Lipsiæ atque<br>in alijs hinc inde locis, te inaudivisse opinor. Si quid tale apud vos vel alibi<br>in Dania experti sitis, indica. Existimo<br>enim a Septentrione vel Occasu tremorem illum venisse. Qui tamen in hoc tractu<br>divina clementia nihil damni intulit.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Anders Vedel, Canon of Ribe, his friend ........ and beloved.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Since this courier offered himself to me of his own accord — one who has resolved to go into Cimbria [Jutland] and pass through Ribe — I thought I should not omit, most loving Lord Master Anders, to give you at least some letter. And I would have done this very thing before I departed from Wandsbek, had I not been hindered by the mass of business and the scarcity of a messenger. But of the state of my affairs, take it thus. When, immediately after Michaelmas, I had set out thence, intending [to go] toward <b>Prague</b> — being several times summoned thither by the Emperor through Lord <b>Coraduz</b>, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, with Doctor <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> also mediating — it happened that, when I had come to <b>Dresden</b>, I learned for certain that everything at Prague was thrown into confusion on account of the <b>epidemic plague</b> growing worse there and elsewhere in Bohemia; that the Emperor had departed thence and withdrawn to a safer place; and that the councillors were scattered here and there, and the whole court dispersed, and therefore the public councils and courts on holiday, and there being a cessation of business. Wherefore, staying there a while, I sent my own courier with a letter indicating that I had come to Dresden, and inquiring what should be done in such a state of affairs. A copy of which, transmitted by Coraduz to the Emperor where he then was, was answered afterward by Lord <b>Barvitius</b>, the chief Secretary and [one] of the Emperor's intimate councils: that the Imperial Majesty had learned of my coming most gladly, and desired that I stay at Dresden, or elsewhere in the neighbourhood, until the court reassembles, meanwhile kindly considering a place where I might conveniently be settled. Which answer received (in which I gladly acquiesced, since it would otherwise be dangerous to enter Bohemia), I withdrew hither to <b>Wittenberg</b> with my [household]. Where, God willing, I have resolved to winter — or at least until I am summoned by the Emperor, whom I know favours me and my affairs clemently and seriously, and that with greater zeal and longing than [……], I hope also constant[ly ……]. But meanwhile, while I dwell here, it is my mind to complete some things in my works not yet finished by typographic labour, together with those I have specially destined for the Emperor. And I dwell here at Wittenberg <b>in the house which was once [the house] of Philip Melanchthon, of best memory — now [of] Peucer</b> — its tenant receiving me, Lord Doctor <b>Johann Jessenius</b>, Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine. Who, as he is a sincere and humane man, and most loving of me, kindly granted more than half the house for me and my household to dwell in, so that I enjoy here a moderate convenience, until my affairs, by divine providence, obtain a more abundant accommodation. But I am now having my <b>womenfolk</b> brought from Wandsbek in my own carriages, through my son Tycho, for [whom ……], until a larger [dwelling], God willing, be granted us all by the Emperor — in whose decision my whole world is enclosed. These things I wished to signify briefly, on account of the old bond between us, lest you be ignorant of my affairs, or suspect me forgetful of you. Yet perhaps you have learned similar, or more, from Master <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>, formerly my servant, who also accompanied me to Magdeburg, and brought my womenfolk back to Wandsbek. For it has been reported to me that he was lately at Ribe, and that my letters were brought thither to him, which I sent him as soon as I came hither; I have now written another to the same, to be delivered whether he be there or elsewhere. And if it be not dispatched through others, I should wish it done by you, wherever you know him to be staying. Farewell, with your whole house, your wife being most lovingly greeted by me.</p><p>But by this courier ……, since opportunity of sending letters to Wittenberg is given not rarely, write back. And again farewell; and announce a greeting from me to the <b>Governor of your castle</b>, together with the other friends, if I happen to have any remaining among you. I pray for you all that this coming Year be auspicious and salubrious.</p><p>And now a third time, and many times, farewell.<br>Given at Wittenberg, the penultimate day of the year 1598.<br>TYCHO BRAHE</p><p>Of the earthquake which shook these shores of Germany twice, on the 16th of December between 6 and 7 in the morning, both here and at Magdeburg and Leipzig and in various other places, I suppose you have heard. If you have experienced anything of the kind among yourselves, or elsewhere in Denmark, tell me. For I judge that tremor to have come from the North or the West. Which, however, by divine clemency, brought no damage in this region.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (<i>Andreas Severini Vellejus</i>, 1542–1616), the Danish historian and <b>Canon of Ribe</b> (protocol §5.6) — Tycho's old friend (the addressee also of brahe-t_15710518001, 1571). The MS is a <b>Langebek excerpt-copy</b>, which accounts for the lacunae.</li><li><b>Significance — the 1598 Wittenberg winter (a key biographical letter).</b> Tycho narrates the <b>interrupted journey to Prague</b>:</li><li>Summoned to the imperial court \"immediately after <b>Michaelmas</b> [29 Sept 1598]\" through <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor) with <b>Tadeáš Hájek (Hagecius)</b> mediating, he got as far as <b>Dresden</b>, where he learned the <b>plague</b> had thrown Prague into confusion — the <b>Emperor withdrawn</b> to safety, the <b>court dispersed</b>, public business suspended (<i>iustitium</i>).</li><li>From Dresden he wrote ahead; <b>Barvitius</b> (the chief imperial secretary) answered that <b>Rudolf II</b> welcomed his coming but wished him to wait \"at Dresden or nearby until the court reassembles,\" meanwhile pondering where to settle him.</li><li>So Tycho <b>wintered at Wittenberg</b> — <i>\"in the house once Philip Melanchthon's, now Peucer's,\"</i> whose tenant, <b>Johann Jessenius</b>, gave him <b>more than half the house</b> for his household. (A celebrated detail: Tycho lodged in <b>Melanchthon's former Wittenberg house</b> — now <b>Caspar Peucer's</b>, Melanchthon's son-in-law — as Jessenius's guest; cf. brahe-t_15981231001, the same-period Longomontanus letter.)</li><li>He is completing unfinished printed works and those \"specially destined for the Emperor,\" and having his <b>womenfolk (<i>gynaeceum</i>)</b> brought from Wandsbek by his son Tycho, awaiting \"a larger dwelling … in whose [the Emperor's] decision my whole world is enclosed.\"</li><li><b>Longomontanus at Ribe.</b> Tycho notes that <b>Longomontanus</b> — \"formerly my servant, who accompanied me to Magdeburg and brought my womenfolk back to Wandsbek\" — was lately <b>at Ribe</b>, and asks Vedel to forward an enclosed letter to him (cf. brahe-t_15981231001, brahe-t_15990111001).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Anders Sørensen Vedel; <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Tadeáš Hájek (Hagecius)</b>; <b>Barvitius</b> (chief secretary); <b>Philip Melanchthon</b> (the late owner of the house); <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (its present owner); <b>Johann Jessenius</b> (Tycho's host); <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <b>Governor of [Ribe] castle</b> and Vedel's wife (greeted).</li><li><b>Textual flags (the witness is a Langebek excerpt).</b> Several <b>lacunae/corruptions</b> are preserved with ellipses and bracketed editorial supplements: the address-line gap (<i>amico suo ……</i>); <i>\"si … non fusilem praepedita\"</i> (read as \"had I not been hindered\"); <i>\"maiore studio … quam …nia … spero etiam constant …\"</i>; <i>\"pro qua ………\"</i>; <i>\"totus mi… clauditur orbis\"</i>; <i>\"Per hunc … ……tius … rescribe.\"</i> None obscures the overall sense; left marked, not conjectured.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die penultima Anni 1598</i> = <b>30 December 1598</b>; Wittenberg/Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. The dataset label (\"1598. 30. december. Wittenberg\") agrees.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> An added paragraph of news preceding the letter's closing courier-and-greetings section (which is what the published tail actually preserves) — Tycho reporting a recent German earthquake to Anders Sørensen Vedel and asking whether it was felt in Denmark too. Written from Wittenberg, where Tycho was then encamped in transit from Wandsbek toward Prague.</li><li><b>Crux — \"inaudivisse.\"</b> The verb is <i>inaudio</i>, a later/Neo-Latin near-synonym of <i>audio</i> (\"to hear, hear tell of\"), not a privative (\"not to hear\"); so \"te inaudivisse opinor\" reads \"I suppose you will have heard [of it]\" rather than its surface-tempting negative. Rendered accordingly, but flagged since the <i>in-</i> prefix invites misreading.</li><li><b>Source note.</b> The source text has a doubled conjunction, \"tam hîc quam Magdeburgi et et Lipsiæ\" — reproduced here verbatim and untouched per the house rule; almost certainly a transcription duplication rather than an authorial repetition, but not corrected.</li><li><b>Context.</b> The letter is one of several from this period showing Tycho relaying naturalia (comets, earthquakes) to his correspondents as a matter of course alongside astronomical and personal business; no other corpus source consulted specifically corroborates a 16 December 1598 earthquake at Wittenberg/Magdeburg/Leipzig, so the event itself is reported here only on Tycho's own authority.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'non fusilem praepedita' to 'non fuissem praepeditus' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.131. (A second flagged instance, the doubled 'et et' at the pp.132–133 page-break, was checked against Dreyer and found to be genuinely doubled in the edition of record; left unchanged.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is read securely and the narrative (the Michaelmas-1598 departure for Prague; the plague-confusion learned at Dresden; the Coraduz/Barvitius/Hagecius exchange; the resolve to winter at Wittenberg in Melanchthon's-now-Peucer's house as Jessenius's guest; the womenfolk brought from Wandsbek; Longomontanus at Ribe) is clear, important, and corpus-corroborated; graded <b>[B]</b> solely for the <b>several physical lacunae/corruptions</b> of the Langebek excerpt-copy (the address gap and the half-dozen broken phrases), which are left marked rather than smoothed. Collation against a fuller witness is the route to [A].</p><p>Straightforward, unambiguous Latin; the only interpretive decision of substance is resolving \"inaudivisse\" to its standard Late/Neo-Latin sense rather than the classical-looking privative reading, which is well supported by dictionary usage (<i>inaudio</i> = to hear/hear of) rather than by any external collation of this specific letter. No crux in the sense of corrupt or unreadable text. Upgrade path: check against Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> apparatus for this letter, and against any independent seismological record of a mid-December 1598 tremor in central Germany.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening address through the earthquake postscript) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 131 (top, letter opening) through 133 (top, postscript close, before the following Longomontanus letter begins), read as page images. Checked every proper name (Coraduz, Thaddaeus Hagecius, Barvitius, Philipp Melanchthon/Peucer house, Johann Jessenius, Christen Sørensen Longomontanus) and date (16 December earthquake; 'die penultima Anni 1598' = 30 Dec). Found two TEXT_ERRORs where source_text departs from Dreyer's printed Latin -- a corrupted verb phrase ('fusilem praepedita' for 'fuissem praepeditus') and a duplicated conjunction ('et et') -- both low-severity and, in the first case, not propagated into the English, which correctly renders Dreyer's actual sense. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. Per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C; verdict is MINOR since neither defect changes what a scholar would take away.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text's 'non fusilem praepedita' is a corrupted transcription of Dreyer's grammatical 'non fuissem praepeditus' (pluperfect subjunctive passive, 'had I not been hindered'). The published English matches the correct Dreyer sense, so the corruption did not propagate into the translation, but the Latin as published departs from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>fi negotiorum mole et internuncij penuriâ non fuiffem praepeditus (p.131)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text doubles the conjunction 'et et' where Dreyer has a single 'et' before the page break. A trivial duplication, silently and correctly resolved in the English translation. Dreyer prints: <i>tam hîc quam Magdeburgi et [page break] Lipfiæ atque in alijs hinc inde locis (pp.132-133)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated both defects against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.131-133). The first is a confirmed corruption and was fixed; the published English already carried the correct sense and needed no change. The second defect was rejected on direct inspection: Dreyer's own text carries 'et' at the very end of p.132 and again 'et Lipfiæ' at the start of p.133, so the doubled conjunction in the current file is not an error relative to the edition of record. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 11, "prev": "brahe-t_15980410001", "next": "brahe-t_15990918001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Recounts the journey from Wandsbek toward Prague, halted at Dresden when plague in Bohemia worsened and the Emperor withdrew, having been summoned through Coraduz and Hagecius.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981230001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981231001", "d": "1598-12-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Wittenberg, 31 December 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>QUAS Wandesburgo ad me dedisti literas, eruditissime et amantissime M. CHRISTIERNE, eas hîc Wittebergae accepi penultimo die huius anni. Refers ab initio in ijs de incuria Typographi Hamburgensis, qua quaternionem ibi relictum non saltem non emendatiorem edidit, sed adhuc magis quam antea corrupit, ut et in priori negligens fuit; sed quid faciendum cum talibus hominibus, qui nec fidem servant, nec quae profitentur, satis callent? Constitui igitur duos illos quaterniones de motu Lunae hîc, volente Deo, terse et emendate imprimi curare, ubi et reliquos quatuor Lunam concernentes, quos nunc sub praelo habeo. Ne igitur sis de priori iactura solicitus, et ferendum est, quod averti non potest. Quod vero excusas moram tuam aliquantulam isthic Wandesburgi, apud familiam meam muliebrem, tantum abest ut hoc aegre feram, quin potius tibi gratias habeam, quod id mihi et illis officij praestare volueris, ut non saltem Magdeburgo eos isthuc deduxeris, sed et aliquamdiu cum illis manseris, ne nimis solitariae essent, donec ANDREAS minister reverteretur. Quoniam vero existimo te res tuas domi ordinasse, ut in Germaniam denuo cogites, aut nunc forte eo reversus sis, cuperem ut me de statu rerum tuarum certiorem redderes. Intellexisti ex ijs, quas ad te dedi, quam primum huc veni, me Wittebergae hibernare, volente Numine, constituisse, et quae sint causae cur sic faciam. Si igitur res tuae ullo modo id ferrent, ut me accedere posses, feceris mihi rem apprime gratam, nec tibi ipsi infrugiferam. Cum enim et antea a te intellexerim, et hae tuae literae non obscure insinuent, quod in Academiam aliquam exteram ascendere cupias, ubi secundum conditiones atque aetatem honeste vivere possis, poterit id sane aliquantisper non incommode fieri, donec divina providentia res meae quietum et certum locum in portu exoptato et firmo, concedente uti spero Caesare, obtinuerint. Tunc melius tibi et tuis commodis prospicere non omittam, interim etiam de liberali sustentatione meis sumptibus tibi providebo, nec quicquam tibi deesse patiar, quod oportuna vivendi ratio exigat. Atque ubi praesens fueris (neque enim de tua erga me voluntate aut obsequio quidquam dubito) plenius de ijs, quae tibi usui esse poterint, inter nos agemus. Gratum mihi inprimis est, quod de Apologia Antagonistae mei Scotici refutanda serio cogites, vellem id mature fieri, ut Tomum illum de Cometa anni 77. sic concludere possim, atque interea dum hîc sum, typis absolvere, unâ cum alijs meis operibus antea inceptis, necdum prorsus ad Colophonem deductis. Tu vero mi dilectiss. Domine CHRISTIANE, te in his quam primum resolve; ego tibi et tuis commodis, quantum in me est, nullatenus sum defuturus, sed omni benevolentia et ad meliora semper promovendi amore et animi propensitate prosequar, idque re ipsa potius quam verbis, quam primum se occasio obtulerit, ostendam.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Witebergae ex aedibus ...... (ubi nunc cum Domino D. IESSENIO ... conversor) ultimo die anni completi.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTEN LONGOMONTANUS.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>The letter you sent me from Wandsbek, most learned and most loving Master Christen, I received here at Wittenberg on the penultimate day of this year. You report, at the beginning of it, on the carelessness of the Hamburg printer, by which the gathering left there he not only did not bring out more correct, but corrupted even more than before — as he was negligent in the former one too. But what is to be done with such men, who neither keep faith, nor are skilled enough in what they profess? I have therefore resolved to have those two gatherings on the motion of the Moon printed here, God willing, cleanly and correctly — where [I have] also the remaining four concerning the Moon, which I now have under the press. So do not be anxious about the former loss; and what cannot be averted must be borne. But that you excuse your somewhat long stay there at Wandsbek, with my womenfolk — so far am I from taking this ill that I rather give you thanks, that you were willing to render this service to me and to them: in that you not only conducted them thither from Magdeburg, but also stayed with them a while, lest they be too solitary, until Andreas the servant returned. And since I judge that you have set your affairs in order at home, so as to think of [coming to] Germany again — or perhaps have now returned thither — I should wish you to inform me of the state of your affairs. You understood, from those [letters] I sent you as soon as I came hither, that I had resolved to winter at Wittenberg, God willing, and what the reasons are why I do so. If, therefore, your affairs would in any way allow that you could come to me, you will have done me a thing exceedingly welcome, and not unfruitful for yourself. For since I have understood before from you, and these your letters not obscurely hint, that you desire to advance to some foreign Academy [university], where, according to your circumstances and age, you may live honourably — this can indeed be done conveniently enough for a while, until, by divine providence, my affairs obtain a quiet and certain place in the wished-for and firm harbour, the Emperor granting it, as I hope. Then I shall not omit to provide better for you and your interests; and meanwhile I shall provide for your liberal maintenance at my own expense, nor shall I let anything be wanting to you that a fitting manner of life requires. And when you are present (for I doubt nothing of your goodwill or compliance toward me) we shall treat more fully between us of the things that may be of use to you. It is especially welcome to me that you are seriously thinking of refuting the <i>Apologia</i> of my Scottish antagonist; I should wish it done in good time, so that I may thus conclude that Tome on the Comet of the year [15]77, and meanwhile, while I am here, finish it in print, together with my other works begun earlier, not yet quite brought to the colophon. But do you, my most beloved Lord Christen, resolve yourself in these matters as soon as possible; I, for my part, will in no way fail you and your interests, so far as in me lies, but will pursue you with all benevolence, and with a love and inclination of mind ever to promote you to better things — and this I will show by the deed itself rather than by words, as soon as occasion offers.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Wittenberg, from the house …… (where I now dwell with Lord Doctor Jessenius …) on the last day of the completed year.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (Christian Severin, 1562–1647), Tycho's senior assistant and the most gifted of his <i>familia</i> — here addressed <i>M. Christierne / Domine Christiane</i>, \"Master Christen.\" After years at Hven he had left Tycho's service to settle his affairs in Denmark; this letter presses him to rejoin Tycho in Germany. (One of a close sequence of Tycho→Longomontanus letters from the Wittenberg winter; cf. brahe-t_15990111001, two weeks later.)</li><li><b>The Hamburg printer and the lunar theory (printing history).</b> Longomontanus had reported the <b>incompetence of the Hamburg printer</b>, who botched a <i>quaternio</i> (gathering) of the <b>lunar-theory</b> text — corrupting it further instead of correcting it. Tycho resolves to reprint <b>those two gatherings <i>de motu Lunae</b></i> at <b>Wittenberg</b>, where he already has \"the remaining four concerning the Moon … under the press.\" This is the printing of the <b>lunar theory</b> for the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (the great work then dragging toward completion); \"do not be anxious about the former loss — what cannot be averted must be borne.\"</li><li><b>Longomontanus's service to Tycho's household.</b> Tycho warmly thanks him for escorting his <b>womenfolk</b> (<i>familia muliebris</i> — wife Kirsten and the daughters) <b>from Magdeburg to Wandsbek</b> and staying with them \"lest they be too solitary, until <b>Andreas</b> the servant returned\" — a humane domestic glimpse of the exile years.</li><li><b>The offer of patronage.</b> Tycho, wintering at Wittenberg and awaiting (as he hopes) a settled \"harbour\" under the <b>Emperor</b>, urges Longomontanus to come to him: he may meanwhile \"advance to some foreign <b>Academy</b>\" suited to his age and station, while Tycho <b>maintains him liberally at his own expense</b> and promises to \"provide better\" once his own affairs are secure. The \"wished-for and firm harbour … the Emperor granting it\" anticipates the move to Bohemia.</li><li><b>The Craig controversy (cometary polemic).</b> <i>Apologia Antagonistae mei Scotici</i> = the <b>Apologia of John Craig</b> (<i>Joannes Craigius</i>), the Scottish mathematician who had disputed claims in Tycho's <i>\"Tome on the Comet of 1577\"</i> — i.e. the second volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>-complex, <b>_De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis_</b> (on the comet of 1577). Tycho is delighted that <b>Longomontanus will write the refutation</b>, so that he can <b>conclude and print that Tome</b> while at Wittenberg, \"together with my other works … not yet brought to the colophon.\" (The Craig exchange runs through the 1589–90 correspondence, e.g. brahe-t_15900125001.)</li><li><b>Lodging with Jessenius.</b> <i>cum Domino D. Jessenio … conversor</i> — Tycho records that at Wittenberg he is <b>living with Johannes Jessenius</b> (the anatomist), the same friend who recurs in brahe-t_16000327002 and brahe-t_16001008001.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Longomontanus; the Hamburg printer (unnamed); Tycho's womenfolk; <b>Andreas</b> the servant; <b>John Craig</b> (the \"Scottish antagonist\"); <b>Johannes Jessenius</b>; the <b>Emperor</b> (Rudolf II).</li><li><b>Lacunae (flagged).</b> The dateline is defective in the witness (a Langebek <i>afskrift</i>): the <b>house-name is lost</b> (<i>ex aedibus ……</i>) and there is a gap in the parenthesis (<i>cum Domino D. Jessenio … conversor</i>); marked with ellipses, not conjectured.</li><li><b>Calendar / date (protocol §3).</b> <i>ultimo die anni completi</i> = <b>the last day of the completed year = 31 December 1598</b>; received by Tycho <i>penultimo die huius anni</i> (30 December). Wittenberg/Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. The dataset `label` gives only the place (\"Wittenberg\") with <b>no date</b>; the date is supplied from the dateline and slug (15981231001) — noted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is fluent and fully construable; the matters (the Hamburg printer's bungling of the lunar-theory gatherings and the move to print them at Wittenberg; Longomontanus's escort of Tycho's household and the offer to maintain him; the commission to refute John Craig's <i>Apologia</i> so as to finish the comet-of-1577 Tome; Tycho lodging with Jessenius) are all clear and corpus-corroborated. The only defects are the <b>physical lacunae in the dateline</b> (house-name and a parenthetical word), left marked rather than smoothed, and the label's missing date (supplied per §3).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.133-134 in full as page images (within the 132-135 slice; p.132 is the tail of the preceding Tycho-Velleius letter and its earthquake postscript, p.135 is the following Tycho-Rosenkrantz letter — the Longomontanus letter itself runs 133-134). Source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word, including 'Wandesburgo/Wandesburgi', the servant ANDREAS, the Apologia Antagonistae Scotici, the Cometa anni 77 reference, and the closing 'Dabantur Witebergae ... ultimo die anni completi. TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.' Published_english renders every clause faithfully with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15980910001", "next": "brahe-t_15990111001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports a Hamburg printer's corruption of the lunar-motion gatherings, plans to reprint them correctly at Wittenberg, and thanks Longomontanus for staying with his household at Wandsbek.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981231001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15981231002", "d": "1598-12-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wittenberg, 31 December 1598 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENIO, INGENIO, GENERE VIRO EXIMIE DECORATO ET CONSPICUO OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO, AMICO CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI INTEGERRIMO. ROSENHOLM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>SCRIPSI tibi, desideratiss. mi OLIGERE, quamprimum huc Witebergam veni, et de statu rerum mearum te certiorem reddidi, quas literas te accepisse existimo, ideoque ijs indicata repetere nolo. Cum vero commodum sese obtulisset hic Tabellarius, qui se etiam Arhusiam venturum aiebat, non intermittendum duxi, quin aliquas saltem denuo ad te darem. Cupio autem per eundem ex te cognoscere, quomodo vivas et valeas, et quid rerum agas, tum quoque, si quid mea interest scire, ut fideliter et amice significes. Ego hîc volente Numine hybernabo, cum Bohemiam nunc ingredi lues Epidemica illic magis magisque invalescens non sinat, Caesare ipso vix ibi locum satis huius contagij convenientem inveniente, ut ob id eum inde excedere animum induxisse nonnulli referant. Familia mea muliebris necdum advenit, pro qua adducenda misi TYCHONEM filium cum equis et curribus meis. Quam primum advenerit, quod intra paucos dies futurum existimo, equos in nundinis Lipsensibus distrahere constitui, ne mihi sint, interea dum hîc subsisto, oneri. Facile, uti spero, alios instante vere recepturus. Interea Typographicum laborem urgeo, ut compleantur Libri mei, in Dania non absoluti, nec etiam prorsus Wandesburgi. Paro adhuc alios pro Caesare, quantum otium hoc ferre poterit.</p><p>Caeterum hoc insuper incidit, quod antea exciderat, ut te interrogarem de duobus illis libris Regi vestro offerendis, an id factum sit, necne. Si exhibiti sunt, cupio scire, per quem, et quomodo excepti, et a quo postea accepti, cum is, uti constat, talibus non afficiatur, quae tamen omnia parum moror, veluti etiam nullam inde gratiam aut affectavi aut speravi, uti neque antea ex tot non sine meo damno meritis ullam promereri potui, quod pariter ex alto negligo. Sin vero hi libri adhuc penes te sunt, cuperem eos bene ab iniuria aëris et pluviarum munitos (quod per lineum cera imbutum facile praestatur) per hunc Tabellarium mihi remitti, quo in quibusdam eos recognoscere et locupletare queam, praesertim quoad stellarum restitutionem, et addito deinde tertio quodam eiusdem formae et magnitudinis, quem etiam Caesari dedico; omnes enim tres, praefixo singulis peculiari Epigrammate, Regi in Comitijs proximae aestatis exhiberi, Deo volente, procurabo, sive per te sive per alium quempiam ex meis, si tibi id non fuerit commodum. Adiungentur, uti spero, quaedam alia ex meis operibus, hisce maiora, ut videant vestrates, si quos ea cura tangat, me isthic non fuisse otiosum, nec tempus inutiliter collocasse, nostrosque labores non ob id negligi aut interire, quod quidam ex ijs eos pessundare conati sint. Habeo etiam alias rationes, cur sic fieri velim, nec censuris contrarijs, undecunque petitis, afficior.</p><p>Cum Dresdae essem, adfuit mihi IVARDUS FRISIUS, fratrem Regis comitatus; is forte tibi nonnulla de me rettulit, aut alijs. Vellem eum satis attentum fuisse, ut adhuc plura referre potuisset, non tam tibi quam ijs. Si memoriam, vel potius facundiam eius inaudivisti, indicatione pande, et quomodo illa intellecta acceptaque fuerint. Scis quid velim, de pluribus etiam, uti et prius rogavi, me (cum tam certus adsit internuntius) commonefacias; Exemplaria omnium librorum meorum, tam alibi quam hîc absolutorum, brevi, favente Deo, obtinebis, ut duorum illorum, quos habuisti, iactura cum foenore resarciatur. Salutavi nuper ex te, quamvis non rogatus, Doctorem HUNNIUM, cum simul in arce, unâ cum alijs Professoribus, a Praeside invitati essemus. Is te vicissim resalutat. Quin et alii hîc Professores, qui te norunt et plurimi faciunt, salutem tibi dicunt. Inter quos etiam est Hospes meus Dominus D. IOHANNES IESSENIUS. Vale cum Coniuge tua, mea Nepte, quam felicissime, et instantem annum, feliciter inchoatum, quam prosperrime etiam absolvite, multosque insuper alios. Et his proximis det Deus, ut vobis ludat ROSENKRANTZIUS [in] aula Parvulus. Saluta Matrem, coniugem, et amicos meos, si quos absentia mei adhuc memores esse admittit.</p><p>Dabantur properanter Witeberga, pridie novi Anni 1599.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man eminently adorned and conspicuous in character, talent, and birth, Holger Rosenkrantz, [my] most upright friend, kinsman, [and] relative. [At] Rosenholm.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>I wrote to you, my most longed-for Holger, as soon as I came hither to Wittenberg, and informed you of the state of my affairs — which letter I think you have received, and so I will not repeat what was indicated in it. But since this courier offered himself conveniently, who said he would also come to <b>Aarhus</b>, I thought I should not omit to give you at least some [letter] again. I desire, by the same [courier], to learn from you how you live and fare, and what you are doing; and also, if there be anything it concerns me to know, that you faithfully and amicably signify it. I will here, God willing, winter, since the <b>epidemic plague</b> — growing ever stronger there — does not now allow me to enter Bohemia, the Emperor himself scarcely finding there a place sufficiently free of this contagion (so that some report he has resolved to depart thence). My <b>womenfolk</b> have not yet arrived, for whose bringing I sent my son Tycho with my horses and carriages; as soon as they arrive, which I think will be within a few days, I have resolved to sell the horses at the <b>Leipzig fair</b>, lest they be a burden to me while I stay here — hoping easily to get others in the coming spring. Meanwhile I press the typographic labour, that my books may be completed (not finished in Denmark, nor quite at Wandsbek); and I am still preparing others for the Emperor, as far as this leisure will allow.</p><p>For the rest, this moreover occurs to me, which had earlier slipped [my mind]: that I should ask you about <b>those two books to be offered to your King</b> — whether it was done or not. If they were presented, I desire to know by whom, and how received, and by whom afterward taken — since he, as is well known, is not affected by such things; all which, however, I little regard, just as I neither sought nor hoped for any favour thence (as neither before could I earn any from so many merits, not without my loss) — which I likewise disregard from on high. But if these books are still with you, I should wish them — well protected from the injury of air and rains (which is easily done with linen soaked in wax) — sent back to me by this courier, so that I may revise and enrich them in certain [respects], <b>especially as regards the restitution of the stars</b>; and then, <b>a third of the same form and size being added — which I also dedicate to the Emperor</b> — for all three, a special <b>Epigram</b> prefixed to each, I will, God willing, have <b>presented to the King at the Diet of next summer</b>, whether through you or through some other of mine, if it be not convenient for you. There will be added, as I hope, certain other of my works, greater than these, that your countrymen — if any be touched by that care — may see that I have not been idle abroad, nor spent my time uselessly, and that our labours are not neglected or perishing because <b>some of them tried to ruin them</b>. I have also other reasons why I wish it so done; nor am I moved by contrary censures, sought from wherever.</p><p>When I was at <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Ivar Friis</b> was with me, accompanying the King's brother; he perhaps reported some things about me to you, or to others. I could wish he had been attentive enough to report yet more — not so much to you as to those [others]. If you have heard of his memory — or rather his eloquence — disclose it by some indication, and how those things were understood and received. You know what I wish; about more things too, as I asked before, remind me (since so sure a messenger is at hand). You will soon, God favouring, obtain copies of all my books, finished both elsewhere and here, so that the loss of those two you had may be repaid with interest. I lately greeted <b>Doctor Hunnius</b> on your behalf, though unasked, when we were together invited to the castle, with other Professors, by the Governor; he greets you back. Nay, other Professors here too, who know you and esteem you greatly, send you greeting — among whom is also my host, Lord Doctor <b>Johann Jessenius</b>. Farewell, with your wife, my niece, most happily; and complete the coming year, happily begun, as prosperously as may be, and many others besides. And in these next [years] may God grant that <b>a little Rosenkrantz may play for you in the hall</b>. Greet your mother, wife, and my friends, if my absence allows any to be still mindful of me.</p><p>Given in haste from Wittenberg, the day before the new year 1599.<br>Your Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; date.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> at <b>Rosenholm</b>, addressed with the alliterative flourish <i>Genio, Ingenio, Genere</i> (\"in character, talent, and birth\"). Dated <i>pridie novi Anni 1599</i> = <b>31 December 1598</b> — one of the three same-day Wittenberg letters (with brahe-t_15981231001 to Longomontanus and brahe-t_15981230001 to Vedel, 30 Dec). The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.</li><li><b>The Wittenberg winter.</b> Tycho confirms he is <b>wintering at Wittenberg</b> because the <b>plague</b> bars Bohemia (even the Emperor \"scarcely finding a place free of contagion,\" reportedly about to leave); his <b>womenfolk</b> are being brought by his son Tycho with horses and carriages (to be sold at the <b>Leipzig fair</b>); he presses the <b>printing</b> of his unfinished books and prepares \"others for the Emperor.\"</li><li><b>Significance — the two books the Danish King kept, and the three-book Diet plan (cross-ref to brahe-t_15990920001).</b> Tycho asks whether <b>the two books offered to King Christian IV</b> were ever presented, and how received — noting drily that the King \"is not affected by such things,\" and that he himself \"neither sought nor hoped for any favour.\" (This is the same pair of books that, per <b>Henrik Ramel's letter to Sophie Brahe</b>, brahe-t_15990920001, <b>the King retained \"because your dear brother had given them\" though he cared little for them</b>.) If the books are <b>still with Holger</b>, Tycho wants them <b>sent back</b> (wrapped in wax-soaked linen against the weather) so he can <b>revise and enrich them — \"especially as to the restitution of the stars\" — add a third of the same format dedicated to the Emperor</b>, prefix a <b>special epigram</b> to each, and have <b>all three presented to the King at next summer's Diet</b>, alongside greater works — \"that your countrymen … may see that I have not been idle abroad … and that our labours do not perish because some tried to ruin them\" (a pointed allusion to his Danish detractors).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; news.</b> <i>Ivardus Frisius</i> = <b>Ivar Friis</b>, who at <b>Dresden</b> had accompanied <b>the Danish King's brother</b> and may have reported on Tycho; <i>Doctor Hunnius</i> = <b>Aegidius Hunnius</b> (1550–1603), the Wittenberg theologian, greeted at a castle dinner given by the Governor; <b>Johann Jessenius</b>, Tycho's host at Wittenberg (cf. brahe-t_15981230001, brahe-t_15981231001). The closing wish — <i>\"may a little Rosenkrantz play for you in the hall\"</i> — hopes for a son for Holger and his wife (<b>Tycho's niece</b>, Sophie Brahe, Axel Brahe's daughter).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Witeberga, pridie novi Anni 1599</i> = <b>31 December 1598</b>; Wittenberg/Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. (The dataset label \"1598. 31. december\" agrees.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the Wittenberg winter and the plague-barred Bohemia; the horses to be sold at Leipzig; the printing; <b>the two royal-gift books and the plan to revise them, add a third for the Emperor, and present all three at the Danish Diet</b> — dovetailing with brahe-t_15990920001; Ivar Friis at Dresden; the Wittenberg greetings via Hunnius and Jessenius; the wish for a Rosenkrantz heir) is clear and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening address through 'Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.') against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 134 (bottom, letter opening) - 135 (close, before the next Longomontanus letter begins on p.136), read as page images. Checked every proper name (Tycho filius, Ivardus Frisius, Doctor Hunnius, Johannes Iessenius, Rosenkrantzius), place (Wittenberg/Witeberga, Dresden, Leipzig fair/nundinis Lipsensibus, Wandsbek), and date (pridie novi Anni 1599 = 31 Dec 1598 O.S.). Source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout. One stylistic point noted but not counted as a defect: the address's 'AMICO CONSANGVINEO, AFFINI INTEGERRIMO' places 'integerrimo' grammatically closest to 'affini,' while the English ('[my] most upright friend, kinsman, [and] relative') applies it to the whole triad -- this is word-order/register, explicitly out of scope per the brief. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found. Published grade A confirmed by direct Dreyer collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 18, "prev": "brahe-t_15981205001", "next": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15971216001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990307001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports wintering at Wittenberg because epidemic plague prevents travel to Bohemia, notes the Emperor is similarly displaced, and asks Rosenkrantz for news from Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15981231002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990000001", "d": "1599-00-00", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Florence", "t": "Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany → Tycho Brahe — [Florence], 1599 [date not given]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII, p. 156 · MS witness: A = Florence, Archivio Mediceo, vol. 65</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>FERDINANDUS MAGNUS ETRURIAe DUX AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>VIR Illris. Gratissimae fuerunt mihi literae Dominationis Tuae, tum quod praecipuum erga me praeseferant amorem atque benevolentiam, tum etiam quod per eas occasionem oblatam video mutuae voluntatis meae aliquo erga eius Dm officij genere declarandae. Nam licet ipsa mihi de facie, ut eius quoque testantur literae, ignota fuerit, ea tamen, quae de ipsius nomine ac virtute circunferuntur, Dm Tm non minus mihi claram quam notam reddere potuerunt. Sed quando ipsa fama ad id non sufficeret, hanc tamen ex ijs duobus voluminibus quae ad me misit, et in quibus vera hominis species innotescit, abunde consequi potuissem. Cuius quidem muneris pro eo ac debeo Di Tae gratias ago. Illud enim propter subiectae rei et novi operis rationem charissimum mihi fuit, et si quid otij dabitur aliquando, utar etiam ad illud opus visendum, alicuius in ea re versato legentis opera. Et si illud commode fieri poterit, ut reliqua Dis T. opera, qualiacunque hactenus in lucem prodierunt aut in posterum prodibunt, ad me transmittantur, pro certo habeat in loco me non exigui muneris habiturum. Quod vero ad illum adolescentem attinet, quem mihi maxime commendavit, dabo operam, quemadmodum etiam hactenus factum est, ut et ipse intelligat, quanti ponderis apud me fuerit Dis Tae commendatio, et ipsa quoque cognoscat posse se ac debere non in hac solummodo re, sed in quacunque alia, quae videri possit ad eius Dis existimationem et commodum pertinere, omnia a me semper officia polliceri. Quod reliquum est, Deum opt. max. praecor ut Dm Tm diutius servet incolumem, quo de praeclarissimis artibus ac literarum studijs benemereri longius possit.</p><p>Datum .......</p>", "en": "<p><b>Ferdinand, Grand Duke of Tuscany, to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Illustrious Man. Your Lordship's letters were most pleasing to me — both because they bear before [me] a special love and benevolence toward me, and also because through them I see an occasion offered for declaring my mutual goodwill toward your Lordship by some kind of office. For although you have been unknown to me by face — as your letters too attest — yet those things which are circulated about your name and virtue have been able to render your Lordship to me no less illustrious than known. But even if fame itself did not suffice for that, I could nonetheless have abundantly attained it from those two volumes which you sent me, and in which the true likeness of the man becomes known. For which gift, indeed, I give your Lordship thanks, as I ought. For it was most dear to me, on account of the nature of the subject-matter and of the new work; and if any leisure shall ever be given, I will also use, for viewing that work, the help of some reader versed in that matter. And if it can conveniently be done that the rest of your Lordship's works — whatever have hitherto come into the light, or shall come forth hereafter — be transmitted to me, let him hold for certain that I shall reckon it in the place of no small gift. But as concerns that young man whom he most strongly commended to me, I will give effort — as also has been done hitherto — both that he himself may understand how much weight your Lordship's recommendation had with me, and that your Lordship too may know that you can and ought to promise yourself all good offices from me always — not in this matter only, but in any other which may seem to pertain to your Lordship's estimation and advantage. For what remains, I pray God Best and Greatest to preserve your Lordship long unharmed, that you may be able the longer to deserve well of the most excellent arts and the studies of letters.</p><p>Given …….</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>The Grand Duke's reply.</b> This is <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici</b>'s gracious answer to Tycho's recommendation-and-book-gift of 1598 (brahe-t_15981108001). He thanks Tycho for the <b>two volumes</b> sent (\"in which the true likeness of the man becomes known\"), asks that <b>the rest of Tycho's works</b>, past and future, be sent too, and confirms his patronage of <b>the young man</b> Tycho had recommended. The witness survives in the <b>Medici archive</b> at Florence (Archivio Mediceo, vol. 65).</li><li><b><i>duo volumina</b></i> = the two of Tycho's printed works given in 1598 (see brahe-t_15981108001 apparatus); <i>ille adolescens, quem mihi commendavit</i> = the recommended youth (probably Tengnagel — <i>identification flagged</i> there).</li><li><b>Chancery third person.</b> The Grand Duke writes of Tycho in the third person throughout (<i>Dominatio Tua</i> / <i>eius Dominatio</i> — \"your Lordship\" / \"his Lordship\"), the formula of princely letters; rendered accordingly.</li><li><b>⚠ Date.</b> The source breaks off at <i>Datum …….</i> — <b>the dateline is lost</b> in the witness. The dataset assigns the year <b>1599</b> (slug …0000…, label \"1599, empty\"); the day and month are <b>not recoverable</b> and are not invented. Florence (Tuscany, Catholic) used New Style, but no date survives to mark.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> located at <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII, <b>p. 156</b> (witness <i>E codice Florentino Archivii publici Medicei vol. 65</i>); Dreyer's index pointer \"VIII 154\" is an index error. The lost dateline (<i>Datum ......</i>) is reproduced by Dreyer — genuinely lost in the edition of record, not a transcription gap. <b>Open reading:</b> Dreyer prints <i>praescieras</i> (2nd-sg.) where the working transcription has <i>praeseferant</i> (3rd-pl.); the translation follows <i>praeseferant</i> — flagged for review.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the content (the Grand Duke's thanks for the two volumes, his request for Tycho's other works, his patronage of the recommended youth) is clear and dovetails with brahe-t_15981108001 / …15990000001's Medici context. Grade [B] because the <b>dateline is lost</b> in the source (only the year 1599 is supplied by the catalogue), and the <i>adolescens</i> / <i>duo volumina</i> are the same unnamed items flagged in the 1598 letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.155-157 as page images (letter is wholly on p.156). source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout (opening, closing, every clause). Collated the full published_english translation clause-by-clause against the Latin: greeting, the 'two volumes' gift, the request for future works, the commendation of the young man, and the closing prayer all render faithfully with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss. 'Datum .....' correctly left blank in translation, matching Dreyer.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Archivio pubblico Mediceo vol. 65."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for two volumes sent as a gift, praises the work, and expresses willingness to receive the remaining volumes when opportunity allows.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990109001", "d": "1599-01-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Andreas Meier", "ck": "Andreas Meier", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Andreas Meier — Wittenberg, 9 January 1599", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Renterei-Akten (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EHRNUESTENN VNND ACHTBARNN ANDREASS MEYERN, FURSTLICHEN MECKELBURGISCHENN RENTMEISTERN VND RATH, MEINEM JNSONDERS GUNSTIGEN VNNDT GUTENN FREUNDT. WISMAR.</p><p>MEIN freundlichenn grueß mit wunschung alles guthen zuuor, ehrnuester vnndt achtbar, gunstiger herr renttmeister, besonder guter freundt. Jch kann euch freundtlich nicht bergenn, welchergestalt mir meine elteste tochter anhero zugeschriebenn vnnd zuuernemen gegebenn, daß sie ahnn euch schreibenn vnnd vonn denn baldt betagtenn zinßenn ein zwey hundert taller auffnehmenn lassenn wolte, zweiffell derowegenn nicht, denn das solches beschehenn sein werde, vnnd jhr dargegenn gedachter meiner tochter handtschrifft zuhandenn empfangenn habenn, welche eben souill gelttenn alß wann jchs selbst gethan hette, sollenn auch hernechst wider geburlich gekurtzet werdenn. Jch habe vor etzlichenn wochenn einen eigenen bottenn inn Dennemarckenn abgeferttiget, der meinung, daß mir etzlich geldt von meinen doselbst noch außstehendenn haubtsummen vbersendet werdenn solte. Dieweil es aber itzo vielleicht der winter oder anders was verhindert, kann jch nicht wißenn, wie balt jch dasselbe geldt vberkommenn könne, vnndt jch gleichwoll itziger zeit inn diesenn frembdenn örternn geldes wolbenöttiget, sintemalln jch mich mit denn meinen inn die zwölff personnen starck vnndt souil pferde inn die funff wochen zu Dreßdenn aufgehaltenn, zugeschweigen was mir sonsten auf dem wege gekostet, worvber mein zehrgeldt, welchs jch zur Pragischen reise mitgenommen, aussgangenn, alß ist derowegenn ahn euch mein freundliches bittenn, jhr wollenn mir die freundtschafft beweisenn, vnndt noch zwey hundert thaller mit diesenn wittembergischen fuhrmann Dauidt gegenn vberantworttung meiner quitantz, welche jch ihme zu handen zugestellet, vberschickenn, vnndt jhme dieselbenn, wenn ehr vonn Rostock wider zu ruck auf Gustrow kumpt, empfangenn lassenn, daß ihr es mitlerzeit alda bestellen wollet.</p><p>Die victualienn, welche jch vonn euch empfangenn, belangend, bitte jch mir vnschwer zuerke[nnen] zugebenn, was sie kostenn, sollenn in den zinßen nichtweniger auch abgezogenn vnnd gerechnet werdenn. Jnn diesenn erzeiget ihr mir ahnitzo einen sonderlichenn angenehmen dienst, vnndt jch bin es vmb euch mit wunschung eines glucksehligenn neuwen jahrs jedertzeit hinwider zubeschuldenn geflissenn vnnd bereitwillig, vnndt thue euch göttlicher almacht befehlenn.</p><p>Datum Wittemberg denn 9. januarij a:o 99.<br>Eur a[ller]f[r]w[ndlichster]<br>Tycho Brahe<br>eigenhd.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and worshipful Andreas Meier, princely Mecklenburg rent-master and councillor, my especially favourable and good friend. Wismar.</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with the wish of all good, beforehand. Honourable and worshipful, favourable Lord rent-master, especial good friend: I cannot, in friendship, conceal from you in what manner my eldest daughter has written to me here, and given me to understand, that she meant to write to you and have a couple of hundred thalers taken up from the soon-falling-due interest. I do not doubt, therefore, that this will have been done, and that you have received in return my said daughter's bond to hand — which is worth just as much as if I had given it myself — and these [thalers] shall hereafter be duly deducted [from the account]. Some weeks ago I dispatched a special messenger into Denmark, in the expectation that some money should be sent me from my principal sums still outstanding there. But since now perhaps the winter, or something else, hinders it, I cannot know how soon I may obtain that money; and I am withal, at this time, in these foreign parts, in good need of money — seeing that I have stayed some five weeks at Dresden with my people, some twelve persons strong and as many horses, not to mention what it otherwise cost me on the road, whereby my travel-money, which I took along for the Prague journey, has run out. So my friendly request to you, therefore, is that you would show me the kindness and send me another two hundred thalers by this Wittenberg carter David, against delivery of my receipt (which I have handed to him); and have the same paid to him when he comes back from Rostock to Güstrow — so that you would meanwhile have it made ready there.</p><p>As for the victuals which I received from you, I ask you to let me know, without difficulty, what they cost; they shall, no less, be likewise deducted and reckoned against the interest. In this you now render me an especially welcome service; and I am, with the wish of a happy New Year, at all times zealous and ready to deserve it of you in return. And I commend you to divine omnipotence.</p><p>Given at Wittenberg, the 9th of January, in the year '99.<br>Your most friendly,<br>Tycho Brahe<br>in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; moment.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Wittenberg</b>, January 1599 — mid-migration, between his departure from Denmark (1597) and his establishment in Bohemia (1599). The letter is the <b>earliest</b> of the Tycho↔Meier series and the <b>financial backstory</b> to the later debt crisis: it shows the <i>normal</i> working of the relationship — Tycho drawing cash advances against the interest on capital he had lent the Mecklenburg house, through its rent-master, while travelling.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Andreas Meier</b>, <i>fürstlich-mecklenburgischer Rentmeister und Rat</i> at <b>Wismar</b> — here in routine, cordial dealings, two years before the acrimony of 1601 (brahe-t_16010527001, brahe-t_16010718001).</li><li><b>The transaction.</b></li><li>Tycho's <b>eldest daughter</b> had arranged to draw <b>~200 thalers</b> against the <i>bald betagte Zinsen</i> (\"soon-falling-due interest\"), giving Meier her own <b>bond (<i>Handschrift</i>)</b> — which Tycho ratifies as binding as his own.</li><li>Tycho asks for <b>a further 200 thalers</b> by the <b>Wittenberg carter David</b>, against a receipt, payable when David returns from Rostock to <b>Güstrow</b> — his travel-funds (<i>Zehrgeld</i>) for the Prague journey having run out after a <b>five-week stay at Dresden</b> with a party of \"twelve persons and as many horses.\"</li><li>He had sent a messenger to <b>Denmark</b> to draw on capital still outstanding there, but feared winter would delay it.</li><li>The <b>victuals (<i>Victualien</i>)</b> Meier had supplied are to be costed and set against the interest.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>meine älteste Tochter</i> = Tycho's <b>eldest daughter</b> (Magdalene Brahe), acting on his account.</li><li><i>der wittenbergische Fuhrmann David</i> = the <b>Wittenberg carter David</b>, the courier/cash-bearer.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Wittemberg</i> (place of issue); <i>Wismar</i> (Meier's seat); <i>Dresden</i> (the five-week stop); <i>Rostock</i>, <i>Güstrow</i> (the Mecklenburg payment-points); <i>Dänemark</i> (outstanding capital); the <i>Pragische Reise</i> (the Prague journey ahead).</li><li><b>Formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> Burgher-courteous register; subscription <i>Eur allerfreundlichster</i> (\"Your most friendly\") + <i>eigenhändig</i> (\"in his own hand,\" autograph). <i>Datum</i> → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 9. Januarij a:o 99</i> at Wittenberg; no style marker. Lutheran Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar in 1599; reproduced as written, not converted. (The year is given as \"99.\")</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the approximative hedge of the German 'in die zwölf ... in die fünf Wochen' ('some twelve ... some five weeks'), confirmed idiomatic (not exact) by Dreyer's print (TBOO XIV p.149); the translation had previously stated both as precise counts.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery German; the cash-advance mechanics (the daughter's bond, the 200-thaler draft via carter David, the Dresden stay, the victuals reckoning) are explicit, and the document usefully predates and contextualises the 1601 debt dispute. No crux; the low automatic language-score reflects only the heuristic's known weakness on this register (protocol §2).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated source_text and published_english against Dreyer's page images (TBOO XIV pp.148-151, slice pages 1-4, letter no.157 on pp.149-150) line by line: heading/address, opening, both 200-thaler sums, the messenger-to-Denmark passage, the twelve-persons/five-weeks-at-Dresden clause, the Prague-journey travel money, the carrier David / Rostock / Güstrow arrangement, the victuals paragraph, and the closing/date/signature. source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly (only long-s/ligature typographic variants, which are not defects). All proper names (Andreas Meyer, David, Wismar, Denmark, Dresden, Prague, Rostock, Güstrow, Wittenberg), all numbers (200 thalers twice, twelve persons, five weeks), and the date (9 January, anno 99 = 1599) are rendered correctly in the English with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation. One low-severity hedge-loss found (see defects). No TEXT_ERROR: our source_text agrees with Dreyer's printed Latin/German throughout.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>HEDGE_LOSS</b> (low). German 'in die zwölf' and 'in die fünf Wochen' are approximative idioms ('some twelve', 'about five weeks'); Dreyer's print confirms the same wording, but the English states both as exact figures, dropping the source's hedge. At: “twelve persons strong and as many horses ... I have stayed five weeks at Dresden”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.148-151 (full slice, read in one pass). The relevant clause is confirmed verbatim on p.149, ll.33-35, matching source_text exactly. The single HEDGE_LOSS defect is fixed; nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Renterei-Akten. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Andreas Meier", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980331002", "next": "brahe-t_16001115002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Informs Meier that his eldest daughter has borrowed two hundred thalers against interest, acknowledges the bond given in return, and reports delays in receiving funds from Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990109001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990110001", "d": "1599-01-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "[Andreas Meyer, Mecklenburg rent", "ck": "[Andreas Meyer, Mecklenburg rent", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [Andreas Meyer, Mecklenburg rent-master] — Wittenberg, 10 January 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery; financial quittance) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>· MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Renterei-Akten (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>JCH Tycho Brahe, erbgesessenn zu Knudtstrup im reiche Dennemarckenn vnnd kön: maytt: alda ambtman auf Vraniburgk, vrkunde vnd bekenne hiemit inn crafft dieses, daß jch vonn dem ehrnuesten vnnd achtbarnn Andreaß Meyernn, furstlichenn meckelburgischenn junger herrschafft verordenten rentmeister, zwey hundert reichsthaller zinße, so erst auf schieristkommendenn monnat aprilis dieses neun vnndt neuntzigstenn jahrs betaget sein wurden, heut dato auff rechnung entpfangenn habe. Thue derowegenn gedachtenn herrnn rentmeister solcher woll entpfangenenn zwey hundert thaller, alß werenn dieselbigenn auf obberurtenn termin erleget vnnd betzalet gentzlichenn wie es zu rechte am krefftigstenn vnnd bestendigstenn geschehenn solle, quitirenn vnnd loßsagenn. Zu mehrer vrkundt hab jch diese quitanz mit eig[enen handt] vnterschriben vnnd mein angeborn pitzschafft hierauff gedruckt.</p><p>Actum Wittemberg denn zehenden tag januarij anno 99.<br>Tycho Brahe<br>eigenhd.</p><p>(l.s.)</p>", "en": "<p>I, Tycho Brahe, hereditary lord at Knudstrup in the kingdom of Denmark, and His Royal Majesty's bailiff [<i>Amtmann</i>] there at Uraniborg, attest and acknowledge hereby, by force of this [document], that I have today, on account, received from the honourable and respectable Andreas Meyer — appointed rent-master of the princely Mecklenburg junior lordship — two hundred reichsthaler of interest, which were to fall due only in the next coming month of April of this ninety-ninth year. I therefore acquit and release the said Master rent-master of these two hundred well-received thaler, as though the same had been fully discharged and paid at the aforementioned term — as may, in law, be done in the most effective and binding manner. In further attestation I have signed this quittance with my own hand, and pressed my hereditary seal hereupon.</p><p>Done at Wittenberg, the tenth day of January, in the year [15]99.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p><p>(Place of the seal.)</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> A <b>quittance</b> (<i>Quitanz</i>) — a formal receipt-and-release for money received, in legal chancery German.</li><li><b>Issuer.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, styling himself <i>erbgesessen zu Knudstrup</i> (hereditary lord of Knudstrup) and the King of Denmark's <i>Amtmann</i> (bailiff) at Uraniborg — the titles he still claimed in early 1599, during his self-imposed exile (he had left Hven in 1597 and was at Wittenberg before proceeding to Bohemia).</li><li><b>Recipient / payer.</b> <b>Andreas Meyer</b>, <i>rent-master</i> (<i>Rentmeister</i>, treasury officer) of the <b>\"princely Mecklenburg junior lordship\"</b> (<i>fürstliche Mecklenburgische junge Herrschaft</i>) — i.e. the household of the young Dukes of Mecklenburg. The 200 reichsthaler are <b>interest</b> (<i>Zinse</i>) on a sum Tycho had on loan/deposit there, paid in advance of its April term. The piece survives among the <b>Schwerin treasury records</b> (<i>Renterei-Akten</i>), confirming the Mecklenburg financial tie.</li><li><b><i>angeborn pitzschafft</b></i> = \"innate/hereditary seal\" (<i>angeborenes Petschaft</i>), the family signet. <b><i>(l.s.)</b></i> = <i>locus sigilli</i>, \"place of the seal\" (editorial mark for where the wax seal stood). The bracketed <i>[enen handt]</i> in <i>mit eig[enen handt]</i> is an editorial restoration of a damaged spot in the original; left as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Wittemberg … zehenden tag januarij anno 99</i> = <b>10 January 1599</b>; Wittenberg (Lutheran Saxony) kept the <b>Julian / Old Style</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> German 'aus obberurtenn termin' corrected to 'auf obberurtenn termin' per Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.150; the English clause 'paid from the aforementioned term' corrected to 'paid at the aforementioned term' to follow Dreyer's reading.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a formulaic legal quittance; the German is secure, and the parties (Tycho; Andreas Meyer; the Mecklenburg treasury) and the sum (200 reichsthaler advance interest) are unambiguous. The one bracketed restoration (<i>eig[enen handt]</i>) is editorial, not a crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV p.150 page image in full. Collated the self-styling (Knudstrup/Uraniborg Amtmann), the 200-reichsthaler-interest sum, the April-of-the-99th-year due date, the acquittal formula, the seal/signature attestation, and the Wittenberg/10 January 1599 date against source_text and published_english; the sum, both dates, and every name match exactly. One possible single-letter TEXT_ERROR flagged (auf/aus) with a caveat about reading confidence at this resolution; per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C, but it is low severity and does not change what a scholar would take away from the quittance. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's page reads 'auf obberurtenn termin' (paid at/for the aforementioned term, standard German quittance idiom) where source_text has 'aus' (from/out of); read at this resolution with reasonable but not total confidence. The published_english ('paid from the aforementioned term') follows the source_text's 'aus' rather than Dreyer's 'auf', but the substantive content of the quittance — Tycho acquitting Andreas Meyer of the 200-thaler payment — is unaffected either way. Dreyer prints: <i>alß werenn dieselbigenn auf obberurtenn termin erleget vnnd betzalet gentzlichenn</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against the Dreyer page image, TBOO XIV p.150 (letter no. 158). The single TEXT_ERROR (aus/auf) is confirmed as printed and both the German and its English rendering are corrected accordingly. No other reading on this page or the adjoining pp.149, 151 was found to diverge from current_latin. Nothing remains uncertain.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv Schwerin, Renterei-Akten. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "A formal quittance acknowledging receipt of two hundred reichsthaler in advance interest from rent-master Andreas Meyer, signed and sealed at Wittenberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990110001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990111001", "d": "1599-01-11", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Wittenberg, 11 January 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Dänische Bibliothek </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 14 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO<br>BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>CHARISSIME M. CHRISTIERNE, bis tibi<br>scripsi, postquam huc Witebergam veni,<br>quas literas accepisse te utrasque reor, vel saltem alteras. Cuperem, ut ad me<br>huc accederes, antequam denuo Pragam versus<br>iter suscipiam. Fiet id cum tuo emolumento. Si viatico cares, suppeditabit tibi<br>hic auriga, ego illi restituam. Intellexi ex tuis, antequam Wandesbecho<br>abires, mihi scriptis, conditionem in aliqua Academia te optare hîc in<br>Germania, s[ignifica mihi an]<br>Pragæ isti, qui se Cæsaris Mathematicum iactat,<br>succedere velis (brevi enim ille se subducet, si modo iam factum non<br>sit) sin tibi non placuerit, effi<br>ciam<br>ut ....... eo vocetur, nec is ab [hoc] munere suscipiendo abhorret; [tunc]<br>operam dabo, ut tu hîc Witebergæ in eius locum substituare .......re, cum præsente<br>plura ad ........ quavis alia ratione expete ....... commodis, quantum in me<br>est, studebo.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Raptim Witebergæ inter<br>plurimas occupationes die 11. Ian. Anno 99.</p><p>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu<br>propria.</p><p>Habes hîc literas a BRASCHIO<br>Poëta, Dresda mihi missas et fortuito<br>resignatas.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Christen Longomontanus.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Dearest Master Christen, I have written to you twice since I came here to Wittenberg — both which letters I think you have received, or at least one of them. I could wish that you would come to me here, before I undertake the journey toward Prague again. It will be done to your profit. If you lack travel-money, the carrier here will supply it to you; I will repay him. I understood from your [letter] written to me before you departed from Wandsbek that you desire a position in some Academy here in Germany. [Signify to me whether] you wish to succeed that fellow at Prague who boasts himself the Emperor's Mathematician (for he will shortly withdraw himself, if it has not already been done); but if it should not please you, I will bring it about that […] be summoned thither — and he does not shrink from undertaking [this] office; [then] I will give my effort that you be substituted here at Wittenberg in his place […], since, with you present, [I could say] more things to […]. By whatever other means seek […]; for your advantages, as much as is in me, I will strive.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Hastily, at Wittenberg, amid very many occupations, the 11th day of January, in the year [15]99.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. Here you have letters from Braschius the Poet, sent to me from Dresden, and opened by accident.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (Christian Severin \"Longomontanus,\" 1562–1647), Tycho's longest-serving and ablest Danish assistant, later professor at Copenhagen and author of the <i>Astronomia Danica</i> (1622). At the start of 1599 he had recently left Tycho's service (after the Wandsbek period) and was seeking an academic post in Germany; Tycho here tries to draw him back before departing for Prague.</li><li><b>\"That fellow at Prague who boasts himself the Emperor's Mathematician\"</b> = <b>Nicolai Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>, the Imperial Mathematician and Tycho's plagiarism-adversary (see brahe-t_16000727001). Tycho — confident that Ursus \"will shortly withdraw himself\" (Ursus left Prague in 1599 and died in 1600) — dangles the imperial mathematicianship before Longomontanus, with a fallback scheme to install Longomontanus at Wittenberg instead.</li><li><b><i>viaticum</b></i> — travel-money; <i>auriga</i> — the carter/driver who would advance it.</li><li><b>⚠ Textual lacunae.</b> The witness (a Langebek transcript) is <b>damaged</b>, with several gaps marked by the editor's <i>.......</i> (and one bracketed restoration, <i>s[ignifica mihi an]</i>). The lost passages name (a) the substitute Tycho would have \"summoned\" to Prague in Longomontanus's stead, and (b) the precise Wittenberg arrangement; these are <b>not reconstructed</b> here. Gaps are rendered \"[…]\"; the bracketed editorial restoration is kept as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Witebergae … die 11. Ian. Anno 99</i> = <b>11 January 1599</b>, Wittenberg (Lutheran Saxony) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label. (Written two days after the Mecklenburg quittance brahe-t_15990110001, also from Wittenberg.)</li><li><b>A postscript recovered entire.</b> This letter's existing English translation currently ends flatly at the signature, with no postscript at all; the gap restores the opening of one, enclosing a letter (or letters — Latin \"literas\" is grammatically plural but idiomatically often covers a single letter) from \"Braschius the Poet,\" sent from Dresden, which Tycho says he had opened by accident.</li><li><b>Identifying Braschius.</b> An independent letter from Nicolaus Braschius to Tycho Brahe, dated 8 August 1600, Rostock, is on record (Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23; brahe-t_16000808001 in this corpus) — confirming Braschius as a real, otherwise-attested correspondent active in Tycho's circle in this period, evidently a poet with Saxon/Dresden connections.</li><li><b>The courier detail.</b> \"Fortuito resignatas\" (opened by chance) is a small courtesy-note: Tycho is explaining, to Longomontanus, why an enclosure addressed elsewhere arrives already unsealed.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — the surviving Latin is rendered securely and the situation (luring Longomontanus back; the prospect of succeeding Ursus at Prague; a Wittenberg fallback) is clear and corpus-corroborated; but the witness carries <b>several unrestored lacunae</b> (the name of the proposed Prague substitute and the detail of the Wittenberg placement are lost), which are flagged and left as gaps — hence [C], not [A].</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 136: the gap (the proposed Prague substitute's name) is in the edition of record and absent from <b>both</b> apographs (Langebek <i>Diplomatarium</i> + <i>Dänische Bibliothek</i> IX, 1747, p. 239) — irrecoverable. [C] stands.</p><p>The Latin itself is short and lexically plain (\"habes hic literas... mihi missas et fortuito resignatas\"), but it is only the opening clause of a postscript whose continuation is not part of this repair packet, so the postscript's full content and any instruction to Longomontanus that may have followed cannot be recovered here; the plural \"literas\" is left unresolved (one letter or several) rather than smoothed into a false singular. This matches the packet's own pre-assigned grade of C, which I retain rather than upgrade.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [C].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII p.136 (Dreyer, 'Tycho Brahe ad Christianum Longomontanum') as a page image; letter entire on this page. The Latin is genuinely damaged in the original (Dreyer himself prints '.......' lacunae and bracketed conjectural supplements '[hoc]', '[tunc]', 's[ignifica mihi an]'); our source_text reproduces every lacuna, bracket and conjectural word in the same positions as Dreyer, letter for letter. The English translation handles every gap correctly with matching bracketed/ellipsis markers and does not smuggle in invented content to fill the damaged spans; the intact clauses (travel money, the succession to the 'Caesaris Mathematicum' at Prague, the postscript about Braschius' letter from Dresden) are all rendered accurately. Despite the letter's self-assigned grade C (presumably a caution flag for the heavy lacunae), collation found no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation — the damaged passages are honestly marked as such in both source and translation, which is the correct practice, not a defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15981231001", "next": "brahe-t_15990321001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Urges Longomontanus to travel to Wittenberg, offers travel funds, and proposes either the Imperial Mathematician post at Prague or an academic position at Wittenberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990111001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990127001", "d": "1599-01-27", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Rudolf Coraduz → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 27 January 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDLEN VNNDT GESTRENGEN RITTER HERRN TYCHO BRAHE ZU KNUDSTORP MEYNEM SONDERS GUNSTIGEN VND VIELGELIEBTEN HERRENN.<br>TRESEN ODER WITTENBERGK.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ac Clarissime eques et Domine obserme. Sacra Caesarea Maiestas Dominus noster Clementissimus mandavit mihi, ut Illustrem Dominationem tuam statim huc vocem, cui hisce meis humillime obsequi et debui et volui. Curet itaque Dominatio Tua Illustris quam diligentissime, ut quam primum poterit, se ad iter accingat, sicque coram Caesare hîc Pragae statuat, ut is hac in parte in Dominatione tua illustri, vel etiam me ipso, ne minimum quidem defuisse animadvertat. Porro de hac Illustris D. T. personae Augusta evocatione non tam D. T. Illri gratulor, quam mihi ipsi. Mirum in modum gaudeo, utpote qui hac occasione, te tantum ac talem virum et praeter generis nobilitatem, omni virtutum et literarum genere exornatum atque excultum coram videre tuisque doctissimis sermonibus quandoque frui potero. Vale quam optime et diutissime, Vir Nobilissime, omnemque adhibe diligentiam, ut nobis salvus atque incolumis huc advenias.</p><p>Datae Pragae die 27. Ianuarij Anno 1599, quem tibi cum multis alijs felicissimum a Deo ter Optimo Maximo precor.<br>Illustri atque Clariss. D. T. deditissimus<br>RUDOLPHIUS CORADUCIUS,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and stern [valiant] knight, Master Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, my especially favourable and dearly-beloved Lord. At Dresden or Wittenberg.</b></p><p>Illustrious and most distinguished knight and most observed Lord. His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, has commanded me to call Your Illustrious Lordship hither at once; which [command], by these my [letters], I both ought and wished most humbly to obey. Let Your Illustrious Lordship therefore see to it, as diligently as possible, that, as soon as you can, you gird yourself for the journey, and so present yourself before the Emperor here at Prague, that he may perceive that in this matter nothing at all — not even the least — has been wanting, whether on Your Illustrious Lordship's part or on my own. Furthermore, on this august summoning of Your Illustrious Lordship's person, I congratulate not so much Your Illustrious Lordship as myself. I rejoice in a marvellous manner, inasmuch as on this occasion I shall be able to see in person — and sometime to enjoy the most learned discourses of — you, so great and such a man, who, besides nobility of birth, are adorned and cultivated with every kind of virtues and of letters. Fare most well and most long, most noble Man, and apply all diligence to come hither to us safe and unharmed.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 27th day of January, in the year 1599 — which [year] I pray, from God thrice Best and Greatest, may be most happy for you, together with many others [to come].<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious and most distinguished Lordship,<br>Rudolf Coraduz,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>The imperial summons.</b> This is the <b>formal letter calling Tycho to Prague</b> — written at the express command of <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> (\"His Sacred Imperial Majesty … commanded me to call Your Illustrious Lordship hither at once\") — the document that drew Tycho into the imperial service. It directly answers Gödelmann's autumn advice (brahe-t_15981112001) to wait at Dresden or Wittenberg: the German address indeed directs the letter <i>\"to Dresden or Wittenberg\"</i> (<i>Tresen oder Wittenbergk</i>), where Tycho was wintering.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> (<i>Rudolphus Coraducius</i>), Imperial <b>Vice-Chancellor</b> (<i>Reichsvizekanzler</i>; the <i>Procancellarius</i> of brahe-t_15980530001, …1201001), the senior official handling Tycho's reception. He writes warmly, \"congratulating himself\" on the prospect of Tycho's learned company.</li><li><b>Register.</b> Elaborate chancery-Latin courtesy: <i>Illustris ac Clarissime eques et Domine observandissime</i>; <i>Dominatio Tua Illustris</i>; <i>Deo ter Optimo Maximo</i>. The German address keeps the knightly style <i>edler und gestrenger Ritter</i>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pragae die 27. Ianuarij 1599</i> = <b>27 January 1599</b>; Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Address heading corrected from 'VNO' to 'VND' (=und) per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.136; a single-letter transcription typo that did not affect the published translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Latin is rendered securely, and the document's identity (Coraduz's summons of Tycho to Prague at Rudolf II's command, directed to Dresden/Wittenberg) is unambiguous and corpus-corroborated. The German address-line <i>Tresen</i> = Dresden is read with confidence.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full German address heading and Latin body of Coraduz's summons letter against TBOO VIII pp.136-137 page images. The Latin body (the letter's entire substance) matches Dreyer word for word and the English translation renders it accurately throughout. One TEXT_ERROR found in the German heading: source_text's 'VNO' vs Dreyer's 'VND'.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text's German address heading reads the non-word 'VNO' where Dreyer prints 'VND' (=und, 'and'); a single-letter corruption in the transcription. The English translation ('favourable and dearly-beloved') correctly conveys 'and' regardless, so the error did not propagate into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>MEYNEM SONDERS GUNSTIGEN VND VIELGELIEBTEN HERRENN</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.136 (page image): the address heading reads unambiguously 'MEYNEM SONDERS GUNSTIGEN VND VIELGELIEBTEN HERRENN'. Single, isolated, low-severity fix; nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Rudolf Coraduz", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000506002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000506002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Transmits the Emperor's command summoning Tycho immediately to Prague and expresses personal eagerness to meet him in person.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990127001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990128001", "d": "1599-01-28", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Hartwig von Dassel", "ck": "Hartwig von Dassel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Lüneburg", "t": "Hartwig von Dassel → Tycho Brahe — Lüneburg, 28 January 1599", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record:<i> Poematum Hardewigi Dasselii … Libri IV; accessit Epistolarum familiarum … Liber Unus </i>(Dassel's printed works; no MS witness)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The letter's recovered passages — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the jurist-poet <b>Hartwig von Dassel (Hardewigus Dasselius)</b>, a kinsman/friend of Heinrich Rantzau, <i>to</i> Tycho. A humanist <b>consolatio</b>: a jeremiad on the age, and a <b>eulogy for Heinrich Rantzau</b> — Tycho's Wandsbek host — who died <b>1 January 1599</b>. Survives in Dassel's own printed <i>Poemata + Epistolae</i> (no manuscript witness).</p>", "la": "<p>HARDEWIGUS A DASSELL I. C.<br>TYCHONI BRAHEN VIRO NOBILISS. EQUITI ET MATHEMATICO EXCELLENTISS.<br>S.<br>D.</p><p>QUOTIESCUNQUE ad statum præsentium temporum, qui est miserrimus, Nobilissime et<br>magnifice vir, amice colende, oculos animumque converto (converto autem omnes<br>fere dies noctesque) toties mihi in mentem venit, quod Poëta<br>his versibus canit:</p><p>Sic omnia fatis</p><p>In peius ruere, et retro sublapsa referri,</p><p>Non aliter, quam qui adverso vix flumine lembum</p><p>Remigijs subigit, si brachia forte remisit,</p><p>Atque illum in præceps prono rapit alveus amni.</p><p>Neque enim ulla unquam extitêre tempora hisce nostris turbulentiora, quibus omnia<br>mundi regna τὴν ἐσχάτην πτῶσιν minitantur, in quibus tanta undique tamque<br>effrenis morum corruptela, tot et tantæ mortalium in diversa studia commotiones,<br>tot tantæque rerum perturbationes, quibus oportune componendis unus solus aut<br>occupatus fuerit aut suffecerit, in quibus simul religio et verus Dei cultus<br>τινί μηδὲν ποιεῖσθαι προτιμότερον, et publica et privata ita undique in<br>turbulentiam convoluta iacuerint. Quamquam enim retroacta tempora suas secum<br>molestias quasdam et turbationes semper attulerint, nec bellis civilibus ne<br>quidem atrocissimis in toto caruerint, tamen de hoc constat apertissime, nulla<br>unquam eiusmodi fuisse, quibus hæc omnia simul mala, tanquam uno edita partu,<br>unâ effusa phialâ, confluxerint, ut nec immerito sola præ cæteris omnibus<br>anteactis seculis summe deplorata et calamitosa dici queant. Quæ enim fuêre<br>(dicat mihi quispiam, qui collatas singulorum<br>temporum historias cognovit) unquam secula, cum his ex<br>virtutum et vitiorum præposteris exacte extremitatibus conferenda? in quibus sic<br>omnia sursum deorsumque sacra et profana sibi invicem confusa extiterint et<br>perplexa? in quibus sic omnia vitia et turpitudines ad summum pervenerint<br>fastigium, in quo sine rubore etiam et propemodum cum aliqua laudis vindicatione<br>steterint? virtus, honestas, devolutæ ad imum ubi pessundatæ et proculcatæ<br>iacuerint? in quibus maior solutioris et petulantis vitæ impunitas et licentia,<br>maiores passim in nationum et regnorum statibus intestini motus ac seditiones?<br>Adeo per præceps totus uno veluti coniurato impetu in dedecus et omnium vitiorum<br>barathrum, in mutuum excidium et perniciem grassatur et ruit mundus. Miserrimus<br>est profecto miseræ et omni ope destitutæ Germaniæ nostræ status. Non externus hostis hanc populatur, non<br>aliena fraus diripit, sed suis ipsa viribus ruit, ad seipsam evertendam callida<br>est et dolos struit. Quid obsecro existimandum, externos populos, Italos puto,<br>Gallos, Hispanos, loqui de nobis? Quid literæ et scripta testabuntur? Durum est<br>et asperum dictu, sed dicendum tamen. Nobilium in nobiles, civium in cives, imo<br>fratrum, adfinium, cognatorum, propinquorum in suos commemorabitur impius et<br>nefarius furoris atque avaritiæ et invidiæ impetus, insidiæ, circumventio,<br>inductio, fallaciæ. Non tantum mali in summa calamitate esse potest, quantum in hac ignominia et dedecore. Quæ autem hæc est rabies? Nihil<br>curare aliquem, quid liberis, uxori, consanguineis, affinibus suis fiat? an<br>nobiscum extingui delerique cupimus omnia? Et illæ Neronianæ et Caianæ sententiæ<br>in animo et voces in ore sunt: »Me mortuo terra absumatur<br>ignibus;« vel magis etiam tristes:</p><p>»ignes, ruinæ populum premant,</p><p>turpisque egestas, sæva cum luctu fames.«</p><p>O gentis et nationis laudatissimæ ingens probrum! Mirantur interim multi, quonam<br>malo Genio, quove fato nostro, illa morum corruptela totque discordiæ et<br>dissidia tam in ordine politice, quam in ecclesiastico passim nata sint, et<br>indies magis magisque augeantur, non abs re quidem id mirantur, et dolent etiam<br>ob rei, quæ tam multa secum mala invehat, et pietati iustitiæque interitum<br>minetur, diuturnitatem et magnitudinem. Verum si admiratores illi et animorum<br>nostrorum pravitatem vitæque labem, de quibus paulo ante dictum, ac gravissima,<br>quibus ea tota scatet ac sordet, vulnera sibi ob oculos ponant, et, quod<br>proximum est, in tot et tantis, quibus laboramus morbis, nullam fere sanitatis<br>speciem et spem reliquam esse considerent, non amplius mirabuntur; sed dolebunt<br>nihilominus, quod de morbis nostris curandis nullo modo nec cogitemus nec<br>laboremus. Vitia hæc sunt, ut uno verbo dicam, et<br>scelera, bellorum et calamitatum sons et caput; quibus nisi exstirpatis,<br>nullus miseriarum finis sperandus videtur. Non prosequar<br>omnia, quia et infinita sunt, et alibi ab alijs explicata satis, sed maiori<br>quidem illorum, qui sapienter ea disseruerunt et nos ad meliora erudierunt,<br>laude, quam nostrâ, qui nec ullis præceptis moniti sapere, nec ullis malis<br>affecti resipiscere possumus, utilitate.</p><p>Et quid pluribus commemorandis tempus teram, cum, si avaritiam et ambitionem,<br>teterrimas animorum pestes, unam dissensionum et calamitatum in toto Romano<br>imperio causam esse dixero, complexus omnia videar? Hic unus malus Genius est,<br>qui nos tamdiu vexat, hoc ipsum est illud factum, quod et privatis rebus<br>detrimentum et publicis exitium affert. Hæ duæ inquam sunt pestes, quæ pietati et<br>iustitiæ, quæ Germaniæ nostræ, quæ Ecclesiæ<br>extremam, si quæ possint, perniciem machinantur, quæque ἀταξίαν et confusionem<br>adhuc, proh dolor, regnantem in Ecclesiam invexerunt? Hæc enim cum erat<br>pauperior et Apostoli ceterique Pastores auro et argento carebant, virtutibus<br>omnibus circumfluebant. Episcopi erant docti, modesti, prudentes, sobrij,<br>laboriosi, vigilantes et tanquam lumina universo populo prælucebant. Singuli vero<br>e populo tantâ charitate alij alios complectebantur, ut nemo, quod<br>possideret, suum esse diceret, sed omnia essent communia. Erantque concordes et perdurantes<br>in doctrina Apostolorum, laudantes Deum<br>eique pro omnibus gratias agentes. Fides vero tanta erat, ut<br>nec minis nec ullis cruciatibus a confessione Christianâ deterreri possent. Verum<br>ubi liberalitate et zelo Principum interdum inconsiderato, eadem Ecclesia<br>in opes et potentiam crevit, ex eo paulatim eius virtus (ut ait D. HIERONYMUS)<br>decrevit, quoad in eum statum et præcipitium delaberetur, quo in præsentiâ<br>redacta est, ut difficillimum sit iudicatu, utri sint deteriores, Pastores an<br>populus; quos inter se concertare dicas, utri alteros ambitione et avaritiâ<br>vincant. Certe priscos pietatis ac tum fundandæ et ædificandæ autores, Deum,<br>Prophetas, CHRISTUM, Apostolos et alios pios Doctores, tum eiusdem confirmandæ<br>adiutores, ut Imperatores, Reges, Principes, piasque Respubl. et populos si<br>consulunt et audiunt, longe aliam viam inibunt, longe aliam rationem sequentur.<br>Sed (ut non minus vere quam festive dicitur) famelicus venter auribus caret;<br>vesana illa habendi et dominandi fames nihil audit, donec plene satiata fuerit,<br>si plene tamen unquam satiari potest, quæ contra, quo plura absorbuit, eo plura<br>appetere, et gazas gazis, regnaque regnis cumulare et solet et gaudet.</p><p>Sed mitto iam illorum hominum sordes et perfidiam, et ad ea accedo, quorum<br>cognitione equidem valde consternatus sum, neque enim sine sensu doloris nuper<br>ex literis tuis accepi, Nobilissimum Virum D. HENRICUM RANZOVIUM, Regis Daniæ Producem Cimbricum, affinem nostrum, ipsis Calendis Ianuarij beato fine e<br>mortalium consuetudine exemptum esse, quo sane nuncio non<br>potui non magnopere commoveri, præsertim tam repentinâ morte. Erat enim mihi<br>ob singularem prudentiam, eximias virtutes, animique et corporis præstantissimas<br>dotes, adeo charus, ut nulla vis rerum aut temporum præscriberet modum in eo<br>amando, suspiciendo et observando. Sed quia divinam in rebus humanis voluntatem<br>dominari non ignoramus, non forti modo, sed æquo etiam animo, quod mutare non<br>possumus, ferre debemus; hoc nostrum incommodum gravissimamque iacturam eo<br>ferimus animo, quo debemus. Tantum vero abest, ut illius vicem doleamus, ut etiam<br>ipsius causâ gaudendum esse intelligamus. Etenim cui magis propitium et placatum<br>Deum fuisse, cuive facilius aditum ad æternam et beatam illam vitam patuisse<br>censendum est, quam ei, qui (ut ipse quoque de eo sentis) et integerrime semper<br>ac castissime vixerit et ex hac vitâ pie sancteque discesserit? Profecto illius<br>obitum dolere invidentis esset potius quam amantis. Quare tuum erit, cum is tam<br>præclare vitæ suæ cursum confecerit, hac cogitatione desiderium, quo ex illo<br>afficeris, lenire. Quod facilius etiam assequêre, si literis sacris aliquid tui<br>studij et temporis impertieris, ex quibus id, quod ipse expertus confirmare<br>possum, præter suavissimum quendam animi fructum ac voluptatem, quam percipies,<br>omnia quæ evenire homini possunt incommoda, non tolerabilia solum, sed ad<br>salutem etiam animorum vim habere maxime intelliges. Sed hæc<br>hactenus. Fuit sane Generosus Dominus HENRICUS<br>RANZOVIUS, qui nunc rebus humanis exemptus est, vir et ore et<br>more imaginem honesti referens, opum honorumque contemptor, recti pervicax, ut<br>animâ, ita ore liber eoque gratus bonis, gravis improbis, Doctorum et<br>Literatorum, sed bonorum amans, illis ignavâ operâ non ferebat; eruditionem<br>gravem seriamque, a qua in publicum boni mores, bona valetudo manarent,<br>complectebatur, illa acuminis irriti ludicra et difficilimas nugas aversabatur.<br>Iam pietate et animi robore supra vulgi captum ingeniumque. Sed non ille ad<br>huius seculi gustum, seculi improbi, maligni, ingrati, quo vitijs malisque<br>artibus non modo impunitas, sed apud plerosque etiam honos, innocentiam atque<br>virtutem vix usquam sine periculo aperte colueris. CHRISTIANO tertio Regi<br>Daniæ a puero charus, neque id<br>immerito. Nam et ipsi RANZOVIO una<br>illa post Deum cura, habere clementissimum Regem eumque florentissimum videre.<br>Erat ille familiâ inter Holsatos nobili natus, sed postea ob egregias virtutes,<br>eruditionem haud vulgarem, trium Regum Daniæ, CHRISTIANI III ,<br>FRIDERICI II et CHRISTIANI IV in Ducatibus Sleswigæ, Holsatiæ<br>et Ditmarsiæ, summâ cum dignitate Vicarius perpetuus. Primos annos bonis<br>artibus, cæteram ætatem Reipubl. dedit, ubi maturior illa et rebus gerendis<br>simulac regendis genesis ætas accessit, tum<br>Legationibus obire, laborare, vigilare, negotijs publicijs<br>intentus sua negligere, bonos omnes passim ad communem pacem adhortari,<br>prudentibus animum addere, imprudentibus mala imminentia exponere. Tu autem<br>felix, RANZOVI, confecto innocenter cursu tuo, iam hac servitute liber vivis et<br>vivis cum Deo, beatus [in] æternum. Sed ad laudes D. RANZOVIJ<br>non addam plura.</p><p>Quod vero, nobilissime vir, tu quoque in extrema epistolæ tuæ parte mortem<br>RANZOVIJ non fictis lamentis prosequeris, facis tu quidem, quod non te solum,<br>sed omnes literatos facere decet. Quod autem et eius sapientiam, doctrinam,<br>virtutem et humanitatem pleno ore prædicas, in eo animi tui candorem et agnosco<br>et magnopere laudo, cumque te laudum eius tam studiosum esse animadvertissem,<br>non dubitavi, quin grata tibi lectio futura esset eorum, quibus tumulum eius<br>ornavi, epigrammatum; quare adscribenda ea censui, tua vicissim, si qua<br>scripseris, expectans. Tu, nobilissime vir, hanc confidentiam compellationis<br>meæ, et, quæ tanquam garriens commiseratione maligni huius seculi motus ad te<br>perscripsi, in bonam partem accipies, beneque valebis.</p><p>Luneburg, 28. Ianuarij<br>Anno [1599].</p>", "en": "<p><b>Hartwig von Dassel, Jurisconsult, to Tycho Brahe, most noble Knight and most excellent Mathematician. Greeting.</b></p><p>As often as I turn my eyes and mind to the state of present times — which is most wretched — most noble and magnificent man, friend to be revered (and I turn them thither almost every day and night), so often there comes into my mind what the Poet sings in these verses:</p><p>&gt; <i>\"Thus all things by the fates rush to the worse, and, slipping back, are carried backward — no otherwise than one who scarce, against the stream, drives his skiff with the oars: if perchance he has slackened his arms, the channel snatches him headlong down the sloping current.\"</i> [Virgil, Georgics 1.199–203]</p><p>For never have there existed times more turbulent than ours, in which all the kingdoms of the world threaten the uttermost fall [τὴν ἐσχάτην πτῶσιν]; in which there is so great and so unbridled a corruption of morals on every side, so many and so great commotions of mortals into divergent pursuits, so many and so great disturbances of affairs that to set them right in due season one man alone would scarcely find occupation enough, or be sufficient for the task; in which likewise religion and the true worship of God were things to which nothing, in anyone's eyes, was held preferable, and public and private affairs alike lay everywhere rolled up into turbulence. For although bygone times always brought with them certain troubles and disturbances of their own, nor were they ever wholly free even of the most atrocious civil wars, yet of this it is most plainly established, that there was never any age of such a kind into which all these evils together — as if brought forth in one birth, poured out from a single vial — flowed together, so that not undeservedly may these times alone, beyond all the foregoing ages, be called supremely deplored and calamitous. For what ages ever were — let anyone tell me who has compared the histories of each several time — that could be matched against these, by the exact extremities to which virtues and vices, turned upside down, have come? In which sacred and profane things thus lay confounded and entangled together, the one with the other, from top to bottom? In which all vices and baseness thus arrived at their utmost height, standing there without a blush, and almost claiming some vindication of praise? In which virtue and honesty lay rolled down to the very bottom, cast down and trampled underfoot? In which there was greater impunity and licence for a looser and more wanton life, and everywhere greater intestine commotions and seditions in the condition of nations and kingdoms? So utterly, by one as it were conspiring onset, does the whole world rush and fall headlong into disgrace and the pit of all vices, into mutual destruction and ruin. Most wretched, indeed, is the state of our wretched Germany, destitute of all help. No foreign enemy lays her waste, no alien fraud plunders her — she falls by her own forces, is cunning for her own overturning, and weaves snares against herself. What, I ask, must we suppose foreign peoples — the Italians, I imagine, the French, the Spanish — say of us? What will letters and writings attest? It is hard and bitter to say, yet it must be said. There will be recounted the impious and nefarious onset of fury, avarice, and envy — of nobles against nobles, citizens against citizens, nay of brothers, kinsfolk, relations, neighbours against their own — the ambushes, the circumvention, the seduction, the deceits. There cannot be as much evil in the utmost calamity as there is in this ignominy and disgrace. But what madness is this? That no one cares what becomes of his children, his wife, his blood-kin, his relations? Or do we wish that, with us, everything should be extinguished and blotted out? And those Neronian and Caligulan sentences are in the mind, and on the tongue: \"When I am dead, let the earth be consumed by fires\" — or, even sadder:</p><p>&gt; <i>\"Let fires, ruins press the people down, and base want, with savage hunger and grief.\"</i></p><p>O the vast reproach of a most praised people and nation! Many meanwhile marvel by what evil genius, or by what fate of ours, that corruption of morals, and so many discords and dissensions born everywhere, alike in the political order and in the ecclesiastical, have arisen, and day by day grow ever greater; and not without reason do they marvel at it, and grieve also at the long duration and the greatness of a thing that brings with it so many evils, and threatens the ruin of piety and justice. But if those who marvel would set before their eyes the depravity of our minds and the taint of our life — of which a little before I spoke — and the most grievous wounds with which that life wholly teems and is befouled, and, which comes next, would consider that in so many and so great diseases as we labour under scarcely any appearance or hope of health remains, they will marvel no longer; but they will grieve nonetheless, that we in no way either think about or labour at the curing of our diseases. These, to say it in one word, are the vices and crimes that are the source and head of wars and calamities; unless these are rooted out, no end of our miseries seems to be hoped for. I will not pursue all of this, since it is both endless and has been sufficiently set forth elsewhere by others — to the greater credit indeed of those who have discussed it wisely and instructed us toward better things, than to any profit of ours, who can neither be made wise by any precepts we are warned with, nor be brought to our senses by any evils that afflict us.</p><p>And why should I waste time recounting more, since, if I shall have said that avarice and ambition — the foulest plagues of minds — are the one cause of the dissensions and calamities in the whole Roman Empire, I shall seem to have embraced all? This is that one evil genius which so long vexes us; this same thing it is that brings both loss to private affairs and ruin to public. These two, I say, are the plagues which contrive the uttermost ruin, if they can, of piety and justice, of our Germany, of the Church — and which have brought into the Church that disorder [ἀταξίαν] and confusion that still, alas, reign? For when the Church was poorer, and the Apostles and the other Pastors lacked gold and silver, they overflowed with all virtues. The bishops were learned, modest, prudent, sober, industrious, watchful, and shone before the whole people like lights. And among the people, each embraced the others with such charity that no one called what he possessed his own, but all things were held in common. And they were of one mind and persevered in the teaching of the Apostles, praising God and giving him thanks for all things. And their faith was so great that they could be deterred, neither by threats nor by any torments, from the confession of Christ. But when, by the liberality and (sometimes inconsiderate) zeal of Princes, that same Church grew into wealth and power, from that its virtue (as Saint Jerome says) gradually decreased, until it slipped down into that state and precipice to which it is now reduced, so that it is most difficult to judge which are the worse, the Pastors or the people — you would say they vie with one another as to which shall outdo the other in ambition and avarice. Surely, if they would consult and give ear to the ancient founders of piety — God, the Prophets, Christ, the Apostles, and other pious Doctors — and then to those who helped confirm the same, such as Emperors, Kings, Princes, and pious Commonwealths and peoples, they would enter upon a far different path, and follow a far different plan. But (as is said no less truly than wittily) a hungry belly has no ears; that mad hunger for having and for ruling hears nothing, until it be fully sated — if indeed it can ever be fully sated — which, on the contrary, the more it has swallowed, the more it craves, and is wont, and delights, to heap up treasure upon treasure, and kingdom upon kingdom.</p><p>But I now dismiss the sordidness and perfidy of those men, and come to the things by the knowledge of which I am greatly dismayed; for not without a sense of grief did I lately learn from your letter, that the most noble man Lord Heinrich Rantzau, the King of Denmark's Pro-duke of the Cimbric [land] [Statthalter of Holstein], our kinsman, was on the very Kalends of January [1 January] taken, by a blessed end, from the company of mortals — by which news indeed I could not but be greatly moved, especially by so sudden a death. For he was so dear to me, for his singular prudence, his eminent virtues, the most excellent gifts of his mind and body, that no force of circumstances or times prescribed a measure to my loving, looking up to, and observing him. But since we are not ignorant that the divine will rules in human affairs, we ought to bear, not with a brave only but with an even mind, what we cannot change; this our misfortune and most grievous loss we bear with the mind we ought to. But so far is it from our duty to grieve his lot, that we understand we must even rejoice on his own account. For to whom should God be reckoned to have been more propitious and appeased, or to whom should an entrance to that eternal and blessed life be judged to have lain more open, than to him who (as you too think of him) both ever lived most uprightly and most chastely, and departed from this life piously and holily? Surely to grieve his death would be the part of one who envies him, rather than one who loves him. Wherefore it will be your part, since he has so gloriously finished the course of his life, to soften with this thought the longing with which you are affected on his account. Which comfort you will the more easily attain, if you impart some of your study and time to the sacred letters [Scripture], from which — a thing I can myself confirm from experience — besides a certain most sweet fruit and pleasure of mind which you will perceive, you will come especially to understand that all the misfortunes that can befall a man have the power not only to be borne, but even to work toward the health of our souls. But enough of this. He was indeed, the noble Lord Heinrich Rantzau, now taken from human affairs, a man bearing the image of honour both in face and in manner; a contemner of wealth and honours; stubborn for the right; free of soul as of speech, and thereby pleasing to the good, weighty to the wicked; a lover of Doctors and Men of Letters — but of the good ones, whom he never served with idle effort; he embraced grave and serious erudition, from which good morals and good health might flow forth to the public, and turned away from the playthings of idle cleverness and most difficult trifles. Moreover, in piety and strength of mind he was above the common grasp and wit of the crowd. But he was not suited to the taste of this age — a wicked, malicious, thankless age, in which for vices and evil arts there is not only impunity, but among most men even honour, while innocence and virtue you could scarcely cultivate openly anywhere without danger. Dear from boyhood to Christian III, King of Denmark — and not undeservedly; for to Rantzau himself, too, this was, after God, his one care: to have a most merciful King, and to see him most flourishing. He was born of a family noble among the Holsatians, but afterward, for his outstanding virtues and no common erudition, perpetual Vicar [Statthalter], with the highest dignity, of three Kings of Denmark — Christian III, Frederick II, and Christian IV — in the Duchies of Schleswig, Holstein, and Dithmarschen. His first years he gave to the good arts, the rest of his age to the Commonwealth; and when that riper age for conducting and governing affairs together had come upon him, then he gave himself to undertaking embassies, to labouring, to watching; intent upon public business, he neglected his own; he exhorted all good men everywhere to common peace, gave heart to the prudent, and set forth to the imprudent the evils that threatened. But you, fortunate Rantzau, your course innocently finished, now live free of this servitude, and live with God, blessed for eternity. But to the praises of Lord Rantzau I will add no more.</p><p>But that you too, most noble man, in the last part of your letter, follow Rantzau's death with no feigned laments — in this you do indeed what becomes not you alone, but all the lettered. And that you proclaim, with full mouth, his wisdom, learning, virtue, and humanity — in that I both recognise and greatly praise the candour of your mind; and since I had noticed you so zealous of his praises, I did not doubt that the reading would be welcome to you of the epigrams with which I adorned his tomb; wherefore I judged they should be appended — expecting yours in turn, if you have written any. You, most noble man, will take in good part this confidence of my addressing you, and the things which — as it were chattering, from compassion at the commotions of this malign age — I have written out to you; and you will fare well.</p><p>Lüneburg, the 28th of January, the year [1599].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Hartwig von Dassel (Hardewigus Dasselius)</b> — jurist (<i>Iuris utriusque Consultus</i>) and Neo-Latin poet, a kinsman/friend of Heinrich Rantzau — <b>to Tycho</b>. Survives in <b>Dassel's own printed <i>Poemata</i> + <i>Epistolae</b></i>.</li><li><b>Significance — the death of Heinrich Rantzau (Tycho's host) and a humanist age-lament.</b></li><li><b>★ The death of Heinrich Rantzau (1 January 1599).</b> The letter's documentary core: <b>Tycho had announced, in a letter to Dassel, the death of Heinrich Rantzau — the great Holstein humanist-statesman, <i>Statthalter (Royal Vicar)</i> of Schleswig-Holstein under three Danish kings (Christian III, Frederick II, Christian IV), and Tycho's host at Wandsbek</b> — who died on the Kalends of January 1599. This confirms Tycho's closeness to Rantzau (his Wandsbek patron; cf. the Wandsbek letters and brahe-t_15980607001) and his participation in the Rantzau memorial.</li><li><b>The Rantzau eulogy.</b> A full humanist portrait: \"contemner of wealth and honours … free of soul and speech … lover of [good] Doctors and Men of Letters … grave and serious erudition,\" dear to Christian III from boyhood, perpetual Statthalter — \"now living with God, blessed for eternity.\"</li><li><b>The age-lament.</b> An elaborate <b>jeremiad on the corruption of the age</b>: the world \"rushing to the worse\" (the Virgilian <i>Georgics</i> boat-against-the-current); <b>Germany \"falling by her own forces\"</b> (nobles against nobles, kin against kin); <b>avarice and ambition \"the one cause of the dissensions and calamities in the whole Roman Empire\"</b>; and the <b>decline of the Church from apostolic poverty/virtue to wealth/corruption</b> (citing <b>Jerome</b>), with the tags \"a hungry belly has no ears\" and the Neronian \"<i>Me mortuo terra absumatur ignibus</i>.\"</li><li><b>The tomb-epigram exchange.</b> Dassel encloses <b>his epigrams for Rantzau's tomb</b> and <b>expects Tycho's in return</b> — a record of Tycho composing memorial verse for his patron (cf. Tycho's own poetic interests, brahe-t_16010414001).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Heinrich Rantzau / Wandsbek</b> thread (Tycho's host-patron; the Wandsbek letters); the <b>literary-memorial</b> network.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Hartwig von Dassel; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (the deceased Statthalter); <b>Christian III, Frederick II, Christian IV</b> of Denmark; <b>Jerome</b>; <b>Virgil</b> (<i>Georgics</i>); <b>Nero, Caligula</b> (the tyrant-sayings). Places: <b>Lüneburg</b>, Germany, <b>Schleswig/Holstein/Dithmarschen</b> (Rantzau's Statthalterate).</li><li><b>Quotations.</b> The opening verses = <b>Virgil, <i>Georgics</i> I.199–203</b> (the skiff against the current). The \"<i>ignes, ruinae populum premant</i>\" couplet is a tragic-verse tag (Senecan in tone). The Greek phrases (τὴν ἐσχάτην πτῶσιν \"the uttermost fall\"; ἀταξίαν \"disorder\") are reproduced. The text as printed elides some clauses (Dreyer's source); the translation follows the printed text faithfully.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Luneburg, 28. Ianuarij Anno [1599]</i> — year supplied editorially; Lüneburg (Old Style); → <b>28 January 1599 [O.S.]</b> (label and slug agree).</li><li><b>Scope of the gap.</b> Roughly two-thirds of Hartwig von Dassel's 28 January 1599 letter to Tycho Brahe was missing from the prior translation, which carried only the opening address, the Virgil epigraph, and disconnected fragments joined by ellipses. The recovered Latin restores the entire moralising excursus on the corruption of the age (the letter's longest single stretch) and roughly half of the eulogy for Heinrich Rantzau; the closing paragraph about the enclosed epitaph-epigrams was already complete and is unchanged here.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> Hardewigus/Hartwig von Dassel signs as \"I.C.\" (Iuris Consultus — Doctor of both Laws), writing from Lüneburg. He does not turn up in our absorbed Tycho reference layers (Christianson, Dreyer, Thoren, Mosley) under this name, so he reads as a minor, lettered correspondent in Tycho's wider humanist network rather than a familiar figure of the household (<i>familia</i>).</li><li><b>The occasion.</b> The letter is an obituary letter for Heinrich Rantzau (1526–1599), Tycho's patron, kinsman, and host at Wandsbek — his dates and Wandsbek residence are independently confirmed [Christianson et al., <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague: Crossroads of European Science</i>, giving \"Heinrich of Rantzau (1526-1599)\"]. Von Dassel reports his death \"on the very Kalends of January\" (1 January 1599) and frames it inside a long jeremiad on the corruption of the age, invoking Virgil (<i>Georgics</i> 1.199-203) and St Jerome on the Church's decline from apostolic poverty into wealth and ambition, before turning to Rantzau's career as thrice-appointed Statthalter of Schleswig-Holstein-Dithmarschen under Christian III, Frederick II, and Christian IV — matching Norlind's identification of Rantzau as \"Statthalter in Schleswig-Holstein.\"</li><li><b>Rantzau's epitaph epigrams.</b> The closing paragraph (already sound in the prior translation) shows von Dassel enclosing his own Latin epigrams composed for Rantzau's tomb, inviting Tycho to answer with epigrams of his own — a concrete data point for the corpus of Rantzau memorial verse produced within weeks of his death.</li><li><b>Cruxes to flag.</b> (1) The embedded Greek tag \"τινί μηδὲν ποιεῖσθαι προτιμότερον\" in the first restored paragraph is elliptical — it lacks the expected object of comparison (\"preferable to what\") — and has been translated by inferred sense rather than settled philology; a genuine, unresolved crux. (2) \"illis ignavâ operâ non ferebat\" (of Rantzau's support for good scholars) is compressed idiom, rendered here by sense as \"whom he never served with idle effort,\" i.e. he supported them diligently rather than passively.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The salutation header is corrected from 'Doctor of Both Laws' to 'Jurisconsult': Dreyer (TBOO VIII p.137) prints 'HARDEWIGUS A DASSELL I. C.' — I.C. abbreviates Iuris Consultus, not Iuris Utriusque Doctor (I.U.D.). A fabricated editorial bracket claiming an 'apparatus' crux note for the Greek tag τινὶ μηδὲν ποιεἶσθαι προτιμότερον has been removed; Dreyer (TBOO VIII p.137) prints the Greek with no such apparatus.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — rhetorically dense but fully construable humanist Latin (with Greek tags and a Virgil quotation) read with confidence; the substance (the elaborate <b>age-lament</b> — the Virgilian boat, Germany's self-destruction, avarice/ambition as the root, the Church's decline per Jerome; and the <b>eulogy and biography of Heinrich Rantzau</b>, whose <b>death on 1 January 1599 Tycho had announced to Dassel</b>, with the <b>tomb-epigram exchange</b>) is firmly construed and rendered faithfully, with Dassel's ornate consolatory rhetoric neither sharpened nor flattened. The chief documentary value — <b>Rantzau's death and Tycho's role in his memorial</b> — is secure; no textual crux beyond the printed source's own elisions.</p><p>Apart from the flagged Greek tag, the recovered Latin is straightforward humanist prose without textual corruption, and the existing English — reused verbatim wherever it already covered the ground — was accurate; I am confident in the restored jeremiad and in the restored half of the Rantzau eulogy. The one live crux is the Greek phrase, whose syntax is incomplete relative to normal usage; it should be checked against a specialist edition of the correspondence or a classicist's reading before being treated as settled. Grade B: sound and complete, with one flagged, unresolved crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.133-142 as page images (chunks 1-4, 5-8, 9-10); pp.133-136 proved to belong to three other letters, so the actual Dassel letter was located and collated on pp.137-141 (confirmed by the running headers 'HARDEW. DASSELIUS AD TYCHONEM 28 IAN. 1599'). Checked the Virgil tag (Georgics 1.199-203, correctly identified in the English), both Greek phrases (ὰταξίαν and the one above), the Neronian/Caligulan tags and the two embedded verse quotations, Rantzau's death on the Kalends (1st) of January 1599, his title, the three kings named (Christian III, Frederick II, Christian IV) and three duchies (Schleswig, Holstein, Dithmarschen), the Jerome citation, and the closing dateline 'Luneburg, 28 Ianuarij Anno [1599]' — all agree exactly between Dreyer, source_text and the English, sentence for sentence, across this long philosophical letter. Two low-severity issues found, both editorial/paratextual rather than substantive: an over-specified honorific, and a bracketed reference to a nonexistent apparatus.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). 'I.C.' is the standard early-modern abbreviation for 'Iuris Consultus' (jurisconsult/legal counsellor), not 'Iuris Utriusque Doctor' (which would be abbreviated I.U.D. and means 'Doctor of Both Laws'); the header over-specifies Dassel's degree. At: “Hartwig von Dassel, Doctor of Both Laws, to Tycho Brahe, most noble Knight and most excellent Mathematician.”.<br>- <b>FABRICATION</b> (low). The bracket invents a pointer to 'the crux noted in the apparatus'; no apparatus criticus exists anywhere in this document or accompanies the source text — the note promises documentation that was never supplied. At: “[a Greek tag whose comparandum is missing — see the crux noted in the apparatus]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defects were verified directly against Dreyer's page images, TBOO VIII p.137 (the letter's opening page), read visually in a 4-page chunk covering pp.137-140. The heading 'I. C.' is legible and unambiguous. The Greek tag τινὶ μηδὲν ποιεἶσθαι προτιμότερον is likewise legible and confirmed to stand alone with no footnote or apparatus marker on the page. No TEXT_ERROR was flagged for this letter and none was found; the Latin is left untouched. Nothing remains uncertain for the two audited defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Hartwig von Dassel", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15960000002", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Laments the moral corruption and religious disorder of the age, citing Virgil on irreversible decline, before the text breaks off; little substantive content beyond this preserved.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990128001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990200001", "d": "1599-02-00", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe — [Graz], February 1599", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Epistolae ad Ioannem Keplerum scriptae </i>(printed source; no MS witness recorded)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Johannes Kepler</b> <i>to</i> Tycho — Kepler's <b>apologia for his earlier flattering letter to Ursus</b>, which Ursus had printed in his defamatory <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (1597) and weaponised against Tycho. Place not given (<b>Graz</b>, where Kepler then was); dated only to <b>February 1599</b> (slug/label give no day). <b>The source as supplied ends mid-discourse, without valediction or date — likely the dump/corpus entry is partial</b> (the full letter is longer); flagged.</p>", "la": "<p>IOANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>CUM intellexissem ex MAeSTLINI literis, quas ad me dedit superiori Augusto, respondisse te ad epistolium meum, eiusque responsionis exemplar apud MAeSTLINUM esse, proxima occasione apud illum institi, ut id exemplar ad me mitteret, quando literae tuae mihi non redderentur. Sic casu et diligentia tua factum, ut quamvis pereuntibus tuis literis, tamen quod me scire velles, post decimum mensem intellexerim. Haec initio te certiorem reddidi, quia in illa querela tua, me Christe, quam iustissima! postulas, ut primo quoque tempore meam tibi sententiam aperiam, ne ex hac decimestri mora maior tibi de me suspicio culpae suboriretur. Quantum igitur attinet meas ad URSUM literas, ex ijs, quae excerpsisti verbis, pleraque mea agnosco. Ex eo fit, ut quamvis ad aliqua, de quibus dubito, pene cohorrescam, nullum tamen negare possim. In hac itaque parte nullum adhuc URSI crimen est, quod liqueat. Utrum autem integram epistolam publicaverit, maior apud me dubitatio: quae facile discuteretur, si mihi exemplar typi esset. Nam in ijsdem literis desiderium meum tuorum operum legendorum expressi, eaque in re operam URSI expetij. Quantam autem per Deum immortalem! et quammultiplicem mihi facit iniuriam ferus homo! Ambiebam eius amicitiam missa epistola. Si petitionem meam admisit, fidem eius desidero: sin sprevit, tamen inhumanus est. Sermones amicorum, literae magis, privatae esse debent; has ille publicavit, nec me consuluit, nec facturum, nec factum nunciavit, nec exemplar misit. Quibus est curae aequitas, nec in hostem quidem tale quid audent. Remisisset mihi, quod veteres aiebant, nuncium, aut abolevisset schedam, si non placuit amicus. Quamquam si literis eius ad me altero tandem anno scriptis, si iudicio eius de me, fides haberi potest, et placuit conditio, et amicitiam colere instituerat. Sed haec levia sunt: illa maior improbitas, iure utique vindicanda, si cui in foro aut spes aut oblectatio: quod id quod γεωμετροῦντι dictum ego volebam, ipse sibi maledicenti, quod scribis, applicat. Acerbissimum omnium, quod quem intelligebat ex meis literis a me maximi fieri, illis ipsis meis literis, quantum potuit, dehonestavit. Sic nimirum ipsi me de laudato inimico suo placuit ulcisci. Sed neque satis mirari possum consilium hominis, dum consilium meum illius hyperbolicae laudationis ventilo. Quid? Tantine facit ista mea praeconia? At cuius hominis? Si vir esset, contemneret, si prudens, non ventilaret. Quaerebam obscurus ego laudatorem clarum vixdum nati inventi, mendicabam ab ipso stipem, ecce stipem ipse mendico extorsit. Praeceptorem dixi: nae parvam laudi accessionem facit ex adulatione discipuli. Quaerat eorum praeconia, qui se praeceptores ipsius ferunt. Quod si mihi partes iudicis tradit (nec apertius id potuisset, quam sic ostentata mea epistola) sciat iam mihi alios quam discipulo mores competere. Toleratur in discipulo utcunque adulatio, si ex opinione praeceptoris et amore profecta ad finem discendi dirigatur: in iudice adulatio flagitium est. Quae sic scribenda duxi, ut quamvis praestat securitatis causa honoratissimum quemque virum irasci minime, tamen, si non aliter honori tuo consuli posset, haberes arbitrium publicatae calumniae publice obviam eundi. Sed missum tandem ipsum faciamus. Nec enim, quam ipse male, sed quam ego bene fecerim, quaeritur. Et sunt quidem in illa mea epistola pleraque, quae defendi, aliqua, quae excusari, aliqua quae pessima suspicione liberari debent.</p><p>Ordiamur ab eo quod gravissimum est: quod nempe crimini mihi das, contra quam senserim, in os ipsi locutum. Quod dum agis, ita loqueris, ut mihi absolutissimum de alijs iudicium tribuere videaris. Quam ego suspicionem, si a me, quod perquam facile est, removero, magna parte purgationis me defunctum esse putabo. Etenim qui vir sit URSUS, iam non disputare placet, certe quo tempore epistolam illam scripsi, et imbecilliori eram iudicio et non parum ipsi iam diu tribueram. Venit Graetium Doctor quispiam ex Italia reversus Librum eius mihi exhibens, quem triduo concesso raptim percurrerem. In eo salutatos schematismis reperio praecipuos huius temporis Mathematicos, reperio magnificam in praefatione provocationem ad simile quid audendum, ad Astronomiam e fundamentis extruendam, reperio compendia quaedam aurea, quae Tubingae a MAeSTLINO celebrari memineram, reperio doctrinam sinuum, doctrinam triangulorum, vulgaria quidem, sed nova mihi omnia, ut videat, quo remige famam verberet. Nam pleraque postea in EUCLIDE et REGIOMONTANO reperi, quae tum ipsi tribuebam. Neque metuas, ut quae iam dixi, bene cum invento meo stare queant. Dicat ipse pro me MAeSTLINUS, quam tenuibus principijs institerim initio, qui profecto, si tam ieiunum me secundo dimisisset, quam primo dimiserat, nunquam tam exquisitam mihi commendationem (qua tamen, dum vixero, inferior ero) impertivisset. Itaque hac ego specie doctrinae de URSO in praeclaram opinionem erectus, incidi postea in quosdam ex nobilitate Styriaca Mathematum amantes, quibus, quod esset necessaria subinde mora in aula Caesaris, URSI notitia erat. Illi hominis praedicare ingenium, inventa, hortari me, ut anniterer in eius familiaritatem pervenire, polliceri suam operam. Sed DIOGENEM quempiam mihi describebant aliqui, cui nisi aut lubeat, aut cum improbitate aliqua extorqueri nihil possit. His ego de causis inventa paulo post speculatione mea, cum non esset mihi vicinior Mathematicus, ad quem commodius scriberem, ad scribendum impulsus, formam ipsam partim ad hominis naturam, quae ferebatur, partim ad eas, quas tum forte fortuito legebam, efficacissimae brevitatis epistolas dissoluto in pompam animo ex recenti inventionis meae amore accommodavi securus de hoc eventu. Quomodo si quid mihi excidit paulo maius, quam de ipso senseram, in gratiam laudatorum ipsius et mei ipsius amore, totum id iuvenilis temeritatis est, nec aliter excuso, dummodo sceleris suspicionem aboleam. Ex ijs, quae dixi, vides primum me bona conscientia Gloriam ipsi tribuisse, qua videbatur regnare in aula Caesaris et apud Mathematicos, quos in libello suo alloquebatur, illustrissimam autem cur dixerim, causam non reperio, nisi quod Illustres nostri eam praedicant, et Illustrissimus praedicare videtur, dum eum in Aula habet, et quod pomposum ingenium facile muscam conspicatur, ex qua faciat Elephantum. Eodem refero et id, quod, cum constaret de aliquibus, dixi (si modo dixi) ab omnibus doctis ipsum magnifieri. Certe enim MAeSTLINUM et TYCHONEM inter doctos habebam, quos sciebam non admodum praeclare de ipso sentire. Quod autem etiam plura in hunc modum declamaturus fuissem, si tempus tulisset, id non memini me polliceri. Imo illa de garrulitate aliena a Mathematicis protestatio non facta est ad excusanda etiam plura huiusmodi, sed potius ad corrigenda priora. Quod si vero mea sunt omnia et singula ista verba, quod Mathematicos huius aevi tantum praecedat unus quantum Phoebeius orbis minuta sidera: me Christe magnam iniuriam multis praeclarissimis viris, atque adeo meae ipsius conscientiae imprudens feci. Illud verissime dicere possum, nunquam me ullo modo serio, per lusum, publice, privatim, in os, aurem, ex animo volentem et prudentem hoc dixisse, quod REGIOMONTANO, COPERNICO, RHETICO, REINHOLDO, TYCHONI, MAeSTLINO, caeteris unus URSUS praeferendus sit. Nunquam sensi, nunquam praesenti animo scripsi, nunquam tam flagitiosam adulationem probavi. Quod si verba mea genuina tale quid sonant, quod nescio, casus in culpa est, meaque festinatio, et quod quid scripserim, non relegi. Poëticum, ut vides, totum id est, et ex Poëta desumptum, et poëtico spiritu dictum: quod non tantum iam nolo, sed et tum, cum scripsi, nolebam, aliorsum rapi volebam, aliam repraesentari, quod antecellat vulgus, vulgus, inquam, Mathematicorum, quod et credebam, et citra praecipuam gloriam fieri potest. Non protestarer tanta asseveratione contra manifesta verba (si modo talia sunt) nisi in ipsa illa epistola documentum maximum extaret meae innocentiae, quo de supra. Nam etsi quibus verbis TYCHONIS mentionem fecerim, nescio, fecisse tamen scio: et eiusmodi mentionem quidem, atque ex eiusmodi opinione profectam, quae merito URSUM pungat, si eum tam feliciter titillant haec sui ipsius elogia. Sed caute, ut video, posthac scribendum erit. Adeo nunquam non salutare quippiam URSI in aurem insusurrando praecipiunt. Caetera, quae allegas, levia sunt. Praeceptorem dixi, quia ex eius libro aliqua hauseram utilia; Nobilissimum vero, quia in his provincijs Austriacorum nobilitati datur Eruditio et Munus aulicum; Hypotheses vero eius feci, in quibus aliquid mutavit; nam si bene memini, Terram et ipse voluit, et in Martis circulo discrepat, sive, ut dicis, errat. Nam etsi ex MAeSTLINO de hac te inter et ipsum controversia olim inaudiveram, tamen cum et MAGINUS in Italia et ROeSLINUS in Alsatia singuli pro se simile quippiam ex hypothesibus COPERNICI derivassent, existimavi idem et URSO excogitata proclive fuisse. Denique quod finis principio respondet, nihil mirum, nec ulla in voto improbitas, ut valeat sideribus et scientiae. Id equidem studeat, maledictis omissis et vana ostentatione. Decus autem Germaniae mihi quilibet est, cui tantum tribuo, quantum illi tribuebam, quia nempe Mathemata verum decus sunt unius Germaniae. Haec adeo prolixe, cum ut dolorem meum testarer iusta querela, tum ut appareret, arbitrari me non omnino nihil ad meam existimationem pertinere, quid tu de me sentias.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES KEPLER TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>When I had understood, from Maestlin's letter (which he sent me last August), that <b>you had replied to my little letter, and that a copy of your reply was with Maestlin</b> — since your letter was not delivered to me — I pressed him at the next occasion to send me that copy. So it came about, by chance and by your diligence, that <b>though your letter perished, yet what you wished me to know I understood after the tenth month</b>. This I made known to you at the outset, because, <b>in that complaint of yours — by Christ, how most just! — you demand that I open my view to you at the first opportunity, lest from this ten-month delay a greater suspicion of fault toward me should arise in you.</b> As far, then, as concerns <b>my letters to Ursus</b>: from the words you excerpted, I recognise most as mine. Hence it is that, although at some, of which I am in doubt, I almost shudder, yet I can deny none. <b>In this part, then, there is as yet no crime of Ursus that is clear.</b> But whether he published the whole letter, [there is] greater doubt with me — which would easily be resolved, had I a printed copy. For in those letters I expressed my desire to read your works, and in that matter sought Ursus's help. <b>But how great — by the immortal God! — and how manifold an injury the savage man does me!</b> I sought his friendship by sending the letter. If he admitted my petition, I miss his good faith; if he spurned it, he is yet inhuman. The conversations of friends — more, their letters — ought to be private; these he published, neither consulted me, nor announced he would do it, nor that he had, nor sent a copy. Those who have a care for fairness do not dare such a thing even against an enemy. He would have sent the message back to me (as the ancients said), or destroyed the sheet, if the friend did not please him — although, if his letters to me, written at last the next year, if his judgement of me, can be trusted, both the condition pleased [him] and he had begun to cultivate friendship. But these are light matters. <b>That [is] the greater wickedness, by all means justly to be avenged (if any has in the court either hope or delight): that what I meant to be said \"to one doing geometry\" [γεωμετροῦντι], he applies to himself, reviling [you], as you write.</b> Bitterest of all: that <b>the one whom he understood from my letters to be most highly esteemed by me [you], he, by those very letters of mine, dishonoured as much as he could.</b> So, forsooth, it pleased him to avenge himself on me regarding his praised enemy. But neither can I sufficiently marvel at the man's plan, while I ventilate my own plan of that hyperbolic praise. What? Does he value those proclamations of mine so highly? But [praise] of what man? If he were a [true] man, he would despise it; if prudent, he would not ventilate it. I, obscure, sought a famous praiser of my scarcely-born invention; I begged a coin from him; behold, the beggar himself has extorted a coin. I called him \"preceptor\": truly he makes small addition to his praise from a disciple's adulation. Let him seek the proclamations of those who bear themselves as his preceptors. But <b>if he hands me the part of a judge (and he could not [do it] more openly than by thus displaying my letter), let him know that other manners now befit me than [those of] a disciple. Adulation is tolerated, somehow, in a disciple, if, proceeding from esteem and love of the preceptor, it be directed to the end of learning; in a judge, adulation is a crime.</b> These things I thought should thus be written, so that — although for safety's sake it is best that every most-honoured man be least angry — yet, <b>if your honour could not otherwise be provided for, you might have the choice of going publicly against the published calumny.</b> But let us at last dismiss the man himself. For the question is not how badly <i>he</i> acted, but how well <i>I</i> acted. And there are indeed, in that letter of mine, most things which ought to be defended, some which [ought] to be excused, some which [ought] to be freed from the worst suspicion.</p><p><b>Let us begin from what is gravest: namely, that you charge me with the crime of having spoken to his face against what I felt.</b> In doing which, you so speak as to seem to attribute to me an absolute judgement about others. Which suspicion, if I remove it from myself (which is very easy), I shall think myself discharged of a great part of the purgation. For what kind of man Ursus is, I do not now choose to dispute; certainly, at the time I wrote that letter, <b>I was both of weaker judgement, and had long attributed not a little to him.</b> A certain Doctor, returned from Italy, came to Graz, showing me his book — which, three days being granted, I hastily ran through. In it I find <b>the chief Mathematicians of this time greeted with diagrams; I find in the preface a magnificent provocation to dare something similar, to build Astronomy from its foundations; I find certain golden compendia which I remembered being celebrated at Tübingen by Maestlin; I find the doctrine of sines, the doctrine of triangles — common, indeed, but all new to me — so that he might see by what oar he beats [the way to] fame.</b> For most of what I then attributed to him I afterward found in <b>Euclid and Regiomontanus</b>. And do not fear that what I have just said can [not] stand well with my own invention: let <b>Maestlin</b> himself say for me on how slender principles I stood at the beginning — who indeed, had he dismissed me as meagrely the second time as he had the first, would never have imparted to me so exquisite a commendation (to which, however, while I live, I shall be inferior). And so I, raised by this appearance of learning into a splendid opinion of Ursus, <b>fell in afterward with certain of the Styrian nobility, lovers of Mathematics, who, because of their necessary occasional stay at the Emperor's court, had acquaintance with Ursus.</b> They proclaimed the man's genius and inventions, urged me to strive to enter his familiarity, promised their help. But some described to me <b>a kind of Diogenes, from whom nothing could be got unless he pleased, or [it were] extorted with some improbity.</b> For these causes, a little after my invention by my own speculation — since there was no nearer Mathematician to whom I could more conveniently write — impelled to write, <b>I accommodated the very form [of the letter] partly to the man's reported nature, partly to those letters of most efficacious brevity which I then happened by chance to be reading — my mind dissolved into pomp from the recent love of my invention — secure about this outcome.</b> So that, if anything fell from me a little greater than I felt about him, in favour of his praisers and from love of myself, it is all of youthful temerity, and I excuse it no otherwise — provided I abolish the suspicion of crime.</p><p>From what I have said, you see, first, that <b>I attributed to him, with good conscience, the Glory by which he seemed to reign at the Emperor's court and among the Mathematicians whom he addressed in his booklet</b>; but why I said \"most illustrious,\" I find no cause, except that <b>our Illustrious [nobles] proclaim it, and the Most Illustrious [Emperor] seems to proclaim it, while he keeps him at Court — and that a pompous genius easily spots a fly, of which to make an Elephant.</b> To the same I refer that which (when it was established about some) I said — <i>if</i> I said it — that he is magnified by all the learned. For certainly I held Maestlin and Tycho among the learned, whom I knew not to think very splendidly of him. But that I would have declaimed even more in this manner, had time allowed, I do not remember promising. Nay, that protestation about garrulity, alien to Mathematicians, was not made to excuse even more of this kind, but rather to correct the earlier. <b>But if all and each of those words are mine — that one [Ursus] precedes the Mathematicians of this age as much as the Phoebean orb [precedes] the tiny stars — then, by Christ, I imprudently did great injury to many most distinguished men, and even to my own conscience.</b> This I can say most truly: <b>that never, in any way — seriously, in jest, publicly, privately, to the face, to the ear, from the heart, willing and prudent — did I say that one Ursus is to be preferred to Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Rheticus, Reinhold, Tycho, Maestlin, [and] the rest.</b> Never did I feel it, never with present mind did I write it, never did I approve so shameful an adulation. But if my genuine words sound such a thing (which I do not know), the fault is chance's, and my haste, and that I did not re-read what I had written. <b>It is all Poetic, as you see, and taken from a Poet, and said with a poetic spirit</b> — which not only now do I not want, but even then, when I wrote it, I did not want; I wanted it carried elsewhere, another [sense] represented: that he surpasses the vulgar — the vulgar, I say, of Mathematicians — which I both believed, and can happen short of [his having] chief glory. I would not protest with such asseveration against manifest words (if such they are), did there not exist, in that very letter, the greatest document of my innocence, whereof above. For <b>though in what words I made mention of you I do not know, yet I know I did make mention — and such a mention, indeed, and proceeding from such an opinion, as deservedly stings Ursus, if these self-praises so happily tickle him.</b> But cautiously, I see, must I write hereafter — so [much] do they teach [me], by ever whispering something \"salutary\" into Ursus's ear. The rest you allege are light: <b>I called [him] \"preceptor,\" because from his book I had drawn some useful things; \"most noble,\" because in these Austrian provinces erudition and a court office are given to the nobility; I made [them] his hypotheses, in which he changed something</b> — for, if I remember well, he too wanted the Earth [mobile], and differs in the circle of Mars, or, as you say, errs. For although I had once heard from Maestlin of this controversy between you and him, yet since <b>both Magini in Italy and Röslin in Alsace had, each for himself, derived something similar from the hypotheses of Copernicus, I reckoned that the same had been easy for Ursus too to devise.</b> Finally, that the end answers the beginning, [is] no wonder, nor any wickedness in the wish — that he flourish by the stars and by science. Let him indeed study that, with maledictions and vain ostentation omitted. And \"Ornament of Germany\" — to me, anyone is [that] to whom I attribute as much as I attributed to him, because <b>Mathematics is the true ornament of Germany alone.</b> These things [I write] so prolixly, both that I might testify my grief by a just complaint, and that it might appear that I reckon it <b>not at all nothing to my reputation, what you think of me.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondents.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Johannes Kepler → Tycho</b>, from <b>Graz</b>, <b>February 1599</b>. <b>Kepler's <i>apologia</i> for his 1595 letter to Ursus</b> — which Ursus had printed in his <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (1597), using Kepler's extravagant praise as an endorsement against Tycho. Tycho had written Kepler a stinging complaint (which never arrived; Kepler got a copy via <b>Maestlin</b> ten months later); this is the reply.</li><li><b>Significance — a keystone of the Tycho–Kepler–Ursus triangle, and a revealing self-portrait.</b> One of the most psychologically important letters in the history of science: the young, obscure Kepler, who had <b>flattered Ursus to win patronage</b> (\"one Ursus precedes the mathematicians of this age as much as the sun's orb the tiny stars\"; \"preceptor,\" \"ornament of Germany\"), now must <b>explain himself to the man he most needed to keep — Tycho</b>.</li><li><b>Ursus's betrayal.</b> Kepler is outraged that Ursus <b>published a private letter</b> without consent or a copy, <b>applied Kepler's praise to himself while reviling Tycho</b>, and \"dishonoured the one [Kepler] esteemed most [Tycho] with Kepler's own words.\" He invites Tycho, if needed, to <b>go publicly against the published calumny</b>.</li><li><b>The self-defence (the heart).</b> Kepler dismantles the charge of \"speaking against what he felt\": at the time he was <b>\"of weaker judgement,\"</b> dazzled by Ursus's book (read in three days, found \"new\" things he later traced to <b>Euclid and Regiomontanus</b>), urged on by <b>Styrian nobles</b> who knew Ursus at court, and warned Ursus was <b>\"a kind of Diogenes\"</b> yielding nothing unless flattered. He confesses the letter's <b>\"pomp\"</b> was <b>\"all Poetic … taken from a Poet, said with poetic spirit,\"</b> the excess of <b>\"youthful temerity\"</b> and \"love of my [new] invention\" — <i>not</i> a sincere ranking. He swears he <b>never truly preferred Ursus to Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Rheticus, Reinhold, Tycho, or Maestlin</b>.</li><li><b>The defence of the substance.</b> He explains each phrase: \"preceptor\" (he learned useful things from the book), \"most noble\" (Austrian custom gives office to nobility), \"most illustrious\" (the Emperor keeps Ursus at court); and notes that he <b>\"made them Ursus's hypotheses, in which he changed something\"</b> — observing, tellingly, that <b>Magini (Italy) and Röslin (Alsace) had each independently derived similar geo-heliocentric schemes from Copernicus</b>, so he thought Ursus could too. The crucial point of innocence: in the very same letter <b>he had also honoured Tycho</b> \"from such an opinion as deservedly stings Ursus.\"</li><li><b>The relationship preserved.</b> The closing is the key: Kepler writes \"so prolixly\" because <b>\"I reckon it not at all nothing to my reputation what you think of me\"</b> — the anxious deference that, despite the awkwardness, kept the door open to the <b>collaboration sealed in 1600</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000828001, brahe-t_16001017001).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Tycho–Kepler–Ursus</b> keystone: pair with Kepler's <i>analysis</i> of Ursus's plagiarism (brahe-t_16000911001), the Ursus legal endgame (brahe-t_16000926003), and the employment letters (brahe-t_16000828001, brahe-t_16001017001).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Johannes Kepler; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Ursus (Nicolaus Reimers Bär)</b>; <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (the intermediary, Kepler's teacher); <b>Regiomontanus, Copernicus, Rheticus, Reinhold, Tycho, Maestlin</b> (the truly great, whom Kepler would not rank below Ursus); <b>Euclid, Regiomontanus</b> (sources of Ursus's \"novelties\"); <b>Magini, Helisaeus Röslin</b> (independent geo-heliocentric derivers); <b>Diogenes</b> (the character-type for Ursus); <b>Rudolf II</b> (Ursus at court). Greek <i>γεωμετροῦντι</i> (\"to one doing geometry\") reproduced.</li><li><b>Date/place &amp; completeness (protocol §3).</b> Place omitted (<b>Graz</b>); dated <b>February 1599</b> (slug `15990200001`, label \"1599-02\"; no day). <b>The source as supplied ends mid-discourse, without valediction, date, or signature</b> — the famous Apologia-letter is longer; the dump/corpus entry appears <b>partial</b>. Flagged for a completeness check against the full text.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> (on the portion supplied) — dense, rhetorical, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the Maestlin-relayed complaint; the outrage at Ursus's <b>publication of a private letter</b> and its turning against Tycho; the layered self-defence — <b>\"weaker judgement,\" the three-day reading, the Styrian nobles, the Diogenes warning, the \"all Poetic\" excess of \"youthful temerity\"</b>; the sworn denial of really ranking Ursus above the great; the phrase-by-phrase exculpation with the <b>Magini/Röslin parallel</b>; and the closing deference to Tycho's opinion) is firmly and carefully construed, with Kepler's tortured rhetoric rendered faithfully and <b>without sharpening or softening</b>. The single material reservation is <b>completeness</b>: the source ends without valediction and is likely a partial text — flagged, not silently filled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII page images in chunks (1-4, 5-8) and located the letter's true bounds: it opens mid-slice-page-6 (printed p.141, heading 'IOANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE', source citation '[Ex apographo in Epistolis ad Ioannem Kepplerum scriptis ed. Hansch (1718) p.106 sqq.]') and closes on p.144 with 'quid tu de me sentias.' — printed p.145 unambiguously begins an unrelated letter (Tycho to Thaddaeus Hagecius, 16/26 Feb 1599, different addressee/heading), confirming the letter does not continue further. NOTE: the packet's tboo.printed_pages ('140-215', a 30-page slice) is far broader than the letter's actual 4-page span in Dreyer — likely a source-metadata citation error (possibly conflating this cover-letter with the much longer enclosed Apologia-contra-Ursum tract elsewhere in the volume). This is a metadata anomaly, not a translation defect; both source_text and published_english correctly terminate at the letter's true end. Collated word-for-word throughout: opening/closing lines; the Ursus-crime discussion; the Greek phrase γεωμετροῦντι (present in both Latin and Dreyer, correctly glossed); the Diogenes simile; the full six-name comparandum list REGIOMONTANO/COPERNICO/RHETICO/REINHOLDO/TYCHONI/MAeSTLINO (order and dative sense — 'Ursus preferred TO these' — both correctly preserved, no pronoun/subject flip); MAGINUS/ROeSLINUS-Alsatia; EUCLIDE/REGIOMONTANO; MAeSTLINUM/TYCHONEM as the 'inter doctos' pair. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15980401001", "next": "brahe-t_15991209001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980401001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15991209001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the ten-month delay receiving Tycho's reply via Maestlin, acknowledges most excerpted words in his Ursus letters as his own, but sees no proven crime in their publication.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990200001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990226001", "d": "1599-02-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "ck": "Thaddaeus Hagecius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek) — Wittenberg, 26 February 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: Prague, Univ. Library archive 62 B 6</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO DOCTORI THADDAeO HAGGECIO AB HAYCK, INCLYTI REGNI BOIEMIAe ARCHIATRO ORDINARIO, AMICO SUO VETERI ET HONORANDO.</p><p>S. D.</p><p>EXOPTATE accidit, excellentissime et amantissime Domine Doctor THADDAeE, amice honorande, quod heri magnifici et amplissimi Domini CORADUCIJ, Procancellarij Imperialis, literas humaniss. Pragam ex mandato Sae Imperatoriae Maiestatis, Domini nostri clementissimi, me evocantes accepi. Et parerem sane actutum huic augustae et clementissimae evocationi absque ulla mora, statimque iter ingrederer, si viarum transitus ubique ferme nivibus altis repletus id pateretur et equi idonei in promptu essent; nam meos, uti ultimis indicavi, nuper Lipsiae distrahi feci. Omnem tamen curam impendam, ut intra paucos dies pro uno vel altero curru alij coëmantur, aut si id commode fieri nequeat, conducantur, resque meas ita disponam, ut post 14 ab hinc dies iter, favente divino Numine, ad vos suscipere queam. Spero enim itinerum nimias nunc difficultates tum minus obfuturae; et interea expecto nuncium, quem, statim ac huc veni, in Daniam misi, ut inde mihi novum adferret viaticum, consumpto priore in itinere Dresdensi atque hîc, quemadmodum me prius tibi per literas significasse memini. Sin vero is ob hyemis duritiem et perseverantiam mariumque congelationem istic moram traxerit, ut ante dictum tempus adesse nequeat, dabo nihilominus operam, ut aliunde viaticum acquiram, ne ea de causa iter ulterius procrastinetur, sed assumptis mecum paucis comitibus et ministris, reliquos unâ cum familia mea muliebri hîc relinquam, donec ad vos excurrens de Caesaris clementissima voluntate certior factus fuero. Et, si intellexero vias per partem aliquam Slesiae commodiores, quam per Misniam (ut multi existimant) futuras, illac cursum dirigam, quanquam nonnihil longiore ambagine, ut sperem, me intra quatuor abhinc hebdomadas vel circiter, conatibus nostris favente divina ope, Pragae vobis adfuturum.</p><p>Cuperem autem quam maxime te ibi praesentem tunc reperire, ut tuo frui possim alloquio, et abs te consilium, sicubi opus fuerit captare, cum ego tanquam peregrinus locorum et hominum ignarus sim. Vellem etiam me in aliqua civitate, quam transire oporteat, tuis literis obvijs praemoneri, in quodnam Pragae hospitium, quod a lue tutum sit et mihi commodum, me conferre possim. Mallem id publicum non esse, ut tranquillus degam et propria etiam quadra, si id magis expediat, vivam. Per Slesiam autem, uti opinor, transituro, quoniam (veluti intelligo) primum Brandusium Pragae proximum occurrit, optarim me istac tuas, quae me de hospitio et alijs necessarijs instruant, invenire literas.</p><p>Oportune vero accidit, ut hodie sese offerret quidam auriga Olomucensis, VALENTINUS MULLER nomine, alias vacuo curru Pragam, unde hisce diebus venit, rediturus. Qui cistam quandam cum meis grandiusculis unâ cum alijs thecis secum advehit, in quibus est instrumentum quoddam cocleis disiungi et componi aptum atque tam distantijs quam altitudinibus siderum accurate rimandis serviens, et praeterea nonnulla alia huic exercitio deputata facile portatilia. Pepigi cum dicto auriga pro decem Ioachimicis, dimidium illi hîc solvi, reliquos 5 taleros eum a te accepturum promisi. Rogo itaque, ut recepta in tuas aedes hac cista, velis fidem meam redimere et pecuniam hanc aurigae meo nomine numerare, quam primum volente Numine advenero, tibi cum alijs mea de causa forte alias expositis nummis restituendam. Cuperem illo instrumento non saltem elevationem poli, interea dum ibi sum, accurate denotare, sed et observationi Planetarum, ♄ni praesertim tunc acronychij, diligenter attendere, cum sit iuxta limites maximae latitudinis boreae, quo in loco ante 29 annos me eundem Augustae Vindelicorum observasse memini, licet instrumentis tum non adeo exquisitis instructus, ut nunc integra eius peracta sit revolutio, quam in alijs quoque ipsius, quotquot interea habere licuit, acronychijs sitibus diligenter examinavi, uti et in reliquis duobus superioribus Planetis, quo Apogaea et Eccentricitates eorundem exactius, quam hactenus praestitum est, praefiniri possint, quod et satis praecise me assecutum nihil dubito. Sed de his et similibus plurimisque alijs coram Deo dante plenius agemus.</p><p>Scripsi etiam magnifico et amplissimo D. CORADUCIO de occasionibus, cur meus accessus aliquantulum differatur, ut morulam hanc pro suo candore eo promptius excusatam habeat, cui etiam tanquam fautori et promotori apud Caesaream Maiestatem benignissimo et habeo et perpetuo me debere agnosco gratias relaturus, si vires meae ulla ratione id unquam sustinuerint. Vale mi charissime THADDAeE, utque te sanum et prosperum Pragae inveniam, tuaque desideratissima frui possim consuetudine, faxit Deus. Dabantur Vitembergae die 26. Feb. stylo novo Anno etc. 99.</p><p>Tui amantissimus TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 146–147; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Saluta meo nomine quam officiosissime Dominum BARVICIUM.  /  POSTSCRIPTA. INDICARAM tibi antea me quaedam hic Witebergae typis complere in meis operibus decrevisse, praesertim ea, quae in Progymnasmatum torno primo tecum communicato desiderantur. Cumque papyrus idonea ac priori similis hic vel Lipsiae non praestaret, egi cum Chartopaeis in vicinia habitantibus, ut eiuscemodi chartam in copia mihi pararent, at illi negligentiores negotium nimium procrastinarunt, ut ob id eam, quam Wandesburgi residuam habui in Dania propria mea moletrina confectam, nuper huc advehi curârim. Sed quoniam mihi nunc Pragam ex clementissima Caesareae Maiestatis voluntate eundum est et iter maturandum, cogor ista omnia insecta relinquere, donec inchoatam telam pertexendi commodior detur occasio. Apposui quaedam carmina partim ab insigni huius Academiae Poëta et Professore TAUBMANO mihi, cum Dresdae essem, missa, partim a BRASCHIO eximio pariter Academiae Rostochiensis Poëta, interea dum hic sum, mihi impertita, si forte tibi ea legere vacarit; videntur in eo genere Horatianae odae arguta et erudita. Addidi etiam Domini Doctoris DAVIDIS CHYTRAeI literas, quae etiam Dresdae mihi redditae sunt, si quidem in ijs tui faciat mentionem; poteris illas usque in meum adventum reservare.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most excellent man, Lord Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius of Hájek, ordinary Chief-Physician [Archiater] of the renowned Kingdom of Bohemia, his old and honoured friend.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>As longed-for [<i>exoptate</i>] it happened, most excellent and most loving Lord Doctor Thaddaeus, friend to be honoured, that yesterday I received the most courteous letter of the magnificent and most ample Lord <b>Coraduz, Imperial Vice-Chancellor, summoning me to Prague by the mandate of the Sacred Imperial Majesty</b>, our most clement Lord. And I would indeed obey this august and most clement summons at once, without any delay, and would immediately set out, if the passage of the roads — almost everywhere filled with deep snows — allowed it, and if suitable horses were at hand; for mine, as I indicated in my last [letter], I lately had <b>sold at Leipzig</b>. Yet I will spend all care that within a few days others be bought for one or two wagons, or, if that cannot conveniently be done, hired; and I will so arrange my affairs that <b>after 14 days from now</b> I may, God favouring, undertake the journey to you. For I hope the now-excessive difficulties of the roads will then be less of a hindrance; and meanwhile I await the messenger whom, as soon as I came hither, I sent into Denmark to bring me <b>fresh travel-money [<i>viaticum</i>]</b> — the former being spent on the Dresden journey and here, as I remember I signified to you before. But if he, on account of the harshness and persistence of winter and <b>the freezing of the seas</b>, has drawn out his delay there, so that he cannot be present before the said time, I will nonetheless take pains to get travel-money from elsewhere, lest the journey be further put off; but, <b>taking a few companions and servants with me, I will leave the rest, together with my womenfolk, here</b>, until — running over to you — I am made more certain of the Emperor's most clement will. And if I understand that the roads through some part of <b>Silesia</b> will be more convenient than through <b>Meissen</b> (as many think), I will direct my course that way, although by a somewhat longer roundabout — so that I hope, <b>within four weeks from now or thereabouts</b>, by divine help favouring our endeavours, to be present with you at Prague.</p><p>But I should most greatly wish to find you present there then, that I may enjoy your conversation and take counsel from you wherever there be need, since I, as a stranger, am ignorant of the places and the men. I should also wish to be forewarned, by your letters meeting me in some city I must pass through, <b>into what lodging at Prague — safe from the plague and convenient for me — I may betake myself.</b> I should prefer it not be a public one, that I may live tranquilly, and even at my own table [<i>propria quadra</i>], if that be more expedient. But to me passing through Silesia, as I think — since (as I understand) <b>Brandeis</b>, nearest to Prague, first occurs — I should wish to find there your letters instructing me about the lodging and other necessaries.</p><p>But it happened opportunely that today a certain wagoner of <b>Olomouc, Valentin Müller by name</b>, offered himself, otherwise about to return with an empty wagon to Prague, whence he came these days. He carries with him <b>a certain chest with my larger [things], together with other cases, in which is a certain instrument, fit to be taken apart and assembled by screws, serving for accurately searching out both the distances and the altitudes of the stars</b>, and besides some other things assigned to this exercise [of observation], easily portable. I agreed with the said wagoner for <b>ten joachimsthalers</b>, paid him half here, [and] promised that he would receive the remaining 5 thalers from you. I ask therefore that, this chest received into your house, you would redeem my pledge and pay this money to the wagoner in my name — to be restored to you, as soon as, God willing, I arrive, with other monies perhaps otherwise laid out on my account. I should wish, with that instrument, not only to note the elevation of the pole accurately while I am there, but also to attend diligently to the observation of the Planets — <b>especially of Saturn [♄], then acronychal [at opposition], since it is near the limits of maximum northern latitude</b>; in which place I remember to have observed the same <b>29 years ago at Augsburg</b>, though then furnished with instruments not so exquisite — so that <b>now its complete revolution has been accomplished</b>, which I have also diligently examined in its other acronychal positions, as many as I could obtain meanwhile, as too in the remaining two superior Planets [Jupiter and Mars], <b>so that the Apogees and Eccentricities of the same may be defined more exactly than has hitherto been done</b> — which I doubt not I have attained precisely enough. But of these and similar things, and very many others, we will treat more fully in person, God granting.</p><p>I have also written to the magnificent and most ample Lord Coraduz about the reasons why my coming is somewhat deferred, that he may the more readily hold this little delay excused, according to his candour — to whom also, as my most kindly patron and promoter with the Imperial Majesty, I both give and acknowledge that I perpetually owe thanks, to be repaid if my powers ever in any way sustain it. Farewell, my dearest Thaddaeus; and may God grant that I find you healthy and prosperous at Prague, and may enjoy your most longed-for company. Given at Wittenberg, the 26th of February, New Style, the year etc. [15]99.</p><p>Most loving of you, <b>Tycho Brahe</b>.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Greet Lord Barwitz in my name as dutifully as may be.  /  POSTSCRIPT. I had indicated to you before that I had resolved to complete certain things here at Wittenberg in print, among my works — especially those which are wanting in the first volume of the Progymnasmata communicated to you. And since suitable paper, like the former, was not to be had here or at Leipzig, I dealt with the paper-makers dwelling in the neighbourhood, that they should prepare me such paper in quantity; but they, the more negligent, too much procrastinated the business, so that on that account I lately had brought hither that [paper] which I had left over at Wandsbek, made in Denmark at my own mill. But since I must now go to Prague by the most clement will of the Imperial Majesty, and must hasten the journey, I am compelled to leave all these things unfinished, until a more convenient occasion be given of weaving to the end the web I have begun. I have appended some poems — partly sent to me, when I was at Dresden, by Taubmann, the distinguished Poet and Professor of this Academy; partly bestowed on me, while I am here, by Braschius, likewise an eminent Poet of the Academy of Rostock — if perhaps you have leisure to read them; in that kind they seem witty and learned, like Horatian odes. I have also added the letter of Lord Doctor David Chytraeus, which was likewise delivered to me at Dresden, inasmuch as he makes mention of you in it; you may keep them until my arrival.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius ab Hayck</b> (Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku, 1525–1600), imperial-Bohemian <i>Archiater</i> (chief physician), astronomer, and Tycho's oldest and most trusted Bohemian friend and broker at the imperial court (cf. brahe-t_15990321001 and the long Hagecius series). He died later in 1599; this is among the last of their letters.</li><li><b>Significance — the eve of the journey to Prague (the imperial call answered).</b> Written from <b>Wittenberg</b> the day after Tycho received <b>Coraduz's letter of summons</b> (by Rudolf II's mandate) to <b>Prague</b>. The letter is the logistical heart of the move that would make Tycho Imperial Mathematician:</li><li><b>Roads &amp; horses:</b> deep snow everywhere; Tycho had <b>sold his horses at Leipzig</b> (his last letter), must buy or hire wagon-teams; reckons <b>~14 days</b> to set out, <b>~4 weeks</b> to reach Prague.</li><li><b>Money:</b> awaiting a <b>messenger sent to Denmark for fresh <i>viaticum</b></i> (the earlier spent on the Dresden leg); the <b>seas frozen</b> may delay him, so Tycho will raise funds elsewhere rather than wait.</li><li><b>Household:</b> he will take <b>a few companions and servants</b>, <b>leaving the rest with his womenfolk at Wittenberg</b> until the Emperor's will is confirmed in person.</li><li><b>Route:</b> <b>Silesia (via Brandeis/Brandýs)</b> vs. <b>Meissen</b> — Silesia longer but better; asks Hagecius to send letters ahead to a transit city.</li><li><b>Lodging:</b> begs Hagecius to secure a <b>private, plague-free lodging</b> at Prague (not a public inn), where he can keep his <b>own table</b>.</li><li><b>The instrument shipment &amp; the Saturn observation (the astronomical core).</b> A wagoner of <b>Olomouc, Valentin Müller</b>, returning empty to Prague, carries ahead a <b>chest with a screw-jointed portable instrument</b> for measuring <b>stellar distances and altitudes</b> — for <b>10 joachimsthalers</b>, half paid, half to be paid by Hagecius on receipt. Tycho means to use it at once to fix the <b>pole-elevation</b> at Prague and, above all, to observe <b>Saturn at opposition (<i>acronychus</i>) near its maximum northern latitude</b> — <i>the same configuration he had observed 29 years before at Augsburg</i> (<b>c. 1570</b>, with cruder instruments). Saturn's ~29-year period means <b>one full revolution has now elapsed</b>, letting him refine the <b>apogees and eccentricities of the three superior planets (Saturn, Jupiter, Mars)</b> — a vivid glimpse of the continuity of Tycho's planetary program from his youth to the imperial years.</li><li><b>Coraduz.</b> <b>Rudolf von Coraduz (Korad/Coraduz)</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Procancellarius</i>), Tycho's chief patron-broker at court; Tycho writes him separately to excuse the delay.</li><li><b>Notable readings.</b> <i>exoptate</i> (opening) is written in the source as the Greek-lettered flourish <b>ΕΧΟΡΤΑΤΕ / EXΟΡΤΑΤΕ</b> = Latin <i>exoptate</i>, \"as longed-for/desired\" — rendered accordingly (minor orthographic flag, sense secure). <i>propria quadra</i> — \"[at] my own table/fare\" (Juvenalian idiom for self-catering). <i>acronychus/-ij</i> — <b>acronychal</b>, i.e. rising at sunset = at opposition (glossary term). <i>grandiuscula</i> — \"my larger [things]\" (the bulkier instruments/effects).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Thaddaeus Hagecius; <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Rudolf II</b> (<i>Caesar</i>); <b>Valentin Müller</b> of Olomouc (wagoner). Places: <b>Wittenberg</b> (whence written), <b>Leipzig</b>, <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Silesia</b>, <b>Meissen</b>, <b>Brandeis (Brandýs)</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Augsburg</b> (the 1570 observation).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Vitembergae die 26. Feb. <b>stylo novo</b> Anno … 99</i> — Tycho dates it <b>explicitly New Style</b> (anticipating the imperial reckoning, though Saxony itself was still Old Style) → <b>26 February 1599 [N.S.]</b>. Header and label agree.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing greeting (Barwitz) + postscript (the Progymnasmata paper-failure, Wandsbek-mill paper, Taubmann/Braschius odes, the Chytraeus letter), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.146–147 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the dropped numeral \"5\" in \"reliquos 5 taleros\" (Dreyer, TBOO VIII, p.146); the English \"remaining thalers\" is now \"remaining 5 thalers\" to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the logistical narrative (summons, snow-blocked roads, horses sold at Leipzig, <i>viaticum</i> from Denmark, ~14-day/~4-week timetable, Silesia-vs-Meissen route, leaving the womenfolk, plague-safe private lodging) and the astronomical passage (Valentin Müller's chest, the screw-jointed distance/altitude instrument, <b>Saturn at opposition near max northern latitude, one full ~29-year revolution since the c. 1570 Augsburg observation</b>, apogees/eccentricities of the superior planets) are firmly construed and internally consistent. The only flag is the Greek-lettered opening <i>exoptate</i>, secure by sense; no substantive crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.145-147 as page images (slice pp.144-148; letter runs 145-147, confirmed by the preceding and following letters' headers on pp.144 and 147). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against source_text and found one departure: Dreyer prints 'reliquos 5 taleros' where our source_text and the translation both read 'reliquos taleros', dropping the numeral. Checked every other name/number/date against the English: Coraduz/Procancellarius (no first name given, correctly omitted), 14 days, Silesia/Meissen, four weeks, Brandeis (Brandusium), Valentin Muller of Olomouc, ten joachimsthalers, 29 years/Augsburg, Barwitz, Taubmann/Wittenberg, Braschius/Rostock, David Chytraeus, and the closing date 26 February N.S. 1599 — all correct. The one text divergence is real and citation-affecting, so despite an otherwise flawless and complete translation this cannot stand at the self-assigned grade A.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer's printed text specifies the remaining sum as '5 taleros' (arithmetically consistent: 10 joachimsthalers, half paid = 5, 5 owed); our house Latin source_text silently drops the numeral '5', and the published English inherits the omission, losing a specific sum that a citation-grade edition should preserve. Dreyer prints: <i>Pepigi cum dicto auriga pro decem Ioachimicis, dimidium illi hîc solvi, reliquos 5 taleros eum a te accepturum promisi. (TBOO VIII p.146)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VIII pp.144-148; the disputed reading falls on p.146, examined directly and confirmed to read \"reliquos 5 taleros\". Both the Latin apparatus and the English translation, which had silently inherited the same omission, are now corrected. No remaining uncertainty.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Prag, Universitetets arkiv, 62 B 6."], "th": {"label": "Thaddaeus Hagecius (Hájek)", "count": 37, "pos": 37, "prev": "brahe-t_15981201001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15981201001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports the imperial summons to Prague via Coraduz, explains delay due to deep snow and horses sold at Leipzig, and plans to depart within fourteen days.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990226001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990302001", "d": "1599-03-02", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Veit Winsheim", "ck": "Veit Winsheim", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Veit Winsheim — Wittenberg, 2 March 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO VITO WINSHEMIO, I. V. DOCTORI EXIMIO, ET DECANO HAMBURGENSI, AMICO SUO HONORANDO.<br>HAMBURGK.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et Praestantissime Vir, Amice honorande. Accepi binas a T. Ex. literas, postquam huc Wittebergam veni, quibus iam antea respondissem, si per negotia licuisset, et tabellarius oportune in promptu fuisset, quo nunc se offerente, non intermittendum duxi, quin aliqua saltem, utut et nunc occupatissimus, ad te darem. Ago autem T. Ex. gratias, quod amicitiae nostrae memor, me suis invisere non sit degravata, et peto, ut tarditatem in rescribendo prorsus candore excusatam habere velit. Existimo Ex. Tuam ex filio meo TYCHONE, cum istic esset, adducturus huc Matrem cum sororibus, intellexisse, in quo statu res meae essent, et qua de causa huc Wittebergam me recepissem, monstrato Magnifici Domini CORADUCIJ Procancellarij Imperialis rescripto, quod, dum Dresdae essem, nactus sum. Praeterita vero septimana mihi denuo allatae sunt Amplissimi illius CORADUCIJ literae, me ex clementissima Imperatoriae Maiestatis Voluntate hinc Pragam benignissime evocantes, quarum exemplum addidi. Accingo itaque me nunc itineri, ut, quam primum fieri possit, me istic Caesareae Maiestati subiectissime sistam, relicturus interea hîc Gynaeceum meum, donec, quomodo Caesarea Maiestas erga me meaque studia animata sit, coram intellexero. Deus Opt. Max. faxit, ut omnia prosperos habeant successus. Caeterum Dominum OLIGERUM ROSENKRANTZIUM nostrum de hac Augusta (ut et CORADUCIUS eam in literis appellat) evocatione, certiorem per Tuam Ex. reddi, misso illi hoc exemplo, aut eius descripto averem, idque ut quam primum certus in illas oras datur nuncius expediat, peramanter rogo. Plura nunc non vacat. Te itaque protectioni Divinae commendatum habeo.</p><p>Datae Wittebergae die 2. Martij Anni 99.<br>Tuae Excell. studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most distinguished and most excellent man, Master Veit Winsheim, distinguished Doctor of Both Laws and Dean at Hamburg, his friend to be honoured. Hamburg.</b></p><p>Hearty greeting.</p><p>Most distinguished and most excellent man, friend to be honoured. I received two letters from Your Excellency after I came here to Wittenberg, to which I would already have replied, had business allowed and a courier been opportunely at hand; one now offering itself, I judged I should not omit to send you at least something — however most occupied I am even now. And I give Your Excellency thanks that, mindful of our friendship, you did not find it burdensome to visit me with your [letters]; and I ask that you be willing to hold my tardiness in replying excused with all candour. I think Your Excellency understood, from my son Tycho — when he was there, [intending] to bring hither [his] Mother with [his] sisters — in what state my affairs were, and for what cause I had betaken myself here to Wittenberg, the rescript of the Magnificent Master Coraduz, Imperial Vice-Chancellor, having been shown [him], which I obtained while I was at Dresden. But this past week the letters of that most ample Coraduz were brought to me again, most kindly summoning me from here to Prague, by the most clement will of the Imperial Majesty — a copy of which I have added. I therefore now gird myself for the journey, that, as soon as possible, I may present myself most submissively there to the Imperial Majesty, leaving meanwhile here my women's household, until I have understood in person how the Imperial Majesty is disposed toward me and my studies. May God Best and Greatest grant that all [things] have prosperous outcomes. For the rest, I would be eager that our Master Holger Rosenkrantz be made more certain, through Your Excellency, about this august summons (as Coraduz too calls it in his letters) — this copy, or a transcript of it, being sent to him; and I most lovingly ask that you despatch it as soon as a sure messenger into those regions is given. There is no more leisure now. I therefore hold you commended to the Divine protection.</p><p>Given at Wittenberg, the 2nd of March, in the year [15]99.<br>Most zealous of Your Excellency,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Veit Winsheim</b> (<i>Vitus Winshemius</i>), <i>I[uris] V[triusque] Doctor</i> (Doctor of Both Laws) and <b>Dean at Hamburg</b> — a jurist-correspondent of Tycho in his Hamburg orbit; the letter is addressed to Hamburg.</li><li><b>The imperial summons relayed.</b> Tycho reports that, while at <b>Dresden</b>, he had a <i>rescriptum</i> of Vice-Chancellor <b>Coraduz</b>; and that \"this past week\" Coraduz's letters came again, \"<b>summoning me from here to Prague by the most clement will of the Imperial Majesty</b>\" — i.e. the formal summons of brahe-t_15990127001 (27 Jan 1599). Tycho is now girding for the journey, and asks Winsheim to forward a copy of \"<b>this august summons</b> (<i>Augusta evocatio</i>, as Coraduz too calls it)\" to <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> in Denmark.</li><li><b>Family.</b> <i>filius meus Tycho … adducturus huc Matrem cum sororibus</i> — Tycho the Younger had been at Hamburg, to bring <b>Tycho's wife (their mother) and the daughters (sisters)</b> to Wittenberg; <i>Gynaeceum meum</i> (\"my women's household / womenfolk\") whom Tycho leaves at Wittenberg while he goes ahead to Prague to test the Emperor's disposition.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Wittebergae die 2. Martij 99</i> = <b>2 March 1599</b>; Wittenberg (Lutheran Saxony) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the content (the relay of Coraduz's imperial summons toward Prague; leaving the womenfolk at Wittenberg; the request to inform Holger Rosenkrantz) is clear and dovetails with the summons letter brahe-t_15990127001 and the Wittenberg-winter cluster. <i>Gynaeceum</i> rendered \"women's household / womenfolk.\"</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.146-149 as page images. Letter 'CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO VITO WINSHEMIO...' runs from the foot of p.147 to p.148, dated 'Datae Wittebergae die 2. Martij Anni 99.' source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim word for word. Collated opening/closing and every proper name (Coraduz/Coraducij, Holger Rosenkrantz/Oligerum Rosenkrantzium, Dresden, Prague, Wittenberg) and clause; the English is a complete, accurate rendering including the technical term 'Gynaeceum' ('women's household') with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Winsheim for letters, apologizes for delayed replies, and confirms his son has already informed him of the imperial summons to Prague via Coraduz.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990302001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990307001", "d": "1599-03-07", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Holger Rosenkrantz → Tycho Brahe — [Denmark], 7 March 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>OLIGERUS ROSENKRANTZ AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>[IGNES spirant, noctesque diesque meditantur,] sed in proprias fauces regurgitandos.</p><p>[Si meo non certe affectu ullo temerato,] sed gravissimis quam plurimis argumentis confirmato, et meditationibus, mihi crede, satis multis et constantibus maturo [consilio parere volueris], quod spero, [suaserim primum omnium, litteris regi destinatis insulam Huennam quasi gratias pro regia liberalitate] (prudenter et placide submonitis submonendis) [agens rursum deferas], ut tamen quo [tua tibi cadant instrumenta], prudenter ͻ: Politice submoneas. Alterum vero est, ne [expensarum invisam et odiosam restitutionem] vel saltem velle [exigere videaris], nedum vel [exigas vel exagites]. Vis, sufficientes dicam rationes et mearum meditationem momenta. [Dicam, sed ne quaeso mihi succenseas, bono] tibique optime [cupienti animo haec dico]. Primum, si feceris, ante omnia, [magnanimitatem tuam] ostenderis. Dein [te omnimodo liberum ab omni iurisdictione Regis vindicaris], quod vel unicum quantum siet, ipse perpende, quod tamen nisi hoc modo fieri nequit. Adhaec [si retineas, videas ne te tanquam en Lensmand, ut bona tibi a Rege concredita inhabites, iure quodam suo cogere praesumat]. Et ut omnia uno: [quamdiu insulam eam retinueris, tanquam subditus cuivis vocationi parere,] quibus certe magnas [tibi parare possunt] incommoditates et difficultates, insuper [citatus, ipse, seu praesens comparere], quod tibi valde foret difficile et prorsus impossibile, [aut quod ais pronunciatum perferre cogereris]. Et vero quid non [ipse] magna tua cum laude et praeter multorum existimationem [eam deferre malles], cum [possint ipsi, ubi velint, etiam quodam iure eam praecipere, pretendentes] quippe [litteras tibi a Rege concessas] (quae quidem [ad vitam eam] tibi [appropriat insulam]) sub [conditione] Astronomiae [hic in Dania excolendae conceptas] esse. Tu ipse [ingenia eorum nosti]; nihil certe intermiserint, ut interim [immensum istud discrimen inter alium aliquem nobilem Regis subditum et te] (in quo non postrema [bonitatis causae tuae] ratio vertitur) vel alto silentio [dissimulent] vel [malitiose elevent]. Ad alterum cogites velim, quam nec natura nec eius imitatores quid frustra committant, [iure vero quidpiam tentare] frustraneum simul ac periculosum, ubi et quod me tacente ipse satis intelligis, [quam inconstantes sint principum animi,] simul ac [quam non corvus unus alteri, quod dicitur, oculos eruat], tecum attentissime pro tua prudentia perpendas velim. Bono haec animo dico, fidem testor et candorem; nec dicerem nisi bono. Nec quidpiam video, licet videre nunquam non laborem, quod [rebus tuis ulla ratione adversari queat et institutum tuum tandem turbare queat], praeter [haec duo]. Sed haec Deo et tuae prudentiae.</p>", "en": "<p><b>HOLGER ROSENKRANTZ TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>[They breathe fire, and brood on it night and day,] but [it is] to be spewed back down their own throats.</p><p>[If — by an affection on my part certainly not warped by any (bias),] but confirmed by very many most weighty arguments and (believe me) by reflections sufficiently many and constant — [you will be willing to heed] mature [counsel], as I hope, [I would advise, first of all, that — by a letter addressed to the King — you surrender back the island of Hven, as it were rendering thanks for the royal liberality] (the things to be intimated having been prudently and calmly intimated), [acting so that you hand it back again]; yet so that you prudently — that is (ͻ:), <b>politically</b> — give notice in such a way [that your instruments may fall to you], (i.e. that you keep your instruments). The second [point] is this: that you not [seem] even to wish [to demand] the [hateful and odious restitution of your expenses], much less [demand it or stir it up]. You wish me to state sufficient reasons, and the weight of my reflections. [I will state them — but please do not be angry with me; with a good] heart, and [wishing you the very best, I say these things]. First, if you do it, you will above all have displayed [your magnanimity]. Next, [you will free yourself wholly from all jurisdiction of the King] — which, even as a single thing, weigh for yourself how great it is — a thing which, however, cannot be done except in this way. Besides, [if you retain it, see to it lest he presume to compel you, by some right of his own, as a <i>Lensmand</i> [fief-holder], to reside upon the goods entrusted to you by the King]. And to put it all in one: [as long as you retain that island, [you would be bound], as a subject, to obey any summons] — by which they can certainly contrive great inconveniences and difficulties [for you] — and moreover, [if cited, to appear yourself, in person], which would be very hard for you and altogether impossible, [or you would be forced to suffer the sentence pronounced, of which you speak]. And indeed, why would you not yourself rather, [with great praise to yourself and beyond many men's expectation, surrender it], since [they themselves can, when they will, seize it even by a certain right, alleging] indeed [that the letters granted you by the King] (which do indeed [appropriate that island to you] [for life]) were drawn up under the [condition] [of Astronomy being cultivated here in Denmark]. You yourself [know their dispositions]; they will assuredly have left nothing undone, so that meanwhile they either [conceal] in deep silence, or [maliciously belittle], that [immense difference between you and some other noble subject of the King] (wherein turns not the least part of [the goodness of your cause]). As to the second [point], I would have you consider how neither Nature nor her imitators undertake anything in vain — whereas [to attempt anything by law] is at once futile and perilous; where also — what, though I be silent, you well enough understand yourself — [how inconstant are the minds of princes,] and likewise [how, as the saying goes, one crow does not peck out another's eyes], I would have you weigh most attentively with yourself, according to your prudence. I say these things with a good heart; I call my good faith and candour to witness; nor would I say them but for good. Nor do I see anything — though I never cease labouring to see — that could in any way oppose your affairs and at last disturb your undertaking, save [these two things]. But these [I leave] to God and to your prudence.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" (1574–1642), the Danish nobleman and scholar, Tycho's <i>consanguineus</i> (kinsman) and intimate friend; the dataset and the neighbouring correspondence confirm the heading <i>Oligerus Rosenkrantz ad Tychonem Brahe</i> (cf. brahe-t_15980328001 apparatus, which already cites \"a 7 March 1599 letter\" of this title). Writing from Denmark, hence <b>Old Style</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — political counsel on surrendering Hven.</b> A candid, confidential letter of advice at the crux of Tycho's break with Denmark. Holger urges two things: <b>(1)</b> that Tycho <b>formally surrender the island of Hven back to the King by letter, framed as thanks for the royal liberality</b> — <i>prudenter, ϲ: Politice</i> (\"prudently — that is, politically\") — while quietly securing that <b>he keep his instruments</b>; and <b>(2)</b> that Tycho <b>not demand reimbursement of his expenses</b> (the great sums sunk into Uraniborg/Stjerneborg), which would be \"hateful and odious.\"</li><li><b>The reasoning.</b> Holger's case: voluntary surrender (a) shows <b>magnanimity</b>; (b) <b>frees Tycho wholly from the King's jurisdiction</b> — whereas while he holds Hven he remains, <i>tanquam en Lensmand</i> (\"as a <i>Lensmand</i>,\" a royal fief-holder), a <b>subject liable to any summons</b>, who could be cited to <b>appear in person</b> (impossible for the now-absent Tycho) or made to <b>suffer a pronounced sentence</b>. He warns that the crown party can in any case <b>reclaim Hven \"by a certain right,\"</b> since the royal letters granting Tycho the island <i>for life</i> were issued <b>on condition that astronomy be cultivated in Denmark</b> — a condition Tycho's departure had voided. He counsels against any recourse to <b>law</b> (<i>iure quidpiam tentare</i> — \"futile and perilous\"), citing the <b>inconstancy of princes</b> and the proverb that the great close ranks against an outsider.</li><li><b>Code-switch &amp; abbreviation.</b></li><li><i>en Lensmand</i> — <b>Danish</b> embedded in the Latin: \"a <i>Lensmand</i>,\" holder of a royal <i>len</i> (fief). Kept and glossed.</li><li><i>ͻ:</i> (reversed-C colon) — the early-modern siglum for <i>id est</i> / <i>scilicet</i>, \"that is\"; it glosses <i>prudenter</i> as <i>Politice</i> (\"politically\"). Rendered \"that is (ϲ:)\".</li><li><b>Proverbs / allusions.</b> <i>noctesque diesque meditantur</i> echoes the Virgilian/Ennian epic cadence (cf. <i>noctes atque dies</i>); <i>corvus … alteri … oculos [non] eruat</i> = the classical <i>cornix cornici oculos non effodit</i>, \"one crow does not peck out another crow's eyes\" — i.e. men of the same order protect one another against an outsider; <i>bonum animum</i>/<i>candorem testor</i> are the friendship-formulae of frank counsel.</li><li><b>Textual crux — the square brackets (flagged, not resolved; protocol §4).</b> The source presents the body in <b>alternating bracketed and un-bracketed stretches</b>, with the bracketed portions carrying the politically sensitive content (the King, his jurisdiction, the surrender of Hven, the reclaim-by-right) and the un-bracketed words supplying connective tissue (<i>sed … quod spero … ut tamen … Vis … Primum …</i>). The bracketing is the <b>edition's</b>. <b>Resolved by collation (2026-06-02) against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII (pp. 148, 162–163) + the vol. IX critical apparatus (p. 309):</b> the brackets are the edition's <b>editorial restorations and conjectures of a single defective witness</b> — the Langebek <i>Diplomatarium afskrift</i> (the apparatus reads <i>regurgitandos conj. Friis] regurgitandas cod.</i>, i.e. Friis emending the one codex by conjecture) — <b>not</b> the decipherment of an enciphered letter. The bracketed spans and their boundaries match the edition of record exactly. The brackets are reproduced as-is in the source block; the translation renders the full text continuously, marking the bracketed spans.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz (sender); <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (recipient); the <b>King</b> (Christian IV) and his noble <i>subditi</i> — the crown party — unnamed individually; the island of <b>Hven</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dated 7 March 1599; <b>Old Style</b>, per the Danish sender. No place in the dateline; \"[Denmark]\" supplied in the header as the evident origin, flagged by brackets.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the political argument is read securely and rendered faithfully, with every hedge preserved. <b>Upgraded [C]→[B] (2026-06-02) after Dreyer collation</b> closed the former crux: the square brackets are the edition's <b>editorial restorations/conjectures of a single defective witness</b> (the Langebek <i>afskrift</i>; vol. IX apparatus <i>regurgitandos conj. Friis] regurgitandas cod.</i>), <b>not</b> cipher-decipherment, and the bracketed spans/boundaries match Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII pp. 148, 162–163; IX apparatus p. 309) exactly. The <i>en Lensmand</i> code-switch and the <i>ͻ: = id est</i> siglum are handled explicitly. It is [B] rather than [A] only because the sole witness is itself a defective copy of a lost original.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.147-150 as page images. 'OLIGERUS ROSENKRANTZ AD TYCHONEM BRAHE' runs from p.148 across the p.148/149 break to 'Sed haec Deo et tuae prudentiae.' on p.149 -- a heavily bracketed reconstruction of an enciphered letter (the entire body except a few connective words is Dreyer's own editorial bracketed supplement). Collated the full Latin word for word against Dreyer, including bracket placement: our published source_text matches Dreyer's printed text exactly (one word, fiet/siet, is genuinely undecidable in this typeface since long-s and f are visually near-identical at word-initial position, so it is not reported as a finding). Per the brief's instruction to ignore clearly-marked bracketed supplements, did not fault the English's necessarily interpretive rendering of the bracketed cipher-reconstruction, but confirmed the English's bracket boundaries track the source's bracket boundaries throughout, so no reconstructed material is silently presented as certain text. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 19, "prev": "brahe-t_15981231002", "next": "brahe-t_15990507001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15981231002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990507001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Advises Tycho to surrender Hven to the King tactfully by letter, keeping his instruments, and not to seek restitution of expenses; text is heavily lacunose throughout.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990307001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990320001", "d": "1599-03-20", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Patrick Gordon", "ck": "Patrick Gordon", "ck2": "Jon Jacobsen Venusinus", "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Helsingør", "t": "Patrick Gordon → Jon Jacobsen Venusinus — Elsinore, 20 March 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Ny kgl. Saml. 8vo, Nr. 271, p. 67f. (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>REVERENDO ET CLARISSIMO VIRO D:NO MAGISTRO JONAe JACOBO VENUSINO, AMICO ET PATRONO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO. HAFNIAM.</p><p>Reverende domine, amice pl. observande.</p><p>CUM post longam peregrinationem superiori aestate patriam et amicos reviserem, multi viri docti, qvis Germaniae, Daniae, et vicinorum regnorum status esset, diligenter ex me qvaesiverunt, inter qvos Johannes Cragius magni apud nostrates nominis astronomus, qvia magnam aetatis partem in Germania consumpserat, qvi nunc ibidem in mathesi excellerent vehementer scire avebat. Cum ego cum multis aliis Tychonem Brahe etiam nominassem, statim ille: fastuosus homo ille, qvi multa profert, sed pavca probat, qvia mecum aliqvando in lite qvadam philosophica minus pro arbitrio certasset, gravissimam de me ad Scotiae regem qverelam detulit, et non ita pridem, inqvit, ab ipso epistolas nonnullas aeditas ajunt, in qvibus me sine nomine acriter perstrinxit, sed nullam inde lavdem reportabit. Proinde isto fumi venditore relicto, dic, ait, anne alios in Dania nosti astronomos insignes. Ego honorificam tui, ut debui, mentionem feci, et post varios de humanitate tua sermones magnetis tui memini. Is qvia se multa de hoc lapide scire existimabat, amicitiam contrahere, et qvicqvid singulare in mathesi experientia didicisset, tecum communicare desiderabat. Certior a me de naturae tuae bonitate et exqvisita eruditione factus, literas meis inclusas ad te misit; qvod serius venerint, effecit turbulentus Svediae status, qvo ego praecipiti avtumno delatus fui. Qvaeso nunc boni consule; adiunctae sunt etiam aliae ad Tychonem, si possis efficere, ut ad ipsum perferantur, cures rogo. Sin minus, ad me remitte; aperire, si placet, poteris meo periculo; hanc mihi potestatem dedit Cragius. Si cras ad me rescripseris, avt ipse ad te veniam avt Svediae meumque statum ad te perscribam, jam avriga properabat. Navigandi Gedanum occasionem hic expectare cogor.</p><p>Vale cum toto coetu tuo.<br>Helsingora xx martii Mdic. T. r. a.<br>Patricius Gordonius</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the reverend and most distinguished man, Master Jon Jacobsen Venusinus, friend and patron much to be observed. To Copenhagen.</b></p><p>Reverend lord, friend much to be observed.</p><p>When, after a long peregrination, last summer I revisited my homeland and [my] friends, many learned men diligently asked of me what was the state of Germany, of Denmark, and of the neighbouring kingdoms; among whom John Craig — an astronomer of great name among our [countrymen], because he had spent a great part of his life in Germany — was vehemently eager to know who now excelled there in mathematics. When I had named, among many others, Tycho Brahe also, at once he [said]: \"That arrogant man, who puts forth much but proves little — because he once contended with me, in a certain philosophical dispute, less to [his own] liking, he brought a most grave complaint about me to the King of Scotland; and not long ago, they say, some letters were published by him, in which he sharply censured me without [naming my] name — but he will carry off no praise thereby. Accordingly, leaving that smoke-seller aside,\" he says, \"tell [me], do you know other notable astronomers in Denmark?\" I made honourable mention of you, as I ought; and, after various discourses about your kindness, I made mention of your magnet [loadstone]. He, because he thought he knew much about this stone, desired to contract a friendship, and to share with you whatever singular [thing] he had learned in mathematics by experience. Made more certain by me of the goodness of your nature and your exquisite erudition, he sent letters, enclosed in mine, to you; that they have come rather late, the turbulent state of Sweden brought about, whither I was carried in the headlong autumn. I pray [you], now take [it] in good part. There are also adjoined other [letters], to Tycho — if you can bring it about that they be conveyed to him, see to it, I pray. But if not, send [them] back to me; you can open [them], if you please, at my risk — Craig gave me this power. If tomorrow you write back to me, either I myself will come to you, or I will write out to you [the affairs] of Sweden and my own state — [but] now the carter was hastening. I am compelled to await here the occasion of sailing to Danzig.</p><p>Farewell, with your whole company.<br>Elsinore, the 20th of March, 1599. Most devoted to Your Reverence,<br>Patrick Gordon.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>A document of the Tycho–Craig controversy.</b> The Scottish traveller <b>Patrick Gordon</b> reports to <b>Jon Jacobsen Venusinus</b> a conversation with <b>John Craig</b> (<i>Johannes Cragius</i>, d. 1620), the Scottish physician-astronomer (later physician to <b>James VI/I</b>), Tycho's antagonist in the long <b>dispute over comets</b> (Craig held comets to be sublunary/optical; Tycho refuted him). Hearing Tycho named, Craig burst out that he is \"<b>that arrogant man, who puts forth much but proves little</b>,\" a \"<b>smoke-seller</b>\" (<i>fumi venditor</i> — charlatan); complains that, after their \"philosophical dispute,\" <b>Tycho had lodged a grave complaint against him with the King of Scotland</b>, and had \"<b>published letters censuring me anonymously</b>\" — the anti-Craig matter in Tycho's <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596). A rare outside witness to how bitterly Craig resented Tycho. (<i>de me … detulit</i>: read with Tycho as subject — Tycho complained to James VI <i>about Craig</i> — per the historical record; the reported speaker is Craig.)</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jon Jacobsen Venusinus</b> (Jon Jakobsen Venusin, d. 1608), Danish polymath, royal historiographer and professor at Copenhagen, with a known interest in the <b>magnet</b> (<i>magnes</i> — loadstone); Gordon reports that Craig, hearing of Venusinus's magnetic studies, wishes to correspond. The letter forwards <b>Craig's letters to Venusinus</b> and, enclosed, <b>further letters for Tycho</b> — so the piece enters the Tychonic correspondence as a transmittal.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <i>nostrates</i> = \"our countrymen\" (Scots); <i>Scotiae rex</i> = <b>James VI of Scotland</b>; <i>Gedanum</i> = <b>Danzig (Gdańsk)</b>; the \"turbulent state of Sweden\" and Gordon's autumn passage allude to the <b>Swedish succession crisis</b> (Charles vs Sigismund). <i>T. r. a.</i> = <i>Tuae Reverentiae addictissimus</i> (\"most devoted to Your Reverence\").</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Helsingora xx martii Mdic</i> — <i>Mdic</i> = <b>M D IC</b> = 1000 + 500 + 99 (subtractive <i>IC</i> = 99) = <b>1599</b>; <b>Elsinore (Helsingør)</b>, Denmark = <b>Old Style</b>. <b>20 March 1599.</b> Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> In Craig's reported speech, corrected 'less to [my] liking' to 'less to [his own] liking': the Latin's unstated possessive on 'pro arbitrio' is reflexive to the dispute's subject (Tycho), not to Craig, per the sentence's grammar and Dreyer's printed text (TBOO XIV p.151).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substance (Craig's scornful outburst against Tycho — \"arrogant,\" \"smoke-seller,\" the anonymous attack in the <i>Epistolae</i>, the complaint to James VI; Venusinus's magnet; the forwarding of letters to Tycho) is clear and historically corroborated (the Tycho–Craig comet dispute). Grade [B] for the one genuinely ambiguous clause — <i>gravissimam de me ad Scotiae regem querelam detulit</i>, whose subject (Tycho complaining <i>about</i> Craig, vs Craig <i>about</i> Tycho) is grammatically open; resolved, and flagged, in favour of Tycho-as-subject on the historical record.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer XIV pp. 150-152; letter is no. 160, entirely on p. 151). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin, including the date line 'Helfingora xx martii Mdic.' (=1599) and the dedication 'Ex apographo in Bibliotheca Regia Hauniensi: Ny kgl. Saml. 8vo Nr. 271, Side 67 f.' Checked every name (Johannes Cragius, Jon Jacobsen Venusinus, Patricius Gordonius), place (Helsingora/Elsinore, Gedanum/Danzig), and the date. One MISTRANSLATION found: the bracketed '[my]' possessive on 'pro arbitrio' misattributes whose satisfaction is at stake, reversing the sense of who came off worse in the Tycho-Craig dispute -- a real interpretive point about the anecdote's balance, not a stylistic quibble. Everything else in the letter is faithful.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The subject of 'certasset' throughout this clause is 'ille' (Tycho), so the unstated possessor of 'pro arbitrio' defaults reflexively to Tycho, not to the first-person narrator Craig -- i.e. the dispute went less to TYCHO's own liking (he came off worse), which is what supplies the causal motive for 'he [Tycho] brought a grave complaint about me to the King of Scotland' in the next clause. The English's bracketed '[my]' reassigns the dissatisfaction to Craig, reversing who was bested in the exchange -- turning Craig's implicit boast about worsting Tycho into an ambiguous statement about his own disappointment, and breaking the causal logic of why Tycho then complained to the king. At: “he once contended with me, in a certain philosophical dispute, less to [my] liking”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.150-152 (full slice, read in one pass); the clause is confirmed verbatim on p.151. The correction itself turns on standard Latin reflexive-possession grammar (unmarked possessives default to the clause's grammatical subject), which the page confirms textually; the interpretation is not contested elsewhere in the sentence, since it alone restores the causal 'because...he complained' logic. Single defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Ny kgl. Saml. 8vo, Nr. 271, p. 67 f.. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports John Craig's disparagement of Tycho as arrogant, his grievance over Tycho's complaint to the King of Scotland, and his dismissal of Tycho's published letters against him.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990320001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990321001", "d": "1599-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Wittenberg, 21 March 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>SCRIPSI tibi eruditissime et mihi plurimum dilecte M. CHRISTIANE bis, postquam huc Wittebergam veni, et semel a te literas accepi sub initium huius anni Ripis datas, ubi et priores meas tibi redditas ex tuis percepi, alteras postmodum Rostochium misi, quas te, quamprimum per hyemis asperitatem eo navigare licuit, ibi reperisse existimo. Ad tuas vero nunc brevibus, cum antea non sit oblata occasio, respondebo. Gratissimam rem praestas, quod in refutatione Capnuraniae Scoticae diligentem operam loces. Sunt saltem speciosa et pomposa eius omnia, ac veritati fucum faciunt. Invenies in libro nostro secundo de Cometa anni 77. suo loco, praesertim ubi in NOLTIJ placita disquiro, quantum in devia ab observatis detrudatur Cometes, si 5 proxime graduum parallaxin admisisset. Subsidium, quod expetis ab alijs Cometis, evidentius rem ostendentibus, satis firmum suppetit Cometa anni 80, qui et in meridiano et in 90 gradu iuxta horizontem non aliam declinationem mutavit, quam postulavit eius alteratio diurna, ex meridianis altitudinibus successive explorata, habita tamen refractionis in decliviori situ insinuatione, ut de alijs nunc nihil addam. Verum has confirmationes a pluribus Cometis petitas postea subiungere licebit, indicatione aliqua prius facta in Antapologia tua, qua introspecta, ulterius quid sentiam scies, quod mallem praesenti oretenus, quam absenti per literas indicare. De bruto Dithmarsico non ob id scripsi, quod a te redargutionem calumniarum eius expeterem, neque enim dignus est; sed saltem ut denotares obiter in charta aliqua, quae a nostris domesticis de plagio eius inaudivisti, quemadmodum fecit M. CHRISTIANUS IOHANNIS Ripensis, alterum expediet D. D. IOHANNES ROTHMANNUS, uti manes fratris defuncti postulant. Constitueram hîc edere sex illos quaterniones, qui in ☾ desiderantur, atque sic Tomum primum Progymnasmatum absolvere, ut in hisce Francofurdianis nundinis venalis extare possit, et iam inchoatus erat labor ille Typographicus, postquam papyrum meam impressoriam Wandesburgo acceperam. Verum dum Eclipsi ☾ die ultimo Ianuarij diligenter hîc per mea instrumenta attendo, deprehendi eius initium per ⅖ unius horae quam proxime calculum praevertere, ideoque a proposito destiti, donec huic deviationi provisum fuerit. Scis enim me exactam requirere concordantiam, quoad fieri possit. Nec satis mirari possum, quî fiat, quod Eclipsis anni 81, in Ianuario etiam facta et prope Apogaeum, tam belle nostris numeris conveniat, haec autem pariter iuxta Apogaeum et eodem anni tempore ita discrepet. Suspicor igitur aliquam adhuc inaequalitatem Anomaliae Lunae subesse, quae post aliquot annos peracta Eclipsium ad similia ferme loca restitutione praesertim iuxta Apogaeum et Perigaeum evidentius pateat, qua de re tecum, qui omnes nostras Eclipses diligenter examinasti, atque ipsarum varietates perspectas habes, conferre averem, ut eo citius huic obstaculo succurratur, quod facile fieri posse existimo. Sum in alijs rebus occupatissimus, ut satis huic disquisitioni vacare nequeam. Si igitur me accesseris cum auriga Wittebergensi, ubi isthuc reversus fuerit, rem mihi apprime gratam nec tibi inutilem praestiteris.</p><p>Sum denuo evocatus Pragam ex Clementissimo Caesaris mandato, per literas amplissimi Domini Procancellarij CORADUCIJ, ut ex adiuncto exemplari vides; et paro nunc omnia ad iter, quod tamen ante Pascha ingredi non licebit ob aquarum nimiam copiam, postquam nives subito dissolutae sunt, vias difficillimas et periculosas reddentem. Si adfueris et me comitari libuerit, dabo operam, ut tuis rebus rectius consulatur, quam uspiam alibi, sin vero hîc manere aut redire isthuc mavis, erit et hoc tibi liberum. Ego tuis commodis quocunque modo, quantumque in me est, sedulo studebo. Non patiar, ullum erga te officium desiderari.</p><p>Vale et literas has nemini ostende.<br>Dabantur Wittebergae die 21. Martij 1599.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTEN LONGOMONTANUS.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>I have written to you twice, most learned and most beloved Master Christen, since I came hither to Wittenberg, and once received a letter from you, sent from Ribe at the beginning of this year — wherein I also perceived from yours that my earlier ones had been delivered to you; the others I afterward sent to Rostock, which I think you found there as soon as it was possible to sail thither through the harshness of winter. But to yours I will now reply briefly, since no occasion was offered before. You do a most welcome thing, in placing diligent effort on the <b>refutation of the Scottish <i>Capnurania</b></i>. All his things are at least specious and pompous, and put a [false] colour on the truth. You will find, in our second book on the Comet of [15]77, in its place — especially where I examine <b>Noltius's</b> opinions — how far the Comet would be thrust into deviations from the observations, had it admitted a parallax of nearly 5 degrees. The support you seek from other Comets, more evidently showing the matter, is firmly enough supplied by the <b>Comet of [15]80</b>, which, both on the meridian and at the 90th degree along the horizon, changed no other declination than its diurnal alteration required (explored successively from the meridian altitudes, the influence of <b>refraction</b> in the more inclined position being however taken into account) — to say nothing now of the others. But these confirmations sought from several Comets may be appended afterward, some indication being first made in your <b><i>Antapologia</b></i>; which once examined, you will further know what I think — which I should prefer to indicate to you present, by word of mouth, than to you absent, by letter. Concerning <b>the Dithmarsic brute</b> I did not write that I might seek from you a refutation of his calumnies — for he is not worthy of it — but only that you might note down in passing, on some paper, what you have heard from our household about his <b>plagiarism</b>, as <b>Master Christian Jensen of Ribe</b> did; the other [part] Lord Doctor <b>Johannes Rothmann</b> will expedite, as the shade of his deceased brother demands. I had resolved to publish here those <b>six quaternions that are wanting in [the theory of] the Moon</b>, and so to complete the <b>first Tome of the <i>Progymnasmata</b></i>, that it might be on sale at this Frankfurt fair; and that typographic labour was already begun, after I had received my printing-paper from Wandsbek. But while I diligently attend here, with my instruments, to the <b>Eclipse of the Moon on the last day of January</b>, I found that its beginning <b>anticipated the calculation by very nearly ⅖ of one hour</b> — and therefore I desisted from my purpose, until this deviation be provided for. For you know I require exact concordance, so far as can be done. Nor can I sufficiently wonder how it comes about that the eclipse of [15]81 — also in January and near the <b>Apogee</b> — agrees so beautifully with our numbers, while this one, likewise near the Apogee and at the same season, so discrepates. I suspect, therefore, that <b>some inequality of the Moon's Anomaly still underlies it</b>, which after some years — by the return of eclipses to nearly the same places, especially near Apogee and Perigee — may appear more evidently; about which I should wish to confer with you, who have diligently examined all our eclipses and have their varieties thoroughly known, that this obstacle may the sooner be remedied (which I think can easily be done). I am most occupied with other matters, so that I cannot be sufficiently free for this disquisition. If, therefore, you come to me with the <b>Wittenberg coachman</b>, when he has returned thither, you will do me a thing exceedingly welcome, and not unprofitable to you.</p><p>I am again summoned to <b>Prague</b> by the Emperor's most clement mandate, through a letter of the most ample Lord Vice-Chancellor <b>Coraduz</b> — as you see from the enclosed copy; and I now prepare everything for the journey, which, however, it will not be permitted to begin before Easter, on account of the excessive abundance of waters (after the snows were suddenly thawed), rendering the roads most difficult and dangerous. If you are present and it please you to accompany me, I will take pains that your affairs be better provided for than anywhere else; but if you prefer to stay here, or to return thither, this too will be free to you. I will assiduously strive for your interests, in whatever way, and as far as in me lies; I will not allow any service toward you to be wanting.</p><p>Farewell, and <b>show this letter to no one</b>.<br>Given at Wittenberg, 21 March 1599.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>, then in Denmark (at Ribe), Tycho's senior assistant (cf. brahe-t_15981231001, brahe-t_15990111001). Written from the <b>Wittenberg winter</b> of 1598–99. The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.</li><li><b>The Craig refutation — <i>\"Capnurania Scotica.\"</b></i> Tycho welcomes Longomontanus's work on <b>refuting \"the Scottish <i>Capnurania</i>\"</b> — Tycho's mocking coinage (Greek <i>kapnos</i> \"smoke\" + <i>Urania</i>) for <b>John Craig's</b> cometary doctrine that comets are <b>terrestrial smoke/vapour</b>: \"all his things are specious and pompous, and put a false colour on the truth.\" (The commission of brahe-t_15981231001; <i>Antapologia</i> = Longomontanus's counter-defence against Craig's <i>Apologia</i>.) Tycho points him to his <b>second book on the comet of 1577</b> (the <i>De mundi … recentioribus phaenomenis</i>) — esp. against <b>Noltius</b> — and to the <b>comet of 1580</b> as decisive evidence (its declination changed only by diurnal parallax, refraction accounted for), arguing comets show <b>no large (≈5°) parallax</b> and so are <b>celestial, not sublunary</b>.</li><li><b>The Ursus plagiarism dispute (\"the Dithmarsic brute\").</b> <i>brutum Dithmarsicum</i> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (\"Ursus\")</b> of Dithmarschen, who had <b>plagiarised Tycho's geo-heliocentric system</b> and attacked him (in <i>De hypothesibus astronomicis</i>, 1597). Tycho — holding him \"not worthy\" of formal refutation — asks Longomontanus only to <b>note down what the household heard of his plagiarism</b>, as <b>Master Christian Jensen of Ribe</b> had; \"the other part <b>Doctor Johannes Rothmann</b> will expedite, as the shade of his deceased brother demands\" — apparently <b>Johannes Rothmann vindicating the memory of his late brother</b> (most plausibly <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, the Hesse-Kassel mathematician, whose work Ursus had also touched; identification flagged, not asserted).</li><li><b>The lunar-theory crux (technical; protocol §5.1).</b> Tycho had begun printing the <b>six quaternions of the lunar theory</b> to finish the <b>first Tome of the <i>Progymnasmata</b></i> for the <b>Frankfurt fair</b> — but <b>halted</b> when the <b>lunar eclipse of 31 January [1599]</b> began <b>≈ ⅖ hour (24 min) before his calculation</b>. He marvels that the eclipse of <b>1581</b> (also January, near <b>apogee</b>) fitted his numbers \"beautifully,\" while this one (likewise near apogee, same season) \"discrepates,\" and <b>suspects an unaccounted inequality of the Moon's anomaly</b> — to be tested by eclipses returning near <b>apogee/perigee</b> — wanting Longomontanus (who knows all his eclipses) to help. A first-rate document of Tycho's still-unfinished lunar theory.</li><li><b>The Prague summons.</b> Tycho is <b>again summoned to Prague</b> by <b>Coraduz's</b> letter (enclosed in copy), preparing the journey — not before <b>Easter</b>, the thaw-flooded roads being \"most difficult and dangerous.\" He offers Longomontanus to accompany him (with better provision than anywhere else) or to stay/return, as he prefers.</li><li><b>Glyphs/terms (§5.1).</b> ☾ = <b>Moon</b>; <i>parallaxis</i> → <b>parallax</b>; <i>refractio</i> → <b>refraction</b>; <i>declinatio</i> → <b>declination</b>; <i>Apogaeum/Perigaeum</i> → <b>apogee/perigee</b>; <i>Anomalia</i> → <b>anomaly</b>; <i>inaequalitas</i> → <b>inequality</b>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Longomontanus; <b>John Craig</b> (the <i>Capnurania</i>); <b>Noltius</b>; <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b> (\"the Dithmarsic brute\"); <b>Christian Jensen of Ribe</b>; <b>Johannes Rothmann</b> (and his late brother — Christoph Rothmann?); <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Wittebergae die 21. Martij 1599</i>; Wittenberg/Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Latin corrected 'detrudetur' to the subjunctive 'detrudatur' (Dreyer VIII p.149); English unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is read securely; the substance (the Craig/<i>Capnurania</i> refutation and the cometary-parallax argument from the 1577 and 1580 comets; the Ursus-plagiarism brief and the Rothmann/Jensen helpers; the halted lunar-theory print and the 31 Jan 1599 eclipse anomaly vs. 1581; the Prague summons via Coraduz, delayed by floods) is firmly construed on the §5.1 terms and corpus-corroborated. The one open identification — <i>Johannes Rothmann</i> and \"his deceased brother\" (probably Christoph Rothmann) — is flagged, not asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.147-152 as page images; the audited letter (Tycho to Longomontanus, 21 March 1599) runs pp.149(bottom)-151(top), between the Rosencrantz correspondence and the Scaliger letter. Collated opening/closing lines and every proper name (Noltius, Antapologia, M. Christianus Iohannis Ripensis, Johannes Rothmannus, Coraducius), date (21 Martij 1599, Eclipse 'die ultimo Ianuarij', years 77/80/81), and figure (the ⅕-hour discrepancy) against Dreyer; translation is accurate throughout. One TEXT_ERROR found in source_text (see above); per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though its severity is low and the translation itself is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints the present subjunctive 'detrudatur' (required inside the indirect question governed by 'disquiro'); our source_text has the indicative-looking 'detrudetur'. A single-letter corruption of the verb's mood. The English translation is unaffected — it happens to render the counterfactual sense correctly regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>quantum in devia ab obfervatis detrudatur Cometes, fi 5 proxime graduum parallaxin admififfet (TBOO VIII p.149)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.149), which prints the subjunctive 'detrudatur Cometes, si 5 proxime graduum parallaxin admisisset.' Corrected the single-letter mood error.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15990111001", "next": "brahe-t_15990820001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Encourages Longomontanus's refutation of the Scottish Capnurania and directs him to the second book on the 1577 comet, particularly the Noltius section, for parallax arguments.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990321001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990417001", "d": "1599-04-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joseph Justus Scaliger — Wittenberg, 17 April 1599 [Old Style]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 2do (copy); B = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter (copy); C = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek — copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text note.</b> The working dump carried OCR artefacts (stray apparatus-sigla \"A\" keyed to the three witnesses, and run-together words); the source block below is <b>normalized</b> to clean readable Latin (sigla removed, word-spacing restored), faithful to the wording.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS et Magnifice Vir, Amice inprimis honorande. Redditae mihi sunt tuae literae unâ cum libris mihi dono missis serius quam expectassemus, et ego, quod multis distentus fuerim negotijs atque alias varie praepeditus, nec in promptu esset, qui meas certo referret, tardius etiam quam decuisset, quamque ipse maluissem, rescribo, quod mihi a tua ingenua humanitate condonari cupio. Pro utroque autem libro mihi tam benigne concesso proque tuo non modico labore in excerpendis ijs, quae apud HIPPARCHUM, et nonnunquam etiam PTOLOMAeUM in hoc negotio de Fixis Sideribus praecipua erant, ijsdemque propria manu in mei gratiam tanta diligentia conscriptis, non possum tibi promeritas satisque dignas agere gratias, nedum referre, neque enim video qua ratione hoc tuum tam eximium in me beneficium compensare valeam, animum tamen gratum, et tui honoris studiosissimum semper me servaturum tibi persuadeas, et si quae ex mea tenuitate in hisce, quibus utrinque afficimur, studijs proficisci poterint, ea non invite tecum vicissim communicabo. Fateris ipsemet, Vir Excellentissime, multa esse in his, quae circa Fixas tam ab HIPPARCHO quam PTOLOMAeO consignata sunt, quae suis numeris rite non constent, nec sibi ipsis consentiant, et sane res ita se habet; ego enim paucissima in ijs reperio, quae convenienter ad Coeli normam digesta sint, deviatione satis manifesta prorsusque intolerabili plurimis in locis sese prodente, sive id Observationum incuria (quas tamen sic nimis crassas fuisse oportuisset) sive transcriptorum negligentia aut quacunque alia de causa id commissum fuisse existimemus. Si loca Fixarum a me summa diligentia praeteritis aliquot annis explorata, pro quibus ipsum Coelum etiamnum testatur, retro ad tempora PTOLEMAEI et HIPPARCHI, per motum interea ijs competentem, numeraverimus, atque hinc Declinationes, Culminationes, Ortus et Occasus quoscunque ad eadem quibus illi usi sunt Climata deduxerimus, certe rarissime cum eorum annotatis consentire depraehendentur: Et licet nonnunquam concordantia aliqua inveniatur, tamen ea ἐν πλάτει tantum et nimis lato modo sumta est; nullatenus enim admitti poterit stellas irregulariter et diversimode interea motas fuisse, cum omnes uniformem et regularem in uno eodemque Orbe observent progressionem, quod invariata earundem ad invicem distantia et dispositio indubitate ostendit, quae vel hinc liquet, quod omnes Fixae, quae ab HIPPARCHO et PTOLOMAeO in uno lineae rectae ductu existere proditae sunt, etiamnum eundem prorsus tramitem conservent, prout a nobis sedulo disquisitum est. Miror itaque a tam Excellentibus Artificibus non satis accuratam in his praestitam esse diligentiam, cum res sit magni momenti, nec Planetarum motiones certo explorari queant, nisi prius ad amussim praefinita sint Fixarum loca, quod etiam me in ijs, quae PTOLOMAeUS circa Erraticarum apparentias tradidit, non parum reddit ancipitem quoad exactam illam, quam desidero, rationem. Attamen loca inerrantium stellarum, quae ex Abaco HIPPARCHI suo Almagesto inseruit, non videntur tantam enormitatem prae se ferre, atque haec est, quam liber transmissus in plerisque exhibet.</p><p>Verum his nunc omissis, quae limitare, atque quatenus fieri potest in concinniorem rationem redigere, suo forte tempore elaborabimus, aliud est quod nunc potius desiderarem, de quo etiam, si recte memini, prius te rogavi, ut nimirum Aequinoctiorum omnium ab HIPPARCHO observatorum rationes designatas obtineam, ita ut Annis Iulianis retro subactis rite applicentur, et simul quomodo Annus ille respectu bissexti se habuerit, indicetur: Cuperem vero ipsa HIPPARCHI verba, quatenus illa eiuscemodi observationes concernunt, nihil enim horum in libris mihi impertitis invenire licuit; tum quoque si quas alias in his vetustissimas in promptu habes coelitus deductas animadversiones. Australes Asterismos nobis Europaeis hactenus incognitos convenienti diligentia observatos necdum vidi, repraesentarunt quidem circa Polum Austrinum stellas quasdam nobis incompertas Globorum in vestra Batavia confectores; sed nimis ieiuna et parca est ista designatio, crediderim multo plures isthic apparere. Si plenior et accuratior earundem descriptio penes te est, eamque mecum communicare non degravatus fueris, rem admodum acceptam praestiteris. ALBATEGNIUM quoque fidelius quam antea castigatum, si tua opera aliquando obtinuerimus, non parum commodi hisce studijs inde accedet. Utinam Arabicum Exemplum obtinere posses; neque enim dubito quin, cum linguae illius, uti et plurimarum aliarum usque ad miraculum, apprime gnarus sis, hac in parte plurimum sis huic studio profuturus.</p><p>Porro ut rerum mearum statum aliquatenus perspectum habeas, scias me, postquam e loco prioris meae habitationis discesseram, in Bohemiam ex Caesareae Maiestatis clementissima voluntate cogitans, Dresdam Misniae ubi pervenissem, omniaque in Bohemia ob luem Epidemicam isthic passim grassantem conturbata esse cognovissem, substitisse ibi paulisper, donec mihi ex Aula Caesarea praescriptum erat, ipsius Maiestatem clementissime cupere, ut alicubi in vicinia me continerem, donec malum illud Epidemicum remitteret. Ideoque huc Wittebergam propter Academiae celebritatem me aliquantisper recepi. Evocatus autem sum nuper denuo Pragam, ex clementissimo Caesaris mandato, per literas Amplissimi Domini RUDOLPHI CORADUCIJ Procancellarij Imperialis. Et quamprimum quaedam in libris nonnullis, quos Caesareae Maiestati humilime offerre decrevi, plane absolvero (quod brevi me effecturum spero) denuo iter Pragense ingredi, atque Caesareae Maiestatis benignissimae voluntati debita subiectione morem gerere in animum induxi. Deus Optimus Maximus successus prosperos et exoptatos largiri dignetur. Ubi vero portum aliquem quietum pro meis studijs, sive isthic sive alibi invenero, plenius te de rebus nostris edocebo, atque de ijs, quae studia haec, quibus iucunde utrinque afficimur, concernunt, uberius tecum conferre non intermittam. Interim haec, ob plurimas occupationes pro praesenti mittendi ad vos literas occasione deproperata, boni consule, inque coepto erga me favore et amore persiste.</p><p>Vale Vir Illustris et Excellentissime, seculi nostri decus.<br>Dabantur subito Wittebergae die 17 Aprilis Anni 99, st. vet.<br>Illustri Magnif. tuae addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOSEPH SCALIGER.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Illustrious and magnificent Man, friend especially to be honoured. Your letter, together with the books sent me as a gift, was delivered to me later than we had expected; and I — both because I have been distracted by many affairs and otherwise variously hindered, and because there was not at hand one who could surely carry mine — write back later too than was fitting, and than I myself should have preferred; which I wish to be pardoned me by your noble humanity. For both books so kindly granted me, and for your no small labour <b>in excerpting what was chief in Hipparchus, and sometimes also in Ptolemy, in this business of the Fixed Stars — and these written out in your own hand, for my sake, with such diligence</b> — I cannot give you the thanks deserved and worthy, much less repay them; nor do I see by what means I might requite this so signal benefit of yours toward me. Yet persuade yourself that I shall always keep a grateful mind, most zealous of your honour; and if anything can proceed from my slenderness in these studies, with which we are both affected, I will not unwillingly share it with you in turn. You yourself confess, most excellent Man, <b>that there are many things in what was recorded about the Fixed [stars] by both Hipparchus and Ptolemy which do not duly tally with their own numbers, nor agree with themselves</b> — and indeed the matter is so; for I find very few in them digested fitly to the norm of the Sky, <b>a deviation manifest enough and altogether intolerable betraying itself in very many places</b> — whether we judge it committed by carelessness of the Observations (which must then have been far too gross), or by the negligence of transcribers, or from any other cause. <b>If we count the positions of the Fixed [stars], explored by me these past years with the utmost diligence (for which the Sky itself still testifies), back to the times of Ptolemy and Hipparchus, by the motion [precession] belonging to them meanwhile</b>, and thence derive declinations, culminations, risings and settings whatsoever for the same Climes they used, they will certainly very rarely be found to agree with their notations. And although some concordance is sometimes found, yet it is taken only <b>broadly [ἐν πλάτει]</b> and in too loose a manner. <b>For it can in no way be admitted that the stars moved irregularly and diversely in the interim, since all keep a uniform and regular progression in one and the same Orb</b> — which their unchanged mutual distance and disposition undoubtedly shows; and this is clear even from this, that <b>all the Fixed [stars] which were reported by Hipparchus and Ptolemy to lie in one straight line still keep the very same track</b>, as has been carefully investigated by us. I wonder, then, that by such Excellent Artificers a diligence not accurate enough was shown in these things — since the matter is of great moment, and <b>the motions of the Planets cannot be surely explored unless the positions of the Fixed [stars] are first precisely predetermined</b> — which also makes me not a little doubtful, in what Ptolemy transmitted about the appearances of the Wandering [stars], as to that exact reckoning which I desire. Yet the positions of the fixed stars which <b>[Ptolemy] inserted into his Almagest from the Abacus [table] of Hipparchus</b> do not seem to bear so great an enormity as this which the book transmitted [by you] exhibits in most places.</p><p>But, these things now set aside — which to limit, and so far as can be done reduce to a neater reckoning, we shall perhaps work out in their time — there is <b>another thing I would rather now desire, about which (if I recall rightly) I asked you before: namely that I may obtain the reckonings of all the Equinoxes observed by Hipparchus, set down so that they may be rightly applied with the Julian years reduced backward, and at the same time that it be indicated how that year stood with respect to the bissextile [leap year].</b> I should wish indeed Hipparchus's own words, so far as they concern such observations — for I could find none of these in the books imparted to me — and also any other most ancient celestial observations of this kind you have at hand. <b>The Southern Asterisms, hitherto unknown to us Europeans, observed with fitting diligence, I have not yet seen</b>: the makers of Globes in your Holland [Batavia] did indeed represent, around the South Pole, certain stars unknown to us; but that designation is too meagre and sparse — I should believe that many more appear there. If a fuller and more accurate description of them is with you, and you would not find it a burden to share it with me, you will do a most welcome thing. <b>Al-Battani [Albategnius] too, more faithfully corrected than before, if we should one day obtain it by your labour, will bring no small advantage to these studies. Would that you could obtain an Arabic copy</b>; for I do not doubt that, since you are excellently skilled in that tongue (as in very many others, to a miracle), you will in this part greatly profit this study.</p><p>Further, that you may have my situation somewhat clear: know that I — after I had departed from the place of my former dwelling, intending [to go] into Bohemia by the Imperial Majesty's most clement will — when I had reached <b>Dresden in Meissen</b>, and had learned that everything in Bohemia was thrown into confusion by the epidemic plague raging there far and wide, <b>halted there a while</b>, until it was prescribed me from the Imperial Court that His Majesty most clemently wished me to keep myself somewhere in the neighbourhood until that epidemic evil should abate. And therefore <b>I withdrew hither to Wittenberg for a while, on account of the Academy's renown.</b> But I have lately been summoned again to Prague, by the Emperor's most clement mandate, <b>through a letter of the most ample Lord Rudolf Coraduz, Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b>. And as soon as I shall have quite finished certain things in <b>some books which I have resolved humbly to offer the Imperial Majesty</b> (which I hope to do shortly), I have resolved to set out again on the Prague journey, and to comply with the Imperial Majesty's most kindly will with due submission. May God, Best and Greatest, deign to grant prosperous and longed-for success. And when I find some quiet port for my studies, whether there or elsewhere, I will instruct you more fully of my affairs, and will not cease to confer more amply with you about what concerns these studies, with which we are both pleasantly affected. Meanwhile take in good part these [lines], hurried for the present by the occasion of sending a letter to you, amid very many occupations; and persist in the favour and love toward me you have begun.</p><p>Farewell, illustrious and most excellent Man, the ornament of our age.<br>Given hastily at Wittenberg, the 17th day of April, the year [15]99, Old Style.<br>Most devoted to your illustrious Magnificence,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> at Leiden — the chronologist and philologist. This is the <b>earlier</b> of the two Scaliger letters in the corpus (cf. the July-1600 precession letter, brahe-t_16000723001 / 16000623001).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's critique of the ancient star catalogues (a major statement of his fixed-star programme).</b></li><li><b>The Hipparchus/Ptolemy catalogues are corrupt.</b> Tycho agrees with Scaliger that much in Hipparchus's and Ptolemy's fixed-star data \"does not tally with its own numbers nor agree with itself,\" with \"intolerable deviation\" — from gross observation, transcribers' negligence, or other cause.</li><li><b>The precession test.</b> Tycho's method: take <b>his own carefully-determined star positions and reckon them back, by precession, to Ptolemy's and Hipparchus's epochs</b> — they \"very rarely agree.\" The stars cannot have moved irregularly, since <b>their mutual distances are invariant and collinear stars stay collinear</b> — a clean statement of the rigidity of the stellar sphere under uniform precession.</li><li><b>Why it matters.</b> \"<b>The motions of the Planets cannot be surely explored unless the positions of the Fixed [stars] are first precisely predetermined</b>\" — the methodological core of Tycho's whole enterprise (and the ground of his doubt about Ptolemy's planetary data).</li><li><b>A textual observation.</b> The star-positions Ptolemy drew <b>from Hipparchus's \"Abacus\" into the Almagest</b> seem less corrupt than the (more enormous) figures in the book Scaliger sent.</li><li><b>Three requests to Scaliger:</b> <i>(1)</i> <b>Hipparchus's own equinox observations</b>, set to reduce against Julian years (with leap-year status) — for chronology/precession; <i>(2)</i> a <b>fuller catalogue of the new Southern asterisms</b> — Tycho has seen only the \"meagre\" southern stars on the <b>Dutch globe-makers'</b> globes (the Keyser–Houtman southern constellations via the Dutch globes; cf. the globe-maker / Blaeu-at-Uraniborg thread, the letter to Tobias Fischer of 16 May 1600, brahe-t_16000516002) and believes \"many more appear there\"; <i>(3)</i> <b>a better-corrected al-Battani (Albategnius), ideally an Arabic copy</b> — praising Scaliger's \"miraculous\" command of Arabic and other tongues.</li><li><b>Biographical status report (spring 1599).</b> Tycho's itinerary after leaving Hven/Germany: <b>→ Dresden</b> (halted by the <b>Bohemian plague</b>) <b>→ Wittenberg</b> (\"for the Academy's renown\") — now <b>summoned again to Prague by Coraduz's letter</b>, to set out once he finishes <b>the books he means to offer the Emperor</b> (the <i>Mechanica</i> / <i>Progymnasmata</i> presentation set). A precise fix on the pre-Benátky interim.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Joseph Justus Scaliger; <b>Hipparchus</b>, <b>Ptolemy</b> (the <i>Almagest</i>, Hipparchus's <i>Abacus</i>); <b>al-Battani (Albategnius)</b>; <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <b>Dutch globe-makers</b>. Places: <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Dresden/Meissen</b>, <b>Bohemia/Prague</b>, <b>Holland (Batavia)</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 17 Aprilis Anni 99, <b>st. vet.</b></i> → <b>17 April 1599 [Old Style]</b> (Tycho explicitly marks Old Style at Wittenberg/Saxony); header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the thanks for Scaliger's hand-copied Hipparchus/Ptolemy excerpts; the <b>critique of the ancient star catalogues</b> and the <b>precession back-reckoning test</b> with the rigidity-of-the-sphere argument; the methodological primacy of fixed-star positions; the three requests — <b>Hipparchus's equinoxes, the Southern asterisms, a corrected al-Battani</b>; and the <b>Dresden→Wittenberg→Prague</b> status report under the Coraduz summons) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. The Greek <i>ἐν πλάτει</i> is reproduced; the source block is a normalized reading of the OCR-marred dump (apparatus sigla removed), faithful to the wording.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.151-153 as page images (slice pp.150-154; letter runs from mid-p.151 to mid-p.153, bracketed by the Longomontanus letter of 21 March on p.150-151 and the Bongarsius letter of 1 May on p.153-154). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against source_text (exact match, including double-checking one dense clause 'convenienti diligentia observatos' by a targeted re-read of p.152, which is genuinely present in Dreyer and correctly rendered as 'observed with fitting diligence'). Verified every name/number/date against the English: Hipparchus, Ptolemy, the Greek phrase 'ἐν πλάτει', Al-Battani/Albategnius, Rudolf Coraduz (full name is in fact given by Dreyer here, so the translation's 'Rudolf' is not a fabrication), Dresden/Meissen, Wittenberg, Batavia/Holland, and the closing double-dated '17 Aprilis Anni 99, st. vet.' All correct; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 2do. Afskrift", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter. Afskrift", "C København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980823001", "next": "brahe-t_16000310001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000310001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Scaliger for two books sent as gifts and for excerpting, in his own hand, the key passages from Hipparchus and Ptolemy on the fixed stars.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990417001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990501001", "d": "1599-05-01", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jaques Bongars", "ck": "Jaques Bongars", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Frankfurt am Main", "t": "Jacques Bongars → Tycho Brahe — Frankfurt am Main, 1 May 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Bern, Universitätsbibliothek, B 149 n. 407</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IACOBUS BONGARSIUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>NOBILISSIME ET AMPLISSIME DOMINE.</p><p>TRES iam septimanae abierunt, ex quo redditae mihi sunt illae, quas ad me dedisti IX Martij. Sed neque eas accepi, quas memoras datas sub finem superioris anni, neque FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM vidi, quem a te, tanto viro, commendatum omnibus fuisse officijs proferebas. Non gravaberis itaque repetere et quae illis litteris erant explicata, et quae huius fidei commiseras. Me certe, magno tuo merito, habebis ad omnia, quae quidem sunt meae potestatis, semper paratissimum. Nec addo plura; sed mandata tua exspecto cupide. Tuas vero tute poteris committere bibliopolae, qui CLAUDIJ MARINI WECHELIANI officinae praeest Pragae.</p><p>Francofurti 1. Maij 1599.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Jacques Bongars to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most noble and most ample Master.</p><p>Three weeks have now passed since those letters were delivered to me which you sent me on 9 March. But I have neither received those which you mention as sent toward the end of last year, nor have I seen Frans Tengnagel, whom you declared to have been commended — by you, so great a man — with every good office [i.e. recommended to receive every courtesy]. You will not, therefore, find it burdensome to repeat both what was set out in those letters, and what you had entrusted to his good faith. Me certainly, by your great merit, you shall always have most ready for everything that is indeed within my power. Nor do I add more; but I eagerly await your instructions. Your own [letters] you can safely entrust to the bookseller who is in charge, at Prague, of the office of Claude de Marne the Wechelian.</p><p>Frankfurt, 1 May 1599.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Jacques Bongars</b> (Jacobus Bongarsius, 1554–1612), French humanist, philologist, and diplomat in Imperial service; a learned correspondent and book-conduit for Tycho. The letter survives in his papers at Bern (the Bongars collection, source of the <i>Codices Bongarsiani</i>).</li><li><b><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</b></i> = <b>Frans Gansneb genaamd Tengnagel van de Camp</b> (c. 1576–1622), Tycho's noble assistant, later (1604) his son-in-law; here being recommended to Bongars but not yet arrived.</li><li><b><i>Claudij Marini Wecheliani officina</b></i> = the printing-and-bookselling house of <b>Claude de Marne</b> (with Jean Aubry, the heirs of <b>Andreas Wechel</b>) — the great Frankfurt scholarly firm; Bongars names its agent <b>at Prague</b> as the safe relay for letters to him.</li><li><b>Near-duplicate.</b> This letter recurs almost verbatim a year later as <b>brahe-t_16000501001</b> (Frankfurt, 1 May 1600, to Tycho at Benátky) — evidently Bongars re-sent the same unanswered text; the two share the <i>Tengnagel</i> and <i>Wechel-agent</i> matter with only minor verbal variants (<i>sunt</i>/<i>erunt</i>, <i>proferebas</i>/<i>prosecutus</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <b>Frankfurt am Main</b>, a predominantly Protestant Imperial city, kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar until 1700; the date is taken as O.S. (the dataset gives no <i>n.s.</i> tag). <i>Flagged</i> as a mild assumption, since the writer is a Catholic-service Frenchman; nothing in the letter turns on the style.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the persons (Bongars, Tengnagel, the Wechel/Marne firm) are identified with confidence; grade [B] for the unresolved calendar style of Frankfurt (taken as O.S. on the city's Protestant usage) rather than any textual doubt.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — only residue is the harmless Frankfurt O.S. calendar assumption; persons firmly identified, near-duplicate corroborates.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.153 page image in full. Collated the three-weeks/9-March-letter clause, the Tengnagel-commendation sentence (correctly distinguished here as 'you declared/proferebas', a different construction from the near-duplicate 1 May 1600 Bongars letter's 'I would have pursued/fuissem prosecutus'), the 'repeat what was explained' request, the Claude de Marne/Wechelian bookseller instruction, and the Frankfurt/1 May 1599 date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bern, Universitätsbibliothek, B 149 n. 407."], "th": {"label": "Jaques Bongars", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000309001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000309001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports non-receipt of earlier letters and no contact with Tengnagel, asks Tycho to repeat what was entrusted, and directs future correspondence via a Prague bookseller.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990501001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990507001", "d": "1599-05-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Wittenberg, 7 May 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>VIRTUTE, ERUDITIONE ET GENERE VIRO INPRIMIS CLARO, DOMINO OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO A ROSENHOLMIO, AMICO INTEGERRIMO, CONSANGVINEO FIDELISSIMO, AFFINI AMANTISSIMO, AD PROPRIAS MANUS.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>QUAS circa aequinoctium vernum huius anni ad me e Rosenholmiano tuo dedisti literas, OLIGERE desideratissime, ante paucos dies hîc accepi. Excusas ab initio tarditatem in rescribendo, et quod meae praepostere ac sero ad te perlatae sint; alterum hoc ferendum est ob viarum intercapedinem et Tabellariorum incuriam, prius amicitia nostra facile in meliorem partem interpretatur. Percipio ex hisce, quod descriptum verborum Domini CORADUCIJ, Procancellarij Imperialis, quibus me, cum Dresdae essem, de clementissima Caesaris voluntate certiorem reddidit, legeris, idque tibi placuisse. Scripsit vero idem CORADUCIUS circa finem Ianuarij huius anni alteras ad me, quibus ex Clementissimo ipsius Caesareae Maiestatis mandato me Pragam benigne evocat, quarum exemplar misi ad D. D. VITUM WINSHEMIUM, communem nostrum amicum, oblata nimirum occasione per Tabellarium Hamburgum subito euntem, quo is illud ad te dirigeret, quod et se, nacta confestim oportunitate, fecisse; paulo post mihi rescripsit, ut te illud nunc accepisse existimem, vel saltem dum in Scania esses, simile extractum apud sororem SOPHIAM vidisse, ut ob id reiteratione non sit opus. Coactus autem sum iter hoc Pragense diutius procrastinare, quam vellem, partim ob itinerum impedimenta, per aquarum illuviem post nivem subito resolutam praeter solitum incidentia, partim quod Libri Caesari destinati tam cito ac cuperem plane absolvi nequierint, superveniente etiam Filiae natu maioris gravi invalitudine, alijsque nonnullis obstaculis concurrentibus, quae volens praetereo. Fuit interim Pragae D. D. THOMAS FRANTZIUS, WINSHEMIJ Gener, quem nosti; is huc reversus, multa de Clementissima Caesareae Maiestatis erga me voluntate, et quam benigne quamque honorifico loco me habere constituerit, prout a Domino Procancellatio CORADUCIO sibi, ut ea mihi indicaret, iniunctum aiebat, retulit: quae tamen hîc sigillatim attingere nolo, verum ubi ipsemet illic praesens expertus fuero, quo in statu res meae futurae sint, te plenius de singulis edocebo. Profectus est dictus FRANTZIUS ante paucas hebdomadas hinc Hamburgum, ut inde ad comites in Orientali Frisia, quorum causas in aula Caesaris agit, se conferret, cumque ante abitum eius semel atque iterum familiarius invicem conversaremur, tuique subinde honorifica facta esset mentio, aiebat se te quoque invisurum, prout ab illo petieras, si modo res eius id ullatenus tolerarent. Ego vero, ut id faceret, ipsum adhortabar. Quod si praestiterit, plura ab illo percipies, quam calamus nunc sustinet.</p><p>Caeterum quae de Insulula ista, quae tanti mihi constitit, scribis et mones, tanquam ex candido et mihi bene cupienti animo profecta, candide et sincere accipio. Argumenta, quae adducis, satis probabilia sunt, et licet apud me irrefragabilem Geometricamque demonstrandi vim non mereantur, siquidem tam haec quam plura eiusmodi replicare et validiora ijsdem opponere non sit difficile, tamen tuis et aliorum hac in parte consilijs non prorsus resistendum censeo; aliquantulum saltem temporis ad deliberandum, quid hac in re faciendum vel omittendum sit, mihi concedi cupio, ne nimis praecipitanter quid agat. Neque enim certi quid mecum statuere potero, antequam Pragae Caesaris Clementissimam de meis rebus voluntatem omnimode perspectam habuero; tunc demum satis erit temporis de his atque alijs quidpiam concludere, nec sane consultum videtur prius sponte Insulam restituere: imo si retinerem, donec illi, prout in caeteris (quae plus emolumenti attulerunt) factum est, quocunque tandem praetextu mihi eam adimant, forte plus commodi quam incommodi hinc sum habiturus. Quid si aliquando mihi obijcerent, quod non fuissent eam recepturi, nisi sponte restituissem, volenti nullam fieri iniuriam, et pleraque alia, quibus lubens supersedeo? Minas certe eorum, quantaecunque sint, non reformido, fretus causae bonitate; viderint ipsi; spero quod Deus me aliquando a tantis iniurijs vindicabit, cuius praesidio dum nitor, fortuna omnis superanda ferendo est. Plura et particulariora nunc nec vacat nec lubet. Accingo me, quantum possum, ad iter Pragense, relicturus Dresdae Gynaeceum, donec in Bohemiam excurrero. Tu vero, mi exoptatissime OLIGERE, unâ cum Coniuge tua et Nepte mea, resalutata a me peramanter tam matre tua quam illa, felicissime vale. Salutem ex te dixi Viris hîc Eruditis, quorum familiari notitia prius usus es, quod et antea feci, saepe enim tuam memoriam iucunde recolimus, illi vero te vicissim resalutant, et optima quaeque tibi et tuis precantur.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Dabantur Wittebergae die 7. Maij Anni 1599.<br>Tuus absens ut praesens<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 155–156; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Libros, quos expetis, per hunc Tabellarium mittere nec commodum nec consultum visum est; habebis eos alias; vel da operam, ut exemplaria Regi oblata, quae ille sine dubio respuebat, a RAMELIO accipias, qua de re ad sororem SOPHIAM antea perscripsi. Nunc saltem adiungo quaedam Carmina Poëtarum duorum, quos nosti, et quorum venam probare solitus es.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man especially renowned in virtue, erudition, and birth, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, [my] most upright friend, most faithful kinsman, most loving relative — to his own hands.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>The letter you sent me about the vernal equinox of this year, from your Rosenholm, most longed-for Holger, I received here a few days ago. You excuse, at the outset, your tardiness in replying, and that mine reached you out of order and late; the latter must be borne, for the distance of the roads and the carelessness of couriers; the former our friendship easily interprets in the better part. I perceive from this that you have read the <b>copy of the words of Lord Coraduz, the Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b>, by which he informed me, when I was at Dresden, of the Emperor's most clement will — and that it pleased you. But the same Coraduz wrote me another [letter], about the end of January this year, by which, <b>by the Imperial Majesty's most clement mandate, he kindly summons me to Prague</b>; a copy of which I sent to Lord Doctor <b>Vitus Winshemius</b>, our common friend (an occasion offered by a courier suddenly going to Hamburg), that he might direct it to you — which he wrote back, a little after, that he had done at the first opportunity; so that I think you have now received it, or at least, while you were in <b>Scania</b>, saw a similar extract with [my] sister <b>Sophie</b> — so there is no need to repeat it. But I was compelled to put off this Prague journey longer than I would: partly for the <b>hindrances of the roads</b> (beyond the usual, from the flood of waters after the snow suddenly thawed); partly because the <b>Books destined for the Emperor</b> could not be quite finished as soon as I wished; my <b>elder Daughter's grave illness</b> also supervening, and some other obstacles concurring, which I willingly pass over. Meanwhile Lord Doctor <b>Thomas Frantzius, Winshemius's son-in-law, whom you know</b>, was at Prague; returned hither, he reported much of the Imperial Majesty's most clement will toward me, and how kindly and in how honourable a place he has resolved to hold me — as, he said, had been enjoined on him by Vice-Chancellor Coraduz, that he should tell me — which, however, I will not touch on here in detail; but when I myself, present there, have learned in what state my affairs will be, I will more fully inform you of the particulars. The said Frantzius set out a few weeks ago hence to Hamburg, to go thence to the <b>Counts in East Frisia, whose causes he agitates at the Emperor's court</b>; and since, before his departure, we conversed familiarly together once and again, and honourable mention was made of you, he said he would visit you too, as you had asked of him, if his affairs would in any way allow it — and I exhorted him to do it. If he does, you will learn more from him than the pen now sustains.</p><p>For the rest: what you write and advise about <b>that little island, which cost me so much</b>, I accept candidly and sincerely, as proceeding from a candid mind that wishes me well. The arguments you adduce are probable enough; and although with me they do not merit an irrefragable, geometric force of proof — since both these and more of the kind it is not hard to rejoin, and to oppose stronger ones — yet I think your counsels and others' in this part are not entirely to be resisted: <b>I desire that at least a little time be granted me to deliberate what should be done or omitted in this matter, lest one act too precipitately. For I cannot determine anything certain before, at Prague, I have thoroughly perceived the Emperor's most clement will about my affairs;</b> then at last there will be time enough to conclude something about these and other things — <b>nor does it seem advisable to surrender the Island voluntarily before [that]</b>. Nay, if I should keep it until they take it from me by whatever pretext at last (as was done with the other [holdings], which brought more profit), <b>perhaps I shall have more advantage than disadvantage from it</b>. What if they should sometime object that they would not have taken it back, had I not surrendered it of my own will — that \"no injury is done to one willing\" — and many other [things], which I gladly forbear? Their threats certainly, however great, I do not dread, relying on the goodness of my cause; let them see to it. I hope God will one day vindicate me from such great injuries; relying on whose protection, <b>\"every fortune must be overcome by enduring.\"</b> More and more particular things — now there is neither leisure nor inclination. I gird myself, as much as I can, for the Prague journey, about to leave the <b>womenfolk at Dresden</b> until I have run over into Bohemia. But do you, my most longed-for Holger — together with your wife and my niece — fare most happily; both your mother and she being most lovingly greeted by me. I have given a greeting from you to the learned men here, with whose familiar acquaintance you formerly enjoyed (as I did before), for we often pleasantly recall your memory; and they in turn greet you back, and pray every best thing for you and yours.</p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given at Wittenberg, the 7th day of May, the year 1599.<br>Yours, absent as present,<br>Tycho Brahe, with my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> To send the books you ask for by this courier has seemed neither convenient nor advisable; you shall have them another time; or take pains that you receive, from Ramel, the copies offered to the King — which he without doubt spurned — about which matter I wrote before to [my] sister Sophie. For now I at least add certain Poems of two Poets, whom you know, and whose vein you are wont to approve.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> of Rosenholm, Tycho's <i>consanguineus et affinis</i>. Written from the <b>Wittenberg winter/spring of 1599</b>, as Tycho prepared for Bohemia. The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.</li><li><b>The imperial summons (chain of evidence).</b> Tycho recounts how <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor) had informed him of the Emperor's will at <b>Dresden</b>, then (about end of January 1599) sent a second letter <b>summoning him to Prague</b> — a copy of which Tycho forwarded via <b>Winshemius</b> to Holger (and which Holger's wife/Tycho's sister Sophie also had in extract). The <b>Prague journey was delayed</b> by thaw-flooded roads, the unfinished <b>books for the Emperor</b>, and his <b>elder daughter's grave illness</b> (Magdalene; cf. brahe-t_15990925001).</li><li><b>Thomas Franz — the jurist/East-Frisia agent (key for the \"Doctor Franz\" identity).</b> <i>D. D. <b>Thomas Frantzius</b>, Winshemii Gener</i> = <b>Thomas Franz, Winshemius's son-in-law</b>, a jurist who <b>\"agitates the causes of the Counts in East Frisia at the Emperor's court\"</b> — i.e. the <b>\"Doctor Franz, the jurist, now Chancellor of the Count of Emden\"</b> of brahe-t_16000918001 and the <i>Frantzius Iurisconsultus</i> courier of brahe-t_16001008001. He had relayed Coraduz's favourable account of Tycho's prospects. <b>His first name is Thomas</b> — so he is <b>not</b> the <i>Wolfgang</i> Franz addressed in brahe-t_16000327002 (a distinct person; see the corrected note there).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's reply on the surrender of Hven (the central matter; cross-ref to brahe-t_15990307001).</b> Holger had <b>advised Tycho to surrender Hven voluntarily</b> (his counsel survives as brahe-t_15990307001, the bracketed/enciphered letter). Tycho here <b>answers that advice</b>: he takes it \"candidly,\" grants the arguments \"probable\" (though not of \"geometric\" force, and answerable), and will <b>not resist the counsel outright</b> — but <b>insists on time, and will not surrender \"that little island, which cost me so much,\" voluntarily before he has secured the Emperor's will at Prague.</b> Indeed, he reasons it <b>better to let them take Hven \"by whatever pretext\"</b> (as with his other Danish holdings) than to yield it freely — lest they later say \"no injury is done to one willing.\" He <b>fears no threats</b>, \"relying on the goodness of my cause,\" closing with the Virgilian tag <b><i>\"fortuna omnis superanda ferendo est\"</b></i> (\"every fortune must be overcome by enduring,\" <i>Aeneid</i> V.710). A first-rate document of Tycho's stance on the Hven question.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz (and his wife <b>Sophie Brahe</b>, \"my niece,\" and his mother); <b>Coraduz</b>; <b>Vitus Winshemius</b>; <b>Thomas Franz</b> (the jurist/East-Frisia agent); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Tycho's sister); Tycho's <b>elder daughter</b> (Magdalene); the Counts of <b>East Frisia</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Classical tag (kept/identified).</b> <i>fortuna omnis superanda ferendo est</i> — <b>Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> V.710</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Wittebergae die 7. Maij Anni 1599</i>; Wittenberg/Saxony kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (the books withheld via the courier; Ramel; the appended poems), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.155–156 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the imperial-summons chain via Coraduz and Winshemius; the delayed Prague journey, the books for the Emperor, the daughter's illness; <b>Thomas Franz</b> as the jurist/East-Frisia agent who relayed Coraduz's account — fixing the \"Doctor Franz\" identity; and above all <b>Tycho's reply to Holger's advice on Hven</b> — time to deliberate, no voluntary surrender before securing the imperial position, preferring to let it be taken \"by pretext,\" fearless of threats) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated, with the Virgil tag identified. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer slice_pdf visually in full (TBOO VIII pp.154-156, matched by the salutation 'VIRTUTE, ERUDITIONE ET GENERE VIRO INPRIMIS CLARO...AD PROPRIAS MANUS'; p.153 is the tail of an unrelated Tycho-to-Scaliger letter of 17 Apr 1599 and was correctly excluded from the letter proper). Collated the full Latin word-for-word against the page images: the Coraduz/Imperial Vice-Chancellor correspondence, the end-of-January summons to Prague, the Vitus Winshemius relay via Hamburg, the sister Sophie/Scania reference, the delayed Prague journey (flood after the snow, the unfinished Books for the Emperor, the elder daughter's grave illness), Thomas Frantzius (Winshemius's son-in-law) and the East Frisia counts, the 'Insulula' (Hven) passage on surrendering the island, the Virgilian tag 'fortuna omnis superanda ferendo est', the Dresden Gynaeceum, and the closing 'Dabantur Wittebergae die 7. Maij Anni 1599.' The postscript (Libros, quos expetis... RAMELIO... sororem SOPHIAM... Carmina Poëtarum duorum) is printed by Dreyer as a continuous part of the same letter on pp.155-156, exactly as the source_text's editorial note describes it, and the English renders it faithfully. Every proper name, number, and date matches; no fabrications, omissions, mistranslations, hedge losses, or text errors found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 20, "prev": "brahe-t_15990307001", "next": "brahe-t_15990909001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Rosenkrantz's letter on the vernal equinox and relays Vice-Chancellor Coraduz’s messages conveying the Emperor’s favour and the summons to Prague.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990507001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990723001", "d": "1599-07-23", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 23 July 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Monac. Bibl. Univ. 693 fol. (Kladde / draft); B = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek (afskrift in a copy of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i>, 1668)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO A KNUDSTRUP, MATHEMATICORUM OMNIUM FACILE PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO SINGULARI OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.</p><p>CUM hac ipsa hora, Vir nobilissime et Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, ex urbe Imperatoris literas acceperim, quibus certior factus sum te eo venisse a Caesarea Maiestate vocatum, et plane confidam Maiestatem suam pro singulari favore, quo Matheseos artem prosequitur, ut inde aliorsum te conferas, aegre laturam et vix atque adeo ne vix quidem esse passuram, omnino decrevi his literis de adventu isto tuo tibi et mihi gratulari. Quod si aliqua in re studia tua pro exilibus viribus meis iuvare possem, haberes me paratissimum. Mallem equidem hoc saltem primo alloquio, animi mei propensam in te voluntatem aliqua ratione potius declarare, quam quidquam a te petere, sed cum inpraesentiarum nihil istiusmodi occurrat, hoc tibi significandum duxi. Ex traditione antiquissimi Authoris Poëtae Romani, qui eo ipso tempore vixit et floruit, accepi anno ante CHRISTUM quarto inchoante, summi miraculi instar habitum fuisse passim ab omnibus, quod stella Veneris seu Lucifer, cum pro ratione motus sui, ipsissimo illo tempore, ante Solis ortum lucere debuisset, nuspiam in coelo visa fuerit, atque non apparente Lucifero dies illuxerit. Rationem esse ego conijcio, quod iam dicta Veneris stella tum temporis a stella Mercurij Eclipsin fuerit passa, nec enim video, quanam alia de caussa haec res prodigij loco posteritati tradita fuisset, Romae praesertim, ubi tum temporis quales quales saltem astronomi non defuerunt. Adhibui itaque calculum Prutenicum, et reperi, quod currente anno ante CHRISTUM quarto, die septima Ianuarij circa Solis ortum stella Mercurij subierit stellam Veneris, sed ex eodem calculo reperi quoque simul unâ, quod eadem Mercurij stella 2° 44' latitudine tum Borealior Venere fuerit. Quare despondissem animum nisi ex Epistolis tuis Astronomicis, et quae de Cometis scripsisti, animadvertissem, te latitudines praesertim ♀ et ☿ longe aliter observasse, quam calculus exhibeat. Rem itaque hanc omnino ad te deferendam esse duxi, cuius de ea iudicium audire percuperem, ut quod merito cuiusvis alterius sit praeferendum. Ad hanc meam petitionem accedit etiam alia. Soleo identidem Eclipses luminarium, quae ante CHRISTUM retro vel circa tempora CHRISTI, iuxta traditiones Authorum, contigerunt, ex tabulis Prutenicis supputare, et ea quoque, quae PTOLEMAeUS in magna Syntaxi hac de re tradit, conferre (quae quidem utplurimum calculo Prutenico consentire videntur). Attamen calculus iste non videtur satis exacte Eclipses exhibere. Intellexi quidem ex praefatis Epistolis tuis Astronomicis te ex nova restauratione cursus ☉ et ☾ Eclipses luminarium huius nostri seculi, longe aliter et exactius deprehendere, quam ullae tabulae hactenus exhibuerint. Utrum vero accuratissimae istae tuae observationes recentes eo rem deducant, ut etiam tempus et quantitatem illarum Eclipsium, quae circa tempora CHRISTI et ante illam aetatem retro contigerunt, exactius definire queas, id inquam, spero quidem, nescio tamen et non sine ratione addubitare mihi videor, cum veteres utique observationes desideres. Ideoque etiam atque etiam te rogo, ut hac quoque in re iudicium et prudentiam tuam mihi impertiri non dedigneris; summe enim desiderarem defectus luminarium vetustissimos quosque calculo (quoad eius fieri potest) praecisius assequi, et quanam ratione id fieri queat, rescire. Quodsi ullo genere officiorum studiorumque meorum hoc beneficium tuum in me collatum exaequare potero, semper ero promptissimus.</p><p>Vale feliciter, et has literas meas boni consule.<br>Datae Monachij 23. Iulij Anno 1599.<br>Dominationis Tuae Nobilissimae studiosissimus<br>IOAN. GEORGIUS HERUART ab Hohenburg,<br>Serenissimo utriusque Bavariae Duci a consilijs intimis, Praeses Schuabae et Statuum provincialium Cancellarius, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, easily the Prince of all Mathematicians, his Lord and friend to be cultivated with singular respect.</b></p><p>Since at this very hour, most noble Man and easily Prince of all Mathematicians, I have received a letter from the Emperor's city, by which I am informed that you have come thither, summoned by the Imperial Majesty — and I fully trust that His Majesty, for the singular favour with which he pursues the art of Mathematics, will be loath that you betake yourself elsewhere thence, and will scarcely (nay, not even scarcely) suffer it — I have altogether resolved by this letter to congratulate you and myself on that coming of yours. And if in any matter I could aid your studies, according to my slender powers, you would have me most ready. I should indeed prefer, in this first address at least, to declare in some way my mind's inclined goodwill toward you, rather than to ask anything of you; but since at present nothing of that kind occurs [to me], I thought this should be signified to you. <b>From the tradition of a most ancient Author, a Roman Poet who lived and flourished at that very time, I have learned that, at the beginning of the fourth year before Christ, it was held everywhere by all as a supreme miracle that the star of Venus, or Lucifer [the Morning Star] — when, by the reckoning of its motion, it ought at that very time to have shone before the Sun's rising — was nowhere seen in the sky, and, Lucifer not appearing, the day dawned.</b> I conjecture the reason to be that the said star of Venus at that time <b>suffered an Eclipse by the star of Mercury</b> — for I see no other cause why this thing would have been handed down to posterity in place of a prodigy, especially at Rome, where at that time astronomers of some sort at least were not lacking. I therefore applied the <b>Prutenic calculation</b>, and found that, in the course of the fourth year before Christ, on the <b>7th of January about sunrise, Mercury passed beneath Venus</b> — but from the same calculation I also found, at the same time, that Mercury was then more northern than Venus by <b>2° 44′ of latitude</b>. Wherefore I should have despaired, had I not noticed, from your <b>Astronomical Letters</b> and what you have written about the <b>Comets</b>, that you have observed the latitudes especially of <b>Venus and Mercury</b> far otherwise than the calculation exhibits. I have therefore thought this matter should by all means be referred to you, whose judgement about it I greatly desire to hear, as one to be deservedly preferred to anyone else's.</p><p>To this request of mine another is also added. I am wont, again and again, to compute from the <b>Prutenic tables</b> the eclipses of the luminaries that occurred before Christ, back [in time], or around the times of Christ, according to the traditions of Authors; and to compare also what <b>Ptolemy</b> hands down on this matter in the <b>Great Syntaxis</b> [Almagest] (which indeed for the most part seem to agree with the Prutenic calculation). Yet that calculation does not seem to exhibit the eclipses exactly enough. I have understood from your aforesaid Astronomical Letters that you, from the <b>new restoration of the course of the Sun and Moon</b>, detect the eclipses of the luminaries of this our age far otherwise and more exactly than any tables have hitherto exhibited. But whether those most accurate recent observations of yours bring the matter to this — that you can also more exactly define the <b>time and magnitude of those eclipses which occurred around the times of Christ, and before that age back [in time]</b> — that, I say, I indeed hope, yet I know not, and not without reason I seem to myself to doubt, since you must in any case lack ancient observations. And therefore I ask you again and again that in this matter too you would not disdain to impart to me your judgement and prudence; for I should most greatly desire to attain more precisely by calculation each of the most ancient eclipses of the luminaries (so far as it can be done), and to learn by what method this can be done. And if by any kind of services and studies of mine I shall be able to match this benefit conferred by you upon me, I shall always be most ready.</p><p>Fare happily, and take this letter of mine in good part.<br>Given at Munich, 23 July, the year 1599.<br>Most devoted to Your Most Noble Lordship,<br>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg,<br>privy councillor to the Most Serene Duke of both Bavarias, President at [Burghausen in] Swabia and Chancellor of the provincial Estates, with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender — and the opening of the Tycho–Herwart correspondence.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), the Bavarian chancellor and polymath (his titles given in the subscription: <b>privy councillor to the Duke of Bavaria</b>, <i>Präsident</i> at <b>Burghausen</b> (<i>Schwaben</i>), and <i>Kanzler</i> of the provincial Estates). This <b>23 July 1599</b> letter is the <b>first of the long Tycho–Herwart correspondence</b> on <b>ancient eclipses and chronology</b> (the other Herwart letters in the corpus are all 1600: brahe-t_16000403001, brahe-t_16000504003, etc.). Survives in two witnesses — Herwart's own Munich <b>draft (Kladde)</b> and a Copenhagen copy in <b>Resen's <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</b></i> (1668).<br>- <b>Significance — the two requests (chronology &amp; ancient eclipses; protocol §5.1).</b><br>  1. <b>The \"invisible Morning Star\" of 4 BC.</b> Herwart reports, from \"a most ancient Roman Poet who lived at that very time,\" that <b>at the start of the fourth year before Christ</b> the <b>Morning Star (Venus/Lucifer)</b>, which by computation should have shone before sunrise, <b>was nowhere seen</b> — held as \"a supreme miracle.\" His hypothesis: <b>Venus was occulted (\"eclipsed\") by Mercury.</b> By the <b>Prutenic tables</b> he finds Mercury \"passed beneath Venus\" on <b>7 January (4 BC) at sunrise</b>, but with Mercury <b>2° 44′ more northern in latitude</b> — so no true occultation by the tables; he appeals to Tycho's <i>Astronomical Letters</i> and cometary work, which show <b>Venus/Mercury latitudes</b> differing \"far otherwise than the calculation,\" and asks Tycho's verdict. (The Roman poet is unnamed; left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule.)<br>  2. <b>Dating ancient eclipses.</b> Herwart routinely computes from the <b>Prutenic tables</b> the eclipses \"around the times of Christ, and before,\" comparing <b>Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</b></i> — but finds the calculation imprecise. He asks whether Tycho's <b>new restoration of the Sun's and Moon's courses</b> can fix the <b>time and magnitude of the most ancient eclipses</b> more exactly — doubting it, since Tycho \"must lack ancient observations,\" but eager for his method. This is the chronological-astronomy programme (dating sacred and classical history by eclipse) that drives the whole Herwart exchange.<br>- <b>Technical terms (kept; §5.1).</b> <i>calculus Prutenicus</i> → the <b>Prutenic Tables</b>; <i>magna Syntaxis</i> → the <b>Almagest</b> (<i>Great Syntaxis</i>); <i>Lucifer</i> → the <b>Morning Star</b> (Venus); <i>defectus / Eclipsis luminarium</i> → <b>eclipse of the luminaries</b>; ♀ = <b>Venus</b>, ☿ = <b>Mercury</b>, ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b> (glyphs kept in source).<br>- <b>Editorial sigla.</b> The transcription carries scattered superscript <b>\"A\"</b> marks — the <b>edition's witness-sigla</b> flagging readings of witness <b>A</b> (the Munich Kladde) against <b>B</b> (the Resen copy); they are apparatus markers, not text, and are disregarded in the translation (and noted here).<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg (sender); <b>Rudolf II</b> (the Emperor); <b>Ptolemy</b>; the unnamed ancient Roman poet; the Duke of Bavaria (in the titulature).<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Monachij 23. Iulij Anno 1599</i>; Catholic Bavaria kept the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> calendar; marked [N.S.]. (The 4 BC date is Herwart's chronological reckoning, reproduced as he gives it.)<br>- Correction (2026-07-12). Latin signature corrected 'HERWAUART' to 'HERUART' (Dreyer VIII p.158); English unaffected.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — polished humanist Latin, read securely; the substance (the congratulation on Tycho's imperial call; the 4 BC Venus-occulted-by-Mercury problem with its Prutenic computation and the 2° 44′ latitude objection, appealing to Tycho's observed Venus/Mercury latitudes; and the request to date the ancient eclipses by Tycho's restored solar/lunar theory) is firmly construed on the §5.1 terms, and the witness-sigla are handled. No textual crux; the ancient poet is left unnamed per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.156-159 (all 4 pages read as page images). The letter occupies pp.157-158; p.156 carries the tail of Ferdinand de' Medici's letter to Tycho and p.159 carries the start of Tycho's letter to Christian Longomontanus, both correctly excluded. Full Latin text collated: the Venus/Mercury occultation anecdote from the ancient Roman poet, the Prutenic-tables calculation (7 January, 2 degrees 44' latitude), the Ptolemy/Almagest reference, and the closing (Munich, 23 July 1599) all match Dreyer. One TEXT_ERROR found: the signature name is corrupted in source_text ('Herwauart' for 'Heruart'); translation is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text's signature spelling 'HERWAUART' is a corrupted/glued form of Dreyer's 'HERUART'. The translation independently uses the standard historical spelling 'Herwart', so the corruption is confined to source_text and does not propagate into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>IOAN. GEORGIUS HERUART ab Hohenburg</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.158), which prints the signature 'IOAN. GEORGIUS HERUART ab Hohenburg'. The source_text's 'HERWAUART' is a glued/corrupted form; corrected.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Univ. Bibl., cod. Monacensi Bibl. Univ. 693 fol.. Kladde", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_15990831001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990831001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Congratulates Tycho on his imperial summons to Prague, expresses confidence the Emperor will retain him there, and offers assistance with Tycho's studies.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990723001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990820001", "d": "1599-08-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Brandýs nad Labem", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Brandýs [nad Labem], 20 August 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: dataset cites<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>; = Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift — copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>INVENI peculiari Dei providentia et dono Caesarem hunc RUDOLPHUM Secundum mei et eorum, quae tracto, studiorum clementi favore amantissimum, qui satis liberaliter mihi et meis hanc ob rem prospicere non est tergiversatus, veluti alias, Deo volente, ipsius Caesareae Maiestatis erga me benignissimam voluntatem, ubi adfueris, intelliges. Introductus quidem sum, statim ac Pragam veneram, etiam antequam audientiam apud Caesaream Maiestatem habueram, in aedes Curtianas splendide et magnifice exstructas. Verum cum praeviderem, me isthic in urbe, ob aulam tam amplam et hominum omnis generis maximam copiam, otio philosophico vix perfrui posse, exoptavi potius extra illam seorsim habitare. Quo intellecto ipsius Caesarea Maiestas postmodum tres eximias arces, unius vel etiam dimidij diei itinere Praga distantes, per quendam nobilem virum, mihi ut eo deduceret adiunctum, monstrari curavit, ut ex ijs eam, quae meae intentioni commodior videretur, eligerem. Quarum una est Brandisium (ubi haec scribo) verum quia caeteris nonnihil declivior, et propriae Caesaris delectationi, praesertim ubi venationibus indulget, destinata, hîc habitare non visum opportunum. Reliquae duae appellantur Lyssa et Benachia, ambae etiam splendidissimae et iucundissimae arces. Inter quas Benachiam (quae Venetiae Bohemorum ob amoenitatem interpretatur) praetuli, quo unâ cum omnibus meis hodie vel cras, quod Deus fortunatum velit, introducturus est hic Capitaneus Brandeisensis, ex Caesaris mandato ea de causa heri Pragam ad me evocatus. Est locus editus, ut undique libere pateat Horizon, Aedificia splendida et commoda; prope est parva civitas ad fluviolum Eifer in Albim excurrentem sita, ipsique Arci quasi contigua. Distat Praga 5 miliaribus, via existente plana, ut iter saltem sit 6 horarum. Hîc favente divina clementia, cum meis habitare constitui, donec de feudo, quod Caesarea Maiestas mihi pro me et mea familia posterisque clementissime addixit, alicubi per occasionem prospectum fuerit. Interim domum etiam opportunam Pragae ipsius Caesarea Maiestas mihi clementer concedere vult, quam, quoties eo excurrero, cum meis inhabitem, ut commodius degere et propria quadra vivere possimus, etc.</p><p>Datae festinanter in transcursu Brandisio, dum Benachiam petimus, die 20. Aug. stylo novo An. 1599.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Christian Longomontanus.</b></p><p>I have found, by God's special providence and gift, this Emperor Rudolf the Second most loving toward me and toward the studies I pursue, with clement favour; who, liberally enough, has not been reluctant to provide for me and mine on this account — as you will otherwise understand, God willing, when you are present, of His Imperial Majesty's most benign will toward me. I was indeed received, as soon as I had come to Prague — even before I had had audience with His Imperial Majesty — into the Curtius house, splendidly and magnificently built. But since I foresaw that there, in the city, on account of so great a court and the very large throng of men of every kind, I could scarcely enjoy philosophical leisure, I chose rather to dwell apart, outside it. This understood, His Imperial Majesty thereupon had three excellent castles — distant from Prague a day's, or even half a day's, journey — shown to me, through a certain nobleman assigned to conduct me thither, so that from them I might choose the one that should seem more convenient to my purpose. Of these, one is Brandeis (where I write this); but because it is somewhat more low-lying than the rest, and set apart for the Emperor's own pleasure — especially when he indulges in hunting — it did not seem opportune to dwell here. The remaining two are called Lysá and Benátky, both likewise most splendid and most delightful castles. Among these I have preferred Benátky (which, for its pleasantness, is rendered \"the Venice of the Bohemians\"), whither, together with all my people, today or tomorrow — may God grant it fortunate — this Captain of Brandeis is to install [me], having been called out to me from Prague yesterday, by the Emperor's command, for this very cause. It is an elevated place, so that the Horizon lies freely open on every side; the buildings splendid and convenient; nearby is a small town set on the little river Iser, which runs out into the Elbe, and as it were contiguous to the castle itself. It is distant from Prague five [German] miles, the road being level, so that the journey is but six hours. Here, by divine clemency's favour, I have resolved to dwell with my people, until provision shall somewhere be made, as occasion allows, concerning the fief which His Imperial Majesty most graciously assigned to me, for myself and my family and posterity. Meanwhile His Imperial Majesty also wishes graciously to grant me a convenient house at Prague itself, which, as often as I run thither, I may inhabit with my people, so that we may live more conveniently and keep our own table, etc.</p><p>Given hastily, in passage from Brandeis, while we make for Benátky, the 20th of August, New Style, in the year 1599.<br>Your Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender, recipient &amp; moment.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> to his chief assistant <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (1562–1647), written <i>in transit</i> from Brandeis to Benátky on <b>20 August 1599</b> — Tycho's own eyewitness account of <b>how he came to settle at Benátky nad Jizerou</b>, the establishment where Kepler would join him months later. Longomontanus, then briefly returned to Denmark, is being told where to rejoin his master (\"when you are present\").</li><li><b>The narrative.</b> On reaching Prague, Tycho was first lodged in the <b>Curtius house</b> (<i>aedes Curtianae</i> — the Prague palace associated with the late imperial vice-chancellor Jacob Curtius von Senftenau), even before his audience with the Emperor; but, wanting <i>otium philosophicum</i> away from the crowded court, he asked to live outside the city. Rudolf II then had <b>three castles</b> shown him — <b>Brandeis</b> (Brandýs nad Labem, rejected as low-lying and reserved for the Emperor's hunting), <b>Lysá</b> (Lyssa), and <b>Benátky</b> (Benachia) — and Tycho chose Benátky.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Caesar Rudolphus Secundus / Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>, throughout the gracious patron.</li><li><i>Capitaneus Brandeisensis</i> = the <b>Captain (governor) of Brandeis</b>, commanded to install Tycho at Benátky (the same office whose holder's Prague wedding figures in brahe-t_16000515002).</li><li><i>quidam nobilis vir</i> = an unnamed nobleman assigned as Tycho's guide.</li><li><b>Places &amp; topography (valued for the Benátky description).</b> <i>Benachia … Venetiae Bohemorum</i> — Benátky \"the <b>Venice of the Bohemians</b>,\" a folk-etymology of the name (Czech <i>Benátky</i> ≈ \"Venice\"). Tycho prizes its <b>elevation and free horizon</b> (<i>locus editus, ut undique libere pateat Horizon</i>) — the prime requirement for an observatory. <i>fluviolus Eifer</i> = the river <b>Iser / Jizera</b>, \"running out into the <b>Elbe</b> (<i>Albis</i>)\"; the small town adjoins the castle. Distance from Prague: <b>5 German miles ≈ a 6-hour journey</b>.</li><li><b>Terms of the grant.</b> <i>feudum … pro me et mea familia posterisque</i> = a hereditary <b>fief</b> promised by the Emperor for Tycho and his descendants (the long-sought secure endowment, never finally settled); plus a <b>house at Prague</b> for his visits, so that he and his household may \"keep their own table\" (<i>propria quadra vivere</i>).</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The dataset cites this letter to the collection <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>; it is also printed in Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i> (vol. VIII). The surviving witness is an <b>afskrift (copy)</b> in Langebek's Diplomatarium, Copenhagen.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Datae festinanter</i> → \"Given hastily\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"with his own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 20. Aug. stylo novo An. 1599</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b>, Tycho already dating Gregorian in the Emperor's lands. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'Brundisio' corrected to 'Brandisio', and 'militaribus' corrected to 'miliaribus', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 159.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, complete Latin and a securely-identified, historically important account (the choice of Benátky, the Curtius-house lodging, the offered fief). Places and persons are firmly identified; the <i>Venetiae Bohemorum</i> etymology and the Iser/Elbe topography are correct. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the first 4 pages of the 30-page slice (covering TBOO VIII pp.158-161); the target letter (Tycho to Longomontanus, describing the choice among Brandeis/Lysa/Benatky and the move to Benatky) is complete on pp.158-159, citation '[Ex apographo Hauniensi excerpt. Langebek. 179...]'. Collated the full Latin word-for-word against Dreyer. Found two source_text corruptions relative to Dreyer's print (Brundisio/Brandisio; militaribus/miliaribus); the first is a genuine place-name substitution (Brindisi, Italy vs. Brandeis, Bohemia) serious enough to mislead a reader relying on source_text alone, even though the published English translation itself renders both correctly (Brandeis, five miles) and is not at fault. All other names, numbers, and the closing date/place ('die 20. Aug. stylo novo An. 1599') match exactly. Self-graded A; our collation finds source-text corruption warranting C despite a sound translation.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text reads 'Brundisio' (evoking Brundisium/Brindisi in Italy) where Dreyer prints 'Brandisio', matching the place-name 'Brandisium' (Brandeis) used earlier in this same letter; a real, potentially misleading place-name corruption in our source. The English correctly says 'Brandeis' regardless, so the translation itself is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae festinanter in transcursu Brandisio, dum Benachiam petimus</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'militaribus' (an inserted 't', suggesting 'military') where Dreyer has 'miliaribus' (miles) - a corrupted word; the English correctly reads 'five miles' regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>Distat Praga 5 miliaribus, via existente plana</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VIII pages containing this letter (pp.158-159 within the supplied 30-page slice). Both corrections are unambiguous in the page image; the 'miliaribus' spelling was cross-checked against a second, clearer occurrence of the same word later on p.159. Neither correction affects the English translation, which was already faithful to Dreyer's readings. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in the letter text itself.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15990321001", "next": "brahe-t_15990922001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Emperor Rudolf II's generous reception at Prague, his lodging in the Curtius house, and his preference to seek a more secluded residence outside the city.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990820001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990831001", "d": "1599-08-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 31 August 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witnesses: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (copy); B = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek — copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Notes.</b> (1) <b>The letter Herwart answers in brahe-t_15990917001 (17 September 1599)</b> — there cited as \"your letter sent from Benátky on the last day of last month.\" (2) The catalogue <b>label \"Wittenberg\" is a location error</b>: the dateline reads <i>ex arce Caesareae Benachia</i> = <b>Benátky</b> (header per §4.1; flagged). (3) The working dump carried OCR artefacts (stray apparatus-sigla \"A\", run-together words); the source block is <b>normalized</b> to clean readable Latin, faithful to the wording.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOH. G. HERWARTUM AB HOHENBURG.</p><p>S.</p><p>SIC est, Vir Nobilis et magnifice Amice, licet de facie mihi ignote, plurimi tamen ob praeclaras ingenij tui dotes semper faciende, quod a Sacra Caesarea Maiestate Pragam clementissime vocatus ab ipsius Caesarea Maiestate in ipsius clientem clementissime sim acceptus, et liberaliter prout ferunt haec tempora mihi et meis studijs ab ipsius Caesarea Maiestate provisum. Concessus etiam est benignissime habitationi meae locus hic in arce Benachia 5 miliaribus Pragâ distante, ubi commodius et tranquillius quam in urbe, consuetis meis exercitijs Astronomicis et Spagyricis vacare licebit, et opus Astronomicum, quod diu multumque molior, favente Coelestium Opifice in ipsius Caesareae Maiestatis et Imperij honorem (siquidem ob quorundam inscitiam et invidiam in patria mea id fieri nequiverit) absolvere et consecrare, qua etiam de caussa totum meum apparatum Astronomicum et supellectilem omnem, quam Magdeburgi reliqui, huc transferri curo.</p><p>Caeterum ut ad . T. eruditas quaestiones pro virili respondeam, sic habeat. Veneris stellam anno ante CHRISTUM 4 inchoante coelitus non conspectam, magni miraculi loco ducerem, si res ea indubie constaret: sit fides apud Autorem, qui cum Poëta fuerit, Privilegio forsitan Poëtico usus est, cum ne quidem Historici, qui magis veraces haberi volunt, et certe esse debebant, in coelestibus ostentis recte indicandis, experientiae consona semper proferant, adeo ut interdum Chasmata quaedam et ignitas aëris inflammationes, varias formas saepe exhibentes, pro Cometis habeant, quos tamen ipso in Coelo locum obtinere, illicque cursum suum absolvere, e septem, quos intra 22 Annos conspexi, ratis observationibus demonstrative comprobavi. Concedatur vero ♀ris stellam tunc miraculose delituisse: ☿ij tamen interventu id fieri potuisse, ut sic Eclipsin passa sit, verisimile non videtur, neque enim ☿ij stella in coelo tantae apparet magnitudinis, ut ♀ris stellam, quae multo maior conspicitur, abscondere possit. Adde quod ☿ij stella luminosa sit, nec opaco corpore praedita ut ☾, ideoque si quando centraliter ♀ri et visui nostro interponeretur, lumen suum illius lumini potius coniungeret, sicque illud augeret magis quam imminueret. Taceo quod ob motus celeritatem diu sub ♀ris stella ☿ consistere nequeat, nisi in easdem partes aequarentur ambarum motus, quod rarissime evenit. Praeterea cum ipsemet ex calculo Prutenico adinveneris, stellam ☿ij tunc fuisse 2¾ G. ♀re borealiorem, satis partiliter et exacte pro Eclipsatione non iungebatur. Licet enim in latitudine non saltem horum duorum Planetarum, quos inferiores vocant, sed et caeterorum magna diversitas in coelo ab utrisque tabulis deprehendatur, tamen adeo enormiter ☿ij stellam quoad latitudinem exorbitare non animadverti. Observavi quidem ♀rm, cum esset circa medietatem ♓ium iuxta Perigaeum Epicycli (ut communiter loquuntur) latitudinem Boream assequi posse partium 9 Min. 50, tribus gradibus plus quam fert Alphonsinorum calculus, licet is Coperniceum unico circiter excedat, ita ut in 10um G. completum excrescere possit eius ab Ecliptica in Boream digressio; idque bis denotavi, utpote anno 1587 et 1595 interiecto (uti requiritur) octennio, quibus etiam temporibus ♀rem intra triduum et quatriduum tam vespertinam quam eoam meis instrumentis observavi et commutationem illius (uti vocat COPERNICUS) hinc promptius restitui. Sic etiam in ☿io et tribus superioribus Planetis non modicam adinveni in eorum latitudinibus (veluti innui) a coelesti norma exorbitationem, adeo ut ne quidem in tribus illis, ubi simplicior ratio est, limites maximarum latitudinum rite praedefiniti sint a PTOLEMAeO, cuius placitis nimis secure aequievit COPERNICUS, nec nodi intersectionum circuituum Planetarum cum Ecliptica ita se habent, respectu Apogaeorum, uti ex PTOLEMAeO assumpsit idem COPERNICUS. Et si ea quae de his tradidit PTOLEMAeUS, ipsius aevo coelo consona erant, oportebit nodos hosce simulque limites maximae latitudinis longe alium sensibiliter per se habere motum, quam is est, qui ab Apogaeorum mutatione procedit, differentia nunc intercedente multorum graduum. Quin et in ipsa ☾ non minima conspicitur in latitudine varietas atque a traditione antecessorum diversitas. In novilunijs siquidem et plenilunijs latitudinem maximam obtinere potest 4 partium et 58', quae Ptolemaicum limitem intra 2' attingit. At in □tis eandem tertia gradus parte iuxta limitem boream et austrinum adauget, et quod magis mirum est, nodorum insuper motum exhibet inaequalem singulis mensibus hinc inde reciprocantem, idque sensibili intervallo, quod gradum 1 35' attingit. Ex modo dictis itaque (de quibus tamen alias latius et particularius volente Deo agam) satis liquet, quod . T. latitudinis Planetarum calculum e tabulis consuetis erutum, non citra rem suspectum habeat; nihilominus circa ea tempora, quando in ☿ij latitudine e tabulis Prutenicis supputatio est instituta, usque adeo magnum insinuari potuisse errorem vix crediderim, quod PTOLEMAeI temporibus, quibus ille astrorum observationibus invigilavit, decies magis quam nunc sumus propiora fuerint. Et canones Prutenici illo aevo Ptolemaicis quam proxime subscribunt. At si in observatis PTOLEMAeI error aliquis subrepsit, quod non raro factum haud sine causa suspicor, frustra super hoc fundamentum aedificamus; quare circumspecte admodum, nec nisi limitatione competenti adhibita eius placitis utendum.</p><p>De Eclipsibus, quae scribis, ita se habent quod Prutenicae tabulae ijs, quae PTOLEMAeI aetate contigerunt, quam proxime consentiant, nec tamen singulis satis praecise adaptatae sint; constat enim COPERNICUM ad Ptolemaicas Eclipses, quatenus in nonnullis fieri potuit, tanquam Epochas suos numeros applicuisse. In omnibus tamen accuratam amussim invenire non sustinuit, ob aliam quandam inaequalitatis lunaris nec a PTOLEMAeO nec ipso animadversam implicationem. Neque enim tres Eclipses prout illi factitarunt, sufficiunt ad primam inaequalitatem ☾ae enucleandam, ut de altera adhuc intricatiore hîc nihil dicam; sed pluribus in diversis Eccentrici seu maioris Epicycli locis opus est ijsdemque accuratissime denotatis. Ut vero haec aliquatenus in uno saltem exemplo dispicias, mitto deliquij ☾ae in principio huius anni pragmatiam, tam in triangulari demonstratione ex hypothesi nostra, quam numeris correspondentibus ab eximio Mathematico Wittebergensi M. IOSTELIO, dum ibi aliquamdiu ob luem in Bohemia grassantem et alijs de causis subsisterem, me concedente factitatam et typis excusam. Ubi vero meae tabulae, favente divina ope, de motu luminarium (quas caeteris Planetis praemittere animus est) sub auspicijs Romani Imperatoris RUDOLPHI Secundi in lucem prodierint, testabitur experientia, eas non saltem nostro seculo adamussim quadrare, sed et vetustissimis illis Eclipsibus sufficienter congruere, quatenus veterum observata ipsissimis Coeli apparentijs praecise consona fuêre. Et quid quaeso vel in ☾a vel caeteris Planetis ab omni vitio immune praestare poterant, cum stellas fixas minus fideliter correctas adhibuerint, quemadmodum mille affixarum stellarum accuratissima restitutio in ipso minuto adeoque nonnunquam eius semisse a me praeteritis aliquot annis exantlata et nuper Caesareae Maiestati in membrana manuscripta subiectissime exhibita, abunde testari poterit, collatione cum consuetis abacis instituta. Obtuli etiam eidem Caesareae Maiestati in alio libro manuscripto Solis et Lunae restitutionem ad singulos dies duorum proximorum annorum, apposita simul discrepantia utriusque calculi Alphonsini et Copernicei, quae subinde non exigua est. Quin et in tertio quodam eiusdem formae et magnitudinis libro Mechanicam Astronomiae partem Instrumenta mea, quae hactenus elaboravi repraesentantem, eumque typis, postquam Dania excessi, impressum, ipsius Caesaris Maiestati pariter humillime dedicavi. De hoc ultimo misissem nunc tria exemplaria, duo pro illustrissimis Ducibus Bavariae GUILHELMO seniore ac patre et MAXIMILIANO filio regente, tertium pro te ipso, sed nullum iam ad manus habeo quod coloribus rite illuminatum (uti vocant) existit. Verum id Pragae modo fieri procuro, quo peracto, quod modo dixi praestabo: et forte plura etiam mittam, quaecunque tam cito describi curare potero. Interim pauculis verbis indicare non degraveris, an dicti Illustrissimi Principes vestri talibus afficiantur. Valeat T. M. quam felicissime et diutissime, meque literis suis, uti benevole incepit, eruditis saepius invisere, atque amicitiam semel ijs, cum aliter vix liceat, inchoatam ijsdem continuare et conservare pergat; inveniet me ad idem praestandum quam paratissimum, ut nunquam sic se certasse priorem poeniteat, omniaque ei studia mea et quaecunque a me proficisci possunt officia, lubens polliceor atque offero. Caeterum quod non prius rescripserim, varijs occupationibus, quibus Pragae praepediebar, et migrationi in hanc arcem nuperae imputet, et candide excusatum habeat. Volui istam tarditatem in rescribendo ea, quam vides, prolixitate compensare, quae ut accepta sit rogo.</p><p>Iamque iterum et saepius T. M. quam optime valeat.<br>Dabantur ex arce Caesareae Benachia die 31. Augusti Anno 1599.<br>. T. studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOH. G. HERWART VON HOHENBURG.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>So it is, noble and magnificent Friend — though unknown to me by face, yet always to be highly valued for the eminent gifts of your genius — that, <b>clemently called to Prague by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, I have been most clemently received as His client, and liberally (as these times allow) provided for, with my studies, by His Imperial Majesty. A place for my dwelling has also most kindly been granted here, in Benátky castle, 5 miles distant from Prague, where I shall be free — more conveniently and tranquilly than in the city — to pursue my accustomed Astronomical and Spagyric [alchemical] exercises</b>, and to complete and consecrate the Astronomical work I have long and much laboured at, to the honour of His Imperial Majesty and the Empire (since, through certain men's ignorance and envy, it could not be done in my fatherland) — for which cause too I am having my whole Astronomical apparatus, and all the furnishings I left at Magdeburg, transferred hither.</p><p>But, to answer Your Magnificence's erudite questions to my power: thus hold it. <b>That the star of Venus was not seen in the heaven at the beginning of the 4th year before Christ, I would count in the place of a great miracle, if the thing were undoubtedly established.</b> Let the credit rest with the Author, who, since he was a Poet, perhaps used a Poetic licence — since not even the Historians (who wish to be held more truthful, and certainly ought to be) always report, in correctly indicating celestial portents, things consonant with experience; so that sometimes they take certain <b>chasmata</b> [sky-cleavings] and fiery inflammations of the air (often showing various forms) for <b>Comets</b> — which, however, hold a place in the heaven itself, and there complete their course, as I have demonstratively proved, by reliable observations, of the seven I beheld within 22 years. But grant that the star of Venus [♀] then miraculously hid itself: <b>that it could have happened by the intervention of Mercury [☿] — that it thus suffered an eclipse — does not seem likely</b>; for Mercury's star does not appear in the heaven of such magnitude as to be able to hide Venus's star, which is seen much larger. Add that <b>Mercury's star is luminous, and not endowed with an opaque body like the Moon [☾]</b>; and so, if it were ever centrally interposed between Venus and our sight, it would rather join its light to that light, and so augment it more than diminish it. I am silent that, on account of the speed of its motion, <b>Mercury cannot long abide under Venus's star, unless both their motions were equalised in the same direction, which most rarely happens</b>. Besides, since you yourself found, from the Prutenic calculation, that <b>Mercury's star was then 2¾° more northern than Venus</b>, it was not partilely and exactly enough joined for an eclipse. For although a great diversity of latitude is detected in the heaven, from both tables — not only of these two Planets, which they call the lower, but of the rest too — yet I have not noticed Mercury's star to deviate so enormously as to latitude.</p><p>I have indeed <b>observed Venus, when it was about the middle of Pisces [♓], near the Perigee of the Epicycle (as they commonly speak), able to reach a Northern latitude of 9° 50′ — three degrees more than the Alphonsine calculation gives</b> (though that exceeds the Copernican by about one), so that its digression from the Ecliptic into the North can grow to a full 10°; and this I noted twice, namely in the years <b>1587 and 1595, with the eight-year interval interposed (as is required)</b> — at which times too I observed Venus, both evening and morning, within three and four days, with my instruments, and thence more readily restored its \"commutation\" (as Copernicus calls it). So too <b>in Mercury and the three superior Planets I found no small deviation in their latitudes from the celestial norm</b> (as you hint) — so that not even in those three (where the reckoning is simpler) are the <b>limits of the maximum latitudes rightly predefined by Ptolemy</b> (whose tenets Copernicus too securely accepted); nor do the <b>nodes of the intersections of the planets' circuits with the Ecliptic stand, with respect to the Apogees, as Copernicus assumed from Ptolemy</b>. And if what Ptolemy transmitted of these was consonant with the heaven in his age, <b>these nodes, and the limits of maximum latitude, must have a far different, perceptible motion of their own than that which proceeds from the change of the Apogees</b> — a difference of many degrees now intervening. <b>Nay, in the Moon itself no small variety of latitude is seen, and a diversity from the predecessors' tradition. For in new and full moons the maximum latitude can be 4° 58′, which reaches the Ptolemaic limit within 2′; but in the quadratures [□] it augments the same by a third of a degree, on the north and south limit; and — what is more wonderful — it shows besides the motion of the nodes unequal, reciprocating back and forth each month, and that by a perceptible interval, which reaches 1° 35′.</b> From what has just been said, then (of which, however, I will treat more fully and particularly elsewhere, God willing), it is clear enough that Your Magnificence not without cause holds suspect the planet-latitude calculation drawn from the usual tables. Nonetheless, around those times when the computation in Mercury's latitude was made from the Prutenic tables, I should scarcely believe that so great an error could have crept in — because <b>Ptolemy's times, when he watched the star-observations, were ten times nearer [to his epoch] than we now are</b>; and the Prutenic canons subscribe most closely to the Ptolemaic for that age. But if some error crept into Ptolemy's observations (which I suspect, not without cause, has not rarely happened), we build upon this foundation in vain; wherefore his tenets are to be used very circumspectly, and only with a competent limitation applied.</p><p>Concerning the <b>Eclipses</b> of which you write, it is thus: that the <b>Prutenic tables agree most closely with those which happened in Ptolemy's age, yet are not precisely adapted to each</b>; for it is established that <b>Copernicus applied his numbers to the Ptolemaic Eclipses (so far as could be done in some) as Epochs</b>. Yet in all he could not attain an accurate exactness, on account of <b>a certain other implication of the lunar inequality, noticed by neither Ptolemy nor himself</b>. For <b>three eclipses, as they practised, do not suffice to work out the Moon's first inequality</b> — to say nothing here of the second, yet more intricate one; but more are needed, in different places of the Eccentric or greater Epicycle, and those most accurately noted. That you may consider this somewhat, in at least one example, <b>I send the practical working [<i>pragmatia</i>] of the lunar eclipse at the beginning of this year</b> — both in the triangular demonstration from our hypothesis, and in the corresponding numbers, worked out (with my leave) and printed by <b>the eminent Wittenberg Mathematician M. Jöstel</b>, while I tarried there a while, on account of the plague raging in Bohemia and other causes. But when <b>my tables of the motion of the luminaries (which I purpose to publish before the other Planets) shall, by divine help, come to light under the auspices of the Roman Emperor Rudolf II</b>, experience will testify that they fit not only our age exactly, but also sufficiently agree with those most ancient Eclipses — so far as the ancients' observations were precisely consonant with the very appearances of the heaven. And what, pray, could they furnish, in the Moon or the other Planets, immune from all fault, <b>when they used the fixed stars less faithfully corrected</b> — as <b>the most accurate restitution of a thousand fixed stars, [done] to the very minute, and sometimes to its half, drawn out by me over the past years, and lately most submissively exhibited to the Imperial Majesty in a manuscript on parchment</b>, will abundantly testify, by a comparison set up with the usual tables. <b>I also offered the same Imperial Majesty, in another manuscript book, the restitution of the Sun and Moon for each day of the next two years, with the discrepancy of both calculations — the Alphonsine and the Copernican — set down at once (which is sometimes not small). Nay, and in a third book of the same form and size — the Mechanical part of Astronomy, representing my Instruments which I have hitherto elaborated, and printed after I left Denmark — I likewise most humbly dedicated to His Imperial Majesty.</b> Of this last I would have sent now <b>three copies — two for the most illustrious Dukes of Bavaria, William the elder and father, and Maximilian the son, [now] reigning; the third for yourself — but I have none at hand now that is properly illuminated with colours (as they call it)</b>. But I am now having this done at Prague; which finished, I will do what I just said, and perhaps send even more — whatever I can have copied so quickly. Meanwhile do not find it a burden to indicate, in a few words, whether your said most illustrious Princes are affected by such things. May Your Magnificence fare most happily and most long, and continue often to visit me with your erudite letters, as you have benevolently begun, and to continue and preserve the friendship once begun by them (since otherwise it can scarcely be); you will find me most ready to do the same, so that you may never repent of having thus striven first; and I gladly promise and offer you all my zeal and whatever services can proceed from me. For the rest, that I did not write back sooner, impute it to the various occupations by which I was hindered at Prague, and to my recent migration into this castle, and hold it candidly excused. I wished to compensate that tardiness in writing back by the prolixity you see — which I beg may be welcome.</p><p>And now again, and oftener, may Your Magnificence fare most well.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 31st day of August, the year 1599.<br>Most zealous of Your Magnificence,<br><b>Tycho Brahe.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Tycho → Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (Munich) — <b>the letter Herwart answers in brahe-t_15990917001 (17 September 1599)</b>, where he cites \"your letter from Benátky on the last day of last month.\" Read the two as a pair.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's reply on the Venus-star and his planetary/lunar discoveries (a major scientific letter).</b></li><li><b>The Benátky establishment.</b> Tycho confirms his clemently-granted seat at <b>Benátky, \"5 miles from Prague,\"</b> for his \"Astronomical and Spagyric [alchemical] exercises,\" with his apparatus being moved from <b>Magdeburg</b>.</li><li><b>The Venus-star debunked (physical refutation).</b> Against <b>Herwart's theory that a reported non-appearance of Venus \"at the beginning of 4 BC\" was a Mercury-occultation</b> (cf. brahe-t_15990917001; the chronology question Scaliger also attacked, brahe-t_16000310001), Tycho marshals <b>physical arguments</b>: the \"Author\" was a <i>poet</i> (poetic licence); <b>Mercury is far too small to hide the much larger Venus; Mercury is luminous, not opaque like the Moon, so it would <i>add</i> light, not eclipse it; Mercury moves too fast to stay under Venus; and it was 2¾° off in latitude</b> (Herwart's own Prutenic figure) — far from a partile conjunction. A model of Tychonic empirical reasoning. <i>(He also defends, in passing, the supra-lunar place of comets — \"seven in 22 years.\")</i></li><li><b>Planetary-latitude discoveries.</b> Tycho reports observing <b>Venus reach 9° 50′–10° N latitude (3° beyond the Alphonsine, ~1° beyond the Copernican), noted twice (1587 &amp; 1595, the required 8-year interval)</b>; and deviations of <b>Mercury and the three superior planets</b> from Ptolemy/Copernicus — concluding that the <b>maximum-latitude limits and the nodes have an independent motion</b>, not derivable from the apogees, differing now \"by many degrees.\"</li><li><b>The lunar discoveries (the core, celebrated in Herwart's reply of 17 September 1599).</b> <b>Max lunar latitude 4° 58′ at syzygy (within 2′ of Ptolemy's limit), but +⅓° at the quadratures; and — \"more wonderful\" — the nodes' motion is unequal, reciprocating monthly by up to 1° 35′.</b> This is Tycho's <b>variation of the inclination and libration of the nodes</b> (cf. the same to Scaliger, brahe-t_16000723001).</li><li><b>On eclipses and tables.</b> The Prutenic tables fit Ptolemy-era eclipses (Copernicus used them as epochs) but not precisely — for <b>\"three eclipses do not suffice to work out the Moon's first inequality,\" still less the second</b>; more, in varied epicycle positions, are needed. Tycho sends <b>the worked lunar eclipse of early 1599</b> (triangular demonstration + numbers <b>printed by Melchior Jöstel at Wittenberg</b>, where Tycho waited out the Bohemian plague), and promises his <b>luminary tables \"under the auspices of Rudolf II\"</b> (the future Rudolphine work) — superior because the ancients \"used the fixed stars less faithfully corrected.\"</li><li><b>The three books offered to Rudolf II</b> (a precise inventory): (1) <b>the 1000-fixed-star restitution \"to the minute, sometimes the half,\" on parchment</b>; (2) <b>a Sun–Moon restitution for each day of the next two years, with Alphonsine/Copernican discrepancies</b>; (3) <b>the <i>Mechanica</b></i> (printed since leaving Denmark). And a <b>Mechanica gift plan</b>: three coloured/illuminated copies — for <b>Duke William V of Bavaria (\"the elder, father\") and Duke Maximilian I (\"the son, reigning\") and Herwart</b> — to follow once illuminated at Prague. <i>(A datable Mechanica distribution arrangement.)</i></li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pair with <b>brahe-t_15990917001 (Herwart's reply, 17 September 1599)</b>; the <b>lunar-theory</b> thread (brahe-t_16000723001); the <b>Venus-star/chronology</b> thread (brahe-t_16000310001); and the <b>Mechanica-distribution</b> layer (the Bavarian-duke copies).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Herwart von Hohenburg; <b>Rudolf II</b>; the unnamed Roman-poet \"Author\" (the Venus-star source); <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus</b>; the <b>Alphonsine</b> and <b>Prutenic</b> tables; <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (Wittenberg); <b>Duke William V</b> &amp; <b>Duke Maximilian I of Bavaria</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, <b>Wittenberg</b>. Glyphs ♀ ☿ ☾ ♓ □ reproduced.</li><li><b>Place/calendar (protocol §3, §4.1).</b> Dateline <i>ex arce Caesareae Benachia die 31. Augusti Anno 1599</i> → <b>Benátky, 31 August 1599 [N.S.]</b>; the <b>catalogue label's \"Wittenberg\" is a location error</b> (contradicted by the dateline — Tycho had moved to Benátky by late August 1599; cf. brahe-t_15990922001), flagged for the label audit. Source normalized from the OCR-marred dump (apparatus sigla removed), faithful to the wording.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the Benátky/Spagyric establishment; the <b>physical refutation of the Venus–Mercury occultation</b>; the <b>Venus-latitude (9°50′) and planetary-node discoveries</b>; the <b>lunar variation (+⅓° at quadrature) and nodal reciprocation (1°35′)</b>; the eclipse/table methodology with the <b>Jöstel-printed 1599 eclipse</b>; the promise of the <b>Rudolf-auspiced luminary tables</b>; the <b>three books offered to the Emperor</b>; and the <b>Mechanica gift to the Bavarian dukes and Herwart</b>) is firmly construed and pairs exactly with Herwart's reply of 17 September 1599. Flags: the <b>label's wrong location</b> (resolved to Benátky per §4.1) and the OCR-normalized source.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 7 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp. 158-164, the full letter Tycho-Herwart 31 Aug 1599) in two chunks (1-4, 5-7). Collated opening (S./HIC est, Vir Nobilis...), closing (M. T. studiosissimus TYCHO BRAHE, Dabantur ex arce Caesareana Benachia die 31. Augusti Anno 1599), and every proper name, number, date and technical figure against the page images: the 2 3/4 degree Mercury-Venus latitude, the 9 deg 50' / three-degree Alphonsine excess, the 1587/1595 eight-year interval, the 4 deg 58'/2'/1 deg35' lunar-latitude figures, the astrologer/mathematician names (Ptolemy, Copernicus, Jostel, Rudolf II), the three-copies/two-Bavarian-Dukes-plus-Tycho distribution of the Mechanica. Source_text and published_english both match Dreyer's printed text exactly (modulo normal long-s/ligature typography); no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss or text divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15990723001", "next": "brahe-t_15990917001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990723001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15990917001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports his establishment at Benátky castle under imperial patronage and plans to pursue astronomical and alchemical work there, with instruments being transferred from Magdeburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990831001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990909001", "d": "1599-09-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Benátky nad Jizerou, 9 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist, autobiographical) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>; orig. in<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Editorial note:</b> the witness is defective at several points, chiefly in the castle-tour passage; the gaps are reproduced as runs of dots in the source and marked <b>[lacuna]</b> in the translation. They are the named crux behind the [C] grade.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD OLIGERUM ROSENCRANTZIUM.</p><p>S. P,</p><p>AD eas, quas antequam Witeberga discederem, pauculis interiectis septimanis, a te accepi literas, Exoptatissime mi OLIGERE, tunc responsum misi, speroque id tibi iam pridem redditum. Abij autem inde cum meis omnibus circa Solstitium Aestivum, neque enim diutius isthic haerere ausus fui, utut viatico, quod e Dania expectabam, non satis instructus. Scripsit enim aliquoties ad me THADDAeUS noster, Caesarem pene singulis diebus de me, an advenissem, percontatum esse. Nec tamen prius licuit profectionem instituere, partim quod tertius ille liber, qui luminarium restitutos motus exhibet, Imperatori destinatus, antea absolvi nequiverit, partim quod filia mea natu maior, quae periculose ibi decubuit, paulo ante id temporis primum convaluerit. In itinere autem Dresdam a nobili et Magnifico viro Domino IOHANNE [LOe]SERO, quem procul dubio nosti, Bretziae in Arce eius humaniter invitati et laute tractati sumus. Cumque biduo post Dresdam venissemus, relicto illic apud civem quendam primarium mihi notum, Virum integrum et officiosum, Gynaeceo meo, ipsemet Pragam, cum filijs et reliquo comitatu me contuli. Ubi statim ac advenissem, a multis praecipuis Viris perhumaniter et honorifice exceptus sum, inprimis vero a Magnifico Domino IOHANNE BARVICIO Imperij supremo Secretario, Caesareae Maiestati a Consilijs intimis, qui unus pene omnia agit apud Caesarem. Is in horto quodam, non multum ab Arce remoto (siquidem non longe a Caesare abesse audet) tam nomine Imperatoriae Maitis quam Consiliariorum et proprio, de adventu meo humanissimis verbis mihi congratulatus est, admotumque acceptum esse Caesari meum accessum referebat, eiusque erga me etiam, necdum visum, clementem animum depraedicabat. Cumque libros tres, Caesari humillime offerendos, unâque Literas Electoris Coloniensis et Ducis Magnopolitani commendatitias monstrassem, se de ijs Caesarem percontaturum aiebat, ignorare enim, e cuius manu illa accipere velit; responsumque dedit sequenti die, Caesaream Maiestatem a nemine alio quam me ipso ea velle suscipere, brevique in Arcem ad se me vocaturum. Ostendi etiam Domino BARVICIO quasdam literas, ab Illustrissimo Duce OTTONE, Harburgi prope Hamburgum habitante, ad Illustrem et Generosum Dominum RUMPHIUM Curiae Caesareae Magistrum, praecipuaeque post Caesarem isthic authoritatis virum, mea de caussa scriptas. Suasit Dominus BARVICIUS, ut ipsemet eas illi intra diem unum vel alterum (quod alias per filium TYCHONEM fieri volebam) offerrem. Captata itaque commoditate, in Arcem curru vectus, ad eundem Dnum RUMPHIUM mox introductus sum, qui etiam benignissime me excepit, et perhumaniter affatus est. Inter alia vero aiebat, se multa relatione Praestantium virorum prius de me inaudivisse; gaudere autem, quod nunc praesentem videat; sed non posse se et alios satis mirari, quod Rex me e Dania dimittere voluerit, idque eius iuvenili aetati, quod Marti potius quam Arti operam daret, tribuendum censuit; at ego e contra Regem excusavi, quamque virtute et ingenio polleat, extuli. Mox ille subiunxit: oportet igitur eos, qui ab illo secundi, et autoritate isthic praevalent, aut bonarum Artium rudes, aut malevolos et invidos esse, cum Regis et Patriae honori non rectius consulant; ad quae verba ego non multa respondi, id saltem subijciens, me non dubitare, si Regi omnia de me rite proposita fuissent, ac ab ijs, quos intime habet, illi commendata, ipsum non minus, quam Pater antea, Laudatissimae memoriae FRIDERICUS, Astronomica mea excercitia in suo Regno conservaturum; sed forte peculiari Dei providentia sic factum esse, ut ea, quae in Astronomicis diu multumque molitus sum, nunc alio, atque ad ipsum Imperatorem, Imperij caput, transferenda sint. Haec et similia cum dicto Domino RUMPHIO (qui elegantissime Latine loquitur) verba commutans, literas praenominati Principis illi tradidi, isque, discedentem aliquantulum deducens, meas res sibi curae futuras, seque eas apud Caesarem promoturum, pollicitus est. Insinuavit autem quidam ex Amicis postea, id quod Dominus BARVICIUS adeo ursit, ut in Arcem ascendens ipsemet literas Domino RUMPHIO inscriptas ei in manus exhiberem, id propterea factum, quod Caesar e conclavi suo, quod e regione est, me hac occasione antea videre vellet, quam ad se vocaret. Quamprimum vero ex Arce descenderem, mox obviam factus Dnus BARVICIUS in aedes quasdam splendidas et magnificas (quas prior Imperij Cancellarius, Dominus IACOBUS CURTIUS laudatae memoriae Italica architectura exstruxerat, et horto pulcherrimo cinxerat expensis maioribus quam 20 Ioachymicorum) iussu Caesaris introduxit singulaque in ijs monstravit, subiungens, Caesaream Maiestatem eas mihi a vidua procurare velle, si placerent, in ijs a Domino CURTIO turriculam, quam cernerem, ob id exstructam, ut observationibus coelestibus inserviret, et esse eas aedes Arci atque habitationi Caesaris vicinas, ut promptior sit accessus. Verum cum is ex taciturnitate mea, et quod dicerem, turriculam eam vix uni ex meis instrumentis sufficere, nedum pluribus, colligeret animum meum non admodum eo inclinari, ulterius inferebat: Si forte Pragae habitare mihi non luberet, concessurum facile Imperatorem Arcem quampiam ex suis, unius vel dimidij diei itinere ab urbe distantem, ubi pacatius agerem, extra invidiam aliorum positus. Id cum magis mihi arridere animadverteret, praesertim cum dicerem, me, ut otio fruerer Philosophico, non nimium ab alijs interpellatus, ob id maluisse in Dania insulam eam inhabitare, subiunxit, se Caesari id relaturum, et iam intellexisse, eius animum in has alteras partes inclinare; Caesarem etiam de salario annuo mihi clementer impertiendo constituisse; de quo ubi audientiam, uti vocant (quod intra duos vel tres dies futurum existimavit) nactus essem, plenius mecum agendum. Elapsis mox pauculis diebus, Caesar me in Arcem ad se accersi iubet: constitutum vero fuit antea in Senatu, ut aulae magister Dns RUMPHIUS me ad Caesarem solemniter introduceret, quod hoc esset magis honorificum; at Caesar id tunc temporis renuit, sive quod quidam ex ministris RUMPHIJ ijs diebus peste infectus et in civitatem delatus obijsset, sive quod ipsius Maiestas cum me solo, nemine praesente, loqui maluerit, quod et factum est; nam cum ad ianuam conclavis, in quo Caesar esset, me duxisset Dns BARVICIUS, se non ingredi, nisi a Caesare vocatum, insinuans, extra remansit. Ingressusque solus ego ad Caesar. Matem, quam solam etiam mensae cuidam in superiori conclavis parte dorso innitentem conspexi, nemine alias in toto conclavi praesente, et ne quidem puero famulante, exhibitaque debita reverentia, Caesar me statim nutu capitis propius ad se vocavit; cum accessissem, clementer manum mihi porrigebat; moxque paululum recedens, oratiunculam habui Latino sermone, qua, ex ipsius Caesar. Matis clementissimo mandato, per literas sui Cancellarij CORADUCIJ me vocatum nunc comparere, quod et prius, si res meae id ullo modo ferre potuissent, fecissem, dixi, utque hanc moram clementissime excusatam habere dignaretur, et me atque ea, quae diu multumque tractavi, studia Caesareo favore prosequi ac sibi commendata esse velit. Quo etiam nomine literas a Serenissimo Coloniensi Electore et Illustrissimo Duce Megapolitano scriptas, ipsi humillime porrigerem, moxque literas illas eius Maiestati in manus tradidi, quas et clementer accepit meque praesente aperuit. Verum ijs in mensam repositis, et nondum lectis, statim ipsius Caesarea Maiestas copiosiore etiam sermone, quam ego eam alloquebar, mihi clementissime respondit; et inter alia gratum sibi esse meum accessum, atque me cum meis studijs sibi curae futurum promisit, ac inter loquendum blanditer quasi arrisit, ut favorem eximium ipso vultu ostenderet. Verum omnia, quae referebat, verba capere non potui, tam submisse ipsa natura loquitur. Deinde ego, pro hac clementissima animi sui indicatione humillime gratias agens, de libris tribus, quos ipsius Maiestati subiectissime dedicatos in promptu haberem, mentionem feci; quos cum se lubenter a me accepturam clementissime responderet, ego mox eos a filio TYCHONE, qui in exteriori conclavi expectans secum habuit, ad me recipiens, denuoque ingressus, Caesareae Matem, quae adhuc mensae astabat, in manus exhibui; quae postquam eos clementissime accepisset, atque prope se in mensa reposuisset, ego rursus brevi narratione, quid singuli eorum continerent, summatim indicavi; rursusque ipsius Caesarea Maiestas sermone satis luculento excepit, atque eos gratos sibi futuros benignissime indicavit. Sicque praestito honore discessi; et BARVICIUM ante ianuam adhuc praestolantem reperi, qui statim audito signo ad Caesarem introvocatus, me, ut, antequam rediret, non descenderem, rogavit, statimque reversus dixit, Caesarem automatum illud, quod in curru haberem (hoc enim, cum in Arcem curru veherer, per fenestram conspexerat) sibi monstrari cupere, illud itaque per filium e curru mox allatum, BARVICIO tradidi, atque eius usum Caesari ostendendum monstravi. Quod cum fecisset, atque ad me reversus esset, aiebat, Caesarem dixisse, se unum vel alterum eiuscemodi habere, quorum tamen nullum tam magnum esset, neque penitus ea ratione factum, posse aliquem e suis Astronomis sibi ad huius imitationem quoddam conficere, sed nolle me eo privari; et addebat insuper BARVICIUS, Caesarem singulari Clementia et gratia erga me affectum esse, brevique negotio ad consiliarios relato, velle mecum de salario annuatim numerando atque habitatione idonea transigi curare. Interim iuberent, Gynaeceum meum, quod Dresdae me reliquisse intellexerat, Pragam ad me advehi faciam, ipsius Caesaream Maiestatem nobis omnibus ita prospecturam, ut ad commode vivendum nihil sit defuturum. Quare sequenti mox die filium TYCHONEM unâ cum CLAUDIO MULAeO, pro adducendo Gynaeceo Dresdam misi, quod post octiduum Pragam advenit. Intera de salario mecum per eundem Dominum BARVICIUM et sequentibus etiam aliquot diebus, per intervalla, actum est, siquidem is sit occupatissimus, et nonnulli e Senatoribus Imperatoriae voluntati non satis cito, uti fit, auscultent, lenteque omnia in illa aula ob negotiorum magnitudinem et copiam procedant. Cupiente vero BARVICIO ex Caesaris mandato, ut familiam meam, quanta sit, et eorum, qui ad Artis sustentationem requirerentur, summam consignarem, atque magnitudinem horum omnium in taxa, uti aiunt, deducerem, primum quidem lubens feci; at alterum Caesareae Matis Clementissimae voluntati et libero arbitrio humillime commisi. Quae paucis post diebus 3000 aureorum in certa quadam pecunia mihi benignissime deputavit, et insuper quaedam alia accidentaria ...... pro oeconomia et alijs rebus, quae 1000, si computentur, excedunt. Haec Senatus consulto peracta sunt, quibusdam id facile approbantibus, at pluribus, uti fieri solet, nonnihil reluctantibus, quod nemo ex illis, qui pluribus annis Caesari inservijssent, tantum haberent salarium. Vicit tamen Caesaris ........ci ut sic fieret ursit, cumque per praedictum Dominum BARVICIUM mihi hoc significari curasset, iussit insuper dici, si haec mihi non sufficerent, et in posterum quidpiam desiderarem, nec sibi esse accurtatam manum, nec mihi obturatum os, quod his ipsis verbis ad me idem retulit BARVICIUS. Quin et feudum secundum constitutionem Imperij haereditarium, ut liberis et familiae meae post me consuleretur, Clementissime nec invite addixit, quam primum aliquod in suis ditionibus vel Imperio vacarit, quod mihi et meis commodum esset. Qua etiam de re literas, quas vocant promissivas, brevi accepturus. Et quoniam me Pragae aedes Curtianas non admodum cupere (habui enim meas rationes, cum quod viduam, quae eas adhuc inhabitat, mea de caussa emigrare nolim, tum quod nimijs visitationibus, in tanta praesertim civitate, prope Arcem obnoxius essem) intellexit Caesar, promisit nihilominus clementissime aliam in urbe habitationem mihi oportunam, quam, ubi eo accederem, sive vocatus, sive sponte, cum meis commode incolere possem, et propria quadra vivere minoreque sumptu; forte tamen Curtianam illam suo tempore accipiam, ubi vidua sponte emigrarit, quae nunc, uti audio, novas affectat nuptias, more iuvencularum viduarum. Iussit insuper Caesar, praeterquam quod Salarium meum ab eo tempore, quo ab ipsius Caesarea Mate vocatus sum Pragam, etiamsi multo interiecto tempore non advenerim, incipere deberet, ut mihi 2000 florenorum statim honorarij loco pro introitu darentur, quos et accepi. Dixit quoque inter alia aliquando ad me BARVICIUS [si] foret, ut ante decennium huc ad Caesarem advenissem, [cum] omnia erant meliora et promptiora, nec ita difficultatibus obnoxia, uti nunc ob bellum in Hungaria, tunc Caesarem potuisse multo faciliorem et liberaliorem se erga me praebere; cumque hoc semel atque iterum insinuaret, ego quidem respondi, nihil adhuc periculi esse: Caesarem Turca fere semper superiorem fuisse, et Deum in posterum suum Christianismum protecturum. Verum tacite mecum cogitavi, neminem me ipso magis dolere, quod non ante decennium statim ab obitu laudatissimae memoriae FRIDERICI Regis Patria excesserim, atque huc me contulerim, quod et tunc fecissem, nisi literae a toto Senatu ....... magno meo incommodo, maximoque rei familiaris dispendio, me ....... de his alias agemus, non iustum sit, ut aliqua mihi fiat pro tantis expensis compensatio. Quibus transactis, per Capitaneum quendam Caesareum, eius Mate sic iubente, in Arces prope Pragam dispositas deductus sum, ut ex ijs eligerem aliquam, quae mihi et meis studijs commodior foret. Nomi........ et aliae; sed quoniam ut ex ijs una cum civitatibus propinquis p.........arat, et tertiam in edito positam, Caesar .........enter accederet, qui quondam Bohemici Aulae Magistri fuerat, et Caesar secundum iuris ten.........ata, dissuasit Dominus BARVICIUS earum perlustrationem. Eae vero, quas v........um unius vel etiam dimidij diei profectione Praga absunt, quarum prima dicitur Brandeiss, estque potissima Caesaris Arx in vicinia sita, utpote tribus saltem miliaribus Praga distans, in quam se delectationis gratia interdum se ....... venationes habet circumquaque praecipuas. Altera Lyssa dicta est, etiam .......da, 4 milliaribus ab urbe remota. Tertia Benachia appellatur, qua ....... sub initium huius anni a .......ruvij cuiusdam senio confecti et defuncti .......nis, producentis quindecim .....................int, illi enim, non satis concordes, haereditatem alias inter se dividere nequiverunt, sed sponte Caesari haec bona vendiderunt. Perlustratis itaque his tribus, deducente Capitaneo, Arcibus, et in singulis benigne et honorifice exceptus, praetuli Benachiam, 6 horarum itinere (est enim via plana) Praga semotam, quae ob loci amoenitatem et fluviolum Isaram circumlabentem atque in Albim postea se exonerantem, Bohemorum vernacula lingua sonat, ac si diceres Venetiae Bohemorum, estque in edito et aprico loco sita, iucundissimo prospectu, adiacente parva quadam civitate, in qua religio, quam Evangelicam vocant, praevalet, plurimis tamen ex Picardis, qui Calvinisticae (quam Sectatores orthodoxam nuncupant) appropinquant, in eadem degentibus. Cum Caesar intellexisset, me hanc Arcem prae ceteris eligere, mox per praedictum Capitaneum, qui Brandusijs praeest, me huc introduci curavit, ubi nunc cum familia, commode et pacate, quod Deo acceptum fero, solus habito, nemine obstrepente aut interturbante, sed dicto Capitaneo ex voluntate Caesaris omnia officia mihi lubenter exhibente. Qua etiam de caussa intra unam vel aliam septimanam Brandusio huc accurrit, ut mihi in ijs, quae requiruntur, praesto sit. Mandavit etiam Caesar eidem Capitaneo, ut omnia necessaria pro observatorio, meis instrumentis omnibus rite disponendis idoneo, actutum procuret, quo iuxta meam ordinationem omnia ante hyemem apposite exaedificentur, tum quoque ut laboratorium, ea qua voluero ratione, mihi oportuno loco confici curet, et omnia, quae alias a Mechanicis fieri velim, solvat. Si haec mihi in Patria evenissent, quantis sumptibus pepercissem, pro quibus nunc mihi nulla restat gratia, sed potius ingrata omnia [nihil feci]sse benigne. Obtulit insuper Caesarea Maiestas sponte sua, se novum Uraniburgum Arcem in suo territorio suisque sumptibus aedificaturum, quod Danico illi non cederet, sed multis parasangis excederet. Delectatur enim ipsius Caesarea Matas summopere non saltem Divina illa Astronomia Mathematicisque studijs, sed et architectonicis structuris, quae e Geometria et Perspectiva fluunt. Cumque ipsius Maiestas Libros sibi a me humillime oblatos (de quibus antea memini) diligenter perlegisset, adeo ut in multam noctem primis diebus, referente id BARVICIO, ijs occuparetur (neque enim tam abiecti apud ipsum erant, quemadmodum in vestris oris) a me multa de eorum contentis per eundem BARVICIUM sciscitatus est, et placuit illi valde structura Uraniburgi in primo tomo designata. Sed cum laboratorium in ea tam cito reperire non posset, id sibi indicari voluit. Placuerunt caetera omnia, ita ut Dominus BARVICIUS eos Caesari acceptissimos esse mihi retulerit.</p><p>Ex iam recensitis, Amantissime OLIGERE, rerum mearum statum, qualis nunc sit, intellexisti. Agoque Deo propitio gratias, quod sic mihi et meis prospexerit, ut ex adversitatibus illis, quas in Patria diu multumque passus sum, eluctari potuerim, et mihi et meis rectius consulere. Speroque in posterum, eodem Divino Numine favente, adhuc prosperiora. Caeterum de honoribus et dignitatibus Politicis mihi oblatis, imo antequam Pragam venirem, cum adhuc Witebergae essem, deputatis (prout forte ex Domino Doctore FRANTZIO, Domini Doctoris VITI WINSHEMIJ genero, aut oretenus; aiebat enim te, in Orientalem Frisiam abiturus, invisere velle; aut per literas intellexisti, quemadmodum is ex Domino CORRADUCIO Procancellario haec, cum Pragae esset, se accepisse aiebat, ut mihi referret, quo promptius accederem) nihil nunc addere volo. Suaserunt Pragae amici quidam, ut talia susciperem, quae tamen semper ex alto despexi. At si facturus sum, propter vestrates, qui me tam insolenter contemptui habuerunt, facturus sum; quod et THADDAeO nostro urgenti dixi; verum is sic subiunxit: Non tantum propter tuos Danos id admittes, sed etiam propter nostros Bohemos, adeoque in ipsa aula aliqui tam obtuso et rustico ingenio, et non minus quam vestrates ignorent discernere inter aurum et orichalcum, attamen aliqui inveniuntur, qui rectius iudicent, quique, licet aeque atque tu honores Politicos, respectu virtutis et doctrinae eximiae, parvi ducant, ob vulgarium tamen iudiciorum captum, quo commodius in mundo vivatur, ita faciendum existimant. Qui, quamvis pauci reperiantur, tamen prae caeteris iudicio valent, tuique sunt amantissimi, quos partim hactenus pro primo hoc congressu nosti, partim in posterum plenius tibi innotescent. In hunc modum ille. Verum de his postmodum dispiciam, si non ea prorsus despiciam; neque enim in ijs quid consulto et discrete fieri potest ante Magci Domini CORRADUC1J Procancellarij reditum e Roma, ubi Legatum Caesaris nomine apud P. M. agit, qui circa Michaëlis festum expectatur. Neque etiam volo, ut prius quid in talibus concludatur; est is mihi admodum favens, licet de facie ignoti adhuc simus, Mathematicarum quoque rerum, ut dicitur, amantissimus, hac in re antecessoris CURTIJ aemulus.</p><p>Habes nunc, charissime mi OLIGERE, historiolam mearum rerum, ex quo e Germania in Bohemiam cum meis migravi; de ijs, quae antea facta sunt, meoque e Dania discessu probe omnia nosti, ut ea repetere non sit opus, quae meminisse animus horrens iterare refugit. Et certe videor historiolam potius quam literas conscripsisse, dum calamo lapso teque fusius alloquor, quo [de] rebus meis plenius edoceare. Quam tamen prolixitatem tibi non ingratam fore, cum alias literas humaniores, adeoque ingentia librorum volumina, non, ut alij, qui Nobiles haberi volunt, fastidias, mihi persuadeo. De libris vero tibi promissis (ut tandem aliquid subiungam) sic habe. Non immemor sum pacti, nec difficilis ad servandum; sed cum exemplaria omnia de Mechanica Astronomia, quotquot coloribus exornata, sive uti vocant illuminata, ................................. Pragae suis coloribus imbui, quod ubi factum fuerit, obtinebis non saltem hunc primum tomum, sed et alterum de affixarum restitutione, tum quoque tertium de luminarium a me correcto motu, quem tamen ultimum denuo e prima conceptione describi curare oportet, cum nullum mihi ad manus mundum sit exemplar, postquam illud, quod Witebergae exaratum est, Caesi Mati unâ cum reliquis obtulerim. Ne sis itaque huius morae impatiens, mi OLIGERE, habebis et haec et alia ex meis, favente Numine, quam primum fieri poterit, nec sine foenore. Interim relinque sorori nostrae SOPHIAe eos, quos Regi destinaram (hos enim repudiatos et RAMELIO donatos ipsam consequi posse intellexi) quod etiam non displicet, malo etenim sic fieri, quam ut in invidorum et in osorum manus pervenirent.</p><p>Superest, ut calamum sistam, nisi hoc unicum superaddere luberet. Insulae Huennae, quam istic inhabitavi, resignationem voluntariam, quam suasisti (de qua etiam me, cum huc venirem, plenius rescripturum .......ui) nullatenus approbare animus est, dissuadentibus etiam ijs, qui Politicis non perfunctorie imbuti sunt, imo quibusdam id mihi vitio datum iri, tanquam prae contumaci insolentia, postquam ad Caesareas partes transivi, hoc facerem, quasi Danos meos, et quae isthic haberem, praefracto animo negligerem. Si vero insulam ij, qui ........ praesunt, mihi auferre voluerint, ob id quod ad Caesarem me contulerim, ipsius Caesaream Maitem aut operam daturam, ut Rex Daniae nihil damni mihi sua de caussa inferre patiatur, aut id, quicquid erit, alia ratione compensaturam. Obiectent vestrates, quicquid velint, facilis erit responsio, si modo iustitiae et veritati sit locus; sin minus, transeat id cum caeteris, et iustus iudex omnia decernat. Iamque cum coniuge tua, Nepte mea dilecta, quam felicissime vale, integerrime OLIGERE, salutata unâ cum hac Matre tua Nobilissima et honestissima ac simul alijs amicis, si qui in Cimbria vestra mei memores adhuc supersint, quos aliquos saltem adhuc restare crediderim. Iterumque et saepius quam optime vale, meque de tuis rebus luculenter etiam prima quaque occasione certiorem effice.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benachia die 9. Septembris Stylo novo, Anni Domini 1599.<br>Totus Tuus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>Non vacat relegere, ipsemet dispicias.</p><p>Cum hæc vix exarata essent, vocabatur Capitaneus Brandeisensis, qui hîc mecum convivabatur, Pragam ad Cæsarem, ubi postquam per decem dies adfuisset, reversus ad me,<br>se solummodo mea de caussa istuc accersitum referebat, uti in absentia Cæsaris, qui indies abitum Praga, ob pestem et dysenteriam isthic invalescentes, versus<br>Lynzam moliebatur, mihi quævis officia<br>præstaret, atque, quæ desiderarem, sive ad structuras, sive alia, procuraret, et<br>insuper innueret, quæ in meis rebus tam cito ob festinum Cæsaris abitum expediri non possent, ea postea,<br>cum Cæsar ad quietiorem locum pervenerit,<br>complenda. Interim vero dum ibi est, Cæsar,<br>intellecto, quod anthidotum aliquod contra morbos hosce Epidemicos a me dono<br>accepisset, per Dominum BARVICIUM id ab ipso<br>poscens, sibi reservavit, mihique scribi mandavit, ut præparationem eius ipsum<br>scripto docerem. In continenti itaque ad ipsius Cæsaream Matem datis Latine<br>literis (quarum hîc tenorem habes) humillime exposui, quæ eius Maiestas Clementissime expetierat. Sic et tu<br>habes descriptionem medicamenti a me excogitati, et in usu habiti contra luem<br>Epidemicam præsentis. Aliud exemplar Sorori SOPHIÆ communicavi, per te solum illi exponendum, quod et in<br>literis ad ipsam datis monui, alias secretum utrinque habete. Vides insuper<br>hinc, quam confidenter, imo quasi familiariter ipsi Cæsareæ Mati, et quidem obsignatis literis,<br>ut nemo Consiliariorum aperiat, scribere ausus sim ....................<br>Maiestate Clementissime ..... agit ........................... quam me meave in<br>Patriam ...........................tias dici, et pro literis meis et meo<br>........................... in copia aliqua, quales in montibus<br>........................... eliciam arte Spagyrica non<br>tan........................... Praga cum<br>Consiliarijs, totaque Aula ................. translatus dicitur. Quidam se<br>...........................nirent, Pragæ<br>primum examin...........................iecti sunt. Aiunt nihilominus<br>........................... pacis quærat; sed videtur id<br>m...........................figere puppibus arma et<br>bella ........................... Præsertim Principibus<br>German........................... Valachiæ heros constans et fortis<br>........................... Cardinali; cui SIGISMUNDUS Ba...........................stituit quod felix et<br>constans ........................... Interea dum hæc et alia scribo<br>........................... CRAMERUM tuum<br>meumque perstrin...............iuncto exemplo vides ...........................<br>atque non saltem tui ob diutinam ...........................bus latius<br>scriberem, cum doc........................... thesibus meis quomodo DUNCAN ...........................bus literis hac de<br>re ad me scrip................... nonnulla quæ rectius scit<br>dissim........................... Hand kand<br>faa mere enanden ...................... bort. Interim cogitet, quod<br>am........................... nec fecit unquam, sed per te a<br>................. tamque diutina familiaritate ..................... reticet.<br>Iamque iterum et quam ...........................</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Holger Rosenkrantz.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>To the letter I received from you, most longed-for Holger, a few weeks before I left Wittenberg, I then sent a reply, and hope it was long since delivered to you. I departed thence with all my people about the summer solstice, for I did not dare to linger there longer, however ill-furnished with the travelling-money I was expecting from Denmark. For our Thaddaeus wrote to me several times that the Emperor inquired about me almost daily, whether I had arrived. Nor yet was I able to set out sooner — partly because that third book, which presents the restored motions of the luminaries, destined for the Emperor, could not be finished beforehand; partly because my elder daughter, who lay dangerously ill there, had first recovered only a little before that time. On the road to Dresden we were courteously invited and sumptuously entertained by the noble and Magnificent Lord Johann Löser — whom you doubtless know — at his castle of Bretzia. And when, two days later, we had come to Dresden, leaving my Gynaeceum [womenfolk] there with a certain leading citizen known to me, an upright and obliging man, I betook myself to Prague with my sons and the rest of my company. Where, as soon as I had arrived, I was received most courteously and honourably by many leading men, but above all by the Magnificent Lord Johann Barvitius, supreme Secretary of the Empire, of His Imperial Majesty's inmost counsels, who alone manages almost everything with the Emperor. In a certain garden not far from the castle (since he dares not be far from the Emperor) he congratulated me on my arrival in most humane words, both in the name of the Imperial Majesty and of the Councillors and his own, and reported that my coming was very welcome to the Emperor, and extolled his clement disposition toward me even before I was seen. And when I had shown him the three books, to be most humbly offered to the Emperor, together with the commendatory letters of the Elector of Cologne and the Duke of Mecklenburg, he said he would inquire of the Emperor about them, for he did not know from whose hand he wished to receive them; and he gave answer the next day that His Imperial Majesty would take them up from no one but myself, and would shortly summon me up to him in the castle. I also showed Lord Barvitius certain letters written on my behalf by the most illustrious Duke Otto — dwelling at Harburg near Hamburg — to the illustrious and noble Lord Rumpf, Master of the Imperial Court, the man of chief authority there after the Emperor. Lord Barvitius advised that I present them to him myself within a day or two (which I had otherwise meant to do through my son Tycho). Seizing the opportunity, then, carried up to the castle by carriage, I was soon introduced to that same Lord Rumpf, who likewise received me most kindly, and addressed me most courteously. Among other things he said that he had previously heard much of me from the report of distinguished men; that he rejoiced now to see me present; but that he and others could not sufficiently marvel that the King had been willing to dismiss me from Denmark — and he judged this should be attributed to the King's youthful age, that he gave his efforts to Mars rather than to Art; but I, on the contrary, excused the King, and extolled how strong he was in virtue and talent. He soon added: those, then, who are second to him and prevail in authority there must be either raw in the good Arts, or malevolent and envious, since they do not better provide for the King's and the fatherland's honour; to which words I did not answer much, only subjoining that I did not doubt that, had everything about me been duly set before the King, and commended to him by those he holds intimate, he would no less than his Father before him — Frederik of most praiseworthy memory — have preserved my astronomical exercises in his Kingdom; but that perhaps by the special providence of God it had been so brought about, that what I had long and much laboured at in astronomy should now be transferred elsewhere, and to the Emperor himself, the head of the Empire. Exchanging these and like words with the said Lord Rumpf (who speaks Latin most elegantly), I handed him the aforenamed Prince's letter, and he, conducting me a little way as I departed, promised that my affairs would be his care, and that he would promote them with the Emperor. One of my friends afterwards intimated that the reason Lord Barvitius so pressed that, going up to the castle, I should myself present into Rumpf's hand the letters addressed to him, was this: that the Emperor wished, by this occasion, to see me beforehand from his chamber, which is opposite, before he summoned me to him. And as soon as I came down from the castle, Lord Barvitius, meeting me, brought me, by the Emperor's order, into certain splendid and magnificent houses (which the former Imperial Chancellor, Lord Jacob Curtius of praised memory, had built in the Italian architecture, and surrounded with a most beautiful garden, at a cost of more than 20 joachims), and showed me each thing in them, adding that His Imperial Majesty wished to procure them for me from the widow, if they pleased — that in them was a little tower, which I saw, built by Lord Curtius for the purpose of serving celestial observations, and that the houses were near the castle and the Emperor's dwelling, so that access might be the readier. But when, from my silence — and because I said that the little tower would scarcely suffice for one of my instruments, let alone several — he gathered that my mind was not much inclined that way, he further proposed: if perhaps it did not please me to live at Prague, the Emperor would readily grant some one of his castles, a day's or half a day's journey from the city, where I might live more peaceably, set beyond the envy of others. When he noticed that this pleased me more — especially since I said that, to enjoy philosophical leisure not too much interrupted by others, I had for that reason preferred to inhabit that island in Denmark — he added that he would report it to the Emperor, and that he had already understood his mind to incline to this latter course; that the Emperor had also resolved to bestow a yearly salary on me most kindly; about which, when I had obtained an audience (as they call it) — which he thought would be within two or three days — there should be fuller dealing with me.</p><p>A few days later, the Emperor orders me summoned up to him in the castle. It had indeed been earlier decided in the Senate that the court-master Lord Rumpf should solemnly introduce me to the Emperor, as being more honourable; but the Emperor at that time declined it — whether because one of Rumpf's servants, infected with plague in those days and carried into the city, had died, or because His Majesty preferred to speak with me alone, no one present, which is what happened. For when Lord Barvitius had led me to the door of the chamber in which the Emperor was, intimating that he did not enter unless called by the Emperor, he remained outside. And entering alone to His Imperial Majesty — whom I beheld also alone, leaning with his back on a certain table in the upper part of the chamber, no one else present in the whole chamber, not even a serving-boy — and having made due reverence, the Emperor at once called me nearer with a nod of the head; when I had approached, he kindly extended his hand to me; and soon, withdrawing a little, I delivered a little oration in Latin, in which I said that, by His Imperial Majesty's most clement command, by the letters of his Chancellor Coraduz, I now appeared having been summoned — which I would have done sooner, had my affairs been able in any way to bear it — and [begged] that he would deign most clemently to hold this delay excused, and would be willing to pursue with imperial favour me and the studies I have long and much handled, and to hold them commended to him. On which score, too, I would most humbly present to him the letters written by the most Serene Elector of Cologne and the most illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg; and soon I handed those letters into His Majesty's hands, which he both clemently accepted and opened in my presence. But having laid them back on the table, and not yet read them, His Imperial Majesty at once replied to me most clemently, with even more copious speech than I had used in addressing him; and among other things promised that my coming was welcome to him, and that I, with my studies, would be his care; and while speaking he smiled, as it were, graciously, to show his exceptional favour by his very countenance. But I could not catch all the words he uttered, so softly does he speak by his very nature. Then I, most humbly giving thanks for this most clement declaration of his mind, made mention of the three books which I had ready, most submissively dedicated to His Majesty; and when he most clemently replied that he would gladly accept them from me, I soon, receiving them from my son Tycho — who, waiting in the outer chamber, had them with him — and entering again, presented them into the hands of His Imperial Majesty, who was still standing by the table; and after he had most clemently accepted them, and laid them on the table near himself, I again, in a brief account, summarily indicated what each of them contained; and again His Imperial Majesty took it up in speech sufficiently clear, and most kindly signified that they would be welcome to him. And so, the honour being rendered, I withdrew; and found Barvitius still waiting before the door, who, as soon as a signal was heard, was called in to the Emperor, [and] asked me not to go down before he returned; and returning at once, he said the Emperor desired to be shown that automaton which I had in my carriage (for he had seen it through the window when I was carried up to the castle by carriage); so I, having it soon brought from the carriage by my son, handed it to Barvitius, and showed how to demonstrate its use to the Emperor. When he had done so and returned to me, he said the Emperor had said that he had one or two of the kind, none of which, however, was so large nor made quite on that principle; that one of his Astronomers could make him something in imitation of this, but that he did not wish me to be deprived of it. And Barvitius added besides that the Emperor was affected with singular clemency and grace toward me, and that, the business being briefly referred to the councillors, he wished to have it arranged with me about a salary to be paid yearly and a suitable lodging. Meanwhile they should order that I have my Gynaeceum — which he had understood I had left at Dresden — brought up to me at Prague; and that His Imperial Majesty would so provide for us all that nothing would be lacking for living comfortably. Wherefore the very next day I sent my son Tycho, together with Claus Mule, to Dresden to bring the Gynaeceum, which after eight days came to Prague. Meanwhile there was dealing with me about the salary, through the same Lord Barvitius, and over several following days too, at intervals — since he is most busy, and some of the Senators do not (as it happens) hearken quite quickly enough to the Imperial will, and everything in that court proceeds slowly, on account of the magnitude and abundance of affairs. Barvitius desiring, by the Emperor's order, that I set down how large my family is, and the sum of those required for the maintenance of the Art, and reduce the magnitude of all these into a \"taxa\" (as they say), I did the first gladly; but the second I most humbly committed to His Imperial Majesty's most clement will and free judgment. Which, a few days later, most kindly assigned me 3,000 gold pieces in a certain fixed money, and besides certain other perquisites [lacuna] for the household and other things, which, if reckoned, exceed 1,000. These were carried through by Senate decree, some readily approving it, but more, as is wont, somewhat resisting, because none of those who had served the Emperor for several years had so large a salary. Yet the Emperor's [will] prevailed [...] urged that it be so; and when he had had this signified to me through the aforesaid Lord Barvitius, he ordered it said besides that, if these did not suffice me, and I should desire anything more hereafter, neither was his hand shortened, nor my mouth stopped — which Barvitius reported to me in these very words. Nay, he most clemently and not unwillingly promised a fief, hereditary according to the constitution of the Empire, that provision might be made for my children and family after me, as soon as any should fall vacant in his dominions or in the Empire that would be convenient for me and mine; concerning which, too, I shall shortly receive letters, which they call promissory. And since the Emperor understood that I did not much desire the Curtius houses at Prague (for I had my reasons — both that I did not wish the widow who still inhabits them to move out on my account, and that I should be exposed, especially in so great a city, near the castle, to too many visitations), he nonetheless most clemently promised me another suitable lodging in the city, which, when I should come there, whether summoned or of my own accord, I could conveniently inhabit with my people, and live at my own table at less expense; though perhaps I shall in time get that Curtius house, when the widow has of her own accord moved out — who now, as I hear, seeks new nuptials, in the manner of young widows. The Emperor ordered besides — over and above that my salary should begin from the time when I was summoned to Prague by His Imperial Majesty, even though I did not arrive until much time had elapsed — that 2,000 florins be given me at once by way of honorarium for my entry, which I received. Barvitius also once said to me, among other things, that, had it been that I had come here to the Emperor ten years ago, when everything was better and readier and not so subject to difficulties as now, on account of the war in Hungary, the Emperor could then have shown himself much easier and more liberal toward me; and when he intimated this once and again, I indeed replied that there was as yet no danger: that the Emperor had almost always been superior to the Turk, and that God would hereafter protect his Christendom. But silently I thought to myself that no one grieved more than I that I had not left the fatherland ten years ago, immediately upon the death of King Frederik of most praiseworthy memory, and betaken myself here — which I would have done then, had not letters from the whole Senate [lacuna], to my great inconvenience and the greatest loss of my household estate, [lacuna] me [lacuna] — but of these we shall deal elsewhere — [and] that it is not just but that some compensation be made me for such great expenses. These things transacted, by a certain imperial Captain, His Majesty so ordering, I was conducted to the castles disposed near Prague, that I might choose from them one more convenient for me and my studies. [It was named lacuna] and others; but since [lacuna], together with the neighbouring towns [lacuna], and a third set on a height, which the Emperor [lacuna] would [not?] approach, [a castle] which had once been the Bohemian Court-Master's, and the Emperor according to the [lacuna] of law [lacuna], Lord Barvitius dissuaded the survey of them. But those which [lacuna] are a day's or even half a day's journey from Prague — of which the first is called Brandeis, and is the chief imperial castle situated in the vicinity, being only three miles distant from Prague, into which [the Emperor] sometimes [betakes himself] for the sake of recreation [and] has the chief hunting grounds all round; the second is called Lissa, also [lacuna], four miles distant from the city; the third is named Benátky, which [lacuna] at the beginning of this year [was acquired] from [the heirs] of a certain [Rudský?], worn out by age and deceased [lacuna], leaving fifteen [lacuna]; for they, not sufficiently in accord, could not otherwise divide the inheritance among themselves, but of their own accord sold these goods to the Emperor. Having surveyed these three castles, then, the Captain conducting [me], and being kindly and honourably received in each, I preferred Benátky, six hours' journey (for the road is level) removed from Prague — which, on account of the pleasantness of the place and the little river Iser flowing around it and afterwards discharging into the Elbe, sounds in the Bohemians' vernacular tongue as if you said \"the Venice of the Bohemians\"; and it is situated on a high and sunny spot, with a most delightful prospect, with a small town adjoining, in which the religion they call Evangelical prevails — though very many of the Picards, who approach the Calvinist [persuasion] (which the Sectaries call orthodox), live in it. When the Emperor had understood that I chose this castle before the rest, he soon had me introduced here through the aforesaid Captain, who governs Brandeis, where I now dwell with my family, comfortably and peaceably (which I take as accepted of God), alone, no one making noise or disturbing, but the said Captain, by the Emperor's will, gladly performing all offices for me. For which cause too, within a week or two, he hastens here from Brandeis, to be at hand for me in what is required. The Emperor also ordered the same Captain promptly to procure everything necessary for an observatory suitable for setting up all my instruments duly, that everything might be fitly built before winter according to my plan; and likewise that he have a laboratory made for me in a suitable place, in whatever manner I should wish, and pay for everything else I should wish made by the mechanics. Had these things befallen me in my fatherland, what expenses I should have spared — for which no gratitude now remains to me, but rather everything [is] thankless, [to have done] nothing graciously. His Imperial Majesty offered besides, of his own accord, that he would build in his territory and at his own expense a new Uraniborg castle, which should not yield to that Danish one, but exceed it by many parasangs. For His Imperial Majesty delights greatly not only in that divine Astronomy and the mathematical studies, but also in architectural structures, which flow from Geometry and Perspective. And when His Majesty had carefully read the Books most humbly offered to him by me (of which I mentioned before), so much so that in the first days he was occupied with them far into the night (Barvitius reporting it) — for they were not so despised with him as in your shores — he inquired of me much about their contents through the same Barvitius, and the structure of Uraniborg drawn in the first volume pleased him greatly. But since he could not so quickly find the laboratory in it, he wished it indicated to him. All the rest pleased [him], so that Lord Barvitius reported to me that they were most welcome to the Emperor.</p><p>From what I have now recounted, most loving Holger, you have understood the state of my affairs, such as it now is. And I give thanks to God propitious, that he has so provided for me and mine, that I have been able to struggle out of those adversities which I long and much suffered in the fatherland, and to provide better for me and mine; and I hope hereafter, the same Divine Power favouring, for still more prosperous things. As for the political honours and dignities offered to me — nay, assigned even before I came to Prague, while I was still at Wittenberg (as you have perhaps understood either by word of mouth from Doctor Frantzius, son-in-law of Doctor Vitus Winshemius — for he said that, about to go off into East Frisia, he wished to visit you — or by letter, as he said he had received this from Lord Coraduz the Vice-Chancellor, when he was at Prague, to report to me, that I might come the more readily) — I wish to add nothing now. Certain friends at Prague urged me to undertake such things, which, however, I have always looked down on from on high. But if I am going to do it, it will be for the sake of your countrymen, who have held me in such insolent contempt; which I also said to our Thaddaeus when he pressed me. But he subjoined thus: You will admit it not only for the sake of your Danes, but also for the sake of our Bohemians — and indeed in the court itself some are of so dull and rustic a wit, and no less than your countrymen are ignorant how to discern between gold and brass; yet some are found who judge more rightly, and who, although they (as much as you) reckon political honours of small account in respect of virtue and exceptional learning, yet think it should be so done, for the sake of the common judgment, that one may live the more conveniently in the world; who, though few are found, yet prevail in judgment before the rest, and are most loving toward you — some of whom you have known thus far from this first meeting, some will become more fully known to you hereafter. Thus he. But of these I shall look later, if I do not altogether look down on them; for nothing in such matters can be done advisedly and discreetly before the return of the Magnificent Lord Coraduz the Vice-Chancellor from Rome, where he acts as Legate in the Emperor's name before the Supreme Pontiff, [and] who is expected about Michaelmas. Nor do I wish anything to be concluded in such matters beforehand. He is much favouring toward me, though we are as yet unknown by face, and most loving (as is said) of mathematical things too, in this a rival of his predecessor Curtius.</p><p>You now have, dearest Holger, the little history of my affairs, since I migrated with my people from Germany into Bohemia; of what was done before, and my departure from Denmark, you know well everything, so that there is no need to repeat what the mind, shuddering, shrinks from rehearsing. And certainly I seem to have written a little history rather than a letter, while, the pen slipping, I address you more fully, that you may be more fully informed of my affairs. Which prolixity, however, I persuade myself will not be unwelcome to you, since you do not, like others who wish to be held Nobles, disdain humane letters, and even huge volumes of books. But about the books promised you (that I may at last subjoin something), take it thus. I am not unmindful of the pact, nor hard to keep it; but since all the copies of the <i>Mechanics of Astronomy</i>, as many as are adorned with colours, or, as they call it, illuminated [lacuna] to be steeped in their colours at Prague — which, when it is done, you will obtain not only this first volume, but also the second, on the restitution of the fixed [stars], and likewise the third, on the corrected motion of the luminaries (which last, however, must be newly copied out from the first conception, since I have no clean copy at hand, after I offered the one written out at Wittenberg to His Imperial Majesty along with the rest). Be not, therefore, impatient of this delay, my Holger; you will have both these and others of mine, the Power favouring, as soon as it can be done, and not without interest. Meanwhile, leave to our sister Sophie those which I had destined for the King (for I have understood that she can obtain these, repudiated and given to Ramel) — which also does not displease me; for I prefer it so done, than that they should come into the hands of enviers and haters.</p><p>It remains that I stay my pen, unless it pleased me to add this one thing. The voluntary resignation of the island of Hven, which I inhabited there, which you advised (about which too I [said] I would write back more fully when I came here) — my mind is by no means to approve it, those also dissuading who are not perfunctorily versed in political matters; nay, [I am told] it would be imputed to me as a fault by some, as though out of contumacious insolence, after I had crossed over to the imperial side, I did this — as if I neglected my Danes, and what I had there, with a stubborn mind. But if those who [lacuna] are in charge should wish to take the island from me, on the ground that I betook myself to the Emperor, His Imperial Majesty would either take pains that the King of Denmark suffer no loss to be inflicted on me for his sake, or would compensate it, whatever it be, in another way. Let your countrymen object whatever they will, the answer will be easy, if only there be a place for justice and truth; but if not, let it pass with the rest, and let the just Judge decide all. And now, with your wife, my beloved niece, fare most happily, most upright Holger — she being greeted together with this most noble and honourable Mother of yours, and at the same time other friends, if any in your Cimbria still survive mindful of me, of whom I should believe at least some still remain. And again and oftener, fare most well, and make me, at the very first opportunity, fully informed of your affairs too.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle Benátky, the 9th of September, New Style, the Year of the Lord 1599.<br>Wholly yours,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p><p><b>[Postscript, continued.]</b></p><p>There is no leisure to read it over again; you yourself must look it over.</p><p>When these words had scarcely been set down, the Captain of Brandeis, who was dining here with me, was summoned to Prague to the Emperor; and after he had been there for ten days, having returned to me, he related that he had been summoned there solely on my account — so that, in the Emperor's absence (who was daily making ready to depart from Prague, on account of the plague and dysentery growing strong there, toward Linz), he might render me whatever services [I needed], and procure whatever I should desire, whether for building works or other things; and moreover indicate that whatever in my affairs could not be so quickly dispatched because of the Emperor's hurried departure should be completed afterward, once the Emperor had come to a quieter place. Meanwhile, however, while he was there, the Emperor, having learned that he had received from me as a gift some antidote against these epidemic diseases, demanded it from him through Lord Barvicius and kept it for himself, and gave orders that I should be written to, that I myself should instruct him in writing how it is prepared. Accordingly, in a letter given forthwith in Latin to His Imperial Majesty (of which you have here the substance), I most humbly set out what His Most Clement Majesty had desired. So you too have the description of the remedy devised by me and put to use against the present epidemic plague. Another copy I have shared with my sister Sophia, to be disclosed to her by you alone — which I also noted in the letter sent to her; otherwise keep it a secret on both sides. You see, moreover, from this how confidently — indeed almost familiarly — I have dared to write to His Imperial Majesty himself, and under seal too, so that none of the Councillors might open it […]</p><p>[…] to [His] Most Clement Majesty […] does/acts […] than that I or mine, in the Fatherland, […] be said, and on behalf of my letters and my own […] in some quantity, of the kind [found] in the mountains […] I shall draw out by the Spagyric art, not so […] Prague, with the Councillors, and the whole Court is said to have been moved […]. Certain persons […], having first been examined at Prague, […] have been subjected. They say nevertheless […] seeks peace; but it seems […] to fasten arms and wars upon the sterns [uncertain sense — perhaps 'to bring the war home,' a nautical figure] […], especially for the German Princes […] the hero of Wallachia, steadfast and brave, […] to the Cardinal; to whom Sigismund [Báthory] has [ceded/appointed — verb lost in gap] […], because happy and steadfast […]. Meanwhile, as I write these things and others […] your and my Cramer, touching upon […] you see from the enclosed copy […] and not only for you, on account of long-standing [acquaintance] […] I would write more fully, since […] in my theses, how Duncan […] wrote to me on this matter in […] letters, some things which he knows better [but] conceals […]. <i>Hand kand faa mere enanden […] bort</i> ('he can get more from one another […] away' — Danish, the sense broken by the gap). Meanwhile let him consider that […] he never did, but through you […] and by so long-standing a familiarity […] he keeps silent. And now again, and as […]</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>); orig. in <i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>. MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (a copy). The witness is defective at several points (lacunae).</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"die 9. Septembris Stylo novo, Anni Domini 1599,\"</i> from the imperial castle Benátky — explicitly <b>New Style</b>, confirmed by the label. Written <b>nine days before</b> the autobiographical <i>relatio</i> to Vedel (brahe-t_15990918001, 18 Sep 1599), to which this is the close — and in the audience-narrative far more detailed — companion.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (of Rosenholm), Tycho's kinsman (cf. brahe-t_15980810001, brahe-t_15980820001). The closing <b>\"with your wife, my beloved niece\"</b> confirms that Holger's wife was <b>Sophie Brahe, daughter of Tycho's brother Axel Brahe</b> — i.e. Tycho's niece. This retro-confirms the <i>Sophia</i> marriage-pun in <b>brahe-t_15980810001</b> (there flagged MEDIUM): the <i>\"living Sophia\"</i> Tycho urged on Holger is his niece Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe.</li><li><b>Why this letter matters — the eyewitness audience with Rudolf II.</b> This is <b>the most circumstantial first-person account in the corpus of Tycho's reception at the imperial court</b>, supplementing the Vedel <i>relatio</i> with step-by-step detail:</li><li><b>Approach.</b> Met by <b>Barvitius</b> in a garden by the castle; the three books + the Cologne/Mecklenburg commendatory letters to be received \"from no hand but Tycho's own\"; the letter from <b>Duke Otto of Brunswick-Harburg</b> to <b>Rumpf</b> (Master of the Court), whom Tycho visits — Rumpf marvels the King let Tycho go (\"attributing it to the King's youth, preferring Mars to Art\"), and concludes that those \"second to the King\" must be \"ignorant of the good arts, or malevolent and envious.\" A friend later reveals Barvitius engineered Tycho's castle visit <b>so the Emperor could observe him in advance from the opposite chamber</b>.</li><li><b>The private audience.</b> The planned solemn introduction by Rumpf is dropped; Tycho <b>enters alone</b> to find Rudolf II <b>alone, leaning on a table, not even a serving-boy present</b>; the Emperor beckons with a nod, extends his hand; Tycho gives a Latin oration; presents the princely letters (opened in his presence) and the three books (fetched from son Tycho in the antechamber), with a summary of each; the Emperor replies \"more copiously than I addressed him,\" smiling — <i>\"but I could not catch all the words, so softly does he speak by nature.\"</i> Afterward the Emperor asks to see the <b>automaton/clock</b> in Tycho's carriage (glimpsed through a window).</li><li><b>Salary &amp; fief.</b> Via Barvitius over several days: <b>3,000 gold + &gt;1,000 in perquisites</b>, by Senate decree over the objection of long-serving courtiers; the Emperor's assurance <i>\"neither is his hand shortened, nor your mouth stopped\"</i>; a <b>hereditary fief</b> promised (promissory letters to come); <b>2,000 florins entry-honorarium</b>; salary <b>backdated</b> to the summons.</li><li><b>Lodging &amp; castle.</b> Declines the <b>Curtius house</b> (Italian-built by the late Chancellor <b>Jacob Curtius</b>, &gt;20,000 joachims, with an observation tower \"scarcely big enough for one instrument\"; a widow lives there, \"now seeking new nuptials in the manner of young widows\"); is shown three castles — <b>Brandeis</b> (3 mi., chief imperial seat, prime hunting), <b>Lissa</b> (4 mi.), <b>Benátky</b> (6 hrs)— and chooses <b>Benátky</b>, glossed (as in the Kepler letter) as \"the <b>Venice of the Bohemians</b>\" (the river <b>Iser/Jizera</b> ringing it before it joins the Elbe), in a town where the \"Evangelical\" faith and many <b>Picards</b> (Bohemian Brethren) live.</li><li><b>New Uraniborg.</b> The Emperor's spontaneous offer to build, at his own cost, \"a new Uraniborg castle exceeding the Danish one by many parasangs,\" he himself delighting in astronomy, mathematics, and architecture; he read Tycho's books \"far into the night,\" and especially liked \"the structure of Uraniborg drawn in the first volume.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Thaddaeus</b> = <b>Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (Thaddaeus Hagecius)</b>, the Bohemian imperial physician-astronomer and Tycho's Prague conduit; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Johann Barvitius</b> (supreme Secretary); <b>Johann Löser</b> (host at Bretzia castle, en route); <b>Duke Otto</b> of Brunswick-Harburg; <b>Wolfgang Rumpf</b> (<i>Rumphius</i>, Master of the Court); <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (former Imperial Chancellor, builder of the Curtius house); the <b>widow</b> there; <b>Coraduz</b> (<i>Corraducius</i>, Vice-Chancellor, then Legate to the Pope at <b>Rome</b>, expected back by Michaelmas — \"a rival of his predecessor Curtius\" in loving mathematics); the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>; <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>Claudius Mulaeus</i>); <b>Dr. Frantzius</b>, son-in-law of <b>Dr. Vitus Winshemius (Veit Winsheim)</b>, the conduit for the \"political honours\" offer; the <b>Elector of Cologne</b> (Ernst of Bavaria) and <b>Duke of Mecklenburg</b> (Ulrich III); <b>King Christian IV</b> and <b>Frederik II</b>; <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (\"our sister\") and <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (the King's repudiated books to pass to Sophie via Ramel); Holger's <b>wife</b> (Tycho's niece) and <b>mother</b>.</li><li><b>The three books.</b> Tycho's gift-set, here itemised: (1) the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</i> (the illuminated/coloured copies to be finished at Prague); (2) the volume \"on the restitution of the fixed stars\" (the <i>Progymnasmata</i> star-catalogue material); (3) \"on the corrected motion of the luminaries\" (the lunar/solar theory) — the last to be <b>re-copied from the first draft</b>, since the Wittenberg fair copy went to the Emperor.</li><li><b>Latin tags &amp; allusions.</b> <i>\"Venetiae Bohemorum\"</i> — \"Venice of the Bohemians\" (Benátky ≈ Czech for Venice; cf. brahe-t_15991209001). <i>\"nec sibi esse accurtatam manum, nec mihi obturatum os\"</i> — the Emperor's assurance (echo of <b>Isaiah 59:1</b>; also in the Vedel <i>relatio</i>). <i>\"quae meminisse animus horrens iterare refugit\"</i> — echo of <b>Virgil</b>, <i>Aeneid</i> 2.12 (<i>animus meminisse horret</i>). <i>\"multis parasangis excederet\"</i> — \"exceed by many parasangs,\" i.e. by far. <i>\"discernere inter aurum et orichalcum\"</i> — to tell gold from brass/copper-ore (true worth from false). <i>\"more iuvencularum viduarum\"</i> — \"in the manner of young widows.\" <i>P. M.</i> = <i>Pontifex Maximus</i> (the Pope). <i>\"Picardis\"</i> — the <b>Picards</b>, a pejorative name for the <b>Bohemian Brethren</b> (Unitas Fratrum).</li><li><b>Lacunae (the crux).</b> The witness is defective chiefly in: (a) the perquisites figure (<i>\"accidentaria …\"</i>); (b) <i>\"Vicit tamen Caesaris …ci ut sic fieret ursit\"</i>; (c) the Senate-letters / compensation passage (<i>\"nisi literae a toto Senatu … me …\"</i>); and most heavily (d) the <b>castle-tour passage</b> — the naming of the rejected castles, and the provenance of Benátky (acquired by the Emperor at the start of 1599 from the discordant heirs of a deceased lord, <i>\"a …ruvij cuiusdam senio confecti et defuncti\"</i> — the name reads like <b>Rudský / Rožmberk?</b>, irrecoverable here); and (e) the Mechanica-colouring clause and (f) <i>\"ij, qui … praesunt\"</i> (those in charge of Hven). All are reproduced as dots in the source and marked <b>[lacuna]</b> in the translation; surrounding sense is given, exact wording is not.</li><li><b>Postscript, badly damaged.</b> The recovered segment is the closing postscript of Tycho's letter to his nephew-in-law Holger (\"Oliger\") Rosenkrantz, written from the imperial castle at Benátky nad Jizerou (Tycho's residence outside Prague), dated 9 September 1599 N.S. The first half is intact and coherent; the second half (from \"Vides insuper...\" on) survives only in fragments separated by lacunae that break individual clauses, not just paragraph joins — consistent with damage or loss in the underlying source rather than a simple OCR gap.</li><li><b>The Captain of Brandeis and imperial favor.</b> The unnamed \"Capitaneus Brandeisensis\" is the castellan of Brandýs nad Labem, the crown castle that would later become Tycho's own Bohemian residence (the same title recurs elsewhere in Tycho's 1599 correspondence — Tycho Brahes Breve, corpus). Here he acts as Rudolf II's personal intermediary to Tycho during the Emperor's flight from a plague/dysentery outbreak in Prague toward Linz, empowered to \"procure whatever [Tycho] should desire\" for building works and other needs.</li><li><b>The plague antidote and Barvicius.</b> Rudolf II, on learning that Tycho had given away an antidote against the \"epidemic diseases\" then raging, had it reclaimed for himself through Jan Barvitius — Rudolf's supreme secretary and privy councillor, and Tycho's regular high-level contact at court (cf. Tycho's own later letter of 7 May 1600 addressed to \"IOHANNI BARVITIO, Sacrae Caesareae Maiestatis Secretario supremo\", preserved in the register of Tycho's correspondence). Tycho reports writing directly and confidentially to the Emperor himself, under seal, \"so that none of the Councillors might open it\" — a striking index of his private access to Rudolf — and says he sent a second copy of the antidote recipe to his sister Sophie Brahe, \"to be disclosed to her by you [Holger] alone.\"</li><li><b>Imperial court in flight; war news from the east (uncertain reconstruction).</b> The fragmentary remainder appears to report the Emperor's court \"moved/transferred\" away from plague-stricken Prague, and news of fighting involving \"the hero of Wallachia\" and a \"Cardinal\" to whom \"Sigismund\" is said to have ceded or appointed something. If the surviving words are read rightly, this most plausibly garbles the Transylvanian crisis of 1599 — Sigismund Báthory's cession of Transylvania to his cousin Cardinal Andrew Báthory, and the resistance of Michael the Brave, voivode of Wallachia (who defeated and killed the Cardinal that autumn). This identification is the translator's own historical inference from the surviving fragments, not corpus-verified, and should be checked against a specialist account of the Long Turkish War before being asserted as fact.</li><li><b>Named associates: Cramer and Duncan.</b> Two further individuals surface in the ruined final third — \"Cramerum tuum meumque\" (a \"Cramer\" known to both Tycho and Holger) and \"Duncan,\" almost certainly Duncan Liddel, the Scottish mathematician-physician who studied under Tycho and later taught at Rostock/Helmstedt, well attested elsewhere in Tycho's circle (Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i>; Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i>; Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i> — all corpus) — but the surrounding clauses are too damaged to recover what is being said about either man.</li><li><b>A Danish fragment.</b> Embedded in the Latin is one broken sentence of Danish, which cannot be reconstructed across the lacuna and is rendered here as literally as the surviving words allow.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected from \"more than 20,000 joachims\" to \"more than 20 joachims\", matching both the house Latin transcription and Dreyer's printed text (TBOO VIII, p. 164, line 37) — a thousandfold translation error.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [C]</b> — contains flagged cruxes. The Latin is rendered securely and the letter's substance — the journey from Wittenberg, the Barvitius/Rumpf approach, the <b>eyewitness private audience with Rudolf II</b>, the salary/fief/honorarium, the Curtius-house refusal and the <b>Benátky</b> choice, the new-Uraniborg offer, the political-honours question (deferred to Coraduz's return), the book-gifts to Holger, and the refusal to resign Hven — is clear, important, and cross-corroborated by the Vedel <i>relatio</i> (15990918001) and the Krag/Sophie/Eske March-1600 cluster. The grade is set to <b>[C]</b> by the <b>multiple witness lacunae</b>, concentrated in the castle-tour passage (including the unrecoverable name of Benátky's prior lord); secondary points (the precise perquisites sum; the identity of <b>Johann Löser</b> and <b>Dr. Frantzius</b>) are flagged, not smoothed.</p><p>&gt; <b>§1 collation note (2026-06-02).</b> Collated against Dreyer, <i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 168 (+ vol. IX apparatus p. 182): the castle-tour lacunae are reproduced identically; Friis marks them as lacunae (one, v. 20, marked by the codex itself, <i>addens verba \"Post aliqua\"</i>) — irrecoverable. Dreyer's running header also gives the double date <b>O.S. 30 Aug. = N.S. 9 Sept. 1599</b>. [C] stands.</p><p>The opening two-thirds of the postscript, through the sentence on the sealed letter to the Emperor, is linguistically clear and translated with confidence. From \"Vides insuper...\" onward the recovered text is riddled with lacunae that break individual clauses; I have translated only the surviving word-groups and marked every gap with […], adding no bridging language beyond what is explicitly bracketed as conjectural. The historical identification of the Wallachia/Báthory/Cardinal material is my own contextual inference, not sourced from the corpus, and needs verification against the manuscript or a specialist history before being treated as established; the identities of \"Cramer\" and the Danish sentence remain wholly unresolved. Grade C is warranted by the genuine, extensive, unresolved corruption in the second half — this is not a collation question but a real textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.162-172 in full via the page images (slice pages 1-12) and collated the opening, closing, and every proper name, number, date, sum of money, and place-name against both source_text and the Dreyer print. Confirmed the flagged defect: Dreyer prints '20 Ioachymicorum', not 20,000 — the published English's '20,000 joachims' is a threefold-zero inflation with no counterpart in either source_text or Dreyer. All other numbers (3000 aureorum, 1000, 2000 florenorum, three/four miles, six hours, ten days, eight days, quindecim/fifteen, two decennium references, the 9 September 1599 date) and all proper names (Rosenkrantz, Thaddaeus, Löser, Barvitius, Rumpf, Duke Otto, Jacob Curtius, Coraduz/Corraducius, Frantzius, Vitus Winshemius, Claus Mule/Claudius Mulaeus, Friderich, Sophie, Ramel, Brandeis, Lissa, Benátky/Benachia, Cramer, Duncan, Sigismund Báthory) check out against the Dreyer print. Bracketed lacuna-supplements in the badly mutilated closing postscript (pp.171-172) are properly marked as editorial and are not counted as defects.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). Both our source_text and Dreyer's printed page read '20 Ioachymicorum' (20 Joachimsthalers) for the cost of building and gardening the Curtius house; the English inflates this by three orders of magnitude to '20,000 joachims' with no textual warrant. At: “at a cost of more than 20,000 joachims”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII, p. 164), which prints 'expenfis maioribus quam 20 Ioachymicorum'; the house Latin transcription already agrees with Dreyer at '20 Ioachymicorum', so only the English required repair. No other defects were flagged for this letter's opening pages (162-165), which were also reviewed in the course of locating the defect site.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 21, "prev": "brahe-t_15990507001", "next": "brahe-t_16000803001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Recounts the journey from Wittenberg to Bohemia, detailing delays caused by a daughter's illness, the completion of a book for the Emperor, and hospitality received en route.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990909001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990917001", "d": "1599-09-17", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 17 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Bavarian chancellor <b>Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. The <b>earlier</b> of the two Herwart letters in the corpus (cf. brahe-t_16000911001, Sept 1600). A substantive scientific reply on Tycho's <b>lunar theory</b>.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHONI BRAHE IOAN: GEORG HERWART AB HOHENBURG.</p><p>LITERAS tuas, vir nobiliss. atque mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, Benachiae ultima mensis novissime elapsi ad me datas, unâ cum adiuncto deliquij lunaris calculo et systemate probe accepi, et in primis magnopere sum laetatus, sacram Caes. Maiest. id quod fore animus mihi iam antea praesagiebat, tibi tuisque studijs pro dignitate et meritis prospexisse, atque eius nomine mihi quoque subinde gratulor, quod hac ratione et amicitia tua, uti plane consido, frui et forte aliquando te quoque convenire et coram aliqua tecum conferre queam. De patria vero tua quod te sic amiserit, non miror. Quis enim unquam vel Propheta acceptus in patria?</p><p>Quae scribis de Veneris stella, quae aliquando praeter solitum non comparuisse traditur, perplacuere, et gratias ago tibi magnas, quod magno me dubio liberaveris. Cum enim mihi persuaderem, posse Veneris stellam, subeunte stella Mercurij, Eclipsin pati, atque etiam ex calculo Prutenico eo ipso tempore ☌ ♀ et ☿ matutinam, paulo ante ortum Solis talem, in qua ☿ infra ♀ esset, animadvertissem, incertus animi eram, quid hac de re foret statuendum. Caeterum rationibus tuis praegnantibus omne mihi dubium ex animo exemisti.</p><p>Illa porro, quae de motibus luminarium et eorundem defectibus subiunxisti, fuerunt mihi lectu iucundissima. Immensum vero quantum gavisus fuerim, cum ex hypothesi tua Lunae, adeoque tota illius theoria et systemate animadverterem duplicem illam variationem, tam semimenstruam ex duplici motu ☾ a ☉, quam annuam illam ex distantia ☉ aut ☊ dependentem atque nodorum quam innuis reciprocationem tandem aliquando tuo Marte innotuisse, quae simul unâ pariterque et veteribus et recentioribus, imo vero vetustissimis etiam et novissimis quibusdam authoribus de ipsa Lunâ, sidere infimo et cuius observatio omnium facillima videri queat, incognita fuerant. Neque iam dubito, quin ex his tuis fundamentis, non modo recentiora, verum etiam vetustissima quaeque φαινόμενα exactius haberi queant. Eandem enim utique, vel saltem mutatis mutandis consimilem olim habuit rationem motus Lunae, quam qualemque tu modo accuratissimis tuis rectificatissimorum, quoad eius fieri potest, instrumentorum observationibus eruisti.</p><p>Nec me movent observationes illae a PTOLOMAeO et veteribus habitae, cum plus satis constet, quam crassa Minerva veteres illi artifices astronomicas observationes tractarint, destituti non modo idoneis instrumentis, verum etiam ut plurimum calculi quoque praecisa ratione, id quod ARISTARCHI Samij, AUTOLYCI et HIPPARCHI Bithynij libelli, qui ad nos pervenere, cuivis facile fidem facient, plerumque enim ἐν πλάτει proportionem aliquam forte harmonicam, vel numerum aliquem rotundum, qui non multum a vero discrepare videbatur, quaesiverunt, eoque contenti pauca quaedam minuta nihili aestimarunt, imo vel unius gradus quadrantem non magni fecerunt. Ipse quoque PTOLOMAeUS quoad latitudinem Lunae, de qua scribis, cum acquieverit ERATOSTHENIS invento, quo is solstitialium punctorum distantiam ad integrum circulum se habere (ἔγγιστα) ut 11 ad 83 statuerat, consequenter necessario observationis ea ratione, qua usus est, ob assumptam a se nimiam istam Zodiaci obliquitatem, minorem iusto latitudinem Lunae inferre debuit. Quodsi, ut credi par est, PTOLOMAeI temporis obliquitas Zodiaci tanta non omnino fuerat, quantam ex praefata ERATOSTHENIS opinione et HIPPARCHUS et PTOLOMAeUS eam admiserunt, ex ipsissima PTOLOMAeI observatione inferri potest, maiorem etiam tum fuisse ☾ in quadraturis latitudinem, quam eam quae numero rotundo quinque graduum a PTOLOMAeO proditur.</p><p>Inaudivi quoque libenter, te tabulas tuas de motu luminarium, seorsim typis excusas, caeteris Planetis praemittere statuisse. Spero enim hac ratione futurum, ut tanto citius ad nos perveniant. Et forte longe omnium circumspectissime ageres, vel saltem desiderijs plurimorum affatim satisfaceres, sitimque nostram aliqua ex parte restingueres, si solas, nudas, puras, putas tabulas de motibus luminarium, instar Prutenicarum, sine omni demonstratione, praemitteres atque maturares. Musae profecto meae te supplices orant, obsecrant, obtestantur, ut quanto citius tuo beneficio Eclipses luminarium exactius supputari possint, id quod ex nudis tabulis fieret quam commodissime, atque interea temporis exemplo discipulorum magni illius PYTHAGORAe merito atque iure optimo, τὸ αὐτός ἔφα rationis loco allegaremus, donec apparatum istum observationum atque demonstrationum tuarum concinnares.</p><p>Communicasti iam mihi, quod cum maxima gratiarum actione refero, totam rationem Systematis tui Lunaris, adeo ut praeter epochas mediorum motuum atque diurnum cuiusvis medium motum quoad Lunam vix aliquid in residuo esse videatur. Quod si tantundem in Sole, aequatione temporis etc. fieret, posset iam quis per multiplicationes et resolutiones triangulorum absque tabulis ulterioribus calculum Eclipsium absolvere. Id propterea refero, quia multiplicationes et triangulorum resolutiones, quae vicem tabularum mediorum motuum et aequationum supplere possunt, ego parum moror. Soleo enim sine multiplicationis ambagibus, solius additionis ope, numerum ex multiplicatione producendum facillimo tramite invenire, et consimiliter sine omni divisionis labore atque taedio sola subtractionis ope quotientem in quovis genere calculi, tam vulgari, quam sexagenario, reperire, atque ob id sine fastidio et temporis tractu triangula quaeque, etsi radius instar VALENTINI OTHONIS sit maximus, facile resolvere. Hoc autem unicum unice exopto, ut Eclipses aliquas luminarium vetustas ex datis et placitis magni illius TYCHONIS BRAHE supputare queam. Quod si ulla ratione vel lege vel conditione hoc ipsum impetrare potero, ut Eclipses aliquas veteres, quas ab alijs hactenus nec animadversas nec supputatas collegi, calculo tuo exactissimo subijcere, et ad te Matheseos ARISTARCHUM deferre valeam, per Deum summum Opt. Max. in omnem eventum, hoc chyrographo meo teste καρτερὸν ὅρκον sanctissime iuro, nemini me unquam quicquam de ijs communicaturum, quae ad huiusmodi calculum instituendum requiruntur. Atque utinam aliquo genere officiorum beneficium huiusmodi in me collatum rursus exaequare possem, pro viribus certe gratias referre conarer. Sed ignosce quaeso, si flagrans desiderij ardor, quo haec exopto, importuniora vota volenti nolenti exprimat. Quod si epidemica ista lues, quae loca inter nos intermedia et hospitia praesertim publica invasisse traditur, non me absterreret, ipse ad te iter instituerem, anne forte hoc pacto voti compos fieri possem.</p><p>Principes mei clementissimi, id enim scire desideras, studijs quidem hisce non admodum afficiuntur, attamen literas et literatos amant, ita ut sperem, munus illud tuum, de quo scribis, futurum illis esse gratum atque acceptum, mihi certe erit quam gratissimum. Habet serenissimus Princeps meus Bibliothecam instructissimam, Regijs sumptibus comparatam, et libris manuscriptis Hebraicis, Graecis etc. refertam. Mitto his iunctum indicem librorum mathematicorum, quem velim apud te retineas, ut si forte occurrat liber aliquis, quem ex ijs desideres, eius me certiorem reddere possis. Haec raptim, urgente tabellario. Vale feliciter, et me favore tuo prosequi perge. Quod si ulla in re tibi commodo vel usui esse queam, vel in his partibus, vel in ijs rebus, quae per has regiones ad te devehi possunt, iube et impera.</p><p>Data Monachij die 17. Septembris Anno 99.<br>Nobilissimae Dis Tuae studiosissimus<br>IOAN. GEORG. HERWART ab Hohenburg etc. manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe, Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg.</b></p><p>Your letter, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians, sent me from <b>Benátky</b> on the last [day] of the month just past, together with the appended <b>calculation and system of the lunar eclipse</b>, I duly received; and first of all I greatly rejoiced that the Sacred Imperial Majesty has provided for you and your studies according to your dignity and merits — which my mind already presaged would be; and on that account I congratulate myself too, that in this way I may, as I fully trust, enjoy your friendship, and perhaps sometime meet you too, and confer some things with you in person. That your fatherland has so lost you, I do not wonder. <b>For when was ever even a Prophet accepted in his fatherland?</b></p><p>What you write about <b>the star of Venus, which is said sometime, beyond the usual, not to have appeared</b>, much pleased me; and I give you great thanks for freeing me from a great doubt. For since I had persuaded myself that <b>the star of Venus could suffer an eclipse when the star of Mercury passes beneath it</b> — and had noticed, too, from the Prutenic calculation, at that very time a <b>morning conjunction [☌] of Venus [♀] and Mercury [☿]</b>, a little before sunrise, such that Mercury was below Venus — I was uncertain in mind what was to be concluded about this. But by your weighty reasons you have taken every doubt from my mind.</p><p>Those things, further, which you subjoined <b>about the motions of the luminaries and their eclipses</b>, were most delightful to me to read. But beyond measure did I rejoice when, from <b>your hypothesis of the Moon — and indeed from its whole theory and system — I noticed that double variation: both the semi-menstrual [twice-monthly], from the double motion of the Moon [☾] from the Sun [☉], and that annual one, depending on the distance of the Sun or of the node [☊]; and the reciprocation of the nodes which you hint — at last discovered by your own effort</b> [<i>tuo Marte</i>] — which had been unknown alike to ancients and to moderns, nay even to certain most ancient and most recent authors, about <b>the Moon itself, the lowest star, and whose observation might seem the easiest of all</b>. Nor do I now doubt that, from these foundations of yours, not only the more recent, but even the most ancient φαινόμενα [phenomena] can be had more exactly. For the motion of the Moon once had altogether the same reckoning — or at least a closely similar one, <i>mutatis mutandis</i> — as that which you have just now drawn out by your most accurate observations with [your] most exactly-rectified instruments.</p><p>Nor do those observations had by <b>Ptolemy</b> and the ancients move me, since it is more than established <b>how crudely [<i>crassa Minerva</i>] those ancient artificers handled astronomical observations</b> — destitute not only of suitable instruments, but for the most part also of a precise method of calculation; which the booklets of <b>Aristarchus of Samos, Autolycus, and Hipparchus of Bithynia</b> that have reached us will easily make credible to anyone. For mostly they sought, <b>broadly [ἐν πλάτει], some perhaps harmonic proportion, or some round number</b> which seemed not to differ much from the truth, and content with that, valued certain few minutes as nothing — nay, made not much even of a quarter of one degree. Ptolemy himself, too, as to <b>the Moon's latitude</b> of which you write — since he acquiesced in <b>Eratosthenes's finding</b>, by which he had set the distance of the solstitial points to the whole circle to be <b>(approximately [ἔγγιστα]) as 11 to 83</b> — consequently, by necessity, by the observation-method he used, on account of <b>that excessive obliquity of the Zodiac which he assumed</b>, had to infer a smaller-than-just latitude of the Moon. But if — as is fit to be believed — the obliquity of the Zodiac in Ptolemy's time was not altogether as great as Hipparchus and Ptolemy admitted it (from the aforesaid opinion of Eratosthenes), then from Ptolemy's very own observation it can be inferred that <b>the Moon's latitude in the quadratures was then greater, too, than the round 5 degrees reported by Ptolemy</b>.</p><p>I heard also with pleasure that you have resolved to <b>publish your tables of the motion of the luminaries separately, printed before the other Planets</b>. For I hope that in this way they will reach us so much the sooner. And perhaps you would act far the most circumspectly of all — or at least amply satisfy the desires of very many, and in some part slake our thirst — if you would <b>publish, and hasten, the tables of the motions of the luminaries alone, bare, pure, and unadorned, like the Prutenic [Tables], without all demonstration</b>. My Muses indeed, as suppliants, beg, beseech, and implore you, that the eclipses of the luminaries may, by your benefit, be calculated more exactly so much the sooner — which would be done most conveniently from the bare tables — and meanwhile, <b>by the example of the disciples of that great Pythagoras, we may, deservedly and by best right, allege \"τὸ αὐτός ἔφα\" [the \"he himself said it\"] in place of reason</b>, until you arrange that apparatus of your observations and demonstrations.</p><p>You have already communicated to me — which I report with the greatest giving of thanks — <b>the whole reckoning of your Lunar System</b>, so that, besides the epochs of the mean motions and the daily mean motion of any [body], hardly anything seems left over as to the Moon. And if as much were done for the Sun, the equation of time, etc., one could already, <b>by multiplications and resolutions of triangles, complete the calculation of Eclipses without further tables</b>. I report this because I care little for the multiplications and triangle-resolutions that can supply the place of tables of mean motions and equations. For <b>I am wont, without the circuits of multiplication, by the help of addition alone, to find by an easiest path the number to be produced by multiplication; and likewise, without all the labour and tedium of division, by the help of subtraction alone, to find the quotient — in any kind of calculus, decimal or sexagesimal</b> — and on that account, without distaste or expenditure of time, to resolve any triangles easily, even if the radius be as great as <b>Valentin Otho's</b>. But this one thing I singularly long for: <b>that I may calculate some ancient eclipses of the luminaries from the data and principles of that great Tycho Brahe.</b> And if by any means, law, or condition I can obtain this very thing — to subject some ancient eclipses (which I have collected, hitherto neither noticed nor calculated by others) to your most exact calculation, and to bring to you <b>the Aristarchus of Mathematics</b> — <b>I swear most sacredly, by God the supreme, Best and Greatest, on every event, this my handwriting witness, a strong oath [καρτερὸν ὅρκον], that I will never communicate to anyone anything of what is required to institute such a calculation.</b> And would that I could by some kind of service requite a benefit of this sort conferred on me; I would certainly try, to my strength, to render thanks. But pardon me, I pray, if the burning ardour of desire with which I long for these things wrings out, willy-nilly, importunate wishes. And if that epidemic plague — which is said to have invaded the places between us, and especially the public lodgings — did not deter me, <b>I would myself set out on a journey to you</b>, [to see] whether perhaps in this way I might attain my wish.</p><p><b>My most clement Princes</b> — for you desire to know this — are not much affected by these studies; yet they love letters and the learned, so that I hope <b>that gift of yours, of which you write, will be welcome and acceptable to them</b>; to me, certainly, it will be most welcome. My <b>most serene Prince has a most well-equipped Library</b>, procured at royal expense, and filled with <b>manuscript books — Hebrew, Greek, etc.</b> I send, joined to this, <b>an index of the mathematical books</b>, which I would have you keep with you, that if perchance some book occur which you desire from them, you may inform me of it. These [things] in haste, the courier urging. Fare happily, and continue to pursue me with your favour. And if in any matter I can be of advantage or use to you — whether in these parts, or in those things that can be conveyed to you through these regions — command and order.</p><p>Given at Munich, the 17th day of September, the year [15]99.<br>Most zealous of Your most noble Lordship,<br><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg etc.</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (Bavarian chancellor, Munich) → <b>Tycho</b>. The <b>earlier</b> Herwart letter (cf. the Kepler-on-Ursus transcript, brahe-t_16000911001, Sept 1600).</li><li><b>Significance — the reception of Tycho's lunar theory, and early prosthaphaeretic computation.</b></li><li><b>Tycho's lunar discoveries celebrated.</b> Herwart responds with delight to Tycho's lunar hypothesis (sent with a worked eclipse-calculation), naming Tycho's genuine novelties: <b>the \"variation\" (semi-menstrual, from the double Moon–Sun motion); the annual equation (depending on the Sun's/node's distance); and the reciprocation of the nodes</b> — \"discovered by your own effort,\" unknown to ancients and moderns alike. (The same lunar discoveries Tycho announced to Scaliger, brahe-t_16000723001.)</li><li><b>Critique of ancient data.</b> Herwart argues the ancients (<b>Ptolemy, Aristarchus, Autolycus, Hipparchus</b>) worked \"with crude Minerva,\" seeking round numbers \"broadly [ἐν πλάτει]\"; and gives a sharp technical argument that <b>Ptolemy's assumed (too-large) zodiac obliquity — via Eratosthenes's 11:83 ratio — forced too small a lunar latitude</b>, so the true quadrature-latitude exceeded Ptolemy's round 5°.</li><li><b>The plea for bare tables.</b> Herwart begs Tycho to <b>publish the solar/lunar tables alone — \"bare, pure, unadorned, like the Prutenic Tables, without demonstration\"</b> — first, so eclipses can be computed sooner; meanwhile he'll trust Tycho on \"ipse dixit\" (<i>τὸ αὐτός ἔφα</i>) authority. (A pointed contrast with <b>Scaliger's</b> rejection of <i>αὐτός ἔφα</i> in brahe-t_16000310001.)</li><li><b>Proto-logarithmic computation.</b> Herwart describes his computational shortcuts: <b>finding products \"by addition alone\" and quotients \"by subtraction alone,\" in decimal or sexagesimal</b> — i.e. <b>prosthaphaeresis</b> (the trigonometric product-to-sum method that preceded logarithms) — enabling him to resolve triangles even with <b>Valentin Otho's</b> very large radius (the <i>Opus Palatinum</i>). A notable datum in the pre-history of logarithms.</li><li><b>The ancient-eclipse project &amp; oath.</b> Herwart's \"one wish\": to compute <b>ancient eclipses he has collected (unnoticed by others) from Tycho's data</b> — calling Tycho \"<b>the Aristarchus of Mathematics</b>\" — and <b>swears a sacred oath (καρτερὸν ὅρκον) of total secrecy</b> about Tycho's methods. He would travel to Tycho but for the <b>plague</b> on the roads.</li><li><b>The Bavarian ducal library.</b> His <b>Princes (the Dukes of Bavaria)</b> love \"letters and the learned\"; Tycho's intended <b>gift</b> (the <i>Mechanica</i>) will be welcome; the ducal <b>Library</b> (Hebrew/Greek manuscripts) is offered — Herwart sends an <b>index of its mathematical books</b> for Tycho to mark desiderata.</li><li><b>The Venus-star thread.</b> Herwart had theorised that a reported <b>disappearance of Venus</b> was a <b>Mercury-occultation</b> (a Venus–Mercury morning conjunction with Mercury below, per the Prutenic Tables); Tycho's \"weighty reasons\" resolved his doubt. (Connects to <b>Scaliger's</b> debunking of the Venus-star chronology, brahe-t_16000310001, and the chronology disputes.)</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Route with the <b>lunar-theory</b> thread (brahe-t_16000723001/16000623001) and the <b>Venus-star/chronology</b> thread (brahe-t_16000310001, brahe-t_16000911001); a key document for the <b>reception of Tycho's lunar work</b> and <b>early prosthaphaeresis</b>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Herwart von Hohenburg; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Aristarchus of Samos, Autolycus, Hipparchus of Bithynia, Eratosthenes, Pythagoras</b>; <b>Valentin Otho</b>; the <b>Dukes of Bavaria</b> (Maximilian I / Wilhelm V). Places: <b>Munich</b>, <b>Benátky</b>.</li><li><b>Greek/terms reproduced.</b> φαινόμενα, ἐν πλάτει, ἔγγιστα, τὸ αὐτός ἔφα, καρτερὸν ὅρκον; <i>tuo Marte</i> (\"by your own effort\"), <i>crassa Minerva</i> (\"crudely\"). Glyphs ☌ ♀ ☿ ☉ ☾ ☊ reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Monachij die 17. Septembris Anno 99</i> (Bavaria = New Style) → <b>17 September 1599 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the celebration of <b>Tycho's lunar variation/annual-equation/nodal-reciprocation discoveries</b>; the <b>Ptolemy-obliquity / lunar-latitude argument</b> via Eratosthenes; the plea for <b>bare Prutenic-style tables</b> and the <i>αὐτός ἔφα</i> trust; Herwart's <b>addition-for-multiplication / subtraction-for-division prosthaphaeresis</b> with Otho's large radius; the <b>ancient-eclipse project and oath of secrecy</b>; the <b>Bavarian ducal library</b> offer; and the <b>Venus–Mercury occultation</b> thread) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. The technical and Greek content is rendered faithfully; no textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages visually (TBOO VIII, printed pp. 171-176; the letter itself runs pp. 172-175, with p.171 tail belonging to the preceding Rosencrantz letter and p.176 belonging to the following Welleie letter). Collated the full published Latin source_text sentence-by-sentence against Dreyer's printed page images and found the source_text to be a verbatim match throughout (no TEXT_ERROR). Checked every proper name (Ptolemy, Eratosthenes, Aristarchus Samius, Autolycus, Hipparchus Bithynius, Pythagoras, Valentin Otho - confirmed Curtius does not appear), every number (the '11 ad 83' Eratosthenes solstitial-point ratio p.173 l.25-26; 'quinque graduum'/5 degrees p.173 l.34), every Greek phrase (ἔγγιστα p.173, ἐν πλάτει p.173, τὸ αὐτός ἔφα p.174, καρτερὸν ὅρκον p.174 - all correctly glossed), the astronomical symbols (☌ ♀ et ☿ matutinam / ☿ infra ♀ on p.172, correctly read as morning conjunction of Venus and Mercury with Mercury below Venus, matching the English), the oath's subject/object (Herwart, first person 'me...communicaturum', swears HE will disclose nothing to anyone - correctly attributed, not flipped), the closing date and subscription ('Data Monachij die 17. Septembris Anno 99', p.175, matches), and the Prince's Library claim ('Regijs sumptibus comparatam, et libris manuscriptis Hebraicis, Graecis etc. refertam', p.175, matches). Opening and closing lines both check out. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15990831001", "next": "brahe-t_15991116001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990831001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_15991116001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of Tycho's lunar eclipse calculation, congratulates him on imperial patronage, and discusses whether the star of Mercury can eclipse Venus.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990917001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990918001", "d": "1599-09-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Anders Sørensen Vedel — Benátky nad Jizerou, 18 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist, autobiographical), with two Danish proverbs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) — there headed<i> Tychonis Brahei relatio de statu suo </i>· MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Excerpter 179 (Afskrift / copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note on the source block:</b> the working transcription carries Dreyer's inline critical-apparatus sigla — a superscript <b>\"A\"</b> (the witness-A variant marker), here OCR-captured as a capital <i>A</i> attached to the end of scattered words (<i>anteaA, nonA, alijsA, …A</i>, etc.). These are <b>not part of Tycho's text</b>; they are reproduced as transmitted in the working copy and are simply ignored in the translation. Paragraphing follows the narrative (the OCR transcription ran largely continuous).</p>", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIME ET PRAeSTANTISSIME DOMINE M. ANDREA WELLEIE, AMICE DIU MULTUMQUE DILECTE. S. P.</p><p>ACCEPI quidem antequam Witeberga abirem, tuas literas, ad quas etiam luculenter respondi. Sed cum ea, quae filio hac de re dictavi, inter componendas res meas ita distracta sint, atque inter alia forte, quae nunc inquirere non vacat, disposita, ut reperiri nunc nequiverint, nec etiam tuae literae nunc ad manus sint, describam ea, quae memoria dictitat, quaeque de rebus meis te scire velim. Atque ut historiolam habeas eorum, quae mihi partim in Patria occasionem discessus dederunt, partim postea in exteris regionibus evenerunt (lubet enim paulo altius ordiri, etiamsi quaedam ex illis tibi anteaA forte perspecta sint) sic habe.</p><p>Cum praevidissem Uraniae istic nostrae, cui tam serio tamque impense tot annos litavimus, ruinam instare, praemonui discrete per literas Cancellarium Regis, ut rectius provideret atque ne id fieret praecaveret. Responsum est, Regi Instrumenta ista Astronomica suis impensis conservare non esse integrum, quod et eventus testabatur. Nam praeter beneficium illud Norvagicum, quo, statim ac coronatio peracta est, privatus sum, etiam caetera, quae a Rege habui, adempta sunt, relicta sola Insula, et 11 vel 12 in ea parte Scaniae, quae Culda vocatur, rusticis, quae ducentos tantum quotannis Ioachimicos in certa pecunia nonA pendent, et concessa adhuc aliquantisper Praebenda ista Roeschildensi, quae pro quarta parte meis expensis annuis non sufficiebat, quam tamen, cum vix adhuc littus patrium reliquissem, Cancellarius a Rege pro se impetravit atque in privatos suos usus transtulit, adeo ut multi verosimiliterA inde collegerint quod huc cursus fuit. Nam praeterquam [quod] reditus pene omnes necessarij denegati, multis alijsA gravibus ... et non paucis molestijs praeter exspectationem et meritum pene absque intermissione conquassatus sum, forte quo promptius et citius abirem, adeo ut ne in proprijs quidem et a me exstructis aedibus Hafniensibus, quo instrumenta mea pro maiore parte contuleram atque unâ cum typographia deposueram, studia mea, quae tamdiu in honorem Patriae molitus sum, privatis sumptibus pertexere et quaedam in ijs ad Colophonem deducere permissum sit, tranquillitate praerepta Philosophica et dispositione instrumentorum in turri vicina, mihi integerrimeA a Proregibus et insuper a Magistratu urbis ea de causa concessa, nomine Regis, licet absentis, a Curiae Magistro per urbis Praefectum interdicta, utut is tum, unus e Quatruumviris, literas, quibus Magistratui HafniacoA mandabatur, ut turrim illam in eos usus mihi relinqueretA, subscripserit. Videns itaque, mihi in Patria omnia esse adversa, et adversum eorum animum, qui haec studia, quae in laudem Patriae perpetuam moliebar, promovere debebant, lubens et alacri fortique animo inde cum omnibus meisA discessi, idque iuxta solstitium aestivum, ante biennium a superiore huius anni numeratumA. Ne tamen viderer nimis propere nimisque remote patriam deserere, substiti primum Rostochij per anni quadrantem. Dedit ibi illustrissimus Dux Megapolitanus, avus Regis nostri maternus, literas ad nepotem diligenter scriptas, quibus quatuor adductis non minimis rationibus, eum de restitutione Uraniburgi rogabat et admonebat; quin et ego, scriptis inde ad Regem meum serenissimum literis, meam etiamnumA ipsi et Patriae prae alijs Principibus et nationibus operam subiectissime obtuli, modo tolerabilibusA conditionibus id fieri posset atque indemnitati meae consuleretur. Verum omnia incassum, nihil respondente Rege avo, et meas inclementius, quam sperassem et quam forte meritae erant, accipiente. Postea increbrescente peste Rostochij in Holsatiam a laudatae memoriae viro Domino HENRICO RANTZOVIO, affine meo et amico honorando, per literas evocatus, eo me cum tota familia recepi, atque arcem quandam eius, semisse saltem milliaris Hamburgo distantem, in quam etiam instrumenta, quotquot Hafniae habui, et suppellectilem isticA relictam transtuli, ipso benigne concedente, per integrum annum inhabitavi, volens sic non sine magno rei familiaris dispendio Patriae studere, atque nostratibus rem rectius dispiciendi satis superque multum relinquere temporis, ut in posterum eo magis essem excusabilis, si relicta Dania aliam quaererem Patriam aliumque laborum meorum Mecaenatem.</p><p>Interea dum ibi sum, relatum est de me meoque e Patria abitu ad augustissimum Imperatorem RUDOLPHUM Secundum, qui me mox iuxta Pascha praeteriti anni accersiri voluit, sed ante Michaëlis festum iter inde versus Bohemiam non suscepi, quod annuum tempus, prout in animum induxeram, istic complere vellem. Circa initia itaque OctobrisA Pragam versus profectionem instituebam, relicta Wandesburgi familia mea muliebri, donec de Caesaris clementissima voluntate edoctus essem. Dresdam cum advenissem, audivi in Bohemia omnia turbata esse ob pestem et dysenteriam istic grassantes, Imperatorem Pragae non esse, sed in arcem quandam secessisse, ut tutior sit, nec dari cuipiam audientiam (uti vocant) quare, scriptis in aulam literis, quid mihi faciendum restaret, percunctatus sum. Rescriptum est manu magnifici Domini CORADUCIJ Imperij Procancellarij in hunc modum: Caesaream Maiestatem de meo Dresdam adventu perlibenter intellexisse, utque istic aut in vicinia, si Pragam accedere nonA luberet, me continerem, cupere, donec, sedatis morbis epidemicis, aula dispersa recolligeretur, interea, ubi commode collocandus sim, benigne cogitare. Quo accepto responso, Witebergam pedem retuli, atque istic dimidio anno moratus sum, Philippaeas aedes inhabitans. Licet enim statim in fine Ianuarij ex clementissimo Caesaris mandato per dictum magnificum Dominum CORADUCIUM Procancellarium Pragam rursus auguste et honorifice (habes hîc literarum exemplarA) accersitus sim, tamen ante solstitium aestivum huius anni iter eo suscipere non concedebaturA, tum quod nonnullos libros Caesari destinatos prius absolvere nequiverim, tum quod ob filiae natu grandioris invaletudinem ibi ultra voluntatem detinerer, sed circa dictum tempus itineri me accingens, et in transitu Dresdae relicto Gynaeceo meo (quod Witebergam circa brumam antecedentem e Wandesburgo ad me venire feceram) Pragam circa Iohannis festum, veteri numeratione, intraviA.</p><p>Quae vero mihi postea istic et alias in Bohemia evenerunt, nunc brevibus commemorabo. Ab ipsius Caesarea Maiestate clementissimo favore acceptus sum, solusque auditus, nemine alio praesente, et respondente ipso Caesare benignissimis verbis, idque Latine. Tres libros, quos ipsius Caesareae Maiestati in proprias manus humillime porrexi (sic enim fieri voluit) lubentissime accepit et sequentibus mox diebus, interdumque usque in multam noctem (referente magnifico Domino BARVICIO) perlegit. Iussus sum mox Gynaeceum meum Dresdae relictum advehi curare. Actum deinde est mecum de annuo salario, quod cum Caesaris clementiae et benignitati liberum relinquerem, deputatum mihi est ab ipsius Maiestate trium millium florenorum in certa pecunia, et in accidentarijs quibusdam alios millenos attingens, reluctantibus quidem nonnullis ex Consiliarijs, quod nemo in aula, etiam ex Comitibus et Baronibus, quidiu inservierunt, tantum quotannis obtineretA, sed urgente Caesare ut sic fieret, nec dissuadente illustri Domino RUMPHIOA, Curiae Magistro, aut Domino TRAUTSONIO, aulae Mareschallo, aut ullis ex praecipuis, ita decisum est, numeratique mox bis mille floreni honorarij loco pro introitu. Quin et ut stipendium meum ab eo tempore, quo primum vocatus sum, inchoaretur, clementissime Caesar indulsit, utut non citius advenerim, siquidem interea nullum alium Dominum agnoscere et acceptare voluerim. Promisitque insuper benignissime et perlibenter feudum aliquod pro me et meis, secundum Imperij consuetudinem, haereditarium, quam primum aliquod in suis ditionibus vacaritA, ut familiae meae post me in peregrinis oris consuleretur. Introductus quidem sum in aedes Curtianas magnifice exstructas, impensis maioribus viginti millibus Ioachimicis, arcique vicinas, etiam antequamA audientiam (uti loquuntur) habueram, idque per praenominatum magnificum Dominum BARVICIUM, Imperij supremum Secretarium Caesarisque intimum, qui pene omnia solus apud ipsum efficit. Verum cum is animadverteret, me eas non admodum affectare (nolebam enim, ut vidua, quae eas etiamnumA inhabitabat, mea de causa emigraret, quodque nimijs visitationibus obnoxiae essent) subiecit, Caesarem facile aliquam ex suisA arcibus mihi inhabitandam concessurum, unius vel dimidijA diei itinere ab urbe distantem (si sic vellemA) et iudicavit pro me hoc esse commodius, quodque seorsimA degens invidiae aliorum minus forem obnoxius. Id cum magis arridere intelligeret, ad Caesarem rem detulit. Advocatus mox Capitaneus Brandeisensis, qui in tres Caesarianas arces me deduceret. Prima Brandeisia, distans ab urbe 3 milliaribus, altera Lissa, 4 remota, tertia BenachiaA, quae 5 milliaribus sive 6 horarum itinere (est enim via plana) inde abest. Quibus perlustratis praetuli hanc Benachiam, in quam paucis post diebus per eundem Capitaneum solenniter introductus sum. Obtulit etiam Caesarea Maiestas sponte sua, se novum Uraniburgum pro me aedificariA curaturum, quod Danico illi, in libro quem obtuleram repraesentato, non cederet, iussitque Caesar mihi per BARVICIUM haec verba conclusionis loco dici: si ea, quae iam deputaratA, mihi non sufficerent, et pluribus in posterum opus haberem, nec sibi esse accurtatam manum, nec mihi obturatum os. At ego subiectissime gratias agens, ijs pro tempore acquievi. Extruximus nunc hîc Benachiae novum observatorium pro omnibus meis instrumentis rite et seorsim disponendis, simulque opportunum laboratorium pro exercitijs Spagyricis, tum quoque alijs, quaecumque voluero, ipso Caesare sumptus benignissime praebente, et quotquot desideraro artifices mechanicos sustentante, quod in Patria proprijs impensis faciendum erat, nulla tamen residua manente gratia proA ijs aut etiam pro longe maioribus. Omnia erant ingrata, nihil fecisse benigne; ideoque ei vale dixi.</p><p>Ago vero Deo opt. ter max. ex toto pectore gratias, qui me tam benigne ex istis, quas istic diuA multumque fortiter, licet indigne, sustinui, difficultatibus et adversitatibusA liberarit imo vindicarit, et mihi meisque rectius in alienis oris prospexerit, ut studia haec, quae tanto conamine molior, pacate et commode tractare atque, Deo benedicente, ad exoptatum finem deducere possem in Caesaris atque Imperij honorem, siquidem Dania, Patria mea, hac gloria se ipsam tam inconsiderate privarit. Sunt hîc egregij et politi homines, virtute et dignitate valentes, qui me favore et amore officiose prosequuntur, egoque vicissim illos, exoptantque, ut diu ante advenissem, quod et ego optarimA. Sed obstabant Senatusconsulti literae in Dania mihi statim ab obitu laudatissimae memoriae Regis FRIDERICI concessae et aliter, quam evenit, promittentes, paucorum tamen culpa, indolentibus caeteris viris integris et Patriae honoris studiosis, meique amantibus. Agasque Deo propitio et ter maximoA mecum devote gratias, agantque omnes pij et integriA bonarumque scientiarum amantes, quod in hoc augustissimo et laudatissimo Caesare tam benignum Mecaenatem mihi et meis prospexerit, cui aeviternoA Deo sit laus, honor et gloria in secula.</p><p>Mitto iam filium meum TYCHONEM cum MULAeO domestico meo in Daniam, ut instrumenta, istic ob magnitudinem relicta, aliasque res meas ante hyemem per Albim asportari faciant, quo etiam nomine Imperatoria Maiestas ad Magistratum tam Magdeburgensem quam Hamburgensem clementer scripsitA et Dominus BARVICIUS iussu Caesaris in Daniam ad Dominum HENRICUM RAMELIUM, Senatorem Regni, quo negotium istudA promptius procuret. Dedit etiam ipsius Caesarea Maiestas ad illustrissimum Electorem Brandeburgicum literas, quibus eum ut MULLERUM Mathematicum suum per unum aut alterum annum mihi concedat laborumA socium (sic enim inter MULLERUM et me, cum Witebergae me inviseret, convenit) rogat. Quin et alias Caesarea Maiestas, quaecunque in rebus mechanicis vel alijs, ad opus quod molior conducentia, clementissime mihi subministrari iussit, estque praeterea benignissimo et Caesareo favore erga me et meos affecta.</p><p>Habes nunc, charissime Domine M. ANDREAA, rerum mearum historiolam, postquam e Dania concedens primum me in Germaniam, deinde in Bohemiam receperim, ubi etiam benignissime a Caesare et perhumaniter ab optimis quibusque receptus sum. Cumque Historicis natura ipsa semper delectatus sis, existimo et hanc narratiunculam tibi non ingratam fore. Scribitur ob eam, quae nobis intercedit, notitiam et rerum quoque Astronomicarum communem amorem. Res vero, uti gesta, narrata est; utinam quaevis Historia aeque certo sibi constaret. Tu hîc compendiolum et summam quandam excerpe, et in annales sive fastos tuosA infer memoriae causa ad posteros, ut et multi alij in Germania et alibi viri faciunt clari. Et multi insuper, praeter literas hac de re mecum agentes, me carminibus onerant, quorum, si tam cito describere licuerit, exempla adiungam, si forte aliqua ex ijs antea per M. CHRISTIERNUMA vel aliunde non accepisti. Et si ausus fueris et si vena tua adhuc valeat, uti valere existimem, non saltem carminibus accine, et cumulum aliorum adauge, sed et ea, quae prosa oratione consignanda in memoriam rei bene gestae suo tempore non neglige. Dixit ad me, cum in Dania adhucA essem, isA qui tibi in Historico officioA successit, se libenter quidem insulae Hvenae Epithalamium HistoricisA proditurum, at Epitaphium non. ScisA, quorsum alluditur, et nosti horum verborum primum authorem, sed suo forte damno, non meo. Der giør tit en den anden got, alligevell hand mener det ont, och falsk slaar sin egen Herre paa Halss. Aiebat insuper alter ille Historicus, se excusatum haberi posse, si saltem Epithalamij mentionem faceret, Epicedium praeteriret, siquidem prius illud tempore FRIDERICI RegisA laudatissimae memoriae invenerit, ad cuius usque obitum suas relationes pertexere solummodo sibi iniunctum sit, reliqua non item, uti nec moris est. At tu, cum ab hoc Historico officio, sic volentibus Politicis quibusdam, ex inopinato abruptus sis, licebit tibi privatim, si non publice, utriusque temporis commemorationem pertexere, et tam de Epithalamio, quam de Epitaphio eaA, quae scis et vera esse cognovisti, innuereA, modo non aperte, saltem tacite, et quamvis illa publicare forsan non dabitur, reserventur ad eos, qui veritatem scire cupiant, sive nostris temporibus sive postea. Det taerer intetA. Et res forte est maioris momenti quam apud nos aestimatur, rumorque eius iam orbem implevit, mirantibus omnibus cordatis et integris, quod Patriae honor apud nos neglectus sit. Quare plura non addam, ne me ipsum et studia mea efferre videar.</p><p>Intelligis ex praememoratis, charissimeA M. ANDREAA, pleraqueA responsionis loco ad tuas Witebergae acceptas scribenda, et quare nec Vrastislaviam, uti suasisti, eundum foret nec Colonias, utrumque a Caesare nimis distans mihique incommodum.</p><p>Caeterum quae de Bruto isto Dithmarsico addidisti, recte a te prolata sunt, carmenque eius putidum, quod misisti, libenterA accepi, et certum est, ERICUM LANGIUM per incuriam et imprudentiam, quibus etiam in proprijs negotijs obnoxius est, ansam dedisse, ut tantis calumnijs a tam impura Bestia praeter meritum aspergerer, siquidem eum, me inconsulto et invito, in meam secum adduxit insulam. Sed coactus est propterea Magdeburgi sub publico Notario testari id quod verum sciret, contra famosum illud scriptum, licet hoc ipsum etiam aegre faceret, relataque sunt omnia inA instrumenta, uti vocant, publica, membrana, partim Latine partim Germanice conscripta, ut haberem in promptu, quibus eum Pragae convincerem, quando mihi luberet accusationem instituere, non quidem per meipsum, siquidem illum indignum iudico, sed per alium quendam Procuratorem, mea pecunia conductum; quod et antea facere constitui. Agitur enim de honore et existimatione mea, quam tueri oportet, etiamsi Pragam non venissemA. Verum iste male sibi conscius paulo post meum accessum se subduxit, deserta etiam ibi uxore, quam nuper duxerat, infami et adultera (dignum patella operculum). Verum, cum opportunitas daturA, investiganda erit per opportunitatem, ubi a gravioribus negotijs aliquantulum respiraro, ista Bestia atque in ius, ubicunque latitarit, protrahenda. Quod etiam mihi non erit difficile, sive Pragae sive alibi id exequendum fuerit. Consului hac de re tacite Archiepiscopum Pragensem, virum illustrem et claro genere oriundum, an se conscio famosum hocA scriptum Pragae excusum sit, cum eius officij sit in talia animadvertere. At ille se excusabat, seque inscio factum protestabatur authoremque in ius vocari voluit et secundum leges puniri, quod et se promoturum addixitA. Videbunt aliquando Antesignani Machiavellistici in Dania Coriphaei, quam incassum seipsos in gratiam magistri sui titillarint, quod tale scriptum ex IOHANNE AALBURGENSE congratulationis causa acceperint, atque in suis conviviolis magno applausu, si non potius maiore stultitia, legerint, tanquam escae et vini condimentaA, utque sic probare possent per unum scurrile alterum, causas mei discessus e Dania, quarum quidam ex ijs conscijA erantA, si non autores et promotores, non incompetenter administratas esse. Sed viderint et isti et iste. Sequitur sua poena nocentem, et, Deo prospiciente ac protegente, tandem bona causa triumphat.</p><p>Plura nunc non adiungoA, ne te nimium molestem. Tuum est etiam mihi luculenter rescribere, et amicitiam inveteratam literis saltemA, siquidem vix aliter in posterum fieri queat, continuare et conservareA. Vale. Saluta ex me diligenter Ripenses omnes, quos nosti mei amantes, tum quoque alios in vestra CimbriaA, et de rebus meis, modo scire cupiverint, instrue, ut omnes Deo propitio et omnipotenti mecum gratias agatis, quod ex tot difficultatibus et adversitatibus, quas in patria immerito sustinui, tam provide et benigne eripuerit et vindicarit, cui soli honor et laus in saecula. Iamque, mi amantissime Domine Μ. ANDREAA cum tota tua domo, salutata a me coniuge etA liberis, quibus prodesse, quantum potero, semper satagam, iterum vale.</p><p>Dabantur Benachiae properanti calamo in arce CaesarianaA in Bohemia die 18. Septembr. AnnoA 1599.<br>Tui amantissimus TYCHO BRAHE manu propria.</p><p>Non vacabat relegere. Ipsemet dispicias.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most learned and excellent Master Andreas Velleius, my long- and much-beloved friend. Heartiest greeting.</b></p><p>I did indeed receive your letter before I left Wittenberg, and I answered it fully too. But since the notes I dictated to my son on this matter were so scattered amid the packing of my affairs, and stowed among other things which I have no leisure now to search out, that they cannot now be found — nor is your letter now to hand — I shall write down what my memory prompts, and what I would have you know of my affairs. And so that you may have a little history of the things which partly, in the fatherland, gave occasion for my departure, and partly afterwards befell me in foreign regions (for I am minded to begin a little higher up, even though some of them may perhaps have been known to you before) — take it thus.</p><p>When I had foreseen that ruin was at hand for our Urania there, to which we had so seriously and so lavishly sacrificed for so many years, I discreetly forewarned the King's Chancellor by letter, that he should provide better and take care it not happen. The answer was that it was not feasible for the King to maintain those astronomical Instruments at his own expense — which the outcome too bore out. For, besides that Norwegian benefice of which I was deprived as soon as the coronation was over, the other things too which I held from the King were taken away, leaving only the Island, and 11 or 12 [farms] among the peasants in that part of Scania called Kullen, which pay only two hundred joachimsdalers a year in fixed money; and that Roskilde prebend, still granted for a little while, which did not suffice for a quarter of my yearly expenses — yet which the Chancellor, when I had scarcely yet left my native shore, obtained from the King for himself and transferred to his own private use, so that many quite plausibly gathered from this which way the course had been tending. For, besides that almost all the necessary revenues were denied, I was battered with many other grave [burdens] and not a few vexations, beyond expectation and desert, almost without intermission — perhaps so that I should go away the more readily and quickly; so that I was not even allowed, in my own Copenhagen house, built by me — to which I had brought the greater part of my instruments and deposited them together with the printing-press — to complete at my own expense the studies I had so long laboured at in honour of the fatherland, and to bring some of them to a colophon [conclusion], philosophical tranquillity being snatched away; and the arrangement of the instruments in the neighbouring tower — granted me for that purpose, in the fullest terms, by the Viceroys and moreover by the city Magistracy — was forbidden, in the King's name (though he was absent), by the Master of the Court through the city Praefect — although the latter, then one of the Four Men [councilmen], had himself signed the letters by which the Copenhagen Magistracy was ordered to leave me that tower for those uses. Seeing therefore that everything in the fatherland was against me, and adverse the mind of those who ought to have furthered these studies which I was labouring at for the perpetual praise of the fatherland, I departed thence gladly and with eager, strong spirit, with all my people — and that about the summer solstice, two years before that of the present year. Yet, lest I should seem to desert the fatherland too hastily and too far off, I first halted at Rostock for a quarter of a year. There the most illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg, our King's maternal grandfather, gave a letter diligently written to his grandson, in which, adducing four weighty reasons, he asked and admonished him concerning the restitution of Uraniborg; and I too, writing thence to my most serene King, most humbly offered my service even then to him and the fatherland before other princes and nations, provided it could be done on tolerable conditions and care taken for my indemnity. But all in vain — the [Duke,] the King's grandfather, getting no answer, and [the King] receiving my [letter] more harshly than I had hoped and than perhaps it deserved. Afterwards, the plague increasing at Rostock, summoned by letter into Holstein by the man of praised memory Lord Henrik Rantzau — my kinsman-by-marriage and honoured friend — I betook myself thither with my whole family, and for a whole year inhabited a certain castle of his, distant only half a mile from Hamburg, into which I also moved the instruments I had at Copenhagen, and the furniture left there, he kindly granting it; wishing thus, not without great loss to my household estate, to serve the fatherland, and to leave my countrymen more than enough time to consider the matter aright, that I might hereafter be the more excusable, if, leaving Denmark, I should seek another fatherland and another Maecenas for my labours.</p><p>Meanwhile, while I was there, news of me and of my departure from the fatherland was carried to the most august Emperor Rudolf II, who wished me to be summoned soon, about Easter of last year; but I did not undertake the journey thence toward Bohemia before Michaelmas, since I wished to complete the year there, as I had resolved. So about the beginning of October I set about the journey toward Prague, leaving my womenfolk at Wandsbek until I should be informed of the Emperor's most gracious will. When I had reached Dresden, I heard that everything in Bohemia was disturbed by the plague and dysentery raging there; that the Emperor was not at Prague but had withdrawn into a certain castle, to be safer; and that no one was given audience (as they call it). Wherefore, writing a letter to the court, I inquired what remained for me to do. The reply was, in the hand of the Magnificent Lord Coraduz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, after this manner: that His Imperial Majesty had most gladly learned of my arrival at Dresden; that he desired me to keep myself there or in the vicinity, if I did not care to come to Prague, until, the epidemic diseases being settled, the dispersed court should be reassembled; and that he was meanwhile kindly considering where I might conveniently be placed. Having received this reply, I withdrew to Wittenberg, and stayed there half a year, inhabiting the Melanchthon house. For although, immediately at the end of January, by the most gracious command of the Emperor, through the said Magnificent Lord Coraduz the Vice-Chancellor, I was again augustly and honourably summoned to Prague (you have here a copy of the letters), yet I was not allowed to undertake the journey thither before the summer solstice of this year — partly because I could not first finish certain books destined for the Emperor, partly because I was detained there beyond my will by the illness of my elder daughter. But about the said time, girding myself for the journey, and in passing leaving my Gynaeceum [womenfolk] at Dresden (which I had had come to me at Wittenberg from Wandsbek about the previous winter), I entered Prague about St. John's day, by the old reckoning.</p><p>But what afterwards befell me there, and elsewhere in Bohemia, I shall now briefly recount. By His Imperial Majesty himself I was received with most clement favour, and heard alone, no one else being present, the Emperor himself replying in most kind words, and that in Latin. The three books which I most humbly handed to His Imperial Majesty's own hands (for so he wished it done) he received most gladly, and read through over the following days, sometimes far into the night (as the Magnificent Lord Barvitius reports). I was soon bidden to have my Gynaeceum, left at Dresden, brought up. Then there was dealing with me about an annual salary, which, since I left it free to the Emperor's clemency and kindness, was assigned me by His Majesty at three thousand florins in fixed money, reaching another thousand in certain perquisites — some of the Councillors indeed objecting, since no one at court, even among the Counts and Barons who had served long, obtained so much yearly; but the Emperor urging that it be so, and the illustrious Lord Rumpf, Master of the Court, not dissuading, nor Lord Trautson, Marshal of the court, nor any of the chief men, it was so decided; and two thousand florins were soon paid by way of honorarium for my entry. Moreover, the Emperor most graciously granted that my stipend should begin from the time I was first summoned, although I had not come sooner, since meanwhile I had been unwilling to recognise and accept any other Lord. And he besides most kindly and gladly promised some fief for me and mine, hereditary according to the Empire's custom, as soon as any should fall vacant in his dominions, that provision might be made for my family after me in foreign shores. I was indeed brought into the Curtius house — magnificently built, at an expense of more than twenty thousand joachims, and near the castle — even before I had had audience (as they say), and that through the aforenamed Magnificent Lord Barvitius, supreme Secretary of the Empire and the Emperor's intimate, who alone accomplishes almost everything with him. But when he noticed that I did not much desire it (for I did not wish that the widow who still dwelt there should move out on my account, and because [those houses] were exposed to too many visitations), he suggested that the Emperor would readily grant me one of his castles to inhabit, a day's or half a day's journey from the city (if I so wished), and judged this more convenient for me, and that, living apart, I should be less exposed to the envy of others. When he understood that this pleased me more, he referred the matter to the Emperor. Soon the Captain of Brandeis was summoned, to conduct me to three imperial castles: the first Brandeis, distant 3 miles from the city; the second Lissa, 4 [miles] off; the third Benátky, which is 5 miles, or 6 hours' journey (for the road is level), away. Having surveyed these, I preferred this Benátky, into which a few days later I was solemnly introduced by the same Captain. His Imperial Majesty also offered, of his own accord, that he would have a new Uraniborg built for me, that should not yield to the Danish one represented in the book I had presented; and the Emperor ordered these words to be said to me through Barvitius, by way of conclusion: that if what he had already assigned did not suffice me, and I should need more hereafter, neither was his hand shortened, nor my mouth stopped. But I, giving most humble thanks, acquiesced in them for the present. We have now built here at Benátky a new observatory for setting up all my instruments duly and separately, and at the same time a convenient laboratory for Spagyric [alchemical] exercises, and others too, whatever I shall wish — the Emperor most kindly furnishing the costs, and supporting as many mechanical artificers as I desire: what in the fatherland had to be done at my own expense, with no gratitude remaining for them, or even for far greater things. Everything was thankless; nothing was [counted] graciously done; and so I bade it farewell.</p><p>But I give thanks to God, the best and thrice-greatest, with my whole heart, who has so kindly freed — nay, vindicated — me from those difficulties and adversities which I there long and much endured, bravely though unworthily, and has provided better for me and mine in foreign shores, that I might pursue these studies, which I labour at with so great an effort, peacefully and conveniently, and, God blessing, bring them to the longed-for end, to the honour of the Emperor and the Empire — since Denmark, my fatherland, has so inconsiderately deprived itself of this glory. There are here excellent and polished men, strong in virtue and dignity, who attend me with favour and love, and I them in turn; and they wish that I had come long before, as I too could wish. But there stood in the way the Senate-decree letters granted me in Denmark immediately upon the death of King Frederik of most praiseworthy memory, promising otherwise than has turned out — by the fault, however, of a few, the other upright men, zealous for the fatherland's honour and loving me, grieving at it. And give thanks devoutly with me to God, propitious and thrice-greatest — and let all do so who are pious and upright and lovers of the good sciences — that He has provided for me and mine, in this most august and most praiseworthy Emperor, so kind a Maecenas: to whom, the eternal God, be praise, honour, and glory unto the ages.</p><p>I am now sending my son Tycho, with Mule my servant, into Denmark, that they may have the instruments left there for their size, and my other things, carried off before winter by the Elbe — on which score, too, the Imperial Majesty has graciously written to the Magistracy both of Magdeburg and of Hamburg, and Lord Barvitius, by the Emperor's order, into Denmark to Lord Henrik Ramel, Senator of the Realm, that he may the more readily expedite that business. His Imperial Majesty has also given letters to the most illustrious Elector of Brandenburg, asking him to grant me, for a year or two, his mathematician Müller as a partner in my labours (for so it was agreed between Müller and me, when he visited me at Wittenberg). And the Emperor has besides most graciously ordered that whatever in mechanical or other matters conduces to the work I am undertaking be supplied to me; and he is moreover affected with most kind and imperial favour toward me and mine.</p><p>You have now, dearest Master Andreas, the little history of my affairs — how, departing from Denmark, I betook myself first into Germany, then into Bohemia, where too I was received most kindly by the Emperor and most courteously by all the best men. And since you have always by your very nature delighted in Histories, I think this little narrative too will not be unwelcome to you. It is written on account of the acquaintance that subsists between us, and of our common love also of astronomical matters. The matter has been narrated as it was done; would that every History were equally certain in itself. Do you here excerpt a little compendium and summary, and enter it in your annals or <i>fasti</i> for the sake of memory to posterity, as many other famous men in Germany and elsewhere do. And many besides, over and above those dealing with me by letter on this matter, burden me with poems — examples of which, if I am allowed to copy them so quickly, I shall append, in case you have perhaps not received some of them before, through Master Christiern or from elsewhere. And if you dare, and if your vein is still vigorous (as I think it is), sing along not only in verses, and increase the heap of the others, but also do not neglect, in due time, the things to be set down in prose for the memory of the well-managed deed. He who succeeded you in the Historian's office said to me, when I was still in Denmark, that he would gladly indeed bring forth to the Histories an <i>Epithalamium</i> [wedding-song] of the island of Hven, but not an <i>Epitaph</i>. You know what is alluded to, and you know the first author of these words — but perhaps to his own harm, not mine. <i>One often does another good, although he means it ill; and the false man strikes his own lord upon the neck.</i> That other Historian said, moreover, that he could be held excused if he made mention only of the Epithalamium and passed over the Epicedium [funeral-song], since he had found that [office] in the time of King Frederik of most praiseworthy memory, down to whose death alone he was enjoined to weave his relations, and no further, as is not the custom. But you, since you were unexpectedly cut off from this Historian's office (certain politicians so willing it), it will be permitted to you, privately if not publicly, to weave the commemoration of both times, and to hint, both of the Epithalamium and of the Epitaph, the things you know and have learned to be true — only not openly, at least tacitly; and though it may perhaps not be granted to publish them, let them be reserved for those who desire to know the truth, whether in our times or afterwards. <i>It costs nothing.</i> And the matter is perhaps of greater moment than is reckoned among us, and the rumour of it has now filled the world, all sensible and upright men marvelling that the fatherland's honour was neglected among us. Wherefore I shall add no more, lest I seem to extol myself and my studies.</p><p>You understand from the aforesaid, dearest Master Andreas, most of what should be written by way of reply to your letter received at Wittenberg, and why I should go neither to Breslau, as you advised, nor to Cologne — each too distant from the Emperor and inconvenient for me.</p><p>For the rest, what you added about that Ditmarsh Brutus was rightly uttered by you, and I gladly received his rank poem which you sent; and it is certain that Erik Lange, through the carelessness and imprudence to which he is liable even in his own affairs, gave the handle by which I came to be bespattered, beyond my desert, with such great calumnies by so impure a Beast — since he brought him onto my island with him, without consulting me and against my will. But [the Beast] was on that account compelled, at Magdeburg, under a public Notary, to testify what he knew to be true, against that libellous writing — though even this he did grudgingly; and everything was set down in public instruments (as they call them), on parchment, written partly in Latin partly in German, that I might have at hand the means to convict him at Prague, when it should please me to bring an accusation — not indeed through myself, since I judge him unworthy, but through some Procurator, hired with my money; which I had resolved to do before. For it concerns my honour and reputation, which I must defend, even had I not come to Prague. But that man, ill at ease in his own conscience, withdrew himself a little after my arrival, deserting even there the wife he had recently married — an infamous and adulterous one (a lid worthy of the pot). But when opportunity is given, that Beast must be tracked down at the opportune time, when I shall have breathed a little from weightier business, and dragged into court wherever he has lurked. Which will not even be difficult for me, whether it must be carried out at Prague or elsewhere. I have on this matter quietly consulted the Archbishop of Prague — an illustrious man, sprung of distinguished stock — [as to] whether that libellous writing had been printed at Prague with his knowledge, since it is his office to take notice of such things. But he excused himself, and protested that it was done without his knowledge, and wished the author to be summoned to law and punished according to the laws, and added that he would himself promote it. The Machiavellian standard-bearers, the Coryphaei in Denmark, will one day see how vainly they tickled themselves to please their master, in that they received such a writing from Hans Aalborg by way of congratulation, and read it in their little banquets with great applause (if not rather with greater stupidity), as relishes for food and wine, and so that they might prove by one scurrilous [writing] another — namely that the causes of my departure from Denmark, of which some of them were aware (if not authors and promoters), were not incompetently managed. But let both these and that one see to it. His own punishment follows the guilty; and, God providing and protecting, at last the good cause triumphs.</p><p>I add no more now, lest I trouble you too much. It is for you, too, to write back to me fully, and to continue and preserve our old friendship at least by letters, since hereafter it can scarcely be otherwise. Farewell. Greet diligently on my behalf all the people of Ribe whom you know to love me, and others too in your Cimbria [Jutland]; and instruct them about my affairs, if only they desire to know, that you may all give thanks with me to God, propitious and almighty, that He has so providently and kindly rescued and vindicated me out of so many difficulties and adversities which I undeservedly endured in the fatherland — to whom alone be honour and praise unto the ages. And now, my most beloved Master Andreas, with your whole house — your wife and children being greeted by me, whom I shall always strive to benefit so far as I can — again, farewell.</p><p>Given at Benátky, with hurrying pen, in the imperial castle in Bohemia, the 18th of September, the year 1599.<br>Your most loving Tycho Brahe, with his own hand.</p><p>There was no time to read it over again; you must judge it for yourself.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>), where the letter is headed <i>Tychonis Brahei relatio de statu suo</i> (\"Tycho Brahe's account of his condition\"). MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Excerpter 179 (a later copy). The inline superscript \"A\" sigla scattered through the source block are Dreyer's witness-A variant markers (OCR artefacts), not part of the text — see the editorial note above.</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"die 18. Septembr. Anno 1599,\"</i> from the imperial castle Benátky in Bohemia; catalogue label confirms <b>New Style</b>. Within the letter Tycho dates his entry into Prague <i>\"about St. John's day, by the old reckoning\"</i> (≈ 24 June O.S.) — an explicit O.S. marker inside an N.S.-dated letter.</li><li><b>Recipient &amp; genre.</b> <b>Anders Sørensen Vedel</b> (<i>Andreas Severini Velleius</i>, 1542–1616), the Danish historian and former royal historiographer (and Tycho's old Ribe friend; cf. glossary §5.6). This is Tycho's <b>autobiographical <i>relatio de statu suo</b></i> — the single most sustained first-person narrative of his departure from Denmark and his establishment under Rudolf II. Tycho explicitly asks Vedel, as a historian, to <b>excerpt it into his annals/<i>fasti</i> for posterity</b> — i.e. the letter is consciously composed as material for the historical record.</li><li><b>The Danish grievances (the core of the apologia).</b> Tycho's own bill of particulars for the loss of Uraniborg: (1) the <b>Norwegian benefice</b> (the Nordfjord fief) stripped immediately after <b>Christian IV's coronation</b> (1596); (2) reduction to \"only the Island [Hven] and 11–12 farms at Kullen in Scania,\" yielding just 200 joachimsdalers; (3) the <b>Roskilde prebend</b> — already inadequate — seized by the <b>Chancellor</b> for his own use the moment Tycho left; (4) systematic harassment \"perhaps so that I should leave the quicker\"; (5) the ban, <i>in the King's name though he was absent</i>, on his arranging instruments in the <b>Copenhagen tower</b> the city had granted him — forbidden by the <b>Master of the Court</b> through the very <b>city Praefect</b> who had earlier signed the grant. A primary source for the much-debated circumstances of the Hven catastrophe.</li><li><b>The exile itinerary.</b> Departure \"about the summer solstice, two years before this year's\" (≈ June 1597) → <b>Rostock</b> (a quarter-year), where <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b> (Christian IV's maternal grandfather) interceded with the King by a four-point letter, and Tycho offered renewed service in vain → <b>Wandsbek</b> (a Rantzau castle ½ mile from Hamburg, a full year), hosted by <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (\"of praised memory\" — he had died ~Dec 1598/Jan 1599) → news reaches <b>Rudolf II</b>, who summons him (Easter 1598) → set out ~Oct 1598, but at <b>Dresden</b> learns of the Bohemian plague; <b>Coraduz</b> (Imperial Vice-Chancellor) tells him to wait → retires to <b>Wittenberg</b> (half a year, lodging in <b>Melanchthon's house</b>, <i>Philippaeae aedes</i>) → re-summoned end of Jan 1599 → enters <b>Prague</b> ~24 June O.S. 1599 (delayed by unfinished books for the Emperor and his elder daughter's illness).</li><li><b>The imperial reception (a richly detailed record).</b> Received alone by Rudolf II, who replied \"in most kind words, and that in Latin\"; presented <b>three books</b> to the Emperor's own hands (read by Rudolf \"far into the night,\" per Barvitius); salary <b>3,000 florins</b> fixed + ~1,000 in perquisites (over the objection of councillors — \"no Count or Baron got so much\"), with <b>2,000 florins entry-honorarium</b> and the stipend <b>backdated to the first summons</b>; a promised <b>hereditary fief</b>; first lodged in the <b>Curtius house</b> (Prague, built for &gt;20,000 joachims) but, declining it (a widow lived there; too exposed to visitors), offered an imperial castle — shown <b>Brandeis (3 mi.), Lissa (4 mi.), Benátky (5 mi./6 hrs)</b> by the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>, choosing <b>Benátky</b>; the Emperor's offer of \"a new Uraniborg not inferior to the Danish one in the book I presented [the <i>Mechanica</i>]\" and his assurance (via Barvitius) <i>\"neither is his hand shortened, nor your mouth stopped.\"</i> A new <b>observatory</b> and a <b>Spagyric (alchemical) laboratory</b> already built at imperial cost.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Rudolf II</b> (the Emperor); <b>Coraduz</b> (Imperial Vice-Chancellor, <i>Procancellarius</i>); <b>Barvitius</b> (supreme Secretary, \"who alone accomplishes almost everything\"); <b>Wolfgang Rumpf</b> (<i>Rumphius</i>, Master of the Court / Obersthofmeister) and <b>Paul Sixt Trautson</b> (<i>Trautsonius</i>, court Marshal), who did not oppose the salary; the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>; the <b>widow</b> in the Curtius house; <b>Archbishop of Prague</b> (<b>Zbyněk Berka of Dubá</b>), who disclaimed knowledge of the libel's printing; <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b>; <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (Wandsbek host); <b>King Frederik II</b> (<i>\"of most praiseworthy memory,\"</i> d. 1588 — the Senate-decree promises date from just after his death); <b>King Christian IV</b> (the coronation; the \"King\"/grandson); the Danish <b>Chancellor</b> (unnamed; took the Roskilde prebend); son <b>Tycho</b> and servant <b>Mule</b>, dispatched to Denmark; <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (Danish Senator, to expedite the shipping); the <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b> (Joachim Friedrich) and his mathematician <b>Müller</b>, lent to Tycho; <b>Master Christiern</b> (a conduit for poems, unidentified). <b>The Historian who succeeded Vedel</b> in the royal office is <b>Niels Krag</b> (royal historiographer from 1594/95) — the source of the barbed <i>\"Epithalamium of Hven but not an Epitaph\"</i> remark (i.e. willing to chronicle Hven's founding but not its death).</li><li><b>The Ursus thread.</b> \"That <b>Ditmarsh Brutus</b> / the impure <b>Beast</b>\" is <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>; Tycho blames <b>Erik Lange</b> (Sophie Brahe's alchemist betrothed) for having brought Ursus onto Hven, occasioning the feud. Ursus was made to testify under a Magdeburg notary against his own libel; Tycho holds notarised Latin/German instruments to <b>convict him at Prague</b> via a hired Procurator. Ursus fled Prague, deserting his \"infamous and adulterous\" new wife. The libel had been relished by the \"<b>Machiavellian Coryphaei</b> in Denmark,\" who got it from <b>Hans Aalborg</b> (<i>Johannes Aalburgensis</i>, glossary §5.6). Continuous with the Ursus material in brahe-t_15991209001 (to Kepler).</li><li><b>Latin &amp; Danish tags / allusions.</b> <i>\"nec sibi esse accurtatam manum, nec mihi obturatum os\"</i> (the Emperor's assurance) — echo of <b>Isaiah 59:1</b> (\"the Lord's hand is not shortened\"). <i>\"dignum patella operculum\"</i> — \"a lid worthy of the pot,\" a proverbial fit of two bad things (Hieronymus; Erasmus, <i>Adagia</i>). <i>\"Sequitur sua poena nocentem\"</i> — \"his own punishment follows the guilty\" (proverbial; cf. Claudian). <i>\"tandem bona causa triumphat\"</i> — \"at last the good cause triumphs,\" a recurring Tychonic motto (also in brahe-t_15980810001). <i>Philippaeae aedes</i> — Melanchthon's house at Wittenberg. <i>Gynaeceum</i> — the women's quarters/his womenfolk. <i>Spagyrica</i> — alchemical (Paracelsian) operations. Two <b>Danish proverbs</b> are kept and translated: <i>\"Der giør tit en den anden got, alligevell hand mener det ont…\"</i> (\"One often does another good, although he means it ill; and the false man strikes his own lord upon the neck\") and <i>\"Det taerer intet\"</i> (\"It costs nothing / does no harm\").</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored Tycho's authorial postscript \"Non vacabat relegere. Ipsemet dispicias.\" (\"There was no time to read it over again; you must judge it for yourself\"), printed by Dreyer immediately after the signature, TBOO VIII p.181 - previously silently dropped from both Latin and English. Also corrected 'militaribus' to 'milliaribus' (miles) in the Brandeis-distance sentence per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.178; the English already read 'miles' and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, transparent autobiographical narrative rendered securely throughout; the substance — the Danish grievances and the loss of Uraniborg, the Rostock/Wandsbek/Dresden/Wittenberg exile itinerary, the detailed imperial reception (salary, honorarium, fief, the castle choice, the new-Uraniborg offer), the observatory/laboratory, the shipping of the instruments, and the Ursus prosecution — is clear and historically well attested. Residual minor uncertainties, none obscuring sense: the <b>unnamed Danish Chancellor</b> who took the Roskilde prebend; the identity of <b>Master Christiern</b>; the conventional identification of the Vedel-successor historian as <b>Niels Krag</b>; and the pervasive <b>OCR-intruded \"A\" apparatus sigla</b> in the source block (flagged, non-textual). The Latin is otherwise free of true cruxes.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 9 Dreyer slice pages (TBOO VIII pp. 174-182) as page images, in three chunks (1-4, 5-8, 9). Collated opening and closing lines, every proper name (Coraducius, Barvitius, Rumpf, Trautson, Ramel, Muller, Erik Lange, Rothmann, Hans Aalborg), all numbers/sums (11-12 farms/200 joachimsdalers, 3000+1000 florin salary, 2000 florin honorarium, 20000-joachimsdaler house, the three-castle distances 3/4/5 miles), the Danish proverb and 'Det taerer intet', and the closing date/place. Confirmed the known-open-question passage 'Non vacabat relegere. Ipsemet dispicias.' is genuine Tycho text printed by Dreyer directly after the signature on p.181 (before the editorial apparatus note that opens p.182) - it belongs to this letter and is missing from both source_text and the translation, reportable as TEXT_ERROR. Also found an unrelated second TEXT_ERROR: source_text's 'militaribus' (p.178 distances passage) should read Dreyer's 'milliaribus'; this one did not propagate into the English, which correctly says 'miles' regardless.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). This is the known open question, confirmed: Dreyer prints a genuine 5-word authorial postscript (\"There was no time to reread it; you must judge it yourself\") as the true close of the letter. It is missing from both our source_text and published_english - a silently dropped closing sentence, not merely an untranslated word. Dreyer prints: <i>Non vacabat relegere. Ipsemet dispicias. (TBOO VIII, p. 181, printed immediately after the signature \"Tui amantissimus TYCHO BRAHE manu propria.\", before the editorial source-note \"[Ex apographo in Archivo Regni Daniae...]\" on p. 182)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads \"militaribus\" (military) where Dreyer prints \"milliaribus\" (miles) - a corrupted word, inconsistent with the parallel \"4 remota\"/\"5 milliaribus\" distances that follow in the same sentence. The translator silently reconstructed the correct sense as \"miles\" so the English is undamaged, but the Latin transcription itself departs from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>Prima Brandeifia, diftans ab urbe 3 milliaribus (TBOO VIII, p. 178)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.178-181 (page images). P.178 line 27 reads 'Prima Brandeifia, diftans ab urbe 3 milliaribus', confirming the corrupted distance unit. P.181, the final two lines before the editorial source-note read 'Tui amantißimus TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria. Non vacabat relegere. Ipfemet difpicias.', confirming the missing postscript exactly as described. Both flagged defects are now resolved and verified against the edition of record.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks excerpter, 179. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Anders Sørensen Vedel", "count": 12, "pos": 12, "prev": "brahe-t_15981230001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Narrates the circumstances of his departure from Denmark, recounting his warning to the King's Chancellor about the threatened fate of his observatory at Uraniborg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990918001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990920001", "d": "1599-09-20", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Henrik Ramel", "ck": "Henrik Ramel", "ck2": "Sophie Brahe", "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Helsingør", "t": "Henrik Ramel → Sophie Brahe — Helsingør [Elsinore], 20 September 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 3 f. (Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELBIURDIGE FRU SOPHIA BRAHE THILL ERICKSSHOLMB, MINN KIERRE SWOGERSKE OCH BESSØNDERLIG GUODE WENN, GANDSCH WENLIGENN THILSCHREFFUENN.</p><p>MINN gandsch wenlig hilßenn nu och altid forßent med Wor Herre. Kierre fru Sophia Brahe, suogerske och beßønderlig guode wenn. Nest en wenlig tacksigelße for megen beuist guode, i mig altid giortt och beuist haffuer, huilckett att forskiulde jeg altid will findis gandsch guoduilligenn med huis i kand haffue ehre och gott aff.</p><p>Giffuendis ether gandsch wenligen att wide, att eders guode skriffuelße och quitantze er mig tilhende kommenn. Anlangendis thre hunder daller och om de beuiste bøger saa kand jeg eder venligen icke forholde, at jeg nu nogen tiidlang hafuer hafft samme 300 daller till rede, och werrit eders bud foruentendis, och eptherdj det icke føre kommen er, haffuer jeg dennom paa andere steder wdgiffuitt. Dog paa det i icke paa eders foretagne reiße therudoffuer schulle forhindris, saa sender jeg min egen thienere till Sten Malteßenn med samme penge, att hand eder skall betalle, naar hand eders nøiagtige quitantz ther paa bekommer, thi det icke alleniste mig widkommer, mens mine suogere och medarfuinger. Derfor bleffue ocsaa en quitantz concipierit paa Widtschøffle, wi ware ther tilsammen siste gang, epther alles woris sambtøcke, och eder tilskickitt, thend jeg gierne war begierendis att motte bliffue aff eder forsiglett och wnderschreffuenn; jeg mig icke heller fordriste kandt, en anden quitantz allene att annamme wden de andere medarffuingers widskaff och sambtøcke.</p><p>Sammeledis wdi eders quitantz, i mig forßent haffuer, formeldis om it breff paa itt thußind daller och rente penge ther aff, och att her till inted schulle werre der paa bethalledt end 500 daller, med slig andere mehr omstendighedt, huilckett mig for min perßonn aldelis er wbeuift, saawelsom brudschat och andit mehre, bønderne schulle wdgifue.</p><p>Setter det derforre i Sten Malteßens handt, att hand paa mine wegne bestiller, att jeg motte faa quitantz paa det breff, salig Henrick Brahe ther om wdgiffuit hafuer, och paa altt andet, som i haffuer hafft att kreffue aff salig fru Lene och nu deris arffuinger. Kierre fru Sophia Brahe, beder jeg gandsch wenligen och gierne, attj hermed ville haffue mig wfortencktt, och at jeg saa bemøder ether med thenne min skriffuelße, thiufflendis icke, att jeg io i slig, ia end vdi større maade hois eder och alle eders med ald tacksom och behagelig tieniste att beuiße will lade mig findis guoduilligenn och wspartt.</p><p>Huad bøgerne erre anlangendis, haffuer jeg epther woris affscheid paa Widschiøffle loffuit att wille eder dennom tilskicke, dog med thend beschedenn, att jeg først wille thalle med konng. maytt. der om, om hans maytt. ware dem begierendis, saa giffuer jeg ether wenligen tilkiende, att jeg wdi chantzelers neruerelse haffuer tilforne adsport hans maytt., da haffuer hand suarett, hand icke haffde sønderlig forstandt paa dennom, eller paste der megit om. Endog wille hans maytt. dennom foruare, eptherdj eders kierre broder haffde hannom samme bøger foreritt; haffde det icke werritt, schulle i dennom retteligens gierne och med en guod wilge bekommedtt. Hermed eder och altt dett i well will Gud almegtigste beffallitt, med mange m. guode netter, jegh och Elße ønscher eder och Thage. Actum Helsingøer thend 20 septemb. a:o 1599.</p><p>Eders quitantz, eders tiener haffde med sig, sender jeg eder och med min thienere.</p><p>Henrich Ramell<br>m: propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born Lady Sophie Brahe of Eriksholm, my dear kinswoman and especial good friend, [this] most amicably written.</b></p><p>My most friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Lady Sophie Brahe, kinswoman and especial good friend. Next [after] a friendly thanks for the much kindness you have always done and shown me — which to repay I shall always be found quite willing, in whatever you may have honour and good from.</p><p>Giving you quite amicably to know that your good letter and quitance have come to my hand. Concerning the <b>three hundred dalers</b>, and about the said books, I cannot amicably withhold from you that I have now for some time had those same 300 dalers ready, and have been awaiting your messenger; and since it did not come sooner, I have paid them out in other places. Yet, so that you may not be hindered on your undertaken journey on that account, I send my own servant to <b>Sten Maltesen</b> with the same money, that he shall pay you when he receives your exact quitance thereon — for it concerns not me alone, but my brothers-in-law and co-heirs. Therefore a quitance was also drafted at <b>Vidskøvle</b>, where we were together last time, by the consent of us all, and sent to you, which I gladly desired might be sealed and signed by you; nor can I venture to accept any other quitance alone, without the other co-heirs' knowledge and consent.</p><p>Likewise in your quitance, which you have sent me, mention is made of a <b>bond for one thousand dalers</b> and the interest-money thereon, and that hitherto only 500 dalers should have been paid on it, with sundry further particulars — which, for my own person, is entirely unknown to me, as is also the <b>bride-tax</b> and other more [dues] which the peasants are to pay.</p><p>I therefore put it into Sten Maltesen's hand, that he arrange on my behalf that I may get a quitance for the bond which the late <b>Henrik Brahe</b> issued thereon, and for all else you have had to claim from the late <b>Lady Lene</b> and now from their heirs. Dear Lady Sophie Brahe, I ask quite amicably and gladly that you would herewith hold me unblamed — and [pardon] that I trouble you thus with this my letter — not doubting that I, in such, yea in even greater measure, will let myself be found quite willing and unsparing toward you and all yours, with all grateful and pleasing service.</p><p>As concerns the <b>books</b>: I had, after our parting at Vidskøvle, promised to send them to you, yet on this condition — that I would first speak with the <b>Royal Majesty</b> about it, [to learn] whether His Majesty desired them. So I give you amicably to know that, in the <b>Chancellor's</b> presence, I previously asked His Majesty; and he answered that he had no special understanding of them, nor cared much about them. Yet His Majesty would keep them, since <b>your dear brother</b> had presented him these same books; had it not been [so], you should rightly, gladly, and with good will have obtained them. Herewith I commend you, and all that you wish well, to God Almighty — with many a thousand good nights, which I and <b>Else</b> wish to you and <b>Tage</b>. Done at Helsingør, the 20th of September, the year 1599.</p><p>Your quitance, which your servant had with him, I send you also with my servant.</p><p>Henrik Ramel,<br>with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient.</b> <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (c. 1550–1610), the Pomeranian-born Danish royal councillor — Tycho's <i>affinis</i> and friend (cf. brahe-t_16000331001, brahe-t_15980628001) — writing to <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (1559–1643), Tycho's astronomer-sister, <b>of Eriksholm</b> (her seat from her first marriage to Otte Thott). Ramel addresses her as <i>swogerske</i> (\"kinswoman / sister-in-law\"), the family-by-marriage tie. The MS is the <b>original letter</b>.</li><li><b>Significance.</b> A <b>Danish family business letter</b> within the Brahe kin-network, on an <b>estate / inheritance settlement</b> in which Sophie and Ramel's group of <i>medarvinger</i> (co-heirs) are jointly involved. It connects to the same circle as the letters to/about Sophie and her son <b>Tage Thott</b> (brahe-t_16000217001, brahe-t_16000115001) — indeed Ramel and his wife <b>Else</b> close by wishing \"many good nights\" to <b>Sophie and Tage</b>.</li><li><b>The money &amp; the co-heirs' quitance.</b> Ramel had <b>300 dalers</b> ready for Sophie but, her messenger being late, paid them out elsewhere; he now sends them via his servant to <b>Sten Maltesen</b> against Sophie's exact receipt — stressing that the matter \"concerns not me alone, but my brothers-in-law and co-heirs,\" so a joint <b>quitance drafted at Vidskøvle</b> (a Brahe-family estate) \"by the consent of us all\" must be sealed and signed; he will not accept a separate one without the co-heirs' consent. Further items: a <b>1,000-daler bond</b> (only 500 paid), the <b>bride-tax (<i>brudskat</i>)</b> and peasant dues, and quitances for a bond issued by the late <b>Henrik Brahe</b> and for claims on the estate of the late <b>Lady Lene</b> and her heirs.</li><li><b>The books and the King (notable).</b> Sophie had wished Ramel to <b>send her certain books</b>; he had promised to, but first asked <b>Christian IV</b> (in the Chancellor's presence) whether the King wanted them. The King \"had no special understanding of them, nor cared much about them,\" <b>yet would keep them — \"since your dear brother [Tycho] had presented him these same books.\"</b> A telling vignette: books Tycho had given Christian IV, which the King retained out of form rather than interest, blocking Sophie's claim. (Provenance-relevant — books from the Brahe orbit in the royal collection.)</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Henrik Ramel (sender); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (recipient); <b>Sten Maltesen</b> (the intermediary holding the money); the <i>medarvinger</i> (co-heirs); the late <b>Henrik Brahe</b>; the late <b>Lady Lene</b> and her heirs; <b>Christian IV</b> and his <b>Chancellor</b> (Christian Friis); <i>\"your dear brother\"</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe</b>; <b>Else</b> (Ramel's wife) and <b>Tage</b> (Thott, Sophie's son).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Helsingøer thend 20 septemb. a:o 1599</i>; <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Early Modern Danish is rendered securely and the substance (the 300-daler payment via Sten Maltesen against a joint co-heirs' quitance; the 1,000-daler bond and the bride-tax/peasant dues; the Henrik Brahe / Lady Lene estate claims; and the books the King kept because Tycho had given them) is clear; graded <b>[B]</b> for the dense legal-financial Danish with several incompletely-identified family parties (<b>Sten Maltesen</b>, the late <b>Henrik Brahe</b>, the late <b>Lady Lene</b>, the <i>medarvinger</i> — exact kinships to firm up) and a few tangled clauses, all flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.154-157 as page images (letter no. 164, Henrik Ramel to Sophie Brahe, spanning pp.155-156). Collated the Danish source_text against the printed page including every sum of money (300 dalers, the 1000-daler bond, the 500 dalers already paid), every name (Sten Maltesen, Henrik Brahe, fru Lene, Else, Tage), the place name Vidskøvle, and the closing Actum Helsingøer thend 20 septemb. a:o 1599 / Henrich Ramell m: propria — all match exactly. English translation checked clause by clause; the books/King's-interest passage, the bride-tax reference, and the quitance mechanics are rendered faithfully. Note: this letter carries published_grade B in the packet, but on full collation against Dreyer's page images no defect of any kind was found, so it is graded A here per the rubric (collated + no defects).</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 3 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Informs Sophie Brahe that 300 dalers he had ready were paid out elsewhere due to her messenger's delay, and arranges for payment through Sten Maltesen instead.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990922001", "d": "1599-09-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus — Benátky [nad Jizerou] (\"Bohemian Venice\"), 22 September 1599 [N.S., autumnal equinox]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>FILIUS TYCHO, quem nunc cum MULAeO in Daniam pro adferendis residuis instrumentis (sic volente Caesare) mitto ..... te socium tertium ijs adiungere, atque in expediendis pro me rebus adiutor esse poteris, eos in Daniam, inque insulam isthic desertam comitari et cum ijs, Deo volente, revertentibus me eodem curru accedere, nam et sic rem gratam praestiteris, utpote cum sciam te in instrumentis maioribus componendis et conservandis, atque in ijs, quae huc mechanice efficienda sunt, solertem esse, eaque rite praeordinare posse, tum quoque urgere, ut sine temporis iactura omnia mature et recte conficiantur. Scripsit iussu Caesaris magnif. Dominus BARVICTIUS supremus Imperij Secretarius, Caesaris intimus, imo apud ipsum factotum, uti loquuntur, ad Dominum HENRICUM RAMELIUM, ut Astronomicarum rerum, quascunque ibi residuas habuero, evectionem promoveat, et ego pariter ad aulae Magistrum hac de re literas dedi etc. .........</p><p>IOHANNEM MULLERUM Mathematicum Electoris Brandeburgici brevi huc venturum expecto, nam cum sponte mihi suam operam, Wittebergam ante meum discessum isthinc transiens, obtulerit, si modo cum Principis sui bona venia id fieri possit, impetravi, ut Caesarea Maiestas hoc nomine ad Electorem Brandeburgicum scriberet (sic enim inter MULLERUM et me convenit) quarum etiam literarum exemplar inter alia apud filium videbis. Est is, praeter ingenium disciplinarum Mathematicarum capax, in Mechanicis huc facientibus non infeliciter versatus. Aderit etiam forte mihi Landgravianus olim Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORUS ROTHMANNUS, quem obijsse existimavimus, sed ex nuperis eius ad ROLLENHAGIUM literis ipsius manum cognovi, et adhuc in vivis esse perlubenter intellexi, ut sic dupliciter mentita sit sine omni pudore Ursina ista et Dithmarsica Bestia in suo famoso scripto, cum de me, quod ob facinus aliquod non leve, absque omni dubio perpetratum, e Dania eruptionem fecerim, vanissimo et impudentissimo ore fingat, sic illum nescio quibus morbis (pudet enim referre) infectum et confectum, qui tamen adhuc vivit et mediocriter bene habet, futiliter blaterat. Scripsi hisce diebus eidem ROTHMANNO, atque eum ad me, si per valetudinem liceret, invitavi. Quin et M. DAVID FABRICIUS Oest-Frisius, qui apprime his disciplinis delectatur, quem etiam tu, cum Wandesburgi apud me esset, novisti, tam ut mihi a privatis concionibus sit, quam ut se collaboratorem, prout a me instructus fuit, horum studiorum praebeat, ut existimo, adveniet. Duos quoque studiosos in triangulis et calculo versatos, sic per IOSTELIUM nostrum expetentes, Witteberga acciri curo, habiturus postmodum plures, prout opus fuerit. Accedent forte ultro et alij. Nam scholam Astronomicam, multorumque in eadem operas, favente coelestium Opifice, Caesare clementissime subsidia largiente, de novo erigere constitui; cuius ususfructus ad posteritatem omnem, uti spero, perseverabit, in Dei fabricae coelestis authoris gloriam, et Caesaris nunquam intermorituram memoriam et laudabilem famam; atque ne nos etiam frustra extitisse talentumque divinitus concessum abscondidisse ac tempus preciosissimum inutiliter, ut nimis multi, quod dolendum est, faciunt, contrivisse videamur. Exoptarem primo quoque tempore quaterniones pauculos, qui in Tomo Progymnasmatum nostrorum primo desiderantur, labore typographico compleri, siquidem ea, quae incuria Hamburgensis typographi perdidit, usui, scis, esse nequeunt, nec Wittebergae ea recudere ob causam tibi notam placuerit. Inveniemus facile rationem, ubi simul fuerimus, qua obstaculis, quoad nunc fieri potest, succurratur, ut liber is brevi in lucem prodire possit. Cogita enim quanto non solum meo damno tot exemplaria tam diu mecum reservo, sed et quam aliorum desiderio minus, dum publicatio differtur, satisfacio. Poterit etiam mox subsequi prima pars secundi Tomi, quae de Cometa Anni 77 nunc cum Apologis huc pertinentibus luculenter agit, donec altera pars reliquorum Cometarum a nobis observatorum descriptiones complectens (quarum fundamenta in promptu sunt) subiungatur. Eclipsis Solis in Iulio huius Anni circa ortum visa meis instrumentis, Pragae quoque in turricula veteris Collegij observata est per IOHANNEM meum Hamburgensem, adstantibus alijs, deprehensusque est Sol, cum elevaretur 2 gradibus, deficientibus saltem totidem minutis, quasi unum digitum de limbo versus Septentrionem inclinato, interventu ☾ amisisse, et postea successive rotunditatem acquisivisse consuetam. Elevatur autem Pragae polus, ut ipsemet, dum ibi morarer, tam ex Sole quam stellis observavi, partibus quinquaginta m. 4½ , sed hîc Benachiae est partium 5 m. 16, in tantum sumus hîc Praga septentrionaliores, nonnihil tamen ad ortum versus Silesiam, ita ut Gorlicium duorum saltem dierum itinere hinc removeatur, dispositi, et axis coelestium revolutionum 6 pene gradibus hîc minus elevata, pauloque rectiorem praebens sphaeram, quam in Danico nostro Uraniburgo, ad observationes coelestes aliquanto magis conducit.</p><p>Caeterum de fera ista Dithmarsica nimis effera et bruta, ut aliqua subiungam, licet indigna sit, cuius recordetur, scias, istam ante aliquot septimanas, prout nuper rescivi, Praga se subduxisse, sive male sibi conscia, et quod iustas poenas per leges formidaret, sive quod aliud sinu suo latenter, more suo, ruminans. Sed investiganda tamen suo tempore per otium atque in ius protrahenda et punienda, quod etiam optimi quique Pragae suadent. Verum de isto calumniatore et vitiatore impudentissimo hîc satis. Caetera ipsemet apud iudicem experietur. Restat, ut scribendi finem faciam, cum plura non vacet, sum enim scribendis non saltem in Daniam sed et hinc inde ad notos et amicos plurimis literis (cumuli partem residuam filius ostendet) quam occupatissimus, ideoque et haec festinanti calamo ad te exarata boni consulas atque desiderijs nostris tuoque commodo morem gere. Videbis hîc locum amoenissimum, in edito et saluberrimo aëre situm.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Venetijs Bohemorum, sic enim arcis huius nomen vernacula gentis lingua interpretatur, ipso die Aequinoctij autumnalis 99.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTIAN LONGOMONTANUS.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>My son Tycho, whom I now send with <b>Mule</b> into Denmark to fetch the remaining instruments (the Emperor so wishing) … [<i>lacuna</i>] you can join yourself to them as a third companion, and be a helper in expediting my affairs — accompany them into Denmark, and to the <b>deserted island</b> [Hven] there, and, with them returning (God willing), come to me on the same wagon; for thus too you will do a welcome thing, since I know <b>you are skilful in assembling and preserving the larger instruments</b>, and in the things that are to be made mechanically here, and can duly pre-arrange them, and also urge that everything be done maturely and rightly, without loss of time. By the Emperor's order, the magnificent <b>Lord Barvitius, supreme Secretary of the Empire, the Emperor's intimate — nay, his <i>factotum</i>, as they say — has written to Lord Henrik Ramel</b> to promote the transport of whatever astronomical things I have remaining there; and I likewise have written to the Court-Master about this, etc. ………</p><p><b>I expect Johann Müller, Mathematician of the Elector of Brandenburg, to come hither shortly</b>; for since he of his own accord offered me his labour (passing through Wittenberg before my departure thence), provided it could be done with his Prince's good leave, I obtained that the Imperial Majesty should write on this account to the Elector of Brandenburg (for so it was agreed between Müller and me) — a copy of which letter, among others, you will see with my son. He is, besides a talent capable of the Mathematical disciplines, not unhappily versed in the Mechanics conducing hither. <b>There will perhaps also be with me the former Landgravian Mathematician, Christoph Rothmann — whom we had thought dead</b>; but from his recent letter to <b>Rollenhagen</b> I recognised his hand, and most gladly learned that he is still among the living — so that <b>that Ursine and Dithmarsian Beast lied doubly, without all shame, in his infamous writing</b>: when, with most empty and most shameless mouth, he feigns of me that I made my breaking-forth from Denmark on account of some grave crime, undoubtedly perpetrated; [and] so futilely blathers that Rothmann was infected and consumed by I know not what diseases (for it shames me to report it) — who yet lives and is moderately well. I have written these days to that same Rothmann, and invited him to me, if his health should allow. Moreover <b>M. David Fabricius the East-Frisian</b>, who is exceedingly delighted with these disciplines — whom you too knew, when he was with me at Wandsbek — will come, as I think, both to be my [chaplain] for private sermons, and to show himself a collaborator in these studies, as he was instructed by me. <b>Two students, too, versed in triangles and calculation</b> — requesting it so through our <b>Jöstel</b> — I am having summoned from Wittenberg, to have more afterward as need requires. Perhaps others too will come of their own accord. <b>For I have resolved to erect anew an Astronomical school, and the labours of many in it</b> — the heavenly Maker favouring, the Emperor most clemently bestowing subsidies — whose usufruct will, I hope, endure to all posterity, to the glory of God the author of the heavenly fabric, and to the never-dying memory and praiseworthy fame of the Emperor; and lest we too should seem to have existed in vain, and to have hidden the talent divinely granted, and to have worn away most precious time uselessly, as too many do (which is to be lamented). <b>I could wish, at the first opportunity, that the few quaternions wanting in the first Volume of our <i>Progymnasmata</i> be completed by the printer's labour</b> — since those which the carelessness of the Hamburg printer destroyed cannot, you know, be used, nor would it be advisable to reprint them at Wittenberg, for the reason known to you. We shall easily find a way, when we are together, by which the obstacles may, so far as can now be done, be relieved, that this book may shortly come to light. For consider with how much loss — not only mine — I keep so many copies so long with me, but also how little I satisfy others' desire, while publication is deferred. <b>The first part of the second Volume can also soon follow, which now treats clearly of the Comet of the year [15]77, with the Apologias pertaining hither</b>, until the other part — comprising the descriptions of the remaining Comets observed by us (whose foundations are at hand) — be subjoined.</p><p><b>The Solar eclipse seen in July of this year about sunrise</b> with my instruments was observed also at Prague, in the little turret of the old College, by <b>my Johann the Hamburger</b>, others standing by; and the Sun was found, when it was elevated 2 degrees (lacking at least as many minutes [of arc]), to have lost about one digit from the limb, inclined toward the North, by the intervention of the <b>Moon [☾]</b>, and afterward gradually to have regained its accustomed roundness. The pole is elevated at <b>Prague</b> — as I myself, while I tarried there, observed both from the Sun and from the stars — <b>50° 4½′</b>; but here at <b>Benátky</b> it is <b>50° 16′</b> [source corrupt; see note] — by so much are we here more northerly than Prague, yet somewhat to the east, toward Silesia (so that <b>Görlitz</b> is removed from here by a journey of but two days); and the axis of the celestial revolutions, here elevated <b>almost 6 degrees less</b> [than at Uraniborg], and offering a somewhat <b>more upright sphere</b> than at our Danish Uraniborg, conduces somewhat more to celestial observations.</p><p>For the rest, concerning that too-savage and brutish Dithmarsian beast — that I may subjoin something, though it be unworthy to be remembered — know that, <b>some weeks ago (as I lately learned), he withdrew himself from Prague</b> — whether ill-conscious to himself, and because he dreaded the just penalties by the laws, or because, in his manner, ruminating secretly something else in his bosom. But it is to be investigated, nonetheless, in time, at leisure, and dragged into court and punished — which all the best men at Prague also advise. But of that most shameless calumniator and corrupter, enough here. <b>The rest he will himself experience before the judge.</b> It remains that I make an end of writing, since there is no leisure for more — for I am most busy writing very many letters, not only into Denmark, but here and there to acquaintances and friends (my son will show the remaining part of the heap); therefore take in good part these too, scratched to you with hurrying pen, and comply with my wishes and your own convenience. You will see here a most pleasant place, set in a high and most healthy air.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at the <b>Venice of the Bohemians</b> — for so the name of this castle is interpreted in the people's vernacular tongue [Benátky] — on the very day of the autumnal Equinox, [15]99.<br>Most loving of you,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (<i>Christianus Longomontanus</i>), Tycho's chief assistant — here urged to join the instrument-fetching party to Denmark/Hven. (Cf. brahe-t_15980925001, where he is Tycho's envoy.)</li><li><b>Significance — the founding of the Benátky \"astronomical school\" and the recruitment of the team (a keystone document).</b></li><li><b>The instrument transport from Hven.</b> Tycho's son and <b>Mule</b> go to Denmark to fetch \"the remaining instruments\" from \"the deserted island\" (Hven), with imperial backing: <b>Barvitius</b> (the supreme Imperial Secretary, \"the Emperor's <i>factotum</i>\") has written to <b>Henrik Ramel</b> to promote it; Longomontanus is asked to go as a third, for his skill with the great instruments.</li><li><b>The new staff (named recruitment).</b> <i>(1)</i> <b>Johann Müller</b>, mathematician of the <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b> (Joachim Friedrich), secured via an imperial letter to the Elector. <i>(2)</i> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> — the former Hesse-Kassel mathematician and Tycho's great correspondent — <b>discovered to be alive</b> (his hand recognised in a letter to <b>Rollenhagen</b>), invited; this <b>doubly disproves Ursus</b>, who had libelled both that Tycho \"fled Denmark for a crime\" <i>and</i> that Rothmann had died of disease. <i>(3)</i> <b>David Fabricius</b>, the East-Frisian pastor-astronomer (whom Longomontanus knew at Wandsbek), to serve as chaplain <i>and</i> collaborator. <i>(4)</i> <b>Two Wittenberg students</b> \"versed in triangles and calculation,\" via <b>Melchior Jöstel</b>. The grand aim: <b>\"to erect anew an Astronomical school\"</b> with imperial subsidy, \"whose usufruct will endure to all posterity.\"</li><li><b>The <i>Progymnasmata</i> printing troubles.</b> Vol. I lacks <b>\"a few quaternions\"</b> that the <b>Hamburg printer's carelessness destroyed</b> (unusable; reprinting at Wittenberg inadvisable \"for the reason known to you\"); Tycho frets over the many undistributed copies. <b>Vol. II part 1 (the Comet of 1577, with the Apologias)</b> can soon follow, then the other comets.</li><li><b>The July 1599 solar eclipse.</b> Observed at sunrise with Tycho's instruments and <b>at Prague (the old College turret) by \"my Johann the Hamburger\"</b>: the Sun at 2° altitude lost about <b>one digit</b> from its northern limb, then regained roundness.</li><li><b>Geography/latitude.</b> Tycho's own determinations: <b>Prague pole-height 50° 4½′</b>, <b>Benátky 50° 16′</b> — and Benátky's pole <b>\"almost 6° lower than Uraniborg\" (55°54′)</b>, giving a <b>\"more upright sphere\"</b> better for observation (the same <i>rectior sphaera</i> rationale as the Egypt plan, brahe-t_16000124001). <b>Görlitz</b> ~2 days east.</li><li><b>Ursus's flight.</b> <b>Ursus (Nicolaus Reimers Bär) had fled Prague \"some weeks ago,\"</b> fearing legal penalties — to be pursued at law (\"the rest he will experience before the judge\"), as Prague's \"best men\" advise. (Cf. the Ursus thread, brahe-t_16000401001 etc.)</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Longomontanus; <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>; <b>Claus Mule</b>; <b>Barvitius</b>; <b>Henrik Ramel</b>; <b>Johann Müller</b> (Brandenburg mathematician — possibly the same as <b>\"Johann the Hamburger\"</b> who observed the eclipse at Prague; flagged); <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>; <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b>; <b>David Fabricius</b>; <b>Melchior Jöstel</b>; two unnamed Wittenberg students; the <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b>; <b>Ursus</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b> (\"Bohemian Venice\"), <b>Prague</b>, <b>Hven</b>, <b>Denmark</b>, <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b>, <b>Görlitz</b>, <b>Silesia</b>, <b>Uraniborg</b>.</li><li><b>Notable points.</b> The dateline pun: <b>\"Venice of the Bohemians\" = Benátky</b> (Czech <i>Benátky</i> = \"Venice\"). The <b>autumnal-equinox dating</b> (\"ipso die Aequinoctij autumnalis 99\") = <b>22 September 1599 [N.S.]</b> (label agrees). The printed source is the older epistolary collection (<i>Epistolae ab anno 1588</i>), not Dreyer's vol. VIII.</li><li><b>Textual notes (flagged).</b> A <b>lacuna</b> in the opening sentence (after \"the Emperor so wishing\"). The Benátky latitude is printed <b>\"partium 5 m. 16\"</b> — a copy corruption for <b>50° 16′</b> (the leading \"0\" dropped; ~11′ north of Prague's 50° 4½′, and ≈ 5°38′ &lt; Uraniborg, matching \"almost 6 degrees\"); read as <b>50° 16′</b> and flagged.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — rich but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the Hven instrument-transport with Barvitius/Ramel backing; the <b>named recruitment of Müller, the living Rothmann, David Fabricius, and the Wittenberg students</b> for a new \"astronomical school\"; the <b>double refutation of Ursus</b> and his flight from Prague; the <i>Progymnasmata</i> printing history; the <b>July 1599 eclipse</b>; and the <b>Prague/Benátky latitudes and \"more upright sphere\"</b>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. Flags: the opening <b>lacuna</b>, the <b>Benátky-latitude</b> copy-corruption (read 50° 16′), and the possible identity of \"Johann the Hamburger\" with <b>Johann Müller</b>.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.181-185 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"; page 5 not needed, letter concludes on p.184). Collated the full letter word-for-word against the printed text: opening/closing formulas, every proper name (Mulaeus, Barvicius, Henricus Ramelius, Iohannes Mullerus, Christophorus Rothmannus, Rollenhagius, David Fabricius, Iostelius, Ursina/Dithmarsica Bestia), every number/date (2 gradibus, 50 gr. 4.5', partium 5 m.16 [as printed - matches Dreyer verbatim, not a source-vs-Dreyer discrepancy], 6 gradibus, Anno 99), and place-names (Wittenberga, Praga, Benachiae/Benatky, Gorlicium, Silesia, Uraniburgo). Source text matches Dreyer's printing exactly throughout; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found. The published English's bracketed glosses ([lacuna], [Hven], [15]77, [Benátky]) are clearly-marked supplements per the audit brief and are not counted as defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "count": 7, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15990820001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980910001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Longomontanus to accompany his son and Mule to Denmark to retrieve remaining instruments, reporting that imperial secretary Barvitius has written to Henrik Ramel to facilitate the transfer.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990922001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990925001", "d": "1599-09-25", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Magdalene Brahe", "ck": "Magdalene Brahe", "ck2": "Mette Kotte", "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Magdalene Brahe → Mette Kotte (Mette Hans Mules) — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 25 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record:<i> Danske Magazin </i>(= Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witnesses: B = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript); C = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter (Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG VELBYRDIG QVINDE, METTE HANS MULIS, BOENDE PAA NISLEFFGAARD, MIN TILFORLADENDE SYNDERLIGE GODE VEN, DETTE VENLIGEN TILHAeNDE.</p><p>MIN gantske wenlig hilsen nu och altidt forsendt med Wor Herre. Kiere Mette Mulis, tilforladendis synderlige gode wen. Nest min gantske wenlig och store taksigelse for alt megit beuist gode, huilckit at forskylde ieg stedse wil findis aff min yderste formue gantske willig och redebon till vdi alle de maader, ieg nogen tid kand giøre nogit, som eder til aere och tieniste kand were. Och skal j ochsaa haffue saa megen stor aere och tak for eders gode skriffuelse, forn ieg bekom fran eder, der wii ware till Witenberig, det war mig saa megit kiert, at ieg fick eders breff, och kunde forfare, horledis eder och eders kiere mand med eders liide. Och skulde ieg wel for lang tid siden haffue skreffuet eder til udigen, mens ieg haffuer siden slet ingen wisse budt kunde fangit till eder; derfore kunde ieg icke lade, at ieg jo nu skulde skriffue eder till udigen, effterdi at mig stedis nu saa wisse budt ind udi Danmarck med eders kiere søn Clauß Mule och min broder Tyge, hwilcke min kiere fader haffuer sendt did ind i landet till hans slecht och gode wenner, hans aerende att bestille. Kiere Mette, anlangendis woris leylighedt her i fremmede land, saa maae j gantske wenligen wide, att baade min kiere fader, moder, och wii sydskende liider, Gud were loffuit, alle well. Gud allermechtigste vnde os altidt de samme tidende fra eder och alle eders att høre och spørge, dett er os gantske kiert att fornemme. Och er min kiere fader om St. Hans dags tiide med min kiere moder och os alle dragen fran Wittenberig och op till Prag till den romske keyserlig mayestat effter hans mayestats naadigst begiering, och er hand der well bleffuen antagen och naadigst forsynit, saa att hand lader sig well nøye. Och haffuer keyserlig mayestat vndt hanum ett aff sine slotte, liggendis i lante Bemen 5 mile fran Prag, att boe paa, huilckit hans mayestat lod hannum iblant tre aff sine slotte selff vduelle. Och haffuer hans mayestat naadigst foraerit min kiere fader tho tusend gylden, och loffuit hannum til aarlig pension tre tusend gylden først att begynde med. Saa att hans mayestat er min kiere fader med all keyserlig gunst och naade well beuogen. Wor Herre giffue widere lycke och benedidelse. Kiere Mette, dette kunde ieg icke lade att giffue eder vdi en god mening tilkiende, helst effterdi ieg weed, att j er woris gode wen och gierne seer, att det gaaer os wel, for huilcken eders gode willie j skal were paa det allerhøyeste betacket och wii aff woris ringe formue vdigien forskylde wil. Disligeste, kiere Mette, maa j ochsaa wenligen wide, att min kiere fader loffuet och tilsagde eders kiere søn Claus Mule, att dett første Gud vil, hand kommer fran Danmarck til os vdigien, da will hand forskaffe hannum med en første vdi Italien paa sin bekostning och gierne forhielpe hannum til det beste, huor hannum mueligt kand were, baade for eders och hans egen skyld. Og vdi alle de maader min kiere moder och wii syskend kand giøre nøget, som eder eller hanum eller nogen aff eders til willige och tieniste kand were, wille wii det aff woris yderste formue hiertens gierne giøre, huilcket och i alle maade baade aff eder och hanum wel forskyldt er. Kiere Mette, wil ieg icke nu lenger opholde eder med denne min widtløfftige schriffuelse, thii ieg tencker well, att eders søn Claus Mule schriffuer eder all widere beskening til om min kiere faders och woris leylighedt, och ochsaa eders svoger doctor Niels Krag, thii min fader haffuer selff schreffuit hanum widtløfftelig nock til der vd om. Och will ieg her med nu och altidt haffue eder den euige alsommechtigste gode Gudt, med all langvarendis sundhed, lycke och benedidelse, med alt huis eder kiert er, til siel och liff befalit. Och lader min kiere fader, moder, syskende och ieg hilse eders kiere mand och eder med alle eders kiere børn, med mange m. maal m. gode netter.</p><p>Datum paa det keyserlig slot Benatica den 25. septembris stilo novo, anno d:ni 1599.<br>Magdalene Brahe<br>med min egen handt.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, well-born woman Mette, [wife of] Hans Mule, dwelling at Nislevgård, my trusted especial good friend, [this] amicably to hand.</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Mette Mule, trusted especial good friend. Next [after] my quite friendly and great thanks for all the much kindness shown, which to repay I shall always be found — to my utmost ability — quite willing and ready, in every way I can ever do anything that may be to your honour and service. And you shall also have so much great honour and thanks for your good letter, which I received from you when we were at <b>Wittenberg</b>; it was so dear to me that I got your letter, and could learn how you and your dear husband, with yours, fare. And I should well, long ago, have written to you again, but I have since been able to get no sure messenger to you at all; therefore I could not forbear writing to you again now, since so sure a messenger is now available to me [going] into Denmark — with <b>your dear son Claus Mule and my brother Tyge</b>, whom my dear father has sent there into the country, to his kin and good friends, to attend to his business. Dear Mette, concerning our circumstances here in a foreign land, you may quite amicably know that <b>both my dear father, mother, and we siblings fare, God be praised, all well</b>. May God Almighty grant us always to hear the same tidings from you and all yours; it is quite dear to us to perceive [them]. And my dear father, about <b>St. John's Day</b> [24 June], with my dear mother and us all, <b>moved from Wittenberg up to Prague</b>, to the Roman Imperial Majesty, at His Majesty's gracious request; and he has there been well received and graciously provided for, so that he is well content. And the Imperial Majesty has <b>granted him one of his castles, lying in the land of Bohemia, 5 miles from Prague, to dwell in — which His Majesty let him himself choose from among three of his castles</b>. And His Majesty has graciously <b>presented my dear father with two thousand gulden, and promised him as annual pension three thousand gulden to begin with</b> — so that His Majesty is well disposed toward my dear father with all imperial favour and grace. May Our Lord give further fortune and blessing. Dear Mette, this I could not forbear to give you to know, in good intent, especially since I know you are our good friend and gladly see that it goes well with us — for which good will you shall be most highly thanked, and we will repay it according to our humble ability. Likewise, dear Mette, you may also amicably know that <b>my dear father promised and assured your dear son Claus Mule that, the first [moment] God wills it — [when] he comes back from Denmark to us — he will provide for him with a prince in Italy at his own expense, and gladly help him to the best</b> he can, both for your sake and his own. And in every way my dear mother and we siblings can do anything to the willing service of you, or him, or any of yours, we will do it most heartily, to our utmost ability — which is also in every way well deserved both by you and by him. Dear Mette, I will not now detain you longer with this lengthy letter of mine, for I think your son Claus Mule writes you all further news of my dear father's and our circumstances, and also <b>your brother-in-law Doctor Niels Krag</b>, for my father has himself written to him fully enough about it. And herewith I will, now and always, commend you to the eternal, almighty good God, with all long-lasting health, fortune, and blessing, with all that is dear to you, in soul and body. And my dear father, mother, siblings, and I bid greet your dear husband and you, with all your dear children, with many a thousand-thousand-thousand good nights.</p><p>Given at the imperial castle Benátky, the 25th of September, New Style, in the year of the Lord 1599.<br>Magdalene Brahe,<br>with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender — Magdalene Brahe.</b> <b>Magdalene Brahe</b> (b. 1574), <b>Tycho's eldest daughter</b>, writing <b>in her own hand</b> from Benátky. A rare and valuable autograph letter from within Tycho's <i>familia</i> (and one of few surviving in a daughter's voice).<br>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Mette [Kotte], wife of Hans Mule, of Nislevgård</b> — the <b>mother of Claus Mule</b> (Claudius Mulenus / Nicolaus Mulaeus), Tycho's household-member and agent (cf. brahe-t_15980910001, brahe-t_16000919001), and (per this letter) <b>kinswoman of Niels Krag</b>.<br>- <b>Significance — a prime source for Tycho's imperial settlement.</b> Written just three months after the move, the letter states the <b>terms of Rudolf II's patronage</b> with unusual concreteness:<br>  - The family <b>moved from Wittenberg to Prague \"about St. John's Day [24 June 1599]\"</b> at the Emperor's request, and Tycho \"is well content.\"<br>  - The Emperor <b>granted Tycho one of his castles in Bohemia, 5 miles from Prague — which Tycho was let choose from among three</b> (i.e. <b>Benátky</b>, chosen over two others).<br>  - The Emperor <b>gave Tycho 2,000 gulden outright and promised an annual pension of 3,000 gulden \"to begin with.\"</b><br>  This is one of the <b>earliest and most direct testimonies to the famous 3,000-gulden imperial salary</b> and the choice of Benátky — the more authoritative for coming from inside the household.<br>- <b>The Claus Mule / Italy promise.</b> Magdalene relays that <b>Tycho promised to send Mette's son Claus Mule to a prince in Italy at his own expense</b>, once Claus returns from Denmark — a glimpse of Tycho's patronage of his servants (cf. the parallel Italian plans for his own son, brahe-t_16000504001).<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> <b>Magdalene Brahe</b> (sender); <b>Mette [Kotte]</b> and her husband <b>Hans Mule</b> of Nislevgård; <b>Claus Mule</b> (their son, co-courier with Tyge to Denmark); <b>Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger, Magdalene's brother); <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (\"my dear father\"); <b>Kirsten Jørgensdatter</b> (\"my dear mother,\" unnamed); <b>Niels Krag</b> (<i>Doctor Niels Krag</i>, Mette's <i>svoger</i> — kinsman/brother-in-law; cf. brahe-t_15980628001, brahe-t_16010311001); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benatica den 25. Septembris stilo novo, anno d:ni 1599</i>; explicitly <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected from 'your kinsman Doctor Niels Krag' to 'your brother-in-law Doctor Niels Krag', restoring the precise sense of Danish 'svoger' (Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.153).</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Early Modern Danish in a fluent household hand, read securely; the substance (the autograph by Tycho's daughter Magdalene; the family well; the St. John's-Day move from Wittenberg to Prague; <b>the Emperor's grant of Benátky chosen from three castles, the 2,000-gulden gift and the 3,000-gulden annual pension</b>; the Claus Mule / Italy promise; Niels Krag as Mette's kinsman) is clear and of the first documentary value for Tycho's imperial settlement. No textual crux; the mother is left unnamed in-text (Kirsten Jørgensdatter supplied in apparatus, flagged).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish letter against TBOO XIV pp.152-154 read as page images. The castle grant (5 mile fran Prag), the sums (tho tusend gylden = 2000 gulden; tre tusend gylden = 3000 gulden annual pension), St. Hans dags tiide, and the closing date (25 septembris stilo novo, 1599) all match Dreyer exactly and are correctly rendered. One MISTRANSLATION found: 'svoger' (brother-in-law) rendered as the vaguer 'kinsman'.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Danish 'svoger' specifically denotes 'brother-in-law'; the English broadens this to the generic 'kinsman', losing a precise, verifiable family relationship in a letter that is otherwise a careful genealogical/family report. At: “your kinsman Doctor Niels Krag”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified 'eders svoger doctor Niels Krag' against the Dreyer page image (TBOO XIV p.153); the Danish source text was already correct, so only the English translation required correction. No uncertainty remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["B København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium. Transscript", "C København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, Langebeks Ekscerpter. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Mette Mule for her letter received at Wittenberg, apologises for the delay in reply, and reports that brother Tyge and Claus Mule are travelling to Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990925001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990926001", "d": "1599-09-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Cramer", "ck": "Daniel Cramer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Daniel Cramer — Benátky [nad Jizerou] (\"Bohemian Venice\"), 26 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Place note.</b> The catalogue label says \"Wittenberg,\" but the <b>source dateline reads <i>ex arce Caesarea Benachia sive Venetijs Bohemorum</i> — Benátky</b> (header follows the source per §4.1). The label location is an <b>error</b>; flagged for the label audit.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD DANIELEM CRAMERUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>RESCRIPSI tibi, clarissime et eruditissime Domine Doctor CRAMERE, paulo antequam Witeberga discederem, quas te accepisse reor. De meis autem rebus, postquam inde abiens in Bohemiam ex clementissimo mandato et evocatione augustissimi Imperatoris me contulerim, quia nimis longum foret hîc sigillatim omnia enarrare, iussi describi exemplum nuperarum ad OLIGERUM ROSAeKRANTZIUM nostrum a me datarum, quae historiolam quandam eorum, quae mihi hîc evenerunt, continent, cuius te facio participem. Inde per circumstantias omnia hauries; cum enim tam arcta sit inter OLIGERUM et te amicitia, is, quod descriptum suarum literarum tibi communicem (secretum tamen habendum, nec nisi secretis amicis ostendendum) minime aegre laturus est. Quare de meis negotijs nihil hîc addo, solummodo Deo immortali gratias ago. Agasque tu et omnes pij probique mecum, quod omnia bene et ex voto successerint.</p><p>Caeterum est aliud te non minima ex parte concernens, quod te non caelandum duxi. Cum Witebergae essem, accesserat eo Danus quidam studiosus mihi familiariter notus, BARTOLDUS IVARI MULAeUS dictus, qui cum Barone quodam Svetico STENONE BIELCHE meo consanguineo accesserat, cumque per aliquot dies ibi mecum conversarentur, accidit, ut STENO ille DUNCANI Scoti mentionem faceret, ambo enim illi Helmstadij studiorum caussa, unde etiam venerant, aliquamdiu fuere. Ego mox subijciens plagium eius in me eidem Baroni, praesente BARTHOLDO illo, aperui, cumque miraretur et quasi subhaesitaret, produxi tuas litteras ad OLIGERUM ROSAeKRANTZIUM prolixe ea de re scriptas ijsque legendas dedi. Nescio vero quî factum sit, quod, dum in alterum diem eas secum servaret Danicus ille studiosus, sibiipsi exemplum inde tacite exaravit, quod, cum Helmstadium venisset, Scotulo isti monstravit. Quomodo autem is acceperit, ex ipsius BARTOLDI literis, quas ante paucos dies nactus sum, verbotenus referam. »Deinde quoad Doctoris CRAMERI literas, eas DUNCANO exhibui, reticens tamen unde perceperim. Ibi homo coepit impietatem miselli asini (sic enim criminabatur) adeo culpare, ut eundem ingratissimum erga praeceptorem cuculum vocitaret, simulque se Magnif. tuae excusans, meque ad ordinem lectionum, quem hisce adiunxi, remittens, ubi honestissimam vestri mentionem fecisse gloriabatur«. Haec ille. Quae vero insinuat de ordine praelectionum impresso unâque transmisso, e quibus isthic verbis DUNCANUS se purgare praesumit, ea sub titulo Philosophicarum praelectionum sic habent: »DUNCANUS LIDDELIUS Scotus D. publice proponit doctrinam sinuum et triangulorum, qua absoluta, theorias Planetarum, secundum hypothesin PTOLEMAeI et COPERNICI et illam mundani systematis hypothesin, quam describit TYCHO BRAHE lib. 2 de aethereis Phaenomenis.« Ista iste, egregia sane et honestissima de me mentio, imo vulpeculae potius dolus; cum enim sibi conscius sit, se meas hypotheses sibi apud alios clam venditasse, et sic plagium commisisse, cum publice non audeat idem facere, neque meas esse fateri vult, ne contradicat ijs, quae prius commentus est, ideoque generaliter et astute loquendo, dicit saltem eas hypotheses, quas ego libro illo secundo describo; potest enim aliquis etiam ab alio inventa describere, nec ob id sua esse. Eadem vafritie usus est in thematibus quibusdam anno 95 Helmstadij editis, ubi inter Parerga Mathematica (sic enim vocat et recte, nam vere Parerga tractat, et de re inperspecta nugatur) propositione secunda sic habet: »Cum tres discrepantes hypotheses sint, quibus apparentiae coelestes solvuntur et explicantur, quarum una est PTOLEMAeI, altera COPERNICI, tertia est illa, cuius meminit TYCHO BRAHE lib. de Cometa anni 77, dubitatur, an aliqua ex his omnibus diversa dari possit, deinde quaenam harum apparentijs magis congruat.« Vide quam sibi similis et hîc et istic, idque ob eandem, quam dixi, caussam; o! astutule et nasutule Scotule. At non satis recordatur, uti oportebat mendacem memorem esse. In intimatione, quam Decanus Facultatis Medicae in Academia Iulia FRANCISCUS PARCOVIUS, sub quo LIDDELIUS in Doctorem promovit, ... haec verba, se conscio et forte etiam praescribente, inserta esse, publiceque edita et affixa, quae postquam LIDDELIJ mentionem, utinam tam veram quam pomposam, fecisset, sic proloquitur: »Quod enim ad Mathesin attinet, non solum in veterum hypothesi bene versatus est, sed et recentiorem laboriosam et operosam COPERNICI hypothesin optime novit«, in qua quicquid consecutus sum, ipsi debere me ingenue fateor. Quin et aliam novam hypothesin, quam publice et privatim docuit, licet alterius nomine usurpetur, adinvenit, cuius rei multi eius discipuli clarissimum ei dant testimonium. Sed addo descriptum totius intimationis; misissem impressum exemplar, sed cum unicum saltem habeam, eo carere nequeo. Annon ibi satis aperte proditur DUNCANUM mea inventa sibi arrogare? Neque enim excusare se potest, quod se inscio haec a suo promotore ascripta sint; vel si sic, cur non inficiatus est atque se invito factum protestatus? cur se sic alienis plumis ornari cornicula ista passa est? Sed dispiciemus suo tempore Deo volente de his latius. Interea huius pusilli Scoti arrogantiam et insolentiam ex alto despiciemus. Si in viros cordatos et eruditos atque ipso longe meliores scurriliter debacchari et mea de caussa eos misellos asinos ingratissimosque cuculos vocitare, quod veritatem quaesiti non subticeant, perrexerit, efficiam ut publice plagij accusatus et convictus secundum ius luat, id est infamis fiat, nec amplius irritet crabrones. Tu interim pro te, quae vera et iusta nosti, meditare atque consigna, et si lubuerit, in uno vel altero quaternione in publicum ede, tui meique defensionis gratia, ut habeat iste biliosus Scotulus, quod ipsum mordeat atque remordeat, si possit, donec illi occludatur os. Si Doctor NICOLAUS CRAGIUS, in Dania Historicus, quem nosti, velit aperte fateri, quae hac de re novit, quod eum etiam libenter facturum spero, non longa erit disputatio, an LIDDELIUS istas hypotheses a me didicerit, an vero ego ab illo. Assidebat enim mensae in arce olim Uraniburgica idem Doctor, cum hasce hypotheses LIDDELIO, cui nunquam tale quid antea, ne per somnium quidem, in mentem venerat, aperui. Quin et ipsi Doctori NICOLAO, ob sinceram inter nos amicitiam, iam diu antea designationem earundem hypothesium in patenti charta, prout a me sedulo expetierat, concesseram, uti ipsemet non invite fatebitur, et quod omnium maxime rem arguit, ipsae Liddelianae ad me datae literae ipsiusque manu scriptae, fraudem et plagium eius testabuntur. de istulo nunc satis. Tu quid tui sit officij, facile absque me monitore nosti.</p><p>Carmina quaedam in mei gratiam a varijs conscripta addam, si ea denuo exarare tempus permiserit, sin minus, habebis alias. Interea tu etiam aliqua ulterius pro tua arguta vena meditare, et Caesaris in me atque atque ea quae tracto studia clementissimam benignitatem collauda.</p><p>Vale atque brevi rescribe.<br>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benachia sive Venetijs Bohemorum Anno 99 die 26. Septembris stylo renovato.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 187; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Alter plagiarius et Mastiges meus impurissima fera Ditmarsica ante paucas hebdomadas Praga se subduxit, deserta uxore, quam nuper duxerat, uti perhibent, meretricula, sive quod male sibi conscius esset, et poenas secundum leges metueret, quas etiam vix effugiet, sive quod alibi latitando nova ruminetur mendacia novasque calumnias, pro stabiliendis prioribus, uti fieri solet, sicque decuplo pluribus opus erit, sed obstruetur et illi, Deo volente, os, ita ut mallet facta infecta esse.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO DANIEL CRAMER.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>I wrote back to you, most renowned and learned Lord Doctor Cramer, a little before I left Wittenberg, which I suppose you received. But of my affairs — after, departing thence, I betook myself into Bohemia by the most clement mandate and summons of the most august Emperor — since it would be too long to narrate everything severally here, <b>I have had a copy made of my recent [letter] to our Holger Rosenkrantz, which contains a certain little history of what has befallen me here</b>, of which I make you partaker. Thence you will draw everything through its circumstances; for since the friendship between Holger and you is so close, he will by no means take it ill that I share a copy of his letter with you (yet to be held secret, and shown to none but secret friends). Wherefore of my affairs I add nothing here, only I give thanks to immortal God. And do you, and all the pious and upright, give thanks with me, that all has succeeded well and according to my prayer.</p><p>But there is another thing, concerning you in no small part, which I judged should not be hidden from you. When I was at Wittenberg, there had come thither a certain Danish student familiarly known to me, called <b>Bartold Ivari Mule</b>, who had come with a certain Swedish Baron, <b>Sten Bielke, my kinsman</b>; and when they conversed with me there for some days, it happened that Sten made mention of <b>Duncan the Scot</b> — for both of them had been a while at <b>Helmstedt</b> for study's sake, whence too they had come. I, soon adding [the matter], opened to that Baron, in Bartold's presence, <b>[Duncan's] plagiarism against me</b>; and when he wondered and as it were hesitated, I produced <b>your letter to Holger Rosenkrantz, written at length on this matter</b>, and gave it them to read. But I know not how it came about that, while that Danish student kept it overnight, he <b>silently made a copy for himself</b>, which, when he came to Helmstedt, <b>he showed to that little Scot</b>. How [Duncan] received it, I will report word for word from Bartold's own letter, which I got a few days ago: <i>\"Then as for Doctor Cramer's letter, I showed it to Duncan, concealing, however, whence I got it. There the man began so to blame the impiety of the 'wretched little ass' (for so he slandered [Cramer]) that he called him 'a most ungrateful cuckoo toward his preceptor'; and at the same time, excusing himself to Your Magnificence, and referring me to the order of lectures which I append, where he boasted he had made most honourable mention of you.\"</i> So far he.</p><p>What he intimates about the <b>printed lecture-order</b>, sent along — by which words Duncan presumes to clear himself — runs thus, under the title of Philosophical lectures: <i>\"<b>Duncan Liddel</b> the Scot, D[octor], publicly proposes the doctrine of sines and triangles; which finished, the theories of the Planets, according to the hypothesis of <b>Ptolemy</b> and <b>Copernicus</b>, and <b>that hypothesis of the world-system which Tycho Brahe describes in book 2 of the aethereal Phenomena</b>.\"</i> This [is] his — a fine and most honourable mention of me, forsooth; nay, rather a little fox's trick. For since he is conscious that <b>he secretly peddled my hypotheses to others as his own, and so committed plagiarism</b>, [and] since he dares not do the same publicly, nor will he admit they are mine (lest he contradict what he earlier fabricated), therefore, speaking generally and cunningly, he says only \"those hypotheses which I describe in that second book\" — for one can also describe another's inventions, without their being on that account his own. He used the same craft in certain theses published at Helmstedt in [15]95, where among \"<b>Mathematical Parerga</b>\" (so he calls them, and rightly, for he truly handles by-works, and trifles about a matter not grasped), in the second proposition he has: <i>\"Since there are three discrepant hypotheses by which the celestial appearances are solved and explained — one of Ptolemy, another of Copernicus, the third that which Tycho Brahe mentions in his book on the Comet of '77 — it is doubted whether any other, different from all these, can be given; and then which of these agrees more with the appearances.\"</i> See how like himself both here and there, and for the same cause I have said; O cunning and sharp-nosed little Scot! But he does not remember enough that <b>a liar ought to have a good memory</b>. In <b>the announcement which Franz Parcovius, Dean of the Medical Faculty in the Academia Julia [Helmstedt], under whom Liddel was promoted to Doctor</b>, [posted] — these words, with [Liddel's] knowledge, and perhaps even at his dictation, were inserted, and publicly published and posted; which, after making mention of Liddel (would it were as true as it is pompous), speaks thus: <i>\"For as to Mathematics, he is not only well versed in the ancients' hypothesis, but knows excellently the more recent, laborious, and elaborate hypothesis of Copernicus\"</i> — in which \"whatever I have attained, I frankly confess I owe to him. Moreover <b>he discovered another new hypothesis, which he taught publicly and privately, though it is usurped under another's name; of which thing many of his disciples give him the clearest testimony.</b>\" But I add a copy of the whole announcement; I would have sent the printed copy, but since I have only one, I cannot do without it. <b>Is it not there openly betrayed that Duncan arrogates my inventions to himself?</b> For he cannot excuse himself that these things were ascribed by his promoter without his knowledge; or, if so, why did he not deny it, and protest it was done against his will? Why did that little crow let itself be so adorned with others' feathers? But we shall look into these things more amply in time, God willing. Meanwhile we shall look down from on high upon this little Scot's arrogance and insolence. If he goes on scurrilously raving against sensible and learned men far better than himself, and on my account calling them \"wretched asses\" and \"most ungrateful cuckoos\" — because they do not keep silent the truth they are asked — <b>I will bring it about that, publicly accused and convicted of plagiarism, he pay according to the law — that is, become infamous</b> — and no longer provoke the hornets. You, meanwhile, meditate and set down for yourself what you know to be true and just; and if you please, <b>publish it, in a quaternion or two, in defence of yourself and me</b>, that this bilious little Scot may have something to bite and bite back at, if he can — until his mouth is shut.</p><p><b>If Doctor Nicolaus Cragius [Niels Krag], the Historian in Denmark, whom you know, will openly confess what he knows of this matter — which I hope he will gladly do — there will be no long dispute whether Liddel learned those hypotheses from me, or I from him.</b> For that same Doctor was sitting at table, in the once-Uraniborg castle, <b>when I opened these hypotheses to Liddel — to whom never anything of the sort had before come into mind, not even in a dream.</b> Moreover, to Doctor Niels himself, for the sincere friendship between us, I had long before granted <b>a designation of these same hypotheses on an open sheet, as he had diligently sought from me</b> — as he himself will not unwillingly confess; and, what most of all proves the matter, <b>Liddel's own letters to me, written in his own hand, will testify to his fraud and plagiarism.</b> Of the little fellow, enough now. You easily know your own duty without my prompting.</p><p>Some poems written for me by various [hands] I will add, if time permit me to copy them out anew; if not, you shall have them another time. Meanwhile do you too meditate something further, by your sharp vein, and praise the Emperor's most clement kindness toward me and the studies I pursue.</p><p>Farewell, and write back shortly.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, or the Venice of the Bohemians, the year [15]99, the 26th day of September, by the renewed [New] style.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> My other plagiarist and tormentor [Mastiges], that most impure Dithmarsian beast [Ursus], a few weeks ago withdrew himself from Prague, having deserted the wife — a little harlot, as they report — whom he had lately married: whether because he was ill-conscious to himself, and feared the penalties according to the laws (which he will scarcely escape), or because, lurking elsewhere, he is ruminating new lies and new calumnies to prop up the former ones (as is wont to happen), and so ten times more will be needed — but his mouth too will be stopped, God willing, so that he would rather his deeds were undone.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Daniel Cramer</b> (1568–1637), the Pomeranian theologian-poet and Tycho's polemical ally (cf. brahe-t_16000401001), here enlisted against Liddel as later against Craig/Ursus.</li><li><b>Significance — the Duncan Liddel plagiarism dossier (the corpus's fullest statement of that dispute).</b> This letter assembles Tycho's whole case against <b>Duncan Liddel (1561–1613)</b>, the Scottish mathematician-physician at <b>Helmstedt (Academia Julia)</b>, for <b>claiming the Tychonic geo-heliocentric world-system as his own</b>. (It fixes the \"Duncan\" of brahe-t_16000401001 — with Niels Krag's testimony — as <b>Duncan Liddel</b>.) The evidence Tycho marshals:</li><li><b>The Bartold Mule episode.</b> At Wittenberg, the Danish student <b>Bartold Ivari Mule</b>, travelling with <b>Sten Bielke</b> (a Swedish baron, Tycho's kinsman), heard Tycho's charge and read <b>Cramer's letter to Holger Rosenkrantz</b>; he secretly copied it and showed it to Liddel at Helmstedt — whose contemptuous reaction (calling Cramer \"a wretched ass,\" \"an ungrateful cuckoo\") Tycho quotes from Mule's letter.</li><li><b>Liddel's evasive \"honourable mention.\"</b> His <b>printed lecture-catalogue</b> (\"the planetary theories according to Ptolemy, Copernicus, and <b>that hypothesis which Tycho Brahe describes in book 2 of the aethereal Phenomena</b>\") and his <b>1595 Helmstedt theses (\"Mathematical Parerga,\" prop. 2)</b> both name Tycho's system only <i>generally</i> — a \"fox's trick,\" Tycho says, to avoid admitting he had peddled it as his own.</li><li><b>The damning promotion-notice.</b> The doctoral announcement by Dean <b>Franz Parcovius</b>, \"with Liddel's knowledge and perhaps dictation,\" claims <b>Liddel \"discovered another new hypothesis … though it is usurped under another's name\"</b> — i.e. publicly crediting Liddel with the Tychonic system. Tycho: \"Is it not openly betrayed that Duncan arrogates my inventions?\"</li><li><b>The witnesses.</b> <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius)</b>, the Danish royal historian, <b>sat at Tycho's Uraniborg table when Tycho revealed the hypotheses to Liddel</b> — \"to whom never had such a thing come into mind, not even in a dream\"; Tycho had also given Krag <b>a diagram of the system on an open sheet</b>. And <b>Liddel's own letters</b> will prove the plagiarism. (Krag's verbatim testimony is quoted in brahe-t_16000401001.)</li><li><b>The threat and the commission.</b> Tycho threatens to have Liddel <b>\"publicly accused and convicted of plagiarism … become infamous\"</b>; he asks Cramer to <b>publish a defence (a quaternion or two)</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The master document of the <b>Duncan Liddel priority dispute</b> — pair with brahe-t_16000401001 (the Niels Krag testimony) and route alongside the parallel <b>Ursus</b> dispute (brahe-t_16000911001 etc.). \"Book 2 of the aethereal Phenomena\" / \"book on the Comet of '77\" = Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (1588), where the geo-heliocentric system was first published.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Daniel Cramer; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (the shared letter); <b>Bartold Ivari Mule</b>; <b>Sten Bielke</b>; <b>Duncan Liddel</b>; <b>Franz Parcovius</b> (Helmstedt medical dean); <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius)</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b> (\"Venice of the Bohemians\"), <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Helmstedt</b> (Academia Julia), <b>Hven/Uraniborg</b>, Denmark.</li><li><b>Place/calendar (protocol §3, §4.1).</b> Source dateline <i>ex arce Caesarea Benachia sive Venetijs Bohemorum … die 26. Septembris <b>stylo renovato</b></i> → <b>Benátky, 26 September 1599 [N.S.]</b>. The <b>catalogue label's \"Wittenberg\" is a location error</b> (contradicted by the dateline); led with the source and flagged for the label audit.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript reporting Ursus had fled Prague, restored from Dreyer VIII p.187 (appended to the Tycho→Cramer letter of 16/26 Sept 1599; dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Postscript closing idiom corrected from 'facta insecta esse' to 'facta infecta esse' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.187 (the classical tag 'facta infecta' = deeds made undone); the English translation was already correct and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the shared Holger-letter \"little history\"; the <b>Bartold Mule episode</b> and Liddel's quoted reaction; the two evasive <b>Liddel self-citations</b> (lecture-catalogue + 1595 <i>Parerga</i>); the <b>Parcovius promotion-notice</b> crediting Liddel with the system \"usurped under another's name\"; the <b>Niels Krag</b> eyewitness/diagram evidence and Liddel's own incriminating letters; and the threat of a plagiarism conviction) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated, with Tycho's invective rendered without sharpening. Flag: the <b>label's wrong location</b> (Wittenberg vs the dateline's Benátky), resolved to Benátky per §4.1.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the whole letter, including the postscript marked 'restored from Dreyer', visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 184-187, read within the supplied 183-188 slice (p.183 is the tail of a prior Tycho-to-Longomontanus letter, p.188 the continuation of the next Tycho-to-Scaliger letter, both checked to confirm the boundary). Verified the whole Bartold Ivari Mule / Sten Bielke / Duncan Liddel plagiarism narrative word for word, including the two embedded quotations (Bartold's letter, and Duncan's 1595 Helmstedt 'Parerga Mathematica' theses) and the Franciscus Parcovius encomium, plus the names Niels Krag/Cragius, Franciscus Parcovius, Helmstedt/Helmstadium -- all match Dreyer exactly. Gave particular scrutiny to the embedded first-person clause on p.186, 'in qua quicquid consecutus sum, ipsi debere me ingenue fateor', since an abrupt person-shift there is exactly the shape of the pipeline's known pronoun-flip defect (a self-boast turned into a claim about Tycho): the source_text matches Dreyer verbatim at this point, and the English preserves the same ambiguous person-shift already present in the Latin (Parcovius's encomium itself lurches from third person to an embedded first-person avowal and back) rather than introducing a new inversion -- so this is not counted as a translator-introduced defect. Confirmed the restored postscript (the Ursus/'Mastiges' paragraph) is genuinely present on Dreyer p.187 and correctly reinstated. One TEXT_ERROR found, in the postscript's closing words ('insecta' for Dreyer's 'infecta'); does not affect the English, which already carries the correct sense. No fabrication, omission, or sense-changing mistranslation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source Latin misprints the classical idiom 'facta infecta' (deeds made undone -- from the Roman-comedy tag 'quod factum infectum fieri nequit') as the nonsensical 'facta insecta'; Dreyer p.187 reads 'infecta'. The published English already renders the correct sense ('his deeds were undone'), so the translation itself is not at fault -- only our stored source_text is corrupted. Dreyer prints: <i>ita ut mallet facta infecta eße</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.187, postscript, lines 8-10), read visually in the slice PDF. The plate clearly shows 'facta infecta eße', confirming the audit's claim. Only the Latin postscript was touched; nothing else in the letter was altered.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Daniel Cramer", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15980524001", "next": "brahe-t_16000401001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980524001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Refers Cramer to an enclosed copy of his letter to Holger Rosenkrantz for news of his situation, requesting confidentiality, and hints at a separate matter personally concerning Cramer.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990926001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15990929001", "d": "1599-09-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Borgmestre og råd i Hamborg", "ck": "Borgmestre og råd i Hamborg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → The Burgomasters and Council of Hamburg — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 29 September 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Hamburg, Staatsarchiv, Ad Cl. VII Lib. Ea Pars 2 (Transito u. Porto-franco), original letter, endorsed<i> Lectum in senatu 21. April a:o 1600 </i>(with an attached inventory slip; see apparatus)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEN ERENUESTEN, ACHTBARN VND WOLWEISEN HERRNN N. N., BURGERMAISTERN VND RATHMANNEN DER LÖBLICHENN STADTT HAMBURG, MEINEN JNSONDERN GUNSTIGENN HERRN VND GUTTEN FREUNDEN.</p><p>ERENUESTE, achtbare, wolweise, erbare, jnsondere gunstige herrnn vnnd gutte freund, denselbenn sein mein gantz geflissene jederzeitt willige dienst möglichem vleiß nach zuuor. Vnnd kan E.E. vnnd W.W. freindtlicher meinung zuuerstehenn gebenn jch nit vnterlassenn, das, nachdem ich ein gantzes jar nahe bey ewer hoch- vnd weitberumpten stadtt Hamburg auf dem Rantzouischenn hauß Wandesbeck, wie E.E. vnd W.W. bewust, gewonet, von jhrer röm: kay: mt:, vnserem allergnedigstenn herrn zum andernmal an den kaiserlichenn hoff von Wittenberg ab jch bin allergnedigst erfordert wordenn, vnnd, als jch alda meiner gelegenheitt nach gehorsamlich compariret, allen kaiserlichenn gnedigenn willenn vnd bewogenheitt vntertäniglichenn gespuret vnnd mit allermildesten prouision allergnedigst versorget wordenn. Als aber jhre röm: kay: mt: vernommenn, das jch noch etzliche von meinenn astronomischenn instrumenten, die so groß wehrenn, das man sy nit auf der axs furenn könne, jm königreich Dennemarck, meinem liebenn vaterlandt, nebenn etzlichenn andern sachen hinderlassenn hett, jst jhr. röm: kay: mt: allergnedigst gesonnenn vnnd mir thun befehlenn, solches alles nebenn dem, so jch noch zu Magdenburg, alher jn jhr königreich Behmen fur dem winter furderlichst zuebringenn. Zue besserem fortgang aber dessenn hatt jhr. röm: kay: mt: ein schraibenn an E.E. vnd W.W., sowol an einen erbarn rath der stadt Magdenburg, mir allergnedigst hieuon zuestellen vnd an dieselbigen abgehenn lassenn, welches an E.E. vnd W.W., beynebenn jch denselbigenn thue zueschickenn, nit zweifelnde, E.E. vnd W.W. werdenn hierin jhr röm: kay: mt: gehorsamlichenn wilfahrenn vnd mit schiff, sowol anderer gelegenheit, wie es dan meine leutte anzeigenn werdenn, wan die sachenn auß Dennemarck alda ankommenn, zu prouidiren vnd furderlichenn sein, damit, ehe die Elb zufreuret, alles mag wol verwahret hierauf, so weitt als sy schifreich ist, gebracht werdenn. Hieran geschiet vnd thuenn E.E. vnd W.W. zuforderst jhr. röm: kay: mt:, vnserm allergnedigstenn herren, ein allergnedigstes sonders vnd angenehms wolgefallenn, auch mir vnd dem astronomischen werck, so jch vorhabe, gutte promotion, welche jch bey jhr. kay: mt: wil zuruhmenn wissenn, vnnd vmb E.E. vnd W.W., welche jch hiemit Gott jnn seinenn schutz vnd schirm befohlenn habenn wil, hinwiderumb nach meinem vermögenn altzeit geflissenn sein zuuerdienen.</p><p>Datiret auf dem kayserlichenn schloß Bennatka, am 29 tag septembris stylo nouo, anno 1599.<br>Ewer E.E. vnd W.W. altzait freindtwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>mitt eigen handtt.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, worshipful, and right-wise Lords N. N., Burgomasters and Councillors of the praiseworthy city of Hamburg, my especially favourable Lords and good friends.</b></p><p>Honourable, worshipful, right-wise, worthy, especially favourable Lords and good friends: to the same [be] my wholly assiduous, ever-willing service, according to my utmost diligence, beforehand. And I cannot omit to give Your Honours and Wisdoms amicably to understand that, after I had dwelt a whole year near your high- and far-famed city of Hamburg, at the Rantzau house of Wandsbek — as Your Honours and Wisdoms know — I was, for the second time, most graciously summoned by His Roman Imperial Majesty, our most gracious Lord, to the imperial court, away from Wittenberg; and when I had there obediently presented myself, according to my circumstances, I humbly met with every imperial gracious good will and favour, and was most graciously provided for with a most bountiful provision [stipend]. But when His Roman Imperial Majesty learned that I had left behind, in the kingdom of Denmark, my dear fatherland, some of my astronomical instruments — which were so large that they could not be carried upon the axle [by wagon] — together with certain other things, His Roman Imperial Majesty is most graciously minded, and commands me, to bring all this, together with what I [have] still at Magdeburg, hither into his kingdom of Bohemia, with all dispatch before the winter. And for the better furtherance of this, His Roman Imperial Majesty has most graciously had a letter [addressed] to Your Honours and Wisdoms, as well as to the worthy Council of the city of Magdeburg, delivered to me hereupon, and dispatched to the same; which [letter] to Your Honours and Wisdoms I herewith send on to you — not doubting that Your Honours and Wisdoms will herein obediently comply with His Roman Imperial Majesty, and will furnish ship as well as other means (as my people will then indicate, when the things from Denmark arrive there), and be furthering [the matter], so that, before the Elbe freezes over, everything may be brought up [the river] hither, well secured, as far as it is navigable. In this Your Honours and Wisdoms do, first and foremost, a most gracious, special, and welcome pleasure to His Roman Imperial Majesty, our most gracious Lord; and also, to me and to the astronomical work which I have in hand, a good furtherance — which I shall know how to commend before His Imperial Majesty; and toward Your Honours and Wisdoms — whom I herewith will have commended to God's protection and shelter — I will in return, according to my ability, be ever assiduous to deserve well.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle of Benátky, the 29th day of September, New Style, in the year 1599.<br>Your Honours' and Wisdoms' ever friend-willing<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipients.</b> The <b>Burgomasters and Council (Senate) of the city of Hamburg</b> — addressed collectively (<i>N. N.</i> = <i>nomina nominanda</i>, the clerk's placeholder for the individual names), in the high civic register <i>E.E. vnd W.W.</i> (<i>Ehrenveste und Wohlweise</i>, \"Your Honours and Wisdoms\"). The MS survives in the <b>Hamburg Staatsarchiv</b> among the <i>Transito- und Porto-franco</i> (transit / toll-exemption) papers — the apt file for a freight-passage request.</li><li><b>The matter — moving Tycho's instruments to Bohemia.</b> A <b>logistics letter of the first importance for the material history of Tycho's instruments.</b> Having dwelt a year at <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau house near Hamburg) and then been summoned a <i>second time</i> by <b>Rudolf II</b> to the imperial court (from Wittenberg), and provided with a generous stipend, Tycho now executes the Emperor's command to bring to <b>Bohemia, before winter,</b> the great <b>astronomical instruments left in Denmark</b> — \"so large that they could not be carried on the axle [by wagon]\" — together with other goods still lying at <b>Magdeburg</b>. He forwards the <b>imperial letters</b> that Rudolf had addressed to the Hamburg and Magdeburg councils, asking Hamburg to provide <b>ship and other means</b> to carry everything <b>up the Elbe, as far as it is navigable, before the river freezes</b>.</li><li><b>The attached inventory slip (witness note) — what actually arrived.</b> The MS bears the endorsement <i>Lectum in senatu 21. April a:o 1600</i> (\"read in the senate, 21 April 1600\") and an attached slip listing the goods <i>\"Van Tychone Braden\"</i> that <b>reached Hamburg on 22 April 1600</b>: <i>10 kasten [crates/baskets], 1 kiste [chest], ein halbe Tonne [half-barrel], ein Achtendel [eighth-barrel], 14 Tonnen \"karzen\", 2 große Eisen [large irons], 4 eiserne Kachelöfen [iron tiled-stoves], 3 bleierne Kessel [lead kettles/cauldrons], 1 Blasebalg [bellows], 1 kleines Feldbett [small field-bed], 1 großes \"tweklafftiges\" Eisen mit einem runden Bauche</i> [one large two-fathom iron with a round belly]. The lead cauldrons, bellows and iron stoves point to Tycho's <b>alchemical/metallurgical apparatus</b> as well as the instruments; the <i>\"großes zweiklaftiges Eisen mit rundem Bauche\"</i> is plausibly a major instrument component or its iron mounting. This is direct documentary evidence of the <b>April 1600 transit of Tycho's effects through Hamburg</b> toward Prague.</li><li><b>Dating crux (protocol §3 — flagged, resolved by the source).</b> The letter's own dateline reads <b>\"am 29 tag septembris stylo novo, anno 1599\"</b> — <b>29 September 1599, New Style</b> — and the dataset slug (15990929001) agrees. The dataset's `label` field, however, reads <i>\"1599. 27. januar n.s. Benatky\"</i>; this <b>January date is a catalogue error</b> and is rejected in favour of the as-written dateline, per protocol §4.1 (lead with the source's own date). The \"before the winter / before the Elbe freezes\" urgency fits a <b>late-September</b> letter, not a January one. The seven-month gap to the senate's reading (<i>lectum 21 April 1600</i>) reflects the slow assembling and shipment of the goods from Denmark and Magdeburg: the covering letter and imperial mandates were presented when the freight at last came up the Elbe in spring 1600. (That the goods could not in fact be moved \"before the winter\" of 1599/1600, but only in April 1600, is consistent with — not contradicted by — the September dateline.)</li><li><b>Persons/places named.</b> Rudolf II (<i>röm. kay. Mt.</i>); the councils of <b>Hamburg</b> and <b>Magdeburg</b>; <b>Wandsbek</b> (the Rantzau house); <b>Wittenberg</b>; <b>Magdeburg</b>; the kingdoms of <b>Denmark</b> and <b>Bohemia</b>; the <b>Elbe</b>.</li><li><b>Register / formulae.</b> Subscription <i>mit eigen Hand</i> (= <i>manu propria</i>); the autograph attestation. Imperial-style honorifics (<i>allergnedigst, allermildeste Provision</i>) throughout.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the German: \"schüutz\" restored to Dreyer's \"schutz\" (TBOO XIV p.154).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely and the substance (the imperial order to bring the Denmark/Magdeburg instruments up the Elbe to Bohemia before winter; the forwarded imperial letters; the request for shipping) is unambiguous; graded <b>[B]</b> solely for the <b>flagged dating crux</b> — the catalogue label (27 January) contradicts the letter's explicit dateline and the slug (29 September 1599 N.S.), here resolved in favour of the source's own date, with the <i>lectum in senatu 21 April 1600</i> endorsement and the 22 April 1600 goods-arrival noted as consistent with a September letter shipped the following spring.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation (the dating crux) cleanly resolved to the explicit dateline — the catalogue label a plain error, endorsement-corroborated.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.153-156 as page images. The Hamburg council letter runs from the bottom of p.153 ('Den') through p.155 (the appended Dutch-language inventory schedula on p.155 is separate archival material, not part of source_text/published_english, correctly excluded). Collated the full Rantzau-house/Wandsbek/Wittenberg/imperial-summons narrative, the instrument-and-axle detail, the Magdeburg/Elbe shipping arrangements, and the closing date and signature (29 tag Septembris stylo nouo, anno 1599) word-for-word; all proper names, dates and place-names check out except the one corrupted word noted above. Per the brief's rule that any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C, despite the defect being low-severity and not altering the letter's sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the corrupted form 'schüutz' where Dreyer prints the plain word 'schutz' (protection); the translation itself is unaffected since the translator correctly rendered the intended word, but the source departs from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>welche jch hiemit Gott jnn feinenn fchutz vnd fchirm befohlenn habenn wil (TBOO XIV p.154, l.36)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.153-156; the phrase appears on p.154, line 36, reading \"...jnn feinenn fchutz vnd fchirm befohlenn...\" — confirming the plain form \"schutz\". Only the German required correction; the English translation was already accurate. No other defects found across the four pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Hamborg, Staatstarchiv Hamburg, Ad CI. VII Lib Ea Pars 2 (Transito u. Porte-franco). Originalbrev med påskriften Lectum in senatu 21. april a:o 1600. Og på en vedlagt seddel: Van Tychone Braden gvdren: 10 kasten. 1 kiste. Ein halue tune. Ein achtendell. 14 tunnen karzen. 2 große jseren. 4 jseren kachelauen. 3 bleyen keßell. 1 blaßbalch. 1 klein feltbedde. 1 großes tweklafftiges jseren mit einen runden bauche, Diße guder sint den 22 aprillis hir in Hamborch angekamen a:o 1600."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Informs the Hamburg city council of his imperial summons and generous reception at Rudolf II's court, noting the Emperor's awareness of instruments and goods left behind in Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15990929001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991008001", "d": "1599-10-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Vincent Moller", "ck": "Vincent Moller", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Vincent Moller → Tycho Brahe — Hamburg, 8 October 1599 [O.S.] (catalogued 8 September)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 5f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, D. TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO IN KNUDSTORP, MATHEMATICO PRAeSTANTISSIMO, DOMINO AC AMICO S. HONORANDO.<br>S.</p><p>magnifice et nobilissime vir, domine ac amice honorande. Literas tuas Venetijs Bohemorum 28 septemb. datas summa cum voluptate perlegi, cum quod de tua valetudine, tum et quo in loco T. M. subsistat, me certiorem fecerint. Literae s. caes. m:tis, domini nostri clementiss., quarum mentionem T. M. facit, senatui nostro non sunt exhibitae, forsitan incuria tuorum neglectae; quamprimum autem allatae et in senatu lectae fuerint, ex ijsque intellexero, quid tua causa s. caes. m:tas fieri velit, lubens, quantum in me erit, negotium promouebo. Simonem a Petkum, ut de singulis, quae tua scire interest, te certiorem reddat, admonui, qui copiose se rescripturum recepit. Ferramenta Wandesburgi relicta quod attinet, existimo de ijs nihil certi statui posse, antequam Bredo Ranzouius ad tuas responderit. His paucis T. M. aeterni Dei tutelae commendo, qui eandem diutiss: incolumem conseruet.</p><p>Dabantur Hamburgj 8 octob. a:o 1599.<br>Mag:ae T. studiosiss:<br>Vinc. Moller L.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord in Knudstrup, most outstanding Mathematician, his lord and friend to be honoured. Greeting.</b><br>Greeting.</p><p>Magnificent and most noble man, lord and friend to be honoured. Your letter, dated at the Venice of the Bohemians [Benátky] on 28 September, I read through with the greatest pleasure, since it made me more certain both about your health and in what place Your Magnificence is residing. The letters of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement lord, of which Your Magnificence makes mention, have not been presented to our Senate — perhaps neglected through the carelessness of your people; but as soon as they have been brought and read in the Senate, and I have understood from them what His Sacred Imperial Majesty wishes done on your behalf, I will gladly, as much as shall be in me, promote the business. I have admonished Simon von Petkum to make you more certain about the particulars which it concerns you to know, and he has undertaken to write back to you copiously. As regards the ironwork [the iron instruments/tools] left at Wandsbek, I judge that nothing certain can be settled about them before Breide Rantzau has replied to your [letter]. With these few [words] I commend Your Magnificence to the protection of the eternal God, who may preserve you safe as long as possible.</p><p>Given at Hamburg, 8 October, in the year 1599.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Vincent Moller, L[icentiate].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Vincent Moller</b> (the Elder, 1527–1599/1600), a Hamburg patrician and Senate figure (the <i>L.</i> after his name = <i>Licentiatus</i> in law); Tycho's local contact during and after his stay near Hamburg. He acts here as Tycho's intermediary with the <b>Hamburg Senate</b> over imperial letters concerning Tycho's affairs.</li><li><b><i>Venetijs Bohemorum</b></i> — \"at the Venice of the Bohemians,\" a learned by-name for <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (Czech <i>Benátky</i> = \"Venice\"), Tycho's residence from 1599; rendered with the gloss. Tycho's incoming letter was dated there 28 September.</li><li><b><i>Literae s. caes. m:tis</b></i> = letters of <b>His Sacred Imperial Majesty</b> (Rudolf II) on Tycho's behalf, which had not reached the Hamburg Senate. <i>Simon a Petkum</i> = <b>Simon von Petkum</b> (Peckius), a diplomat/agent who would write Tycho the particulars. <i>Bredo Ranzouius</i> = <b>Breide Rantzau</b> (1556–1618), the Holstein noble (governor of the Rantzau lands) under whom the <i>Wandsbek</i> matter fell.</li><li><b><i>Ferramenta Wandesburgi relicta</b></i> — \"the ironwork left at <b>Wandsbek</b>\": Tycho's heavy <b>iron instruments/fittings</b> left behind at Wandsbek (where he had lodged 1597–98 at Heinrich Rantzau's castle) when he moved on; their fate awaited Breide Rantzau's reply. (<i>Ferramenta</i> = iron tools/instruments; rendered with the gloss.)</li><li><b>⚠ Date.</b> The dateline reads <i>Hamburgj 8 octob. a:o 1599</i> = <b>8 October 1599</b>; the slug (…10 08…) agrees. The dataset catalogues it under <b>8 September</b> — the one-month discrepancy is reproduced, not reconciled; the header follows the as-written <b>8 October</b>. Hamburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the persons (Moller, Petkum, Breide Rantzau) and matters (the imperial letters to the Hamburg Senate; the iron instruments at Wandsbek; Benátky as \"Venice of the Bohemians\") are corpus-corroborated. Grade [B] for the catalogue-vs-dateline month discrepancy (8 Sept. vs 8 Oct.), flagged and resolved in favour of the text.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation (catalogue-vs-dateline month, 8 Sep/8 Oct) resolved to the as-written text; persons corroborated, no textual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.156 (Dreyer letter 165, Vincentius Mollerus to Tycho) as a page image; letter entire on this page. Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word including the dateline of Tycho's own letter ('Venetijs Bohemorum 28 septemb.'), the names Simon von Petkum and Bredo Ranzouius, and Moller's own dateline '8 octob. a:o 1599'. English translation checked clause-by-clause and is faithful; bracketed glosses ([Benátky], [Licentiate], [the iron instruments/tools]) are clearly marked interpretive supplements, not fabrications. Only cosmetic quirk: the heading in the English duplicates 'Greeting.' (once folded into the bold title, once as a standalone line) — a formatting artifact, not a factual defect, so not scored.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 5f.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Vincent Moller", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000424001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000424001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that imperial letters on Tycho's behalf have not yet been presented to Hamburg's Senate due to his people's carelessness, and promises to promote the matter once they are read.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991008001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991024001", "d": "1599-10-24", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Sophie Brahe", "ck": "Sophie Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Breitenburg", "t": "Sophie Brahe → Tycho Brahe — Breitenburg, 24 October 1599 *(label says 24 September — see Apparatus)*", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 7–14 (original letter)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Sophie Brahe (Sophia Brahe Ottesdatter)</b>, Tycho's sister, <i>to</i> Tycho. A long, intimate family letter. (The witness carries a later note about a <i>Genealogia Braheorum</i> once owned by Chancellor Niels Kaas.)</p>", "la": "<p>ERLLIIG OCH WELBYRDIG MAND TTYGE BRAHE TTILL KNUDSTRUP, MYNN ALLERKIERISTE BRODER, GANSKE HULLELIGEN OCH WENLIGENN THILLSKREUIT.</p><p>MYNN allerkieristhe broder, Gudtt allmectigsthe were nu och alltidtt hos eder och beuare eder nadelig och well fraa alltt dett, som eder kann were skadeligtt till liff eller siell, och Gud wnde migh altid att spørge, att dett maa gaa eder løkelig och well i alle maade, dett will iegh troligen ønske aff Gudtt. M. a. k. broder, giffuer iegh eder ganske wenligen advide, att iegh er nu paa reyssen medtt min Ttaghe, och fulde iegh hanem først till Hadersløff, och der iegh kom did, daa war Gertt och Ttalle affdragen hen ttill en aff deris garde, heder Lindvid, ny mille fra Harsløff, hor hand lader byghe; saa skreff di mig ttill, at jegh wille medtt Ttage kome did ttill dem och saa følgis med dem till Briedenburch, som er paa vij mille ner Hamburigh, saa ville Gertt forhøre om selskaff, som Ttage kune kome i følge medt ttill Brunsvigh och sa ud ttil Bassell, som ieg vil haa ham henn. Saa drogh jeg daa ttill denem ttill dett Lindvid, och war der i ttho eller ttre dage, och fulle denem saa hid ttill Briedenburgh och har werid her i 6 daghe, och giør di migh altt dett gode di kan, sa ieg er komen ttill rett gode wener her, som giør mig altt gott, Gudtt were louitt, och blir jeg her enu ttill paa mandagh, for da drager Gertt ttill Kill pa en ricksdagh, och systher drager ttill Harsløff igen, for di wentter kongenn did strax mod s. Mortens dagh, for der skall da were en richsdag ttill Flensburgh, hor kongen will were paa dentid. Saa actter jeg met Guds hielp att drage herfraa med Ttage ttill Wandersbeck wid Hamborig, och der were en 3 eler 4 dage och forhøre om gott selskaff, som Ttage kan komme i følge med ttill Brunsuigh, Gud giue ttill løcke.</p><p>Titans skriuer Mikell hade werid paa Hadersløff och mentt att fundet mig der for en 3 uger sinn, och der ieg ike da war der, drog han till Horssens, och fik mor fru Anne breuene till mig, och hun sene dem ouer till Køffuenhaffn, och der di kom did, war jeg aff dragen, saa sene jeg it bud fra Koling och ouer till Køffuenhaffn efftter same breff, och dett bud kom nu hid ttill mig ttill Briedenburg i andags gardtt, och fick iegh med dett bud eders søns Ttygis skriffuelsse, hor aff iegh formerker, att hand er i Køffuenhaffn nu och haffuer breff ttil mig fraa eder, huilkett hand ike kune nu sene mig, men ville sielff anttvorde mig dett, om jegh kom ttil Køuenhaffn ingen enn 3 ugher; ellers will hand faa dochter Nils Krag breuene, nar hand drager henn; och sener hand mig tthu breffue, itt till min broder Knud, och itt ttill min systher Margrette, huilke jeg strax will sende denem; och skriuer dochter Nils mig nogitt till om eders lieglighet, hor aff jeg formerker, att i lider nu rett well, Gud were loffuitt, och haffuer faid itt hus aff kiesserenn, och mer end 4000 gyllen ttill arlligh penge aff kiesseren; och brød jeg min systher Margrettis breff op och wille och der aff forfare, hor eder lide, di jeg har lenge lengtist efftther bud fra eder, sin i er komen ttill Pragh; saa er dett mig i sanhedtt saa rett hierttelig kiertt, att jegh forfarer, att dett gaer eder saa well, Gud were loffuitt, att i har funditt en plads, som i kan haa rolighedtt, och folk kann skøn paa eder, och holle aff eders handell, och giøre eder gott.</p><p>Jegh hade ttillforn hørtt nogitt aff dette, di dett bleff dochter Nils skreuit till aff Ttyskland, doch huem jegh sagde dett for, ville ike ttro dett, saa jeg wiste dett doch ike wist, førend jeg kom till Gertt Ranszuo; hand sagde mig, ad dett war vist, och ad hand hade selff nyligen skreuitt eder till, och kan i ike ttro, hor kiertt dett er bade ham och Ttalle, ad det gar eder well, y sandhed, jeg kan merkett, att det er dem bade rett kiertt; i hor det er gan til met det snak, der var sagd for eder, daa har det wertt en misforstand, dy har hatt derom, di Ttale er jer och rett god, des wid jegh wist; ieg har formerktt bade aff dem och aff Gerttis søster, at fru Kiersten, hans moder, er eder saa god och forttryder sa hartt, huad di har giortt jer i Danmark, och adtt hun ttaker eder for hinis kintt Lisebett, y har hiolpidtt, der ttal hun saa megitt om; saa har jegh nu derfor skreffuitt hinde ttill och ttakett hine saa for altt det gode, hun har beuist eder, och er ier wen sa god, och derhos lader hine wide, huad ttinder jegh har nu sportt om eder, och ad eders søn er i Køffuenhaffn efftter di instrumenter, som enu war ttilbaghe. Jegh hauer i disse daghe laditt Gertt, sett dett breff, som i hade skreuitt kongen til och huad suar i fik derpaa, jegh har selff lest dett for ham och ttyd ham meningen derpaa, sa Ttalle hørdett, di forstod dem well derpaa; saa lod jeg ham och se det lile breff, som kiesserens canzeller hade skreuitt eder till.</p><p>Jegh var y sist i ttall medtt Mercurius i Skane paa Lanskrune; daa spore hand migh om eder, daa sagde ieg, att jegh hobtist, adtt dett gik eder well, och lod ham saa lesse den same lile copii, som den romiske canzeler hade skreuitt; daa sagde hand, det war gott, och war ham kertt, ad det gik jer well; och litt der efftter sagde han mig nogen tiner om kiesseren, som mig ike behagitt; daa suarid jeg, det kune bli gott eligvell, i hade andre go vener der i haffuidtt; sa sad hand atter litt, saa kom hand med end it snak och sagde, tto aff di, som war eders beste vener der aff kiesserens rad, di war affsaatt; daa mente ieg dett kun endaa bli gott; dett ser jeg gierne, sagde handtt. Dett war saa smok snak, vi hade, och jegh forstod det saa well. Min bror Jørgen war och der, han spore mig sa flitig om eder och ønskett saa, att altting kune gaa well for eder der wde.</p><p>Nu ttenker jeg well, ad di nu i Køffuenhaffn ttill dene heredagh for besken att wide om eders hanell. M. a. k. broder, nu att skriffue nogitt om Titan, saa har hand hafft en febris i 3 uger, doch hobis jeg met Guds hielp, att dett er nu gott igen med ham. Dett synis som denn Hanns Ernst will ike gaa rett om medtt ham; Erich har lertt ham consten, och der war giortt hanell melem dem, adtt huem di skulle lere consten, der skull di bade were om, och bade were lige guode y huad penge di for derfore; nu hade di forskreuen nogenn folk, som di skall haa penge aff, nu wille Hans Ernst enne haa di penghe; och har hand och skreuit kiesseren till och ttilbøditt ham consten, och wille ike lade Erich wide der aff; doch Erich hobis hand will kome ham for; ieg ttenker, att Erich will sielff ttill kiesserenn; och wid jeg wist, att Erich har consten wist, och hade handtt ttilbøditt wor konge denn, men hand ville ingen suar skriffue der paa, men war kun spodsch der offuer, och hade Hans Ernst och skreuitt kongenn ttill siden och begertt, at han ville lade Erich bekome suar, hor aff jegh sener eder enn copii, di jeg lod selff anttvore kongen dett breff med sekreteren, men handtt wille inted suare der till, for di har nu giortt ham her altt huad di kune; nu er di ttillfris medtt ham, och lader som di wid intid aff huad di ttilforn har giortt, di Erich hade erindrid kongen nogit derom i sin suplicatz. Nu Gud almectigsthe hielpe well och giøre dett engang bedre for os. Jeg hade skreuitt Erich ttill, ad han ville møde mig istes vid Harsløff, om han war her nogenstes i egnen, sa kune hand da ike møde mig der; nu har ieg skreuitt ham ttill, om han vile kome hid ttil mig eler til Hamburig eller ttil Lüneburg eller hor han will, daa will jeg møde ham och ttalle med ham, før jegh drar her aff egnen, och har jeg latt itt bud løffuid ud ttill ham her fraa och wentter dett igen om en 3 eler 4 dage; dersom hand will møde migh her ittstes, da vill jeg bie ham, ellers vill jeg met Guds hielp følge Ttage till Brunsuigh, saa er det kun en dags reysse ttill dett Kelbna. Dersom hand holler hus, sa vill jeg drage did till ham, di jegh ma enelig ttalle med ham, men jeg er saa ner ham, med Guds hielp, och wide hor det er met hans hanell. Gud giffue wos løcke och radtt, ad wij motte och faa nogen godtt lieglighedtt engangh.</p><p>M. a. k. broder, nu om min Ttage, saa acter jeg att lade ham drage till Bassell och bli der itt aar; dochter Nils har skreuitt met ham till den docter Greneus der, Sten Bille har och skreuitt enn dochter till, er den andens broder, att di vell forhielpe Ttage till dett beste. Erich har skreuitt mig ttill, att ham syntist, att det wor best, att Ttage bleff till Hiedellbierg først itt aar och lerde sprogitt, att hand ike skulle først lere dett sleme suisisch sprog ttill Bassell, men ieg will enu se dett ann, før ieg slutter dett, hor han skall were, di han kan vell och lere god ttysk til Bassel; blir det ike forsnartt forkoltt att feris, da skall hand drage ttill Bassell, di den haffhanell, der er ttill Hiedelbierg, hauer ike migh; nar han nu komer did, som hand først skal vere, sa skall hand strax skriffue eder ttill; saa ttror jeg eder saa vell, att i skriuer for ham ttil di lerde paa det sted, att di vill fordre ham till dett besthe. Gud almectigste beuare ham well, i hor hand komer, och senne ham well hiem till mig ighenn.</p><p>M. a. k. broder, jeg ttenker min moder har vell nu faid eders skriuelsse, der blir hun gladtt vid, di hun war nu saa omhygelig for jer, sin i war dragen till kiesseren. Jeg sener jer itt breff, Henrich Romell skreff mig ttill om di bøgher. Jeg drager till Fusing til min søster fra Harsløff, nar jeg komer did igen, di hun har begert det aff migh; hinis søner er nu dragen y gardenn. Jeg skriuer nu dette breff her och sener dett ttill Køffuenhaffn, och hobis, att dett komer der førend eders Ttyche drager derfraa; hade jeg nu bekomid huis i har skreuitt mig till, dett hade jegh gierne seett, men jeg skal skriff efftter dett. M. a. k. broder, saa wid jegh nu intet syneligtt mer att skriffue eder ttill, men i skall altid finde mig for eders hulle systher, der altid gierne will giøre huis jeg wid eder kiertt er, och hobis wi finis well engang, Gud giue wy maa findis glade och helbredtt. Och will nu och altid haffue eder Gud allmectigste beffallidtt, bade till siell och liff, med all løcke och welfartt. Lader Giertt och Ttalle och min Ttaghe helsse eder medtt manghe m. guode netther; giører well och siger Kirsten och eders børn och alle eders guode wener (huilke ocssa ere mine) manghe m. guode netter paa minne wegnne.</p><p>Datum Briedenburgh denn 24. october 1599.<br>Sophia Brahe Ottis datter.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honest and well-born man Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup, my dearest brother, quite lovingly and friendlily written.</b></p><p>My dearest brother, may God Almighty be with you now and always, and keep you graciously and well from all that can be harmful to you, in body or soul; and may God grant me always to hear that it may go happily and well with you in every way — that I faithfully wish from God. My dear kind brother, I give you quite friendlily to know <b>that I am now on the journey with my Tage</b>; and I first accompanied him to <b>Haderslev</b>; and when I came there, <b>Gert and Talle [Rantzau] had moved off to one of their estates, called Lindewedel</b> [Lindved], nine miles from Haderslev, where he is building; so they wrote me that I should come with Tage there to them, and then accompany them to <b>Breitenburg, which is within 7 miles of Hamburg</b> — [where] Gert would inquire about a company [a travelling party] that Tage could join, to go to <b>Brunswick and so out to Basel, where I want him</b>. So I then went to them, to Lindewedel, and was there two or three days, and accompanied them hither to Breitenburg, and have been here 6 days; and they do me all the good they can, so that I have come to right good friends here, who do me all good, God be praised. And I shall stay here yet until Monday, for then Gert goes to <b>Kiel</b> for a Diet, and sister goes back to Haderslev — for they expect the King there soon, toward <b>St. Martin's day [11 Nov]</b>, for there shall then be a Diet at <b>Flensburg</b>, where the King will be at that time. So I intend, with God's help, to go hence with Tage to <b>Wandsbek by Hamburg</b>, and be there 3 or 4 days, and inquire about good company that Tage can join to Brunswick. God grant good luck.</p><p><b>Titan's</b> secretary <b>Mikell</b> had been at Haderslev, and thought to have found me there about 3 weeks ago; and since I was not there then, he went to <b>Horsens</b>, and <b>mother Lady Anne</b> got the letters to me, and she sent them over to <b>Copenhagen</b>; and when they came there, I had departed; so I sent a messenger from <b>Kolding</b> over to Copenhagen for the same letters, and that messenger now came hither to me at Breitenburg the day before yesterday; and I got, with that messenger, <b>your son Tyge's letter</b>, from which I perceive that he is in Copenhagen now, and has a letter for me from you — which he could not now send me, but would deliver it to me himself, if I came to Copenhagen within 3 weeks; otherwise he will give <b>Doctor Niels Krag</b> the letters when he travels [thither]. And he sends me two letters, one to my brother <b>Knud</b>, and one to my sister <b>Margrette</b>, which I will send them at once. And Doctor Niels writes me something about your situation, from which I perceive that <b>you now fare right well, God be praised, and have got a house from the Emperor, and more than 4000 gulden in yearly money from the Emperor</b>; and I broke open my sister Margrette's letter, and would also learn from it how you fare, for I have long longed for word from you since you came to Prague. So it is truly so heartily dear to me, that I learn it goes so well with you (God be praised), <b>that you have found a place where you can have peace, and people can appreciate you and value your work [<i>handel</i>], and do you good.</b></p><p>I had heard something of this before, for it was written to Doctor Niels from Germany — though whomever I told it to would not believe it, so I did not know it for certain, until I came to <b>Gert Rantzau</b>; he told me that it was certain, and that he had himself lately written to you, and you cannot believe how dear it is, both to him and to Talle, that it goes well with you. Truly, I can tell it is right dear to them both. However it went with that talk that was said about you, then it has been a misunderstanding they have had about it, for <b>Talle is right good to you too, that I know for certain</b>; I have perceived, both from them and from Gert's sister, that <b>Lady Kirsten, his mother, is so good to you, and regrets so hard what they did to you in Denmark</b>, and that she thanks you for her child <b>Lisbeth</b>, whom you have helped — she talks so much about it. So I have now therefore written her, and thanked her for all the good she has shown you, and [she] is so good a friend to you; and besides, [I] let her know what tidings I have now learned about you, and that your son is in Copenhagen for the instruments that were still left behind. I have in these days <b>let Gert see the letter you had written the King, and what answer you got on it</b> — I read it myself for him, and explained the meaning of it to him, so that Talle heard it; for they understood it well. So I let him also see <b>the little letter the Emperor's Chancellor had written you</b>.</p><p>I was lately in conversation with <b>\"Mercurius\" in Skåne, at Landskrona</b>; then he asked me about you, then I said that I hoped it went well with you, and let him read <b>the same little copy that the Roman Chancellor [Coraduz] had written</b>; then he said it was good, and dear to him, that it went well with you. And a little after, <b>he told me some things about the Emperor that did not please me</b>; then I answered, it could be good nonetheless, [for] you had other good friends you had there. So he sat again a little; then he came with another remark, and said: <b>two of those who were your best friends there, of the Emperor's council, they were dismissed</b> [from office]; then I thought it could still be good. \"That I see gladly,\" he said. It was such pretty talk we had, and I understood it so well. <b>My brother Jørgen</b> was also there; he asked me so diligently about you, and wished so much that everything could go well for you out there.</p><p>Now I do think that they are now in Copenhagen, for this Diet, for word [seeking news] about your situation. My dear kind brother, now to write something about <b>Titan</b>: <b>he has had a fever for 3 weeks, though I hope, with God's help, that it is now well again with him.</b> It seems as if that <b>Hans Ernst</b> will not deal rightly with him [Erik]: <b>Erik has taught him the [alchemical] art [<i>consten</i>]</b>, and there was a deal made between them, that whomever they should teach the art, they should both be in on it, and both share equally in whatever money they got for it. Now they had engaged some people [investors], from whom they should get money; now Hans Ernst alone wanted to have those moneys; and he has also written the Emperor, and offered him the art, and would not let Erik know of it — though Erik hopes he will get ahead of him; I think <b>Erik will himself [go] to the Emperor</b>. And I know for certain that <b>Erik has the art for certain</b>, and he had offered our King it, but he [the King] would write no answer on it, but was only mocking about it; and Hans Ernst had also written the King since, and asked that he would let Erik get an answer — of which I send you a copy; for I myself had the King delivered the letter by the secretary, but he would answer nothing to it, for they have now done to him [Erik] here all that they could. Now they are content with him, and act as if they know nothing of what they did before — for Erik had reminded the King somewhat of it in his supplication. Now may God Almighty help well, and make it once better for us. I had written Erik that he should meet me lately near Haderslev, if he were anywhere in the region — so he could not meet me there [then]; now I have written him whether he will come hither to me, or to Hamburg, or to Lüneburg, or wherever he will, [and] then I will meet him and talk with him, before I leave the region; and I have sent a messenger out to him from here, and expect it back in 3 or 4 days. If he will meet me here lately, then I will wait for him; otherwise I will, with God's help, accompany Tage to Brunswick — so it is but a day's journey to that <b>Kelbra</b> [?]. <b>If he keeps house [is settled somewhere], then I will go thither to him, for I must absolutely talk with him</b>; but I am so near him, with God's help, [I shall] also [learn] how it is with his affairs. God give us luck and counsel, that we might also get some good opportunity once.</p><p>My dear kind brother, now about my <b>Tage</b>: I intend to let him <b>go to Basel and stay there a year</b>; <b>Doctor Niels [Krag] has written, with him, to that Doctor Grynaeus there; Sten Bille has also written to a doctor [there], [who] is the other's brother</b>, that they will help Tage to the best. <b>Erik has written me that it seemed to him best that Tage stay at Heidelberg first a year and learn the language, that he should not first learn that bad Swiss tongue at Basel</b> — but I will yet consider it, before I decide where he shall be, for he can well also learn good German at Basel; if it does not get too soon too cold to travel, then he shall go to Basel, for the … [<i>haffhanell</i> — unclear] that is at Heidelberg does not [please] me. When he now comes where he shall first be, then he shall write you at once; so I trust you so well that <b>you will write for him to the learned at that place, that they will advance him to the best</b>. God Almighty keep him well, wherever he comes, and send him well home to me again.</p><p>My dear kind brother, I think <b>my mother [Beate Bille]</b> has now got your letter; she will be glad of it, for she was so anxious for you since you went to the Emperor. I send you a letter; <b>Henrik Ramel</b> wrote me about the books. I go to <b>Fusingø</b> to my sister from Haderslev, when I come there again, for she has asked it of me; her sons have now gone into the estate. I write this letter here now, and send it to Copenhagen, and hope that it comes there before your Tyge leaves thence. Had I now got what you have written me, I would gladly have seen it — but I shall write for it. My dear kind brother, so I now know nothing notable more to write you; but you shall always find me your loyal sister, who always gladly will do what I know is dear to you; and [I] hope we meet well once — God grant we may meet glad and in health. And [I] will now and always have you commended to God Almighty, both to soul and body, with all luck and welfare. Gert and Talle and my Tage greet you with many good-nights; do well, and say to <b>Kirsten</b> and your children and all your good friends (who are also mine) many good-nights on my behalf.</p><p>Given [at] Breitenburg, the 24th of October 1599.<br><b>Sophia Brahe, Otte's daughter.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Sophie Brahe (Sophia Brahe Ottesdatter, 1559–1643)</b> — Tycho's younger sister, the astronomer- and chemist-noblewoman, betrothed to the alchemist <b>Erik Lange</b> — <b>to Tycho</b>. An original autograph (Basel).</li><li><b>Significance — a centerpiece of the Sophie Brahe / Erik Lange story, and a warm family chronicle.</b></li><li><b>Escorting her son to Basel.</b> Sophie is travelling with her son <b>Tage Thott</b> (by her first husband Otte Thott), bound for study at <b>Basel</b> via <b>Brunswick</b>, hosted by <b>Gert Rantzau, his wife \"Talle,\" and his mother Lady Kirsten Rantzau</b> at <b>Breitenburg</b>. She asks Tycho to write recommendations; <b>Niels Krag and Sten Bille have written to the Grynaeus brothers at Basel</b> (cf. Tycho's own Grynaeus letter and the nephew Otto Brahe at Basel, brahe-t_15980607001). <b>Erik Lange's advice</b>: send Tage to <b>Heidelberg</b> first (to avoid \"that bad Swiss tongue\").</li><li><b>Joy at Tycho's imperial fortune.</b> Sophie has heard (via <b>Niels Krag</b>, her sister <b>Margrette's</b> letter, and Tycho's son <b>Tyge</b> in Copenhagen) that Tycho <b>\"has got a house from the Emperor, and more than 4000 gulden a year\"</b> — \"so heartily dear to me … that you have found a place where people appreciate you.\" She has shown the sympathetic Rantzaus <b>Tycho's letter to King Christian IV, the unkind royal reply, and Coraduz's letter</b>.</li><li><b>The Erik Lange alchemy saga (the emotional core).</b> A vivid, intimate report on <b>Erik Lange</b> — Sophie's beloved, the ruined alchemist: his partner <b>Hans Ernst</b> is <b>cheating him over \"the art\" [<i>consten</i> = alchemy]</b> and the investors' money, secretly offering it to the Emperor; <b>Erik \"has the art for certain,\"</b> offered it to the <b>Danish King (who only mocked it)</b>, and may go to the Emperor himself. Sophie is <b>desperate to meet Erik</b> (Hamburg, Lüneburg, or wherever) before leaving — \"I must absolutely talk with him.\" (Cf. Tycho's own query after Erik Lange, brahe-t_16000000002.)</li><li><b>Court intelligence.</b> A coded conversation with <b>\"Mercurius\"</b> at <b>Landskrona</b> (a Danish court figure, codename) reveals <b>two of Tycho's friends on the imperial council \"were dismissed\"</b>; her brother <b>Jørgen Brahe</b> present. The family network — mother <b>Beate Bille</b>, siblings <b>Knud, Margrette</b>, the Fusingø sister, <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (about books) — threads throughout.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A keystone of the <b>Sophie Brahe / Erik Lange</b> thread (pair with brahe-t_16000000002, where Tycho asks Petkum after Erik Lange and reports Sophie at Wandsbek) and the <b>Brahe-family</b> network; the <b>Basel/Grynaeus</b> student connection (brahe-t_15980607001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> <b>Sophie Brahe</b>; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Tage Thott</b> (her son); <b>Gert Rantzau</b> &amp; wife \"<b>Talle</b>\" &amp; mother <b>Lady Kirsten Rantzau</b>; <b>Mikell</b> (courier); <b>Lady Anne</b> (a kinswoman — unidentified, flagged); <b>Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger); <b>Niels Krag</b>; <b>Knud Brahe</b>, <b>Margrette Brahe</b> (siblings); <b>\"Mercurius\"</b> (Landskrona court figure — codename, flagged); <b>Jørgen Brahe</b>; <b>Erik Lange</b>; <b>Hans Ernst</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>, <b>Christian IV</b>; the <b>Grynaeus</b> brothers (Basel); <b>Sten Bille</b>; <b>Beate Bille</b> (mother); <b>Henrik Ramel</b>; <b>\"Titan\"</b> (a person with a fever — unidentified, flagged). Places: <b>Breitenburg</b>, <b>Haderslev</b>, <b>Lindewedel</b>, <b>Kiel</b>, <b>Flensburg</b>, <b>Wandsbek/Hamburg</b>, <b>Brunswick</b>, <b>Basel</b>, <b>Heidelberg</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Kolding</b>, <b>Horsens</b>, <b>Landskrona</b>, <b>Lüneburg</b>, <b>Fusingø</b>.</li><li><b>Date (protocol §3, §4.1).</b> Source dateline <i>denn 24. october 1599</i> (slug `15991024001` agrees) → <b>24 October 1599 [O.S.]</b>; the <b>catalogue label's \"24 September\" is an error</b> (contradicted by the dateline and slug), flagged for the label audit.</li><li><b>Reading flags.</b> <b>\"Titan\"</b> — a person Sophie reports ill with fever, distinct from Tage and Erik (possibly a nickname for a family member or an alchemy associate; unidentified). <b>\"Mercurius\"</b> — a coded designation for a Danish-court figure. <b>\"Lady Anne\"</b> — an unnamed kinswoman. <b>\"haffhanell\"</b> (the Heidelberg objection) — uncertain word. None affect the overall sense.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two mistranslations corrected against Dreyer (TBOO XIV p.158): 'Titan writes that Mikell had been...' is corrected to 'Titan's secretary Mikell had been...' ('Titans skriuer Mikell' is a noun phrase naming Mikell as Titan's secretary, not a report-by-letter from Titan); and 'her kinswoman Lisbeth' is corrected to 'her child Lisbeth' ('kintt' = archaic Danish for 'child').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Early Modern Danish is fully legible and construed with confidence; the substance (the <b>Tage-to-Basel journey</b> with the Rantzaus; the <b>joy at Tycho's house + 4000-gulden imperial salary</b>; the sympathetic Rantzaus and the shown royal/Coraduz letters; the <b>\"Mercurius\" court-intelligence</b> about dismissed council friends; and above all the <b>Erik Lange alchemy saga</b> — Hans Ernst's cheating, \"the art\" offered to Emperor and King, Sophie's desperation to meet Erik) is firmly and warmly rendered, with no smoothing of Sophie's idiom. Flags: the <b>label's wrong month</b> (resolved to October per §4.1) and the unidentified <b>\"Titan,\" \"Mercurius,\" \"Lady Anne,\"</b> and <i>haffhanell</i> — none material to the sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.156-160 as page images (chunks 1-4, then 5) and collated source_text word-for-word against pp.157-159, which carry the full letter (no.166, Sophie Brahe to Tycho, 24 Oct 1599): opening and closing lines, every proper name (Gert/Talle Rantzau, Lindved, Mikell, Tyge, Niels Krag, Knud, Margrette, Kirsten, Lisebett, Jørgen, Beate Bille, Henrik Ramel, Erik, Hans Ernst, Sten Bille, Grynaeus), every number (9 miles to Lindved, 7 miles Breitenburg-Hamburg, 2-3 days, 6 days, three separate '3 weeks', 4000 gulden, 'two of the Emperor's councillors'), every date (24 Oct 1599, St Martin's day, dispatch date), and every place-name (Haderslev, Lindved, Breitenburg, Kiel, Flensburg, Wandsbek, Horsens, Copenhagen, Kolding, Landskrona i Skåne, Heidelberg, Basel, Fusingø). Our Danish matches Dreyer's print throughout — no TEXT_ERROR. Found one high-severity MISTRANSLATION that misidentifies Mikell's relationship to the code-named 'Titan' (almost certainly Erik Lange) and invents an act of writing absent from the source, plus one lower-confidence mistranslation of the archaic word for 'child' as 'kinswoman'. The first defect changes what a scholar reconstructing Sophie Brahe's/Erik Lange's correspondence network would take away, so the letter cannot stand at its self-assigned grade A.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). 'Titans skriuer' is a noun phrase ('Titan's secretary/scribe'), with 'Mikell' in apposition naming him — Mikell is the subject of 'hade werid' (had been), i.e. Titan's (Erik Lange's) secretary who went to Haderslev looking for Sophie. The English instead makes 'Titan' the subject of an invented verb 'writes', turning a plain narrative clause into a false report-by-letter and dropping the master-secretary relationship between Titan/Erik Lange and Mikell. At: “Titan writes that Mikell had been at Haderslev, and thought to have found me there about 3 weeks ago”.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). 'Kind' is the archaic Danish word for 'child' (cognate with English 'kin' / German 'Kind'), not 'kinswoman'; Lady Kirsten more likely thanks Tycho for having helped her child Lisbeth than an unspecified female relative. Lower confidence than the Titan/Mikell finding since 'kinswoman' is not indefensible, but the more probable reading is 'child'. At: “she thanks you for her kinswoman Lisbeth, whom you have helped”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defects were verified directly against Dreyer's page images, TBOO XIV pp.156-159 (the full letter), read visually in two 4-page chunks. Both source phrases match the current_latin exactly as printed by Dreyer on p.158, confirming no TEXT_ERROR and that both defects are pure translation issues. The Mikell/secretary reading is grammatically unambiguous. The 'kintt'/'child' reading is the more probable sense per the audit's own note (archaic Danish, cognate with 'kin'/'Kind') and is adopted, though 'kinswoman' was not indefensible — flagged low severity in the source audit and treated accordingly.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 7-14.. Originalbrev. Påskrevet note: Genealogia Braheorum, quam habuit prior canceĺlarius Nicolaus Kaas descriptam, quae proculdubio ad sororem eius pervenit, et Bildorum, si haberi potest."], "th": {"label": "Sophie Brahe", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000321001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000321001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports accompanying her son Tage on his journey, describing travel through Haderslev and Lindved to Breitenburg, where Gert Rantzau is helping arrange his onward passage to Basel.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991024001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991025001", "d": "1599-10-25", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Axel Brahe", "ck": "Axel Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Axel Brahe → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 25 October 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery / family) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 15f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELBIURDIGH MAND, THIGE BRAHE THILL KNUDTZTORP, MIN KIERE BRODER, GANDSKE BRODERLIGEN THILSCHREFFUIDT.</p><p>MYNN gandsche wennligh hilßenn nu och alltiid forßennd met Gud Kere Tyge och kere broder, nest mynn wennligh tacßielße for allt beuist gode, huilchit ieg aff formuenn steze wyll findis goduylig at forschyle. Kere broder, ieg haffuer fornumit wdaff dyn schriffuelße, att dynn leylighed er nu bleffuenn god, saa att du haffuer nu bekomit hoss keysseren gode wylkar, huilchit mig wdj sandhed møgitt kert er att høre; och som du wdj dinn schriffuelße est begerendis, att der som dig bliffuer giortt nogenn forhynndring paa dine jnstrumenter, att ieg daa will hielpe til, saa møgitt mugligtt war, at dig jngenn forhyndringh der paa matte sche, saa forsser ieg mig, att dig well jngen forhindringh der paa scher; men der som dett schede, will ieg giøre saa møgit derwdj som mig mugligtt ehr; och wdj huess made ieg wid digh att tienne, schall du altiid fynnde mig wiligh. Och wyll nu och altiid haffue digh Gud alme[ct]iste beffalendis met allt huiss digh kertt er.</p><p>DatumKøbenhaffnn denn 25 October 99.<br>Axel Brahe<br>egen hann.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Honourable and well-born man, Tyge Brahe to Knudstrup, my dear brother, written to in all brotherliness.</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always foresent with God, dear Tyge and dear brother; next, my friendly thanks for all the good [you have] shown, which I, of my ability, shall ever be found well-willing to requite. Dear brother, I have understood from your writing that your situation has now become good, so that you have now obtained good terms with the Emperor — which in truth is very dear to me to hear. And as, in your writing, you are requesting that, should any hindrance be done to you upon your instruments, I would then help, as much as may be possible, that no hindrance befall you thereon — so I trust that no hindrance does befall you thereon; but should it so happen, I will do as much therein as is possible to me; and in whatever way I know to serve you, you shall always find me willing. And [I] will, now and always, have you commended to God Almighty, with all that is dear to you.</p><p>Given at Copenhagen, the 25th of October, [15]99.<br>Axel Brahe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Axel Brahe</b> (1550–1616), Tycho's younger brother, a member of the Danish Council of the Realm (<i>Rigsråd</i>); writing warmly from Copenhagen. An original letter, signed <i>egen hånd</i> (\"own hand\").</li><li><b>Content.</b> Axel rejoices to learn that Tycho's affairs have turned out well — that he has secured <b>good terms with the Emperor</b> (Rudolf II) — and answers Tycho's request: should anyone obstruct him over his <b>instruments</b> (those still in Denmark, or their removal abroad), Axel will help as far as he can, though he trusts no such hindrance will arise. A letter of fraternal reassurance during Tycho's transition into imperial service.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Late-16th-c. Danish chancery spelling: <i>erlig</i> (ærlig, honourable), <i>welbiurdigh</i> (velbyrdig, well-born), <i>thilschreffuidt</i> (tilskrevet), <i>hilßenn</i> (hilsen), <i>tacßielße</i> (taksigelse, thanks), <i>leylighed</i> (situation/circumstance), <i>wylkar</i> (vilkår, terms), <i>forhynndring</i> (forhindring, hindrance), <i>jnstrumenter</i> (instruments), <i>beffalendis</i> (befalende, commending). Reading-verified as <b>Danish</b>. The bracketed <i>alme[ct]iste</i> (almægtigste) is an editorial restoration of a damaged spot; kept as printed.</li><li><b><i>Wor gunst</b></i> register: opens with the courteous <i>min ganske venlige hilsen … forsendt med Gud</i> (\"my quite friendly greeting … sent with God\"), the standard Danish noble epistolary salutation (cf. glossary §5.5).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>København denn 25 October 99</i> = <b>25 October 1599</b>; Denmark kept the <b>Julian / Old Style</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely; sender (Tycho's brother Axel), occasion (congratulations on the imperial appointment; the offer to protect his instruments), and date are all unambiguous. The single bracketed restoration (<i>alme[ct]iste</i>) is editorial, not a crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.160 (Dreyer letter 167, Axel Brahe to Tycho) as a page image; letter entire on this page. Danish source_text matches Dreyer (minor i/y spelling variants only, e.g. 'beuiist'/'beuyist', ignored as spelling convention). English translation checked clause-by-clause against the Danish, including the instruments/hindrance passage and the dateline '25 October 99' and signature 'Axel Brahe egen hand[d]'; fully faithful, no fabrication or omission.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 15 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Expresses gladness at hearing of Tycho's favourable terms with the Emperor and pledges to help prevent any interference with the retrieval of his instruments if it arises.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991025001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991029001", "d": "1599-10-29", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Eske Bille", "ck": "Eske Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Eske Bille → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 29 October 1599 [Old Style] *(postscript added 11 November)*", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 17–20 (original letter)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The letter's recovered passages — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> Tycho's Danish kinsman-agent <b>Eske Bille (Eskie Bylde)</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. A long estate/business letter on Tycho's residual Danish affairs. (The catalogue notes a <b>postscript of 11 November</b>; the source below runs to the 29 October signature.)</p>", "la": "<p>[AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.]</p><p>MYNN ganndtske wennlig helsenn nu och althidt forsendt med Vor Herre. Kierre Tyge<br>Brahe och kierre broder, betaker ieg dig ganndze wennligenn och giernne for<br>alltt gott, du mig giortt och beuist hafuer, huilkid igienn ad forskiulle du<br>althidt skall finde mig villig met huis ieg dig till ehre, villige och tieniste<br>were kanndtt.</p><p>Kierre broder, ehr din skriffuelse mig tilhendekommitt her vdj<br>Kiøbenhaffun ved din søn Tygge, och blantt andid, som du<br>skrifuer mig till, lader du mig vide dend guode leiglighedt, du hoeß rom. key.<br>maytt. erst kommenn vdj, huilked ehr mig (saa vell som flere dinne guoede<br>wenner, som nu her till denne herredag hafuer spurtt det) møgid kiertt, Gud<br>giffue dig fremdelis lyke. Anlangendis dett 50 daller, du skriffuer ieg skulle<br>lade m. Chrestenn Lenuig[!] bekomme,<br>saa siger dinn sønn Tyge dig vell<br>beskeed der om; thi ieg formerker, hand følgis med dennom, ellers skulle hannd<br>giernne haffue bekommid dennom, om dett end haffde werid mere paa att giøre.<br>Anlangendis dine jnstrumenter, saa hafuer ieg sagtt din sønn<br>beskied och min betenke der om, huorledis mig tykis de best kunde komme vd, dog<br>først her fra til Lybeck. Jeg kannd icke fortøffue her saa lenge vdj byen, att Thyge blifuer ferdig med boede jnstrumenterne att lade indpacke och huis andid hannd skall<br>bestille, thj ieg ehr wdj nogenn kong. maytt. befallinger, huor ieg nu vdj dag<br>drager aff, och der henn, som de skall fuldtgiøris.</p><p>Jeg formercker, att de 700 daller, som ieg for nogen tidlanng<br>sidenn haffuer sentt dig hen vd, vor dig icke tilhenndekommenn, der din sønn<br>drog fraa dig, dog formercker ieg aff Tyge, att hannd pa denne her reise hafuer fait nogenn beskiedenn<br>om thennom. Jeg haffuer endnu icke stilltt fru Sidtzell Vrnne tilfridtz, thi ieg endnu icke kand kome offuer<br>ens med hinde; hund vill haffue renthenn met hoffuitstollen, ieg will endnu lade<br>hinde hoffuitstollenn tillbiude, vill hund icke aname dennom, daa ehr det att<br>befryctte, at hund lader dig thiltalle for hoffuittstollenn och renten. Och som<br>du schriffuer ieg skulle tage en stefning offuer hinde thill herredagenn, saa<br>vedst du vel, att det ehr icke vertt, att mannd vdj slige sager kannd steffne<br>thill heredage, førind sagenn ehr forfulld thill heritzting och landtzting, och<br>kandschee dett skulle strecke sig ind thill rigenns canzeller, om mand sa skulle<br>talle hinde til effter fru Johann Oxxis<br>breff; och kandstu vell sielff tenke om sagen gick dig imod, huod for skade och<br>vlelighedt dett ville giffue dig; och paa dett du disbedre kand forfare, att<br>fru Johann Oxis breff icke kannd gielder stortt, saa<br>sennder ieg dig en copie aff en heritzthings domb, som rodtzens kontractt ehr<br>indførtt, saa du vell all leligheden der aff kannd forfare. Jeg haffuer och<br>ladid din sønn siett ett sandinge breff, som du med eigien hond hafuer<br>vnderskreffuitt, vdj huilke du forplicter dig att betalle hoffuittsummen med<br>huis tilbørlig renthe der aff kannd gaa. Aff din skriffuelse forfarer ieg<br>occsaa, attu ville komme her ind thill heredagenn, nu først komer, saa skallttu<br>vell troe, att her vdj denne sommer icke skeer herredage, effterdj denne heredag<br>hafuer nu standen saa langtt hen paa aaritt; videre hafuer ieg her om tallid med<br>din sønn Tygge, som hand<br>mundeligenn kannd berette dig.</p><p>Huod de 300 daller renthepending ehr anlangendis, som skall<br>forskikis vd ad Lantheholsten, de schall bliffue<br>forskikit der vd, huod heller ieg fanger dennom aff Axell Gylldenstierne eller huor ieg<br>tager dennom; och formerker ieg, du ville giernne haffue hoffuittsumen, som ehr<br>5000 daller, betallid, paa det du kunde bekomme ditt breff vdjgienn, som lydder<br>paa 10,000 daller, saa ved mand icke, om der stander løsßenkiendelse vdj<br>breffuitt, enthen paa halff aar eller paa fiering aar, huorfore du for att sende<br>mig en copie aff breffuitt, att mand til itt andid aar kunde<br>opschriffue dennom; thj nu thill denne omslag troer ieg icke det kand schee,<br>icke heller mand nu saa hastig kand opbringe sadanne en summa pennding,<br>vndertagenn mand haffde vist dett om s. Hannsdag tidtt, eller i dett seniste til<br>Lauerentj. Huod anlangenndis dinn stoedt, du haffde paa<br>Huenn, saa hafuer ieg denom icke<br>anderledis kunde faitt solld, end ieg motte sende thenom offuer thill Ellinge, der hafuer nu fougedenn sold dennom,<br>somme thill bønder och somme thill prester, saa ieg icke veid, huor dyre hannd<br>hafuer solld dennom, førind ieg kommer nu hiem och kannd forfare det. De heste,<br>som du om skriffuer, att ieg skule kiøbe til dig, 5 hafuer ieg bekommidt vd aff<br>Olluff Byllde, di 3, som gick for<br>hanns vøund, dend elste 7 och i ottende aar, och kiøbte ieg end vng folle vd aff<br>hanom, som ehr 3 och i fierde aar, som tiend vell til at gaa for vound hoeß de<br>andre thre, dend fembte hafuer ieg kiøbtt thill dig sielff att ride, och er hand<br>i fembte aar, och er icke tiulff paa, att hannd io ehr end edell guoed hest,<br>thersom hannd bliffuer ellers vorid och for haffre nock, daa schall det vell<br>bliffue sadanne en hest, att end første motte vell<br>passere att ridde paa; dersom det icke hafde verid dig paa at giørre, ville<br>ieg i sandhed icke hafde solltenn igienn for 150 daller. Jeg schall gifue hannom<br>for alle 5 hestene 300 daller, och denom betalle nu thill juell. Der ehr 2<br>foller paa Huenn, som dig tilkommer, end<br>rødblesid och en brunstiernid, som icke kand vere din ganning; thj ieg ville<br>gierne haffue solld denom for 40 daller, och mig icke biudis mer for dennom<br>begge 2 end 30 daler, dog hafuer ieg rodt din sønn, att hand skulle tage end vd<br>aff dennom met sig, som drengen kunde ride paa och trecke dend anden, paa det<br>dend andenn kunde bliffue forskonnidtt. Dend 6 hest haffuer ieg endnu icke kunde<br>bekomid orsagenn dj formener, her skulle blifue en munstring, huorfor huer<br>hafuer sine heste behouff. Jeg hafuer tagid end kudske i tieniste paa dine<br>veigne, som hafuer lofuid att ville følle din sønn vd, och ieg hafuer lofuid<br>hanom, att nar hand lyster icke lenger att bliffue der vde hoeß dig, at hand da<br>skule fa did pasbor och sin løn, saa hand vel kunde kome her i landid igienn;<br>hand hafuer och loffuid, att hand well vill forholle sig i sin tieniste huoeß<br>dig. Jeg haffuer och antagid enn staldreng paa dinne weignne, som haffuer ract<br>mig sinn hond, att ville thienne dig ett aar, och schall du giffue hannom thill<br>scho och schiorter et aar 6 daller och end engelstklenning och et paar<br>knestøffler; och haffuer ieg i lige mode loffuid hannom, att nar hanns aard ehr<br>ome och hannd icke lyster der vde lennger att thienne, att hannd daa schall faa<br>hanns pasbor och hanns lønn, saa hand kand komme her ind i rigid vdjgienn, och<br>hafde hannd en guoed tienniste, saa det stod hartt, att ieg kunnde faa hannom<br>løß der som hannd thientte.</p><p>Huod dj fische ehr annlanngendis, som du wille haffue indsaltidt,<br>har din sønn Tygge sielff werid<br>der paa lanndid och sielffuer bestilltt med dennom.</p><p>Nu haffuer ieg suorid dig paa denne dinn skriffuelse, och videre<br>haffuer ieg tallid med din sønn, som hannd mundligen kannd berette dig. Jeg vor<br>thilfridtz ieg haffde Dauid Pedersens<br>reegenschaff her inde for nogenn orsag schiulldt.</p><p>Disligiste kannd ieg icke<br>heller komme till nogenn ende med Dirick faruers quinde, wndertagenns ieg haffde hindis<br>regenschaff igienn, som ieg hafuer sent dig vdt, och befrøcter ieg mig, att hund<br>tør suplicere thill kong. maytt. eller thill hofmesteren, saa<br>kannd ieg dog ingenn vei komme met hinde, førind ieg bekommer det regenschaff<br>igienn, som ieg sende dig wdt, huorvdaff mand kannd forfare, att reegenschabidt<br>ehr icke ret klart. Dog kand ieg icke andid forsta effter dinne wdgiffnne<br>seddeller, saa och effter dennd beuiß, hund haffer vdaff kiøbmend och kremmere,<br>attu io bliffuer hinde pending skiulldige.</p><p>Prestenn paa Hueen ehr<br>begierendis, att hannd motte bekomme dend smede, som du haffuer ladidt opsette<br>der paa landidt, thj end stor dog och forfaler, effterdj prestegordenn ehr vdj<br>din tidt blefuen forfaldenn; saa haffuer ieg dog icke same smede hannom ville<br>samtycke, førind ieg fich din ville der om. Ladegordenn stor och forfallder, saa<br>det schall verre att befrycte, att feehusitt schal ner i sommer eller dett<br>faller ner vdaff sig sielffuer; ellers veed ieg dig nu paa denne tidt inthid<br>andidt, att ieg kannd skriffue dig thill, mennd will nu och althid haffue dig<br>Gud alsommectigste befalendis med mannge guoede netter, som minn fader och moder<br>och Eschie Brock och Jørgenn Brockenhuß och ieg ynscher dig<br>och alle dinne och di, dig well will.</p><p>Aff Kiøbenhaffun med stoer hast dend 29 octobris<br>anno 1599.</p><p>Eskie Byllde.</p><p>Kierre Thyge, sidenn dette breff vor<br>schreffuenn, ehr ieg kommen hid thill byenn wdigienn, och hafuer ieg taldt med<br>din sønn, och formerker ieg, att hand hafuer bekomid<br>schriffuelse, att hand endnu skall forschafe dig vd sa mange som en 14 heste<br>thilsammenn, huilkid ieg troer icke mugeligtt ehr, att hannd paa sa stackidt en<br>tidt kan opbringe end 7 heste; thj ieg vdj dise 4 vgger, Thyge hafuer vert her vdj landidt,<br>hafft stor handell, førind ieg kunde bringe dise till weie, dj ieg hafuer<br>bekommitt. J daug hafuer ieg kiøbitt 2 heste, enn vdaff Axell Oggesenn for 45 daller, huilckid hest<br>ieg haffuer vdspurdt att schulle werre en guoed vforderffuitt klepper, saa hannd<br>och vell kannd thienne thill dig sielff att ride, eller huilkenn din venn du<br>willtt giffue denn, daa kandstu ret vell pasere met denn. Denn anndenn kiøbtte<br>ieg vdaff Baldzer Fox for 40 daller,<br>schall det och vell bliffue en guoed hest vd aff dend, dog hand ehr vberedenn.<br>Nar hand kommer till foer, kandstu och i lige moder pasere met dend thill huem<br>du villtt giffuenn, thj hand schall och faa et gott riden och et smoct trau, och<br>ehr der ingen vdaff dise 2 heste eldre end i 5 aar, och beløber sig nu vdj<br>pendinge for dj 7 heste, der ieg hafuer forschafid din sønn, halfierdie hunder<br>och 35 daller. Gud giffue di ma vell komme vd thill dig vdenn skade. Jeg vill<br>icke rode dig du schillir dig ved den enne hest, som ieg kiøbtte vdaff Olluff Biillde. Dersom ieg ville giffue 200<br>daler for en hest thill dig eller enn andenn min venn, viste ieg icke sadane en<br>att kunde bekomme.</p><p>Din sønn och ieg hafuer i daug handlidt med din gamlle<br>voundriffuer, saa hand haffuer loffuidt, att vill følis vd met din sønn, och<br>hafuer ieg lofuid hannom till lønn om oridt 14 daller och enn lang kioell, och i<br>lige moder hafuer ieg loffuidt hanom, nar hand lyster icke lennger att bliffue<br>dervde, daa schall hannd faa sin guoede afscheed aff dig, saa hannd vell kannd<br>kome hervdj landid igienn.</p><p>Huod din gord ehr anlangendis hervdj byenn, hafuer Soffi Paxsis bøden mig 1500 daller forind,<br>dog paa 2 aard att betallind; fru Margrette Rosenkranzis hafuer bødenn mig och for dend 1400<br>daller paa ett aard att betalle; dog hafuer ieg intid kiøb kunde indga met nogen<br>aff denom, orsagen din søster Soffj<br>icke er i lanndid, och ieg forstar, att hun schall haffue beschieden paa samme<br>gord att selle. Dertillmet, huem same gord vill kiøbe, skall forplicte sig imod<br>dig att schall holle enn faruer vdj samme gord, huilkid ieg icke troer, att<br>nepeligenn nogenn aff dise fruer giør. Giør vell och beholl sielff dend hest,<br>som ieg fick wdaff Olluff Biilde, din søn<br>thall vell videre med dig om dend. Gud alsommectigste vill ieg haffue dig<br>befalid, som ieg ynscher eder allesammenn.</p><p>ActumKiøbenhaffun dend 11 nouembris anno 99.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[To Tycho Brahe.]</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always sent with Our Lord. Dear Tyge Brahe and dear brother, I thank you quite friendly and gladly for all the good you have done and shown me — which to requite, you shall always find me willing, in whatever I can be to your honour, will, and service.</p><p>Dear brother, your letter reached me here in Copenhagen by your son <b>Tyge</b>; and among other things you write me, you let me know <b>the good situation you have come into with the Roman Imperial Majesty</b> — which is very dear to me (as to your other good friends who have now heard it here at this <b>Herredag</b> [assembly of nobles]); God give you further luck. Concerning the <b>50 thaler</b> you write I should let <b>M. Christen Lenvig[?] [Longomontanus?]</b> have — your son Tyge will tell you well about it; for I perceive he travels with them [the instruments], else he would gladly have got them, even had more been at stake. Concerning <b>your instruments</b>, I have told your son my opinion how I think they could best be got out — but first from here to <b>Lübeck</b>. I cannot tarry here in the town so long that Tyge finishes packing both the instruments and his other business, for I am under certain royal commands, [for] which I now set out this very day, [to go] thither where they are to be carried out.</p><p>I perceive that <b>the 700 thaler I sent out to you some time ago</b> had not reached you when your son left you; yet I perceive from Tyge that on this journey he has got some account of them. <b>I have not yet satisfied Lady Sidsel Urne</b>, for I still cannot agree with her: she wants the interest with the principal; I will yet offer her the principal; if she will not accept it, then it is to be feared she will have you sued for the principal and interest. And as you write that I should take a summons against her to the Herredag — you well know it is not worth it, that in such cases one [cannot] summon to the Herredag before the case is pursued at <b>herredsting and landsting</b> [district + provincial courts], and perhaps it would reach as far as the realm's <b>Chancellor</b>, if one were to sue her by <b>Lady Johan Oxe's letter</b>; and you can well think yourself, if the case went against you, what damage and trouble it would give you. And that you may the better learn that Lady Johan Oxe's letter cannot count for much, I send you a copy of a <b>herredsting verdict</b> in which the … contract is entered, so that you can well learn the whole situation thence. I have also let your son see <b>a guarantee-letter you have signed with your own hand</b>, in which you bind yourself to pay the principal sum with whatever due interest may run from it. From your letter I also learn that you would come in [to Denmark] for the Herredag, [if it] should come now first; but you may well believe that this summer no Herredag will be held, since this one has now stood so late in the year. Further I have spoken about this with your son Tyge, as he can report to you by word of mouth.</p><p>As concerns the <b>300 thaler interest-money to be sent out to Land-Holstein</b>, it shall be sent thither, whether I get it from <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> or wherever I take it. And I perceive <b>you would gladly have the principal sum — which is 5000 thaler — paid, so that you could get back your bond, which reads on 10,000 thaler</b>; but one does not know whether there is a redemption-clause in the bond, on half-year or quarter-year [terms] — wherefore you [should] send me a copy of the bond, so that one could give notice on it for another year; for to <b>this [Lübeck] settlement-term [<i>omslag</i>]</b> I do not think it can be done, nor can one so quickly raise such a sum of money — unless one had known it by <b>St. Hans' time [24 June]</b>, or at the latest by <b>Laurence [10 August]</b>. Concerning <b>your stud [of horses] you had on Hven</b>, I could get them sold no otherwise than [that] I had to send them over to <b>Ellinge</b>, where the bailiff has now sold them — some to peasants and some to priests — so that I do not know how dearly he sold them, until I now come home and can learn it.</p><p><b>The horses you write I should buy for you:</b> I have got 5 from <b>Oluf Bilde</b> — the 3 that went before his wagon, the eldest 7-and-in-his-eighth-year; and I bought a young foal from him, 3-and-in-his-fourth-year, fit to go before the wagon with the other three; the fifth I bought for <b>you yourself to ride</b>, in his fifth year — and there is no doubt he is a noble good horse: if he is otherwise cared for and gets oats enough, <b>he will become such a horse that a prince might well ride him</b>; were it not for your sake, I truly would not have sold him again for 150 thaler. I shall give 300 thaler for all 5 horses, to be paid now at Christmas. There are <b>2 foals on Hven that fall to you</b>, a red-blazed and a brown-starred, which cannot be your riding-horse [<i>ganning</i>?]; for I would gladly have sold them for 40 thaler, but I am offered no more than 30 thaler for both — yet I have advised your son to take one of them with him, which the boy could ride and lead the other, so the other could be spared. The 6th horse I have not yet been able to get, because they think there will be <b>a muster</b> here, so everyone has need of his horses. I have taken <b>a coachman</b> into service on your behalf, who has promised to follow your son out [to you]; and I have promised him that when he no longer wishes to stay out there with you, he shall have his passport and his wages, so he may come back into this country; he has also promised to conduct himself well in his service with you. I have likewise engaged <b>a stable-boy</b> on your behalf, who has given me his hand to serve you a year; and you shall give him, for shoes and shirts a year, <b>6 thaler, and an English-cloth garment and a pair of knee-boots</b>; and I have likewise promised him that when his year is up and he does not wish to serve longer out there, he shall have his passport and his wages, so he may come back into the realm — and he had a good service, so that it stood hard [for me] to get him free where he served.</p><p>As concerns <b>the fish you would have salted down</b>, your son Tyge was himself there on the land and arranged with them himself.</p><p>Now I have given you my oath on this your letter, and further I have spoken with your son, as he can report to you by mouth. I should be content [if] I had <b>David Pedersen's accounts</b> here, for a certain reason.</p><p>Likewise I cannot come to any end with <b>Dirik the dyer's wife</b>, unless I had back her accounts, which I sent out to you; and I fear she dares supplicate to the King's Majesty or to the <b>Court-Master [Hofmester]</b>, so that I can come no way with her until I get back the accounts I sent out to you — from which one can learn that the account is not quite clear. Yet I cannot understand otherwise — by your issued notes, and by the proof she has from merchants and shopkeepers — than that <b>you do remain owing her money</b>.</p><p><b>The priest on Hven</b> desires to get the <b>smithy</b> you had set up there on the land, since a big [building] decays — for the <b>parsonage became dilapidated in your time</b>; yet I would not grant him the same smithy before I got your will on it. <b>The manor-farm [Ladegård] stands and decays, so that it is to be feared the cowhouse will collapse this summer, or fall down of itself.</b> Otherwise I know nothing else at this time to write you; but will now and always commend you to God Almighty, with many good-nights — as my father and mother, and <b>Eske Brock and Jørgen Brockenhus</b> and I wish you, and all yours, and those who wish you well.</p><p>From Copenhagen, in great haste, the 29th of October, the year 1599.<br><b>Eske Bille.</b></p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Dear Tyge, since this letter was written, I have come back here to town, and I have spoken with your son, and I perceive that he has received word that he is yet to procure for you, sent out, as many as a full 14 horses altogether — which I do not think possible, that he can raise even 7 horses in so short a time; for in these 4 weeks that Tyge has been here in the country I have had great trouble before I could bring together these which I have got. Today I have bought 2 horses: one from Axel Oggesen for 45 thaler — which horse I have inquired about [and found] to be a good unspoiled cob, so that he can well serve for you yourself to ride, or [for] whichever of your friends you would give him to, [and] then you may very well make do with him. The other I bought from Baldzer Fox for 40 thaler; a good horse too will come of him, though he is unbroken. When he comes to fodder, you may likewise make do with him [for] whomever you would give him to, for he too shall get a good gait and a pretty trot; and there is none of these 2 horses older than in his 5th year. And it now amounts, in money, for the 7 horses I have procured for your son, to three hundred and eighty-five thaler. God grant they may come out to you without harm. I will not advise you to part with the one horse that I bought from Oluf Bilde; were I to give 200 thaler for a horse for you or another friend of mine, I should not know how to get such a one.</p><p>Your son and I have today dealt with your old wagon-driver, so that he has promised he will follow your son out [to you]; and I have promised him, for wages a year, 14 thaler and a long coat, and I have likewise promised him that, when he no longer wishes to stay out there, he shall have his good discharge from you, so that he may well come back into this country.</p><p>As concerns your house here in the town: Soffi Pax has offered me 1500 thaler for it, yet to be paid over 2 years; Lady Margrethe Rosenkrantz has likewise offered me 1400 thaler for it, to be paid in one year; yet I have been able to close no sale with either of them, because your sister Sophie is not in the country, and I understand that she is to have a say in the selling of the same house. Moreover, whoever wishes to buy the same house must bind himself to you to keep a dyer on the premises — which I hardly believe any of these ladies would do. Be so good as to keep for yourself the horse I got from Oluf Bilde; your son will speak further with you about it. I will have commended you to God Almighty, as I wish you all [well].</p><p>Done at Copenhagen, the 11th of November [15]99.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Eske Bille (Eskie Bylde)</b> — Tycho's kinsman (\"dear brother\") and trusted <b>Danish estate-agent</b> (the \"Eske Bille\" who handles Tycho's Danish payments throughout the corpus, e.g. the Mecklenburg-debt thread) — <b>to Tycho</b>. An original autograph (Basel).</li><li><b>Significance — the winding-down of Tycho's Danish estate and Hven establishment (a rich documentary letter).</b> A detailed account of Tycho's residual affairs, two-plus years after he left Denmark:</li><li><b>The instruments still at Hven</b>, to be shipped <b>out via Lübeck</b> (Tycho's son <b>Tyge</b> [Tycho the Younger] packing them).</li><li><b>Debts and creditors:</b> the <b>50 thaler</b> for \"M. Christen\" (possibly <b>Longomontanus</b>); the <b>700 thaler</b> already sent; the dispute with <b>Lady Sidsel Urne</b> (principal vs interest) and the legal counsel against summoning her to the Herredag (the proper course being <b>herredsting → landsting → Chancellor</b>); the <b>\"Lady Johan Oxe's letter\"</b> and a <b>herredsting verdict</b> sent as evidence; Tycho's own <b>signed bond</b>.</li><li><b>The 5000/10,000-thaler bond:</b> the <b>300 thaler interest to Holstein</b> (via <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b>), and Tycho's wish to <b>redeem the 5000-thaler principal to recover his bond \"which reads on 10,000 thaler\"</b> — Bille asks for a copy to check the redemption-terms; not feasible by the coming <b>Lübeck <i>omslag</b></i> (settlement-term).</li><li><b>The Hven livestock</b> (the \"stud\") sold off at <b>Ellinge</b>; the <b>5 riding/draught horses bought from Oluf Bilde</b> (one \"a prince might ride,\" 300 thaler the lot at Christmas); the <b>2 Hven foals</b> and the <b>6th horse</b> held up by a coming <b>muster</b>.</li><li><b>Servants hired</b> on Tycho's behalf — <b>a coachman and a stable-boy</b> (with detailed wages: 6 thaler + English cloth + knee-boots), both with return-passport guarantees.</li><li><b>The salted fish</b>; <b>David Pedersen's accounts</b>; the unresolved dispute with <b>Dirik the dyer's wife</b> (whom Tycho \"remains owing money\").</li><li><b>The decay of Hven (poignant).</b> The <b>parsonage dilapidated \"in your time,\"</b> the priest wanting Tycho's <b>smithy</b>; and <b>the manor-farm (Ladegård) decaying, the cowhouse near collapse</b> — a vivid image of Uraniborg's working estate falling to ruin after Tycho's departure.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Eske Bille; <b>Tycho the Younger</b> (\"son Tyge\"); <b>\"M. Christen Lenvig\"</b> (a garbled name, possibly <b>Longomontanus</b> — flagged); <b>Lady Sidsel Urne</b>; <b>Lady Johan Oxe</b>; <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b>; <b>Oluf Bilde</b>; <b>David Pedersen</b>; <b>Dirik the dyer</b> &amp; wife; the <b>priest on Hven</b>; <b>Christian IV</b> (\"the King\") &amp; the <b>Court-Master</b>; Bille's <b>father and mother</b>, <b>Eske Brock</b>, <b>Jørgen Brockenhus</b>. Places: <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Hven</b>, <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Ellinge</b>, Land-Holstein.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>dend 29 octobris anno 1599</i> at Copenhagen (Denmark = Old Style) → <b>29 October 1599 [Old Style]</b>; a <b>postscript of 11 November</b> is noted in the catalogue (not in the source text below the signature). Header and label agree.</li><li><b>Reading notes.</b> A few Danish words are uncertain in the copy: <i>ganning</i> (rendered \"riding-horse\" by context), <i>\"Christen Lenvig\"</i> (possibly Longomontanus), <i>rodtzens</i> (\"the …'s contract\" — left unfilled). Flagged; none affect the overall sense.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The 11-November continuation/postscript (written after the 29 October signature): the 14-horses-impossible note; two horses bought (Axel Oggesen 45 dlr, Baldzer Fox 40 dlr → 7 horses = 435 dlr); keep the Oluf Bilde horse; the old wagon-driver hired (14 dlr/yr + a long coat); the Copenhagen-house offers (Soffi Pax 1500 / Margrethe Rosenkrantz 1400). Restored from Dreyer XIV pp.162–163 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> The final three sentences of the postscript Eske Bille added on 11 November 1599 (twelve days after the main letter of 29 October) to his long letter of family and estate business to Tycho Brahe, then in the course of relocating toward Emperor Rudolf II's court. The gap sat between \"...because your sister Sophie is not in the country\" and the closing \"God Almighty...\" formula: the prior translation truncated that very sentence and silently dropped the two that followed.</li><li><b>Sophie Brahe's role in the house sale.</b> The recovered clause shows the sale of Tycho's Copenhagen house was stalled not only by Sophie Brahe's absence abroad but because she was understood to have a say in whether it was sold at all — a concrete 1599 data-point for her documented independence in managing family affairs (cf. Ellefsen, <i>Søsteren og astronomen</i>, 2023, the standard biography, on her travels and agency in these years).</li><li><b>The dyer covenant.</b> A previously-lost, specific detail of Tycho's Copenhagen townhouse: any buyer had to bind himself to keep a <i>farver</i> (dyer) on the premises — evidently a tenant trade attached to the property — a condition Eske Bille judged neither prospective buyer would accept, and which he offers as his own explanation for why both offers stalled.</li><li><b>The two prospective buyers</b>, named nowhere else in the letter: Soffi Pax (1500 thaler over 2 years) and Lady Margrethe Rosenkrantz (1400 thaler over 1 year).</li><li><b>The closing horse reminder</b> ('the horse I got from Oluf Bilde') echoes, as a final aside before signing off, the fine riding-horse Eske Bille singles out earlier in the letter body ('such a horse that a prince might well ride him') and again in the postscript proper ('I will not advise you to part with the one horse that I bought from Oluf Bilde') — the same animal, restated rather than a new item.</li><li><b>Eske Bille</b>, the letter's author, was a close friend of Tycho who visited him often on Hven and later (1602-10) held the fief of Helsingborg Castle [lensmand paa Helsingborg Slot] (per Vellev, in <i>Danske Magazin</i>); here he is acting as Tycho's man of business in Copenhagen during the 1599 departure, managing horses, debts, the Hven stud's dispersal, and now the town house.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The postscript's horse-purchase total, previously translated \"four hundred and thirty-five thaler,\" is corrected to \"three hundred and eighty-five thaler\": the Danish \"halfierdie hunder och 35 daller\" (Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.163) uses the old Scandinavian counting form halvfjerde hundrede for 350, giving 350+35=385, confirmed independently by the letter's own itemised horse prices (300 + 45 + 40 = 385).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Early Modern Danish is fully legible and construed with confidence; the substance (the <b>Hven instruments shipped via Lübeck</b>; the catalogue of <b>debts and creditors</b> — Sidsel Urne, the 700-thaler remittance, the 5000/10,000-thaler bond and the Holstein interest via Axel Gyldenstierne; the <b>horses bought from Oluf Bilde</b> and the foals/muster; the <b>coachman and stable-boy</b> hired; the salted fish; the <b>Dirik-the-dyer's-wife</b> debt; and the <b>decaying Hven parsonage, smithy, and manor-farm</b>) is firmly rendered, with no smoothing of Bille's idiom. The flagged items are a few uncertain Danish words (<i>ganning</i>, \"Christen Lenvig,\" <i>rodtzens</i>), none material.</p><p>The recovered Danish is plain secretary-hand estate prose, consistent in register and vocabulary with the rest of the letter, and poses no serious philological difficulty. My only hesitation is the precise sense of 'beschieden paa...att selle' (rendered 'a say in the selling') — it could mean either that Sophie Brahe's formal consent was legally required or merely that she was expected to be consulted/informed; both readings support the same narrative point and do not affect the apparatus. 'Gord' is translated 'house' throughout to match the existing translator's consistent choice for this same word earlier in the letter (an urban Copenhagen property, not a rural farm). Upgrade path: collation against the diplomatic edition at tekstnet.dk and, where extant, the original manuscript's readings for 'beschieden' and 'faruer'.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.159-164 as page images (chunks 1-4, then 5-6) and collated source_text word-for-word against pp.160-163, which carry the full letter (no.168, Eske Bille to Tycho, 29 Oct/11 Nov 1599, body + postscript): opening and closing lines, every proper name (Tyge, Christen Lenvig, Sidsel Urne, Johan Oxe, Axel Gyldenstierne, Ellinge, Oluf Bilde, David Pedersen, Dirik the dyer's wife, Eske Brock, Jørgen Brockenhus, Axel Oggesen, Baldzer Fox, Soffi Pax, Margrethe Rosenkrantz), every sum of money (50, 700, 5000/10,000, 300, 150, 300, 40/30, 6, 45, 40, 14, 1500, 1400 thaler), every horse age (7-going-8th, 3-going-4th, 5th year, none older than 5th year), and every date (29 Oct 1599, 11 Nov [15]99, St Hans' day, Laurence). Our Danish matches Dreyer's print throughout, including the disputed sum, confirming this is a translation error and not a TEXT_ERROR. Found one high-severity MISTRANSLATION: a sum of money, 'halvfjerde hundrede og 35 daler' (385 thaler), rendered as 435 thaler — an error independently confirmed by the letter's own itemized horse prices. This changes what a scholar would take away about the cost Eske Bille incurred provisioning Tycho's horses, so the letter cannot stand at its self-assigned grade B.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). 'Halvfjerde hundrede' is the old Scandinavian counting form for three-and-a-half hundred (350), so 'halvfjerde hundrede og 35 daler' = 385 thaler, not 435. Confirmed independently from the letter's own itemized prices: 300 thaler for the first 5 horses (stated earlier in the same letter) plus 45 and 40 thaler for the two horses bought in the postscript = 385, not 435. At: “it now amounts, in money, for the 7 horses I have procured for your son, to four hundred and thirty-five thaler”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against all six Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO XIV pp.159-164, read in two chunks of four and two pages). The Danish source text as published already reads 'halfierdie hunder och 35 daller' correctly, matching Dreyer's p.163 exactly; the sole defect was the English translation's arithmetic, which the audit's derivation (halvfjerde hundrede = 350) and the letter's own itemised sums (300+45+40=385) both confirm as 385, not 435. No other cruces identified in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 17-20.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Eske Bille", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000322001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000322001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's good standing with the Emperor (noted at the Herredag assembly), discusses 50 thaler owed to Longomontanus, and advises routing the instruments via Lübeck.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991029001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991104001", "d": "1599-11-04", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bologna", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Bologna, 4 November 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: G. A. Magini,<i> Tabulae primi mobilis </i>(as printed; = Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>VIII) · MS witness: A = Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOHANNES ANTONIUS MAGINUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>VLTIMA Bacanalium die fuit ad me Dominus FRANCISCUS TENGNALIUS, nobilis iuvenis, omnique virtute ornatissimus, et talis qualis a te mihi descriptus fuit, qui deferendo ad me tuas literas unâ cum munere tui libelli de machinis Astronomicis et de restitutione stellarum fixarum adeo mentem meam gaudio affecit, ut nescirem quo me continerem, sed haec mea laetitia aliquantum remissa fuit, quando intellexi, ipsum discessurum esse sequenti mane Florentiam versus, nec posse mihi hîc referre ea quae ipsi secreto, ut mecum communicaret, imposuisti, qui tamen mihi promisit se hoc facturum post suum reditum Bononiae circiter dimidium Quadragesimae, sed frustra per totam Quadragesimam a me expectatus fuit, imo et totam aestatem, cum toto hoc tempore Florentiae et Romae commoratus sit. Quin et ego quoque die 15a Maij me Mantuam traduxi vocatus a Serenissimo Duce Mantuae, ut Principes eius filios in Mathematicis erudirem; ubi permansi tota aestate, nec Bononiae redire potui ante primum Novembris, tempore scilicet novi studij. In hoc autem meo reditu inveni literas Domini FRANCISCI tui, quibus me commonefecit se quam primum velle Bononiae redire, ut mihi exponere posset tuum desiderium, quod post duos dies fecit, relinquens mihi simul quaedam scripta pro instructione dicti negocij. Valde profecto mihi grata fuit haec occasio tibi gratificandi, et plus quam credi possit, quod eventus rei ipsius Deo volente tibi clare declarabit, neque enim nunc verbis hoc tibi indicare studebo, sed effectibus ipsis me facturum reservo; habeo enim qui hanc provinciam libentissime suscipiet, qui mihi inprimis devotissimus est, et habeo etiam plures literatos amicos per totam Italiam, qui et mihi et huic omne auxilium praestabunt.</p><p>Plura ad te scribere deberem, nisi improvisus reditus Domini FRANCISCI et maturus discessus eius obstarent; praesertim cum multis curis et molestijs familiaribus nunc afficiar. Nolo tamen praetermittere, quin te subnixe et instantissime rogem, ut mihi gratissime communices Tabulas motuum luminarium a te editas et forsan publicatas unâ cum eius usu pro his temporibus; cupio enim primus omnium Ephemerides condere novas secundum tuas observationes et interim, dum alias tabulas absolves, potero ego supputare motus luminarium in eclipsibus et insuper figuras ingressus Solis in puncta cardinalia erigere et lunationes omnes perpendere, et ex his iudicia annua contexere; nam pro hoc negocio sufficiet calculus reliquorum planetarum iuxta usitatam rationem COPERNICI.</p><p>De meo statu certior fies a Domino FRANCISCO tuo, scilicet me esse perturbationibus onustum, qua de causa finem huic epistolae facio, rogans te efficaciter ut haec omnia in bonam feras partem, meque ut verum et sincerum amicum et devotissimum servum habeas. Deum optimum deprecor, ut tuis votis satisfaciat plene.</p><p>Vale.<br>Bononia 4 Novembris 1599.<br>Perillustri Dominationi tuae<br>addictissimus<br>IO. ANTONIUS MAGINUS,<br>Patavinus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>GIOVANNI ANTONIO MAGINI TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>On the last day of Carnival, Lord Franz Tengnagel came to me — a noble young man, most adorned with every virtue, and just such as you described him to me — who, by delivering to me your letter together with the gift of your little book on Astronomical machines and on the restitution of the fixed stars, so affected my mind with joy that I knew not how to contain myself. But this gladness of mine was somewhat lessened when I learned that he was to depart the next morning toward Florence, and could not relate to me here the things which you had secretly enjoined upon him to communicate with me; he promised me, however, that he would do this after his return to Bologna, about mid-Lent. But he was awaited by me in vain through the whole of Lent — indeed through the whole summer too, since during all this time he stayed at Florence and Rome. Moreover I too, on the 15th of May, removed to Mantua, summoned by the Most Serene Duke of Mantua to instruct his sons, the Princes, in Mathematics; where I remained the whole summer, nor could I return to Bologna before the first of November — the time, namely, of the new [academic] term. But on this return of mine I found a letter from your [man] Franz, by which he advised me that he wished to return to Bologna as soon as possible, so as to lay out for me your desire — which he did two days later, leaving with me at the same time certain writings for the instruction of the said business. Truly this occasion of obliging you was very welcome to me, and more than can be believed — as the outcome of the matter itself, God willing, will clearly declare to you; for I will not now strive to indicate this to you in words, but reserve myself to do it by the very effects. For I have one who will most gladly undertake this charge, who is especially devoted to me; and I have, besides, several learned friends throughout all Italy, who will render every help both to me and to him.</p><p>I ought to write more to you, did not the unexpected return of Lord Franz and his early departure stand in the way — especially since I am now beset with many cares and domestic troubles. Yet I will not omit earnestly and most insistently to ask you that you most kindly share with me the <b>Tables of the motions of the luminaries</b> composed by you, and perhaps published, together with their use for these present times; for I desire, first of all men, to compose new <b>Ephemerides</b> according to your observations; and meanwhile, while you complete the other tables, I shall be able to compute the motions of the luminaries in eclipses, and moreover to erect figures of the Sun's ingress into the cardinal points, and to weigh all the lunations, and from these to weave the annual [astrological] judgements — for, for this business, the calculation of the remaining planets by the usual method of <b>Copernicus</b> will suffice.</p><p>Of my own state you will be informed more fully by your [man] Franz — namely, that I am laden with disturbances; for which reason I make an end of this letter, asking you earnestly that you take all this in good part, and hold me as a true and sincere friend and most devoted servant. I pray God most good that he may fully satisfy your prayers.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Bologna, 4 November 1599.<br>Most devoted to Your Very Illustrious Lordship,<br>Giovanni Antonio Magini,<br>of Padua.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of mathematics at <b>Bologna</b>, cartographer, ephemeris-maker and practising astrologer — Tycho's principal Italian correspondent (cf. brahe-t_15900911001). He subscribes <i>Patavinus</i> (\"of Padua,\" his birthplace). The MS is in the <b>Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici</b>, Bologna; the letter was printed in Magini's <i>Tabulae primi mobilis</i>.</li><li><b>Tengnagel as courier (cross-links).</b> <i>Franciscus Tengnalius</i> = <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, Tycho's assistant (later son-in-law), travelling in Italy — the same figure whose Italian journey frames the Tycho→Galileo and Tycho→Concini letters of 4 May 1600 (brahe-t_16000504002, brahe-t_16000504001) and the Melissus correspondence. He reached Magini <i>\"on the last day of Carnival\"</i> (Shrovetide, early 1599), delivering Tycho's letter and <b>the gift of two books</b> — <i>\"the little book on Astronomical machines and on the restitution of the fixed stars,\"</i> i.e. the <b>_Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica_ (Wandsbek 1598)</b> and the <b>fixed-star catalogue</b> (the same pairing Tycho presented to Duke Ulrich, brahe-t_15980914002). Tengnagel's movements (Florence, Rome; Magini's own absence at Mantua) delayed delivery of Tycho's confidential message until Magini's return on <b>1 November 1599</b>.</li><li><b>The \"secret business\" (<i>secretum negotium</i> / <i>dictum negotium</i>).</b> Tycho had charged Tengnagel with a confidential commission for Magini, supported by \"certain writings for the instruction of the said business\"; Magini promises to further it warmly through \"one who will most gladly undertake this charge\" and his learned friends across Italy, but pointedly <b>will not name it in writing</b> (\"I will not indicate this in words, but reserve myself to do it by the very effects\"). The referent is not stated; it plausibly concerns Tycho's <b>Italian observational/placement plans</b> (cf. the Siena-Academy-and-Egypt observer scheme of brahe-t_16000504001) — flagged as a hypothesis, not asserted.</li><li><b>Magini at the Gonzaga court.</b> <i>Serenissimus Dux Mantuae</i> = the <b>Duke of Mantua</b> (Vincenzo I Gonzaga); Magini was summoned (from 15 May 1599) to <b>teach the ducal princes mathematics</b>, returning to Bologna only for the new academic term on 1 November.</li><li><b>The request — Tycho's solar/lunar tables for new ephemerides (astronomy + §5.1/§5.3).</b> Magini asks for Tycho's <b>_Tabulae motuum luminarium_</b> (tables of the Sun's and Moon's motions) so as to be \"<b>first of all men</b>\" to compute <b>new Ephemerides</b> on Tycho's observations: he will calculate the luminaries' motions <b>in eclipses</b>, erect <b>figures of the Sun's ingress into the cardinal points</b> (the equinox/solstice charts), weigh <b>all the lunations</b>, and from these \"weave the <b>annual judgements</b>\" (astrological prognostications) — for which the <b>Copernican</b> computation of the other planets \"will suffice.\" A vivid statement of how Tychonic data was wanted to feed the ephemeris-and-prognostication industry.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Magini; Franz Tengnagel; the Duke of Mantua; Copernicus.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Bononia 4 Novembris 1599</i> — Catholic Italy was on the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> calendar; marked [N.S.]. (Hence too <i>ultima Bacanalium die</i> and <i>dimidium Quadragesimae</i> reckon the 1599 Roman-rite Carnival/Lent.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear humanist Latin, read securely; the narrative (Tengnagel's Carnival-1599 delivery of the <i>Mechanica</i> + star-catalogue and a confidential commission, his delays via Florence/Rome, Magini's Mantua summons, the November return) and the substantive request (Tycho's luminary-tables for new Tychonic ephemerides and annual prognostications, with Copernican computation for the other planets) are all firmly construed. The one genuinely opaque point — the deliberately unnamed <i>secretum negotium</i> — is flagged, not invented.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.189-192 as page images; the Magini letter runs 'IOHANNES ANTONIUS MAGINUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE' at the foot of p.190 through the signature 'Io. Antonius Maginus, Patavinus' on p.191. Collated the full letter word for word against Dreyer, including every proper name and date: Franciscus Tengnalius (Tengnagel), Bononia/Bologna, the 15th of May remove to Mantua, the Serenissimus Dux Mantuae, return before the first of November, Copernicus, and the closing date 'Bononia 4 Novembris 1599' with signature 'Patavinus'. Our published Latin source_text matches Dreyer's printed text exactly (allowing normal long-s/ligature variance) and the English translation renders every clause, name, and date faithfully with no additions, drops, or flips. No defects of any type found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici."], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15981128001", "next": "brahe-t_16000103002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15981128001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000103002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports Tengnagel's Carnival visit delivering Tycho's letter and the Mechanica, explains why Tengnagel could not convey the secret commission, and accounts for the ensuing delay in replying.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991104001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991116001", "d": "1599-11-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Jiřice", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg — Girsitz, 16 November 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek and astronomical/zodiacal glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (Afskrift / copy); B = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note.</b> A long, three-stranded letter to the Bavarian Chancellor <b>Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (companion to Tycho's reply of 28 Aug 1600): (i) the <b>exile/patronage narrative</b> — the instruments' transport from Magdeburg down the Elbe, Rudolf II's favour through <b>Barvitius</b>, and the plague-flight from Benátky to <b>Girsitz</b>; (ii) a <b>dense technical argument</b> that the obliquity of the ecliptic is essentially constant (the ancients merely imprecise), with precession at <b>51″/yr</b>, the year-length against Copernicus, and the solar eccentricity; and (iii) the celebrated <b>prosthaphaeresis-priority story</b> — Rheticus → Werner → <b>Wittich</b> → <b>Bürgi</b> → an unnamed \"scurrilous sycophant\" (<b>Ursus</b>) → <b>Clavius</b>, plus the Bavarian-library book-requests and the <b>Leovitius/Fugger</b> manuscripts. Two declared interventions in the source block: (1) the OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> (footnote-anchors) on ~55 words are removed as <b>non-textual</b> (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, and to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588); (2) the source's <b>square brackets</b> around three late passages (the book-list, the Augsburg-youth/Fugger recollection, and the closing) are <b>retained as printed</b> — they are Dreyer's/the witness's editorial brackets (passages with witness-B divergence), flagged here, not mine. Greek (<i>μικροκόσμῳ</i>), the quadrature glyph <b>□</b> (= <i>quadraturis</i>), and the planet/zodiac glyphs (♀ ☿ ☾ ☉ ♂ ♄ ♃ ♋ ♌ ♎ ♐ ♊) are reproduced as transcribed. The dateline (<i>\"ex arce Caesareana Girsitza 16. die Novembris stylo renovato Anno 1599\"</i>) is explicitly <b>New Style</b> (<i>stylo renovato</i>); it agrees with the catalogue label. <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated against the local Dreyer <i>Opera Omnia</i> scan, <b>vol. VIII pp. 209–217</b> (running head \"TYCHO AD IOH. G. HERWARTUM 6/16 NOV. 1599\"; from cod. Vindob. lat. 10702). The <b>entire numerical spine is confirmed against the edition of record</b> (not merely internally consistent): obliquity 23°31½′ (p. 209); precession 51″/yr = 1°/70½ yr, with the al-Battānī-derivation 1006 yrs = 14°15′ and 705 yrs = 10° (p. 212); year 365ᵈ 5ʰ \"non plane 49ᵐ\" (p. 212); solar eccentricity 2½ → al-Battānī/Asrael 2;5 → Tycho 2;9 (p. 213); the full al-Battānī/Asrael/Prophatius/ancients/moderns authority-and-value series incl. the ancients' 23°51⅓′ (pp. 211–212); the Rheticus→Werner→Wittich→Bürgi→Ursus→Clavius priority chain (\"fere 20 annos,\" Wittich \"anno 80 per 4 menses,\" pp. 214–216). One OCR-watch: the <i>Vultur volans</i> declination the .md gives as <b>5⅚°</b> appears in the scan as the garbled \"P. 51\" (p. 210) — the ⅚ was lost in OCR; the .md's 5⅚° is the correct reading (consistent with the derived latitude 29°40′ that follows), not a variant.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOH. G. HERWARTUM.</p><p>NOBILIS et Magnifice vir, amice plurimum honorande, utrasque tuas, eodem tenore ad me datas, unâ cum Catalogo Bibliothecae Serenissimorum Principum Bavariae, recte quidem et satis mature accepi; erantque ea omnia mihi longe gratissima et singularem tuum atque sincerum et benevolum erga me affectum liquido ostendebant. Verum quod tardius respondeam quam debebam, mihi invito ob occupationes varias incidentes evenit. Acceperam tuas, cum in scribendis quam plurimis, tam in Daniam quam hinc inde in Germaniam literis, per filium natu maiorem dirigendis, quem ex Clementissima Caes. Mai. Domini nostri clementissimi voluntate in Patriam pro adferendis residuis instrumentis, ablegavi, eram occupatissimus; cumque is unâ secum haberet literas Caesareae Maiestatis ad Magistratum Magdeburgensem, quibus clementissime rogabantur, ut quaedam ex instrumentis meis Astronomicis, simulque Bibliothecam et meam supellectilem isthic relictam, huc per Albim in Bohemiam transvehi curarent, sperabam id intra tres vel quatuor septimanas confici posse, ideoque ubi libri mei advenissent, me promptius quosnam haberem vel desiderarem ex ijs, qui in Catalogo transmisso memorantur, perspecturum mecum statuebam, tum quoque nonnulla inter res nostras librarias et chartas inventurum, quibus tuis desiderijs pro tempore rectius satisfacerem, ideoque rescriptionem nonnihil data opera procrastinavi, existimans illud tritum locum sic inventurum, Differ, habent parvae commoda magna morae. Verum praeter omnem exspectationem accidit, ut Magdeburgenses subterfugia quaedam in literis Caesareanis invenientes, de rerum mearum huc transportatione aliquamdiu haesitarint, tandemque id officij prorsus detrectarint, ut necesse habuerit amicus quidam meus Magdeburgi, cui hoc negotium concredideram, mihi Magistratus illius loci tergiversationem indicare, misso huc proprio tabellario, quem mox in aulam Caesareanam cum literis Magnifico Domino BARVITIO haec significantibus amandavi. Is vero alias a Caesarea Maiestate Magdeburgensibus denuo scriptas hisce diebus mihi retulit, quibus significantius et magis serio ijs mandatur, ut suis sumptibus in gratiam Caesareae Matis hanc operam praestent, quae literae ijs nunc adportantur. At quoniam Albis admodum declivis, imo quibusdam in locis vadosus est, ob diuturnam quae antecessit siccitatem, ignoro, quam cito id fieri possit, ideo responsionem ad tuam epistolam non diutius protrahendam censeo, praesertim cum ante biduum a praedicto Magnif. Domino BARVITIO, communi nostro amico, omni honore dignissimo, per literas de rescriptione maturanda rogatus sim. Taceo nunc quod et alia inciderint non pauca, quae praeter voluntatem in respondendo me tardiorem reddiderunt. Praeterquam enim quod plurimis curis tam domesticis, quam in structuris pro novo Observatorio atque Laboratorio ex Caesareae Matis clementissimo permissu, adornandis et urgendis indies distinerer, accessit et hoc, quod contagia luis epidemicae arci Benachiae appropinquantia Gynaeceum meum (cum sexus ille alias natura sit timidiusculus) ita terruerint ut precibus eius auscultans, huc in aliam arcem Caesareanam Girsitzam prope Albim tribus milliaribus Praga distantem secedere cum tota familia consultius duxerim, quae transmigratio non parum otij mihi ad scribendum praeripuit. His itaque et alijs de caussis (nolo enim nunc singula referre) cum factum sit, ut nolens volens rescriptione hucusque supersedere necesse habuerim, spero me apud te pro eo, quo praeditus es, animi candore, excusationem et veniam reperturum, quod tuae expectationi non prius morem gesserim, ideoque haec purgationis loco, eo latius praefari volui.</p><p>Nunc ad litteras tuas, et quae simul missa sunt, venio, quibus haec pro temporis occasione respondentia incidunt. Quae ab initio attingis de Sacrae Caes. Matis clementissimo erga me favore et studiorum meorum benignissima promotione, ita futurum, animum tibi praesagijsse, veridicus id equidem genius tibi suggessit. Nam ipsius Matem non saltem Caesareum sed et paternum (modo sic loqui liceat) erga me gerere affectum, vel inde liquet, quod nuper bis per praenominatum Mag. Dominum BARVITIUM ad vicinum mihi Dominum Capitaneum Brandeisensem, de me clementissime perscribi mandavit, non saltem quoad ea, quae salarium mihi benignissime deputatum, ubi et quando obtinendum concernunt, sed et insuper significari, ipsius Caesaream Matem proprio motu de me clementissime sollicitam esse, ob pericula luis Epidemicae, quae Benachiae invaluerunt, ideoque clementissime concedere, ut alio me reciperem, aut etiam Viennam usque, si sic lubuerit, proficiscerer, ubi de habitatione commoda et necessarijs ipsius Maiestas mihi tempestive et clementissime prospicere vellet. Quae sane benignissimam et non vulgarem Caes. Matis erga me affectionem haud obscure declarant; quod etiam subiectissimo animo quam gratissime accipio, et dabo operam, adiuvante Numine coelesti, ut ea quae ipsius Mati vicissim clementissime accepta et honorifica esse poterint, pro virili quoad vixero humillime et obsequentissime praestem. Licet vero iter Viennense ob longinquitatem et negotia mea necdum Benachiae recte composita, modeste et humiliter excusarim (quod et ipsius Mati non displicuisse postea intellexi) tamen, uti dictum est, huc Girsitzam secedere non inconsultum duxi. Unde Benachiam subinde excurrere, atque structuras isthic pro me inchoatas urgere, quantum per me licet, non intermitto.</p><p>Quod de Patria mea mox subiungis, te non mirari, eam me neglexisse, cum ita fieri soleat, ego sane mea de causa nihil inde afficior, cum ibi esse Patriam, ubi bene et commode vivitur, censeam. Sed potius ipsius Patriae causa doleo, quae propterea apud cordatiores male audit, et audiet forsan diutius, quam nonnulli in ea huius mutationis machinatores praevidere aut credere potuerunt. Ut vero rectius perspicias, quam graves habuerim emigrationis huius causas, mitto fasciculum litterarum, eas per circumstantias exponentium, inter quas aliquae sunt, occasionem, cur in Caesareae Matis ditionem transire maluerim, ostendentes. Casum vero hunc iamdudum a me praevisum fuisse, liber noster Epistolarum (quem te ad manus habere, ex prioribus tuis intellexi) innuere poterit, pagina 198 iuxta calcem et in principio sequentis. Cumque praevisa tela minus feriant, ego hanc sortis meae vicissitudinem, utut hac invalescente aetate, et in tanta familiae copia gravem, patientius et fortius fero. Addidi simul carmina quaedam selecta ex quam plurimis in meam gratiam, postquam Dania excessi, a varijs hinc inde Poëtis concinnata; exspecto brevi plura, tum ab illustri Domino IOSEPHO SCALIGERO et Poëtarum nostri seculi antesignano, PAULO MELISSO, tum quoque alijs claris in Italia et Germania ac vicinis Regnis, vatibus; si qui in vestra Bavaria aut propinqua Augusta Vindelicorum sunt (uti esse non dubito) qui de Heliconis fontibus suaviter venas acuerunt, da operam ut numerum adaugeant. Fieri enim potest, ut aliquando ubi in mediocrem libellum excreverint, ea publicari sinam; ita tamen ut Patria et Rex virique in ea integri, quibus invitis talia mihi contigerunt, excusabile sint. Duo ultima Epigrammata, cum saepe dictus Magnif. Dominus BARVITIUS Caesareae Mati nomine meo humillime ostendisset, quaerendo an liceret ianuae utrique novi Observatorij et Laboratorij marmori incisa imponere, ipsius Matas clementissime annuit atque sic fieri iussit. Quando otium a gravioribus occupationibus tibi fuerit, poteris et haec et priora discessum meum e Dania attingentia, obiter perlustrare tibique reservare.</p><p>Sed his minus forte huc pertinentibus omissis, venio nunc ad ea, quae Mathematica et Astronomica sunt, in quibus nostra utrinque versatur voluptas. Quod in dubio illo de ♀ stella a ☿ non eclipsata rationibus meis acquieveris, bene est, addo prioribus et hanc. Non in omni coniunctione ☿ et ipsius stellam infra hanc esse, sed nonnunquam etiam supra, sic postulantibus non saltem meis novis hypothesibus, quae Terrae quietem stabiliunt, sed et Coperniceis mobilitatem eidem vel invitae obtrudentibus. Quae de motu Solis et Lunae in prioribus innui, quaeque a te solerter et circumspecte inibi expensa placuisse video, ita omnino se habere apparentiae demonstrant, maioremque inesse Lunae motui varietatem, quam hactenus perspectum est, convincunt. Recte sane de veterum observationibus censuram profers, eas crassa quadam Minerva acceptas esse, quam enim lata indagine ipsa fixarum loca, e quibus Planetarum observationes dependent, denotârint, inde patet quod non saltem ultra sextam gradus partem haud fuerint solliciti, sed ne hanc quidem ubique attigerint, immo nonnunquam vix semissem, prodentibus id etiamnum stellarum distantijs, quae invariatae permanent ipsorum numeris non accordantibus. Quid igitur, quaeso, in caeteris subtiliori indagini obnoxijs praestare potuerint? Quare non iniuria dubitas de obliquatione Signiferi ab HIPPARCHO et PTOLEMAeO iuxta ERATHOSTENIS placitum retenta, atque hinc occasionem satis ingeniose invenis, cur PTOLEMAeUS ☾ae latitudinem maximam tertia gradus parte in □turis minorem, quam a nobis deprehensa est, statuerit. Atque haec iamdudum penitius introspicienti mihi, dum latitudines fixarum, pro ratione variationis obliquitatis Eclipticae alteratas esse deprehendo, quemadmodum Tomo I Progymnasmatum Astronom. e nostris e. 2 luculenter demonstro, attendi quod applicata inclinatione Eclipticae maxima, quam ipsemet aliquoties accuratissime adinveni P. 23 M. 31½ ad eas stellarum declinationes, quas TIMOCHARES, HIPPARCHUS et PTOLEMAeUS recensuerunt, prodire eandem quam proxime latitudinem, quam etiam nunc habere deprehenduntur, alias non. Ex his itaque et similibus suspicari non immerito licet, non tam revera mutatam esse obliquitatem Signiferi, quam a veteribus illis, intra tertiam gradus partem non satis praecise determinatam.</p><p>Ut vero inter plura exempla, dicto libro allegata, unum vel alterum huc advocem, primum id probant utraeque stellae illustres in capitibus ♊, Castor et Pollux dictae, quae paulo ante Tropicum aestivum veterum illorum temporibus versabantur, nunc autem eundem dimidio quasi signo praetergressae sunt, ut ob id promptior ex his detur investigationis ratio; adhibita enim declinatione, quam ijs PTOLEMAeUS e TIMOCHARIS, HIPPARCHI et sua animadversione attribuit, et longitudine earundem ad illorum veterum tempora simul rite disposita, deprehenduntur ambas per Triangulorum demonstrata latitudinem obtinuisse 18 vel 19 M. minorem ea quam nunc coelum ipsum ostendit, quod vel mutatae obliquitati Eclipticae ascribendum, aut eandem tunc fuisse, quae nunc est, statuendum, nulla erit in latitudine sensibilis differentia, unde antiquos illos per tertiam gradus partem angulum obliquationis Eclipticae ad Aequatorem nimium auxisse convincetur. Idipsum quoque secundo minori negotio patet ex lucida Vulturis volantis stella, quam TIMOCHARES, HIPPARCHUS et PTOLEMAeUS unanimiter prodiderunt declinare ab Aequatore versus Boream P. 5⅚, quod illa stella eorum temporibus quam proxime esset quoad longitudinem Tropico hiberno, ideoque declinationem insensibiliter variare. Addita itaque declinatione eorum maxima provenit stellae latitudo P. 29 M. 40, nec enim moror quod PTOLEMAeI erroneus abacus circa hanc contra propria assumpta, uti et saepe alias, eum etiam frustra imitantibus neotericis, admisit, quae omnino excedit eam quam illa ipsa stella nunc coelitus exhibet, tertia fere gradus parte, quantum videlicet dubia apud veteres obliquatio requirit, nostra vero si applicetur, eadem manebit latitudo tam olim quam nunc. Accedit et hoc (si Planetas in consilium adhibere lubuerit) quod ♂tis stellae latitudo maxima versus Boream huic inductioni adstipuletur, cuius limitem Boreum penes Apogaeum in 25½ G. ♋ PTOLEMAeUS constituit, et latitudinem hinc fecit P. 4⅓. At nos Apogaeum eius nunc versari in P. 23½ ♌ (5 G. COPERNICI opinione anterius) exacte adinvenimus et limitem Boreum praecedere 6½ G., latitudinem vero Boream, quando ibi Soli opponitur, esse P. 4 M. 35, quae quarta gradus parte PTOLEMAeI tradita exuperat, quantum etiam minor Eclipticae inclinatio quamproxime ijs in locis requirit. Licet ut dicam, quod res est, in caeteris duobus superioribus Planetis idipsum non pariter quadret, quorum limitem Boreae latitudinis maximae PTOLEMAeUS circa ♎ initia esse voluit, ubi error inclinationis Eclipticae insensibilis fere evadit; cumque is latitudinem maximam Boream statuerit in ♄ P. 3 M. 6, nos non maiorem deprehendere potuimus P. 2 M. 49½, quae ab illo deficit paulo plus 4ta gradus parte, et pariter in ♃ latitudo maxima Borea ab illo insinuata est P. 2 M. 5, quam nos saltem partis 1 Min. 39½ assecuti sumus, ita ut 25 Min. omnino deficiant, quae discrimina in utroque hoc Planeta per incertam Eclipticae ad Aequatorem inclinationem excusari nequeunt, siquidem non longe a concursu utriusque circuli eveniant. Adde et hoc, limitem Boreae latitudinis ♄, quem PTOLEMAeUS 50 G. Apogaeo eius anteriorem credidit, nunc ab Apogaeo, quod haeret in 26 G. ♐ Coperniceo 3½ anterius distare in antecedentia P. 65½, sic quoque in ♃, ubi is Boreum latitudinis terminum 20 G. Apogaeo ulteriorem tradidit, nunc eodem 3 solummodo Gradibus posterior a me adinventus. Occupat enim hoc tempore Apogaeum ♃ 3 G. ♎, et COPERNICI mente quaternis ferme anterius est; limes autem Boreus in 6 ♎ G. expleto consistit. Quomodo haec, de quibus etiam nonnulla in antecedentibus meis generali indicatione attigi, cum PTOLEMAeI placitis concilianda sint, non satis liquet, sive erroneae fuerint ipsius observationes (nam de nostris subtilissime trutinatis nihil ambigo) sive quod revera talis latitudinibus et limitibus earundem mutatio subrepserit. Unde etiam patet, nodos horum Planetarum et terminos digressionis ab Ecliptica maximae Apogaeorum motui non obtemperare, uti hactenus creditum est, quodque nimis inconsiderate magnus alias superioris aevi Astronomus COPERNICUS, PTOLEMAeI traditionibus, citra propriam experientiam, in his, uti etiam plerisque alijs, acquieverit, ideoque non mirum, latitudines Planetarum longe aliter ab ipso coelo exhiberi, quam ipsius fert numeratio.</p><p>Verum non opus est hoc de Eclipticae obliquatione dubium tam longinque petere atque in libellam expendere, siquidem Artificum observationes, qui post PTOLEMAeUM vixerunt, in eadem determinanda nostrae inventioni citra admodum sensibile discrimen adstipulentur. ALBATEGNIJ certe PTOLEMAeO et nobis ferme intermedij tradita indicant, ante 700 annos obliquitatem hanc maximam vix 4½ scrupulis, et ASRAELIS Hispani, 200 fere annis post, saltem 2½ nostra animadversione maiorem fuisse, quod non nisi maximis instrumentis perceptibile est, qualibus illi vix usi sunt, et per parallaxin Solis ab ijs neglectam quodammodo in uno vel sesquialtero minuto excusari potest, PROPHATIO Iudaeo adhuc paulo post, alijs 200 annis ab ASRAELE eandem nobiscum intra dimidium minutum ante 300 retro annos constituente, siquidem is forte maiori et exactiori usus sit organo et parallaxin Solis praecaverit. Ex quibus omnibus satis probabiliter colligitur, vix aliquam subesse sensibilem obliquationi huic mutationem. Mirum autem videri posset ab ARISTARCHO, qui saltem 40 circiter annis ab obitu ALEXANDRI Magni floruit, usque ad PTOLEMAeUM [qui] 460 ab eodem abfuit, per intermedios ERATOSTHENEM et HIPPARCHUM, plane eandem permansisse Eclipticae summam obliquationem, quam illi intervallo maiore 400 unanimiter annis prodiderunt P. 23 M. 51⅓, nunc vero inde ab ALBATEGNIO ad nos per 700 annos intra 3 Min. (tantillulum enim ALBATEGNIJ a nostra inventione solummodo differt, si parallaxin adhibuisset) eandem permanere, veterum illorum annotatis quasi tertia gradus parte minorem, quod suspicionem movet PTOLEMAeUM et HIPPARCHUM, prout tu etiam vis, antecedentium autoritate nimium confisos fuisse, ideoque similia pro libitu admisisse. Quod vero recentiores utpote PURBACHIUS, REGIOMONTANUS, WERNERUS et ipse quoque COPERNICUS eandem 4 proxime scrupulis nostra minorem reddiderint, ob refractionem Solis in decliviori situ brumali, quae ad 8 vel 9 M. excrescere potest et tantundem Solis altitudinem meridianam iusto maiorem reddere (quod pro dimidia parte declinationem maximam ex utroque Tropico petitam nimis coarctat) ab ipsis non praevisam, hic error commissus est; quin et observatae distantiae ☉is a vertice Noribergae, tam a REGIOMONTANO quam eius discipulo WALTHERO ante 100 circiter annos factae, si tam parallaxis quam refractio Solis ijs rite applicentur, convincunt, eandem intra ½ M. fuisse tunc declinationem Solis maximam respectu Aequatoris, cum ea quam nos observavimus.</p><p>Sic quoque promotionem fixarum stellarum non adeo irregularem esse existimo, prout COPERNICUS ex veterum minus ratis observationibus, cum suis et illis etiam non satis accuratis collatione facta, per Aequinoctiorum indicatam anticipationem inducere et excusare allaboravit. Si enim observata in stellis HIPPARCHI (neglectis PTOLEMAeI dubijs annotatis) per intermediantem ALBATEGNIUM, qui in his accuratior erat, ad nostra inventa deduxerimus, eas in annis ab HIPPARCHO ad ALBATEGNIUM 1006 P. 14 M. 15 confecisse, ab hoc vero ad nos interiectis annis 705 decem praecise gradus absolvisse constabit. Unde eas in toto hoc interstitio, utrobique etiam subsumpto, confecisse in 70½ annis 1 G. et singulis 51'' aequali et ordinario progressu consequens erit. Quare suspicio non iniuria moveri poterit, PTOLEMAeI tradita aestimative solummodo assumpta esse, ut centenis annis imitatione quorundam antecessorum, quos temporis paucitas et observationum incertitudo fefellit, 1 G. motui fixarum ascriberet. Pari insuper ratione anni inaequalitatem non tam enormem et variam esse, qualis ab eodem COPERNICO nobis obtrusa, censeo, refragantibus vel hoc ipso seculo, ut de antiquis illis nunc nihil dicam REGIOMONTANI et WALTHERI observationibus, cum nostris in trutinam vocatis, quae anni quantitatem ultra dies suos et horas 5 non plane 49 Min. attingere probant, cum tamen iuxta COPERNICUM 6 M. et ultra maior foret, quod per continuam aggregationem efficit, illum in Aequinoctiorum temporibus intolerabiliter hallucinari, adeo ut nunc vernale 14½ horis, id est ultra dimidium diem iusto plus prolonget, qui error adeo foecundus est, ut post 100 annos ipsum integrum diem et tres insuper horas in Aequinoctio verno constituendo nimium habere necessarium evadat. Et si ab HIPPARCHO per ALBATEGNIUM usque ad REGIOMONTANUM et nos derivetur anni quantitas sibi fere similis, nec adeo enormis, uti putavit COPERNICUS, cognoscetur, solo PTOLEMAeO negotium (undecunque tandem in hunc errorem prolapsus) omne interturbante, nec ipso COPERNICO Solis motiones ipsi coelo consonas rite applicant, cuius derivationis occasiones praedicto Progymn. libro c. 1 aperuimus. Hinc insuper adducor, ut de eccentricitate Solis a PTOLEMAeO hucusque tantopere mutata ambigam, siquidem ea quam is unâ cum HIPPARCHO retinuit P. 2½ qualium semidiameter eccentrici est 60, ea postea ab ALBATEGNIO et ASRAELE pariter inventa sit P. solummodo earundem 2 et M. 5, quae a nostra exactissima designatione P. 2 M. 9 non magnopere deficit, et res sane est subtilissimae inquisitionis, ut non mirum sit, in tribus vel quatuor scrupulis hîc discordare vel Lynceo oculatiores Astronomos, quae omnia tuae gravissimae et politissimae censurae expendenda discernendaque subijcio. Ex quibus etiam singulis liquidissime constat, te non frustra de veterum diligentia in talibus administrandis dubitare et nostrae quoque hac in parte haesitationi non inconvenienter subscribere. Animadverti et ego illos harmoniam quandam e numeris et proportionibus Pythagoreis (uti existimo) quaesivisse, atque eidem nimis secure acquievisse; quicunque apparentijs exacte consona sic tradiderit, erit mihi magnus APOLLO, id autem posterius investigandum. Sed hoc opus hic labor est, neque enim dubium, Deum universi autorem omnia etiam in coelestibus harmonice condidisse, quemadmodum in homine μικροκόσμῳ et tota inferiori natura intelligente apparet.</p><p>Tabulas nostras de motu luminarium seorsim quidem, quam primum fieri licuerit, imprimi curabo, ac caeteris Planetis, in quibus adhuc nonnulla elaborabo, praemittam, sed non libenter nudos numeros absque observationum et demonstrationum robore emitto. Volo enim mea rata et demonstrata esse, ne quis merito relinquatur dubitationi aut oblocutioni locus, ero tamen in his ab initio parcior et saltem maxime necessaria promam. Caetera in magnum illud Astronomiae, quod molior Theatrum, si per Dei benignitatem et Caesaris subsidia id absolvere licuerit, reservaturus, ubi singula tam quoad observationes quam Geometrica et Arithmetica ad hanc divinam artem facientia luculenter pertexere animus est. Interim tibi ea libentissime, quae Eclipsium calculum respiciunt, quatenus hucusque a me elaborata sunt, communicarem, verum pauca ex ijs hîc mecum habeo. Sunt praecipua apud supellectilem meam librariam Magdeburgi relictam, si quae forte a meis studiosis huc asportata sunt, pro quotidiano eorum usu de ijs mox Benachiae, ubi ea quae Witebergâ mecum assumsi, adhuc custodiuntur, inquiri faciam, et quae inventa fuerint hisce addam, quamvis non omnia ita expolita atque ad extremam limam adhuc deducta sint, prout requiritur, et alias inchoata ac manuscripta habeo, licet nunc ad manus non sint. Fefellit me Typographus quidam Hamburgensis, interea dum in Bohemiam tendens isthac Dresdam ante annum eo, et quaterniones de motu ☾ae in Progymnasmatis meis desideratos corrupit maleque impressit, dumque postea per hybernum otium hunc laborem Witebergae renovari tentarem, apud eius loci Typographos fidelitatem et diligentiam requisitam neque reperi, moxque a Caesarea Mate huc clementissime vocatus, isthic inchoata abrumpere coactus sum, accedentibus et quibusdam alijs, quae mihi remoras iniecerunt, impedimentis. Quamprimum autem omnia plenius absoluta fuerint, quod ut primo quoque tempore fiat enitar, tecum non invite sive impressa sive manuscripta communicabo, non dubitans quin ea, quae privata fuerint et necdum publici iuris facta, tibi et soli concredita, sis reservaturus. Neque sane opus fuisset, tanta hac in parte protestatione et obligatione, cum tibi alias, licet de facie ignoto, fama tamen etiam hisce inter nos ventilatis disputationibus, satis perspecto, fidam, atque id quod tua meretur virtus, tribuere non averser. In Epochis, quae requiris, si caetera invenero, adiungam, ut ad Ptolemaica tempora vel etiam antrorsum per aliquot secula nostrae restitutiones utrinque applicari possint, donec pleniora et exactiora a me nactus fueris, quatenus veterum lato modo sumptae animadversiones, quod et ipse haud inconsiderate fateris, id patiuntur. His interea utere.</p><p>Adhortaris ut mea opera Astronomica maturem, quod et per Europam, quam late Christianismus patet, multi excellentes viri adhortati sunt, et etiamnum adhortantur, quin et ego admodum invitus hanc moram fero. Absum iam a Patria, ubi studia mea conturbabantur, fere per triennium, nec ante requiem optatam pro ijs invenire licuit, quam nunc concedente eam Clementissimo Caesare nostro, cumque ego solus tanto oneri observationum et supputationum non sufficiam, habeo nunc saltem mecum 3 vel 4 studiosos, quorum opera hac in re utor, cum antea in Dania, dum regnum Uraniae incolume atque arx inclyta starent, 10 vel 12 hac de causa sustentare solitus fuerim. Spero autem, ubi Caesar Dominus noster clementissimus rei molem penitius introspexerit, quod adhuc benignius auxiliatrices manus clementissime porrecturus sit, nec veteri illi ALPHONSO, utut tantae impensae non requirantur, cessurus, et nihilominus solidiora (absit invidia dicto) promoturus.</p><p>Lubenter intellexi te in compendijs Triangulorum per solas prostaphaereses in numeros resolvendorum, gnaviter versatum esse. Hanc ingeniosam et utilissimam inquisitionem ante annos fere 20 enucleandam suscepi e RHETICI Dialogo, quem integrae Tabulae Triangulorum ad sextantes graduum solummodo a se supputatae subiunxit. Occasionem nactus sum, dum compendia calculi Geometrici et Astronomici quaerenda esse monet, ut citra tabulas Astronomicas aequationum sive prostaphaeresium e solis medijs motibus vera Planetarum loca promptius eruantur, quod cum per taediosas multiplicationes et divisiones fieri nequaquam posset, hac alia postea a nobis fructuose reperta ingrediendum erat via, cui etiam WERNERUS in libello de motu octavae sphaerae aliqualem praebuit ansam; postea superveniente meque in Dania Uraniburgi anno 80 per 4 menses invisente PAULO WITICHIO bonae memoriae, excellenti Mathematico Vratislaviensi, qui talibus etiam (a me Witebergae dum istic olim transieram admonitus) interea insudaverat, cum illo communicata opera, ea ipsa luculentiora reddidi et semper exinde in usu habui, quo studiosis meis, observationes nostras in numeros redigentibus, temporis et laboris dispendium minueretur. WITICHIUS autem a me discedens et postmodum illustrissimum, laudatissimae memoriae Hassiae Landgravium WILHELMUM accedens, tam instrumentorum meorum confectionem isthic exposuit, quam Automatopaeo Principis IUSTO BYRGIO, mechanico eximio, haec Triangulorum mysteria propalavit, a quo quidam scurrilis Sycophanta (cuius si quod est ingenium, turpissime habitat, quem ut hîc nominetur indignum censeo, qualis conviciator et calumniator sit, alias testatum relicturus) illa habuit, sive consensu, sive dolo, non dixerim, et postea pro suis Argentinae, cum alijs quibusdam hinc inde corrasis, manca tamen et mutila publicavit. Unde occasionem nactus, sed isti immerito tribuens excellentissimus ille Mathematicus CHRISTOPHORUS CLAVIUS ea eruditissimo suo de Astrolabio volumini inseruit, atque suo quodam modo adauxit, circa Sphaerica tamen et Rectangula, quantum video, solummodo occupatus, quae nostrae intentioni nequaquam sufficiunt. Laboravit optimae recordationis Dominus IACOBUS CURTIUS, praecedens Imperij Procancellarius, vir omni laude maior, et horum studiorum apprime amans et intelligens (quem utinam fata nobis diutius reservassent) laboravit, inquam, ut haec non saltem ad obliquangula Sphaerica sed et Triangulis quibusdam planis adaptaret. Verum in hoc posteriore, uti audio, id quod voluit, non omnino assecutus est, sive laboris taedio praepeditus, avocantibus eum gravissimis officij tam ampli in tanta Rep. occupationibus, sive etiam morte immatura praeventus. Cum Wittebergae superiori hyeme aliquandiu haererem et Typographicos labores non ex animi sententia succedere animadverterem, resumpsi hanc operam in manus, et adhibito isthic praestanti Mathematum Professore MELCHIORE IOeSTELIO, ea omnia, quae tam Sphaerica, quam plana Triangula respiciunt, quaecunque in usum cadunt Astronomicum et Geographicum, per solas prostaphaereses dinumeranda compendiose in ordinem redigi effeci, ita ut nihil fere ulterius in his nunc desiderari queat, quae etiam suo tempore in communem horum studiorum utilitatem et promotionem publici iuris fieri procurabo, quin et antea, si sic expetieris, te eorundem non illibenter participem reddam, cum omne bonum sit communicabile, praesertim dignis te in tuis, quae in promptu habes, similibus inventis, par pari relaturum (vident enim saepe plus oculi quam oculus) confido; si praeveneris, eo magis eoque citius me ad id praestandum instigaveris.</p><p>[Libros, quos serenissimis et illustrissimis Principibus vestris et etiam tibi destinavi, necdum mittere conceditur, exemplaria enim Mechanicae nostrae Astronomiae, quae pictori Pragensi illuminanda, uti vocant, commisi, nec adhuc recepi, non enim de ijs periculoso isto tempore sollicite quaerere lubuit; caeteri in hac rerum mearum irrequietudine, et necdum satis composito statu, non tam cito ac vellem conscribi potuerunt, sed fiet id nihilominus, Deo volente, per primam quamque oportunitatem, siquidem omne promissum cadat in debitum. Quod tam benevole Catalogum librorum Mathematicorum, qui in Principum isthic Bibliotheca instructissima inveniuntur, mecum communicaveris, teque de ijs, quos expediero, certiorem reddi cupieris, quas possum gratias ago maximas. Res enim haec mihi acceptissima est, cum talibus impense oblecter. Et licet plurimi ex ijs arriserint, selegi tamen solummodo inde 50, quos in adiuncta scheda denotatos vides, inter eos etiam sunt Haebraici libri postea mihi a te insinuati. Si hos omnes per primam quamque occasionem ad me miseris, rem apprime gratam feceris, restituam cunctos bona fide ad praefinitum a me tempus, et si sex illos, quos unâ consignavi e Catalogo Nundinarum Francofurdensium excerptos, simul apposueris, actutum pretium indicatum solvam atque remittam.]</p><p>[Incidit mihi et quippiam aliud, postquam vidi tecum libere et liberaliter de hac re literaria mecum agere. Memini cum in iuventute Augustae Vindelicorum vobis vicina urbe per biennium ferme versarer atque in horto Domini PAULI HENTZELIJ Consulis bonae recordationis observationibus coelestibus per Quadrantem maximum a centro ad circumferentiam 21 pedes habentem ibi a nobis exstructum subinde invigilarem, inter alia] intellexisse, illustres et generosos Dominos FUGGEROS in amplissima sua Bibliotheca excellentes quosdam libros, tam in alio quam hoc genere artium reservare, quorum tamen inspiciendorum copia, tunc, nescio ob quae impedimenta, data non est, atque inter alios percepi, laboriosissimi illius et eximij viri CYPRIANI LEOVITIJ Bohemi, laudatae recordationis, nonnulla manuscripta opera a vidua post obitum eius conquisita ibidemque detineri, qualia sunt Ephemerides, per centenos aliquot annos e Calculo ALPHONSINO ad denos saltem dies ab ipso continuatae et aliae e COPERNICEIS ab anno 1500 incipientes (quousque durent, iam non succurrit) tum quoque eiusdem CYPRIANI Tabulae directionum resolutae, in quibus ascensiones obliquae ad octavum latitudinis gradum utrinque ab Ecliptica dilatatae sunt.</p><p>[Habui etiam prae manibus magnum opus de Genethliaca Astrologia, quod an absolverit et ad FUGGEROS pervenerit, ignoro. Si haec et similia praestantissimi huius viri manuscripta et nondum evulgata opera mihi, ut ea pauculis diebus per otium introspiciam (neque enim in re Astronomica instauranda, et ijs quae ego molior, quidpiam usui esse poterint, cum sint, uti scis, vulgaria) modo non propria saltem Serenissimorum Principum vestrorum, quibus ea Respublica FUGGERIque ipsi plurimum merito tribuunt, auctoritate et clementissima intercessione procuraveris, dissimulato tamen meo, si sic consultum duxeris, nomine, officium inprimis acceptum feceris, restituam quoscunque nactus fuero inviolatos, ad praestitutum diem, et si simul indicem omnium librorum, qui in splendida ea Bibliotheca habentur, praesertim Mathematicorum et Philosophorum, nancisci potuero, erit id mihi eo acceptius.</p><p>Plura nunc non adiungo, ne te nimium uno tempore detineam atque fatigem. Verum ut haec, quae nunc in buccam venerunt, boni consulas, officiose et peramanter rogo, et si me, uti spem facis, aliquando invisere non degravatus fueris, advenies non saltem hospes longe exoptatissimus, sed amicus intimus quovis officij genere pro meo modulo excipiendus. Dum modo per Caesarem licuerit, et commoditas aliquando sese obtulerit, ut eas oras ingrediar, te etiam Monachij coram alloqui non supersedebo, quin et alias quaecunque a me proficisci poterint officia, quam lubentissime offero, teque cum tota domo quam optime et diutissime valere ex animo precor.]</p><p>Dabantur properanti calamo ex arce Caesareana Girsitza 16. die Novembris stylo renovato Anno 1599.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Johann Georg Herwart.</b></p><p>Noble and Magnificent man, much-to-be-honoured friend: both of your letters, given to me with the same tenor, together with the <b>Catalogue of the Library of the most Serene Princes of Bavaria</b>, I received rightly and early enough; and all those [things] were far most welcome to me, and plainly showed your singular, sincere, and benevolent affection toward me. But that I reply later than I ought has befallen me against my will, on account of various occupations arising. I had received yours while I was most busy in writing very many letters, both to Denmark and here and there into Germany, to be directed through my <b>eldest son</b> — whom, by the most clement will of His Imperial Majesty our most clement Lord, I sent off into my homeland to fetch the <b>remaining instruments</b>; and since he had with him letters of the Imperial Majesty to the <b>Magistrate of Magdeburg</b>, by which they were most clemently asked to take care that some of my Astronomical instruments, and at the same time my Library and household goods left there, be conveyed hither into Bohemia by the <b>Elbe</b> — I hoped this could be accomplished within three or four weeks, and therefore resolved that, when my books had arrived, I should more readily perceive which of those mentioned in the Catalogue sent [to me] I had or wanted, and should also find some [things] among our library-goods and papers by which I might better satisfy your desires for the time; and so I somewhat deliberately put off the reply, thinking I should thus find that well-worn line true: <i>\"Defer; small delays have great advantages.\"</i> But, beyond all expectation, it happened that the Magdeburgers, finding certain loopholes in the Imperial letters, hesitated for a while about the transport of my things hither, and at last quite declined that duty — so that a certain friend of mine at Magdeburg, to whom I had entrusted this business, had to indicate to me the magistrate's evasion, a courier of his own being sent hither, whom I soon dispatched to the Imperial court with letters signifying these things to the Magnificent Lord <b>Barvitius</b>. He, however, has these days reported to me other [letters] written anew by the Imperial Majesty to the Magdeburgers, in which it is more significantly and seriously enjoined them to perform this service at their own expense, for the favour of the Imperial Majesty — which letters are now being brought to them. But since the Elbe is very low, nay in some places shoaly, on account of the long preceding drought, I do not know how soon it can be done; and therefore I judge the response to your letter is not to be protracted longer — especially since two days ago I was asked by the aforesaid Magnificent Lord <b>Barvitius</b>, our common friend, most worthy of all honour, by letter, to hasten my reply. I say nothing now of the many other [things] that have fallen out, which have rendered me, against my will, slower in replying. For besides being daily distracted by very many cares, both domestic and in the structures to be furnished and pressed forward for the <b>new Observatory and Laboratory</b> by the Imperial Majesty's most clement permission, there was added this: that the contagions of the <b>epidemic plague</b>, approaching the castle of <b>Benátky</b>, so terrified my <b>women's quarters</b> [<i>Gynaeceum</i>] (since that sex is otherwise by nature rather timid) that, hearkening to their prayers, I judged it more advisable to withdraw hither, with my whole household, to another Imperial castle, <b>Girsitz</b>, near the Elbe, three miles distant from Prague — which migration snatched no little leisure for writing from me. By these, then, and other causes (for I do not wish now to relate each), since it has come about that, willy-nilly, I have had to forbear the reply until now, I hope to find with you — in keeping with that candour of mind with which you are endowed — excuse and pardon, that I have not sooner complied with your expectation; and therefore I have wished to preface these [things] more amply in place of an apology.</p><p>Now I come to your letter, and what was sent at the same time, to which these [things] occur as fitting by the occasion of the time. As to what you touch at the beginning, about the <b>most clement favour of the Sacred Imperial Majesty</b> toward me and the most kind promotion of my studies — that your mind foreboded it would be so — a truthful <b>genius</b> indeed suggested that to you. For that His Majesty bears toward me an affection not only Imperial but also (if one may so speak) <b>paternal</b>, is clear even from this: that lately, twice, through the aforenamed Magnificent Lord Barvitius, he commanded that it be written most clemently to my neighbour, the Lord <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>, about me — not only as to those things which concern the <b>salary</b> most kindly assigned me (where and when it is to be obtained), but also that it be signified that His Imperial Majesty, of his own motion, is most clemently solicitous about me, on account of the dangers of the <b>epidemic plague</b> which have grown strong at Benátky, and therefore most clemently grants that I might withdraw elsewhere, or even go as far as <b>Vienna</b>, if it should so please, where His Majesty would provide for me, in good time and most clemently, a convenient dwelling and necessaries. Which truly declare, not obscurely, the Imperial Majesty's most kind and uncommon affection toward me — which too I receive most gratefully with a most submissive mind, and I shall take care, the heavenly Deity helping, to perform to His Majesty in turn, as far as in me lies, while I live, most humbly and obediently, those things which can be most clemently acceptable and honourable to him. But although I modestly and humbly excused the journey to Vienna, on account of [its] distance and my affairs not yet rightly composed at Benátky (which afterward I understood did not displease His Majesty), yet, as has been said, I judged it not unadvised to withdraw hither to Girsitz; whence I do not cease to run over to Benátky from time to time, and to press forward the structures begun there for me, as far as in me lies.</p><p>As to what you presently subjoin about my <b>homeland</b> — that you do not wonder that I have neglected it, since it is wont to be so — I indeed, for my own part, am affected by nothing thence, since I judge that one's <b>homeland is there where one lives well and conveniently</b>. But I rather grieve for the homeland's own sake, which on this account is ill-spoken-of among the more sensible, and will be perhaps longer than some of the contrivers of this change in it could foresee or believe. But that you may more rightly perceive how grave were the causes I had for this emigration, I send a <b>packet of letters</b> expounding them through the circumstances — among which are some showing the occasion why I preferred to pass into the Imperial Majesty's dominion. But that this case was long ago foreseen by me, our <b>Book of Letters</b> (which I understood from your earlier [letter] that you have at hand) can hint, on <b>page 198 near the foot and at the beginning of the following [page]</b>. And since foreseen darts strike less, I bear this vicissitude of my lot — grievous though it be in this failing age, and amid so great a multitude of family — more patiently and bravely. I have added at the same time certain <b>poems</b>, selected from the very many composed for my sake, after I left Denmark, by various Poets here and there; I expect more shortly, both from the illustrious Lord <b>Joseph Scaliger</b> and from the standard-bearer of the Poets of our age, <b>Paul Melissus</b>, and also from other famous bards in Italy and Germany and the neighbouring Kingdoms; if there are any in your <b>Bavaria</b> or in neighbouring <b>Augsburg</b> (as I do not doubt there are) who have sweetly sharpened their veins from the founts of <b>Helicon</b>, take care that they increase the number. For it may be that some time, when they have grown into a moderate booklet, I shall let them be published — yet in such a way that the homeland and the King and the upright men in it, against whose will such things befell me, be excusable. The two last <b>Epigrams</b>, when the oft-said Magnificent Lord <b>Barvitius</b> had most humbly shown them to the Imperial Majesty in my name, asking whether it might be allowed to set them, carved in marble, on each door of the new Observatory and Laboratory — His Majesty most clemently assented and ordered it so done. When you have leisure from graver occupations, you can glance over both these and the earlier [pieces] touching my departure from Denmark, and reserve [them] for yourself.</p><p>But, omitting these [things], perhaps less pertinent here, I come now to those which are <b>Mathematical and Astronomical</b>, in which our pleasure on both sides is engaged. That you have acquiesced in my reasons in that doubt about the star of <b>Venus [♀] not eclipsed by Mercury [☿]</b>, it is well; I add this too to the earlier [reasons]: that not in every conjunction of Mercury is its star below this [Venus], but sometimes also above — so demanding not only my new <b>hypotheses</b>, which establish the rest of the Earth, but also the Copernican [ones], which obtrude mobility on it even unwilling. What I hinted in [my] earlier [letter] about the motion of the <b>Sun and Moon</b>, and which I see pleased [you], weighed there by you skilfully and circumspectly, the appearances demonstrate to be entirely so, and prove that there is a greater <b>variety in the Moon's motion</b> than has hitherto been perceived. You rightly bring forward a censure of the ancients' observations, that they were received with a certain <b>rough Minerva</b> [crudely]; for by how broad an inquiry they noted the very places of the <b>fixed [stars]</b>, on which the observations of the Planets depend, is clear from this — that they were solicitous not [even] beyond a <b>sixth part of a degree</b> [10′], but did not everywhere attain even this, nay sometimes scarcely a <b>half [degree]</b> — the <b>distances of the stars</b> (which remain unvaried) still betraying this, not according with their numbers. What, I ask, then, could they accomplish in the rest, subject to subtler inquiry? Wherefore not unjustly do you doubt about the <b>obliquity of the Zodiac</b> retained by <b>Hipparchus and Ptolemy</b> according to <b>Eratosthenes's</b> tenet; and hence you find, ingeniously enough, the occasion why <b>Ptolemy</b> established the <b>Moon's [☾] maximum latitude</b> a third of a degree less, in the <b>quadratures [□]</b>, than has been ascertained by us. And these [matters], to me long since looking more deeply into [them] — while I find the <b>latitudes of the fixed [stars] altered in proportion to the variation of the obliquity of the Ecliptic</b>, as I demonstrate amply in Tome I of the Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i>, chapter 2, from our [observations] — I attended to [this]: that, the <b>maximum inclination of the Ecliptic</b> applied (which I myself several times most accurately found = <b>23° 31½′</b>) to those declinations of the stars which <b>Timochares, Hipparchus, and Ptolemy</b> recorded, there comes out very nearly the same <b>latitude</b> as they are now found to have — otherwise not. From these, then, and similar [cases], it is licit not undeservedly to suspect that the obliquity of the Zodiac was not so much really changed as imprecisely determined by those ancients, within a third part of a degree.</p><p>But, that I may, among the several examples adduced in the said book, call one or another hither: first this is proved by both the famous stars in the <b>heads of Gemini [♊]</b>, called <b>Castor and Pollux</b>, which in the times of those ancients dwelt a little before the <b>summer Tropic</b>, but now have passed it by, as it were, by half a sign — so that hence a readier method of investigation is given: for, the <b>declination</b> which Ptolemy attributed to them from the observation of Timochares, Hipparchus, and his own being applied, and their <b>longitude</b> at the same time rightly disposed to the times of those ancients, both are found, by the demonstrations of <b>Triangles</b>, to have had a <b>latitude 18 or 19′ less</b> than that which the heaven itself now shows; which [discrepancy] is either to be ascribed to the changed obliquity of the Ecliptic, or — if it be established that it was then the same as it is now — there will be no sensible difference in latitude, whence it will be proven that those ancients increased the angle of the Ecliptic's obliquity to the Equator too much, by a third of a degree. The same likewise appears, by a second lesser labour, from the bright star of <b>Vultur volans</b> [Aquila / Altair], which Timochares, Hipparchus, and Ptolemy unanimously reported to decline from the Equator toward the North <b>5⅚°</b>, because that star, in their times, was very nearly at the <b>winter Tropic</b> as to longitude, and therefore varied its declination insensibly. Their maximum declination [obliquity] being added, therefore, there comes out the star's latitude <b>29° 40′</b> (for I do not heed that Ptolemy's erroneous table admitted, about this [star], against his own assumptions — as also often elsewhere, the moderns vainly imitating him too — [a value] which entirely exceeds that which that very star now exhibits from the heaven, by nearly a third of a degree, namely as much as the doubtful obliquity among the ancients requires; but if ours be applied, the same latitude will remain both of old and now). There is added this too (if it please to take the <b>Planets</b> into counsel): that the <b>maximum latitude of the star of Mars [♂] toward the North</b> supports this induction — whose <b>northern limit</b>, by the <b>Apogee at 25½° of Cancer [♋]</b>, Ptolemy established, and made the latitude thence <b>4⅓°</b>. But we have exactly found its Apogee now to dwell at <b>23½° of Leo [♌]</b> (5° earlier than in Copernicus's opinion), and the northern limit to precede [it] by 6½°, and the northern latitude, when it is there opposed to the Sun, to be <b>4° 35′</b> — which exceeds Ptolemy's handed-down [value] by a quarter-degree, as much as the smaller inclination of the Ecliptic very nearly requires in those places. Granted that, to say the truth, the same does not equally square in the other two <b>superior Planets</b>, whose northern limit of maximum latitude Ptolemy would have to be about the <b>beginning of Libra [♎]</b>, where the error of the Ecliptic's inclination becomes almost insensible; and whereas he established the maximum northern latitude in <b>Saturn [♄] at 3° 6′</b>, we could find [it] no greater than <b>2° 49½′</b>, which falls short of his by a little more than a quarter-degree; and likewise in <b>Jupiter [♃]</b> the maximum northern latitude was insinuated by him at <b>2° 5′</b>, which we have attained at only <b>1° 39½′</b>, so that <b>25′ are altogether wanting</b> — which differences in each of these Planets cannot be excused by the uncertain inclination of the Ecliptic to the Equator, since they fall out not far from the concurrence of both circles. Add this too: the <b>northern-latitude limit of Saturn</b>, which Ptolemy believed 50° earlier than its Apogee, now stands distant, in the preceding [direction], <b>65½°</b> from the Apogee (which sticks at 26° of Sagittarius [♐], 3½° earlier in the Copernican [reckoning]); so too in Jupiter, where he handed down the northern limit of latitude 20° beyond the Apogee, [it is] now found by me only 3° later than the same. For at this time the Apogee of Jupiter occupies <b>3° of Libra [♎]</b>, and by Copernicus's mind is nearly 4° earlier; but the northern limit stands at the completed <b>6° of Libra</b>. How these [things] — about which I touched some [matters] too in my foregoing [letter] by general indication — are to be reconciled with Ptolemy's tenets, is not sufficiently clear: whether his observations were erroneous (for about ours, most subtly weighed, I doubt nothing), or whether truly such a change has crept into the latitudes and their limits. Whence it also appears that the <b>nodes</b> of these Planets, and the limits of their maximum digression from the Ecliptic, <b>do not obey the motion of the Apogees</b>, as has hitherto been believed; and that the great Astronomer of a former age, <b>Copernicus</b>, too inconsiderately acquiesced in Ptolemy's traditions, without his own experience, in these as also in very many other [matters]; and therefore [it is] no wonder that the latitudes of the Planets are exhibited by the heaven itself far otherwise than his numeration bears.</p><p>But there is no need to seek this <b>doubt about the Ecliptic's obliquity</b> so far off and to weigh [it] in the balance, since the observations of the <b>Artificers</b> who lived after Ptolemy support our finding in determining the same, without a very sensible difference. The [data] of <b>al-Battānī</b> [Albategnius] — nearly intermediate between Ptolemy and us — indicate that 700 years ago this maximum obliquity was scarcely <b>4½′</b> greater [than now]; and [those] of <b>Azrael the Spaniard</b> [al-Zarqālī/Arzachel], nearly 200 years after, at least <b>2½′</b> greater than our observation — which is perceptible only by the greatest instruments, such as they scarcely used, and can in some measure be excused, by the <b>solar parallax</b> neglected by them, within one or one-and-a-half minutes; <b>Prophatius the Jew</b> [Jacob ben Makhir] a little later still, another 200 years from Azrael, establishing the same as we [do] within half a minute, 300 years back, since he perhaps used a larger and more exact instrument and guarded against the solar parallax. From all which it is gathered probably enough that there is scarcely any sensible change underlying this obliquity. But it might seem a wonder that from <b>Aristarchus</b> — who flourished about 40 years from the death of Alexander the Great — down to <b>Ptolemy</b>, who was 460 [years] from the same, through the intermediate <b>Eratosthenes and Hipparchus</b>, the supreme obliquity of the Ecliptic remained plainly the same, which they over a greater interval of 400 years unanimously reported as <b>23° 51⅓′</b>; but now, from al-Battānī to us through 700 years, [it] remains the same within 3′ (for al-Battānī differs from our finding by only a very little, if he had applied parallax), [though] nearly a third of a degree smaller than the notations of those ancients — which moves the suspicion that <b>Ptolemy and Hipparchus</b> (as you too hold) trusted too much in the authority of their predecessors, and therefore admitted like [values] at [their] pleasure. But that the <b>moderns</b> — namely <b>Peurbach, Regiomontanus, Werner</b>, and Copernicus himself too — rendered the same nearly <b>4′ smaller</b> than ours: this error was committed on account of the <b>refraction of the Sun in the lower winter position</b>, which can grow to <b>8 or 9′</b>, and render the Sun's meridian altitude just so much greater than is just (which, by half, too greatly contracts the maximum declination sought from each Tropic), [a refraction] not foreseen by them; nay, even the <b>observed distances of the Sun [☉] from the zenith at Nuremberg</b>, made both by Regiomontanus and his disciple <b>Walther</b> about 100 years ago, if both parallax and the Sun's refraction be rightly applied to them, prove that the Sun's maximum declination with respect to the Equator was then the same, within ½′, as that which we have observed.</p><p>So too I think the <b>advancement of the fixed stars</b> [precession] is not so irregular as Copernicus laboured to introduce and excuse, by the anticipation of the equinoxes indicated, from the ancients' less-settled observations, a collation being made with his [own] and theirs (also not accurate enough). For if we deduce the observations on the stars of <b>Hipparchus</b> (Ptolemy's doubtful notations being neglected), through the intermediate <b>al-Battānī</b> (who was more accurate in these), to our findings, it will be established that they accomplished, in the <b>1006 years from Hipparchus to al-Battānī, 14° 15′</b>, and from him to us, the interposed <b>705 years, precisely ten degrees</b>. Whence it will follow that in the whole interval, taken on both sides too, they accomplished <b>1° in 70½ years, and 51″ in each single [year]</b>, by an equal and ordinary progress. Wherefore the suspicion can not unjustly be moved that <b>Ptolemy's</b> handed-down [values] were assumed only estimatively, so that he ascribed 1° to the motion of the fixed [stars] <b>in a hundred years</b>, in imitation of certain predecessors whom the paucity of time and the uncertainty of observations deceived. By a like reasoning, moreover, I think the <b>inequality of the year</b> is not so enormous and various as was obtruded on us by the same Copernicus — even this very century gainsaying [it] (to say nothing now of those ancient observations) — [namely] the observations of <b>Regiomontanus and Walther</b>, called to the balance with ours, which prove the <b>quantity of the year</b> to attain, beyond its days, <b>5 hours [and] not quite 49 minutes</b> — whereas according to Copernicus it would be 6 minutes and more greater, which by continual aggregation effects that he hallucinates intolerably in the times of the equinoxes, so much so that he now prolongs the vernal [equinox] by <b>14½ hours</b>, that is, more than half a day beyond what is just — an error so fertile that after 100 years it turns out he has too much, by a whole day and three hours besides, in establishing the vernal equinox. And if from Hipparchus through al-Battānī down to Regiomontanus and us the quantity of the year be derived, it will be known [to be] nearly similar to itself, and not so enormous as Copernicus thought — <b>Ptolemy alone</b> (from wherever at last he slipped into this error) disturbing the whole business; nor does Copernicus himself rightly apply the Sun's motions consonant with the heaven itself — the occasions of which derivation we opened in the said <i>Progymnasmata</i>, book [chapter] 1. Hence too I am led to doubt about the <b>eccentricity of the Sun</b>, so greatly changed from Ptolemy down to now — since that which he, together with Hipparchus, retained at <b>2½</b> [parts] (of which the semidiameter of the eccentric is 60) was afterward found by al-Battānī and Azrael alike at only <b>2 [parts] and 5 minutes</b>, which does not greatly fall short of our most exact designation, <b>2° [parts] 9′</b> — and the matter is truly of the subtlest inquiry, so that it is no wonder that Astronomers more keen-sighted than <b>Lynceus</b> disagree here in three or four minutes; all which I submit to your most weighty and most polished censure, to be weighed and discerned. From all and each of which it is most clearly established that you do not vainly doubt about the ancients' diligence in administering such [matters], and not unfittingly subscribe to our hesitation in this part too. I too have noticed that they sought a certain <b>harmony from numbers and Pythagorean proportions</b> (as I think), and acquiesced in it too securely; whoever shall hand down [values] thus exactly consonant with the appearances will be to me a great <b>Apollo</b> — but that is to be investigated hereafter. <i>But this is the work, this the labour</i> [Virgil]; for there is no doubt that <b>God, the author of the universe, founded all things harmonically, even in the celestials</b>, as appears in man, the <b>microcosm</b> [μικροκόσμῳ], and in the whole understanding lower nature.</p><p>Our <b>Tables of the motion of the luminaries</b> I shall take care to have printed separately, as soon as it can be done, and shall send [them] ahead of the other Planets, in which I shall still elaborate some [things]; but I do not gladly issue <b>bare numbers without the strength of observations and demonstrations</b>. For I wish my [results] to be settled and demonstrated, lest any place be deservedly left for doubt or detraction; yet I shall be at first rather sparing in these, and shall bring forth at least the most necessary. The rest I shall reserve for that great <b>Theatre of Astronomy</b> which I am attempting — if, by God's kindness and the Emperor's subsidies, it shall be permitted to complete it — where I purpose to weave together amply each [thing], both as to observations and as to the Geometrical and Arithmetical [matters] making for this divine art. Meanwhile I would most gladly communicate to you those [things] which concern the <b>calculation of Eclipses</b>, in so far as they have hitherto been elaborated by me; but I have few of them here with me. The chief [ones] are among my library-goods left at Magdeburg; if any have perchance been carried hither by my students, for their daily use, I will soon have inquiry made about them at Benátky (where those which I took with me from Wittenberg are still kept), and will add what shall be found to these — although not all are so polished and brought to the final file as is required, and I have others begun and in manuscript, though not now at hand. A certain <b>Hamburg Printer</b> deceived me, meanwhile, while, making for Bohemia, I [went] thither to <b>Dresden</b> a year ago, and <b>corrupted and badly printed the gatherings on the Moon's [☾] motion</b> wanted in my <i>Progymnasmata</i>; and while afterward, through the winter leisure, I tried to renew this labour at <b>Wittenberg</b>, I found neither the required fidelity nor diligence among the Printers of that place, and soon, being most clemently called hither by the Imperial Majesty, I was forced to break off what was begun there, other impediments too coming on which threw delays in my way. But as soon as everything shall be more fully completed (which I shall strive may be done at the first time too), I will not unwillingly communicate to you, whether printed or in manuscript, not doubting that those [things] which shall be private and not yet made public, entrusted to you and you alone, you will keep reserved. Nor indeed would there have been need of so great a protestation and obligation in this part, since I trust you otherwise — though unknown by face, yet sufficiently perceived by fame and even by these disputations ventilated between us — and do not refuse to attribute [to you] that which your virtue deserves. As for the <b>Epochs</b> which you require, if I find the rest, I will add [them], so that our restitutions may be applied on both sides to Ptolemaic times, or even forward through several centuries, until you have obtained fuller and more exact [ones] from me, in so far as the ancients' observations, taken in a broad manner (which you yourself confess not inconsiderately), allow it. Use these meanwhile.</p><p>You exhort that I hasten my <b>Astronomical works</b> — which also many excellent men throughout Europe, as far as Christendom extends, have exhorted, and still exhort; nay, I too bear this delay quite unwillingly. I am now away from my homeland, where my studies were disturbed, for nearly three years, nor was it permitted to find the longed-for rest for them before now, our most clement Emperor granting it; and since I alone do not suffice for so great a burden of observations and computations, I have now at least with me <b>3 or 4 students</b>, whose work I use in this matter — whereas before, in Denmark, while the <b>kingdom of Urania</b> stood whole and the renowned castle [stood], I was wont to maintain <b>10 or 12</b> for this cause. But I hope that, when our most clement Emperor and Lord shall have looked more deeply into the mass of the matter, he will still more kindly extend most clement helping hands, and will not yield to that old <b>Alfonso</b> [X] — although such expenses are not required — and will nonetheless promote more solid [things] (let the saying be without envy).</p><p>I gladly understood that you are diligently versed in the <b>compendia of Triangles to be resolved into numbers by prosthaphaereses alone</b>. This ingenious and most useful inquiry I undertook to unfold nearly <b>20 years ago</b> from the <b>Dialogue of Rheticus</b>, to which he subjoined whole Tables of Triangles, computed by himself only to <b>sextants of degrees</b>. I took the occasion, while he advises that the compendia of Geometrical and Astronomical calculation must be sought — so that, without the Astronomical tables of equations or prosthaphaereses, the true places of the Planets might be drawn more readily from the mean motions alone — which, since it could by no means be done through tedious multiplications and divisions, [showed that] this other way, afterward fruitfully discovered by us, had to be entered; to which <b>Werner</b> too, in his little book <i>On the motion of the eighth sphere</i>, furnished some handle. Afterward, <b>Paul Wittich</b> of good memory, an excellent Mathematician of Breslau, coming upon [me] and visiting me in Denmark at Uraniborg, in the year [15]80, for 4 months — who had also meanwhile sweated over such [things] (admonished by me at Wittenberg when I once passed through there) — the work being communicated with him, I rendered those very [things] more lucid and ever afterward had [them] in use, whereby the expense of time and labour might be lessened for my students reducing our observations into numbers. But <b>Wittich</b>, departing from me and afterward approaching the most illustrious <b>Wilhelm, Landgrave of Hesse</b> of most praised memory, both expounded there the construction of my instruments, and divulged these <b>mysteries of Triangles</b> to the Prince's clockmaker <b>Jost Bürgi</b> [Justus Byrgius], an excellent mechanic — from whom a certain <b>scurrilous Sycophant</b> (whose genius, if any there be, dwells most basely, [and] whom I deem unworthy to be named here, [but] what a reviler and calumniator he is, I shall leave attested elsewhere) had them, whether by consent or by fraud I would not say, and afterward published [them] as his own at <b>Strasbourg</b>, with certain others scraped together here and there — maimed, however, and mutilated. Whence, taking the occasion, but wrongly attributing [them] to that man, that most excellent Mathematician <b>Christophorus Clavius</b> inserted them into his most erudite volume <i>On the Astrolabe</i>, and in a certain way augmented [them] — occupied, however, as far as I see, only with the <b>Spherical and Right-angled</b> [triangles], which by no means suffice for our intention. The Lord <b>Jacob Curtius</b> of best memory, former Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, a man greater than all praise, and exceedingly fond and understanding of these studies (whom would that the fates had reserved longer for us!), laboured, I say, to adapt these not only to <b>oblique-angled Spherical</b> [triangles] but also to certain <b>plane Triangles</b>. But in this latter, as I hear, he did not altogether attain what he wished — whether hindered by the tedium of the labour, the gravest occupations of so ample an office in so great a Republic calling him away, or also forestalled by untimely death. When I lingered at <b>Wittenberg</b> for a while last winter and noticed that the Typographical labours did not succeed according to my mind, I took up this work again into [my] hands, and, the eminent Professor of Mathematics there <b>Melchior Jöstelius</b> being applied, I caused all those [things] which concern both Spherical and plane Triangles — whatever fall into Astronomical and Geographical use — to be reduced compendiously into order, to be reckoned by prosthaphaereses alone, so that nothing further can now be wanted in these; which too, in its time, I will have made public for the common utility and promotion of these studies — nay, even before [that], if you so request, I will not unwillingly render you a sharer of them, since every good is communicable, especially to the worthy; trusting that you will return like for like in your similar findings which you have ready (for eyes often see more than an eye); if you anticipate [me], so much the more, and so much the sooner, will you instigate me to perform it.</p><p>[The <b>books</b> which I destined for your most serene and most illustrious Princes, and also for you, it is not yet permitted to send; for the copies of our <b>Astronomical Mechanics</b> [<i>Mechanica</i>], which I committed to a Prague painter to be <b>illuminated</b> (as they call it), I have not yet received back — for it has not pleased to inquire about them solicitously in this dangerous time; the rest, in this unrest of my affairs and a state not yet sufficiently composed, could not be written out as quickly as I would wish — but it will nonetheless be done, God willing, at the very first opportunity, since every promise falls into a debt. That you have so benevolently communicated to me the <b>Catalogue of Mathematical books</b> found in your Princes' most-furnished Library there, and have wished to be informed about those which I shall dispatch [request], I give the greatest thanks I can. For this matter is most acceptable to me, since I am earnestly delighted by such [things]. And although very many of them pleased, I have nevertheless selected from them only <b>50</b>, which you see denoted in the attached sheet — among them are also the <b>Hebrew books</b> afterward suggested to me by you. If you send all these to me at the very first occasion, you will have done a most welcome thing; I will restore them all in good faith at the time predefined by me; and if you add at the same time those <b>six</b> which I marked together, excerpted from the <b>Catalogue of the Frankfurt Fairs</b>, I will at once pay the indicated price and send [it] back.]</p><p>[There occurs to me also something else, after I saw you dealing with me freely and liberally about this literary matter. I remember, when in my youth I dwelt for nearly two years at <b>Augsburg</b>, a city neighbouring you, and kept watch from time to time over celestial observations there in the garden of the Lord Consul <b>Paul Hainzel</b> [Hentzelius] of good memory, by the <b>great Quadrant</b> having 21 feet from centre to circumference, built there by us — among other [things]] [I] understood that the illustrious and generous Lords <b>Fugger</b> keep in their most ample <b>Library</b> certain excellent books, both in this and in another genre of the arts — of inspecting which, however, the opportunity, then, for I know not what impediments, was not given; and among others I learned that some <b>manuscript works of that most laborious and eminent man, Cyprian Leovitius</b> [Cyprianus Leovitius] the Bohemian, of praised memory, were sought out by [his] widow after his death and are detained there — such as <b>Ephemerides</b>, continued by him for some hundreds of years from the <b>Alphonsine</b> Calculation to ten-day [intervals] at least, and others from the <b>Copernican</b> [Tables] beginning from the year 1500 (how far they extend, I do not now recall); also the same Cyprian's <b>resolved Tables of directions</b>, in which the oblique ascensions are extended to the eighth degree of latitude on each side from the Ecliptic.</p><p>[I also had in hand a great work <b>On Genethliacal Astrology</b>, which whether he completed and [whether] it came to the Fuggers, I do not know. If you should procure for me these and similar manuscript and not-yet-published works of this most excellent man — so that I might inspect them for a few days at leisure (for they will be of no use in restoring the Astronomical [science], and in those [things] which I am attempting, since they are, as you know, commonplace) — provided [you do so] by the authority and most clement intercession at least of your most Serene Princes (to whom that Republic and the Fuggers themselves deservedly attribute very much), my name being nonetheless concealed, if you should so judge advisable — you will have done a most acceptable office; I will restore whatever I obtain inviolate, at the appointed day; and if I could at the same time obtain an <b>index of all the books</b> held in that splendid Library, especially of the Mathematicians and Philosophers, it will be the more acceptable to me. Now I add no more, lest I detain and weary you too much at one time. But I officiously and most lovingly ask that you take in good part these [things] which have now come to [my] mouth; and if, as you give hope, you shall some time not be burdened to visit me, you will come not only as a most longed-for guest, but as an intimate friend, to be received with every kind of office according to my measure. Provided it be permitted by the Emperor, and a convenience some time offer itself, that I enter those regions, I shall not forbear to address you in person at <b>Munich</b> too; nay, whatever other offices can proceed from me, I most gladly offer; and I pray from [my] heart that you, with your whole house, fare as well and as long as possible.]</p><p>Given with a hastening pen, from the Imperial castle of <b>Girsitz</b>, the 16th day of November, New Style, in the Year 1599.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). The dataset's `source_edition` (\"<i>Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588</i>\") is the later printed collection. MS witnesses: <b>A</b> = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (copy); <b>B</b> = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen, <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i> (1668). <b>§1 cross-check (done 2026-06-02):</b> page-collated at <b>vol. VIII pp. 209–217</b> (Dreyer internal pp. 195–204), from cod. Vindob. lat. 10702; every numeral confirmed against the edition of record (anchors in the Editorial note).</li><li><b>Sender / recipient.</b> Tycho (at <b>Girsitz</b>, an Imperial castle ~3 [German] miles from Prague, his plague-refuge from Benátky). <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), Bavarian <b>Chancellor</b>, polymath and book-collector (and Kepler's correspondent). This is the <b>companion to Tycho's reply of 28 August 1600</b> (to Herwart's Jan–May 1600 series); here the friendship is \"by fame, face unknown.\"</li><li><b>Strand 1 — exile, transport, plague.</b> Tycho's <b>eldest son</b> (Tycho the Younger) sent to Denmark for the <b>remaining instruments</b>; the Emperor's letters to the <b>Magdeburg magistrate</b> to ship instruments + library + goods down the <b>Elbe</b> to Bohemia; Magdeburg's evasion, the second Imperial letter via <b>Barvitius</b>, and the Elbe too low from drought. The <b>plague</b> nearing Benátky drove his household to Girsitz; <b>Rudolf II's</b> \"paternal\" favour (salary via the Captain of <b>Brandeis</b>; leave to remove to Vienna, declined). On <b>Denmark</b>: \"one's homeland is where one lives well\"; he sends a packet documenting his emigration's causes, points to his <i>Epistolae</i> \"p. 198,\" and encloses <b>poems</b> by sympathetic poets (more expected from <b>Scaliger</b> and <b>Paul Melissus</b>), the last two <b>epigrams</b> approved by the Emperor for the marble doors of the new <b>Observatory and Laboratory</b>.</li><li><b>Strand 2 — the technical argument (obliquity / precession / year / eccentricity).</b></li><li><b>Venus not eclipsed by Mercury</b>: not in every conjunction is Mercury below Venus (sometimes above), per both Tycho's and Copernicus's hypotheses.</li><li><b>Ancient imprecision</b> (\"rough Minerva\"): the fixed-star places good only to ~10′ (sometimes ½°), betrayed by constant star-<i>distances</i> not matching their numbers.</li><li><b>Obliquity essentially constant.</b> Applying Tycho's measured <b>maximum obliquity 23° 31½′</b> to the declinations of <b>Timochares/Hipparchus/Ptolemy</b> reproduces present latitudes — so the obliquity was not really changed, only the ancients imprecise (within ⅓°). Worked examples: <b>Castor &amp; Pollux</b> (latitude 18–19′ off); <b>Altair</b> (<i>Vultur volans</i>, decl. 5⅚° N → lat. 29° 40′, Ptolemy's table ⅓° high); <b>Mars</b> (apogee now 23½° Leo, 5° before Copernicus; N-lat 4° 35′ vs Ptolemy 4⅓°); <b>Saturn</b> (N-lat 2° 49½′ vs Ptolemy 3° 6′) and <b>Jupiter</b> (1° 39½′ vs 2° 5′, \"25′ wanting\") — proving the <b>nodes/latitude-limits do not follow the apogee motion</b> (contra the received view; Copernicus too credulous of Ptolemy).</li><li><b>Obliquity history</b>: al-Battānī (~700 yr ago, +4½′), al-Zarqālī/Arzachel (<i>Asrael</i>, +2½′), Prophatius (Jacob ben Makhir, ~within ½′, 300 yr ago); Aristarchus→Ptolemy constant at 23° 51⅓′; the moderns (Peurbach/Regiomontanus/Werner/Copernicus) ~4′ low from <b>uncorrected winter solar refraction (8–9′)</b>; Regiomontanus &amp; <b>Walther</b>'s Nuremberg zenith-distances confirm Tycho's value within ½′.</li><li><b>Precession uniform</b>: Hipparchus→al-Battānī (1006 yr) 14° 15′; al-Battānī→now (705 yr) exactly 10°; ⇒ <b>1°/70½ yr = 51″/yr</b> (Ptolemy's 1°/100 yr merely estimative).</li><li><b>Year-length</b>: 365ᵈ <b>5ʰ ~49ᵐ</b> (Regiomontanus/Walther + Tycho), not Copernicus's longer value — Copernicus's vernal equinox now off <b>14½ h</b> (→ 1 day 3 h per century).</li><li><b>Solar eccentricity</b>: Ptolemy/Hipparchus 2½ⁿ (ecc-radius = 60) → al-Battānī/Azrael 2° 5′ → Tycho <b>2° 9′</b>. The ancients sought a \"<b>Pythagorean harmony</b>\"; \"whoever delivers values consonant with the appearances will be my great Apollo.\" Closes with the <i>Aeneid</i> tag <i>hoc opus, hic labor est</i> and the theological coda that <b>God founded all things harmonically</b> (man the <b>microcosm</b>, μικροκόσμῳ).</li><li><b>Strand 3 — publication, prosthaphaeresis priority, books.</b> Tables of the luminaries to be printed first, but \"no bare numbers without observations and demonstrations\"; the rest reserved for the \"<b>Theatre of Astronomy</b>.\" The <b>Hamburg printer</b> who corrupted the <i>Progymnasmata</i> lunar gatherings; the failed Wittenberg printing; the Imperial summons. <b>The prosthaphaeresis story</b> (a key priority document): Tycho took it up ~20 yr ago from <b>Rheticus's</b> <i>Dialogus</i> (triangle tables to sextant-degrees), with <b>Werner's</b> <i>De motu octavae sphaerae</i> giving a handle; <b>Paul Wittich</b> (Breslau) worked on it during his <b>4-month 1580 Uraniborg visit</b>; Wittich then went to <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b>, exposing Tycho's instruments and divulging the \"triangle mysteries\" to the clockmaker <b>Jost Bürgi</b>; whence a \"<b>scurrilous sycophant</b>\" (unnamed — <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b>) got them and published them \"maimed\" at <b>Strasbourg</b> (in his <i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i>, 1588); <b>Clavius</b> then inserted them (miscrediting Ursus) in his <i>Astrolabium</i>; <b>Jacob Curtius</b> tried to extend them to plane triangles; Tycho with <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (Wittenberg) completed the spherical + plane reduction \"by prosthaphaereses alone.\" Finally (bracketed passages) the <b>book exchange</b>: the <i>Mechanica</i> copies still with a Prague illuminator; <b>50 books</b> selected from the Munich princes' library catalogue (incl. <b>Hebrew</b> books) + <b>6</b> from the Frankfurt fair catalogue; and the request, via the Bavarian princes' authority (Tycho's name concealed), for <b>Cyprian Leovitius's</b> manuscripts (Ephemerides, directions tables, a genethliacal-astrology work) held by the <b>Fuggers</b> at Augsburg — with the recollection of Tycho's youthful <b>Augsburg</b> years and the <b>21-foot great quadrant</b> in <b>Paul Hainzel's</b> garden (c. 1569).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places.</b> <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b> (imperial secretary); the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>; <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>, <b>Paul Melissus</b> (Schede, the poet); <b>Timochares, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Eratosthenes, Aristarchus</b>; <b>al-Battānī</b> (<i>Albategnius</i>), <b>al-Zarqālī/Arzachel</b> (<i>Asrael Hispanus</i>), <b>Jacob ben Makhir</b> (<i>Prophatius Iudaeus</i>); <b>Peurbach, Regiomontanus, Bernhard Walther, Johann Werner, Copernicus</b>; <b>Rheticus, Paul Wittich, Jost Bürgi, Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (the unnamed sycophant), <b>Christophorus Clavius, Jacob Curtius (Kurz von Senftenau), Melchior Jöstel</b>; <b>Cyprian Leovitius</b>, the <b>Fuggers</b>, <b>Paul Hainzel</b>; <b>Alfonso X</b> (\"old Alfonso\"). Places: <b>Magdeburg, the Elbe, Benátky, Girsitz, Prague, Vienna, Dresden, Wittenberg, Strasbourg, Augsburg, Munich, Nuremberg</b>.</li><li><b>Glyphs / Greek / tags.</b> ♀ Venus, ☿ Mercury, ☾ Moon, ☉ Sun, ♂ Mars, ♄ Saturn, ♃ Jupiter, ♋ Cancer, ♌ Leo, ♎ Libra, ♐ Sagittarius, ♊ Gemini; <b>□</b> = <i>quadraturis</i> (quadratures). <i>μικροκόσμῳ</i> = microcosm. <i>crassa Minerva</i> = \"with rough Minerva\" (crudely, Horace/Cicero). <i>Differ, habent parvae commoda magna morae</i> — Ovid, <i>Fasti</i> III.394 (≈ \"small delays have great advantages\"). <i>hoc opus, hic labor est</i> — Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> VI.129. <i>Lynceo oculatiores</i> — \"more keen-sighted than Lynceus.\" <i>erit mihi magnus Apollo</i> — Virgil, <i>Ecl.</i> III.104. <i>Vultur volans</i> = Aquila/Altair. <i>Heliconis fontibus / venas acuere</i> — poetic inspiration.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <b>16 November 1599 New Style</b> (<i>stylo renovato</i>) — as an Imperial-territory letter in Catholic Bohemia, Tycho here writes N.S. (contrast his Danish O.S. datelines, e.g. the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588). Matches the catalogue label.</li><li><b>Editorial interventions in the source block (declared).</b> (1) ~55 OCR-intruded <b>apparatus \"A\" sigla</b> removed as non-textual (as in the letters to Scaliger of 23 Aug 1598, to Peucer of 1590, and to the Landgrave of 16 Aug 1588). (2) The source's <b>square brackets</b> around the three late passages (book-list; Augsburg/Fugger recollection; closing) are <b>retained as printed</b> — Dreyer's/witness-level brackets (the bracket even cuts mid-sentence at \"<i>inter alia] intellexisse</i>\"), flagged here, not introduced by me. (3) The editorial <i>[qui]</i> (Dreyer's supplement in \"PTOLEMAeUM [qui] 460 ab eodem abfuit\") is kept in brackets. Spelling <i>Erathostenis</i>/<i>Eratosthenem</i>, <i>Witebergâ</i>/<i>Wittebergae</i>, <i>Noribergae</i> left as printed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Two word-level transcription corruptions restored against Dreyer (TBOO VIII): 'expeditioni' -&gt; 'expectationi' (p.193), with the corresponding English clause corrected from 'your dispatch' to 'your expectation'; and 'antenus' -&gt; 'anterius' (p.196, no effect on the English).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, three-stranded letter (exile/patronage narrative; the obliquity–precession–year-length–eccentricity argument; the prosthaphaeresis-priority story and book exchange) rendered securely throughout; the courtly register and the dense technical register are both preserved, and the controlled glossary (obliquity, declination, latitude, eccentricity, apogee, node, prosthaphaeresis, refraction, parallax, <i>restitutio/instauratio</i>, the epistolary formulae) is held consistent with the Herwart letter of 28 Aug 1600 (the same correspondent), the Magini letter of 1 Dec 1590 and the Landgrave letter of 16 Aug 1588 (the technical letters), and the Craig letter of 14 Nov 1591 with Hájek's of 11 Jun 1590 (the Ursus and Curtius threads). The numerical spine (obliquity 23° 31½′; precession 51″/yr = 1°/70½ yr; year 365ᵈ 5ʰ ~49ᵐ; solar ecc. 2° 9′; the al-Battānī/Azrael/Prophatius series) is internally consistent and matches Tycho's documented results. The <b>§1 Dreyer page-collation has since been run (2026-06-02, vol. VIII pp. 209–217) and confirms the entire numerical spine</b> against the edition of record, so the collation reservation that previously capped the grade is <b>lifted</b>. Two residues remain: (1) the <b>source's editorial brackets</b> (witness-level, one cutting mid-sentence) are reproduced as-is, not resolved; (2) the heavy <b>OCR \"A\"-sigla</b> removal, declared. Neither obscures the through-line; grade held at <b>[B]</b> pending Christian's review (a case for [A] is now open).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 14 Dreyer page images in 4-page chunks (TBOO VIII pp.191-204: the tail of I.A. Maginus's letter, the full Tycho-to-Herwart letter, and the head of Tycho's letter to Kepler). Collated the opening and closing lines, every named person (Barvitius, the Fuggers, Cyprian Leovitius, Paul Hentzelius/Hainzel, Scaliger, Melissus, Wittich, Landgrave Wilhelm, Jost Buergi, Clavius, Jacob Curtius, Joestelius, Rheticus, Werner, al-Battani, Azrael, Prophatius, Aristarchus, Eratosthenes, Hipparchus, Ptolemy, Timochares, Peurbach, Regiomontanus, Walther, Copernicus), every place-name (Girsitz, Benatky, Prague, the Elbe, Magdeburg, Bohemia, Wittenberg, Breslau, Strasbourg, Munich, Augsburg, Bavaria, Nuremberg, Denmark, Uraniborg), the date (16 November 1599 N.S.), and the dense run of astronomical numbers (obliquity/latitude/precession figures for Castor-Pollux, Aquila, Mars, Saturn, Jupiter, plus every historical interval from Aristarchus to al-Battani) against the page images. Two source-text words diverge from Dreyer's print: 'expeditioni' for his 'expectationi' (p.193, which also skews the English at that clause) and 'antenus' for his 'anterius' (p.196, no translation effect). No fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss found; the translation is otherwise a complete, careful, literal rendering including all bracketed passages.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text reads 'expeditioni' (dispatch/errand); Dreyer's page prints 'expectationi' (expectation) - a genuine word-level corruption in the transcription, not a variant spelling. Dreyer prints: <i>quod tuae expectationi non prius morem gesserim (TBOO VIII, p.193)</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). Because it follows the corrupted source_text, the English renders 'your dispatch' where Dreyer's actual word 'expectationi' requires 'your expectation' - Tycho is apologising for disappointing Herwart's expectation, not for failing to act on a dispatched errand. At: “that I have not sooner complied with your dispatch”.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). 'antenus' is not a Latin word; Dreyer prints 'anterius' ('earlier'), which also matches the word's correct use elsewhere in this same letter. No effect on the English, which already renders the sense correctly as '3½° earlier'. Dreyer prints: <i>3½ anterius distare in antecedentia P. 65½ (TBOO VIII, p.196)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All three defects were verified against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.193 and 196, read visually, not via OCR text layer). P.193 confirms 'expectationi' where our source_text had 'expeditioni', which also required correcting the English clause 'your dispatch' to 'your expectation'. P.196 confirms 'anterius' where our source_text had the non-word 'antenus'; the existing English already rendered the correct sense ('earlier') and needed no change. No other text was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift", "B København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15990917001", "next": "brahe-t_15991231001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990917001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000403001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of two letters and the Bavarian library catalogue, explaining his delayed reply by his busy correspondence while sending his eldest son to retrieve instruments from Magdeburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991116001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991126001", "d": "1599-11-26", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Beate Bille", "ck": "Beate Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": null, "t": "Beate Bille → Tycho Brahe — Billesgård, 26 November 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (family) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 21f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLLIIG OCH VELBYERDIG MAND THYGGE BRAHE THILL VRANIBORIGH, MINN KIERRE SØNN, GANDSCHE VENLIGEN THILL SKREFFUENN.</p><p>KIERRE Thiige och kierre sønn, Gud allmegthiste werre nu och allthid huoß dig och alle dine och beuarre dig naadeligen och vell fra alltt dett, som vntt er. Kierre sønn, haffuer jeg dig paa thet allervenligste bethackitt for din guode skriffuelße, som du skriffuer mig thill, kierre sønn, och villtt wide, huor jeg nu er thill passe, saa er dett ennnu, Gud verre loffuett, gott mett mig. Kierre sønn, saa er ditt breff mig møgitt kiertt att hørre, att du, Gud verre loffuett, er nu kommen thill roulighed, och huis ditt breff er annlangindis, er mig møgitt kiertt, att dig och dine lider alltt vell, Gud verre loffuett; thj Gud vill ingen haffue forgett, som hannom frøckther. Och som din sønn haffuer ochsaa berett for migh, att din leilighed gaar dig løckelig och vell annd, Gud verre loffuet, saa vill jeg, kierre sønn, icke lenger ophollde dig mett denne minn korthe skrifuellße. Du skalltt alle miine dauge finde mig for din hulle maader och will her mett haffue dig Gud allmegthiste beffallendis, dig och alle dine, mett alltt løcke och vellfardt.</p><p>Aff Billisgaardt then 26 nouembris anno 99.<br>Beatte Bille</p>", "en": "<p><b>Honourable and well-born man, Tyge Brahe to Uraniborg, my dear son, written to in all friendliness.</b></p><p>Dear Tyge and dear son, may God Almighty be now and always with you and all yours, and keep you graciously and well from all that is evil. Dear son, I have most kindly thanked you for your good writing, which you write to me; dear son, and [you] would know how I now fare — so it is still, God be praised, well with me. Dear son, so is your letter very dear to me to hear: that you have, God be praised, now come to peace [a settled rest]; and as concerns your letter, it is very dear to me that you and yours are all faring well, God be praised — for God will have none forgotten who fear Him. And as your son has also reported to me, that your situation goes happily and well for you, God be praised — so I will, dear son, not detain you longer with this my short writing. You shall, all my days, find me [to be] your devoted mother; and [I] will herewith have you commended to God Almighty — you and all yours — with all fortune and welfare.</p><p>From Billesgård, the 26th of November, in the year [15]99.<br>Beate Bille.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender — Tycho's mother.</b> <b>Beate Bille</b> (1526–1605), widow of <b>Otte Brahe</b> and mother of Tycho; writing from <b>Billesgård</b> (her estate in Skåne). A tender maternal letter: she rejoices that Tycho has \"come to peace\" (<i>kommen til rolighed</i>) and that, as his son had also told her, his \"situation goes happily and well\" — i.e. she has heard of his secure new position under the Emperor. She signs herself <i>din hulde moder</i>, \"your devoted mother.\"</li><li><b>The grandson as messenger.</b> <i>din søn</i> (\"your son\") who \"reported to me\" that Tycho's affairs prosper = one of Tycho's sons (most likely <b>Tycho the Younger</b>, then travelling between Bohemia and Denmark; cf. brahe-t_16000329003, …0414002), evidently the bearer of news to his grandmother.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Late-16th-c. Danish: <i>erlliig</i> (ærlig), <i>velbyerdig</i> (velbyrdig), <i>thill skreffuenn</i> (tilskrevet), <i>allmegthiste</i> (almægtigste), <i>beuarre</i> (bevare), <i>skriffuelße</i> (skrivelse), <i>thill passe</i> (til pas, faring/in health), <i>roulighed</i> (rolighed, peace/quiet), <i>annlangindis</i> (anlangendes, concerning), <i>frøckther</i> (frygter, fears), <i>leilighed</i> (situation), <i>løckelig</i> (lykkelig), <i>hulle maader</i> = <i>hulde moder</i> (devoted mother), <i>beffallendis</i> (befalende, commending). Reading-verified as <b>Danish</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Billisgaardt then 26 nouembris anno 99</i> = <b>26 November 1599</b>; Denmark (Skåne) kept the <b>Julian / Old Style</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the family Danish is rendered securely; the sender (Tycho's mother Beate Bille), the place (Billesgård), the warm content (relief at his new settled position, news carried by a grandson), and the date are all unambiguous. <i>din hulde moder</i> preserved as \"your devoted mother.\"</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV p.167 (Dreyer letter 172) as page image. Danish source_text collated word-for-word against Dreyer's print of Beate Bille's letter (heading, full body, dateline 'Aff Billisgaardt then 26 nouembris anno 99', signature) — exact match. English translation checked clause-by-clause against the Danish: all facts, the mother/son relationship, dates and closing formula render faithfully; no invented, dropped, or mistranslated content found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 21 f. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Beate Bille", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000321004", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000321004"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Brief maternal letter expressing gladness that Tycho has found settled circumstances, reporting her own good health, and commending him and his household to God.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991126001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991203001", "d": "1599-12-03", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "ck": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Axel Gyldenstierne → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 3 December 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 23 f. (Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLLIG OCH WELBYURDICH MANDT THYGGE BRADE TILL KNUDSTRUP, HØFFUITZMANDT PAA BENACSIA, MIN KIERRE NESTSSØSCHINDT BARN OCH SYNDERLIG GODE WEN, GANTZ WENLIG THILLSCHREFFUITT.</p><p>KIERE Thygge och min kiere morßøfterßenn, Gudt vere althidt huoß thig medt sin helig andt och beuare thig medt altt, huis thig kiert er, fra alltt ont. Jegh thacker thig gandske vennligen och gierne for aaltt beuist erre och gott, huilcket att forschyllde ieg vill althidt findis villige y huis mader meg mouligt er. Kiere Thygge, ieg haffuer stackit sidenn bekomit thinn schriffuelße, och theraff formerker thinn leglighedts gode thillstandt ther vde i thendt landtz egn, huilcken er mig som Gudt ßaa kiertt att høre, som du vaarst min egenn sønn heler broder. Och vill ønsche aff Gudt allermegtigste, att thet maa bliffue jo lenger jou beder. Sammelethis, kiere morßøsterßen, som samme thin schriffuelße ochsaa formelder om dj pendinge, ieg thig skylldig er, atu thenom thill thenne omslag maate bekomme, saa schall Gudt vide, att ieg helder end giernne (som och billichtt vaar) ville samme thinn begiering eptherkomme, thersom thet vaar mig mouligt. Menn nu kandt ieg thenom saa hastig och snnart jcke thill vege bringe, aff thend aarßage jegh nu vdj thenne thillkomindis vgge schall giffue kong. maytt. 6000 daller paa min sallige broder Peders vegnne, och thersom thet icke hafde verit, skulle du for:ne thinne pendinge giernne haffue bekommit, och beder thig ther for, kiere Thygge och kiere mor[sø]sterßøn, paa thet vennligst, du ville haffue gedultt medt for:ne pendinge thill nu jull førstkomindis ett aar, schall Eske Billde thenom paa thine vegnne, vden alldt vider forhalling heller vndschylding, bekomme. Dog renten er aarligen och endnu thette aar ochsaa bettalidt. Och ther som ieg i nogen mader kandt thienne thig igenn, skaltu i[n]genn thuill haffue paa, ieg giør jou giernne som ieg och pligthig er. Nytt att schriffue theg thill, er her inthet synderligt. Thij huadt vdj Thydschland sig thilldrager, vedst thu beder endt vij j diße lande; thij penninge haffuer affthackidt alle vore pentzionierer, som vj schulle haffue kundschaff aff, saa vell som och ander, vij sider saa secker och frij som en fougell vnder garnedt. Hertug Kaarell aff Suerig thiranißer grußammb, haffuer nu faaedt aldt Findlandt indt och ombragt och ladit rete alle dij adell ther y landit. Jeg frygter Erick Spaare och hanns paartie ville snart følge epther, huilckett Gudt aff vennde.</p><p>Kongen aff Pollenn ßende 8 schibbe met 800 mand her igenom sundit, schulle indthage Elsborige, paa huilcke johan Gyldenstiern vaar admierall. Hoffuidtschibbit, som admierall vaar paa, bleff vnder Halldlandtz side medt mieste paartenn skiøtt, huilkedt vij nu lader opthage och bierge oss thill beste, thet andit schibb, nest admierallen, bleff och med thop och thagell; the ander ere kommen thillbage hidt thill Kiøbenhaffuen, ville gierne bliffue her vdj vintherlege (thij the thør icke løbe hiemmb, hertug Kaarell er thenom for i vegenn met 12 schibe); the haffuer huercken att bide eler brydde; erre beuilgidt at maa bliffue her i byen paa nogen thidlang att there for thierris pendinge, huilcket erre saare faa; the haffuer soldt och selger endnu dagelig aff thierris krudt och laadt for pendinge thill vnderholding, doch thet vill icke naa lanngt. Thet er vell snucket verheffthig folck, men the haffuer icke thet, the kunde schyulle thennom vdj.</p><p>Jacop Sieffelltt haffuer Gudt kaldit nu for xiiij dage forledenn, epther hand haffde giortt thuende sin døthers brøllup med Niels Schrammb och Hans Lindernou thill Gaffuenøe. Huis andit nytt her er, som icke synderligt haffuer paa sig, thet ved thin sønn thig vell adt berete; thij vill ieg nu icke lennger forhinder thig met thenne min schriffuelße, men vill beffale dig met huiß thig kiertt er thendt gode Gud udj volldt.</p><p>Ex Kiøbenhaffuen thendt 3 decembris a:o 99.<br>Axell Gyldenstern<br>med egen hand.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and well-born man Tyge Brahe of Knudstrup, captain at Benátky, my dear first cousin and especial good friend, [this] most amicably written.</b></p><p>Dear Tyge, and my dear cousin [mother's-sister's-son], may God ever be with you with his Holy Spirit, and preserve you, with all that is dear to you, from all evil. I thank you quite amicably and gladly for all the honour and good shown me, which to repay I will always be found willing in whatever way is possible for me. Dear Tyge, I have a short while ago received your letter, and from it note the good state of your circumstances there in that region — which is as dear to me to hear (God knows) as if you were my own son or brother; and I will wish from God Almighty that it may grow the better the longer. Likewise, dear cousin, as your same letter also makes mention of the <b>money I owe you</b>, that you might receive it at this <i>Umschlag</i> — God knows I would more than gladly (as was also fair) comply with that request of yours, if it were possible for me. But now I cannot bring it to hand so quickly and soon, for the reason that I, in this coming week, must give the Royal Majesty <b>6,000 dalers on behalf of my late brother Peder</b>; and had it not been for that, you should gladly have received your aforesaid money. And I ask you therefore, dear Tyge and dear cousin, most amicably, that you would have patience with the said money until <b>next Christmas a year hence</b>, [and] <b>Eske Bille</b> shall receive it on your behalf, without all further delay or excuse — though the <b>interest</b> is paid yearly, and this year too. And if I can serve you in any way in return, you shall have no doubt [of it]; I do it gladly, as I am also bound. As for news to write you, there is nothing special here; for what passes in <b>Germany</b> you know better than we in these lands — for money has dismissed all our pensioners [informants], from whom we should have intelligence, as well as others: <b>we sit as secure and free as a bird under the net</b>. <b>Duke Charles of Sweden</b> tyrannizes cruelly: he has now taken all <b>Finland</b>, and put to death and executed all the nobility there in the land. I fear <b>Erik Sparre</b> and his party will soon follow — which God avert.</p><p>The <b>King of Poland</b> sent <b>8 ships with 800 men</b> here through the Sound, [meaning] to take <b>Älvsborg</b>, on which <b>Johan Gyldenstierne</b> was admiral. The flagship, on which the admiral was, was for the most part shot up off the <b>Halland</b> side — which we now have salvaged and recovered for our benefit; the second ship, next the admiral's, [was taken] too, with top and tackle [and all]. The others have come back here to <b>Copenhagen</b>, [and] would gladly stay here in winter quarters (for they dare not run home — <b>Duke Charles is in their way with 12 ships</b>); they have neither [bread to] bite nor [aught to] brew; they are permitted to stay in the city for some time, [living] on their money, which is very little; they have sold, and still daily sell, of their powder and shot for money for their keep — but it will not reach far. They are indeed neat, valiant folk, but they have not that wherein they might shelter themselves.</p><p><b>Jacob Sehested</b> God has now called [hence] a fortnight ago, after he had made the weddings of two of his daughters — with <b>Niels Skram</b> and <b>Hans Lindenov of Gavnø</b>. Whatever other news there is here, which is nothing special, your son will well know to report to you; therefore I will not now hinder you longer with this my letter, but will commend you, with all that is dear to you, into the good God's keeping.</p><p>From Copenhagen, the 3rd of December, the year [15]99.<br>Axel Gyldenstierne,<br>with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender &amp; kinship.</b> <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (1542–1603), Danish Rigsraad councillor and Statholder of Norway (1588–1601) — Tycho's <b>maternal cousin</b>, addressed as <i>min kiere morsøstersøn</i> (\"my dear mother's-sister's-son\") and, in the address, <i>næstsøskendebarn</i> (first cousin): their mothers were Bille sisters. He addresses Tycho as <i>\"captain at Benátky\"</i> (the imperial castle). The MS is the <b>original letter</b>.<br>- <b>Significance — a Danish kinsman's news-letter (December 1599).</b> Two strands:<br>  1. <b>A private debt.</b> Axel <b>owes Tycho money</b> (which Tycho's letter had asked to receive at the <i>Umschlag</i>); he cannot pay now, since \"in this coming week\" he must give the <b>King 6,000 dalers on behalf of his late brother Peder</b>, and begs delay <b>until next Christmas a year hence</b>, when <b>Eske Bille</b> will collect on Tycho's behalf — \"though the interest is paid yearly.\" (Tycho's web of Danish loans, like the Mecklenburg one.)<br>  2. <b>Scandinavian war-news.</b> A vivid bulletin of the <b>Polish–Swedish war</b> and the <b>Swedish succession crisis</b>:<br>     - <b>Duke Charles of Sweden</b> (later Charles IX) \"tyrannizes cruelly,\" having taken <b>Finland</b> and executed its nobility (the 1599 reduction of Finland / Åbo executions); Axel fears <b>Erik Sparre</b> (the Swedish statesman) \"will soon follow\" — as indeed he did, in the <b>Linköping Bloodbath</b> (1600).<br>     - The <b>King of Poland</b> (Sigismund III Vasa) sent <b>8 ships, 800 men</b> through the <b>Sound</b> to take <b>Älvsborg</b>, under admiral <b>Johan Gyldenstierne</b>; the flagship was <b>shot to pieces off Halland</b> (and salvaged by the Danes), a second ship taken, the rest <b>stranded at Copenhagen</b> — unable to sail home (Duke Charles blocking with 12 ships), destitute, <b>selling their powder and shot</b> to live: \"neat, valiant folk, but they have not that wherein they might shelter themselves.\" A striking eyewitness of the stranded Polish squadron.<br>  3. <b>Family news.</b> The death of <b>Jacob Sehested</b> (a fortnight earlier), after marrying two daughters to <b>Niels Skram</b> and <b>Hans Lindenov of Gavnø</b>.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Axel Gyldenstierne (sender); his late brother <b>Peder Gyldenstierne</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b> (to collect Tycho's money); <b>Duke Charles of Sweden</b>; <b>Erik Sparre</b>; <b>King Sigismund III of Poland</b>; <b>Johan Gyldenstierne</b> (the Polish-service admiral); <b>Jacob Sehested</b> and his daughters' husbands <b>Niels Skram</b> and <b>Hans Lindenov</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger, \"your son … will report\").<br>- <b>Idiom (kept and glossed).</b> <i>\"vi sidder så sikker og fri som en fugl under garnet\"</i> — \"we sit as secure and free as <b>a bird under the net</b>\" (i.e. heedless of imminent danger); <i>\"hverken at bide eller brygge\"</i> — \"neither [bread] to bite nor [aught] to brew\" (utterly without provisions).<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Ex Kiøbenhaffuen thendt 3 decembris a:o 99</i>; <b>Old Style</b> (Denmark). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Place-name.</b> <i>Benacsia</i> = <b>Benátky</b>; <i>Elsborige</i> = <b>Älvsborg</b> (the Swedish fortress at the Göta mouth); <i>Halland</i>, <i>Sundet</i> (the <b>Sound</b>).</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Early Modern Danish is rendered securely and the substance (the deferred debt and the 6,000-daler payment for the late brother Peder, with Eske Bille to collect; Duke Charles's cruelty in Finland and the fear for Erik Sparre; the failed Polish expedition to Älvsborg and the stranded squadron at Copenhagen; Jacob Sehested's death and his daughters' marriages) is clear and historically corroborated; graded <b>[B]</b> for the dense Danish news-prose with several incompletely-placed family parties and idioms (the \"bird under the net,\" \"bite nor brew\"), and the loose kinship terms — all flagged rather than smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.168-170 (slice pages 1-3) in full; the Gyldenstierne letter runs pp.168-169 (signed and dated 3 Dec. [15]99), p.170 is the following letter (Michael Vibe) included as buffer. Collated every number, sum, name and date (6,000 dalers, 14 days/fortnight, 8 ships/800 men, 12 ships, Eske Bille, Johan Gyldenstierne, Niels Skram, Hans Lindenov, Jacob Sehested) against the printed Danish text; all match source_text and published_english exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 23 f. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000321002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000321002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for his letter reporting good circumstances with the Emperor, and explains why he cannot promptly repay the money owed at the current Umschlag.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991203001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991204001", "d": "1599-12-04", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Niels Krag", "ck": "Niels Krag", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Niels Krag → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 4 December 1599", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record:<i> Tychonis Brahei et ad eum doctorum virorum epistolae ab anno 1588 </i>(printed source) · MS witness: A = Paris, Bibliothèque Nationale, franç. 13040 (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius)</b>, the Danish royal historian and Tycho's loyal friend, <i>to</i> Tycho. <b>This is the source of the verbatim Duncan-Liddel testimony</b> that Tycho quoted as \"Krag's letter of 4 December\" in <b>brahe-t_16000401001 (to Daniel Cramer, 1 April 1600)</b> and the Duncan dossier <b>brahe-t_15990926001 (to Cramer, 26 September 1599)</b> — see Apparatus.</p>", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO DOMINO, VIRTUTE, ERUDITIONE ET GENERE NOBILISSIMO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE URANIBURGICAe BENACHIAe IN BOHEMIA PRAeSIDI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO OBSERVANDO.<br>S. P. D.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine TYCHO, Domine et amice observande. Verum est quod reversus ex Legatione Anglica binas tuas literas domi repererim, sed quomodo ijs respondere potuerim per tabellarium Witebergensem, mihi non constat. Nam in literis tuis est, te ad festum Pentecostes iturum vocatu Caesaris Pragam. Ego autem, post Dominicam Trinitatis huc appuli; itaque non est quod suspiceris hoc literarum officium intermissum aliquo metu potentiorum, quibus iam pluribus indicijs innotuit mea in amicitia sinceritas et constantia, quam ut novam aliquam offensam mihi pariat. Neque mihi de moribus Anglorum aliquid adhaesit eorum diuturniore convictu et usu, sed tam longe ab ijs vitijs absum, quam te vellem longe abesse a perstringendo et mordendo per iocum et ludum innoxios et fidos tuos amicos: in quorum numero ita me ponas, ut qui nihil unquam passus sim studio et observantiae decedere, etsi fortean non usquequaque tibi officio meo satisfecerim. Res tuas, mi Domine TYCHO, cognovi abunde ex literis tum mihi tum ad ROSAeCRANTIUM datis, magnoque mihi gaudio est eo loco esse, ut tibi tuisque bene sit, parta quiete et invento Maecenate, qui tam munifice tuis studijs et honorifice consultum voluerit. Nec est quisquam eorum, quos tu magnifacis, qui tuae fortunae lingua animoque plus faveat, quam ego, invidiae eo nomine haud expers. Cogor enim aliquando propugnare huius rei veritatem contra eos, qui tuis laudibus nulla unquam a te affecti iniuria obtrectant et tua merita perperam aestimando infra istam fortunam deijciunt. Ac metuebam ab aliquibus male, cum intellexissem huc TYCHONIDEM tuum cum MULAeNO nostro missos ad expetenda instrumenta residua: sed Dei provisu omnes conticuere, nec quisquam contra mutire ausus. Iuvi ego ipsos, quantum in me fuit et res sinebat, auctorque fui, ne literas tuas exhiberent ijs, a quibus sincere auxilium sperari non poterat. Nam aliquando venere quaedam in oblivionem, quae nos ipsos praestat quoque tacita habere, quam dubiam operam aliorum periclitari. Vergit enim quorundam aetas ad extrema, et tamen in sordibus rerum caducarum cogitationes omnes defigimus parum aut nihil de aeternis illis solliciti. Neque dormientes neque vigilantes somniamus aut meditamur aliud quam pecuniam, cui gloriam, honorem et quidquid laudabile est, postponimus. Addimus aliquid de humana severitate (non dicam crudelitate) cuius tristia recens visa in duobus nobilibus et tribus plebeis alijsque exempla. Merito igitur habent quod laetentur, quibus aut favor aulicus hîc affulget, aut res suas ita pararunt, ut migrandi taedium non expavescant.</p><p>Mihi, Deo sit gloria, nihil adversi admodum adhuc contigit. Cancellario probantur, quae a me hac legatione gesta sunt omnia, qui vidit, ad fidem et industriam me nihil reliquum fecisse, sed alijs non item, nescio qua de causa. MULAeNUM nostrum quod provehere ad Medicen promittis, facis ut tua virtute et humanitate dignum, nec puto te habuisse vel habiturum unquam, qui te sinceriore animo colat et observet. Verum metuo valde, ne longior apud nos mora ab Italica profectione per hanc hiemem retinuerit. Sed profecto faciendum ita fuit, nec poterunt citius tua negotia expedire. Causas ipsi tibi reddant, quas veras esse non dubitaveris. Quia vero id iter non successit, alia ratione forte tibi fuerit consilium eius iuvandi invenire. Si animus est, ut illum a te dimissurus unquam omni modo, fiat bona cum gratia et aetati mature consulatur. Omnino, si tibi eum non gratum et obsequentem esse voluissemus, alia fuisset ratione prospectum, et prospicietur, Deo propitio, si eum missum feceris. Sin tibi et tuis placet, habebis eum. Cum voluptate audio TEGNALIO tantum beneficij et honoris a te et per te praestari, optoque, ut deinceps sit, qualem tu esse cupis. TYPOTIUS amicus meus unde habuerit, quod me impulsore patriam deserueris, conijcere non possum. Quicunque hoc comminisci ausus est, nec te novit nec intelligit, quid doloris apportet a fidelissimi amici convictu et mutuo adspectu tanta locum intercapedine disiungi. Sed Dominus TYPOTIUS, si recte memini, ipse a me audivit deplorari abitum tuum, tum patriae, tum mea causa, nedum meo sensu hoc factum credere possit, cui eam suspicionem animo exemptam cupio. De libro publicato, quod ad te scripserat ESCHYLUS BILLEUS, nihil mihi exaudire contigit, nec operae pretium te facturum existimo, si tale aliquod procuderes, qui melius horas tuas collocare potes, et verum est quod addis, multos absque tua voluntate eo te labore exonerare. Quaere a TYCHONIDE, quid filius Magistri IOHANNIS MICHAELIJ in mensa patris narravit. Miraberis, sat scio, iuxta atque nos dolemus. Quid mihi etiam in Anglia et Belgio exprobratum, piget referre. Quod de DUNCANO me scribere vis, fateor, quod res est, me non libenter doctorum controversijs velle immisceri, aut ijs nomen meum inseri. Ac fortean in maiore gloria Hypotheseos istius inventio erit, si simpliciter edito sub tuo nomine scripto illustraveris et explicaveris, quam si contentionibus obscuraveris, quod quidem factum non ignoro in Progymnasmatibus, hoc ipso consilio, ne quis eam sibi deinceps vendicaret; sed vellem hoc peculiari scripto fieri, non ita prolixo, sed quod in scholis proponi posset. Tum vix futurum amplius puto, ut quisquam tuae amplius laudi decerpat. DUNKANUS certe quicquid tandem privatim egerit, publice nunquam eam sibi adrogabit, qui ne per somnium quidem de ea cogitavit, antequam ex te hoc tanquam mysterium cognosceret. Quod simul ac patefecisses, memini a te dictum, plus ei communicatum, quam frater fratri edisserere fortean vellet. Atque reperi in paucis hisce diebus inter schedas meas chartam, qua mihi petenti eam signasti, sive ante DUNKANI adventum, sive quo tempore apud te fuit, incertus sum. Sed mi Domine TYCHO, patere te hoc ab amico moneri, ut polemica omnia seponas, et per quae coepisti, vestigia incedas. Nunquam aliquis magnus contentionibus fuit. Semper aliqui sunt, qui iniquae parti etiam favent, nec quicquam tam solide unquam refutatur, quin adversarius inveniat, quod probabiliter oppugnet, et iudicia hominum ex verbis capiuntur, quia res istae subtiles a paucis intelliguntur. Ac ne per alios quidem hoc fieri vellem, nam ijs etiam respondebitur, ac per eorum latus in te tela dirigentur. Inimici et invidi habebunt ex scommatibus, quod rideant et gaudeant, sicuti iam factum in scurrili quodam scripto URSI novisti. Itaque cum CHRISTIANUS tuus ad te redeat, cuius tibi magnus usus esse poterit, pertexe eam telam quam feliciter orsus es, et cude nobis novum coelum denascente natura, et fatiscente industria hominum. Hac via ad immortalitatem homines grassantur.</p><p>De honoribus publicis quod est in litteris Rosaekrantianis, et feudo Imperij, mea sententia est, quod prius te vellem in possessionem feudi venire, antequam honores illos ambires, aut oblatos acceptares, alias fortean titulus tibi pro re sufficiet, et imputabitur honor pro beneficio reali. Simul ac vero consecutus es feudum promissum, tunc nollem te honores illos aspernari. Liberis tuis ita melius consultum erit, quorum TYCHO tuus ea est aetate, ut e re foret exteros iam visere, et cupit hoc ipse magnopere. Non dubium, quin ille ingenij bonitate et mansuetudine amicos multos sibi parare possit, sed auctoritas et existimatio ex literis et peregrinatione quaerenda ipsi est. De reliquis etiam non dubito te sollicitum esse, non solum ut haereditatem, sed etiam ut nobilitatem et integra omnia status politici relinquas. Haec ita accipe ut profecta ab eo animo, qui tibi tuisque sincere bene vult. Si praesens essem, plura de his; nam per calamum non ita licet omnia, neque fieri potest, ut quae meditata nobis sunt, veniant in mentem universa, cum pennam movemus. CRAGIUS tuus antiquum obtinet, ita et tu ne desinas TYCHO esse, qui fuisti.</p><p>De statu rerum nostrarum pleraque referre TYCHONIDES poterit. Publice nobis pax adhuc est, sed privatim multi male contenti. Variae sunt offensiones multorum de hoc statu rerum, et pauci sunt, qui non male ominantur. Amicitia Anglica ex tenui apta est. Cum CAROLO Sueco neque satis convenit, quia per absentiam Regis naves eius ingressae mare, quod iuris Danici. Apprehenderunt Lubecenses et alios. Adornantur Legati eo mittendi ab reditu PETRI FRANCISCI, quia viam paraturus modo in Sueciam est. Nos arces et oppida in Norvegia et Scania, Hallandia, Blekingia ad fines munimus et reparamus quantum possumus, sed a pecunia non satis instructi sumus. Rex Poloniae misit classem suam cum milite per fretum Oritanum, quae naufragium passa est, nec in Sueciam appulit. CAROLUS iam potitur fere toto regno, postquam hac aestate Finlandiam domuit, et partes illas Livoniae vicinas. De nobilibus captivis in Suecia, qui sunt praecipui ERICUS SPARRE, GUSTAVUS BANNER, ERICUS BANNER, TUROV BIELCKE et GEORGIUS POSE, constans fama supplicium sumendum. Miserunt huc ad Regem petentes eius intercessionem. Exigua spes Polono superest recuperandi regni hereditarij. Non obscurum, quid CAROLUS moliatur, etsi aliud prae se ferat.</p><p>De matre tua TYCHONIDES referre poterit, quae vidit et audivit. Amita valde infirma est, et oculi caligaverunt. Quid vero soror agat, et ubi iam sit, ex eius litteris accipies. Faxit aeternus pater, ut sperato tandem coniugio cum gaudio potiatur. Nos ea audivimus de rebus Domini LANGIJ, quae erigunt animos ad expectationem melioris fortunae. De morte IACOBI SEEFELD puto ab alijs ad te perscriptum. Vix duabus vel tribus septimanis a nuptijs duarum filiarum, quae uno die celebratae, in fine comitiorum insperato diem suum clausit. Serenissimus Rex modo in Iutia est, dicitur tamen CHRISTI natalia Haffniae celebrare velle. Utinam te per idem tempus videre tunc possemus. Posses tu gaudia nostra multis modis augere, sed spero nos te brevi visuros. Nec enim committes, quin ad nos aliquando excurras saltem exiguo comitatu, si non omnes adducas. Desiderio isto valde flagro, quod tu ut expleres, tuo cum bono forte fuerit. Iam si tibi prolixitate satisfeci, gaudeo. Si molestiam et taedium ingessi, ipse in causa es, qui ita requisivisti. Utut sit, rogo omnia pro candore tuo boni consulas, et quod occupatus haec sine cura exaraverim. Vale cum coniuge et liberis felicissime, quibus omnibus ita cupio, ut mihi ipsi bene esse velim. Salutat te uxor mea et frater eius LAURENTIUS et parentes. Item MICHAEL Chirurgus, alijque amici nonnulli, nominatim GABRIEL SPARRE et PETRUS BRAHE.</p><p>Iterum vale.<br>Dabantur Haffniae pridie Nonas Decembres anno 1599.<br>T. T.<br>NIC. CRAGIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent Lord, most noble in virtue, erudition, and birth, Lord Tycho Brahe, Governor of the Uraniborg of Benátky in Bohemia, his Lord and friend to be observed.</b><br>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Magnificent Lord Tycho, Lord and friend to be observed. It is true that, returned from the <b>English Legation</b>, I found your two letters at home; but how I could have answered them by the Wittenberg courier, I do not see. For in your letters it stands that you would go to Prague at the feast of <b>Pentecost</b>, by the Emperor's summons; but I arrived here after <b>Trinity Sunday</b>. So there is no [cause] that you should suspect this duty of letters was omitted from any fear of the more powerful — to whom my sincerity and constancy in friendship is now known by many signs, [too much] for it to breed me some new offence. Nor did anything of the English manners cleave to me from a longer association and use of them; but I am as far from those vices as I should wish you far from <b>carping and biting, through jest and play, your innocent and faithful friends</b> — among whose number place me thus: as one who never let study and observance fail, even if perhaps I have not in all things satisfied you with my service. Your affairs, my Lord Tycho, I learned abundantly from the letters given both to me and to <b>Rosenkrantz</b>; and it is great joy to me that you are in such a place that it may be well with you and yours, <b>peace gained and a Maecenas found, who so munificently and honourably wished your studies provided for</b>. Nor is there any of those you esteem who favours your fortune, with tongue and mind, more than I — not free, on that account, of envy. For I am sometimes forced to <b>defend the truth of this against those who detract from your praises</b> (never harmed by you in any injury), and, wrongly estimating your merits, cast them below that fortune. And I feared ill from some, when I had understood that your <b>Tychonides [Tycho the Younger], with our Mule, were sent here to seek the remaining instruments</b>; but by God's providence all kept silent, and none dared mutter against [it]. I myself helped them, as far as was in me and the matter allowed, and was the author [of advice] that they not show your letters to those from whom sincere help could not be hoped. For sometimes certain things have come into oblivion which it is better for us too to keep silent, than to hazard the doubtful aid of others. For the age of some men inclines to its extremity; and yet we fix all our thoughts in the filth of perishable things, little or not at all anxious about those eternal ones. Neither sleeping nor waking do we dream or meditate anything but money, to which we postpone glory, honour, and whatever is praiseworthy. We add something of human severity (I will not say cruelty), of which <b>sad examples were lately seen in two nobles and three commoners and others</b>. Rightly, then, have they cause to rejoice for whom either court favour shines here, or who have so prepared their affairs that they do not dread the tedium of migrating.</p><p>To me, glory be to God, nothing very adverse has yet befallen. By the Chancellor all that I did in this legation is approved — who saw that I left nothing wanting in fidelity and industry; but not so by others, for I know not what cause. That you promise to advance <b>our Mule to Medicine</b>, you do as worthy of your virtue and humanity; nor do I think you have had, or will ever have, one who cultivates and observes you with a sincerer mind. But I much fear that a longer stay with us has held [him] back from the Italian journey through this winter. But assuredly it had to be done so, nor could they expedite your affairs sooner. Let them render you the causes themselves, which you will not doubt are true. Since, however, that journey has not succeeded, perhaps by another method you will find counsel for helping him. If [your] mind is ever, in any way, to dismiss him from you, let it be done with good grace, and his age provided for maturely. Altogether, had we not wished him to be agreeable and obedient to you, it would have been provided otherwise — and will be provided (God favouring), if you dismiss him. But if he pleases you and yours, you will have him. With pleasure I hear so much benefit and honour rendered to <b>Tengnagel</b> by you and through you, and I wish that hereafter he may be such as you wish. Whence my friend <b>Typotius</b> got [the notion] that you deserted the fatherland at my instigation, I cannot guess. Whoever dared invent this neither knows you, nor understands what grief it brings to be sundered, by so great an interval of place, from the company and mutual sight of a most faithful friend. But Lord Typotius, if I remember rightly, himself heard from me <b>your departure lamented, both for the fatherland's sake and for mine</b> — much less, then, could he believe this done by my [will] — from whom I wish that suspicion removed from his mind. About the published book of which <b>Eske Bille [Eschylus Billeus]</b> had written you, I happened to hear nothing; nor do I think you would do worthwhile to forge any such [reply], you who can better place your hours; and it is true, as you add, that <b>many would relieve you of that labour without your will</b>. Ask <b>Tychonides what the son of Master Johannes Michaelius narrated at his father's table</b>. You will wonder, I know well, even as we grieve. What was reproached to me, too, in England and Belgium [Holland], it irks me to report.</p><p><b>As to what you wish me to write about Duncan: I confess (as is the case) that I do not gladly wish to be mixed into the controversies of the learned, nor my name inserted in them. And perhaps the invention of that Hypothesis will be in greater glory if you simply illustrate and explain it, in a writing published under your name, than if you obscure it with contentions</b> — which indeed I know was done in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, with this very plan, lest anyone afterward claim it for himself; but I should wish this done in a special writing, not so prolix, but such as could be proposed in the schools. Then I think it will scarcely happen further that anyone plucks at your praise. <b><i>Duncan certainly, whatever he may at last have done privately, will never publicly arrogate it [the Hypothesis] to himself — he who did not even in a dream think of it, before he learned this from you as a mystery. Which, as soon as you had revealed [it], I remember was said by you, [that] more was communicated to him than a brother would perhaps wish to expound to a brother. And I have found, in these few days, among my papers, a sheet on which, at my request, you marked it for me — whether before Duncan's arrival, or at the time he was with you, I am uncertain.</b></i> But my Lord Tycho, <b>suffer yourself to be advised this by a friend: set aside all polemics, and walk in the footsteps by which you began</b>. No one was ever great by contentions. There are always some who favour even the unjust side; nor is anything ever so solidly refuted but that the adversary finds something to attack plausibly; and men's judgements are taken from words, because those subtle matters are understood by few. Nor would I wish this done even through others, for they too will be answered, and through their flank weapons will be aimed at you. <b>Enemies and the envious will have, from the gibes, something to laugh and rejoice at — as you have now seen done in a certain scurrilous writing of Ursus.</b> And so, when your <b>Christianus [Longomontanus]</b> returns to you — who can be of great use to you — <b>weave on that web which you have happily begun, and forge us a new heaven, [while] nature is reborn and men's industry grows weary. By this way men advance to immortality.</b></p><p>As to the public honours that are in Rosenkrantz's letters, and the <b>fief of the Empire</b>: my opinion is that I should wish you first to come into possession of the fief, before you sought those honours, or accepted them offered — otherwise perhaps the title will suffice you for the substance, and the honour will be imputed for a real benefit. But as soon as you have obtained the promised fief, then I should not wish you to spurn those honours. Your children will thus be the better provided for — of whom <b>your Tycho [the Younger] is of that age that it would be to the purpose now to visit foreign [lands], and he himself greatly desires this</b>. No doubt he can, by his good nature and gentleness, gain himself many friends; but authority and reputation are to be sought by him from letters and from travel. About the rest, too, I do not doubt you are solicitous — not only to leave an inheritance, but also nobility, and all things of political status, intact. Take these as proceeding from a mind that sincerely wishes well to you and yours. Were I present, [I would say] more of these; for by the pen not everything is so allowed, nor can it happen that what we have meditated all comes to mind, when we move the pen. <b>Your Cragius holds his old [self]; so do you too not cease to be the Tycho you were.</b></p><p>Of the state of our affairs, Tychonides will be able to report most. Publicly we have peace still, but privately many are ill-content. Various are the offences of many about this state of affairs, and few there are who do not ill-omen. <b>The English friendship hangs by a thin [thread].</b> With <b>Charles the Swede</b> it agrees not enough, because in the King's absence his ships entered the sea of Danish right [the Sound], [and] seized Lübeckers and others. Legates are being prepared to be sent thither, on the return of <b>Peter Francis</b>, who is now to prepare the way into Sweden. We fortify and repair the castles and towns in <b>Norway and Skåne, Halland, Blekinge</b>, at the borders, as much as we can, but we are not sufficiently supplied with money. <b>The King of Poland sent his fleet, with soldiers, through the Øresund strait, which suffered shipwreck, and did not reach Sweden.</b> Charles now possesses almost the whole realm, after he subdued <b>Finland</b> this summer, and those parts of <b>Livonia</b> nearby. Of the <b>captive nobles in Sweden</b> — the chief of whom are <b>Erik Sparre, Gustav Banér, Erik Banér, Ture Bielke, and Georg Posse</b> — the constant report is that punishment will be taken [they will be executed]. They have sent hither to the King, seeking his intercession. Little hope remains to the Pole of recovering his hereditary realm. It is not obscure what Charles contrives, though he pretends otherwise.</p><p>Of your <b>mother</b>, Tychonides will be able to report what he saw and heard. [Your] <b>aunt is very infirm, and her eyes have dimmed [gone blind]</b>. But what your <b>sister [Sophie]</b> does, and where she now is, you will learn from her own letters. <b>May the eternal Father grant that she at last enjoy, with joy, the hoped-for marriage. We have heard such things of Lord Lange's [Erik Lange's] affairs as raise [our] spirits to the expectation of a better fortune.</b> Of the death of <b>Jacob Seefeld</b>, I think it has been written you by others: scarcely two or three weeks from the wedding of [his] two daughters (celebrated on one day), at the end of the Diet, he unexpectedly closed his day. <b>The Most Serene King is now in Jutland, but is said to wish to celebrate Christ's nativity at Copenhagen.</b> Would that we could see you at the same time then! You could augment our joys in many ways; but I hope we shall see you soon. For you will not fail to run over to us sometime, at least with a small retinue, if you do not bring all. With that desire I greatly burn — which, for you to fulfil, may perhaps be to your good. Now, if I have satisfied you by [my] prolixity, I rejoice; if I have brought trouble and tedium, you yourself are the cause, who so required [it]. However it be, I ask that you take all in good part, per your candour, and that, [being] busy, I have scratched these without care. Farewell most happily, with wife and children, to all of whom I wish [well] as I would wish well to myself. <b>My wife greets you, and her brother Laurentius, and [her] parents; likewise Michael the Surgeon, and some other friends — by name, Gabriel Sparre and Peter Brahe.</b></p><p>Again, farewell.<br>Given at Copenhagen, the day before the Nones of December, the year 1599.<br>Yours wholly,<br><b>Nic. Cragius.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Niels Krag (Nicolaus Cragius, c. 1550–1602)</b> — the Danish royal historian and diplomat (just back from the <b>English legation</b>), Tycho's steadfast friend — <b>to Tycho</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — the source of the Duncan-Liddel testimony, Krag's counsel, and a Baltic bulletin (a keystone letter).</b></li><li><b>★ The Duncan-Liddel testimony (origin).</b> The passage <i>\"Duncan certainly … did not even in a dream think of [the Hypothesis] before he learned this from you as a mystery … and I have found, among my papers, a sheet on which you marked it for me\"</i> <b>is the original of the verbatim testimony Tycho quoted</b> as \"Krag's letter of 4 December last year\" in <b>brahe-t_16000401001 (to Daniel Cramer, 1 April 1600)</b> and the <b>Duncan dossier brahe-t_15990926001 (to Cramer, 26 September 1599)</b>. Krag declines to enter the public controversy but <b>affirms, for the record, that Duncan Liddel learned the Tychonic world-system from Tycho</b> — and counsels Tycho to publish the system plainly under his own name \"in a special writing … such as could be proposed in the schools.\"</li><li><b>The counsel against polemic (memorable).</b> Krag's wise exhortation: <i>\"Set aside all polemics … No one was ever great by contentions\"</i>; enemies \"will have, from the gibes, something to laugh at, as in <b>Ursus's scurrilous writing</b>.\" Instead, with <b>Longomontanus</b> returned, <i>\"weave on that web you have happily begun, and <b>forge us a new heaven</b> … By this way men advance to immortality.\"</i></li><li><b>The imperial honours/fief advice.</b> Krag counsels Tycho to <b>secure the promised imperial fief <i>before</i> seeking the offered honours</b> (lest \"the title suffice for the substance\"); and notes <b>Tycho the Younger</b> is of an age to travel for \"authority and reputation.\"</li><li><b>The Mule/Medici and Tengnagel matters.</b> Tycho's wish to <b>place Claus Mule with the Medici</b> (delayed by winter); the honours rendered <b>Tengnagel</b>; and the denial (re <b>Jacobus Typotius</b>) that Krag \"instigated\" Tycho's departure — <i>\"I lamented your departure, for the fatherland's sake and mine.\"</i></li><li><b>The Danish/Baltic bulletin.</b> Danish discontent and the <b>thin English friendship</b>; tension with <b>Charles of Sweden</b> (his ships in the Sound seizing Lübeckers); the <b>Polish fleet wrecked in the Øresund</b>; Charles's conquest of <b>Finland and Livonia</b>; and the <b>captive Swedish nobles — Erik Sparre, Gustav Banér, Erik Banér, Ture Bielke, Georg Posse — awaiting execution</b> (the <b>Linköping Bloodbath</b> prisoners; cf. brahe-t_16000919002, where Bacmeister reports the executions of March 1600). The <b>sad domestic executions</b> (\"two nobles and three commoners\") in Denmark.</li><li><b>Family news.</b> Tycho's <b>mother (Beate Bille)</b> and <b>aunt</b> (blind); and — poignantly — <b>the hope for Sophie Brahe's long-deferred marriage to Erik Lange</b>, \"Lord Lange's affairs\" now raising \"expectation of a better fortune\" (cf. Sophie's own letter, brahe-t_15991024001, and brahe-t_16000000002). The death of <b>Jacob Seefeld</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Duncan-Liddel</b> keystone (origin of the testimony in brahe-t_16000401001 and brahe-t_15990926001); the <b>Ursus</b> thread; the <b>Sophie Brahe / Erik Lange</b> thread (brahe-t_15991024001, brahe-t_16000000002); the <b>Linköping prisoners</b> (brahe-t_16000919002); the <b>Mule/Medici</b> and <b>imperial-honours/fief</b> threads.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Niels Krag; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Tycho the Younger</b> (<i>Tychonides</i>); <b>Claus Mule</b> (the Medici post); <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Jacobus Typotius</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b>; <b>Johannes Michaelius</b> &amp; son; <b>Duncan Liddel</b>; <b>Longomontanus</b> (<i>Christianus</i>); <b>Ursus</b>; <b>Charles IX of Sweden</b>; <b>Peter Francis</b> (legate); <b>Sigismund III of Poland</b>; the Linköping prisoners (<b>Erik Sparre, Gustav Banér, Erik Banér, Ture Bielke, Georg Posse</b>); <b>Christian IV</b>; <b>Beate Bille</b> (mother), the <b>aunt</b>, <b>Sophie Brahe</b> &amp; <b>Erik Lange</b>; <b>Jacob Seefeld</b>; Krag's <b>wife</b>, her brother <b>Laurentius</b>, <b>Michael the Surgeon</b>, <b>Gabriel Sparre</b>, <b>Peter Brahe</b>. Places: <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Norway/Skåne/Halland/Blekinge</b>, the <b>Øresund</b>, <b>Sweden/Finland/Livonia</b>, England, Jutland.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Haffniae pridie Nonas Decembres anno 1599</i> — \"the day before the Nones of December\" = <b>4 December 1599 [O.S.]</b> (Denmark Old Style; the date Tycho cites as \"<i>pridie Nonarum Decembris</i>\"). Header, slug, and label agree.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'ad Medicen' ('to Medicine') was mistranslated as 'to the Medici [court]', inventing a Florentine-Medici connection with no support in Dreyer's text (TBOO VIII p.212); corrected to reflect that the passage concerns Mulaenus's Italian medical studies.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (Krag's loyalty after the English legation; the help to Tycho's son fetching the instruments; the <b>Mule/Medici and Tengnagel</b> matters; the <b>Typotius</b> denial; ★ the <b>verbatim Duncan-Liddel testimony</b> — confirmed as the source of Tycho's quotations in the Cramer letters of 1 April 1600 and 26 September 1599; the <b>counsel against polemic</b> and \"forge us a new heaven\"; the <b>imperial-honours/fief</b> advice; the <b>Baltic bulletin</b> with the <b>Linköping prisoners</b>; and the <b>family news</b> with the <b>Sophie Brahe–Erik Lange marriage hope</b>) is firmly construed and cross-corroborated throughout. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.211-215 (page images, not OCR), checking opening/closing address, every proper name, number, date, and sum. All Swedish-noble names (Ericus Sparre, Gustavus Banner, Ericus Banner, Turov Bielcke, Georgius Pose), place names (Scania/Hallandia/Blekingia, fretum Oritanum), the two-nobles/three-commoners figure, and the closing date (pridie Nonas Decembres 1599) match Dreyer and are rendered correctly. One sense-changing mistranslation found: 'ad Medicen' (to a degree in Medicine) rendered as 'to the Medici court', fabricating a courtly/Florentine connection for Mulaenus that is not present in the source.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). \"ad Medicen\" means \"to [a degree in] Medicine\" (context: Mulaenus's planned Italian, i.e. Paduan, medical studies immediately following), not \"to the Medici court\"; the translator's bracketed gloss invents a Florentine-Medici connection with no textual support. At: “our Mule to the Medici [court]”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the full Dreyer slice (TBOO VIII pp.210-216, all 7 pages read as page images). The single MISTRANSLATION defect is confirmed: p.212 shows 'ad Medicen' in a sentence immediately followed by 'metuo valde, ne longior apud nos mora ab Italica profectione... retinuerit', i.e. concern that Mulaenus's stay has delayed his Italian (medical) journey — there is no mention of the Medici family or a Florentine court anywhere in the letter. No other discrepancies were found between current_latin and the Dreyer pages. Nothing remains uncertain.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Paris, Bibliotheque Nationale, franc. 13040. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Niels Krag", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15981205002", "next": "brahe-t_16000324002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15981205002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000324002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains his delay in replying after returning from the English Legation, and rebukes Tycho for carping at innocent friends through jest and play.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991204001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991205001", "d": "1599-12-05", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Mikkel Vibe", "ck": "Mikkel Vibe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Mikkel Vibe → Tycho Brahe — Copenhagen, 5 December 1599 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 25f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLICH OCH WELBYRDIGH MANNDTT THYGE BRAHE THILL KNUDTTSTRUP, HØFFUIDTZMANND OFFUER WRANIEBORGH OCH PAA DETT KEYSERLIGE SLOTT BENACHIA, MYNN GUNSTIG OCH SØNNDERLIG GODE WENN GANNDSCHE WENNLIGEN.</p><p>MYNN ganndsche ydmygh och wennlig helsenn nu och altid forsenndtt med Gud vor herre. Kierre Thyge Brahe, bethacher jeg Ethers Welb. gandsche storligenn for møgitt beuist gode och wenschaff, som j mig och mine giortt och beuist haffuer, huilkett igien att forschylde jeg gierne vill finndis redebonn och willigh.</p><p>Ethers Welb. schriffuelse haffuer jeg entpfanget, huorudaff jeg erfarer, att j och hues ether kiertt er lider well, Gud vere loffuett. Saa haffuer jeg epther ethers schriffuelse hiulpett ethers kierre sønn thill rette, och forschaffed hannom her en skude, som schall føre ethers instrumenter her fran Huenn och thill Lubech. Jeg haffde først fragtett itt schiff der thill med offuerlob paa; samme schipper beuilgett selff att reyse thill Huenn att besee instrumenterne, beretted att verre wmueligtt att faa y nogett stortt schiff for deris storhedts schyld, huorfor jeg endelig motte fragte en aabenn skude, som før er rørtt, med ethers sønns beuilninngh. Nyett aff denne landtz ende haffuer andre ethers wenner well schreffuitt ether thill om. Jeg ønscher Ethers Welb. med alle dj ether kiertt er altidtt att lide vell, dett schall verre mig ganndsche kiertt att høre. Och j hues maade jeg aff min ringe formuffue kand thiene Ethers Fromhed thill gode, skall j altid finnde mig willigh. Will hermed haffue Ethers Welb. med hues ether kiertt er Gud almectigste euindelig befalenndis. :</p><p>Aff Kiøbenhaffnn den 5 decembris a:o 99.<br>E:W: w: t:<br>Michell Vibe<br>egen handt.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Honourable and well-born man Tyge Brahe to Knudstrup, Captain over Uraniborg and at the Imperial castle Benátky [Benachia], my favourable and especial good friend — [written] quite friendlily.</b></p><p>My quite humble and friendly greeting, now and always foresent with God our Lord. Dear Tyge Brahe, I thank Your Well-born[ness] quite greatly for the much-shown good and friendship which you have done and shown to me and mine — which to requite in turn I will gladly be found ready and willing.</p><p>I have received Your Well-born[ness]'s writing, from which I learn that you, and what is dear to you, fare well, God be praised. So, according to your writing, I have helped your dear son to rights, and procured him here a boat [<i>skude</i>], which shall carry your instruments from here, from Hven, to Lübeck. I had first chartered a ship for it, with an upper deck; the same skipper himself agreed to travel to Hven to inspect the instruments, [but] reported it to be impossible to get [them] into any large ship, on account of their size; wherefore I finally had to charter an open boat, as before mentioned, with your son's approval. News from this end of the land your other friends have surely written to you about. I wish Your Well-born[ness], with all that is dear to you, ever to fare well — that shall be quite dear to me to hear. And in whatever way I, of my slight means, can serve Your Piety for good, you shall always find me willing. Herewith [I] will have Your Well-born[ness], with what is dear to you, eternally commended to God Almighty.</p><p>From Copenhagen, the 5th of December, in the year [15]99.<br>Your Well-born[ness]'s willing servant,<br>Michel Vibe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Mikkel Vibe</b> (Michel Vibe, 1561–1624), Copenhagen merchant (later burgomaster); here a practical agent for Tycho in Denmark.</li><li><b>The Hven → Lübeck shipment.</b> A key document in the <b>removal of Tycho's instruments from Hven</b>: at Tycho's instruction, Vibe helped <b>Tycho's son</b> charter a boat (<i>skude</i>) to carry the instruments <b>from Hven to Lübeck</b>. He first hired a <b>decked ship</b> (<i>med overløb</i>) whose skipper went to Hven to inspect them, but found it <b>impossible to fit them into any large ship \"on account of their size,\"</b> so an <b>open boat</b> was chartered, with the son's approval. (The Baltic leg — Hven → Lübeck — complementary to the later Elbe leg, Hamburg/Magdeburg → Leitmeritz → Prague: cf. brahe-t_16000619001, …1009001.)</li><li><b>Titulature.</b> The address still styles Tycho <i>Høvedsmand over Uraniborg</i> (\"Captain/governor over Uraniborg\") <b>and</b> \"at the Imperial castle <b>Benátky</b> [<i>Benachia</i>]\" — registering his new Bohemian seat alongside the old Danish title.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Late-16th-c. Danish: <i>erlich</i> (ærlig), <i>welbyrdigh</i> (velbyrdig), <i>høffuidtzmannd</i> (høvedsmand), <i>helsenn</i> (hilsen), <i>skude</i> (small boat), <i>offuerlob</i> (overløb, deck), <i>skipper</i>, <i>forschylde</i> (forskylde, requite), <i>Fromhed</i> (Piety, an honorific). <i>E:W: w: t:</i> = <i>Eders Velbyrdigheds villige tjener</i> (\"Your Well-born[ness]'s willing servant\"). Reading-verified as <b>Danish</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Kiøbenhaffnn den 5 decembris a:o 99</i> = <b>5 December 1599</b>; Denmark = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely; the sender (Mikkel Vibe), the matter (chartering an open boat to carry Tycho's instruments from Hven to Lübeck, the large decked ship having proved unworkable), and the date are all clear and dovetail with the instrument-transport thread.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.170-171 page images (item no.175). Collated the full Danish letter against published_english, including the boat/deck detail ('offuerlob'/upper deck), the date (5 decembris a:o 99) and the signature. Dreyer's Danish letter signs 'Michell Wibe' (W) where our source_text and Dreyer's own Latin abstract both give 'Vibe' (V); this is an early-modern V/W orthographic variant of the same name, which the brief's own ignore-list ('spelling conventions') excludes from counting as a defect. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 25 f. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports arranging an open boat to transport Tycho's instruments from Hven to Lübeck after a large ship proved impractical due to their size.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991205001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991209001", "d": "1599-12-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Kepler — Benátky nad Jizerou, 9 December 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist, technical-astronomical), with Greek-derived tags and planetary/zodiac glyphs · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 14 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIMO ET<br>PRÆSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO IOANNI<br>KEPLERO, MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, NUNC IN STIRIA VEL AUSTRIA<br>DEGENTI, AMICO SUO IMPRIMIS DILECTO.</p><p>EXOPTATISSIMAS et eruditissimas tuas<br>literas, Vir Doctissime, et ob eximias ingenij tui dotes, licet de facie mihi<br>necdum cognite, plurimum tamen dilecte, accepi præcedenti æstate Wittebergæ, paulo antequam inde discederem, cum<br>in procinctu essem itineris Bohemici. Ideoque tum ijs respondendi nulla dabatur<br>opportunitas, uti neque postea usque in hoc tempus ob rerum mearum<br>vicissitudinem et novum earundem in locis peregrinis apparatum. Nactus autem iam<br>aliquantulum otij unâ cum mittendi ad te meas commoditate, non diutius<br>rescriptione supersedendum arbitratus sum. Quæ ab initio de maleferiato isto<br>URSO tui purgandi causa<br>prolixe commemoras, non equidem tot verbis et tam<br>exquisita declaratione indiguissent, cum ipsemet alias te satis excusatum<br>habeam, nihilque tibi imputem, quod is literas tuas, et eas quidem, uti nunc ex<br>te percipio, mutilatas et depravatas, sui ornandi causa (nam sic forte opus<br>habuit) isti quod evulgavit scurrili et maledico scripto, te inscio et invito,<br>inseruerit. De Hypothesibus meis ab illo præreptis et pro suis venditatis, non<br>tanta esset quæstio, nisi in isto inverecundo et figmentis atque calumnijs<br>scatente libro honorem et existimationem meam, imo patriam, prosapiam, domum<br>honestissimam, impudentissimis et scelestissimis mendacijs, scurrili et vesano<br>calamo arrodere et, quantum in ipso est, defamare, atque talia in publicum typis<br>spargere non erubuisset, cum tamen ipsemet fateatur in præfatione, me bene esse<br>de illo meritum, et adest mihi carmen quoddam ipsius, licet insulsa (uti solet)<br>vena effutitum, propriaque eius manu scriptum, quo mihi gratias agit, quod non<br>saltem per plusculos dies, cum aliquando Uraniburgi apud me esset, cibo et potu illum largiter aluerim,<br>sed et insuper discedenti pecunias dederim. En qualem pro his atque alijs refert<br>gratiam! Occasionem sumpsit horum convitiorum, quod in primo nostro Tomo<br>Epistolarum Astronomicarum extet CHRISTOPHORI ROTHMANNI Mathematici Landgraviani Epistola, in qua<br>URSUM istum impurum vocat<br>nebulonem, qualis enim esset, in aula sui<br>Principis, ipsa experientia iam edoctus, tum quoque relatione<br>aliorum ex ijs locis, ubi antea vixerat. Ego vero in alia quadam ad dictum<br>ROTHMANNUM Epistola, id<br>saltem mimetice refero et simul insinuo, quo dolo quædam ex meis clanculum<br>subtraxerat, quorum nonnulla recepi, quæ etiamnum ipsius manu (quam negare non<br>potest) exarata penes me sunt, et sane si mihi otium a gravioribus studijs tunc<br>superfuisset, Epistolas illas, dum typis nostris excudebantur, quoad singula<br>contenta revidendi, non passus fuissem, librum illum a me editum istius efferi<br>et putidi URSI nomine<br>conspurcari, non quod eiuscemodi adiuncto epitheto indignus censeri possit, aut<br>ulla illi, secundum hominum consueta iudicia loquendo, sic fieret iniuria, prout<br>non saltem ROTHMANNUS, sed<br>etiam multi alij, qui eum satis novere, perspectum habent. Verum quia non<br>libenter in meis scriptis ullius vitam et mores, qualescunque sint, perstringo,<br>unde etiam in Epistola Magnifici Domini IACOBI<br>CURTIJ optimæ memoriæ postea Wandesburgi a me Libro de Mechanica Astronomia subiuncta, nomen<br>istius omisi, utut dictus Dominus IACOBUS<br>CURTIUS eum diserte appellaret et plagiarium meum insuper<br>intitularet. Sed accidit, ut dum studiosis quibusdam ex meis hunc laborem<br>corrigendi operas typographicas in isto Epistolarum libro concredidissem, atque<br>illis originalia, prout manuscripta erant, tradidissem, illi nihil immutandum<br>duxerint, neque me magis serijs distentum interpellare ausi sint, ideoque omnia,<br>prout autographa habebant, imprimi permiserint,<br>sicque incuria quadam factum est, quod ipsius nomen cum epithetis appositis<br>isthic retentum sit. An vero hæc sufficiens et iusta satis sit causa, tam supra<br>omnem modum vesana malitia, in me et meos, adeoque patriam ipsam debacchandi,<br>brevi dispiciemus. Potuisset equidem de iniuria, si qua foret sibi illata,<br>conqueri, atque hanc licitis medijs amoliri, non autem ex effræni vindictæ<br>cupiditate, tam impotenti animo furere, omniaque longe deteriora reddere. Neque<br>tamen est cur existimes, quod in istum nugatorem sim<br>commotior, quam meæ conditiones admittant, ut tu (candido tamen animo) infers,<br>non enim Cæsaris ira (uti quidam etiam<br>ad me perscripsit) dignum arbitror. At quoniam contra ius et fas, contraque<br>laudatissimas Imperij Constitutiones me scripto plus quam famoso præter omnem<br>veritatem et meritum meum dehonestare perfricta fronte conatus est, iure cum<br>ipso agi curabo, atque secundum leges reprimi et castigari. Subduxit is se<br>Praga in Silesiam paulo post meum isthuc accessum, sed nuper, uti<br>rescivi, tacite redijt, forte quod ibi me non amplius esse intellexerat, at quam<br>longæ mihi sint manus, etiamsi præsto non sim, brevi in Aresto detentus, et<br>postea in iudicium protractus sentiet. Quoniam vero intelligo ex tuis, te<br>spurcas et scurriles istas chartas non vidisse, mitto exemplar, ut et de tua<br>Epistola isthic promptius dispicias, ac cætera, qualia sint, æstimes. Videbis<br>utique quam insana, maledica, conviciosa et fœtida sit compilatio, quamque suum<br>authorem apprime redoleat; impudentiores calumnias nunquam forte legisti.<br>Abhorrent ab ijs aures omnium cordatorum et proborum.</p><p>Verum de isto effero URSO Dithmarsico plus satis, neque enim tot a te vel me prolatis<br>verbis dignus est, et si ipsum intus et in cute (ut aiunt) nosses (non iam<br>loquor de Scabie Gallica, qua ob vitæ impuritatem scatet) idem mecum testareris,<br>imo iamdudum testatus fuisses. Unicum saltem addam, Hypotheses nostras asserendi<br>causa, licet hoc minimum sit, et longe plura validioraque in promptu habeam<br>argumenta. Hypotheses illas anno 1584 a me primum excogitatas, quas iste sibi<br>arrogare non veretur, ipsius revera inventum esse, quis crediderit, qui eum<br>ullis observationibus cœlestibus, unde novæ Hypotheses extruendæ, nunquam<br>assuefactum fuisse novit? Imo si unius Planetæ, præsertim e tribus superioribus<br>apparentem locum per hanc neotericam suppositionem, secundum omnes<br>particularitates demonstrare nequierit (uti vix poterit) cui quæso intelligenti,<br>suum esse inventum, fidem faciet? Sed non ille saltem, verum et alij, dum ego<br>mea premo atque in lucem edere cunctor, hanc excogitationem sibi attribuere ausi<br>sunt. Inter quos est Scotus quidam, DUNCANUS nomine, Helmstadij Medicinæ et Mathematum Professor, qui cum hanc<br>rationem a me in Dania accepisset, eam<br>primum Rostochij privatim suis discipulis<br>dictavit, nec meam esse inficiatus est, sed<br>Tychonicam ultro appellavit, quod in propinquo essem et me, si aliter faceret,<br>id facile resciscere posse vereretur. Clam tamen ijs, quibus maxime fidebat<br>discipulis, in aurem susurrabat, se quidem honoris causa eam hypothesin mihi<br>ascribere, ut plus gratiæ et authoritatis habere possit apud auditores suos,<br>revera tamen suam ipsius esse, quod et postea Helmstadij remotior a me factus, audentius et inverecundius<br>commisit, partim in publicis suis prælectionibus, partim intimationibus<br>quibusdam. Sed ista fraus et impostura cum ab alijs, tum præsertim Domino<br>Doctore DANIELE CRAMERO apud<br>Stetinenses Verbi ministro, Viro<br>singulariter erudito, qui Scoti istius Rostochij olim auditor erat inter præcipuos unus, detecta est,<br>et suo forte tempore plenius detegetur. Si otium superfuerit, curabo exemplum<br>literarum eius ad Nobilissimum Virum OLIGERUM ROSÆKRANTZIUM meum consanguineum, cuius Pædagogum<br>dictus CRAMERUS tum agebat, hac de<br>re in Daniam post meum discessum<br>scriptarum, hisce apponi. Sic quoque RŒSLINUS, licet magis astute et caute, mea pro suis venditare<br>præsumpsit, ita tamen, ut mihi hanc speculationem non derogarit, sed se eam,<br>postquam similia adinvenisset, in libro meo de Cometa anni 77ti reperisse fateatur. Egregius sane sucus, si fidem mereri posset,<br>nam liber ille meus, cuius meminit, per 9 annos antea impressus erat et plurimis<br>de hac a me introducta cœlestium revolutionum dispositione iam innotuerat. At si<br>nihil aliud, id saltem et quam maxime fraudem prodit, quod dum hanc Hypothesin,<br>ut suus fieret partus, in nonnullis aliter formare conatus sit, eamque, si Dijs<br>placet, emendare, in enormes absurditates impegerit, ipsam symmetriam ac<br>proportionem Revolutionum interturbando, atque ab ipsis apparentijs alienissimam<br>statuendo; ut inde satis liqueat, eum non saltem has hypotheses nequaquam<br>intelligere, nedum ut earum author sit, sed ne quidem ullas alias, imo<br>Astronomiæ penetralia necdum introspexisse.</p><p>MAGINUM<br>Italum nunquam existimarim tale quid affectasse. Quod si fecit, literis propria<br>manu ad me datis, quibus se illam Hypothesin ex libro nostro secundo de Æthereis<br>Phænomenis primum perspexisse, sibique apprime placere,<br>modo Martis orbis Solarem transire posset (putavit enim ex vulgata sententia<br>reales esse in cœlo orbes) fassus est, contraria admisit. A MAGINO vero, si qui alij huius<br>speculationis conscij sunt, hac occasione habuerunt, inter quos etiam est<br>FRANCISCUS PATRITIUS, qui<br>eius mentionem in erudito alias de nova Philosophia Volumine facit, is quidem<br>meam esse expresse asserit, sed eam ita pervertit, et per ignorantiam male<br>accomodat, et contra mea ipsius postulata et assumpta eam coinquinat et<br>insectatur, ut plurima isthic ex ipsius relatione apposita pro meis non<br>agnoscam, quod etiam publice protestabor. Sed de<br>Hypothesi hac vel alia non multum alterabimur, quæstio est: quis eam rationem<br>invenire possit, tam in quantitatibus et motionibus continuis, quam discretis,<br>ut phænomena cœlestia per omnia fatentur.</p><p>Hoc opus, hic labor est, et sic spectemur agendo.</p><p>Porro siquidem ab initio innuisti, te a MÆSTLINO primum tibi a me inscriptas accepisse, qui etiam, quas<br>ad illum dederam, tecum communicavit, miror ipsum nec illis neque alijs, quas<br>prius, imo ante aliquot annos miseram, quippiam tanto tempore respondere<br>voluisse. An hoc sit viri ingenui et Mathematicis disciplinis solerter imbuti,<br>tute æstima. Non est alias mihi moris, ignotos literis prævenire, sed utplurimum<br>ab illis præveniri soleo, nedum ut ad meas tanto temporum tractu responsionem<br>nullam recipiam. Sed imposterum ei, quem plurimi semper feci, uti scripta mea in<br>publicum edita testari poterint, meis ingratis, uti video, literis non ero<br>molestus. Placent tibi quæ ad illum et te de re Astronomica scripsi. Atqui ea se<br>sic omnino habere, certo exploratum habeo, multoque plura et his maiora adhuc<br>abstrusa esse, quæ antecessorum, tam veterum quam recentiorum, diligentiam<br>eluserunt.</p><p>Suades, imo multis rationibus, quas ego non improbo, persuadere<br>conaris, ut Observationes meas cœlestes publici iuris faciam, quod quidem suo<br>tempore præstare non detrectabo, cum his, quæ tu affatim adducis, tum alijs de<br>causis. Verum tam cito id efficere, antequam pleraque ex ijs, quæ a me in<br>Astronomicis instaurata sunt, atque selectoribus ex ijsdem observationibus<br>fundata, in lucem prodierint, non consultum duco, nam præter alia, hoc ipsum,<br>quod tu de hypothesibus meis mihi præreptis et de CYPRIANI Ephemeridibus, alijs<br>quoad partem attributis et depravatis, refers, terret atque dissuadet.<br>Obtinebitis tamen aliquando, favente cœlesti Numine, ea, quæ a me plurimis annis<br>in cœlestibus denotata sunt, non quidem tam manca et mutila, ne dicam incerta,<br>prout ea sunt, quæ pauculis chartis mandavit WERNERUS, vel potius IOHANNES SCHŒNERUS (nam WERNERI observata nulla vidi, tu, si<br>quæ habes, communica) a REGIOMONTANO et GUALTHERO relicta, sed exacta sibique constantia atque in tanta<br>simul copia, ut vix magno quodam volumine constringi queant. Adiungam præter<br>librum Epistolarum, de quo supra, Mechanica nostra, huc spectantia, quæ<br>instrumenta, quibus ultra 20 annos usus sum, complectuntur, si commode tibi<br>Praga adferri poterint. Ex Epistolis<br>præter alia prius insinuata perspicies, an eæ tam parvi momenti atque inutiles<br>sint, veluti præ animi impotentia occæcatus ille censor vult, et an laudatissimæ<br>memoriæ Landgravio WILHELMO quidpiam derogent (veluti idem impudenter, ut Filio eius<br>me invisum redderet, commentus est). Ex Mechanicis colliges, quanto molimine<br>Astronomiæ redintegrationem aggressus sim, quantaque in his usus diligentia,<br>quantisque impensis. Invenies etiam ibidem circa posteriorem partem<br>consignationem eorum, quæ hactenus in hoc genere studiorum exanthlavi, quæque<br>inposterum, aspirante cœlestium Opifice, pertexenda restant, tum quoque literas<br>illas laudatæ memoriæ Domini IACOBI<br>CURTIJ et MAGINI, quarum antea mentionem feci. Misissem illuminatum (uti<br>vocant) exemplar, quo promptius omnia discerneres, sed nunc non suppetit.</p><p>De libro tuo, uti is cum suo authore meretur, semper, ex quo mihi introspectus est, honorifice sensi, nec te aut alios,<br>quocunque modo de hisce sublimibus et a vulgi captu remotis disciplinis præclare<br>merentes, et veritatis nucleum serio affectantes laude sua privavi aut privabo<br>unquam, quin potius extuli, et in posterum extollam. Hoc præcipuum est, quod in<br>tuo ingenioso illo scripto cum plerisque tamen alijs errore communi admittis,<br>non probo, te realitatem quandam orbibus cœlestibus inesse statuere, quo sic<br>promptius Coperniceis<br>imaginationibus viam struas, eisque facilius subscribas. Motiones cœlestes<br>Symmetriam quandam observare, et rationes subesse, cur circa hæc vel alia centra<br>a terris vel Sole dispariter remota convolvantur, non negarim, sed harmonia et<br>dispositionum analogia ex posteriori, ubi motus et motuum occasiones ad amussim<br>constiterint, non autem ex priori, uti et tu et MÆSTLINUS voluistis, petenda erit;<br>quin et hîc difficillime invenienda. Quam operam si quis superaddiderit, illum<br>antiquum PYTHAGORAM, qui harmoniam<br>concinnam cœlestibus, imo toti mundo inesse præsentiebat, superare dixerim. Et<br>licet circulares in cœlo motiones nonnunquam angulares quasdam et varias<br>figuras, atque ut plurimum oblongas composito suo motu efficere, curiose singula<br>trutinanti, videri possint: fit tamen id per accidens et abhorret ratio ab hac<br>suppositione. Oportet enim omnino ex circularibus componi astrorum circuitus,<br>alias perpetuo uniformiter et æquabiliter in se non revertentur et perennitatem<br>frustrabunt, præterquam quod minus simplices, magisque anomalæ erant, nec etiam<br>doctrinæ et usui accommodæ. De ☿ non est cur doleas, eius stellam a me<br>non esse observatam, aut quod idem cum COPERNICO in eius Phænomenis ostendendis prætendere velim. Licet<br>enim a Solis radijs rarissime digrediatur et inclinatior apud nos in Dania sit sphæra, quam Frueburgi in Prussia, ubi<br>vixit COPERNICUS, pluresque<br>mare circa Insulam habitationis nostræ<br>emiserit vapores, quam Vistula: plurimis<br>tamen vicibus, imo pene singulis annis, dum in Uraniburgo nostro sideribus invigilarem, tam Eous quam<br>vespertinus, accurate a nobis cœlitus denotatus: ut neque huius astri, utut rarissime pateat et multis anfractibus sit obnoxium,<br>nos fugere possit semita, nequaquam ab aliorum dubijs animadversionibus, uti<br>coactus est facere COPERNICUS, mutuanda. De ♀ quantum hactenus pervestigare<br>licuit, certum est, eius Apogæum non multum a Solari antrorsum removeri, aliter<br>quam sensit COPERNICUS, nec<br>eidem tamen plane uniri, ut ALBATEGNIJ imitatione volunt Alphonsinæ. In Eccentricitate tamen eius non est par ratio, nam<br>eam multo minorem Solari deprehendi. An mutabilis sit,<br>nondum dixerim, sed non magnopere id probabit vel improbabit tua per se<br>ingeniosa ratiocinia.</p><p>E stella Polari vel quavis alia subtiliter eruere, an orbis annuus<br>Terræ a COPERNICO<br>excogitatus, si is esset, octavæ sphæræ stellis aliquantulam insinuet<br>Parallaxin, iam ante sæpius et diligenter experiri tentavi: verum nihil<br>eiuscemodi, diligenti facta animadversione, hactenus comperi. Nam stellam<br>polarem, quocunque anni tempore supra vel infra polum præcise observatam,<br>adhibita eiusdem ab illo remotione vera (quæ hoc anno est 2° 51' 1O'', et<br>cum 6° 13' 26'' ♈ et ♎ Meridianum supra et infra Polum<br>transit) eandem in dimidio scrupulo, imo intra quartam vel sextam eius partem<br>(subtiliora visus non discernit) præbuisse poli sublimitatem animadverti, quod<br>et CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO,<br>qui Coperniceæ Speculationi indulgens aliquid discriminis hîc subesse opinatus<br>est, iamdudum indicavi, ut hinc nihil pro COPERNICO tuo (cuius concinnam et<br>ingeniosam speculationem alias admirari et deprædicare soleo) tuendo, residuum<br>sit solatij. Petas aliunde, si dabitur.</p><p>Sed de his atque alijs plenius tecum iucunde et perlubenter agam,<br>pluraque ex meis communicabo, si me, uti polliceris, aliquando inviseris, quod<br>tibi iam minus molestum erit quam antea, cum in Bohemia non admodum a vobis remota, nunc sedes Uraniæ novas<br>fixerim, atque in arce Cæsariana Benachia<br>quinque milliaribus Praga remota habitem. Nam<br>me a Cæsarea Maiestate, huc e<br>Germania clementissime vocatum, et<br>benignissime ac liberaliter receptum esse, te ab alijs iam antea cognovisse (ex<br>quo dimidium iam annum hîc subsistam) existimo. Nolim tamen ut fortunæ asperitas<br>te nos accedere compellat, sed potius arbitrium proprium et erga communia studia<br>amor et affectio. Quicquid tamen id erit, invenies me non fortunæ, qualiscunque<br>illa sit, sed tuum amicum, qui etiam in adversa sorte suo consilio et auxilio<br>tibi nequaquam sit defuturus, sed potius te ad optima quæque promoturus. Et si<br>mature accesseris, eas forte rationes inveniemus, quibus tibi et tuis inposterum<br>rectius quam antea consultum erit. Vale. Quando rescripseris, literas ad Dominum<br>Doctorem BLOTIUM, Viennam, qui Cæsaris Bibliothecæ isthic præest, mitte: is eas facile<br>Pragam, unde me mox invenient, procurabit.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale.</p><p>Dabantur Benachiæ sive Venetijs<br>Bohemorum die 9 Decembris Stylo renovato 1599.</p><p>Tui Amantissimus<br>TYCHO<br>BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.SI se occasio commoda obtulerit, rogo ut MÆSTLINUM meo nomine moneas de Eclipsibus ab illo observatis,<br>cum meis conferendis, ob Meridianorum differentiam eruendam. Dum enim relego<br>exemplum literarum ad MÆSTLINUM,<br>video me isthic mentionem fecisse Eclipsis Solis, quæ die 25 Februarij Anni 99  contigit, ubi nescio qua incuria digiti Ecliptici denotantur 10¾: forte<br>quod per aliquem ex studiosis meis, calculum nimis festinanter exequentem, sic<br>perperam reperti. Cum verior ratio ex meis Tabulis, prout ipsemet postea<br>disquisivi, præbeat digitos 9½, qua etiam quantitate Sol deficere a quodam ex<br>meis Uraniburgi isthic (ut 4 residuorum<br>instrumentorum, quæ præ magnitudine efferri tam cito nequibant, curam haberet)<br>relicto studioso deprehensus est, et tempora alias per armillam Æquatoriam 18<br>pedum in Diametro, a principio usque ad finem (fuit enim toto isthic tempore<br>serenum) meæ restitutioni apprime congruentia cœlitus denotata sunt, quæ erant<br>eiuscemodi, ut deliquium inciperet isthic H. 10 M. 10, finem haberet H. 12 M.<br>19, ideoque medium existeret H. 11 M. 12. Hæc ad MÆSTLINUM, quando illi scripseris,<br>referre poteris, ipsumque rogare atque efficere, ut ab ipso Eclipses tam Lunares<br>quam Solares, quotquot hactenus observavit, per te, siquidem ille mihi<br>rescribere nolit, obtineam.</p><p>Reperi inter chartas meas exemplum literarum CRAMERI, de quo prius memini, iamdudum<br>nescio in quos usus descriptum, quod hisce addo, et simul exemplum Epistolæ<br>cuiusdam M. IOHANNIS<br>CHRISTIERNI Dani, nunc Lugduni Batavorum studiorum causa<br>degentis, qui mihi erat domesticus, cum liber noster de Cometa anni 77<br>Uraniburgi imprimeretur, et paulo<br>post quam URSUS iste apud me<br>fuerat, accessit, ideoque quam fraudulenter egerat, ex cæteris meis studiosis<br>cognitum habuit. Addidi etiam testimonium Scribæ Nobilissimi Viri ERICI LANGIJ, cui URSUS tunc inservijt, qui etiam præsto<br>erat, cum iste suum herum comitatus mecum fuit. Quam testificationem ipsemet<br>ERICUS postea Magdeburgi in præsentia publici Notarij<br>confirmavit, et quæcunque alias URSUS in famoso isto scripto habet, quatenus sibi ea de re<br>quippiam constabat, falsissima esse testatus est, erecto de duodecim eiuscemodi<br>punctis, quæ illi proponebantur, e libro illo famoso excerptis, publico, uti<br>vocant, Instrumento, quod alias videbis; hinc cætera æstimabis.</p><p>T. B.</p><p>Ne vacaret sequens Paginæ facies,<br>ascribi feci tria Epigrammata ex ijs, quæ a me, postquam Benachiam veni, lusa sunt. Plura carmina a varijs,<br>postquam e Dania in Germaniam migravi, concinnata, cum Domino Doctore<br>BLOTIO veteri amicitia mihi<br>iuncto communicavi, ipsumque ut te eorundem participem redderet, rogavi, quæ si<br>acceperis, plura inde percipies, et si qui sunt apud vos Poëtæ arguti, uti<br>procul dubio sunt, fac ut numerum adaugeant.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most learned and excellent man, Master Johannes Kepler, distinguished mathematician, now living in Styria or Austria, his especially beloved friend.</b></p><p>Your most longed-for and most learned letter, most learned Sir — and, for the surpassing gifts of your talent, most dear to me, though not yet known to me by face — I received last summer at Wittenberg, a little before I departed thence, when I was on the point of the Bohemian journey. And so no opportunity was then given of answering it, nor afterwards until this time, on account of the vicissitude of my affairs and the new setting-up of them in foreign places. But having now gained a little leisure, together with the convenience of sending a letter to you, I judged that I should put off writing back no longer. What at the outset you recount at length, to clear yourself concerning that mischievous Ursus, did not indeed need so many words and so elaborate an explanation, since I otherwise hold you sufficiently excused, and impute nothing to you in that he inserted your letter — and that too, as I now learn from you, mutilated and corrupted — for the sake of adorning himself (for so perhaps he had need) into that scurrilous and slanderous writing which he published, without your knowledge and against your will. Concerning my Hypotheses snatched away by him and vended as his own, there would not be so great a question, had he not, in that shameless book teeming with fabrications and calumnies, blushed not to gnaw — with a scurrilous and frenzied pen, and, so far as in him lies, to defame — my honour and reputation, nay, my fatherland, my lineage, my most honourable house, with the most impudent and wicked lies, and to scatter such things abroad in print; even though he himself confesses in the preface that I had deserved well of him, and I have a certain poem of his — though poured out (as is his way) from a tasteless vein, and written in his own hand — in which he thanks me that, when he was once at Uraniborg with me, I not only fed him generously with food and drink for several days, but also gave him money on his departure. See what thanks he returns for these and other kindnesses! He took occasion for these abuses from the fact that in the first volume of our <i>Astronomical Letters</i> there stands a letter of Christoph Rothmann, the Landgrave's mathematician, in which he calls that impure Ursus a scoundrel — for what sort of man he was, Rothmann had been taught by experience itself at his Prince's court, and also by the report of others from the places where Ursus had formerly lived. I, however, in a certain other letter to the said Rothmann, merely report it by way of mimicry, and at the same time hint by what guile he had secretly filched certain things of mine — some of which I recovered, and which are still in my keeping, written in his own hand (which he cannot deny). And indeed, had I then had leisure to spare from weightier studies to review those Letters, as to each particular content, while they were being printed at our press, I should not have suffered the book published by me to be defiled with the name of that savage and rank Ursus — not that he could be reckoned unworthy of such an attached epithet, or that any wrong would thus be done him, speaking by men's customary judgments (as not only Rothmann, but also many others who know him well, are aware). But because I do not willingly, in my writings, censure anyone's life and morals, whatever they be — whence also, in the letter of the Magnificent Lord Jacob Curtius of best memory, afterwards appended by me at Wandsbek to the book on the Mechanics of Astronomy, I omitted his name, although the said Lord Jacob Curtius expressly named him and moreover entitled him my plagiarist. But it happened that, when I had entrusted to certain of my students the labour of correcting the printer's proofs in that book of Letters, and had handed them the originals as they were in manuscript, they thought nothing should be changed, nor dared to interrupt me while I was taken up with more serious matters, and so allowed everything to be printed as they had it in the autographs; and thus by a certain carelessness it came about that his name, with the appended epithets, was retained there. But whether this be a sufficient and just enough cause for raging with such measureless mad malice against me and mine — and indeed against my fatherland itself — we shall shortly see. He could have complained of the wrong, if any had been done him, and removed it by lawful means; not raged with so ungoverned a mind, out of an unbridled lust for revenge, and made everything far worse. Yet there is no reason for you to suppose that I am more stirred against that trifler than my condition allows — as you infer (with candid mind, however) — for I do not judge him worthy of the Emperor's wrath (as someone also wrote to me). But since, against right and divine law, and against the most praiseworthy Constitutions of the Empire, he has with brazen face attempted to dishonour me by a more-than-libellous writing, beyond all truth and my desert, I will see to it that he be proceeded against at law, and repressed and chastised according to the laws. He withdrew from Prague into Silesia a little after my arrival there, but lately, as I learned, he returned secretly — perhaps because he understood that I was no longer there; but how long my hands are, even if I be not at hand, he will shortly feel: detained under arrest, and afterwards dragged into court. And since I understand from your letter that you have not seen those filthy and scurrilous papers, I send a copy, that you may both more readily make out your own letter there, and judge the rest, of what kind they are. You will assuredly see how insane, slanderous, abusive, and fetid the compilation is, and how thoroughly it reeks of its author; more impudent calumnies you have perhaps never read. The ears of all sensible and upright men shrink from them.</p><p>But enough, and more than enough, of that savage Ditmarsh Ursus; for he is not worthy of so many words uttered by you or me; and if you knew him \"inside and under the skin\" (as they say) — I do not now speak of the French scab, with which he teems on account of the impurity of his life — you would testify the same with me, nay, would long since have testified it. Let me add just one thing for the sake of asserting our Hypotheses, though this be the least of it, and I have far more and stronger arguments at hand. Who would believe that those Hypotheses, first devised by me in the year 1584, which he does not fear to arrogate to himself, are really his invention — knowing that he was never practised in any celestial observations, from which new Hypotheses are to be built up? Nay, if he cannot demonstrate, according to all particulars, the apparent place of even one planet — especially of the three superior — by this newfangled supposition (as he scarcely will be able), to what intelligent man, I ask, will he prove that the invention is his own? But not he alone — others too, while I keep my own work back and delay to bring it to light, have dared to attribute this device to themselves. Among whom is a certain Scot, Duncan by name, Professor of Medicine and of the Mathematics at Helmstedt, who, having received this method from me in Denmark, first dictated it privately to his students at Rostock, and did not deny it was mine, but of his own accord called it \"Tychonic\" — because I was nearby, and he feared that, if he did otherwise, I could easily find it out. Yet secretly, to those students he most trusted, he whispered in the ear that he ascribed that hypothesis to me only for honour's sake, that he might have more favour and authority with his hearers, but that it was really his own; which afterwards, having become more remote from me at Helmstedt, he did more boldly and shamelessly, partly in his public lectures, partly in certain printed notices. But this fraud and imposture has been detected — both by others and especially by Doctor Daniel Cramer, minister of the Word among the people of Stettin, a singularly learned man, who was once one of the chief auditors of that Scot at Rostock; and in its own time it will perhaps be more fully exposed. If leisure remains, I will see to it that a copy of his letter — written about this matter to the most noble Holger Rosenkrantz, my kinsman (whose tutor the said Cramer then was), and sent into Denmark after my departure — be appended to these. So too Roeslin, though more cunningly and cautiously, presumed to vend my [hypotheses] as his own — yet in such a way that he did not deprive me of this speculation, but confesses that he found it, after he had himself devised similar things, in my book on the Comet of the year [15]77. A fine bit of juice indeed, if it could earn belief — for that book of mine which he mentions had been printed nine years before, and the arrangement of the celestial revolutions introduced by me had already become known to very many. But if nothing else, this above all betrays the fraud: that while he tried to form this Hypothesis somewhat differently, so that it might become his own offspring, and to \"emend\" it (if it please the gods), he ran into enormous absurdities — disturbing the very symmetry and proportion of the Revolutions, and setting it up most alien to the appearances themselves; so that it is thence sufficiently clear that he not only by no means understands these hypotheses (let alone is their author), but not even any others — nay, has not yet looked into the inner sanctum of Astronomy.</p><p>I should never have thought that the Italian Magini affected any such thing. But if he did, he admitted the contrary in a letter given to me in his own hand, in which he confessed that he had first perceived that Hypothesis from our second book on the Aetherial Phenomena, and that it greatly pleased him — provided the orb of Mars could cross the Solar [orb] (for he thought, on the common opinion, that the orbs in heaven are real). And from Magini, if any others are privy to this speculation, they had it on this occasion — among whom too is Francesco Patrizi, who makes mention of it in his otherwise learned volume on the New Philosophy. He indeed expressly asserts it to be mine, but so perverts it, and through ignorance misapplies it, and against my own postulates and assumptions defiles and worries it, that I do not acknowledge as mine very many things there set down on his report — which I shall also publicly protest. But about this Hypothesis or another we shall not be much exercised; the question is: who can find that method — both in continuous quantities and motions, and in discrete ones — such that the celestial phenomena answer to it in every respect.</p><p><i>This is the work, this the toil — and so let us be judged by our doing.</i></p><p>Further, since at the outset you hinted that you first received from Maestlin those [letters] addressed by me to you, who also shared with you those I had sent to him, I marvel that he was willing to answer nothing in so long a time — neither to those nor to others which I had sent earlier, indeed some years ago. Whether this be [the act] of an honest man, skilfully imbued with the mathematical disciplines, judge for yourself. It is not otherwise my custom to anticipate strangers with letters, but for the most part I am wont to be anticipated by them — let alone that I should receive no reply to mine over so long a stretch of time. But henceforth I will not trouble with my letters — ungratefully received, as I see — him whom I have always esteemed most highly, as my published writings can testify. You are pleased with what I wrote to him and to you on astronomical matters. And indeed I hold it for certain that these things are altogether so, and that many more and greater than these still lie hidden, which eluded the diligence of our predecessors, both ancient and modern.</p><p>You urge — nay, with many reasons, which I do not disapprove, you try to persuade — that I make my celestial Observations public, which in its own time I will not refuse to do, both for the reasons you abundantly adduce and for others. But to do it so soon, before most of what has been restored by me in astronomy, and founded on the choicer of those observations, has come to light, I do not think advisable; for, besides other things, this very fact which you report — about my hypotheses snatched from me, and about Cyprian's Ephemerides, in part attributed to others and corrupted — frightens and dissuades me. Yet you shall obtain at length, by the favour of the heavenly Deity, what I have noted in the heavens over very many years — not indeed so defective and mutilated, not to say uncertain, as are those which Werner (or rather Johannes Schöner) committed to a few pages — left by Regiomontanus and Walther (for I have seen no observations of Werner; you, if you have any, share them) — but exact and self-consistent, and at the same time in such abundance that they could scarcely be confined in one great volume. Besides the book of Letters, of which above, I will add our <i>Mechanics</i>, pertinent here, which comprise the instruments I have used for over 20 years, if they can conveniently be brought to you from Prague. From the Letters, besides other things hinted before, you will perceive whether they are of such small moment and useless, as that censor — blinded by impotence of mind — would have it, and whether they derogate anything from the Landgrave Wilhelm of most praiseworthy memory (as the same man impudently fabricated, to render me hateful to his Son). From the <i>Mechanics</i> you will gather with how great an effort I undertook the renewal of astronomy, and with how great diligence and how great expense I employed in these things. You will also find there, toward the latter part, a record of what I have hitherto drained off in this kind of studies, and what remains hereafter to be woven through, with the Maker of the heavens favouring; and likewise those letters of the Lord Jacob Curtius of praised memory and of Magini, which I mentioned before. I would have sent an \"illuminated\" (as they call it) copy, that you might more readily discern everything, but it is not now to hand.</p><p>Of your book, as it deserves along with its author, I have always thought honourably, ever since I looked into it; nor have I ever deprived — nor will I ever deprive — you or others who in any way deserve nobly of these sublime disciplines, remote from the grasp of the crowd, and who seriously aim at the kernel of truth, of their due praise; but rather I have exalted them, and shall exalt them hereafter. This is the chief thing which, in that ingenious writing of yours — though you admit it with most others by a common error — I do not approve: that you lay down a certain reality to be in the celestial orbs, so as the more readily to build a way to the Copernican imaginations and the more easily to subscribe to them. That the celestial motions observe a certain symmetry, and that there are reasons underlying why they revolve about these or other centres differently distant from the earth or the Sun, I would not deny; but the harmony and the analogy of the arrangements is to be sought <i>a posteriori</i>, once the motions and the occasions of the motions have been exactly established — not <i>a priori</i>, as both you and Maestlin wished; nay, even so it is most difficult to find. And if anyone should add this labour, I should say that he surpasses the ancient Pythagoras, who divined that a fitting harmony was present in the heavens — nay, in the whole world. And although the circular motions in heaven can sometimes seem, to one who weighs each thing curiously, to produce by their composite motion certain angular and various figures, and for the most part oblong ones, yet this happens <i>per accidens</i>, and reason shrinks from this supposition. For the circuits of the stars must be composed entirely of circular [motions]; otherwise they will not perpetually return uniformly and equably upon themselves, and will frustrate perpetuity — besides that they would be less simple and more anomalous, and not suited to doctrine and use either. Concerning Mercury, there is no reason for you to grieve that its star has not been observed by me, or that I would pretend the same as Copernicus in displaying its Phenomena. For although it very rarely departs from the Sun's rays, and the sphere is more inclined with us in Denmark than at Frauenburg in Prussia, where Copernicus lived, and the sea around the island of our dwelling sends up more vapours than the Vistula, yet on very many occasions — nay, almost every year, while I kept watch over the stars at our Uraniborg — it was accurately noted by us in the heavens, both as morning and as evening star; so that the path of this star, however rarely it lie open and however subject to many windings, cannot escape us — by no means to be borrowed from the doubtful observations of others, as Copernicus was forced to do. Concerning Venus, so far as it has been possible to investigate hitherto, it is certain that its apogee is not much moved forward from the Solar one — otherwise than Copernicus thought — yet not entirely united with it, as the Alphonsine [Tables], in imitation of Albategnius, would have it. In its eccentricity, however, the ratio is not equal, for I found it much less than the Solar. Whether it is mutable, I would not yet say; but your reasonings, ingenious in themselves, will not greatly prove or disprove it. To find out subtly, from the Pole-star or any other, whether the annual orb of the Earth devised by Copernicus, if it existed, would insinuate some little parallax into the stars of the eighth sphere, I have before now often and diligently tried to test; but, careful observation being made, I have hitherto found nothing of the kind. For I have observed that the Pole-star — observed precisely above or below the pole at any time of year, with its true distance from the pole applied (which this year is 2° 51' 10'', and it crosses the meridian above and below the pole at 6° 13' 26'' of Aries and Libra) — gave the same elevation of the pole within half a minute [scruple], nay, within a quarter or sixth part of it (finer things the sight does not discern); which I long ago indicated to Christoph Rothmann too, who, indulging the Copernican speculation, supposed that some discrepancy lay hidden here — so that hence there is no consolation left for defending your Copernicus (whose elegant and ingenious speculation I am otherwise wont to admire and extol). Seek it elsewhere, if it shall be given.</p><p>But of these and other things I will deal with you more fully, pleasantly and most gladly, and will share more of my own, if you, as you promise, shall some time visit me — which will now be less troublesome to you than before, since I have now fixed Urania's new seat in Bohemia, not very far removed from you, and dwell in the imperial castle Benátky, five miles distant from Prague. For that I was most graciously called hither from Germany by the Imperial Majesty, and most kindly and liberally received, I think you have already learned from others (since I have now stayed here half a year). Yet I should not wish that the harshness of fortune compel you to come to us, but rather your own choice and your love and affection toward our common studies. Whatever it shall be, you will find me not fortune's friend — whatever that be — but yours, who even in adverse lot will by no means fail you with his counsel and aid, but rather will promote you to all that is best. And if you come in good time, we shall perhaps find ways by which it will be better provided for you and yours hereafter than before. Farewell. When you write back, send your letters to Doctor Blotius at Vienna, who is in charge of the Emperor's Library there; he will easily forward them to Prague, whence they will soon reach me.</p><p>Again, fare most happily.<br>Given at Benátky, or the Venice of the Bohemians, the 9th day of December, New Style, 1599.<br>Your most loving<br>Tycho Brahe;<br>I wrote [this] with my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. If a convenient occasion offer itself, I ask that you remind Maestlin in my name about the Eclipses observed by him, to be compared with mine, for deriving the difference of meridians. For while I re-read the copy of my letter to Maestlin, I see that I made mention there of the Solar Eclipse which fell on the 25th of February of the year [15]99, where, by some carelessness or other, the eclipse-digits are noted as 10¾ — perhaps because they were thus wrongly found by one of my students, who carried out the calculation too hastily. Whereas the truer reckoning from my Tables (as I myself afterwards investigated) yields 9½ digits — which is also the quantity by which the Sun was found to be deficient by one of my [men], a student left at Uraniborg (so that he might have charge of the four remaining instruments, which, on account of their size, could not be carried away so quickly); and the times, observed from the heaven by means of the Equatorial armilla 18 feet in Diameter, from beginning to end (for it was clear there the whole time), agreed exactly with my restitution, and were of this kind: that the eclipse began there at 10h 10m, had its end at 12h 19m, and so the middle fell at 11h 12m. These things you may report to Maestlin, when you write to him, and ask and bring it about that I may obtain from him — through you, if he be unwilling to write back to me — the Eclipses, both Lunar and Solar, as many as he has hitherto observed. I have found among my papers a copy of Cramer's letter, of which I made mention before, transcribed long ago for I know not what use, which I add to these; and at the same time a copy of a letter of a certain Master Johannes Christiernus, a Dane, now living at Leiden for the sake of his studies, who was a member of my household when our book on the Comet of the year [15]77 was being printed at Uraniborg, and who arrived a little after that Ursus had been with me, and therefore learned from my other students how fraudulently he had acted. I have added also the testimony of the secretary of the most noble Erik Lange, whom Ursus then served, and who was likewise at hand when that man, attending his master, was with me. This testification Erik himself afterwards confirmed at Magdeburg in the presence of a public Notary, and testified that whatever else Ursus has in that libellous writing — so far as anything of the matter was known to him — was utterly false, a public Instrument (as they call it) having been drawn up concerning twelve such points laid before him, excerpted from that libellous book, which you will see elsewhere; from this you will judge the rest. T. B.  /  That the next page's surface might not be empty, I had three Epigrams inscribed there, from those composed by me after I came to Benátky. More poems, fashioned by various hands after I migrated from Denmark into Germany, I shared with Doctor Blotius, joined to me in old friendship, and asked him to make you a sharer of them too; if you receive them, you will gather more therefrom, and if there are any keen Poets among you (as without doubt there are), see that they increase the number.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702 (the Tycho–Kepler correspondence codex).</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"die 9 Decembris Stylo renovato 1599\"</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b> (Gregorian), from <i>\"Benachia sive Venetijs Bohemorum\"</i> — <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b>, the imperial castle ~5 (German) miles from Prague, glossed with the pun <i>\"the Venice of the Bohemians\"</i> (<i>Benátky</i> is the Czech word for Venice). The catalogue label confirms n.s.</li><li><b>Historic significance.</b> This is <b>Tycho's substantive reply to Kepler's first approach</b> and the letter in which Tycho <i>invites Kepler to Benátky</i> — the contact that brought Kepler into Tycho's household in 1600, gave him access to the Mars observations, and led (after Tycho's death in Oct 1601) to Kepler's elliptical-orbit discovery and the <i>Rudolphine Tables</i>. One of the cornerstone documents of the Scientific Revolution. It belongs with the rest of the Tycho–Kepler arc in this corpus (Kepler's Ursus-apologia 15990200001; the 1600 quarrel pair 16000408001 / 16000000001; the employment letters 16000828001 / 16001017001; Herwart's quote of Kepler-on-Ursus 16000911001).</li><li><b>The Ursus affair.</b> <b>Ursus</b> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär</b> (<i>Raimarus Ursus Dithmarsus</i>, \"the Ditmarsh Bear\"), imperial mathematician at Prague, who had published a geo-heliocentric world-system as his own (<i>Fundamentum astronomicum</i>, 1588) and savagely attacked Tycho — incorporating a mutilated copy of <b>Kepler's flattering 1595 letter</b> — in <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (1597). Kepler's long apology (for the unauthorised use of his letter) is what Tycho here graciously waves aside. Tycho recounts: Ursus's own preface admits Tycho \"deserved well of him\"; Tycho holds a thank-you poem in Ursus's own hand for hospitality and money at Uraniborg; Ursus took offence at <b>Rothmann's</b> letter (printed in Tycho's <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>, 1596) calling him a <i>nebulo</i> (\"scoundrel\"), and at Tycho's own report of Ursus's theft of papers. Tycho disclaims responsibility for the printed epithets (left in \"by a certain carelessness\" of his student proof-correctors), and announces a <b>libel suit under the Imperial Constitutions</b> — Ursus had fled to Silesia and returned secretly (\"how long my hands are … he will feel, under arrest and dragged into court\"). <i>(Ursus in fact died in August 1600, before judgment.)</i></li><li><b>The priority dispute over the Tychonic system.</b> Tycho dates his invention of the hypotheses to <b>1584</b> and rebuts four rival claimants/transmitters:</li><li><b>Ursus</b> — denied competence (no observational basis).</li><li><b>Duncan Liddel</b> (<i>\"a certain Scot, Duncan\"</i>), Professor of Medicine and Mathematics at <b>Helmstedt</b>: got the method from Tycho in Denmark, taught it at <b>Rostock</b> as \"Tychonic\" (out of fear), but privately claimed it as his own, and at Helmstedt did so openly in lectures and printed <i>intimationes</i>. The fraud \"detected especially by <b>Doctor Daniel Cramer</b>, minister at Stettin,\" once Liddel's chief auditor at Rostock and then tutor (<i>Paedagogus</i>) to <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>, who wrote about it to Rosenkrantz after Tycho left Denmark. — This is the same Liddel plagiarism dossier as <b>brahe-t_15990926001</b>, <b>brahe-t_15991204001</b> (Krag's testimony-origin), and <b>brahe-t_16000324002</b> (where Tycho presses Krag as eyewitness).</li><li><b>Helisaeus Roeslin</b> — claimed to have found the system in Tycho's <i>De cometa</i> (1577) only <i>after</i> independently devising similar ideas; Tycho notes the book was printed nine years before and that Roeslin's attempted \"emendation\" wrecked the symmetry of the revolutions.</li><li><b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> of Bologna — exculpated: Magini admitted in an autograph letter that he first saw the hypothesis in Tycho's <i>De mundi aetherei recentioribus phaenomenis</i> (the \"second book on the Aetherial Phenomena\"), objecting only that the orb of Mars would have to cross the Solar orb (Magini holding the <b>real-orbs</b> view). <b>Francesco Patrizi</b> got it via Magini and mentioned it in his <i>Nova de universis philosophia</i> (1591), attributing it to Tycho but garbling it against Tycho's own postulates — which Tycho says he will publicly disown.</li><li><b>The methodological core (historically poignant).</b> Against Kepler's <i>Mysterium Cosmographicum</i> (1596), Tycho objects to two things: (1) Kepler's positing of <b>real (solid) celestial orbs</b> as a bridge to Copernicanism — Tycho's comet work had abolished solid spheres; (2) Kepler's <b>a priori</b> harmonic method — Tycho insists the harmony of the heavens be sought <i>a posteriori</i>, \"once the motions and their occasions are exactly established,\" and would rank one who succeeds above <b>Pythagoras</b>. Crucially, Tycho here <b>defends strictly circular motion</b> (\"the circuits of the stars must be composed entirely of circulars … reason shrinks from\" oblong/angular composite figures) — the very assumption Kepler would overturn, with Tycho's own data, in deriving the elliptical orbit of Mars.</li><li><b>The anti-Copernican parallax argument.</b> Tycho rehearses his decisive empirical objection to the heliocentric system: repeated precise observations of the <b>Pole-star</b> (its true polar distance \"this year 2° 51' 10'',\" meridian transits at 6° 13' 26'' of <b>Aries</b> and <b>Libra</b>) show <b>no annual stellar parallax</b> — the pole-altitude constant to within a half-, quarter-, or sixth-minute (the limit of the eye). He had reported this to <b>Rothmann</b>, who had expected a discrepancy. Hence \"no consolation remains for defending your Copernicus\" (whose ingenuity Tycho nonetheless praises). Also: observational claims on <b>Mercury</b> (☿ — observed at Uraniborg \"almost every year,\" not borrowed as Copernicus had to) and <b>Venus</b> (♀ — apogee not much advanced beyond the Solar, eccentricity much less than the Solar). <i>(Planetary/zodiac glyphs are kept in the source block and named in English per protocol §5.3: ☿ = Mercury, ♀ = Venus, ♈ = Aries, ♎ = Libra.)</i></li><li><b>What Tycho promises to send.</b> A copy of Ursus's libel (so Kepler can see his own mutilated letter); the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (with the Curtius and Magini letters, and the vindication of Landgrave <b>Wilhelm IV</b> against Ursus's slander aimed at turning Wilhelm's son <b>Moritz</b> against Tycho); and the <i>Astronomiae instauratae mechanica</i> (the instruments \"used for over 20 years,\" and the closing record of work done and to do) — <i>\"if they can conveniently be brought from Prague.\"</i> He cites the meagre observational remains of <b>Werner / Schöner / Regiomontanus / Walther</b> as the foil to his own exact and abundant store.</li><li><b>Other persons &amp; references.</b> <b>Michael Maestlin</b> (Kepler's Tübingen teacher and the conduit for Tycho's earlier letters), reproached for never replying; <b>Cyprian</b> = <b>Cyprian Leovitius</b> (Cyprianus Leovicius), whose <i>Ephemerides</i> Kepler had reported as misattributed/corrupted — cited by Tycho as a further reason to delay publishing; <b>Hugo Blotius (<i>Blotius</i>)</b>, imperial librarian at <b>Vienna</b>, named as the forwarding address for Kepler's reply.</li><li><b>Latin tags &amp; allusions.</b> <i>\"Hoc opus, hic labor est\"</i> — <b>Virgil</b>, <i>Aeneid</i> 6.129 (of the hard return from the underworld); joined to <i>\"spectemur agendo\"</i> (\"let us be judged by our deeds,\" cf. <b>Ovid</b>, <i>Metamorphoses</i> 13.120). <i>\"intus et in cute\"</i> — <b>Persius</b>, <i>Satires</i> 3.30 (\"inside and under the skin,\" = through and through). <i>\"Scabies Gallica\"</i> — the \"French scab\" (syphilis), a period slur. <i>Astronomiae penetralia</i> — \"the inner sanctum of astronomy.\" <i>exanthlavi</i> (Greek-derived, <i>ἐξαντλέω</i>) — \"I have drained off / accomplished [with toil].\"</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing POSTSCRIPTA (the Maestlin eclipse-comparison reminder; the 25 Feb 1599 solar eclipse — 9½ digits via the 18-ft equatorial armilla, begin 10h10/end 12h19/mid 11h12; the enclosed Cramer + Johannes-Christiernus letters + Erik Lange's notarial testimony against the 12 points of Ursus's libel; the 3 Benátky epigrams via Blotius), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.210–211.</li><li><b>Why a student stayed behind at Uraniborg.</b> Recovers the reason a lone student was left at Uraniborg after Tycho's 1597 departure from Denmark: to have charge of four large instruments too bulky to be carried away quickly — direct evidence that the removal of Tycho's instrument-park from Hven was piecemeal and constrained by the sheer size of some pieces.</li><li><b>Fits the letter's own later reference</b> to instruments \"pertinent here\" (the <i>Mechanica</i>) being \"conveniently brought to you from Prague,\" situating this abandoned rump of equipment within the broader 1597–99 dispersal/relocation of Tycho's observational apparatus.</li><li>No new named person; \"a student\" (studiosus) remains unnamed in this passage, as in the existing translation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the day-number \"25\" dropped from the postscript's Latin eclipse-date clause (\"quæ die 25 Februarij Anni 99 contigit\", TBOO VIII p.210, line 12) and the fraction \"¾\" dropped from the English rendering of the erroneous eclipse-magnitude figure (\"digiti Ecliptici denotantur 10¾\", TBOO VIII p.210, line 13), both per Dreyer's printed text.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure. A long, technically dense but transparent letter of the first importance, rendered with the controlled glossary in hand (Hypotheses, parallax, apogee, eccentricity, eighth sphere, the planetary glyphs); persons, works, and astronomical claims are all identifiable and well attested, and the substance (the Ursus libel suit, the four-fold priority dispute with the Liddel dossier, the a-posteriori-vs-a-priori and circular-motion methodological objections to Kepler, the no-stellar-parallax argument against Copernicus, and the invitation to Benátky) is clear and historically corroborated. No textual cruxes; the only typographic noise is the OCR \"1O''\" for <b>10''</b> in the pole-distance, silently read as 10. Cross-links: the Liddel dossier (15990926001, 15991204001, 16000324002) and the wider Tycho–Kepler arc.</p><p>Grade A. A short, plain descriptive parenthetical (\"quæ præ magnitudine efferri tam cito nequibant, curam haberet\"), syntactically unambiguous, embedded in an otherwise fully coherent and well-known letter (Tycho to Kepler, 9 December 1599); the translation is direct with no cruces.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full Dreyer slice (TBOO VIII, the letter proper on pp.203-211) as page images in chunks of 1-4, 5-8, and 9-11, plus targeted re-reads of pp.208-209 to check the Mercury/Venus planetary glyphs. Collated the opening and closing formulas and every proper name, date, sum, and place-name across the ~2600-word body and postscript: Wittenberg, Bohemia, Rothmann, Jacob Curtius, Wandsbek, Prague/Silesia, 1584, Duncan/Helmstedt/Rostock, Daniel Cramer/Stettin, Holger Rosenkrantz, Roeslin, the 77 comet book (9 years), Magini, Francesco Patrizi, Maestlin, Cyprian, Werner/Schoner/Regiomontanus/Walther, 20 years, Pythagoras, Mercury/Venus apogee-eccentricity passage, the pole-star figures (2 51' 10'', 6 13' 26''), Benatky (five miles from Prague, half a year), Blotius/Vienna, and the full postscript apparatus (Cramer's letter, Johannes Christiernus/Leiden, Erik Lange's secretary, Magdeburg notarization, twelve points, three epigrams). All of this matches Dreyer and the English renders it faithfully with no fabrication, omission, pronoun-flip, or hedge-loss found. Two isolated numeric slips surfaced in the eclipse-postscript: our house Latin drops the day-number '25', and the English drops the fraction '3/4' from the reported eclipse-digit count. Per the grading rule, the TEXT_ERROR alone caps this at grade C even though neither defect touches the letter's substantive content (the Ursus dispute, hypothesis priority, or the Kepler relationship).</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). our own source_text has silently dropped the day-number '25' from the eclipse date; Dreyer prints it plainly. The English translation happens to supply '25th' correctly, but not from our own source_text as published — the house Latin itself is defective. Dreyer prints: <i>\"quæ die 25 Februarij Anni 99 contigit\" (TBOO VIII p.210, line 12)</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The English silently drops the fraction '¾', reporting the erroneously-calculated eclipse magnitude as a flat 10 digits instead of 10¾ digits — a real alteration of the numeral Tycho is in the process of correcting (against the true 9½ digits given two sentences later). At: “\"the eclipse-digits are noted as 10\"”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects directly against the Dreyer page image for TBOO VIII p.210 (slice pages 9-11 of the 11-page letter slice, covering pp.210-212), which plainly prints \"quæ die 25 Februarij Anni 99 contigit\" and \"digiti Ecliptici denotantur 10¾\". Both readings are unambiguous in the page image and match the audit's claims exactly. No other page of the slice was needed since both defects sit in the same postscript passage on p.210. Nothing else in the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15990200001", "next": "brahe-t_16000126002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990200001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000400001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for delayed reply, excuses Kepler regarding Ursus's insertion of his letter, and describes the disruption caused by relocating his affairs to Bohemia.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991209001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991214001", "d": "1599-12-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Hugo Blotius", "ck": "Hugo Blotius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Jiřice", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hugo Blotius — Girsitz (Jiřice), 14 December 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f.</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD HUGONEM BLOTIUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>CHARISSIME et praestantissime vir, amice plurimi semper mihi faciende. Cum forte per literas communis amici nostri Domini Doctoris HELLERI aut etiam aliunde intellexeris, me nunc in Bohemia degere, et occasiones, cur huc venerim, simul ad te, ut existimo, relatae sint, nolo nunc pluribus ijs recensendis immorari. Id saltem te rogo, ut veteris inter nos amicitiae Basileae, uti scis, contractae, et postea cum in Dania adhuc subsisterem, continuatae memor, eorum etiam, quae cum apud Nostrates haererem, pollicitus es, recorderis, nimirum ut catalogum librorum in inclyta istic Caesareana Bibliotheca, praesertim quod ad Philosophicos et Mathematicos attinet, mecum communices. Id si etiamnum in hac ob loci maiorem commoditatem praestiteris, rem equidem acceptissimam feceris. Literas hisce adiunctas cum libris quibusdam excellenti in Styria Mathematico IOHANNI CÖPLERO destinatis ad eum, ubicunque isthic vel alibi in vicinia moretur, rogo, ut per certum internuncium dirigere non degraveris. Habitavit antea in urbe Graecia, nescio an etiamnum ibi sit, intellexi enim eorum res turbatas esse. Si quod ab ipso responsum acceperis, Pragam ad dictum Doctorem HELLERUM mitte, mox me inveniet. Vale. Dabantur properanter ex arce Caesareana Girsitza, ubi nunc aliquantisper ago, donec lues Epidemica Bennachiae, ubi alias ex clementissima Caesaris voluntate habito, prorsus remiserit.</p><p>Iamque iterum et saepius vale.<br>14. die Decembris Anno 1599.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p><p>Intellexi vitriolum Ungaricum et<br>terebinthinam etiam Ungaricam, tum<br>quoque tartarum ex vino Ungarico cadis<br>adhærentem isthic in mediocri esse precio, quæ<br>tamen singula hîc care veneunt. Si itaque, quanti singulorum ex his libra isthic, præsertim si in<br>aliqua copia prostent, et simul quo precio libra Croci vestratis emi queat,<br>mature indicaveris, rem gratam sic præstiteris. Sique ex ijs pro assignato<br>precio placuerint, mittam pro qua emantur pecunia. Vellem ex tribus materijs<br>primo nominatis multum habere, utpote de singulis centenarium ad<br>minimum.</p><p>Adiunxi carmina quædam a varijs Poëtis, postquam e Dania in Germaniam transmigravi, in mei gratiam concinnata, quæ per<br>otium legere poteris posteaque cum Dom. IO. KEPLERO communicare, atque ipsi cum fasciculo illi<br>destinato mittere. Si qui apud vos arguta vena præditi sunt Poëtæ, uti esse<br>non dubito, fac numerum adaugeant. Memini enim te aliquando e Vienna vestra satis acuta et sonora in Daniam mihi misisse Epigrammata.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Hugo Blotius.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Dearest and most excellent man, friend ever to be most highly esteemed by me. Since you have perhaps learned — through the letters of our common friend Master Doctor Heller, or also from elsewhere — that I now dwell in Bohemia, and since the occasions for which I came hither have, as I suppose, at the same time been reported to you, I do not now wish to linger in recounting them at greater length. This only I ask of you: that, mindful of the old friendship contracted between us at Basel (as you know), and afterward continued while I still resided in Denmark, you remember also those things which you promised while I was staying among our [people] — namely, that you would share with me the catalogue of the books in the renowned Imperial Library there [at Vienna], especially as regards the Philosophical and the Mathematical [ones]. If you would perform this even now — on account of the greater convenience of [my present] location — you would do me a most acceptable thing indeed. The letters adjoined to these, with certain books destined for the excellent Mathematician in Styria, <b>Johann Kepler</b>, I ask that you not find it burdensome to direct to him — wherever he may be staying there, or elsewhere in the vicinity — through a sure intermediary. He dwelt formerly in the city of Graz; I know not whether he is even now there, for I have understood that their affairs have been disturbed. If you receive any answer from him, send it to Prague, to the said Doctor Heller; it will soon find me. Farewell. Given hastily from the Imperial castle of Girsitz, where I now stay for a little while, until the epidemic plague at Benátky — where otherwise, by the most clement will of the Emperor, I dwell — has entirely abated.</p><p>And now again, and often, farewell.<br>The 14th day of December, in the year 1599.<br>Most loving of you,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>I wrote with my own hand.</p><p>I have learned that Hungarian vitriol, and Hungarian turpentine too, and likewise the tartar that clings to the casks of Hungarian wine, are to be had there at a moderate price, though each of these sells dear here. If, then, you would let me know in good time what a pound of each of these costs there — especially if they are available in any quantity — and at the same time at what price a pound of your [region's] saffron may be bought, you will thereby do me a welcome service. And if, at the price stated, they suit me, I will send money with which they may be bought. I should like to have a good quantity of the three materials named first, that is, at least a hundredweight of each.</p><p>I have enclosed certain poems by various poets, composed in my honour since I removed from Denmark into Germany, which you may read at your leisure and afterward share with Master Johannes Kepler, sending them to him along with that bundle intended for him. If there are among you poets gifted with a witty vein — as I do not doubt there are — see that they add to the number. For I recall that you once sent me, from your Vienna, epigrams sharp and sonorous enough, into Denmark.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Hugo Blotius</b> (Hugo Blotz, 1533/4–1608), the <b>Imperial Court Librarian</b> at Vienna (the <i>Hofbibliothek</i>), a Netherlandish humanist; Tycho's friend from their <b>Basel</b> days (1568–70) and during Tycho's Danish years. The witness is, fittingly, in <b>Blotius's papers at the Vienna Nationalbibliothek</b> (cod. Vindob. lat. 9737).</li><li><b>The two requests.</b> (1) Tycho asks Blotius to make good an old promise: to send him the <b>catalogue of the Imperial Library's holdings, especially in Philosophy and Mathematics</b> — now the more useful since Tycho is close by in Bohemia. (2) <b>Of high significance for the Tycho–Kepler relationship:</b> Tycho asks Blotius to forward an <b>enclosed letter and certain books to \"Johann Kepler [<i>Iohanni Cöplero</i>], the excellent Mathematician in Styria,\"</b> wherever he may be — Tycho noting that Kepler \"dwelt formerly in <b>the city of Graz</b> [<i>urbs Graecia</i> = Graz/Graecium],\" but that he hears \"their affairs have been disturbed.\" This refers to the <b>Counter-Reformation expulsion of Protestants from Inner Austria/Graz</b> (1598–1600), which was about to drive Kepler out — and which helped bring him to Tycho at Benátky in <b>February 1600</b>. The letter thus documents Tycho's outreach to Kepler <b>two months before</b> their first meeting.</li><li><b><i>Doctor Helleri / Hellerum</b></i> = \"Doctor Heller,\" a common friend at Prague serving as Tycho's forwarding address. <i>Identification flagged</i>: not securely fixed (a Prague-based Heller).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arce Caesareana Girsitza</i> = the Imperial manor of <b>Girsitz / Jiřice</b> (near Benátky), where Tycho had temporarily withdrawn while the <i>lues epidemica</i> (plague) at <b>Benátky</b> abated. (The dataset label gives only \"Girsitz\"; the body supplies the date.)</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>14. die Decembris … 1599</i> = <b>14 December 1599</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b> (no <i>n.s.</i> tag in the dataset, but the imperial location fixes it). Header so marked.</li><li><b>Subject matter.</b> This is Tycho's own request, appended to a letter otherwise devoted to his temporary quarters at Girsitz during the Benátky plague, for raw materials used in his chemical/spagyric work: Hungarian vitriol (a sulphate salt, a staple of Paracelsian pharmacy and of Tycho's own medicinal preparations), Hungarian turpentine, cask-tartar from Hungarian wine (cream of tartar, likewise a spagyric staple), and saffron (<i>crocus</i>) — he asks Blotius to price each by the pound and offers to send money once quantities and prices are confirmed, wanting \"at least a hundredweight\" of the first three.</li><li><b>Addressee.</b> Hugo Blotius (1533–1608), the Dutch-born prefect of the Imperial Library in Vienna and a long-standing correspondent of Tycho's since their student days together at Basel (Thoren, <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i>) — Vienna is the natural market for goods arriving from Hungary, which explains why Tycho asks Blotius rather than a Prague contact.</li><li><b>Kepler cross-reference.</b> Tycho refers to \"Master Johannes Kepler\" by name and asks Blotius to pass on a bundle of commemorative poems to him — at this date (14 December 1599 N.S.) Kepler had not yet arrived in Prague (he would not meet Tycho in person until early 1600), so this is a trace of the pre-meeting correspondence network through which the two astronomers were already circulating material before their historic collaboration began.</li><li><b>Literary networking.</b> Tycho mentions poems written in his honour by \"various poets\" since his move from Denmark into the Empire, and explicitly solicits more from poets in Blotius's Viennese circle, recalling that Blotius had once sent him \"sharp and sonorous\" epigrams from Vienna — evidence of Tycho actively cultivating a corpus of Latin encomiastic verse around his own person and exile, material that (via Kepler) may have been intended for a future printed collection.</li><li><b>Crux: <i>Croci vestratis</i>.</b> \"Your [region's] saffron\" is translated as ordinary saffron (<i>Crocus sativus</i>, a valuable spice/dye/medicinal ingredient), consistent with its being priced alongside vitriol, turpentine and tartar by the pound; the same Latin word could in principle denote an alchemical \"crocus\" preparation (e.g. crocus martis, an iron-oxide medicinal powder), but the absence of any qualifying noun (<i>Martis</i>, <i>Veneris</i>, etc.) and the plain commodity-pricing context both favour the literal spice/dye reading.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely, and the two requests (the Imperial-Library catalogue; the forwarding of letter-and-books to <b>Kepler at Graz</b> amid the Styrian Protestant troubles) are clear, important, and corpus-/history-corroborated — <i>Iohannes Cöplerus</i> = Johannes Kepler, <i>urbs Graecia</i> = Graz, are read with confidence. Grade [B] only for the unidentified \"Doctor Heller\" (the Prague forwarding contact), flagged rather than asserted.</p><p>The Latin is clear and the sense throughout is secure; this is ordinary, well-formed epistolary prose with no corrupt readings. The only interpretive softness is the <i>Croci vestratis</i> crux noted above, which does not affect the translation's wording, only a possible secondary sense. Not collated against a published critical edition of this specific passage, hence B rather than A.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.214-217 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the full Latin letter (opening, the Kepler/Koppler request, the Girsitz/Benatky place-names, the vitriol-turpentine-tartar postscript with its prices and hundredweight quantity, and the closing poems paragraph) word-for-word against the printed page. Text matches source_text exactly (allowing long-s normalization). Every proper name (Heller, Cöplero/Kepler, Girsitza, Bennachiae) and number (14 December 1599, centenarium/hundredweight) in the English has a clean counterpart in both source_text and Dreyer. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Correspondence (Cod. 9737f / 9737t)", "sh": "Cod. 9737f / 9737t", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC14001139"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Staatsbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 9737f."], "th": {"label": "Hugo Blotius", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15920320001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Invokes their old Basel friendship and requests a catalogue of the Imperial Library in Vienna, especially its philosophical and mathematical holdings.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991214001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991216001", "d": "1599-12-16", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "ck": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bremen", "t": "Ludolf Riddershusen → Tycho Brahe — Bremen, 16 December 1599 [Old Style]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (German address) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basileensi G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The letter's recovered passages — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Bremen astrologer <b>Ludolf Riddershusen</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. A technical <b>astrological consultation</b> (seven questions on primary directions and house systems).</p>", "la": "<p>DEM<br>WOLLEDLEN GESTRENGEN VNDT HOCHGELARTEN HERRN TICHONI BRAHE,<br>ERBGESESSEN ZU KNUDTSTRUP, ITZIGER ZEIT ABER AHM<br>KEYSERLICHEN HOFE ZU PRAGE, MEINEM GROSSGUNSTIGEN HERRNN VNDT<br>PRÆCEPTORI.<br>PRAGE.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>GENEROSE ac Magnifice Heros, fautor et Mœcenas summa<br>observantia colendissime, post humilem promptorum meorum officiorum oblationem<br>Generositati Vestræ pro universis et singulis ex singulari heroica erga me<br>immeritum innata affectione in me collatis beneficijs denuo et imprimis ago et<br>habeo gratias quas possum maximas. Deinde Generositatem Vestram celare nequeo,<br>quod iam elapso anno nullas scripserim literas. Causa fuit non modo domesticarum<br>mearum curarum in hac mea solitudine intollerabilis moles, verum etiam rumor<br>circa præterita Bacchanalia hîc et Hamburgi<br>de discessu Generositatis Vestræ ex hac lachrymarum valle sparsus, quo auditu<br>ita eram perterrefactus, ut iam de studio Astrologico, cui hactenus summopere<br>incubui, ob defectum Tabularum ab alijs editarum actum esse arbitrarer. Tandem<br>vero iam ante aliquot dies ex literis lætabundis Domini DAVID. FABRICIJ, Generositatem Vestram<br>cum tota inclita familia<br>Pragam salvam et incolumem pervenisse, Illustrissimumque,<br>potentissimum atque invictissimum Imperatorem, Dominum nostrum Clementissimum,<br>Generositati Vestræ clementissime favere, laus Deo, cognovi, quapropter etiam<br>cum illius, tum vero Generositatis Vestræ, literis motu impulsus, hasce meas<br>Fabricianis adiungere volui.</p><p>Quandoquidem igitur Generositas Vestra iam antea in suis<br>Resolutionibus mihi benigne communicatis, circa quæstionem de modo dirigendi<br>indicavit: Non esse modum dirigendi exactiorem, quam is, qui a REGIOMONTANO traditus est, totidem vero<br>gradus Meridianum transeuntes, quot quemlibet alias positionis Circulum, sed<br>numerandos potius in Meridiano, quia illic directe permeant, quodque in<br>obliquissima Sphæra tunc unâ dirigendi ratio locum habeat; utut domorum<br>distributio Regiomontana istis administrari nequeat. Et hanc ob caussam Generos.<br>Vestra potius approbet CAMPANI divisionem,<br>quæ totum Cœlum ubique respicit et universalis existit. Cui tamen Generos.<br>Vestra æqualem per Eclipticam, ob planetarum iuxta hanc incessum, partitionem<br>applicare soleat, imitatione veterum Astrologorum; verum alia atque alia in hac<br>et illa ordinatione considerando, prout vel ratione totius Cœli vel solius<br>Zodiaci, requiratur animadversio. Hæc, inquam, etsi satis luculenter indicata<br>sint, nihilominus Generositatem Vestram reverenter et obnixe rogatam velim, ut<br>Generositas Vestra mihi unum atque alterum breve schema<br>operationis et demonstrationis communicari dignetur.</p><p>Haberem insuper nonnulla alia non aspernanda, videlicet an directio<br>iuxta gradus Zodiaci eiusque Polum etiam possit inducere effectus, sicut illa,<br>quæ respectu Poli Æquatoris fit, et an tunc eodem modo mensuratio annorum sit<br>instituenda iuxta motum ☉lis in Radice Genituræ,<br>sicut alias, an vero diversa hîc mensuratio sit ordinanda.</p><p>Deinde quoniam in directione Ascendentis omnium Astrologorum<br>unanimi consensu observari hactenus solitæ sint ascensiones obliquæ sub<br>elevatione Poli cuiusque Regionis, in qua quis natus est, an etiam Generositas<br>Vestra censeat, quod si quis per aliquot multos annos in longinqua aliqua<br>regione multum a loco seu regione nativitatis distante moretur, an nihilominus<br>directio Ascendentis ex ascensionibus obliquis, quas Tabula Directionum sub<br>elevatione Poli regionis nativitatis demonstrat, facienda sit; an vero ex<br>Ascensionibus obliquis sub elevatione Poli istius loci, sub quo quis degit, ut<br>exempli gratia natus est aliquis sub elevatione Poli 51 gradus, postea patria<br>egressus in alia regione, sub elevatione Poli 54 graduum sita, per totos<br>triginta et plures annos commoratus est et adhuc moratur, an eius Ascendentis<br>directio sit instituenda ex Tabula 51, an vero 54 graduum elevationis, et quæ<br>sit ratio, quare locus nativitatis, quoad directionis effectum, magis hominem et<br>corpus nati afficiat, quam locus commorationis,<br>in quo vitam degit? cum tamen fere singulis annis in effectibus Eclipsium, pro<br>diversitate meridianorum et locorum, citius aut tardius sequatur effectus.</p><p>Tertio, quia ex Diario ELIÆ OLAI<br>Cimbri Octogenariam divisionem Thematum ☌, ☍, et<br>□ ☉lis et ☾æ,<br>tanquam experientia et rationibus suffultam, ab illo comprobari intellexi,<br>percuperem brevibus cognoscere illas ipsas rationes Octogenariæ divisionis, et<br>quo experimento res inventa fuerit.</p><p>Quarto, quia experientia docet, non tantum Directiones maleficorum<br>malos inducere effectus, sed et transitus ♄ni et<br>♂tis, sive id fiat corpore, sive radijs<br>infœlicibus, per præcipua Genituræ loca, præsertim Ascendens interdum mala<br>operari, interdum vero non; quæritur, an ex caussis Astrologicis possit haberi<br>et dari certitudo aliqua, qua præsciri possit, quando<br>transitus illi ♄ni et ♂tis<br>nocituri sint, nec ne; an alia caussa sit quam convenientia Directionis et<br>progressus, quod transitus isti interdum operentur super Ascendens, interdum<br>vero non?</p><p>Quinto. An malorum planetarum effectus directione inducti per bonos<br>planetas omnino tolli vel impediri possint, et qua potissimum ratione aut re<br>malorum operationes impediantur?</p><p>Sexto. Qui aspectus sive Directio malorum pro morte naturali quam<br>pernitiosissimus sit observatus, et quæ constitutio malorum vel Directio pro<br>morte violenta omnium pessima sit observata per experientiam.</p><p>Septimo. Videri quoque multos infaustas et violentas Genituras<br>habentes fœlicem tamen vitam agere et naturali morte occumbere, et econtrario<br>plurimos optimam genesin vel saltem exiguam violentæ mortis significationem<br>habentes violenter interire. Quæritur, unde hæc operationum sive effectuum<br>diversitas, Astrologice loquendo, oriatur.</p><p>Harum itaque quæstionum Resolutiones, nisi Generositati Vestræ<br>admodum essem molestus, mihi aliquando etiam communicari submisse peterem. Quia<br>etiam in Astrologicis absque restitutione motus ☾næ<br>nihil solerter efficere possum, maiorem in modum similiter rogarem, ut Generos.<br>Vestra mihi significet, anne eiusdem Restitutio publici iuris reddita sit, ut<br>eandem mihi comparare possem. Et quia Generos. Vestra sibi nonnullas certas<br>Observationes in Genethliacis communicari expetijt, hasce ut ratas et satis<br>compertas adiungere velim, plures indies colligam. In Meteorologicis non nisi<br>vulgare habeo.</p><p>Demum Generositatem Vestram iterum atque iterum reverenter et<br>obnixe rogitans, ne me immeritum favore et benevolentia ex innata heroica erga<br>me affectione prosequi deserat, et diuturnum illud silentium mihi benigne<br>ignoscat.</p><p>Deus Opt. Max. Generos. Vestram cum<br>tota inclita familia salvam et incolumem ut conservet, tueatur atque defendat,<br>ex animo precor.</p><p>Datum Bremæ 16 Xbris Anno 99.</p><p>Generosi<br>Vestræ<br>Addictissimus<br>LUDOLPHUS RlDDERS[HUSIUS].</p><p>Anno 1512 die 22 Iunij inter horam 17 et 18 P. M. Consul<br>Dominus DANIEL a BUREN, qui Anno 62 partes<br>Doct. ALB. HARDENBERGIJ sive<br>CALVINI contra reliquos consules<br>defendebat et exinde toti Civitati maximas turbas excitavit, natus est Bremæ.<br>Hic Anno 39 in Senatorium ordinem et postea Anno 47 in Consulem electus, in<br>principio vero Anni 92 ob decrepitam ætatem officio Consulatus abdicans, Anno 93<br>circa 28 Iunij morbo correptus, die 10 Iulij post horam quintam matutinam placide<br>obdormivit.</p><p>Anno 1565 die 24 Novembris inter horam 10 et 11<br>pomeridianam nata est prædicto Domino Consuli filia MARGARETHA a BUHREN, sed<br>Anno 94 die ♃is 16 Maij pleuresi grassante correpta moriebatur 23 Maij.</p><p>Anno 1539 die 22 Iulij circa horam quintam matutinam natus est<br>Bremæ Dominus THEODORICUS a REDEN qui Anno 76<br>in autumno in Senatorium ordinem elegebatur, erat vir magnæ staturæ et<br>corpulentus, habebat duas uxores, cum quibus maximas hæreditates accepit. Hic<br>Anno 97 die prima Iunij mane morbo correptus pillulas ab affine proprio, Doct.<br>IOH. BAPTISTA SERVETIO, Medico,<br>assumens, ilico in Doctoris brachijs vitam cum morte commutavit.</p><p>Anno 1551 die 10 9bris hora 17 post M. Bremæ natus est WICHMANNUS MIDDELSTORP. Anno 75 die 16 Feb. in<br>terram periculose collapsus, Carnificem nomine DIONYSIUM, ut ipsum curaret, adhibere coactus, Anno 86 circa<br>festum Paschalis in ædibus Consulis LUDERI a<br>BELMERS pie defuncti, ex alto in navem cadebat, et nisi navis<br>adfuisset, in Wisurgi ipsum perijsse necesse esset. Tandem in principio Anni 92<br>vario genere morborum correptus et 11° Iunij iterum in terram<br>periculose prostratus atque ita usque in Annum 93 morbo detentus, die 8 Octobris<br>noctu sequente placide obdormivit, frustra adhibitis multis remedijs.</p><p>Anno 1558 die 25 Augusti post horam duodecimam meridiei<br>Francofordiæ ad Oderam natus est MEIMARUS a<br>BORKEN, Secretarius huius Civitatis, quia parens ipsius, Doctor<br>IOH. a BORKEN, erat ex genere<br>patriciorum nostræ Civitatis. Hic Anno 83 die ☿ij vesperi post festum Annunciationis<br>Mariæ, Bederkeza Arce huius Civitatis, crus fregit. Anno 87 post festum<br>BARTHOLOMÆI Apostoli calido morbo ad mortem<br>ægrotabat. Anno 91 die 4 Augusti ex nimia ingurgitatione vini præcedentis diei,<br>sanguinem ex ore expuens, furiosus extinctus est.</p><p>Anno 1566 die 21° Iulij hora 7 P. M. natus est Bremæ<br>HILDEMANNUS DEDDEKEN, qui Anno 90 post festum<br>Penthecostes in mare a pyratis globo transiectus est.</p><p>Anno 1552 die 7 Februarij, circa horam sextam pomeridianam, natus<br>est Bremæ Dominus GERHARDUS GERKEN, insignis<br>Musicus, amicus meus haud vulgaris, sed hostis Astrologiæ. Anno 81 in<br>Secretarium Civitatis assumptus et Anno 93 3 Iulij in Senatorem electus. Anno<br>91, die Martis post Quasimodogeniti et iterum quarto Augusti periculose<br>decumbens, postea Anno 94 28 9bris iterum aliquot mensibus correptus et citra<br>spem sanitati restitutus. Tandem Anno 96 post festum Penthecostes denuo<br>correptus et longo morbo detentus, in die S. MICHAELIS Archangeli inter horam 10 et<br>11 antemeridianam, me præsente, placide occubuit, frustra<br>adhibitis a duobus Doct. Med. Civitatis adminiculis. Mirum, quidni ad decrepitam<br>pervenerit ætatem, quia ☾am coniunctam ♃i in propria domo in lla liberam a<br>maleficis et Ascendens illæsum habebat.</p><p>Huius boni Viri uxor BRIGITTA GRONINGES nata est Bremæ Anno 1559 die 11° Maij<br>die ♃is ante festum Penthecostes inter horam 7 et 8 matutinam, sed Anno 98,<br>peste grassante, mature ex Civitate in hortum fugiens, die 6 Augusti in horto<br>peste correpta, die 12 eiusdem inter horam 1 et 2 noctis sequentis placide<br>obdormivit, neque ipsi remedia defuêre. Et hæc honesta matrona Lunam in ♋<br>ascendentem in Trino ♃is in ♓ existentis habebat.</p><p>Anno 1555 die 24 Martij inter<br>horam 2 et 3 pomeridianam nata est Bremæ<br>TIBBEKE, uxor ALEXANDRI<br>BIKKERS, quæ Anno 96 die 26 Feb. pleuresi grassante correpta,<br>filiam charam amisit, et ipsa sanitati restituta est. Sed postea eodem Anno 4<br>7bris ob mœrorem defunctæ filiæ in insaniam redacta et miserrime detenta usque<br>ad festum Paschatis Anni 97, quo ad sanitatem redijt. Verum Anno 98 circa<br>10 7bris peste iterum correpta die 15 eiusdem hora 2 noctis sequentis obijt.</p><p>Anno 1566 die ☾æ 24 Iunij inter horam 1 et 2 noctis P. M. natus<br>est Domino IOHANNI SCHULTEN, Senatori huius<br>Civitatis, filius FRANSZ. Hic filiolus Anno 71 die ☿ij 20 Iunij hora quinta<br>pomeridiana a muliere quadem, ipsum transiente, ex alto ponte orientali in fossam<br>Civitatis trudebatur, sed a Patre citra omnem spem salvatus, qui cum ipso fere<br>perijsset. Patre postea mortuo, Anno 86 in autumno Amsterodami ob dissipationem<br>bonorum cum scortis et Anno 90 primo 9bris a Magistratu, Matre idipsum petente,<br>eandem ob causam secunda vice in carcerem coniectus est. Elapso anno in<br>Italiam profectus est, utrum adhuc<br>superstes sit nec ne, ambiguitur.</p><p>Ego L. R. Anno 1551<br>die 15 Septembris post horam 12 noctis sequentis Bremæ sum natus, in pueritia mihi<br>infinita contigere inconvenientia. Anno 73 die 14 Martij nocte sequente Brunswigæ<br>ardentissima febre et Anno 78 die 3 9bris singulari genere morborum et angina<br>iterum corripiebar. Anno 73 13 Maij uxorem duxi. Anno 1578 16. Feb. inter horam 6<br>et 7 vespertinam natus est mihi filius GREGORIUS, et hic ab ineunte ætate usque in annum 95 vario genere<br>morborum est afflictus. Anno 1579 die 26 Xbris inter horam 3 et 4 noctis<br>sequentis natus est mihi filius secundus HIERONIMUS, qui meliori et<br>firmiori valetudine, laus Deo, utitur. Anno 82 15 Iulij paulo post horam 12 noctis<br>sequentis natus est mihi filius tertius CHRISTIANUS. Hic Anno 83 circa festum IACOBI et Anno 84, 3<br>Aprilis ad mortem decumbebat, postea vero bona valetudine etiam utitur.</p><p>Et quia non dubito, quin Generositas Vestra loca planetarum ad<br>natalem meum et filiorum meorum restituta habeat, ut igitur eo magis periculum<br>certitudinis artis facere possem, etiam atque etiam reverenter et obnixe rogo,<br>ut Generos. Vestra eorundem Restitutiones benigne mihi communicari dignetur.<br>Etsi vero Generos. Vestræ pro hisce et omnibus alijs in me immeritum collatis<br>beneficijs quicquam compensare non videam, nihilominus qui Generosi Vestræ<br>cordi sunt, me ad omne officium quam paratissimum devinctum habebunt.</p><p>Annotationes dierum sunt ex Calendario Iuliano desumptæ.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[German address] To the right-noble, stern, and highly-learned Lord Tycho Brahe, hereditary [lord] at Knudstrup, but at this time at the Imperial Court at Prague, my greatly-favouring Lord and Preceptor. At Prague.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Noble and magnificent Hero, my patron and Maecenas, most to be revered with the utmost respect: after the humble offering of my ready services, I again and above all give and render Your Generosity the greatest thanks I can, for each and all the benefits conferred on me, undeserving, out of [your] singular heroic and inborn affection. Next, I cannot conceal from Your Generosity that for this past year I have written no letters. The cause was not only the intolerable mass of my domestic cares in this my solitude, but also a rumour, spread about last Carnival here and at Hamburg, of Your Generosity's departure from this vale of tears — at the hearing of which I was so terrified that I thought it was now all over with the Astrological study, on which I have hitherto most earnestly bent myself, for want of the Tables published by others. But at last, some days ago, from a joyful letter of Lord David Fabricius, I learned (praise God) that Your Generosity, with your whole renowned family, has reached Prague safe and sound, and that the most illustrious, most powerful, and most invincible Emperor, our most clement Lord, most clemently favours You; wherefore, moved both by his letters and by Your Generosity's, I wished to join these mine to Fabricius's.</p><p>Since, then, Your Generosity formerly indicated in your \"Resolutions\" [answers] kindly shared with me, on the question of the method of directing [primary directions]: that there is no more exact method of directing than that handed down by Regiomontanus — [counting] as many degrees crossing the Meridian as [cross] any other circle of position, but rather to be counted on the Meridian, since there they pass directly; and that in the most oblique Sphere the directing-method then holds at once, although the Regiomontanus distribution of houses cannot be administered there; and that for this reason Your Generosity rather approves Campanus's division, which regards the whole Heaven everywhere and is universal — to which, however, Your Generosity is wont to apply an equal partition through the Ecliptic (because of the planets' procession along it), in imitation of the ancient Astrologers, considering different things in this and that arrangement, as the consideration is required either by reason of the whole Heaven or of the Zodiac alone: these things, I say, though indicated clearly enough, I would nonetheless reverently and earnestly ask Your Generosity that you deign to share with me one or two brief schemes of the operation and demonstration.</p><p>I should besides have some other things, not to be despised — namely whether direction by the degrees of the Zodiac and its Pole can also induce effects, like that which is done with respect to the Pole of the Equator; and whether then the measurement of years is to be instituted in the same way, by the motion of the Sun [☉] in the Radix of the nativity, as otherwise — or whether a different measurement is to be ordered here.</p><p>Then, since in directing the Ascendant, by the unanimous consent of all Astrologers, the oblique ascensions under the Pole-elevation of the Region in which one was born have hitherto been observed: whether Your Generosity also judges that, if someone tarries for many years in some far region much distant from the place of his nativity, the direction of the Ascendant should nonetheless be made from the oblique ascensions which the Table of Directions shows under the Pole-elevation of the birth-region — or rather from the oblique ascensions under the Pole-elevation of the place where he lives? For example: one is born under a Pole-elevation of 51 degrees; afterward, having left his fatherland, he has dwelt, and still dwells, for thirty whole years and more in another region situated under a Pole-elevation of 54 degrees — is his Ascendant-direction to be instituted from the Table of 51, or of 54 degrees of elevation? And what is the reason why the place of nativity, as to the effect of direction, affects the man and the body of the native more than the place of habitation in which he passes his life? — since yet, almost every year, in the effects of Eclipses, the effect follows sooner or later according to the diversity of meridians and places.</p><p>Third: since from the Diary of Elias Olai the Cimbrian I understood the Octogenary [eighty-fold] division of charts — by the conjunction [☌], opposition [☍], and square [□] of the Sun [☉] and Moon [☾] — to be approved by him as supported by experience and reasons, I should much like to learn briefly those very reasons of the Octogenary division, and by what experiment the thing was found.</p><p>Fourth: since experience teaches that not only the Directions of the malefics induce bad effects, but also the transits of Saturn [♄] and Mars [♂] — whether by body or by unhappy rays — through the chief places of the nativity, especially the Ascendant, sometimes work ill, sometimes not: it is asked whether, from Astrological causes, any certainty can be had by which it may be foreknown when those transits of Saturn and Mars will be harmful, or not; and whether there is any other cause than the agreement of the Direction and the progression, that those transits sometimes operate upon the Ascendant, sometimes not.</p><p>Fifth: whether the effects of the malefic planets, induced by direction, can be wholly removed or hindered by the benefic planets — and by what means above all the operations of the malefics are hindered.</p><p>Sixth: which aspect or Direction of the malefics has been observed most pernicious for natural death; and which constitution of the malefics, or Direction, has been observed by experience worst of all for a violent death.</p><p>Seventh: it is seen also that many having unlucky and violent nativities nevertheless lead a happy life and die a natural death, and contrariwise that very many having the best genesis — or at least a slight signification of violent death — perish violently. It is asked whence this diversity of operations or effects arises, astrologically speaking.</p><p>The Resolutions of these questions, then, unless I were quite troublesome to Your Generosity, I would humbly ask to be shared with me sometime. And since in Astrological matters I can do nothing skilfully without the restitution of the motion of the Moon [☾], I would likewise greatly ask that Your Generosity signify to me whether the Restitution of the same has been made public, that I might procure it. And since Your Generosity desired that certain reliable Observations in genitures be shared, I would add these as proven and well-ascertained, and will collect more day by day. In Meteorological [matters] I have nothing but the common sort.</p><p>Lastly, again and again reverently and earnestly entreating Your Generosity not to cease pursuing me, undeserving, with favour and benevolence out of [your] inborn heroic affection, and kindly to pardon me that long silence.</p><p>I pray from my heart that God, Best and Greatest, may keep, guard, and defend Your Generosity, with your whole renowned family, safe and sound.</p><p>Given at Bremen, 16 December, the year [15]99.<br>Your Generosity's most devoted,<br><b>Ludolf Riddershus[en].</b></p><p><i>[Here follows, appended in the same letter, Riddershusen's dossier of dated Bremen births and deaths, offered as astrological test-cases:]</i></p><p>In the year 1512, on the 22nd of June, between the 17th and 18th hour P.M., was born at Bremen Consul Lord Daniel von Büren — who in the year [15]62 defended the party of Doctor Albert Hardenberg, or of Calvin, against the rest of the consuls, and thereby stirred up the greatest disturbances for the whole City. He was elected in the year [15]39 into the Senatorial order, and afterward in the year [15]47 [elected] Consul; but at the beginning of the year [15]92, resigning the office of the Consulate on account of decrepit age, in the year [15]93, about the 28th of June, seized with sickness, on the 10th of July, after the fifth hour of the morning, he fell peacefully asleep.</p><p>In the year 1565, on the 24th of November, between the 10th and 11th hour in the afternoon, was born to the aforesaid Lord Consul a daughter, Margaretha von Büren; but in the year [15]94, on Thursday [♃], the 16th of May, seized by a raging pleurisy, she died on the 23rd of May.</p><p>In the year 1539, on the 22nd of July, about the fifth hour of the morning, was born at Bremen Lord Theodoricus von Reden, who in the year [15]76, in autumn, was elected into the Senatorial order; he was a man of great stature and corpulent, and had two wives, with whom he received very great inheritances. This man, in the year [15]97, on the first of June, in the morning, seized with sickness, and taking pills from his own kinsman, Doctor Johann Baptista Servetius, physician, straightway in the Doctor's arms exchanged life for death.</p><p>In the year 1551, on the 10th of November, at the 17th hour after noon, was born at Bremen Wichmann Middelstorp. In the year [15]75, on the 16th of February, he collapsed dangerously to the ground, and was compelled to employ the executioner, by name Dionysius, to treat him; in the year [15]86, about the feast of Easter, in the house of Consul Luder von Belmers — [the latter] having piously died — he fell from a height into a ship, and had the ship not been there, he must needs have perished in the Weser. At length, at the beginning of the year [15]92, seized with various kinds of sicknesses, and on the 11th of June again dangerously laid low upon the ground, and so detained by sickness until the year [15]93, on the 8th of October, in the night following, he fell peacefully asleep, though many remedies had been applied in vain.</p><p>In the year 1558, on the 25th of August, after the twelfth hour of noon, was born at Frankfurt an der Oder Meimarus von Borken, Secretary of this City, since his parent, Doctor Johann von Borken, was of the patrician stock of our City. This man, in the year [15]83, on Wednesday [☿] in the evening after the feast of the Annunciation of Mary, at Bederkesa, the castle of this City, broke his leg. In the year [15]87, after the feast of Bartholomew the Apostle, he lay sick unto death of a hot [feverish] disease. In the year [15]91, on the 4th of August, from an excessive over-indulgence in wine the day before, spitting blood from his mouth, he died in a frenzy.</p><p>In the year 1566, on the 21st of July, at the 7th hour P.M., was born at Bremen Hildemann Deddeken, who in the year [15]90, after the feast of Pentecost, was shot through with a [musket-]ball by pirates at sea.</p><p>In the year 1552, on the 7th of February, about the sixth hour in the afternoon, was born at Bremen Lord Gerhard Gerken, a distinguished Musician, no ordinary friend of mine, but an enemy of Astrology. In the year [15]81 he was taken on as Secretary of the City, and in the year [15]93, on the 3rd of July, elected Senator. In the year [15]91, on the Tuesday after Quasimodogeniti [the Sunday after Easter], and again on the fourth of August, he lay dangerously ill; afterward, in the year [15]94, on the 28th of November, again seized [with illness] for some months, and restored to health beyond hope. At length, in the year [15]96, after the feast of Pentecost, seized anew and detained by a long sickness, on the day of St Michael the Archangel, between the 10th and 11th hour before noon, in my presence, he peacefully passed away, though aids had been applied in vain by two Doctors of Medicine of the City. It is a wonder that he did not attain to a decrepit age, since he had the Moon [☾] in conjunction with Jupiter [♃] in its own house, in the eleventh [house], free from the malefics, and the Ascendant unafflicted.</p><p>This good man's wife, Brigitta Gröninges, was born at Bremen in the year 1559, on the 11th of May, Thursday [♃] before the feast of Pentecost, between the 7th and 8th hour in the morning; but in the year [15]98, the plague raging, fleeing betimes from the City into a garden, on the 6th of August, in the garden, she was seized by the plague; on the 12th of the same [month], between the 1st and 2nd hour of the night following, she fell peacefully asleep — nor were remedies lacking to her. And this honest matron had the Moon in Cancer [♋] ascending, in Trine to Jupiter [♃], [Jupiter] being in Pisces [♓].</p><p>In the year 1555, on the 24th of March, between the 2nd and 3rd hour in the afternoon, was born at Bremen Tibbeke, wife of Alexander Bikkers, who in the year [15]96, on the 26th of February, seized by a raging pleurisy, lost a dear daughter, and was herself restored to health. But afterward, in the same year, on the 4th of September, from grief for her dead daughter, she was reduced to insanity, and most wretchedly detained [in that state] until the feast of Easter of the year [15]97, at which she returned to health. But truly in the year [15]98, about the 10th of September, seized again by the plague, on the 15th of the same [month], at the 2nd hour of the night following, she died.</p><p>In the year 1566, on Monday [☾], the 24th of June, between the 1st and 2nd hour of the night, P.M., was born to Lord Johann Schulten, Senator of this City, a son, Fransz. This little son, in the year [15]71, on Wednesday [☿] the 20th of June, at the fifth hour in the afternoon, was pushed by a certain woman, passing by him, from the high eastern bridge into the City moat, but was saved by his Father beyond all hope — who himself very nearly perished along with him. His father having afterward died, in the year [15]86, in autumn, at Amsterdam, on account of squandering his goods with harlots, and in the year [15]90, on the first of November, by the Magistrate — his Mother herself requesting it — for the same cause he was, a second time, cast into prison. The year having elapsed, he set out for Italy; whether he is still living or not is uncertain.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I, L. R., was born at Bremen in the year 1551, on the 15th of September, after the twelfth hour of the night following; in my boyhood endless misfortunes befell me. In the year [15]73, on the 14th of March in the night following, at Brunswick I was seized with a most burning fever, and in the year [15]78, on the 3rd of November, again with a singular kind of diseases and with quinsy. In the year [15]73, on the 13th of May, I took a wife. In the year 1578, on 16 February, between the 6th and 7th hour of the evening, my son Gregorius was born to me, and he, from his earliest age until the year [15]95, was afflicted with various kinds of diseases. In the year 1579, on the 26th of December, between the 3rd and 4th hour of the night following, my second son Hieronimus was born to me, who enjoys (praise God) better and firmer health. In the year [15]82, on the 15th of July, a little after the 12th hour of the night following, my third son Christianus was born to me. He, in the year [15]83 about the feast of [St] James, and in the year [15]84, on 3 April, lay sick unto death, but afterwards likewise enjoys good health. And since I do not doubt that Your Generosity has the places of the planets restored for my own nativity and my sons', that I might therefore the more make a test of the art's certainty, I again and again reverently and earnestly ask that Your Generosity deign kindly to share with me the Restitutions of the same. And although I do not see that I can repay Your Generosity anything for these and all the other benefits conferred on me, undeserving, nevertheless those who are dear to Your Generosity will find me bound and most ready for every service. The notations of the days are taken from the Julian Calendar.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Ludolf Riddershusen (Riddershusius)</b> — a Bremen astrological student/practitioner who treats Tycho as his \"Preceptor\" — <b>to Tycho</b> at Prague.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho as astrological authority, and a death-rumour.</b></li><li><b>The \"Tycho is dead\" rumour.</b> A notable biographical datum: <b>a rumour of Tycho's death circulated at Bremen and Hamburg around Carnival 1599</b> (\"his departure from this vale of tears\"), terrifying Riddershusen — corrected only \"some days ago\" by <b>David Fabricius's</b> letter reporting Tycho safe at Prague and favoured by the Emperor. (Fabricius is the link — cf. his recruitment in brahe-t_15990922001.)</li><li><b>A genuine astrological consultation (seven questions).</b> The body is a window on <b>Tycho's reputation as an astrological authority</b> and on late-Renaissance technical astrology. Riddershusen first recapitulates Tycho's earlier \"Resolutions\" on <b>primary directions</b> — endorsing the <b>Regiomontanus</b> method of directing but preferring the <b>Campanus house-division</b> (as \"universal\"), with an <b>equal ecliptic partition</b> \"in imitation of the ancient astrologers\" — then poses seven questions: (1) a worked scheme of the directing operation; (2) zodiacal-pole vs equatorial-pole direction and the time-measure; (3) <b>the relocation problem</b> — whether to direct the Ascendant from the <b>birthplace</b> latitude (51°) or the <b>place of long residence</b> (54°), and <i>why</i> birthplace dominates; (4) the predictability of harmful <b>Saturn/Mars transits</b>; (5) whether <b>benefics can cancel malefic directions</b>; (6) the worst configurations for natural vs <b>violent death</b>; (7) why charts and outcomes so often diverge.</li><li><b>The \"Octogenary division\" and Elias Olai.</b> Riddershusen cites the <b>\"Diary of Elias Olai the Cimbrian\" (Elias Olsen Morsing</b>, Tycho's former Hven assistant and diarist) for an <b>\"Octogenary [80-fold] division\"</b> of charts by Sun–Moon conjunction/opposition/square — and asks its rationale. <i>(A trace of Tycho's Hven astrological/meteorological diary tradition.)</i></li><li><b>The lunar tables.</b> Riddershusen says he \"can do nothing in astrology <b>without the restitution of the Moon's motion</b>\" and asks whether Tycho's lunar <b>Restitution is yet published</b> — echoing the same demand from Magini (brahe-t_16010220001) and the lunar work referenced to Scaliger (brahe-t_16000723001).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The Sun/Moon/Saturn/Mars and aspect glyphs (☉ ☾ ♄ ♂ ☌ ☍ □) are reproduced as transcribed. Notes the <b>death-rumour</b> and the <b>Elias Olsen Morsing octogenary-division</b> datum.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Ludolf Riddershusen; <b>David Fabricius</b> (the bearer of the good news); <b>Regiomontanus</b>, <b>Campanus</b> (house systems); <b>Elias Olai / Elias Olsen Morsing</b> (the \"Cimbrian,\" Tycho's diarist); <b>Rudolf II</b>. Places: <b>Bremen</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Knudstrup</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Bremae 16 Xbris [Decembris] Anno 99</i> → <b>16 December 1599 [Old Style]</b> (Bremen reckoning; no <i>stylo</i> marker; label gives no N.S.). Header read Old Style.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing genealogical postscript (Riddershusen's own + his three sons' nativities, with a request to compute their restitutions; \"ex Calendario Iuliano\"), restored from Dreyer VIII p.221 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>What was recovered.</b> The lost interior passage is Riddershusen's own appended dossier of eleven dated Bremen birth-and-death notices (consuls, a secretary, a musician-senator, and their wives/children), several carrying explicit astrological configurations (Moon–Jupiter aspects, Ascendant condition). It sits between the signed close of the main letter (\"Ludolphus Riddershusius\") and the already-published autobiographical postscript beginning \"Ego L. R. Anno 1551...\" — i.e. it is Riddershusen practising exactly what he asks Tycho for earlier in the same letter: \"certain reliable Observations in genitures,\" supplied as raw test-data for the astrological questions posed above.</li><li><b>Elias Olai Cimber, identified.</b> The \"Diario Eliæ Olai Cimbri\" cited for the \"Octogenary division\" (question three, already-published portion) is almost certainly Elias Olsen Morsing's <i>Diarium astrologicum et meteorologicum anni 1586</i> — the first book printed at Tycho's own Uraniborg press, by Tycho's own assistant (\"Elias Olai Cimber, discipulus Tychonis,\" per the Opera Omnia index of correspondents). Riddershusen is thus citing back to Tycho a work by a member of Tycho's own household.</li><li><b>Two fabricated dates corrected.</b> The previously published partial translation supplied \"the 15th of September\" (for Riddershusen's own 1551 birth) and \"15 July\" (for his son Christianus's 1582 birth). The complete source text given for this retranslation has no day-number in either place (\"Anno 1551 die Septembris\"; \"Anno 82 Iulij\"). Both are corrected here to reflect the actual source, with the gap marked; the origin of the earlier \"15\"s is unexplained and should be checked against the manuscript or a critical edition before being reused.</li><li><b>Bremen persons.</b> Daniel von Büren, Theodoricus von Reden, Wichmann Middelstorp, Meimarus von Borken, Hildemann Deddeken, Gerhard Gerken and their households are purely local Bremen civic figures (consuls, senators, a City secretary) not otherwise identified in the consulted corpus (Christianson, Dreyer, Thoren); confirming them would require Bremen municipal/archival sources outside our current holdings.</li><li>Crux: \"in lla\" in the Gerken entry, read here as \"in the eleventh [house]\" (i.e. undecima domo), but the abbreviation is not certain.</li><li>Crux: \"Mirum, quidni ad decrepitam pervenerit ætatem\" (Gerken entry) is rhetorically ambiguous; rendered as ironic — Gerken in fact died at about forty-four despite the favourable Moon-Jupiter configuration Riddershusen describes, which is presumably the point being made.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer, TBOO VIII pp.215-222. Restored two dropped day-numbers silently missing from the source text: 'die 15 Septembris' (Riddershusen's own birth, p.221) and 'Anno 82 15 Iulij' (birth of his son Christianus, p.221), both translated into the English, removing the 'day not given in the source' hedges. Also corrected three cosmetic corruptions against Dreyer's text with no effect on the English: the German address heading 'HERNNTICHONI' to 'HERRN TICHONI' (p.216), 'locum' to the plural 'loca' (p.221), and 'comæ pensare' to the single word 'compensare' (p.221).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>death-rumour</b> at Bremen/Hamburg corrected by <b>David Fabricius</b>; the recapitulation of Tycho's <b>Regiomontanus/Campanus</b> directing doctrine; the <b>seven technical astrological questions</b> — including the relocation/latitude problem, Saturn/Mars transits, benefic mitigation, violent-death configurations; the <b>Elias Olsen Morsing \"Octogenary division\"</b>; and the request for Tycho's <b>published lunar tables</b>) is firmly construed, with the technical astrological terminology rendered faithfully and no sharpening. The astrological glyphs are reproduced; the abbreviated signature <i>Ridders[husius]</i> is editorially completed.</p><p>The already-published main body (the astrological questions to Tycho) is reused essentially unchanged, since it was already sound. The newly recovered Bremen chronicle is grammatically straightforward, formulaic necrological Latin, and all dates, hours, and astrological symbols have been preserved exactly as given in the source; confidence there is high apart from the two localized cruxes flagged above (\"in lla\" and the \"Mirum, quidni\" clause) and the unidentified Bremen persons. The two corrected dates are a substantive, load-bearing fix: the earlier translation's \"15th\"s are not supported by the full source text supplied for this retranslation and should not be reused without checking the manuscript or a critical edition directly. Uncollated against a published edition; grade B rather than A on that basis, notwithstanding the input metadata's prior A grade.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.215-222 as page images in three passes (1-4, 5-8, and a close re-read of p.218 and p.221) and collated the full letter plus its appended Bremen-births/deaths dossier line by line against source_text and published_english. Every name, date, and number in the biographical dossier (Daniel von Büren, Margaretha von Büren, Theodoricus von Reden, Wichmann Middelstorp, Meimarus von Borken, Hildemann Deddeken, Gerhard Gerken, Brigitta Gröninges, Tibbeke/Alexander Bikkers, Johann Schulten/Fransz) checked out exactly against Dreyer, and the astrological-questions section (Regiomontanus/Campanus, the 51/54-degree example, the Octogenary conjunction/opposition/square of Sun and Moon, Saturn/Mars transits) also checked out — the translation of the substantive content is faithful, with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found. However, our source_text itself departs from Dreyer in five places, all in the autobiographical closing section on p.221: two dropped day-numbers ('15' before Septembris and before Iulij) that silently remove dates Dreyer actually gives (the translator honestly flagged these as 'day not given in the source' rather than inventing them, but the information loss is real and traces to our source_text, not the translator), plus three minor corruptions (a glued/transposed address heading, 'locum' for Dreyer's 'loca', and 'comæ pensare' for Dreyer's 'compensare'). Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of the translation's own fidelity.</p><p><b>5 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text glues and transposes the two words 'HERRN TICHONI' into 'HERNNTICHONI'; Dreyer prints them as two clean words. Cosmetic — English correctly renders 'Lord Tycho Brahe' regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>HERRN TICHONI BRAHE (p.216, address heading)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer gives the day as '15 Septembris'; our source_text has silently dropped the '15'. The English translator noticed the gap and flagged it honestly ('day not given in the source') rather than fabricating, but the published sentence still omits a date Dreyer actually prints. Dreyer prints: <i>Ego L. R. Anno 1551 die 15 Septembris post horam 12 noctis sequentis Bremæ sum natus (p.221, line 1)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Same fault pattern as the previous entry: Dreyer reads 'Anno 82 15 Iulij' (15 July); our source_text drops the '15'. Again honestly flagged in translation rather than fabricated, but the date is genuinely recoverable from Dreyer and is missing from our text. Dreyer prints: <i>Anno 82 15 Iulij paulo post horam 12 noctis sequentis natus est mihi filius tertius CHRISTIANUS (p.221, line 10)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer has plural 'loca' (agreeing with the participle 'restituta'); our source_text has singular 'locum', a minor grammatical corruption. Does not affect the English rendering, which is correct either way. Dreyer prints: <i>quin Generositas Vestra loca planetarum ad natalem meum et filiorum meorum restituta habeat (p.221, line 14-15)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's single word 'compensare' (to repay) is split/garbled into 'comæ pensare' in our source_text. The translator correctly inferred the intended sense ('repay') despite the corruption, so the English is unaffected, but the source_text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>quicquam compensare non videam (p.221, line 18)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All five defects were verified directly against page images of Dreyer, TBOO VIII pp.215-222 (the full slice for this letter), read in two 4-page chunks. Both date defects and the 'loca'/'compensare' corruptions appear on p.221 exactly as the audit's `dreyer` field states; the address-heading defect is confirmed on p.216. No defect was rejected — the audit's claims all match the printed edition. Nothing outside these five defect sites was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000420002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000420002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for past benefits and explains a year's silence caused by domestic troubles and a false rumour of his death circulating at Carnival.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991216001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991219001", "d": "1599-12-19", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Christophorus Henniochus", "ck": "Christophorus Henniochus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Christoph Henniochus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 19 December 1599 [O.S.] (catalogued Wittenberg)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET GENEROSO TYCHONI BRAHAe, DOMINO IN KNUSTORPH ET HUENA, DOMINO ET FAUTORI SUO, OMNI OFFICIJ GENERE COLENDO.</p><p>LITERIS tuis, Magnifice et Generose Do[mine] TYCHO, instructus, in cognitionem ex CHYTRAeO et alijs praesentis tui status et conditionis veni, de quo me certiorem factum esse summopere gaudeo. Gratulor etiam tibi de tanto Moecenate, opere et studio tuo digno, quo non solum tibi prospectum, sed toti posteritati plurimum inserviri intelligo. Chymicus Silesius, quo de antea ad te perscripsi, Sittae in Silesia degit, vocaturque IACOBUS KINDLER, olim ibidem vel in vicinia scholae Cantor. Artificium, quod illum celebrem facit, describitur in lib. Vexationum PARACELSI sub titulo Haec est ars, cursumque sphaerae Saturninae vocant, quo omnes planetae terrestres, mediante Saturno et uno minerali adiecto, exiguo negocio in lapides vertuntur, qui deinceps nulla vi ignis aut vulcano quantumvis torvo, postliminium perveniunt, ↄ: in metalla redeunt. Huius revolutionis spectator ipse fui atque dirigere scio, sed finem, quem author ibidem promittit, assequi ignoro. Literas tuas FRANCISCO SCHRODERO Sundensi inscriptas, quotquot ad me pervenerunt, summa fidelitate isthic misi, nec non ad responsionem illum adhortatus sum, ac suam taciturnitatem illi exprobravi, ad quod mihi nuper respondit, tibi quoque suis literis Witebergam delatis responsum esse. Sero forte, quod tuus discessus illinc praevenerit illud, aut quicquid intervenerit.</p><p>Nova, quae hîc habemus, ex adiuncto scripto cognosces. Alias forma Academiae et Reipub. eadem est, quam reliquisti; discursum Suecicum tibi quoque misissem, sed nulla hîc exemplaria, ut qui Stockholmiae impressus est, prostant.</p><p>Vale, nec non qui tibi curae sunt, et me inter tuos familiares retineto.<br>Data Rostochij 19. Decembris anni 1599.<br>T. Magnif. studiosiss.<br>Christopho. Henniochus,<br>Medicus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and generous Tycho Brahe, lord in Knudstrup and Hven, his lord and patron, to be honoured with every kind of office.</b></p><p>Instructed by your letter, magnificent and generous Master Tycho, I came into knowledge of your present status and condition — from Chytraeus and from others — about which I greatly rejoice to have been made more certain. I also congratulate you on so great a Maecenas [patron], worthy of your work and study, by which I understand that not only have you been provided for, but the whole of posterity is greatly served. The Silesian chemist, about whom I wrote to you before, lives at Zittau [Sitta] in Silesia, and is called <b>Jacob Kindler</b>, formerly Cantor of the school there or in the vicinity. The art which makes him famous is described in the <i>Book of Vexations</i> of Paracelsus, under the heading \"This is the art,\" and they call it the <i>course of the Saturnine sphere</i>, by which all the terrestrial planets [the metals], by means of Saturn [lead] and one mineral added, are with little trouble turned into stones — which thereafter, by no force of fire, or by however grim a Vulcan, \"come back from exile,\" that is, return into metals. Of this revolution I was myself a spectator, and I know how to direct it; but the end which the author there promises, I know not how to attain. Your letters inscribed to Franz Schröder of Stralsund, as many as reached me, I sent thither with the utmost fidelity, and also exhorted him to a reply, and reproached him with his silence; to which he lately answered me that an answer had also been [given] to you, by his letters carried to Wittenberg — late, perhaps, because your departure thence forestalled it, or [because] something intervened.</p><p>The news which we have here, you will learn from the enclosed writing. Otherwise the form of the Academy and the commonwealth is the same as you left it; I would also have sent you the Swedish discourse, but there are no copies here — being one that was printed at Stockholm, [where such things] are on sale.</p><p>Farewell — and likewise those who are a care to you — and keep me among your familiars.<br>Given at Rostock, 19 December, in the year 1599.<br>Most zealous of Your Magnificence,<br>Christoph Henniochus,<br>Physician.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Christoph Henniochus</b> (<i>Medicus</i>, a physician) — the alchemical correspondent of brahe-t_16000329003 (Tycho's reply, 29 March 1600). This letter (19 Dec 1599) is the one Tycho there acknowledges as \"brought by my son at the beginning of this year,\" and it is here that Henniochus <b>identifies the Silesian alchemist</b> Tycho asked after.</li><li><b>The alchemist identified.</b> <i>Chymicus Silesius … Sittae in Silesia degit, vocaturque Iacobus Kindler, olim … scholae Cantor</i> — the man is <b>Jacob Kindler</b>, living at <b>Zittau</b> (<i>Sitta</i>; in Upper Lusatia, near Silesia), a former school <b>Cantor</b>. His \"art\" is the <b>[pseudo-]Paracelsian <i>Liber Vexationum</b></i> process called the <i>cursus sphaerae Saturninae</i> (\"course of the Saturnine sphere\"): the <b>metals</b> (<i>planetae terrestres</i> — the seven \"planets\" = the seven metals of alchemy) are, \"by means of <b>Saturn</b> [= lead] and one added mineral,\" turned into \"stones\" and then back into metals (<i>postliminium</i> = \"[returning] from exile,\" a Roman-law term for restored status). Henniochus witnessed the operation and can run it, but cannot reach the <i>finis</i> (the promised goal). The symbol <b>ↄ:</b> = <i>id est</i> (\"that is\"). Reinforces the Tychonic alchemy thread (cf. brahe-t_15860301001, …16000329003).</li><li><b><i>Maecenas</b></i> = Tycho's new imperial patron (Rudolf II); Henniochus had learned of Tycho's new footing \"from <b>Chytraeus</b> and others\" (David Chytraeus of Rostock, then in his last year). <i>Franciscus Schröderus Sundensis</i> = <b>Franz Schröder of Stralsund</b>, the delinquent correspondent of brahe-t_16000329003. <i>discursus Suecicus</i> = a \"Swedish discourse\" (a political pamphlet on the Swedish succession war) printed at <b>Stockholm</b>.</li><li><b>⚠ Place of writing.</b> The dateline reads <i>Data Rostochij</i> = <b>Rostock</b>; the dataset catalogues the letter under <b>Wittenberg</b>. The internal references are mixed — Henniochus forwarded Tycho's letters via <b>Wittenberg</b> and reports that \"the form of the Academy … is the same as you left it\" (Tycho had wintered at Wittenberg in 1599) — which may be why the catalogue says Wittenberg; but the <b>as-written dateline (Rostock)</b> governs the header, with the discrepancy flagged. Either way the place is <b>Lutheran = Old Style</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>19. Decembris anni 1599</i> = <b>19 December 1599, Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label's date (the place differs, as noted).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substance is clear and valuable (the identification of the Silesian alchemist <b>Jacob Kindler of Zittau</b> and his <i>Liber Vexationum</i> \"Saturnine\" process; Schröder of Stralsund; the Stockholm \"Swedish discourse\"), dovetailing with Tycho's reply brahe-t_16000329003. Grade [B] for the <b>Rostock-vs-Wittenberg</b> place discrepancy (dateline vs catalogue and internal Wittenberg references), flagged and resolved in favour of the dateline.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation (Rostock-vs-Wittenberg place) resolved to the dateline; persons identified, substance clear, no textual crux.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.220-223 as page images. Letter 'MAGNIFICO ET GENEROSO TYCHONI BRAHÆ, DOMINO IN KNUSTORPH ET HUENA...' runs from the foot of p.221 to p.222, dated 'Data Rostochij 19. Decembris anni 1599.' source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim. Collated opening/closing, all proper names (Chytraeus, Jacob Kindler, Zittau/Sittae, Franz Schröder of Stralsund/Sundensi, Wittenberg, Stockholm) and the alchemical jargon (the 'Saturnine sphere', terrestrial planets/metals) against the Latin; all accurately rendered. One clause about the Swedish discourse's unavailability carries an interpretive bracketed gloss ('[where such things] are on sale') that is clearly marked as supplied and does not contradict the source — per the brief's instruction to ignore bracketed supplements, not counted as a defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Christophorus Henniochus", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000329003", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000329003"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Congratulates Tycho on his imperial patronage and describes Jacob Kindler, a Silesian alchemist at Zittau practising a Paracelsian art of transmuting metals into stones.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991219001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_15991231001", "d": "1599-12-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Jiřice", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg — Girsitz [Jiřice], 31 December 1599 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO GEORGIO HERWARTO AB HOHENBURGK, SERENISSIMO UTRIUSQUE BAVARIAe DUCI A CONSILIJS INTIMIS, PRAeSIDI SCHWABAe ET STATUUM PROVINCIALIUM CANCELLARIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO. MÜNCHEN.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>POSTQUAM priores, circa medium Novembris ad te, Vir Nobilissime et Praestantissime, directas, et paulisper, donec in Aulam eas mittendi atque inde ad te procurandi suppeteret commoditas, mecum retentas, tandem cum adiunctis chartis emisissem, accidit, ut aliae tuae eodem ferme tempore, cum meae illae prolixiores scriptae, mihi redderentur, idque primum paulo ante solstitium hybernum instantibus ferijs Natalitijs, quae licet breviusculae fuerint, plurimum tamen mihi acceptae, cum quod te in amicitia nostra sic, donec aliter liceat, continuanda, perseverantem nec scribendi occasionem ullam intermittentem ostenderent; tum quia te patienter moram meam in rescribendo ferre (siquidem nulla in ijs usus esses, quod non immerito facere licuisset, expostulatione) inde colligerem, utut caussae huius tarditatis tibi necdum perspectae erant, quas te nunc acceptis meis cognovisse atque ratas habuisse mihi polliceor.</p><p>Accessit et hoc, quod literae illae tuae steriles non fuerint, sed triplices in Sole Cassellis factas tribus diversis, attamen continuis annis sedulas observationes easdemque Geometrice et per numeros pro eruendo inde Apogaeo et Eccentricitate Solari expensas, foecundo partu secum adferrent. Fuit equidem id mihi admodum gratum, cum animadversiones illas prius non viderim. Neque enim a laudatissimae memoriae GULIELMO Principe Landtgravio aut etiam eius Mathematico CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO mihi antea (quod omissum miror) communicatae erant, alias Epistolarum Tomo primo, qui literas ijsdem annis inter nos commutatas habet, eas unâ cum competente disquisitione et cum nostris inventis collatione inseruissem, ut ob id probe a te factum, quod vel ex hac omissione coniecturam non fallacem conceperis, eas prius ad manus meas non pervenisse; eoque rectius et acceptius mihi nunc impertiris.</p><p>Multum autem me torsit, quod prima introspectione animadverterim, Apogaeum quidem Solis cum nostro utcunque congruere. Nam dimidij gradus differentia discerni in tam subtili Pragmateia difficulter potest. Nec etiam sensibiliter quid officit, siquidem iuxta ipsum Apogaeum non nisi unico minuto longitudinem Solis variet, in caeteris locis adhuc minus. Verum ubi Eccentricitatem trifariam investigatam et ubique quasi in ipsis secundis sibi constantem conspexissem, a nostro nihilominus deficere quasi 6 minutis de 60 partibus semidiametri Eccentrici, mirum in modum, quid sub his scrupulis lateret scrupuli, haesitavi. Cum enim revisis meis Observationibus, quas circa eosdem dies in Uraniburgo nostro Danico habueram, et supputato simul loco Solis e nostro restituto calculo, quae Eccentricitatem praesupponit part. 2 m. 9 ad minimum, viderem longitudinem ☉ ubique intra unicum minutum illi congruere, quam observata Cassellana tribuerunt, nec possibile esset, ex ijsdem datis tam prope consentientibus, adhibito non fallaci demonstrationis processu, qualem tuum quoque esse cognovi, tam diversimodam nihilominus prodire Eccentricitatem, quid taciti sese insinuasset obstaculi, non ita facile ab initio patuit. Nam et alia ab ea, qua tu usus, ratione, per prostaphaereses nimirum, repetito tuo pro maiori parte calculo; visus est is recte se habere. Nec poterat refractionis insinuatio quae in primo exemplo, ubi ☉ 27 proxime gradibus 2 min. in ultimo, Sole tertium ♒ gradum transeunte, et 19 saltem gradibus elevato eandem 5 minutorum ingerebat (ut de intermedio, in quo haec diversitas minus sensibilis erat, nihil addam) nostram intentionem turbare; siquidem loca ☉ in Ecliptica cum nostris numeris adeo prope consentientia, ROTHMANNUM non saltem Parallaxes Solis sed et refractiones, de quo aliquoties a me (uti ex Epistolis a te citatis liquet) admonitus erat, solerter praecavisse, satis probarunt. Neque etiam, quod Eclipticae obliquatio maxima dimidio scrupulo minor in calculo transmisso assumpta sit, quam a nobis praesupposita, tam evidentem in Eccentricitate hac discrepantiam parere potuit, sicuti neglecta temporis aequatio insensibilem variationem suggerere. Circumspecte itaque omnia (uti tunc mihi videbar) in tuis demonstrationibus et superstructis numeris trutinando, et meas quoque rationes, quae ab Aequinoctijs et medietate ♉ atque ♌ in Eccentricitate atque Apogaeo ☉ eruendis paulo adhuc maiore tam compendio quam certitudine procedunt, denuo sub calculi incudem revocando, nihilque in ijs aberratum esse comprobando, abieci, tanquam mihi ipsi non satisfaciens, hanc aliquantisper disquisitionem; qua tamen peractis sacris Natalitijs denuo in manus resumpta et toto tuo calculo a principio usque in finem (quod antea circa extrema neglectum erat, ex quo caetera tam belle constarent) tandem inventum est, in quo latuerit huius discrepantiae nodus. Cum enim circa ipsum calculi exitum, quando ex radice quadrata, quae in omnibus tribus exemplis sibi ferme similis est, atque in numerum 3591 quam proxime conspirat, perventum est, ut inde Eccentricitatis quantitas eliceretur, depraehendimus, quod per incuriam aliquam, quod facile inter gravissimas tuas occupationes alias fieri potuit, aut si a quopiam alio haec Pragmateia instituta est (neque enim cuius sit indicasti) numerus ille in Tabulam sinuum intromissus quantitatem Eccentricitatis perperam monstrarit, ideoque iusto minorem, cum proportionaliter saltem, prout 60 loco totius radij acciperentur, eruenda foret, sicque eadem quam proxime mecum prodibit Eccentricitatis determinatio; quae etiam in pauculis scrupulis secundis, nullius pene momenti, nostram excedat potius, quam quod 6 minutis ab ea deficiat. Imo si observationes in Sole a nobis Anno 1583 diligenter factitatas in consilium adhibuerimus, eadem cum transmissa inquisitione prodibit Eccentricitas. Nam et nos eandem tunc adinvenimus part. 3592, qualium radius est sive sinus totus 100000, sive part. 2 m. 9⅓, qualium idem 60, Apogaeo etiam tunc in part. 5¾ ♋ reposito; et eodem per septennium usque in Annum 1590, qui medius est trium ex tuis, per additionem 9 minutorum, prout requirit Apogaei mutatio a PTOLEMAeO eousque facta (nam quae in libro modo misso Apogaei notatur progressio aliquanto tardior est, eo quod ex observatione REGIOMONTANI ante 100 Annos facta peculiari consilio saltem derivata sit, uti et Anni quantitas iusto paulo minor assumpta, nec tamen in utrisque his tanta est discrepantia, ut fert COPERNICI ratiocinatio) in ipso scrupulo (quod mirum est) cum Landtgraviano Anni 1590 Apogaeo consentiens. Ut vero promptius et plenius percipias, qua methodo et quibus dedomenis nos et Apogaeum et Eccentricitatem ☉ verificaverimus, atque unde haec minutula differentia in nostra numeratione inter quaesita Anni 1583 et aliorum quorundam postea subsequentium irrepserit, en habes Progymnasmatum nostrorum de Astronomia instaurata Tomum primum, quatenus rerum nostrarum variae conturbationes et vicissitudines eum hactenus typis complere permiserunt. Quali Titulo in publicum brevi, favente coelesti Numine, emittendus, et quae in eodem superaddenda restant, prima circumposita charta manifestat. Illic cap. statim primo haec Solaris curriculi restitutionem concernentia affatim expediunt. Quae de ☾ appendicis loco eidem subnectenda, suo tempore videbis. Secundum caput restitutionem affixarum Stellarum accuratissimam usque in octavum centenarium inde ab ipso fundamento extruit, tandemque canonice recenset. Quibus postea eas, quae desiderantur, usque ad integrum millenarium supplevi. Reliqua voluminis pars de supra modum miraculosa Anni 1572 Stella luculenter tractat. Invenies alias in hoc opere nonnulla ex ijs, quae prius inter nos ventilata sunt, plenius disquisita, et non pauca alia, quae tuo (uti spero) arridebunt ingenio. Consule hunc boni, tibique soli reserva, donec eundem auctiorem et plane absolutum obtineas, unâ cum alijs quibusdam ex meis in hoc genere studiorum laboribus. Sphalmata Typographica, quae nonnulla inter revidendum comperi, tute, qui rem ipsam probe capis, facile restitues. Vide quid pepererint ternae ternorum etiam Annorum Solis observationes mihi a te benevole missae. Si plures in alijs etiam Astris sive Cassellis sive alibi sedulo factas procuraveris, neque illae sine foenore redibunt. Intellexi WERNERUM quasdam a se habitas animadversiones coelestes in publicum êdidisse, quae si praeter eas, quas de aëris mutatione vidi, aliquid circa Phaenomena Astrorum coelitus denotata habent, et tibi de eo libro quidpiam constat, cuperem exemplar mihi aliquantisper concedi.</p><p>Pluribus te nunc morari nolo; sed expecto brevi tam ad priores meas, quam hasce responsionem. Id saltem superaddam, inventionem Apogaei et Eccentricitatis ex Observatis Anni 1583 a me, prout in libro, quem nunc mitto, expositam, mihi magis placere alterâ, quam deinceps ex subsequentibus Annis adhibui; siquidem aequationis temporis ratio isthic applicata, postea vero omissa. Sicque Apogaeum et Eccentricitas e Landtgravianis observatis petita rectius convenient cum nostrâ illâ priori (uti dixi) annotatione, licet differentia, quae alias intercidit, nullius pene sit aestimationis. Quam tamen discrepantiunculam observationes ☉ hîc in Bohemiâ, favente Numine, continuandae facile diriment; siquidem Aequator 6 ferme gradibus hîc altior quam in Daniâ ad quadragesimum Gradum extet, et ob id nulla Solis refractio, quae sensibus obvia sit, iuxta ipsa Aequinoctia (prout in Daniâ sit) sese insinuet.</p><p>Vale itaque quam optime, et Anni huius instantis iubilaei, ac plurium aliorum curricula felici animi et corporis rerumque externarum statu transige.</p><p>Dabantur Girtzitzae, unde cras, volente Deo, Benaticam reverti constitui, pridie Calend. Ianuar. Anni Domini 1600.<br>Tuae Magae addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE manu propria scripsi.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 226; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Apposui calculum Apogaei et Eccentricitatis Solis, in tribus transmissis exemplis per prostaphaereses nobis usitatas reiteratum, qui a tuo vix differt, exceptâ Eccentricitatis, prout in literis monui, emendatione. Adiunxi quoque Odam quandam non invenustam, mihi hisce diebus e Pomeraniâ missam, ut eam caeteris tibi communicatis carminibus annectere queas.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Lord Georg Herwart von Hohenburg, Privy Councillor to the most serene Duke of both Bavarias, President of Swabia, and Chancellor of the Provincial Estates, his Lord and friend especially to be honoured. Munich.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>After I had at last sent off — with the appended papers — my earlier [letter], directed to you about the middle of November (and kept by me a little while, until the convenience of sending it to the Court, and procuring it thence to you, should be at hand), it happened that <b>another letter of yours was delivered me at almost the same time as that more prolix one of mine was written</b> — and that first a little before the winter solstice, with the Christmas holidays at hand. Though it was rather brief, yet it was most welcome to me, both because it showed you persevering in our friendship (thus to be continued, until it may be otherwise), and missing no occasion of writing; and because I gathered thence that you patiently bear my delay in replying — since you used in it no expostulation, which you might not undeservedly have done — although the causes of this tardiness were not yet clear to you; which I trust you have now, on receiving mine, recognised and held excused.</p><p>This was added too, <b>that your letter was not sterile, but brought with it, in a fertile birth, three diligent observations of the Sun made at Kassel in three different but continuous years, and the same worked out geometrically and by numbers, for deriving thence the solar Apogee and Eccentricity.</b> This was indeed very welcome to me, since I had not seen those observations before. For they had not previously been communicated to me by the Landgrave <b>Wilhelm</b>, of most praised memory, nor by his Mathematician <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (which I wonder was omitted); otherwise I should have inserted them in the <b>first Volume of the Epistles</b> (which has the letters exchanged between us in those very years), together with a competent disquisition and a comparison with my own findings. So that it was rightly done by you, that even from this omission you formed a not-false conjecture that they had not reached my hands before; and you the more rightly and acceptably impart them to me now.</p><p><b>But it much tortured me that, at first inspection, I noticed the Sun's Apogee indeed somehow to agree with mine</b> — for a half-degree's difference can with difficulty be discerned in so subtle a practical computation [<i>Pragmateia</i>]; nor does it perceptibly hurt anything, since at the Apogee itself it varies the Sun's longitude by but one minute, in the other places still less. <b>But when I beheld the Eccentricity — investigated three ways, and everywhere constant to itself almost in the very seconds — nonetheless falling short of mine by about 6 minutes of the 60 parts of the Eccentric's semidiameter, I hesitated wondrously, [as to] what scruple lurked beneath these scruples.</b> For when, my own Observations revised (which I had made about the same days at our Danish Uraniborg), and the Sun's place at once computed from our restored calculation (which presupposes an Eccentricity of 2° 9′ at the least), I saw the Sun's longitude everywhere agreeing within a single minute with what the Kassel observations gave — and it was not possible, <b>from the same data so closely agreeing, using a non-fallacious process of demonstration (such as I found yours too to be), for nonetheless so differing an Eccentricity to come out</b> — what silent obstacle had insinuated itself was not so easily clear at first. For by a method other than yours (namely, by <b>prosthaphaereses</b>), repeating your calculation for the most part, it seemed to stand correctly. Nor could the insinuation of <b>refraction</b> disturb my intention — which in the first example, where the Sun [was near 27°], in the last (the Sun passing the third degree of <b>Aquarius [♒]</b>, and elevated only 19°), introduced the same [of] 5 minutes (to say nothing of the intermediate, in which this diversity was less perceptible) — since the Sun's places in the Ecliptic, agreeing so closely with my numbers, sufficiently proved that <b>Rothmann had skilfully forestalled not only the Sun's Parallaxes but also the refractions</b> (about which he had several times been admonished by me, as is clear from the Epistles you cited). Nor could the fact that the maximum obliquity of the Ecliptic was assumed half a scruple less in the transmitted calculation than was presupposed by me produce so evident a discrepancy in this Eccentricity — any more than the neglected equation of time could suggest more than an insensible variation. So, weighing all things circumspectly (as I then thought) in your demonstrations and the numbers built upon them, and recalling my own reckonings too (which proceed — for deriving the Sun's Eccentricity and Apogee from the Equinoxes and the middle of <b>Taurus [♉] and Leo [♌]</b> — with even a little more compendium and certainty) to the anvil of calculation, and proving that nothing had been erred in them, <b>I threw this disquisition aside for a while, as not satisfying myself</b>. Yet, when the sacred Christmas-days were over, taking it again in hand and [going through] your whole calculation from the beginning to the end (which before had been neglected about the extremes, since the rest agreed so nicely), <b>at last it was found wherein the knot of this discrepancy lurked.</b> For around the very exit of the calculation — when, from the square root (which in all three examples is almost similar to itself, and conspires most closely to the number <b>3591</b>), it was reached, so that thence the quantity of the Eccentricity might be elicited — we detected that, <b>by some carelessness (which could easily otherwise happen amid your gravest occupations, or if this computation was instituted by some other — for you did not indicate whose it is), that number, introduced into the Table of sines, wrongly showed the quantity of the Eccentricity, and so [made it] less than just, since it ought to be derived proportionally</b> (taking 60 for the whole radius); <b>and so the same determination of the Eccentricity comes out most closely with mine</b> — exceeding mine, indeed, by a few seconds of almost no moment, rather than falling short of it by 6 minutes. Nay, if the <b>solar observations diligently made by me in the year 1583</b> be brought into counsel, the same Eccentricity comes out as the transmitted inquiry. For I too then found it <b>3592 parts</b> (of which the radius, or total sine, is 100000) — or <b>2° 9⅓′</b> (of which the same is 60) — the <b>Apogee then placed at 5¾° Cancer [♋]</b>; and the same, through seven years to the year <b>1590</b> (which is the middle of your three), by adding <b>9 minutes</b> (as the Apogee's change from Ptolemy down to then requires — for the Apogee-progression noted in the book just sent is somewhat slower, since it was derived by a special method from an observation of <b>Regiomontanus</b> made 100 years before, as also the year-quantity was assumed a little less than just; yet in both these the discrepancy is not so great as Copernicus's reckoning bears) — agreeing <b>in the very minute (which is wonderful) with the Landgrave's 1590 Apogee.</b></p><p>But that you may the more readily and fully perceive <b>by what method and from what data I have verified the Sun's Apogee and Eccentricity</b>, and whence this minute difference in my numeration crept in between the result of 1583 and certain others subsequently following — <b>behold, you have [here] the first Volume of our <i>Progymnasmata</i> of Restored Astronomy</b>, so far as the various disturbances and vicissitudes of my affairs have hitherto permitted me to complete it in print. With what Title it is shortly to be published (Heaven favouring), and what remains to be added in it, the first appended sheet makes manifest. <b>There, in the very first chapter, these things concerning the restitution of the Solar course are amply dispatched.</b> What is to be subjoined about the <b>Moon</b> by way of appendix, you will see in time. <b>The second chapter builds the most accurate restitution of the fixed Stars, up to the eighth hundred, from the very foundation, and at last reviews them canonically; to which I afterward supplied those that are wanting, up to the full thousand. The remaining part of the volume treats clearly of the supremely miraculous Star of the year 1572</b> [the supernova]. You will find, besides, in this work some of the things formerly ventilated between us more fully investigated, and not a few others which will (as I hope) please your genius. Take it in good part, and reserve it to yourself alone, until you obtain the same augmented and quite completed, together with some others of my labours in this kind of study. The typographical errors (of which I found some while revising), you, who grasp the matter itself well, will easily correct. <b>See what those three solar observations of three years, benevolently sent me by you, have produced!</b> If you procure more, diligently made — in other Stars too, whether at Kassel or elsewhere — these too will not return without interest. I understand that <b>Werner</b> has published some celestial observations of his own; if, besides those on the change of weather which I have seen, they have anything concerning the Phenomena of the Stars noted from the heaven, and you know anything of that book, I should wish a copy granted me for a while.</p><p>I do not wish to delay you longer now; but I expect shortly a reply both to my earlier [letter] and to this. This only will I add: that <b>the invention of the Apogee and Eccentricity from the observations of the year 1583 (as set forth in the book I now send) pleases me more than the other which I afterward used from the subsequent years</b> — since the reckoning of the equation of time was applied there, but afterward omitted; and so the Apogee and Eccentricity sought from the Landgrave's observations will agree more rightly with that prior annotation of mine (as I said), though the difference, which otherwise intervenes, is of almost no estimation. This little discrepancy, however, the <b>observations of the Sun to be continued here in Bohemia (Heaven favouring) will easily resolve — since the Equator stands here almost 6 degrees higher than in Denmark, toward the fortieth degree, and on that account no Sun-refraction, perceptible to the senses, insinuates itself near the very Equinoxes (as it does in Denmark).</b></p><p>Fare, then, most well, and pass the course of this coming Jubilee Year, and of many others, in a happy state of mind and body and outward affairs.</p><p>Given at Girsitz — whence tomorrow, God willing, I have resolved to return to Benátky — on the day before the Kalends of January, in the year of the Lord 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I have appended the calculation of the Sun's Apogee and Eccentricity, repeated through the prosthaphaereses used by us in the three transmitted examples, which scarcely differs from yours — save for the correction of the Eccentricity, as I noted in the letter. I have also added a certain Ode, not without charm, sent to me from Pomerania these last days, that you may annex it to the other poems shared with you.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Hans Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (Munich) — addressed with his full Bavarian titles (Privy Councillor, President of Swabia, Estates' Chancellor). The third Tycho↔Herwart letter in the corpus (cf. brahe-t_15990831001 and brahe-t_15990917001; and the later brahe-t_16000911001).</li><li><b>Significance — a forensic analysis of the Kassel solar observations, and the sending of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</b></li><li><b>The Kassel solar data.</b> Herwart had sent <b>three diligent solar observations made at Kassel in three successive years</b> (the <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV / Christoph Rothmann</b> observatory), reduced for the <b>solar Apogee and Eccentricity</b> — never before seen by Tycho (who would else have printed them in his <i>Epistolae</i> vol. I).</li><li><b>The error hunt (a model of Tychonic forensic computation).</b> Tycho found the <b>Apogee agreeing</b> (a half-degree being negligible) but the <b>Eccentricity short of his own by ~6′ of 60</b> — yet the underlying solar longitudes agreed \"within one minute.\" After excluding <b>refraction</b> (Rothmann had guarded against parallax <i>and</i> refraction, on Tycho's earlier urging), <b>obliquity</b>, and the <b>equation of time</b>, he <b>set the puzzle aside over Christmas</b>, then <b>traced the discrepancy to a computational slip</b>: a square-root value (~<b>3591</b>) <b>fed into the sine-table without the proper proportional scaling (radius = 60 vs 100000)</b> — once corrected, the Kassel Eccentricity <b>matches his own</b>. His own values: <b>Eccentricity 2° 9⅓′ (= 3592 of 100000), Apogee 5¾° Cancer in 1583</b>, advancing <b>~9′ over 7 years to 1590</b>, \"agreeing in the very minute with the Landgrave's 1590 Apogee.\" (He notes the <i>Progymnasmata</i>'s slower apogee-progression derives from a <b>Regiomontanus</b> observation a century back.)</li><li><b>The <i>Progymnasmata</i> vol. I sent (a contents-inventory).</b> Tycho sends Herwart the <b>first volume of the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</b></i> as far as printed: <b>ch. 1</b> the solar restitution; an appendix on the <b>Moon</b>; <b>ch. 2</b> the <b>fixed-star catalogue (built to 800, supplied to a full 1000)</b>; the rest on the <b>Star of 1572 (the supernova)</b>. To be kept private until the completed copy. <i>(A datable Progymnasmata presentation to Herwart.)</i></li><li><b>The Bohemia advantage.</b> Tycho notes solar observations are <i>better</i> in Bohemia: the <b>Equator ~6° higher than in Denmark, so no sensible refraction near the equinoxes</b> — promising to resolve the residual discrepancy.</li><li><b>Werner.</b> Tycho asks for a copy of <b>Werner's</b> published celestial observations, if they bear on star-phenomena (not just weather).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pair with the other Herwart letters (brahe-t_15990831001, brahe-t_15990917001, brahe-t_16000911001); the <b>Kassel / Wilhelm IV / Rothmann</b> observational thread; and the <b>Progymnasmata printing/distribution</b> thread (brahe-t_16000107001, brahe-t_15990922001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Herwart von Hohenburg; <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel</b> (the late); <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Regiomontanus, Copernicus</b>; <b>Werner</b> (a published observer); the <b>Star of 1572</b>. Places: <b>Girsitz (Jiřice)</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Kassel</b>, <b>Uraniborg/Denmark</b>, <b>Munich</b>. Glyphs ☉ ☾ ♒ ♉ ♌ ♋ reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Girtzitzae … pridie Calend. Ianuar. Anni Domini 1600</i> — \"the day before the Kalends of January\" = <b>31 December 1599 [N.S.]</b> (slug `15991231001`, label \"31 december n.s.\"; the \"Anni Domini 1600\" reflects the imminent new year). Header and label agree.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (the appended solar apogee/eccentricity calculation + a Pomeranian Ode), restored from Dreyer VIII p.226 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense, highly technical Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Kassel solar-observation analysis</b> — the Apogee agreement, the Eccentricity discrepancy of ~6′, the exclusion of refraction/obliquity/time-equation, and the resolution to a <b>sine-table proportionality error</b> around the value 3591; Tycho's own <b>2°9⅓′ / 5¾° Cancer (1583)</b> solar parameters and the apogee-progression note; the <b>Progymnasmata vol. I contents-inventory</b> and presentation; the Bohemia-refraction advantage; and the Werner request) is firmly and carefully construed, with the numerical and computational detail rendered faithfully. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII page images in chunks (1-4, then 4 again at higher isolation, then 5-7). The letter opens on p.222 (heading 'MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO GEORGIO HERWARTO AB HOHENBURGK...', source note '[E codice Monacensi Bibl. Univ. 693 in 2°]' — p.221 is the tail of an unrelated Riddershusius letter plus a full Hennochius-to-Tycho letter) and closes on p.226 with signature 'TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria scripsi' followed immediately, same page, by the postscript 'Apposui calculum Apogaei et Eccentricitatis Solis...ut eam caeteris tibi communicatis carminibus annectere queas.' and source note '[E codice Basileensi G I 35]'; p.227 begins an unrelated letter (Tycho to Vincentius Pinellius). The packet's editorial note flagging the postscript as 'restored... dropped by the working transcription' is consistent with what Dreyer prints — the postscript is genuinely part of the same letter on the same page, verbatim in both source_text and published_english. Collated all numbers (3591, 3592, 9⅓ arcmin, 100000, 60, Apogee 5¾ Cancer, 1583, 1590, +9 minutes, 6 degrees, 40th degree), names (Wilhelm Landgrave, Christoph Rothmann, Magini, Röslin, Copernicus, Regiomontanus, Ptolemy, Werner), and places (Girsitz/Jiřice, Benátky, Munich, Kassel, Bohemia, Denmark) — all match. One glyph required a second look: Dreyer's small fraction after 'part. 2 m. 9' initially looked like '¾' rather than source's '⅓'; arithmetic check (3592/100000 × 60 = 2.1552 → 2° 9.31′ ≈ 9⅓′, not 9.75′) confirms '9⅓' is correct and Dreyer's glyph was misread on first pass, not a real TEXT_ERROR. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15991116001", "next": "brahe-t_16000403001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990917001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000403001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receiving Herwart's latest brief letter just before the winter solstice and thanks him for patience with delayed replies and continued friendship.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_15991231001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000000001", "d": "1600-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Sigfrid Rindschat", "ck": "Sigfrid Rindschat", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Sigfrid Rindscha[d]t — [Benátky nad Jizerou], 1600 [undated — see Apparatus]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 124 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD SIGFRIDUM RINDSCHAT].</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och altid forsend met Vor Herre. Welbørdig her Seyffert Rindtschadt, gunstige gode wen, nest min wenlig tacksigellße for allt beuist gode, huilket igien att forskylde ieg stedze will findis aff formuen willig, y hues maade ieg kand giøre nogit, som eder till aere och gode kan werre. Giffuendis eder derhos gandske wenligen tillkiende, att eders gode och vellmeente skriffuelße er mig for en 10 eller 12 vger siden tillhende kommen met m. Johanne Keplero, som fuldis hid y dette kongerige Behemen met den welbørdige fryherre, her Johan Friderick Hoffman, eders slecht och byr och min gunstige gode wenn, och war samme eders skriffuelße mig megit kierkommen, saa well som forbeneunte m. Kepler, thi ieg haffde y langthiidt adtraad att wille wijde, hor y war och horledis det gick eder, haffde och, der ieg bode paa Wandeßbechk wed Hamburgk paa itt aars thiid att giørre, førend røm. key. may., voris allernaadigst herre, lodt naadiste kallde mig hid till sig, skreffuit itt breff till eder och sendt det ind vdi Jüdland, y den ein, som ieg haffde meent, atty bode, mens ieg fick beskeen ygien, att y war dragen derfran hen y eder faederne landtt.</p><p>Angaendis forbemelte m. Keplero, war hans ankombst mig ochsaa saare angenehm, thi ieg haffuer vdj lang thiidt holdit megit aff hannom for hans herlige ingenium och skickelligheet skyldt y die mathematische och astronomische konster, som y wijde mig ocsaa att haffue y sønderlighedt løst till, och der hand war kommen till Prag, sende strax derepther forbeneunte her Jehann Friderich Hoffmann hannom hid till mig, och bleff hand saa her hos mig met samme her Hoffmans gode wille och samtøcke ved 2 maaneder. Siden drog handt till her Hoffman vdi Prag ygien och var der hos hannom vdj 2 eller 3 wger, epther hans beger, och der hans welbørdighed war saa dragen hen y itt baadt, for hans sundhedt skyld, lod hand mester Keplerum komme hidt till mig igien, paa det ieg, som tillforn, ydremere kunde conuersere met hannom y die astron[om]ische konster, intill hand kunde bekomme god gelegenhed att drage tillbage vdjgien y Stiermarck, och haffde hand achtedt sig adsteedt did hen y en aff disse dage met første leylighed, som hand kunde bekomme fra Prag.</p><p>Mens midlertiidt er hidkommen till mig aff Danmarck en herremand aff fornemme adell och Rossenkrantz slecht, der y landit børdig, som er och min nehr slecht och bør. Handt er tillsinds att drage ind vdi Østerich, der underdanigst att praesentere sin tienist høyborne erßhertzog Mathias, och siden att lade bruge och forsøye sig y den wngerische krig, och achter hand sig nest Gudz hielp vdj morgen herfran och hen till Pilsen, hor røm. key. may. endnu er, der att forsøye, om handt kand forhuerue en forschrifft till høybemelte ershertzog Matias, att hans først: naade wille thage hannom vdi tieniste, och att hand wille lade hannom werre sig met all gunst och naade befallid. Saa haffuer ieg tallt met samme Rossenkrantz, att hand wille tage mester Keplerum paa wognen met sig till Wien y Østerich och staa hans tering, huilket hand och godwilligen haffuer loffuid att wille giørre for min skyld; haffuer ieg och raad och offuertallt mester Kepler, att hand sin reysse wille forholle till samme herremand kommer hid ygien fra Pilsen, huilket ieg haabis att kunde skee inden 14 dage y det seeniste, saa att hand da kandt følgis med hanom lettelligen och foruden all bekaastning intill Wien. Och endog dette søntis mester Kepler att wille falle for langt, thi hand yller hiem ygien for hues hand haffuer att giøre for herrene y Stirmarkid och for sin høstruff skyld, saa haffuer hand dog omsier herudj beuilliget och eptherfølt mit raad, eptherdi hand formerkte, att ieg mente det gott och troligen met hannom, som han och stedtze herepther skall befinde hos mig, epthersom ieg och mercker hannom att werre wertt affholdid och promouerit till det beste. Kierre herr Syberet, dette kunde ieg icke lade att giffue eder paa denne tiid tillkiende, om m. Keplero, som y kommenderit till mig, paa det y kunde wijde aarsagen, horfor hand bliffuer saa lenge wde, och haffue hannom wndskyldit, baade hos die andre herrer vdj Styrmarket och hos eder selff, och wdj ligge maade lade giffue hans høftruff, naar eder stedis boedt, dette tillkiende. Jeg skriffuer nu icke ydermere suar paa eders skriffuelße till mig for tidsendz korthedt skyld, menß achter det nest Gudz hielp att giørre met m. Kepler, naar hand drager did y landit ygien, thi hand haffuer set sig forre paa weyen att besøge eder, saa achter ieg att suare eder nochsom till alle puncter, som eders breff indholder och enda ydermere. Medlertid beder ieg eder gandske gierne, att y will haffue mig vndskyldid och ochsesaa handle det, saa att det bliffuer icke tagid m. Kepler till mødstøcke, att [hand] bliffuer saa lenge vde. Ydermere aarsag dertill skall y, nest Gudz hielp, faa att wiide y min anden skriffuelße, naar hand ankommer. Och will hermed nu och alltid haffue eder Gud allmechtugste befalid, giører vell och siger eders kierre høstruff, fru Magdalene Munck, mange gode netter paa min och min kierre høstruff och børns weyne.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Bennatickden.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO SIGFRID RINDSCHAT].</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed by Our Lord. Well-born Sir Seyfried Rindschadt, favourable good friend: after my friendly thanks for all the kindness shown me — which to requite I shall always be found willing according to my means, in whatever way I can do anything that may be to your honour and good. Giving you herewith, quite kindly, to know that <b>your good and well-meant letter came to my hands some 10 or 12 weeks ago, with Master Johannes Kepler</b>, who came hither into this Kingdom of Bohemia with the well-born baron, <b>Sir Johann Friderich Hoffmann</b> — your kinsman and townsman, and my favourable good friend — and that same letter of yours was very welcome to me, as was the aforesaid Master Kepler; for I had long desired to know where you were and how it fared with you. I had also — when I dwelt at <b>Wandsbek near Hamburg</b> for about a year, before the Roman Imperial Majesty, our most gracious lord, graciously had me called hither to him — <b>written a letter to you and sent it into Jutland</b>, to the place where I had thought you dwelt; but I received word back that you had moved away thence into your fatherland.</p><p>Concerning the aforesaid <b>Master Kepler</b>, his arrival was likewise very agreeable to me; <b>for I have long thought much of him, for the sake of his splendid genius and aptitude in the mathematical and astronomical arts</b> — in which, as you know, I too have particular delight. And when he had come to Prague, the aforesaid Sir Johann Friderich Hoffmann straightway sent him hither to me; and so <b>he stayed here with me, with the said Sir Hoffmann's good will and consent, about 2 months</b>. Then he went back to Sir Hoffmann in Prague, and was there with him 2 or 3 weeks at his request; and when his lordship had gone off to a [healing] bath for his health's sake, he let Master Kepler come hither to me again, <b>so that I, as before, might further converse with him in the astronomical arts</b>, until he could get good opportunity to travel back again into <b>Styria</b>; and he had intended to set off thither one of these days, with the first occasion he could get from Prague.</p><p>But meanwhile there has come to me from Denmark <b>a gentleman of distinguished nobility and of the Rosenkrantz family</b>, born there in the country, who is also my near kinsman and relative. He is minded to travel into <b>Austria</b>, there most humbly to present his service to the high-born <b>Archduke Matthias</b>, and then to let himself be employed and try himself in <b>the Hungarian war</b>; and he intends, with God's help, tomorrow [to go] from here on to <b>Pilsen</b>, where the Roman Imperial Majesty still is, there to try whether he can obtain a letter of recommendation to the said Archduke Matthias, that his princely Grace would take him into service and let him be commended to him with all favour and grace. So <b>I have spoken with this same Rosenkrantz, that he would take Master Kepler on his wagon with him to Vienna in Austria and bear his expenses — which he has willingly promised to do for my sake</b>; and I have also advised and prevailed upon Master Kepler to hold off his journey until this same gentleman comes here again from Pilsen — which I hope can happen within 14 days at the latest — <b>so that he may then travel with him easily, and without any cost, as far as Vienna</b>. And although this seemed to Master Kepler that it would fall out too long [a delay] — for he hastens home again, on account of what he has to do for the lords in Styria, and for his wife's sake — yet he has at last consented herein and followed my advice, since he perceived <b>that I meant it well and faithfully toward him</b> (as he shall also always find with me hereafter), seeing that I judge him worthy to be esteemed and advanced to the best.</p><p>Dear Sir Syfried, this I could not omit to make known to you at this time, concerning Master Kepler whom you commended to me, so that you might know <b>the reason why he stays away so long, and may have him excused — both with the other lords in Styria and with you yourself</b> — and likewise let his wife know this, when you get a messenger. I do not now write a fuller reply to your letter, for shortness of time, but intend, God willing, to do it through Master Kepler when he travels thither into the country again — for he has resolved to visit you on the way — so that I intend to answer you sufficiently on all the points your letter contains, and even more besides. Meanwhile I beg you quite gladly that you will hold me excused, and also handle it so that <b>it not be held against Master Kepler that he stays away so long</b>. Further reason for it you shall, God willing, get to know in my next letter, when he arrives. And herewith I will now and always have you commended to God Almighty. Fare well, and bid your dear wife, <b>Lady Magdalene Munck</b>, many good-nights on behalf of me and my dear wife and children.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle of Benátky.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Sigfrid / Seyfried Rindscha[d]t</b> (the address varies: <i>Seyffert Rindtschadt</i>, <i>herr Syberet</i>, <i>Sigfrid</i>) — Kepler's patron-connection in <b>Styria/Graz</b>, a kinsman and \"townsman\" of the baron <b>Hoffmann</b>; his wife is <b>Lady Magdalene Munck</b> (a Danish-sounding name, suggesting a Dano-Styrian link that explains Tycho's writing him in Danish). Not further identified here.<br>- <b>Significance — the <i>warm</i> face of the Tycho–Kepler crisis (companion to the bitter Jessenius letter).</b> This Danish draft was written <b>from Benátky in the very same weeks as the angry letter to Jessenius (brahe-t_16000408001, 8 April 1600)</b> — and shows the opposite register entirely. To Kepler's patron, Tycho:<br>  - <b>praises Kepler's \"splendid genius (<i>herlige ingenium</i>) and aptitude in the mathematical and astronomical arts,\"</b> saying his arrival was \"very agreeable\";<br>  - narrates Kepler's stay matter-of-factly (≈2 months with Tycho → 2–3 weeks back with Hoffmann in Prague → returned to Tycho while Hoffmann took a cure);<br>  - <b>arranges and funds Kepler's return to Styria</b> — persuading a visiting <b>Rosenkrantz kinsman</b> (bound for Archduke Matthias's service in the Hungarian war, via Pilsen and Vienna) to carry Kepler <b>on his wagon to Vienna at no cost</b>, and persuading Kepler to wait ~14 days for him;<br>  - asks the patron to <b>excuse Kepler's long absence</b> to \"the other lords in Styria\" and to Kepler's wife, insisting <b>\"I meant it well and faithfully toward him … I judge him worthy to be esteemed and advanced to the best.\"</b><br>  The juxtaposition is the heart of the document: even while venting fury to the mediator, Tycho protects Kepler's standing with his employers and pays his way home. <i>(The reconciliation that followed, and Kepler's eventual succession, are prefigured here far more than in the Jessenius letter.)</i><br>- <b>Biographical details.</b> Confirms Kepler <b>travelled to Bohemia with Hoffmann</b>; the <b>two-month first residence</b> at Benátky; the shuttling to Prague and back; and Tycho's own earlier <b>year at Wandsbek near Hamburg</b> before the imperial call, during which he had tried (in vain) to reach Rindschat by a letter sent into Jutland.<br>- <b>The unnamed Rosenkrantz.</b> \"A gentleman … of the Rosenkrantz family … my near kinsman,\" bound for <b>Archduke Matthias</b> and the <b>Hungarian (Turkish) war</b> — not individually identified; left as given.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Sigfrid/Seyfried Rindschat; <b>Johannes Kepler</b>; <b>Johann Friderich Hoffmann</b> (baron, Kepler's host); <b>Archduke Matthias</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b> (at Pilsen); the unnamed <b>Rosenkrantz</b> kinsman; <b>Lady Magdalene Munck</b> (Rindschat's wife); Kepler's wife (Barbara, unnamed). Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b>/Hamburg, <b>Jutland</b>, <b>Styria</b>, <b>Austria/Vienna</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>.<br>- <b>Date (protocol §3).</b> Undated in the draft (\"Dated at the imperial castle of Benátky,\" no day); slug `16000000001` and label \"Benatky\" carry only the year. <b>Internal evidence</b> — \"10–12 weeks since your letter came with Kepler,\" Kepler now about to leave for Styria, the Rosenkrantz departing \"tomorrow\" for Pilsen — ties it <b>to the same spring-1600 weeks as the 8 April 1600 Jessenius letter</b>; dated here <b>[Benátky, c. April 1600]</b> without inventing a precise day.<br>- <b>Language note.</b> Tycho's <b>Early Modern Danish</b> chancery hand, read securely; spelling is heavily phonetic (<i>ingenium</i>, <i>conuersere</i>, <i>promouerit</i> are his own Latinisms in the Danish). The witness is a <b>draft (Kladde)</b> in the Basel autograph collection.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Source text corrected against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia XIV, page 205: \"Vor Horre\" corrected to \"Vor Herre\" (the standard Danish greeting-formula \"Our Lord\"). The English translation already read correctly and required no change.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Danish is fully legible and construed with confidence; the substance (the warm praise of Kepler's genius; the factual narrative of his Benátky residence with Hoffmann's consent; the arrangement of his funded return to Styria via the Rosenkrantz kinsman; the plea to excuse his long absence to his Styrian patrons and wife; Tycho's protestation of goodwill) is firmly rendered, with no sharpening of the courteous register. The recipient (Rindschat) and the unnamed Rosenkrantz kinsman are left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule; the date is given as a flagged internal-evidence estimate.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images TBOO XIV pp.204-207 (letter runs from the bottom of p.205, entry no.199, through the top of p.207, ending \"Bennatick den\" with Dreyer's own footnote \"Dies deest\" confirming the day-of-month is genuinely missing, matching the title's [undated] flag; p.208 begins a different, unrelated letter, so not needed). Collated every proper name (Kepler, Johan Friderich Hoffmann, the Rosenkrantz kinsman, Archduke Matthias, Magdalene Munck), every number (10-12 weeks, 2 months, 2-3 weeks, 14 days), and every place-name (Wandsbek, Hamburg, Jutland, Prague, Styria, Austria, Pilsen, Vienna, Benátky) against both source_text and the Dreyer image; all match and all are rendered accurately in published_english. One TEXT_ERROR found in source_text itself (see defects) as required by the grading rule; per brief, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it is low-severity and does not affect the sense a scholar would take from the translation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our stored source_text reads \"Vor Horre\", which is not a Danish word; Dreyer's page image clearly prints \"Vor Herre\" (\"Our Lord\"), the standard period greeting-formula. The English translation already renders this correctly as \"Our Lord\", so the corruption is confined to our source_text field and does not propagate into the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>MIN gandfke wenlig hilfen nu och altid forfend met Vor Herre.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The single defect site was collated against Dreyer, Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia volume XIV, page 205 (page image, not the OCR text layer). Confirmed: the opening line reads \"MIN gandfke wenlig hilfen nu och altid forfend met Vor Herre.\" This is a long Danish letter; only the flagged site was checked, per the prime rule of changing only what the listed defect requires. No other cruxes were noted.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 124 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges a letter brought by Kepler on his arrival with Baron Hoffmann, and mentions failed earlier correspondence sent from Wandsbek to Jutland.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000000001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000000002", "d": "1600-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Simon von Petkum", "ck": "Simon von Petkum", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Simon von Petkum — [Benátky nad Jizerou], 1600 [undated — c. February/March; see Apparatus]", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / Low German), with embedded Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 69–72 (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Note.</b> Tycho's <b>reply to Simon von Petkum's letter of 12 Feb 1600 (brahe-t_16000212001)</b>, answering it point by point. A <b>draft (Kladde)</b>; the source breaks off without signature.</p>", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD SIMONEM DE PETKUM]</p><p>MEIN freundtlichen grues mit wunschung alles gutts zuuor, erbar vnd furnemer Symon von Petkum, inbesonders gutter freundt. Eur schreiben hab ich, zu Hamburgk den 12 februarij diesen jhars datirt, bekommen, vnd daneben die beide von magistro Dauide Fabricio vnnd herrn Ritherhausen zu Brehmen. Was nun anlanget die brieffe an mir hero zu bestellen, gilt es mir gleich, ob ihr sie gehn Leipzig vnd von dannen auff Dressden an mein wirt, oder auch auff Nurrenbergk vnd von dannen hieher, oder auff Prag ahn herrn doctor Heller, auff die kleine zeitte wonhafftig, dirigirt, so werde ich sie wohl bekommen, so ferne ihr nicht gelegenheit habt, von dannen dieselbige brieffue an mir directe zu schicken.</p><p>Der her burgmeister Vincentius Mullerus schreibt, das jhre key. may. ahn die herrn von Hamburgk gethanes schreiben jhnen noch nicht geliebert sey, aber wen das gescheen vnd er daraus vernummen, was jhr may. von meinetwegen allergnedigst begerren thutt, will ehr, so vill ihme ihmmer meuglig, helffen die sache promouiren, worfur ich mich gegen ihme thu bedancken, vnd hab ihme selbst weitern bescheidt dauon zugeschrieben, welchen brieff ihr ihme von meinetwegen könnet vberantwortten. Das dieselbige literae caesareae noch nicht alda den herrn sein vberliebert, kan ich gedencken, das die vhrsache sey, das mein factor zu Lübeck, Jochum Boch, den selbigen brieff bej sich behelt, bis er die jnstrumenten neben dem andern gutt gehn Hamburgek schicke, vnd wan das mitlerweill nicht gescheen ist, so wirt ehr es halt thun, wovon ich ime auch geschrieben hab, welchen brieff ihr alßbalt könnet zu ihm schicken. Vnd so balt die instrumenten vnd das ander gutt, so ich zu Lubch hab, gehn Hamburg ist ankommen, so bitt ich freundtlich ihr wollet bei den herren alda soliciterern, das sie wollen die furordnung thun, das man kuntte schiffe dazu bekommen, das es alles sambt was ihr alda habt, so vhnverkaufft ist, köntte ihr key. may. zum gnedigsten gefalln hirauff in Bohemen gefurt werden, vnd so die herren wollen ein von ihren eygenen diener dabey haben, der auff köntte sehen, das alle ding richtig zuginge, wehre es mir sehr angenem, vnd so sie mülstein oder andern wahren, welche alhie leichter alß da bey euch zu bekommen, wieder hin ab füren zu lassen gesint, auff das die schiff nicht ledich zu hauß kemen, köntte derselbige auch darauf auffsehn haben, vnd ich woltte auch gehrn meuglichen fleiß anwenden, das solchs mit dem ehesten vnd mit vortheill ausgericht werde.</p><p>Vnd wie wohl ihr meinet, das nutzlich kuntte sein, das ihr may. noch ein schreibent ließ an den erbarn rath alda der sachen wegen abgehen, so achte ich es vhnvonnötten, dieweill das vorrige schreibent noch verhanden oder villeigt nun schon vberliebert. Ich vorsiehe mich, das die hern von Hamburgk werden sich wohl wissen gehorsamblich nach jhr key. may. ehrsten schreiben wissen zu richten, das es keines mehreren bedarff, vnd das sie keineswegens thun werden wie die Magdaeburger, ahn welchen jhr key. may. zuo mahl von meinetwegen geschrieben, vnd sie gleichwohl subterfugia gefunden vnd die schuere belegerung, so sie vor ein halbhundert jahr gelitten, praesendirt, das sie solchs nicht thun köntten. Welchs jhr may. hart hatt verdrossen, vnd sie den kop darvber schuttelt, vnd zu vhnwillen auffgenohmen. Die rehtte wahren auch gahr spötsch darüber, das sie durfften jhr may. so ein ringes abslagen, vnd hat einer von ihnen, welcher teglich mehr alß andere zu jhr may. zutritt hat, diesse formalia verba darzu gesagt: Wir hetten gemeint, die von Magdeburgk wehren hoffleutte, nuhn vormercken wihr, daß sie bauren sein, es kan wohl die zeit kommen, das sie jhre may. wiederumb bedarffen, alsdan kan mahn wohl ein bessere vnd mehr glaublich enthschuldung vnd praetext finden, alß sie itzunder thun, das sie also repulsam bekommen. Haec ille, audientibus quibusdam allijs, tum quoque meo sollicitatore, dixit, vnd hat alßbalt jhr may. gnedigst befolen, das ein ander brieff wurde geschrieben ad reuerendum capittulum vnd die ehrwerdige dumbherrn zu Magdaeburgk, daß sie solchs jhr may. zum gnedichsten gefallen thun wolten, welchs sie auch sich darzu gantz gehorsamlich erbotten. Vnd ich thu den brieff itzunder zu jhnen verschicken, mallet jam renatus Magdaeburgensis se id officij Caesari non denegasse et nunc libenter, si liceret, obtemperaret, sed sero sapiunt Phryges, Caesar etiam non uult amplius, ut per ipsos id fiat et potius opera reuerendi capituli uti dictum. Wan etzliche brieffue von hern Josepho Scaligero oder sunst auß Niederlant ankommen, oder bescheit von dem ephemeridum compilatore, so last mihr dieselbige mit ehester gelegenheit bekommen, vnd sendet diesen beigelegten brieff ahn magistrum Dauidem Fabricium nebenst dem andern ahn herrn Ritterhusium zu Brehmen; wehn derselbe den ahn Fabricium bekommet, so wirt er in wohl alßbalt gehen Resterhaffuen vorschaffuen. Ich vormercke auß des heern Dauidis Fabrici schreibend das so ehr einige gutte gelegenheit alhie köntte bekomen, das er nicht vhngneigt wehre allhie zu sein, worauff ich im gutten bescheit geschrieben, wolte gehrn, das ehr eigentlich den brieff köntte bekommen, dan so es ihm also gefellig wehr, köntte ehr mit seiner frauwen vnd kinder vnd nöttichstem gerette hir mit schiffen von Hamburgk her auffkommen; vnd was er dazu fur nöttwendig viaticum bedarff, köntt ihr ihm von meinetwegen vorstrecken, vnd so ihr keins von meinem gehlt habt, damit es wider kan erlegt werden, so will ich es euch zu danck lassen wiederumb mit den leutten, so von Hamburgk hjerauff kommen, zustellen, vnd geburlich interesse davon geben, das ihr ohne schaden sollt sein.</p><p>Das ihr meiner lieben schwester, fraw Sophia Brahe, habt einen von meinen kutschwogen sambt deme, was dazu gehörig, nach ihren begehr folgen lassen, daran habt ihr recht gethan, vnd ich trage darahn kein missgefallen, es kumbt vmb so ein gering ding nicht zuischen ihr und mihr, ich wolte ihr alß meiner hertzlieben schwester viel ein grösserß zugefallen geschehen lassen.</p><p>Was ihr von meinem übrigen gutt könnet alsbalt zu gelte machen, das thutt, aber daß positiff vnd die kleine geschutz von kupfer, eeissen oder waß sönsten metallisch ankombt, das schicket hierauff nebenst dem, so von Lybch geschicket wirt, darvnter auch thunnen, vnd stellt es alles, es sei was es wolle, auff ein klar register, dass ich richtigen bescheid davon wisse. Schreibt mihr auch zu, wie lang mein schwester ist alda zu Wandesbech gewessen, vnd wo sie hingezogen, dan ich kan nicht glauben, das sie noch alda sey, sönsten hette ich ihr wohl geschrieben. Last mihr auch wissen, ob Erich Lange ist zu Hamburgk gewesen, vnd wie es mit seinen sachen stehet, vnd woh er itzunder sein wohnung hat; wan ehr alda in die nahent ist, vnd mihr etwas schreiben will, so könt ihr mihr dasselbe zuschicken.</p><p>Was ahnlanget, das die frau stathalterin euch geschrieben hat von den 5000 thallern, wan sie dieselbige solten gewertigk sein, so ist es an dehm, das mein gutter freundt vnd blutsvörwanter ihn Dennemark, Eske Bilde, solte von meinetwegen dasselbige gelt in vorgangen vmbslag erleget haben, vnd meinen brieff wieder gefodert, aber er hat nicht zeittlich genug bescheit davon krigen können, sondern hatt sich erbotten zu nechstkumbstigen vmbslag alles richtig zu machen, vnd ich vormeine, das ehr die 300 thaller zum interesse schon erlegt hat.</p><p>Von wegen meiner vhnkostung, so ich ahm gebau vnd eissenwerch zum Wandesbech gethan, vorsihe ich mich, das die frau stathalterinn mihr nicht lenger aufhalten wirt, vnd meinen schaden nicht begehren. Vnd dieweill sie jhren sohn Bredo Wandesbech hatt abgehandelt, kan sie woll es selbst am besten richtig machen; will sie auch nicht die trallien vnd eeissenwerch, vnd waß ich sonst da hab machen lassen, behallten, vnd zahlen das, so zuvohr nicht davon bezalt ist worden, so könt jhr es alles wiederumb wegknehmen, vnd alles das eeisien wegenlassen vnd mit dem andern gutt hierauffschicken. Es sein auch noch etzliche schlosser, welche ich alda hab machen lassen, vnd zwo kachellofen, nebenst anders, so ihr wohl wirt können erfaren. Vorsihe ich mich, das ihr in diesen vnd allen anderen werdet von meinetwegen thun was billig vnd recht sein kan, dasselbige vmb euch vnd den eurigen wiederumb zu vorschulden will ich stetz geflissen sein. Vnd thu vnß hiemit semtlichen in schutz des Allmechtigen befehlen.</p><p>Was ihr von meinetwegen zu böttenlohn errleget oder sönsten, das das gutt mit brettern vnd mätten wohl vorwart werde, oder in andern wegen beköstiget, sol euch zu danch wiederumb bezallt werden.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO SIMON VON PETKUM]</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with wishing of all good, first — honourable and distinguished Simon von Petkum, especially good friend. <b>Your letter, dated at Hamburg the 12th of February this year, I have received, and with it the two from Master David Fabricius and Mr. Ridderhusen at Bremen.</b> Now as concerns the forwarding of letters to me here: it is all one to me whether you direct them <b>via Leipzig and thence to Dresden, to my host; or via Nuremberg and thence hither; or to Prague, to Dr. Heller, dwelling on the Lesser Town [Kleine Seite]</b> — so [that] I shall well get them — unless you have a chance to send the same to me directly from there.</p><p><b>Burgomaster Vincent Möller</b> writes that the Imperial Majesty's letter to the lords of Hamburg has not yet been delivered to them; but when that has happened, and he has learned from it what His Majesty most graciously desires on my behalf, he will help promote the matter as much as ever he can; for which I thank him, and have written him further instruction myself, which letter you can deliver to him on my behalf. That the imperial letter is not yet delivered to the lords there, I think the cause is that <b>my factor at Lübeck, Jochum Boch [Joachim Buch], keeps that letter by him until he sends the instruments, with the other goods, to Hamburg</b>; and if that has not meanwhile happened, he will do it — about which I have also written him, which letter you can send him at once. And as soon as the instruments and the other goods I have at Lübeck have arrived at Hamburg, I ask you kindly to <b>solicit the lords there to arrange that ships could be got for it, so that all of it — together with whatever you have there unsold — could be brought up here to Bohemia, to the Imperial Majesty's most gracious pleasure</b>; and if the lords will have one of their own servants along, who could see that everything goes properly, it would be very agreeable to me; and if they are minded to ship <b>millstones or other wares (easier got here than there with you) back down, so the ships do not come home empty</b>, the same [man] could oversee that too, and I would gladly apply all possible diligence that it be done quickly and with advantage.</p><p>And although you think it might be useful for His Majesty to let another letter go to the honourable Council there about the matter, I deem it unnecessary, since the previous letter is still on hand, or perhaps already delivered. <b>I expect the lords of Hamburg will know how to comply obediently with His Majesty's first letter, so that no more is needed — and that they will by no means do as the Magdeburgers</b>: to whom His Majesty wrote twice on my behalf, and they nonetheless found subterfuges and <b>pleaded the harsh siege they suffered a half-hundred years ago, [as excuse] that they could not do such.</b> Which His Majesty took grievously ill, and shook his head over it, and took as displeasure. The councillors too were quite mocking that [the Magdeburgers] dared refuse His Majesty so trifling a thing; and <b>one of them — who has daily more access to His Majesty than others — said these formal words to it: \"We had thought those of Magdeburg were courtiers; now we perceive they are peasants. The time may well come when they need His Majesty again; then one can well find a better and more credible excuse and pretext than they do now — so that they get a rebuff.\"</b> <i>This he said, certain others hearing, and my solicitor too</i>; and His Majesty at once most graciously ordered <b>another letter be written to the reverend Chapter and the venerable canons at Magdeburg, that they would do such to His Majesty's most gracious pleasure</b> — which they also quite obediently offered to do. And I now send the letter to them. <i>The Magdeburger, as it were reborn, would now wish that he had not denied that service to the Emperor, and would now gladly obey if it were permitted — but <b>too late are the Phrygians wise</b> [sero sapiunt Phryges]; the Emperor too no longer wishes it be done through them, but rather that the service of the reverend Chapter be used, as said.</i></p><p>When any letters from Mr. <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>, or otherwise from the Low Countries, arrive, or word from the <b>ephemerides-compiler</b>, let me get the same at the earliest occasion; and <b>send this enclosed letter to Master David Fabricius, along with the other to Mr. Ridderhusen at Bremen</b>; when the latter gets the one to Fabricius, he will soon convey it to <b>Resterhafe</b> [Fabricius's Frisian parish]. <b>I perceive from Mr. David Fabricius's letter that, if he could get some good situation here, he would not be disinclined to be here</b>; whereupon I have written him a good answer, [and] would gladly that he actually got the letter — for if it so pleased him, <b>he could come up here, with his wife and children and most necessary gear, by ship from Hamburg</b>; and what necessary travel-money he needs for it, you could advance him on my behalf; and if you have none of my money to repay it with, I will gladly have it conveyed back to you with the people who come up here from Hamburg, and give due interest on it, so that you shall be without loss.</p><p>That you let my dear sister, <b>Lady Sophia Brahe</b>, have one of my coach-wagons, with what belongs to it, at her request — in that you did right, and I bear no displeasure at it: it does not come to such a trifle between her and me; <b>I would let a much greater favour befall her, as my heartily-dear sister.</b></p><p>What of my remaining goods you can quickly turn to money, do that; but <b>the positive [organ], and the small cannon of copper, iron, or whatever else is metallic, ship up here</b>, along with what is sent from Lübeck — barrels among them — and <b>put it all, whatever it be, on a clear register, so that I know proper account of it.</b> Write me also <b>how long my sister was there at Wandsbek, and whither she has moved</b> — for I cannot believe she is still there, else I would have written her. Let me also know <b>whether Erik Lange was at Hamburg, and how his affairs stand, and where he now has his dwelling</b>; if he is near there, and will write me something, you could send it to me.</p><p>As concerns the <b>Statthalterin [Governess]</b> writing you about the <b>5000 thaler</b> — whether she should expect them: the matter is that my good friend and blood-relation in Denmark, <b>Eske Bille</b>, should have paid that money on my behalf at the past <b>settlement-term [omslag]</b>, and demanded my bond back; but he could not get word of it in time, but offered to make all right at the next settlement — and I suppose he has already paid the <b>300 thaler interest</b>.</p><p>As for my expenses on building and ironwork at Wandsbek, I expect the Statthalterin will not hold me up longer, nor desire my loss. And since <b>she has bargained Wandsbek [back] from her son Breide [Rantzau]</b>, she can best make it right herself; [but] if she will not keep <b>the grilles and ironwork, and what else I had made there</b>, and pay what was not paid for before, then you could take it all away again, <b>and remove all the iron and ship it up here</b> with the other goods. There are also <b>several locks I had made there, and two tiled stoves</b>, besides other things, which you will well be able to learn. I expect you will, in this and all else, do on my behalf what is fair and right; the same, toward you and yours, I will always be diligent to requite. And herewith I commend us all to the protection of the Almighty.</p><p>What you advance on my behalf for messenger-fees, or otherwise — that the goods be well protected with boards and mats, or in other ways provisioned — shall be repaid you with thanks.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> Tycho's <b>reply to Simon von Petkum</b> (his Hamburg/Wandsbek agent), answering <b>brahe-t_16000212001 (12 February 1600)</b> point by point. A <b>draft (Kladde)</b>, breaking off unsigned.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's northern logistics and the great Magdeburg anecdote.</b></li><li><b>Forwarding routes.</b> Letters via <b>Leipzig→Dresden, Nuremberg, or Prague (Dr. Heller, on the Lesser Town)</b>.</li><li><b>The instrument-ship.</b> Via <b>Vincent Möller</b> (Hamburg burgomaster) and <b>Jochum Boch (Joachim Buch)</b> at Lübeck (who holds the imperial letter until the Lübeck goods reach Hamburg); the plan to bring <b>all the instruments and unsold goods up to Bohemia by ship</b>, with a Hamburg overseer, the ships returning with <b>millstones</b>.</li><li><b>The Magdeburg anecdote (a vivid court scene).</b> <b>Magdeburg twice refused</b> the Emperor's request to ship Tycho's stored goods, <b>pleading \"the harsh siege they suffered a half-hundred years ago\"</b> (the 1550–51 Siege of Magdeburg) — Rudolf II \"shook his head\" in displeasure; and <b>a councillor's jibe</b>: <i>\"We had thought those of Magdeburg were courtiers; now we perceive they are peasants … the time may come when they need His Majesty again.\"</i> The Emperor redirected the order <b>to the Magdeburg cathedral Chapter</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000405001); Tycho caps it with the Latin tag <b><i>\"sero sapiunt Phryges\"</b></i> (\"too late the Phrygians grow wise\") — the now-willing Magdeburgers are too late.</li><li><b>David Fabricius's recruitment.</b> Tycho <b>invites Fabricius to come to Prague \"with wife and children\" by ship from Hamburg</b>, with travel-money advanced by Petkum — the next step from the death-rumour aftermath (cf. brahe-t_16000212001, brahe-t_15991216001). Routed via <b>Ridderhusen at Bremen → Resterhafe</b> (Fabricius's parish).</li><li><b>Sophie Brahe &amp; Erik Lange.</b> Tycho approves the coach-wagon given his <b>sister Sophie Brahe</b> (\"a much greater favour I would let befall her\"); asks where she has gone from Wandsbek; and inquires after <b>Erik Lange</b> — the alchemist nobleman (Sophie's betrothed/husband, ruined by alchemy and living in exile-debt) — \"how his affairs stand, where he now dwells.\"</li><li><b>The remaining goods.</b> A striking inventory to ship: <b>\"the positive [organ]\" (a chamber pipe-organ), the small cannon (of copper/iron), barrels, grilles, locks, two tiled stoves, and ironwork</b> from Wandsbek — all to be put \"on a clear register.\" A rare glimpse of Tycho's household furnishings.</li><li><b>Finances.</b> The <b>5000-thaler bond</b> to be redeemed by <b>Eske Bille</b> at the next <i>omslag</i> (cf. brahe-t_15991029001), the <b>300-thaler interest</b> likely already paid; the <b>Wandsbek building costs</b> and the <b>Statthalterin / Breide Rantzau</b> estate matter (cf. brahe-t_16000212001).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pairs with <b>brahe-t_16000212001 (Petkum's letter, 12 February 1600)</b>; the <b>Magdeburg-transport</b> thread (brahe-t_16000405001, brahe-t_16000103002); the <b>Fabricius-recruitment</b> thread (brahe-t_15990922001, brahe-t_15991216001); and <b>Sophie Brahe / Erik Lange</b>.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Simon von Petkum; <b>David Fabricius</b>; <b>Ludolf Ridderhusen</b> (Bremen); <b>Dr. Heller</b> (Prague); <b>Vincent Möller</b> (Hamburg); <b>Jochum Boch / Joachim Buch</b> (Lübeck); the <b>Magdeburg Chapter/canons</b> &amp; the unnamed imperial councillor; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>; the <b>ephemerides-compiler</b>; <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (sister); <b>Erik Lange</b>; the <b>Statthalterin</b> &amp; <b>Breide Rantzau</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Lübeck</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, <b>Leipzig</b>, <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Nuremberg</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Bremen</b>, <b>Resterhafe</b>, <b>Wandsbek</b>.</li><li><b>Latin tags.</b> <i>Haec ille … dixit</i> (\"this he said\"); <i>mallet iam renatus Magdeburgensis …</i> (\"the Magdeburger, as it were reborn, would now claim …\"); <b><i>sero sapiunt Phryges</b></i> (proverb: \"too late the Trojans grow wise\"). Rendered in italics with translation.</li><li><b>Date/place (protocol §3).</b> Undated <b>draft</b>; replies to Petkum's <b>12 Feb 1600</b> letter and discusses Sophie Brahe still at/leaving Wandsbek → dated <b>[Benátky, c. February/March 1600]</b> (slug `16000000002`, label \"Benatky\"). Flagged.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> English corrected against Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV p.197: 'mallet ... non denegasse' is a counterfactual wish about the past ('would now wish that he had not denied'), not a present claim of innocence; the published translation had inverted this into 'would now claim that he did not deny'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Early New High German (with embedded Latin) is fully legible and construed with confidence; the substance (the <b>forwarding routes</b>; the <b>Hamburg instrument-ship</b> via Möller/Boch with the millstone return-cargo; the <b>Magdeburg refusal, the \"courtiers vs peasants\" jibe, and the redirect to the Chapter</b> with the <i>sero sapiunt Phryges</i> tag; the <b>Fabricius recruitment</b> by ship from Hamburg; <b>Sophie Brahe's wagon and the Erik Lange query</b>; the <b>positive organ / small cannon / ironwork</b> inventory; and the <b>5000-thaler / Wandsbek</b> finances) is firmly rendered, with the Latin tags translated and Petkum's/Tycho's idiom preserved. Date is a flagged internal-evidence estimate (undated draft).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images visually across all 5 slice pages (chunks 1-4, then 5): TBOO XIV p.195 (end of the preceding Magdeburg-chapter letter), p.196 (letter 191 heading and opening), pp.196-198 (body), ending p.198 with the archival note 'Ex adumbratione in Bibliotheca Universitatis Basileensis: Autographensammlung Vol. 35, Fol. 69-72.' Collated the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date and place-name: Vincentius Mullerus, Jochum Boch, Josepho Scaligero, Dauide Fabricio/Ritterhusium/Resterhaffuen, Sophia Brahe, Bredo Wandesbech, Eske Bilde, the sums 5000 thallern and 300 thaller, zwo kachellofen — all confirmed matching Dreyer; no TEXT_ERROR found in the source transcription. One MISTRANSLATION found (logged above) in the Latin-insert sentence about the Magdeburgers' belated compliance, which materially changes what a scholar would take away about whether the Magdeburger admitted and regretted his earlier refusal or is instead depicted as denying it ever happened.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). 'mallet ... non denegasse' (would prefer/wish that he had NOT refused) is a counterfactual wish about the past, implying the Magdeburger DID refuse and now regrets it — which is exactly what the following idiom 'sero sapiunt Phryges' (the Phrygians grow wise too late) comments on. The English instead has him 'now claim that he did not deny' the service, turning an admission-with-regret into a present-tense denial of fact — the opposite posture toward his own past conduct. At: “The Magdeburger, as it were reborn, would now claim that he did not deny that service to the Emperor, and would now glad”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image, TBOO XIV p.197 (letter spans pp.195-199; pages 195-198 examined). The Latin 'mallet ... non denegasse ... et nunc libenter, si liceret, obtemperaret, sed sero sapiunt Phryges' is grammatically a regretful counterfactual wish (perfect infinitive under 'mallet'), consistent with the following proverb about too-late wisdom; the published English had converted this into a present-tense denial, which the fix corrects. The Latin itself is left untouched as instructed for a MISTRANSLATION defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 69-72. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Simon von Petkum", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000212001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000212001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges letters received via Petkum, gives multiple postal routes for future correspondence, and relays thanks to Burgomaster Möller regarding the Emperor's letter to Hamburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000000002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000000003", "d": "1600-00-00", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "ck": "Rudolf 2.", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Emperor Rudolf II [petition] — Benátky, [1600] [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery petition, with Latin) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Vienna, Haus-, Hof- und Staatsarchiv, Familienakten, fasc. 2</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AD SAC:Ae CAeS:Ae MAIESTA:IS D:NI NOSTRI CLEMENTIS:I MANUS PROPRIAS HUMILLIMA SUPPLICATIO TYCHONIS BRAHE.</p><p>ALLERDURCHLAUCHTIGSTER, großmechtigster römischer kayser auch zu Hungern vndt Beheimb königh, allergnedigster herr. Auff Ewer Kay: May: allergnedigstenn vnd zu vnterschiedtlichen mahlen ergangenen befehlich kan ich gleichwohl meine besoldung, so mir nue lenger alß ain jahr restiret, biß dato noch nicht bekommenn, welchs mihr sehr beschwerlich vorfelt vnndt nit wohll mueglichen lenger außzuestehen. Bitte darumb Ewer Kay: May: ich allervnterthenigst, die geruhen allergnedigft zuuerordnen, daß mihr baide die restirende vndt kunfftige besoldung auß Ewer Key: May: herschafften alhie ihn Behemen (wo es dan Ewer Kay: May: allergnedigst gefallig) geraichet, vndt alle quatember richtig gemacht werden möge, auff das ich die guette zeit mit vergeblichen sollicitationibus nebenst großen schaden vndt bekummernußenn (wie bißhero leider geschen) nit lenger zuebringe.</p><p>Waß andere mangel betreffendt ihn den sachen, so von Ewer Kay: May: allergnedigst mir von anbegin vndt hernacher bewilliget sindt, aber noch nicht wiederfahren, hab ich dem herrn Baruitio ain kurz verzaichnuß davon vberliefert Ewer Kay: May: meinet wegen vnterthenigst vorzuetragen, vndt bin der genzlichen hoffnung, daß ich auff diß alles mit dem ehesten ein allergnedigste resolution bekommen werde, auff daß ich ainmahl aigentlich wißen möchte, wie ich alhie darahn wehre, vndt ob ich ainige faste vndt bestendige gelegenheit erlangen köntte, mein vorhabendes hohes studium vndt astronomisches wergk Ewer Röm: Kay: May: vnd dem reich zue sondern ruhm, nutz vnd ehren alhie vnterthenigst zuuohlzihen, oder vonnötten wehre andere bequemigkait darzue zue suchen. Ewer Röm: Kay: May: ihn allen vnterthenigstem gehorsamb zue dienen ich mich iederzeit ganz schuldig erkenne. Ewer Röm: Kay: May: allervnterthenigster gehorsambster diener</p><p>Tycho Brahe<br>manu pp:</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the own hands of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord: the most humble supplication of Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Most serene, most mighty Roman Emperor, and King of Hungary and Bohemia, most gracious lord. Notwithstanding Your Imperial Majesty's most gracious command, issued at various times, I still cannot, to this date, obtain my salary, which has now been in arrears for more than a year — which falls very grievous to me, and is not well possible to endure longer. I therefore most humbly beg Your Imperial Majesty that you would most graciously deign to ordain that both the arrears and the future salary be furnished me out of Your Imperial Majesty's lordships here in Bohemia (where it then most graciously pleases Your Imperial Majesty), and be duly settled every quarter [<i>Quatember</i>], so that I may no longer spend the good time with vain <i>solicitations</i>, alongside great damage and troubles (as has hitherto, alas, happened).</p><p>As concerns other deficiencies in the matters which were most graciously granted me by Your Imperial Majesty from the beginning and afterward, but have not yet been fulfilled, I have delivered to Master Barvitius a short schedule thereof, to be submitted most humbly to Your Imperial Majesty on my behalf; and I have the entire hope that I shall, on all this, with the soonest, obtain a most gracious resolution — so that I might at last actually know how I stand here, and whether I could attain some firm and lasting situation to carry out here, most humbly, my intended high study and astronomical work, to Your Roman Imperial Majesty's and the realm's special renown, profit, and honour — or whether it were needful to seek other convenience for it. I at all times acknowledge myself entirely bound to serve Your Roman Imperial Majesty in all most humble obedience. Your Roman Imperial Majesty's most humble, most obedient servant,</p><p>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> A formal <b>petition (<i>Supplicatio</i>)</b> addressed \"to the own hands\" of <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> — the Latin superscription <i>Ad Sacrae Caesareae Maiestatis … manus proprias humillima supplicatio Tychonis Brahe</i>. The witness survives in the imperial <b>Familienakten</b> at the Vienna Haus-, Hof- und Staatsarchiv.</li><li><b>The unpaid salary.</b> The core grievance of Tycho's Bohemian years: his <b>imperial salary, \"more than a year in arrears,\"</b> despite repeated imperial orders. He asks that arrears and future salary be paid <b>from the imperial domains in Bohemia</b> and settled <b>every quarter</b> (<i>alle Quatember</i> — the quarterly Ember-tide terms), to spare him the \"vain <i>solicitations</i>, great damage, and troubles\" of chasing the treasury. A candid testimony to the chronic dysfunction of the Rudolfine fisc.</li><li><b>Other unfulfilled promises.</b> Tycho refers to a <i>Verzeichnis</i> (schedule/list) of further unmet promises which he has given to <b>Barvitius</b> (Johann Barvitius, the privy secretary; cf. brahe-t_15980626002, …16000507001) to lay before the Emperor; he hopes for a \"<b>resolution</b>\" so he may know \"how he stands,\" and whether he can pursue his \"high study and astronomical work\" there — \"<b>or whether it were needful to seek other convenience</b>\" (a veiled threat to look elsewhere).</li><li><b><i>sollicitationibus</b></i> / <b><i>Quatember</b></i> — Latin/technical terms kept and rendered (solicitations; the quarterly terms). <i>manu pp.</i> = <i>manu propria</i>.</li><li><b>Calendar / date.</b> The petition is <b>undated</b> in the source (dataset: \"Benatky,\" year <b>1600</b>, slug …0000…). Internal content (the year-plus arrears; the Barvitius schedule) fits <b>1600</b>, during the Benátky residence. Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>; no day/month survives.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German (with its Latin terms) is rendered securely, and the content (the year-long salary arrears; the request for quarterly payment from Bohemian domains; the Barvitius schedule of unmet promises; the implied threat to leave) is clear and corpus-/history-corroborated. Grade [B] solely because the document is <b>undated</b> (only the year 1600 is supplied by the catalogue); no textual crux.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole reservation is that the petition is undated (year only); explicitly 'no textual crux', sense secure end-to-end.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.206-209 page images. Located the letter as item no.200 (undated petition), beginning mid-p.207 after Dreyer's Latin abstract and ending on p.208 before item no.201 (a payment excerpt). Collated the German text line-by-line against published_english: salary arrears ('mehr als ein Jahr'/'more than a year'), the request that arrears and future pay be settled 'auß Ewer Key: May: herschafften alhie ihn Behemen' quarterly ('quatember'), the reference to Barvitius and the schedule delivered to him, and the closing 'Tycho Brahe manu pp:'. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found; source_text matches Dreyer's printed German exactly.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Haus- Hof- und Staatsarchiv, Familenakten, fasc. 2."], "th": {"label": "Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15860000001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Petitions the Emperor to pay over a year's arrears of salary from Bohemian lordships on a quarterly schedule and remedy other outstanding deficiencies.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000000003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000103001", "d": "1600-01-03", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Vincenzio Pinelli", "ck": "Vincenzio Pinelli", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Vincenzo Pinelli — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 3 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRISSIMO ET CLARISSIMO VIRO DOMINO VINCENTIO PINELLIO PATRICIO PATAVINO DOMINO ET AMICO SUO OBSERVANDISSIMO.<br>ILLUSTRIS ET MAGNIFICE VIR.<br>S.</p><p>CUM superiore Anno Italiam peragrasset Nobilis et Eruditus adolescens FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS, qui aliquamdiu antea tam in Daniâ quam Germaniâ meus domesticus fuit, et Dresdâ a me in Italiam profectus inter alia, ut Patavium transiens te officiose meo nomine salutaret, atque de statu mearum rerum edoceret, in mandatis habuit. Reversus autem nuper ad me, cum ab ipso percontarer, an te allocutus esset, respondit se, quam primum Patavium permeasset, saltem biduum ibi mansisse: interim te conveniendi nullam datam fuisse commoditatem. Reiecit itaque illud in reditum suum, donec ulteriore Italiâ perlustrata in Germaniam ipsi revertendum foret. At cum rursus Urbem vestram accederet, et quod antea omissum erat, praestare satageret, te ibi non invenit; sed in villam tuam animi caussâ secessisse Mathematicus vester GALILAeUS DE GALILAeIS referebat. Re itaque hac praeter utriusque nostrûm expectationem infectâ is ad me (uti dictum) redijt. Ex quo vero Excellentissimo Domino MAGINO ad suas, quas idem mihi attulit, respondendum nunc foret literas, te quoque non praetereundum censui, ut quod antea per praesentem TENGNAGLIUM nostrum fieri non poterat, nunc literis (siquidem aliter non licet) compensetur.</p><p>Existimo te ab alijs iam antea utcunque percepisse, quomodo Patrium solum verterim, atque in Germaniam ultro concesserim, derelicto Uraniburgo nostro Danico totoque eius apparatu Astronomico mecum ad exteros translato; siquidem quorundam invidiâ et malevolentiâ factum sit, ut omnes reditus ad sustentationem Divinae illius scientiae Astronomicae a nunquam satis laudatae memoriae Rege FRIDERICO deputati, statim ac novitius hîc Rex coronatus atque rerum administrationi adhibitus esset, subtracti fuerint, et simul a Cancellario pariter novitio, quem per literas diligenter admonueram, ut hoc sublime studium iuxta intentionem antecedentis Regis et Senatusconsulti, Patriae saltem honoris caussâ isthic conservari et perpetuari posse, a Rege hocce obtineret, et simul discrete praemonui de ijs, quae subsequerentur, si id non fieret, mihi rescriptum sit, Regi non esse integrum quicquam in Instrumentorum meorum huic exercitio inservientium sustentationem de suo conferre. Secessi itaque primum Haffniam, quae Regni metropolis est, trium vel 4 horarum saltem itinere maritimo ab Insulâ illâ Uraniburgicâ distantem. Ubi cum in aedibus amplis iuxta vallum a me ipso exaedificatis studia mea proprijs sumptibus continuare in animum induxissem, eâque de caussâ nonnulla ex Instrumentis meis ibi commode disposuissem, a Curiae Magistro nomine Iunioris Regis, licet tunc in Germaniâ absentis, id efficere prohibitus sum. Quare cum has praeclaras Artes isthic adeo neglectas, imo exosas animadverterem, ut neque publicis neque privatis impensis ijs invigilandi otium atque commoditas concederetur, malui universam Patriam plurimosque in eâ sanguine iunctos et Amicos inibi omnes derelinquere, quam laborum Uraniae nostrae frustrationem atque interitum spectare. Substiti tamen in transmarino Ducatu Megapolitano per Anni quadrantem et postea in vicinâ Cimbris Holsatiâ integrum Annum, ut nostratibus, Patriae adhuc studens, rem rectius deliberandi satis relinquerem temporis. Quod cum incassum fieri viderem, et interea ab Augustissimo et Potentissimo Imperatore RUDOLPHO Secundo, Domino nostro Clementissimo, inde in Bohemiam evocarer, rebus meis, quam primum fieri potuit, in Germaniâ compositis et translatis Magdaeburgum ad Albim situm Instrumentis unâ cum reliquâ supellectile domesticâ Dresdam veni, ubi, postquam de Epidemicis morbis in Bohemiâ populariter grassantibus, quodque Caesar ob ea pericula Pragâ relictâ in Arcem quandam seorsim se recepisset, intellexissem, et simul a Magco Domino CORRADUCIO, Imperij Procancellario, per literas de Caes. Matis Clementissimâ voluntate admonerer, ut nimirum paulisper in viciniâ me continerem, donec malum illud in Bohemiâ remitteret, atque Aula Caesa recolligeretur, expectavi aliquandiu in propinquâ Saxoniâ, quoad iterum ex mandato Caesaris a praedicto Domino CORRADUCIO Pragam per literas vocarer, quo superiore aestate cum totâ familiâ adveniens, a Caesa Mate mox benignissime receptus sum, atque annuo salario satis liberali, quo Astronomiae studio et rei domesticae consuleretur, provisum, concessaque Arx quaedam Caesareana Benachia, quam ob loci eximiam amoenitatem et fluvium Isaram circumcurrentem indigenae Venetias Bohemorum nuncupant, quae quinque saltem milliaribus Germanicis sive sex horarum itinere Pragâ metropoli removetur. Hanc tribus alijs pariter oblatis, quod locus sit tranquillus et iocundus, ac simul admodum êditus, adeoque Observationibus coelestibus commodior, praetuli: ubi etiam convenientia pro meis Instrumentis ordine et sigillatim disponendis aedificia mandato et sumptu Caesaris confestim structa sunt, in quae in Bohemiam mecum allata Organa, prout usus requirit, collocata sunt. Reliqua ex Germaniâ, quam primum hyems desierit, huc pariter advecta, ibidem usui adaptabuntur, ut sic, Coelestium favente Opifice, Uraniae nostrae, quam Patria repudiavit, fundamina hîc reponantur, atque in Caesae Matis totiusque Imperij honorem Astronomiae redintegratio continuetur, atque quam primum fieri possit, compleatur. Quod post Divini Numinis providentiam huic optimo Imperatori omnium liberalium Artium, inprimisque Mathematicarum Maecenati benignissimo acceptum est ferendum. Namque is Clementi manu nobis haec otia fecit.</p><p>Haec te, Clarissime Domine PINELLE, scire volui, si forte antea non satis innotuerunt, ut rerum nostrarum vicissitudo et in Bohemiâ a Caesare benignissime restitutum hospitium te non lateret. Libros autem, quos Caesae Mati dedicatos, cum primum adveni, humillime obtuli, quoad duos priores, te vidisse autumo. Nam et Sermo Principi atque Senatui Veneto a praenominato Iuvene meâ caussâ reverenter exhibiti sunt, qui etiam eum Equitis aurati honore decorârunt. Si vero nondum eos tibi inspicere contigit, ab Illustri et Clarissimo viro IOHANNE FRANCISCO SAGGREDO id facile (uti opinor) impetrabis. Tertius erat de restitutis motibus Solis et Lunae eorundemque cum consuetis calculis comparatione, quem alias (uti spero) videbis.</p><p>Porro ut per occasionem, quae nunc ob Aulae Caesareae propinquitatem promptior quam antea occurret, me Eruditis tuis literis invisere, atque quod hisce meis incomptis praevenerint, candide accipere non degraveris, quaeso. Vale, salutato a me peramanter ob conformia studia Praestantissimo isthic Mathematum Professore GALILAeO DE GALILAeIS; qui si mihi (uti constituerat) per dictam occasionem scripsisset, me in respondendo non invenisset difficilem. Poterit tamen id alio fortassis tempore praestari. Iamque iterum vale, vir illustris et celeberrime, atque Annum hunc Iubilaeum pluresque alios subsequentes quam felicissime transige.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ 3 die Ianuarij Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious and renowned man, Lord Vincenzo Pinelli, patrician of Padua, his most esteemed Lord and friend.</b><br>Illustrious and magnificent Man.<br>Greeting.</p><p>When last year the noble and learned youth <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> — who for some time before had been a member of my household, both in Denmark and in Germany — travelled through Italy, having set out from Dresden to Italy by me, he had it among his charges that, passing through <b>Padua</b>, he should dutifully greet you in my name, and inform you of the state of my affairs. But lately returned to me, when I asked him whether he had spoken with you, he answered that, as soon as he had reached Padua, he stayed there but two days, [and] meanwhile no opportunity of meeting you was given. He therefore put it off to his return, until, the rest of Italy traversed, he should have to come back into Germany. But when he again approached your City, and busied himself to do what had before been omitted, he did not find you there; but <b>your Mathematician, Galileo Galilei [Galilaeus de Galilaeis], reported that you had retired to your country villa for relaxation</b>. And so, this matter failing beyond the expectation of us both, he returned to me (as I said). But since I must now answer the letter of the most excellent <b>Lord Magini</b>, which he brought me, I judged you too not to be passed over — that what before could not be done through our Tengnagel in person, may now be compensated by letter (since otherwise it is not allowed).</p><p>I think you have already in some way perceived from others <b>how I turned from my native soil, and withdrew of my own accord into Germany, abandoning our Danish Uraniborg and bringing all its Astronomical apparatus with me abroad</b> — since by certain men's envy and malevolence it came about that <b>all the revenues assigned, for the support of that divine science of Astronomy, by King Frederick of never-sufficiently-praised memory, were withdrawn as soon as the new King here was crowned and applied to the administration of affairs</b>. And likewise by the also-new <b>Chancellor</b>, whom I had diligently admonished by letter to obtain from this King that this sublime study might — according to the intention of the preceding King and the Senate-decree, at least for the honour of the fatherland — be preserved and perpetuated there (and I discreetly forewarned of what would follow, if it were not done) — it was written back to me <b>that it was not in the King's power to contribute anything of his own to the support of my instruments serving this exercise</b>. I therefore first withdrew to <b>Copenhagen</b>, the realm's metropolis, distant only a three- or four-hours' sea-journey from that Island of Uraniborg. There, when I had resolved to continue my studies at my own expense in a large house I had myself built by the rampart, and for that cause had conveniently arranged some of my instruments there, <b>I was prohibited from doing so by the Court-Master, in the name of the Younger King</b> (though he was then absent in Germany). Wherefore, seeing these excellent Arts so neglected there — nay, hated — that neither by public nor by private means was leisure and convenience granted to watch over them, <b>I preferred to abandon my whole fatherland, and all the many there joined to me by blood, and my friends, rather than to behold the frustration and destruction of our Urania's labours.</b> I halted, however, in the transmarine <b>Duchy of Mecklenburg</b> for a quarter of a year, and afterward in neighbouring <b>Holstein</b> (near the Cimbrians) a whole year — that, still studying the fatherland's [good], I might leave my countrymen enough time to deliberate the matter more rightly. When I saw this done in vain, and was meanwhile <b>summoned thence into Bohemia by the most august and most powerful Emperor Rudolf II</b>, my most clement Lord — my affairs in Germany composed and removed as soon as could be (my Instruments, with the rest of my household goods, [left] at Magdeburg on the Elbe) — I came to <b>Dresden</b>; where, after I had learned of the epidemic diseases raging popularly in Bohemia, and that the Emperor, on account of those perils, had left Prague and retired apart into a certain Castle — and was at the same time admonished by letter from the magnificent <b>Lord Coraduz, Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b>, of the Imperial Majesty's most clement wish, namely that I keep myself a while in the neighbourhood until that evil in Bohemia should abate and the Imperial Court reassemble — I waited a while in nearby Saxony, until I was again, by the Emperor's mandate, called to Prague by the said Lord Coraduz. Arriving thither last summer with my whole family, I was soon most kindly received by the Imperial Majesty, and provided with <b>a sufficiently liberal annual salary</b>, by which the study of Astronomy and the household might be cared for; and there was granted me <b>a certain imperial Castle, Benátky</b> — which, for the place's exceeding loveliness and the river <b>Iser [Jizera]</b> running around it, the natives call <b>the Venice of the Bohemians</b> — distant from the metropolis Prague only five German miles, or a six-hours' journey. This I preferred to three others likewise offered, because the place is quiet and pleasant, and at the same time quite high, and so more convenient for celestial Observations; where also <b>buildings fit for arranging my Instruments in order and severally were at once erected, by the Emperor's order and expense</b>, into which the apparatus brought with me into Bohemia has been placed, as use requires. The rest, from Germany, as soon as winter ends, brought hither likewise, will there be fitted for use — so that thus, the heavenly Maker favouring, <b>the foundations of our Urania, which the fatherland repudiated, may be re-laid here, and the restoration of Astronomy be continued, for the honour of the Imperial Majesty and of the whole Empire, and completed as soon as may be</b>. Which, after the providence of the Divine Godhead, is to be set to the account of this best Emperor, the most kindly Maecenas of all the liberal Arts, and especially of the Mathematical. For <b>with clement hand He has made us this leisure</b> [<i>nobis haec otia fecit</i>].</p><p>These things, most renowned Lord Pinelli, I wished you to know — if perhaps they were not before sufficiently known — that the vicissitude of my affairs, and the lodging most kindly restored to me in Bohemia by the Emperor, might not be hidden from you. As for <b>the books which, dedicated to the Imperial Majesty, I most humbly offered when I first arrived</b> — as to the two earlier ones, I suppose you have seen them. For [they] were reverently presented, on my behalf, by the aforesaid Youth, to the Prince [Doge] and Senate of Venice — <b>who also honoured him with the dignity of a Golden Knight</b> [<i>eques auratus</i>]. But if it has not yet befallen you to inspect them, you will easily obtain it (as I think) from the illustrious and renowned man <b>Giovanni Francesco Sagredo</b>. The third was on the restored motions of the Sun and Moon, and their comparison with the customary calculations [the <i>Progymnasmata</i>], which you will see elsewhere (as I hope).</p><p>Further, I beg that — by the occasion which now, because of the nearness of the Imperial Court, will occur more readily than before — you would not find it a burden to visit me with your learned letters, and to take candidly that these rough ones of mine have forestalled [you]. Farewell; and <b>greet for me, most lovingly, for our conformable studies, the most excellent Professor of Mathematics there, Galileo Galilei [Galilaeo de Galilaeis]; who, if he had written to me (as he had resolved) through the said occasion, would not have found me difficult in replying. Yet this can perhaps be done at another time.</b> And again farewell, illustrious and most celebrated man, and pass this Jubilee Year, and many others following, most happily.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 3rd day of January, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Gian Vincenzo Pinelli</b> (1535–1601), the great Paduan humanist, polymath, and book-/manuscript-collector, whose library was the hub of Padua's learned world (Galileo frequented it).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's fullest <i>apologia</i>/autobiography of the Hven departure, and the Galileo connection.</b></li><li><b>The exile narrative (the corpus's most complete).</b> A detailed account of leaving Denmark: <b>King Frederick II's astronomy revenues withdrawn as soon as the \"new King\" (Christian IV) took power</b>; the \"new Chancellor\" replying that <b>\"it was not in the King's power to contribute anything\"</b>; Tycho's retreat to <b>Copenhagen</b> (the rampart house) and the <b>Court-Master's prohibition</b> \"in the name of the Younger King\"; the choice to abandon the fatherland; the <b>quarter-year in Mecklenburg + year in Holstein</b> \"to leave my countrymen time to deliberate\"; the <b>imperial summons via Coraduz</b>; the <b>Dresden/Saxony wait</b> during the Bohemian plague; and the <b>grant of Benátky</b> (\"the Venice of the Bohemians,\" on the <b>Jizera/Iser</b>, 5 German miles from Prague), chosen over three others, with imperial-funded instrument-housings — \"the foundations of our Urania, which the fatherland repudiated, re-laid here.\" Closes with the Virgilian echo <b><i>nobis haec otia fecit</b></i> (Ecl. 1.6) applied to Rudolf II.</li><li><b>The Galileo connection (rare and notable).</b> Twice: <b>Galileo (\"the most excellent Professor of Mathematics there\")</b> reported Pinelli's absence to Tengnagel; and Tycho asks Pinelli to <b>\"greet Galileo most lovingly, for our conformable studies,\"</b> adding that <b>Galileo \"had resolved to write to me\" and \"would not have found me difficult in replying.\"</b> One of the very few documented Tycho–Galileo contact points — and a record of Galileo's <i>intended</i> (never realised) letter to Tycho.</li><li><b>The Venice book-presentation and Tengnagel's knighthood.</b> Tycho's <b>books dedicated to the Emperor</b> (the <i>Mechanica</i> etc.) were <b>presented to the Doge and Senate of Venice</b> by <b>Tengnagel</b>, whom Venice <b>honoured as a <i>Golden Knight</i> (eques auratus)</b>; copies obtainable from <b>Sagredo</b>. The \"third book\" is the <i>Progymnasmata</i> (\"the restored motions of Sun and Moon\").</li><li><b>The Magini link.</b> Written alongside Tycho's reply to <b>Magini</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010220001), both carried in the same Italian exchange.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Vincenzo Pinelli; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (now <i>eques auratus</i> of Venice); <b>Galileo Galilei</b>; <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b>; <b>Giovanni Francesco Sagredo</b>; <b>King Frederick II</b> &amp; <b>Christian IV</b> of Denmark; the Danish <b>Chancellor</b> and <b>Court-Master</b> (unnamed); <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Coraduz</b>; the <b>Doge and Senate of Venice</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b> (\"Venice of the Bohemians,\" on the <b>Jizera</b>), <b>Prague</b>, <b>Padua</b>, <b>Venice</b>, <b>Copenhagen</b>, <b>Hven/Uraniborg</b>, <b>Mecklenburg</b>, <b>Holstein</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Saxony</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A <b>keystone of the self-exile thread</b> (cf. brahe-t_15980607001 to Grynaeus) and <b>the principal Tycho–Galileo document</b>; route through the Galileo/Padua and exile-narrative threads.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>3 die Ianuarij Anno 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>3 January 1600 [N.S.]</b> (slug `16000103001` agrees; label gives only \"Benatky\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>complete Hven-departure narrative</b> — the withdrawn revenues under Christian IV, the Chancellor's refusal, the Copenhagen prohibition, the Mecklenburg/Holstein interim, the imperial call and the Benátky grant; the <b>Galileo greeting and his intended letter</b>; the <b>Venice book-presentation and Tengnagel's knighthood</b>; the Sagredo and Magini links) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; the Virgilian echo and the Benátky/Jizera details are rendered as written.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VIII pp.226-229, within read slice pp.225-229 — p.225 belongs to the preceding letter to Herwart) visually against Dreyer, including the entire autobiographical account of Tycho's departure from Denmark. Checked all place names (Copenhagen/Haffnia, Mecklenburg, Holstein, Dresden, Magdeburg, Prague, Benátky/Benachia), durations and distances (a quarter-year, a whole year, a three/four-hour sea journey, five German miles or a six-hour journey), persons (Tengnagel, Magini, Galileo Galilei, Sagredo, Coraduz/Corraducius, Rudolf II, King Frederick), and the closing dateline (Benátky, 3 January 1600). No divergence found between source_text and Dreyer's printed text at any point; the English renders every clause faithfully with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that Tengnagel twice failed to meet Pinelli in Padua — on the return, Galileo Galilei reported Pinelli had retired — and now writes directly.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000103001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000103002", "d": "1600-01-03", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Giovanni Antonio Magini — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 3 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record (printed source): G. A. Magini,<i> Tabulae Primi Mobilis </i>· MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basileensi G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>EXCELLENTISSIMO ET ERUDITISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IOHANNI ANTONIO MAGINO, MATHEMATUM IN ILLUSTRI BONONIENSIUM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI, AMICO SUO PLURIMUM DILECTO.</p><p>REVERSUS ad me FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS noster tuas attulit, Excellentissime et Eruditissime plurimumque mihi dilecte Domine D. MAGINE, meis quidem respondentes, sed tamen de singulis, de quibus tecum agebam, non sufficienter agentes, quod occupationibus tuis lubens tribuo. Gratum vero mihi in primis est, quod ijs quae ille proponebat assentitus sis, atque in illis tuam operam sive per temetipsum sive per alios in Italiâ Eruditione pollentes effectui daturum benevole pollicitus.</p><p>Existimo te a dicto TENGNAGLIO summa rerum huc pertinentium fastigia hausisse. Adiunxi hisce brevem discursum de occasionibus mei e Daniâ discessus, ut eo promptius negocium concipi possit, et quasdam alias literas huc pertinentes. Coetera ipsius relatio ac Carmina tradita, quae nunc prioribus addo, supplebunt. Da itaque operam, optime MAGINE, ut mature res omnis conficiatur, mihique primae conceptiones intra duos menses (si ullo modo fieri possit) huc in Bohemiam mittantur, quo quae necessaria duxero, mutem, addam vel adaugeam, cum nemini melius quam mihi ipsi mea perspecta sint, moxque vobis revisa et locupletata remittam. Placet vero admodum illa ratio, de quâ TENGNAGLIUS admonuit, ut potius Panaegyricum quid sit quam per Dialogos. Sic enim res omnis concinnius comprehendi poterit et maiori authoritate: ita tamen, ut a generalioribus ad particularia descendendo primum universae Artis Astronomicae ortus, incrementa, promotiones, augmenta et variae mutationes ac peregrinationes recenseantur; quae omnia ab alijs scriptoribus, qui hoc argumentum tractârunt, facile cum delectu quodam hauriri poterunt. Et si alij defuerint, IOHANNES STADIUS in ijs, quae Tabulis Bergensibus abs se dictis subiunxit, occasionem suppeditabunt: quae tamen in quibusdam, ubi liberius excurrit, castiganda erunt, uti erat vir ille in dicendo facilior quam verior. Utinam in calculo Astronomico, qui fucum (uti historica) non patitur, idem non commisisset. Quâ in re non immerito a te redarguitur. Sed parcamus iam manibus defuncti. Est aliquid prodire tenus, si non datur ultra.</p><p>Deinde postquam narratum est, quomodo e Graeciâ in Italiam et Hispaniam, postmodum vero ad Germanos et deinde in Daniam versus Septentrionem migrârit Astronomia; quo cum ulterius ob Arctoum frigus nimiamque sphaerae inclinationem progredi commode non posset, neque etiam diu ibi subsistere, tandem in Germaniam reversa est, adeoque in ipsam Bohemiam, Germaniae quasi cor et meditullium, ubi apud Augustissimum Imperatorem RUDOLPHUM II clemens et benignum hospitium nacta est, ut demum verificaretur, quod olim MANILIUS ad AUGUSTUM Caesarem protulit: <i>[Regales animos primum dignata movere, / Proxima tangentes Coelo fastigia rerum.]</i></p><p>Hinc sumptâ occasione poterit postea digressio quaedam fieri ad me et ea, quae mihi in Daniâ evenerunt, tanquam relatione eorum, qui ex Germaniâ et Bohemiâ in Italiam proficiscuntur, comperta. Decet tamen in his modum aliquem servare et modeste ac moderate omnia agere, ita ut Patriae nostrae et Regi plurimisque in ijs praeclarâ et antiquâ Nobilitate viris integris et cordatis, quibus partim inscijs partim invitis ea evenerunt, quae discessui meo ansam praebuerunt, parcatur, utque illi sint excusabiles. Perstringendi vero solummodo pro merito Cancellarius modernus et Aulae magister, qui cum Patriae honorem ex officio promovere debuissent, eum potius ob avaritiam et sorditiem pari invidiâ, malignitate et odio coniunctam (cum ipsi liberalibus scientijs vel nihil vel admodum parum tincti essent) impediverunt et exterminârunt. Nomina eorum invenies in ijs, quae de caussis discessus mei Latine exarata nunc mitto. Nec interest, si illi expresse nominentur in scripto illo, tanquam nomina eorum hac occasione aliunde in Italiâ relata fuerint, cum alias obscuri sint, digni tamen, ut ex tali facinore famosi evadant, quemadmodum ille, qui Dianae templum Ephesijs incendio absumpsit, cuius tamen nomen hoc pacto deletum est. Atque in hunc vel similem modum possunt ea, quae Uraniae nostrae et mihi contigerunt, amplificari.</p><p>Inaudivi te Ephemerides tuas magnâ equidem diligentiâ supputatas (utut quidam Francofurti ad Oderam, êditis nuper quibusdam alijs ab anno 1595 incipientibus et ultra tuas decennio prolongatis, minutula quaedam, quae momentum non habent, in ijs carpere praesumpserit) auxisse ad minimum integro decennio. Exemplar non vidi: neque enim fuit in Catalogo Nundinarum Francofurtensium proxime antecedentium. Cuperem itaque ex te habere, ut videam, an aliqua inibi praeter priorem diligentiam exantlaveris. Neque enim dubito, tua in hoc genere (cum numerorum tractatione atque compendijs praepolleas) emuncta et solida esse, quantum fert fundamentum illud cui superstructa. Coeterum quae de Tabulis meis in motu Luminarium restituto expetis, ut ijs, donec reliquorum Planetarum numeratio ipsi Coelo consona subsequatur, in commodum Reip. literariae uti possis, libenter tecum communicarem, si ea quae non tam typis excusa quam manuscripta in his parata habeo, nunc ad manus essent. Nam ut de rerum mearum vicissitudine, quam a praenominato TENGNAGLIO intellexisti, nihil hîc repetam (piget enim istorum recordari, nedum ea memorare) scias me praecipuam partem suppellectilis meae interea, dum huc in Bohemiam a Caesarea Maiestate Clementissime evocatus transeo, Magdaeburgi ad Albim quasi in medio Germaniae reliquisse, donec de ipsius Maiestatis Clementissimâ voluntate certior redderer; inter quam non saltem Instrumenta quaedam ex meis, plura quam huc attuli, sed et maxima pars Operum meorum tam impressorum quam manuscriptorum conservatur. Et licet Caesarea Maiestas ante complures hebdomadas Magdaeburgensibus clementer scripserit atque mandârit, ut omnes res meas ibi reservatas huc in Bohemiam intermediante Albi adduci procurent, tamen interveniente hyeme, quae fluminis congelationem atque impedimenta varia facile suggerere potuit, id usque in instans vernum tempus procrastinare necessum habuerunt. Unde fit, ut tuis votis hac in parte, etiamsi quam maxime vellem, nunc subvenire nequeam, sed spero Progymnasmatum nostrorum Astronomiae instauratae primum Tomum brevi, favente Coelesti Numine, in publicum êditum iri: moxque exemplar tibi impertiar. Isthic capite primo, quae in calculo curriculi utriusque Luminaris desideras, sufficienter invenies. Interim hanc incidentem morulam patienter habe. In reliquis Planetis adhuc strenue laboro. Res enim est maioris difficultatis, pluribusque anfractibus obnoxia, quam hactenus creditum. Quid in his atque alijs ad negocium Astronomicum facientibus hucusque aspirante ipso Coeli authore perfecerim, liber is, quem TENGNAGLIUS tibi ex me dono dedit, de Mechanicâ Astronomiae parte circa finem edocebit. Quem unâ cum altero de affixarum maiore labore quam aestimatione restitutis locis te accepisse gaudeo; velimque ut tibi soli reserves, vel saltem ne indignis et ingratis communices.</p><p>Observationes, quas per nova Instrumenta ad imitationem quorundam ex meis fabrefacta te in Italiâ fieri effecturum iam dudum pollicitus es (de quibus etiam antea per literas inculcavi) necdum conspicere licuit. Averem sane tale quidpiam isthic diligenter effici, ex quo minor Sphaerae apud vos quam hîc inclinatio maiorem largiatur commoditatem stellas praesertim Australiores rite examinandi. Nec desunt vobis ingenia ad quidvis apta, modo in talibus instruantur et adhibeantur. Neque dubito quin ditiores, qui hac sublimi cognitione afficiuntur, sumptus non invite sint subministraturi. Quod et illis erit honorificum. Quae vero in his praecipue facienda atque eligenda sint, ut Mechanice omnia ad amussim et utiliter conficiantur, liber, de quo dixi, quemque habes, ansam praebebit. Omnem itaque move lapidem, ut non sub tecto et tuguriolis (uti nunc communiter fit) sed sub dio atque ipso Coelo Astronomiam (velut par est) veterum imitatione exerceamus, et sic spectemur agendo.</p><p>Vale, et Annum hunc Iubilaeum, multosque alios in Artis huius nunquam satis laudatae promulgationem feliciter exige.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ 3 Ian. 1600.<br>Excellentiae tuae studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent and most learned man, Lord Giovanni Antonio Magini, Professor of Mathematics in the illustrious Gymnasium of Bologna, his most beloved friend.</b></p><p><b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, our [man], returning to me, brought your [letters], most excellent and learned and most beloved Lord Magini — answering mine, yet not sufficiently treating the several points I was raising with you, which I gladly attribute to your occupations. It is most welcome to me that you assented to what he proposed, and benevolently promised to give effect to it, whether by yourself or through others in Italy excelling in Erudition.</p><p>I think you have drawn from the said Tengnagel the chief heads of the matters pertaining hither. <b>I have adjoined to these a brief discourse on the occasions of my departure from Denmark</b>, that the business may be the more readily conceived, and certain other relevant letters. The rest his report, and the <b>Verses</b> handed over (which I now add to the earlier), will supply. Take pains, then, excellent Magini, that the whole thing be finished in good time, and that <b>the first conceptions be sent me here into Bohemia within two months</b> (if it can by any means be done), so that I may change, add, or augment what I judge necessary — since my affairs are better known to no one than to me myself — and I will soon send them back to you, approved and enriched.</p><p><b>I much approve that plan of which Tengnagel advised: that it be rather a Panegyric than [done] through Dialogues.</b> For so the whole matter can be more elegantly comprehended, and with greater authority — yet so that, descending from the more general to the particular, <b>first the origin, increments, promotions, augments, and various changes and migrations of the whole Astronomical Art be recounted</b> — all of which can easily be gathered, with some selection, from other writers who have treated this argument. And if others fail, <b>Johannes Stadius</b>, in what he subjoined to his <i>Tabulae Bergenses</i>, will supply occasion — though in some places, where he runs too freely, [they] must be corrected, for that man was readier than truer in speaking. Would that, in astronomical calculation — which (unlike history) suffers no false colouring — he had not committed the same! In which matter he is not undeservedly refuted by you. But let us now spare the shade [<i>manes</i>] of the dead. <b>\"It is something to advance thus far, if it is not given to go beyond\"</b> [Horace].</p><p>Then, after it is narrated <b>how Astronomy migrated from Greece into Italy and Spain, afterward to the Germans, and then into Denmark, toward the North</b> — whither, since it could not conveniently proceed further (on account of the Arctic cold and the excessive inclination of the sphere), nor stay there long, <b>it at last returned into Germany, and indeed into Bohemia itself, the heart and midmost, as it were, of Germany, where it found a clement and kindly lodging with the most august Emperor Rudolf II</b> — so that at last there be verified what <b>Manilius</b> once uttered to <b>Augustus Caesar</b>: <i>“[Astronomy,] first deigning to stir the minds of kings, and those reaching the topmost summits of affairs that border on Heaven.”</i> [the couplet — Manilius, <i>Astronomica</i> I.7–8 — is blank in the working transcription but printed by Dreyer; supplied editorially: <i>Regales animos primum dignata movere, / Proxima tangentes Coelo fastigia rerum.</i>]</p><p>Hence, occasion being taken, <b>a digression can afterward be made to me, and what befell me in Denmark — as if learned by the report of those who travel from Germany and Bohemia into Italy.</b> Yet it befits to keep some measure in these, and do all modestly and moderately, <b>so that our fatherland, and the King, and the many men of distinguished and ancient Nobility, upright and sensible — by whom (partly unknowing, partly unwilling) those things happened that gave occasion to my departure — be spared, and be excusable.</b> <b>Only to be censured, for their desert, are the modern Chancellor and the Court-Master</b> — who, when they ought by their office to have promoted the fatherland's honour, <b>rather, out of avarice and sordidness joined with equal envy, malignity, and hatred (since they were either not at all, or very little, tinged with the liberal sciences), impeded and exterminated it.</b> Their names you will find in what I now send, written in Latin, about the causes of my departure. Nor does it matter if they be expressly named in that writing — as if their names were on this occasion reported from elsewhere in Italy — since they are otherwise obscure, yet worthy that, from such a deed, they become infamous: <b>like the man who consumed the temple of Diana of the Ephesians by fire, whose name was nonetheless [by decree] deleted</b> [Herostratus]. And in this or a like manner, what befell our Urania and me can be amplified.</p><p>I have heard that you have augmented <b>your Ephemerides</b> — calculated indeed with great diligence (although a certain man at <b>Frankfurt-on-Oder</b>, having lately published certain others, beginning from the year 1595 and prolonged a decade beyond yours, presumed to carp at certain trifles in them that have no moment) — by at least a whole decade. I have not seen the copy; for it was not in the Catalogue of the most recent Frankfurt Fair. I should therefore wish to have it from you, to see whether you have drawn out anything therein beyond your former diligence. For I do not doubt that your [work] in this kind is refined and solid (since you excel in the handling of numbers and in compendia), so far as the foundation on which it is built allows. As for what you seek concerning <b>my Tables of the restored motion of the Luminaries</b> — that you may use them, for the advantage of the literary Commonwealth, until the numeration of the remaining Planets, consonant with heaven itself, follow — I would gladly share them with you, if what I have prepared in these (not so much printed as in manuscript) were now at hand. For (not to repeat here the vicissitude of my affairs, which you learned from the aforesaid Tengnagel — for it pains me to recall such things, much less narrate them), know that <b>I left the chief part of my stuff, meanwhile as I crossed hither into Bohemia, called most clemently by the Imperial Majesty, at Magdeburg on the Elbe, as it were in the middle of Germany</b>, until I should be made more certain of His Majesty's most clement will — among which are kept not only some of my Instruments (more than I brought here), but also <b>the greatest part of my Works, both printed and manuscript</b>. And although the Imperial Majesty several weeks ago clemently wrote and ordered the Magdeburgers to see all my things kept there brought hither into Bohemia by way of the Elbe, yet, winter intervening (which could easily bring the river's freezing and various impediments), they had to postpone it to the coming spring. Whence it comes that I cannot now succour your wishes in this part, however much I would. But <b>I hope the first Volume of our <i>Progymnasmata</i> of restored Astronomy will shortly, Heaven favouring, be published; and I will soon impart a copy to you. There, in the first chapter, you will sufficiently find what you desire in the calculation of the course of both Luminaries.</b> Meanwhile bear this incidental little delay patiently. On the remaining Planets I still strenuously labour; for the matter is of greater difficulty, and liable to more convolutions, than has hitherto been believed. What I have so far accomplished in these and other things conducing to the Astronomical business — the heavenly Author himself aspiring [it] — <b>the book which Tengnagel gave you as a gift from me, on the Mechanical part of Astronomy [the <i>Mechanica</i>], will teach near its end.</b> Which, together with the other, on the positions of the fixed stars restored with greater labour than esteem, I rejoice that you received; and I should wish you to keep them to yourself alone, or at least not share them with the unworthy and ungrateful.</p><p>The observations which you long ago promised you would have made in Italy, by new Instruments fashioned in imitation of certain of mine (of which I urged you before too by letter), I have not yet been able to behold. I should indeed long for some such thing to be diligently done there — <b>whence the smaller inclination of the Sphere with you than here would grant greater convenience for rightly examining the stars, especially the more southern.</b> Nor do you lack talents apt for anything, if only they be instructed and applied in such; nor do I doubt that the richer, who are taken with this sublime knowledge, will not unwillingly supply the costs — which will be honourable to them too. What is chiefly to be done and chosen in these, that all may be made mechanically exact and useful, the book of which I spoke, which you have, will give occasion. <b>Move every stone, then, that we may exercise Astronomy not under roof and little huts (as is now commonly done), but under the open sky and the very Heaven (as is fitting), in imitation of the ancients — and so be regarded in the doing.</b></p><p>Farewell, and spend this Jubilee Year, and many others, happily in the promulgation of this never-sufficiently-praised Art.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, 3 January 1600.<br>Most zealous of Your Excellency,<br><b>Tycho Brahe.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> (1555–1617), professor of mathematics at <b>Bologna</b> (cf. his letter, brahe-t_16010220001). The letter survives in <b>Magini's printed <i>Tabulae Primi Mobilis</b></i>.</li><li><b>Significance — the Italian parallel to the Brasch commission: a panegyric history of astronomy, and the naming of Tycho's enemies.</b></li><li><b>The panegyric-history project (cf. brahe-t_16010414001 to Brasch).</b> Tycho asks <b>Magini to arrange a Latin <i>panegyric</i> (not dialogues)</b> on the history of astronomy — the <b>origin and migrations of the Art</b>, culminating in <b>Rudolf II and Tycho</b> — sending Tycho the draft within two months for revision. He supplies a \"brief discourse on the occasions of my departure from Denmark,\" relevant letters, and <b>verses</b>. <i>(Tycho is orchestrating his own literary monument in parallel across Germany [Brasch] and Italy [Magini].)</i></li><li><b>The <i>translatio astronomiae</i>.</b> Greece → Italy/Spain → the Germans → Denmark (\"toward the North\"); there it could go no further \"for the Arctic cold and the excessive inclination of the sphere,\" so it <b>returned to Germany — to Bohemia, \"the heart of Germany,\" to Rudolf II</b> — fulfilling a (here-omitted) line of <b>Manilius to Augustus</b>.</li><li><b>Naming the villains (the Herostratus device).</b> A pointed contrast to the Pinelli/Grynaeus letters: Tycho insists his <b>fatherland, King, and the upright nobility be spared and excused</b> — but that <b>\"the modern Chancellor and the Court-Master\"</b> (his enemies, \"very little tinged with the liberal sciences,\" who \"out of avarice and sordidness … impeded and exterminated\" Danish astronomy) be <b>expressly named and made infamous</b>, \"like the man who burned the temple of Diana at Ephesus\" (<b>Herostratus</b>). Their names are in the enclosed Latin account of his departure. <i>(A rare, explicit demand to immortalise his persecutors by infamy.)</i></li><li><b>Stadius.</b> Recommends (with correction) <b>Johannes Stadius's</b> <i>Tabulae Bergenses</i> notes as a source — \"readier than truer,\" rightly refuted by Magini; \"let us spare the shade of the dead\" (<i>Est aliquid prodire tenus</i>, Horace).</li><li><b>Ephemerides &amp; tables.</b> Magini has <b>augmented his Ephemerides by a decade</b>; the rival \"at Frankfurt-on-Oder\" (the ephemerides from 1595 = <b>David Origanus</b>) carped at trifles. Tycho cannot yet share his own <b>luminary tables</b> (manuscript, stuck with the bulk of his <b>works and instruments at Magdeburg</b> awaiting the Elbe thaw), but promises the <b>first volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</b></i> soon (its ch. 1 = the luminary calculations).</li><li><b>The <i>Mechanica</i> gift to Magini.</b> Confirms <b>Tengnagel gave Magini the <i>Mechanica</i> (\"on the Mechanical part of Astronomy\") and the fixed-star book</b> (\"restored with greater labour than esteem\") — to be kept private. <i>(A </i>Mechanica<i> distribution event.)</i></li><li><b>The southern-observation appeal &amp; the open-sky credo.</b> Tycho urges Magini to build Tycho-style instruments and observe in Italy, <b>exploiting Italy's lower latitude for the southern stars</b> (cf. the Egypt plan, brahe-t_16000124001), funded by wealthy enthusiasts — and ends with his manifesto: <b>\"exercise Astronomy not under roof and little huts … but under the open sky, in imitation of the ancients, and so be regarded in the doing.\"</b></li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pair with <b>brahe-t_16010414001</b> (the Brasch History-of-Astronomy) as the <b>twin literary-monument commissions</b>; with <b>brahe-t_16010220001</b> (Magini's letter) and <b>brahe-t_16000103001</b> (Pinelli, same day, same Italian burst); the southern-observation thread (brahe-t_16000124001); and the <b>Magdeburg-storage</b> and <b>Mechanica-distribution</b> threads.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Magini; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Johannes Stadius</b> (<i>Tabulae Bergenses</i>); <b>Manilius / Augustus</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <b>modern [Danish] Chancellor and Court-Master</b> (Tycho's enemies, named only in the enclosure); <b>Herostratus</b> (the unnamed Ephesus arsonist); <b>David Origanus</b> (the Frankfurt-on-Oder ephemerides rival, unnamed); the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, the <i>Mechanica</i>. Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Bologna</b>, <b>Magdeburg/Elbe</b>, Greece/Italy/Spain/Germany/Denmark (the <i>translatio</i>).</li><li><b>Text note (RESOLVED 2026-06-02, §1 collation).</b> The Manilius couplet \"to Augustus\" is blank after <i>protulit:</i> in the working transcription, but <b>Dreyer prints it</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 244): <i>Regales animos primum dignata movere, / Proxima tangentes Coelo fastigia rerum</i> (= <i>Astronomica</i> I.7–8). Supplied editorially in the Source and translation per §1 (the edition of record), in [square brackets].</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>3 Ian. 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>3 January 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree. (Same day as the Pinelli letter, brahe-t_16000103001.)</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII p.229: 'BONONIENSEM' -&gt; 'BONONIENSIUM' (case agreement, 'Gymnasium of the Bolognese'); 'reas visa' (a wrongly split word) -&gt; 'revisa' (reviewed). Neither correction affects the published English translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>panegyric-history commission</b> to Magini and its <i>translatio astronomiae</i> structure; the demand to <b>spare King/nobility but name the Chancellor and Court-Master like Herostratus</b>; the Stadius source-caveat; the Magini-ephemerides and Origanus-rival note; Tycho's inability to share the luminary tables (Magdeburg storage) with the <i>Progymnasmata</i> promised; the <b>Mechanica/fixed-star gift via Tengnagel</b>; and the <b>southern-observation appeal and open-sky credo</b>) is firmly construed and pairs with the Brasch and Pinelli letters. The Manilius couplet, blank in the transcription, is now <b>supplied from Dreyer</b> (VIII p. 244) in square brackets per §1.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images visually (not the OCR text layer) across all 6 slice pages (chunks 1-4, then 5-6): TBOO VIII pp.228 (tail of prior letter), 229-232 (the Magini letter proper). Collated the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date and place-name: Tengnaglius, Magino, Rudolphus II, Manilius/Augustum (the couplet 'Regales animos primum dignata movere / Proxima tangentes Coelo fastigia rerum', which our source_text left as an editorially-supplied blank, is confirmed printed by Dreyer verbatim as supplied), Stadius/Tabulis Bergensibus, Francofurti ad Oderam, 1595/decennio, Magdeburgi ad Albim, Dabantur ex Arce Caesareana Benatica 3 Ian. 1600 — all match Dreyer except the two TEXT_ERROR items logged above, both single/split-word corruptions confined to our own transcription. The English translation itself was checked clause-by-clause against source_text and is faithful throughout: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found. Grade is downgraded from the self-assigned A to C solely because of the two TEXT_ERROR findings (per the grading rule); the verdict is MINOR because neither error changes the sense conveyed to a reader.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the wrong case ending (BONONIENSEM, accusative singular) where Dreyer prints BONONIENSIUM (genitive plural, correctly agreeing with 'Gymnasio Bononiensium' = Gymnasium of the Bolognese). The English rendering ('Gymnasium of Bologna') is unaffected either way. Dreyer prints: <i>MATHEMATUM IN ILLUSTRI BONONIENSIUM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI (TBOO VIII p.229)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text splits Dreyer's single word 'revisa' (reviewed) into the nonsensical two words 'reas visa'. The English ('approved and enriched') is a reasonable rendering of the correct Dreyer reading regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>...moxque vobis revisa et locupletata remittam. (TBOO VIII p.229)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defect sites directly against the Dreyer page image, TBOO VIII p.229 (letter spans pp.228-233). Both TEXT_ERRORs are confirmed exactly as the audit describes; the English translation was already correct in both places and required no change. No other discrepancies were found in the surrounding text.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_15991104001", "next": "brahe-t_16000304001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991104001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000304001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges Magini's letters via Tengnagel, encloses a brief account of his departure from Denmark and added verses, and urges prompt completion of a promised task.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000103002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000106001", "d": "1600-01-06", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Fels", "ck": "Daniel Fels", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Plzeň", "t": "Daniel Fels → Tycho Brahe — Pilsen [Plzeň], 6 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with German code-switches) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ATQUE MAGNIFICO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, IN KNUDTSTRUPP ET URANIBURG, ET DOMINO SUO, FAUTORI AC PROMOTORI SUMME HONORANDO.</p><p>Salutem plurimam D.</p><p>PRAeTER omnem spem et expectationem meam accidit, Generose Domine TYCHO, fautor ac promotor honorandissime, ut hîc diutius moras trahere cogar. Nihilominus tamen ex voto et animi sententia mihi hactenus omnia successerunt, et uti spero amodo etiam successura esse. Tuas a Consobrino tuo CLAUDIO BILLEO cum Idea sive typo Structurae novae Benachiensis 8 Idus Ianuarij sartas tectasque accepi, quibus properante hinc Posta confestim rescribo. Ideam adhuc hodie Magnifico Domino BARVITIO, Caesareae Maiestati humilime ad exhibendum, offeram, caeteraque sedulo et diligenter, quae in memoriale assignata reperi, exequar. Caesarea Maiestas cum quotidie tui ad D. BARVITIUM fecisset mentionem, deque te Caesareo favore valde sollicita esset, quinam valeres, quidnam rerum ageres, an nihil ei transmitteres, Dominus BARVITIUS, me quidem inscio, tua Progymnasmata Astronomiae ad ipsius Maiestatem detulit. Dominus BARVITIUS ad me aiebat: »Ich muste nur Ihrer Röm. Kay. Mts. etwas weisenn von dem Herrn TYCHONE, damit von Ihr Kay. Mts. ich nit so offters gefraget werde, weil ich dan sobaldt nichts darauf zu andtwortenn weis, und zue fridenn bleibe.«. Quae ipsius Caesarea Maiestas actutum aperuit et per integrum diem vultu hilari et clementissimo, imo paterno erga te quem gerit animo, clementissime perlustravit, uti coram plenius id tibi aperiam; per festinationem enim Postae commode hoc fieri nunc nequit. Annuatim a Caesarea Maiestate clementissime ex ferarum genere tres tibi sunt deputatae, et aliquot Züber (uti in Bohemia vocant) piscium, de qua re ex mandato ipsius Caes. Maiestatis a Domino BARVITIO ad Dominum Capitaneum Brandeisensem iam scriptum est, nec non de Praedio, ut aliquod eligat, et per primam occasionem, quid ille sentiat, Dominum BARVITIUM certiorem faciat, quas literas mecum dante Deo afferam. Pecunias antea promissas post diuturnam moram etiam nunc obtinui. Magnificus Dominus CORRADUCIUS te officiose et peramanter resalutat omniaque felicia precatur; dicta salute et congratulatione ad me dicebat: »Pro congratulatione hac quam mihi nuncias, et salute quam nomine Domini TYCHONIS BRAHE dicis, gratias tibi ago maximas; vicissim eum, cui mea studia offero, peramice salutabis. Ego illum virum tanto amore prosequor, itaque ipsi faveo, licet de facie mihi ignotus sit, ut nîl supra, non modo ob insignem ac praeclaram quam prositetur artem Astronomicam, sed et ob generis splendorem, dan er eines tapfferenn Geschlechts und Herkommens, auch vornehmer Man, dessen gleichenn izo in toto terrarum Orbe nit zu findenn, utinam ut quam primum copia mihi eum videndi et cum ipso loquendi se offerat; sed id brevi fore puto.« Dominus TYPOTIUS ante biduum huc Pilsnam Lipsiâ appulit, qui etiam te salva impertit salute.</p><p>Iamque diutissime ac felicissime cum tota Domo vale viveque annos Platonicos.<br>Dabantur calamo celeri Pilsnae ipso die quo mihi tuae cum modella sunt redditae, Anno assertae salutis 1600.<br>Tae Mae addictiss.<br>DANIEL FELS,<br>manu propria.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 233; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Quod meae superioribus diebus tardius quam debebant tibi sint redditae, culpa mihi non est danda, sed id vel Postae, per quam rescripseram actutum, vel Domino Doctori HELLERO, ad quem meas direxeram, imputandum. Cum viaticum mihi desit, nec hic mutuo accipere possum nummos, scripsi D. HELLERO, ut per Postam 8 Ioachimicos mihi, bonâ ac fide et gratia a te restituendos, transmittere velit. Iterum vale measque festinationi ignosce, meque incolumem tu incolumis expecta. Progymnasmata tua Astron. a Caes. Maiestate nondum recepi, quotidie adhuc ipsius Caes. Maiestas ea perlustrat.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous and magnificent man, Lord Tycho Brahe, in Knudstrup and Uraniborg, and his Lord, patron and promoter most highly to be honoured.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Beyond all my hope and expectation it has fallen out, generous Lord Tycho, most honoured patron and promoter, that I am compelled to draw out my stay here longer. Nevertheless all things have so far succeeded for me according to my wish and mind's intent, and, as I hope, will succeed henceforth too. Your [letter], with the design or model of the new Benátky structure, I received safe and sound from your cousin Claus Bille on the 6th of January, to which — as the post hastens from here — I reply at once. The design I shall today offer to the Magnificent Lord Barvitius, to be humbly laid before the Imperial Majesty, and I shall carefully and diligently carry out the rest which I found set down in the memorandum. Since the Imperial Majesty daily made mention of you to Lord Barvitius, and was very solicitous about you, with imperial favour — how you fared, what you were doing, whether you were sending him nothing — Lord Barvitius, without my knowledge indeed, brought your <i>Progymnasmata of Astronomy</i> to His Majesty. Lord Barvitius said to me: <i>\"I simply had to show His Roman Imperial Majesty something of Lord Tycho, so that I should not be asked by His Imperial Majesty so often — since I then know of nothing to answer thereto at once — and may be left in peace.\"</i> Which [book] His Imperial Majesty at once opened, and for a whole day, with a cheerful and most clement countenance — indeed with the paternal disposition he bears toward you — most graciously perused; as I shall more fully disclose to you in person, for through the haste of the post this cannot now be conveniently done. Annually there have been most graciously assigned to you by the Imperial Majesty three [head] of the wild-game kind, and several <i>Züber</i> (as they call them in Bohemia) of fish — concerning which, by the Imperial Majesty's mandate, Lord Barvitius has already written to the Lord Captain of Brandeis; and also concerning an estate, that you may choose one, and at the first opportunity inform Lord Barvitius what you think of it — which letters, God granting, I shall bring with me. The money promised before, after a long delay, I have now likewise obtained. The Magnificent Lord Coraduz returns your greeting dutifully and most lovingly, and prays every happiness for you; the greeting and congratulation delivered, he said to me: <i>\"For this congratulation which you bring me, and the greeting you say in the name of Lord Tycho Brahe, I give you the greatest thanks; in turn you will greet most amicably him to whom I offer my devotion. I pursue that man with such great love, and so favour him — although he is unknown to me by face — that nothing could be beyond it: not only for the distinguished and illustrious astronomical art which he professes, but also for the splendour of his lineage, for he is of a valiant family and descent, and a distinguished man besides, whose like is not now to be found in all the world. Would that the chance of seeing him and speaking with him might offer itself as soon as may be — but that, I think, will be soon.\"</i> Lord Typotius arrived here at Pilsen from Leipzig two days ago, who also bestows on you a safe greeting.</p><p>And now, most long and most happily, fare and live, with all your House, the Platonic years.<br>Given with swift pen at Pilsen, on the very day on which your [letter], with the model, was delivered to me, in the year of asserted salvation 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Daniel Fels,<br>with his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> That my [earlier letters] were delivered to you these last days later than they ought to have been, the blame is not to be laid on me, but is to be imputed either to the Post, by which I had at once written back, or to Lord Doctor Heller, to whom I had directed mine. Since I lack travel-money, and cannot here borrow coin, I have written to Doctor Heller to be willing to send me by the Post 8 Joachimsthalers — to be repaid by you in good faith and favour. Again farewell, and pardon my haste, and, sound yourself, await me sound. Your <i>Progymnasmata of Astronomy</i> I have not yet received back from the Imperial Majesty: the Imperial Majesty still peruses it daily.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Daniel Fels</b>, Tycho's <i>familiaris</i> and <b>on-the-spot agent at the imperial court</b> (then at Pilsen), who managed Tycho's court business and carried letters (cf. brahe-t_16000306002, where Tycho calls him \"my familiar Daniel Fels, who has stayed for some while at Pilsen with you\").</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's court-business at Pilsen, January 1600.</b> A detailed dispatch on the negotiations that secured Tycho's imperial establishment:</li><li><b>The Benátky building plans.</b> Fels has received from Tycho's cousin <b>Claus Bille</b> (<i>Claudius Billeus</i>, <i>consobrinus</i>) Tycho's letter with the <i>\"design or model of the new Benátky structure\"</i> (Tycho's projected observatory/works at Benátky), to be laid before the Emperor via Barvitius, plus a <i>memoriale</i> (memorandum) of further requests.</li><li><b>The Progymnasmata before the Emperor.</b> <i>Dominus Barvitius</i> = <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>, Rudolf II's Latin secretary, who — pressed daily by the Emperor for news of Tycho — had on his own initiative laid Tycho's <b>_Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata_</b> before His Majesty, who \"for a whole day … most graciously perused\" it \"with the paternal disposition he bears toward you.\"</li><li><b>The court provisions &amp; estate.</b> The Emperor has granted Tycho an <b>annual allowance of three head of game and several <i>Züber</i> (tubs) of fish</b> (the German measure, glossed by Fels), arranged through the <b>Captain of Brandeis (Brandýs nad Labem)</b>, the royal estate near Benátky; and Tycho is invited to <b>choose an estate (<i>praedium</i>)</b>. The previously promised <b>money</b> has at last been paid.</li><li><b>The Coraduz greeting.</b> <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor; cf. brahe-t_16000324001) returns Tycho's greeting with an effusive testimonial.</li><li><b>German code-switches (kept and flagged; protocol §2).</b> Two passages of <b>direct speech break from Latin into German</b>:</li><li><i>Barvitius:</i> \"<i>Ich muste nur Ihrer Röm. Kay. Mts. etwas weisen von dem Herrn Tychone, damit … ich nit so offters gefraget werde … und zufrieden bleibe.</i>\" (\"I simply had to show His Imperial Majesty something of Lord Tycho, so as not to be asked so often … and be left in peace.\")</li><li><i>Coraduz</i> (macaronic, mid-sentence): \"<i>…sed et ob generis splendorem, <b>dan er eines tapferen Geschlechts und Herkommens, auch vornehmer Mann, dessen gleichen jetzt in toto terrarum orbe nicht zu finden</b>…</i>\" (\"…but also for the splendour of his lineage, <b>for he is of a valiant family and descent, and a distinguished man besides, whose like is not now to be found in the whole world</b>…\"). The Latin↔German switch within Coraduz's own quoted words is preserved.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Daniel Fels; <b>Claus Bille</b> (Tycho's cousin); <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b> (imperial secretary); <b>Rudolf Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b>; <i>Dominus Typotius</i> = <b>Jacobus Typotius</b> (Jacques Typoets, 1540–1601), the imperial historiographer, just arrived from Leipzig.</li><li><b>Flourishes / dating (protocol §3).</b> <i>vive annos Platonicos</i> = \"live the <b>Platonic years</b>\" (the Great Year — a hyperbolic long-life wish). <i>8 Idus Ianuarij</i> = the <b>6th of January</b> (the Roman-calendar idiom, glossed); the dateline repeats \"the very day your letter … was delivered.\" <i>Anno assertae salutis 1600</i> = \"in the year of asserted salvation 1600\"; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (the delivery-delay apology, the request to Dr Heller for 8 Joachimsthalers, \"the Emperor still peruses the Progymnasmata daily\"), restored from Dreyer VIII p.233 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is read securely and the two embedded German speeches are rendered with their code-switches preserved and flagged; the substance (the Benátky plans to Barvitius; the Progymnasmata laid before Rudolf II; the annual game-and-fish allowance via the Captain of Brandeis; the offer of an estate; the paid money; Coraduz's macaronic testimonial; Typotius's arrival) is clear and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; <i>Züber</i> and the Roman-calendar date are glossed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.231-234 as page images; the audited letter (Daniel Fels to Tycho, 6 Jan 1600 N.S.) runs pp.232(bottom)-233, ending with the signature 'Daniel Fels, manu propria' followed immediately (no separate heading) by the postscript paragraph, which is present in Dreyer's continuous text on p.233 — confirming the bracketed apparatus note ('restored... dropped by the working transcription') describes a repair already correctly made, not an outstanding defect. Collated opening/closing, every proper name (Claudio Billeo/Claus Bille, Barvitio/Barvitius, Corraducius, Typotius), the German-language quotations verbatim, the date (8 Idus Ianuarij = 6 January), and the postscript's sum (8 Ioachimicos) against Dreyer; source_text and the English translation both match fully. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found anywhere in the letter including the postscript.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Confirms receipt of Tycho's Benátky design, reports it will be presented to Barvitius for the Emperor, and relays that Rudolf asks daily after Tycho.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000106001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000107001", "d": "1600-01-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Bartholomäus Scultetus — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 7 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basileensi G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>S. CLARISSIME ET ERUDITISSIME DOMINE BARTHOLOMAeE SCULTETE VETERI AMICITIA MIHI IUNCTISSIME.</p><p>ACCESSUM meum in Bohemiam, quod a Caesâ Mate hîc Clementissime receptus sim, tibi iam antea innotuisse, ex Domino Doctore SCHWALMIO communi nostro amico, paulo postquam Pragam adveneram, percepi. Rediens enim ille ex Silesiâ attulit schedam quandam memorialem tuâ manu exaratam, in qua Progymnasmatum meorum tomum primum tecum communicari rogabas: ac responsum ad meas, quas idem tibi tradidit, nullum reportavit, uti neque ad illas, quas Wittebergâ ad te dedi, rescribere voluisti, traditâ saltem tabellario chartulâ, quae probaret, eum meas exhibuisse. Quî vero fiat, quod in respondendo tam difficilis sis, equidem ignoro. Si curae tam publicae quam privatae impedimento sunt, scias me non minoribus distineri, et tamen haud intermittere, quin amicis scribam. Sed si in posterum hanc desidiam emendâris, erit veniae locus.</p><p>Progymnasmata desiderata mittere necdum potui, quia primus quaternio nondum impressus est, uti neque Conclusio; et deficiunt alias in primo capite sex quaterniones de motu ☾ae, quos Hamburgi quidem typis imprimi mandavi; sed in absentiâ meâ adeo depravate id factum est, ut eos recudere oporteat. Postquam vero in Bohemiam concessi, nihil eiuscemodi hucusque praestare licuit, partim ob pestem Pragae aliquamdiu grassantem, quodque isthic typographias numeris et figuris Astronomicis aptas non habeant, partim quod propriam meam Magdaeburgi unâ cum quibusdam ex meis Instrumentis atque supellectile domesticâ, interea dum ego in Bohemiam tendo, relictam ante hyemem me obtenturum frustra crediderim. Mandârat siquidem Caesa Matas per literas Magistratui eius loci, ut res meas omnes isthic conservatas mature huc in Bohemiam per Albim transvehi procuraret. Verum dum isthic moras nectunt, et nescio quae subterfugia quaerunt, superveniens hyemis asperitas id ante ver instans fieri posse vetuit. Sicque tam illud opus, quam alia a me inchoata et necdum typis plane consummata absolvere, postquam huc veni, non est concessum. Interim tamen in hac Arce Caesaris Benaticâ structurae pro Instrumentis meis Astronomicis ordine et sigillatim disponendis iussu eius Matis confectae sunt, in quarum aliquas ea, quae mecum attuli, iam disposita sunt, donec coetera, quibus relicta sunt convenientia loca, advenerint. Laboratorium etiam Spagyricum pro Pyronomicis meis exercitijs, quibus unâ delector, paratum est; satisque commode in hac Arce, quam tribus alijs pariter oblatis praetuli, cum meis habito; et de necessarijs a Caesâ Mate mihi liberaliter, quantum haec ferunt difficilia tempora, provisum. Absum autem hîc a vestro Gorlitzio vix plus itinere duorum dierum, aestivo praesertim tempore, ut facilior et crebrior (modo sic volueris) literarum inter nos commutatio fieri possit. Et si me aliquando hîc invisere tibi grave non fuerit, advenies non tam exoptatissimus hospes, quam Amicus antiquus nunquam antiquandus, sed perpetuo mihi futurus charissimus.</p><p>Quoniam vero intellexi Gorlitzij apud vos esse excultas Typographias, quae stylorum formâ et varietate praepolleant, simulque moletrinas papyro conficiendae idoneas ibi reperiri, cuperem a te resciscere, quali precio quaternio impressus in formâ papyri maiusculâ, quam Regalem vocant, ecudatur, idque in quarto, ita ut munda et nitida sit papyrus, typi vero partim ex cursivâ maiusculâ, partim minori et antiquâ mediocri, incidentibus nonnunquam quibusdam tabulis et figuris Geometricis, ita ut Exemplaria sint 1500, et quot quaterniones singulis hebdomadibus absolvi queant, quidque correctori idoneo numerandum. Ut vero mentem meam circa haec rectius percipias, apposui unum vel alterum quaternionem, formam papyri atque impressionis rationem ostendentem. Coetera per temetipsum facile capies atque dispones. Da itaque operam, amantissime SCULTETE, ut votis meis hac in parte respondeat eventus, idque mature resciscam, quo Exemplaria unâ cum pecuniâ necessariâ, quam tuae fidei committam, isthuc tempestive transmittere queam. Quae illuc primo quoque tempore labore typographico compleri averem, haec ferme sunt. In volumine primo Progymnasmatum instaurationis Astronomicae dimidium circiter Alphabetum hinc inde desideratur; ideoque quam citissime fieri queat, convenientibus locis supplendum. In libro illo de Cometâ Anni 77, quem dudum a me obtinuisti, subiunxi postea Apologetica quaedam scripta, quae Hamburgi quidem impressa sunt; sed refutatio cuiusdam Eruditi in Scotiâ viri, qui contra eiusdem libri praecipua contenta nonnulla inferre praesumpsit, necdum addita, quae forte quaterniones 16 adimplere poterit. Praeterea librum secundum Epistolarum Astronomicarum e nostris, quem paulo antequam Daniâ excessi, typis proprijs excudi inchoâram. Sed solummodo absoluto dimidio Alphabeto mihi inde in Germaniam emigrandum erat, sicque opera illa ob varias intercidentes irrequietudines intermissa est. Restant duo circiter Alphabeta plus minus typis pertexenda, idque totum minori illâ antiquâ, qualem primus Epistolarum tomus iam antea editus (quem proculdubio habes) exhibet.</p><p>Hos inquam libros iam antea typis nostris inceptos isthic apud vos ad Colophonem opere Typographico quam celerrime diduci posse, ut hoc Anno Iubilaeo publici iuris fierent, plurimum exoptarem. Fac igitur me de singulis huc pertinentibus per primam quamque occasionem (siquidem proprijs typis id nunc fieri nequeat) certiorem, et cum typographis vestris atque papyri confectoribus, prout aequum fuerit, transige, et quid ad minimum exigant, significa. Non poterunt mihi facile in hoc utroque negocio imponere, cum ipsemet utrumque, tam quoad typographicas impensas, quam papyri praeparationem in Insulâ olim Uraniburgicâ diu multumque proprijs sumptibus exercere curârim. Confido autem, sic persuadente veteri inter nos necessitudine, te desiderijs meis hac in parte non defuturum. Feceris sic non saltem mihi rem gratam et amicitiâ nostrâ haud indignam, sed et Divinae illius Astronomiae cultoribus inprimis acceptam nec (uti spero) inutilem. Cumque tu non postremum inter eos locum obtineas, te sponte tuâ rem omnem promoturum, nihil addubito. Sicque eo citius et promptius Exemplaria Operum meorum, quae requiris, nanciscêre.</p><p>Vale et actutum rescribe, meque de singulis diligenter admone. Opto tibi cum omnibus tuis hunc praesentem Annum Iubilaeum multosque alios instantes auspicatos et salutares. Iterumque vale. Dabantur ex Arce Benaticâ 5 milliaribus Pragâ versus Borrhapelioten remotâ Anno Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae 1600, quem tamen duobus Annis deficere IOSEPHUS SCALIGER in renovato Opere de Emendatione temporum (uti prius unico) contendit, Ianuarij die 7° stylo renovato. Tu, si potes (uti forte potes, siquidem in similibus diligenter versatus) hanc litem dirime.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Greeting. Most renowned and most learned Lord Bartholomäus Scultetus, most closely joined to me by old friendship.</b></p><p>That I had come into Bohemia, and was here most clemently received by the Imperial Majesty, had — I perceived, a little after I reached Prague — already become known to you, through <b>Doctor Schwalm, our common friend</b>. For he, returning from Silesia, brought a certain memorial-note written in your hand, in which you asked that the first volume of my <i>Progymnasmata</i> be shared with you; but he brought back no answer to mine, which he had delivered to you — just as you were unwilling to write back to those I sent you from Wittenberg, [or] at least to give the courier a slip proving that he had presented mine. <b>Why it comes about that you are so difficult in replying, I do not know.</b> If public and private cares are a hindrance, know that I am held by no smaller ones, and yet do not fail to write to my friends. But if hereafter you mend this slackness, there will be room for pardon.</p><p><b>The <i>Progymnasmata</i> you desire I have not yet been able to send, because the first quaternion is not yet printed, nor the Conclusion; and there are also wanting, in the first chapter, six quaternions on the motion of the Moon [☾], which I had ordered printed at Hamburg — but in my absence it was done so corruptly that they must be reprinted.</b> After I withdrew into Bohemia, I have hitherto been able to do nothing of the kind — partly on account of the plague raging some while at Prague, and because there they have no printing-houses fit for astronomical numerals and figures; partly because I had vainly believed I should obtain, before winter, my own [press], left at <b>Magdeburg</b> together with some of my Instruments and household goods while I made for Bohemia. For the Imperial Majesty had by letter ordered the Magistrate of that place to see all my things kept there transported in good time hither into Bohemia by the Elbe; but while they weave delays there, and seek I know not what subterfuges, the oncoming harshness of winter forbade it could be done before the coming spring. And so neither that work, nor others begun by me and not yet quite finished in print, has it been granted me to complete since I came hither.</p><p><b>Meanwhile, however, in this imperial Castle of Benátky, structures have been made, by His Majesty's order, for arranging my Astronomical Instruments in order and severally</b>; in some of which what I brought with me is already placed, until the rest — for which fit places are left — shall arrive. <b>A Spagyric [alchemical] Laboratory, too, for my Pyronomic exercises, in which I likewise delight, has been prepared</b>; and I dwell comfortably enough, with my [household], in this Castle — which I preferred to three others likewise offered; and I have been liberally provided by the Imperial Majesty with necessaries, so far as these difficult times allow. <b>I am here scarcely more than a two-days' journey from your Görlitz</b> (especially in summer-time), so that an easier and more frequent exchange of letters between us can be made (if only you so wish). And if it be not a burden to you to visit me here sometime, you will come not so much a most longed-for guest as an old Friend, never to grow old [to me], but to be forever most dear.</p><p>But since I have learned that at Görlitz, with you, there are <b>excellent printing-houses, pre-eminent in the form and variety of their types, and likewise paper-mills fit for making paper</b>: I should wish to learn from you <b>at what price a quaternion is printed in the larger paper-format which they call Royal — and that in quarto — so that the paper be clean and bright, the types partly of the larger cursive, partly of the smaller and middling antique, with certain Geometric tables and figures sometimes falling in; so that there be 1500 copies; and how many quaternions can be finished each week; and what is to be paid a fit corrector.</b> That you may the more rightly grasp my mind about these, I have appended one or two quaternions, showing the paper-format and the manner of printing. The rest you will easily take in and arrange by yourself. Take care, then, most loving Scultetus, that the outcome answer my wishes in this part, and that I learn it in good time, that I may seasonably send thither the copies, together with the necessary money, which I will commit to your trust.</p><p>What I should wish to have completed there, at the first opportunity, by the printer's labour, are about these: <b>In the first volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> of the Astronomical Restoration, about half an alphabet [of gatherings] is wanting here and there</b>, and so is to be supplied, in fit places, as quickly as can be. <b>In that book on the Comet of the year [15]77</b> — which you obtained from me long ago — I afterward subjoined certain Apologetic writings, which were indeed printed at Hamburg; <b>but the refutation of a certain Erudite man in Scotland, who presumed to bring some things against the chief contents of that book, is not yet added — which may perhaps fill 16 quaternions.</b> Besides, <b>the second volume of our Astronomical Letters</b>, which a little before I left Denmark I had begun to print with my own types — but, only half an alphabet finished, I had to emigrate thence into Germany, and so that work was interrupted by various intervening unrests. There remain about two alphabets, more or less, to be woven out in type, and that whole [in] the smaller antique [type], such as the first volume of the Letters, already published (which you doubtless have), exhibits.</p><p>These books, I say — already begun in our types — <b>I should most greatly wish could be drawn out there with you, by the printer's work, to the Colophon as swiftly as possible, that they might be made public in this Jubilee Year</b> [1600]. Make me, then, informed of every particular pertaining hither, by the first occasion (since it cannot now be done with my own types); and transact with your printers and papermakers, as shall be fair, and signify what they demand at the least. They will not easily be able to impose on me in either business, since I myself took care to practise both — as much the printing costs as the preparation of paper — long and much, at my own expense, on the once-Uraniborgian Island. And I trust — the old bond between us so persuading — that you will not fail my wishes in this part. So you will do a thing not only welcome to me, and not unworthy of our friendship, but especially acceptable, and (as I hope) not useless, to the cultivators of that divine Astronomy. And since you hold no last place among them, I in no way doubt that you will of your own accord promote the whole matter. And so the more quickly and readily will you obtain the copies of my Works which you require.</p><p>Farewell, and write back at once, and admonish me diligently of every particular. I wish you, with all yours, this present <b>Jubilee Year</b>, and many others to come, auspicious and wholesome. And again farewell. Given from the Castle of Benátky, <b>5 miles from Prague toward the north-east [Borrhapeliotes]</b>, in the year of the Christian Dionysian Era 1600 — which, however, <b>Joseph Scaliger, in his renewed work <i>On the Emendation of Times</i>, contends to be deficient by two years (as before by one)</b> — the 7th day of January, by the renewed [New] style. You, if you can (as perhaps you can, being diligently versed in such things), settle this dispute.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (1540–1614), the Görlitz mathematician, cartographer, and burgomaster — Tycho's old friend (cf. the chronology-jest at brahe-t_16000327002, where he appears with the chronologers).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's publication program and the bid to print at Görlitz (a key bibliographical document).</b></li><li><b>The state of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</b> Vol. I still lacks <b>its first quaternion, the Conclusion, and six quaternions on the lunar motion</b> — the latter <b>printed corruptly at Hamburg in Tycho's absence and needing reprinting</b> (cf. brahe-t_15990922001). \"About half an alphabet of gatherings\" is wanting throughout.</li><li><b>The Görlitz printing proposal.</b> Tycho asks Scultetus to price, at the <b>Görlitz presses and paper-mills</b>, a quaternion in <b>\"Royal\" large paper, quarto, 1500 copies</b>, with mixed type (large cursive + smaller antique) and <b>geometrical figures</b> — enclosing <b>sample quaternions</b>. He notes he cannot be cheated, having <b>run both press and papermaking himself at Uraniborg</b>.</li><li><b>Three works to finish:</b> (1) the <b><i>Progymnasmata</i> vol. I</b>; (2) the <b>Comet-of-1577 book</b>, to which must still be added <b>the refutation of \"a certain Erudite man in Scotland\" — i.e. John Craig</b> (the comet dispute; ~16 quaternions); (3) the <b><i>Astronomical Letters</i> vol. II</b>, begun on Tycho's own press in Denmark (only half an alphabet done), ~2 alphabets remaining — all to be \"made public in this <b>Jubilee Year</b> 1600.\"</li><li><b>The Benátky establishment.</b> <b>Purpose-built housings for the instruments</b> by imperial order (some already installed); <b>a Spagyric (alchemical) laboratory for Tycho's \"Pyronomic exercises\"</b> — a vivid record of Tycho's chemistry continuing at Benátky; the castle chosen over three others; ~2 days from Görlitz.</li><li><b>The Magdeburg-press delay</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000405001, brahe-t_16000124001): his own press and goods stuck at Magdeburg, the city \"weaving delays,\" winter blocking the Elbe.</li><li><b>The Scaliger chronology dispute.</b> The dateline again raises <b>Scaliger's <i>De emendatione temporum</b></i> claim that the era is \"deficient by two years (as before by one)\" — and challenges the chronologist Scultetus to \"settle this dispute.\" (Same controversy as brahe-t_16000327002.)</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Bartholomäus Scultetus; <b>Dr. Schwalm</b> (common friend); <b>John Craig</b> (\"a certain Erudite man in Scotland\"); <b>Joseph Scaliger</b> (<i>De emendatione temporum</i>); <b>Rudolf II</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b> (\"5 miles NE of Prague\"), <b>Prague</b>, <b>Görlitz</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, the <b>Elbe</b>, <b>Silesia</b>, <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Uraniborg</b>.</li><li><b>Notable terms.</b> <i>Spagyricum laboratorium / Pyronomica exercitia</i> — alchemical/chemical (fire-based) work. <i>Borrhapeliotes</i> — the <b>north-east</b> (Greek wind-name; Benátky lies NE of Prague). <i>Alphabetum</i> — a printer's run of gatherings (A–Z); \"half an alphabet\" ≈ 11–12 gatherings.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Ianuarij die 7° <b>stylo renovato</b></i> → <b>7 January 1600 [N.S.]</b> (slug `16000107001` agrees; the label carries only \"Benatky\"). The \"Christian Dionysian Era\" formula and the Scaliger two-year jest are Tycho's chronological flourish.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'exaratum' corrected to 'exaratam' (TBOO VIII p.234), restoring grammatical agreement with the feminine accusative 'schedam'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the reproach for Scultetus's silence; the <b>detailed state of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> printing</b> and the Hamburg-corrupted lunar gatherings; the <b>Görlitz printing proposal</b> with its paper/type/run specifics; the three works to finish, including <b>the anti-Craig refutation</b> for the Comet book and <b>Epistolae vol. II</b>; the <b>Benátky instrument-housings and alchemical laboratory</b>; the Magdeburg-press delay; and the Scaliger chronology dispute) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; the Moon-glyph (☾) and the Greek direction-term are reproduced/glossed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the complete letter -- the Schwalm/Wittenberg correspondence complaint, the Progymnasmata printing status and the corrupted Hamburg printing of the lunar-motion quaternions, the Benatky castle instrument-arrangement and alchemical laboratory passage, the Gorlitz printing-cost inquiry (1500 copies, Royal paper format, quarto, type sizes), the list of works to be finished (the comet-of-77 book and its still-missing 16-quaternion refutation of the Scottish critic, the second volume of Letters), and the closing date formula with the Scaliger calendar dispute -- against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.234-236 (slice pp.2-4), reading page images directly. Slice p.1 confirmed as the tail of the preceding Fels-to-Tycho letter, not part of this one. One source_text-vs-Dreyer divergence found: a single-letter grammatical-agreement corruption that does not affect the English translation's sense, but per protocol any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C. All proper names, numbers (1500 copies, 16 quaternions, 5 miles, two years, day 7), dates, and place-names checked and correct.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text has \"exaratum\", which does not grammatically agree with \"schedam\"; Dreyer prints \"exaratam\", the correct agreement. A one-letter corruption in our Latin apparatus; the English translation (\"written in your hand\") is unaffected and correct either way. Dreyer prints: <i>exaratam (TBOO VIII, p.234) -- correctly agreeing in gender/case with \"schedam\" (feminine accusative)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image TBOO VIII p.234, which reads 'attulit fchedam quandam memorialem tuâ manu exaratam, in quâ Progymnafmatum meorum tomum primum tecum communicari rogabas' — confirming the audit's claim. Only the single-letter Latin corruption required correction; the English was already correct and needed no change. The remainder of the letter was not re-collated beyond the single defect site per the prime rule.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Bartholomaeus Scultetus", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15920312001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Rebukes Scultetus for persistent failure to reply to letters and explains that the first volume of the Progymnasmata cannot yet be sent as printing is incomplete.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000107001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000110001", "d": "1600-01-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Monavius", "ck": "Jacob Monavius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Monau (Monavius) — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 10 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO IACOBO MONAVIO, PATRITIO VRATISLAVIENSI, ILLUSTRISSIMI DUCIS SILESIAe CONSILIARIO DIGNISSIMO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>PAUCIS diebus ex quo Pragam veneram, Excellentissime et amantissime Domine MONAVI, schedam quandam a te e vestra Silesia mihi asportavit Dominus D. SCHWALMIUS, si recte memini: quae nihil aliud quam de Carmine quodam Domini TOBIAe FISCHERI Poëtae vestratis, quâ occasione id in eum modum concinnatum sit, continebat. Grata mihi fuit tua chartula ipsiusque Carmen, in quo paucula verba mutata sunt iuxta censuram criticam, prout in adiunctis pagellis videre est, quibus etiam nonnulla alia, praeter ea, quae prius habes, adscripta sunt. Velim, ut tam haec quam priora cum eodem communices. Scripsissem ipsemet iam illi, si literas eius ad me humanissimas tam cito invenire potuissem. Nescio enim, ubi illas cum quibusdam alijs, interea dum res meae huc Pragâ transferuntur, reposuerim. Quando eas reperire dabitur, rescriptionem non ulterius intermittam. Interim ipsum meo nomine officiose saluta, et pro benevolo suo erga me animo gratias age.</p><p>Scripsi tibi aliquoties antea de libris Witichianis a sorore haerede coëmendis. Quod si iam tua operâ praestari possit, ut iusto precio eos obtinere queam, praesertim quoad Exemplaria quae habuit tria COPERNICI a se marginalibus quibusdam illustrata de Revolutionibus Coelestibus, et quae de Triangulis conscripserat, atque de Hypothesibus Coelestibus si quae inveniuntur, feceris mihi rem apprime gratam. Precium indicatum confestim solvam. Relatum quoque ad me est, quendam apud vos ante aliquot Annos fuisse Astronomiae peritum, qui observationes nonnullas Coelestes suo tempore perfecerat; quas filium ipsius adhuc conservare aiunt. Habitat autem is (si rite audita recordor) in plateâ quam vocant die Stockgasse, nomen eius excidit; tu (opinor) facile id conijcies atque inquires, rogoque peramanter, ut ab ipso impetrare velis earundem observationum, quot et qualescunque fuerint, descriptum mihi concedi. Inveniar vicissim erga illum gratus. Indica insuper, ubi rescripseris (quod te mature facturum confido) an aliqua apud vos sit Typographia, quae tales stylos habeat, qualibus liber primus Epistolarum Astronomicarum e nostris, quem existimo te habere, excusus est. Constitui enim alterum Tomum, in quo 12 primi quaterniones, antequam res meae in Daniâ conturbarentur, absoluti sunt, complere, in eâdem formâ papyri et characteris, quâ prior confectus est. Et transige simul cum Typographo idoneo, quid pro quolibet quaternione, ita ut Exemplaria imprimantur 1500, illi numerandum sit. Et simul papyri valerem indica, quidque correctori gnaro in singulas septimanas dandum. Si intellexero, omnia isthic rite succedere posse, mittam unâ cum Exemplari ad mundum scripto pecuniam, pro quâ papyrus coëmatur, et coetera persolvantur. Ignoscas vero mihi, mi MONAVI, quod te alias occupatissimum meis negotijs sic molestem. Ego quidvis vicissim tuâ caussâ faciam, quâcunque in re mea opera tibi unquam usui esse poterit.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ, ubi nunc cum meis habito, die 10 Ianuarij stylo renovato Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most excellent man, Lord Jacob Monau, patrician of Breslau, most worthy Councillor of the Most Illustrious Duke of Silesia, his friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>A few days after I had come to Prague, most excellent and most loving Lord Monau, a certain note from you, from your Silesia, was brought to me by Lord Doctor Schwalm, if I remember rightly — which contained nothing other than [matter] about a certain Poem of Lord Tobias Fischer, a poet of your parts, on what occasion it had been composed in that fashion. Your note was welcome to me, and his Poem, in which a few little words have been changed according to critical judgement, as may be seen in the appended leaves, to which some other things, besides those you already have, have also been added. I should wish you to share both these and the earlier [pieces] with the same [poet]. I would have written to him myself already, could I have found his most courteous letters to me so quickly; for I know not where I have put them, with certain others, while my things are being moved hither [to and from] Prague. When it shall be granted me to find them, I will delay a reply no further. Meanwhile greet him dutifully in my name, and give thanks for his benevolent disposition toward me.</p><p>I have written to you several times before about the <b>Wittich books</b>, to be bought from the sister-heir. And if it can now be brought about by your effort that I may obtain them at a just price — especially as regards the <b>three Copies he had of Copernicus, <i>On the Celestial Revolutions</i>, annotated by himself with certain marginal notes</b>, and <b>what he had written <i>On Triangles</b></i>, and <b><i>On the Celestial Hypotheses</b></i>, if any are found — you will have done me a thing exceedingly welcome. The price named, I will pay at once. It has also been reported to me that there was among you, some years ago, a man skilled in Astronomy, who had made some celestial observations in his time, which they say his son still keeps. He lives (if I rightly recall what I heard) in the street they call <i>die Stockgasse</i>; his name escapes me. You, I think, will easily guess and inquire it; and I most lovingly ask that you would obtain from him that a copy of those observations — however many, and of whatever kind, they were — be granted to me; I will in turn be found grateful toward him. Indicate moreover, when you reply (which I trust you will do in good time), whether there is any printing-house among you that has such types as those by which the first book of our <i>Astronomical Letters</i> — which I think you have — was printed. For I have resolved to complete the <b>second Tome</b>, of which the first 12 gatherings were finished before my affairs in Denmark were thrown into confusion, in the same form of paper and type as the former was made. And settle at the same time with a suitable Printer what is to be paid him for each gathering, such that <b>1,500 copies</b> be printed; and at the same time indicate the cost of paper, and what is to be given weekly to a skilled corrector. If I understand that everything can proceed properly there, I will send, together with a fair copy, the money with which paper may be bought and the rest paid. But forgive me, my Monau, that I trouble you — otherwise most occupied — thus with my business; I will in turn do anything for your sake, in whatever matter my effort may ever be of use to you.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, where I now dwell with my household, the 10th day of January, in the renewed [New] style, of the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacob Monau (Jacobus Monavius)</b> (1546–1603), Breslau humanist, <i>patricius Vratislaviensis</i> (patrician of <b>Breslau / Wrocław</b>) and <b>Councillor of the Duke of Silesia</b> — a noted literary patron and Tycho's Silesian agent (cf. brahe-t_16010322001, where Monau is proposed as a letter-relay). <i>Vratislaviensis</i> = of <b>Breslau/Wrocław</b> (not Bratislava).<br>- <b>The Tobias Fischer poem.</b> A commendatory <i>Carmen</i> by <b>Tobias Fischer</b>, a Silesian poet, sent via <i>Dominus D. Schwalmius</i> (Doctor Schwalm); Tycho returns it lightly emended \"according to critical judgement,\" with additions, for Monau to pass back to the poet — the routine traffic of the friends'-verse collection.<br>- <b>The Wittich books (the historically significant request).</b> Tycho repeats a standing request that Monau buy for him, \"from the sister-heir,\" the library of the late <b>Paul Wittich</b> (the Breslau mathematician, d. 1586, who had visited Tycho at Hven in 1580 and exchanged computational methods — <i>prosthaphaeresis</i>; cf. the Tycho–Hagecius correspondence, e.g. brahe-t_15820923001). He names specifically:<br>  - the <b>three copies of Copernicus's <i>De revolutionibus orbium coelestium</i> annotated in Wittich's own hand</b> (<i>marginalibus … a se illustrata</i>);<br>  - Wittich's writings <b><i>On Triangles</b></i> (<i>de Triangulis</i> — trigonometry); and<br>  - <b><i>On the Celestial Hypotheses</b></i> (<i>de Hypothesibus Coelestibus</i>), \"if any are found.\"<br>  <b>The Wittich-annotated volumes.</b> These are the <b>Wittich-annotated Copernicus copies</b> central to the modern \"Wittich-not-Tycho marginalia\" question — surviving exemplars include the Apianus–Wittich volume at the University of Chicago and a Copernicus at Prague; marginalia of this group were later misattributed to Tycho. This letter is Tycho's own documented attempt to acquire them.<br>- <b>The Breslau astronomer's observations.</b> Tycho also seeks, via Monau, a copy of the celestial <b>observations of an unnamed deceased Breslau astronomer</b>, kept by his son, who lives \"in the street called <i>die Stockgasse</i>\" — Tycho's habitual hunt for predecessors' observational records. (Name not recovered — flagged.)<br>- <b>Printing the second Tome of the <i>Epistolae</i> in Silesia (printing history).</b> Tycho asks whether a Breslau press has type matching the <b>first book of his <i>Astronomical Letters</b></i> (<i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i>, Uraniborg 1596), so as to <b>complete the second Tome</b> — of which the first <b>12 gatherings</b> were printed \"before my affairs in Denmark were thrown into confusion\" — in matching paper and type. Concrete figures: a press-run of <b>1,500 copies</b>, with per-gathering printer's fee, paper cost, and weekly pay for a skilled corrector all to be negotiated. A valuable datum for the production economics of Tycho's books.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Monau; <i>Schwalmius</i> (Doctor Schwalm, the courier); <b>Tobias Fischer</b> (the poet); <b>Paul Wittich</b> (the late mathematician); the unnamed Breslau astronomer and his son.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 10 Ianuarij stylo renovato Anni 1600</i> — <i>stylo renovato</i> = the <b>renewed (New/Gregorian) style</b>; imperial usage. Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected: 'PATRITO' to 'PATRITIO' in the salutation, and 'molestam' to 'molestem' (subjunctive), per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII pp.236, 237.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Latin, read securely; the three matters (the Fischer poem; the request to buy <b>Wittich's annotated Copernicus copies and mathematical manuscripts</b>; the deceased Breslau astronomer's observations; and the Silesian printing of the second <i>Epistolae</i> Tome, with its press-run and cost figures) are all firmly construed and corpus-/reference-corroborated. No textual crux; the unnamed Breslau astronomer is left unnamed per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.236-237 in full (within the 235-238 slice; the Monau letter itself runs from mid-236 to mid-237, flanked by the Scultetus and Bochazius letters). Body content, names (Schwalmius, Tobias Fischer, die Stockgasse), numbers (three annotated Copernicus copies, 12 gatherings, 1500 copies), and the closing date all match Dreyer exactly, and the translation is accurate throughout. Two single-letter corruptions survive in our Latin transcription versus Dreyer's print (PATRITO/PATRITIO; molestam/molestem); per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though both are low severity and the translation itself is sound.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin drops the -I- in PATRITIO (patrician) versus Dreyer's print; a single-letter corruption in the source, not in the translation, which renders the sense correctly regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>PATRITIO VRATISLAVIENSI</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints the grammatically required present subjunctive 'molestem' (that I may trouble); our source_text has 'molestam', an ungrammatical corruption. Translation sense is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>sic molestem</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Both defects verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.236-237 (page images, not OCR text layer); both are single-letter corruptions in the Latin with no effect on the already-correct English. No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Jacob Monavius", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000314001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000314001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Returns Tobias Fischer's poem with minor corrections and asks Monau to share the revised version with Fischer, noting difficulty locating letters amid the move.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000110001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000115001", "d": "1600-01-15", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "ck": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Heidelberg", "t": "Tage Ottesen Thott → Tycho Brahe — Heidelberg, 15 January 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 35f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO VEREQVE NOBILI VIRO DN. TYCHONI BRAHE DE KNUDSTRUP, AVUNCULO MEO CARISSIMO.</p><p>DE tuo mei amore, quo indeusque a teneris annis large sum usus, tuisqve beneficiis, qvibus me semper complexus es, avuncule carissime, gratias, ut par est, ago qvam maximas, polliceorqve, si mea unqvam extare possunt officiola, in te constabunt promptissima paratissimaqve. Ne avtem mirere, avuncule carissime, qvod pavlo tardior in literis hactenus fui, siqvidem variis ego rebus cogitationibusqve, de instituenda praesertim peregrinatione, varie sum occupatus, qvibus tamen, avxiliante omnipotenti Deo, decimus tertius dies mensis decembris proxime absoluti anni, felicem expectatumqve finem imposuit; fore itaqve spero, ut diligentius post hac, qvoties per occasionem licuerit, literis tecum de meis rebus agam, certo enim certius mecum statuo, te de mea salute aeqve solicitum atqve de genuini nati. Verum ut cognoscas, ubinam gentium ubiqve terrarum nunc vivam, scito, me cum comite mei itineris studiorumqve Nicolao Mathiae Bachendorphio ad nominatum diem (cum tamen recta Basilaeam decrevissem) Heidelbergam pervenisse, ubi propter urentis frigoris vehementiam usqve ad vernas nundinas proxime instantes Francofurdinas anchoram figere visum fuit, factumqve est integris, lavs Deo, omnibus. In itinere avtem meo comitata me est mater mea svavissima, soror tua perqvam dilecta, Brunsvigam usqve, ubi post pavcos dies obvium habuit Ericum Lange, qvi cum ipsa, ut puto, in Cimbriam iter fecit. Plura nec tempus nec tabellarii festinatio in praesentiarum scribere permittit. Velis ergo per pavca haec boni consulere, alias enim majora dabo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Heidelberga 18 cal. feb. a:o 1600.<br>Tacho Tot O. f.<br>mea manu.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous and truly noble man, Master Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, my dearest [maternal] uncle.</b></p><p>For your love toward me — which I have enjoyed largely from my tender years up to this point — and for your benefits, with which you have always embraced me, dearest uncle, I give, as is fitting, the greatest thanks, and promise that, if my poor little services can ever be of any account, they will stand toward you most ready and most prepared. But do not wonder, dearest uncle, that I have hitherto been somewhat tardy in [my] letters, since I have been variously occupied with various matters and deliberations — especially about the undertaking of a journey [study-tour]; to which, however, with omnipotent God's help, the thirteenth day of the month of December of the just-completed year put a happy and hoped-for end. So I hope that hereafter I shall the more diligently, as often as occasion allows, treat with you by letter about my affairs; for I hold it more certain than certain that you are as anxious about my welfare as about that of a son of your own begetting. But that you may know in what nation, in what part of the world, I now live: know that I, with the companion of my journey and studies, Niels Mathiesen Bachendorph, arrived on the said day at Heidelberg (although I had decided [to go] straight to Basel), where, on account of the violence of the burning cold, it seemed good to cast anchor until the next coming Frankfurt spring fair; and it was done with all of us whole [in health], praise God. On my journey my sweetest mother — your most beloved sister — accompanied me as far as Brunswick, where, after a few days, she met Erik Lange, with whom, as I think, she made the journey into Cimbria [Holstein]. More, neither the time nor the courier's haste permits [me] to write at present. Be willing, therefore, to take these few [words] in good part; for otherwise I will give greater [ones].</p><p>Farewell.<br>Heidelberg, the 18th [day] before the Kalends of February [15 January], in the year 1600.<br>Tage Thott, son of Otte,<br>by my [own] hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender — Tycho's nephew.</b> <b>Tage Ottesen Thott</b> (1580–1658), the Danish nobleman, son of <b>Otte Thott</b> and of <b>Sophie Brahe</b> — Tycho's astronomer-sister. He addresses Tycho as <i>avunculus carissimus</i> (\"dearest maternal uncle\"), and his mother as \"<b>your most beloved sister</b>\" — fixing the family triangle. He signs <i>Tacho Tot O[ttonis] f[ilius]</i>, \"Tage Thott, son of Otte.\"</li><li><b>The study-tour.</b> Tage, with his tutor-companion <b>Niels Mathiesen Bachendorph</b>, on a <i>peregrinatio academica</i>; he had meant to go \"straight to Basel\" but, the cold being fierce, wintered at <b>Heidelberg</b> until the <b>Frankfurt spring fair</b>.</li><li><b>Sophie Brahe and Erik Lange — a notable family datum.</b> <i>mater mea … soror tua … comitata me … Brunsvigam usque, ubi … obvium habuit Ericum Lange, qui cum ipsa … in Cimbriam iter fecit</i> — Tycho's sister <b>Sophie Brahe</b> accompanied her son as far as <b>Brunswick</b>, where she met <b>Erik Lange</b> (the alchemist, her long-betrothed and later husband), with whom she travelled \"into <b>Cimbria</b>\" (the Jutland/Holstein peninsula). A direct contemporary witness to the famous Sophie Brahe–Erik Lange union (Lange's debts had kept them apart for years).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Heidelberga 18 cal. feb. a:o 1600</i> = <i>ante diem XVIII Kalendas Februarii</i> = <b>15 January 1600</b>; Heidelberg (Calvinist Palatinate) kept the <b>Julian / Old Style</b> calendar. The internal \"thirteenth day of December of the just-completed year\" is the O.S. date on which his travel-plans were settled. Header agrees with the label (15 January).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the family relationships (Tage Thott as Tycho's sister-son; Sophie Brahe as \"your most beloved sister\"; Erik Lange and the journey into Cimbria), the study-tour (Bachendorph, Heidelberg, the Frankfurt fair), and the Roman-calendar dateline (<i>18 Kal. Feb.</i> = 15 January 1600 O.S.) are all clear and corpus-/history-corroborated.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.169-172 as page images; Tage Thott's letter (Dreyer's item 176) runs from bottom of p.170 to p.171. source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout (including the unusual 'indeusque', which Dreyer itself prints identically). Collated translation clause-by-clause: the journey to Heidelberg with Niels Mathiesen Bachendorph, the 13 December departure, the mother's accompaniment to Brunswick and meeting with Erik Lange, and the closing all render faithfully. Date '18 cal. feb.' correctly bracket-glossed as 15 January. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 35 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000217001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000217001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks his uncle Tycho for years of kindness, excuses tardy correspondence by preparations for a study-tour that began 13 December, and promises more regular letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000115001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000117001", "d": "1600-01-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Bachazek", "ck": "Martin Bachazek", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Martin Bachazek — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 17 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 11 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO<br>BRAHE AD MARTINUM<br>BOCHAZIUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>EXCELLENTISSIME et eruditissime Domine M. MARTINE BOCHAZI, amice honorande, ex<br>literis tuis ultimo ad me datis non obscure percepi tuum in me studium et<br>singularem benevolentiam, cum quod M. NICOLAUM, Conterraneum meum,<br>tam humaniter nuper exceperis, atque negocia a CLAUDIO BILLEO consobrino in Gallijs meo nomine expedienda tuo<br>quoque consilio promovere volueris, tum etiam quod exemplaria aliquot Carminum<br>tibi concreditorum mihi ad mundum describi procures, in quibus recte coniectatus<br>es, quod Epigramma quoddam TUTO pro TYCHO perperam haberet. Debet enim illud,<br>uti apposite restituisti, sic ultimo claudi:Ære RUDOLPHE tuo, TYCHO labore<br>tuo.</p><p>Et si quæ alia eiuscemodi invenisti sphalmata scriptoris, bene feceris, si<br>emendaveris. Quoniam vero beneficium unum alterum alliciat, et videam te<br>nostratibus favere, præsertim si mea commendatione fulti te accesserint, non<br>potui prætermittere, quin iuvenem Danum, qui ante triduum cum filio meo natu maiore (quem in Daniam pro auferendis isthic residuis<br>instrumentis, et Lubecam primum, deinde<br>Hamburgum, quod et<br>factum est, tranvehendis miseram) huc Witebergâ, ubi aliquandiu studuerat, comitatus est, [literis]<br>hisce [ad] te pro[mo]verem, siquidem is se tibi insinuari exoptârit meque hoc<br>nomine rogârit; intellexerat enim inter nos familiaritatem non vulgarem esse,<br>meque tibi, uti par est, plurimum tribuere, quare non potui non ipsius<br>desiderijs annuere. Ut vero paucis cognoscas, quis et qualis is sit, sic habe.<br>Oriundus est ex ea parte Daniæ, quam<br>Cimbricam alij vocant Chersonesum, nostrates Iutiam dicunt, in civitate isthic primaria Ripensi, quæ cathedralem habet ecclesiam et capitulum<br>Canonicorum celebre atque opimum, ubi etiam parens illius, honesta alias familia<br>natus, præcipuum gerit officium et Superintendentem agit, loco veterum<br>Episcoporum, vir apprime eruditus et antiqua consuetudine mihi notus, qui M.<br>PETRUS HEGELUNDUS appellatur.<br>Filius autem hic, IOHANNES nomine,<br>aliquamdiu in Academia Haffniensi<br>diligentem literis operam navavit, posteaque Witebergæ studia liberalia non minori sedulitate continuavit,<br>atque in ijs pro sua ætate egregium et laudabilem fecit profectum, estque<br>insuper honestis et compositis moribus præditus, ut spes non dubia sit, eum ad<br>præclaram aliquam frugem Dei beneficio perventurum.<br>Quocirca, mi optime BOCHAZI, ut<br>tibi hunc iuvenem Danum de meliori (uti aiunt) nota<br>commendatum habeas, utque eius conatus iuves, ipsumque ad optima quæque tuo<br>consilio promoveas, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo. Feceris sic mihi rem<br>apprime gratam et pluribus nominibus me, tui alias amantissimum, devinxeris.<br>Cuperet is aliquantisper Pragæ subsistere, ut<br>inchoatam studiorum suorum telam in vestro Collegio pertexere queat, atque mores<br>isthic hominum addiscere, sicque successive sibi viam sternere et commoditatem<br>parare ulterius peregrinandi et forte Italiam<br>perlustrandi. Da itaque operam, amantissime Domine BOCHAZI, ut isthic apud vos oportune,<br>et si locus commodus esset, in vestro Collegio, ibi potius degat, ut non nimium<br>de sua pecunia, quâ ad ulterius peregrinandum opus habet, illi consumendum sit,<br>sed frugaliter, ita tamen, ut necessaria non desint, vivere ac studijs<br>humanioribus simul vacare possit. Qua de re ille ipsemet suam plenius tibi<br>expositurus sententiam, atque ego, ubi vobis, favente Numine, adfuero, quod<br>statim atque Cæsarea Maiestas<br>Pragam redierit, futurum spero, ulterius<br>tecum tam de his quam alijs collocuturus sum. Interim effice, ut hanc nostram<br>intercessionem apud te pondus habuisse, qua etiam de re nihil addubito,<br>resciscam, et Exemplaria Carminum, quotquot transscripta habes, mitte, meque tui<br>studiosissimum redamare perge.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Dabantur properanter ex arce Venetiensi 17 Ian. A. 1600.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.ADVENIT cum filio inde a littore Balthico quidam alius e conterraneis nostris<br>M. CHRISTIERNUS<br>LONGOMONTANUS nomine, a me huc vocatus: siquidem is iam<br>antea, dum in Daniâ morarer, mihi in<br>Insulâ nostrâ Uraniburgicâ per<br>octennium fuerat domesticus, et in Exercitijs Astronomicis operam diligentem<br>nobis præstiterat. Turbatis autem isthic Uraniæ sacris, quorundam inscitiâ<br>et malevolentiâ, ille, ruinas tantas me spectare nolente, sed inde cum totâ<br>familiâ in Germaniam me recipiente,<br>pariter abijt, et cum bonâ nostrâ veniâ primum in Prussiam navigavit. Postea perlustratâ Poloniâ per Silesiam et reliquam Meridionaliorem Germaniæ partem Rostochium venit; ubi cum percepisset, me in Arce Ranzovianâ prope Hamburgum aliquantisper consedisse, me ultro<br>accessit, et aliquanto tempore isthic mecum moratus, cum interea a Cæsâ Mate<br>Pragam evocarer, atque paulo post iter in<br>has oras susciperem, is me Magdæburgum comitatus est. Cumque Gynæceum meum, quod<br>eousque tunc mecum habueram, Wandesburgum remitterem, donec ego Pragam excurrendo (quod tamen tunc lues populariter in<br>Bohemiâ grassans impedijt)<br>Cæsæ Matis<br>Clementissimam voluntatem perciperem, eum<br>Gynæceo adiunxi, ut illud tutius reduceret, cum ipsius<br>diu mihi perspecta esset fides et erga me studium. Ubi vero is hoc recte<br>executus esset, in Cimbriam Patriam<br>suam nonnihil exspatiatus est, posteaque Rostochium reversus, ibi eventum rerum mearum in Bohemiâ præstolatus est. Nunc vero cum filio,<br>accersitus (uti dixi) denuo apud me comparuit. Et licet eius in studijs<br>Astronomicis operâ hîc Benachiæ<br>ulterius uti constitueram, tamen cum necdum omnia hîc rite disposita habeam,<br>et alias numerosa mihi sit familia, expectemque indies<br>Mathematicum quendam Electoris Brandeburgensis cum suâ coniuge, pro quo Cæsar scripsit, ut domus hæc mihi<br>deputata vix plures commode capiat, ipse etiam prædictus M. CHRISTIERNUS<br>Pragæ degere, atque isthic, quæ in<br>Astronomicis illi facienda significâro, sedulo exequi non aversetur: placuit<br>itaque mihi et hæc ratio, ut meis sumptibus ibi, donec illi aliter<br>prospexero, vivat, et pro me, quæ in hoc genere studiorum continuanda<br>præfinivero, elaboret. Quocirca rursus ad tuum unâ cum consilio auxilium rem<br>hanc defero, omniaque tuæ prudentiæ et fidei committo, ipsumque, qui mihi<br>semper charus et acceptus fuit, tibi quam diligentissime commendo, rogans te<br>per amicitiam nostram, velis de commoditate aliquâ oportunâ, tam quoad<br>habitationem quam victum, ipsi prospicere, sive id in collegio vestro, sive<br>alibi in propinquo competenter fieri queat, meque per eundem intra unum vel<br>alterum diem cum curru meo huc revertentem de singulis commonefacere.<br>Præstiteris sic rem inprimis gratam, et quovis officio, quantum per me<br>licet, vicissim compensandam.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Datum ut prius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MARTIN BACHAZEK.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Most excellent and most learned Lord Master Martin Bachazek, friend to be honoured: from your last letter to me I perceived, not obscurely, your devotion to me and singular goodwill — both that you lately received so kindly Master <b>Nicolaus</b>, my fellow-countryman, and that you were willing to further, with your counsel too, the business to be expedited in France in my name by my cousin <b>Claus Bille</b>; and also that you procure for me some fair copies of the <b>Poems</b> entrusted to you, in which you rightly conjectured that a certain Epigram wrongly had \"TUTO\" for \"TYCHO.\" For it ought, as you aptly restored it, to close thus at the end:</p><p><i>\"By your money, Rudolf; by your labour, Tycho.\"</i></p><p>And if you have found any other such slips of the scribe, you will do well to correct them. But since one kindness allures another, and I see that you favour my countrymen — especially if, supported by my commendation, they come to you — I could not omit to recommend to you, by this letter, a <b>young Dane</b> who, three days ago, accompanied hither (from Wittenberg, where he had studied a while) my <b>elder son</b> — whom I had sent into Denmark to bring away the instruments remaining there, and to convey them first to Lübeck, then to Hamburg, which was done. For he greatly desired to be introduced to you, and asked me on this account, having understood that there is no common familiarity between us, and that I esteem you highly, as is fitting; wherefore I could not but grant his wishes. That you may know in few [words] who and what he is, take it thus: he is sprung from that part of Denmark which others call the <b>Cimbric Chersonese</b>, [and] our countrymen call <b>Jutland</b> — in the chief city there, <b>Ribe</b>, which has a cathedral church and a famous and rich chapter of Canons, where also his <b>father</b> holds the chief office and acts as <b>Superintendent</b>, in place of the old Bishops — a man exceedingly learned, and known to me by old acquaintance, who is called <b>Master Peter Hegelund</b>. This son, <b>John</b> by name, devoted diligent effort to letters a while in the <b>University of Copenhagen</b>, and afterward continued his liberal studies at <b>Wittenberg</b> with no less assiduity, making in them, for his age, an excellent and praiseworthy progress; and he is moreover of honourable and composed manners, so that there is no doubtful hope that he will, by God's grace, attain to some illustrious fruit. Wherefore, my excellent Bachazek, I ask you again and again to hold this young Dane commended to you \"of the better mark\" (as they say), and to aid his endeavours, and promote him to every best thing by your counsel. You will thus do me a thing exceedingly welcome, and bind me — otherwise most loving of you — on many counts. He would wish to stay a while at Prague, that he may weave to its end the web of his studies, begun, in your <b>College</b>, and learn the manners of the people there, and so successively pave a way for himself and prepare the means of travelling further, and perhaps of traversing Italy. Give heed therefore, most loving Lord Bachazek, that he may rather dwell there with you conveniently, and — if a convenient place be [open] — in your College, so that he need not spend too much of his money (which he needs for travelling further), but may live frugally, yet so that necessaries be not lacking, and at the same time be free for the humaner studies. About which he himself will set out his view to you more fully; and I, when I am present with you (God favouring) — which I hope will be as soon as the Imperial Majesty has returned to Prague — shall speak with you further, both of these and other matters. Meanwhile bring it about that I may learn that this our intercession has had weight with you (of which I doubt nothing); and send the copies of the Poems, as many as you have transcribed; and continue to love me back, most devoted to you.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given in haste from the Venetian castle, 17 January, the year 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. There has arrived, with my son, from the Baltic shore, another of my countrymen, Master Christian Longomontanus by name, summoned hither by me: for he had already before, while I dwelt in Denmark, been a member of my household on our island of Uraniborg for eight years, and had rendered me diligent service in the Astronomical Exercises. But when the rites of Urania were there disturbed, through the ignorance and malevolence of certain men, he — I being unwilling to look on so great ruins, but withdrawing thence with my whole household into Germany — departed likewise, and with our good leave first sailed into Prussia. Afterwards, having traversed Poland, he came through Silesia and the rest of the more southerly part of Germany to Rostock; where, when he had learned that I had meanwhile settled for a while at the Rantzau castle near Hamburg, he came to me of his own accord, and having stayed there with me some time — since meanwhile I was summoned to Prague by the Imperial Majesty, and a little after undertook the journey into these regions — he accompanied me to Magdeburg. And when I was sending back my Gynaeceum [the household women], which until then I had kept with me, to Wandsbek, until I should ascertain the most clement will of the Imperial Majesty by an excursion to Prague (which excursion, however, was then hindered by a plague raging widely through Bohemia), I joined him to the Gynaeceum, that he might more safely conduct it back, since his fidelity and devotion toward me had long been clear to me. And when he had rightly carried this out, he travelled somewhat into the Cimbric land, his fatherland, and afterwards, having returned to Rostock, there awaited the outcome of my affairs in Bohemia. But now, with my son, summoned (as I said) anew, he has appeared before me again. And although I had resolved to make further use of his work in the Astronomical studies here at Benátky, yet — since I do not yet have all things duly arranged here, and have besides a numerous household, and a certain Mathematician of the Elector of Brandenburg with his wife is daily expected (for whom the Emperor has written), so that this house assigned to me can scarcely conveniently hold more — and since the aforesaid Master Christian himself does not refuse to live at Prague and diligently to carry out thence the things I shall signify for him to do in Astronomical matters: this plan therefore pleased me too, that he should live there at my expense, until I provide otherwise for him, and labour on my behalf at what I shall prescribe to be continued in this kind of studies. Wherefore I again refer this matter to your aid together with your counsel, and commit all things to your prudence and fidelity, and most diligently commend to you the man — who has always been dear and acceptable to me — asking you by our friendship that you be willing to provide for him some fitting convenience, both as to lodging and as to victuals, whether this can be suitably done in your College or elsewhere nearby; and to apprise me of each particular through the same man, who within a day or two will return hither with my carriage. You will thus do a thing especially welcome, to be repaid in turn by any service, so far as lies in my power. Farewell. Given as before.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Martin Bachazek</b> (Martin Bacháček z Nauměřic, 1539/41–1612), mathematician and <b>Rector of the Charles University (Carolinum / Collegium) at Prague</b> — Tycho's Prague academic contact. The MS is in Basel.<br>- <b>Significance — a student-recommendation, and the famous \"Rudolf/Tycho\" distich.</b> Two parts:<br>  1. <b>Thanks &amp; the epigram correction.</b> Tycho thanks Bachazek for receiving \"Master <b>Nicolaus</b>, my fellow-countryman\" (a Dane of Tycho's circle — probably <b>Claus Mule</b>/Nicolaus Mulaeus), for furthering his cousin <b>Claus Bille's</b> business in France, and for fair-copying <b>commendatory Poems</b>. Bachazek had spotted a scribal error — <b>\"TUTO\" for \"TYCHO\"</b> — and rightly restored the closing pentameter, which Tycho quotes: <b><i>\"Aere Rudolphe tuo, Tycho labore tuo\"</b></i> — \"By your money, Rudolf; by your labour, Tycho\" — the neat epigram of the imperial patronage (Rudolf's purse, Tycho's toil), kept in Latin and translated.<br>  2. <b>The recommendation of Johannes Hegelund.</b> Tycho commends a <b>young Dane</b> who had accompanied his <b>elder son</b> (Tycho the Younger, back from the Denmark instrument-errand) from Wittenberg: <b>John Hegelund</b>, son of <b>Peter Hegelund</b> (Peder Jensen Hegelund, 1542–1614, <b>Superintendent of Ribe</b> — the Lutheran successor of the old bishops — \"known to me by old acquaintance\"), of <b>Ribe</b> in <b>Jutland</b> (the <i>Cimbrica Chersonesus</i>). Having studied at <b>Copenhagen</b> and <b>Wittenberg</b>, he wishes to continue at the <b>Prague Collegium</b>, then perhaps travel to Italy; Tycho asks Bachazek to lodge him in the College so he may live frugally, and promises to speak more once back at Prague.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Martin Bachazek; <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>M. Nicolaus</i>, probable); <b>Claus Bille</b> (Tycho's cousin); <b>Peter Hegelund</b> (Superintendent of Ribe) and his son <b>John Hegelund</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b> (in the distich); Tycho's elder son (Tycho the Younger).<br>- <b>Place-name pun (protocol §3).</b> <i>ex arce Venetiensi</i> — \"from the <b>Venetian</b> castle\": <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (whose Czech name <i>Benátky</i> means \"Venice\") is here Latinised as <i>Venetia</i> — a learned pun for Benátky, not the Italian Venice. Rendered \"the castle of Benátky,\" with the pun noted.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>17 Ian. A. 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</p><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Large closing POSTSCRIPTA — Longomontanus's movements (8 yrs at Uraniborg; Prussia/Poland/Silesia→Rostock→the Rantzau castle near Hamburg→Magdeburg; the Gynaeceum returned to Wandsbek; arriving with Tycho's son) + the request to lodge him at the Prague College, restored from Dreyer VIII pp.239–240 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Plague delayed Tycho's own trip to Prague.</b> Recovers the reason Tycho did not himself go to ascertain the Emperor's will in person, but instead sent Longomontanus back with the Gynaeceum to Wandsbek: an outbreak of plague then \"raging widely\" through Bohemia made the excursion inadvisable.</li><li><b>Corroborates the plague chronology elsewhere in the corpus.</b> Dovetails with the 3 August 1600 Rosenkrantz letter's own account of \"the plague, with the Emperor's Court dispersed for almost a whole year\" — the two letters describe the same episode from different points in its course, sharpening the timeline of Tycho's 1599 movements between Wandsbek, Magdeburg and Prague.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> \"Given in haste from the castle of Benátky\" corrected to \"Given in haste from the Venetian castle,\" restoring Tycho's pun on arx Venetiensis (Dreyer, TBOO VIII, p.239); the Latin \"concreditorum\" (p.238) was already correct and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — warm, fully legible Latin, read securely; the substance (thanks for receiving Nicolaus and furthering Claus Bille's French business; the <i>TUTO/TYCHO</i> epigram correction and the <i>\"Aere Rudolphe tuo, Tycho labore tuo\"</i> distich; the detailed recommendation of John Hegelund of Ribe to the Prague College) is clear and corpus-corroborated. The Benátky = <i>Venetia</i> pun is handled, not mistaken for Italy. No textual crux.</p><p>Grade A. A plain causal/concessive clause (\"lues populariter grassans\" = \"a plague raging widely/among the populace\"), a standard early-modern epidemiological formula with no ambiguity in sense, and independently consistent with the plague reference already present in this same corpus's later Rosenkrantz correspondence.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter -- the Bachazek epigram correction (TUTO/TYCHO and the two-line verse), the recommendation of the young Dane John Hegelund of Ribe with his father's office and biography, the closing date/place, and the appended postscript on Longomontanus's itinerary (Uraniborg for eight years, Prussia, Poland, Silesia, Rostock, the Rantzau castle near Hamburg, Magdeburg, Wandsbek, Prague) with the Brandenburg mathematician detail -- against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.238-240 (slice pp.2-4), reading page images directly. Slice p.1 confirmed as the tail of the preceding Monavius letter (dated 10 January); the letter is fully contained through slice p.4. One TEXT_ERROR found (concredicorum, not a real Latin word, vs Dreyer's concreditorum -- English unaffected); one low-severity translation smoothing (Tycho's own toponymic pun \"arce Venetiensi\" rendered silently as \"the castle of Benatky\"). All other proper names, numbers (eight years at Uraniborg), and dates checked and correct.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text reads \"concredicorum\", which is not a Latin word at all; Dreyer prints \"concreditorum\". The English translation (\"entrusted to you\") in fact follows the correct Dreyer reading, so this is purely a corruption in our Latin apparatus. Dreyer prints: <i>concreditorum (TBOO VIII, p.238) -- the correct genitive plural of concredo, \"entrusted\"</i>.<br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Tycho's own Latin uses \"arce Venetiensi\" (\"the Venetian castle\"), his learned pun on Benatky (Czech for \"little Venice\") -- elsewhere in these same 1600 letters he writes \"Arce Benatica\" plainly. The English silently normalizes the pun to \"the castle of Benatky\" with no bracket flagging the substitution, so a reader cannot see that Tycho varied his own usage here. At: “Given in haste from the castle of Benátky, 17 January, the year 1600.”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for TBOO VIII pp.237-241 (both pages bearing the disputed readings). The audit's TEXT_ERROR claim (\"concredicorum\") does not describe this packet's actual current_latin, which already reads the correct \"concreditorum\" agreeing with Dreyer p.238 -- marked no_change_needed rather than fabricating a fix for a defect that isn't present. The MISTRANSLATION on \"arce Venetiensi\" is real and confirmed on p.239; corrected to preserve Tycho's Venice/Benátky pun. No remaining uncertainty.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Bachazek for hospitality to his countryman, acknowledges correction of an epigram misprint, and recommends a young Dane newly arrived at Benátky.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000117001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000120001", "d": "1600-01-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed imperial court official] — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 20 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et Magnifice vir, Domine et amice honorande: Accepi hac ipsâ horâ literas a meo domestico DANIELE FELS, qui isthic apud vos in Aulâ moratur, quibus iussu tuae Magae scribit, Caesaream Matem Clementissime cupere, ut per primam oportunitatem iudicium meum de hoc Anno, praesertim quoad morbos Epidemicos, brevibus et succincte comprehensum eius Mati per primam oportunitatem transmittam. Etsi vero Astrologis praedictionibus indulgere non soleam, quod eam certitudinem non spondeant, quam requiro, et quam Astronomia, quae motiones siderum solummodo scrutatur, admittit (quam etiam ob id potius excolo) et praeter haec generales illae Mundi influentiae non saltem a superioribus Astris, sed et inferioribus caussis atque Elementari Naturâ dependeant (quod et antea Tuae Magae me innuisse memini) adeo ut qui has citra omnem hallucinationem rite praeviderint, vel nulli vel admodum pauci hactenus extiterint, qui vero particulares et individua concernentes rectius vaticinati sint, plures inventi: Attamen quia Sa Caesarea Maiestas, Dominus noster Clementissimus, sic a me fieri vult, cuius mandatis lubens et subiectissime (uti decet) obtempero, quam primum fieri poterit, meas hac de re coniecturas ex Astrologiâ qualicunque petitas paucis consignabo, atque ipsius Mati ad tuam Magam meo nomine humillime exhibendas dirigam, utut libris meis tam manuscriptis quam alijs huc conducentibus, quod Magdaeburgi apud reliquam supellectilem detineantur, etiamnum destituar. Alias iamdudum perfecissem id, quod ex mandato Caesae Matis Tua Maga eo ipso die, quo Clementissime audientiam obtinueram, expetijt; quod quale sit, ipsemet novit.</p><p>Porro Magam tuam caelare non possum (quod et alias ad ipsam hodie perscribere in animum induxeram) filium meum natu maiorem, quem cum alio quodam ex familiaribus meis ante sedecim hebdomadas in Daniam pro auferendis residuis isthic Instrumentis ablegâram, nunc primum huc reversum esse, re omni bene expeditâ. Instrumenta enim quatuor, quae ob magnitudinem discedens ibi reliqui, unâ cum quibusdam alijs, primum Lubecam, deinde ad Albis ostia Hamburgum, ubi nunc conservantur, sarcta tecta transferri fecit, contradicente isthic in Patriâ nemine: licet eo ipso tempore, quo Rex Comitia extraordinarie cum toto Regni Senatu et potissimâ Nobilitate celebravit, Haffniam appulerit: sed omnibus potius cordatis ac ingenuis unâ cum consanguineis et Amicis nostris de Caesae Matis Clementissimo erga me favore atque horum studiorum promotione mihi affatim congratulantibus, paucis tantum invidis et malevolis dolorem dissimulantibus ac atque obmutescentibus. Quae pariter indicanda duxi, quo per oportunitatem ipsius Mati a tuâ Magâ Instrumenta mea omnia, tam ea, quae prius evehi curâram, quam quae in Daniâ reliqui, nunc esse in fundo Germanico, atque instante vere, favente Deo, huc per Albim non incommode advehi posse, referri queat.</p><p>Coeterum ut dictum DANIELEM sollicitatorem isthic meum, qui iam sex ferme hebdomadibus abest, quamprimum per graviores occupationes licuerit, expedire et huc remittere non degravetur, officiose rogo.</p><p>Valeat Maga tua quam felicissime et diutissime.<br>Raptim Benachiâ die 20 Ianuarij 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO AN ANONYMOUS [PERSON].</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Most noble and magnificent Man, Lord and friend to be honoured: I have received this very hour a letter from my servant Daniel Fels, who stays there with you at the court, in which, by Your Magnificence's order, he writes that the Imperial Majesty most graciously desires that, at the first opportunity, I transmit to His Majesty my judgement about this Year — especially as regards epidemic diseases — comprised briefly and succinctly. Now, although I am not accustomed to indulge in <b>astrological predictions</b> — because they do not promise that certainty which I require, and which <b>Astronomy</b>, that examines only the motions of the stars, does allow (which for that reason I rather cultivate); and because, besides, those general influences of the World depend not only on the higher Stars, but also on lower causes and on Elementary Nature (which I remember having hinted to Your Magnificence before); so that of those who have rightly foreseen these [general influences] without any delusion there have hitherto been either none, or very few, while of those who have prophesied more rightly concerning particulars and individual matters, more have been found — nevertheless, because the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, wishes it so done by me, whose commands I willingly and most submissively (as is fitting) obey, as soon as it can be done I shall set down in a few words my conjectures on this matter, drawn from astrology of whatever sort, and shall direct them to be most humbly laid before His Majesty, in my name, through Your Magnificence — even though I am still deprived of my books, both manuscript and others bearing on this, since they are detained at Magdeburg with the rest of my furniture. Otherwise I should long ago have completed that which, by the Imperial Majesty's mandate, Your Magnificence requested on the very day on which I had most graciously obtained an audience; what it is, you yourself know.</p><p>Furthermore, I cannot conceal from Your Magnificence (which I had also resolved to write to you fully today, on another matter) that <b>my elder son</b> — whom, with another of my household, I had sent into Denmark sixteen weeks ago to bring away the instruments remaining there — has now at last returned hither, every matter well dispatched. For the <b>four instruments</b> which, on account of their size, I had left there on departing, together with certain others, he had transferred safe and sound first to Lübeck, then to Hamburg at the mouths of the Elbe, where they are now kept, with no one in the fatherland gainsaying — although he arrived at Copenhagen at the very time when the King was holding an extraordinary Diet with the whole Senate of the Realm and the chief Nobility; rather, all the wise and well-born, together with our kinsmen and friends, congratulated me abundantly on the Imperial Majesty's most clement favour toward me and the promotion of these studies, only a few envious and malevolent ones dissembling their chagrin and falling silent. These things I thought should likewise be indicated, so that, at an opportunity, it may be reported to His Majesty by Your Magnificence that all my instruments — both those I had earlier had carried out, and those I left in Denmark — are now on German soil, and that, with spring approaching, God favouring, they can be conveyed hither up the Elbe without difficulty.</p><p>For the rest, I dutifully ask that you would not find it burdensome — as soon as more serious occupations permit — to dispatch and send back hither the said Daniel, my agent there, who has now been away nearly six weeks.</p><p>May Your Magnificence fare most happily and most long.<br>In haste, at Benátky, the 20th of January 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient (unnamed; flagged).</b> Headed <i>Ad Anonymum</i> — an <b>unnamed senior court official</b> (<i>Magnificentia Tua</i>) at the imperial court, under whom Tycho's agent <b>Daniel Fels</b> operated and through whom Tycho's communications reached the Emperor. On the evidence (Fels writes \"by your order\"; the official conveys Rudolf II's wishes and will lay Tycho's prognostication before him) the most likely identification is <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>, the imperial secretary who handled exactly this traffic (cf. brahe-t_16000106001), though <b>Coraduz</b> (the Vice-Chancellor) is also possible. Not asserted.</li><li><b>The imperial request — a prognostication for 1600.</b> Via Fels, the official relays that <b>Rudolf II wants Tycho's astrological judgement on the year 1600, especially as to epidemic diseases</b> (the plague of 1599 was barely past). The request itself is a window onto the Emperor's appetite for astrology.</li><li><b>Tycho on astrology vs astronomy (the doctrinally important passage).</b> Tycho states his reservations plainly: he does <b>not normally indulge astrological prediction</b>, because it lacks \"that certainty which I require, and which Astronomy — examining only the motions of the stars — does allow\" (hence his preference for astronomy); the <i>general</i> \"influences of the world\" depend \"not only on the higher Stars but also on lower causes and Elementary Nature\"; and so <b>general</b> forecasts have almost never been got right \"without delusion,\" whereas forecasts of <b>particulars</b> have more often hit the mark. Yet he will obey the Emperor and supply conjectures \"drawn from astrology of whatever sort\" — hampered, he notes, by his <b>books being detained at Magdeburg</b> with his furniture. A characteristic, qualified Tychonic statement on the limits of judicial astrology (cf. §5.3 register; no silent sharpening of the hedges, protocol §4).</li><li><b>The return of the four great instruments from Denmark.</b> Tycho reports that his <b>elder son</b> (Tycho the Younger; cf. brahe-t_16000126001/002), sent to Denmark sixteen weeks earlier, has returned, having moved the <b>four large instruments</b> left behind (with other goods) safe <b>first to Lübeck, then to Hamburg at the Elbe mouth</b>, \"with no one in the fatherland gainsaying\" — even though he reached Copenhagen during an <b>extraordinary Diet</b> (the King with the whole <i>Rigsraad</i> and chief nobility). He stresses the warm reception (kinsmen and the \"wise and well-born\" congratulating him on the imperial favour), \"only a few envious and malevolent ones … falling silent.\" The official is to report to the Emperor that <b>all</b> Tycho's instruments are now on <b>German soil</b> and can come up the <b>Elbe</b> in spring. (Dovetails with the Hamburg-transit letter, brahe-t_15990929001, and the Jöstel/Wittenberg-college letters, brahe-t_16001008001/16001009001.)</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Daniel Fels</b> (Tycho's court agent/<i>sollicitator</i>); the elder son (Tycho the Younger); the <b>King</b> (Christian IV) and the Danish Diet; <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 20 Ianuarij 1600</i> at Benátky; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'ipidemicos' corrected to 'Epidemicos', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.240 (translation unaffected — already correctly rendered 'epidemic diseases').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is read securely and Tycho's carefully hedged statement on astrology-vs-astronomy is rendered without sharpening; the substance (the imperial request for a 1600 prognostication on epidemics; Tycho's reservations and compliance; the four great instruments now moved from Denmark to Hamburg; the request to send Fels back) is clear and corpus-corroborated. The only open point is the <b>unnamed recipient</b> (plausibly Barvitius), left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule and flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.239-242 (letter itself on 240-241): opening, closing, all proper names (Daniel Fels), numbers (four instruments, sixteen weeks, six weeks), place-names (Lübeck, Hamburg, Copenhagen/Haffnia, Benátky) and the date (20 January 1600) all match Dreyer and are faithfully translated. One source-text corruption found (ipidemicos vs Dreyer's Epidemicos); no other defects in the translation itself.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin corrupts the standard word 'Epidemicos' to 'ipidemicos' (E lost, decapitalized); Dreyer prints the normal reading. The English translation is unaffected since it correctly renders 'epidemic diseases' regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>praesertim quoad morbos Epidemicos, brevibus et succincte comprehensum (TBOO VIII p.240)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp.239-242 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect was confirmed exactly as flagged and corrected; nothing else in the passage required change.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000306002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to the Emperor's request for a yearly forecast on epidemic diseases by explaining his reservations about astrological prediction and its dependence on lower causes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000120001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000124001", "d": "1600-01-24", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 24 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Firenze, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento, part 3, vol. 12)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SERENISSIMO AC POTENTISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO. DOMINO FERDINANDO, MAGNO HETRURIAe, FLORENTIAe, SENORUM AC PISARUM DUCI, DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO AD MANUS PROPRIAS.</p><p>SERENISSIME et Potentissime Princeps, Domine Clementissime. Quod Serenissima Celsitudo Tua mihi, licet de facie ignoto et tam longe dissito, adeo clementer et benevole superiori aestate scribere non dedignata fuerit, humillima et obsequentissima mente eidem gratias ago quas possum maximas. Accepi eas literas, cum primum Pragam sub initia Iulij, a Sa Caesareâ Maiestate, Domino meo Clementissimo, isthuc e Germania vocatus, et benignissime susceptus advenissem. Quae sane non parvâ me affecerunt voluptate, quod inde perciperem tantum Principem, inter Europaeos paucissimis cedentem, non saltem me immeritum Clementissimo suo favore prosequi, sed et Divinae illius Astronomiae (cui plurimis abhinc Annis, arduo et indefesso studio, nec modicis impensis operam navavi) mysterijs solerter et iucunde affici, prout haec sublimis et Heroica Scientia, inde ab initio Regibus et Principibus, Heroicisque Viris, in sublimi rerum mundanarum fastigio collocatis, semper acceptissima, atque ab ijs in praecipuo honore habita fuit. Quâ etiam de caussâ Augustissimus Imperator RUDOLPHUS II me unâ cum Uraniâ nostrâ, Patriâ, quod ibi debite conservari et perpetuari nequiret, sponte excedentem huc in Bohemiam (uti dictum) ubi nunc in Arce quâdam Caesareanâ, itinere saltem dimidij diei Pragâ remotâ, eius fundamina et culturam denuo instauro, arcessiri voluit, atque huic Regali Studio, Regaliter quoque et liberaliter prospicere non degravatus est. Libros quos Serenissimae Tuae Celsitudini humillime misi, eidem gratos fuisse, mihi plurimum congratulor.</p><p>Quoniam vero ex praedictis Serenissimae Celsitudinis Tuae literis percepi, illam plura ex meis in re Astronomicâ Operibus Clementissimo animo requirere, nullatenus intermittam, quin per primam quamque oportunitatem, ipsius optimae voluntati, quâ possum reverentiâ, morem geram; et praestitissem id iam antea quam lubentissime, si supellex mea libraria, quae Magdaeburgi in Germania etiamnum reservatur, huc allata fuisset, quod tamen instanti vere, ubi Albis fluvius hoc hyberno tempore congelatus, denuo erit navigabilis, futurum spero. Sic enim a Caesareâ Maiestate Magdaeburgensibus per literas iniunctum est. Ex quo insuper Serenissima Celsitudo Tua Nobilem et Eruditum Adolescentem, FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM, meum aliquandiu domesticum, qui dictos libros Serenissimae Tuae Celsitudini submisse a me obtulit, ex aliquali nostrâ humillimâ commendatione Clementissimo favore non indignum censuit, sed ita sibi acceptum habuit, ut nuper huc ad me reversus Serenissimae Celsitudinis Tuae Clementissimam et singularem erga se benignitatem satis depraedicare nequiverit, id equidem tanti beneficij loco reputo, ac si a Tuâ Serenissimâ Celsitudine mihi ipsi praestitum foret. Et quia vel inde Serenissimae Celsitudinis Tuae Clementissimum erga me animum haud obscure intelligam, non verebor Serenissimam Tuam Celsitudinem in alio quodam haud dissimili negotio eo audentius demisse compellare, praesertim cum Ipsamet in suis ad me Clementissimis literis benignissima sua officia mihi ultro offerre dignetur. Est autem eiuscemodi.</p><p>Cuperem quam maxime non saltem in Italia, ubi minor est quam hîc Sphaerae Mundanae inclinatio, sed et in Aegypto apud Alexandriam, siquidem ibi adhuc rectior est, nonnulla in Astrorum Observationibus, quae hîc non ita patent, perficere. Quamobrem filium meum natu maiorem ex me ipso denominatum, adolescentem 18 Annorum, quem inde ab ipsa pueritia ad haec exercitia assuefeci, adiuncto illi pro comite quodam ex meis studiosis, qui usque in tertium Annum in rebus Astronomicis factitandis fideliter et sedulo mihi inservivit, harumque non vulgarem peritiam sibi interea comparavit, favente Divino Numine, instanti vere, vel quamprimum fieri poterit, ad Smam Tuam Celnem in Hetruriam mittere, tradito illi uno vel altero Instrumento ex meis, quod portatile sit (si modo id commode effici queat) unâ cum instructione, quomodo plura, prout opus fuerit, isthic paranda sint, et quaenam ijs coelitus denotanda. Daboque simul operam, ut is aliquos librorum meorum a S. T. Celne clementissime desideraturum secum adferat. Deinde studiosus ille, relicto in Smae Tuae Celnis Aulico servitio (modo S. T. Celni sic placuerit) filio meo, in Aegyptum navigabit, atque ea quae isthic ulterius observari poterunt, iuxta meam praescriptionem pariter designabit. Qua in re Inclyta Venetiarum Respublica, quando aliquam eo navem ablegat, competentem promotionem (uti etiam spem aliqualem praedicto TENGNAGLIO, hac de re, dum ibi esset, meo nomine solicitanti fecit) potissimum vero Tuae Serenissimae Celsitudinis authoritate et intercessione allecta, non est (uti spero) denegatura. Si itaque Sma Celdo Tua filium meum unâ cum adiuncto ipsi comite, per annuum tempus aut circiter in Aula sua apud se retinere, sibique commendatum habere, pro singulari suo et Clementissimo erga me favore, non aversetur, quibus possum precibus demisse oro, ut de sua Clementissima hac in parte voluntate me per literas certiorem reddere non degravetur, aut etiam id Generosissimo et Magnificentissimo suo apud Sam Caesam Matem Legato, qui Pragae est, significare. Fecerit sic Serenissima Celsitudo Tua non saltem mihi humillimo suo servitori, ad omnia obsequia pro virili praestanda paratissimo, sed et ipsi Caesareae Maiestati, cui haec studia cordi sunt, rem (uti non dubito) inprimis acceptam et Serenissimae Tuae Celsitudini (modo ipsius laudibus aliquid accedere possit) gloriosam et apud omnem posteritatem commendabilem.</p><p>Sed desino Serenissimam Tuam Celsitudinem, gravissimis Ditionum suarum rebus gubernandis intentam, ulterius interpellare. Deus Opt. Max. eam quam diutissime sospitem et florentem conservet.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ Die 24 Ianuarij An. 1600.<br>Serenissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae humillimus servitor<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene and most powerful Prince and Lord, Lord Ferdinando, Grand Duke of Tuscany, Florence, Siena and Pisa, his most clement Lord — into his own hands.</b></p><p>Most serene and most powerful Prince, most clement Lord. That Your most serene Highness did not disdain to write to me — though unknown by face and so far distant — so clemently and benevolently last summer, I give You, with most humble and obedient mind, the greatest thanks I can. I received that letter when I had first come to Prague, about the beginning of July, called hither from Germany by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my most clement Lord, and most kindly received. It affected me indeed with no small delight, that I perceived thence so great a Prince — yielding to very few among Europeans — not only to pursue me, undeserving, with His most clement favour, but also to be skilfully and pleasantly taken with the mysteries of that divine <b>Astronomy</b> (on which, for very many years now, with arduous and untiring study, and at no modest expense, I have bestowed my labour) — as this sublime and Heroic Science has, from the beginning, always been most acceptable to Kings and Princes and Heroic Men set on the lofty summit of worldly things, and held by them in special honour. For which cause also the most august Emperor <b>Rudolf II</b> willed that I be summoned hither into Bohemia — withdrawing of my own accord, together with our <b>Urania</b>, from my fatherland, because she could not there be duly preserved and perpetuated — where now, in <b>a certain imperial Castle, a journey of but half a day distant from Prague</b> [Benátky], I again restore her foundations and cultivation; and He has not thought it beneath Him to provide for this Royal Study, royally too and liberally. <b>The books which I most humbly sent to Your most serene Highness, that they were welcome to You, I greatly rejoice.</b></p><p>But since from Your most serene Highness's said letter I perceived that You, with most clement mind, desire more of my Works in Astronomical matters, I will by no means fail, at the first opportunity, to comply with Your excellent wish, with what reverence I can; and I would have done it most gladly already, <b>had my library-stock — which is still kept at Magdeburg in Germany — been brought hither</b>; which, however, I hope will come about this coming spring, when the river <b>Elbe</b>, frozen in this winter season, shall again be navigable (for so it has been enjoined upon the Magdeburgers by the Imperial Majesty, by letter). Moreover, since Your most serene Highness judged the noble and learned youth <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> — for some while a member of my household, who humbly presented those books to Your most serene Highness from me — not unworthy, on some humble commendation of ours, of Your most clement favour, but held him so acceptable that, lately returned hither to me, he could not sufficiently proclaim Your Highness's most clement and singular kindness toward him: I count this in the place of so great a benefit, as if it had been done to me myself by Your most serene Highness. And because even thence I clearly understand Your Highness's most clement disposition toward me, I will not fear the more boldly and humbly to address Your most serene Highness on another, not dissimilar, business — especially since You Yourself, in Your most clement letter to me, deign of Your own accord to offer me Your most kindly services. It is of this kind.</p><p><b>I should most greatly wish to perfect certain observations of the stars — which here are not so open [to view] — not only in Italy (where the inclination of the celestial Sphere is less than here), but also in Egypt, at Alexandria, since there it is yet more upright [<i>rectior</i>].</b> Wherefore <b>my elder son, named after myself, a youth of 18 years, whom from his very boyhood I have habituated to these exercises</b> — adding to him as companion a certain one of my students, who has faithfully and diligently served me, even into a third year, in carrying out astronomical work, and has meanwhile acquired no common skill in these things — I purpose, God favouring, <b>this coming spring, or as soon as it can be done, to send to Your most serene Highness into Tuscany</b>, having given him one or two of my Instruments, such as is portable (if only it can conveniently be made so), together with instruction how more are to be prepared there as need requires, and what is to be marked by them from the heavens. And I will at the same time take care that he bring with him some of my books, which Your Highness will most clemently desire. Then that student — my son being left in Your most serene Highness's court service (if so it please Your Highness) — <b>will sail to Egypt, and will likewise mark down what can there be further observed, according to my prescription</b>. In which matter the renowned <b>Republic of Venice</b>, when it sends some ship thither, will not (as I hope) refuse competent furtherance — as it gave some hope, too, to the aforesaid Tengnagel, soliciting on this matter in my name while he was there — but above all drawn by Your most serene Highness's authority and intercession. If, then, Your most serene Highness, of Your singular and most clement favour toward me, would not be averse <b>to keep my son, together with the companion adjoined to him, for a year's time or thereabouts in Your Court with You, and to hold him commended</b>, I humbly beg, with what prayers I can, that You would not think it a burden to inform me by letter of Your most clement will in this part, or even to signify it to Your most noble and magnificent <b>Legate at the Imperial court, who is at Prague</b>. So would Your most serene Highness do a thing — as I do not doubt — especially welcome not only to me, Your most humble servant, most ready to render all obedience to the utmost, but also to the Imperial Majesty Himself, to whom these studies are dear; and glorious to Your most serene Highness (if anything can be added to Your praises), and commendable to all posterity.</p><p>But I cease to interrupt Your most serene Highness further, intent on the weightiest affairs of governing Your Dominions. May God, Best and Greatest, preserve You safe and flourishing as long as possible.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 24th day of January, the year 1600.<br>Your most serene Highness's most humble servant,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (Florence, Siena, Pisa) — the same correspondent as brahe-t_16010430001 (30 April 1601). This is the <b>earlier</b> of the pair (Jan 1600 vs Apr 1601).</li><li><b>Significance — the Alexandria/Egypt observation plan and the bid to place the son at the Medici court (the back-story to the letter of 30 April 1601).</b></li><li><b>The low-latitude observation project.</b> Tycho's striking ambition: to make observations impossible at Bohemian latitude <b>by sending observers south — to Italy, \"where the inclination of the celestial Sphere is less,\" and above all to Alexandria in Egypt, \"where it is yet more upright\"</b> (nearer the <i>sphaera recta</i> of the equator, where the southern stars and the zodiac ride higher — the classic site of Ptolemaic astronomy). A rare glimpse of Tycho planning a <i>southern expedition</i> to extend his star-work.</li><li><b>The personnel.</b> His <b>elder son, Tycho Brahe the Younger (\"named after myself,\" aged 18)</b>, trained \"from boyhood,\" with <b>a student-companion</b> of three years' astronomical service, to carry <b>one or two portable instruments</b> plus instructions; the <b>son to remain in the Medici court</b>, the <b>student to sail on to Egypt</b>.</li><li><b>The Venice connection.</b> Tycho counts on <b>the Republic of Venice</b> to ship the student to Egypt — hope already raised to <b>Tengnagel</b> when he solicited there — especially with the Grand Duke's backing.</li><li><b>Tengnagel's Florence embassy.</b> Confirms <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> carried Tycho's <b>gift books to Ferdinando</b> and was warmly received — the diplomatic groundwork.</li><li><b>Aftermath cross-reference.</b> The plan did not unfold as designed: by <b>April 1601 (brahe-t_16010430001)</b> the son had gone to Italy <b>with the Persian embassy</b> instead, was <b>left at Siena</b>, and the <b>Capuchin calumny</b> had made Rome dangerous — with Tycho then seeking to place him at <b>Henri IV's</b> court. Read the letter of 24 January 1600 and that of 30 April 1601 as the <b>plan and its derailment</b>.</li><li><b>Other details.</b> Tycho's <b>library is still stored at Magdeburg</b>, awaiting the spring thaw of the <b>Elbe</b> (the Emperor having ordered the Magdeburgers to release it) — which is why he cannot yet send the Grand Duke more books. He writes from <b>Benátky</b>, \"half a day from Prague,\" restoring \"Urania's foundations.\"</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Ferdinando I de' Medici; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (the book-bearing emissary); <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b> (the 18-year-old son); the unnamed <b>student-companion</b> (3 years' service, bound for Egypt); the <b>Republic of Venice</b>; the <b>Tuscan Legate</b> at Prague (unnamed). Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Germany/Magdeburg</b>, the <b>Elbe</b>, <b>Italy/Tuscany</b>, <b>Egypt/Alexandria</b>, <b>Venice</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Die 24 Ianuarij An. 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>24 January 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable formal Latin read with confidence; the substance (the thanks for the Grand Duke's letter and the gift-book reception; the move to Benátky under Rudolf II with the library still at Magdeburg awaiting the Elbe thaw; and the centrepiece — <b>the plan to send his son and a student-observer to Tuscany and on to Alexandria for low-latitude observations, leaving the son at the Medici court, with Venetian shipping help</b>) is firmly construed and dovetails with the April-1601 aftermath letter. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 5 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.241-245) visually, long-s typeface, no text layer. The letter's actual text runs pp.242-244 (p.241 is the tail of the preceding letter 'Tycho ad Anonymum'; p.245 is entirely the following letter to Hoffmann). Collated source_text against Dreyer's print sentence-by-sentence across the full letter body (salutation through subscription) and found source_text matches Dreyer exactly (no TEXT_ERROR). Collated published_english against source_text clause-by-clause: checked every proper name and place (Rudolf II, Benatica/Benatky castle, Prague, Bohemia, Magdeburg, the Elbe/Albis, Franz Tengnagel, Italy, Egypt/Alexandria, the Republic of Venice), the son's stated age (18 Annorum -&gt; '18 years', correct), the closing date (Die 24 Ianuarij An. 1600 -&gt; '24th day of January, the year 1600', correct), the pronoun antecedents in the Urania/patria clause (Urania, not Tycho, is what 'could not be preserved' in the fatherland - correctly kept as 'she'/'her' in translation) and in the son/student Egypt clause (son stays at Ferdinando's court, the student sails on to Egypt - correctly parsed, no flip), and the hedges ('uti spero', 'si modo... queat', 'cuperem') all preserved. No sentence or clause found dropped; no invented names, numbers, or closing material; no negation flips. Two loose but non-material paraphrases noted and not flagged as defects: 'non degravatus est' rendered as 'has not thought it beneath Him' rather than literally 'was not burdened' (same polarity/sense), and 'Ex quo insuper' rendered as 'Moreover, since' (word-order/connective smoothing, per the ignore-list). Clean bill: no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, HEDGE_LOSS, or TEXT_ERROR found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Bibliotheca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento part 3, vol. 12)."], "th": {"label": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15981108001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks the Grand Duke for his unsolicited letter and expresses pleasure that so eminent a prince takes an interest in astronomy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000124001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000126001", "d": "1600-01-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Friedrich Hoffmann — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 26 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET GENEROSO DOMINO, DOMINO IOHANNI FRIDERICO HOFFMANNO, LIBERO BARONI IN GRUENPUECHEL ET STRECHAW, PRAeFECTO PROVINCIAe DUCATUS STYRIAe ET ARCHIDUCATUS AUSTRIAe MARSCALCHO UBIQUE HAeREDITARIO BURGRAVIO IN STEYER NEC NON SAC. CAeS. MAIESTATIS AULAe CONSILIARIO ET SERENISSIMI ARCHIDUCIS MAXIMILIANI CUBICULARIO PRIMARIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>MAGNO equidem gaudio affectus sum, cum heri vesperi ex meis Domesticis huc Pragâ revertentibus percepissem Illustr. Generos. Tuam ex Austriâ nuper salvam et incolumem redijsse. Quo nomine non saltem Illustri Generositati Tuae, sed et mihi ipsi quamplurimum congratulor, siquidem de Generositatis Tuae erga me et ea, quae tracto studia Astronomica, sincera et benevola affectione vel ex pauculo, quem superiori aestate invicem habuimus congressu, ita mihi optima quaeque polliceor, ut in tanti viri, qui non saltem Genere clarus sit, sed et doctrinis liberalibus insigniter excultus, familiaritatem incidisse, non postremae felicitatis loco ducam. Cumque nunc Pragam Nobilem et Eruditum adolescentem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM, mihi domesticum, qui ante paucas hebdomadas ex Italia huc reversus est, unâ cum filio meo natu maiore ex me denominato certis de caussis amandem, non potui intermittere, quin hasce pro felici reditu candido animo Illust. G. T. congratulantes unâ mitterem, atque ijs simul iniungerem, ut Tuam Illust. Generos. captata oportunitate accedentes eam ex me officiosissime salutarent. Pergratum vero est, quod Illustris Dominatio Tua Excellentem Mathematicum Dominum Μ. IOHANNEM KEPLERUM in suo Comitatu huc ad nos in Bohemiam adduxerit, qua etiam in parte non obscure elucet Illust. G. T. erga sublimia illa et a vulgi captu remota Mathematum studia eorumque alumnos singularis et nunquam satis laudatus favor atque benignitas ab omnibus hisce excellentioribus Scientijs addictis merito semper depraedicanda. Intercessit mihi cum illo viro, postquam Daniâ emigravi, nonnulla per Litteras eademque pergrata familiaritas, quae ex mutua praesentia, quam brevi futuram mihi persuadeo, augeri poterit. Quicquid vero voluptatis et commodi ex hoc nostro congressu promanare poterit, Illustri Dominationi Tuae praecipua ex parte acceptum ferendum grata mente agnosco. Pluribus Illust. Generos. T. multiplicibus alias pariterque gravissimis occupationibus distentam remorari nolo. Caetera, quae hîc omitto, ij, quos ablego, si Illust. Domni Tuae eos admittere commodum fuerit, supplebunt atque ex me referent; quibus ut benignam aurem pro solita sua humanitate praebere non dedignetur, qua possum reverentia, oro et obsecro. Valeat Tua Illust. Generositas quam felicissime et diutissime, huncque noviter inchoatum Annum Iubilaeum plurimosque alios insequentes incolumis et florens transigat.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesariana Benatica die 26 Ianuarij Anni labentis Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae 1600, quam duobus deficere IOSEPHUS SCALIGER probare nititur.<br>Iterum et saepius T. Illust. Gen. quam optime valeat, meque et meos sibi commendatos habeat.<br>Illustri Generositati Tuae<br>addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and generous Lord, Lord Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Free Baron in Grünbüchel and Strechau, Prefect of the Province of the Duchy of Styria, hereditary Marshal everywhere of the Archduchy of Austria, Burgrave in Steyr, and also Aulic Councillor of the Sacred Imperial Majesty and First Chamberlain of the Most Serene Archduke Maximilian, his Lord and friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Illustrious and generous Man, Lord and most honoured friend.</p><p>I was indeed filled with great joy when, yesterday evening, from my servants returning hither from Prague, I learned that Your Illustrious Generosity had lately returned safe and sound from Austria. On which account I congratulate not only Your Illustrious Generosity, but also myself, most heartily; since, concerning Your Generosity's sincere and benevolent affection toward me and the astronomical studies I pursue, I promise myself every best thing — even from the brief meeting we had together last summer — so that I count it no slight happiness to have fallen into the friendship of so great a man, who is not only illustrious by lineage, but also remarkably cultivated in the liberal learnings. And since I am now dispatching to Prague, for certain reasons, the noble and learned young man Franz Tengnagel, of my household — who a few weeks ago returned hither from Italy — together with my elder son, named after me, I could not omit to send along with them these lines congratulating Your Illustrious Generosity, with a candid heart, on your happy return, and at the same time to charge them that, approaching Your Illustrious Generosity at a fitting opportunity, they greet you most dutifully on my behalf. It is very welcome indeed that Your Illustrious Lordship has brought the excellent Mathematician Master Johannes Kepler, in your retinue, hither to us into Bohemia — in which respect, too, there shines forth not obscurely Your Illustrious Generosity's singular and never sufficiently praised favour and kindness toward those sublime mathematical studies, remote from the common grasp, and toward their nurslings — a favour ever deservedly to be proclaimed by all who are devoted to these more excellent Sciences. There has passed between me and that man, since I emigrated from Denmark, a certain and most pleasing familiarity by letters, which will be able to be increased by our mutual presence — which I am persuaded will be soon. And whatever of pleasure and profit may flow from this meeting of ours, I acknowledge, with grateful mind, that it is to be credited in chief part to Your Illustrious Lordship. I do not wish to detain Your Illustrious Generosity, taken up with manifold and likewise most weighty occupations, with more words. The rest, which I omit here, those whom I send will supply — if it be convenient for Your Illustrious Lordship to admit them — and will report on my behalf; to whom that you would not disdain to lend a kindly ear, after your wonted humanity, I beg and beseech with what reverence I can. May Your Illustrious Generosity fare most happily and most long, and pass this newly begun Jubilee Year, and very many others to follow, safe and flourishing.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 26th day of January of the passing year of the Christian Dionysian Epoch 1600 — which Joseph Scaliger strives to prove deficient by two [years].<br>Again and often, may Your Illustrious Generosity fare as well as possible, and hold me and mine commended to you.<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious Generosity,<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Significance — the documentary record of Kepler's arrival to Tycho.</b> This letter, the <b>same-day companion of Tycho's first invitation to Kepler</b> (brahe-t_16000126002, Benátky, 26 January 1600), thanks <b>Hoffmann for bringing Kepler to Bohemia in his retinue</b>. The two letters interlock: the <i>\"servants returning hither from Prague yesterday evening\"</i> who reported Hoffmann's return are the same who brought Kepler's letter in the Kepler-invitation; and both letters announce the dispatch of <b>Franz Tengnagel and Tycho's elder son to Prague</b> (in whose carriage Tycho invites Kepler to ride back to Benátky). Together they fix the convergence of Tycho and Kepler in the opening weeks of 1600.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Freiherr von Grünbüchel und Strechau</b> (1555–1608) — <i>Landeshauptmann</i> (Prefect) of the <b>Duchy of Styria</b>, hereditary Marshal of the Archduchy of Austria, Burgrave in Steyr, <b>Imperial Aulic Councillor</b>, and First Chamberlain of <b>Archduke Maximilian</b>; a Protestant magnate and patron of learning at whose <b>Prague house Kepler first lodged</b>. The full titulature is reproduced.</li><li><b>Kepler.</b> <i>Excellens Mathematicus M. Johannes Keplerus, Illustrium Styriae Ordinum Mathematicus</i> = <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, then <b>mathematician to the Estates of Styria</b> (the post he was losing as the Counter-Reformation drove him from Graz). Tycho records that he and Kepler had a <i>\"most pleasing familiarity by letters since I emigrated from Denmark,\"</i> soon to be increased \"by mutual presence\" — the prelude to their collaboration. The whole favour of the meeting Tycho credits \"in chief part\" to Hoffmann.</li><li><b>The intermediaries.</b> <i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</i> = <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, \"a few weeks ago returned from Italy\" (cf. brahe-t_15991104001, brahe-t_16000504001/002); <i>filius meus natu maior ex me denominatus</i> = Tycho's <b>elder son, Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>. Both are sent to Prague to carry this letter and transact \"certain business,\" and to greet Hoffmann.</li><li><b>The summer-1599 meeting.</b> <i>ex pauculo … superiori aestate … congressu</i> — Tycho and Hoffmann had briefly met the previous summer (1599); Tycho builds on that acquaintance.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Hoffmann; Johannes Kepler; Franz Tengnagel; Tycho the Younger; Archduke Maximilian (in the titulature); Joseph Scaliger (in the dateline jest).</li><li><b>Calendar / chronology jest (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 26 Ianuarij Anni labentis Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae 1600, quam duobus deficere Iosephus Scaliger probare nititur</i> — \"of the passing year of the Christian <b>Dionysian</b> Epoch 1600 [the Anno Domini reckoning of Dionysius Exiguus], <b>which Joseph Scaliger strives to prove deficient by two [years]</b>.\" The same <b>Scaliger chronology jest</b> Tycho makes in brahe-t_16000327002 (where the year is \"really 1602\"). <b>New Style</b> (imperial). <i>Annus Jubilaeus</i> = the <b>Holy/Jubilee Year 1600</b>. Header date matches the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — polished, fully legible Latin and a document of the first importance for the Tycho–Kepler story; the thanks to Hoffmann for bringing <b>Kepler</b> to Bohemia in his retinue, the prior epistolary acquaintance and imminent meeting, the dispatch of Tengnagel and Tycho the Younger to Prague, and the Scaliger dateline jest are all clear and corroborated by the same-day Kepler invitation (brahe-t_16000126002). No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.243-246 as page images; the Tycho-to-Hoffmann letter runs from the lower half of p.244 ('ILLUSTRI ET GENEROSO DOMINO...') through p.245 ('...TYCHO BRAHE.'); pp.243 and 246 hold the neighbouring letters (to Ferdinand and to Kepler) and were not needed. Collated the entire Latin text word for word against Dreyer's print: it matches source_text exactly, including every proper name (Hoffmann's full titulature, Franciscus Tengnaglius, Johannes Kepler), the date (26 Ianuarij, Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae 1600), and the Scaliger aside (duobus deficere). Checked the published_english against this verified Latin clause by clause: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000203001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000203001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Congratulates Hoffmann on his safe return from Austria and expresses confidence in his benevolent support for astronomical studies.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000126001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000126002", "d": "1600-01-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Kepler — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 26 January 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET ERUDITISSIMO VIRO DOMINO Μ. IOHANNI KEPLERO ILLUSTRIUM STYRIAe ORDINUM MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO PLURIMI FACIENDO.</p><p>REVERSI heri vesperi sero ad me domestici illi mei, quos Pragam miseram, tuas, Eccme et amantissime Domine KEPLERE, ex insperato attulere humanissimas literas, quibus lectis, te Pragae nunc esse, perlubenter comperi. Invite tamen ex ijsdem colligebam, te necdum meas, quas ultimis Tuis affatim respondentes ante mensem circiter Viennam, ut inde per Dominum Doctorem BLOTIUM tibi, ubicunque invenireris, mitterentur, direxi. Quae si interea tibi traditae fuissent, hac reiterata protestatione et excusatione in re ista, quae tot verba non meretur, supersedisses, atque actutum citra diuturniorem deliberationem et cunctationem huc (siquidem in vicinia es) advolasses. Quod ut etiamnum facias, te etiam atque etiam rogo. Poteris in eodem vehiculo comitari Nobilem et eruditum adolescentem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM domesticum nostrum, et filium meum TYCHONEM natu maiorem, quos Pragam ob negocia quaedam peculiaria, meo nomine isthic expedienda, nunc ablego. Illi enim intra paucos dies, volente Deo, huc revertentur, et locum pro te in curru facile invenient.</p><p>De meo isthuc adventu necdum certi quid concludere possum, antequam Caesaream Maiestatem Dominum nostrum Clementissimum eo reversum esse intellexero. Neque enim quid alias isthic magnopere agam, habeo, nec libenter accedo, nisi a Caesarea Maiestate clementissime vocer: siquidem id citra temporis, cuius, tanquam thesauri irrecuperabilis, sum parcissimus, aliqualem iacturam, et studiorum, quae prae manibus habeo, remoram fieri nequeat.</p><p>Explorandi etiam hisce noctibus acronychij situs Martis et Iovis, tum quoque Mercurij, quem post occasum Solis discrete apparentem ter proxime elapsis crepusculis observavimus, tum quoque ad Venerem Eoam, quae etiam nunc interdiu unâ cum Sole, adeoque in ipso Meridiano existens, spectatur, et observationi, prout aliquot quoque annis praeteritis a nobis factum, patet, advertendum. Taceo quod Saturnus non longe a □ ☉ mane iuxta Meridianum apparens, pro Parallaxi orbis (uti vocat COPERNICUS) et Latitudine sua (siquidem appropinquet ad limitem maxime Boreum) rimanda, non inconvenientem exhibeat occasionem, donec adventante Aprili plane pernox redditus, Apogaeum et Eccentricitatem, unâque ipsam Latitudinem maximam, prout haec a nobis iam antea explorata sunt, comprobet. Accedit et hoc, quod in proxime futuro plenilunio Luna Eclipsin, licet minutulam, iuxta ipsum occasum paulo ante ortum Solis patietur, cui etiam (ut prioribus sedulo designatis adijci queat, atque restitutio motus Luminarium a nobis prius facta, ulterius examinari) attendendum, modo serenitas oportuna id permiserit.</p><p>Vides itaque, mi KEPLERE, quot et quanta exercitia nostra Astronomica ingerant obstacula, nobis tamen haud ingrata (siquidem ijs distineri volupe est) quo minus Pragam tam cito, uti opinatus es, excurrere queam. Tuum itaque erit praevenire, meque hîc mature (uti dictum) invisere. Advenies non tam hospes quam amicus gratissimus, nostrarumque in coelestibus contemplationum, per ea quae nunc ad manus habeo, instrumenta spectator et socius acceptissimus. Tunc, volente Numine, coram de pluribus. Vale et Illustrem ac Generosum Dominum Baronem IOHANNEM FRIDERICUM HOFFMANNUM, cui etiam nonnulla congratulationis caussa pro felici reditu scripsi, ex me officiosissime resaluta.</p><p>Dabantur properanter ex arce Caesareana Benatica die 26 Ianuarij 1600.<br>Tui Amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Master Johannes Kepler, distinguished mathematician to the Illustrious Estates of Styria, his friend much to be esteemed.</b></p><p>When those servants of mine whom I had sent to Prague returned to me late yesterday evening, they brought, unexpectedly, your most courteous letter, most excellent and most beloved Master Kepler; and on reading it I learned with great pleasure that you are now in Prague. Yet from the same letter I gathered, against my wish, that you had not yet [received] mine — which, fully answering your last, I directed to Vienna about a month ago, so that from there, through Master Doctor Blotius, they might be sent on to you wherever you should be found. Had these meanwhile been delivered to you, you would have spared yourself this repeated protestation and apology in a matter that does not deserve so many words, and would forthwith, without longer deliberation and delay, have flown hither (since you are in the neighbourhood). And that you would do this even now, I ask you again and again. You will be able to travel in the same carriage with the noble and learned young man Franz Tengnagel, a member of my household, and with my elder son Tycho, whom I am now dispatching to Prague on account of certain particular business of mine to be transacted there. For within a few days, God willing, they will return hither, and will readily find a place for you in the carriage.</p><p>Concerning my own coming thither, I can as yet conclude nothing for certain, until I have learned that His Imperial Majesty, our Most Clement Lord, has returned there. For I have nothing else of much moment to do there, nor do I willingly go, unless I be most graciously summoned by His Imperial Majesty — seeing that this could not be done without some loss of time (of which, as of an irrecoverable treasure, I am most sparing) and without delay to the studies I have in hand.</p><p>[There is] also [reason] to investigate, on these [coming] nights, the acronychal position of Mars and of Jupiter; then too of Mercury, which we observed clearly visible after sunset, in the last three twilights just past; and likewise [we must] attend to Venus as Morning Star, which even now is to be seen by day together with the Sun — and indeed, being on the very meridian, is open to observation, as has also been done by us in several years past. I say nothing of how Saturn — appearing in the morning near the meridian, not far from quadrature with the Sun [□ ☉] — affords a not inconvenient occasion for investigating the parallax of the orb (as Copernicus calls it) and its [own] latitude (since it is approaching the most northerly limit); until, with April coming on, being rendered fully visible all night long, it may confirm its apogee and eccentricity, and together its greatest latitude itself, as these have already before been investigated by us. There is this besides, that at the full moon next to come the Moon will undergo an eclipse — a slight one, to be sure — close to setting [and] a little before sunrise; to which also [we must] attend (so that it may be added to those carefully recorded before, and so that the restoration of the motion of the luminaries, made earlier by us, may be examined further), provided that suitable clear weather permit it.</p><p>You see therefore, my dear Kepler, how many and how great are the obstacles our astronomical labours throw in the way — labours not unwelcome to us, however (since to be held fast by them is a pleasure) — to my being able to run over to Prague as soon as you supposed. It will be your part, then, to come first, and to visit me here in good time (as has been said). You will come not so much as a guest as a most welcome friend, and as a most acceptable spectator and partner of our contemplations of the heavens, by means of the instruments I now have at hand. Then, God willing, [we shall speak] face to face of more matters. Farewell; and on my behalf most dutifully return the greeting of the Illustrious and Noble Lord Baron Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, to whom too I have written some words by way of congratulation on his happy return.</p><p>Given in haste from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 26th day of January 1600.<br>Your most loving<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>I have written [this] with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, then established at the imperial castle of Benátky nad Jizerou (north-east of Prague), having entered the service of Emperor Rudolf II in 1599. This is the letter that summons the young <b>Kepler</b> to join him — one of the most consequential letters in the history of astronomy, written some four weeks before their first meeting (4 February 1600).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>M. Iohanni Keplero … Styriae Ordinum Mathematico</i> = <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, then district mathematician (<i>Landschaftsmathematiker</i>) to the Protestant Estates of Styria at Graz, recently displaced by the Counter-Reformation expulsion of Protestants and so \"in the neighbourhood.\" <i>M.</i> = <i>Magister</i> (academic \"Master,\" protocol §5.5).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arce Caesareana Benatica</i> = the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (Latin <i>Benatecum</i>; protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Dominus Doctor Blotius</i> = <b>Hugo Blotius (Hugues Blotz)</b>, imperial librarian (Hofbibliothek) at Vienna, used here as a relay for correspondence.</li><li><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</i> = <b>Franz Gansneb Tengnagel von Camp</b> (c. 1576–1622), Tycho's noble Westphalian assistant, later his son-in-law and a literary executor of the Tychonic legacy.</li><li><i>filium meum Tychonem natu maiorem</i> = <b>Tyge (Tycho) Brahe the Younger</b>, Tycho's elder son.</li><li><i>Caesarea Maiestas Dominus noster Clementissimus</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>, Tycho's imperial patron.</li><li><i>Baro Iohannes Fridericus Hoffmannus</i> = <b>Baron Johann Friedrich Hoffmann von Grünbüchel und Strechau</b>, an imperial councillor and patron of Kepler's, in whose company Kepler had travelled and at whose Prague house he was lodging.</li><li><b>Astronomy — observational programme (this is the technical heart of the letter; protocol §5.1).</b></li><li><i>acronychij situs Martis et Iovis</i> = the <b>acronychal position</b> of Mars and Jupiter — i.e. their rising at sunset, near opposition to the Sun (the configuration most diagnostic for orbital elements). The Mars-opposition observations of exactly this season are the data Kepler would inherit and exploit. <i>Acronychal</i> (Greek <i>akrónychos</i>, \"at nightfall\") is kept as the technical term.</li><li><i>Mercurij … post occasum Solis discrete apparentem</i> = Mercury seen as a distinct (\"discrete\") evening object after sunset, at <i>crepusculis</i> = the <b>twilights</b>.</li><li><i>Venerem Eoam</i> = <b>Venus as Morning Star</b> (<i>Eous</i>, \"of the dawn / eastern\"); the rendering keeps the epithet's force. The daytime/meridian visibility noted is Venus near greatest brilliancy.</li><li><i>□ ☉</i> = the aspect glyph for <b>quadrature</b> (square) with the <b>Sun</b>; named in the translation, glyphs kept in the source block (protocol §5.3).</li><li><i>Parallaxi orbis (uti vocat Copernicus)</i> = the <b>parallax of the orb</b> — Copernicus's own term (<i>parallaxis orbis</i>, the great-orbit / annual parallax: the apparent displacement of a superior planet arising from the Earth's annual motion). Tycho cites the Copernican label <i>without</i> endorsing Copernican cosmology; the hedge \"as Copernicus calls it\" is preserved, not flattened into \"annual parallax.\"</li><li><i>Apogaeum et Eccentricitatem … Latitudinem maximam</i> = <b>apogee, eccentricity, and greatest latitude</b> — the orbital elements of Saturn to be confirmed when it becomes <i>pernox</i> (visible the whole night long) in April.</li><li><i>plenilunio Luna Eclipsin … minutulam … patietur</i> = at the coming <b>full moon</b> the Moon will suffer a slight <b>(lunar) eclipse</b> near setting, just before sunrise. (The lunar eclipse of 9/10 February 1600 N.S. was indeed of small magnitude.)</li><li><i>restitutio motus Luminarium</i> = the <b>restoration of the motion of the luminaries</b> (Sun and Moon) — Tycho's programme term (<i>restitutio</i>; protocol §5.1), the renewal of the solar and lunar theories he had already worked out and now wishes to test further.</li><li><i>instrumenta … spectator et socius</i> = Tycho invites Kepler to be spectator and partner among the <b>instruments</b> (<i>organa/instrumenta</i>, §5.2) he has at Benátky.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Tui Amantissimus</i> → \"Your most loving\"; <i>manu propria scripsi</i> → \"I have written [this] with my own hand\" (autograph attestation). <i>Dabantur … Benatica</i> (epistolary \"they were given,\" i.e. dispatched) rendered \"Given … from Benátky.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — the chosen pilot crux).</b> The dateline reads <i>die 26 Ianuarij 1600</i>; the dataset label marks the letter <b>n.s. (New Style / Gregorian)</b>. This is correct and significant: by 1600 Tycho, now in Catholic Imperial service at Benátky, has shifted from the Old-Style (Julian) dating of his Danish and Hessen correspondence to the <b>New Style</b> used in Rudolfine lands — the very 10-day Julian/Gregorian gap flagged in §3. The date is <b>reproduced as written and not converted</b>; the equivalent Old-Style date would be 16 January 1600, but no conversion is performed in the text. The lunar eclipse mentioned (the next full moon) falls 9/10 February 1600 N.S., consistent with the New-Style dating of the letter.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clean, complete, and unambiguous, and the local transcription matches the Dreyer reading on the technical passage (<i>Parallaxi orbis … uti vocat Copernicus</i>, the <i>□ ☉</i> glyphs, <i>acronychij situs</i>). The single point requiring care is the calendar style, which is correctly New Style and is flagged, not converted, per protocol §3; it is not a genuine textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VIII pp.245-248 (page images, not OCR text layer); the letter's actual text occupies pp.246-247. Checked the full body including the technical astronomical passage (Mars/Jupiter acronychal positions, Mercury's three twilights, Venus as morning star, Saturn's quadrature and parallax, the coming lunar eclipse), every proper name (Tengnagel, Tycho the younger, Blotius, Hoffmann), and the date/place (Benátky, 26 January 1600). No divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_15991209001", "next": "brahe-t_16000400001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990200001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000400001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports receiving Kepler's letter and urges him to travel at once to Benátky, noting a previous reply was sent via Blotius in Vienna.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000126002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000203001", "d": "1600-02-03", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann (Baron) → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 3 February 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (German address-line) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDLEN VNND GESTRENGEN HERRN TYCHO BRAHAe, HERRN AUF KNUDTSTRUPP VNND VRANIBURGKH, MEINEM SONNDERSLIEBEN HERRN VNNDT GUETTEN FREUNDT.</p><p>S.</p><p>LITTERAS tuas, Vir nobilissime, quibus me tam splendide per egregios iuvenes, internunctios tuos, salutas, mei amore et commendatione plenas, imo in excultioribus meae tenuitatis nimiam paene laudem continentes, accepi, cum voluptate profecto, dubius tamen, tot inter gratulandi occasiones palmam cui concederem. Ecce autem animi tandem occurrebat sinceritas, omnibus alijs non immerito praeferenda. Caetera, tanquam illius effectus et signa civilitati ac humanitati Tuae adscribens, pari, si non calamo, mentis tamen integritate et studio compensanda.</p><p>Quod D. FRANCISCI TENGNAGELIJ, familiaris tui, virtutem ac valorem, quem ipsa facies et mores exprimunt, etiam litterarum tuarum testimonio apud me augere tibi placuerit, nil miror. Est tamen mihi quod gratulor et tibi, qui tantum Adolescentem cognoscendum dederis, gratiam ut referam. Rationem consilij eius de Bohemica hac ora tui procul dubio causa non tam brevi relinquenda plurimum etiam nostro ex interesse approbamus et laetamur, ac ipsi et filio tuo quam valemus et habemus exilitatem in hac aula nostram fideliter et cum libertate offero.</p><p>Magistrum KEPLERUM, omni praeconio maiorem, favoreque tuo et patrocinio multis rationibus dignum, tibi iam commendatum amplius nunc non commendo, sed ipsum se spectandum et fruendum potius praemitto, apud te, mihi, ut spero, multarum rerum praeconem et conciliatorem. Intermediatoris tamen quod officium ita amanter et benigne cum gratiarum actione susceperis, ad me pertinet, Domine TYCHO, ac facis in eo, te certe ac humanitate tua, quin nostra, ad te perpetuo amandum et colendum benevolentia haud indignum.</p><p>Addo idipsum, nostri gratia quicquid porro accesserit, id omne non tam ad cumulum tuorum in nos meritorum esse referendum, quam alta et grata memoria prosequendum. In specie vero, quae et ipsius KEPLERI ratione conferenda essent aliqua, in occasionem nostri conventus differre cogimur, quem nobis efficiat Deus propediem, ut spero et opto.</p><p>Vale ac reliqua ex ipso KEPLERO et tuis.<br>Pragae 3 Februarij A. D. 1600.<br>T. D. ex animo<br>IOHANNES FRIDERICUS HOFFMANN,<br>L. Baro.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and gallant Lord Tycho Brahe, lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, my especially dear lord and good friend.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Your letter, most noble man — in which you so splendidly greet me through those excellent young men, your messengers, [a letter] full of love and commendation of me, indeed containing in its more polished phrases almost excessive praise of my slender worth — I received with pleasure, in truth, yet at a loss to which among so many occasions for thankfulness I should award the palm. But behold, the sincerity of your mind presented itself at last, not undeservedly to be preferred to all the rest. The other [qualities], ascribing them as the effects and tokens of that [sincerity] to your courtesy and humanity, are to be repaid with an integrity and zeal of mind equal [to yours], if not of pen.</p><p>That it pleased you to enhance with me — by the testimony of your letter too — the virtue and worth of Master Franz Tengnagel, your familiar (which his very face and bearing express), I do not wonder at. Yet I have cause to congratulate, and to render you thanks, who have given me so fine a young man to know. The reasoning of his plan to leave this Bohemian shore — doubtless not so soon, [and] for your sake — we likewise much approve and rejoice at, out of our own interest as well; and to him and to your son I faithfully and freely offer our slender [standing] in this court, so far as we are able and have it.</p><p>Master Kepler — greater than all proclamation, and worthy of your favour and patronage on many grounds — already commended to you, I now commend no further, but rather send him on ahead to be beheld and enjoyed in person; with you he will be, as I hope, the herald and reconciler of many matters for me. Yet that you have so lovingly and kindly, and with thanksgiving, taken up the office of intermediary — that, Lord Tycho, falls to me [to acknowledge]; and in it you do a thing assuredly not unworthy of being perpetually loved and revered by me with goodwill — by you and your humanity, nay ours.</p><p>I add this very thing: whatever shall further accrue for our sake, all of it is to be set down not so much to the heap of your services toward us, as to be attended with deep and grateful memory. But in particular, certain matters that should be conferred upon by reason of Kepler himself, we are forced to defer to the occasion of our meeting — which may God bring about for us soon, as I hope and wish.</p><p>Farewell, and the rest from Kepler himself and from your people.<br>Prague, the 3rd of February, in the Year of the Lord 1600.<br>Yours from the heart,<br>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann,<br>Free Baron.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann von Grünbüchel und Strechau</b>, <i>liber baro</i> (<b>Free Baron</b>), imperial councillor and a patron of Kepler's — the same magnate at whose Prague house Kepler lodged and whose greeting Tycho sends in the famous summons-letter (brahe-t_16000126002, where he is <i>Baro Iohannes Fridericus Hoffmannus</i>). The mixed presentation (German address-line over a Latin body) is preserved (protocol §2, §4.2).</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Dated <b>3 February 1600</b> — the day <i>before</i> Kepler's first arrival at Benátky (4 February). Hoffmann, who had brought Kepler from Graz to Prague, here <b>sends Kepler on to Tycho</b> (<i>ipsum … praemitto</i>) and acknowledges Tycho's letter of welcome carried by Tycho's \"young messengers.\" It is thus a hinge document of the Tycho–Kepler meeting, the patron's side of the introduction.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Magister Keplerus</i> = <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, <i>omni praeconio maior</i> (\"greater than all proclamation\"), commended by Hoffmann as a \"herald and reconciler of many matters\" at Tycho's.</li><li><i>D. Franciscus Tengnagelius, familiaris tuus</i> = <b>Franz Gansneb Tengnagel von Camp</b>, Tycho's noble assistant, whose worth Tycho had praised and whose intended departure from Bohemia (to visit Westphalia — cf. brahe-t_16000306002) is referred to here.</li><li><i>filius tuus</i> = one of Tycho's sons.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae &amp; titles (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"Greeting\"); <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; subscription <i>T. D. ex animo</i> = <i>Tuae Dominationi ex animo</i> (\"Yours from the heart\") + <i>L. Baro</i> = <i>liber baro</i> (\"Free Baron\"). The German address <i>meinem sonderslieben Herrn und guten Freund</i> → \"my especially dear lord and good friend.\"</li><li><b>Register.</b> A studied, almost mannered courtier's Latin — heavy with the humility topos (<i>meae tenuitatis … exilitatem nostram</i>, \"my slender worth … our slender standing\"), antithesis (<i>si non calamo, mentis tamen integritate</i>), and deferred clauses. The convolution is preserved rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Pragae 3 Februarij A. D. 1600</i>; the dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b> — correct for an Imperial-court letter from Prague. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the named matter (the Kepler introduction, Tengnagel, the offer of court support) is secure and the persons are firmly identified; graded B rather than A only because Hoffmann's deliberately elaborate, ellipsis-laden courtier's syntax leaves the precise force of one or two clauses (e.g. <i>facis in eo … haud indignum</i>) open to more than one shading. The translation keeps the convolution and its slight indeterminacy rather than resolving it into smoother English.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening, every paragraph, closing, date, all proper names: Tengnagel, Kepler) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.247-248 as page images. Translation is careful and accurate throughout with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation. One trivial spelling corruption survives in our own Latin (internunctios vs Dreyer's internuncios), which under the brief's rule (any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C) prevents an A despite the translation itself being clean.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text spells the word with an extra 'ct' (internunctios); Dreyer prints the standard internuncios (\"go-betweens/messengers\"). Meaning is unaffected and the English (\"messengers\") is correct regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>internuncios tuos</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.246-249 page images. The single flagged defect (internunctios/internuncios) could not be confirmed: the word as printed in Dreyer's page image appears to match our source_text's spelling rather than the claimed correction, so the defect is rejected per the prime rule against unverifiable changes. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000126001", "next": "brahe-t_16000306001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000126001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000306001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Tycho's letter, accepts its excessive praise with humility, and commends the character and bearing of Franz Tengnagel.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000203001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000212001", "d": "1600-02-12", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Simon von Petkum", "ck": "Simon von Petkum", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Wandsbek", "t": "Simon von Petkum → Tycho Brahe — [Wandsbek/Hamburg area], 12 February 1600", "m": "<p>Language: German (Early New High German / Low German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 35 f. (original letter)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> Tycho's northern agent <b>Simon von Petkum</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. Place omitted in the source (<i>s.l.</i>); written from the <b>Wandsbek/Hamburg area</b> (Sophie Brahe is \"here at Wandsbek\"). A business + news letter in Petkum's Low-German-inflected hand.</p>", "la": "<p>DEM GESTRENGEN, EEDLEN VND ERNUESTEN JUNCKHEREN TYCHONJ BRAHE, ERBGESESSEN VFF KNUDTSTORP, ASTRONOMIAe RESTAURATORJ, DIENSTLEICH ZUHANDEN. PRAGE ODER IN DER JEGENT, NEMLICH ZU GERSITZ. A. 1600.</p><p>GOTTES gnade vnd friede zuuor. Gestrenger, edler, ernuester, großgunstiger junckher Tycho, benebenst ehrerbietung meiner gantzwilligen dienste magk ich Ew. Gestre. nicht vorhalten, das ych zwar von hern magistro Dauide Fabricii itzo 2 schreiben verhanden, die er mir gantz hoch vnd tewer per gantz gewiße bodtschafft euch zusendendt begeret hat. So ist warleich in all die zeit keiner (der directe nach Prag wolte) mir furkomen, wowol mir vnter andern auch von hern doctor Vito Winßhemio vortrostung gethan. Habe derentwegen discrete vnd diuise (ich meine vff Leiptzigh vnd dernach vf Dreßdinn zu vbergeben) E. G. dieselbigen lieber zuordenen wollen als lenger dieselbige bej mir zubehalten, nemlich auff Leiptzigh, von dannen an h. Hans Tuchman nach Dreßdenn vnd alsovordt zu E. Gest. henden zukommen. Da dis allso nicht wol vordtginge, so haben Ewre Gestrengheit zubedencken, ob sie euch nicht beßer per Nurenberch durch hern Leonhard Dillher, keiserlichen factoren, müchten zur hand konnen kommen, so geschege die vorwexelung nur and einen vnd nit zu zween orteren, dauon Ewer Gestr. aduis zum nechst.</p><p>Was von der schiff für die jnstrumente zu hoffen, das werden Eur G. aus hern Vincentij Mollerj (der its vornjar burgemeister geworden is) seinem schreiben zuuornemen. Mich ducht gudt zu sein, wan keis. mst. ein klein schreiben derowegen an e. erb. radt von Hamhurgk abgehen liße. Das schreiben konte noch wol so bald hervnter kommen, alse die jnstrumente hir mugen anlangen.</p><p>Vor acht tagen hab ich briefe von Dauide Fabricio bekommen, darinnen ich sein gantz liebreiches gemut kegen Ew. Gestr. erspure, want ime felschleich (Godt lob) berichtet worden Euw. Gest. obitus. Er thut in seinem schreiben nicht anders alß wen ime sein liebester vatter oder son abgestorben were.</p><p>Dieweil er mir nunmer einen seiner schwege[r] vnd freunde Ludolfs Ridderhusen zu Bremen namkundich gemacht hat, habe ich daher nu gelegenheit, ime dieselbige wochentleich, so offt es nodt es, zusenden.</p><p>Damit ich inn in sodaner betrubnis nicht lange hielte, habe ich stracks volgendes tages ime vber Bremen geschrieben, vnd der sachen anders berichtet, nemlich das ich noch den 14 nouembris briefe von E. Gestr. vnderzeichnet empfangen hette, er solte es dafür halten, das das nicht wurde war sein, mit Gottes hülffe. Vnd kumpt mir frombt vor, das er der briefe keinen (die ich vor dieser seiner anordening zu waßer bej schyppen zu Norden vnd in die grete wonende gegeben habe) vberkommen hat. Die schiffere haben dennoch mir daßelbige veste gelobet. So kunte ich auch anders keine botten haben, dan an den ordt gelangen keine botten. Ich hoffe dennoch er wirdt sie teslich bekommen haben.</p><p>Vber Bremen is henfurt der gewiße pas, weils der Ludolff Ridderhusen es vff sich nimpt, dieselbig vordtzusenden.</p><p>Heut habe ich abermal E. Gest. brieff ime bej meinem eingeslag zugesant, das er abermall ewer wolstandes wirdt mügen confirmiret sein.</p><p>Jmgleichen auch an hern Josephum Scaligerum geschrieben vnd der Ew. Gest. schreiben darneben einen amsterdamisch botten selber in die handt gegeben vnd in dauon bezallt, jn meinem schreiben mir erbotten Ew. Gestr. gerne zu ubersenden all daßjennige, was mir geschicket wurd.</p><p>Was dem conditore nouarum ephemeridum Euerarto zu Bruxell wonende belanget, weis ich nicht woll, ob ich bej im was wurde ausrichten, darumme das die briefe von hinnen durch viendelandt vber Antorff gehen müssen. Vnd müchte man sich der vnkost trosten, so mangellt es mir doch an kundtschafft mit ime. Vnd tesleichen, die warheit zu bekennen, ist es mir gar aus dem sinne kommen. Nu ich aber vormercke, das E. Gest. damit ein will geschieht, so will ichs per tertium vnterlegen. Einer genomet Peter vp Meer, köningkleicher hispanischer radt vnd befellhaber, ist mir familiaris, durch denselbigen meine ich in mein schreiben beizubringen vnd andtwordt zuerhalten, so habe ich einen zu Antorff, genomet Johan tho Westen, bei demseluigen werden diesen brief zwischen Brüßell vnd Hamborch hinc inde eingethan, der soll das bottenloen mir vorlegen.</p><p>Des junckher swester Sophia is alhie zu Wandesbeke. Derselbigen habe ich vff ir beger vollgen lassen j kutswagen mit dem was von zugehorigen darbej, war; jtem noch j radt, welches er sunst mangelte. Daßelbige was dafur sollj will sie E. Gestr. gudt thun. Vorhoffe mich gentzleich daranne keinen vndanch begangen haben, das vbrige wagengerede is vast geringe vnd verdorben gut. Sie konte nicht mer dan ein gudt radt (zu den die bej erster kutsen weren) finden odder aussuchen laßen.</p><p>Aus Dennemarck habe ich nicht sunderlichs, habe auch zwar mit fleis darnach nicht erkundet. Hertzogh Carl hat numer die gantze swedische cron zu seinem willen. Der polnische kuningk haltet dieser sachen halber itzundt oder wirdts bald in Polen halten; was er vornemen wirdt, gibt zeit. Aus Muscouia zeitung, das kuningk Erich aus Sweden, sonn Gustaus, alda bej den großfürsten woll gehalten wirde. Wan es im an furmügen nicht mangelte, wolte er sich inbillden, auch zur krone Sweden zugehoren. Man vormeint aber, der muscowiter wirde mit hertzogk Carl oder mit Sweden den angestelleten friede nicht brechen. Engelant (wie man sagt) tractiret den friede mit denn hispanischen. Dy hollender slagen alle fridesmiddell aus. Einer der fornembste midt vnter denen von Amsterdam hat an einen guten fründt also geschrieben: Wan vnsern staten vnd regenten gegeben wurde eine gantze große oxßenhaut, darauff zuschreiben alle die conditiones pacis, die inen nur gelustede, vnd man inen alle daßelbige nachgeben wolte, so würden sie dennoch den frieden nicht begern. Sie die hollender haben zuuor ein placat (wie sie es nennen) lassen ausghen, darinne sie cauiere vnd idermennichlich steden, stenden vnd potentaten zu gebieten, sich vnterwinden, zu interdicerende die segelation auis Spanien mit getreide. Vnsere schiffe haben sie etlike in Hollant eingebracht, vnd es gestrigis tages beschedt bej den botten alhie angebracht worden, das eines derselbigen schiffe mit vrteil vnd rechte confiscieret worden sey. Die staten vntersthen sich mer hochheit alse wol menniger hoger potentat nicht thun dorfte.</p><p>Magr. Georgius Frobenius mus vff ostern Wandesbech reumen. Die frome stathalterinn hat es iren sone Breden wider abgehandelln, gibt fur, das sie selbest es gebrauchen will. Der vnkostung halber, so Ew Gestr. an gebeuen gethan, folget noch von Keile nen sunderlige erklerung. Will aber vmme das andtwordt bej Frobenio anhalten.</p><p>Die stadthaltersche schreibt mir, das ich mich erkleren müge, ob ich nicht weis, wannen her sie der 5000 thaler soll gewertich sein. Jch habe ir geandtwordet, das E. Gestr. das durch Esche Bilde zuuorrichtende angeordnet haben.</p><p>Jn eile we[i]s ich E. G. mer nicht zuschreiben, one alleine wunsche euch samptt euw. lieben vieltugentreichen husfrouen vnd tochteren allesamptt, wie auch den sonen, gesundtheit vnd wolfartt. Datum eilentz den 12 februarij a. 1600.</p><p>Auch is meine frundtleiche bitte, Ew. Gest. müchten meinen nepotj befolen mir auch zuschreiben vnd seinen progressum vnd zustant zuuormelden. Got gebe, das es ime auch wol ghehe. Ew. Gest. dienstwilliger</p><p>Simon von Petkum.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 188; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Damit das bottenlon Ew. Gest. nicht dobbellt gerechnet werde, gebe ich E. Gest. zuwißen, das ich von hinnen aus vff Leiptßigh, Bremen, Leiden etc. bezale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the stern, noble, and honourable Squire Tycho Brahe, hereditary [lord] of Knudstrup, Restorer of Astronomy, to be delivered into [his] hands at his service. At Prague, or in the region, namely at Görlitz. A[nno] 1600.</b></p><p>God's grace and peace first. Stern, noble, honourable, greatly-favouring Squire Tycho, alongside the offering of my wholly-willing services I cannot withhold from Your Honour that I have on hand 2 letters from <b>Master David Fabricius</b>, which he begged me, most earnestly and dearly, to send you by quite sure messenger. But truly, in all this time, none (who wished to go directly to Prague) has come to me — though among others <b>Dr. Veit Winsheim</b> too gave me some hope. I have therefore resolved, discreetly and in divided fashion (I mean, to hand them on via Leipzig and then via Dresden), to dispatch them to Your Honour rather than keep them longer with me — namely to <b>Leipzig</b>, thence to <b>Mr. Hans Tuchman</b> at <b>Dresden</b>, and so on into Your Honour's hands. Should this not go well, Your Honour should consider whether they might not better reach you <b>via Nuremberg, through Mr. Leonhard Dillher, the imperial factor</b>, so that the trans-shipment happen at one place and not two; of which, Your Honour's advice next.</p><p>What is to be hoped of the <b>ship for the instruments</b>, Your Honour will learn from the letter of <b>Mr. Vincent Möller</b> (who this spring became burgomaster). It seems to me good if the <b>Imperial Majesty would let a small letter go out, on that account, to the honourable Council of Hamburg</b>. The letter could well come down here as soon as the instruments may arrive here.</p><p>Eight days ago I got letters from David Fabricius, in which I perceive his wholly loving heart toward Your Honour; <b>for he had been falsely (God be praised) informed of Your Honour's death</b>. In his letter he behaves no otherwise than as if his dearest father or son had died.</p><p>Since he has now made known to me one of his brothers-in-law and friends, <b>Ludolf Ridderhusen at Bremen</b>, I now have the opportunity to send to him [Fabricius] the same weekly, as often as is needed.</p><p>That I might not hold him long in such grief, I wrote him the very next day via Bremen, and informed him of the matter otherwise — namely that I had received letters signed by Your Honour <b>as late as 14 November</b>; he should hold it that it [the death] will not be true, with God's help. And it seems strange to me that he got none of the letters (which, before this arrangement of his, I gave to water-going skippers living at <b>Norden</b> and in the <b>Krummhörn [Grete]</b>). The skippers nonetheless firmly promised me [delivery]; nor could I have other messengers, for no [land] messengers reach that place. Yet I hope he will have got them by now.</p><p>Via Bremen is henceforth the sure route, since Ludolf Ridderhusen takes it upon himself to forward them.</p><p>Today I have again sent Your Honour's letter to him [Fabricius] in my enclosure, so that he may again be confirmed of your good state.</p><p>Likewise I have written also to <b>Mr. Joseph Scaliger</b>, and besides gave Your Honour's letter into an Amsterdam messenger's own hand, and paid him for it; in my letter I offered to forward gladly to Your Honour everything that may be sent to me.</p><p>As for <b>the maker of new ephemerides, Everart, dwelling at Brussels</b>, I do not well know whether I should accomplish anything with him, because the letters from here must go through enemy-country [the war-zone], via <b>Antwerp</b>. And though one might console oneself about the cost, I yet lack acquaintance with him; and likewise, to confess the truth, it had quite slipped my mind. But now that I perceive Your Honour's will is to have it done, I will arrange it through a third party. One named <b>Peter up Meer, royal Spanish councillor and commander</b>, is familiar to me; through him I mean to convey my letter and obtain an answer; so I have one at Antwerp named <b>Johan tho Westen</b>, by whom these letters between Brussels and Hamburg will be put in, back and forth, [and] who shall advance me the messenger-fee.</p><p>The Squire's sister <b>Sophia is here at Wandsbek</b>. At her request I let her have 1 coach-wagon, with what belonged to it; item, one more wheel, which was otherwise lacking. What it should cost, she will make good to Your Honour. I hope entirely to have committed no ingratitude in this; the rest of the wagon-gear is very paltry and spoiled goods. She could find, or have sought out, no more than one good wheel (besides those that were with the first coach).</p><p>From Denmark I have nothing special, nor indeed have I diligently inquired. <b>Duke Charles now has the whole Swedish crown to his will.</b> The Polish King holds, or will soon hold, [a diet] in Poland on account of this matter; what he will undertake, time will tell. News from <b>Muscovy</b>, that <b>King Erik of Sweden, son of Gustav, is well kept there by the Grand Duke</b> [<i>see note</i>]; if he did not lack means, he would fancy himself to belong to the Swedish crown too. But it is thought the Muscovite will not break the arranged peace with Duke Charles or with Sweden. <b>England</b> (as they say) negotiates peace with the <b>Spanish</b>. The <b>Dutch</b> reject all means of peace. One of the foremost among those of <b>Amsterdam</b> wrote to a good friend thus: <i>\"If our States and regents were given a whole great ox-hide on which to write all the conditions of peace that pleased them, and all of it were granted them, they would still not want peace.\"</i> The Dutch have previously issued a <b>placard</b> (as they call it), in which they presume to command and forbid all cities, estates, and potentates … <b>to interdict the sailing from Spain with grain</b>. Some of our ships they have brought into Holland; and yesterday news came by the messenger here that one of those ships <b>has been confiscated by judgement and law</b>. The States arrogate to themselves more sovereignty than many a high potentate might dare to do.</p><p>Master <b>Georg Frobenius</b> must vacate <b>Wandsbek</b> by Easter. The pious <b>Governess [Statthalterin]</b> has bargained it back from her son <b>Breide [Rantzau]</b>, pretending she wishes to use it herself. As to the cost which Your Honour laid out on buildings, a special declaration follows yet from <b>Kiel</b>; but I will press Frobenius for the answer.</p><p>The Governess writes me that I should declare whether I do not know whence she is to expect the <b>5000 thaler</b>. I have answered her that Your Honour arranged this to be discharged through <b>Eske Bille</b>.</p><p>In haste I know nothing more to write to Your Honour, except only [that] I wish you, together with your dear, very virtuous housewife and daughters all, as also the sons, health and welfare. Dated in haste, the 12th of February, A[nno] 1600.</p><p>Also it is my friendly request that Your Honour might charge <b>my nephew</b> to write to me too, and report his progress and condition. God grant it go well with him too. Your Honour's service-willing,<br><b>Simon von Petkum.</b></p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> So that the messenger-fee be not reckoned to Your Honour twice over, I give Your Honour to know that I pay [it] from here onward via Leipzig, Bremen, Leiden, etc.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Simon von Petkum</b> — Tycho's agent/factor in the Hamburg–Wandsbek region, who managed his northern affairs after the move south — <b>to Tycho</b> at Prague. An original autograph (Basel).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's northern logistics, the death-rumour, and a war bulletin.</b></li><li><b>The death-rumour and Fabricius's grief (links to Riddershusen's letter of 16 Dec 1599).</b> Confirms, from another angle, the <b>false rumour of Tycho's death</b> (winter 1599/1600): <b>David Fabricius</b>, \"falsely informed of Your Honour's death,\" grieves \"as if his dearest father or son had died.\" Petkum reassures him (citing Tycho's letter of 14 Nov). <b>Ludolf Ridderhusen of Bremen</b> — the astrologer-correspondent of <b>brahe-t_15991216001</b> — is here identified as <b>Fabricius's brother-in-law</b> and the new <b>Bremen forwarding-hub</b> for letters to Fabricius. (Both letters — of 16 December 1599 and 12 February 1600 — report the death-rumour and the Fabricius connection — a clean cross-link.)</li><li><b>The forwarding network.</b> Routes Tycho's correspondence via <b>Leipzig → Hans Tuchman at Dresden</b>, or via <b>Nuremberg → Leonhard Dillher (imperial factor)</b>; to Fabricius via <b>Bremen/Ridderhusen</b> and Frisian <b>skippers (Norden, the Krummhörn)</b>; to <b>Scaliger</b> via an <b>Amsterdam messenger</b>.</li><li><b>The instrument-ship.</b> News awaited from <b>Vincent Möller</b> (newly burgomaster of Hamburg) on the <b>ship for the instruments</b>; Petkum suggests <b>an imperial letter to the Hamburg Council</b> to speed it (cf. the Magdeburg/Elbe transport thread, brahe-t_16000405001 etc.).</li><li><b>The Brussels ephemerides-maker.</b> Tycho wants contact with <b>\"Everart,\" a maker of new ephemerides at Brussels</b> — difficult, since letters must cross the <b>Dutch–Spanish war-zone</b> via Antwerp; Petkum will route via <b>Peter up Meer</b> (a Spanish councillor) and <b>Johan tho Westen</b> at Antwerp. (Identity of \"Everart\" not established.)</li><li><b>Sophie Brahe at Wandsbek.</b> Tycho's <b>sister Sophie Brahe</b> is at <b>Wandsbek</b>; Petkum gives her a coach-wagon and a wheel.</li><li><b>The war bulletin.</b> <b>Charles of Sweden</b> holds the whole crown; the Polish diet; a (garbled) report of <b>\"King Erik of Sweden, son of Gustav,\" kept in Muscovy</b> [historically problematic — see note]; <b>England–Spain peace talks</b>; the <b>Dutch refusing peace</b> (the memorable <b>ox-hide</b> quip) and their <b>placard embargoing the Spanish grain-trade</b>, with Hanseatic ships confiscated in Holland. A vivid snapshot of the closing <b>Eighty Years' War / Baltic</b> situation, winter 1600.</li><li><b>Wandsbek estate matters.</b> <b>Georg Frobenius</b> must quit Wandsbek by Easter (the <b>Statthalterin</b> — Heinrich Rantzau's heirs' line — having \"bargained it back\" from her son <b>Breide Rantzau</b>); a cost-declaration for Tycho's buildings pending from <b>Kiel</b>; and the <b>5000 thaler</b> to be discharged through <b>Eske Bille</b>.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Simon von Petkum; <b>David Fabricius</b>; <b>Veit Winsheim</b>; <b>Hans Tuchman</b> (Dresden); <b>Leonhard Dillher</b> (Nuremberg); <b>Vincent Möller</b> (Hamburg burgomaster); <b>Ludolf Ridderhusen</b> (Bremen, Fabricius's brother-in-law); <b>Joseph Scaliger</b>; <b>\"Everart\"</b> (Brussels ephemerides-maker); <b>Peter up Meer</b>; <b>Johan tho Westen</b> (Antwerp); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Tycho's sister); <b>Charles of Sweden</b>; <b>Sigismund III of Poland</b>; <b>\"King Erik son of Gustav\"</b> (in Muscovy); <b>Georg Frobenius</b>; the <b>Statthalterin</b> &amp; <b>Breide Rantzau</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b>. Places: Wandsbek, Hamburg, Prague, Görlitz, Leipzig, Dresden, Nuremberg, Bremen, Norden/Krummhörn, Amsterdam, Brussels, Antwerp, Kiel, Denmark, Sweden, Poland, Muscovy, Holland.</li><li><b>Note — the \"King Erik\" report.</b> <i>\"King Erik of Sweden, son of Gustav … kept by the Grand Duke [of Muscovy]\"</i> is <b>historically problematic</b>: Gustav Vasa's son <b>Erik XIV</b> died in 1577. Petkum is relaying a <b>stale or garbled rumour</b> (possibly a confusion, a pretender, or old news of the deposed line); rendered exactly as the source has it and flagged, per the no-fabrication / no-silent-correction rule.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>den 12 februarij a. 1600</i> — no <i>stylo</i> marker; the Hamburg/Wandsbek region was <b>Old Style</b> in 1600; read <b>12 February 1600 [O.S.]</b> (label gives no N.S.; place <i>s.l.</i>, supplied Wandsbek/Hamburg).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Second closing postscript after the signature \"Simon von Petkum.\", restored from Dreyer XIV p.188 (dropped by the working transcription). NB Dreyer prints this German original in vol XIV (the file's \"vol VIII\" tag points to Tycho's reply).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Low-German-inflected Early New High German is fully legible and construed with confidence; the substance (the <b>death-rumour and Fabricius's grief</b>; the <b>Ridderhusen/Bremen forwarding link</b>; the Leipzig/Dresden/Nuremberg routes; the instrument-ship and Hamburg-Council suggestion; the Brussels-ephemerides contact across the war-zone; <b>Sophie Brahe at Wandsbek</b>; the Swedish/Polish/Muscovy/Dutch–Spanish war bulletin with the ox-hide quip; and the Wandsbek-estate and 5000-thaler/Eske-Bille business) is firmly rendered, with no smoothing of Petkum's idiom. The one explicitly flagged item is the <b>anachronistic \"King Erik son of Gustav\" rumour</b>, reproduced as written.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the entire letter against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.172-174, including every proper name and place (David Fabricius, Veit Winsheim, Leipzig, Dresden, Hans Tuchman, Nuremberg, Leonhard Dillher, Vincent Möller, Hamburg, Ludolf Ridderhusen, Bremen, Norden, Joseph Scaliger, Everart/Brussels, Antwerp, Peter up Meer, Johan tho Westen, Sophia (Tycho's sister) at Wandsbek, Duke Charles, King Erik of Sweden, Georg Frobenius, Breide Rantzau, Kiel, Eske Bille), every number (14 November, 5000 thaler), and the date (12 February 1600). Latin/German source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly throughout — no TEXT_ERROR. The postscript that the packet's apparatus notes was 'restored from Dreyer p.188' (outside this 5-page slice, so not independently re-verified here) is internally consistent between source_text and published_english. One clause on the Dutch grain-shipping placard uses a garbled macaronic German/Latin construction ('cauiere vnd idermennichlich...'); the English smooths this into an ellipsis rather than word-for-word rendering, but the substantive sense (Dutch authorities presuming to command cities, estates, and potentates to interdict grain-sailing from Spain) is preserved — judged not a fidelity defect given the source clause's genuinely garbled syntax. No FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 35 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Simon von Petkum", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000000002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000000002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports holding two letters from David Fabricius and describes a routing plan to forward them via Leipzig and Dresden.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000212001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000217001", "d": "1600-02-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "ck": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Tage Ottesen Thott — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 17 February 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 24 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD TACHONEM THOTT].</p><p>SP. Id equidem erat, quod soror mihi intima, mater tua, charissime nepos, ad me die 24 octobris superioris anni, cum Bredenbergae in Holsatia esset, perscripsit, missis in Daniam ad filium meum Tychonem literis, qui ibi tunc pro auferendis residuis meis jnstrumentis reliquaque supellectile aliquamdiu morabatur et sub initia huius anni, re omni ex voto peracta, huc ad me in Bohemiam reversus est: te nimirum, quamprimum ad quietas sedes in illa tua peregrinatione pervenires, de loco, ubi subsisteres, deque rerum tuarum conditionibus, mihi scripturum, quod te nunc ex matris voluntate atque constantia, a qua te non degeneraturum mihi polliceor, effecisse, perlubenter video. Placet, quod Heidelbergae ob hyemis asperitatem substiteris, donec vere futuro Basilaeam petas. Quamprimum vero de tempore, quo isthuc tibi proficiscendum erit, me edocueris (quod ut mature facias, velim), mittam tibi literas, quibus clarissimo viro d. d. Johanni Jacobo Grynaeo te diligenter commendabo. Nec dubito, quin is vel mea de caussa res tuas sibi curae esse sinat. Est enim is, licet de facie utrinque ignoti simus, mei amantissimus, et bis vel ter, postquam Dania excessi, cultissimas et officij ac humanitatis plenas ad me dedit literas. Quibus etiam respondi, licet ultimae meae, quas Vitebergae nonnihil haerens ad ipsum per tabellarium Danicum Escillum, quem nosti, direxi, mihi ab eodem reportatae sint, quod ipsum domo abfuisse caussaretur. At si nulla commoditas, antequam Heidelberga abieris, mihi denuo scribendi concessa fuerit, fiat id saltem, ubi Basilaeam perveneris; ibique mox dictum illum ecc:mum Grynaeum accedens ipsum ex me officiose saluta. Nec dubito, quin, ubi intellexerit, te meum ex sorore nepotem esse mihique charum, te et res tuas ad optima quaeque suo consilio (a quo etiam te deflecti nolim) sit promoturus. Ante omnia vero pietati, virtuti et bonis literis strenue operam nava, ut cultior in patriam revertaris, et quam maxime tibi a pravo cave sodalitio, quod multos perdidit; et praeceptori tuo recta monenti lubens auschulta. Coetera Deus providebit. Adjunctas ecc:mo illi Paulo Melisso, poetarum nostri seculi antesignano, una cum carminibus, dicta prius ex me salute, porrige, et si quid rescripserit, per oportunitatem mitte. Salutem etiam dic m. Jacobo Christmanno, isthic mathematico et Graecae linguae (uti opinor) professorj, simulque indica, Uraniam nostram, quae me ex Dania huc intra saltus Bohemicos volens comitata est, sub auspiciis et clementissimo favore augustissimi imperatoris Rudolphi secundi hospicium benignum et commodum hic nactam esse; in quo nova suorum sacrorum ponat fundamina, prioribus illis apud Danos non cedentia, nec adeo (uti speramus) fastidita aut lubrica, ut vix repedationem sit subitura. Quod et Pythagoras in suis scite dictis inconsultum duxit. Si res tuae sic tulerint, ut ante reditum in patriam has etiam oras perlustrare non invium fuerit, me hic invise. Neque enim citra rem fuerit, te aulam caesaream, quae Pragae est, atque horum hominum mores spectare.</p><p>Vale cum praeceptore tuo, quem etiam salvere iubeo, quam optime.<br>Dabantur properanter ex arce caesareana Benatica, quinque milliaribus Praga remota, quam nunc indulto jmperatoris cum meis tranquille inhabito, anno 1600 die 17 februarij stilj renovati.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO TAGE THOTT].</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting. This indeed was what my most intimate sister, your mother, dearest nephew, wrote to me on the 24th of October of last year, when she was at Bredenberg in Holstein, in a letter sent into Denmark to my son Tycho — who was then staying there a while to bring away my remaining instruments and the rest of my furniture, and at the beginning of this year, every matter accomplished according to my wish, returned hither to me in Bohemia: namely, that you, as soon as you should reach quiet quarters in that peregrination of yours, would write to me about the place where you were lodging, and about the conditions of your affairs — which I see most gladly you have now done, in accordance with your mother's will and constancy, from which I promise myself you will not degenerate. I am pleased that you have halted at Heidelberg on account of the harshness of winter, until in the coming spring you make for Basel. As soon as you inform me of the time when you must set out thither — which I should wish you to do in good time — I will send you a letter by which I will diligently commend you to the most renowned man Lord Doctor Johann Jacob Grynaeus. Nor do I doubt that he, even for my sake, will make your affairs his care. For he, though we are unknown to each other by face, is most loving of me, and two or three times since I left Denmark has sent me most cultivated letters, full of courtesy and humanity; to which I also replied — although my last, which (lingering a while at Wittenberg) I sent him by the Danish courier Eskil, whom you know, was brought back to me by the same, because he pleaded that Grynaeus had been away from home. But if no convenience of writing to me again be granted before you leave Heidelberg, let it at least be done when you reach Basel; and there, approaching that most excellent Grynaeus soon, greet him dutifully on my behalf. Nor do I doubt that, when he understands you are my nephew by my sister and dear to me, he will advance you and your affairs to every best thing by his counsel — from which I should not wish you turned aside. But above all, devote yourself strenuously to piety, virtue, and good letters, that you may return more cultivated to your fatherland; and beware as much as you can of bad company, which has ruined many; and gladly hearken to your tutor when he advises rightly. The rest God will provide. The enclosed [letter], together with the poems, hand to that most excellent <b>Paul Melissus</b>, standard-bearer of the poets of our age, having first given a greeting from me; and if he write anything back, send it at an opportunity. Say a greeting also to Master <b>Jacob Christmann</b>, the mathematician there and (as I think) professor of the Greek tongue; and at the same time indicate that our <b>Urania</b>, who willingly accompanied me from Denmark hither within the Bohemian glades, has, under the auspices and most clement favour of the most august Emperor Rudolf II, found here a kindly and convenient lodging, in which she may lay new foundations of her sacred rites, not inferior to those former ones among the Danes — nor (as we hope) so disdained, or so slippery, that she would scarcely undergo a retreat; which Pythagoras too, in his shrewd sayings, deemed ill-advised. If your affairs so allow that, before your return to the fatherland, it be not out of your way to traverse these regions too, visit me here; for it would not be without profit for you to behold the imperial court, which is at Prague, and the manners of these people.</p><p>Farewell, with your tutor — whom I also bid be greeted — as well as may be.<br>Given in haste from the imperial castle of Benátky, five miles distant from Prague, which I now by the Emperor's indulgence peacefully inhabit with my household; the year 1600, the 17th day of February, of the renewed style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tage Ottesen Thott</b> (1580–1658), Tycho's <b>nephew</b> — <i>\"my nephew by my sister.\"</i> His own letter to Tycho from Heidelberg, 15 January 1600 (brahe-t_16000115001), survives. He was then on his <i>peregrinatio academica</i> (study-tour), wintering at <b>Heidelberg</b> with his travelling-companion Niels Mathiesen Bachendorph before going on to <b>Basel</b>.</li><li><b>His mother = Sophie Brahe.</b> <i>Soror mihi intima, mater tua</i> — Tycho's \"most intimate sister,\" who wrote from <b>Bredenberg in Holstein</b> (24 Oct 1599), is <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (1559–1643), Tycho's learned astronomer-sister; Tage was her son by her first husband, <b>Otte Thott</b> of Eriksholm. The identification is fixed by the companion letter (brahe-t_16000115001), where the mother \"met <b>Erik Lange</b> at Brunswick and made the journey into Holstein\" — Erik Lange being Sophie's second husband (the alchemist).</li><li><b>The commendation to Basel.</b> Tycho promises a letter of recommendation to <b>Johann Jacob Grynaeus</b> (1540–1617), the Basel theologian (rector of the university; cf. brahe-t_15941023001), with whom Tycho corresponded though they never met; the Danish courier <b>Eskil</b> had carried Tycho's last letter to Grynaeus (returned, Grynaeus being away). Tycho's avuncular counsel: piety, virtue, good letters; beware bad company; heed the tutor.</li><li><b>The Heidelberg contacts.</b></li><li><b>Paul Melissus</b> (Schede), \"standard-bearer of the poets of our age\" — Tage is to deliver an enclosed letter and poems to him (the recurring Melissus thread, cf. brahe-t_16000305001, brahe-t_16000329004).</li><li><b>Jacob Christmann</b> (1554–1613), Heidelberg orientalist and astronomer. Tycho calls him \"professor of the <b>Greek</b> tongue (as I think)\" — <i>hedged, and in fact slightly off:</i> Christmann was professor of <b>Hebrew</b>, then the first professor of <b>Arabic</b> at Heidelberg. The hedge (<i>uti opinor</i>) is preserved, the correction noted here (no silent emendation).</li><li><b>The Urania passage (the letter's eloquent heart).</b> Tycho bids Tage tell Christmann that <b>\"our Urania\"</b> — the Muse of astronomy — \"<b>who willingly accompanied me from Denmark hither within the Bohemian glades</b>,\" has under <b>Rudolf II's</b> favour found \"a kindly and convenient lodging, in which she may lay new foundations of her sacred rites, not inferior to those former ones among the Danes\" — and (Tycho hopes) not so \"disdained or slippery\" that she must again <b>retreat</b> (<i>repedatio</i>), \"which <b>Pythagoras</b> too, in his shrewd sayings, deemed ill-advised\" (the Pythagorean precept against turning back / retracing one's steps). A poignant figure for the transplantation of his astronomy from Hven to Bohemia.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Tage Thott; <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (his mother, Tycho's sister); Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger); <b>Johann Jacob Grynaeus</b>; the courier <b>Eskil</b>; <b>Paul Melissus</b>; <b>Jacob Christmann</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Pythagoras</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 17 Februarij stylo renovato anni 1600</i> — <b>New Style</b> (imperial); the note <i>quinque milliaribus Praga remota</i> fixes Benátky \"five miles from Prague.\" Header agrees with the label (the recipient's own letter, by contrast, is O.S. Heidelberg — the gap is preserved across the pair).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — warm, fully legible Latin, read securely; the occasion (the nephew Tage Thott's study-tour, the promised commendation to Grynaeus at Basel, the Heidelberg errands to Melissus and Christmann, the Urania-transplanted-to-Bohemia passage) is clear and corpus-corroborated, and the mother is securely identified as Sophie Brahe. The one slip — Christmann as \"Greek\" professor — is Tycho's own, hedged in the source and flagged, not smoothed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.170-173 as page images. Letter no. 177, '[TYCHO BRAHE AD TACHONEM THOTT]', runs from the header on p.171 through 'anno 1600 die 17 februarij ftilj renovati' on p.172 (pp.170 and 173 belong to the flanking Vibe and Petkum letters). Collated the entire letter word-for-word against the page images: the opening date reference (24 October), all proper names (Grynaeus, Eskil the courier, Paul Melissus, Jacob Christmann, Urania personified), the number of miles (5, from Benatky to Prague), and the closing date (17 February 1600, new style) all match exactly with no discrepancy between source_text, Dreyer, and the published translation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 24 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Tage Ottesen Thott", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000115001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000115001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to his nephew's letter, reports his son's return with instruments from Denmark, and plans to send a recommendation for Tage's stay in Basel.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000217001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000302001", "d": "1600-03-02", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Wolfgang Franzius", "ck": "Wolfgang Franzius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Wolfgang Franzius → Tycho Brahe — Wittenberg, Laetare Sunday [2 March] 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET GENEROSO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, ASTRONOMORUM NOSTRI SECULI PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET PATRONO DILECTISSIMO.</p><p>S. P. D.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS et Generose Domine TYCHO, patrone observandissime, ex litteris, quas proxime per M. CHRISTIANUM misi, Generositati Vestrae innotescere potuit, quo in statu sint res Transylvani, qui ad Generositatem Vestram excurrerat. Petijt is interim aliquoties a me, ut eum apud Generositatem Vestram humiliter excusarem. Idoneum alium nullum possum exquirere, ut ut percuperem. Forsan autem in viciniâ vestrâ aliquis oblatus est.</p><p>Benedictus sit Dominus Deus noster, qui iam tandem Angelo percutienti iuga Bohemica ex misericordiâ suâ frenum iniecit. Is clementissime Halcyonia et aërem saluberrimum unâ cum longâ vitâ Generositati Vestrae concedat, ut in emolumentum publicorum studiorum et ad gloriam nominis divini omnia illa, quae feliciter coepit, ad exoptatissimum finem perducere possit. Speramus etiam rabiem Theologicam interim ibi nonnihil deferbuisse. Etsi vero nihil scriptione magnopere dignum nunc temporis habebam, tamen D. D. IESSENIUM, communem amicum nostrum, tanquam planetam ab Eclipticâ Witebergensi nunc iterum in latitudinem meridionalem Bohemicam excurrentem, sine aliquibus meis ad Generositatem Vestram pervenire nolui. Commendo Generositatem Vestram cum omnibus eidem addictis divinae protectioni fideliter.</p><p>Witebergae dominicâ Laetare, non secundum Lilianos, sed secundum Iulianos fastos, licet nunc concurrant in paucis, anno Christi 1600, quem tamen praeter IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM etiam BARTHOLOMEUS SCULTETUS 1601 esse comprobat.<br>G. V. studiosiss.<br>WOLFG. FRANZIUS</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and generous man, Master Tycho Brahe, prince of the astronomers of our age, lord and most beloved patron.</b></p><p>Hearty greeting.</p><p>Illustrious and generous Master Tycho, most observed patron: from the letters which I lately sent through Master Christian [Longomontanus], it could become known to Your Generosity in what state are the affairs of the Transylvanian who had run off to Your Generosity. He has meanwhile asked me several times to excuse him humbly with Your Generosity. I can seek out no other suitable [man], however much I might wish to. But perhaps in your vicinity someone has presented himself.</p><p>Blessed be the Lord our God, who has now at last, out of his mercy, cast a bridle on the Angel smiting the Bohemian regions. May He most clemently grant Halcyon days and most healthful air, together with long life, to Your Generosity, so that — to the profit of public studies and to the glory of the divine name — you may be able to bring all those things which you have happily begun to a most-wished-for end. We hope, too, that the Theological frenzy has meanwhile cooled down somewhat there. And although I had nothing now greatly worth the writing, yet I did not wish Doctor Jessenius, our common friend — like a planet now again running out from the Wittenberg Ecliptic into Bohemian southern latitude — to reach Your Generosity without some lines of mine. I faithfully commend Your Generosity, with all those devoted to you, to the divine protection.</p><p>Wittenberg, on Laetare Sunday — not according to the Lilian [Gregorian], but according to the Julian calendar, though they now coincide in but few [points] — in the year of Christ 1600, which, however, besides Joseph Scaliger, Bartholomäus Scultetus too proves to be 1601.<br>Most zealous of Your Generosity,<br>Wolfgang Franzius.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Wolfgang Franz(ius)</b> (1564–1628), theologian at <b>Wittenberg</b>; a learned, witty letter from Tycho's Saxon circle.</li><li><b>The Transylvanian.</b> <i>res Transylvani, qui ad Generositatem Vestram excurrerat</i> — an unnamed <b>Transylvanian</b> who had \"run off\" to Tycho and whom Franzius cannot replace; he asks Tycho to excuse him. (Background to Tycho's shifting household of assistants.) <i>M. Christianus</i> = <b>Longomontanus</b>, the earlier courier.</li><li><b>The plague lifting.</b> <i>Angelo percutienti iuga Bohemica … frenum iniecit</i> — \"[God] has cast a bridle on the <b>Angel smiting the Bohemian regions</b>\" (the plague-angel; <i>iuga</i> = \"ridges/yokes,\" here the lands of Bohemia): the <b>abatement of the Bohemian plague</b> of 1599–1600 (cf. the plague at Benátky in brahe-t_15991214001). <i>Halcyonia</i> = \"halcyon [calm] days.\" <i>rabies Theologica</i> = the \"Theological frenzy\" (the confessional/dogmatic controversies) — hoped to have cooled.</li><li><b>Jessenius as a \"planet.\"</b> <i>D. D. Iessenium … tanquam planetam ab Ecliptica Witebergensi … in latitudinem meridionalem Bohemicam excurrentem</i> — a charming astronomical conceit for <b>Jan Jessenius</b> (Jan Jesenský, 1566–1621), the anatomist and Tycho's friend (later author of Tycho's funeral oration), here \"running south,\" like a planet leaving the ecliptic, <b>from Wittenberg to Bohemia</b> — and carrying this very letter.</li><li><b>⚠ Calendar — and the year-number dispute.</b> Franzius dates pointedly <i>dominicâ Laetare … non secundum Lilianos, sed secundum Iulianos fastos</i> — \"on <b>Laetare Sunday</b> (the 4th of Lent), by the <b>Julian</b>, not the <b>Lilian</b> [Gregorian, after Aloysius Lilius] calendar.\" In 1600 (Julian) Easter fell on 23 March O.S.; <b>Laetare Sunday = 2 March 1600 O.S.</b>, matching the slug. His \"they now coincide in but few [points]\" is a learned aside on the two calendars. He adds the <b>century-numbering controversy</b>: that the year \"is 1601\" according to <b>Joseph Scaliger</b> and <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (the Görlitz mathematician) — i.e. the debate over whether 1600 or 1601 completed the century. All preserved, not \"corrected.\" Wittenberg (Lutheran) = O.S.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the persons (Franzius, Longomontanus, Jessenius), the conceits (the plague-angel bridled, Jessenius the \"planet\"), and the elaborate calendar/year dating (Laetare Sunday = 2 March 1600 O.S.; the Lilian-vs-Julian and Scaliger/Scultetus 1600-vs-1601 notes) are all clear and corpus-/history-consistent and reproduced without sharpening.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.263-266 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.264-265, text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Jessenius, Joseph Scaliger, Bartholomaeus Scultetus) and the calendar dispute (Julian dating giving 1600 vs. Scultetus's 1601) all correctly rendered. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Wolfgang Franzius", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000327002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000327002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that the runaway Transylvanian has asked forgiveness, wishes Tycho good health after plague in Bohemia, and hopes theological disputes have subsided.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000302001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000304001", "d": "1600-03-04", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Bologna", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Bologna, 4 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record (printed source): G. A. Magini,<i> Tabulae primi mobilis </i>· MS witness: A = Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Magini</b> (at <b>Bologna</b>) <i>to</i> Tycho (then at Benátky — hence the catalogue label \"Benatky,\" which names the <i>recipient's</i> seat, not the place of writing). Magini's reply to Tycho's letter of 3 January 1600 (<b>brahe-t_16000103002</b>); it <b>originates the Bernardino Baldi plan</b> that Tycho approves in <b>brahe-t_16010124001 (24 January 1601)</b>.</p>", "la": "<p>IOHANNES ANTONIUS MAGINUS AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>TUAS ad me literas 3° Ianuarij datas et mihi opera Clarissimi Domini IOANNIS FRANCISCI SAGREDI Patricij Veneti et amicissimi mei redditas accepi die 22 Februarij. Eae mirum in modum, et quantum dici vix potest, gratae fuere; sed ijs respondere statim non potui ob praematurum tabellarij discessum; nactusque otium integrae hebdomadis ad te pluribus quam umquam antea scribendum putavi. Tuum illud praecipuum negotium mihi tanta fiducia mandatum pro eo ac debeo curabo, nihilque praetermittam, quod ad dignitatem tuam pertinere videatur. Res est maximi ponderis, proinde haud negligenter tractanda, sed consideranda accuratissime. Huc usque, ut fatear ingenue, ut etiam tu ipse affirmas, non aliam tibi operam dedi, nisi ut vertendas Latine curarem epistolas illas Germanice scriptas, quae ad octo sunt, quod mihi praestitit Germanus quidam medicinae studiosus, idque in gratiam Transylvani cuiusdam mei auditoris: ubi diu laborassem in inveniendo eius rei interprete, propterea quod nullum familiariter nossem eorum, qui Bononiae sunt e natione Germanica, qui perpauci sunt. Ac certe tum domesticis, tum scholasticis et publicis et privatis negotijs, tum valetudine parum firma impeditus, (cum distillatione laborarim dies complures huiusce hiemis culpa; quae omnium quas umquam meminerim inclementissima huc usque fuit ob nivium incredibilem vim omnia obsidentium, ut vix aliud agere, quam ad ignem pellendo frigori sedere potuerim, libellum aliquem ibi incommode legendo) etsi maxime voluissem, non multum tamen in rebus Dominationis tuae promovere potuissem. Sed deinceps mitescente caelo, spero me non poenitendam operam tibi navaturum. Primum etenim ex omnibus hisce scriptis excerpam brevia aliquot capita et quasi summam conficiam ad facile alios edocendos idoneam, cuius plura exempla ubi fecero, ad varios amicos transmittam.</p><p>Assentior tibi de narratione rerum tuarum non in dialogo facienda sed occasione panegyrici. Sed venit mihi tamen in mentem quod rationi nostrae multo sit conducibilius atque aptius. Intelligo BERNARDINUM BALDUM Urbinatem Guastallae Abbatem, qui olim edidit HERONIS opus de machinis se movendis e Graeco a se ipso translatum, multis abhinc annis provinciam suscepisse scribendarum vitarum praestantium Mathematicorum. Hominem quidem de facie non novi, sed tamen scio, cum aliquando Bononia transiret, domum ad me venisse, ut me de facie nosset, ut mihi postea dictum, nam me non invenit. Ex hoc spero, si quid ab eo petam, tamquam a mei studioso, facile impetraturum. Itaque scribere ad eum cogito et cum eo agere, ut vitam tuam diligenter conscribat, et ad me eius historiae exemplum mittat: illud postea cum encomijs tui poëticis coniungere licebit, et qui encomia illa collegerit, idem sibi ab illo eius historiae copiam factam dicere poterit. Sed is tamen prius diligenter de omnibus rebus, quae argumenti eius erunt, edocendus est. Ego quaecumque huc spectantia colligere potero, non praetermittam; sed bimestre spatium pusillum est, quando ij, quorum opera indigemus, suas quoque habent occupationes, quibus distinentur. Hoc mihi in huiusmodi negotio optimum consilium videbatur: quod meum tamen iudicium tuae prudentiae permitto.</p><p>Eclipsin Solarem anni 1598 instrumentis mathematicis non observavi, cum hîc prae manibus non haberem idoneum instrumentum. Horologio tamen ex pulvere, quod quadrantes horarum indicabat, accomodato ad horam exortus Solis, percepi eclipsin ipsam tardiorem fuisse hora pene una, quam prodat calculus Prutenicus, et eius magnitudo novem puncta non excessit, quamvis expectaretur hîc maior obscuratio. Hoc idem et Romae et Patavij observatum fuit, scilicet tardiorem fuisse ad integram fere horam et minorem fuisse obscurationem. Exemplar epistolae Illustrissimi Cardinalis SS. QUATTUOR, nepotis felicis memoriae INNOCENTIJ octavi Summi pontificis, in qua mihi de illa eclipsi, prout Romae contingit, historiam narrat, ad te mitto, ut videas, si ex illa quidquam haurire potes, et si tibi bona occasio de illo honorifice loqui patere poterit, mihi gratissimum erit; est enim patronus meus humanissimus, cui maxime debeo ob innumera beneficia in me collata. Nullas praeteritis temporibus feci observationes ob varia impedimenta, cum semper integras epistolas conscripserim apud aliquos Cardinales et Principes, quando scilicet hîc erant a scholis vacationes. Quod tempus maxime huic negotio erat idoneum.</p><p>Laboravi non parum, ut perciperem tuam rationem supputandi Lunarem cursum ex quinternione mihi relicto a D. FRANCISCO TENGNALIO, a pag. 113 incipiente. Notandum vero quod haec omnia, quae de revolutionibus etc. usque ad pag. 120, sed non sine magno labore rem tandem assequutus sum, cum nullum extet exemplum dictae computationis, et cum apud me non sint, quae de octavae sphaerae ac de Solis motu exarasti, tamquam praecedentia hunc Lunarem motum. Unde pro habendo Solis motu ex tuis fundamentis confugi ad Ephemeridem tuam motus Solis ad annum 1591 mihi a te olim liberaliter communicatam, notavique in ea Solem attigisse locum Apogaei, nempe gr. 5. 32' ♋ die 17 Iunij, h. 13,44 p. m., hîcque nulla est differentia inter verum motum et medium: excerpsi deinde motum Solis medium ex meis tabulis congruentem dicto temporis momento et inveni quidem ipsum scr. 2' 52'' minorem, dempta prius a loco ☉ tuo aequinoctiorum praecessione, quam gr. 28 17' 42'' tunc te praesupposuisse arbitratus fui. Quocirca mihi videtur his temporibus posse elicere medium Solis motum ex meis tabulis addendo eidem 2' 52'', ut cum tuis radicibus concordet. Posthaec varia ratione supputavi locum ☾ ex tuis tabulis, idque ad diem 9 Ianuarij anni 1587 atque etiam ad 15m et 16m eiusdem, quibus observasti verum ☾ locum per instrumenta, ut recenses pag. 56 tuarum Epistolarum astronomicarum, cuius libri reliquit mihi folium D. GELLIUS tuus a pag. 49 usque ad 56. At veram supputationis ☾ formam secundum tua placita non recte prius cognovi, quam confugissem ad triangulorum operationes, quibus certior factus fui, te aequationes illas exactissime colligere non curasse, facturum postea id meliori occasione, scilicet in tuo Theatro absolutissimo, cum ex angulo maximae 2i epicicli aequationis gr. 7 41' 15'' a te per observationes deprehenso colligatur recta AD a centro Terrae ad centrum primi Circelli par. 98677 qualium DE datur 1100. Sed ex angulo minimae aequationis perigeae elicitur recta AD 101312. Forsan lato modo pro dictis aequationibus definiendis sumpsisti dictam rectam AD tanquam sinum integrum, quod etiam mihi confirmat variatio centri maxima a te posita m. 45' 20'', cum recta AB semidimetiens scilicet parvi circelli ad mundi centrum sit 1320, qui numerus sinus est m. 45' 20'' vel 45' 24''. Experiri volui, quantam efficeret varietatem semidiameter CD parvi Circelli, super quo moveri statuis centrum primi epicicli, et inveni angulum maximae aequationis primi epicicli gr. 7 27' 43'', centro primi epicicli versante in Apogaeo parvi illius Circelli, statuendo scilicet rectam AD particularum 101640 iuxta tuos numeros. Idem angulus maximae aequationis in Perigaeo illiusmet Circelli prodit gr. 7 30' 35''; different, a priore m. 2' 52''. Sed uterque horum angulorum sensibili differentia ab angulo maximae aequationis a te definito gr. 7. 41¼ discrepat, ut suspicer commensurationes orbium Lunarium a te positas aliqua correctione indigere, praesertim quod videam etiam calculum ex tuis tabulis prodeuntem ad unguem tuismet observationibus non congruere, quod in bonam accipias partem velim, tanquam a sincero et ingenuo viro prodiens, qui zelotypia tui honoris laborat. Ab initio variationem orbis non solum subducebam a medio motu ☾ a Sole, vel eidem addebam iuxta tabulam, sed etiam a medio argumento vel anomalia: sed postea ex calculo triangulorum cognovi te aequationem anomaliae ex duplici illa variatione, quam argumentum medium subit, composuisse, quod mihi valde placuit. Suppositio haec tua Lunaris mirum arguit tuum ingenium et praestantiam: sed maxime praecavendum est, ne numeri ex ipsa derivantes ab observationibus vitio tuorum supputatorum discrepent. Non me latet magnum ac permolestum esse laborem in supputandis hisce aequationum tabulis. Ego tamen, cum compendia quaedam mira in computationibus triangulorum possideam a me nuper excogitata, exiguam persentirem molestiam in his tabulis de novo ad 2a scrupula exactissime construendis. Quod libenter tibi significare volui, ut si mea opera posthac indigueris, libere possis uti, praesertim pro reliquis quinque planetis, quorum unamquamque tabulam in decem diebus perfecte absolverem. Quod tibi incredibile videri non debet. Tabulam enim primi mobilis universalem habeo a me ad singula decem minuta (ad instar illius REGIOMONTANI, quae est ad singulos gradus) supputatam, et alias insuper tabulas, quibus calculationes triangulorum sphaericorum per ingressus laterales absolvo. Et in planis triangulis quando anguli inquirendi sunt, eademmet tabula utor artificio quodam a me invento, quod etiam locum habet in triangulo obliquangulo, in quo, datis duobus lateribus cum angulo ab ijs comprehenso, inquirendi veniunt anguli, qua operatione indigemus tantopere in aequationibus planetarum definiendis.</p><p>Scias profecto me adeo tui esse studiosum et observantissimum, ut nihil aliud cogitem, quam ut tibi me gratum probem. Quin et hoc te non latere volo, tantopere mihi esse in optatis, ut me adhuc vivo prodeat in lucem correctio caelestium motuum, ut nulli parcendum labori existimem, quo id assequi liceret, etsi nihil ex eo ad gloriam profici putem, sed satis habeam fructum illum, ut scilicet mihi tantum usui sint in exercenda Astrologia iudiciaria vulgo dicta, qua mirum in modum delector.</p><p>Ephemeridum mearum unum tantum tomum ab anno 1598 incipientem et in annum 1610 desinentem denuo impressum evulgavi, in quem nihil novi reposui, praeter auctuaria quaedam in Isagogicis, cum compendio directionum secundum viam rationalem, et Quadrante Directorio ad mentem PTOLEMAeI. Exemplar ad te libenter mitterem, si congrua mihi esset occasio; aestate hac proxima alter tomus, viginti feto quentes annos complectens, excudetur; et tunc enixe curabo, ut ad te utriusque tomi Exemplar deferatur.</p><p>Vidi nuper quae contra me inutiliter effutivit DAVID ORIGANUS in editione suarum Ephemeridum, et exactissime cognovi illum unum esse ex illis, qui ex insectandis et censurandis alijs gloriam sibi quaerere conantur; sed poenitebit aliquando se de me quicquam protulisse, ubi ostendero ipsum gravius in suis Ephemeridibus a calculo Prutenico aberrasse, quam a me factum sit, praesertim in postremis illis annis, ultra scilicet 1620, cum meas ibi desinentes Ephemerides non habuerit prae oculis, quod maximo argumento erit, et aperte satis demonstrabit, ipsum plurima ex meis desumpsisse. Pro emersionibus et occultationibus Planetarum ac Fixarum mutuavi arcus visionum a PTOLEMAeO definitos, quos parum a vero recedere in fixis puto, sed in Planetis credo cum STADIO esse varios, penes differentiam ipsorum Planetarum a Terra. Si quid notatu dignum ex tuismet observationibus circa hosce arcus habes, illud mihi humanissime communicare ne graveris; quod te subnixe oro ac deprecor: polliceor enim ingenue, quidquid illud erit, a te recepisse testaturum.</p><p>Vale, vir praeclarissime, et me, ut soles, ama ac addictissimum habe.<br>Bononia 4 Martij MDC.<br>Perillustri Dominationi tuae addictissimus<br>IO. ANTONIUS MAGINUS, Patavinus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES ANTONIUS MAGINUS TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Your letter to me, dated the 3rd of January and delivered me by the agency of the most renowned <b>Lord Giovanni Francesco Sagredo, a Venetian patrician and my dearest friend</b>, I received on the 22nd of February. It was wonderfully — and beyond what can scarcely be said — welcome; but I could not answer it at once, on account of the courier's premature departure; and, having got the leisure of a whole week, I thought I should write you more [fully] than ever before. <b>That chief business of yours, committed to me with such trust, I will care for as I ought, and will omit nothing that may seem to pertain to your dignity. It is a matter of the greatest weight, and so to be treated not negligently, but considered most accurately.</b> Hitherto, to confess frankly (as you yourself also affirm), I have given you no other work than to see those <b>letters written in German — there are about eight — translated into Latin</b>; which a certain German student of medicine performed for me, and that for the sake of a certain Transylvanian hearer of mine — after I had long laboured to find a translator for the matter, because I knew familiarly none of those who are at Bologna of the German nation, who are very few. And certainly, hindered both by domestic and by scholastic and public and private business, and by health none too firm (for I have laboured with a catarrh [<i>distillatio</i>] for many days, by the fault of this winter — which has hitherto been the most inclement of all I can remember, for the incredible force of the snows besieging everything, so that I could scarcely do anything but sit by the fire driving off the cold, inconveniently reading some little book there) — even had I most wished it, I could not have advanced much in Your Lordship's affairs. But hereafter, the weather growing milder, I hope to do you work not to be regretted. For first I will <b>excerpt some brief chapters from all these writings, and make, as it were, a summary fit for easily instructing others</b>; of which, when I have made several copies, I will send them to various friends.</p><p><b>I agree with you that the narration of your affairs should be made not in dialogue, but on the occasion of a panegyric. But there comes to mind, nonetheless, something much more conducive and apt to our plan. I understand that Bernardino Baldi of Urbino, Abbot of Guastalla — who once published Hero's work <i>On Self-Moving Machines</i>, translated by himself from the Greek — undertook, many years ago, the province of writing the Lives of distinguished Mathematicians.</b> I do not indeed know the man by face; but yet I know that, when he once passed through Bologna, he came to my house, to know me by face (as was told me afterward, for he did not find me). From this I hope that, if I ask anything of him, as of a well-wisher of mine, I shall easily obtain it. <b>And so I think to write to him, and to deal with him, that he diligently write your Life, and send me a copy of that history; it can afterward be joined with the poetic encomia of you, and whoever shall have collected those encomia can say that the copy of that history was made for him by [Baldi].</b> But he must first be diligently informed of all the matters which will be his argument. Whatever pertaining hither I can collect, I will not omit; but a two-month space is little, when those whose work we need have their own occupations too, by which they are detained. This seemed to me the best counsel in a business of this kind — which judgement of mine, however, I leave to your prudence.</p><p><b>The Solar Eclipse of the year 1598 I did not observe with mathematical instruments, since I had not here at hand a suitable instrument.</b> Yet, by a sand-clock [<i>horologium ex pulvere</i>] which marked the quarters of hours, adjusted to the hour of the Sun's rising, <b>I perceived that the eclipse itself was later by nearly a whole hour than the Prutenic calculation gives, and its magnitude did not exceed nine points [digits], although a greater obscuration was expected here.</b> This same thing was observed also at Rome and at Padua — namely, that it was later by nearly a whole hour, and the obscuration smaller. <b>A copy of the letter of the most illustrious Cardinal of the Santi Quattro — nephew of Innocent VIII, the Supreme Pontiff of happy memory — in which he narrates to me the history of that eclipse, as it happened at Rome, I send to you</b>, that you may see whether you can draw anything from it; and if a good occasion to speak honourably of him may lie open to you, it will be most welcome to me — for he is my most humane patron, to whom I owe much, for innumerable benefits conferred on me. I made no observations in past times, on account of various impediments, since I always <b>wrote whole letters [stayed?] with certain Cardinals and Princes</b> — namely, when there were here the vacations from the schools; which time was most fit for this business.</p><p><b>I laboured not a little to perceive your method of computing the Lunar course, from the quinternion left me by Lord Franz Tengnagel, beginning at p. 113</b> — and be it noted, all these things which [you wrote] about the revolutions etc., up to p. 120; but not without great labour did I at last attain the matter, since no example of the said computation exists, and since I do not have what you wrote about the eighth sphere and the Sun's motion, as preceding this Lunar motion. Whence, in order to have the Sun's motion from your foundations, <b>I resorted to your Ephemeris of the Sun's motion for the year 1591, formerly liberally communicated to me by you</b>; and I noted in it that the Sun reached the place of the Apogee — namely <b>5° 32′ Cancer [♋], on 17 June, at 13h 44′ p.m.</b> — and here there is no difference between the true motion and the mean. I then excerpted from my tables the Sun's mean motion agreeing with the said moment of time, and found it indeed <b>2′ 52″ less</b>, having first subtracted from your Sun's place the precession of the equinoxes — which I reckoned you then presupposed [to be] <b>28° 17′ 42″</b>. Wherefore it seems to me that for these times I can elicit the Sun's mean motion from my tables by adding 2′ 52″ to it, that it may agree with your roots. After this I computed, by various methods, the Moon's place from your tables — and that for the 9th of January 1587, and also for the 15th and 16th of the same, on which you observed the Moon's true place by instruments, as you recount on p. 56 of your <i>Astronomical Letters</i>, of which book your <b>[Gellius Sascerides]</b> left me a leaf, from p. 49 to 56. <b>But the true form of the Moon's computation, according to your principles, I did not rightly know before I had resorted to triangle-operations</b> — by which I was made the more certain that <b>you did not take care to collect those equations most exactly, [intending] to do that afterward on a better occasion, namely in your most absolute Theatre</b> [the <i>Mechanica</i>/<i>Progymnasmata</i>]; since, from the angle of the maximum equation of the second epicycle — <b>7° 41′ 15″, found by you through observations</b> — there is collected the straight line <b>AD</b>, from the centre of the Earth to the centre of the first little circle, [as] <b>98677 parts</b>, of which <b>DE</b> is given [as] 1100; but from the angle of the minimum perigeal equation there is elicited the straight line AD [as] <b>101312</b>. Perhaps, broadly, for defining the said equations, you took the said straight line AD as the whole sine — which the maximum variation of the centre, posited by you [as] <b>45′ 20″</b>, also confirms to me, since the straight line <b>AB</b> (the semidiameter, namely, of the small circle to the world's centre) is <b>1320</b>, which number is the sine of 45′ 20″ or 45′ 24″. I wished to test how great a variation the semidiameter <b>CD</b> of the small circle — on which you posit the centre of the first epicycle to move — would make; and I found the maximum-equation angle of the first epicycle [to be] <b>7° 27′ 43″</b>, the centre of the first epicycle being at the Apogee of that small circle (positing, namely, the straight line AD of 101640 parts, according to your numbers); the same maximum-equation angle, at the Perigee of that same circle, comes out [as] <b>7° 30′ 35″</b> — differing from the prior by 2′ 52″. <b>But each of these angles differs, by a sensible difference, from the maximum-equation angle defined by you [as] 7° 41¼′ — so that I suspect that the commensurations of the Lunar orbs posited by you need some correction</b>, especially since I see that even the calculation coming out of your tables does not agree to the nail with your own observations — which I would have you take in good part, as proceeding from a sincere and ingenuous man, who labours with jealousy [zeal] for your honour. From the beginning I subtracted the orb-variation not only from the Moon's mean motion from the Sun (or added it, according to the table), but also from the mean argument or anomaly; but afterward, from the triangle-calculation, <b>I learned that you composed the anomaly-equation from that double variation which the mean argument undergoes — which pleased me much. This Lunar supposition of yours wonderfully argues your genius and excellence; but it must most be guarded lest the numbers deriving from it differ from the observations, by the fault of your computers.</b> It does not escape me that it is a great and most troublesome labour to compute these tables of equations. Yet I, since I possess <b>certain wonderful compendia in the computations of triangles, lately devised by me</b>, should feel little trouble in constructing these tables anew, to seconds, most exactly. Which I gladly wished to signify to you, that, if you need my work hereafter, you may freely use it — especially for <b>the remaining five planets, each table of which I would perfectly complete in ten days</b>. Which ought not to seem incredible to you. For I have <b>a universal Table of the primum mobile, computed by me to single ten-minutes</b> (after the likeness of that of Regiomontanus, which is to single degrees), and besides other tables, by which I complete the calculations of spherical triangles by lateral entries; and in plane triangles, when the angles are to be sought, I use the same table, by a certain artifice invented by me — which also holds place in the oblique triangle, in which, two sides being given with the angle comprehended by them, the angles come to be sought — the operation we so greatly need in defining the equations of the planets.</p><p>Know assuredly that I am so zealous and observant of you that I think of nothing else than to prove myself grateful to you. Nay, I would not have this hidden from you: that <b>it is so much in my wishes that the correction of the celestial motions come to light while I am yet alive</b>, that I think no labour is to be spared, by which it might be attained — even though I think nothing is profited from it toward glory, but have fruit enough [in] this: namely, that <b>they be of so much use to me in practising the judicial Astrology, as it is commonly called, in which I wonderfully delight.</b></p><p>Of my <b>Ephemerides</b> I have published anew only one volume, beginning from the year 1598 and ending in the year 1610, into which I put nothing new, except certain augmentations in the Introductory [parts], with a compendium of directions according to the rational way, and a Directory Quadrant after the mind of <b>Ptolemy</b>. I would gladly send you a copy, were a fit occasion mine; this coming summer the second volume, comprising the twenty following years, will be printed; and then I will earnestly take care that a copy of both volumes be delivered to you.</p><p>I saw lately what <b>David Origanus</b> uselessly blurted against me in the edition of his Ephemerides, and I have most exactly recognised that he is one of those who try to seek glory for themselves by attacking and censuring others; but he will someday repent of having put forth anything about me, when I shall show that <b>he erred more gravely, in his Ephemerides, from the Prutenic calculation, than was done by me</b> — especially in those last years, beyond 1620, namely, when he did not have my Ephemerides (ending there) before his eyes; which will be the greatest argument, and will clearly enough demonstrate that he took very much from mine. For the emersions and occultations [risings and settings] of the Planets and Fixed [stars], I have borrowed the <b>vision-arcs defined by Ptolemy</b>, which I think recede little from the truth in the fixed [stars]; but in the Planets I believe, with <b>Stadius</b>, that they are various, according to the difference of the Planets themselves from the Earth. If you have anything worthy of note, from your own observations, about these arcs, do not find it a burden to communicate it to me most humanely — which I earnestly beg and entreat of you; for I frankly promise that I will testify to have received whatever it be from you.</p><p>Farewell, most distinguished man, and love me, as you are wont, and hold [me] most devoted.<br>Bologna, 4 March 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your most illustrious Lordship,<br><b>Giovanni Antonio Magini, of Padua.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Magini</b> (Bologna) → <b>Tycho</b> — the reply to <b>Tycho's letter of 3 January 1600 (brahe-t_16000103002)</b>; survives in Magini's printed <i>Tabulae primi mobilis</i>.</li><li><b>Significance — the origin of the Baldi plan, the 1598 eclipse, and a detailed lunar-theory analysis.</b></li><li><b>★ The Bernardino Baldi proposal (origin).</b> Magini, agreeing that Tycho's <i>Life</i> should come \"on the occasion of a panegyric, not in dialogue,\" <b>proposes commissioning Bernardino Baldi of Urbino (Abbot of Guastalla, translator of Hero's <i>On Self-Moving Machines</i>) to write Tycho's biography for his <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</b></i>, then attaching the poetic encomia — with a cover story that the Life supplied the encomia. <b>This is the proposal Tycho approves in brahe-t_16010124001 (24 January 1601)</b> — the Italian self-monument scheme. Magini has had <b>eight German letters translated into Latin</b> (via a German medical student) toward it; the harsh Bologna winter and his catarrh delayed the rest.</li><li><b>The 1598 solar eclipse.</b> Magini observed it only crudely (a <b>sand-clock</b>, no proper instrument): <b>nearly an hour later than the Prutenic tables, magnitude ~9 digits</b> (as at Rome and Padua). He encloses the <b>Cardinal of the Santi Quattro's</b> account (the Cardinal being \"nephew of Innocent VIII\" and Magini's patron), asking Tycho to speak honourably of him — the thread Tycho takes up in his reply of 24 January 1601.</li><li><b>★ The lunar-theory analysis (the technical core).</b> Magini works painstakingly through <b>Tycho's multi-epicycle lunar model</b> (from the <b>Tengnagel quinternion</b>, pp. 113–120; plus a leaf of the <i>Epistolae</i> left by <b>Gellius Sascerides</b>), reconstructing the solar mean-motion offset (+2′52″; precession 28°17′42″) and the lunar geometry (lines <b>AD, DE, AB, CD</b>; the small circle; the first/second epicycle). He finds the <b>maximum second-epicycle equation 7°41′15″</b> implied by Tycho's observations, but his own re-derivation gives only <b>7°27′43″–7°30′35″</b> — so <b>\"the commensurations of the lunar orbs need some correction,\"</b> the calculation \"not agreeing to the nail with your own observations.\" He praises the model (\"wonderfully argues your genius\") but warns of <b>computers' errors</b>, and <b>offers to recompute all the planetary equation-tables</b> (each in ten days) using his own <b>prosthaphaeretic compendia</b> and his <b>10-minute <i>primum mobile</i> table</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010220001).</li><li><b>Astrology, ephemerides, Origanus, heliacal arcs.</b> Magini wants Tycho's corrected motions chiefly <b>for judicial astrology, \"in which I wonderfully delight\"</b> — not for glory. His <b>Ephemerides</b> (1598–1610 reprint + a coming 20-year volume); his promised refutation of <b>David Origanus</b> (who \"took much from mine\"); and the <b>heliacal vision-arcs</b> (Ptolemy for the fixed stars; variable for planets, \"with Stadius\") — all the matters Tycho addresses in his reply of 24 January 1601.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pairs with <b>brahe-t_16000103002 (the letter answered)</b> and <b>brahe-t_16010124001 (Tycho's reply approving Baldi, 24 January 1601)</b>; the <b>Magini</b> correspondence (brahe-t_16010220001, brahe-t_15981128001); the <b>lunar-theory</b> thread (Herwart, brahe-t_15990917001); the <b>Cardinal SS. Quattro</b> and <b>Origanus</b> threads.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Magini; <b>Giovanni Francesco Sagredo</b>; the <b>German medical student</b> &amp; the <b>Transylvanian hearer</b>; <b>Bernardino Baldi</b> (the <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</i>; his Hero translation); the <b>Cardinal of the Santi Quattro</b> (nephew of <b>Innocent VIII</b>); <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (the quinternion); <b>Gellius Sascerides</b> (the <i>Epistolae</i> leaf); <b>David Origanus</b>; <b>Johannes Stadius</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Regiomontanus</b>; the <i>Mechanica</i>/<i>Progymnasmata</i> (\"your Theatre\"). Places: <b>Bologna</b>, <b>Rome</b>, <b>Padua</b>, <b>Benátky</b> (the recipient). Glyphs ☉ ☾ ♋ reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Bononia 4 Martij MDC</i> → <b>Bologna, 4 March 1600 [N.S.]</b> (Italy New Style). The catalogue <b>label \"Benatky\" names the recipient's seat, not the place of writing</b> (Magini wrote from Bologna) — noted, not a date error.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> 'Intelligo' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.251) restored for the corrupted imperative 'Intellige'; Magini is reporting what he has gathered about Baldi, not instructing Tycho. English corrected from 'Understand that' to 'I understand that'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense, technical Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Baldi <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</i> proposal</b> and the eight-letter translation; the harsh-winter delay; the <b>1598-eclipse sand-clock observation</b> and the Cardinal SS. Quattro's enclosed account; the <b>detailed reconstruction and critique of Tycho's lunar model</b> — the solar-offset derivation, the lettered lunar geometry, the <b>7°41′ vs 7°27′–7°30′ equation-angle discrepancy</b> implying the orbs \"need correction,\" and the offer of prosthaphaeretic recomputation; and the <b>astrology motive, the Ephemerides, the Origanus quarrel, and the heliacal arcs</b>) is firmly and carefully construed. The lunar geometry is rendered literally (the lettered points refer to Tycho's diagram, not reproduced); no textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.249-255 as page images (chunks 1-4, 5-7). Collated the Magini letter proper (pp.250-254) name-by-name, number-by-number against the English: 3 Jan./22 Feb. dates, Sagredi, Bernardino Baldi/Abbot of Guastalla/Hero translation, the 1598 solar eclipse (nearly one hour late, nine points, observed also at Rome and Padua), Cardinal of SS. Quattro/nephew of Innocent VIII, the Tengnagel quinternion pp.113-120, the 1591 solar Ephemeris (5°32' Cancer, 17 June, 13h44m), every lunar-theory figure (2'52\", 28°17'42\", 9/15/16 Jan. 1587, pp.49-56, Gellius Sascerides, 7°41'15\", AD 98677/DE 1100, AD 101312, 45'20\", AB 1320, CD, 7°27'43\", AD 101640, 7°30'35\", 7°41¼'), five planets/ten days, the Regiomontanus-style ten-minute table, the Ephemerides 1598-1610 plus the next twenty years, David Origanus and the year 1620, Ptolemy/Stadius, and the 4 March 1600 dateline — all agree between Dreyer, source_text and the English. One departure: Dreyer's 'Intelligo' vs. our source_text's 'Intellige', which the translation follows without flagging.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the imperative 'Intellige' (Understand!) where Dreyer prints the indicative 'Intelligo' (I gather/understand, governing an accusative-infinitive); the English follows the altered imperative rather than Magini's actual reported statement, changing the sentence from Magini relaying known news to Magini instructing Tycho. Dreyer prints: <i>Intelligo BERNARDINUM BALDUM Urbinatem Guaftallae Abbatem, qui olim edidit HERONIS opus de machinis fe movendis e Graeco a fe ipfo tranflatum, multis abhinc annis provinciam fufcepiffe fcribendarum vitarum praeftantium Mathematicorum. (TBOO VIII p.251)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The single defect was verified directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.251, within the letter's own slice, read pages 1-4), which unambiguously prints 'Intelligo' at the head of the sentence. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici."], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_16000103002", "next": "brahe-t_16010124001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000103002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010124001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges a confidential commission, reports arranging the translation of some eight German letters into Latin, and discusses assistance from a Transylvanian student.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000304001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000305001", "d": "1600-03-05", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Paulus Melissus", "ck": "Paulus Melissus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Heidelberg", "t": "Paulus Melissus (Schede) → Tycho Brahe — Heidelberg, 5 March 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE, IMPERATORIS RUDOLPHI II ASTRONOMO, AMICO S. S. IN AULA IMPERATORIS VEL BENATICA 5 MEILEN VON PRAG.</p><p>SAePIUS de te mihi percontanti, BRAHEE nobilissime, nihil certi potuit significari a gentili tuo, qui heic commoratur studiorum gratiâ. Nam sese intellexisse aiebat, fore ut in Galliam te penetrares, nisi res tuae in aulâ Caesareâ optatissimum sortirentur exitum. Nunc autem vehementer gaudeo, Uranien tuam repperisse locum speculationi commodissimum, idque beneficio munificentissimi Imperatoris RUDOLPHI II. TAUBMANNUS et BRASCHIUS, Poëtae laureati, me submonuerant de quibusdam carminibus in Tychen tuam componendis, quae nunc cum hisce litteris accipies, et cum reliquis amicorum lucubrationibus, si placuerint, coniunges. Antea quoque a DAVIDE CHYTRAeO v. cl. reddita mihi fuerant exempla nonnulla epistolarum inter te et cancellarium Danicum commutatarum, ex quibus statum (vel potius ruinam) rerum tuarum, cum etiamnum in patriâ degeres, plus satis intellexi. Ea CHYTRAeO remisi, ita illo volente.</p><p>De exemplari Astronomiae instauratae, in quo plurimae coloribus depictae machinae visuntur, magnas tibi et ago et habeo gratias: ut et de fasciculo elegantissimorum carminum unâ coniunctorum, quae sanê libenter legi: sed tamen vehementer indolui, rem eo pervenisse, ut sponte exilium susciperes: quod nihilominus tibi ex animi sententiâ successit, fruituro meliore commoditate quam ipsâ in patriâ. Nuper fui adsiduus in manuscriptis quibusdam codicibus, ut viderem, nunquid reperirem antiqui de lapide illo Philosophico, ad quem inveniendum tot labores hactenus frustra desudavere. Investigabo etiam, si forte quid ad nos pervenerit a veteribus de rebus Astronomicis. Hoc enim te maximê expetere video. Fasciculo, quem TAUBMANNUS iussu tuo ad me transmiserat, epistola tua non fuit addita. Hanc unicam nuperrimê abs te exaratam a Dano quodam nobili accepi, cui vicissim hanc meam concredidi. Rectê fecisses, si Pragae mihi quempiam nominasses, cui fasciculum committerem, tibi porro transmittendum. Amicus illic est mihi summus WENCESLAUS LAVINIUS ab OTTENFELDO, medicus excellentissimus, cum quo Lutetiae familiarissimê vixi; ubi tum simul erat illustris Baro CAROLUS a ZEROTINO Moravus; Norimbergae autem est CAeSAR CALANDRINUS, vir ornatissimus, quem adhuc vivere arbitror. Hic litteris transmittendis operam nobis suam haudquaquam denegaturus erit.</p><p>Scripsi haec festinabundus, cum Oda quaepiam ZEROTINO Baroni inscripta ad prelum adornanda esset.</p><p>Tu bene vale et me ama.<br>Dat. Haidelbergae die V. Martij veteris, anno MDC.<br>P. MELISSUS.</p><p>CRAMERUM vellem in Senarijs suis observare<br>quarto loco Iambum, qui etiam in secundo requiritur necessario. Corriget igitur,<br>ubicumque spondeus positus est, si vult imitari HORATIUM et SENECAM in Trag.<br>Produxit syllabam no in Pannoniæ et ge corripuit in Indulge. Et hoc limandum.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and most excellent man Tycho Brahe, astronomer of Emperor Rudolf II, [his] friend, greeting. At the court of the Emperor, or at Benátky, 5 miles from Prague.</b></p><p>When I have rather often inquired about you, most noble Brahe, nothing certain could be told me by your kinsman who is staying here for the sake of his studies. For he said he had understood that you would press on into France, unless your affairs at the imperial court should obtain a most longed-for outcome. But now I greatly rejoice that your Urania has found a place most convenient for observation, and that by the bounty of the most munificent Emperor Rudolf II. Taubmann and Brasch, laureate Poets, had prompted me about certain poems to be composed upon your Tyche; these you will now receive with this letter, and will join, if they please, with the other lucubrations of [your] friends. Earlier too, by the renowned David Chytraeus, some copies of letters exchanged between you and the Danish chancellor had been delivered to me, from which I understood more than enough the state — or rather the ruin — of your affairs, while you still lived in your homeland. These I returned to Chytraeus, he so wishing.</p><p>For the copy of the <i>Astronomia instaurata</i>, in which very many engines [instruments] are seen depicted in colours, I both give and owe you great thanks; as also for the bundle of most elegant poems joined with it, which I read very gladly. Yet I grieved deeply that the matter had come to this, that you should of your own will take up exile — which has, none the less, turned out for you according to your heart's wish, you being about to enjoy a better convenience than in your homeland itself. Lately I have been assiduous over certain manuscript codices, to see whether I might find anything of the ancients about that Philosopher's Stone, to find which so many labours have hitherto sweated in vain. I will also investigate whether perhaps anything has come down to us from the ancients on Astronomical matters; for I see you seek this above all. To the bundle which Taubmann had transmitted to me by your order, your letter was not added. This one [letter], very lately written by you, I received from a certain Danish nobleman, to whom in turn I entrusted this of mine. You would have done rightly had you named me someone at Prague to whom I might commit a bundle, to be passed on to you. I have there a great friend, Wenceslaus Lavinius von Ottenfeld, a most excellent physician, with whom I lived on the most familiar terms at Paris — where at that time was likewise the illustrious Baron Karl von Žerotín, the Moravian; while at Nuremberg is Caesar Calandrinus, a most distinguished man, whom I think to be still living. He will by no means deny us his service in forwarding letters.</p><p>I have written this in haste, since a certain Ode inscribed to Baron Žerotín had to be made ready for the press.</p><p>Fare you well, and love me.<br>Given at Heidelberg, the 5th day of March, Old [Style], in the year 1600.<br>P. Melissus.</p><p>I could wish that Cramerus, in his Senarii, would observe an Iamb in the fourth place, which is also necessarily required in the second. Let him correct, then, wherever a spondee has been set, if he wishes to imitate Horace and Seneca in [his] Tragedy. He has lengthened the syllable \"-no\" in \"Pannoniae,\" and shortened \"-ge\" in \"Indulge.\" And this must be filed smooth.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> humanist letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Paulus Melissus (Paul Schede Melissus)</b> (1539–1602), the celebrated German Neo-Latin poet, imperial <i>poeta laureatus</i> and <b>librarian of the Bibliotheca Palatina at Heidelberg</b> — writing to congratulate Tycho on his settlement under Rudolf II and to send commendatory verse.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, \"astronomer of Emperor Rudolf II\"; the address carries a German routing-tag — <i>am Hof des Kaisers oder zu Benátky, 5 Meilen von Prag</i> (\"at the Emperor's court, or at Benátky, 5 miles from Prague\").</li><li><b>The poems &amp; the verse-economy.</b></li><li><i>Taubmannus</i> = <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b> (1565–1613), Wittenberg poet and professor; <i>Braschius</i> = <b>Brasch</b>, the Rostock poet (cf. brahe-t_15980905001) — both <i>Poetae laureati</i> who prompted Melissus to compose verses <i>in Tychen tuam</i> (\"on your Tyche\" — a pun: Greek <i>Týchē</i> \"Fortune\"/the Muse, ≈ Tycho's name), enclosed here for Tycho's collection of friends' poems.</li><li><i>exemplar Astronomiae instauratae … machinae coloribus depictae</i> = Melissus thanks Tycho for a <b>colour-illustrated copy of the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</b></i> (Wandsbek 1598), with its hand-coloured instrument plates — a notable detail on the deluxe presentation copies.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>gentilis tuus</i> = a <b>kinsman of Tycho's</b> (one of the Brahe nephews then studying in Germany) at Heidelberg.</li><li><i>David Chytraeus</i> = <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600), the Rostock theologian-historian, who had shown Melissus copies of Tycho's letters with the <b>Danish chancellor</b> documenting \"the ruin of your affairs\" before the exile.</li><li><i>Wenceslaus Lavinius ab Ottenfeldo</i> = <b>Václav Lavín z Ottenfeldu</b>, Bohemian physician, Melissus's intimate from his <b>Paris (<i>Lutetia</i>)</b> days, proposed as a Prague relay.</li><li><i>Carolus a Zerotino Moravus</i> = <b>Karel starší ze Žerotína</b> (Karl von Žerotín the Elder, 1564–1636), the Moravian magnate, also known to Melissus from Paris and the dedicatee of an Ode now at press.</li><li><i>Caesar Calandrinus</i> = <b>Cesare Calandrini</b>, at <b>Nuremberg</b>, another possible courier.</li><li><b>The alchemy/antiquity note.</b> Melissus offers to search the Palatina's <b>manuscript codices</b> for ancient lore on the <b>Philosopher's Stone</b> (<i>lapis Philosophicus</i>) and on <b>astronomy</b> — \"for I see you seek this above all,\" a glance at Tycho's alchemical and source-gathering interests.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Heidelberga</i> = Heidelberg; <i>Lutetia</i> = Paris; <i>Norimberga</i> = Nuremberg.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S. S.</i> in the address ≈ <i>salutem [plurimam]</i>; <i>bene vale et me ama</i> → \"fare well, and love me\"; <i>Dat.</i> = <i>Datum</i> → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — explicit Old Style).</b> Dateline <i>die V. Martij <b>veteris</b></i> = \"5 March, <b>Old Style</b>\" — the <b>Calvinist Palatinate kept the Julian calendar</b>; marked expressly <i>veteris [styli]</i>, to be contrasted with Tycho's New-Style Prague datelines. Year <i>MDC</i> = 1600. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A closing paragraph of technical literary criticism, added just before the letter's actual farewell (which the published tail preserves) — the humanist poet Melissus giving Tycho a metrical critique to pass on (or simply reporting his own editorial work) on verses in <i>senarii</i>, the six-foot iambic line used in Latin comedy and tragedy.</li><li><b>The sender.</b> Paulus Melissus (Paul Schede, 1539–1602), the leading German Neo-Latin poet of his generation, court librarian at Heidelberg; his closing lines (in the published tail) mention preparing an ode to Baron Karl von Žerotín for the press at the very moment of writing — i.e., this is a working poet's letter, and the Cramerus critique belongs to that same professional register.</li><li><b>The metrical point, unpacked.</b> In the iambic senarius, feet 2, 4, and 6 traditionally require a true iamb (short-long) rather than a substitutable spondee (long-long); Melissus is telling Cramerus that his fourth foot needs the same strictness already expected of the second, on the model of Horace and Seneca's tragic verse — plus two specific scansion faults (an incorrectly long \"-no\" in \"Pannoniae\"; an incorrectly short \"-ge\" in \"Indulge\").</li><li><b>\"Cramerus\" — not identified in the corpus consulted.</b> No figure of this name surfaced in the Tycho-circle biographical sources searched; he was presumably a minor Neo-Latin poet in Melissus's or Tycho's orbit whose verses (perhaps addressed to Tycho, given the letter's destination) needed polishing before publication. Flagged as an open identification rather than guessed at.</li><li><b>Register note.</b> \"Diatribam\"/technical-prosody vocabulary aside, this passage is a useful reminder that Tycho's correspondence network extended into pure literary humanism — verse composition and correction as a shared court accomplishment, not merely astronomy.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — polished humanist Latin, read securely; the literary network (Melissus, Taubmann, Brasch, Chytraeus) and the proposed couriers (Lavín, Žerotín, Calandrini) are firmly identified, the <i>in Tychen</i> pun and the coloured <i>Mechanica</i> copy are correctly construed, and the explicit <i>veteris styli</i> dating is handled per protocol §3. No crux.</p><p>The prosodic vocabulary (Iambus, spondeus, quarto/secundo loco) is standard classical-grammar terminology and the translation of the technical content is confident. The identity of \"Cramerus\" and the specific poem being corrected (possibly connected to the same Žerotín ode-for-the-press mentioned in the published tail, possibly a separate piece addressed to Tycho or the Pannonian/Hungarian theme suggested by \"Pannoniae\") could not be pinned down from the corpus consulted, which keeps this at B. Upgrade path: search Melissus's published <i>Odae</i> / correspondence editions and Neo-Latin literary-history surveys (e.g. Skovgaard-Petersen &amp; Zeeberg's Nordic Neo-Latin bibliography) for \"Cramerus.\"</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.266-269 as page images; the Melissus-to-Tycho letter runs from p.267 ('ILLUSTRI ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE...') through the top of p.268, ending with the Cramerus postscript; p.266 and the rest of p.268-269 belong to neighbouring letters (Wittich-books letter, and the Fischerus letter) and were not needed. Collated the full Latin text plus postscript word for word against Dreyer: matches source_text exactly, including all proper names (Chytraeus, Taubmannus, Braschius, Wenceslaus Lavinius von Ottenfeld, Carolus a Zerotino, Caesar Calandrinus) and the date (V. Martij veteris, anno MDC). Checked published_english against this verified Latin clause by clause: no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends commendatory poems prompted by Taubmann and Brasch, reports learning of Tycho's difficulties with the Danish chancellor, and notes Tycho's kinsman is studying in Heidelberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000305001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000306001", "d": "1600-03-06", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Friedrich Hoffmann — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 6 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET GENEROSO DOMINO, DOMINO IOHANNI FRIDERICO HOFFMANNO, LIBERO BARONI IN GRUENPUECHEL ET STRECHAU, PRAeFECTO PROVINCIAe DUCATUS STYRIAe ET ARCHIDUCATUS AUSTRIAe, MARSCHALCHO UTROBIQUE HAeREDITARIO, BURGRAVIO IN STEYER NEC NON SAC. CAeS. MAI. AULAe CONSILIARIO ET SERENISSIMI ARCHIDUCIS MAXIMILIANI CUBICULARI PRIMARIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>QUOD Illris Dn° tua Nobilem et Eruditissimum Adolescentem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM et filium meum TYCHONEM non solum adeo comiter et benevole exceperit (neque enim Illris Dnis tuae eximiam humanitatem sibi exhibitam huc reversi satis depraedicare poterant) sed et cultissimis suis literis sincera erga me benevolentiâ refertissimis mihi rescribere non degravata sit, pro eo Illi Dni tuae gratias ago quas possum maximas: si quâ in re mea opella illi unquam usui esse possit, quam lubentissime pro virili relaturus.</p><p>Quae M. KEPLERUS (cuius ingenium et consuetudo mihi perplacent, ut dignum eum censeam, qui ametur et ad optima quaeque promoveatur) oretenus ad me retulit, percepi. Et quantum ad primum attinet, de studioso illo hîc alendo et informando, actutum respondi, me non saltem in hoc, sed longe maioribus Illri Dni tuae pro meo modulo gratificaturum: alterum satis praevidi confici non posse, prius quam expressum ab Aulâ Caesareâ decretum in Cameram mitteretur; quod iam conscriptum esse intellexi. At ubi haereat, me latet. Quia vero a dicto TENGNAGLIO cognovi, quod, cum ante quatriduum Brandeisium concessisset, ut cum Domino Capitaneo quaedam meo nomine loqueretur, eumque illic non repertum Pragae quaesivisset, Illrem quoque Dnnem tuam pauculis alloquutus sit, quodque consilium illud de KEPLERO atque illo alio, cum illud Illi Dni tuae paucis exponeret, eidem placuerit, deque eo ipsamet mecum ulterius loqui velit, dabo operam, ut quamprimum nonnulla, quae pro Caesareâ Mate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, elaboro, confecta fuerint (nam prius hinc abire vix ausim) assumpto mecum M. KEPLERO Pragam excurram, quo apud Illrem Dnnem tuam hac de re, prout ea suaserit, deliberemus et certi aliquid statuamus. Neque enim ipse KEPLERUS Illri Dnone tuâ inconsultâ quicquam concludere voluit. Quod et ego probo, siquidem absque eiusdem consensu et suasu nihil nobis faciendum censeam. Intellexi quidem quodammodo, nonnullas difficultates sese insinuare, quibus tamen (uti spero) ex Illris Dnis tuae prudenti provisu facile occurremus, ijsque medelam adhibebimus. Quare omnia circa hoc negotium, donec cum Illri Dne tuâ praesentes de eo egerimus, differenda arbitramur.</p><p>Quae de Mechanico illo Artifice ab Illri Dne tuâ accersendo antedictus TENGNAGLIUS pariter mihi exposuit, cognitu grata erant. Si eum Illris Dn° tua, ubi accesserit, ad me (uti constituit) amandârit, efficiam, ut in Instrumentis ad observationes ratas idoneo modo fabricandis componendisque instructior ad ipsam redeat. Excogitavi iam nuper quoddam ad varios usus minimo negocio conducens, quodque simul per longinqua itinera commodum sit portatu: idque eam praesertim ob causam, quam TENGNAGLIUS noster Illri Dni tuae secreto aperiet. Praebebit et hoc circuli Sextantem, et non saltem Altitudines et Siderum distantias etiam ultra sextam Coeli partem usque in Quadrantem, imo etiam eo ampliores, quoad intercapedines, solerter et satis subtiliter rimabitur, atque insuper longitudines ac latitudines Planetarum per totum Zodiacum minimo labore et absque taediosâ supputatione; tum quoque ascensiones rectas et declinationes Stellarum non longe ab Aequatore utrinque dissitarum promet. Quin et, quod plus est, earundem a Meridiano Aequatorias distantias pro temporis momento inter observandum perquirendo et subinde examinando suppeditabit: ut de alijs quibusdam minoribus utilitatibus nihil addam. Ubi vero artifex ille advenerit, illi structuram eius proponam, et si confectioni absolvendae idoneus (uti existimo) fuerit, cum Illri tuâ Dnne id, quicquid est, lubens communicabo, atque succinctum et multiplicem eius usum eidem reserabo. Plura nunc non addo, ne Illrem Dnem tuam nimium remorer. Si quae hîc desiderantur, a TENGNAGLIO uberius, si vacârit, percipiet.</p><p>Valeat itaque Illris Dn° tua quam optime et diutissime.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ die 6 Martij A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and generous Lord, Lord Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Free Baron in Grünbüchel and Strechau, Prefect of the Province of the Duchy of Styria and of the Archduchy of Austria, hereditary Marshal of both, Burgrave in Steyr, and also Aulic Councillor of the Sacred Imperial Majesty and First Chamberlain of the Most Serene Archduke Maximilian, his Lord and friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Illustrious and generous man, Lord and most honoured friend.</p><p>That Your Illustrious Lordship received the noble and most learned young man Franz Tengnagel and my son Tycho not only so courteously and kindly (for, returning hither, they could not sufficiently proclaim the remarkable humanity shown them by Your Illustrious Lordship), but also did not find it burdensome to write me back with your most cultivated letter, most full of sincere benevolence toward me — for this I give Your Illustrious Lordship the greatest thanks I can; and if in any matter my poor service can ever be of use to you, I will most gladly repay it to the best of my power.</p><p>What Master <b>Kepler</b> — <b>whose genius and company please me greatly, so that I judge him worthy to be loved and promoted to every best thing</b> — related to me by word of mouth, I have understood. As to the first point, about that <b>student to be maintained and instructed here</b>, I answered at once that I would gratify Your Illustrious Lordship not only in this, but in far greater things, according to my measure. The other [point] I sufficiently foresaw could not be accomplished before an express decree from the Imperial Court were sent to the Chamber — which I understand has now been drawn up, but where it sticks, escapes me. And since I learned from the said Tengnagel that, when four days ago he had gone to <b>Brandeis</b> to speak with the Lord Captain in my name, and, not finding him there, had sought him at Prague, he also addressed Your Illustrious Lordship in a few words — and that <b>that plan about Kepler, and about that other [person]</b>, when he set it out to you in few words, pleased you, and you wish to speak with me further about it yourself — I will take pains that, <b>as soon as certain things I am elaborating for the Imperial Majesty are finished (for I would scarcely dare to leave hence before), I will, taking Master Kepler with me, run over to Prague</b>, that we may deliberate with Your Illustrious Lordship about this, as you shall advise, and settle something certain. For Kepler himself was unwilling to conclude anything without consulting Your Illustrious Lordship — which I too approve, since I judge nothing should be done by us without your consent and advice. I have indeed understood, in a way, that <b>some difficulties insinuate themselves</b>, which however (I hope) we will easily meet by Your Illustrious Lordship's prudent provision, and apply a remedy to them. Wherefore I think all things concerning this business should be deferred until we treat of it, present, with Your Illustrious Lordship.</p><p>What the aforesaid Tengnagel likewise set out to me about <b>that mechanical craftsman to be summoned by Your Illustrious Lordship</b> was pleasant to learn. If Your Illustrious Lordship, when he has come, sends him to me (as you have determined), I will see that he returns to you better instructed in fabricating and assembling, in a suitable manner, <b>instruments for sure observations</b>. I have lately devised <b>something serving various uses with minimal trouble, and at the same time convenient to carry on long journeys</b> — and that especially for the reason which our Tengnagel will secretly disclose to you. This will furnish a <b>Sextant of the circle</b>, and will skilfully and subtly enough search out not only <b>altitudes</b> and the <b>distances of stars</b> even beyond the sixth part of the heaven, up to a Quadrant — nay, even greater than that, as to the intervals — and moreover the <b>longitudes and latitudes of the planets</b> through the whole Zodiac, with minimal labour and without tedious computation; and will also yield the <b>right ascensions and declinations of stars</b> set not far from the Equator on either side. Nay, and (what is more) it will supply their <b>equatorial distances from the Meridian</b> for the moment of observation, by searching out and then examining — to say nothing of certain other smaller utilities. When that craftsman has arrived, I will propose its structure to him; and if he be fit (as I think) to complete the making, I will gladly share it, whatever it is, with Your Illustrious Lordship, and unfold to you its compact and manifold use. I add no more now, lest I detain Your Illustrious Lordship too much. If anything is wanting here, you will learn it more fully from Tengnagel, if you have leisure.</p><p>May Your Illustrious Lordship therefore fare as well and as long as possible.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 6th day of March, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Freiherr von Grünbüchel und Strechau</b> (Landeshauptmann of Styria, Imperial Aulic Councillor; full titulature as in brahe-t_16000126001) — <b>Kepler's patron</b>, in whose Prague house Kepler first lodged.<br>- <b>Significance — the negotiation of Kepler's employment with Tycho.</b> A key letter in the <b>Tycho–Kepler convergence</b> of spring 1600: Tycho responds to proposals Kepler had brought \"by word of mouth,\" brokered through Hoffmann (to whom Tengnagel had spoken at Prague). Two points:<br>  1. <b>A student to be maintained and taught</b> by Tycho — which Tycho readily grants (\"not only this, but far greater things\").<br>  2. <b>\"That plan about Kepler\"</b> — the terms of Kepler's own attachment, requiring <b>an imperial decree to the [Court-]Chamber</b>. Tycho promises that, once his current imperial work is done, <b>he will come to Prague with Kepler to settle matters with Hoffmann</b> — \"for Kepler himself was unwilling to conclude anything without consulting\" his patron. The phrase <i>\"some difficulties insinuate themselves\"</i> discreetly registers the early friction (the quarrel that produced Kepler's apology, brahe-t_16000400001). Tycho's warm verdict — <i>\"Kepler's genius and company please me greatly … worthy to be loved and promoted\"</i> — is notable on the eve of their collaboration.<br>- <b>A new portable instrument (technical; protocol §5.2).</b> Hoffmann is to send Tycho a <b>mechanical craftsman</b> to be trained in instrument-making. Tycho describes a newly <b>devised compact, travel-portable instrument</b> — <i>\"a sextant of the circle\"</i> — that measures <b>altitudes</b> and <b>star-distances up to a quadrant (and beyond)</b>, <b>planetary longitudes and latitudes</b> through the zodiac \"without tedious computation,\" <b>right ascensions and declinations</b> near the equator, and <b>meridian (hour-angle) distances</b> at the moment of observation. The reason for it Tengnagel will \"secretly disclose.\" A valuable record of Tycho's late instrument-invention for the Bohemian establishment.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Hoffmann; <b>Johannes Kepler</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger); the <b>Captain of Brandeis (Brandýs)</b>; the imperial <b>Chamber</b>; Archduke Maximilian (in the titulature); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 6 Martij A. 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the negotiation of Kepler's and the student's terms through Hoffmann, with Tycho's high estimate of Kepler and the plan to settle it at Prague; the mechanical craftsman and the new portable \"sextant of the circle\" with its measuring capabilities) is firmly construed on the §5.2 instrument terms and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Tycho to Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, 6 March 1600) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.254-256 page images. Every proper name and title (Grunbuchel, Strechau, Styria, Austria, Steyr, Maximilian, Franz Tengnagel, Tycho [son], Kepler, Brandeis) and the closing date (6 March 1600) matches Dreyer word for word, including the full instrument-description paragraph (sextant, quadrant, altitudes, longitudes/latitudes, right ascensions, declinations, equatorial distances). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000203001", "next": "brahe-t_16000406001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000203001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Hoffmann for his courteous reception of Tengnagel and his son Tycho, and introduces Kepler by praising his mathematical genius.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000306001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000306002", "d": "1600-03-06", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an anonymous imperial official at Pilsen] — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 6 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM.</p><p>NOBILISSIME ET MAGNIFICE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDE.</p><p>ETSI tam propriâ notitiâ quam relatu familiaris mei DANIELIS FELS, qui aliquandiu Pilsnae apud vos substitit, satis exploratam habeo molem occupationum Mag. tuae, quae parum illi temporis legendis et scribendis epistolis relinquit: non esse tamen illi ingratas meas literas, tanto confidentius mihi persuadeo, quanto minus, urgentibus rebus, a scribendo abstinere possum.</p><p>Mitto ad Mag. tuam Nobilem et egregie doctum Iuvenem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGL, quo de iam antea, cum Pragae essem, cum Maga tua locutus sum, quod nonnulla mihi negotia, quae M. tuae recensebam, apud Reverendissimum et Serenissimum Archiepiscopum et Electorem Coloniensem expediverit. Fuit is primum in Dania meus aliquandiu domesticus, inde meae migrationis comes in Germaniam. Cum Latinam et Gallicam linguam calleret, Italiam etiam adivit, eamque linguam primum in aula Magni Ducis Hetruriae, postea Romae in aula R. Cardinalis a Dietrichstein probe addidicit. Nuper vero inde reversus in animum induxit primo patriam, quae est in extremis Westphaliae finibus, [et] parentes suos invisere, post, ubi huc, favente Numine, redierit, S. Caes. Mti, Domino nostro clementissimo, si Maiestas eius hac ipsum clementiâ dignata fuerit, in ministerijs aulicis, quibuscunque eidem usui esse possit, operam fidelissimam subiectissime offerre. Est in eo, praeter singularem doctrinam studiorumque cum politicorum tum philosophicorum cognitionem, virtutis amor, morum integritas et gratia: quod facile M. tua deprehendet, ubi notitiam eius qualemcumque ceperit. Rogo itaque M. tuam quam officiosissime, uti sibi hunc iuvenem admodum commendatum habeat, consilioque et intercessione suâ ad optima quaeque promoveat. Commisi praeterea illi paucula quaedam de meis negocijs nondum omnino expeditis, quae M. tuae, quando commodum erit, vel in memoriam revocet, vel a me significet. Quae M. tuae ad ea respondere placuerit, huic concredat, si non per ipsum, quem an hac viâ remeaturus sit, incertus sum, tum per antedictum DANIELEM, quem unâ mitto, ad me referenda.</p><p>Valeat Ma tua quam diutissime et felicissime.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareanâ Benaticâ die 6 Martij 1600.<br>Magae Tuae studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to an anonymous [correspondent].</b></p><p>Most noble and magnificent man, lord and friend to be honoured.</p><p>Although, both from my own knowledge and from the report of my familiar Daniel Fels — who has stayed for some while at Pilsen with you — I am well enough acquainted with the mass of Your Magnificence's occupations, which leaves you little time for reading and writing letters, yet that my letters are not unwelcome to you I persuade myself the more confidently, the less, with matters pressing, I can refrain from writing.</p><p>I send to Your Magnificence the noble and excellently learned young man Franz Tengnagel, of whom I already spoke before with Your Magnificence, when I was at Prague — [asking] that he might expedite for me certain matters which I laid before Your Magnificence, with the Most Reverend and Most Serene Archbishop and Elector of Cologne. He was first, in Denmark, for some time a member of my household; thereafter the companion of my migration into Germany. Being well versed in the Latin and French tongues, he went also to Italy, and learned that language thoroughly — first at the court of the Grand Duke of Tuscany, afterward at Rome, at the court of the Reverend Cardinal von Dietrichstein. Lately, however, returned thence, he has resolved first to visit his homeland, which lies in the farthest bounds of Westphalia, and his parents; afterward, when he shall have returned hither, God favouring, to offer most submissively his most faithful service to His Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord — should His Majesty deem him worthy of this clemency — in whatever courtly offices he can be of use. There is in him, besides singular learning and a knowledge of studies both political and philosophical, a love of virtue, integrity of character, and grace — which Your Magnificence will readily perceive, once you have taken any acquaintance of him at all. I therefore ask Your Magnificence most dutifully to hold this young man highly commended, and by your counsel and intercession to advance him to all that is best. I have besides entrusted to him a few small matters concerning my business not yet wholly dispatched, which he may, when convenient, either recall to Your Magnificence's memory or signify on my behalf. Whatever Your Magnificence shall be pleased to answer to these, let him entrust to this man, to be reported back to me — if not through himself (for I am uncertain whether he will return by this route), then through the aforesaid Daniel, whom I send along with him.</p><p>May Your Magnificence fare as long and as happily as possible.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 6th day of March 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Anonymous</b> in the source (<i>ad anonymum</i>): a <b>high imperial official resident at Pilsen (Plzeň)</b>, where Rudolf II's court had withdrawn — addressed throughout as <i>Magnificentia tua</i>, \"Your Magnificence.\" His identity is not recoverable from the text; he is Tycho's contact for advancing both Tengnagel's suit and Tycho's own pending business at court.</li><li><b>Purpose.</b> A <b>letter of recommendation</b> carrying Franz Tengnagel to the official, doubling as a progress-note on Tycho's affairs.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Franciscus Tengnagl</i> = <b>Franz Gansneb Tengnagel von Camp</b> (c. 1576–1622), Tycho's noble assistant and future son-in-law. The letter is a valuable <b>biographical notice</b> of him: first a member of Tycho's household <i>in Denmark</i>, then companion of Tycho's <b>migration into Germany</b> (1597); skilled in Latin and French; sent to <b>Italy</b>, where he perfected Italian, first at the <b>court of the Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (Ferdinando I de' Medici, Florence), then <b>at Rome in the court of Cardinal von Dietrichstein</b>; now planning to visit his parents in Westphalia before entering imperial service. His earlier errand to the Archbishop-Elector of Cologne is also noted.</li><li><i>Daniel Fels</i> = Tycho's <i>familiaris</i> stationed at <b>Pilsen</b>, his on-the-spot agent and the carrier of the reply.</li><li><i>Reverendissimus et Serenissimus Archiepiscopus et Elector Coloniensis</i> = <b>Ernst of Bavaria, Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b>, with whom Tengnagel had expedited business of Tycho's.</li><li><i>Magnus Dux Hetruriae</i> = the <b>Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (<b>Ferdinando I de' Medici</b>); <i>R. Cardinalis a Dietrichstein</i> = <b>Cardinal Franz Seraph von Dietrichstein</b> (created cardinal 1599), at Rome.</li><li><i>S. Caes. Maiestas, Dominus noster clementissimus</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Benatica arx</i> = Benátky; <i>Pilsna</i> = <b>Plzeň</b> (the court's seat); <i>Westphaliae extrema fines</i> = Tengnagel's Westphalian homeland; <i>Italia, Roma</i> = Italy and Rome.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Valeat … quam diutissime et felicissime</i> → \"fare as long and happily as possible\"; <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary) → \"Given\"; subscription <i>Magae Tuae studiosiss[imus]</i> = <i>Magnificentiae Tuae studiosissimus</i> → \"Most devoted to Your Magnificence.\" Tycho's honorific oscillates between third person (<i>illi … M. tuae</i>) and direct address; the shift is preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 6 Martij 1600</i>; dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b>, consistent with the Benátky/Prague series of spring 1600. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'subsistit' corrected to 'substitit' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 256.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean humanist Latin; the substance (the Tengnagel recommendation, with its unusually full biographical detail, and the named patrons — the Cologne Elector, the Tuscan court, Cardinal Dietrichstein) is secure. The recipient is anonymous in the source itself, not through any reading difficulty; no crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Daniel Fels introduction, the Tengnagel commendation to the Pilsen official, the Cologne Archbishop-Elector business, closing instructions) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.256-257 as page images, with a targeted re-check of the Fels sentence. Translation is accurate and complete throughout. One tense-level corruption survives in our own Latin (subsistit vs Dreyer's substitit); per the brief's rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it is inconsequential to meaning and the English already renders it correctly.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'subsistit' (present tense, 'stays/resides'); Dreyer prints 'substitit' (perfect tense, 'has stayed/remained'). Verified by a targeted close re-read of the page image. The English translation actually matches Dreyer's perfect tense, not our own published source text — i.e. the translator was more faithful to Dreyer than our source_text is. Dreyer prints: <i>qui aliquandiu Pilfnae apud vos fubftitit</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.255-258 page images. Dreyer's 'fubftitit' is unambiguous on p.256. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000120001", "next": "brahe-t_16000308002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends Tengnagel to expedite business at the Cologne electoral court, describing his household background, travels in Italy, and command of Latin and French.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000306002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000308001", "d": "1600-03-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Rudolf Coraduz — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 8 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 35</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD RUDOLPHUM CORRADUCIUM.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET MAGNIFICE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE OBSERVANDISSIME.</p><p>QUANTUM me sollicitum habuit vagus quidam rumor de adversâ Illris Dnis tuae valetudine, tanto maiori gaudio sum affectus, postquam eam salvam et incolumem e longinquo et difficili itinere Italico, legatione perfunctam amplissimâ, in Bohemiam reversam accepi. Quo nomine Deo Opt. maximo gratias ago, eumque precor, ut in posterum quoque Dem Tam incolumem ac florentem Reip. et literis reservet. Non dubito, quin iam antea Do Ta ex alijs cognoverit, quomodo Clementissimae voluntati Sae Caesae Matis, quam per literas mihi, cum adhuc Witebergae subsisterem, Do Ta, priusquam in Italiam iret, significavit, superiori Iulio (siquidem remorae quaedam inopinatae id prius non permiserint) subiectissime paruerim, et postquam huc in Bohemiam veni, in Sae Caesae Matis clientelam benignissime sim receptus. Quare huius rei prolixiori commemoratione apud Dem Tam alias occupatissimam supersedeo.</p><p>Id potius agam, ut Illrem Dem Tam debitâ cum reverentiâ obnixe rogem, ut in eâ benevolentiâ, quâ me nihil tale antea promeritum sic complexa est, ut Sae Caesae Mati (quod et ultro facturam confido) me commendare meaque studia promovere non sit degravata (quamobrem etiam gratias ago quas possum maximas, et acturus sum, quoad vixero) in eâ, inquam, benevolentiâ in posterum quoque persistat, meque, sicubi fuerit opus, auxilio consilioque adiuvet. Nam licet de Caesae Matis, cuius Clementissimum erga me favorem ratis indicijs expertus sum, in me perseveranter fovendo constantiâ, quâ alias Heroice praedita est, nullatenus addubitem, cum tamen ego hîc peregrinus habear, et instituto vitae, studiorum ratione, non minus quam locorum intervallo ab Aulâ vestra sim remotus: facile praevideo, quam multa incidere possint, quae ab Illri De Tuâ, prudentiâ, rerum usu, authoritate, dignitate inprimis pollente, longe melius, quam per me ipsum caveri, et, prout res postulârit, emendari queant. Neque enim possum mihi omnino polliceri, tutiorem me hîc fore, quam antea in Daniâ, ab invidorum morsibus, eorumque, qui sinistre de ijs, quae tracto, studijs loquentur et sentient, oblocutionibus. Etenim si isthic, ubi radices altius et firmius egeram, ac Consanguineorum, qui in praecipuâ dignitate ibidem constituti, non exiguâ manu stipatus et munitus eram, hoc tamen inertiae et invidiae virus amoliri non ita dabatur, quin haec mea studia fastidiret et non ubique obvios plurimorum Annorum labores irritos reddere tentaret, aut (quod potius elegi) me meamque domum universam in peregrinas oras cum magnâ rei, maiori temporis irrecuperabilis iacturâ importunitate suâ migrare faceret: quanto igitur magis huic communi omnium, qui praeter vulgarem consuetudinem aliquid arduum exantlare satagunt, fortunae iam hîc in exteris locis expositus ero, nullâ familiae avitae potentiâ fultus, nisi mihi Deus Opt. max. de fidis, candidis, Dominationisque Tuae similibus Amicis et fautoribus prospexerit, qui, aut quâ dexteritate cavendi, aut quâ lenitate consopiendi, aut quo animo excipiendi sint, si qui erunt eiuscemodi adversarij occulti vel aperti, oportune norint. Quod ut Do Ta tantâ promptitudine faciat, quantum ipsi est ad id faciendum facultatis, atque ut hoc, quod molior, Astronomicum exercitium, huc in honorem Caesaris et Imperij a Tuâ inter primos Dne translatum, tutari etiam et promovere non desinat, id equidem rogarem illam operosius, nisi mihi de praeclarissimâ et spontaneâ eiusdem voluntate iam antea liquido constaret. Quae brevitatis caussâ hîc omisi, quodque ea calamus non facile sustineret, illa Do Tua, ubi vacaverit, oretenus audiet ab exhibitore harum, Nobili et Erudito Iuvene FRANCISCO TENGNAGEL, meo domestico, nuper ex Italiâ ad me redeunte, quem Illrem Dem Tuam iam antea Romae proculdubio vidisse existimo. Qui cum Di Tae, cuius est observantissimus, conditiones suas, et quid maxime optet, exponere cupiat, eius caussâ Dem Tuam etiam atque etiam quam officiosissime rogo, uti pariter et illum suâ benevolentiâ complectatur et ad optima quaeque promoveat. Quicquid in ipsum beneficij contulerit, id veluti mihi quoque praestitum perpetuo gratâ mente agnoscam.</p><p>Valeat Illris Dno tua quam diutissime et felicissime, et hanc meam in scribendo audaciam ac prolixitatem pro suo candore sincere interpretetur.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ 8 Martij 1600.<br>Illri Doi Tuae deditiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>Mitto per prædictum TENGNAGLIUM duos libros ex meis unâ cum Globo quodam Cœlesti<br>ad restitutionem stellarum a me factam accurate elaboratum. Quæ ut Illris Dno Tua hac vice, donec plura in<br>hoc studiorum genere ad colophonem, volente Numine, diduxero, boni consulat,<br>reverenter oro.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO RUDOLF CORADUZ.</b></p><p>Illustrious and magnificent man, Lord and friend most to be respected.</p><p>As much as a certain vague rumour of Your Illustrious Lordship's ill health held me anxious, with so much greater joy I was filled when I learned that you — safe and unharmed, having discharged a most ample legation — had returned to Bohemia from the long and difficult Italian journey. On which account I give thanks to God Best and Greatest, and pray him that in future too he preserve Your Lordship unharmed and flourishing, for the Commonwealth and for letters. I do not doubt that Your Lordship has already learned from others how, <b>last July</b> (since certain unexpected delays did not permit it earlier), I most submissively obeyed the most clement will of the Sacred Imperial Majesty — which Your Lordship had signified to me by letter, while I still stayed at <b>Wittenberg</b>, before you went into Italy — and how, after I came hither into Bohemia, I was most kindly received into the Sacred Imperial Majesty's clientage. Wherefore I refrain from a longer recollection of this before Your Lordship, otherwise most occupied.</p><p>I will rather do this: that I earnestly ask Your Illustrious Lordship, with due reverence, that — in that benevolence with which you have so embraced me (who had deserved nothing such before), that you did not find it burdensome to <b>commend me to the Sacred Imperial Majesty and promote my studies</b> (which I trust you will also do of your own accord — for which too I give the greatest thanks I can, and will give as long as I live) — in that benevolence, I say, you would also persist hereafter, and assist me with help and counsel wherever there be need. For although I in no way doubt the Imperial Majesty's constancy (with which he is otherwise heroically endowed) in perseveringly fostering me — whose most clement favour I have proved by sure tokens — yet, since <b>I am held here a stranger</b>, removed from your Court by my manner of life and pursuit of studies no less than by the interval of place, I easily foresee how many things may occur which can be guarded against, and (as the matter requires) amended, far better by Your Illustrious Lordship — pre-eminent in prudence, experience, authority, and dignity — than by myself. <b>For I cannot altogether promise myself that I shall be safer here than before in Denmark from the bites of the envious, and the slanders of those who will speak and think ill of the studies I pursue.</b> For if there — where I had struck roots more deeply and firmly, and was surrounded and fortified by no small band of kinsmen established in chief dignity — yet this <b>poison of sloth and envy</b> could not be so removed but that it disdained these my studies, and tried to render void the labours of very many years (not everywhere [easily] met), or (what I rather chose) by its importunity drove me and my whole house to migrate into foreign regions, with great loss of property and greater loss of irrecoverable time: <b>how much more, then, shall I now be exposed, here in foreign places, to this fortune common to all who strive to accomplish something arduous beyond the common run — supported by no power of an ancestral family — unless God Best and Greatest provide me with faithful, candid friends and patrons, like Your Lordship</b>, who may opportunely know with what dexterity such adversaries (hidden or open, if any there be) are to be guarded against, or with what gentleness lulled, or with what spirit met. That Your Lordship would do this with as much readiness as you have ability to do it, and would not cease also to protect and promote this <b>astronomical exercise which I undertake — transferred hither, to the honour of the Emperor and Empire, by Your Lordship among the first</b> — this indeed I would ask of you more laboriously, did not your most excellent and spontaneous goodwill already clearly stand established to me. What I have omitted here for brevity (and what the pen would not easily sustain), Your Lordship, when at leisure, will hear by word of mouth from the bearer of these — the noble and learned young man <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, my servant, lately returning to me from Italy, whom I think Your Illustrious Lordship doubtless already saw at Rome. Since he desires to set out to Your Lordship (of whom he is most respectful) <b>his own conditions, and what he most wishes</b>, for his sake I ask Your Lordship again and again, most dutifully, that you would likewise embrace him with your benevolence and promote him to every best thing. Whatever benefit you confer on him, I will perpetually acknowledge, with grateful mind, as though performed to me too.</p><p>May Your Illustrious Lordship fare as long and happily as possible, and interpret sincerely, after your candour, this my audacity and prolixity in writing.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, 8 March 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious Lordship,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p><p>I send, through the aforesaid Tengnagel, two of my books, together with a certain Celestial Globe accurately worked up according to the restitution of the stars made by me. Which [gifts], for this time — until, the Divine Will permitting, I shall have brought more matters of this kind of study to their conclusion — I reverently beg Your Illustrious Lordship to take in good part.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Rudolf Coraduz (Coraduz)</b>, <b>Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b> — one of Tycho's principal court patrons (the <i>Coraduz/Barvitius</i> axis; cf. brahe-t_16000324001, where Coraduz gives the \"no equal in astronomy\" testimonial; brahe-t_16000813001).</li><li><b>Significance — a patronage letter, and Tycho's eloquent statement of an exile's vulnerability.</b></li><li><b>Thanks &amp; the chain of patronage.</b> Tycho rejoices at Coraduz's safe return from a great <b>Italian legation</b> (after a false rumour of his illness), and recalls that it was <b>Coraduz who, by letter to Tycho at Wittenberg (before going to Italy), conveyed the Emperor's summons</b> — which Tycho obeyed \"last July,\" and was \"received into the Imperial Majesty's clientage.\" (This fixes Coraduz as the channel of the imperial call.)</li><li><b>The request — continued protection against detractors.</b> The heart of the letter is a candid, moving plea. Though he trusts the Emperor's \"heroic constancy,\" Tycho — <i>\"held here a stranger,\"</i> far from the Court \"by my manner of life and pursuit of studies no less than by the interval of place\" — <b>fears he will be no safer \"from the bites of the envious\" than he was in Denmark</b>. There, \"where I had struck roots more deeply … fortified by no small band of kinsmen in chief dignity,\" the <b>\"poison of sloth and envy\"</b> still disdained his work and drove him into exile \"with great loss of property and greater loss of irrecoverable time\"; <b>how much more exposed now, \"supported by no power of an ancestral family,\"</b> unless God grant him \"faithful patrons like Your Lordship\" to guard against, lull, or meet \"adversaries hidden or open.\" A first-rate self-portrait of the imperial exile's precariousness (cf. the same theme in the Ursus letter, brahe-t_16000328001).</li><li><b>Coraduz's role.</b> Tycho credits him as <b>among the first to have \"transferred hither\" the astronomical work \"to the honour of the Emperor and Empire.\"</b></li><li><b>Tengnagel's suit.</b> The bearer, <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (lately back from Italy, whom Coraduz had seen at Rome), wishes to lay before Coraduz <b>his own conditions and wishes</b> — i.e. to advance his own court career (he would marry Tycho's daughter Elisabeth in 1601); Tycho commends him warmly, \"whatever benefit you confer on him, I will acknowledge as done to me.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Rudolf Coraduz; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>. The \"kinsmen in chief dignity\" allude to the Brahe/Bille connection in the Danish Rigsraad.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>8 Martij 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A paragraph preceding the letter's close describing an actual gift-parcel — two of Tycho's own printed books plus a celestial globe embodying his corrected star positions — being carried to the recipient by a named courier, Tengnagel. This is the kind of concrete presentation-copy evidence that anchors the reception history of Tycho's works.</li><li><b>Franz (Frans) Tengnagel.</b> Franciscus Gansneb Tengnagel van Camp (1573–1636), Westphalian nobleman, pupil and assistant in Tycho's household at Prague, engaged at this date to marry Tycho's daughter Elisabeth Brahe (the wedding followed in 1601); later an imperial courtier who managed the Brahe heirs' long dispute over Tycho's observational legacy after his death [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000); Christianson et al., <i>Tycho Brahe and Prague</i> (2002); Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890)].</li><li><b>\"Restitution of the stars.\"</b> <i>Restitutio stellarum</i> is Tycho's own standing technical term for his systematically re-derived, corrected fixed-star catalogue — the basis on which contemporary globe-makers (see also the 1594 Aslakssøn letter in this same batch) were building improved celestial globes; rendered here as \"restitution\" to match established English usage in the secondary literature (Gingerich, Thoren, Mosley).</li><li><b>The recipient, Rudolf Coraduz.</b> Rudolf von Coraduz (Corraduc, Corraducius), who became imperial vice-chancellor after the death of Jakob Kurz von Senftenau in 1594, was one of Tycho's closest contacts within Rudolf II's inner circle at Prague alongside Secretary Johannes Barvitius [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000); Christianson, <i>Tycho Brahe and the Measure of the Heavens</i> (2020)] — explaining the deferential, gift-bearing tone of this letter, written from Benátky in the first months of Tycho's Bohemian exile.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent, rhetorically rich Latin, read securely; the substance (thanks for Coraduz's safe return and his role as channel of the imperial summons; the candid plea for continued protection against envy, with the powerful exile-vulnerability passage; the commendation of Tengnagel's court-suit) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; the dense honorific abbreviations (<i>Do Ta</i>, <i>Illri De Tuâ</i>, <i>Sae Caesae Matis</i>) are silently expanded, the source block keeping them.</p><p>The Latin is clear and formulaic (a standard courtly gift-transmission formula), and every named person in the passage is independently corroborated in the corpus. No corruption or ambiguity of any consequence. Nothing further needed to raise confidence beyond routine collation against the printed Opera Omnia text of this letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.257-259, read via page images covering the slice's pp.256-259) clause-by-clause against source_text and published_english, including the Wittenberg/last-July 1599 timeline, the kinsmen-and-envy passage, the Tengnagel introduction and errand, the closing formula, and the gift postscript (two books + a celestial globe accurately worked to Tycho's star restitution). Every proper name, date, and clause collates exactly against Dreyer's print; the translation is faithful throughout with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error. Confirms the published grade A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Rejoices at Coraduz's safe return from Italy and recounts his own arrival in Bohemia in July upon the Emperor's summons.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000308001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000308002", "d": "1600-03-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed councillor at the Cologne electoral court] — Benátky, 8 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM.</p><p>NOBILISSIME ET EXCELLENTISSIME VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE OBSERVANDISSIME.</p><p>QUOD tam amanter Dno Va, quo tempore Wandesburgi adhuc in Holsatiâ haerebam, humanissimis et pariter doctissimis suis literis me praevenire voluerit, tanto gratius id mihi accidit, quanto Clarissimorum et Praestantissimorum virorum amicitias caeterorum familiaritati semper praeferendas duxi. Quod vero prius ijs a me responsum non sit, Dno Va non meae, quae nulla ad Amicis par pari referendum esse solet, negligentiae, sed locorum intervallo, peregrinationibus meis continuis, et quod vix ulla interim ad Sermam eius Celnem scribendi certa occasio hucusque sese obtulerit, tribuet. Quam nunc per Nobilem et Eruditum Iuvenem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGEL nactus, qui apud vos prius erat et nuper ex Italia reversus iter istuc in Patriam parat, et Sermam eius Celnem, si ullo modo ob viarum et praedonum grassantium pericula licuerit, humillime compellare animum induxit, has gratitudinis erga se meae testes exarare volui, etiam atque etiam rogans, ut Dno Va negocia illa, quae Sermae eius Celni dictus TENGNAGLIUS oretenus (si eam submisse accedere contigerit) vel literis expositurus est, authoritate et intercessione suâ promovere non gravetur. Quae ipsemet (ubi, uti spes est, accesserit) TENGNAGLIUS clare et seorsim insinuare poterit, quo responsum mature nanciscar. Fecerit D. V. mihi hac in parte rem inprimis acceptam, et quibusvis, quae a me proficisci unquam poterunt, officijs vicissim pro virili compensandam.</p><p>Valeat Dno Va quam felicissime.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ die 8 Martij 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to an unnamed [correspondent].</b></p><p>Most noble and most excellent man, lord and most observed friend.</p><p>That Your Lordship was willing to anticipate me so lovingly — at the time when I was still lingering at Wandsbek in Holstein — with your most kind and equally most learned letter, fell to me the more gratefully, the more I have always reckoned the friendships of the most distinguished and excellent men to be preferred to the familiarity of the rest. But that no answer was earlier given them by me, Your Lordship will ascribe not to my negligence — of which there is wont to be none, since among friends like must be returned for like — but to the interval of places, to my continual travels, and to [the fact] that scarcely any sure occasion of writing to his Most Serene Highness has hitherto, meanwhile, offered itself. Which [occasion] I have now obtained through the noble and learned youth Frans Tengnagel — who was formerly with you, and, lately returned from Italy, prepares the journey thither into [his] homeland, and has resolved most humbly to address his Most Serene Highness, if in any way it be permitted, on account of the dangers of the roads and of marauding robbers. [Therefore] I wished to write these [lines] as witnesses of my gratitude toward you, asking again and again that Your Lordship not find it burdensome to promote — by your authority and intercession — those affairs which the said Tengnagel will set forth to his Most Serene Highness by mouth (if it befall him to approach her humbly), or by letters. Which the same Tengnagel (when, as is hoped, he shall have approached) will be able to insinuate clearly and separately, so that I may obtain a timely answer. Your Lordship will have done me, in this matter, a thing especially acceptable, and to be compensated in turn — to the best of my ability — by whatever offices can ever proceed from me.</p><p>May Your Lordship fare most happily.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 8th day of March, 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient — unnamed.</b> The companion of brahe-t_16000309002 (written the next day), to the <b>same Cologne electoral channel</b>: an unnamed <i>most noble and excellent</i> official who had written first to Tycho while he was at <b>Wandsbek in Holstein</b> (1597–98), and who can promote Tycho's affairs \"with his Most Serene Highness\" — i.e. <b>Elector Ernst of Cologne</b>. <i>Identification flagged</i>: not named (the two anonymous Cologne-court letters may be to the same person or to two different councillors).</li><li><b>Tengnagel.</b> <i>Franciscus Tengnagel</i> — \"formerly with you,\" now returned from <b>Italy</b> and bound for his <b>homeland</b>, will approach the Elector \"if the dangers of the roads and of marauding robbers permit,\" and carry Tycho's business by word or letter (cf. brahe-t_16000309002, the same errand).</li><li><b><i>par pari referendum</b></i> — \"like to be returned for like\" (the friendship-reciprocity maxim); <i>praedonum grassantium pericula</i> — \"the dangers of marauding robbers,\" a realistic note on travel in the war-disturbed Empire of 1600.</li><li><b><i>Serma eius Celnis</b></i> = <i>Serenissima eius Celsitudo</i>, \"his Most Serene Highness\" (the Elector). <i>Dno Va</i> = <i>Dominatio Vestra</i>, \"Your Lordship.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ die 8 Martij 1600</i> = <b>8 March 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b> (dataset-tagged <i>n.s.</i>). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the matter (a delayed reply, now sent via Tengnagel, asking the recipient to promote Tycho's affairs with Elector Ernst of Cologne) is clear and dovetails with the companion brahe-t_16000309002 and the Cologne-channel letters. Grade [B] only because the <b>recipient is unidentified</b> (a Cologne-court official), flagged rather than named.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.258-260 page images. Located the letter beginning mid-p.259 ('TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM') after the Corraducius letter's close, running to the closing 'Valeat Dno Va quam felicissime. Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica die 8 Martij 1600.' Collated the full text against published_english including the proper name Franciscus Tengnagel, the place-name Wandesburgi/Wandsbek, and the date 8 March 1600; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000306002", "next": "brahe-t_16000329001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for a delayed reply, attributing it to travel and lack of a reliable courier, and sends Tengnagel to petition the Elector of Cologne.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000308002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000309001", "d": "1600-03-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jaques Bongars", "ck": "Jaques Bongars", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacques Bongars — Benátky, 9 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO, MAGNIFICO ET NOBILI VIRO, DOMINO I. BONGARSIO, SERENISSIMI ET POTENTISSIMI GALLIARUM REGIS APUD GERMANIAe PRINCIPES LEGATO DIGNISSIMO, AMICO SUO VETERI ET INPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>MAGNIFICE ET NOBILISSIME VIR.</p><p>ETSI sub finem superioris Anni per Magistrum quendam natione Danum, Consobrinum meum, CLAUDIUM BILLEUM, in Gallias comitantem et Francofurtum (uti statuerat) transeuntem, ad te literas dederim, ac de quibusdam negocijs commonefecerim, cum tamen hic Nobilis et Eruditus Iuvenis FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS, meus domesticus meaeque e Dania emigrationis Comes, ex Italiâ nuper ad me reversus ad Nundinas Francofurtenses ire cogitet, ut inde in Comitatu mercatorum tutius Patriam suam, quae est in extremae Westphaliae finibus, accedere atque Parentes invisere queat, postmodum, favente Numine, huc reversurus: non intermittendum duxi, quin unâ cum eodem (qui rerum mearum apprime gnarus est, easque plenius ad te referre poterit) denuo scriberem. Nec tamen ea, quae de Privilegio a Sermo et Potentissimo Galliarum Rege impetrando, deque debito Optimae memoriae DANZAeI nobis solvendo tunc temporis rogavi, iterum pluribus inculcare lubet, cum de tuâ erga me voluntate tam in his quam alijs nihil addubitem, et praedictus TENGNAGLIUS haec ipsa oretenus promptius expositurus sit. Id saltem hac vice officiose rogatum cuperem, ut de quâdam aliâ re, quam is secreto tuae fidei ex me committet, quid fieri posse vel secus existimaveris, dispicias, quo is tam de hac quam coeteris, ubi se huc receperit, mihi referre queat. Quem etiam, prout amicitiâ non indignum experieris, ut tuâ benevolentiâ complectaris, quaeso. Plura non addo. Neque etiam calamus omnia tuto sustinet. Sed is instar vivae Epistolae omnia rectius expediet.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ die 9 Martij 1600. Mae<br>Tuae studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous, magnificent, and noble man, Master J. Bongars, most worthy Legate of the Most Serene and Most Powerful King of the Gauls [of France] among the Princes of Germany, his old friend and especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Magnificent and most noble man.</p><p>Although toward the end of last year I gave you letters — through a certain Master, a Dane by nation, my cousin Claude Bille, who was accompanying [travellers] into France and passing through Frankfurt (as he had determined) — and reminded [you] about certain affairs: yet, since here the noble and learned youth Frans Tengnagel, my domestic and the companion of my emigration from Denmark, lately returned to me from Italy, thinks of going to the Frankfurt fair, so that thence, in the company of merchants, he may the more safely approach his homeland (which is on the borders of farthest Westphalia) and visit his parents — afterward, the Deity favouring, to return hither — I judged it ought not to be omitted to write again, together with the same (who is exceedingly well-versed in my affairs, and will be able to report them to you more fully). Nor, however, does it please me to inculcate again, at greater length, those things which I then asked — about the Privilege to be obtained from the Most Serene and Most Powerful King of the Gauls, and about the debt of [Charles de] Dançay, of best memory, to be paid to us — since I doubt nothing of your goodwill toward me, both in these and in other [matters], and the aforesaid Tengnagel will more readily set forth these very things by mouth. This only I would this time dutifully ask of you: that, concerning a certain other matter, which he will secretly commit to your good faith on my behalf, you consider what can — or cannot — be done, so that he may report to me both about this and about the rest, when he returns hither. And I beg you to embrace him with your benevolence, as you will find him not unworthy of friendship. I add no more — nor indeed does the pen safely bear everything; but he, like a living letter, will more rightly despatch everything.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 9th day of March, 1600.<br>Most zealous of Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacques Bongars</b> (Bongarsius, 1554–1612), here in his diplomatic office: <b>Legate of the King of France (Henri IV) to the Princes of Germany</b> (cf. brahe-t_15990501001, …16000501001, the Frankfurt book-trade letters). An \"old friend.\"</li><li><b>The two standing requests.</b> Tycho recalls (from his year-end letter, carried by his cousin <b>Claude Bille</b> [<i>Claudius Billeus</i>], a Dane bound for France via Frankfurt) two matters he had asked Bongars to advance: (1) a <b>printing/sales Privilege to be obtained from the King of France</b> for his works; and (2) <b>\"the debt of Dançay, of best memory, to be paid to us\"</b> — i.e. a sum connected with the late <b>Charles de Dançay</b> (Danzaeus, d. 1589), the long-serving <b>French ambassador to Denmark</b> and Tycho's friend and patron. A third, confidential matter Tengnagel will convey orally and \"secretly.\"</li><li><b>Tengnagel.</b> <i>Franciscus Tengnaglius, meus domesticus meaeque e Dania emigrationis Comes</i> — Frans Tengnagel, \"the companion of my emigration from Denmark,\" back from Italy and bound (via the Frankfurt fair, in a merchant convoy, for safety) to his home \"on the borders of <b>farthest Westphalia</b>\" — once more the \"<b>living letter</b>\" (<i>instar vivae Epistolae</i>; cf. brahe-t_16000308002/…0309002). <i>calamus omnia tuto [non] sustinet</i> — \"the pen does not safely bear everything,\" the discretion-topos.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ die 9 Martij 1600</i> = <b>9 March 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b> (same day as the Cologne-channel letter brahe-t_16000309002). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed an extraneous 'te' after 'saltem' in the Bongars letter; Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.260, l.24) has none.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the matters (the French royal Privilege; the Dançay debt; Tengnagel's homeward journey via Frankfurt; the confidential errand) are clear, with Bongars and Tengnagel firmly identified. Grade [B] only for the precise nature of the <b>Dançay debt</b> (a sum connected to the late French ambassador Charles de Dançay), which the source leaves unspecified — flagged, not over-specified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.259-261 as page images. Letter 'GENEROSO, MAGNIFICO ET NOBILI VIRO, DOMINO I. BONGARSIO...' runs entirely on p.260 (Benátky, 9 March 1600). Collated the full Latin against source_text and the English against both: every proper name (Claude Bille/Claudium Billeum, Frans Tengnagel/Franciscus Tengnaglius, Dançay/Danzaei), the itinerary details (Westphalia, the Frankfurt fair), and the closing formula all match and are accurately rendered. One TEXT_ERROR found (an inserted 'te' not in Dreyer's print, discussed above); per the grading rule this caps the grade at C even though it is low-severity and does not mislead a reader. Confirms rather than overturns the pipeline's own self-assigned grade of B, but for a different, more precise reason (a specific verified textual variant, not unverified caution).</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's print (p.260, line 24) lacks the pronoun 'te' that source_text inserts after 'saltem'; without it Dreyer's own Latin is grammatically elliptical (the supine 'rogatum' needs an accusative subject), so the added 'te' looks like a silent editorial restoration rather than a corruption, but it is nonetheless a departure from what Dreyer prints. The English follows source_text and is not itself mistranslated. Dreyer prints: <i>Id saltem hac vice officiose rogatum cuperem, [no 'te']</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.259-261 (full slice, read in one pass); the clause is confirmed verbatim on p.260, l.24. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Jaques Bongars", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15990501001", "next": "brahe-t_16000501001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990501001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000501001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Notes a previous letter sent via cousin Claude Bille and commends Tengnagel, traveling to the Frankfurt fair before returning to his homeland in Westphalia.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000309001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000309002", "d": "1600-03-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed councillor at the Cologne electoral court] — Benátky, 9 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM</p><p>GENEROSE AC MAGNIFICE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE INPRIMIS HONORANDE.</p><p>CUM hic Nobilis et Eruditus Adolescens FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS, quem tua Ma iam antea novit, nuper ex Italiâ huc in Bohemiam ad me reversus, in Patriam suam Parentum invisendorum gratiâ nunc cogitet, et in itinere aut reditu Sermum Electorem Coloniensem, Dominum nostrum Clementissimum, denuo humillime accedere, nisi nimia viarum pericula id vetent, omninô constituerit: idcirco vacuum meis ad Mam tuam literis venire non sum passus, ex quo Mae tuae erga me humanissima officia gratâ mente etiamnum recolam, et perpetuo eorundem meminerim, paria relaturus, si vires sufficiant, is aut occasio ulla sese exhibeat. Licet vero quae ad Mam tuam perscribam, praeter ea, quae is commodius et tutius oretenus exponet, non habeam, tamen Mam tuam hisce officiosissime salutare volui, utque dictum TENGNAGLIUM sibi quam commendatissimum habeat, et utriusque nostrûm negotia apud Sermum Principem, prout antea quoque perlubenter et benevole praestitit, promovere atque expeditionem maturam impetrare non gravetur. Cumque idem TENGNAGLIUS instar vivae Epistolae futurus sit, et tam me quam se ipsum concernentia Mae tuae plene et secreto indicaturus, ulteriore scriptione supersedeo, ac per eundem, quam primum fieri possit, favente Numine, ad me revertentem responsum expecto. Deus opt. max. Mam tuam Reip. et bonis literis quam diutissime prosperam et incolumem servet.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ, quam nunc unâ cum meis inhabito, die 9 Martij 1600.<br>Mae Tuae studiosiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to an unnamed [correspondent].</b></p><p>Generous and magnificent man, lord and especially honoured friend.</p><p>Since the noble and learned youth Frans Tengnagel — whom Your Magnificence already knew before — lately returned hither to me, into Bohemia, from Italy, and now thinks of [going] into his homeland, for the sake of visiting his parents, and has altogether resolved, on the journey or the return, again most humbly to approach the Most Serene Elector of Cologne, our most clement Lord, unless the excessive dangers of the roads forbid it: I therefore have not suffered [him] to come empty of letters from me to Your Magnificence — seeing that I still recall Your Magnificence's most kind offices toward me with grateful mind, and shall perpetually remember them, intending to render the like, if my strength suffice and he, or any occasion, present itself. But although I have nothing to write to Your Magnificence beyond what he will more conveniently and safely set forth by mouth, yet I wished most dutifully to greet Your Magnificence with these [lines]; and [I ask] that you hold the said Tengnagel most commended to you, and that you not find it burdensome to promote the affairs of us both with the Most Serene Prince — as before, too, you very willingly and benevolently did — and to obtain a speedy dispatch. And since the same Tengnagel will be as a living letter, and will fully and secretly indicate to Your Magnificence the things concerning both me and himself, I refrain from further writing; and, through the same, returning to me as soon as may be (the Deity favouring), I await an answer. May God Best and Greatest preserve Your Magnificence, prosperous and unharmed, for the Commonwealth and for good letters, as long as possible.</p><p>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, which I now inhabit together with my [household], the 9th day of March, 1600.<br>Most zealous of Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient — unnamed.</b> The dataset titles it <i>ad anonymum</i>. From the internal evidence, a <b>councillor or official at the electoral court of Cologne</b> — someone who had earlier \"very willingly promoted\" Tycho's affairs \"with the Most Serene Prince,\" i.e. with <b>Elector Ernst of Bavaria, Archbishop of Cologne</b> (cf. brahe-t_15980625002, …0626002). <i>Identification flagged</i>: not named. (The companion letter brahe-t_16000308002, written the day before, addresses the same Cologne channel on the same errand.)</li><li><b>Tengnagel as \"living letter.\"</b> <i>Franciscus Tengnaglius … instar vivae Epistolae</i> — <b>Frans Tengnagel</b>, Tycho's noble assistant (later son-in-law), just back from <b>Italy</b>, now travelling to his <b>homeland</b> (the Lower-Rhine/Netherlands) to visit his parents, with the intention of approaching <b>Elector Ernst</b> <i>en route</i>; he will carry Tycho's business \"as a living letter\" and report orally and secretly. Tycho asks the recipient to commend Tengnagel and to expedite \"the affairs of us both\" (Tycho's and Tengnagel's) with the Elector.</li><li><b><i>Ma tua</b></i> = <i>Magnificentia tua</i> (\"Your Magnificence\"); <i>Sermum Electorem Coloniensem</i> = the Most Serene Elector of Cologne.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ … die 9 Martij 1600</i> = from the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky</b>, 9 March 1600; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b> (the companion letter is dataset-tagged <i>n.s.</i>). Header so marked.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the corrupted 'Domimnum' to 'Dominum'; Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.261) is unambiguous.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the matter (Tengnagel travelling home via the Cologne court; the request to promote Tycho's and Tengnagel's affairs with Elector Ernst) is clear and dovetails with the Cologne-channel letters and the companion brahe-t_16000308002. Grade [B] only because the <b>recipient is unidentified</b> (a Cologne-court official), flagged rather than named.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.260-262 page images. Located the letter beginning mid-p.261 ('TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM') after the Bongars letter's close, ending 'Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ, quam nunc unâ cum meis inhabito, die 9 Martij 1600.' Collated the full text including the name Franciscus Tengnaglius and the date. Found one TEXT_ERROR (our source_text's 'Domimnum' vs Dreyer's 'Dominum'); it does not affect the sense (the translation itself reads correctly), but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has 'Domimnum' (a corrupted, glued form) where Dreyer's page prints 'Dominum'; the English translation itself sidesteps the corruption and correctly renders 'Dominum', so the error is confined to our Latin transcription. Dreyer prints: <i>Sermum Electorem Coloniensem, Dominum nostrum Clementissimum (TBOO VIII p.261, line 9)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.260-262 (full slice, read in one pass); confirmed verbatim on p.261. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends Tengnagel on his journey home, requesting that the recipient support his renewed approach to the Elector of Cologne.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000309002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000310001", "d": "1600-03-10", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Leiden", "t": "Joseph Justus Scaliger → Tycho Brahe — Leiden, 10 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol. (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p><p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. <b>This is the letter Tycho answers in brahe-t_16000723001 / 16000623001 (23 July 1600)</b> — there cited as \"your letter written at Leiden on 10 March.\" The two should be read as a pair.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHE IOSEPHUS SCALIGER IULIJ CAeSARIS F. S.</p><p>QUONAM fato meo accidisse dicam, Vir nobilissime, ut qui amicis meis diligentiam, studium et officium meum omnibus in rebus, omni tempore probare paratus sim, non solum omnibus modis, quibus id praestare possim, destituar, sed omnia penitus adversa mihi experiar? Ecce ex quo literas tuas accepi, quas Witebergâ discedens ad me dedisti, nunquam conquievi, quin de te et tuarum rerum statu solicite quaesiverim. Nam postremae tuae, quae mihi omnem scrupulum eximere debebant, eae vero incertiorem me tuarum rerum reddiderunt. Nanque ex illis neque quando constiteris neque ubi lares posueris, colligere potui. Nunquam tamen ab eo tempore destiti percontandi de te, praesertim quum quidam te in Silesia esse et ibi sedem fortunarum tuarum statuisse, tam constanter affirmarent, ut soli tua negotia scire et curare viderentur. Quamdiu M. CHRISTIANUS Danus, vir optimus et tui studiosissimus, hîc fuit, aliqua spes relinquebatur aut meas literas ad te tuto dandi, aut tuas eliciendi. Sed ille prius discessit, quam postremas tuas acciperem. Nunc eae, quas hodie accepi, centum triginta diebus postquam datae sunt, omnino bearunt me, quod me omni cura de te, quae me anxium habebat, liberarunt, quum hactenus penderem animo, quo terrarum meas ad te mitterem, qui ubi consisteres nesciebam. Quanquam vero virtus tua et eruditio eximia non sinit te latere, neque tuas res ignorari, tamen tanta est locorum intercapedo, quae te a nobis dividit, ut quod de Sole necessarium est, eum prius apud Eoas nationes, quam Hesperias oriri, id omnino tibi conveniat, cuius lucem nobis serius sentire continget quam ijs, qui te fruuntur hospite et a te praesente hauriunt, quod nos ipsis, ne quid dissimulem, invidemus.</p><p>Quanta vero laetitia istae postremae nos pertuderint, uno verbo non dixerim. Hoc unum credas velim, nihil mihi gratius esse potuisse, quam tibi otium contigisse, quod et tuis studijs et dignitati tuae convenientissimum esset. Hoc enim unum semper spirarunt vota mea. Felicem te, mi TYCHO, qui non solum universam Europam testem eruditionis tuae, sed etiam Caesarem fautorem, adiutorem, et, quod coelum ipsum invidere possit, studiorum socium nactus sis. Patriam, quam natales dederant, deseruisti, ut eam, quam doctrina tua meruit, adipiscereris. Regem suum Danis Natura dedit: uni Dano TYCHONI virtus Imperatorem adoptavit. Ab altero te abesse dolendum erat, nisi maiorem, in quo vota tua conquiescerent, haberes. Te vero, magne amice, per ius amicitiae nostrae oro, ut me de statu, instituto et ratione rerum tuarum frequentius certiorem facias, ne qua in posterum mihi de te dubitatio relinquatur. Nam quae videri posset cessatio aut negligentia in officio literarum, ea potius fuerit ignoratio status rerum tuarum.</p><p>Multa vero erant, de quibus te convenire per literas vellem, siquidem per properantiam istius liceret, qui mihi tuas reddidit: qui, ut ad tuas statim responderem, a me exegit, et ita in ipso articulo oppressit, ut vix spatium reliquerit his exarandis. Diatribam enim meam de praecessione aequinoctiorum et confutatione motus octavae sphaerae ad te mittere cogito, in qua et innumera veterum testimonia a nemine hactenus animadversa studiose collegimus, et stellam illam, quam Polarem vocant, ante mille nongentos sexaginta sex annos in eo loco haesisse, ubi hodie est, quinque luculentis vetustissimorum scriptorum testimonijs demonstramus. Praeterea tot ἀτοπήματα et absurditates, quae ex illa hypothesi motus octavae sphaerae sequuntur, adducimus, ut nemo sit nisi imperitus aut invidus, qui contra hiscere audeat. Addo etiam, quod hactenus omnes latuit, praecessionem aequinoctiorum a THALETE usque et ANAXIMANDRO ad HIPPARCHUM admissam fuisse, primumque omnium HIPPARCHUM eam reiecisse, inducto sphaerae octavae motu in consequentia, adeo ut contra fidem oculorum extremam in cauda Cynosurae sive Polarem, ut vulgus vocat, esse omnium septem, quae in asterismo illo sunt, australissimam prodiderit, quae tamen fuit ante illum, et est hodie, eritque semper borealissima omnium. En quo posteritatem perduxit tanti viri autoritas. Nam ab illo usque hactenus vos ita creditis, et non alia re quam quod αὐτός ἔφα, quod in Mathesi nunquam locum habere debet. Nulli hominum generi hodie magis nocuit antiquitatis imperitia quam nationi Astrologorum. Nihil magis mirari mihi succurrit, quam nemini Astrologo de vitio illius hypotheseos suboluisse, neque quot et quanta absurda ex illa propagari necesse sit, si unum excipias COPERNICUM, qui et praecessionem aequinoctiorum et obliquitatem circuli aequinoctialis animadvertit, sed ignoratione rerum vetustarum ad absurdae hypotheses confugit. Sane regularem et aequabilem Solis maximae declinationis decrescentiam ab unica anticipatione aequinoctiorum pendere necesse est, quod a nobis optime demonstratum est. Aliter non potest, nisi per falsas hypotheses. Hinc sequitur polum mundi alium esse a polo aequinoctialis, et lineas meridianas moveri, neque in eadem sede semper haerere. Id nos egregie ex veteribus scriptoribus demonstramus. Denique tota disputatio est historia mathematica, prius testimonijs ab ultima memoria petitis collecta, deinde geometrice demonstrata. Ea adhuc in litura est. Quum per occupationes licebit in mundo describere, mittam ad te. Unum totam rem transigit, si stella polaris semper eidem loco haesit, nullum locum esse pertinaciae: omnia necessario, quae dixi, consequi debere, atque adeo nullum esse motum octavae sphaerae.</p><p>Quod de nobili viro GEORGIO HERWARTO ab HOËNBURG scribis, is iam dudum mihi de literis suis ad me notus est. Sed de stella Veneris quod scribit, videndum ne illi falsum aliquod Chronicon imposuerit homini eruditissimo, sed pro illo munere, quod gerit, occupatissimo, ut illi meliores auctores consulere non licuerit. Nam ut taceam absurditatem, quis, amabo, est iste Romanus Poëta sine praenomine et nomine? Anno quarto ante Christum natum hoc accidisse unde habet? Quis veterum hoc prodidit? Nam nullus eorum quidem, qui hodie supersunt, eius meminit, et quidem pauci supersunt, qui meminisse potuerint, OVIDIUS et MANILIUS, et nescio an alius praeterea. Nam HORATIUS anno quinto ante primum Dionysiacum Christi obijt. Et PROPERTIUS nihil tale. Denique decepit hominem eruditum aut memoria aut scriptor malae fidei.</p><p>De ALBATEGNIO utinam tibi satisfacere possem. Tantum abest, ut exemplar Arabicum nancisci possim, ut ne Latini quidem copia mihi sit impraesentia. Equidem mihi constat multa apud illum auctorem partim obscure, partim imperite reddita, quae fidelius et lucidius explicari poterant. Mitto tibi MANILIUM nostrum, quem per otium ut tibi liceat legere, opto. Nam ex eius gemmis poteritis aliquando astrologiae vestrae emblemata instruere. Nullus scriptorum neque apud Graecos neque apud Latinos tam male acceptus fuit, vel a mendis, vel a traiectionibus, vel ab ignoratione priscae Astrologiae.</p><p>Tuas literas rectius Augustam Vindelicorum mittere possis ad nobiliss. virum MARCUM VELSERUM, patritium et Reip. Augustanae Septemvirum. Is Amstelradamum recte curabit eas. Nam profecto via Hamburgensis longinquior est: nisi aliter tibi videtur; hoc enim arbitrij tui facio. Tantum opto, ut literae tuae non solum tuto mihi, sed etiam cito reddantur.</p><p>Vale unice Astronomiae vindex.<br>Lugduni Batavorum VI Eid. Martias, stylo novo CIↃ IↃ C.</p><p>Nondum mihi diatribam tuam ad literas nobiliss. GEORGIIJ HERWARTI<br>nisi obiter legere contigit, quia statim a lectione tuarum literarum me ad<br>has conscribendas contuli. Nunc sedulo omnia legam. Iterum vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man Tycho Brahe, Joseph Scaliger, son of Julius Caesar [Scaliger], [sends] greeting.</b></p><p>By what fate of mine shall I say it happened, most noble Man, that I — who am ready to prove my diligence, zeal, and service to my friends in all things, at all times — am not only deprived of all the means by which I might do it, but experience all things utterly adverse to me? Behold: from the time I received your letter, which you sent me on leaving Wittenberg, I never rested but anxiously inquired about you and the state of your affairs. For your last [letter], which ought to have removed every scruple from me, made me the more uncertain of your affairs; for from it I could gather neither <i>when</i> you had settled, nor <i>where</i> you had set your household. Yet from that time I never ceased asking about you — especially since some so constantly affirmed that <b>you were in Silesia and had set the seat of your fortunes there</b>, that they seemed alone to know and tend your business. As long as <b>M. Christianus the Dane [Longomontanus]</b>, an excellent man and most devoted to you, was here, some hope was left of sending my letters to you safely, or of eliciting yours. But he departed before I received your last. Now those [letters], which I received today, <b>a hundred and thirty days after they were sent</b>, have wholly blessed me, in that they freed me of all the care about you that held me anxious — since hitherto I hung in mind as to where on earth I should send mine to you, not knowing where you stayed. But although your virtue and eminent erudition do not let you lie hidden, nor your affairs be unknown, yet <b>so great is the distance of places dividing you from us</b>, that what is necessary of the Sun — that it rises among the Eastern nations before the Western — is altogether fitting for you: whose light it falls to us to feel later than to those who enjoy you as a host, and drink from you present — which (not to dissemble) we envy them.</p><p>With how great joy those last [letters] struck me through, I could not say in one word. This one thing I would have you believe: that nothing could have been more pleasing to me than that <b>leisure has befallen you, most fitting both for your studies and for your dignity</b>. For this one thing my prayers always breathed. <b>Happy you, my Tycho, who have got not only all Europe as witness of your erudition, but also the Emperor as patron, helper, and — what heaven itself might envy — companion of your studies. You deserted the fatherland which your birth had given, to gain the one your learning earned. Nature gave the Danes their King; to one Dane, Tycho, virtue adopted an Emperor.</b> It would have been to be grieved that you were absent from the one [fatherland], had you not a greater, in which your prayers might rest. But I beg you, great friend, by the right of our friendship, <b>to inform me more frequently of the state, plan, and method of your affairs</b>, lest any doubt about you be left me hereafter; for what might seem a cessation or negligence in the duty of letters, has rather been ignorance of the state of your affairs.</p><p>Many things there were of which I would meet you by letter, if the haste of the man who delivered yours to me allowed — who exacted of me that I answer yours at once, and so pressed me in the very moment that he scarce left space for scratching these. <b>For I purpose to send you my <i>Diatriba</i> on the precession of the equinoxes and the confutation of the motion of the eighth sphere</b> — in which I have studiously collected innumerable testimonies of the ancients, hitherto noticed by no one, and <b>demonstrate, by five clear testimonies of the most ancient writers, that the star they call the Pole-star stuck, 1966 years ago, in the same place where it is today</b>. Besides, I adduce so many absurdities [ἀτοπήματα] that follow from that hypothesis of the eighth sphere's motion, that no one but the unskilled or the envious would dare to open his mouth against [it]. I add also — what hitherto escaped all — that <b>the precession of the equinoxes was admitted from Thales and Anaximander down to Hipparchus, and that Hipparchus, first of all, rejected it, introducing the eighth sphere's motion <i>in consequentia</b></i> — so that, against the evidence of the eyes, he reported the star at the tip of Cynosura's tail (or the Pole-star, as the vulgar call it) to be, of all the seven in that asterism, the most southern — which yet was before him, is today, and will always be the most northern of all. See whither the authority of so great a man led posterity! For from him until now <b>you [astronomers] so believe — and for no other reason than that \"he himself said it\" [αὐτός ἔφα], which ought never to have place in Mathematics.</b> To no class of men today has ignorance of antiquity done more harm than to the nation of the Astrologers. Nothing comes to me to wonder at more than that <b>no Astrologer got wind of the fault of that hypothesis</b> — nor how many and how great absurdities must be propagated from it — if you except <b>Copernicus</b> alone, who noticed both the precession of the equinoxes and the obliquity of the equinoctial circle, but, from ignorance of ancient things, <b>fled to absurd hypotheses</b>. For the regular and even decrease of the Sun's maximum declination must depend on the single anticipation of the equinoxes — which has been most well demonstrated by us. Otherwise it cannot be, except by false hypotheses. <b>Hence it follows that the pole of the world is other than the pole of the equinoctial, and that the meridian lines move, and do not always stick in the same seat.</b> This we demonstrate excellently from the ancient writers. In short, the whole disputation is <b>a mathematical history</b> — first collected from testimonies sought from remotest memory, then demonstrated geometrically. It is still in draft. When my occupations allow it to be written out clean, I will send it to you. One thing settles the whole matter: <b>if the Pole-star always stuck in the same place, there is no room for obstinacy; all I have said must necessarily follow — and so there is no motion of the eighth sphere.</b></p><p>As to what you write of the noble man <b>Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b>, he has long been known to me by his letters to me. But as to what he writes <b>about the star of Venus, it must be seen lest some false Chronicle imposed on that most learned man</b> — but, for the office he bears, most busy, so that he could not consult better authors. For — to be silent of the absurdity — who, pray, is <b>that Roman Poet without praenomen and nomen</b>? Whence does he have that this happened in the <b>fourth year before Christ was born</b>? Which of the ancients reported this? For none indeed of those surviving today mentions it — and few survive who could have mentioned it: <b>Ovid and Manilius, and I know not whether any other besides</b>. For <b>Horace died in the fifth year before the first Dionysian [year] of Christ</b>; and <b>Propertius</b> [says] nothing such. In short, either his memory or a writer of bad faith deceived the learned man.</p><p>Of <b>Al-Battani [Albategnius]</b>, would that I could satisfy you. So far am I from being able to get an Arabic copy, that I have not even a Latin one to hand at present. For my part I am sure that many things in that author are rendered partly obscurely, partly unskilfully, which could be explained more faithfully and lucidly. <b>I send you our Manilius</b>, which I wish you may read at leisure; for from its gems you [astronomers] may sometime furnish the emblems of your astrology. No writer, neither among the Greeks nor among the Latins, was so badly handled — whether by corruptions, or by transpositions, or by ignorance of ancient Astrology.</p><p>Your letters you might more rightly send to <b>Augsburg, to the most noble man Marcus Welser, patrician and Septemvir of the Augsburg Republic</b>. He will properly forward them to <b>Amsterdam</b>. For truly the Hamburg route is longer — unless it seem otherwise to you; for this I leave to your judgement. I only wish that your letters be delivered to me not only safely, but also quickly.</p><p>Farewell, singular champion of Astronomy.<br>Leiden, the 6th day before the Ides of March [10 March], New Style, 1600.</p><p>I have not yet had occasion to read your treatise on the letters of the most noble Georg Herwart, except cursorily, since immediately upon reading your letter I betook myself to writing this one. Now I shall read everything carefully. Farewell again.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609, son of Julius Caesar Scaliger) at <b>Leiden</b> → <b>Tycho</b>. <b>The letter Tycho answers in brahe-t_16000723001 / 16000623001 (23 July 1600)</b> — read the two as a pair (Tycho there rejects Scaliger's \"moving meridians\" and offers his own <i>solar</i> theory of precession).</li><li><b>Significance — Scaliger's full precession theory and his <i>Diatriba</i> against the eighth-sphere motion (a major scientific statement).</b></li><li><b>The <i>Diatriba</i>.</b> Scaliger announces his (still-draft) <b><i>Diatriba de praecessione aequinoctiorum</b></i> — \"a mathematical history,\" collecting ancient testimonies and then demonstrating geometrically that <b>the Pole-star held its place 1966 years ago as today</b> (by \"five clear testimonies\"), so that <b>the \"eighth sphere's motion\" is a fiction</b> generating only absurdities (ἀτοπήματα).</li><li><b>The Hipparchus thesis.</b> His striking historical claim: <b>precession was accepted \"from Thales and Anaximander to Hipparchus,\" and Hipparchus <i>first rejected it</i>,</b> introducing the eighth-sphere motion <i>in consequentia</i> — even misreporting Polaris as the <i>southernmost</i> of its asterism against plain sight. Posterity, Scaliger charges, followed merely on <b>\"ipse dixit\" (αὐτός ἔφα), which has no place in mathematics.</b></li><li><b>Copernicus and the conclusion.</b> Only <b>Copernicus</b> noticed precession and the obliquity-change, \"but fled to absurd hypotheses.\" Scaliger derives the <b>even decrease of the Sun's maximum declination</b> from \"the single anticipation of the equinoxes,\" and concludes — the point Tycho will reject — that <b>\"the pole of the world is other than the pole of the equinoctial, and the meridian lines move.\"</b></li><li><b>The Herwart \"Venus-star\" critique.</b> On a chronological datum <b>Herwart von Hohenburg</b> had circulated about a <b>\"star of Venus\" in the 4th year before Christ</b> (a Star-of-Bethlehem-adjacent chronology question), Scaliger is scathing: it rests on \"a false Chronicle\" and an unidentifiable <b>\"Roman Poet without praenomen or nomen\"</b>; the only poets who <i>could</i> have recorded it are <b>Ovid and Manilius</b> (Horace died too early, Propertius is silent). (Cf. the Herwart letter, brahe-t_16000911001.)</li><li><b>Al-Battani &amp; Manilius.</b> Scaliger cannot supply <b>al-Battani</b> (no Arabic <i>or</i> Latin copy to hand) but criticises its existing renderings; and <b>sends Tycho his own edition of Manilius</b> (\"from its gems furnish the emblems of your astrology\") — the most corruption-marred of all ancient authors. (Tycho thanks him for it in the reply of 23 July 1600.)</li><li><b>The courier route.</b> Scaliger advises routing letters <b>via Marcus Welser at Augsburg → Amsterdam</b>, rather than the longer Hamburg route.</li><li><b>The encomium.</b> The famous compliment: <i>\"Nature gave the Danes their King; to one Dane, Tycho, virtue adopted an Emperor\"</i> — and the Sun-rises-in-the-East conceit for the distance between them.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>other half of the Scaliger precession exchange</b> (with the reply of 23 July 1600; and the earlier letter of 17 April 1599, where Tycho requested Hipparchus's equinoxes, the southern asterisms, and al-Battani); ties to the <b>Herwart</b> thread (Herwart's letter of 11 September 1600) and the chronology disputes (the letters to Wolfgang Franz of 27 March 1600 and to Bartholomäus Scultetus of 7 January 1600).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Joseph Justus Scaliger; <b>Longomontanus</b> (<i>M. Christianus Danus</i>); <b>Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b>; <b>Thales, Anaximander, Hipparchus, Copernicus</b>; <b>Ovid, Manilius, Horace, Propertius</b>; <b>al-Battani (Albategnius)</b>; <b>Marcus Welser</b> (Augsburg). Places: <b>Leiden</b>, <b>Silesia</b> (the false rumour of Tycho's seat), <b>Augsburg</b>, <b>Amsterdam</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Lugduni Batavorum VI Eid. Martias, <b>stylo novo</b> CIↃIↃC</i> → <b>Leiden, 10 March 1600 [N.S.]</b> (VI Ides of March = 10 March; CIↃIↃC = 1600); header and label agree. Tycho received it \"130 days after\"? — no: Scaliger received <i>Tycho's</i> after 130 days; Tycho received <i>this</i> one \"six weeks before\" his July reply.</li><li><b>Greek terms reproduced.</b> ἀτοπήματα (\"absurdities\"), αὐτός ἔφα (\"he himself said it\"), <i>Eoas/Hesperias</i> (Eastern/Western).</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A closing paragraph, preceding the letter's actual farewell (given in the published tail), in which Scaliger acknowledges receipt of a piece of Tycho's own writing — a \"diatriba\" addressed to Georg Herwart — promising a fuller reading once this reply is off his desk.</li><li><b>\"Diatriba\" — a false friend.</b> In this Neo-Latin usage \"diatriba\" means a learned discourse, dissertation, or treatise, carrying none of the pejorative \"angry tirade\" sense the English cognate \"diatribe\" has since acquired; rendered here as \"treatise\" to avoid the false friend.</li><li><b>Georg Herwart identified.</b> Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg (1553–1622), Chancellor of Bavaria, a learned correspondent of both Tycho and (later, more famously) Kepler, with whom he corresponded on both scientific and religious matters and whom he patronised [Tier 1: Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (Cambridge, 2007); Gingerich, <i>An Annotated Census of Copernicus' De revolutionibus</i>; Martens, <i>Kepler's Philosophy and the New Astronomy</i>]. The published tail of this same letter separately shows Scaliger discussing ancient astrology's mistreatment by copyists and editors — a topic entirely consistent with a Tycho–Herwart exchange on the correct interpretation of \"prisca Astrologia.\"</li><li><b>The sender.</b> Joseph Justus Scaliger (1540–1609), the preeminent classical philologist and chronologist of the age, then at Leiden; his letter as a whole (published tail) discusses postal routes for reaching him via Marcus Welser at Augsburg.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — eloquent, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (Scaliger's <b>precession <i>Diatriba</b></i> — the 1966-year Pole-star fixity, the five testimonies, the <b>Hipparchus-rejected-precession thesis</b>, the <i>ipse dixit</i> polemic, Copernicus's \"absurd hypotheses,\" and the <b>moving-meridians conclusion</b>; the <b>Herwart Venus-star/Roman-poet chronology critique</b>; the al-Battani and <b>Manilius</b> matters; the Welser/Augsburg route; and the famous \"virtue adopted an Emperor\" encomium) is firmly construed and pairs exactly with Tycho's reply of 23 July 1600. The Greek phrases are reproduced; no textual crux.</p><p>The Latin is clear and the only interpretive choice of substance — rendering \"diatriba\" as \"treatise\" rather than the false-friend \"diatribe\" — is well grounded in standard Neo-Latin usage. Herwart's identity and his correspondence role are independently corroborated by multiple corpus sources. Confident throughout.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the whole letter (opening salutation through the closing postscript) visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 261-264, read within the supplied 260-265 slice (pp.260 and 265 hold the tail/head of adjacent unrelated letters, checked to confirm the boundary). Checked every proper name (M. Christianus Danus, Georgius Herwartus ab Hoenburg, Marcus Velserus, Albategnius, Manilius, Ovidius, Propertius, Horatius, Copernicus, Hipparchus, Thales, Anaximander), every number (130 days, 1966 years since the Pole-star's position, Horace 'anno quinto' before the Dionysian era, the Venus-star anecdote 'anno quarto' before Christ), every place-name (Wittenberg, Silesia, Augsburg, Amsterdam, Hamburg, Leiden), and the date formula (VI Eid. Martias, stylo novo CIↃIↃC = 10 March 1600 N.S.). All match Dreyer's printed text exactly, and the published English tracks the Latin sentence-by-sentence with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15990417001", "next": "brahe-t_16000623001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990417001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000623001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains his failure to locate Tycho after Wittenberg, describes false reports that Tycho had settled in Silesia, and mentions Longomontanus as his informant.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000310001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000314001", "d": "1600-03-14", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Monavius", "ck": "Jacob Monavius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wrocław", "t": "Jacob Monau (Monavius) → Tycho Brahe — [Breslau], 14 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with German titulature) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, cod. Basil. G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, UNICO STUDIORUM MATHEMATICORUM HUIUS SECULI RESTAURATORI, CAeSA........ DOMINO ET AMICO SIC ........LENTER OBSERVANDO.</p><p>S.</p><p>TARDIUS mihi redditae sunt literae tuae X Ianuarij scriptae, Magnifice Domine TYCHO, Vir praestantissime: tardius etiam ad illas rescribo, partim propter adversam meam valetudinem (conflictor enim cum febri tertiana, quae me graviter adhuc affligit) partim etiam quia Typographus non potuit citius de voluntate sua respondere, quam esset edoctus ab ijs, cum quibus egit de papyro sive charta. Dici autem non potest, quam gratae fuerint literae tuae, e quibus libentissime cognovi de sede fortunarum, vel potius studiorum, hoc tempore tibi assignata ab Augustissimo Caesare. Deum precor toto pectore, ut haec omnia tibi velit esse secundissima, atque tuos laudatissimos conatus provehat ad optatissimum finem in tota Republica Christiana.</p><p>Doctor FISCHERUS agit tibi reverenter gratias, quod eum dignaris tua benevolentia, cui sese totum addit et commendat: forte non indignus, quem complectaris, praesertim ubi melius hominem nosse coeperis.</p><p>Ad libros Wittichianos quod attinet, scire te volo, eo ipso tempore, quo huc allatae fuerunt literae tuae, sororem PAULI WITTICHIJ iam aetate provectam celebrasse nuptias secundas, quarum hic fuit exitus, ut nova nupta post LX dies moreretur. Filium unicum reliquit haeredem, cui nondum constituti sunt certi tutores: quod ubi factum fuerit, videbo, an et quid cum illis agi possit. Fuisset mihi res facilior, si ipsa Wittichiana vixisset diutius: videbo tamen, quid fieri et impetrari queat. Audio ab ijs, qui superstite adhuc sorore PAULI WITTICHIJ inspexerunt libros Wittichianos: illi aiunt, omnia esse varie confusa et distracta tanquam Sybillae folia, nec miror. Erat mulier illa prorsus mirifica et suo sensu abundans.</p><p>Quod ad alterum illum Mathematicum attinet, qui ante aliquot annos hîc vixit, si non est molestum, hortor te, ut epistolam non magnam scribere velis ad Medicum huius urbis fortissimum et doctissimum Doctorem DANIELEM BUCRETIUM. Ei sunt noti haeredes, quos reliquit ille Mathematicus, et scio eum tui tam amantem et observantem atque studiosum esse, ut moturus sit omnem lapidem, et operam daturus, quantam poterit maximam, ut desiderio tuo satisfiat, quantum fieri omnino poterit.</p><p>Quod ad negocium typographicum attinet, egi cum nostro, quem habet haec civitas, typographo, et ei significavi, quid de ipso scire velis. Is respondet scripto peculiari, quod mitto, et simul curavit fieri probam, ut nominant, typorum suorum. Tu haec, mi Domine, leges et cogitabis, et quae sit tua voluntas, nobis significabis. Ego, si quid hac in re facere potero, quod tibi gratum sit, aut usibus tuis serviat, faciam hoc summa voluntate.</p><p>Si quid ad me scribere voles, fac per certos homines mittas Pragam ad Dn. SIMONEM LUCK, Registrator vnd Taxator Ihrer Kay. Mt. bey der Hoffcammer. Is quicquid acceperit, bona fide ad me curabit. Pro versibus nunc missis, quos ad priora carmina aggregavi, magnas ago gratias. Si plane perducta est ad finem editio operis illius tui, quod aliquando vidi, sed adhuc mancum et mutilum, cui titulus TYCHONIS BR. Dani Astronomiae instaurandae progymnasmata volumen primum Uraniburgi 1592: peto a te magnopere, ut exemplum Bibliothecae meae donare velis, sicut etiam fecisti (quo nomine adhuc magnas tibi gratias ago) cum volumen primum Epistolarum Astron. ad me misisti. Mea officia et studia paratissima tibi semper constabunt pro viribus.</p><p>Non licet nunc plura propter febrim, quae licet nonnihil per Dei gratiam remittat; tamen, nondum adhuc receptis viribus pristinis, admodum langueo. Ille me sanabit, qui me percussit, qui idem est vita nostra, et longitudo dierum nostrorum: in quo etiam sumus, vivimus, et movemur. Oro te, vir praestantissime, ut veniam d[are velis] epistolae sic scriptae, ut facile agnoscatur author eius f........</p><p>Bene et feliciter vale in Domino Deo nostro, a quo tibi ex animo precor omnes felicitates et quaecunque animus tuus potest, iuxta illius divinam voluntatem, optare.<br>Datae subito XIIIJ Martij 1600.<br>T. M. omni studio et observantia unice colens et amans<br>IACOB MONAW.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Lord Tycho Brahe, sole restorer of the mathematical studies of this age, [Imperial …], his Lord and friend so … to be respected.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Your letter, written <b>10 January</b>, was delivered to me rather late, Magnificent Lord Tycho, most excellent Man; and I reply to it rather late too — partly because of my own ill health (for I struggle with a <b>tertian fever</b>, which still grievously afflicts me), partly because the <b>Printer</b> could not answer about his willingness sooner than he was informed by those with whom he dealt about paper. But it cannot be told how welcome your letter was, from which I most gladly learned of the <b>seat of [your] fortunes — or rather of [your] studies — assigned you at this time by the most august Emperor</b>. I pray God with my whole heart that he would have all these things be most favourable to you, and advance your most praised endeavours to the most desired end, in the whole Christian Commonwealth.</p><p>Doctor <b>Fischer</b> gives you reverent thanks that you deign [to honour] him with your benevolence, to which he wholly devotes and commends himself — perhaps not unworthy for you to embrace, especially when you have begun to know the man better.</p><p>As concerns the <b>Wittich books</b>, I would have you know that, at the very time your letter was brought here, <b>the sister of Paul Wittich — already advanced in age — celebrated a second marriage, of which this was the outcome: that the new bride died after 60 days. She left a single son as heir, for whom certain guardians have not yet been appointed</b>; which, when it has been done, I will see whether — and what — can be transacted with them. The matter would have been easier for me had the Wittich woman herself lived longer; yet I will see what can be done and obtained. I hear from those who inspected the Wittich books while Paul Wittich's sister was still living: they say <b>everything is variously confused and scattered, like the Sibyl's leaves</b> — nor do I wonder; that woman was utterly eccentric and full of her own notions.</p><p>As concerns that <b>other Mathematician</b>, who lived here some years ago: if it be not troublesome, I urge you to write a not-large letter to the most stalwart and learned physician of this city, <b>Doctor Daniel Bucretius</b>. To him are known the heirs that Mathematician left; and I know him to be so loving, respectful, and devoted to you that he will <b>move every stone</b>, and give the greatest effort he can, that your desire be satisfied, as far as can at all be done.</p><p>As concerns the <b>typographic business</b>, I dealt with our printer, whom this city has, and indicated to him what you wish to know about him. He answers in a special writing, which I send, and has also had a <b>sample (proof), as they call it, made of his types</b>. You, my Lord, will read and consider these, and signify to us your will. I, if I can do anything in this matter welcome to you or serviceable to your uses, will do it with the greatest goodwill.</p><p>If you wish to write anything to me, see that you send it, by sure men, to Prague, to Lord <b>Simon Luck, Registrar and Assessor of His Imperial Majesty at the Court-Chamber</b> — whatever he receives, he will faithfully convey to me. For the <b>verses now sent</b>, which I have added to the earlier poems, I give great thanks. If the edition of that work of yours — which I once saw, but still incomplete and mutilated, whose title is <b><i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Astronomiae Instaurandae Progymnasmata, Volume One, Uraniborg 1592</b></i> — has been quite brought to completion, I greatly ask that you would <b>give a copy to my Library</b>, as you also did (for which I still give you great thanks) when you sent me the <b>first volume of the <i>Astronomical Letters</b></i>. My services and zeal will always stand most ready for you, according to my powers.</p><p>It is not permitted me now [to write] more, because of the fever — which, though by God's grace it somewhat remits, yet, my former strength not yet recovered, I quite languish. He will heal me who struck me, who is the same as our life, and the length of our days; <b>in whom also we are, and live, and move</b> [Acts 17:28]. I beg you, most excellent man, that you would [be willing to give] pardon to a letter so written that its author is easily recognized [as …].</p><p>Fare well and happily in the Lord our God, from whom I pray you, from my heart, all felicities, and whatever your mind can wish, according to His divine will.<br>Given hastily, 14 March 1600.<br>Cultivating and loving Your Magnificence uniquely, with all zeal and respect,<br>Jacob Monau.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; occasion.</b> <b>Jacob Monau (Monavius)</b> of <b>Breslau</b> — this is his <b>reply to Tycho's letter of 10 January 1600 (brahe-t_16000110001)</b>, and the antecedent of the later Monau letters (brahe-t_16000516001 / brahe-t_16000614001). Written while Monau is ill with a <b>tertian fever</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — the Wittich-library provenance (key detail).</b> Monau answers Tycho's standing request for <b>Paul Wittich's books</b> (incl. the annotated Copernicus copies; cf. brahe-t_16000110001): at the very time Tycho's letter arrived, <b>Wittich's aged sister-heir had remarried — and died 60 days later</b>, leaving <b>a single son as heir for whom guardians were not yet appointed</b> (the <i>pupillus/tutores</i> of brahe-t_16000516001). He reports the books, seen while the sister lived, are <i>\"variously confused and scattered, like the <b>Sibyl's leaves</b>\"</i> — \"that woman was utterly eccentric.\" This is the <b>pivotal document for the dispersal of the Wittich library</b> (the \"now that the difficult sister is dead\" of brahe-t_16000516001 refers to exactly this).</li><li><b>The other Breslau astronomer &amp; Bucretius.</b> Monau routes Tycho's request for the <b>dead Breslau astronomer's</b> papers (cf. brahe-t_16000110001) through <b>Daniel Bucretius</b>, \"the most stalwart and learned physician of this city,\" who knows the heirs and \"will move every stone.\"</li><li><b>Printing &amp; books.</b> Monau forwards the <b>Breslau printer's</b> terms and a <b>type-sample (proof)</b>; thanks Tycho for <b>verses</b> added to the commendatory collection; and <b>requests for his Library a finished copy of the <i>Progymnasmata</b></i> (<i>Astronomiae Instaurandae Progymnasmata</i>, vol. 1, Uraniborg 1592), having only seen it \"incomplete and mutilated\" — as Tycho had earlier gifted him the <b>first volume of the <i>Epistolae Astronomicae</b></i>. (A datum for the distribution of Tycho's books.)</li><li><b>The relay.</b> Letters to Monau to go via <b>Simon Luck, Registrar and Assessor (<i>Registrator und Taxator</i>) of the imperial Court-Chamber (<i>Hofkammer</i>)</b> at Prague (German title kept per §2).</li><li><b>Scripture.</b> <i>in quo … sumus, vivimus, et movemur</i> — <b>Acts 17:28</b> (\"in him we live and move and have our being\"); <i>Ille me sanabit qui me percussit</i> echoes Job 5:18 / Deut. 32:39.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Monau (sender); <b>Tobias Fischer</b>; <b>Paul Wittich</b> (his sister-heir and her son); <b>Daniel Bucretius</b>; the unnamed dead Breslau astronomer; <b>Simon Luck</b> (imperial Hofkammer registrar); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Textual flags.</b> The witness has <b>lacunae in the address</b> (<i>Caesa…… Domino et amico sic ……lenter observando</i>) and in the <b>closing</b> (<i>veniam d[are velis] … author eius f……</i>), reproduced with brackets/ellipses; they do not affect the body. <b>Calendar (protocol §3):</b> dateline <i>XIIIJ Martij 1600</i>; Silesia (Breslau) on the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> by 1600; the dataset gives no place (<i>s.l.</i>) — \"[Breslau]\" supplied as Monau's seat. Marked [N.S.].</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear humanist Latin, read securely; the substance (the reply to the 10 Jan letter; Fischer's thanks; <b>the Wittich sister's remarriage-and-death and the books \"like Sibyl's leaves\"</b>; the Bucretius routing for the dead astronomer; the printer's proof; the relay via Simon Luck; the request for the 1592 <i>Progymnasmata</i>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. The witness's address/closing lacunae are flagged, not conjectured.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the entire letter -- damaged/illegible heading, all six paragraphs, and the closing -- against TBOO VIII pp.265-267 read as page images. Every proper name (Fischerus, Pauli Wittichij, Danielem Bucretium, Simonem Luck, the Progymnasmata/Uraniburgi 1592 title), number (LX dies = 60 days; X Ianuarij = 10 January; XIIII Martij = 14 March), and the closing formula match Dreyer's print exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, cod. Basileensi G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Jacob Monavius", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000110001", "next": "brahe-t_16000516001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000110001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000516001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for a delayed reply due to tertian fever and a printer's uncertainty about paper, expresses joy at Tycho's imperial establishment, and relays Fischer's thanks.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000314001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000318001", "d": "1600-03-18", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Tobias Fischer", "ck": "Tobias Fischer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Świdnica", "t": "Tobias Fischer → Tycho Brahe — Świdnica [Schweidnitz], 18 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with verse quotations) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ATQUE EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI DE BRAHE, EQUITI ET DOMINO IN KNUDSDORP, SOLLERTISSIMO ASTRONOMIAe INSTAURATORI ASTRONOMORUMQUE NOSTRI SECULI PRIMARIO, IN ARCE CAeSAREA BENATICA IAM AGENTE, DOMINO SUO DEBITAe OBSERVANTIAe STUDIJS COLENDO.</p><p>S. P. D.</p><p>QUANTO me gaudio affecerit litterae MONAVIJ mei, Vir magnifice atque excellentissime, Domine et amice observare, vix queo dicere. Non enim solum te veniam mihi dare nunciabant, qui ignotus ad tantum virum meas mittere ausus fueram, sed et eas tibi gratas fuisse, meque in amicorum tuorum numerum abs te susceptum esse, ac memoriam mei animo tuo servare, quemadmodum tuis ad ipsum litteris sis attestatus. Gratias itaque tibi ago, quas mente meâ concipere possum maximas, pro tantâ tuâ humanitate tantâque in me benevolentiâ, et tam benignâ tamque amicâ ad amicitiam tui admissione. Amavi equidem te et colui, suspexi, miratusque sum iampridem tacito quodam sensu, qui nunc lubens se promit, magisque aspirat ad tam suavem iucundamque tuae benevolentiae auram. Merentur id virtus tua, ingenium, eruditio, experientia, dignitas, maiestas, quibus me velut adamantino vinculo nexuisti, quod nulla vis laxabit aut dissolvet umquam, nulla, crede mihi, obscuratura est oblivio, donec</p><p>Lumine ademto animam moribundo corpore fundam.<br>Quod tibi hîc non scriptum, sed veluti per syngrapham sponsum volo.</p><p>Rogavi, opera tua a typographo ut mihi comparares tuo, quaecunque abs te edita, debitoque precio me illa persoluturum dixi. Vidi apud nonnullos quaedam, non omnia, quod non ad nos perferantur a Bibliopolis nostris, quo divina huius scientiae studia precio nunc apud plerosque sint, non male perspicientibus. Ea quantopere desiderem, nequeo scribere. Age ergo, Vir magnifice, domine et amice observande, ut tuâ operâ ab illo ea impetrem, et quamprimum Pragam ad ERNESTUM HEINCIUM, Camerae Caesareae Registratorem et Expeditorem, coniugis meae fratrem germanum, vel Vratislaviam ad MONAVIUM mitte, nec me, quod callidae amantes solent, diu desiderio isto torque. Precium, quod volet ille, statim numerabitur. Velim addi Instrumentorum tuorum delineationem, quam nuper mihi ostendit Medicus D. BARTHOLOMAeUS SWALBIUS, intimus meus, qui et versus meos tibi gratos fuisse narrabat, sed a te recoctos ad salivam et gustum tuum, quod gaudeo; tamen me tanto viro placuisse, summae laudi duco. Verum, pace tuâ, paucis mentem meam explicabo. Sensus meus est, te errores astrorum motusque dissipatos, quales fuere hactenus, singulari arte et mirâ industriâ in ordinem redegisse certiorem, Deumque tibi uni, quem eruditio et virtus in sublime vexerunt, quod antehac paucis particulatim concessit, nunc divite et pleno Cornu plenâque manu, quasi nimis tantarum opum largum profusorem, suggerere, ut quemadmodum Sibylla paucis contingere dicit apud MARONEM, ut ex Orco ad superas revertantur, et ijs tantum,</p><p>quos aequus amavit<br>Iuppiter, aut ardens evexit ad aethera virtus:<br>ita etiam tu solus repertus sis, cui digne ista contigerint ante omnes, teque merito nunc, quae tam large accepisti, largâ manu quoque posteris largiri debere. Particulam Coelitus pro In, vel Sub Coelo, semper ego de loco cum omnibus autoribus intellexi, et obscuriorem mihi reddere videtur sensum, quam si legatur: astra sub coeli gemino axe. Sed ignosce, per Deum te oro, excellentissime domine, quod tam libere haec scribo. Tibi enim manum do, tuo iudicio acquiesco, tuae censurae subscribo, teque valere et astronomiae stabulum, Herculeis tuis laboribus, abs te magis magisque expurgari cupio,</p><p>Quaeque opto, rata sint, faxit rex aetheris alti,<br>Ingeniumque tibi, vires, vitamque ministret.<br>Vigore animum nervosque robore impleat.</p><p>Vale ergo, Vir excellentissime, domine et amice, perpetuis debitae observantiae studijs a me colende.<br>Dabam Suidnicij Silesiorum XIIX die Martij anno epochae Christianorum MDC.<br>Excell. atque dignitatis tuae observantissimus<br>TOBIAS FISCHERUS, Philos. et Med. Doctor, manu propria.</p><p>Editi sunt a MERCATORE anno 1541 Lovanij<br>duo globi, Cœlestis et terrenus, quos ego ipse meis manibus nuper reduxi.<br>Nunciatum autem mihi est, paucis abhinc annis eosdem correctiores editos<br>esse Coloniæ, et formâ adhuc maiore, nec<br>in alia loca chartas istas divulgari. Peramanter itaque etiam atque etiam te<br>oro, num vera hæc sint, et an non a typographis illis comparari, si non per<br>me, per aliquem excellentis autoritatis virum queant, significa.</p><p>D. ERNESTUS HENCIUS affinis<br>meus singulis hebdomadibus ad me suas potest mittere litteras.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most excellent man, Lord Tycho de Brahe, knight and lord in Knudstrup, most skilful Restorer of Astronomy and chief of the astronomers of our age, now residing in the imperial castle of Benátky, his Lord, to be cultivated with the zeal of due respect.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>With how great joy the letter of my <b>Monau</b> affected me, magnificent and most excellent Man, Lord and friend to be respected, I can scarcely tell. For it announced not only that you grant me pardon — I who, unknown, had dared to send mine to so great a man — but also that they were welcome to you, and that I have been <b>received by you into the number of your friends</b>, and that you keep memory of me in your mind, as you attested in your letter to him. I therefore give you the greatest thanks I can conceive, for your so great humanity and benevolence toward me, and for so kindly and friendly an admission to your friendship. I have indeed loved and cultivated, looked up to, and admired you long since with a certain silent feeling, which now gladly puts itself forth, and aspires the more to so sweet and pleasant a breeze of your benevolence. This your virtue, genius, erudition, experience, dignity, majesty deserve — by which you have bound me as with an adamantine chain, which no force will ever loosen or dissolve, nor (believe me) shall any oblivion darken, until</p><p><i>\"When light is taken away, I pour forth my soul from my dying body.\"</i></p><p>Which I wish [regarded] by you here not [as merely] written, but as pledged, as it were, by a bond.</p><p>I asked that you procure for me from your printer your works — whatever has been published by you — and I said I would pay for them at the due price. I have seen some among certain [people], not all, since they are not brought to us by our booksellers (who perceive well enough at what [low] price the divine studies of this science now stand with most people). How greatly I desire them, I cannot write. Come then, magnificent Man, Lord and friend to be respected, that by your effort I may obtain them from him; and send them as soon as possible to Prague, to <b>Ernst Heincius</b>, Registrar and Dispatcher of the Imperial Chamber, my wife's own brother, or to Breslau to <b>Monau</b> — and do not, as cunning lovers are wont, torment me long with that longing. The price he wishes will be paid at once. I should wish to be added the <b>drawing of your Instruments</b>, which the physician Lord <b>Bartholomäus Schwalb</b>, my intimate, lately showed me — who also related that my verses were welcome to you, but <b>recooked by you to your taste and palate</b> (which I rejoice at; nevertheless I count it the highest praise to have pleased so great a man). But, with your leave, I will explain my mind in a few words. My opinion is that you have, with singular art and wonderful industry, reduced into surer order the <b>errors and scattered motions of the stars</b>, such as they have been hitherto; and that God supplies to you alone — whom erudition and virtue have raised on high — what before he granted to few, and [only] piecemeal, now with a rich and full Horn [of plenty] and full hand, as if [you were] too lavish a dispenser of such great riches: so that, just as the Sibyl says in <b>Maro</b> [Virgil] that it befalls few to return from Orcus to the upper world, and to those only</p><p><i>\"whom favouring Jupiter loved, or whose blazing virtue raised to the heavens\"</i> —</p><p>so you too alone have been found, to whom these things have worthily befallen before all; and that you deservedly ought now to bestow with liberal hand on posterity what you have so liberally received. The particle <b><i>Coelitus</b></i> [\"from heaven\"], for <i>In</i> or <i>Sub Coelo</i> [\"in\" or \"under heaven\"], I have always understood, with all authors, of place; and it seems to render the sense more obscure to me than if it be read: <i>\"the stars under the twin axis of heaven.\"</i> But forgive me — by God I beseech you, most excellent Lord — that I write these things so freely; for I give you my hand, I acquiesce in your judgement, I subscribe to your censure, and I desire that you fare well, and that the <b>stable of astronomy</b> be more and more cleansed by you, by your <b>Herculean labours</b>:</p><p><i>And may the things I wish be ratified — may the King of high heaven grant it,</i><br><i>and supply you genius, strength, and life;</i><br><i>may he fill your mind with vigour, and your sinews with strength.</i></p><p>Farewell, then, most excellent Man, Lord and friend, to be cultivated by me with perpetual zeal of due respect.<br>Given at Schweidnitz [Świdnica] of the Silesians, the 18th day of March, in the year of the Christian epoch 1600.<br>Most respectful of Your Excellence and dignity,<br>Tobias Fischer, Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, with his own hand.</p><p>Two globes, a celestial and a terrestrial, were published by Mercator in the year 1541 at Louvain, which I myself recently brought home with my own hands. But I have been informed that a few years ago the same [globes], more corrected, were published at Cologne, and in a still larger format, and that these sheets are not made available anywhere else. I therefore beg you, most affectionately, again and again, to inform me whether this is true, and whether they cannot be procured from those printers — if not through me, then through some man of excellent standing.</p><p>Master Ernestus Hencius, my kinsman, can send his letters to me every week.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Sender.</b> <b>Tobias Fischer</b>, Silesian poet and physician (<i>Philosophiae et Medicinae Doctor</i>), of <b>Schweidnitz (Świdnica)</b> — the <i>\"Doctor Fischer\"</i> of the Monau correspondence (brahe-t_16000110001, brahe-t_16000516001, brahe-t_16000614001), whose commendatory poem Tycho had emended. This is his <b>effusive letter of thanks</b> on being admitted to Tycho's friendship (which Tycho had signalled in his letter to Monau).<br>- <b>Significance — a humanist fan-letter, and a book-request.</b> Beyond the lavish encomium, two concrete points:<br>  1. <b>Request for Tycho's published works.</b> Fischer asks Tycho to have his <b>printed works</b> sent (he has seen \"some, not all,\" the booksellers not stocking such little-valued science), to be delivered to <b>Ernst Heincius</b> — Registrar and Dispatcher of the <b>Imperial Chamber</b> at Prague, <b>Fischer's brother-in-law</b> — or to <b>Monau</b> at Breslau; \"the price will be paid at once.\" He also wants the <b>engraved delineation of Tycho's instruments</b>, which his intimate, the physician <b>Bartholomäus Schwalb</b> (<i>Swalbius</i> — the <i>Schwalmius</i> courier of brahe-t_16000110001), had shown him. Schwalb reported Fischer's verses \"welcome to [Tycho], but <b>recooked to your taste</b>\" — i.e. Tycho's emendation (cf. brahe-t_16000110001/516001).<br>  2. <b>A textual nicety in Tycho's verse.</b> Fischer, \"with your leave,\" questions the word <b><i>Coelitus</b></i> (\"from heaven\") in one of Tycho's verses, preferring (for clearer local sense) <i>\"astra sub coeli gemino axe\"</i> (\"the stars under the twin axis of heaven\") — a courteous scholarly demurral, instantly subordinated to Tycho's judgement.<br>- <b>The verse quotations (kept as verse; protocol §4).</b><br>  - <i>\"Lumine ademto animam moribundo corpore fundam\"</i> — \"when light is taken away, I pour forth my soul from my dying body\" (a hexameter, apparently Fischer's own / adapted), pledging undying friendship.<br>  - <b><i>Virgil, Aeneid</i> VI.129–130</b>: <i>\"…quos aequus amavit / Iuppiter, aut ardens evexit ad aethera virtus\"</i> — \"whom favouring Jupiter loved, or whose blazing virtue raised to heaven\" (the Sibyl, on the few who may return from the underworld) — applied as a figure for Tycho's unique divine gift; the surrounding <i>Sibylla … apud Maronem … ex Orco ad superas revertantur</i> paraphrases the same passage (<i>Aen.</i> VI.126–129).<br>  - The closing tricolon (<i>\"Quaeque opto, rata sint…\"</i>) — Fischer's own verse-prayer for Tycho's genius, strength, and life.<br>  - The <i>astronomiae stabulum … Herculeis tuis laboribus … expurgari</i> figure casts Tycho's reform of astronomy as <b>Hercules cleansing the (Augean) stable</b>.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Tobias Fischer (sender); <b>Jacob Monau</b>; <b>Ernst Heincius</b> (imperial Chamber registrar, Fischer's brother-in-law); <b>Bartholomäus Schwalb</b> (physician). Tycho styled <i>Astronomiae Instaurator</i> (\"Restorer of Astronomy\").<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Suidnicij Silesiorum XIIX [=18] die Martij anno … MDC</i>; Silesia (Schweidnitz) kept the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> by 1600; marked [N.S.].<br>- <b>What it is.</b> The recovered opening of a letter from Tobias Fischer, a physician and philosopher writing from Świdnica (Schweidnitz) in Silesia, to Tycho Brahe, dated 18 March 1600 New Style — the surviving published text is only the closing valediction and verses.<br>- <b>The globes.</b> Fischer reports having personally brought home a set of Gerard Mercator's celebrated 1541 Louvain celestial and terrestrial globes, and asks Tycho to confirm a rumour of an improved, larger Cologne reissue of restricted distribution. Adam Mosley's <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> quotes this identical passage independently (drawing on the printed Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia) in a discussion of Tycho's own instrument- and globe-collecting around 1600 [Tier 1: Mosley, <i>Bearing the Heavens</i> (2007)] — strong independent corroboration of both text and sense.<br>- <b>Context.</b> Mercator produced this globe pair at Louvain within the circle of Gemma Frisius and the other Louvain cosmographers who, a decade later, also shaped John Dee's training — the same intellectual milieu behind Sophia's \"Louvain Core Group\" concept in Dee studies, though this letter itself carries no Dee connection.<br>- <b>Ernestus Hencius.</b> Fischer names \"D. Ernestus Hencius,\" his kinsman (\"affinis meus\"), as a weekly courier for correspondence between them; no independent identification of this person has been found in the corpus — worth flagging as an open prosopographical lead.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — florid but fully construable humanist Latin, read securely; the substance (the thanks for admission to friendship; the request for Tycho's printed works and instrument-plate via Heincius or Monau; Schwalb's report and the emended verses; the courteous <i>Coelitus</i>-vs-<i>sub coelo</i> textual demurral) is clear, and the verse quotations are identified (the <b>Aeneid VI</b> Sibyl passage; Fischer's own lines) and kept as verse per §4. No textual crux.</p><p>High confidence — the Latin is plain and the specific content (the Mercator globes, the rumoured Cologne reissue) is corroborated by an independent modern scholarly quotation of the same passage in Mosley 2007, drawing on the printed Opera Omnia. No crux in the translation. Ernestus Hencius remains unidentified; that is a gap in the apparatus, not in the translation itself.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter and both trailing postscripts against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.267-270 (page images, read visually). Page 271 was confirmed to belong to the next letter (Iessenius to Tycho, 14/24 March 1600), so nothing of this letter falls outside the read range. Every name, number and date checked (Monavij, Ernestum Heincium / Ernestus Hencius, Bartholomaeus Swalbius, the Virgil tag 'quos aequus amavit Iuppiter...', Mercatore anno 1541 Lovanij / Coloniae, Dabam Suidnicij XIIX die Martij anno epochae Christianorum MDC) matches Dreyer exactly. No defects found; this independently confirms the pre-existing [A] grade rather than merely accepting it on the worker's say-so.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Tobias Fischer", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000516002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000516002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Expresses joy at learning through Monau that his unsolicited letter was well received and that Tycho has admitted him to his circle of friends.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000318001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000321001", "d": "1600-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Sophie Brahe", "ck": "Sophie Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Sophie Brahe — Benátky nad Jizerou, 21 March 1600 [N.S.] (= 11 March O.S.)", "m": "<p>Language: Early Modern Danish (personal/chancery), with Latin tags · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 42–51 (Kladde / Tycho's own draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO AD SOPHIAM BRAHE].</p><p>KIERRE søster Sophi, Gud allmechtigste werre nu och allthiid hoß dig och beuare dig naadelligen och well fra allt det, som dig skadellig kan werre, och nest megen thacksigelse for allt beuist gode, for huilket du althiid skall finde mig som din hulde broder, der gierne giør huis dig thill aere och gode kan werre. Bekom ieg din skriffuelße met min søn Tyge nogit epther nøtaarsthag, endog du icke da haffde bekommit det lange breff, som ieg skreff dig thill met hannom, for du icke da warst thilstede wdj Danmarck, menß ieg tencker well, att du det siden haffuer bekomit och nochsom forfarit deraff min leylighedt, saa ieg achter icke fornøden att repetere nogit deraff vdigien. Och fornam ieg aff samme din skriffuellse, att du warst wddragen y Lantheholsten och thill Hamborg met din søn Thage, hannom widre paa sin forfremning thill Heidelberg eller Basell att hensende, huilket mig war kiert att høre, att han en gang kunde komme vd och forsøge sig, och haffuer hand schreffuit mig thill nogitt for jull fra Heidelberg och ladet mig wide, att hannom der lidet well och att han achtid sig met skolmestern nu paa foraarid thill Bassel, och ieg skreff hannom beskening och min mening thillbage vdigien, och commenderit hannom die fornembste lerde mend, som ieg der war bekient medt. Han lod mig ocsaa wiide, hor wit du følte hannom och huem der kom thill dig och hort y siiden droge, dog han wiste icke nogen eyglig besked, hor wit det warede, menß du giffuer mig well selff baade det och andit y din skriffuelße thillkiende.</p><p>Och som du formelder yblant andit y samme dit breff om hueß ord du och Mercurius haffuer [hafft] met hinanden wdj Landskrone, och han derudjblant skulde haffde sagt nogit om keysern, som dig icke skulde behage, huilket ieg gierne wille wiide huad dett war, thi ieg wed inted, han met sandhedt kunde sige om hanß keys. may., som dig for min skyld kunde ilde behage. Ieg tacker den gode Gudt, som haffuer forsønit mig saa god en from och mild herre vdj hannom, saa att ieg gierne lader mig nøye dermedt. Hanss maytt. beuisser mig icke alleniste allt gott och lader mig intid fatiß, menß occesaa haffuer naadigst aff sig selff lige som en faderlige omhu for mig, huilket hanß may. lod nocksom paakenne y denne forgangen aars store søgdom och skrøffuellighed, som wanckede her vdj landit, thi hanß may. haffde nogle gange bod hid och lod forfare, horledis det haffde sig met mig och mine, och wilde, dersom mig saa behagede, att ieg begaff mig medt mine hen thill Wien y Osterich, hor hanß may. wilde vdj thiide lade forordne mig god verrelße och vnderholdning met megen anden keyserlig øngist och naade, som hanß maytt. aff sin egen fromme och naadigst wille stedze beuiser mig, och lader forskaffue mig huad ieg begerer, och lader yblant medt forskicke mig wildbrad aff hiorte, hinde och wilde swin, och der thillig attskillige leffuendiß ferske fisk vdj kiøckennit. Haffuer och nøligen naadigst depoterit mig en bequem ladegaar her nehr wed slottid, hor ieg kand lade holde fennid och soe saa megit thill aff allehonde slags, som ieg mest kan offuerkomme; er och nogen smocke fiskewan derhos, will och wnde mig en smock haffue och wingaar her wed slotid att haffue nøtte aff och forløste mig y. Saa att hanß may. giør mig aff sin egen naadigst wille mehre thill gode, end ieg selff thortte begierre, endog hanß may. haffuer endnu inted sønderlig set aff min handell, thi mine fornemste jnstrumenter erre endnu thillbage vdj Tydskland, for die icke kunde komme hid op formedelst Elben førend wintern er offuerstanden. Nu haffuer hanß may. paa nø skreffuit thill Magdeburg derudom, saa att ieg wentter dennom met første mulighed. Siden achter hanß may. sig hid thill slottid att besee dennom och min anden handell, bade met distelering och andit, thi hanß may. haffuer en sønderlige løst och behagellighed thill allt saadant. Och thillspurte hanß may. for nogen wger siden sin øffuerste secreterer, h. Baruitio, som er hanß factotum, met huilken han mest gider thallt, om han inted haffde aff mig, som han kunde lade hannom see; da sagde han, att ieg nølig haffde sent hannom en aff mine bøyer, som icke endnu war alldelis ferdig trøckt, och wilde wiide hanß raad, om ieg motte didicere hanß may. den vnderdanigst; daa motte han strax hentte hanom samme bog, som kaldis Astronomiae Instauratae progymnasmata, och keysern haffde den saa hoß sig lenger end y fiorten dage, och leste thit och megit der y, och behagit hannom saare well, och befoll att sige mig, att han enderlig wille haffue, att ieg ingen anden skulde dedicere den end hanß maytt. selff, och skulde sette derudj for alle die anden preueleger en epistel thill hanß maytt. selff, och att ieg skulle lade thengne neden for titellen, att den war begønt vdj Danmarck, menß fuldkommit och vdgaait her paa Benatky y sitt kongerige Behemen. Och lod ocsaa sige mig, huad werß hanß may. willde haffue wdhuggen y marmullsteen offuer die døre paa die thuende portaller, som giørris thill hueß hanß may. laader her bøge for mig, det ene thill hueß der muriß thill itt sønderligt obseruatorium att sette alle mine jnstrumenter ordentlich och paa ree vdj, saa ad itt huert kan haffue sitt vnderskedlig rom att staa vdj, det andit thill itt sønderligt laboratorium, saa att hanß may. kan paa den ene side gange ned aff det huß, som staar ledigt thill hanß maytt. att werre wdj, naar han kommer hid, och gange saa igienom dette alltsammen och besee huad hanß maytt. løster. Och ieg kan occsaa aff dett andit huß, standendiß paa den anden siide, huilket ieg alldeliß haffuer inde, gange ned igienom laboratoriumit och siden paa ree thill alle jnstrumenterne. Och haffuer skriffueren, som stander for regenskaffuit, beregnit, att hueß allerede her thill saadant er giort, skulle haffue kostid icke langt fra thuthussend thaller, saa att dett dog icke nehr hellten er ferdigt for winterenß skyld, som paakom och war meget horer end den pleier att werre her vdj landit, saa man icke endnu well kan mure vde y lufften. Saadant formelder ieg, paa det du kanst wijde, att det seder ingenlunde saa sammen, som Mercurius wille, att du skulde tro, och war enda megit andit att berette derimod, om pennen kunde det tholle och det icke wilde bliffue forlangt. Och det han sagde, att tho aff die, som warre mine beste wenner y keyserenß gaar, die warre affsatt och vdj wnaade, det er lige saa probatum, som det andit, thi keisern haffuer ingen aff sine raad affsatt, er icke heller saa lett thill att giørre saadant, om die end skønt kunde forskylde dett, saa att ieg icke kunde wiide, huem eller huad hand mente dermed, andit end att han kunde sige nogit, som dig kunde fortrøde, och danne det epther sin egen affect. Keiserens och rigens vicecanceller, her Coraducius, som skreff først epther mig aff keyserens befalling, haffuer mesten parten werrit her aff landitt y en legation for keysern thill Rom, och der hand først for nogen vger siden war komen thill keysern wdjgien thill en bø wed naffn Pilsen, 15 mille herfra, hor keiseren haffuer werrit och endnu er siden sist y augusto y dette forgangene aar for søgdom skyld, som vdj Prag och ellers mesten alle wegne haffuer suarligen regierit, och der ieg nogit epther same her Coraducius war ankommen haffde en aff mine folck did thill Pilsen med nogit, som keysern skulde haffue, gick same min absender thill her Coraducium och grøsid hanom paa mine wegne och congratulerid hannom, att han war saa løkelligen och well aff den lange reise kommen hid hiem ygien. Huad han da suarede, kanst du forfare aff denne hoßliggendis fortengnelße, som er vddragen aff itt breff, den samme, som ieg vdskickede, skreff mig thill medlerthid menß han laa der. Och denne h. Coraducius er den fornembste aff keyserns inderste raad, som allt det rømiske riges første bestillinger ligger paa; huad han siger och will haffue frem, giører die andre icke lettelig imodt, ei heller thager keysern nogens andere ord derfor, naar en thingest behoffuer hannom selff well, saa ad Mercurius epther sin angeborne wstadig complex slog feill daa y sin thallenhedt. Det andit, som du skriffuer om den, der skulle haffue bekommit de bøyer du weest, och nu bereter anderledis end thillforn, derpaa giør ieg min gietzing, att dett seder anderlediß sammen, och adt de første ord erre throlliger, de siste kun att komme aff det andit hussis betødelser, store och waffladellig oefftro [?], saa att alle baade hielper, yhor ringe die erre, huilket man for att haffue gedult met och dessimulere, dog kanst du well thill sin thiid laade so himellige forfare formedelst nogen anden, horledes det seder sammen dermedt, thi dersom bøyerne er der, som hand sagde, findis die well der endnu; en aff die høylerte vdj Kiøbenhaffn pleier att forware nøiellen derthill, saa att dette er lettelligen att forfare. Det er mig lige got, hor samme bøyer erre, nar mine auffuendsmen och wuenner kun icke haffuer faait dennom, dog saa ieg helst, att du haffde dennom y din foruaring. Den samme wilde icke werdis thill att suare mig paa mit breff, som ieg skreff hanom thill med min søn, for ieg trode hanom well frem for nogen anden derudj raadit, och erlangit det ocsaa, att herr Baruitius skreff hanom thill epther keyse[r]ns befalling, paa mine weigne, huilket hand dog ellers icke lettelligen giør; hanom wille hand heller inted suare. Endog han sagde thill min søn, att hand wilde sende breffuene, die ieg skulle haffue, vd thil hertzog Vldrigß kanseller docter Bordingius, saa haffuer ieg dog endnu intedt fornumit derthill, menß det maa saa pasere; han haffuer saa mange breffue, som han bekomer aff mig. Der wille icke heller den lille Mons Bertelsen fordemøde sig att suare mig nogit paa mit bref, som ieg fortroeligen haffde skreffuit hannom thill, horudom ieg och nogit formeltte y min siste skriffuelse thill dig; kandske hand torde icke for den forbemelte planetis skyld, huilken formedelst sin wstadighed och wfixitet er icke att bøgge paa, menß tør slage hannom feil, førend hand troer det.</p><p>Det du skriffuer om Titan, saa er det mig kiert att høre, att han kunde komme thillrette met hueß hand sig haffuer forretagit, och wille ieg gierne wijde ret besken derom, horledis det sad sammen dermet. Jeg haffuer inted endnu kundid fornummit, att Hans Ernst skulde haffue skreffuet keysern nogit thill derom, och war det icke heller radelligt, att man giorde saadant, førend det war aldelis nochsom proberit, att konsten war wiß, och att der war nogen fordell hoß, thi der haffuer werrit saa mange, som haffuer giffuit saadant forre hoß keysern, och dog inted hafft paa sig, saa hand tror det icke nu letelligen, eyheller kunde hanß inderste raad, som leser hues breffue hannom thillskriffuis, well fordrage, att hand ganger om met saadant, eptherdj der er sked stor bekaastning thill forgeffuis thillforn derudoffuer. Och en ell[er] 2 aff dennom haffuer macht att thage breffuene op, som hannom thillskriffuis, och dersom die indeholder nogit, som dennom icke behager, daa thier die stille dermedt och lader hannom inted faa det att wiide; det sker well y støre handell end dette kan werre. Derfor om saadant war wist, och slet ingen feil derpaa, daa skulde en, som hans may. gad thallt met och trode well, sigen hans may. det muntligen, saa ingen wiste deraff, eller och det skulde skriffuis hans may. thill, indelugt y en sedell vdj itt beseillet breff, saa for die andre inted att wijde, huad sedellen indeholder, om det behaget hannom, menß han lader dennom kun siden faa breffuit att see, om hand will. Saaledis pleier ieg att giøre, nar ieg haffuer nogit att giffue hans may. thillkiende, som enten hans may. selff eller mig [er] macht paa liggendis; thi hans may. will det saa haffue, och der maa ingen thage mit breff op, naar der staar skreffuit vden paa: Ad Caesareas Maiestatis manus proprias. Derfor wiste ieg well god lempe och rod derthill, att hans maytt. skulde fange ret besken derom att wijde, saa ingen skulde merke det, dersom ieg war thillforn nochsom wiß derpaa, att der war slet ingen mangell y sagen, thi ellers thorde ieg slet inted giffue det forre, enten muntligen eller skrifftligen; thi hans may. thror den icke mehre well, som engang falerer hannom, for hand er fix och bestandig y hanß handell. Die wijde well, att ieg vnderthiiden y saa made skriffuer hans may. thill, och hans may. det saa will haffue, derfor motte ieg nochsom høre nu ieg war nøligen vdj Prag, baade hos die høye och lauffue, ligesom ieg skulle haffue skreffuit hans may. thill och raad hannom aff, att hand icke skulde komme thill Prag førend paaske war gangen, thi der war fare hos. Och det kunde icke hielpe, att ieg sagde ney derfor, thi det er y sandhed icke sked, ieg saa selff gierne, att hans may. wilde snart komme did; die mente, att ieg icke wilde besta det, for hand haffde forbød mig att lade andre wijde saadant, och somme mente, att det war ret giørt, att ieg warit hannom ad, hues ieg wiste. Somme, der haffuer profit och gaffn aff, naar hand er thill Prag, fortrød det och tog mig det thill møstøcke, saa att ieg matte lade vndskulde mig thill hoffue, att saadant inted war sked, for die andre raad skyld, der er, som helder wilde werre wdj Prag end ligge der saa lenge thill Pilsen, som keysern er, thi die haffuer icke der saa god gelegenheid. Menß folck skall thalle, for fee haffuer icke lert det.</p><p>Det Titan lod skrifftlig thillbøde kongen der y landit kønsten, war icke forsicktellig giort aff hannom, wden hand haffde nogen anden mening dermed; ieg wiste well, att bode han och die andre, som hand mest tror, skulle kun werre spocsk derpaa och haffe vidunder deraff, och Titan ingen suar bekome der paa igien, thi naar personen er suspect och forhadit, da er det suspect och ilde tagit huad den forregiffuer, om det war endskønt ret och sandingen probatum est. Jeg willde, att du sende mig en copi aff samme Titans suplicas for ieg wilde wijde, huad hand derudj haffde erindret kongen. War ieg som hand, da wilde ieg inted bemøde dennom mehre, och saa litt thencke paa dennom, som die paa mig. Ieg haffuer y lang thiid ingen breffue bekommit fra hannom, endog ieg to eller tre gange haffuer skreffuit hanom thill, wed icke heller thillwisse, hor han nu er, menß du skriffuer mig well thill allt, huiß dig er wittelligt om hannom och hans handell, thi ieg lengis effter att wijde dett. Och skriff mig occsaa thill allt, hues du west, som mig kan angaa, och hues der siges vdj landit, bode aff wenner och wuenner, siden die haffuer fa[n]git min leylighed och wilkaar att wijde, anderledis end die kandske thillforn haffde ment. Och lad mig ocsaa wijde, horledis det haffuer sig med voris venner och kønninger; och dersom nogen will skriffue mig nogit thill, da lad denom wijde, att dig nu stedis bod, huilket du ocsaa kanst giffue woris søster Margrette wdj thiide thillkiende, om hun will skriffue mig nogen suar paa mit siste breff, och lad mig wiide, horledis det ganger hindes øngste søner, siiden die er komen thill hoffe, och ocsesaa om voriß moster datter sønner, die vnge Longer, som ieg hører ocsaa att werre vdj gaaren. Gud beuare dennom alle fra det, som skadelligt kand werre. Och forglem ellers inted andit aff hues du west, som ieg wille wiide, som du icke heller pleier att giørre, thi du west well, att ieg well gieder lest lange breffue heller end att leye vdj kort, och eptherdj att ieg ocsaa wed, att du kanst fordrage det, skriffuer ieg dig diß witløfftiger thill, thi herr ganger icke huer dag bod emellom. Dog will ieg icke nu lenger opholde dig, mens nu och allthiid haffue dig Gud allmechtigste befallit, hand beuare dig och dine naadelligen och well fran allt ont; giør well och sig voris morbrøder sønner och døttre met alle andre voris gode wenner mange gode netter paa mine wegne. Claus Bilde Stensøn war her hos mig paa en fieringaars thiid att giøre, siden drog han ind wdj Franckerig, och fuldis mester Nielß Hammer met hanom, som var komen hid y same thid, som kan sprokit och kenner nogen aff folken der vdj landit. Hand loffuede mig att wilde flittelligen tage vare paa Clauß och ramme hans gaffn och beste hor hannom muegligt er, siden haffuer ieg ingen bod bekommit fraa dennom, och haffuer ieg skreffuit hans moder itt breff till derom, huilket du kanst forskaffue hinder. Sig ocsaa jomfru Dorete mange gode netter paa mine vegne.</p><p>Iterum et saepius quam felicissime vale.<br>Daterit paa det keyserlige slott Benatky den 21 dag martij, som er den 11 hoß eder, anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho to Sophie Brahe.]</b></p><p>Dear sister Sophie, may God Almighty be now and always with you, and guard you graciously and well from all that may be harmful to you; and next, with much thanksgiving for all the kindness you have shown — for which you shall always find me your devoted brother, who gladly does whatever may be for your honour and good. I received your letter by my son Tyge somewhat after New Year's Day, though you had not then received the long letter I wrote to you by him, because you were not at that time present in Denmark; but I well believe that you have since received it, and learned sufficiently from it of my situation, so that I think it not needful to repeat any of it again. And I gathered from that same letter of yours that you had travelled into Holstein and to Hamburg with your son Tage, to send him onward, for his advancement, to Heidelberg or Basel — which I was glad to hear, that he might at last get out into the world and try himself; and he wrote to me somewhat before Christmas from Heidelberg, and let me know that he did well there, and that he intended to go with his schoolmaster now in the spring to Basel; and I wrote him back word and my opinion in reply, and commended to him the foremost learned men with whom I was acquainted there. He also let me know how far you accompanied him, and who came to you, and whither you then journeyed — though he knew no exact account of how long it lasted; but you yourself will surely make known to me both that and other things in your letter.</p><p>And whereas you mention, among other things in that same letter of yours, the words you and Mercurius had with one another at Landskrona — and that among them he was said to have said something about the Emperor that did not please you, which I should gladly know what it was; for I know nothing that he could truthfully say about His Imperial Majesty that could, for my sake, displease you. I thank the good God, who has provided me so good, pious, and gentle a lord in him, that I gladly content myself therewith. His Majesty not only shows me every kindness and lets me lack nothing, but also has, of his own grace, as it were a fatherly care for me — which His Majesty let be sufficiently felt in this past year's great sickness and pestilence that went about here in the land; for His Majesty several times sent here and had inquiry made how it stood with me and mine, and wished, if it so pleased me, that I should betake myself with my people away to Vienna in Austria, where His Majesty would in good time have good lodging and maintenance arranged for me, with much other imperial favour and grace — as His Majesty, of his own goodness and most gracious will, constantly shows me, and has procured for me whatever I desire, and among other things has game sent to me, of hart, hind, and wild boar, and therewith sundry living fresh fish for the kitchen. He has also lately most graciously assigned me a convenient grange here near the castle, where I may keep cattle and sows, and as much of all kinds as I can manage; there is also some fine fishing-water with it; and he will grant me too a fine garden and vineyard here by the castle, to have profit from and to take my pleasure in. So that His Majesty, of his own most gracious will, does me more good than I myself durst desire — even though His Majesty has as yet seen nothing in particular of my work, for my chief instruments are still back in Germany, since they could not come up here by the Elbe before the winter is past. Now His Majesty has written anew to Magdeburg about it, so that I expect them at the first opportunity. Afterwards His Majesty intends to come here to the castle, to view them and my other work, both with distilling and otherwise, for His Majesty has a special delight and pleasure in all such things. And some weeks ago His Majesty asked his chief secretary, Lord Barvitius — who is his factotum, with whom he most likes to talk — whether he had nothing of mine that he could let him see; then he said that I had lately sent him one of my books, which was not yet quite finished printing, and that I wished his advice whether I might most humbly dedicate it to His Majesty; whereupon he had at once to fetch him that same book, which is called <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i>; and the Emperor kept it by him longer than a fortnight, and read often and much in it, and it pleased him exceedingly, and he commanded that I be told that he absolutely wished I should dedicate it to none other than His Majesty himself, and that I should set in it, before all the other privileges, an epistle to His Majesty himself, and that I should have it noted below the title that it was begun in Denmark, but completed and issued here at Benátky in his kingdom of Bohemia. And he also let me be told what verses His Majesty wished to have carved in marble over the doors on the two portals that are being made for the building His Majesty is having built here for me: the one for what is being raised as a special observatory, to set all my instruments in, in order and in a row, so that each may have its own distinct space to stand in; the other for a special laboratory — so that His Majesty may, on the one side, go down from the house that stands ready for His Majesty to lodge in when he comes here, and so pass through all this and view whatever His Majesty pleases. And I too can, from the other house standing on the other side, which I have entirely to myself, go down through the laboratory and then in a row to all the instruments. And the clerk in charge of the accounts has reckoned that what has already been done here toward this must have cost not far from two thousand thalers, so that it is yet not nearly half finished, on account of the winter, which came on and was much harder than it is wont to be here in this land, so that one cannot yet well do masonry out in the open air. I report such things so that you may know that matters by no means stand as Mercurius would have you believe; and there were yet much else to relate against it, if the pen could bear it and it would not grow too long. And as for his saying that two of those who were my best friends at the Emperor's court were dismissed and in disgrace — that is just as well \"proven\" as the rest; for the Emperor has dismissed none of his councillors, nor is it so easy to do such a thing, even should they deserve it; so that I could not tell whom or what he meant by it, other than that he could say something to vex you, and shape it after his own bent. The Emperor's and the Empire's Vice-Chancellor, Lord Coraducius — who first wrote for me, by the Emperor's command — has for the most part been away from the country on a legation for the Emperor to Rome; and when, some weeks ago, he had first come back to the Emperor, to a town by the name of Pilsen, 15 [Danish] miles from here, where the Emperor has been and still is since the end of August in this past year, on account of the sickness which has raged grievously in Prague and almost everywhere else — and when, somewhat after this same Lord Coraducius had arrived, I had sent one of my people to Pilsen with something the Emperor was to have, this same envoy of mine went to Lord Coraducius and greeted him on my behalf, and congratulated him that he had come home again so happily and well from the long journey. What he then answered, you may learn from this enclosed memorandum, excerpted from a letter that the same man I dispatched wrote to me meanwhile, while he was lying there. And this Lord Coraducius is the foremost of the Emperor's innermost council, upon whom all the chief business of the Roman Empire rests; what he says and wishes to advance, the others do not easily oppose; nor does the Emperor take anyone else's word against it, when a matter closely concerns himself — so that Mercurius, after his inborn inconstant temperament, missed the mark in his talkativeness. The other thing you write, about the one who was to have received the books you know of, and who now reports otherwise than before: on that I make my guess that the matter stands otherwise, and that the first words are the more trustworthy, the later ones springing only from the other house's promptings — great and unceasing self-conceit [reading uncertain] — so that all are helpers in it, however slight they be; which one must have patience with and dissemble. Yet you may well in due time have it secretly inquired into, through someone else, how the matter stands; for if the books are there, as he said, they will surely still be found there — one of the learned men in Copenhagen is wont to keep the key to it, so that this is easy to ascertain. It is all the same to me where those books are, so long as my enviers and enemies have not got them — though I should best like that you had them in your keeping. The same man would not deign to answer me on my letter, which I wrote to him by my son (for I trusted him well, above any other in the council), and I obtained it, too, that Lord Barvitius wrote to him by the Emperor's command, on my behalf — which he otherwise does not easily do; him too he would rather not answer. Although he told my son that he would send the letters I was to have out to Duke Ulrich's chancellor, Doctor Bording, yet I have still heard nothing of it; but let it pass — he has so many letters that he gets from me. Nor would little Mogens Bertelsen deign to answer me anything on my letter, which I had written to him in confidence, concerning which I also mentioned something in my last letter to you; perhaps he durst not, for the sake of the aforesaid planet, which, by reason of its inconstancy and unfixedness, is not to be built upon, but may fail him before he expects it.</p><p>What you write about Titan — it is dear to me to hear that he could come to rights with what he has undertaken; and I should gladly know the true account of it, how the matter stood. I have not yet been able to learn that Hans Ernst should have written anything to the Emperor about it; nor would it be advisable that one do such a thing before it were quite sufficiently proven that the art was certain, and that there was some profit in it; for there have been so many who have proposed such things to the Emperor, and yet had nothing in them, so that he does not now easily believe it; nor could his innermost council, who read whatever letters are written to him, well endure that he busy himself with such things, since great expense has been incurred in vain over this before. And one or two of them have authority to open the letters written to him; and if they contain anything that does not please them, they keep silent about it and let him learn nothing of it — that happens even in greater matters than this can be. Therefore, if such a thing were certain, and no fault at all in it, then someone whom His Majesty liked to talk with and trusted well should tell His Majesty it by word of mouth, so that no one knew of it; or else it should be written to His Majesty, enclosed in a note within a sealed letter, so that the others should not know what the note contains, if it pleased him — while he afterwards lets them merely see the [outer] letter, if he will. Thus I am wont to do, when I have something to make known to His Majesty that is of importance either to His Majesty himself or to me; for His Majesty will have it so, and no one may open my letter when there stands written on the outside: <i>To His Imperial Majesty's own hands.</i> Therefore I should well know good means and counsel for His Majesty to come to the true account of it, so that no one should notice it — provided I were beforehand sufficiently sure of it, that there was no defect at all in the matter; for otherwise I durst by no means propose it, either by mouth or in writing, for His Majesty no longer trusts well the man who once fails him, since he is fixed and constant in his dealings. They know well that I sometimes write to His Majesty in this way, and that His Majesty will have it so; therefore I had to hear enough, now that I was lately in Prague, both from high and low, as though I had written to His Majesty and advised him against coming to Prague before Easter was past, because there was danger about. And it could not help that I said no to it, for it has truly not happened: I myself would gladly that His Majesty would soon come thither. They thought I would not admit it, because he had forbidden me to let others know such things; and some thought it rightly done that I should warn him of what I knew. Some, who have profit and gain when he is at Prague, resented it and took it amiss of me, so that I had to excuse myself at court — that no such thing had happened — for the sake of the other councillors, [some of] whom would rather be in Prague than lie so long there at Pilsen where the Emperor is, for they have not such good accommodation there. But people must talk, for cattle have not learned how.</p><p>That Titan offered the King in that country the art in writing was not prudently done of him — unless he had some other purpose by it; I knew well that both he and the others whom he most trusts would only be mocking about it and make a jest of it, and that Titan would get no answer to it in return; for when the person is suspect and hated, then whatever he proposes is suspect and ill-received, even though it were right and the truth <i>demonstrated.</i> I should wish that you send me a copy of that same supplication of Titan's, for I should like to know what he had reminded the King of in it. Were I as he, I would trouble them no more, and think as little of them as they of me. I have for a long time received no letters from him, though I have written to him two or three times; nor do I know for certain where he now is; but you will surely write me all that is known to you about him and his affairs, for I long to know it. And write me also all that you know that may concern me, and what is said in the land, both by friends and enemies, now that they have learned my situation and circumstances — otherwise than they perhaps had thought before. And let me also know how it stands with our friends and kindred; and if anyone wishes to write me anything, let them know that a messenger is now available to you — which you may also make known in time to our sister Margrethe, if she will write me any answer to my last letter; and let me know how it goes with her youngest sons, now that they have come to court, and also about our mother's-sister's-daughter's sons, the young Langes, who I hear are also at the court. God guard them all from what may be harmful. And forget nothing else of what you know that I should wish to know — as indeed you are not wont to do — for you know well that I would gladly read long letters rather than be put off with short ones; and since I also know that you can bear it, I write to you the more at length, for here a messenger does not pass between us every day. Yet I will not now detain you longer, but now and always have commended you to God Almighty; may He guard you and yours graciously and well from all evil. Fare well, and bid our maternal uncles' sons and daughters, with all our other good friends, many good nights on my behalf. Claus Bilde, Sten's son, was here with me for about a quarter-year's stay; then he went into France, and Master Niels Hammer accompanied him — who had come here at the same time, who knows the language and is acquainted with some of the people there in that country. He promised me that he would diligently look after Claus and seek his advantage and best wherever possible for him; since then I have had no word from them; and I have written his mother a letter about it, which you can convey to her. Bid Mistress Dorthe, too, many good nights on my behalf.</p><p>Again, and oftentimes, fare most happily.<br>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky, the 21st day of March — which is the 11th with you — the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung Vol. 35, Fol. 42–51, marked <i>Kladde</i> — i.e. <b>Tycho's own draft/concept</b>, not the dispatched fair copy. (The heading <i>[Tycho ad Sophiam Brahe]</i> is the editor's.)</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> The dateline is itself a textbook O.S./N.S. statement: <i>\"den 21 dag martij, som er den 11 hoß eder\"</i> — \"the 21st of March, which is the 11th with you,\" Tycho spelling out the 10-day Julian/Gregorian gap between his Imperial-court (New Style) dating and his sister's Danish (Old Style) reckoning. The catalogue label <i>\"1600. 21 marts n.s.\"</i> agrees. Written from <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (<i>Arx Benatica</i>). This is the Danish family-register parallel to the Latin letter <b>brahe-t_16000324002</b> (to Niels Krag, 24 March N.S.) — the same week, the same news (plague, the Vienna offer, the instruments stranded in Germany, the <i>Progymnasmata</i> dedication, Coraduz, the Prague-before-Easter rumour), here in the intimate vernacular.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Sophie Brahe (1559–1643)</b>, Tycho's youngest sister — the learned \"Urania\" of the family (astronomy, medicine, horticulture, genealogy), Tycho's lifelong confidante and sometime observing companion. Widow of Otte Thott of Eriksholm.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><b>Tyge / \"my son\"</b> — Tycho Brahe the Younger, the courier.</li><li><b>Tage</b> — <b>Tage Ottesen Thott</b> (1580–1658), Sophie's son by Otte Thott, sent via Holstein/Hamburg to study at Heidelberg and Basel; later a leading Scanian magnate.</li><li><b>Mercurius</b> — a derisive <b>code-name</b> for an unidentified person who spoke with Sophie at <b>Landskrona</b> and disparaged the Emperor/Tycho's standing; Tycho rebuts his gossip point by point and puns on the planet Mercury — <i>\"after his inborn inconstant temperament … the aforesaid planet, which, by reason of its inconstancy and unfixedness, is not to be built upon.\"</i> Identity not recoverable from the text.</li><li><b>the Emperor / His Majesty</b> — <b>Rudolf II</b> (here at Pilsen, sheltering from plague), whose \"fatherly care,\" gifts of game and fish, grant of a grange, garden and vineyard, and reading of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> are recounted with evident warmth.</li><li><b>Lord Barvitius</b> — <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>, Rudolf II's chief secretary and <i>factotum</i> (cf. brahe-t_16000324002).</li><li><b>Lord Coraduz (<i>Coraducius</i>)</b> — <b>Rudolf von Coraduz</b>, Imperial Vice-Chancellor (<i>Reichsvizekanzler</i>), who first wrote summoning Tycho to Bohemia on the Emperor's command; back from a legation to Rome, now at Pilsen; \"the foremost of the Emperor's innermost council.\" (The standing pairing Coraduz/Barvitius recurs across the imperial-period correspondence.)</li><li><b>\"the one who was to have received the books you know of\"</b> — the keeper of Tycho's presentation books left locked in <b>Copenhagen</b> (the key held by \"one of the learned men in Copenhagen\") — the same book-deposit business as the Holger Rosenkrantz letter <b>brahe-t_15980810001</b>; the man now reports inconsistently. Left unnamed by Tycho.</li><li><b>Duke Ulrich's chancellor, Doctor Bording</b> — chancellor of <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b> (cf. brahe-t_15980816001), an intended forwarding-conduit for Tycho's letters.</li><li><b>little Mogens Bertelsen (<i>Mons Bertelsen</i>)</b> — a Danish correspondent who failed to answer Tycho's confidential letter; not further identified.</li><li><b>Titan</b> — an alchemical <b>code-name</b> for someone pursuing a \"<i>kønst</i>\" (the <i>art</i> = transmutation) and offering it in writing, by a supplication, to the King of Denmark. Given the alchemical register and Sophie Brahe's circle, this is <b>most plausibly Erik Lange</b> (Sophie's betrothed, the gold-seeking alchemist who beggared himself in the <i>magnum opus</i>); <i>identification MEDIUM — kept as the code-name in the translation.</i> <b>Hans Ernst</b> (unidentified) is named as a possible intermediary to the Emperor for the same \"art.\"</li><li><b>the King</b> — <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>, to whom \"Titan\" addressed his supplication; Tycho foresees only mockery, since a \"suspect and hated\" petitioner gets no hearing \"even though it were right and <i>demonstrated.</i>\"</li><li><b>sister Margrethe (<i>Margrette</i>)</b> — another Brahe sister; her \"youngest sons\" newly at court.</li><li><b>\"the young Langes\" (<i>die vnge Longer</i>)</b> — kinsmen (sons of a maternal cousin) at court; family of the Lange house (the same house as Erik Lange).</li><li><b>Claus Bilde, Sten's son (<i>Claus Bilde Stensøn</i>)</b> — young Danish noble who lodged ~3 months with Tycho, then travelled to France; <b>Master Niels Hammer</b> his tutor-companion.</li><li><b>Mistress Dorthe (<i>jomfru Dorete</i>)</b> — a member of Sophie's household/kin, greeted in closing.</li><li><b>Bibliographic detail — the <i>Progymnasmata</i> dedication (important).</b> Tycho reports Rudolf II's express commands regarding the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (Tycho's great work on the 1572 nova and solar/lunar theory): (1) sole <b>dedication to the Emperor himself</b>, (2) an <b>epistle to His Majesty placed before all the privileges</b>, and (3) a <b>title-note that the work was \"begun in Denmark, but completed and issued at Benátky in his kingdom of Bohemia.\"</b> A primary-source datum on the genesis of the printed <i>Progymnasmata</i> (which was in fact completed and printed posthumously, 1602, by Tycho's heirs at Prague). The \"<i>two portals</i>\" verses to be carved in marble over the observatory and laboratory doors are the same imperial commission described to Krag in brahe-t_16000324002 (the <i>\"Dania quae renuit\"</i> epigrams).</li><li><b>The confidential-letter protocol (a vivid procedural detail).</b> Tycho describes how he by-passes the screening council: matters of importance go to the Emperor by word of mouth through a trusted intimate, or as a <b>sealed note inside a sealed letter</b> endorsed <i>Ad Caesareae Maiestatis manus proprias</i> (\"to His Imperial Majesty's own hands\"), which no councillor may open — and notes that one or two councillors otherwise have authority to open and suppress incoming letters. This privilege is the very thing that fuelled the (false) rumour, retailed in Prague \"from high and low,\" that Tycho had astrologically advised the Emperor to stay away from plague-struck Prague until after Easter.</li><li><b>Latin tags.</b> <i>probatum (est)</i> — \"proven / demonstrated\" (twice, used ironically of Mercurius's tales and earnestly of Titan's \"art\"); <i>Ad Caesareae Maiestatis manus proprias</i> — the letter-endorsement formula; the valediction <i>Iterum et saepius quam felicissime vale</i> — \"Again, and oftentimes, fare most happily.\" Proverb: <i>\"folk must talk, for cattle have not learned how\"</i> — Tycho's wry dismissal of court gossip.</li><li><b>Reading crux.</b> <i>\"de siste kun att komme aff det andit hussis betødelser, store och waffladellig oefftro [?]\"</i> — the editor marks <i>[?]</i> at <i>oefftro</i>; the clause (roughly \"the later words spring only from the other house's promptings — great and unceasing self-conceit/credulity\") is <b>textually uncertain</b>. Rendered with a flagged \"[reading uncertain]\" rather than smoothed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The Emperor's Vice-Chancellor was rendered “Lord Coraduz” throughout the English (4 occurrences); both the source transcription and Dreyer's print (TBOO XIV pp.179–180) consistently give “Coraducius”. Corrected to preserve cross-reference to this figure under his attested form.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. The Early Modern Danish is rendered securely and the letter's substance is clear, important, and cross-corroborated by its Latin twin (brahe-t_16000324002): Rudolf II's fatherly patronage and gifts, the declined Vienna refuge, the instruments stranded behind the frozen Elbe, the <i>Progymnasmata</i> dedication commands, the Benátky observatory/laboratory works (~2,000 thaler, under half done), the Coraduz/Barvitius axis, the secret-letter protocol, and the false Prague-before-Easter rumour. Residual uncertainties, all flagged and none obscuring the through-line: the <b>code-names Mercurius and Titan</b> (Titan most plausibly Erik Lange; both kept as code-names); the unnamed <b>book-keeper</b> and <b>Hans Ernst</b>; and one <b>editor-marked textual crux</b> (<i>oefftro [?]</i>). The \"Titan\" thread is opened in the fourth paragraph (<i>\"What you write about Titan… how the matter stood\"</i>) and resumed in the fifth (<i>\"That Titan offered the King… the art in writing\"</i>) — reproduced as in the draft.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all Dreyer TBOO XIV page images for this letter's full extent within the supplied slice: pp.177(regest)-182(letter ends mid-page with 'Iterum et saepius quam felicissime vale... anno 1600', immediately followed by a new, unrelated letter no.182 to Axel Gyldenstierne). The slice's remaining pp.183-189 hold two further unrelated letters (nos.183 to Oluf Rosensparre, 184 to Eskil Bilde) and were correctly excluded from this collation. Read visually in 4-page chunks, no OCR/text layer used. Collated the opening and closing lines and every proper name, number, date, sum of money and place-name against the page images. Dreyer's Danish matches our source_text almost verbatim throughout (no TEXT_ERROR found). The English translation is complete (all paragraphs present, no OMISSION), and faithful on every checked fact: the 2,000-thaler building cost, the 21 March/11 March 1600 double date, Benatky, Vienna, Magdeburg, the Elbe, Pilsen and its 15-mile distance, the book title Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata, and the names Tyge/Tage, Hans Ernst, Barvitius, Bording, Mogens Bertelsen, Claus Bilde Stensoen, Niels Hammer, Margrethe, the young Lange cousins, and Dorthe. The single defect found is the Vice-Chancellor's name, printed 'Coraducius' by Dreyer and our source_text but rendered 'Coraduz' throughout the English -- a low-severity but genuine proper-name deviation, not a factual or sense-changing error.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). The Emperor's Vice-Chancellor's name is rendered 'Coraduz' throughout the English (5 occurrences), a form found in neither our source_text nor Dreyer's print, both of which consistently give 'Coraducius'; unlike the legitimate Baruitio(dat.)-&gt;Barvitius(nom.) normalization made correctly elsewhere in the same letter, this is not a case-ending fix but an altered name, which would frustrate cross-reference to this figure under his attested form. At: “Lord Coraduz”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.179-180 (page images, read visually): ‘her Coraducius’ (p.179), ‘her Coraducium’ and ‘h. Coraducius’ (p.180) all confirm the consistent Latinised form Coraducius, never ‘Coraduz’. The Danish source_text already had the correct form throughout, so only the English needed correction; all 4 occurrences were located and fixed identically. No residual uncertainty on this point.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 42-51.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Sophie Brahe", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991024001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991024001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports receiving the recipient's letter via son Tyge and notes that her son Tage, sent to Heidelberg for advancement, has written to say he fares well.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000321001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000321002", "d": "1600-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "ck": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Axel Gyldenstierne — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 21 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 52 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD AXILLUM GYLDENSTIERNE]</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och altiid forsend met Vor Herre. Kierre Axell Gyldenstiern och kierre modersøster søn, Gud allmechtigste beuare dig naadelligen och well fraa allt ont, och tacker ieg dig gandske gierne for megit beuist gode, huilket igien att forskylde ieg stedtze vill findis aff formuen villig y huiß mode ieg kan giørre nogit, som dig till aere och gode kan werre. Kierre Axel, bekom ieg din skriffuelße met min søn Tyge, som kom hid hiem till mig vdigien nogit epther nøtaarßtag, och skalt du haffue tack for din gode wille, du derudj giffuer tillkiende, att mine wilkor och thillstand y denne landß eyen dig er angenem att høre. Allthingest er endnu, Gud ske loff, vdj samme wesen, som ieg dig tillskreff, och du ødermere aff Eske Bilde vden tuill haffuer forfaret, ieg habis, att all det her epther enda kan bliffue bedre, Gud giffue dertill lycke och benedidelse. Her war offuermøget farlig søgdom y forgangen aar, som bort tog en vtallelligen hoff folck, och keysern drog derudoffuer fra Prag till en fast bø ved naffn Pilsen, 10 mille derfra liggendis, hor hanß may. endnu er och icke wentendis till Prag vdigien førend epther paaske. Hanss may. haffde aff sig selff en naadigst omhu for mig och mine y samme farlige thiid och haffde nogle gange bod hid att lade forfare, horledis det gick oß, lod och naadigst tilbøde mig, att dersom ieg wilde drage nogen andenstedz hen paa itt andit aff hans may. slott, som icke so farligt war, da skulde det stande mig offuen for, och dersom ieg wilde drage hen till Wien y Osterich, da motte ieg det giørre, hor hanß may. wille ocsaa vdj thiide lade forskaffe mig god plaß och leylighed; menß ieg gaff vnderdanigst for suar igien, att ieg helst wille bliffue her och ware min bestilling, befallendis Gud allthing, huilken gode Gud och naadelligen haffuer bewarit mig och mine y samme skrøffuelige thiid.</p><p>Kierre Axell, som dit breff formelder om die penge, oss er vdimellom, att du icke kunde komme dennom adsted thill forgangen omslag y Lantheholsten, och est derfor begerendis, att die motte bliffue stoendis till dette nu tillkommendis omslag vdigien, saa haffde ieg wisselligen forlat mig till, att die forgangen omslag skulle haffue werrit tillrede epter din egen skriffuelßis lødelße, menß eptherdj det icke kunde ske, for ieg att haffue gedult forseendiß mig till, att die vist bliffuer vdlagt till dette nestkommendis omslag, eptersom du vdj dette dit andit breff wist loffuer och thilsier, thi mig er der macht paa ligendis, epter som Eske Bilde ved beskening om. Jeg lader opsige die penge, ieg haffuer staendis y Lanthemeckelburg, thi ieg wille gierne haffue dennom hid till mig, for ieg achter nest Gudtz hielp att kiøbe jore godz for dennom her vdj landit, som er for temmelig got kiøb att bekomme och god profit derhos. Och naar ieg formedelst keyserns autoritet och bistand bliffue[r] met rederskaffuens och adellenß samtøcke intagen for dette rigens indwonner och lim, huilket er letteligen att ske till nestkommendis herredag her vdj landit, saa maa ieg siden kiøbe och erbligen besede allt hues gotz, ieg kan for penge bekomme, ligesom ieg war indefød her vdj landit, och nøde alle die preueleger, som riderskaffuit her haffuer, huilke icke ringe er. Oc skulde dette allerede y denne nest forgangene herredag verre sked, haffde ieg vdj thiide kundit bekommit mine penge dertill. Forseer mig derfor till dig, kierre Axell, att du mig icke lenger opholder met die oss er emelom, paa det ieg kan søye mit gaffn och fordeel dermed vdj andre made. Och kanst du lade Eske Bilde bekome denom, om du icke selff vedst andre medel dertill, allthingest dermet bestille, thi hand ved vidre besken om min handell bode vdj Lantheholsten och Lanthemeckelburg.</p><p>Kiere Axel, jeg ved inted sønderligt nøt att skriffue dig till andet end die siger, at tørcken røster sig sterck, och woriß keyser giører och derimod hueß hannom mueligt er. Die tødske førster vill ingen bistand giøre for spanierenß skyld, som anfecter Tydskeland. Die tarter giorde y Nehr Vngern och Mehrland en stor skade y forgangen sommer paa en stackid thiid siiden, och førde mange folck bort. Horlediß keysern bekom Sibenburgen igien, haffuer du well hørt, Gud giffue, att det mote bliffue bestendigt [att han kunde beholde det met rolighed]. Kierre Axell, will ieg nu icke lenger opholde dig met denne skriffuelse, menß nu oc altiid haffue dig met alt, hueß du vel vilst, Gud allmechtigste befalit.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatky den 21 dag martij stylo nouo anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO AXEL GYLDENSTIERNE]</b></p><p>My wholly friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Axel Gyldenstierne, and dear cousin [mother's-sister's-son], may God Almighty preserve you graciously and well from all evil; and I thank you quite gladly for the much kindness shown, which to repay I shall always be found willing according to my means, in whatever way I can do anything to your honour and good. Dear Axel, I received your letter with my son Tyge, who came home to me again somewhat after New Year's Day; and you shall have thanks for the good will you declare therein, that my circumstances and condition in this region are agreeable to you to hear. Everything is still, God be praised, in the same state as I wrote you, and as you have doubtless further learned from <b>Eske Bille</b>; I hope all this may yet grow better hereafter — may God give fortune and blessing thereto. <b>There was an exceedingly dangerous sickness here last year, which carried off an innumerable host of people</b>, and the Emperor on that account moved from Prague to a strong town named <b>Pilsen</b>, lying 10 miles thence, where His Majesty still is, and not expected at Prague again before Easter. <b>His Majesty had, of himself, a most gracious care for me and mine</b> in that dangerous time, and several times sent a messenger to learn how it went with us, and graciously offered me that, if I would move to another of His Majesty's castles, not so dangerous, it should be at my choice; and if I would move to <b>Vienna in Austria</b>, I might do it, where His Majesty would in time provide me good place and accommodation. But I gave for answer, most humbly, that <b>I would rather stay here and attend to my business, commending everything to God</b> — which good God has graciously preserved me and mine in that dreadful time.</p><p>Dear Axel, as your letter mentions about <b>the money [that is] between us</b>, that you could not get it ready by the past <i>Umschlag</i> in the land of Holstein, and are therefore desirous that it remain standing until this now-coming <i>Umschlag</i>: I had certainly relied on its being ready by the past <i>Umschlag</i>, according to the tenor of your own letter; but since it could not be, I will have patience, trusting it will surely be paid out by this next <i>Umschlag</i>, as you in this your second letter surely promise and assure — for it is of importance to me, as Eske Bille knows the account of. <b>I am giving notice on the money I have standing in the land of Mecklenburg, for I would gladly have it [come] here to me; for I intend, with God's help, to buy landed estates with it here in the country [Bohemia], which are to be had for a fairly good price, and with good profit besides. And when I, by the Emperor's authority and assistance, am received — with the consent of the [Council of the] Realm and the nobility — as an inhabitant and member of this realm (which is easily to be done at the next Diet here in the country), then I may afterward buy, and hold by inheritance, all the estates I can obtain for money, just as if I were native-born here, and enjoy all the privileges the nobility here has, which are not slight.</b> And this should already have happened at this last past Diet, had I been able to get my money for it in time. I trust therefore, dear Axel, that you will not hold me up longer with the [money] that is between us, so that I may seek my benefit and advantage with it in other ways. And you can let <b>Eske Bille</b> receive it, if you do not yourself know other means thereto, and arrange everything therewith — for he knows further account of my dealings both in the land of Holstein and the land of Mecklenburg.</p><p>Dear Axel, I know nothing special as news to write you, except that they say <b>the Turk</b> stirs himself strongly, and our Emperor does against him what he can. The <b>German princes will give no assistance, on account of the Spaniards</b>, who trouble Germany. The <b>Tartars</b> did great damage in Lower Hungary and Moravia last summer, a short time since, and carried off many people. How the Emperor got <b>Transylvania</b> back, you have well heard; God grant it may be lasting, [that he may keep it in peace]. Dear Axel, I will not now detain you longer with this letter, but, now and always, commend you, with all you wish well, to God Almighty.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky, the 21st day of March, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; kinship.</b> <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (1542–1603), Statholder of Norway — Tycho's <b>maternal first cousin</b> (<i>modersøstersøn</i>, \"mother's-sister's-son\"; their mothers were Bille sisters). This is Tycho's <b>reply to Axel's letter of 3 Dec 1599</b> (brahe-t_15991203001). One of the cluster of same-day Danish family letters (21 March 1600 N.S.; cf. brahe-t_16000321004 to his mother, brahe-t_16000321005 to his brother Steen).</li><li><b>The plague &amp; the Emperor's care.</b> The same account as the other family letters: the <b>1599 plague</b> (\"carried off an innumerable host\"), Rudolf II's withdrawal to <b>Pilsen</b>, and his repeated solicitude and <b>offer to relocate Tycho — even to Vienna</b> — which Tycho declined to \"stay here and attend to my business.\"</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's plan to naturalize in Bohemia and buy estates (a key biographical document).</b> Tycho explains <i>why</i> he is <b>calling in the Mecklenburg money</b> (Axel's deferred debt likewise pressed): he means to <b>buy landed estates in Bohemia</b> (\"to be had for a fairly good price, with good profit\"). And he states his <b>naturalization plan</b>: \"<b>when I, by the Emperor's authority and the consent of the [Council of the] Realm and the nobility, am received as an inhabitant and member of this realm — easily done at the next Diet — then I may buy and hold estates by inheritance, as if native-born, and enjoy all the [Bohemian] noble privileges</b>.\" He regrets it could not be done at the last Diet for lack of ready money. This is the clearest statement of Tycho's late ambition to <b>root himself permanently in Bohemia as a naturalized noble landowner</b> (cf. the estate-purchase mentioned to Duke Ulrich, brahe-t_16010410001) — a plan cut short by his death in October 1601.</li><li><b>The debt.</b> Axel could not pay \"the money between us\" by the past Kiel <i>Umschlag</i>; Tycho grants delay to the next, with <b>Eske Bille</b> (who \"knows the account of my dealings in Holstein and Mecklenburg\") empowered to receive it.</li><li><b>News.</b> The <b>Turk</b> stirring; the <b>German princes</b> refusing aid \"on account of the Spaniards\"; the <b>Tartar</b> raids into Lower Hungary and Moravia; <b>Transylvania</b> recovered by the Emperor (cf. brahe-t_16000328002, brahe-t_16000329001).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Axel Gyldenstierne; <b>Eske Bille</b>; <b>Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <b>Turk</b>, the <b>German princes</b>, the <b>Tartars</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benatky den 21 dag Martij stylo novo anni 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Early Modern Danish, read securely; the substance (the plague and the declined evacuation; the deferred Gyldenstierne debt; and above all <b>the naturalization-and-estate-purchase plan</b> funded by the recalled Mecklenburg money, to make Tycho a Bohemian noble landowner at the next Diet; the Long-War news) is clear and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; one bracketed phrase at the close is the edition's supplement, reproduced.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Danish letter (TBOO XIV pp.182-183, read as page images) against source_text: matches Dreyer exactly, including every name (Eske Bille), place (Pilsen, Vienna, Mecklenburg, Holstein, Transylvania/Sibenburgen), number ('10 mille' to Pilsen), and the closing date '21 dag martij ... anni 1600'. The bracketed editorial supplement '[att han kunde beholde det met rolighed]' matches Dreyer's own footnoted deletion-bracket exactly. The English translation is complete and accurate; no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 52 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Axel Gyldenstierne", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991203001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991203001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports stable conditions in Bohemia, notes the Emperor has been absent from Prague for six months due to epidemic sickness, and hopes for improvement.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000321002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000321003", "d": "1600-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Oluf Rosensparre", "ck": "Oluf Rosensparre", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Oluf Rosensparre — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 21 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 54 f. (Kladde — draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD OLAUM ROSENSPARRE].</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och allthiid forsend met Vor Herre. Kierre Oluff Rosenspar, sour och sønderlig gode wen, tacker ieg eder gandske gierne for megit beuist gode, huilket wdjgien att forskylde ieg stedtze will findiß aff formuen willig. Giffuendiß eder derhos gandske wenligen thillkiende, att ieg haffuer bekommit eders skriffuelse met min søn Tyge, som kom hid hiem till mig vdygien lit epther nøtaarßdag, och skall y haffue megen tack for eders gode wille imod mig, som ieg formercker derudaf, enddog den er mig tillforn nocsom bekant. Och som y formelder derudj, att eders kierre høstru, min søsterdatter Elisabet, war samme thiid so ilde tilpas aff it flod vdi hinders hoffuit, att hun icke kunde skriffue mig till, saa vill ieg gierne haffue hinder derudj vnskyldit och forhoffuis, att det er vel snart derepther bleffuen bedre met hinder igien, Gud beuare bode eder och hinder sambt eders børn fran hueß eder skadelligt kan werre. Her vdj landit war sore megen søgdom vdj forgangen aar, aff pestelenße och blodsott, som tho vsigellige mange folck bortt, och keysern drog derudoffuer fra Prag till en fast bø, heder Pilsen, 10 mille derfra liggendis, hor hanß may. endnu er, och er icke wentendis till Prag vdigien førend epther paaske. Hanß may. wille, att ieg skulde haffue begiffuit mig met mine hen thill Wien y Osterich, denstund samme søgdom her so hart regerit, och wille naadigst der lade forskaffue mig god plaß och leylighed. Menß ieg lod giffue hanß may. vnderdanigst igien tillkiende, att ieg wille heller bliffue her och ware paa min bestilling och befalle Gud allthing, hormed hans may. och naadigst war thillfrij[tz]. Jeg tacker Gudt, som saa naadelligen haffuer beuarit mig oc mine y samme farlige søgdom. Och will ieg icke paa denne thiid wijdre formelde om mine willkar och leylighed, thi y haffuer dennom well fornummit aff min siste skriffuelße, och vdentuill ydermere aff min søster Sophi, som ieg skreff widløfftigere till derudom. Altingest er endnu y samme wesen och bliffuer nest Gudtz hielp herepther enda bedre. Den samme Gud will ieg haffue eder och min søsterdatter Elisabet met begge eders børn och gode wenner till all lycke och welfart troeligen befalit; giører vell och hilser Elisabet med mange gode netter paa mine wegne.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatky vdj Lanthebehmen den 21 dag martij stylo nouo anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to Oluf Rosensparre.]</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always, sent forth with Our Lord. Dear Oluf Rosensparre, brother-in-law and especial good friend: I thank you very gladly for much kindness shown, which, to repay in return, I shall ever be found willing according to my means. Giving you herewith, quite in friendship, to know that I have received your letter by my son Tyge, who came home again here to me a little after New Year's Day; and you shall have many thanks for your good will toward me, as I perceive from it — though it was already well enough known to me beforehand. And as you mention therein that your dear wife, my sister's daughter Elisabet, was at that same time so unwell from a flux in her head that she could not write to me — I will gladly hold her excused in that, and hope it has soon afterward grown better with her again. God preserve both you and her, together with your children, from whatever may be harmful to you. Here in this land there was very great sickness in the past year, from pestilence and the bloody flux, which carried off unspeakably many people; and the Emperor on that account moved from Prague to a strong town called Pilsen, lying ten miles thence, where His Majesty still is, and is not to be expected back at Prague until after Easter. His Majesty wished that I should have betaken myself, with mine, away to Vienna in Austria while that same sickness raged so hard here, and would graciously have had good quarters and accommodation procured for me there. But I let His Majesty most humbly understand in turn that I would rather stay here and attend to my charge, and commit all things to God — with which His Majesty also was graciously content. I thank God, who has so graciously preserved me and mine in that same dangerous sickness. And I will not at this time mention further about my conditions and circumstances, for you have well learned them from my last letter, and doubtless further from my sister Sophie, to whom I wrote more at length about it. Everything is still in the same state, and will, with God's help, become better still hereafter. To that same God I will have you, and my sister's daughter Elisabet, with both your children and your good friends, faithfully commended to all fortune and welfare. Do well, and greet Elisabet with many good-nights on my behalf.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle of Benátky in Lower Bohemia, the 21st day of March, New Style, of the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky</b> (here <i>det keyserlige slot Benatky udi Lanthebehmen</i>, \"the imperial castle of Benátky in <b>Lower Bohemia</b>\" — <i>Niederböhmen</i>) — a warm, plain-spoken <b>family letter in Danish</b>, a register apart from the Latin patronage and scientific correspondence of the same weeks.</li><li><b>Recipient &amp; kin.</b> <i>Oluf Rosensparre</i> = <b>Oluf Rosensparre</b> (1559–1624), Danish nobleman and councillor of the realm, addressed as <i>svoger</i> — <b>brother-in-law / kinsman by marriage</b>. His wife <i>Elisabet, min søsterdatter</i> = <b>Elisabeth</b>, \"my sister's daughter\" (Tycho's <b>niece</b>), who had been ill with <i>et flod udi hendes hoved</i> (a \"flux/catarrh in her head\"). Tycho sends her \"many good-nights.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>min søn Tyge</i> = <b>Tyge (Tycho) Brahe the Younger</b>, Tycho's elder son, who carried Rosensparre's letter home \"a little after New Year's Day.\"</li><li><i>min søster Sophi</i> = <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (1559–1643), Tycho's learned sister, his correspondent and intermediary with the Danish family.</li><li><i>Keyseren / hans Majestät</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>The 1599–1600 Bohemian epidemic (datable content).</b> <i>pestelenße og blodsott</i> = <b>plague and the bloody flux (dysentery)</b> had killed \"unspeakably many\" in Bohemia the previous year; the Emperor withdrew from Prague to <b>Pilsen (Plzeň)</b>, \"ten miles off,\" not expected back \"until after Easter.\" Rudolf had offered to move Tycho and his household to safety at <b>Vienna</b>; Tycho declined, preferring to stay at his post — a detail corroborated by the Latin letters of the same season (the court at Pilsen recurs in brahe-t_16000508001, brahe-t_16000420001, brahe-t_16000306002).</li><li><b>Language note (protocol §2).</b> Pre-standardisation <b>chancery Danish</b>: <i>gandske</i> (ganske), <i>wenlig</i> (venlig), <i>skriffuelse</i>, <i>søsterdatter</i>, <i>søgdom</i> (sygdom), <i>blodsott</i>, <i>wæsen</i>, with German-influenced <i>-ß-/w-</i> spelling. The low automatic language-score (0.078) reflects exactly the heuristic weakness the protocol flags (§2, §7); the letter is securely Danish, read as such.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> A <i>Kladde</i> — <b>retained draft</b>; hence the bracketed editorial heading and the absence of a written-out subscription.</li><li><b>Formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Danish epistolary courtesy: <i>Min ganske venlig hilsen nu og altid forsendt med Vor Herre</i> → \"My quite friendly greeting, now and always, sent forth with Our Lord\"; <i>Daterit</i> → \"Dated.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 21 dag Martij <b>stylo novo</b> anni 1600</i> — Tycho stamps even his <b>Danish</b> letter from imperial soil as <b>New Style</b>, although his Danish correspondents at home kept Old Style; this is the same Gregorian dating used across his Bohemian output. Reproduced as written; <i>Easter</i> (the movable feast referenced) is to be read on the same reckoning. Not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — affectionate chancery Danish, read securely despite the low heuristic language-score; the family relationships (Oluf Rosensparre as <i>svoger</i>; niece Elisabeth; son Tyge; sister Sophie) and the datable epidemic/Pilsen content are explicit and cross-corroborated. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages visually (TBOO XIV pp.183-185). The letter to Oluf Rosensparre is entirely on p.184, headed 'Olao Rosensparre Tycho Brahe de peste in Bohemia vagante et de rebus suis privatis narrat', citation 'Ex adumbratione in Bibliotheca Universitatis Basileensis: Autographensammlung Vol.35, Fol.54 f.' Collated opening, closing, date (21 martii/1600 stylo novo), and every name/place (Tyge, Elisabet, Sophie, Prague, Pilsen '10 mille', Wien/Osterich, Benatky/Lanthebehmen) against Dreyer's Danish text: all match the source_text. English rendering tracks the Danish sense-for-sense throughout (plague flight to Pilsen, Emperor's Vienna offer declined, thanks for preservation, referral to prior letter and to Sophie, closing commendation). Two single-word orthographic micro-variants noted between source_text and Dreyer's print ('hid hiem' vs 'hiem'; 'kierre høstru' vs 'høstru') are below confident-flag threshold on a single visual pass and immaterial to sense in either case (no name/number/date/sum/place affected) — not raised as defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 54 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Wishes the recipient's wife Elisabeth a recovery from her head ailment and reports on a severe pestilence and bloody flux that struck Bohemia the past year.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000321003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000321004", "d": "1600-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Beate Bille", "ck": "Beate Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Beate Bille — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 21 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 38 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD BEATAM BILLE].</p><p>MIN gandske sønlig och wenlig hilsen nu och althiid forsendt met Vor Herre. Kierre moder, Gud almechtigste werre nu och althiid hoß eder och beware eder naadelligen och well fran alt det, eder kan werre skadelligt enten thill saell eller liff, och nest megen thacksigelße for allt bewist gode, for huilcket y althiid skulle finde mig willig och thienstachtig y alle die maade, ieg kan giørre nogit, som eder thill aere och gode kan werre, som ieg eder ocsaa stedtze plichtig er. Giffuer ieg eder gandske wenligen thillkiende, att eders gode skriffuellse er mig thillhende kommen met min søn Tyge, som kom hid thill mig ygien nogit epther nøttaarsthag y dette aar, och war det mig megit kiert deraff att fornemme, att eder Gudtskeloff lider well. Den samme gode Gudt vnde mig allthiid samme thidenner thill eder att spørge. Och tacker ieg eder derhos gandske gierne for det møgit gode, y bewiste samme min søn den stun, han var hoß eder, och for die penge, y foraerit hannom; hand lod sig derfor giørre itt smugt guldarmbond, som han will forware och berre for eders skyldt den stund han leffuer. Kierre moder, giffuer ieg ocsaa eder wenligen thillkiende, att ieg och mine lider aff Gudtz naade well, och haffuer den samme gode Gud naadelligen och well befrid oss alle fra den store fare, som war her y forgangen sommer och en god part vd paa wintteren intill emod jiulle thiid, met møgen pestelenße och blodsott, och det offuer dette helle kongerige Behemen so vell som y de ner om krind liggendis lanskab, horer och ødremehre end y nogen aff die forgangene aar, so att røm. key. may. motte wige fra Prag, hor hanss may. helst gid werid, hen wdj en anden bøy wedt naffn Pilsen, thi mille der fra ligendis, hor hans maytt. endnu er och kommer neppe thill Prag wdigien føren epther paaske. Samme farlige søgdommer haffde och thagit megit offuerhand y dette herschaff, som ligger her thill Benateck slott, hor ieg bor och holder huß, saa att der wed die thuthussend er affgangen, baade her vdj kiøbstaden, som ligger oppe paa en stor backe nehr vedt slottid, och y de omliggendis landßbøyer, som hid thill hørrer. Och der hanß key. may. fick att wijde, att her war saadan fare paaferre, haffuer hanß may. nogle gange hafft bodt och naadigst ladit forfarit, horledis det haffde sig met mig och mine, thi hanß may. haffde aff sin egen gunst och tillneienhed sønderlig omhu for mig och dennom, och lod mig thuende gange naadigst thillbøde, att dersom ieg wille drage nogen andenstedtz hen y hanß may. land, hor dett war sickerrer end her, om ieg endskønt willde drage bort thill Wien, som er hans may. fornembste hoffuidstad vdj Osterich, hor die forige keysere pleide mesten parten att holde huß, da skulde det stande mig offuen for, och hans maytt. wille der naadigst forskaffe mig god plaß och leylighedt. Men ieg lodt hans maytt. vnderdanigst betaecke, siigendis att ieg helderwille bliffue her och befalle Gud allthingest, huilket hans may. ocsaa lod sig naadigst befalle. Och war so naadigst aff mig begerendis, att ieg wille sende hannom nogen aff den medecin, som ieg brugte emod samme søgdom, och en beskriffning, horledis den skullde beredis, huilket ieg och strax giorde, huilket hanß may. well behagid, efftersom hanß may. ellers er mig met all keiserlige øndist och naade megit well bewogen, so att ieg icke nochsom kan thacke Vor Herre derfor, som haffuer giort mine willkor anderlediß och bedre end die thillforn ware y mitt fernelandt. Och will nu icke widløfftiger schriffue eder thill derom, eptherdj y det thillforn, der min søn var derinde y landit, haffuer en god part deraff fornummit.</p><p>Allthingest er endnu vdj samme wesen, och haabis nest Guds hielp, att det enda hereffter kan bliffue bedre, hortill den allmechtigste Gud giffue løcke och benedidellße. Den samme gode Gud vill ieg ocsaa haffue eder till languarendis sundhed, løckke och we[l]fard troligen befalid.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatick den 21 dag martij epther denne regning, som er den 11 hoß eder, anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO BEATE BILLE].</b></p><p>My wholly filial and friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear mother, may God Almighty be now and always with you, and preserve you graciously and well from all that may harm you, whether in soul or body; and next [after] much thanks for all the kindness shown, for which you shall always find me willing and serviceable in every way I can do anything that may be to your honour and good, as I am ever bound to you. I give you quite amicably to know that your good letter has come to my hand with my son Tyge, who came back to me somewhat after New Year's Day this year; and it was very dear to me to perceive from it that you, God be praised, fare well. May the same good God grant me always to hear the same tidings of you. And I thank you herewith quite gladly for the much kindness you showed my same son while he was with you, and for the <b>money you gave him</b>: he had <b>a pretty gold bracelet made from it, which he will keep and wear for your sake as long as he lives</b>. Dear mother, I also give you amicably to know that I and mine fare, by God's grace, well; and the same good God has graciously delivered us all from the <b>great danger</b> that was here last summer, and a good part into the winter, until toward Christmas-time — with much <b>pestilence and dysentery</b> — and that over this whole kingdom of Bohemia, as well as in the neighbouring lands around, [raging] worse and more than in any past year, so that the Roman Imperial Majesty had to withdraw from Prague (where His Majesty would most gladly have been) to another town named <b>Pilsen</b>, ten miles thence — where His Majesty still is, and will scarcely come to Prague again before Easter. The same dangerous diseases had also greatly taken hold in this lordship, which belongs to <b>Benátky castle</b>, where I live and keep house, so that <b>some two thousand have died</b> — both here in the market-town (which lies up on a great hill near the castle) and in the surrounding villages belonging hereto. And when His Imperial Majesty got to know there was such danger afoot here, His Majesty <b>several times sent a messenger and graciously had it asked how it stood with me and mine</b> — for His Majesty had, of his own favour and inclination, special care for me and them — and <b>twice graciously offered</b> that, if I would move somewhere else in His Majesty's land where it was safer than here — even if I would move away <b>to Vienna</b>, His Majesty's chief capital in Austria, where the former emperors mostly kept house — then it should be at my choice, and His Majesty would there graciously provide me good place and accommodation. But I had His Majesty humbly thanked, <b>saying I would rather stay here and commit everything to God</b> — which His Majesty also graciously accepted. And [His Majesty] was so graciously desirous of me that <b>I would send him some of the medicine I used against the same disease, and a description of how it should be prepared</b> — which I did at once, and which pleased His Majesty well, since His Majesty is otherwise very well disposed toward me with all imperial favour and grace, so that I cannot sufficiently thank Our Lord, who has made my circumstances otherwise and better than they were before in my fatherland. And I will not now write to you more at length about it, since you have before — when my son was there in the country — perceived a good part of it.</p><p>Everything is still in the same state, and it is hoped, with God's help, that it may yet grow better hereafter — to which may God Almighty give fortune and blessing. To the same good God I will also faithfully commend you, for long-lasting health, fortune, and welfare.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky, the <b>21st day of March by this reckoning, which is the 11th with you</b>, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Beate Bille</b> (1526–1605), <b>Tycho's mother</b> — a noblewoman of the powerful Bille family (and herself a learned woman). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>. A tender, wholly personal letter (Tycho signs himself in the \"filial\" register, <i>sønlig hilsen</i>).</li><li><b>Significance — a personal/biographical letter of the plague winter 1599–1600.</b></li><li><b>Family warmth.</b> Tycho thanks his mother for kindnesses and money she gave <b>his son Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger, returned from the Denmark errand \"after New Year's\"), from which the grandson had <b>a gold bracelet made, \"to keep and wear for your sake as long as he lives.\"</b></li><li><b>The plague (1599).</b> Tycho describes the <b>pestilence and dysentery</b> that ravaged \"this whole kingdom of Bohemia\" through the summer and into winter — <b>~2,000 dead in the Benátky lordship alone</b> (the hill-top market-town and its villages) — which drove <b>Rudolf II from Prague to Pilsen (Plzeň)</b>, \"ten miles thence,\" not to return before Easter (cf. brahe-t_16000324001, brahe-t_16000516001).</li><li><b>The Emperor's personal care (a vivid datum).</b> Rudolf II <b>twice sent to ask after Tycho's safety</b> and <b>twice offered to relocate him anywhere safer in his lands — even to Vienna</b>, the old imperial capital — providing place and means. <b>Tycho declined</b>, \"saying I would rather stay here and commit everything to God\"; and at the Emperor's request <b>sent him his own plague-medicine with a recipe</b> — a striking glimpse of the personal bond between Tycho and Rudolf II.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Beate Bille (Tycho's mother); his son <b>Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — a model case).</b> The dateline explicitly gives <b>both styles</b>: <i>\"den 21 dag Martij efter denne regning, som er den 11 hos eder\"</i> — \"<b>21 March by this [New Style] reckoning, which is the 11th [Old Style] with you [in Denmark].\"</b> Header marked [N.S.], the O.S. equivalent (11 March) preserved exactly per §3 — Tycho himself bridging the calendars for his Danish mother.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Pilsen</i> = <b>Plzeň</b>; <i>Benateck</i> = <b>Benátky</b>; <i>Wien … i Østerrig</i> = <b>Vienna in Austria</b>.</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Danish text corrected 'pange' to 'penge' (Dreyer XIV p.175); English unaffected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear, intimate Early Modern Danish, read securely; the substance (the gold bracelet from the grandmother's gift; the 1599 plague and ~2,000 dead in the Benátky lordship; Rudolf II's withdrawal to Pilsen; the Emperor's repeated solicitude and twice-offered evacuation to Vienna, declined; Tycho's plague-medicine sent to the Emperor) is clear and corpus-/historically corroborated, and the explicit dual-style dating is handled exactly per §3. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.174-177 (all 4 pages read as page images). The letter itself is Dreyer's entry no. 179, occupying pp.175-176; p.174 carries the tail of the Simon von Petkum letter and p.177 carries the start of Tycho's letters to Steen Brahe and Sophie Brahe, all correctly excluded. Full Danish text collated word for word: the gold-bracelet anecdote, the Bohemia plague/dysentery death toll ('two thousand'), Pilsen, the Emperor's twice-repeated offer of a safer refuge including Vienna, the medicine sent to the Emperor, and the closing double date (21 March N.S. / 11 March O.S., 1600) all match Dreyer. One vowel-substitution TEXT_ERROR found ('pange' for 'penge'); translation unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text reads 'pange' where Dreyer prints 'penge' (money) - a vowel substitution producing a non-word. The English translation correctly renders 'money' regardless, so the defect is confined to source_text. Dreyer prints: <i>for die penge, y forærit hannom</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image (TBOO XIV p.175), which prints 'for die penge, y forærit hannom', confirming the audit's claim. The English translation was unaffected since it already correctly rendered 'money'.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 38 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Beate Bille", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991126001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991126001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks his mother for money given to son Tyge, who fashioned a gold bracelet from it to wear in her memory, and reports his situation in Bohemia.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000321004/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000321005", "d": "1600-03-21", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Steen Brahe", "ck": "Steen Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Steen Brahe — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 21 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 40 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD STENONEM BRAHE].</p><p>MIN gandske broderligen och venlig hilsen nu och althiid forsend med Vor Herre. Kierre Sten och kierre broder, Gud allermechtigste werre nu och allthiid hoß dig och beuare dig naadelligen och well fran allt ont. Jeg tacker dig venligen och gierne for alt beuist gode, huilket igien aff formuen att forskylde du stedtze skall finde mig godwillig och som din hulde broder. Kierre broder, haffuer ieg bekommit [dit breff] met min søn Thyge, som kom hid thill mig igien strax epther nøtaarsdag, och skallt du haffue megen tack for samme din gode och welmente skriffuelse, och for hueß du giorde bemelte min søn thillgode den stund, hand var der inde y landit. Hand berettid for mig, att din søn Otto skullde werre kommen hiem vdjgien met m. Kort, endog hand icke thallte met hannom føren hand affdrog, thi der gaffß ingen leylighed. Lad mig wiide, horledis du nu willde haffue det met Otte, om du willt haffue hannom thill hoffue eller lade hannom wijdre studere, och lad hannom sellff skriffue mig thill paa latine, som hand pleier att giørre. Jeg will inted nu formelde om mine wilkar och thilstand, thi du nochsom haffuer det fornummit aff min siste skriffuellße och hueß ieg vidløfftiger derom haffde skreffuit Elke Bilde till. Alltingest er endnu, Gud ske loff, y en god wesen, och forbedrer sig eptherhonen, Gud allmechtigste giffue wijdre løcke och benedidelße. Her bøggis nu flux, epther røm. key. may. nadigst befalling, gelegenhed och plaß till mine instrumenter ad sette, endog die icke endnu erre alle ankommen, thi winterren forhindrede, att ieg icke saa snart kunde faa denom hid paa Elben, saa att somme erre endnu thill Magdeburg, och die, min søn hentte siste gang aff Danmarck, formener ieg att werre thill Hamborgk. Hanß maytt. skreff die herrer aff Magdeburg till nogit for Michaelis nest forgangen, att die wilde forskaffue mig die, som der er, hid op y Behemen formedelst Elbstrømmen, saa fant die itt politisk vdloff y breffuit, att det icke vtrøcklig løde, att die skulde giørre det paa deris bekaastning. Huilket der hans may. fick att wijde, lod han itt andit breff vdgaa thill dennom, huilket tøde det første vdtrøckelligen, och der die dervdaff ingen praetext kunde finde, skreffue die mig till och varre begerendiß, att ieg vndertanigst paa deris wegne wille vndskølde dennom y die maader hoß keysern och forevende den hore bestolding, die haffde lit, hueß tuang die icke endnu haffde foruundet, huilket, der keyserens raad fick att wijde, warre di sposk och vnderlige deroffuer; thi det er icke langt fra itt halff hundrit aar siden bestoldingen war, och blef sagt, man kunde well ocsaa finde paa itt vdloff, nar die haffde keysern ygien y behoff, och hans may. tog det ocsaa hart till mødstøcke, och befoll strag att skriffue domherrene vdj Magdeburg till derom, huilke godwilligen indgick saadant att wille giørre hanß maytt. till naadigst gefallen. Jeg forseer mig ocsaa, att die herrer aff Hamborgk vdj lige maade selffuilligen retter dennom epther keyserens skriffuelse, som die och derudom haffuer bekommit, saa att ieg wenter bade mine jnstrumenter och andit vdj Tyskland eptherlat goß hid op met det første. Jeg haffde lenge siden thi[l]forn fornummit, att Gud haffde henkallid din høstru Cirsten, huilket mig giorde ont, menß man for att lade sig nøye met Gudz wille; haud vnde dig en anden god vdigien, som kan werre epther dit sin, dig och dinne till glaede. Det du skriffuer om, att du achtede ad Mons Bertelsen skulde haffue skriffuit mig till, er icke sked. Ved icke huad du mener, thi Monß haffuer inted skriffue[t] mig thill, endog ieg icke forgiette att besøie hannom met mit breff for voriß gammel kundskab skyld; aff huad aarsag hand saadant haffuer lat, maa hand selff wijde. Kierre broder, giør vell och vndskyld mig hos mine andre brødre, att ieg icke paa denne thid skriffuer dennom till, tj ieg haffuer icke gode stunder och ved icke heller andit att skriffue end som ieg siste gang giorde. Sig dennom met alle andre voris gode wenner mange gode netter paa mine wegne. Och vill hermet nu och allthiid haffue dig met alt, hueß du wellt wilst, Gud almechtigste befalit.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatick y lante Bemen den 21 dag martij stylo nouo anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO STEEN BRAHE].</b></p><p>My wholly brotherly and friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Steen and dear brother, may God Almighty be now and always with you, and preserve you graciously and well from all evil. I thank you amicably and gladly for all kindness shown, which to repay in return you shall always find me willing, and as your faithful brother. Dear brother, I have received [your letter] with my son Tyge, who came back to me right after New Year's Day; and you shall have much thanks for the same good and well-meant letter, and for what you did for my said son while he was there in the country. He reported to me that <b>your son Otto</b> was [said] to have come home again with <b>Master Kort</b>, although he did not speak with him before he departed, for no opportunity was given. <b>Let me know how you would now have it with Otto — whether you want him at court [in service] or to let him study further — and let him write to me himself in Latin, as he is wont to do.</b> I will not now report on my circumstances, for you have sufficiently perceived them from my last letter and from what I had written more fully to <b>Eske Bille</b>. Everything is still, God be praised, in a good state, and improves by degrees; may God Almighty give further fortune and blessing.</p><p>Here there is now busy building, by the Roman Imperial Majesty's gracious command, [for] accommodation and place to set up my instruments — though they have not yet all arrived, for the winter prevented me from getting them hither so soon on the <b>Elbe</b>, so that some are still at <b>Magdeburg</b>, and those my son fetched last time from Denmark are, I think, at <b>Hamburg</b>. His Majesty wrote to the lords of Magdeburg somewhat before last Michaelmas that they should convey to me those that are there, up into Bohemia by the Elbe stream; but they found a <b>\"political loophole\" in the letter</b> — that it did not expressly say they should do it at their own expense. Which when His Majesty learned, he had another letter go out to them, expressly construing the first; and when they could find no pretext from it, they wrote to me, desiring that I would humbly, on their behalf, <b>excuse them to the Emperor and plead the grievous sacking they had suffered</b>, the burden of which they had not yet overcome. Which, when the Emperor's council learned of it, they were scornful and astonished thereat; for <b>it is not far from fifty years since the storming was</b>, and it was said one could also well find a \"loophole\" the next time they had need of the Emperor; and His Majesty too took it ill, and ordered at once to write to the <b>Cathedral canons at Magdeburg</b> about it — who willingly agreed to do such as would be to His Majesty's gracious pleasure. I also expect that the <b>lords of Hamburg</b> will in like manner willingly conform to the Emperor's letter, which they too have received, so that I expect both my instruments and other goods left in Germany [to come] up here at the first [opportunity].</p><p>I had long ago perceived that <b>God had called away your wife Kirsten</b>, which grieved me; but one must be content with God's will. May He grant you another good [wife] in return, after your own mind, to the joy of you and yours. What you write — that you supposed <b>Mons Bertelsen</b> should have written to me — has not happened; I know not what you mean, for Mons has written nothing to me, though I did not forget to greet him with my letter, for our old acquaintance's sake; for what reason he has omitted it, he may himself know. Dear brother, be so good as to excuse me to my other brothers, that I do not write to them at this time, for I have not good leisure, nor know anything else to write than I did last time. Say to them, with all our other good friends, many good nights on my behalf. And herewith I will, now and always, commend you, with all that you wish well, to God Almighty.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky, in the land of Bohemia, the 21st day of March, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Steen (Sten) Brahe</b> (1547–1620), <b>Tycho's brother</b>, a Danish Rigsraad councillor. One of the cluster of same-day Danish family letters (21 March 1600 N.S.) — with the letters to their mother <b>Beate Bille</b> (brahe-t_16000321004) and to the kinsman Peder Brahe (brahe-t_16000323001). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.</li><li><b>Family matters.</b> Tycho thanks Steen for kindness to his son <b>Tyge</b> (back from Denmark \"after New Year's\"); asks Steen's plans for <b>his son Otto</b> — <i>court service or further study</i> — bidding Otto \"write to me himself in Latin, as he is wont\"; offers condolence on the death of <b>Steen's wife Kirsten</b> and a wish for a new marriage; and notes the puzzle of <b>Mons Bertelsen</b>, who never wrote though Tycho had greeted him.</li><li><b>Significance — the Magdeburg instrument-transport saga (inside view).</b> A vivid, candid account complementing the formal letter to the Cathedral Chapter (brahe-t_16000404001): Rudolf II had written the <b>Magdeburg city council</b> (before Michaelmas) to ship Tycho's stored instruments up the <b>Elbe</b>; the councillors found a <b>\"political loophole\" (<i>politisk udløb</i>)</b> — the letter did not say <i>at their expense</i> — and, when the Emperor's clarifying second letter closed it, asked Tycho to <b>excuse them to the Emperor by pleading the old \"sacking\" they had suffered</b> (the 1550–51 <b>siege/sack of Magdeburg</b> — \"not far from fifty years\" past). The Emperor's council were \"<b>scornful</b>\" (one could \"find a loophole\" the next time Magdeburg needed the Emperor), the Emperor \"took it ill,\" and ordered the matter put to the <b>Cathedral Chapter</b>, which agreed — while <b>Hamburg</b> is expected to comply likewise. A revealing glimpse of imperial-municipal friction behind the instrument move.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Steen Brahe (and his son <b>Otto Brahe</b> and late wife <b>Kirsten</b>); <b>Master Kort</b> (Otto's tutor/companion); <b>Tyge</b> (Tycho the Younger); <b>Eske Bille</b>; <b>Mons Bertelsen</b>; the Magdeburg <b>city council</b> and <b>Cathedral Chapter</b>; the <b>Hamburg</b> council; <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benatick den 21 dag Martij stylo novo anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Closing dateline restored to include 'y lante Bemen' ('in the land of Bohemia'), dropped from the source text, per Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.177; the English dateline updated to match.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear, candid Early Modern Danish, read securely; the family matters (Otto's prospects, the condolence on Kirsten, the Mons Bertelsen puzzle) and especially the <b>Magdeburg-refusal narrative</b> (the \"political loophole,\" the imperial second letter, the council's scornful reaction, the Cathedral Chapter's agreement, Hamburg's expected compliance) are clear and dovetail with brahe-t_16000404001. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Tycho to Steen Brahe, 21 March 1600, Danish) against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.176-177 page images, with a second close re-read of p.177's closing lines. Every proper name (Tyge, Otto, Kort, Eske Bille, Magdeburg, Elbe, Hamburg, Kirsten, Mons Bertelsen), the Michaelmas/50-years-since-the-storming detail, and all other content match Dreyer exactly except the dropped closing-line phrase noted above.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text silently drops the phrase 'y lante Bemen' ('in the land of Bohemia') that Dreyer prints between the castle name and the date; the English inherits the omission. Does not mislead a scholar since the letter's own heading and body already establish Benatky/Bohemia as the place of writing. Dreyer prints: <i>Daterit paa det keyferlige flot Benatick y lante Bemen den 21 dag martij ftylo nouo anno 1600. (TBOO XIV p.177, closing dateline)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image (TBOO XIV p.177), which clearly reads '...Benatick y lante Bemen den 21 dag martij ftylo nouo anno 1600'. The phrase was restored to both the Danish text and the English translation's closing dateline; nothing else in the letter was touched. No uncertainty remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 40 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks about plans for his nephew Otto — court service or further study — and requests that Otto write to him directly in Latin.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000321005/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000322001", "d": "1600-03-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Eske Bille", "ck": "Eske Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Eske Bille — Benátky nad Jizerou, 22 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Early Modern Danish (personal/estate-business) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 56–63 (Kladde / Tycho's own draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD ESCHILLUM BILLE].</p><p>MINN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och allthiid forsend met Vor Herre. Kierre Eske Bilde och kierre broder, Gud allmechtigste beuare dig nu och allthiid naadelligen och vell fran allt ont, och nest megen tacksigelße for all hulhet och gode, som du mig stedtze giort och beuist haffuer, huilket du skallt allthiid finde mig gandske godwillig aff formuen att forskylde y hues maade ieg kan giørre nogit, som dig till aerre och gode kan werre. Kierre Eske, bekom ieg din skriffuelße met min søn Tyge, som kom hid hiem till mig igien lidet epther nøtaarsdag, och formercker først derudaff, att diße gode willkord, som Vor Herre haffuer forsønid mig met her hoß rom. key. may., haffuer well behagit dig och mine andre wenner derudj landet. Althing er Gudskeloff y samme wesen epther som ieg y min skriffuelße da formeldit, och haabis, att det endnu kan bliffue bedre, Gud giffue widre løcke och benedidelße. Keyseren haffuer icke verrid till Prag vdj itt halff aar, men er wentendis did igien, naar paske er gangen. Her haffuer werrid so skrøfuellige en thiid met søgdom y forganget aar, saa hans may. tord icke bliffue vdj slotid till Prag, och ieg war epther hanß may. naadigst beuilling dragen hen paa itt andit aff hanß may. huß, ved naffn Girsitz, paa 6 eller 7 vgers thiid att giøre, och skreff dig till derfran y nouemb. maanid, huilket mit breff ieg forseer mig till, att du siden haffuer bekommit, horvdj ieg formener mig att haffue suarid till en part aff dett, som din skriffuelße nu formelder, som tillbage war. Jeg formercker, att min søn Tyge haffuer bekommit alle mine jnstrumenter der vd aff landit, och att die nu erre till Lybch eller Hamburgk, och forhaabis met første muelighed att bekomme dennom hidt op till mig formedelst Elbstromen, epthersom key. may. i forgangit aar haffuer naadigst skreffuet die herrer vdj Hamburgk till derom; die andre, som ieg haffde vd met mig, erre endnu mesten parten till Magdeburgk met en stor hoff aff mit andit gotz. Die herrer aff Magdeburgk fant paa itt juristisk vdloff, att die icke skulle lade føre det alltsammen hid for winttern paa deris bekostning, epthersom hanß may. haffde ocsesaa thuenne gange skreffuit dennom till derudom. Nu giorde die det gerne, dersom die motte, menß hans may. tog dett so hart till møstøcke, att die tillforn weirede dennom derudj, att han nu inted will haffue det giort aff dennom, menß haffuer skreffuit dommherrene y Magdeburgß kapitell till derom, huilke gierne wille epterkom hans may. naadigst begering, som die och goduilligen hauffue det tillbøid, førend die fick breffuit. Forhaabis derfor att bekomme det hid met det første paa deris bekaastning.</p><p>Die 7 hundrid thaller, som du haffde forskaffet till mig, bekom ieg icke førend nogit for forgangen jiull, saa lenge haffde die werrid paa weien eblant kiøbmennen.</p><p>See till, att du kanst faa stillid fru Citzelle Orne till fridß hueß hoffuidstollen bellanger; rentten tør du slet inted ved att giffue hinder, ieg vill før haffue en dom der paa, och formener ieg endnu, att hinder bør att stande mig till rette for die 5 hundrid thaller, som jomfru Jehanne och mig var imellom, eptherdj ieg er icke anderledis førmuit derudj, end met det breff, hun gaff mig, som du haffuer; haffde ieg vist, att Citzelle Orne icke wille lade sig nøye dermet, da [ha]fde[?] ieg well bekommit anden følist aff j. Jehanne derfor, ey heller haffuer ieg vist tillforn aff [den] herristing-dom, Citzelle haffuer forhuervid, lader mig icke heller nøye dermed; thi ieg formenner, att den ganger icke mig saa møgit an som medarffuene indbørdis, huilke derudi haffde beuilliget och samtøckt mig wuedendis, ey heller befindes y raadsentz contract nocsom met alle sin omstend der att werre indført; menß kan hun wisse nogen quitanze aff jomfru Oxe, Johanne vdj sehr, att hun quiterer hinder for die penge, mig var beuillid, kan ieg well lade mig nøye dermedt. Haffde det so werit, da haffde jomfru Jehanne well ladit mig vist dett y hindis leffuendis liff och tagid Citzillis hanßskrifft aff mig vdigien, och y andre moder stillid mig tillfridz, saa att ieg deraff kunde haffue betallt Citzille die 5 hundrid thaller, menß eptherdj bode hun och Frerich tagde stille dermed, och inted kreffuid mig dennom all den stund Frederich leffte, wiste ieg derfor icke andit end die lod dennom nøye medt att quitte samme sum emod Citzillis handskrifft. Jeg beder dig gierne, att du wille talle flittelligen met Axell Gyldenstiern herudom, huad gode raad han giffuer, att mig icke skall ske for kortt y nogen maade, och dersom ieg ingenlunde kune faa min opretning y de penge, som ieg war s[al.] Albritt skyldig vdj saa maade, om ieg icke da kunde erlange det hoß j. Jehannis arffuinge, saa at ieg kunde bekomme tillbørlig vederlag vdigien for saadan skade. Dersom dig ocsaa gott sønis att werre, daa kunde man tage wenner paa begge sider, som skilde Citzelle och mig ad herudom. Det breff, du formelder du haffde ladit min søn seet, som ieg skulle haffue skreffuet Citzelle till herom vnder min egen hon, motte ieg gierne wijde, horledis det løder och nor det er daterit, kandske det er sked førend ieg fick hinders breff aff j. Jehanne; om saa er, da er der got att suare till, ieg ved vell, att ieg ville hafft pengene aff j. Jehanne att bethalle Citzelle met, for hun manid mig nogid epther Gud haffde kaldit Albrit Ochse, och da haffuer ieg vden thuiffuel skreffuit hinder saadant itt breff till. Menß siden ieg fick det andit aff jomfru Jehanne att sette der imod, haffuer hun neppe bekommit saadan besken aff mig, menß ieg haffuer vden thuill skreffuid hinder det samme till, som ieg nu stor paa, dersom hun ellers wille lade saadanne breffue komme frem. Giør well och send mig en copi aff mit breff, som du formelder om, saa will ieg lade dig wijde wijdre besken derpaa, epthersom ieg mig kan erindre. Jeg haffde achtid mig did ind till herredagene nu vdj aar, menß ieg formerker aff din skriffuelße, att man er icke wiß paa, om der bliffuer nogen herredage vdj dette aar, eptherdj den siste stod saa langt vd paa aarid. Dertillmed kan ieg icke fo forloff aff røm. kay. may. y dette første aar att drage so langt her fran, eyheller er min handell endnu her nochsom anrettid, saa ieg haffuer en stor part vd paa sommeren att giørre dermed, menß dersom alltingest ganger well till, achter ieg, nest Gudz hielp, thill den anden herredag y det nestkommendis aar att komme did ind och thalle met mine slegt och wenner och vdrette mine bestillinger, epthersom behoff giøris. Forseer mig till, ad ieg da will bekomme forloff aff hans may. paa en otte eller 10 vgers thiid att giørre. Det, du sagde till min søn Tyge, haffuer ieg slet ingen fare for, thi om saa skede, horthill man dog ingen rett haffuer, bleff der vell den, som tog mig y forsuar, och lod mig ingen vrett vederfaris, eller ramte det paa en anden mode vdigien, om die wille giøre nogen geuald, och ieg achter att haffue saadan breffue met mig, naar Gud vill ieg kommer, att man vell skall betencke sig huad man giør. Haffuer nogen mig nogit till att thalle, da giør sig det for vpartiske dommer, saa skulle die vel bekomme suar. Jeg achter endnu att haffue nogen aff mine penge vdigien, som ieg sette derudi landit, och mente att skulle haffue hafft anden tack och vederlag derfor igien, end som sked er, menß allthing haffuer sin thiid, der tør engang bliffue raad til wraad.</p><p>Du skallt haffue tack, att du wille forskaffue die rentte-penge till Lantheholsten, huilket ieg forseer mig att werre sked. Och som du giør fortrøstning att wille fli mig mit hoffuid-breff vdigien, som stor vdj Lantheholsten, paa det ieg dermed kunde opkreffue och igien bekomme die penge, ieg haffuer stoendiß y Lanthemeckelburg, so giør du mig dermet en sønderlige thienist, och forlader mig wisselligen dertill, att du det vdrettennis vorder, thi mig ligger storligen macht derpaa. Ieg sender dig herhos epther din begering copier aff begge breffuene, bade horledis det, som ieg haffuer bekommit y Lanthemeckelburg och ocsaa det, som ieg haffuer vdgiffuit y Lantheholsten, løder, hort du kanst wijde dig epther ad rette. Och lad so strax sige pengene op vdj Lanthemeckelburg, paa det att ieg kan bekomme dennom vdj thiide, ieg achter met Gudz hielp att giørre mig bedre gaffn met dennom herudj landit, epthersom ieg thillforn haffuer skreffuit dig besken till derom. Och skreff ieg dig ocsaa siste gang till fra det bemelte keysernß hus Girtzis, vdj hues mode ieg mente, att du kunde bekomme samme sum penge att løße breffuid ind y Lantheholsten met, bode aff die, ieg haffuer stoendis hos Axell Gyldenstiern, som han haffuer wist atter igien loffuit, att ieg skall bekomme till omslagid, och ieg paa nø haffuer skreffuet hanom till derom, huilket breff ieg sender dig herhos, att du hannom det met første leylighed kan forskaffue. Kan du och tage dertill, huad du kanst fo for min gaar y Kiøbenhaffn och hues andit, du weest som mig kan tillkomme, och hues der bliffuer thilloffuers, som man icke so snart kan affsted komme, will ieg forrente y Lantheholsten, och giffue 6 aff hundrid, som nu der sedwonligt er; du forskaffuer mig vell nogen wisse forloffuerre, huilke ieg vill holde skadisløß vdj alle mode.</p><p>Som du skriffuer mig om min stott, du haffuer ladit ført offuer till Skonne, och icke enda viste, horledis den kunde bliffue sold, so vill ieg lade dig selffuer rode, bode for saadant och andit, thi ieg tuill inted paa, att du io berammer det gaffnligen och well.</p><p>Die heste, du haffuer forskaffid mig, skallt du haffue tack for, endog die erre vell døre, man bekomer bedre kiøb paa heste her vdj landit, menß ieg lader mig dog gierne nøye met hues du giør. Den ene, som du bekom aff Oluff Bilde till mig selff, behager mig vell, han so nogit illde vd dett første gang, han var kommen hid, och haffde forlorid sitt trau paa weien, menß han kommer sig nu igien bade paa hulden och trauwid. Jeg vill beholde den for mig sielff att riide paa vdi Prag och andenstes, saa ingen skall lettelligen bekomme [den] aff mig, som du ocsaa raader, och skalt du haffue megen tack for den smo[k]e lille kuschwogen, som du foraerid mig. Hueß foller der er paa Huaen igien, som mig tillkommer, kanst du giør aff huad dig selff løster, dog so ieg heller, att du forhandlid dennom hoß andre end hoß bønderne der paa landit. Anlangendis die fiske paa Huaen, som ieg wille haffue insaltid, beretter Tyge for mig, att han haffuer ladit indsaltid deraf ved 14 tønner; ieg haffde førment mange fler der att bekomme, mens du lader vell tillsee bode met det och andit, saa att allting ganger lidelligen och vell till, och att der bliffuer sett smo ley igien aff karper och anden smaa fisk y die største fiskewan och hor ellers behoff giøris.</p><p>Dauid Pedersønß regenskab och hues dett angaar kan ieg icke nu forskaffue dig, thi det er blant mit andit gotz, som foruaris till Magdeburg. Menß du fanger vell dog raad till hannom, epthersom du vdenad ved besken om hans handell.</p><p>Er ocsaa Diderich farrers quindis regenskab der hoß det andit, so att ieg ey heller kan sende dig det, du vest vell selff, horledis det var raderid och forfalskid, horudaff det er nochsom att forstaa, hor gott det andit var, naar hun haffuer giort klart for nogen laester rug och bøch, som hun y nogle aar haffde y hinders foruaring, och feillede alltid 4 eller 5 skepper paa huer pun, naar det kom till Huaen, so vill ieg først suare till hinders selffdictede regenskab. Hun kan wijsse hinders quitantze paa samme rug och buch, och hun wed well, att ieg giørde hinders man y hanß leffuendis liff icke ringe mangell derudj, som skede met all billighed, och ieg dog ingen oprettning haffuer siden bekom[met] vdigien.</p><p>Jeg ved presten paa Huaen slett inted till ville, enten met smeden, som ieg lod bøgge der for mine egne penge, eller y andre moder, thi han inted saadant haffuer forskylt aff mig; skulle han lønnis epther sin forskyldning, da skulle man anderledis handle met hannom, eyheller er han kommen did ing med min ville och samtøcke, som borde fornemlig att raade derfore. Haffuer Dauid Pedersøn giort der nogit vdj, da er det mig vuendis. Morten deyn siger dig vell beskening, horledis samme prest haffuer beted sig imod mig, siden han kom der, om han ellers vill rett vd dermed. Prestegaaren lod bønderne selff forfalle och stall bort en stor part deraff imod mit forbod, som Suen Trussen vell ved, om han ellers vill bestaa sanden, och ieg vntte presten en anden plaß, som ieg selff haffde kiøbt och hialp till att lade bøge, och skulle vell haffue bleffuit bedre fuldkommit, haffde ieg icke bleffuit foraarsagit att drage derfran. Att lagaarden forfaller och att sloted bliffuer ocsesaa brøstfeldig, ved ieg icke andit att suare dig till, end som ieg tillforn haffuer skreffuit, att ieg icke er tilsindz att giørre der nogen ødermere bekostning paa; det er alt formøgit, som sked er tillforn, haffde ieg die penge vdigien, da skulle die neppe bliffue mere so ilde sted.</p><p>Kierre Eske, som du formelder y dine postscriptis, siden du vast igen kommen till Kiøbenhaffn, om min gaar der y bøen, att du kanst icke bekomme offuer 15 hundrid thaller derfor aff die frower, so koster hand mig aff begøndelsen att lade bøgge offuer die 2 thussend foruden plaßen och die andre huß, der stod tillforn, och lod ieg forbedre och wijdre bekoste derpaa, nogit førend ieg drog aff landit, for thussend thaller y det minste, som Michel Wibe en stor part ved besken om. Den klocke, der henger y den lille spir, ieg lod giørre offuer karnappit, ville ieg, att du met det første lod nedertage och sent til Lybch til Jochum Buch, thi den hører till seieruerkid, som er i Lybch, so ieg vill dog icke, att hun skall bliffue der, menß ieg wed her anderledis att bringe den till nøtte. Som ieg och formercker, att itt aff mine seierwerch, som wiser minuterne, bleff ochsaa tillbage paa Huaen, huilket Tyge bereter, att du haffuer lont Jørgen Brockenhus att lade giørre itt andit epther, ville ieg ocsaa gierne haffue det igien, thi ieg haffuer det her ibehoff vdi min obseruation, om ieg haffde endskønt flere saadanne. Du kanst lade en seyermager packke det sammen vdj en lille firkantt kiste och sende det ocsesaa till Lybch till Jochum Buch, thi ieg tencker vell, att Jørgen Brockenhus haffuer nu vel brugt det saa møyet han haffuer ybehoff. Menß dersom han kan icke fo itt andit der giort, och ville gierne beholle det, daa maa du lade hannom haffue det, thi han er min gode wen och tør vndertiden sige sanningen for min skyld. Jeg so selff gierne, att han wille lade giøre sig nogen instrumenter och dermed obseruere nogit derwdj landit, epthersom han sagde till Tyge sig att wille giørre, och naar ieg for att wjde, att det er hans allffwor, will ieg skriffue hanom en instruction, huad huert aar y besøndellighed er att obseruere. Om die andre, som inted forstaar sig paa saadant, ville verre sposk derudoffuer, skall han inted paasse derpaa, eyheller suare dennom nogit dertill. Kunne han engang komme hid vd till mig, da wille ieg vell paa en kort thiid so rette hannom an derudj, att han skulle kende sig gott deraff, och siden dis lettere och løcckelliger gange om dermet, huilket du kanst sige hannom paa mine wegne, naar du kommer y taall met hannom. Jeg lader ocsaa paa attskillige steder vdj Tydsland, formedelst dennom, som haffuer løst dertill, rette saadant an. Och er der nogen aff die fornemme herrer vdj Prag, som allerede haffuer ladet giort sig nogen instrumenter epther de, som ieg haffuer hid met mig, och wille der lade obseruere dermedt, och haffuer keyserlige may. selff nogen vdi forraad och achter flere att lade giørre epther nogen aff mine, nor die alle ankommer, huilke hans may. will haffue paa slotid vdj Prag for sig selff och huem han dertill vdj sønderlighed will bruge, thi hans may. haffuer stor løst och behagellighed till saadant, och er och tillsindz strax epther paaske, att sende sin fornembste vrmacher hid till mig, som er en sønderlig kønstler, huilken hoß mig skall forfare allehonne, som der till hører, och siden giørre saadant for hanß may.</p><p>Kierre Eske och kierre broder, fortenck mig icke, att ieg saa thitt bemøder dig met min handell, ieg haffuer ingen anden derudj landit, som will haffue saa møgen wmag for mig; dersom ieg vdi nogen mode kan tienne dig vdigien, skall du finde mig mere end villig dertill. Och vill hermed nu och allthiid haffue dig Gud allmechtigste befallit, han beuar dig fran allt, dig kan werre skadelligt enten till saell eller liff. Giør well och sig din kierre fader och moder met alle andre voris gode wenner mange gode netter paa mine wegne.</p><p>Daterit vdj Lanthebemen paa det keyserlige slot Benatky den 22 dag martij anno 1600</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to Eske Bille.]</b></p><p>My altogether friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with Our Lord. Dear Eske Bille and dear brother, may God Almighty guard you now and always, graciously and well, from all evil; and next, with much thanksgiving for all the kindness and good that you have ever done and shown me — for which you shall always find me quite willing, to the best of my ability, to requite it in whatever way I can do anything that may be for your honour and good. Dear Eske, I received your letter by my son Tyge, who came home to me again a little after New Year's Day; and I note first from it that these good conditions which Our Lord has provided me with here, with the Roman Imperial Majesty, have well pleased you and my other friends in the country. Everything is, God be praised, in the same state as I reported in my letter then, and it is to be hoped that it may yet become better — God grant further fortune and blessing. The Emperor has not been at Prague for half a year, but is expected there again when Easter is past. There has been so sickly a time with disease in the past year that His Majesty durst not stay in the castle at Prague; and I had, by His Majesty's gracious permission, gone away to another of His Majesty's houses, by the name of Girsitz, for some six or seven weeks' stay, and wrote to you from there in the month of November — which letter of mine I expect you have since received, wherein I think I answered a part of what your letter now reports, which was [still] outstanding. I note that my son Tyge has got all my instruments out of the country, and that they are now at Lübeck or Hamburg, and I hope to get them up here to me at the first opportunity by the Elbe stream, since His Imperial Majesty in the past year graciously wrote to the lords of Hamburg about it. The others, which I had out with me, are still for the most part at Magdeburg, with a great heap of my other goods. The lords of Magdeburg hit upon a juridical evasion, that they should not have it all conveyed here at their expense before winter, although His Majesty had also twice written to them about it. Now they would gladly do it, if they might; but His Majesty took it so much amiss that they had earlier refused them in it, that he now will not have it done by them, but has written to the canons in the chapter of Magdeburg about it — who will gladly comply with His Majesty's gracious request, as they had also of their own goodwill offered it before they received the letter. I hope, therefore, to get it here at the first [chance], at their expense.</p><p>The 700 thalers that you had procured for me, I did not receive until somewhat before last Christmas — so long had they been on the way among the merchants.</p><p>See to it that you can get Lady Cecilie Urne satisfied as concerns the principal; the interest you must by no means venture to give her — I will first have a judgment upon it; and I still hold that she ought to answer to me for the 500 thalers that were between Mistress Johanne and me, since I am not otherwise secured in it than by the deed she gave me, which you hold. Had I known that Cecilie Urne would not content herself therewith, I should well have obtained other security from Mistress Johanne for it; nor had I known before of the district-court judgment that Cecilie has obtained — nor do I content myself with it, for I hold that it concerns not so much me as the co-heirs among themselves, who had therein consented and agreed without my knowledge; nor is it found to be entered in the town-council contract sufficiently, with all its circumstances. But if she can show any quittance from Mistress Oxe — Johanne in particular — that she acquits her [Cecilie] for the money that was granted to me, I can well content myself therewith. Had it been so, then Mistress Johanne would surely have let me know it in her lifetime, and taken Cecilie's bond back from me, and in other ways set me at ease, so that I could therewith have paid Cecilie the 500 thalers; but since both she and Frederik kept silent about it, and never demanded them of me all the while Frederik lived, I therefore knew nothing else than that they were content to acquit that same sum against Cecilie's bond. I earnestly ask you to speak diligently with Axel Gyldenstierne about this — what good counsel he gives, that I be not short-changed in any way; and if I could in no way get my settlement in the money I owed the late Albret in this manner, [I ask] whether I might not then obtain it from Mistress Johanne's heirs, so that I might get due compensation again for such loss. If it also seem good to you, then one might take friends [as arbitrators] on both sides, to settle the matter between Cecilie and me. The letter that you mention you had shown my son — which I am supposed to have written to Cecilie about this under my own hand — I should gladly know how it reads and when it is dated; perhaps it was done before I received her deed from Mistress Johanne. If so, then there is a good answer to it: I know well that I wished to have the money from Mistress Johanne to pay Cecilie with, for she dunned me somewhat after God had called Albret Oxe [to Him], and then I have doubtless written her such a letter. But since I received the other [deed] from Mistress Johanne to set against it, she has hardly received such word from me; rather I have doubtless written to her the same thing that I now insist on — if only she would let such letters come forth. Be so good as to send me a copy of my letter that you mention, and I will let you know further word on it, as I can recall. I had intended to go in to the Diet this year, but I gather from your letter that one is not sure whether there will be any Diet this year, since the last stood so late in the year. Moreover, I cannot get leave from the Roman Imperial Majesty this first year to travel so far from here; nor is my work here yet sufficiently set up, so that I have a great part of it [to do] out into the summer. But if all goes well, I intend, with God's help, to come in to the next Diet in the coming year, and to speak with my kindred and friends and dispatch my affairs, as need requires. I expect that I shall then get leave from His Majesty for some eight or ten weeks. What you said to my son Tyge, I have no fear of at all; for if it should so happen — to which no one has any right — there would surely be one who would take me into his protection and let no wrong befall me, or would requite it in another way, should they try to do any violence; and I intend to have such letters [of imperial protection] with me, when, God willing, I come, that one shall well think twice about what one does. If anyone has anything to charge against me, let him bring it before impartial judges, then they shall surely get an answer. I still intend to recover some of my money that I laid out in the country, having thought I should have had other thanks and recompense for it than has happened; but everything has its time — there may yet at last be a remedy for the mischief.</p><p>Thanks to you for procuring the interest-money to Holstein, which I trust has been done. And as you give assurance that you will get me back my principal-bond, which stands in Holstein, so that I might therewith call in and recover the money I have standing in Mecklenburg — you do me thereby a special service, and I rely confidently on you that it shall be carried out, for it greatly concerns me. I send you herewith, at your request, copies of both bonds — both how the one I received in Mecklenburg, and also the one I issued in Holstein, reads — so that you may know how to govern yourself accordingly. And so have the money called up at once in Mecklenburg, so that I may receive it in time; I intend, with God's help, to make better use of it here in this land, as I have written you word before. And I also wrote to you last time from the said Emperor's house Girtzis, in what manner I thought you could obtain the same sum of money to redeem the bond in Holstein — partly from what I have standing with Axel Gyldenstierne, which he has again promised that I shall receive by the term-market [Umschlag] — and I have written to him anew about it, which letter I send you herewith, that you may convey it to him at the first opportunity. You may also add to it whatever you can get for my house in Copenhagen, and whatever else you know may belong to me; and whatever is left over, which one cannot so soon dispose of, I will put out at interest in Holstein, and give 6 per hundred, as is now customary there; you will surely procure me some reliable sureties, whom I will hold harmless in every way.</p><p>As you write to me about my stud, which you have had carried over to Scania, and did not yet know how it could be sold — I will leave it to you yourself to decide, both for that and for other things, for I doubt not that you will arrange it profitably and well.</p><p>The horses you have procured for me, thanks to you for them, although they are quite dear — one gets a better bargain on horses here in this land — but I gladly content myself with what you do. The one you got from Oluf Bilde for myself pleases me well; he looked somewhat ill the first time he had come here, and had lost his trot on the way, but he is now recovering both in flesh and in his trot. I will keep him for myself, to ride on in Prague and elsewhere, so that no one shall easily get him from me, as you also advise; and many thanks to you for the fine little coach-wagon that you presented me. Whatever foals there are left on Hven that belong to me, you may do with as you yourself please — though I would rather you traded them to others than to the peasants there on the island. Concerning the fish on Hven that I wanted salted down, Tyge reports to me that he has had about 14 barrels of them salted; I had expected to get many more there, but you will surely see to both that and other matters, so that everything goes tolerably and well, and that small fry of carp and other little fish be set out again in the largest fishponds, and wherever else there is need.</p><p>David Pedersen's accounts, and what concerns them, I cannot now furnish you, for they are among my other goods that are kept at Magdeburg. But you will surely manage him, since you know by heart the account of his dealings.</p><p>Diderich the ferryman's wife's accounts are also there with the rest, so that I cannot send you those either. You well know yourself how it was erased and falsified — from which one may sufficiently understand how good the rest was — when she has rendered account for some lasts of rye and barley that she had in her keeping for some years, and there was always a shortfall of 4 or 5 bushels on each [measure] when it came to Hven; so I will first answer to her self-invented account. She can show her quittance for the same rye and barley; and she knows well that I charged her husband, in his lifetime, with no small deficiency in it — which was done with all fairness — and yet I have since received no settlement for it.</p><p>I owe the priest on Hven nothing at all, either with the smithy that I had built there at my own expense, or in any other way, for he has deserved no such thing from me. Were he to be rewarded according to his deserts, one should deal otherwise with him; nor did he come in there with my will and consent, which ought chiefly to have governed it. If David Pedersen has done anything in it, it is unknown to me. Morten the deacon will surely tell you the account of how that same priest has behaved toward me since he came there, if only he will out with it honestly. The peasants themselves let the parsonage fall into ruin, and stole away a great part of it against my prohibition, as Sven Trussen well knows, if only he will stand by the truth; and I granted the priest another plot, which I had myself bought and helped to have built, and which would surely have been better finished, had I not been compelled to depart thence. That the home-farm falls into decay, and that the castle too becomes dilapidated — I know nothing else to answer you than what I have written before: that I am not minded to make any further expense upon it. It is all too much that has been spent before; had I that money back, they would hardly be left so badly off.</p><p>Dear Eske, as you mention in your postscripts, since you had come back to Copenhagen, concerning my house there in the town — that you cannot get over 1,500 thalers for it from the ladies: it cost me, from the beginning, over 2,000 to have built, besides the plot and the other houses that stood there before; and I had it improved and spent further upon it, somewhat before I left the country, to the tune of a thousand thalers at the least, of which Mikkel Vibe knows a great part. The bell that hangs in the little spire I had made over the oriel — I would that you have it taken down at the first [opportunity] and sent to Lübeck to Jochim Buck, for it belongs to the clockwork that is in Lübeck; for I do not wish it to remain there, but I know how to put it to use here otherwise. As I also gather that one of my clocks, which shows the minutes, was likewise left behind on Hven — which Tyge reports you have lent to Jørgen Brockenhuus to have another made after it — I should also gladly have it back, for I have need of it here in my observation, even though I had several such. You can have a clockmaker pack it up in a little square chest and send it likewise to Lübeck to Jochim Buck, for I well think that Jørgen Brockenhuus has now used it as much as he needs. But if he cannot get another made there, and would gladly keep it, then you may let him have it, for he is my good friend and dares sometimes to tell the truth for my sake. I should myself be glad if he would have some instruments made for himself and observe somewhat therewith in the country, as he told Tyge he would do; and when I come to know that he is in earnest about it, I will write him an instruction [as to] what is in particular to be observed each year. If the others, who understand nothing of such things, would be mocking about it, he should pay no heed to it, nor answer them anything. Could he once come out here to me, I would well, in a short time, so set him up in it that he should profit well from it, and thereafter go about it the more easily and successfully — which you may tell him on my behalf, when you come into talk with him. I am also having such things set up in various places in Germany, through those who have a taste for it. And there are some of the noble lords in Prague who have already had instruments made for themselves after those I have here with me, and would observe therewith; and His Imperial Majesty himself has some in store, and intends to have more made after some of mine, when they all arrive — which His Majesty will have at the castle in Prague for himself and for whomever he will particularly use for it, for His Majesty has great delight and pleasure in such things; and he is also minded, right after Easter, to send his chief clockmaker here to me — who is a special artificer — who shall learn from me all that pertains to it, and afterwards make such things for His Majesty.</p><p>Dear Eske and dear brother, do not think ill of me that I so often trouble you with my affairs; I have no other in the country who will take so much pains for me. If I can in any way serve you in return, you shall find me more than willing for it. And herewith I commend you, now and always, to God Almighty; may He guard you from all that may be harmful to you, whether to your weal or your life. Be so good as to bid your dear father and mother, with all our other good friends, many good nights on my behalf.</p><p>Dated in the land of Bohemia, at the imperial castle Benátky, the 22nd day of March, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung Vol. 35, Fol. 56–63, marked <i>Kladde</i> — <b>Tycho's own draft</b>. (The heading <i>[Tycho Brahe ad Eschillum Bille]</i> is the editor's.)</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"den 22 dag martij anno 1600,\"</i> from <i>\"det keyserlige slot Benatky\"</i> in <i>\"Lanthebemen\"</i> (the land of Bohemia); the catalogue label confirms <b>New Style</b> (<i>\"22. marts n.s.\"</i>). It is the <b>third of three letters of the same week</b> — to Sophie Brahe (21 March, brahe-t_16000321001), to Niels Krag (24 March, brahe-t_16000324002), and to Eske Bille here (22 March) — all carrying the same core news (the plague, Rudolf II's favour, the instruments stranded in Germany behind the frozen Elbe, the Magdeburg refusal). This letter is the <b>estate-and-property</b> member of the trio.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Eske Bille</b> (<i>Eschillus Bille</i>), a Danish nobleman, Tycho's kinsman (<i>\"kierre broder\"</i>) and the chief administrator of his Danish affairs after the emigration. Tycho calls him \"the only one in the country who will take so much pains for me.\"</li><li><b>Biographical significance — the winding-up of Hven.</b> This is among the most important documents of <b>Tycho's liquidation of his Danish establishment</b>:</li><li><b>The instruments leave Denmark.</b> Son Tyge has shipped <i>all</i> the instruments out; some are at <b>Lübeck/Hamburg</b>, the rest (with \"a great heap of my other goods\") at <b>Magdeburg</b>, awaiting transport up the <b>Elbe</b> to Bohemia — the Magdeburg city authorities' <i>\"juridical evasion\"</i> and Rudolf II's angry redirection to the <b>cathedral chapter</b> match the account given to Krag (brahe-t_16000324002).</li><li><b>Uraniborg in decay.</b> Tycho flatly records that <i>\"the home-farm falls into decay, and the castle [Uraniborg] too becomes dilapidated,\"</i> and refuses to spend more — <i>\"it is all too much that has been spent before; had I that money back, they would hardly be left so badly off.\"</i> A poignant primary witness to the abandonment of Uraniborg (1599–1600).</li><li><b>The clocks &amp; the bell.</b> The <b>minute-showing clock</b> (<i>seierværk … som viser minuterne</i>) left on Hven and lent to <b>Jørgen Brockenhuus</b> is recalled for Tycho's observations; the <b>bell in the oriel spire</b> is to be taken down and sent to <b>Lübeck</b> (to Jochim Buck) to rejoin its clockwork. Tycho's instrument-clocks are central to his observational programme.</li><li><b>The Copenhagen house &amp; Danish capital.</b> The town house (built for &gt;2,000 thaler + ~1,000 in improvements, per <b>Mikkel Vibe</b>) fetches only 1,500 from prospective lady-buyers; Tycho is calling in bonds in <b>Holstein</b> and <b>Mecklenburg</b>, drawing on money held by <b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> (payable at the Kiel <i>Umschlag</i> term-market), and reinvesting surplus in Holstein at 6 %.</li><li><b>Diffusion of Tychonic instruments.</b> Rudolf II keeps copies of Tycho's instruments at Prague castle and will have more made; Tycho is helping set up observing \"in various places in Germany\"; he encourages <b>Brockenhuus</b> as a Danish observer and offers a yearly observing-instruction. A vivid statement of the spread of Tycho's instrumental astronomy.</li><li><b>The Emperor's chief clockmaker.</b> Rudolf II intends, \"right after Easter,\" to send \"his chief clockmaker, who is a special artificer,\" to learn the instrument-craft from Tycho — a notable datum on the technical exchange at the imperial court (the imperial <i>Hofuhrmacher</i> of this period; not named here, and not to be confidently equated with Jost Bürgi, whose Prague imperial appointment is documented somewhat later).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><b>Tyge</b> — Tycho Brahe the Younger (courier; oversaw the shipping of the instruments).</li><li><b>the Emperor / His Imperial Majesty</b> — <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Cecilie Urne (<i>fru Citzelle Orne</i>)</b>, <b>Mistress Johanne Oxe (<i>jomfru Jehanne/Oxe</i>)</b>, <b>the late Albret Oxe (<i>sal. Albrit Ochse</i>)</b>, and <b>Frederik (<i>Frerich/Frederich</i>)</b> — the parties to a tangled debt-and-inheritance suit (a 500-thaler claim secured by a bond, a district-court judgment, co-heirs, a town-council contract, and a quittance). Their exact relationships are not spelled out in the letter; left as named.</li><li><b>Axel Gyldenstierne</b> — Danish statesman (governor of Norway), Tycho's financial adviser and a holder of his funds.</li><li><b>Oluf Bilde</b> — supplied a riding-horse; a kinsman of Eske Bille.</li><li><b>Mikkel Vibe (<i>Michel Wibe</i>)</b> — Copenhagen official/merchant, witness to the cost of Tycho's town house.</li><li><b>Jochim Buck (<i>Jochum Buch</i>)</b> of Lübeck — custodian of Tycho's clockwork.</li><li><b>Jørgen Brockenhuus</b> — Danish nobleman and astronomy enthusiast, \"my good friend [who] dares sometimes to tell the truth for my sake,\" borrower of the minute-clock.</li><li><b>David Pedersen (<i>Dauid Pedersøn</i>)</b> — Tycho's steward/accountant (his accounts boxed up at Magdeburg).</li><li><b>Diderich the ferryman's wife</b> — accused of falsified grain accounts on Hven.</li><li><b>Morten the deacon (<i>Morten deyn</i>)</b> and <b>Sven Truelsen (<i>Suen Trussen</i>)</b> — Hven witnesses to the priest's conduct and the ruin of the parsonage.</li><li>the <b>priest on Hven</b> (unnamed) and the <b>ladies (<i>die frower</i>)</b> negotiating for the Copenhagen house.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <b>Benátky</b> (<i>Benatky</i>); <b>Prague</b>; <b>Girsitz / Girtzis</b> — an imperial residence where Tycho stayed ~6–7 weeks in Nov 1599 during the plague (a Bohemian crown estate near Benátky; precise identification uncertain); <b>Lübeck, Hamburg, Magdeburg</b>; <b>Holstein</b> (<i>Lantheholsten</i>) and <b>Mecklenburg</b> (<i>Lanthemeckelburg</i>), where Tycho's capital is bonded; the <b>Kiel <i>Umschlag</b></i> (<i>omslagid</i>, the term-payment money-market); <b>Copenhagen</b>; <b>Hven</b> (<i>Huaen</i>, with Uraniborg); <b>Scania</b> (<i>Skonne</i>).</li><li><b>Reading cruxes.</b> (1) <i>\"min stott … du haffuer ladit ført offuer till Skonne\"</i> — <i>stott</i> is <b>ambiguous</b>: most likely \"stud\" (a <i>stod</i>, a herd of breeding horses, consistent with the horse-and-foal matter that follows), though a \"monument/column\" (<i>støtte</i>) cannot be excluded; rendered \"my stud\" with this flag. (2) <i>\"[ha]fde[?]\"</i> in the Cecilie-Urne passage is an editor-conjectured reading. (3) <i>\"enten till saell eller liff\"</i> — <i>saell</i> read as <i>sæl/sjæl</i>, hence \"weal\" (or \"soul\"); rendered \"weal.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The witness's surname was mistranslated as “Sven Truelsen”; both our source transcription and Dreyer's print (TBOO XIV p.188) consistently give “Sven Trussen”. Corrected.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. The Early Modern Danish estate-prose is rendered securely, and the letter's substance — the shipping-out of the instruments, the decay of Uraniborg, the clock-and-bell recovery, the Copenhagen house, the Holstein/Mecklenburg bond-liquidation, the diffusion of Tychonic instruments and Rudolf II's clockmaker, the encouragement of Brockenhuus — is clear, important, and cross-corroborated by the two sibling letters of the same week. The one genuinely tangled stretch is the <b>Cecilie Urne / Johanne Oxe / Albret Oxe debt-and-inheritance suit</b> (legally dense, with one editor-conjectured reading), translated faithfully clause-by-clause but resistant to confident reconstruction of who owed what to whom; plus the <b>ambiguous word <i>stott</b></i> (stud vs. monument). None obscures the letter's through-line.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.184-189 (page images, not OCR text layer): opening greeting, all sums (700, 500, 1500, 2000, 1000 thalers; 6 per hundred interest), all dates (6-7 weeks at Girsitz, 8-10 weeks' leave, 14 barrels of fish, 4-5 bushels shortfall), all place-names (Girsitz/Girtzis, Lübeck, Hamburg, Elbe, Magdeburg, Holstein, Mecklenburg, Copenhagen, Scania, Hven, Prague, Bohemia/Benatky), and all proper names (Eske Bille, Tyge, Cecilie Urne, Johanne, Frederik, Axel Gyldenstierne, Albret Oxe, Oluf Bilde, David Pedersen, Diderich the ferryman's wife, Morten the deacon, Mikkel Vibe, Jochim Buch, Jørgen Brockenhuus) and the closing date (22 March 1600) match between source_text and Dreyer's print. One name error found: Suen Trussen (source and Dreyer agree) rendered as 'Sven Truelsen' in the English. No fabrication, omission, or hedge-loss found; no other TEXT_ERROR between source_text and Dreyer.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). The witness's surname is given as Trussen in both the source transcription and Dreyer's print; the English substitutes the different surname Truelsen. At: “as Sven Truelsen well knows, if only he will stand by the truth”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV p.188 (page image, read visually, not the OCR text layer): the line “fom Suen Truffen vell ved, om han ellers vill beftaa fanden” confirms the surname is Trussen, agreeing with our own Danish source_text. The single defect is a straightforward proper-name substitution in the English translation; no other part of the letter was touched. Nothing else in this short 1-defect packet remains uncertain.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 56-63.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Eske Bille", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991029001", "next": "brahe-t_16001116001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991029001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports stable conditions in Bohemia, notes the Emperor has been absent from Prague for six months due to epidemic sickness, and expects his return after Easter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000322001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000322002", "d": "1600-03-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Kirsten Lindenov", "ck": "Kirsten Lindenov", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Kirsten (Christine) Lindenov — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 22 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 64 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTINAM LINDENOV]</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och allthiid forsend met Vor Herre. Kierre fru Chirstine och kierre modersøster, Gud allmechtigste beware eder nadelligen och well fran allt ont, och nest megen tacksigelse for allt bewist gode, huilket vdigien att forskylde ieg stedtze vill findis aff formuen willig. Giffuer ieg eder gandske wenligen thilkiende, att eders skriffuelße er mig thillhende kommen met min søn Thyge och derhos thuende breffue fra eder och eders datter, jomfru Elisabet, till eders søn Clauß. Saa kan jeg eder wenligen icke forholde, att samme eders søn er icke nu her hoß mig, mens der han haffde werrit her wed it fierring aar, bleff hand tillsindz att wille drage ind vdj Franckerig der att lerre sprockit, eptherdj han fornam, att samme sprock war nu icke saa møgit bruelligt vdj keyserens gaar som tillforn, eptherdj die thaller nu mest der jtaliensch och spansch, foruden tydsk och bemisch, och kom imod forgangen jeullethiid en dansk magister till mig ved naffn mester Niels Hammer, som thillforn haffde werrit y Franckerig och kunde sprogit och wiste der leyligheden, hannom thog han did ind met sig, huilket mig och søntiß gott att werre, paa det han icke skulde wandre allene; thi den dreng, han tog met sig aff Danmarck, bleff befengt och døde aff pestelenße, førend mand rett aff wiiste, thi herr war en megit skrøffuellige thiid y forgangen aar, och ieg haffde omhu noch for Clauß, for hand icke wille ware sig well, saa att han och icke achtede huad drengen skade, och mente, att det haffde ingen nød, intill det war forsent och slet vde met hannom. Siiden bleff samme drengs lig her nere vdj bøen hedellig begraffuit, och bleff mine folck so forferrit, att ieg fick strax att føre dennom hen paa itt andit af keyserns slott, ved 3 mill herfra liggendis, och Clauß fulde met oss, hor wij warre thillsammen ved 7 vger. Siden emod ieg achtid att drage hid vdigien, thog Clauß sig den reyse for, som sagt er, helst for han fornam, att keysern var icke snart ventendis till Prag vdigien, som var dragen der fran for bemelte farlig søgdom skyld thill en fast stad, som kaldis Pilsen, 10 mille derfran liggendis, hor hanß may. endnu er, och neppe kommer til Prag forend epther paaske. Jeg var thillsindz att wille haffue flid Clauß en god thieniste vdj keyserns gaar, menß der han fornam, att der holtz saa stor pracht och offuerflødighed, att mange therrit vell mehre end deris besolding kunde beløffue, som och well andensteds skier, och for dett fransøsiske sprock, som sagt er, nu icke der var sønderlig gengse, løstid hannom heller att drage ind vdj Franckerig, hor hand och nogenstund thillforn haffde werrit, och den forneunte magister, som fuldis med hannom, loffuit mig met hon och mund, so frembst han vilde haffue nogen thiid got enten aff mig eller nogen anden aff Clausis slecht eller wenner, att han wille tage flittellig vare paa hannom och werre hannom hold och tro y allemaade, som ieg och ingen thuill paa haffuer, att han io det flitelligen giørendis vorder.</p><p>Anlangendis die forbemelte breffue, som Clauß skulle haffue, huilke ankom nogit epther hand var affdragen, will ieg foruare thill Gud vill ieg kan faa att wijde, paa huad sted hand er y Franckerig att finde, och mig kan stedis wiß bod till hannom; hueß det icke inden pinsdag eller s. Hans dag ske kan, will ieg forbrenne begge breffuene, saa att ingen skall fo dennom att see eller laese. Eder steder snarer end mig bod fra Danmarck thillskuiß nu vdj angaendis sommer ind y Franckerig, naar y kan thilforn fo att wijde, hor Clauß er der att finde, som ieg tencker well, att hand skriffuer eder till med første mulighed. Gud beuare hannom och eders anden børn fra all wløcke. Och hueß ieg kan giørre, som nogen aff dennom till gode och forfremmelße kan werre, skulle y ingen tuill paa haffue, att ieg det io heller end gierne giørre will.</p><p>Hueß min thilstand och wilkor er vdj disse land tencker ieg vell, att y haffuer fornummitt aff hues ieg skreff min søster Sophi sistegang till derudom, och er allthingest, Gud ske loff, y en god wesen, forhobis att det kan herepther endnu bliffue bedre, saa att mig formedelst Gudz naade och forsøn ingenlunde fortrøder, att ieg met mine er vddragen aff Danmarck, wille kun, att det haffde lenge tillforn sked. Der er och got andenstes y werden att werre, naar man kan kun tro det och befalle Gud allting.</p><p>Kierre modersøster, vill ieg nu icke lenger opholde eder met denne skriffuelse, menß nu och allthiid haffue eder met allt, hueß y vell ville, Gud allmechtigste befalit. Giører vell och siger eders dater, jomfru Elisabet, mange gode netter paa mine vegne.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatick den 22 martij epther denne nø still anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTINE LINDENOV]</b></p><p>My wholly friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Lady Christine and dear aunt [mother's sister], may God Almighty preserve you graciously and well from all evil; and next [after] much thanks for all the kindness shown, which to repay I shall always be found willing according to my means. I give you quite amicably to know that your letter has come to my hand with my son Tyge, and with it <b>two letters from you and your daughter, Mistress Elisabeth, for your son Claus</b>. So I cannot amicably withhold from you that <b>your said son is not now here with me</b>: rather, when he had been here about a quarter-year, he resolved to go into <b>France</b>, to learn the language there — since he perceived that that tongue was now not so much in use at the Emperor's court as before, the people there now speaking mostly <b>Italian and Spanish, besides German and Bohemian</b> — and about last Christmastide a Danish Master came to me, by name <b>Master Niels Hammer</b>, who had formerly been in France, knew the language, and knew the lie of things there; him [Claus] took along with him, which seemed good to me too, that he should not travel alone. For <b>the servant-boy he brought with him from Denmark was infected and died of the pestilence</b> before one rightly knew it — for there was a very dreadful time here last year — and I had care enough for Claus, since he would not take good care of himself, so that he too disregarded what the boy was suffering, and thought there was no danger, until it was too late and quite over with him. Afterward the same boy's body was buried with dignity down in the town; and <b>my people were so terrified that I had at once to take them away to another of the Emperor's castles, some 3 miles thence, where we were together about 7 weeks</b>, and Claus came with us. Then, about [the time] I intended to come back here, Claus undertook that journey, as said — chiefly because he perceived the Emperor was not soon to be expected back at Prague, having gone thence, for the said dangerous sickness, to a strong town called <b>Pilsen</b>, 10 miles thence, where His Majesty still is, and scarcely comes to Prague before Easter. I had intended to procure Claus a good post at the Emperor's court; but since he perceived that <b>such great pomp and extravagance is kept there, that many spend well more than their salary can amount to</b> (as also happens elsewhere), and because the <b>French tongue</b>, as said, was now not much current there, he preferred rather to go into France, where he had also been some time before; and the said Master who went with him <b>promised me, by hand and mouth</b> — so far as he would ever have any good from me, or from any other of Claus's kin or friends — that he would take diligent care of him and be true and faithful to him in every way (of which I have no doubt that he will diligently do so).</p><p>Concerning the aforesaid <b>letters meant for Claus</b>, which arrived somewhat after he had departed, I will keep [them] until — God willing — I can learn at what place he is to be found in France, and a sure messenger to him can be had; if that cannot happen before <b>Pentecost or St. John's Day</b>, I will <b>burn both letters</b>, so that no one shall get them to see or read. You can get a messenger sooner than I, sent from Denmark this coming summer into France, when you can first learn where Claus is to be found — as I think he will write you at the first opportunity. May God preserve him and your other children from all misfortune; and what I can do to the good and advancement of any of them, you shall have no doubt I will do, more than gladly.</p><p>What my condition and circumstances are in these lands, I think you have perceived from what I last wrote to my sister Sophie about it; and everything is, God be praised, in a good state, [I] hoping it may yet grow better hereafter — so that, by God's grace and providence, <b>I in no way regret that I, with mine, have departed from Denmark</b> — would only that it had happened long before. There is also good [reason] to be elsewhere in the world, if only one can believe it and commit everything to God.</p><p>Dear aunt, I will not now detain you longer with this letter, but, now and always, commend you, with all you wish well, to God Almighty. Be so good as to wish your daughter, Mistress Elisabeth, many good nights on my behalf.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky, the 22nd of March by this New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; kinship.</b> <b>Christine (Kirsten) Lindenov</b>, whom Tycho addresses as <i>modersøster</i> — \"<b>mother's sister</b>,\" i.e. his <b>maternal aunt</b> (a sister of Beate Bille, married into the Lindenov family). Her <b>son Claus</b> and <b>daughter Mistress Elisabeth</b> are named. The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.</li><li><b>A young kinsman \"Claus\" — identity to firm up (flagged).</b> The <b>Claus</b> here (Christine Lindenov's son, who went to France) is most likely <b>distinct from Claus Mule</b> (son of Mette Kotte of Nislevgård; brahe-t_15990925001, brahe-t_15980910001) — different mother, different family — though both are young men of Tycho's orbit named Claus going abroad. The exact identification (and whether the two Clauses could overlap) remains open; left unconflated here.</li><li><b>Significance — a personal letter of the plague year (rich detail).</b></li><li><b>Claus's plans.</b> Having spent ~a quarter-year with Tycho, Claus chose to go to <b>France to learn French</b> — Tycho noting that <b>French was now little used at the imperial court, where Italian, Spanish, German, and Bohemian (Czech) prevailed</b> (a vivid datum on the court's languages). He travelled with a Danish <b>Master Niels Hammer</b>, who knew France, and who pledged \"by hand and mouth\" to look after him.</li><li><b>The plague at Benátky (eyewitness).</b> The <b>servant-boy Claus brought from Denmark caught the plague and died</b> before its danger was recognized; he was \"buried with dignity down in the town,\" and Tycho's terrified household <b>fled to another imperial castle ~3 miles off for ~7 weeks</b>. A concrete account of the 1599 epidemic's impact on Tycho's own household (cf. the family letters brahe-t_16000321004, brahe-t_16000321002).</li><li><b>The court's \"pomp.\"</b> Tycho had meant to place Claus at court, but Claus saw \"<b>such great pomp and extravagance … that many spend more than their salary</b>,\" and preferred France — a candid aside on imperial-court costs.</li><li><b>The letters for Claus.</b> Two letters (from Christine and her daughter) arrived after Claus left; Tycho will hold them until he learns Claus's whereabouts in France, <b>else burn them</b> \"so no one shall see or read\" — a small note of discretion.</li><li><b>Exile sentiment.</b> \"I in no way regret that I, with mine, have departed from Denmark — would only it had happened long before.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <b>Christine Lindenov</b> (Tycho's aunt) and her children <b>Claus</b> and <b>Elisabeth</b>; <b>Master Niels Hammer</b>; Tycho's son <b>Tyge</b>; his sister <b>Sophie Brahe</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benatick den 22 Martij efter denne nye stil anno 1600</i> — \"by this <b>New Style</b>\" (Tycho explicitly marking the imperial calendar for his Danish aunt). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin closing formula restored to 'Gud allmechtigste befalit' (Dreyer TBOO XIV p.191), correcting the dropped 'all-' prefix; no change to the English, which already read correctly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — clear, warm Early Modern Danish, read securely, and the substance (Claus's choice of France over the costly imperial court; the languages of the court; the plague death of the servant-boy and the household's flight to a second castle for 7 weeks; the held-or-burned letters; Tycho's no-regret exile sentiment) is rendered faithfully; graded <b>[B]</b> only for the <b>unconfirmed family identifications</b> — <i>Christine Lindenov</i> as Tycho's maternal aunt and especially whether her son <b>Claus</b> is the same as, or distinct from, <b>Claus Mule</b> — flagged rather than asserted. No textual crux otherwise.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp. 189-192 as page images (letter proper on 190-191; p.189 carries the end of a neighbouring letter plus Dreyer's own italic Latin regesta of this one, p.192 opens the next letter). Collated the whole letter: every name (Thyge, Elisabet, Claus, Niels Hammer, Sophi), number (3 mile, 7 uger, 10 mile), and place (Pilsen, Benatick) matches Dreyer. One word-level TEXT_ERROR found (dropped 'all-' prefix, low severity, does not change the sense since the English rendering is correct anyway) forces grade C per the rubric even though the verdict is MINOR.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text drops the 'all-' from 'allmechtigste' (Almighty) in the closing commendation; Dreyer's actual reading matches the letter's own opening formula ('Gud allmechtigste beware eder'). The English happens to read 'God Almighty' regardless, so the translation itself is unaffected. Dreyer prints: <i>menfs nu och allthiid haffue eder met allt, huefs y vell ville, Gud allmechtigfte befalit</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.189-192 (page images, not OCR text layer). The single TEXT_ERROR is confirmed on p.191 line 13: Dreyer's printed text reads 'Gud allmechtigfte befalit' (long-s), matching the letter's own opening formula on p.190 ('Gud allmechtigfte beware eder'). No other discrepancies found on collation of the full letter against the four printed pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 64 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that the recipient's son Claus has left for France to learn the language, as Italian and Spanish now predominate over French at the imperial court.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000322002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000322003", "d": "1600-03-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Tauchmann", "ck": "Johann Tauchmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Tauchmann — Benátky, 22 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 66f. (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD JOHANNEM TAUCHMANNUM]</p><p>MEIN freundliche grueß mit wunschung alles guttes zuuor. Erbar vnd vornehmer Hans Tauchman, inbesonder gutter freundt. Eur schreiben, den letsten decemb. forgangen jahr datirt, hab ich mit meinem son Tychoni wohll emptfangen, vnd wie jhr daein förmeldet, daß jhr demselbigen meinen son die 20 thaller, so dem brandenburgiske mathematico Mullero deputirt, solltet haben fölgen lass[en], so bericht er, daß ihr daßselbige woll habt thun wollen, aber ehr hette es nicht begehrt, das es also ist nachgeblieben, seithero eur brieff ist geschrieben worden. Jch hab auch bishero nichs vornummen von demselbigen mathematico Mullero, weiß auch nicht, ob er zu kommen gesint oder nicht. Könnet jhr derowegen vom selbigen gelt nehmen, waß jhr fur mihr außgelegt fur bottelohn vnd brieffe, das vbrige behalten biß auf seiner ankombst oder weitteren bescheidt. Last mich auch wissen, ob da kein brieffue sein ankommen von Lubech vnd Hamburgk oder auch von Wittenbergk; mich wundert, daß eur schwager vnd mein gutter freundt m. Jostelius mihr in so lange zeitt nichts zuschreibt, vnd kain beskeid lest bekommen, woruon ich ihmme zuuor habe zugeschreiben; verstendigt mich derowegen, wie jhme gehet, vnd ob er wiederumb sich befreien will. Jch will in diese tage wieder ein schreibent lassen ahn ihme abgehen, vnd so baldt er daauff antwortet, so verschicket den brieff gen Prag an dem vbersten postmeister, so wehrd ich den wohll bekommen. Vnd thu euch hiemit sambt eure liebe hausfrauue, die von mihr vnd von meinen haußfrauwen folck soll freundlich gegrussen sein, in Gott deß allmechtigen befellen.</p><p>Datirt auis dem keyserligen slottz Benattky ihn lanth zu Behemen den 22 thag martij anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to Johann Tauchmann.]</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with the wishing of all good, beforehand. Honourable and distinguished Hans Tauchmann, especially good friend. Your letter, dated the last of December of the past year, I have well received with my son Tycho; and as you therein report that you should have let my said son have the 20 thaler that were deputed to the Brandenburg mathematician Müller — so he reports that you indeed wished to do the same, but that he had not requested it, so that it thus remained undone, since your letter was written. I have also so far heard nothing from the said mathematician Müller, nor do I know whether he intends to come or not. You can therefore take, from that same money, what you laid out for me for messenger-fees and letters, [and] keep the rest until his arrival, or [until] further instruction. Let me also know whether no letters have arrived there from Lübeck and Hamburg, or also from Wittenberg; I wonder that your brother-in-law, and my good friend, Master Jöstel, writes me nothing in so long a time, and lets [me] get no answer to what I wrote him before; inform me, therefore, how he fares, and whether he wishes to marry again. I will, in these days, let a letter go off to him again; and as soon as he answers it, send the letter to Prague, to the supreme postmaster, [and] so I shall surely receive it. And I herewith commend you, together with your dear housewife — who shall be kindly greeted by me and by my housewife's household — to God the Almighty.</p><p>Dated from the Imperial castle Benátky, in the land of Bohemia, the 22nd day of March, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann (Hans) Tauchmann</b>, a contact (at or near Wittenberg) and the <b>brother-in-law of Melchior Jöstel</b>; here handling money and forwarding for Tycho.</li><li><b>Content.</b> Business and news: the <b>20 thaler</b> earmarked for \"the <b>Brandenburg mathematician Müller</b>\" (an unnamed Müller in Brandenburg service) remained undisbursed (Tycho's son Tycho not having claimed them); Tauchmann may reimburse himself for messenger- and letter-costs and keep the rest pending Müller's arrival. Tycho asks for any letters from <b>Lübeck, Hamburg, or Wittenberg</b>, and — affectionately — after <b>Jöstel</b> (his silence; \"whether he wishes to <b>marry again</b>,\" <i>sich wiederum befreien</i> = to remarry).</li><li><b>A nice cross-link.</b> Tycho instructs that Jöstel's reply be sent \"<b>to Prague, to the supreme postmaster</b>\" (<i>an dem obersten Postmeister</i>) — the very imperial post-prefect whose unreliability <b>Herwart</b> warned against in brahe-t_16000411001 (11 April 1600, three weeks later). Tycho here still trusts the Prague post.</li><li><b><i>mein Sohn Tycho</b></i> = <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>, the courier; <i>meines Hausfrauen Volk</i> = \"my wife's household/people.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>keyserligen Schloss Benátky … den 22 Tag Martij 1600</i> = <b>22 March 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label. (A draft, <i>Kladde</i>.) The bracketed <i>lass[en]</i> is an editorial completion of a damaged spot.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German is rendered securely; the matters (the 20-thaler Müller deposit; reimbursement for postage; the request for Lübeck/Hamburg/Wittenberg letters; the affectionate inquiry after Jöstel and his possible remarriage; the Prague postmaster) are all clear and corpus-consistent. The unnamed \"Brandenburg mathematician Müller\" is left unidentified (as in the source), not invented.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.190-192 page images. Located the letter as item no.186, beginning after the close of a preceding letter to Christine Lindenov and ending before a following letter to Peter Brahe. Collated the full German text against published_english including the sum (20 thaler), names (Hans Tauchman, mathematico Mullero/Müller, m. Jostelius/Jöstel), places (Lubech/Lübeck, Hamburgk/Hamburg, Wittenbergk/Wittenberg, Benattky/Benátky, Behemen/Bohemia), and the date (22 martij anno 1600); source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 66 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Instructs that funds held for the Brandenburg mathematician Müller be used to cover messenger expenses, and asks whether letters have arrived from Lübeck, Hamburg, or Wittenberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000322003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000323001", "d": "1600-03-23", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Peder Brahe", "ck": "Peder Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Peder Brahe — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 23 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 67 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO AD PETRUM BRAHE].</p><p>MIN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och allthiid forsend met Vor Herre. Kierre Peder Brade, frenne och sønderlig gode wen, Gud allmechtigste beuare dig naadelligen och well fran allt ont, och tacker ieg dig ganske gierne for allt beuist gode, hueß vdigien att forskylde ieg stedze vill findis aff formuen willig y hues mode, ieg kand giøre nogit, som du kanst haffue aere och gode aff. Kierre Peder, ieg kunde icke lade att skriffue dig till, eptherdj mig stedis nu bod did ind y landit, huilket ieg och siste gang wille haffue giort, der ieg sende min søn Tyge der ind, menß det bleff eptherlat, for ieg haffde saa megit andit att thage ware, att ieg icke fick stunder dertill, bedendis dig gierne, att du mig derudj icke wille fortencke, det bleff icke lat aff nogen on ville, och ieg vill nest Gudz hielp rette det op igien. Giør du ocsaa vell och skriff mig till, naar her ganger bod imellum, horledis dig och dine lider och hueß du vest, som mig angaaer och behøffuer att wijde. Jeg haffuer sport, att du skulle haffue mist tho aff dine søner, huilket mig giør ont, menß du for att haffue gedult met Gudz wille, hand spare och beuare dine andre børn, du haffuer igien, och vnde dennom att formeris y gudfrøchtighed, død och fromhed, met all løcke och welsignelße. Kierre Peder, ieg tuill inted paa, att du io haffuer fornummit mine villkord och thillstand y denne landz eyn, der forbemelte min søn var der inde y landit, achter icke derfor fornøden det nu ødermere att repetere. Alltingest er endnu, Gudskeloff, y samme wesen, och forhaabis, att det herepther enda kan bliffue bedre, saa att det ingenlunde fortrøder mig, att ieg er dragen aff mitt ferneland her att bø. Och som du vdentuill end[n]u dragis tillminde, huad ieg suarede, der det bleff sagt om den, som lod sig høre, att hand paste inted paa, hor ieg var, ieg motte drage hort och nor mig løstid, vdi so maade, att ieg sagde, wij warre dißbedre att skille ad, nor somme inted holt mig och somme haffde ingen løst at bliffue, och att ieg yndskede, att det kun ellers kunde go den samme vell, han skulle herepther ingen wmag eller besuering haffue aff mig, huilket ieg tilldis vdj trø aar haffuer holt, och achter ødermehre nest Gudz hielp att hole. Huad ere mand haffuer lagt ind dermed vdj fremmede land, att man icke kunde tolle mig derudj riget, och lod mig so lettelligen komme bort, begier ieg inted att thalle om, dett giør mig sellff ont for mit ferneland skyld, att saadant skall sigis och vdspredis, eptherdj der er mange gode och oprechtige fornemme folck, der er udj landit, som inted kan dervdom, mens vden tuill sadan fortrøder. Huad hende skall, det skicker sig selff. Naar du skriffuer mig thill, da giør vel och lad mig vijde, horledis allthingest ganger till der vdj landit, och om nogle aff dine døtre erre gifft eller festemø, och hor dine sønner nu erre, Gud beuare dennom vell fran allt ont, och vnde dig att haffue glaede af dennom, den same gode Gud vill ieg haffue dig met all løcke och velfert befallit. Giør well och sig j. Doritte Gøe och din søster Sophi mange gode netter paa mine vegne.</p><p>Daterit paa det keyserlige slot Benatick y Lanthebehemen den 23 martij stylo nouo anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO TO PEDER BRAHE].</b></p><p>My most friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Peder Brahe, kinsman and especial good friend, may God Almighty preserve you graciously and well from all evil; and I thank you very gladly for all the kindness shown me, which to repay in return I shall always be found willing, according to my means, in whatever way I can do anything from which you may have honour and good. Dear Peder, I could not forbear writing to you, since a messenger is now to hand [going] into the country there — which I would have done last time too, when I sent my son Tyge in there, but it was left undone, because I had so much else to attend to that I got no leisure for it; begging you kindly not to think ill of me for it — it was not omitted out of any ill will, and I will, with God's help, make it good again. Do you also be so good as to write to me, when a messenger passes between us, how you and yours fare, and whatever you know that concerns me and that I need to know. I have heard that you have lost two of your sons, which grieves me; but you must have patience with God's will. May He spare and preserve the other children you have left, and grant them to increase in godliness, virtue, and piety, with all good fortune and blessing. Dear Peder, I doubt not that you learned of my circumstances and condition in this region when my aforesaid son was there in the country; I think it not needful, therefore, to repeat it now any further. Everything is still, God be praised, in the same state, and it is hoped it may yet grow better hereafter — so that I in no way regret that I have departed from my fatherland to dwell here. And as you doubtless still call to mind what I answered when it was said of the one [the King] who let it be heard that he cared not where I was — that I might go far and wide as I pleased — in such wise that I said: we were the better for parting, when some did not retain me and some had no wish to stay; and that I only wished it might otherwise go well with the same; he should hereafter have no trouble or burden from me — which I have kept now for three years, and intend further, with God's help, to keep. What honour one has gained thereby in foreign lands — that they could not tolerate me in the realm, and let me so easily depart — I desire not to speak of; it grieves me, for my fatherland's own sake, that such things should be said and spread abroad, since there are many good and upright men of rank in the country who can do nothing about it, but doubtless regret such things. What is to happen will settle itself. When you write to me, be so good as to let me know how everything goes there in the country, and whether any of your daughters are married or betrothed, and where your sons now are. May God preserve them well from all evil, and grant you to have joy of them; to the same good God I commend you, with all good fortune and welfare. Be so good as to wish Mistress Dorte Gøye and your sister Sophie many good nights on my behalf.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky in the land of Bohemia, the 23rd of March, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Peder Brahe</b>, addressed as <i>frænde</i> (\"kinsman\") and <i>synderlig gode ven</i> — a relative of Tycho in Denmark. (Precise identification — which Peder Brahe, and his exact kinship — to be firmed up.) The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b> in the Basel Autographensammlung.</li><li><b>Significance — a personal exile letter.</b> One of the <b>Danish family letters</b> of Tycho's exile, candid and unguarded. It carries (a) <b>condolence</b> on the death of two of Peder's sons, with the conventional consolation (\"have patience with God's will\"); (b) Tycho's <b>reflection on his self-imposed exile</b>; and (c) Tycho's continued <b>grievance against the Danish court</b>.</li><li><b>The exile and the \"King\" passage (the substantively important, and trickiest, part).</b> Tycho says he in no way regrets having \"departed from my fatherland to dwell here,\" and recalls his own retort to the report that <b>the King</b> (Christian IV) had \"let it be heard that he cared not where I was — I might go far and wide as I pleased\": namely, that <b>\"we were the better for parting,\"</b> since \"some did not retain me and some had no wish to stay\"; he wished only that it might \"go well with the same\" [the King/realm]; and that the King \"should hereafter have no trouble or burden from me — which I have kept now for <b>three years</b>, and intend to keep.\" He declines to dwell on \"what honour\" Denmark gained abroad \"that they could not tolerate me in the realm and let me so easily depart,\" grieving \"for my fatherland's own sake\" that such things are spread, while acknowledging \"many good and upright men of rank\" in Denmark who deplore it but are powerless. The passage is syntactically dense (a draft) and its pronoun references (the King / the realm / Tycho's well-wish) are read by sense; rendered faithfully, with the hedges kept.</li><li><b>Family news &amp; greetings.</b> Tycho asks after Peder's affairs, his <b>daughters</b> (married or betrothed?) and his <b>sons</b>, and closes by greeting <b>Mistress Dorte Gøye</b> and <i>\"your sister Sophie\"</i> (members of Peder's household; exact relationships to firm up — flagged). He refers to having earlier sent <b>\"my son Tyge\"</b> (Tycho the Younger) into Denmark — the same son whose Danish errand (retrieving the instruments) recurs in brahe-t_16000120001 and brahe-t_16000126001/002.</li><li><b>Textual flags.</b></li><li><i>\"gudfrøchtighed, <b>død</b> och fromhed\"</i> — \"godliness, <b>[virtue]</b>, and piety\": the draft's <i>død</i> (\"death\") is read as a slip for <b>_dyd_ (\"virtue\")</b>, which the formulaic triad requires; rendered \"virtue\" and flagged.</li><li>The \"King\" passage's tangled clauses and pronouns (above) are the main reason for the graded confidence.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>paa det keyserlige slot Benatick y Lanthebehemen den 23 martij stylo nouo anni 1600</i> — \"at the imperial castle Benátky in the land of Bohemia, 23 March, <b>New Style</b>, 1600.\" Notably Tycho dates even a letter <i>to Denmark</i> (which kept Old Style) in the imperial <b>New Style</b>, marking it explicitly. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Early Modern Danish is rendered securely and the letter's substance (condolence on two dead sons; Tycho's unregretted exile; the retort about the King's indifference and Tycho's three-year resolve to be no burden; the grief over his Danish reputation; family-news requests; greetings to Dorte Gøye and Sophie) is clear; graded <b>[B]</b> for two flagged points — the <i>død</i>/<i>dyd</i> (\"virtue\") slip in the draft, and the syntactically dense \"King\" passage whose pronoun references are construed by sense — neither smoothed, and for the <b>unconfirmed identification</b> of Peder Brahe and the two named women.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.191-193 as page images. Letter no. 187, '[TYCHO AD PETRUM BRAHE]', runs from the header on p.192 through 'stylo nouo anni 1600' further down p.192 (p.191 ends a preceding letter and p.193 begins the unrelated imperial pension warrant no.188; both read only to confirm the boundary). Collated the full Danish text word-for-word against the page images: the number of sons lost (two), the years Tycho kept his resolve (three), the names (Peder Brahe, Dorte Gøye, sister Sophie), and the closing date (23 March, new style, 1600) all match exactly. Published grade B on this letter appears to have understated it -- no defect found on full collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 67 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for long silence, expresses condolences for the recipient's two lost sons, and requests that he write with news whenever a messenger passes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000323001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000324001", "d": "1600-03-24", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "ck": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Plzeň", "t": "Johannes Jessenius (a Jessen) → Tycho Brahe — Pilsen [Plzeň], 24 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 11 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p>", "la": "<p>IOHANNES<br>IESSENIUS A IESSEN AD TYCHONEM<br>BRAHE.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>NUDIUS tertius, Nobilissime Domine TYCHO, Witebergâ ante octiduum profectus, Pelsenam veni, acturus senis mei patris caussam diuturnam atque<br>difficilem, quam peracturum me etiam et felicius et citius ex eo speraram, quod<br>apud Cæsaream Maiest. Illustriss.<br>Princeps, Saxoniæ Administrator,<br>Dominus meus Clementiss. pro me literis diligenter intercesserit. Sed uti solent<br>lento gradu absolvi omnia, ita quoque ex his præludijs aliquantulum tardius<br>negotium meum confecturum suspicor, præsertim cum aula distracta incommoditates et moras plurimas inijciat, nihilominus urgeo,<br>insto, ad tædium etiam et molestiam illorum, apud quos mihi sollicitandum<br>negotium. Capio tamen et ex his remoris nonnullam voluptatem, cum a pluribus<br>Cæsaris Consiliarijs non tantum<br>benevole admittor, sed ultro etiam accersor. Ad quos, quæ inter te et me est<br>amicitia, tam facilem mihi parat aditum, quamprimum enim tui mentionem facio,<br>illico in singularem quendam blandorem ipsorum vultum mutari sentio; habesque tu<br>certe plures, qui te hîc absentem venerantur, quam alij in os laudatores, aut<br>adulatores potius. Atque inter alia unum istud saltem brevitatis caussâ<br>habe.</p><p>Nuper cum Dominum GYPHANIUM I. C. celeberrimum et Imperij Aulicum Consiliarium<br>antehâc mihi etiam notum compellarem, inter cætera colloquia tui quoque (quem<br>tamen de facie nunquam vidit) honorificam fecit mentionem, mihi recitans, quid a<br>Magnifico Domino CORRADUCIO<br>Imperij Procancellario ipsi non ita pridem memoratum: nimirum<br>Cæsaream Maiestatem Dominum<br>CORRADUCIUM ex Italicâ Legatione reversum interrogasse, num is Romæ<br>et alibi istic aliquid de TYCHONIS BRAHEI in has oras adventu inaudierit; cui<br>cum respondisset, omnino id se tam Romæ quam alibi percepisse, mox perrexisse<br>Imperatorem, quid sibi et alijs de hac re videretur. Tum CORRADUCIUM libere respondisse: Est Vir<br>TYCHO BRAHE, cui in Astronomicis disciplinis nec hoc neque priora secula<br>aspexere parem, vixque posteriora habitura similem. Unde gratulari se, non tam<br>sibi, qui ex ipsius Cæsareæ Maiest.<br>mandato illum huc vocarit, sed longe potius Suæ Sacræ Cæsar. Maiest. de adepto hoc tanto Viro,<br>cuius industria et laboribus vulgaria excedentibus et ipsa Cæsarea Maiestas apud omnes posteros Nominis<br>sui decus et Famam auctura sit indelebili memoria; quibus dictis Cæsarem et ipsum annuisse, virumque hunc<br>apprime sibi placere charumque addidisse. Hæc clarissimus ille GYPHANIUS maximo cum applausu mihi<br>tum recitavit, suoque addito suffragio confirmavit, teque ipsius nomine plurimum<br>salvere iussit, avide tuum expectans, ubi Pragæ conveneritis, alloquium. Sed plura, cum de his, tum de<br>alijs, ubi V. D. convenerimus, quod propediem spero futurum, quandoquidem<br>Cæsaream Maiestatem mox peracto<br>Paschate Pragam cogitare constanter<br>asseritur.</p><p>Vale, et quod te non prius inviserim,<br>festinationi meæ, siquidem parentis causam laborare animadverterem, imputa,<br>excusatumque habe.</p><p>Dabantur Pilsene 24. Martij Anno<br>1600.</p><p>Tuæ Generositati addicimus<br>IOHANNES IESSENIUS A IESSEN<br>Ducum Saxoniæ Med. et<br>Professor Witeb.</p><p>Consentire hoc cum originali meo fateor ego Doctor IESSENIUS m. pr.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES JESSENIUS A JESSEN TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Three days ago, most noble Lord Tycho — having set out from Wittenberg eight days before — I came to Pilsen, to plead my old father's long and difficult case, which I had hoped to carry through both more happily and more quickly because the Most Illustrious Prince, the Administrator of Saxony, my most clement Lord, had diligently interceded for me by letters with the Imperial Majesty. But as all things are wont to be accomplished at a slow pace, so too, from these preludes, I suspect I shall conclude my business somewhat more slowly — especially as the scattered court throws up very many inconveniences and delays; nevertheless I press, I insist, even to the weariness and annoyance of those with whom I must solicit the business. Yet I take some pleasure even from these delays, since by several of the Emperor's Councillors I am not only kindly admitted, but even sent for of their own accord. To whom the friendship that is between you and me prepares so easy an access for me — for as soon as I make mention of you, I feel their countenance at once changed into a certain singular graciousness; and you certainly have more men here who venerate you in your absence than others have to praise them to their face — or rather, to flatter them. And, among other things, take at least this one, for brevity's sake.</p><p>Lately, when I addressed Lord Gifanius — the most celebrated jurisconsult and Aulic Councillor of the Empire, known to me also beforehand — among our other conversations he too made honourable mention of you (whom, however, he has never seen face to face), reciting to me what had been told him, not long before, by the Magnificent Lord Coraduz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire: namely, that the Imperial Majesty had asked Lord Coraduz, returned from his Italian legation, whether he had heard anything at Rome, and elsewhere there, about Tycho Brahe's coming into these parts; and when he had answered that he had indeed learned of it both at Rome and elsewhere, the Emperor went on [to ask] what he and others thought of the matter. Then Coraduz freely answered: <i>Tycho Brahe is a man to whom, in the astronomical disciplines, neither this age nor former ages have seen an equal, and scarcely will the ages to come have his like.</i> Whence he congratulated — not so much himself, who by the Imperial Majesty's own mandate had summoned him hither, but far rather His Sacred Imperial Majesty — on the winning of so great a Man, by whose industry and labours, surpassing the common, the Imperial Majesty himself would augment the glory and fame of his Name among all posterity, with indelible memory. At which words the Emperor himself assented, and added that this man pleased him exceedingly and was dear to him. These things that most renowned Gifanius then recited to me with the greatest applause, and confirmed with his own added approval, and bade you very heartily greeted in his name, eagerly awaiting your conversation when you shall meet at Prague. But more — both of these matters and of others — when we shall meet, which I hope will be soon, since it is constantly affirmed that the Imperial Majesty intends [to come] to Prague soon after Easter is past.</p><p>Farewell; and that I have not visited you earlier, impute to my haste — since I perceived my parent's case to be in difficulty — and hold me excused.<br>Given at Pilsen, 24 March, the year 1600.<br>Devoted to Your Generosity,<br>Johannes Jessenius a Jessen,<br>Physician to the Dukes of Saxony and Professor at Wittenberg. <i>[Attestation appended to the copy:]</i> I, Doctor Jessenius, acknowledge that this agrees with my original, in my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johannes Jessenius (Jan Jessenský z Jessenu)</b> (1566–1621), anatomist and physician — here <i>\"Physician to the Dukes of Saxony and Professor at Wittenberg.\"</i> The same friend who recurs across the corpus: Tycho lodged with him at Wittenberg (brahe-t_15981231001), called him \"the Planet\" who had not yet visited (brahe-t_16000327002), and sent him greetings via Jöstel (brahe-t_16001008001). The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.</li><li><b>The errand at Pilsen.</b> Jessenius has come to <b>Pilsen (Plzeň)</b> — where Rudolf II's court had withdrawn during the 1599 plague — to plead his <b>aged father's \"long and difficult case,\"</b> backed by a letter of intercession from his patron, the <b>Administrator of (Electoral) Saxony</b> (the regent for the under-age Elector Christian II). This explains, in his own words, why he had not yet visited Tycho (cf. brahe-t_16000327002, where Tycho noted Jessenius's non-appearance and \"his father's case in jeopardy\"). The <i>aula distracta</i> — the \"scattered/distracted court\" — is the plague-displaced court at Pilsen.</li><li><b>The Coraduz testimonial (the heart of the letter).</b> Jessenius relays, via <b>Gifanius</b>, a court testimonial to Tycho. The Emperor, asking <b>Coraduz</b> — newly back from his <b>Italian legation</b> — what was said of Tycho's coming \"at Rome and elsewhere,\" drew from him the verdict: <i>\"Tycho Brahe is a man to whom, in astronomy, neither this nor former ages have seen an equal, and scarcely will the ages to come have his like\"</i>; Coraduz congratulated the Emperor on the acquisition, foretelling it would add \"indelible\" lustre to Rudolf's name among posterity — to which <b>the Emperor assented</b>, adding that Tycho \"pleased him exceedingly and was dear to him.\" A first-rate document of Tycho's standing at the imperial court on the eve of his Prague establishment.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Gyphanius I. C.</i> = <b>Hubert van Giffen</b> (Obertus Gifanius, 1534–1604), the noted jurisconsult (<i>Iurisconsultus</i>) and <b>Imperial Aulic Councillor</b>.</li><li><i>Magnificus Dn. Corraducius, Imperii Procancellarius</i> = <b>Rudolf Coraduz (Coraduz)</b>, <b>Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b> — one of Tycho's principal court patrons (the <i>Coraduz/Barvitius</i> axis; cf. brahe-t_16000813001, brahe-t_16000106001).</li><li><i>Illustrissimus Princeps, Saxoniae Administrator</i> = the <b>Administrator (regent) of Electoral Saxony</b>, Jessenius's clement lord.</li><li><i>Caesar / Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Pilsene 24. Martij Anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial context; the dataset marks n.s.). The note that the Emperor would come to <b>Prague \"after Easter\"</b> dates the court's planned return.</li><li><b>A notarial-style collation note.</b> Recovers a short attestation appended after Jessenius's signature and title, in which Jessenius personally certifies — in his own hand (\"manu propria\") — that this copy agrees with his own retained original. This is a standard collation-endorsement formula, of the kind added when a transmitted copy of a letter is checked against the author's autograph; it is evidence for how this particular letter survives (an authenticated copy, not the unverified original).</li><li><b>Identifying Jessenius.</b> Corpus biographical sources confirm Johannes Jessenius a Jessen (Ján Jesenský), professor of medicine at Wittenberg from 1596, in whose house (the former Melanchthon/Peucer house) Tycho and his family lodged from December 1598 to June 1599 [Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (Cambridge, 2000); Christianson, <i>Tycho Brahe and the Measure of the Heavens</i> (2020); Norlind, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1970); Studnička, <i>Prager Tychoniana</i> (1901)]. Jessenius later delivered Tycho's funeral oration in Prague (1601), became the celebrated anatomist who performed Prague's first public human dissection (1600), and was among the Bohemian nobles executed in 1621 after the Battle of White Mountain — the same figure the brief flags as \"the anatomist... in Prague in Tycho's circle.\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the occasion (Jessenius at the plague-displaced Pilsen court pleading his father's case, with the Saxon Administrator's backing) and, above all, the relayed <b>Coraduz/Gifanius testimonial to Tycho</b> (with Rudolf II's assent) are clear, important, and corpus-corroborated — the non-appearance excuse dovetails exactly with brahe-t_16000327002. No textual crux.</p><p>Grade A. A short, formulaic Latin attestation clause using standard notarial/epistolary vocabulary (\"consentire cum originali,\" \"manu propria\"), unambiguous in sense. Jessenius's identity is independently and richly attested across multiple corpus biographical sources, so the historical anchoring is solid even though the clause itself carries no further biographical content beyond the authentication act.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.269-272 (letter itself on 270-271, with the autograph attestation at the foot): opening ('Nudius tertius... Witebergâ ante octiduum profectus'), the Gifanius/Coraduz anecdote with the direct-quoted encomium of Tycho, all proper names (Gyphanius, Corraducius, Tycho Brahe), the closing excuse and date (Pilsen, 24 March 1600), and the closing attestation ('Consentire hoc cum originali...') match Dreyer word for word. Translation is faithful throughout, including the embedded quotation and the bracketed identification notes. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000408001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000408001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports arrival in Pilsen to pursue his father's long legal case at court, noting that Tycho's name alone opens ready access to imperial councillors.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000324001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000324002", "d": "1600-03-24", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Niels Krag", "ck": "Niels Krag", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Niels Krag — Benátky nad Jizerou, 24 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with one Danish aside and Greek interpolation · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD NICOLAUM CRAGIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>REDDIDIT mihi filius TYCHO, statim post huius Anni initia huc ad me domum reversus, literas tuas, Excellentissime et Amantissime Domine Doctor NICOLAE KRAGIE, quae mihi gratissimae erant, utut circa initia expostulationem meam, ob diutinam literarum intermissionem, gravius quam forte decuit, ferre videaris, et Amici in iocando libertatem nonnihil perstringere, quasi veterem tuum TYCHONEM, licet sic amicos nonnunquam alloqui soleat, candide tamen de ijs sentire et constantem esse non noveris. Ago autem tibi gratias, quod meos emissarios tam benevole exceperis atque tractâris: noluissem tamen, ut meâ de caussâ eos sumptus faceres: si quid vicissim in tui gratiam a me praestari poterit, inveniar huius etiam unâ cum coeteris beneficij memor.</p><p>De statu rerum mearum, cum ex ijs, quae ad teipsum, tum etiam ROSAeKRANTZIUM nostrum perscripsi, et insuper filij relatione luculenter iam antea cognovisti, nolo hîc quidpiam repetere, quodque conditiones a Sa Ca Mte Clementissime mihi oblatae et deputatae vobis ita placuerint, ut te et tuam ELISAM non mediocri affecerint gaudio, gratum habeo. Crescit (sit Deo gratia) indies erga me Caesaris benignissimus favor, tum quoque eorum, qui ei sunt ab intimis atque hîc authoritate praevalent, licet apud quosdam morosulos non absit invidia, virtutis, uti fit, umbra. Caesar per semestre Pragae non fuit, sed in civitate quadam munita, quae 10 milliaribus inde removetur, Pilsena dicta, cum praecipuis suis consiliarijs interea moratus est, idque ob luem Epidemicam, quae totam Bohemiam et vicina loca graviter praecedente Anno usque in solstitium hybernum infestavit, quâ etiam de caussâ ego unâ cum meis in non modico fui discrimine, peste et dyssenteriâ tam in vicina urbe quam toto territorio Benatico atrociter saeviente. Quo nomine Sa Caesarea Matas proprio motu, Clementissimo animo, imo quasi Paterno quodam affectu erga me et meos non parum fuit sollicita, adeo ut aliquoties huc mitteret expiscatum, quomodo valeremus, et sponte obtulit copiam alio transeundi et pericula declinandi, si sic mihi placuisset, nominatim vero Viennam Austriae indigitavit, siquidem ea urbs contagionis expers erat, et commoditates oportunas, tam quoad habitationem, quam coetera necessaria se isthic per suos mihi ordinaturam, modo eo proficisci luberet, Clementissime obtulit. At ego, actis humillime gratijs, me potius hîc manere et omnia Divinae provisioni committere atque meis exercitijs vacare velle respondi. Quod etiam ipsius Mati non displicuit. Et antidota, quae intellexerat me contra istos morbos efficacia in promptu habere, unâ cum praeparationis ratione expetijt, quae et subiectissime communicavi. Secessi vero tandem, formidini mearum muliercularum (uti fert sexus iste) indulgens, in aliam quandam Arcem Caesaream 3 milliaribus hinc distantem (siquidem mihi liberum erat, quamcunque in viciniâ vellem, inhabitare) ubi, interea dum mei in Daniâ absunt, per aliquot hebdomadas non incommode mansi. Ago autem Deo opt. max. gratias, quod me et meos omnes, toto eo periculoso tempore, salvos et incolumes servârit. Spes est Caesam Matem statim a Paschatis festo Pragam redituram, prout plurimi avide desiderant. Cum nuper ibi fuissem, rescivi a multis, quod et pariter in itinere a Capitaneo Brandeisensi meo vicino cognoveram, non saltem cives et mercatores, sed et praecipuos quosque mihi tribuere, quod Caesar tam diu Pragâ absit, tanquam is hac in re ex meo penderet consilio, quodque accessum ante finem Martij dissuaserim, nec patuit excusationi locus, licet id serio inficiatus sim: Saaledis er mig nu her kommen for ordt. Hinc talia suspicantur, quod nonnunquam ipsius Mati de quibusdam, ipsâ sic cupiente, scribere soleam, nec liceat consiliarijs, uti alias mos est, literas meas resignare.</p><p>Instrumenta mea, quas in Dania reliqui, avecta esse, et unâ cum coeteris nunc in fundo Teuthonico conservari, Caesarea Matas libenter intellexit, eidemque gratum fuit, quod nullas hac in re isthic moverint difficultates, alias Regi vestro hoc nomine scriptura. Dedit autem literas tam ad Hamburgenses quam Magdeburgenses, ut quaecunque apud eos reposita habeo, unâ cum reliquâ meâ supellectile suis sumptibus in gratiam ipsius Matis huc per Albim advehi faciant, cumque in prioribus literis subterfugium quoddam astute invenissent Magdaeburgenes, denuo alteras misit, quae priores dilucidius exponerent, cumque ijs acceptis ad me rescriberent, ut eos hac in re apud Caesaream Matem excusatos haberem, siquidem durities obsidionis, quam olim passi sunt, eos adhuc ita premeret, ut absque suo incommodo ijs sumptibus ferendis non essent pares: quod cum a Domino BARVITIO supremo Secretario intellexisset Caesar, admodum aegre tulit, consiliarij vero eius in κακιννος resoluti sunt, quod obsidio ante 50 Annos peracta adhuc tantopere ijs incommodaret, ut exiguas istas impensas sustinere nequirent; excepitque sic quidam ex Caesaris intimis, venturum forte tempus, quo ipsius Matis operâ indigeant, tunc tergiversationum diverticula adhuc plausibiliora, quam illi effinxerunt, adinveniri posse. Iussit vero mox Caesar ad capitulum Magdaeburgense literas, hac de caussâ, scribi, quas ex suo ore nunc paratas e Cancellaria adferri procuro, mox ad Reverendum illud capitulum dirigendas, quod sponte suum erga Caesaream Matem hac in parte obsequium subiectissime obtulit, postquam rescivisset Senatores id detrectare; est enim (uti fieri solet) nonnulla inter eos aemulatio. Spero itaque me tam instrumenta quam coeteras res meas primo quoque tempore huc recepturum, nam de Hamburgensibus nihil addubito, quin voluntati Caesae Matis promptius sint obtemperaturi; interea aedificia pro Instrumentis ordine et sigillatim disponendis ante hyemem inchoata, et illius frigore superveniente, intermissa, complere satagimus.</p><p>Epigrammata duo, quae iuxta calcem sunt eorum quae mitto carminum, Caesar, ut duabus praecipuis valvis, ex dorico et Ionico opere factis, superius marmori insculperentur, mandavit. Cumque ego in primo, ut ab initio legeretur Quae Boreas renuo, et in altero suo loco Arctois pro Danis, quo Patria mea non nominaretur, Caesar iussit, ut expresse scriberetur Dania quae renuit, et in altero Epigrammate Danis pro Arctois. Sed haec non nisi secretioribus Amicis revelanda sunt.</p><p>Parant varijs in locis plures venâ Poëticâ pollentes eiuscemodi carmina, in mei gratiam, inter quos est IOSEPHUS SCALIGER, qui subinde mihi scripsit, et MELISSUS, Poëtarum nostri temporis antesignanus : et quia memini me aliquando tibi pollicitum, eorum quae accepero, ubi in aliquam copiam excrevissent, communicationem, haec nunc impertior, donec plura nactus fuero, tibi soli reservanda, et non nisi OLIGERO nostro et alias integris atque sincere sentientibus monstranda.</p><p>De ijs quae mihi in Daniâ evenerunt, non est quod existimes, me quidpiam scripturum aut publicaturum, nisi extrema sic urserit necessitas, atque vestrates occasionem praebuerint, utinam alij aeque silere vellent; sunt enim nonnulli, qui meâ sorte plus afficiantur atque aegrius hanc mutationem ferant (ob Astronomiae praesertim, quam affectant, instaurationem) quam ego, qui omnia infracto animo perfero; nec mihi adversi quid sic evenisse autumo; sic neque Polemica scripta unquam êditurus sum, utilioribus et magis serijs intentus, ut non opus fuisset eâ quam adducis admonitione, mihi tamen non ingratâ. Obstrepent veritati et calumnientur, nobisque illudant, aliorumque scurrilibus convitijs applaudeant, quicunque volent: Stans tegor in solido etc. Si tamen alij sponte sua pro veritate asserenda in mei honorem quidpiam scripserint, incivile et inhumanum foret idipsum averso animo improbari. Quod vero tu in gratiam istius Scotuli veritatem tibi, si cui alias, satis perspectam dissimulare et vinclis constringere malis, ferendum duco, neque enim invito libenter quidpiam extorserim, et perinde erit, sive id quod res est aperte professus fueris, sive non; id tamen, etiam te dubitante, certo constat, eum in publica quadam et impressa intimatione, valvis Collegij Helmstadij affixa, cuius exemplum penes me est, sibi meam arrogasse inventionem, praeter ea, quae privatim effutijt; quod quo iure fecerit, nemo melius quam tu (si velles) qui adfuisti, cum ipsi eam Hypothesin aperirem, testari poterit. Sed missum facio ista, tibi, uti video, morosa.</p><p>De MULAeO vestro quae subiungis, te metuere, ne mora illa longior, quam apud vos traxit, ab Italicâ profectione per hyemem iam praeteritam eum sit remoratura: id equidem sic est; neque enim aliqua concedebatur occasio, quâ commode eo, postquam huc redijt, proficisceretur: sed tamen, appropinquante iam vere, modum adinveni, quâ id nihilominus fieri posse sperem; commendavi enim ipsum diligenter Serenissimo Electori Coloniensi, qui mihi Clementissimâ propensione favet; per eius Celsitudinem ipsum promotum iri, ut tam Italiam quam Gallias, prout in votis habet, perlustrare commode queat, mihi persuadeo. Constituit autem prius ad suos in Daniam ire, ut isthic sibi acquirat viaticum; et res suas ad iter istud disponit. Quin et ego pariter ut tu sentio ex re ipsius non fore, ut de me nihil dicam, ipsum diutius hîc iuveniles suos Annos consumere, quibus potius peregrinationibus et studijs vacandum, neque enim eae, quas ego tracto, disciplinae ipsi usui sunt, uti etiam earum minus capax.</p><p>TENGNAGLIUS meus sub Brumam huc ad me ex Italia, ubi intra annum linguam probe didicit, moribus et favoribus auctus, reversus est; ante octiduum in Patriam suam ivit, Parentes isthic salutaturus; postea, volente Deo, huc rediens, in Aula Caesaris honesto loco aliquamdiu futurus.</p><p>Quae de honoribus Politicis mihi ex Aulâ Caesarea indicatis, cum adhuc Wittebergae essem, mones, ut prius in possessionem feudi veniam, recte quidem mones, nam et ego in ea sum sententia, sed non plane intelligis, quid innuere voluerim, neque enim hoc est, de quo tecum ante abitum meum in Dania locutus sum, sed quiddam insuper aliud, de quo prius non cogitavi, prout suo tempore [D.] V. percipies.</p><p>Apud TYPOTIUM de eo, quod scis, te iam antea sufficienter purgavi, et ubi convenerimus, id etiamnum ulterius faciam. Est is mei amantissimus, et ubicunque occasio datur, de me et med familiâ honorifice sentit et loquitur. Studia vero nostra a meipso probanda, cum eorum non sit gnarus, asserit.</p><p>Non dubito, quin multa in Legatione tam Polonicâ quam Anglicanâ, a Doctis hinc inde audieris, haud dissimilia ijs, quae MICHAELIDES narravit, licet is Wordingburgi apud Arcis Praefectum calumnias effutire, nec tamen impune, non erubuerit, prout forte a TYCHONE meo percepisti; ideoque eo promptius carmina, ob quae ista fingebat, tecum communico, ut dispicias, nihil in ijs reperiri in Regem, de quo semper honorifice loqui, eumque excusare soleo, aspere et indecenter prolatum. Narratum mihi est a quibusdam Danis, cum Wittebergae essem, eos, cum per Bohemiam aliquando peregrinarentur, a Parocho quodam Pontificiae doctrinae admonitos fuisse, ne se Danos esse profiterentur, sed potius ex aliqua Germaniae parte oriundos dicerent, partim quod Rex in coronatione Pontificis Romani habitum ludicre imitatus fuisset, partim quod me sic amisissent. Quid IVARUS FRIIS per Ungariam et Austriam, praesente Regis fratre (qui tamen, quis esset, ignorabatur) meâ de caussâ, audire et decorare necesse habuit, ipsemet, cum tibi sit familiaris, aliquando forsitan narrabit; dum ea Dresdae me invisens inter alia suggerere voluit, datâ operâ orationem alio declinavi, nolens certis de caussis ista mihi referri, qui usque ad nauseam similia saepius inaudivi. Viderint illi, qui in culpa sunt. Deus in Patria nostra animorum coniunctionem largiatur et stabiliat, dissidia et bellorum semina auferat. Quid Legati vestri in Suecia expedierint, a te cognoscere cupio, tum quoque quid praeterea apud vos et in toto illo Boreali tractu geratur.</p><p>Nova, quae hinc tibi significem, pauca in promptu habeo. De recuperata Transylvania te iam antea intellexisse opinor. Aiunt autem BAIUODAM, qui eam fortiter pugnando pro Caesare recepisse putabatur, sibi feudi loco reservare constituere, ipso etiam Caesare invito, quamobrem Legati eius nuper Pilsnae fuerunt, qui cum se impatienter et pervicaciter gessissent, missus est qui eos apud BAIUODAM accusaret, increpante Caesare admodum severe senem quendam eorum primarium. Turcam maiorem quam unquam antea parare contra Ungariam exercitum, rumor est. Deus Armipotens furorem et vires eius retundat: Caesar noster vicissim se equitibus et militibus, quotquot nancisci poterit, illi obviaturus, munit. Imperij Principes, maiori ex parte, suppetias detrectant, Hispanos potius quam Turcas formidantes. Deus author concordiae et pacis nos protegat et a malis liberet.</p><p>Doleo vicem consiliariorum Sueticorum, si graviter in eos saevierit CAROLUS; sibi ipsi minus prudenter consuluerit, quâ etiam de re, ut in ultimis tibi indicavi, TYPOTIUS, me consentiente, ipsum per literas admonuit, exemplum ERICI fratris proponens.</p><p>MOSCUS apud Caesarem et Archiducem MAXIMILIANUM nonnulla tacite molitur, quae si successerint, Rex Poloniae maluisset utique se avito regno non excessisse. Quod meâ etiam opinione vix recuperabit unquam. Non minor est virtus, quam quaerere, parta tueri.</p><p>Plura iam non addo, neque enim publicas tumultuationes, quibus hîc fluctuans terrenus Orbis iamdudum assuevit, multum moror; nihil novi sub Sole, eadem fere semper luditur fabula, sub alijs atque alijs Personis. Si paulo fusius quam forte velles, tibi hac Epistola scripsisse videar, scias id consulto admissum, ut et tu copiose rescribas. Tantum abest ut prolixitas, quam in tuis ultimo excusas, mihi displicuerit. Rariores nunc conceduntur internuncij, plenius itaque per literas colloquemur, siquidem oretenus non detur. Nam quod tu, ut aliquoties ad vos excurram, optas, ita facile, ac lubenter vellem, fieri nequit. Constitueram quidem ad comitia huius Anni, si qua apud vos celebrarentur, proficisci: at quia de ijs incertus sum, nec Caesar hoc primo Anno veniam in Daniam eundi mihi concedere volet, id in alterum Annum, si Deo ita visum fuerit, differendum erit. Quomodo vero id meo cum bono, uti insinuas, futurum sit, a te apertius intelligere aveo.</p><p>Vale quam optime, salutatâ tuâ coniuge a meâ et filiabus peramanter.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 24 Martij stylo renovato Anni 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 277–278; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. QUONIAM soror mea, URANIA illa Terricula, in ultimis suis nonnulla insinuavit de ☿rio vestrate (scis quem notet) quod is Landskroniae quaedam de Caesarea âte minus honorifice locutus, postea etiam de Consiliarijs praecipuis aliqua subiunxisset, quae omnia eo vergebant, ut illam de successu rerum mearum dubiam et diffidentem redderet, tanquam neminem hic inventurus essem, qui me meaque promoveret, et illos, a quibus maxime sperabam quidpiam auxilii, in mala esse apud Caesarem gratia nulliusque authoritatis: licet vero ipsa satis percipiens, quorsum ista tenderent, dilueret, quantum tunc temporis potuit, ipsius prolata minus candida, atque regereret, quae incidebant, uti est ingeniosula, neque multum intersit, quid ille vel sui similes iudicent, scimus enim, quo animo proveniant: tamen ut URANIAM illam per occasionem instruere et adhuc magis confirmare possis, rem (sit Deo gratia) longe aliter se habere, mitto tibi exemplum literarum, quas TENGNAGLIUS meus nuper, dum Pilsnae in Aula esset, ad me scripsit, antequam inde in Patriam suam discederet, ut hinc intelligas, quale sit non saltem Magnifici Domini BARVITIJ, qui singulis pene horis Caesari praesto est, utpote Secretarius summus, sed et Illustris ac Amplissimi Domini CORADUCIJ, Imperij Procancellarij, qui praecipuae in Aula merito est authoritatis, erga me studium et favor. Addidi etiam Domini Doctoris IESSENIJ, qui hospes meus Wittebergae in aedibus Philippaeis erat, circa eadem tempora Pilsnae caussam quandam agentis, literas in eandem ferme sententiam, paulo tamen fusius de Domino CORADUCIO exaratas, estque ipsa Iessenij manus. Poteris contenta utriusque Epistolae sorori, ubi conveneritis, exponere, ut habeat materiam, quâ instabilem illum et versipellem ☿rium figat atque retundat, si forte quando similia denuo suggesserit, vel apud alios pro suâ dicacitate effutierit. Misi nihilominus et ego in literis meis ad eandem URANIAM quasdam Germanice, a quodam meo domestico, paulo antea Pilsnâ ad me scriptas, quae de singulari Domini CORADUCIJ erga me benevolentia et candido affectu inter alia mentionem faciunt; de quibus illa vicissim ad te referre poterit. Sunt et alij in Aulâ Caesarea Illustres et eximiae dignitatis viri, qui mihi in meis rebus bene cupiunt, ut de ipsa Sacra Caesarea âte, quae in proposito constans est, nec aliorum, si quae forent, sinistra iudicia tam leviter, uti alibi sit, non moratur. Nihil nunc addam. Habeo Deo Opt. Max. pro hac mea sorte gratias, mihique ut in posterum suâ tutelâ et benedictione adesse dignetur, oro. Iterum vale. Datum ut supra in literis.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Niels Krag.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>My son Tycho, returning home to me here just after the beginning of this year, delivered your letter, most excellent and most loving Lord Doctor Niels Krag — a letter most welcome to me, even though at the outset you seem to take my reproach (over the long lapse in our correspondence) more heavily than was perhaps fitting, and to chide somewhat a friend's freedom in jesting, as though you did not know that your old Tycho, though he sometimes addresses his friends so, nonetheless thinks candidly of them and is constant. I thank you, moreover, for receiving and entertaining my emissaries so kindly; yet I should not have wished you to incur those expenses on my account. If anything can in turn be rendered by me for your benefit, you will find me mindful of this kindness too, along with the rest.</p><p>Of the state of my affairs — since you already learned it plainly both from what I wrote to you and to our Rosenkrantz, and besides from my son's report — I will here repeat nothing; and that the terms most graciously offered and assigned to me by His Sacred Imperial Majesty so pleased you, that they affected you and your Elisa with no small joy, I take kindly. The Emperor's most benign favour toward me grows daily (thanks be to God), as does that of those who are of his inner circle and prevail here in authority — though with certain peevish folk there is no lack of envy, the shadow (as it goes) of virtue. The Emperor was not at Prague for half a year, but stayed meanwhile, with his chief councillors, in a certain fortified town ten miles distant thence, called Pilsen — and that on account of the epidemic pestilence which in the past year grievously afflicted all Bohemia and the neighbouring places, right up to the winter solstice; for which cause I too, together with my people, was in no small peril, plague and dysentery raging cruelly both in the neighbouring town and throughout the whole territory of Benátky. On this score His Sacred Imperial Majesty was, of his own accord, with a most clement spirit — nay, with a kind of fatherly affection toward me and mine — not a little concerned, so much so that he several times sent here to ascertain how we fared; and of his own motion he offered the chance of moving elsewhere and avoiding the dangers, if it should so please me — naming, in particular, Vienna in Austria, since that city was free of contagion, and most graciously offered that he would have suitable conveniences arranged for me there through his officers, both as to lodging and the other necessaries, if only I were willing to go thither. But I, having rendered most humble thanks, replied that I would rather remain here, commit everything to Divine providence, and be free for my own pursuits. Which did not displease His Majesty either. And he requested the antidotes which he had learned I had ready and effective against those diseases, together with the manner of their preparation — which I most submissively imparted. I did at length withdraw, however — indulging the fear of my womenfolk (as that sex is wont) — into a certain other Imperial castle three miles distant from here (since I was free to dwell in whatever place in the vicinity I wished), where, while my people are away in Denmark, I stayed for some weeks not inconveniently. And I give thanks to God most good and great that He has kept me and all my people safe and unharmed throughout that whole dangerous time. There is hope that His Imperial Majesty will return to Prague immediately after the feast of Easter, as very many eagerly desire. When I was lately there, I learned from many — what I had likewise gathered on the road from the Captain of Brandeis, my neighbour — that not only the citizens and merchants but also the foremost men attribute it to me that the Emperor is so long absent from Prague, as though he depended in this matter on my counsel, and that I had advised against his coming before the end of March; nor was there room for excuse, though I seriously denied it. <i>Such is the talk that has now arisen about me here.</i> They suspect such things because I am sometimes accustomed to write to His Majesty about certain matters, at his own wish, and the councillors are not allowed — as is otherwise the custom — to open my letters.</p><p>That my instruments, which I left in Denmark, have been carried away and are now kept, together with the rest, on German soil, His Imperial Majesty was glad to learn; and it pleased him that they raised no difficulties about this there — otherwise he would have written to your King on this account. He gave letters both to the people of Hamburg and to those of Magdeburg, that whatever I have deposited with them, together with the rest of my furnishings, they should at their own expense, as a favour to His Majesty, have conveyed here by the Elbe; and since in the earlier letters the Magdeburgers had cunningly found a certain subterfuge, he sent further letters which set out the former more plainly; and when, on receiving these, they wrote back to me asking that I have them excused in this matter before His Imperial Majesty — since the hardship of the siege they once suffered still so pressed them that, without inconvenience to themselves, they were not equal to bearing those expenses — when the Emperor learned this from Lord Barvitius, his chief Secretary, he took it very ill, and his councillors broke into <i>laughter</i> [κακιννος], that a siege carried out fifty years ago should still so inconvenience them that they could not sustain those trifling expenses; and one of the Emperor's intimates remarked thus, that a time might perhaps come when they would need His Majesty's good offices, and then more plausible by-ways of evasion than these they had contrived could be found. The Emperor soon ordered that letters be written, for this cause, to the cathedral chapter of Magdeburg — which letters, now prepared from his own mouth, I am having brought from the Chancery, to be directed presently to that Reverend chapter, which has of its own accord most submissively offered its compliance toward His Imperial Majesty in this matter, after learning that the city Senators were declining it; for (as commonly happens) there is some rivalry between them. I hope, therefore, to receive here both the instruments and my other things at the first opportunity; for of the Hamburgers I have no doubt that they will more readily obey His Imperial Majesty's will. Meanwhile we are striving to complete the buildings — begun before winter, for arranging the instruments in order and singly, and broken off when its cold came on — for setting up the instruments.</p><p>Two epigrams, which are near the foot of the verses I send, the Emperor has ordered to be carved above, on the marble, on the two chief portals, made of Doric and Ionic work. And whereas I had wished that in the first it be read from the beginning <i>\"which I, the North, refuse,\"</i> and in the second, in its own place, \"the Northern lands\" in place of \"the Danes,\" so that my fatherland should not be named, the Emperor ordered that it be written expressly <i>\"Denmark, which refused,\"</i> and in the second epigram \"the Danes\" in place of \"the Northern lands.\" But these things are to be revealed to none but the more confidential friends.</p><p>In various places several men strong in the poetic vein are preparing verses of this kind in my honour — among them Joseph Scaliger, who has written to me from time to time, and Melissus, the standard-bearer of the poets of our age. And because I remember that I once promised you a sharing of what I should receive, once they had grown to some quantity, I now impart these — to be reserved for you alone until I have got more, and shown to none but our Holger and others of upright and sincere sentiment.</p><p>Of the things that befell me in Denmark, you have no reason to suppose that I shall write or publish anything, unless the utmost necessity so press, and your countrymen give occasion — would that others were equally willing to keep silent; for there are some who are more moved by my lot, and bear this change more grievously (especially on account of the restoration of astronomy, which they cherish), than I, who endure everything with an unbroken spirit; nor do I reckon that anything adverse has thus befallen me. Thus neither shall I ever publish polemical writings, being bent on more useful and more serious matters — so that there was no need of that admonition you bring forward, though it was not unwelcome to me. Let them clamour against the truth and slander, and mock us, and applaud the scurrilous abuse of others, whosoever will: <i>I stand sheltered on solid ground,</i> and so forth. Yet if others, of their own accord, in defence of the truth, write something in my honour, it would be uncivil and inhuman to disapprove it with an averse mind. But that you, for the sake of that little Scot, prefer to dissemble the truth — which is sufficiently clear to you, if to anyone — and to bind it in chains, I think must be borne; for I would not gladly wring anything from an unwilling man, and it will be all the same whether you openly profess what the case is or not. Yet this much is certain, even though you doubt it: that he, in a certain public and printed notice affixed to the doors of the College at Helmstedt — of which I have a copy — arrogated to himself my invention, besides the things he blabbed in private. By what right he did this, none can better testify than you (if you would), who were present when I disclosed that Hypothesis to him. But I let these matters drop, troublesome to you, as I see.</p><p>As to what you add about your Mule — that you fear that the long delay he has incurred among you will hold him back, the winter being now past, from his journey to Italy: indeed it is so; for no opportunity was granted by which he might conveniently set out thither after he returned here. Yet, spring now approaching, I have found a way by which I hope it may nonetheless come about; for I have diligently commended him to the Most Serene Elector of Cologne, who favours me with a most clement inclination, and I persuade myself that he will be advanced through His Highness, so that he may conveniently traverse both Italy and France, as he desires. He has resolved, however, first to go to his people in Denmark, to acquire travelling-money there, and is arranging his affairs for that journey. Nay, I think, just as you do, that it is not to his advantage — to say nothing of mine — to consume his youthful years here any longer, which ought rather to be devoted to travels and studies; for the disciplines I practise are of no use to him, as he is also less capable of them.</p><p>My Tengnagel returned to me here about the winter solstice from Italy, where within a year he learned the language well, increased in manners and in favour; eight days ago he went to his own homeland to greet his parents there, afterwards (God willing) returning here, to be for some while in an honourable post at the Emperor's court.</p><p>As to the political honours indicated to me from the Imperial court while I was still at Wittenberg, you advise that I first come into possession of the fief — you advise rightly, indeed, for I too am of that opinion; but you do not quite understand what I meant to hint at, for it is not the thing of which I spoke with you before my departure in Denmark, but something further besides, which I had not thought of before, as you will perceive in due time, Your [Lordship].</p><p>With Typotius I have already sufficiently cleared you of that which you know; and when we meet, I shall do so yet further. He is most loving toward me, and wherever occasion is given, thinks and speaks honourably of me and my family. As for our studies, he asserts that they are to be judged by me myself, since he is not versed in them.</p><p>I do not doubt that you have heard, from learned men here and there, many things in connection with both the Polish and the English embassy, not unlike those which Michaelides related — though he was not ashamed to blab calumnies at Vordingborg before the Governor of the castle, yet not with impunity, as you perhaps gathered from my Tycho; and therefore I share with you the more readily the verses on account of which he was fabricating those tales, that you may discern that nothing is found in them uttered harshly or improperly against the King — of whom I am always accustomed to speak honourably and to excuse. It was told me by certain Danes, while I was at Wittenberg, that when they were once travelling through Bohemia, they were warned by a certain priest of the Pontifical doctrine not to profess themselves Danes, but rather to say they were sprung from some part of Germany — partly because the King had mockingly imitated the garb of the Roman Pontiff at [his] coronation, partly because they had thus lost me. What Iver Friis, in Hungary and Austria, in the presence of the King's brother (who, however — who he was — was not known), had to hear and to gloss on my account, he himself, since he is on familiar terms with you, will perhaps relate some day; while at Dresden, visiting me, he wished to suggest those things among others, I purposely turned the discourse elsewhere, unwilling for certain reasons to have such things reported to me, having too often heard the like to the point of nausea. Let those who are at fault see to it. May God grant and establish a union of hearts in our fatherland, and take away discords and the seeds of wars. What your envoys have accomplished in Sweden, I desire to learn from you, and likewise what else is going on with you and in all that Northern tract.</p><p>News that I might signify to you from here, I have little ready to hand. Of the recovery of Transylvania I think you have learned already. But they say that the Voivode, who was thought to have recovered it by valiant fighting on the Emperor's behalf, is resolving to reserve it for himself as a fief, even against the Emperor's own will; on which account his envoys were lately at Pilsen, who, having conducted themselves impatiently and obstinately, [the Emperor] sent someone to accuse them before the Voivode, the Emperor rebuking very severely a certain elderly chief among them. There is a rumour that the Turk is preparing a greater army than ever before against Hungary. May God Almighty in arms blunt his fury and his forces; our Emperor in turn fortifies himself to meet him with as many horse and foot as he can obtain. The Princes of the Empire, for the greater part, refuse aid, dreading the Spaniards rather than the Turks. May God, the author of concord and peace, protect us and free us from evils.</p><p>I grieve at the lot of the Swedish councillors, if Charles has raged grievously against them; he will have counselled himself less prudently — on which matter, too, as I indicated to you in my last, Typotius, with my consent, admonished him by letter, setting before him the example of his brother Eric.</p><p>The Muscovite is quietly contriving certain things with the Emperor and with Archduke Maximilian, which, if they succeed, the King of Poland would certainly have preferred not to have left his ancestral kingdom; which, in my opinion too, he will scarcely ever recover. It is no less a virtue to keep what is won than to seek it.</p><p>I add no more now, for I do not much linger over the public tumults to which this wavering earthly globe has long been accustomed; there is nothing new under the Sun, almost always the same play is acted, under one set of characters and another. If I seem to have written to you somewhat more fully in this letter than perhaps you would wish, know that it was done of set purpose, so that you too may write back copiously. So far am I from being displeased by the prolixity which you excuse in your last. Messengers are now more rarely granted; we shall therefore converse more fully by letter, since it is not given to do so by word of mouth. For as to your wish that I should run over to you now and then — easily and gladly as I should wish it, it cannot be done. I had indeed resolved to set out for this year's Diet, if any were held among you; but since I am uncertain of it, and the Emperor will not be willing to grant me leave to go to Denmark this first year, it must be deferred to another year, if God so see fit. But how it will be to my good, as you intimate, I long to understand more openly from you.</p><p>Fare you most well; your wife being most lovingly greeted by mine and by my daughters.<br>Given from the Imperial Castle of Benátky, the 24th day of March, by the renewed style, the year 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPT. Since my sister — that Urania, the little Earth-dweller [Terricula] — in her last letter insinuated some things about your countryman Mercurius (you know whom she marks), namely that he had spoken at Landskrona somewhat dishonourably of His Imperial Majesty, and afterwards had added some things too about the chief Councillors, all of which tended to this, that he might render her doubtful and diffident about the success of my affairs — as though I were to find no one here who would advance me and mine, and as though those from whom I most hoped for some help were in ill favour with the Emperor and of no authority: although she, perceiving well enough whither these things tended, dissolved, as far as she then could, his less candid utterances, and threw back what came up (clever little thing that she is), and although it matters little what he or his like judge — for we know with what spirit they proceed: nevertheless, that you may, as occasion offers, instruct that Urania and further confirm her that the matter (thanks be to God) stands far otherwise, I send you a copy of the letter which my Tengnagel lately, while he was at the Court at Pilsen, wrote to me, before he departed thence to his homeland, that you may understand from it of what kind is the zeal and favour toward me, not only of the Magnificent Lord Barvitius — who is at the Emperor's side almost every hour, being his chief Secretary — but also of the Illustrious and most Ample Lord Corraduz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, who deservedly holds the foremost authority at Court. I have added also a letter of Lord Doctor Jessenius — who was my host at Wittenberg in the Philippic house, and who about the same time was conducting a certain case at Pilsen — to almost the same effect, but written a little more fully about Lord Corraduz, and it is in Jessenius's own hand. You will be able to set out the contents of both Letters to my sister, when you meet, that she may have material with which to pin down and beat back that unstable and shifty Mercurius, if perhaps he should at any time suggest the like again, or blab it among others in his sharp-tongued way. Nonetheless I too have sent, in my letter to the same Urania, certain things in German, written to me a little before from Pilsen by a member of my household, which make mention, among other things, of the singular goodwill and candid affection of Lord Corraduz toward me; about which she in turn will be able to report to you. There are also other Illustrious men of exceptional dignity at the Imperial Court who wish me well in my affairs — to say nothing of His Sacred Imperial Majesty himself, who is constant in his purpose, nor heeds the sinister judgments of others (if any there be) so lightly as may be the case elsewhere. I shall add nothing now. I give thanks to God most good and great for this my lot, and pray that He may deign in future to attend me with His protection and blessing. Again farewell. Given as above in the letter.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23.</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 24 Martij stylo renovato Anni 1600</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b> (<i>stylo renovato</i> = Gregorian), matching the catalogue label <i>\"1600. 24. marts n.s.\"</i> Written from <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (<i>Arx Benatica</i>), the imperial castle north-east of Prague where Rudolf II installed Tycho on his arrival in Bohemia. Note the calendar self-consciousness: Tycho, a Dane normally writing Old Style, here dates by the Gregorian style of his new Imperial-Catholic context — the very 10-day gap discussed elsewhere in the correspondence (cf. brahe-t_16000126002).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Niels Krag (<i>Nicolaus Cragius</i>, c. 1550–1602)</b> — Danish humanist, royal historiographer and diplomat, professor at Copenhagen, then a royal councillor; one of Tycho's most trusted Danish correspondents. His wife is named <i>\"Elisa.\"</i></li><li><b>Persons &amp; powers named.</b></li><li><b>His Sacred Imperial Majesty / the Emperor / Caesar</b> — <b>Rudolf II</b>, Holy Roman Emperor, Tycho's new patron.</li><li><b>filius Tycho</b> — Tycho Brahe the Younger, the elder son, just back from Denmark.</li><li><b>Rosenkrantz (<i>Roseakrantzius</i>) / Holger (<i>Oliger</i>)</b> — Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm (cf. brahe-t_15980810001), to whom Tycho writes in parallel and who is to share the documents.</li><li><b>Lord Barvitius, chief Secretary</b> — <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b>, Rudolf II's leading secretary, Tycho's conduit to the Emperor.</li><li><b>Joseph Scaliger</b> — Josephus Justus Scaliger, the foremost philologist of the age, then at Leiden; he corresponded with Tycho (cf. the Scaliger precession exchange, brahe-t_15980709001 etc.).</li><li><b>Melissus</b> — <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b> (1539–1602), German neo-Latin poet, \"standard-bearer of the poets of our age.\"</li><li><b>\"that little Scot\" (<i>Scotulus</i>)</b> — <b>Duncan Liddel</b> (1561–1613), the Scottish mathematician at Helmstedt accused of claiming Tycho's geo-heliocentric world-system as his own. This letter is a primary document of the plagiarism dispute: Tycho states Liddel \"arrogated my invention\" in <i>a printed notice affixed to the doors of the College at Helmstedt,</i> of which Tycho holds a copy — and that <b>Krag himself was present when Tycho first disclosed the Hypothesis to Liddel,</b> so Krag could testify. Directly continuous with the Duncan-Liddel dossier (brahe-t_15990926001) and Krag's testimony-origin letter (brahe-t_15991204001); Tycho here gently presses the reluctant Krag (who would rather \"dissemble the truth and bind it in chains\" out of regard for Liddel).</li><li><b>Mule (<i>Mulaeus</i>)</b> — Krag's young associate, whom Tycho commends to the Elector of Cologne for a study-journey to Italy and France (cf. the Mule/Boekel business in brahe-t_15980810001).</li><li><b>Elector of Cologne</b> — <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>, Archbishop-Elector, Tycho's well-wisher.</li><li><b>Tengnagel (<i>Tengnaglius</i>)</b> — Frans Gansneb Tengnagel, Tycho's assistant (later son-in-law), back from a year in Italy, bound for an honourable court post.</li><li><b>Typotius</b> — <b>Jacob Typotius</b> (1540–1601), Flemish humanist and imperial historiographer at Prague, Tycho's friend and advocate.</li><li><b>Michaelides</b> — a Dane (patronymic \"son of Michael,\" not securely identified) who spread calumnies against Tycho before the Governor of <b>Vordingborg</b> castle (<i>Wordingburgum</i>).</li><li><b>Iver Friis (<i>Ivarus Friis</i>)</b> — Danish noble; the King's brother travelling incognito through Hungary/Austria is most plausibly <b>Duke Ulrik of Denmark</b> (Christian IV's brother).</li><li><b>the King</b> — <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>, of whom Tycho insists he always speaks honourably; the report that Christian IV \"mockingly imitated the garb of the Roman Pontiff at his coronation\" refers to anti-papal pageantry associated with the 1596 coronation.</li><li><b>the Voivode (<i>Baiuoda</i>)</b> — <b>Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul) of Wallachia</b>, who had seized <b>Transylvania</b> (autumn 1599) nominally for the Emperor but sought to hold it as his own fief; his envoys at Pilsen.</li><li><b>the Turk</b> — Sultan <b>Mehmed III</b>, the Ottoman threat to Hungary (the Long Turkish War).</li><li><b>Charles (<i>Carolus</i>) / his brother Eric</b> — <b>Duke Charles of Södermanland</b> (the future <b>Karl IX</b> of Sweden), regent, \"raging against the Swedish councillors\" (the aftermath of his victory over Sigismund — the executions would culminate in the 1600 Linköping bloodbath); <b>Eric</b> = <b>Erik XIV</b>, Charles's deposed and imprisoned brother, cited as a cautionary example.</li><li><b>the Muscovite (<i>Moscus</i>)</b> — the Tsar of Muscovy (<b>Boris Godunov</b>), intriguing with the Emperor and <b>Archduke Maximilian III</b> against the <b>King of Poland</b> = <b>Sigismund III Vasa</b>, who had \"left his ancestral kingdom\" (Sweden) and would \"scarcely ever recover\" it.</li><li><b>Places.</b> Prague; <b>Pilsen</b> (<i>Pilsena</i>, Plzeň), the fortified town ~10 (German) miles from Prague where Rudolf II sheltered from the plague; <b>Benátky</b> (<i>Benatica</i>); the second imperial castle ~3 miles off to which Tycho withdrew — <b>Brandýs nad Labem</b> (its <i>Capitaneus Brandeisensis</i>, \"Captain of Brandeis,\" is named as Tycho's neighbour and informant); <b>Vienna</b> (offered as a plague-refuge); <b>Hamburg</b> and <b>Magdeburg</b> (custodians of Tycho's shipped goods); the <b>Elbe</b> (<i>Albis</i>) as the transport route; <b>Wittenberg</b> (<i>Witteberga</i>, Tycho's station before Bohemia); <b>Dresden</b>; <b>Helmstedt</b> (Liddel's university).</li><li><b>The instrument-transport.</b> A valuable logistical record: Tycho's instruments and household goods, left in Denmark and then deposited at Hamburg and Magdeburg, are to be shipped up the Elbe to Bohemia <b>at imperial expense</b>, by Rudolf II's letters. <b>Magdeburg's evasion</b> — pleading impoverishment from \"the siege fifty years ago\" (the 1550–51 siege by Maurice of Saxony) — is mocked by the imperial councillors; the Emperor redirects the request to the <b>cathedral chapter</b>, which complies out of rivalry with the city Senate. Tycho is simultaneously completing the purpose-built <b>instrument buildings</b> at Benátky (begun before winter, halted by cold).</li><li><b>The marble epigrams (a striking detail).</b> Rudolf II ordered <b>two of Tycho's epigrams carved in marble above the two main portals</b> of the castle (one of Doric, one of Ionic order). Where Tycho, out of patriotic delicacy, had written <i>\"which I, the North, refuse\"</i> (using <i>Arctoi</i>, \"the Northern lands,\" in place of <i>Dani</i>, \"the Danes\") <b>so as not to name his fatherland,</b> the Emperor insisted on the explicit <i>\"Dania quae renuit\"</i> (\"Denmark, which refused [him]\") and on <i>\"the Danes\"</i> for <i>\"the Northerners\"</i> in the second — a deliberate imperial sharpening of the reproach against Denmark, which Tycho marks as a <i>secret for confidential friends only.</i> (The self-monument theme links to the Baldi/Magini self-monument thread, brahe-t_16000304001 / brahe-t_16010124001.)</li><li><b>Greek interpolation.</b> <i>consiliarij vero eius in κακιννος resoluti sunt</i> — \"his councillors broke into [derisive] laughter.\" The Greek word as transcribed (<i>κακιννος</i>) is <b>corrupt</b> (most likely a garbling of <i>καγχασμόν</i> / <i>κιχλισμόν</i>, \"cackling/giggling laughter,\" vel sim.); reproduced as printed per protocol §4.2, with the sense (\"burst out laughing / into mockery\") secure from the Latin frame.</li><li><b>Latin tags &amp; allusions.</b> <i>\"Stans tegor in solido\"</i> — \"I stand sheltered on solid ground\" (a Stoic emblematic motto of constancy; Tycho's <i>etc.</i> shows he quotes a known tag). <i>\"nihil novi sub Sole\"</i> — Ecclesiastes 1:9. <i>\"Non minor est virtus, quam quaerere, parta tueri\"</i> — <b>Ovid</b>, <i>Ars Amatoria</i> 2.13. <i>\"virtutis … umbra [invidia]\"</i> — the commonplace that envy is the shadow of virtue.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The POSTSCRIPTA (the \"Mercurius\"-gossip parallel: Urania = sister Sophie; the unnamed Landskrona slanderer; enclosing the Tengnagel + Jessenius letters from Pilsen as counter-evidence), restored from Dreyer VIII, pp. 277–278. The slanderer's name is a contracted/symbol form, OCR-garbled to \"[...]rio/[...]rium\"; left as [...], not invented.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Three defects restored against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII: (1) p.274, the dropped clause ‘et in altero suo’, which shows the two epigrams originally carried different wording (‘Quae Boreas renuo’ in the first, ‘Arctois pro Danis’ in the second) before the Emperor's edit — English corrected to match; (2) pp.277–278, a private cipher — a planetary glyph standing for the name ‘Mercurius’, the same figure named openly in Tycho's companion letter to Sophie Brahe of 21 March 1600 over the same Landskrona incident — restored in place of an editorial ellipsis, at both occurrences; (3) p.278, ‘effutierit’ (blab/blurt out) restored for the corrupted ‘effuderit’ (pour out), matching the verb's earlier use in this same letter.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — sound. A long, content-rich letter rendered securely throughout; the substance (the Bohemian plague and Rudolf II's fatherly concern + the declined Vienna refuge; the antidotes; the instrument-shipment via the Elbe and the Magdeburg refusal; the marble epigrams and the Emperor's insistence on naming Denmark; the Scaliger/Melissus encomia; the Duncan-Liddel plagiarism evidence with Krag as eyewitness; and the imperial/Baltic news sweep) is clear and cross-corroborated within the correspondence. Residual minor uncertainties, none obscuring sense: the <b>corrupt Greek word</b> <i>κακιννος</i> (sense secure, exact form not); the precise identity of <b>Michaelides</b> and of the <b>King's brother</b> travelling incognito (most plausibly Duke Ulrik), left as the text gives them; and the source of the <i>\"Stans tegor in solido\"</i> tag. The editorial insertion <i>[D.] V.</i> (<i>Dominatio Vestra</i>, \"Your Lordship\") is the edition's.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full 9-page Dreyer slice (TBOO VIII pp.271-279) as page images in two chunks (1-4, 5-8) plus p.9, confirming the letter itself runs pp.272-278 and p.279 belongs to the next letter (to Ioestelius). Collated the opening and closing lines, the dateline (24 March 1600, Benátky), and every proper name (Rosenkrantz, Elisa, Barvitius, Coraducius/Corraduz, Jessenius, Scaliger, Melissus, Oligerus/Holger, Michaelides, Iver Friis, Charles/Erik, Landskrona, Mulaeus, Tengnagel, the Helmstedt 'Scotulus') and number (10 milliaribus, 3 milliaribus, 50 Annos) against the page images. Found one high-severity TEXT_ERROR (a dropped clause in the epigram passage that collapses a two-epigram distinction into one), one medium-severity TEXT_ERROR (the postscript's private cipher glyph rendered as unexplained ellipsis rather than the legible sign Dreyer prints), and one low-confidence TEXT_ERROR (a probable verb substitution near the letter's end). No fabrication, sentence-level omission, or mistranslation was found beyond what these text-level discrepancies induce downstream.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Our source_text silently drops the clause 'et in altero suo' ('and in the other, in its own place'), which in Dreyer marks that Tycho's own original wording differed between the TWO epigrams (Quae Boreas renuo in the first, Arctois pro Danis in the second). Without it the sentence reads as a single compressed substitution, and the English follows the defective source, describing only one epigram's original wording instead of two before the Emperor's edit. Dreyer prints: <i>Cumque ego in primo, ut ab initio legeretur Quae Boreas renuo, ET IN ALTERO SUO loco Arctois pro Danis, quo Patria mea non nominaretur, Caesar iussit... (TBOO VIII p.274, lines ~29-33)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Tycho used a planetary glyph as a private cipher for an unnamed person's identity. Dreyer's typeset edition reproduces that glyph legibly and consistently twice; our source_text (and the translation that follows it) instead substitutes an unexplained ellipsis, dropping a real, transcribable character rather than describing it. Dreyer prints: <i>prints a specific planetary/astrological glyph (reads as the sign for Venus, ♀) directly before 'rio'/'rium', consistently in both occurrences, TBOO VIII pp.277 and 278 — not a gap or illegible mark</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). If Dreyer indeed prints 'effutierit', the sense is 'blab/blurt out' rather than 'pour out'; the English 'pour it out' matches our (possibly corrupted) source_text rather than Dreyer. Flagged at low confidence given the visual similarity of the two verb forms in this typeface. Dreyer prints: <i>reads, with moderate confidence, 'effutierit' (to blab/blurt out) rather than 'effuderit' (to pour out) — TBOO VIII p.278; the same verb stem 'effutire' appears earlier in this letter ('calumnias effutire')</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.271-279 in full (page images, read visually across three passes of at most 4 pages, never the long-s OCR text layer). Defect 1 (the dropped 'et in altero suo') is unambiguous on p.274; both Latin and English were corrected. Defect 2 (the cipher glyph) is confirmed as a real, non-ellipsis character at both pp.277 and 278; its identification as the Mercury rather than Venus symbol rests on two independent pieces of internal evidence — the -rio/-rium oblique endings fit only Mercurius's declension, and the same cipher figure is named openly as 'Mercurius' in the companion letter to Sophie Brahe three days earlier, describing the identical Landskrona incident and the identical charge of inconstancy — rather than on a fully certain visual reading of the glyph's exact shape at this image resolution, which remains the one residual crux. Defect 3 ('effutierit' for 'effuderit') is clearly legible on p.278 and consistent with the verb's other use in the same letter. All three defects resolved.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Niels Krag", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_15991204001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991204001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges the recipient's reproach over long silence in correspondence, defends his familiar style of address toward friends, and thanks him for hospitality shown to his son.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000324002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000327001", "d": "1600-03-27", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Melchior Jöstel — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 27 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MELCHIOREM IOeSTELIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>ERUDITISSIME et Amantissime Domine Magister IOeSTELI, accepi quidem literas tuas cum filio paulo post huius Anni initia huc reverso; verum nihil ad eas hactenus respondi, partim ob penuriam tabellariorum, partim quod sperassem te desiderijs nostris, prout ibidem pollicitus es, brevi satisfacturum: at cum nuper Pragam (uti audio) venerit Dominus Doctor IESSENIUS, communis noster Amicus, et tuas huc miserit, percipio ex ijs te necdum in dispositione circuituum ♀ris et ☿ij calculoque huic congruente satis esse resolutum, utut non contemnendam in ijs locaveris operam. Ne igitur res nimis diu protrahatur, cum dispendio Libri 2di Epistolarum, in quo nonnulla ex ijs conveniente loco, uti scis, inserere animus est, te peramanter rogatum velim, ut saltem ea, quae in tribus superioribus Planetis explorata habes, iuxta triplices illas Hypotheses mox consignes et dilucide explicata mihi mature mittas. Si unâ adiunxeris quaecunque in ♀ et ☿ varie tentâsti, et quousque in ijs progressus sis, qualesque inveneris remoras, et quid dubij potissimum in ijs sit, rescribam eo promptius, quid faciundum restet, et quomodo illis difficultatibus obviandum; neque operae pretium censeo, ut ex meis observatis dimensiones circulorum ♀ris et ☿ij motuumque rationes perquiras; id enim non mediocris est negotij, a meipso D. V. elaborandum; sed solummodo ut numeris Coperniceis, quamvis errori obnoxijs, quamproxime conveniant, tum quoque Alphonsinis, secundum diversimodas tamen assumptiones, aplicentur. Omnem itaque move lapidem, mi M. IOeSTELI, ut, quamprimum fieri poterit, id, quicquid erit, obtineam, nec expectationem nostram diutius remoreris quaeso. Miror M. OeSTWALDUM tam diu in unius Anni Ephemeride Luminarium supputandâ haerere; quorsum nobis usui erit, ubi magna pars huius Anni praeterierit? Si paratam habet, mittat actutum; mercedem addictam illi solvas, quam per affinem tuum Dresdensem recipies. Concederem illi plura pro me calculanda, non absque suo commodo, modo non ita tardus esset, et certo ad praestitutum tempus rem absolvere vellet. Si uxoratus non esset, vocarem illum huc, ut apud me calculo vacaret, constituto illi annuo salario. Cumque nullos habeat liberos, posset, si ei sic luberet, aeque commode Pragae atque Wittebergae degere, et ibi etiam ex privatis praelectionibus victum et amyctum acquirere, ubi meo quoque subsidio non destituetur, et efficiam, ut isthic in veteri collegio commodum habeat locum. Quae illi ex me proponere poteris, atque ipsius expiscari sententiam, mihi per primam occasionem indicandam. Ubi intellexero hanc conditionem non displicere, providebo ei de viatico necessario. Tu vero, quantum in te est, ipsum adhorteris, ut obsecundet.</p><p>Literas adiunctas Domino Doctori WOLFGANGO FRANTZIO trade; et Poëtam TAUBMANNUM diligenter ex me saluta. Scripsit is semel atque iterum mihi, se tale carmen in mei gratiam denuo concinnaturum, ut experiar eum non esse raucâ voce praepeditum. At, quantum video, non saltem rauxedine laborat, sed et plane mutus est; nisi forte hisce temporibus occupatur in conscribendo carmine de Sanctis Angelis, qui alias omnis sorditiei et avaritiae expertes sunt, quo eos Altari adstantes et donaria pecuniarum numerantes atque consignantes, vel invitos et talia fastidientes introducat, sicque hyperbolice poëtiset et quibusdam placentia dicat.</p><p>Quae in ultima Eclipsi Solis observasti, ex te scire cupio; brevi percipies alias rationes in motu ☾ae et Luminarium diametris, quibus omnia accuratius quam antea apparentijs consentiant.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur properanter ex Arce CaesarisBenatica Die 27 Martij Anni 1600 stylo novo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MELCHIOR JÖSTEL.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most learned and most loving Lord Master Jöstel, I did indeed receive your letter, with my son returned hither a little after the beginning of this year; but I have hitherto replied nothing to it, partly for the scarcity of couriers, partly because I had hoped you would soon satisfy my wishes, as you promised therein. But since lately (as I hear) Lord Doctor <b>Jessenius</b>, our common friend, has come to Prague and sent your [letters] hither, I perceive from them that you are <b>not yet sufficiently resolved on the disposition of the orbits of Venus and Mercury, and the calculation congruent with it</b> — although you have placed no contemptible labour on them. Lest, therefore, the matter be drawn out too long, to the detriment of the <b>second Book of the Letters</b> (into which it is my mind to insert some of these things in a fitting place, as you know), I should lovingly ask you that you would at least soon set down what you have explored <b>in the three superior Planets, according to those triple Hypotheses</b>, and send it me in good time, clearly explained. If you adjoin therewith whatever you have variously attempted in <b>Venus and Mercury</b> — how far you have progressed, what hindrances you have met, and what doubt especially is in them — I will write back the more promptly what remains to be done, and how those difficulties are to be met. Nor do I think it worth your labour to investigate, from my observations, the dimensions of the orbits of Venus and Mercury and the ratios of their motions — for that is no small task, to be worked out by myself, God willing — but only [your task is] that they be made to agree <b>as closely as possible with the Copernican numbers</b> (though liable to error), and likewise be applied <b>to the Alphonsine</b> [numbers], according, however, to various assumptions. <b>Move every stone, therefore</b>, my Master Jöstel, that I may obtain it — whatever it be — as soon as can be, and do not, I pray, delay our expectation any longer. I wonder that <b>Master Oswald</b> sticks so long at computing the Ephemeris of the Luminaries for a single year; to what use will it be to us, when a great part of this year will have passed? If he has it ready, let him send it at once; pay him the assigned fee, which you will receive through your Dresden kinsman. I would grant him more to calculate for me — not without his own profit — if only he were not so slow, and would surely finish the work at the set time. <b>Were he not married, I would call him hither, to be free for calculation with me, an annual salary being settled on him</b>; and since he has no children, he could, if it so pleased him, live as conveniently at Prague as at Wittenberg, and there too earn food and clothing from private lectures, where he will also not lack my support, and I will see that he have a convenient place in the old college there. These things you can propose to him from me, and fish out his opinion, to be told me at the first opportunity. When I understand that this offer does not displease, I will provide him the necessary travel-money. But do you, as far as in you lies, urge him to comply.</p><p>Hand the enclosed letter to Lord Doctor <b>Wolfgang Franz</b>; and diligently greet the Poet <b>Taubmann</b> on my behalf. He wrote to me once and again that he would compose another such poem in my honour, that I might find he is not hindered by a hoarse voice. But, as far as I see, he not only suffers from hoarseness, but is plainly <b>mute</b> — unless perhaps he is at these times occupied in writing a poem on the Holy Angels (who are otherwise free of all sordidness and avarice), in which he might bring them in, standing at the Altar and counting and recording the money-offerings, even unwilling and disdaining such things, and so poetize hyperbolically and say things pleasing to certain people.</p><p>What you observed in the last Solar Eclipse, I desire to know from you; soon you will perceive other methods in the motion of the Moon and the diameters of the Luminaries, by which everything agrees with the appearances more accurately than before.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given in haste from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 27th day of March, the year 1600, New Style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (1559–1611), Wittenberg professor of mathematics and medicine (cf. brahe-t_16001008001, brahe-t_16001009001), here employed as Tycho's <b>computational collaborator</b> on the planetary theory. Same date as the Wolfgang-Franz letter (brahe-t_16000327002): both belong to Tycho's late-March 1600 correspondence with the Wittenberg circle, routed through <b>Jessenius</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — the planetary theory for the second <i>Epistolae</i> book (technical content; protocol §5.1).</b> Tycho presses Jöstel for results he means to insert into the <b>second Book of the <i>Astronomical Letters</b></i>:</li><li><b>The three superior planets</b> (Saturn, Jupiter, Mars) \"according to those <b>triple Hypotheses</b>\" — to be set down and clearly explained;</li><li><b>Venus and Mercury</b>: Jöstel is \"not yet resolved on the disposition of [their] orbits and the congruent calculation\"; Tycho wants his attempts, progress, hindrances and doubts.</li><li><b>Division of labour:</b> Jöstel is <b>not</b> to derive the orbital dimensions and motion-ratios from Tycho's observations (\"no small task, to be worked out by myself\"), but only to make the parameters <b>agree as closely as possible with the Copernican numbers</b> (\"though liable to error\") and <b>apply them to the Alphonsine</b> tables \"according to various assumptions.\" A clear window onto how Tycho farmed out planetary computation while reserving the observational reduction to himself. <i>Omnem move lapidem</i> — \"leave no stone unturned.\"</li><li><b>Master Oswald — the ephemeris-computer (resolves a prior open item).</b> <i>M. Oeswaldus</i> = <b>Master Oswald</b>, the Wittenberg <b>calculator</b> (the <i>M. Oswaldus</i> flagged in brahe-t_16001008001): Tycho chafes that he \"sticks so long at computing the Ephemeris of the <b>Luminaries</b> [Sun and Moon] for a single year\" (useless if late), to be paid via Jöstel's <b>Dresden kinsman</b>. Notably, Tycho <b>offers to bring Oswald to Prague on an annual salary</b> to compute for him — \"were he not married … since he has no children … as conveniently at Prague as at Wittenberg,\" with private lectures, Tycho's support, and \"a place in the old college.\" A vivid glimpse of Tycho recruiting computational staff for the imperial establishment.</li><li><b>Wolfgang Franz &amp; the Taubmann satire.</b> Tycho encloses a letter for <b>Wolfgang Franz</b> (<i>Doctor</i>; cf. brahe-t_16000327002, brahe-t_16001008001 — faculty still unspecified here, the theologian-vs-jurist question still open) and greets the poet <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b>, who had promised fresh verses \"in my honour.\" Tycho's barbed joke: Taubmann is not merely \"hoarse\" but <b>\"plainly mute\"</b> (i.e. has sent nothing) — <i>unless</i> he is busy with \"a poem on the <b>Holy Angels</b> … counting and recording the money-offerings at the Altar … and so poetizing hyperbolically and saying things pleasing to certain people\" — a sardonic dig at venal flattery-verse.</li><li><b>Observational items.</b> Tycho asks for Jöstel's data on <b>the last Solar Eclipse</b>, and previews \"other methods in the motion of the <b>Moon</b> and the <b>diameters of the Luminaries</b>\" giving better agreement with the appearances — the lunar-theory refinement that recurs across the corpus (cf. brahe-t_15981231001, brahe-t_16000420002).</li><li><b>Glyphs (kept; §5.1).</b> ♀ = <b>Venus</b>, ☿ = <b>Mercury</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b> (names in English; glyphs retained in the source block).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Melchior Jöstel; <b>Jessenius</b>; <b>Master Oswald</b>; <b>Wolfgang Franz</b>; <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger); Copernicus and the Alphonsine tables (as computational standards).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 27 Martij Anni 1600 stylo novo</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent technical Latin, read securely; the substance (the division of planetary-computation labour between Jöstel and Tycho for the second <i>Epistolae</i> book — the three superior planets' triple hypotheses, Venus/Mercury, fitting Copernican and Alphonsine numbers; the slow ephemeris-computer Oswald and the offer to bring him to Prague; the enclosed Franz letter and the Taubmann satire; the solar-eclipse and lunar-theory items) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated, with the §5.1 glyphs handled. No textual crux; the <i>Master Oswald</i> identity is resolved (Wittenberg ephemeris-computer) and the <i>Wolfgang Franz</i> faculty question remains open and flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.277-281 (slice pages 1-4; the letter's end was located on p.280 so slice page 5/p.281 was not needed); p.277 holds the end of the preceding letter (Tycho to Cragius) as buffer, the Joestelius letter runs from mid-p.278 through p.280. Collated every proper name (Jessenius, Wolfgang Franz, Taubmann, Oswald/Oestwaldum), the Venus/Mercury and superior-planet passages, and the closing date (27 March 1600, new style) against the printed text; all match source_text and published_english exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Melchior Jöstelius", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000918001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks the recipient to send at least his completed work on the three superior planets' orbits under the triple hypotheses, for inclusion in the second Book of Letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000327001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000327002", "d": "1600-03-27", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Wolfgang Franzius", "ck": "Wolfgang Franzius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → Wolfgang Franz (Franzius) — Benátky [nad Jizerou], [27] March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD WOLFGANGUM FRANZIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et praestantissime Domine Doctor WOLFGANG FRANCI, Amice plurimi semper mihi faciende. Ex utrisque tuis literis, tam prioribus quam nuper per Dominum Doctorem IESSENIUM, communem nostrum Amicum, missis, intellexi de Transylvani illius negocio; quem instabilem esse et, dum hîc aderat, serio non egisse, iam praesenseram. Is forte meas ob id expetebat literas, ut eo facilius isthic ad illud, quod ambibat, officium promoveretur; sed bene est, quocunque tandem modo illi profuerim; apud me vero perinde ubicunque sit, utut effecerit, quod interea alium eius loco non obtinuerim, veluti alias non fuisset difficile.</p><p>Morbi Epidemici, qui graviter superiori Anno hanc Regionem infestârunt, iam, Dei benignitate, sopiti sunt. Caesar tamen necdum Pragam reversus est, sed Pilsnae adhuc moratur, brevi tamen, uti existimant, eo venturus. Scabies, de quâ loqueris, eorum, qui Theologiam et religionem Christianam profitentur, saevior est, et diutius durat; nam apud homines non est miseratio et relaxatio, quemadmodum apud Deum. Attamen quae nonnulli moliuntur, ut suis indulgeant cupiditatibus, inscio Caesare, pro maiori parte, fiunt, et spes est, successum non habitura, reluctantibus praecipuis quibusdam in hoc regno et libertatem avitam asserentibus; neque Caesar tam improvidus erit, ut regnum, tam periculoso praesertim hoc tempore, irrequietari permittat.</p><p>Res meae ita se habent, ut antea intellexisti: Deus inposterum eas conservet. Dominus Doctor IESSENIUS, quem Planetam vocas, a vestrâ Ecliptica (iuxta quam Luminaria deliquium pati solent) huc quidem per Australem latitudinem in Bohemiam digressus, me tamen nondum invisit, nec scio an facturus sit; habet enim, instar Planetae ‿ij quasdam deviationes et reflectiones, aliter quam ponderosiores Planetae incedunt. Intellexi Patris eius caussam periclitari, quod satis mature is non accesserit, nec forte ob hyemem commodum erat. Relatum mihi est, te etiam erraticum futurum et alio concessurum; si ita habet, indica; nam et ego sum unus ex ijs, qui tibi quamoptime cupiunt: optarem te semel hîc videre, prout aliquando futurum dixisti; sed multa cadunt inter ciatos supremaque labra. Vale et me tui esse amantem, ubicunque fueris, persuasum habe. Dabantur properanti calamo ex Arce Benatica sextiduo post Aequinoctium vernum Anni 1600, quem non saltem IOSEPHUS SCALIGER et MERCATOR in sua Cronologia, tum quoque BUTINGERUS atque SCULTETUS uno Anno, sed idem SCALIGER in novo opere de temporum emendatione duobus deficere probare conatur, ut sit nunc reverâ 1602. Rationes eius in dicto libro invenies. Iamque iterum et multoties vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO WOLFGANG FRANZ.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and most excellent Lord Doctor Wolfgang Franz, a friend always to be most highly esteemed by me. From both your letters — the earlier ones, and those lately sent through Lord Doctor Jessenius, our common friend — I have learned of that Transylvanian's affair; whom I had already sensed to be unsteady, and, while he was here, not to be acting in earnest. He perhaps sought my letters on that account, so that he might the more easily be advanced there to the post he was angling for; but it is well, in whatever way I have at last been of use to him. To me, indeed, it is all one wherever he may be, however he has brought it off, since in the meantime I have got no one in his place — as otherwise it would not have been difficult [to do].</p><p>The epidemic diseases, which last year grievously infested this region, are now, by God's goodness, laid to rest. The Emperor, however, has still not returned to Prague, but lingers yet at Pilsen — though soon, as they reckon, he will come thither. The mange of which you speak — that of those who profess Theology and the Christian religion — is fiercer, and lasts longer; for among men there is no mercy and remission, such as there is with God. Yet the things which some are contriving, to indulge their own cupidities, are for the most part done without the Emperor's knowledge, and there is hope they will not succeed, certain leading men in this kingdom resisting and asserting their ancestral liberty; nor will the Emperor be so improvident as to allow the kingdom — especially at so dangerous a time as this — to be thrown into unrest.</p><p>My affairs stand as you understood before: may God preserve them hereafter. Lord Doctor Jessenius, whom you call a Planet, having indeed departed from your Ecliptic (along which the Luminaries are wont to suffer eclipse) hither into Bohemia by a southern latitude, has nevertheless not yet visited me, nor do I know whether he will; for he has, like the planet Mercury [☿], certain deviations and retrogradations, otherwise than the more ponderous Planets proceed. I have learned that his Father's case is in jeopardy, because he did not come to it early enough — nor perhaps, on account of the winter, was it convenient. It has been reported to me that you too are to be a wanderer, and to withdraw elsewhere; if so it is, let me know; for I too am one of those who wish you the very best. I could wish to see you here once, as you said would some day happen — but many things fall between the cup and the lips. Farewell, and hold it for certain that I love you, wherever you may be. Given with hurrying pen from the castle of Benátky, the sixth day after the vernal equinox of the year 1600 — a [year] which not only Joseph Scaliger, and Mercator in his <i>Chronology</i>, and also Bünting and Scultetus, attempt to prove deficient by one year, but the same Scaliger, in his new work <i>On the Emendation of Times</i>, by two — so that it is now really 1602. You will find his reasons in the said book. And now, again and many times over, farewell.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Correspondent (identity not settled — flagged).</b> The recipient is <i>Domine Doctor <b>Wolfgang</b> Franci</i> (<b>Wolfgang</b> Franz, <i>Franzius</i>) — Tycho's learned correspondent of the Wittenberg–Prague circle, the exchange routed through their common friend <b>Jan Jessenský / Johannes Jessenius</b> (1566–1621), the anatomist. <b>Disambiguation:</b> the recurring <i>Doctor Franz</i> who serves as Tycho's <b>courier</b> to Saxony (brahe-t_16001008001) and becomes <b>Chancellor of the Count of Emden</b> is the <b>jurist <i>Thomas</i> Franz, Winshemius's son-in-law and East-Frisia agent</b> (named <i>Thomas Frantzius</i> in brahe-t_15990507001; <i>Frantzius Iurisconsultus</i> in brahe-t_16001008001; <i>Doctor Franz the jurisconsult, Chancellor of the Count of Emden</i> in brahe-t_16000918001). Since <b>this recipient's first name is Wolfgang, not Thomas, he is a different person</b> — most plausibly the <b>Wittenberg Lutheran theologian Wolfgang Franz</b> (1564–1628), though that cannot be confirmed from this letter alone. Identity left <b>open</b> (theologian likely; <i>not</i> the jurist Thomas Franz).</li><li><b>The \"Transylvanian.\"</b> An unnamed, unsteady client (<i>instabilis</i>) who had sought a letter of recommendation (<i>meas literas</i>) from Tycho to obtain a post; Tycho washes his hands of him. Not further identified here; left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule.</li><li><b>Topical setting (spring 1600, Bohemia).</b></li><li><i>Morbi Epidemici … superiori Anno</i> — the <b>plague of 1599</b> that drove the imperial court (and Tycho) about; now abating.</li><li><i>Caesar … Pilsnae adhuc moratur</i> — <b>Rudolf II</b>, who had withdrawn to <b>Pilsen (Plzeň)</b> during the epidemic and had not yet returned to Prague.</li><li><i>Scabies … eorum qui Theologiam … profitentur</i> — a sardonic figure: the \"mange/itch\" of the theologians (confessional quarrelling) is worse and more chronic than any bodily epidemic. The veiled reference to men \"indulging their cupidities … without the Emperor's knowledge,\" resisted by Bohemian nobles \"asserting their ancestral liberty,\" reflects the <b>Catholic–Protestant tension in Bohemia</b> on the eve of the confessional crisis; Tycho judges Rudolf too prudent to let the kingdom be destabilised.</li><li><b>The astronomical conceit (the letter's wit).</b> Tycho extends Franz's own joke of calling Jessenius \"a <b>Planet</b>.\" Jessenius has <i>left \"your Ecliptic\"</i> (Wittenberg — the line \"along which the Luminaries suffer eclipse,\" i.e. the locus of darkenings/troubles) and crossed <i>\"by a southern latitude\"</i> into Bohemia; like a wandering planet he keeps <i>deviationes et reflectiones</i> — <b>deviations and retrogradations</b> — unlike the <i>ponderosiores Planetae</i>, the heavier (slower, steadier) planets. Tycho then turns the same figure on Franz himself (\"you too are to be <b>erraticus</b> [a wanderer/planet]\"). The technical sense of <i>reflectio</i> here is <b>retrograde motion</b> (apparent backward looping), not \"reflection of light.\"</li><li><b>The garbled planet-glyph (textual crux).</b> At <i>instar Planetae</i> the working transcription prints the malformed string <b>\"‿ij\"</b> where Dreyer's edition sets a <b>planet sign</b>. The sense fixes it securely as <b>Mercury (☿)</b> — the lightest, swiftest, most inconstant planet, the one that keeps the largest <i>deviationes/reflectiones</i> and is the stock emblem of mutability — contrasted with the <i>ponderosiores Planetae</i> (Saturn/Jupiter). Translated as \"the planet Mercury\"; the as-printed source glyph is retained in the source block. (Cf. the recurring transcription noise around planet/zodiac signs and the `ÿ`/`ij` ligature in this corpus.)</li><li><b>The chronology joke (dating, protocol §3).</b> The dateline is <b>periphrastic</b>: <i>sextiduo post Aequinoctium vernum Anni 1600</i> = \"the sixth day after the vernal equinox of 1600.\" The N.S. vernal equinox of 1600 fell on ~21 March, giving <b>≈ 27 March 1600 [N.S.]</b> (the catalogue date). Tycho cannot resist appending the era controversy: the chronologers <b>Joseph Scaliger</b> (<i>De emendatione temporum</i>, 1583/1598), <b>Gerardus Mercator</b> (<i>Chronologia</i>, 1569), <b>Heinrich Bünting</b> (<i>Chronologia</i>; \"Butingerus\"), and <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (the Görlitz mathematician, Tycho's own friend) argue the received year is short — most by one year, Scaliger's newer work by two — <i>\"so that it is now really 1602.\"</i> A jest, not a re-dating: the letter's own date stands at 1600 [N.S.].</li><li><b>Proverb.</b> <i>Multa cadunt inter ciatos (cyathos) supremaque labra</i> — \"many things fall between the cup and the lips,\" the classical <i>inter calicem supremaque labra</i> (\"there's many a slip 'twixt cup and lip\"); kept as the idiom and glossed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Wolfgang Franz; Johannes Jessenius (the \"Planet\"); the unnamed Transylvanian; Rudolf II (<i>Caesar</i>); Joseph Scaliger; Gerardus Mercator; Heinrich Bünting; Bartholomäus Scultetus.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Arx Benatica</i> = the castle of <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b>, Tycho's imperial seat before the move into Prague.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin reads securely throughout and the wit (Jessenius-as-planet, the theologians' \"mange,\" the chronology jest) is unambiguous; graded [B] only for the one <b>flagged glyph crux</b> (the malformed <i>‿ij</i> for the Mercury sign ☿), where the reading is fixed by sense rather than by a clean transcription. The identification of \"Butingerus\" as <b>Bünting</b> (chronologer) rather than Bullinger is adopted on contextual grounds and noted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 280-281 via the page images. Source_text matches Dreyer exactly, including the Mercury-glyph aside on Jessenius, the proverbial tags (bonus dormitat Homerus's cousin \"multa cadunt inter ciatos supremaque labra\"), and the closing chronological dispute over the year 1600/1602 with Scaliger, Mercator, Bünting and Scultetus named correctly. The date math (sixth day after the vernal equinox = 27 March) checks out against the letter's own title. Translation is accurate throughout; no defects found. Upgraded from the self-assigned published_grade B to A on the strength of full, clean collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Wolfgang Franzius", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000302001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000302001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that the epidemic has subsided and the Emperor lingers at Pilsen but is expected soon in Prague, and discusses an untrustworthy Transylvanian who had sought his letters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000327002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000328001", "d": "1600-03-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Jessenius", "ck": "Johannes Jessenius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Jessenius — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 28 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOHANNEM IESSENIUM A IESSEN.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME Domine Doctor IESSENI, amice plurimum semper colende, scripsit quidam domesticus noster, quem nuper Pilsnae in Aula habui, in rebus meis solicitantem, se a praecipuo quodam Saae Caesae Matis, Domini nostri Clementissimi, Consiliario subintellexisse, URSUM quendam Dithmarsicum supplicationem iam pridem de me conquerentem Consiliarijs porrexisse, sed ignominiose, acri adhibitâ obiurgatione, reiectam: cumque is, qui haec mihi insinuavit, nihil praeterea de contentis supplicationis istius addiderit, et idem Pilsnâ in Patriam suam, quae ad Westphaliam est, abierit, nec eius reditum intra tres vel quatuor menses expectem, ac nihilominus mea intersit tempestive scire, quid impudens iste de me conqueri ausus fuerit, cum illum nullo pacto, imo ne verbulo quopiam, sive per me sive per alios, toto tempore quo in Bohemiâ sum, laeserim, utut iam antea intolerandas ab ipso perpessus iniurias atque calumnias, quas in isto famoso, quod ante triennium Pragae contra me in publicum edidit scripto (quo putidius et vanius, cordatorum iudicio, hoc seculum vix vidit) conglomerare non erubuit; sed peculiari consilio, tum quoque ob graviorum occupationum distractionem, omnia, ac si ea ignorarem, dissimulavi: et certe, si mihi obviam fieret URSUS iste, eum non nôssem, nec mihi constitit, an is interea Pragae, an vero alibi fuerit, nam de ipso percontari non dignatus sum, ut nisi eius supra modum perfricta frons et scurrilitas, mentiendique inverecundia mihi non alias constarent, mirari satis non possem, quid ille de me, ita taciturno, nullâque molestiâ illum afficiente, querelae loco, apud Amplissimos Dominos Consiliarios adferre possit: Neque enim quidpiam habet, quod veraciter dicat, nisi hoc ipsum obijcere velit, quod taceam, atque ex alto ipsius nugas hactenus despexerim, suo tamen forte tempore in ipsum retorquendas, quamvis per eas non tam meae famae atque existimationi (quae mille eiuscemodi Sycophanticos allatratus non moratur) nocuerit, quam quod suam petulantem dicacitatem et scurrilem naturam, convitijs et maledictis scatentem, toti Mundo omnibusque sincere iudicantibus, suo se iudicio, tanquam sorex, prodens, propalaverit. Licet vero Prudentissimi Domini Consiliarij illius levitatem morum et verborum forte non ignorantes, querelas istas non admiserint, sed ipsum obiurgatum dimiserint: ne tamen aliqua suspicio adhuc latitare possit, ipsum nonnulla, haud iniuria, conquestum fuisse, sed occasiones illi, a me vel meis, ut hoc faceret, datas esse: nosti enim illud nimis in mundo tritum: Calumniare audacter, semper aliquid adhaeret; te per amicitiam nostram, quibus possum precibus, rogo, velis omnem movere lapidem, ut exemplar supplicationis eius adipiscar, vel si id commode fieri nequeat, saltem ut summam eorum, quae continebat, resciscam. Quod si alio modo obtineri non possit, spero te apud Magcum et Nobilissimum Dominum MECKERUM (quem non dubijs inditijs, et etiam te, cum ultimo Pragae simul essemus, idipsum referente, mihi sincere faventem persuasum habeo) id, quicquid est, percepturum. Feceritis sic non saltem ille, sed et tu mihi rem admodum acceptam, atque ut mihi a talibus subdolis et immeritis delationibus inposterum praecavere valeam, haud inutilem, imo fortean etiam necessariam. Sic enim fieri conditiones meae nunc exigunt; sum enim hîc peregrinus, nec Illustrium aut authoritate praecellentium Virorum consanguinitate et necessitudine, ut antea in Patriâ, fultus, quin et praeterea hîc extra Aulam vitam ago umbratilem, ignarus, quid isthic de me in quamcunque partem dicatur, nec alias libenter rebus me immisceo alienis, sed meis solummodo vacans studijs, Spartam, quam nactus sum, pro virili ornare satago, ut in honorem optimi Imperatoris atque Imperij ea, favente supremo Numine, moliri atque exequi queam, quae ad omnem posteritatem utilia et memorabilia futura confido. Sed tu, mi amantissime IESSENI, pro ea quae nobis intercedit familiaritate, omnia satis perspicis, ut ulteriore commemoratione non sit opus, et te nostris hac in parte desiderijs non defuturum omnino mihi polliceor.</p><p>Vale et praedictum Magcum Dominum MECKERUM ex me officiosissime saluta.<br>Dabantur festinanti calamo ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 28 Martij Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOHANNES JESSENIUS A JESSEN.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned Lord Doctor Jessenius, a friend always much to be cultivated: a certain servant of ours, whom I lately had at the court at <b>Pilsen</b>, soliciting in my affairs, has written that he gathered from a certain chief Councillor of the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, that <b>a certain Ursus, the Dithmarsian, had some time ago presented to the Councillors a supplication complaining about me — but [that it was] ignominiously rejected, with sharp rebuke applied</b>. And since he who intimated this to me added nothing further about the contents of that supplication, and the same man has gone from Pilsen into his fatherland, which is in Westphalia (nor do I expect his return within three or four months), while it nonetheless concerns me to know in good time <b>what that impudent fellow dared to complain of about me</b> — when I have in no way, nay, not by any little word, whether through myself or through others, injured him the whole time I am in Bohemia (although I had already before suffered intolerable injuries and calumnies from him, which he did not blush to heap up in that <b>infamous writing he published at Prague against me three years ago</b> — than which, in the judgement of the wise, this age has scarcely seen anything more putrid and vain) — but, by particular design, and from the distraction of weightier occupations, I dissembled everything, as if I knew nothing of it. And certainly, if that Ursus came to meet me, I would not know him; nor is it established to me whether he was meanwhile at Prague or elsewhere, for I have not deigned to inquire about him — so that, did I not otherwise know his beyond-measure brazen front, his scurrility, and his shameless lying, I could not sufficiently wonder what he could bring as a complaint against me — I so silent, and afflicting him with no annoyance — before the Most Ample Lords Councillors. For he has nothing he may truly say, unless he wishes to object this very thing: that I am silent, and have hitherto <b>from on high despised his trifles</b> (to be retorted upon him, perhaps, in their own time) — though by them he has harmed not so much my fame and reputation (which heeds not a thousand such sycophantic barkings) as that he has made manifest, to the whole world and all sincere judges, his own petulant scurrility and scurrilous nature, teeming with insults and curses — betraying himself by his own judgement, <b>like a shrew-mouse</b>. But although the most prudent Lords Councillors — perhaps not ignorant of his lightness of manners and words — did not admit those complaints, but dismissed him rebuked: yet, lest some suspicion may still lurk that he complained of some things not without cause, but that occasions were given him, by me or mine, to do it (for you know that saying too trite in the world: <b><i>\"Slander boldly — something always sticks\"</b></i>) — I ask you, by our friendship, with what prayers I can, that <b>you would move every stone, that I may obtain a copy of his supplication</b> — or, if that cannot conveniently be done, at least that I may learn the sum of what it contained. And if it cannot be had another way, I hope you will get it, whatever it is, with the Magnificent and most noble Lord <b>Mecker</b> (whom, by no doubtful signs — and you too reporting the same, when we were last together at Prague — I hold to be sincerely favourable to me). You will thus do me — not he alone, but you too — a thing very welcome, and not useless (nay, perhaps even necessary), that I may guard myself henceforth against such crafty and undeserved accusations. For so my present conditions require: I am here <b>a stranger</b>, not propped (as before in my fatherland) by kinship and the bond of illustrious or pre-eminently authoritative men; and besides, here I live a <b>shadowy [retired] life outside the Court</b>, ignorant of what is said there about me in any direction; nor do I otherwise gladly meddle in others' affairs, but, devoting myself only to my studies, <b>strive to adorn, to the best of my power, the Sparta I have obtained</b> — that, to the honour of the best Emperor and of the Empire, I may, by the favour of the supreme Power, contrive and accomplish things I trust will be useful and memorable to all posterity. But you, my most loving Jessenius, by the familiarity that passes between us, perceive all this well enough, so that there is no need of further mention; and I altogether promise myself that you will not fail my wishes in this part.</p><p>Farewell, and greet the aforesaid Magnificent Lord Mecker from me most dutifully.<br>Given with hurrying pen from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 28th day of March, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johannes Jessenius (Jan Jessenský)</b>, the anatomist and Tycho's intimate (cf. brahe-t_16000324001, brahe-t_16000327002, brahe-t_16001008001), well placed at the imperial court.</li><li><b>Significance — a principal document of the Tycho–Ursus feud.</b> This letter concerns <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär, \"Ursus\"</b> (c. 1551–1600), of <b>Dithmarschen</b> — the imperial mathematician who had <b>plagiarised Tycho's geo-heliocentric world-system</b> and savaged him in print. Tycho has learned (via a servant, from a chief imperial councillor) that <b>Ursus lodged a supplication/complaint against him with the Councillors, which was <i>ignominiously rejected with sharp rebuke</b></i> — and asks <b>Jessenius to obtain a copy of that supplication</b> (or at least its substance), if necessary through the favourable councillor <b>Lord Mecker</b>, \"that I may guard myself against such crafty and undeserved accusations.\" This belongs to the <b>legal/printed contest of 1597–1600</b> (Tycho was preparing the <i>Apologia</i> against Ursus that became, posthumously, the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> appendix / the suit pursued after Ursus's death in 1600). Cross-links to brahe-t_15990321001 (the \"Dithmarsic brute\" and the plagiarism brief).</li><li><b>The earlier defamatory book.</b> <i>\"that infamous writing he published at Prague against me three years ago\"</i> = <b>Ursus's <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i> (Prague, 1597)</b> — \"than which, in the judgement of the wise, this age has scarcely seen anything more putrid and vain.\" Tycho stresses he had met all this with <b>deliberate silence</b> (\"from on high I have despised his trifles\"), having \"in no way injured him … the whole time I am in Bohemia.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's self-portrait (notable).</b> A striking statement of his position as <b>imperial exile</b>: \"I am here <b>a stranger</b>, not propped by kinship and the authority of great men as before in my fatherland,\" living \"<b>a shadowy life outside the Court</b>,\" meddling in nothing, \"devoting myself only to my studies\" — and so the more exposed to undeserved slander, hence the need to know the charge. The closing <i>\"the Sparta I have obtained, I strive to adorn\"</i> (<i>Spartam quam nactus es, hanc orna</i> — the classical maxim of doing one's duty in one's appointed station) frames his astronomical work \"to the honour of the best Emperor and of the Empire … useful and memorable to all posterity.\"</li><li><b>Proverbs / figures (kept and glossed).</b> <i>\"Calumniare audacter, semper aliquid adhaeret\"</i> — <b>\"Slander boldly, something always sticks\"</b> (the maxim of calumny's residue); <i>tanquam sorex … prodens</i> — \"betraying himself <b>like a shrew-mouse</b>\" (the proverb <i>sorex suo perit indicio</i>, the shrew destroyed by its own squeak); <i>Sycophanticos allatratus</i> — \"sycophantic barkings.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Johannes Jessenius; <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>, \"the Dithmarsian\"; <b>Lord Mecker</b> (a favourable imperial councillor); <b>Rudolf II</b> and the imperial <b>Councillors</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 28 Martij Anni 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'bonorem' to 'honorem' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.282.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — eloquent, fully construable Latin, read securely; the substance (Ursus's rejected supplication against Tycho; the request that Jessenius procure a copy or summary, via Lord Mecker; the recall of Ursus's 1597 Prague defamation and Tycho's studied silence; the self-portrait as a defenceless <i>peregrinus</i> devoted to his \"Sparta\") is firmly construed and is a key document of the Tycho–Ursus controversy. The proverbs (<i>calumniare audacter</i>; <i>sorex</i>; <i>Spartam … orna</i>) are identified and glossed. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (opening, every proper name -- Iessenius/Ieesseni, Ursus Dithmarsicus, Meckerum -- and the closing date '28 Martij Anni 1600') against TBOO VIII pp.281-282 read as page images. Translation is faithful throughout; the sole defect is a one-letter corruption in our own source_text ('bonorem' for Dreyer's 'honorem'), which per protocol forces grade C despite the translation itself being unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the non-word 'bonorem' where Dreyer clearly prints 'honorem' (b/h corruption); the published English correctly renders 'honour' regardless, so the fault is confined to the corpus text, not the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>ut in honorem optimi Imperatoris atque Imperij ea, favente supremo Numine, moliri atque exequi queam (TBOO VIII p.282)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated the single defect against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.282), which clearly reads 'ut in honorem optimi Imperatoris atque Imperij'. A one-letter correction; the translation was already correct and needed no change. No uncertainty remains in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Jessenius", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000507002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks the recipient to investigate the contents of a suppressed supplication by Ursus the Dithmarsian, which complained against him to imperial councillors but was ignominiously rejected.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000328001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000328002", "d": "1600-03-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "David Chytraeus", "ck": "David Chytraeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → David Chytraeus — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 28 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD DAVIDEM CHYTRAeUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>REVERENDE et Clarissime vir, Amice inprimis honorande. Literas tuas iuxta initia Novembris superioris Anni Rostochio ad me datas paulo post huius primordia per filium huc revertentem accepi, proque singulari tua, quam isthic affatim declaras, erga me affectione et benevolentiâ gratias habeo plurimas. Respondissem citius et quae in mei gratiam annuis tuis Historijs inserere decrevisti paulo aliter formata misissem, nî mihi tuae tardius quam existimabas fuissent redditae; defectus etiam tabellariorum effecit, ut id prius exequi non licuerit. Remitto autem nunc chartam illam, iuxta quam ea, quae de Caesa Mate ob me huc vocatum et benignissime exceptum memorare constituisti, in quibusdam, uti vides, certis de caussis mutata consignavi, omnia tamen tuo arbitrio, an sic vel aliter scribi velis, relinquens; acceptumque mihi est, quod hoc officium in mei gratiam non detrectes, et spero id Caesae Mati placiturum. Quia vero relationi de hoc praeterito Anno illa immisceri non poterant, haud incongruum duxerim ad huius 16 Centenarij, quem Iubilaeum vocant, eadem applicare, sumptâ occasione, quod hoc anno Progymnasmata mea Instaurationis Astronomicae et nonnulla alia Opera, in Daniâ prius inchoata, sub auspicijs Augustissimi Imperatoris RUDOLPHI Secundi, Domini nostri Clementissimi, eius praesidio et liberalitate fultus, in publicum ediderim; sic enim, Deo conatibus nostris aspirante, me effecturum spero.</p><p>Habeo insuper gratias, quod ea, quae isthic in Orbe Arctoo proximis temporibus gesta sunt, communicaveris. Inaudivi CAROLUM Ducem totâ Sueciâ iam potitum; utinam victoriâ illâ moderate uti vellet, nec nimis in eos, qui illi restiterunt, saevire, et Regni Senatoribus (inter quos est optimus ille ERICUS SPARRAeUS noster) parcere, ut pacatius et firmius eius sit in posterum Regimen, ne exitum infelicem sortiatur effera vindictae cupiditas. Exemplum habet domesticum in ERICO fratre.</p><p>Quid in Dania Patria mea geratur, et an novas res isthic moliantur, qui fatis praesunt publicis, necdum mihi liquet. Optandum, ut pace hac, quâ multis iam Annis gaudent, diu frui possint, nec internis aut externis irrequietudinibus turbentur. Batavos pacis conditiones quascunque suspectas habere et renuere, accepi, adeo ut quidam apud eos Primarius nuper Hamburgum in hunc modum (prout eius formalia verba ad me relata sunt) perscripserit: Si concederetur Statibus Hollandiae in membrana, quae aequaret magnitudine bovinum tergus, quascunque ipsimet vellent pacis conditiones, consignare, easdemque ratas se habituros addicerent adversarij, tamen pacificationem omnem aversaturos, cum toties sub tali praetextu delusi sint.</p><p>De Ungaricis rebus nihil hîc, relatu dignum, scitur; per hyemem transactam parum a Caesareanis effectum est. Dicitur Turca maiorem parare exercitum quam unquam antea. Caesar noster vicissim, quantum potest, se contra ipsum armat, utut Imperij Principes pro maiore parte propter Hispanorum incursiones subsidia denegent et quaedam alia agant. WAIUODA pro se recuperatam Transylvaniam feudi loco retinere laborat, cuius Legati admodum insolenter et tumultuarie nuper Pilsnae se gesserunt, idque Imperator indigne ferens misit quendam ex suis, qui ea de re apud WAIUODAM conquereretur. Dicunt aliquot millia Gallicorum equitum et vicinarum Regionum adventare contra Turcas in gratiam Caesaris belligeraturi. Quantam stragem ediderint superiori Anno Tartaricae incursiones in superiori Ungaria et parte aliqua Moraviae, existimo te iam antea percepisse. Deus tam eorum quam Turcarum furores et Tyrannidem reprimere atque a cervicibus Christianorum avertere dignetur. Ubi plura de rebus, quae ibi atque in viciniâ gesta sunt, intellexero, te eorum reddere conscium non intermittam.</p><p>Caesar adhuc Pilsnae abest, ubi ultra semestre proxime elapsum mansit, ob luem Epidemicam, quae gravissime antecedente anno totam Bohemiam et propinquas regiones infestavit, paulo post Pascha, uti existimant, Pragam rediturus. Deus ipsius Matem nobis quam diutissime florentem atque incolumem conservet. Cui etiam Divino Numini te tuosque commendo. Vale et generum tuum Dominum Doctorem IOHANNEM FREDERUM, cui pro carminibus missis gratias habeo, ex me peramanter saluta; scripsissem ei, si otium suppetijsset.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 28 Martij Anno 1600 stylo novo.</p><p>CIRCA hoc tempus inclaruit apud Augustissimum et<br>potentissimum Imperatorem RUDOLPHUM eius<br>nominis secundum in Bohemia et<br>circumiacenti Germania ac vicinis regnis<br>TYCHO BRAHE ex illustri et perantiquo stemmate nobilitatis Danicæ oriundus interque fratres suos in regno<br>illo viros splendidos et dignitate præpollentes natu maximus. Is enim, cum<br>plurimis retro annis divinæ Astronomiæ a mendis repurgandæ et redintegrandæ<br>incredibiles labores et sumptus exantlasset, hoc anno sua Astronomicæ<br>instaurationis Progymnasmata et quædam alia in hoc sublimi studiorum genere sub<br>auspicijs dicti Imperatoris optimi publici iuris fecit, ut Astronomia quoque<br>suum sic quoddam inveniret Iubilæum. Occasio autem, quod is e Daniâ Patriâ suâ<br>primum in Germaniam , deinde Bohemiam præter omnium expectationem migravit, hæc<br>fuit. Ante annos 24 ex clementissimâ provisione laudatissimæ memoriæ Regis<br>Danorum FRIDERICI II<br>TYCHO in insula<br>Hellesponti Danici<br>Huænna arcem Uraniburgum pro exercitijs sideralis scientiæ isthic commode<br>peragendis decore et sumptuose extruxit et in eâ plurima instrumenta Astronomica<br>adeo magnifice et solide elaborata, ut vix similia orbis alibi viderit, a se<br>inventa et confecta disposuit (veluti etiam in Historiâ nostrâ Arctoâ suo loco<br>aliqualem huius Uraniburgi fecimus<br>mentionem) quæ omnia, quoad vixit dictus Serenissimus Rex, isthic in debito usu liberaliter<br>conservabantur, quemadmodum etiam post eius luctuosum obitum per totum octennium<br>in Interregno, spe insuper facta, tam a Clementissimo Rege illo FRIDERICO, quam postmodum Regni eius cunctis<br>Senatoribus, quod illa omnia in honorem Regni et Regis ibidem competenter<br>sustentanda et perpetuanda forent. Verum cum non saltem præter ipsius TYCHONIS,<br>sed et multorum aliorum, tam intra quam extra Regnum illud, opinionem id<br>postmodum aliter ceciderit, ille, ne tantus apparatus Astronomicus tantique ab<br>illo per 21 annos ibi exantlati labores paulatim collabascerent tandemque<br>prorsus interirent, maluit Patriâ dulcissimâ et plurimis de nobilitate primariâ<br>sanguine iunctis cæterisque amicis charissimis derelictis, sponte unâ cum tota<br>domesticâ familiâ in peregrina transire potius quam huius tam insperatæ et<br>dolendæ ruinæ spectator esse, ideoque ante triennium, anno nimirum 1597, circa<br>solstitium æstivum, assumptâ secum (uti dictum) totâ domo suâ, primum huc<br>Rostochium enavigavit, ubi per anni quadrantem substitit, quo in hac viciniâ<br>Danis suis rem rectius dispiciendi, Patriæ adhuc studens, tempus relinqueret.<br>Quâ etiam de caussâ hinc<br>ingruente hyeme in Holsatiam, Daniæ pariter conterminam, se recepit, eo per literas<br>Nobilissimi et optimæ memoriæ Herois, Domini HENRICI RANTZOVIJ, Regij isthic Vicarij, affinis sui, invitatus,<br>a quo arcem Wandesburgum, urbi Hamburgæ vicinam, cum suis inhabitandam accepit,<br>ibique annuum tempus moratus est. Neque enim longius<br>indicatâ de caussâ se, utut conditiones non incommodæ interim oblatæ essent,<br>conferre voluit. Dum itaque ibi subsistit, ad nunquam satis laudatum Imperatorem<br>RUDOLPHUM de hoc TYCHONIS e Patriâ<br>discessu rumor perfertur. Quare ipsius Cæsarea<br>Maiestas, uti est erga omnes liberales disciplinas, inprimis vero<br>hoc sublime Astronomiæ studium, quod omnibus ætatibus regales animos, cœlo nata<br>pectora movit, ut id in precio habuerint, animo favorabili et benignissimo<br>TYCHONEM, de quo antea inaudierat, eum hisce serio invigilare, multumque<br>operæprecij locasse, ad se vocatum in Bohemiam cum totâ familiâ traduxit, ipsumque Clementissime<br>acceptum in arce quadam e suis, quinque miliaribus Pragâ, Regni Metropoli, dissitâ,<br>Bennaticâ dictâ, ut isthic otio fruens<br>philosophico inchoatam Astronomicæ restitutionis telam pertexeret, alijsque<br>ingenuo homine dignis exercitijs vacaret, collocavit, et sumptibus ad tam arduos<br>labores requisitis liberaliter et benignissime ei prospexit, utut ob belli<br>Turcici diuturnitatem difficiliora quam<br>alias tunc forent tempora. Res sane summi Orbis Monarchæ liberalitate et<br>munificentiâ dignissima, atque ad omnem posteritatem deprædicanda. Quin et de<br>novo Uraniburgo, quod Danico illi non cederet, se convenienti loco et tempore,<br>pro TYCHONIS Instrumentis ibi collocandis et rite usurpandis, se provisurum,<br>atque ipsi et familiæ eius Feudo idoneo, ne alienigenæ redditi in posterum ad<br>honestam sustentationem necessarijs destituerentur, benignissime provisurum,<br>Clementissimâ voluntate addixit, quemadmodum hæc et similia ad nos et alios hinc<br>inde viros præstantes et eruditos non sine omnium voluptate a fide dignis relata<br>et scripta accepimus. Sic Generosus ille TYCHO BRAHÆUS, Astronomiæ cultorum<br>nostri seculi antesignanus, cuius sublime ingenium et indefessum in hisce<br>præcipuis disciplinis, quæ circa siderum motus versantur, illustrandis studium,<br>quod toti Daniæ singulari ornamento et honori superioribus annis fuerat, eâ<br>relictâ, in Cæsaris ditiones concessit,<br>ibique portum quietum suis studijs, non sine Dei peculiari provisu, a<br>potentissimo Imperatore adeptus est. Faxit author Universi, cuius gloriam cœli<br>enarrant, et opera manuum ostendit æther, ut in Augustissimi Imperatoris<br>RUDOLPHI II, qui hoc negocium suo<br>clementissimo favore et liberalitate promovit, totiusque Imperij Germanici decus<br>et honorem utilitatemque, quo ad sidera durant, nunquam intermoriturum omnia<br>vergant et stabiliantur.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO DAVID CHYTRAEUS.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Reverend and most renowned man, friend especially to be honoured. Your letter, sent me from Rostock about the beginning of November of last year, I received a little after the beginning of this [year], through my son returning hither; and for your singular affection and benevolence toward me, which you abundantly declare therein, I give very many thanks. I would have replied sooner, and would have sent — formed a little otherwise — those things you resolved to insert in my honour into your <b>annual Histories</b>, had yours not reached me later than you thought; the lack of couriers, too, prevented my doing it earlier. But I now return that <b>sheet</b>, in which I have set down — in certain places changed, as you see, for sure reasons — those things you resolved to record <b>about the Imperial Majesty, on account of my being called hither and most kindly received</b>, leaving all, however, to your judgement, whether you wish it written so or otherwise; and it is welcome to me that you do not decline this service in my honour, and I hope it will please the Imperial Majesty. But since those things could not be inserted into the account of this past Year, I should think it not incongruous to apply the same to [the account of] this 16th-century [year], which they call the <b>Jubilee</b> — taking the occasion that <b>this year I have published the <i>Progymnasmata</i> of my Astronomical Restoration, and some other Works begun earlier in Denmark, under the auspices of the most august Emperor Rudolf II</b>, our most clement Lord, supported by his protection and liberality; for so, God favouring our endeavours, I hope to bring it about.</p><p>I have thanks besides, that you have communicated what has lately been done there, in the Northern world. I have heard that <b>Duke Charles has now gained all Sweden</b>; would that he would use that victory moderately, not rage too much against those who resisted him, and <b>spare the Senators of the Realm (among whom is that best man, our Erik Sparre)</b>, that his rule may be more peaceful and firmer hereafter, lest a savage desire of vengeance meet an unhappy outcome. He has a domestic example in [his] brother <b>Erik</b>.</p><p>What is happening in Denmark, my fatherland — and whether those who preside over public destinies are contriving new things there — is not yet clear to me. It is to be wished they may long enjoy this peace, which they have had now for many years, and not be disturbed by inner or outer unrests. I have heard that <b>the Dutch hold suspect and refuse any peace conditions whatever</b> — so much so that a certain leading man among them lately wrote to Hamburg in this manner (as his exact words were reported to me): <b>that if it were granted to the States of Holland to set down, on a parchment that would equal an ox-hide in size, whatever conditions of peace they themselves wished — and the adversaries pledged to hold the same ratified — yet they would reject all pacification, since they have so often been deceived under such pretext.</b></p><p>Of Hungarian affairs nothing worth relating is known here; during the past winter little was accomplished by the imperial [forces]. The <b>Turk</b> is said to be preparing a greater army than ever; our Emperor in turn arms himself against him as much as he can, although the Princes of the Empire for the most part <b>deny subsidies on account of the Spaniards' incursions</b> and do certain other things. The <b>Voivode</b> strives to retain Transylvania, recovered for himself, in the manner of a fief; his envoys lately behaved quite insolently and tumultuously at <b>Pilsen</b>, and the Emperor, taking it ill, sent one of his men to complain of the matter to the Voivode. They say some thousands of <b>French cavalry</b>, and [those] of neighbouring regions, are coming to wage war against the Turks for the Emperor's sake. How great a slaughter the <b>Tartar incursions</b> wrought last year in Upper Hungary and some part of Moravia, I think you have already perceived. May God deign to repress the furies and tyranny both of them and of the Turks, and to turn [them] away from the necks of Christians. When I learn more of what is done there and in the neighbourhood, I will not fail to make you aware of it.</p><p>The Emperor is still away at <b>Pilsen</b>, where he has stayed beyond the past half-year on account of the <b>epidemic plague</b> which last year most grievously infested all Bohemia and the neighbouring regions — about to return to Prague, as they think, a little after Easter. May God preserve His Majesty for us, flourishing and unharmed, as long as possible; to which Divine Power I also commend you and yours. Farewell; and most lovingly greet from me your son-in-law Lord Doctor <b>Johann Freder</b>, to whom I give thanks for the poems sent; I would have written to him, had leisure sufficed.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 28th day of March, the year 1600, New Style.</p><p>About this time there rose to renown, at the court of the most august and most powerful Emperor Rudolph, the second of that name, in Bohemia and the surrounding parts of Germany and the neighbouring kingdoms, Tycho Brahe — sprung from an illustrious and most ancient line of Danish nobility, and, among his brothers, men splendid and pre-eminent in rank within that kingdom, the eldest by birth. For he, having in very many years past expended incredible labours and expense in purging and restoring divine Astronomy from its errors, in this year, under the auspices of the said most excellent Emperor, made public his Progymnasmata of the Restoration of Astronomy and certain other works in this sublime kind of study, so that Astronomy too might thus find, as it were, its own Jubilee.</p><p>Now the occasion of his migrating, beyond everyone's expectation, from Denmark his native land, first into Germany and then into Bohemia, was this. Twenty-four years ago, by the most gracious provision of King Frederick the Second of Denmark, of most praised memory, Tycho built handsomely, and at great expense, on the island of Hven in the Danish Sound, the castle of Uraniborg, for the convenient carrying-out there of the exercises of the science of the stars; and in it he set up very many astronomical instruments, wrought so magnificently and solidly that the world has scarcely seen the like elsewhere, invented and made by himself — just as we too, in our Historia Arctoa, have in its proper place made some mention of this Uraniborg. All of this, for as long as the said Most Serene King lived, was there generously maintained in its due use, just as also, after his lamentable death, throughout the whole eight years of the Interregnum, hope was moreover given — [earlier] by that most gracious King Frederick himself, and afterward by all the Senators of his Kingdom — that all these things would be suitably supported and perpetuated there, in honour of the Kingdom and of the King.</p><p>But since afterward, contrary to the expectation not only of Tycho himself but of many others, both within and outside that Kingdom, this turned out otherwise, he — lest so great an astronomical apparatus, and labours of such magnitude expended by him there over twenty-one years, should gradually collapse and at last utterly perish — chose rather, having left behind his most beloved native land, very many kinsmen of the foremost nobility joined to him by blood, and his other dearest friends, of his own accord to pass into foreign parts together with his whole household, than to be a spectator of this so unhoped-for and lamentable ruin. And so, three years ago now, namely in the year 1597, about the summer solstice, having taken with him, as has been said, his whole household, he first sailed hence to Rostock, where he remained for a quarter of a year, so as to leave time, in that neighbourhood, for his fellow Danes to look more rightly into the matter — he being still solicitous for his native land.</p><p>For that reason too, with winter coming on, he withdrew from there into Holstein, likewise bordering on Denmark, invited thither by letters of the most noble Lord Heinrich Rantzau, hero of most excellent memory, the Royal Viceroy there, his kinsman; from him he received the castle of Wandsbek, near the city of Hamburg, to inhabit with his household, and there he stayed a year's time — for, the reason having already been indicated, he was unwilling to settle any further off, however not inconvenient the terms meanwhile offered to him might have been.</p><p>While, then, he was staying there, a report of Tycho's departure from his native land was carried to the never sufficiently praised Emperor Rudolph. Wherefore His Imperial Majesty — being disposed, as he is, toward all the liberal disciplines, but chiefly toward this sublime study of Astronomy, which in every age has moved royal minds and hearts born for heaven to hold it in high esteem — with a favourable and most benign spirit, having heard beforehand of Tycho and that he was earnestly devoted to these pursuits and had achieved much of worth, summoned him to himself and brought him across into Bohemia with his whole household. Having received him most graciously, he settled him in a certain one of his castles, five miles distant from Prague, the capital of the Kingdom, called Benátky, so that there, enjoying philosophical leisure, he might weave to completion the web of the restoration of Astronomy which he had begun, and might have leisure for other pursuits worthy of a man of liberal condition; and he provided for him, liberally and most kindly, the expenses required for labours so arduous — even though, on account of the length of the Turkish war, the times were then more difficult than they would otherwise have been. A thing indeed most worthy of the liberality and munificence of the greatest Monarch of the world, and to be proclaimed to all posterity.</p><p>Nay more, [the Emperor] pledged, with most gracious will, that as to a new Uraniborg, which should not yield to the Danish one, he would himself provide, at a fitting place and time, for the placing and proper use there of Tycho's Instruments; and that he would most kindly provide him and his household with a suitable fief, lest, once rendered foreigners, they should in future be left destitute of what is necessary for an honest maintenance — even as we ourselves have received these and similar things, related and set down by trustworthy men, not without delight to us all, [addressed] to us and to other excellent and learned men here and there.</p><p>Thus that noble Tycho Brahe, standard-bearer among the votaries of Astronomy in our century — whose sublime genius and untiring zeal for illustrating these foremost disciplines concerned with the motions of the stars had in former years been a singular ornament and honour to all Denmark — having left that land behind, passed over into the Emperor's dominions, and there, not without God's own particular providence, obtained from the most powerful Emperor a quiet harbour for his studies. May the Author of the Universe, whose glory the heavens declare and whose handiwork the sky displays, grant that all things may incline toward, and be established in, the never-dying glory, honour, and profit — for as long as the stars endure — of the most august Emperor Rudolph the Second, who has advanced this business with his most gracious favour and liberality, and of the whole German Empire.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; occasion.</b> <b>David Chytraeus</b>, the Rostock historian — Tycho's reply to <b>a Chytraeus letter of \"about the beginning of November [1599]\"</b> (a later one than brahe-t_15981007001, the 7 Oct 1598 letter), continuing the <b>annual-history thread</b>.</li><li><b>The migration-narrative for the <i>Saxonia</i> (cross-ref).</b> Tycho returns the <b>sheet</b> on which Chytraeus had drafted his account \"about the Imperial Majesty … on account of my being called hither,\" with Tycho's <b>emendations \"for sure reasons,\"</b> all left to Chytraeus's judgement. Since it could not fit the prior year's account, Tycho proposes applying it to <b>the <i>Jubilee</i> year 1600</b>, on the occasion that \"<b>this year I have published the <i>Progymnasmata</i> of my Astronomical Restoration</b>\" under Rudolf II's auspices. (The same narrative Sturtz and Brasch also pursued; cf. brahe-t_16010415002, brahe-t_16000920002, brahe-t_16000916002. Chytraeus would die in 1600, leaving the work unfinished.)</li><li><b>The Scandinavian news.</b> <b>Duke Charles of Sweden</b> \"has now gained all Sweden\"; Tycho hopes he will be <b>moderate and spare the Senators — \"that best man, our Erik Sparre\"</b> (executed at the Linköping Bloodbath, 1600) — warning by the \"domestic example\" of Charles's deposed brother <b>Erik XIV</b>. (Cf. brahe-t_15991203001, brahe-t_16010418001.)</li><li><b>The Dutch \"ox-hide parchment\" anecdote (a vivid set-piece).</b> Tycho relays a striking figure of <b>Dutch distrust of Spanish peace overtures</b>: a Holland leader wrote that even if the <b>States of Holland</b> were allowed to write <i>their own</i> peace terms \"on a parchment as large as an ox-hide,\" and the enemy pledged to ratify them, <b>they would still refuse all pacification — \"so often have they been deceived under such pretext.\"</b> A memorable contemporary image of the Eighty Years' War deadlock.</li><li><b>The Hungarian war.</b> The <b>Turk</b> preparing a great army; the Emperor under-supported (the German princes denying subsidies, citing <b>Spanish incursions</b>); the <b>Voivode</b> (<b>Michael the Brave</b>) seeking Transylvania <i>as a fief</i>, his envoys \"insolent\" at Pilsen; <b>French cavalry</b> expected; the <b>Tartar incursions</b> into Upper Hungary and Moravia. (Cf. brahe-t_16000926002, brahe-t_16010415002.)</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> David Chytraeus (and his son-in-law <b>Johann Freder</b>, <i>Frederus</i>); <b>Charles of Sweden</b> and his brother <b>Erik XIV</b>; <b>Erik Sparre</b>; the <b>Voivode</b> (Michael the Brave); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 28 Martij Anno 1600 stylo novo</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label. (Same date as the Ursus letter, brahe-t_16000328001.)</li><li><b>What the recovered text is.</b> A third-person Latin biographical notice of Tycho (\"CIRCA hoc tempus inclaruit...\") that forms the missing head of the letter catalogued as Tycho Brahe → David Chytraeus, Benátky, 28 March 1600; the published tail (\"non intermittam... Vale...\") is Tycho's own first-person close. Since the recovered text never shifts into Tycho's own voice, it reads as an enclosure carried within his covering letter rather than as part of the letter proper — and the exact seam between the two is itself unrecovered.</li><li><b>Not a private scrap.</b> The identical incipit (\"CIRCA hoc tempus inclaruit apud Augustissimum...\") recurs in the corpus's <i>Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia</i> (vol. VIII, among the documents around Tycho's letter to Duke Ferdinand of 18/28 January 1601), confirming this text had an established place in the printed correspondence record and is not merely a manuscript fragment lost to the ingest defect.</li><li><b>The \"Historia Arctoa\" authorship crux.</b> The aside \"as we too, in our Historia Arctoa, have in its proper place made some mention of this Uraniborg\" is illuminated by a separate, later Brahe letter on record in the register of Tycho's correspondence, in which Tycho refers explicitly to \"Historia Arctoa ab optimo illo CHYTRAEO inde ab Anno millesimo quingentesimo inchoata\" — \"the Historia Arctoa, begun by that excellent Chytraeus from the year 1500\" — almost certainly Chytraeus's own <i>Chronicon Saxoniae</i> (Rostock 1590; Leipzig 1593), which Tycho hoped to see extended to a full century by 1600. This makes it likely the \"we\" of the enclosure speaks in Chytraeus's own authorial voice — i.e. that this is, or closely follows, Chytraeus's own draft chronicle entry on Tycho for the year 1600, which Tycho is here returning (corrected or endorsed) under cover of his letter. FLAGGED AS CRUX: the precise authorship and transmission chain — Chytraeus drafting it and sending it to Tycho for correction, versus Tycho supplying a third-person self-notice (a known contemporary convention) for Chytraeus to copy verbatim — cannot be settled from this fragment alone.</li><li><b>Internal chronology checks out exactly.</b> Uraniborg \"twenty-four years ago\" (built from 1576, correct for a document of March 1600), \"twenty-one years\" of labour there (1576–1597), departure \"three years ago... 1597\" via a quarter-year at Rostock then a year at Rantzau's Wandsbek, and the Danish Interregnum given as \"eight years\" (Frederick II's death 1588 to Christian IV's 1596 majority) — all match the documented record (Dreyer 1890; Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> / <i>The Measure of the Heavens</i>).</li><li><b>The Emperor's unfulfilled promises.</b> The notice records, as of March 1600, Rudolf II's pledge to build Tycho a Bohemian \"new Uraniborg\" and to grant him and his family a fief so that, as émigrés, they would not be left without honest means of support — a specific, datable undertaking that can be set against what was actually delivered before Tycho's death in October 1601 (Benátky, then a Prague town residence, no purpose-built observatory, and only partial family provision).</li><li><b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> appears as the Royal Viceroy in Holstein and Tycho's kinsman who hosted him at Wandsbek Castle in 1597–98, consistent with Lohmeier (2000) on Rantzau.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the returned-and-emended migration-narrative sheet and the plan to set it in the Jubilee-1600 <i>Saxonia</i> with the <i>Progymnasmata</i> publication; the Swedish news and the plea to spare Erik Sparre; the <b>Dutch \"ox-hide parchment\" anecdote</b>; the Hungarian-war survey with Michael the Brave, the denied subsidies, the Tartar incursions) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>The Latin is a dense, periodic panegyric style but syntactically unambiguous throughout, and I am confident in the sentence-level translation. The one substantive open question is external to the Latin itself: whose voice speaks as 'we' in 'our Historia Arctoa,' and by what route this third-person notice of Tycho reached him for enclosure in his own letter to Chytraeus. I have grounded the identification of the Historia Arctoa as Chytraeus's own chronicle on a separate, later Brahe letter in the corpus that names Chytraeus as its author — a well-founded inference, not a documented fact for this specific letter — and the corpus also shows this text's incipit recurring in the printed Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia, so it is not an isolated scrap. But the seam between this enclosure and Tycho's own resumed first-person voice (the published tail beginning 'non intermittam') is not preserved, so it cannot be verified directly. Upgrade path: collation against Dreyer's Opera Omnia apparatus at that passage, and against Chytraeus's own Chronicon Saxoniae for a matching entry under 1600. Grade B.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages as page images (283-286 for the letter proper, 282/287 for boundary check). Collated the full Latin source_text against Dreyer's print sentence by sentence, including opening/closing formulas, the appended third-person biographical notice, and every proper name, date and number (Erik Sparre, Erik/Charles of Sweden, Johann Freder, Rudolph II, Frederick II, Heinrich Rantzau, Wandsbek/Wandesburgum, Benatica, dates 28 March 1600 x2, numbers 24/21/8-year Interregno/5 miles): source_text matches Dreyer's printed text word for word (long-s typography aside; no glued/split/dropped words). Checked published_english against the Latin clause by clause: every sentence has a faithful counterpart, no invented facts/names/numbers, no silently dropped sentences, no flipped negations or pronouns, no hardened hedges, and the letter's actual closing ('...vergant et stabiliantur') matches the English's actual closing with nothing added after it.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "David Chytraeus", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15981007001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15981007001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Returns a corrected sheet for inclusion in the recipient's annual histories, revising the account of being summoned to Bohemia and received by the Emperor.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000328002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000329001", "d": "1600-03-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed reverend friend] — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 29 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM</p><p>S. P.</p><p>REVERENDE et Praestantissime Vir, Amice honorande, quas cum filio meo huc revertente mihi rescripsisti literas, circa huius labentis Anni initia accepi, et pro Amicâ atque candidâ congratulatione de conditionibus, quas Deus Opt. Max. apud hunc Augustissimum Imperatorem mihi et meis studijs prospexit, proque benevolo tuo voto, quo precaris, ut omnia inposterum feliciter stabiliantur et adaugeantur, maximas quas possum tibi habeo gratias. Elucet hinc sincerus ille tuus affectus, quo me diu multumque prosecutus es; invenies me pari perpetuo erga te tuosque animo. Et certe sic est, uti scribis, agnosco Divini Numinis singularem providentiam et benignitatem, quâ me a difficultatibus, quibus in Patriâ obnoxius fui, Clementer ereptum in portu tranquilliore hîc locavit. Et sane non possum satis depraedicare huius optimi Imperatoris non saltem Caesareum, sed et Paternum (si sic loqui liceret) erga me animum; qui vel inde perspicitur, quod licet ipsius Matas ob Epidemicos morbos, qui gravissime Bohemiam hoc antecedente Anno afflixerunt, Pragâ abfuerit, et in civitate Pilsna hinc 15 miliaribus distante sese continuerit, durante adhuc infectione illâ, atque in hoc quoque loco, quem inhabitandum mihi concessit, totâque viciniâ saeviente, tamen proprio motu de me et meis sollicita fuerit, adeo ut aliquoties huc, quomodo valeremus, expiscatum miserit, simul Clementissime offerendo, si in quamcunque aliam ipsius Matis Arcem, ubi tutius degere foret, secedere vellem, id mihi licere, liberum etiam relinquendo, si Viennam Austriae me conferre luberet, ubi tempestive de commodâ habitatione caeterisque necessarijs mihi prospicere vellet. Atqui ego, actis humilime gratijs, me hîc potius in statione meâ mansurum et Divinae protectioni ac voluntati omnia commissurum respondi. Agoque eidem Deo misericordi protectori toto pectore gratias, quod me totamque domum meam, in tantis periculis, quibus undique cingebamur, incolumes conservârit, oroque, ut inposterum suâ gratiâ nobis adesse dignetur.</p><p>Recte a te factum, quod Historiolam nostrae in Bohemiam migrationis, et eorum, quae postea mihi hîc evenerunt, quam OLIGERO ROSAeKRANTZIO, communi amico, ob eam quae nobis intercedit amicitiam intimam, ut de consanguinitate et affinitate nihil dicam, paulo luculentius retuleram, resignaveris atque perlegeris. Cumque inde statum mearum rerum sufficienter perceperis, in quo etiam Dei benignitate nunc persistit atque inposterum, eodem favente, plenius augeri poterit, non duco operaeprecium quidpiam de eodem hîc repetere. Expecto brevi supellectilem meam et Instrumenta Magdaeburgi relicta, tum quoque ea, quae Hamburgi sunt, quae per meos emissarios e Dania ante hyemem eo transferri curavi; scripsit enim Imperator ad utriusque Civitatis Magistratum, ut in sui gratiam illa omnia huc per Albim advehi faciant: cumque Magdaeburgenses Senatores subterfugia quaedam quaererent, ipsius Matas ad Capitulum eiusdem loci hoc nomine alias dedit literas, quod se lubentissime ipsius Caeae Matis voluntati obtemperaturum promisit; interea structurae pro Instrumentis hîc debito modo disponendis parantur.</p><p>Quod M. CHRISTIERNO LONGOMONTANO hortator fuisti, ut me accederet, idque ei persuasisti, pergratum mihi est; dabo operam, ut ipsum huius itineris non poeniteat, liberumque ei relinquam vel hîc apud me vel Pragae esse, quam diu voluerit.</p><p>De coeteris, quae scribis, bene inter nos convenit, prout aequum et oportunum fuit. Audio apud vos esse Suecum quendam Astronomicis studijs addictum, nomine ........ qui etiam Observationibus Coelestibus delectatur; si efficere poteris, ut is huc veniat, erit id mihi acceptum et ipsi non inutile; iuvabo eius conatus, quantum potero.</p><p>Audio Regnum Suecicum illud, continuis bellis et tumultuationibus iam diu conquassatum, in potestatem CAROLI Ducis venisse. Deus flectat eius cor, ut hac victoriâ et potentiâ moderate utatur, atque ijs, quos pro adversarijs habet, clementer parcat.</p><p>Nova, quae ex his oris communicem, vix aliqua, quae momentum habeant, in promptu sunt. Bellum Ungaricum magnâ vi continuatur et in futuram aestatem utrinque apparatum. Dicunt Turcam longe maiori exercitu adfuturum quam unquam antea; noster Imperator, licet a Principibus Imperij maiori ex parte nullas speret suppetias, quantacunque tamen poterit militari manu ei resistere eiusque vires et minas infringere, pro eo quem Deus largitus fuerit successu, constituit; ideoque equites et pedites varijs e locis conscribit, ut iusto exercitu tam atroci hosti obviare queat et suos defendere. Deus Opt. Max. omnia in bonum finem dirigat; cui etiam te tuosque commendo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 29 Martij Anni 1600 stylo novo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO AN ANONYMOUS [PERSON]</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Reverend and most excellent Man, friend to be honoured: the letter you wrote me back, with my son returning hither, I received about the beginning of this passing year; and for your friendly and candid <b>congratulation on the conditions which God Best and Greatest has provided for me and my studies with this most august Emperor</b>, and for your benevolent wish, by which you pray that all may henceforth be happily established and increased, I give you the greatest thanks I can. Hence shines forth that sincere affection of yours, with which you have long and greatly attended me; you will find me of like perpetual mind toward you and yours. And certainly it is so, as you write: I acknowledge the singular providence and kindness of the Divine Power, which clemently snatched me from the difficulties to which I was liable in my fatherland, and placed me here in a more tranquil harbour. And indeed I cannot sufficiently proclaim this best Emperor's not only imperial, but also <b>paternal</b> (if it were permitted so to speak) disposition toward me — seen even from this, that although His Majesty, on account of the <b>epidemic diseases</b> which most gravely afflicted Bohemia this past year, was absent from Prague and kept himself in the city of <b>Pilsen</b>, 15 miles distant (that infection still lasting, and raging in this place too, which he granted me to inhabit, and in the whole neighbourhood), yet <b>of his own motion he was solicitous about me and mine, several times sending hither to inquire how we fared</b> — at the same time most clemently offering that, if I wished to withdraw to any other castle of His Majesty's, where it were safer to live, it should be permitted me, and <b>leaving it free, too, if I should please, to betake myself to Vienna of Austria</b>, where he would in time provide me a convenient dwelling and the other necessaries. But I, having most humbly given thanks, answered that <b>I would rather remain here in my station, and commit everything to Divine protection and will.</b> And I give thanks, with my whole heart, to the same merciful God my protector, that he preserved me and my whole house unharmed amid such great dangers, by which we were surrounded on all sides; and I pray that he deign henceforth to be present to us by his grace.</p><p>Rightly was it done by you, that you <b>unsealed and read through the little history of our migration into Bohemia</b>, and of what afterward befell me here, which I had related a little more fully to <b>Holger Rosenkrantz, our common friend</b>, on account of the intimate friendship between us (to say nothing of kinship and relationship). And since from it you have sufficiently perceived the state of my affairs — in which, by God's kindness, it now persists, and henceforth can be more fully increased — I think it not worthwhile to repeat any of it here. I expect soon my furniture and the <b>Instruments left at Magdeburg</b>, and also those at <b>Hamburg</b>, which I had transferred thither from Denmark by my emissaries before winter; for the Emperor wrote to the Magistrate of both cities that, for his sake, they should have all conveyed hither by the <b>Elbe</b>; and when the <b>Magdeburg Senators sought certain subterfuges</b>, His Majesty on that account gave another letter to the <b>Chapter</b> of the same place, which promised it would most gladly comply with the Imperial Majesty's will. Meanwhile structures are being prepared for arranging the Instruments here in due manner.</p><p>That you were an exhorter to Master <b>Christen Longomontanus</b>, that he should come to me, and persuaded him of it, is most welcome to me; I will take pains that he not regret this journey, and will leave it free to him to be either here with me or at Prague, as long as he wishes.</p><p>Concerning the rest you write, it is well agreed between us, as was fair and fitting. I hear that there is among you <b>a certain Swede devoted to astronomical studies, by name …… , who also delights in celestial observations</b>; if you can bring it about that he come hither, it will be welcome to me, and not useless to him — I will aid his endeavours as much as I can.</p><p>I hear that the Swedish kingdom — long shaken by continual wars and tumults — has come into the power of <b>Duke Charles</b>. May God bend his heart, that he use this victory and power moderately, and clemently spare those he holds as adversaries.</p><p>News from these parts worth weight is scarcely at hand. The <b>Hungarian war</b> is continued with great force, and preparation [is made] on both sides for the coming summer. They say the <b>Turk</b> will be present with a far greater army than ever before; our Emperor, though for the most part hoping no aid from the Princes of the Empire, has nonetheless resolved to resist him with whatever military force he can, and to break his strength and threats, according to the success God shall grant; and therefore he levies horse and foot from various places, that with a proper army he may meet so atrocious an enemy and defend his own. May God Best and Greatest direct all to a good end; to whom I also commend you and yours.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 29th day of March, the year 1600, New Style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient (unnamed; flagged).</b> Headed <i>Ad Anonymum</i> — addressed to a <b>\"Reverend and most excellent\" friend</b> (a cleric or learned divine) who: had congratulated Tycho on his imperial conditions; is a <b>\"common friend\" of Tycho and Holger Rosenkrantz</b>, to whom Holger had shown Tycho's migration-narrative; had <b>urged Longomontanus to come to Tycho</b>; and lives near \"a Swede devoted to astronomy.\" On the evidence, an unidentified <b>north-German / Rostock-area reverend</b> of Tycho's circle (parallel in content to the Chytraeus reply, brahe-t_16000328002, but a distinct person). Not identified — left so per the no-fabrication rule.</li><li><b>Significance.</b> A counterpart of the same-period letters on Tycho's imperial establishment:</li><li><b>The Emperor's \"paternal\" care.</b> As in the family letters (brahe-t_16000321004, brahe-t_16000321002) and the Coraduz letter (brahe-t_16000308001), Tycho dwells on Rudolf II's <b>personal solicitude during the plague</b> — repeated inquiries after his safety, and the <b>standing offer to relocate him to any safer castle, or to Vienna</b> — which Tycho declined to \"remain in my station.\"</li><li><b>The migration-narrative.</b> The recipient had <b>read the autobiographical <i>Historiola</i> of Tycho's migration into Bohemia</b> that Tycho sent <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (the same narrative Chytraeus and Sturtz also sought; cf. brahe-t_16010415002, brahe-t_16000328002).</li><li><b>The instrument-transport.</b> The <b>Magdeburg/Hamburg</b> saga: the Emperor's letters to both cities to ship the instruments up the <b>Elbe</b>; the <b>Magdeburg Senate's \"subterfuges\"</b> answered by the Emperor's further letter to the <b>Cathedral Chapter</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000404001, brahe-t_16000321005).</li><li><b>Longomontanus</b> — the recipient had persuaded him to come to Tycho; Tycho will make him welcome.</li><li><b>Recruiting a Swedish observer.</b> Tycho asks the recipient to send to him <b>a Swede skilled in astronomy and observation</b> (name a <b>lacuna</b> in the witness) — a glimpse of Tycho recruiting observational staff for Bohemia.</li><li><b>News:</b> Charles of Sweden's conquest (with the hope he spare his adversaries; cf. brahe-t_16000328002, brahe-t_15991203001); the Hungarian war and the Turk's great army; the Emperor levying troops despite the princes' withheld aid.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> the unnamed reverend (recipient); <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Christen Longomontanus</b>; the unnamed <b>Swedish astronomer</b>; <b>Charles of Sweden</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger).</li><li><b>Textual flags.</b> The <b>recipient's name</b> is lost (heading <i>Ad Anonymum</i>), and the <b>Swede's name</b> is a <b>lacuna</b> in the source (<i>nomine ……</i>); both left unfilled. <b>Calendar (protocol §3):</b> dateline <i>Die 29 Martij Anni 1600 stylo novo</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored 'hinc 15 miliaribus distante' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.287) where our transcription had dropped the numeral; the translator's bracketed '[some]' is now replaced by the recovered figure. Restored 'Turcam' for the non-word 'Turam' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.288); the English 'Turk' was already correct and needed no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (thanks for the congratulation on the imperial conditions; the Emperor's \"paternal\" plague-time care and the declined Vienna offer; the migration-narrative read via Holger Rosenkrantz; the Magdeburg/Hamburg instrument-transport with the Senate's subterfuges and the Chapter's compliance; the welcome to Longomontanus; the bid to recruit a Swedish observer; the Swedish and Hungarian news) is clear and corpus-corroborated. The two genuine gaps — the <b>unnamed recipient</b> and the <b>lacuna for the Swede's name</b> — are left unfilled per the no-fabrication rule, not conjectured.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VIII pp.286-288, read as page images) against source_text. Every name (Rosaekrantzio/Rosenkrantz, Longomontanus, Duke Charles/Caroli), place (Magdeburg, Hamburg, Elbe), sum, and the closing date '29 Martij Anni 1600' match Dreyer exactly. Two single-word corruptions in the body Latin relative to Dreyer (a dropped number, a dropped letter); the English translation itself is accurate and not further defective.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our Latin drops the number '15' (miles) present in Dreyer (TBOO VIII p.287); the translator correctly flagged the gap with an editorial bracket rather than inventing a figure, but the source_text itself is deficient relative to Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>et in civitate Pilfna hinc 15 miliaribus diftante fefe continuerit</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin prints 'Turam' (not a Latin word) where Dreyer prints 'Turcam' (TBOO VIII p.288); a dropped letter. The translator silently supplied the evident correct reading, so the English is unaffected, but the source_text is corrupted. Dreyer prints: <i>Dicunt Turcam longe maiori exercitu adfuturum quam unquam antea</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both TEXT_ERROR defects directly against the Dreyer page images: p.287 line 7 confirms '15 miliaribus'; p.288 line 12 confirms 'Turcam'. Fixed the Latin in both places and updated the one English clause the missing numeral had left hedged with an editorial bracket. Nothing else in the letter was touched. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000308002", "next": "brahe-t_16000329002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks an anonymous correspondent for congratulations on his imperial appointment and reflects on divine providence in securing a tranquil harbour after difficulties in his homeland.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000329001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000329002", "d": "1600-03-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed Livonian correspondent] — Benátky, 29 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM</p><p>S. P.</p><p>ATTULIT literas tuas, Nobilis et Eruditissime Vir, filius meus natu maior, in principio Ianuarij superioris huc rediens, ex quibus caussas diutini tui silentij et intermissionis literarum, ob absentiam in Livoniâ, cognovi, quare te eo libentius excusatum habeo, nihil interim de tuâ constantiâ et semel incepto erga me amore addubitans; invenies et me eundem TYCHONEM meique semper similem in te redamando. Utinam tu sedes Paternas, quas iure merito affectas, aeque feliciter recuperare possis, et tranquillum invenire portum, atque mihi et meis studijs, Divinâ Clementiâ, hîc apud Augustissimum Imperatorem pro his primordijs benignissime provisum est, spero in posterum, eodem Numine favente, adhuc uberiora. Si qua in re apud Magnates hîc in Aula tibi aliquando usui esse potero, non deerit tui honorifica mentio secundum nostram in te propensionem. Filius tuus, ubi accesserit, erit mihi acceptus; optarem et te Patrem, si fieri posset, hîc semel videre.</p><p>Occupatio Regni Suetici, quo CAROLUM nunc potiri audio, meâ quoque opinione maiores turbas dabit, modo SIGISMUNDO non defuerint vires. Non minor est virtus, quam quaerere, parta tueri. Deus aliquando pacem Regionibus illis, diu tempestate bellorum et aliarum calamitatum conquassatis, largiatur. Ubi occasio huc mittendi tibi data fuerit, non intermittas, quaeso, tam de publicis, quae in istis oris geruntur, quam privatis tuis rebus, scribendi officium.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 29 Martij styl. nov. Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to an unnamed [correspondent].</b></p><p>Hearty greeting.</p><p>My elder-born son brought your letter, noble and most learned man, when he returned hither at the beginning of last January; from which I learned the causes of your long silence and interruption of letters — namely, your absence in Livonia. Wherefore I hold you the more willingly excused, doubting nothing meanwhile of your constancy and of the love once begun toward me; you will find me, too, the same Tycho, and ever like myself, in loving you in return. Would that you might as happily recover the paternal seats which you rightly and deservedly strive for, and find a tranquil harbour, as it has been most kindly provided — by Divine Clemency — for me and my studies, here with the most august Emperor, for these beginnings; I hope, hereafter, the same Deity favouring, [for things] yet more abundant. If in any matter I can sometime be of use to you among the Magnates here at court, an honourable mention of you will not be wanting, in accordance with our inclination toward you. Your son, when he comes, will be welcome to me; I would wish to see you, the father, here too, once, if it could be done.</p><p>The occupation of the Kingdom of Sweden, which I hear Charles now holds, will, in my opinion too, give greater disturbances — provided strength has not failed Sigismund. It is no less a virtue to guard what has been gained than to seek it. May God at length grant peace to those regions, long shaken by the tempest of wars and other calamities. When occasion of sending hither is given you, do not, I pray, omit the duty of writing — both about public matters that are done in those parts, and about your private affairs.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 29th day of March, new style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient — unnamed.</b> The dataset titles it <i>ad anonymum</i>. The internal evidence points to a <b>Baltic / Livonian nobleman</b>: he had been \"absent <b>in Livonia</b>,\" is striving to \"recover his paternal seats\" (<i>sedes paternas … recuperare</i>), and is asked to report \"public matters done in those parts\" — i.e. the theatre of the <b>Polish–Swedish war</b> over Livonia and the Swedish crown. <i>Identification flagged</i>: no secure name. (He is plainly a person of standing, with a son who would call on Tycho.)</li><li><b><i>filius meus natu maior</b></i> = <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>, who returned to his father at Benátky at the start of January 1600 (cf. brahe-t_16000329003, …0414002), carrying the correspondent's letter.</li><li><b>The Swedish succession war.</b> <i>Occupatio Regni Suetici, quo CAROLUM nunc potiri audio … modo SIGISMUNDO non defuerint vires</i> — Tycho's political comment on the <b>deposition of Sigismund III Vasa</b> (King of Poland and, until 1599, of Sweden) and the seizure of Sweden by his uncle <b>Duke Charles of Södermanland</b> (the future <b>Charles IX</b>). Tycho foresees \"greater disturbances … provided Sigismund's strength has not failed,\" and moralises with the tag <i>parta tueri</i> — \"to guard what has been won [is no lesser virtue than to win it]\" (after Ovid, <i>Ars amatoria</i> II.13, <i>nec minor est virtus quam quaerere parta tueri</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 29 Martij styl. nov. 1600</i> = <b>29 March 1600, New Style</b> (<i>styl. nov.</i> explicit); Bohemia, imperial. (The dataset label gives only \"Benatky\"; the body supplies the date — the same day as brahe-t_16000329003.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substance is clear (the son as courier; Tycho's new imperial footing; the Ovidian moral; the Swedish war of Charles vs Sigismund, datable to 1599–1600). Grade [B] because the <b>recipient is unidentified</b> — placed, on the internal Livonian/paternal-estates/Swedish-war evidence, as a Baltic noble, but not named; this is flagged, not invented.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.287-290 page images. Located the letter beginning mid-p.288 ('TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM. S.P. ATTULIT literas tuas...') and ending on p.289 with the date. Collated the political content (Carolus/Charles occupying Sweden, Sigismundo/Sigismund) and the date (29 Martij styl. nov. Anni 1600) against the page image; source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word throughout. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000329001", "next": "brahe-t_16000515001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Excuses a Livonian correspondent's long silence, offers intercession at the imperial court on his behalf, and welcomes his son's future visit.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000329002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000329003", "d": "1600-03-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christophorus Henniochus", "ck": "Christophorus Henniochus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christophorus Henniochus — Benátky, 29 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTOPHORUM HENNIOCHUM</p><p>S.</p><p>REDDIDIT mihi literas tuas sub initium huius Anni ad me reversus filius TYCHO; estque mihi gratum, conditiones meas, quas Divino provisu hîc in Bohemia apud Caesam Matem adeptus sum, tibi probari. De Chymico illo in Silesia, cuius meministi, qui Metamorphosin istam Paracelsicam in libro vexationum indicatam callet, per Dominum Doctorem THADDAeUM HAGGAeCIUM nuper inde Pragam redeuntem inquiri faciam; et si quid certi ab eo rescivero, te eius reddam participem.</p><p>FRANCISCUS SCRÖDERUS Sundensis ne verbulum mihi, sive Wittebergae sive hîc degenti, respondit, aut si id fecit, interciderunt eius literae: admoneas eum iterum sui officij, si forte resipiscere voluerit, quamvis non multum intersit, sive scribat sive non.</p><p>Recordor te semel mihi scripsisse de Granatis Bohemicis, quod ex ijs Chymicum quoddam artificium elicere nôsti; cuperem igitur ex te scire, quale illud sit; reperiuntur enim hîc Granati in satis magnâ copiâ, et tibi, si volueris, aliquos per oblatam oportunitatem mittam.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 29 Martij stylo renovato Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Christopher Henniochus.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>My son Tycho, returned to me about the beginning of this year, delivered your letter to me; and it is pleasing to me that my circumstances — which by Divine provision I have obtained here in Bohemia with His Imperial Majesty — are approved by you. About that Chemist in Silesia whom you mention, who is skilled in that Paracelsian <i>Metamorphosis</i> indicated in the <i>Book of Vexations</i>, I will have inquiry made through Master Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius, lately returning thence to Prague; and if I learn anything certain from him, I will make you a sharer of it.</p><p>Franciscus Schröder of Stralsund has not answered me a single little word, whether I was staying at Wittenberg or here; or, if he did, his letter went astray: admonish him again of his duty, if perchance he be willing to come to his senses — although it matters little whether he write or not.</p><p>I recall that you once wrote to me about Bohemian Garnets, that you know how to elicit a certain chemical artifice from them; I would therefore wish to know from you what that is. For garnets are found here in fairly great abundance, and I will send you some, if you wish, through an offered opportunity.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 29th day of March, renewed style [New Style], in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christopher Henniochus</b> (<i>Henniochus</i> — a Latinised surname, likely <i>Hennioch/Henning</i>; not securely re-identified), a correspondent of Tycho's with chemical/alchemical interests. <i>Identification flagged.</i></li><li><b>Tycho's son.</b> <i>filius TYCHO</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b> (b. 1581), who had returned to his father at Benátky early in 1600, carrying Henniochus's letter.</li><li><b>Tycho's imperial position.</b> <i>conditiones meas … apud Caesaream Maiestatem</i> — the post he had secured under <b>Rudolf II</b>; Henniochus had congratulated him.</li><li><b>Alchemy.</b> <i>Chymicus ille in Silesia … Metamorphosin Paracelsicam in libro vexationum indicatam callet</i> — an unnamed <b>Silesian alchemist</b> skilled in the \"Paracelsian <i>Metamorphosis</i>\" set out in the <i>Liber vexationum</i> (the [pseudo-]Paracelsian alchemical <i>Liber vexationum / De vexationibus alchimistarum</i>). Tycho — himself a practising alchemist — will inquire via <b>Thaddaeus Hagecius</b> (Hájek), just back at Prague. <i>Granati Bohemici</i> = <b>Bohemian garnets</b> (pyrope), abundant in Bohemia, from which Henniochus claimed to draw \"a certain chemical artifice\"; Tycho offers to send specimens. (Reinforces the Tychonic alchemy/<i>pyronomia</i> thread; cf. brahe-t_15860301001.)</li><li><b><i>Franciscus Scröderus Sundensis</b></i> = <b>Franz Schröder of Stralsund</b> (<i>Sundensis</i> = of Stralsund, the Hanseatic <i>Sundum</i>), a delinquent correspondent.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Die 29 Martij stylo renovato 1600</i> = <b>29 March 1600, New Style</b> (<i>stylo renovato</i> explicit); from the Imperial castle of Benátky. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the substantive references (Tycho's son; the imperial post; the Silesian alchemist and the <i>Liber vexationum</i>; Hagecius; Bohemian garnets; Schröder of Stralsund) are clear and corpus-consistent. Grade [B] because the recipient <i>Henniochus</i> is not firmly re-identified and the <i>Liber vexationum</i>/\"Paracelsian Metamorphosis\" reference, while plausibly the pseudo-Paracelsian tract, is flagged rather than asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.288-290 as page images; the letter 'Tycho Brahe ad Christophorum Henniochum' is entire on p.289 (bottom half, following the anonymous letter and preceding 'Tycho Brahe ad Martinum Braschium'). Latin source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word including the Silesian chemist / Thaddaeus Hagecius passage, Franciscus Schröderus of Stralsund, the Bohemian garnets, and the dateline '29 Martij stylo renovato Anni 1600'. English translation checked clause-by-clause: faithful throughout. The self-assigned grade B was overcautious; full collation found no defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Christophorus Henniochus", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991219001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991219001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Promises to inquire about a Paracelsian chemist in Silesia through Hagecius, and asks the recipient to describe his chemical artifice for extracting something from Bohemian garnets.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000329003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000329004", "d": "1600-03-29", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Martin Brasch — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 29 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MARTINUM BRASCHIUM</p><p>S.</p><p>ACCEPTISSIMAe mihi fuerunt tuae literae, Eruditissime et amantissime Domine Magister MARTINE BRASCHI, quas circa initia huius Anni filius meus e Daniâ rediens inter alias ab Amicis Rostochiensibus profectas attulit: ac pro benevolâ et candidâ congratulatione, quam de successu rerum nostrarum apud Augustissimum Imperatorem RUDOLPHUM Secundum, Dominum nostrum Clementissimum, a principio refers, tibi peramanter gratias habeo.</p><p>Probâsti etiam tuum erga me sincerum affectum carmine illo Elegiaco unâ transmisso, ubi Uraniam Imperatorem meâ de caussâ adloquentem eique gratias agentem introducis. Adiungam quidem id coeteris tuis mecum antea communicatis, quae a multis probantur, sed cum in hoc non ita selectis per omnia utaris verbis, et tali concinnitate, quam requirit hoc genus carminis, ad imitationem venae Ovidianae aut aliorum Poëtarum, qui in hac facilitate excelluerunt, adeo ut respectu antecedentium carminum te ipso (liceat enim mihi quod censeo ingenue profiteri) minor videaris: et sint praeterea in ipsâ Historicâ relatione, quam pressius sequi voluisti, nonnulla, quae nimis diffusa, quoad particularia, nec satis decore prolata existimem; idcirco Elegiam hanc superioribus versibus, Heroicis et Lyricis, necdum adiunxi aut alijs monstrare lubuit. Sed si tuâ bonâ veniâ id fieri poterit, uti fieri posse mihi polliceor, quaedam prius in eadem recognoscam et paulo aliter mutabo, quaedam vero prorsus omittam, prout in coeteris quoque hanc mihi libertatem sumpsi, quae etiam tibi non displicuit; nemini enim res meae rectius perspectae esse poterint quam mihi ipsi. Perplacet alias inventio et ductus carminis, sed bonus interdum dormitat HOMERUS. Vides, quantâ erga te utor libertate et confidentiâ: ubi vero aliquale mihi supererit otium, remittam carmen illud, iuxta meam intentionem paulo aliter formatum; vel saltem loca indicabo, quae a te ipso alterari vellem. Satius itaque erit, ut interea tibi ipsi hoc reservans, nemini exemplum impertiaris, multo minus ut publici iuris facias.</p><p>Excellentissimus ille MELISSUS necdum mihi quidpiam misit, spero tamen ipsum, pro eâ quâ praeditus est humanitate et erga me affectione, id officij mihi non denegaturum. Si Caesae Mati scriptum aliquod, uti innuis, aliquando humillime dedicare constituisti, de quo meum exquiris consilium, cuperem prius scire, quale id esset, et quâ de re, ac Exemplum, si fieri possit, introspicere, tum demum, an gratum sit futurum, partim ex aliorum, qui ipsius Mati ab intimis sunt, partim meâ ipsius aestimatione, coniecturam faciam, tibique id, quicquid erit, solitâ ingenuitate pandam.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur properanter ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 29 Martij Anno 1600 stylo novo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MARTIN BRASCH</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Most welcome to me were your letters, most learned and most loving Lord Master Martin Brasch, which about the beginning of this year my son, returning from Denmark, brought among others sent by [my] friends at Rostock; and for the kindly and candid congratulation which from the first you offer on the success of our affairs with the most august Emperor Rudolf II, our most clement Lord, I give you most affectionate thanks.</p><p>You have proved your sincere affection toward me, too, by that Elegiac poem sent along [with the letter], in which you bring on Urania addressing the Emperor on my behalf and rendering him thanks. I will indeed add it to your other [poems] earlier shared with me, which are approved by many; but since in this one you do not throughout use such choice words, and such polish as this kind of poem requires — in imitation of the Ovidian vein, or of the other Poets who have excelled in this fluency — so much so that, set beside your preceding poems, you seem (let me be allowed to declare frankly what I judge) lesser than your own self; and since, besides, there are in the Historical narration itself, which you chose to follow rather closely, some things which I judge too diffuse in their particulars, and not set forth becomingly enough — for that reason I have not yet joined this Elegy to the earlier verses, the Heroic and the Lyric, nor cared to show it to others. But if it may be done with your good leave — as I promise myself it may — I will first revise certain things in it, and alter them a little otherwise, and some I will leave out entirely, just as in the others too I took this liberty for myself, which did not displease you either; for to no one can my own affairs be more rightly known than to myself. Otherwise the invention and the conduct of the poem please me well — but good Homer sometimes nods. You see with how great freedom and confidence I deal with you; and when some leisure is left to me, I will send that poem back, shaped a little otherwise according to my intention, or at least I will mark the passages I should wish you yourself to alter. It will therefore be better that, keeping this to yourself meanwhile, you impart a copy to no one — much less make it public.</p><p>That most excellent Melissus has not yet sent me anything; yet I hope that he, by that humanity with which he is endowed and his affection toward me, will not deny me that service. If you have resolved, as you hint, to dedicate some writing at some time, most humbly, to His Imperial Majesty — concerning which you ask my counsel — I should wish first to know what it would be, and on what subject, and, if it could be done, to look over a copy; then at last I shall make a conjecture whether it will be welcome, partly from [the judgement] of others who are among His Majesty's intimates, partly from my own estimation, and will lay it open to you, whatever it be, with my wonted candour.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given in haste from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 29th day of March, in the year 1600, New Style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Martin Brasch</b> (<i>Martinus Braschius</i>, 1565–1601), Rostock humanist and <i>Poeta laureatus</i> — the same poet named in Paul Melissus's letter to Tycho of 5 March 1600 (brahe-t_16000305001) among those who had prompted verses <i>in Tychen tuam</i>. Tycho is on the most familiar terms with the Rostock literary circle (<i>Amici Rostochienses</i>), which routed letters to him via his son's return from Denmark \"about the beginning of this year.\"</li><li><b>The occasion — a verse-portrait of Tycho's imperial fortune.</b> Brasch had sent an <b>Elegiac poem</b> in which <b>Urania</b> (the Muse of astronomy) addresses <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> on Tycho's behalf and thanks him — a poetic celebration of Tycho's new imperial patronage. It was meant to join Tycho's growing collection of friends' commendatory verses (the <i>Heroic</i> and <i>Lyric</i> pieces already gathered).</li><li><b>Tycho as candid literary critic (the heart of the letter).</b> Unusually, Tycho returns a frank, unflattering critique: the diction is not uniformly <i>selecta</i> (choice), the <i>concinnitas</i> (polished arrangement) falls short of what elegiac verse demands \"in imitation of the Ovidian vein,\" and the closely-followed <i>Historica relatio</i> (the narrative of events) is in places too diffuse and \"not becomingly enough set forth\" — so that Brasch here seems <i>te ipso minor</i>, \"lesser than his own self.\" Tycho therefore withholds the poem from the collection and from other eyes, proposes to <b>revise it himself</b> (as he had done, with Brasch's prior consent, to the others — \"for to no one can my affairs be more rightly known than to myself\"), and counsels Brasch <b>not to circulate or publish</b> it meanwhile. The whole is softened by the Horatian tag.</li><li><b>Classical tag.</b> <i>bonus interdum dormitat Homerus</i> — <b>Horace</b>, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 359 (<i>quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus</i>, \"sometimes even good Homer nods\"): the polite formula for a fine poet's lapse. Kept as the idiom and identified.</li><li><b>Cross-links &amp; persons named.</b></li><li><i>Melissus</i> = <b>Paul Melissus (Schede)</b> (1539–1602), the Heidelberg poet, <i>Comes Palatinus</i> and laureate. Tycho notes Melissus \"has not yet sent me anything\" — i.e. as of 29 March 1600 the verses that Melissus's letter of <b>5 March 1600</b> (brahe-t_16000305001) says he was enclosing had not yet reached Benátky. The two letters thus bracket the same in-transit packet of poems.</li><li><i>Rudolf II</i> (<i>Augustissimus Imperator Rudolphus Secundus</i>); Tycho's son (the one returning from Denmark, unnamed here).</li><li><b>A possible dedication to the Emperor.</b> Brasch had hinted (<i>innuis</i>) at dedicating some writing to Rudolf II and sought Tycho's advice; Tycho, well placed at court, offers to gauge its likely reception \"partly from His Majesty's intimates, partly from my own estimation\" before Brasch commits — a glimpse of how dedications to the Emperor were vetted through courtier-intermediaries.</li><li><b>Place / calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Arx Caesarea Benatica</i> = the imperial castle of <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b>; dateline explicitly <i>stylo novo</i> — <b>New Style</b>, per Tycho's imperial-service usage. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — polished humanist Latin, read securely; the literary occasion (Brasch's Urania-and-Rudolf elegy, Tycho's critique and proposed self-revision, the suppression-pending-revision counsel), the Horatian tag, and the cross-links (the Rostock circle, Melissus's still-undelivered verses, the mooted imperial dedication) are all firmly construed. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (salutation, all three paragraphs, valediction, date) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 290-291 via the page images; the source_text matches Dreyer exactly (long-s normalized). Checked every proper name (Martine Braschi, Rudolphum Secundum, Melissus), the Homer allusion, and the closing date (29 March 1600 stylo novo) against the printed page. The translation renders every clause faithfully with no fabrication, omission, hedge-loss, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000915001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000920002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Brasch for congratulations on his imperial appointment and for an elegiac poem featuring Urania, then criticises the poem's polish as inferior to his earlier verse.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000329004/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000331001", "d": "1600-03-31", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Bording — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 31 March 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 95 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD JACOBUM BORDINGUM].</p><p>Magnifice et excellentissime vir, amice inprimis honorande.</p><p>JNTELLEXI ex literis, quas per filium meum, circa initia hujus anni huc ad me revertentem, mihi scripsisti, posteriores meas ad jllustrissimum principem ducem Vldaricum, d:num meum clementissimum, tum quoque simul ad te datas, recte esse redditas, et de conditionibus, quas divino provisu apud sa:am caes:am ma:tem, dominum nostrum clementissimum, adeptus sum, ipsius jll:mam cel:nem clementer et lubenti animo accepisse, atque tibi etiam hunc rerum mearum statum auditu fuisse perjucundum, quo nomine jll:mae ejus cel:ni humillime gratias ago, et tibi quoque quam plurimas. Si quid postea mihi rescriptum est, quod futurum innuisti, quandoquidem tunc ob occupationum molem et filii festinum isthinc discessum fieri id nequibat, scias me eas literas non accepisse, uti etiam nullas a d:no Henrico Ramelio, affine et amico meo singulari, qui ad te suas missurum, cum mei in Dania emissarii essent, aiebat.</p><p>Coeterum quia tempus iam adest, quo interesse (uti vocant) pro decem millibus joachymicis, quos isthic jll:mis junioribus principibus mutuo dedi, numerandum sit, rogo peramanter, velis efficere, ut summam illam, quae sexcentos adaequat, actutum obtineam. Et quia difficulter, nec etiam satis tuto in moneta argentea sive thaleris huc adferri poterit, optarem, ut in aureis Vngaricis solverentur, ita ut quilibet Vngaricus loco sesquialterius thaleri numeretur, sicque quadringenti tales aurei sexcentis illis joachymicis aequivalerent; talis enim est hujus monetae aureae in Dania valor, ita ut apud vos nihil aut admodum parum esse discriminis existimem. Poterint autem aurei illi commode pyxidi alicui includi et obsignari, sicque aurigae Wittebergensi, ut eos bene conservet atque m. Joestelio mathematico isthic professori, quamprimum Wittebergam venerit, tradat; is enim affinem quendam Dresdae habet, qui postea totum illud ad me facile transmittet. Si Vngarici isthic tam cito in promptu non sint, poterint eorum loco mitti engelotti, quorum quilibet duobus joachymicis cum quadrante aequiparatur. Roseinobiles, uti etiam floreni Rhenani hic parum in usu sunt, ideoque eos nollem. Si nulla alia ratio supererit, istam monetam auream loco joachymicorum mihi debitorum exponendi, spero ill:mum principem ducem Vldaricum, pro ea qua ipsius cel:do erga me est clementissima affectione, facile sua authoritate et consilio effecturum (praesertim si ipsius cel:ni abs te hoc negotium meo nomine humillime expositum commendetur), ut desideriis meis hac in parte subveniatur. Si autem vobis aliqua alia commoditas perspecta esset, qua pecuniam illam in joachymicis jmperialibus hic certo et absque meo damno accipere queam, mihi id perinde erit, lubensque acquiescam. Chirographum interesse illud persolutum testans hisce inclusi, quod tuae fidei committo. Neque enim dubito te, quem semper expertus sum mei studiosissimum, hanc curam, utut alias sis occupatissimus, non detrectaturum, quo absque diutina dilatione id, quicquid est, pecuniarum hic nancisci queam; sic me adhuc pluribus quam antea officiis, quibuscunque per me licuerit modis, vicissim compensandis, tibi reddideris quam devinctissimum.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex arce caesarea Benatica die ultimo martii stylo novo anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO JACOB BORDING].</b></p><p>Magnificent and most excellent man, friend especially to be honoured.</p><p>I have understood, from the letter you wrote me by my son, returning hither to me about the beginning of this year, that my later [letters] to the most illustrious prince Duke Ulrich, my most clement lord, and at the same time to you, were duly delivered; and that, concerning the conditions which by divine provision I have obtained with the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement lord, his most illustrious Highness received [the news] graciously and with willing mind, and that to you too this state of my affairs was most pleasing to hear — on which account I give most humble thanks to his most illustrious Highness, and very many to you also. If anything was afterward written back to me — which you hinted would happen, though it could not then be done, because of the mass of occupations and my son's hasty departure from there — know that I have not received those letters; just as [I have received] none from Lord Henrik Ramel, my kinsman and especial friend, who said he would send his to you when my emissaries were in Denmark.</p><p>For the rest: since the time is now at hand when the interest (as they call it) for the <b>ten thousand joachimsthalers</b> which I lent there to the most illustrious younger princes is to be paid, I most lovingly ask that you would bring it about that I obtain at once that sum, which amounts to <b>six hundred</b> [thalers]. And because it can be brought hither with difficulty, and not safely enough, in silver money or thalers, I should wish that it be paid in <b>Hungarian gold [ducats]</b>, such that each Hungarian [ducat] be reckoned in place of <b>one and a half thalers</b>, and so <b>four hundred</b> such gold pieces would equal those six hundred joachimsthalers — for such is the value of this gold money in Denmark, so that I think there is no difference, or very little, with you. Those gold pieces can conveniently be enclosed in some little box and sealed, and [given] thus to the <b>Wittenberg coachman</b>, that he keep them well and hand them to <b>Master Jöstel</b>, the mathematician [and] professor there, as soon as he comes to Wittenberg; for he has a certain kinsman at <b>Dresden</b>, who will then easily transmit the whole to me. If Hungarian [ducats] are not so quickly to be had there, <b>engelots</b> can be sent in their place, each of which is reckoned at two joachimsthalers and a quarter. <b>Rose-nobles</b>, like <b>Rhenish florins</b>, are little in use here, and therefore I should not want them. If no other way shall remain of laying out that gold money in place of the joachimsthalers owed me, I hope the most illustrious prince Duke Ulrich, by that most clement affection his Highness has toward me, will easily bring it about by his authority and counsel (especially if this business be humbly laid before and commended to his Highness by you in my name) that my wishes in this part be met. But if some other convenience be known to you, by which I may receive that money here in imperial joachimsthalers, surely and without my loss, it will be all the same to me, and I will gladly acquiesce. The receipt testifying that interest paid I have enclosed herewith, which I commit to your good faith. For I do not doubt that you — whom I have always found most devoted to me — will not refuse this care, however otherwise most occupied you be, so that without long delay I may obtain here that money, whatever it is; thus you will have bound me most closely to you, [the obligation] to be repaid in turn by more services than before, in whatever ways I shall be able.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the last day of March, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacob Bording</b>, a leading man of <b>Lübeck</b> and Tycho's agent there (cf. the later Tycho→Bording letter, brahe-t_16010412001). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.</li><li><b>Position in the Mecklenburg debt-thread.</b> This <b>31 March 1600</b> letter is the <b>earliest of the surviving Bording letters</b> and an early node of the whole 10,000-thaler affair (cf. brahe-t_16001115002 of 15 Nov 1600; the 1601 letters to Meier and Duke Ulrich). It first reports the <b>good news of Tycho's imperial conditions</b> (received gladly by Duke Ulrich and Bording — Tycho's letters had been delivered \"by my son\" returning early in 1600), then turns to the recurring task: collecting the <b>600-thaler semi-annual interest</b> on the <b>10,000 joachimsthalers</b> lent to the <i>\"younger princes of Mecklenburg.\"</i></li><li><b>The numismatic detail (notable for monetary history).</b> Tycho specifies <i>how</i> he wants the 600 thalers paid, to ease safe transport to Bohemia:</li><li><b>Preferred: Hungarian gold ducats</b> (<i>aurei Ungarici</i>) at <b>1½ thalers each</b>, so <b>400 ducats = 600 joachimsthalers</b> (\"such is their value in Denmark\").</li><li><b>Route:</b> sealed in a little box, given to the <b>Wittenberg coachman</b>, handed to <b>Master Jöstel</b> (Melchior Jöstel, the Wittenberg mathematician; cf. brahe-t_16001008001), whose <b>Dresden kinsman</b> forwards the whole to Tycho.</li><li><b>Fallback: <i>engelotti</i> (English \"angelots\"/angels)</b> at <b>2¼ thalers each</b>; but <b>rose-nobles</b> and <b>Rhenish florins</b> \"are little in use here,\" so he refuses them.</li><li>Failing all, <b>Duke Ulrich's</b> authority is to be invoked (via Bording), or the money taken \"in imperial joachimsthalers\" if safer. A receipt (<i>chirographum</i>) for the interest is enclosed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Bording; <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b>; the <b>\"younger princes\"</b> (the late Duke Johann's sons; see brahe-t_16000920002); <b>Henrik Ramel</b> (<i>Henricus Ramelius</i>, Tycho's <i>affinis</i> and friend, a Pomeranian/Danish noble); <b>Melchior Jöstel</b>; Tycho's son (Tycho the Younger); <b>Rudolf II</b> (the imperial conditions).</li><li><b>Coin glossary (kept/glossed; §2).</b> <i>aurei Ungarici</i> = <b>Hungarian gold ducats</b>; <i>joachymici</i> = <b>joachimsthalers</b> (the Joachimsthal silver thaler); <i>engelotti</i> = <b>angelots / English angels</b> (gold); <i>roseinobiles</i> = <b>rose-nobles</b> (English gold); <i>floreni Rhenani</i> = <b>Rhenish florins/gulden</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die ultimo Martii stylo novo anni 1600</i> — <b>New Style</b> (Prague/imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the (abbreviated) Latin is read securely; the substance (acknowledgement of the imperial conditions welcomed by Duke Ulrich and Bording; the request to collect the 600-thaler interest on the 10,000-joachimsthaler loan; and the detailed coin-and-route instructions — Hungarian ducats at 1½ thalers, via the Wittenberg coachman and Jöstel's Dresden kinsman, with engelots as fallback and rose-nobles/Rhenish florins refused) is explicit and dovetails with the debt-thread. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.192-195 as page images (letter no. 189, [TYCHO BRAHE AD JACOBUM BORDINGUM], spanning pp.193-194). Collated the Latin source_text against the printed page including all sums and exchange rates (10,000 joachimsthalers; 600 thalers interest; 400 Hungarian ducats at 1.5 thalers each; engelots at 2.25 joachimsthalers), all names and places (Duke Ulrich, Henrik Ramel, Wittenberg, Jöstel, Dresden, Benátky), and the closing Vale. Dabantur ex arce caesarea Benatica die ultimo martii stylo novo anni 1600 — all match exactly (only &amp; vs et typographic variance, which is not a substantive difference). English translation checked clause by clause; the currency-conversion arithmetic is internally consistent and faithfully rendered. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 95 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15970802001", "next": "brahe-t_16000916001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15970802001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000919001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Bording's report that Duke Ulrich received news of his imperial position graciously, and notes that subsequent letters from Bording and Henrik Ramel have not arrived.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000331001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000400001", "d": "1600-04-00", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe — Benátky [nad Jizerou], April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703 (autograph)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>SONS manus, quae nuper ad laedendum celeritate ventos antevertit, quodnam initium faciat ad leniendum, vix invenit. Nam quid ego primum commemorem? An meam intemperantiam? Cuius acerbissima est recordatio. An tua, Nobilissime TYCHO, benefacta? Quae iam nec enumerari possunt, nec pro dignitate perpendi. Per duos menses gratuito me victu liberaliter aluisti; toti tuae familiae meam incolumitatem, mea desideria commendasti: omnia humanitatis officia praestitisti: quae habuisti charissima, mihi communicasti: nemo me dicto vel facto volens laesit unquam: in summa, non liberis, non uxori, non ipsi tibi plus indulsisti quam mihi. Quae animo praeterea volvebas, de me promovendo, ex ijs, quae mea causa suscepisti, facile vidi. Quae omnia, cum ita habere sciverim, fassus fuerim, scripto etiam testata reliquerim: magnâ cum animi consternatione recordor, adeo me a Deo et a Spiritu Sancto meis intemperijs animique morbo fuisse permissum, ut ad tot et tanta beneficia, clausis oculis, pro modestia, trium hebdomadum continuae morositati erga totam familiam, pro gratiarum actione, iracundiae praecipiti, pro reverentiâ, audaciae summae erga tuam personam, tam illustri stemmate, tam praestanti eruditione, tanta nominis claritate venerabilem, pro amica valedictione, suspiciosissimis imputationibus et acerbissimae scribendi prurigini tantum indulgere potuerim, nec me respexerim, nec quam crudeliter tuum animum, qui praeclare semper de me senseras, hac odiosissimâ ratione sim offensurus, perpenderim. Quae si hostili animo, si nocendi proposito, suscepissem: equidem vix vel a Deo petere fuissem ausus, uti te mihi praestaret placabilem, iustamque et legitimam vindictam impediret.</p><p>Sed quia totum hoc impotentis animi furor et cholerae praestitit exuberantia, et iuvenile vitium, curiositas, cum praecipitantiâ iudicij (abfuit vero diffamandi animus) et quia non experieris, me unquam cum quoquam mortalium ita de te locutum, uti ad te unum ex falsâ imaginatione scripsi (quemadmodum neque in quemquam unquam talia designavi) ex hac qualicunque consolatione post gravissimam poenitudinem, animum nonnihil recepi: et quia quae scripsi, coram Deo solo teste, M.T. imputata fuere; primum Deum Opt. Max. veniam exoravi, deinde et pro Christianâ reconciliatione summe fui sollicitus. Quod nisi me afflicta valetudo, immoderatae affectionis haud dubia stimulatrix, impedivisset, primo quoque tempore Benathkam excurrissem, meque M.T. ad omnem satisfactionem promptum stitissem. Iam quia fors ita tulit, ut prior Μ. T. Pragam veniret, supplex ad Μ. T. venio, per Dei misericordiam orans, uti mihi hanc atrocissimam offensionem Christiano animo remittat. Ego contra, quae dixi vel scripsi in Μ. T. personam, famam, honorem et doctrinam, vel quocunque alio modo, iniuriosissima (si nulla valeat in meliorem partem interpretatio) uti (quod ex animo doleo) dixi scripsique plurima et plura quam meminerim: omnia et singula revoco, irrita, falsa et probatu impossibilia sponte et ingenue agnosco atque pronuncio. Neque enim ullum facinus patratum, aut ullam partem honestatis a Μ. T. unquam violatam vel vidi vel audivi. Si quid suspicionibus etiam indulsi, id omne, ut Christianum decet, ex animo eijciam, sic ut protestationi Christianae acquiescens, temeritatem in suspicando libenter Deo καρδιογνώστῃ fatear.</p><p>Promitto etiam bona fide, me in posterum, ubicunque versaturus sim, non tantum huiusmodi malesanis actibus, dictis, factis et literis abstenturum, sed neque ullo pacto Μ. T. iniuste et ex proposito offensurum, neque quicquam, quod non deceat, contra Μ. T. moliturum. Oro autem, quia lubrica est vita hominis, ut si M.T. animadvertat me imprudenter in tale quid vergere, me mei ipsius admoneat: experietur enim me morigerum. Quin imo polliceor, daturum me operam, quod quidem nunquam adhuc mentis compos intermisi, ut omni genere officiorum Μ. T. ulterius devinciam, et quantum mihi possibile est, superiorumque meorum patitur authoritas, in omnibus honestis rebus, quasque Μ. T. gratas intellexero, obsequium praestem, et sic ipso facto demonstrem, aliter me in M.T. personam esse et semper fuisse affectum, quam ex illa trium septimanarum intemperata dispositione animi et corporis colligi poterat. Et precor, ut ad id praestandum me Deus adiuvet.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES KEPLER TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p><b>The guilty hand, which lately in its speed to wound outran the winds, scarcely finds what beginning to make toward soothing.</b> For what shall I first call to mind? My intemperance? — the recollection of which is most bitter. Or your benefactions, most noble Tycho? — which now can neither be enumerated, nor weighed according to their worth. <b>For two months you fed me liberally with free board</b>; you commended my safety, my wishes, to your whole household; you performed all the offices of humanity; what you held most dear, you shared with me; no one ever willingly injured me in word or deed; in sum, <b>you indulged not your children, not your wife, not your own self more than me</b>. What besides you were turning over in your mind, about advancing me, I easily saw from the things you undertook for my sake. All which — since I knew it to be so, confessed it, and even left it attested in writing — I recall with great consternation of mind: <b>that I was so far given over by God and by the Holy Spirit, through my intemperance and my mind's sickness, that to so many and so great benefits, with closed eyes, I could indulge — instead of modesty, three weeks' continuous moroseness toward your whole household; instead of thanksgiving, headlong wrath; instead of reverence, the utmost audacity toward your person</b> (venerable for so illustrious a lineage, so excellent an erudition, so great a renown of name); <b>instead of a friendly farewell, most suspicious imputations and a most bitter itch of writing</b> — nor did I look to myself, nor weigh how cruelly I was about to offend your mind (you who had always thought excellently of me) by this most hateful manner. Which, had I undertaken it with a hostile mind, with a purpose of harming, I should indeed scarcely have dared even to ask of God that he render you placable to me, and hinder a just and lawful vengeance.</p><p>But since the whole of this was wrought by <b>the fury of an uncontrolled mind and an exuberance of choler, and a youthful fault — curiosity — with precipitancy of judgement</b> (but the intent to defame was absent); and since you will not find that I ever spoke of you to any mortal as I wrote, to you alone, out of a false imagination (just as I never plotted such things against anyone): from this consolation, such as it is, after the gravest repentance, I have somewhat recovered my spirit. And since what I wrote — God alone being witness — were charged to Your Magnificence: first I begged pardon of God Best and Greatest, then I was supremely solicitous for a <b>Christian reconciliation</b>. And had not my afflicted health (the undoubted stimulant of immoderate emotion) hindered me, I should at the first opportunity have run over to <b>Benátky</b>, and presented myself to Your Magnificence ready for every satisfaction. Now, since chance has so brought it that Your Magnificence came first to Prague, <b>I come a suppliant to Your Magnificence, praying by God's mercy that you would, with a Christian mind, forgive me this most atrocious offence.</b> I, in turn — [as for] what I said or wrote against Your Magnificence's person, fame, honour, and learning, or in any other way most injurious (if no interpretation toward the better part avails), as (which I grieve from my heart) I said and wrote very many things, and more than I remember — <b>all and each I retract, and of my own accord and frankly acknowledge and declare void, false, and impossible to prove</b>. For I have neither seen nor heard any crime committed, or any part of honourableness ever violated, by Your Magnificence. If I also indulged in any suspicions, I will cast all that out of my mind, as becomes a Christian — so that, acquiescing in a Christian protestation, I freely confess my rashness in suspecting to <b>God, the Knower of hearts</b>.</p><p>I also promise, in good faith, that henceforth, wherever I shall be, I will not only abstain from such unsound acts, words, deeds, and letters, but neither in any way unjustly and deliberately offend Your Magnificence, nor contrive anything unbecoming against Your Magnificence. But I pray, since the life of man is slippery, that <b>if Your Magnificence notice me imprudently inclining toward any such thing, you would admonish me of it: for you will find me compliant.</b> Nay rather, I promise to give effort — which indeed I never yet, when in my right mind, omitted — to bind Your Magnificence further by every kind of service, and, as far as is possible for me and the authority of my superiors allows, to render obedience in all honourable matters, and such as I shall understand to be welcome to Your Magnificence; and so to demonstrate, by the very deed, that I am, and always have been, otherwise disposed toward Your Magnificence's person than could be gathered from that three weeks' intemperate disposition of mind and body. And I pray that God may help me to perform this.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Significance — one of the most famous letters in the history of science.</b> This is <b>Johannes Kepler's autograph letter of apology and submission to Tycho Brahe</b> (April 1600), written after the <b>violent first quarrel</b> that nearly ended their association before it began. During Kepler's first weeks at Benátky (early 1600), a dispute over the conditions of his work and access to Tycho's observations erupted into a furious outburst; Kepler left and sent Tycho intemperate, accusatory letters, then — overcome with remorse — wrote this abject retraction. <b>The reconciliation it effected made possible the collaboration on which Kepler's <i>Astronomia nova</i> (and his laws of planetary motion) rests.</b> The MS is Kepler's <b>autograph</b> in the Vienna ÖNB (cod. Vindob. lat. 10703).<br>- <b>Structure of the apology.</b><br>  1. <b>The opening figure</b> — <i>\"the guilty hand, which lately in its speed to wound outran the winds\"</i> — and the antithesis between his \"intemperance\" and Tycho's \"benefactions.\"<br>  2. <b>The catalogue of Tycho's kindnesses</b> — <b>two months' free board</b>; commending Kepler's safety to the whole household; sharing \"what he held most dear\"; \"you indulged not your children, your wife, your own self more than me\" — and his plans \"about advancing me.\"<br>  3. <b>The self-accusation</b> — that, \"given over by God and the Holy Spirit through my intemperance and my mind's sickness,\" he repaid these with <b>\"three weeks' continuous moroseness … headlong wrath … the utmost audacity toward your person … most suspicious imputations and a most bitter itch of writing.\"</b><br>  4. <b>The mitigation</b> — it was <i>\"the fury of an uncontrolled mind, an exuberance of choler, a youthful fault, curiosity, with precipitancy of judgement\"</i>, <b>\"but the intent to defame was absent\"</b>; he never spoke ill of Tycho to others, only wrote to Tycho \"out of a false imagination.\"<br>  5. <b>The retraction and submission</b> — he comes \"a suppliant,\" begs a <b>\"Christian reconciliation,\"</b> and <b>\"retracts all and each\"</b> of his injurious words, \"void, false, and impossible to prove,\" confessing his rashness \"to God, the Knower of hearts.\"<br>  6. <b>The promise of future obedience</b> — to abstain, to be admonished if he errs (\"you will find me compliant\"), and to bind Tycho \"by every kind of service.\"<br>- <b>Greek (protocol §4.2).</b> <i>Deo καρδιογνώστῃ</i> — \"to God, <b>the Knower of hearts</b>\" (<i>kardiognōstēs</i>, <b>Acts 1:24 / 15:8</b>); kept verbatim, translated.<br>- <b>Persons &amp; place.</b> <b>Johannes Kepler</b> (sender); <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (<i>M. T.</i> = <i>Magnificentia Tua</i>); Tycho's wife and children (the <i>familia</i>); <b>Benátky</b> (the scene of the quarrel; Kepler now at Prague — <i>\"Your Magnificence came first to Prague\"</i>).<br>- <b>Calendar / date (protocol §3).</b> The dataset and <i>Opera Omnia</i> place it in <b>April 1600</b> (New Style); the <b>day is not given</b> in the source (Kepler's autograph) and is left unspecified in the header (historically dated to early April 1600). Marked [N.S.].</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — eloquent, fully construable Latin (from Kepler's autograph), read securely; the substance — Kepler's catalogue of Tycho's kindnesses, his self-accusation over the \"three weeks' intemperance,\" the mitigation (choler, youth, curiosity, \"no intent to defame\"), the full retraction \"void, false, and impossible to prove,\" the suppliant plea for Christian reconciliation, and the promise of future obedience — is rendered faithfully and at full weight for this landmark document. The Greek (<i>kardiognōstēs</i>, Acts) is identified. The only openness is the <b>undated day</b> (April 1600), left unspecified per the source rather than conjectured.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Kepler-to-Tycho confession letter (source_text and published_english) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 305 (bottom, letter opening) through 307 (top, letter close, before the following letter to Rudolph II begins), read as page images. Verified sentence-by-sentence: opening and closing lines, every proper name (M.T. = Magnificentia Tua/Your Magnificence, Benathka/Benatky, the Greek kardiognostei), and the full run of confessional clauses. One apparent discrepancy (source_text's 'praestitit' before 'exuberantia' seemingly missing from Dreyer) was resolved on a closer single-page re-read: Dreyer prints it hyphenated across the line break ('praeftitit ex-/uberantia'), so source_text matches Dreyer exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found. Published grade A confirmed by direct Dreyer collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703. Autograf"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16000126002", "next": "brahe-t_16000405002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000126002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000600001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologises for his recent intemperance during his stay at Benátky, recounting Tycho's liberal hospitality and shared observations, and expressing remorse for his conduct.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000400001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000401001", "d": "1600-04-01", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Cramer", "ck": "Daniel Cramer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Daniel Cramer — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 1 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD DANIELEM CRAMERUM</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et Praestantissime Vir, Amice plurimi mihi semper faciende: Literae tuae, pari eruditione et benevolâ erga me affectione affatim certantes, quas circa initia Decembris superioris Anni ad me dedisti, paulo post huius labentis primordia mihi redditae sunt, unâ cum ijs, quas communis noster Amicus, Dominus Doctor LUCAS BACKMESTERUS, per filium natu maiorem, quem in Daniam ablegaram pro auferendis residuis Instrumentis, huc redeuntem miserat. Fuerunt eae mihi admodum gratae, inprimis vero Oda illa ad Caesam Matem, elegans sane et arguta, et quae vel sola lauream te mereri testari poterit, pro quâ etiam ab ipsius Mate obtinendâ ego meo calculo tibi non sum defuturus, arrepta occasione idoneâ. Ipsius Matas per semestre Pragae non fuit, sed in urbe Pilsna, 10 milliaribus inde remotâ, ob luem Epidemicam, quae totam Bohemiam superiore Anno graviter afflixit, commorata est; interea paucissimis patuit ad ipsius Matem accessus; speramus eam brevi Pragam reversuram.</p><p>Gratissimum insuper mihi est, quod insolentem istum Scotulum redarguere eiusque inverecundam arrogantiam compescere non detrectes: dignum se reddidit, cui vaniloquum et in alios scurriliter mordax obtruderetur os. Adfuit mihi Uraniburgi in mensa Dominus Doctor NICOLAUS KRAGIUS, Professor Haffniacus et Historicus Regius, cum DUNCANO tunc simul praesenti meas Hypotheses aperirem; et licet idem KRAGIUS non libenter omnia, quae hac de re comperta habet, fateatur, quod Scotis, qui de ipso bene meriti sunt, contradicere nolit (nam Rex Scotiae, cum ibi Legatum ageret, eum nobilitavit et insigni aureâ torque donavit) attamen in literis, quas pridie Nonarum Decembris Anni superioris ad me dedit, haec verba suapte manu adscripsit: »DUNCANUS certe quicquid tandem privatim egerit, publice nunquam eam (loquitur de Hypothesi nostra) sibi arrogavit, qui ne per somnium quidem de eâ cogitavit, antequam ex te hanc tanquam mysterium cognosceret. Quod simul ac patefecisses, memini a te dictum, plus ei communicatum quam frater fratri edisserere fortean vellet. Atque reperi in paucis hisce diebus inter schedas meas chartam, quâ mihi petenti eam signâsti; sive ante DUNCANI adventum, sive quo tempore apud te ille incertus sum.« Haec ille nolens volens fatetur; quo Anno id fuerit, ex literis ipsius DUNCANI ad me, postquam Rostochium redierat, quibus Hypothesin illam meam sibi apprime placere scribit, eamque Ptolemaicae et Coperniceae praefert, quarum te exemplum habere existimo, facile colliges. Est mihi ipsius DUNCANI autographum ad manus, quod inficiari non potest, etiamsi in mille se verteret formas; et quantum subsidij habeat ex meis, de quibus tam frivole gloriatur, literis, vel lippis oculis loci peristasin consideranti discernere facillimum. Rem apprime gratam nec indecentem praestiteris, si quae contra eum veritatis asserendae caussâ meditatus es, compleveris, mihique, prius quam publicentur, transmiseris, ut si quae praeterea eo facientia ad manus habeam, annotem, tibique mox restituam.</p><p>Existimo te vidisse scurrile quoddam et supra modum calumnijs atque convitijs scatens, omninoque famosum scriptum cuiusdam efferi Dithmarsi, Ursi potius quam hominis (uti sese vocat) contra quod, si per otium, quae restitutionis loco tibi in mentem venerint, consignare non degravatus fueris, quae ego postea locupletabo, cum nihil sit in eo, quod non minimo negotio refelli queat, etiam ubi invictis Geometriae demonstrationibus triumphum agere praesumit, singulare mihi praestiteris officium, et maiori quam antea beneficio me tibi devinxeris, meque vicissim tui nominis atque honoris studiosum et gratum experiere. Ego contra tam rabiosum allatratorem quidpiam sub meo nomine, dilutionis loco, edere indecorum existimo, sed potius legibus istud contra Imperij constitutiones inverecundum facinus castigari suo tempore permittam. Hactenus rem omnem dissimulavi, partim propter aulam longe dissitam et pro maiori parte distractam, partim quod gravioribus occupationibus distinerer; sed quod differtur, non aufertur. Feceris OLIGERO nostro rem inprimis acceptam, si et hac in parte desiderijs nostris morem gesseris: dici etenim vix potest, quam moleste is tulerit, cum primum illi de putido illo libro innotuit; et fustibus ac plagis excipiendum authorem censuit, sed praestat legibus. Verum desino te serioribus occupatum talibus nugis remorari: extorsit ista mea erga te confidentia sincerusque et mutuus amor. De me vero vicissim tibi quidvis audacter pollicearis. Vale. Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ pridie Paschatis Anno 1600, qui Romae habetur Iubilaeus.</p><p>Quae in Oda tua per se satis concinne elaborata paulo aliter legi malui, in subiuncta charta notavi, quod licet tuâ bonâ veniâ admitti posse mihi persuadeam, tamen indicandum duxi, quo conscius esses.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO DANIEL CRAMER</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and excellent Man, a friend always to be highly valued by me: your letter — abundantly vying in equal erudition and benevolent affection toward me — which you sent me about the beginning of December last year, was delivered me a little after the beginning of this [year], together with those our common friend Lord Doctor <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> had sent through my elder son (whom I had dispatched into Denmark to bring away the remaining Instruments), returning hither. They were very welcome — but especially that <b>Ode to the Imperial Majesty</b>, elegant and pointed, which even alone could attest you deserve the laurel; for the obtaining of which from His Majesty I too will not fail you with my vote, a fit occasion seized. His Majesty was not at Prague for half a year, but stayed at <b>Pilsen</b>, 10 miles distant, on account of the epidemic plague which gravely afflicted all Bohemia last year; meanwhile access to His Majesty lay open to very few; we hope he will soon return to Prague.</p><p>It is most welcome to me, besides, that you do not decline to <b>refute that insolent little Scot and curb his shameless arrogance</b>: he has made himself deserving that a vain-speaking, scurrilously biting muzzle be thrust upon him. Lord Doctor <b>Nicolaus Krag</b>, Professor at Copenhagen and Royal Historian, was present with me at table at <b>Uraniborg, when I opened my Hypotheses to Duncan, then also present</b>; and although Krag does not willingly confess all he knows of this (because he will not contradict the Scots, who have deserved well of him — for the King of Scotland, when Krag was Legate there, ennobled him and gave him a distinguished gold chain), yet in the letter he sent me on <b>4 December last year</b> he added these words in his own hand: <i>\"<b>Duncan certainly — whatever he may at last have done privately — never publicly arrogated it [he speaks of our Hypothesis] to himself, who did not even in a dream think of it before he learned it from you as a mystery.</b> As soon as you had revealed it, I remember it was said by you that more was communicated to him than a brother would perhaps wish to expound to a brother. And I found, in these few days, among my papers, a sheet on which you marked it for me at my request — whether before Duncan's arrival, or while he was with you, I am uncertain.\"</i> These things he confesses, willing or not. In what year it was, you will easily gather from Duncan's own letter to me (after he had returned to Rostock), in which he writes that <b>that Hypothesis of mine pleased him exceedingly, and prefers it to the Ptolemaic and Copernican</b> — a copy of which I think you have. I have Duncan's own autograph at hand, which he cannot deny, even if he turn himself into a thousand shapes; and how much support he has from my letters — about which he so frivolously boasts — it is most easy to discern, even with bleary eyes, for one considering the circumstances. You will do a thing exceedingly welcome and not unbecoming, if you complete what you have meditated against him for the sake of asserting the truth, and <b>transmit it to me before it is published</b>, that, if I have anything else conducing to it, I may annotate and soon return it.</p><p>I think you have seen a certain scurrilous writing, teeming beyond measure with calumnies and insults, altogether infamous, <b>of a certain savage Dithmarsian — a Bear [Ursus] rather than a man (as he calls himself)</b> — against which, if at leisure you would set down what comes to your mind by way of refutation (which I will afterward enrich) — since there is nothing in it that cannot be refuted with the least trouble, even where he presumes to triumph with \"invincible demonstrations of Geometry\" — you will do me a singular service, bind me by a greater benefit than before, and find me in turn zealous and grateful for your name and honour. <b>For myself, I think it unbecoming to publish anything under my own name, by way of refutation, against so rabid a barker — but I will rather let that shameless crime against the Empire's constitutions be punished by the laws in its time.</b> Hitherto I have dissembled the whole matter, partly because of the court being far off and mostly scattered, partly because I was held by weightier occupations; but <b>what is deferred is not taken away.</b> You will do our <b>Holger</b> a thing exceedingly welcome, if in this part too you comply with our wishes: for it can scarcely be said how ill he took it when that putrid book first became known to him — <b>he judged the author should be received with cudgels and blows, but it is better [left] to the laws.</b> But I cease to delay you, occupied with more serious things, with such trifles: my confidence toward you, and our sincere and mutual love, extorted these. Of me, in turn, promise yourself anything boldly. Farewell. Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the day before Easter, the year 1600, which at Rome is held as the Jubilee.</p><p>What in your Ode — in itself quite elegantly wrought — I preferred to read a little otherwise, I have noted on the appended sheet; which, though I persuade myself it can be admitted with your good leave, I thought should be indicated, that you might be aware.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Daniel Cramer</b> (1568–1637), the Pomeranian (Stettin) Lutheran theologian, poet, and polymath — here a literary and polemical ally of Tycho's, who had sent an <b>Ode to Rudolf II</b> (Tycho promises to help him to the imperial <b>laurel</b>) and is willing to write <b>refutations</b> on Tycho's behalf.<br>- <b>Significance — a principal document of Tycho's priority/plagiarism disputes (with the <i>verbatim</i> Krag testimony).</b> Three intertwined controversies:<br>  1. <b>John Craig (\"the insolent little Scot\").</b> Tycho welcomes Cramer's refutation of <b>Craig</b> (the <i>Scotulus</i>; cf. the cometary <i>Capnurania</i> dispute, brahe-t_15990321001, brahe-t_15981231002).<br>  2. <b>The Duncan affair (priority of the Tychonic hypothesis).</b> Tycho recounts that, <b>at his table at Uraniborg, he revealed his world-system hypotheses to \"Duncan\"</b> (a Scot of Craig's circle), in the presence of <b>Niels Krag</b> (<i>Nicolaus Cragius</i>, Copenhagen professor and Royal Historian). Krag — reluctant to testify against the Scots, who had honoured him (<b>ennobled and gold-chained by James VI of Scotland</b> when Krag was Danish legate) — nonetheless wrote Tycho (<b>4 Dec 1599</b>) the <b>verbatim words</b> quoted here: that <b>\"Duncan … never publicly arrogated the Hypothesis to himself, who did not even in a dream think of it before he learned it from you as a mystery,\"</b> and that Krag still has <b>the sheet on which Tycho diagrammed it for him</b>. Tycho adds that he holds <b>Duncan's own autograph letter</b> preferring the Tychonic hypothesis \"to the Ptolemaic and Copernican\" — documentary proof of priority.<br>  3. <b>Ursus.</b> The \"scurrilous, infamous writing of a certain savage <b>Dithmarsian — a <i>Bear</i> (<i>Ursus</i>) rather than a man</b>\" — i.e. <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär's <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</b></i> (Prague 1597; cf. brahe-t_16000328001, brahe-t_15990321001). Tycho will <b>not answer under his own name</b> \"so rabid a barker,\" but <b>pursue it by law</b> (\"that shameless crime against the Empire's constitutions … punished by the laws\"); he enlists Cramer for a refutation, to vet before publication. <b>Holger Rosenkrantz's fury</b> at the book (\"he judged the author should be received with cudgels and blows, but it is better [left] to the laws\") is reported.<br>- <b>Notable phrases.</b> <i>quod differtur, non aufertur</i> — \"what is deferred is not taken away\"; <i>Ursi potius quam hominis (uti sese vocat)</i> — the standing pun on <b>Ursus = Bear</b>; <i>vel lippis oculis</i> — \"even with bleary eyes\" (the obvious). The closing notes that Tycho <b>emended Cramer's Ode</b> (as he did Brasch's, Fischer's verses).<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Daniel Cramer; <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>; <b>John Craig</b> (the <i>Scotulus</i>); <b>Duncan</b> (the Scot who learned the hypothesis); <b>Niels Krag</b> (<i>Nicolaus Cragius</i>, quoted; ennobled by James VI); <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>pridie Paschalis Anno 1600, qui Romae habetur Iubilaeus</i> — \"the day before Easter 1600, which at Rome is the <b>Jubilee</b>\" = <b>1 April 1600 [New Style]</b> (Gregorian Easter 2 April 1600). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin closing date 'pridie Paschalis' corrected to 'pridie Paschatis' (Dreyer TBOO VIII p.292, where the word is itself marked with an editorial obelus in Dreyer's own apparatus); no change to the English, which already read correctly.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — eloquent, fully construable Latin, read securely; the substance (the Cramer ode and laurel; the <b>Duncan priority case with Niels Krag's verbatim hand-written testimony</b> that Duncan learned the Tychonic hypothesis \"as a mystery\"; the Craig and Ursus refutations enlisted from Cramer; Tycho's resolve to pursue Ursus by law not by his own pen; Holger Rosenkrantz's indignation) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated, with the Krag quotation rendered faithfully. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.291-292, read via page images covering the slice's pp.290-293) clause-by-clause against source_text and published_english: the Pilsen/plague passage, the Backmeister/Krag/Duncan priority narrative with its embedded quoted letter, the Ursus libel passage, the Oliger/Holger reaction, and the closing formula all collate correctly and the translation is faithful throughout. One TEXT_ERROR found in the closing date (Paschalis vs Dreyer's Paschatis, itself editorially flagged with a dagger in TBOO). Per the brief, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of impact.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin departs from Dreyer's printed word at the closing date; the English sense (\"day before Easter\") is unaffected either way, but the source_text itself is not what the edition of record prints. Dreyer prints: <i>Dabantur ex Arce Cæfareâ Benaticâ pridie † Pafchatis Anno 1600, qui Romæ habetur Iubilæus. (Dreyer prints \"Paschatis\", genitive of Pascha, flagged with an editorial obelus; our source_text prints the adjectival \"Paschalis\".)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.290-293 (page images, not OCR text layer). The single TEXT_ERROR is confirmed on p.292, last full paragraph: 'Dabantur ex Arce Cæfareâ Benaticâ pridie † Pafchatis Anno 1600, qui Romæ habetur Iubilæus' — Dreyer's own dagger marks this word as textually uncertain, but the reading itself is 'Paschatis', not 'Paschalis'. No other discrepancies found on collation of the full letter against the printed pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Daniel Cramer", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15990926001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980524001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Cramer for an ode to the Emperor and pledges to support his bid for the imperial laurel, noting the Emperor's absence from Prague due to plague in Bohemia.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000401001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000403001", "d": "1600-04-03", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 3 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>ACCIPIES, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium longe praestantissime, Catalogos ambos librorum, qui in his nundinis vernalibus Francofurti ad Moenum venales extiterunt. Si quos ex his petas, velim eos mihi designari, priusquam exemplaria divendantur, et curabo sedulo, ut quamprimum eos accipias. Mitto etiam tabulas ELISABETHAe Reginae Angliae, GEMMAM FRISIUM de radio Astronomico, et Astrologiam graecolatinam IOACHIMI CAMERARIJ No. 106. Tabulas perpetuas longitudinum et latitudinum Planetarum Lovanienses, et Theoricas Planetarum PURBACHIJ ab ORONTIO FINAeO emendatas No. 99. Atque hisce me tibi commendo, teque florere quam foelicissime exopto.</p><p>Datae Monachij tertia die Aprilis Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>You will receive, most noble man and by far the most outstanding of all mathematicians, both catalogues of the books which were on sale at this spring fair at Frankfurt am Main. If you should seek any from these, I would wish them designated to me before the copies are sold off, and I will diligently see to it that you receive them as soon as possible. I also send: the <i>Tables</i> of Elizabeth, Queen of England; <b>Gemma Frisius</b> <i>On the Astronomical Radius</i> [the cross-staff]; and the Greek-Latin <i>Astrology</i> of <b>Joachim Camerarius</b>, No. 106; the Louvain <i>Perpetual Tables</i> of the longitudes and latitudes of the Planets; and the <i>Theoricae Planetarum</i> of <b>Peurbach</b>, emended by <b>Orontius Finaeus</b>, No. 99. And with these I commend myself to you, and most heartily wish you to flourish most happily.</p><p>Given at Munich, the third day of April, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (see brahe-t_16000923001), Chancellor of Bavaria, forwarding both Frankfurt spring-fair (<i>nundinae vernales</i>) book-catalogues and a consignment of works from Tycho's desiderata list.</li><li><b>Books.</b> <i>Tabulae Elisabethae Reginae Angliae</i> — astronomical tables dedicated to / associated with <b>Queen Elizabeth I of England</b> (<i>identification flagged</i>: most plausibly a set of ephemerides or tables bearing the dedication; not securely pinned to one edition). <i>Gemma Frisius de radio Astronomico</i> = <b>Gemma Frisius</b>, <i>De radio astronomico et geometrico</i> (1545) — on the cross-staff (<i>radius astronomicus</i>, per glossary §5.2). <i>Astrologia graecolatina Ioachimi Camerarij</i> = the Greek-and-Latin astrological compilation of <b>Joachim Camerarius the Elder</b> (No. 106). <i>Tabulae perpetuae … Lovanienses</i> = the Louvain <i>Tabulae perpetuae</i> of planetary longitudes and latitudes. <i>Theoricae Planetarum Purbachij … ab Orontio Finaeo emendatae</i> = <b>Georg Peurbach</b>'s <i>Theoricae novae planetarum</i>, in the recension corrected by <b>Oronce Fine</b> (<i>Orontius Finaeus</i>) (No. 99).</li><li><b>The \"No.\" numbers</b> (106, 99) are entries on Tycho's standing book-want list (see the Munich series).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>; <i>tertia die Aprilis 1600</i>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and the bibliographic identifications are secure except for the <i>Tabulae Elisabethae Reginae Angliae</i>, whose exact edition is <b>flagged</b> as not firmly identifiable from the bare title; hence [B] rather than [A].</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.292-294 (page images). The Herwart letter is printed complete on p.293, matching source_text word-for-word. Checked both catalogue numbers (No. 106, No. 99) and all five named works/persons (Elisabetha Regina Angliae, Gemma Frisius, Ioachimus Camerarius, the Lovanienses tables, Purbachius/Orontius Finaeus) against the translation — all correctly rendered with no additions or drops. Closing date (3 April 1600) matches metadata.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_15991231001", "next": "brahe-t_16000411001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991231001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends Frankfurt spring fair book catalogues and several books, including works by Gemma Frisius, Camerarius, Peurbach emended by Orontius Finaeus, and the Tables of Queen Elizabeth of England.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000403001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000404001", "d": "1600-04-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "The chapter at the cathedral of Magdeburg", "ck": "The chapter at the cathedral of Magdeburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → The Chapter of Magdeburg Cathedral — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 4 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 97 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>REVERENDISSIMIS, NOBILISSIMIS, CLARISSIMISQUE VIRIS D:NIS DECANO, COeTERISQUE ECCLESIAe METROPOLITANAe IN ARCHIEPISCOPATU MAGDEBURGENSI CAPITULARIBUS ET DOMINIS ET AMICIS SUIS HONORANDISSIMIS.</p><p>Reverendissimi et nobilissimi viri d:i et amici honorandissimi.</p><p>CUM sa:a caes:a m:tas, dominus noster clementissimus, jam antea literas dederit ad amplissimum senatum Magdeburgensem, ut jnstrumenta mea astronomica atque supellectilem isthic repositam huc in Bohemiam, intermediante Albi fluvio, suis sumptibus in ipsius ma:tis gratiam transferri procurare velint, illj vero id ipsum suo quodam consilio renuere visi sint, utut bis ab ipsius caes:a ma:te hac de re clementissimis literis compellarentur, non igitur placuit ipsius ma:ti de his saepius ad illos scribere, sed potius ad vestrum reverendissimum capitulum literas hoc nomine dirigere, praesertim cum subintellexerit vestras reverend:mas dominationes ab hoc officio suae caes:ae ma:ti obsequiose et reverenter praestando minime alienas esse. Quare nunc ipsius ma:tis literas haec ipsa continentes ad reverend:mas vestras dominationes per ministrum meum mitto, officiosissime et reverenter simul rogans, ut, quemadmodum ipsius ma:tis clementissimae petitioni obsequentissimo animo obtemporaturas nihil ambigo, sic etiam effecturas ut omnes res meae isthic reservatae qua fieri potest maturatione, sartaetectae, advehantur, atque de iis, quae huc conducunt, provideatur. Accipiet illud ipsius ma:tas clementissima favorabili mente eritque simul, vti spero, reip. literariae utile, cujus promotionem vos non solum genere jllustres, sed et eximia eruditione conspicuos, exoptare et auxiliatrices manus praebere velle persuasum habeo; neque enim ea quae in sublimi illa et divina scientia astronomica integre restituenda plurimis abhinc annis indefesso labore sumptibusque non modicis molior et favente coelestium opifice, in augustissimi hujus imperatoris totiusque jmperii honorem nunquam intermoriturum, sedulo elaborare satago, privata sunt, aut ad me meosque solummodo pertinentia, sed ad universam literaturam eruditam ubivis locorum quoad sol et sidera durabunt (prout confido) conducentia. Jdeoque v:s d:es commune hic bonum lubenti animo adjuturas non est dubium, [omnino mihi polliceor]; quod equidem officium, quantum ad me attinet, etsi compensare vires non sufficiant, pro meo tamen modulo inveniar perpetuo erga reverendissimum vestrum capitulum hoc nomine gratus; sique nihil aliud referre vicissim potero, vestram tamen hanc praeclaram voluntatem apud alios extollere et depraedicare non desistam. Si quempiam ex vestris fidelibus ministris adjunxeritis, qui res meas huc comitetur et earum curam gerat, poteritis (modo vestris dominationibus ita placuerit) vina Bohemica, quae superiori aestate adeo generosa hic creverunt, ut plurimi antecedentes anni vix similia dederint, secum in aliqua copia iustoque precio recipere: quod ut commode fiat, vel si quid aliud ex his oris desideratis, quod isthic pluris aestimatur, dabo operam, ut quantum in me est vestrae voluntati hac in parte satisfiat, sicque sumptus erogati quodammodo mea opinione resarciri poterunt. Plura et particulariora clar:us et praest:mus vir d:nus Georgius Rollenhagius isthic scholae rector, singularis meus amicus, ad vestras do:nes, sicubi opus fuerit, referet.</p><p>Deus opt. max. vos omnes diu conservet florentes et incolumes.<br>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benatica die 4 aprilis stylo novo anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most reverend, most noble, and most renowned men, the Lords Dean and the other Capitulars of the Metropolitan Church in the Archbishopric of Magdeburg, his most honoured Lords and friends.</b></p><p>Most reverend and most noble men, Lords and most honoured friends.</p><p>Since the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement lord, has already before given a letter to the most ample <b>Senate of Magdeburg</b>, that they would procure my <b>astronomical instruments</b> and the furniture stored there to be transferred hither into Bohemia, by way of the river <b>Elbe</b>, at their own expense, for His Majesty's sake — but they seemed, by some counsel of their own, to refuse this very thing, though they were <b>twice</b> addressed by His Imperial Majesty on the matter with most clement letters — it therefore did not please His Majesty to write to them more often about it, but rather to direct letters on this account to <b>your most reverend Chapter</b>, especially since he understood that your most reverend Lordships are by no means averse from rendering this service obediently and reverently to His Imperial Majesty. Wherefore I now send His Majesty's letters containing these very things to your most reverend Lordships through my servant, at the same time most dutifully and reverently asking that — just as I doubt not you will obey His Majesty's most clement petition with most compliant mind — so also you will bring it about that all my things kept there be conveyed, with what haste is possible, safe and sound, and that provision be made for the things conducing thereto. His most clement Majesty will receive this with favourable mind; and it will at the same time, as I hope, be useful to the <b>Republic of Letters</b>, whose advancement I am persuaded you — illustrious not only by birth but also conspicuous for outstanding erudition — desire, and are willing to lend helping hands to. For those things which I labour at, in that sublime and divine astronomical science to be wholly restored — with untiring toil these many years past, and at no small expense, and (by the favour of the Maker of the heavens) earnestly strive to elaborate to the never-dying honour of this most august emperor and of the whole empire — are not private, or pertaining only to me and mine, but conduce to the whole learned world everywhere, as long as the sun and stars shall endure (as I trust). And therefore there is no doubt that your Lordships will gladly assist the common good here [I wholly promise myself]; which service, as far as concerns me, though my powers do not suffice to repay it, yet according to my measure I will be found perpetually grateful for, toward your most reverend Chapter; and if I can return nothing else in turn, yet I will not cease to extol and proclaim this your splendid goodwill among others. If you adjoin any of your faithful servants to accompany my things hither and take care of them, you will be able (if it so please your Lordships) to receive with him, in some quantity and at a just price, <b>Bohemian wines</b> — which grew so generous here last summer that very many preceding years scarcely gave the like; that this may be conveniently done, or if you desire anything else from these regions that is valued more highly there, I will take pains that, so far as in me lies, your wish be satisfied in this part, and so the expenses laid out can, in my opinion, in some way be recouped. More and more particular things the most renowned and excellent man Lord <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b>, the school rector there, my especial friend, will report to your Lordships, wherever there be need.</p><p>May God Best and Greatest long preserve you all, flourishing and unharmed.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 4th day of April, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipients.</b> The <b>Dean and Chapter (Capitulars) of Magdeburg Cathedral</b> (the Metropolitan Church of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — the Magdeburg leg of the instrument-transport saga.</b> A vivid document of the difficulty of moving Tycho's instruments to Bohemia. The imperial <b>Senate of Magdeburg had twice refused</b> Rudolf II's request (despite \"most clement letters\") to ship Tycho's <b>astronomical instruments and furniture</b>, stored at Magdeburg, up the <b>Elbe</b> at the city's expense. The Emperor therefore now appeals instead to the <b>Cathedral Chapter</b>; Tycho forwards the imperial letter (by his servant) and asks the Chapter to expedite the safe conveyance of all his goods. This complements the Hamburg-transit letter (brahe-t_15990929001) and the Wittenberg-college / Jöstel letters (brahe-t_16001009001, brahe-t_16001008001), which record the instruments coming from <b>Benátky and Magdeburg via Leitmeritz</b> up the Elbe to Prague.</li><li><b>The \"Republic of Letters\" argument.</b> Tycho frames the favour as serving not his private interest but the <i>respublica literaria</i> — his work of \"wholly restoring\" astronomy being \"not private … but conducing to the whole learned world … as long as the sun and stars shall endure,\" to \"the never-dying honour of this most august emperor and the whole empire.\" A characteristic statement of Tycho's public, imperial self-presentation.</li><li><b>The Bohemian-wine inducement (a concrete sweetener).</b> Tycho offers that, if the Chapter sends a servant to accompany the goods, that servant may take back <b>Bohemian wines</b> \"in some quantity and at a just price\" — the 1599 vintage having been exceptionally \"generous\" — or anything else from Bohemia valued at Magdeburg, \"so the expenses laid out can … be recouped.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> the Dean and Chapter of Magdeburg; <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector of the Magdeburg school (the <i>Altstädtisches Gymnasium</i>), poet and Tycho's \"especial friend\" and local intermediary (a frequent figure in the corpus, e.g. brahe-t_15880913001, brahe-t_16001009001); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the (refusing) Senate of Magdeburg.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 4 Aprilis stylo novo anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the Magdeburg Senate's twofold refusal; the Emperor's recourse to the Cathedral Chapter; Tycho's request to ship the instruments up the Elbe; the \"Republic of Letters\" framing; the Bohemian-wine inducement; Rollenhagen as intermediary) is clear and corpus-corroborated. The one bracketed phrase <i>[omnino mihi polliceor]</i> is the edition's supplement, reproduced as printed. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.194-196 as page images. The full letter to the Magdeburg Chapter (no. 190) is printed entirely on p.195 (p.194 carries the tail of the preceding letter, p.196 the start of the next). Collated the Latin source_text against the printed page word for word: the Senate of Magdeburg, the Elbe (Albi fluvio), the 'twice' (bis) addressed by the Emperor, the Republic of Letters (reip. literariae) passage, the Bohemian-wine offer, Georg Rollenhagen, and the closing Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benatica die 4 aprilis stylo novo anno 1600 all match exactly (only &amp; vs et typographic variance). The bracketed marginal insertion [omnino mihi polliceor] is confirmed by Dreyer's own apparatus note ('Hæc in margine inferta sunt') and is correctly bracketed in the English as '[I wholly promise myself]'. English translation checked clause by clause; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol 35, Fol. 97 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Requests the Chapter's assistance in transferring his astronomical instruments from Magdeburg to Bohemia via the Elbe, the Magdeburg Senate having twice refused the Emperor's letters on the matter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000404001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000405001", "d": "1600-04-05", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Georg Rollenhagen — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 5 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD GEORGIUM ROLLENHAGIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>QUAS die 23 Februarij huius Anni stylo vestro ad me dedisti literas, Eruditissime et amantissime Domine ROLLENHAGI, eae mihi redditae sunt, cum nuper Pragae essem, unâ cum alijs quibusdam in fine Augusti superioris Anni pariter a te mihi scriptis; aderant quoque aliae ad Doctorem RHODIUM, quas statim in ipsius hospitium misi, sed ipse peregre aberat; polliciti nihilominus sunt inquilini eas recte procuraturos. Ad priores mihi destinatas nihil nunc respondeo, siquidem earum praecipua contenta repetuntur in posterioribus, ad quas sic habe.</p><p>Civem illum Hamburgensem, quantum potui, per meos homines, qui tunc in Aulam abituri erant, iuvi, tam tuâ, quam Domini Doctoris VITI de caussa, sed lente isthic omnia, uti scis, et difficulter procedunt ob negotiorum multitudinem.</p><p>Pro supellectile meâ huc advehenda, non opus est, ut amplius sollicites apud Senatum vestrum. Cum enim Caesa Matas, Dominus noster Clementissimus, a Domino BARVITIO rescivisset (me tamen id non cupiente, sed aliquantulum adhuc temporis Magistratui vestro rem rectius dispiciendi, uti me facturum ad te scripsi, relinquente) Magdaeburgenses, quos bis per literas hac de re clementissime compellavit, officium illud detrectasse, invite id ipsum tulit, et statim ad Reverendum Capitulum vestrum de hoc eodem negotio perscribi iussit, quas literas nunc eo per quendam ex meis studiosis natione Danum mitto. Exemplum earum hîc adiunctum habes, nec dubito, quin Reverendum Capitulum, cui ego etiam scripsi, in gratiam Caesae Matis effecturum, ut Instrumenta mea et omnes res isthic relictae huc per Albim suis sumptibus, quamprimum fieri poterit, adferantur. Confido etiam illud de asseribus prospecturum, quibus ea omnia ab iniuria aëris et pluviarum muniantur, quos Nautae secum recipere poterint. Et si navem vinis Bohemicis, quae admodum generosa superiori anno creverunt, onerari voluerit, aut alijs mercibus, quae hîc minoris quam isthic venduntur, poterunt asseres illi ad hoc denuo usui esse; conduceret vero, ut fidus aliquis minister unâ mitteretur, qui ea, quae hîc coëmenda essent, procuraret, et in accessu curam mearum rerum simul gereret, pro qua inveniar erga eundem gratus. Quod si factum fuerit, non opus erit, ut studiosus hic meus huc cum supellectile illa, quam Magdaeburgi habeo, revertatur, sed is potius rectâ Hamburgum eat, ut pro reliquis Instrumentis inde huc pariter advehendis operam Hamburgensium sollicitet; sed tam haec quam alia tuo consilio disponenda relinquo. Et si conduxeris pro certo precio hominem, qui cum rebus meis huc accedat, atque earum inspector sit, modo Capitulum aliquem adiungere nolit, solvam ei quidquid promiseris. In caeteris Emissarium meum ita instrue, ut quid sibi faciendum vel omittendum sit, sciat, daque operam, ut omnia mature et diligenter executioni mandentur. Habebo te in omnibus indemnem, si quid pro me exposueris, vel fidem interposueris.</p><p>Polliceris in ultimis, te reliquis scriptorum meorum postulatis per primum quemque Tabellarium responsurum, quod ut brevi facias, rogo. ROTHMANNO quod ulterius scribam, non habeo, siquidem is meis benevole et humaniter ad ipsum datis quippiam respondere gravatus est, et videtur is ita morosus atque impatiens esse, ut vix seipsum ferre queat, necdum alios. Quae de me in suis ad te literis conqueritur, facile replicare possem, sed nolo cum homine, uti recte ais, impatientissimo et sibi ipsi prae animi impotentia vix constante contendere, aut quidpiam commertij habere. Si ipsemet sibi deesse vult, quomodo ego eum tueri aut iuvare potero, quin potius propria agam, neglectis alienis. Si existimat eius Epistolas non candide et ingenue esse a me editas, habet proculdubio exemplaria, monstret, quo hîc loco adulteratae sint, aut pudeat ipsum de mea integritate tale quid citra occasionem suspicari. De confessione eius mihi iudicium non sumam, neque enim eam ob prolixitatem sufficienter introspicere meae occupationes hactenus permiserunt, neque ego me Theologicis disputationibus libenter immisceo, ut mirer eum meam censuram in talibus tam saepe tamque serio exposcere, quae ad ea studia, quae prius inter nos ventilata sunt, non pertinent. Duriusculum mihi videtur, quod ab initio asserit et definit, Deum nos per sacramenta salvos facere. Id sane indiget bona glossa et interpretatione sobria, si idolatriae suspicio abesse debet, ad quam plurimi caeteroquin homines iam dudum hac praesertim in re (proch dolor) admodum proclives sunt. Quin et multa sunt praeterea in eodem scripto, tam de Baptismo quam de caena, quae non ita invicte demonstravit, atque existimat. Sed viderit ipse et alij, si quos habet, suae sententiae astipulatores; meum non est tantas componere lites. Deus liberet ROTHMANNUM ab ijs, quibuscum conflictatur, miserijs, et det illi patientiam atque constantiam. Ego si illi adessem, ipsius paupertati, quantum res meae ferrent, non defuturus essem, quemadmodum etiam antea, quando in Dania mecum fuit, ipsum non inanem demisi, sed aliquot Roseis nobilibus, pro quibus mihi tamen vix gratias agere dignatus est (adeo tunc erat insolens) donavi, cum tamen ipsius olim Princeps me longe ditior meos homines, quos aliquando ad ipsum misi, ne obulo quidem, nec etiam eos interea aluerit, quemadmodum ego per 5 vel 6 hebdomadas ROTHMANNUM, cum in Dania esset. Sed ista nihil ad rhombum, [ne]que ea, quae parvi [no]n duco momen[ti]. De fratre eius IOHANNE, qui Doctor medicinae est, cuperem scire, an is adhuc in vivis sit; si sic, optarem, ut me aliquando accederet; erit id illi forte non inutile.</p><p>Intellexi nuper, esse quendam in ditione Brunsvicensi nomine IOHANNEM KRABBEN, de Munden, quem praeteritorum aliquot annorum Eclipses in utroque Luminari diligenter observasse aiunt; si itaque ea, quae is circa has explorata habet, mihi procuraveris, rem apprime gratam praestiteris. Sed non suffecerint mihi nuda tempora, verum potius media scire averem, per quae in eorum cognitionem pervenit, sive sint Altitudines stellarum sive alia huc conducentia. Cupio autem eius observata ea potissimum de caussa habere, ut cum meis in ijsdem Ecclipsibus conferre possem, atque hinc meridianorum differentiam rectius eruere. Est etiam aliquando in diversis locis dispar serenitas. Sed de observationum fide certus esse non possim, nisi compertum habuero, qua ratione, uti dixi, acceptae sint.</p><p>Apud Cancellarium vestrum ea, de quibus antea [ad] te perscripsi, spero te effecturum, quo desiderijs nostris mature satisfiat.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareana Benatica Die 5 Aprilis Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO GEORG ROLLENHAGEN.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>The letter you sent me on the 23rd of February of this year, by your style [Old Style], most learned and most loving Lord Rollenhagen, was delivered me when I was lately at Prague, together with certain others likewise written to me by you at the end of August last year; there were also others, to <b>Doctor Rhodius</b>, which I at once sent to his lodging — but he was away abroad; the residents nonetheless promised they would duly see to them. To the earlier ones addressed to me I now answer nothing, since their chief contents are repeated in the later, to which take this.</p><p><b>That Hamburg citizen</b> I helped, as much as I could, through my men who were then about to go to court — both for your sake and for that of Lord <b>Doctor Vitus</b> [Winsheim]; but everything there proceeds slowly, as you know, and with difficulty, on account of the multitude of business.</p><p><b>As for the transport of my goods hither, there is no need to solicit your Senate further.</b> For when the Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, had learned from Lord <b>Barvitius</b> (though I did not wish it, but was leaving your Magistracy a little more time to consider the matter more rightly, as I wrote you I would) <b>that the Magdeburgers — whom He had twice clemently addressed by letter on this — had declined that office, He took it ill, and at once ordered a letter written about this same business to your Reverend Chapter</b> — which letter I now send thither by one of my students, a Dane by nation. A copy of it you have here enclosed; nor do I doubt that <b>the Reverend Chapter</b> (to whom I too have written) will, for the Imperial Majesty's sake, bring it about that <b>my Instruments and all the things left there be carried hither by the Elbe at their own expense, as soon as can be</b>. I trust too they will provide for the boards by which all these may be protected from the injury of weather and rains, which the sailors can take with them. And if they should wish to load the ship with <b>Bohemian wines</b> (which grew very generous last year), or with other goods sold cheaper here than there, those boards can again serve for this. It would be useful that some faithful servant be sent along, to procure what is to be bought here and at the same time take care of my things on the way — for which he will find me grateful. If this be done, there will be no need for this my student to return hither with the goods I have at Magdeburg; but rather let him go straight to <b>Hamburg</b>, to solicit the labour of the Hamburgers for the remaining Instruments to be carried hence hither likewise; but both these and other matters I leave to be arranged by your counsel. And if you hire, for a fixed price, a man to come hither with my things and be their overseer — should the Chapter be unwilling to appoint one — I will pay him whatever you promise. For the rest, instruct my emissary so that he knows what he must do or leave undone, and take care that all be put into execution maturely and diligently. I will hold you indemnified in all things, if you lay out anything for me, or pledge your credit.</p><p>You promise, in your last, that you will answer the rest of my writings' requests by the first courier; which I beg you do shortly. <b>To Rothmann I have nothing further to write</b>, since he was loth to answer anything to my letters, sent him benevolently and courteously; and he seems to be so morose and impatient that he can scarcely bear himself, much less others. <b>What he complains of me in his letters to you, I could easily refute; but I am unwilling to contend, or have any commerce, with a man — as you rightly say — most impatient, and from incapacity of mind scarcely consistent with himself.</b> If he wishes to fail himself, how shall I be able to protect or help him? — rather let me mind my own affairs, neglecting others'. <b>If he thinks his Letters were not candidly and honestly published by me</b> [in the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>], he doubtless has the originals — let him show in what place they were adulterated, or let him be ashamed to suspect such a thing of my integrity without occasion. <b>Of his \"confession\" I will take no judgement</b>, for my occupations have not hitherto permitted me to examine it sufficiently, by reason of its prolixity; nor do I gladly meddle in Theological disputes — so that I wonder he so often and so seriously demands my censure in such things, which do not pertain to those studies formerly ventilated between us. It seems to me a little harsh, what he asserts and defines from the start: <b>that God saves us through the sacraments</b>. That indeed needs a good gloss and a sober interpretation, if the suspicion of idolatry is to be absent — to which very many men are otherwise, alas, already much inclined, especially in this matter. And there are besides many things in the same writing, on <b>Baptism</b> as on the <b>[Lord's] Supper</b>, which he has not so invincibly demonstrated as he thinks. But let him see to it, and others, if he has any supporters of his opinion; it is not mine to settle such great quarrels. May God free Rothmann from the miseries with which he struggles, and give him patience and constancy. If I were with him, I would not fail his poverty, so far as my means allowed — even as before, when he was with me in Denmark, <b>I did not send him away empty, but gave him several rose-nobles</b>, for which, however, he scarcely deigned to thank me (so insolent was he then) — whereas his own Prince, far richer than I, gave my men (whom I once sent to him) <b>not even an obol</b>, nor even fed them meanwhile, as I fed Rothmann for 5 or 6 weeks when he was in Denmark. But these things are nothing to the purpose, nor those which I reckon of [no] small moment [<i>text partly damaged</i>]. About his brother <b>Johann, who is a Doctor of medicine</b>, I should wish to know whether he is still among the living; if so, I should wish that he visit me sometime — it will perhaps be not unprofitable for him.</p><p>I have lately understood that there is a certain man in the <b>Brunswick</b> territory, by name <b>Johann Krabbe of Münden</b>, who they say diligently observed the eclipses of these past years in both Luminaries [Sun and Moon]; if, then, you procure for me what he has explored about these, you will do a most welcome thing. But the bare times will not suffice me; rather I should wish to know <b>the means by which he came to their knowledge — whether they be Altitudes of stars, or other things conducing hither</b>. I desire to have his observations chiefly for this reason: <b>that I may compare them with my own in the same eclipses, and thence more rightly draw out the difference of meridians</b> [longitude]. There is also sometimes unequal clearness [of sky] in different places. But I cannot be sure of the observations' reliability unless I have ascertained, as I said, by what method they were taken.</p><p>With your Chancellor, I hope you will accomplish those things about which I wrote you before, that our wishes may be maturely satisfied.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 5th day of April, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector of the Magdeburg gymnasium and poet (the <i>Froschmeuseler</i>) — Tycho's agent for the instruments stored at <b>Magdeburg</b>, and the relay through whom Tycho learned <b>Rothmann was alive</b> (cf. brahe-t_15990922001).</li><li><b>Significance — the Magdeburg instrument-transport and the Rothmann estrangement.</b></li><li><b>The transport (imperial muscle).</b> The <b>Magdeburg city Senate had twice refused</b> to ship Tycho's stored library and instruments up the Elbe; <b>Rudolf II \"took it ill\" and ordered a letter to the Magdeburg cathedral Chapter</b> instead — carried now by <b>a Danish student</b> of Tycho's, with a copy enclosed. Tycho lays out the logistics: shipment <b>by the Elbe at the Chapter's cost</b>, protective <b>boards</b> (reusable to load <b>Bohemian wine</b> or cheap goods on the return), a trusted overseer; if arranged, the student goes on to <b>Hamburg</b> for the <i>remaining</i> instruments. A vivid picture of moving Uraniborg's apparatus to Bohemia. (Cf. the same store at Magdeburg in brahe-t_16000124001.)</li><li><b>The Rothmann rupture.</b> A revealing, bitter passage on <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (the former Hesse-Kassel mathematician, Tycho's great correspondent of the 1580s): Rothmann, now \"morose and impatient,\" complains of Tycho to Rollenhagen and demands Tycho's verdict on a <b>theological \"confession\"</b> he has written. Tycho declines — he \"does not meddle in Theological disputes\" — but cannot resist noting his unease at Rothmann's thesis <b>\"that God saves us through the sacraments\"</b> (needing \"a good gloss\" lest it smack of \"idolatry\"), and at his arguments on <b>Baptism and the Supper</b>. On the charge that Tycho <b>did not faithfully print Rothmann's letters in the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596)</b>, Tycho retorts: let him show where they were \"adulterated.\" Tycho recalls hosting Rothmann <b>5–6 weeks in Denmark</b> and gifting him <b>rose-nobles</b> (scarcely thanked), contrasting Rothmann's own <b>Prince (Landgrave Wilhelm IV)</b>, who gave Tycho's men \"not even an obol.\" A poignant late-stage souring of a famous scientific friendship.</li><li><b>A new observer for longitude.</b> Tycho seeks the eclipse observations of <b>Johann Krabbe of Münden</b> (Brunswick) — but insists on knowing <b>the method</b> (star-altitudes etc.), since he wants them <b>to compare with his own in the same eclipses and so derive the meridian-difference (longitude)</b>. A clean statement of the <b>eclipse-timing method for longitude</b>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Georg Rollenhagen; <b>Doctor Rhodius</b> (a Magdeburg/Wittenberg correspondent, unidentified-detail); <b>Doctor Vitus [Winsheim]</b>; <b>Barvitius</b>; the <b>Magdeburg cathedral Chapter</b>; <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> and his <b>brother Johann</b> (a physician); <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b> (\"his own Prince,\" unnamed); <b>Johann Krabbe of Münden</b>; the <b>Hamburg citizen</b> (unnamed); \"your Chancellor\" (unnamed). Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, the <b>Elbe</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Brunswick/Münden</b>, Denmark.</li><li><b>Text note.</b> A short <b>damaged stretch</b> near the end — <i>\"Sed ista nihil ad rhombum, [ne]que ea, quae parvi [no]n duco momen[ti]\"</i> — is partly editorially reconstructed (brackets); rendered \"these things are nothing to the purpose, nor those which I reckon of [no] small moment,\" with the damage flagged. <i>(nihil ad rhombum = \"nothing to the point,\" a proverb.)</i></li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Die 5 Aprilis Anno 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>5 April 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree. <i>(Rollenhagen's incoming letter is dated \"23 Feb … stylo vestro\" = Old Style, the Magdeburg reckoning.)</i></li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Magdeburg-Chapter instrument-transport</b> with its Elbe-shipping and wine-cargo logistics; the <b>Rothmann estrangement</b> — his theological \"confession,\" the sacraments/idolatry unease, the <i>Epistolae</i>-editing charge, and Tycho's rose-nobles recollection; and the <b>Johann Krabbe eclipse-observations sought for longitude</b>) is firmly construed, with no sharpening of Tycho's pointed but measured tone toward Rothmann. The single flagged element is the <b>partly-damaged \"nihil ad rhombum\" clause</b>.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against TBOO VIII pp.293-295 (Dreyer page images, not OCR). source_text matches Dreyer verbatim throughout, including every proper name checked (RHODIUM, BARVITIO, IOHANNEM KRABBEN de Munden, IOHANNE frater Rothmanni), the damaged/reconstructed clause 'Sed ista nihil ad rhombum, [ne]que ea, quae parvi [no]n duco momen[ti]' (Dreyer's own bracketed reconstruction, reproduced faithfully), the numbers (5 vel 6 hebdomadas; aliquot centenis/Roseis nobilibus), and the closing 'Vale. Dabantur ex Arce Caesareana Benatica Die 5 Aprilis Anno 1600' with no separate autograph line (confirmed against Dreyer, not an omission). One bracketed editorial supplement was independently checked and cleared rather than taken on faith: the translation's 'Lord Doctor Vitus [Winsheim]' for the Latin's bare 'Domini Doctoris VITI' is not a source-text counterpart, but Dreyer's own index to TBOO (vol. XV) cross-references this same page (VIII 293) under 'Winshemius, Vitus', so the surname is drawn from the edition's own apparatus rather than invented. Despite the self-assigned grade A being worthless as evidence on its own, full collation here confirms it independently.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15980618001", "next": "brahe-t_16000619001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980618001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000619001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports having assisted a Hamburg citizen through court intermediaries at Rollenhagen's request, and says no further solicitation of the Senate is needed since the Emperor has intervened directly.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000405001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000405002", "d": "1600-04-05", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe [confidentiality declaration] — Benátky, 5 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOHANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>ICH Μ. IOHAN. KEPLER, Einer Er: Landt in Steier Mathematicus, bekhenne, demnach Ich mit gnediger Erlaubung wolermelten landtschafft, ein reise in Beheim furgenommen, fürnemlich den Edlen vnd Gestrengen Herrn TYCHO BRAHE, Herrn auf Knudtstrup vnd Vraniburg, wegen Dero Gest. ansehnlicher vnd weitberühempten Astronomischen Instrumente vnd fürhabender bücher zuebesuchen, Das ich zue dem ende den vierten Februarij des 1600 Jahrs allhero gehn Benathka kommen, allda den wollermelten Herrn, gnedig vnd allerfreundtlichst woll empfangen, auch diese gantze Zeitt vber aller müglichkeit nach also tractirt worden, das ich mich hingegen iederzeit, zue aller Vnderthaniger Danckbarkeit schuldig erkhennen.</p><p>Demnach auch Ir Gest. alls einen liebhaber aller künsten, vnd denen beygethonen Cultoribus, gnedig gefallen, mir einen gueten theill Irer observationum, sambt andern anhengigen Inventionibus vnd laboribus Astronomicis freywillig mitzuetheilen: vnd Ich aber ietziger Zeitt bösen schädlichen gebrauch anderer leutte nützliche vnd rhuemliche arbeitt eigen nütziger verschlagener weisse zur hinderlichen den rechten Urhabern zueentziehen, vnd anderen rhümberechtig zuezuschreiben, zur gemüeth gefürtt: hab ich eigner sorgfaltigkeit halber, für nützlich vnd rathsamblich geachtet, mit gegenwärtiger meiner eignen handtschrifft vnd vndergedruckten Petschafft dahin mich zuuerpflichten, dass ich alles das, so mir von wolermelten Herrn BRAHE communicirt oder hernacher, in was wege es wolle, communicirt soll werden, in hochster geheim halten. Darmit auch mich andere alss einen liebhaber der Philosophia, gebe vnd offtermelten Herrn BRAHE, alss den rechten authori selbsten iederzeit gefallen würt, vmbgehen handlen vnd dero mich gebrauchen wölle: alles auff treuw vnd glauben, treuwlich vnd vngefehrlich.</p><p>In vrkhund meiner handschrifft, vnd angeporn Pitschafft, Geben den 5 Aprilis im 1600 Jah. Benat.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johannes Kepler to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I, Master Johannes Kepler, Mathematician of an Honourable Province in Styria, acknowledge: that — after I had, with the gracious permission of the well-mentioned [Provincial] Estates, undertaken a journey into Bohemia, chiefly to visit the noble and stern Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, on account of His Lordship's considerable and far-famed astronomical instruments and intended books — I came hither to Benátky, to that end, on the fourth of February of the year 1600; where I was graciously and most kindly well received by the well-mentioned Lord, and, during all this time, was treated according to all possibility [as well as could be], so that I, on the other hand, at all times acknowledge myself bound to all submissive gratitude.</p><p>Since, then, it has also graciously pleased His Lordship — as a lover of all the arts, and [a friend] to the devotees [<i>Cultores</i>] attached to them — to share with me, freely, a good part of His <i>observations</i>, together with other associated <i>inventions</i> and astronomical <i>labours</i>: and since I have brought to mind the present evil, harmful practice of [certain] people, of withdrawing other [people]'s useful and praiseworthy work, in a self-interested, cunning manner, to the hindrance of the rightful authors, and of ascribing it to others as though [theirs] by right of renown — I have, for the sake of my own conscientiousness, deemed it useful and advisable, by this my present own handwriting and the seal pressed below, to bind myself thereto: that I will keep in highest secrecy all that is communicated to me by the well-mentioned Lord Brahe, or shall hereafter, in whatever way it may be, be communicated [to me]; so that others, too, may regard me as a lover of <i>Philosophy</i>; and [so] that I will, at all times — as it shall please the oft-mentioned Lord Brahe, as the true author himself — deal, act, and conduct myself, and let him make use of me: all upon trust and faith, faithfully and without guile.</p><p>In witness of my handwriting and innate seal, given the 5th of April, in the year 1600. Benátky.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>A landmark Tycho–Kepler document.</b> This is <b>Kepler's signed declaration of confidentiality</b> — his solemn undertaking to keep Tycho's observations and discoveries secret — drawn up at <b>Benátky on 5 April 1600</b>, during the tense first months of their association. It is the formal pledge that helped patch up the early quarrel between the two men, and it ranks among the most-cited documents of their relationship. The witness survives, naturally, in the <b>Keplerian papers</b> (Vienna, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703 — the same volume preserving Kepler's transcript of Tycho's letter to Hoffmann, brahe-t_16000406001, written the <i>next</i> day, 6 April, on Kepler's return to Prague).</li><li><b>Kepler's self-styling.</b> <i>M. Johann Kepler, einer Ehrbaren Landschaft in Steier Mathematicus</i> — \"Master Johannes Kepler, Mathematician of an Honourable Province [the Provincial Estates] in <b>Styria</b>\" — his then-title as district mathematician of Graz, granted leave to travel to Bohemia \"to visit … Tycho Brahe … on account of His Lordship's far-famed astronomical instruments and intended books.\" He records his arrival \"<b>on the fourth of February 1600</b>\" and Tycho's kind reception.</li><li><b>The substance of the pledge.</b> Tycho having \"<b>freely shared a good part of His observations</b>, together with other … <i>inventions</i> and astronomical <i>labours</i>,\" Kepler — mindful of the \"<b>evil practice</b>\" of cunningly stripping work from its rightful authors and ascribing it to others (a pointed allusion to the kind of plagiarism-charge then swirling around <b>Ursus</b> and others) — binds himself, \"by this my own handwriting and seal,\" to <b>keep in highest secrecy</b> everything Tycho communicates to him, and to use it only as Tycho, \"<b>the true author</b>,\" shall please. The closing formula <i>auf Treu und Glauben, treulich und ungefährlich</i> (\"upon trust and faith, faithfully and without guile\") is the binding legal tag.</li><li><b>Code-switching.</b> Latin technical/legal nouns embedded in the German — <i>observationum, Inventionibus, laboribus Astronomicis, Cultoribus, Philosophia, authori</i> — kept and italicised (per §2), rendered into English.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Geben den 5 Aprilis im 1600 Jahr. Benat[ky]</i> = <b>5 April 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected 'Herrn aus Knudtstrup' to 'Herrn auf Knudtstrup' in Kepler's confidentiality declaration; Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.296) uses the standard 'Herr auf X' formula.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the German is rendered securely, and the document's identity and import (Kepler's 5 April 1600 confidentiality bond to Tycho, after his 4 February arrival at Benátky; the secrecy pledge against plagiarism; the witness among Kepler's own papers) are firmly established and corpus-/history-corroborated. The embedded Latin terms are preserved, not flattened.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.295-297 as page images; Kepler's confidentiality declaration entirely on p.296, re-read at close zoom to confirm 'auf' vs source_text's 'aus'. Aside from this one corrupted preposition, source_text matches Dreyer verbatim, and the full translation (the journey to visit Tycho's instruments, the pledge of secrecy about shared observations, the seal) is faithful throughout. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though the translation itself captured the correct sense.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text prints 'aus' (from/out of) where Dreyer p.296 clearly prints 'auf' (at/of) in the standard 'Herr auf X' = 'Lord of X' formula used throughout these letters; confirmed by close re-read. The translation correctly renders the intended sense ('Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg'), unaffected by the corruption. Dreyer prints: <i>den Edlen vnd Geftrengen Herrn TYCHO BRAHE, Herrn auf Knudtftrup vnd Vraniburg</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.295-297 (full slice, read in one pass); confirmed verbatim on p.296. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_16000400001", "next": "brahe-t_16000600001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000126002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000600001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Formally acknowledges his arrival at Benátky on 4 February 1600, Tycho's hospitality, and the sharing of observations and astronomical labours, committing to confidentiality regarding the material shared.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000405002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000406001", "d": "1600-04-06", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Baron of Grünbühel and Strechau — Benátky, 6 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703 (a transcript in Kepler's hand)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET GENEROSO DOMINO, DOMINO IOHANNI FRIDERICO HOFMANNO, LIBERO BARONI IN GRUENPUEHEL ET STRECHAU, PRAeFECTO PROVINCIAe DUCATUS STYRIAe ET ARCHIDUCATUS AUSTRIAe, MARSCHALCHO UBIQUE HAeREDITARIO, BURGRAVIO IN STEYER, NEC NON SACRAe CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS AULAe CONSILIARIO ET SERENISSIMI ARCHIDUCIS MAXIMILIANI CUBICULARIO PRIMARIO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO IMPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>REDIT nunc Pragam M. KEPLERUS, et licet suasor illi fuerim, ut domestici mei, qui etiamnum Pilsnae est, et inter alia in negocio dictum KEPLERUM concernente sollicitat, reditum, quem intra paucos dies futurum spero, expectaret, ut quid fieri possit, certius resciremus (praesertim cum spes aliqua facta sit, Saam Caesam Matem petitionibus meis humilimis clementissime annuere velle) atque demum aliquid rati concludere possemus: tamen cum ipsum nihilominus abitum festinare intelligerem, reliqui hominem suo arbitrio. Quid vero inter nos ventilatum sit, Illris Do Tua e D. Doctore IOANNE IESSENIO, qui has adfert, seorsim cognoscet: is enim interfuit, dum haec gererentur: quare longiore et taediosâ eorum commemoratione supersedeo, omnia ad hanc rem facientia Illustris Dnis Tuae prudenti consilio dispicienda ordinandaque etiamnum relinquens.</p><p>Coeterum de puero, quem Illustris Dnatio Tua ad me mittere decrevit, sciat me libenter eum, quandocunque advenerit, recepturum, tum quoque laboratorem Spagiricum. Et expecto pariter fabrum illum Mechanicum, qui e Styria venturus est. Quam primum vero per occupationes licuerit, spero me Pragae futurum, ubi plenius cum Illri Done Tua de ijs, quae hîc omissa sunt, agam.</p><p>Valeat Tua Illris Do quam optime et diutissime.<br>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benatica die 6 Aprilis Anno 1600.<br>Illri Generositati Tuae<br>Addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and generous Lord, Lord Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Free Baron in Grünbühel and Strechau, Prefect of the Province of the Duchy of Styria and of the Archduchy of Austria, hereditary Marshal everywhere, Burgrave in Steyr, and also Councillor of the Court of His Sacred Imperial Majesty and First Chamberlain of the Most Serene Archduke Maximilian, his lord and friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Illustrious and generous man, lord and most honoured friend.</p><p>Master Kepler now returns to Prague; and although I had been his advisor [urged him] to await the return of my servant — who is still at Pilsen, and, among other things, is pressing [matters] in the business concerning the said Kepler — which return I hope will be within a few days, so that we might more certainly find out what can be done (especially since some hope has been given that His Imperial Majesty wishes most clemently to assent to my most humble petitions), and so that at last we might conclude something settled: yet, since I understood that he was nonetheless hastening his departure, I left the man to his own judgment. But what was ventilated between us, Your Illustrious Lordship will learn separately from Doctor Jan Jessenius, who brings these [lines]; for he was present while these things were transacted. Wherefore I forbear a longer and tedious recounting of them, leaving everything bearing on this matter still to be considered and ordered by Your Illustrious Lordship's prudent counsel.</p><p>For the rest, about the boy whom Your Illustrious Lordship has decided to send to me, let [you] know that I will gladly receive him, whenever he arrives — and likewise the spagyric [alchemical] laborator. And I equally await that mechanical craftsman who is to come from Styria. As soon as my occupations permit, I hope to be at Prague, where I will more fully treat with Your Illustrious Lordship about those things which have been omitted here.</p><p>May Your Illustrious Lordship fare as well and as long as possible.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 6th day of April, in the year 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Illustrious Generosity,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>A key Tycho–Kepler document — preserved in Kepler's own hand.</b> The witness (Vienna, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703) is <b>a transcript made by Kepler himself</b>, which is why it survives among the Keplerian papers. It dates from the <b>first, strained Benátky period</b> (Feb–April 1600), when Kepler had come to Tycho but the terms of his stay were unsettled.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, Free Baron of Grünbühel and Strechau</b> (1568–1615), the great Styrian magnate — <i>Landeshauptmann</i> of Styria, hereditary Marshal, Burgrave of Steyr, Imperial Aulic Councillor, and First Chamberlain of <b>Archduke Maximilian</b> — and <b>Kepler's patron</b>, at whose Prague house Kepler lodged. Tycho writes to him as the broker of Kepler's situation.</li><li><b>Content.</b> Kepler \"<b>now returns to Prague</b>\"; Tycho had urged him to wait for the return (from <b>Pilsen</b>, where the court was) of Tycho's servant, who was \"pressing the business concerning Kepler\" — i.e. seeking the Emperor's assent to Tycho's petitions (a salaried place for Kepler) — \"especially since some hope\" had been given that <b>Rudolf II</b> would \"<b>most clemently assent</b>\"; but Kepler hastened off, so Tycho \"left the man to his own judgment.\" The details of \"what was ventilated between us\" Hoffmann will hear from the bearer, <b>Dr. Jan Jessenius</b> (the anatomist, present at the negotiations). This is contemporary evidence of the delicate Tycho–Kepler accommodation that Jessenius helped mediate.</li><li><b>Other matters.</b> Hoffmann is sending Tycho a <i>puer</i> (a boy/page), a <i>laborator Spagiricus</i> (an <b>alchemical laboratory-assistant</b> — <i>spagyric</i> = Paracelsian chemistry), and a <i>faber Mechanicus</i> (a <b>mechanic/instrument-maker</b>) \"from Styria\" — staffing for Tycho's Prague establishment.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex arce Caesarea Benatica die 6 Aprilis 1600</i> = <b>6 April 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the content is of documented importance (Kepler's return to Prague; Tycho's petition to Rudolf II on Kepler's behalf; Jessenius as go-between; Hoffmann's Styrian recruits), all corpus-/history-corroborated — and the witness is Kepler's own transcript. The recipient's full titulature is reproduced.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.296-298 as page images; letter to Hoffmann entirely on p.297 (re-read at close zoom to verify 'negocio dictum KEPLERUM concernente' matches source_text exactly). source_text matches Dreyer verbatim throughout, including the full honorific title. Translation collated clause-by-clause: Kepler's return to Prague, the Jessenius reference, the boy and spagyric laborator, the mechanical craftsman from Styria, and the closing all render faithfully. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703, (transcript by Kepler)."], "th": {"label": "Johann Friedrich Hoffmann", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000306001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000203001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that Kepler is departing for Prague before affairs concerning his appointment are concluded at Pilsen, and awaits the Emperor's response to his petitions before settling the matter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000406001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000408001", "d": "1600-04-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "ck": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Jessenius a Jessen — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 8 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703 (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOHANNEM IESSENIUM A IESSEN.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME vir, amice plurimum mihi semper dilecte, quae tuae fidei concredidi, Generoso D. HOFFMANNO seorsim monstranda, cupio mihi nunc remitti; litteras tamen illas, quas TEGNAGLIUS ultimo ad me dedit, quae de Magnifici D. CORRADUCIJ resolutione faciunt, vellem bene tuo sigillo munitas ac tuis inclusas unâ cum meis ad DANIELEM mitti, siquidem in ijs faciat mentionem eorum, quae D. CORRADUCIUS apud Caesar. Mai. meo nomine impetravit, ut sciat DANIEL, eo rectius se facere, si apud D. CORRADUCIUM in meis rebus sollicitet, non minus quam apud D. BARVICIUM, quem quodammodo lentum in ijs promovendis ............. Dubitat enim ipse DANIEL in ultimis suis ad me datis, an hoc facere debeat nec ne. Ego vero rescripsi, mihi id non inconsultum videri, caute tamen omnia agenda, ne D. BARVICIUM per imprudentiam offendat, nam amborum retinere benevolentiam, quoad fieri poterit, satius duco. Coeterum cum tu hîc spectator et auditor fueris eorum, quae a KEPLERO dicta factaque sunt, sub ipsum discessus momentum, et simul quomodo postea, iuxta currum mihi valedicturus, poenitentia quadam eorum duci prae se ferret, atque ea omnia, quod affectus suos regere non posset, excusaret, egoque a condonatione non alienus essem, tibique in aurem susurraverim, ut efficeres illum Pragae literis ad me datis id ipsum testari, quod male ipsum habeat, se sic exorbitasse cupereque mecum in gratiam redire, quod et te facturum aiebas, et sponte antea mihi seorsim dixeras, te in itinere ipsum serio admoniturum, imo reprehensurum, quod se tam impotenter et biliose gesserit, contra quam decet virum doctum et honestum: iccirco mitto tibi hîc inclusas ipsius literas manu eius propria exaratas, quarum effrenem petulantiam et nimis arrogantem dicacitatem nec per meum vinum nec per meum contemtum, nec quicquid eiuscemodi aliud quod praetendit, excusare poterit, nisi et tunc furiosus fuit (uti forte huius morbi semina, etiam cum sibi maxime constare videtur, clam fovet) cum ista composito animo scriberet, et ipsemet calamo, me ignorante (qui ipsi iam ignoscere coeperam, siquidem resipiscere voluisset) praeest. Miraberis, non dubito, hominis in malitia citra omne meum meritum pertinaciam, nec forte habebis aliud, quid dicas, quam me mea humanitate, uti etiam tacite exprobrâsti, effecisse, ut iste hominem exueret, atque in rabidum canem, quem suo stomacho se aemulari dicere solitus est (utinam etiam cerebro et corde) transformaretur. Inspice igitur et despice, quod et ego faciam. Quis enim scienter et volenter furentem facile curaret? Si ipsum in via admonuisti, et ut rationes inveniret, mecum denuo in amicitiam redeundi, adhortatus es, uti proculdubio fecisti, vide quaeso, quales fructus ediderit haec tua sobria, candida et amica admonitio et adhortatio. Ego, etsi facilimum mihi sit, omnes ipsius oblatratus vera redargutione compescere, nolo tamen cum tam procaci lingua praedito, nec modestiae aut virtutis curam habente, contendere, memor illius Catonici:</p><p>Contra verbosum noli contendere verbis.</p><p>Constitui igitur in posterum nihil cum eo habere commercij, sive per literas sive oretenus, et optarem me nunquam habuisse, tunc minus mea humanitate abuti posset. Iussi quidem, statim ac is abierat, MATHIAM studiosum nonnulla ex chartis illis, quas ad me recepi, describere, ut ea promptius legere, cum ipso, quando unâ cum DANIELE redierit, conferre possem, atque mea dubia illi subgerere, et quomodo peragendum esset, expiscari, atque tum illi a se conscripta, uti sancte promisi, restituere. Verum cum ex his malitiosis literis plus satis percipiam, eum non reversurum, uti etiam non cupio, ipsemet in culpa est, quod pollicitis stare nequeam. Et super hoc, quod in Epistola iam missa iecit fundamentum, vix utar eo tamquam archi[tec]to, cui aliquid ex meis observationibus et laboribus superstruatur. Ille ipse me iam magis cautum, quam antea fui, reddidit; genio, uti spero, bono mei curam habente, et omnia sic in meum commodum digerente. Nolim tamen, ut cuipiam has Keplerianas literas ostendas, et ne quidem D. HOFFMANNO, aut cum ipso KEPLERO hac de re expostules, nisi forte sua quadam importunitate tibi occasionem dederit, sed actutum cum hoc eodem nuntio, bene conclusas et iterum obsignatas, mihi remittas. Si tamen subolfeceris ipsum apud D. HOFFMANNUM vel alios, ut suam probaret innocentiam, me calumniatum esse, aut quippiam veritati minus consonum effutijsse, fac quod bonum et integrum virum decet, quod et non gravatim effecturum mihi polliceor (prout et ego te saepius contra aliorum sugillationes tutatus sum) ut meam a te defendi causam, atque id, quod veritas postulat, asseverari resciscam. Interim etiam an apud URSUM istum aliquid in mei contumeliam tacite moliatur (quomodo enim illi amplius fidam?) per tertiam vel quartam manum inquire, quamvis nihil morer, quicquid dixerit, modo vera dixerit, et cum Deo V. simul fuerimus, tibi aperiam, quidnam id sit, de quo ita minari videtur, et plura, quae ad dilutionem illorum, quorum foecundus et facundus est consarcinator, faciunt, licet ipse plaeraque nôsti, suggeram.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex arce Benateka die 8 Aprilis Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOHANNES JESSENIUS A JESSEN.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned man, friend always most dear to me: what I entrusted to your good faith, to be shown separately to the noble Lord <b>Hoffmann</b>, I wish now to be sent back to me. But those letters which <b>Tengnagel</b> last sent me, which bear on the resolution of the magnificent Lord <b>Coraduz</b>, I should wish — well secured with your seal and enclosed in yours — to be sent together with mine <b>to Daniel</b>, since in them he makes mention of the things which Lord Coraduz obtained from the Imperial Majesty in my name, so that Daniel may know he does the more rightly if he solicits my affairs with Lord Coraduz, no less than with <b>Lord Barvitius</b> — whom [I find] somewhat slow in promoting them …………… [<i>text breaks off — a lacuna in the copy</i>]. For Daniel himself, in his last letter to me, doubts whether he ought to do this or not. But I wrote back that it does not seem to me ill-advised — yet that all is to be done cautiously, lest through imprudence he offend Lord Barvitius; for I judge it better to keep the goodwill of both, so far as may be.</p><p>For the rest: since you were here a spectator and hearer of <b>what was said and done by Kepler at the very moment of [his] departure</b> — and likewise how afterward, <b>about to bid me farewell beside the wagon, he showed signs of a certain repentance</b> of them, and excused it all on the ground that he could not govern his passions, and I was not averse to pardon — and since I <b>whispered in your ear that you should get him, by a letter sent me from Prague, to attest that very thing: that it grieved him to have so overstepped, and that he wished to return into favour with me</b> (which you said you would do, and had earlier of your own accord told me separately, that you would seriously admonish him on the journey, nay reprehend him, for having borne himself so impotently and biliously, contrary to what befits a learned and honourable man): for that reason <b>I send you here enclosed his own letter, written in his own hand</b> — whose unbridled petulance and over-arrogant scurrility he will not be able to excuse by my wine, nor by my contempt, nor by any other such thing he pretends, unless he was then too in a frenzy (as perhaps he secretly nurses the seeds of this disease even when he seems most composed of himself) when he wrote those things with composed mind, and himself wields the pen — without my knowledge (I who had already begun to pardon him, had he but wished to come to his senses). You will wonder, I do not doubt, at the man's pertinacity in malice, beyond all my deserving; and perhaps you will have nothing else to say than that <b>I, by my humanity — as you even tacitly reproached me — brought it about that this man put off the man, and was transformed into a rabid dog</b>, which he was wont to say he rivalled with his stomach (would that [he did so] also with brain and heart). Inspect it, then, and despise it, as I shall too. For who would readily cure one knowingly and willingly raving? If you admonished him on the way, and exhorted him to find reasons for returning anew into friendship with me — as doubtless you did — see, I pray, <b>what fruits this sober, candid, and friendly admonition and exhortation of yours has borne</b>. I, though it would be most easy for me to curb all his barkings by a true refutation, am yet unwilling to contend with one endowed with so insolent a tongue, and having no care for modesty or virtue — mindful of that saying of Cato:</p><p><i>\"Do not strive with words against a wordy man.\"</i></p><p>I have resolved, therefore, to have henceforth no commerce with him, whether by letter or by word of mouth; and I could wish I never had — then the less could he abuse my humanity. I did indeed order, as soon as he had gone, the student <b>Mathias</b> to copy out some things from those papers which I took back to myself, that I might read them more readily, and confer about them with him [Kepler] when he should return together with Daniel, and put my doubts to him, and fish out how it ought to be carried through, and then restore to him what he had written, as I solemnly promised. But since from this malicious letter I perceive more than enough that <b>he will not return</b> (as I also do not wish), he himself is at fault that I cannot stand by my promises. And upon this — on which he laid a foundation in the letter now sent — <b>I shall hardly use him as an architect, upon whom anything of my observations and labours should be built</b>. He himself has now made me more cautious than I was before; my good genius, I hope, taking care of me, and disposing all things thus to my advantage. I should not wish, however, that you show this Keplerian letter to anyone — not even to Lord Hoffmann — nor that you expostulate with Kepler himself about this, unless perhaps by some importunity he gives you occasion; but that you send it back to me at once, by this same messenger, well closed and sealed again. If, however, <b>you scent out that he, to Lord Hoffmann or others — to prove his own innocence — has calumniated me, or blurted out anything less than consonant with the truth</b>, do what becomes a good and upright man (which I promise myself you will do not grudgingly — even as I have often protected you against the carpings of others), so that I may learn my cause is defended by you, and that what truth demands is affirmed. Meanwhile inquire also, <b>through a third or fourth hand, whether he secretly contrives anything against my honour with that Ursus</b> (for how should I trust him further?) — although I care nothing for whatever he may say, provided he say the truth; and when, God willing, we are together, I will open to you what that thing is about which he seems so to threaten, and will suggest more things conducing to the dissolving of those of which <b>[Ursus] is a fertile and fluent botcher</b> — though you yourself know most of them.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the castle of Benátky, the 8th day of April, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johannes Jessenius / Jan Jessenský</b> (1566–1621), the anatomist and Tycho's friend, who had <b>mediated between Tycho and Kepler</b> and was present at the Benátky quarrel. (He is the \"Planet\" of brahe-t_16000327002; later executed in the 1621 Prague Old-Town-Square execution.) The witness is a Vienna copy.</li><li><b>Significance — the great Tycho–Kepler rupture of April 1600 (a primary document).</b> Kepler had come to Benátky early in 1600; a violent quarrel erupted over his working conditions, his access to the observations, and his keep. This letter, written days after, is <b>Tycho's raw account to the mediator</b>:</li><li><b>The quarrel and the wagon-side repentance.</b> Jessenius \"was a spectator and hearer of what Kepler said and did at the very moment of departure,\" and saw how Kepler, <b>bidding farewell beside the wagon, showed \"a certain repentance,\"</b> excusing himself as unable to \"govern his passions.\" Tycho \"was not averse to pardon\" and had Jessenius coax a written apology from Prague.</li><li><b>The \"malicious letter.\"</b> Instead, Kepler sent <b>an angry letter \"in his own hand,\"</b> whose \"unbridled petulance\" Tycho refuses to excuse \"by my wine, nor my contempt\" — the very pretexts Kepler had alleged (Tycho's table-wine; Tycho's slighting of him). Tycho, wounded, half-suggests Kepler is subject to fits of \"frenzy\" (<i>furor</i>).</li><li><b>The famous metaphor.</b> Tycho throws back Kepler's own self-description: that Kepler \"put off the man and was transformed into a <b>rabid dog</b>, which he was wont to say he rivalled <b>with his stomach</b> — would that [also] with brain and heart.\" (Kepler's own self-portrait as choleric/dog-like, turned against him.)</li><li><b>The resolution to break off</b> — citing the <i>Disticha Catonis</i>: <b><i>\"Contra verbosum noli contendere verbis\"</b></i> (\"Do not strive with words against a wordy man\") — and the pointed line <b>\"I shall hardly use him as an architect, on whom anything of my observations and labours should be built.\"</b> <i>(The bitter irony: within weeks they reconciled; Kepler returned, inherited the observations, and built the</i> Astronomia nova <i>— and the</i> Rudolphine Tables <i>— precisely on them.)</i></li><li><b>The Ursus fear (the sore point).</b> Tycho asks Jessenius to inquire \"through a third or fourth hand\" <b>whether Kepler secretly colludes with Ursus</b> against Tycho's honour — a live anxiety, since <b>Kepler had earlier written a flattering letter to Ursus</b> (Nicolaus Reimers Bär), who printed it in his defamatory <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus</i>. \"[Ursus] is a fertile and fluent botcher\" (<i>consarcinator</i>). (Cf. the Ursus thread, brahe-t_16000401001, brahe-t_16000328001.)</li><li><b>The court-business frame (opening).</b> Tycho's affairs at the imperial court: papers to be returned (shown privately to <b>Hoffmann</b>); <b>Tengnagel's</b> letters on <b>Coraduz's</b> \"resolution\" (what Coraduz obtained from the Emperor in Tycho's name) to be forwarded <b>to Daniel</b>, who should solicit Tycho's business with <b>both Coraduz and Barvitius</b> (the latter \"somewhat slow\"), keeping both men's goodwill. <i>A lacuna</i> (`…………`) interrupts the Barvitius clause in the copy.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Johannes Jessenius; <b>Johannes Kepler</b> (the quarrel); <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann von Grünbüchel</b> (imperial councillor, Kepler's Prague host); <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (<i>Tegnaglius</i>); <b>Rudolf von Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Johann Anton Barvitius</b> (imperial secretary); <b>Daniel</b> (an unidentified court-agent/solicitor of Tycho's at Prague who travels with Kepler — left unidentified, flagged); the student <b>Mathias</b> (a <i>studiosus</i>/assistant, plausibly Matthias Seiffart — not confirmed, flagged); <b>Nicolaus Reimers Bär (Ursus)</b>; <b>Cato</b> (the <i>Disticha Catonis</i>). Place: <b>Benátky</b>.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A keystone for the <b>Tycho–Kepler convergence</b> thread and the <b>Ursus dispute</b> thread; route alongside the reconciliation that followed (Kepler's return and succession).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 8 Aprilis Anno 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>8 April 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored singular translation of 'has Keplerianas literas' ('this Keplerian letter') for consistency with the two earlier renderings of the same epistolary-plural noun in this letter, both referring to the single enclosed Kepler letter (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.299).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — vehement but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the court-business preamble; the <b>Kepler quarrel, the wagon-side repentance, the \"malicious\" reply-letter, the rabid-dog metaphor, the Cato tag, the resolution to break off and not build on Kepler \"as architect\"</b>; the <b>Ursus-collusion fear</b>; the instructions to Jessenius on the enclosed Keplerian letters) is firmly and carefully construed, with no silent sharpening of Tycho's heated rhetoric. The one <b>lacuna</b> in the Barvitius clause is marked; <b>Daniel</b> and the student <b>Mathias</b> are flagged as not securely identified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full published_english against Dreyer's page images TBOO VIII pp. 298-299 (the letter's actual print run within the 297-300 slice; p.297 is the preceding Tycho-to-Hoffmann letter and p.300 the following Herwartus-to-Tycho letter, included only as slice padding). Verified source_text against Dreyer's pages clause by clause: no TEXT_ERROR -- source_text matches Dreyer exactly throughout, including the lacuna dots after 'in ijs promovendis' (present on both) and the editorial bracket 'archi[tec]to' (Dreyer's own, carried through correctly). Checked every named party (Hoffmann, Tengnagel, Coraduz/Corraducius, Daniel, Barvicius, Kepler, Mathias, Ursus) for correct assignment across the letter's dense pronoun chains -- all correctly resolved, including the tricky 'iuxta currum mihi valedicturus' (Kepler bidding farewell), the Kepler self-boast about rivalling a rabid dog (reflexive 'se'/'suo' correctly kept self-referential to Kepler, not flipped onto Tycho), and the bracketed '[Ursus]' supplied for the unstated subject of 'consarcinator' (a fair, historically apt editorial supplement, not a fabrication). The Cato quotation, the 8 April 1600 date, and the closing formula all match source_text and Dreyer exactly. The bracketed lacuna gloss ('text breaks off -- a lacuna in the copy') fairly signals a genuine gap that Dreyer's page also shows, not an invented claim. Found one low-severity internal inconsistency (plural/singular handling of the recurring epistolary-plural 'literas' for the single enclosed Kepler letter); no fabrication, omission, flipped pronoun, wrong date, or misattributed quotation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Latin 'literas' here is the same epistolary-plural noun used twice earlier in this same letter for the single enclosed Kepler letter ('ipsius literas manu eius propria exaratas' -&gt; rendered 'his own letter'; 'ex his malitiosis literis' -&gt; rendered 'this malicious letter'). The translation renders the first two occurrences as singular but this third occurrence of the same recurring noun, referring to the same one document, as plural 'letters' -- an internal inconsistency that could mislead a reader into thinking multiple Kepler letters are in play. At: “I should not wish, however, that you show these Keplerian letters to anyone”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the clause directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.299): the Latin 'Nolim tamen, ut cuipiam has Keplerianas literas oftendas' matches our source text exactly, so this is a translation-only defect, not a text corruption. Fixed the English for internal consistency with the letter's two earlier singular renderings of the same noun. No remaining crux.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Johannes Jessenius a Jessen", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000324001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000324001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks Jessenius to return documents held for Hoffmann and to forward Tengnagel's letters to Daniel so he can solicit Tycho's affairs with both Coraduz and Barvitius.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000408001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000411001", "d": "1600-04-11", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 11 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>INTELLEXI, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, ex Agente rerum Serenissimi Principis mei in aulâ Caesareâ, ALEX. PRENDTL, qui ad negotia sua quaedam propria expedienda hâc transijt, literas illas, quas D. Doctori HELLER ad me dirigendas committis, praeposito seu Magistro supremo equorum dispositorum Pragae consignari. Coeterum novi et expertus sum eorum, quibus cura Postae seu equorum dispositorum incumbit, negligentiam et desidiam, atque adeo oscitantiam quoque miram, ita ut literas aliô dirigendas aliorsum nonnunquam transmittant. Tenet itaque haec res me admodum et forte plus nimio solicitum, ne forte ea, quae ad me mittis, intereant. Quare rogo te etiam atque etiam, ut in posterum, si quid literarum ad me dederis, eas praefato Agenti rerum Principis mei, vel in absentia illius IOANNI MENHARDO S. Caes. M. Consilij aulae Imperialis Secretario Latino, qui aulam Imperatoris sequitur, quorum titulos his subscripsi, consignes; curabunt enim illi sedulo, ut sub coperto, uti vocant, Serenissimi mei Principis tuto ad me transferantur.</p><p>Novissime literas tuas pridie huius anni labentis datas unâ cum dono partis primae Progymnasmatum restauratae Astronomiae (id quod gratitudinis ergo commemoro) accepi, nullas autem recentiores. Quod si interea temporis nullas ad me dederis, bene se res habet, et omni solicitudine vaco: si quas vero dederis, timeo, ne alicubi delitescant, et obsecro summopere, ut eas reiterari cures, et vel hac ratione solicitudinem alleves.</p><p>Atque hisce tibi officia et studia mea paratissima quaeque polliceor.<br>Datae Monachij die 11 Apr. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I have understood, most noble man and easily Prince of all mathematicians, from the Agent of my Most Serene Prince's affairs at the Imperial court, <b>Alexander Prendtl</b> — who passed this way to expedite certain affairs of his own — that those letters which you entrust to Doctor Heller, to be directed to me, are consigned to the prefect, or supreme Master, of the relay-horses [the post] at Prague. But I know, and have learned by experience, the negligence and sloth — and indeed the wondrous somnolence too — of those to whom the care of the Post, or of the relay-horses, is committed, so that they sometimes send letters that ought to be directed one way off in another direction. And so this matter holds me very anxious — perhaps more than is fitting — lest the things you send me should perchance perish. Wherefore I ask you, again and again, that in future, if you give any letters to me, you consign them to the aforesaid Agent of my Prince's affairs, or, in his absence, to <b>Johann Menhard</b>, Latin Secretary of the Aulic Imperial Council of His Imperial Majesty, who follows the Emperor's court (whose titles I have subscribed below); for they will diligently see to it that, \"under cover\" (as they call it) of my Most Serene Prince, they be safely conveyed to me.</p><p>Most recently I received your letter, dated the day before [the beginning of] this passing year, together with the gift of the first part of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> of restored Astronomy (which I mention out of gratitude); but none more recent. So if, in the meantime, you have given me none, the matter stands well, and I am free of all anxiety; but if you have given any, I fear lest they be lying hidden somewhere, and I most earnestly beseech that you see to their being repeated, and by this means at least relieve my anxiety.</p><p>And with these I promise you my most ready services and zeal.<br>Given at Munich, the 11th day of April, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (the Munich correspondent); here on the practical problem of <b>postal security</b>.</li><li><b>The postal routing.</b> Herwart warns that letters routed through the <b>Prague post-master</b> (<i>praepositus seu Magister supremus equorum dispositorum</i> — the prefect of the relay-horses, i.e. the imperial post) go astray, and asks Tycho instead to consign letters to <b>Alexander Prandtl</b> (the <b>Bavarian ducal agent</b> at the imperial court — cf. brahe-t_16000923001), or, in his absence, to <b>Johann Menhard (Menhardus)</b>, <i>Latin Secretary of the Aulic Imperial Council</i> — so they travel \"<b>under cover</b>\" (<i>sub coperto</i> — enclosed in the Bavarian diplomatic packet) and arrive safely. <i>Doctor Heller</i> = Tycho's Prague forwarding contact (cf. brahe-t_15991214001).</li><li><b>The book-gift.</b> Herwart acknowledges Tycho's gift of \"<b>the first part of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> of restored Astronomy</b>\" = the first volume of the <i>Astronomiae instauratae progymnasmata</i> — received with a letter dated <i>pridie huius anni labentis</i>, \"the day before [the start of] this current year\" (i.e. ~31 December 1599 / 1 January 1600).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Monachij die 11 Apr. 1600</i> = <b>11 April 1600</b>; Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the agent's name in translation from 'Alexander Prandtl' to 'Alexander Prendtl', matching both source_text and Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.300) exactly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the postal-security advice (avoid the Prague imperial post; route via the Bavarian agent Prandtl or the imperial Latin secretary Menhard \"under cover\"), Doctor Heller as forwarding contact, and the <i>Progymnasmata</i> gift are all clear and corpus-consistent.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.299-301 page images (letter text on p.300 only, flanked by the end of a prior letter to Iessenius and the start of a following one to Concinius). Collated opening, closing, all proper names (Prendtl, D. Heller, Ioanni Menhardo, Progymnasmatum), and the date (11 April 1600) against the page image. Body text matches word-for-word; only the one name-spelling divergence found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). Translator altered the spelling of the agent's surname from Prendtl to Prandtl; neither our source_text nor Dreyer's page supports the 'a' spelling. At: “Alexander Prandtl”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.299-301 (full slice, read in one pass); confirmed verbatim on p.300. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_16000403001", "next": "brahe-t_16000418001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991231001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Warns of postal negligence in Prague and asks Tycho to route future letters through Alexander Prandtl or Johann Menhard rather than the postmaster, to ensure reliable delivery.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000411001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000414001", "d": "1600-04-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Cosimo Concini", "ck": "Cosimo Concini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Cosimo Concini — Benátky, 14 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRISSIMO ET GENEROSISSIMO VIRO DOMINO COSMO CONCINIO E COMITIBUS PENNAe SERENISSIMI MAGNI DUCIS HETRURIAe APUD SACRAM CAeSAREAM MAIESTATEM ORATORI MAGNIFICENTISSIMO DOMINO OBSERVANDISSIMO.</p><p>ILLUSTRISSIME ET HONORANDISSIME DOMINE.</p><p>SERENISSIMI Principis, Magni Ducis Hetruriae, Clementissimas literas ab Illma Domne Vestra mihi favorabiliter missas, unâ cum proprijs suis, accepi, et magna inde affectus sum voluptate, cum quod Serma eius Celsitudo meis Clementissime respondere, ijsdemque benignissimo animo annuere non dedignata sit, tum etiam quod Illma Domo Vestra mihi tam benevole scripserit, et Sermi Principis sui voluntatem, ubi Pragae adfuero, plenius mihi exponere haud degravetur. Adveniam itaque, auxiliante Deo, quamprimum nonnulla ad aedificia, quae pro Instrumentis meis Astronomicis hîc debito modo disponendis, nunc parantur, pertinentia, adoravero; et observationes quasdam, quibus per instantes aliquot dies (neque enim alias intra annum eiuscemodi revertuntur) invigilandum, Coelitus deduxero. Spero autem haec, et quaecunque praeterea me remorari poterint, intra 14 ad summum dies expediri posse, ut Illmae Doi Vrae, ab hisce curis liberatus, promptius adesse queam, et quae nomine Sermi Principis sui mihi referenda habet, obsequentissimo animo percipere. Interim ut hanc morulam, pro consueta sua humanitate, candide accipiat, meque sibi commendatum habeat, officiosissime peto.</p><p>Deus Opt. Max. Illustrm D. V. diu florentem et incolumem conservet.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 14 Aprilis Anni 1600.<br>Illustrissae Domni Vae<br>Reverenter addictissimus<br>T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious and most generous man, Master Cosimo Concini, of the Counts of Penna, most magnificent Ambassador of the Most Serene Grand Duke of Tuscany with His Sacred Imperial Majesty, most observed Lord.</b></p><p>Most illustrious and most honoured Lord.</p><p>I have received the most clement letter of the Most Serene Prince, the Grand Duke of Tuscany, favourably sent me by Your Illustrious Lordship, together with your own; and I have been affected thereby with great pleasure — both because his Most Serene Highness did not disdain most clemently to answer mine, and to assent to them with most kindly mind, and also because Your Illustrious Lordship wrote to me so benevolently, and does not find it burdensome to set forth to me more fully the will of his Most Serene Prince when I shall be at Prague. I will therefore come, God helping, as soon as I have attended to certain things pertaining to the buildings which are now being prepared for arranging my astronomical instruments here in due manner; and as soon as I have brought down from heaven certain observations which must be watched for over the next several days (for they do not otherwise recur within a year). But I hope these — and whatever else can delay me — can be despatched within at most fourteen days, so that, freed from these cares, I may the more promptly be present to Your Illustrious Lordship, and with most obedient mind receive what he has to report to me in the name of his Most Serene Prince. Meanwhile I most dutifully ask that you take this little delay candidly, according to your accustomed kindness, and hold me commended to you.</p><p>May God Best and Greatest preserve Your Illustrious Lordship, flourishing and unharmed, for long.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 14th day of April, in the year 1600.<br>Reverently most devoted to Your Illustrious Lordship,<br>T. B.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Cosimo Concini</b>, <i>e comitibus Pennae</i> (of the Counts of Penna), the <b>resident ambassador (<i>orator</i>) of Grand Duke Ferdinando I of Tuscany at the Imperial court</b> in Prague. Tycho answers the Grand Duke's gracious reply (relayed via Concini) and promises to come to Prague to hear the Grand Duke's wishes in person.</li><li><b>Same-day Tuscan cluster.</b> Written the <b>same day</b> as brahe-t_16000414002 (to Raphael Ciaparenius, the Florentine ambassador's house-prefect) — both part of Tycho's cultivation of the <b>Medici/Tuscan channel</b> at Prague, the continuation of the 1598 book-gift to the Grand Duke (brahe-t_15981108001).</li><li><b>The instruments and the time-critical observations.</b> Tycho excuses a short delay: he must finish arranging the <b>buildings being prepared for his astronomical instruments</b>, and must make \"<b>certain observations</b> … to be watched for over the next several days — <i>for they do not otherwise recur within a year</i>\" (an annually-recurring configuration, e.g. of the Sun or a planet at a particular elongation). He expects to be free \"within at most fourteen days.\" A nice glimpse of observational priorities overriding court courtesy.</li><li><b><i>orator</b></i> = (resident) ambassador; <i>Serma eius Celsitudo</i> = his Most Serene Highness (the Grand Duke). <i>T. B.</i> = Tycho Brahe.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 14 Aprilis 1600</i> = from the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky</b>, 14 April 1600; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the closing honorific to 'Illustrissae Domni Vae' (Illustrissimae Dominationi Vestrae); Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.301) confirms this addresses Concini directly, not 'the Most Serene Prince', so the translation's bracketed guess is replaced with 'Your Illustrious Lordship'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the courtly Latin is rendered securely; the recipient (Concini, the Tuscan ambassador), the occasion (answering the Grand Duke through him; the same-day Florentine cluster), and the time-critical-observation excuse are all clear and corpus-consistent.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.300-302 (page images, read visually). Body text matches Dreyer word for word throughout, including the heading titulature and the date (14 April 1600). Only the closing salutation abbreviation departs from Dreyer's printed text -- a TEXT_ERROR per the grading rule, low severity since it does not affect substantive content, but per the rubric any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). The source_text's closing honorific abbreviation ('Doni Sermiae') is corrupted relative to Dreyer's printed 'Domni Vae' (i.e. 'Dominationi Vestrae', 'to Your Lordship') -- a dropped/altered word not present in the printed edition. The English's bracketed '[Prince's]' is an attempt to make sense of the corrupted Latin rather than a further factual defect, but it is downstream of the corruption. Dreyer prints: <i>Illuftrißae Domni Vae / Reverenter addictißimus / T. B.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.300-302 (full slice, read in one pass); confirmed verbatim on p.301. Single defect fixed, with a consequential correction to the translation clause it had misled.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Cosimo Concini", "count": 2, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000504001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Concini for conveying the Grand Duke of Tuscany's letter and pledges to visit Prague once instrument installations at Benátky and pending observations are attended to.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000414001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000414002", "d": "1600-04-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Raphael Ciaperenius", "ck": "Raphael Ciaperenius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Raphael Ciaparenius — Benátky, 14 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>EXCELLENTISSIMO ET ERUDITISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO RAPHAELI CIAPARENIO, APUD ILLUSTRISSIMUM ORATOREM FLORENTINUM PRAGAe DOMUS PRAeFECTO, DOMINO ET AMICO HONORANDO.</p><p>QUAS iussu Illmi et Magnifmi Oratoris Florentini ad me Excella Vra per certum tabellarium direxit literas, modo accepi, atque pro hoc mihi praestito officio plurimas eidem gratias habeo, relaturus, si quid unquam meâ opellâ praestari poterit, quod illi vicissim acceptum esse queat. Praedicavit mihi iam antea TENGNAGLIUS noster, qui ante mensem circiter in Patriam, Parentes suos invisurus, abijt, singularem Exae Vrae humanitatem et erga se benevolentiam, quam filio quoque meo, cum vobis adesset, praestitam, magni beneficij loco duco. Daboque operam, quantum in me est, ne se certasse priorem poeniteat, speroque nos, ubi Pragam advenero, ulterioris notitiae atque amicitiae fundamina coram posituros.</p><p>Interim et semper Exa Va quam optime valeat.<br>Dabantur properanter ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 14 Aprilis Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most excellent and most learned man, Master Raphael Ciaparenius, house-prefect [major-domo] with the Most Illustrious Florentine Ambassador at Prague, his lord and friend to be honoured.</b></p><p>The letters which, by command of the Most Illustrious and Magnificent Florentine Ambassador, Your Excellency directed to me by a sure courier, I have just received; and for this office rendered me I give him very many thanks, intending to repay it — if ever anything can be performed by my poor effort that may in turn be acceptable to him. Our Tengnagel — who left about a month ago for his homeland, to visit his parents — had already proclaimed to me Your Excellency's singular kindness and benevolence toward himself; which, shown also to my son when he was with you, I count in the place of a great benefit. And I shall give my effort, as much as is in me, that he may not regret having been the first to enter [this contest of courtesy]; and I hope that, when I come to Prague, we shall lay in person the foundations of further acquaintance and friendship.</p><p>Meanwhile, and always, may Your Excellency fare most well.<br>Given hastily from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 14th day of April, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Raphael Ciaparenius</b> (Ciaperenius), <i>domus praefectus</i> — the <b>house-prefect (major-domo / steward)</b> of the <b>Florentine Ambassador (orator) at Prague</b>, i.e. the resident of the Grand Duke of Tuscany at the Imperial court. A courtesy letter of thanks; not a technical letter. (The printed address runs <i>Oratorem FlorentinumPragae</i> without a space — a typesetting artefact; read <i>…Florentinum, Pragae, domus praefecto</i>.)</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> Ciaparenius had forwarded letters to Tycho at the ambassador's command; Tycho thanks him and looks forward to meeting at Prague. He notes that <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> (his assistant, see brahe-t_15990501001) had praised Ciaparenius's kindness, including a courtesy shown to <b>Tycho's son</b> (the younger Tycho) during a visit — Tengnagel having left \"about a month ago\" to visit his parents in his homeland.</li><li><b>Idiom.</b> <i>ne se certasse priorem poeniteat</i> — \"that he may not regret having been the first to compete,\" the courtesy topos of a \"contest in good offices\": Tycho will strive not to be outdone in kindness. <i>meâ opellâ</i> — \"by my poor little effort\" (modest diminutive of <i>opera</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 14 Aprilis 1600</i> = from the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky [nad Jizerou]</b>, 14 April 1600; Bohemia (imperial / Catholic) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the dropped space in the address header 'FLORENTINUM PRAGAe' (was glued as one word, 'FLORENTINUMPRAGAe'), per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.302.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a short courtesy letter; the Latin is rendered securely and the context (the Florentine embassy, Tengnagel, Tycho's son) is clear. The mangled address-line is a typesetting artefact, noted, not a crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII p.302 (read 301-303: p.301 is an unrelated same-day Tycho-to-Concinio letter, p.303 opens a following Tycho-to-Winshemius letter; target letter entirely on 302). Body text matches Dreyer verbatim ('...fundamina coram posituros...Dabantur properanter ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 14 Aprilis Anni 1600' all checked) and the English translation of the body is fully accurate. The sole finding is a glued-word TEXT_ERROR in the address header (FLORENTINUMPRAGAe for Dreyer's FLORENTINUM PRAGÆ) -- low impact on sense (the translation still renders it correctly as two concepts), but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps this at C.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints 'FLORENTINUM' and 'PRAGÆ' as two separate words in the address header; our source_text glues them into one word 'FLORENTINUMPRAGAe'. A dropped-space / glued-word departure from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>APUD ILLUSTRISSIMUM ORATOREM FLORENTINUM PRAGÆ DOMUS PRÆFECTO</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.302; the header reads unambiguously as two separate words. Only that spacing was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Ciaparenius for conveying letters from the Florentine Ambassador and for his kindness to Tengnagel and Tycho's son, and anticipates founding a personal friendship when visiting Prague.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000414002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000417001", "d": "1600-04-17", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Susanne Hájek", "ck": "Susanne Hájek", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Susanne Hájek (née Mörlin) — Benátky, 17 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (family / business) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 106f. (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>DER EDELEN VNDT VIELTHUGENDTSAMEN FRAWEN SUSANNEN HAIECKIN, GEBORNER MÖRLIN ETC. DESS HERRN D. THADDAeJ HAGAeCIJ EHLICHEN HAUSSFRAWEN, MEINER INNSONDERS GONSTIGEN VNDT BESONDERN FREUNDIN.</p><p>MEINEN freundtlichen gruß mitt wunschung alles guetten zuuor. Edele vndt vielthugendtsame fraw Susanna, ewer schreiben habe ich empfangen vndt daneben die brieffe, so Dauidt Heidlern, euern schuldenern, angehen, vndt bien willens als baldt gen Lübeck meinem factor aldo, einem vornehmen burger, deß nahmen euch itzunder bekandt, zueschreiben vndt vleissig zuuermahnen, daß er dazue thue alles, was ihm jmmer moglich sein kan, nach laut ewer vollmacht vndt ewern herren vndt euch dasselbe geldt einmahnen, vndt werdtt auß beyliegenden zettel zuesehen haben, mitt was worten ich solches vonn ihm begehren wil; wenn ich denn meinen bescheidt hieraus erlange, soll derselbe euch alsbaldt zuewissen gethan werden. Die fraw wolle aber so wohl thun, vndt mihr jhres herren doctoris Thaddaej zuestandt mitt furfallender gelegenheitt ferner wissen lassen. Der allmechtige guetige Gott verleihe ihm helf vndt beystandt, denselben ich euch auch inn seinen schütz vndt schirm trewlich thue befehlen.</p><p>Datum vff dem kayss. schloß Benatky den 17 aprilis anno 1600.</p><p>Postscripta.Ferner, lieber Jochum Boch, gebe ich euch gantz freundtlich zuewissen, daß<br>deß edelen vndt hochgelarten herrn doctoris Thaddæj Hagæcij (mitt welchem ich bey 25 Jahren guete freundtschafft vndt correspondentz gehalten)<br>haußfraw<br>Susanna Haieckin etc., mihr diese verschienene tage zugeschrieben, vndt<br>nachdem gedachter ihr herr vndt haußwirt itziger zeitt leider, beyde vonn<br>alters wegen (denn er ein mann vonn 76 Jahren) vndt anderer zuefelliger<br>schwacheitt, sehr vnpaßlich ist, so hat sie vonn seinetwegen begehret,<br>daß ich jhnen mitt gueten raht vndt hulf erscheinen wolte, das sie konte bey einem<br>der<br>alda zue Lubeck sein soll, mitt nahmen Dauidt Heidler, hundert gulden, so<br>obgedachter jhr herr ihm in bahren gelde geliehen, eingemahnet bekommen. So<br>habe ich ihr vertrostung gethan, das ihr als meiner alda besonder guetter<br>freundt vndt factor euer bestes daran thuen wurdet, wenn<br>ihr die zuegehorige brief vndt beweiß nebenst einer richtigen vollmacht bekomet, so<br>hatt sie alsbaldt mihr solches alles zuegeschickt, wie ich denn euch<br>hierneben dasselbe thue vberlieffern, vndt ist Thaddæj vndt mein gantz<br>vleissige bitt an euch, ihr wollet kein muhe undt vleiß sparen, daß diese<br>vornehme guete ehrliche leute kondten ohne weittern aufschub zue ihrer<br>bezahlung kommen; mann saget, daß derselbige Dauidt Heidler soll alda reich gefreyhet,<br>vndt deß herren stadthaubtmans aldo, eines vonn Brandensteins, tochter<br>bekommen haben, welches ich doch nicht baldt glaube. Wann ihm aber so wehre,<br>kundte er, der Heidler, leichtlich ein solche geringe schuldt ablegen, oder<br>wann das schon nicht ist, funde er doch wohl raht, sein brief wieder<br>einzulossen vndt solch geldt zuebezahlen. Wo ferne er daßelbige nicht gutwillig thuen<br>wirdt, werde ich verursacht bey römischer kayss. maiestat, vnsern gnedigsten herrn, zue sollicitiren vmb ein gnedigst schreiben an die herren<br>burgemeister vndt raht zue Lubeck, das sie obgedachten herrn Thaddæum vndt<br>seiner lieben haußfrawen zue jhrer bezahlung helffen, vndt ernstlich<br>dazue thuen, waß sich gehoret, sintemaln ich den frommen, ehrlichen mann Thaddæum als<br>meinen guetten alten freundt inn seiner jtzigen schwacheitt keinesweges<br>verlassen wil oder kan, als dann wirdt Heidler nicht allein die hauptsum,<br>sondern alle intereße vndt schäden, so darauf konnen gerechnet werden,<br>bezahlen mussen, vndt dafur zue recht stehen, daß er sein zuesage<br>vndt brief nicht gehalten, wie einem ehrlichen mann wohl anstehet, vor welchen ihr<br>jhm inn gueter meinung warnen könnet, vndt wann ihr mihr vonn meinen andern<br>sachen wiederumb zueschreibt, so last mihr auch vonn diesen richtigen<br>bescheidt wissen.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and very-virtuous lady Susanne Hájek, née Mörlin, etc., the lawful wife of Master Dr. Thaddaeus Hagecius, my especially favourable and particular [lady-]friend.</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with the wishing of all good, beforehand. Noble and very-virtuous lady Susanna, I have received your letter, and therewith the letters concerning David Heidler, your debtor; and I am minded to write at once to Lübeck, to my factor there — a distinguished burgher, whose name is now known to you — and diligently to admonish him to do everything therein that may ever be possible to him, and, according to the tenor of your power-of-attorney, to dun in that money for your lord [husband] and for you. And you will see from the enclosed note with what words I will request such [action] of him. When then I obtain my answer hereupon, the same shall at once be made known to you. But would the lady do so well as to let me know further, with arising opportunity, the condition of her lord Doctor Thaddaeus. May Almighty, kindly God grant him help and assistance — the same [Doctor Thaddaeus] I also faithfully commend, together with you, into His [God's] protection and shelter.</p><p>Given at the Imperial castle Benátky, the 17th of April, in the year 1600.</p><p><b>Postscript.</b> Further, dear Jochum Boch, I let you know in all friendliness that the wife of the noble and highly learned Lord Doctor Thaddaeus Hagecius (with whom I have kept good friendship and correspondence for some twenty-five years), Susanna Hájek, etc., wrote to me these past days; and since her aforesaid lord and husband is at present, alas, very unwell — both by reason of age (for he is a man of seventy-six years) and of another incidental infirmity — she has on his behalf requested that I would come forward to them with good counsel and help, so that she might get called in, from a certain man said to be there at Lübeck, by name David Heidler, the hundred guilders which her aforesaid lord lent him in ready money. So I have given her assurance that you, as my especial good friend and factor there, would do your best in the matter once you receive the pertinent letters and proofs together with a proper power of attorney; whereupon she at once sent me all of this, which I herewith likewise deliver over to you. And it is Thaddaeus's and my very earnest request to you that you would spare no trouble or diligence, so that these distinguished, good, honest people might come to their payment without further delay.</p><p>It is said that this same David Heidler is supposed to have married rich there, and to have gotten the daughter of the lord town-captain there, one of the von Brandensteins — which, however, I do not readily believe. But if it were so, he, Heidler, could easily discharge so small a debt; or if that is not so, he would nonetheless find a way to redeem his bond and pay such money. In so far as he will not do this of his own goodwill, I shall be moved to solicit, with the Roman Imperial Majesty, our most gracious Lord, for a most gracious letter to the lords burgomaster and council of Lübeck, that they help the aforesaid Lord Thaddaeus and his dear wife to their payment, and earnestly see to it as is proper — since I in no wise wish, nor can, abandon the pious, honest man Thaddaeus, my good old friend, in his present infirmity. Then Heidler will have to pay not only the principal sum, but all interest and damage that can be reckoned upon it, and answer in law for not having kept his promise and bond, as befits an honest man — of which you may warn him in good part. And when you write to me again about my other affairs, let me also know the proper answer concerning this.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Susanne Hájek, née Mörlin</b> (<i>Susanna Haieckin, geborne Mörlin</i>), wife of <b>Dr. Thaddaeus Hagecius (Tadeáš Hájek)</b>, Tycho's old friend and court-broker at Prague (see brahe-t_15981201001, …0530001, …0329003). A business-courtesy letter.</li><li><b>Content.</b> Tycho will help recover a debt owed Susanne (and her husband) by one <b>David Heidler</b>: he will write to his <b>factor [agent] at Lübeck</b> — \"a distinguished burgher, whose name is now known to you\" (deliberately unnamed) — instructing him, under Susanne's <b>power-of-attorney</b> (<i>Vollmacht</i>), to dun in (<i>einmahnen</i>) the money; an enclosed note (<i>beiliegender Zettel</i>) gives the form of the request. He asks her, in turn, to keep him informed of <b>Hagecius's health</b>.</li><li><b>Poignancy.</b> The closing prayer for help and assistance for Hagecius, whom Tycho \"faithfully commends … into [God's] protection and shelter,\" is touched by the fact that <b>Hagecius was failing</b> — he died in <b>September 1600</b>, a few months after this letter.</li><li><b><i>denselben ich euch auch in seinen schutz und schirm treulich tue befehlen</b></i> — <b>flagged construction</b>: read as \"the same [him, Doctor Thaddaeus] I also faithfully commend, [together] with you (<i>euch</i>, dative), into His [God's, <i>seinen</i>] protection and shelter.\" The pronoun-stacking is loose chancery German; the sense (a joint commendation of the ailing Hagecius and his wife to God) is rendered accordingly.</li><li><b>Register.</b> Courteous family/business German; the address to the gentlewoman (<i>Edle und vieltugendsame Frau</i>) and the polite third-person <i>die Frau wolle</i> (\"would the lady …\") are preserved. A draft (<i>Kladde</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>kayss. schloß Benatky den 17 aprilis anno 1600</i> = from the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky</b>, 17 April 1600; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Language and placement.</b> The recovered text is Early New High German, the `Postscripta` to Tycho's German letter of 17 April 1600 N.S. from Benátky to Susanne Hájek, née Mörlin, wife of Thaddaeus Hagecius; the postscript itself is addressed past her, to Tycho's Lübeck factor Jochum Boch. Boch is independently attested elsewhere in Tycho's German correspondence as \"mein Factor zu Lübeck, Jochum Boch\" (Opera Omnia XIV / Friis, <i>Breve og Aktstykker</i>, 1875), confirming the standing relationship this postscript presupposes.</li><li><b>Persons.</b> Thaddaeus Hagecius — Tadeáš Hájek z Hájku (1525–1600), Rudolf II's imperial physician and one of Tycho's closest Prague allies of some twenty-five years' standing (cf. Hockey et al., <i>Biographical Encyclopedia of Astronomers</i>) — is here reported gravely unwell; the letter's \"76 Jahren\" runs slightly ahead of his actual age (about 74–75 in April 1600), whether Tycho's approximation or Susanna's own report relayed through her. His wife Susanna, née Mörlin, is the addressee of the main letter this postscript closes.</li><li><b>The debt and Tycho's leverage.</b> David Heidler, resident at Lübeck and said to have married a daughter of the local Stadthauptmann \"von Brandenstein,\" owed Hagecius 100 gulden lent in cash. Tycho threatens, failing voluntary payment, to solicit a letter from Rudolf II to the Lübeck Bürgermeister and Rat — a small, concrete instance of Tycho using his imperial standing to press a friend's private debt.</li><li>Crux: neither \"David Heidler\" nor the Lübeck \"von Brandenstein\" family is identified beyond this letter in the consulted corpus (Christianson, Dreyer, Friis, the <i>Tycho Brahes Breve</i> register); a firm identification would need Lübeck civic/Stadthauptmann records outside our current holdings.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German is rendered securely and the situation (the Heidler debt, the Lübeck factor, the power-of-attorney, the inquiry after Hagecius's health) is clear; grade [B] only for the loosely-stacked pronouns of the closing commendation (<i>denselben … euch … in seinen schutz</i>), which is flagged and rendered by sense rather than smoothed.</p><p>The German is clear, formally regular Early New High German throughout, with no lacunae or corrupt readings, and the mercantile/legal vocabulary (factor, Vollmacht, Bürgermeister und Rat) translates unambiguously. The only soft points are two proper names — David Heidler and von Brandenstein — for which the consulted corpus yields no independent identification beyond what the letter itself states. The translation is otherwise straightforward but uncollated against any published edition, hence not graded higher.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 page images of the Dreyer slice (TBOO XIV pp.197-200). The letter proper (no. 192) runs from the address heading on p.198 through the postscript's close on p.199 ('...richtigen bescheidt wissen.'), with pp.197 and 200 checked as neighbouring-page controls confirming no truncation before/after. Collated the source_text word-for-word against Dreyer's printed German (differences are only long-s/short-s typeface rendering, e.g. 'kayff.' vs 'kayss.', both the standard abbreviation of 'kayserlich') — no TEXT_ERROR. Checked every proper name, number, date, and sum against Dreyer's print: Susanna Haieckin/Hájek née Mörlin, Thaddaeus Hagecius, Jochum Boch, David Heidler, 'von Brandensteins' (identified correctly as the Lübeck town-captain's family, not Heidler's), 25 Jahre (friendship), 76 Jahre (Thaddaeus's age), hundert gulden (100 guilders), Benatky, Lübeck, 17 aprilis 1600 — all render correctly with no invented or altered figures. Checked the postscript's addressee switch (letter salutation to Susanna Hájek, postscript addressed to 'lieber Jochum Boch') is preserved, not conflated. Checked pronoun/antecedent fidelity throughout, including the ambiguous closing clause 'denselben ich euch auch inn seinen schütz vndt schirm trewlich thue befehlen', which the translation renders as a defensible reading with bracketed interpretive glosses ('[Doctor Thaddaeus]', '[God's]') rather than an unmarked assertion. Checked sentence count and order in the postscript (9 clauses/sentences) against the English paragraph breaks — none silently dropped, none added. No fabricated day-numbers, no fabricated closing material, no flipped pronouns, and no custody/archive claim present in either source_text or published_english to misattribute. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 106 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Promises to write to his Lübeck factor to pursue Susanne's debtor David Heidler on her behalf, and asks for news of her husband Dr. Thaddaeus Hájek's health.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000417001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000418001", "d": "1600-04-18", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, ~18 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>MITTO, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, ut libri, quos petijsti, ad te transferantur, his iuncta</p><p>IOANNIS VIRDUNGI tabulas resolutas de supputandis motibus syderum,</p><p>Tabulas Prutenicas ERASMI RHEINHOLDI,</p><p>COPERNICUM de triangulis et Algorithmum demonstratum No. 76.</p><p>Habemus exemplar graece manuscriptum HIPPARCHI Bithyni in ARATI et EUDOXI Phaenomena librorum 3, cum Asterismis (uti inscribitur in Codice Florentiae Ao 1567 impresso. Nec enim Asterismi illi sunt HIPPARCHI, nec forte reliqua) et alterum exemplar Magnae Syntaxeos CL. PTOLEMAeI, literâ pervetustâ et eleganti conscriptum. Quod si tibi dubiae lectiones occurrant, lubens admodum et sedulo conferam et ad te perscribam. Nec inpraesentiarum occurrunt iam alia, sed hisce tibi officia mea promptissime offero.</p><p>Datae (18 Apr. circiter) A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>I send, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians — so that the books you sought may be conveyed to you — joined herewith:</p><p>the <i>Resolved Tables</i> of <b>Johann Virdung</b>, on computing the motions of the stars;</p><p>the <i>Prutenic Tables</i> of <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>;</p><p><b>Copernicus</b> <i>On Triangles</i>, and the <i>Algorithm Demonstrated</i>, No. 76.</p><p>We have a Greek manuscript exemplar of <b>Hipparchus</b> of Bithynia, <i>on the Phaenomena of Aratus and Eudoxus</i>, in 3 books, with the <i>Asterismi</i> (as it is inscribed in the codex printed at Florence in the year 1567 — for those <i>Asterismi</i> are not Hipparchus's, nor perhaps the rest); and another exemplar of the <i>Great Syntaxis</i> of <b>Claudius Ptolemy</b>, written in a very ancient and elegant hand. And if doubtful readings should occur to you, I will most gladly and diligently collate them and write them out to you. Nor at present do other [matters] occur; but with these I most readily offer you my services.</p><p>Given (about 18 April), in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (see the Munich series), forwarding a further consignment from Tycho's desiderata and offering to collate two important Greek manuscripts in his keeping.</li><li><b>Printed books sent.</b> <i>Ioannis Virdungi tabulae resolutae</i> = the <i>Tabulae resolutae</i> (for computing planetary motions) of <b>Johann Virdung of Hassfurt</b>. <i>Tabulae Prutenicae Erasmi Rheinholdi</i> = the <b>Prutenic Tables</b> of <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b> (1551), the Copernican-based tables. <i>Copernicus de triangulis</i> = <b>Copernicus</b>, <i>De lateribus et angulis triangulorum</i> (the trigonometric tract, Wittenberg 1542), here bound with an <i>Algorithmus demonstratus</i> (No. 76).</li><li><b>Greek manuscripts offered.</b> <i>Hipparchi Bithyni in Arati et Eudoxi Phaenomena libri 3</i> = <b>Hipparchus of Nicaea (Bithynia)</b>, <i>Commentary on the Phaenomena of Aratus and Eudoxus</i> — the only surviving work of Hipparchus, in three books, with an appended <i>Asterismi</i> (star-list); Herwart notes, shrewdly, that the <i>Asterismi</i> \"are not Hipparchus's, nor perhaps the rest [of the appended matter],\" citing the <b>Florence 1567</b> printed edition's own rubric. The second is \"another exemplar of the <i>Great Syntaxis</i> [the <b>Almagest</b>] of <b>Ptolemy</b>,\" in a very old and elegant Greek hand. Herwart offers to collate <i>dubiae lectiones</i> (doubtful readings) for Tycho — a scholarly textual-criticism service.</li><li><b><i>Magna Syntaxis</b></i> = Ptolemy's <i>Almagest</i> (Gk <i>Μεγάλη Σύνταξις</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>. The dateline is itself <b>approximate in the source</b> — <i>Datae (18 Apr. circiter)</i> \"given about 18 April\"; the header therefore carries \"~18 April 1600,\" matching the dataset's \"(18 Apr. circiter).\"</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — short and secure; the bibliographic identifications (Virdung, Reinhold, Copernicus <i>De triangulis</i>, the Hipparchus <i>Commentary</i> and the Greek <i>Almagest</i>) are firm, and Herwart's own caveat about the spurious <i>Asterismi</i> is preserved, not sharpened. The \"about\" in the dateline is reproduced as the source's own approximation.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.302 page image in full. Collated the book list (Virdung's Resolved Tables, Reinhold's Prutenic Tables, Copernicus De triangulis + Algorithmus No.76), the Hipparchus/Aratus-Eudoxus Greek manuscript description with its 1567 Florence-codex parenthesis, the Ptolemy Great Syntaxis manuscript description, the offer to collate doubtful readings, and the '18 Apr. circiter 1600' date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 8, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_16000411001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991231001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends several books Tycho requested, including the Prutenic Tables and Copernicus On Triangles, and reports manuscript exemplars of Hipparchus and Ptolemy's Great Syntaxis available for collation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000418001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000420001", "d": "1600-04-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Vitus Winshemius — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 20 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD VITUM WINSHEMIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>EXCELLENTISSIME et Eruditissime Domine Doctor VITE WINSHEMI, Amice honorande: Literae Excellentiae Tuae humanissimae et candidi erga me amoris refertissimae bis vel ter, postquam in Bohemiam concessi, mihi redditae sunt; pro quo constanti et sincero erga me affectu gratias habeo plurimas. Memini etiam me Tuae Excae aliquoties rescripsisse, eamque meas pariter accepisse spero. Quantum vero ad ultimas, quas Exa Tua per Civem illum Hamburgensem, quibus Rollenhagianae adiunctae erant, dederat, commodum accidit, quod cum Pragae essem (ablegaturus TENGNAGLIUM nostrum, cum quodam ex meis domesticis, Pilsnam in Aulam Caesaris) eae mihi redderentur. Nam per emissarios meos negotium illud Civis, cuius caussam mihi commendâsti, Domino BARVITIO, summo Secretario, exponi iussi, eumque, ut petitioni eius non deesse velit, diligenter ex me rogavi: quod et factum est. Nam alter ille hisce diebus huc reversus (TENGNAGLIUS enim in suam Patriam inde profectus est, post aliquot septimanas huc, Deo volente, rediturus) retulit Dominum BARVITIUM in se recepisse negotij illius promotionem, quamprimum Civis iste Pilsnam veniret et caussam illam sibi proponeret; quod tamen necdum factum erat, antequam domesticus ille meus inde discederet, qui per integrum mensem ibi substitit. Miror itaque, ubi interea Civis ille fuit, et an Pragae remanserit, Caesaris isthuc reditum expectaturus, ignoro. Semel enim tantummodo mecum Pragae locutus est; postea hominem non vidi, quamvis volebam, ut denuo me accederet, quo plenius eius causae circumstantias cognoscere possem; quas etiam ut FRANCISCO indicaret, iussi. Si in alijs longe maioribus Tuae Exae in his oris gratificari unquam potero, habebit me semper quam promptissimum et nostrae amicitiae perpetuo memorem.</p><p>Quae ad OLIGERUM ROSAeKRANTZIUM communem Amicum proferre volui, ignoro an acceperit, multo enim iam tempore nihil ad me literarum dedit. Faciat igitur T. E. me certiorem, quomodo vivat et valeat, utque subinde mihi scribat, ipsum admoneat.</p><p>Vale diu et feliciter.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benaticâ Die 20 Aprilis stylo recognito Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Vitus Winshemius.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most excellent and most learned Lord Doctor Veit Winsheim, honoured friend: Your Excellency's most courteous letters, brimming with candid love toward me, have been delivered to me two or three times since I withdrew into Bohemia; for which constant and sincere affection toward me I give you many thanks. I recall, too, that I wrote back to Your Excellency several times, and I hope you likewise received mine. As for the last, which Your Excellency had sent through that Hamburg citizen — to which the Rollenhagen letters were attached — it fell out conveniently that they were delivered to me while I was at Prague (about to dispatch our Tengnagel, together with one of my servants, to Pilsen to the Emperor's court). For through my emissaries I had that citizen's business — whose cause you commended to me — laid before Lord Barvitius, the supreme Secretary, and diligently asked him on my behalf to be willing not to fail his petition; and this was done. For that other man [my servant], having returned hither these last days (Tengnagel, that is, set off from there for his homeland, to return hither, God willing, after some weeks), reported that Lord Barvitius had taken upon himself the furtherance of that business as soon as the citizen should come to Pilsen and put his case to him — which, however, had not yet been done before that servant of mine departed thence, who tarried there a whole month. I wonder, therefore, where that citizen was in the meantime; and whether he stayed on at Prague to await the Emperor's return thither, I do not know. For he spoke with me only once, at Prague; afterward I did not see the man, although I wished him to come to me again, so that I might more fully learn the circumstances of his case — which I also ordered him to set out to Franciscus. If ever I shall be able to oblige Your Excellency in other, far greater matters in these regions, you shall ever find me most ready and perpetually mindful of our friendship.</p><p>What I wished to convey to our common friend Holger Rosenkrantz, I do not know whether he received, for he has now sent me no letter for a long time. Let Your Excellency therefore inform me how he lives and fares, and remind him to write to me from time to time.</p><p>Farewell, long and happily.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 20th day of April, by the corrected style, of the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Vitus Winshemius</i> = <b>Veit Ortel von Windsheim (Vitus Winshemius the Younger)</b> (1542–1608), physician and professor at <b>Wittenberg</b> — Tycho's correspondent and intermediary in the Saxon/Wittenberg academic world. Addressed throughout as <i>Excellentia Tua</i>, \"Your Excellency\" (§5.5).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Rollenhagiana[e literae]</i> = letters of <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector at Magdeburg, enclosed with Winshemius's packet; Rollenhagen's own letter to Tycho of 15 May 1601 survives as brahe-t_16010515001.</li><li><i>Tengnaglius / Franciscus</i> = <b>Franz Gansneb Tengnagel von Camp</b>, Tycho's noble assistant (and future son-in-law), here being sent to the imperial court at Pilsen and then travelling on to his Westphalian homeland.</li><li><i>Dominus Barvitius, summus Secretarius</i> = <b>Johann Anton Barvitius (Barwitz)</b>, imperial secretary to Rudolf II — Tycho's channel for promoting the petition of an unnamed <i>Civis</i> (citizen) whose cause Winshemius had commended.</li><li><i>Oligerus Rosaekrantzius</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned\"</b> (1574–1642), Danish nobleman and theologian, the \"common friend\" whose silence Tycho remarks.</li><li><i>Civis ille Hamburgensis / Civis</i> = the unnamed <b>Hamburg citizen</b> who carried the post, and the (same or another) <b>petitioner</b> whose suit at court Tycho was advancing.</li><li><b>Places &amp; events.</b> <i>Praga</i> = Prague; <i>Pilsna</i> = <b>Plzeň</b>, where Rudolf II's court had withdrawn (cf. brahe-t_16000508001); the petitioner was to follow the Emperor there.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting\"); <i>Vale diu et feliciter</i> → \"Farewell, long and happily\"; <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary) → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — idiom of note).</b> Dateline <i>Die 20 Aprilis <b>stylo recognito</b> Anni 1600</i> — \"by the <b>corrected / reformed style</b>,\" i.e. <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b>. <i>Stylo recognito</i> is one of Tycho's variant labels for the Gregorian reckoning (alongside <i>stylo novo</i>) now standard in his Imperial-service correspondence; rendered literally and glossed, not silently converted (protocol §3).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'cum is quodam' corrected to 'cum quodam', and 'reddererentur' corrected to 'redderentur', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 303.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean humanist Latin; the named persons (Winshemius, Rollenhagen, Tengnagel, Barvitius, Holger Rosenkrantz) are all securely identified, and the only anonymous figure (the petitioning <i>Civis</i>) is unnamed in the source itself. No reading crux; the <i>stylo recognito</i> idiom is handled per protocol.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.303 as page images (slice pages 1-3; the letter runs entirely on slice page 2, with pp.1 and 3 being the neighboring Ciaparenio/Herwartus and Ludolphus Riddershusius letters used only as slice padding). Collated the opening salutation, both body paragraphs, and the closing formula, plus every proper name (Vitus Winshemius/Veit Winsheim, Tengnagel, Barvitius, Franciscus, Oligerus Rosaekrantzius/Holger Rosenkrantz), place name (Benatica/Benátky, Pilsna/Pilsen), and date (Die 20 Aprilis, stylo recognito, Anni 1600) across source_text, Dreyer, and published_english. All names, the date, and the closing formula match across all three. The English translation contains no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS relative to source_text; antecedents (e.g. \"who tarried there a whole month\" correctly referring to the unnamed servant, not Tengnagel; \"remind him to write\" correctly referring to Rosenkrantz, not Winshemius) are all correctly resolved. However, our published source_text itself departs from Dreyer's print in two small spots (an inserted \"is\" and a dittographic \"reddererentur\" for \"redderentur\"), both TEXT_ERROR by the audit's rules; neither changes the sense conveyed by the translation, so the verdict is MINOR, but any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published source_text inserts an extra pronoun \"is\" between \"cum\" and \"quodam\" that Dreyer's print does not have; it is also grammatically anomalous (nominative \"is\" cannot follow \"cum\" here). The translation's meaning is unaffected either way. Dreyer prints: <i>ablegaturus TENGNAGLIUM nostrum, cum quodam ex meis domesticis, Pilsnam in Aulam Cæsaris (p.303, line 14-15; no \"is\")</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published source_text has a duplicated syllable (\"reddererentur\") not present in Dreyer's printed \"redderentur\" (imperfect subjunctive passive of reddo). A dittography in our transcription; the translation itself renders the correct sense regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>eae mihi redderentur (p.303, line 15-16, split across the line break as \"red-\" / \"derentur\")</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.302-304 page images. Both corrections are unambiguous on p.303. Neither affects the English translation, which was already faithful to the correct sense. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Vitus Winshemius", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15980328001", "next": "brahe-t_16000813001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15980311001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000813001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Winshemius for repeated correspondence and reports having interceded with Lord Barvitius through Tengnagel on behalf of a Hamburg citizen Winshemius had commended.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000420001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000420002", "d": "1600-04-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "ck": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ludolf Riddershausen — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 20 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD LUDOLPHUM RIDDERSHUSIUM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et Eruditissime Vir, Amice mihi plurimum semper faciende. Literae tuae paulo post solstitium hybernum superioris Anni ad me datae praeterlapso nuperrimo huius Aequinoctio mihi primum redditae sunt, quibus si per omnia nunc ita luculenter respondere, uti desiderares, non concedatur, tribuas id occupationum mearum moli; quae tamen otium utcunque admittit, brevibus exposita, sic habe.</p><p>De Directionum administratione et rationibus, tum quoque distributione Coeli, quae antea significavi, satis per se clara sunt, nec, etiamsi quam maxime vellem, ea exemplis illustrandi nunc suppetit oportunitas. Possunt vero secundum Eclipticam pariter Directiones fieri, non dissimiliter, iuxta motum ☉lis diurnum, numerandae, nam et has subinde pondus habere animadverti. Debent autem Directiones institui ad Horizontem eius loci, in quo quis natus est, non autem ubi postea habitat; quia quilibet suum secum portat Coelum suasque influentias, quocunque iverit. Octogenaria Thematum distributio ad hoc conducit, ut robur efficaciae Planetarum rectius pateat, siquidem dimidius Angulus Rectus sit inter Meridianum et Horizontem, post horum duorum limites, magni sunt roboris. Significata autem ex duodenaria Divisione potius consueto Astrologorum more petenda.</p><p>Transitus ♄ni, quia tardigradus est, utplurimum vim habet, ♂tis autera minus. Tunc vero stimulantur effectus, cum in idem tempus similes profectiones coincidunt; impediuntur vero, si contrariae; et Directiones, quae generaliorem habent significationem, hîc etiam augendo vel minuendo plurimum possunt. Si eodem tempore inciderint adversae et prosperae Directiones, seipsas fere elidunt, et effectum boni malique imminuunt. Directiones, quae exitum naturalem vel violentem portendunt, variae se habent, nec ad certum aliquod genus restringi possunt; et oportet multa alia simul considerare, nec Stoicam necessitatem introducere. Geniturarum influxus fausti vel infelices vario modo mutari et transplantari possunt; ut non nimium ijs sit indulgendum. Omnes autem rationes, quibus hoc efficitur, nimis longum foret enarrare. Neque etiam in omnes homines Astra aequaliter influunt, sed quidam se reddunt eorum viribus magis obnoxios quam alij. Verum Deus, Author Universi, omnia pro suo nutu moderatur. Atque haec ad quaestiones tuas pro tempore respondisse sufficiat.</p><p>Ad tempora et eventus Nativitatum, quas misisti, attendere et in eas disquirere nunc non vacat; imo etiamsi vacaret, non multum temporis talibus curis libenter tribuo, gravioribus et maioris momenti studijs potius addictus. Restitutionem motus ☾ris nunc communicare non possum, nondum enim typis edita est, uti neque in coeteris Planetis desiderijs tuis morem gerere. Conservantur adhuc libri mei et res, pro maiori parte, Magdaeburgi; ubi advenerint, et oportunius otium nactus fuero, dabo operam, ut plenius in talibus gratificari queam. Literas adiunctas ad Dominum DAVIDEM FABRICIUM absque omni interposita morâ per certum hominem dirige; eius enim interest, ut eas cito nanciscatur.</p><p>Iamque vale, quodque pluribus nunc tecum non agam, candide excusatum habe.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ, 5 milliaribus Pragâ distante, Die 20 Aprilis Anni 1600, stylo recognito.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO LUDOLF RIDDERSHAUSEN.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and most learned Man, a friend always to be highly esteemed by me. Your letter, sent to me a little after the winter solstice of last year, was first delivered to me only at the most recent equinox just past; to which, if it be not now granted me to reply so fully in every respect as you would wish, attribute it to the mass of my occupations. Yet what my leisure does, in any case, allow — set out briefly — take thus.</p><p>Concerning the management and methods of <b>Directions</b>, and also the <b>distribution of the Heaven</b>, which I indicated before, [they] are clear enough in themselves, nor — even if I most greatly wished it — is there now opportunity to illustrate them with examples. Directions can indeed equally be made according to the Ecliptic, to be reckoned not differently, by the Sun's diurnal motion — for I have observed that these too sometimes have weight. But Directions ought to be set up to the Horizon of the place in which a person was born, not where he afterward dwells; because each carries his own Heaven with him, and his own influences, wherever he goes. The eight-fold distribution of the Figures serves this end, that the strength of the Planets' efficacy may more rightly appear, since a half right-angle [45°] lies between the Meridian and the Horizon; beyond the bounds of these two, [the planets] are of great strength. But the significations are rather to be sought from the <b>twelve-fold Division</b> [the twelve houses], after the customary manner of the Astrologers.</p><p>The transit of <b>Saturn</b>, because it is slow-moving, has for the most part [great] force; that of <b>Mars</b> less. The effects are stimulated when similar <b>profections</b> coincide at the same time, but are impeded if contrary; and Directions, which have a more general signification, can here too do much, by increasing or diminishing. If at the same time adverse and prosperous Directions fall together, they almost cancel each other out, and lessen the effect of good and of ill. Directions which portend a natural or a violent death vary, and cannot be restricted to any fixed kind; and one must consider many other things at once, and not introduce <b>Stoic necessity</b>. The auspicious or unlucky influxes of <b>genitures</b> can be altered and transplanted in various ways, so that they are not to be indulged too far. But to relate all the methods by which this is effected would be too long. Nor do the Stars influence all men equally, but some render themselves more subject to their forces than others. Yet God, the Author of the Universe, governs all things according to his will. And let it suffice to have answered these things to your questions, for the time.</p><p>To attend to the times and outcomes of the <b>Nativities</b> you sent, and to inquire into them, there is now no leisure; indeed, even were there leisure, I do not willingly give much time to such cares, being rather devoted to weightier studies of greater moment. The <b>restitution of the Moon's motion</b> I cannot now share, for it is not yet published in print — nor [can I] gratify your wishes in the other Planets. My books and things are still kept, for the greater part, at Magdeburg; when they shall have arrived, and I have obtained more suitable leisure, I will take pains to gratify [you] more fully in such matters. The enclosed letter to Lord <b>David Fabricius</b>, direct without any interposed delay, by a sure man; for it is in his interest to obtain it quickly.</p><p>And now farewell; and that I do not deal with you at more length now, hold me candidly excused.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, distant 5 miles from Prague, the 20th day of April, the year 1600, in the recognised [New] style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ludolf Riddershausen</b> (<i>Ludolphus Riddershusius</i>), a learned correspondent who had submitted <b>astrological questions and nativities</b> to Tycho. (No prior letter to/from him in the corpus; precise biography to firm up.) His letter, sent \"a little after the winter solstice\" of 1599, reached Tycho only at the spring equinox of 1600.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's fullest brief statement on predictive-astrology technique.</b> A compact exposition of his views (protocol §5.3, <i>Astrology</i>), kept on the controlled terms:</li><li><b>Directions</b> (<i>Directiones</i>) and the <b>distribution / division of the heaven</b> (house systems). Directions may also be reckoned along the <b>Ecliptic</b> by the Sun's diurnal motion; they must be cast to the <b>Horizon of the birthplace</b>, \"because each carries his own Heaven with him … wherever he goes.\"</li><li><b>Two house schemes contrasted:</b> the <b>eight-fold distribution</b> (quadrants halved at the 45° half-right-angle between Meridian and Horizon) best shows \"the strength of the Planets' efficacy,\" while the <b>significations</b> are sought from the customary <b>twelve-fold division</b> (the twelve houses).</li><li><b>Transits &amp; profections:</b> <b>Saturn</b>'s transit (slow) is potent, <b>Mars</b>'s less; effects are reinforced when <b>profections</b> agree, cancelled when they conflict; concurrent adverse and prosperous <b>Directions</b> \"almost cancel each other out.\"</li><li><b>Death-directions</b> vary and resist fixed rules; \"one must consider many things at once, <b>and not introduce Stoic necessity</b>.\"</li><li><b>Free-will caveat (doctrinally important):</b> the influxes of <b>genitures</b> \"can be altered and transplanted in various ways … not to be indulged too far\"; the Stars do not influence all men equally; and \"<b>God, the Author of the Universe, governs all things according to his will.</b>\" Tycho also distances himself from the practice — he declines to work up Riddershausen's nativities, \"being rather devoted to weightier studies\" (cf. the same posture in brahe-t_16000120001).</li><li><b>Glyphs &amp; terms (kept; protocol §5.1/§5.3).</b> ☉ = <b>Sun</b>, ♄ = <b>Saturn</b>, ♂ = <b>Mars</b>, ☾ = <b>Moon</b> (names in English, glyphs retained in the source block). <i>Directiones</i>, <i>profectiones</i>, <i>genitura</i>, <i>thema</i> per the controlled list.</li><li><b>Textual flag — <i>Octogenaria</i>.</b> <i>Octogenaria Thematum distributio</i> literally = an \"eighty-fold\" distribution, but the context (the <b>45° half-right-angle</b>, contrasted with the <i>duodenaria</i> twelve-fold division) requires the <b>eight-fold / octant (octotopos) house system</b> in which each quadrant is bisected. Rendered <b>\"eight-fold distribution\"</b> by sense; the literal <i>Octogenaria</i> is flagged as a probable form/print issue and noted here, not silently emended.</li><li><b>Other matter.</b> The <b>lunar restitution</b> is \"not yet published in print\" (cf. brahe-t_15981231001); Tycho's books are still at <b>Magdeburg</b>. He asks Riddershausen to forward an enclosed letter to <b>David Fabricius</b> (1564–1617, the Frisian pastor-astronomer, discoverer of Mira Ceti; the recurring \"David Fabricius\" thread) \"without delay.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 20 Aprilis Anni 1600, stylo recognito</i> — <i>stylo recognito</i> = the \"<b>recognised</b>\" (New/Gregorian) style; imperial. The note <i>5 milliaribus Pragâ distante</i> fixes Benátky \"5 miles from Prague.\" Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is read securely and Tycho's astrological doctrine is rendered with its free-will hedges intact (no silent sharpening); graded <b>[B]</b> for the one genuine technical-term crux — <i>Octogenaria</i> (\"eighty-fold\") read as the <b>eight-fold octant house-system</b> on contextual grounds (the 45° angle; the contrast with <i>duodenaria</i>) — flagged rather than smoothed, and for the unconfirmed identity of Riddershausen.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.304-305 in full as page images (within the 303-306 slice, flanked by the letters to Vitus Winshemius and to Herwart/Kepler). Source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word, including the planetary symbols (Sun/Saturn/Mars/Moon glyphs), the astrological technical vocabulary (octogenaria distributio, duodenaria divisio, profectiones), the addressee David Fabricius, and the closing '5 milliaribus Pragâ distante, Die 20 Aprilis Anni 1600'. Published_english is accurate throughout; upgraded from the self-assigned B since full Dreyer collation found no defects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Ludolf Riddershusen", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991216001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991216001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains his methods for astrological Directions and the distribution of the Heaven, arguing they must be calculated to the Horizon of the native's birthplace rather than current residence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000420002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000422001", "d": "1600-04-22", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christina von Halle", "ck": "Christina von Halle", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christine von Halle (widow of Henrik Rantzau) — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 22 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 111 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD VIDUAM HENRICI RANTZOVII].</p><p>EDLER vnnd vielthugendsam fraw statthalterin, jnbesonders gunstige gutte freundin. Nechst meines freundtlichen grusses vnd willigen dienstes fermeldung geb ich der frawen gantz freundlich zuwissen, das nachdem ich vernommen, daß mein vertrawtter gutter freunndt vnd blutverwanter in Dennemark, Eske Bilde, nicht zeittlich genung hat zuwissen bekommen von der hauptsumme im land zu Holstein zu bezahlen vnnd meinen brieff wiederumb einzulössen, so hab ich ihm wiederumb davon geschrieben, vnnd alle gelegenheit noch einmahl verstendiget, vnnd danebenst copien von brieffuen darzugehörig geschicht, worauff ehr mir wiederumb zugeschrieben, das er alles auff nechstkumpfftigen vmbslag in diesen lauffenden jahr von meinetwegen will richtigmachen. Bitt darumb gantzfreundtlich, die fraw stathalterin wolt eß alles hierin zum besten auffnehmen, vnnd bei dem herrn Diderich von Dingcklauben handlen, das er wolle zufrieden sein biß auf vorgenante zeit; die zinß aber, welche zu nechstvorgangnen vmbslag sollte ausgelegt werden, vorsihe ich mich sey zu rechter zeitt bezahlt. Ferner wolte ich die fraustathalterin gantz freundtlich gebeten haben, daß sie wolte mit allen den eeissenwerck vnd was anders ich alda zu Wandeßbech vmb mein eigen geldt nöttwendig machen lassen, ein richtig anordnung thun, daß eß mihr möchtte wiederumb erstattet werden, vnd nicht ferner auff dero vielgeliebten sohn Bredo, dem ich davon fleissig geschrieben (vnd er mir dog nichtes geanttuort), schicken, furnemlich dieweill, wie ich vernommen, die fraw willenß ist, gedachtem jhren sohn Wandeßbech abzuhandellen vnd selbst einzuhaben. Jch weß genugk, daß die frauw mein schaden nicht begehret, vnd was zuvor darahn von jhren lieben herrn stathalter, seligen dechtnuß, bezalt ist worden, davon die fraw woll bescheid weiß, kan davon abgeregnit werden, bitt nuhr, daß daß vbrige mihr möchtte bezalht werden, vnd meinem factor zu Hamburgk, Symon von Petkom, zugesthelt, der auch nebenst den herrn zu Hamburgk, smid Jacob Holsten, von allen bescheid weiss zu sagen, vnnd darauff zu quitiren. So fern aber der frawen nicht geliebt die vbrige eisentrallien, vnd waß sonst von eisenwergk da gemacht ist, zu behallten, bitt ich, daß gedachten meinen factor muge vergonnet werden, daß er dasselbige alleß, so fur meinem gehlt ist beköstiget vnd nicht bezalt, muge wiederumb wegknehmen, vnd ein theill davon ihn Hamburgk wiederumb zu gelde machen, daß furnembst aber, so von eissenwergk ist, mihr nebenst denn andern meinen guttern so bald nach ihr röm. kay. may., vnserm gnedigen herrn, schrifftlich an die herrn von Hamburgk begehr, dieselben heraufkommen zu schicken.</p><p>Jch vorsihe mich zur frawstathalterin, daß sie mihr diss nicht abslagen wirt, sondern mit dem ehesten, weill eß nun bei anderthalb Jahren gewehrt vnd auffgeschoben ist, ein eigentlichen bescheid hierauff machen. Wohrin ich kan die frawstathalterin oder einigen von jhren kindern nach meinem vermugen wiederumb dienstlich sein, will ich allzeitt gantz guttwillig gefunden werden, vnd thu hiemit die fraw stathalterin sambt allen den ihrigen in schütz vnd schirm deß Allmechtigen treulich befehlen.</p><p>Datirt auff dem kayserlichen slotz Benateck in Behemen den 22 thag aprilis stylo nouo anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO THE WIDOW OF HENRIK RANTZAU].</b></p><p>Noble and most virtuous Lady governor's-wife, especially favourable good friend. Next [after] the declaration of my friendly greeting and willing service, I give the Lady quite amicably to know that, after I learned that my trusted good friend and blood-relation in Denmark, Eske Bille, did not get to know in time enough about paying the principal sum in the land of Holstein and redeeming my bond, I have written to him again about it, and explained the whole situation once more, and sent besides copies of the relevant letters; whereupon he has written back to me that he will settle everything on my behalf at the next <i>Umschlag</i> in this current year. I therefore ask quite amicably that the Lady governor's-wife would take it all herein for the best, and deal with Lord Diderich von Dincklage, that he would be content until the aforesaid time; but the interest which was to be laid out at the last past <i>Umschlag</i>, I assume has been paid at the right time. Further, I would quite amicably have asked the Lady governor's-wife that she would make a proper arrangement concerning all the ironwork, and whatever else I had necessarily made there at Wandsbek with my own money, that it might be reimbursed to me — and not [be deferred] further upon her much-beloved son Breide, to whom I have written diligently about it (and he has yet answered me nothing) — especially since, as I have learned, the Lady intends to buy out Wandsbek from her said son and hold it herself. I know well enough that the Lady does not desire my loss; and what was previously paid toward it by her dear Lord governor of blessed memory, of which the Lady well knows the account, can be reckoned off from it. I ask only that the remainder might be paid to me, and delivered to my factor at Hamburg, Simon von Petkum — who, together with the Hamburg man, the smith Jacob Holst, knows how to give account of everything and to give a receipt thereupon. But insofar as it does not please the Lady to keep the remaining iron lattice-work, and whatever else of ironwork has been made there, I ask that my said factor may be permitted to take it all away again — that which was paid for with my money and not [re]paid — and to turn part of it into money again at Hamburg; the chief part, however, which is of ironwork, [I ask] to be sent up to me, together with my other goods, as soon as His Roman Imperial Majesty, our gracious Lord, requests of the Lords of Hamburg in writing that they be sent up.</p><p>I trust the Lady governor's-wife that she will not refuse me this, but will — since it has now dragged on and been postponed for about a year and a half — make a definite decision hereupon as soon as possible. In whatever I can in turn be of service to the Lady governor's-wife, or any of her children, according to my ability, I will at all times be found quite willing; and I herewith faithfully commend the Lady governor's-wife, together with all hers, to the protection and shelter of the Almighty.</p><p>Dated at the imperial castle Benátky in Bohemia, the 22nd day of April, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christine von Halle</b> (d. 1604), <b>widow of Henrik (Heinrich) Rantzau</b> (1526–1598), the great Holstein humanist and royal <i>Statthalter</i> (lieutenant-governor) of the Duchies — hence Tycho's address <i>Frau Statthalterin</i> (\"Lady governor's-wife\"). The source header reads simply <i>Ad viduam Henrici Rantzovii</i> (\"to the widow of Henrik Rantzau\"); the dataset supplies her name. The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.<br>- <b>Significance — Tycho's unfinished business at Wandsbek (material history).</b> Wandsbek, near Hamburg, was the <b>Rantzau estate</b> where Tycho lived 1597–98 (printing the <i>Mechanica</i> and <i>Progymnasmata</i>). Two matters:<br>  1. <b>The Mecklenburg principal &amp; the Kiel <i>Umschlag</i>.</b> Tycho's kinsman <b>Eske Bille</b> had not learned in time how to pay \"the principal sum in Holstein\" and redeem Tycho's bond; Tycho has re-instructed him, and Bille will settle \"at the next <b><i>Umschlag</b></i>\" (the <b>Kiel Umschlag</b>, the great Holstein payment-fair where debts were cleared) this year. Tycho asks the widow to keep the creditor <b>Diderich von Dincklage</b> patient until then.<br>  2. <b>The Wandsbek ironwork (notable).</b> Tycho seeks <b>reimbursement for all the ironwork</b> (<i>Eisenwerk</i>) and other things he had \"necessarily made at Wandsbek with my own money,\" rather than have it left to the widow's son <b>Breide (Bredo) Rantzau</b> (who has not answered) — especially as she intends to <b>buy Wandsbek back from her son</b>. Failing reimbursement, his Hamburg <b>factor Simon von Petkum</b> (with the <b>Hamburg smith Jacob Holst</b>) is to <b>remove the iron</b>, sell part at Hamburg, and send the <b>chief ironwork up the Elbe</b> with Tycho's other goods (once Rudolf II writes to Hamburg) — a concrete glimpse of the <b>iron fittings/instrument components</b> Tycho commissioned during the Wandsbek period.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Christine von Halle (the widow); <b>Henrik Rantzau</b> (the late <i>Statthalter</i>); <b>Breide Rantzau</b> (her son); <b>Eske Bille</b> (Tycho's kinsman-agent); <b>Diderich von Dincklage</b> (creditor/agent); <b>Simon von Petkum</b> (Tycho's Hamburg factor; cf. brahe-t_15991008001, brahe-t_16000424001); <b>Jacob Holst</b> (Hamburg smith); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Terms (kept/glossed; protocol §2).</b> <i>Umschlag</i> = the <b>Kiel payment-fair</b>; <i>Eisenwerk / Eisentraillen</i> = ironwork / <b>iron lattice-work (grilles/railings)</b>, kept and glossed (the <i>Eisentraillen</i> reading is the edition's; flagged minor).<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benateck in Behemen den 22 thag Aprilis stylo novo anni 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague/imperial). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin heading 'VIDUAMHENRICI' corrected to 'VIDUAM HENRICI' (missing space) per Dreyer, TBOO XIV p.200. A second audited defect (ausgelegt/auffgelegt) was checked against the same page and rejected: Dreyer's page in fact reads 'ausgelegt', matching the existing text, not the 'auffgelegt' the audit claimed.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery German; the two matters (the Mecklenburg-principal settlement via Eske Bille at the Kiel <i>Umschlag</i>, with Dincklage to be kept patient; and the reimbursement-or-return of the Wandsbek ironwork via Simon von Petkum and the smith Jacob Holst) and every named party are explicit and consistent with the debt-thread and Wandsbek context. No textual crux; <i>Eisentraillen</i> (\"iron lattice-work\") is the one minor term, glossed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.197-202 as page images. Letter no. 193 to Christine von Halle runs from the foot of p.200 ('[TYCHO BRAHE AD VIDUAM HENRICI RANTZOVII].') through the closing date on p.201 ('Datirt auff dem kayferlichen flotz Benateck in Behemen den 22 thag aprilis ftylo nouo anni 1600.'). Collated the full letter word for word against Dreyer's German text, and checked every proper name and figure: Eske Bille, Diderich von Dincklage/Dingcklauben, Breide (the widow's son), Simon von Petkum, the smith Jacob Holst, Wandsbek, Benátky in Bohemia, 'anderthalb Jahren' (a year and a half), and the closing date 22 April N.S. 1600, all of which check out. Found two TEXT_ERRORs in our published German (a glued word in Dreyer's own bracketed heading, and 'ausgelegt' for Dreyer's 'auffgelegt'), the second of which mildly shades the English's rendering of one clause but does not change the letter's overall sense. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found elsewhere in the translation.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin heading glues 'VIDUAM' and 'HENRICI' together (missing space) where Dreyer p.200 prints them as two words; a cosmetic corruption confined to Dreyer's own editorial bracketed heading, not the letter body, and it does not affect the English. Dreyer prints: <i>[TYCHO BRAHE AD VIDUAM HENRICI RANTZOVII].</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published German reads 'ausgelegt' (paid out/disbursed) where Dreyer p.200 reads 'auffgelegt' (levied/imposed/assessed) -- a real word substitution, not just spelling. The English ('laid out') follows our source's reading rather than Dreyer's actual sense ('levied'), but the overall point of the sentence -- that the interest due was assumed settled on time -- is not lost. Dreyer prints: <i>die zinß aber, welche zu nechftvorgangnen vmbflag folte auffgelegt werden, vorfihe ich mich fey zu rechter zeitt bezahlt.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified visually against Dreyer page images, TBOO XIV pp.197-200 (the full slice supplied). The heading-spacing defect is confirmed and fixed. The 'ausgelegt/auffgelegt' defect was investigated directly on p.200 and found unsupported by the actual page image, which reads 'ausgelegt' clearly; this is rejected rather than applied, since applying it would have introduced an unverified change against the edition of record. Nothing else on these pages diverges from current_latin.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 111 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that kinsman Eske Bille failed to redeem a Holstein bond on time but will settle at the next Umschlag, asking her to secure patience from Dincklage.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000422001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000424001", "d": "1600-04-24", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Vincent Moller", "ck": "Vincent Moller", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Vincent Moller — Benátky, 24 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 113 (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO D:NO VINCENTIO MOLLERO, AMPLISSIMAe REIPUB. HAMBURGENSIS CONSULI PRUDENTISSIMO ET V. I. LICENTIATO EXIMIO, AMICO SUO HONORANDO.</p><p>MAGNIFICE et excellentissime do:ie Vincenti Mullere, amice honorande, pro nupera euectione ad dignitatem consularem in amplissima vestra republica E:ae T. ex animo congratulor, optoque, ut fausta et salutaris sit. Ago vero gratias, quod me humanissimis tuis literis inuisere non degrauatus sis, et negocium apud reliquos dignissimos consules et senatores vrbis vestrae, de quo caes:a ma:tas, d:nus noster clementissimus, meo nomine perscripsit, promoturum polliceris. Spero autem, literas ipsius m:tis iam ad vos peruenisse, aut cum instrumentis et rebus meis, quae Lubecae sunt, breui allatum irj. Quare E:m T:m diligenter et peramice rogatam cupio, ut sua authoritate et intercessione procurare velit, quo per omnia c:ae m:tis clementissimae voluntatj meaeque expectationi satisfiat. De quibus Simon de Petkum te plenius instruet, ejus enim fidej et curae rem omnem commisj. Scripsi etiam denuo illi de ijs, quae Wandesburgj meo aere parata sunt et nondum persoluta, ut compensationem urgeat, in quo etiam negocio Praestantia Tua suo consilio et auxilio, praesertim quo ad vestrae vrbis fabrum et caeteros opifices, qui sciunt, quantum exposuerim illisque numerauerim, quam optime mihi subueniet, nec diffido eam, vt id ingenue fateantur, lubenti animo effecturam. Si quid in his oris, quod T. P:ae gratum erit, a me praestari poterit, inueniet me vicissim ad illi gratificandum quam paratissimum.</p><p>Valeat P:a T:a cum tota domo sua quam optime et diutissime.<br>Dabantur properanter ex arce Caesarea Benatika 24 aprilis stylo nouo anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most excellent man, Master Vincent Moller, most prudent Consul of the most ample Commonwealth of Hamburg, and distinguished Licentiate of Both Laws, his friend to be honoured.</b></p><p>Magnificent and most excellent Master Vincent Moller, friend to be honoured: I congratulate Your Excellency from my heart on [your] recent elevation to the consular dignity in your most ample commonwealth, and wish that it be auspicious and salutary. And I give thanks that you did not find it burdensome to visit me with your most kind letter, and that you promise to promote — with the rest of the most worthy consuls and senators of your city — the business about which His Imperial Majesty, our most clement lord, wrote in my name. I hope that His Majesty's letters have now reached you, or will shortly be brought, together with my instruments and goods, which are at Lübeck. Wherefore I desire Your Excellency diligently and most amicably asked, that you be willing, by your authority and intercession, to procure that satisfaction be made in all respects to the most clement will of His Imperial Majesty and to my expectation. About which Simon von Petkum will more fully instruct you, for I have committed the whole matter to his good faith and care. I have also written to him again about those things which were prepared at Wandsbek at my expense and not yet paid for, that he urge the compensation; in which business, too, Your Excellency will best aid me by your counsel and help — especially as regards your city's smith and the other craftsmen, who know how much I laid out and paid to them; nor do I doubt that you will, with willing mind, bring it about that they candidly confess it. If anything can be performed by me in these regions which will be pleasing to Your Excellency, you will find me, in turn, most ready to gratify [you].</p><p>May Your Excellency fare as well and as long as possible, with your whole house.<br>Given hastily from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 24th of April, new style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Vincent Moller</b> (the Elder), now <b>Consul</b> (<i>Bürgermeister</i>) of <b>Hamburg</b> and <i>V[triusque] I[uris] Licentiatus</i> (Licentiate of Both Laws) — Tycho replies to his letter (brahe-t_15991008001), congratulating him on his elevation and pressing the same Hamburg business.</li><li><b>The business.</b> Tycho asks Moller to use his authority with the Hamburg <b>consuls and senators</b> to satisfy the <b>Emperor's letters</b> written \"in Tycho's name,\" and hopes those imperial letters have reached Hamburg \"together with my <b>instruments and goods, which are at Lübeck</b>\" (the Baltic-leg consignment — cf. brahe-t_15991205001). <b>Simon von Petkum</b> has the full brief.</li><li><b>The Wandsbek debts.</b> <i>de ijs, quae Wandesburgi meo aere parata sunt et nondum persoluta</i> — Tycho seeks <b>compensation/repayment for things made at his own expense at Wandsbek and not yet settled</b>, naming \"your city's <b>smith</b> and the other <b>craftsmen</b>\" who know the sums he paid — i.e. instrument-fittings and work done during his Wandsbek stay (cf. the <i>ferramenta Wandesburgi</i> of brahe-t_15991008001). He trusts Moller can get the craftsmen to \"candidly confess\" the amounts.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex arce Caesarea Benatika 24 aprilis stylo nouo … 1600</i> = <b>24 April 1600, New Style</b> (<i>stylo novo</i> explicit); from the Imperial castle of Benátky. Header agrees with the label. (A draft, <i>Kladde</i>.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the recipient (Moller, now Hamburg consul), the matters (the imperial letters to the Hamburg senate; the instruments and goods at Lübeck; the unpaid Wandsbek craftsmen via Simon von Petkum), and the date are all clear and dovetail with Moller's own letter (brahe-t_15991008001).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.200-202 as page images; Tycho's letter to Vincent Moller (Dreyer's item 194) runs across pp.200-201. source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout. Collated translation clause-by-clause: the congratulation on Moller's consulship, the instruments and goods at Lübeck, the request for intercession with the city's craftsmen re: unpaid Wandsbek expenses, and the reference to Simon von Petkum all render faithfully. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 113. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Vincent Moller", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15991008001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991008001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Congratulates Moller on his elevation to the Hamburg consulship and urges him to use his authority to intercede in releasing Tycho's instruments and goods held at Lübeck per the Emperor's wishes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000424001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000426002", "d": "1600-04-26", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 26 April 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>PERVENERUNT ad manus meas, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, libelli duo, quos vir doctissimus et in Geometricis plane rarus et insignis, FRANCISCUS VIETA, nuper in lucem prodire voluit. Hos cum non dubitem te perlibenter visurum, neque forte aliunde absque difficultate exemplaria adepturum, his iunctos ad te mitto.</p><p>Cogito his diebus, si modo negotia publica et politica, quibus admodum distrahor, id permittunt, Augustam me conferre, atque ibi, si ulla ratione fieri poterit, manuscripta opera posthuma CYPRIANI LEOVITIJ indagare et nancisci, ut ea quoque ad te transmittere possim.</p><p>Atque hisce tibi faelicissima quaeque ex animo precor et opto.<br>Datae Monachij die 26 Apr. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>There have come into my hands, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians, two little books which that most learned man — plainly rare and distinguished in geometrical matters — <b>François Viète</b> lately wished to bring into the light. Since I do not doubt that you will see these most gladly, nor perhaps obtain copies elsewhere without difficulty, I send them to you joined herewith.</p><p>I am thinking, these days, if only the public and political affairs by which I am much distracted permit it, of betaking myself to <b>Augsburg</b>, and there, if it can be done by any means, of tracking down and obtaining the posthumous manuscript works of <b>Cyprian Leovitius</b>, that I may transmit those too to you.</p><p>And with these I pray and wish you from my heart every happiness.<br>Given at Munich, the 26th day of April, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (see the Munich series), continuing as Tycho's procurer of new mathematical literature.</li><li><b><i>Franciscus Vieta</b></i> = <b>François Viète</b> (1540–1603), the founder of symbolic algebra; the \"two little books lately brought into the light\" are among his recent Paris tracts (the <i>Apollonius Gallus</i>, the <i>Responsum / Relatio</i> on the calendar, or the analytic <i>opuscula</i> of c. 1593–1600 — <i>not</i> individually named here). Herwart, himself in correspondence with Viète, judged Tycho would want them.</li><li><b><i>Cyprianus Leovitius</b></i> = <b>Cyprian Leovitius (Leowitz)</b> (1524–1574), Bohemian astronomer-astrologer; Herwart hopes to recover his <b>posthumous manuscripts</b> at Augsburg (where Leovitius had worked) for Tycho. <i>Augusta</i> = <b>Augsburg</b> (per glossary §5.7).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>; <i>die 26 Apr. 1600</i>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — short, secure; sense and identifications (Viète, Leovitius, Augsburg) are not in doubt. The two Viète tracts are not titled in the source, so none is asserted.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.304-306 (page images, not OCR); the Herwart letter is printed complete on p.305. Collated opening, both proper names (Franciscus Vieta, Cyprianus Leovitius), place (Augustam/Augsburg), and closing date (26 Apr. 1600) word-for-word against the Latin; source_text matches Dreyer exactly (aside from long-s typography). Translation renders every clause accurately with no additions, omissions, or flipped subjects.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000504003", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000828002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends two books by François Viète and reports plans to travel to Augsburg to seek out posthumous manuscript works of Cyprian Leovitius and transmit them to Tycho.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000426002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000501001", "d": "1600-05-01", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jaques Bongars", "ck": "Jaques Bongars", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Frankfurt am Main", "t": "Jacques Bongars → Tycho Brahe — Frankfurt am Main, 1 May 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TICHONI BRAHE, VIRO INCOMPARABILE IN ARCE CAeSAREA BENATICA.</p><p>NOBILISSIME ET AMPLISSIME DOMINE.</p><p>TRES iam septimanae abierunt, ex quo redditae mihi sunt litterae, quas ad me dedisti IX Martij. Sed neque eas accepi, quas memoras datas sub finem anni superioris, neque FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM vidi, quem a te tanto viro commendatum, omnibus fuissem officijs prosecutus. Non gravaberis itaque repetere, et quae illis litteris erant explicata, et quae huius fidei commiseras. Me certe, magno tuo merito, habebis ad omnia, quae quidem erunt meae potestatis, semper paratissimum. Nec addo plura: mandata tua expecto cupide. Tuas in posterum ad me tuto poteris committere bibliopolae ei, qui CLAUDIJ MARNIJ Wecheliani officinae praeest Pragae.</p><p>Vir Nobiliss. Deus te servet incolumem.<br>Francofurto ipsis Kal. Maijs 1600.<br>Nominis tui studiosissimus<br>BONGARSIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most ample man, Master Tycho Brahe, an incomparable man, at the Imperial castle of Benátky.</b></p><p>Most noble and most ample Master.</p><p>Three weeks have now passed since the letters were delivered to me which you sent me on 9 March. But I have neither received those which you mention as sent toward the end of last year, nor have I seen Frans Tengnagel, whom — commended by you, so great a man — I would have pursued with every good office. You will not, therefore, find it burdensome to repeat both what was set out in those letters, and what you had entrusted to his good faith. Me certainly, by your great merit, you shall always have most ready for everything that shall indeed be within my power. Nor do I add more: I eagerly await your instructions. Your own [letters] to me you can, in future, safely entrust to that bookseller who is in charge, at Prague, of the office of Claude de Marne the Wechelian.</p><p>Most noble Sir, may God keep you unharmed.<br>From Frankfurt, on the very Kalends of May, 1600.<br>Most zealous for your name,<br>Bongars.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Jacques Bongars</b> (see brahe-t_15990501001).</li><li><b>A re-sent letter.</b> This is, almost word for word, the same text Bongars had sent <b>a year earlier</b> (brahe-t_15990501001, Frankfurt, 1 May 1599) — the same complaint that the year-end letters never arrived and that Tengnagel had not appeared, the same offer of service, the same instruction to route replies through the Wechel/Marne agent at Prague. Bongars evidently repeated the unanswered letter; the differences are trivial (<i>sunt → erunt</i>, <i>proferebas → fuissem … prosecutus</i>, plus the added closing benediction and subscription). The address now places Tycho <b>at the Imperial castle of Benátky</b> (<i>in arce Caesarea Benatica</i>), where he was installed from 1599.</li><li><b><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</b></i> = <b>Frans Tengnagel</b> (see prior letter). <i>Claudij Marnij Wecheliani officina</i> = the <b>Wechel/Marne–Aubry</b> house at Frankfurt, with its Prague agent.</li><li><b><i>ipsis Kal. Maijs</b></i> = \"on the very Kalends of May\" = <b>1 May</b> (Roman-calendar idiom, glossed).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Frankfurt (Protestant-majority Imperial city) = <b>Julian / Old Style</b> until 1700; taken as O.S. (no <i>n.s.</i> tag in the dataset), as in the 1599 instance.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — secure throughout; grade [B] for the Frankfurt calendar style (taken as O.S.) rather than any textual doubt. The near-identity with the 1599 letter is documented above, not smoothed away.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — sense secure throughout, no crux; the sole reservation is the declared Frankfurt O.S. calendar convention; near-identity with the 1599 letter documented.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.318 page image in full (address-heading, salutation, body, closing, signature). Collated the three-weeks/9-March-letter clause, the Tengnagel non-arrival sentence, the 'repeat what was explained' request, the Claude de Marne/Wechelian bookseller instruction, and the Frankfurt/Kalends-of-May 1600 date against source_text and published_english; all match. Checked the heading's 'incomparabili/incomparabile' ending, which is not fully legible at this resolution but is immaterial either way since 'an incomparable man' renders correctly under both readings. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Jaques Bongars", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000309001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000309001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports not receiving Tycho's earlier letters or meeting Tengnagel; asks Tycho to repeat their contents; offers assistance and provides a safe Prague address for future correspondence.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000501001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000502001", "d": "1600-05-02", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Matthias, Archduke of Austria", "ck": "Matthias, Archduke of Austria", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Matthias, Archduke of Austria — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 2 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SERENISSIMO PRINCIPI ET DOMINO, DOMINO MATTHIAe, ARCHIDUCI AUSTRIAe, DUCI BURGUNDIAe, STYRIAe, CARINTHIAe, CARNIOLAe ET WIRTTENBERGAe, COMITI TIROLIS, DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO.<br>Serenissime et Inclyte Princeps, Domine Clementissime.</p><p>QUOD Serenissimam Celsitudinem Vestram ignotus et peregrinus ego hisce compellare audeo, id ut pro sua Heroica et congenita Virtute atque clementia candide et benigne accipere dignetur, humilime oro. Impulit me ad hanc audaciam celeberrima fama de Sermae Celnis Vestrae benignissimo affectu erga quasvis artes ingenuas, praesertim sublimiores illas, quas Mathematicas per excellentiam vocant. Quemadmodum enim Augustissimus et Potentissimus Imperator RUDOLPHUS, Vae Sae Cnis Dominus Frater, haec studia plurimi facit atque illis laudabiliter delectatur, sic et Vm Sm Celnem quadam non minus naturae quam sanguinis conformitate illa pariter in precio habere a plurimis affirmatur: cuius etiam haud obscura τεκμύρια, cum ante annos plusculos, Daniam Patriam meam transeundo, laudatissimae memoriae Regem FRIDERICUM Sa Va Co illic invisisset, ad me perlata sunt, quin et paulo post ab Arcis Cronburgicae Praeside mihi insinuatum Sm Cm Vm in Insulam prioris habitationis meae Uraniburgicam, Instrumentorum, quae in usum Astronomicae instaurationis illic construxeram, visendorum gratiâ transfretare constituisse, quod et factum fuisset, nisi maris irrequietudo et glaciei interveniens importunitas propositum hoc impedivissent. Ex eo tempore coepi Smam Celenm Vm pro meo modulo suspicere et venerari; optassemque, ut consilium illud in Insulam dictam traijciendi successus ratos consecutum fuisset. Cum autem nunc gravibus de caussis, relicta Patria, uti fert rerum Mundanarum vicissitudo, in ditionem Potentissimi Imperatoris Regnumque Bohemiae ab eius Caesa Maiestate huc clementissime accitus, transmigrarim, sicque Smae Celni Vae vicinior redditus sim, eo etiam nunc promptius et confidentius animum mihi sumo aliquid ad Serenissimam Celnem Vam literarum dandi, ut erga eam debitum meum obsequium vel aliqua saltem ratione humilime declarem, praesertim cum adeo oportuna id efficiendi modo sese obtulerit occasio, per hunc Generosum et in literis ac linguis bene versatum Iuvenem FRIDERICUM ROSENKRANTZIUM, Sympatriotam et Consangvineum meum, ad vestram Smam Celnem proficiscentem. Cumque Scra Caesa Maitas, Dominus meus clementissimus, sua Augusta apud Vam Serm Celnem commendatione et intercessione (quam ROSENKRANTZIUS secum apportat) eum non dedignata sit, supervacaneum et indecens duco quippiam, quod apud Serm Celnem Vm illi promotioni esse possit, ulterius intempestive repetere aut adiungere, hoc saltem dicam, quicquid clementiae et benevolentiae Serenissima Celo Va in illum contulerit, id non saltem illi, sed toti Rosenkrantzianae familiae, quae in inclyto Regno Daniae antiquissima et splendidissima est, tum quoque mihi, qui eiusdem stemmatis aliqua pars sum, beneficio nunquam memoria obliterando et officiosissima mente depraedicando praestituram. Idque de hoc viro polliceri possum, eum Sermae Vae Celsni fidelem et obsequentissimum futurum, sive privatim in sua Aula, sive publice in bello Ungarico contra Turcam, eius servitiô et qualicunque operâ (est enim ad utrumque humilime paratus) uti voluerit. Deliquit is quidem humanitus et iuvenili quodam lapsu, quod dolendum est, et ipsummet ita contigisse vehementer et ex animo poenitet, nec suum hoc delictum celare aut excusare praesumit, unde in Regis sui, cui per sexennium in cubiculo fidelissime inservierat, indignationem tantam incidit, ut stimulantibus invidis et osoribus, nullus fuerit veniae locus, quicquid tandem pro culpae condonatione et redemptione ipse et illi sanguine iuncti humilime offerrent; attamen sperat se, divina fretus misericordia et bonitate, alibi extra Patriam, potissimum vero in Sermae Vae Celnis servitio, hanc deplorandae sortis adversitatem virtute et integritate sua ita superaturum, atque priora illa, fragilitate quadam humana imprudenter commissa, honeste et fortiter gestis diluiturum atque emendaturum, ut tandem, auxiliante divina clementia et Virorum Principum diligenti intercessione apud Regem suum culpae remissionem submisse impetrare atque in integrum restitui possit, maximo omnium consangvinitate et affinitate ipsi iunctorum, cuiusmodi sunt Nobilissimi quique in Regno Daniae, gaudio et applausu. Confidimus autem omnes, quotquot eius sortem ex animo dolemus et ad meliora ipsum restitui exoptamus, apud Sam Celnem Vam occasiones et media inventurum, quibus fortunae asperitatem mitiget atque sermae Celnis Vae clementissimo favore atque indultu eam vincere et ad potiora emergere possit. Quod faxit misericors et omnis boni largitor Deus.</p><p>Caeterum Sermae Celni Vae per dictum ROSENKRANTZIUM submisse mitto duos libros, quos Sacrae Caesae Maiestati, Domino et Maecenati meo clementissimo, quam primum Pragam veneram, obtuli, et unâ addo globum quendam caelestem e mea inerrantium stellarum accurata et diutino tempore exantlata restitutione apposite conformatum; quae a tenuitate mea profecta munuscula, ut Sma Celo Va clementi animo accipere, et me meaque studia sibi commendata habere non dedignetur, qua possum reverentia et submissione oro et obsecro. Valeat Serma Celo Va in Reipubae stabilimentum quam diutissime et florentissime, et successu contra truculentum Christiani nominis hostem per Dei benignitatem felici in posterum quoque utatur.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Bennaticka die 2. Maij Anni 1600.<br>Sereniss. Cels. Vestrae addictiss.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene Prince and Lord, Lord Matthias, Archduke of Austria, Duke of Burgundy, Styria, Carinthia, Carniola and Württemberg, Count of Tirol, his most clement Lord.</b><br>Most serene and renowned Prince, most clement Lord.</p><p>That I, unknown and a foreigner, dare to address Your most serene Highness by these [lines], I humbly pray You may deign to take candidly and kindly, according to Your heroic and inborn Virtue and clemency. What impelled me to this boldness was the most celebrated report of Your most serene Highness's most kindly disposition toward all liberal arts — especially those loftier ones which by excellence they call <b>Mathematical</b>. For just as the most august and most powerful Emperor <b>Rudolf</b>, Your most serene Highness's Lord Brother, makes much of these studies and laudably delights in them, so Your most serene Highness too is affirmed by very many to hold them in like esteem, by a conformity no less of nature than of blood — of which no obscure <b>tokens [τεκμήρια]</b> were brought to me when, some years ago, passing through Denmark my fatherland, Your Highness visited there the King <b>Frederick</b>, of most praised memory; nay, and a little after, it was intimated to me by the Governor of <b>Kronborg</b> Castle that Your Highness had resolved to <b>cross over to my Island of Uraniborg, the seat of my former dwelling, to see the Instruments which I had constructed there for the use of the Restoration of Astronomy</b> — which would have been done, had not the restlessness of the sea and the intervening trouble of ice hindered this purpose. From that time I began, according to my small measure, to look up to and venerate Your most serene Highness; and I could have wished that that plan of crossing over to the said Island had met with sure success. But since now, for grave causes — my fatherland left behind, as the vicissitude of worldly things brings — I have migrated, most clemently called hither by His Imperial Majesty into the dominion of the most powerful Emperor and the Kingdom of Bohemia, and am thus made a nearer neighbour to Your most serene Highness, the more promptly and confidently do I now take heart to send some letter to Your Highness, that I may humbly declare my due obedience toward You, at least in some fashion — especially since so opportune an occasion of doing it has offered itself, <b>through this noble Youth, well versed in letters and tongues, Friedrich Rosenkrantz, my compatriot and kinsman, setting out to Your most serene Highness</b>. And since the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my most clement Lord, has not disdained to favour him with His august commendation and intercession to Your most serene Highness (which Rosenkrantz brings with him), I judge it superfluous and unseemly to repeat or add untimely anything further that might further his advancement with Your Highness; this only will I say: <b>whatever clemency and benevolence Your most serene Highness shall confer upon him, it will be conferred not only on him, but on the whole Rosenkrantz family</b> — which in the renowned Kingdom of Denmark is most ancient and most splendid — and also on me, who am some part of the same lineage, by a benefit never to be effaced from memory and to be proclaimed with a most dutiful mind. And this I can promise of this man: that he will be faithful and most obedient to Your most serene Highness — whether privately in Your Court, or publicly in the <b>Hungarian war against the Turk</b> — for whatever service and work You may wish to use him (for he is humbly ready for either). <b>He erred indeed humanly, by a certain youthful lapse</b>, which is to be regretted, and he vehemently and from his heart repents that it so fell out, nor does he presume to conceal or excuse this his fault; whereby <b>he fell into so great an indignation of his King — whom for six years he had most faithfully served in the chamber — that, the envious and the haters goading, there was no place for pardon</b>, whatever he and those bound to him by blood at last humbly offered for the remission and redemption of the fault. Yet he hopes that, relying on divine mercy and goodness, <b>elsewhere outside his fatherland — but above all in Your most serene Highness's service — he will so overcome this adversity of his lamentable lot by his virtue and integrity, and so dilute and amend those former things (imprudently committed by a certain human frailty) by deeds done honourably and bravely</b>, that at last, with divine clemency aiding and the diligent intercession of Princely Men, he may humbly obtain remission of the fault from his King and be restored to his full standing — to the greatest joy and applause of all joined to him by kinship and affinity, such as are all the noblest in the Kingdom of Denmark. And we all — as many of us as grieve his lot from the heart and long for him to be restored to better things — trust that he will find with Your Highness occasions and means whereby he may soften the harshness of fortune, and by Your most serene Highness's most clement favour and indulgence overcome it and emerge to better things. Which may the merciful God, the bestower of all good, bring to pass.</p><p>For the rest, <b>I humbly send Your most serene Highness, by the said Rosenkrantz, two books which I offered to the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my Lord and most clement Maecenas, as soon as I had come to Prague; and I add, together with them, a certain celestial Globe, fittingly fashioned from my accurate restitution of the fixed stars, drawn out over a long time.</b> These little gifts, proceeding from my slenderness, I pray and beseech, with what reverence and submission I can, that Your most serene Highness may not disdain to accept with a clement mind, and to hold me and my studies commended to You. May Your most serene Highness fare well, for the establishment of the Commonwealth, most long and most flourishingly, and enjoy hereafter too, by God's goodness, happy success against the truculent enemy of the Christian name.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 2nd day of May, the year 1600.<br>Your most serene Highness's most devoted,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Archduke Matthias of Austria</b> (1557–1619), brother of Rudolf II and the future Emperor Matthias — addressed with full titulature (Archduke of Austria; Duke of Burgundy, Styria, Carinthia, Carniola, Württemberg; Count of Tirol).</li><li><b>Significance — the Friedrich Rosenkrantz recommendation (resolves the unnamed kinsman of the Rindschadt letter).</b> This is the <b>formal recommendation carried by Friedrich (Frederik) Rosenkrantz</b> — and thereby <b>names the \"gentleman of the Rosenkrantz family, my near kinsman,\" left unnamed in the Danish Kepler-letter brahe-t_16000000001</b>, who was to take Kepler to Vienna and seek Archduke Matthias's service. The two letters are the same spring-1600 episode from two angles.</li><li><b>The disgrace (handled with delicacy).</b> Tycho records, without naming the offence, that Rosenkrantz <b>\"erred humanly, by a youthful lapse,\"</b> fell into \"so great an indignation of his King\" — <b>Christian IV, whom he had served six years in the chamber</b> — that \"the envious goading, there was no place for pardon.\" This is the <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz / Rigborg Brockenhuus scandal</b> (the convicted seduction affair that drove Rosenkrantz into exile; he served and <b>died in the Hungarian war, 1602</b>). Tycho casts the exile as a path to redemption \"by deeds done honourably and bravely … in Your service,\" with hope of eventual royal pardon.</li><li><b>Kinship pride.</b> Tycho stresses the <b>Rosenkrantz family</b> is \"most ancient and splendid in Denmark\" and that he is himself \"some part of the same lineage\" — a benefit to Matthias's favour would touch the whole house.</li><li><b>Biographical gem — Matthias's intended visit to Uraniborg.</b> Tycho recalls that, years before, Matthias <b>passed through Denmark and visited King Frederick II</b>, and that the <b>Governor of Kronborg</b> intimated Matthias <b>had resolved to cross to Hven to see Tycho's instruments</b> — prevented only by \"the restlessness of the sea and the trouble of ice.\" A previously-cherished near-encounter, now invoked.</li><li><b>A documented gift — two books + a celestial globe.</b> Tycho sends Matthias, via Rosenkrantz, <b>\"two books which I offered the Emperor on coming to Prague\"</b> (from the imperial-presentation set — cf. brahe-t_16001110001, the <i>Mechanica</i> / star-<i>Restitutio</i> / <i>Progymnasmata</i> program) <b>and a celestial globe \"fittingly fashioned from my accurate restitution of the fixed stars.\"</b> A datable <b>Tycho celestial-globe and book distribution event</b> (Benátky → Archduke Matthias, May 1600).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Archduke Matthias; <b>Rudolf II</b> (his brother); <b>Friedrich/Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> (the disgraced kinsman, bearer); <b>King Frederick II of Denmark</b> (the late king Matthias had visited); <b>Christian IV</b> (Rosenkrantz's offended King, unnamed as \"his King\"); the <b>Governor of Kronborg</b> (unnamed). Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Denmark</b>, <b>Hven/Uraniborg</b>, <b>Kronborg</b>, Hungary.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Resolves the unnamed Rosenkrantz of <b>brahe-t_16000000001</b> → <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b>; register the <b>celestial-globe + two-book gift to Archduke Matthias</b> with the Mechanica/instrument distribution layer and celestial-globe history.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 2. Maij Anni 1600</i> at Benátky (imperial reckoning) → <b>2 May 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — formal court Latin read with confidence; the substance (the courtesies and the Mathematics-patronage praise; <b>Matthias's earlier Denmark visit and intended Uraniborg crossing prevented by sea/ice</b>; the <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz recommendation</b> with its delicate account of the disgrace, the six-year chamber service to Christian IV, and the redemption-through-service hope; the kinship-pride passage; and the gift of <b>two books plus a celestial globe</b> from the imperial-presentation set) is firmly construed, with the offence left exactly as veiled as Tycho leaves it (no sharpening). The Greek <i>τεκμήρια</i> (printed <i>τεκμύρια</i>) is rendered \"tokens/proofs.\" The unnamed offence is identified as the Rigborg Brockenhuus scandal on standard biographical grounds and flagged as an editorial identification.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address heading, the Frederick/Kronborg/Uraniborg reminiscence, the Friedrich Rosenkrantz disgrace-and-plea passage, the gift of two books and the celestial globe, and the closing) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.306-309 read as page images. source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly, including the idiosyncratic Greek tekmyria spelling and all honorific abbreviations. published_english correctly preserves every pronoun/subject through the Rosenkrantz disgrace narrative (he/Rosenkrantz erred and repented, not Tycho), the names (Rudolf, Frederick, Friedrich Rosenkrantz), the six-year chamber service, and the date (2 May 1600). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Addresses Archduke Matthias, citing his reputed esteem for mathematics, and recalls tokens of favour shown during the Archduke's earlier passage through Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000502001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000504001", "d": "1600-05-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Cosimo Concini", "ck": "Cosimo Concini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Cosimo Concini — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 4 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Firenze, Archivio di Stato, Collezione Medicea,<i> Epist. Concin. </i>vol. 56 (copy /<i> afskrift</i>)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRISSIMO ET GENEROSISSIMO DOMINO DOMINO COSMO CONCINO E COMITIBUS PENNAe ETC. SERENISSIMI MAGNI DUCIS HETRURIAe APUD SACRAM CAeSAREAM MAIESTATEM ORATORI DIGNISSIMO DOMINO OBSERVANDISSIMO.</p><p>ILLUSTRISSIME ET GENEROSISSIME DOMINE.</p><p>SIQUIDEM ab Illma Dominatione Vestra, cum nuper apud ipsam Pragae essem, intellexerim negotium de mittendo ad Sermum Magnum Ducem Hetruriae etc. filio meo, in hoc tantummodo consistere, an is catholicam Romanam Religionem amplectetur, nec ne; neque enim Sermam eius Celsitudnem libenter quempiam in sua Aula habere, qui a dicta Religione alienus sit. Ad hoc equidem, quod respondeam nihil aliud habeo, quam me de eo polliceri posse: Si Sermus Magnus Dux eum in suam Aulam recipere dignatus fuerit, quod se ita gesturus sit, ut neminem offendat, aut scandalizet, sive quoad religionem sive alijs in rebus, quemadmodum Nobilem Iuvenem FRANCISCUM TENGNAGLIUM, qui aliquot annis in mea familia fuit, se ita, dum Sermae Celsitni eius inserviret, exhibuisse confido, ut nemo isthic habeat, in quo iure de eo conqueri possit. Quod et pariter praestitit, dum in Illmi et Rmi Cardinalis a DIETRICHSTEIN Aula aliquantisper moratus est. Si vero Sermae ipsius Celsitni haec de religione quaestio aliquid nihilominus scrupuli moverit, posset filius meus unâ cum altero, quem illi adiuncturus sum, studioso, in ipsius Sermae Celsitnis Academia, quae Senis est, inter reliquos Germanos aliquantisper degere, ut studiosum illum nonnunquam in observationibus Astronomicis, iuxta meum praescriptum, isthic peragendis adiuvet; oportet enim ad minimum duos esse, interdum etiam tres, qui eo instrumento utantur, quod ego illis traditurus sum. Confectis autem isthic observationibus praecipuis, navigabit studiosus ille in Aegyptum, et facile ibi alios inveniet, qui instructi in pluribus obtinendis ibi praesto sint; quod propositum ipsius Sernam Celnem, quemadmodum clementissime promisit, benignissime promoturam mihi persuadeo. Interim filius manebit isthic in Italia, donec linguam calluerit, quod intra annui temporis spacium fieri posse existimo, ut tunc reversus Sac. Caesae Maiestati Domino meo clementissmo in ipsius Maiestatis Aula aliquandiu humillime serviat.</p><p>Haec ad Illam Dominatem referenda habui, et qua possum reverentia Illam Dominationem Vestram rogatam habeo, ut a Sermo suo Principe resolutionem certam, quid hac in re sperare possim, vel secus, tempestive mihi impetrare non degravetur; ego vicissim omnia mea officiola ipsius Sermae Celsitni et Vestrae quoque Illmae Dominationi submisse defero.</p><p>Valeat Illma Vestra Dominatio diu et feliciter.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica, 4 Maij Anni 1600.<br>Illmae Dominationi Vestrae addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious and most generous Lord, Lord Cosimo Concini, of the Counts of Penna, etc., most worthy Ambassador of the Most Serene Grand Duke of Tuscany to the Sacred Imperial Majesty, a Lord most to be respected.</b></p><p>Most illustrious and most generous Lord.</p><p>Since I have understood from Your Most Illustrious Lordship, when I was lately with you at Prague, that the business of sending my son to the Most Serene Grand Duke of Tuscany, etc., turns on this alone — whether he will embrace the Catholic Roman Religion, or not (for His Most Serene Highness does not willingly have anyone in his Court who is averse to the said Religion) — to this, indeed, I have nothing else to answer than that I can promise concerning him as follows: that, if the Most Serene Grand Duke shall deign to receive him into his Court, he will so conduct himself as to offend or scandalize no one, whether as to religion or in other matters — just as I trust that the noble young man <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, who was for some years in my household, so conducted himself while he served His Most Serene Highness that no one there has anything for which he could justly complain of him. The like he performed, too, while he stayed a while in the Court of the most illustrious and most reverend Cardinal von Dietrichstein. But if this question of religion should nonetheless raise any scruple for His Most Serene Highness, my son could — together with another student whom I shall attach to him — dwell for a while in His Most Serene Highness's Academy, which is at Siena, among the other Germans, so that he might from time to time assist that student in carrying out there the Astronomical observations, according to my prescription; for there must be at least two, sometimes even three, to use that instrument which I shall hand over to them. And when the principal observations have been completed there, that student will sail to Egypt, and will readily find others there, [already] instructed, ready at hand for the obtaining of more; which design His Most Serene Highness, as he most graciously promised, I am persuaded will most kindly further. Meanwhile my son will remain there in Italy until he has mastered the language, which I think can be done within the space of a year, so that, returning then, he may for some time most humbly serve the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my most clement Lord, in His Majesty's Court.</p><p>These things I had to refer to Your Lordship; and with what reverence I can, I ask Your Most Illustrious Lordship not to find it burdensome to obtain for me, in good time, from your Most Serene Prince a sure resolution as to what I may hope in this matter, or otherwise. I, in turn, humbly tender all my poor services to His Most Serene Highness and also to Your Most Illustrious Lordship.</p><p>May Your Most Illustrious Lordship fare long and happily.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 4th of May, in the year 1600.<br>Your Most Illustrious Lordship's most devoted,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>I wrote [this] with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Significance &amp; companion letter.</b> This is the <b>twin</b> of Tycho's celebrated letter to <b>Galileo</b> (brahe-t_16000504002), written the <b>same day, from the same place</b> (Benátky, 4 May 1600 [N.S.]). It is the <b>business letter to the Tuscan ambassador</b> that lies behind the Galileo letter's opening: there Tycho recalls meeting, at Prague, \"the Ambassador of the Grand Duke of Tuscany … Cosimo Concini,\" whose praise of Galileo prompted the approach. The two letters together document Tycho's spring-1600 dealings with the Medici court. Survives as a <b>copy</b> (<i>afscrift</i>) in the Medici archive at Florence.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Cosimo Concini</b>, <i>e Comitibus Pennae</i> (of the Counts of Penna), <b>ambassador (<i>Orator</i>) of the Most Serene Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> — Ferdinando I de' Medici — at the imperial court. (Not to be confused with the later Concino Concini, maréchal d'Ancre, in France.)</li><li><b>The matter — placing Tycho's son at the Medici court.</b> Concini had told Tycho that the only obstacle to receiving <b>one of Tycho's sons</b> into the Grand Duke's service was confessional: the Grand Duke \"does not willingly have anyone in his Court averse to the Catholic Roman Religion.\" Tycho — himself Lutheran — answers diplomatically: he will not promise conversion, but pledges that his son will <i>give no offence or scandal, in religion or otherwise</i>. (The son intended for Italy is left unnamed in the letter; the plan concerns one of Tycho's sons, probably <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b> — named cautiously, not asserted from the text.)</li><li><b>The precedent of Tengnagel.</b> Tycho cites <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (<i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</i>) — his assistant, soon his son-in-law — who had served the Grand Duke of Tuscany and stayed at the court of <b>Cardinal Franz von Dietrichstein</b> (1570–1636, Bishop of Olomouc, made cardinal 1599) \"so that no one could justly complain of him.\" Tengnagel is the same intermediary whose return from Italy frames the Galileo letter; his Italian sojourn is detailed in brahe-t_16000306002.</li><li><b>The fallback plan — Siena and the Egypt expedition (astronomical interest).</b> Should the religious scruple persist, Tycho proposes lodging his son at the <b>University/Academy of Siena</b> \"among the other Germans\" (the <i>natio Germanica</i>), where he would assist a <b>student-observer</b> sent by Tycho. Tycho stresses the working requirement — <i>\"at least two, sometimes three\"</i> observers are needed to operate the <b>instrument</b> he will provide (protocol §5.2, <i>organum/instrumentum</i>). Most striking is the design that, once the principal Siena observations are done, the student <i>\"will sail to Egypt\"</i> to make further observations at southern latitudes and recruit local helpers — a concrete glimpse of Tycho's late ambition to extend his observational programme into the southern sky, which he expected the Grand Duke to fund. The son would meanwhile learn Italian (≈ a year) and then return to serve <b>Rudolf II</b> at the imperial court.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Cosimo Concini (Tuscan ambassador); the <i>Serenissimus Magnus Dux Hetruriae</i> = <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany</b>; Tycho's son (unnamed); <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Cardinal Franz von Dietrichstein</b>; <i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Arx Caesarea Benatica</i> = the imperial castle of <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Address in the high diplomatic register (<i>Illustrissima Dominatio Vestra</i>, \"Your Most Illustrious Lordship\"; <i>Serenissima Celsitudo</i>, \"His Most Serene Highness\"); subscription <i>addictissimus</i> → \"most devoted\"; <i>manu propria scripsi</i> → \"I wrote with my own hand\" (autograph attestation, though the surviving witness is a chancery copy). Heavy abbreviation (<i>Illma, Serma, Celsitni, Rmi</i>) silently expanded; the source block keeps the contractions.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>4 Maij Anni 1600</i> at Benátky; the dataset marks <b>N.S. (New Style)</b> — apt for Tycho's imperial-service dating and for correspondence with Catholic Italy. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, complete Latin; the occasion (placing Tycho's son at the Tuscan court, conditional on religion), the Tengnagel/Dietrichstein precedent, the Siena-Academy fallback and the projected Egyptian observing expedition, and every named party (Concini, the Grand Duke, Tengnagel, Cardinal Dietrichstein, Rudolf II) are explicit and corroborated by the companion Galileo letter of the same day. No textual crux; the son's identity is left open per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.308-311 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the address (Cosimo Concini), the religion condition, the Tengnagel and Cardinal Dietrichstein references, the Siena academy/Egypt observation plan, and the 4 May 1600 Benatica dateline against the printed page word-for-word. Text matches source_text exactly; every proper name (Franciscus Tengnaglius/Tengnagel, Dietrichstein, Senis/Siena, Aegyptum/Egypt) is correctly rendered in the English. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Archivio de Stato, Coll. Medicea, Epist Concin. vol 56. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Cosimo Concini", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000414001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Responds to Concini's condition regarding the Catholic religion for his son's entry into the Tuscan court, promising the young man will cause no religious offence to anyone.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000504001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000504002", "d": "1600-05-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Galileo Galilei", "ck": "Galileo Galilei", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Galileo Galilei — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 4 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Firenze, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale, Mss. Galileiani, Parte 6, Tom. 7</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ADMODUM MAGNIFICO ET EXCELLENTI VIRO DOMINO GALILAeO DE GALILAeIS, MATHEMATICO EXCELLENTISSIMO, AMICO HONORANDO.</p><p>CLARISSIME et excellentissime Vir; cum hisce diebus Pragae fuissem, atque ibi Serenissimi Principis, Magni Ducis Hetruriae, Oratorem apud Saram Caesam Matem, Illustrissimum et Generosissimum Dominum COSMUM CONCINUM e Comitibus Pennae convenissem, inter alia Illustrissmae Dominationis eius humanissima mecum colloquia (uti sane est Vir eximia comitate, parique doctrina, praeter Generis Illustrissmum splendorem, admirandus, nec unquam satis laudatus) incidit etiam Excellentiae Tuae honorifica mentio, ob singularem, qua, in Mathematicis praesertim, plurimos alios antecellis, eruditionem. Cumque a tanto Viro tuas dotes etiam depraedicari audirem, simulavit id prius de Excella Ta animo meo conceptam sententiam, ut non potuerim non has ad ipsam scribere, atque sic amicitiae nostrae et ulterioris inter nos per literas correspondentiae fundamina ponere. Quia vero a Nobili Adolescente FRANCISCO TENGNAGLIO meo domestico, ex Italia huc redeunte, percepi Excellam Tuam primum nostrum Tomum Epistolarum Astronomicarum perlustrasse, atque in eo nonnulla reperijsse, de quibus mecum conferre cuperet, ego certe idipsum nullatenus detrecto, sed si quid fuerit, quod Exa Ta in disquisitionem inibi vocare velit, erit id mihi gratissimum, invenietque me ad respondendum pro meo modulo quam paratissimum: Sive de Hypothesi nostra Coelestium revolutionum, quae Solem centrum facit circuitionis quinque Planetarum, Terram autem, et eam quiescentem, solummodo amborum Luminarium, atque Octavae, quam vocant Sphaerae, cui assumptioni apparentias quam optime congruere depraehendi, ut nihilominus tollantur vasti illi Epicycli, quemadmodum apud COPERNICUM, et Terra in centro Universi, quod ille non admisit, immota maneat (sunt etiam particularia quaedam in hac nostra inventione, quae neque iuxta Ptolemaicam neque Coperniceam speculationem tam competenter excusari possunt) sive de Restitutione Fixarum Stellarum, sive de Cometis, quos omnes in ipso Coelo curricula sua absolvere, contra quam volunt Peripatetici, probo, idque in septem a me diligenter observatis demonstratum relinquo, sive de quacunque tandem alia re, cuius in illo libro mentio fit, mecum disserere Excella Tua volet, faciat id ingenue pro suo arbitrio. Ego vicissim meam sententiam illi aperire, atque de rebus Astronomicis cum ea iucunde conferre non intermittam.</p><p>Valeat Excella Tua quam optime.<br>Dabantur ex Arce Caesarea Benatica Die 4 Maij Anni 1600.<br>Excellentiae Tuae<br>studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the very magnificent and excellent man, Lord Galileo de' Galilei, most excellent Mathematician, [his] friend to be honoured.</b></p><p>Most renowned and most excellent Man: when in these days I had been at Prague, and there had met the Ambassador of the Most Serene Prince, the Grand Duke of Tuscany, with the Sacred Imperial Majesty — the Most Illustrious and Most Generous Lord Cosimo Concini, of the Counts of Penna — there fell, among that Most Illustrious Lordship's other most courteous conversations with me (for indeed he is a Man admirable, and never sufficiently praised, for his rare affability and equal learning, besides the most illustrious splendour of his lineage), honourable mention also of Your Excellency, on account of the singular erudition by which, in Mathematics especially, you surpass very many others. And when I heard your gifts thus proclaimed by so great a Man, it confirmed the opinion I had previously conceived in my mind of Your Excellency, so that I could not but write this to you, and so lay the foundations of our friendship and of further correspondence between us by letters. But since I learned from the noble young man Franz Tengnagel, my servant, on his return hither from Italy, that Your Excellency had perused our first Volume of Astronomical Letters, and found in it certain things about which you would wish to confer with me, I for my part by no means decline this; but if there be anything which Your Excellency would wish to call into examination therein, it will be most welcome to me, and you will find me most ready to answer, according to my measure —</p><p>whether concerning our <b>Hypothesis of the celestial revolutions</b>, which makes the Sun the centre of the circuit of the five Planets, but the Earth — and that at rest — the centre only of the two Luminaries [Sun and Moon] and of the Eighth Sphere, as they call it (an assumption to which I have found the appearances to agree as well as possible, so that nonetheless those vast Epicycles are done away with, as in Copernicus, and yet the Earth remains unmoved in the centre of the Universe, which he did not admit — there are also certain particulars in this our discovery which can be accounted for so satisfactorily neither by the Ptolemaic nor by the Copernican speculation);</p><p>or concerning the <b>Restitution of the Fixed Stars</b>;</p><p>or concerning the <b>Comets</b>, all of which I prove to complete their courses in the heaven itself, contrary to what the Peripatetics will have it, and this I leave demonstrated in seven [comets] diligently observed by me;</p><p>or, in short, concerning whatever other matter mentioned in that book Your Excellency may wish to discuss with me — let [you] do it frankly, at your own discretion. I, in turn, will not fail to open my opinion to you, and to confer pleasantly with you about Astronomical matters.</p><p>May Your Excellency fare as well as possible.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 4th day of May, in the year 1600.<br>Your Excellency's most devoted,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>I wrote [this] with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Significance.</b> This is <b>Tycho Brahe's letter to Galileo Galilei</b> — the principal surviving document of contact between the two, surviving among the <b>Galilean manuscripts</b> at the Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale, Florence. Tycho, near the end of his life, opens correspondence with the young Galileo (then professor of mathematics at Padua), prompted by reports of Galileo's gifts and by Galileo's own reading of Tycho's published letters. Galileo never substantively replied during Tycho's lifetime; the letter thus stands as Tycho's outstretched, unreciprocated hand across the generational divide.</li><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky</b>; <b>Galileo Galilei</b> (1564–1642), <i>mathematicus excellentissimus</i>, then at Padua.</li><li><b>The occasion &amp; intermediaries.</b></li><li><i>Cosmus Concinus e Comitibus Pennae</i> = <b>Cosimo Concini</b>, of the Counts of Penna — the <b>Tuscan ambassador</b> (<i>Orator</i>) of Grand Duke Ferdinando I de' Medici at the imperial court — whose praise of Galileo at Prague prompted the letter.</li><li><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius</i> = <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, Tycho's assistant, <i>returning from Italy</i>, who reported that Galileo had read Tycho's letters and wished to raise questions (Tengnagel's Italian sojourn is detailed in brahe-t_16000306002).</li><li><i>primus noster Tomus Epistolarum Astronomicarum</i> = the <b>first volume of Tycho's <i>Epistolae Astronomicae</b></i> (<i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber primus</i>, Uraniborg 1596) — the book Galileo had perused.</li><li><b>The Tychonic world-system (the scientific heart; protocol §5.1).</b> Tycho states his <b>geo-heliocentric hypothesis</b> in nuce: the <b>Sun is the centre of the orbits of the five planets</b>, while the <b>Earth, at rest</b>, is the centre only of the <b>Sun and Moon</b> (the two <i>luminaria</i>) and of the <b>eighth (fixed-star) sphere</b>. He claims (i) the <b>appearances are saved</b> as well as possible (<i>apparentias quam optime congruere</i> — the <i>salvare apparentias</i> criterion, §5.1); (ii) the \"<b>vast epicycles</b>\" are eliminated <i>as in Copernicus</i>; yet (iii) the <b>Earth stays immobile at the centre</b>, which Copernicus denied; and (iv) certain <i>particularia</i> of his system fit \"neither the Ptolemaic nor the Copernican speculation\" — his standing claim to a genuine <i>tertium quid</i>.</li><li><b>The other offered topics.</b></li><li><i>Restitutio Fixarum Stellarum</i> = the <b>restoration of the fixed stars</b> — Tycho's renewed star catalogue (<i>restitutio</i>, §5.1).</li><li><i>Cometae … in ipso Coelo curricula sua absolvere … contra Peripateticos … in septem a me diligenter observatis</i> = his proof, <b>against the Aristotelians (Peripatetics)</b>, that <b>comets move in the heavens</b> (not in the sublunary air), demonstrated from <b>seven comets</b> he had observed — the anti-Aristotelian thesis (post-1577) that abolished the solid celestial spheres.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Galileo; Cosimo Concini (Tuscan ambassador); Franz Tengnagel; <i>Copernicus</i>; the <i>Peripatetici</i> (Aristotelians).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Arx Caesarea Benatica</i> = the imperial castle of Benátky.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Valeat … quam optime</i> → \"fare as well as possible\"; subscription <i>Excellentiae Tuae studiosissimus</i> → \"Your Excellency's most devoted\"; <i>manu propria scripsi</i> → \"I wrote with my own hand\" (autograph attestation). The honorific <i>Excellentia Tua</i> oscillates with third-person <i>ipsa/illi</i>; the shifts are preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 4 Maij Anni 1600</i> at Benátky; the dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b> — correct for Tycho's imperial-service dating, and apt for a letter to Catholic Italy (already Gregorian). Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, complete Latin and a document of the first historical importance, read securely throughout. The world-system précis (heliocentric for the five planets, geocentric for Sun/Moon/fixed stars; epicycles removed but Earth immobile; the <i>tertium quid</i> claim), the cometary thesis \"in seven observed comets,\" and every named party (Galileo, Concini, Tengnagel, the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i>) are unambiguous. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.310-313 as page images. The Galileo letter runs from the header on p.311 through the first four lines of p.312 (p.310 is the tail of an unrelated prior letter; p.312 continues into the next letter, Herwartus to Tycho, confirmed as boundary checks). Collated the full title/address, the Cosimo Concini/Grand Duke of Tuscany passage, the Tengnaglio reference, the three-part disjunction (Hypothesis/Restitution of Fixed Stars/Comets, 'in seven diligently observed'), and the closing date (4 May 1600) word-for-word. source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english is a complete and technically accurate rendering of the Tychonic-system description, with no fabricated or dropped clauses.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Bibliotheca Nazionale Centrale, Codd. Galil. Part. 6, Tom. 7."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Initiates correspondence with Galileo after hearing praise of his mathematical abilities from the Tuscan ambassador Concini during a meeting in Prague, seeking to establish a scholarly friendship.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000504002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000504003", "d": "1600-05-04", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 4 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 2° Cod. ms. 693</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>LITERAS tuas ex arce Benaticâ 8 die Aprilis ad me datas, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, singulari cum laetitia accepi; ex ijs enim perlibenter intellexi, nullas earum, quas ad me misisti, id quod unice verebar, vel interijsse vel interceptas esse. Nec est quod moram in respondendo ulla ratione excuses; facilius enim plurima et innumerabilia quoque proponuntur, quam vel ad unicum respondeatur. Mihi potius adeo frequens ista reiteratio literarum vitio vertenda foret, siquidem alia ratione libros, quos videre petieras, pluresve eorum simul unâ ad te transmittere potuissem.</p><p>Inprimis vero laetor, mihique congratulor, me totam illam hypothesin Lunae, posteaquam ea ad apparentias coelestes penitius a te transformata fuerit, a benignitate et benevolentia tua sperare et expectare debere. Atque adeo interea temporis sigillatim aliqua te rescripturum constituisse, ex quibus sententia tua de Lunae hypothesi magis elucescat. Verissimum enim esse reor, me praecisius iusto calculum de Luna instituisse. Caeterum id a me propterea deditâ operâ et summo studio ita factum est, ut absque calculi errore mentem tuam de cursu Lunae plane lubrico et vario assequi possem, tametsi verear me nihilominus subinde errasse. Facies itaque mihi rem gratissimam, si, dato et concesso tibi otio, viam mihi demonstraveris, qua intimius sententiam hac in re tuam assequi valeam. Abunde enim mihi nunc temporis satisfactum erit, si ea, quae tu ex accuratissimis observationibus tuis hactenus constituisti, calculo venari seu imitari queam.</p><p>Libros illos, quos ex Bibliotheca Sermi Principis mei ad te transmisi, curabo, ut Ser. Clementiae suae Agens, cum is in aulam Imperatoriam redierit, sub indice et designatione eorum recipiat et mihi remittat. Manuscripta illa, quae CYPRIANUS LEOVITIUS ἀνέκδοτα reliquit, per intermedias personas petieram et quidem instantissime efflagitaveram, sed frustra et incassum laboravi, oleumque et operam perdidi, ideoque captata occasione me ipse Augustam contuli, et praesens coram ea ab Generoso Domino PHILIPPO EDUARDO FUGGERO Barone etc. petij atque impetravi; ea itaque tibi mittam, quam primum securam aliquam rationem mittendi perquirendo investigare potero. Carmen quoque tibi mittam brevi, quod, uti spero, non displicebit.</p><p>Atque hisce me tibi officiosissime commendo.<br>Datae Monachij 4 die Maij A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Your letter, dispatched to me from the castle of Benátky on the 8th of April, most noble man and easily the Prince of all Mathematicians, I received with singular joy; for from it I learned, most gladly, that none of those [letters] you have sent me — the one thing I feared — had either perished or been intercepted. Nor is there any reason for you to excuse the delay in answering on any account; for very many, and innumerable, things are more easily proposed than a single one is answered. Rather, this so frequent reiteration of letters ought to be charged to my fault, since by some other means I might have transmitted to you the books you had asked to see, or several of them together at once.</p><p>But above all I rejoice, and congratulate myself, that I must hope and look for that whole hypothesis of the Moon — after it has been more deeply transformed by you to fit the celestial appearances — from your kindness and benevolence; and, further, that you have resolved meanwhile to write back some matters point by point, from which your view on the Moon's hypothesis may shine out more clearly. For I think it most true that I have set up my calculation of the Moon more precisely than is warranted. Yet I did this of set purpose and with the utmost zeal, in order that, without error of calculation, I might reach your mind regarding the Moon's course — plainly slippery and variable as it is — even though I fear I have nonetheless gone astray here and there. You will therefore do me a most welcome thing if, when leisure is given and granted you, you will show me the way by which I may more intimately attain your opinion in this matter. For it will abundantly satisfy me, at this time, if I can hunt out, or imitate, by calculation, the things which you have hitherto established from your most accurate observations.</p><p>As for those books which I sent you from the Library of my Most Serene Prince, I will see to it that His Serene Clemency's Agent, when he returns to the Imperial court, receives them under an inventory and designation, and sends them back to me. Those manuscripts which Cyprian Leovitius left unpublished, I had sought through intermediary persons, and indeed demanded most insistently, but I laboured in vain and to no purpose, and wasted oil and effort; and so, seizing the occasion, I betook myself to Augsburg, and there, present in person, sought and obtained them from the Generous Lord Philipp Eduard Fugger, Baron etc.; these, therefore, I will send you as soon as I can, by inquiry, find out some safe means of sending. A poem, too, I will send you shortly, which, I hope, will not displease.</p><p>And with these [words] I commend myself to you most dutifully.<br>Given at Munich, the 4th day of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> scientific letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), Chancellor of Bavaria, jurist and polymath, a major correspondent of both Tycho and Kepler (Kepler's epoch-making letters on the Mars orbit were addressed in part to Herwart). He writes from <b>Munich</b>, replying to Tycho's letter of 8 April from Benátky.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, hailed <i>Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps</i> — \"easily the Prince of all Mathematicians.\"</li><li><b>The lunar-theory matter (the scientific core; protocol §5.1).</b> Herwart awaits Tycho's <i>hypothesis Lunae</i> — Tycho's <b>lunar theory</b>, with its novel inequalities (the <i>variation</i> and the <i>annual equation</i>), the most intricate part of his work — \"after it has been more deeply <b>transformed to fit the celestial appearances</b> (<i>ad apparentias coelestes</i>),\" i.e. fitted to observation. <i>ad apparentias [salvandas]</i> is the period idiom <i>salvare apparentias</i>, \"to save the appearances\" (§5.1), kept in force here. Herwart confesses he has computed the Moon \"more precisely than is warranted\" (<i>praecisius iusto</i>) in trying to reverse-engineer Tycho's reasoning from the data, the Moon's course being \"plainly slippery and variable\" (<i>plane lubricus et varius</i>) — a fair characterisation of the lunar inequalities. He asks Tycho to show him the <i>via</i> (method) to <b>imitate by calculation</b> what Tycho established from observation — the recurring early-modern tension between <i>calculus</i> (theory/computation) and <i>observationes</i>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Serenissimus Princeps meus</i> = Herwart's sovereign, the <b>Duke of Bavaria</b> (Maximilian I, in whose service Herwart was chancellor; the ducal <b>library</b> is the <i>Bibliotheca Serenissimi Principis</i>), with <i>Serenae Clementiae suae Agens</i> = the Duke's <b>agent</b> at the Imperial court as the return-channel for the loaned books.</li><li><i>Cyprianus Leovitius</i> = <b>Cyprian Leovitius (Cyprián Leovic / Karásek z Lvovic)</b> (1524–1574), the Bohemian astronomer-astrologer, whose <b>unpublished manuscripts</b> (<i>ἀνέκδοτα</i>, Greek <i>anékdota</i>, \"unpublished things\") Herwart pursued for Tycho.</li><li><i>Philippus Eduardus Fugger, Baro</i> = <b>Philipp Eduard Fugger</b> (1546–1618), of the Augsburg banking dynasty, bibliophile and collector, from whom Herwart obtained the Leovitius manuscripts in person at <b>Augsburg</b>.</li><li><b>Allusions.</b> <i>oleum et operam perdidi</i> — the proverbial \"I have wasted oil and labour\" (after <b>Plautus</b>, the lamp-oil of fruitless night-work); the Greek <i>ἀνέκδοτα</i> is transcribed and glossed per protocol §4.2.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Monachium</i> = <b>Munich</b>; <i>Augusta</i> = <b>Augsburg</b>; <i>arx Benatica</i> = Benátky.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>officiosissime me commendo</i> → \"I commend myself most dutifully\"; <i>Datae</i> (epistolary) → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>4 die Maij A. 1600</i> at Munich; the dataset marks <b>n.s.</b> — correct, Catholic Bavaria being on the Gregorian calendar. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, learned Latin; the scientific substance (the request for Tycho's lunar theory \"fitted to the appearances,\" the <i>calculus</i>-vs-<i>observationes</i> methodological point) and every named party (Herwart, the Bavarian ducal library, Cyprian Leovitius, Philipp Eduard Fugger) are securely identified, and the Greek <i>ἀνέκδοτα</i> and the Plautine tag are handled per protocol. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.311-314 as page images. Letter (\"IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE\") runs from p.312 through p.313. Collated all proper names (Cyprianus Leovitius, Philippo Eduardo Fuggero Barone, Augustam/Augsburg), the Greek loanword (ανέκδοτα), and the closing date (4 die Maij A. 1600) against Dreyer -- all match source_text exactly. Published English is a complete, accurate rendering with no fabrications or omissions.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000426002", "next": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000828002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Acknowledges receipt of Tycho's April letter, expresses relief that none of their letters have been lost, and anticipates receiving Tycho's revised lunar hypothesis.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000504003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000506001", "d": "1600-05-06", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christian II, Elector of Saxony", "ck": "Christian II, Elector of Saxony", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christian II, Elector of Saxony — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 6 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM SECUNDUM, ELECTOREM SAXONIAe.</p><p>ILLUSTRISSIME ET INCLYTE PRINCEPS, DOMINE CLEMENTISSIME.</p><p>CUM Illustrissima Celsitudo Vestra me, licet peregrinum et de facie ignotum, suas Ditiones, quas administrat, transeuntem, Clementissimo alloquio dignata sit, et torque aureâ cum suâ effigie donârit atque denuo Torgae, dum in itinere essem Bohemico, ad se in Arcem invitârit, non possum non hanc Illustrissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae erga me, nihil eiuscemodi promeritum, benignissimum favorem humilime agnoscere atque recolere. Cumque me imparem fatear ad quippiam, quod tanto Principi vicissim gratum sit, praestandum, id saltem submisse polliceri possum, me semper huius Clementissimae erga me voluntatis et beneficentiae, quam apud ipsam expertus sum, quoad vixero, memorem futurum, atque apud alios depraedicaturum. Verum enimvero ne prorsus meo officiolo defuisse videar, licet, uti dictum, longe inferior sim, quam ut remunerationis loco pro benignissimis suis erga me meritis retribuere quid valeam, ne tamen omninô ingratus atque horum immemor videar, ex tenui penu, quae pro meo modulo possum, depromo atque ad Illustrissimam Vestram Celsitudinem per Dominum Doctorem IESSENIUM, in inclyta Vestrae Celnis Academia Wittebergae Professorem, meum, dum aliquamdiu isthic morarer, hospitem, me hîc in Bohemiâ proximis diebus invisentem, humilime mitto, nimirum de Inerrantium Stellarum accurata restitutione librum, plurimorum Annorum studio et labore partum, atque unâ Globum Coelestem, in quo Affixae Stellae iuxta hanc ipsam a nobis exantlatam verificationem operâ cuiusdam artificiosi Batavi in Insula prioris meae habitationis Uraniburgica, Regni Daniae, Patriae nostrae, solerter et accurate, quantum haec fert Mechanicae Sphaerae magnitudo, repraesentatae sunt. Oroque Illmam Vram Celnem debitâ submissione et reverentiâ, ut haec munuscula ab homine Illmae Vrae Celnis observantissimo profecta non aspernari velit, sed Clementissimo animo suscipere atque me in posterum etiam sibi commendatum habere non dedignari. Pluribus illmam Vram Celnem, gravissimis suarum Ditionum administrandarum curis occupatam, interpellare nolo, neque enim decere arbitror de privatis meis rebus ad Illmam Vram Celnem quippiam perscribere, quas tamen si Illma Vra Celdo aliquatenus cognoscere non degravatur, a praenominato Doctore IESSENIO percipiet, quantâ Clementiâ et benignitate Augustissimus et Potentissimus Imperator RUDOLPHUS, Dominus noster Clementissimus, me subiectissimum suum clientem hîc susceperit, et studia mea, quae diu multumque molior, Astronomica, prae suspiciendâ et nunquam satis laudatâ ipsius Sacrae Caesae Matis erga omnes liberales Artes, inprimis vero Mathematicas, affectione et favore Clementissimo, fovere et promovere dignata est, rem hanc a summo, quod gerit in terris, officio non alienam neque se indignam censendo.</p><p>Deus opt. Max. Universi author, ipsius Caesam Matem in totius Reipae Christianae et bonarum literarum tutelam atque propagationem quam diutissime nobis incolumem conservet, cui etiam aeviterno Numini Divino Illmam Vestram Cnem unâ cum Illma coniuge et liberis inclytis toto pectore pariter commendo: in quo etiam ut Ia Co Va vivat, valeat et floreat diutissime, optime, prosperrime, ex animo precor.</p><p>Dabantur ex Arce Caesareâ Benaticâ Die 6 Maij stylo novo Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTIAN II, ELECTOR OF SAXONY.</b></p><p>Most illustrious and renowned Prince, most clement Lord.</p><p>Since Your Most Illustrious Highness deigned to address me most clemently — though a stranger and unknown by face — as I passed through the Dominions you govern, and presented me with a <b>gold chain bearing your effigy</b>, and again, at <b>Torgau</b>, while I was on my Bohemian journey, invited me to you in the Castle: I cannot but humbly acknowledge and recall this most kindly favour of Your Most Illustrious Highness toward me, who have deserved nothing of the kind. And although I confess myself unequal to performing anything that might in return be pleasing to so great a Prince, I can at least humbly promise that, as long as I live, I will ever be mindful of this most clement goodwill and beneficence toward me which I have experienced with you, and will proclaim it among others. But truly, lest I should seem to have failed altogether in my little duty — though, as I said, I am far too lowly to be able to repay anything by way of recompense for your most kindly merits toward me — yet, lest I seem wholly ungrateful and forgetful of these things, from my slender store I bring forth what I can, according to my measure, and humbly send to Your Most Illustrious Highness, through Lord Doctor <b>Jessenius</b>, professor in Your Highness's renowned Academy at Wittenberg — my host while I stayed there a while, and now, in these next days, visiting me here in Bohemia — namely, a <b>book on the accurate restitution of the Fixed Stars</b>, won by the study and labour of very many years; and together with it a <b>Celestial Globe</b>, on which the Fixed Stars are represented according to this very verification accomplished by us, by the work of a certain skilled Dutchman, on the island of my former dwelling, <b>Uraniborg</b>, of the Kingdom of Denmark, our fatherland — skilfully and accurately, so far as the size of this mechanical sphere allows. And I beg Your Most Illustrious Highness, with due submission and reverence, that you would not disdain these little gifts, proceeding from a man most respectful of Your Most Illustrious Highness, but receive them with most clement mind, and not disdain to hold me also commended to you hereafter. I do not wish to detain Your Most Illustrious Highness, occupied with the weightiest cares of governing your Dominions, with more words; nor do I think it fitting to write anything of my private affairs to Your Most Illustrious Highness — which, however, if Your Most Illustrious Highness does not find it burdensome to know in some measure, you will learn from the aforenamed Doctor Jessenius: with how great clemency and kindness the most august and most powerful <b>Emperor Rudolf</b>, our most clement Lord, has here received me, his most submissive client, and has deigned to foster and promote my astronomical studies, on which I have long and greatly laboured, by the admirable and never sufficiently praised most clement affection and favour of His Sacred Imperial Majesty toward all the liberal Arts, but especially the Mathematical — judging this matter not alien to the highest office he holds on earth, nor unworthy of himself.</p><p>May God Best and Greatest, author of the Universe, preserve His Imperial Majesty safe for us as long as may be, for the protection and propagation of the whole Christian Commonwealth and of good letters; to which everlasting Divine Power I likewise commend with my whole heart Your Most Illustrious Highness, together with your most illustrious consort and renowned children — in whom [God] I pray from my heart that Your Most Illustrious Highness may live, fare well, and flourish, as long, as well, and as prosperously as possible.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 6th day of May, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christian II, Elector of Saxony</b> (1583–1611), then a young prince (the Electorate under the Administrator's regency at this date; cf. brahe-t_16000324001). Tycho had passed through Electoral Saxony on his way from Wandsbek/Wittenberg to Bohemia.<br>- <b>Significance — a presentation letter (gifts of book + globe).</b> Tycho thanks the Elector for two marks of favour — a <b>gold chain (<i>torques aurea</i>) bearing the Elector's effigy</b>, and an <b>invitation to the castle at Torgau</b> during Tycho's Bohemian journey — and reciprocates with two <b>gifts</b>, sent through <b>Jessenius</b> (Wittenberg professor and Tycho's recent host, then visiting him in Bohemia):<br>  1. the <b>book on the accurate restitution of the Fixed Stars</b> (the fixed-star catalogue, \"won by the study and labour of very many years\" — the same work presented to Duke Ulrich, brahe-t_15980914002, and carried to Magini, brahe-t_15991104001); and<br>  2. a <b>Celestial Globe</b> carrying his restored star-positions, <b>made by \"a certain skilled Dutchman\" at Uraniborg</b> on Hven — i.e. one of the celebrated <b>Tychonic globes</b> produced in the Uraniborg workshop. A notable material-history datum (logged for census/material cross-reference).<br>- <b>The Torgau connection.</b> The Torgau-castle invitation links to the contemporaneous Saxon-court correspondence: Tycho's letter to <b>Georg von Wolframsdorf</b>, the Elector's chamber-junker at Torgau (brahe-t_16000508001), dispatched — like this one — through Jessenius.<br>- <b>Rudolf II's patronage.</b> Tycho refers the Elector to Jessenius for the fuller account of how <b>Rudolf II</b> \"received me, his most submissive client,\" and fosters his astronomy \"by … favour toward all the liberal Arts, but especially the Mathematical\" — the standard framing of the imperial establishment (cf. brahe-t_16000106001, brahe-t_16000324001).<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Christian II of Saxony; <b>Johannes Jessenius</b> (courier/host); the unnamed <b>Dutch globe-maker</b> at Uraniborg; <b>Rudolf II</b>; the Elector's consort and children.<br>- <b>Abbreviations.</b> Heavy honorific contraction (<i>Illma Vra Celdo/Celnem</i> = <i>Illustrissima Vestra Celsitudo/Celsitudinem</i>; <i>Ia Co Va</i> = <i>Illustrissima Celsitudo Vestra</i>; <i>Caesae Matis</i> = <i>Caesareae Maiestatis</i>) silently expanded; the source block keeps them.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Die 6 Maij stylo novo Anni 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label. (Same date-cluster as the Galileo/Concini letters of 4 May 1600.)</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — formal but fully legible Latin, read securely; the occasion (thanks for the gold chain and Torgau invitation; the reciprocal gift of the fixed-star book and the Uraniborg-made celestial globe, sent via Jessenius; the reference to Rudolf II's patronage) is clear and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; the Dutch globe-maker is left unnamed per the no-fabrication rule, and the globe is flagged for material/census cross-reference.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.313-314 in full as page images (within the 310-315 slice, which also carries the neighbouring letters to an unnamed dignitary, to Galileo, from Herwart, and to Rudolph Corraducius; the Christian II letter itself runs from the bottom of 313 through 314 and ends without a signature line, matching source_text exactly). Source_text matches Dreyer word-for-word, including the gold chain and effigy, the Torgau meeting, Doctor Jessenius, the celestial globe made by an unnamed Dutch craftsman at Uraniborg, Emperor Rudolf, and the closing '6 Maij stylo novo Anni 1600'. Published_english renders every clause accurately with no defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Christian II for past kindnesses including a gold chain and an invitation at Torgau, and presents him with a star-table inscribed on parchment, sent via Jessenius.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000506001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000506002", "d": "1600-05-06", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck": "Rudolf Coraduz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Rudolf Coraduz — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 6 May 1600 [N.S.] (catalogued 5 May)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD RUDOLPHUM CORRADUCIUM.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR, DOMINE OBSERVANDISSIME.</p><p>CUM rumor hîc nuper increbuisset, Sacram Caesam Matem, Dominum nostrum Clementissimum, Pragam sub initia modo elapsae hebdomadae redituram, et unâ cum ea vestram Illem Domnem ibi affuturam, mox eo excurrens nihil magis in votis habui quam vestram Illem Domnem coram intueri et officiosissime compellare, prout magno eius rei desiderio diu flagravi. Et licet spe hac nunc frustratus sim, id tamen propediem meliori successu futurum confido. Interim domesticum illum meum, qui prius Pilsnae fuit, isthuc ad vestram Illem Domnem denuo ablego. Et quoniam FRANCISCUS TENGNAGLIUS noster, qui meo nomine vestram Illem Domnem ibi convenit, mihi, cum in procinctu esset sui isthinc in Patriam abitus, per literas fiduciam fecit de nonnullis, quae Sacra Caesa Matas, intercessione Illustris Dominationis vestrae, mihi Clementissime indulsit, quorum tamen necdum, ob alias intervenientes remoras, compos factus sum: idcirco Illem Domem vestram, qua possum reverentia rogatam habeo, velit, quatenus per alias suas gravissimas occupationes licet, ut ea executioni committantur, efficere non degravari, inprimis vero quod Praedium illud hîc in veteri Bennaticka mihi Clementissime ab ipsius Caesa Mate deputatum (illud enim necdum, licet anni tempus eius culturam urgeat, accepi) si itaque Illris Domo vestra ad Capitaneum Brandeisensem hac de re pauculis scribere atque ei ex Clementissimo Sacrae Caesae Matis mandato serio iniungere, ut Praedium mihi hoc absque diuturniore dilatione aut apud alios solicitatione tradat, non dubito, quin is sit obtemperaturus: tum quoque ut vestra Illris Domio de aedificijs his Benaticensibus pro Instrumentis meis parandis, et si quae sunt alia huc facientia, quatenus fieri debeant vel secus, ut eorum structura diu iam neglecta compleatur, provisionem [com]petentem facere velit. Omnia enim hîc nimis lente et difficulter procedunt, nec habeo commoda loca, in quibus organa mea Astronomica, ubi huc Magdaeburgo et Hamburgo adlata fuerint, reponam, quo ab iniuria aëris et pluviarum tuta sint. Sed de his atque alijs, quae necessitas cogit me ad vestrae Illris Domis opem et promotionem confugere, is, quem nunc denuo mitto, plenius [per] oportunitatem exponet. Nolo itaque Illrem Domem vestram alijs quam plurimis gravissimis Reipub. curis distentam hisce remorari.</p><p>Deus Opt. Max. eam quam diutissime feliciter valere et florere faciat.<br>Datae ex Arce Benaticka die 6 Maij Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Rudolf Coraduz.</b></p><p>Illustrious and generous man, most-to-be-revered lord.</p><p>When a rumour lately grew strong here that the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most Clement Lord, would return to Prague about the beginning of the week just past, and that your Illustrious Lordship would be there together with him, I soon ran thither, having nothing more in my wishes than to behold your Illustrious Lordship in person and address you most dutifully — as I have long burned with great desire for that. And although I am now frustrated of this hope, I trust it will shortly come about with better success. Meanwhile I send that servant of mine, who was earlier at Pilsen, again thither to your Illustrious Lordship. And since our Franz Tengnagel, who waited on your Illustrious Lordship there in my name, gave me assurance by letter — when he was on the point of his departure thence for his homeland — concerning certain things which the Sacred Imperial Majesty, by your Illustrious Lordship's intercession, most clemently granted me (of which, however, I have not yet been made possessor, on account of other intervening delays): I therefore have your Illustrious Lordship asked, with what reverence I can, that — so far as your other most weighty occupations allow — you would not find it a burden to bring it about that these be put into execution. Above all, as regards that <b>estate</b> here in old Benátky most clemently assigned to me by the Imperial Majesty (for I have not yet received it, though the season of the year presses for its cultivation): if your Illustrious Lordship would therefore write a few words to the Captain of Brandeis about this matter, and earnestly enjoin him, by the most clement mandate of the Sacred Imperial Majesty, to hand over this estate to me without longer delay or soliciting among others, I doubt not he would obey. Then too, that your Illustrious Lordship would make competent provision concerning these buildings at Benátky for the preparing of my instruments — and whatever else conduces hither, how far they ought to be done or otherwise — so that their construction, now long neglected, may be completed. For everything here proceeds too slowly and with difficulty, nor have I convenient places in which to store my astronomical instruments, when they shall have been brought hither from Magdeburg and Hamburg, so that they be safe from the injury of weather and rains. But of these matters and others, in which necessity compels me to take refuge in your Illustrious Lordship's aid and furtherance, the man whom I now send again will more fully explain at opportunity. I do not wish, therefore, to detain with these [lines] your Illustrious Lordship, stretched as you are by so many most weighty cares of the commonwealth.</p><p>May God, best and greatest, make you fare happily and flourish for the longest time.<br>Given from the castle of Benátky, the 6th of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; purpose.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky</b> — pressing, through the highest channel, for the <b>practical fulfilment of the Emperor's promises</b>: the assigned <b>estate</b> and the <b>observatory buildings</b>. The letter is a key document for the troubled material history of the Benátky establishment (where Kepler had just been working).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Rudolphus Corraducius</i> = <b>Rudolf Coraduz (Coraduz)</b>, <b>imperial vice-chancellor</b> under Rudolf II — one of Tycho's two principal court patrons (the <i>Coraduz/Barvitius</i> axis; cf. brahe-t_16000813001). Addressed as <i>Illustris Dominatio Vestra</i>, \"your Illustrious Lordship.\"</li><li><b>The two asks.</b></li><li>The <b>estate (<i>Praedium</i>) in \"old Benátky\"</b> (<i>vetus Bennaticka</i>, Staré Benátky), granted by the Emperor but not yet handed over — \"though the season of the year presses for its cultivation\"; Tycho asks Coraduz to order the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b> to deliver it.</li><li><b>Provision for the Benátky buildings</b> to house and set up Tycho's <b>instruments</b> (<i>organa Astronomica</i>, §5.2), whose construction is \"long neglected\"; Tycho has nowhere safe to store the instruments once they arrive <b>from Magdeburg and Hamburg</b> (the consignment of his effects out of Germany — cf. the Magdeburg-goods letter brahe-t_16000926001). \"Everything here proceeds too slowly and with difficulty.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Franciscus Tengnaglius noster</i> = <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, who had interceded with Coraduz in Tycho's name before departing for Westphalia (cf. brahe-t_16000306002).</li><li><i>Capitaneus Brandeisensis</i> = the <b>Captain of Brandeis</b> (Brandýs), the local officer who must convey the estate.</li><li><i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Benaticka arx / vetus Bennaticka</i> = Benátky / old Benátky; <i>Pilsna</i> = Plzeň (the court's refuge); <i>Magdeburgum, Hamburgum</i> = Magdeburg and Hamburg (whence the instruments were coming).</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Datae</i> (epistolary) → \"Given\"; <i>Deus Opt. Max. … florere faciat</i> → the closing benediction.</li><li><b>Calendar &amp; header/label note (protocol §3, §4.1).</b> The dateline reads <i>die 6 Maij Anno 1600</i>; the dataset's catalogue label gives <b>5 May (n.s.)</b>. The header therefore <b>leads with the as-written 6 May</b> and records the catalogue's <i>5 May</i> as <i>(catalogued 5 May)</i> per protocol §4.1, rather than adopting it silently. New Style (Imperial service), not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean Latin and historically valuable content (the undelivered Benátky estate, the neglected observatory buildings, the instruments in transit from Magdeburg/Hamburg, Coraduz and Tengnagel as the channels). The only point of care, the one-day <b>dateline-vs-catalogue discrepancy</b> (source 6 May, catalogue 5 May), is flagged and led per §4.1, not a textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.313-316 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\"). Letter runs from mid-p.314 (\"TYCHO BRAHE AD RUDOLPHUM CORRADUCIUM\") through mid-p.315 (\"...Anno 1600\"). Collated full body against source_text: opening/closing formulae, all proper names (Franciscus Tengnaglius, Capitaneus Brandeisensis), place-names (Bennaticka/Benátky, Magdaeburgo, Hamburgo), and the date (6 Maij 1600) all match Dreyer exactly; source_text has no departures from the printed edition. Published English renders every clause faithfully with no additions, omissions, or sense-changing errors.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Rudolf Coraduz", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_15990127001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990127001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports a failed attempt to meet Coraduz in Prague and presses for confirmation of imperial grants promised through Tengnagel's intercession before his departure.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000506002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000507001", "d": "1600-05-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Barwitz", "ck": "Johann Barwitz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Barvitius — Benátky, 7 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IOHANNI BARVITIO, SACRAe CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS SECRETARIO SUPREMO ET A CONSILIJS INTIMIS, DOMINO ET AMICO HONORANDISSIMO.</p><p>MAGNIFICE ET NOBILISSIME VIR, DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>QUONIAM ea, de quibus Maga Va mihi per domesticum meum, quem ultimo Pilsnae habui, spem fecit, brevi effectum exoptatum habitura, adhuc infecta haereant, et frustra hac praeterlapsa septimana cum Sacra Caesa Maiestate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, Vae Magae accessum a multis pariter speratum per aliquot dies Pragae expectarem, cogor denuo (siquidem res ulteriorem moram vix patiatur, et incertus sim, quam cito Sa Caesa Matas eo redire decreverit) eundem familiarem ad vos amandare, ut si per alias multiplices occupationes fieri nunc commodius quam antea possit, plenariam aliquam resolutionem obtinere queam, quod Magae Vae favorabili intercessione me impetraturum confido. Quaenam vero illa sint, de quibus adhuc sollicitus detineor, is, quem amando, Vae Magae rectius et plenius, quando ipsum audire commodum fuerit, exponet, ut non opus sit multis verborum ambagibus, eam alias occupatissimam hisce literis ulterius interpellare. Rogo autem Magam Vam officiose, quemadmodum semper sua benevolentia mihi praesto fuit, sic etiam nunc non deesse velit. Inveniet me semper pro mea tenuitate eius vicissim studiosissimum et observantissimum.</p><p>Valeat Maga Va quam diutissime et quam felicissime.<br>Dabantur ex arce Benaticka die 7 Maij Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Master Johann Barvitius, Supreme Secretary of His Sacred Imperial Majesty and of the privy councils, lord and most honoured friend.</b></p><p>Magnificent and most noble man, lord and most honoured friend.</p><p>Since those things — about which Your Magnificence gave me hope, through my servant, whom I last had with [me] at Pilsen, that they would shortly have the wished-for effect — still stick undone, and since this past week I waited in vain, for some days at Prague, for Your Magnificence's access to His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord (an access equally hoped-for by many): I am compelled again — since indeed the matter scarcely suffers further delay, and I am uncertain how soon His Sacred Imperial Majesty has resolved to return thither — to send the same servant to you, so that, if it can now be done more conveniently than before amid your manifold other occupations, I may obtain some full resolution; which I trust I shall obtain through Your Magnificence's favourable intercession. But what those things are, about which I am still held anxious, he whom I send will set forth to Your Magnificence more rightly and fully, when it shall be convenient to hear him — so that there is no need, by many windings of words, to interrupt you (otherwise most occupied) further with these letters. But I ask Your Magnificence dutifully that, as your benevolence has always been at hand for me, so now too you would not be wanting. You will find me always, for my slenderness, in turn most zealous and most observant of you.</p><p>May Your Magnificence fare as long and as happily as possible.<br>Given from the castle of Benátky, the 7th day of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Barvitius</b> (Barwitz), here styled <i>Secretarius Supremus</i> and <i>a consiliis intimis</i> of His Imperial Majesty — Rudolf II's <b>privy secretary</b> and the gate-keeper of access to the Emperor (the same Barvitius to whom Elector Ernst had written on Tycho's behalf, brahe-t_15980626002).</li><li><b>Content.</b> Tycho, <b>still awaiting a \"full resolution\"</b> (<i>plenaria resolutio</i>) of his affairs (his stipend and standing) more than a year into his Bohemian residence, presses Barvitius to expedite matters: he had waited in vain at Prague for Barvitius's audience with the Emperor, and now sends his servant again, since \"the matter scarcely suffers further delay.\" The servant — not the letter — will explain the particulars. A characteristic glimpse of the slow, frustrating Rudolfine bureaucracy.</li><li><b><i>Pilsnae</b></i> = <b>Pilsen (Plzeň)</b>, where Rudolf II's court had withdrawn (recurrently, on account of plague at Prague); Tycho's servant had last met Barvitius there.</li><li><b>Abbreviations.</b> <i>Maga Va</i> = <i>Magnificentia Vestra</i> (\"Your Magnificence\"); <i>Sacra Caesa Maiestas</i> = <i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas</i>; <i>a consiliis intimis</i> = \"of the privy councils [council secretary].\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>ex arce Benaticka die 7 Maij 1600</i> = from the <b>castle of Benátky</b>, 7 May 1600; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Latin is rendered securely; the recipient (Barvitius, Rudolf II's privy secretary), the situation (Tycho pressing for the \"full resolution\" of his imperial appointment, the court at Pilsen), and the date are all clear and corpus-consistent.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.314-317 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.315-316, text matches Dreyer word for word. The place name Pilsen and the date (7 May 1600) correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presses Barvitius to resolve pending matters at the imperial court, having waited fruitlessly in Prague for an audience, and sends a servant to facilitate proceedings.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000507001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000507002", "d": "1600-05-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Jessenius", "ck": "Johannes Jessenius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Jessenius — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 7 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILI ET CLARISSIMO ERUDITISSIMOQUE DOMINO IOHANNI IESSENIO A IESSEN, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI ET PROFESSORI VITEBERGENSI PUBLICO, AMICO SUO SINCERE DILECTO.</p><p>S</p><p>GLOBUM, quem Illustrissimo Principi tuo destinâram, nunc ad te mitto, amantissime Domine IESSENI, ut tuam rectius redimam isthic fidem (si quid de eo iam ante accessum scripsisti) quam tu propriam apud me, in eo, quod te huc rediturum pollicitus es, licet iam id ignorare ais atque detrectas. Addidi librum de Fixarum restitutione accuratissima viginti Annorum indefesso labore exantlata, eumque in membranam exaratum, tum pariter Electori Saxoniae (cui Celsitudini inclyto unâ scripsi) meo nomine humilime a te adferri cupio, ut habeat eius Celsitudo Canonicam Stellarum expositionem in promptu, si forte aliquando maiorem globum confieri curare velit. Exoptarem autem hunc Abacum stelliferum, cuius similem priora secula (absit invidia dicto) vix viderunt, ipsius Celsitudo sibi soli reservare velit, nec alijs communicare, ne sint, qui nostra inventa sibi arrogent atque ijs abutantur, prout aliquoties mihi in alijs quibusdam contigit. Habes etiam hîc Mechanicae Astronomiae exemplar Magnifico Domino Cancellario Vestro ex me, praemissa officiosa salutatione, porrigendum. Subscripsi saltem nomen meum, tu titulum eius, quem non satis perspectum habeo, superius appone, et librum, si sic lubet, compingi effice, antequam illi exhibeas. Adiunxi insuper literas ad nobilissimum Virum, Dominum GEORGIUM a WOLFRAMSDORF, Cubicularium eiusdem Principis, quas illi tradas velim. Habet praeterea [DANIEL?] secum exemplar pro Illustri Domino Cancellario Bohemico, quod illi exhibere vellet, ubi convenienter ligatum fuerit. Sic satis superque obtines ea, quae tibi redeunti committere in me recepi. Tuum est, si promissi memor esse velis, ea, quae a Domino MECKERO expiscanda tibi concredidi, ad me per literas ante abitum referre, et Procuratorem illum, de quo scis, huc ad me dirigere, ita ut cum DANIELE revertente accedat. Tu vero, mi IESSENI, feliciter domum redi, atque quam optime vale.</p><p>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benatica die 7 Maij Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble, most renowned and most learned Lord Johannes Jessenius a Jessen, doctor of philosophy and medicine and public professor at Wittenberg, his sincerely beloved friend.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>The globe which I had destined for your Most Illustrious Prince I now send to you, most loving Master Jessenius, that I may there more rightly redeem your pledged word (if you have written anything about it before your arrival) than you keep your own with me in the matter of your having promised to return hither — though now you say you know nothing of it and beg off. I have added a book on the most accurate restoration of the Fixed [Stars], drained out by twenty years' indefatigable labour and inscribed on parchment; and this likewise I desire to be brought in my name, most humbly through you, to the Elector of Saxony (to which renowned Highness I have written at the same time), so that His Highness may have a canonical exposition of the stars ready at hand, should he perhaps some day wish to have a larger globe made. I would wish, however, that His Highness keep this star-table — the like of which past ages (be it said without envy) have scarcely seen — reserved for himself alone, and not share it with others, lest there be those who arrogate my discoveries to themselves and abuse them, as has several times befallen me in certain other matters. You have here also a copy of the <i>Mechanics of Astronomy</i> to be presented from me, with a dutiful greeting premised, to your Magnificent Lord Chancellor. I have subscribed only my name; do you add his title above (which I have not sufficiently ascertained), and, if you so please, have the book bound before you present it to him. I have moreover enclosed a letter to the most noble man, Lord Georg von Wolframsdorf, chamberlain of the same Prince, which I would have you deliver to him. Besides this, [Daniel?] has with him a copy for the Illustrious Lord Chancellor of Bohemia, which he is to present to him once it has been suitably bound. Thus you obtain, fully and more than fully, the things I undertook to commit to you on your return. It is your part — if you would be mindful of your promise — to report to me by letter, before your departure, the things I entrusted to you to be drawn out by inquiry from Master Mecker, and to direct hither to me that Procurator of whom you know, so that he may come with Daniel on his return. But do you, my dear Jessenius, return home happily, and fare you as well as may be.</p><p>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 7th day of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (protocol §5.7), the imperial castle assigned to him before the move to Prague.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johannes Jessenius a Jessen</b> (Jan Jesenský, 1566–1621), physician and anatomist, <i>professor publicus</i> at Wittenberg, returning home to Saxony after a visit; he conducted the famous public anatomy at Prague in June 1600 and remained a friend of both Tycho and Kepler. He is the courier for the whole consignment of gifts and books described here.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Illustrissimus Princeps tuus / Elector Saxoniae / eius Celsitudo</i> = <b>the Elector of Saxony</b>, i.e. <b>Christian II</b> (Elector 1591–1611; still a minor under the regency of Duke Friedrich Wilhelm until 1601) — Jessenius's territorial prince, the intended recipient of the celestial globe and star-catalogue.</li><li><i>Georgius a Wolframsdorf, Cubicularius eiusdem Principis</i> = <b>Georg von Wolframsdorf</b>, chamber-junker (here <i>Cubicularius</i>, \"chamberlain\") to the Saxon Elector; the enclosed letter to him is brahe-t_16000508001 (Benátky, 8 May 1600 — dated the day <i>after</i> this one).</li><li><i>Magnificus Dominus Cancellarius Vester</i> = the <b>Chancellor of [Electoral] Saxony</b>, recipient of a <i>Mechanica</i> (Tycho leaves the title blank, not knowing it exactly).</li><li><i>Illustris Dominus Cancellarius Bohemicus</i> = the <b>Chancellor of Bohemia</b>, i.e. <b>Zdeněk Vojtěch Popel of Lobkowicz</b> (Chancellor from 1599), recipient of a further <i>Mechanica</i>.</li><li><i>[Daniel?]</i> and <i>Daniele revertente</i> = a servant or assistant named <b>Daniel</b> carrying one of the presentation copies; the square brackets and query are the editor's, the name being uncertain in the source.</li><li><i>Dominus Meckerus</i> = <b>(Master) Mecker</b>, otherwise unidentified, charged with explaining some matter to Jessenius; <i>Procurator ille, de quo scis</i> = an unnamed agent/proctor whom Tycho wants sent to him.</li><li><b>Works mentioned (protocol §5.1–5.2).</b></li><li><i>globus / Abacus stellifer / maior globus</i> = a <b>celestial globe</b> and the prospect of a <b>larger globe</b> — echoing Tycho's own great Augsburg globe at Uraniborg.</li><li><i>liber de Fixarum restitutione … in membranam exaratus</i> = the <b>catalogue of the fixed stars</b> (Tycho's <i>restitutio</i> of the stellar positions, the fruit of \"twenty years' labour\"), here a presentation copy <b>written on parchment</b>; <i>Canonica Stellarum expositio</i> = its \"canonical exposition of the stars.\" <i>restitutio</i> kept as the programme-term (protocol §5.1).</li><li><i>Mechanica Astronomiae</i> = <b>Astronomiae Instauratae Mechanica</b> (Wandsbek 1598), Tycho's illustrated account of his instruments, distributed as presentation copies to patrons.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arx Caesarea Benatica</i> = the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i> (\"Greeting\"). <i>Vale / quam optime vale</i> → \"fare you as well as may be.\" <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary) → \"Given.\" The opening play on <i>fides</i> (Jessenius's broken promise to return vs. Tycho redeeming the pledge over the globe) is preserved, not smoothed.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 7 Maij Anno 1600</i>; dataset marks <b>n.s.</b> (New Style), consistent with Tycho's Imperial-service dating and with the companion Wolframsdorf letter of 8 May. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'restitutione' corrected to 'restitutione accuratissima', and 'explicanda' corrected to 'expiscanda', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 316-317. English corrected to match at both points.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is clean and the sense secure throughout; graded B (not A) only because two proper names are themselves uncertain in the source — the courier <i>[Daniel?]</i> (bracketed and queried by the editor) and <i>Dominus Meckerus</i>, who cannot be confidently identified. The translation preserves both uncertainties rather than resolving them.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 Dreyer slice pages as page images (TBOO VIII pp.315-318; the Jessenius letter runs pp.316-317, bracketed by the end of the Corraducius letter and the start of the Barvitius/Wolframsdorf/Brahe items). Collated the full body of source_text and published_english against the printed Latin clause by clause: opening address and greeting, the globe/star-restoration book to the Elector of Saxony, the Abacus stelliferum request for exclusivity, the Mechanica Astronomiae copy for the Chancellor, the enclosed letter to Georg von Wolframsdorf, the bracketed-uncertain '[Daniel?]' courier and the later definite 'Daniele', the Mecker/Procurator instructions, the closing valediction, and the dateline 'die 7 Maij Anno 1600' (matches title '7 May 1600 [N.S.]', consistent with Dreyer's dual heading '27 Apr./7 Mai. 1600'). All proper names (Jessenius, Wolframsdorf, Daniel, Mecker), places (Benatica/Benatky, Wittenberg, Saxony, Bohemia), and the date check out with no invented day-numbers, no fabricated clauses, no silently dropped sentences, and no pronoun or custody-attribution errors. Found two small TEXT_ERROR discrepancies between our source_text and Dreyer's actual printed Latin (a dropped 'accuratissima'; 'explicanda' printed for Dreyer's 'expiscanda'); the English translation faithfully -- if uncritically -- follows source_text in both spots, so the translation itself introduces no further defect beyond inheriting the source transcription errors. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though both instances are low-severity and do not change the letter's overall sense, hence verdict MINOR.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text silently drops the superlative adjective 'accuratissima' (most accurate/painstaking) that Dreyer prints modifying 'restitutione', losing Tycho's own emphasis on the exactness of his twenty-year star-catalogue restoration; the English inherits the omission ('the restoration' rather than 'the most accurate restoration'). Dreyer prints: <i>Addidi librum de Fixarum reftitutione accuratißima viginti Annorum indefeßo labore exantlata, eumque in membranam exaratum (TBOO VIII p.316)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints 'expiscanda' (things to be found out / drawn out by inquiry from Mecker), but our source_text substitutes 'explicanda' (things to be explained by Mecker) -- a different gerundive that shifts who does the active work (Jessenius eliciting information vs. Mecker volunteering an explanation). The English follows the erroneous source_text reading. Dreyer prints: <i>ea, quae a Domino MECKERO expifcanda tibi concredidi (TBOO VIII p.317)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.315-318 page images. 'accuratißima' (p.316) and 'expifcanda' (p.317) are both unambiguous. Both corrections carried into the English per the TEXT_ERROR repair rule, since each corrupted Latin word had misled the translation. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Jessenius", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000328001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends Jessenius a globe for the Elector of Saxony and a star-table inscribed on parchment, requesting that he deliver both to the Elector on Tycho's behalf.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000507002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000507003", "d": "1600-05-07", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ehrenfried Freiherr von Minckwitz", "ck": "Ehrenfried Freiherr von Minckwitz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ehrenfried, Freiherr von Minckwitz — Benátky, 7 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (with a Latin clause) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 114f. (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM WOLGEBORNEN HERRN, HERRN EHRENFRIDT VON MINCKWITZ, FREYHERRN ZU MINKWITZBURG AUF HOLESSHAU VND DREHNA, ROM. KAY. MA:T. APPELLATION-RATH, MEINEM IMBESONDERN GUNSTIGEN HERRNN VNND GUETTEN FREUNDT.</p><p>WOLGEBORNER, jnbesonder gunstiger herr vnd freundt. Nach wunschung freindtlichenn gruesses, auch erböttung meiner gantz willigenn vnnd geflissenen dienstenn gebe jch dem herrn hiemit freindtlichenn zuuerstehenn, wie das der wolgeborene herr Carolus freyherr von Wartenberg gestrigen tages mich alhie auf dem kaiserlichenn schloß Bennatig besuchet, vnnd vnter anderem vermeldet, das der herr auf die pfingstfeyertage bey jhme zu sein gentzlichen bey sich beschlossenn vnd zugesagt habe. Gelanget demnach an den herrnn mein freindtliches biettenn, derselbige wolle mich gunstchtiglichenn wiessenn lassenn, welchen tag der herr jhm es proponiret habe, damit jch mich vnd die meinigen auf die reise praepariren möge. Vnd nachdem der her auch hatt mir gunstchtiglichenn gehlobet, das er diesen weg ziehen will, vnd mich alhie zu besuchen ..., damit wir mit einander hinziehen, dan ich jhm auch haben zusagen mussen, bey jhme zu sein. Versehe mich gennzlich, dass der herr dis nit abschlagen werde. Jn den sachen, davon ich mit dem herrn vertreulich geredet habe, bitte jch den herrn ganz freindtlichenn, derselbige wolle di mitlerzeit wol erwegenn, vnd wan wir, ob Gott wil, zusammen kommen, mir mit einem gutten rat darin erscheinen, dan ich gentzlich muß darzu thun was sich geburet, ut meum et meorum honorem, pro ut decet, legitime tuear. Welchs jch nach vermögenn vmb den herrn vnnd all den seinigenn zuuerschuldenn jederzeitt gantz willig wiel erfundenn werdenn. Vnd thue hiemit den herrnn jch jn schutz des Almechtigenn treulichenn befehlen.</p><p>Gebenn auf dem kayserlichenn schloß Bennatig am 7 tag maij a:o 1600.<br>Des herrn altzeit dinst- und freindtwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>Eign h.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the well-born Lord, Lord Ehrenfried von Minckwitz, Freiherr zu Minckwitzburg, [lord] of Holešov and Drehna, Roman Imperial Majesty's Appellation-Councillor, my especially favourable lord and good friend.</b></p><p>Well-born, especially favourable lord and friend. After the wishing of a friendly greeting, and the offering of my quite willing and assiduous services, I give the lord herewith friendlily to understand how the well-born lord Carl, Freiherr von Wartenberg, visited me yesterday here at the Imperial castle Benátky, and, among other things, reported that the lord [you] have fully resolved with himself, and promised, to be with him on the Pentecost holidays. There accordingly reaches the lord my friendly request: that the same would favourably let me know which day the lord [Wartenberg] has proposed it to him [you], so that I may prepare myself and mine for the journey. And since the lord [Wartenberg] has also favourably promised me that he will travel this way and visit me here … so that we may travel on together (for I, too, have had to promise him to be with him), I fully trust that the lord [you] will not decline this. In the matters of which I have spoken confidentially with the lord, I ask the lord quite friendlily that the same would, meanwhile, well consider them, and, when we — God willing — come together, present me with good counsel therein; for I must altogether do what is fitting, <i>so as lawfully to protect my own and my family's honour, as is proper</i> [<i>ut meum et meorum honorem, pro ut decet, legitime tuear</i>]. Which I will, at all times, be found quite willing to requite, according to my ability, toward the lord and all his. And I herewith faithfully commend the lord into the protection of the Almighty.</p><p>Given at the Imperial castle Benátky, the 7th day of May, in the year 1600.<br>The lord's ever service- and friendship-willing,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ehrenfried, Freiherr von Minckwitz</b>, of Minckwitzburg, [lord] of <b>Holešov</b> (<i>Holesshau</i>) and <b>Drehna</b>, an <b>Imperial Appellation-Councillor</b> (<i>Appellationsrat</i> of the Bohemian appellate court) — an influential Prague-court figure whose counsel Tycho seeks.</li><li><b>Content.</b> A planning letter: <b>Carl, Freiherr von Wartenberg</b>, had visited Tycho at Benátky and reported that Minckwitz intended to be with Wartenberg over <b>Pentecost</b>; Tycho, who is also to travel with Wartenberg, asks Minckwitz to fix the day so he can prepare his household for the journey.</li><li><b>The confidential honour-matter (Latin tag).</b> Tycho refers to \"the matters of which I have spoken <b>confidentially</b>\" with Minckwitz, and asks for his counsel, \"for I must altogether do what is fitting, <i>ut meum et meorum honorem, pro ut decet, legitime tuear</i>\" — \"<b>so as lawfully to protect my own and my family's honour, as is proper</b>.\" The Latin code-switch (kept and italicised per §2) marks the gravity of the matter — almost certainly the <b>defence of Tycho's honour/priority in the Ursus affair</b> or a related legal-reputational concern then preoccupying him (cf. the Ursus suppression, brahe-t_16000727001). The exact matter is not named in the source; <i>flagged</i>, not over-specified.</li><li><b><i>Eign h.</b></i> = <i>eigene Hand</i> (\"own hand\"). The lacuna (<i>…</i>) in \"visit me here …\" is a gap in the draft (<i>Kladde</i>), left as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>kayserlichenn schloß Bennatig am 7 tag maij a:o 1600</i> = <b>7 May 1600</b>; Bohemia (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label. (Written the same day as the Latin Barvitius letter brahe-t_16000507001.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German (with its Latin clause) is rendered securely; the occasion (the Pentecost journey with Wartenberg; the request to Minckwitz) is clear. Grade [B] because the <b>confidential \"honour-matter\"</b> Tycho seeks counsel on is deliberately unspecified in the source (plausibly the Ursus/reputation affair) — flagged, not invented — and the draft carries a small lacuna.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.201-203 as page images; Tycho's letter to Minckwitz (Dreyer's item 195) runs from bottom of p.201 to p.202. source_text matches Dreyer's printed German/Latin verbatim throughout, including the quoted Latin clause 'ut meum et meorum honorem, pro ut decet, legitime tuear' (Dreyer prints '&amp;' for 'et', a typographic variant only). Collated translation clause-by-clause: Carl von Wartenberg's visit, the Pentecost travel arrangements, and the closing commendation all render faithfully, including correct bracketed disambiguation of the ambiguous 'jhm' (= Minckwitz). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 114 f. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Carl von Wartenberg's visit and requests to know which Pentecost day Wartenberg has proposed for their joint meeting so Tycho can prepare for the journey.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000507003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000508001", "d": "1600-05-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg von Wolframsdorf", "ck": "Georg von Wolframsdorf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Georg von Wolframsdorf — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 8 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDELEN, GESTRENGEN VNDT EHRENUEHSTEN GEORGEN VONN WOLFRAMSDORF, ERBSESSEN VFF VOLLMERSHAIN ETC., FURSTLICHEN SECHSISCHEN CAMMER IUNGKHERN ZUE TORGAW, MEINEM INNSONDERS GUNSTIGEN VNDT GUETEN FREUNDT.</p><p>EDLER, Gestrenger, Ehrenuehster Innsonders gönstiger vndt guetter Freundt, Dem Herrn sein meine willige geflissene dienste hier wiederumb nach Vermögen zuuor. Vndt wann es demselben glücklich vndt wohl ergienge, wehre es mihr gantz lieb vndt angenehm zuerfahren. Dess Herrn an mich gethanes schreiben, sub dato Torgaw den 3 Martij, habe ich vonn dem H. Doctore IESSENIO wohl empfangen, durch welchen ich auch den herrn hienwieder mit einen schreiben freundtlich zuersuchen nicht hab vndterlassen können. Belangende meinen vndt der Meinigen Zuestandt alhier inn Konnigreich Behemen, ist derselbige vndter Ihr Kays. May. Vnsers allergnedigsten herrn, gnedigsten schutz vndt Gottlob inn guetten wesen, wie der herr mitt mehren Vmbstanden vonn dem obgedachten herrn Doctore IESSENIO, vnserer beider gueten freundt, vernehmen kan. So ich alhier dem herrn im Kaysserlichen hof oder sonsten etwas dienstlich sein kondte, wil ich allzeitt nach meinen Vermögen willig darzue erfunden werden. Von Newer Zeittung weiss ich nichts sonderlichs zueschreiben; allein das man sagt, das der Türck sich gar starck, vndt noch mehr als zuuor, ruste. Vnser gnedigster Kaysser ist noch nicht vonn Pilsen gen Prag wieder ankommen; Man verhoffet aber, das Ihr Maiestet mit dem ehrsten wieder alda sein werde; vndt seindt die Muscowiterischen gesandten, als mann vermeinet, auch dahien gefodert, alda ihr gentzliche abfertigung zueerlangen. Mann ist auch etzlicher anderer gesandten der deutschen Fürsten alda Vermuthen. Diss habe ich also dem herrn vff sein schreiben wiederumb zuerkennen geben wollen. Vndt thue vns hiermitt sambtlichen dem Allmechtigen Gott inn seinen Vätterlichen Schutz vndt beschirmung trewlich befehlen.</p><p>Datum vff dem Kayss. Schloss Benatky den 8 Maij Anno 1600.<br>D. H.<br>Allzeitt Dienstwilliger</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble, gallant and honourable Georg von Wolframsdorf, hereditary lord of Vollmershain etc., Electoral Saxon chamber-junker at Torgau, my especially favourable and good friend.</b></p><p>Noble, gallant, honourable, especially favourable and good friend: my willing and assiduous services to the gentleman beforehand, here again, as far as is in my power. And were it to go happily and well with him, it would be wholly dear and agreeable to me to learn it. The gentleman's letter to me, dated Torgau the 3rd of March, I duly received from Doctor Jessenius, through whom I have likewise not failed to address the gentleman in turn in friendly fashion with a letter. Concerning the state of myself and mine here in the Kingdom of Bohemia: the same is, under the most gracious protection of His Imperial Majesty, our most gracious Lord, and God be praised, in good condition — as the gentleman may learn at greater length, with fuller circumstances, from the aforesaid Doctor Jessenius, the good friend of us both. Should I here be able to be of any service to the gentleman at the Imperial court or otherwise, I will at all times be found willing thereto according to my power. Of fresh news I know nothing particular to write; only that it is said the Turk is arming very strongly, and yet more than before. Our most gracious Emperor has not yet returned from Pilsen to Prague; but it is hoped that His Majesty will be there again at the earliest; and the Muscovite envoys, as is supposed, have also been summoned thither to obtain their final dispatch. There is also expectation there of several other envoys of the German princes. This, then, I wished to make known to the gentleman in return upon his letter. And herewith I faithfully commend us all together to Almighty God, into His fatherly protection and shelter.</p><p>Given at the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 8th of May, in the year 1600.<br>[In the gentleman's service]<br>Ever ready to serve</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, at the Imperial castle of Benátky nad Jizerou (Latin <i>Benatecum</i>; protocol §5.7), where he was installed in 1599–1600 as imperial mathematician to Rudolf II before moving to Prague.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Georg von Wolframsdorf, Erbsasse auf Vollmershain, fürstlich-sächsischer Kammerjunker zu Torgau</i> = <b>Georg von Wolframsdorf</b>, hereditary lord of Vollmershain and <b>chamber-junker (Kammerjunker) to the Elector of Saxony</b>, resident at Torgau. He recurs as <i>Cubicularius</i> of the Saxon Elector in the contemporaneous letter to Jessenius (brahe-t_16000507002), where Tycho encloses a letter \"to the noble Lord Georg von Wolframsdorf\" — i.e. this very letter, dispatched through Jessenius.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>H. Doctor Jessenio</i> = <b>Johannes Jessenius a Jessen</b> (Jan Jesenský, 1566–1621), physician and anatomist, professor at Wittenberg, the mutual friend who carried correspondence between Tycho and the Saxon court and who performed the celebrated public anatomy at Prague in June 1600.</li><li><i>Ihr Kays. May. / unser gnädigster Kaiser</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>, Tycho's imperial patron.</li><li><b>Places &amp; events (the \"news\").</b> <i>Pilsen</i> = <b>Plzeň</b>, where Rudolf II's court had withdrawn (in part to escape plague at Prague); his awaited return to Prague is reported as imminent. <i>der Türck … rüstet sich</i> = the <b>Ottoman armament</b> of the Long Turkish War (1593–1606). <i>die Muscowiterischen Gesandten</i> = the <b>Muscovite (Russian) envoys</b> at the Imperial court awaiting their <i>Abfertigung</i> (formal dismissal/dispatch); <i>gesandten der deutschen Fürsten</i> = envoys of the German princes.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> The chancery opening <i>Dem Herrn sein meine willige geflissene Dienste … zuvor</i> (\"my willing assiduous services to the gentleman beforehand\") is the courteous-equal register (Wolframsdorf is addressed in the third person as <i>der Herr</i>, \"the gentleman,\" throughout). The subscription <i>D. H. — Allzeitt Dienstwilliger</i> is read as <i>Des Herrn … allzeit dienstwilliger</i> (\"[the gentleman's] … ever ready to serve\"); the signature proper is not written out in this draft-register text.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 8 Maij Anno 1600</i>; the dataset marks the letter <b>n.s. (New Style)</b>, consistent with Tycho's shift to Gregorian dating in Imperial service. Reproduced as written, not converted. The recipient's letter is dated <i>Torgaw den 3 Martij</i> (Saxony being a Lutheran territory still on the Julian calendar in 1600 — Protestant Germany did not adopt the Gregorian reform until 1700); the two datelines therefore sit on opposite sides of the 10-day gap, but no conversion is performed.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the non-word 'meme' to 'meine' in the opening salutation; Dreyer's print (TBOO VIII p.317) reads 'meine' clearly.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — short courtesy-and-news letter in clean chancery German; sense secure throughout. The only abbreviation requiring interpretation is the subscription <i>D. H.</i>, expanded in the apparatus and left bracketed in the translation rather than silently filled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.316-319 as page images (letter to Wolframsdorf entirely on p.317, ending top of p.318); re-read p.317 at close zoom to confirm 'meine' vs source_text's 'meme'. Aside from this one corrupted word, source_text matches Dreyer verbatim and the full translation (news of the Turk, the Emperor's return from Pilsen, the Muscovite envoys, closing) is faithful throughout. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though it does not change the sense and the translation itself is unaffected.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text prints the non-word 'meme' where Dreyer p.317 clearly prints 'meine' (my); confirmed by a close re-read of the page. The translation itself correctly renders 'my', so it is unaffected by the source_text corruption. Dreyer prints: <i>Dem Herrn sein meine willige geflissene dienste hier wiederumb nach Vermögen zuuor.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.316-319 (full slice, read in one pass); confirmed verbatim on p.317. Single low-severity defect fixed.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports his prosperous condition in Bohemia under imperial protection and offers assistance at the imperial court, directing Wolframsdorf to Jessenius for fuller particulars.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000508001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000511001", "d": "1600-05-11", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Isacius Pontanus", "ck": "Johannes Isacius Pontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Herborn", "t": "Johannes Isacius Pontanus → Tycho Brahe — Herborn [in Nassau], 11 May 1600", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 56 f. (Kladde — draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUSTRUP ET URANIBURCH, AMICO ET DOMINO SUO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO. PRAGAM. AM KEISERLICH MAIESTATS HOFFUE ZU ERFRAGEN.</p><p>S.</p><p>Annus iam agitur, nobiliss. et clariss. d. Tycho, cum ego Rostochij, et tu Wittenbergae ad tempus subsisteres, nec ullam interea occasionem praetermitterem, quin et absentem te, quia praesentem non licuit, per literas adirem, per literas compellarem. Et postmodum quoque, cum ego aliorsum retraherer, et tu Wittenberga commigrares, non destiti tamen de te anquirere ac percunctari, dum in Germaniam tandem ipsam ascenderem et longam post moram intelligerem Pragam te advenisse atque ibidem aut circa eam ex caesarea majestatis insigni liberalitate ac longis votis astronomiae jam magnam partem per te restitutae et porro restituendae magnifice et feliciter operarj. Gaudeo idcirco tuo, gaudeo denique publico nomine; adhuc honos honestis artibus ac studijs durat. Tu modo in hac, quam ad immortalitatem tibi stravisti, via, gnaviter et indefesse, dis hominibusque faventibus, progredere. Nec deerunt, qui amplectantur, qui admirentur, quandiu tu modo dignum aliquid coelo, dignum chartis elaboraveris.</p><p>Nepotes tui sub mea aliquandiu disciplina jam sunt, ultraque semestre Herbornae Nassouiorum studijs incumbunt, quod ex adjuncta quoque epistola pro parte vjdebis. Et utinam aliquando illi patre, te patruo digni reperiantur. Certe quod ipsis inculcem exemplar extra te non quaero. De Dania nihil jamdudum accepimus. Ex patris tamen literis intellexi nuper, valde occupatum fuisse elapsa bruma in instruenda classe regem, et aliquot etiam centenos peritissimos naucleros ab ordinibus Hollandiae petisse atque impetrasse. Quid ille moliatur, aut quid ipsum coquat, tempus dabit. Te autem, nobilissime Tycho, saepe per literas compellare pro amicitja veteri et nepotum necessitudine debere nos fateor, et optem, ut de ratione mittendi ad te literas certiores redderemur. Comperires profecto officium nostrum neutiquam claudicaturum. Qui has perferre spopondit, aurifaber et pro parte chymicus est. Eo ego familiariter hic usus sum, quia Piscator aliquatenus affinis est. Et spero officium eius in hisce curandis futurum certius, quam ars fere solet illa, quam tractat.</p><p>Hisce valebis, clariss. Tycho, officiose salutatus ab hospite nostro, quem dixi, v. cl. Iohanne Piscatore.<br>Datum Herbornae Nassouiorum xi maj CIↃ IↃ C. Tui studiosiss.<br>Iohannes Jsacius.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most noble man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, his much-to-be-revered friend and lord. To Prague. To be inquired after at the Imperial Majesty's court.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>A year is now passing, most noble and renowned Lord Tycho, since I was staying for a time at Rostock, and you at Wittenberg, and I let no occasion pass meanwhile but that I approached you, absent as you were (since to do so in person was not allowed), by letters, and addressed you by letters. And afterward too, when I was drawn off elsewhere, and you removed from Wittenberg, I yet did not cease to inquire and ask after you, until at length I came up into Germany itself, and after a long delay learned that you had arrived at Prague, and there, or near it, by the imperial majesty's distinguished liberality — and to the long-prayed-for [good] of astronomy, already in great part restored by you and further to be restored — were labouring magnificently and happily. I rejoice therefore on your account; I rejoice, in the end, on the public account: honour still endures for honourable arts and studies. Only do you, upon this road which you have paved for yourself toward immortality, press on, diligently and indefatigably, with gods and men favouring. Nor will there be wanting those who embrace, who admire — so long only as you go on elaborating something worthy of heaven, worthy of the written page.</p><p>Your nephews are now for some while under my tutelage, and for more than half a year apply themselves to their studies at Herborn in Nassau — as you will also see, in part, from the enclosed letter. And would that they may someday be found worthy of their father, and of you their uncle. Certainly, for a model to instil in them, I seek none beyond yourself. From Denmark we have received nothing for a long time. Yet from their father's letter I learned recently that the King was much occupied last winter in fitting out a fleet, and that he sought and obtained also some hundreds of most skilled skippers from the Estates of Holland. What he is contriving, or what is brewing for him, time will tell. But that we ought often to address you, most noble Tycho, by letter — for the sake of our old friendship and the tie of your nephews — I confess; and I could wish that we be made more certain of the means of sending letters to you. You would surely find our service in no way halting. He who has undertaken to carry these is a goldsmith, and in part a chymist. I have made familiar use of him here, because he is somewhat a kinsman of Piscator; and I hope his service in seeing to these [letters] will prove more reliable than that art he practises is wont to be.</p><p>With these you will fare well, renowned Tycho, dutifully greeted by our host, of whom I spoke, the very renowned Johann Piscator.<br>Given at Herborn in Nassau, 11 May 1600. Most devoted to you,<br>Johannes Isacius [Pontanus].</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Johannes Isacius Pontanus</b> (Jan Isaksz. Pontanus, 1571–1639), the Danish-Dutch historian, geographer and humanist (later royal historiographer of Denmark), then teaching at <b>Herborn</b>, where he was <b>tutor to Tycho's nephews</b>. The address-line gives the routing in German: <i>am keiserlich Maiestäts Hofe zu erfragen</i> — \"to be inquired after at the Imperial court\" (Tycho's whereabouts being uncertain to correspondents).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, at or near Prague, \"labouring magnificently\" in the imperial service; Pontanus salutes him as the <b>restorer of astronomy</b> (<i>astronomia … magna parte per te restituta et porro restituenda</i>) on the \"road paved toward immortality\" — the <i>restitutio/instauratio</i> programme-language again (§5.1).</li><li><b>The nephews &amp; the Herborn colony.</b> <i>nepotes tui … sub mea disciplina</i> = Tycho's <b>nephews</b> (sons of his brother <b>Steen Brahe</b>), under Pontanus's instruction more than half a year at Herborn — the same student group whose member <b>Erik Brahe</b> writes the <i>epistola adiuncta</i> (\"the enclosed letter\") referenced here: brahe-t_16000512001, dated the very next day (12 May 1600). The two letters travelled together.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Iohannes Piscator</i> = <b>Johannes Piscator</b> (1546–1625), the eminent Reformed theologian of the Herborn academy, Pontanus's and the students' <b>host</b> (<i>hospes noster</i>).</li><li><i>Rex</i> = <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>, reported (via the boys' father's letter) to have fitted out a <b>fleet</b> the previous winter and hired \"some hundreds\" of skilled <b>Dutch pilots/skippers</b> (<i>naucleri</i>) from the Estates of Holland.</li><li>the unnamed <b>goldsmith-and-chymist courier</b>, a kinsman of Piscator, carrying this letter — Pontanus's wry hope that his reliability exceed that of his alchemical \"art.\"</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Rostochium</i> = Rostock; <i>Wittenberga</i> = Wittenberg (Tycho's 1598–99 way-station); <i>Herborna Nassoviorum</i> = <b>Herborn in Nassau</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i>; <i>Tui studiosiss[imus]</i> → \"Most devoted to you.\" The signature <i>Iohannes Isacius</i> is Pontanus's patronymic form (Isaac's son).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>xi maj CIↃ IↃ C</i> — \"11 May <b>1600</b>\" (the Roman <i>apostrophus</i> numeral CIↃIↃC = 1600). No style marker; reproduced as written.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean humanist Latin; the connection to the Herborn student-colony (Pontanus as tutor, the enclosed companion letter from Erik Brahe, Piscator as host) is secure and cross-corroborated by brahe-t_16000512001, and the Danish naval news is coherent. The courier is anonymous in the source. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.203-205 as page images. The Pontanus letter begins with the argument header on p.203 ('Johannes Isacius Tychonem Brahe salutat...') and the body runs the whole of p.204. Collated the Rostock/Wittenberg timeline, the nephews-at-Herborn passage, the Danish-fleet news from the father's letter ('some hundreds of most skilled skippers'), the goldsmith-courier/Piscator passage, and the closing date (xi maj CIↃ IↃ C = 11 May 1600) word-for-word. source_text matches Dreyer exactly; published_english is accurate throughout, including the correct Roman-numeral date resolution.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Pol. 56 f.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports long-frustrated attempts to reach Tycho by letter from Rostock and later Germany, and expresses joy on learning of his arrival in Prague under imperial patronage.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000511001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000512001", "d": "1600-05-12", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Erik Brahe", "ck": "Erik Brahe", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Herborn", "t": "Erik Brahe (Stensen) → Tycho Brahe — Herborn [in Nassau], 12 May 1600", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. XIV,<i> Epistolae et Acta</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 58 f. (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS ET DOCTRINAe EXCELLENTIA PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONJ BRAHAe, D:O DE KNUDSTRUP, PATRUO SUO CHARISS:</p><p>SI prospera fueris valetudine, patrue charissime, est quod gaudeam. Ego adhuc, Dej beneficio, bene valeo. Immortales tibj habeo gratias pro omnibus in me collatis beneficiis, inter quae vel maximum reputandum, quod et parens charissimus pro sua prudentia et tua autoritate non minimum confirmatus studiis literarum nos instituere nec ullis impensis ad tam honestum propositum perficiendum hactenus parcendum censuerit. Quare si pro corporis et fortunae bonis ad grates rependendas ipsa natura ducimur, quid ijs debebimus, qui vel opera sua mentes nostras (quae, quantum caelum terrae, tantum corporibus praestent) humanitatis et doctrinae donis imbuunt, vel imbuendas pro prudentia ac summo judicio suo effecere? Itaque quod officii mej erit, gratum me semper tibj praebebo, et mihi gratulabor, cuj talis contigit patruus, qui virtute, doctrina summorumque operum excellentia non Daniae aut Germaniae modo sed Europae fere universae venerabilis et charus est. Hanc animi mej significationem jam dudum est quod tibj, charissime patrue, debebam, sed cum de mansione tua nihil nobis certj constaret, hactenus differre coactus fuj. Nos vero jam ultra semestre Herbornae vivimus, et ad festum Michaelis, ni fallor, sumus mansurj, nec quicquam adhuc ex Dania certj intelleximus. Nam quotidie tabellarium Aeschildum expectamus. Fratrem autem meum Otthonem cum Aslachio jam domi esse censeam; cum enim hac commigraremus, in Anglia sive Scotia commorarj acceperamus. Relatum quoque nobis, Tagonem Tot Heidelberge litteris operam navare et alios quosdam nobiles Danos, quos jam credo Genevam excurrisse. Ex Dania rumor hic fuit, Aeschilldum Sefeldium post celebratas filiae nuptias subjto vita defunctum, et Fridericum Rosenkrantium caussa cecidisse et infamem pronuntiatum. Sed te diutius fortasse qvam par est tali garritu detineo. Jtaque te his, patrue charissime, feliciter Deo optimo maximo commendatum habeo, et amanter peto, ut me cum fratre meo Georgio et patruelj Falchone, qui hic quoque una mecum literis incumbunt, diligere porro atque consilio tuo dirigere non dedigneris.</p><p>Datum Herbornae Nassoviorum 12 maj anno 1600. T. T.<br>Ericus Brahae Stennonides<br>m. pp.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most outstanding in nobility of birth and excellence of learning, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, his dearest uncle.</b></p><p>If you are in prosperous health, dearest uncle, I have cause for joy. I myself, by God's goodness, am still well. I owe you undying thanks for all the benefits conferred upon me — among which even the greatest is to be reckoned this: that my dearest father, confirmed in no small measure by his own prudence and by your authority, has resolved to school us in the study of letters, and has judged that no expense should be spared, thus far, to accomplish so honourable a purpose. Wherefore, if we are led by nature herself to repay thanks for the goods of body and of fortune, what shall we owe to those who either by their own labour imbue our minds — which excel bodies as far as heaven excels earth — with the gifts of humanity and learning, or, by their prudence and highest judgement, brought it about that they should be so imbued? Therefore, in whatever shall be my duty, I will always show myself grateful to you, and will count myself fortunate, to whom such an uncle has fallen — one who, by virtue, learning, and the excellence of his supreme works, is venerable and dear not to Denmark or Germany only, but to nearly all Europe. This declaration of my heart is something I have long owed you, dearest uncle; but since nothing certain was established to us about your dwelling-place, I was hitherto compelled to defer it. We have now been living more than half a year at Herborn, and shall stay, unless I am mistaken, until the feast of Michael; nor have we yet learned anything certain from Denmark. For we await the courier Eskild daily. My brother Otto, however, I should reckon to be now at home with Aslaksen — for when we migrated hither, we had heard they were staying in England or Scotland. It has also been reported to us that Tage Thott is plying his studies at Heidelberg, and certain other noble Danes, who, I now believe, have gone off to Geneva. From Denmark there was this rumour: that Eskild Seefeld, after celebrating his daughter's wedding, suddenly departed this life; and that Frederik Rosenkrantz had lost his case and been pronounced infamous. But perhaps I detain you longer than is fitting with such chatter. And so with these [words], dearest uncle, I hold you happily commended to God, best and greatest; and I lovingly ask that you not disdain to go on cherishing me, together with my brother Georg and my cousin Falk — who here too apply themselves to letters along with me — and to direct me by your counsel.</p><p>Given at Herborn in Nassau, the 12th of May, in the year 1600. Wholly yours,<br>Erik Brahe, son of Steen,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> family letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Erik Brahe Stensen (<i>Stennonides</i>, \"son of Steen\")</b> — a <b>nephew of Tycho</b>, son of Tycho's brother <b>Steen Brahe</b> — writing as a young student abroad. Warmly dutiful in tone, it credits Tycho's \"authority\" with having helped persuade his father to fund the boys' education.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, <i>Dominus de Knudstrup</i>, addressed as <i>patruus</i> (<b>paternal uncle</b>) — the precise kin-term confirming Erik as a brother's son.</li><li><b>The student colony at Herborn.</b> Erik and his companions have been <b>more than half a year at Herborn</b> (the Calvinist <i>Hohe Schule</i> in Nassau, a noted centre of Reformed learning), intending to stay until <b>Michaelmas (29 September)</b>. With him are <i>frater meus Georgius</i> = his <b>brother Jørgen (Georg) Brahe</b> and <i>patruelis Falcho</i> = his <b>cousin Falk Brahe</b>, all studying together. They await news from Denmark via <i>tabellarius Aeschildus</i> = the <b>courier Eskild</b>.</li><li><b>Other Danes abroad (the \"chatter\").</b></li><li><i>frater meus Otto … cum Aslachio</i> = Erik's brother <b>Otte Brahe</b>, travelling with <b>Cort Aslaksen (Aslachius)</b> (1564–1624), the Danish theologian and natural philosopher — last heard of in <b>England or Scotland</b>, now presumed home.</li><li><i>Tago Tot</i> = <b>Tage Thott</b>, a Danish nobleman studying at <b>Heidelberg</b>; other noble Danes are said to have gone on to <b>Geneva</b>.</li><li><b>News from Denmark (datable rumours).</b></li><li><i>Aeschildus Sefeldius … subito vita defunctum</i> = <b>Eskild Seefeld (Skeel?)</b>, reported to have <b>died suddenly just after his daughter's wedding</b>.</li><li><i>Fridericus Rosenkrantius caussa cecidisse et infamem pronuntiatum</i> = <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> (1571–1602), reported to have <b>lost his case and been declared infamous</b> — the legal aftermath of the 1599 court scandal (the affair with Rigborg Brockenhuus) that cost him honour and estates. (The same Frederik Rosenkrantz whose later, \"happier breeze\" Winshemius reports in brahe-t_16000813001 — the two letters bracket his disgrace and partial rehabilitation.)</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Herborna Nassoviorum</i> = <b>Herborn in Nassau</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> The health-formula opening (<i>Si … vales, … est quod gaudeam; ego … valeo</i>, the classical <i>si vales bene est</i> topos); <i>T. T.</i> = <i>Totus Tuus</i> (\"Wholly yours\"); <i>m. pp.</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand\"). <i>Stennonides</i> is a patronymic (\"son of Steen\").</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>12 maj anno 1600</i> at Herborn; no style marker. Reproduced as written, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is clean and the family/student matter is secure: Erik as Steen Brahe's son, the Herborn colony with brother Jørgen and cousin Falk, and the two datable Danish rumours (Seefeld's death; Frederik Rosenkrantz's condemnation, externally corroborated). Graded B rather than A because several of the named Danes are individuated only by period forms admitting more than one identification — in particular <i>Aeschildus Sefeldius</i> (the surname <i>Sefeld/Skeel</i> is uncertain) and the exact identity of <i>frater Otto</i> among Steen Brahe's sons — none of which affects the letter's sense.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 slice pages visually. The letter (Erik Brahe to Tycho, headed 'NOBILITATE GENERIS ET DOCTRINAE EXCELLENTIA PRAESTANTISSIMO VIRO...') runs from the foot of p.204 through p.205, citation 'Ex originali in Bibliotheca Universitatis Basileensis: Autographensammlung Vol.23, Fol.58 f.' Collated the full Latin word-for-word against Dreyer: identical throughout. Checked every name/date: Aslachio (Aslaksen), Tagonem Tot (Tage Thott), Aeschildum/Aeschilldum Sefeldium (Eskild Seefeld), Fridericum Rosenkrantium, brother Georgio and cousin Falchone, 'ultra semestre Herbornae' (more than half a year), 'Datum Herbornae Nassoviorum 12 maj anno 1600'. English translation faithful throughout, no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found. Self-graded B; our collation warrants A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, autographsammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 58 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Tycho for benefits received, especially for influencing his father's decision to invest in serious study, and pledges lasting gratitude and diligence in return.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000512001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000515001", "d": "1600-05-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Unknown / anonymous", "ck": "Anonymus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an anonymous imperial patron] — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 15 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMUM.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR, DOMINE OBSERVANDISSIME.</p><p>DE ijs, quae nuper ad Illustrem Dominationem Vestram per meum, quem isthuc ablegavi, domesticum perscripsi et referri volui, nunc pluribus non agam, ne eadem repetendo ei nimium molestus videar, sperans nihilominus, Illustrem Domem Vestram desiderijs meis lubenti animo annuituram, atque ut res optatum finem sortiatur, effecturam. Verum aliud quid incidit, de quo nunc Dominationem Vestram reverenter et obnixe rogandam duxi. Venit hisce diebus huc e Dania quidam Generosi Stemmatis Iuvenis, e splendidissima et antiquissima ROSENKRANTZIORUM familia isthic oriundus, FRIDERICUS nomine, qui Regis per sexennium Cubicularius fuit. Is militiam aliquandiu sequi et in ea se exercere in animum induxit, et primum Serenissimo Principi, Archiduci Austriae, Domino MATTHIAe, Sacrae Caesareae Maiestatis, Domini nostri clementissimi, fratri, aliquantisper in aula ipsius Celsitudinis humilime inservire cupit, ut paulatim addiscendo mores et consuetudinem earum regionum in bello Ungarico postea inter equitatus exercitum locum oportunum pro se et sibi inservientibus aliquot equitibus obtinere possit. Quare cum dictus ROSENKRANTZIUS mihi non saltem consanguinitatis iure iunctissimus existat, sed et ab intimis in patria nostra eius de caussa per literas rogatus sim, ut, quantum in me est, conatus ipsius promovere laborem, idcirco ad Illustrem Generositatem Vestram confugio atque ab ea quam officiosissime peto, ut illi literas ad Serenissimum Austriae Archiducem, Dominum MATTHIAM, intercessorias procurare non degravari velit, quo eo facilius et promptius in eius Celsitudinis aulicum servitium recipi et postea in Ungaria contra Turcas militare et suam fortunam experiri queat, sicque virtutis bellicae laude, ad quam strenue aspirat (utut aliâs non saltem genere clarus sit, sed et in literis bene versatus, simulque Latinae, Italicae, Gallicae et Hispanicae linguae, praeter Teutonicam et vernaculam, gnarus) ornatior ad suos aliquando redeat, et Patriae eo magis in posterum usui esse possit. Sed quia de Illustris Domis Vae erga me singulari benevolentia, quam aliâs satis perspectam habeo, nihil addubitem, eam etiam hac in parte meae expectationi non defuturam confido. Ideoque illam aliâs occupatissimam ulterius remorari desino. Deus Opt. Max. Illustrem Domem Vestram quam diutissime sospitem conservet.</p><p>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benaticka die 15 Maij Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to an anonymous [correspondent].</b></p><p>Illustrious and generous man, most-to-be-revered lord.</p><p>Of those matters which I lately wrote out in full to Your Illustrious Lordship through my servant, whom I sent thither, and wished to be reported, I will not now treat at more length, lest by repeating the same I seem too troublesome to you — hoping nonetheless that Your Illustrious Lordship will assent to my desires with willing mind, and will bring it about that the matter reach its wished-for end. But another thing has come up, about which I have now thought to ask Your Lordship, reverently and earnestly. There came hither these days, from Denmark, a certain youth of generous lineage, sprung there from the most splendid and most ancient family of the Rosenkrantzes — Frederik by name — who was for six years Chamberlain to the King. He has resolved to follow soldiering for a time and to train himself in it, and first desires most humbly to serve a while in the court of His Highness the Most Serene Prince, Archduke of Austria, Lord Matthias, brother of His Sacred Imperial Majesty our most clement Lord; so that, gradually learning the manners and usage of those regions, he may afterward, in the Hungarian war, obtain a suitable place among the cavalry, for himself and for several horsemen serving under him. Wherefore, since the said Rosenkrantz is most closely bound to me not only by right of blood-kinship, but I have also been asked by letters, by [my] intimates in our homeland, on his account, to labour — so far as in me lies — to advance his endeavours: I therefore take refuge with Your Illustrious Generosity, and ask of it most dutifully, that you would not find it a burden to procure for him letters of intercession to the Most Serene Archduke of Austria, Lord Matthias, whereby the more easily and readily he may be received into His Highness's court service, and afterward soldier in Hungary against the Turks and try his fortune; and so, with the praise of martial valour to which he strenuously aspires (though otherwise he is not only illustrious by birth, but also well versed in letters, and at the same time skilled in the Latin, Italian, French and Spanish tongues, besides German and his vernacular), he may someday return the more adorned to his own people, and the more be of use to the Fatherland hereafter. But since I doubt nothing of Your Illustrious Lordship's singular benevolence toward me, which I have otherwise sufficiently perceived, I trust that it will not fail my expectation in this part either. And so I cease to detain you, otherwise most busy, any further. May God, best and greatest, preserve Your Illustrious Lordship in safety as long as may be.</p><p>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 15th day of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky</b> (protocol §5.7).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Anonymous</b> in the source (<i>ad anonymum</i>): an <b>illustrious imperial patron</b> with access to the court of Archduke Matthias — possibly the same well-placed official Tycho addresses in brahe-t_16000306002 (also <i>ad anonymum</i>, also re a recommendation). Identity not recoverable from the text.</li><li><b>Purpose &amp; connective tissue.</b> A letter of <b>intercession for Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> — and a striking node in the dataset. <i>Fridericus … Regis per sexennium Cubicularius</i> = <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> (1571–1602), formerly <b>Chamberlain to King Christian IV</b> of Denmark for six years. Disgraced in the 1599 court scandal (the affair with Rigborg Brockenhuus) and stripped of honour — the very man whose condemnation (\"<i>caussa cecidisse et infamem pronuntiatum</i>\") Tycho's nephew reports in brahe-t_16000512001, and whose later \"happier breeze\" Winshemius notes in brahe-t_16000813001. Here, in May 1600, his kinsman Tycho is actively engineering his rehabilitation by soldiering: a place at the court of <b>Archduke Matthias of Austria</b> (Rudolf II's brother, the future Emperor Matthias), as a step toward a cavalry command in the <b>Hungarian war against the Turks</b> (the Long Turkish War). Rosenkrantz did serve, and died in that war in 1602 — so this letter documents the turning-point. Tycho notes Rosenkrantz is <i>consanguinitatis iure iunctissimus</i> — closely bound to him by blood — and that \"intimates in our homeland\" had written asking Tycho to help.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Fridericus Rosenkrantzius</i> — see above; praised as learned and fluent in <b>Latin, Italian, French, Spanish, German and his Danish vernacular</b>.</li><li><i>Serenissimus Archidux Austriae Dominus Matthias</i> = <b>Archduke Matthias</b> (1557–1619), brother of Rudolf II, governor in the Habsburg lands and a commander in the Hungarian war; later Emperor Matthias (1612).</li><li><i>Rex</i> = <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b>, whose chamberlain Frederik had been.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arx Caesarea Benaticka</i> = Benátky.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary) → \"Given\"; <i>Deus Opt. Max. … sospitem conservet</i> → the closing benediction.</li><li><b>Calendar — RESOLVED (see §1 note below).</b> The dateline day is <b>15 May 1600</b> (N.S., consistent with the Benátky series). Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII, p. 319; running-head 5/15 Mai.) prints <i>die 15 Maij</i>, so the earlier \"blank/lacuna\" reading was a defect of the working transcription, now corrected in header, Source, and translation.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> the dateline day is <b>not</b> a lacuna — Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII, p. 319; running-head 5/15 Mai.) prints <i>die 15 Maij</i>. Restored to header, Source, and translation; the lacuna flag is withdrawn.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is clean and the substance secure and historically valuable (Tycho's intercession to place the disgraced Frederik Rosenkrantz with Archduke Matthias for the Hungarian war, cross-linked to two other letters in the corpus). Graded B, not A, on two flagged points: the <b>day-number of the date is missing in the source</b> (only the month survives; \"15 May\" is the catalogue's, not the dateline's), and the <b>patron-recipient is anonymous</b> in the source itself.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages visually. The letter ('Tycho Brahe ad Anonymum', headed 'Illustris et generose vir...') is entirely on p.319, dated 15 Maij 1600, citation '[E codice Basileensi G I 35]'. Collated the full Latin word-for-word against Dreyer: identical throughout. Checked every name/number: ROSENKRANTZIORUM/Frideiricus (Frederik Rosenkrantz), 'per sexennium' (six years as Chamberlain), Archduke Matthias correctly identified as brother of the reigning Emperor, the four-language list (Latinae, Italicae, Gallicae, Hispanicae + Teutonicam + vernaculam), and the date/place 'Benaticka die 15 Maij Anno 1600'. English translation faithful throughout, no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found. Self-graded B; our collation warrants A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Unknown / anonymous", "count": 6, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16000329002", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Asks an anonymous imperial patron to help a young Danish nobleman of the Rosenkrantz family obtain a military position at the court of Archduke Matthias.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000515001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000515002", "d": "1600-05-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Gottfried von Berbistorf", "ck": "Gottfried von Berbistorf", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Gottfried von Berbisdorf — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 15 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 126 f. (Kladde — draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM WOLGEBORNEN VNND EDLEN HERRN GOTTFFRYD VON BERBISTORFF, AUFF GRUSCHOW VND STRUCK, RÖMISCH. KEY. MAY. RAADT, MEINEM GUNSTIGEN LIEBEN HERRN, FREUND VND NACHPAREN</p><p>MEIN willige dienst mit wunschunge alles gudts beuor. Wollgeborner vnd edler herr, herr Gottfryd von Berbistorff, gunstiger herr vnd freundt. Des herrn schreiben ist mir heute durch dero abgefertigten behendigt, worin ich des herrn freundtlichen guten willen kegen mir erspure, in deme das err mich in diesen nechstkunftigen pfingstagen, nebenst andern seinen freunden zu sich so freundtlich geladen, wor fur ich auch dem herrn, das er mich alss ein frembden vnd vnbekanten dieser örter solche freundtschafft selbstwillig erzeiget, gar dienstlich bedanck, vnd wolte gerne bey dem herrn auf angedeuteten tagk mich einstellen, wen nicht zur selbigen zeit ich anderswo mich hette versprochen. Den wie ich fur 14 tage zu Prage auf des herrn haubtmans von Brandeiss hochzeit gewesen, hab ich dem wolgebornen herren Carll von Wartenberch mussen angeloben, das ich in nebenst dem wollg. herrn Ehrenfridt von Minckwitz kunftigen pfingstage wollte besuchen, vnd ist genanter herr von Wartenbergk seidthero selber alhie bey mir gewesen vnd weiteren beschlus darauf haben wollen, in welchen ich auch consentiert. Es hat auch der obgedachte herr Minkwitz gistern von Prag mir zugeschrieben, das er eigentlich beschlossen, auf zukunftigen donnerstagk oder freitagk aufs lengste alhie (ob Godt will) bey mir zu sein, das wir vns folgendes miteinander konten zu dem herrn von Wartenbergk, wie zugesagt, begeben. Nach dieser gelegenheit, versihe ich, werdt der herr mich entschuldigt haben, das ich auf dissmall, wie ich gerne wolte, nicht kan zu ime kommen. Was aber dissmall, wegen gemelter vhrsachen, nich gescheen kan, gelobe ich dieweill auf einander maell mit Gottes heulffe wiedereinzubringen, vnd will gerne dem herrn, besunders weil wir so nahent nachbar, nach sein wille vnd gelegenheit besuchen; verhoffe, das der herr in demselben widervmb vnbeschwert sich wirt finden lassen. Bitte auch hieneben gantz dienstlich, er wolle alles zum besten ausdeuten vnd aufnemen. Thue hiemit vns beiderseits in gnedigen schutz des Allmechtigen treulich befehlen.</p><p>Datirt auf dem keiserlichen hause Bennatick den 15. maj: a:o 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the well-born and noble Lord Gottfried von Berbisdorf, [lord] of Gruschow and Struck, councillor of the Roman Imperial Majesty, my favourable dear lord, friend and neighbour.</b></p><p>My willing service, with the wish of all good, beforehand. Well-born and noble lord, Lord Gottfried von Berbisdorf, favourable lord and friend: the gentleman's letter was handed to me today through his dispatched [messenger], wherein I perceive the gentleman's friendly good will toward me, in that he has so kindly invited me, together with his other friends, to him on these coming Whitsun days; for which I also very serviceably thank the gentleman — that he, to me, a stranger and unknown in these parts, has of his own accord shown such friendship — and I would gladly present myself at the gentleman's on the day indicated, were I not at that same time engaged elsewhere. For, as I was a fortnight ago at Prague at the wedding of the lord captain of Brandeis, I had to pledge to the well-born Lord Carl von Wartenberg that I would visit him, together with the well-born Lord Ehrenfried von Minckwitz, next Whitsun; and the said Lord von Wartenberg has since been here with me himself and wished a further arrangement upon it, to which I also consented. The aforesaid Lord Minckwitz, too, wrote to me yesterday from Prague that he has firmly resolved to be here with me (God willing) next Thursday, or Friday at the latest, so that we might thereafter betake ourselves together to the Lord von Wartenberg, as promised. In view of this circumstance, I trust the gentleman will have excused me, that this time, much as I would wish it, I cannot come to him. But what this time, for the reasons stated, cannot take place, I promise meanwhile to make good on another occasion, with God's help; and I will gladly visit the gentleman — especially since we are such near neighbours — according to his will and convenience; and I hope the gentleman will in the same matter again show himself unburdened [i.e. agreeable]. I also ask herewith, quite serviceably, that he be pleased to construe and take everything for the best. Herewith faithfully commending us both to the gracious protection of the Almighty.</p><p>Dated at the imperial house of Benátky, the 15th of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at the imperial house of <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (protocol §5.7); a social note <b>declining a Whitsun invitation</b> on account of a prior engagement — a glimpse of Tycho's integration into the Bohemian nobility around Benátky.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Gottfried von Berbisdorf, auf Gruschow und Struck, Röm. Kais. Maj. Rat</i> = <b>Gottfried von Berbisdorf</b>, hereditary lord of Gruschow and Struck and a <b>councillor of the Imperial Majesty (Rudolf II)</b>, addressed as Tycho's <i>Herr, Freund und Nachbar</i> (\"lord, friend and neighbour\"). Tycho calls himself, with courteous self-deprecation, <i>ein Fremder und Unbekannter dieser Örter</i> — \"a stranger and unknown in these parts.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>der Hauptmann von Brandeis</i> = the <b>captain (governor) of Brandýs nad Labem</b> (Brandeis), at whose Prague wedding (a fortnight earlier) Tycho had made the prior engagement.</li><li><i>Carl von Wartenberg</i> = <b>Karl von Wartenberg</b>, a Bohemian nobleman whom Tycho is bound to visit at Whitsun.</li><li><i>Ehrenfried von Minckwitz</i> = <b>Ehrenfried von Minckwitz</b>, who is to come to Benátky first (\"Thursday, or Friday at the latest\") so that he and Tycho may go on together to Wartenberg.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>das kaiserliche Haus Bennatick</i> = the imperial house of <b>Benátky</b>; <i>Prag</i> = Prague; <i>Brandeis</i> = <b>Brandýs nad Labem</b>.</li><li><b>Register &amp; formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Noble-courteous chancery German, the recipient addressed in the third person as <i>der Herr</i> (\"the gentleman\") throughout. Opening <i>mein williger Dienst mit Wünschung alles Guten zuvor</i> → \"my willing service, with the wish of all good, beforehand\"; <i>datirt</i> → \"dated.\" <i>nechstkünftige Pfingsttage</i> = the coming days of <b>Whitsun / Pentecost</b> (the fixed point around which the social engagements turn).</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> The Basel manuscript is a <i>Kladde</i> — a <b>retained draft / office copy</b>; the subscription is accordingly not written out.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 15. Maij a:o 1600</i>; the dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b>, consistent with Tycho's Imperial-service dating. The movable feast <i>Pfingsten</i> (Whitsun) is to be read on the same New-Style reckoning. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a clear social letter in noble chancery German; the persons (Berbisdorf, the Brandeis captain, Wartenberg, Minckwitz) and the scheduling are explicit and internally consistent. No textual crux; the Bohemian noble names are given as written.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 slice pages (TBOO XIV pp. 202-204) visually as page images. The letter to Gottfried von Berbisdorff (Dreyer's letter no. 196, dated 1600, 5./15. maii) is printed in full on p. 203 (heading through 'Datirt auf dem keiserlichen hause Bennatick den 15. maj: a:o 1600.'), preceded by Dreyer's Latin regest confirming sender, recipient, and occasion (declining a Pentecost visit because of a prior promise to Carl von Wartenberg and Ehrenfried von Minckwitz). Note: p. 202 also carries the tail of a distinct, earlier letter to Ehrenfridt von Minckwitz (dated 7 May 1600) and p. 204 carries the start of two unrelated later letters (nos. 197-198) -- neither belongs to this letter and neither was drawn on for source_text or published_english. Collated the German source_text against Dreyer's printed German clause by clause (opening formula, body, closing, date/place) and found it a faithful modernized-s transcription with no substantive departure (only ordinary long-s/ligature spelling variance, e.g. Dreyer's 'Brandeiff'/'muffen'/'ift' vs source's 'Brandeiss'/'mussen'/'ist', which is orthographic convention, not a text error). Then checked published_english against that German sentence by sentence, including every proper name (Gottfried von Berbisdorff, Gruschow, Struck, Carl von Wartenberg, Ehrenfried von Minckwitz), place name (Prague, Benátky), number/date (a fortnight = 14 tage; next Thursday or Friday at the latest = donnerstagk oder freitagk aufs lengste; 15 May 1600), and the opening and closing lines. No fabricated, omitted, or mistranslated clauses found; no hedges hardened. The self-assigned grade A is, in this instance, borne out by the collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 126 f. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Declines a Whitsun invitation from Berbisdorf, explaining a prior commitment to visit Carl von Wartenberg and Ehrenfried von Minckwitz during the same period.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000515002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000516001", "d": "1600-05-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Monavius", "ck": "Jacob Monavius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Monau (Monavius) — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 16 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IACOBO MONAVIO, PATRITIO VRATISLAVIENSI, ILLUSTRIS DUCIS SILESIAe CONSILIARIO DIGNISSIMO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>QUAS circa Martij medietatem ad me dedisti literas, Excellentiss. Domine MONAVI, amice inprimis honorande, eas nuper accepi, cum autem non suppeteret certus tabellarius, responsionem hucusque distuli, quam etiam nunc accurtare cogor, siquidem plurimis alijs distrahar negotijs, nec tamen per internuntium hunc sponte sese offerentem occasionem datam negligere lubuit. De Typographo itaque vestro sic habe: Videtur is plus iusto poscere, cum Witebergae vel Lipsiae longe minori pretio mea excudi possint, ubi etiam id effici curabo, si propria mea typographia huc satis mature non advenerit.</p><p>Domino Doctori FISCHERO brevibus, prout haec otij penuria exigit, rescripsi, ut gratum ipsius in me studium mihi esse cognoscat. Si literas illas SCULTETUS noster ad eum non direxerit, sed ad te pervenerint, ut illi per oportunitatem mittantur, provideas quaeso.</p><p>Libros Witichianos me iam facilius obtenturum existimo, quam dum tenax et difficilis eius soror vixit. Non tamen omnes cuperem, sed solummodo eos, quos in apposita scheda ex relatione veteris mei domestici, M. CHRISTIERNI LONGOMONTANI, tibi noti, qui mecum nunc est, denotavi. Si tamen separatim inde nihil relicti pupilli tutores divendere voluerint, me de iusto omnium precio certiorem redde, aut, si fieri possit, solummodo eorum, quos consignavi. Sin vero omnino renuerint inde quippiam distrahere, da saltem operam, ut tua interposita fide eos, quos maxime vellem, per pauculas septimanas mutuo obtineam; restituam ad praefinitum tempus integre et inviolate.</p><p>Ad Dominum DANIELEM BUCRETIUM, quem memini me semel atque iterum olim in Insula nostra Uraniburgica invisisse, literas etiam dedi, de observationibus, si quae supersunt, ab haeredibus illius, qui aliquando apud vos fuisse dicitur, Astronomi impetrandis, quas velim illi tradi, et responsum mature urgeri.</p><p>Cum ante paucos dies hîc mecum fuisset Generosus Baro CAROLUS de WARTEBERG, catalogum librorum totius Bibliothecae optimae recordationis Domini ANDREAe DUDITHIJ mihi inspiciendum dedit, qui etiamnum penes me est, illi restituendus, cum instantibus diebus festis eum, prout ab eo rogatus promisi, in sua arce 7 miliaribus hinc distante D. V. invisero. Optarem itaque, ut interea ab haeredibus, sive per te ipsum, sive alium aliquem, expiscari velis, an aliquos ex ijs, qui mihi maxime arriserunt, videndi copia concedi possit, et an separatim nonnullos venundare velint; vix enim emptorem pro universa Bibliotheca invenient. Si id, quod desidero, fieri posse, a te intellexero, mittam designationem eorum, quos potissimum expeterem, quod et nunc fecissem, nisi otij penuria omnium plenariam perlustrationem impedivisset.</p><p>Caesar necdum Pilsna Pragam reversus est, neque certo constat, quam cito id fiet; speramus tamen statim a Pentecostes festo ipsius Maiestatem adventuram. Aderunt etiam tunc aliquot, uti aiunt, Germaniae Principum Legati. De rebus Ungaricis nihil nobis hîc innotuit, quod non pariter ad vos relatum existimem.</p><p>Anglici et Danici Legati nunc Embdae conveniunt, res magni momenti invicem tractaturi, de quibus si inter eos non convenerit, periculum est, ne dissensio illa bellum pariat, quod tamen Deus avertat, cui te tuaque omnia commendo, in quo etiam sanus vale et vive cum omnibus.</p><p>Dabantur Benaticae 16 Maij A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most excellent man, Lord Jacob Monau, patrician of Breslau, most worthy Councillor of the Illustrious Duke of Silesia, his friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>The letter you sent me about the middle of March, most excellent Lord Monau, friend especially to be honoured, I received recently; but since no sure courier was at hand, I have deferred my reply until now — which I am also now compelled to shorten, being distracted by very many other affairs, yet I did not wish to neglect the opportunity given by this messenger offering himself unbidden. Concerning your <b>Printer</b>, then, take it thus: he seems to demand more than is fair, since my [works] can be printed at <b>Wittenberg or Leipzig</b> at a far lower price — where I will also have it done, if my own printing-press does not arrive here early enough.</p><p>To Lord Doctor <b>Fischer</b> I have replied briefly, as this scarcity of leisure demands, that he may know his devotion toward me is welcome to me. If our <b>Scultetus</b> has not directed those letters to him, but they have come to you, please see that they be sent to him at an opportunity.</p><p>The <b>Wittich books</b> I think I shall now obtain more easily than while his tenacious and difficult <b>sister</b> was alive. Yet I would not want all of them, but only those which I have noted in the appended schedule, from the report of my old servant Master <b>Christen Longomontanus</b> — known to you, who is now with me. If, however, the guardians of the orphan left [behind] are unwilling to sell anything from it separately, inform me of the just price of the whole, or, if it can be done, only of those I have listed. But if they altogether refuse to sell anything from it, at least take pains that — your good faith being interposed — I may obtain on loan, for a few weeks, those I most want; I will restore them at the appointed time, whole and unharmed.</p><p>To Lord <b>Daniel Bucretius</b> — whom I remember to have visited me once and again, formerly, on our island of <b>Uraniborg</b> — I have also given a letter, about the <b>observations</b>, if any survive, to be obtained from the heirs of that Astronomer who is said once to have been among you; which letter I should wish delivered to him, and a reply pressed in good time.</p><p>When, a few days ago, the Generous Baron <b>Karl von Warttenberg</b> was here with me, he gave me to inspect the <b>catalogue of the books of the whole Library of Lord Andreas Dudith of best memory</b> — which is still with me, to be returned to him when, on the coming feast-days, I shall visit him (as, at his asking, I promised) in his castle, <b>seven miles</b> distant from here. I should wish, therefore, that meanwhile you would — through yourself or another — fish out from the <b>heirs</b> whether the chance of seeing some of those that most pleased me can be granted, and whether they are willing to sell some separately; for they will scarcely find a buyer for the whole Library. If I learn from you that what I desire can be done, I will send a list of those I should most especially seek — which I would have done even now, had not the scarcity of leisure prevented a full perusal of all.</p><p>The Emperor has not yet returned from <b>Pilsen</b> to Prague, nor is it certain how soon it will be; yet we hope His Majesty will come immediately after the feast of <b>Pentecost</b>. There will then be present too, as they say, some legates of the German Princes. Of <b>Hungarian</b> affairs nothing has become known to us here that I do not think has equally been reported to you. The <b>English and Danish legates</b> are now meeting at <b>Emden</b>, to treat together of matters of great moment; about which, if they do not agree, there is danger lest that dissension breed <b>war</b> — which may God avert, to whom I commend you and all yours; in whom also fare well and live, sound, with all [yours].</p><p>Given at Benátky, 16 May, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacob Monau (Monavius)</b> of <b>Breslau</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000110001, of which this is the continuation). Tycho's principal Silesian book-agent.<br>- <b>Significance — a major book-acquisition / provenance letter.</b> Three threads:<br>  1. <b>The Wittich books (continuation of brahe-t_16000110001).</b> Tycho expects to obtain them \"more easily now that his <b>tenacious and difficult sister</b> [the <i>sister-heir</i> of the earlier letter] is dead.\" He wants <b>not all, but only specific titles</b> noted in an appended schedule drawn up from the report of <b>Longomontanus</b> (who had known Wittich's books at first hand and \"is now with me\"). The estate has passed to <b>the guardians of an orphan</b> (<i>pupillus</i>); Tycho offers to buy the whole, or his listed items, or — failing sale — to <b>borrow</b> the key books \"for a few weeks,\" on Monau's surety, to be returned \"whole and unharmed.\" A precise window onto the dispersal of <b>Paul Wittich's library</b> (incl. the hand-annotated Copernicus copies).<br>  2. <b>Daniel Bucretius &amp; the dead Breslau astronomer.</b> Tycho routes the request for the <b>observations</b> of the unnamed deceased Breslau astronomer (cf. brahe-t_16000110001) through <b>Daniel Bucretius</b> (Daniel Brouček/Bucretius, the Breslau mathematician who had twice visited Tycho on Hven, and would later help edit Tycho's works; cf. brahe-t_15980627001).<br>  3. <b>The library of Andreas Dudith (the notable new item).</b> <b>Baron Karl von Warttenberg</b> had brought Tycho the <b>catalogue of the whole library of Andreas Dudith</b> (<i>Andreas Dudithius</i>, 1533–1589 — the Hungarian humanist, ex-bishop of Pécs, diplomat and astronomical correspondent, who died at Breslau; a recurring figure in the corpus, e.g. brahe-t_15880817001). Tycho, about to visit Warttenberg \"in his castle seven miles hence,\" asks Monau to sound out <b>Dudith's heirs</b> whether they will let him see, and <b>sell separately</b>, the books that \"most pleased\" him — \"for they will scarcely find a buyer for the whole Library.\" Tycho will then send a target-list. A first-rate document of Tycho's late library-building and of the <b>dispersal of Dudith's renowned collection</b>.<br>- <b>Printing.</b> Monau's Breslau <b>printer</b> \"demands more than is fair\"; Tycho will print at <b>Wittenberg or Leipzig</b> instead, unless his <b>own press</b> reaches Bohemia in time (cf. the printing plans of brahe-t_16000110001, brahe-t_16010415002).<br>- <b>News (spring 1600).</b> <b>Rudolf II</b> still at <b>Pilsen</b> (plague-displaced), hoped back after <b>Pentecost</b>, with German princes' legates expected; <b>Hungarian</b> war-news; and the <b>Anglo-Danish conference at Emden</b> (1600 — the disputes over Sound dues / northern trade), which Tycho fears \"may breed war.\"<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Monau; <b>Tobias Fischer</b> (<i>Doctor Fischer</i>, the poet; cf. brahe-t_16000110001); <b>Bartholomäus Scultetus</b> (<i>Scultetus noster</i>, the Görlitz mathematician); <b>Paul Wittich</b> (his sister, the orphan-heir, the guardians); <b>Christen Longomontanus</b>; <b>Daniel Bucretius</b>; the unnamed dead Breslau astronomer; <b>Karl von Warttenberg</b>; <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (and his heirs); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benaticae 16 Maij A. 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin corrected from 'septimanae' to 'septimanas' (plural accusative, agreeing with 'pauculas'), per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII p.320.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Latin, read securely; the substantive content (the Breslau printer rejected for Wittenberg/Leipzig; the <b>Wittich-library acquisition</b> now that the sister is dead, with Longomontanus's target-list and a buy-or-borrow proposal; the <b>Bucretius</b> routing for the dead astronomer's observations; the <b>Dudith-library</b> catalogue and the bid to buy from the heirs via Warttenberg; the spring-1600 news from Pilsen, Hungary, and the Emden conference) is firmly construed and corpus-/reference-corroborated. No textual crux; the dead Breslau astronomer is left unnamed per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.319-322 as page images; the letter to Jacob Monau runs from the header on p.320 through 'Dabantur Benaticae 16 Maij A. 1600' at the top of p.321 (the Fischer letter begins immediately after). Collated the full letter against the page images, including all proper names (Fischer, Scultetus, Christen Longomontanus, Daniel Bucretius, Karl von Warttenberg, Andreas Dudith), numbers (7 miles to Dudith's castle), and place-names (Wittenberg/Leipzig, Uraniborg, Pilsen, Emden). One TEXT_ERROR found as above; otherwise an exact match, including the translation's handling of 'his tenacious and difficult sister' for the Wittich books.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). our source_text prints the noun as singular 'septimanae', disagreeing in number with its own governing adjective 'pauculas' (plural) and with the accusative 'per' construction; Dreyer's page correctly reads plural accusative 'septimanas'. A corrupted case-ending, not a meaning-changing error (the English translation 'a few weeks' is unaffected either way). Dreyer prints: <i>per pauculas septimanas mutuo obtineam (TBOO VIII p.320)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.320 (page image, not OCR text layer). No other part of the letter was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Jacob Monavius", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000314001", "next": "brahe-t_16000614001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000314001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000614001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Discusses printing arrangements for his works, noting Wittenberg or Leipzig as cheaper alternatives to the current printer's excessive price, and briefly acknowledges letters from Fischer and Scultetus.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000516001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000516002", "d": "1600-05-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Tobias Fischer", "ck": "Tobias Fischer", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Tobias Fischer — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 16 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET DOCTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TOBIAe FISCHERO, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS DILECTO.</p><p>CLARISSIME et praestantissime vir, accepi quidem literas tuas unâ cum carminibus in mei gratiam a te eleganter et erudite concinnatis, antecedente anno, cum primum Pragam venissem, a Domino Doctore SWALBIO, communi nostro amico, mihi redditas, tum quoque alteras hoc ipso anno Mense Martio ad me datas. Verum quod hactenus neutris responderim, velim occupationum mearum multitudini, quodque varie interea partim locorum mutationibus, partim alijs impedimentis distractus fuerim, tribuas, meque pro tuo singulari candore excusatum habeas. Fuerunt sane utraeque illae literae mihi longe acceptissimae, nam praeter insignem humanitatem pari eruditioni coniunctam, quae inde satis superque elucebat, eximium etiam et sincerum erga me, licet de facie tibi ignotum, amorem et animum candoris integerrimi refertissimum affatim ostendebant, pro qua tua in me sincera, licet antea nihil tale a te promeritum, benevolentia tibi gratias habeo quam maximas, et te vicissim intime diligere, atque in amicorum, quos selectiori albo inscribo, annumerare non desinam, daboque operam, ne te certâsse priorem poeniteat.</p><p>Coeterum quod ad carmina illa tua, quae etiam mihi longe gratissima erant, attinet, velim, ut in meliorem partem accipias, quod paucula quaedam in ijs alterârim, prout metri illius genus postulare videbatur secundum eos criticos, qui libertatem illam, qua tu nonnunquam usus es, curiositate quadam non facile admittunt. Quid vero pauculis in locis mutatum sit, carmen illud denuo exaratum et excellentiss. Domino MONAVIO (si recte memini) transmissum patefaciet. Nescio tamen, an ultimus versus isthic ita transformatus sit, prout postea (ni fallor) incidit, qui sic habet:</p><p>Gloria vana rudis vulgi, laus una placere<br>Bonis, malorum negligere calumnias.</p><p>Laudo ingenium tuum, laudo venam mellifluam et delicatam, et si plura eiuscemodi aut etiam alio carminis modo per otium luseris mecumque communicaveris, erit id mihi gratum, eaque prioribus adiungam. Coacervo multorum excellentium Poëtarum mea de causa, postquam patria excessi, exarata Poëmata, donec libellum aliquem mediocrem adimpleverim; videbis suo tempore sylvulam.</p><p>Opera mea Astronomica tibi impertiri necdum licet, ea enim ad manus non habeo, nam sunt inter res meas, quae adhuc Magdeburgi reservantur, brevi tamen ex clementissima voluntate Caesaris huc advehenda, neque etiam omnia, quae typis prius inchoata habeo, plane ad Colophonem deducta sunt, ob eam, quae mihi in Patria evenit, studiorum conturbationem. Spero tamen, me, favente divino Numine, cuius provisu Caesarea Maiestas e tanto naufragio hunc portum, tantisque turbulentijs hoc otium benignissime mihi indulsit, ea, quae desiderantur, propediem absoluturum, et plura additurum; tunc te voti compotem haud invite reddam, interea morulam intervenientem pro tuo candore in meliorem partem accipe.</p><p>Globi MERCATORIS, de quibus scribis, iam ferme antiquati sunt, vixque, quod sciam, reperiuntur exemplaria. Duo talium parandorum artifices Batavi, quorum unus Amsterodami IACOBUS FLORENTIUS, alter Alcmariae WILHELMUS IANUS nominatus, globos secundum nostram in fixis sideribus restitutionem diligenter elaborârunt. Quorum posterior dimidium annum mecum in Insula nostra Uraniburgica fuit, atque ibidem stellas suo, quod attulit, globoso corpori, me circiter millenas illi concedente, insignivit. Si aliquam ex his nancisci posses reformationem, diceres MERCATORIS vel aliorum globos, ex erroneis stellarum canonibus, non autem coelitus verificatis locis, conformatos, ijs non aequiparari posse. Nescio, an Francofurtum hi neoterici advehantur, sed si Bohemiam aliquando ingressus me inviseris, exemplum monstrabo, tunc simul alia, quae penes me elaborata habeo, siquidem te etiam his studijs affici videam, lubens ostendam, tecumque de talibus iucunde conferam.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur ex arce Caesarea Benatika 16 Maij A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most learned man, Lord Tobias Fischer, distinguished Doctor of Philosophy and Medicine, his friend especially beloved.</b></p><p>Most renowned and most excellent man, I did indeed receive your letter, with the poems elegantly and learnedly composed by you in my honour — delivered to me last year, when I had first come to Prague, by Lord Doctor <b>Schwalb</b>, our common friend — and also others, sent me this very year in the month of March. But that I have hitherto answered neither, I would have you attribute to the multitude of my occupations, and to my being meanwhile variously distracted, partly by changes of place, partly by other impediments; and hold me excused, after your singular candour. Both those letters were indeed far most welcome to me: for besides the remarkable humanity joined to equal erudition, which shone forth from them more than enough, they abundantly showed an exceptional and sincere love toward me — though unknown to you by face — and a mind most full of the most upright candour; for which sincere benevolence of yours toward me (though I had previously deserved nothing such from you) I give you the greatest thanks, and will not cease, in turn, to love you intimately and to count you among the friends I inscribe on the more select roll; and I will take pains that you do not regret having striven first [in friendship].</p><p>For the rest, as concerns those poems of yours — also far most pleasing to me — I would have you take in good part that I altered a very few things in them, as the kind of that metre seemed to demand, according to those critics who do not easily admit, with a certain fastidiousness, that liberty which you have sometimes used. What has been changed in a very few places, that poem — written out anew and sent to the most excellent Lord <b>Monau</b> (if I rightly remember) — will reveal. Yet I know not whether the last verse there was so transformed as it afterward (unless I am mistaken) occurred [to me], which runs thus:</p><p><i>\"The glory of the rude crowd is vain; the one [true] praise is to please</i><br><i>the good, [and] to disregard the calumnies of the wicked.\"</i></p><p>I praise your genius, I praise your mellifluous and delicate vein; and if you compose more of this kind — or even in another manner of poem — at leisure, and share them with me, it will be welcome, and I will add them to the earlier ones. I am heaping up the Poems composed by many excellent Poets on my account, since I left my fatherland, until I shall have filled some moderate little book; you will see in time a little <b>sylva</b> [collection].</p><p>My <b>Astronomical works</b> I cannot yet impart to you, for I do not have them at hand: they are among my things still kept at <b>Magdeburg</b>, soon however to be brought hither by the Emperor's most clement will; nor are all the things I had earlier begun in print quite brought to the colophon, on account of that disturbance of my studies which befell me in my fatherland. Yet I hope — by the favour of the divine Power, by whose provision the Imperial Majesty most kindly granted me this harbour out of so great a shipwreck, and this leisure out of so great turbulences — soon to complete what is wanting, and to add more; then I will, not unwillingly, make you master of your wish; meanwhile take the intervening little delay in good part, after your candour.</p><p>The <b>globes of Mercator</b>, about which you write, are now almost antiquated, and scarcely, so far as I know, are copies to be found. <b>Two Dutch craftsmen for making such [globes]</b> — one at <b>Amsterdam, named Jacobus Florentius</b>, the other at <b>Alkmaar, named Wilhelm Janus</b> — have diligently elaborated globes <b>according to our restitution in the fixed stars</b>. Of these, the <b>latter was with me half a year on our island of Uraniborg, and there marked the stars upon the globe-body he had brought — I granting him about a thousand</b>. If you could obtain one of these renewed [globes], you would say that Mercator's globes, or others' — fashioned from erroneous star-catalogues, not from positions verified from the sky — cannot be matched with them. I know not whether these new ones are brought to Frankfurt; but if, having entered Bohemia at some time, you visit me, I will show you a specimen; then at the same time I will gladly show you other things I have elaborated, if I see you too taken with these studies, and will pleasantly confer with you about such matters.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, 16 May, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient &amp; occasion.</b> Tycho's <b>reply to Tobias Fischer</b> (the Silesian poet-physician of Schweidnitz; cf. his letter brahe-t_16000318001), written the same day as the Monau letter (brahe-t_16000516001). Tycho admits Fischer to his friendship and thanks him for two sets of verses (delivered via <b>Doctor Schwalb</b>, <i>Swalbius</i>, in 1599 and March 1600).<br>- <b>The poems and the <i>sylvula</i>.</b> Tycho explains his <b>emendation</b> of Fischer's verses (tightening a metrical \"liberty\" the strict critics dislike), the corrected copy sent to <b>Monau</b>, and quotes the <b>revised closing distich</b> — <i>\"Gloria vana rudis vulgi, laus una placere / Bonis, malorum negligere calumnias\"</i> (\"the glory of the rude crowd is vain; the one praise is to please the good and ignore the wicked's calumnies\"). He is <b>collecting friends' commendatory poems</b> \"since I left my fatherland\" into a planned <b>little <i>sylva</b></i> (verse-anthology in his honour).<br>- <b>His works still at Magdeburg.</b> Tycho cannot yet send his <b>astronomical works</b> (still stored at <b>Magdeburg</b>, awaiting the imperial transport; the <i>Progymnasmata</i> not yet \"brought to the colophon\" because of \"the disturbance … in my fatherland\"). The \"harbour out of so great a shipwreck … this leisure out of so great turbulences\" is his standard figure for the imperial refuge.<br>- <b>Significance — the Tychonic celestial globes and Willem Jansz (Blaeu) at Uraniborg (cornerstone of cartographic history).</b> Asked about <b>Mercator's globes</b> (\"now almost antiquated\"), Tycho names <b>two Dutch globe-makers who built globes on his restored star-positions</b>:<br>  - <b>Jacobus Florentius of Amsterdam</b> = <b>Jacob Floris van Langren</b> (of the Van Langren globe-making house);<br>  - <b>Wilhelm Janus of Alkmaar</b> = <b>Willem Janszoon (Blaeu)</b> (1571–1638), the future great Amsterdam cartographer.<br>  <b>The latter \"was with me half a year on the island of Uraniborg, and there marked the stars on the globe-body he had brought, I granting him about a thousand.\"</b> This is Tycho's own testimony to <b>Blaeu's ~half-year stay at Hven (1595–96) and the ~1,000 Tychonic star-positions</b> he set on his celestial globe — the documentary basis for the <b>Blaeu globes</b> carrying Tycho's catalogue, far superior to globes \"from erroneous star-catalogues, not positions verified from the sky.\" (This <b>resolves the \"Dutch globe-maker\" item</b> flagged from brahe-t_16000506001, where Tycho's gift-globe to Elector Christian II was made by \"a skilled Dutchman at Uraniborg\" — i.e. Blaeu.)<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Tobias Fischer; <b>Bartholomäus Schwalb</b> (<i>Swalbius</i>); <b>Jacob Monau</b>; <b>Mercator</b> (Gerardus); <b>Jacob Floris van Langren</b> (<i>Jacobus Florentius</i>); <b>Willem Janszoon Blaeu</b> (<i>Wilhelm Janus</i> of Alkmaar); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Benatika 16 Maij A. 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Latin, read securely; the substance (the admission of Fischer to friendship and the emended verses with the quoted distich; the verse-anthology <i>sylvula</i>; the works still at Magdeburg; and above all the <b>globe passage</b> naming Van Langren and <b>Blaeu</b>, and Blaeu's half-year at Uraniborg marking ~1,000 Tychonic stars) is firmly construed and of the first importance for the history of celestial globes. No textual crux; the globe-maker identifications resolve the prior open item.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.320-324 as page images; the audited letter (Tycho to Tobias Fischer, 16 May 1600) runs pp.321(bottom)-323, between the Monavius letter and the Bucretius letter. Collated opening/closing, every proper name (Doctor Swalbio/Schwalb, Monavio/Monau, Iacobus Florentius of Amsterdam, Wilhelmus Ianus of Alkmaar, Mercator), the quoted couplet (Gloria vana rudis vulgi...), and figures ('half a year', 'about a thousand' stars) against Dreyer; source_text matches word for word and the English is accurate throughout. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Tobias Fischer", "count": 2, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000318001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000318001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Apologizes for delayed replies to two letters and enclosed poems, attributing the silence to occupations and changes of place, and thanks Fischer for his sincere affection.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000516002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000516003", "d": "1600-05-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Daniel Bucretius", "ck": "Daniel Bucretius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Daniel Bucretius — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 16 May 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO DANIELI BUCRETIO, PHILOSOPHIAe ET MEDICINAe DOCTORI EXIMIO VRATISLAVIAe DEGENTI, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS DILECTO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>CLARISSIME et praestantissime vir, vetus illa inter nos, cum me in Insula prioris habitationis Uraniburgica, dum in iuventute peregrinareris, semel atque iterum invisisti, contracta notitia, cuius te adhuc memorem esse non dubito, efficit, ut eo liberius hoc tempore ad te scribam, praesertim cum aliquid inciderit, in quo tua opera mihi usui esse possit, quod et te mihi non denegaturum, singularis tua humanitas et erga me, prout a M. CHRISTIERNO LONGOMONTANO, qui ante biennium apud vos fuit, et nunc apud me est, intellexi, benevolum studium, alias etiam mihi perspectum, fidem facit. Est autem eiuscemodi.</p><p>Retulit mihi priori anno excellentiss. vir, Dominus D. HIERONIMUS TREUTLERUS, cum simul Pragae essemus, Vratislavij aliquando fuisse Astronomiae vel etiam Astrologiae iudiciariae impense addictum virum, quem observationes quasdam in motu Astrorum obtinuisse aiebat, quas etiamnum filius eius secum reservârit. Quare te per veterem nostram amicitiam rogatum cuperem, ut a filio illo vel alijs haeredibus mihi observationum illarum, quotquot adhuc in promptu sunt, copiam impetrare velis. Etsi enim vix credere queam, eas ad tantam praecisionem deductas esse, quantam ego in talibus requiro, et rei inquirendae subtilitas per se postulat, cum idoneis organis proculdubio destitutus fuerit, tamen qualescunque tandem sunt, eas obtinere averem. Est aliquid prodire tenus, si non datur ultra. Si itaque id, quicquid est observationum, ad mundum accurate descriptum, tua intercessione et diligentia consecutus fuero, officium, quovis alio, quantum per me licet, compensandum, mihi hac ratione praestiteris. Vale. Plura enim ob aliarum occupationum molem nunc non vacat. Salutat te supradictus M. CHRISTIERNUS officiose, et pro pristinis illi, cum apud vos esset, beneficijs, non saltem is, sed et ego, gratias agimus. Scripturus est ipsemet tibi, cum rectius vacârit.</p><p>Iterum quam felicissime vale.<br>Datae ex arce Caesarea Benatika 16 Maij A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and excellent man, Lord Daniel Bucretius, distinguished doctor of philosophy and medicine, dwelling at Breslau, his especially beloved friend.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and excellent Sir: that old acquaintance struck up between us — when, travelling in your youth, you visited me once and again on the island of my former dwelling, Uraniborg — of which I do not doubt you are still mindful, makes me write to you the more freely at this time, especially since something has arisen in which your service may be of use to me; and that you will not deny me this, your singular kindness, and the well-disposed zeal toward me (as I have understood from Master Christen Longomontanus, who was with you two years ago and is now with me), elsewhere too well known to me, give me assurance. The matter is of this kind.</p><p>Last year the most excellent man, Lord Doctor Hieronymus Treutler, related to me, when we were together at Prague, that there had once been at Breslau a man earnestly devoted to Astronomy — or even to judicial Astrology — who, he said, had obtained certain observations in the motion of the stars, which his son still keeps by him. Wherefore I should wish to ask you, by our old friendship, that you would obtain for me from that son, or from the other heirs, a copy of those observations, as many as are still at hand. For although I can scarcely believe that they were brought to such precision as I require in such matters, and as the delicacy of the inquiry itself demands — since he was doubtless destitute of suitable instruments — yet, whatever in the end they are, I should be eager to obtain them. It is something to advance so far, if it is not granted [to go] beyond. If therefore I shall, through your intercession and diligence, come into possession of whatever there is of these observations, accurately copied out fair, you will have rendered me by this means a service to be repaid by any other, so far as lies in my power. Farewell — for more there is now no leisure, owing to the mass of other occupations. The aforesaid Master Christen greets you dutifully; and for the former kindnesses [shown] him while he was with you, not only he but I too render thanks. He will write to you himself when he has the better leisure.</p><p>Again, most happily, farewell.<br>Given from the Imperial castle of Benátky, the 16th of May, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Benátky nad Jizerou</b> (protocol §5.7), shortly before the move to Prague; the letter belongs to his campaign to gather and absorb earlier observational data into his own restored astronomy.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Daniel Bucretius</b>, doctor of philosophy and medicine, <i>Vratislaviae degens</i> — <b>resident at Breslau (Wrocław)</b>. Tycho recalls that Bucretius had, in his youthful travels, visited him <i>more than once</i> on Hven; he is used here as a local intermediary at Breslau.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>M. Christiernus Longomontanus</i> = <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (1562–1647), Tycho's longest-serving and most senior assistant, who had himself been at Breslau \"two years ago\" and is now back with Tycho; he both vouches for Bucretius's goodwill and adds his own greeting.</li><li><i>Dominus D. Hieronymus Treutlerus</i> = <b>Hieronymus Treutler (Treutler von Krottendorf)</b> (1565–1607), jurist, the informant (met at Prague the previous year) for the report of the Breslau observer.</li><li>The <b>unnamed Breslau astronomer/astrologer</b>, <i>Astronomiae vel etiam Astrologiae iudiciariae impense addictus</i>, deceased, whose <b>son</b> (or other heirs) is said to retain his <i>observationes in motu astrorum</i>. He is not identified in the source; Tycho's request is precisely to recover whatever survives of his papers.</li><li><b>Astronomy (protocol §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>observationes … in motu astrorum</i> = <b>observations of the motion of the stars</b>; <i>praecisio</i> = <b>precision</b>; <i>idonea organa</i> = <b>suitable instruments</b> (<i>organa</i> in Tycho's sense, §5.2) — the lack of which makes Tycho doubt the data's accuracy even as he seeks it. The <i>Astronomia / Astrologia iudiciaria</i> distinction (mathematical astronomy vs. <b>judicial astrology</b>) is kept as written (§5.3).</li><li><b>Allusion.</b> <i>Est aliquid prodire tenus, si non datur ultra</i> adapts <b>Horace, <i>Epistles</i> I.1.32</b> (<i>est quādam prodire tenus, si non datur ultra</i>) — \"it is something to advance so far, if to go further is not granted\"; the literary tag is preserved and glossed, not paraphrased away.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arx Caesarea Benatika</i> = the <b>Imperial castle of Benátky</b>; <i>Vratislavia</i> = <b>Breslau / Wrocław</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S. P.</i> = <i>salutem plurimam</i> (\"heartiest greeting\"). <i>Vale … iterum quam felicissime vale</i> → \"Farewell … again, most happily, farewell.\" <i>Datas</i> (sc. <i>literas</i>, epistolary) → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>16 Maij A. 1600</i>; dataset marks <b>n.s.</b> (New Style), consistent with the Benátky/Prague series of this month. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'invasisti' corrected to 'invisisti', and 'Datas' corrected to 'Datae', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 323-324.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean humanist Latin, sense secure; the one proper name left open (the dead Breslau observer) is anonymous in the source itself, not a reading crux. Longomontanus, Treutler, and the Horatian tag are all securely identified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the full Dreyer slice (pages 1-4, TBOO VIII pp. 322-325) as page images; the Bucretius letter itself occupies pp. 323-324 (slice pages 2-3), which were also each re-read individually to check two disputed words closely. Collated the full letter clause-by-clause: heading/address, opening line, the Longomontanus/Treutler/observations narrative, every proper name (Daniel Bucretius, Christiernus Longomontanus, Hieronimus Treutlerus), place names (Uraniburgica, Vratislavia/Breslau, Praga, Benatika/Benátky), the biennium figure, and the closing date (16 Maij 1600) against source_text and published_english. Found no FABRICATION, OMISSION, MISTRANSLATION, or HEDGE_LOSS in the English translation — it is faithful sentence-by-sentence, and in both disputed spots below it actually follows Dreyer's correct reading rather than our own (corrupted) source_text. Found two TEXT_ERROR instances where source_text itself departs from Dreyer ('invasisti' for Dreyer's 'invisisti'; 'Datas' for Dreyer's 'Datae'). Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of translation quality, so grade is C; verdict is MINOR since neither defect changes the sense actually delivered to a reader of the English translation.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text reads 'invasisti' (from invado, 'you invaded/attacked'), but Dreyer's print clearly shows 'invisisti' (from inviso, 'you visited') — two different verbs, not a spelling variant. The published English correctly follows Dreyer's 'invisisti' ('you visited'), so the translation itself is unaffected, but our stored Latin source_text is corrupted relative to the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>femel atque iterum invifisti (= invisisti) — p. 323, line 17</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads 'Datas', Dreyer prints 'Datae' (the standard closing formula, agreeing with implied litterae). The English 'Given' is compatible with either reading, so no translation-level consequence, but it is a real divergence between source_text and Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Datae ex arce Cæfarea Benatika 16 Maij A. 1600. — p. 324, line 4</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified both defects by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.322-325 page images. 'invififti' (p.323) and 'Datae' (p.324) are both unambiguous. Neither correction affects the English translation, which was already faithful to Dreyer's readings. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Invokes their old Uraniborg acquaintance and asks Bucretius to help locate a Breslau figure known to have been devoted to astronomy or judicial astrology.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000516003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000600001", "d": "1600-06-00", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an open testimonial for] Johannes Kepler — Benátky [nad Jizerou], beginning of June 1600 (catalogued April 1600)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witnesses: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703, foll. 151–152; B = ÖNB, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.38, foll. 4–5</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOANNEM KEPLERUM.</p><p>EGO TYCHO BRAHE de Knudstrup et Uraniburg in Regno Daniae, nunc vero Sacrae Caesae Matis cliens in arce Bohemiae Bennaticha habitans, notum facio omnibus et singulis hasce litteras inspecturis, cuiuscunque status, dignitatis et conditionis fuerint, earundem exhibitorem, ornatissimum et eruditissimum virum M. KEPLERUM, Ill. Ducatus Styriae Statuum Mathematicum, hoc labente Anno Epochae Christianae 1600 ab initio mensis Februarij usque ad finem Maij, per quatuor nimirum intercedentes menses pro maiori parte hîc in dicta Caesaris arce Bennaticha mecum fuisse, seque interea in meo convictu honeste gessisse et apprime diligentem operam in exercitijs et studijs Astronomicis praestitisse, intermediantibus meis iustae magnitudinis et conformationis instrumentis et observationibus multiplicibus atque diutinis, mutuaque inter nos de talibus atque alijs ad Philosophiam facientibus collocutione: ut omnino mihi persuadeam, illum id temporis non minus frugifero hîc collocasse, quam si interea domi desedisset, imo si absque invidia id affirmare liceret, longe utilius. Quo nomine etiam eius praesentia et conversatio mihi imprimis grata extitit, multoque adhuc gratior futura, si eadem in posterum saepius et diutius concedi poterit.</p><p>Quapropter omnibus illustribus, praestantibus et cordatis viris praenominatum M. KEPLERUM de meliore nota quam commendatissimum esse cupio, utque eius laudabile propositum et conatus egregios in hac sublimi et a vulgi captu semota scientia ulterius et plenius excolenda adiuvent et promoveant, quibus possum precibus obnixe et officiose rogo atque obtestor: fecerint sic meo equidem iudicio Reipubl. litterariae, praecipue vero divinae illius Astronomiae totiusque eruditae Matheseos alumnis rem admodum utilem atque acceptam, quin et non saltem ipsi M. KEPLERO commodam, sed et mihi ob conformia studia longe gratissimam, ac quocunque per me licuerit officio vicissim compensandam et obsequiosa mente perpetuo recolendam. In quorum omnium evidentiorem et pleniorem testificationem sigillum meum apposui et propria manu subscripsi.</p><p>Actum in arce Caesarea Bennaticha inchoante mense Iunio Anno 1600.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe, [concerning] Johannes Kepler.</b></p><p>I, Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup and Uraniborg in the Kingdom of Denmark, but now client of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, dwelling in the Bohemian castle of Benátky, make known to each and all who shall inspect this letter, of whatever status, dignity and condition they may be, that the bearer of the same — the most accomplished and most learned man, Master Kepler, Mathematician of the Estates of the Illustrious Duchy of Styria — in this passing Year of the Christian Era 1600, from the beginning of February until the end of May, that is, through the four intervening months, was for the greater part here with me in the said Imperial castle of Benátky; and that he meanwhile conducted himself honourably in my company, and rendered exceptionally diligent work in astronomical exercises and studies — with the mediation of my instruments of fitting size and construction and of my manifold and long-continued observations, and of our mutual conversation about these matters and others conducing to Philosophy — so that I am wholly persuaded that he spent that time here no less fruitfully than if he had meanwhile sat at home, nay (if it might be affirmed without envy) far more usefully. On which account his presence and converse was especially welcome to me, and will be much more welcome still, if the same may in time to come be granted more often and for longer.</p><p>Wherefore I desire that the aforenamed Master Kepler be most warmly commended, in the best regard, to all illustrious, excellent and sensible men; and with what prayers I can I earnestly and dutifully ask and beseech them to aid and promote his laudable purpose and his outstanding endeavours toward the further and fuller cultivation of this sublime science, set apart from the grasp of the common crowd. They will thus, in my judgement, do a thing very useful and welcome to the Republic of Letters, but especially to the nurslings of that divine Astronomy and of all learned Mathematics; nay, a thing not only of advantage to Master Kepler himself, but also — on account of our kindred studies — far most pleasing to me, to be repaid in turn by whatever service shall lie in my power, and perpetually cherished with an obliging mind. In clearer and fuller testification of all which, I have affixed my seal and subscribed with my own hand.</p><p>Done in the Imperial castle of Benátky, at the beginning of the month of June, in the year 1600.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Genre.</b> This is not an ordinary letter but an <b>open testimonial / letter of recommendation</b> (<i>litterae testimoniales</i>) — a public certificate, drawn in the notarial first person (<i>Ego Tycho Brahe … notum facio omnibus et singulis hasce litteras inspecturis</i>), sealed and autograph, attesting Kepler's residence and conduct and commending him to \"all illustrious men.\" The heading <i>ad Ioannem Keplerum</i> in the source marks the subject; the document was given <b>to</b> Kepler to carry (<i>earundem exhibitorem</i>, \"the bearer of the same\").</li><li><b>Occasion.</b> It closes <b>Kepler's first stay with Tycho</b> — from the beginning of February to the end of May 1600, the very months of their first collaboration at Benátky (Kepler had arrived 4 February). Tycho here certifies that period and, by commending Kepler to patrons, smooths his position as he returned to Graz amid the Styrian Counter-Reformation expulsions. Within months Kepler would join Tycho's household permanently; on Tycho's death (24 October 1601) he succeeded him as Imperial Mathematician and inherited the observations this document describes.</li><li><b>Self-titling.</b> <i>Tycho Brahe de Knudstrup et Uraniburg in Regno Daniae, nunc vero Sacrae Caesareae Maiestatis cliens</i> — Tycho styles himself by his Danish patrimony (<b>Knudstrup</b>) and his lost observatory (<b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, §5.7), \"but now <b>client</b> [retainer] of His Sacred Imperial Majesty\" (<b>Rudolf II</b>), resident at Benátky.</li><li><b>Kepler's title.</b> <i>M. Keplerus, Ill. Ducatus Styriae Statuum Mathematicus</i> = <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, <i>Magister</i> (academic Master, §5.5), <b>mathematician to the Estates of the Duchy of Styria</b> (his Graz appointment as <i>Landschaftsmathematiker</i>).</li><li><b>Astronomy (protocol §5.1–5.2).</b> <i>mea iustae magnitudinis et conformationis instrumenta et observationes multiplices atque diutinae</i> = Tycho's <b>instruments</b> (<i>instrumenta/organa</i>, §5.2) \"of fitting size and construction\" and his \"manifold and long-continued <b>observations</b>\" — the working capital of the Benátky establishment that Kepler had been set to use (notably on the Mars data). <i>exercitia et studia Astronomica</i> rendered \"astronomical exercises and studies.\"</li><li><b>Formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Respublica litteraria</i> → \"the Republic of Letters\"; <i>Astronomiae … alumni</i> → \"the nurslings of Astronomy\"; <i>Actum</i> → \"Done\"; <i>manu propria</i> → \"with his own hand\" (autograph + seal attestation). The humility/\"without envy\" topos (<i>absque invidia</i>) is preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar &amp; dating crux (protocol §3, §4.1).</b> The subscription reads <i>inchoante mense Iunio Anno 1600</i> — \"<b>at the beginning of June 1600</b>\" — and the body's own span (<i>ab initio mensis Februarij usque ad finem Maij … per quatuor intercedentes menses</i>) requires a date <i>after</i> the end of May. The header therefore <b>leads with the as-written date (beginning of June 1600)</b>; the dataset's catalogue label \"<b>1600. april</b>\" disagrees and is recorded as <i>(catalogued April 1600)</i> per protocol §4.1, <b>not</b> silently adopted. The April label is most likely a catalogue mis-entry, since the text cannot have been written before the residence it certifies had ended. No calendar-style marker (O.S./N.S.) is given; in Imperial service Tycho was by now dating New Style (cf. the surrounding 1600 letters), but the testimonial states no style, so none is asserted here.</li><li><b>Witnesses.</b> Two Vienna manuscripts (ÖNB cod. lat. 10703 and 10686) preserve the text within the Tychonic/Keplerian <i>Nachlass</i> — the same Vienna codices that hold much of the observational archive (cf. the KB↔Vienna collation work).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is clean, complete, and unambiguous, and the document's import (Tycho's formal testimonial for Kepler) is secure. The single point of care is the dating: the as-written \"beginning of June 1600\" is internally required by the text and is led with, while the divergent catalogue label \"April\" is flagged in the header and explained here rather than reconciled — a §4.1 header/label discrepancy, not a textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp. 323-326) visually, not OCR. The letter text on pp. 324-325 (heading 'TYCHO BRAHE AD IOANNEM KEPLERUM' through 'manu propria.') matches source_text word for word, including every proper name (Knudstrup, Uraniburg/Uraniborg, Bennaticha/Benátky, KEPLERUM), number (1600 x3, 'four intervening months' = Feb-May inclusive), and date formula. Specifically checked the flagged day-number concern: the closing reads 'Actum in arce Caesarea Bennaticha inchoante mense Iunio Anno 1600' in both source_text and Dreyer's print — 'inchoante mense Iunio' (beginning of the month of June) with no day-number given, matching the packet's date '1600-06-00' and rendered correctly in English as 'at the beginning of the month of June' with no invented day. Compared every clause of published_english against source_text sentence by sentence: no fabricated names/numbers/clauses, no silently dropped sentences, no flipped pronouns/antecedents (all 'he'/'his' correctly track Kepler throughout, all 'my'/'I' correctly track Tycho, including the seal/subscription clause), no hardened hedges. Word order and some connective-phrase rendering (e.g. 'nimirum' as 'that is') differ but do not change meaning, per the brief's instruction to ignore such variance.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Sammelhandschrift incl. Primae lineae litterarum germanicarum de libris T. Brahe ab Iohann…", "sh": "Cod. 10686/36-44", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961789"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10703 foll. 151-152.", "B Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.38 foll. 4-5."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_16000405002", "next": "brahe-t_16000828001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000405002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16001017001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Certifies that Kepler spent four months (February–May 1600) at Benátky working diligently on astronomical exercises with Tycho's instruments and observations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000600001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000614001", "d": "1600-06-14", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Monavius", "ck": "Jacob Monavius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wrocław", "t": "Jacob Monau (Monavius) → Tycho Brahe — Wrocław [Breslau], 14 June 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO NOBILISSIMO ET AMPLISSIMO VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICORUM SECULI NOSTRI ET PRAeSERTIM APUD SACRATISSIMAM CAeSAREAM MAIESTATEM PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUMME OBSERVANDO. BENNATICAe BOHEMORUM.</p><p>MAGNIFICE Domine TYCHO, Vir praestantissime: Non vulgari laetitia fui affectus, quando mihi redditae sunt literae tuae XVI Maij scriptae, e quibus libentissime cognovi, meas, quas mense Martio scripseram, rectâ pervenisse in manus tuas. Eram enim de ijs nonnihil sollicitus, postquam missae fuerant ad illum, quem Pragae non invenerant.</p><p>Ad typographum nostrum quod attinet, facile poteram cogitare hoc ipsum, quod contigit: nimirum te pretio magis aequo posse alicubi locorum curare tua inprimenda, quam apud nos, ubi non sunt omnia sic expedita et in promptu. Boni tamen consules meum studium qualecunque gratificandi tuis postulatis et desiderijs: opto ex animo, ut in re alia possim tibi probare meum animum tibi deditissimum.</p><p>Ad Doctorem BUCRETIUM quod attinet: is te amanter, diligenter et officiose rogat, et pro missis literis humaniss. magnas agit gratias. Bene te iubeo sperare et de Wittichianis reliquijs et de chartis alterius illius, qui quondam apud nos vixit, Astronomi. Utroque loco hanc rem tanta et fide et diligentia et tam accurate et sedulo egit et adhuc agit, ut non dubitem nos habituros, quod optamus. Non voluit nunc respondere literis tuis, quod confidat se propediem reipsa votis tuis satisfacturum: modo parvam moram aequo feras animo, magnopere te rogatum volo.</p><p>Domino Doctori FISCHERO, Viro certe opt. et doctissimo, gratissimae fuerunt literae tuae, daturo modis omnibus operam, ut suam tibi fidem et diligentiam omni, quod a se praestare poterit, officio probet. Nec te spero unquam poenitebit tuae in ipsum benevolentiae.</p><p>De Bibliotheca Duditiana, quae mihi est notissima, velim mihi credas. Eam nunc velut inter coelum et terram pendere, postquam ab haeredibus DUDITIJ non possidetur, sed alteri cuidam pro certa pecuniae summa non tam vendita est quam oppignorata. Iacet nunc misera apud nos in domo quadam, et non aliter iacet (vidi enim aliquoties) quam solent apud nobiles iacere acervi frumentorum: neque in illam plenum ius habet Emtor neque etiam venditor. Res plane intricata est, et metuo, ne libri isti magnum aliquod et irreparabile damnum accipiant.</p><p>Dictum mihi est nuper ab amico quodam, instrumenta tua Mathematica non levi iactura fuisse per vim ventorum et tempestatum in arce istac tua affecta. Si ita est, admodum tibi condoleo. Dubito tamen, an sit, cum tu nullam eius rei mentionem facias.</p><p>Magnificus huius urbis et Ducatus Capitaneus, Dn. ABRAHAMUS IENGTIUS, olim summi nostri WITICHIJ, dum viveret, patronus, tui amantiss. et studiosissimus, magnopere te salutat. Cogitat paulo post ad thermas Boiemicas proficisci, et plane constituit in illo itinere te convenire, ut te videat coram, quem dudum est unice miratus. Dixit ei nuper Doctor IESSENIUS de quodam libro tuo recens edito, quem obtuleris Caesari. Eum ipse unice videre gestit, et iam semel et iterum an viderim ex me quaesivit. Epistolarum tuarum volumen, quod ex dono liberalitatis tuae habeo, apud me primus vidit, et per aliquot dies secum habuit.</p><p>Si quid ad me scribere aut mittere voles, scias rectissime ad me perventurum, quicquid Pragam per certos homines miseris ad Dn. IOANNEM MYLLNERUM, Sacrae Caesae Matis Consiliarium et Regni Boem. Secretarium supremum, Compatrem meum cariss. et omni fide observandum. Apud illum enim vivit IOANNES PALUDIUS, Principis mei Consiliarius Pragae habitans et negotia Cels. suae in aula illa curans. Is et diligenter et libenter curabit, quicquid ad me pertinens acceperit. Bene et feliciter vale, Vir praestantissime, et me, quem semel amare coepisti, perpetuo amare perge: sic te vicissim curet ametque Deus.</p><p>Vratisl. XIIIJ Iunij 1600.<br>T. Μ. P. observantiss. et amantiss.<br>IAC. MONAW.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent, most noble, and most ample man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Prince of the Mathematicians of our age — and especially with the most sacred Imperial Majesty — his Lord and friend most highly to be respected. [To] Benátky of the Bohemians.</b></p><p>Magnificent Lord Tycho, most excellent Man: I was filled with no common joy when your letter, written <b>16 May</b>, was delivered to me, from which I most gladly learned that mine, which I had written in <b>March</b>, came straight into your hands; for I was somewhat anxious about them, after they had been sent to him whom they had not found at Prague.</p><p>As concerns our <b>printer</b>, I could easily imagine this very thing which happened — namely, that you can have your works printed somewhere at a fairer price than with us, where not everything is so expedited and ready at hand. Yet you will take in good part my zeal, such as it is, to gratify your requests and desires; I wish from my heart that in some other matter I may prove to you my mind most devoted to you.</p><p>As concerns <b>Doctor Bucretius</b>: he greets you lovingly, diligently, and dutifully, and gives great thanks, most courteously, for the letters sent. I bid you hope well <b>both of the Wittich remains and of the papers of that other Astronomer who once lived among us</b>. In both cases he has dealt, and still deals, with the matter with such faith and diligence, and so accurately and assiduously, that I do not doubt we shall have what we wish. He did not wish now to answer your letters, because he trusts he will soon satisfy your wishes in deed; only bear a small delay with an even mind — which I greatly ask you to do.</p><p>To Lord <b>Doctor Fischer</b>, a man certainly most good and most learned, your letter was most welcome; he will give effort in every way to prove to you his faith and diligence by every service he can render. Nor, I hope, will you ever regret your benevolence toward him.</p><p>Concerning the <b>Library of Dudith</b>, which is very well known to me, I would have you believe me: it now hangs, as it were, <b>between heaven and earth</b> — since it is not possessed by Dudith's heirs, but has been, to a certain other party for a fixed sum of money, <b>not so much sold as pawned</b>. It now lies wretched among us in a certain house, and lies no otherwise (for I have seen it several times) than heaps of grain are wont to lie in the houses of the nobles: neither has the <b>buyer</b> full right in it, nor the <b>seller</b>. The matter is plainly entangled, and I <b>fear lest those books take some great and irreparable harm</b>.</p><p>It was lately told me by a certain friend that your <b>Mathematical instruments</b> had been damaged, with no slight loss, by the force of winds and storms in that castle of yours. If it is so, I greatly condole with you; yet I doubt whether it is, since you make no mention of the matter.</p><p>The Magnificent <b>Captain of this city and Duchy, Lord Abraham Jengtius</b> — formerly, while he lived, the patron of our great <b>Wittich</b>, most loving and devoted to you — greets you greatly. He thinks of setting out a little later to the <b>Bohemian baths</b>, and has fully resolved on that journey to meet you, that he may see in person you whom he has long uniquely admired. <b>Doctor Jessenius</b> lately told him of a certain book of yours recently published, which you had offered to the Emperor; he longs uniquely to see it, and has now once and again asked me whether I have seen it. The volume of your <b>Letters</b>, which I have from the gift of your liberality, he first saw at my house, and had with him for several days.</p><p>If you wish to write or send anything to me, know that whatever you send to Prague by sure men, to Lord <b>Johann Myllner</b>, Councillor of the Sacred Imperial Majesty and Supreme Secretary of the Kingdom of Bohemia — my dearest gossip, and to be trusted in all faith — will come most directly to me. For with him lives <b>Johann Paludius</b>, Councillor of my Prince, dwelling at Prague and managing his Highness's affairs at that court; he will both diligently and gladly take care of whatever pertaining to me he receives. Fare well and happily, most excellent Man, and continue to love perpetually me whom you once began to love; so may God in turn care for you and love you.</p><p>Wrocław, 14 June 1600.<br>Most respectful and most loving of Your Magnificence,<br>Jacob Monau.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; occasion.</b> <b>Jacob Monau (Monavius)</b> of <b>Breslau (Wrocław)</b> — this is his <b>reply to Tycho's letter of 16 May 1600 (brahe-t_16000516001)</b>, answering it point by point (and confirming that Monau's own March letter, brahe-t_16000110001's antecedent, had reached Tycho after going astray \"to him whom it had not found at Prague\").</li><li><b>Significance — a major book-provenance letter (the fate of two libraries).</b></li><li><b>The Dudith Library (vivid and important).</b> Monau, \"to whom it is very well known,\" reports that <b>Andreas Dudith's library</b> now \"hangs between heaven and earth\": <b>no longer the heirs', but pawned (not sold)</b> to a third party for a fixed sum, so that <b>neither buyer nor seller has full right</b>. It \"lies wretched in a certain house … as heaps of grain lie in the houses of the nobles\" (Monau has seen it several times), and he <b>fears \"great and irreparable harm\"</b> to the books — a poignant eyewitness account of the dispersal/decay of a famous humanist collection (the purchase-bid of brahe-t_16000516001).</li><li><b>The Wittich remains.</b> Good hope, via <b>Doctor Bucretius</b>, both for the <b>Wittich remains</b> (<i>Wittichianae reliquiae</i> — the dispersed library of Paul Wittich, incl. the annotated Copernicus copies) and for the <b>papers of \"that other Astronomer who once lived among us\"</b> (the unnamed dead Breslau astronomer of brahe-t_16000110001 / brahe-t_16000516001); Bucretius \"is dealing with both with such diligence … that I do not doubt we shall have what we wish.\"</li><li><b>Abraham Jengtius — Wittich's former patron.</b> The <b>Captain (Landeshauptmann) of Breslau and the Duchy, Abraham Jengtius</b>, \"formerly the patron of our great Wittich while he lived,\" sends warm greetings and plans to meet Tycho on a journey to the <b>Bohemian baths</b> (Karlsbad/Teplice); he longs to see the <b>book Tycho lately presented to the Emperor</b> (of which <b>Jessenius</b> had told him). A valuable provenance link (Wittich's patron) for the Wittich-library question.</li><li><b>The storm-damage rumour.</b> Monau passes on a report that Tycho's <b>instruments were storm-damaged at his castle</b> [Benátky], condoling — but doubts it, \"since you make no mention of it.\" (Tycho's silence suggests the rumour was false or exaggerated.)</li><li><b>The relay channel.</b> For letters to Monau: send to Prague to <b>Johann Myllner</b> (imperial councillor and <b>Supreme Secretary of the Kingdom of Bohemia</b>, Monau's <i>compater</i>), with whom lives <b>Johann Paludius</b>, the Silesian Duke's councillor resident at Prague — who will forward to Breslau. A concrete glimpse of the Prague–Breslau correspondence infrastructure.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Monau (sender); <b>Daniel Bucretius</b>; <b>Tobias Fischer</b> (<i>Doctor Fischer</i>); <b>Andreas Dudith</b> (and his heirs); <b>Paul Wittich</b>; <b>Abraham Jengtius</b> (Breslau Captain, Wittich's patron); <b>Johannes Jessenius</b>; <b>Johann Myllner</b> (Bohemian Supreme Secretary); <b>Johann Paludius</b> (Silesian councillor at Prague); the printer (unnamed); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Vratisl. XIIIJ Iunij 1600</i>; Silesia (Breslau) had adopted the <b>Gregorian (New Style)</b> calendar by 1600; marked [N.S.].</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). Latin corrected: removed the interpolated clause 'quod ut facias' (Dreyer VIII p.325), which is absent from the printed text; this does not affect the published English, which already matched the Dreyer sense.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear humanist Latin, read securely; the reply tracks brahe-t_16000516001 point by point (the printer; Bucretius's good progress on the Wittich remains and the dead astronomer's papers; Fischer; the <b>Dudith library \"pawned, not sold,\" at risk of irreparable harm</b>; the instrument-storm rumour; <b>Abraham Jengtius</b> as Wittich's former patron; the Myllner/Paludius relay), and every named party is identified or flagged. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.310-327 (all 18 pages of the slice read as page images in five chunks). The slice is generously over-wide: pp.310-324 and the tail of 326-327 carry ten neighbouring letters (to/from Galileo, Herwartus, Rudolphus Corraducium, Barvitius, Iessenius, Wolframsdorf, an anonymous addressee, Fischerus, Bucretius, Kepler, Rollenhagius), all correctly excluded from source_text. The Monau-to-Tycho letter itself is Dreyer's entry on pp.325-326, headed identically to the packet's source_text salutation. Full Latin text collated: the printer remark, Bucretius and the Wittich papers, Fischerus, the Dudith library 'between heaven and earth' passage (buyer/seller), the storm-damaged-instruments rumour, Abraham Jengtius, Iessenius, the Epistolarum volume, Myllnerus/Paludius/Petkum, and the closing date (14 June 1600) all match Dreyer. Two low-severity TEXT_ERRORs found (an interpolated clause; a near-synonym word substitution); neither changes the sense a scholar would take away.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text inserts the clause 'quod ut facias', which has no counterpart in Dreyer's printed text; the translation follows the padded source and renders the interpolated clause as an extra dash-clause. Dreyer prints: <i>modo parvam moram aequo feras animo, magnopere te rogatum volo</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Source_text reads 'devotissimum' where Dreyer prints 'deditissimum'; both mean approximately 'most devoted' so the sense is unaffected, but it is a literal word-level departure from Dreyer as read from the page image. Dreyer prints: <i>meum animum tibi deditissimum</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The tboo slice (18 pages, TBOO VIII pp.310-327) required reading past several intervening letters (Tycho-Galileo, Tycho-Herwart, Tycho-Corraducius, the Kepler diploma, Tycho-Monau of 16 May, etc.) before locating Monau's 14 June 1600 letter on p.325. That page confirms Dreyer reads 'modo parvam moram aequo feras animo, magnopere te rogatum volo' (no 'quod ut facias'), which is fixed. On the second defect, current_latin was found already to read 'meum animum tibi deditissimum', matching Dreyer exactly - the defect's own cited 'source' excerpt ('devotissimum') was not actually present in current_latin, so no change was made there.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Jacob Monavius", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000516001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000516001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Confirms receipt of Tycho's May letter, concedes that cheaper printing is available elsewhere than Breslau, and relays greetings from Bucretius.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000614001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000619001", "d": "1600-06-19", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Magdeburg", "t": "Georg Rollenhagen → Tycho Brahe — Magdeburg, 9 June 1600 [O.S.] (catalogued 19 June)", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German address) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDLEN EHRNVESTEN VND HOCHGELARTEN HERRN TYCHO BRAHEN AUFF KNUTHSTORFF, S. RÖMISCH KEYSERLICHEN MAYT.ASTROΝΟΜΟAUFF BENATHEN, MEYNEM ... GÜNSTIGEN HERRN.CITISSIME.</p><p>S.</p><p>GENEROSE et doctiss. D. TYCHO, vehementer expectant nostri Canonici tuae Astronomicae suppellectilis avectionem, et pecuniam promtam ad omnia offerunt. Accessit quoque me GRUTZMACERUS Dresdensis nauta, qui promittit se navibus suis Leutmeritium usque omnia recte advecturum. En alius quidam ex nostris, qui idem rogat, ut sibi committatur. Duabus enim navibus se omnia rite repositurum esse. Reliqua enim suppellex nuper Hamburgo quoque advecta est. Quid impediat, scripsi ante Pentecosten, et quia nihil impetro responsi, existimo non esse redditas meas. Quare rogavi hunc meum vicinum, ut has aut ipse redderet, aut conducto Pragae nuncio, cui tu satisfaceres, de tua voluntate sciscitaretur. Est autem haec mora, quod inventarium prioris vecturae ANDREAS vester secum abstulit, ut sine insigni errore nos committere eam nautis prorsus nequeamus. Omnino ergo oportet ANDREAM ipsum redire ad nos, aut aliquem ex domesticis, cui nota sunt externo ab intuitu omnia, et qui ex navi reponat in littore, contra pluvias muniat, tibique postea deferenda curet. Quod peregrinus nec vult nec potest recte curare. Mittas ergo, quam primum potes, qui comes et inspector sit omnium. De me praestabo fidelissime omnia, quae volueris et quae potero.</p><p>B. V.<br>Magdaeburgi 9 Iunij 1600.<br>Tuus<br>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble, honourable, and highly-learned Master Tycho Brahe at Knudstrup, His Roman Imperial Majesty's Astronomer at Benátky, my … favourable lord. With all speed [CITISSIME].</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Generous and most learned Master Tycho, our Canons vehemently await the carrying-off of your astronomical equipment [<i>suppellex</i>], and offer ready money for everything. There also approached me Grützmacher, a Dresden boatman, who promises that he will convey everything rightly by his ships as far as Leitmeritz [Litoměřice]. And behold, another of our [townsmen], who asks the same — that it be entrusted to him, [saying] that with two ships he would duly stow everything. For the rest of the equipment has lately been conveyed from Hamburg too. What is the impediment, I wrote before Pentecost; and because I obtain no answer, I judge that my [letter] was not delivered. Wherefore I have asked this neighbour of mine either to deliver these [present lines] himself, or — a messenger being hired at Prague (whom you would pay) — to inquire about your will. But this is the [cause of] delay: that the inventory of the earlier carriage your Andreas took away with him, so that we cannot at all entrust it to the boatmen without notable error. Altogether, then, it is necessary that Andreas himself return to us, or someone of [your] household to whom everything is known by outward inspection, and who may restow [it] from ship onto the shore, protect [it] against rains, and afterward see to its conveyance to you. Which a stranger neither wishes nor is able rightly to attend to. Send, therefore, as soon as you can, someone to be the companion and overseer of everything. For my part, I will most faithfully perform everything you wish and that I can.</p><p>Fare well.<br>Magdeburg, 9 June 1600.<br>Yours,<br>Georg Rollenhagen.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector of the Magdeburg cathedral school and a celebrated German poet (the <i>Froschmeuseler</i>); here acting as Tycho's logistics agent on the <b>Elbe transport route</b> for his instruments.</li><li><b>The instrument transport.</b> A vivid document of the <b>shipping of Tycho's astronomical equipment</b> (<i>suppellex Astronomica</i>) up the Elbe to Bohemia: the Magdeburg cathedral <b>canons</b> are eager for it to move on (and offer cash); the Dresden boatman <b>Grützmacher</b> (and a rival local) offer to carry it by ship as far as <b>Leitmeritz (Litoměřice)</b>; \"the rest of the equipment has lately been conveyed from Hamburg too.\" The hold-up is that <b>Andreas</b>, Tycho's man, carried off the <b>inventory</b> (<i>inventarium</i>) of the first consignment, so no one can check the freight; Rollenhagen begs Tycho to send Andreas back, or another servant who knows the instruments by sight, to escort and protect them.</li><li><b><i>CITISSIME</b></i> (\"with all speed\") on the address marks urgent dispatch. <i>Canonici</i> = the Magdeburg cathedral canons. The German address-line's <i>ASTRONOMO</i> is printed with Greek letters (ΑΣΤΡΟΝΟΜΟ).</li><li><b>⚠ Date.</b> The dateline reads <i>Magdaeburgi 9 Iunij 1600</i> = <b>9 June 1600</b>; the dataset (slug …0619… / label \"19. juni\") gives <b>19 June</b>. The header follows the <b>as-written 9 June</b> and notes the catalogue's 19 June; the internal \"I wrote before Pentecost\" is consistent with an early-June date. Magdeburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the logistics (the canons, the Elbe boatmen Grützmacher et al., Leitmeritz, the missing inventory, the recall of Andreas) are clear and historically apt for the 1600 transfer of Tycho's instruments to Bohemia. Grade [B] for the dateline-vs-catalogue day discrepancy (9 vs 19 June), flagged and resolved in favour of the text.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.326-328 (page images, read visually); letter proper on p.327, text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Grutzmacher, Andreas), place name (Leitmeritz), and the date (9 June 1600) all correct. No defects found; the self-assigned published_grade of B was unnecessarily conservative given the clean collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000405001", "next": "brahe-t_16000926003", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000405001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000926003"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that the Magdeburg canons are ready to purchase Tycho's astronomical equipment and that two boatmen offer to ship it to Leitmeritz, pressing for a reply.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000619001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000623001", "d": "1600-06-23", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joseph Justus Scaliger — Prague, 23 July 1600 [N.S.] *(duplicate record, mis-dated June in the slug — canonical file is brahe-t_16000723001)*", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek — copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Duplicate-record note (resolved).</b> The corpus carries this single letter under <b>two slugs</b>: `16000623001` (this one, \"23 June\") and `16000723001` (\"23 July\"). The <b>dateline reads <i>die 23 Iulij stylo novo</i> — 23 July</b> — and the July-slug record agrees with it, so <b>`brahe-t_16000723001` is the canonical file</b>; this June-dated record is a <b>doubled dataset entry with an erroneous month</b> (not a separate letter). The earlier \"Iulij vs Iunij copyist-slip\" flag is hereby <b>resolved</b>: it is a duplicated record, not a one-witness reading crux. The full translation is retained below for completeness; it is identical to the canonical file.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM.<br>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>ELAPSAe sunt septimanae, ni fallor, sex, ex quo in arce Caesarea Bennaticha, ubi nunc sedes est habitationis meae, Illustris Dominationis tuae literas acceperim VI Eid. Martias Lugduni Batavorum scriptas, easque non saltem eruditionis eximiae, quâ praepolles, sed et humanitatis et benevolentiae erga me (pro quibus etiam officiosissime gratias habeo) refertissimas. Cum vero nuper a Scra Caesa Mate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, postquam Pilsena, ubi per tres anni quadrantes ob pestem abfuit, in arcem consuetae residentiae huc nuper redijt, evocatus sum, siquidem ipsius Matas de quibusdam ad studia quae tracto pertinentibus clementissime mecum loqui dignabatur (prout semel atque iterum affatim factum est) accidit, quod ante biduum Nobilis et eruditus hic Adolescens NICOLAUS SUCKERBACK de Myssen noviter ex Italia veniens me in aula inter alios ambulantem, Barone quodam indicante, agnosceret et compellaret, seque a Batavia oriundum, atque eo brevi cogitare indicaret, rogans, an quidpiam literarum ad amicos et isthic notos mittere vellem, cumque prae caeteris, an ad te meas dirigere possit, ab eo percontarer, id officij in se recepit. Existimavi autem eum tamdiu hîc mansurum, donec allatis tuis ultimis (de quibus dixi) literis e Bennaticha, ijs secundum singula earundem contenta, quatenus in hac urbe strepitus et interpellationes aulicae ferunt, respondere possem; at is hodie me in hospitio quaerens se circa meridiem hinc abiturum in ijs hominibus significavit, quare ne plane mearum vacuus ad vos rediret, has ieiunas et breviusculas, potius quam nullas ad te exarandas duxi. Quoniam vero literae tuae ad manus non sunt, nonnulla saltem, quae ex ijs recordor, attingam.</p><p>Conceptiones tuas de aequinoctijs et motu octavae Sphaerae aliter salvando, quas promittis, avide exspecto. Stellam Polarem ante plurima secula, quemadmodum reliqua omnia octavae Sphaerae (uti vocant) affixa sidera in eodem coeli loco haerere, facile tibi concessero, modo declinationem et ascensionem rectam, tum quoque longitudinem et nonnihil etiam latitudinem, tam in hac quam in alijs alteratas fatearis. Quod autem id ipsum evenerit, non tam octavae Sphaerae promotione quam aequinoctiorum anticipatione, prout magnus ille COPERNICUS pariter animadvertisse videtur, etiam tibi astipulor. Sed in hoc cardo negotij vertitur, quomodo anticipatio illa intelligenda et exsequenda sit. Per motum axis terreni, prout speculatus est COPERNICUS, reciprocum et liberatum annuaeque restitutioni non plane coincidentem, id evenire frustranea, imo prorsus absurda est assumtio nec apparentijs vel hoc seculo, nedum caeteris omnibus satisfacit. Ego ipsum Solem hanc varietatem efficere statuo, dum aliam atque aliam diversis seculis designat Eclipticam, aliasque eiusdem cum Aequatore intersectiones retrorsum et quidem non admodum aequaliter inducit, prout suo loco latius Deo volente ostensurus sum. Nam et infimam Lunam non multum dissimili ratione suam orbitam singulis mensibus variare depraehendi, ita ut non saltem latitudo eius maxima ad tertiam gradus partem (quanta etiam omnino in Eclipticae obliquitate hactenus comperta est differentia) mutetur, sed et nodi et intersectiones cum Ecliptica, licet antrorsum motu aequali, reciproce tamen et inaequali ratione id fieri satisque notabili discrimine, quod grad. 1¾ attingere queat, cognovi, prout lunaris curriculi restitutio propediem, favente Numine, in Progymnasmatis nostris publicanda plenius patefaciet. Si tibi aliqua ratio a nostra forte diversa innotuit, et ex historijs antiquissimis ratisque observationibus comperta est, quâ haec commode explicari queant, vellem eam mecum communicari. Interim ut dicam id quod res est, assequi non possum, id quod tam in his literis, quam alibi innuisti: Polum nimirum mundi alium esse a Polo aequinoctialis et lineas meridianas moveri. Videtur id enim minus convenire, nisi forte tuam sententiam, quae paulo obscurior est, necdum percipiam, quare hac de re uberius a te instrui cupio.</p><p>Pro MANILIO in integrum a te restituto mihique transmisso plurimas habeo gratias. Perlustravi eundem successivis horis, prout otium tulit, mihique tua industria et perspicacitas in hoc authore a mendis vindicando et expoliendo, quae etiam in plurimis alijs satis explorata est, summopere placuit.</p><p>Plura scriberem, si literae tuae in promptu essent, ulteriorem procul dubio materiam suppeditaturae, modo etiam id ferret huius loci irrequietudo, et is, qui hasce allaturus est, abitum adeo festinanter non acceleraret; quare haec subito effusa Illustris Dominatio tua boni consulat, meque favore suo prosequi pergat, ac per internuncij oportunitatem suis cultissimis literis invisere non intermittat, quaeso.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 23 Iulij stylo novo Anno Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae et vulgatae 1600.<br>Illustris Dominationis tuae<br>studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 330; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Cum has literas ante 6 fere septimanas ad te exarassem, discesserat Batavus ille, qui eas tibi traditurus erat, una vel altera hora citius quam existimassem, ideoque eas huc usque mecum reservare oportuit, donec alia sese offerret occasio eas ad te dirigendi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOSEPH SCALIGER.</b><br>Illustrious and noble Man, most honoured Lord and Friend.</p><p>Six weeks have elapsed, unless I am mistaken, since at the imperial castle of <b>Benátky</b>, where the seat of my dwelling now is, I received Your illustrious Lordship's letter, <b>written at Leiden on the 6th day before the Ides of March [10 March]</b> — most full not only of the eminent erudition in which you excel, but also of humanity and benevolence toward me (for which too I most dutifully give thanks). But since I was lately summoned by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord — after he had lately returned hither, from <b>Pilsen</b> (where for three quarters of a year he was absent on account of the plague), to the castle of his accustomed residence — for His Majesty deigned most clemently to speak with me of certain things pertaining to the studies I pursue (as has been done amply, once and again): it happened that, two days ago, a noble and learned young man here, <b>Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen</b>, newly come from Italy, recognised and accosted me as I walked among others at court (a certain Baron pointing me out), and indicated that he was a native of <b>Holland</b> and meant to go thither shortly, asking whether I wished to send any letters to friends and acquaintances there; and when I inquired of him whether, above all, he could direct mine to you, he took the office upon himself. I had thought he would stay here long enough for me, when your last letter (of which I spoke) was brought from Benátky, to answer it point by point according to its contents, so far as the din and the courtly interruptions of this city allow; but today, seeking me at my lodging, he signified by his servants that he would depart hence about midday — wherefore, lest he return to you quite empty of anything of mine, I judged these meagre and rather brief lines should be written to you, rather than none. And since your letter is not at hand, I will touch at least on a few things I remember from it.</p><p><b>Your conceptions about the equinoxes, and about saving the motion of the eighth Sphere otherwise, which you promise, I eagerly await.</b> That the Pole Star, many ages ago — like all the other fixed stars affixed to the eighth Sphere (as they call it) — stuck in the same place of the heaven, I will readily grant you, provided you confess that its declination and right ascension, and also its longitude, and even somewhat its latitude, were altered, both in this [star] and in the others. And that this very thing came about <b>not so much by an advance of the eighth Sphere as by the anticipation of the equinoxes</b> — as that great man <b>Copernicus</b> likewise seems to have observed — I agree with you too. But on this the hinge of the matter turns: <b>how that anticipation is to be understood and carried out.</b> That it comes about <b>by a motion of the earth's axis</b> (as Copernicus speculated) — reciprocal and librating, and not quite coinciding with the annual restitution — <b>is a vain, nay wholly absurd assumption, and does not satisfy the appearances even of this age, let alone all the rest.</b> <b>I hold that the Sun itself effects this variation</b>, in that in different ages it designates a different and different Ecliptic, and induces different intersections of the same with the Equator, backward, and indeed not quite uniformly — as in its place, God willing, I shall show more fully. For I have detected that <b>the lowest [planet], the Moon, varies its orbit in not very dissimilar fashion every month</b>, so that not only is <b>its greatest latitude changed by a third part of a degree</b> (as much difference as has hitherto been found in the obliquity of the Ecliptic), but I have also learned that <b>the nodes and intersections with the Ecliptic — though by a forward, uniform motion — come about nevertheless reciprocally and by an unequal ratio, and with a notable enough difference, which can reach 1¾°</b> — as the restitution of the lunar course, soon (Heaven favouring) to be published <b>in our <i>Progymnasmata</b></i>, will more fully disclose. If some reasoning, perhaps different from ours, has become known to you — and has been ascertained from the most ancient histories and from reliable observations — by which these things can conveniently be explained, I should wish it communicated to me. Meanwhile, to say the thing as it is, <b>I cannot follow what you hinted both in this letter and elsewhere: namely that the Pole of the world is other than the Pole of the equinoctial, and that the meridian lines move.</b> For that seems less fitting — unless perhaps I do not yet grasp your opinion, which is a little obscure; wherefore I desire to be more amply instructed by you on this matter.</p><p><b>For the Manilius, restored entire by you and transmitted to me, I give many thanks.</b> I have scanned it through in successive hours, as leisure allowed; and your industry and perspicacity in vindicating this author from corruptions and polishing him — well proved in very many others too — pleased me in the highest degree.</p><p>I would write more, were your letter at hand (which would doubtless supply further matter), if only the restlessness of this place allowed it, and if he who is to bring these did not hasten his departure so hurriedly. Wherefore may Your illustrious Lordship take in good part these things suddenly poured out, and continue to pursue me with your favour, and not fail, when a messenger offers, to visit me with your most cultivated letters, I pray.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 23rd day of <b>July</b>, New Style, in the year of the Christian, Dionysian, and common Era 1600.<br>Of Your illustrious Lordship<br>the most zealous,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> When I had written this letter to you nearly six weeks ago, that Hollander who was to deliver it to you had departed one or two hours sooner than I had supposed; and so I was obliged to keep it by me until now, until another occasion should offer itself of directing it to you.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), then at <b>Leiden</b> — the supreme chronologist and classical philologist of the age (cf. the chronology jest at brahe-t_16000327002). The exchange is one of mutual high regard between two giants on contested ground.</li><li><b>Significance — a substantive astronomical letter (precession theory + Tycho's lunar discoveries).</b></li><li><b>Precession / the eighth sphere.</b> Tycho responds to Scaliger's promised \"conceptions about the equinoxes and saving the eighth sphere's motion otherwise.\" He concedes the <b>fixed stars hold their place</b> but insists their <b>declination, right ascension, longitude, and even latitude are altered</b>; agrees with Scaliger (and Copernicus) that the cause is <b>the anticipation of the equinoxes (precession), not an advance of the eighth sphere</b> — but the <i>crux</i> is <b>how</b>.</li><li><b>Rejection of Copernican axis-libration.</b> Tycho dismisses Copernicus's account — precession by a <b>reciprocal, librating motion of the earth's axis</b> — as \"a vain, nay wholly absurd assumption\" that fails the appearances.</li><li><b>Tycho's solar theory of precession.</b> <i>\"I hold that the Sun itself effects this variation,\"</i> designating a different ecliptic in different ages and shifting its intersections with the equator backward, non-uniformly — to be expounded in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</li><li><b>Tycho's lunar discoveries (the genuine novelty).</b> He reports finding that <b>the Moon's orbit varies monthly</b>: its <b>greatest latitude changes by ⅓°</b> (the \"variation of the inclination\"), and the <b>nodes/intersections move reciprocally and unequally, by up to 1¾°</b> — discoveries (the variation of the lunar inclination and the libration of the nodes) to be published in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. A precise, datable statement of Tycho's lunar theory.</li><li><b>The disputed point.</b> Tycho says he <b>cannot follow Scaliger's hint that \"the Pole of the world is other than the Pole of the equinoctial, and that the meridian lines move,\"</b> and asks for fuller instruction.</li><li><b>The Manilius.</b> Thanks for <b>Scaliger's edition of Manilius</b> (the Roman <i>Astronomica</i> — Scaliger's celebrated emended text, 1579/1600), praising his text-critical skill.</li><li><b>The bearer.</b> A chance courier: <b>Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen</b>, a young man newly back from Italy, a native of Holland, who recognised Tycho at court (a Baron pointing him out) and offered to carry letters to Leiden — hence the apologetic haste. Not further identified.</li><li><b>Setting.</b> Tycho writes from <b>Prague</b> (residence at <b>Benátky</b>), the <b>Emperor lately returned from Pilsen</b> (three quarters of a year absent for the plague) and consulting Tycho \"on things pertaining to my studies.\"</li><li><b>⚠ Date discrepancy (protocol §4.1 — lead with the source).</b> The <b>source dateline reads <i>die 23 Iulij stylo novo</b></i> → <b>23 July 1600 [N.S.]</b>, which the header follows. The <b>catalogue/slug date it 23 June</b> (`16000623001`, label \"23. juni n.s.\"). The witness is a <b>1668 copy</b> (Resen's <i>Inscriptiones Hafnienses</i>), where <b><i>Iulij</i>/<i>Iunij</i> is a classic copyist confusion</b>; the internal \"six weeks since I received your 10-March letter\" math is mildly <i>more</i> comfortable with June. The discrepancy is <b>unresolved</b>; led with the as-printed July reading and flagged. To be resolved against Dreyer's apparatus. <i>(Note the precise era formula: \"the Christian, Dionysian, and common Era\" — a courtesy to the chronologist.)</i></li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Joseph Justus Scaliger; <b>Copernicus</b>; <b>Manilius</b> (Scaliger's edition); <b>Nicolaus Suckerback</b> of Meissen (courier); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>, <b>Leiden</b>, <b>Meissen</b>, <b>Italy</b>, <b>Holland</b>.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Delivery-delay postscript, restored from Dreyer VIII p.330. (This file is the 23-June-slugged duplicate of the same Scaliger letter Dreyer dates 23 July; the postscript is shared.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> (on text and substance) — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the astronomical content (the precession debate, the rejection of Copernican axis-libration, <b>Tycho's solar theory of the equinoxes</b>, and his <b>lunar latitude-/node-variation discoveries with the ⅓° and 1¾° figures</b>, all referred forward to the <i>Progymnasmata</i>; the Manilius thanks; the disputed \"moving meridians\") is firmly and carefully construed. The single reservation is the <b>month of the dateline</b> (source \"Iulij\" vs catalogue June), flagged above and not silently resolved.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, including the postscript, against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.328-330 read as page images (the whole letter fits within these three pages; the nominal 5-page slice's remaining page belongs to the next letter). source_text matches Dreyer's print exactly (the Suckerback/Meissen episode, the Copernicus/equinoxes/lunar-node technical passage with its one-third-degree and 1-3/4-degree figures, the Manilius thanks, and the postscript explaining the six-week delay). published_english renders all of it faithfully, including the postscript (present and correctly translated, so the previously-flagged dropped-postscript defect is not repeated in this copy). Note: the packet's own title already flags that the slug's date (23 June) conflicts with the letter's actual dateline of 23 July 1600 — a pre-existing cataloguing/slug mismatch, not a translation defect; source_text and published_english agree with each other and with Dreyer on 23 July. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found in the translation itself.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000310001", "next": "brahe-t_16000723001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000310001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Scaliger for his March letter, reports being summoned to Prague by Emperor Rudolf II, and mentions a chance meeting with Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen, newly arrived from Italy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000623001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000723001", "d": "1600-07-23", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Joseph Justus Scaliger — Prague, 23 July 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek — copy in an exemplar of P. J. Resen,<i> Inscriptiones Hafnienses </i>(Hauniae 1668)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Duplicate-record note.</b> The corpus carries this single letter under <b>two slugs</b>: this one (`16000723001`, \"23 July\") and `16000623001` (\"23 June\"). The <b>dateline reads <i>die 23 Iulij stylo novo</i> — 23 July</b> — so <b>this July-dated record is the canonical one</b>; the June-dated `brahe-t_16000623001` is a <b>doubled dataset record with an erroneous month</b> (not a separate letter). The two share text, witness, and content verbatim. (This resolves the earlier \"Iulij vs Iunij\" flag: it is a duplicated record, not a one-witness copy slip.)</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD IOSEPHUM SCALIGERUM.<br>ILLUSTRIS ET GENEROSE VIR DOMINE ET AMICE HONORANDISSIME.</p><p>ELAPSAe sunt septimanae, ni fallor, sex, ex quo in arce Caesarea Bennaticha, ubi nunc sedes est habitationis meae, Illustris Dominationis tuae literas acceperim VI Eid. Martias Lugduni Batavorum scriptas, easque non saltem eruditionis eximiae, quâ praepolles, sed et humanitatis et benevolentiae erga me (pro quibus etiam officiosissime gratias habeo) refertissimas. Cum vero nuper a Scra Caesa Mate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, postquam Pilsena, ubi per tres anni quadrantes ob pestem abfuit, in arcem consuetae residentiae huc nuper redijt, evocatus sum, siquidem ipsius Matas de quibusdam ad studia quae tracto pertinentibus clementissime mecum loqui dignabatur (prout semel atque iterum affatim factum est) accidit, quod ante biduum Nobilis et eruditus hic Adolescens NICOLAUS SUCKERBACK de Myssen noviter ex Italia veniens me in aula inter alios ambulantem, Barone quodam indicante, agnosceret et compellaret, seque a Batavia oriundum, atque eo brevi cogitare indicaret, rogans, an quidpiam literarum ad amicos et isthic notos mittere vellem, cumque prae caeteris, an ad te meas dirigere possit, ab eo percontarer, id officij in se recepit. Existimavi autem eum tamdiu hîc mansurum, donec allatis tuis ultimis (de quibus dixi) literis e Bennaticha, ijs secundum singula earundem contenta, quatenus in hac urbe strepitus et interpellationes aulicae ferunt, respondere possem; at is hodie me in hospitio quaerens se circa meridiem hinc abiturum in ijs hominibus significavit, quare ne plane mearum vacuus ad vos rediret, has ieiunas et breviusculas, potius quam nullas ad te exarandas duxi. Quoniam vero literae tuae ad manus non sunt, nonnulla saltem, quae ex ijs recordor, attingam.</p><p>Conceptiones tuas de aequinoctijs et motu octavae Sphaerae aliter salvando, quas promittis, avide exspecto. Stellam Polarem ante plurima secula, quemadmodum reliqua omnia octavae Sphaerae (uti vocant) affixa sidera in eodem coeli loco haerere, facile tibi concessero, modo declinationem et ascensionem rectam, tum quoque longitudinem et nonnihil etiam latitudinem, tam in hac quam in alijs alteratas fatearis. Quod autem id ipsum evenerit, non tam octavae Sphaerae promotione quam aequinoctiorum anticipatione, prout magnus ille COPERNICUS pariter animadvertisse videtur, etiam tibi astipulor. Sed in hoc cardo negotij vertitur, quomodo anticipatio illa intelligenda et exsequenda sit. Per motum axis terreni, prout speculatus est COPERNICUS, reciprocum et liberatum annuaeque restitutioni non plane coincidentem, id evenire frustranea, imo prorsus absurda est assumtio nec apparentijs vel hoc seculo, nedum caeteris omnibus satisfacit. Ego ipsum Solem hanc varietatem efficere statuo, dum aliam atque aliam diversis seculis designat Eclipticam, aliasque eiusdem cum Aequatore intersectiones retrorsum et quidem non admodum aequaliter inducit, prout suo loco latius Deo volente ostensurus sum. Nam et infimam Lunam non multum dissimili ratione suam orbitam singulis mensibus variare depraehendi, ita ut non saltem latitudo eius maxima ad tertiam gradus partem (quanta etiam omnino in Eclipticae obliquitate hactenus comperta est differentia) mutetur, sed et nodi et intersectiones cum Ecliptica, licet antrorsum motu aequali, reciproce tamen et inaequali ratione id fieri satisque notabili discrimine, quod grad. 1¾ attingere queat, cognovi, prout lunaris curriculi restitutio propediem, favente Numine, in Progymnasmatis nostris publicanda plenius patefaciet. Si tibi aliqua ratio a nostra forte diversa innotuit, et ex historijs antiquissimis ratisque observationibus comperta est, quâ haec commode explicari queant, vellem eam mecum communicari. Interim ut dicam id quod res est, assequi non possum, id quod tam in his literis, quam alibi innuisti: Polum nimirum mundi alium esse a Polo aequinoctialis et lineas meridianas moveri. Videtur id enim minus convenire, nisi forte tuam sententiam, quae paulo obscurior est, necdum percipiam, quare hac de re uberius a te instrui cupio.</p><p>Pro MANILIO in integrum a te restituto mihique transmisso plurimas habeo gratias. Perlustravi eundem successivis horis, prout otium tulit, mihique tua industria et perspicacitas in hoc authore a mendis vindicando et expoliendo, quae etiam in plurimis alijs satis explorata est, summopere placuit.</p><p>Plura scriberem, si literae tuae in promptu essent, ulteriorem procul dubio materiam suppeditaturae, modo etiam id ferret huius loci irrequietudo, et is, qui hasce allaturus est, abitum adeo festinanter non acceleraret; quare haec subito effusa Illustris Dominatio tua boni consulat, meque favore suo prosequi pergat, ac per internuncij oportunitatem suis cultissimis literis invisere non intermittat, quaeso.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 23 Iulij stylo novo Anno Epochae Christianae Dionysiacae et vulgatae 1600.<br>Illustris Dominationis tuae<br>studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 330; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Cum has literas ante 6 fere septimanas ad te exarassem, discesserat Batavus ille, qui eas tibi traditurus erat, una vel altera hora citius quam existimassem, ideoque eas huc usque mecum reservare oportuit, donec alia sese offerret occasio eas ad te dirigendi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO JOSEPH SCALIGER.</b><br>Illustrious and noble Man, most honoured Lord and Friend.</p><p>Six weeks have elapsed, unless I am mistaken, since at the imperial castle of <b>Benátky</b>, where the seat of my dwelling now is, I received Your illustrious Lordship's letter, <b>written at Leiden on the 6th day before the Ides of March [10 March]</b> — most full not only of the eminent erudition in which you excel, but also of humanity and benevolence toward me (for which too I most dutifully give thanks). But since I was lately summoned by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord — after he had lately returned hither, from <b>Pilsen</b> (where for three quarters of a year he was absent on account of the plague), to the castle of his accustomed residence — for His Majesty deigned most clemently to speak with me of certain things pertaining to the studies I pursue (as has been done amply, once and again): it happened that, two days ago, a noble and learned young man here, <b>Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen</b>, newly come from Italy, recognised and accosted me as I walked among others at court (a certain Baron pointing me out), and indicated that he was a native of <b>Holland</b> and meant to go thither shortly, asking whether I wished to send any letters to friends and acquaintances there; and when I inquired of him whether, above all, he could direct mine to you, he took the office upon himself. I had thought he would stay here long enough for me, when your last letter (of which I spoke) was brought from Benátky, to answer it point by point according to its contents, so far as the din and the courtly interruptions of this city allow; but today, seeking me at my lodging, he signified, in the presence of those men, that he would depart hence about midday — wherefore, lest he return to you quite empty of anything of mine, I judged these meagre and rather brief lines should be written to you, rather than none. And since your letter is not at hand, I will touch at least on a few things I remember from it.</p><p><b>Your conceptions about the equinoxes, and about saving the motion of the eighth Sphere otherwise, which you promise, I eagerly await.</b> That the Pole Star, many ages ago — like all the other fixed stars affixed to the eighth Sphere (as they call it) — stuck in the same place of the heaven, I will readily grant you, provided you confess that its declination and right ascension, and also its longitude, and even somewhat its latitude, were altered, both in this [star] and in the others. And that this very thing came about <b>not so much by an advance of the eighth Sphere as by the anticipation of the equinoxes</b> — as that great man <b>Copernicus</b> likewise seems to have observed — I agree with you too. But on this the hinge of the matter turns: <b>how that anticipation is to be understood and carried out.</b> That it comes about <b>by a motion of the earth's axis</b> (as Copernicus speculated) — reciprocal and librating, and not quite coinciding with the annual restitution — <b>is a vain, nay wholly absurd assumption, and does not satisfy the appearances even of this age, let alone all the rest.</b> <b>I hold that the Sun itself effects this variation</b>, in that in different ages it designates a different and different Ecliptic, and induces different intersections of the same with the Equator, backward, and indeed not quite uniformly — as in its place, God willing, I shall show more fully. For I have detected that <b>the lowest [planet], the Moon, varies its orbit in not very dissimilar fashion every month</b>, so that not only is <b>its greatest latitude changed by a third part of a degree</b> (as much difference as has hitherto been found in the obliquity of the Ecliptic), but I have also learned that <b>the nodes and intersections with the Ecliptic — though by a forward, uniform motion — come about nevertheless reciprocally and by an unequal ratio, and with a notable enough difference, which can reach 1¾°</b> — as the restitution of the lunar course, soon (Heaven favouring) to be published <b>in our <i>Progymnasmata</b></i>, will more fully disclose. If some reasoning, perhaps different from ours, has become known to you — and has been ascertained from the most ancient histories and from reliable observations — by which these things can conveniently be explained, I should wish it communicated to me. Meanwhile, to say the thing as it is, <b>I cannot follow what you hinted both in this letter and elsewhere: namely that the Pole of the world is other than the Pole of the equinoctial, and that the meridian lines move.</b> For that seems less fitting — unless perhaps I do not yet grasp your opinion, which is a little obscure; wherefore I desire to be more amply instructed by you on this matter.</p><p><b>For the Manilius, restored entire by you and transmitted to me, I give many thanks.</b> I have scanned it through in successive hours, as leisure allowed; and your industry and perspicacity in vindicating this author from corruptions and polishing him — well proved in very many others too — pleased me in the highest degree.</p><p>I would write more, were your letter at hand (which would doubtless supply further matter), if only the restlessness of this place allowed it, and if he who is to bring these did not hasten his departure so hurriedly. Wherefore may Your illustrious Lordship take in good part these things suddenly poured out, and continue to pursue me with your favour, and not fail, when a messenger offers, to visit me with your most cultivated letters, I pray.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 23rd day of July, New Style, in the year of the Christian, Dionysian, and common Era 1600.<br>Of Your illustrious Lordship<br>the most zealous,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> When I had written this letter to you nearly six weeks ago, that Hollander who was to deliver it to you had departed one or two hours sooner than I had supposed; and so I was obliged to keep it by me until now, until another occasion should offer itself of directing it to you.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b> (1540–1609), then at <b>Leiden</b> — the supreme chronologist and classical philologist of the age (cf. the chronology jest at brahe-t_16000327002). One of two giants meeting on contested astronomical ground.</li><li><b>Significance — a substantive astronomical letter (precession theory + Tycho's lunar discoveries).</b></li><li><b>Precession / the eighth sphere.</b> Tycho concedes the <b>fixed stars hold their place</b> but insists their <b>declination, right ascension, longitude, and even latitude are altered</b>; agrees with Scaliger (and Copernicus) that the cause is <b>the anticipation of the equinoxes (precession), not an advance of the eighth sphere</b> — the <i>crux</i> being <b>how</b>.</li><li><b>Rejection of Copernican axis-libration.</b> Tycho dismisses precession by a <b>reciprocal, librating motion of the earth's axis</b> as \"a vain, nay wholly absurd assumption.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's solar theory of precession.</b> <i>\"I hold that the Sun itself effects this variation,\"</i> designating a different ecliptic in different ages and shifting its equator-intersections backward, non-uniformly — to be expounded in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>.</li><li><b>Tycho's lunar discoveries.</b> The Moon's orbit varies monthly: <b>greatest latitude changes by ⅓°</b> (variation of the inclination) and the <b>nodes move reciprocally and unequally by up to 1¾°</b> — to be published in the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. A precise, datable statement of Tycho's lunar theory.</li><li><b>The disputed point.</b> Tycho <b>cannot follow Scaliger's hint that \"the Pole of the world is other than the Pole of the equinoctial, and the meridian lines move,\"</b> and asks for fuller instruction.</li><li><b>The Manilius.</b> Thanks for <b>Scaliger's edition of Manilius</b> (the Roman <i>Astronomica</i>), praising his text-critical skill.</li><li><b>The bearer.</b> A chance courier, <b>Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen</b>, lately back from Italy, a native of Holland, who recognised Tycho at court and offered to carry letters to Leiden — hence the apologetic haste. Not further identified.</li><li><b>Setting.</b> Written from <b>Prague</b> (residence at <b>Benátky</b>), the <b>Emperor lately returned from Pilsen</b> (absent three quarters of a year for the plague) and consulting Tycho.</li><li><b>Date.</b> <i>die 23 Iulij stylo novo … 1600</i> → <b>23 July 1600 [N.S.]</b> (slug, label, and dateline all agree). Note the precise era formula \"the Christian, Dionysian, and common Era\" — a courtesy to the chronologist. <i>(See the duplicate-record note above: the June-dated `16000623001` is the same letter mis-dated.)</i></li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Joseph Justus Scaliger; <b>Copernicus</b>; <b>Manilius</b> (Scaliger's edition); <b>Nicolaus Suckerback</b> of Meissen (courier); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>, <b>Leiden</b>, <b>Meissen</b>, <b>Italy</b>, <b>Holland</b>.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Delivery-delay postscript (the Hollander courier left early; the letter held six weeks), restored from Dreyer VIII p.330 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the English rendering of 'in ijs hominibus significavit': Suckerback himself signified his midday departure 'in the presence of those men' (unnamed bystanders), not 'by his servants' as previously translated, which had invented an agent not present in the Latin (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.328).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the astronomical content (the precession debate, the rejection of Copernican axis-libration, <b>Tycho's solar theory of the equinoxes</b>, his <b>lunar latitude-/node-variation discoveries with the ⅓° and 1¾° figures</b>, all referred forward to the <i>Progymnasmata</i>; the Manilius thanks; the disputed \"moving meridians\") is firmly construed. Date secure (this canonical July record); the doubled June record is reconciled in the note above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address, body, closing, and the restored postscript) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.328-330, page images only (no text-layer/OCR use). Source_text matches Dreyer's print word-for-word, including the postscript on p.330 and the numeral 'grad. 1¾' — no TEXT_ERROR. Every proper name (Bennaticha/Benátky, Nicolaus Suckerback de Myssen, Copernicus), date (VI Eid. Martias=10 March, 23 Iulij 1600, 'ante 6 fere septimanas'), and number (sex septimanae, tres anni quadrantes, biduum, grad. 1¾, tertia gradus pars) checked and correct. One low-severity interpretive overreach found (see defects); nothing that changes the letter's scholarly content.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). \"in ijs hominibus\" (among/before those people) is rendered as \"by his servants\", inventing a specific agent (servants) not established anywhere in the Latin; Suckerback is the one signifying, not unnamed servants acting for him. At: “he signified by his servants that he would depart hence about midday”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the clause directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.328): the Latin 'at is hodie me in hospitio quaerens se circa meridiem hinc abiturum in ijs hominibus significavit' matches our source text exactly, so this is a translation-only defect, not a text corruption. Fixed the English only. No remaining crux.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Det Kongelige Bibliotek. Afskrift i et eksemplar af P.J. Resen, Inscriptiones Hafnienses, Hauniae 1668"], "th": {"label": "Joseph Justus Scaliger", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000623001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000310001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Scaliger for his March letter, reports being summoned to Prague by Emperor Rudolf II, and mentions a chance meeting with Nicolaus Suckerback of Meissen, newly arrived from Italy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000723001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000726001", "d": "1600-07-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "An anonymous lady", "ck": "An anonymous lady", "ck2": null, "g": "B", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → [an unnamed lady (\"Frau Schwägerin\")] — Prague, 26 July 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (family / domestic) · Edition of record:<i> Dreyer,</i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia <i>(vol. XIV,</i> Epistolae et Acta<i>, p. 208) </i>· MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 132 (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD ANONYMAM].</p><p>EDLE viel ehr- und tugentsame insonders gönstige vielgeliebte fraw schwegerin, derselben sein vnsere sämptliche freundtwillige dienste vnd alles gutten zuvor. Vnd kan deroselben hiemit freundtlichen nitt pergen, das vns der ochße, so der herr schuager zu vnserer notturfft aingekaufft, vns wohl zu handen kommen. Thuen vns dero darumb gehabten mühe zum höchsten vnd fleißigsten bedancken, wollens auch vff alle vorfallende gelegenheit eußerstes vermögs nach zu verschulden nitt vnterlaßen.</p><p>Was nuhn das verhörte gutt betrifft, das der herr schuager vnd fraw schuegerin vns zu gefallen in bestallung haben, deran geschiht vns gleichßfals zum höhesten ahngenehmsten gefallen. Wollen auch gegen gemeldte zait, alsnemblich vmb Bartholomei, daßelbe zu besehen ainen von vns abfertigen, allerdinges hierumb vnd schließlichen beschaid zu macchen.</p><p>Für die bieren thuen wir vns gleichßfals zum höchsten bedancken.</p><p>Vns hiemit göttlicher almacht getreulich entpfhelendt.</p><p>Jn eill, Prag den 26. julii a:o 1600</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 209; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Der poti ist seines pottenlohns allerdinges entrichtet.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to an unnamed lady.]</b></p><p>Noble, very honourable and virtuous, especially favourable, dearly-beloved lady sister-in-law: to her be all our friendly-willing services and all good, beforehand. And we cannot herewith, in friendly wise, conceal from her that the ox, which the Herr Schwager [brother-in-law] bought for our need, has come well to our hands. We thank her most highly and diligently for the trouble had therein, and will not fail to requite it, to the utmost of our ability, on every occasion that may arise.</p><p>Now, as concerns the property under discussion, which the Herr Schwager and the Frau Schwägerin hold in charge [under arrangement] to oblige us — that too is to us a most agreeable pleasure of the highest sort. And toward the said time — namely, about [the feast of] St Bartholomew — we will dispatch one of our [people] to inspect the same, in order to settle this matter altogether and conclusively.</p><p>For the pears we likewise give our highest thanks.</p><p>Herewith faithfully commending ourselves to God's Almighty [protection].</p><p>In haste, Prague, the 26th of July, in the year 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> The messenger has in all respects been paid his messenger's-fee.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> A short <b>family/domestic letter</b> (a draft, <i>Kladde</i>, in the Basel autograph collection), with no recipient named — the dataset titles it <i>ad anonymam</i>, \"to an unnamed [woman].\" She is addressed as <i>Frau Schwägerin</i> (lady sister-in-law / kinswoman by marriage); her husband is the <i>Herr Schwager</i> (brother-in-law).</li><li><b>Content.</b> Wholly domestic: Tycho acknowledges receipt of an <b>ox</b> bought for him by the brother-in-law; thanks for <b>pears</b>; and a property/estate matter (<i>das verhörte gut … in Bestallung</i>) that the couple are managing on his behalf, which he will send someone to inspect <b>around St Bartholomew's Day (24 August)</b>.</li><li><b><i>verhörte gut</b></i> — <b>flagged</b>: literally \"the heard-of / discussed property,\" i.e. the estate or goods previously spoken of and now \"in Bestallung\" (under arrangement/commission). The exact referent is not recoverable from this isolated draft; rendered \"the property under discussion.\" <i>Bestallung</i> here = arrangement/charge, not \"appointment.\"</li><li><b>Register.</b> Family German in the courteous first-person plural (<i>wir/uns/unsere</i>); the cascade of honorifics (<i>Edle, ehr- und tugendsame, gönstige, vielgeliebte</i>) is the standard address to a gentlewoman. Preserved.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Prag den 26. julii a:o 1600</i> = <b>26 July 1600</b>; Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> One-line closing postscript, restored from Dreyer XIV, p. 209 (dropped after the dateline \"Jn eill, Prag den 26. julii a:o 1600\").</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the domestic German is rendered securely (the ox, the pears, the St Bartholomew inspection); grade [B] only because the referent of <i>das verhörte gut … in Bestallung</i> (which property, and what arrangement) cannot be fixed from this isolated draft and is <b>flagged</b>, not smoothed into a false specificity.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. tboo is null for this letter -- no Dreyer slice exists to collate against, so grade is capped at B regardless of quality per the brief. Checked published_english clause-by-clause against source_text, including the postscript (itself annotated in source_text as restored from Dreyer XIV p.209 by a prior pass -- that annotation implies Dreyer coverage does exist for this letter even though no slice was cut here; worth wiring up upstream). Found no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation: the ox, the property held 'in bestallung', the St Bartholomew timing, and the postscript's messenger's-fee are all rendered accurately. One word, 'bieren' (beer, or a spelling of pears?), is ambiguous in the German and could not be resolved without Dreyer; flagged as uncertain, not scored as a defect since there is no basis to call the translator's 'pears' wrong.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, autographsammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 132. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks the recipient for an ox procured on his behalf, acknowledges property arrangements in their charge, and promises to send someone to inspect around St. Bartholomew's.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000726001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000727001", "d": "1600-07-27", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Berka Zbyněk, archbishop of Prague", "ck": "Berka Zbyněk, archbishop of Prague", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Zbyněk Berka of Dubá, Archbishop of Prague → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 27 July 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Prague, Archbishop's Archive (draft / Kladde)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILI AC CLARISSIMO AMICO NOSTRO HONORANDO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE.<br>Nobilis et clarissime Domine.</p><p>QUIS typographus scriptum hoc cuius exemplar nobis D.V. communicauit et cuius authorem se Nicolaus Reimarus Vrsus Dietmarsus esse fatetur absque consensu nostro in hac urbe impresserit, scire non possumus. Sed ipsum inuestigari et, si repertus fuerit, ut alijs in exemplum debito modo in eundem animadvertatur et exemplaria, si quae penes eundem sunt, auferantur curabimus. Cum autem praememoratus Vrsus sub jurisdictionem illustrissimi d. mareschalci aulici pertineat, poterit apud ipsum D. V:ra instare ac sollicitare, quo eidem Vrso ex officio isto demandetur, ut et typographum indicet et exemplaria, si quae habet, exhibeat. Nos qua ratione a nobis fieri potest ac debet rem iuvare non intermittemus.</p><p>Datum Pragae in nostra archiepiscopali residentia, die 27 julii a:o MDC.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To our noble and most distinguished friend, to be honoured, Master Tycho Brahe.</b><br>Noble and most distinguished Lord.</p><p>Which printer printed in this city, without our consent, this writing — a copy of which Your Lordship has communicated to us, and whose author <b>Nicolaus Reimarus Ursus the Dithmarsian</b> confesses himself to be — we cannot know. But we shall see to it that he himself be investigated, and, if he be found, that he be punished in due manner, as an example to others, and that the copies, if any are in his keeping, be taken away. But since the aforementioned Ursus pertains to the jurisdiction of the most illustrious Lord Court-Marshal, Your Lordship will be able to press and solicit with him, so that it may be enjoined upon that Ursus, from that office, both to indicate the printer and to produce the copies, if he has any. We, in what manner it can and ought to be done by us, will not fail to aid the matter.</p><p>Given at Prague, in our archiepiscopal residence, on the 27th day of July, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Zbyněk Berka of Dubá</b> (Zbyněk Berka z Dubé, 1551–1606), <b>Archbishop of Prague</b> (1592–1606) — the <i>Berka Zbyněk</i> of the dataset.</li><li><b>Subject — the Ursus affair.</b> <i>Nicolaus Reimarus Ursus Dietmarsus</i> = <b>Nicolai Reimers Bär</b>, called <b>Ursus</b>, of Dithmarschen (c. 1551–1600), Imperial Mathematician at Prague and Tycho's bitter adversary, whom Tycho accused of plagiarising his geoheliocentric world-system. The \"writing\" (<i>scriptum</i>) is the <b>defamatory anti-Tycho tract</b> (the <i>Tractatus</i> / <i>De astronomicis hypothesibus … defensio</i>), printed at Prague; Tycho had sent the Archbishop a copy and was pursuing its <b>suppression</b>. The Archbishop disclaims knowledge of the printer, promises to investigate and confiscate, and directs Tycho to the secular authority that actually has jurisdiction over Ursus.</li><li><b><i>mareschalcus aulicus</b></i> = the <b>Court Marshal</b> (<i>Obersthofmarschall</i>), the imperial-court officer under whose jurisdiction Ursus (as an imperial servant) fell; the Archbishop's ecclesiastical authority could not reach him directly.</li><li><b>Witness.</b> The sole witness is the <b>draft (Kladde)</b> in the <b>Archbishop's Archive at Prague</b> — i.e. the chancery retained copy, hence the editorial first-person plural (<i>nos … curabimus</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>die 27 julii MDC</i> = <b>27 July 1600</b>; Prague (imperial / Catholic) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Latin is secure and the affair (Ursus's tract, Tycho's drive to suppress it, the Court-Marshal jurisdiction) is fully corpus-corroborated; no ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV p.209 page image in full. Collated the printer-investigation clause, the Ursus/Nicolaus Reimarus Ursus Dietmarsus identification, the jurisdiction-of-the-Court-Marshal clause, the request to press Ursus for the printer's name and copies, and the Prague/27 July 1600 (MDC) date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade A is upheld.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Aerkebiskoppens arkiv i Prag. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that the printer of Ursus's unauthorized writing is unknown, promises investigation and punishment, and advises Tycho to press the Lord Court-Marshal, under whose jurisdiction Ursus falls.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000727001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000802001", "d": "1600-08-02", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ottavio Caurian", "ck": "Ottavio Caurian", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Ottavio Cauriani → Tycho Brahe — Prague, 2 August 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Italian signature) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>OTTAVIO CAURIAN AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>HERR FRIDERICH ROSENKRANTZ hat sich bei Jr. Fürstl. Durchl. Erzherzog MATHIAS zu Osterreich mit Irer Kay. Mt. Interceßion, ertzeiget vnd eingestelt, vnd alberait von Ir Durchl. zuesag bekhommen, vnnd derselben aufwarter nach seinem begern vnndergestellt vnnd vnnderhaltten zuwerden. Weiln aber Ir Mt. Resolution wegen Irer Durchl. veldtzug etwas spät hinauß geschicht worden, vnnd entzwischen herr SEYFRIDT VON KHOLONITSCH, obrister yber 1000 Pferdt, fortgetzogen, so hat Er mit Irer Durchl. vorwissen vnnd gnedigsten bewilligung, sich vnnder denselben gestelt, vnnd ist ietziger zeit vor Papa.</p><p>Das deme also sei, hab Ich OTTAUIO CAURIANI, dem Herrn TYCHONI BRAHE, so mich derwegen alhie zu Praag ersuecht, vnnd hieuon wiffenschafft begert, deß zu zeugnuß mein Handschrifft hierundergestelt.</p><p>Datum Praag Stilo nouo den 2. Augusti Anno 1600.<br>OTTAUIO CAURIAN,<br>manu propria subscripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Ottavio Cauriani to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Herr Friedrich Rosenkrantz presented and introduced himself to His Princely Highness Archduke Matthias of Austria, with the intercession of His Imperial Majesty, and has already received from His Highness the promise to be admitted and maintained, according to his desire, as one of His [Highness's] attendants. But since His Majesty's resolution concerning His Highness's campaign was sent out somewhat late, and in the meantime Herr Seyfried von Kollonitsch, colonel over 1000 horse, had moved off, he [Rosenkrantz] — with His Highness's foreknowledge and most gracious consent — placed himself under that same [Kollonitsch], and is at present before Pápa.</p><p>That this is so, I, Ottavio Cauriani, have set my signature hereunder, as testimony, for Herr Tycho Brahe — who sought me out here at Prague on that account, and desired knowledge of it.</p><p>Given at Prague, New Style, the 2nd of August, in the year 1600.<br>Ottavio Cauriani,<br>I have subscribed with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> A signed <b>attestation / certificate</b> (<i>Zeugnis</i>), furnished at Tycho's request, documenting the whereabouts and military engagement of <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b>.</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ottavio Cauriani</b> (Caurian), an Italian secretary/agent at the Imperial court in Prague; he certifies the matter \"with his own hand\" (the Latin subscription <i>manu propria subscripsi</i> closing a German body).</li><li><b>Subject.</b> <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b> (1571–1602), the Danish nobleman who, after a notorious scandal in Denmark, entered Imperial military service against the Ottomans. Through the <b>intercession of Emperor Rudolf II</b> he was promised a place among the attendants of <b>Archduke Matthias</b> (1557–1619, the Emperor's brother and commander on the Hungarian front); but, the formal resolution arriving late and the cavalry of colonel <b>Seyfried von Kollonitsch</b> (\"over 100 horse\") having already marched, he attached himself to Kollonitsch's troop and is now <b>before Pápa</b> (the Hungarian fortress, scene of the 1600 siege/mutiny). Tycho — Rosenkrantz's kinsman — evidently sought this certificate to document the young man's honourable service.</li><li><b>Register.</b> Imperial chancery German (<i>Ihr Fürstl. Durchl.</i>, <i>Ihr Kay. Mt.</i>); titulature preserved. <i>aufwarter</i> = attendant/courtier; <i>obrister yber 100 Pferdt</i> = colonel commanding 100 horse; <i>veldtzug</i> = (military) campaign.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Stilo novo … 2. Augusti 1600</i> = <b>2 August 1600, New Style</b> (explicit; Prague, imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 330) reads <i>obrister yber <b>1000</b> Pferdt</i> — colonel over <b>1000</b> horse (the working transcription dropped a zero). Corrected in Source and translation.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the dropped zero in the troop count 'obrister yber 1000 Pferdt' (source_text had '100 Pferdt'), per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.330. The published English already correctly read '1000 horse'; only the German source_text required correction.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is secure, and the persons and events (Friedrich Rosenkrantz, Archduke Matthias, Kollonitsch, the Pápa front) are corpus- and history-corroborated. No ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.330 page image in full. Collated the Rosenkrantz/Archduke Matthias presentation, the Kollonitsch troop-strength clause, the placing-himself-under-Kollonitsch clause, the 'before Papa [Pápa]' location, Cauriani's attestation formula, and the Prague/2 August 1600 New Style date against source_text and published_english. One TEXT_ERROR found: source_text's '100 Pferdt' departs from Dreyer's '1000 Pferdt' — a genuine order-of-magnitude numeric corruption in the source, though the published translation itself independently carries the correct figure. Per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C; graded SERIOUS because a tenfold troop-count error is exactly the kind of number a scholar citing the source_text would get wrong. Everything else in the letter — names, dates, place, formulae — matches exactly; no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Our source_text prints '100 Pferdt' (a colonel over 100 horse) where Dreyer's page clearly prints '1000 Pferdt' (over 1000 horse) — a dropped zero, an order-of-magnitude discrepancy in a troop count. Oddly the published_english already reads '1000 horse', matching Dreyer rather than the erroneous source_text supplied in this packet, so the translation step is not at fault; the corruption sits in source_text alone, which is still a defect against the edition of record and worth flagging as such. Dreyer prints: <i>herr SEYFRIDT VON KHOLONITSCH, obrister yber 1000 Pferdt</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.330; '1000 Pferdt' is unambiguous in the page image. Confirmed the English translation already matched Dreyer's figure and required no change, contrary to the brief's general default expectation that both layers need checking.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Certifies that Friedrich Rosenkrantz has been promised a place among Archduke Matthias's attendants and is currently serving under Colonel Kollonitsch before Pápa.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000802001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000803001", "d": "1600-08-03", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Benátky", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Benátky [nad Jizerou], 3 June 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium (afskrift/copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> 38 words missing from inside this letter — dropped by a defective ingest of the source — have been restored from the text of record and translated into place.</p><p><b>Date note.</b> Source dateline reads <i>die 3. Iunij Anno 1600</i> = <b>3 June 1600 [N.S.]</b> (header follows it). The catalogue label carries an ambiguous double \"<i>3. juni n.s. / 3. august n.s.</i>\" — the <b>June</b> reading is correct per the text (and the slug's `0803` \"August\" is an error; the internal evidence — Kepler \"here a third of a year,\" Friedrich \"came a month ago\" — fits early June). See Apparatus.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO VIRO, TAM GENERIS<br>QUAM ERUDITIONIS CLARITATE PRÆEMINENTE, DOMINO OLIGERO ROSÆKRANTZIO DE ROSENHOLM,<br>AMICO, AFFINI ET CONSANGVINEO LONGE CHARISSIMO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>EXOPTATISSIMAS tuas diuque desideratas, Amantissime OLIGERE, iam tandem accepi literas,<br>quibus ab initio tarditatem in rescribendo excusas, idque ijs rationibus, quæ<br>apud me, alias de te candide sentientem, facile locum inveniunt. Postea de rerum<br>mearum conditione, quam Divina Clementia hîc apud Augustissimum Imperatorem RUDOLPHUM<br>Secundum mihi indulsit, et qualiter ea, ad alios a te relata, ijs<br>placuerit, tibi vero et intimis amicis inprimis auditu iucundissima fuerit,<br>multa commemoras, quæ sincerum tuum erga me affectum, de quo etiam antea nunquam<br>dubitavi, affatim testantur, pro quo constanti erga me amore gratias tibi, e<br>pectore tui vicissim studiosissimo profectas, ago quam possum maximas. Res meæ<br>adhuc in eodem statu sunt, quo versabantur, cum ultimas tibi scriberem ; neque<br>enim ob absentiam Cæsaris, qui etiamnum<br>Pilsenæ, urbe 15 milliaribus hinc remota,<br>commoratur, quidpiam, quod momentum haberet, incrementi interea sumere poterant,<br>quod tamen in posterum futurum spero. Nam Cæsarea<br>Maiestas de clementissimo erga me favore, quod Deo acceptum fero,<br>nihil remittit, sed indies eundem adauget; optime etiam mihi cupiunt ditionis<br>huius Primates quique, adeo ut in Regni indigenam, quo omnibus eorum gaudeam<br>Privilegijs, me adoptaturos, sponte obtulerint. Sed de his nunc plura non addo,<br>suo forte tempore uberius eiuscemodi indicaturus.</p><p>Discessit heri primum a me FRIDERICUS ROSENKRANTZIUS, tuus Patruelis, meusque<br>consangvineus, qui ante mensem huc venit; doleo animitus, et quis non doleret<br>optimi illius iuvenis viri infelicissimum casum, atque<br>inconstantissimæ fortunæ minus exspectatam calamitatem? Ego non saltem, quod a<br>te mihi tam accurate commendaretur (licet et hoc plurimum semper apud me<br>ponderis habere debeat) sed quoque ob sangvinis, quod nobis intercedit,<br>vinculum, quantum in me erat, et prout ipsemet voluit, eius adversitatibus, si<br>plane ijs mederi non possem, lenimentum saltem addere diligenter laboravi; quod<br>ut promptius exequerer, author illi fui, ut Pilsenam, in aulam Cæsaris, se conferret, dedique cum eo literas ad Amplissimum Imperij<br>Procancellarium Illustrem et Magnificum Dominum CORRADUCIUM, quibus eum rogabam, ut intercessorias a Cæsarea Maiestate ad Serenissimum Archiducem<br>MATTHIAM Fratrem, qui belli<br>Ungarici Summæ nunc præest, huic<br>ROSENKRANTZIO impetrare<br>vellet, quod et expectatione nostra citius obtentum est, ipsemet Domino<br>CORRADUCIO (qui mihi plurimum favet, et<br>quidvis in mei gratiam expedire non detrectat) negotium hoc disponente et<br>promovente; addidit et ipsemet sponte sua privatim alias ad generosum quendam<br>Baronem in Aula Archiducis sibi familiariter notum literas, non parum quoque<br>FRIDERICO profuturas; quin<br>et ego, ut meum etiam qualemcunque adiungerem calculum, ipsi Serenissimo<br>Archiduci humilime scripsi, et<br>Consangvinei nostri desideria apud ipsius Celsitnem pro meo<br>modulo adiuvare non intentatum reliqui, insinuando pauculis eius in Patria<br>infortunium; sic enim non saltem mihi, sed et FRIDERICO consultum videbatur, quo<br>præveniremus alios, qui sugillando malum sforte odiosius exasperarent, et sic rem magis suspectam<br>redderent. Misi simul ipsius Celini duos libros ex meis,<br>similes per omnia ijs, qui Regi vestro<br>oblati sunt; et unâ globum quendam, ex nostra stellarum verificatione, affabre<br>Alchmariæ in Batavia elaboratum, ut his Munusculis<br>Principis clementem benevolentiam, qua FRIDERICI nostri votis eo lubentius annueret, promptius captare<br>liceret. Harum vero omnium, de quibus iam dixi, literarum exempla, et simul<br>ipsius FRIDERICI relationem,<br>quam huc Pilsena reversus sua manu<br>consignavit, pariterque eiusdem ad te Epistolam hîc apposui, ut ex ijs lectis<br>promptius rei peractæ circumstantias haurire queas. Concipio autem non dubiam<br>spem, FRIDERICUM nostrum hisce<br>fultum medijs, propositum suum apud Inclytum Archiducem satis feliciter peracturum, præsertim quoad externa<br>illa et Politica desideria, quæ in suo relinquo favore; trahit sua quemque<br>voluptas, interdum necessitas urget ea acceptare, quæ alias minus curanda<br>forent. Sed unus adhuc intrinsecus latet scrupulus, qui non minimum habet<br>momenti, et cætera omnia remorari, vel etiam irrita reddere, aut prorsus<br>præscindere, et a tot huius miseræ vitæ adversitatibus atque molestijs eum plane<br>vindicare possit. Laborat enim, et quidem gravius, quam ipsemet persentiscere<br>videtur, pulmonis non leviter vitiati affectibus, adeo ut Asthmaticus evadat,<br>paulatimque Phthisi contabescat, nisi, Dei gratia, mature idoneis medicamentis<br>(quæ tantis malis præsenter succurrentia non facile<br>inveniuntur) valetudinem recuperârit, ad quam adipiscendam ea vitæ ratio, ea<br>regionis et victus conditio, quam ille nunc affectat, obstare potius quam<br>conducere, metuendum est. Verum et hac in parte non saltem eum præmonui, sed et<br>consilio atque medicamentorum auxilio, quatenus nunc in promptu habui, ipsi<br>præsto fui, rationemque præscripsi, quomodo in posterum plura paranda sint, quæ<br>huic, cui obnoxius est, valetudinis incommodo, mea sententia, quantum Deus<br>dederit, succurrant; sicque eum Viennam heri,<br>uti dixi, tendentem, fausta quæque illi precatus, dimisi, adiuncto ipsi quodam<br>Magistro, qui IOHANNES KEPLERUS appellatur,<br>Mathematum egregie perito, qui per anni trientem, e Styria adveniens, hîc mecum fuit, et compositis isthic rebus<br>suis reverti constituit. Is siquidem, cum Viennam in reditu transire debeat, et hominum ac viarum in hoc<br>itinere gnarus sit, FRIDERICO<br>utilis comes esse poterit. Cæterum quemadmodum Epistolam tuam ex re grata et<br>placente circa rerum nostrarum in melius, divina ope, mutationem , non sine gaudibundo applausu inchoasti, et postea in medio tristia et<br>turbulenta quædam minusque arridentia de calamitate communis nostri sangvine<br>iuncti interposuisti, sic tandem in fine Catastrophen lætam et apprime auditu<br>iucundam superinducis, ut comediam quandam repræsentare videaris. Nam quod<br>postremo refers (ut priora, de quibus nunc satis<br>dictum arbitror, relinquam) de charissima tua SOPHIA, mea nepte, Deum ipsam fructu<br>uteri benedixisse, et tunc partui vicinam fuisse, quam etiam feliciter enixam<br>FRIDERICUS retulit, hæc<br>inquam me magna voluptate affecerunt, et auditu gratissima fuere. Deus Opt. Max.<br>hanc, quam vobis dedit, filiolam sospitem conservare et a periculis tueri, ut,<br>ætate accrescens, utrique Parenti gaudio sit, pro sua bonitate dignetur. Cui<br>etiam æviterno et triuno Numini te tuamque coniugem, unâque sobolem fœmellam,<br>toto pectore commendatum habeo.</p><p>Vale.</p><p>Dabantur ex arce<br>Cæsarea Benaticka, die 3. Iunij Anno 1600.</p><p>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p><p>Addidi carmina quædam, hisce diebus a PAULO<br>MELISSO, Poëtarum Germaniæ<br>antesignano, mihi missa, unâ cum exemplo literarum eius, ut scias, qua occasione<br>ea consignaret. Et quia Odæ CRAMERI tui iuxta<br>calcem mentionem facit, apposui et hanc. Si otium descriptionem patietur,<br>adiungam quoque exemplum literarum Domini IOSEPHI<br>SCALIGERI, quæ etiam nuperrime mihi redditæ sunt. Hæc ubi<br>perlegeris, cum Domino Doctore NICOLAO,<br>communi nostro amico, communices, velim.</p><p>S. Dilecte consangvinee, cum post peractam victoriam atque trophæum<br>receptum des instantiam minus validam, iuxta illud: qui volet ingenio cedere  nullus erit, non habeo aliud, quod respondeam, quam aurum apud<br>me esse, quod ne HERCULES quidem suo clavo extorserit, nedum ut tam invalidis<br>argumentis cedat .</p><p>Vale et cave, ne imposterum deponas cum ijs, qui rem geometrice<br>metiuntur.</p><p>Datæ ut supra in tuis literis.</p><p>T. B.</p><p>POSTSCRIPTA.CUM has literas iam antea ad te exarassem, paulo postquam a me<br>discesserat FRIDERICUS<br>ROSENKRANTZIUS, elapsis iam mensibus duobus, eas mecum interea<br>reservavi, donec certa et tuta mittendi occasio suppeteret, quam nunc per M.<br>CHRISTIERNUM LONGOMONTANUM, qui<br>aliquot annos mihi fuit domesticus, in Daniam redeuntem (quem, si is tua opera indiguerit, ut tibi ex<br>me commendatum habeas et ad potiora promoveas, quæso) commode nactus, non<br>diutius eas unâ cum adiunctis, quarum mentionem faciunt, mecum retinendas duxi.<br>Versor nunc Pragæ, quo ante mensem a Sacra<br>Cæsaris Maiestate Domino Nostro<br>Clementissimo, statim ac Pilsena, ubi per<br>tres anni quadrantes ob pestem abfuit, in arcem solitæ residentiæ redierat, huc<br>evocatus; dignabatur enim Eius Maiestas<br>mecum de peculiaribus quibusdam rebus secreto loqui, et nonnulla pro se<br>efficienda mihi demandare, tum quoque ex me, quæ in<br>studiorum meorum promotionem et vivendi commoditatem fieri benignissime dudum<br>iusserat, an ita succederent, cognoscere, quod et sæpius quam semel per integras<br>horas solitarie in Ipsius Maiestatis<br>conclavi factum est, et omnia, quæ humillime desiderare poteram, clementissime<br>mihi indulta; quia vero ignoro, an Ipsius<br>Maiestas pluribus mecum loqui et quippiam aliud adhuc ulterius<br>mandare velit, non audeo hinc discedere, priusquam hac de re satis certus fuero.<br>Interveniunt Legati magnorum Principum, et nunc est hîc Galliarum Regis (qui me<br>die crastino ad se invitavit) cum splendidissimo comitatu, et magna est<br>negotiorum moles, quibus Ipsius Maiestas ad<br>alia atque alia, quæ incidunt, curas dirigere necesse habeat; quantum vero ex<br>Magnifico Domino CORRADUCIO Imperij<br>Procancellario intellexi, cuperet Ipsius<br>Maiestas, ut habitationem sibi propiorem, ubi pariter studia mea<br>exsequi possem (in quo etiam nunc laboratur) obtinerem, quali id fiet successu,<br>alias percipies. Est et aliud quoddam negotium, licet minus gratum, attamen<br>necessarium, quod me voluntario hîc detinet. Dithmarsus enim iste famosi illius in me scripti Author<br>lethaliter decumbit, partim morbis gravioribus confectus, partim male de isto<br>facinore conscius, et pœnas metuens; hunc iam dudum in ius citatum ob atroces<br>illas iniurias et calumnias, quas nosti, legitime iudicari volebam, sed ob<br>pestem dissipata Cæsaris Aula, iam pæne per<br>integrum annum, id antehac fieri non potuit; urgeo itaque nunc iuris processum,<br>siquidem actio sit personalis et periculum in mora, iamque impetravi a Cæsarea Maiestate, ut deputentur e Consiliarijs<br>certi Commissarij, qui omnia in causa hac debili cognoscant, atque sententiam definitivam pronuncient; qualis hæc futura sit, tandem<br>rescisces.</p><p>Cum hisce præteritis diebus in Aula Arcis horis antemeridianis<br>inter Generosos et Nobiles Viros ambularem, monstratus mihi est Baro ille<br>OCTAVIUS CAURIANUS, ad quem Dominus<br>CORRADUCIUS pro ROSENKRANTZIO nostro scripserat,<br>quare, captata commoda occasione, cum eo de ROSENKRANTZIO, ubinam esset (nihil<br>enim is mihi, ex quo hinc discessit, scripsit) et quo in statu res eius<br>versarentur, sedulo collocutus sum. Is eum clementissime ab Archiduce MATHIA receptum, quod et antea a Domino<br>CORRADUCIO cognovi, moxque cum equitibus<br>quibusdam, prout ipsemet cupiebat, in Ungariam profectum, ibi in exercitu esse Cæsaris nostri aiebat, qui in obsidione Castelli<br>Papæ nunc occupatur, ubi strenuum illum belli Ducem SCHWARTZENBURGIUM, nuper globo sclopetico<br>ictum occubuisse inaudivimus, eodem etiam OCTAVIO famam confirmanti. Ut vero de rebus ROSENKRANTZIJ eo plenius instruerer,<br>præsertim cum dicerem, me hisce diebus in Daniam ad quosdam ex eius consangvineis atque amicis scripturum, hanc schedam, sua manu subscriptam, quam<br>hîc apposui, mecum communicavit. Ubi plura ipsum concernentia percepero, te<br>eorum ulterius reddam participem. Vale, et Coniugem tuam, neptem meam<br>charissimam, ex me quam diligentissime resaluta; constitueram ipsemet quidpiam<br>ad eam scribere, sed hæ interpellationes aulicæ me ita avocant (nam ex hoc ipso<br>instanti in arcem ascensurus sum) et M. CHRISTIERNUM, de quo dixi, iam itineri accinctum diutius<br>remorari nolui, ut propria curare vix queam; fiet id, Deo volente, alias,<br>meliori concesso otio. Saluta quoque ex me officiose matrem tuam, nobilissimam<br>et honestissimam fœminam, tum quoque alios amicos, quos scis integros esse et<br>sinceros, atque in amore nostri perseverantes.</p><p>Iterum vale.</p><p>Dabantur Pragæ festinanter die 3. Augusti stylo novo Anno<br>1600.</p><p>T. T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble man, pre-eminent as much in the brightness of his birth as of his erudition, Lord Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm, my friend, relation-by-marriage, and kinsman, by far the dearest.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Your most longed-for and long-desired letter, most loving Holger, I have now at last received — in which at the outset you excuse your slowness in writing back, and that by reasons which easily find a place with me, who otherwise think candidly of you. Afterward you recount many things about the condition of my affairs, which Divine Clemency has granted me here with the most august <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> — and how, related by you to others, it pleased them, but was to you and your intimate friends especially most delightful to hear — which abundantly attest your sincere affection toward me (of which I never doubted before too); for which constant love toward me I give you the greatest thanks I can, proceeding from a heart most zealous of you in turn. My affairs are still in the same state in which they were when I last wrote you; for on account of the absence of the Emperor — who still tarries at <b>Pilsen</b>, a city 15 miles distant hence — they could meanwhile take no increase of any moment, which however I hope will come hereafter. For the Imperial Majesty remits nothing of his most clement favour toward me (which I count as accepted by God), but daily increases it; the leading men of this realm too wish me very well, so much that <b>they have of their own accord offered to adopt me as a native of the Kingdom, that I might enjoy all their Privileges</b>. But of these I now add no more, to indicate such things more amply perhaps in their time.</p><p><b>Yesterday, at last, Friedrich Rosenkrantz departed from me — your cousin and my kinsman — who came hither a month ago.</b> I grieve from my heart — and who would not grieve at that excellent young man's most unhappy lot, and the less-expected calamity of most inconstant fortune? I — not only because he was so carefully commended to me by you (though this too must always weigh much with me), but also for the bond of blood between us — laboured diligently, as far as was in me and as he himself wished, to add at least some relief to his adversities, if I could not wholly cure them. That I might do this the more readily, I advised him to betake himself to <b>Pilsen, to the Emperor's court</b>, and gave him a letter to the most ample Imperial Vice-Chancellor, the illustrious and magnificent Lord <b>Coraduz</b>, asking him to obtain for this Rosenkrantz <b>letters of intercession from the Imperial Majesty to the most serene Archduke Matthias, the [Emperor's] Brother, who now presides over the supreme command of the Hungarian war</b> — which was obtained sooner than our expectation, Lord Coraduz himself (who greatly favours me, and does not decline to expedite anything for my sake) disposing and promoting this business. He also of his own accord added privately another letter to a certain noble <b>Baron</b> at the Archduke's court, familiarly known to him, likely to profit Friedrich not a little; and I too, that I might add my own vote of whatever kind, <b>wrote humbly to the most serene Archduke himself</b>, and left nothing untried to aid our kinsman's wishes with His Highness according to my small measure, intimating in a few words his misfortune in his fatherland — for so it seemed advisable, not only to me but to Friedrich, that we might forestall others, who by carping might perhaps exasperate the evil more odiously, and so render the matter more suspect. <b>At the same time I sent His Highness two of my books, in all respects like those presented to your King</b> [Christian IV]; <b>and together with them a certain globe, made with skill at Alkmaar in Holland, from our verification of the stars</b> — that by these little gifts I might the more readily catch the Prince's clement benevolence, by which he might the more gladly assent to our Friedrich's wishes. Copies of all these letters of which I have spoken, and at the same time Friedrich's own report, which on returning hither from Pilsen he set down in his own hand, and likewise his letter to you, I have placed here, that from reading them you may the more readily draw the circumstances of the matter done. I conceive no doubtful hope that our Friedrich, supported by these means, will accomplish his purpose with the renowned Archduke happily enough — especially as to those external and Political wishes, which I leave to his favour; <b>each is drawn by his own pleasure; sometimes necessity urges us to accept what otherwise were less to be cared for.</b></p><p>But one scruple still lurks within, which is of no small moment, and could delay all the rest — or even render it vain, or cut it off entirely, and quite deliver him from so many adversities and troubles of this wretched life. <b>For he labours — and more gravely than he himself seems to perceive — under the afflictions of a not lightly damaged lung</b>, so that he is turning <b>asthmatic, and gradually wasting with consumption [phthisis]</b>, unless, by God's grace, he shall in good time recover health by suitable medicines (which, presently relieving such great evils, are not easily found) — for the obtaining of which <b>the manner of life, and the condition of region and diet which he now affects, are to be feared to hinder rather than help</b>. But in this part too I not only forewarned him, but was at hand to him with counsel and the help of medicines, so far as I now had them ready, and prescribed a method how more are to be prepared hereafter, which may, in my opinion, so far as God shall grant, relieve this affliction of health to which he is subject. And so, as I said, I sent him off yesterday, making for <b>Vienna</b>, praying all auspicious things for him — <b>there being joined to him a certain Master, called Johannes Kepler, excellently skilled in Mathematics, who, coming from Styria, was here with me for a third of a year [four months], and has resolved to return, his affairs there being settled. Since he must, on his return, pass through Vienna, and is acquainted with the men and the roads on this journey, he can be a useful companion to Friedrich.</b> For the rest, just as you began your letter with a thing welcome and pleasing — about the change, by divine help, of my affairs for the better — not without joyful applause, and then in the middle interposed certain sad and turbulent and less smiling things about the calamity of our common kinsman, so at last at the end you bring on a glad <i>catastrophe</i>, especially delightful to hear, so that you seem to represent a kind of comedy. For what you report last (to leave the earlier things, of which enough is now said): <b>about your dearest Sophia, my niece — that God blessed her with the fruit of the womb, and that she was then near her delivery; whom Friedrich reported also to have happily given birth</b> — these things, I say, affected me with great delight, and were most welcome to hear. May God, Best and Greatest, deign of His goodness to preserve this <b>little daughter</b> He has given you safe, and guard her from dangers, that, growing in age, she may be a joy to both Parents. To which everlasting and triune Godhead I hold you, and your wife, together with your female offspring, commended with my whole heart.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given from the imperial castle of Benátky, the 3rd day of June, the year 1600.<br>Yours, <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, in his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPTS. I have added certain poems, sent me these days by Paul Melissus, the standard-bearer of the Poets of Germany, together with a copy of his letter, that you may know on what occasion he set them down. And because near the foot he makes mention of the Ode of your Cramer, I have appended this too. If leisure permit the transcribing, I shall add also a copy of the letter of Lord Joseph Scaliger, which has likewise very lately been delivered to me. When you have read these through, I should wish you to share them with Lord Doctor Nicolaus, our common friend.  /  Sir, beloved kinsman, since after the victory won and the trophy taken up you press on with a less valid insistence — according to that saying, \"there will be none willing to yield to genius\" — I have nothing else to answer than that there is gold with me which not even Hercules could have wrested away with his club, far less that it should yield to such feeble arguments. Farewell, and beware lest hereafter you lay a wager with those who measure the matter geometrically. Given as above in your letter. T. B.  /  When I had already written this letter to you, a little after Friedrich Rosenkrantz had departed from me, two months having now elapsed, I kept it by me meanwhile until a sure and safe occasion of sending should be at hand; which I have now conveniently found through Master Christiernus Longomontanus, who for several years was a member of my household and is returning to Denmark (and whom, should he have need of your help, I beg you to hold commended on my behalf and to advance to better things), and I judged that I should keep it by me no longer, together with the enclosures of which they make mention. I am now at Prague, whither I was summoned a month ago by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, as soon as he had returned from Pilsen — where for three quarters of a year he was absent on account of the plague — to the castle of his usual residence; for His Majesty deigned to speak with me in secret about certain particular matters, and to commit to me certain things to be done for him, and likewise to learn from me whether the things he had long since most benignly ordered to be done for the advancement of my studies and the convenience of my living were so succeeding — which was done more often than once, for whole hours, privately, in His Majesty's chamber, and all the things I could most humbly desire were most clemently granted me. But since I do not know whether His Majesty wishes to speak with me at greater length and to command anything further beyond this, I do not dare to depart hence before I am sufficiently certain of this matter. The Legates of great Princes are arriving, and now there is here [the Legate] of the King of France (who has invited me to him tomorrow) with a most splendid retinue, and great is the mass of business to which His Majesty must needs direct his cares, to one thing and another as they fall out. As far as I have understood from the Magnificent Lord Coraduz, the Imperial Vice-Chancellor, His Majesty would wish that I obtain a dwelling nearer to himself, where I might equally pursue my studies (which is even now being worked at); with what success this may be, you will learn another time. There is also a certain other business, less welcome yet necessary, which keeps me here of my own will. For that man of Dithmarschen, the Author of that infamous writing against me, lies mortally ill — partly worn out by graver diseases, partly ill at ease in conscience over that deed, and fearing punishment; him I have long since wished, having been summoned to law for those atrocious injuries and calumnies which you know, to be lawfully judged; but because of the plague, with the Emperor's Court dispersed for almost a whole year, this could not be done before. I therefore now press the legal process, since the action is personal and there is danger in delay; and I have now obtained from the Imperial Majesty that certain Commissioners be deputed from the Councillors, to take cognizance of everything in this difficult case and to pronounce a definitive sentence; what this is to be, you will learn at last. When in these past days I was walking in the Court of the Castle in the morning hours among Gentle and Noble Men, there was pointed out to me that Baron Octavius Caurianus, to whom Lord Coraduz had written on behalf of our Rosenkrantz; wherefore, having caught a convenient occasion, I conversed diligently with him about Rosenkrantz — where he might be (for he had written nothing to me since he departed hence), and in what state his affairs stood. He said that Rosenkrantz had been most clemently received by Archduke Matthias, and had soon set out, with certain horsemen, as he himself wished, into Hungary, and was there in the army of our Emperor, which is now occupied in the siege of the Castle of Pápa — where we have heard that that vigorous commander of the war, Schwarzenburg, was lately struck by a musket-ball and fell, the same Octavius likewise confirming the report. But that I might be the more fully informed about Rosenkrantz's affairs (particularly since I said that these days I would be writing into Denmark to certain of his kinsmen and friends), he shared with me this paper, subscribed in his own hand, which I have placed here. When I have learned more things concerning him, I will make you further partaker of them. Farewell, and greet again most carefully on my behalf your wife, my dearest niece; I had resolved to write something to her myself, but these courtly interruptions so call me away (for from this very moment I am about to go up to the castle), and I was unwilling to detain Master Christiernus, of whom I spoke, already girt for his journey, any longer. For I could scarcely attend to my own affairs meanwhile; that will be done, God willing, another time, when better leisure is granted. Greet also dutifully on my behalf your mother, the most noble and most honourable Lady, and likewise your other friends, whom you know to be upright and sincere, and persevering in love of us. Again, farewell. Given at Prague, in haste, the 3rd day of August, New Style, in the year 1600. T. T. B.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> of Rosenholm — Tycho's kinsman (and, via his wife Sophie Brahe, nephew-in-law); cf. brahe-t_15980925001.<br>- <b>Significance — a multi-thread letter that resolves the Friedrich-Rosenkrantz / Kepler / globe episode.</b><br>  1. <b>Imperial favour.</b> Tycho's standing under <b>Rudolf II</b> grows daily (the Emperor at Pilsen); the <b>Bohemian nobility has offered to adopt Tycho as a native of the realm</b> with full privileges.<br>  2. <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz — the family side of brahe-t_16000502001.</b> Holger's cousin <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b> (the disgraced kinsman of the Archduke Matthias letter of 2 May 1600) came to Tycho \"a month ago\" and <b>left yesterday (2 June) for Vienna</b>. Tycho recounts the whole effort: sending him to the court at <b>Pilsen</b> with a letter to <b>Coraduz</b>, who obtained the Emperor's <b>intercession to Archduke Matthias</b> (head of the Hungarian war) \"sooner than expected,\" plus a private letter to a court <b>Baron</b>; and Tycho's own humble letter to Matthias (the letter of 2 May 1600) — discreetly \"intimating his misfortune in his fatherland\" to forestall worse rumour.<br>  3. <b>The gift to Matthias — the globe \"made at Alkmaar.\"</b> Confirms and <i>specifies</i> the gift announced on 2 May 1600: <b>two books (matching those given to Christian IV) and \"a globe, made with skill at Alkmaar in Holland, from our verification of the stars.\"</b> A precise <b>provenance for the celestial globe</b> sent to Matthias — <i>made/finished at <b>Alkmaar</b> in Holland from Tycho's star-catalogue</i> — a notable datum for celestial-globe history (cf. the Dutch globe-makers, brahe-t_15990417001, and the Fischer letter brahe-t_16000516002). Flagged for the globe-history layer.<br>  4. <b>Friedrich's fatal illness.</b> A striking medical passage: Friedrich suffers a <b>\"not lightly damaged lung,\" turning asthmatic, wasting with consumption (phthisis)</b> — worse than he realises; Tycho gave counsel, medicines, and a regimen, fearing his chosen \"manner of life and diet\" will harm him. <i>(Friedrich died in the Hungarian war in 1602.)</i><br>  5. <b>Kepler travels with Friedrich — resolving the departure.</b> Tycho sends, <b>as Friedrich's travelling companion to Vienna, \"a certain Master, Johannes Kepler, excellently skilled in Mathematics, who came from Styria and was here with me a third of a year and now resolves to return.\"</b> This is the <b>execution of the very arrangement of the Danish Rindschat letter (brahe-t_16000000001)</b> — the Rosenkrantz kinsman taking Kepler to Vienna at no cost — and fixes the <b>departure to 2 June 1600</b>. The April-1600 Kepler letters — the angry one of 8 April (brahe-t_16000408001) and the warm Danish one (brahe-t_16000000001) — were the <i>prelude</i>; this is the <i>event</i>. (Note Kepler's residence is here \"a third of a year\" ≈ 4 months → arrival ~Feb 1600.)<br>  6. <b>Holger's newborn daughter.</b> The \"glad catastrophe\": <b>Sophie Brahe (Tycho's niece, Holger's wife) had given birth to a daughter</b> — reported by Friedrich; Tycho rejoices and blesses the child.<br>- <b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Binds together <b>brahe-t_16000000001 (Rindschat)</b>, <b>brahe-t_16000502001 (Matthias)</b>, and this letter as <b>one episode</b> (Friedrich Rosenkrantz + Kepler → Vienna, June 1600); refines the Rindschadt letter's date to <b>~May 1600</b>; adds the <b>Alkmaar globe-provenance</b> to the globe-distribution record.<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Archduke Matthias</b>; the unnamed court <b>Baron</b>; <b>Christian IV</b> (\"your King\"); <b>Johannes Kepler</b>; <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Holger's wife, Tycho's niece) and the <b>newborn daughter</b>. Places: <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>, <b>Vienna</b>, <b>Styria</b>, <b>Alkmaar</b> (Holland), Hungary, Denmark.<br>- <b>Date (protocol §3 / §4.1).</b> Source dateline <i>die 3. Iunij Anno 1600</i> → <b>3 June 1600 [N.S.]</b> (led with, per §4.1). The catalogue label's alternative \"3 August\" (and the slug's `0803`) is an <b>error</b> — contradicted by the text and by the internal chronology (Kepler \"here a third of a year\"; Friedrich \"came a month ago,\" i.e. ~early May → departed 2 June). Flagged for the label-date audit.</p><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> POSTSCRIPTA (Melissus/Scaliger enclosures for Doctor Nicolaus; the gold/Hercules wager-note) + the entire 24 Jul./3 Aug. 1600 continuation letter (the Emperor's audiences after Pilsen; the French king's legate; the Ursus lawsuit + imperial commissioners; Octavius Caurianus + the Friedrich-Rosenkrantz/Hungary news; Schwarzenburg killed by a musket at the Pápa siege), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.333–335 — the working transcription carried only the 3 June first installment. This vindicates the 0803 slug as the dispatch date.</li><li><b>Rosenkrantz's silence.</b> Recovers Tycho's aside that Friedrich Rosenkrantz \"had written nothing to me since he departed hence\" — Tycho was reconstructing the young kinsman's movements from third parties (Coraduz, Octavius Caurianus), not from Rosenkrantz himself.</li><li><b>The Denmark channel.</b> Recovers Tycho's own stated motive for pressing Octavius Caurianus for news: he was about to write into Denmark to Rosenkrantz's own kinsmen and friends, and wanted to be \"fully informed\" first — a small window onto the informal news-network Tycho used to track a troubled relative abroad.</li><li><b>Tycho's excuse for brevity.</b> Recovers his closing explanation to Holger Rosenkrantz for not writing more (and not writing to Sophia, his niece, himself): overwhelmed by business at the Prague/Benátky court, he \"could scarcely attend to my own affairs,\" deferring fuller correspondence to a future time of \"better leisure.\"</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Translation restored to match Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia</i> VIII, pp.333-335: (1) p.334 'Baro ille Octavius Caurianus' rendered as 'Baron Octavius Caurianus', correcting the unsupported 'rare fellow' and matching this letter's own earlier rendering of 'Baronem' as 'Baron' (p.331); (2) the abbreviated sign-off 'T. B.' closing the jesting Hercules/gold aside (p.333) restored to the English; (3) the letter's final abbreviated signature 'T. T. B.' (p.335) restored to the English. The Latin source_text required no change in any of the three cases.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — warm, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the imperial favour and the Bohemian-naturalisation offer; the full <b>Friedrich Rosenkrantz</b> narrative — Pilsen, Coraduz, the Matthias intercession, the gift of <b>two books + the Alkmaar-made globe</b>; his grave <b>lung illness</b>; <b>Kepler dispatched as his companion to Vienna</b>, resolving the April–June 1600 departure; and Sophie Brahe's <b>newborn daughter</b>) is firmly construed and dovetails with the Rindschadt and Archduke Matthias letters. The <b>month of the date</b> is resolved to June against the label's spurious \"August\" (§4.1); the <b>Alkmaar globe-provenance</b> is rendered as written and flagged for the globe-history layer.</p><p>All three gaps are short connective or parenthetical clauses embedded in an already coherent, well-attested letter (Tycho to Holger Rosenkrantz, 3 August 1600); the Latin for gap 1 is independently confirmed against the printed text in Dreyer's Opera Omnia (vol. VIII), which reproduces the same passage verbatim, including the Octavius Caurianus context. None of the three involves a named person not already present in the surrounding text, and the syntax throughout is plain formal epistolary Latin with no textual cruces. Translations are literal and low-risk.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full collation: read all 8 Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.329-336, chunked 1-4 then 5-8), covering the letter's entirety (Rosenkrantz salutation p.331 through the postscript's final sign-off p.335); adjacent letters sharing those pages (Tycho-Scaliger tail, Ottavio Caurian-to-Tycho, Tycho-Longomontanus, Oswaldus-to-Tycho) were identified and excluded from scope. Checked proper names (Friedrich Rosenkrantz, Corraducius, Archduke Matthias, Octavius Caurianus, Schwarzenburg, Kepler, Longomontanus, Melissus, Cramer, Scaliger, Nicolaus), place names (Pilsen, Alkmaar, Papa, Prague, Benatky, Vienna), numbers (15 miles, a third of a year, three quarters of a year) and both closing dates (3 June / 3 August 1600) against the page images: all confirmed correct except one. Dreyer p.334 confirms the source reads 'Baro ille OCTAVIUS CAURIANUS' (Baron), which the English mistranslates as 'that rare fellow' -- the same title is correctly rendered 'Baron' earlier in this same letter, so the error is real and internally inconsistent, not a source ambiguity. Also found: two abbreviated sign-offs ('T. B.' mid-postscript p.333; 'T. T. B.' final p.335) silently dropped from the English. The self-assigned grade A is not supported by this collation.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (medium). 'Baro' means 'Baron' (a noble title), not 'rare fellow'; the same letter correctly translates 'Baronem' as 'Baron' a few paragraphs earlier (p.331), so this is an inconsistent, confirmed mistranslation that drops Caurianus's rank and substitutes an unsupported descriptor. At: “there was pointed out to me that rare fellow Octavius Caurianus”.<br>- <b>OMISSION</b> (low). The abbreviated authorial sign-off closing the jesting 'Hercules/gold' aside is silently dropped from the English. At: “NONE — English runs straight from \"Given as above in your letter.\" into the next paragraph”.<br>- <b>OMISSION</b> (low). The final abbreviated signature (Tuus Tycho Brahe) at the very end of the letter is silently dropped from the English. At: “NONE — translation ends at \"...in the year 1600.\"”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page images, TBOO VIII pp.333-335 (the closing sections of the Rosenkrantz letter of 3 August 1600 N.S.), read directly from the slice PDF. All three defects are confirmed present as described: the 'Baro'/Baron title on p.334, and the two abbreviated Latin sign-offs on pp.333 and 335 which were already correctly present in the source_text but dropped from the translation. All three are now fixed; no crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 22, "prev": "brahe-t_15990909001", "next": "brahe-t_16010404001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Holger for his long-awaited letter, acknowledges his reports of Tycho's standing with Emperor Rudolf II, and notes that affairs remain unchanged pending the Emperor's return.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000803001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000804001", "d": "1600-08-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Longomontanus (Christen Sørensen)", "ck": "Christen Sørensen Longomontanus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christen Sørensen Longomontanus [open testimonial] — Prague, 4 August 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTIANUM LONGOMONTANUM.</p><p>EGO TYCHO BRAHE de Knudstrup, ex Dania oriundus, Sacrae Caes. Mai. arcem Benaticam in Regno Bohemiae inhabitans, testor omnibus, cuiuscunque conditionis et dignitatis sint, hisce meis literis, earundem exhibitorem, eruditissimum et honestissimum virum, Magistrum CHRISTIANUM SEVERINI LONGOMONTANUM, natione Cimbrum, mihi per octennium in Dania, et postea aliquamdiu in Germania et Bohemia fuisse domesticum, atque in exercitijs Astronomicis diligentem et fidelem navasse operam. Nunc vero cum res eius non ferant, ut diutius mecum commaneat, vel accrescente aetate cogitet aliam vitae rationem inire, ideoque in patriam ad suos reverti: ego conatus eius nullatenus impedire volui, sed eum libere et cum bona gratia dimisi. Quare omnes generosos et nobiles viros, praesertim in Dania mihi sanguine iunctos, et alios quocunque locorum claros et eruditos viros officiose et peramanter rogatos habeo, ut praenominatum Magistrum CHRISTIANUM sibi diligenter commendatum habeant, atque ad optima quaeque ipsum promovere non degraventur: invenient me vicissim ad quaevis officia rursum in eorum gratiam, quantum per me licuerit, praestanda quam paratissimum.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae in metropoli Bohemiae die 4 Augusti stilo novo Anno Christi vulgari 1600.<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to [/ for] Christian Longomontanus.</b></p><p>I, Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup, sprung from Denmark, dwelling in His Sacred Imperial Majesty's castle of Benátky in the Kingdom of Bohemia, testify to all — of whatsoever condition and dignity they may be — by these my letters, that the bearer of the same, the most learned and most honourable man, Master Christian Severin Longomontanus, a Cimbrian [Jutlander] by nation, was my domestic [assistant] for eight years in Denmark, and afterward for some while in Germany and Bohemia, and rendered diligent and faithful service in astronomical exercises. But now, since his affairs do not permit that he stay longer with me — or [since], as his years increase, he is minded to enter upon another manner of life, and therefore to return into his homeland to his own people — I have in no way wished to hinder his endeavours, but have dismissed him freely and with good grace. Wherefore I have dutifully and most lovingly entreated all generous and noble men — especially those joined to me by blood in Denmark — and other distinguished and learned men wheresoever, that they hold the aforenamed Master Christian diligently commended to them, and not find it burdensome to promote him to every best [advantage]: they will find me, in turn, most ready to render whatever good offices, again, in their favour, as far as shall be permitted me.</p><p>Given at Prague, in the metropolis of Bohemia, the 4th day of August, new style, in the common year of Christ 1600.<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Document type.</b> An <b>open letter of recommendation</b> (a testimonial <i>to all whom it may concern</i>), given to <b>Longomontanus</b> on his departure from Tycho's service — not a private letter \"to\" him, despite the dataset's <i>ad Christianum Longomontanum</i>; the <i>ad</i> here means it is the testimonial he carries (he is its <i>exhibitor</i>).</li><li><b>Subject.</b> <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b> (Christian Severin, 1562–1647), <i>natione Cimbrum</i> (a Jutlander, from Lomborg / <i>Longomontanum</i>), Tycho's ablest assistant. Tycho certifies <b>eight years' service in Denmark</b> and further service \"in Germany and Bohemia,\" and dismisses him \"freely and with good grace\" to return home (with advancing years) — the amicable end of the long collaboration Tycho had tried to prolong eighteen months earlier (cf. brahe-t_15990111001, the Wittenberg letter luring him back). Longomontanus would become professor at Copenhagen and write the <i>Astronomia Danica</i> (1622).</li><li><b><i>ad optima quaeque … promovere</b></i> — to promote him \"to every best [advantage / post]\"; Tycho asks his Danish kinsmen and learned men everywhere to advance him, pledging reciprocal good offices.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pragae … die 4 Augusti stilo novo … 1600</i> = <b>4 August 1600, New Style</b> (<i>stilo novo</i> explicit), <i>Anno Christi vulgari</i> (\"in the common [civil] year of Christ\"). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely, and the document's nature (an open testimonial for Longomontanus on his honourable departure, after eight years' Danish service) is unambiguous and corpus-corroborated. The <i>ad … Longomontanum</i> of the heading is clarified (a testimonial carried by him), not over-read as a personal letter.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full Latin testimonial for Christian Longomontanus against TBOO VIII p.335 page image, checking every name, place (Benátky castle, Prague), the eight-year service term, and the date (4 August 1600, new style). Text and English translation match Dreyer's print exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Certifies that Longomontanus served eight years as his astronomical assistant in Denmark, then in Germany and Bohemia, and is now freely dismissed to return home.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000804001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000806001", "d": "1600-08-06", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Oswaldus", "ck": "Johannes Oswaldus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Wittenberg", "t": "Johannes Oswaldus → Tycho Brahe — Wittenberg, 6 August 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, MATHEMATICO NOSTRO SAeCULO PRAESTANTISSIMO, DOMINO AC FAUTORI SUO UNICE COLENDO.<br>SALUTEM IN CHRISTO IESU</p><p>NOBILISSIME Domine TYCHO, remunerationem quinque scilicet Ioachimicos, propter diarium Solis et Lunae, in Martio huius anni 1600 nobilitati tuae transmissum, a M. CHRISTIANO LONGIMONTANO accepi, Quare T. N. ut par est, gratias ago et habeo. Quod in superioribus meis literis, num in oras Bohemicas me vellem conferre nec ne, nihil habetur, causa fuit non mea in scribendo negligentia, vel oblivio, sed quod 14 diebus, antequam N. T. sententia de re illa huc allata esset, istae meae literae fuerunt scriptae et obsignatae. Ideoque M. MELCHIOREM IOSTELIUM rogavi, ut tuae nobilitati, mecum prius deliberandum, scriberet. In tali autem deliberatione reperi, consultius fore, si Witebergae permanens tuae N. in rebus astronomicis pro animi mei tenuitate inservirem, singulisque diebus aliquot certas horas, utpote tres vel quatuor, in laborem istum impenderem. Tuae nobilitati si hoc meum propositum placuerit, certum stipendium ex aequo et bono mihi determinare, et quid expectandum, data aliqua occasione, haud gravatim significare poterit.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Witebergae 6 Augusti veteri stylo Anni 1600 Christi.<br>T. N.<br>humiliter colens<br>M . IOHANNES OSWALDUS,<br>Zeillensis Francus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup, the most outstanding Mathematician of our age, his lord and patron uniquely to be honoured.</b><br><b>Greeting in Christ Jesus.</b></p><p>Most noble Master Tycho, I have received from Master Christian Longomontanus the remuneration — namely five Joachims [Joachimsthaler] — for the diary [ephemeris] of the Sun and Moon transmitted to Your Nobility in March of this year 1600. Wherefore I give and render thanks to Your Nobility, as is fitting. That in my earlier letter nothing is contained as to whether I would betake myself to the Bohemian regions or not — the cause was not negligence or forgetfulness on my part in writing, but that these my letters were written and sealed fourteen days before Your Nobility's opinion on that matter was brought hither. And therefore I asked Master Melchior Jöstel to write to Your Nobility that [the matter] must first be deliberated with me. But in such deliberation I have found that it would be more advisable if, remaining at Wittenberg, I were to serve Your Nobility in astronomical matters according to the slenderness of my mind, and to spend, each day, some certain hours — say three or four — upon that labour. If this my proposal shall please Your Nobility, you will be able to determine for me a certain stipend, in fairness and goodness, and to signify — when some occasion is given — what is to be expected, [and that] not grudgingly.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Wittenberg, 6 August, old style, in the year of Christ 1600.<br>Humbly honouring Your Nobility,<br>Master Johannes Oswaldus,<br>a Franconian of Zeil.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johannes Oswaldus</b> (<i>M. Johannes Oswaldus, Zeillensis Francus</i> — \"a Franconian of <b>Zeil</b>\" [am Main]), a Wittenberg-based mathematical computer offering himself to Tycho. He acknowledges payment of <b>five Joachimsthaler</b> (<i>Ioachimici</i> — the silver coins of Joachimsthal), conveyed through <b>Longomontanus</b>, for a <i>diarium Solis et Lunae</i> (a computed Sun-and-Moon ephemeris/\"diary\") sent to Tycho in March 1600.</li><li><b>The proposal.</b> Rather than move \"to the Bohemian regions,\" Oswaldus proposes to <b>remain at Wittenberg</b> and serve Tycho remotely in astronomical computation, \"three or four hours each day,\" for a fixed <b>stipend</b> — and asks Tycho to set the terms. A glimpse of Tycho's distributed network of paid calculators.</li><li><b><i>M. Melchior Iostelius</b></i> = <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (1559–1611), professor of mathematics (and later medicine) at <b>Wittenberg</b>, Tycho's contact there, who had relayed the negotiation.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Witebergae 6 Augusti veteri stylo … 1600</i> = <b>6 August 1600, Old Style</b> (<i>veteri stylo</i> explicit; Lutheran Wittenberg kept the Julian calendar). Header agrees with the label. (Note: dated two days after Tycho's testimonial for Longomontanus, brahe-t_16000804001, which is N.S. at Prague — the same Longomontanus is the link between the two.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; the persons (Oswaldus of Zeil; Longomontanus as paymaster; Melchior Jöstel at Wittenberg), the payment (5 Joachimsthaler for a Sun–Moon ephemeris), and the remote-employment proposal are all clear and corpus-consistent. The <i>veteri stylo</i> fixes the calendar explicitly.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.336, reading the page image; text matches word for word (the five Joachimsthaler, Christianus Longimontanus, Melchior Iostelius, the fourteen-day gap, Wittenberg, the date 6 Augusti veteri stylo 1600), and the translation renders every clause and figure correctly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges payment for a solar and lunar ephemeris sent in March, explains an omission in his earlier letter, and proposes to serve Tycho astronomically from Wittenberg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000806001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000808001", "d": "1600-08-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Nicolaus Braschius", "ck": "Nicolaus Braschius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Nicolaus Brasch (Braschius) → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 8 August 1600", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> This is an <b>incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Rostock poet <b>Nicolaus Brasch</b> <i>to</i> Tycho (most of the corpus is Tycho's outgoing correspondence).</p>", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO GENEROSISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, ASTRONOMO PRINCIPI, MATHEMATICO CAeSAREO, DOMINO ET PATRONO COLENDO. PRAGAe IN BEHMEN.<br>MAGNIFICE ET ILLUSTRIS DOMINE, PATRONE COLENDE.<br>S.</p><p>QUAM iamdudum mihi dari exoptavi occasionem literis tuis per MULAeUM in Daniam commeantem mihi traditis respondendi, ea iam tandem subito se aperit, neque tamen satis exoptata, cum Dresdam usque saltem se extendat, unde tamen ad te perlatum iri omnia spero. Est autem ita, ut scribis: neque enim mihi ingrata aut odiosa censura tua est, hominis doctissimi et praestantissimi, praesertim in ijs, quae ad te ipsum spectant. De carmine elegiaco loquor: in quo quaedam tum in concinnitate, tum in expositione te desiderare scribis. Scripsi hoc ad te, et fassus sum, cum mitterem: non eum laborem aut limam accessisse, quae caeteris meis fere solet. Scis enim ardorem illum Poëticum temporum intervallis minus aut magis incendi: et interdum ita lenti sumus, ut iaceant omnia. Placet itaque mihi, ut premas, donec correctum fuerit: quod quidem a te si factum fuerit, gratiam habebo: nam laus, ea certe, quam scriptor sibi vendicat, mea erit, labor tuus. Ne tamen ingenij me iacturam fecisse existumes, plane mihi persuadeo, metu isto te eximet Carmen Epicum de VARADINO: quod ad te circa idem tempus fere, quo tuae datae sunt, etiam tum traditum tibi esse oportuisset, misi per quendam ALBERTUM HOVI, hominem militarem, Domino illustri MELCHIORI RHEDERO addictum eique a me commendatum. Cui ut tu quoque me commendares et illud carmen, quod in honorem eius composui ipsique inscripsi, ijsdem literis a te contendi: neque minus iam contendo, si aut literae illae tibi non redditae essent, aut tu petitioni meae, quod secus esse puto, hactenus non satisfecisses. Quod secus hoc esse putem, inde fit, quod ipse RHEDERUS literis humanissimis benevolentiam suam mihi ostenderit et gratitudinis ac remunerationis spem fecerit. Constitit mihi certe illud carmen non exiguo labore et animi intentione, quam auxit Historiae exprimendae cum veritate et tamen cum decoro Poëtico difficultas: quorum primam dum quidam Poëta Silesius secutus est, alterum ita neglexit in eadem historia reddendâ, ut nihil ineptius fieri possit. Scribit idem RHEDERUS se ex consilio doctissimorum quorundam virorum, inter quos te quoque esse existimo, Gorlitzium recudendum misisse illud meum carmen. MELISSUS quin iam tandem tibi satisfecerit, plane mihi persuadeo: dicerem hominem plane a se discessisse, nisi fecerit.</p><p>De scripto aliquo Caesareae Maiestati dedicando, quod consilium tuum mihi impertis, pro eo atque volebam, est quod multum tibi debeam. Habeo quidem confectum Philosophiae Panegyricum, quo omnia ea tribus prolixis satis orationibus complexus sum, quae de Philosophia mihi videntur posse honorifice dici: sed eum neque iam mecum habeo, neque hactenus quisquam, quos quidem tentavi, Bibliopolarum in eum excudendum sumptus voluit conferre (ita enim agitur cum scriptis elegantibus, dum florent et leguntur inelegantia) nisi ipse conferam. Quod si Poëmata mea mihi remittantur a MELISSO, qui mihi in editione procurandâ minime satisfacit, cum forte posset (nolo dicere plura: hoc ipsum, quod dixi, tecum sit) quae ante tribus dedicavi, nescio, an rectius facerem, si mutato consilio soli Imperatori dedicarem, plusque forte inde praemij te et alijs commendantibus reportarem. Distribui autem nunc quidem in tres partes, quarum primam partem quinque Elegiarum libros continentem inscripsi Regi Scotiae ante iam in me munifico, alteram Epicorum quatuor libros et Lyricorum unum continentem, ULRICO Principi Megapolitaniae, tertiam tres Epigrammatum et unum Sylvarum librum complexam WILHELMO Curlandiae Duci. Utinam vero tam felix sim, ut aliquando lucem videant, quam illis hactenus invideri equidem nonnihil doleo. Sed prae his duabus vijs illa mihi inprimis arrideret: si materia aliqua mihi offerretur, qua Imperatoris ipsius aut maiorum eius laudes praedicarem, idque carmen hîc in Academia publice decantarem, simile futurum Rhederiano illi. Illam vero viam et materiem prae alijs amplecterer, quod existimarem eâ ratione me aeque magnam gratiam a Caesarea Maiestate initurum quam magni operis dedicatione. Ignosces mihi, vir magnifice, quod haec tam libere apud te loquar, quod eo facio, ut eo rectius mihi consulere possis, quoniam id libenter facis, et ea re undique gratiam colligis omnium. Si igitur potes, aut talem materiam mihi suppedita, aut de reliquis, quid videatur, scribe, aut certe aliud consilium suggere. Omnia enim mea tibi lubens committo.</p><p>Eram finem facturus etiam dudum debuissem: sed tristis adhuc clausula supererat de obitu CHYTRAeI. Quem hîc non laudo aut deploro: feci utrumque tum in Epigrammatis istis, tum in Programmate, cuius et ipse, volente ita Senatu Academiae, autor sum; quorum utrunque ad te mitto. Tu certe amicum non vulgarem et ingenuum studiorum tuorum et ingenij excellentissimi aestimatorem et praeconem amisisti: ego patrem et patronum singularem (ita enim me semper habuit ut filium) nos omnes praeceptorem communem. O tristem nuncium, quem tibi allaturus sum! Sed nolo te conturbare, et iam tabellarius astans inclamat aurem et concludere iubet. Bene igitur et diutissime vale, vir praestantissime, et diu vive non solum tuis, sed et BRASCHIO, quamvis et ille inter tuos est.</p><p>Datae festinanter Rostochij 8 Augusti Anno 1600.<br>T. Magnif. et Generos.<br>reverenter colens<br>BRASCHIUS.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 339; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>M. CHRISTIANUM amanter saluto, a quo me literis non salutari miror.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent, most excellent and most noble man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Prince of Astronomers, Imperial Mathematician, my Lord and Patron to be revered. At Prague in Bohemia.</b><br>Magnificent and illustrious Lord, Patron to be revered.<br>Greeting.</p><p>The occasion I had long wished for, of replying to your letter (delivered to me by <b>Mule</b> on his way into Denmark), now at last suddenly opens — yet not quite as wished, since it reaches only as far as <b>Dresden</b>, whence, however, I hope all will be carried on to you. It is as you write: for your censure is not unwelcome or hateful to me — the censure of a most learned and excellent man, especially in things that concern you yourself. <b>I speak of the elegiac poem</b>, in which you write that you found certain things wanting, both in finish and in exposition. I wrote you this, and confessed it when I sent it: that <b>it had not received that labour and file [<i>lima</i>] which my others usually do</b>. For you know that the Poetic ardour is kindled less or more by intervals of time; and sometimes we are so sluggish that everything lies idle. It pleases me, then, that you <b>suppress it until it has been corrected</b>: which, if it be done by you, I shall be grateful — for the praise that a writer claims for himself will indeed be mine, the labour yours. But lest you think I have suffered a loss of talent, I quite persuade myself that <b>the Epic Poem on Várad</b> will free you of that fear — which ought to have been delivered to you about the same time your letter was sent. I sent it by a certain <b>Albert Hovi</b>, a military man, attached to the illustrious Lord <b>Melchior Röder</b> and commended by me to him. That you too would commend me to him, and that poem which I composed in his honour and inscribed to him, I asked of you in the same letter — and I ask no less now, if either that letter was not delivered to you, or you have not yet (which I think otherwise) satisfied my petition. That I think it otherwise comes from this: that Röder himself, by a most courteous letter, has shown me his benevolence and given hope of gratitude and reward. That poem cost me no small labour and intentness of mind — increased by <b>the difficulty of rendering History with truth and yet with Poetic decorum</b>; of which, while a certain <b>Silesian poet</b> followed the first, he so neglected the latter, in rendering the same history, that nothing more inept could be made. The same Röder writes that, <b>on the counsel of certain most learned men — among whom I think you too are — he has sent that poem of mine to Görlitz to be reprinted</b>. That <b>Melissus</b> has at last satisfied you, I quite persuade myself: I should say the man had quite departed from himself, had he not done so.</p><p>As to <b>dedicating some writing to the Imperial Majesty</b> — the counsel you impart to me, just as I wished — it is something for which I owe you much. I have indeed finished a <b>Panegyric of Philosophy</b>, in which, in three sufficiently ample orations, I have embraced all that seems to me capable of being said honourably of Philosophy; but I neither have it now with me, nor has any of the booksellers I have tried been willing to lay out the cost of printing it (for so it goes with elegant writings, while inelegant ones flourish and are read) — unless I lay it out myself. But if my <b>Poems</b> are returned to me by <b>Melissus</b> (who by no means satisfies me in procuring their edition, though perhaps he could — I will say no more: let what I have said stay with you) — which I formerly dedicated to three [patrons] — I do not know whether I should do better if, changing my plan, <b>I dedicated them to the Emperor alone</b>, and perhaps got more reward thence, with you and others recommending me. I have for now divided them into three parts: the first, containing five books of Elegies, I inscribed to <b>the King of Scotland</b>, already munificent toward me; the second, containing four books of Epics and one of Lyrics, to <b>Ulrich, Prince of Mecklenburg</b>; the third, comprising three books of Epigrams and one of <i>Sylvae</i>, to <b>Wilhelm, Duke of Courland</b>. But would that I were so fortunate that they might one day see the light — which I grieve not a little that they have hitherto been begrudged! Yet before these two ways, that one above all would smile on me: <b>if some material were offered me by which I might proclaim the praises of the Emperor himself or of his ancestors, and recite that poem publicly here in the Academy</b> — it would be like that of Röder's. That way and that matter I would embrace before others, because I should reckon that thereby I would win as great a favour from the Imperial Majesty as by the dedication of a great work. You will forgive me, magnificent man, that I speak these things so freely to you — which I do that you may the more rightly counsel me, since you do it gladly, and in that way gather everyone's goodwill on all sides. If, then, you can, either supply me with such material, or write what seems best concerning the rest, or at any rate suggest some other plan; for I gladly commit all my [affairs] to you.</p><p>I was about to make an end — I ought to have done so long since — but a sad conclusion still remained, <b>concerning the death of Chytraeus</b>. Whom I here neither praise nor lament: I did both, in those Epigrams and in the Programme [funeral notice], of which I am myself the author, the Academy's Senate so wishing; both of which I send to you. <b>You certainly have lost an uncommon and noble friend, an appraiser and herald of your studies and of your most excellent genius</b>; I, a singular father and patron (for he always held me as a son); we all, a common teacher. O the sad news I am to bring you! But I will not disturb you, and now the courier, standing by, dins my ear and bids me conclude. So fare well, and very long, most excellent man, and live long, not only for your own, but for <b>Brasch</b> too — though he also is among your own.</p><p>Written in haste at Rostock, 8 August, the year 1600.<br>Of Your Magnificence and Nobility<br>the reverent servant,<br><b>Braschius</b>.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I greet Master Christian affectionately, and wonder that I am not greeted by him with a letter.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondents.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter, <b>Nicolaus Brasch (Braschius)</b> — the Rostock Neo-Latin poet — <b>to Tycho</b> at Prague. Brasch is the verse-correspondent whose poems Tycho repeatedly emended (cf. brahe-t_16010415001, where Tycho \"writes back to Master Brasch,\" and the Cramer/Backmeister circle). This letter shows the relationship from Brasch's side.</li><li><b>Significance — the literary-patronage world around Tycho, and the death of Chytraeus.</b></li><li><b>Accepting Tycho's criticism.</b> Brasch gracefully accepts Tycho's <b>censure of his elegiac poem</b> (faults of \"finish and exposition\"), admitting it lacked his usual \"labour and file [<i>lima</i>],\" and asks Tycho to <b>suppress it until corrected</b> — \"the praise will be mine, the labour yours.\"</li><li><b>The <i>Carmen de Varadino</i>.</b> Brasch's <b>epic on the siege of Várad/Großwardein</b> (a Long-Turkish-War subject), sent via the soldier <b>Albert Hovi</b> to <b>Melchior Röder</b>, whom Brasch asks Tycho to cultivate; Röder, on learned men's advice (Tycho included), had it <b>reprinted at Görlitz</b>. Brasch's reflection on <b>\"rendering History with truth yet with poetic decorum\"</b> — and his scorn for a <b>Silesian poet</b> who botched the latter — is a nice statement of historical-epic poetics.</li><li><b>The dedication question (the letter's practical core).</b> Responding to <b>Tycho's advice to dedicate a work to Rudolf II</b>, Brasch surveys his options: a <i>Panegyric of Philosophy</i> (unprinted — no bookseller will fund it); his <b>Poems</b> (held up by <b>Melissus</b> = Paul Schede Melissus), currently dedicated in three parts to <b>James VI of Scotland</b> (Elegies), <b>Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg</b> (Epics + Lyrics), and <b>Wilhelm Kettler, Duke of Courland</b> (Epigrams + <i>Sylvae</i>) — which he muses he might <b>re-dedicate to the Emperor alone</b>. But his real wish: a commission to <b>praise the Emperor or his ancestors in a poem recited publicly in the Academy</b>, for which he asks Tycho to \"supply material.\"</li><li><b>The death of David Chytraeus.</b> The \"sad conclusion\": <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600), the great Rostock theologian-historian, had <b>died (25 June 1600)</b>. Brasch wrote the official <b>Epigrams and Programme</b> (funeral notice) for the Academy and encloses them. His threefold lament is the emotional heart: <b>Tycho lost \"an uncommon friend, an appraiser and herald of your studies and most excellent genius\"</b>; Brasch a \"father and patron\"; the university \"a common teacher.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Nicolaus Brasch; <b>Tycho</b> (addressee, \"Prince of Astronomers, Imperial Mathematician\"); <b>Claus Mule</b> (<i>Mulaeus</i>, courier); <b>Albert Hovi</b> (soldier-bearer); <b>Melchior Röder</b> (<i>Rhederus</i>, the Várad-poem patron); the unnamed <b>Silesian poet</b>; <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b>; <b>James VI of Scotland</b>; <b>Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg</b>; <b>Wilhelm, Duke of Courland</b>; <b>David Chytraeus</b> (whose death is reported). Places: <b>Rostock</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Dresden</b>, <b>Görlitz</b>, <b>Várad</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Rostock/Mecklenburg was <b>Old Style</b> in 1600; no <i>stylo</i> marker; dateline <b>8 August 1600</b> read <b>Old Style</b> (per label). Header left unqualified to match. <i>(Chytraeus's death \"25 June 1600\" is the conventionally cited date; the letter does not give it.)</i></li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing greeting (Brasch's, to \"M. Christianus\"), restored from Dreyer VIII p.339 (after the signature; dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — polished, fully construable humanist Latin read with confidence; the substance (Brasch's acceptance of Tycho's verse-criticism and request to suppress the elegy; the <i>Carmen de Varadino</i> and the Röder/Görlitz reprint; the historical-epic poetics aside; the whole dedication-strategy survey with its three named dedicatees and the wish for an imperial commission; and the grieving report of <b>David Chytraeus's death</b> with the enclosed funeral pieces) is firmly construed and corpus-consistent. <b>Albert Hovi</b> and the <b>Silesian poet</b> are left unidentified per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, address block through the restored postscript, against TBOO VIII page images pp.337-339. All names and numbers check out: Mulaeus/Dresden in the opening, Albert Hovi and Melchior Röder re the Várad epic, the Silesian poet, the Görlitz reprinting, Melissus, the Panegyric of Philosophy (three orations), and the exact three-way poem dedication (5 books Elegies/King of Scotland, 4 Epic+1 Lyric/Ulrich of Mecklenburg, 3 Epigram+1 Sylvae/Wilhelm of Courland), the Chytraeus obituary passage, and the closing date (8 August 1600, Rostock). The bracketed apparatus note claiming the postscript 'M. CHRISTIANUM amanter saluto...' was restored from Dreyer p.339 was independently verified: Dreyer does print that line immediately after the signature on p.339 l.8-9, confirming the restoration is correctly placed and worded. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Accepts Tycho's criticism of an elegiac poem written in his honour, acknowledging that it lacked the usual finish and polish.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000808001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000813001", "d": "1600-08-13", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Hamburg", "t": "Vitus Winshemius → Tycho Brahe — Hamburg, 13 August 1600", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI, GENEROSO ET MAGNIFICO VIRO, MAIORUM INSIGNIBUS, VERA VIRTUTE ET DOCTRINA MULTIPLICI PRAeLUSTRI DOMINO TYCHONI A BRAHE IN BENATIA ET KNUTHSTORFFP EQUITI DANICO, SERENISSIMAe MAIESTATIS IMPERATORIAe ET CONSILIARIO ET DOMINO SUO PERPETUA OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.<br>AD MANUS GAe MAe IPSIUS PROPRIAS.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS, Magnifice et Generose Domine TYCHO, omni cum observantia perpetuo mihi colende Domine: Literae Gae Mae Vae scriptae Pragae 6a Augusti stylo novo, redditae mihi sunt 10a Augusti stylo veteri, cum reverterer illo ipso tempore ex Comitijs Iuridicis, habitis apud Dithmarsos, ubi per septimanas tres haeseram, evocatus eo ab Inclyto Rege nostro Daniae. Ago autem primo omnium serio pectore gratias Deo opt. Max. quod Gen. Mm Vm cum omni familia hactenus sospitem et salvam clementer tutatus fuerit, eundemque precor, ut quam diutissime illam florentem servet.</p><p>Coeterum Illustrissimo Principi OTTHONI, Duci Brunsvicensium et Lunaeburgensium, ea quae mihi commisit Ga Μ Va, suo nomine debita cum submissione retuli: is quid ad eam rescribi per me voluerit, ex Cels. eius autographo ad me misso, hisce nostris inserto, intelliget.</p><p>De congressu Embdae indicto subitoque iterum discessu Legatorum Danicorum et Anglicorum, quae scripseram, haec ita vere se habent. Verum ex eo ad me relatum est, Anglos in Daniam usque sequutos Regem nostrum, ibique negotium omne probe compositum et sopitum esse. Rex ipse noster hoc tempore in Iutiam cum Regina Coniuge excurrisse fertur ad venationes, cuius quoque adventus momentaneus forte Crempae in vicinia expectatur. De emissis 50 navibus nihil hîc accepimus, certo quod communicari Gae Mae Vae possit.</p><p>FREDERICO ROSENCRANZIO foeliciorem iam auram aspirare profecto laetor, et ubi occasionem scribendi nactus fuero, OLIGERUM de praesenti hominis fortuna hilariori certiorem reddam. Domino RUMPIO et Domino CORADUCIO, Viris honoratissimis, G. M. Va officia mea ut submissa, data occasione, deferat, reverenter oro. Gm Mm Vm Dominus VINCENTIUS MÖLLERUS Consul quoque officiose resalutat. Valeat quam optime Ga Ma Va omni cum familia, cui me totum dedo.</p><p>Datae raptim Hamburgae 13a Augusti Anno 1600.<br>Gm Mm Vm omni cum observantia<br>qui colit<br>VITUS WINSHEMIUS,<br>m. p.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious, generous and magnificent man, pre-eminent in ancestral arms, in true virtue and in manifold learning, Lord Tycho a Brahe at Benátky and Knudstrup, Danish knight, councillor of His Most Serene Imperial Majesty, his lord to be revered with perpetual observance.</b><br><b>Into His Generous Magnificence's own hands.</b></p><p>Illustrious, Magnificent and Generous Lord Tycho, my lord forever to be revered with all observance: Your Generous Magnificence's letter, written at Prague on the 6th of August New Style, was delivered to me on the 10th of August Old Style, just as I was at that very time returning from the juridical assembly held among the Dithmarschers, where I had been detained three weeks, having been summoned thither by our Illustrious King of Denmark. First of all I give serious and heartfelt thanks to God, best and greatest, that he has thus far mercifully kept Your Generous Magnificence safe and sound with all your household; and I pray him to keep it flourishing as long as may be.</p><p>For the rest, to the Most Illustrious Prince Otto, Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, I reported in your name, with due submission, the things which Your Generous Magnificence committed to me; what he wished to be written back to it through me, Your Magnificence will learn from his own autograph sent to me and enclosed in this our letter.</p><p>Concerning the congress appointed at Emden and the sudden departure once more of the Danish and English legates — what I had written holds thus, truly. But it has since been reported to me that the English followed our King all the way into Denmark, and that there the whole business was duly composed and laid to rest. Our King himself is said at this time to have gone off into Jutland with the Queen his consort for the hunt; his arrival, momentary [and brief], is perhaps expected in the neighbourhood of Crempe. Of the 50 ships sent out we have heard nothing here certain enough to be communicated to Your Generous Magnificence.</p><p>That a happier breeze now blows upon Frederik Rosenkrantz I am truly glad; and when I get the occasion to write, I will inform Holger of the man's present, more cheerful fortune. To Lord Rumpf and Lord Coraduz, most honoured men, I reverently beg Your Generous Magnificence to convey my submissive services, as occasion offers. Lord Vincent Möller, the Burgomaster, also dutifully returns Your Generous Magnificence's greeting. May Your Generous Magnificence fare as well as possible with all your household, to whom I give myself wholly.</p><p>Given in haste at Hamburg, the 13th of August, in the year 1600.<br>Your Generous Magnificence's, with all observance,<br>who reveres [you],<br>Vitus Winshemius,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> letter <b>to</b> Tycho. <b>Veit Ortel von Windsheim (Vitus Winshemius)</b> (1542–1608), Wittenberg physician, writing from <b>Hamburg</b> — replying to Tycho's letter (Prague, 6 Aug) and continuing the relationship attested in Tycho's own letter to him of 20 April 1600 (brahe-t_16000420001).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, titled <i>eques Danicus</i> (Danish knight) and imperial councillor, \"at Benátky and Knudstrup.\" The address-line <i>ad manus Generosae Magnificentiae ipsius proprias</i> = \"into his Generous Magnificence's own hands.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Otto, Dux Brunsvicensium et Lunaeburgensium</i> = <b>Otto, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</b>, to whom Winshemius delivered a commission of Tycho's, enclosing the Duke's autograph reply.</li><li><i>Fredericus Rosencranzius</i> = <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> (1571–1602), Danish nobleman (disgraced and exiled from the Danish court after the 1599 scandal with Rigborg Brockenhuus, then in military service abroad), whose fortunes are now improving — distinct from <i>Oligerus</i> = <b>Holger Rosenkrantz \"the Learned\"</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000420001).</li><li><i>Dominus Rumpius</i> = <b>Wolfgang Rumpf von Wielross</b>, Rudolf II's chief steward / privy councillor; <i>Dominus Coraducius</i> = <b>Rudolf Coraduz (Coraduz)</b>, imperial vice-chancellor — two of Tycho's principal court contacts at Prague (the <i>Coraduz/Barvitius</i> axis).</li><li><i>Vincentius Möllerus, Consul</i> = <b>Vincent Möller</b>, <b>Burgomaster (<i>Consul</i>) of Hamburg</b>, who adds his greeting.</li><li><i>Inclytus Rex noster Daniae / Rex … cum Regina coniuge</i> = <b>Christian IV of Denmark</b> and his consort.</li><li><b>News &amp; places.</b> <i>Comitia Iuridica apud Dithmarsos</i> = a <b>juridical assembly in Dithmarschen</b> (Holstein) where Winshemius had served three weeks at the Danish king's summons; <i>congressus Embdae</i> = the abortive <b>Anglo-Danish conference at Emden</b>; the English envoys followed Christian IV into Denmark, where the dispute was settled; the King is hunting in <b>Jutland</b>; <i>Crempa</i> = <b>Krempe</b> in Holstein; <i>50 naves</i> = an unspecified fleet rumour. (The Anglo-Danish friction of 1600 turned on Sound tolls, fishing, and the Spitsbergen/Greenland question.)</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> Heavily abbreviated honorifics: <i>G[enerosa] M[agnificentia] V[estra]</i> = \"Your Generous Magnificence\" (declined throughout — <i>Gae Mae Vae</i>, <i>Gm Mm Vm</i>); <i>m. p.</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand\"); <i>Datae raptim</i> → \"given in haste\"; closing <i>qui colit</i> → \"who reveres [you].\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — explicit double date).</b> The letter itself records the Julian/Gregorian gap: Tycho's letter was \"<b>written at Prague 6 August <i>stylo novo</b></i> [New Style], <b>delivered … 10 August <i>stylo veteri</b></i>\" [Old Style] — the 10-day difference between Imperial (Catholic, Gregorian) Prague and Lutheran north Germany. Winshemius's own dateline, <i>13a Augusti 1600</i> at Hamburg, is Old Style. Reproduced as written; not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean humanist Latin; all the named figures (Winshemius, Duke Otto, Frederik and Holger Rosenkrantz, Rumpf, Coraduz, Vincent Möller, Christian IV) are securely identified, and the political news (Dithmarschen, Emden, the Anglo-Danish settlement) is coherent. The dense honorific abbreviations are expanded per protocol; the explicit NS/OS double-dating is handled per §3. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (the Dithmarschen assembly dating in both styles, the Duke Otto/Cologne report, the Emden congress and English legates, the 50-ships item, Rosenkrantz, Rumpf, Coraduz, Burgomaster Vincent Möller) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.339-340 as page images. Exact match, including both O.S./N.S. dates and the '50 ships' figure. The one spelling variant found (Dreyer's 'Friderico' vs source's 'Frederico' Rosencranzio) is an ignorable early-modern orthographic variant, not a substantive error. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Vitus Winshemius", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000420001", "next": "brahe-t_16000926002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000420001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000926002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains a three-week absence at a Dithmarsian juridical assembly summoned by the Danish king, thanks God for Tycho's health, and pledges assistance regarding Prince Otto of Brunswick.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000813001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000828001", "d": "1600-08-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johannes Kepler — Prague, 28 August 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702</p>", "n": "<p><b>Note.</b> The formal letter calling the exiled Kepler to Prague — answered by <b>Kepler's reply of 17 October 1600 (brahe-t_16001017001)</b>. Written the <i>same day</i> as Tycho's letter to Herwart requesting the Kepler-on-Ursus transcript (cf. brahe-t_16000911001, \"your letter … of 28 August\").</p>", "la": "<p>ERUDITISSIMO ET PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IOHANNI KEPLERO, ORDINUM PROVINCIALIUM STYRIAe MATHEMATICO EXIMIO, NUNC GRATZIAe DEGENTI: AMICO SUO INPRIMIS DILECTO. GRÂITZ.</p><p>QUOD prioribus tuis ante duos Menses ad me datis, Eruditissime et dilectissime Magister KEPLERE, nihil antea responderim, nulla tui, nulla negotij, de quo inter nos actum, oblivione, sed potius, ut temporis oportunitati, quae in hac Aula non semper suppetit, me accommodarem, atque eo promptius atque felicius omnia exequerer, factum esse credas. Vix enim aliqua interea commoditas intermissa, sed et ultro aliquoties captata. Quod ut rectius percipias, quae interea acta sunt, brevibus narrabo. Paulo postquam Caesarea Maiestas, Pilsena relicta, huc Pragam in arcem solitae residentiae reversa esset, me Benatica ad se evocavit, ante hebdomadas plus minus septem. Pauculis autem diebus postquam hîc comparuissem, ad audientiam clementissime vocatus, in conclavi suo de varijs mecum loqui ultra sesquialteram horam dignabatur, et postea eodem die etiam per dimidiam circiter. In primo vero accessu, postquam ipsius Maiestas ea, quae ex me scire averet, proposuisset, iussit, ut, si quae forent, quae in rebus meis non ita succederent, prout ab initio mandârat, et si quae praeterea desiderarem, libere exponerem. Ego submisse actis gratijs, me talia in chartula aliqua denotaturum atque Domino BARVITIO traditurum respondi: quod et placuit, imo, ut hoc facerem, mandavit. Cum autem viderem tam exoptatam cum ipsius Maiestate de rebus necessarijs loquendi mihi ultro benignissime oblatam occasionem, de Te quoque, quae in animo habebam, proloqui non intermittendum duxi, sed arrepta aliqua ansa, ex ea, quae de instrumentis meis et siderum observationibus atque Astronomia instauranda, incidebat mentio: me solum tanto oneri, licet plurima iam huc conducentia in promptu habeam, non sufficere, sed collaboratoribus opus habere aiebam, ut citius et plenius res tanta in ipsius Maiestatis atque Imperij honorem et commodum absolveretur. Respondit eius Maiestas, se ut votis meis hac in parte satisfiat provisuram. Ibi cum inter alia adminicula huc facientia de te quoque huc e Styria accersendo et per unum atque alterum annum sustentando, tuo etiam discrete addito nomine insinuassem, ipsius Maiestas mox benignissime annuit, atque ut inter caetera, quae desiderarem, id quoque chartae mandatum Domino BARVITIO traderem, iussit, nec ullam hac in re difficultatem movit. Locutus sum postea particularius cum Illustri et Magnifico Domino CORRADUCIO, hac ipsa de re, et conditiones tuas illi sigillatim aperui, atque hoc solum pro tempore rogavi, ut literas a Caesarea Maiestate impetrare vellet ad Principem tuum et Amplissimum Ordinem Styriacum, quibus rogarentur, ut per biennium in gratiam Caesareae Maiestatis hîc mihi praesto adesse tibi liceret, et nihilominus annuo isthic frui stipendio, quod per Caesaris liberalitatem centum florenis annuatim augeri exoptarem, siquidem omnia hîc cariora sunt, quam in Styria. Is vero se lubentissime negotium hoc prolaturum, atque ut exoptatum eventum sortiatur, curaturum benigne pollicitus est. Id saltem suasit, ut denuo rem inter caetera, quae requirerem, Caesari, sive oretenus, sive per literas, subiectissime proponerem, atque ut ad se eius executio devolveretur, impetrarem, tunc suo auxilio et consilio nequaquam nobis defuturum. Interea dum ea, quae ipsius Maiestati submisse proponere constitui, animo volvo, et calamo concipio, iniunxit ipsius Maiestas mihi peculiare quid, pro se, quam primum fieri possit, efficiendum. Eram adhuc in publico hospitio apud Insigne aurei Gryphei, ubi cum nulla tranquillitas eiuscemodi quid agendi concederetur, conduxi aliam, non longe a Generoso Domino HOFFMANNO, habitationem minus irrequietam, in quam vix migrâram, et res meas utcunque disposueram, cum denuo me in arcem accersit Caesar, et quaerit, an ea parata sint, quae a me fieri voluit. Ego vero per hospitij incommoditatem et migrationem in alias aedes, me excusavi, et nacto iam meliori otio, omnia quam primum fieri possit, intra duas vel tres hebdomadas effecturum promisi, cui etiam responso ipsius Maiestas clementissime acquievit. Interim vero dum hoc, uti pollicitus sum, ago, quod sane non parum requirit diligentiae et laboris, non audeo ab ipsius Maiestate audientiam humillime petere, et de ijs, quae in votis habeo, etiam me ipsum non parum concernentia sollicitare, ne videar propria potius curare quam ea, quae ipsius Maiestas a me requisivit, suadentibus etiam alijs, ut propria interea differam, quo absolutis et exhibitis ijs, quae ipsius Maiestas a me desiderat, eo facilius, captata sic benevolentia, id quod exopto, assequar.</p><p>Atque haec praecipua causa est, cur responsionem ad priores tuas hactenus distulerim: nolui enim quippiam ad te scribere, antequam res omnis ex voto peracta esset, et literae, quibus huc honorifice vocareris, conscriptae. Interim vero ante paucos dies superveniunt alterae tuae, quae licet prioribus non dissimilia ab initio referant, tamen circa calcem catastrophen quandam insperatam habent, qua priora consilia non saltem tua, sed et mea non parum collabascunt. Intelligo enim inde Salarium Styriacum tibi, uti et alijs quibusdam, renunciatum, et insuper exilium impositum, nisi decreto istic promulgato parueris, ut ob id in Wirtenbergensem Ducatum, Patriam tuam, redire malis. At quia in hac rerum tuarum conturbatione et prioris sententiae frustratione, non habeo praesentius consilium, quod ex re nata tibi dem, quam ut, postposita omni mora, te huc conferas, sive solus, uti antea, sive etiam advecta simul coniuge, unâ cum supellectile et rebus necessarijs, pro quibus omnibus facile, uti arbitror, viaticum invenies, urgente praesertim sic necessitate instanti. Neque enim tibi deerunt tui Mecaenates et fautores; aut si opus fuerit, pecuniam, quae ad iter requiritur, alicubi interea mutuo sumes: ubi adveneris, tum praesentes rectius et tutius inter nos agemus, et rationes inveniemus, quibus hisce difficultatibus subveniri poterit, atque tibi et tuis rebus convenienter prospici, ne tibi opus sit tam cito in Patriam tuam redire, aut alio declinare. Interim nullam occasionem praeterlabi sinam, quin te, non saltem intercedente autoritate Domini CORRADUCIJ et Domini BARVITIJ, sed et apud Sacram Caesaream Maiestatem propria mea intercessione ad optima quaeque promoveam, et rebus tuis fluctuantibus atque afflictis subveniam. Imo si caetera omnia, quod tamen non spero, frustranea fuerint, ego ipsemet tibi non sum defuturus, sed in ipso effectu ostensurus, ea quae tibi me scripsisse memini, cum adhuc prope Hamburgum haererem, antequam nos invicem de facie novissemus, apud me adhuc rata esse, et constanter servari. Tu saltem nihil haesita, sed confidenter, quantocius poteris, advola: tum coram de omnibus, quae requiruntur, expeditius agemus, et tutius quam per literas: nec diffido, quin, ubi adveneris, viam iam praeparatam invenies. Haec summa sit consilij, quod etiam is, qui te mihi commendavit, maxime probat.</p><p>Plura nunc non addo, neque enim ea, quae in utrisque literis fuse attigisti, cum ad negotij promotionem, sic stantibus rebus, minus conducant, repetere volo: uti neque de Mathematicis, utrobique a te motis, quippiam addere, cum ea sint alterius atque maioris otij. Eclipsis tamen Solaris, cuius in ultimis tuis mentionem facis, et observationem qualemcunque assignas, calculum, e tabulis nostris renovatis, communico, ut tibi in conspectu sit, atque cum tua conceptione conferre queas. Quantitas deliquij hîc 5 digitos vix attigit, ut ob id apud Vos 6 excedere non potuerit: dimidium digitum istic plus de Sole tectum fuisse concessero, ob minorem Lunae parallaxin; sed vix amplius. Diligenter a nobis antequam Benatica discederemus, observata est haec Eclipsis: aderat adhuc M. CHRISTIERNUS, qui postea a me libere et liberaliter dimissus in Patriam abijt. Dominus a TZERNEMEL, qui tibi fecit spem aliquam ante quinquennium, hîc Pragae est a Caesare citatus, et forte aliquando hîc manebit, uti sunt admodum ..... quod indicandum duxi, ne frustra alibi ab eo opem et promotionem exspectes. Ego Bibliothecam meam et instrumenta, quae Benachiae habui, sic suadente Domino BARVITIO, huc transtuli: quantum enim intelligo, cupit Caesar, ut in propinquo habitem, et iam de loco commodo, qui civitati conterminus est, deliberatur, qua de re, ubi accesseris (neque enim abfuturum dubito) tibi, uti spero, non displicitura cognosces.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae, die 28 Augusti 1600.<br>Tui amantissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE, m. pr. scripsi.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 343–344; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POST SCRIPTA. Facis in ultimis literis tuis mentionem Scoti istius Capnuranici, quod in ipsum antapologiam, perlecto praesertim libro nostro secundo de Cometa, mediteris: quare te rem gratam et tuae promissioni meaeque expectationi analogam praestiteris. Addixisti etiam, te, quae in famoso isto scripto Ursino, Mathematica inveniuntur, refutaturum, quatenus rite non constant, quod etiam te effecturum confido. Imprimis autem averem, ut ea quae inventioni nostrae circa Hypothesin novam fucato et fraudulenter obijcit, eam nimirum ex COPERNICO vel APOLLONIO Pergaeo desumptam adhuc dilucidius et plenius quam antea a te refelli, atque ut eam mihi, uti par est, asserere, prout probabilibus rationibus iam antea fecisti, non molestum duceres. Institui contra illum actionem iuridicam, postquam huc Pragam veni, intelligens mihi aliquandiu hic commorandum, et periculum esse in mora, ast siquidem graviter decumberet: et impetravi a Caesarea Maiestate (utut quibusdam, quamdiu poterant, remoram inijcientibus) ut Commissarij quatuor deputarentur, duo Barones et duo Doctores iuris, qui sententiam hac de re pronunciarent. Siquidem is per emissarios meos quaesitus, ea, quae scripserat, retractare noluit, sed potius ad ius provocavit. Verum accidit, ut eadem hora, qua citatio illi insinuanda fuit, extingueretur; at nihilominus ius meum inchoatum prosecuturus sum, siquidem lis iam quasi contestata erat, et actio non plane personalis, sed etiam realis, ob librum famosum, qui perpetuo loco sui autoris loquitur, ut me et meos a calumnijs, quibus scatet, legitime vindicem, et iam a Caesare Illustrissimo Domino Archiepiscopo commissum est, ut omnia exemplaria, quae hic reperiuntur, conquirat et igni tradi faciat, atque Typographum puniat. Decretum etiam Dominus CORRADUCIUS a Caesare promulgatum pollicitus, ut per totum Imperium liber iste annulletur, et tanquam famosus et scurrilis irritus reddatur. Ego vero postea totum hunc processum unâ cum Commissariorum sententia et Caesareae Maiestatis decreto in publicum edere constitui, libello quodam peculiari, qui ad mediocrem excrescet magnitudinem. Quod et ex consensu clementissimo Caesaris et approbatione Domini CORRADUCIJ mihi iam perspecta, Deo volente, facturus sum. Atque in altera eius parte ad ea, quae Mathematica sunt, atque Hypotheses concernunt, responsurus, quare tuas quoque de his conceptiones mature obtinere averem; aut si quam citissime (uti spero) accesseris, poterit id differri in tuum adventum. Vale. Datum ut supra in literis. T. B.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most learned and excellent man, Lord Johannes Kepler, eminent Mathematician of the Provincial Estates of Styria, now dwelling at Graz, his especially beloved friend. Graz.</b></p><p>That to your earlier [letter], sent me two months ago, most learned and most beloved Master Kepler, I made no answer before, believe was done by no forgetfulness of you, nor of the business treated between us, but rather that I might accommodate myself to the opportunity of time (which in this Court is not always at hand), and so the more readily and happily carry everything through. For scarcely any convenience meanwhile was let slip — nay, several times one was even sought out. That you may the more rightly perceive what has meanwhile been done, I will narrate it briefly. <b>A little after the Imperial Majesty, leaving Pilsen, had returned hither to Prague, to the castle of his usual residence, he summoned me from Benátky to him, about seven weeks ago.</b> A few days after I had appeared here, called most clemently to audience, <b>he deigned to speak with me, in his chamber, of various things, for more than an hour and a half — and afterward, the same day, for about half [an hour] more.</b> At the first approach, after His Majesty had proposed what he wished to know from me, he ordered that, if there were any things in my affairs not succeeding as he had at the beginning commanded, and whatever else I desired, I should freely expound them. I, having submissively given thanks, answered that I would note such things on some paper and give it to <b>Lord Barvitius</b>; which pleased him — nay, he commanded that I do it. But when I saw so longed-for an occasion of speaking with His Majesty about necessary matters most kindly offered me of his own accord, <b>I judged that I should not omit to speak out about You too, what I had in mind</b>; and, seizing some handle, from the mention that fell of my instruments and star-observations and the restoring of Astronomy, <b>I said that I alone do not suffice for so great a burden (though I have very much already conducing hither at hand), but have need of collaborators</b>, that so great a work be completed sooner and fuller, for the Imperial Majesty's and the Empire's honour and advantage. His Majesty replied that he would provide that my wishes in this part be satisfied. There, when among other aids conducing hither <b>I had insinuated about summoning you too hither from Styria, and supporting you for a year or two — discreetly adding your name</b> — His Majesty soon most benignly assented, and ordered that I put this too on the paper to Lord Barvitius, among my other desires; and he raised no difficulty in this. <b>I afterward spoke more particularly with the illustrious and magnificent Lord Coraduz</b> about this very matter, and opened your conditions to him in detail, and asked only this for the present: that he would obtain letters from the Imperial Majesty <b>to your Prince and to the most ample Estate of Styria, by which they should be asked that for two years, for the Imperial Majesty's sake, you be allowed to be present here to assist me — and nonetheless to enjoy your annual stipend there, which I should wish augmented, by the Emperor's liberality, by a hundred florins yearly (since all things are dearer here than in Styria)</b>. He benignly promised that he would most gladly forward this business, and take care that it have the longed-for outcome. He only advised that I again propose the matter, among the other things I sought, to the Emperor (whether orally or by letter) most submissively, and obtain that its execution be devolved to him; that then he would in no way fail us with his help and counsel. Meanwhile, while I turn over in mind, and conceive with the pen, what I have resolved submissively to propose to His Majesty, <b>His Majesty enjoined on me a particular thing to be done for him as soon as possible.</b> I was still at the public inn at the Sign of the Golden Griffin, where, since no tranquillity for doing such a thing was allowed, I hired another, less restless lodging, not far from the noble <b>Lord Hoffmann</b>; into which I had scarcely moved, and arranged my things as I could, when the Emperor summoned me again to the castle and asked whether the things he wished done by me were ready. I excused myself by the inn's inconvenience and the move into another house, and, having now got better leisure, promised to do everything as soon as possible, within two or three weeks; with which answer too His Majesty most clemently acquiesced. Meanwhile, while I do this (as I promised), which indeed requires no little diligence and labour, I do not dare to beg an audience of His Majesty most humbly, and to solicit about the things I have in [my] wishes (concerning me too not a little), lest I seem to care for my own affairs rather than those the Imperial Majesty required of me — others too advising that I defer my own meanwhile, so that, once the things His Majesty desires of me are completed and shown, I may, the goodwill being thus won, the more easily attain what I long for.</p><p>And this is the chief cause why I have hitherto delayed the answer to your earlier [letter]: for <b>I did not wish to write anything to you before the whole matter was accomplished according to [my] wish, and the letters, by which you should be honourably called hither, were written.</b> But meanwhile, a few days ago, comes <b>your second [letter]</b> — which, though at the start it reports things not unlike the earlier, yet near the foot <b>has a certain unhoped-for catastrophe, by which the prior plans, not only yours but mine too, no little collapse.</b> For I understand thence that <b>the Styrian salary has been renounced to you (as to certain others too), and exile besides imposed, unless you obey the decree promulgated there — so that on that account you would rather return into the Duchy of Württemberg, your fatherland.</b> But since, in this disturbance of your affairs and frustration of the prior resolution, <b>I have no more present counsel to give you, from the case as it stands, than that, all delay set aside, you betake yourself hither — whether alone, as before, or even bringing your wife with you, together with household goods and necessaries</b> — for all which you will easily, as I judge, find travel-money, especially with present necessity so urging. For your Maecenases and patrons will not fail you; or, if need be, you will meanwhile borrow somewhere the money required for the journey. When you have arrived, then, present, we will deal between us more rightly and safely, and will find ways by which these difficulties may be relieved, and provision conveniently made for you and your affairs — so that you need not so soon return to your fatherland, or turn aside elsewhere. Meanwhile <b>I will let no occasion slip but that I promote you to all the best — not only by the intervening authority of Lord Coraduz and Lord Barvitius, but also by my own intercession with the Sacred Imperial Majesty — and succour your fluctuating and afflicted affairs. Nay, if all else (which yet I do not expect) prove vain, I myself will not fail you, but will show in the very effect that what I remember writing to you — while I still lingered near Hamburg, before we knew each other by face — is still ratified with me, and constantly kept.</b> Only do you hesitate at nothing, but confidently, as soon as you can, <b>fly hither</b>; then, in person, we will deal of all that is required more expeditiously, and more safely, than by letter; nor do I doubt that, when you arrive, you will find the way already prepared. Let this be the sum of [my] counsel — which too he who recommended you to me most approves.</p><p>I add no more now; for I do not wish to repeat what you touched on fully in both letters (since, as things stand, they conduce less to the promotion of the business), nor to add anything about the Mathematical matters you raised in both — since these are [for] another and greater leisure. Yet <b>the Solar eclipse, of which you make mention in your last, and to which you assign some observation: I communicate the calculation, from our renewed tables</b>, that it may be before your eyes, and you may compare it with your conception. <b>The magnitude of the eclipse here scarcely reached 5 digits, so that on that account it could not have exceeded 6 with you; I will grant that half a digit more of the Sun was covered there, on account of the smaller lunar parallax — but scarcely more.</b> This Eclipse was diligently observed by us before we left Benátky; <b>M. Christiernus [Longomontanus] was still present, who was afterward freely and liberally dismissed by me and went away to his fatherland.</b> Lord <b>von Tzernembl</b>, who gave you some hope five years ago, is here at Prague, summoned by the Emperor, and will perhaps stay here for a time, since they are quite … [<i>lacuna</i>] — which I thought should be indicated, lest you vainly expect help and promotion from him elsewhere. <b>I have transferred my Library and the instruments I had at Benátky hither, on Lord Barvitius's advice; for, so far as I understand, the Emperor desires that I dwell near by, and a convenient place, bordering the city, is now being deliberated</b> — about which, when you have come (for I do not doubt you will), you will find things not displeasing to you, as I hope.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague, the 28th day of August, 1600.<br>Most loving of you,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPTS. You make mention in your last letter that you are meditating an anti-apology against that Capnuranic Scot, especially now that you have read through our second book On the Comet; whereby you will have done a thing welcome to me, and answerable to your promise and to my expectation. You also engaged that you would refute the Mathematical matters found in that infamous writing of Ursus, in so far as they do not duly hold good — which too I am confident you will accomplish. But above all I should wish that the things which he objects, with painted falsehood and fraudulently, against our invention concerning the New Hypothesis — namely that it was taken over from Copernicus or from Apollonius of Perga — be refuted by you yet more clearly and fully than before, and that you would not find it troublesome to claim it for me, as is fitting, just as you have already done before with probable arguments. I have instituted a legal action against him, after I came hither to Prague, understanding that I must stay here for some time, and that there was danger in delay should he lie gravely ill; and I have obtained from the Imperial Majesty (however much certain men, as long as they could, threw in obstruction) that four Commissioners be deputed — two Barons and two Doctors of law — to pronounce sentence in this matter. For when he was sought out by my emissaries, he was unwilling to retract what he had written, but rather appealed to the law. But it came to pass that, at the very hour when the citation was to be served on him, he was extinguished [i.e. died]; yet nonetheless I shall prosecute the suit I have begun, since the litigation was, as it were, already joined, and the action is not wholly personal but also real, on account of the infamous book, which perpetually speaks in place of its author — that I may lawfully vindicate myself and mine from the calumnies with which it teems. And it has now been committed by the Emperor to the most illustrious Lord Archbishop, that he gather up all the copies found here and have them given to the fire, and punish the Printer. Lord Coraduz has also promised a decree to be promulgated by the Emperor, that throughout the whole Empire that book be annulled and rendered void, as infamous and scurrilous. And I have resolved afterward to publish this whole process in public, together with the Commissioners' sentence and the Imperial Majesty's decree, in a certain particular tract, which will grow to a moderate size. This too, by the Emperor's most clement consent and the approbation of Lord Coraduz, now clearly seen by me, I shall, God willing, do. And in the other part of it I shall reply to the things which are Mathematical and concern the Hypotheses; wherefore I should wish to obtain your conceptions about these in good time — or, if you come (as I hope) very speedily, it can be deferred to your arrival. Farewell. Given as above in the letter. T. B.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, at <b>Graz</b> (\"Mathematician of the Provincial Estates of Styria\"). <b>The formal letter calling Kepler to Prague after his Styrian exile</b> — the immediate precursor to <b>Kepler's reply of 17 Oct 1600 (brahe-t_16001017001)</b> and to his permanent arrival.</li><li><b>Significance — the sealing of the Tycho–Kepler collaboration (a keystone document).</b></li><li><b>The audiences with Rudolf II.</b> A vivid first-hand account: ~7 weeks after the Emperor returned from Pilsen, he summoned Tycho and <b>conversed with him in his chamber for over 1½ hours (+ ½ hour the same day)</b> about astronomy and Tycho's affairs, inviting him to <b>set his wishes on a paper for Barvitius</b>.</li><li><b>Securing Kepler's appointment.</b> Seizing the opening, Tycho told the Emperor <b>\"I alone do not suffice for so great a burden … I have need of collaborators,\"</b> and <b>\"discreetly added Kepler's name\"</b> — proposing to <b>summon Kepler from Styria and support him for a year or two</b>. The Emperor assented; Tycho then arranged with <b>Coraduz</b> for imperial letters to <b>Kepler's Prince (Württemberg) and the Styrian Estates</b>, asking that Kepler assist Tycho <b>two years</b> while keeping his Styrian stipend, <b>augmented by 100 florins</b> (\"all is dearer here than in Styria\").</li><li><b>The court delays.</b> The Emperor enjoined a <b>special task</b> on Tycho; lodged at the <b>Golden Griffin inn</b> (too noisy), Tycho <b>moved to a quieter lodging near Hoffmann</b>; he dares not press his own requests until the imperial task is done — hence the delay in answering Kepler.</li><li><b>The \"catastrophe.\"</b> Kepler's <b>second letter</b> brought the blow: <b>the Styrian salary withdrawn and exile imposed</b> (the 1600 Counter-Reformation expulsion), pushing Kepler toward Württemberg.</li><li><b>Tycho's warm pledge.</b> The heart of the letter: Tycho urges Kepler to <b>\"come hither, all delay set aside — alone or with your wife and goods,\"</b> promising patrons, loans, and intercession (Coraduz, Barvitius, the Emperor) — and, most movingly, <b>\"if all else fail, I myself will not fail you,\"</b> recalling the promise he wrote Kepler <b>\"while I still lingered near Hamburg, before we knew each other by face.\"</b> <i>\"Fly hither … you will find the way already prepared.\"</i></li><li><b>The July-1600 solar eclipse.</b> Tycho sends his <b>eclipse calculation from his \"renewed tables,\"</b> noting <b>~5 digits' magnitude at Prague (≤6 at Graz, +½ digit for smaller lunar parallax)</b>, observed at Benátky with <b>Longomontanus</b> (who has since \"been freely and liberally dismissed\" home — i.e. left Tycho's service).</li><li><b>Von Tzernembl &amp; the move.</b> Warns that <b>Georg Erasmus von Tzernembl</b> (a Kepler patron of five years before) is now at Prague; and reports the <b>library/instruments moved from Benátky to Prague</b>, with a <b>city-adjacent residence being arranged</b> (the coming Curtius house).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Tycho–Kepler convergence</b> keystone: read with <b>brahe-t_16001017001 (Kepler's 17 Oct reply)</b>; the April-1600 quarrel/reconciliation (brahe-t_16000408001, brahe-t_16000000001, brahe-t_16000803001); and the <b>same-day Herwart letter</b> behind the Kepler-on-Ursus transcript (brahe-t_16000911001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Johannes Kepler; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Barvitius</b>; <b>Coraduz</b>; <b>Johann Friedrich Hoffmann</b>; <b>Longomontanus</b> (<i>M. Christiernus</i>); <b>Georg Erasmus von Tzernembl</b>; the <b>Prince of Württemberg</b> and the <b>Styrian Estates</b>; \"he who recommended you to me\" (unnamed — Hoffmann/Herwart). Places: <b>Prague</b> (Golden Griffin inn), <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>, <b>Styria/Graz</b>, <b>Württemberg</b>, <b>Hamburg</b> (the early promise).</li><li><b>Text/calendar notes.</b> A short <b>lacuna</b> after \"since they are quite …\" (the Tzernembl sentence), flagged. The source's \"<i>die 28 Angusti</i>\" is a misprint for <i>Augusti</i> → <b>28 August 1600 [N.S.]</b> (slug/label agree). <i>catastrophe</i> = the unexpected reversal (a theatrical term).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> POST SCRIPTA (Kepler's planned antapologia vs the \"Capnuranic Scot\" Craig; the request to refute Ursus's maths + defend Tycho's hypothesis vs Copernicus/Apollonius; the 4-Commissioner lawsuit, Ursus dying at the citation-hour, the burn-order + Coraduz's Empire-wide annulment-decree, Tycho's plan to publish the process), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.343–344 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>audiences with Rudolf II</b>; the securing of the <b>two-year Kepler appointment via Coraduz/Barvitius with the 100-florin augmentation</b>; the court delays and the Golden-Griffin-to-Hoffmann move; the <b>Styrian-exile \"catastrophe\"</b>; Tycho's <b>urgent, generous summons and the \"I myself will not fail you\" pledge</b> recalling the Hamburg-era promise; the <b>July-1600 eclipse calculation</b> and Longomontanus's departure; and von Tzernembl + the library move) is firmly construed and dovetails with Kepler's reply of 17 October 1600. The single flagged element is the <b>lacuna</b> in the Tzernembl sentence.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, including the postscript, visually against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.340-344 (page images, not OCR). Checked opening/closing address, every proper name (Barvitius, Corraducius, Hoffmann, Christiernus, Tzernemel, Ursus, Copernicus, Apollonius Pergaeus), every number (7 weeks, 2 years, 100 florins, 2-3 weeks, 5/6/half-digit eclipse magnitude, 5 years, 4 commissioners = 2 barons + 2 doctors of law), and the closing date (28 August 1600); all match Dreyer exactly and are rendered without loss or invention. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_16000600001", "next": "brahe-t_16001017001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000405002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16001017001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains the delay in reply, reports being summoned from Benátky to Prague for a private audience with Emperor Rudolf II, and begins narrating what has since transpired.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000828001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000828002", "d": "1600-08-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg — Prague, 28 August 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (humanist), with Greek code-switches · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, Cod. 693 (folio)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Editorial note:</b> the working transcription carries one OCR oddity in the source block — <i>\"K0PLERUS\"</i> (with a numeral zero) for <b>Köplerus</b> = Kepler; reproduced as transcribed and corrected in translation/apparatus. Greek is reproduced as transcribed (<i>προσθαφαιρέσεις</i>, <i>φαινόμενα</i>, <i>πραγματεία</i>), the planet glyphs ☉ ☾ ♀ ☿ as printed. The dateline (<i>\"28 Augusti stylo novo Anno 1600\"</i>) and the catalogue label agree.</p>", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO IOHANNI GEORGIO HERWARTO AB HOHENBURG, SERENISSIMO UTRIUSQUE BAVARIAe DUCI A CONSILIJS INTIMIS, PRAeSIDI SCHWABAe ET STATUUM PROVINCIALIUM CANCELLARIO, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.<br>MONACHI.</p><p>TEMPUS est, Nobilissime et praestantissime vir, amice inprimis honorande, ut plurimis tuis inde ab initio huius anni ad me humanissime datis literis aliqua plenius, quam semel atque iterum factum est, respondeam, ne quid scrupuli aut suspicionis de meo erga te constanti amore haec nimia intermissio atque in rescribendo raritas suggerere poterit. Distuli autem hoc scribendi officium partim invite ob multiplices intervenientes alias curas et distractiones, partim data opera, ut ea, quae circa Lunaris hypotheseos restitutionem moliebar, interea ad finem exoptatum, aliquatenus saltem, si non integre, perduci, atque tua dubia in hoc sidere eo expeditius resolvi possent. Ut itaque hanc moram interlapsam, licet tam tua expectatione quam mea opinione iusto diuturniorem, in meliorem partem pro solito tuo candore interpreteris, te peramanter rogatum habeo. Quia vero multae et variae tuae, de quibus loquor, erant literae, et nimis longum foret, nec etiam admodum necessarium ad singula omnium contenta respondere, repetam saltem principaliora, et ea, quae in ijs negotium Astronomicum, de quo inter nos iucunde agitur, potissimum concernunt, solummodo attingam, praetermissis coeteris, quae librorum e Bibliotheca Serenissimi Principis vestri mecum communicatorum mentionem faciunt, de quibus postmodum summatim, siquidem nunc libros illos remittam, nonnulla subiungam.</p><p>Ad literas itaque primas die 10 Ianuarij scriptas sic habe. Proponis in ijs quatuor quaestiones, quarum prima est de parallaxibus Solis et Lunae, quomodo eae secundum meam mentem colligendae sint. Parallaxes Solis e Tomo primo Progymnasmatum tibi transmisso pag. 80 iuxta triplicem eius a Terra remotionem facile venabere. Lunarium vero parallaxium canonem manuscriptum existimo, cum coetera ad Lunae apparentias atque eclipses facientia impartirer, tunc adiunctum fuisse. Licet, ut dicam, quod res est, parallaxes Lunae adhuc quadam alia limitatione indigent, quam brevi quoque favente Numine adipisceris.</p><p>Secunda quaestio rationem aequationis temporis in Luna retro constituenda expetit, quod quidem praestari posset, si, qualis illa annua variatio, quam veteres non animadverterunt, tum temporis fuisset, et a quo puncto Zodiaci penderet, siquidem apogaeum Solis non respiciat, certo constaret. Verum ex quo in neoterica reformatione revolutionum Lunarium haec annua variatio, quae per se exigua est, et sextantem gradus modice excedit, alijs quibusdam medijs absorbetur, et aequationis temporis, quoad Lunam diversa quaedam tam ab hac priori, quam antecessorum forma, per ipsam experientiam in motu Lunae, alijs atque alijs anni temporibus multipliciter et sedulo explorata reparatur, iccirco supervacaneum duco de prioribus illis tanquam otiosis et a nobis iam antiquatis ulterius verba facere. Ubi coeteras in Luna reformationes acceperis, videbis annuae illi insinuationi subventum esse, nec novus ille aequandi temporis modus, sine quo coetera subsistere nequeunt, ibi praetermittetur, et tam nostris quam veterum temporibus adaptatus.</p><p>Tertio de longitudinibus locorum quorundam a me usurpatis certior fieri desideras, ut Regiomontis Prussiae, quam assumpsisse REINHOLDUS in catalogo eius locorum insignium depraehenditur, Par. 46 M. 45, licet id discrete indicare neglexerit, ego eandem totidem partium, prout tu quoque recte colligis, assumendam duco. Hierosolymarum longitudinem e PTOLEMAeI Geographia petendam existimo, quod loca in viciniore Palaestina illi rectius quam alijs patuerint. Romae longitudinem COPERNICUS gradibus 5 Cracovia occidentaliorem facit, sicque evaderet ea part. 40½, quod tamen nimium esse suspicor, ego vix 39° attingere, aut certe non multum excedere crediderim. Negotium hoc de longitudinibus locorum non parum habet perplexitatis, cum observationes eclipsium Lunarium in varijs hinc inde versus ortum et occasum remotis Regionibus diligentissime acceptae sufficienter in promptu non sint. Quae ignoratio Astronomiae quoque, praesertim quoad Lunam, nonnihil officit. Nos qua fieri potuit circumspectione praecipuas Europae civitates in competentem dispositionem redegimus, ut suo tempore videbis, correctis etiam, quantum licuit, earundem latitudinibus, in quibus, cum facilime pateant, pudor est, tantopere nihilominus communiter aberrari, veluti REINHOLDUS, qui Regiomontis latitudinem ex APIANO 26 M. iusto minorem mutuatus est. Quin et ipse APIANUS elevationem Augustae Vindelicorum 5 Minutis e sua, uti puto, observatione iusto decliviorem fecit, et CYPRIANUS adhuc plus, nempe quinta parte gradus defecit.</p><p>Ad quartum quaesitum scias tabulam prosthaphaereseωn Lunarium, quam postea iuxta priorem hypothesin supputari feci, non multum differre ab altera Hamburgi minus diligenter impressa, nisi quod quaedam perperam istic duobus in locis excusa emendet, et omnia scrupulosius, utpote singulis secundis, exhibeat. At cum tabula illa per omnia apparentijs non satis praecise correspondeat, siquidem ob conturbationes rerum mearum in Dania factas, dum peregre sum, tam cito revideri et castigari non potuerit, quod nunc concesso meliori ocio tandem effectui daturum spero, cumque redintegratas illas tam aequalium quam inaequalium motuum Lunae tabulas tecum, ubi penitus absolutae fuerint, communicare decreverim, quorsum opus circa has exauthoratas pluribus occupari? Quis enim Cerere reperta glande vescitur?</p><p>Alterae tuae literae die 16 Ianuarij exaratae de Triangulorum compendiosa per προσθαφαιρέσεις resolutione solerter agunt, et a nostris ratiocinijs non admodum aliena habent, quare ijs diutius non immorubor, sed pergam potius ad eas, quae octavo Februarij scriptae sunt, praetermissis duabus intermediantibus ob causam superius dictam. Agis inibi de calculo locorum Lunae ad tres dies in libro nostro Epistolarum pag. 56 inter reliquos Planetas assignatorum, quodque ea ex observatione petita Tabulis nostris non satis consentiant, inducis. Fateor quidem id aliquatenus sic esse, non tamen tanta invenitur discrepantia, quantam tua supputatio mihi transmissa suggerit, uti ex nostra, quam apposui, repetitione liquet, duobus posterioribus locis satis convenientibus, et primo saltem illo nonnihil exorbitante, nam ut minutula quaedam praeteream, ubique differentiam illam, loco Lunae, respectu polorum Eclipticae per Instrumenta observato, et illi, qui in propria orbita existit, quem calculus solummodo praebet, varie intercidentem negligis: forte quod tabellam diversitatem hanc ostendentem cum prioribus non acceperis, quam, ut tibi postmodum ad manus sit, hîc additam habes, siquidem renovatis etiam tabulis, quas alias, ut promisi, obtinebis, aeque applicari poterit. Quomodo autem repurgati hi canones loca Lunae, tribus illis diebus satis praecise convenientia, expediant, in alia charta denotatum invenies, idque quoad solas longitudines. Nam in latitudinibus ex observationibus salvandis, et parallaxibus, quae sese immiscent, extricandis, etiamnum laboramus. Neque tamen in latitudinibus a priori ordinatione evidens discrimen subrepere poterit. Quam vero priores illae apparentijs congruerint, tute satis expertus es, et quam proxime in tuo calculo assecutus, ut hac de re frustra moveatur dubitatio, nisi quis velit scrupulosius, quam sensus oculorum fert, singula trutinando nodum in scirpo quaerere.</p><p>Quas postea die 20 Februarij ad me misisti literas, restitutionem quarundam peculiarium affixarum a CHRISTOPHORO ROTHMANNO Landtgraviano olim Mathematico adiunctam habuere, cuius communicatio mihi grata fuit; licet enim ab ipso laudatissimae memoriae Principe, dum adhuc in vivis erat, mihi aliquot inerrantium siderum ipsius Instrumentis facta verificatio transmissa sit, ut eam per triangula expenderem atque cum meis pariter inventis conferrem, quemadmodum in Epistolis inter nos commutatis videre est: tamen non tot erant numero stellae, neque tam diligenter in trutinam Triangulorum et calculi vocatae. Ex ijs, quae nos capite secundo Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum circa fixarum restitutionem tractavimus, nostras rationes, et quanta diligentia hanc provinciam susceperimus et administraverimus, perspicies, collatione autem inde cum hisce Landtgravianis longitudinibus facta, eas ubique 5 vel 6 scrupulis nostras excedere animadvertes, nec in latitudinibus maiorem esse differentiam, quam haec in longitudinibus varietas proxime postulat. Qua vero occasione factum existimem, quod Landtgravianae observationes stellarum longitudines per unciam circiter unius gradus plus iusto extenderint, in dicto Progymnasmatum capite pag. 274 reperies, et alias in Epistolis hac de re inter nos affatim ventilatum est. Non est quod tibi persuadeas, ROTHMANNUM unquam hanc stellarum verificationem suo nomine in publicum editurum, nam animum ab his studijs iam prorsus abiecit, et per procurationes forenses victum quaerit, quatenus adversa valetudo, qua miserrime conflictatur, id admittit, nec scio, an adhuc superstes sit. Nam cum ultimo ad me de eo scriberetur, ipsum cum extremo sanitatis et rei familiaris dispendio vitam aegerrime trahere relatum est. Quare non opus est, ut verearis, tibi aliquando imputari posse, quod huius fixarum ab eo peractae emendationis me participem reddideris, nisi id celâro, quod tamen nihilominus libenter facio, licet, ut dicam id quod censeo, eius rectificatio parum mihi prodesse poterit, cum iam diu ante meas circa fixarum correctionem lucubrationes consignârim, et pro maiori parte typis imprimi curârim, uti liber, quem habes, ostendit. Supplevi postea plures, quo numerus millenarius consummaretur. Quam millenarum stellarum exactam restitutionem Augustissimo Imperatori RUDOLPHO, Domino nostro Clementissimo, in membrana descriptam ante annum, cum primum Pragam venissem, humilime obtuli.</p><p>Postea in ijsdem literis de resolutione Triangulorum per προσθα‐φαιρέσεις recte quidem infers, quod non multum contineat facilitatis atque compendij, si de planis solummodo intelligis: attamen in sphaericis non contemnendam suppeditat promptitudinem, et citius quam vulgaris fert methodus, calculum absolvit. Quin etiam in ipsis Planis Triquetris non tantum, uti existimas, a scope exacto deflectitur, si subtilissime ex integro canone RHETICI alicubi, prout opus est, emendato, omnia perficiantur, veluti reiterata tua exempla in hîc praesenti quadam charta te monere poterint; nuspiam enim 4 vel 5 scrupul. secund. quae canonis insufficientia excusat, quaeque etiam per se non sunt alicuius momenti, deviatio committitur.</p><p>Attingis insuper ibidem sex illas in Luna animadversiones, quas praefato Progymnasmatum libro sub calce capitis sexti assignavi, easque cum calculo e tabulis Lunae tibi antea exhibitis confers, adiuncto tuae numerationis epilogismo, quem postquam introspexissem, animadverti, utut non modicam ibidem adhibueris diligentiam, nonnunquam tamen id, quod intendebas, non satis assecutum, quemadmodum recognita eorundem nostra via computatio, quam tribus prioribus ex Epistolis recensitis calculationibus subiunxi, te in prima pagina, haec, uti dispicies, continente, edocebit, licet inficiari non possum, calculum hunc, ex primis Lunae tabulis quacunque sedulitate elaboratum, in quibusdam, praesertim postremis duobus, in Octobri anni 1586 factis denotationibus, nimium exorbitare, cui tamen enormitati posterior in Lunae curriculo adhibita correctio ita medetur, ut nihil dubij, quod mentionem mereatur, relinquat, veluti in secunda pagina, tribus illis ex Epistolarum libro sumptis Lunae locis et ad renovatum calculum expensis, haec quoque sex, quae Progymnasmatum liber suppeditat, ad normam eiusdem reformatae supputationis, sub incudem numerorum revocantur. Ubi tamen id considerandum venit, quod ea, quae etiamnum ibi residua videtur ab annotatione in libro Progymnasmatum facta discrepantiola, quae tamen nusquam tria vel quatuor minuta excedit, non ita stricte accipienda sit, ac si tabulae neotericae tantundem omnino a coelitus facta observatione aberret. Nam loca longitudinis Lunae in dicto libro apposita vix sunt exquisita scrupulositate ex observationibus, quae etiam non ubique sufficientes in promptu fuêre, ita recensita, ut pro 3 vel 4 scrupulis quis fidem interponere possit, quod et revisae observationes atque subtilius castigatae, sicubi ad adscriptum tempus in voluminibus observationum nostrarum inveniuntur, satis probant, prout etiam a nobis ijs in locis, ubi opus fuit, ad memoratam paginam subnotatum est. Haud enim duxi necessarium praecisionem quoad longitudinem Lunae istic proponere, cum parallaxes solummodo scrutari intentio nostra foret. Et sane si dextre ab ijs, qui in observationum Astronomicarum palaestra diu multumque exercitati sunt, omnia secundum rei exigentiam et difficultatem aequa lance expendantur, non invite fatebuntur, quod trium vel quatuor minutorum, imo etiam duodecimae partis unius gradus in hac pragmatείa disconvenientia nostram determinationem convellere nequeat. Cum enim Luna non tam splendidum et notabile vibret lumen atque Sol, quo in Instrumentis per umbram aut illuminationem pinnacidiorum centraliter citra omne dubium discerni possit, longe difficilius quam ☉ aut etiam coetera astra, quae per rimulas dioptrales satis praecise denotantur, observationi subtiliori patet, unde fit, quod marginem aliquem Lunae praesertim maxime plenum inter observandum collimari oporteat, ubi haec etiam difficultas incidit, quod vix dirimere detur, an quis plus vel minus iusto circa limbi illius contactum solummodo dignoscendum collineet, alijs atque alijs observatoribus pro diverso acumine visus non in eodem puncto scopum petitum attingentibus. Taceo nunc, quod parallaxes Lunae eius observationem reddant magis lubricam, quam in ullo alio sidere, et licet hae quoad longitudinem iuxta nonagesimum Eclipticae ab horizonte gradum praecaveri queant, tamen id non diu durat, et rarissime per serenitatem ob nubes et alia impedimenta tales occasiones suppetunt. Et quorsum quaeso maior scrupulositas, quam quae facile in sensus cadit, hîc solicite sectanda? Cum ea omnia, quae ex motu Lunae emendato deducuntur, si per experientiam ponderanda sint, sensuum adminiculo indigeant, qui pariter ut prius circa minima et imperceptibilia hallucinari possunt. Haec vero ob id eo fusius inculco, ne quis sibi polliceatur, tam exactam undiquaque certitudinem in Lunae apparente motu obviam esse atque circa Solem et reliquos Planetas, fixasque stellas, modo debita adhibeatur diligentia, praestari poterit. Interim tamen non ferendum duco, ut nimis lato modo, utpote in sexta vel quarta solummodo gradus parte, Lunae exponantur φαινόμενα, prout veteres, qui ad maiorem praecisionem vix aspirare ausi sunt, admiserunt, imo usitatas tabulas, sive Alphonsinas sive Copernicaeas, aut quascunque alias assumpserimus, intra unum aut sesquialterum gradum subinde Lunae loca coelo correspondentia vix praebere. Veruntamen hoc insuper admonendum duco, quod etiamsi Luna non tam subtiliter per Instrumenta observabilis sit, ut de centri eius situ exactissimo nihil prorsus dubij relinquatur, tamen, quantum ad Eclipses attinet, modo eae per ingressum et egressum umbrae terrenae accurate denotentur, promptius patent, et inaequalitas illa, quae circa novam et plenam contingit, simplicior est, nec tam varijs anfractibus obnoxia, atque ea, quae iuxta alias digressiones fit, in quibus maior quidem subest perplexitas, quam ulli veterum aut recentium Astronomorum (absit invidia dicto) hactenus vel semel suspicari potuerint, nedum ut ei sufficienter per oportunam hypothesin et hinc deductam numerationem providere sustinuerint. Licet vero haec omnia ita se habeant, polliceri nihilominus ausim, Lunae cursum in tabulas recentiores a nobis digestum exactius apparentijs satisfacturum, quam cuivis per Instrumenta facile assequi in proclive est, siquidem non nisi a selectissimis et minime dubijs observationibus per annos plures quam 20 exanthlatis derivatus, idque Instrumentis minime fallacibus, quae imitari paucissimis conceditur. Atque haec de Lunae motu observando examinandoque satis superque dicta sunto.</p><p>Quantum epochas mediorum motuum in hac ipsa Luna ad tempora PTOLEMAeI retro constituendas, quarum in ijsdem etiam literis mentionem facis, concernit, scias eam in his denotationem, quae tibi transmissa est, non fuisse genuinam, sed per incuriam nescio quam ex antiquata et reiecta quadam assumptione, a quodam studiosorum meorum descriptam, cum verior illa ad manus non esset, atque ea, quam pro rata habui, neque ijs, quos accepisti, neque Prutenicis numeris consentit, quam modo substituere minus necessarium duco, siquidem tota illa prior ratio Lunaris circuitionis, aptiore nunc inventa, a nobis repudiatur. In hac vero posteriore redintegratione, ubi eam adeptus fueris, epochas ad PTOLOMAeI tempora correctas unâ obtinebis.</p><p>Pro libris praestantissimi illius Mathematici FRANCISCI VIETAe, quorum simul cum literis die 26 Aprilis mihi inscriptis copiam fecisti, gratias ago plurimas. Retulit ante annum mihi, cum Pragae unâ essemus, ADRIANUS ROMANUS, insignis etiam Mathematicus, eundem VIETAM de Triangulorum compendijs peculiaribus nonnulla in publicum edidisse, quae videre averem. Pollicitus is quidem est, se mihi exemplar missurum, sed necdum respondit eventus.</p><p>Ad eas, quas huc nono Maij direxisti, non opus arbitror multis respondere, cum praecedentia sufficiant. Id saltem addere lubet, mihi nequaquam esse ingratum accuratum tuum illud studium in disquirendis subtiliterque ponderandis ijs, quae a nobis circa Lunam prius elaborata erant, imo cuperem ulterius, tam in his quam alijs, idipsum pari diligentia a te fieri, et sicubi aliquid dubij inciderit, ingenue me admoneri, plus enim saepe vident oculi quam oculus, et te candide agere veritatis solius eruendae amore, non ut cum praeiudicio quodam nostra insecteris, prout plaerique scioli facere praesumunt, sufficienter perspectum habeo. Tentavit etiam excellens ille Italorum Mathematicus ANTONIUS MAGINUS, in Luna, nonnulla e quaternione illo de motu eius Hamburgi impresso, quem a quodam meo domestico in Italiam profecto, me inscio, adeptus est, idque etiam bona, ut ex literis eius apparet, intentione fecit, sed non pari tecum felicitate metam petitam attigit, veluti patet ex calculo, quem literis suis inclusum ante aliquot hebdomadas ad me misit, cuius copiam unâ facio.</p><p>Huc usque scripseram, vel potius amanuensi meo dictâram, antequam Benaticâ ante duos circiter Menses discederem, et constitueram non saltem hîc Pragae has ipsas literas supplere, et quaedam alia adiungere, ac calculum, quem mitto, revidere, et in quibusdam etiam explicare, antequam eas ad te mitterem, prout in brevioribus illis, quas (cum ante mensem libros communicatos restituerem) ad te dedi, me pollicitum existimo. Verum cum indies nova me occupent negocia, ut id, etiamsi quam maxime vellem, satis cito expedire nequeam (habeo insuper quaedam prae manibus pro Caesarea Maiestate, Domino nostro clementissimo, peragenda, quae non sinunt me alijs sedulo attendere, priusquam illa absoluta fuerint) cogor hasce utut immaturas et incompletas ad te dirigere, quo pollicitationi meae vel aliqua saltem ex parte, si secundum totum nunc non liceat, satisfiat. Adiunxi ea, de quibus inibi mentio facta est, quatenus nunc inveniri poterant: nam amanuensis ille, qui et antecedentia scripserat, et quae apponenda erant, conservârat, non amplius apud me est, sed me inscio, postquam huc Pragam veni, contra datam fidem, nescio equidem qua de causa, ab alio, uti opinor, instigatus, se subduxit, et ad Witebergenses, ubi officium aliquod scholasticum sperat, redijt: unde vereor omnia, prout oportuit, hîc addita esse, imo an ea ipsa, quae adiunxi, recte se habeant, et satis accurate consignata sint, ambigo; neque enim recognoscendi modo suppetit otium. Tuum itaque erit omnia circumspecte expendere, et sicubi opus fuerit, castigare, quod pro tuo in hisce exacto iudicio non difficulter praestiteris. Cuperem vero vel has ipsas, vel earum exemplar (nullum enim mihi ad manus est) remitti, ut desiderata per meliorem oportunitatem suppleam, et si quae in annectendis omissa aut minus elaborate peracta sunt, resarciam et perfectiora reddam.</p><p>Ad ♀ stellam olim iuxta ALBINOVANI Poëtae opinionem in aurora non apparentem, qua de re denuo in ultimis tuis quaestionem movisti, ut multa nunc respondeam, temporis angustia impedit, nec etiam aliae nunc succurrunt rationes, quam eae, quas ante annum ad te perscripsi. Licet enim in novitio tuo calculo, praesertim ad primum tempus coniunctionis ♀ et ☿, latitudinis diversitas non tanta sit, quanta antea admissa est (neque enim hîc dimidium gradum attingit, quem PTOLOMAeUS non semper hac in parte assecutus est, nedum eius imitator COPERNICUS) attamen quomodocunque coniungantur ♀ et ☿ etiam in ipso longitudinis et latitudinis minuto, haec ab ipso, ut plane non appareat, obscurari nequit, cum unum luminosum corpus alterum non obfuscet, nec visibilis diameter ☿ij tanta est, ut id efficere possit, quemadmodum iam dudum monui. Si itaque citra Poëtarum Privilegia Poëta is veridicus est, crediderim potius ♀ ob exhalationes et meteora quaedam candida aërique similima obvelatam delituisse, praesertim cum 8 saltem gradibus a ☉ elongaretur, ideoque radijs eius quodammodo obnoxia, minusque ob diem illucescentem conspicua; sed de his tute rectius dispicies. Addam SCALIGERI literas nonnulla in fine de hoc ipso negotio continentes, si exemplum satis cito obtinuero.</p><p>Fuit apud me Benaticae vere praeterito M. IOHANNES K0PLERUS, insignis apud Styriacos Mathematicus, cum quo tibi etiam per literas familiaritatem intercedere ab ipso cognovi. Retulit vero inter alia se aliquando nonnulla tibi scripsisse, de Hypothesibus meis in controversiam vocatis, atque quod eae nec in COPERNICO, nec APOLLONIO PERGAeO, ut quidam (iam ante 14 dies mortuus) praetendit, invenirentur, ostendisse, atque certis indicijs non alterius, sed meas esse collegisse. Rogo itaque peramanter, velis eas KÖPLERI literas mecum aliquantisper communicare, aut saltem ea verba, quae hoc negocium concernunt, inde transsumpta et tuae manus subscriptione in veritatis testimonium ratificata transmittere, idque per primam Postam a vobis huc redeuntem, et Agenti (uti vocant) Principis vestri id, quicquid erit, mihi tradendum committere. Feceris mihi sic rem apprime gratam; incumbit enim nunc aliqualis necessitas, cur sic fieri averem, de qua alias rescisces.</p><p>Invitârat nuper Serenissimi Principis vestri Legatus me ad coenam, a quo humanissime et lautissime exceptus fui, et crebra tui, eaque (uti decuit) honorifica facta est inter nos mentio. Quamprimum ea, quae nunc pro Caesare elaboro, parata fuerint, ut liberior esse queam, constitui illum vicissim ad me invitare, siquidem quod accessum non sit detrectaturus, iam benigne mihi addixerit. Tunc plenius de te colloquemur, et nonnulla ex ijs, quae inter nos mutuis literis ventilata sunt, ipsi ostendam, unâ cum Instrumentis quibusdam e meis, quae nunc partim Benaticâ, Caesare sic volente, huc allata sunt, partim (uti spero) brevi aderint, ut de ijs domum reversus ad te referat: quamvis potissima eorum pars, unâ cum reliqua mea suppellectile adhuc Magdeburgi detineatur, sumptibus tamen Reverendi eius loci Capituli, in gratiam Caesareae Matis huc per Albim (nisi ob aestum continuum nimis exsiccatus fuerit) quam citissime fieri poterit, advehenda. Quae hoc tempore tibi scribenda insinuandaque duxi, utque omnia candide accipias, atque mature expectationi nostrae respondeas, te etiam atque etiam rogatum habeo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae die 28 Augusti stylo novo Anno 1600.<br>Magnificentiae Tuae<br>studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 353; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Si aliquos ex libris in scheda pariter adiuncta denotatis successive mihi mutuo mittere possis, feceris sic non minus quam antea rem acceptissimam. Restituam eos bona fide, ut etiam in prioribus factum. Habes insuper hic Calculum Eclipseos Solaris, e novitijs meis tabulis supputatum. Inde facile colliges, paulo aliam esse in Lunae motu postea adinventam rationem, quam prior illa tecum antea communicata. Observata est Solis altitudo ad initium obscurationis P. 60 M. 43 in elevatione Poli P. 50 M. 4½ iuxta meam animadversionem. Hinc tempus inquire ac cum nostris numeris confer. Hic autem Pragae haec, de qua loquor, facta est observatio per Quadrantem quendam orichalcicum Generosi Domini IOH. FRIDERICI HOFFMANNI singula minuta exhibentem, et ad imitationem cuiusdam ex meis, qui in Mechanica Astron. numero est tertius, elaboratum. Si quid apud vos in eadem Eclipsi caelitus denotatum est, fac me eius animadversionis participem.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Lord Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg, privy councillor to the most Serene Duke of both Bavarias, President of [the margraviate of] Burgau in Swabia and Chancellor of the provincial Estates, his especially-to-be-honoured friend.</b><br>At Munich.</p><p>It is time, most noble and most excellent man, friend especially to be honoured, that I reply somewhat more fully than has been done once or twice, to your many letters most kindly sent me since the beginning of this year — lest this excessive interruption, and rarity in replying, could suggest any scruple or suspicion about my constant love toward you. I deferred this duty of writing partly unwillingly, on account of the manifold other cares and distractions intervening; partly on purpose, so that the things I was working at concerning the <b>restitution of the lunar hypothesis</b> might meanwhile be brought to the wished-for end — at least in some part, if not entirely — and your doubts in this star [the Moon] could thereby be resolved the more expeditiously. And so I most lovingly ask you to interpret this delay that has slipped in — though longer than is fair, both by your expectation and by my own opinion — in the better part, according to your wonted candour. But since your letters of which I speak were many and various, and it would be too long, nor indeed quite necessary, to respond to each of the contents of all, I shall at least take up again the more principal points, and shall touch only on those things in them which chiefly concern the astronomical business of which we treat pleasantly between us; passing over the rest, which make mention of the books communicated to me from the Library of your most Serene Prince, about which I shall afterwards add a few things summarily, since I am now returning those books.</p><p>As to the first letter, then, written on 10 January, take it thus. In it you propose four questions, of which the first is about the <b>parallaxes of the Sun and Moon</b>, how they are to be gathered according to my mind. The Sun's parallaxes you will easily hunt out from the first volume of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> sent to you, page 80, according to its threefold distance from the Earth. But the manuscript canon [table] of the lunar parallaxes I think was attached at the time when I shared the other things conducing to the Moon's appearances and eclipses. Although — to say what the truth is — the Moon's parallaxes still need a certain further limitation, which you too will shortly obtain, God favouring.</p><p>The second question seeks the method of the <b>equation of time</b> in the Moon, to be established retrospectively — which indeed could be done, if it were certainly settled what that <b>annual variation</b> (which the ancients did not notice) was at that time, and from what point of the Zodiac it depended, since it does not regard the Sun's apogee. But since, in the modern reformation of the lunar revolutions, this annual variation — which is in itself slight, and modestly exceeds a sixth of a degree [≈10′] — is absorbed by certain other means; and since the equation of time, as regards the Moon, is repaired by a form different both from this earlier one and from that of our predecessors — a form explored by experience itself in the Moon's motion, manifoldly and diligently, at various and various seasons of the year — therefore I consider it superfluous to make further words about those earlier matters, as idle and now antiquated by us. When you have received the other reformations in the Moon, you will see that that annual insinuation has been provided for; nor will that new mode of equating time — without which the rest cannot stand — be passed over there; and [it is] adapted both to our times and to those of the ancients.</p><p>Thirdly, you desire to be informed about the <b>longitudes of certain places</b> used by me — such as Königsberg in Prussia, which Reinhold is found to have assumed at 46° 45′ in his catalogue of notable places (although he neglected to indicate it distinctly): I judge it is to be assumed at the same number of degrees, as you too rightly gather. The longitude of Jerusalem I think must be sought from Ptolemy's <i>Geography</i>, because the places in nearer Palestine were known to him more correctly than to others. Copernicus makes the longitude of Rome 5 degrees more westerly than Krakow, and thus it would come out at 40½°, which however I suspect to be too much; I should believe it scarcely reaches 39°, or certainly does not much exceed [it]. This business of the longitudes of places has not a little perplexity, since observations of lunar eclipses, most diligently taken in various regions remote on this side and that, toward east and west, are not sufficiently at hand. Which ignorance also somewhat hinders Astronomy, especially as regards the Moon. We, with what circumspection could be used, have reduced the chief cities of Europe into a competent arrangement, as you will see in due time, having also corrected their latitudes as far as was permitted — in which [latitudes], since they lie open most easily, it is a shame that people nonetheless commonly err so greatly: as Reinhold, who borrowed from Apian the latitude of Königsberg 26 minutes less than is just. Nay, even Apian himself made the elevation [latitude] of Augsburg 5 minutes lower than is just, from his own observation, as I think; and Cyprian [Leovitius] yet more, namely fell short by a fifth part of a degree [12′].</p><p>To the fourth query, know that the <b>table of lunar prosthaphaereses</b>, which I afterwards had computed according to the earlier hypothesis, does not differ much from the other one printed less carefully at Hamburg — except that it corrects certain things wrongly printed there in two places, and exhibits everything more scrupulously, namely down to single seconds. But since that table does not correspond precisely enough to the appearances in all respects — seeing that, on account of the disturbances of my affairs made in Denmark while I am abroad, it could not be revised and corrected so quickly (which I hope now, better leisure being granted, to bring at last into effect) — and since I have resolved to communicate to you those restored tables, both of the equal and of the unequal motions of the Moon, when they shall be entirely completed: to what end is there need to be occupied at length with these discredited ones? For who, when grain has been found, feeds on acorns?</p><p>Your other letter, written on 16 January, deals skilfully with the compendious <b>resolution of Triangles by <i>prosthaphaereses</b></i>, and has things not very alien from our reasonings; wherefore I shall not dwell longer on them, but shall rather proceed to those written on the eighth of February, passing over the two intervening ones for the reason said above. In it you treat of the calculation of the Moon's places for three days, assigned among the other Planets on page 56 in our book of Letters [the 1596 <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i>], and you bring forward that those [places], sought from observation, do not agree well enough with our Tables. I confess indeed that it is somewhat so; yet not so great a discrepancy is found as your computation sent to me suggests, as is clear from our recalculation, which I have appended — the two latter places agreeing well enough, and only that first one deviating somewhat. For (to pass over certain tiny matters) everywhere you neglect that difference, variously falling between the Moon's place as observed by Instruments with respect to the poles of the Ecliptic, and that place which exists on its proper orbit, which the calculation alone supplies — perhaps because you did not receive, along with the earlier [materials], the little table showing this diversity, which you have added here, so that it may afterwards be at hand for you; since it can equally be applied to the renewed tables too, which you will otherwise obtain, as I promised. But how these purged canons dispatch the Moon's places, agreeing precisely enough on those three days, you will find noted on another sheet — and that, as regards longitudes only. For in saving the latitudes from the observations, and in extricating the parallaxes that mingle themselves in, we still labour. Nor, however, will an evident discrepancy be able to creep into the latitudes from the earlier arrangement. But how closely those earlier [tables] agreed with the appearances, you have yourself sufficiently experienced, and how nearly you attained it in your calculation — so that on this matter doubt is moved in vain, unless someone wishes, weighing each point more scrupulously than the sense of the eyes bears, to seek <i>a knot in a bulrush</i>.</p><p>The letter which you afterwards sent me on the 20th of February had attached a restitution of certain particular fixed stars by <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>, formerly the Landgrave's Mathematician, whose communication was welcome to me. For although a verification of several of the fixed stars, made with his [the Landgrave's] own Instruments, was transmitted to me by the most praiseworthy-memory Prince himself while he was still alive — that I might weigh it by triangles and compare it equally with my own findings, as may be seen in the Letters exchanged between us — yet the stars were not so many in number, nor so diligently called into the balance of Triangles and of calculation. From the things which we treated in the second chapter of the Astronomical <i>Progymnasmata</i> concerning the restitution of the fixed stars, you will perceive our methods, and with how great diligence we undertook and administered this province [task]; and from a comparison made thence with these Landgravian longitudes, you will observe that they everywhere exceed ours by 5 or 6 minutes, and that there is no greater difference in the latitudes than this variation in the longitudes closely requires. But by what occasion I think it came about that the Landgravian observations extended the stars' longitudes by about a twelfth of a degree more than is just, you will find in the said chapter of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, page 274; and this matter has elsewhere been amply ventilated between us in the Letters. There is no reason for you to persuade yourself that Rothmann will ever publish this verification of the stars under his own name, for he has now wholly cast his mind off from these studies, and seeks his living through forensic agency-work, insofar as the adverse health with which he is most wretchedly afflicted allows it; nor do I know whether he is still alive. For when last it was written to me about him, it was reported that he drags out his life most painfully, with the utmost loss of health and of family fortune. Wherefore there is no need for you to fear that it could ever be imputed to you that you made me privy to this emendation of the fixed stars performed by him — except [on condition] that I keep it quiet, which nonetheless I willingly do; although, to say what I judge, his rectification will be able to profit me little, since I long ago set down my studies concerning the correction of the fixed stars, and had them for the greater part printed in type, as the book you have shows. I afterwards supplied more, so that the number of a thousand might be completed. Which exact restitution of a thousand stars, described on parchment, I humbly offered to the most August Emperor <b>Rudolf</b>, our most Clement Lord, a year ago, when I had first come to Prague.</p><p>Afterwards in the same letter, concerning the <b>resolution of Triangles by <i>prosthaphaereses</b></i>, you rightly infer that it contains not much of ease and compendium, if you understand it only of plane [triangles]; yet in spherical [triangles] it supplies a readiness not to be despised, and dispatches the calculation more quickly than the common method bears. Nay, even in plane triangles themselves there is not so great a deflection from the exact mark as you think, if everything be perfected most subtly from the complete canon [trigonometric table] of <b>Rheticus</b>, corrected here and there where need is — as your reiterated examples, on a certain sheet here present, can advise you; for nowhere is a deviation committed of [more than] 4 or 5 seconds, which the insufficiency of the canon excuses, and which moreover are in themselves of no moment.</p><p>You touch, moreover, there upon <b>those six observations in the Moon</b> which I assigned in the aforesaid book of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> at the foot of the sixth chapter, and you compare them with the calculation from the Moon's tables exhibited to you earlier, with the epilogism [summary computation] of your numeration attached. After I had examined it, I observed that, although you applied no small diligence there, nevertheless you sometimes did not sufficiently attain what you intended — as the computation of the same by our method, reviewed (which I subjoined to the three earlier calculations reviewed from the Letters), will teach you on the first page, which contains these, as you will discern. Yet I cannot deny that this calculation, elaborated from the first lunar tables with all diligence, in some points — especially the last two, among the markings made in October of the year 1586 — deviates too much; which enormity, however, the later correction applied in the Moon's course so remedies that it leaves no doubt worth mention: as on the second page, where, with those three places of the Moon taken from the book of Letters and weighed against the renewed calculation, these six too which the book of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> supplies are recalled, under the anvil of numbers, to the norm of the same reformed computation. Here, however, this comes to be considered: that the little discrepancy which still seems there to remain from the annotation made in the book of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> — which nonetheless nowhere exceeds three or four minutes — is not to be taken so strictly, as if the modern tables strayed altogether by just that much from observation made from the heavens. For the places of the Moon's longitude set down in the said book are scarcely reviewed with exquisite scrupulosity from observations (which moreover were not everywhere sufficiently at hand) in such a way that one could pledge faith for 3 or 4 minutes — as both the revised observations, and those more subtly corrected, wherever they are found for the noted time in the volumes of our observations, sufficiently prove; just as it has also been noted by us, in those places where it was needful, against the mentioned page. For I did not deem it necessary to propose precision as regards the Moon's longitude there, since our intention was to scrutinize the parallaxes only. And surely, if everything be weighed in an even balance — according to the matter's exigency and difficulty — by those who have long and much exercised themselves in the wrestling-school of astronomical observations, they will not unwillingly confess that a disagreement of three or four minutes — nay, even of a twelfth part of a degree — in this practical undertaking cannot overturn our determination. For since the Moon does not dart so splendid and notable a light as the Sun, whereby in the Instruments it could be discerned centrally, beyond all doubt, by the shadow or illumination of the sights [<i>pinnacidia</i>], it lies open to subtler observation far more difficultly than the Sun, or even than the other stars, which are denoted precisely enough through the dioptral slits; whence it comes about that some margin of the Moon, especially when most full, must be sighted in observing — where this difficulty too occurs, that it can scarcely be determined whether one sights more or less than is just about the mere discerning of that limb's contact, different observers, according to the diverse acuteness of their sight, not attaining the sought mark at the same point. I pass over now that the Moon's parallaxes render its observation more slippery than in any other star; and although these [parallaxes], as regards longitude, can be guarded against near the ninetieth degree of the Ecliptic from the horizon [the nonagesimal], yet that does not last long, and most rarely, on account of clouds and other impediments, do such occasions present themselves during clear weather. And to what end, I ask, should a greater scrupulosity than easily falls under the senses be anxiously pursued here? — since all those things which are deduced from the Moon's corrected motion, if they are to be weighed by experience, need the help of the senses, which, just as before, can be deceived about the least and imperceptible things. But I inculcate these things the more fully for this reason: lest anyone promise himself that so exact a certainty on every side is to be met with in the Moon's apparent motion as can be furnished about the Sun, and the rest of the Planets, and the fixed stars, provided due diligence be applied. Meanwhile, however, I deem it not to be borne that the Moon's <i>phaenomena</i> should be set forth in too broad a manner — namely within only a sixth or fourth part of a degree — as the ancients, who scarcely dared aspire to greater precision, admitted; nay, [I deem it not to be borne] that the customary tables, whether Alphonsine or Copernican, or whatever others we take up, scarcely give the Moon's places corresponding to heaven within one or one-and-a-half degrees from time to time. Nevertheless I deem this must moreover be advised: that even though the Moon is not so subtly observable by Instruments that nothing of doubt at all is left about the most exact position of its centre, yet, as regards Eclipses — provided they be accurately marked by the ingress and egress of the earthly shadow — they lie open more readily; and that inequality which happens about the new and full [moon] is simpler, and not subject to so many windings, as that which occurs at the other digressions [elongations], in which there lies a greater perplexity than any of the ancient or recent Astronomers (let it be said without envy) have hitherto even once been able to suspect, much less have managed to provide for it sufficiently by an opportune hypothesis and a numeration deduced therefrom. But although all these things are so, I should nonetheless dare to promise that the Moon's course, digested by us into more recent tables, will satisfy the appearances more exactly than it is easy for anyone to attain by Instruments — seeing that it is derived only from the most select and least doubtful observations, drawn out through more than 20 years, and that with Instruments least fallacious, which it is granted to very few to imitate. And let these things be said, enough and more than enough, about observing and examining the Moon's motion.</p><p>As far as concerns the <b>epochs of the mean motions</b> in this very Moon, to be established retrospectively to the times of Ptolemy — of which you also make mention in the same letter — know that the notation in these matters which was transmitted to you was not genuine, but was, through some carelessness, copied by one of my students from a certain antiquated and rejected assumption, since the truer one was not at hand; and that the notation which I held as valid agrees neither with those [numbers] you received, nor with the Prutenic numbers — which I deem it less necessary now to substitute, since that whole earlier method of the lunar circuit is repudiated by us, a more fitting one having now been found. But in this later restoration, when you have obtained it, you will at the same time obtain the epochs corrected to Ptolemy's times.</p><p>For the books of that most excellent Mathematician <b>François Viète</b>, a copy of which you furnished me together with the letter inscribed to me on 26 April, I give very great thanks. A year ago, when we were together at Prague, <b>Adriaan van Roomen</b>, also a distinguished Mathematician, reported to me that the same Viète had published certain things on particular compendia of Triangles, which I should be eager to see. He indeed promised that he would send me a copy, but the outcome has not yet answered to it.</p><p>To those [letters] which you directed hither on the ninth of May, I think there is no need to reply at length, since the preceding points suffice. This at least it pleases me to add: that that accurate zeal of yours, in investigating and subtly weighing the things earlier elaborated by us concerning the Moon, is by no means unwelcome to me; nay, I should wish that the same be done by you further, both in these and in other matters, with equal diligence, and that, wherever any doubt occurs, I be candidly advised — for <i>eyes often see more than an eye</i> — and I hold it sufficiently clear that you act candidly out of love of eliciting truth alone, not so that you may attack our work with some prejudice, as most smatterers presume to do. That excellent Italian Mathematician <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> also attempted, in the Moon, some things from that gathering [<i>quaternio</i>] on its motion printed at Hamburg — which he obtained, without my knowledge, from a certain member of my household who had set out into Italy — and he did this, too, with good intention, as appears from his letter; but he did not attain the sought goal with felicity equal to yours, as is clear from the calculation which, enclosed in his letter, he sent me some weeks ago, a copy of which I enclose along [with this].</p><p>Thus far I had written — or rather had dictated to my amanuensis — before I departed from Benátky about two months ago; and I had resolved not only to supplement these very letters here at Prague, and to add certain other things, and to revise the calculation which I send, and even to explain it in some points, before I sent them to you — as I think I promised in those shorter [letters] which I gave you (when, a month ago, I returned the books communicated [to me]). But since new business daily occupies me, so that I cannot dispatch it quickly enough, even if I most greatly wished — (I have moreover certain things in hand to be carried out for <b>His Imperial Majesty</b>, our most clement Lord, which do not allow me to attend diligently to other things before they are finished) — I am compelled to direct these [pages] to you, however immature and incomplete, so that my promise may be satisfied at least in some part, if it may not now be satisfied as to the whole. I have attached the things of which mention is made therein, insofar as they could now be found; for that amanuensis — who had both written the foregoing and had kept the things that were to be appended — is no longer with me, but, after I came hither to Prague, without my knowledge and against his pledged faith, instigated by another (as I think), withdrew himself — I do not indeed know for what cause — and returned to the Wittenbergers, where he hopes for some scholastic office. Whence I fear lest not everything has been added here as it ought; nay, I am in doubt whether the very things I attached are correct, and accurately enough set down; for there is at present no leisure for reviewing them. It will therefore be yours to weigh everything circumspectly, and, wherever need be, to correct it — which by your exact judgment in these matters you will perform without difficulty. But I should wish either these very [pages], or a copy of them (for I have none at hand), to be sent back, so that I may supply what is wanting through a better opportunity, and, if any things in the appended materials have been omitted or done less elaborately, may repair them and render them more perfect.</p><p>As to the star <b>Venus [♀]</b>, once not appearing at dawn according to the opinion of the Poet <b>Albinovanus</b> — about which you again raised a question in your last [letter] — the narrowness of time prevents my replying much now; nor do other reasons now occur to me than those which I wrote to you a year ago. For although in your new calculation — especially at the first time of the conjunction of Venus [♀] and Mercury [☿] — the difference of latitude is not so great as was earlier admitted (for here it does not reach half a degree, which Ptolemy did not always attain in this part, much less his imitator Copernicus), nevertheless, however Venus and Mercury be conjoined, even in the very minute of longitude and latitude, Venus cannot be obscured by it [Mercury] so that it does not appear at all, since one luminous body does not darken another, nor is the visible diameter of Mercury so great that it could effect this, as I advised long ago. If therefore — apart from the Privileges of Poets [poetic licence] — that Poet is truthful, I should rather believe that Venus lay hidden, veiled by exhalations and certain white meteors most similar to air, especially since it was elongated at least 8 degrees from the Sun [☉] and therefore somewhat subject to its rays, and less conspicuous on account of the dawning day; but you will discern these things more rightly yourself. I shall add a letter of <b>Scaliger</b>, containing at the end some things about this very business, if I obtain a copy quickly enough.</p><p>There was with me at Benátky, last spring, Master <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, a distinguished Mathematician among the Styrians, with whom I have learned from him that you too have a familiarity by letters. He reported, among other things, that he had at some time written some things to you about my <b>Hypotheses</b> being called into controversy, and that he had shown that they are found neither in Copernicus nor in <b>Apollonius of Perga</b> — as a certain man (now dead these 14 days) pretended — and that he had gathered, by certain indications, that they are not another's, but mine. I therefore most lovingly ask that you would communicate Kepler's letter with me for a little while, or at least transmit those words which concern this business, excerpted from it and ratified by the subscription of your hand in testimony of the truth; and that you do this by the first Post returning hither from you, and entrust it — whatever it shall be — to your Prince's Agent (as they call him), to be delivered to me. You would thus do me a thing especially welcome; for some necessity now presses, on account of which I should be eager that it be done so — of which you will learn at another time.</p><p>The <b>Legate of your most Serene Prince</b> had lately invited me to dinner, by whom I was most kindly and most sumptuously received, and frequent — and (as was fitting) honourable — mention of you was made between us. As soon as the things I now elaborate for the Emperor shall be ready, that I may be freer, I have resolved to invite him in turn to me, since he has already kindly assured me that he will not decline the visit. Then we shall converse more fully about you, and I shall show him some of those things which have been ventilated between us in mutual letters, together with certain of my <b>Instruments</b> — which now have partly been brought hither from Benátky, the Emperor so willing, and partly (as I hope) will shortly be here — so that, having returned home, he may report about them to you; although the most important part of them, together with the rest of my equipment, is still detained at Magdeburg, to be conveyed hither, however, at the expense of the Reverend Chapter of that place, for the favour of His Imperial Majesty, by the <b>Elbe</b> (unless it be too dried up on account of the continual heat), as quickly as it can be done. These are the things which at this time I deemed should be written and intimated to you; and I ask you again and again that you receive everything candidly, and reply in good time to our expectation.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague, the 28th day of August, New Style, the year 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Magnificence,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>I wrote with my own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> If you could lend me by turns some of the books noted in the schedule likewise attached, sending them to me successively, you would thus do me a thing no less welcome than before. I shall restore them in good faith, as was done with the earlier ones too. You have here, moreover, the Calculation of a Solar Eclipse, computed from my newer tables. Thence you will easily gather that a method somewhat different in the Moon's motion has since been discovered, from that earlier one communicated to you before. The Sun's altitude was observed, at the beginning of the obscuration, [at] 60° 43′, at an elevation of the Pole of 50° 4½′, according to my observation. From this seek the time, and compare it with our numbers. This observation of which I speak was made here at Prague by a certain quadrant of brass [orichalc] belonging to the Generous Lord Johann Friedrich Hoffmann, exhibiting single minutes, and elaborated in imitation of one of mine, which is the third in number in the Mechanica of Astronomy. If anything has been marked from the heavens with you in the same Eclipse, make me a sharer in that observation.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Edition of record.</b> Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</i> vol. VIII (<i>Epistolae</i>). MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, Cod. 693 (folio). The working transcription carries one OCR artifact — <i>\"K0PLERUS\"</i> (numeral zero) for <b>Köplerus</b> = Kepler; reproduced in the source block, corrected silently to \"Kepler\" in the translation.</li><li><b>Date / place (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"Pragae die 28 Augusti stylo novo Anno 1600\"</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b>, the calendar Tycho now uses in Imperial Catholic Prague (he had reached Bohemia in 1599 and was in Rudolf II's service). Written <b>at Prague</b>, <i>manu propria</i>; agrees with the catalogue label (\"1600. 28. august n.s.\"). The body itself is dated by a sequence of Herwart's incoming letters — <b>10 January, 16 January, [—] February, 8 February, 26 April, 9 May</b> (1600); the third of these (with Rothmann's star-restitution) is given only as <i>\"die Februarij\"</i> with <b>the day-number missing in the text</b> (flagged, not supplied).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), Bavarian privy councillor and chancellor at Munich, learned chronologist and patron of mathematics, in active correspondence with both Tycho and Kepler. The superscription's <i>\"Praesidi Schwabae\"</i> = President of the margraviate of <b>Burgau</b> in Swabia; <i>\"Statuum provincialium Cancellario\"</i> = Chancellor of the (Bavarian) provincial Estates.</li><li><b>The lunar programme (the through-line).</b> The letter is dominated by Tycho's <b>restitution of the lunar theory</b> — the work he says delayed his reply. He distinguishes the still-imperfect <i>\"earlier hypothesis\"</i> tables (including the lunar-prosthaphaeresis table reprinted, carelessly, at <b>Hamburg</b>) from the <i>\"restored tables of both the equal and unequal motions\"</i> still in progress; promises the <b>lunar parallax</b> canon a \"further limitation\"; treats the <b>annual variation</b> (\"which the ancients did not notice,\" <i>\"exceeds a sixth of a degree\"</i> ≈10′) and a <b>new equation-of-time</b> mode for the Moon; and insists on the difficulty of the inequality \"at the other digressions\" (i.e. away from syzygy) — the perplexity \"no astronomer ancient or recent could even suspect,\" Tycho's claim of priority for what became known as the lunar <b>variation</b> (and the <b>annual equation</b>). He cautions repeatedly against demanding precision finer than the senses allow for the Moon (limb-sighting, parallax, the rarity of clear observation near the <b>nonagesimal</b>), while promising his new tables will beat any instrument-based attempt — derived from <i>\"more than 20 years\"</i> of select observations.</li><li><b>Books / works cited.</b> The <i>Astronomiae Instauratae Progymnasmata</i> (vol. I): <b>p. 80</b> (solar parallax by threefold solar distance), <b>ch. 2</b> + <b>p. 274</b> (fixed-star restitution; the Hessen-longitude excess), <b>ch. 6</b> sub fine (the six lunar observations, incl. the October 1586 markings). The 1596 <i>Epistolae astronomicae libri</i> (\"our book of Letters\"): <b>p. 56</b> (the Moon's places for three days). The <b>thousand-star catalogue</b> <i>\"described on parchment\"</i> offered to Rudolf II \"a year ago\" on first reaching Prague (= the <i>Stellarum octavae sphaerae restitutio</i>; cf. 15980508001). The carelessly-printed <b>Hamburg \"quaternio\"</b> on lunar motion (a pirated tract Tycho's runaway household-member carried to Italy, whence Magini got it).</li><li><b>The prosthaphaeretic method (§5: <i>prosthaphaeresis</i>).</b> Two passages treat the <b>resolution of triangles by <i>prosthaphaereses</b></i> (Greek, retained) — the additive-subtractive trigonometric technique (precursor to logarithms): Tycho concedes it offers little economy for <b>plane</b> triangles but a \"readiness not to be despised\" for <b>spherical</b> ones, faster than the common method, provided one works from <b>Rheticus's</b> complete trigonometric canon (the <i>Opus Palatinum</i> / great <i>Canon</i>), \"corrected where need is\" — claiming deviations no greater than 4–5 seconds. The same theme links to <b>Viète</b> and <b>Adriaan van Roomen</b> (van Roomen reported, at Prague the year before, that Viète had published \"particular compendia of triangles\").</li><li><b>Rothmann's fixed-star verification.</b> <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> (Hesse-Kassel mathematician under Landgrave <b>Wilhelm IV</b>, d. 1592 — \"the most praiseworthy-memory Prince\") had compiled a restitution of certain fixed stars, forwarded to Tycho via Herwart. Tycho judges the <b>Landgravian</b> longitudes run ≈<b>5–6′</b> (≈ a twelfth of a degree) above his own — an error he had diagnosed (<i>Progymn.</i> p. 274) and \"amply ventilated\" with Kassel in the 1596 <i>Epistolae</i>. He reports Rothmann <b>abandoned astronomy</b>, lives by \"forensic agency-work\" in wretched health, \"nor do I know whether he is still alive\" — a notable biographical datum (Rothmann in fact died c. 1600). Tycho assures Herwart the sharing carries no risk to him and that Rothmann's rectification can profit Tycho little, his own thousand-star catalogue being long since complete and largely printed.</li><li><b>The longitudes/latitudes of places.</b> Königsberg longitude <b>46° 45′</b> (after <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>, author of the <i>Prutenic Tables</i>); Jerusalem from <b>Ptolemy's <i>Geography</b></i>; Rome — against <b>Copernicus's</b> 40½° (5° west of Krakow) — Tycho argues <b>scarcely 39°</b>. He lists latitude errors: Reinhold borrowing Königsberg's latitude <b>26′</b> short from <b>Peter Apian</b>; Apian making <b>Augsburg</b> 5′ low; <b>Cyprianus</b> [<b>Leovitius</b>, the Augsburg astronomer] short by <b>a fifth of a degree</b> (12′).</li><li><b>The priority dispute &amp; Ursus's death (the urgent thread).</b> <i>\"A certain man, now dead these 14 days\"</i> who claimed Tycho's world-system <b>hypotheses</b> were already in <b>Copernicus</b> or <b>Apollonius of Perga</b> = <b>Nicolaus Reimers Ursus</b> (Bär), the Imperial mathematician and Tycho's bitter antagonist, who died in <b>August 1600</b> — fixing this passage to mid-August. <b>Kepler</b> (with Tycho at Benátky \"last spring,\" i.e. 1600) had written to Herwart demonstrating the hypotheses are Tycho's own, not found in Copernicus/Apollonius; Tycho urgently asks Herwart to send <b>Kepler's letter, or a notarised extract under Herwart's own hand \"in testimony of the truth,\"</b> by the first post via the Bavarian Prince's <b>Agent</b> — \"some necessity now presses.\" This is documentary ammunition for the <b>Ursus libel suit</b> (cf. 15991209001, 15990918001, 15980820001). The <i>\"certain man… 14 days\"</i> dating and the Ursus identification are mutually corroborating.</li><li><b>The Venus-at-dawn crux (Albinovanus).</b> Herwart's recurring literary-astronomical question: the <b>Poet Albinovanus</b> (Pedo Albinovanus, the Augustan poet) reported <b>Venus</b> invisible at dawn. Tycho's reply: a Venus–Mercury conjunction cannot account for it (\"one luminous body does not darken another,\" Mercury's disc far too small), so — granting the poet truthful \"apart from poetic licence\" — Venus was likely <b>veiled by white atmospheric exhalations/meteors</b> and dimmed by proximity to the Sun (only ~8° elongation) in the brightening dawn. Tycho offers to enclose a relevant <b>Scaliger</b> letter.</li><li><b>The Imperial/Bavarian court frame.</b> Tycho is at <b>Prague</b> working \"for the Emperor\" (<b>Rudolf II</b>); a <b>Bavarian Legate</b> has feasted him and will be shown Tycho's letters and <b>instruments</b>; the instruments are partly arrived from <b>Benátky</b> (by the Emperor's will), partly still detained at <b>Magdeburg</b>, to come down the <b>Elbe</b> at the cathedral Chapter's expense \"unless it be too dried up by the continual heat\" (cf. the Elbe-transport thread in 16000324002 / 16000322001). A vivid composition detail: the letter was <b>dictated to an amanuensis at Benátky</b>, who then <b>deserted to Wittenberg</b> (\"instigated by another\"), carrying off the appended materials — so Tycho sends it \"immature and incomplete\" and asks for it (or a copy) back.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Herwart von Hohenburg; <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>; <b>Ptolemy</b>; <b>Copernicus</b>; <b>Peter Apian</b>; <b>Cyprianus Leovitius</b>; <b>Christoph Rothmann</b> and <b>Landgrave Wilhelm IV</b> of Hesse-Kassel; <b>Rheticus</b>; <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>; <b>François Viète</b>; <b>Adriaan van Roomen</b>; <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b>; <b>Johannes Kepler</b>; <b>Apollonius of Perga</b>; <b>Ursus</b> (unnamed — \"a certain man, dead these 14 days\"); <b>Albinovanus</b> (the poet); <b>Joseph Justus Scaliger</b>; the <b>Bavarian Legate</b>.</li><li><b>Latin &amp; Greek tags.</b> <i>\"Quis Cerere reperta glande vescitur?\"</i> — \"Who, having found grain [Ceres], feeds on acorns?\" (the old proverb of progress past primitive food). <i>\"nodum in scirpo quaerere\"</i> — \"to seek a knot in a bulrush\" = to manufacture difficulty where none exists (Plautus/Ennius; Terence). <i>\"plus vident oculi quam oculus\"</i> — \"eyes see more than an eye\" (two heads better than one). <i>\"sub incudem numerorum revocantur\"</i> — \"are recalled under the anvil of numbers\" (re-forged by calculation). <i>\"absit invidia dicto\"</i> — \"let it be said without envy.\" Greek code-switches retained: <i>προσθαφαιρέσεις</i> (prosthaphaereses), <i>φαινόμενα</i> (phaenomena), <i>πραγματεία</i> (pragmateia, \"practical undertaking\"). Measure idiom: <i>sextans gradus</i> = ⅙° (10′); <i>uncia / duodecima pars gradus</i> = 1⁄12° (5′); <i>quinta pars gradus</i> = ⅕° (12′); <i>sesquialter gradus</i> = 1½°; <i>scrupulum</i> / <i>scrupulum secundum</i> = minute / second of arc.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> the day-number of Herwart's <i>die Februarij</i> letter — earlier flagged as a source lacuna — <b>is present in Dreyer</b> (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII, p. 347): <i>die <b>20</b> Februarij</i>. Restored here in the Source and translation; the lacuna flag is withdrawn. (This was the chief reservation behind the [B] grade — a case for [A] is now open.)</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Post-signature block (a book-loan request + a solar-eclipse observation on Hoffmann's brass quadrant, modelled on the third instrument of the Mechanica), restored from Dreyer VIII, p. 353 (dropped after \"manu propria scripsi\"). (OCR \"obligationis\" corrected by sense to \"obscurationis\".)</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> The postscript's 'in Solis motu' has been corrected to 'in Lunae motu' (and the English accordingly to 'the Moon's motion'), per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.353, which prints the crescent-moon glyph + genitive 'ae', distinct from the sun-glyph used two lines later in the same sentence.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — sound. A long, dense, technically continuous letter rendered securely throughout; the substance (the lunar-theory restitution and Tycho's variation/annual-equation priority; the Rothmann fixed-star verification and Rothmann's biographical decline; the place-longitude/latitude corrections; the prosthaphaeretic triangle method on Rheticus's canon; the Viète/van Roomen and Magini threads; the urgent request for Kepler's anti-Ursus testimony, tied to Ursus's death \"14 days ago\"; the Venus-at-dawn/Albinovanus crux; the Bavarian-Legate and Elbe-instrument frame) is clear and cross-corroborated by the surrounding Prague correspondence. Two minor flags keep it from [A]: a <b>lacuna in the source</b> (the day-number of the February letter, <i>\"die Februarij,\"</i> is missing — not supplied), and a slightly compressed clause (<i>\"nisi id celâro, quod tamen nihilominus libenter facio,\"</i> rendered \"except [on condition] that I keep it quiet, which I nonetheless willingly do\"). Neither obscures the through-line; the one OCR artifact (<i>\"K0PLERUS\"</i>) is editorial, not textual.</p><p><i>Grade review (2026-06-03): upgraded [B]→[A] — the sole capping crux (the </i>die Februarij<i> lacuna) resolved by §1 collation (Dreyer: 'die 20'); residue editorial OCR + one stylistic clause.</i></p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII page images in three chunks (slice pages 1-4 = TBOO 343-346, 5-8 = 347-350, 9-12 = 351-354). Collated the full letter body line-by-line against the images, including the address block, all four numbered questions, every proper name (Reinhold, Ptolemy, Copernicus, Apian, Cyprianus/Leovitius, Rothmann, Rudolf, Viete, Adrianus Romanus, Magini, Albinovanus, Kepler, Scaliger, Hoffmann), every date (10 Jan, 16 Jan, 8 Feb, 20 Feb, 26 Apr, 9 May, 28 Aug 1600), every number/coordinate (Par.46 M.45; part.40 1/2; 39deg; 26 M.; 5 Minutes; pag.80; pag.56; pag.274; 5-6 scrupuli; October 1586; P.60 M.43; P.50 M.4 1/2), and the closing dateline and signature -- all match Dreyer exactly except the one postscript substitution noted above. This is confirmed on a direct re-read of TBOO VIII p.353 in isolation to verify the disputed glyph.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). Our Latin source_text substitutes 'Solis' (the Sun) for Dreyer's 'Lunae' (the Moon) in the postscript; the English then compounds the error by asserting a newly-discovered method in solar theory. Dreyer's reading is also the only one that coheres with the rest of the letter, which is entirely a discussion of revised lunar tables/theory and never otherwise revisits solar theory -- a scholar reading the published English would wrongly conclude Tycho flagged a solar-theory revision here. Dreyer prints: <i>Inde facile colliges, paulo aliam eſſe in [Moon-symbol]æ motu postea adinventam rationem, quam prior illa tecum antea communicata. (TBOO VIII p.353, line ~14: the crescent-moon glyph + 'æ' genitive suffix = 'Lunae', immediately followed on the next line by the same convention '☉is altitudo' = 'Solis altitudo' for the Sun -- the two symbols are distinct and used correctly elsewhere on the same page.)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.353, slice pages 9-12 of 12): the crescent-moon symbol followed by 'ae' is clearly legible and distinct from the separate sun-glyph in the same sentence ('☉is altitudo'). The correction also restores internal coherence, since the letter's postscript concerns lunar tables and eclipse observation throughout, never revisiting solar theory. No remaining uncertainty on this point.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000504003", "next": "brahe-t_16000908001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000504003", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000908001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Apologizes for infrequent replies to Herwart's many letters, explains the delay by work on the restitution of the lunar hypothesis, and promises to address Herwart's doubts.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000828002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000908001", "d": "1600-09-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Augsburg", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Augsburg, 8 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>CUM primum, Vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, ad me misisti carmina illa, quae de rebus tuis sunt composita, subijt animum, quod quondam IACOBUS CURTIUS Procancellarius Imperij, piae memoriae (qui mihi assiduus studiorum collega et contubernalis in Gallia et Italia, exinde amicus intimus et quidem pars animae meae, dum vixit, extitit) mea suggestione motus, desideravit carmen aliquod huius generis obtinere a D. PHILIPPO MENZELIO, Medicinae D. et Professore celebratissimo, cum is in his partibus Poëtas reliquos longe antecellere existimetur, sed ob distractiones tam lectionis publicae quam et praxeos Medicinalis modo memorati D. MENZELIJ frustra et incassum laboravit. Idcirco plane constitui nullum non lapidem movere, donec optatum ego adpiscar. Id aegerrime quidem, ut verum fatear, sed tamen iam tandem aliquando impetravi, et his iunctum ad te mitto, quod spero et confido tibi non futurum ingratum.</p><p>Mitto etiam veteres quosdam Geographos nunc primum in lucem editos, mihique inscriptos, inter quos SCYLAX Caryandensis est, cuius et STRABO et CICERO meminit, author sane vetustissimus, ipso HERODOTO et THUCYDIDE antiquior, ut qui temporibus DARIJ primi HYSTASPIS filij floruerit, penes quem aliqua reperies, quae alibi non extent. Atque simul unâ te bene valere et quam foelicissime florere ex animo opto. Datae Augustae die 8 Sept. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>When first, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians, you sent me those poems which have been composed about your affairs, it came into my mind that once <b>Jacob Curtius</b>, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire, of pious memory — who, while he lived, was my assiduous study-colleague and chamber-fellow in France and Italy, and thereafter my most intimate friend, and indeed a part of my soul — moved by my suggestion, desired to obtain some poem of this kind from Master <b>Philipp Menzel</b>, Doctor of Medicine and a most celebrated Professor, since he is reckoned far to surpass the rest of the Poets in these parts; but, on account of the distractions both of public lecturing and of medical practice of the said Master Menzel, he laboured in vain and to no purpose. Therefore I plainly resolved to move every stone [to leave no stone unturned] until I should attain the thing wished-for. This, most laboriously indeed (to confess the truth), but yet now at length at last, I have obtained; and I send it to you joined herewith — which I hope and trust will not be unwelcome to you.</p><p>I send also certain ancient Geographers, now for the first time brought into the light and inscribed [dedicated] to me, among whom is <b>Scylax of Caryanda</b> — whom both Strabo and Cicero mention — an author indeed most ancient, older than Herodotus and Thucydides themselves, inasmuch as he flourished in the times of Darius the First, son of Hystaspes; in whom you will find some things which are not extant elsewhere. And at the same time I wish, from my heart, that you fare well and flourish most happily. Given at Augsburg, the 8th day of September, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (see the Munich series), here writing from <b>Augsburg</b> (where he had gone partly to recover Leovitius's manuscripts — cf. brahe-t_16000426002).</li><li><b>The Menzel poem.</b> Herwart had at last extracted a commemorative <b>poem on Tycho</b> from <b>Philipp Menzel</b> (Menzelius), Doctor of Medicine and the most admired Latin poet \"in these parts,\" who had earlier (at the prompting of Herwart's late friend <b>Jacob Curtius</b>) been too busy with lecturing and medical practice to write one. <i>nullum non lapidem movere</i> — \"to move every stone,\" i.e. leave no stone unturned (the proverbial <i>omnem movere lapidem</i>).</li><li><b><i>Iacobus Curtius Procancellarius Imperij</b></i> = <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau</b> (Jacob Curtius, d. 1594), Imperial Vice-Chancellor, Herwart's student-companion in France and Italy and \"part of his soul\" — the same Curtius commended in brahe-t_15980626002 as having known Tycho's work.</li><li><b>The ancient geographers.</b> <i>veteres Geographos nunc primum in lucem editos, mihique inscriptos</i> = a newly-printed edition of <b>minor Greek geographers</b>, <b>dedicated to Herwart</b> — the <b>Augsburg 1600 edition by David Höschel</b> (<i>Geographica … Marciani Heracleotae, Scylacis Caryandensis …</i>). <i>Scylax of Caryanda</i>: the (pseudo-)Scylax <i>Periplus</i>; Herwart's flourish — \"older than Herodotus and Thucydides, flourishing in the time of <b>Darius I, son of Hystaspes</b>, cited by Strabo and Cicero\" — follows the ancient testimonia (the historical Scylax served Darius I; the surviving <i>Periplus</i> is in fact later, a point Herwart, like his age, does not raise).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Augustae die 8 Sept. 1600</i> = <b>8 September 1600</b>; <b>Augsburg</b> had adopted the <b>Gregorian / New Style</b> calendar (from 1583); taken as N.S. (The dataset label gives only \"Augsburg\" without a date; the body supplies 8 Sept. 1600.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the content (the Menzel poem secured via the memory of Jacob Curtius; the Höschel Augsburg 1600 <i>Geographi</i> dedicated to Herwart, with Scylax of Caryanda) is firmly identified and corpus-consistent. The ancient over-dating of the <i>Periplus</i> is the writer's own (period-standard) view, preserved with a note, not corrected into the text.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. The 13-page slice (TBOO VIII pp.343-355) holds multiple adjacent letters (an earlier Tycho-to-Herwart letter of 18/28 Aug 1600 occupies most of it); our target letter (Herwart to Tycho, 8 September 1600) is located on pp.353(bottom)-354(top), confirmed by its opening clause and by the closing 'Datae Augustae die 8 Sept. A. 1600.' Text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Jacob Curtius, Philipp Menzel, Scylax of Caryanda, Strabo, Cicero, Herodotus, Thucydides, Darius I son of Hystaspes) all correctly rendered. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000828002", "next": "brahe-t_16000911001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Recounts earlier failed efforts to obtain a laudatory poem from Philipp Menzel in Tycho's honour, and announces that he has now succeeded in procuring one, sent herewith.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000908001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000911001", "d": "1600-09-11", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 11 September 1600 [N.S.] *(quoting Kepler's letter of 30 May 1599)*", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.4</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Bavarian chancellor <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> <i>to</i> Tycho — whose body is a <b>verbatim transcript of Johannes Kepler's letter to Herwart of 30 May 1599</b> analysing the <b>Ursus plagiarism dispute</b>. The two diagrams Kepler drew are not reproduced in the source (marked <b>[Figure]</b> below).</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO A KNUDSTRUP ET URANIBURGO, MATHEMATICORUM OMNIUM FACILE PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO SINGULARI OBSERVANTIA COLENDO. PRAGAe.</p><p>CUM praeteritis diebus, vir nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, Augusta transirem, ipse consignavi Theatrum tuum Mechanicum Generoso viro, D. PHILIPPO EDUARDO FUGGERO. Egit ille tum gratias ingentes, et pollicitus est, se tibi in scriptis quoque gratias acturum. Hac ipsa die domum reversus reperi litteras tuas die 28. Augusti stylo novo Pragae exaratas, quibus inter alia desideras quantocitius ea tibi transmitti, quae M. IOANNES KEPLERUS de hypothesibus tuis antehac ad me scripserit. Est is sane Mathematicus insignis, mihique tam eo nomine, quam et ob candorem suum perquam acceptus: quare nollem eum quavis ratione vel occasione offendere. Sed cum scribas fato iam functum illum, qui sibi hypotheses illas arrogaverat, et non modo pro re nata, verum etiam pro benevolentia tua, qua me complecteris, plane mihi persuasum sit, hanc communicationem amico meo D. KEPLERO non fore ingratam, idcirco morem gerere tibi statui. Scripsit autem is 30. die Maij anno proxime elapso 1599 haec verba:</p><p>»Fatetur URSUS, se hypotheses illas suas non a seipso habere nisi, quatenus aliquid differt a TYCHONE. Contendit vicissim TYCHO, quas profiteatur hypotheses (qua parte discrepant a Copernicanis) eas se suo Marte invenisse nec aliquid simile uspiam vidisse. Contra URSUS, et suas et TYCHONIS deductas esse APOLLONIJ PERGAeI iam pridem circa tempora CHRISTI publicatis hypothesibus. Huius sui pronunciati caussam reddit loca aliqua COPERNICI, in quibus partim ipsa hypothesis TYCHONIS expresse ponatur ab ipso COPERNICO, partim vero APOLLONIO PERGAeO ascribatur. Atqui nihil minus ijs locis COPERNICUS dicit quam hoc ipsum quod vult URSUS. Neque enim TYCHONIS hypothesin exprimit (fallitur ex allusione URSUS) nec eam APOLLONIO PERGAeO ascribit. Cum ergo fateatur URSUS 1. se hausisse suam hypothesin aliunde, 2. invenisse expressam in COPERNICO et attributam PERGAeO, 3. eandemque esse quam habet TYCHO: fatetur ergo se descripsisse illam ab aliquo, qui idem dicit quod TYCHO.</p><p>Notetur hoc, quod qui suas URSO monstrarit hypotheses (dempto exiguo quod mutatu facile est) is eadem plane ὑποτιθεῖ cum TYCHONE. Quis autem is est? URSUS dicit, COPERNICUM illum esse, et in eo PERGAeUM praetereaque neminem. Nemo itaque est Author Ursinarum praeter aut PERGAeUM, in COPERNICO, aut TYCHONEM. At ego dico, PERGAeUM non esse Authorem Ursinarum, nec COPERNICUM. Ergo solus TYCHO praetereaque nemo fuit URSO commonstrator ad suas hypotheses. Firmamentum caussae in eo est, ut genuinum sensum COPERNICI habeamus. Caetera confessione sua concinnavit URSUS ipse. Ergo lib. 3. cap. 25. Revolutionum, ubi Tychonias URSUS a COPERNICO putat exprimi, largitur quidem COPERNICUS aliquos ex motibus, quos ipse Terrae tribuit, Soli transcribi posse: sed non id largitur de motu annuo, nec de motibus 5 Planetarum capitalibus ibi agit: nec id fert argumentum libri, quod versatur in explicatione solius motus Solaris (sive terrestris) sine respectu motuum caeterarum stellarum. Ad evidentiam rei describam duo schemata mentem COPERNICI explicantia:</p><p>[Figura] Haec est hypothesis COPERNICI, circa unum orbem Terrae vel Solis.</p><p>[Figura] Haec est mutatio, quam libro 3. cap. 25. etiam possibilem ponit, et in dubio relinquit.</p><p>In priore schemate A centrum et mundi et corporis Solaris. B centrum Epicycli, quod salvat eccentricum (COPERNICUS ex epicyclis et magno orbe facit eccentricum, idem esse ipse ait). Et B illud aequaliter per anni spacium circa A circumit. C centrum Epicyclij quod mutationem praestat eccentricitatis. Illud C itidem annuo motu circumit circa B, in partem contrariam. D Terra est, quae intra annos 3434 circa C circumagitur.</p><p>In altero schemate F est centrum mundi et motus Terrae H, quae annuo motu adhuc aequaliter circumit. E est centrum corporis Solaris, quod circumit circa G centrum in annis 3434. G est centrum epicycli, qui Solem E fert, praestans apogaeo Solis progressum, nam in annis 17188 secundum COPERNICUM et Prutenicas circa F circumit, sub fixis. Itaque movebitur quidem loco suo Sol duobus motibus, sed tardissime, nec multum. Sunt enim circelli valde angusti ad comparationem eius circuli, in quo, in utroque schemate, Terra currit. At URSUS, seu inscitia, seu stultitia (qui haec neminem intellecturum speravit) putat COPERNICUM de tali motu Solis loqui, qui sit similis motui Terrae annuo Copernicano: quique aliquid in parallaxibus 5 Planetarum inferat. Nam hi duo, soli secundo loco concessi a COPERNICO, in consideratione caeterorum Planetarum negliguntur, sive Terrae sive Soli attribuantur: quamvis postulante id demonstratione exiguum aliquid inferant, quod videtur sentire in observationibus TYCHO. Haec de primo loco.</p><p>In reliquis locis ex COPERNICO allegatis lib. 5. c. 3, 35 adhuc multo peiorem causam habet URSUS. COPERNICUS mentionem APOLLONIJ PERGAeI eiusque lemmatij ex PTOLOMAeO transtulit, quod miror non legisse URSUM in PTOLOMAeO. Sententia PERGAeI toto fere genere alia est. Non enim versatur ille in consideratione systematis 5 Planetarum, quorum centrum in corpore Solis sit, ut TYCHO atque COPERNICUS et quidam antiqui: sed solum in eo ut dicat, qualem proportionem habere debeat Epicyclus cuiuslibet Planetae, seorsim ad suum orbem proprium et horum ad motum anomaliae commutationis et motum aequalem periodicum, ut ex tali proportione sequatur Planetae illius statio et regressio. Sic etiam defectus eius hypotheseos in eo fuit, quod PERGAeUS opinatus est, quemlibet Planetam una tantum inaequalitate variari, quae a PTOLOMAeO salvatur per epicyclum, nesciens ipsum etiam epicycli centrum inaequaliter incedere, sive secundum PTOLOMAeUM, in Eccentrico. Hunc allegatorum locorum sensum esse omnes professores dicent. At URSUS aut caecus aut vafer, interpositione parenthetica vocis (mobilis) persuadere vult, PERGAeUM vel pro eo COPERNICUM loqui de communi illo motu omnium 5 Planetarum, propter Solem, centrum illorum, annuo motu circumactum: de quo motu TYCHONIS hypotheses sonant, quique a COPERNICO Terrae tribuitur. Atque hoc est firmamentum causae. Qui haec perpendit, illi iam ultro suspicio oritur ex omnibus circumstantijs. Fatetur se furti philosophici reum, se Magdeburgi sententiam TYCHONIS de mundi habitu publicam fecisse, sed iuxta suam, natam scilicet ex quadam matre, quae nuspiam est, ut modo quaerendo comperimus.«</p><p>Hactenus illa, quae M. KEPLERUS ad me scripsit. Ad alia quae scribis, responsum habebis, cum primum mihi fuerit tantum otij, atque hisce tibi officia et studia mea offero promptissime.</p><p>Datae Monachij die 11. Sept. Anno 1600.<br>Nobilissimae T. Dni Addictissimus<br>IOAN. GEORG. HERWART AB HOHENBURG, manu propria.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, easily the Prince of all Mathematicians, his Lord and friend to be revered with singular respect. At Prague.</b></p><p>When in past days, most noble man and easily Prince of all Mathematicians, I was passing through <b>Augsburg</b>, I myself delivered <b>your <i>Theatrum Mechanicum</i> [the <i>Mechanica</i>] to the noble Lord Philipp Eduard Fugger</b>. He then gave great thanks, and promised that he would thank you in writing too. This very day, returned home, I found your letter written at Prague on the 28th of August, New Style, in which, among other things, you desire that there be transmitted to you as quickly as possible <b>what Master Johannes Kepler had earlier written to me about your hypotheses</b>. He is indeed a distinguished Mathematician, and most welcome to me both on that account and for his candour; wherefore I should not wish to offend him by any means or occasion. But since you write that <b>the one who had arrogated those hypotheses to himself [Ursus] is now dead</b>, and since I am fully persuaded — both from the case as it stands and from your benevolence, with which you embrace me — that this communication will not be unwelcome to my friend Lord Kepler, I have therefore resolved to comply with you. Now he wrote, on the 30th day of May of the year just past, 1599, these words:</p><p><i>\"<b>Ursus admits</b> that he does not have those hypotheses of his from himself, except so far as something differs from Tycho. <b>Tycho</b> in turn contends that the hypotheses he professes (in the part where they differ from the Copernican) he found by his own effort [</i>suo Marte*], and saw nothing similar anywhere. <b>Ursus</b>, on the contrary, [contends] that both his and Tycho's are derived from the hypotheses of <b>Apollonius of Perga</b>, published long ago around the times of Christ. The cause of this his assertion he gives as certain passages of <b>Copernicus</b>, in which partly Tycho's hypothesis itself is expressly set down by Copernicus, partly is ascribed to Apollonius of Perga. But Copernicus says nothing less in those passages than this very thing which Ursus wants. For he neither expresses Tycho's hypothesis (Ursus is deceived by the allusion) nor ascribes it to Apollonius of Perga. Since, then, Ursus admits: (1) that he drew his hypothesis from elsewhere; (2) that he found it expressed in Copernicus and attributed to Perga; (3) that it is the same as Tycho's — <b>he therefore admits that he copied it from someone who says the same as Tycho.</b></p><p>Let this be noted: that <b>whoever showed Ursus his hypotheses (apart from a trifle easily changed) posits [ὑποτιθεῖ] plainly the same as Tycho.</b> But who is that? Ursus says it is <b>Copernicus</b>, and in him <b>Perga</b>, and no one else. There is therefore no author of Ursus's [hypotheses] except either Perga (in Copernicus) or Tycho. But I say that <b>Perga is not the author of Ursus's, nor Copernicus. Therefore Tycho alone, and no one else, was Ursus's informant for his hypotheses.</b> The foundation of the case lies in this: that we have the genuine sense of Copernicus. The rest Ursus has framed by his own confession. So, in <b>Book 3, ch. 25 of the <i>Revolutions</b></i>, where Ursus thinks the Tychonic [hypotheses] are expressed by Copernicus: Copernicus does indeed grant that some of the motions which he attributes to the Earth can be transcribed to the Sun — but he does <b>not</b> grant this of the annual motion, nor does he there treat the principal motions of the 5 Planets; nor does the book's argument bear it, which is engaged in explaining the solar (or terrestrial) motion alone, without respect to the motions of the other stars. For evidence of the matter I will describe two diagrams explaining Copernicus's mind:</p><p><b>[Figure]</b> This is Copernicus's hypothesis, about the single orb of the Earth or Sun.</p><p><b>[Figure]</b> This is the alteration which in Book 3, ch. 25 he sets down as also possible, and leaves in doubt.</p><p>In the former diagram, <b>A</b> [is] the centre both of the world and of the Solar body. <b>B</b> [is] the centre of the Epicycle which saves the eccentric (Copernicus makes the eccentric out of epicycles and the great orb, [and] says it is the same thing). And that B circles uniformly about A through the space of a year. <b>C</b> [is] the centre of the Epicycle which produces the variation of the eccentricity. That C likewise circles about B by an annual motion, in the contrary direction. <b>D</b> is the Earth, which is carried about C within 3434 years.</p><p>In the other diagram, <b>F</b> is the centre of the world and of the motion of the Earth <b>H</b>, which still circles uniformly by an annual motion. <b>E</b> is the centre of the Solar body, which circles about the centre <b>G</b> in 3434 years. G is the centre of the epicycle which carries the Sun E, producing the progress of the Sun's apogee — for in 17188 years, according to Copernicus and the Prutenic [Tables], it circles about F, beneath the fixed [stars]. So the Sun will indeed be moved from its place by two motions, but most slowly, and not much; for the little circles are very narrow in comparison with the circle in which, in both diagrams, the Earth runs. But <b>Ursus, whether from ignorance or from folly</b> (who hoped no one would understand these things), <b>thinks Copernicus speaks of such a motion of the Sun as is similar to the Copernican annual motion of the Earth, and which would introduce something into the parallaxes of the 5 Planets.</b> For these two [motions], granted to the Sun [only] in the second place by Copernicus, are neglected in the consideration of the other Planets, whether they be attributed to Earth or to Sun — although, the demonstration requiring it, they introduce some little thing, which Tycho seems to perceive in [his] observations. So much for the first passage.</p><p>In the remaining passages alleged from Copernicus, <b>Book 5, ch. 3 [and] 35</b>, Ursus has a still much worse case. Copernicus transferred the mention of Apollonius of Perga and his little lemma <b>from Ptolemy</b> — which I wonder Ursus did not read in Ptolemy. <b>Perga's view is of an almost wholly different kind.</b> For he is not engaged in the consideration of the system of the 5 Planets whose centre is in the Sun's body (as Tycho, and Copernicus, and certain of the ancients), but only in this: to state what proportion the Epicycle of each Planet ought to have, severally, to its own proper orb, and these to the motion of the anomaly of commutation and the equal periodic motion — so that from such proportion the station and regression of that Planet may follow. So too the defect of his hypothesis was this, that <b>Perga supposed each Planet to be varied by only one inequality</b> (which by Ptolemy is saved by an epicycle), not knowing that the centre of the epicycle itself also proceeds unequally — that is, according to Ptolemy, on the Eccentric. That this is the sense of the cited passages, all the professors will say. But <b>Ursus, either blind or crafty, by the parenthetic insertion of the word \"(mobile),\" wants to persuade</b> [us] that Perga, or in his place Copernicus, speaks of that common motion of all 5 Planets — on account of the Sun, their centre, carried round by an annual motion — of which motion Tycho's hypotheses speak, and which by Copernicus is attributed to the Earth. <b>And this is the foundation of the case.</b> Whoever weighs these things, to him suspicion now arises of its own accord from all the circumstances. <b>[Ursus] confesses himself guilty of philosophical theft: that at Magdeburg he made public Tycho's view of the structure of the world, but according to his own [version] — born, namely, of a certain \"mother\" that exists nowhere, as we have just now found by inquiry.\"</b></p><p>Thus far what Master Kepler wrote to me. To the other things you write, you shall have an answer as soon as I have so much leisure; and herewith I offer you my services and zeal most readily.</p><p>Given at Munich, the 11th day of September, the year 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your most noble Lordship,<br><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b>, in his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondents.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622) — Bavarian chancellor, learned correspondent of Kepler — <b>to Tycho</b>. The body is a <b>verbatim transcript of Kepler's letter to Herwart of 30 May 1599</b>, sent at Tycho's request (Tycho's letter of 28 Aug 1600).</li><li><b>Significance — Kepler's brief in the Tycho-v-Ursus plagiarism case (a keystone of the dispute).</b> This document carries, in Kepler's own words, his <b>logical and technical demolition of Ursus's plagiarism defence</b>:</li><li><b>The logical core.</b> Ursus claimed both his and Tycho's world-systems derive from <b>Apollonius of Perga</b> (via passages of <b>Copernicus</b>). Kepler shows Copernicus neither states Tycho's hypothesis nor ascribes it to Apollonius; therefore, from Ursus's own three admissions, <b>\"Tycho alone, and no one else, was Ursus's informant\"</b> — Ursus copied the geo-heliocentric scheme from someone \"who says the same as Tycho,\" and that someone can only be Tycho. <i>(The pivot: \"the foundation of the case lies in having the genuine sense of Copernicus.\")</i></li><li><b>The technical refutation.</b> Kepler reads the cited Copernican passages (<b><i>De revolutionibus</i> III.25; V.3, V.35</b>) in detail — with <b>two diagrams</b> of Copernicus's solar/terrestrial models (centres A–D; F–H; the 3434-year and 17188-year cycles, per the <b>Prutenic Tables</b>) — to show Ursus <b>misreads</b> Copernicus's minor solar motions as the annual motion affecting planetary parallaxes, and <b>misreads Apollonius's lemma</b> (transmitted from <b>Ptolemy</b>), which concerns only single-inequality epicycle proportions, not a heliocentric five-planet system. Ursus's case rests on a <b>dishonest parenthetic insertion of \"(mobile).\"</b></li><li><b>The verdict.</b> Kepler brands Ursus <b>\"guilty of philosophical theft,\"</b> who published Tycho's world-structure at <b>Magdeburg</b> as his own, dressed up as born \"of a mother that exists nowhere.\"</li><li><b>Context.</b> This is the analysis that underlies <b>Kepler's <i>Apologia Tychonis contra Ursum</b></i> (commissioned by Tycho). Its transmission here (Sept 1600), with Tycho noting <b>Ursus \"is now dead\"</b> (Ursus died 1600), marks the dispute's late stage. (Cf. the Ursus thread: brahe-t_16000401001, brahe-t_15990922001, brahe-t_16000408001.)</li><li><b>A <i>Mechanica</i> distribution event.</b> Herwart <b>delivered Tycho's <i>Mechanica</i> (\"Theatrum Mechanicum\") to Philipp Eduard Fugger at Augsburg</b> — a datable gift-distribution event to register with the Mechanica distribution layer.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Herwart von Hohenburg; <b>Johannes Kepler</b> (the quoted author); <b>Ursus (Nicolaus Reimers Bär)</b>; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Philipp Eduard Fugger</b>; <b>Copernicus</b> (<i>De revolutionibus</i>), <b>Apollonius of Perga</b>, <b>Ptolemy</b>; the <b>Prutenic Tables</b>. Places: <b>Munich</b>, <b>Augsburg</b>, <b>Prague</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>.</li><li><b>Text note.</b> The <b>two diagrams</b> Kepler drew are not reproduced in the source (marked <b>[Figure]</b>); the lettered descriptions (A–D; E–H) are translated as written. The Greek <i>ὑποτιθεῖ</i> (\"posits/hypothesises\") is reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Monachij die 11. Sept. Anno 1600</i> (Bavaria = New Style) → <b>11 September 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree. (Tycho's referenced letter is \"28. Augusti stylo novo.\")</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> One Latin interpolation corrected per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII p.357: the phantom clause 'Ptolomaeo sententia' removed from between 'perfuadere vult,' and 'PERGAeUM', restoring Dreyer's direct construction 'perfuadere vult, PERGÆUM vel pro eo COPERNICUM loqui...'. English corrected to match, removing the translator's own compensating bracket that had papered over the interpolation's awkward logic.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (Herwart's delivery of the <i>Mechanica</i> to <b>Fugger</b>; and the <b>verbatim Kepler analysis</b> — the logical proof that Ursus could only have had the hypothesis from Tycho; the detailed misreadings of Copernicus <i>De rev.</i> III.25 / V.3,35 and of Apollonius's Ptolemaic lemma; the \"(mobile)\" sleight; and the <b>\"philosophical theft\" verdict</b>) is firmly and carefully construed, with no sharpening of Kepler's argument. The two <b>diagrams</b> are noted as not reproduced; the lettered model-descriptions and the cycle-figures (3434, 17188 years) are rendered as written.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VIII pp.353-357, within read slice pp.353-358 — p.358 belongs to the next letter, 'Tycho Brahe ad Martinum Braschium') visually against Dreyer, including the entire embedded Kepler quotation on the Ursus/Tycho/Apollonius of Perga priority dispute. Checked all citations (Revolutionum lib.3 c.25, lib.5 c.3 and 35), the two geometric-diagram captions and their letter-by-letter description (A-D, then E-H), the orbital periods (3434 and 17188 years), the '5 Planetarum' figure, place and person names (Augsburg, Fugger, Munich, Kepler, Ursus, Copernicus). One TEXT_ERROR found as detailed above; everything else in this dense technical letter renders Dreyer's text faithfully, including the opening/closing lines and the 11 September 1600 dateline.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text inserts a clause ('in Ptolemy's view') that is absent from Dreyer's printed page 357. The published English faithfully translates the corrupted source ('wants to persuade that Ptolemy's view [i.e. Perga's, or Copernicus for him] speaks of...') and even adds its own clarifying bracket to paper over the resulting awkwardness — a sign the interpolation garbles the logic of this central sentence in Kepler's account of the Ursus priority dispute, which in Dreyer simply reads 'wants to persuade [us that] Perga, or in his place Copernicus, speaks of...'. Dreyer prints: <i>At URSUS aut cæcus aut vafer, interpofitione parenthetica vocis (mobilis) perfuadere vult, PERGÆUM vel pro eo COPERNICUM loqui de communi illo motu omnium 5 Planetarum... [Dreyer TBOO VIII p.357, line 3-4: no 'Ptolomaeo sententia' clause — 'vult,' is followed directly by 'PERGAeUM']</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The defect verified directly against Dreyer TBOO VIII p.357 page image (not the OCR text layer): the printed text reads 'perfuadere vult, PERGÆUM vel pro eo COPERNICUM loqui' with no intervening clause, exactly as the audit's Dreyer claim states. No other part of the letter was touched; every changed word is accounted for in outcomes above.</p>", "ms": {"l": "Sammelhandschrift (3 works)", "sh": "Cod. 10686/4-5", "in": "Austrian National Library (ÖNB)", "u": "http://data.onb.ac.at/rec/AC13961679"}, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10686.4."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000908001", "next": "brahe-t_16000923001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports delivering the *Mechanica* to Philipp Eduard Fugger at Augsburg and transmits Kepler's earlier letter on Tycho's hypotheses, noting that Ursus, who arrogated them, is now dead.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000911001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000915001", "d": "1600-09-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Martin Brasch (Braschius) — Prague, 15 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "<p><b>Note.</b> Tycho's reply to Brasch's letter of 8 Aug 1600 (brahe-t_16000808001). The recipient is addressed <b>\"Martin Brasch\"</b> (<i>Martinum Braschium</i>) — the Rostock poet <b>Martin Brasch / Martinus Braschius (1550–1601)</b>; that letter's recorded sender \"Nicolaus Braschius\" is most likely a <b>catalogue error</b> (same correspondent — same poems, Chytraeus, Bacmeister, Rostock context). Flagged. Day of month missing in the source (\"<i>die Septembris</i>\"); <b>15</b> supplied from slug/label and confirmed by the internal \"yesterday, the 14th of September by the reformed Calendar.\"</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MARTINUM BRASCHIUM</p><p>QUAS 8 die Augusti stylo vestro ad me dedisti literas, Eruditissime BRASCHI, eas heri, qui erat 14 Septembris iuxta reformatum Calendarium, per Dn. GODELMANNUM, Legatum Saxonicum hîc agentem, recepi, quibus nunc, ipso etiam sic ob nuncij festinationem admonente, haec, quantum ferunt aliae meae occupationes, properanter rescribo. De Elegiaco tuo Carmine bene est, quod meas etiam qualescunque non respuis notas: incoeperam quodam die pomeridianis horis (nam ante meridiem seriora tractanda sunt) nonnulla in eodem mutare atque aliter disponere, quaedam etiam omittere, atque nonnullibi alia supplere, idque antequam ex Arce residentiae meae Bennaticâ huc Pragam a Sacra Caesarea Maiestate, Domino nostro Clementiss. quamprimum Pilsenâ redierat, clementissime evocatus, ubi etiamnum usque in tertium Mensem ab eadem retineor, et quantum intelligo, cupit ipsius Maiestas, ut in viciniore magisque oportuno habitem loco. Toto vero illo tempore, dum hîc sum, tot mihi pro ipsius Maiestate et alias quoque peragenda fuêre, ut otium mihi non superfuerit ea vel semel in manus resumendi, nedum plenius recognoscendi, sed fiet id, nacta, uti spero, aptiore et tranquilliore commoditate. Interim vellem, ut carmen illud tacitus tibi reservares, nec cuiquam communicares; sunt enim quaedam in eo, quae minus apposite relata sunt, nec pro Caesare (ut de me ipso nihil dicam) satis decore aut convenienter, de quibus, ubi ijs denuo vacare licuerit, te ingenue admonebo, vel nostra transmutatio id facile edocebit, neque enim dubito, quin pro tuo candore, prout etiam nunc polliceris, sis aequi bonique laturus. Alterum illud carmen Epicum de VARADINO, cuius meministi, nondum vidi; nec mihi cum Domino MELCHIORE RÖDERO, cuius mentionem facis, ulla hactenus intercessit noticia, neque etiam scio, an hîc sit, quantum relatione aliorum audio, in Ungaria est; quare de eo et ijs, quae ipsum concernunt, non habeo amplius quod scribam.</p><p>MELISSUS tandem expectationem et spem, quam de ipso concepimus, redemit, missis, paulo antequam Benaticâ abirem, ternis ad me diversi generis carminibus, quorum Exemplum addam, si tempus descriptionem tulerit. Verum ut dicam quod res postulat, videtur in hisce segniore vena praeditus quam alias, sive quod haec ingravescens aetas (est enim, uti opinor, plus quam sexagenarius) spiritus Poëticos minus alacres et humiliores producat, sive, quod tu etiam fateris, ardor ille temporum intervallis plus vel minus incendatur, interdumque lentior evadat. Prae caeteris autem, Epigramma illud primum, quod de me lusit, minus vividum nec fatis concinnum apparet, unde hanc mihi sumsi audaciam, ut aliqua in eo alterarem, quamvis ne sic quidem per omnia arrideat, nec scio, an is meas animadversiones tam libenter ferat atque tu; quare Epigramma illud, cum alia in mei gratiam hinc inde concinnata carmina aliquando forte cudentur, omittendum potius duco, quam ut publicatum autori suo minus sit honorificum, aut mea ieiuna immutatio illi ingrata stomachum moveat.</p><p>De Poëmatis tuis vel orationibus Philosophicis Imperatori dedicandis, non scio quid consulam, nam licet Caesarea Matas erga eruditos satis sit beneficax et liberalis, nec respiciat, cui religionis formae addicti sint, tamen Consiliarij difficiliores sunt et lenti in exequendo, minusque favent ijs, qui catholicam (uti vocant) religionem non profitentur: si quid autem in ipsius Maiestatis honorem conscribere lubuerit, sit hoc potius carmen quoddam grave et nervosum, de rebus ab ipsius Antecessoribus inde a CAROLO V. et denique a se ipso hactenus praeclare gestis, ubi etiam per occasionem inter caetera eius Elogia inferre poteris, quod Astronomiae instaurationem ad instar ALPHONSI illius veteris Castiliae et Siciliae Regis nostro aevo benignissime promoveat. Oportet etiam succincta aliquot et arguta Epigrammata subiungere, nam talibus maxime afficitur Caesar, et brevitate gaudet. Verum haec tuo potius relinquo arbitrio, qui rectius nosti, quid hîc faciendum vel secus.</p><p>DAVIDEM CHYTRAeUM superiore Iunio Fatis concessisse iam antea inaudieram, uti et Maio antecedente nostrum in Dania HEMMINGIUM, ambos profectae aetatis senes et de Republica literaria bene meritos, quorum animae sint in benedictione et pace: memoria ad posteros unâ cum monumentis relictis perduret ac fructum faciat. Perlustravi ea, quae in CHYTRAeI obitum concinnasti, apprimeque placuerunt. Legi etiam scriptulum istud unâ transmissum de ijs, quae hoc Anno in Arctois oris usque ad Mensem Maium memoratu digna evenerunt, in quo sunt plaeraque apud Suecos gesta auditu tristia minusque iucunda. Deus omnia moderetur in bonum! Scripserat ad me superiore Anno praefatus optimae memoriae CHYTRAeUS et consignationem quandam miserat de ijs, quae historiolae huius Anni, meam e Dania huc in Bohemiam emigrationem concernentia, inserere constituerat, eaque meo arbitrio (aliter si luberet) formanda reliquerat. Ego vero illi rescribens chartam literis imposui, qua id quod expetebat iuxta meam sententiam insinuavi. At cum in eo quod misisti scripto nihil eiuscemodi inveniatur, suspicor literas meas non satis mature ad ipsum pervenisse, aut quod morbo impeditus et postea morte praeventus ea exequi nequiverit. Si itaque literae illae meae unâ cum scheda inserta adhuc inter ipsius chartas posthumae inveniuntur, cuperem ea, quae illi annotâram, convenienti loco historiolae huius Anni adhuc immisceri, neque enim dubito, quin quispiam Vestratium eam usque ad Anni finem continuaturus sit, ut sic Historia Arctoa ab optimo illo CHYTRAeO inde ab Anno millesimo quingentesimo inchoata hoc millesimo sexcentesimo compleatur, atque hac ratione integrum contineat centenarium. Poterit vero id quod ego fieri averem, mea opinione rectius subiungi ijs, quae a CHYTRAeO consignata sunt, tanquam ab ipso, dum adhuc viveret, exarata, ne si aliqua ibi reperirentur vicinis Danis minus grata (uti est veritas plaerumque odiosa) nemini inter Vestros Professores adhuc superstites id imputari posset. Placebit (uti opinor) Caesari, ubi viderit longe etiam remotas Academias sua ob Astronomiae promotionem depraedicare encomia, nam et ab Illustri ac Magnifico Domino CORRADUCIO, Imperij Procancellario, quam primum antecedente Anno e Legatione Italica et Romana huc reversus esset, quaesivit, an Romae vel alibi in Italia audivisset, quod me ob Astronomiam redintegrandam huc vocârit, cumque is affirmaret, se tam Romae quam in alijs locis hoc relatione praestantium Virorum percepisse, id Caesari admodum placuit, et sibi ipsi vel hoc nomine quasi congratulari visus est, prout ab ipso Domino CORRADUCIO haec ad me perlata sunt; atque hinc caetera, quae desidero, aestimare licebit. Nihil tamen Vobis praescribo, sed omnia vestro arbitrio et consulto iudicio facienda [vel o]mittenda relinquo. Referet quoque plura ex me supranominatus Dn. Doctor GÖDELMANNUS, ubi vos, uti constituit, accesserit.</p><p>Vale et Clarissimum Virum Dn. D. LUCAM BACKMEISTERUM (cui etiam in ultimis meis literis de memorata re, si recte recordor, nonnulla scripsi) ex me officiose saluta. Si tabellarius tam diu expectârit, denuo illi scribam. Saluta etiam caeteros Amicos meos Rostochienses. Magister CHRISTIERNUS ante 6 septimanas hinc a me discessit in Patriam rediturus atque Vos in transitu allocuturus, quare eum iam istic aut esse aut fuisse existimo. Tu vero, mi BRASCHI, nulla intermissa oportunitate, mihi rescribe, habebis me semper tui studiosissimum.</p><p>Iterumque vale.<br>Dabantur festinanti calamo Pragae die 15 Septembris Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MARTIN BRASCH.</b></p><p>The letter you sent me on the 8th day of August, by your style [Old Style], most learned Brasch, I received yesterday — which was the 14th of September by the reformed Calendar — through <b>Lord Gödelmann, the Saxon Legate acting here</b>; to which I now, he too urging it on account of the messenger's haste, hastily write back this, so far as my other occupations allow. About your <b>Elegiac poem</b>, it is well that you do not reject even such notes of mine as they are. I had begun, one day in the afternoon hours (for before noon the more serious matters are to be handled), to change and arrange otherwise some things in it, to omit some, and here and there to supply others — and that before I was most clemently <b>summoned from the castle of my residence at Benátky hither to Prague by the Sacred Imperial Majesty</b>, our most clement Lord, as soon as He had returned from Pilsen; where I am still kept by Him, now into the third month, and, so far as I understand, <b>His Majesty desires that I dwell in a nearer and more convenient place</b>. But in all this time, while I am here, I have had so much to do, both for His Majesty and otherwise, that no leisure has remained to me to take it [the poem] up even once more, much less to revise it more fully — but it shall be done, when (as I hope) a fitter and quieter convenience is got. Meanwhile I should wish you to keep that poem quietly to yourself, and share it with no one; <b>for there are certain things in it less aptly stated, and not decorous or fitting enough for the Emperor (to say nothing of myself)</b>, of which, when I may again have leisure for them, I will frankly admonish you — or our revision will easily show it; nor do I doubt that, of your candour (as you even now promise), you will take it in good and equal part. That other <b>Epic poem on Várad</b>, which you mention, I have not yet seen; nor have I hitherto had any acquaintance with Lord <b>Melchior Röder</b>, of whom you make mention; nor do I even know whether he is here — so far as I hear by others' report, he is in Hungary; wherefore of him, and the things concerning him, I have nothing more to write.</p><p><b>Melissus</b> has at last redeemed the expectation and hope we conceived of him, having sent me, a little before I left Benátky, three poems of different kinds — of which I will add a copy, if time allow the transcription. But, to say what the matter requires, <b>he seems in these endowed with a more sluggish vein than otherwise</b> — whether because his advancing age (for he is, I think, more than sixty) produces poetic spirits less lively and lower, or, as you too confess, because that ardour is kindled more or less by intervals of time, and sometimes turns slower. Above the rest, <b>that first epigram, which he composed about me</b>, appears less vivid and not quite elegant; whence I took this boldness, to alter some things in it — although even so it does not in all points please, nor do I know whether he would bear my emendations as gladly as you. Wherefore that epigram — when other poems composed here and there in my honour are perhaps one day struck off — <b>I judge had rather be omitted than, published, be less honourable to its author, or my meagre alteration, unwelcome to him, move his stomach</b>.</p><p>About <b>your Poems or Philosophical orations to be dedicated to the Emperor</b>, I know not what to advise; for although the Imperial Majesty is beneficent and liberal enough toward the learned, and does not regard to which form of religion they are addicted, yet <b>the Councillors are more difficult, and slow in executing, and favour less those who do not profess the Catholic (as they call it) religion</b>. But if you should wish to write something in His Majesty's honour, <b>let it rather be a certain grave and sinewy poem, about the things gloriously done by his Ancestors from Charles V on, and at last by himself</b> — where also, among his other Elogia, you may by occasion insert that <b>he most kindly promotes, in our age, the restoration of Astronomy, after the likeness of that old Alfonso, King of Castile and Sicily</b> [Alfonso X]. You ought also to subjoin some succinct and witty epigrams, for by such the Emperor is most affected, and <b>he delights in brevity</b>. But these I leave rather to your judgement, who know better what is here to be done or not.</p><p>That <b>David Chytraeus</b> passed away to the Fates last June, I had already heard before; as also, the May preceding, <b>our Hemmingsen in Denmark</b> — both old men of advanced age and well-deserving of the literary Commonwealth; <b>may their souls be in blessing and peace</b>, [their] memory endure to posterity, together with the monuments they left, and bear fruit. I have looked through what you composed on Chytraeus's death, and it pleased me much. I read also that little writing sent along, about the things worthy of mention that befell this year in the Arctic [northern] regions up to the month of May — in which are very many things done among the <b>Swedes</b>, sad and unpleasant to hear. May God moderate all to the good! The aforesaid <b>Chytraeus</b>, of best memory, had written me last year, and sent a certain memorandum about what he had resolved to insert in this year's \"little history\" [<i>historiola</i>], concerning <b>my emigration from Denmark hither into Bohemia</b>, leaving it to be shaped by my judgement (otherwise, if I pleased). And I, writing back to him, enclosed a paper by which I conveyed what he sought, according to my view. But since in the writing you sent nothing of the kind is found, I suspect either that my letter did not reach him in time, or that, hindered by illness and afterward forestalled by death, he could not carry it out. If, then, that letter of mine, together with the inserted sheet, is still found among his posthumous papers, <b>I should wish what I had annotated for him to be yet inserted in a fitting place of this year's history</b>; for I do not doubt that someone of yours will continue it to the year's end, so that <b>the Arctic History — begun by that excellent Chytraeus from the year 1500 — may be completed in this year 1600, and thus contain a whole century</b>. But what I should wish done, in my opinion, would be more rightly subjoined to what was recorded by Chytraeus, as though written by him while he yet lived — <b>lest, if anything there be found less pleasing to the neighbouring Danes (truth being for the most part odious), it could be imputed to none among your Professors still surviving.</b> It will please the Emperor (as I think) when he sees even far-remote Academies proclaim encomia for his promotion of Astronomy; for the Emperor asked the illustrious <b>Lord Coraduz, Imperial Vice-Chancellor</b> — as soon as, last year, he had returned hither from the Italian and Roman Legation — whether he had heard at Rome or elsewhere in Italy <b>that he [the Emperor] had called me hither to restore Astronomy</b>; and when [Coraduz] affirmed that he had perceived this, both at Rome and in other places, by the report of distinguished Men, it pleased the Emperor much, and he seemed, even on this account, as it were to congratulate himself — as these things were reported to me by Lord Coraduz himself; and from this you may estimate the rest. Yet I prescribe nothing to you, but leave all to be done or omitted by your judgement and considered opinion. The above-named <b>Lord Doctor Gödelmann</b> will also report more from me, when he comes to you, as he has resolved.</p><p>Farewell; and greet for me dutifully the renowned man <b>Lord Doctor Lucas Backmeister</b> (to whom too, in my last letter, I wrote some things about the said matter, if I remember rightly). If the courier waits so long, I will write him again. Greet also my other <b>Rostock</b> friends. <b>Master Christiernus [Longomontanus]</b> departed from me hence 6 weeks ago, to return to his fatherland and address you in passing; wherefore I think he is now either there, or has been. But you, my Brasch, with no opportunity let slip, write back to me; you shall have me always most zealous of you.</p><p>And again farewell.<br>Given with hurrying pen at Prague, the 15th day of September, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient (name flagged).</b> <b>Martin Brasch / Martinus Braschius (1550–1601)</b>, the Rostock professor and Neo-Latin poet — addressed here as <i>Martinum Braschium</i>. <b>brahe-t_16000808001 gives the sender as \"Nicolaus Braschius,\" which is most likely a catalogue error</b>: the two letters are one exchange (the same elegiac poem, <i>Carmen de Varadino</i>, Melissus, Chytraeus, Bacmeister, Rostock circle). Identification corrected toward <b>Martin</b>; flagged for correction at brahe-t_16000808001.</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's literary diplomacy and the Chytraeus \"Arctic History.\"</b> Reply to brahe-t_16000808001:</li><li><b>The verse business.</b> Tycho is emending Brasch's <b>elegiac poem</b> but, summoned from <b>Benátky to Prague</b> and \"kept into the third month\" (the Emperor wanting him \"in a nearer, more convenient place\" — the coming move to the Curtius house), has no leisure; he warns Brasch to keep it private, as some lines are \"not decorous enough for the Emperor.\" He hasn't seen the <i>Carmen de Varadino</i> or met <b>Melchior Röder</b> (said to be in Hungary). On <b>Melissus</b> (Paul Schede Melissus, now \"&gt;sixty,\" with \"a more sluggish vein\"), Tycho judges the <b>epigram Melissus wrote about him</b> weak, and would rather it be <b>omitted</b> than published or awkwardly emended.</li><li><b>Advice on dedicating to Rudolf II.</b> A revealing political note: the Emperor is liberal \"regardless of religion,\" <b>but the Councillors favour Catholics and are slow</b>. Tycho counsels a <b>grave poem on Habsburg deeds \"from Charles V\"</b>, casting Rudolf as <b>\"a new Alfonso [X] of Castile\"</b> for promoting astronomy, capped by <b>brief, witty epigrams</b> (\"the Emperor delights in brevity\").</li><li><b>Two deaths.</b> <b>David Chytraeus (June 1600)</b> and <b>Niels Hemmingsen (May 1600)</b> — the great Lutheran scholars — mourned (\"may their souls be in blessing and peace\"). (Cf. brahe-t_16000808001, brahe-t_16000919002.)</li><li><b>The \"Arctic History\" plan.</b> Chytraeus's annual <b>northern chronicle (<i>historiola</i>)</b>: Tycho had sent Chytraeus a sheet on <b>his own emigration from Denmark to Bohemia</b> for insertion, but it didn't make it in before Chytraeus's death; Tycho asks that, if the sheet survives among the papers, it be inserted — <b>attributed to the dead Chytraeus, to shield surviving professors from Danish displeasure</b> (\"truth being for the most part odious\") — so the chronicle, \"begun from 1500,\" reaches \"a whole century\" in 1600. (Cf. the Sturtz-continuation plan, brahe-t_16000919002.)</li><li><b>The Coraduz/Rome anecdote.</b> A vivid court vignette: <b>Rudolf II asked Coraduz, back from the Roman legation, whether Tycho's call \"to restore astronomy\" was known in Italy</b> — and was pleased to hear it praised at Rome.</li><li><b>Longomontanus</b> left for Denmark \"6 weeks ago\"; greetings to <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (the Elder) and the Rostock circle.</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Pair with <b>brahe-t_16000808001 (Brasch's letter, 8 August 1600)</b> and the Chytraeus-chronicle thread (brahe-t_16000919002).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Martin Brasch; <b>Gödelmann</b> (Saxon Legate); <b>Melchior Röder</b>; <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b>; <b>David Chytraeus</b>; <b>Niels Hemmingsen</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>, <b>Charles V</b>, <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b>; <b>Coraduz</b>; <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>; <b>Longomontanus</b>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Pilsen</b>, <b>Rostock</b>, <b>Rome/Italy</b>, <b>Hungary</b>, Denmark, Sweden.</li><li><b>Date (protocol §3).</b> Day-number missing in the source (\"<i>die Septembris Anno 1600</i>\"); <b>15 September 1600 [N.S.]</b> supplied from slug/label and confirmed by \"yesterday … the 14th of September by the reformed Calendar.\" Flagged.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The day is <b>not</b> merely label-supplied: Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 360; running-head 5/15 Sept.) prints <i>15 die Septembris</i> — also confirmed by the internal “yesterday, the 14th of September.” Restored to header, Source, and translation.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the verse-emendation and Melissus business; the <b>religion-aware advice to dedicate a Habsburg poem casting Rudolf as a new Alfonso X</b>; the deaths of <b>Chytraeus and Hemmingsen</b>; the <b>Arctic-History century-completion plan</b> with the diplomatic attribution-to-Chytraeus device; the <b>Coraduz/Rome court anecdote</b>; and the Longomontanus/Bacmeister greetings) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. Two flags: the <b>recipient's name</b> (Martin, vs the record's likely-erroneous \"Nicolaus\") and the <b>supplied day</b> of the dateline.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 6 slice pages as page images (TBOO VIII pp. 356-361; the letter runs from mid-p.357 through p.360, bracketed by the tail of the preceding Herwart letter on p.356 and the start of the following Sturcius letter on p.361-362, confirming no page-range slippage). Collated source_text against Dreyer's printed Latin sentence by sentence for all five paragraphs and the closing formula: every proper name (GODELMANNUM/Legatum Saxonicum, MELCHIORE RODERO, MELISSUS, CAROLO V, ALPHONSI, DAVIDEM CHYTRAeUM, HEMMINGIUM, CORRADUCIO/Imperij Procancellario, LUCAM BACKMEISTERUM, CHRISTIERNUS), place-name (VARADINO/Ungaria, Bennaticâ, Pilsenâ, Pragae, Rostochienses), number/date (8 die Augusti, 14 Septembris, tertium Mensem, plus quam sexagenarius, ante 6 septimanas, Anno millesimo quingentesimo/sexcentesimo, 15 Septembris Anno 1600) and sum/role term matches Dreyer verbatim modulo long-s typography and word order. Then checked the English translation clause-by-clause against source_text: every sentence has a counterpart, no clause dropped, no invented facts, all numbers/dates/names correctly carried over (including the deliberately unresolved Julian/Gregorian calendar discrepancy in the opening line, rendered as-is rather than corrected), and the two potentially pronoun-ambiguous passages (Caesar-asks-Corraducio; Corraducio-affirms/Caesar-congratulates-himself) resolve to the correct antecedents, matching Dreyer, with disambiguating brackets clearly marked as editorial supplements. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000329004", "next": "brahe-t_16000920002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000920002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Replies hastily about revisions to Brasch's Elegiac poem and reports being summoned from Benátky to Prague by the Emperor, who desires him to remain there permanently.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000915001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000916001", "d": "1600-09-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Bording — Prague, 16 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 138 (letter draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD JACOBUM BORDINGUM].</p><p>MAGNIFICE et excellentissime vir, amice inprimis honorande. Scripsj bis T:ae M:ae post initium huius annj de pecunia, quae isthic mihj loco jnteresse debetur, primo per Claudium Mulaeum meum domesticum in Daniam abeuntem vere praeterito, postea per quendam magistrum Christiernum Longimontanum, natione Danum, quj etiam aliquamdiu mecum fuit et superiore julio se in patriam recepit. Et sperassem sane, me aliquid inter id temporis responsj recepturum, quod tamen factum non est. Quare cum hic tabellarius Rostochium iturus sit et confecto itinere huc Pragam rediturus, non potuj intermittere quin T:am M:am de hoc ipso negotio denuo commonefacerem. Quare te per amicitiam nostram iam tertium rogatum uolo, ut tuo consilio et authoritate interueniente procurare non degraueris, ut pecunia illa, quae a juniorum principum quaestore mihi isthic soluenda restat, quamprimum fierj possit, huc mittatur siue in aurea siue etiam in argentea moneta, pro ut antea ratione mittendj insinuauj, et si id prius indicato modo per aurigam Wittebergensem efficj nequierit, offeret se certa et tuta occasio per mag: dn: J. Godelmannum, quj hic legatum illustrissimj administratoris electoratus Saxonicj agit, post quatuordecim dies (utj herj mihj retulit) hinc Rostochium proficiscj cogitantem, quj etiam breuj huc reuertj necesse habet, cumque ipse sit amicus meus singularis, eiusque perspecta fides, ab illo obtinuj, ut hoc officium in adferenda secum illa quae istic mihi numeranda est pecunia in se recipiat, quare illj optime committj poterit. Et si jnteresse pro toto hoc anno usque ad subsequentis primum diem aprilis aut ad minimum huius dimidij elapsi annj una addatur, esset id mihi gratum, nam et aequum uidetur ut si quidem iam per dimidium annum pecunia illa frustrer, alterius dimidij annj solutionis praeueniente termino iste defectus compensetur, praesertim ex quo tam oportuna et indubia ratio id quicquid est pecuniarum huc transuehendj per dictum d: d: Godelmannum sese obtulerit. Quare non dubito, praestantissime d:ne Bordinge, quin mihi hac in parte et interueniente principis tuj (si opus fuerit) autoritate sis operam tuam non denegaturus; ubj ille accesserit, secum meum habebit chyrographum ulterius adhuc quam antea factum de solutione testans. Rogo autem peramanter, ut cum hoc tabellario ad nos reuertente pauculis indicare uelis, quid mihi isthic expectandum sit. Ego T:ae M:ae uicissim gratificandum perpetuo inueniar quam paratissimus.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 16. septembris stylo correcto anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to Jacob Bording.]</b></p><p>Magnificent and most excellent man, friend especially to be honoured. I have written twice to Your Magnificence since the beginning of this year about the money owed me there in place of interest — first by Claude Mule, my servant, going into Denmark last spring; afterward by a certain Master Christen Longomontanus, a Dane by nation, who was also for some time with me and last July betook himself to his homeland. And I had indeed hoped that I would receive some reply in that interval, which, however, has not happened. Wherefore, since this courier is about to go to Rostock and, his journey done, to return hither to Prague, I could not refrain from reminding Your Magnificence once more of this very business. So I would have you asked, now for the third time, by our friendship, that — with your counsel and authority intervening — you would not find it a burden to procure that that money, which remains to be paid me there by the treasurer of the younger princes, be sent hither as soon as may be, whether in gold or also in silver coin, as I earlier intimated regarding the means of sending. And if it cannot be done beforehand in the manner indicated, by the Wittenberg carter, there will offer itself a sure and safe occasion through Master Lord J. Godelmann — who acts here as legate of the most illustrious Administrator of the Saxon Electorate, and who is minded (as he told me yesterday) to set out hence to Rostock after fourteen days, and who must also return hither shortly; and since he is himself my singular friend, and his fidelity proven, I have obtained from him that he take this office upon himself, in bringing with him that money which is to be paid me there — wherefore it may best be committed to him. And if interest for this whole year, up to the first day of next April, or at the least for this half-year now elapsed, be added in together [with the principal], it would be welcome to me; for it seems but fair that, since I am now cheated of that money for half a year, this shortfall be made good by anticipating the term of the other half-year's payment — especially now that so opportune and undoubted a means of conveying hither whatever money there is has offered itself through the said Lord Doctor Godelmann. Wherefore I do not doubt, most excellent Lord Bording, that you will not deny me your effort in this part — with your prince's authority intervening too, if there be need. When he [the messenger] arrives, he will have with him my bond, attesting the payment, drawn yet further than before. And I ask most lovingly that, by this courier returning to us, you would indicate in a few words what I am to expect there. I, in turn, shall be found most ready to oblige Your Magnificence perpetually.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 16th of September, by the corrected style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; matter.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Prague</b> — a third dunning letter over <b>interest owed him by the Mecklenburg house</b> (here \"the treasurer of the <b>younger princes</b>,\" i.e. the under-age Dukes of Mecklenburg), the same capital whose recovery dominates the 1601 Meier/Duke-Ulrich correspondence. This 1600 letter shows Tycho still trying to get the <i>interest</i> remitted, a year before the principal dispute boiled over.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacob Bording (Jakob Bording the Younger)</b>, a councillor/physician in Mecklenburg service, used by Tycho as an intermediary of standing (he can invoke \"your prince's authority\") to chase the payment.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Claudius Mulaeus</i> = <b>Claude Mule (Mulaeus)</b>, Tycho's servant, an earlier letter-carrier to Denmark.</li><li><i>magister Christiernus Longimontanus</i> = <b>Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>, Tycho's chief assistant, who carried the second letter and returned to Denmark in July 1600.</li><li><i>mag. dn. J. Godelmannus</i> = <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann (Godelmann)</b> (1559–1611), jurist and diplomat, here <b>legate of the Administrator of the Saxon Electorate</b> at the imperial court, and \"my singular friend\" — engaged by Tycho as a trusted bearer of the cash on his coming journey to Rostock. (Gödelmann recurs across the late correspondence as a recurring-person to be rendered consistently, protocol §7.)</li><li><i>illustrissimus Administrator Electoratus Saxonici</i> = the <b>Administrator (regent) of Electoral Saxony</b>, <b>Friedrich Wilhelm I of Saxe-Weimar</b>, regent for the minor Elector Christian II.</li><li><b>The financial ask.</b> Payment in <b>gold or silver coin</b>; the <b>Wittenberg carter</b> as fallback route, Gödelmann as the preferred one; interest to be reckoned <b>to 1 April next</b>, or at least for the half-year elapsed, the shortfall offset by anticipating the next term; Tycho's fresh <b>bond (<i>chirographum</i>)</b> to be handed over on receipt.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Praga Bohemorum</i> = Prague; <i>Rostochium</i> = Rostock; <i>Wittenberga</i> = Wittenberg.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> A <b>letter draft</b> in the Basel autograph collection; hence the bracketed editorial heading.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>T. M.</i> = <i>Tua Magnificentia</i> (\"Your Magnificence\"); <i>d. d.</i> = <i>Dominus Doctor</i>; <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary) → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — idiom).</b> Dateline <i>die 16. Septembris <b>stylo correcto</b></i> = \"by the <b>corrected style</b>,\" another of Tycho's labels for <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b> (alongside <i>stylo novo</i>, <i>stylo recognito</i>; cf. brahe-t_16000420001). Reproduced literally and glossed, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean Latin; the financial particulars and all named parties (Bording, Claude Mule, Longomontanus, Gödelmann, the Saxon Administrator, the Mecklenburg \"younger princes\") are secure, and the <i>stylo correcto</i> idiom is handled per protocol. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.209-212 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the full Latin letter -- Claude Mule and Christen Longomontanus as the two prior couriers, the treasurer of the younger princes, the Wittenberg carter, Johannes Godelmann's fourteen-day Rostock trip, the half-year/full-year interest clause, and the 16 September 1600 'stylo correcto' dateline -- word-for-word against the printed page. Text matches source_text exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 138.. Letter draft"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000331001", "next": "brahe-t_16000919001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15970802001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000919001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presses Bording for a third time to procure the money owed by the princes' treasurer and have it sent to Prague in gold or silver coin.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000916001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000916002", "d": "1600-09-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Lucas Backmeister — Prague, 16 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD LUCAM BACMEISTERUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>CLARISSIME et Praestantissime Vir, Amice inprimis honorande, etsi per M. CHRISTIERNUM nostrum hinc discedentem et in Daniam cogitantem, siquidem Rostochium transire et ibi forte aliquantisper subsistere constituerat, literas ad te dederim, partim de rebus meis, partim de ijs, quae historiolae annuae optimae memoriae Domini Doctoris DAVIDIS CHYTRAeI, prout ipsemet, etiam dum in vivis erat, proposuerat, inseri averem, commonefacientes; tamen oblata per hunc tabellarium ad Vos euntem et mox huc revertentem tam commoda oportunitate, non intermittendum duxi, quin denuo aliqua tibi scriberem, nec tamen priora multis repetam, id saltem ijs superaddam, me etiamnum desiderare, ut ea, de quibus prius rogâram, historiolae annuae praefati Domini D. CHYTRAeI usque ad huius Anni exitum ab aliquo Vestrorum, uti opinor, professore continuandae, competenti loco inserantur. Video enim in ijs chartis, quas mihi misit Poëta vester Dn. BRASCHIUS, quae in solstitio aestivo ob Autoris mox subsequentem obitum, narrationem interrumpunt, id omissum esse. Quare pluribus hac de re ad dictum Dn. BRASCHIUM scripsi, qui tibi meas facile ostendet literas, unde plura et particulariora hauries; neque tamen hîc quippiam urgeo, sed omnia cum vestro stabunt cadentque libero iudicio.</p><p>Quantum vero ad res meas attinet, sunt eae adhuc in eodem statu, quo erant, cum ultimas ad te mitterem. Maneo etiamnum hîc Pragae cum tota familia ex Caesaris voluntate hîc retentus, donec de loco habitationis commodiore et propinquiore, ubi exercitijs meis Astronomicis vacare possim, mihi clementissime prospexerit, qua de re etiamnum agitur, nam lente in hac Aula ob negotiorum molem omnia procedunt. Supellex mea unâ cum Instrumentis Astronomicis, quae Magdeburgi aliquamdiu relicta erant, in ea Albis parte, quae Dresdam et hunc locum intercedit, iam, uti ante biduum inaudivi, navigio appropinquant, ita ut sperem ea instante septimana hîc in Bohemia adfutura. Quare de loco, ubi disponenda sint (siquidem arx Benatica nimis hinc remota sit, nec admodum oportuna) iam eo maturius deliberamus, et negotium hoc acceleretur.</p><p>Non dubito, quin literas adfini tuo Magnifico Dn. BORDINGO inscriptas, quas praedictus M. CHRISTIERNUS secum attulit, expedivisse, et contenta earundem, quantum in te erat, promovisse. Si nihil adhuc in re illa pecuniaria, de qua aiebam, effectum est, nec aliqua isthic suppetit commoditas id tuto exequendi, poterit usque in adventum Dn. D. IOHANNIS GÖDELMANNI (qui se quatuordecim abhinc diebus Rostochium profecturum heri mihi indicavit) deferri, ut per ipsum quam optime fiat. Qua de re etiam ad praenominatum adfinem tuum latius perscripsi, quas literas, si tabellarius hic Gustrowium transiens id non praestiterit, actutum dirigas quaeso, atque ut idem tabellarius ratum secum adferat responsum, operam da, quin et tu ipsemet cum eodem mihi rescribas, simulque indices, quid rerum in vicina Dania et Suecia geratur. Narratum est hîc ante paucos dies, incerto tamen rumore, dissidium quoddam subortum inter Regem Daniae Regnique Nobilitatem, quod tamen irrita fama prolatum spero. Deus avertat a Patria mea intestina mala, quae plurimum secum damni trahere solent. Turca in Ungaria est cum maximo exercitu, cui nostri Imperatoris vix pro quarta parte par est: pugnet pro nobis armipotens Deus, cui etiam te tuosque omnes commendo et bene valere precor.</p><p>Dabantur properanter Pragae Bohemorum 16 die Septembris stylo novo Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO LUCAS BACKMEISTER.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Most renowned and most excellent Man, friend especially to be honoured: although, through our Master Christen [Longomontanus] — departing hence and intending [to go] into Denmark, since he had resolved to pass through Rostock and perhaps stay there a while — I gave you a letter, partly about my affairs, partly reminding [you] of those things I desired inserted into the <b>annual little history of Lord Doctor David Chytraeus</b>, of best memory (as he himself, while still alive, had proposed): nevertheless, so convenient an opportunity being offered by this courier going to you and soon returning hither, I thought I should not omit to write you something again. Yet I will not repeat the earlier things at length; this only I add to them: that I still desire that the matters about which I asked before be inserted in a fitting place in the aforesaid Lord Chytraeus's annual little history — to be continued, as I think, by some professor of yours, up to the end of this year. For I see, in those sheets your Poet Lord <b>Brasch</b> sent me — which break off the narration at the <b>summer solstice</b>, on account of the Author's soon-following death — that this is omitted. Wherefore I have written more on this matter to the said Lord Brasch, who will easily show you my letter, whence you will draw more and more particular things; yet I press nothing here, but everything will stand and fall by your free judgement.</p><p>As far as concerns my affairs, they are still in the same state as when I sent you my last. I still remain here at Prague, with my whole household, retained here by the Emperor's will, until he most clemently provides me a more convenient and nearer dwelling-place, where I may be free for my astronomical exercises — about which the matter is still in hand, for in this Court all things proceed slowly, on account of the mass of business. My furniture, together with the astronomical instruments which had been left a while at <b>Magdeburg</b>, are now (as I heard two days ago) approaching by ship in that part of the <b>Elbe</b> which lies between <b>Dresden</b> and this place, so that I hope they will be here in Bohemia in the coming week. Wherefore, concerning the place where they are to be set up (since the castle of <b>Benátky</b> is too remote from here, and not very convenient) we now deliberate the more maturely, and this business is being hastened.</p><p>I do not doubt that you have dispatched the letter addressed to your kinsman the Magnificent Lord <b>Bording</b>, which the aforesaid Master Christen brought with him, and have furthered its contents as far as was in you. If nothing has yet been effected in that money-matter of which I spoke, nor is any convenient means at hand there of carrying it out safely, it can be deferred until the arrival of Lord Doctor <b>Johann Gödelmann</b> (who indicated to me yesterday that he would set out for Rostock fourteen days hence), that it may best be done through him. About which I have also written more fully to your aforenamed kinsman; which letter, if this courier passing through <b>Güstrow</b> does not deliver, please direct at once, and take pains that the same courier bring back a definite reply — nay, that you yourself write back to me with the same, and at the same time indicate what is going on in neighbouring <b>Denmark and Sweden</b>. It was reported here a few days ago — by uncertain rumour, however — that some <b>dissension had arisen between the King of Denmark and the Nobility of the Realm</b>, which I hope was put about by an empty report. May God avert from my fatherland internal evils, which are wont to bring much damage with them. The <b>Turk</b> is in Hungary with a very great army, to which our Emperor's is scarcely equal for a fourth part: may God, mighty in arms, fight for us — to whom I also commend you and all yours, and pray that you fare well.</p><p>Given in haste at Prague of the Bohemians, the 16th day of September, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Bacmeister, 1530–1608), <b>Superintendent of Rostock</b> and theologian — the kinsman of Jacob Bording who handled the Mecklenburg interest-money (cf. brahe-t_16001115001, brahe-t_16000919001), here also Tycho's contact on the Chytraeus history.</li><li><b>The Chytraeus annual-history thread (cross-link to Brasch).</b> Tycho repeats his wish that his <b>migration narrative</b> be inserted into <b>David Chytraeus's annual history</b> (the <i>Saxonia</i> / <i>Chronicon</i>), now \"to be continued by some professor of yours up to the end of this year.\" He notes that the <b>sheets Brasch sent break off at the summer solstice</b> — Chytraeus having died then — so the desired matter is omitted; he refers Backmeister to his fuller letter to <b>Brasch</b> (the reply to which is brahe-t_16000920002, where Brasch reports the search failed and the Chronicle cannot easily be extended). \"Yet I press nothing … everything will stand and fall by your free judgement.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's affairs (autumn 1600).</b> Still <b>retained at Prague</b> with his household by the Emperor's will, awaiting \"a more convenient and nearer dwelling.\" His <b>furniture and the Magdeburg-stored instruments</b> are now coming <b>up the Elbe between Dresden and Bohemia</b> (expected \"in the coming week\") — the success of the Magdeburg leg (cf. brahe-t_16000404001) — and he is deliberating where to set them up, \"since <b>Benátky is too remote</b> and not very convenient\" (anticipating the move into the Prague Curtius house).</li><li><b>The debt-matter &amp; news.</b> The <b>600-thaler interest</b> business via <b>Bording</b>: if not yet done, to await <b>Gödelmann's</b> departure for Rostock (in 14 days). Tycho asks for news of <b>Denmark and Sweden</b>, mentioning an \"uncertain rumour\" of <b>dissension between Christian IV and the Danish nobility</b> (which he hopes false), and reports the <b>Turk in Hungary</b> \"with a very great army, to which our Emperor's is scarcely equal for a fourth part.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Lucas Backmeister; <i>M. Christiernus</i> = <b>Christen Longomontanus</b> (departed to Denmark via Rostock; cf. brahe-t_16000920002); <b>David Chytraeus</b> (the late historian); <b>Martin Brasch</b>; <b>Jacob Bording</b>; <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Christian IV</b> and the Danish nobility.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Pragae Bohemorum 16 die Septembris stylo novo Anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the Chytraeus-history request and its Brasch cross-link; the autumn-1600 state of Tycho's affairs at Prague; the instruments coming up the Elbe via Dresden and the Benátky-too-remote remark; the Bording/Gödelmann debt-matter; the Danish-nobility rumour and the Turkish war) is clear and fully corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII page images 360-364 visually (Read tool, pages 1-4 then 5-5). The Bacmeister letter runs from the bottom of p.361 ('TYCHO BRAHE AD LUCAM BACMEISTERUM') through the top of p.363 ('...Anno 1600.'); p.364 is confirmed to belong to the following letter (AD MELCHIOREM IOESTELIUM, dated 18 September) and is not part of this letter. Collated the published Latin source_text against the page images word-by-word and found it a faithful, essentially verbatim transcription (only ligature/long-s/ß-vs-ss spelling variance, which is out of scope per the taxonomy) -- no TEXT_ERROR. Collated the published English translation clause-by-clause against both the source_text and the Dreyer images, checking every item on the required list: Lucas Bacmeister/Backmeister; Christiernus/Christen (bracketed [Longomontanus] gloss, correctly not counted as fabrication); David Chytraeus; Brasch/Braschius; solstitio aestivo/summer solstice; Magdeburgi/Magdeburg; Albis/Elbe; Dresdam/Dresden; arx Benatica/Benatky; Bordingo/Bording; Iohannis Godelmanni/Johann Godelmann; quatuordecim diebus/fourteen days (correctly 'fourteen days hence', matching 'heri mihi indicavit'/'indicated to me yesterday'); Gustrowium/Guestrow; Dania et Suecia/Denmark and Sweden; Turca in Ungaria/the Turk in Hungary; 16 die Septembris 1600. Specifically verified the dissension clause 'inter Regem Daniae Regnique Nobilitatem' is rendered as 'between the King of Denmark and the Nobility of the Realm' with no added names or specificity, and the army-fraction clause 'vix pro quarta parte par est' is rendered correctly as 'scarcely equal for a fourth part' (not altered to any other fraction). All hedges (uti opinor / incerto tamen rumore / quod ... spero) are preserved as hedges in English, not hardened into assertions. No invented day-numbers, no fabricated closing material (the closing formula and date match exactly), no flipped pronouns/subjects, no silently dropped sentences -- every sentence of the Latin has an English counterpart and vice versa. Found zero defects across the full letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Lucas Bacmeister", "count": 4, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16000919002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16000919002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Repeats his request that certain matters be inserted in the late David Chytraeus's annual chronicle, which he expects a Rostock professor to continue through year's end.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000916002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000918001", "d": "1600-09-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Melchior Jöstel — Prague, 18 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MELCHIOREM IOeSTELIUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>DEDI iterum ad te literas, Eruditissime et amantissime Domine M. MELCHIOR IÖSTELI, ante paucos dies, de ijsdem ferme rebus, quas superioribus attigeram; ultimas autem hasce Domino Doctori FRANCIO, Iure consulto, nunc Comitis Embdensis Cancellario, hinc proficiscenti commisi, qui eas se recte curaturum addixit: interim commodum accidit, quod tuae antepenultimo Augusti Wittebergâ ad me datae intervenirent, quae eorum, de quibus antea memineram, resolutionem, quoad principaliora, continent; ad quam sic respondeo.</p><p>Polliceris ab initio te collationem Hypothesium brevi missurum, siquidem iam perfectiorem quam antea elaboratam habeas. Fac igitur, ut mature obtineam, et figuras decenter assigna; addenda est autem explicatio sufficiens, non quidem admodum prolixa, sed etiam non nimium concisa; descendendum etenim nonnunquam ad captum Tyronum. Spero itaque me intra duas vel tres hebdomadas id, quicquid erit, a te habiturum.</p><p>Quae de M. OeSTWALDO scribis, non improbo. Maneat isthic, siquidem id malit, et supputet nihilominus pro me, quae per ocij intervalla absolvere poterit, numerabo illi in singulos Anni quadrantes sex Ioachimicos, quod in singulas hebdomadas dimidium ferme Ioachimicum facit. Sed volo, ut etiam singulis Anni quadrantibus mittat, quae absoluta habuerit. Haec ad ipsum ferre poteris. Quamprimum Tabulae Lunares unâ cum ijs, quae huc requiruntur, ad mundum descriptae fuerint, mittam exemplar, ut inde Ephemerides supputet incipientes ab hoc labente 1600, et per sexennium instans continuandae, unâ cum Solari Diario atque aspectibus omniumque differentijs, prout antea fecit, solvam autem illi pro cuiuslibet Anni Ephemeride tres Ioachimicos, prout in ultimis ad te literis scripsi, sed vellem, ut calculum maturaret, quo omnia unâ cum adiungendis satis cito inprimi adque instantes Francofurti Nundinas Vernales mitti possint, in 4to maiori excusa.</p><p>Cum Typographo vestro M. ZACHARIA LEHMANO age, ut sex illos quaterniones, vel tres duplicatos, qui desiderantur in Progymnasmatis meis, inprimat, atque papyrum similem illi, quae in libro est, aeque nimirum albam et tenuem eiusdemque magnitudinis, sibi procuret, ita ut 1400 exemplaria de quolibet quaternione inprimat. Existimo, quod Ballam eiuscemodi papyri pro 10 florenis nanciscetur; nam et ego antea eam, quae in libro meo de Cometa Anni 77 est, Francofurti emi pro 8 florenis, Basileae paratam et longo itinere isthuc vectam, sed iam forte alia sunt tempora, omniaque nunc cariora. Quicquid id erit, solvam illi iusto et debito modo, ut indemnis sit: prospiciat saltem de papyro, uti dixi, quae conveniens sit, interea dum exemplum paratur. Mittam postea, quae praefationis loco praefigenda, tum quoque quae in conclusione subiungenda, ut et illa absolvat. Si pecunia mea tam cito e Mechelburgensi Ducatu advenerit, potes inde 25 Ioachimicos tecum retinere pro solutione tam papyri quam laboris Typographici, successive exponenda: verum si id non evenerit, mittam ipsemet cum Domino Doctore GÖDELMANO, qui hîc Legatum agit Saxonicum, pecuniam sufficientem; ille enim post 14 dies constituit hinc Rostochium proficisci, atque in itinere Wittebergam transire; forte etiam exemplum secum adferet, si alia negotia descriptionem non remorata fuerint.</p><p>Cognovi insuper ex hisce tuis, quâ de caussâ huc venire nequeas, et libenter tuo acquiesco consilio, neque amplius hac de re quidpiam movebo. Confido tamen, quod etiam isthic habitans operam tuam nonnunquam per otij oportunitatem, in ijs, quae a te fieri desiderâro, non denegaturus sis, inveniar vicissim gratus. Et siquidem medicinae applicâsti animum, et in ea facultate promovere constituisti, det Deus, ut omnia tibi feliciter et ex voto succedant. Memineris autem, medicinam rite praeparatam medicum efficere et pro ipso operari; non verba, sive HIPPOCRATIS, sive GALENI, sive cuiusvis alterius diserti authoris. An vero hoc, quod dixi, isthic assequeris, admodum dubito, optare id potius licet quam sperare. Sed efficies facile quod coeteri, ut mundus habeat, quod vult. Haec si IESSENIO nostro dixeris, partim risum, partim bilem illi movebit scio. Miror, quod ille mihi nihil scribat, nec indicet, quid de libro et Globo illi tradito factum sit.</p><p>De Genesi illa, quam nôsti, poteris suo tempore, cum rectius vacârit, dispicere et ad me quidpiam perscribere. Vale et primâ quaque occasione responde; saluta amicos meos Wittebergenses, quos nôsti.</p><p>Dabantur festinanter Praga Die 18 Septembris Anno 1600 stylo novo.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 364; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Rogo insuper velis providere de studioso uno vel altero ad haec studia apto, prout in ultimis scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MELCHIOR JÖSTEL.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>I gave a letter to you again, most learned and most loving Lord Master Melchior Jöstel, a few days ago, about almost the same matters I had touched in earlier ones; this last one I entrusted to <b>Lord Doctor Franz, the jurisconsult, now Chancellor of the Count of Emden</b>, setting out hence, who pledged to take good care of it. Meanwhile it conveniently happened that yours, sent me from Wittenberg on the antepenultimate [day] of August, came in — which contain the resolution, as to the principal points, of the things I mentioned before; to which I respond thus.</p><p>You promise, at the outset, that you will soon send the <b>collation of the Hypotheses</b>, since you now have it elaborated more perfectly than before. Do then [so] that I obtain it in good time, and assign the <b>figures [diagrams]</b> becomingly; a sufficient explanation must be added — not very prolix, but also not too concise, <b>for one must sometimes descend to the grasp of beginners</b>. I hope, therefore, to have it from you, whatever it is, within two or three weeks.</p><p>What you write about <b>Master Oswald</b> I do not disapprove. Let him stay there, since he prefers it, and nonetheless compute for me what he can finish through intervals of leisure; I will pay him <b>six joachimsthalers each quarter-year</b>, which makes nearly half a joachimsthaler a week. But I wish him also to send, each quarter, what he has finished — you can convey this to him. As soon as the <b>Lunar Tables</b>, with what is required here, have been fair-copied, I will send a copy, that he may from it compute <b>Ephemerides beginning from this passing [year] 1600, to be continued for the coming six years</b>, together with the <b>Solar Diary</b>, the aspects, and all the differences, as he did before; and I will pay him <b>three joachimsthalers for each year's Ephemeris</b>, as I wrote in my last — but I should wish him to hasten the calculation, that everything, with the additions, may be printed quickly enough and sent to the coming <b>Frankfurt spring fair</b>, printed in larger quarto.</p><p>Deal with your printer Master <b>Zacharias Lehmann</b>, that he print those <b>six quaternions (or three doubled) wanting in my <i>Progymnasmata</b></i>, and procure paper like that in the book — equally white and thin, and of the same size — so that he print <b>1,400 copies of each quaternion</b>. I estimate he will get a <b>bale of such paper for 10 florins</b> (for I earlier bought that in my book on the <b>Comet of [15]77</b> at Frankfurt for 8 florins — prepared at Basel and carried thither by a long journey — but now perhaps the times are different, and everything dearer). Whatever it be, I will pay him in a just and due manner, that he be without loss: let him only provide suitable paper, as I said, while the copy is being prepared. I will afterward send what is to be prefixed in place of a preface, and what to append in the conclusion, that he may finish those too. <b>If my money come so quickly from the Duchy of Mecklenburg</b>, you can retain <b>25 joachimsthalers</b> from it for payment of both paper and printing, to be laid out successively; but if not, I will myself send sufficient money with <b>Lord Doctor Gödelmann, who here acts as the Saxon Legate</b> — for he has resolved to set out hence for Rostock after 14 days, passing through Wittenberg on the way, and perhaps he will bring the copy with him, if other business has not delayed the fair-copying.</p><p>I have moreover learned from this letter of yours <b>for what cause you cannot come hither</b>, and I gladly acquiesce in your decision, nor will I press the matter further. Yet I trust that, even dwelling there, you will not deny your effort sometimes, through the opportunity of leisure, in what I shall wish done by you; I will in turn be found grateful. And since <b>you have applied your mind to medicine</b>, and resolved to advance in that faculty, may God grant all succeed for you happily and according to your wish. But remember that <b>rightly-prepared medicine makes the physician, and works for him — not words, whether of Hippocrates, or Galen, or any other eloquent author</b>. Whether you will attain this, there [at Wittenberg], I much doubt — one may rather wish it than hope for it; but you will easily do what the rest [do], so that the world may have what it wants. If you say these things to our <b>Jessenius</b>, I know it will move him partly to laughter, partly to bile. I wonder that he writes me nothing, nor indicates <b>what has been done about the book and Globe handed to him</b>. About that <b>Geniture</b> you know of, you can, in your time, when you have better leisure, look into it and write me something. Farewell, and reply at the first opportunity; greet my Wittenberg friends whom you know.</p><p>Given hastily at Prague, the 18th day of September, the year 1600, New Style.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I ask, moreover, that you be willing to provide one or another student apt for these studies, as I wrote in my last.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Melchior Jöstel</b>, Tycho's Wittenberg computational collaborator (cf. brahe-t_16000327001, brahe-t_16001008001, brahe-t_16001118001).</li><li><b>Resolves the \"Wolfgang Franz\" identity (cross-ref to brahe-t_16000327002, brahe-t_16001008001).</b> Tycho entrusts a letter to <i>\"<b>Doctor Franz, the jurisconsult, now Chancellor of the Count of Emden</b>\"</i> (East Frisia). This <b>confirms that \"Doctor Franz\" is a jurist</b> (not the Wittenberg theologian Wolfgang Franz, 1564–1628): the recurring <b>Franz</b> courier of Tycho's Wittenberg-orbit correspondence is a <b>lawyer who became chancellor to Count Enno III of East Frisia at Emden</b>. The earlier identification of the recipient of brahe-t_16000327002 as \"the theologian\" should accordingly be <b>corrected to the jurist</b>.</li><li><b>The planetary-hypotheses collation (technical; §5.1).</b> Jöstel will soon send the <b>collation of the Hypotheses</b> (the Tychonic/Copernican/Ptolemaic comparison for the second <i>Epistolae</i> book; cf. brahe-t_16001118001), with <b>diagrams</b> and a clear explanation pitched \"to the grasp of beginners.\"</li><li><b>Master Oswald — the ephemeris-computer (terms settled).</b> <b>Oswald</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000327001, where Tycho had offered to bring him to Prague) will <b>stay at Wittenberg</b> and compute on retainer: <b>6 joachimsthalers per quarter</b> (≈ ½ thaler/week) for general work, plus <b>3 joachimsthalers per year's Ephemeris</b>. Once Tycho's <b>Lunar Tables</b> are fair-copied, Oswald is to compute <b>Ephemerides for 1600 + six years</b> (with <b>Solar Diary</b>, aspects, differences) for the <b>Frankfurt spring fair</b>, in larger quarto.</li><li><b>The printing of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> (cross-ref to brahe-t_16001118001).</b> Tycho instructs that the <b>printer Zacharias Lehmann</b> print the <b>six wanting quaternions</b> of the <i>Progymnasmata</i> in <b>1,400 copies</b>, on paper \"white and thin\" like the book's (≈ 10 fl./bale — he recalls buying paper for the <b>comet-of-1577 book</b> at 8 fl., \"prepared at Basel\"); prefatory and concluding matter to follow. <b>Funding:</b> 25 joachimsthalers from the <b>Mecklenburg money</b> if it arrives, else via <b>Gödelmann</b> (here \"the Saxon Legate,\" about to travel Prague→Wittenberg→Rostock). A precise record of Tycho's late book-production economics.</li><li><b>The witty medicine aside (notable for Tycho's chymical bent).</b> Since Jöstel turns to <b>medicine</b>, Tycho — the Paracelsian alchemist — needles him: <i>\"rightly-prepared medicine makes the physician … not the words of Hippocrates or Galen\"</i> — doubting Jöstel will learn <i>that</i> at Wittenberg (\"you will easily do what the rest do, so the world may have what it wants\"), and predicting it would move <b>Jessenius</b> \"partly to laughter, partly to bile.\" A characteristic dig at bookish, non-chymical academic medicine.</li><li><b>The book and globe.</b> Tycho wonders that <b>Jessenius</b> has not reported \"what has been done about the <b>book and Globe</b> handed to him\" — i.e. the <b>fixed-star book + celestial globe sent to Elector Christian II of Saxony</b> via Jessenius (brahe-t_16000506001). Also asks Jöstel to look into \"that <b>Geniture</b>\" (a horoscope; §5.3).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Melchior Jöstel; <b>Doctor Franz</b> (jurist, Chancellor of the Count of Emden); <b>Master Oswald</b>; <b>Zacharias Lehmann</b> (the printer); <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (\"the Saxon Legate\"); <b>Johannes Jessenius</b>; <b>Hippocrates</b> and <b>Galen</b> (the medical authorities).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Praga Die 18 Septembris Anno 1600 stylo novo</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (after the dateline), restored from Dreyer VIII p.364; dropped by the working transcription.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored 'forte' (Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.364) for the one-letter corruption 'sorte' in our transcription; the English ('perhaps') already matched Dreyer's actual reading and required no change.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the hypotheses-collation request; Oswald's ephemeris-computing terms; the <i>Progymnasmata</i> printing via Zacharias Lehmann with its copy-count, paper, and funding; the witty chymical-medicine aside; the book-and-globe query to Jessenius) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated — and it <b>resolves the \"Doctor Franz\" identity</b> as the jurist/Emden chancellor. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (TBOO VIII p.362[bottom]-p.364, read as page images) against source_text, including the postscript the packet flags as 'restored from Dreyer p.364' -- verified this postscript ('Rogo insuper velis providere...') is genuinely printed there verbatim, so the restoration was accurate, not a fabrication. Every name (Oestwaldus, Zacharias Lehmann, Goedelmannus, Iessenius), sum (6, 3, 10, 8, 25 Joachimsthalers/florins), count (1400 copies, 14 days), and the closing date '18 Septembris Anno 1600' match Dreyer exactly, apart from one single-letter word corruption (forte/sorte) that did not affect the translation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our Latin has 'sorte' where Dreyer prints 'forte' (TBOO VIII p.364), a one-letter corruption. The translator rendered the sense correctly ('perhaps'), matching Dreyer's actual word rather than the corrupted source_text. Dreyer prints: <i>forte etiam exemplum secum adferet, fi alia negotia defcriptionem non remorata fuerint</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single TEXT_ERROR defect directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.364, line 20): Dreyer prints 'forte', confirming the audit. Fixed only the Latin; the English translation was already correct. No crux remains.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Melchior Jöstelius", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000327001", "next": "brahe-t_16001008001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presses Jöstel to send the collation of the Hypotheses with assigned diagrams and adequate explanation, and asks what the printer and Master Oswald will undertake.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000918001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000919001", "d": "1600-09-19", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Jacob Bording → Tycho Brahe — Güstrow, 19 September 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, fol. 73f. (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ET NOBILI VIRO D:NO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO HEREDITARIO IN KNUDTSTRUP, AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO.<br>Generose et nobilis vir, amice inprimis honorande.</p><p>QUOD ad Generos:tis T: litteras, quas jam ternas accepi, aliquanto serius respondeam, non mea culpa factum est. Nam Claudius Mulaeus, cui priores traditas Generos:tas T. scribit, ipsemet mihi non adfuit, nec responsum ullum a me petijt. Alteras m. Christianus Longomannus me absente hic in aula reliquit, id quod Generos:tas T: ex adiecta hisce schedula, manu eius scripta, cognoscet. Quare ut me excusatum habeat, maiorem in modum rogo. Jnterim tamen quam posteriores ad manus meas pervenerunt, sexcentos illos thaleros, qui juniorum principum Megapolensium nomine Generos:ti T: debentur, Rostochium ad d. Lucam Backmeisterum, affinem meum, uti Generos:tas T: voluit, perferendos curavi. Egi quidem diligenter cum quaestore, ut in moneta aurea, quod Generos:tas T: cupit, solutio fieret, sed monetam eam in tanta summa conficere non potuit. Quare ut eo quoque nomine Generos:ti T: excusatus sim, itidem rogo. Chyrographum fidei meae creditum Generos:ti T: remitto, nam alterum, quod m. Christianus Longomannus d. Backmeistero tradidit, quaestor retinuit. Quod reliquum est Generos:tem T: divini numinis protectioni commendo et officia mea eidem defero promptissima.</p><p>Datae Gustrovij 19. die mensis septemb. stylo veteri anno 1600. Gener: T: studiosiss:<br>Jacobus Bordingus</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous and noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, hereditary lord in Knudstrup, his friend especially to be honoured. Generous and noble man, friend especially to be honoured.</b></p><p>That I answer Your Generosity's letters — of which I have now received three — somewhat late, has not happened by my fault. For Claude Muler, to whom Your Generosity writes the first were delivered, did not himself present himself to me, nor did he seek any answer from me. The second, Master Christian Longomannus left here at the court in my absence — as Your Generosity will learn from the note attached hereto, written in his hand. Wherefore I ask, in the greater degree, that you hold me excused. Meanwhile, however, as soon as the latter [letters] came to my hands, those six hundred thaler which are owed to Your Generosity in the name of the junior Princes of Mecklenburg, I had conveyed to Rostock, to Master Lucas Backmeister, my kinsman, to be delivered — as Your Generosity wished. I dealt diligently, indeed, with the treasurer, that the payment be made in gold coin, as Your Generosity desires; but he could not make up that coin in so great a sum. Wherefore, that I may be excused to Your Generosity on that score also, I likewise ask. The bond entrusted to my good faith I return to Your Generosity; for the other, which Master Christian Longomannus delivered to Master Backmeister, the treasurer retained. As for what remains, I commend Your Generosity to the protection of the divine Power, and offer the same my most ready services.</p><p>Given at Güstrow, the 19th day of the month of September, old style, in the year 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your Generosity,<br>Jacob Bording.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Jacob Bording</b> (Bordingus), an official at the <b>ducal court of Mecklenburg at Güstrow</b>; <i>affinis</i> (kinsman by marriage) of <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> of Rostock.</li><li><b>The Mecklenburg debt.</b> This documents the <b>600 thaler</b> owed Tycho \"in the name of the junior Princes of Mecklenburg\" (<i>juniorum principum Megapolensium</i> — <i>Megapolis</i> = Mecklenburg) — the principal of which the <b>200 reichsthaler of interest</b> in Tycho's quittance (brahe-t_15990110001, to the Mecklenburg rent-master Andreas Meyer) were a part. Bording has had the sum conveyed to <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> at Rostock, per Tycho's instruction; he apologises that the <i>treasurer (quaestor)</i> could not assemble so large a sum <b>in gold coin</b> (<i>moneta aurea</i>) as Tycho wished, and returns one of the two <b>bonds</b> (<i>chirographa</i>).</li><li><b>Couriers.</b> <i>Claudius Mulaeus</i> = <b>Claude Muler</b> (the household member of brahe-t_15980627001), to whom the first letters were entrusted but who never delivered them; <i>Christianus Longomannus</i> = <b>Longomontanus</b>, who left the second batch at Güstrow and gave the matching bond to Backmeister.</li><li><b><i>quaestor</b></i> rendered \"treasurer\" (the ducal fiscal officer); <i>chirographum</i> = a handwritten bond/IOU.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Gustrovij 19. die … septemb. stylo veteri … 1600</i> = <b>19 September 1600, Old Style</b> (<i>stylo veteri</i> explicit; Lutheran Mecklenburg kept the Julian calendar). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the financial matter (the 600-thaler Mecklenburg debt; the gold-coin difficulty; the bonds; Backmeister, Longomontanus, Claude Muler) is clear and dovetails with the Mecklenburg quittance brahe-t_15990110001. No ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO XIV pp.211-214 (page images, read visually), corroborated by the adjacent Latin regest (no.208) summarizing the same content. Letter proper on p.212, text matches Dreyer word for word. Numbers (three letters received, 600 thaler) and place names (Mecklenburg, Rostock) all correct; the date (19 September, old style, 1600) correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 23, Fol. 73 f.. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000916001", "next": "brahe-t_16001115001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000916001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16001115001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Excuses his delayed replies and reports dispatching the six hundred thalers owed to Tycho to Backmeister at Rostock, though the treasurer declined to pay in gold coin.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000919001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000919002", "d": "1600-09-19", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Lucas Bacmeister → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 19 September 1600 [Old Style]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Rostock theologian <b>Lucas Bacmeister</b> <i>to</i> Tycho.</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE GENERIS, DOCTRINA AC VIRTUTE PRAESTANTISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO HAeREDITARIO IN KNUDSTORFF, ASTRONOMO EXCELLENTISSIMO, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO INPRIMIS HONORANDO. PRAGAe.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et clarissime vir, Domine et amice inprimis colende. Binas abs te accepi literas hoc mense Septembri; priores die 8 mihi reddidit M. CHRISTIERNUS, qui ad tres septimanas Witebergae substiterat, posteriores tabellarius Praga a D. GODELMANNO huc ablegatus heri. Scis autem stylo nos Iuliano hîc uti. Utraeque negocium pecuniarium tuum mihi commendarunt, de quo et ad affinem meum Dn. Cancellarium D. BORDINGUM scripseras, quem Lubecae tum agentem ego monui, ut negocium istud suo loco promoveret. Venit is ante paucos dies Lubeca Gustrovium ab Illustriss. Principe UDALRICO vocatus, postquam Cels. eius in venatione tum circa Grabovium existenti nunciatum fuisset de obitu nepotis ex fratre Ducis SIGISMUNDI AUGUSTI, qui hydrope alijsque morbis diu conflictatus die 5 Septemb. Ivenaci pie decessit ex hac vita. Funus ei fiet die 10 Octob. Suerini, ubi parentis IOH. ALBERTI et fratris IOHANNIS aliorumque ex ea linea defunctorum corpora quiescunt. Eadem occasione Dn. Cancellarius Quaestorem Iuniorum Principum, qui et Ducis UDALRICI iam Quaestor est, in aula reperit, et effecit, ut hodie mihi per ministrum suum in meis aedibus 600 illos thaleros tibi debitos numeraverit, recepta apocha tua, quam interim apud me servaveram. Hanc ego pecuniam usque ad adventum D. GODELMANNI, quem huc brevi accessurum tibique in receptione pecuniae gratificaturum scribis, penes me retinebo. Ut autem in auream monetam commutem, haudquaquam fieri potest. Rarissime enim hîc vel minimum aureum cernimus, omni auro vel in Belgium vel in superiorem Germaniam, ubi maiori precio quam hîc censetur, hinc translato. Id tamen faciam, si potero, ut thaleros novos Belgicos, quorum multi summae huic intersunt, in alios Imperiales, uti vocant, commutem; hos enim gratiores istic esse arbitramur quam illos.</p><p>Porro quod ad continuationem Chronici D. CHYTRAeI p. m. qui postridie Iohannis nobis valedixit, attinet, neminem hoc quidem in loco eam ad rem magis idoneum esse iudico quam D. CHRISTOPHORUM STURTZIUM, qui historiarum lectioni in hac Academia magna cum laude praeest, ingenioque, memoria et facundia excellit. Quamprimum igitur Concilium Academiae convenerit, mentionem huius negocij et tuae commonefactionis inijciam, nec dubito, quin Domini Professores, quorum plerosque aetate, doctrina et prudentia graves esse non ignoras, id constituent, quod et Academiae nostrae et nomini magni illius CHYTRAeI honorificum et publice utile erit. Adveniente vero D. GODELMANNO, de eo, quod ad te privatim spectat, postremae Chronici parti inserendo, unâ cum D. CLINGIO, cum haeredibus D. CHYTRAeI libenter et diligenter agam, et si quae alia a me proficisci poterunt grata tibi officia, in illis promptum me semper reperies, donec vixero. Senesco enim iam, et post mensem, si Deus voluerit, annum 70 aetatis meae completurus sum. Facio tamen adhuc mediocriter vocationis meae in Ecclesia et Academia, per Dei gratiam, officia. M. CHRISTIERNUS paucis hîc diebus mansit, festinans in Daniam. Ego sane hunc virum nobiscum vivere et iuventutem nostram literariam in studijs Astronomicis, quae post obitum praestantissimi D. BRUCAeI segnius hîc tractata et culta sunt, erudire optarem. Tuas res honesto et incolumi adhuc statu esse, ac velle S. Caes. Mai. ut Pragae sibi adsis propius, gaudeo, et tibi tuisque deinceps etiam fausta feliciaque omnia precor.</p><p>Rumor isthuc delatus de dissidio inter Regem Daniae et nobilitatem suborto vanus est. Rex hoc tempore in Holsatia est cum coniuge, et genethliacis pompis ac convivio Gottorpij interfuturus dicitur, ubi soror eius IOHANNI ADOLPHO Holsatiae duci nupta filium peperit, quo et BUGISLAUS Pomeraniae dux legatum D. HENR. CAMERARIUM misit. Venationibus se Rex in Iutia oblectavit, magno cervorum numero caeso. Extruit passim munitiones Halmstadij et Overscherae in Sueciae finibus, quo cives ex vicinis oppidulis Rundeby et alijs migrare iussit, item Krempae in Holsatia. CAROLUS dux Sueciam clam nunc totam tenet pacifice, regiam vero coronam sibi a subditis oblatam nondum acceptare voluit, supplicio capitis 4 regni proceres captivos, ERICUM SPARRE Cancellarium, GUSTAVUM et STENONEM BANNER, et TURONEM BIELKEN, a concilio ordinum regni damnatos, affecit. Sunt et alij in Finlandia interfecti. Iam cum valido exercitu in Livonia est, omniaque Suecis hactenus subdita circa Revaliam cum hac ipsa urbe tenet, et Regi Poloniae bellum facturus existimatur, qui per Capitaneum suum GEORGIUM FARNSBECH exercitum circa Rigam conscripsit, qua de re certiora navibus nostris Riga redeuntibus cognoscemus. Cum Lubecensibus inducias pepigit, qui per legatos suos iam de pace cum eo agunt. Nuper duo Lubecensium Consules intra 24 horas obierunt, nec admodum bene inter Senatum et cives eius urbis convenit ob turbas Suecicas, quibus improvide Senatus inconsultis civibus se et urbem immiscuit, unde magna civibus in mari damna illata a CAROLO fuerunt.</p><p>In hisce regionibus, Dei beneficio, tranquillitas est, ac messem habuimus serenam et uberrimam. In Dania quoque segetes feliciter provenerunt, sed quia serior illic messis est, aër agricolis non adeo benignus fuit.</p><p>Postremo commendo tibi affinem meum ABRAHAMUM STOPIUM, qui istic aliquamdiu apud sororem et affinem vixit, eiusque negocium, quod haud dubie tibi exposuit, in quo si commodare ipsi apud Dn. Vicecancellarium vel alios proceres poteris, mihi quoque rem gratam feceris.</p><p>Bene et diu cum omnibus tuis vale.<br>Dabantur Rostochij 19 Septemb. stylo veteri Anno 1600.<br>Nobil. et Excel. T. studiosiss.<br>LUCAS BACMEISTERUS D.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most excellent in nobility of birth, learning, and virtue, Lord Tycho Brahe, hereditary Lord of Knudstrup, most excellent Astronomer, his Lord and friend especially to be honoured. At Prague.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most noble and renowned man, Lord and friend especially to be revered. I have received two letters from you this month of September: the earlier, <b>M. Christiernus [Longomontanus]</b> — who had tarried about three weeks at Wittenberg — delivered me on the 8th; the latter, a courier sent hither from Prague by <b>Lord Gödelmann</b>, yesterday. You know that <b>we use the Julian style here</b>. Both commended to me your money-business, about which you had also written to my kinsman, the Lord Chancellor <b>Dr. Bording</b>, whom — then acting at Lübeck — I admonished to promote that business in its place. He came a few days ago from Lübeck to <b>Güstrow</b>, summoned by the most illustrious <b>Prince Ulrich</b>, after His Highness — then hunting near <b>Grabow</b> — had been told of <b>the death of his brother's son, Duke Sigismund August</b>, who, long afflicted with dropsy and other diseases, <b>piously departed this life at Ivenack on 5 September</b>. His funeral will be on <b>10 October at Schwerin</b>, where rest the bodies of his father <b>Johann Albert</b>, his brother <b>Johann</b>, and others deceased of that line. On the same occasion the Lord Chancellor found at court the <b>Treasurer of the Younger Princes</b> (who is now also Duke Ulrich's Treasurer), and brought it about that today, through his minister, <b>he paid me at my house those 600 thalers owed to you, my receiving your receipt</b> [<i>apocha</i>], which I had kept by me meanwhile. This money I will retain with me until the arrival of <b>Lord Gödelmann</b>, who, you write, will come hither shortly and assist you in receiving the money. <b>To exchange it into gold coin cannot be done at all</b>: very rarely here do we see even the smallest gold piece, all gold being carried hence either into the Low Countries or into upper Germany, where it is rated at a higher price than here. Yet I will do this, if I can: <b>exchange the new Belgian thalers</b> (many of which are in this sum) <b>into others, \"Imperial\" as they call them</b>; for we judge these more welcome there than those.</p><p>Further, as to the <b>continuation of the Chronicle of Dr. Chytraeus</b> (of pious memory, who <b>bade us farewell the day after [the feast of St.] John</b> [= 25 June]): I judge no one in this place fitter for the task than <b>Dr. Christoph Sturtz</b>, who presides over the lecture in history at this Academy with great praise, and excels in talent, memory, and eloquence. As soon, then, as the Council of the Academy meets, I will bring forward mention of this business and of your reminder; nor do I doubt that the Lord Professors — most of whom, as you know, are weighty in age, learning, and prudence — will resolve what will be both honourable to our Academy and to the name of that great Chytraeus, and publicly useful. And when Lord Gödelmann arrives, <b>about that which privately concerns you, to be inserted in the last part of the Chronicle</b>, I will gladly and diligently deal — together with <b>Dr. Clingius</b> — with Chytraeus's heirs; and if any other services welcome to you can proceed from me, you will always find me ready in them, as long as I live. <b>For I am now growing old, and after a month, God willing, I shall complete the 70th year of my age</b>; yet I still discharge moderately, by God's grace, the duties of my calling in the Church and the Academy. M. Christiernus stayed here a few days, hastening into Denmark. I should indeed wish this man to live among us, and to instruct our literary youth in the <b>Astronomical studies — which, since the death of the most excellent Dr. Brucaeus, have been more sluggishly handled and cultivated here</b>. That your affairs are still in an honourable and safe state, and that the Sacred Imperial Majesty wishes you to be nearer to him at Prague, I rejoice; and I pray all things auspicious and happy for you and yours henceforth too.</p><p><b>The rumour carried thither, of a dissension arisen between the King of Denmark and his nobility, is empty.</b> The King is at this time in <b>Holstein</b> with his consort, and is said to be about to attend the birth-festivities and banquet at <b>Gottorp</b>, where his sister, married to <b>Johann Adolf, Duke of Holstein</b>, has borne a son — to which <b>Bogislaw, Duke of Pomerania</b>, sent as legate <b>Dr. Heinrich Camerarius</b>. The King has delighted himself with hunting in <b>Jutland</b>, a great number of stags being killed. He builds fortifications here and there — at <b>Halmstad</b> and <b>Överskär</b> on the Swedish borders (whither he has ordered the townsmen from the neighbouring little towns, Rundeby and others, to migrate), likewise at <b>Krempe</b> in Holstein. <b>Duke Charles</b> now secretly holds all <b>Sweden</b> peacefully, but has not yet been willing to accept the royal crown offered him by his subjects; he has <b>inflicted capital punishment on four captive nobles of the realm, condemned by the council of the estates: Erik Sparre the Chancellor, Gustav and Sten Banér, and Ture Bielke</b> [the Linköping Bloodbath]; and others have been killed in Finland. Now he is with a strong army in <b>Livonia</b>, and holds all that was hitherto subject to the Swedes around <b>Reval</b>, with that city itself, and is thought about to make war on the <b>King of Poland</b> — who, through his captain <b>Georg Farensbach</b>, has levied an army around <b>Riga</b>; whereof we shall learn more certainly from our ships returning from Riga. With the <b>Lübeckers</b> he has struck a truce; they now treat with him for peace through their legates. Lately two of the Lübeck consuls died within 24 hours; nor is it well agreed between the Senate and the citizens of that city, over the Swedish troubles, into which the Senate has imprudently entangled itself and the city without consulting the citizens — whence great damages at sea have been inflicted on the citizens by Charles.</p><p>In these regions, by God's benefit, there is tranquillity, and we have had a serene and most abundant harvest. In Denmark too the crops have come up happily, but since the harvest is later there, the weather has not been so kind to the farmers.</p><p>Lastly, I commend to you my kinsman <b>Abraham Stopius</b>, who lived there a while with his sister and kinsman, and his business (which he doubtless explained to you), in which, if you can accommodate him with the Lord Vice-Chancellor or other nobles, you will do me too a welcome thing.</p><p>Fare well, long, with all yours.<br>Given at Rostock, 19 September, Old Style, the year 1600.<br>Of Your Nobility and Excellency the most zealous,<br><b>Lucas Bacmeister, Doctor.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Lucas Bacmeister → Tycho.</b> ⚠ <b>Identity correction (flagged).</b> This letter says the writer \"will complete his <b>70th year</b> after a month\" → <b>born c. 1530</b> → this is <b>Lucas Bacmeister the Elder (1530–1608)</b>, the Rostock theologian and church superintendent — <b>not</b> the Younger (1570–1638) named in the apparatus of brahe-t_16010415001. The financial-intermediary \"Bacmeister\" of the corpus is the <b>Elder</b>; the identification at brahe-t_16010415001 should be revised.</li><li><b>Significance — a dense Baltic news-letter, with the Linköping Bloodbath.</b></li><li><b>The Mecklenburg money.</b> The <b>600 thalers</b> of interest paid (via Chancellor <b>Bording</b> and the <b>Treasurer of the Younger Princes</b>, on the occasion of <b>Duke Sigismund August of Mecklenburg's</b> death and funeral arrangements at Schwerin); Bacmeister holds the sum for <b>Gödelmann's</b> arrival, and — unable to get gold (all exported) — will swap the <b>new Belgian thalers into Imperial thalers</b>. (Tycho's side: brahe-t_16010415001.)</li><li><b>Chytraeus's <i>Chronicle</i>.</b> Confirms <b>David Chytraeus died 25 June 1600</b> (\"the day after St. John's\"; cf. brahe-t_16000808001). Bacmeister proposes <b>Christoph Sturtz</b> to continue Chytraeus's <i>Chronicle</i> and will handle, with <b>Dr. Clingius</b> and the heirs, the matter <b>\"that privately concerns you, to be inserted in the last part\"</b> — i.e. some notice of <b>Tycho</b> in the <i>Chronicle</i>.</li><li><b>Rostock astronomy.</b> Wishes <b>Longomontanus</b> would stay to teach — astronomy at Rostock having languished <b>since Heinrich Brucaeus's death</b> (Tycho's old Rostock connection).</li><li><b>The news bulletin.</b> <i>(a)</i> The <b>Danish king–nobility \"dissension\" rumour is false</b>; <b>Christian IV</b> is in Holstein, attending the birth at <b>Gottorp</b> (his sister <b>Augusta</b>, wife of <b>Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp</b>, bore a son; <b>Bogislaw of Pomerania</b> sent <b>Dr. Heinrich Camerarius</b>); the King's Jutland hunting and his border fortifications (<b>Halmstad, Överskär, Krempe</b>). <i>(b)</i> <b>The Linköping Bloodbath (March 1600):</b> <b>Duke Charles</b> (future Charles IX) holds Sweden, has not yet taken the crown, and has <b>executed the four nobles Erik Sparre, Gustav Banér, Sten Banér, and Ture Bielke</b>; the <b>Polish–Swedish war in Livonia</b> (Charles around <b>Reval</b>; <b>Georg Farensbach</b> levying for Poland at <b>Riga</b>); the <b>truce with Lübeck</b> and the Senate-vs-citizens rift there.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Lucas Bacmeister (the <b>Elder</b>); <b>Longomontanus</b> (<i>M. Christiernus</i>); <b>Gödelmann</b>; Chancellor <b>Bording</b>; Duke <b>Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b>; <b>Duke Sigismund August of Mecklenburg</b> (d. 1600) and his father <b>Johann Albert I</b> and brother <b>Johann</b>; the <b>Treasurer of the Younger Princes</b>; <b>Christoph Sturtz</b>; <b>David Chytraeus</b>; <b>Dr. Clingius</b>; <b>Heinrich Brucaeus</b>; <b>Christian IV</b>; <b>Augusta of Denmark</b> &amp; <b>Johann Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp</b>; <b>Bogislaw [XIII] of Pomerania</b>; <b>Heinrich Camerarius</b>; <b>Duke Charles of Sweden</b>; <b>Erik Sparre, Gustav Banér, Sten Banér, Ture Bielke</b>; <b>Georg Farensbach</b>; <b>Abraham Stopius</b>; <b>Coraduz</b> (the Vice-Chancellor, unnamed). Places: Rostock, Prague, Wittenberg, Güstrow, Grabow, Ivenack, Schwerin, Holstein, Gottorp, Jutland, Halmstad, Överskär, Rundeby, Krempe, Sweden, Finland, Livonia, Reval, Riga, Lübeck, Denmark.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>19 Septemb. <b>stylo veteri</b> Anno 1600</i> → <b>19 September 1600 [Old Style]</b> (Bacmeister explicitly notes \"we use the Julian style here\"); header and label agree. <i>(Sigismund August's death-day is left blank in the source — \"[—] September\" — flagged.)</i></li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the day-number dropped from the source Latin: Dreyer (TBOO VIII p.377, l.20-21) gives 'die 5 Septemb.' for the death of Duke Sigismund August's nephew at Ivenack; the English placeholder '[—] September' is now '5 September'.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected 'Regem Denmark' to 'Regem Daniae' in the Latin per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.378, l.21 (no change to the English, already unambiguous).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the ungrammatical 'tres septimanae' to the accusative-of-duration 'tres septimanas' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.377, l.11-12 (no change to the English, already correct).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the money payment and currency problem; the Chytraeus-<i>Chronicle</i> continuation with Sturtz and the Tycho-notice; the Rostock-astronomy wish; and the whole Baltic bulletin — the false Danish-dissension rumour, the Gottorp birth, the <b>Linköping Bloodbath with its four named victims</b>, the Livonian war, the Lübeck truce) is firmly construed and historically corroborated. Two flags: the <b>Bacmeister-the-Elder identity correction</b> (affecting brahe-t_16010415001), and the <b>blank death-day</b> of Sigismund August in the source.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter, opening address through signature, against TBOO VIII pp.377-379 page images. Every proper name, sum, and place-name in the dense political/dynastic report checks out and is translated faithfully: the 600 thalers, the 24-hour deaths of the two Lübeck consuls, Bacmeister's 70th year, and the string of place-names (Güstrow, Grabow, Schwerin, Gottorp, Jutland, Halmstad, Överskär, Krempe, Livonia, Reval, Riga) and persons (Bording, Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Johann Adolf of Holstein, Bogislaw of Pomerania, Heinrich Camerarius, Sturtz, Clingius, Brucaeus, the four executed Swedish nobles Sparre/Banér×2/Bielke, Georg Farensbach, Abraham Stopius) all match Dreyer. Three TEXT_ERROR deviations were found in our Latin source layer against Dreyer (a dropped day-number, a vernacular word substituted for the Latin, a grammatical-case corruption); none originates in the translator's English, but the dropped date is a real, citable fact lost from the source layer, so this is graded down despite the translation itself being sound.</p><p><b>3 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Our source_text drops the day-number '5' before 'Septemb.'; Dreyer gives the death of Duke Sigismund August's nephew as 5 September. The translator handled the gap responsibly with a bracketed placeholder rather than inventing a date, but the source layer itself is corrupted and the recoverable fact (the day) is missing from it. Dreyer prints: <i>qui hydrope alijfque morbis diu conflictatus die 5 Septemb. Ivenaci pie deceßit ex hac vita. (TBOO VIII p.377, l.20-21)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text substitutes the vernacular word 'Denmark' where Dreyer prints the Latin genitive 'Daniae'. The English translation is unaffected (the referent is unambiguous either way), but the Latin source text has been corrupted. Dreyer prints: <i>Rumor ifthuc delatus de dißidio inter Regem Daniae et nobilitatem fuborto vanus eft. (TBOO VIII p.378, l.21)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has the ungrammatical nominative/genitive 'tres septimanae' where Dreyer prints the correct accusative-of-duration 'tres septimanas'. No effect on the (correctly rendered) English. Dreyer prints: <i>qui ad tres feptimanas Witebergae fubftiterat (TBOO VIII p.377, l.11-12)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated all three defects directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII pp.376-379): p.377 confirms both 'tres septimanas' (l.11-12) and 'die 5 Septemb.' (l.20-21), and p.378 confirms 'Regem Daniae' (l.21). All three are genuine Latin transcription corruptions in our source text, now corrected; only the missing day-number had any bearing on the English, which has been restored accordingly. No remaining crux.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Lucas Bacmeister", "count": 4, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16000916002", "next": "brahe-t_16010311001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000916002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010415001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges two letters about Tycho's money owed, reports advising Bording to act on the matter, and mentions the death of Duke Sigismund August of Mecklenburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000919002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000920001", "d": "1600-09-20", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Friedrich Taubmann", "ck": "Friedrich Taubmann", "ck2": null, "g": "C", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Friedrich Taubmann — Prague, 20 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (verse and prose) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD TAUBMANNUM.<br>Num, TAUBMANE, tibi vox rauca in faucibus haeret?<br>Piscibus et stupidis mutior esne feris?<br>Forte Poëtarum quia mos est fingere quidvis,<br>Nos etiam vanis ludis imaginibus.<br>At praestare fidem vanos quoque Iura Poëtas,<br>Et rata pollicitis debita inesse iubent.<br>Solve igitur, quae iure mihi tu Carmina debes,<br>Falsaque (cum nequeas vera referre) refer.</p><p>EN habes hîc antesignani vestri tuique plasmatoris MELISSI (qui tamen raucos et mutos Poëtas creavit nunquam) Carmina nuper ad me missa, ubi nonnunquam dissimilis a seipso videtur. Adest etiam Doctoris cuiusdam Ingolstadiensis Heroicum, quod per Cancellarium Bavaricum, Dominum IOHANNEM GEORGIUM HERWARTUM ab HOHENBURG (cum quo mihi creberrima, ob studia, quibus impense afficitur, Mathematica, intercedit per literas familiaritas) hesterna vespera Monachio accepi: Tu lege, dispice, cognosce, et loquela recuperata, alio genere supple; ni mature parueris, Iambos in te vibrabo, et ex muto sordoque caecum, imo truncum te reddam, aut in saxum convertam, tanquam alter PERSEUS capite Gorgonio te petens. Sed prius Caducaei usum tibi adimam, et per MELISSUM tuum degradari faciam, alias enim nefas foret, sacros vates castigare et merita poena afficere.</p><p>Tuque vale, et tenerum cum matre valere columbum<br>Effice, sic vobis pullulet alma domus,<br>TYCHONISque memor, vates qui suspicit, esto,<br>Sique voles, carmen non breve mitte brevi.<br>Deproperabam Pragâ die 20 Septembr. stylo reformato Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Taubmann.</b></p><p><i>Is your voice, Taubmann, stuck hoarse in your throat?</i><br><i>Are you more mute than fishes and dull beasts?</i><br><i>Perhaps, because the poets' way is to feign whatever they please,</i><br><i>you make sport of us, too, with empty images.</i><br><i>But the Laws bid that even vain poets keep faith,</i><br><i>and that due, ratified obligations inhere in promises.</i><br><i>Pay up, then, the Poems which you owe me by right,</i><br><i>and render falsehoods (since you cannot render truths).</i></p><p>Behold, here you have the Poems — lately sent to me — of your party's standard-bearer and your own moulder, Melissus (who, however, never created hoarse and mute Poets), in which now and again he seems unlike himself. There is also at hand the Heroic [poem] of a certain Doctor of Ingolstadt, which I received yesterday evening from Munich, through the Bavarian Chancellor, Master Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg (with whom I have a most frequent familiarity by letters, on account of the Mathematical studies to which he is keenly devoted). You — read, examine, take note; and, your speech recovered, make good [your debt] with another piece. Unless you obey in good time, I will brandish Iambics against you, and from mute and deaf will render you blind — nay, maimed — or turn you into stone, like a second Perseus attacking you with the Gorgon's head. But first I will take from you the use of the Caduceus, and have you degraded through your own Melissus; for otherwise it would be impious to chastise sacred bards and visit them with deserved punishment.</p><p><i>And fare you well; and make the tender dove fare well, with its mother;</i><br><i>so may a nurturing house teem [with young] for you;</i><br><i>and be mindful of Tycho, who looks up to the bards;</i><br><i>and, if you will, send before long a poem — not a short one.</i><br>I hurried [this] off from Prague, on the 20th day of September, reformed style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b> (1565–1613), professor of poetry at <b>Wittenberg</b>, the celebrated humanist wit and Latin poet, protégé of Melissus. A <b>playful verse-and-prose letter</b> mock-scolding Taubmann for failing to send a promised poem, in the bantering register of the late-humanist <i>poetae</i>; the threats (Perseus, the Gorgon, the Caduceus, \"Iambics\") are jocular.</li><li><b>Persons.</b> <i>antesignanus vester … plasmator Melissus</i> = <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b> (1539–1602), the doyen (\"standard-bearer\" and \"moulder\") of the German neo-Latin poets, whose own <i>Carmina</i> on Tycho's affairs Tycho encloses (cf. brahe-t_16001116003). <i>Doctor Ingolstadiensis</i> = an unnamed Ingolstadt doctor whose <i>Heroicum</i> (heroic-verse poem) Herwart had just forwarded. <i>Cancellarius Bavaricus … Herwart ab Hohenburg</i> = <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (the Munich correspondent of the whole 1600 series).</li><li><b>Mythological play.</b> <i>Iambos vibrabo</i> — \"I'll brandish iambics\" (the invective metre of Archilochus). <i>alter Perseus capite Gorgonio</i> — Tycho jokingly threatens to turn Taubmann to stone with the Gorgon's (Medusa's) head. <i>Caducei usum tibi adimam … per Melissum tuum degradari faciam</i> — \"I'll strip you of the Caduceus [Mercury's herald-staff, the poet's emblem] and have you degraded through your Melissus\" — i.e. drummed out of the poets' guild; <i>sacros vates</i> (\"sacred bards\") is the conventional dignity of poets.</li><li><b>Closing verse.</b> <i>tenerum cum matre columbum</i> — \"the tender dove with its mother\": an affectionate domestic figure (Taubmann's wife and child / household); <i>vobis pullulet alma domus</i> — a wish for a fruitful household. The whole sealed by the demand for \"a poem — not a short one.\"</li><li><b>⚠ Calendar / dateline.</b> The printed dateline <i>Pragâ die Septembr. stylo reformato Anno 1600</i> gives <b>no day-number</b> (\"on the … day of September\"); the <b>day 20</b> is supplied from the dataset (slug …0920… / label \"20. september n.s.\"). <i>stylo reformato</i> = \"reformed style\" = <b>New Style</b> (Prague, imperial). Header dates <b>20 September 1600 [N.S.]</b>, the day-number flagged as editorially supplied.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02):</b> the day \"20\" is <b>not</b> merely editorial — Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII, p. 365; running-head 10/20 Sept.) prints <i>die 20 Septembr.</i> Restored to the Source dateline; the \"supplied from the dataset\" qualification is withdrawn.</li><li><b>Verification note (2026-07-12).</b> Re-examined the 'sordo'/'surdo' TEXT_ERROR flag; confirmed by direct inspection that the required Dreyer page (TBOO VIII, on or after p.364) is absent from the supplied slice, which ends at p.363. Left unchanged; the defect is rejected as unverifiable against the edition of record.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin verse and prose are rendered faithfully (verse kept as verse, per §4), and the literary game (the Melissus and Ingolstadt poems, Herwart as conduit, the mock-mythological threats) is clear and corpus-consistent. Grade [B] rather than [A] because (1) the dateline lacks its day-number, supplied as <b>20</b> from the catalogue and flagged, and (2) a witty poetic letter inevitably leaves fine shades of tone (e.g. <i>plasmator</i>, \"moulder/creator\") open to alternative renderings; no substantive crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], confirmed.</b> <b>Not collated against Dreyer</b> — no page reference for this letter, so the grade is capped at [B] however sound the translation looks. PIPELINE DEFECT: tboo.printed_pages states TBOO VIII pp.334-366 (33 pages) but the slice_pdf physically contains only 30 pages. Read all 30 pages (TBOO 334-363) and confirmed the correspondence progresses chronologically day-by-day from 24 July through 16 September 1600 (Corraducius/Rosenkrantz, Longomontanus, Oswaldus, Braschius, Herwart x2, Sturcius, Bacmeisterus, Ioestelium) with no letter to Taubmann anywhere in that span; the 20 September 1600 Taubmann letter must fall on the missing pp.364-366. Could not collate against Dreyer as a result. Audited published_english against source_text alone instead: found no fabrication or omission in the poem or prose paragraph (names Melissus, Herwart, Perseus, and the date all check out internally), but flag the likely 'sordo'/'surdo' source-text corruption above. Recommend the slice be regenerated with the correct page range so this letter can be properly collated.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text reads 'sordo' (from sordus, not a standard Latin word / closest sense 'filthy'), but the translation renders it as 'deaf', which matches 'surdo' (deaf) - the far more sensible companion to 'muto' (mute) in a mute-deaf-blind-maimed escalation. This looks like a one-letter corruption in source_text ('sordo' for 'surdo'), silently corrected by the translator rather than flagged. Cannot be confirmed against Dreyer because the relevant page is missing from the provided slice. Dreyer prints: <i>UNVERIFIABLE - required Dreyer page absent from slice</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> The supplied slice (30 pages, nominally TBOO VIII pp.334-366) in fact runs only to p.363 — I read pp.334-337, 350-353, and 360-363 directly and confirmed the letter dated 20 September 1600 ('Tycho Brahe ad Taubmannum') lies beyond the end of the range actually provided. Because the disputed word cannot be checked against Dreyer, and per protocol a TEXT_ERROR must be confirmed against the edition of record before the Latin is changed, the defect is rejected rather than fixed on internal inference alone. A real crux survives: whether 'sordo' is a corruption of 'surdo' remains textually plausible (the mutus-surdus-caecus-truncus escalation, and the translation's own pre-existing 'deaf') but is not confirmed against the printed page.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Chides Taubmann in verse for silence and owed poems, encloses verses by Melissus and a heroic poem from an Ingolstadt doctor received via Herwart von Hohenburg.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000920001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000920002", "d": "1600-09-20", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Martin Brasch → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 20 September 1600 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ILLUSTRI ET MAGNIFICO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, ASTRONOMO FACILE NOSTRI TEMPORIS PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET PATRONO COLENDO.<br>PRAGAM IN BEHMEN.</p><p>ILLUSTRIS et magnifice Domine, patrone colende. Ut doleo priores meas tibi non redditas esse, ita laetor posteriores te accepisse: quibus quod prolixe et humanissime respondes, pergratum mihi est. De Elegia mea statues, utcunque libet, nihil me offendet, neque a me edetur, antequam tu volueris. De MELISSI carminibus plane tibi assentior. Alius quodammodo et quasi vulgaris videtur in istis carminibus: vel senectus hoc facit (ut ipse solet interdum queri) vel laboris fuga: certe isti odae omnis lima et intentio videtur deesse. Sed haec tibi in aurem dixerim, apud ipsum nolim proferri, qui me nuper etiam Elogio ornavit in carmine de obitu CHYTRAeI, quod mitto. Diligentissime egi cum CHYTRAeI filijs de Designatione ista tuae migrationis in Germaniam. Dixerunt se legisse et meminisse, quod patri reddita esset: sed quamvis accurate inquisita, reperta nondum est. Deinde etsi reperiatur, nobis tamen non liquet, quomodo ea possit in huius anni Chronicon regeri ad finem, ut vis, cum incurrat reipsa in praecedentem. Praeterea Chronicon illud iam editum est post mortem CHYTRAeI in id tempus, quousque ipse deduxit: nam ut quispiam Venerem imperfectam perficiat, huius anni extrema absolvat, fortasse nemo reperietur. Exemplum tibi mitto. Sed in tui gratiam facile recudi posset, nisi priora obstarent: et hoc quoque, quod iam haec Exemplaria in hominum manibus versentur. Nihil sane est, quod malim, quam tibi gratificari, sed tu ipse de his cogitabis et statues, et si quid in mentem venerit, nos tantum docebis. Locutus sum de hoc negocio etiam cum D. LUCA, qui suam sententiam sine dubio ipse perscribet.</p><p>De carmine ad Caesaream Maiestatem tali, quale innuis, quid fieri possit, dispiciam, quando otium nactus fuero. Modo versor in campo Eloquentiae et Intemperantiam ac sectatores eius vehementi oratione seu libro castigo. Erasmianum illud erit quiddam, ut spero. Sed haec ante dicens, in lente unguentum quaero.</p><p>Obijt nuper SIGISMUNDUS Megapolitaniae princeps, IOHANNIS ante defuncti (cuius duo filij supersunt) frater: corpus eius humabitur Suerini 10 Octobris stylo veteri. CAROLUS dux Sueciae cum magno exercitu et tota familia contulit se in Livoniae partes; quid moliturus et quo res evasura sit, videbimus. Transylvaniam periclitari audimus, et propter adventum Turcae et tyrannidem Walachi, de quibus ex illis locis nos incertiora audimus, vos, qui prope adestis, certa.</p><p>Irascor illi, qui effecit, ut suppressis literis meis VARADINUM nostrum nondum legeris. Habe vero iam etiam sero: quod tibi probatum iri omnino confido, magno equidem mihi labore constitit. Elegantius videbitur, si conferetur cum inepto quodam Silesij Carmine, qui idem argumentum susceperat ante, sed sine Minerva et Gratijs, adeoque nimis pueriliter. RHEDERUM audio id misisse ad Typographiam Lignicensem, ut recudatur.</p><p>M. CHRISTIANUS iam discessit in Daniam, cum ante mihi adfuisset. Tu ut diutissime valeas et in Germania nostra floreas, utque tibi diu etiam incolumis sit ille Caesar optimus et clementissimus: illud vero est, quod opto et voveo summopere.</p><p>Bene vale, vir magnifice.<br>Datae Rostochij 20 Septemb. Anno 1600.<br>T. Magnif.<br>reverenter colens<br>MARTINUS BRASCHIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the illustrious and magnificent man, Lord Tycho Brahe, easily the prince of the astronomers of our time, Lord and patron to be revered. [To] Prague in Bohemia.</b></p><p>Illustrious and magnificent Lord, patron to be revered. As I grieve that my earlier letters were not delivered to you, so I rejoice that you received the later ones; and that you answer them at length and most courteously is most pleasing to me. Concerning my Elegy, you will decide however you please: nothing will offend me, nor will it be published by me before you wish it. Concerning <b>Melissus's poems</b> I plainly agree with you. He seems somehow different, and as it were commonplace, in those poems — either old age does this (as he himself is wont sometimes to complain) or the avoidance of labour; certainly that ode seems to lack all polish and concentration. But these things I would say in your ear; I should not wish them brought out to him, who lately even adorned me with a eulogy in his poem on the death of <b>Chytraeus</b>, which I send. I have dealt most diligently with Chytraeus's sons about that account of your migration into Germany. They said they had read it, and remembered that it had been delivered to their father; but, though carefully sought, it has not yet been found. And then, even if it be found, it is not clear to us how it could be inserted into this year's Chronicle at the end, as you wish, since it really falls into the preceding [year]. Besides, that Chronicle has already been published, after Chytraeus's death, up to the time to which he himself carried it down; for, that someone should complete an unfinished Venus, and finish off the last parts of this year — perhaps no one will be found. I send you a copy. But for your sake it could easily be reprinted, did not the earlier [matters] stand in the way — and this too, that these copies are already in men's hands. There is indeed nothing I would rather do than gratify you; but you yourself will think and decide about these things, and if anything come to mind, you have only to instruct us. I have spoken about this business also with Lord <b>Lucas</b> [Backmeister], who will doubtless write out his own opinion himself.</p><p>Concerning a poem to the Imperial Majesty, such as you hint at, I will consider what can be done, when I have got leisure. Just now I am engaged in the field of Eloquence, and chastise Intemperance and its followers with a vehement oration, or book; it will be something Erasmian, I hope. But, saying these things beforehand, I seek ointment among lentils [I lavish pains on a trifle].</p><p>Recently died <b>Sigismund, prince of Mecklenburg</b>, brother of the previously deceased <b>Johann</b> (whose two sons survive); his body will be buried at Schwerin on 10 October, Old Style. <b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b>, with a great army and his whole household, has betaken himself into the parts of <b>Livonia</b>; what he intends, and how the matter will turn out, we shall see. We hear that <b>Transylvania</b> is in peril, both from the coming of the Turk and the tyranny of the Wallachian — about which we hear more uncertain [reports] from those places, [while] you, who are near, [hear] sure ones.</p><p>I am angry at the one who brought it about that — my letters being suppressed — you have not yet read our <b><i>Varadinum</b></i>. But have it now, even if late; which I am wholly confident will win your approval, for it cost me indeed great labour. It will seem the more elegant if compared with a certain inept Poem by a Silesian, who had taken up the same subject before, but without Minerva and the Graces, and so too childishly. I hear that <b>Rhederus</b> has sent it to the Liegnitz printing-house, to be reprinted.</p><p><b>Master Christen</b> [Longomontanus] has now departed into Denmark, having before been with me. That you may fare most long, and flourish in our Germany, and that that best and most clement Emperor may long be safe for you — that, indeed, is what I wish and vow most greatly.</p><p>Fare well, magnificent man.<br>Given at Rostock, 20 September, the year 1600.<br>Reverently revering Your Magnificence,<br>Martin Brasch.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Significance — Brasch's reply to Tycho's literary-critique letter.</b> This is <b>Martin Brasch's direct answer to Tycho's letter of 29 March 1600 (brahe-t_16000329004)</b>, responding point by point. Brasch (1565–1601), the Rostock humanist and <i>Poeta laureatus</i>, opens by noting that his <i>earlier</i> letters miscarried but the <i>later</i> reached Tycho, who answered \"at length and most courteously.\"<br>- <b>Point-by-point with letter 106.</b><br>  1. <b>The Elegy.</b> Brasch accepts Tycho's verdict entirely: \"you will decide however you please … nor will it be published by me before you wish\" — yielding the suppress-pending-revision control Tycho had asked for.<br>  2. <b>Melissus's verses.</b> Brasch \"plainly agrees\" with Tycho's low estimate of <b>Paul Melissus's</b> recent poems — \"commonplace … either old age or the avoidance of labour … that ode lacks all polish and concentration\" — but begs discretion (<i>in aurem</i>, \"in your ear\"), since Melissus had just honoured <i>him</i> with a eulogy in a poem on <b>David Chytraeus's death</b> (enclosed).<br>  3. <b>The Chytraeus migration-narrative.</b> Brasch reports on the hunt for Tycho's <i>Designatio</i> of his <b>migration into Germany</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010415002, where Sturtz pursues the same): Chytraeus's <b>sons</b> recall it was delivered to their father but cannot find it; and even if found, it cannot be fitted into \"this year's Chronicle\" (it belongs to the previous year), which is <b>already published</b> to the point Chytraeus reached before dying — \"for, that someone should complete an <b>unfinished Venus</b> [the topos of Apelles' Venus, left unfinished at his death and which none dared complete], perhaps no one will be found.\" He has consulted <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Superintendent of Rostock; cf. brahe-t_16001115002), who will write separately.<br>  4. <b>The imperial poem.</b> Brasch will consider the <i>carmen ad Caesaream Maiestatem</i> Tycho had hinted at, when free; he is now writing an \"Erasmian\" oration against Intemperance.<br>- <b>News (autumn 1600) — cross-links.</b><br>  - <b>Death of Sigismund of Mecklenburg</b>, \"brother of the previously deceased <b>Johann</b> (whose <b>two sons survive</b>)\" — to be buried at <b>Schwerin, 10 October O.S.</b> This identifies the <b>\"younger princes of Mecklenburg\"</b> of Tycho's 10,000-thaler loan (brahe-t_16000331001, brahe-t_16001115002 etc.) as <b>Duke Johann's two surviving sons</b>.<br>  - <b>Charles of Sweden</b> (later Charles IX) gone into <b>Livonia</b> with an army — the Polish-Swedish war (cf. Henniochus, brahe-t_16010321001).<br>  - <b>Transylvania imperilled</b> by \"the Turk and the tyranny of the Wallachian\" [Michael the Brave] — the Long War (cf. Sturtz, brahe-t_16010415002); Brasch notes Tycho, being \"near,\" has the surer reports.<br>- <b>Brasch's own work.</b> His poem <b><i>Varadinum</b></i> (on <b>Várad / Oradea</b>, the Transylvanian fortress), at last sent; he commends it against \"a certain inept Silesian poem … without Minerva and the Graces\" on the same subject, which <i>Rhederus</i> has sent to the <b>Liegnitz (Legnica) press</b> to be reprinted.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Martin Brasch (sender); <b>Paul Melissus</b>; <b>David Chytraeus</b> (and his sons); <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>; <b>Sigismund</b> and <b>Johann</b> of Mecklenburg; <b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b>; the <b>Wallachian</b> (Michael the Brave); <i>Rhederus</i>; <b><i>M. Christianus</i> = Christen Sørensen Longomontanus</b>, who \"has now departed into Denmark\" (cf. brahe-t_15981231001, where Tycho urged him to come — he had been with Brasch en route); <b>Rudolf II</b> (\"that best and most clement Emperor\").<br>- <b>Allusions / proverbs (kept and glossed).</b> <i>Venerem imperfectam perficiat</i> — the unfinished Venus of <b>Apelles</b> (none dared complete it); <i>in lente unguentum quaero</i> — \"ointment among lentils,\" the classical figure for misplaced/lavished effort; <i>sine Minerva et Gratijs</i> — \"without Minerva and the Graces,\" i.e. without skill or charm.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Rostochij 20 Septemb. Anno 1600</i>; Lutheran Rostock kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar — confirmed by Brasch's own <i>\"10 Octobris stylo veteri\"</i> for the burial. Header marked [O.S.].<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin address heading corrected from 'PRAGAMIN BEHMEN.' to 'PRAGAM IN BEHMEN.' per Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.379; no change to the English, which already read correctly.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent humanist Latin, read securely; the reply tracks letter 106 point for point (the Elegy yielded to Tycho's judgement; agreement on Melissus's weak verses; the failed search for the Chytraeus migration-narrative and the Chronicle problem; the imperial poem), the autumn-1600 news dovetails with the corpus (the Mecklenburg succession identifying the \"younger princes,\" the Livonian war, the Transylvanian peril), and the learned allusions are identified. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO VIII page images: p.378 (context, end of preceding Bacmeister letter), p.379 (letter heading + opening through 'fortasse nemo reperietur'), p.380 (body through closing, signature 'MARTINUS BRASCHIUS.'), and p.381 (confirmed this page belongs to the following letter, Tycho to Jostel, not ours — no spillover). Checked every proper name, number, date and place-name specified in the brief: MELISSI, CHYTRAeI (x2), D. LUCA (matches the immediately preceding letter in Dreyer, signed 'LUCAS BACMEISTERUS D.', so the bracketed [Backmeister] gloss is well-founded, not fabricated), SIGISMUNDUS Megapolitaniae princeps / IOHANNIS (predeceased brother, two surviving sons), Suerini 10 Octobris stylo veteri, CAROLUS dux Sueciae, Livoniae, Transylvaniam, Turcae, Walachi, VARADINUM, Silesij, RHEDERUM, Typographiam Lignicensem, and the closing date/place Rostochij 20 Septemb. 1600 -- all render exactly as Dreyer prints them, with no fabricated, omitted, or mistranslated content. Specifically checked the flagged name: Dreyer prints 'M. CHRISTIANUS' (not CHRISTIERNUS) at p.380 line 26, matching source_text exactly -- no TEXT_ERROR there. The English's bracketed '[Longomontanus]' identification is a reasonable, clearly-marked editorial gloss (M. Christianus/Christen departing for Denmark via Rostock fits Longomontanus's known movements that week) and is not flagged. One low-severity TEXT_ERROR found: the address heading 'PRAGAM IN BEHMEN' (two words in Dreyer) appears glued as 'PRAGAMIN BEHMEN' in our source_text; this does not affect the (correct) English translation. No fabricated day-numbers, phrases, closings; no dropped sentences; no flipped pronouns; no custody misattribution. Per the brief's grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of severity (the qualifying clause 'that changes what a scholar would take away' governs only OMISSION/MISTRANSLATION, not FABRICATION or TEXT_ERROR); hence verdict MINOR / grade C, consistent with the sibling letter brahe-t_15760823001 which surfaced the same category of low-severity source-transcription slip.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text glues \"PRAGAM\" and \"IN\" into one word \"PRAGAMIN\"; Dreyer prints them as two separate words. Trivial transcription slip with no effect on meaning or on the (correct) English rendering. Dreyer prints: <i>PRAGAM IN BEHMEN. (p.379, two separate words in the address heading beneath the salutation)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against the Dreyer page images for this letter (TBOO VIII pp.378-381, all 4 pages read). P.379 clearly prints 'PRAGAM IN BEHMEN.' as two separate words beneath the salutation heading. No other cruces found on the four pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000915001", "next": "brahe-t_16001116003", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000915001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16001116003"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Agrees that Melissus's recent poems lack their usual polish, defers to Tycho on publication of the Elegy, and reports efforts with Chytraeus's sons about Tycho's chronicle entry.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000920002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000923001", "d": "1600-09-23", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 23 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with a German delivery-note) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23, fol. 242r</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO DOCTISSIMOQUE VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, DOMINO A KNUDSTRUP ET URANIBURGO, MATHEMATICORUM OMNIUM FACILE PRINCIPI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO SINGULARI OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.<br>PRAGAe.<br>IN ABWESEN HERRN ALEXANDER PRENDTL FÜRSTL. BAYER. AGENTEN ABZUEGEBEN.</p><p>MITTO, Vir Nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile princeps, his iunctos libros ex ijs, quos desideras, APOLLONIJ Pergaei Opera No. 73 et Tabulas BLANCHINI, PRUGNERI et PURBACHIJ No. 34. Tibique foelicissima quaeque ex animo precor et opto.</p><p>Datae Monachij die 23 Sept. A. 1600.<br>D. T. Nobilmae<br>Addictissimus<br>IOAN. GEORG. HERWART AB HOHENBURGK.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most learned man, Master Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, easily the prince of all mathematicians, his lord and friend to be honoured with singular respect.</b><br><b>At Prague.</b><br><b>In the absence of Herr Alexander Prendtl, the Bavarian ducal agent, to be delivered.</b></p><p>I send, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians, joined herewith, these books out of those that you desire: the <i>Works</i> of Apollonius of Perga, No. 73, and the <i>Tables</i> of Blanchinus, Prugner, and Peurbach, No. 34. And I pray and wish you from my heart every happiness.</p><p>Given at Munich, the 23rd day of September, in the year 1600.<br>Most devoted to Your most noble [self],<br>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), Chancellor of Bavaria at Munich, jurist and learned polymath who corresponded with both Tycho and Kepler; here acting as Tycho's book-procurer at the Frankfurt fairs and the Munich/Augsburg market.</li><li><b>Delivery.</b> The German address-line directs that the packet be left, in his absence, with <b>Alexander Prandtl</b> (<i>Prendtl</i>), the <b>Bavarian ducal agent</b> (<i>fürstlich-bayerischer Agent</i>) at Prague — the standard conduit for Herwart's consignments to Tycho. (The Prague Bavarian agency is the link confirmed in the Tycho-thesaurus Prague layer.)</li><li><b>The \"No.\" numbers</b> (No. 73, No. 34) are items on <b>Tycho's own book-desiderata list</b>, which Herwart was working through; they recur across this Munich series (see brahe-t_16000403001, …0418001, …0426002, …1127001).</li><li><b>Books.</b> <i>Apollonij Pergaei Opera</i> = the <i>Works</i> (chiefly the <i>Conics</i>) of <b>Apollonius of Perga</b>. <i>Tabulae Blanchini, Prugneri et Purbachij</i> = the astronomical tables of <b>Giovanni Bianchini</b> (<i>Blanchinus</i>), <b>Nicolaus Prugner</b>, and <b>Georg Peurbach</b> (<i>Purbachius</i>) — the same trio Tycho asks after again in November (brahe-t_16001127001).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>; <i>die 23 Sept. 1600</i>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a brief, transparent covering note; sense and all identifications secure.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.365-367 (page images). The Herwart letter, including its full superscription and the 'In Abwesen Herrn Alexander Prendtl...' delivery instruction, is printed complete on p.366, matching source_text word-for-word. Checked both catalogue numbers (No. 73, No. 34) and all named works/persons (Apollonius Pergaeus, Blanchinus, Prugnerus, Purbachius, Alexander Prendtl) against the translation — all correctly rendered. Closing date (23 Sept. 1600) matches metadata.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23, fol. 242r."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16000911001", "next": "brahe-t_16001108001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends two books Tycho requested: the Works of Apollonius of Perga and the astronomical tables of Blanchinus, Prugner, and Peurbach.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000923001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000926001", "d": "1600-09-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "The dean of the chapter at the cathedral of Magdeburg", "ck": "The dean of the chapter at the cathedral of Magdeburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → The Dean of the Chapter of Magdeburg Cathedral — Prague, 26 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, fol. 144 (Kladde — draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD DECANUM CAPITULI MAGDEBURGENSIS].</p><p>REUERENDISSIME et nobilissime domine decane. Do:ie honorandissime, advenerunt res meae, quae Magdeburgi repositae erant, hisce praeteritis diebus Leutmeritzium quousque Albis versus Bohemiam est nauigabilis. Quod autem in gratiam sac:ae caes:ae maiestatis, domini nostri clementissimi, Dominatio Vestra vna cum caeteris reuerendissimi capituli Magdeburgensis collegis eas tam prompte et liberaliter proprijs sumtibus huc procurare non degrauati estis, id ipsius maiestas clementissimo animo acceptissimum habuit, prout ab ipsius maiestate, cui hoc vestrum obsequentissimum officium ante paucos dies, cum in horto prope arcem illi subiectissime adessem, commendabam, non saltem ipsemet cognoui sed et heri à magf:co d:no Baruitio, supremo secretario et ipsius ma:ti a consilijs intimo, percepi, qui etiam hac de re se ad vestri reuerendissimi capituli vicecancellarium Chilianum Stösserum scripsisse mihi insinuauit. Ego vero pro mea persona hanc vestram benignissimam voluntatem, quod meis desiderijs tam libere et libenter annueritis, et supellectilem, qua diu non sine incommodo destitutus sum, ad me direxeritis, gratias vestris impensis et habeo et debeo immortales; cumque ad compensandum imparem me agnoscam, id saltem polliceri possum me perpetuo grata mente hoc singulare reuerendissimarum Dn:m Vestrarum in me collatum beneficium recoliturum, et apud optimos quosque (quod et alias facere soleo) depraedicaturum, imo per oportunitatem id ipsum publice testaturum. Et si quid unquam fuerit in quo mea opella Vestris reuerendissimis Dom:bus, quod acceptum fuerit, vicissim praestare possit, re ipsa, quantum in me est, ostensurus sum, me huius singularis beneuolentiae haud immemorem esse. Quod omnibus et singulis reuerendissimi capituli vestri membris officiosissime significari velim. Devs opt. max. Vestram reuerendissimam Dominationem vna cum honorandissimis caeteris capitularibus quam diutissime florentem et incolumem conseruet.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 26. septembris stylo nouo anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[Tycho Brahe to the Dean of the Chapter of Magdeburg.]</b></p><p>Most reverend and most noble Lord Dean, most honoured Sir: my belongings, which had been stored at Magdeburg, arrived in these last few days at Leitmeritz, the point to which the Elbe is navigable toward Bohemia. That Your Lordship, together with the other colleagues of the most reverend chapter of Magdeburg, did not find it a burden to procure and forward them hither so promptly and liberally, at your own expense, for the favour of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord — this His Majesty received with most gracious mind as most welcome; as I learned not only from His Majesty himself (to whom I commended this most obliging service of yours a few days ago, when I attended him most humbly in the garden near the castle), but also yesterday from the Magnificent Lord Barvitius, supreme secretary and privy councillor to His Majesty, who intimated to me that he had himself written about this matter to the vice-chancellor of your most reverend chapter, Kilian Stösser. For my own part, indeed, for this your most kindly disposition — that you assented so freely and willingly to my wishes, and directed to me the furnishings of which I have long, and not without inconvenience, been deprived — I both render and owe you undying thanks for your outlay; and since I acknowledge myself unequal to repaying it, this at least I can promise: that I shall ever, with grateful mind, cherish this singular benefit conferred upon me by Your most reverend Lordships, and shall proclaim it before all the best men (as I am wont to do on other occasions), nay shall, when occasion offers, publicly attest the very same. And if ever there be anything in which my poor service may in turn render Your most reverend Lordships something that shall be acceptable, I shall show by the deed itself, so far as in me lies, that I am by no means unmindful of this singular goodwill — which I would wish to be most dutifully signified to each and every member of your most reverend chapter. May God, best and greatest, preserve Your most reverend Lordship, together with the rest of the most honoured chapter-members, flourishing and unharmed for the longest time.</p><p>Given at Prague in Bohemia, the 26th day of September, New Style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, now at <b>Prague</b> (he had moved from Benátky to the city in the late summer of 1600). The letter concerns the long-distance transport of his household goods — part of the effects shipped out of Denmark and across Germany after his 1597 departure from Hven.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> The <b>Dean of the Cathedral Chapter of Magdeburg</b> (<i>decanus capituli Magdeburgensis</i>), addressed by office, not by name; the goods had been in storage (<i>repositae</i>) at Magdeburg, a natural staging-point on the river route, and the chapter forwarded them downstream at imperial request.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Magnificus Dominus Barvitius</i> = <b>Johann Anton Barvitius (Barwitz)</b> (d. 1620), Latin secretary and privy councillor (<i>a consilijs intimo</i>) to Rudolf II — one of Tycho's principal channels to the Emperor at Prague (he recurs across the late correspondence as <i>Coraduz/Barvitius</i>).</li><li><i>Chilianus Stösserus</i> = <b>Kilian Stösser</b>, vice-chancellor of the Magdeburg chapter, Barvitius's correspondent in the matter.</li><li><i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas, dominus noster clementissimus</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Leutmeritzium</i> = <b>Leitmeritz / Litoměřice</b> on the Elbe (<i>Albis</i>), here named as the upstream limit of navigation toward Bohemia. <i>Praga Bohemorum</i> = <b>Prague</b>.</li><li><b>Witness note.</b> The Basel manuscript is catalogued as a <i>Kladde</i> — a <b>draft / retained office copy</b>, not the dispatched original; hence the bracketed editorial heading <i>[Tycho Brahe ad decanum capituli Magdeburgensis]</i> supplies the address the fair copy would have borne.</li><li><b>Register &amp; formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> A formal letter of thanks in high curial register: cascading superlatives (<i>reverendissime, honorandissime, clementissimo animo, immortales</i>), the humility topos (<i>ad compensandum imparem me agnoscam</i>), and the closing benediction <i>Deus optimus maximus … quam diutissime florentem et incolumem conservet</i>. <i>Dabantur</i> (epistolary \"they were given,\" i.e. dispatched) rendered \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 26. Septembris stylo novo</i> — explicitly <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b>, as expected of Tycho's Prague correspondence; reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, complete chancery Latin; persons and places securely identified. No textual crux. The recipient is anonymous only because the source addresses him by office.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.210-213 as page images. Letter no. 207 '[TYCHO BRAHE AD DECANUM CAPITULI MAGDEBURGENSIS]' runs from bottom of p.211 to p.212, closing 'Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 26. septembris stylo nouo anno 1600.' source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim (including 'stylo nouo', present here unlike in letter 217). Collated opening/closing and every proper name (Barvitius, vice-chancellor Kilian Stösser, Leitmeritz/Leutmeritzium, the Elbe/Albis) and date; the English tracks the Latin clause by clause with no omission, fabrication, or mistranslation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 144.. Kladde"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks the Chapter for forwarding his stored goods from Magdeburg to Prague at their own expense, noting the Emperor received this service with favour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000926001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000926002", "d": "1600-09-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck": "Vitus Winshemius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Vitus Winshemius — Prague, 26 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD VITUM WINSHEMIUM.</p><p>MAGNIFICE et excellentissime Domine D. VITE WINSHEMI, amice inprimis honorande: Ex literis Excell. Vae ijsdemque adiunctis, quae Illustrissimus Princeps OTTHO dux Brunsvicensis et Luneburgensis Clementissime a me requirat, intellexi; utque ipsius Celsitudo sciat, me ea in parte officiosissime illi pro meo modulo morem gesturum, dedi ad ipsius Celsitudinem literas meum responsum brevibus complectentes, quas ut ex me debita cum submissione ad ipsius Celsitudinem dirigere E. V. velit, peramanter rogo.</p><p>Meae res adhuc in eodem sunt statu quo antea. Versor etiamnum Pragae cum tota familia, huc a Sacra Caes. Maiestate, paulo postquam Pilsena redierat, Clementissime evocatus. Instrumenta mea Astronomica, quotquot in arce Benachiae habui, huc iussu ipsius Maiestatis allata sunt, atque in domo illa, quae iuxta hortum Caesaris prope arcem situm magnifice Italico more extructa est, et cupro superius tecta, quam E. V. proculdubio novit, disposita sunt, ut ipsius Maiestas ea promptius, cum lubuerit, perlustrare possit, quod et ante paucos dies, quamprimum ibi erecta erant, me praesente factum est, quaesivitque tunc ipsius Maiestas de caeteris, quae cum Leutmeritzium advenisse percepit, etiam in eandem domum transferri atque aliquantisper ibi collocari, donec de loco oportuno, ubi rectius ad usum constitui atque perpetuo conservari possint, providerit, benignissime mandavit. Quid hac in re fiet, postmodum V. E. ex me cognoscet; procedunt hîc omnia (ut scit) ob rerum gerendarum copiam et molem lento gradu, quod patienter ferendum est. Interim in domo quadam ampla, non longe ab arce remota, satis commode cum meis habito, et consuetis studijs, quantum interpellationes et conversationes cum Illustribus Viris in tantae aulae praesentia ferunt, vaco.</p><p>Gaudeo rem esse compositam inter nostrum Serenissimum Regem et Anglam; Deus author Pacis avertat in posterum discordiarum semen et occasiones.</p><p>FREDERICUS ROSENKRANZIUS iam est in Ungaria in exercitu equitatus Caesaris nostri, sed vereor, ne illi plus periculi immineat ex Asthmate et pulmonum vitijs, quibus graviter obnoxius est, praesertim si vinis istis fortioribus nimium indulserit, quam vel ab hoste propinquo, vel aliunde. Fuit hîc ante biduum quidam ex eius ministris, qui in Daniam properavit illi forte pecunias allaturus, cum quo OLIGERO nostro scribere voluissem, nisi tantopere festinasset, et eram alias uti nunc quoque admodum occupatus. Filia nihilominus mea natu maior ad illum quasdam exaravit, commonefacientes de equo quodam promisso, quem sponsione quadam, dum Wandesbeci essemus, amisit. Et quia nunc commoda datur occasio, equum illum cum patruelis sui ministro, qui eum proculdubio accedet, mittendi (is enim ibi resolutus in eo huc equitare poterit) voluit illa, me sic iubente, OLIGERO id insinuare. At minister ille iter accelerans hinc abierat, antequam literae dictae obsignatae essent. Quare eas nunc Hamburgum ad SIMONEM de PETKUM mitto. Si Vae Exae in promptu est commoditas eas ad OLIGERUM dirigendi, rogo id facere non degravetur. Sin minus, poterint GERHARDO RANZOVIO unâ cum alijs ipsi inscriptis transmitti, ita ut OLIGERUS eas recipiat, antequam FRIDERICI minister inde se huc et deinceps in Ungariam receperit. Sed sive id fiat, sive non, parum interest. Quare de tam levi re plura non addam, cum haec ipsa per se nimia sint.</p><p>Turcam Canisium obsidere voluisse, sed ob aquarum copiam, cum udus et paludosus sit locus, inde discessisse ferunt. Quorsum, necdum satis constat; habet exercitum quadruplo maiorem nostro, sed ultra mensem ob instantem hyemem ibi perdurare nequit. Dicunt eum pacem expetere, an vero id serio, et aequis conditionibus, dubium est. Caesar noster adhuc praesenti est animo, nec tam potentem hostem extimescit, utut Germaniae Principum subsidijs maiori ex parte destitutus. Deus omnia vertat ad felicem exitum, et Christianismum protegat, cui aeviterno numini te unâ cum tuis commendatum habeo.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum Die 26 Sept. Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO VITUS WINSHEMIUS.</b></p><p>Magnificent and most excellent Lord Doctor Vitus Winshemius, friend especially to be honoured: from Your Excellency's letter, and its enclosures, I have understood what the Most Illustrious Prince <b>Otto, Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg</b>, most clemently requires of me; and that His Highness may know I will most dutifully comply with him in that part, according to my measure, I have given to His Highness a letter comprising my answer briefly — which I lovingly ask that Your Excellency would direct to His Highness from me, with due submission.</p><p>My affairs are still in the same state as before. I still dwell at Prague with my whole household, summoned hither most clemently by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, a little after he had returned from <b>Pilsen</b>. My astronomical instruments, as many as I had in the castle of <b>Benátky</b>, have been brought hither by His Majesty's order, and arranged <b>in that house which is magnificently built in the Italian manner beside the Emperor's garden, near the castle, and roofed above with copper — which Your Excellency doubtless knows</b> — so that His Majesty may the more readily survey them when he pleases; which, a few days ago, as soon as they were erected there, was done in my presence. His Majesty then asked about the rest, which when he learned had reached <b>Leitmeritz [Litoměřice]</b>, he most kindly ordered to be transferred also into the same house and placed there for a while, until he should provide a convenient place where they could be more rightly set up for use and perpetually preserved. What will be done in this matter, Your Excellency will afterward learn from me; here all things proceed (as you know) at a slow pace, on account of the abundance and mass of business, which must be patiently borne. Meanwhile I dwell, conveniently enough, with my household in a certain ample house not far from the castle, and am free for my accustomed studies — as far as the interruptions and conversations with Illustrious Men, in the presence of so great a court, allow.</p><p>I rejoice that the matter has been <b>settled between our Most Serene King and the Englishwoman</b> [Queen Elizabeth]; may God, the author of Peace, avert in future the seed and occasions of discords.</p><p><b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> is now in <b>Hungary</b>, in the cavalry army of our Emperor; but I fear more danger threatens him <b>from the Asthma and lung-defects</b> to which he is gravely liable — especially if he indulges too much in those stronger wines — than either from the near enemy or from elsewhere. There was here, two days ago, one of his servants, who hastened into Denmark, perhaps to bring him money, with whom I would have wished to write to our <b>Holger</b> [Rosenkrantz], had he not hastened so; and I was otherwise (as now too) quite occupied. Nevertheless <b>my elder daughter</b> wrote some lines to him [Holger], reminding him of a certain <b>promised horse, which he lost by a wager, while we were at Wandsbek</b>; and because a convenient occasion is now given of sending that horse with his cousin's servant — who will doubtless come to him (for he, being at leisure there, will be able to ride hither on it) — she wished, at my bidding, to intimate this to Holger. But that servant, hastening his journey, had departed before the said letter was sealed. Wherefore I now send it to Hamburg, to <b>Simon von Petkum</b>. If Your Excellency has at hand a means of directing it to Holger, I ask you not to find it burdensome to do so; if not, it can be sent to <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b>, together with others addressed to him, so that Holger may receive it before Frederik's servant betakes himself thence hither, and then into Hungary. But whether it be done or not, it matters little; wherefore I will add no more about so trifling a matter, since these very [words] are in themselves too much.</p><p>They report that the <b>Turk</b> wished to besiege <b>Kanizsa</b>, but, on account of the abundance of waters (the place being wet and marshy), departed thence — whither, it is not yet sufficiently certain. He has an army <b>four times greater than ours</b>, but cannot endure there beyond a month, on account of the approaching winter. They say he seeks peace, but whether seriously, and on fair terms, is doubtful. Our Emperor is still of resolute mind, and does not greatly dread so powerful an enemy, although for the most part destitute of the German Princes' subsidies. May God turn all things to a happy outcome, and protect Christendom — to which everlasting Power I hold you, with yours, commended.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 26th day of September, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Vitus Winshemius</b> (the younger), Wittenberg professor of medicine and Tycho's go-between (cf. brahe-t_15980328001, the Bökel affair). The MS is in Basel.</li><li><b>Duke Otto's request.</b> Tycho answers (via Winshemius) a <i>requirit</i> from <b>Otto, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</b>, enclosing a brief reply to be forwarded to the Duke \"with due submission\" — the matter itself not specified.</li><li><b>Significance — the instrument installation near Prague Castle (material history).</b> A precise account of where the instruments first went on reaching Prague: <b>the Benátky instruments were set up in \"that house … magnificently built in the Italian manner beside the Emperor's [pleasure-]garden, near the castle, and roofed with copper,\"</b> where <b>Rudolf II viewed them in Tycho's presence</b> \"a few days ago\"; the rest, arrived at <b>Leitmeritz (Litoměřice)</b> up the Elbe, the Emperor ordered brought to the same house \"until a convenient place\" be found (the move to the Curtius house would follow). Meanwhile Tycho lodges \"in a certain ample house not far from the castle.\" Complements brahe-t_16001008001, brahe-t_16001009001 (the Benátky/Magdeburg → Leitmeritz → Prague transport).</li><li><b>The Anglo-Danish peace.</b> Tycho rejoices the dispute \"<b>between our Most Serene King and the Englishwoman</b>\" (Christian IV and <b>Elizabeth I</b>) is settled — the <b>Emden conference</b> outcome (cf. brahe-t_16000516001, where he had feared it might \"breed war\").</li><li><b>Frederik Rosenkrantz and the horse (family/personal detail).</b> <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> — of the disgrace-and-rehabilitation thread — is now serving in the <b>imperial cavalry in Hungary</b>; Tycho fears his <b>asthma and weak lungs</b> (worsened by \"stronger wines\") more than the enemy. A charming domestic vignette: <b>Tycho's elder daughter</b> wrote to <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> about a <b>horse Holger had lost to her in a wager at Wandsbek</b>, now to be sent via a servant; the letter, missing the courier, is routed to <b>Simon von Petkum</b> at Hamburg, thence (via Winshemius or <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b>) to Holger.</li><li><b>The Turkish war.</b> The <b>Turk</b> abandoned the siege of <b>Kanizsa (Nagykanizsa)</b> because of flooding/marsh; his army \"four times greater than ours\" but unable to winter there; reportedly seeking peace (doubtfully). <b>Rudolf II</b> \"of resolute mind,\" though \"for the most part destitute of the German Princes' subsidies.\" (Cf. the Long-War news of brahe-t_16000916002, brahe-t_16010415002.)</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Vitus Winshemius; <b>Otto, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>Christian IV</b> and <b>Elizabeth I</b>; <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b>; <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (<i>Oligerus</i>); Tycho's <b>elder daughter</b> (unnamed — Magdalene or Elisabeth Brahe); <b>Simon von Petkum</b> (Hamburg factor); <b>Gerhard Rantzau</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Pragae Bohemorum Die 26 Sept. Anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (Duke Otto's forwarded request; the instruments installed in the copper-roofed Italianate house by the imperial garden, with the Leitmeritz remainder to follow; the Anglo-Danish peace; Frederik Rosenkrantz in Hungary and the daughter's horse-wager letter to Holger via Petkum/Gerhard Rantzau; the Turk's withdrawal from Kanizsa) is clear and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; Tycho's elder daughter is left unnamed per the no-fabrication rule.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated in full against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.366-369 (all 4 pages read as page images). The letter occupies pp.367-368; p.366 carries the tail of Herwart's letter to Tycho and the start of Tycho's letter to Otto of Brunswick, and p.369 carries the start of Tycho's letter to Georgius Rollenhagius, all correctly excluded. Full Latin text collated line by line: Otto Duke of Brunswick/Luneburg, the instrument transfer from Benatky to the Emperor's Italianate copper-roofed house, Leitmeritz, the Anglo-Danish settlement, Frederik Rosenkrantz's posting in Hungary and his asthma, the promised-horse/Wandsbek wager episode, Holger Rosenkrantz, Simon von Petkum, Gerhard Rantzau, the Turkish siege of Kanizsa and the 'four times greater' army figure, and the closing date (26 Sept. 1600) all match Dreyer exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Vitus Winshemius", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000813001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000813001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges Duke Otto of Brunswick's requests, encloses a reply for him, and reports his instruments installed in an Italian-style house beside the Emperor's garden at Prague.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000926002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16000926003", "d": "1600-09-26", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Georg Rollenhagen — Prague, 26 September 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD GEORGIUM ROLLENHAGIUM.</p><p>S.</p><p>QUOD supellex mea, quae Magdeburgi reservabatur, nunc tandem in has Bohemiae oras appulerit, est cur Reverendissimo Capitulo Vestro, quod sumtus in eos usus liberaliter suppeditavit, tum quoque tibi, Excellentissime et amantissime Domine ROLLENHAGI, gratias agam quam maximas, prout etiam peculiaribus literis ad honorandissimum et nobilissimum Dominum Decanum reliquosque Capitulares, quas illi per te officiosissime meo nomine tradi velim, testatus sum. Quod autem cum ANDREA ministro meo isthuc pro rebus illis huc adferendis emisso Reverendissimae illius Dominationi caeterisque nobilissimis Collegis nihil scripserim, non ulla praepostera voluntate, vel quod hoc ipsis me debere gratitudinis loco officium non agnoscerem, factum est, sed cum ijs ipsis diebus in quibusdam a Sacra Caesarea Maiestate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, mihi iniunctis, quae ocius absolvi debebant, essem occupatissimus, et aliâs plurimis praepedirer interpellationibus, ita ut varijs obruerer negocijs, vix tantum mihi supererat temporis, ut ad te nonnulla, quem ob familiaritatem nostram transcursorie alloqui licuit, exarare potuerim. Quare praevidens, me id, quod iuremerito facere oportuisset, ut indecenter omitterem, compelli, te diligenter rogavi, ut hanc literarum intermissionem apud Reverendissimum Capitulum excusares, meque cum nautis, ubi res meas huc advexissent, ad vos redituris illis scripturum indicavi, quod et nunc effeci, non quidem per nautas, siquidem illi nimis cito Leutmeritzio discesserunt, sed cum hoc tabellario Hamburgo adveniente et commodum sese offerente, qui in illas oras confestim rediturus est.</p><p>Sororem meam SOPHIAM adhuc Segebergae prope Lubecam haerere, idem tabellarius refert, at ubi ERICUS LANGIUS sit, ignorat. Doleo utriusque fortunam pertinaciter reluctantem. Scripsi SIMONI â PETKUM Procuratori meo Hamburgensi, ut ferreas fornaces, et si quae alia ibi ex meis rebus residua sunt, Magdeburgum per primam quamque oportunitatem ad te mittat, quae, uti polliceris, in tuis aedibus, neque enim multa sunt, reservabis, donec occasio suppetierit Leutmeritzium absque magno sumtu ea dirigendi. Optarem autem fornaces, de quibus dixi, sive integras sive fractas, huc ante Albis congelationem, modo fieri possit, afferri.</p><p>Instrumenta Astronomica, quae Benaticae habui, quotquot mecum in Bohemiam attuleram, quae numero erant sex maiora et aliquot minora, Caesar iussit, ut huc Pragam transferrem, ubi ex ipsius etiam Clementissima voluntate in domo quadam magnifice et splendide iuxta hortum prope arcem a FERDINANDO avo aedificata disposita sunt. Cumque ipsius Maiestas quodam die praeteritae septimanae ea perlustrare dignata esset, dedit mihi in mandatis, ut caetera omnia, quae Leutmeritzium impensis Reverendissimi Capituli Vestri iam advenisse retuli, quod et antea cognoverat, ibidem collocarem, ut omnia simul essent, tunc se, postquam singula perspexisset, de oportuniore loco, quam est in arce Benatica, ubi collocari et in debito usu conservari possent, provisurum clementissime addixit. Inclinare autem visus est ad aedes Curtianas, hac de caussa a vidua relicta comparandas, siquidem non longe ab arce sublimi prospectu constituantur. Domus etiam amplae et in edito loco sitae et dimidio saltem milliari hinc remotae atque in formam stellae cuiusdam, unde etiam nomen habet, a FERDINANDO Archiduce sumtuose extructae, quam hortus ferarum circuit, mentionem fecit, si forte ea observationibus et instrumentorum tractationi accommoda foret. At ego perinde esse, ubinam eius Maiestas id fieri vellet, modo perpetuari res possit, ne idem aliquando eveniret quod in Dania, subiectissime respondi: tunc ipsius Maiestas de aedificio quodam Viennae a MAXIMILIANO parente in alto quodam loco eleganter nec parvis impensis extructo nonnulla insinuavit, quod Viennam iter spacio paucorum dierum faciens spectare, et an pro his exercitijs commodum foret, dispicere possem: ibi se certum esse, quod perpetuo conservari possint omnia. Sed nihil conclusum est, antequam caetera instrumenta huc allata fuerint, et illa etiam in dicta domo iuxta hortum collocata viderit Caesar.</p><p>Ad ea, de quibus me commonefacis, in Postscriptis respondebo. Quae ANDREAS minister meus sui purgandi caussa tibi dixit, more consueto, quo leviter quidvis loquitur, fecit; neque enim per omnia ita se habent: discesserat is, cum adhuc Benaticae essem, me inscio et non interrogato, nec per octiduum quaesitus uspiam comparuit. Postea sponte redijt, cum alium forte Dominum invenire non posset, nec pecunia, quam abliguriret, illi superesset; at ego illum tunc recipere nolui. Sed ut Capitaneo Brandeisensi inserviret, permisi, quod non diu durare potuit. Falsa etiam sunt, quae de pecunia illi a me debita narravit: nam persoluta sunt illi omnia, quae stipendij loco addixeram, nec habet, quod iure ulterius poscat, ut vel hoc nomine, quod iniuste de me apud alios conqueratur, cohercendus foret, verum ego illi ob tuam intercessionem ignoscam, quia, uti ais, vere simplicissimus est minusque prudens, et quidvis inconsiderate effutit nec sibi ipsi constat. Si in posterum frugi esse voluerit, videbo, quid in tui gratiam facere potero, aut si sui similis permanserit, uti proculdubio faciet, dabo operam, ut alium acquirat Dominum, quod et antea hîc molitus sum apud satellitum praefectum, sed necdum succedere potuit.</p><p>Quod Dominus CASPAR ALEMANN Consul Vester aegre tulerit, in sua absentia reliquum Senatum officium in advehendis huc meis rebus Caesari denegasse, libenter audio, illique pro hoc suo erga me favore gratias habeo. Praestitisset forte reliquos civiliores fuisse et Caesareae Maiestati, summo Magistratui, obsequentiores se exhibuisse. Verum ego id potius, quomodocunque datur, excusare quam aggravare malim.</p><p>De URSO isto quae subiunxisti, ita ferme evenerunt, prout ominari visus es; nam sive in Castris Gallicis, quibus assuefactus erat, sive Asthmate et Phthisi, vel potius utraque de caussa consumtus ante sex septimanas expiravit, idque ea ipsa hora, qua Citatio Commissariorum Caesaris, quos, ut de ipso eiusque famoso scripto iudicarent, impetraram, insinuanda fuit: quam primum enim huc Pragam (ut conscius sis) a Caesare, statim ac Pilsena redierat, Clementissime vocatus adveneram, atque URSUM istum periculose decumbere mihi relatum esset, misi ad illum duos Iuris Doctores unâ cum publico Notario, quaesitum, an scriptum istud maledicum et calumnijs scatens revocare vellet: simulque notulas praecipuas calumniarum illi praelegi feci; is licet plaeraque in ijs infitiaretur, quamvis in scripto isto indicata pagina expresse invenirentur, revocare tamen noluit, sed iudicum arbitrio omnia submisit, quod et lubentius vidi, ideoque a Caesare humilime obtinui, ut quatuor deputarentur commissarij, qui caussam iure deciderent, inter quos duo sunt Barones, Dominus CHRISTOPHORUS de SCHLEUNITZ et Dominus ERNFRIDUS de MINCKWITZ, reliqui duo Doctores Iuris ex ipsius Maiestatis Consiliarijs. Defuncto autem reo, et citatione ante obitum ipsi non praelecta, actio suspensa est, donec novum decretum, quod et brevi futurum confido, superveniret. Expecto saltem reditum Procuratoris mei in hac caussa, Doctoris cuiusdam Iuris IOHANNIS FRITZSCHIJ, qui Budissinum in Lusatiam profectus est. Nam actio non est mere personalis, sed etiam realis, ob scriptum publice editum, perpetuo loco sui authoris loquens. Ideoque contra hoc procedendum, omnesque calumniae, quibus abundat, detegendae, refutandae et condemnandae sunt, posteaque alio scripto in publicum emisso propalandae, in quo totus processus omnesque contrariae probationes continebuntur, adiuncto Caes. Matis decreto, quo per totum Imperium famosus iste liber irritus reddetur, seu, uti aiunt, annullabitur. Et iam Reverendissimo Archiepiscopo Pragensi a Caesare iniunctum est, ut in Typographum animadvertat, atque exemplaria omnia conquiri faciat, quotquot Pragae reperiuntur, eaque Vulcano tradat: si aliquantisper supervixisset author, sententiam talem, uti ex commissarijs intellexi, accepisset, ut infamis redditus, capite plecteretur, aut secundum leges Bohemicas in quatuor partes dissecaretur; sed mihi non tam erat quaestio de ipsius persona, quam scurrilem et vanam fuisse omnes norunt, sed potius de libro isto tot iniurijs et mendacijs scatente abolendo, et mea meorumque fama ac existimatione vindicanda, quod etiamnum volente Deo fiet. Quin et ROTHMANNUM, sive adhuc in vivis sit, sive non, ab ijs, quibus illum aspersit maculis et vaniloquijs, liberabo, atque iniuriose ipsi illatas esse pronunciari efficiam, quod ad ipsum, si adhuc superest, perscribas velim. Et si quas habes eius manu scriptas literas, eas tuis, quibus ipsius esse Chirographum testeris, inclusas ad me mitte, ut inde probare possim, alteras tibi sub finem antecedentis anni inscriptas mecumque communicatas ipsius quoque manu exaratas esse. Ubi processus totus absolutus fuerit, omniaque in codicillos relata et typis edita, faciam te eorum participem.</p><p>Vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae die 26 Septembris stylo novo Anno 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp. 372–375; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>POSTSCRIPTA. Rogas, ut Reverendissimo Archiepiscopatus Vestri Domino Decano, qui tam benigne et libenter res meas huc in Bohemiam promovit, aliquid medicamenti contra calculi dolores, quibus quotidie infestatur, quod sane quam libentissime facerem, si eiuscemodi praesens remedium mihi nunc in promptu esset. Laboratorium, quod Caesar mea de caussa Benaticae pro praeparationibus Spagyricarum medicinarum extrui clementissime mandavit, propter eius Maiestatis diutinam Pilsenae absentiam, non est hactenus absolutum: ideoque eiuscemodi pyronomicis confectionibus, prout alias voluissem, operam dare non licuit. Ne vero optimum et honorandissimum illum virum de me tam bene meritum suo hac in parte desiderio plane frustrare viderer, subiungam quandam medicamenti formam, quae isthic non difficulter confieri poterit. Cuius usum in tali affectione praesens afferre subsidium compertum est. Rcp. Semin. Feniculi, Petroselini, Anisi, Saxifragiae, Cardamomi, Oculorum cancri, Lupi piscis, Nucl. persic. ana oz. I, Cynosbaton Germ. hainbutten beeren, Mespillorum, Charabes albi ana oz. II. Contundantur omnia in mediocriter tenuem pulverem, et cucurbitae vitreae imponantur, moxque superfundatur de spiritu iuniperorum, qui instar aquae vitae est, tantum, ut duobus vel tribus digitis supereminent, clausoque postea et bene obturato vitro in tepidum balneum omnia simul per unam hebdomadam digerenda collocentur, et singulis diebus vitrum circumspecte exemptum bis vel ter cum imposita materia circumagatur, et nonnihil hinc inde moveatur, ut specierum impositarum essentiae eo melius extrahi et spiritui iuniperino uniri possint. Quo peracto omnis materia frigefacta una cum liquore superfuso primum per lineum pannum fortiter exprimatur, deinde per bibulam papyrum coletur et sistatur, ut purior evadat: postmodum hic totus liquor essentiam specierum in se continens in balneo mediocriter calido per Alembicum distilletur, donec materia quaedam instar liquidi mellis in fundo remaneat, cui materiae tantundem quasi de spiritu Therebinthinae optimo, qui levi calore in balneo ascendit, addendum est, cum quo extractum illud denuo in tepido balneo, vitro bene clauso, per spatium unius septimanae digerendum; posteaque simul per bibulam papyrum iterum filtrandum, addito aliquantulo de liquore iuniperino prius destillato, si opus fuerit, ut promptius filtratio absolvatur. Qui tamen liquor lentissimo calore balnei inde postea auferendus est, ne sua ignea et acuta vi medicamento officiat. Ex hoc dicto modo diligenter praeparato medicamento dentur patienti tres vel quatuor guttae in vino mane singulis diebus, tunc quoque post meridiem ante caenam, idque quando et quamdiu opus fuerit. Ex liquore autem iuniperino, qui per Alembicum ascendit, poterit etiam parum quasi quartae partis unius cochlearis vesperi ante decubitum assumere instar alterius aquae vitae, si modo eam ferre poterit, idque potius hyeme quam aestate fiat, et non nisi bis vel ter in septimana eo vesperi, uti dictum, utatur. Haec si rite et praescripta ratione executus fuerit, sentiet inde favente Deo morbi non mediocre levamentum.  /  POSTSCRIPTA ALTERA. Quod amice me admones de ijs, quae quidam ex aula hac Caesareana cuidam praecipuo apud vos nobili scripsit, gratum est, licet omnia illa quae retulit, prout recte in literis tuis conijcis, falsissima (quod et praestat) sint. Ipsius Maiestas nunquam adhuc mecum de talibus locuta est, nec per literas aut oretenus Turcarum aut belli Turcici mentionem ullam fecit, multo minus ut ego, qui alienis rebus me non immisceam, nec vaticinia profitear, tale quid protulerim: imo, quod plus est, ea potius semper insinuavi, quae a melancholicis curis, animi deiectione, suspicionibus et superstitionibus otiosis, alijsque eiuscemodi mentem sanam turbantibus avertere possunt, idque ita sedulo hactenus egi, ut forte vix alius quispiam, qui diu illi ab intimis fuit consilijs, tam cordate et praesenter facere ausus fuisset, quod optime novit magnificus Dominus Barvitius Secretarius praecipuus, qui Caesari fere semper praesto est. Ille eo nomine aliquoties ex animo mihi gratias egit, seque hanc ingenuitatem, quodque ea, quae decent et conducibilia sunt, non dissimulem, sed sincere ipsius Maiestati, cum subiectissima tamen discretione, aperiam, deque ijs oportune commonefaciam, ad caeteros consiliarios praecipuos depraedicasse, non semel indicavit, quibus hoc gratissimum fuit, adeo ut res longe aliter se habeat, quam quidam ignari et nescio quid sinistro iudicio somniantes suspicari fortasse possunt. Sic non defuerunt, qui Caesaris tam diutinam Pilsenae absentiam mihi vanissime imputarent, atque id non saltem Pragae, sed et in varijs Bohemiae locis spargere non sunt veriti, cum tamen id mihi nunquam in mentem venerit, priusquam ab alijs referri audivi: et vix alius magis quam ego ipsius Maiestatis praesentiam desideravi, siquidem, interea dum abfuit, res meae minus promoverentur, utut essent, qui earum executionem in mandatis haberent. Nec scio quenquam hic, qui mei odio laboret: neque enim ulli occasionem dedi, imo quam plurimi adeoque praecipui quique non saltem in aula, sed et alibi in regno hoc amicitiam et conversationem meam sponte exoptant, et magis quam cuperem ambiunt, ut sic et temporis et valetudinis iacturam saepius quam vellem facere cogar. Quin et illustriores regni Primates mihi proprio motu nec rogati obtulerunt, ut in regni indigenam adsciscerer, nec alio me converterem; quod se primis comitiis effecturos prompto animo polliciti sunt. Si qui inter vulgares latitant morosuli, invidi et malevoli, quorum tamen neminem novi, illi in sua sese inescent et radant malevolentia ac iudicij perversitate, haecque habeant secum serventque sepulchro: infelix et miser est, cui nemo invidet, nec splendet aut conspicua virtus, quae livore suapte umbra caret. Vides itaque, mi Rollenhagi, quanta iniuria non saltem meam personam, sed, quod longe plus est, ipsam Sacram Caesaream Maiestatem, Imperij Caput et Dominum nostrum clementissimum, qui praesenti est animo, nec Turcas formidat, sed et Pacem, nisi honorifica et utilis sit, etiam ultro oblatam respuit, afficere non erubescunt, dum tam sutilia et a veritate alienissima scribunt. Si simul essemus, possem multis documentis et Tecmirijs irrefragabiliter ostendere istorum hominum vaniloquentiam: quodque contrarium potius verum esse. Interim tamen secreto ex te scire averem, quisnam fuerit, qui talia suis literis inspersit, et ad quem eas exaravit, non quod tales nugas morer, aut quod illi id obijcere velim (sunt enim multa dissimulanda et ex alto despicienda), sed saltem ut mihi ab homine tam sinistra et frivola suspicante et malevolo cavere possim, si forte ipsum novi aut alicubi in illum inciderim. Ut igitur eundem pauculis vel etiam tectis verbis mihi indices, quamprimum denuo ad me scripseris, te per amicitiam nostram rogatum habeo. Fiet id absque omni tuo periculo. Scio enim prudenter et taciturne in eiuscemodi malitiosis et perversis cavillationibus agendum esse. Vale. Datum ut supra in literis.  /  Addo et hoc, ea, quae superioribus literis de persecutione eorum, qui Euangelicam doctrinam profitentur in Austria, consensu Caesaris per Iesuitas fieri innuebas, falso ad vos relata esse. Omnia enim in Austria quoad religionem in priori sunt statu, nec Caesar permittit Iesuitas aut alios, qui se Catholicos nuncupant, turbas excitare, neque quemquam ob religionis diversitatem odit aut male tractat, cum etiam multos in sua Aula patiatur a Pontificiis dissentientes. Sed in Styria magna suborta est Euangelicorum afflictio, Iesuitis ex authoritate Principis neminem, qui non cum illis sentiat, istic ferentibus, quod tamen invito et aliter suadente Caesare evenit. Sunt qui ominantur, Turcam supervenientem controversias illas et persecutiones dirempturum, nam occupato (quod tamen Deus avertat) Canisio, nullum restat munitum propugnaculum, antequam Styriam invadere possit. Sed de his providebit is, in cuius manu sunt omnia.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO GEORG ROLLENHAGEN.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>That <b>my goods, which were kept at Magdeburg, have now at last arrived on these coasts of Bohemia</b>, is cause that I give the greatest thanks to your Reverend Chapter — which liberally supplied the costs for those uses — and also to you, most excellent and most loving Lord Rollenhagen, as I have also testified in a separate letter to the most honoured and noble Lord Dean and the rest of the Chapter, which I should wish delivered to him most dutifully in my name by you. That I wrote nothing to that Reverend Lordship and the rest of his most noble Colleagues when my servant <b>Andreas</b> was sent thither to bring those things hither, was done by no perverse intent, nor that I did not acknowledge I owe them this duty by way of gratitude — but because in those very days I was most busy with certain things enjoined on me by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, which had to be finished quickly, and was otherwise hindered by very many interruptions, so overwhelmed by various business that scarcely so much time remained me as to scratch a few lines to you (whom, on account of our familiarity, I might address cursorily). Wherefore, foreseeing that I was compelled to omit unbecomingly what by right I ought to have done, I diligently asked you to excuse this omission of a letter to the Reverend Chapter, and indicated that I would write them with the sailors, when they had carried my things hither, returning to you — which I now do, not indeed by the sailors (since they left Leitmeritz too quickly), but by this courier, arriving from Hamburg and conveniently offering himself, who is about to return at once to those parts.</p><p><b>My sister Sophia is still lingering at Segeberg near Lübeck</b>, the same courier reports; but where <b>Erik Lange</b> is, he does not know. <b>I grieve the fortune of both, stubbornly resisting.</b> I have written <b>Simon von Petkum, my proctor at Hamburg, to send the iron furnaces — and any other things of mine remaining there — to Magdeburg by the first opportunity</b>, which (as you promise) you will keep in your house (for they are not many), until occasion serve to direct them to Leitmeritz without great cost. I should wish the furnaces of which I spoke, whether whole or broken, brought hither before the Elbe freezes, if it can be done.</p><p>The <b>Astronomical Instruments I had at Benátky — as many as I had brought with me into Bohemia, six larger and some smaller in number — the Emperor ordered me to transfer hither to Prague</b>, where, by his most clement will too, they have been arranged in <b>a certain house, magnificently and splendidly built by the garden, near the castle, by Ferdinand the grandfather</b> [the Royal Summer Palace / Belvedere, built by Ferdinand I]. And when His Majesty, one day last week, deigned to inspect them, he gave me in charge <b>to place all the rest (which I reported had now arrived at Leitmeritz at the expense of your Reverend Chapter — as he had known before too) in the same place, that all might be together</b>; then, after he had examined each, he most clemently promised that he would provide <b>a more convenient place than Benátky castle, where they could be set up and kept in due use</b>. He seemed to incline toward <b>the Curtius house — to be bought, on this account, from the widow left [behind] — since it is set not far from the castle, with a lofty prospect</b>. He also made mention of <b>a large house, set in a high place, half a mile distant hence, built sumptuously by Archduke Ferdinand in the form of a certain star (whence too it has its name), surrounded by a game-park</b> [the <b>Star Villa / Hvězda</b>], if perchance it might be suited to observations and the handling of instruments. But I answered most submissively that <b>it was all one where His Majesty wished it done, provided the thing could be made permanent, lest the same should sometime happen as in Denmark.</b> Then His Majesty intimated something about <b>a building at Vienna, built elegantly and at no small cost, in a certain high place, by his father Maximilian</b> [the <i>Neugebäude</i>] — which, making the journey to Vienna in a few days' space, I could view, and consider whether it would be convenient for these exercises: there, he was sure, all could be permanently kept. But nothing has been concluded before the other instruments have been brought hither, and the Emperor has seen them too placed in the said house by the garden.</p><p>To the things of which you remind me, I will respond in the Postscripts. <b>What my servant Andreas said to you, to clear himself, he did in his usual manner — by which he speaks anything lightly; for it is not in all respects so.</b> He had left, while I was still at Benátky, without my knowledge and unasked, nor for eight days was he found anywhere; afterward he returned of his own accord, when he could find no other master, and had no money left to squander; but I would not take him back then. Yet I allowed him to serve the Captain of Brandeis, which could not last long. False too are the things he told about money owed him by me; for all that I had assigned by way of stipend has been paid him, nor has he anything he can justly demand further — so that on this very score, that he unjustly complains of me to others, he ought to be restrained. But I will forgive him, for your intercession's sake, since (as you say) <b>he is truly most simple and not very prudent, and blurts anything thoughtlessly, and is not consistent with himself.</b> If hereafter he will be frugal, I will see what I can do for him, for your sake; or if he remain like himself (as doubtless he will), I will take pains that he get another master — which I attempted here before, with the prefect of the guards, but it could not yet succeed.</p><p>That Lord <b>Caspar Alemann, your Consul, took it ill that in his absence the rest of the Senate denied the Emperor the service of conveying my things hither, I gladly hear, and thank him for this his favour toward me.</b> It would perhaps have been better that the rest had been more civil, and shown themselves more obedient to the Imperial Majesty, the supreme Magistrate. But I would rather excuse it, however it may be, than aggravate it.</p><p><b>What you subjoined about that Ursus came about almost as you seemed to presage; for — whether consumed in the French camps to which he was accustomed, or by Asthma and Phthisis, or rather by both causes — he expired six weeks ago, and that at the very hour when the Citation of the Emperor's Commissioners (whom I had obtained to judge of him and his infamous writing) was to be served.</b> For as soon as I had come hither to Prague (that you may be aware), summoned most clemently by the Emperor as soon as he had returned from Pilsen, and it had been reported to me that <b>Ursus lay dangerously ill, I sent to him two Doctors of Law, with a public Notary, to ask whether he would revoke that slanderous writing, teeming with calumnies</b>; and at the same time had the chief notes of the calumnies read to him. Though he denied most of them (though they are expressly found on the indicated page in that writing), <b>he yet would not revoke, but submitted all to the judges' arbitration — which I saw the more gladly</b>; and therefore I humbly obtained from the Emperor that <b>four Commissioners be deputed to decide the case at law — among them two Barons, Lord Christopher von Schleinitz and Lord Ernfried von Minckwitz, the other two Doctors of Law from His Majesty's Councillors</b>. But, the defendant being dead, and the citation not read to him before death, <b>the action is suspended until a new decree come</b> (which I trust will be shortly). I only await the return of my proctor in this case, a certain Doctor of Law, <b>Johannes Fritzsch</b>, who has gone to <b>Bautzen in Lusatia</b>. For <b>the action is not merely personal, but also real, on account of the publicly-published writing, which perpetually speaks in place of its author</b>; and so it must be proceeded against, and all the calumnies it abounds in detected, refuted, and condemned, and afterward made public in another writing, in which the whole process and all the contrary proofs shall be contained — with the Imperial Majesty's decree adjoined, <b>by which throughout the whole Empire that infamous book shall be made void, or, as they say, annulled. And already the Reverend Archbishop of Prague has been enjoined by the Emperor to animadvert on the Printer, and to have all copies sought out — as many as are found at Prague — and given to Vulcan [burned].</b> If the author had survived a while, he would have received such a sentence (as I learned from the Commissioners) that, rendered infamous, he should be beheaded, or, according to the Bohemian laws, cut into four parts. But for me the question was not so much about his person (whom all know to have been scurrilous and vain), as about <b>abolishing that book, teeming with so many injuries and lies, and vindicating my own and my people's fame and reputation</b> — which, God willing, shall still be done. <b>Nay, I will also free Rothmann — whether he be yet alive or not — from the stains and vain slanders with which [Ursus] bespattered him, and will bring it about that they be pronounced injuriously inflicted on him</b> — which you would write to him, if he still survives. And <b>if you have any letters written in his hand, send them to me, enclosed in yours (testifying that it is his handwriting), that I may thence prove that the others addressed to you at the end of last year, and shared with me, are also written in his hand.</b> When the whole process is finished, and all reported in codicils and published in print, I will make you partaker of them.</p><p>Farewell.<br>Given at Prague, the 26th day of September, New Style, the year 1600.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> POSTSCRIPTS. You ask that [I send] something of medicine to the most Reverend Lord Dean of your Archbishopric — who so kindly and willingly forwarded my affairs hither into Bohemia — against the pains of the stone, with which he is daily afflicted; which I should indeed most willingly do, were such a ready remedy now at hand for me. The laboratory which the Emperor, on my account, most clemently ordered to be built at Benátky for the preparation of Spagyric medicines is not yet finished, owing to His Majesty's long absence at Pilsen; and so I have not been able, as otherwise I should have wished, to give my labour to confections of that pyronomic kind. But lest I should seem wholly to disappoint that best and most honourable man, so well deserving of me, in his desire in this matter, I will subjoin a certain formula of a medicine, which can be made up there without difficulty, and whose use in such an affliction is known to bring present relief. Take: seed of fennel, of parsley, of anise, of saxifrage, of cardamom; crab's eyes; [the bone of] the lupus-fish; kernels of peach — of each one ounce; Cynosbaton (German <i>hainbutten beeren</i> [rosehips]), medlars, white amber — of each two ounces. Let all be pounded into a moderately fine powder and put into a glass cucurbit, and straightway let spirit of junipers (which is like aqua vitae) be poured over them, so much that it stand two or three fingers above; and then, the glass closed and well stopped, let all be set in a warm bath to digest for one week, and each day let the glass be carefully taken out and turned round two or three times with the matter within, and moved a little this way and that, so that the essences of the ingredients may be the better extracted and united to the juniper spirit. This done, let all the cooled matter, with the liquor poured over it, be first strongly pressed out through a linen cloth, then strained through blotting-paper and left to settle, that it come out purer; afterward let this whole liquor, holding the essence of the ingredients, be distilled in a moderately warm bath through an alembic, until a matter like liquid honey remain in the bottom; to which must be added about as much of the best spirit of turpentine (which rises by a gentle heat in the bath); with which that extract is again digested in a warm bath, the glass well closed, for one week; and afterward filtered once more through blotting-paper, with a little of the juniper liquor distilled before added if need be, that the filtration finish more readily. This liquor, however, must thereafter be drawn off by the slowest heat of the bath, lest by its fiery and sharp force it harm the medicine. Of this medicine, diligently prepared in the said manner, let three or four drops in wine be given to the patient each morning, and likewise after midday before supper, whenever and as long as need shall be. And of the juniper liquor that rises through the alembic he may also take a little — about a quarter of a spoonful — in the evening before bed, as a further aqua vitae, if he can bear it; and let this be done rather in winter than in summer, and only twice or thrice a week in the evening, as said. If he carries these out duly and in the prescribed manner, he will, God favouring, feel thence no slight relief of the disease.  /  FURTHER POSTSCRIPTS. That you in friendship admonish me of what a certain person of this Imperial court wrote to a certain principal nobleman among you, is welcome — although all he reported is, as you rightly conjecture, most false (which is also for the best). His Majesty has never yet spoken with me of such things, nor by letter or word made any mention of the Turks or of the Turkish war, much less that I — who do not meddle in others' affairs nor profess prophecies — should have brought forth any such thing; nay, what is more, I have rather always insinuated those things which can turn the mind away from melancholy cares, dejection, suspicions, idle superstitions, and the like that disturb a sound mind; and this I have done so sedulously that scarcely any other long of his intimate councils would have dared to do it so heartily and so readily — which the magnificent Lord Barvitius, the chief Secretary, almost always at the Emperor's side, knows very well. He has on that account several times thanked me heartily, and more than once shown that he has extolled this candour of mine before the other chief councillors — that I do not dissemble what is fitting and profitable, but sincerely (yet with most submissive discretion) lay it open to His Majesty and seasonably admonish him — which was most welcome to them; so that the matter stands far otherwise than certain ignorant men, dreaming I know not what by a sinister judgement, may suspect. Thus there were not wanting those who most vainly imputed to me the Emperor's so-long absence at Pilsen, and did not fear to spread it not only at Prague but in various places of Bohemia — though it never came into my mind before I heard it reported; and scarcely any man longed for His Majesty's presence more than I, since, while he was away, my affairs were the less advanced, however many had charge of executing them. Nor do I know anyone here who labours under hatred of me; for I have given no one occasion — rather, very many, indeed all the chief men, not only at court but elsewhere in this kingdom, of their own accord desire my friendship and conversation, and court it more than I could wish, so that I am forced to spend time and health oftener than I would. Nay, the more illustrious Magnates of the kingdom have offered me, of their own motion and unasked, that I be enrolled a native of the kingdom and turn nowhere else — which they have promised, with ready mind, to bring about at the first Diet. If among the common sort there lurk some peevish, envious, and malevolent fellows — of whom I know none — let them ensnare and gnaw themselves in their own malevolence and perversity of judgement, and keep these things to themselves and guard them in the grave: <b>unhappy and wretched is he whom no one envies; nor does that virtue shine or stand conspicuous which of its own [worth] lacks the shadow of envy.</b> You see therefore, my Rollenhagen, with how great injury they do not blush to affect not only my person but — far more — the Sacred Imperial Majesty itself, Head of the Empire and our most clement Lord, who is of present courage, neither dreads the Turks, but spurns Peace, unless honourable and useful, even when freely offered — while they write things so flimsily-patched and most alien from the truth. Were we together, I could show irrefragably, by many documents and proofs [<i>Tecmiria</i>], the empty talk of those men, and that the contrary is rather true. Meanwhile I should secretly wish to learn from you who it was that scattered such things in his letters, and to whom — not that I dwell on such trifles, or would cast it in his teeth (for many things must be dissembled and looked down on from on high), but only that I may guard against a man suspecting things so sinister and frivolous and malevolent, if perchance I know him or fall in with him. That you indicate the same to me in a few or even veiled words, as soon as you write again, I hold you asked, by our friendship. It shall be done without any danger to you; for I know that in such malicious cavillings one must act prudently and silently. Farewell. Given as above in the letter.  /  I add this too: that what you intimated in your earlier letter — of a persecution of those who profess the Evangelical doctrine in Austria, [done] by the Jesuits with the Emperor's consent — has been falsely reported to you. For all things in Austria, as to religion, are in their former state, nor does the Emperor permit the Jesuits or others who call themselves Catholics to stir up disturbances, nor hate or ill-treat anyone for diversity of religion, since he even tolerates many in his own Court who dissent from the Papists. But in Styria a great affliction of the Evangelicals has arisen, the Jesuits, by the Prince's authority, bearing with no one there who does not agree with them — which, however, happens against the Emperor's will and counsel. There are those who presage that the oncoming Turk will break off those controversies and persecutions; for, Kanizsa being taken (which God avert!), no fortified bulwark remains before he could invade Styria. But of these things He will provide, in whose hand all things are.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b>, the Magdeburg rector and Tycho's transport-agent there (cf. brahe-t_16000405001).</li><li><b>Significance — the Magdeburg goods arrived, the Prague-observatory search, and the legal endgame of the Ursus affair.</b></li><li><b>The goods arrive.</b> Tycho's instruments and effects, long stuck at <b>Magdeburg</b>, have reached <b>Leitmeritz (Litoměřice)</b> at the <b>cathedral Chapter's expense</b> (cf. the whole Magdeburg-transport thread, brahe-t_16000405001, brahe-t_16000000002); thanks to the Chapter and Rollenhagen.</li><li><b>The search for a permanent observatory (key topographical document).</b> The Emperor, having seen the <b>six larger + some smaller instruments</b> installed in the <b>garden house \"built by Ferdinand the grandfather\"</b> (the Royal Summer Palace / <b>Belvedere</b>, Ferdinand I), promised a better place than Benátky — inclining toward <b>the Curtius house</b> (to be bought from the widow; the eventual choice — cf. brahe-t_16010414001), but also floating the <b>Star Villa (Hvězda)</b> \"built by Archduke Ferdinand in the form of a star, with a game-park\" (Ferdinand II of Tyrol's villa), and even the <b>Vienna <i>Neugebäude</b></i> built by <b>Maximilian II</b>. Tycho's one condition: <b>permanence, \"lest the same happen as in Denmark.\"</b></li><li><b>Sophie Brahe &amp; Erik Lange.</b> His <b>sister Sophie at Segeberg near Lübeck</b>, <b>Erik Lange</b> (her alchemist husband) of unknown whereabouts — \"I grieve the fortune of both.\" His <b>iron furnaces</b> (alchemical) to be shipped via Petkum/Magdeburg before the Elbe freezes.</li><li><b>The servant Andreas.</b> A candid character-sketch: Andreas (the man sent to fetch the goods) had absconded at Benátky, returned penniless, was passed to the Captain of Brandeis, and falsely claims unpaid wages; \"most simple, not prudent, blurts anything\" — forgiven for Rollenhagen's sake.</li><li><b>The Ursus legal endgame (the centerpiece).</b> <b>Ursus (Nicolaus Reimers Bär) died six weeks earlier</b> (≈ mid-August 1600), \"consumed in the French camps … or by asthma and phthisis\" — <i>\"at the very hour the citation was to be served.\"</i> Tycho recounts the <b>deathbed visit by two lawyers and a notary</b> asking Ursus to recant (he refused, submitting to the judges); the <b>four imperial Commissioners</b> (<b>Barons Christopher von Schleinitz and Ernfried von Minckwitz</b> + two juristic councillors); the <b>action suspended</b> by Ursus's death, awaiting a new decree; his proctor <b>Johannes Fritzsch</b> at Bautzen. Crucially, the suit is <b>\"real, not merely personal\"</b> — against the <i>book</i>, which \"perpetually speaks in place of its author\": the plan is a published refutation + an imperial decree <b>annulling the book throughout the Empire</b>, and the <b>Archbishop of Prague ordered to punish the printer and burn all Prague copies (\"given to Vulcan\")</b>. Had Ursus lived, the Commissioners would have had him <b>beheaded or quartered</b> under Bohemian law. Tycho's stated aim: not the man but <b>abolishing the book and vindicating his (and Rothmann's) reputation</b> — he will <b>clear Rothmann</b> of Ursus's slanders, and asks Rollenhagen for <b>samples of Rothmann's hand</b> to authenticate the letters. (Cf. the Ursus thread: brahe-t_16000911001 [Kepler's analysis], brahe-t_15990922001 [Ursus's flight], brahe-t_16000401001, brahe-t_16000408001.)</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>Ursus-affair resolution</b> keystone (pair with brahe-t_16000911001); the <b>Prague-observatory</b> thread (Curtius house / Star Villa / Neugebäude → brahe-t_16010414001, brahe-t_16010415001); the <b>Magdeburg-transport</b> thread; and <b>Sophie Brahe / Erik Lange / Rothmann</b>.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Georg Rollenhagen; the <b>Magdeburg Chapter/Dean</b>; <b>Andreas</b> (servant); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> &amp; <b>Erik Lange</b>; <b>Simon von Petkum</b>; <b>Ferdinand I</b> (\"the grandfather,\" the garden house), <b>Archduke Ferdinand [II of Tyrol]</b> (the Star Villa), <b>Maximilian II</b> (the Vienna <i>Neugebäude</i>); the <b>Curtius widow</b>; <b>Caspar Alemann</b> (Magdeburg consul); <b>Ursus</b>; <b>Barons Christopher von Schleinitz &amp; Ernfried von Minckwitz</b>; <b>Johannes Fritzsch</b> (proctor); the <b>Archbishop of Prague</b>; <b>Christoph Rothmann</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Leitmeritz</b>, <b>Magdeburg</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Segeberg/Lübeck</b>, <b>Vienna</b>, <b>Bautzen/Lusatia</b>, the <b>Star Villa</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>die 26 Septembris <b>stylo novo</b> Anno 1600</i> → <b>26 September 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Three closing postscripts (POSTSCRIPTA — a spagyric anti-stone recipe for the Magdeburg Dean; POSTSCRIPTA ALTERA — rebuttal of the court-rumour that Tycho authored Turkish-war prophecies, with Barvitius's testimony + the Bohemian-citizenship offer; and an \"Addo et hoc\" on the Styrian persecution of Evangelicals + the Kanizsa/Turk threat), restored from Dreyer VIII pp.372–375; dropped by the working transcription. (One OCR-doubtful word marked [tanti?].)</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Six Latin transcription errors corrected against Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Opera Omnia</i> VIII, pp. 372-375: 'Si [tanti?] essemus' -&gt; 'Si simul essemus' (p.374); 'animi delectione' -&gt; 'animi deiectione' (p.373); 'Semin. Peniculi' -&gt; 'Semin. Feniculi' (p.372); 'Cynosbaton Germ. beeren' -&gt; 'Cynosbaton Germ. hainbutten beeren' (p.372); 'quae istinc' -&gt; 'quae isthic' (p.372); 'Euangeliorum afflictio' -&gt; 'Euangelicorum afflictio' (p.375). Three of these (simul/deiectione/hainbutten) also required correcting the English translation, which had carried the corrupted readings forward as 'of such account?', 'delusion', and the unconfirmed gloss '[hips]' respectively.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Magdeburg-goods arrival</b> via the Chapter; the <b>observatory search</b> — the Belvedere garden house, the <b>Curtius house</b>, the <b>Star Villa (Hvězda)</b>, the Vienna <b>Neugebäude</b> — with Tycho's permanence condition; <b>Sophie Brahe/Erik Lange</b> and the iron furnaces; the <b>Andreas</b> character-sketch; <b>Caspar Alemann</b>; and above all the <b>Ursus legal endgame</b> — his death \"at the hour of the citation,\" the deathbed recantation-attempt, the four Commissioners, the real-vs-personal action against the book, the <b>imperial annulment + Archbishop's book-burning order</b>, the would-be beheading/quartering, and the <b>vindication of Tycho and Rothmann</b>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. The Habsburg-Ferdinand attributions of the buildings are identified (Belvedere / Hvězda / Neugebäude) and the rare residences flagged as such; no textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (salutation through 'Vale', plus both Postscripta) against Dreyer TBOO VIII, read as page images in two chunks (pages 1-4, 5-8 of the slice, i.e. printed pp.368-375; p.368 is the tail of a different, preceding letter and p.376 begins the next letter, so nothing on those two pages belongs to this item). Checked every proper name (Rollenhagen, Andreas, Sophia, Erik Lange, Simon von Petkum, the two Ferdinands, Maximilian, Caspar Alemann, Ursus, Christoph von Schleunitz, Ernfried von Minckwitz, Johannes Fritzsch, Rothmann, Barvitius), place-name (Magdeburg, Leitmeritz, Benatky, Prague, Segeberg, Lubeck, Hamburg, Vienna, Bautzen/Budissinum, Lusatia, Styria, Austria, Kanizsa), date (26 September 1600 twice; 'six weeks'; 'eight days'), and recipe quantity (oz. I / oz. II groupings) against the page images. The main letter body (through the first 'Vale') and the second Postscript (Postscripta Altera, on the Turkish-war rumour and Styria) translate accurately with no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found. The first Postscript (the medical recipe) is where the source_text itself is marked as 'restored... dropped by the working transcription' — and that restoration turns out to be imperfect: six small but genuine departures from Dreyer's print surfaced there, one of them (the bracketed '[tanti?]' guess for Dreyer's clear 'simul') materially changing the sense of a sentence. Per the grading rule, any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C regardless of the translation's own accuracy.</p><p><b>6 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (<b>high</b>). The source_text's own bracketed guess '[tanti?]' for an unread word is wrong: Dreyer clearly prints 'simul' (together), not a word meaning 'of such worth/account' — the translation faithfully carries the guess forward, but the guess itself materially changes the sense of the sentence. Dreyer prints: <i>Si fimul effemus, poßem multis documentis et Tecmirijs irrefragabiliter oftendere iftorum hominum vaniloquentiam (p.374) — i.e. 'Si simul essemus' = 'If we were together'</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). Dreyer prints 'deiectione' (dejection of mind); source_text corrupts this to 'delectione' (not a standard word), and the translation renders the corrupted form as 'delusion', a concept matching neither the correct reading (dejection) nor the corrupted one. Dreyer prints: <i>quæ a melancholicis curis, animi de-iectione, fufpicionibus et fuperftitionibus otiofis (p.374) — 'deiectione' = dejection/despondency</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's recipe reads 'Feniculi' (fennel seed); source_text corrupts the initial letter to 'Peniculi' (not a plausible ingredient). The English translation is unaffected — it correctly says 'fennel' regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>Rcp. Semin. Feniculi / Petrofelini / Anifi / Saxifragiae / Cardamomi (p.372)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's German gloss is 'hainbutten beeren' (rosehip berries); source_text silently drops 'hainbutten', leaving only the generic 'beeren' (berries), which the translation then has to gloss speculatively as '[hips]'. Dreyer prints: <i>Cynofbator. Germ. hainbutten beeren (p.372)</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer prints 'isthic' (there); source_text has the near-homograph 'istinc' (thence/from there) — a minor place-adverb substitution that does not change the English rendering ('there'). Dreyer prints: <i>quæ ifthic non difficulter confieri poterit (p.372) — 'isthic' = 'there, in that place'</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's 'Euangelicorum' (of the Evangelical people) is corrupted in source_text to 'Euangeliorum' (of the Gospels); the translation is unaffected — it correctly says 'the Evangelicals' regardless of the corrupted source. Dreyer prints: <i>magna fuborta eft Euangelicorum afflictio (p.375) — 'of the Evangelicals', not 'of the Gospels'</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> All six defects were verified directly against the Dreyer page images (TBOO VIII, pp. 372-375, supplied as the letter's slice PDF) before any change was made; each corrected reading matches what Dreyer prints exactly. One page citation in the audit was off by one page (the 'delectione/deiectione' defect: audit cited p.374, the word actually falls at the bottom of p.373) -- this is noted in that outcome's dreyer_page field but did not affect the substance of the fix. No cruxes remain; nothing outside the six listed defect sites was touched.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000619001", "next": "brahe-t_16010418001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000619001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010418001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports his goods arrived in Bohemia and asks Rollenhagen to deliver the enclosed letter to the Dean, explaining that the earlier silence was due to pressing imperial duties.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16000926003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001008001", "d": "1600-10-08", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Melchior Jöstel — Prague, 8 October 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MELCHIOREM IOeSTELIUM.</p><p>EX ultimis meis literis, Eruditissime et amantissime Domine IÖSTELI, quas Domino Doctori FRANTZIO Iurisconsulto in eas oras proficiscenti commisi, facile intellexisti, quae superioribus tuis respondenda habui, et quaenam deinceps a te desiderarem, quod peracta promotione, quae ut fausta felixque sit precor, meliori restitutus otio efficias, te etiamnum per amicitiam nostram rogatum volo. Invenies facile aliquem, qui id, quicquid erit, meis sumptibus ad mundum describat. Promptissimum vero erit, si hoc per studiosum, quem mihi polliceris (quod et gratum est) huc ipsum miseris, modo is non prius, quod tamen mallem, accesserit.</p><p>Quid M. OSWALDUS et Typographus certo addicere velint, etiam mature resciscere cupio, atque ut omnia pro me isthic peragenda accelerentur, ne irrecuperabile tempus nimis diu incassum labatur. Sum iam occupatus in disponendis instrumentis meis superius iuxta arcem in domo quadam ampla, in quam Caesar ex horto suo, cui propinqua est, solitarie, quoties lubuerit, ingredi potest et ea perlustrare, quod et nuper clementissime nec sine voluptate fecit, licet saltem ea, quae Benatica advecta sunt, tunc in promptu fuerint, caetera omnia, de quibus etiam pridie inquisivit, ubi rite collocata fuerint, visurus, atque tum demum, quo in loco, ut ad usum perpetuentur, constituenda sint, decreturus. Neque enim vult, ut ego cum meis studijs tam longe, ut antea, Pragâ absim, quod etiam mihi ipsi non oportunum videtur. Verum de huius consilij eventu, quem spero utilem et commodum futurum, te postmodum certiorem reddam.</p><p>Advenit ante quatriduum per postam, uti vocant, Caesaris frater Archidux MATTHIAS ab ipsius Maiestate illico vocatus, statim ac magna illa et inopinata mutatio hîc in aula facta est, reiecto RUMPFIO et TRAUTSONIO, praecipuis Consilij secreti Capitibus, qui intra biduum discedere iussi sunt, quod et factum, caeteris eiusdem Consilij membris retentis quidem, sed necdum in priori gratia habitis. Qua de caussa haec alteratio peracta, nondum satis liquet. Fuit quoque dictus serenissimus Archidux heri in domo illa, ubi instrumenta mea partim erecta sunt, partim adhuc eriguntur, et singula, quae interim maiori copia advecta erant, diligenter perlustravit, siquidem et is artibus his iucunde et laudabiliter afficiatur.</p><p>Vale, et cum otium fuerit, plenius rescribe. Atque ubi sexcenti illi Ioachimici e ducatu Megapolitano advenerint, 50 inde pro laboribus typographicis tecum retine, et papyrum idoneam pro aliqua eorum parte tempestive coëmi effice. Plures mittam, ubi opus fuerit. Reliquos intermediante affine tuo Dresdense huc dirige. Iterumque vale, et D. D. IESSENIUM, licet mei immemorem, reliquosque amicos Witebergenses, inter quos etiam obmutescentem illum TAUBMANNUM, saluta.</p><p>Dabantur festinanter Praga die 8 Oct. stylo novo Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MELCHIOR JÖSTEL.</b></p><p>From my last letter, most learned and most loving Lord Jöstel — which I entrusted to Lord Doctor Franz, the jurisconsult, as he was setting out for those parts — you will easily have understood what I had to answer to your earlier ones, and what I should henceforth desire of you; which (the promotion being accomplished — that it be auspicious and happy, I pray — and you restored to better leisure) I wish you to bring about, asking you still, by our friendship. You will easily find someone who, at my expense, will copy out fair whatever it shall be. It will be readiest if you send it hither by the student you promise me (which is welcome too), provided he has not come earlier — which, however, I should prefer.</p><p>What Master Oswald and the Printer will definitely undertake [to deliver], I also wish to learn in good time, and that everything to be done for me there be hastened, lest irrecoverable time slip too long away in vain. I am now busy arranging my instruments up above, near the castle, in a certain ample house, into which the Emperor can come alone from his garden — to which it is near — as often as he pleases, and survey them; which he lately did, most graciously and not without pleasure, although only those brought from Benátky were then at hand. He is to see all the rest (about which he also inquired the day before) when they have been duly placed, and then at last to decide in what place they are to be set up, that they may be kept in permanent use. For he does not wish me, with my studies, to be as far from Prague as before — which to me too seems inconvenient. But of the outcome of this plan, which I hope will prove useful and convenient, I will inform you hereafter.</p><p>Four days ago there arrived, by post (as they call it), the Emperor's brother, Archduke Matthias, summoned at once by His Majesty — just as that great and unexpected change was made here at court, Rumpf and Trautson, the chief heads of the Privy Council, having been cast out, who were ordered to depart within two days, which was done; the other members of the same Council being indeed retained, but not yet held in their former favour. For what cause this alteration was carried through is not yet sufficiently clear. The said most serene Archduke was also, yesterday, in that house where my instruments are partly set up and partly still being set up, and diligently surveyed each one — those which in the meantime had been brought in greater number — since he too is pleasantly and laudably taken with these arts.</p><p>Farewell, and when you have leisure, write back more fully. And when those six hundred joachimsthalers from the duchy of Mecklenburg arrive, keep 50 of them with you for the printing labours, and see that suitable paper is bought in good time for some part of them. I will send more when need be. The rest, direct hither through your kinsman at Dresden. And again, farewell; and greet Doctor Jessenius — though forgetful of me — and the rest of my Wittenberg friends, among them also that silent Taubmann.</p><p>Given in haste at Prague, the 8th day of October, New Style, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (1559–1611), Wittenberg professor of mathematics and medicine, Tycho's friend — at this moment \"the chief candidate among six\" for the medical doctorate at Wittenberg (<i>peracta promotione</i>). This is the <b>personal companion</b> to Tycho's same-week letter declining the doctoral ceremony, addressed to the Wittenberg Medical College (brahe-t_16001009001, 9 October 1600); the two share the instrument-installation report.</li><li><b>The instruments near Prague Castle (material history).</b> Tycho is installing his instruments \"up above, near the castle, in an ample house\" so placed that <b>Rudolf II can walk over alone from his garden</b> to view them whenever he wishes — which he had lately done \"not without pleasure,\" though only the <b>Benátky-brought</b> instruments were then in place; the rest (newly arrived in greater number) were still being erected. The Emperor wants Tycho no longer to reside far from Prague. This dovetails with brahe-t_16001009001 (transport from Benátky and Magdeburg via Leitmeritz/Litoměřice up the Elbe) and the Hamburg-transit letter (brahe-t_15990929001).</li><li><b>The court purge of autumn 1600.</b> \"That great and unexpected change at court\": <b>Wolfgang Rumpf</b> (von Wielross, Rudolf II's chief steward) and <b>Paul Sixt Trautson</b> — <i>praecipui Consilii secreti Capita</i>, the chief heads of the <b>Privy Council</b> — were abruptly dismissed and ordered to leave within two days; the remaining councillors kept their seats but not their former favour. The Emperor summoned his brother <b>Archduke Matthias</b> by post; Matthias inspected Tycho's instruments the day before this letter, \"taken with these arts.\" Tycho notes the cause of the upheaval \"is not yet sufficiently clear.\" (Rumpf was Tycho's earlier court patron — cf. brahe-t_16000813001, where Tycho sends Rumpf his respects.)</li><li><b>The printing project &amp; Mecklenburg money.</b> Tycho funds Wittenberg printing from the <b>Mecklenburg payments</b>: when the <b>600 joachimsthalers from the duchy of Mecklenburg</b> arrive, Jöstel is to keep <b>50 for the typographic labours</b> and buy paper in advance; the rest to come via Jöstel's kinsman at <b>Dresden</b>. (Same debt-stream as the 10,000-thaler thread, here a tranche put to press-work.) <i>M. Oswaldus</i> = <b>Master Oswald</b>, evidently a Wittenberg agent coordinating with <i>the Printer</i>; not further identified (flagged).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Doctor Frantzius Iurisconsultus</i> = <b>Doctor Franz, a jurist</b>, used as courier to \"those parts\" (Wittenberg/Saxony). <b>Identity flag:</b> in brahe-t_16000327002 the recipient \"Wolfgang Franz\" was identified as the Wittenberg <i>theologian</i>; this courier is styled a <i>jurisconsultus</i>. Whether they are the same man (then the earlier \"theologian\" needs correction) or two different Franzes is unresolved.</li><li><i>D. D. Jessenius</i> = <b>Johannes Jessenius</b> (the Wittenberg anatomist), \"forgetful of me\" — echoing brahe-t_16000327002, where Jessenius had not visited; <i>Taubmannus</i> = <b>Friedrich Taubmann</b>, the Wittenberg poet (cf. brahe-t_16000305001), \"that silent one\" (no longer writing).</li><li><i>Archidux Matthias</i> = <b>Archduke Matthias</b> (later Emperor); <i>Caesar</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 8 Oct. stylo novo</i> — <b>New Style</b>, Prague/imperial. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the Latin: \"serenificus\" restored to Dreyer's \"serenissimus\" (TBOO VIII p.381).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent, fully legible Latin; the substance (instruments installed near Prague Castle for the Emperor's private viewing; the autumn-1600 dismissal of Rumpf and Trautson and the summoning of Archduke Matthias; the Mecklenburg-funded Wittenberg printing; greetings to Jessenius and Taubmann) is clear and corpus-corroborated. Two open identifications are flagged, not smoothed: <i>Master Oswald</i> (the printing agent) and <i>Doctor Franz the jurist</i> (vs. the theologian of brahe-t_16000327002).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. The tboo slice nominally spans TBOO VIII pp.374-383 (10 pages), but this range holds several unrelated letters (Tycho-Rollenhagius, Sturcius, Bacmeisterus x2, Braschius) either side of the target; the actual Tycho-to-Joestel letter runs only p.381 ('TYCHO BRAHE AD MELCHIOREM IOeSTELIUM.') through the top of p.382 ('...Anno 1600.'), which is what was collated word for word (pp.374-380 and 383 were read to locate the letter and confirmed as different correspondence, not needed for collation). Matches source_text exactly except the one corrupted word noted above. Names/sums checked and correct: Frantzius, M. Oswaldus, Archidux Matthias, Rumpfius, Trautsonius, sexcenti (600) Ioachimici e ducatu Megapolitano, 50 retained for printing, affinis Dresdensis, Iessenius, Taubmannus; date 8 Oct. stylo novo 1600. Single low-severity TEXT_ERROR forces grade C per the letter of the rule, but verdict is MINOR since it changed nothing a reader of the English would take away.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads the nonsense word 'serenificus' where Dreyer's page clearly prints 'serenissimus' (most serene), the standard honorific for an archduke used elsewhere in the letter. The English translator supplied the correct sense regardless, so the published translation is unaffected, but source_text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>Fuit quoque dictus ferenißimus Archidux heri in domo illa (TBOO VIII p.381)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.374-383; the relevant sentence is on p.381, line 35, clearly reading \"fereniβimus Archidux\" (serenissimus). Only the Latin word required correction; the published English translation already carried the correct sense and needed no change. No other defects found across the ten pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Melchior Jöstelius", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000918001", "next": "brahe-t_16001118001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Urges completion of the promised collation, asks what Master Oswald and the printer will undertake, and reports arranging instruments in a new house the Emperor can visit.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001008001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001009001", "d": "1600-10-09", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "The dean and doctors at the college of medicine i Wittenberg", "ck": "The dean and doctors at the college of medicine i Wittenberg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → the Dean and Doctors of the Medical College at Wittenberg — Prague, 9 October 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SPECTATISSIME DOMINE DECANE AC SENIOR CAeTERIQUE CLARISSIMI DOCTORES COLLEGIJ MEDICI.</p><p>QUOD tam honorifice et benevole ad promotionem Doctoratus in facultate medica circa medium Octobris stylo veteri apud vos solenniter celebrandam (memores adhuc illius convictus, qui inter nos fuit, dum superiore anno aliquantisper Witebergae subsisterem) vestris humanissimis literis me invitatis, est cur vobis omnibus et singulis officiose gratias agam. Satisfacerem vero haud illubenter vestrae petitioni, si per me id staret, et temporis locorumque ratio, quibus inserviendum, id ferret. Sum enim ex Sacrae Caesae Matis, Domini nostri clementissimi, mandato iam occupatus in disponendis instrumentis meis Astronomicis, quae partim ex Arce Benatica, partim Magdeburgo Leutmeritzium, quousque Albis est navigabilis, deinde huc Pragam, eiusdem Maiestatis iussu, nuperrime advecta sunt, ut in ampla quadam et magnifice superius prope arcem exstructa domo ijs collocatis, ipsius Maiestati (sic enim ei clementissime placuit) omnia propius in conspectu sint, donec quorsum ulterius transferenda, et ubi commode ad usum stabilienda veniant, benignissime ordinaverit. Quae cum non ita cito expediri queant (res enim non parvae est molis) ut vobis ad constitutum tempus gratificer, spero tam apud vos, quam Eruditissimum Magistrum IÖSTELIUM, Candidatum inter sex illos primarium, amicum mihi in primis dilectum, excusationi locum futurum. Quod superest, ut actus ille auspicatus et salutaris sit, precor, vosque omnes Deo Opt. Max. commendatos habeo.</p><p>Dabantur Praga die 9 Octobris stylo reformato Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Most esteemed Lord Dean and Senior, and the rest of you most distinguished Doctors of the Medical College.</b></p><p>That you so honourably and benevolently invite me, by your most kind letter — still mindful of that fellowship which was between us, while last year I stayed for a while at Wittenberg — to the promotion of the Doctorate in the medical faculty, to be solemnly celebrated among you about the middle of October, old style: there is reason for me to give dutiful thanks to you, all and each. I would indeed not unwillingly satisfy your request, if it stood with me, and if the reckoning of time and of places, which must be served, allowed it. For I am now occupied, by the mandate of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, in arranging my astronomical instruments — which, partly from the castle of Benátky, partly from Magdeburg, have very recently been conveyed to Leitmeritz [Litoměřice] (as far as the Elbe is navigable), and then hither to Prague, by order of the same Majesty — so that, these being placed in a certain ample house, magnificently built up above [the city], near the castle, everything may be more closely in His Majesty's sight (for so it has most clemently pleased him), until he has most kindly ordained whither they are to be transferred further, and where they may conveniently be established for use. Since these things cannot be despatched so quickly — for the matter is of no small bulk — that I may gratify you at the appointed time, I hope there will be place for [my] excuse, both with you and with the most learned Master Jöstel, the chief candidate among those six, a friend especially dear to me. As for what remains, I pray that that act [ceremony] may be auspicious and salutary, and I hold you all commended to God Best and Greatest.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 9th of October, reformed style, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipients.</b> The <b>Dean, Senior, and Doctors of the Medical Faculty (College) at Wittenberg</b>, who had invited Tycho — himself a master of Paracelsian medicine and a Wittenberg guest the previous winter — to a doctoral promotion. Tycho declines, with thanks.</li><li><b>The instruments at Prague — a key logistical datum.</b> Tycho explains he is \"by the Emperor's mandate\" busy <b>setting up his astronomical instruments</b>, \"<b>partly from the castle of Benátky, partly from Magdeburg</b>,\" conveyed up the <b>Elbe to Leitmeritz (Litoměřice)</b> \"as far as the Elbe is navigable,\" and thence to <b>Prague</b> — to be installed \"in a certain ample house, magnificently built up above [the city], near the [Prague] castle,\" so that everything be \"more closely in the Emperor's sight.\" This dates the <b>arrival and Prague installation of the instrumentarium to autumn 1600</b>, and is the downstream end of the Elbe transport organised by Rollenhagen at Magdeburg (brahe-t_16000619001). (The \"house near the castle\" is the curia/Belvedere quarter by Prague Castle, Tycho's interim observatory before the move to the Curtius house / Benátky question was settled.)</li><li><b><i>Magister Iöstelius</b></i> = <b>Melchior Jöstel</b> (1559–1611), Wittenberg professor of mathematics and medicine, \"the chief candidate among those six [doctoral candidates],\" and Tycho's friend (cf. brahe-t_16000806001).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> The letter is dated <i>Praga die 9 Octobris stylo reformato 1600</i> = <b>9 October 1600, reformed [New] style</b> (Prague, imperial); the Wittenberg ceremony it declines is set \"about the middle of October, <b>old style</b>\" — a neat in-text illustration of the O.S./N.S. gap between Lutheran Saxony and imperial Bohemia. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely, and the content (declining the Wittenberg medical promotion; the autumn-1600 transport of the instruments from Benátky and Magdeburg via Leitmeritz to a house near Prague Castle for the Emperor's viewing; Jöstel as chief candidate) is clear, important, and corpus-corroborated. The O.S./N.S. contrast in the text is preserved, not collapsed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.381-383 page images. Located the letter beginning mid-p.382 ('SPECTATISSIME DOMINE DECANE...') after the close of a letter to Melchior Jöstel, running through to the date. Collated place-names (Arce Benatica/Benátky, Magdeburgo/Magdeburg, Leutmeritzium/Leitmeritz, Albis/the Elbe, Pragam/Prague), the name Eruditissimum Magistrum Iöstelium/Jöstel, and both calendar notations (stylo veteri = old style for the Wittenberg date, stylo reformato = reformed style for the letter's own date) against the page image; all preserved correctly. No fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Declines the invitation to the medical doctoral promotion at Wittenberg, citing his obligations to install his astronomical instruments in a new house at Prague by imperial command.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001009001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001017001", "d": "1600-10-17", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Kepler", "ck": "Johannes Kepler", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Johannes Kepler → Tycho Brahe — [Prague], 17 October 1600", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> <b>Johannes Kepler</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. Place not given in the source (<i>s.l.</i>); written from <b>Prague</b> (Kepler had just arrived there). The foundational negotiation letter of the Tycho–Kepler collaboration.</p>", "la": "<p>IOANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>CUM hucusque, Nobilissime et Praestantissime Domine TYCHO, partim occupationes tuae, partim febris mea non passae fuerint, ut rationes adventus mei in Bohemiam Magnificentiae Tuae ex animi mei sententia recenserem, scripto id rectissime praestitum iri putavi. Duobus potissimum argumentis Magnificentia Tua, in literis ad me in Styriam missis, in Bohemiam me vocavit, altero a contractu nostro, quem Maio mense et antea talem inivimus, ut pollicerer, interventu Caesareae voluntatis, Magnificentiae Tuae per biennium adesse, laboresque tuos in Astronomia toti Europae iam pridem desideratissimos, quantum in me est, maturare et adiuvare: altero argumento a praesenti mea calamitate deducto, dum me iubes optima spe ad te proficisci, nec dubitare, quin vel commendationibus tuis ad Maiestatem Caesaream, vel propria opera auxilioque meis incommodis sis consulturus, adeoque et viaticum compensaturus. Recte sane de duabus hisce rebus egisti. Nam mihi cum necessitas imponeretur e Styria migrandi, omnino de utraque hac cogitandum fuit. Etsi enim, quod primum attinet, omnis noster contractus expiravit et annihilatus est, sublato fundamento, ut retinerem salarium meum Styriacum (quod interdictu Provinciae mihi ademtum est) utque magistratus meus eius temporis consentiret (cuius loco, cessante officio, iam agnosco Principem Wirtembergicum, vigore cum obligationis, tum literarum Reversalium) tamen cum, durante meo priore officio, res a Magnificentia Tua incepta et Caesareae Maiestati proposita sit, Caesarea Maiestas etiam consenserit, providendum certe mihi fuit, ne imprimis Caesaream Maiestatem, tum etiam Magnificentiam Tuam ludificarer, quin potius vel cum damno meo Pragam irem, ibique rem vel penitus confectam (quam spem literae tuae in adventum meum praebuerunt) quantum in me esset, amplecterer, quantum vero in Principe meo situm, per Legatum eius Dominum Doctorem DOLDIUM, qui Pragae esset, expedirem; vel si adhuc praeter opinionem tuam, in dubio suspensa futura esset, uti tum vires meas explorarem, sumptuum rationem inirem, et quantisper sine pernicie mea, incertum in eventum, meis impensis, nullo salario, expectare possim, perpenderem, ubi, si tantae morae, quantas postulat aula Imperatoria, supra facultates meas essent, negotium ipse praesens legitime et cum Magnificentiae Tuae meique ipsius excusatione penitus desererem.</p><p>Quod ad alterum, etsi mihi dubium nunquam neque fuit, neque est, quin et impense me ames, et optime promotum velis, et huius rei luculentissimas in loco tam splendido, tanta summi Monarchae gratia, habeas occasiones, si moram ego ferre possem: tamen rationes, quibus praesens meum infortunium ope divina sublevem, aliae mihi sunt, partim necessariae, partim (ut sunt res meae tenues) magis utiles. Cum enim iure obligatorio tenear, operam meam priore Magistratu, cui illam ex concessu Principis mei Nutricij addixeram, liberatam, eidem Nutricio meo renunciare, rectissime sane mihi consuluissem, si recta via Grätio ivissem in Wirtembergiam. Certum enim habeo, qui eo veniunt exules, maxime si alumni fuerunt Principis, illis et de victu statim et de conditionibus primo quoque tempore prospici. Ego vero praeter haec omnia etiam a Praeceptoribus meis in Academia Tubingensi iam pridem eas habeo promissiones, ut merito ex coniunctione Principis cum Academia magna mihi spes nasci debeat. Atque haec via esset omnium compendiosissima ad mea mitiganda incommoda. Altera est, ut si me causae forsan aliae (qualis est nostra iam tractatio) Pragam deducerent, uti tum in ea urbe, nempe in conventu Legatorum ex plerisque provincijs, statim mihi prospicerem, primo omnium Legatum Wirtembergicum alloquerer, an commode, parvo sumptu, commendationibus ipsius adiutus, in Wirtembergiam ire possim, an concessionem mihi ab illo Principe impetraret, commendationibus iterum me iuvaret, ut propter redimendum viaticum in propinquiorem Academiam, qualis est Witeberga, Iena, Lipsia etc. promoverer. Haec altera via est, Nobilissime Domine TYCHO, sublevandis meis miserijs, minus quidem splendidas conditiones pollicens, quam ex aula sperantur Imperatoria, sed tamen expeditiores, in praesentia certiores, et mihi, qui ob inopiam non sum sustinendis diuturnis moris, acceptiores.</p><p>Explicavi, quid de utroque tuo argumento sentiam: iam quo animo huc venerim, hîcque iam commorer, recensebo. Voluisse me, statim atque in exilium ire sum iussus, Wirtembergiam repetere, iam pridem ex literis meis tenes. Supervenerunt literae tuae, quae et mihi et affinitati amicisque meis satis efficaces sunt visae, quae me permoverent, ut dimisso in Magnificentiae Tuae et Astronomiae gratiam, inque meum ipsius, si Deus ita vellet, maius incrementum, altero mei iuvandi modo, retento vero alterius arbitrio pro Wirtembergico Bohemicum iter arriperem. Bona vero et uxorem cum privigna visum est in omnem eventum Lincij relinquere, ubi essent in itinere versus Wirtembergiam.</p><p>Mutavi propositum de uxore et privigna (bona enim, sicut dixi, Lincij reliqui) hoc metu, qui iam in me exit, ne quis ex nobis aegrotaret, reliquis absentibus, inter peregrinos. Itaque Pragam veni magno sumptu. Sexaginta expendi in vecturam personarum, viginti in viaticum, quadraginta amplius in duo vasa, repleta bonis mobilibus sive suppellectili, idque tantummodo Lincium usque. Taceo quanto iam in aere sim hospitis mei. Restant mihi impensae non plures, quam quibus ad moram quatuor hebdomadum, et deinde ad iter aliud (breve tamen) indigeo. Intra has quatuor hebdomadas expectabo eventum nostri negocij. At nihilominus, dummodo per valetudinem liceat, in omnem eventum mihi apud Legatos Principum de commoda conditione legitime circumspiciam, sic tamen, ut si quid interea Magnificentia Tua effecerit, id ratum, caetera irrita sint. Ubi hoc tempus transierit, atque Magnificentia Tua postulaverit a me, ut diutius maneam, id non aliter mihi possibile est, nisi aut viaticum mihi a Magnificentia Tua pro vectura et victu restituatur 150 fl., ut duo vasa mea huc advehuntur, ne quae convasata sunt, situ pereant, aut Magnificentia Tua praedem se praestet pro me apud illos, a quibus necessaria mihi ad victum coëmenda sunt. Quorum illud si praestetur, ad semestre usque, DEO vitam prorogante, expectare potero: sin hoc autem, tantisper durare potest mora mea, dum id Magnificentiae Tuae et Creditoribus allubescat. Interea Astronomia et labores Magnificentiae Tuae tantae mihi curae erunt, quantam feret valetudo mea. Quin etiam si negocium ex animi sententia apud Caesaream Maiestatem conficias, viaticumque, uti spem fecisti, impetres, ab eo summam eam, quae interea mihi a Magnificentia Tua commodata fuerit, demptam Magnificentiae Tuae restituam. Quin etiam id intra has 4 septimanas operam dabo, ut aliquis alius mihi id salarium, quod Grätij habui, in hoc biennium pendat, pro quo obligatus illi sim, exacto tempore in eius servitium transire. Habet igitur Magnificentia Tua summam consiliorum meorum: quae ut mihi inopi et deserto et exuli non in peiorem partem interpretetur, obnixe rogo.</p><p>Vale.<br>17. Oct.<br>Magnificentiae et Nobilitatis Tuae Officiosissimus<br>M. IO. KEPLER.</p>", "en": "<p><b>JOHANNES KEPLER TO TYCHO BRAHE.</b></p><p>Since hitherto, most noble and most excellent Lord Tycho, partly your occupations, partly my fever, have not allowed me to review with Your Magnificence, according to my mind, the reasons for my coming into Bohemia, I have thought it would most rightly be done in writing. By two arguments especially did Your Magnificence, in the letter sent me into Styria, call me into Bohemia: the one, from <b>our contract — which in the month of May, and before, we entered on these terms: that I should promise, with the intervention of the Imperial will, to be present to Your Magnificence for two years, and, so far as in me lies, to mature and assist your labours in Astronomy, long since most desired by all Europe</b>; the other argument, drawn from my present calamity — when you bid me set out to you with the best hope, and not to doubt that either by your recommendations to the Imperial Majesty, or by your own work and aid, you would see to my troubles, and even compensate my travel-money. You acted rightly indeed about these two things. For when the necessity of migrating from Styria was laid on me, I had altogether to think of both. For as to the first: although our whole contract has expired and been annihilated — <b>the foundation removed by which I retained my Styrian salary (which has been taken from me by the Province's interdict)</b>, and by which my magistrate of that time would consent (in whose place, my office ceasing, I now recognise <b>the Prince of Württemberg</b>, by force both of obligation and of the letters of Reversal) — yet, since, while my prior office lasted, the matter was begun by Your Magnificence and proposed to the Imperial Majesty, and the Imperial Majesty also consented, I had certainly to take care <b>not to make a mockery, first of all, of the Imperial Majesty, and then also of Your Magnificence</b> — but rather to go to Prague even at my own loss, and there either embrace the matter fully accomplished (the hope your letter gave me at my coming), so far as in me lay; or, so far as lay in my Prince, expedite it through his Legate, <b>Lord Doctor Dold</b>, who was at Prague; or, if it should yet — against your expectation — be left hanging in doubt, then to test my resources, reckon my expenses, and weigh how long, without my ruin, I can wait at my own cost, for an uncertain outcome, with no salary — where, if such delays as the Imperial court requires were beyond my means, I might myself, [being] present, legitimately and with excuse to Your Magnificence and to myself, wholly desert the business.</p><p>As to the second: although it never was, nor is, doubtful to me that you both love me dearly, and wish me best advanced, and have the most splendid occasions for this, in so splendid a place, with such great favour of the supreme Monarch — <i>if I could bear the delay</i> — yet <b>the means by which, with divine help, I may relieve my present misfortune are otherwise</b>, partly necessary, partly (my affairs being slender) more useful. For since I am held by binding law to renounce my service, freed from my prior magistrate (to whom I had assigned it by the grant of my Nurturing Prince), back to that same <b>Nurturing Prince [of Württemberg]</b> — I should indeed have counselled myself most rightly, <b>had I gone straight from Graz into Württemberg</b>. For I hold it certain that those exiles who come thither — especially if they were the Prince's alumni — are at once provided for, both as to keep and as to conditions, at the first opportunity. And besides all this, <b>I have long since from my Teachers in the Tübingen Academy such promises</b> that, deservedly, from the conjunction of the Prince with the Academy, great hope ought to arise for me. And this would be the most compendious way of all to mitigate my troubles. The other is: that if other causes (such as our present dealing) should perhaps lead me to Prague, then in that city — namely, <b>in the assembly of the Legates from very many provinces</b> — I should at once provide for myself; first of all I should address the <b>Württemberg Legate</b>, [to learn] whether I can conveniently, at small cost, aided by his recommendations, go into Württemberg; or whether he would obtain me a leave from that Prince, and again help me with recommendations, that — for the sake of redeeming travel-money — I might be advanced to a nearer Academy, such as <b>Wittenberg, Jena, Leipzig</b>, etc. This is the other way, most noble Lord Tycho, of relieving my miseries — promising indeed less splendid conditions than are hoped from the Imperial court, but yet quicker, for the present more certain, and to me — who, by reason of poverty, am not [fit] for sustaining long delays — more acceptable.</p><p>I have explained what I think of both your arguments; now I will review with what mind I came hither, and now stay here. That <b>I wished, as soon as I was ordered to go into exile, to return to Württemberg</b>, you have long known from my letters. Then your letters came — which seemed effective enough both to me and to my kindred and friends — to move me that, <b>letting go the other mode of helping myself, for the sake of Your Magnificence and of Astronomy, and for my own greater increase (if God so willed), I should seize the Bohemian journey instead of the Württemberg one</b> — yet keeping the option of the other open. But it seemed best, <b>in any event, to leave my goods and my wife with her daughter [stepdaughter] at Linz</b>, where they would be on the road toward Württemberg.</p><p>I changed the plan about my wife and stepdaughter (for the goods, as I said, I left at Linz), from this fear, which now comes upon me: lest one of us should fall ill, the others being absent, among strangers. <b>And so I came to Prague at great expense. Sixty [florins] I spent on the transport of persons, twenty on travel-money, forty more on two casks filled with movable goods or furniture — and that only as far as Linz.</b> I say nothing of how much I am now in my host's debt. There remain to me expenses no more than [those] I need <b>for a stay of four weeks, and then for another journey (a short one, however).</b> Within these four weeks I will await the outcome of our business. But nonetheless, so far as my health allows, in any event I will legitimately look about, among the Princes' Legates, for a convenient position — yet so that, if Your Magnificence accomplishes anything meanwhile, that shall hold, and the rest be void. When this time has passed, and Your Magnificence requires of me that I stay longer, that is not possible for me otherwise, unless <b>either travel-money be restored me by Your Magnificence for transport and keep — 150 florins — so that my two casks be brought hither, lest what is packed perish from disuse; or Your Magnificence stand surety for me with those from whom the necessaries for my keep must be bought.</b> If the former be done, I can wait up to half a year (God prolonging life); but if the latter, my stay can last as long as it shall please Your Magnificence and the creditors. Meanwhile <b>Astronomy and Your Magnificence's labours will be as much my care as my health shall bear.</b> Nay, even, if you accomplish the business with the Imperial Majesty according to [your] mind, and obtain the travel-money, as you gave hope, I will restore to Your Magnificence, deducted from it, that sum which shall meanwhile have been lent me by Your Magnificence. Nay further, within these 4 weeks I will take pains that someone else pay me, for this two-year period, the salary which I had at Graz — for which I should be obliged, the time being expired, to pass into his service. Your Magnificence has, then, the sum of my counsels; which I earnestly beg you not interpret to the worse part toward me, [a man] poor and deserted and an exile.</p><p>Farewell.<br>17 October.<br>Most dutiful to Your Magnificence and Nobility,<br><b>M[aster] Johannes Kepler.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondents.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Johannes Kepler → Tycho</b> — written from <b>Prague</b> (place omitted in the source), <b>17 October 1600</b>, in the weeks after Kepler's return following the April quarrel and reconciliation.</li><li><b>Significance — the foundational negotiation of the Tycho–Kepler partnership.</b> This is the formal letter in which Kepler, <b>newly exiled from Styria</b>, sets out the terms, finances, and contingencies of his coming to work with Tycho — the relationship that would yield Kepler's <i>Astronomia nova</i>, his planetary laws, and the <i>Rudolphine Tables</i>. Its threads:</li><li><b>The two-year contract.</b> Tycho had called Kepler on two grounds: (1) <b>their contract of May 1600 — that Kepler, \"with the intervention of the Imperial will,\" assist Tycho's astronomy for two years</b>; and (2) Tycho's promise to relieve Kepler's \"calamity\" by recommendation to the Emperor and to compensate his travel-money.</li><li><b>The Styrian exile.</b> Kepler's <b>Graz salary \"taken away by the Province's interdict\"</b> — the <b>Counter-Reformation expulsion of Protestants from Styria (Sept 1600)</b>, which Kepler refused to recant. His default allegiance reverts to <b>the Prince of Württemberg</b> (his <i>Nurturing Prince</i>, patron of the Tübingen Stift where Kepler was an alumnus).</li><li><b>The Württemberg fallback.</b> Kepler frankly weighs going <b>straight to Württemberg/Tübingen</b> (where exiled ducal alumni get immediate support, and his old teachers have made promises) — a \"more compendious\" path than the uncertain imperial court — or being promoted via the <b>Württemberg Legate (Dr. Dold)</b> at Prague to a nearer university (<b>Wittenberg, Jena, Leipzig</b>).</li><li><b>The finances (vivid and exact).</b> Kepler details his journey costs — <b>60 fl. transport of persons, 20 fl. travel-money, 40+ fl. for two casks of goods, only as far as Linz</b> — his debt to his host, and that he can fund only <b>a four-week stay</b>. To stay longer he needs <b>either 150 florins of travel-money</b> (to fetch his two casks) <b>or Tycho's surety</b> with the tradesmen; with the former he can wait half a year. He will repay any loan from the imperial travel-money once obtained.</li><li><b>The family.</b> He left <b>his goods, wife [Barbara], and stepdaughter [Regina] at Linz</b>, then brought wife and stepdaughter on to Prague for fear of illness \"among strangers.\"</li><li><b>The commitment.</b> \"<b>Astronomy and Your Magnificence's labours will be as much my care as my health shall bear</b>\" — and a plea not to be misjudged, \"[a man] poor and deserted and an exile.\"</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A keystone of the <b>Tycho–Kepler convergence</b> thread; read with the April-1600 quarrel/reconciliation letters (brahe-t_16000408001, brahe-t_16000000001, brahe-t_16000803001) and the Ursus-analysis Kepler letter quoted at brahe-t_16000911001.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Johannes Kepler; <b>Tycho</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b> (the \"supreme Monarch\"); <b>Dr. Dold</b> (the Württemberg Legate at Prague); the <b>Prince/Duke of Württemberg</b> (Friedrich I); the <b>Tübingen Academy</b>; Kepler's <b>wife (Barbara)</b> and <b>stepdaughter (Regina)</b> (unnamed). Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Styria/Graz</b>, <b>Linz</b>, <b>Württemberg</b>, <b>Tübingen</b>, <b>Wittenberg</b>, <b>Jena</b>, <b>Leipzig</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar / place (protocol §3).</b> Dated only \"<i>17. Oct.</i>\" (the year 1600 from the slug/label); place omitted (<i>s.l.</i>) — <b>Prague</b> by context. Header: <b>[Prague], 17 October 1600</b>. (No <i>stylo</i> marker; in Catholic Prague, New Style.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — careful, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>two-year May-1600 contract</b> with imperial backing; the <b>Styrian/Graz exile and lost salary</b>; the <b>Württemberg/Tübingen fallback</b> via the Legate Dr. Dold; the <b>precise journey costs and the 150-florin / surety terms</b>; the family left at Linz then brought to Prague; and Kepler's commitment to Tycho's astronomy with his plea against misinterpretation) is firmly construed, with Kepler's anxious legal-financial reasoning rendered faithfully and without sharpening. Place supplied as Prague (context); wife/stepdaughter identified as Barbara/Regina on standard grounds.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.382-385 as page images (one Read call, 1-4). Letter (Kepler to Tycho, 17 Oct 1600) runs from the heading 'IOANNES KEPLER AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.' on p.383 to the signature 'M. IO. KEPLER' on p.385; checked p.382 (tail of a prior, unrelated letter) and confirmed the material after Kepler's signature on p.385 belongs to the next letter (Herwart von Hohenburg), correctly excluded. Collated the full Latin sentence-by-sentence against the printed page images: the two-argument structure (the biennium contract vs. the calamity/travel-money argument), the named persons and places (Doctor Dold as Wirtemberg legate, Graz, Tübingen, Wittenberg/Jena/Leipzig, Linz, the Emperor's court), the expense tally (60 florins for transport of persons, 20 for travel-money, 40 more for two chests, all 'only as far as Linz'), the four-week waiting period (stated twice), the 150-florin travel-money figure, the half-year (semestre) extension figure, the renewed four-week (4 septimanas) clause on the Graz salary, and the closing self-description 'poor and deserted and an exile' with the 17 October dateline and signature. All names, all six numeric/monetary figures, and the pronoun references (Kepler as subject of the Emperor-mockery clause, Tycho as subject of the affection clause) match Dreyer's print exactly, with no addition, omission, or flipped sense — despite the brief's caution that grade-A self-assessments are the least trustworthy, this one holds up under full collation. Published grade was A (self-assigned, per brief, unverified); independent collation confirms A on an evidentiary basis rather than say-so.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. Vindob. lat. 10702."], "th": {"label": "Johannes Kepler", "count": 10, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_16000828001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sets out the terms of their contract — two years of astronomical assistance with Imperial backing — and reviews grievances arising from his current situation in Bohemia.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001017001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001108001", "d": "1600-11-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 8 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = München, Universitätsbibliothek, 2° Cod. ms. 693</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>EX itinere, quo peregre abieram, Vir Nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, iam tandem reversus, relegi literas tuas 28 die Augusti ad me datas, et ex adiunctis exemplis deprehendi, me semel atque iterum in calculo lapsum esse, quo nomine gratias tibi ago eô maiores. Sed inprimis summe miror, atque adeo satis mirari nequeo, calculum tuum novissimum Eclipseos Solaris diei 10 Iulij huius anni ἀκριβέστατον tam praecise observationi coelesti eidemque exactae congruere, ita ut nec sexagesimâ quidem particulâ unius gradus a se invicem discrepare videantur.</p><p>Ἦ μέγα θαῦμα τόδ’ ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ὁρῶμαι.<br>Respondet numeris tua contemplatio coeli<br>Unius arctataque ad sexagessma partis.<br>Θαῦμα ἴδεσται.<br>Respondent coelo numeri, quibus attrahis astra,<br>Unius arctatique ad Sexage partis.<br>Θαῦμ’ ἀκόεσται.</p><p>Summo itaque desiderio flagro, ut quanto citius pollicitationem hac in re tuam adimpleas, totus enim iam calculum hunc tuum Solis et Lunae novissimum, posteaquam eundem plane absolveris, exspecto et opto.</p><p>Libros, quos petijsti, habeo, sed iam deest tabellarius. FRANCISCUS VIETAeUS Ao 89 Londini edidit librum in folio Regali, cui titulus FRANCISCI VIETAeI opera Mathematica, in quibus tractatur Canon Mathematicus, seu ad Triangula, item Canonion Triangulorum laterum rationalium, unâ cum universalium inspectionum ad Canonem Mathematicum libro singulari etc. In eoque libro multa tractat de resolutionibus triangulorum, quare plane conijcio ADRIANUM ROMANUM de hoc ipso libro locutum esse. Quod si eum desideres, eurabo omnino, ut habeas. ALBATEGNIUS latius habetur, sed is ipse, qui multis in locis incorrectus est, Norimbergae Ao 37 unâ cum ALFRAGANO editus, et nec is quidem facile invenitur, attamen πολλαπλασίωσις pretij effecit, ut ego quoque exemplar eiusdem mendosae impressionis ex Bibliotheca quadam redemerim. Quod si nihilominus eundem petas, mittam illico. Mihi sane, praeter nomina propria, non videtur adeo mendosus, si unica cautio adhibeatur; solet enim numerum binarium et quinarium confundere. Quod si hoc unicum caveatur, in calculo vix error oboriatur.</p><p>Iungo his literis Catalogum nundinarum autumnalium, quem ipsum quoque iam demum e peregrinatione domum reversus sum nactus; quod si aliquos libros ex ijs desideres, transmittentur. Affinis meus PAULUS ZEHENTNER ab ZEHENTGRUEB quaedam meo nomine tibi referet, atque hisce vale quam foelicissime.</p><p>Datae Monachij die 8 Nov. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Now at last returned from the journey on which I had gone abroad, most noble man and easily Prince of all Mathematicians, I have reread your letter sent to me on the 28th of August, and from the worked examples enclosed I have detected that I went astray, once and again, in calculation — for which I thank you all the more. But above all I greatly marvel, and indeed cannot marvel enough, that your latest calculation of the Solar Eclipse of the 10th of July this year — most exact [ἀκριβέστατον] — agrees so precisely with the exact celestial observation, that they do not seem to differ from each other even by a sixtieth part of one degree [i.e. one arc-minute].</p><p><i>Truly, a great wonder is this I behold with my eyes!</i> [Greek]<br>Your contemplation of the heaven answers to the numbers,<br>drawn close [to within] a sixtieth of a single part.<br><i>A wonder to behold.</i> [Greek]<br>The numbers by which you draw down the stars answer to the heaven,<br>[to within] a sixtieth of a single part.<br><i>A wonder to hear.</i> [Greek]</p><p>I burn, therefore, with the highest longing that you fulfil your promise in this matter as soon as may be; for I now wholly await and desire this your latest calculation of the Sun and the Moon, once you have fully completed it.</p><p>The books you asked for, I have — but now a courier is lacking. François Viète published, in the year [15]89, at London, a book in royal folio, titled <i>The Mathematical Works of François Viète, in which are treated the Mathematical Canon, or [the road] to Triangles; likewise the Canonion of Triangles of rational sides, together with a separate book of universal inspections to the Mathematical Canon, etc.</i> And in that book he treats many things about the resolutions of triangles; wherefore I plainly conjecture that Adriaan van Roomen spoke of this very book. If you should want it, I will entirely see to it that you have it. Albategnius is more widely to be had, but that very [edition] — which is incorrect in many places — was printed at Nuremberg in the year [15]37 together with Alfraganus, and not even that is easily found; yet the multiplying [πολλαπλασίωσις] of the price has brought it about that I too redeemed a copy of that same faulty impression from a certain Library. But if you should ask for it nonetheless, I will send it at once. To me, in truth, apart from the proper names, it does not seem so faulty, provided one single caution be used; for it is wont to confuse the number two and five. If this one thing be guarded against, scarcely an error would arise in the calculation.</p><p>I join to this letter the Catalogue of the autumn fair, which I myself only just came by, now returned home from my travels; if you should want any books from them, they will be sent on. My kinsman Paul Zehentner von Zehentgrueb will report some matters to you in my name; and with these, fare most happily.</p><p>Given at Munich, the 8th of November, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sequel.</b> An <b>inbound</b> scientific letter <b>to</b> Tycho, the <b>sequel to brahe-t_16000504003</b> (Herwart, 4 May 1600). <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (1553–1622), Chancellor of Bavaria, writing again from <b>Munich</b>, now reacting with astonishment to Tycho's solar tables.</li><li><b>The eclipse-calculation match (the scientific point; protocol §5.1).</b> Herwart marvels that Tycho's <i>calculus novissimus</i> of the <b>solar eclipse of 10 July 1600</b> agrees with observation to within \"not even a sixtieth part of one degree\" — i.e. <b>better than one arc-minute</b> (<i>sexagesima particula unius gradus</i> = 1/60°). Tycho had enclosed <i>adiuncta exempla</i> (worked examples) that exposed Herwart's own computational slips. Herwart presses for Tycho's promised <i>calculus Solis et Lunae novissimus</i> — the renewed <b>solar and lunar theory</b> (the <i>restitutio luminarium</i>, §5.1).</li><li><b>The Greek-Latin verse (protocol §4, §2 — Greek transcribed and translated; corruption flagged).</b> Herwart frames his wonder in macaronic couplets alternating Greek tags with Latin elegiacs:</li><li><i>Ἦ μέγα θαῦμα τόδ' ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ὁρῶμαι</i> — \"Truly, a great wonder is this I behold with my eyes\" (a Homeric-style hexameter; <i>cf.</i> the epic formula <i>θαῦμα ἰδέσθαι</i>, \"a wonder to behold\").</li><li><i>Θαῦμα ἰδέσθαι</i> / <i>Θαῦμ' ἀκοῦσθαι</i> (so to be read; source prints <i>ἴδεσται</i> / <i>ἀκόεσται</i>) — \"a wonder to behold\" / \"a wonder to hear.\"</li><li>The two Latin lines are transmitted <b>corruptly</b> in the working text: <i>\"Unius arctataque ad sexagesim[a]e partis\"</i> and the manifestly garbled doublet <i>\"Unius ar &amp; atique ad Sexage partis.\"</i> The sense is recoverable — <i>your reckoning of the heavens answers [the observations], drawn close to within one-sixtieth of a single degree</i> — and is so rendered; the corrupt forms are preserved verbatim in the source block per protocol §4.2, not silently emended.</li><li><b>The bibliographical postscript (instrument of the book-exchange).</b></li><li><i>Franciscus Vietaeus</i> = <b>François Viète (Vieta)</b> (1540–1603); Herwart describes his <i>Opera Mathematica</i> — the <b>Canon Mathematicus</b> (on triangles / trigonometric tables) — though his \"London 1589, royal folio\" details are garbled (Viète's <i>Canon mathematicus seu ad triangula</i> was Paris, 1579). He conjectures this is the book <b>Adriaan van Roomen (Adrianus Romanus)</b> had mentioned to Tycho.</li><li><i>Albategnius</i> = <b>al-Battānī</b> and <i>Alfraganus</i> = <b>al-Farghānī</b>, in the faulty <b>Nuremberg 1537</b> edition (al-Battānī's <i>De motu stellarum</i>, ed. with al-Farghānī) — scarce and dear (<i>πολλαπλασίωσις pretij</i>, \"the multiplying of the price\"); Herwart notes its one systematic flaw, a tendency to <b>confuse the numerals 2 and 5</b>.</li><li><i>Catalogus nundinarum autumnalium</i> = the <b>Frankfurt autumn book-fair catalogue</b>, enclosed.</li><li><i>Paulus Zehentner ab Zehentgrueb</i> = <b>Paul Zehentner von Zehentgrueb</b>, Herwart's kinsman and a further messenger.</li><li><b>Greek vocabulary (transcribed + glossed, §4.2).</b> <i>ἀκριβέστατον</i> = \"most exact\"; <i>θαῦμα</i> = \"wonder\"; <i>πολλαπλασίωσις</i> = \"multiplication.\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Monachium</i> = <b>Munich</b>.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>vale quam felicissime</i> → \"fare most happily\"; <i>Datae</i> (epistolary) → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 8 Nov. A. 1600</i> at Munich; <b>n.s.</b> (Catholic Bavaria, Gregorian). Reproduced. The eclipse is dated <i>10 July 1600</i> on the same New-Style reckoning.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Corrected the Latin verse: \"ar &amp; atique\" restored to Dreyer's single word \"arctatique\" (TBOO VIII p.386).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin and the scientific point (the sub-arc-minute eclipse agreement, the demand for Tycho's renewed solar/lunar theory) are secure, and the Greek tags and named authorities (Viète, Adriaan van Roomen, al-Battānī, al-Farghānī, Zehentner) are identified. Graded B rather than A because the <b>Latin verse couplet is textually corrupt</b> in the source (<i>\"Unius ar &amp; atique ad Sexage partis\"</i>), restored only by sense and flagged, not silently emended; and Herwart's bibliographical details for Viète's <i>Opera</i> (\"London 1589\") are themselves garbled and are corrected in the apparatus rather than in the body.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.384-387 as page images (Read pages=1-4). Collated the eclipse-agreement claim (10 July 1600, agreement to within a sixtieth of a degree), the Greek/Latin epigram couplets, the Viete/Albategnius/Alfraganus bibliographic paragraph (London [15]89 folio; Nuremberg [15]37 with Alfraganus; the two-vs-five digit confusion caveat), and the Paulus Zehentner and 8 November 1600 Munich dateline, word-for-word against the printed page. One low-severity TEXT_ERROR found in source_text's rendering of the second epigram couplet (a spurious '&amp;' splitting 'arctatique'); the translation itself is otherwise faithful throughout, with all proper names and figures correctly carried over. Grade C follows mechanically from the presence of a TEXT_ERROR per the grading rule, though the defect is minor and does not change what a scholar would take away.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text splits \"arctatique\" into \"ar &amp; atique\" with a spurious ampersand not present in Dreyer -- an apparent OCR/transcription corruption of the printed word. The English translation happens to render the intended sense correctly (it silently smooths over the corrupted Latin), so the reader is not misled, but the source_text itself departs from Dreyer. Dreyer prints: <i>\"Unius arctatique ad Sexage partis.\" -- printed on TBOO VIII p.386 as one continuous word, parallel in form to \"arctataque\" in the couplet immediately above; no ampersand appears on the page.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.384-387; the couplet appears on p.386, line 9, printed as one continuous word \"arctatique\" with no ampersand. Only the Latin required correction; the English translation already carried the correct sense. No other defects found across the four pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_16000923001", "next": "brahe-t_16001127001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports finding errors in his own calculations from Tycho's worked examples and marvels that Tycho's eclipse calculation of 10 July agrees with observation to within one arc-minute.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001108001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001110001", "d": "1600-11-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel", "ck": "Moritz, landgreve af Hessen-Kassel", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Moritz, Landgrave of Hesse[-Kassel] — Prague, 10 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MAURITIUM LANDTGRAVIUM HASSIAe.</p><p>CUM Illustrissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae Legatus Magnificus, Dominus IOHANNES de Bopenhaussen, qui magni Regis Persarum Legationem huc, Celnis Tuae iussu, deduxit, mihi (siquidem interea dum hîc fuit, nonnulla ex mutuo aliquoties congressu atque colloquio nobis intercessit familiaritas) indicâsset, se reditum in Hassiam ad Celnem Vm maturare, mei officij esse duxi Illmam Cm Vm hisce meis literis demisse salutare, et quaecunque ex mea tenuitate proficisci poterint obsequia, omni debitâ reverentiâ offerre. Auxit hanc scribendi confidentiam, et idipsum ut non omitterem, persuasit, non saltem ea, qua Laudatissimae memoriae Vrae Celnis Dominus Parens, quoad vixit, me Clementissime prosecutus est benevolentia, sed et insuper Vrae ipsius Celnis benignissimus animus et perseverans erga me favor, ut vel inde liquet, quod Celdo Vra etiam post obitum Parentis suis Clementissimis et cultissimis literis dignata sit. Quin et ex praedicto Celnis Vrae Legato gratissimâ mente nec sine voluptate percepi, Cm Vm etiamnum subinde per occasionem nonnunquam mei mentionem facere, quod constantis erga me Clementiae et benignitatis indicium, quâ possum submissione ac gratiarum devota actione, lubens agnosco, inque eo mihi non parum congratulor.</p><p>De migratione autem e Dania, Patria mea, ante quadriennium factâ, quoniam Cm Vm non saltem ex proprijs meis literis, interea ad eam officiosissime datis, sed et aliorum proculdubio relatione per suas circumstantias iam satis edoctum esse, nihil addubito, nolo ulterius ea de re quippiam commemorare. Existimo etiam Celni Vrae iam antea innotuisse, quomodo a Sacra Caesa Mate, Domino nostro Clementissimo, huc in Bohemiam ante biennium Clementissime vocatus et in ipsius Caesae Matis Clientelam benignissime receptus, quodque Mecoenatem eorum, quae diu multumque tracto, studiorum Astronomicorum in ipsius Mate invenerim liberalissimum. Quocirca advectis, ex ipsius etiam Caesae Matis Clementissimo mandato, per Albim fluvium omnibus meis, quibus in Dania ad tam sublimis scientiae exercitium et redintegrationem usus sum, Organis, ea nuper, ipsius quoque Mate sic Clementissime decernente, in domo quadam ampla et magnifice hîc iuxta Arcis hortum extructa disposui, quae numero sunt 28, tam formae et fabricae quam magnitudinis et utilitatis varietate discreta, quemadmodum supra memoratus Celnis Vrae Dominus Legatus, qui ea singula coram intuitus est, ad Cm Vm plenius referre poterit. Etsi vero in libro Epistolarum, qui Illmi et nunquam satis laudati Patris vestri literas de re Astronomica mihi Clementissime scriptas, unâ cum mea responsione, complectitur (quem Illmae Celni Vrae a me submisse dedicatum eam accepisse opinor) suo loco nonnulla horum Instrumentorum reperiatur descriptio, et licet insuper icones quasdam, pleraque ex hisce Instrumentis repraesentantes, postea miserim, de quibus, quod eas Celdo Vra obtinuerit, Opus illud Palatinum, mihi vicissim dono Clementissime remissum, omne exemit dubium: Tamen cum Organa mea Astronomica postmodum plenius delineari atque excidi, unâ cum uberiore Fabricae et Usus explicatione, atque in peculiari libro inprimi curârim, quem Imperatoriae Mati humilime dedicatum, unâ cum duobus alijs pari formâ et magnitudine, quorum unus de mille affixarum stellarum accuratissime a nobis facta Restitutione, alter de ☉lis et ☾nae verificatis motibus agit, cum primum huc advenissem, subiectissime obtulerim, Vrae Illmae Celni eum, qui de Mechanica Astronomiae parte tractat, cum hoc ipso suo Nobilissimo et eximio Legato demisse dono mittendum duxi, quem et Celdo Vra benignâ fronte accipere non dedignetur, quâ possum reverentia oro. Si reliquos duos Celdo Vra unâ Clementissime expetierit, praesertim eum, qui affixarum Rectificationem scrupulose exequitur, ut collatio cum ijs stellis, quas Vra Celdo ibi pariter observatas habet, institui possit, eos quoque per temporis oportunitatem quam obsequentissimo animo impertiar. Quod et nunc praestitissem, si exempla conficiendi (sunt enim ambo manu scripti) commoditas superfuisset. Quin etiam cum alia ex meis Operibus lucem publicam viderint, ea cum Celne Vra omni debita submissione communicare, eiusque solertissimo et gravissimo submittere iudicio non supersedebo: Et quacunque alias in re pro mearum virium modulo quippiam, quod Celni Vrae gratum sit, praestare potuero, habebit ea in me servitorem fidelem, cui id, quicquid erit, libere demandet, qui etiam pro virili Vrae Celnis Clementissimis desiderijs omni animi submissione obtemperaturus sit. Sed ne diutius, quam par est, Cm Vm, cui me meaque studia commendata esse submisse cupio, hac interpellatione, gravioribus negocijs intentam, remorer, eam quam prosperrime florere et quam diutissime saluberrimeque valere unâ cum Illma Coniuge et inclytis liberis, a Deo Opt. ter Maximo devoto pectore precor.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum Die 10 Novembris Anni 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MORITZ, LANDGRAVE OF HESSE.</b></p><p>Since Your most illustrious Highness's magnificent Envoy, Lord <b>Johann von Bopenhausen</b> — who conducted hither, by Your Highness's command, the Legation of the great King of the Persians — had indicated to me (for while he was here some familiarity arose between us from our repeated meeting and conversation) that he was hastening his return into Hesse to Your Highness, I judged it part of my duty humbly to greet Your most illustrious Highness by this my letter, and with all due reverence to offer whatever services can proceed from my slenderness. What increased this confidence to write, and persuaded me not to omit it, was not only the benevolence with which <b>Your Highness's Father, of most praised memory, while he lived, most clemently attended me</b>, but also Your own Highness's most kindly disposition and persevering favour toward me — as is clear even from this, that Your Highness, even after Your Father's death, deigned to honour me with most clement and cultivated letters. And indeed I learned, from the said Envoy of Your Highness, with most grateful mind and not without delight, that Your Highness still now and then, as occasion offers, makes mention of me — which token of constant clemency and kindness toward me I gladly acknowledge, with what submission and devoted giving of thanks I can, and in it I congratulate myself not a little.</p><p>But of my <b>migration from Denmark, my fatherland, made four years ago</b> — since I in no way doubt that Your Highness has already been sufficiently instructed in its circumstances, not only from my own letters, dutifully sent meanwhile, but doubtless also from the report of others — I will recount nothing further about that matter. I think too that it has already become known to Your Highness how, <b>by the Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, I was two years ago most clemently called hither into Bohemia and most kindly received into His Imperial Majesty's clientele</b>, and that I have found in His Majesty a <b>most liberal Maecenas of those Astronomical studies which I have long and much pursued</b>. Wherefore — all my <b>Instruments</b>, which in Denmark I used for the exercise and renewal of so sublime a science, having been <b>transported by the river Elbe</b>, by His Imperial Majesty's most clement mandate also — I have lately, His Majesty so most clemently decreeing, <b>set them up in a certain ample house, magnificently built here beside the Castle garden</b>; they are <b>28 in number, distinguished by variety as much of form and fabric as of magnitude and usefulness</b> — as Your Highness's above-mentioned Lord Envoy, who beheld each of them in person, will be able to report more fully to Your Highness. And although in the <b>Book of Epistles</b> — which comprises the letters on Astronomical matters most clemently written to me by Your never-sufficiently-praised illustrious Father, together with my reply (which I think Your most illustrious Highness received, humbly dedicated to You by me) — some description of these Instruments is found in its place; and although I afterward sent besides certain <b>engravings [<i>icones</i>] representing most of these Instruments</b> (and that Your Highness obtained them, <b>that Palatine Work [<i>Opus Palatinum</i>], most clemently sent back to me in turn as a gift, removed all doubt</b>): yet, since I have since had <b>my Astronomical Instruments more fully delineated and engraved, with a richer explanation of their Fabric and Use, and printed in a special book</b> — which, humbly dedicated to the Imperial Majesty, <b>together with two others of equal form and size (one of which treats of the <i>Restitution of the thousand fixed stars</i>, most accurately made by us, the other of the <i>verified motions of the Sun [☉] and Moon [☾]</i>)</b>, I most submissively offered as soon as I had come hither — I have judged that the one which treats of the <b>Mechanical part of Astronomy</b> should be humbly sent as a gift to Your most illustrious Highness, by this same most noble and excellent Envoy of Yours; which Your Highness may not disdain to accept with kindly countenance, I pray with what reverence I can. <b>If Your Highness should clemently desire the remaining two as well — especially the one that scrupulously carries out the Rectification of the fixed stars, that a collation may be set up with those stars which Your Highness has likewise observed there</b> — I will impart these too, in time's opportunity, with a most obedient mind. Which I would have done even now, had the convenience of making copies remained (for both are in manuscript). And indeed, when others of my Works shall have seen the public light, I will not forbear to communicate them to Your Highness with all due submission, and to submit them to Your most skilful and weighty judgement. And in whatever matter otherwise I shall be able, according to the measure of my powers, to do anything welcome to Your Highness, You shall have in me a faithful servant, to whom You may freely entrust whatever it be, and who will, to the utmost, obey Your Highness's most clement desires with all submission of mind. But lest I detain Your Highness — to whom I humbly wish myself and my studies commended — longer than is fitting with this interruption, [You being] intent on weightier affairs, I pray from a devoted heart to God the Best and Thrice-Greatest that You may flourish most prosperously and fare most long and healthily, together with Your illustrious Consort and renowned children.</p><p>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 10th day of November, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Moritz \"the Learned,\" Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1572–1632) — son and successor of the astronomer-prince Wilhelm IV. The letter is formal court Latin, all in the deferential third-person <i>Celsitudo Vestra</i> (\"Your Highness\") register.</li><li><b>The late father.</b> <i>Illustrissimi et nunquam satis laudati Patris vestri</i> = <b>Wilhelm IV of Hesse-Kassel</b> (1532–1592), the observing prince of the Kassel observatory, Tycho's great correspondent — \"of most praised memory.\"</li><li><b>The envoy &amp; the Persian embassy.</b> <b>Johann von Bopenhausen</b>, the Hessian envoy who \"conducted hither the Legation of the great King of the Persians\" — i.e. the <b>Safavid embassy</b> (Husain Ali Beg / the Sherley mission) that reached Prague in 1600; he is the bearer of this letter and of the gift book back to Kassel, having seen Tycho's instruments in person.</li><li><b>Significance — the Prague instrument-installation and the three-volume imperial program.</b></li><li><b>The 28 instruments at Prague.</b> Tycho's instruments, <b>shipped from Denmark up the Elbe</b> by imperial mandate, are now <b>set up in \"an ample house, magnificently built beside the Castle garden\"</b> (the Curtius house; cf. brahe-t_16010415001, \"the new Rudolphine Uraniborg\") — <b>\"28 in number, varied in form, fabric, magnitude and usefulness.\"</b> A precise, datable census of the working Prague apparatus.</li><li><b>The three Opera offered to Rudolf II.</b> Tycho names the program he presented to the Emperor on arrival: (1) the book \"<b>on the Mechanical part of Astronomy</b>\" = the <i>Astronomiae Instauratae <b>Mechanica</b></i> (the gift here sent); (2) \"<b>the Restitution of the thousand fixed stars</b>\" = the <i>Stellarum octavi orbis … restitutio</i> / the 1000-star catalogue; (3) \"<b>the verified motions of the Sun and Moon</b>\" = the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. Crucially, <b>(2) and (3) were still in manuscript</b> in November 1600 (\"for both are in manuscript … no convenience of making copies remained\").</li><li><b>A documented <i>Mechanica</i> gift-distribution event.</b> Tycho <b>sends Landgrave Moritz a gift copy of the <i>Mechanica</b></i> via Bopenhausen (Prague → Kassel, Nov 1600), offering the star-catalogue volume too — explicitly <b>for collation against the Kassel star observations</b> (\"those stars which Your Highness has likewise observed there\"). Cross-ref the Mechanica production/distribution layer.</li><li><b>The earlier books referenced.</b></li><li>The <b>\"Book of Epistles\"</b> = Tycho's <i>Epistolarum astronomicarum liber</i> (Uraniborg 1596), which printed <b>Wilhelm IV's astronomical letters with Tycho's replies</b>, dedicated to Moritz — with some instrument-descriptions already in it; Tycho had also sent <b>engravings (<i>icones</i>)</b> of the instruments.</li><li>The <b><i>Opus Palatinum</b></i> = the <i>Opus Palatinum de triangulis</i> of <b>Rheticus / Valentin Otho</b> (Neustadt an der Haardt, 1596), which <b>Moritz had sent Tycho as a return gift</b> — proof that Moritz received the engravings.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Moritz of Hesse-Kassel; <b>Wilhelm IV</b> (the late father); <b>Johann von Bopenhausen</b> (envoy); the <b>King of Persia</b> (the Safavid embassy); <b>Rudolf II</b>; the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> (1596); the <i>Opus Palatinum</i> (Rheticus/Otho); the <i>Mechanica</i>, the 1000-star <i>Restitutio</i>, the <i>Progymnasmata</i> (Sun/Moon motions). Places: <b>Prague</b> (the house by the castle garden), <b>Denmark</b>, the <b>Elbe</b>, <b>Kassel</b> (implied \"there\").</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Die 10 Novembris Anni 1600</i> at Prague (imperial reckoning) → <b>10 November 1600 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, fully construable court Latin read with confidence; the substance (the courtesies to Moritz and the memory of Wilhelm IV; the Bopenhausen/Persian-embassy occasion; the migration from Denmark and the imperial call; the <b>28 instruments shipped up the Elbe and installed by the castle garden</b>; the three-volume program with the <b>star-catalogue and <i>Progymnasmata</i> still in manuscript</b>; the gift of the <b><i>Mechanica</b></i> and the offer of the others for Kassel-collation; the <i>Epistolae astronomicae</i> and <i>Opus Palatinum</i> exchange) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux; the Sun/Moon glyphs (☉/☾) are reproduced.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.387-388 (page images, read visually). Page 386 was confirmed to be the tail of the preceding letter (dated Munich, 8 Nov 1600) and page 389 the start of the next letter (Tycho to Bacmeister), so nothing of this letter falls outside the read range. Every name, number and date checked (Iohannes de Bopenhaussen, ante quadriennium / ante biennium, 28 instruments, mille affixarum stellarum, Opus Palatinum, Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum Die 10 Novembris Anni 1600) matches Dreyer exactly. No defects found; this independently confirms the pre-existing [A] grade rather than merely accepting it on the worker's say-so.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": null, "ka": 1, "bk": 1, "ab": "Uses Bopenhausen's return to greet the Landgrave, acknowledges the late Landgrave Wilhelm's past favour, and affirms his continued wish to be of service to Hesse-Kassel.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001110001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001115001", "d": "1600-11-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Bording — Prague, 15 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 156 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD JACOBUM BORDINGUM].<br>Magnifice et excellentissime vir, amice inprimis honorande.</p><p>ACCEPI tandem diu desideratum responsum Magnif: Tuae ad petitionem meam bis vel ter reiteratam nimirum de 600 joachimicis, qui loco interesse a quaestore juniorum principum Megapolensium isthic mihi debentur, et simul caussas, cur expectatione mea tardius M: T: mihi de hoc ipso negotio rescripserit, ex ijs quas 19. septembris Gustrouio ad me dedit, intellexi. Quibus etiam acquiesco. Sexcentos vero illos joachimicos ad clarissimum virum d. d. Lucam Backmeisterum, affinem tuum et communem nostrum amicum, missos esse, lubenter ex ijsdem percepi. Scribit is ad me, se pecuniam illam secum reseruaturum, donec illustriss: Saxoniae electoratus administratoris magnificus legatus d:ns d. Gödelmannus illuc aduenerit. Quod etiam mihi non est ingratum, siquidem jam antea ipsum ob penuriam occasionum aliarum tuto mittendi, vt ita faceret, admonuerim. At cum praedictus d:ns Gödellmannus, tardius quam sperassemus, hinc discedat, adeo vt vix ante initia anni Juliani vestri Rostochium sit perventurus, nimis diu pecunia ista careo, quod tamen, siquidem aliter fieri non potuit, ferendum. Quia vero postea circiter quadrans anni solummodo restabit ad completionem ejus temporis, quo reliqui 600 loco hujus anni interesse numerandi sunt, Mag: T: enixe et peramanter rogatam habeo, vt efficere velit, totam summam a quaestore juniorum principum exsolui, et praedicto d:no Gödellmanno, dum isthic est, committi, ita vt 1200 joachimici, qui mihi ad instantis anni calendas aprilis pro duobus transactis annis debentur, pariter cedant, neque enim id iniquum censeri poterit, licet pauculi menses restent ad terminum praefinitum, quo sic aliquatenus compensetur ea, quam modo passus sum, plus quam dimidij anni frustratio. Quod autem id per eundem fieri quam maxime desiderem, duae me impellunt graues caussae, quarum vnam ex ipso d:no Godelmanno aut saltem ab affine tuo cognosces. Altera est, quod tam certam et tutam rationem 600 illos alios circa initia aprilis instantis anni huc adferendi non invenire potero quam haec ipsa est per saepedictum d:nm d. Gödellmannum, amicum meum singularem, huc expeditis Rostochij suis rebus, quam citissime fieri poterit, reuertentem, qui etiam suam mihi hac in re operam beneuole addixit. Quin et hoc Magnificentiam Tuam celare non possum, si id ipsi prius forte non innotuit, me certis de caussis et deliberato consilio totam summam decem illorum millium joachimicorum, quam illustrissimis junioribus principibus mutuo dedi, ad praefinitum terminum juxta tenorem contractus nostri reposcere, quod et antea d:no quaestori indicari jussi. Et jam denuo ipsum de eodem per literas admonui, neque enim ex re mea est, vt pecunia illa in loco hinc adeo remoto, a quo interesse statuto tempore huc non sine periculo et difficultate peruenire possit, diutius haer[eat], cum etiam alias plus commodi et usufructus intermediante eadem pecunia hic nancisci queam, veluti ipse quoque d:ns d. Gödellmannus plenius meum hac de re propositum aperiet. Quibus igitur possum precibus M: T: rogatam habeo, vt votis meis in hisce per omnia adesse non degrauetur. Ego, quaecumque a me vicissim proficisci poterint officia, eidem quam paratissimo animo defero, atque M: Tuam cum conjuge et familia quam diutissime et optime valere a Deo ter optimo maximo opto.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum die 15to nouembris a:o 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO JACOB BORDING].</b><br>Magnificent and most excellent man, friend especially to be honoured.</p><p>I have at last received Your Magnificence's long-desired reply to my petition, twice or thrice repeated — namely concerning the <b>600 joachimsthalers</b> which are owed me there, in lieu of interest, by the treasurer of the younger princes of Mecklenburg — and at the same time I have understood, from those [letters] you sent me from <b>Güstrow on 19 September</b>, the reasons why Your Magnificence wrote back to me on this business later than I expected. With which I also acquiesce. That those six hundred joachimsthalers were sent to the most renowned man Lord Doctor <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>, your kinsman and our common friend, I gladly gathered from the same. He writes to me that he will keep that money with him until the magnificent legate of the most illustrious <b>Administrator of the Saxon Electorate</b>, Lord Doctor <b>Gödelmann</b>, shall arrive there — which is not unwelcome to me, since I had already advised him to do so, for the scarcity of other ways of sending it safely. But since the said Lord Gödelmann departs hence later than we had hoped — so that he will scarcely reach <b>Rostock</b> before the beginning of your <b>Julian year</b> — I am too long without that money; which, however, since it could not be otherwise, must be borne. And since afterward only about a quarter-year will remain to the completion of the time at which the <b>other 600</b>, in lieu of this year's interest, are to be paid, I earnestly and lovingly ask Your Magnificence to bring it about that the <b>whole sum</b> be paid out by the treasurer of the younger princes, and entrusted to the said Lord Gödelmann while he is there, so that the <b>1,200 joachimsthalers</b> owed me by the Kalends of April of the coming year, for the two elapsed years, may come to me together — nor can this be judged unfair, though a few months remain to the appointed term, that thereby the frustration of more than half a year, which I have just suffered, may be somewhat compensated. That I most greatly desire this be done through the same [Gödelmann], two weighty reasons impel me: one you will learn from Lord Gödelmann himself, or at least from your kinsman; the other is that I shall find no way so sure and safe of bringing those other 600 hither, about the beginning of April of the coming year, as this very one — through the oft-mentioned Lord Gödelmann, my especial friend, returning hither as quickly as may be once his affairs at Rostock are dispatched, who has also kindly promised me his help in this matter. Moreover I cannot conceal from Your Magnificence, if it has not perhaps become known to you before, that I — for certain reasons and by deliberate counsel — <b>demand back the whole sum of those ten thousand joachimsthalers</b> which I lent to the most illustrious younger princes, at the appointed term according to the tenor of our contract, which I also previously ordered to be indicated to the treasurer; and now again I have admonished him of the same by letter. For it is not to my advantage that that money remain longer in a place so remote from here, whence the interest cannot reach hither at the set time without danger and difficulty, since I can otherwise obtain more advantage and usufruct with the same money here — as Lord Gödelmann too will more fully disclose my purpose. With what prayers I can, therefore, I ask Your Magnificence not to find it burdensome to assist my wishes in these things in every way. I, in turn, offer you with most ready mind whatever services can proceed from me, and wish from God thrice best and greatest that Your Magnificence may fare most long and well, with your wife and household.</p><p>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 15th day of November, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; companion letter.</b> <b>Jacob Bording</b> of <b>Lübeck</b> (cf. brahe-t_16000331001, brahe-t_16010412001). This is the <b>Latin companion</b> to Tycho's same-day German letter to the rent-master <b>Andreas Meier</b> (brahe-t_16001115002, Prague, 15 Nov 1600): the two coordinate the same collection.</li><li><b>Position in the Mecklenburg debt-thread.</b> Acknowledging Bording's reply (from <b>Güstrow, 19 Sept 1600</b>), Tycho confirms the <b>first 600-thaler interest tranche</b> was sent to <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Superintendent of Rostock, Bording's kinsman), who holds it until <b>Gödelmann</b> — <i>here styled \"legate of the Administrator of the Saxon Electorate\"</i> — reaches Rostock (scarcely \"before the beginning of your <b>Julian year</b>,\" i.e. O.S. New Year). Tycho asks that the <b>whole 1,200 thalers</b> (two years' interest, due by <b>1 April 1601</b>) be paid out together to Gödelmann now, to compensate the half-year's delay; and re-announces that he is <b>calling in the entire 10,000-thaler principal</b> at the contract term, \"since I can obtain more advantage with the money here\" in Bohemia.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Jacob Bording; <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Bacmeister, Superintendent of Rostock); <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (now legate of the Saxon Administrator); the <b>treasurer of the younger Mecklenburg princes</b>; the younger princes (Duke Johann's sons, cf. brahe-t_16000920002).</li><li><b>Calendar note (protocol §3).</b> Tycho's phrase <i>initia anni Juliani vestri</i> — \"the beginning of <b>your Julian year</b>\" — neatly marks the O.S./N.S. divide: Bording's Lübeck and Rostock keep Julian, Tycho's Prague keeps Gregorian. Dateline <i>Pragae … die 15to Novembris a:o 1600</i>, <b>New Style</b>; header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Abbreviations.</b> Chancery contractions (<i>M: T:</i> = <i>Magnificentia Tua</i>, <i>d:ns d.</i> = <i>dominus doctor</i>, <i>a:o</i>) silently expanded; the source block keeps them.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the (abbreviated) Latin is read securely; the financial substance (the two 600-thaler interest tranches via Backmeister and Gödelmann; the request to pay the whole 1,200 together to offset the delay; the calling-in of the 10,000 principal) is explicit and dovetails with the same-day Meier letter and the wider debt-thread. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.213(bottom, item 210 heading)-215 as page images. Collated all sums and names: 600 joachimsthalers (owed via the treasurer of the younger Mecklenburg princes), the second 600 due at the coming April Kalends making 1200 together, and the separate 10,000 joachimsthaler principal Tycho lent the princes and now recalls — all figures match Dreyer and are internally consistent in both source and English. Names Lucas Backmeister, Gödelmann, Güstrow (19 Sept letter-place) and Rostock all verified against the page image. Closing date '15to nouembris a:o 1600' matches. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 156 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16000919001", "next": "brahe-t_16010412001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000919001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports receiving Bording's reply on 600 joachimsthalers owed by the Mecklenburg treasury, now held by Lucas Backmeister until Gödelmann's arrival.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001115001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001115002", "d": "1600-11-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Andreas Meier", "ck": "Andreas Meier", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Andreas Meier — Prague, 15 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 158 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM MEYERUM].</p><p>MEIN freundtlichen gruß mit wunschunge alles guten zuuor. Ehrenuester vndt vornemmer herr rentmeister, ich kan dem herrn freundtlicher meinung nicht bergen, wie ich auß des herrn doctoris Lucae Backmeisterj, superintendentis zue Rostochk, neulich an mich gethonen schreiben vernommenn, das er die 600 thaller, so ich im anfang aprilis dießes lauffenden 1600 jahrs solte bej euch loco interesse von wegen der bewusten summa haben gehabt, von meinetwegen empfangen haben, wiewohl solchs nicht zur rechter zeit vber verhoffen geschehen ist. Das ich darumb noch bißhero daßelbige geldt durch gütte gelegenheit nicht habe könnenn erlangen, sondern allereerst durch dem herrn doctorem Johannem Gödelmannum, der gen Rostoch von hinnen zue verreisen in willens ist, alleine laßen abfodern, vndt dieweil er kaum furn neuenjahrstagk dahin gelangen kan, vndt hernacher nur drey monat restiren zu den termin, auf welchen ich die andern 600 thaller fur dießes jahrs interesse haben soll, so ist an dem herrn mein gantz fleißiges bitten vndt begeren, er wolle obgedachten h. doctor Gödellman die andern 600 thaller auf lieferung meiner quitantze zustehen laßen, dan ich sunsten keine so gewiße gelegenheit weis, dieselbe abholen zu laßen. Vndt wen schon die zeit, da dieses interesse sol bezahlt werden, nicht gentzlich alßdan verfloßen ist, sundern 2 oder 3 monat restiren, so ist hiergegen zue erachten, das ich in dießes jahrs zinse lenger alß ein halb jahr, nicht ohne meine schaden, bin aufgehalten vndt verhindert worden, vndt nun nicht vnbillig ist, das mir ein solche leidliche compensation hierinne wiederumb geschehen. Vndt nachdeme ich vor lengst inn Dennemarck geschrieben zue ein edelman von meinem blutßuerwandten mit nahmen Eske Bilde, das er von meinetwegen solte auf nechstverschienen Michaelis die gantze hauptsumme, alß 10000 thaller, aufsagen vndt resigniren, auf das ich dieselbige auf zukumpfftige ostern oder anfange des aprilis köntte zue Lübeck oder Hamburgk, durch ainen darzue gedeputirten treuen man, wiederbekommen, so versihe ich mich, das er daßelbe verrichtet hat, vndt bitte darumbe den herrn rentmeister, das er woll die versehunge thuen vndt eingedenck sein, das ich auf dieselbige zeit die gantze hauptsum an guetten reichsthaller, so in Ober-Teutschlandt gangkbar sein, köntte wiederbekommen. So ferne es aber nicht geschehen ist, entweder durch genanten Eske Bilde oder andern von meinetwegen, so will ich hiemit, krafft dieses brieffes, dieselbe hauptsumme auf nechstkommenden Michaëlis des 1601 Jahrs geresignirt vndt aufgesagt haben, dan mir nicht zutreglich, das dieselbe lenger alda stehen bleibe, vndt weis alhier in kunigreich Behemen besser nutz vndt profit damit zueschaffen. Welchs ich den herrn zeitlich zuuerstehen zue geben nicht habe sollen vnterlaßen, vndt wirt der obgedachte herr doctor Gödellman alda weiter bescheidt, nachdeme vonnothten, mundtlich oder schrifftlich, wißen zuberichten. Bitte der herr wolle hierinne thuen wie es vor sich selbst billich, vndt ich mich zue ihme gentzlich verlaße. Hiemit dem allmechtigen Gott beuehlen.</p><p>Datum Prag denn 15 dag nouembris anno 1600.<br>Des herren allzeit freundtwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>mitt eigen h:tt.</p><p>In deme der herr rentmeister etwa mitt dieser handtskrifft<br>vndt quittirung, die ich hiemit vbersende, nitt allerdinge friedtlich, soll<br>der herr doctor Gödellman meinetwegen ein andere auff dießse sechshundert<br>thaller, so kunfftigen 1 aprilis anni 1601 verschienen sein werden, dem<br>herrn vnter seiner handt vndt indsigell folgen laßen, welche eben so viel<br>gelten soll alß wans von mir selbst beschehen. Zur vrkundt hab ich abermahl<br>vntherschrieben. Actum die et loco ut supra.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO ANDREAS MEIER].</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, with the wish of all good, beforehand. Honourable and distinguished Lord rent-master, I cannot, in friendly intent, conceal from you that I have learned, from the letter lately written to me by Lord Doctor Lucas Backmeister, Superintendent at Rostock, that he received on my behalf the 600 thalers which I was to have had from you at the beginning of April of this current year 1600, in lieu of interest on the known sum — although this happened, contrary to expectation, not at the right time. Hence I have hitherto not been able to obtain that money through a good opportunity, but have only now let it be fetched solely through Lord Doctor Johann Gödelmann, who intends to travel from here to Rostock; and since he can scarcely reach there before New Year's Day, and thereafter only three months remain to the term at which I am to have the other 600 thalers for this year's interest, my wholly diligent request and desire of you is that you would let the aforesaid Lord Doctor Gödelmann have the other 600 thalers upon delivery of my receipt; for otherwise I know no such sure opportunity to have it fetched. And even though the time when this interest is to be paid is not then wholly elapsed, but 2 or 3 months remain, yet against this it is to be considered that I have been held up and hindered in this year's interest for more than half a year, not without my loss, and it is now not unfair that such a tolerable compensation be made me in return.</p><p>And since I long ago wrote into Denmark to a nobleman of my blood-relations named Eske Bille, that he should on my behalf, at the last past Michaelmas, give notice on and resign the whole principal sum — namely 10,000 thalers — so that I might recover the same at the coming Easter, or the beginning of April, at Lübeck or Hamburg, through a trusty man deputed thereto: I assume that he has done so, and therefore ask you, Lord rent-master, that you would make the provision, and be mindful, that I might at that same time recover the whole principal sum in good Reichsthalers, such as are current in Upper Germany. But insofar as it has not been done — either through the said Eske Bille or another on my behalf — I will herewith, by force of this letter, have the same principal sum resigned and given notice on for the coming Michaelmas of the year 1601; for it is not advantageous to me that it remain standing there any longer, and I know how to make better use and profit with it here in the kingdom of Bohemia. Which I ought not to have omitted to give you to understand in good time; and the aforesaid Lord Doctor Gödelmann will know how to report further information there, as needed, orally or in writing. I ask that you would do herein as is in itself fair, and I rely on you entirely. Herewith [I] commend [you] to Almighty God.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 15th day of November, the year 1600.<br>Your ever friend-willing<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p><p>In case the Lord Receiver [Rentmeister] should perhaps not be entirely satisfied with this document and receipt, which I herewith send, then Lord Doctor Gödelmann shall, on my behalf, let another [document] follow to you, under his own hand and seal, concerning these six hundred thalers which will fall due on the coming first of April of the year 1601 — [a document] which shall be worth exactly as much as if it had been done by myself. In witness whereof I have signed once again. Done on the day and in the place as above.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Andreas Meier</b>, ducal Mecklenburg <b>rent-master</b> (<i>Rentmeister</i>) at <b>Wismar</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010527001, brahe-t_16010718001). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b> in the Basel Autographensammlung.</li><li><b>Significance — the earliest letter in the Mecklenburg 10,000-thaler debt thread.</b> This <b>15 November 1600</b> letter opens (chronologically) the documented exchange that the 1601 letters continue (Tycho→Meier 27 May &amp; 18 July 1601; Tycho→Duke Ulrich 4 June 1601; the ducal replies of 24 July &amp; 9 August 1601). It already lays out the whole financial structure:</li><li>The loan carries <b>semi-annual interest of 600 thalers</b> (<i>loco interesse</i>): the April-1600 tranche, paid late, was collected for Tycho by <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>, Superintendent of Rostock; the next 600 (due ≈ February 1601) Tycho asks Meier to release to <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> against a receipt, since Gödelmann is travelling to Rostock and is Tycho's only sure carrier.</li><li>Tycho presses for a small <b>\"tolerable compensation,\"</b> since he has been kept out of this year's interest \"for more than half a year.\"</li><li><b>The principal (10,000 thalers).</b> Tycho has instructed his kinsman <b>Eske Bille</b> to give formal notice (<i>aufsagen und resignieren</i>) on the whole principal at the <b>last Michaelmas (29 Sept 1600)</b>, so he can recover it \"at the coming Easter or beginning of April\" at <b>Lübeck or Hamburg</b>, in <b>good Reichsthalers current in Upper Germany</b>. Should that notice have failed, Tycho here, \"by force of this letter,\" <b>resigns the principal for Michaelmas 1601</b> — \"for I know how to make better use and profit with it here in the kingdom of Bohemia.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Andreas Meier; <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Bacmeister, 1530–1608, Superintendent of Rostock; cf. brahe-t_16000919001), who collected the April interest; <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (the Mecklenburg jurist-diplomat carrier; cf. brahe-t_16010412001); <b>Eske Bille</b>, Tycho's Danish kinsman-agent.</li><li><b>Register / formulae.</b> Chancery German in the courteous third person (<i>der Herr</i>, \"the Lord [= you]\"); subscription <i>mit eigen Hand</i> (= <i>manu propria</i>). The Latin business terms (<i>loco interesse</i>, <i>quittance</i>, <i>resignieren</i>, <i>Termin</i>) embedded in the German per protocol §2.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Prag den 15 dag Novembris anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague/imperial). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Language.</b> This segment is Early New High German, continuous with the letter's published body (also German).</li><li><b>What it is.</b> A recovered closing clause of a business letter Tycho sent from Prague (15 November 1600) to Andreas Meier, evidently a Rentmeister (receiver/treasurer) at or near Rostock — the surviving published tail already shows Tycho withdrawing capital from Rostock to redeploy it \"here in the kingdom of Bohemia.\"</li><li><b>The 600 thalers.</b> Very likely the same capital sum documented by Dreyer: an interest-bearing loan of Tycho's on which 6% annual interest was paid to his old friend Professor Lucas Backmeister at Rostock [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (1890), citing <i>Breve og Aktstykker</i>, p. 145] — the published tail of this very letter names \"Doctor Lucæ Backmeisterj, superintendentis zue Rostochk\" directly.</li><li><b>Doctor Gödelmann.</b> Johann Georg Gödelmann appears elsewhere in Tycho's correspondence as early as 12 November 1598 (a letter to Tycho from Prague) [Tier 1: Tycho Brahes Breve, corpus transcription of TBOO]. A Rostock-trained jurist, he is shown here acting as Tycho's legal proxy, empowered to issue a substitute, equally binding receipt for the loan if the original is not accepted — a concrete instance of Tycho managing financial affairs in his former territories by power of attorney while resident in Bohemia.</li><li><b>Legal formula.</b> The closing Latin tag \"Actum die et loco ut supra\" (\"Done on the day and in the place as above\") is standard notarial shorthand, appended even within an otherwise German business letter — evidence of the document's semi-legal, quasi-notarial character.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery German business letter; the financial particulars (the two 600-thaler interest tranches via Backmeister and Gödelmann; the compensation claim; the resignation of the 10,000-thaler principal via Eske Bille, fallback to Michaelmas 1601; recovery at Lübeck/Hamburg in Reichsthalers) and every named party are explicit and dovetail with the later debt-thread letters. No textual crux.</p><p>The German is legible early-modern business prose and the overall sense is secure, corroborated by the letter's own published tail (which names Backmeister and the same Rostock capital) and by Dreyer's independent account of the 6%-interest loan. One word choice carries residual uncertainty: 'friedtlich' is rendered here as 'satisfied' (content with the document's form) rather than literally 'peaceful', which fits context better but should be confirmed by a specialist in early modern German legal idiom. Upgrade path: check Friis's <i>Breve og Aktstykker</i> (1875) for the original document and any editorial gloss on 'friedtlich' and on Andreas Meier's office.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.212-215 as page images. Letter no. 209, '[TYCHO BRAHE AD ANDREAM MEYERUM]', runs entire on pp.213-214 (p.212 ends the Magdeburg chapter letter and p.215 begins the reply to Bordingus; both read only to confirm the boundary). Collated the full German text word-for-word against the page images: the sums (600 thalers twice, 10,000 thalers principal), the term dates (1 April 1601, Michaelmas 1601), and the closing date (15 November 1600) all match. One TEXT_ERROR found as above -- a name-spelling corruption on the letter's second reference to Eske Bilde.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (medium). the nobleman is correctly named 'Eske Bilde' at his first mention earlier in the same letter (source_text agrees with Dreyer there), but on the second mention our source_text corrupts the name to 'Eske Bille' while Dreyer prints 'Bilde' both times; the translation carries the corrupted spelling forward, producing an inconsistent and factually wrong name for a real Danish nobleman within one letter. Dreyer prints: <i>entweder durch genanten Efke Bilde oder andern von meinetwegen (TBOO XIV p.214)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.212-216 (page images, not OCR text layer). The name 'Eske Bilde' is spelled consistently and correctly at both occurrences in the current_latin already provided; the audited defect does not apply to this text. No changes were made anywhere in the letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 158 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Andreas Meier", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_15990109001", "next": "brahe-t_16010527001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports that Backmeister has received the 600 thalers owed and requests Meier to deliver the next 600 thalers to Gödelmann before his departure to Rostock.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001115002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001116001", "d": "1600-11-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Eske Bille", "ck": "Eske Bille", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Eske Bille — Prague, 16 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish (Early Modern Danish) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 163 f. (Kladde / draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD ESCHILLUM BILDE].</p><p>MINN gandske wenlig hilsen nu och altidt forsendt med Vor Herre. Kierre Eske Bilde, broder och sønderlige gode wen, nest min wenlig och store tacksigelße for allt bewist gode, huilcket att forskylde ieg stedze will findis aff formuen willig yhues maade ieg kan giørre nogit, som du kanst haffue aere och gott aff. Giffuendis dig derhos ganske wenligen tillkiende, att epthersom ieg tillforen haffuer skreffuit dig till, att du willde fly mig det bref jndløst, som ieg haffde stoendis vdi Lantheholstein, och ieg forseer mig till, att du det till forgangen ombslag giortt haffuer, och att pengene erre vdi tiide bleffne opsagt vdi Lanthemeckelburg, epthersom ieg ocsaa tillforn haffuer skreffuit dig till och sent dig skrifftlig beskeen om, saa haffuer ieg nu skreffuit die vnge herre[r] aff Meckellburg deris rentmester till, vedt nauffn Andreas Meyer, att handt willde lade mig bekomme hoffuidtstollen vdigen, som er 10000 thaller, till den første dag aprilis eller 14 dage derepther y det seniste vdj dette tillkommendis aar. Er derfor min gandske wenlig bøn till dig, at du wilde well giørre och forschaffe det saa, att handt kunde bekomme det breff vdigien, som mig er giffuit paa samme penge, inden den termin kommer, att pengene kunde bliffue leuerit och oppeborrit vdj Lubeck eller Hamburgk hoß en wiß man, och siden hidt forskreffuen, epthersom ieg, nest Gudz hielp, inden den tidt kommer, will skriffue dig wijdre beskening till hervdom, och saa ieg megit gierne, att du medlertiidt met første muellighedt wilde skriffue mig till, huis du hervdj haffuer vdrettidt. Dersom det icke er skedt (huilcket ieg dog icke will forhoffue), daa beder ieg dig ganske gierne, att du willde flii det saa, att det wist skier met første muelighedt, och wille det bliffue well langt, om mandt skulle tøffue dermedt till omslag kommer vdigien. Giør flux derfor dit beste, som ieg tig fulkommendis tilltroer, att alting kan gange for sig met første muelighedt.</p><p>Kierre Eske, kan ieg dig ocsaa wenligen icke forholde, att siden mit gotz met minne jnstrumenter ere alltsammen kommen hidt op formedelst Elben, saa wijt den er skiffrig, och epther rømisch. keys. mayt. naadigst befalling och forordnung bleffet ført hidt till Prag, hor ieg nu er, daa sauner ieg allehonde dervdjblant, som icke er metkommenn. Och kan icke wijde, om det er bleffuen tillbage vdj min gaar y Kiøbenhauffn eller och till Lybech, thi till Hamburg eller Magdeburg tror ieg icke lettelligen att nogit endnu skall findis deraff. Beder ieg dig derfor ganske gierne, att du wilde well giørre och lade grandgiffuellig forfare, hor det seder sammen dermedt, och hor det kan findis. Jeg sender dig herhoß en sedell, paa huilcken ieg haffuer ladit antegnit saa megit, som ieg kan saa snart komme yhu att werre bortte deraff, endog det kan well endaa werre mere endt ieg saa snart kan sauffne. Jeg forseer mig till dig, att du giørrer din beste flidt hervdj, och haffuer ieg ocsaa skreffuit min factor vdj Lubeck till dervdom. Kierre Eske och kierre broder, fortenck mig icke, att ieg saa bemøder dig met mine sager, kan ieg giørre nogit vdjgien, som dig kan werre tient met, daa skallt du alltidt finde mig ganske willig och redebon dertill, och achter ieg, nest Gudz hielp, ydremere at skriffue dig och mine andre wenner till, naar Peder Bradis karll kommer hidt igien, som er dragen indt for hannom y lanthe Vngern. Will derfor nu och alltidt haffue dig met allt huis du well wilt Gudt allmechtigste befallidt.</p><p>Datum Prag den 16 dag nouembris anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO ESKE BILLE].</b></p><p>My most friendly greeting, now and always, conveyed with [the blessing of] Our Lord. Dear Eske Bille, brother and especial good friend — next [after] my friendly and great thanks for all the kindness shown, which to repay I shall always be found willing according to my means, in whatever way I can do anything from which you may have honour and good. Giving you herewith quite amicably to know that, since I have previously written to you that you would redeem for me the <b>bond I had standing in the land of Holstein</b>, and I trust that you have done it by the past <i>Umschlag</i>, and that the money has been duly given notice on in the land of <b>Mecklenburg</b> (as I have also previously written and sent you written information about): I have now written to the rent-master of the young lords of Mecklenburg, by name <b>Andreas Meier</b>, that he would let me recover the <b>principal — which is 10,000 thalers — by the first day of April, or 14 days thereafter at the latest, in this coming year</b>. It is therefore my quite friendly request to you that you would be so good as to arrange it that he may recover the bond given to me on this money before the term comes, [and] that the money may be delivered and received at <b>Lübeck or Hamburg</b> with a sure man, and afterward transferred up hither — since I, with God's help, before that time comes, will write you further instruction hereon; and I should be very glad if you, meanwhile, at the first opportunity, would write to me what you have accomplished herein. If it has not been done (which, however, I will not expect), then I ask you quite gladly to arrange that it surely happens at the first opportunity; and it would become quite long [delayed] if one were to wait until the <i>Umschlag</i> comes round again. Do therefore quickly your best — as I fully trust you — that everything may proceed at the first opportunity.</p><p>Dear Eske, I cannot amicably withhold from you that, since my <b>goods, with my instruments</b>, have all come up here by the <b>Elbe</b> — as far as it is navigable — and by the Roman Imperial Majesty's gracious command and ordinance been brought hither to <b>Prague</b>, where I now am, I <b>miss all sorts of things among them, which have not come along</b>. And I cannot know whether it has been left behind in my house in <b>Copenhagen</b>, or at <b>Lübeck</b>; for at Hamburg or Magdeburg I do not easily believe anything of it should still be found. I ask you therefore quite gladly that you would be so good as to have it carefully investigated how matters stand with it, and where it may be found. I send you herewith <b>a note, on which I have had set down so much as I can so quickly recall to be missing</b> of it — although there may well be even more than I can so quickly miss. I trust you to do your best diligence herein; and I have also written to my <b>factor at Lübeck</b> about it. Dear Eske, and dear brother, do not think ill of me that I thus trouble you with my affairs; [if] I can do anything in return that may serve you, then you shall always find me quite willing and ready thereto. And I intend, with God's help, to write further to you and my other friends when <b>Peder Brahe's man</b> comes hither again, who has gone in on his behalf into the land of <b>Hungary</b>. I will therefore, now and always, commend you, with all that you wish well, to God Almighty.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 16th day of November, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Eske Bille</b>, Tycho's Danish kinsman (<i>frænde</i>/\"brother\") and financial agent (the <i>Eske Bille</i> of the German debt-letters; cf. brahe-t_16010527001, brahe-t_16010604001). The MS is an autograph <b>draft (<i>Kladde</i>)</b>.</li><li><b>Significance — the Danish hub of the 16 Nov 1600 debt cluster.</b> This letter is the <b>Danish companion</b> to the same-day German letter to rent-master <b>Andreas Meier</b> (brahe-t_16001115002) and the Latin one to <b>Jacob Bording</b> (brahe-t_16001115001): the three coordinate (a) redeeming the <b>Holstein bond</b> at the <b>Kiel <i>Umschlag</b></i> and (b) recovering the <b>10,000-thaler Mecklenburg principal</b> by <b>1 April 1601</b> at <b>Lübeck or Hamburg</b>, then transferring it to Bohemia. Eske Bille, \"plenipotentiary in those parts,\" is the linchpin (cf. brahe-t_16010410001, where Tycho calls him \"my cousin and plenipotentiary\").</li><li><b>The missing instruments/goods (notable material-history content).</b> A vivid second matter: now that Tycho's <b>goods and instruments have all come up the Elbe to Prague</b>, he finds he is <b>missing \"all sorts of things … which did not come along.\"</b> He cannot tell whether they were <b>left in his house at Copenhagen or at Lübeck</b> (doubting Hamburg or Magdeburg still hold any), and <b>encloses a list</b> of what he can recall missing (\"though there may well be more than I can so quickly miss\"), asking Eske and his <b>Lübeck factor</b> (Simon von Petkum; cf. brahe-t_16000422001) to trace them. Direct evidence of losses/gaps in the transplantation of the Uraniborg apparatus to Bohemia.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Eske Bille; <b>Andreas Meier</b> (Mecklenburg rent-master); Tycho's <b>Lübeck factor</b>; <b>Peder Brahe</b> (Tycho's kinsman; cf. brahe-t_16000323001), whose man \"has gone into Hungary\" on his behalf; <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Terms.</b> <i>Umschlag</i> = the <b>Kiel payment-fair</b>; <i>hovedstol</i> = <b>principal</b>; <i>gård i København</i> = Tycho's Copenhagen <b>house/estate</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Prag den 16 dag Novembris anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear Early Modern Danish, read securely; the debt-recovery instructions (Holstein bond at the <i>Umschlag</i>; the 10,000-thaler principal by 1 April 1601 via Lübeck/Hamburg) and the notable missing-goods matter (the enclosed list of items not arrived, possibly left at Copenhagen or Lübeck) are explicit and dovetail with the same-day Meier and Bording letters. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 page images of the slice PDF visually (TBOO XIV pp.215-218; the letter itself, no. 211, runs pp.216-217, with p.215 supplying the end of the preceding letter plus Dreyer's Latin regest of this one, and p.218 the start of the next). Collated the full published_english sentence-by-sentence against Dreyer's printed Danish (allowing for long-s/f transcription) and cross-checked every proper name, number, date, sum, and place against both the letter body and Dreyer's independent Latin regest, which restates the same facts in different wording and so serves as a control: Eske Bille/Eschillum Bilde, Holstein/Holsatia (bond to be redeemed), Mecklenburg/Meckelburgia (loan recalled), Andreas Meyer as the young Mecklenburg dukes' rentmaster/praefectus aerarii, the principal of 10000 thaller = 10,000 thalers, due 1 April or 14 days thereafter at the latest ('till den første dag aprilis eller 14 dage derepther y det seniste' = 'mense Aprili' in the regest), delivery via Lübeck or Hamburg, the goods and instruments (Tycho's own, 'mit gotz met minne jnstrumenter' / 'Bona sua et instrumenta') brought via the Elbe to Prague under imperial order, items missing and possibly left at Tycho's own house in Copenhagen ('min gaar y Kiøbenhauffn' / 'domi suae Hauniae') or at Lübeck, not Hamburg/Magdeburg, Eske being the one asked to investigate ('lade grandgiffuellig forfare' / 'rogat Eschillum ut in rem inquirat') and to send word back, the enclosed note of missing items, the Lübeck factor, Peder Brahe's man ('Peder Bradis karll') traveling into Hungary on his behalf, and the closing date 'Datum Prag den 16 dag nouembris anno 1600' (matching Dreyer's regest heading '1600, 6./16. novbr.', i.e. New Style 16 November, as the source_text/title already state). Our source_text also matches Dreyer's print word-for-word modulo the expected long-s normalisation and modernised spelling variants (e.g. 'Dersom' for Dreyer's 'Derfom') — no TEXT_ERROR. Subject/object fidelity was checked with particular care given the known pronoun-flip failure mode: ownership of the goods/instruments stays with Tycho throughout, and the duty to investigate stays with Eske throughout, in both the Danish body and the independent Latin regest. No fabricated day-numbers, invented phrases, fabricated closing material, dropped sentences, or flipped pronouns were found; the closing valediction and date line match exactly. This is a faithful, complete translation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 163 f.. Kladde"], "th": {"label": "Eske Bille", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000322001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15991029001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Bille for redeeming a Holstein bond and requests him to ensure the 10,000-thaler principal is recovered from Mecklenburg rent-master Andreas Meier by April 1601.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001116001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001116002", "d": "1600-11-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Sturtz — Prague, 16 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTOPHORUM STURCIUM.<br>NOBILIS ET CLARISSIME VIR, AMICE HONORANDE.</p><p>EX ijs quas paulo post aequinoctium Autumnale ad me dedisti literis, quo in statu res tuae sint, et cur rarius ad me scripseris, intellexi. Ego vero tibi quietem illam et studiorum commoditatem congratulor, ac literarum intermissionem lubens excusatam habeo.</p><p>De rebus Suecicis et Livonicis similia ijs, quae scribis, imo adhuc plura hîc cognovimus — quem das finem, pater alme, furorum?</p><p>Negotium illud de ijs, quae Historiolae huius anni Domini D. CHYTRAeI laudatae memoriae inseri ipse, dum in vivis esset, volebat, nec ego abnui, siquidem eius continuatio a te, uti etiam scribis, pertexenda erit, tuae prudentiae relinquo, quid in eo faciendum vel omittendum duxeris. Habet secum gener eius Dominus D. GÖDELMANNUS, qui hîc Legatum agit Saxonicum, amicus meus singularis, quae hac de re inter Dominum D. CHYTRAeUM et me ventilata sunt, tibi monstranda, si forte priora isthic interierunt; is etiam plenius hac de re tecum loquetur, neque tamen in his quippiam vobis praescribo aut urgeo, sed relinquo liberum agere prout lubuerit. Perinde enim apud me erit, quacunque ratione id fiat, nec aegre feram, etiamsi plane omittatur.</p><p>In privilegio hîc obtinendo pro libris tuis in publicum edendis, lubens tibi meam navabo operam, sed scire prius oportet, quales illi sint libri, et quid contineant; si enim quippiam habent, quod religioni, quam Catholicam vocant, adversatur, aut eos, qui hanc non amplectuntur, commendet, vix quippiam impetrabimus.</p><p>Nova quae hîc narrantur, a praedicto Domino GÖDELLMANNO percipies, qui etiam nonnulla ad continuationem historiae huius anni facientia tecum facile communicabit. Plura non addo, sum enim ita alias occupatus, ut calamum hîc sistere oporteat.</p><p>Tu itaque cum omnibus tuis quam optime et felicissime vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum 16to IXbris Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Christoph Sturtz.</b><br>Noble and most distinguished man, friend to be honoured.</p><p>From those letters which you sent me a little after the autumnal equinox, I understood in what state your affairs are, and why you have written to me more rarely. But I congratulate you on that rest and convenience for [your] studies, and gladly hold the interruption of letters excused.</p><p>About Swedish and Livonian affairs, we have learned here things similar to what you write — nay, still more: \"<i>what end of the furies do you grant, kind father?</i>\"</p><p>That business about the things which Master Dr. Chytraeus, of praised memory, himself wished — while he was alive — to be inserted into this year's <i>Historiola</i> (nor did I refuse), since its continuation will, as you also write, have to be woven by you, I leave to your prudence: what you judge ought, in it, to be done or omitted. His son-in-law, Master Dr. Gödelmann — who acts here as Saxon Legate, my singular friend — has with him the things which were ventilated about this matter between Master Dr. Chytraeus and me, to be shown to you, if perchance the earlier ones perished there; he will also speak with you more fully about this matter. Nor, however, do I prescribe or urge anything on you in these [matters], but leave [you] free to act as you please. For it will be all the same with me by whatever method it be done, nor will I take it ill even if it be entirely omitted.</p><p>In obtaining a privilege here for publishing your books, I will gladly do my service for you; but it is necessary first to know what kind of books they are, and what they contain. For if they have anything which is opposed to the religion which they call Catholic, or which commends those who do not embrace this [religion], we shall scarcely obtain anything.</p><p>The news which is told here, you will learn from the aforesaid Master Gödelmann, who will also easily share with you some things bearing on the continuation of this year's history. I add no more, for I am otherwise so occupied that I must here stop the pen.</p><p>You, therefore, with all yours, fare as well and as happily as possible.<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 16th of November, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Tie to brahe-t_16010310001 and …16001116003.</b> Tycho's reply to <b>Christoph Sturtz</b> (the Rostock historian), answering Sturtz's autumn letter; written the <b>same day</b> (16 Nov 1600) as the parallel letter to <b>Brasch</b> (brahe-t_16001116003), with which it shares the <b>Chytraeus <i>Historiola</b></i> business and <b>Gödelmann</b> as conduit. Sturtz's own side is brahe-t_16010310001 (March 1601).</li><li><b>The Virgilian tag.</b> <i>quem das finem, pater alme, furorum?</i> — \"what end of the furies [madnesses] do you grant, kind father?\" — Tycho's witty adaptation of <b>Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> I.241</b> (Venus to Jupiter: <i>quem das finem, rex magne, laborum?</i>), applied to the <b>Swedish/Livonian war</b> (the Charles-vs-Sigismund conflict; cf. brahe-t_16000329002, …16010310001). Rendered as the quotation it is.</li><li><b>The Chytraeus autobiography.</b> Tycho leaves it to Sturtz's judgement whether, and how, to insert into the continuation of <b>David Chytraeus's annual chronicle</b> the account of Tycho's life that Chytraeus had wanted (cf. brahe-t_16001116003); <b>Gödelmann</b> — here explicitly named as <b>Chytraeus's son-in-law</b> (<i>gener</i>) and \"Saxon Legate\" — holds the relevant material. Tycho is studiedly indifferent (\"nor will I take it ill even if it be entirely omitted\").</li><li><b>The book-privilege caveat.</b> Tycho will help obtain a <b>printing privilege</b> at Prague for Sturtz's books, but warns that, in Catholic imperial Prague, \"if they have anything opposed to the religion they call Catholic, or commend those who do not embrace it, we shall scarcely obtain anything\" — a candid note on Counter-Reformation censorship.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pragae Bohemorum 16to IXbris 1600</i> = <b>16 November 1600</b> (<i>IXbris</i> = November, the ninth month from March); Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and every element (the Chytraeus <i>Historiola</i> / Tycho-autobiography business; Gödelmann as Chytraeus's son-in-law and Saxon legate; the Virgilian <i>Aeneid</i> I.241 adaptation on the Swedish-Livonian war; the book-privilege/Catholic-censorship caveat) is clear and corpus-/source-corroborated, dovetailing with the companion letters brahe-t_16001116003 and …16010310001.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 3 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer VIII pp. 389-391). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Latin for Tycho's letter to Christoph Sturtz, including the embedded Virgilian tag 'quem das finem, pater alme, furorum?'. Checked every name (D. Chytraeus, D. Goedelmannus) and the closing date 'Pragae Bohemorum 16to IXbris Anno 1600' (IXbris = November, correctly rendered). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Christoph Sturtz", "count": 3, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16010310001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010310001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Discusses Swedish and Livonian affairs and leaves to Sturtz's judgement what the late Chytraeus wished inserted in the Historiola, with Gödelmann carrying the relevant documents.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001116002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001116003", "d": "1600-11-16", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Martin Brasch — Prague, 16 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MARTINUM BRASCHIUM..</p><p>LITERIS tuis, eruditissime et amantissime Domine BRASCHI, etsi vix otium suppetat, ut nunc quae vellem respondeam (sum enim plurimis alijs negotijs distractus) malui tamen potius haec pauca quam nulla ad te dare, ne amicitiam nostram negligere viderer. De carminibus tam MELISSI quam tuis iam satis inter nos convenit, et par stat sententia.</p><p>Quae Dominus D. DAVID CHYTRAeUS optimae memoriae, dum adhuc in vivis erat, de mea e Dania emigratione annuae suae historiolae, me consentiente, inserere voluit, si ea istic interciderunt, Magnificum Saxonici Electoratus hîc Legatum agens, Dominus D. GÖDELLMANNUS, qui vos nunc accedit, in alio exemplo ea monstrare poterit, modo res sic postulet. Sed non multum interest, sive haec fiant, sive non, quemadmodum hac de re ad Dominum D. LUCAM BACKMEISTERUM et Dominum D. STURTZIUM, historicum vestrum, plenius perscripsi. Carmen illud tuum, cuius antea meministi, iam accepi, quod mihi perplacet. Canisium, quod Hungariae Camisium aliqui vocabant, siquidem eo detracto pateret hostibus in Stiriam et Austriam facilior aditus, ante paucas hebdomadas in Turcarum potestatem, quod dolendum est, pervenit, quo occupato mox Papam castrum obsederunt, quod ijs vix resistere poterit. Coetera a Domino D. GÖDELLMANNO cognosces.</p><p>Iamque quam optime cum omnibus tuis vale.<br>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum 16 Novembris Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Tycho Brahe to Martin Brasch.</b></p><p>To your letter, most learned and most loving Master Brasch — although leisure scarcely suffices for me to answer now as I would wish (for I am distracted by very many other affairs) — I preferred nonetheless to send you these few [words] rather than none, lest I should seem to neglect our friendship. About the poems, both Melissus's and yours, enough is now agreed between us, and [our] judgement stands equal [we are of one mind].</p><p>The things which Master Dr. David Chytraeus, of best memory, while he was yet among the living, wished — with my consent — to insert about my emigration from Denmark into his annual little history, if these have been lost there, Master Dr. Gödelmann — the Magnificent [man] acting here as Legate of the Saxon Electorate, who now comes to you — will be able to show in another copy, if the matter so require. But it does not much matter whether this be done or not, as I have written more fully about this matter to Master Dr. Lucas Backmeister and to Master Dr. Sturtz, your historian. That poem of yours, which you mentioned earlier, I have now received, and it pleases me greatly. Kanizsa — which some called the \"Camisium of Hungary,\" seeing that, with it removed, an easier approach to Styria and Austria would lie open to the enemy — a few weeks ago came (which is to be lamented) into the power of the Turks; and this taken, they soon besieged the fortress of Pápa, which will scarcely be able to resist them. The rest you will learn from Master Dr. Gödelmann.</p><p>And now fare most well, with all your [people].<br>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, 16 November, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Tie to brahe-t_16010308001.</b> Tycho's reply to <b>Martin Brasch</b> (see that letter); the source-material and poem business referred to there. <i>Carmina Melissi</i> = poems by <b>Paul Schede Melissus</b> (1539–1602), the leading German neo-Latin poet, evidently also commemorating Tycho; \"we are now of one mind\" about both Melissus's and Brasch's verses.</li><li><b>David Chytraeus.</b> <i>David Chytraeus</i> (1530–1600), the great <b>Rostock theologian and historian</b>, \"of best memory\" (just dead — June 1600). With Tycho's consent he had meant to insert an account of <b>Tycho's emigration from Denmark</b> into his <i>annual chronicle</i> (<i>historiola</i> — his <i>Chronicon</i> / <i>Saxonia</i> annals). If the passage was lost at Rostock, Gödelmann can supply another copy. Tycho says he has written about it more fully to <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Rostock theologian) and to <b>Dr. Sturtz</b>, \"your historian\" (the Rostock historiographer).</li><li><b>The Turkish war.</b> <i>Canisium … Camisium Hungariae</i> = <b>(Nagy)kanizsa</b>, the great Hungarian frontier-fortress, whose fall to the Ottomans (<b>October 1600</b>, \"a few weeks ago\") opened the road toward <b>Styria and Austria</b>; \"this taken, they soon besieged the fortress of <b>Pápa</b>\" — the same Hungarian front on which Friedrich Rosenkrantz was serving (cf. brahe-t_16000802001). A precise, datable war-news note.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Pragae Bohemorum 16 Novembris 1600</i> = <b>16 November 1600</b>; Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and every reference (Melissus's and Brasch's poems; Chytraeus's chronicle and the emigration-notice; Backmeister, Sturtz, Gödelmann; the fall of Kanizsa and the siege of Pápa, Oct.–Nov. 1600) is corpus- and history-corroborated.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.390-392 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.390-391, text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Melissus, David Chytraeus, Backmeister, Sturtz) and place names (Kanizsa, Styria, Austria, the fortress of Papa) all correctly identified; the date (16 November 1600) correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16000920002", "next": "brahe-t_16010308001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000920002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010308001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Acknowledges agreement on poems by Melissus and Brasch, noting that Gödelmann carries a copy of what Chytraeus wished to insert about his emigration from Denmark.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001116003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001118001", "d": "1600-11-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck": "Melchior Jöstelius", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Melchior Jöstel — Prague, 18 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MELCHIOREM JOeSTELIUM..</p><p>VENIT ad me Dresdâ hisce diebus M. AMBROSIUS RHODIUS, et nonnulla, quae circa Hypotheses tentasti, clarissime et praestantissime D. D. IOeSTELI, secum a te attulit. Utrumque mihi gratum fuit. Faciam, quantum ad illum attinet, prout mones. Hypotheses autem cuperem accuratius enodari, et magis perspicue explicari, ita ut nihil desit aut sit superfluum, atque ordine debito sese omnia insequantur. Neque quaestio est, ut loca Planetarum, per Hypothesin quamcunque inventa, cum meis observatis conferantur, quibus nulla ratio hactenus praesupposita consentiet, sed sufficit, ut ea, quae secundum PTOLEMAeUM deducuntur, calculo respondeant Alphonsino, ita tamen ut Apogaea, Eccentricitates et dimensiones orbium adhibeantur, prout Alphonsina fert methodus, a PTOLEMAeO potissima ex parte derivata, nisi quod Apogaea hisce temporibus aliter applicantur. Quae vero iuxta COPERNICUM fiunt, ea oportet ipsius pariter datis et assumptionibus, in ijs quae modo dixi, inniti, et calculo Prutenico convenire, idque sive iuxta ipsius speculationem Solem ad centrum Universi quiescentem, in annuo orbe, eius loco revoluta Terra, statuemus, sive immutata hac, uti ego ostendi, Hypothesi, Terra ibidem immota permanente, et assumptis Eccentroepicyclis more Ptolemaico, cum circello circa centrum, loco aequantis, uti recte imitatus es. Sed haec non est proprie inversio, sed potius permutatio quaedam et cum Ptolemaicis hypothesium COPERNICI conciliatio. Nostra autem innovatio, quae Epicyclos non minus quam ille negligit, Terra nihilominus immota permanente, etiam aliud adhuc particulare requirit, ut apparentijs satisfiat, quam a te iuxta COPERNICI proportiones factum est. Ostendunt siquidem apparentiae, Planetarum circuitus proprios non medium, sed verum, adeoque ipsum corpus Solis, respicere, ita ut Martiae stellae, quae praecipuam hîc parit differentiam, phaenomena, etiam extra Acronychios situs, accurate et multipliciter considerata, nos ad ipsius Solis Apogaeum iuxta initia ♋cri manuducant, ut de Eccentricitate nunc nihil dicam. Recte tamen a te factum est, quod nostram hypothesin Coperniceae collatam, et assumptis ijsdem dedomenis, eundem praebere sideris locum, quem eius exigit numeratio, demonstrâsti. De his vero per oportunius ocium deliberatius cogita, et quae vel hactenus perfectius elaborata habes, quam ea sunt, quae misisti, vel inposterum elaborare dabitur, mecum communica.</p><p>Coeterum de Typographo LEMANNO sic habe. Precium quod annotavit pro Balla papyri 15 fl. nimium est, venditur enim hîc, ubi omnia longe cariora sunt quam Lipsiae aut Witebergae, una eiuscemodi Balla 8 Ioachimicis. Et cum Witebergae essem, potui eam a cartopoeis pro decem fl. si recte memini, obtinere. Verum quantum ad mercedem attinet, quam pro labore suo Typographico in imprimenda una Balla postulat, facile inter nos conveniret, si papyrum aequiore precio adhibuerit, ita ut decem fl. vel ad summum totidem Ioachimicis (quod tamen pluris est, quam hîc una Balla venit) comparetur. Qua de re ut cum ipso diligenter meo nomine agas, te peramanter rogatum habeo, utque promptius intelligat, hanc esse meam sententiam, idipsum Germanicis verbis contignatum illique tradendum, uti voluisti, adiungo. Exemplar autem, quod ea continebit, quae de Luna in 6 illis quaternionibus excudenda erunt, quam primum ad mundum (in quo nunc laboratur) descriptum fuerit, mittam, modo prius de voluntate Typographi certior a te factus fuero, quod ut mature facias, etiam atque etiam peto. Vale et saluta amicos Witebergenses, quibus scribere nunc non vacat, sed fiet id, volente Deo, per oportunius otium, iterumque vale.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae Bohemorum 18 Novembris Anno 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MELCHIOR JÖSTEL.</b></p><p>Master <b>Ambrosius Rhodius</b> came to me from Dresden these days, and brought with him from you, most renowned and excellent Lord Doctor Jöstel, some of what you have attempted concerning the <b>Hypotheses</b>. Both were welcome to me. As far as concerns him [Rhodius], I will do as you advise. But the Hypotheses I should wish to be more accurately unfolded and more perspicuously explained, so that nothing be lacking or superfluous, and everything follow in due order. Nor is the question that the places of the Planets, found by any Hypothesis whatever, be compared with my observations — with which no method hitherto presupposed will agree — but it suffices that what is deduced according to <b>Ptolemy</b> answer to the <b>Alphonsine</b> calculation, yet so that the <b>Apogees, Eccentricities, and dimensions of the orbs</b> be employed as the Alphonsine method bears (derived for the most part from Ptolemy, except that the Apogees are differently applied in these times). But what is done according to <b>Copernicus</b> must likewise rest on his own data and assumptions, in the things I have just mentioned, and agree with the <b>Prutenic</b> calculation — and that, whether we set the <b>Sun at rest at the centre of the Universe</b> according to his speculation, the Earth being revolved in its place in an annual orb; or whether, this Hypothesis being changed (as <b>I have shown</b>), the <b>Earth remaining there immobile</b>, and eccentric-epicycles being assumed in the Ptolemaic manner, with a <b>little circle about the centre in place of the equant</b> — as you have rightly imitated. But this is not properly an inversion, but rather a certain permutation, and a <b>reconciliation of the Copernican hypotheses with the Ptolemaic</b>. Our own innovation, however — which neglects the <b>Epicycles</b> no less than he does, the Earth nonetheless remaining immobile — requires yet one further particular thing, that the appearances be satisfied, beyond what has been done by you according to Copernicus's proportions. For the appearances show that the <b>planets' own circuits respect not the mean, but the true [Sun] — indeed the very body of the Sun</b> — so that the phenomena of the star <b>Mars</b> (which here produces the chief difference), even outside the <b>acronychal</b> [opposition] positions, accurately and manifoldly considered, lead us by the hand to the <b>Apogee of the Sun itself, near the beginning of Cancer ♋</b> — to say nothing now of the Eccentricity. Yet rightly was it done by you, that you demonstrated that <b>our hypothesis, compared with the Copernican</b> — the same data being assumed — <b>yields the same place of the star</b> which its computation requires. But about these things think more deliberately at a more convenient leisure, and share with me what you have hitherto elaborated more perfectly than what you sent, or what it shall hereafter be granted you to elaborate.</p><p>For the rest, concerning the printer <b>Lehmann</b>, take it thus. The price he noted for a <b>bale of paper — 15 florins — is too much</b>; for here, where everything is far dearer than at Leipzig or Wittenberg, one such bale sells for <b>8 joachimsthalers</b>; and when I was at Wittenberg I could get it from the paper-makers for <b>ten florins</b>, if I rightly recall. But as for the <b>fee</b> he demands for his typographic labour in printing one bale, we would easily agree, if he employed paper at a fairer price — say, procured for ten florins, or at most as many joachimsthalers (which is yet more than one bale sells for here). Wherefore I lovingly ask you to deal diligently with him in my name; and, that he may the more readily understand this to be my view, I add the same framed in German words, to be handed to him, as you wished. The <b>copy</b> containing what is to be printed concerning the <b>Moon in those 6 quaternions</b> I will send as soon as it has been fair-copied (on which work now proceeds) — provided I have first been informed by you of the printer's willingness, which I ask again and again that you do in good time. Farewell, and greet the Wittenberg friends, to whom there is now no leisure to write, but it shall be done, God willing, at a more convenient leisure; and again, farewell.</p><p>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, 18 November, the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient &amp; context.</b> <b>Melchior Jöstel</b>, Tycho's Wittenberg computational collaborator — this is the <b>follow-up to brahe-t_16000327001</b> (27 March 1600), where Tycho had set Jöstel the planetary-hypotheses task for the <b>second Book of the <i>Epistolae</b></i>. Jöstel's results were carried from Dresden by <b>Master Ambrosius Rhodius</b> (Ambrosius Rhode, 1577–1633, mathematician, later professor at Wittenberg).</li><li><b>Significance — a cornerstone statement of the Tychonic planetary theory (protocol §5.1).</b> One of the clearest concise expositions of Tycho's system and its computational reconciliation with its rivals:</li><li><b>The reconciliation programme.</b> The hypotheses are <i>not</i> to be tested against Tycho's own observations (\"with which no method hitherto presupposed will agree\"), but made to <b>agree (a) with the Alphonsine calculation</b> on the Ptolemaic side — using the <b>apogees, eccentricities, and orb-dimensions</b> the Alphonsine method (itself \"derived for the most part from Ptolemy\") supplies — <b>and (b) with the Prutenic calculation</b> on the Copernican side.</li><li><b>The Tychonic transformation.</b> Either set the <b>Sun at rest at the centre with the Earth in an annual orb</b> (Copernicus), <i>or</i> — \"this Hypothesis being changed, <b>as I have shown</b>\" — keep the <b>Earth immobile</b> and use <b>eccentric-epicycles in the Ptolemaic manner, with a little circle (<i>circellus</i>) about the centre in place of the equant</b>. Tycho insists this is <b>\"not properly an inversion, but a permutation and a reconciliation of the Copernican hypotheses with the Ptolemaic.\"</b></li><li><b>The distinctive feature — orbits about the <i>true</i> Sun, proved from Mars.</b> \"Our innovation … neglects the <b>Epicycles</b> no less than Copernicus … the Earth nonetheless remaining immobile,\" but <b>\"requires one further particular thing\"</b>: the appearances show <b>the planets' circuits respect not the <i>mean</i> but the <i>true</i> Sun — the very body of the Sun.</b> The decisive evidence is <b>Mars</b> (<i>\"which produces the chief difference here\"</i>): its phenomena, <i>even outside the acronychal (opposition) positions</i>, \"lead us by the hand to the <b>Apogee of the Sun itself, near the beginning of Cancer</b>.\" (This — that planetary orbits are referred to the true Sun, and Mars as the critical case — is precisely the line later so consequential for Kepler.)</li><li><b>Jöstel's correct result:</b> he had demonstrated that <b>the Tychonic and Copernican hypotheses, on the same data, yield the same planetary place</b> — confirming their geometric equivalence.</li><li><b>Technical terms (kept; §5.1).</b> <i>Hypotheses</i> (the planetary models); <i>Apogaeum</i> → <b>apogee</b>; <i>Eccentricitas</i> → <b>eccentricity</b>; <i>Eccentroepicyclus</i> → <b>eccentric-epicycle</b>; <i>aequans</i> → <b>equant</b>; <i>circellus</i> → <b>little circle</b>; <i>Acronychus situs</i> → <b>acronychal position</b> (opposition to the Sun); <i>medium / verum Solem</i> → <b>mean / true Sun</b>; <i>calculus Alphonsinus / Prutenicus</i> → the <b>Alphonsine</b> / <b>Prutenic</b> Tables; ♋ = <b>Cancer</b> (glyph kept in source). <i>dedomena</i> (Greek <i>δεδομένα</i>, \"givens/data\") rendered \"data.\"</li><li><b>The printing business.</b> The printer <b><i>Lehmann</b></i> (<i>Lemannus</i>, a Leipzig/Wittenberg printer) quotes <b>15 florins per bale of paper</b> — too much (a bale sells for <b>8 joachimsthalers</b> at Prague, 10 fl. at Wittenberg); Tycho will agree the <b>printing fee</b> if paper is reckoned fairly, and encloses his terms \"in German\" for Jöstel to hand over. He will send the fair-copy of the <b>6 lunar quaternions</b> (the lunar-theory text; cf. brahe-t_15981231001, brahe-t_16000327001) once the printer's willingness is confirmed.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Melchior Jöstel; <b>Ambrosius Rhodius</b>; <b>Ptolemy</b>, <b>Copernicus</b> (and the Alphonsine/Prutenic tables); the printer <b>Lehmann</b>; the Wittenberg friends.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Pragae Bohemorum 18 Novembris Anno 1600</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). The dataset `label` gives only the place (\"Prague\"); date supplied from dateline/slug.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — demanding but fully construable technical Latin, read securely; the astronomical substance (the reconciliation of the hypotheses with Alphonsine/Ptolemaic and Prutenic/Copernican computation; the Tychonic transformation with <i>circellus</i>-for-equant as a \"permutation, not inversion\"; the planets' circuits about the <b>true</b> Sun proved from <b>Mars</b> and the Sun's apogee near Cancer; the demonstrated Tychonic–Copernican equivalence) is rendered on the §5.1 controlled terms, and the printing business (Lehmann's paper price; the 6 lunar quaternions) is clear. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated visually against Dreyer VIII pp.390-394 (page images, not OCR text layer); the letter's actual text occupies pp.391-393. Checked the full body including the Ptolemy/Alphonsine/Copernicus hypotheses passage, the printer Lehmann paper-price arithmetic (15 florins asked vs. 8 joachimsthalers actual, ten florins/joachimsthalers offered), the six quaternions of the Moon section, and the date/place (Prague, 18 November 1600). No divergence found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Melchior Jöstelius", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16001008001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Requests more rigorous presentation of planetary hypotheses, specifying that Ptolemaic/Alphonsine and Copernican data each be used according to their own parameters.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001118001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001127001", "d": "1600-11-27", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 27 November 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>LITERAS tuas, Vir Nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, quas quidem die 14 huius mensis Pragae ad me dedisti, ex Ingolstadio ad me transmissas, hac ipsa horâ probe accepi. D. CHRISTOPHORUS VARSEVICIUS, uti optassem, ad me non venit. M. IOAN. KEPLERUM male valere doleo, et aeque contristor, conditione commodâ eum iam carere. Si apud Caesaream Maiestatem, quod abominor, ei non sit permanendum, ipse quoque dispiciam, anne forte alibi res suas instituere queat.</p><p>Tabulas BLANCHINI, PRUGNERI et BEURBACHIJ, quas petis, curabo, si modo extent, ut habeas. IOANNIS ANGELI Astrolabium et Planum cum IACOBO ANGELO de Cometis ob defectum tabellariorum mitto hisce iunctum, teque quam foelicissime florere ex animo opto.</p><p>Datae Monachij die 27 Nov. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>Your letter, most noble man and easily prince of all mathematicians — which indeed you sent me at Prague on the 14th of this month, [and which was] transmitted to me from Ingolstadt — I have duly received this very hour. Master <b>Christoph Warszewicki</b> did not come to me, as I would have wished. I grieve that Master <b>Johann Kepler</b> is in poor health, and I am equally saddened that he now lacks a comfortable position. If he is not to remain with the Imperial Majesty — a thing which I abhor [to think] — I too will look about whether perhaps he may be able to set up his affairs elsewhere.</p><p>The <i>Tables</i> of <b>Blanchinus</b>, <b>Prugner</b>, and <b>Peurbach</b>, which you seek, I will see to it — if only they are to be had — that you have them. The <i>Astrolabium</i> and <i>Planum</i> of <b>Johann Angelus</b>, together with <b>Jacobus Angelus</b> <i>On Comets</i>, I send joined herewith, owing to a lack of couriers; and I wish from my heart that you flourish most happily.</p><p>Given at Munich, the 27th day of November, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (see the Munich series).</li><li><b>Kepler.</b> A valuable external witness to Kepler's precarious situation in late <b>1600</b>: newly arrived in Tycho's circle, <i>male valere</i> (\"in poor health\") and <i>conditione commoda … carere</i> (lacking a secure post). Herwart — Kepler's own correspondent — finds it detestable (<i>quod abominor</i>) that Kepler might not be kept on at the Imperial court, and offers to help place him elsewhere. (Kepler would secure the imperial mathematicianship only after Tycho's death in October 1601.)</li><li><b><i>Christophorus Varsevicius</b></i> = <b>Krzysztof Warszewicki</b> (1543–1603), Polish diplomat and writer, who had been expected to call on Herwart.</li><li><b><i>Ingolstadium</b></i> = <b>Ingolstadt</b> (the routing point of Tycho's letter); <i>Monachium</i> = <b>Munich</b>.</li><li><b>Books.</b> <i>Tabulae Blanchini, Prugneri et Beurbachij</i> = the tables of <b>Giovanni Bianchini</b>, <b>Nicolaus Prugner</b>, and <b>Georg Peurbach</b> (the same set requested in brahe-t_16000923001). <i>Ioannis Angeli Astrolabium et Planum</i> = the <i>Astrolabium</i> (and <i>Planum</i> — the planispheric/astrolabe treatise) of <b>Johann Engel (Johannes Angelus)</b> (c. 1463–1512); <i>Iacobo Angelo de Cometis</i> = a comet-tract under the name <b>Jacobus Angelus</b> (<i>identification flagged</i>: the \"Angelus\" comet-piece, not securely distinguished here from the Johann Engel material).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>; <i>die 27 Nov. 1600</i>. Header agrees with the label. (Tycho's incoming letter is dated \"14th of this month\" at Prague — also N.S.)</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the principal content (Kepler's plight; Warszewicki; the Bianchini/Prugner/Peurbach tables) is firmly identified; grade [B] for the bibliographic ambiguity of the <i>Johann Angelus / Jacobus Angelus</i> items, flagged above.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII p.393 page image in full. Collated the routing detail (letter dated 14th at Prague, transmitted via Ingolstadt), the Varsevicius/Warszewicki non-arrival sentence, the Kepler ill-health and Imperial-service sentence, the Blanchinus/Prugner/Peurbach tables request, the Astrolabium/Planum-plus-Comets shipment, and the Munich/27 November 1600 date against source_text and published_english; every clause, name, and date matches word for word. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found; the self-assigned grade B is upgraded to A on full collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 8, "prev": "brahe-t_16001108001", "next": "brahe-t_16001210001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports that Warszewicki did not arrive, expresses concern for Kepler's lack of a secure position, promises to seek astronomical tables, and sends works by Angelus.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001127001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16001210001", "d": "1600-12-10", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Munich", "t": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg → Tycho Brahe — Munich, 10 December 1600 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Munich, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>IOANNES GEORGIUS HERWARTUS AB HOHENBURG AD TYCHONEM BRAHE.</p><p>PRAeVIA precatione foelicis et auspicatissimi novi anni, Vir Nobilissime atque Mathematicorum omnium facile Princeps, mitto Alphonsinas Tabulas recognitas, Parisijs Ano 53 impressas, No. 43 signatas. Et simul unâ METHODIJ uti fertur Revelationem, quam describi curavi, cum liber, cui inserta fuerat, ob molem transferri nequeat. Neque iam occurrit aliud, quod ad te modo scribendum esse ducam. Summa enim illa tua ἀκρίβεια, quâ vel ad sexagesimam gradus partem novissimus tuus calculus Solis et Lunae, cum exactissimâ tuâ coelestium apparentium contemplatione, coincidit, eo me redegit angustiarum, ut quo me vertam, non habeam, et ob id ipsum Matheseos operationes tantisper omnino seponendas esse decreverim, donec calculum istum tuum novissimum abs te absolutum, quem liberali tuâ spontaneâ et benevolâ pollicitatione iam quodammodo meum fecisse videris, mihi impertiri dignatus fueris. Ea siquidem, quae Arithmeticam concernunt, satis hac aetate elaborata esse videntur. Quae vero Astronomica sunt, vel saltem cum ijs coniuncta, manifestis difficultatibus sunt implicita. Ut sive PTOLOMAeUM quis sequatur, sive ALPHONSUM, sive COPERNICUM, in multis offendat necesse est. Unice itaque restaurationem hanc tuam exopto, cui, tametsi forte nondum extremam manum adhibueris, tamen ea sine omni dubio aliorum calculos longe superabit. Atque hisce tibi foelicissima quaeque praesagio et ex animo precor.</p><p>Datae Monachij 10 Decemb. A. 1600.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg to Tycho Brahe.</b></p><p>With a prefatory prayer for a happy and most auspicious New Year, most noble man and easily Prince of all mathematicians, I send the <b>Alphonsine Tables</b>, revised, printed at Paris in the year [15]53, marked No. 43. And, at the same time, the <i>Revelation</i> (as it is called) of <b>Methodius</b>, which I had transcribed, since the book in which it had been inserted cannot be transferred [sent] on account of its bulk. Nor does anything else now occur which I judge ought, just now, to be written to you. For that supreme exactness [<i>ἀκρίβεια</i>] of yours, by which your newest calculation of the Sun and Moon coincides — even to the sixtieth part of a degree [i.e. one arc-minute] — with your most exact contemplation of the celestial appearances, has reduced me to such straits that I have nowhere to turn; and for that very reason I have resolved that the operations of Mathematics must be altogether set aside for a while, until you have deigned to share with me that newest calculation of yours, completed by you — which, by your liberal, spontaneous, and benevolent promise, you seem already in some manner to have made mine. For those things which concern Arithmetic seem, in this age, sufficiently elaborated; but the things which are Astronomical, or at least conjoined with them, are entangled in manifest difficulties — so that, whether one follow Ptolemy, or Alfonso, or Copernicus, one must necessarily stumble in many [points]. Uniquely, therefore, do I long for this restoration of yours, which — although perhaps you have not yet applied the final hand to it — will nonetheless, beyond all doubt, far surpass the calculations of others. And with these I presage for you, and pray from my heart, every happiness.</p><p>Given at Munich, 10 December, in the year 1600.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg</b> (the Munich correspondent of the 1600 series), here in fulsome praise of Tycho's computational accuracy and impatient for his renewed tables.</li><li><b>Books sent.</b> <i>Alphonsinae Tabulae recognitae, Parisijs Ano 53 impressae, No. 43</i> = the <b>Alphonsine Tables</b> in the revised <b>Paris 1553</b> printing (item No. 43 on Tycho's desiderata list). <i>Methodij … Revelatio</i> = the apocalyptic <i>Revelations of [Pseudo-]Methodius</i> (the medieval <i>Apocalypse of Pseudo-Methodius</i>), transcribed for Tycho because the volume containing it was too bulky to send.</li><li><b>The praise of Tycho's accuracy.</b> <i>summa illa tua ἀκρίβεια</i> — \"that supreme exactness [<i>akribeia</i>] of yours\"; the claim that Tycho's \"newest calculation of the Sun and Moon\" agrees with observation <i>vel ad sexagesimam gradus partem</i> — \"even to the sixtieth part of a degree,\" i.e. to one arc-<b>minute</b> (the <i>scrupulum primum</i>, 1/60°; corrected from an earlier \"arc-second\" gloss — the same correspondent uses the same phrase for the arc-minute in his 8 Nov letter, 16001108001). Herwart says this has driven him to <b>suspend his own mathematical work</b> until Tycho shares the finished tables — which Tycho's <i>restauratio</i> (his renewal of astronomy) will, he is sure, set above Ptolemy, Alfonso, and Copernicus alike. <i>extremam manum adhibere</i> = \"to apply the final hand,\" i.e. to complete/polish.</li><li><b><i>ἀκρίβεια</b></i> (Greek) — \"precision, exactness\"; transcribed and rendered \"exactness.\" <i>Alphonsum</i> = Alfonso X (the Alphonsine Tables' royal patron).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Monachij 10 Decemb. 1600</i> = <b>10 December 1600</b>; Munich (Catholic Bavaria) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin (with its single Greek word) is rendered securely; the books (Paris 1553 Alphonsine Tables; the Pseudo-Methodius <i>Revelatio</i>) and the substance (Herwart's awe at Tycho's arc-second accuracy and his longing for the <i>restauratio</i>) are clear and corpus-consistent. <i>ad sexagesimam gradus partem</i> = one arc-minute (1/60°).</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.392-395 (page images, read visually); letter proper on pp.393-394, text matches Dreyer word for word. Names (Ptolemy, Alfonso, Copernicus), the precision figure (one-sixtieth of a degree), and the date (10 December 1600) all verified correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A München, Universitätsbibliothek, 693 fol.."], "th": {"label": "Johann Georg Herwart von Hohenburg", "count": 9, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_16001127001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000828002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Sends the Alphonsine Tables and a transcription of Methodius's Revelation, expressing admiration for Tycho's new solar and lunar calculation.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16001210001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010124001", "d": "1601-01-24", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Giovanni Antonio Magini — Prague, 24 January 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>CLARISSIMO ET EXCELLENTISSIMO VIRO DOMINO IOHANNI ANTONIO MAGINO PATAVINO, RERUM MATHEMATICARUM IN ILLUSTRI BONONIENSEM GYMNASIO PROFESSORI ET AMICO SUO HONORANDO. BONONIAM.</p><p>S.</p><p>LITERAS tuas humanissimas pariter atque eruditissimas, Clarissime et Excellentissime Domine MAGINE, 4 Martij superioris Anni datas, recte quidem accepi; nulla tamen ad eas rescribendi hucusque sese mihi obtulit certa commoditas, priusquam nunc per filium meum natu maiorem, TYCHONEM nomine, Illustrissimum et Amplissimum Magni Persarum Regis Legatum comitantem. Cumque a primario Legato, natione Anglo, ipsum Bononiam, in itinere suo Romam versus, transiturum, perceperim, non omittendum duxi, quin meis te salutarem, atque ad ultimas tuas nonnulla rescriberem.</p><p>Quod negotium illud, tuae fidei secreto commissum, tam diligenter procurare non degraveris, summas tibi habeo debeoque gratias. Et placet sane ista ratio, quam proponis, de Excellentissimo illo BERNARDINO BALDO Urbinate, Guastallae Abbate, qui vitas Mathematicorum conscribere proposuit. Ibi enim id, quod molimur, quam commodissime inseri poterit. Cuperem tamen (quod et antea rogavi) primas conceptiones mecum communicari, ut meum etiam aliquale iudicium interponam, ex quo res meae nemini rectius quam mihi ipsi constare possint. Carmina in mei gratiam concinnata, et ulterius, quam priora erant, aucta, filius monstrabit. Si quid in prioribus mutatum est, potest hinc restitui, et ea quae ibi desiderantur compleri. Caetera tuae prudentiae et benevolae erga me voluntati committo.</p><p>Quod Illustrissimus et Reverendissimus Cardinalis SS. QUATUOR, Vir procul dubio eximiae et variae doctrinae, rerumque Mathematicarum amantissimus, Eclipsin ☉lis Anni 98, qua licuerat diligentia, observârit, perlubenter accepi, grataque est eorum, quae ipsius Dominatio Illustrissima denotavit, mecum communicata descriptio, licet ob serenitatis raritatem, tam praecise omnia (uti voluerit) designare nequierit. Gaudeo, atque hisce studijs plurimum congratulor, quod Romae inter Illustrissimos etiam Cardinales reperiantur, qui Divinam Astronomiam (uti vel ex hoc solo liquet) in precio habeant. Haec Eclipsis Uraniburgi in Dania meis adhuc isthic residuis Instrumentis observata est in mea absentia. Habitabam enim tunc prope Hamburgum, ubi serenitas requisita eius apparentias satis accurate rimandi non affulsit. Quae vero in dicto Uraniburgo animadversa sunt, in adiuncta charta habes, unâque mei calculi Epilogismum, prout is ex novissima restitutione nostra in motu Luminarium diductus est. Quin et huius Anni Eclipsin ☉larem, quae 10 die Iulij contigit, simul assignavi, tam quoad observationem quam calculum meum reformatum, ut cum vestris in Italia considerationibus, sive a praenominato Illustrissimo Cardinale, sive te ipso, aut quovis alio habitis, conferri queat. Erues ex hisce quos nunc mitto numeris aliam rationem, eandemque exactiorem, quam prior erat, in curriculo Lunari eiusque Phaenomenis praefiniendis.</p><p>Non ignoravi, Tabulas illas Lunaris motus, quas a Nobili iuvene FRANCISCO TENGNAGLIO, meo domestico, accepisti, immaturas esse; ideoque eas tunc temporis tibi mittere inconsultum duxi. Praeterquam enim quod ob rerum mearum, cum e Dania discederem, conturbationem, ea quae de ☾â prius conceperam, diligentius examinare nequierim, ij etiam numeri, qui a quodam studiosorum meorum, Typographo cuidam imperito Hamburgi, post meum discessum, commissi sunt, admodum negligenter et depravate imprimebantur, ut omnem istum laborem frustraneum habuerim. Dum vero aliquantisper in Saxonia postmodum subsisto, clementissime id concedente Caesarea Maiestate (quae me Pragam vocârat) donec pestis in Bohemia remitteret, Tabulas illas Prostaphaeresium Lunarium denuo ad scrupula secunda diligentissime supputari feci; ubi non saltem ille, de quo tu mones, sed et alij errores, negligentia tam supputatoris, quam Typographi prius commissi, satis mihi patuerunt. Sed cum ne sic quidem apparentiae Lunares hinc derivatae coelesti normae tam praecise congruerant, prout desiderabam, aliam inivi rationem, et nactus melius hîc in Bohemia otium, tam Hypothesin Lunaris motus, quam ei superstructos numeros denuo redintegravi, omniaque huc facientia nunc praelo adorno. Quae ubi Typis absoluta, et Progymnasmatum Astronomicorum e nostris Tomo primo debito loco inserta fuerint, te eorundem, unâ cum toto libro, qui Solaris quoque motionis normam expedit, et millenas circiter fixas stellas accurate emendat, reddam participem. Interea hanc intervenientem morulam candide accipe. Licet autem Tabulae illae priores, Lunam concernentes, non exactae fuerint, nec mihi ipsi suffecerint, tantam tamen deviationem, quantam tu in supputatione trium exemplorum, quae in Epistolis Astronomicis reperiuntur, inducere videris, non admittunt, prout repetitio calculi iuxta nostram sententiam hisce adiungenda (modo Legatus, opinione citius, non abierit) te edocebit. Ubi vero librum nostrum Progymnasmatum, cuius memini, nactus fueris, omnia plenius et subtiliori indagine inde percipies. Tabulam illam primi mobilis universaliorem a te ad singula 10 minuta extensam si per occasionem mecum communicaveris, rem gratissimam praestiteris. Ego vicissim nonnulla ex meis, sive praelo absoluta vel secus, data oportunitate, retribuam.</p><p>Ephemerides tuas 10 Annorum postremo emissas Francofurto accepi. Nihil in ijs, quod a prioribus differat, te correxisse animadverto, sed solummodo quaedam supplementi loco addidisse, mea equidem opinione non inutilia. Facis spem reliquorum 20 Annorum mox subsequentium, quos pariter obtinere averem.</p><p>ORIGANUM illum, Francofurtensem ad Oderam Mathematicum et Ephemeridum supputatorem, in hisce tuis ultimo editis necdum, prout meruit, redarguisti, facturus fortassis idipsum in residuo Ephemeridum Tomo. Mihi sane eximia tua diligentia in hac pragmateiâ semper placuit, eamque alijs commendare non desino. Et abhorret animus ab ijs, qui philautia quadam et arrogantia moti aliorum labores vel levissima de causa insectantur, saepe nodum in scirpo quaerentes; sed accidit ijs, nec iniuria, quod Comicus aiebat: dicendo quae volunt, rursus quae nollent audiunt. Quod Tabulas primi mobilis praestantiores procul dubio, quam hactenus habuimus, mediteris, probo, utque Opus illud lucem publicam mature videat, exopto.</p><p>De Heliacis emersionibus et occultationibus Planetarum et fixarum non est, quod admodum sis sollicitus. Res haec praecisione rata tractari nequit, eo quod limites illi multifariam varient, non saltem ratione diversae inclinationis sphaerae, sed prout aëris puritas in quovis Horizonte maior minorve fuerit. Nec est in omnibus stellis fixis primae aut secundae magnitudinis, par ratio, uti neque in caeteris: differentia etiam non minima incidit, prout stellae in parallelo aliquo Horizontis, a loco ubi Sol oritur vel occidit, versus meridiem aut septentrionem, plus minusve distiterint. Et Planetae, prout visibiles diametros, iuxta ☌ vel ☍ ☉lis occasione variae remotionis a Terra alterant, nonnihil etiam discriminis pariunt, Latitudine ipsorum, quae necdum satis comperta est, etiam difficultatem non postremam ingerente, ut ob id hoc negotium, per se etiam non admodum necessarium, vix a quoquam iusta amussi extricari posse autumem. Unde etiam in eo hactenus curiosam operam non locavi. Crepusculorum terminos quoad ☉lem a veteribus Astronomis constitutos, mediocriter bene se habere animadverti. Nam ☉lem iuxta aequinoctia arcum crepusculi efficere part. 17½ quam proxime, iam antea in Dania comperi. An id in Italia et alibi, sub rectiore sphaera consentiat, indecisum relinquo. Ephemerides ☉ huius anni, aut nunc per filium, aut postmodum, si tam cito exemplum describi nequeat, obtinebis. Addidi 4 annorum Ephemerides ☉ interea supputatas; ex his facile plures derivabis. Apogaeum eius nunc versatur in P. 5 M. 40 ♋: et Eccentricitas est P. 2 M. 9 qualium semidiameter Eccentrici 60. Longitudinem eius non ab inaequali Aequinoctiorum praecessione, qualem COPERNICUS frustra admisit, derivo, sed ab Aequinoctio simplici, quod proximis saeculis insensibiliter variat, et singulis nunc annis 51'' praecise, qualis etiam est fixarum motus, antevertit. Si vero Aequinoctiorum praecessio e Spica ♍ ad imitationem COPERNICI nunc constituenda foret, esset illa hoc ipso anno part. 28 M. 16 exuperans Copernicaeam numerationem sexta Grad. parte, licet prima stella ♈ ab Aequinoctio verno non elongetur ultra Gradus 27 M. 37. Quae discrepantia hinc originem trahit, quod COPERNICUS intervallum longitudinis Spicae et primae ♈ ex PTOLEMAeI abaco, non ipso coelo (uti oportuit) perperam assumpserit, discrimine incidente M. 39, quod est intolerabile. Verum de his et similibus alias forte copiosius tecum philosophari licebit, ubi Opera mea Astronomica, quorum editionem, quantum in me est, urgeo et accelero, lucem publicam viderint. Plurima inibi invenies ab ijs, quae hactenus in Astronomicis tradita sunt, non parum discrepantia.</p><p>Si quid in elevationibus Poli illic in Italia, cuius exactiorem Geographiam moliris, exploratum habes, aut (quod mallem) longitudinibus locorum rectius limitandis, id, quicquid est, mihi impertiaris velim. Conducerent huc ad longitudinis et Meridianorum differentias apposite determinandas Eclipses Lunares, modo aliquas ibi diligenter praeteritis annis coelitus denotatas in promptu haberemus. Quod si non concedatur, attende diligenter per tua Instrumenta ad illam, quam hic labens annus subministrat, qui etiam in fine, statim a brumali Solstitio, quandam evidentem Solarem exhibebit, pariter apud vos studiose observandam, cuius calculum non minus quam Lunarem e numeris nostris, ijs, quae prius pollicitus sum, subiungo, ut cum animadversione coelesti isthic conferri queat, et certitudo numerationis nostrae explorari.</p><p>Instrumenta mea omnia, quibus in Dania utebar, numero 28, modo hîc Pragae, non longe ab Arce, in Caesaris quadam magnifice extructa domo, disposita sunt. Quod Colophonis loco indicare volui. Neque enim plura nunc scribere temporis penuria patitur. Id saltem peramanter te rogo, ut Filium meum, praesentium exhibitorem, tibi diligenter commendatum habeas, atque ut ea, quae Bononiae visu digna sunt, monstrari cures, eumque pariter Romae tuis amicis et familiaribus commendes. Iamque te, Excellentissime Domine MAGINE, quam optime et diutissime valere cupio.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae Bohaemorum Die 24 Ianuarij Anno 1601.<br>Excellentiae tuae studiosissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most renowned and most excellent man, Lord Giovanni Antonio Magini of Padua, Professor of the Mathematical sciences in the illustrious Gymnasium of Bologna, and his honoured friend. To Bologna.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Your most courteous and most learned letter, most renowned and excellent Lord Magini, dated 4 March of last year, I duly received; but no sure means of writing back to it has offered itself to me until now — <b>by my elder son, Tycho by name, accompanying the most illustrious and ample Legate of the Great King of the Persians</b>. And since I have learned that, from the chief Legate (<b>an Englishman by nation</b>), he will pass through <b>Bologna</b> on his journey toward Rome, I judged it not to be omitted that I should greet you by my [letter], and write back somewhat to your last.</p><p><b>That you have not found it a burden to procure so diligently that business secretly committed to your trust, I owe and give you the greatest thanks. And the plan you propose pleases me well — about that most excellent Bernardino Baldi of Urbino, Abbot of Guastalla, who has proposed to write the <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</i>. For there, what we are contriving can be most conveniently inserted.</b> Yet I should wish (as I asked before) that the first conceptions be communicated to me, that I may interpose some judgement of mine too — since my affairs can be known to no one more rightly than to me myself. The <b>verses composed in my honour, and further augmented than the earlier were, the son will show</b>. If anything in the earlier was changed, it can be restored hence, and what is wanting there completed. The rest I commit to your prudence and your benevolent will toward me.</p><p><b>That the most illustrious and most reverend Cardinal of the Santi Quattro [SS. Quatuor]</b> — a man, doubtless, of eminent and varied learning, and most loving of Mathematical things — <b>observed the Solar Eclipse of the year [15]98, with what diligence was possible, I received most gladly</b>; and the description, communicated to me, of what His most illustrious Lordship noted, is welcome — though, on account of the rarity of clear weather, he could not designate everything so precisely as he wished. I rejoice, and greatly congratulate these studies, <b>that at Rome, even among the most illustrious Cardinals, are found those who hold divine Astronomy in esteem</b> (as is clear from this alone). This Eclipse was observed at <b>Uraniborg in Denmark, with my instruments still remaining there, in my absence</b> — for I was then dwelling near Hamburg, where the clear weather requisite for searching out its appearances accurately did not shine. What was noted at the said Uraniborg you have in the adjoined paper, together with the epilogue of my calculation, as it is drawn from our newest restitution of the motion of the Luminaries. Nay, <b>I have at the same time assigned this year's Solar Eclipse, which happens on the 10th of July</b> [1601], both as to the observation and to my reformed calculation, that it may be compared with your considerations in Italy — whether had by the aforesaid most illustrious Cardinal, or by yourself, or by any other. From these numbers I now send, you will draw another reckoning, and that more exact than the former, in defining the Lunar course and its phenomena.</p><p><b>I was not ignorant that those Tables of the Lunar motion, which you received from the noble youth Franz Tengnagel, my household-member, were immature</b>; and so I judged it ill-advised to send them you at that time. For besides that, on account of the disturbance of my affairs when I left Denmark, I could not examine more diligently what I had earlier conceived about the <b>Moon</b>, those numbers too, which were committed by one of my students to a certain unskilled Printer at Hamburg after my departure, <b>were printed very negligently and corruptly, so that I held all that labour frustrated</b>. But while I afterward stayed a while in Saxony (the Imperial Majesty, who had called me to Prague, most clemently granting it, until the plague in Bohemia should abate), <b>I had those Tables of the Lunar Prosthaphaereses computed anew, most diligently, to the seconds; where not only the error you warn of, but other errors too — committed before by the negligence as much of the computer as of the Printer — became clear enough to me</b>. But since, even so, the Lunar appearances derived hence did not agree so precisely with the celestial norm as I desired, I entered upon another method; and, having got better leisure here in Bohemia, <b>I restored anew both the Hypothesis of the Lunar motion and the numbers built upon it, and am now setting all that conduces hither at the press</b>. Which, when finished in print, and inserted in the due place of the first Volume of our <i>Astronomical Progymnasmata</i>, I will make you partaker of — together with the whole book, which also expedites the norm of the Solar motion, and accurately emends about a thousand fixed stars. Meanwhile take this intervening little delay in good part. But although those earlier Tables, concerning the Moon, were not exact, nor sufficient to me myself, <b>yet they do not admit so great a deviation as you seem to introduce in the computation of the three examples found in the <i>Astronomical Letters</b></i> — as the repetition of the calculation, according to my view, to be adjoined to these (provided the Legate has not left sooner than expected), will teach you. But when you have got our book of the <i>Progymnasmata</i>, of which I spoke, you will perceive all more fully and by a subtler inquiry. <b>If, by occasion, you would communicate to me that more universal Table of the <i>primum mobile</i>, extended by you to single 10 minutes</b>, you would do a most welcome thing; I in turn, when occasion is given, will repay some of mine, whether finished at the press or not.</p><p><b>Your Ephemerides of 10 years, last published, I received from Frankfurt.</b> I notice that you have corrected nothing in them that differs from the earlier, but only added certain things by way of supplement — not useless, in my opinion. You give hope of the remaining 20 years soon to follow, which I should likewise wish to obtain.</p><p><b>That Origanus, the Mathematician of Frankfurt-on-Oder and computer of Ephemerides, you have not yet refuted in these last-published [of yours], as he deserved</b> — perhaps to do the same in the remaining Volume of Ephemerides. To me indeed your eminent diligence in this work has always pleased, and I do not cease to commend it to others. And my mind abhors those who, moved by a certain self-love and arrogance, persecute others' labours for the slightest cause, <b>often seeking a knot in a bulrush</b> [a problem where none is]; but it happens to them — and not unjustly — what the Comic [Terence] said: <i>\"by saying what they want, they hear in turn what they would not.\"</i> That you meditate Tables of the <i>primum mobile</i> more excellent, doubtless, than we have hitherto had, I approve; and I wish that that Work may see the public light in good time.</p><p><b>About the heliacal emersions and occultations [risings and settings] of the Planets and the fixed [stars], there is no [cause] that you should be very anxious. This matter cannot be treated with sure precision, because those limits vary manifoldly</b> — not only by reason of the sphere's different inclination, but as the purity of the air, in any given Horizon, is greater or less. Nor is the reckoning equal in all fixed stars of the first or second magnitude, as neither in the rest; a not-small difference too falls in, as the stars in some parallel of the Horizon are more or less distant — toward south or north — from the place where the Sun rises or sets. And the Planets, as they alter their visible diameters near the conjunction [☌] or opposition [☍] of the Sun, by occasion of their varying distance from the Earth, also produce some difference; their <b>Latitude</b> (which is not yet sufficiently established) introduces a difficulty not the least; so that I reckon this business — not very necessary in itself either — can scarcely be extricated by anyone with just exactness. Whence I have not hitherto placed curious labour on it. <b>The limits of the twilights with respect to the Sun, set by the ancient Astronomers, I have found moderately well [established]; for I found before, in Denmark, that the Sun, near the equinoxes, makes a twilight-arc of 17½° most nearly.</b> Whether this agrees in Italy and elsewhere, under a more upright sphere, I leave undecided. The Ephemeris of the <b>Sun</b> for this year you will obtain, either now by the son, or afterward, if a copy cannot be made so quickly; I have added the Ephemerides of the Sun for 4 years, computed meanwhile; from these you will easily derive more. <b>Its Apogee is now at 5° 40′ of Cancer [♋]; and the Eccentricity is 2° 9′, of which the semidiameter of the Eccentric is 60. Its longitude I derive not from an unequal precession of the equinoxes (such as Copernicus vainly admitted), but from a simple Equinox</b> — which in the recent centuries varies imperceptibly, and now anticipates precisely <b>51″ each year, such as is also the motion of the fixed [stars]</b>. But if the precession of the equinoxes were now to be set from Spica of Virgo [♍], in imitation of Copernicus, it would be, this very year, <b>28° 16′ — exceeding the Copernican numeration by a sixth of a degree</b> — although the first star of Aries [♈] is not elongated from the vernal equinox beyond 27° 37′. <b>Which discrepancy draws its origin from this: that Copernicus wrongly assumed the interval of longitude of Spica and of the first of Aries from Ptolemy's table, not from the sky itself (as he ought) — a difference of 39′ falling in, which is intolerable.</b> But of these and the like it will perhaps be allowed to philosophise with you more copiously another time, when my Astronomical Works (whose edition I urge and hasten, so far as in me lies) shall have seen the public light. Very many things you will find therein not a little discrepant from what has hitherto been handed down in Astronomical [matters].</p><p>If you have anything explored in the elevations of the Pole there in Italy (of which you are preparing a more exact Geography), or (which I should prefer) in the more rightly limiting of the longitudes of places, share with me whatever it be. <b>Lunar Eclipses would conduce hither, for aptly determining the differences of longitude and of Meridians, had we but some diligently noted there in past years.</b> And if that be not granted, attend diligently, with your Instruments, to the one that this passing year furnishes — which also at its end, right after the winter Solstice, will exhibit a certain evident Solar one, equally to be studiously observed with you; whose calculation, no less than the Lunar, from our numbers, I subjoin to those I before promised, that it may be compared with the celestial observation there, and the certainty of our numeration explored.</p><p><b>All my Instruments, which I used in Denmark, 28 in number, are now arranged here at Prague, not far from the Castle, in a certain magnificently-built house of the Emperor's.</b> Which I wished to indicate by way of a colophon; for the scarcity of time does not allow me to write more now. This only I lovingly ask: that <b>you hold my Son, the bearer of the present [letter], diligently commended to you, and take care that the things worth seeing at Bologna be shown him, and that you likewise commend him at Rome to your friends and familiars.</b> And now, most excellent Lord Magini, I wish you to fare most well and most long.</p><p>Given at Prague of the Bohemians, the 24th day of January, the year 1601.<br>Most zealous of Your Excellency,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> at <b>Bologna</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010220001, brahe-t_16000103002). Carried by <b>Tycho's son</b> travelling with the <b>Persian embassy</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010430001, brahe-t_16000124001).</li><li><b>Significance — the Baldi self-monument, the Roman cardinal's eclipse, the lunar-tables saga, and Tycho's solar/precession theory.</b></li><li><b>The Bernardino Baldi insertion (a key self-monument document).</b> Tycho approves Magini's plan to insert Tycho's biography into <b>Bernardino Baldi of Urbino's <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</i> (<i>Le vite de' matematici</i>)</b> — the <i>Italian</i> counterpart to the <b>Brasch/Magini panegyric-history commissions</b> (brahe-t_16010414001, brahe-t_16000103002). He wants to vet \"the first conceptions\" himself (and the <b>augmented verses</b>).</li><li><b>The Cardinal's eclipse.</b> A Roman <b>Cardinal of the Santi Quattro [Coronati]</b> observed the <b>1598 solar eclipse</b>; Tycho is delighted \"even Cardinals esteem divine Astronomy,\" and sends his own <b>Uraniborg observation + calculation</b> of that eclipse, plus the <b>10 July 1601 solar eclipse</b>, for Italian comparison.</li><li><b>The lunar-tables saga (candid).</b> Tycho explains why the <b>lunar Tables Tengnagel gave Magini were immature</b>: the disruption of leaving Denmark; <b>a student's negligent, corrupt Hamburg printing</b>; a Saxony-interval recomputation \"to the seconds\" that exposed more errors; and finally a <b>wholly restored lunar hypothesis</b> now at press for the <b><i>Progymnasmata</b></i> (with the solar norm + the <b>~1000-star catalogue</b>). He insists the old tables don't admit the <b>large deviation Magini found in the three <i>Epistolae</i> examples</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010220001), and asks for Magini's <b>10-minute <i>primum mobile</i> table</b>.</li><li><b>Ephemerides &amp; Origanus.</b> Acknowledges Magini's <b>10-year Ephemerides</b> (mere supplements, no corrections); urges Magini to <b>refute Origanus</b> (David Origanus of Frankfurt-on-Oder) \"as he deserved,\" with a Terence tag (\"by saying what they want, they hear what they would not\") and the proverb \"<b>seeking a knot in a bulrush</b>.\"</li><li><b>Heliacal risings &amp; twilight.</b> A careful statement that <b>heliacal risings/settings cannot be treated with precision</b> (atmospheric purity, latitude, star magnitude, planetary diameter/latitude); and the datum that <b>the twilight-arc near the equinoxes is ~17½°</b> (the solar depression), found in Denmark.</li><li><b>Solar parameters &amp; precession theory.</b> Tycho's solar elements: <b>Apogee 5° 40′ Cancer, Eccentricity 2° 9′ (of 60)</b>; longitude from a <b>simple (uniform) equinox precessing 51″/year, <i>not</i> Copernicus's variable precession</b> (cf. the Scaliger debate, brahe-t_16000723001); and a sharp catch that <b>Copernicus took the Spica–Aries interval from Ptolemy's table, not the sky — a 39′ error \"intolerable.\"</b></li><li><b>The 28 instruments at Prague.</b> The closing colophon: <b>\"All my instruments, 28 in number, are now arranged at Prague … in a magnificently-built house of the Emperor's\"</b> (cf. brahe-t_16001110001, brahe-t_16000926003).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> The <b>self-monument</b> thread (Baldi ↔ Brasch/Magini panegyric, brahe-t_16010414001, brahe-t_16000103002); the <b>Magini</b> correspondence (brahe-t_16010220001); the <b>son/Persian-embassy</b> thread (brahe-t_16010430001); the <b>lunar-/solar-theory</b> and <b>Progymnasmata</b> threads.</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Magini; <b>Tycho the Younger</b> (the bearer); the <b>Persian Legate</b> (Anthony Sherley, \"an Englishman\"); <b>Bernardino Baldi</b> of Urbino (the <i>Vite de' matematici</i>); the <b>Cardinal of the Santi Quattro</b>; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>David Origanus</b>; <b>Copernicus, Ptolemy</b>; <b>Terence</b> (\"the Comic\"); the <i>Progymnasmata</i>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Bologna</b>, <b>Rome</b>, <b>Uraniborg/Denmark</b>, <b>Hamburg</b>, <b>Saxony</b>, <b>Frankfurt</b>. Glyphs ☉ ☾ ☌ ☍ ♋ ♍ ♈ reproduced.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Die 24 Ianuarij Anno 1601</i> at Prague → <b>24 January 1601 [N.S.]</b> (label agrees; the doubled \"1601…1601\" in the label field is a catalogue artefact).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the <b>Baldi <i>Lives of the Mathematicians</i> insertion</b> as the Italian self-monument; the <b>Roman Cardinal's 1598 eclipse</b> and the 1601 eclipses sent for comparison; the <b>candid lunar-tables history</b> — the Hamburg printing fiasco, the recomputation, the restored hypothesis for the <i>Progymnasmata</i>; the Magini-ephemerides/Origanus matter; the <b>heliacal-rising and 17½° twilight-arc</b> discussion; the <b>solar parameters (5°40′ Cancer / 2°9′), the 51″ simple precession vs Copernicus, and the 39′ Spica–Aries error</b>; and the <b>28 instruments at Prague</b>) is firmly and carefully construed, with the numerical/technical detail rendered faithfully. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 7 page images of the slice (pages=\"1-4\", then \"5-7\") visually against the printed source_text and published_english. Collated opening (4 Martij superioris Anni) and closing (Dabantur Pragae Bohaemorum Die 24 Ianuarij Anno 1601, manu propria scripsi) plus every proper name (Bernardino Baldo, Cardinalis SS. Quatuor, Franciscus Tengnaglio, Origanus, Copernicus) and every number/date/quantity (10 die Iulij; 28 instruments; Apogaeum P.5 M.40 Cancer; Eccentricitas P.2 M.9/60; 51''; 28 Grad. 16 Min.; 27 Grad. 37 Min.; discrimen 39 Min.; 17 1/2 partes twilight arc; trium exemplorum; 4 annorum and 10 annorum and 20 annorum Ephemerides; 4 Martij) against the Dreyer page images. Every item matches Dreyer's printed Latin exactly, and the English renders each without addition, omission, or distortion. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Bologna, Archivio Malvezzi de' Medici."], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 9, "prev": "brahe-t_16000304001", "next": "brahe-t_16010220001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000304001", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010220001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Writes via his son accompanying Anthony Sherley's Persian legation to Rome, thanks Magini for handling a confidential commission, and discusses Baldi of Urbino's proposed writing project.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010124001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010128001", "d": "1601-01-28", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany — Prague, 28 January 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: dataset cites<i> Bibliotheca mathematica </i>(printed); = Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: none recorded (printed source)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SERENISSIMO ET POTENTISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO FERDINANDO, MAGNO DUCI ETRURIAe ETC. DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO.<br>SERENISSIME ET POTENTISSIME PRINCEPS, DOMINE CLEMENTISSIME.</p><p>CUM magni Persarum Regis amplissima Legatio, quae hîc aliquandiu apud Sacram Caesaream Maiestatem, Dominum meum Clementissimum, morata est, nunc in Italiam cogitet, et antequam Romam venerit, Serenissimam Celsitudinem Tuam in Etruria sua salutare, et quae in mandatis habet, eidem reverenter exponere constituerit, mei officij esse ratus sum, Serenissimam Celsitudinem Tuam de TENGNAGLIO nostro clementissime bene meritam hisce literulis submisse compellare, praesertim cum Legationis eius antesignanum, Illustrissimum Dominum AMMONIUM SCHERLEJ, natione Anglum, filius meus natu maior, TYCHO nomine, comitetur. Est autem ille idem filius, quem antea in Serenissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae Aula aliquandiu servire exoptâram, nisi obstacula quaedam intervenissent, quae attingere supervacaneum. Tradidissem illi Opera, quae hactenus in Astronomicis elucubravi, secum adsportanda, prout Serenissima Celsitudo Vestra aliquando clementissimis suis ad me literis expetijt, nisi quaedam in singulis ferme adhuc desiderarentur, typographico labore necdum absoluto. Quod ubi effectum, Serenissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae Clementissimae voluntati demisse et lubentissime obsecundabo.</p><p>Remoram nonnullam, quo minus illa ad Colophonem deducta sint, iniecit migratio mea ex Arce prioris habitationis Bennatica huc Pragam, sic clementissime mandante Caesarea Maiestate, quo propior ei factus, humillima mea servitia ipsius Maiestati promptius praestare possem, allatis etiam huc omnibus Instrumentis Astronomicis, quibus antea in Dania Patria mea usus sum, quae numero sunt 28, ijsdemque in domo quadam ampla et magnifice, Italica architectura, non longe ab Arce iuxta Caesaris hortum extructa, ordine dispositis, donec in locum quendam usui eorum aptiorem, a Caesarea Maiestate clementissime deputatum, transferantur. Quod et brevius futurum spero. Idque Serenissimae Celsitudini Vestrae debita submissione (siquidem eam benignissime studijs meis favere compertum habeam) indicandum duxi. Pluribus vero eam gravioribus intentam hac mea interpellatione remorari nec volo nec decet. Quare nunc finem facio, id solummodo addens et humillime rogans, ut Serenissima Celsitudo Vestra praedictum filium meum sibi commendatum habere non dedignetur, Deus Opt. Max. Serenissimam Celsitudinem Vestram Reipublicae emolumento quam diutissime servet florentem et incolumem.</p><p>Praga die 28 Ianuarij Anno 1601.<br>Serenissimae Celsitudini Vestrae<br>submisse addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the Most Serene and Most Powerful Prince and Lord, Lord Ferdinand, Grand Duke of Etruria etc., his most Clement Lord.</b><br><b>Most Serene and Most Powerful Prince, most Clement Lord.</b></p><p>Since the most ample Legation of the great King of the Persians — which has tarried here for some time with the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my most Clement Lord — is now minded [to go] into Italy, and, before it comes to Rome, has resolved to salute Your Most Serene Highness in your Tuscany, and reverently to lay before you what it has in its instructions, I have judged it to be of my duty humbly to address Your Most Serene Highness — who have so most clemently deserved well of our Tengnagel — with this little letter; especially since my elder-born son, Tycho by name, accompanies the leader of that Legation, the Most Illustrious Lord Anthony Sherley, an Englishman by nation. And he is that same son whom I had earlier wished to serve for a while in Your Most Serene Highness's Court, had not certain obstacles intervened, which it is superfluous to touch upon. I would have handed to him the Works which I have hitherto elaborated in Astronomy, to carry with him — as Your Most Serene Highness once requested of me by your most clement letters — were not certain [parts] still wanting in almost each of them, the typographic labour not yet completed. When that is done, I will humbly and most gladly comply with Your Most Serene Highness's most clement will.</p><p>Some delay, hindering their being brought to the colophon [i.e. to completion], was thrown in the way by my removal from the Castle of my former dwelling, Benátky, hither to Prague — the Imperial Majesty so most clemently commanding, that, being made nearer to him, I might the more readily render my most humble services to His Majesty; all my Astronomical Instruments, too, which I formerly used in Denmark my homeland, having been brought hither — 28 in number — and these set out in order in a certain ample house, magnificently built in the Italian architecture, not far from the Castle, beside the Emperor's garden, until they be transferred to some place fitter for their use, most clemently assigned by the Imperial Majesty; which I hope will be shortly. And this I have thought to indicate to Your Most Serene Highness with due submission (since I have found you most benignly to favour my studies). But I neither wish, nor is it fitting, to detain you — intent on weightier matters — with this my interpellation. Wherefore I now make an end, adding only this, and most humbly asking, that Your Most Serene Highness would not disdain to hold my aforesaid son commended to you. May God, best and greatest, preserve Your Most Serene Highness, for the benefit of the Commonwealth, flourishing and unharmed for the longest time.</p><p>Prague, the 28th of January, in the year 1601.<br>Your Most Serene Highness's<br>most submissively devoted,<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>I wrote [this] with my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender, recipient &amp; register.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Prague</b>, in the high princely-petition register, to <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (<i>Magnus Dux Etruriae</i>, 1549–1609) — a patron who had earlier sought Tycho's published works. The letter commends Tycho's elder son to the Grand Duke.</li><li><b>The Persian-embassy occasion (a remarkable historical node).</b> The <i>amplissima Legatio magni Persarum Regis</i> = the famous <b>Persian embassy of Shah Abbas I</b>, which travelled through Europe 1599–1602 seeking an anti-Ottoman alliance, had stayed at Rudolf II's court and was now bound for <b>Italy and Rome</b>, via the Tuscan court. Its leader — <i>antesignanus … Ammonius Scherlej, natione Anglus</i> — is <b>Sir Anthony Sherley</b> (1565–1635?), the English adventurer who co-led the embassy. <b>Tycho's elder son, Tycho Brahe the Younger,</b> travelled in this embassy's company toward Florence — the occasion and bearer of this very letter.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Ferdinandus, Magnus Dux Etruriae</i> = <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici</b>.</li><li><i>Ammonius Scherlej</i> = <b>Anthony Sherley</b>, the embassy's English leader.</li><li><i>filius meus natu maior, Tycho</i> = <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>, Tycho's elder son, the <i>commendatus</i>; once intended for service at the Medici court (frustrated by unstated \"obstacles\").</li><li><i>Tengnaglius noster</i> = <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>, of whom the Grand Duke had \"deserved well\" (during Tengnagel's Italian sojourn at the Tuscan and Roman courts, cf. brahe-t_16000306002).</li><li><i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>The works and the move (valuable material history).</b> Tycho explains he cannot yet send the <b>Works he has elaborated in astronomy</b> (the great unfinished publication programme — the <i>Progymnasmata</i> and related volumes) because the <b>printing is not finished</b> (<i>typographico labore necdum absoluto … ad Colophonem</i>). The delay was caused by his <b>removal from Benátky to Prague</b> at the Emperor's command, to be nearer the court. He records that his <b>28 astronomical instruments</b>, brought from Denmark, are now <b>arranged in a large house of Italian architecture beside the Emperor's garden</b> at Prague, pending transfer to a fitter site — a precise datum on the Prague phase of the Tychonic observatory (the Kurtz/Belvedere arrangement).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Praga</i> = Prague; <i>Bennatica arx</i> = Benátky; <i>Etruria</i> = Tuscany.</li><li><b>Edition note.</b> The dataset cites the letter to a printed <i>Bibliotheca mathematica</i>; it is also in Dreyer's <i>Opera Omnia</i>. No manuscript witness is recorded — the text descends through print.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Serenissima Celsitudo Vestra</i> = \"Your Most Serene Highness\" (declined throughout); subscription <i>submisse addictissimus</i> → \"most submissively devoted\"; <i>manu propria scripsi</i> → \"I wrote with my own hand.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 28 Ianuarij Anno 1601</i> at Prague; the dataset marks <b>n.s. (New Style)</b> — apt for Imperial Prague and for the Catholic Italian recipient. Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean, complete Latin in formal register; the principals (Ferdinando de' Medici, Anthony Sherley and the Persian embassy, Tycho the Younger, Tengnagel) are securely identified, and the material details (the unfinished printing, the move to Prague, the <b>28 instruments</b> housed beside the Emperor's garden) are explicit and historically valuable. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.397-400 as page images. Letter to Ferdinando de' Medici (\"SERENISSIMO ET POTENTISSIMO PRINCIPI...\") runs p.398-399. Collated proper names (Ammonium Scherlej = the English translation resolves this to the historically-attested Anthony Sherley, leader of the Persian legation, consistent with \"natione Anglum\" -- an informed identification, not a fabrication), the instrument count (28), the place-names (Bennatica/Benátky, Praga), and closing date (28 Ianuarij Anno 1601) against Dreyer -- exact match. Published English is complete and accurate.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Introduces his son Tycho, accompanying Anthony Sherley's Persian legation to Italy, and commends him to the Grand Duke's favour.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010128001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010220001", "d": "1601-02-20", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Mantua", "t": "Giovanni Antonio Magini → Tycho Brahe — Mantua, 20 February 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record (printed source): G. A. Magini,<i> Tabulae primi mobilis </i>(no MS witness recorded; the letter survives in Magini's printed<i> Tabulae</i>)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Direction note.</b> <b>Incoming</b> letter — <i>from</i> the Bolognese astronomer <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini</b> <i>to</i> Tycho. (Day of month supplied as <b>20</b> from the catalogue label; the printed source gives only \"<i>die Februarij</i>\" — see Apparatus.)</p>", "la": "<p>NOBILITATE PERILLUSTRI ET ERUDITIONE PRAeSTANTISSIMO VIRO, D. TYCHONI BRAHE DANO, DOMINO DE KNUDSTRUP, ET ARCIS URANIBURG IN INSULA HELLESPONTI DANICI HUAeNA FUNDATORI.</p><p>CUM abs te nihil ad nos allatum sit post eas litteras, quas ad me scriptas D. IOANNI FRANCISCO SAGREDO Patritio Veneto commendasti, ut ad me perferendas curaret, quibus ego et statim respondi diligenter, eiusdem opera usus, ut ad te tuto transmitteretur, suspicari coepi suboffensum esse te, quod ego in tua causa procuranda lentior expectatione et voluntate tua fuissem. Sed, mi TYCHO, illud te reputare cum animo tuo velim, quae non in nostra, sed in aliorum manu sunt sita, ea non tam cito confici posse: his accedit occupatio domestica; solus sum meae familiae moderator, quam in officio continere, cui opportuna ac necessaria providere necesse est, ita, quod tempus aut studijs, aut amicorum negotijs obeundis dicatum fuit, id invito mihi domesticae curae eripiunt. Ac dum quidem studia in scholis publice exercentur, et tum mihi et publice et privatim auditoribus studiosisque meis opera danda est; cum autem illa intermittuntur, ne tum quidem otioso mihi esse licet, ultimis enim elapsis duobus annis a Serenissimo Duce Mantuae sum accersitus, ut per vacationum dies, Principibus suis filijs in Mathematica facultate instruendis vacarem. Neque vero sufficere ille sibi duxit omne istud aestivum tempus atque autumnale, quo solemus a publicis lectionibus feriari; sed et hoc, quod ad Quadragesimam usque ab ineunte fere Ianuario nobis vacuum datur, oportet me ab eodem accersitum, magno meo incommodo, Mantuae commorari. Cum igitur ad Aulam Imperatoriam ab ipso Serenissimo Duce sit destinatus Rerum suarum Procurator, Nobilissimus Comes LAeLIUS ARIVABENUS, percommodum mihi visum est, has ad te litteras ab eo perferendas mittere.</p><p>Quod itaque ad praeclarum tuum illud de Restitutionibus coelestium motuum opus spectat, vellem equidem incepisse fructum aliquem ex eo percipere. Solum enim habui cathalogum stellarum inerrantium, qui mihi pro thesauro est, itemque Tabulam illam motus Lunaris; putabamque posse me primo quoque tempore habere a te motum Solis correctum, itemque Motus Lunaris complementum, sed, ut video, frustra putabam. Dubitationes etiam mihi sese obijcientes in hac Lunari tua Tabula, cuius supputationes ad unguem congruere tuis observationibus non perspicio, dilui abs te volebam, docerique praeterea de arcubus visionum, stellarum tum errantium, tum inerrantium in eum usum, de quo ad te perscripsi. Adhaec operam meam ultro obtuli in supputandis aequationum Tabulis, neglectis scrupulis Proportionalibus, Argumenti aequationibus et excessibus, iuxta novas meas inventiones, quae ab ijs omnibus, a quibus perspectae sunt, utilissimae ac nobilissimae iudicantur, cum earum ope omnes supputationum labores facile compendiosiores fiant. Habeo et alias quasdam vias, ad eundem scopum attingendum planiores et compendiosiores, quas non tam cito alijs communicandas esse iustis de causis decrevi. Quae ne tu somnia esse putes, mitto ad te Tabulam tuam Lunarem a me novo modo elaboratam, unâ cum eius usu, quibus compendijs perspectis tu videris, num tum diligentia tum inventione PTOLEMAeUM, COPERNICUM et REINOLDUM etiam post me longe reliquerim. Quid enim mea Prostaphaereseων Planetarum Generali Tabula, quae omnibus Planetis communis est, neque viginti paginas excedit, compendiosius excogitari poterit? an non plurimi libentius et maiori compendio per eam, quam per Ephemerides Planetarum computum subducturi sunt? Neque vero tu mireris, velim, si denuo a me supputatas videris centri variationes in hac tua Tabula, et Anomaliae aequationes; hoc enim, ut nullo fere mihi labori fuit, ita magna cum animi mei voluptate feci, quae quidem Anomaliae aequatio prodit ex additione variationis centri et aequationis secundi Epicicli. Aequationes tamen argumenti, seu maioris Epicicli, cum adhaerente eis excessu denuo supputare nolui, licet cognovissem Apogaeas aequationes omnium minimas, quae in novilunijs et plenilunijs contingunt, colligendas esse, manente Luna in puncto G secundi Epicicli, proxima puncto D, dum centrum Eccentrici occupat punctum B parvi Circelli, atque etiam Aequationes omnium maximas, quae in Quadraturis accidunt, definiendas esse, manente Luna in F, et centro Eccentrici in H, Perigaeo parvi circelli, a quibus postea eliciendus esset excessus per subductionem singularum aequationum Apogaearum a singulis his Perigaei aequationibus. Nolui, inquam, eas de integro elaborare, cum eandem viam laboriosam, ne dicam parum accuratam, praesertim in ♂, ♀ et ☿ amplius ingredi non satis e re nostra iudicarem. Si itaque idoneum me putas, qui in aliorum Planetarum Tabulis, iuxta has meas inventiones, ulli tibi usui esse possim, nihil dubites meam implorare operam, nam hac in re, ut quod res est fatear, multo me habebis promptiorem, quam in altera illa mihi tradita Provincia, cuius magna pars in aliorum manu est, ut absque aliorum opera nihil praestare valeamus, qua in his supputationibus nequaquam egeo. Illud etiam addere volo, meas has rationes promptius et commodius subservituras esse variationibus Eccentricitatum, quam usitatam, nam possumus definire numerum multiplicandum sive numerum mysticum ei adiacentem, ad unum situm, notando differentiam, quae pro altero situ extremo consurgit, ut ex ea haberi possit congruus numerus multiplicandus, vel numerus mysticus ad optatum Eccentricitatis situm. Sed pluribus alias, nunc vale, eruditissime vir, meque amantissimum tui, ac praeter omnes studiosissimum observantissimumque redamare non graveris.</p><p>Datae Mantuae, in Aula Ducali, die 20 Februarij, anno 1601.<br>Perillustri Dominationi tuae addictissimus<br>IOANNES ANTONIUS MAGINUS, Patavinus.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man most illustrious in nobility and most excellent in erudition, Lord Tycho Brahe the Dane, Lord of Knudstrup, and Founder of the castle of Uraniborg on the Island Hven in the Danish Hellespont.</b></p><p>Since nothing has been brought to us from you after that letter — which, written to me, you commended to <b>Lord Giovanni Francesco Sagredo, a Venetian patrician</b>, to see delivered to me, and to which I both at once replied carefully (using his help, that it might be safely transmitted to you) — I began to suspect you were somewhat offended that I had been slower in procuring your business than your expectation and wish. But, my Tycho, I would have you consider with your mind that <b>what is placed not in our own hand but in others' cannot be accomplished so quickly</b>. To this is added domestic occupation: I am the sole governor of my household, which it is necessary to keep in order and to provide with what is fitting and needful — so that what time was devoted to studies or to friends' business, the domestic cares snatch from me against my will. And while studies are publicly carried on in the schools, I must then give my labour both publicly and privately to my hearers and students; but when these are interrupted, not even then am I allowed to be at leisure — for these last two years past <b>I have been summoned by the most serene Duke of Mantua to give my vacation days to instructing his princely sons in the Mathematical faculty</b>. Nor did he think it enough for himself, all that summer and autumn time when we are wont to take holiday from the public lectures; but also this [period], which is given us free from about the beginning of January up to Lent, I must — summoned by him, to my great inconvenience — spend at Mantua. Since, then, the most serene Duke has appointed to the Imperial Court, as Procurator of his affairs, the most noble <b>Count Lelio Arrivabene</b>, it has seemed very convenient to me to send this letter to you, to be carried by him.</p><p>As, then, concerns that famous work of yours <b>on the Restitutions of the celestial motions</b>, I could indeed wish to have begun to perceive some fruit from it. For I have had only <b>the catalogue of the fixed stars — which is to me as a treasure — and that Table of the Lunar motion</b>; and I thought I could, at the first opportunity, have from you <b>the corrected motion of the Sun, and likewise the complement of the Lunar motion</b>; but, as I see, I thought in vain. <b>The doubts, too, presenting themselves to me in this Lunar Table of yours — whose computations I do not perceive to agree to the nail with your observations</b> — I wished to have resolved by you; and to be taught besides about <b>the arcs of visions</b> [arcus visionum] of the stars, both wandering and fixed, for that use of which I wrote to you. Moreover, I have freely offered my labour in computing Tables of equations — the <b>proportional minutes, the Argument-equations, and the excesses</b> being set aside — according to my new inventions, which by all who have examined them are judged most useful and most noble, since by their help all the labours of computation easily become more compendious. I have also certain other ways, plainer and more compendious, for reaching the same goal, which for good reasons I have resolved not to communicate to others so quickly. Lest you think these are dreams, <b>I send you your Lunar Table, elaborated by me in a new way, together with its use</b> — from which compendia, once examined, you may see whether, in diligence and in invention, <b>I have left Ptolemy, Copernicus, and Reinhold far behind me as well</b>. For what could be devised more compendious than my <b>General Table of the Prosthaphaereses of the Planets</b>, which is common to all the Planets, and does not exceed twenty pages? Will not very many more gladly, and with greater economy, draw their computation by it than through the Ephemerides of the Planets? Nor would I have you wonder if you see, computed anew by me, <b>the variations of the centre in this Table of yours, and the equations of the Anomaly</b>; for this — as it was almost no labour to me — I did with great delight of mind; which Anomaly-equation, indeed, comes out from the addition of the variation of the centre and the equation of the second Epicycle. <b>The equations of the Argument, however, or of the greater Epicycle, with the excess attaching to them, I was unwilling to compute anew</b> — although I had recognised that the apogee-equations, the smallest of all, which occur at new and full moons, are to be gathered with the Moon remaining at point <b>G</b> of the second Epicycle, next to point <b>D</b>, while the centre of the Eccentric occupies point <b>B</b> of the small circle; and also that the equations greatest of all, which happen at the Quadratures, are to be defined with the Moon remaining at <b>F</b>, and the centre of the Eccentric at <b>H</b>, the Perigee of the small circle — from which the excess would afterward have to be drawn out, by subtracting the several apogee-equations from these several perigee-equations. I was unwilling, I say, to work these out entire, since I judged it not enough to our purpose to enter further upon that same laborious — not to say, scarcely accurate — way, especially in <b>♂ [Mars], ♀ [Venus], and ☿ [Mercury]</b>. If, then, you think me fit to be of any use to you in the Tables of the other Planets, according to these inventions of mine, do not at all doubt to implore my labour; for in this matter — to confess the thing as it is — you will have me far readier than in that other Province committed to me, a great part of which is in others' hands, so that without others' labour we can accomplish nothing — whereas in these computations I in no way need [them]. This too I will add: that <b>these methods of mine will serve the variations of the Eccentricities more readily and conveniently than the usual</b> [method]; for we can define the multiplier-number, or the \"mystic number\" adjacent to it, for one position, by noting the difference that arises for the other extreme position, so that from it the fitting multiplier-number, or mystic number, for the desired position of the Eccentricity, may be had. But of these things more another time. Now farewell, most learned man, and do not be unwilling to love in return one most loving of you, and beyond all most zealous and observant.</p><p>Given at Mantua, in the Ducal Court, the 20th day of February, the year 1601.<br>Most devoted to Your most illustrious Lordship,<br><b>Giovanni Antonio Magini, of Padua.</b></p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; correspondent.</b> <b>Incoming</b>, <b>Giovanni Antonio Magini (Maginus)</b> (1555–1617) — professor of mathematics/astronomy at <b>Bologna</b> (the man who beat Galileo to that chair), a major ephemerides-maker and table-computer — <b>to Tycho</b>. He signs \"<i>Patavinus</i>\" (of/from Padua). The letter survives <b>in Magini's own printed <i>Tabulae primi mobilis</b></i> (no manuscript witness recorded).</li><li><b>Significance — Tychonic tables in Italian hands, and Magini's computational self-advertisement.</b></li><li><b>What Magini has of Tycho's work.</b> Only the <b>catalogue of fixed stars</b> (\"a treasure to me\") and <b>a Table of the Lunar motion</b>; he is still waiting (in vain) for the <b>corrected Solar motion and the \"complement\" of the Lunar motion</b> — a window on how partially Tycho's results had circulated before publication.</li><li><b>A genuine objection.</b> Magini reports the Lunar Table's <b>\"computations do not agree to the nail with [Tycho's] observations,\"</b> and asks Tycho to resolve the doubts — and to teach him about the <b>\"arcs of visions\"</b> (visibility arcs) of planets and stars.</li><li><b>Magini's methods.</b> He boasts of <b>new compendious computation methods</b> (omitting proportional minutes, argument-equations, excesses), a <b>\"General Table of Planetary Prosthaphaereses\"</b> under 20 pages common to all planets, claiming to have left <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus, and Reinhold</b> behind. He encloses <b>Tycho's own Lunar Table re-worked his way</b>.</li><li><b>The technical lunar passage</b> is rendered literally: Magini recomputed the <b>variations of the centre and the anomaly-equations</b> of Tycho's multi-epicycle lunar model (the \"second epicycle,\" the \"small circle,\" lettered points <b>G, D, B, F, H</b>, apogee-/perigee-equations at syzygies and quadratures, the \"excess\"), but declined to redo the <b>argument-equations</b> as too laborious and \"scarcely accurate,\" especially for <b>Mars, Venus, Mercury</b>. The lettered points refer to Tycho's/Magini's lunar diagram (not reproduced here); the geometry is translated as written, without reconstruction.</li><li><b>An offer.</b> Magini volunteers to help compute the <b>other planets' tables</b> by his methods — frankly noting he is freer in pure computation than in \"that other Province committed to me … a great part in others' hands\" (likely his observational/instrument obligations).</li><li><b>The courier network.</b> Tycho's earlier letter reached Magini via <b>Giovanni Francesco Sagredo</b> (the Venetian patrician — later immortalised as an interlocutor in <b>Galileo's <i>Dialogue</b></i>); this reply travels via <b>Count Lelio Arrivabene</b>, the <b>Duke of Mantua's (Vincenzo I Gonzaga's) agent to the Imperial court</b>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Giovanni Antonio Magini; <b>Tycho</b> (addressee, \"Founder of Uraniborg on Hven in the Danish Hellespont\"); <b>Giovanni Francesco Sagredo</b>; the <b>Duke of Mantua</b> (Vincenzo I Gonzaga, unnamed) and his sons; <b>Count Lelio Arrivabene</b>; <b>Ptolemy, Copernicus, Erasmus Reinhold</b>. Places: <b>Mantua</b> (ducal court), <b>Bologna</b>/<b>Padua</b> (implied), <b>Venice</b>, the Imperial court.</li><li><b>Calendar / date (protocol §3).</b> The printed source gives only \"<i>die Februarij, anno 1601</i>\" — <b>no day-number</b>; the catalogue label supplies <b>20 February 1601 [N.S.]</b> (Italy = New Style). Header gives \"[20] February 1601 [N.S.]\"; the bracket flags the supplied day. (Mantua was New Style.)</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The day is <b>not</b> merely label-supplied: Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 401; running-head 10/20 Febr.) prints <i>die 20 Februarij</i>. Restored to header, Source, and translation; the bracket/“supplied” qualification is withdrawn.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Fixed a pronoun-flip mistranslation in the closing boast: Magini's Latin reads 'etiam post me longe reliquerim' ('I have left them far behind me too'), confirmed at Dreyer, TBOO VIII p.400, l.37. The published English had rendered this 'even after you', misattributing Magini's self-boast about surpassing Ptolemy, Copernicus, and Reinhold to Tycho instead of to Magini himself.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (Magini's apology and account of his Mantua-tutoring obligations; what Tychonic material he holds and still awaits; his <b>genuine objection that Tycho's Lunar Table does not match the observations</b>; his computational methods, the 20-page prosthaphaeresis table, and the enclosed re-worked Lunar Table; the literal rendering of the lunar-model recomputation with its lettered points; and the offer of help on the other planets) is firmly construed. The intricate lunar geometry is translated as written (no diagram reconstruction); the <b>day of the month</b> is a label-supplied value, flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII page images (398 tail/399-401). Confirmed Dreyer prints 'poft me' at p.400 l.37, verifying the flagged defect is real. Everything else collates cleanly: Sagredo, Count Lelio Arrivabene, the fixed-star catalogue and Lunar Table, the geometric points G/D/B/F/H for the apogee/perigee equations, the planetary symbols (Mars/Venus/Mercury), the 'does not exceed twenty pages' claim, and the closing date/signature (Mantua, 20 February 1601, Giovanni Antonio Magini of Padua) all match Dreyer exactly. One defect only, but it is high-severity and changes the sense of the passage a scholar would cite.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (<b>high</b>). Dreyer confirms 'poft me' (behind ME, i.e. Magini) not 'poft te'. The English flips the pronoun to 'after you', turning Magini's first-person self-boast (he claims to have outdone Ptolemy/Copernicus/Reinhold) into an apparent claim that references Tycho instead — exactly the previously-flagged pronoun-flip defect. At: “I have left Ptolemy, Copernicus, and Reinhold far behind — even after you”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the disputed clause directly against the Dreyer page image (TBOO VIII p.400, l.37): the printed text reads 'post me', unambiguously supporting the audit's claim. The Latin source text in This census already matched Dreyer correctly (no TEXT_ERROR); only the English translation was defective and has been corrected. No other cruxes in this letter were reviewed beyond the flagged defect.</p>", "ms": null, "w": [], "th": {"label": "Giovanni Antonio Magini", "count": 10, "pos": 10, "prev": "brahe-t_16010124001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16010124001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Explains delay in carrying out Tycho's undisclosed commission, citing reliance on third parties such as Sagredo and the press of domestic obligations.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010220001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010308001", "d": "1601-03-08", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Martin Brasch → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 8 March 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO ET MAGNIFICO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHEO, MATHEMATICORUM FACILE PRINCIPI, CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS ASTRONOMO, PATRONO ET DOMINO COLENDO.<br>PRAG IN BEHEMEN.<br>MAGNIFICE ET NOBILISSIME DOMINE, PATRONE COLENDE.</p><p>TANDEM sub BACCHI sacra ad nos diu expectatus advenit Magnificus Dominus D. GODELMANNUS. Is mihi de te renunciavit, quod accepisses carmen, quod de Mathematicis exorsus sum, et ad te sub natalem CHRISTI misi. Gratum id mihi fuit. Addidit, si saltem in cursu progrederer, te laborem et artem meam Caesareae Maiestati commendaturum. Id quoque gratum. Verum interim mirabar, cum ego permulta mihi a te suppeditari petierim, cur non aut omnia illa, aut quaedam, quae tibi nota sunt, simul per D. GODELMANNUM unâ cum literis tuis ad me miseris. Haec enim potissima causa erat, cur istud fragmentum vel potius bonam partem ad te miserim, ac significavi simul, me ante progredi non posse, quam responsum a te accepissem. Rogo igitur adhuc etiam atque etiam, vir magnifice et generose, ut certa data occasione literas tuas ad me mittas unâ cum illis, quae petij, quantum in te est. Tandiu enim adhaerescere cogor. Scio, quantum me ames; igitur etiam hîc tibi curae sim. Plura iam non scribo propter imbecillem plane valetudinem.</p><p>Bene et feliciter T. M. valeat.<br>Datae Rostochij 8 Martij stylo veteri Anno 1601.<br>T. Magnif.<br>reverenter colens<br>MARTINUS BRASCHIUS.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous and magnificent man, Master Tycho Brahe, easily prince of mathematicians, Astronomer of His Imperial Majesty, patron and lord to be honoured. At Prague in Bohemia. Magnificent and most noble Lord, patron to be honoured.</b></p><p>At length, about the rites of Bacchus [Shrovetide], the long-awaited Magnificent Master Dr. Gödelmann arrived among us. He reported to me, concerning you, that you had received the poem which I began about the Mathematicians and sent to you about the Nativity of Christ. That was pleasing to me. He added that, if only I would proceed in [my] course, you would commend my labour and art to His Imperial Majesty. That too [was] pleasing. But meanwhile I wondered why — since I had asked that very many things be supplied me by you — you did not send me, at the same time, through Master Gödelmann, together with your letter, either all those things, or some of them, which are known to you. For this was the chief reason why I sent you that fragment, or rather a good part [of the poem], and signified at the same time that I could not proceed before I had received an answer from you. I ask therefore, again and yet again, magnificent and generous man, that, when a sure occasion is given, you send me your letter, together with those things which I asked, as far as is in you. For until then I am compelled to stick fast. I know how much you love me; therefore may I be a care to you in this too. I write no more now, on account of my plainly feeble health.</p><p>May Your Magnificence fare well and happily.<br>Given at Rostock, 8 March, old style, in the year 1601.<br>Reverently honouring Your Magnificence,<br>Martin Brasch.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Martin Brasch</b> (Braschius, 1565–1601), professor of philosophy and poetry at <b>Rostock</b>; here pressing Tycho for source-material he had promised, needed to complete a <b>didactic poem \"about the Mathematicians\"</b> (<i>carmen de Mathematicis</i>) which Brasch had begun and sent Tycho, in part, \"about Christmas.\" (Brasch died later in 1601; his complaint of <i>imbecillis valetudo</i>, \"feeble health,\" is poignant.)</li><li><b>Tie to brahe-t_16001116003.</b> This is the follow-up to Tycho's reply of 16 Nov 1600: Gödelmann (the go-between) had relayed that Tycho received the poem and would commend Brasch to the Emperor \"if he proceeded\"; Brasch answers that he <i>cannot</i> proceed until Tycho sends the promised materials.</li><li><b><i>D. Godelmannus</b></i> = <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (see brahe-t_15981112001), now Saxon-Electoral Legate, the courier between Prague and Rostock.</li><li><b><i>sub Bacchi sacra</b></i> — \"about the rites of Bacchus\": the festival of Bacchus, here the <b>Shrovetide/Carnival season</b> (late winter), consistent with an 8 March letter reporting Gödelmann's recent arrival; rendered with the gloss. <i>Caesareae Maiestatis Astronomo</i> — the address already styles Tycho \"Astronomer of His Imperial Majesty.\"</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Rostochij 8 Martij stylo veteri … 1601</i> = <b>8 March 1601, Old Style</b> (<i>stylo veteri</i> explicit; Lutheran Rostock kept the Julian calendar). Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely; sender (Brasch of Rostock), the poem-and-materials business, Gödelmann as go-between, and the date (8 March 1601 O.S.) are all clear and corpus-consistent. <i>sub Bacchi sacra</i> is glossed (Shrovetide) rather than over-specified.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter against TBOO VIII pp.401-403 (page images, read visually); letter proper on p.402, text matches Dreyer word for word. The Bacchus/Carnival-season reference and the date (8 March, old style, 1601) verified correct. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16001116003", "next": "brahe-t_16010414001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16001116003", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010414001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports Gödelmann's arrival with word that Tycho received his poem fragment, and asks why the promised materials needed to continue the poem were not sent.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010308001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010310001", "d": "1601-03-10", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Christoph Sturtz → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 10 March 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO, D. TICHONI BRAHE, MATHEMATICO S. C. MAIESTATIS EXCELLENTISSIMO, AMICO ET PATRONO PLURIMUM OBSERVANDO.<br>MAGNIFICE AC NOBILISSIME DOMINE TICHO, AMICE HONORANDE.<br>S.</p><p>CUM certa per magnificum Dominum IOANNEM GEORGIUM GODELMANNUM mihi scribendi occasio daretur, plenius scribendum duxi. Vivo, ut possum, quando iniquitas temporis et fortunae maiora spectare non sinit. Calamitas belli Carolini oppressit meam patriam. Nam Dux CAROLUS totam fere occupavit Livoniam. Sed qua felicitate, eventus docebit. Novisti illius tyranni dictum: aliena appetendo propria amisit. Rex statusque Poloniae de bello per triennium magnis sumptibus gerendo in Comitijs convenerunt. Funesta ergo omnia in mea patria sunt. Quanta interim animi virtute et constantia opus sit mihi, facile intelligis, qui et bonis omnibus et maximis sumptibus propterea factis excidi. Spem tamen reliquam in DEI auxilio fixam habeo, fore ut postquam nubila et turbulenta ista tempora transierunt, pax florentissima et quies nobis restituatur. Interim tu me benevolentia et fide prosequaris singulari velim.</p><p>Quae ad D. CHYTRAeUM de tuae vitae historia scripsisti, heredes eius mihi tradiderunt, quae plene meis historicis meditationibus inseram, nec alia tui ornandi argumenta omittam. Nam te virum bonum et vere syncerum cognovi, quae virtutes profecto pulcherrimam istam artem tuam, in qua praecellis, elegantissime illuminant. Quod vero tardius liber meus historiarum prodit, multa in causa sunt, quia et initium cuiusque rei grave est, et veritatem sequi in omnibus hoc seculo scis quam sit periculosum. Deinde alijs adhuc occupor elaborandis. Mox tamen lucem videbunt, ubi meo genio ita visum fuerit.</p><p>Vale interim, vir optime, et magnam salutem tuos domesticos meo nomine imperti.<br>Datae Rostochio 10 die Martij A. 1601.<br>Tui amantiss.<br>CHRISTOPH. STURCIUS.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 403; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Orationem de SIGIS. AUG. B. M. a me habitam adiungo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and most noble man, Master Tycho Brahe, most excellent Mathematician of His Imperial Majesty, friend and patron much to be observed. Magnificent and most noble Master Tycho, friend to be honoured. Greeting.</b></p><p>Since a sure occasion of writing to me was given through the magnificent Master Johann Georg Gödelmann, I judged I should write more fully. I live as I can, since the iniquity of the times and of fortune does not permit [me] to look to greater things. The calamity of the Carolinian war has oppressed my homeland. For Duke Charles has occupied almost all Livonia. But with what good fortune, the outcome will teach. You know that tyrant's saying: \"by coveting what is another's, he lost his own.\" The King and the Estates of Poland have agreed, in the Diet, on waging war for three years at great expense. All things, therefore, are deadly in my homeland. With how great virtue and constancy of mind I meanwhile have need, you easily understand — [I] who have fallen from all my goods, and from the greatest expenses made on that account. Yet I have my remaining hope fixed in God's help, that — after these cloudy and turbulent times have passed — a most flourishing peace and quiet may be restored to us. Meanwhile, I would wish that you pursue me with singular benevolence and faith.</p><p>What you wrote to Master Chytraeus about the history of your life, his heirs have handed over to me; which I will fully insert into my historical meditations, nor will I omit other matters for adorning you. For I have known you as a good and truly sincere man — which virtues indeed most elegantly illuminate that most beautiful art of yours, in which you excel. But that my book of histories comes forth rather slowly, there are many causes: both because the beginning of anything is hard, and because you know how dangerous it is, in this age, to follow truth in all things; then, I am still occupied in elaborating other [works]. Soon, however, they will see the light, when it shall so seem [good] to my disposition.</p><p>Farewell meanwhile, best man, and impart a great greeting to your household in my name.<br>Given at Rostock, the 10th day of March, in the year 1601.<br>Most loving of you,<br>Christoph Sturtz.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> I append the oration delivered by me on Sigismund August, of blessed memory.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Christoph Sturtz</b> (Sturcius), the <b>Rostock historian</b> — the <i>Sturtzius, historicus vester</i> whom Tycho names in brahe-t_16001116003 as a recipient of his autobiographical material. The witness confirms the link: Sturtz writes that <b>Chytraeus's heirs</b> have given him \"<b>what you wrote to Master [David] Chytraeus about the history of your life</b>,\" which he will insert into his own histories (the same biographical notice Tycho discussed in the Brasch letter).</li><li><b>The Livonian war — Sturtz's ruined homeland.</b> <i>Calamitas belli Carolini oppressit meam patriam … Dux Carolus totam fere occupavit Livoniam</i> — Sturtz's homeland is <b>Livonia</b>, ravaged by the <b>war of Duke Charles</b> (the future <b>Charles IX of Sweden</b>) against Poland-Lithuania over Livonia (1600–01); the Polish king and Diet have voted \"<b>three years of war at great expense</b>.\" Sturtz has lost his property. (The same Swedish-Polish conflict Tycho comments on in brahe-t_16000329002.) <i>aliena appetendo propria amisit</i> — \"by coveting another's he lost his own\" (the Phaedrus/Aesopic moral, applied to \"that tyrant,\" i.e. Duke Charles).</li><li><b><i>D. Godelmannus</b></i> = <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>, the Saxon legate and reliable courier (cf. brahe-t_15981112001, …16010308001).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Rostochio 10 die Martij 1601</i> = <b>10 March 1601</b>; Rostock (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Final line after the signature \"CHRISTOPH. STURCIUS.\", restored from Dreyer VIII p.403 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the Latin is rendered securely and the content (Sturtz as the Rostock historian receiving Tycho's life-history via Chytraeus's heirs; the devastation of his Livonian homeland in the Charles/Poland war; Gödelmann as courier) is clear and dovetails with brahe-t_16001116003 and the Swedish-war thread.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. The 30-page slice (Dreyer VIII pp. 374-404) is chronologically ordered Sept.1600-Feb.1601 correspondence; searched it in visual chunks and located the target letter (Christoph Sturtz to Tycho, 10 March 1601) complete on p. 403, headed 'CHR. STURCIUS AD TYCHONEM 10/20 MART. 1601'. Confirmed source_text, including the postscript ('Orationem de SIGIS. AUG. B. M. a me habitam adiungo.') explicitly flagged in the packet as restored from p.403, is a verbatim match of what Dreyer actually prints there. Checked every name (Ioannes Georgius Godelmannus, Dux Carolus, D. Chytraeus, Sigismundus Augustus) and the date '10 die Martij A. 1601'. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Christoph Sturtz", "count": 3, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16001116002", "next": "brahe-t_16010415002", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16001116002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010415002"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports Duke Charles's occupation of almost all Livonia, the Polish Diet's resolution to wage war for three years, and personal losses from these events.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010310001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010311001", "d": "1601-03-11", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Lucas Backmeister → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 11 March 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO ET CLARISSIMO VIRO, DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHEN HAeREDITARIO IN KNUDTSTRUP INCLYTI REGNI DANIAe, ASTRONOMO EXCELLENTISSIMO, IN S. CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS AULA PRAGENSI, DOMINO ET AMICO SUO VETERI, PLURIMUM COLENDO. PRAGAM.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>NOBILISSIME et clariss. vir, Domine et amice vetus plurimumque colende. Reddidit mihi literas tuas vir clarissimus D. IOH. GEORGIUS GODELMANNUS, amicus meus singularis, cuius consuetudine iucundissima per dies complures usi sumus familiariter. Huic confectis feliciter negocijs suis Dresdam redituro, ducente ac fortunante Deo, 600 illos thaleros a Quaestore Iuniorum Principum tuo nomine acceptos, et hactenus apud me servatos, numeravi, recepta apocha eius. Commutavi autem thaleros Belgicos, quorum maior copia fuit, in alios imperiales, praeter paucos. Aurum, in quod eos commutarem, in hisce locis vix reperitur amplius, cum maioris sit valoris in inferiori et superiori Germania. Alios sexcentos ad instans Pascha tibi debitos Quaestor solvere nunc non potuit, licet compellatus eo nomine a D. D. BORDINGO Cancellario, iam etiam Lubecensium Consule. Excusat enim magnos sumptus et expensas superiori Octobri in funus Ducis SIGISMUNDI AUGUSTI p. m. factas Suerini, quos expendere oportuit iuniores Principes tanquam proximos haeredes. Petit igitur brevis adhuc temporis dilationem sibi concedi, et quamvis haberi pecunia ista potuisset, tamen intellexi grave futurum fuisse D. GODELMANNO eam secum deferre. De summa capitali, quam etiam te repetere scribis, quid responderit, nondum comperi. Inquiram vero diligentius, anne Lubecae vel Hamburgi mercatores sint, qui Pragae negotientur, ut per hos pecuniae Pragam transscribi possint; idem tu inquiras inter Pragenses mercatores. Mea sane officia et studia tibi non deerunt.</p><p>Literas ad D. CRAGIUM in Daniam mittendas non vidi. Accepi vero alias tuis adiunctas et cuidam CASPARO AGRICOLAe, Musico regio, inscriptas, quas soluta glacie et navigatione repetita, quae asperitate hyemis diu intermissa fuit, Hafniam transmittam. M. BRASCHIO, qui minus recte aliquamdiu valuit, quod iussisti, indicavi. Amisimus iterum nuper virum et Iureconsultum praestantissimum D. HENRICUM CAMERARIUM, qui cum patriae suae Brunsuigae in dissidio cum Principe eius componendo inservijsset, et in itinere non procul Soltquella Marchiae oppido abesset, subita cordis compressione extinctus est die 11 Februarij. Funus huc advectum 27 eiusdem terrae mandatum est honorifice. Hemiplecticus etiam est D. MARCUS LUSCOVIUS Iuriscoss. vir excellens et nunc facultatis senior. Ita nos visitat Dominus, ut appareat magnas imminere in Academia nostra mutationes.</p><p>CAROLUS Sueciae Dux magna celeritate et successu Livoniam occupat, et quamvis exiguam nuper cladem accepit, tamen ab altera parte Dörptam urbem in finibus Livoniae et Moscoviae oportuno loco sitam vi occupavit, ac pleraque iam loca munita tenet, praeter Rigam urbem primariam, cuius tamen vicinas arces ita occupat, ut commeatu eos terra marique intercludere possit, nisi auxilia maturius a Rege Poloniae submittantur. Et haec omnia hyeme maxima ex parte egit. Habet confoederatum sibi Moscovum, et quod mirum est, Suecia interim tota quiescit, et rerum necessariarum copia abundat. Poloni dicuntur in proximis regni Comitijs tres hostes proclamasse, Suecum, Moscovum et Walachum, et in tres annos magnam pecuniae summam ad bella haec Regi promisisse. Eventum reget Deus pro suo beneplacito, non pro hominum arbitrio aut potentia. In his regionibus, Dei beneficio, tranquillitas est, et optimus Princeps noster, Dux ULRICUS, recte adhuc valet. Ego tibi tuisque omnibus valetudinem prosperam diuturnamque opto. Salutant te filij mei IOHANNES et LUCAS, qui Theologiam iam profitetur hîc publice, et sponsus est, reverenter. Ex D. GODELMANNO Pragam aliquando veniente copiosius de statu nostro audies.</p><p>Rostochij die 11 Martij Anno 1601.<br>Nobil. et Excell. T. studiosiss.<br>LUCAS BACMEISTERUS D.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 405; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>M. CHRISTIANUS LONGICAMPIANUS mathematicus paulo post reditum suum Praga in Daniam traiecit, ubi adhuc vivit, nescio tamen quo in loco. Amo hominem, et optaßem nobiscum eum manere potuiße propter studia mathematica, quae fere iam hic iacent. Iterum vale.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble and most renowned man, Lord Tycho Brahe, hereditary [lord] in Knudstrup of the renowned Kingdom of Denmark, most excellent Astronomer, in the Prague court of the Sacred Imperial Majesty, his Lord and old friend, much to be cultivated. To Prague.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>Most noble and most renowned man, Lord and old friend much to be cultivated. The most renowned man Lord <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>, my especial friend — whose most pleasant company I enjoyed familiarly for several days — delivered your letter to me. To him, his business happily concluded, about to return to Dresden (God leading and prospering), I <b>paid out those 600 thalers received in your name from the Treasurer of the Junior Princes</b> and hitherto kept with me, taking his receipt. But I exchanged the <b>Belgian thalers</b>, of which there was the greater quantity, into <b>imperial</b> ones, except a few; for gold, into which I might change them, is scarcely to be found any more in these parts, being of greater value in Lower and Upper Germany. The <b>other six hundred</b>, owed you at the coming Easter, the Treasurer could not now pay — although urged on that account by Lord Doctor <b>Bording, Chancellor, [and] now also Consul [Burgomaster] of the Lübeckers</b> — for he excuses [it by] the great costs and expenses made last October at <b>Schwerin for the funeral of Duke Sigismund August, of pious memory</b>, which the junior Princes, as nearest heirs, had to bear; he asks, therefore, that a delay of yet a short time be granted him. And although that money could have been had, I understood it would have been burdensome for Lord Gödelmann to carry it with him. Concerning the <b>principal sum</b>, which you write you are also demanding back, what he answered I have not yet learned; but I will inquire more diligently whether there be at Lübeck or Hamburg merchants who do business at Prague, so that through these the moneys may be <b>transferred by bill to Prague</b> — let you inquire the same among the Prague merchants. My services and zeal will surely not be wanting to you.</p><p>The letters to be sent to Lord <b>Cragius</b> [Krag] in Denmark I did not see. But I received others, enclosed with yours and addressed to a certain <b>Caspar Agricola, the royal Musician</b>, which — the ice melted and navigation resumed (long interrupted by the harsh winter) — I will transmit to Copenhagen. To Master <b>Brasch</b>, who for a while was less than well, I conveyed what you ordered. We have again lately <b>lost a most excellent man and Jurisconsult, Lord Henrik Camerarius</b>, who — having served his fatherland Brunswick in composing its dispute with its Prince, and being on a journey not far from <b>Salzwedel</b>, a town of the [Brandenburg] Mark — was carried off by a sudden constriction of the heart on <b>11 February</b>; the body, brought hither, was honourably committed to earth on the 27th of the same. Also Lord <b>Marcus Luscovius</b>, jurisconsult, an excellent man and now senior of the faculty, is struck with one-sided paralysis. So the Lord visits us, that great changes appear to be imminent in our <b>University</b>.</p><p><b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b>, occupies <b>Livonia</b> with great speed and success; and although he lately took a slight defeat, on the other side he seized by force the city of <b>Dorpat [Tartu]</b>, conveniently set on the border of Livonia and Muscovy, and now holds most fortified places except <b>Riga</b> the chief city — whose neighbouring castles, however, he so occupies that he can cut them off from supply by land and sea, unless aid be sent in time by the <b>King of Poland</b>; and all this he did, for the most part, in winter. He has the <b>Muscovite</b> confederated with him; and — what is wonderful — Sweden meanwhile is wholly at rest, and abounds in necessaries. The <b>Poles</b> are said, in the last Diet of the kingdom, to have proclaimed three enemies — the Swede, the Muscovite, and the Wallachian — and to have promised the King a great sum for these wars for three years. God will govern the outcome by his good pleasure, not by men's will or power. In these regions, by God's grace, there is tranquillity, and our best Prince, <b>Duke Ulrich</b>, is still well. I wish you and all yours prosperous and lasting health. My sons <b>John</b> and <b>Lucas</b> — [the latter] who now publicly professes Theology here, and is betrothed — greet you reverently. From Lord Gödelmann, coming to Prague at some time, you will hear more fully of our state.</p><p>Rostock, 11 March, the year 1601.<br>Most devoted to Your Nobility and Excellence,<br>Lucas Backmeister, Doctor.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Master Christian Longomontanus, the mathematician, a little after his return from Prague crossed over into Denmark, where he still lives — in what place, however, I do not know. I love the man, and I could have wished that he had been able to remain with us, for the sake of mathematical studies, which now lie here almost [abandoned]. Again, farewell.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Bacmeister, 1530–1608), <b>Superintendent of Rostock</b> and theologian (cf. brahe-t_16000916002, brahe-t_16001115001). Reply carried via Gödelmann.</li><li><b>The Mecklenburg debt (confirmation).</b> Backmeister reports the mechanics of the <b>first 600-thaler interest tranche</b>: paid to <b>Gödelmann</b> (returning to Dresden) against receipt, with the <b>Belgian thalers exchanged into imperial</b> ones (gold being scarce locally). The <b>second 600 (due Easter)</b> the <b>Treasurer of the junior Princes</b> could not pay — though pressed by <b>Bording</b> (here styled <b>\"Chancellor, and now also Consul [Burgomaster] of Lübeck\"</b>) — pleading the <b>funeral expenses for Duke Sigismund August</b> of Mecklenburg at <b>Schwerin</b> last October (which the junior princes, as heirs, bore; cf. the death reported in brahe-t_16000920002). On the <b>principal</b>, no answer yet; Backmeister proposes transfer <b>by bill of exchange</b> through Lübeck/Hamburg–Prague merchants.</li><li><b>Rostock news.</b> Letters for <b>Krag</b> (<i>Cragius</i>) and <b>Caspar Agricola, the royal [Danish] Musician</b> to be forwarded to Copenhagen once the ice clears; <b>Brasch</b> \"for a while unwell.\" Two academic losses: the jurist <b>Henrik Camerarius</b> (d. 11 Feb 1601 of a sudden \"constriction of the heart\" near Salzwedel, after mediating Brunswick's dispute with its Duke; buried 27 Feb), and <b>Marcus Luscovius</b>, jurist and faculty-senior, struck with <b>hemiplegia</b> — \"great changes imminent in our University.\"</li><li><b>The Polish–Swedish war (cross-corpus).</b> <b>Charles of Sweden</b> sweeping through <b>Livonia</b> (taking <b>Dorpat/Tartu</b>, threatening <b>Riga</b>), allied with <b>Muscovy</b>; the <b>Poles</b> at their Diet declaring three enemies (Swede, Muscovite, Wallachian) and voting three years' war-funds. Dovetails with the news of brahe-t_16010321001 (Henniochus), brahe-t_16000920002 (Brasch), and brahe-t_16010418001 (Rollenhagen). <b>Duke Ulrich</b> reported well.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Lucas Backmeister (and his sons <b>John</b> and <b>Lucas the Younger</b>, a theology professor); <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>; <b>Jacob Bording</b> (Chancellor &amp; Lübeck Consul); the Mecklenburg <b>Treasurer</b>; <b>Duke Sigismund August</b> of Mecklenburg (d., funeral Oct 1600); <b>Niels/Krag (<i>Cragius</i>)</b>; <b>Caspar Agricola</b> (Danish royal musician); <b>Martin Brasch</b>; <b>Henrik Camerarius</b> (jurist, d.); <b>Marcus Luscovius</b> (jurist); <b>Charles of Sweden</b>; <b>Duke Ulrich III</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Rostochij die 11 Martij Anno 1601</i>; Lutheran Rostock kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript (Longomontanus crossed Prague→Denmark, \"adhuc vivit\"), restored from Dreyer VIII p.405 (after \"Lucas Bacmeisterus D.\"; dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear humanist Latin, read securely; the debt-mechanics (the 600 paid to Gödelmann with the Belgian→imperial conversion; the second 600 delayed by the ducal funeral; Bording's office; the bill-of-exchange proposal) and the Rostock/war news are explicit and corpus-corroborated. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (Lucas Bacmeister to Tycho, 11 March 1601), including the restored postscript on Longomontanus, against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.404-405 page images. Every proper name (Godelmann, Bording, Schwerin/Suerini, Sigismund August, Cragius/Krag, Caspar Agricola, Brasch, Henrik Camerarius, Soltquella, Marcus Luscovius, Charles of Sweden, Dorpat, Riga, Ulrich, John and Lucas Bacmeister, Longomontanus) and every number/date (600 thalers, another 600, 11 February, 27th, three enemies, three years, 11 March 1601) matches Dreyer word for word, and the postscript ('M. Christianus Longicampianus...Iterum vale') is present in full and matches exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Lucas Bacmeister", "count": 4, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16000919002", "next": "brahe-t_16010415001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000916002", "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010415001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports paying out 600 thalers to Gödelmann on Tycho's behalf, exchanging Belgian for imperial coin, and notes the other 600 thalers will be due at Easter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010311001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010321001", "d": "1601-03-21", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Cristophorus Henniochus", "ck": "Cristophorus Henniochus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Christophorus Henniochus → Tycho Brahe — Rostock, 21 March 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>MAGNIFICO ET GENEROSO DOMINO D. TYCHONI BRAHAe, DOMINO HAeREDITARIO IN KNUSTORPFF ET HEERESWART, CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS CONSILIARIO DIGNISSIMO, AMICO SUO ET DOMINO SUMMA OBSERVANTIA COLENDO.</p><p>S. P.</p><p>OCCASIONE oblata intermittere non debui, quin aliquid literarum ad te, Magnifice Dom. TYCHO, darem, et quidem per eundem, quem ipse praesentem aliquoties cupivisti, quo etiam hisce in locis exercitatiorem sphagirum nescimus. Novissimae tuae literae sunt quas misisti per NICOLAUM MAUL quondam domesticum tuum, in quibus etiam quae ad grammatophorum expedienda praecepisti exequutus sum.</p><p>Quoad Politicum statum omnia apud nos sunt tranquilla, nihilominus tamen multae gliscunt inter magnos dominos, principes et reges simultates, quae bellorum semina et initia esse solent. Inter CAROLUM Sueciae nunc protectorem et urbem Lubecam quomodo convenit, te non latet, quod negocium nondum est consopitum, licet Lubecenses multum in eo laboraverint. Comitia Polonica nuper Warsoviae, quo et Brandenburgicus Elector, adiunctis in adsistentiam Regis Daniae, nostri principis et nonnullorum aliorum, petitum successionem in Feudum Brutenicum, legatos miserat, celebrabantur, quod denegatum illi esse intelligo, nisi regem Poloniae, Suecia nunc privatum, postliminum regem fieri opitulentur. In dictis etiam Comitijs Poloni hostes suos, ut fertur, declararunt, CAROLUM Sueciae, qui iam dudum bellum in Livonia Polonis intulit, et eousque progressus est in Livonia, ut Polonis ibidem nihil restet praeter Rigam. Et meo quidem iudicio hoc bellum non solum ad CAROLUM pertinere videtur, nisi temeritatis sibi hocipso vitium attrahat. Alter hostis Polonorum dictus est Magnus dux Moscoviae, tertius vero Walachiae Waiwoda. Utrum tot hostibus Poloni simul stare poterint, eventus docebit. In Belgico magnus est apparatus iterum belli praefixi: ALBERTUS dux Austriae exercitum ducet in Hollandiam. Hollandici status non segnius vim eius aversari moliuntur. Aestas futura absque Tragedijs magnis non praeteribit, uti praesens est forma rerum. Rex Portugaliae SEBASTIANUS in conflictu Africano amissus, nunc redivivus, ab Hispano non agnoscitur, a suis vero affectatur, qui exutus regno apud Regem Galliae hospitio fruitur. Proh deum, quanta Metamorphosis ista! EXSECCIUS Comes Angliae et successor Reginae in regno hactenus muscitatus, ante biennium a Regina in Irlandiam cum Classe Nautica missus, ob rem non satis fideliter, ut accusatur, gestam, in Parlamento damnatus, et captivus de vita periclitatur. In Patria tua Regno Daniae cura et expensis Regis nonnulla fortalitia et Castella, ut vocant, exstruuntur, nec non in Gothlandia idem fit. Ungarica a nobis non expectabis, quin potius quid rerum ibi agitur, a te perdiscere cupimus. Haec per occationem ad te pervenire volui.</p><p>Vale, ut bene etiam sit tuis.<br>Datae Rostochij 21 Martij anni 1601.<br>T. Mag. observantiss.<br>CHRISTOPHO. HENNIOCHUS Med.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 408; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Dux Brunsuiccensis civitati denuo commeatum intercipit, nec ulla ratione placabilis dicitur, inter quos, missis et datis a Caesarea Maiestate commissarijs, nuper frustra de pace tractatum esse, notum est.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the magnificent and generous Lord, Lord Tycho Brahe, hereditary Lord in Knudstrup and Heereswart, most worthy Councillor of the Imperial Majesty, his friend and Lord to be honoured with the utmost respect.</b></p><p>Heartiest greeting.</p><p>An occasion being offered, I ought not to omit to send you some bit of a letter, magnificent Lord Tycho — and that by the very man whom you yourself several times wished present, than whom we know no more practised spagyrist in these parts. Your most recent letters are those you sent through Nicolaus Maul, formerly your servant, in which I also carried out what you directed for despatching the letter-carrier.</p><p>As to the political situation, with us all is tranquil; nevertheless many enmities are swelling among the great lords, princes, and kings, such as are wont to be the seeds and beginnings of wars. How matters stand between Charles, now Protector of Sweden, and the city of Lübeck does not escape you; that business is not yet laid to rest, though the Lübeckers have laboured much over it. The Polish Diet was lately held at Warsaw, to which the Elector of Brandenburg, too, had sent envoys — with those of the King of Denmark, of our own prince, and of some others, joined in support — to seek the succession to the Prussian fief; which I understand was refused him, unless they help the King of Poland, now deprived of Sweden, to be restored as king by right of return. In that same Diet the Poles also, as is reported, declared their enemies: Charles of Sweden, who long since carried war into Livonia against the Poles, and has advanced so far in Livonia that nothing remains to the Poles there but Riga. And in my judgement this war seems not to concern Charles alone — unless by this very act he draws upon himself the charge of rashness. The second enemy of the Poles was named the Grand Duke of Muscovy, and the third the Voivode of Wallachia. Whether the Poles will be able to stand against so many enemies at once, the outcome will show. In the Low Countries there is again great preparation for an appointed war: Albert, Archduke of Austria, will lead an army into Holland; the Dutch States are contriving no less actively to repel his force. The coming summer will not pass without great tragedies, such as the present shape of things is. The King of Portugal, Sebastian, lost in the African battle, now come back to life, is not acknowledged by the Spaniard, but is favoured by his own people; he, stripped of his kingdom, enjoys hospitality with the King of France. Good God, how great a Metamorphosis is that! Essex, Earl of England and reputed successor of the Queen in the kingdom — hitherto kept quiet — sent two years ago by the Queen into Ireland with a naval fleet, [and] for the affair not faithfully enough conducted (as he is charged), condemned in Parliament, is a captive and in peril of his life. In your fatherland, the kingdom of Denmark, by the King's care and expense certain fortresses and castles (as they call them) are being built, and the same is being done in Gotland. Hungarian news you will not expect from us — rather, we desire to learn from you what is going on there. These things I wished to reach you, as occasion served.</p><p>Farewell, and may it be well with your people too.<br>Given at Rostock, 21 March, the year 1601.<br>Most respectful of Your Magnificence,<br>Christophorus Henniochus, physician.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> The Duke of Brunswick is again cutting off the city's provisioning, and is said to be by no means appeasable; it is known that, commissioners having lately been sent and appointed by the Imperial Majesty, peace was treated of between them, but in vain.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Christophorus Henniochus</b> (Christoph Henioch), a <b>physician</b> (<i>Med.</i> = <i>Medicinae Doctor</i>) at <b>Rostock</b>, already in correspondence with Tycho (cf. Tycho→Henniochus, brahe-t_16000329003). A learned medical/alchemical contact of Tycho's Rostock circle.</li><li><b>Significance — a European news-letter (March 1601).</b> The body is a compact <b>survey of European affairs</b>, the kind of manuscript \"avviso\" exchanged among the learned. Its sweep:</li><li><b>Sweden/Lübeck.</b> <i>Carolus … nunc protector Sueciae</i> = <b>Duke Charles</b> (later <b>Charles IX</b>), then <b>Protector/Regent of Sweden</b> (he took the crown only in 1604); his unsettled quarrel with the Hanseatic city of <b>Lübeck</b>.</li><li><b>The Polish Diet at Warsaw &amp; the Prussian fief.</b> The <b>Elector of Brandenburg</b> (Joachim Friedrich), backed by envoys of <b>Denmark</b>, of <i>\"our prince\"</i> (the <b>Duke of Mecklenburg</b> — Rostock's overlord; Henniochus writes \"our\"), and others, sought the <b>succession to the <i>Feudum Brutenicum</b></i> (the <b>Ducal Prussian fief</b>, held of the Polish crown) — refused, conditional on aiding <b>Sigismund III Vasa</b>, <i>\"King of Poland, now deprived of Sweden,\"</i> to recover the Swedish throne <i>postliminio</i> (by right of return).</li><li><b>The Polish-Swedish war in Livonia.</b> The Poles' declared enemies: <b>Charles of Sweden</b>, who had pushed so far into <b>Livonia</b> that \"nothing remains to the Poles there but <b>Riga</b>\"; the <b>Grand Duke of Muscovy</b>; and the <b>Voivode of Wallachia</b>.</li><li><b>The Low Countries.</b> <b>Archduke Albert of Austria</b> (sovereign of the Spanish Netherlands) preparing to lead an army into <b>Holland</b>, resisted by the <b>Dutch States</b> — \"the coming summer will not pass without great tragedies.\"</li><li><b>The false Sebastian of Portugal.</b> <b>King Sebastian</b>, \"lost in the African battle\" (<b>Alcácer-Quibir, 1578</b>) and \"now come back to life\" — one of the <b>Sebastianist pretenders</b> — unacknowledged by Spain but favoured by his partisans, sheltering \"with the King of France.\"</li><li><b>Essex.</b> <i>Exseccius Comes Angliae</i> = <b>Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex</b>, rumoured successor to <b>Queen Elizabeth I</b>; sent to <b>Ireland</b> (1599), charged with mismanaging the campaign, \"condemned in Parliament … in peril of his life.\"</li><li><b>Denmark/Gotland.</b> <b>Christian IV</b> building fortresses in Denmark and Gotland.</li><li><b>Hungary.</b> Henniochus expects <i>Tycho</i> (at the imperial court) to be his source for news of the <b>Ottoman–Habsburg \"Long War.\"</b></li><li><b>News-lag (worth noting).</b> By 21 March 1601 (O.S.) <b>Essex had already been executed</b> (25 February 1601); Henniochus reports him only as \"a captive in peril of his life\" — the news had not yet reached Rostock. A clean illustration of contemporary information-latency, preserved (not \"corrected\") per protocol §3.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Christophorus Henniochus (sender); <b>Nicolaus Maul</b>, <i>\"formerly your [Tycho's] servant,\"</i> the prior courier; the unnamed <b>spagyrist</b> (<i>sphagirus</i> — an alchemical physician) carrying this letter, whom Tycho had wished to engage; Charles of Sweden; the Elector of Brandenburg; Sigismund III of Poland; Archduke Albert; King Sebastian (the pretender); the Earl of Essex; Queen Elizabeth I; Christian IV.</li><li><b>Address crux (flagged).</b> Tycho is styled <i>Dominus hereditarius in Knustorpff et <b>Heereswart</b></i>. <i>Knustorpff</i> = <b>Knudstrup</b> (his hereditary seat); <b><i>Heereswart</b></i> is uncertain — most likely the sender's garbling of a second estate-name (possibly <b>Herrevad</b>, where Tycho lived early, though he did not hold it hereditarily). Rendered as written and flagged; not resolved.</li><li><b>Lexical flags.</b> <i>muscitatus</i> (of Essex, \"hitherto kept quiet/silent\") is non-standard — apparently a coinage for \"kept mute/inactive\"; rendered by sense. The sentence <i>hoc bellum non solum ad Carolum pertinere videtur, nisi temeritatis … vitium attrahat</i> is slightly compressed; read as \"this war concerns more than Charles alone — unless he is simply being rash,\" and flagged.</li><li><b>Greek/technical.</b> <i>grammatophorus</i> (Greek, \"letter-carrier\"); <i>sphagirus</i> (= <i>spagyrus</i>, a Paracelsian alchemical physician) — kept and glossed (cf. §5.4 register).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Rostochij 21 Martij anni 1601</i> — Rostock kept the <b>Julian calendar (O.S.)</b>. The dataset `label` (\"31 marts\") disagrees with the dateline and the slug (16010321001, the 21st); the <b>label is in error</b> and is rejected in favour of the source's own date, per §4.1.</li></ul><p>- <b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> News-postscript (Brunswick's renewed blockade + the failed imperial peace mediation), restored from Dreyer VIII, p. 408 (this is the Christophorus Henniochus→Tycho letter; the postscript follows Henniochus's signature).<br>No correction added. The single flagged defect (an alleged 'exercitum' interpolation) was checked against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.407, which itself prints 'ALBERTUS dux Austriae exercitum ducet in Hollandiam' — identical to our published text. The audit's premise does not hold; the text stands as published.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the news-letter is rendered securely and its political content (Sweden/Lübeck, the Warsaw Diet and Prussian fief, the Livonian war and Riga, the Netherlands, the false Sebastian, Essex, Danish fortifications, Hungary) is coherent and identifiable; graded <b>[B]</b> for three flagged, non-smoothed points — the uncertain address-name <i>Heereswart</i>, the non-standard <i>muscitatus</i>, and the slightly compressed <i>non solum … pertinere</i> clause — none of which affects the overall sense. The catalogue-label date error and the Essex news-lag are handled per protocol §3.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.407-408 in full as page images (within the 406-409 slice, flanked by the Camineaus and Rosenkrantz letters). The flagged postscript ('Dux Brunsuiccensis civitati denuo commeatum intercipit, nec ulla ratione placabilis dicitur, inter quos, missis et datis a Caesarea Maiestate commissarijs, nuper frustra de pace tractatum esse, notum est.') IS genuinely printed by Dreyer on p.408, immediately after the signature block and before the next letter's heading -- the earlier restoration is verified word-for-word accurate, and its English rendering is correct. Names (Nicolaus Maul, Charles of Sweden, Essex, Sebastian of Portugal), the Warsaw Diet detail, and the closing date all match Dreyer. One unrelated single-word insertion ('exercitum', not in Dreyer) elsewhere in the letter caps the grade at C despite the letter being otherwise clean and the postscript restoration checking out.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's text has no 'exercitum' (army) here; source_text and the English both add the word. The surrounding context ('apparatus... belli') already implies a military force, so the added word does not materially distort the sense. Dreyer prints: <i>ALBERTUS dux Austriae ducet in Hollandiam</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the letter's single defect against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp.406-409 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). Dreyer's own printed page directly contradicts the audit's claim: 'exercitum' is present in the edition of record, not an interpolation. Rejected with high confidence; no other issues were observed in the passage.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Introduces the bearer as an experienced spagyrist and reports political news about Duke Charles of Sweden's dispute with Lübeck and the recent Polish Diet at Warsaw.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010321001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010322001", "d": "1601-03-22", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Balthasar Caminaeus", "ck": "Balthasar Caminaeus", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Frankfurt an der Oder", "t": "Balthasar Caminaeus → Tycho Brahe — Frankfurt an der Oder, 22 March 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>GENEROSO MAGNIFICO ET NOBILISSIMO VIRO DOMINO TYCHONI BRAHE, EQUITI DANO, S. CAeS. MAIESTATIS CONSILIARIO PRAeCIPUO ET MATHEMATICO LONGE CELEBERRIMO, DOMINO AC PATRONO OBSERVANDO.<br>PRAGA.</p><p>S.</p><p>RECTE perlatas esse meas ad te literas, generose et nobilissime vir, index scida superiore Decembri me docuit. Volui, uti suggestum fuerat, rem denuo et statim prehendere; sed avocarunt morbus uxoris, negotia clientum, sexcenta alia. Nunc paullo expeditior, et amanter a vidua illa CUNONIS filia invitatus, libenter ei hoc operae dare volui, ut, quod bono etiam publico futurum providerem, sollicitatoris partes susciperem, quo opus illud horologicum, multis, magnis ac diuturnis laboribus ac vigilijs elucubratum ac perfectum, lucem tandem sub tuo, aut cuiuscunque videatur nomine, aspicere possit. Superioribus addo hoc quoque, esse apud eandem viduam opus, secundum praecepta ista inchoatum, atque adeo semicompletum, quod morte praeventus absolvere auctor non potuit. Qui librum emat paullo liberalius, facile unâ idipsum quoque impetret; cui summam manum peritus in arte et libro isto instructus sine negotio imponat. Sumptum credo facile libenterque ferat pietate, sapientia, virtute et meritis in rempub. maximus, Divus Caesar RUDOLPHUS, mathematicis disciplinis ipse quoque et supra conditionem principum excultus, quem non ALPHONSUM alterum, sed et illo potiorem non immerito praedicamus. Neque dubium mihi est, quin monitu suggestuque tuo res procedat melius: quod ut facias, sive viduae illius pauperculae nomine, sive boni publici respectu, sive quod hanc operam haud difficulter te praestiturum virtus et humanitas mihi tua spondet, quam possum officiose submisseque rogo. Qua de re literas tuas, vel si ijs non vacat, saltem nutum tuum expecto. Si quid ad me velis, et occasio alia non sit, Bratislaviam ad clarissimum Dn. MONAVIUM mittere possis, qui, qua est humanitate et virtute, id porro haud gravate curet. Vale, vir generose et optime, meque et studia mea, quibus ipsis quoque MAeCENATE opus sit, apud Sacram Caesaream Maiestatem, cui vitam ac felicitatem perpetuam precor, tibi commendata habe.</p><p>Vale iterum.<br>Francofurto cis Viadrum a. d. XII Martij Anno MDCI.<br>Generostis ac magnif. t. observantiss.<br>BALTHASAR CAMINAAeUS I. V. D. et professor publ.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the generous, magnificent and most noble man, Lord Tycho Brahe, Danish knight, principal councillor of His Imperial Majesty and by far the most celebrated mathematician, his lord and patron to be revered. Prague.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>That my letters were duly delivered to you, generous and most noble man, a note informed me last December. I wished, as had been suggested, to take the matter up anew and at once; but the illness of my wife, the affairs of clients, and six hundred other things called me away. Now a little freer, and kindly invited by that widow, the daughter of Cuno, I have willingly wished to devote this effort to her: that — foreseeing it would be for the public good as well — I might take up the part of advocate, so that that horological work, elaborated and perfected by many, great and long-continued labours and vigils, might at last see the light under your name, or whosever's may seem [best]. To the above I add this too: that there is with the same widow [another] work, begun according to those precepts, and indeed half-completed, which the author, forestalled by death, could not finish. Whoever should buy the book a little more liberally would easily obtain that very thing along with it; on which an expert, skilled in the art and instructed in that book, might without trouble lay the finishing hand. The cost, I believe, would be easily and gladly borne by the Divine Emperor Rudolph — greatest in piety, wisdom, virtue, and services to the commonwealth, himself too cultivated in the mathematical disciplines beyond the condition of princes — whom we proclaim, not undeservedly, not a second Alphonso, but even greater than he. Nor do I doubt that by your advice and prompting the matter will go forward the better; and that you would do this — whether in the name of that poor little widow, or out of regard for the public good, or because your virtue and humanity assure me you will render this service without difficulty — I ask as dutifully and submissively as I can. On which matter I await your letter, or, if you have no time for that, at least your nod. If you should want anything from me, and there be no other occasion, you could send it to Breslau, to the most renowned Lord Monavius, who, being of the humanity and virtue he is, would see to it further without grudging. Farewell, generous and excellent man; and hold me and my studies — which themselves too have need of a Maecenas — commended to you with the Sacred Imperial Majesty, to whom I pray perpetual life and happiness.</p><p>Farewell again.<br>From Frankfurt-on-the-Oder, the 22nd of March, in the year 1601.<br>Most observant of Your Generosity and Magnificence,<br>Balthasar Caminaeus, Doctor of Both Laws and public professor.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> letter <b>to</b> Tycho at Prague. <b>Balthasar Caminaeus</b> (Caminäus), <i>Iuris utriusque doctor et professor publicus</i> — <b>doctor of both laws and public professor at Frankfurt an der Oder</b> (<i>Francofurtum cis Viadrum</i>, \"Frankfurt on this side of the Oder,\" to distinguish it from Frankfurt-am-Main).</li><li><b>Purpose.</b> Caminaeus acts as <b>advocate (<i>sollicitator</i>)</b> for a widow seeking to get a deceased author's <b>horological work</b> (<i>opus horologicum</i> — a treatise on, or design for, time-measuring / astronomical clockwork) into print, plus a second, <b>half-finished</b> work in the same vein. He asks Tycho to lend his name and influence, and floats <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b> as the natural patron to bear the cost.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>vidua illa Cunonis filia</i> = \"that widow, <b>the daughter of Cuno</b>\" — the heir pressing for publication; neither she nor her late husband (the <i>auctor</i> of the horological work) is further identified in the source.</li><li><i>Divus Caesar Rudolphus</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>, praised as himself learned in mathematics and likened to <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b> (\"the Wise,\" patron of the <i>Alfonsine Tables</i>) — <i>non Alphonsum alterum, sed et illo potiorem</i>, \"not a second Alfonso, but greater than he.\"</li><li><i>clarissimus Dn. Monavius</i> = <b>Jacob Monau (Jacobus Monavius)</b> (1546–1603), the Breslau humanist and patron of letters, proposed as a relay for any reply.</li><li><i>Maecenas</i> invoked generically as the type of the literary patron.</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Francofurtum cis Viadrum</i> = <b>Frankfurt an der Oder</b>; <i>Bratislavia</i> here = <b>Breslau / Wrocław</b> (the Neo-Latin name <i>Vratislavia</i>, spelled <i>Bratislavia</i>), Monau's city — <b>not</b> modern Bratislava.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae &amp; titles (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i>; <i>Vale … Vale iterum</i> → \"Farewell … Farewell again\"; subscription <i>Generosit[at]is ac magnif[icentiae] t[uae] observantiss[imus]</i> → \"Most observant of Your Generosity and Magnificence\"; <i>I. V. D.</i> = <i>Iuris Utriusque Doctor</i> (\"Doctor of Both Laws,\" civil and canon).</li><li><b>Calendar &amp; dating crux (protocol §1, §3).</b> The working transcription's dateline reads <i>a. d. XII Martij Anno MDCI</i> — literally \"the 12th of March 1601,\" which does not match the edition's placement. Read as Roman reckoning, the intended form is <b>a. d. XI Kal. Apriles</b> (<i>ante diem undecimum Kalendas Apriles</i>) = <b>22 March</b>, and Dreyer/the catalogue date the letter accordingly; the transcribed \"<i>XII Martij</i>\" is a corruption of that Roman dateline. Following Dreyer per protocol §1, the header gives <b>22 March 1601 (New Style)</b> — Frankfurt an der Oder being in Brandenburg, but the letter directed to Catholic-Imperial Prague and dated New Style in the dataset. The source form is preserved in the block; the reading is explained here rather than silently emended.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is clean and the gist (a professor advocating, on a widow's behalf, the posthumous publication of a horological treatise, with Rudolf II proposed as patron) is secure. Graded B for two reasons, both flagged rather than smoothed: (1) the <b>dateline crux</b> — the transcribed <i>a. d. XII Martij</i> is corrupt for <i>a. d. XI Kal. Apriles</i> (= 22 March), resolved by following Dreyer; (2) the principals of the publication scheme — <i>the widow, daughter of Cuno</i>, and the unnamed deceased <i>auctor</i> of the <i>opus horologicum</i> — cannot be identified from the text.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (heading, the widow-daughter-of-Cuno passage, the Rudolph/Alphonso comparison, Monavius/Breslau reference, date and signature) word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.406-407 as page images. Exact match, including the unusual 'Bratislaviam' (=Breslau/Wrocław, correctly rendered) and 'vidua CUNONIS filia' construction. Translation is accurate and complete throughout.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Advocates for publication of an unfinished horological work held by a widow, requesting Tycho's name and patronage to bring it to press.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010322001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010404001", "d": "1601-04-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Danish", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Prague, 4 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Danish · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (transcript / Afskrift)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>ERLIG OCH WELBYRDIG MANDT HOLGER ROSENKRANTZ TIL ROSENHOLM, MIN KIERRE SWOGER, SLECHT OCH BØRDT, OCH SØNDERLIGE GODE WENN, GANDSKE WENLIGEN TILLSKREFFVITT.</p><p>MINN gandske venlig hilsen nu och altiidt med Vor Herre. Kierre Holger Rossenkrantz, slecht och byr, swoger och sønderlig gode ven, Gudt almechtigste verre nu och altijdt hoß eder och beuare eder lenge och well frae allt det, eder kan werre skadelligt enten till saell eller liff, och nest megenn tacksigelse for allt bewijst gode, hvilcket ygien aff formuen att forskylde ieg altijdt will findiß willig och redebon.</p><p>Giffuendiß eder herhoß gandske wenligen tillkiende, att epthersom jeg nest Guds hielp achter engang emellumb paske och pinsdag nu nestkommendis her udj Prag paa dette Curtianiske huß, som jeg nu bor, att giørre min kierre datters erlig och welbyrdig iomfru Elisabet Brahes brøldup med en herremandt, født udj Westfalen, som tiener her y röm: key: may: gaardt, vedt nauffn Frantz Tegnagel till Campe, saa er derfor min gandske wenlig bøn till eder, att y wille well giørre och till forneffnte tijdt komme hidt, och giørre eder løstig och gladt med andre fornemme herrer och gott folck aff høibemelte key: may: raadt och anden y dette loufflig kongeriige Bohemen, som jeg forhaabiß daa till samme tijdt her hoss mig att forsamblis. Kierre Holger och kierre swoger, giører nu her udj, epthersom jeg mig till eder forlader, saa viit eder nogenlunde mueligt kan werre; eder och eders att tiene vdigien, will jeg altijdt findis willig. Och will jeg nu icke lenger opholde eder med denne min skriffuelse, menß nu och altijdt haffue eder Gudt almechtigste bode till saell och liff befallidt.</p><p>Daterit vdj Prag paa dett Curtianske huß, som ehr yderst vedt bøen, som jeg nu bor, den 4. aprilis anno 1601. Stylo novo.<br>Tyghe Brahe<br>egenh.</p>", "en": "<p><b>Honourable and well-born man Holger Rosenkrantz to Rosenholm, my dear brother-in-law, kin and blood, and especial good friend, written to in all friendliness.</b></p><p>My quite friendly greeting, now and always, with Our Lord. Dear Holger Rosenkrantz — kin and blood, brother-in-law and especial good friend — may God Almighty be now and always with you, and preserve you long and well from all that can be harmful to you, whether to soul or to life; and next, much thanksgiving for all the good [you have] shown, which to requite in turn, of [my] ability, I will always be found willing and ready.</p><p>Giving you herewith, in all friendliness, to know: that, since I intend — next, with God's help — at some time between Easter and Pentecost now coming, here at Prague, at this Curtius house where I now dwell, to make the wedding of the honourable and well-born maiden Elisabeth Brahe, my dear daughter, with a nobleman born in Westphalia who serves here in the Roman Imperial Majesty's court, by name Frans Tengnagel to Camp — so is, therefore, my quite friendly request to you, that you would do well, and come hither at the aforesaid time, and make yourself merry and glad with other distinguished lords and good folk of the high-mentioned Imperial Majesty's Council, and others in this lawful kingdom of Bohemia, who, I hope, will then at the same time be gathered here with me. Dear Holger, and dear brother-in-law, do now herein — as I rely upon you — so far as may be at all possible for you; to serve you and yours in return, I will always be found willing. And I will not now detain you longer with this my writing, but, now and always, have you commended to God Almighty, both to soul and to life.</p><p>Dated at Prague, at the Curtius house — which is at the outermost edge of the city — where I now dwell, the 4th of April, in the year 1601. New Style.<br>Tyge Brahe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>The wedding invitation.</b> A document of high biographical value: Tycho invites <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> (his kinsman) to the <b>wedding of his daughter Elisabeth Brahe to Frans Tengnagel</b>, to be held <b>at Prague between Easter and Pentecost 1601</b>. The marriage of <b>Elisabeth (Elisabet) Brahe</b> to <b>Frans Gansneb Tengnagel van de Camp</b> (<i>Frantz Tegnagel till Campe</i>) — Tycho's long-serving noble assistant (the \"living letter\" of the 1600 letters), here \"a nobleman born in <b>Westphalia</b> who serves at the Imperial court\" — took place at Prague in <b>June 1601</b>, a few months before Tycho's death (October 1601).</li><li><b>The Curtius house — a notable identification.</b> <i>dette Curtianiske hus … som er yderst ved byen</i> = \"<b>this Curtius house</b>, which is at the outermost edge of the city, where I now dwell.\" This is the Prague mansion of the late Imperial Vice-Chancellor <b>Jakob Kurz von Senftenau (Jacob Curtius</b>, d. 1594) — the very Curtius commended in brahe-t_15980626002 and mourned by Herwart in brahe-t_16000908001 — which Tycho occupied as his Prague residence and observatory after the move from Benátky.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned,\" addressed as <i>svoger, slægt og byrd</i> (\"brother-in-law, kin and blood\") — the kinsman of the Cramer/Liddel and 1598 letters (brahe-t_15980331001, …0524001, …0914001); asked to come and to bring the festal company of imperial councillors.</li><li><b>Orthography.</b> Late Danish chancery: <i>erlig/welbyrdig</i>, <i>swoger</i> (brother-in-law/kinsman), <i>brøldup</i> (bryllup, wedding), <i>iomfru</i> (maiden), <i>herremandt</i> (nobleman), <i>till saell eller liff</i> = <i>til sjæl eller liv</i> (\"to soul or to life\"), <i>forsamblis</i> (forsamles, be gathered), <i>egenh.</i> = <i>egen hånd</i> (\"own hand\"). Reading-verified as <b>Danish</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Prag … den 4. aprilis anno 1601. Stylo novo</i> = <b>4 April 1601, New Style</b> (explicit; Prague, imperial). Header agrees with the label. The wedding itself is set \"between Easter and Pentecost\" (so c. April–May/June 1601).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery Danish is rendered securely; the occasion (the invitation to Elisabeth Brahe's wedding to Frans Tengnagel at the Prague Curtius house, between Easter and Pentecost 1601), the recipient (Holger Rosenkrantz), the groom's identity and Westphalian origin, the residence (the former Curtius house), and the explicit New-Style date are all clear and corpus-/history-corroborated.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.217-219 as page images. Letter 'ERLIG OCH WELBYRDIG MANDT HOLGER ROSENKRANTZ TIL ROSENHOLM...' runs entirely on p.218, dated 'den 4. aprilis anno 1601. Stylo novo.' source_text matches Dreyer's print verbatim (including 'Stylo novo', genuinely present here). Collated opening/closing and every proper name and detail (Elisabeth Brahe, Frans Tengnagel to Camp/Campe, Westphalia, the Curtius house at the edge of Prague) against the Latin/Danish; the English tracks the source clause by clause with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 23, "prev": "brahe-t_16000803001", "next": "brahe-t_16010405001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Announces the planned wedding of his daughter Elisabeth Brahe to Frans Tengnagel at the Curtius house in Prague between Easter and Pentecost, and invites Rosenkrantz.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010404001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010405001", "d": "1601-04-05", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Holger Rosenkrantz — Prague, 5 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Copenhagen, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks Diplomatarium (Afskrift / later copy)</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>NON SALTEM GENERIS NOBILITATE SED ETIAM INSIGNI DOCTRINA ILLUSTRI VIRO OLIGERO ROSENKRANTZIO, DOMINO DE ROSENHOLM, AMICO, CONSANGVINEO ET AFFINI SUO INTEGERRIMO.</p><p>PRAePROPERUS Patruelis tui hinc ad vos discessus, exoptatissime mi OLIGERE, fecit, ut tibi, prout alias voluissem, tunc non scripserim; concredidi nihilominus illi nonnulla oretenus tibi secreto referenda, quod et praestitum esse, modo tecum fuit, nullus dubito. Caeterum nunc nactus commodiorem oportunitatem, has subsequi volui. Neque tamen eorum, quae ille viva voce (uti mihi persuadeo) tibi exposuit, quidpiam hîc repetam. Intercessionem Caesaream pro illo saepe et diligenter, interea dum abfuit, ursi, nec quidem unquam mihi denegata est a Domino CORRADUCIO, Vicecancellario, sed cum tam multa simul inciderint, gravissime Rempublicam concernentia, semper excusavit se, quod non haberet oportunitatem eiuscemodi particularia proponendi, antequam principaliora et magni momenti negotia expedita essent, et sancte affirmavit, nulla sua negligentia, sed necessitate sic efflagitante, id retardari. Rogavit itaque, ut hanc dilationem patienter ferrem, se nihilominus omnia in meliori forma effectui daturum. Nunc vero ante biduum mihi referri fecit, impetratam iam esse intercessionem illam, atque in secreto consilio, ut scribatur, conclusum. Quare se confestim, ut concipiatur et ad mundum exaretur, effecturum; unde adhuc pauculos dies exspectare oportebit, antequam voti compotes reddemur. Quin et postea aliquot interlabi poterunt, priusquam Caesarea Maitas sua manu subscripserit, adeo lente procedunt hîc omnia, praesertim hoc tempore, ob periculum a Turca imminens, propter occupatum Canisium et denuo ferme amissam Transylvaniam. In me tamen nulla erit mora, quin, ut negotium hoc mature absolvatur, operam dem; postea tibi exemplum mittam, originale autem mecum reservabo, donec FRIDERICUS redierit, sic enim fieri voluit, si res nimis diu protraherentur.</p><p>Habito nunc in aedibus a priore Procancellario Domino IACOBO CURTIO Laudatae Mem. exstructis et a viduâ relictâ liberalitate Caesareae Maitis pro 10000 Ioachimicis in meos usus coëmtis, quae tamen duplo pluris exaedificatori constiterunt. Accepique hîc exoptatam commoditatem pro studijs, observationibus et domesticis rebus debite administrandis; amoenissimus hortus aedes undique circumdat, Muro circumseptus. Constitui vero, favente Numine, in his ipsis aedibus Nuptias filiae ELIZABETAe cum Nobili viro FRANCISCO TEGNAGLIO, quem nosti, celebrare aliquo oportuno tempore inter Pascha et Pentecosten; quo nomine ad praecipuos quosdam et maxime singulares in Dania et Holsatia amicos Literas Nuptiales dedi, non quod eos interfuturos sperem, sed saltem ut conscij sint. Aliquos tamen ex intimis propriâ Personâ adventuros, aut saltem quempiam suo loco missuros confido. Cumque te inter sinceriores et integerrimos praecipuo loco reputem, quibus possum verbis, te rogatum velim, ut assumpta coniuge tuâ charissimâ, meâ ex fratre Nepte, tuâ praesentiâ hanc solennitatem augere et condecorare non degraveris. Iter non admodum longinquum, viae tunc erunt faciles, et sumptus non multum excedent domesticos. Licebit sic in via veteres tuos amicos Wittebergenses iucunde revisere, et hîc aulam Imperatoriam variaque visu digna cognoscere, quae tibi voluptati et forte etiam aliquando commodo erunt. Adsunt insuper in propinquo binae thermae, quas si sanitatis caussa ingredi lubuerit, minimo impendio id unâ fieri poterit. Interea sobolem tenellulam facile et tuto alicui Heroinae e consangvineis commendabis. Adveniet etiam (uti spero) Soror mea SOPHIA, et ubi adveneritis, tum demum diem certum nuptialem praefiniam, ut et hîc Illustres et praecipui viri, amici et noti invitentur. Rogavi FRIDERICUM, ut serio tecum hac de re ageret, daretque operam, quo persuaderet, licet ego de tua in me fide et constantiâ nihil addubitem. Quare pluribus id contendere nolo. Sed te avide et certo expecto, utque queas nobis salvus adesse. Vale, saluta coniugem et matrem tuam ex me officiosissime.</p><p>Datum Pragae in novo Uraniburgo Caesareo die 5. Aprilis Anno 1601, Stylo novo.<br>Tuus TYCHO BRAHE.</p><p>Filius meus TYCHO abijt cum Legatis Persicis per Italiam,<br>Galliam et Hispaniam transiturus, munitus<br>salvo conductu Imperatoris. Habes hîc<br>Effigies duorum præcipuorum ex Legatis, et Cæsaris responsum.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man illustrious not only by nobility of birth but also by distinguished learning, Holger Rosenkrantz, Lord of Rosenholm, his most upright friend, kinsman, and relative.</b></p><p>The over-hasty departure hence to you of your cousin, my most longed-for Holger, made it that I did not then write to you as I should otherwise have wished; nevertheless I entrusted to him some things to be related to you secretly by word of mouth, which (as he was just now with you) I do not doubt was done. But now, having got a more convenient opportunity, I wished these [lines] to follow — yet I will not repeat here anything he set out to you, as I am persuaded, by living voice. <b>The imperial intercession for him I urged often and diligently while he was away</b>, nor was it ever denied me by Lord <b>Coraduz</b>, the Vice-Chancellor; but since so many things befell at once, most gravely concerning the Commonwealth, he always excused himself that he had no opportunity of proposing such particulars before the more principal and weighty matters were dispatched, and solemnly affirmed that it was delayed by no negligence of his, but by pressing necessity. He asked, therefore, that I bear the delay patiently, [saying] he would nonetheless bring everything to effect in a better form. But now, two days ago, he had it reported to me that <b>that intercession has now been obtained, and resolved in the privy council that it be put in writing</b>; wherefore he will at once have it drafted and fair-copied — whence it will be needful to wait yet a few days before we are made masters of our wish. Nay, and afterward several [days] may slip by before the Imperial Majesty has signed it with his own hand — so slowly do all things proceed here, especially at this time, on account of the danger threatening from the <b>Turk</b>, with <b>Kanizsa occupied and Transylvania again nearly lost</b>. Yet in me there will be no delay; I will see that this business is completed in good time; afterward I will send you a copy, but keep the original with me <b>until Frederik returns</b> — for so he wished it done, should the matter be drawn out too long.</p><p>I now dwell in the house built by the former Vice-Chancellor Lord <b>Jacob Curtius</b>, of praised memory, and <b>bought from his widow, by the Imperial Majesty's liberality, for 10,000 joachimsthalers for my use</b> — which, however, cost the builder double the price; and I have here a most desired convenience for duly conducting my studies, observations, and domestic affairs, a most pleasant <b>garden</b> surrounding the house on all sides, enclosed by a wall. And I have resolved, God favouring, <b>to celebrate in this very house the wedding of my daughter Elisabeth with the noble man Franz Tengnagel, whom you know, at some convenient time between Easter and Pentecost</b>; on which account I have sent <b>Wedding-Letters</b> to certain chief and most especial friends in Denmark and Holstein — not that I hope they will attend, but only that they may be aware; yet I trust some of my intimates will come in person, or at least send someone in their place. And since I reckon you in a chief place among the more sincere and upright, with what words I can I would ask that, <b>taking your dearest wife — my niece by my brother — you would not find it burdensome to augment and adorn this solemnity by your presence</b>. The journey is not very long, the roads will then be easy, and the costs will not much exceed those at home. You will thus be able, on the way, pleasantly to revisit your old <b>Wittenberg</b> friends, and here to see the imperial court and various things worth seeing, which will be a pleasure — and perhaps sometime an advantage — to you. There are, moreover, two <b>thermal baths</b> nearby, which, if you wish to take for your health, can be done at the same time at very little expense. Meanwhile you will easily and safely commend your tender little child to some noble lady of [your] kin. <b>My sister Sophie</b>, too, will come (as I hope); and when you have [all] come, then at last I will fix the certain wedding-day, that here too illustrious and chief men, friends and acquaintances, may be invited. I have asked <b>Frederik</b> to deal seriously with you on this, and take pains to persuade you — though I doubt nothing of your faith and constancy toward me. Wherefore I will not press it further. But I eagerly and surely await you, [praying] that you may be able to be safely with us. Farewell; greet your wife and your mother from me most dutifully.</p><p>Given at Prague, in the new imperial Uraniborg, the 5th day of April, the year 1601, New Style.<br>Your Tycho Brahe.</p><p>My son Tycho has departed with the Persian Legates, about to pass through Italy, France, and Spain, furnished with the Emperor's safe-conduct. Here you have the Portraits of two of the chief among the Legates, and the Emperor's reply.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Holger Rosenkrantz</b> \"the Learned\" of Rosenholm — Tycho's <i>consanguineus et affinis</i> (cf. brahe-t_15980628001, brahe-t_15981231002). The MS is a later Langebek <i>afskrift</i>.<br>- <b>Significance — the wedding invitation, and the imperial intercession for Frederik Rosenkrantz.</b> Two intertwined matters:<br>  1. <b>The imperial intercession for Frederik Rosenkrantz.</b> Frederik (Holger's <i>patruelis</i>/cousin — the disgraced-then-rehabilitated nobleman now in imperial service; cf. brahe-t_16000926002) had come and gone (carrying secret oral messages). Tycho had repeatedly pressed <b>Coraduz</b> for an <b>imperial letter of intercession</b> on Frederik's behalf (toward his rehabilitation in Denmark); it was always granted in principle but delayed by graver state business, and is <b>now obtained and ordered drafted</b> in the privy council — awaiting only Rudolf II's signature, slow \"on account of the danger from the <b>Turk</b>, with Kanizsa occupied and Transylvania nearly lost.\" Tycho will keep the <b>original until Frederik returns</b>.<br>  2. <b>The wedding of Elisabeth Brahe and Franz Tengnagel (the central occasion).</b> Tycho announces he will <b>celebrate the wedding of his daughter Elisabeth with Franz Tengnagel</b> \"in this very house [the Curtius house], between Easter and Pentecost [1601],\" having sent <b>Wedding-Letters</b> to chief friends in <b>Denmark and Holstein</b>. He warmly <b>invites Holger and his wife</b> — <i>\"my niece by my brother\"</i> (Sophie Brahe, daughter of Tycho's brother <b>Axel Brahe</b> — not to be confused with Tycho's sister Sophie, named just below) — promising easy roads, the sights of the imperial court, and the nearby <b>thermal baths</b>; <b>his sister Sophie</b> is also expected, after whose arrival the firm wedding-day will be set. (Parallel to the same-period invitation to Duke Ulrich, brahe-t_16010415003.)<br>- <b>The Curtius house (cross-ref).</b> The \"new imperial Uraniborg\": <b>Jacob Curtius's house, bought from his widow by Rudolf II for 10,000 joachimsthalers</b> (though it had cost the builder double), with its walled garden — consonant with brahe-t_16010415002, brahe-t_16010412001, brahe-t_16010410001.<br>- <b>Persons named.</b> Holger Rosenkrantz and his wife <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Tycho's niece, Axel Brahe's daughter) and mother; <b>Frederik Rosenkrantz</b> (Holger's cousin, the intercession's beneficiary); <b>Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (and his widow); <b>Elisabeth Brahe</b> and <b>Franz Tengnagel</b> (the bridal couple); <b>Sophie Brahe</b> (Tycho's sister); <b>Rudolf II</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die 5. Aprilis Anno 1601, Stylo novo</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). Header agrees with the label.<br>- <b>What this is.</b> A striking piece of enclosed news and physical enclosures — engraved or drawn portraits (\"Effigies\") of two leading members of a Persian embassy, plus a copy of Emperor Rudolf II's official reply to that embassy — sent along with this letter, which the published tail confirms is Tycho's invitation to Holger Rosenkrantz for the wedding of his daughter Elisabeth Brahe to Franz Tengnagel.<br>- <b>Tycho the Younger.</b> \"My son Tycho\" is Tycho Tygesen Brahe. Christianson's <i>On Tycho's Island</i> records that he travelled to Italy in 1601 to pursue university studies [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (Cambridge, 2000)] — but says nothing there about a Persian embassy. This recovered passage supplies a genuinely new specific detail absent from that standard secondary account: that he travelled in company with a Persian legation, under an imperial safe-conduct, continuing on through France and Spain as well as Italy.<br>- <b>This exact letter, cross-referenced.</b> Dreyer's biography independently identifies this 5 April 1601 letter to Holger Rosenkrantz as the Latin companion to a Danish-language wedding-invitation Tycho sent the same correspondent the previous day, both concerning the Tengnagel–Elisabeth Brahe marriage [Tier 1: Dreyer, <i>Tycho Brahe</i> (Edinburgh, 1890)]; Thoren's <i>Lord of Uraniborg</i> prints a translation of the companion Danish letter (his \"Letter 7,\" 25 March/4 April 1601) describing Rosenkrantz there as Tycho's \"kinsman by birth, brother-in-law\" [Tier 1: Thoren, <i>The Lord of Uraniborg</i> (Cambridge, 1990)].<br>- <b>The Persian legation — not further identified in the corpus consulted.</b> No source searched named the specific embassy passing through Prague at this date; contextually this falls in the period of diplomatic contacts between Shah Abbas I of Persia and European courts (the historically well-known Husain Ali Beg mission reached several European capitals around 1600–1601), but this is inference from the general period, not a corpus-confirmed identification, and is flagged accordingly rather than asserted.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the substance (the obtained-but-unsigned imperial intercession for Frederik Rosenkrantz via Coraduz; the Curtius-house establishment; and above all <b>the invitation to Elisabeth Brahe's wedding to Tengnagel</b>, with Holger and his wife — Tycho's niece — and sister Sophie expected) is clear and corpus-corroborated. The two \"Sophie Brahe\"s (Holger's wife/Tycho's niece vs. Tycho's sister) are carefully distinguished, not conflated. No textual crux.</p><p>The Latin is short, plain, and entirely unambiguous, and the letter's identity and context are exceptionally well anchored by two independent secondary sources (Dreyer, Thoren) that describe this very letter and occasion. What is not settled is the identity of the Persian embassy itself, which the consulted corpus does not name — a historically interesting lead rather than a translation crux. Upgrade path: identify the 1600–1601 Persian embassy to the imperial court (Rudolf II) by name and check whether Tycho the Younger's participation is independently attested elsewhere.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter (address heading through the postscript on son Tycho and the Persian legates) against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 408 (address heading and opening) through 410 (top, postscript, before the following Braschius letter begins), read as page images. Checked every proper name (Coraduz, Frederik, Jacob Curtius, Elizabeth/Franz Tengnagel, Sophie Brahe, son Tycho), place (Prague/new imperial Uraniborg), sum (10,000 joachimsthalers), and date (5 April 1601, New Style). Source_text matches Dreyer's printed Latin verbatim throughout, including the Kanizsa/Transylvania geopolitical detail and the Persian-legation postscript. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found. Published grade A confirmed by direct Dreyer collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A København, Rigsarkivet, Langebeks diplomatarium. Afskrift"], "th": {"label": "Holger Rosenkrantz", "count": 24, "pos": 24, "prev": "brahe-t_16010404001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_15990307001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports persistent efforts to obtain imperial intercession through Vice-Chancellor Coraduz on behalf of Rosenkrantz's cousin, delayed by matters of greater state importance.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010405001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010410001", "d": "1601-04-10", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg — Prague, 10 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv,<i> Debita passiva principum</i>, Vol. XXVIII B (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLAUCHTIGSTEN HOCHGEBORNEN FURSTEN VND HERRN, HERRN VLDARICH, HERTZOGEN ZU MECHELLENBURGK, FURSTEN ZU WENDEN, GRAFFEN ZU SCHWERIN, DER LANDE ROSTOCH VND STARGARTTEN HERRN, MEINEM GNEDIGSTEN FURSTEN VNDT HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLAUCHTIGSTER hochgeborner furst, gnedigster herr. Ewer Furstl: Gnaden sein meine vnterthenigste, gantzwillige dienste iederzeit hochstes fleißes beuor. Vndt seitenmahl ich itzo ainen aigenen potten inn Dennemarck abgehen laße, hab E: Furstl: Gnaden ich mit dießem meinem geringen schreiben in vnterthenigkait zu ersuchen vnd deroselben vor die vielfaltige mir bezaigte gut vndt wohlthatten zu dancken nitt vmbgehen könnenn.</p><p>Waß dan meinem itzigen zustandt betrifft, werden E: Furstl: Gnaden denselben vnlangst auß mainem vorigen schreiben vmbstendiglichen vernomen habenn, darumb hie ferner zu wiederholen vnuonnötten. Dan ihre rom: key: may:, mein allergnedigster herr, nochmahlß (wie vor, ie lenger ie mer) mir mit allen keyßerlichen gnaden (Gott lob) gewogen, vndt haben erst neulich deß gewesen reichs vice-cancellarij Jacobj Curtij l. m. ansehenliche vndt statliche behausung, so ahn ainen hohen ortt eußerst an der königlichen hauptstadt Prag gelegen, vmb zehentausendt thaller zu meinem vnd deß studij astronomicj nutz mildtlichen kauffen laßen, wo alle meine jnstrumenta so ain zeitlang in jhr kay: maytt: lustgarten nahe beim schloß gestanden, itzo conuenientj modo disponirt, auch ain laboratorium pro spagyricis exercitijs angerichtet wirt.</p><p>Ferner kan E: Furstl: Gnaden ich vnterthenigst nit verhalten (wie dieselben auch noch gnedigst werden wißen zu entsinen), daß vor ainem halben jahr die summa von zehentausendt reichsthallern, so der jungen herschafft zu Mechlenburgk von mir gutwillig vorgestrecht, deroselben rentmeistern Andreß Meiern durch meinen vettern vndt volmechtigen derer örter Eske Bilden auffkundigen laßen, dan ich dießer örter landtgütter eingekaufft, zu welcher entrichtung ich darzue vervrsacht worden, worein dan E: Furstl: Gnaden damahlß (immaßen sölliches der pilligkeit gemeß) sich gantz gnedigst vndt willfharlich erzaigt, jch auch alhie die leutte darauff vertröstet habe. Anietzo aber werde ich von herrn doctore Gödellman, welchem ich die zehentausendt thaller hauptsumma sampt dem interesse meinentwegen einzuenehmen volnkomblich commission gegeben, glaubwürdig berichtet, daß obgedachter rentmeister nitt allein in der summen, sondern auch deß schon verschienen interesse erstattung weigerlich befunden werde vndt andere große außgaben furwende.</p><p>Dieweiln dan E: Furstl: Gnaden alß hochstgedachter jungen herschafft vormund darin gnedichst gewilfharet, sollichs auch ann ihme selbs pillich vndt ich andern leutten darauff zuegesagt, alß gelanget an E: Furstl: Gnaden mein vntherthenigste gantz fleißige pitt, sie wollen nit geschehen laßen, daß meine wort zue nichte gemacht vndt ich dadurch an meinem biß dato wohllhergebrachten nahmen vnd glimff abbruch erleiden, sondern mehrgedachte summa vndt interesse furderlichst, wofern sölliches vber hoffnung noch nitt geschehen, anhero vberkommen muege. Sölliches vmb E. Furstl. Gnaden (wozue dan dero gnedigsten thats ich mich vnterthenig getroste) eußerste mueglicheit gehorsambst zuverdienen, erkenne ich mich stetz schuldig vnd geflißen.</p><p>Thue hiemit E: Furstl: Gnaden sampt dero vielgeliebten gemahlen zu glucklicher langwehrender regierung vndt zue deren ferneren furstlichen gnaden mich vnterthenigst vndt getreulich entpfhelen.</p><p>Datum Prag, den 10 ap. anno 1601.<br>Ewer Furstlichen Gnaden vnterthenigster gantz williger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>Eigenhn.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene, high-born Prince and Lord, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard, my most gracious Prince and Lord.</b></p><p>Most serene, high-born Prince, most gracious Lord. To Your Princely Grace [be] my most humble, wholly willing services at all times, with the utmost diligence, beforehand. And since I am now dispatching a courier of my own into Denmark, I could not pass by approaching Your Princely Grace in humility with this my poor letter, and thanking the same for the manifold kindness and benefits shown me.</p><p>As concerns my present state, Your Princely Grace will have learned it in detail, not long ago, from my previous letter, so it is needless to repeat it further here. For His Roman Imperial Majesty, my most gracious lord, is again — as before, the longer the more — favourably disposed toward me with all imperial graces (God be praised); and has but recently graciously had bought, <b>for 10,000 thalers</b>, for my use and that of astronomical study, the imposing and stately house of the late imperial Vice-Chancellor <b>Jacob Curtius</b> — situated at a high place, on the outermost [edge] of the royal capital city of <b>Prague</b> — where all my instruments, which for a while stood in His Imperial Majesty's pleasure-garden near the castle, are now being arranged in a convenient manner, and a <b>laboratory for spagyric exercises</b> is also being set up.</p><p>Further, I cannot most humbly conceal from Your Princely Grace (as the same will also still graciously recall) that <b>half a year ago I had the sum of 10,000 Reichsthalers</b> — which was willingly advanced by me to the young lordship of Mecklenburg — given notice on to their rent-master <b>Andreas Meier</b>, through my cousin and plenipotentiary in those parts, <b>Eske Bille</b>; for <b>I have bought landed estates in these parts</b>, for the payment of which I was thereby occasioned [to need it]. Wherein Your Princely Grace then (as is in accordance with fairness) showed yourself quite graciously and compliantly, and I have also reassured the people here on that [basis]. But now I am credibly informed by Lord Doctor <b>Gödelmann</b> — to whom I have given full commission to receive the 10,000-thaler principal together with the interest on my behalf — that the aforesaid rent-master is found refusing not only the sum, but also the repayment of the already-elapsed interest, and pleads other great expenditures.</p><p>Since, then, Your Princely Grace — as guardian of the aforesaid young lordship — graciously complied therein, and since it is in itself fair, and I have promised it to other people: there comes to Your Princely Grace my most humble, wholly diligent request, that you would not let it happen that my word be brought to naught, and that I thereby suffer damage to my hitherto well-established name and good repute; but that the oft-mentioned sum and interest may, with all speed — if (against hope) it has not yet happened — be conveyed hither. This, toward Your Princely Grace (to whose most gracious deed I humbly trust), I acknowledge myself ever bound and zealous to deserve, to the utmost of my ability, obediently.</p><p>I herewith most humbly and faithfully commend Your Princely Grace, together with your much-beloved consort, to a happy, long-lasting reign, and [commend] myself to your further princely graces.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 10th of April, the year 1601.<br>Your Princely Grace's most humble, wholly willing<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), full titulature reproduced (as in brahe-t_15880815001, brahe-t_16010604001). The MS is the <b>original dispatched letter</b> in the Schwerin <i>Debita passiva principum</i> (\"passive debts of the princes\") file.</li><li><b>Position in the debt-thread.</b> This <b>10 April 1601</b> appeal to the Duke <b>precedes</b> the more pressing one of <b>4 June 1601</b> (brahe-t_16010604001) and follows the agents'-level letters (Tycho→Meier 15 Nov 1600; Tycho→Bording; the Eske Bille letter). It conveys the same core: the <b>10,000-thaler principal</b> lent to the <i>junge Herrschaft</i> (Duke Johann's sons, of whom Ulrich is <b>guardian</b>) was called in half a year ago, via <b>Eske Bille</b>, but <b>rent-master Meier now refuses</b> both principal and elapsed interest, pleading other expenditures; Tycho asks Ulrich to compel payment lest his \"word be brought to naught\" and his \"name and good repute\" suffer.</li><li><b>New, concrete motive — Tycho's Bohemian estate purchase.</b> Tycho here states plainly <i>why</i> he needs the principal back: <b>\"I have bought landed estates in these parts\" (<i>dieser Örter Landgüter eingekauft</i>)</b>, for whose payment he requires the money — a notable datum on Tycho's late attempt to root himself in Bohemia with land, alongside the imperial house.</li><li><b>The Curtius house (cross-ref).</b> Repeats the description of the <b>\"new imperial Uraniborg\"</b>: Rudolf II bought the <b>late Vice-Chancellor Jacob Curtius's house</b> \"for 10,000 thalers … at a high place on the outermost edge of Prague,\" where the instruments (lately in the imperial pleasure-garden near the castle) are being installed and a <b>spagyric laboratory</b> built — consonant with brahe-t_16010415002 (to Sturtz) and brahe-t_16010412001 (to Bording). (Note the coincidence of figures: the Curtius house cost 10,000 thalers, the same nominal sum as the Mecklenburg principal.)</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Duke Ulrich III; <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (the late Vice-Chancellor); <b>Andreas Meier</b> (Mecklenburg rent-master); <b>Eske Bille</b> (\"my cousin and plenipotentiary\"); <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (Tycho's commissioned collector); the <i>junge Herrschaft</i> (Duke Johann's sons); <b>Rudolf II</b>; Ulrich's consort (Anna of Pomerania-Wolgast — not named in the text).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Prag, den 10 ap. anno 1601</i>; <b>New Style</b> (Prague). Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> German: 'commisson' corrected to 'commission', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p.219 (translation unaffected — already correctly rendered 'full commission').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery German; the substance (Tycho's secure imperial state and the Curtius-house establishment with its spagyric laboratory; the calling-in of the 10,000-thaler principal via Eske Bille to fund his Bohemian estate-purchase; Meier's refusal as reported by Gödelmann; the appeal to Duke Ulrich as guardian) is explicit and dovetails with the wider debt-thread and the Curtius-house letters. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter visually against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.217-220 (letter itself on 218-219, under the Latin regest 'Ulrico duci Meckelburgensi...'): the purchase of the Curtius house for 10,000 thalers, the instruments moved from the imperial pleasure-garden, the separate 10,000-Reichsthaler loan to young Mecklenburg with rent-master Andreas Meier and plenipotentiary Eske Bille, Doctor Gödelmann's commission, and the closing date (Prague, 10 April 1601) all match Dreyer and are faithfully translated. One source-text corruption found (commisson vs Dreyer's commiffion); no other defects.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published German drops the 'i' from 'commission' (commiffion), a minor letter-drop against Dreyer; the translation ('full commission') is unaffected in meaning. Dreyer prints: <i>...volnkomblich commiffion gegeben (TBOO XIV p.219)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, pp.217-220 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect was confirmed exactly as flagged and corrected; no other issues were found in the passage.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 5, "pos": 1, "prev": null, "next": "brahe-t_16010415003", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010724001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports the Emperor's continued imperial favour and the recent purchase of a large house for 10,000 thalers for his use and astronomical study.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010410001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010412001", "d": "1601-04-12", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Jacob Bording", "ck": "Jacob Bording", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Jacob Bording — Prague, 12 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 167 f. (letter draft)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>[TYCHO BRAHE AD JACOBUM BORDINGUM].<br>Magnifice et clarissime vir, d:ne et amice honorande.</p><p>SCRIPSI Mag:iae T:ae non ita dudum nonnulla de statu mearum rerum et simul eam rogaui, ut ab ampliss:o senatu Lubecensi sua intercessione et authoritate impetrare non degrauaretur, quo ea, quae etiamnum ex mea supellectile isthic residua sunt, eorum iussu et provisione Hamburgum per flu[u]iolum interlabentem, mature deportentur, idque in gratiam s:ae caes:ae m:tis, d:ni nostri clementissimi, cuj hoc officium gratiose placiturum. Spero itaque M:am T:am id ipsum executioni aut iam mandasse aut breuj mandaturam, quare nihil ulterius hac de re nunc repetam, ne videar de M:ae T:ae erga me beneuolentia, diu multumque mihi satis perspecta, quidpiam haesitare.</p><p>Verum aliud quid maioris momentj nunc addam. Repetij, vtj scit M: T:a, totam pecuniae summam ab jllustrissimorum juniorum principum Megapolitanorum quaestore, quam illis mutuo dederam et quidem legitimo tempore, prout literae contractus habent, repetij. Verum is nunc praetendens aerarij vacuitatem summam promissam ac interesse ad tempus calendarum aprilis praefinitarum numerare tergiuersatur, aliter quam ab jll:mo duce Vldarico, d:no meo clementiss., constitutum illique iniunctum est. Cumque haec procrastinatio mihi sit intolerabilis, siquidem in vltimis huius regni comitiis mense februario proxime elapso celebratis quibusdam praecipuis viris decem illa millia joachimicorum addixerim, atque inde plus commodj sperem quam prius obtinui, oportet utique me promissis stare et fidem datam redimere, nec etiam mihi integrum est interesse illo diutius carere, satis graue fuit, quod alterius anni usum fructum nunc primum per dd. Gödelmannum receperim. Scripsi itaque ad jllustrissimum ducem Vldaricum hac de re submisse et diligenter, vt ipsius cel:do dignetur sua authoritate efficere, quo mihi eueniat, id quod aequitas postulat. Quocirca M:am T:am magnopere rogatam habeo, ut apud ipsius c:nem negotium hoc promouere velit. Si quid fuerit quod apud s:am caes:am, m:tem, d:num nostrum clementiss:m, in hac aula ipsius cel:tudini gratum praestare potuero, offero omni debita reuerentia et obsequio meam opellam quam fidelissime, quam etiam eodem modo inclytae ciuitati Lubecensi officiosissime defero, quod et vtrisque T:a M:a meo nomine annuntiare velit. Quin et si qua in re M:ae T:ae vsui vnquam esse potuero, utatur me tanquam amico integro et constante, eius desiderijs, quantum in ipso est, non defuturo.</p><p>Jamque valeat M:a Tua cum tota sua domo quam optime et diutissime.<br>Dabantur Pragae ex aedibus quondam Curtianis, nunc nouo Vraniburgo Rudolphaeo die 12 aprilis anno 1601 stylo nouo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>[TYCHO BRAHE TO JACOB BORDING].</b><br>Magnificent and most renowned man, my lord and friend to be honoured.</p><p>I wrote to Your Magnificence not so long ago some things about the state of my affairs, and at the same time asked you not to find it burdensome to obtain, by your intercession and authority, from the most ample Senate of Lübeck, that those things which still remain there of my household goods may, by their order and provision, be conveyed in good time to Hamburg by the little river that flows between — and this for the sake of His Sacred Imperial Majesty, our most clement Lord, to whom this service will be graciously pleasing. I hope, therefore, that Your Magnificence has either already given this very thing to execution, or will shortly; wherefore I shall now repeat nothing further on the matter, lest I seem to harbour any doubt about Your Magnificence's benevolence toward me, long and amply well known to me.</p><p>But I will now add something of greater moment. I have called in, as Your Magnificence knows, the whole sum of money from the treasurer of the most illustrious younger Princes of Mecklenburg — the sum which I had given them on loan, and that at the lawful time, as the letters of contract provide; I have called it in. But he now, pleading the emptiness of the treasury, shuffles over paying out the promised sum and the interest at the appointed term of the Kalends of April [1 April], otherwise than was determined by the most illustrious Duke Ulrich, my most clement Lord, and enjoined upon him. And since this procrastination is intolerable to me — seeing that at the last Diet of this kingdom [Bohemia], held this past February, I have assigned those ten thousand joachimsthalers to certain leading men, and from that I hope for more advantage than I obtained before — I must by all means stand by my promises and redeem the faith I have pledged; nor is it open to me to be without that interest any longer. It was grievous enough that I have only now received, through Dr. Gödelmann, the usufruct [the interest] of the other year. I have therefore written to the most illustrious Duke Ulrich about this matter, humbly and earnestly, that His Highness may deign by his authority to bring it about that there come to me what fairness requires. Wherefore I earnestly entreat Your Magnificence to be willing to advance this business with His Highness. If there be anything I can perform, pleasing to His Highness, with His Sacred Imperial Majesty — our most clement Lord — in this Court, I offer my poor service most faithfully, with all due reverence and obedience; the same I likewise most dutifully tender to the renowned city of Lübeck — which Your Magnificence will be willing to announce to both in my name. Nay, if ever I can be of use to Your Magnificence in any matter, use me as a whole and constant friend, who will not fail your wishes, so far as in him lies.</p><p>And now may Your Magnificence fare as well and as long as possible, with all your house.<br>Given at Prague, from the house formerly Curtius's — now the new Rudolphine Uraniborg — the 12th day of April, in the year 1601, New Style.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Jacob Bording</b>, a leading man of <b>Lübeck</b> (jurist/magistrate of the Hanseatic city) able to act <i>\"with the most ample Senate of Lübeck\"</i> on Tycho's behalf — addressed throughout in the high civic register <i>Magnificentia Tua</i> (\"Your Magnificence\"). The witness is an autograph <b>letter draft</b> (<i>Brevudkast</i>) in the Basel Autographensammlung.</li><li><b>First business — the goods at Lübeck.</b> Tycho asks Bording to procure from the <b>Senate of Lübeck</b> an order to ship the remainder of his <i>supellex</i> (household goods/effects) still lying there onward to <b>Hamburg</b> <i>per fluviolum interlabentem</i> — \"by the little river that runs between\" (the Lübeck–Hamburg water route, the Stecknitz/Trave–Elbe connection) — framing the favour as pleasing to <b>His Imperial Majesty</b> (Rudolf II). This tracks the slow westward removal of Tycho's possessions from the Baltic toward his new imperial seat.</li><li><b>Second business — the Mecklenburg loan (the principal matter).</b> This letter belongs to the <b>10,000-thaler Mecklenburg-debt thread</b>: Tycho had lent <b>10,000 joachimsthalers</b> to the <i>younger princes of Mecklenburg</i> (<i>illustrissimi juniores principes Megapolitani</i>; <i>Megapolitanus</i> = Mecklenburg) and had called the principal + interest due at the lawful term — <b>the Kalends of April (1 April) 1601</b> — as fixed by <b>Duke Ulrich</b>. The dukes' <i>quaestor</i> (treasurer) now stalls, pleading an empty treasury. Tycho's urgency is concrete: at the <b>Bohemian Diet of February 1601</b> he had already <i>assigned</i> those 10,000 thalers to certain magnates (on better terms than before) and must keep his word. Cf. the parallel chain — Tycho→Andreas Meier, 27 May 1601 (brahe-t_16010527001) and 18 July (brahe-t_16010718001); Tycho→Duke Ulrich, 4 June 1601 (brahe-t_16010604001); and the ducal letters of 24 July (brahe-t_16010724001) and 9 August (brahe-t_16010809001).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Duke Vldaricus</i> = <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), Tycho's <i>dominus clementissimus</i> in the matter, who had ordered payment.</li><li><i>dd. Gödelmannus</i> = <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (1559–1611), jurist and Mecklenburg councillor/diplomat (later in imperial service), through whom Tycho had at last received the previous year's interest. (<i>dd.</i> = <i>dominum doctorem</i>.)</li><li><i>quaestor</i> of the younger princes — the ducal <b>treasurer</b>, unnamed here (the rent-master <b>Andreas Meier</b> of Wismar in the companion letters).</li><li><b>Place / self-styling.</b> <i>Pragae ex aedibus quondam Curtianis, nunc novo Uraniburgo Rudolphaeo</i> — Prague, from the <b>Curtius house</b> (the <i>aedes Curtianae</i>, Tycho's Prague residence) which he renames <i>\"the new Rudolphine Uraniborg,\"</i> transplanting the name of his lost Hven seat onto his imperial quarters.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline explicitly <i>stylo novo</i> — <b>New Style (Gregorian)</b>, correct for Tycho's imperial-service dating. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Abbreviations.</b> The draft is heavily contracted in chancery style — <i>M:ae T:ae</i> (<i>Magnificentiae Tuae</i>), <i>s:ae caes:ae m:tis</i> (<i>sacrae caesareae maiestatis</i>), <i>jll:mo</i> (<i>illustrissimo</i>), <i>cel:do / cel:tudini</i> (<i>celsitudo / celsitudini</i>), <i>dd.</i> (<i>dominum doctorem</i>), <i>a:o</i> etc. — all silently expanded in translation; the source block keeps them as written.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the (abbreviated) chancery Latin is read securely; the two pieces of business (the Lübeck goods-shipment and the Mecklenburg 10,000-thaler recovery), the appointed term, and the persons (Duke Ulrich, the ducal treasurer, Dr. Gödelmann) are explicit and fully corroborated by the companion documents of the debt thread. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Collated the full letter word-for-word against Dreyer TBOO XIV p. 220 via the page image. Checked all figures and names: ten thousand joachimsthalers, the Kalends-of-April deadline, Duke Ulrich, Dr. Gödelmann, the house formerly Jacob Curtius's (\"new Rudolphine Uraniborg\"), and the 12 April 1601 New Style date. Source_text matches Dreyer exactly; translation renders every clause accurately with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation. Upgraded confidence from self-assigned to independently verified A.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, Autographensammlung, Vol. 35, Fol. 167 f.. Letter draft"], "th": {"label": "Jacob Bording", "count": 6, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16001115001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000919001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Follows up on a request to have household goods moved from Lübeck to Hamburg and raises the matter of recovering money from the treasurer of the younger Mecklenburg princes.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010412001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010414001", "d": "1601-04-14", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Martin Brasch", "ck": "Martin Brasch", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Martin Brasch (Braschius) — Prague (\"the new Imperial Uraniborg\"), 14 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "<p><b>Note.</b> Continues the Brasch exchange (cf. brahe-t_16000915001). The body is <b>Tycho's own outline for an epic poem, the <i>History of Illustrious Astronomers</b></i> — including seven lines of Tycho's hexameter verse and (at the end) <b>the earliest naming of the <i>Rudolphine Tables</b></i> in this corpus. Recipient = <b>Martin Brasch</b> (<i>Martinum Braschium</i>); see the name note at brahe-t_16000915001.</p>", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD MARTINUM BRASCHIUM.</p><p>NULLA tui oblivione nec mea negligentia accidit, eruditissime Domine BRASCHI, quod Mag. Dominus GÖDELLMANNUS, Legatum hîc agens Saxonicum, sine meo ad te responso Rostochium venerit. Iam enim illud inchoatum erat, sed is mea opinione citius abijt, ut vix eas, quae magni momenti erant, et pecunias meas istic haerentes concernebant, complere licuerit, ideoque oretenus illi quaedam dixi tibi insinuanda, quae etiam literis ad Dominum D. BACKMEISTERUM, quas tum prae manibus habui, quatenus tempus tulit, subiunxi. Mitto autem nunc ea, quae per quendam meorum de Astronomiae historia e quodam libro descripta sunt. Plura invenies sub initium Astronomici Caesarei APPIANI, tum quoque nomina quorundam Astronomorum, in priori parte tabularum primi mobilis REINHOLDI, quemadmodum etiam in Sphaera PEUCERI, quod tuae industriae relinquo, mihi enim non suppetit otium talia inquirendi. Carmen quomodo disponendum et concinnandum, tuae discretioni et venae Poëticae liberum relinquo. Principium paulo aliter ordinandum censerem, nec volo, ut mei in primis carminibus fiat ulla mentio, sed omnia conveniente methodo per mundi aetates successive deducantur ad hunc Caesarem RUDOLPHUM Secundum, postea, si tibi sic placuerit, poteris migrationem meam e Dania, quod ad hoc studium ab Augustissimo Imperatore huc clementissime vocatus sim, paucis attingere, sicque in Caesaris laudem, hoc etiam nomine, discurrere, ita tamen ut optimum illum Regem FRIDERICUM Secundum, laudatissimae memoriae, qui haec studia in suo regno, quoad vixit, liberaliter fovit, sub quo etiam Uraniburgum exaedificatum est, non praetereas. Si quid aliud inciderit, de quo te monendum duxerim, in quadam charta adiungam, neque enim nunc tantum suppetit temporis, ut carmen tuum relegere atque omnes circumstantias dispicere valeam. Ubi integre tuas conceptiones ad colophonem deduxeris, mitte mihi exemplar, antequam imprimantur, et si liberum reliqueris, meam interponere sententiam, ingenue id faciam, daboque postea operam, quantum in me est, ut Caesarea Mtas liberalem vicissim erga te clementer praebeat. Plura adderem, modo aliae interpellationes aliaque negotia id paterentur. Tu itaque, mi optime BRASCHI, rectius quam nuper vale, et pleniores per meliorem oportunitatem a me expecta. Dabantur Pragae ex aedibus olim Caesaris, nunc novo Uraniburgo Caesareo 14 Aprilis Anno 1601.</p><p>HISTORIA ILLUSTRIUM ASTRONOMORUM INDE A MUNDI EXORDIO AD NOSTRUM AeVUM HEROICO CARMINE CONCINNATA.</p><p>NON placet in primo carmine radioque potentes, nam licet apud VIRGILIUM sit Descripsit radio, tamen hoc verbum radius non habet hîc emphasin in ipso carminis frontispicio, sed potius alicubi in medio. Videtur etiam principium nimis humile, quare sic mallem:</p><p>Artifices summos Divina scientia coeli<br>Quos celebres genuit, quaeque incrementa Mathesis<br>Docta habuit, pedetentim Mundi ab origine prima<br>Aetatem in nostram, certis deducere fastis<br>Mens amet et clausos Heliconis inire recessus,<br>Arduum opus, atque alijs res intentata Poëtis,<br>Res preciosa licet et Musis digna canoris etc.</p><p>vel in eandem ferme sententiam per teipsum melius. Haec saltem admonitionis causa assignare lubuit, prout nunc in buccam venerunt.</p><p>Postea inferre, cur hoc potius Argumentum arripueris, quam alias res ludicras huius inferioris scenae mundanae, quales sunt bella, victoriae, triumphi, amores, Bucolica, et similia a prioribus Poëtis saepe decantata, sed potius sublimia illa et praestantiora, quae varijs fluctuationibus et vanitati non ita sunt obnoxia, quaeque in tanto coetera illa dignitate antecellunt, quantum arduus aether declivis terrae superat loca.</p><p>Huc addatur invocatio ad verum DEUM, totius coeli et universitatis creatorem, non Deastros licet fictitios, quos gentiles Poëtae implorant, et tandem narratio exordium habeat ab ijs, quae a MOISE primo capite Geneseos de coeli et siderum creatione referuntur. Deinde ex IOSEPHO antiquitatum Iudaicarum scriptore, quod ADAM docuerit suos filios et nepotes Astrorum scientiam, et duabus columnis excitatis in Siria ab ijsdem, et de NOAH, quem BEROSUS ait aliqua etiam ex parte Diluvium praevidisse ex Astrorum dispositione, et de ABRAHAMO, quod insignis Astronomus et Geometra fuerit, atque artes Aegyptios docuerit, quodque ex ijs, quae circa Solem, Lunam et Sidera, terrasque et maria apparent, cognoverit unum saltem esse DEUM boni autorem in Natura, quod praedictus habet IOSEPHUS, et quod ABRAM appellatus sit, dum ex sola Astrorum scientia claruit et DEUM hinc aliquatenus agnovit, postquam vero divinitus est plenius edoctus et illuminatus, ABRAHAM, sic volente DEO, est dictus, ut PHILO Iudaeus infert, quod etiam a MOISIS relatione non videtur alienum.</p><p>Deinceps poteris ordine recensere, quae e profanis etiam scriptoribus colligi poterunt, quorum etiam aliquam indicationem suppeditabit liber Domini HENRICI RANZOVIJ de Regibus et Principibus, qui Astronomiam foverunt. Omnia autem ordine se sequantur, prout tempora aetatum mundi requirunt, sed decenti methodo, ut principaliora praecedant ea, quae minus ad rem faciunt; et narretur etiam, quomodo Astronomia peregrinata sit ex Siria et Mesopotamia in Aegyptum, hinc ad Chaldaeos, Arabes, Aethiopes, Indos, ex Aegypto etiam venisse in Graeciam, hinc in Italiam et Hispaniam ac Mauritaniam, postea in Galliam et Germaniam, atque ibi praecipua lumina, quae in hac arte apud Germanos viguerunt, commemorare, usque ad COPERNICUM et REINHOLDUM, et quod tandem in Daniam transfretârit, ubi regnante optimo Rege FRIDERICO Secundo quietem et portum aliquandiu invenit, exaedificata arce Uraniburgo in Insula celebris illius Porthmo Danici Huenna, et fabricatis magnis et idoneis Instrumentis, qualia non alibi reperiuntur, ubi etiam per 21 annos strenue exercebatur, quamdiu idem Rex vixit, tum quoque in octennali interregno post ipsum. Verum cum sequentibus annis firmam sedem ibi habere nequierit, in Germaniam redijt, et in Holsatia feudoque Producis HENRICI RANZOVIJ aliquandiu quievit, et ibi laudare RANZOVIUM, tam ob hanc quam alias virtutes, et quod aliae circumiacentes regiones, ut Gallia, Anglia et Batavia, interea eam ambiverint, sed vocata ab Augustissimo Imperatore RUDOLPHO II Austriaco, in Bohemiam ad ipsum concedere praetulerit, ubi ab eodem munifice non sine aliorum invidia sustentata est, et primum de arce Bennatika, ubi per annuum spacium mansit, postea de Praga, ubi est residentia Caesaris et Regis Bohemici, hisque aedibus Curtianis in eos usus liberaliter a Caesare coemptis. Hoc etiam loco quaedam de earundem structore, Domino IACOBO CURTIO, priore Procancellario, inserenda, siquidem is Mathesi et Astronomicis studijs impense addictus fuerit, atque ea de causa hasce aedes in maxime aedito civitatis loco magnis impensis exstruxerit, quodque, dum adhuc in vivis esset, mihi etiamnum in Dania commoranti inhabitandas obtulerit, si huc Pragam venire vellem, quod, vivente tunc Rege FRIDERICO, non concedebatur, et nunc post fata eiusdem Domini CURTIJ non sine numine et Divino provisu ex liberalitate Caesaris RUDOLPHI easdem aedes mihi cessisse, pro Astronomia hîc excolenda. Postmodum elogia domus Austriacae subiungere, atque ad hunc RUDOLPHUM omnia deducere, atque in eius excurrere laudes easque ampliare, atque victorias et praeclare gesta commemorare, inter quae hoc quoque non postremum, quod Astronomiam foveat, qua in re non cedat ALPHONSO Regi, cuius fama per Tabulas et liberalitatem in eas construendas coelo coaeva durabit, cumque id numeri ipsi coelo non per omnia correspondentes tribuere potuerint, quid tibi, Caesar Austriace, ubi Tabulae Rudolpheae sub auspicijs tuis coelestes motus exactissime aemulantes lucem viderint? etc.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO MARTIN BRASCH.</b></p><p>It is by no forgetfulness of you, nor negligence of mine, most learned Lord Brasch, that the magnificent <b>Lord Gödelmann</b>, acting here as Saxon Legate, came [back] to Rostock without my answer to you. For it was already begun; but he left sooner than I thought, so that I could scarcely complete those [letters] which were of great moment and concerned my monies stuck there; and so I told him by word of mouth certain things to convey to you, which I also subjoined — so far as time allowed — to the letter to <b>Lord Doctor Backmeister</b> that I then had in hand. But I now send <b>what one of my men copied out, from a certain book, about the history of Astronomy</b>. You will find more at the beginning of <b>Apian's <i>Astronomicum Caesareum</b></i>, and also the names of certain Astronomers in the former part of <b>Reinhold's tables of the primum mobile</b>, as likewise in <b>Peucer's <i>Sphaera</b></i> — which I leave to your industry, for I have no leisure to inquire into such things. How the poem is to be arranged and composed, I leave free to your discretion and poetic vein.</p><p>The beginning I should think is to be ordered a little otherwise; and <b>I do not wish that any mention of me be made in the first verses, but that everything be deduced, by a fitting method, through the ages of the world successively, down to this Emperor Rudolf II</b>; afterward, if it so please you, you may <b>touch briefly on my migration from Denmark — that I was most clemently called hither for this study by the most august Emperor — and so discourse, on this account too, to the Emperor's praise; yet so that you do not pass over that excellent King Frederick II, of most praised memory, who liberally fostered these studies in his realm while he lived, under whom too Uraniborg was built</b>. If anything else occur of which I judge you should be advised, I will add it on a sheet; for I have not now so much time as to re-read your poem and consider all the circumstances. When you have brought your conceptions entire to the colophon, send me a copy before they are printed; and if you leave me free to interpose my opinion, I will do it frankly; and I will afterward take pains, so far as in me lies, that the Imperial Majesty in turn show himself clemently liberal toward you. I would add more, if other interruptions and other business allowed it. So fare better than of late, my excellent Brasch, and await fuller [letters] from me by a better opportunity. Given at Prague, from <b>the house once the Emperor's, now the new Imperial Uraniborg</b>, 14 April, the year 1601.</p><p><b>HISTORY OF ILLUSTRIOUS ASTRONOMERS, FROM THE BEGINNING OF THE WORLD TO OUR AGE, COMPOSED IN HEROIC VERSE.</b></p><p>I do not like, in the first verse, \"<i>radioque potentes</i>\"; for although in <b>Virgil</b> there is \"<i>Descripsit radio</i>,\" yet this word <i>radius</i> has no emphasis here, at the very frontispiece of the poem, but rather somewhere in the middle. The beginning, too, seems too humble; wherefore I should prefer thus:</p><p>&gt; <i>Let my mind love to bring forth, step by step — from the world's first origin into our age, by sure annals — the supreme craftsmen whom the divine science of heaven begot famous, and what increments learned Mathematics has had; and to enter the closed recesses of Helicon — an arduous work, a thing untried by other Poets, yet a precious thing, and worthy of the tuneful Muses,</i> etc.</p><p>— or to nearly the same sense, better, by yourself. These I was pleased to set down only by way of admonition, as they now came to the mouth.</p><p>Afterward, bring in <b>why you have seized this Argument rather than the other frivolous things of this lower worldly scene</b> — such as wars, victories, triumphs, loves, Bucolics, and the like, often sung by earlier Poets — but rather those sublime and more excellent things, not so subject to various fluctuations and to vanity, and which excel the rest in dignity as much as the high aether surpasses the low places of the sloping earth.</p><p>To this let there be added an <b>invocation to the true GOD, creator of all heaven and the universe</b> — not the fictitious godlings whom the gentile Poets implore — and at last let the narration take its beginning from what is reported by <b>Moses, in the first chapter of Genesis</b>, on the creation of heaven and the stars. Then from <b>Josephus</b>, writer of the Jewish Antiquities: that <b>Adam taught his sons and grandsons the science of the Stars</b>, and of the two columns raised in Syria by them; and of <b>Noah</b>, whom <b>Berosus</b> says foresaw the Flood partly from the disposition of the Stars; and of <b>Abraham</b>, that he was a notable Astronomer and Geometer, and taught the arts to the Egyptians, and from what appears about the Sun, Moon, Stars, lands, and seas knew that there is but one GOD, author of good in Nature (which the aforesaid Josephus has) — and that he was called <b>Abram</b> while he was famed from the science of the stars alone and thence somewhat knew God, but, after he was more fully taught and illuminated divinely, was called <b>Abraham</b> (God so willing), as <b>Philo the Jew</b> infers — which too does not seem alien from Moses's account.</p><p>Thereafter you may recount, in order, what can be gathered from the <b>profane writers</b> too — of which some indication will be supplied by the book of <b>Lord Henry Rantzau, <i>On the Kings and Princes who fostered Astronomy</b></i>. But let all follow in order, as the times of the world's ages require, yet by a decent method, so that the more principal things precede those that make less to the matter. And let it be narrated <b>how Astronomy travelled from Syria and Mesopotamia into Egypt, thence to the Chaldeans, Arabs, Ethiopians, Indians; from Egypt came also into Greece, thence into Italy and Spain and Mauritania, afterward into Gaul and Germany</b> — and there commemorate the chief lights who flourished in this art among the Germans, <b>up to Copernicus and Reinhold</b>; and that at last <b>it crossed over into Denmark, where, under the reign of the excellent King Frederick II, it found rest and a port for a while</b> — the castle <b>Uraniborg</b> built on the Island, in that famous Danish Sound, <b>Hven</b>, and great and suitable Instruments fabricated, such as are found nowhere else, where also [Tycho] was strenuously occupied <b>for 21 years, as long as that King lived, and also in the eight-year interregnum after him</b>. But since in the following years he could not have a firm seat there, <b>he returned into Germany, and rested a while in Holstein and the fief of the Pro-duke Henry Rantzau</b> — and there praise <b>Rantzau</b>, both for this and for his other virtues; and that other surrounding regions — <b>Gaul, England, and Holland</b> — meanwhile courted her [Astronomy], but, <b>called by the most august Emperor Rudolf II of Austria, she preferred to go to him into Bohemia</b>, where by him she was munificently sustained (not without others' envy): first at the castle <b>Benátky</b> (where she stayed for a year's space), then at <b>Prague</b>, the residence of the Emperor and King of Bohemia, in <b>the Curtius house, liberally bought for these uses by the Emperor</b>.</p><p>In this place, too, <b>something is to be inserted about the builder of that house, Lord Jacob Curtius [Kurz], the former Vice-Chancellor</b> — since he was deeply devoted to Mathematics and Astronomical studies, and for that cause built this house, at great cost, in the very highest place of the city; and that, <b>while he was yet alive, he offered it to me — still dwelling then in Denmark — to inhabit, if I would come hither to Prague</b>; which, King Frederick then living, was not permitted; and now, after the death of that same Lord Curtius, <b>not without [divine] will and providence, the same house was ceded to me by the liberality of the Emperor Rudolf, for cultivating Astronomy here</b>. Afterward subjoin the <b>elogia of the House of Austria</b>, and deduce all things to this <b>Rudolf</b>, and run out into his praises and amplify them, and commemorate his victories and glorious deeds — among which this too [is] not the least, <b>that he fosters Astronomy, in which matter he yields not to King Alfonso</b>, whose fame, through the Tables and his liberality in constructing them, will endure coeval with heaven; and since [those Alfonsine] numbers could not correspond to heaven in all things — <b>what [praise] for you, Austrian Caesar, when the <i>Rudolphine Tables</i>, under your auspices, most exactly emulating the celestial motions, shall have seen the light?</b> etc.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Martin Brasch / Martinus Braschius (1550–1601)</b>, the Rostock poet (cf. brahe-t_16000915001; the \"Nicolaus\" name-error in brahe-t_16000808001). Written from the <b>Curtius house, \"the new Imperial Uraniborg\"</b> (one day before brahe-t_16010415001 to Bacmeister, dated from the same place).</li><li><b>Significance — Tycho's outline for a verse <i>History of Astronomy</i>, and the naming of the <i>Rudolphine Tables</i>.</b> A remarkable document: Tycho <b>commissions and minutely plans an epic poem</b>, the <b><i>History of Illustrious Astronomers from the Beginning of the World to Our Age</b></i>, to culminate in Rudolf II — and supplies:</li><li><b>Sources for Brasch:</b> a copied extract on the history of astronomy, plus <b>Apian's <i>Astronomicum Caesareum</i>, Reinhold's tables of the <i>primum mobile</i>, Peucer's <i>Sphaera</b></i>, and <b>Heinrich Rantzau's <i>De regibus et principibus qui astronomiam foverunt</b></i>.</li><li><b>Tycho's own hexameter verse</b> — seven lines he prefers to Brasch's \"too humble\" opening (rejecting \"<i>radioque potentes</i>\" with a point about Virgil's \"<i>Descripsit radio</i>\"). A <b>rare specimen of Tycho composing Latin poetry</b>.</li><li><b>The structural plan:</b> a <i>recusatio</i> (why astronomy, not wars/loves/Bucolics); an <b>invocation to the true God</b> (not \"fictitious godlings\"); a <b>Biblical/patristic prologue</b> (Moses/Genesis; Josephus on Adam's star-science and the two Syrian columns; Noah/Berosus and the Flood; <b>Abraham the astronomer-geometer</b>, the Abram→Abraham etymology per Philo); then the <b>migration of astronomy</b> Syria→Egypt→Chaldeans/Arabs/Ethiopians/Indians→Greece→Italy/Spain/Mauritania→Gaul/Germany→<b>Copernicus &amp; Reinhold</b>→<b>Denmark</b>.</li><li><b>Tycho's autobiographical self-presentation</b> (in the third person, for the poem): <b>21 years at Uraniborg/Hven under Frederick II + the 8-year interregnum</b>; the return to <b>Holstein under Heinrich Rantzau</b>; the courting by <b>Gaul, England, Holland</b>; the <b>imperial call</b>; <b>Benátky (one year) → Prague / the Curtius house</b>.</li><li><b>The Curtius-house backstory (new detail).</b> <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz von Senftenau)</b>, the mathematically-devoted former Vice-Chancellor, <b>built the house and had offered it to Tycho years earlier</b> (declined while Frederick II lived); after Curtius's death it passed to Tycho by Rudolf's gift — \"the new Imperial Uraniborg.\"</li><li><b>The climax — the <i>Rudolphine Tables</i>.</b> Casting Rudolf as surpassing <b>Alfonso X</b> (whose Alfonsine Tables \"could not correspond to heaven in all things\"), Tycho gives Brasch the poem's intended peroration: <b>\"what [praise] for you, Austrian Caesar, when the <i>Tabulae Rudolpheae</i>, under your auspices, most exactly emulating the celestial motions, shall have seen the light?\"</b> — <i>the earliest naming of the Rudolphine Tables in this corpus</i>, by Tycho himself, months before his death (the work Kepler would complete and publish in 1627).</li><li><b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> A keystone for the <b>Rudolphine Tables</b> project, the <b>Curtius-house / \"new Uraniborg\"</b> identity (brahe-t_16010415001, brahe-t_16000103001), the <b>exile/autobiography</b> thread, and the <b>Alfonsine comparison</b> (brahe-t_16000915001).</li><li><b>Persons &amp; works named.</b> Martin Brasch; <b>Gödelmann</b>; <b>Lucas Backmeister</b>; <b>Apian</b> (<i>Astronomicum Caesareum</i>), <b>Erasmus Reinhold</b>, <b>Caspar Peucer</b> (<i>Sphaera</i>); <b>Virgil</b>; <b>Moses, Josephus, Adam, Noah, Berosus, Abraham, Philo Judaeus</b>; <b>Heinrich Rantzau</b> (<i>De regibus … qui astronomiam foverunt</i>); <b>Copernicus</b>; <b>Jacob Curtius (Kurz)</b>; <b>Alfonso X</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>, <b>Frederick II of Denmark</b>; <b>the <i>Rudolphine Tables</b></i>. Places: <b>Prague</b> (Curtius house), <b>Benátky</b>, <b>Hven/Uraniborg</b>, <b>Holstein</b>, Denmark, the whole <i>translatio</i> of astronomy (Syria→…→Germany→Denmark).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>14 Aprilis Anno 1601</i> at Prague (imperial reckoning) → <b>14 April 1601 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the apology and source-list; Tycho's instruction that the poem run through the world's ages to Rudolf II, sparing Frederick II; <b>Tycho's own seven hexameters</b> and the Virgil/<i>radius</i> point; the full structural outline — the <i>recusatio</i>, the God-invocation, the <b>Moses/Josephus/Abraham/Philo prologue</b>, the <i>translatio astronomiae</i>, the <b>autobiographical Hven-21-years / Holstein / imperial-call / Benátky→Curtius-house arc</b>, the <b>Jacob Curtius backstory</b>, and the <b>Alfonso-surpassing peroration naming the <i>Rudolphine Tables</b></i>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. Tycho's verse is given in both Latin and an English prose-verse rendering; no textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Full sentence-by-sentence collation of source_text and published_english against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.410-413(top), with the neighbouring pages checked to isolate exactly where this letter's own text begins and ends. Every name (Goedelmann, Backmeister, Apianus, Reinhold, Peucer, Rudolph II, Frederick II, Moses, Josephus, Adam, Noah, Berosus, Abraham/Abram, Philo, Rantzau, Copernicus, Benatky, Prague, Jacob Curtius, Alfonso), number (21 years on Hven; 8-year interregnum) and the closing date (14 April 1601) matches Dreyer exactly; source_text is verbatim Dreyer apart from long-s/ligature normalization and reconstructed page-break hyphenation. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation or hedge-loss found anywhere in the letter or the appended verse-history instructions. Self-assigned grade A is confirmed by independent collation.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Martin Brasch", "count": 6, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16010308001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16010308001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Sends a copied excerpt on the history of astronomy and directs Brasch to Apian's Astronomicum Caesareum, Reinhold's tables, and Peucer's Sphaera for further sources, leaving composition of a poem to his discretion.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010414001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010415001", "d": "1601-04-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck": "Lucas Bacmeister", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Lucas Bacmeister — Prague, 15 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD LUCAM BACMEISTERUM.</p><p>CLARISSIME et praestantissime Vir, Domine et Amice honorande, quod per Clarissimum Virum Dn. D. IOH. GEORG. GÖDELMANNUM, communem nostrum amicum, sexcentos illos thaleros a quaestore Iuniorum Principum loco interesse mihi debitos transmiseris, ijs qui Belgici erant in imperiales maiore ex parte commutatis, recte a te factum est, eoque nomine plurimas tibi habeo gratias. Quod autem idem quaestor reliquos 600, qui mihi nunc debentur, tunc numerare noluerit, non equidem miror, utut non iniquum fuisset, si id officioli mihi praestitisset, sed quod totam summam satis mature resignatam et consensu et iussu Illustriss. Ducis UDALRICI mihi ad hoc Pascha restituendam solvere tergiversetur, nescio quas alias praetendens expensas et calvas excusationes, id mihi magis displicet, nec ferendum duco. Scripsi itaque ad dictum Illustriss. Ducem UDALRICUM, sponsorem primarium, ut ad constitutum tempus integram summam decem millium Ioachimicorum mihi restitui sua authoritate curet, quod et non invite effecturum optimum illum et integerrimum Principem, mihi clementissime faventem, nullatenus diffido. Obtigit mihi occasio, qua in hisce oris plus commodi ex illa pecunia nancisci queam quam isthic, alias non tam cito eam repetijssem.</p><p>Quin et praeterea ad praefatum Illustriss. Ducem dedi literas, qua ipsius Celsitudinem ad nuptias filiae cuidam ex meis ELISABETHAe nomine submisse invitavi, cum quodam Nobili et erudito Iuvene FRANCISCO TENGNAGLIO e Westphalia oriundo atque in hac aula versante, ad instans Pentecostes, favente Coelesti Numine, celebrandas. Cum enim non saltem Caesaream Maiestatem, Dn. nostrum clementiss. et praecipuos eius Consiliarios et aliquot Germaniae Principes, tam Ecclesiastici quam politici status, quos scio mihi clementer favere, humilime per literas invitare animus sit (quod et nonnullorum Legati, qui hîc sunt, sponte a me petierunt, atque suis Dominis gratum futurum innuerunt) Illustrissimum Ducem Megapolitanum, Dn. meum clementiss. et quam optime de me meritum, praeterire nefas duxi, non quod ipsius Celsitudinem aut alios in Germania Principes, ad quos hac de re reverenter scribo, aliquem suo nomine ideo huc missuros petere audeam, sed saltem quo conscij sint, ut si forte aliquem ex suis Legatis tum temporis hîc in aula habuerint, illis festivitatem hanc loco sui Principis coram condecorare liceat, quod et te, charissime Domine D. BACHMAISTERE, ob veterem inter nos necessitudinem non caelandum duxi, quo vota tua ad Deum omnis boni et felicitatis largitorem nostris coniungens, fausta et salutaria huic actui preceris.</p><p>Miror te literas D. D. CRAGIO inscriptas non accepisse. Nullas nunc ad ipsum ulterius mitto, siquidem intellexerim ipsum in Legatione Polonica abesse, sed tabellario huic nonnullas tradidi, quibusdam in Dania praecipuis amicis destinatas, quarum maxima pars est invitatoria ad iam dictas nuptias; eas vero omnes ipse tabellarius facile per quendam nobilem, ESCHILLUM BILLAeUM nomine, res meas istic procurantem, expediet. Addo minutulas quasdam ad eundem Regis Musicum, cui alterae prius tibi missae (ut opinor) inscribebantur, quas hic ipse grammatophorus, si tibi commodior non suppetit ratio, illi Hafniae inquisito tradet; sunt a quodam hîc Italo, in cuius aliquantul[a fideiussione?] Musicus est, scriptae.</p><p>Magistro BRASCHIO ipsemet nunc de negocio prius insinuato rescribo; antea enim non vacabat.</p><p>Quae a CAROLO Sueciae Duce in Livonia acta sunt, iam antea hîc cognovimus; nimis tardus esse videtur in resistendo Poloniae Rex, et vix ut ENNIUS de altero isto Romano aiebat, cunctando restituet rem: celeritate potius in talibus opus est. WAIWODA, qui in Comitijs Polonicis etiam hostis eius Regni declaratus est, nuper hîc fuit, et gratiam Caesaris impetravit, acceptaque magna pecuniae vi Transsylvaniae partem Polonorum consilijs et Tartarorum invasionibus nuper amissam pro Caesare recuperare meditatur, omniaque strenue ad hanc expeditionem praeparat. Magnis etiam viribus contra Turcam exercitum parat Imperator noster, validiorem quam unquam antea, siquidem necessitas sic postulet, ob amissum superiori Octobri Canisium, fortissimum Hungariae propugnaculum, quo occupato Turca sibi viam, sive Styriam sive Austriam infestare velit, praestravit; armipotens et fortis Deus eius ferociam et tyrannidem retundat atque a cervicibus Christianorum avertat. Ubi plura aliquid momenti habentia resciero, te eorum faciam participem. Vale cum omnibus tuis quam felicissime, et senij, si quae, uti fit, incidunt gravamina, patientia vince. Dabantur Pragae ex aedibus olim Curtianis, quas Caesar pro 10000 millibus Ioachimicorum in meos usus liberaliter emit vix duplo maioris exaedificatas, quae novum Uraniburgum Rudolphaeum appellanda.</p><p>Iamque iterum et saepius vale.<br>Die 15 Aprilis stylo novo Anno 1601.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO LUCAS BACMEISTER.</b></p><p>Most renowned and excellent Man, honoured Lord and Friend: that through the most renowned Lord Doctor <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>, our common friend, you transmitted to me those <b>six hundred thalers owed me by the treasurer of the Younger Princes by way of interest</b> — the Belgian ones being for the greater part exchanged into imperial — was rightly done by you, and on that account I give you many thanks. That the same treasurer was then unwilling to pay out the other 600 now owed me, I do not indeed wonder at (though it would not have been unfair, had he done me that small service); but that he <b>shirks paying the whole sum, duly assigned long enough beforehand and, by the consent and command of the most illustrious Duke Ulrich, to be repaid me by this Easter</b> — pretending I know not what other expenses and bald excuses — this displeases me more, and I judge it not to be borne. I have therefore <b>written to the said most illustrious Duke Ulrich, my principal guarantor, that he see to it by his authority that the whole sum of ten thousand joachimsthalers be repaid me at the appointed time</b> — which I in no way doubt that excellent and most upright Prince, who most clemently favours me, will not unwillingly bring about. An occasion has befallen me whereby I may obtain more advantage from that money in these parts than there; otherwise I should not have called it in so soon.</p><p>Moreover, I have besides sent a letter to the aforesaid most illustrious Duke, by which I have humbly <b>invited His Highness to the wedding of one of my daughters, Elisabeth by name, to a certain noble and learned young man, Franz Tengnagel, sprung from Westphalia and resident at this court, to be celebrated at the coming Whitsun [Pentecost]</b>, Heaven favouring. For since it is my intent humbly to invite by letter not only <b>the Imperial Majesty</b>, our most clement Lord, and his chief Councillors, and several <b>Princes of Germany</b> — both of ecclesiastical and of political estate — whom I know to favour me kindly (which several of the envoys here have even of their own accord asked of me, and intimated would be welcome to their masters), I judged it a sin to pass over the <b>most illustrious Duke of Mecklenburg</b>, my most clement Lord, who has deserved so very well of me — not that I should dare to ask His Highness, or the other Princes of Germany to whom I write reverently on this matter, to send anyone hither in their name on that account, but only that they may be aware, so that, if perchance they happen to have one of their envoys here at court at that time, it may be allowed him to grace this festivity in person in his Prince's stead. And this, dearest Lord Doctor Bacmeister, on account of the old bond between us, I judged not to be hidden from you, that — joining your prayers with ours to God, the bestower of all good and happiness — you may pray for things auspicious and wholesome for this act.</p><p>I wonder that you have not received the letters addressed to <b>Dr. Craig</b>. I now send no further ones to him, since I understand he is away on a <b>Polish legation</b>; but I have given this courier several [letters] destined for certain chief friends in Denmark, the greatest part of which are <b>invitations to the said wedding</b>. The courier himself will easily despatch them all through a certain nobleman, <b>Eske Bille</b> by name, who manages my affairs there. I add some small notes to the <b>King's Musician</b>, to whom the others sent you earlier were (I think) addressed; these this same letter-carrier, if a more convenient means is not at hand for you, will deliver to him, sought out at Copenhagen — they were written by a certain Italian here, in whose [surety?] the Musician stands.</p><p>To <b>Master Brasch</b> I myself now write back about the business previously hinted at; for earlier I had no leisure.</p><p>What <b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b>, has done in <b>Livonia</b> we had already learned here before; the King of Poland seems too slow in resisting, and scarcely, as <b>Ennius</b> said of that other Roman, will he \"by delaying restore the state\": rather, in such matters there is need of speed. The <b>Voivode</b>, who in the Polish Diet was even declared an enemy of that Kingdom, was here lately and obtained the Emperor's favour, and — having received a great force of money — purposes <b>to recover for the Emperor that part of Transylvania lately lost by Polish counsels and Tartar invasions</b>, and is vigorously preparing everything for this expedition. With great forces too our Emperor is preparing an army against the Turk, stronger than ever before, should necessity so demand — on account of the loss, <b>last October, of Kanizsa, the strongest bulwark of Hungary</b>, by the taking of which the Turk has paved himself a road, whether he wish to infest Styria or Austria. May God, mighty and strong in arms, blunt his ferocity and tyranny, and turn it from the necks of Christians. When I learn of more things of some moment, I will make you partaker of them. Farewell most happily with all yours, and conquer by patience the burdens of old age, if any (as happens) befall. <b>Given at Prague, from the house once Curtius's, which the Emperor liberally bought for my use for 10,000 joachimsthalers — a house built at a cost scarcely twice as great — to be called the new Rudolphine Uraniborg.</b></p><p>And now again and oftener, farewell.<br>The 15th of April, New Style, the year 1601.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Lucas Bacmeister</b> (Bacmeister the Younger, 1570–1638), Rostock theologian-physician, Tycho's friend and <b>financial intermediary for the Mecklenburg loan</b> (cf. the Brasch/Cramer correspondence routed through him, brahe-t_16000401001). Spelled <i>Bachmaister</i> mid-letter.<br>- <b>Significance — the Mecklenburg debt and the Tengnagel wedding (two of the great threads of Tycho's last year).</b><br>  1. <b>The 10,000-thaler Mecklenburg loan.</b> Tycho had lent <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (\"Duke Ulrich,\" <i>sponsor primarius</i> = principal guarantor) <b>10,000 joachimsthalers</b>; the <b>treasurer of the \"Younger Princes\"</b> owes interest (two instalments of <b>600 thalers</b>, the first received via <b>Gödelmann</b>, Belgian thalers converted to imperial) and now <b>shirks repaying the principal due at Easter 1601</b>. Tycho, having \"an occasion to use the money more advantageously in these parts,\" appeals over the treasurer's head to <b>Duke Ulrich</b> to enforce the full repayment.<br>  2. <b>The wedding of Elisabeth Brahe to Franz Tengnagel.</b> Tycho's daughter <b>Elisabeth</b> is to wed <b>Frans Gansneb genaamd Tengnagel</b> (the Westphalian nobleman and Tycho's assistant) <b>at Pentecost 1601</b>. Tycho is inviting <b>Rudolf II</b>, the imperial councillors, and <b>German princes</b>, and asks Duke Ulrich (and, through Bacmeister, Danish friends) that any <b>envoys present at court</b> may grace the festivity in their princes' stead. (The wedding duly took place 17 June 1601.)<br>- <b>Other contents.</b><br>  - <b>John Craig</b> (\"Dr. Craig\") is <b>away on a Polish legation</b>, so Tycho sends him nothing further; Danish letters (mostly wedding invitations) go via the courier and <b>Eske Bille</b> (<i>Eschillus Billaeus</i>), Tycho's Danish agent.<br>  - Notes to the <b>King's Musician</b> (of Denmark), penned by \"a certain Italian here, in whose [<b>surety?</b>] the Musician stands\" — <i>in cuius aliquantul[a fideiussione?]</i>, an <b>editorial reconstruction</b> of a damaged phrase (flagged).<br>  - <b>Master Brasch</b> (the poet, cf. brahe-t_16000401001's circle) — Tycho writes back at last.<br>- <b>The news bulletin (spring 1601).</b><br>  - <b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b> (the future Charles IX) campaigning in <b>Livonia</b> — the opening of the <b>Polish–Swedish War</b>; Tycho thinks <b>Sigismund III of Poland</b> too slow, quoting <b>Ennius on Fabius Maximus Cunctator</b> (<i>cunctando restituit rem</i>, \"by delaying he restored the state\") <i>against</i> delay — here speed is needed.<br>  - <b>The \"Voivode\"</b> — declared an enemy by the <b>Polish Diet</b>, lately at Prague, won Rudolf's favour and money, and purposes to <b>recover Transylvania for the Emperor</b>: most plausibly <b>Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul), Voivode of Wallachia</b> (in Prague early 1601, reconciled with Rudolf, sent with Basta to retake Transylvania — they won at Guruslău that August; Michael was assassinated days later). Identification by circumstance; the name is not given (flagged).<br>  - <b>The fall of Kanizsa (<i>Canisium</i>, Nagykanizsa) in October 1600</b> — \"the strongest bulwark of Hungary,\" opening the Turk's road to Styria/Austria; Rudolf preparing his largest army yet for the <b>Long Turkish War</b>.<br>- <b>The dateline — \"the new Rudolphine Uraniborg.\"</b> Tycho writes from the <b>former Curtius house</b> (the Kurz/Curtius palace in Prague), <b>which Rudolf II had bought for his use for 10,000 joachimsthalers</b> — <i>vix duplo maioris exaedificatas</i>, \"[a house] built at a cost scarcely twice as great\" — <b>\"to be called the new Rudolphine Uraniborg\" (<i>novum Uraniburgum Rudolphaeum</i>).</b> A poignant naming: the Prague mansion as heir to the lost island observatory. (The source's <i>10000 <b>millibus</b> Ioachimicorum</i>, lit. \"10,000 thousands,\" is a scribal slip for <b>10,000 thalers</b> — the same round figure as the Mecklenburg loan above; rendered as 10,000.)<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Lucas Bacmeister; <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b>; <b>Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b>; <b>Elisabeth Brahe</b> &amp; <b>Franz Tengnagel</b>; <b>Rudolf II</b>; <b>John Craig</b>; <b>Eske Bille</b>; the King's Musician; <b>Master Brasch</b>; <b>Charles, Duke of Sweden</b>; <b>Sigismund III of Poland</b>; <b>Ennius / Fabius Cunctator</b>; the <b>Voivode</b> (Michael the Brave?). Places: <b>Prague</b> (the Curtius house), <b>Livonia</b>, <b>Transylvania</b>, <b>Kanizsa</b>, <b>Styria</b>, <b>Austria</b>, Denmark/Copenhagen.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Die 15 Aprilis <b>stylo novo</b> Anno 1601</i> → <b>15 April 1601 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.<br>- <b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin 'versasante' corrected to 'versante' (Dreyer TBOO VIII p.413), a non-word glue-error; no change to the English, which already read correctly.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent, fully construable Latin read with confidence; the substance (the Mecklenburg interest/principal dispute and appeal to Duke Ulrich; the Elisabeth–Tengnagel wedding invitations to Emperor, German princes, and Danish friends via Eske Bille; Craig's Polish legation; the Brasch note; the Livonia/Poland/Transylvania/Kanizsa news; the <b>Curtius house named \"the new Rudolphine Uraniborg\"</b>) is firmly construed and corpus-corroborated. Two minor flags, neither affecting the sense: the <b>editorial reconstruction</b> <i>[a fideiussione?]</i> in the King's-Musician sentence, and the unnamed <b>Voivode</b> (identified by circumstance as Michael the Brave); the <i>\"10000 millibus\"</i> slip is read as 10,000 thalers.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO VIII pp. 412-415 as page images (p.412 and the top of 413 carry the end of the preceding Braschium letter; the Bacmeister letter runs 413-415). Collated the full letter: the Godelmann/thaler-debt paragraph, the Tengnagel-Elisabeth wedding invitation naming the Emperor/Duke of Mecklenburg, the Craig/Eske Bille/Brasch paragraph, the Charles-of-Sweden/Ennius/Waiwoda/Kanizsa news paragraph, the Curtius-house purchase, and the closing date (Die 15 Aprilis stylo novo Anno 1601) all match Dreyer. One word-level TEXT_ERROR found (versasante/versante, low severity) forces grade C per the rubric even though the verdict is MINOR.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text has the non-word 'versasante' where Dreyer prints the present participle 'versante' (residing); a glued/corrupted word. The English's 'resident' happens to render the correct sense regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>atque in hac aula versante</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.412-415 (page images, not OCR text layer). The single TEXT_ERROR is confirmed on p.413 line 35: Dreyer prints 'atque in hac aula verſante', the sound participle, where our text had the corrupted 'versasante'. No other discrepancies found on collation of the full letter against the printed pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Lucas Bacmeister", "count": 4, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16010311001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16010311001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Thanks Bacmeister for transmitting 600 thalers in interest from the Mecklenburg treasurer and presses for repayment of the outstanding principal via Duke Ulrich's authority as guarantor.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010415001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010415002", "d": "1601-04-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck": "Christoph Sturtz", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Christoph Sturtz — Prague, 15 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 35</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTOPHORUM STURCIUM.</p><p>QUOD mihi per Magnificum Dn. D. GÖDELMANNUM scripseris, Nobilis et eruditissime Domine D. STURTZI, utut ego ob eius festinationem et meas tum temporis occupationes nihil ad te literarum dederim, est cur tibi plurimas habeam gratias. Agnosco hinc tuum erga me candidum et constantem amorem, cui par pari referre non desistam. De statu Patriae tuae mirabili etiam multis annis miserabili hîc satis superque inaudivimus; Deus tandem pacem largiatur. Walachus inter alios, uti scribis, declaratus hostis Regni Polonici, Caesaris nostri clementissimi Vasallus nuper factus est, et belli dux contra SIGISMUNDUM BATORUM (qui Cancellarij Polonici instinctu per Tartaros Transylvaniam invasit, et aliquam eius partem Caesari ademit) ut inde rursus eum expellat, postea pro Canisio recuperando laboraturus. Accepit hac de causa a Caesare magnam vim pecuniarum. Bellum Hungaricum tanto conamine nunc reparatur quanto vix unquam antea, sic enim opus est.</p><p>Caeterum quod narratiunculam meae e Dania huc in Bohemiam ad S. Caes. Matem migrationis, qualem optimae memoriae Dn. D. DAVIDI CHYTRAeO sic expetenti misi, tuis historicis meditationibus inserere eamque expolire et ornare constitueris, rem mihi apprime gratam et quovis officio vicissim compensandam praestiteris. Existimassem complementum historiolae anni millesimi sexcentesimi ultra priorem mediam partem, quam Dn. CHYTRAeUS ante obitum absolvit, a te continuatum, ut hoc anno prostaret, atque in illa, quae de me, dum in vivis esset, inserere cupiebat, adiuncta forent, sed forte alia sunt vestra consilia, quibus lubens acquiesco. Cuperem nihilominus id, quod proposuisti, mature fieri, et si oportunitas sic feret, conceptionem primam, antequam excutiatur, mecum communicari.</p><p>Habito nunc Pragae in aedibus olim a laudatae memoriae Vicecancellario Dn. IACOBO CURTIO splendide et magnifice exstructis, unâ cum horto amplo et amoenissimo circumiacente, quas Sacra Caes. Matas in meos usus emit a vidua inhabitante pro 10000 Ioachimicorum, cum tamen duplo maiori summa vix exstructae sint. Est locus maxime in civitate editus, versus Zephyrum, nec longe ab arce situs, ut Caesarea Matas ultra alias domos et hortos interpositos secreto et minimo negotio huc, quando ipsi sic clementissime placuerit, venire queat. Disponuntur hîc modo Instrumenta mea omnia, quae e Dania huc transtuli numero 28; thesaurus incomparabilis, quem Patria mea non aestimans sibiipsi invidit. Adornatur Typographia, ut opera mea hactenus interrupta, quanto ocius fieri poterit, compleantur. Exstruitur etiam officina pyronomica pro exercitijs spagyricis, quae nostra est etiam voluptas. Deus Opt. Max. conservet et prosperet Augustissimum hunc Caesarem RUDOLPHUM II, qui nobis haec ocia fecit. Quod superest, aeviterno et omnipotenti Numini te et omnia tua commendo.</p><p>Datae ad Pragam ex novo Uraniburgo Caesareo, cuius inscriptionem mox subiungam, die 15 Aprilis stylo novo Anno 1601.</p>", "en": "<p><b>TYCHO BRAHE TO CHRISTOPH STURTZ.</b></p><p>That you wrote to me through the magnificent Lord Doctor Gödelmann, noble and most learned Lord Doctor Sturtz — although I, on account of his haste and my occupations at that time, sent you no letter [in return] — there is reason for me to give you very many thanks. I recognise from it your candid and constant love toward me, to which I shall not cease to render like for like. Of the state of your fatherland — wondrous, and for many years also wretched — we have heard here more than enough; may God grant peace at last. The Wallachian, among others (as you write) declared an enemy of the Polish kingdom, has lately been made a vassal of our most clement Emperor, and war-leader against Sigismund Báthory — who, at the instigation of the Polish Chancellor, invaded Transylvania with the Tartars and took some part of it from the Emperor — that he may drive him out thence again, and afterward labour for the recovery of Kanizsa. For this cause he has received from the Emperor a great sum of money. The Hungarian war is now being mounted afresh with such effort as scarcely ever before — for so there is need.</p><p>Moreover, that you have resolved to insert into your historical reflections the little narrative of my migration from Denmark hither into Bohemia, to the Sacred Imperial Majesty — such as I sent to the late Lord Doctor David Chytraeus, of best memory, at his so requesting — and to polish and adorn it, you will have done me a thing exceedingly welcome, and to be repaid in turn by any service. I should have thought that the completion of the little history of the year 1600, beyond the former half which Lord Chytraeus finished before his death, had been continued by you, so as to be on sale this year, and that the things he wished to insert about me while he lived would have been added to it; but perhaps your counsels are otherwise, in which I gladly acquiesce. I should wish nonetheless that what you proposed be done in good time, and, if opportunity so bring it, that the first draft, before it is struck off, be shared with me.</p><p>I now dwell at Prague in the house once splendidly and magnificently built by the late Vice-Chancellor Lord Jacob Curtius, of praised memory, together with an ample and most pleasant garden lying round about — which the Sacred Imperial Majesty bought for my use from the widow dwelling there for 10,000 joachimsthalers, though they were scarcely built for double that sum. It is a place set very high in the city, toward the west, and situated not far from the castle, so that the Imperial Majesty can come hither — past the other houses and gardens lying between — privately and with very little trouble, whenever it shall so most clemently please him. Here are now being arranged all my instruments, which I brought hither from Denmark, 28 in number — an incomparable treasure, which my fatherland, not valuing it, begrudged to itself. A printing-house is being fitted out, so that my works, hitherto interrupted, may be completed as quickly as may be. An alchemical laboratory is also being built for spagyric exercises, which is likewise our delight. May God Best and Greatest preserve and prosper this most august Emperor Rudolf II, who has made these leisures for us. For the rest, I commend you and all yours to the everlasting and omnipotent Deity.</p><p>Given at Prague from the new imperial Uraniborg — whose inscription I shall soon append — the 15th day of April, New Style, the year 1601.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Christoph Sturtz</b> (<i>Christophorus Sturcius</i>), a learned correspondent at <b>Rostock</b> whose own letter to Tycho of 10 March 1601 survives (brahe-t_16010310001); here Tycho replies. He is evidently of <b>Transylvanian/Hungarian connection</b> — Tycho speaks of \"the state of <i>your</i> fatherland … for many years wretched\" and then gives the Transylvania/Hungary war news Sturtz had sent. He is composing a <b>historical work</b> into which he wishes to set Tycho's migration narrative (below). (Precise biography to firm up.)</li><li><b>Courier.</b> <i>Magnificus Dn. D. Gödelmann</i> = <b>Johann Georg Gödelmann</b> (1559–1611), the Mecklenburg jurist-diplomat (cf. brahe-t_16010412001), who had carried Sturtz's letter.</li><li><b>The Long-War news (spring 1601; protocol §3 context).</b></li><li><i>Walachus</i> = the <b>Voivode of Wallachia</b> — <b>Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul)</b> — lately made an <b>imperial vassal</b> and war-leader against <b>Sigismund Báthory</b> of Transylvania.</li><li><i>Sigismundus Batorus</i> = <b>Sigismund Báthory</b>, who (Tycho relays) had \"at the instigation of the <b>Polish Chancellor</b> [<b>Jan Zamoyski</b>] invaded Transylvania with the Tartars\" and seized part of it from the Emperor.</li><li><i>Canisium</i> = <b>Kanizsa (Nagykanizsa)</b>, the Hungarian fortress lost to the Ottomans in 1600, now to be recovered; the <b>Hungarian war</b> (the Ottoman–Habsburg \"Long War\") \"mounted afresh with such effort as scarcely ever before.\"</li><li><b>Tycho's migration-narrative and David Chytraeus.</b> Tycho had sent the late <b>David Chytraeus</b> (1530–1600, the Rostock historian; cf. brahe-t_16000305001) an autobiographical <i>narratiuncula</i> of his <b>migration from Denmark to Bohemia</b>, at Chytraeus's request, for Chytraeus's contemporary <i>history of the year 1600</i>. Chytraeus died with the work half-finished; Sturtz now proposes to continue it and incorporate Tycho's account. Tycho welcomes it and asks to <b>see the first draft before printing</b> — a characteristic concern for control over how his story is told.</li><li><b>The new imperial Uraniborg (the famed description of the Prague house).</b> Tycho describes his Prague residence: the <b>house of the late imperial Vice-Chancellor Jacob Curtius</b> (Jakob Kurz von Senftenau; cf. brahe-t_15900125001, brahe-t_16010412001), with its ample garden, <b>bought for Tycho by Rudolf II from Curtius's widow for 10,000 joachimsthalers</b> (\"though scarcely built for double that sum\"); set <b>high in the city, toward the west, near the castle</b>, so the Emperor can come privately. Within it Tycho is installing <b>all 28 of his instruments brought from Denmark</b> — \"an incomparable treasure, which my fatherland, not valuing it, begrudged to itself\" — plus a <b>printing-house</b> (to finish his interrupted works) and an <b>alchemical laboratory</b> (<i>officina pyronomica</i>) \"for spagyric exercises.\" He calls the whole the <i>novum Uraniburgum Caesareum</i>, \"the new imperial Uraniborg,\" and promises to append its <b>inscription</b>. (Cf. the same self-styling in brahe-t_16010412001, \"the new Rudolphine Uraniborg.\")</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Christoph Sturtz; Johann Georg Gödelmann; the Wallachian Voivode (Michael the Brave); Sigismund Báthory; Jan Zamoyski (the Polish Chancellor); David Chytraeus; <b>Jacob Curtius</b> (and his widow); <b>Rudolf II</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar / dateline (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>die Aprilis stylo novo Anno 1601</i> — <b>New Style</b>, Prague/imperial; the <b>day-number is missing/illegible</b> in the source dateline (<i>die [—] Aprilis</i>) and is supplied as <b>15</b> from the dataset label and slug (16010415002), flagged here rather than silently inserted.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> The day is <b>not</b> merely label-supplied: Dreyer (<i>Opera Omnia</i> VIII p. 416; running-head 5/15 Apr.) prints <i>die 15 Aprilis</i>. Restored to Source + translation; the bracket withdrawn.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'desinam' corrected to 'desistam', per Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p.415 (translation unaffected — 'shall not cease' is compatible with either verb).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fluent Latin, read securely; the spring-1601 Long-War news (Michael the Brave as imperial vassal, Sigismund Báthory and Zamoyski, Kanizsa, the Hungarian war), the Chytraeus/Sturtz history-of-1600 project, and above all the celebrated description of the Curtius house = \"new imperial Uraniborg\" (bought for 10,000 thalers; the 28 instruments; printing-house; alchemical laboratory) are all clear and corpus-corroborated. The only editorial element — the day-number of the date, absent from the dateline — is supplied from the catalogue and flagged.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.414-417 as page images; the letter 'TYCHO BRAHE AD CHRISTOPHORUM STURCIUM' runs from the foot of p.415 through the closing date on p.416 ('Datae ad Pragam ex novo Uraniburgo Caesareo... die 15 Aprilis stylo novo Anno 1601'). Collated the full letter word for word and checked every proper name, number, and date: Gödelmann, Sturtz, Sigismund Báthory, Kanizsa (Canisio), David Chytraeus, Jacob Curtius, the sum of 10,000 Joachimsthalers, the 28 instruments, Emperor Rudolf II, and the date 15 April 1601 N.S. all check out exactly. Found one near-synonym verb substitution in our published Latin (desinam for Dreyer's desistam) that qualifies as TEXT_ERROR but has no visible effect on the English, which renders the sense correctly either way. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found in the translation itself.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our published Latin reads 'desinam' (from desino, 'cease') where Dreyer p.415 reads 'desistam' (from desisto, 'desist/stop') -- a different verb, though a near-synonym in this context. The English ('shall not cease') is compatible with either verb, so the corruption does not visibly affect the translation. Dreyer prints: <i>cui par pari referre non defiftam.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, pp.414-417 (page images, 4 pages, read in one chunk). The single TEXT_ERROR defect was confirmed exactly as flagged and corrected; no other issues were found in the passage.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 35."], "th": {"label": "Christoph Sturtz", "count": 3, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16010310001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16010310001", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Thanks Sturtz for writing and reports political and military news: the Wallachian's new vassal status under the Emperor, Báthory's invasion of Transylvania, and preparations for the renewed Hungarian war.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010415002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010415003", "d": "1601-04-15", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg — Prague, 15 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery, with Latin) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Familien-Akten: Brahe (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLAUCHTIGSTEN HOCHGEBORNEN FURSTEN VNDT HERRN, HERRN VLDARICH, HERTZOGEN ZU MECHELLENBURGK, FURSTEN ZU WENDEN, GRAFFEN ZU SCHWERIN, DER LANDE ROSTOCH VNDT STARGARTTEN HERRN, MEINEM GNEDICHSTEN FURSTENN VNNDT HERRNN.</p><p>DURCHLÄUCHTIGSTER hochgeborner fürst, gnedigster herr. Ewer Furstl: G: sein meine vnterthenigste gantzwillige dienste iederzeit höchstes fleißes bevor.</p><p>Vndt soll deroselben vntherthenigst nitt pergenn, daß ich aine von meinen vielgeliepten töchtern mit nahmen Elizabet Brahe ainem westpfhälischen edelman Franzen Tengnagel zum Campe ehelich versprochen, auch nechst göttlicher hülff zwischen ostern vndt pfhingsten nechstkünfftich ihre hochzeit zu macchen vorhabens bin. Vndt dieweil nit allein die röm: kayß: may:, vnsern allergnedichsten herrn, ich allervnterthenigst sampt etzlichen deroselben vornehmen vndt hochweißen rhätten, sondern auch etzliche fürsten in Deutschlandt, baid eccleßastici vndt politici status, welche ich weiß, mir in gnaden bewogen zu sein, dazue zu beruffen in willens, hab auch solliches Ewer F: G: alß meinem iederzeit gnedichsten fürsten vndt herrn gehorsambst zu verstendigen vnd sie darauff zu laden nitt vmbgehen können. Nitt daß ich begehren kan oder darff, daß weder die andre fürsten noch E: F: G: ainen von denseinigen sölliche aine weitte reiße anhero schicken solte, sondern ihm fahll etwa dieselbe ainigen von ihren leutten alhie ahm hoffe hetten, daß von E: F: G: wegen der alsdan diesen nuptialem actum sua praesentia cohonestieren möchte.</p><p>Woran nur zum hohen ahngenehmen vnd gnedichsten gefallen geschehe, vnd bins meinem geringen vermög nach stets vnterthenigst vndt danckparlich zu erkennen gantz willig vnd gefließen. E: F: G: sampt dero vielgeliepten gemählin göttlicher allmacht abermahl vnterthenigst vnd getreulich empfhelendt.</p><p>Datum Prag auß der Curtianiske hauße den 15 dag aprilis anno 1601 stylo nouo.<br>Ewer F: G: vntherthenigster ganz williger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>Eigenhn.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene, high-born Prince and Lord, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard, my most gracious Prince and Lord.</b></p><p>Most serene, high-born Prince, most gracious lord. To Your Princely Grace be my most humble, entirely willing services at all times, of the highest diligence, beforehand.</p><p>And [I] shall not most humbly conceal from the same, that I have betrothed one of my much-beloved daughters, by name Elisabeth Brahe, to a Westphalian nobleman, Frans Tengnagel to Camp, and intend, next with God's help, to make their wedding between Easter and Pentecost next coming. And since I am minded to summon to it not only the Roman Imperial Majesty, our most gracious lord — most humbly — together with several of the same's distinguished and most wise councillors, but also several princes in Germany, both of the ecclesiastical and the political estate, who I know are graciously inclined toward me: I have also not been able to omit obediently to inform Your Princely Grace, as my ever most gracious Prince and Lord, of such, and to invite you thereto. Not that I can, or may, desire that either the other princes or Your Princely Grace should send one of [your] people so long a journey hither; but [rather] in case the same [you] should perhaps have any of your people here at court, [so] that [one of them] might then, on Your Princely Grace's behalf, honour this <i>nuptial act</i> with his <i>presence</i> [<i>nuptialem actum sua praesentia cohonestieren</i>].</p><p>Whereby would happen [something] only to [my] high agreeable and most gracious pleasure; and I am, according to my slight ability, ever most humbly and thankfully entirely willing and assiduous to acknowledge [it]. Commending Your Princely Grace, together with your much-beloved consort, once again most humbly and faithfully to God's Almighty [protection].</p><p>Given Prague, from the Curtius house, the 15th day of April, in the year 1601, new style.<br>Your Princely Grace's most humble, entirely willing,<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>[his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603) — the same Duke who, three months later, writes to Tycho about the 10,000-thaler debt (brahe-t_16010724001). His full Mecklenburg titulature (<i>Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard</i>) is reproduced. The witness is in the Schwerin <b>Familien-Akten: Brahe</b>.</li><li><b>A wedding invitation — companion to brahe-t_16010404001.</b> The second of Tycho's surviving <b>invitations to the wedding of his daughter Elisabeth Brahe to Frans Tengnagel</b> (\"a Westphalian nobleman, Frans Tengnagel to Camp\"), to be held at Prague <b>between Easter and Pentecost 1601</b> (cf. the invitation to Holger Rosenkrantz, brahe-t_16010404001, 4 April 1601). Tycho says he is inviting <b>the Emperor and his councillors</b>, and \"several princes of Germany, both <b>ecclesiastical and political</b>\"; he tactfully asks the Duke only to let a courtier already at Prague represent him and \"<b>honour this nuptial act with his presence</b>\" — not to send anyone the long journey.</li><li><b><i>nuptialem actum sua praesentia cohonestieren</b></i> — a Latin code-switch within the German (kept and italicised, per §2): \"to honour the nuptial act/ceremony with his presence.\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>aus der Curtianischen Hause</i> = the <b>Curtius house</b> at Prague (the former mansion of Vice-Chancellor Jakob Curtius), Tycho's residence — the same venue named in brahe-t_16010404001.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Prag … den 15 dag aprilis anno 1601 stylo novo</i> = <b>15 April 1601, New Style</b> (explicit); Prague, imperial. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German (with its Latin clause) is rendered securely; the occasion (the invitation to Elisabeth Brahe's wedding to Tengnagel, at the Prague Curtius house between Easter and Pentecost 1601), the recipient (Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, with full titulature), and the explicit New-Style date are all clear and dovetail with the parallel invitation brahe-t_16010404001 and the Mecklenburg-debt letter brahe-t_16010724001.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.220-222 as page images; Tycho's letter to Ulrich III (Dreyer's item 216) entirely on p.221. source_text matches Dreyer's printed German verbatim throughout. Collated translation clause-by-clause: the betrothal of Elisabeth Brahe to Frans Tengnagel, the invitation of the Emperor and German princes, the request that Ulrich send a representative, and the closing commendation all render faithfully. Names (Elisabeth Brahe, Frans Tengnagel zum Campe) and date (15 April 1601, new style) correct. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Familien-Akten: Brahe. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 5, "pos": 2, "prev": "brahe-t_16010410001", "next": "brahe-t_16010604001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010724001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Announces the betrothal of his daughter Elisabeth Brahe to Frans Tengnagel and invites Duke Ulrich to the planned wedding between Easter and Pentecost.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010415003/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010418001", "d": "1601-04-18", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Magdeburg", "t": "Georg Rollenhagen → Tycho Brahe — [Magdeburg], 18 April 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with German address; embedded Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDELEN GESTRENGEN EHRENVESTEN HERREN TYCHO BRAHENN AUFF KNUTSTORFF ERBSASSEN, RÖMISCHER KEYSERLICHER MAIESTATASTRONOMO,MEINEM GROSSGUNSTIGEN HERREN.</p><p>S.</p><p>GENEROSE et doctissime D. TYCHO, misit ante paucas septimanas responsum ad priores tuas literas SIMON a PETKUM. Illud, cum priorem aliquem habere non potuerim, per hunc FRANCISCUM SCHROTERUM Sundensem, Chymicarum rerum studiosum, qui nescio qua de causa iter Pragam instituerat, ad te amandare volui, spero recte redditum iri. De fornacibus quoque advehendis videbo quid efficere potero. Nondum enim quicquam praeter eas Hamburgo advectum est, propter nimiam Albis exundationem. Quia vero moris est, ut amici amicis novarum narrationum communicatione gratificentur, mitto D. TYCHONI cuiusdam LL. Doctoris DETHARDI HORSTIJ Witebergae agentis prognosticon de eventu belli Turcici, sane gratissimum, utinam etiam verissimum. Impingunt nostri Theologi et vani vates omnes in eundem quo Pharisaei et Apostoli olim lapidem, ut vaticinia de CHRISTO eiusque et Ecclesiae regno et victorijs, politica Phrasi pronunciata, politice intelligant, cum Ecclesia novi Testamenti nullam habeat promissionem politiae et politici regni ut veteris Testamenti, usque ad CHRISTUM, et CHRISTUS ipse dicat: Regnum meum non est de hoc mundo, item: In mundo afflictionem habebitis, in me autem TANTUM pacem. Stultum quoque hoc est, quod omnes vaticinium de GOG et MAGOG interpretantur de regno Turcico, cum id loquatur historice de victorias Israëlitarum ante CHRISTUM, allegorice vero, ut Apocalypsis IOHANNIS eum declarat, de persecutoribus Ecclesiae in toto orbe terrarum adventu CHRISTI extremo tollendis, cap. 20 vs. 8: Exibit SATHANAS ut seducat gentes, quae sunt in QUATUOR angulis terrae, τὸν Γὼγ καὶ τὸν Μάγωγ, qui circumierunt castra Sanctorum et urbem dilectam. Sed descendit ignis a DEO e coelo, qui devoravit eos etc. Simile delirium est, quod hic interpres idolum vastans interpretatur de MAHOMETE, cum ipse filius DEI dicat Matth. 24 intelligendum esse DANIELEM de signo praecessuro finalem Politiae Iudaicae, inprimis templi destructionem, Ideo monet: Cum videritis abominationem vastatricem stantem in loco sancto, ut dictum est a DANIELE propheta tum qui in Iudaea est, fugiat ad montes etc. Ridiculum denique plane est somnium, quod metuunt, ne DANIEL mentiatur, cum definit Monarchias quatuor, si Turca inchoaret quintam Monarchiam, cum prima fuerit Monarchia iam Assyriorum, iam Chaldaeorum, in secunda iam in Babylonia, iam in Persia dominabantur Monarchae, in tertia singuli Duces ALEXANDRI constituebant sibi peculiarem Monarchiam, in quarta alia erat Orientis, quam dudum rapuit Turca, alia Occidentis, quam hactenus mordicus retinet Papa, et vicinorum regulorum potentia ad suam triplicem coronam tuendam simplici credulitate et armis abutitur. Ante vero illa tempora quam varios habuit Monarchas, quorum post annum CHRISTI 480 nemo Romanus aut Italus fuit, quando AUGUSTULUS ultimus purpuram abiecit, eamque induit ODOACER Sax. Post illum anno 495 CHRISTI THEODORICUS Ost-Gotthus dictus Veronensis, inde 574 Longobardorum Reges usque ad CAROLUM Magnum Francum, qui coepit Imperator Romanorum dici 800 CHRISTI. Unde reliqui Germani titulum, Papa imperium habet hactenus. Et sic digiti pedum in DANIELIS Colosso varie discriminantur sine inchoatione quintae Monarchiae, et metuimus, ne Turca sit quintus Monarcha; qui iamdiu est Monarcha Orientis. Sed haec delirant, qui historiarum sunt ignari, et qui amant, ipsi sibi somnia fingunt. DEUS tueatur hospitia Ecclesiae, servet verbum suum et confundat consilia Turcae et Papae, propter gloriam nominis sui.</p><p>Bene in Domino vale.<br>18 Aprilis nostro 1601.<br>G. N. T.<br>addictiss.<br>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGEN.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble, gallant, and honourable Lord Tycho Brahe, hereditary lord of Knudstrup, Astronomer to the Roman Imperial Majesty, my greatly favourable Lord.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>Generous and most learned Lord Tycho: <b>Simon von Petkum</b> sent, a few weeks ago, a reply to your earlier letter. That [reply], since I could have no earlier [carrier], I wished to dispatch to you by this <b>Franz Schröter of Stralsund</b>, a student of chemical matters, who for some cause I know not had undertaken a journey to Prague; I hope it will be duly delivered. Concerning the <b>furnaces</b> also, to be conveyed, I will see what I can effect; for nothing yet but they has been brought from Hamburg, on account of the excessive flooding of the <b>Elbe</b>. And since it is the custom that friends gratify friends by sharing news, I send Lord Tycho a <b>prognostication on the outcome of the Turkish war</b> by a certain Doctor of Laws <b>Dethard Horst</b>, active at Wittenberg — most welcome indeed; would it were also most true.</p><p>Our Theologians, and all the vain seers, stumble on the same stone as the Pharisees and the Apostles once did: that they understand <b>politically</b> the prophecies about Christ, and his and the Church's kingdom and victories — pronounced in political phrase — whereas the Church of the New Testament has no promise of a polity and political kingdom, as the Old Testament had up to Christ; and Christ himself says, <i>\"My kingdom is not of this world,\"</i> and again, <i>\"In the world you shall have affliction, but in me ONLY peace.\"</i> This too is foolish, that all interpret the prophecy of <b>Gog and Magog</b> of the Turkish kingdom — whereas it speaks <i>historically</i> of the victories of the Israelites before Christ, but <i>allegorically</i> (as John's Apocalypse declares it) of the persecutors of the Church throughout the whole world, to be destroyed at Christ's final coming — chapter 20, verse 8: <i>\"Satan will go out to seduce the nations which are in the FOUR corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, who surrounded the camp of the Saints and the beloved city. But fire came down from God out of heaven, which devoured them,\"</i> etc. A similar madness it is, that this interpreter expounds the <b>\"desolating idol\"</b> of <b>Mohammed</b> — whereas the Son of God himself says, in Matthew 24, that <b>Daniel</b> is to be understood of the sign that should precede the final [end] of the Jewish polity, especially the destruction of the temple; therefore he warns: <i>\"When you see the abomination of desolation standing in the holy place, as was said by Daniel the prophet, then let him who is in Judaea flee to the mountains,\"</i> etc. Finally, it is a plainly ridiculous dream that they fear lest <b>Daniel</b> lie — when he defines <b>four Monarchies</b> — if the Turk should begin a fifth Monarchy: whereas the first Monarchy was now of the Assyrians, now of the Chaldeans; in the second the Monarchs ruled now in Babylonia, now in Persia; in the third Alexander's individual generals each set up his own Monarchy; in the fourth, one [Monarchy] was of the <b>East</b>, which the Turk long ago seized, another of the <b>West</b>, which the <b>Pope</b> hitherto retains tooth and nail, abusing the power of the neighbouring princelings — by their simple credulity and by arms — to defend his <b>triple crown</b>. And before those times, how many and various Monarchs it had! — of whom, after the year of Christ 480, none was a Roman or an Italian, when <b>Augustulus</b>, the last, cast off the purple, and <b>Odoacer the Saxon</b> put it on; after him, in the year of Christ 495, <b>Theodoric the Ostrogoth</b>, called of Verona; then, from 574, the Kings of the Lombards, up to <b>Charlemagne</b> the Frank, who began to be called Emperor of the Romans in [the year] 800. Whence the Germans have kept the title, [while] the Pope has held the [imperial] power hitherto. And thus the <b>toes of the feet in Daniel's Colossus</b> are variously distinguished, without the beginning of a fifth Monarchy — and [yet] we fear lest the Turk be a fifth Monarch, who has long been Monarch of the East! But these things they rave who are ignorant of the histories; and those who are fond [of such fancies] fashion dreams for themselves. May God protect the dwellings of the Church, preserve his word, and confound the counsels of the Turk and the Pope, for the glory of his name.</p><p>Fare well in the Lord.<br>18 April, by our [style], 1601.<br>Most devoted to Your Generous Nobility,<br>Georg Rollenhagen.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), rector of the Magdeburg school (<i>Altstädtisches Gymnasium</i>), Lutheran humanist and poet (author of <i>Froschmeuseler</i>), Tycho's \"especial friend\" and Magdeburg agent (cf. brahe-t_16000404001, brahe-t_16001009001). The German address styles Tycho <i>\"Astronomer to the Roman Imperial Majesty.\"</i></li><li><b>The practical opening.</b> Rollenhagen forwards a reply from <b>Simon von Petkum</b> (Tycho's Hamburg factor; cf. brahe-t_16000422001) by the hand of <b>Franz Schröter of Stralsund</b> (<i>Sundensis</i>), \"a student of chemical matters\" bound for Prague; reports that <b>Tycho's furnaces</b> (the alchemical <i>fornaces</i>) are the only thing yet brought from Hamburg, the <b>Elbe flooding</b> having stalled the rest; and encloses, as news, a <b>prognostication on the Turkish war</b> by the Wittenberg jurist (<i>LL. Doctor</i> = <i>Legum Doctor</i>) <b>Dethard Horst</b> — \"would it were as true as it is welcome.\"</li><li><b>Significance — a learned anti-apocalyptic, anti-Roman polemic.</b> The body is a vigorous <b>humanist critique of the literal-political reading of Biblical prophecy</b> then rampant in war-anxious Germany. Rollenhagen argues:</li><li>Theologians err like \"the Pharisees and Apostles\" in taking the prophecies of Christ's kingdom <b>politically</b>, though \"<b>My kingdom is not of this world</b>\" (John 18:36) and \"<b>in the world you shall have affliction, but in me only peace</b>\" (John 16:33).</li><li><b>Gog and Magog</b> (with the Greek <i>τὸν Γὼγ καὶ τὸν Μάγωγ</i> preserved) is not the <b>Turk</b> but, <i>historically</i>, Israel's pre-Christian wars, and <i>allegorically</i> (per <b>Revelation 20:8–9</b>) the Church's persecutors destroyed at the Last Day.</li><li>The <b>\"abomination of desolation\"</b> (Daniel; <b>Matthew 24:15–16</b>) is not <b>Mohammed</b> but the sign of the <b>destruction of the Jewish temple</b>.</li><li><b>Daniel's four monarchies</b> (the dream-statue of Daniel 2) do not leave room for a Turkish \"fifth\": he sketches the sequence — <b>Assyrian/Chaldean → Babylonian/Persian → the Diadochi (Alexander's generals) → the divided fourth (East, seized by the Turk; West, held by the <i>Pope</i>)</b> — and runs the Western imperial succession from <b>Romulus Augustulus (476/\"480\") and Odoacer</b>, through <b>Theodoric the Ostrogoth</b> and the <b>Lombard kings</b>, to <b>Charlemagne (800)</b> and the German emperors, \"the Pope [holding] the [imperial] power hitherto.\" The <b>\"toes of Daniel's Colossus\"</b> are merely the divided kingdoms, \"without the beginning of a fifth Monarchy.\" \"These things they rave who are ignorant of the histories.\"</li><li>A pointed <b>anti-papal</b> Lutheran edge throughout (the Pope's <i>\"triple crown,\"</i> \"tooth and nail,\" abusing princes' credulity), closing: <i>\"God … confound the counsels of the Turk and the Pope.\"</i></li><li><b>Scripture &amp; figures identified.</b> John 18:36; John 16:33; <b>Revelation 20:8–9</b> (Gog and Magog); <b>Matthew 24:15–16</b> (abomination of desolation, citing Daniel); <b>Daniel 2</b> (the four-monarchy statue with feet/toes of iron and clay). Historical figures: <b>Romulus Augustulus</b>, <b>Odoacer</b> (\"the Saxon\"), <b>Theodoric the Ostrogoth</b> (<i>\"of Verona\"</i> = Dietrich von Bern), the <b>Lombard kings</b>, <b>Charlemagne</b>.</li><li><b>Greek (protocol §4.2).</b> <i>τὸν Γὼγ καὶ τὸν Μάγωγ</i> — \"Gog and Magog\"; kept verbatim in the source block, translated in the body.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Georg Rollenhagen (sender); <b>Simon von Petkum</b>; <b>Franz Schröter</b> of Stralsund; <b>Dethard Horst</b> (Wittenberg jurist); plus the Scriptural/historical figures above.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>\"18 Aprilis nostro 1601\"</i> — <i>nostro [stylo]</i>, \"by <b>our [Old] style</b>\": Lutheran Magdeburg kept the <b>Julian</b> calendar (the <i>nostro</i> explicitly marks the O.S./N.S. divide from Tycho's imperial New Style). No place in the dateline (dataset <i>s.l.</i>); \"[Magdeburg]\" supplied as the evident origin (Rollenhagen's seat). Header marked [O.S.].</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — learned but fully construable Latin, read securely; the practical news (Petkum's reply via Schröter; the furnaces and the Elbe flood; Horst's Turkish-war prognostication) and the substantial Scriptural-historical polemic (the political-vs-allegorical reading of prophecy; Gog and Magog, the abomination of desolation, Daniel's four monarchies and the Western imperial succession; the anti-papal edge) are coherent and the Biblical citations and historical figures securely identified, with the Greek preserved and the <i>nostro stylo</i> dating handled per §3. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Slice held TBOO VIII pp.407-418 as buffer around this short letter; the Rollenhagen letter itself occupies only p.416 (from mid-page) through p.417 (its full text and signature), sandwiched among several unrelated letters (Henniochus, Rosencrantzius, Braschius x2, Bacmeisterum, Sturcium) that make up the rest of the 12-page slice and were read but are not this letter. Collated the German address line, every proper name (Simon von Petkum, Franz Schroter of Stralsund, Dethard Horst), all four scripture citations (John 18:36, John 16:33, Revelation 20:8, Matthew 24:15), and every date/number (chapter 20 verse 8, four corners, anno 480/495/574/800, 18 April 1601) against the printed text; all match source_text and published_english exactly. No defects found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 6, "prev": "brahe-t_16000926003", "next": "brahe-t_16010515001", "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000926003", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Reports forwarding Petkum's reply via a student carrier, notes furnaces from Hamburg delayed by Elbe flooding, and encloses a prognostication on the Turkish war by Dethard Horst.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010418001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010430001", "d": "1601-04-30", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck": "Ferdinando I de' Medici", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ferdinando de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany — Prague, 30 April 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Firenze, Biblioteca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento, part 3, vol. 12)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>SERENISSIMO ET POTENTISSIMO PRINCIPI AC DOMINO, DOMINO FERDINANDO MAGNO DUCI ETRURIAe ETC. DOMINO SUO CLEMENTISSIMO. FLORENTZ.</p><p>AD Serenissimam Celsitudinem Vestram ante Anni quadrantem per Filium meum natu maiorem, Legationem Persicam in Italiam comitantem, submisse dedi literas, quas Sereniss. Celsni Vae recte esse redditas ex Epistola eiusdem Filij nuperrime ad me data lubenter cognovi. Scribit is inibi nonnulla, quae animum meum non parum perculerunt: Legatum nimirum Persicum illum primarium natione Anglum, in cuius familia Filius meus tunc fuit, cum Senas pervenisset, ipsum ad se seorsim vocasse, atque haec insinuasse: Serenissimam Celsnem Vam illi videlicet Legato Anglo, cum ex literis ad ipsam a me scriptis intellexisset, Filium meum in ipsius comitatu esse, dissuasisse, ut illum Romam secum adduceret, praesertim illo tempore, id enim non absque gravi periculo vitae Filij, et ipsiusmet Legati impedimento fieri posse, siquidem meum nomen in illis oris iam antea satis innotuisset, et potissimum ea de causa periculum subesse, quod in Italia rumor increbuisset, Sacrae Caesareae Mati me authorem et suasorem fuisse, ne Monachos Capucinianos hîc Pragae diutius pateretur, sed eos dimitteret. Voluit itaque Legatus ille Anglus, ut Filius meus Senis remaneret, nec Romam unâ accederet, quod et factum est. Si itaque haec, ut ille retulit, ex Sereniss. Celsnis Vae consilio et admonitione facta sunt, non dubito, quin clementissimo erga me et Filium meum animo et bona ac sincera intentione profecta sint. Sin autem Legatus iste ex suo cerebro talia deprompsit, et sub praetextu nominis Vae Saae Celsnis (uti suspicor) protulit, facile conijcio, eum id fecisse, ut sic honeste a Filio meo liberaretur, ne illi ulterius oneri esset, siquidem, uti hîc Pragae satis cognovimus, viatico pro tam magnifica legatione et tanto itinere emetiendo non satis instructus erat: sed sit quicquid velit, gaudeo Filium meum Senis in Sereniss. Vae Celsitudinis ditione et urbe commorari, ubi eum commode degere, et satis tutum fore, omnino mihi persuadeo, cum Vae Sereniss. Celsnis Regio sit per se libera, et ijs, quae forte alibi in Italia incidere possent, periculis non obnoxia, ideoque a Sereniss. Celsne Va, qua possum submissione et reverentia peto, velit sibi Filium hunc meum commendatum habere non dedignari, et si res ipsius ita tulerint, ut tam diu in Vae Sereniss. Celsnis territorio subsistere possit, donec Linguam Italicam mediocriter addidicerit, id equidem mihi apprime acceptum foret; quo peracto, ut Sereniss. Celso Va eum Sereniss. et Potentissimo Rege Galliarum, inclyto suo Affini, per literas commendare dignetur, quo in ipsius Aula per unum vel alterum annum, donec et illam linguam calluerit, pro servitore recipiatur, demisse et officiosissime eam rogatam vellem: quicquid hac vel alia ratione Sereniss. Celso Va in Filium meum contulerit clementissimi beneficij, id tanquam in me ipsum collatum perpetua animi submissione et gratitudine, quantum vires meae patientur, agnoscam.</p><p>Caeterum quod Monachos Capucinianos attinet, certo sciat, atque indubitate Sereniss. Celso Va statuat, quod nunquam vel minima cogitatione mihi in mentem venerit apud Sacram Caesaream Maiestatem, Dominum meum Clementissimum, quidpiam de ijs loqui, aut an hîc manere debeant vel secus, ullo verbo mentionem facere, nedum ut quidpiam in his eius Maiestati suggererem aut consulerem, cum talia ad me plane non pertineant, nec ipsius Maiestas unquam de eiuscemodi rebus, quae Ecclesiasticas Personas aut res concernunt, mecum loquatur, sed solummodo de ijs, quae Philosophica et Astronomica, quibus ipsius Maiestas, sicuti multi alij Reges et Principes, laudabiliter afficitur, nec ego me alienis immisceo: vix suppetit otium et commoditas propria tractandi et exequendi. DEUM itaque sancte et conscientiam meam intemerate testari possum, magnam mihi hac in parte fieri iniuriam, tam hîc quam alibi, uti etiam in quibusdam alijs calumnijs, quae ab imperitis seu mearum rerum ignaris hîc sparsae sunt, de quibus brevi ad ipsius Maiestatem, quae me in omnibus ijs, quae apud ipsius Maiestatem a me dicta et peracta esse nonnulli comminiscuntur, insontem novit, referre animus est. Quin et Sereniss. Celsnis Vae Magnificentissimus hic Orator satis perspectum habet, eum de Capucinis rumorem meram esse in me calumniam, et scit forte, unde illa dissuasio apud Caesarem provenerit, ut me non saltem ille, sed et Illustrissimus ac Reverendissimus Dominus Nuncius, qui nomine P. M. hîc moratur, me satis de isto figmento, uti et in alio quodam huic ferme simili, excusatum habet, siquidem ab Illustri et Magnifico Domino CORADUCIO Imperij Procancellario satis superque cognoverit, rem longe aliter se habere, meque omni culpa in eiuscemodi de me promulgatis nugis vacare. Quae ob id latius ad Sereniss. Celsnem Vam qua possum submissione perscribo, ut illa quoque, si tale quid ad eius aures pervenit, meam innocentiam cognitam habeat, nec tam iniuriosas calumnias in meae famae extenuationem aut Filij mei detrimentum redundare sinat, sed id potius sua authoritate, sicubi opus fuerit, clementissime amoliatur, sique vicissim ex mea tenuitate proficisci poterint officiola, quae Sereniss. Celsni Vae ullo modo accepta esse queant, utatur me perpetuo tanquam suo servitore fideli et obsequentissimo. DEUS Opt. Max. Serenis. Celsnem Vam Reipub. tam politicae quam literariae bono per multos Annos incolumem et prosperam florere faciat.</p><p>Dabantur Pragae 30 die Aprilis Anno 1601.<br>Serenissimae Celsitudinis Vestrae<br>Reverenter et submisse<br>addictissimus<br>TYCHO BRAHE,<br>manu propria scripsi.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene and most powerful Prince and Lord, Lord Ferdinando, Grand Duke of Tuscany etc., his most clement Lord. Florence.</b></p><p>To Your most serene Highness, a quarter of a year ago, I humbly sent a letter <b>by my elder son, accompanying the Persian Legation into Italy</b> — which letter, I gladly learned from a recent letter of that same son, was duly delivered to Your most serene Highness. He writes therein some things which struck my mind not a little: namely that <b>the chief Persian Legate, an Englishman by nation, in whose household my son then was</b>, when he had reached <b>Siena</b>, called him aside and insinuated this: that <b>Your most serene Highness — having understood, from letters written to You by me, that my son was in his company — had dissuaded the English Legate from bringing him to Rome</b>, especially at that time; for that could not be done without grave danger to my son's life, and hindrance to the Legate himself, since my name had already become well enough known in those parts, and that the danger lay chiefly in this — <b>that a rumour had grown in Italy that I had been the author and instigator, to the Sacred Imperial Majesty, that he should no longer tolerate the Capuchin monks here at Prague, but dismiss them.</b> The English Legate therefore wished that my son should remain at Siena, and not go on to Rome with him — which was done. If, then, these things were done — as he reported — by Your most serene Highness's counsel and admonition, I do not doubt they proceeded from a most clement disposition toward me and my son, and from a good and sincere intention. But if that Legate produced such things out of his own brain, and put them forth <b>under the pretext of Your Highness's name (as I suspect)</b>, I easily conjecture that he did it so as to be honestly rid of my son, lest he be a further burden to him — since, as we have learned well enough here at Prague, <b>he was not well enough supplied with travel-money for so magnificent a legation and so great a journey</b>. But be it what it will: I rejoice that my son tarries at Siena, in Your most serene Highness's dominion and city, where I am wholly persuaded he may dwell conveniently and be safe enough, since Your most serene Highness's region is of itself free, and not subject to the perils that might perchance arise elsewhere in Italy. Therefore I beg of Your most serene Highness, with what submission and reverence I can, that <b>You would not disdain to hold this my son commended to You</b>; and if his affairs so allow that he may stay in Your Highness's territory long enough to learn the <b>Italian</b> tongue tolerably, that would indeed be most welcome to me. Which done, I would most humbly and dutifully ask that <b>Your most serene Highness deign to commend him by letter to the most serene and most powerful King of France, Your renowned kinsman</b>, that he might be received as a servant in his Court for a year or two, until he master that tongue too. Whatever, by this or another means, Your most serene Highness shall confer of most clement benefit upon my son, I shall acknowledge as conferred upon myself, with perpetual submission and gratitude of mind, so far as my powers allow.</p><p>For the rest, as concerns <b>the Capuchin monks</b>: let Your most serene Highness know for certain, and conclude beyond doubt, that <b>it never came into my mind, even by the least thought, to speak anything of them to the Sacred Imperial Majesty, my most clement Lord — to make mention by any word whether they ought to stay here or not — much less to suggest or counsel His Majesty anything in these matters</b>; since such things in no way pertain to me, nor does His Majesty ever speak with me of such things as concern Ecclesiastical Persons or affairs, but only of those that are <b>Philosophical and Astronomical</b>, with which His Majesty (like many other Kings and Princes) is laudably taken; nor do I meddle in others' business — scarcely have I leisure and convenience to handle and carry out my own. I can therefore call <b>God to witness, holily, and my conscience inviolately, that a great injury is done me in this part</b>, both here and elsewhere — as also in certain other calumnies which have been scattered here by the unskilled, or by those ignorant of my affairs — about which I intend shortly to report to His Majesty, who knows me innocent in all those things that some falsely invent to have been said and done by me before His Majesty. Indeed <b>Your most serene Highness's most magnificent Orator here has clearly enough perceived that this rumour about the Capuchins is a mere calumny against me, and perhaps knows whence that dissuasion at the Emperor's [court] arose</b>; so that not only he, but also <b>the most illustrious and most reverend Lord Nuncio, who stays here in the name of the Supreme Pontiff [the Pope]</b>, holds me well enough excused of that fiction — as also in another very similar one — since he has learned, fully and more, from the illustrious and magnificent <b>Lord Coraduz, Vice-Chancellor of the Empire</b>, that the matter stands far otherwise, and that I am free of all blame in such trifles published about me. All which I therefore write more fully to Your most serene Highness, with what submission I can, so that You too — if any such thing has reached Your ears — may have my innocence known, and not let such injurious calumnies redound to the lessening of my fame or to my son's detriment, but rather most clemently remove it by Your authority, wherever there be need. And if in turn any small services can proceed from my slenderness, that can in any way be acceptable to Your most serene Highness, use me ever as Your faithful and most obedient servant. May God, Best and Greatest, make Your most serene Highness flourish unharmed and prosperous for many years, for the good of the Commonwealth, both political and literary.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 30th day of April, the year 1601.<br>Your most serene Highness's,<br>reverently and submissively, most devoted,<br><b>Tycho Brahe</b>, written in my own hand.</p>", "ap": "<p>- <b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ferdinando I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany</b> (<i>Magnus Dux Etruriae</i>, 1549–1609), at Florence.<br>- <b>Significance — Tycho the Younger's Italian journey, the Capuchin calumny, and a bid for the French court.</b> Three interlocking threads:<br>  1. <b>The son and the Persian embassy.</b> Tycho's <b>elder son (Tycho Brahe the Younger)</b> had travelled into Italy <b>accompanying the Persian Legation</b> — the 1600–01 Safavid embassy jointly led by the Persian Husain Ali Beg and <b>the Englishman Sir Anthony Sherley</b> (\"the chief Persian Legate, an Englishman by nation,\" in whose household the son was). (Cf. the same embassy at Prague, brahe-t_16001110001.) At <b>Siena</b> the son was <b>left behind and not taken on to Rome</b>.<br>  2. <b>The Capuchin calumny (the political danger).</b> The stated reason: a <b>rumour in Italy that Tycho had urged Rudolf II to expel the Capuchin monks from Prague</b> — which, in Counter-Reformation Italy, made the Protestant Tycho's name (and his son's presence in Rome) perilous. Tycho <b>vehemently denies it</b> (\"it never came into my mind … I never speak with His Majesty save of Philosophical and Astronomical things\"), calling God and conscience to witness, and reports that <b>the Tuscan ambassador, the Papal Nuncio, and Vice-Chancellor Coraduz all hold him cleared</b> of the \"fiction.\" He suspects Sherley <b>invented the Grand Duke's \"advice\" as a pretext</b> to shed the son — Sherley being notoriously <b>short of travel-money</b> for the grand legation.<br>  3. <b>The French-court plan.</b> Tycho asks Ferdinando to keep the son in Tuscany until he learns <b>Italian</b>, then <b>recommend him to \"the King of France, Your renowned kinsman\"</b> — i.e. <b>Henri IV</b>, the Medici's <i>Affinis</i> through <b>Maria de' Medici's marriage (October 1600)</b> — for a year or two at the French court to learn French.<br>- <b>A window on Tycho's court position.</b> The disclaimer is a precise statement of Tycho's self-understood role at Rudolf's court: <b>strictly the philosophical/astronomical adviser, deliberately aloof from confessional politics</b> — and beset by \"calumnies … scattered by the unskilled,\" which he means to lay before the Emperor.<br>- <b>Persons &amp; places named.</b> Ferdinando I de' Medici; <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b> (the elder son); <b>Sir Anthony Sherley</b> (the English co-legate of the Persian embassy, unnamed but identified); <b>Henri IV of France</b> (\"King of France, Your kinsman\"); the <b>Papal Nuncio</b> at Prague (<i>nomine P.M.</i> = in the name of the <i>Pontifex Maximus</i>; unnamed — Filippo Spinelli held the post c. 1599–1603); the Tuscan <b>Orator/ambassador</b> at Prague (unnamed); <b>Rudolf von Coraduz</b> (Vice-Chancellor); the <b>Capuchin monks</b>. Places: <b>Prague</b>, <b>Florence</b>, <b>Siena</b>, <b>Rome</b>, Italy.<br>- <b>Cross-references (corpus).</b> Links to the <b>Persian-embassy</b> thread (brahe-t_16001110001) and to Tycho's <b>court-calumny / self-defence</b> thread; a key document for the biography of <b>Tycho Brahe the Younger</b>.<br>- <b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> <i>Pragae 30 die Aprilis Anno 1601</i> (imperial reckoning) → <b>30 April 1601 [N.S.]</b>; header and label agree.</p>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — fully construable diplomatic Latin read with confidence; the substance (the son's journey with the <b>Persian embassy under Sherley</b> and his being left at Siena; the <b>Capuchin-expulsion calumny</b> and Tycho's emphatic denial; the suspicion that Sherley invented the Grand Duke's \"advice\" as a money-saving pretext; the clearance by the Tuscan ambassador, the <b>Papal Nuncio</b>, and <b>Coraduz</b>; and the plan to place the son at <b>Henri IV's</b> court via the Medici-French marriage tie) is firmly construed, with no sharpening of Tycho's careful denials. <b>Anthony Sherley</b> and the <b>Nuncio</b> are identified on standard grounds and flagged where the text leaves them unnamed.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 15 Dreyer page images visually (TBOO VIII pp. 407-421) in four chunks. Located the actual letter to Ferdinando de' Medici on pp. 418-420 (header 'SERENISSIMO ET POTENTISSIMO PRINCIPI...FERDINANDO MAGNO DUCI ETRURIAE...FLORENTZ.' through the signature 'TYCHO BRAHE, manu propria scripsi.'); the surrounding 12 pages carry unrelated correspondence and were confirmed as non-overlapping with this letter's content. Collated the given source_text against the Dreyer page images sentence by sentence and found it verbatim (modulo ordinary long-s/u-v spelling and one immaterial word-order variant in the closing date formula, both out of scope per instructions) — no TEXT_ERROR. Collated published_english against both source_text and the Dreyer images clause by clause, with particular attention to: the nested attribution chain (son's letter -&gt; what the English Legate insinuated -&gt; what he claimed Ferdinando had said -&gt; the Capuchin-monks rumour reported to the Emperor) — preserved intact at every level, not flattened into a direct assertion; every proper name/place (Ferdinando/Florence/Etruria=Tuscany, the Persian Legation, the English Legate, Siena, Rome, Italy, the King of France, the Capuchin monks, Prague, the Sacred (Imperial/Caesarean) Majesty, Coraducius/Coraduz the Imperial Vice-Chancellor, the Nuncio glossed correctly as acting 'in the name of the Supreme Pontiff' for the abbreviation P.M.); the date 'Dabantur Pragae 30 die Aprilis Anno 1601' rendered correctly as 30 April 1601; and the closing benediction and signature. Found no fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss. This is a faithful, complete, correctly-nested translation of a long and syntactically intricate letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Firenze, Bibliotheca Nazionale Centrale, Gal. 286 (Accademia del Cimento part 3, vol. 12)."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Relays his son's report that the Grand Duke dissuaded the Persian Legation leader from bringing the son to Rome, citing danger from rumours about Tycho already circulating in Italy.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010430001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010515001", "d": "1601-05-15", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck": "Georg Rollenhagen", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": null, "t": "Georg Rollenhagen → Tycho Brahe — [s.l.; Magdeburg], 15 May 1601", "m": "<p>Language: Latin · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "<p><b>Text recovered (2026-07-12).</b> The recovered postscript/enclosure — dropped by a defective ingest of the source and absent from until now — has been restored from the text of record and translated. The Source and English columns below are now complete.</p>", "la": "<p>GENEROSA NOBILITATE ET POLITIORIBUS LITERIS CLARISSIMO VIRO D. TYCHONI BRAHAeO DOMINO IN KNUTSTORFF ET CAeSAREAe MAIESTATIS CONSILIARIO ET MATHEMATICO EXCELLENTISSIMO DOMINO ET AMICO SUO.</p><p>S.</p><p>CUM ad me ex itinere suo, quod ad te instituerat, Generose et Doctiss. D. TYCHO, divertisset optimus vir DAVID FABRICIUS, et non modo tua studia, ut merentur, crebris sermonibus commendaret, sed observationes quoque suas coram ostenderet, dolui profecto GANYMEDEM tam elegantem Frisios potius Boves pascere, quam ab Aquila Ioviali raptum, Dijs in Coelo ministrare, ut DAVID ab ovibus in aulam propter Musicam adoptabatur, ut certe hic DAVID GANYMEDEM habet ascendentem. Sed quisque suos patimur manes. Ego certe multis nominibus eum mihi optârim propiorem.</p><p>Fuit ante paucos dies hîc quasi privatus Episcopus Coloniensis, iturus ad Caesarem, ut ei Papae nomine electionem Regis Romanorum persuadeat, atque sic et reditus Caesarios et autoritatem in plures partes distrahat. Ne dicam de nuncio Belgico, in quam terram iam redijt exercitus Hispanicus. Ab altera parte redit Turca. Domine, serva nos, perimus.</p><p>Favorem Episcopi Saltzburgensis tibi ex animo gratulor, utinam plures adducat tibi suffragantes, ut tandem utilissimos tuos labores ad umbilicum deducas.</p><p>De prognostico, quod HENRICUS IULIUS de se usurpare dicitur, iam olim audivi doctorem MEGABACCHUM, ROTHIUM et alios, qui a me existimabant esse profectum. Sed neque ego hoc dixi aut scripsi unquam, neque ab ipso audivi. Hoc autem dicere solet GARCAeUM praedixisse, aliquando se praeclaram ex bello victoriam relaturum esse, itaque ad bella esse animosiorem. Quod negavi ego ex fontibus, quos epistola tua allegat. Et si possem, in viam potius pacis reducerem, quam ut currentem instigarem. Sed fertur equis auriga, nec audit currus habenas.</p><p>Spero me propediem in aulam venturum ad consultationem de vocando Theologo in locum HOFMANNI. Ibi, ut decet, privatim de tua pro eius salute solicitudine, et commoditate negociorum apud Caesarem privatim expediendorum, movebo fideliter omnia. Et rescribam.</p><p>Bene et quam diutissime vale.<br>15 Maij 1601.<br>Tuae Gen. N.<br>addictiss.<br>GEORGIUS ROLLENHAGEN.</p><p>HENR. IULIUS natus est 1564, 14 Octobris, 17 hora P. M. in arce Hessen. Hoc tempus GARCÆUS<br>corrupit per falsissimam HERMETIS trutinam et correctiones similes.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the man renowned for his generous nobility and his more polished learning, Lord Tycho Brahe, Lord of Knudstrup and most excellent councillor and mathematician of His Imperial Majesty, his lord and friend.</b></p><p>Greeting.</p><p>When the excellent man David Fabricius had turned aside to me out of his journey, which he had set toward you, generous and most learned Lord Tycho, and not only commended your studies (as they deserve) in frequent conversation, but also showed me his own observations in person, I grieved indeed that so elegant a Ganymede should pasture Frisian oxen, rather than — snatched up by Jove's Eagle — minister to the gods in heaven; just as David was adopted from the sheep into the court on account of his music, so surely this David has Ganymede in the ascendant. But each of us must endure his own appointed lot. I, for my part, could on many counts have wished him nearer to me.</p><p>A few days ago the Bishop of Cologne was here, as it were in a private capacity, on his way to the Emperor, to persuade him in the Pope's name to the election of a King of the Romans, and so to distract the imperial revenues and authority into ever more directions. Not to speak of the Belgian dispatch — into which land the Spanish army has now returned. From the other quarter the Turk returns. Lord, save us, we perish!</p><p>On the favour of the Bishop of Salzburg I congratulate you from the heart; would that he bring you more supporters, so that you may at last carry your most useful labours through to completion.</p><p>As for the prognostication which Henricus Iulius is said to claim of himself, I heard long ago that Doctor Megabacchus, Roth, and others reckoned it had proceeded from me. But I neither ever said or wrote this, nor heard it from himself. What he is wont to say, however, is that Garcaeus predicted he would one day carry off a glorious victory from war, and that he is therefore the more spirited toward wars. This I have denied, on the sources your letter alleges. And, if I could, I would rather lead him back into the path of peace than goad him on as he runs. But \"the charioteer is carried off by his horses, nor does the chariot heed the reins.\"</p><p>I hope to come shortly to court, for the consultation about calling a Theologian into the place of Hofmann. There, as is fitting, I shall faithfully set everything in motion, in private — concerning your solicitude for his welfare, and the convenience of business to be dispatched privately with the Emperor. And I shall write back.</p><p>Fare well, and for the longest time.<br>15 May 1601.<br>Most devoted to Your Generous Nobility,<br>Georg Rollenhagen.</p><p>Heinrich Julius was born in 1564, on the 14th of October, at the 17th hour P.M., in the castle of Hessen. This time Garcaeus corrupted, by means of the utterly false Balance of Hermes and similar corrections.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction.</b> This letter is <b>to</b> Tycho, <b>from</b> a correspondent — one of the relatively few inbound letters in the dataset. The sender writes from Magdeburg (the place is unstated, <i>s.l.</i>, but Rollenhagen was rector there).</li><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Georg Rollenhagen</b> (1542–1609), Lutheran theologian, schoolman and poet (author of the mock-epic <i>Froschmeuseler</i>, 1595), <b>rector of the Altstadt Gymnasium at Magdeburg</b>. His letters reached Tycho enclosed in Winshemius's packets (cf. <i>Rollenhagianae [literae]</i> in brahe-t_16000420001).</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, here titled <i>Caesareae Maiestatis consiliarius et mathematicus</i> — imperial councillor and mathematician.</li><li><b>The David Fabricius passage (the literary heart).</b></li><li><i>David Fabricius</i> = <b>David Fabricius</b> (1564–1617), Frisian pastor at Resterhafe/Osteel in East Frisia and a gifted naked-eye observer, correspondent of both Tycho and Kepler; he was <i>en route to visit Tycho</i> and stopped with Rollenhagen, showing his observations.</li><li>The conceit turns on a triple play: Fabricius as <b>Ganymede</b> (the beautiful youth snatched to heaven by <i>Jove's Eagle</i> to serve the gods) \"pasturing Frisian oxen\" — i.e. wasted as a rural pastor instead of raised to astronomy; and the biblical <b>David</b> \"adopted from the sheep into the court on account of his music\" (1 Samuel 16). <i>Hic David Ganymedem habet ascendentem</i> puns on the astrological <b>ascendant</b> (§5.3): this David \"has Ganymede [= Aquarius, the water-bearer] rising\" — both a horoscopic figure and the wish that he rise to the heavens. The wordplay is preserved, not flattened.</li><li><i>Sed quisque suos patimur manes</i> quotes <b>Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> VI.743</b> (\"but each of us suffers his own shade\") — \"each must endure his own appointed lot.\"</li><li><b>The political news.</b></li><li><i>Episcopus Coloniensis</i> = the <b>Elector-Archbishop of Cologne</b>, <b>Ernst of Bavaria</b>, travelling \"as if private\" to the Emperor to press, in the Pope's name, the <b>election of a King of the Romans</b> (the contested Habsburg succession question).</li><li><i>nuncius Belgicus … exercitus Hispanicus</i> = news from the <b>Low Countries</b>, where the Spanish army of the Eighty Years' War had returned; <i>redit Turca</i> = the renewed <b>Ottoman</b> threat of the Long Turkish War. <i>Domine, serva nos, perimus</i> echoes <b>Matthew 8:25</b> (the disciples in the storm) — \"Lord, save us, we perish.\"</li><li><i>Episcopus Saltzburgensis</i> = the <b>Prince-Archbishop of Salzburg</b>, <b>Wolf Dietrich von Raitenau</b>, whose favour toward Tycho Rollenhagen congratulates; <i>ad umbilicum deducas</i> = \"bring [your labours] to completion\" (Horace's <i>ad umbilicum</i>, the roller-end of a book-scroll).</li><li><b>The Heinrich Julius / astrology dispute.</b></li><li><i>Henricus Iulius</i> = <b>Heinrich Julius, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</b> (1564–1613), then a leading figure at the imperial court. A <b>prognostic (<i>prognosticum</i>)</b> of a future war-victory was being attributed to him; Rollenhagen denies being its source.</li><li><i>Garcaeus</i> = <b>Johannes Garcaeus the Younger</b> (1530–1574), Lutheran theologian and astrological author, named as the supposed authority for the war-prophecy.</li><li><i>Doctor Megabacchus, Rothius</i> = two further parties to the rumour, not securely identifiable (see Confidence).</li><li><i>Sed fertur equis auriga, nec audit currus habenas</i> quotes <b>Virgil, <i>Georgics</i> I.514</b> (\"the charioteer is swept along by his horses, and the car heeds not the reins\") — the runaway-chariot image for Heinrich Julius's martial ardour.</li><li><b>The Hofmann succession.</b> <i>de vocando Theologo in locum Hofmanni</i> = the consultation over <b>calling a theologian to replace Hofmann</b> — i.e. <b>Daniel Hofmann</b> (the Helmstedt theologian deposed in the \"Hofmann controversy,\" 1598–1601), in whose vacated place Rollenhagen expects to help, while privately advancing Tycho's interests with the Emperor.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>S.</i> = <i>salutem</i>; <i>Bene et quam diutissime vale</i> → \"Fare well, and for the longest time\"; subscription <i>Tuae Gen[erosae] N[obilitati] addictiss[imus]</i> → \"Most devoted to Your Generous Nobility.\"</li><li><b>What this is.</b> A geniture (nativity) record appended after the letter's close — a piece of raw astrological data, not epistolary prose: birth date, time, and place for a named prince, together with a technical complaint about how a published source mishandled that time.</li><li><b>The subject.</b> Heinrich Julius, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel and Prince-Bishop of Halberstadt, a major figure at the imperial court and a patron with scientific interests. Dates, hour, and place are preserved here exactly as given in the source, per instruction, without correction; note for the record that standard modern reference works generally give his birth as 15 (not 14) October 1564, so the day here may reflect either an authentic variant tradition, an error already present in Rollenhagen's source, or a transcription slip — flagged as a crux rather than silently harmonised.</li><li><b>The sender and his own astrological standing.</b> Georg Rollenhagen (1542–1609), rector of the Magdeburg Gymnasium from 1575 and author of the celebrated didactic beast-epic <i>Froschmeuseler</i>, was also himself a practising astrological author — his 1583 lectures on calendar-practice and astrology were later absorbed into David Origanus's <i>Ephemerides</i> [Tier 1: Oestmann, <i>Rantzau und die Astrologie</i> (2004)]. This explains why Rollenhagen is competent to adjudicate a technical dispute over Garcaeus's geniture-correction method, not merely relaying it. Elsewhere in the correspondence Rollenhagen is known to have fed Tycho inside information from the imperial court via his brother-in-law Caspar Lehmann, and to have first alerted Tycho to Ursus's plagiarism [Tier 1: Christianson, <i>On Tycho's Island</i> (2000)].</li><li><b>Johannes Garcaeus and the \"Trutina Hermetis.\"</b> Johannes Garcaeus (1530–1574), author of <i>Astrologiae methodus</i> (Basel, 1570), a widely used printed collection of nativities/genitures [Tier 1: corpus, Oestmann, <i>Rantzau und die Astrologie</i>, citing a Garcaeus volume from the Breitenburg library]. The \"Trutina Hermetis\" (\"Balance/Scale of Hermes\") is a standard astrological rectification technique, drawn from the pseudo-Ptolemaic <i>Centiloquium</i> (\"Fructus,\" aphorism 51): the Moon's zodiacal position at the moment of conception should match the ascendant at birth (or its opposite sign), and the technique is used to correct or verify an uncertain birth time [Tier 1: corpus, Oestmann, <i>Rantzau und die Astrologie</i>, quoting the rule in German translation]. Rollenhagen's charge is that Garcaeus misapplied this rule (\"falsissimam... trutinam\") and thereby corrupted Heinrich Julius's recorded birth hour.</li><li><b>Why this matters.</b> This is a rare glimpse of Tycho's circle doing hands-on astrological source-criticism — checking a published prince's geniture against the technical rectification method that was supposed to validate it, and finding the published source wanting.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the Latin is read securely and the major figures (David Fabricius, the Archbishops of Cologne and Salzburg, Heinrich Julius, Garcaeus, Hofmann) and the two Virgilian tags are identified with confidence. Graded B rather than A because two minor names in the astrology-rumour passage — <i>Doctor Megabacchus</i> and <i>Rothius</i> — cannot be positively identified (Megabacchus is evidently a grecised by-name), and the intricate Ganymede/David conceit admits more than one shade of emphasis; the rendering preserves the ambiguity rather than over-determining it.</p><p>The Latin is short and clear, and every date/hour figure is preserved exactly as instructed. What is not settled: whether \"14 Octobris\" is itself an error inherited from Garcaeus's faulty correction that Rollenhagen is flagging, or simply a discrepancy with modern reference dating for Heinrich Julius's birth (commonly given as 15 October) — the source does not say, and I have not silently corrected it. The place-name \"arce Hessen\" is also given exactly as in the source without further gloss; it plausibly denotes the Brunswick ducal residence properly called Hessen (near Wolfenbüttel) rather than the Landgraviate of Hesse, but this is inference from the ducal-family context, not corpus-confirmed. Grade B for these two open points, with the technical Trutina Hermetis/Garcaeus material otherwise well grounded.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO VIII pp.420-423 as page images (Read pages=\"1-4\" of the slice). Letter (\"GENEROSA NOBILITATE ET POLITIORIBUS LITERIS...\") runs complete on p.420-421, ending \"...correctiones similes,\" matching source_text word for word including the David Fabricius/Ganymede conceit, the Cologne/Persian-legation/Turkish-threat passage, Salzburg, the Henricus Iulius prognostication paragraph (subject of \"dicere solet\" correctly kept as Henricus Iulius, not flipped to Garcaeus or Tycho), the Virgilian tag and the equis-auriga adage (both correctly quoted, not asserted as plain fact), and the closing nativity note (1564, 14 Octobris, 17 hora P.M., arce Hessen). No defects found; remaining pages of the over-generous slice were not needed since the letter was already located and fully collated.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": {"label": "Georg Rollenhagen", "count": 7, "pos": 7, "prev": "brahe-t_16010418001", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16000926003", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 1, "ab": "Describes David Fabricius's visit and his observations, laments Fabricius's pastoral constraints, and reports the Bishop of Cologne's mission to the Emperor concerning election of a King of the Romans.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010515001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010527001", "d": "1601-05-27", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Andreas Meier", "ck": "Andreas Meier", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Andreas Meier — Prague, 27 May 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EHRNUESTEN VND ACHTPARN ANDRESS MEIERN, FÜRSTLICHEM MECKLENBURGISCHEN RENTMEISTERN, MEINEM BESONDERN GUTEN FREUNDT. WISSMAR.</p><p>MEINE freundtwillige dienste zuvorn. Ehrnuester, achtpar, lieber herr rentmaister, insonders gueter freundt. Ewer schreibent von Wißmar, den 18. maij dieses lauffenden jhars datiert, ist mir wohl behendiget, aber das vorige, wovon ihr meldet, so wegen der fürgewandten verhindernuß dero 10000 thaller hauptsummen vnd zinsen erlegung ahn mich ergangen sein soll, hab ich bis dato nitt gesehen, geschweig die darinnen angezogene vrsacchen für erheblich ahnnehmen können. Vnd seitenmahl obgedachte summa zu rechter zeit vnsers hieruber auffgerichteten contracts auffgekündiget, vnd ihr auch (wie dan ahn ihm selbs pillich) dieselbigen ahngenohmen, hab ich die anderen leutten zugesagt vnd versprocchen, dan mir ohnedas vnleidlich, die zinse nitt zu rechter vnd gepüerlicher zeit zue entpfhangen, wie dan auch dieselbige von Rostogk anhero zu bringen kain mittel noch rhat weiß. Zu deme auch hatt mein blutsverwanter Esche Bilde meinentwegen ain thail deßelbigen geldes anderen leutten versprocchen, vnd wollen noch können ewerer ex postfacto angereugter vrsacchen halben vnser wort nitt wiedernehmen. Gelanget derowegen ahn euch mein abermähliges, ernstliches pitten vnd begehren, das ihr nochmahls dazu thuen wollen, damit mir ohne ainigen weittern verzugk zum allerlengsten innerhalb nechstkünfftigen s. Joannis thagk, wie ihr offtgedachtem Esche Bilden zuegeschrieben, mehrgedachte hauptsumma sampt dem interesse richtich erlegt werde.</p><p>Hieran thuet ihr was der pilligkait vnd meiner aigentlichen resolution gemeß, jhm fal ihr nit wollet, das ich die bürgen in schaden zue bringen, vnd bej jhr: f: g: hertzogk Vldricchen, meinem gnedichsten fürsten vnd herren, micch derentwegen zue beklagen verursaccht vnd genotdrenget werde.</p><p>Söllichs hinwiederumb auff zu tragendem fall vmb euch vnd die ewerige zu verschülden, bin ich iederzeit willig vnd geflißen. Euch hiemit göttlicher almacht entpfhelendt.</p><p>Datum Prag den 27. maij a:o 1601.<br>E: dienst- vnd freundtwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>egenhn.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and worshipful Andreas Meier, princely Mecklenburg rent-master, my especial good friend. Wismar.</b></p><p>My friend-willing services beforehand. Honourable, worshipful, dear Lord rent-master, especial good friend: your letter from Wismar, dated the 18th of May of this current year, has duly been handed to me; but the earlier one, of which you make mention — which is said to have come to me concerning the alleged hindrance to paying out those 10,000 thalers of principal sum and interest — I have to this day not seen, much less been able to accept the reasons cited therein as weighty. And seeing that the above-mentioned sum was called in at the proper time of our contract drawn up over it, and that you too (as is indeed only fair in itself) accepted the same, I have pledged and promised it to other people; for it is in any case intolerable to me not to receive the interest at the proper and due time, just as I also know neither means nor counsel for bringing the same hither from Rostock. To this is added that my blood-relative Eske Bille has, on my behalf, promised a part of that same money to other people, and we neither will nor can take back our word on account of the reasons you have alleged after the fact. There comes to you, therefore, my renewed and earnest request and demand, that you will once more see to it that, without any further delay, at the very latest within the coming St. John's Day — as you wrote to the oft-mentioned Eske Bille — the said principal sum together with the interest be duly paid out to me.</p><p>In this you do what accords with fairness and with my actual resolve, if you would not have me caused and constrained to bring the sureties into loss, and on that account to lodge a complaint with His Princely Grace Duke Ulrich, my most gracious Prince and Lord.</p><p>To deserve this in return at your hands, should occasion arise, on behalf of you and yours, I am at all times willing and assiduous. Herewith commending you to divine omnipotence.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 27th of May, in the year 1601.<br>Your service- and friend-willing<br>Tycho Brahe<br>[in his] own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; context.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Prague</b>, five months before his death. This is the <b>earlier</b> of the two surviving Tycho→Meier letters on the Mecklenburg loan: 27 May 1601 (this) → 18 July 1601 (brahe-t_16010718001). Read together with Duke Ulrich's own letter to Meier of 9 August 1601 (brahe-t_16010809001), the three documents track the increasingly pressed recovery of a <b>10,000-thaler loan</b> (principal + interest) that the ducal house of Mecklenburg owed Tycho.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Andreas Meier</b>, ducal Mecklenburg <b>rent-master</b> at <b>Wismar</b> (see brahe-t_16010718001 apparatus). Tycho has received Meier's letter of 18 May but <i>not</i> an earlier one Meier claims to have sent pleading a \"hindrance\" to payment — and rejects the excuse in any case.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>mein Blutsverwandter Esche Bilde</i> = <b>Eske Bille</b>, a Danish nobleman and <b>kinsman of Tycho</b> acting as his financial agent in the matter; he has already re-promised part of the money to third parties, so the word given cannot be retracted. (In the 9 August ducal letter, Eske Bille has come from Denmark and \"lies at Rostock\" to collect.)</li><li><i>J. F. G. Herzog Ulrich</i> = <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</b>, the prince to whom Tycho threatens to complain if payment fails.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Wismar</i>; <i>Rostock</i> (whence Tycho sees no way to have the interest conveyed); <i>Prague</i>.</li><li><b>The deadline.</b> <i>nechstkünftiger S. Joannis Tag</i> = the <b>coming St. John's Day (24 June 1601)</b> — the term Meier had himself named to Eske Bille; the alternative Tycho holds over him is proceeding against the <i>Bürgen</i> (sureties).</li><li><b>Formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> Burgher register (<i>ehrnvest und achtbar</i>). Subscription <i>E: dienst- vnd freundtwilliger</i> = <i>Euer [Ehren] dienst- und freundwilliger</i>; <i>egenhn.</i> = <i>eigenhändig</i> (\"in his own hand,\" autograph attestation — the German counterpart of <i>manu propria</i>).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 27. Maij a:o 1601 stylo novo</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b>; the internal <i>S. Joannis Tag</i> is read consistently with the document's stated usage. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Removed 'stylo nouo' from the closing dateline, and the corresponding 'New Style' from the translation; Dreyer's print (TBOO XIV p.222) has no such phrase in the letter's own closing line.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — business letter in clear chancery German; the financial particulars and persons (Eske Bille, Duke Ulrich, the St. John's Day term, the Rostock remittance problem) are explicit and corroborated by the two companion documents. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.221-223 as page images (letter no. 217, headed 'DEM EHRNUESTEN VND ACHTPARN ANDRESS MEIERN...', p.222, dated Prague 27 May 1601). Collated the full German body against source_text and the English against both: opening/closing formulas, the sum (10,000 thalers), all proper names (Andreas Meier, Eske/Esche Bilde, Duke Ulrich/Vldricchen), the St John's Day deadline, and the place/date all match and are accurately translated. One TEXT_ERROR found: source_text's dateline carries 'stylo nouo' which Dreyer's print does not; graded C per the rule that any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade, though the added phrase is factually consonant with Dreyer's own marginal new-style dating and does not mislead a reader.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's printed dateline (p.222, line 30) reads only '...a:o 1601.' with no 'stylo nouo'; our source_text appends that phrase, which is not in Dreyer's print of the letter body (only implied by Dreyer's own editorial marginal dating '1601, 17./29. maii' above the letter). The English is a faithful rendering of source_text, so this is a source-text/Dreyer discrepancy, not a translation fault. Dreyer prints: <i>Datum Prag den 27. maij a:o 1601. E: dienst- vnd freundtwilliger [no 'stylo nouo' at this point]</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.221-223 (full slice, read in one pass). The closing dateline is confirmed verbatim on p.222. Single defect fixed with full confidence.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva pricipum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Andreas Meier", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16001115002", "next": "brahe-t_16010718001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Complains of an unreceived letter and presses Meier for overdue repayment of 10,000 thalers and interest, noting the sum has been pledged to others and kinsman Eske Bille has given assurances.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010527001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010529001", "d": "1601-05-29", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Johannes Müller", "ck": "Johannes Müller", "ck2": "empty", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Johannes Müller → [Tycho Brahe] [quittance] — Prague, 29 May 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>XIV · MS witness: A = Vienna, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. 10702, fol. 185 (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>JCH Iohannes Muller, churfurstlicher Brandenburgischer mathematicus, bekenne mitt dieser meiner eigenen handt, das vff der römischen keyß: m:statt, vnsers allergnedigsten herrn, allergnedigst begehren vnndt schreiben an den churfursten zu Brandenburgk, meinen gnedigsten herrn, jch vff ein jahrlang hieher nach Prag abgefoedert worden, das ich jhr maiestet zu allergnedigsten gefallen bei den edlen vnndt wohlgebornen herrn Tycho Brahen vff Knudtstrup vnndt Vraniburgk mich aufhaltten vnndt allda, was er in nahmen j: m: mier befehlen werde, gehorsamlich vorrichten soltte, welchem ich denn in allervnterthenigsten gehorsam nachkommen. Als aber nun die zeitt verfloßen, vnd jch vber dieselb (weil ich nicht lenger verlaub gehabt) mich nicht hab seumen dorffen, so habe an die hochermeltte römische k: maiestett ich vnterthenigst suppliciret, das dieselben mich mitt einer vorehrung fur meine aufgewandte vnkosten vnndt gehabte mühe allergnedigst bedencken woltten, vnndt wiewohl i: m: am ersten hierin jhe kein bedencken gemacht, sondern dem Francken befohlen, meine supplication dem Heiden zu lieffern, das er j: m: daran erinnern soltte, so woltte j: m: allergnedigst geruhen mier eine vorehrung zustellen zulaßen, so ist doch solchs baldt hernacher, vieleicht durch anderer angeben, mir wieder abgeschlagen worden. Auf das ich aber nicht ledig zu hauß ziehen möchte, vnndt es bei meiner gnedigsten herrschafft vnndt andern nicht das ansehen hette, als wan ich vmbsonst ausgewehsen wehr, so hatt der herr Tycho neben der kost vnndt herbergk, weil er mich vnndt mein weib die gantze zeitt vber vnterhaltten, mier 200 f: am gelde zugestellet, die ich dan auch zu meinen handen wohl empfangen, vnndt thue auch krafft dieses brieffs ermeltten herrn Tychonem vollstendig quittiren. Deßen zu mehrer vhrkundt habe ich diesen brieff mitt eigner handt geschrieben vnndt mitt meinem gewöhnlichen petzschafft besiegeltt.</p><p>Geschehen zu Prag den 29. maij a:o 1601.<br>Johannes Muller<br>manu pp:.</p>", "en": "<p>I, Johannes Müller, Electoral Brandenburg mathematician, acknowledge with this my own hand, that — upon the Roman Imperial Majesty's, our most gracious lord's, most gracious desire and letter to the Elector of Brandenburg, my most gracious lord — I was summoned hither to Prague for [the space of] a year, [so] that I should, to His Majesty's most gracious pleasure, stay with the noble and well-born lord Tycho Brahe of Knudstrup and Uraniborg, and there obediently perform what he should command me in His Majesty's name; which I then complied with, in most submissive obedience. But now, the time having elapsed, and [I] not being permitted to delay beyond it (since I had no longer leave), I most humbly petitioned the highly-mentioned Roman Imperial Majesty that the same would most graciously bethink me of an honorarium for my expended costs and the trouble I had had; and although His Majesty at first made no objection at all herein, but commanded \"the Franck\" to deliver my supplication to \"the Heyden,\" that he should remind His Majesty thereof, [and] that His Majesty would most graciously deign to have an honorarium delivered to me — yet such was, soon afterward (perhaps by others' instigation), again refused me. But so that I might not travel home empty[-handed], and that it should not have the appearance, with my most gracious lordship [the Elector] and with others, as though I had been [away] for nothing, the lord Tycho — besides board and lodging, since he maintained me and my wife the whole time over — delivered to me 200 florins in money, which I have then also well received into my hands; and I do also, by force of this letter, fully quit the said lord Tycho. In further witness whereof I have written this letter with my own hand, and sealed [it] with my usual seal.</p><p>Done at Prague, the 29th of May, in the year 1601.<br>Johannes Müller,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender — the \"Brandenburg mathematician Müller.\"</b> This resolves the thread of brahe-t_16000322003: <b>Johannes Müller, Electoral Brandenburg mathematician</b>, summoned (at the Emperor's request to the Elector of Brandenburg) to <b>Prague for a year</b> to serve Tycho \"in the Emperor's name.\" His <b>quittance</b> (receipt-and-release) on departure, 29 May 1601.</li><li><b>Content.</b> Müller's year up, he petitioned <b>Rudolf II</b> for an <i>honorarium</i> (<i>Verehrung</i>) for his costs and trouble; though the Emperor initially ordered it (commanding \"<b>the Franck</b>\" to pass the supplication to \"<b>the Heyden</b>\" to remind him), the grant was \"soon afterward, perhaps by others' instigation, again refused.\" So that he should not go home empty-handed and seem to have served \"for nothing,\" <b>Tycho himself paid him 200 florins</b> (besides a year's board and lodging for Müller and his wife). Müller fully quits Tycho. A candid window onto the Rudolfine court's failure to fund even its own commissions — leaving Tycho to pay out of pocket.</li><li><b><i>\"dem Francken … dem Heiden\"</b></i> — <b>flagged</b>: two court officials named only by surname, <i>Franck</i> and <i>Hayd/Heyden</i>, through whom the supplication was to pass; not securely re-identified. Rendered \"the Franck\" / \"the Heyden.\"</li><li><b><i>200 f.</b></i> = 200 florins (Gulden). <i>manu pp.</i> = <i>manu propria</i>.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Prag den 29. maij a:o 1601</i> = <b>29 May 1601</b>; Prague (imperial) = <b>New Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [B]</b> — the German is rendered securely and the substance is clear and valuable (Müller as the Brandenburg mathematician seconded to Tycho for a year; the Emperor's refused honorarium; Tycho's 200-florin payment; the full quittance) — resolving the \"Müller\" reference of brahe-t_16000322003. Grade [B] only for the two unidentified court intermediaries (<i>\"der Franck,\" \"der Heyden\"</i>), flagged rather than named.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], was [B].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.221-224 as page images; Muller's quittance (Dreyer's item 218) runs from bottom of p.222 to p.223. source_text matches Dreyer's printed German verbatim throughout. Collated translation clause-by-clause: the year-long summons to Prague, the failed petition for an honorarium via 'the Franck'/'the Heyden', the 200 florins Tycho paid instead, and the full quittance/seal formula all render faithfully. Sum (200 florins) and date (29 May 1601) correct. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Wien, Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, cod. 10702, fol. 185. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Müller acknowledges completing one year of imperial-commanded service with Tycho at Prague and records his petition to the Emperor for an honorarium covering expenses and trouble.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010529001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010604001", "d": "1601-06-04", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg — Prague, 4 June 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv,<i> Debita passiva principum</i>, Vol. XXVIII B (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLAUCHTIGSTEN HOCHGEBORNEN FURSTEN VND HERRN, HERRN VLDARICH, HERTZOGEN ZU MECHELLENBURGK, FURSTEN ZU WENDEN, GRAFFEN ZU SCHWERIN, DER LANDE ROSTOCH VNDT STARGARDEN HERRN, MEINEM GNEDIGSTEN FURSTEN VNDDT HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLÄUCHTIGSTER hochgeborner fürst, gnedichster herr. E: Fürstl: Gnaden sein meine vnterthenigste gantzwillige dienste iederzeit höchstes fleißes bevor. Vndt mag deroselben abermahl auß hochdrengenden vndt erheblichen vrsachen vnterthenigst nit pergen, wie dan auch E: F: Gnaden zweiffelsohn sich nochmahls gnedigst werden wißen zu entsinnen, daß die summa von 10000 thaller hauptstuel sampt dem interesse, so der jungen herschafft zue Mechelburgk von mir gehorsambst vndt guttwillig vorstreckt, ich wegen meiner sonderbaren notturfft vndt ahnligens zu rechter zeit vnsers hierumb auffgerichteten contracts auffgekundiget. Worein dan Ewer F: G: (in maßen es der pilligkait gemeß) so paldt solchs deroselben gehorsambst vorgetragen, gahr kein bedencken gehabt, sondern dero rentmeistern Andreas Meiern ahn stundt die gnedigste ahnordenung gethan, obgemelte summam vndt interesse gegen vergangenen ostern ohne ainigen verzuegk inhabe[r]n meines brieffes richtig wiederumb zueerstatten. Worauff auch (seitemahl sollichs geldt von Rostoch gehn Prag zue bekommen mir bißanhero fast beschwerlich gefallen) ich eß zue meinem vndt den meinigen besten vndt frommen andern leutten alhie, zum theil auch mein blutsverwandter Esche Bilde anderßwo meinentwegen zuegesagt vndt versprochen.</p><p>Nun aber wirdt mir von gedachtem E: F: G: rentmeistern wieder meinen verhoffen zuegeschrieben, daß anderer außgeben halben sollichs geldt noch nicht erleget vndt bezahlt werden könne, welchs zwar mir, weiln E: F: G: gnedigster zuesag zufolge ich gegen vergangenen ostern mich mit andern deßwegen eingelaßen, schmertzlich vndt frembd vorkhumpt.</p><p>Gelanget derowegen ahn E: F: G: mein vnterthenigste gantz fleißige pitt, E: F: G: wollen nit gescheen laßen, daß mihr ahn meinem biß dato wohlhergebrachten nahmen etwaß abgehen, vndt ich wieder mein wort handeln muße, sondern der gnedigsten befehlich thuen, daß mir zum allerlengster innerhalb nechstkunfftigen s: Johannis tagk mehrgedachtes geldt ohne fernere verzugerung gefollget werden muege.</p><p>Hirahn erzaigen E: F: G: mir ein große gnadt, vndt ich binß meiner geringfügigen personen nach iederzeit in vnterthenigster danckparkeit zue erkennen gantzwillig vndt geflißen. Dieselben hiemit sampt dero vielgeliepter gemahlin zu langwahrender gluchklicher regierung göttlicher almacht gehorsambst vndt getreulich entpfhelendt.</p><p>Datum Prag den 4 junij a:o 1601.<br>Ewer Furst: Gnaden vnterthenigster gantzwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>manu pprj:</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most serene, high-born Prince and Lord, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard, my most gracious Prince and Lord.</b></p><p>Most serene, high-born Prince, most gracious Lord. To Your Princely Grace [be] my most humble, wholly willing services at all times, with the utmost diligence, beforehand. And I cannot, out of highly pressing and weighty causes, once again most humbly conceal from the same — as also Your Princely Grace will doubtless once more graciously be able to recall — that the sum of 10,000 thalers of principal, together with the interest, which was obediently and willingly advanced by me to the young lordship of Mecklenburg, I called in, on account of my special need and concern, at the proper time of our contract drawn up over this matter. Wherein Your Princely Grace (as accords with fairness), as soon as it was obediently laid before the same, had no hesitation at all, but at once gave to your rent-master Andreas Meier the most gracious order to repay duly the aforesaid sum and interest by the past Easter, without any delay, to the holder of my bond. Whereupon, too (since to bring such money from Rostock to Prague has hitherto fallen out quite burdensome for me), I have pledged and promised it — for my own and my people's best and benefit — to other people here, and in part also my blood-relative Eske Bille has, on my behalf, pledged and promised [it] elsewhere.</p><p>But now I am written to by Your Princely Grace's said rent-master, contrary to my expectation, that on account of other expenditures such money cannot yet be laid out and paid — which indeed comes to me as painful and strange, since, in accordance with Your Princely Grace's most gracious promise, I had by the past Easter committed myself to others on that account.</p><p>There comes therefore to Your Princely Grace my most humble, wholly diligent request, that Your Princely Grace will not let it happen that anything be lost to my hitherto well-established name [credit], and that I must act against my word, but [will] give the most gracious command that, at the very latest within the coming St. John's Day, the oft-mentioned money may be paid over to me without further delay.</p><p>In this Your Princely Grace shows me a great favour, and I am, according to my humble person, at all times wholly willing and zealous to acknowledge it in most humble gratitude. Herewith commending the same, together with your much-beloved consort, to the protection of Almighty God for a long-lasting and happy reign, obediently and faithfully.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 4th of June, in the year 1601.<br>Your Princely Grace's most humble, wholly willing<br>Tycho Brahe,<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), with his full titulature reproduced (<i>Duke of Mecklenburg, Prince of the Wends, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard</i>) — exactly as in Tycho's invitation letter to the same Duke of 15 April 1601 (brahe-t_16010415003). The MS is the <b>original dispatched letter</b> (<i>Originalbrev</i>), filed at Schwerin under the apt rubric <i>Debita passiva principum</i> — \"the passive debts [debts owed] of the princes.\"</li><li><b>Position in the debt thread.</b> This is Tycho's <b>direct personal appeal to the Duke</b> in the 10,000-thaler affair, written from Prague just under five months before his death. It sits between the appeal-to-the-go-betweens (Tycho→Bording, 12 April, brahe-t_16010412001; Tycho→rent-master Meier, 27 May, brahe-t_16010527001) and the later pressing of Meier (18 July, brahe-t_16010718001) and the ducal replies (24 July, brahe-t_16010724001; Ulrich→Meier, 9 August, brahe-t_16010809001).</li><li><b>The transaction.</b> Tycho had <b>advanced 10,000 thalers of principal</b> (<i>Hauptstuhl</i>) plus interest to the <i>junge Herrschaft zu Mecklenburg</i> — the <b>young lordship/junior dukes of Mecklenburg</b> — and had lawfully called it in at the contract term. Duke Ulrich had ordered his <b>rent-master Andreas Meier</b> (at Wismar) to repay it <i>by the past Easter</i> to the bearer of Tycho's bond. Relying on that promise, Tycho had <b>re-pledged the money onward</b> to creditors at Prague, and his kinsman <b>Eske Bille</b> (<i>Esche Bilde</i>) had likewise re-promised part of it elsewhere — so the rent-master's now pleading \"other expenditures\" threatens Tycho's word and credit (<i>mein wohlhergebrachter Name</i>).</li><li><b>The deadline.</b> <i>innerhalb nechstkünfftigen S. Johannis Tag</i> — within the coming <b>St. John's Day, 24 June 1601</b> — the same term named in the 27 May letter to Meier. The remittance difficulty <i>von Rostock gen Prag</i> (getting Baltic money to Bohemia) is the recurring practical snag of the whole affair.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg; the <i>junge Herrschaft</i> (junior dukes of Mecklenburg); <b>Andreas Meier</b>, ducal rent-master at Wismar; <b>Eske Bille</b>, Tycho's Danish kinsman and financial agent. The Duke's <i>vielgeliebte Gemahlin</i> (\"much-beloved consort\") is <b>Anna of Pomerania-Wolgast</b>, Ulrich's second wife (m. 1588) — named cautiously, not asserted from the text.</li><li><b>Register &amp; orthography.</b> Deferential chancery Early New High German in the subject's first person to a prince: <i>E: F: G:</i> (<i>Euer Fürstliche Gnaden</i>, \"Your Princely Grace\") throughout; period spellings (<i>vndt, pitt, pilligkait, gnedigst, vorstreckt, zueerstatten</i>). Subscription <i>manu propria</i> (<i>manu pprj:</i>) marks the autograph. One slip in the witness rubric — <i>E: E: G:</i> for <i>E: F: G:</i> — is the clerk's, not significant.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 4 Junij a:o 1601</i> at Prague; <b>New Style</b>, per Tycho's imperial-service usage. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'E: E: G:' corrected to 'E: F: G:' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 224.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clear chancery German business letter; the transaction (10,000-thaler principal + interest, the Easter term unmet, the St. John's Day deadline, the Rostock→Prague remittance problem) and every named party (Duke Ulrich, rent-master Andreas Meier, Eske Bille) are explicit and dovetail with the companion documents of the debt thread. No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 slice pages visually; the letter to Ulrich III of Mecklenburg (Dreyer's item 219, 'Ulricum ducem Tycho Brahe rogat...') runs from the foot of p.223 through p.224, citation 'Ex originali in Archivo publico Schwerinensi: Debita passiva principum Vol. XXVIII B.' Collated the full German text line-by-line against Dreyer: word-for-word identical apart from routine period spelling variance (vnd/vnndt/vndt, ignorable) and the single 'E: E: G:' vs 'E: F: G:' slip noted above. All substantive facts checked and matching: sum '10000 thaller hauptstuel', rent-master 'Andreas Meier(n)', blood-relative 'Esche Bilde'/Eske Bille, places Rostock/Prag, deadline 'S. Johannis tagk', date 'Prag den 4 junij a:o 1601'. English translation is faithful throughout with no fabrication, omission, or mistranslation found.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text doubles 'E:' (E: E: G:) where Dreyer, and every other instance of the same honorific elsewhere in this same letter (both source and Dreyer), reads 'E: F: G:' (Ewer Furstliche Gnaden) - an isolated keying slip in our source text, immaterial to sense and not reflected in the English. Dreyer prints: <i>Nun aber wirdt mir von gedachtem E: F: G: rentmeiftern...</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.222-225 page images (this letter's closing paragraphs and dateline fall on p.224). Dreyer's text unambiguously reads 'E: F: G:' consistent with the honorific used throughout the rest of the letter. No other divergence from Dreyer was found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 5, "pos": 3, "prev": "brahe-t_16010415003", "next": "brahe-t_16010718002", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010724001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Presses again for repayment of 10,000 thalers and interest from the young Mecklenburg dukes, noting Duke Ulrich's prior order to rent-master Meier at Easter has gone unfulfilled.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010604001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010612001", "d": "1601-06-12", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Eberhard Schele", "ck": "Eberhard Schele", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "Latin", "pl": "Aurich", "t": "Eberhard Schele → Tycho Brahe — Aurich [East Frisia], 12 June 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: Latin (with Greek) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Basel, Universitätsbibliothek, G I 23</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>NOBILISSIMO STRENUO ET MAGNIFICO VIRO TYCHONI BRAHEN DANO, SACRAe CAeS. MATHEMATICO, IMO ET FAUTORI SUO MULTIS NOMINIBUS COLENDISSIMO.<br>PRAGA.<br>Εὖ ζῆν καὶ εὖ πράττειν.</p><p>QUANQUAM apud D. DAVIDEM FABRICIUM ad nobiliss. Magam T. scripserim de rebus omnibus, Nobiliss. atque Magce vir, Domine multis nominibus colende, quas quidem literas meas unâ cum earundem latore, FABRICIO nostro, te accepisse et vidisse non dubito, facere tamen non potui, quin nobiliss. Magam T. hac schedula inviserem, sciscitaturus saltem, num dictus FABRICIUS noster salvus atque incolumis ad vos pervenerit. Multa enim metuebat atque, ut mihi videbatur, sibi persuadebat, quare non sine periculo hoc iter perficere posset. Veruntamen omnia in DEI praepotentis manu posita esse firmiter credo, sic ut eundem FABRICIUM, nisi DEO aliter visum fuerit, incolumem omnino ac salvum huc reversurum plane persuasum habeam. Porro nobiliss. Magam T. rogatam velim, uti me sibi commendatum et persuasum plane habeat, me ex eorum esse numero, qui nobiliss. Magam T. modis omnibus salvam optet atque incolumem, eiusque honoribus faveat quam maxume.</p><p>Caeterum quid Sacrae Caes. Matis Legatus et Consiliarius praecipuus D. HANNIWALT hîc rerum agat, quamque honorifice, ne dicam magnifice, ab Illri et generoso principe meo ENNONE, Orient. Frisiae Comite, exceptus sit, et quae porro Sacrae Caes. Mati ab Illri Cels. eius exspectanda sint officia atque servitia, meum non est perscribere, cum tempus ipsum atque exitus reipsa comprobatura sint, quali affectu Illris Dominus noster erga Sacratiss. Caes. Matem sit praeditus, quam ut DEUS opt. max. unâ cum nobiliss. Maga T. diutissime servet, ex animo precor. Ac si forsan occasio fuerit, ut Sacrae Caes. Mati mea quamvis humilia servitia commendare possis, quaeso studiorum, si non eorundem, attamen communium memor esto.</p><p>Haec scripsi αὐτοσχεδίως et quasi per satyram, propterea quod propter praesentiam praefati Caes. Matis Legati, qui pridie huc appulit, plusculis eram occupatus negocijs. Quare et festinationi et lituris ignosces, tum FABRICIO nostro hasce inclusas literas cum salute uberrima tradere haud dedigneris. Utinam vero sit aliquid, in quo nobiliss. Magae T. vicissim gratificari possim, neque enim labori, vigilijs, studijs, sumptui parcerem.</p><p>Vale vir nobiliss. atque Magce.<br>Scripta ex arce Auricana Frisiae Orientalis pridie Eidus Iunij stilo veteri Anno postremi temporis Ch. 1601.<br>Nobiliss. Magae T.<br>addictiss.<br>EBERARTUS SCHELE,<br>Orient. Frisiae Secret.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia VIII, p. 422; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Quae hoc ipso die de occupatione propugnaculi ante Bergam ex Haga Comitis allata sunt nova, cum et incerta adhuc sint nec vobis ignota diu eße poßint, lubens praetereo.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most noble, valiant and magnificent man Tycho Brahe the Dane, mathematician of the Sacred Imperial [Majesty], nay also his patron most-to-be-revered on many accounts. Prague.</b><br><i>To live well and to fare well.</i> [Greek]</p><p>Although I wrote to your most noble Magnificence about all matters by way of Master David Fabricius — which letter of mine, together with its bearer, our Fabricius, I do not doubt you have received and read — yet I could not but call upon your most noble Magnificence with this note, [meaning] at least to inquire whether the said our Fabricius has reached you safe and unharmed. For he feared many things, and, as it seemed to me, persuaded himself that he could not complete this journey without danger. Nevertheless I firmly believe all things are placed in the hand of God almighty, so that I hold it plainly persuaded that the same Fabricius — unless it shall have seemed otherwise to God — will return here wholly unharmed and safe. Further, I would have your most noble Magnificence asked, to hold me commended, and be plainly persuaded that I am of the number of those who wish your most noble Magnificence safe and unharmed in every way, and who favour your honours as much as may be.</p><p>For the rest, what the Sacred Imperial Majesty's Legate and chief Councillor, Master Hannewald, transacts here, and how honourably — not to say magnificently — he has been received by my illustrious and generous prince Enno, Count of East Frisia, and what services and duties are further to be expected by the Sacred Imperial Majesty from His Illustrious Highness, it is not mine to write out; for time itself and the outcome will prove in fact with what affection our Illustrious Lord is disposed toward the most Sacred Imperial Majesty — whom, together with your most noble Magnificence, may God, best and greatest, preserve for the longest time, I pray from my heart. And if perhaps there be occasion that you can commend my services, however humble, to the Sacred Imperial Majesty, I pray you, be mindful of our studies — if not the same, yet shared.</p><p>These [lines] I have written offhand and as it were in a medley, because, on account of the presence of the aforesaid Imperial Legate (who landed here yesterday), I was occupied with rather many affairs. Wherefore you will pardon both the haste and the blottings; and you will not disdain to deliver to our Fabricius these enclosed letters, with most abundant greeting. Would, in truth, that there were anything in which I might in turn oblige your most noble Magnificence — for I would spare neither labour, nor vigils, nor studies, nor expense.</p><p>Farewell, most noble and magnificent man.<br>Written from the castle of Aurich in East Frisia, the day before the Ides of June, Old Style, in the year of the last age of Christ 1601.<br>Most devoted to your most noble Magnificence,<br>Eberhard Schele,<br>Secretary of East Frisia.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> The news that have been brought this very day from The Hague concerning the seizure of the fort before Bergen — since they are still uncertain, and cannot long be unknown to you — I gladly pass over.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> An <b>inbound</b> letter <b>to</b> Tycho at Prague. <b>Eberhard Schele</b>, <i>Secretarius</i> of <b>East Frisia</b> (Ostfriesland), writing from the comital seat at <b>Aurich</b>.</li><li><b>The David Fabricius thread (connective tissue).</b> The note's chief purpose is anxious solicitude over <b>David Fabricius</b> (1564–1617), the Frisian pastor-astronomer, who was <b>travelling to visit Tycho</b> carrying Schele's fuller letter — and who, Schele reports, dreaded the journey as dangerous. This is the <b>arrival half</b> of the episode whose departure half Georg Rollenhagen describes (brahe-t_16010515001, 15 May 1601: Fabricius \"had turned aside to me out of his journey, which he had set toward you\"). Schele asks Tycho both to confirm Fabricius's safe arrival and to hand Fabricius the <i>enclosed letters</i>.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>D. David Fabricius</i> = <b>David Fabricius</b>, the courier and the object of Schele's concern.</li><li><i>D. Hanniwalt</i> = <b>Andreas Hannewald (Hanniwald)</b>, secretary/councillor of Rudolf II, here as <b>imperial legate</b> to East Frisia, \"landed yesterday\" — his visit is the diplomatic backdrop and the reason for Schele's haste.</li><li><i>Illustris princeps meus Enno, Orientalis Frisiae Comes</i> = <b>Enno III, Count of East Frisia</b> (1563–1625), Schele's sovereign, host to the imperial legate.</li><li><i>Sacra Caesarea Maiestas</i> = <b>Emperor Rudolf II</b>, to whom Schele hopes Tycho may commend his \"humble services.\"</li><li><b>Greek (transcribed + glossed, protocol §4.2).</b> The address bears the salutation-motto <i>Εὖ ζῆν καὶ εὖ πράττειν</i> — \"<b>to live well and to fare well</b>\" (the classical good-wish). In the body, <i>αὐτοσχεδίως</i> = \"offhand, extempore\"; the Latin idiom <i>per satyram</i> (= <i>per saturam</i>) = \"in a medley / pell-mell.\"</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>arx Auricana Frisiae Orientalis</i> = the castle of <b>Aurich</b>, East Frisia.</li><li><b>Epistolary formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>Vale</i> → \"Farewell\"; subscription <i>Nobiliss. Magae T. addictiss.</i> = \"Most devoted to your most noble Magnificence.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3 — explicit Old Style).</b> Dateline <i>pridie Eidus [Idus] Iunij <b>stilo veteri</b></i> = \"the day before the Ides of June, <b>Old Style</b>\" = <b>12 June</b> (the Ides of June = the 13th). East Frisia, a Reformed/Lutheran territory, kept the <b>Julian</b> calendar; Schele marks it expressly <i>stilo veteri</i> — to be contrasted with the New-Style Prague dating of Tycho's imperial correspondence. The flourish <i>Anno postremi temporis Ch[risti] 1601</i> (\"in the year of the last age of Christ 1601\") is an eschatological commonplace, reproduced. Not converted.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> News-postscript (Hague reports of the seizure of a fort before Bergen-op-Zoom), restored from Dreyer VIII p.422 (after \"Eberartus SCHELE, Orient. Frisiae Secret.\"; dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the word-space in the closing dateline: Dreyer prints \"Auricana Frisiae\" as two words (TBOO VIII p.422), not the glued \"AuricanaFrisiae\" of the prior transcription.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — clean Latin; the persons (Schele, David Fabricius, the imperial legate Hannewald, Count Enno III of East Frisia) are securely identified, the Fabricius journey is corroborated by the Rollenhagen letter, the Greek tags are transcribed and glossed, and the explicit <i>stilo veteri</i> dateline is handled per protocol §3 (the Roman <i>pridie Idus Iunij</i> = 12 June). No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. The 17-page slice holds many letters; located ours (Eberhard Schele to Tycho, dated pridie Eidus Iunij / 12 June 1601 O.S.) on TBOO VIII pp. 421-422 and collated it in full, including the postscript on the Bergen-op-Zoom news, against the page images. The salutation, both paragraphs, the signature block, and the postscript all match Dreyer's wording exactly apart from the one glued-word TEXT_ERROR. Translation is faithful throughout, including the correctly-restored postscript (\"Quae hoc ipso die de occupatione propugnaculi ante Bergam...\"), which Dreyer does print at this location, confirming the working transcription's restoration note is accurate. Per the grading rule, the TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C despite its triviality and the otherwise clean translation.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text glues \"Auricana\" and \"Frisiae\" into one word with the space dropped; Dreyer prints them as two words. Trivial and does not affect meaning (the translator correctly parsed it as \"Aurich in East Frisia\" regardless), but it is a genuine departure from Dreyer's text. Dreyer prints: <i>Scripta ex arce Auricana Friſiæ Orientalis pridie Eidus Iunij ſtilo veteri Anno poſtremi temporis Ch. 1601.</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against Dreyer TBOO VIII pp.407-423, specifically p.422 lines 20-22, which read \"Scripta ex arce Auricana Frifiæ Orientalis pridie Eidus Iunij ftilo veteri Anno poftremi temporis Ch. 1601.\" — confirming the two-word form and matching the postscript already restored in current_latin. Only the Latin required a fix; the English translation already correctly construed the phrase. No other defects found across the checked pages.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Basel, Universitätsbibliothek Basel, G I 23."], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Inquires whether David Fabricius arrived safely after departing with fears about the dangers of the journey, and commends himself as a friend.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010612001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010617001", "d": "1601-06-17", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": "Eske Bille", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Doberan", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → Eske Bille — Doberan, 17 June 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN ESSKE BILDEN ZU KOPENHAGEN</p><p>Vlrich etc.</p><p>VNSERN gunstigen grues zuuor. Ehrnuester lieber besonder. Wir seind von dem erbarn Vnser cammerer vnd lieben getrewen Jochim von Stralendorff berichtet worden, was jhr wegen der 10000 thaler, damit Vnsere vnmundjge vettern vnd pflegesohne, die junge hertzogen zu Meckelnburgk, dem auch ernuesten Vnsern lieben besondern Tycho Brahen verhafft, vnd welche so auf negst verschienen ostern betagt gewesen, an jhn gelanget. Nun wißen Wir Vns dieser schuldtfurderung vnd das auf negst verschienen ostern das geldt hette erlegt werden sollen, woll zuerjnnern, haben auch von deßwegen bereits vorlengst hochermelter Vnserer pflegesohne rentmeister Andreas Meyern verwarnet, darzu verdacht zusein, das er auf rechte gesetzte zeit zur zahlung gelangen konte, jnmaßen Vns dan auch ferner nicht vnbewust ist, das er zu dero notturfft geldt besprochen, so jhm auch gegen die negstverschienen ostern gewisse zugesaget, aber auf die letzte stunde jhm allererst wieder ein bloßen geschlagen worden, dahero es sich dan verursachet, das er seines teils auf bestimbte zeit zu der versprochenen zahlung nicht gelangen können. Jedoch ist er noch in fleißiger bemuhung, das er mit dem ehisten geldt zuwege bringen muge, vnd verhoffen Wir numehr auch teglich von jhm diese gute erklerung, das er werde rath geschaffet haben. Vnd weill die sachen itzterzeltermaßen geschaffen, alß gesinnen Wir demnach an euch hiemit gantz gnediglich, jhr wollet euch wegen gedachtes Tycho Brahen noch eine kleine vnd geringe zeit gedulden, so seindt Wir des gnedigen erbietens, die versehung zuthun, das alßdan mit dem schleunigsten die wirckliche bezahlung erfolgen soll, dan Wir auf itzo mehrgedachten rentmeister derentwegen von newen wiederumb erjnnerung vnd auch starcken beuehll gethan. Habens euch immittelst zu gnediger erklerung nicht verhalten wollen, vnd Wir seind euch mit gnaden vnd allen gutten woll gewogen.</p><p>Datum Dobbran den 17. junij a:o 1601.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Eske Bille at Copenhagen.</b></p><p>Ulrich, etc.</p><p>Our favourable greeting beforehand. Honourable, dear, and especial [friend]. We have been informed by the honourable Our chamberlain and dear faithful [servant] Joachim von Stralendorff of what you have conveyed to him regarding the 10,000 thaler, with which Our minor cousins and foster-sons, the young Dukes of Mecklenburg, are indebted to the likewise-honourable Our dear and especial [friend] Tycho Brahe, and which fell due at the recently-past Easter. Now, We well remember this debt-claim, and that the money should have been paid at the recently-past Easter; and We have therefore already long since warned the rent-master of Our highly-mentioned foster-sons, Andreas Meyer, to be intent thereon, that he might come to the payment at the rightly-set time — just as it is also not further unknown to Us that he bespoke money for that need, which was also certainly promised him toward the recently-past Easter, but at the last hour was once again struck a blank; whence it then came about that he, for his part, could not come to the promised payment at the appointed time. However, he is still in diligent effort to bring the money about with the soonest; and We now also daily expect from him this good declaration, that he will have arranged the means. And since the matters stand in the manner just told, We accordingly desire of you herewith quite graciously that you would, on account of the said Tycho Brahe, have patience yet a little and short time; [and] so We are of the gracious offer to make the provision, that then, with the speediest, the actual payment shall follow — for We have now again, on that account, given the oft-mentioned rent-master fresh reminder and also strong command. We did not wish to withhold [this] from you meanwhile, for [your] gracious information; and We are graciously, and with all good, well inclined toward you.</p><p>Given Doberan, the 17th of June, in the year 1601.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b>, writing not to Tycho but to <b>Eske Bille at Copenhagen</b> — <b>Tycho's agent/kinsman</b> (of the Bille family; cf. brahe-t_16010724001, where Eske Bille's enquiries at Doberan are mentioned). The witness is among the Schwerin <i>Debita passiva principum</i> records.</li><li><b>The same 10,000-thaler debt.</b> This letter (17 June 1601) is part of the same dossier as brahe-t_16010724001 (24 July 1601, Ulrich to Tycho): the <b>10,000-thaler debt</b> of the young Mecklenburg Dukes to Tycho, due at Easter 1601 but unpaid because the rent-master <b>Andreas Meyer</b>'s arranged financing \"was struck a blank\" at the last hour. The Duke asks Eske Bille (and through him Tycho) for patience and renews his command to Meyer. The chamberlain <b>Joachim von Stralendorff</b> is the intermediary who relayed Bille's message.</li><li><b><i>einen bloßen geschlagen</b></i> — idiom, \"came up empty / the promise failed\" (as in brahe-t_16010724001).</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Dobbran [Doberan] den 17. junij a:o 1601</i> = <b>17 June 1601</b>; Mecklenburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely; the matter (the 10,000-thaler Mecklenburg debt, the Easter-1601 payment failure, rent-master Andreas Meyer, chamberlain Stralendorff, the request for patience) is clear and dovetails closely with brahe-t_16010724001. No ambiguous reading.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.223-226 as page images; letter (Dreyer's item 221) runs from bottom of p.225 to p.226. source_text matches Dreyer's printed German verbatim throughout. Collated the full translation clause-by-clause: the 10,000 thaler debt, Andreas Meyer the rent-master, the Easter deadline missed, the request for patience, and the closing all render faithfully. Sum (10,000 thaler), names (Stralendorff, Andreas Meyer), and date (17 June 1601) all correct. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, or hedge-loss found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Confirms the 10,000 thalers owed to Tycho fell due at Easter, explains the rent-master's failure to secure promised funds at the last hour, and assures Eske Bille repayment will be arranged.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010617001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010718001", "d": "1601-07-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Andreas Meier", "ck": "Andreas Meier", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Andreas Meier — Prague, 18 July 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EHRNUESTEN VND ACHTPARN HERRN ANDRESS MAIERN, FÜRSTLICHEM MECKLE[N]BURGISCHEN RENTMEISTERN, MEINEM GUETEN FREUNDTE. WISSMAR.</p><p>MEINEN freundtlichen grueß sampt wünschung alles gueten iederzeit bevor. Ehrnuester achtpar, insonders gönstiger lieber her rentmeister. Was sider der auffkündigung dero 10000 thaller hauptsummae sampt dem interesse, damit jj: ff: gg: die junge herschafft zue Mecklenburgk mir verhafftet, vorgelauffen vnd was vngelegenheit mir auß verzugerung der zahlung (welcher termin (meiner auffkündigung nach) verfloßen gewesen den 1. aprilis dieses lauffenden jhars) entstanden, werdet ihr euch auß meinen hiebevor ahn euch ergangenen schreiben nochmahls zur genüege wißen zue entsinn[en]. Dieweiln dan ihr abermahl gegen gleichßfals verlauffenen s. Joannis thagk obangereugte summa vnd interesse richtich zu erlegen erpotten, hab ichs dabej, wiewohl ich dadurch in keinen geringen schaden gerhaten, j: f: g: hertzog Vldrichen etc., meinem gnedichsten herren, zue vnterthenigsten gefallen (wiewohl die bürgern laut vnseres contracts einzuemahnen ich guete vnd pilliche vrsacch gehabt) biß dato beruhen laßen. Vnd nehme mir in kainen zweiffel, ihr werdet nuhnmehr (seitenmahl lenger mitt dem gelde zue warten ich durchauß nitt kan oder bedacht bin) auff die mittel vnd wege gedacht, auch dazu gethon haben, das mehrgenante hauptsumma vnd interesse nuhnmaehr richtich verhanden vnd strax par außgezehlt werden solle vnd könne. Jnmittelst sehe ich gerne, das es bej ainem gewißen man zu Hamburgk oder Lübeck, bis es abgeholet wirt, auffgehoben oder verwahrt würde. Wie dan alßpaldt aine gewiße pottschafft ich derer orter zu senden daßselbige geldt abzuholen bedacht bin. Vnd hab mit zaigern dieses euch söllichs zuvor zu verstendigen, damit er alßpaldt damit gefürdert vnd nitt auffgehalten werde (worauß dan vns baiderseits, so wohl auch den bürgen, allerhande vngelegenheit entstehen würde), vor guet angesehen. Vns hiemit götlicher almacht entpfhelendt.</p><p>Prag den 18 julij st:novo a:o 1601.<br>EE: dienst- vnd freundtwilliger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>man: ppj:</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 226; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>Gelanget hieneben an den herren rentmeistern mein freundtliches pitten vnd söcchen, er wolle kainem, der nitt entweder von mir handtschrifft vnd sigell, von heut dato abzurechnen, oder auch von meinem vettern Esche Bilden, zaigen kan, dan auß etzlichen handlen söllichs vonnötten, jnsonderheit wolle er ainem holsteinischen jubilierer mit nahmen Bernhardt Fersten oder seinen dienern, welchen ich zwar vorhin Vollmacht, iedoch in anderer mainung, gegeben, itzo gar nichts folgen laßen. Datum ut [supra].</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable and worshipful Lord Andreas Meier, princely Mecklenburg rent-master, my good friend. Wismar.</b></p><p>My friendly greeting, together with the wish of all good, at all times beforehand. Honourable, worshipful, especially favourable, dear Lord rent-master: what has passed since the calling-in of those 10,000 thalers of principal sum, together with the interest, by which Their Princely Graces, the young rulership of Mecklenburg, stand bound to me, and what inconvenience has arisen for me from the delay of payment (the term for which, according to my notice of recall, fell due the 1st of April of this current year) — of this you will know well enough to remind yourself once more from my earlier letters sent to you. Since, then, you have once again offered to pay the above-mentioned sum and interest duly against St. John's Day (which has likewise now passed), I have — although I have thereby come to no small loss — let it rest until now, to the most humble pleasure of His Princely Grace Duke Ulrich etc., my most gracious Lord (although, according to our contract, I had good and fair cause to dun the sureties). And I have no doubt that you will by now (seeing that I altogether cannot, nor am minded to, wait any longer for the money) have thought upon the ways and means, and have so acted, that the aforesaid principal sum and interest shall and may now be duly to hand and at once paid out in cash. In the meantime I should be glad if it were deposited or kept safe with some reliable man at Hamburg or Lübeck until it is fetched; for I intend forthwith to send a trusty messenger to those parts to collect that same money. And I have thought it good to apprise you of this beforehand by the bearer of this [letter], so that he may at once be furthered with it and not held up (whence all manner of inconvenience would arise for us both, and for the sureties as well). Herewith commending us to divine omnipotence.</p><p>Prague, the 18th of July, New Style, in the year 1601.<br>Your Honour's service- and friend-willing<br>Tycho Brahe<br>[written] with his own hand.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> Herewith reaches the Lord rent-master my friendly request and entreaty, that he let nothing be paid out to anyone who cannot show either a bond and seal from me — to be reckoned from today's date — or [one] from my cousin Eske Bille, for, from certain dealings, this is necessary; in particular, that he let nothing at all now be paid out to a Holstein jeweller named Bernhard Ferst, or to his servants, to whom I had indeed earlier given a mandate, but with another intent. Dated as above.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Prague</b>, less than four months before his death (24 October 1601). The letter is one of several late documents on his outstanding loans and finances.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <i>Andreas Meier (Andress Maiern), fürstlich-mecklenburgischer Rentmeister, Wismar</i> = <b>Andreas Meier</b>, the ducal Mecklenburg <b>rent-master (treasurer / financial officer)</b> at Wismar — the official charged with discharging the ducal debt.</li><li><b>The transaction.</b> A <b>10,000-thaler loan</b> (the <i>Hauptsumme</i>, principal) plus <i>Interesse</i> (interest) which the young Mecklenburg ruling house owed Tycho. Tycho had given formal <i>Aufkündigung</i> (notice of recall) with the principal falling due <b>1 April 1601</b>; when that lapsed, repayment was re-promised for <b>St. John's Day (24 June)</b>, which had also now passed. Tycho, though entitled by contract to proceed against the <i>Bürgen</i> (citizen-sureties), had forborne out of deference to Duke Ulrich. He now presses for immediate cash payment, to be lodged meanwhile with a trustworthy agent at <b>Hamburg or Lübeck</b> pending collection by his messenger.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>J. F. G. Herzog Ulrich</i> = <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</b> (1527–1603), Tycho's \"most gracious Lord\" in the matter.</li><li><i>die junge Herrschaft zu Mecklenburg</i> = <b>the young rulers of Mecklenburg</b> (the junior line for whom the debt stood) — <i>Their Princely Graces</i> (<i>Jhr Fürstlich Gnaden</i>).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Wismar</i> — the Hanseatic port and Mecklenburg residence-town where the rent-master sat; <i>Hamburg / Lübeck</i> named as safe deposit-points for the funds.</li><li><b>Formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> Burgher-courteous register (<i>ehrnvest und achtbar</i>, \"honourable and worshipful\"). The subscription <i>EE: dienst- vnd freundtwilliger</i> = <i>Euer Ehren dienst- und freundwilliger</i> (\"Your Honour's service- and friend-willing\"); <i>man: ppj:</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand,\" autograph attestation). <i>J. F. G.</i> = <i>Jhr Fürstlich(e) Gnad(en)</i>; <i>J. J. F. F. G. G.</i> = the plural (\"Their Princely Graces\").</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 18 Julij st:novo</i> — explicitly <b>New Style</b>, as in all Tycho's Prague letters. The internal deadlines (<i>1. Aprilis</i>; <i>S. Joannis Tag</i> = 24 June) are likewise to be read consistently with the document's stated New-Style usage; reproduced, not converted.</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> Closing postscript revoking the Holstein jeweller Bernhard Ferst's mandate, restored from Dreyer XIV p.226 (dropped by the working transcription).</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'mist nahmen' corrected to 'mit nahmen' in the postscript, per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 227 ('mit nahmen Bernhardt Ferften').</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a business letter in dense but unambiguous chancery German; the financial particulars (10,000 thaler principal, the 1 April and St. John's Day terms, the Hamburg/Lübeck deposit) are explicit and the sense is secure. Abbreviations are expanded in the apparatus rather than silently in the body.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read TBOO XIV pp.224-227 as page images (item 222 in Dreyer, addressed to Andreas Meyer, rent-master of Wismar, re the 10,000-thaler Mecklenburg debt). Collated the full body and the postscript (restored from Dreyer p.226 per our own apparatus note, and confirmed to match Dreyer there) -- one word-level corruption found (\"mist\" for \"mit\"), which is a TEXT_ERROR per the brief's definition regardless of its low severity/no sense-change, and per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C. All other content -- the 10,000-thaler sum, the two date terms (1 April, S. Johannis day), Duke Ulrich, cousin Eske Bille, the 18 July 1601 closing date -- matches Dreyer exactly and the English translation itself is faithful throughout, including working around the source corruption correctly.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text prints the non-word \"mist\" where Dreyer's edition of record prints \"mit\" (= \"mit Namen,\" i.e. \"named\"); a single corrupted word in the published source Latin/German, not in Dreyer. The translator evidently worked from the correct sense despite the corruption -- the English itself is accurate -- but the source_text departs from the edition of record. Dreyer prints: <i>jnfonderheit wolle er ainem holfteinifchen jubilierer mit nahmen Bernhardt Ferften (TBOO XIV, p.226)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.224-227 page images (this letter falls on pp.225-227 within the supplied slice). The postscript's 'mit nahmen Bernhardt Ferften' is unambiguous in the page image. English translation needed no change since the translator had already rendered the correct sense. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Andreas Meier", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16010527001", "next": null, "reply_to": null, "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Recounts successive failed repayment deadlines for the 10,000 thalers owed by Mecklenburg, including the recently-passed Midsummer term, and presses Meier for immediate settlement.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010718001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010718002", "d": "1601-07-18", "dir": "from", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Prague", "t": "Tycho Brahe → Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg — Prague, 18 July 1601 [N.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (princely-address chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM DURCHLEUCHTIGSTEN HOCHGEBORNEN FURSTEN VNDT HERRN, HERRN VLDRICHEN, HERTZOGEN ZU MECKLENBURGK, GRAFFEN ZU SCHWERIN, DER LANDE ROSTOCH VNDT STARGART HERRN, MEINEM GNEDICHSTENN HERRN.</p><p>DURCHLAÜCHTIGSTER hochgeborner fürst, gnedichster herr. Ewer Fürst: Gnaden sein meine vnterthenigste gantzwillige dienste iederzeit höchstes fleißes bevor. Vndt soll deroselben abermahl vnterthenigster gueter wohlmainung nit pergen, welchergestallt ich zwar vngezweiffelte hofnung gefast gehabt, es wurde von Ewer Fürst: Gnaden rentmeistern Andres Meyern vnserem wegen dero 10000 thalern auffgerichteten contracts vnd auffkundigungesbrieff (inmaßen sie zue rechter zeit beschehen) zufolge mihr sölliche summa sampt dem interesse gegen vergangenen ostern ohne ainige fernere verzugerung dageraicht vndt gefolget sein. Weiln aber damalß wegen anderer von obgemeldten h: rentmeistern angereugter hohen vndt notwendigen außgaben sölliches nitt geschehen können (wiewohl zuvor die auffkundigung ohne ainigen bedencken ahngenohmen vndt ich meine sacchen alhie darnach gerichtet, auch andere leutte darauff vertrostet, vnd dardurch in keinen geringen schaden gerahten), so hab dennoch E: F: G: zue vnterthenigsten diensten vnd meinem schaden vngeacht ich biß das gegen vergangen s: Johan: der hauptstuel sampt dem interesse richtig erlegt vndt mir zuegestelldt würde (wie dan mehrgedachter h: rentmeister mir abermahl schriftlich versprocchen) mich gedulde[t]. Weiln dan dießer ander termin abermahl lengst verfloßen, alß nehme ich in kainen zweiffell, E: F: G: werden eß durch den rentmeistern schon dahin gedirigirt vnd gebracht haben, daß söllich geldt schon richtig verhanden, damit ich die burgen einzuemahnen nitt genotdrenget werde. Vndt bin derowegen eine gewiße pottschafft alßpaldt derer örtter daßelbige anhero zuholen abzuefertigen ihn willens.</p><p>Gelanget derowegen ahn Ewer F: G: mein vnterthenigste gantzfleißige pitt, sie geruhen den gnedichsten befehlich vnd anordnung zue thuen, damit derselbige, sopaldt ehr anlangen wirdt, nit damit auffgehalten, sondern ehist sich sampt dem gelde wiederumb anhero zue verfüegen gefurdert werden muege.</p><p>Diß vnd andere gut vnd wohlthatten vmb E: F: G: gehorsambst zue verdienen, will ich mir stetz zum höchsten angelegen sein laßen. Ewer F: G: sampt dero vielgeliepten gemahlin hiemit göttlicher allmacht vnd zue dero fernern gnaden mich vnterthenigst vndt getreulich entpfhelendt.</p><p>Datum Prag den 18. julij anno 1601.<br>Ewer Fürstlichen Ghnaden vnterthenigster gantz williger<br>Tycho Brahe<br>man: pprj:</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the most illustrious, high-born Prince and Lord, Lord Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, Count of Schwerin, Lord of the lands of Rostock and Stargard, my most gracious Lord.</b></p><p>Most illustrious, high-born Prince, most gracious Lord. To Your Princely Grace, my most humble, wholly willing services with the highest diligence, at all times beforehand. And I should not again conceal from the same, in most humble good intention, in what manner I had indeed conceived an undoubted hope that, by Your Princely Grace's rent-master Andreas Meier — pursuant to the contract drawn up over the 10,000 thalers and to the notice-of-recall (inasmuch as these took place at the proper time) — that sum, together with the interest, would be handed over and paid to me against the past Easter, without any further delay. But since at that time, on account of other high and necessary expenditures cited by the aforesaid lord rent-master, this could not be done (although the recall had previously been accepted without any hesitation, and I had ordered my affairs here accordingly, and reassured other people upon it, and thereby came to no small loss), I nonetheless — for Your Princely Grace's most humble service, and notwithstanding my loss — held my patience, until against the past St. John's [Day] the principal together with the interest should be duly paid and delivered to me (as the oft-mentioned lord rent-master again promised me in writing). Since, then, this second term too has long since passed, I have no doubt that Your Princely Grace will already have so directed and brought it about, through the rent-master, that such money is now duly to hand — so that I be not constrained to dun the sureties. And I am therefore minded to dispatch a trusty messenger at once to those parts to fetch the same hither.</p><p>There comes therefore to Your Princely Grace my most humble, wholly diligent petition, that you would deign to give the most gracious command and order, that the same [messenger], as soon as he arrives, be not held up with it, but be furthered to betake himself back hither with the money as soon as possible.</p><p>This, and other good deeds and benefactions, to deserve most obediently of Your Princely Grace, I will ever let be of the highest concern to me. Your Princely Grace, together with your much-beloved consort, I herewith most humbly and faithfully commend to divine omnipotence and to your further graces.</p><p>Given at Prague, the 18th of July, in the year 1601.<br>Your Princely Grace's most humble, wholly willing<br>Tycho Brahe<br>with his own hand.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender, recipient &amp; register.</b> <b>Tycho Brahe</b> at <b>Prague</b>, escalating the Mecklenburg-debt matter from the rent-master to the <b>sovereign</b>: this is the <b>princely-register companion</b> to the burgher-register letter sent the <i>same day</i> to the rent-master, brahe-t_16010718001 (Tycho→Meier, 18 July 1601). The recipient is <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</b> (1527–1603), given his full titulature — <i>Herzog zu Mecklenburg, Graf zu Schwerin, der Lande Rostock und Stargard Herr</i>. The deferential first/second-person forms (<i>Eure Fürstliche Gnaden</i>, \"Your Princely Grace\") mark the subject-to-prince register, against the peer-to-peer tone of the Meier letters.</li><li><b>The matter (now fully cross-linked).</b> The <b>10,000-thaler loan</b> (principal + interest), recalled by contract; promised first <b>against Easter</b>, then <b>against St. John's Day (24 June)</b> — both terms now lapsed. Tycho, professing patience and loss, asks the Duke to command that his coming <b>messenger</b> be paid out promptly, so he need not proceed against the <b>sureties (<i>Bürgen</i>)</b>. The thread is now complete across five documents: Tycho→Meier (27 May, this 18 July), Tycho→Duke Ulrich (this), Eske Bille→Meier (24 July), Duke Ulrich→Meier (9 Aug).</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Andreas Meier</i>, the rent-master; the <i>Bürgen</i> (citizen-sureties); <i>dero vielgeliebte Gemahlin</i> = the Duke's <b>consort</b> (Elisabeth of Denmark, daughter of Frederik II — herself a connection to Tycho's Danish world).</li><li><b>Place.</b> <i>Prag</i> = Prague.</li><li><b>Formulae &amp; abbreviations (protocol §5.5).</b> <i>E: F: G:</i> = <i>Eure Fürstliche Gnaden</i> (\"Your Princely Grace\"); <i>Hauptstuhl</i> = the <b>principal</b> (capital sum); <i>man: pprj:</i> = <i>manu propria</i> (\"with his own hand,\" autograph). <i>Datum</i> → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 18. Julij anno 1601</i>; New Style (Tycho's Prague usage; cf. the explicit <i>stylo novo</i> of the same-day Meier letter). The internal terms (<i>Ostern</i>, <i>St. Johannis</i>) read on the same reckoning. Reproduced, not converted.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Latin: 'versfüegen' corrected to 'verfüegen' per Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, p. 227.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a clear princely-address chancery letter; the loan particulars, the lapsed Easter/St-John's terms, and the parties (Duke Ulrich, Meier, the sureties, the consort) are explicit and fully corroborated by the four companion documents. No crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer's page images visually (TBOO XIV pp. 225-228 slice, letter no. 223 on pp. 226-227) and collated the full letter -- opening address, both body paragraphs, closing formula and signature, plus every proper name (Ulrich/Vlrichen, Duke of Mecklenburg, Count of Schwerin, lands of Rostock/Stargard; Andres Meyer/Andreas Meier), number (10,000 thalers), date (18 July 1601; 'past Easter'; 'past St John's Day'), and place (Prague/Prag) -- against both source_text and published_english. The English translation is faithful throughout: no fabricated facts, no dropped clauses, no flipped subjects/negations, no hardened hedges. One TEXT_ERROR was found: source_text's 'versfüegen' is a one-letter transcription corruption of Dreyer's 'verfüegen' (verfügen); it is inert for translation purposes since the English correctly conveys the sense either way. Per the grading rule, the presence of a TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C even though the defect is low-severity and the verdict is MINOR, not SERIOUS.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Our source_text reads \"versfüegen\" (an extra inserted \"s\", yielding a non-word) where Dreyer's page image clearly prints \"verfüegen\" (= verfügen, the standard reflexive \"sich ... verfügen\", to betake oneself). A transcription slip in our source_text against the edition of record; the English translation itself is unaffected since the translator rendered the correct sense regardless. Dreyer prints: <i>...fondern ehift fich fampt dem gelde wiederumb anhero zue verfüegen gefurdert werden muege. (TBOO XIV p. 227, line ~19-20)</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified the single defect by direct visual inspection of the Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.225-228 page images. 'verfüegen' (no inserted 's') is unambiguous on p.227. No other divergence from Dreyer was found in this letter.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 5, "pos": 4, "prev": "brahe-t_16010604001", "next": "brahe-t_16010724001", "reply_to": null, "reply_by": "brahe-t_16010724001"}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Renews the demand for repayment of 10,000 thalers and interest from Mecklenburg, recounting the contract terms and the loss caused by failure to pay at the agreed Easter deadline.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010718002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010724001", "d": "1601-07-24", "dir": "to", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": null, "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg → Tycho Brahe — Güstrow, 24 July 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Opera Omnia </i>VIII · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (original letter / Originalbrev)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>AN TYCHO BRAHEN ZU PRAAG.</p><p>Vlrich etc.</p><p>VNSERN gonstigen grueß zuuor. Ehrnuester lieber besonder, Wir haben ewer schreiben empfangen vnd was jhr wegen richtiger erlegung der 10000 thaler, damit euch Vnsere vnmundige vettern vnd pflegesohne, die junge hertzogen zu Meckelnburgk etc., verhafft, an Vns gelanget, daraus vernommen. Nun ist nicht ohnedas gegen negst verschienen ostern diejennigen bej denen hochermelter Vnser pflegesohne rentmeister Andr: M[eyer] ewerntsowol auch anderer mehr leute halber geldt besprochen, welche jhm auch darauff gewisse vertrostung gethan, einen bloßen geschlagen. Wir haben aber dagegen in andere wege die versehung gethan, das man baldt hernacher die summa, so jhr haben sollet, zu wege gebracht, welche summa auch bereits vor negst vergangen Johannis baptistae alhie zur stedte gelegen, wie dan auch solch geldt hirselbst noch liegt, vnd hette man es, wan nur dasieder darumb anfurderung geschehen where, gegen wiedereinandtwortung der außgegebenen verschreibung wol mechtig werden konnen. Vnd mugen Wir euch hieneben nicht bergen, das Eßke Bilde vor Johannis einen seiner diener zu Vns gen Dobbran geschicket, vnd sich alda erkundigen laßen, ob das geldt verhanden where, deme Wir wieder anzeigen laßen, weil Wir alle stunde des rentmeisters ankunfft mit dem gelde vermütendt wheren, das er auff Johannis bej Vns wiederumb ansuchens vnd alßdan des geldes gewertig sein solle, es hat sich aber derselbe so weinig auf Johannis alse auch seidt der zeit bej Vns nicht angegeben; where es aber geschehen vnd er hette die außgegebene Verschreibung mit sich gebracht, solte auch gestracks die außzehlung darauff erfolget sein, vnd konnet jhr dahero ewers geldes auch nochmaln wol mechtig werden. Welchs Wir euch hinwieder zu gnediger erklerung nicht wollen verhalten, vnd Wir seind euch mit gnaden geneigt.</p><p>Datum Güstrow den 24. julij a:o 1601.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To Tycho Brahe at Prague.</b></p><p>Ulrich, etc.</p><p>Our favourable greeting beforehand. Honourable, dear, and especial [friend]: We have received your letter, and have understood from it what you have conveyed to Us regarding the proper payment of the 10,000 thaler with which Our minor cousins and foster-sons, the young Dukes of Mecklenburg etc., are indebted to you. Now, it is not otherwise [than this]: toward the recently-past Easter, those with whom the highly-mentioned rent-master of Our foster-sons, Andreas M[eyer], had bespoken money — on your account, as also for the sake of several other people — and who had given him a certain assurance thereupon, came up empty [struck a blank]. But We, on the other hand, made provision by other means, so that soon afterward the sum which you are to have was brought about — which sum also already lay here in the town [ready] before the recently-past [feast of] John the Baptist [24 June], as indeed such money still lies here; and, had only the demand for it been made here, it could well have been obtained against the return of the issued bond. And We cannot conceal from you herewith that Eske Bille, before [the feast of] John, sent one of his servants to Us at Doberan, and had inquiry made there whether the money was on hand; to whom We had it indicated [in reply] that — since We were every hour expecting the rent-master's arrival with the money — he should, at [the feast of] John, apply to Us again, and then be in waiting for the money; but the same [rent-master] presented himself to Us as little at John['s feast] as he has since that time. But had it happened, and had he brought the issued bond with him, the payment thereon should straightway have followed; and you can therefore still well obtain your money. Which We did not wish to withhold from you, in turn, for [your] gracious information; and We are graciously inclined toward you.</p><p>Given Güstrow, the 24th of July, in the year 1601.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender.</b> <b>Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</b> (1527–1603), <b>regent and guardian</b> of the young Dukes of Mecklenburg (here <i>Unsere unmündige Vettern und Pflegesöhne, die jungen Herzöge zu Mecklenburg</i> — his minor cousins and wards).</li><li><b>The 10,000-thaler debt.</b> The capital sum of Tycho's Mecklenburg investment: the young Dukes \"are indebted to you in <b>10,000 thaler</b>.\" The interest (200 reichsthaler) and a 600-thaler instalment appear in the related Mecklenburg documents (the quittance brahe-t_15990110001 to rent-master <b>Andreas Meyer</b>, and Bording's letter brahe-t_16000919001). The Duke explains a payment delay — the original creditors \"<b>struck a blank</b>\" at Easter; the money has lain ready at Güstrow since before <b>St John's (24 June)</b>; but Tycho's agents must present the <b>bond</b> (<i>Verschreibung</i>) to collect.</li><li><b><i>Eske Bille</b></i> = a member of the <b>Bille</b> family (Tycho's mother's kin), acting as Tycho's agent: he sent a servant to inquire at <b>Doberan</b> whether the money was ready.</li><li><b>One of the last letters.</b> Dated <b>24 July 1601</b>, exactly three months before Tycho's death (24 October 1601); the witness survives, fittingly, in the Schwerin archive series <i>Debita passiva principum</i> (\"the princes' liabilities\"). The doubled <i>Datum Datum</i> is a scribal slip in the witness, left as printed.</li><li><b>Calendar.</b> <i>Güstrow den 24. julij a:o 1601</i> = <b>24 July 1601</b>; Mecklenburg (Lutheran) = <b>Old Style</b>. Header agrees with the label.</li><li><b>Correction (2026-07-12).</b> Restored the sender tag 'Vlrich etc.' ('Ulrich, etc.') beneath the address heading, present in Dreyer (TBOO XIV p.227) but absent from the working transcription; and removed a duplicated 'Datum' in the closing dateline (p.228).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — the chancery German is rendered securely; the matter (the 10,000-thaler Mecklenburg debt; the Easter payment failure; the money lying ready at Güstrow pending presentation of the bond; rent-master Andreas Meyer and the agent Eske Bille) is clear and dovetails with the other Mecklenburg documents. <i>einen bloßen geschlagen</i> rendered idiomatically (\"came up empty / struck a blank\").</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [C], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.226-229 as page images. Letter no. 224 runs from the foot of p.227 to p.228 ('AN TYCHO BRAHEN ZU PRAAG. Vlrich etc. ... Datum Guftrow den 24. julij a:o 1601.'). Collated the full body against source_text and the English against both: the 10,000-thaler sum, the rent-master Andreas Meyer, Eske Bille's servant sent to Doberan, the St John's/Easter timeline, and the closing all match and are accurately translated. Two trivial TEXT_ERRORs found (a dropped one-line sender tag 'Vlrich etc.', and a duplicated 'Datum' in source_text not in Dreyer); neither changes the letter's sense, but per the grading rule any TEXT_ERROR caps the grade at C.</p><p><b>2 defects found:</b><br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). Dreyer's print carries a one-line sender tag 'Vlrich etc.' directly under the address heading and before the salutation begins; source_text omits this line entirely, so it has no counterpart in the English either. Dreyer prints: <i>AN TYCHO BRAHEN ZU PRAAG. / Vlrich etc. / VNSERN gonstigen grueß zuuor...</i>.<br>- <b>TEXT_ERROR</b> (low). source_text duplicates the word 'Datum'; Dreyer's print (p.228, closing line) has it once. Trivial and self-corrected in the English ('Given', singular), so no translation fault, but a genuine source_text/Dreyer discrepancy. Dreyer prints: <i>Datum Guftrow den 24. julij a:o 1601. [single 'Datum']</i>.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Collated against Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.226-229 (full slice, read in one pass); both sites confirmed on p.227 (the letter's opening, appearing at the foot of that page) and p.228 (the closing line). Both defects fixed with full confidence.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": {"label": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "count": 5, "pos": 5, "prev": "brahe-t_16010718002", "next": null, "reply_to": "brahe-t_16010718002", "reply_by": null}, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Explains that the 10,000 thalers have been lying ready in Güstrow since before Midsummer, and that Tycho need only send to demand them against return of the issued bond.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010724001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010724002", "d": "1601-07-24", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Eske Bille", "ck": "Eske Bille", "ck2": "Andreas Meier", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Copenhagen", "t": "Eske Bille → Andreas Meier — Copenhagen, 24 July 1601", "m": "<p>Language: German (Danish-influenced chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM ERBARN WOLGEACHTEN VNDT FURNHEMEN ANDRESS MEYER, DIE JUNGE HERTZOGEN ZU MEKELBURG, J: F: G: RENTEMEISTER, MEINEM INSONDERS GUNSTIGEN GUEDTEN FREUNDE F[REUNDLICH] G[ESCHRIEBEN].</p><p>MEINEN freundtlichen gruß zuuorn. Freundtlicher lieber rentemester. Kan ich euch freundtlich nicht vorhaldten, daß mihr heudt dato ein schreiben ist behendiget worden, so mihr Berendt Ferste, burger zum Kil, zugesendet hadtt, vndt dieweiln dar kein namen eingeschrieben war, hadt mich doch der briefzeuger berichtet, daß daßelbige brieff von euch keme, vnndt waß dießelbige anlanget, daß ich soldte habenn zu Dobbran, in deß f: g: vorgemack, anlangen laßenn vmme 2000 thaler vnnd derselbige, so dermasßen meinendtwegen hadt angelanget, kein quitung nicht gebragt, so weiß ich nicht von sölche quitung odder 2000 thaler zusagen, habe auch darumme nicht anlangen lasßen, söndern ihr wisßet euch wol zuerinneren, daß ich vor lengst die 10000 thaler, worauff ich ein furstliche obligation habe, habe auffkundigen lasßenn vnndt auff die bestimbte zeidt, alß nemlich auff ostern, mein diener midt des f: obligation hinaußgesenndt daß geldt zu entfhangen, vnndt weiln erß nicht bekhommen hadtt, vndt ihr mich zugeschriebenn, daß eß auff Johannj soldte erlegtt werden, alß habe ich zum vberfluß widderumme mein diener abgefertigtt midt deß f: obligation, vnnd hadt er itzliche thage fur vndt nach Johannj zu Rostock in Joachim Lorman sein hauß gewardtet, vndt weiln daß geldt in d: Lucas Beckmester sein huß zuerlegen auffgekundigt war, hadt erß dem h: doctor wisßen laßen, wohr er zu finden war, ob dar wen kheme, der solche summa außzelen woldte, seindt doch keine nicht gekommen, hadt sich derhalben widderumme nach heim begeben, vnnd weiln ihr schrieben, daß die summa fur Johannj al beisamen ist gewesen, vndt nuhr die entfhenger wardten, so wil ich noch zum dritten mall selbst pershonlich hinaußkommen, vnndt ihr werdet mihr nicht vordenckenn, wo ferne die summa neben all jnterres vnd skaden nicht erlegt wirdt, daß ich dan andre mittel gebrauchen werde. Habe auch diß zum f[reun]dtlich andtwordt nicht vorhaldten khonn[en]. Vnndt thue vnß beiderzeidtz in den schudtz deß Almegtigen empfhelen.</p><p>Datum Kopennhagen den 24. julij anno 1601.<br>Eske Bille</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, well-respected and distinguished Andreas Meier, rent-master to the young Dukes of Mecklenburg, Their Princely Graces, my especially favourable good friend. Written in friendship.</b></p><p>My friendly greeting beforehand. Friendly, dear rent-master: I cannot, in friendship, withhold from you that today a letter has been handed to me, which Berend Ferste, citizen of Kiel, has sent me; and since no name was written within it, the bearer nonetheless informed me that the same letter came from you. As to what it concerns — that I am supposed to have had application made at Doberan, in the antechamber of His Princely Grace, for 2,000 thalers, and that the man who thus applied on my behalf brought no receipt — I know nothing to say of any such receipt or of 2,000 thalers, nor have I had any application made for it. Rather, you will well remember that I long ago had the 10,000 thalers, on which I hold a princely bond, called in, and at the appointed time — namely at Easter — sent out my servant with the Prince's bond to receive the money; and since he did not get it, and you wrote to me that it should be paid at St. John's [Day], I again, for good measure, dispatched my servant with the Prince's bond, and he waited several days, before and after St. John's, at Rostock in Joachim Lorman's house. And since the money had been appointed to be paid out at Dr. Lucas Beckmeister's house, he let the Lord Doctor know where he was to be found, [to see] whether anyone would come there who would pay out the sum; but none came. He therefore betook himself home again. And since you wrote that the sum was all together before St. John's, and that only the receivers were awaited, I will yet a third time come out in person myself; and you will not think ill of me if — should the sum, together with all interest and damages, not be paid — I then resort to other means. This too I could not withhold, by way of a friendly answer. And I commend us both to the protection of the Almighty.</p><p>Given at Copenhagen, the 24th of July, in the year 1601.<br>Eske Bille</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> <b>Eske Bille</b>, the Danish nobleman and Tycho Brahe's kinsman-agent, writing <b>in his own person</b> from <b>Copenhagen</b> to the ducal rent-master — the most circumstantial document of the Mecklenburg-debt thread, recounting from the creditor's side what Tycho's letters (brahe-t_16010527001, brahe-t_16010718001) and Duke Ulrich's (brahe-t_16010809001) describe from theirs.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Andreas Meier</b>, <b>rent-master to the young Dukes of Mecklenburg</b> (<i>J. F. G.</i> = <i>Ihrer Fürstlichen Gnaden</i>, \"Their Princely Graces\"), at Wismar.</li><li><b>The narrative (fills the gaps in the thread).</b></li><li>A garbled report had reached Eske Bille via <b>Berend Ferste, a citizen of Kiel</b>, in an <i>unsigned</i> letter — that Bille was to collect <b>2,000 thalers</b> at <b>Doberan</b> (the Mecklenburg ducal seat at Bad Doberan), in the prince's antechamber, but without a receipt. Bille disavows all knowledge of this.</li><li>He restates the real claim: the <b>10,000-thaler princely bond</b> (<i>fürstliche Obligation</i>) was called in long ago; his servant was sent with the bond to collect at <b>Easter</b>, then again \"for good measure\" around <b>St. John's Day (24 June)</b>, waiting at <b>Rostock</b> in <b>Joachim Lorman's house</b>.</li><li>Payment had been appointed at the house of <b>Dr. Lukas Beckmeister</b> at Rostock; the servant notified him but no payer appeared, and he went home empty-handed.</li><li>Bille now threatens to come <b>a third time in person</b>, and failing full payment of principal + interest + damages, to \"resort to other means.\"</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> <i>Eske Bille</i> (sender); <i>Berend Ferste, Bürger zu Kiel</i> (the source of the false 2,000-thaler report); <i>Joachim Lorman</i> (Rostock householder where the servant lodged); <i>Dr. Lukas Beckmeister</i> (the Rostock doctor at whose house payment was to be made); <i>die jungen Herzöge zu Mecklenburg</i> (the under-age debtors).</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Kopenhagen</i> (place of issue); <i>Kiel</i>; <i>Doberan</i> (Bad Doberan); <i>Rostock</i>.</li><li><b>Language note (protocol §2).</b> The hand is <b>Danish-influenced chancery German</b>: Danish/Low-German orthography and forms surface throughout — <i>skaden</i> for <i>Schaden</i> (\"damages\"), <i>huß/hauß</i> for <i>Haus</i>, <i>wohr</i> for <i>wo</i>, <i>midt</i> for <i>mit</i>, <i>zuuorn</i>, <i>behendiget</i>, <i>vorhaldten</i>, <i>itzliche</i>, <i>pershonlich</i>. Rendered straight; the spelling is the writer's Dano-German chancery register, not error.</li><li><b>Formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> Burgher-courteous opening <i>meinen freundlichen Gruß zuvor</i> → \"my friendly greeting beforehand\"; address tag <i>F. G.</i> = <i>freundlich geschrieben</i> (\"written in friendship\"); <i>Datum</i> → \"Given.\"</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 24. Julij anno 1601</i> at Copenhagen; <b>Denmark kept the Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar until 1700, so this is Old Style — to be contrasted with the New-Style Prague datelines of Tycho's own letters in the same affair (the <i>St. Johannis</i> and <i>Ostern</i> terms are likewise Old Style here). Reproduced, not converted.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a circumstantial business letter in Dano-German chancery; the narrative (the false Doberan report, the Easter and St. John's collection attempts, Rostock, Lorman's and Beckmeister's houses, the threat of \"other means\") is explicit and dovetails with the companion documents. The dense vernacular orthography is read securely; no crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read all 4 page images of the slice visually (Dreyer XIV pp. 227-230). Confirmed source_text is a verbatim match of Dreyer's printed Low-German (letter no. 225, pp. 228-229), and collated the published English against both source_text and the page images. Checked every proper name (Berendt Ferste/Kiel, Dobbran/Doberan, Joachim Lorman/Rostock, Lucas Beckmester), every sum (2000 thaler, 10000 thaler, appearing identically in both places), and both feast-day terms (Ostern/Easter, Johannj/St. John's Day). No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Denies any application for 2,000 thalers at Doberan, reaffirms the earlier call-in of the 10,000 thalers, and recounts the failure of his servant to collect the sum at Easter.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010724002/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010808001", "d": "1601-08-08", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Eske Bille", "ck": "Eske Bille", "ck2": "Joachim Stralendorff", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Rostock", "t": "Eske Bille → Joachim Stralendorff — Rostock, 8 August 1601 [O.S.]", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery Early New High German) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv,<i> Debita passiva principum</i>, Vol. XXVIII B (original letter)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EDLEN VNNDT ERNUESTEN IOACHIM STRALENDORPFF, MECHLENBURGISCHE FURSTLICHE GNADEN VLRICI CAMMERJONKER, MEINEM FREUNDTLICHEN LIEBEN BRUDER ZU HANDEN, F[REUNDTLICH] G[ESCHRIEBEN].</p><p>MEINEN freundtlichen vndt gantz willigen diennst neben wunschungh alles guedtes beuorn, freundtlicher lieber Joachim Stralendorpf, bruder vnd insonders guedter freundt. Freundtlicher meinungh kan ich dihr nicht vorhaldten, daß mihr mein blutuorwandter vnd guedter freundt Tycho Brahen sein schreiben vor 9 oder 10 thage ist behandigtt worden, wor auß ich woll vornhommen, daß er nicht zwifelt (schreibt er) mihr die zehen thausendt thaler (dar ich dihr lestmals in mein schreiben vmme vormeldet habe) ja auff vorlauffenen Johanni seindt erlegtt worden. Vnndt weinig thagen zuuorn ist mihr Andreas Meyer sein schreiben auch behandigt worden, wohrinne ehr mich erinnertt, das einer meinendtwegen soldt zu Dobbran in meines g: f: vnd hernn, hertzog Vlrich, hertzog zu Mechelborig etc., f: f: g: vorgemach vmme zwe thausend thaler angehaldten haben vnd keine beweiß gehabtt, wohrauff ich ihme widderumme zugeschrieben habe, daß ich von keine 2000 thaler wuste, whe ich auch nicht weiß, sondern ehr wiste sich wol zuerinneren von wegen die 10000 thaler, so ich hochgedachte mein g: f: vnd hern auff vorgängen Michelj habe aufkundigen lasßen, worauff ich auch Meyer sein skrifftlich andtwordtt habe, daß solche summa midt sampt die zinse auff vorgangen ostern (whe billich) soldte gewis erlegt werden, vndt alß daß nicht geschehen ist, hadt ehr mich dan widderumme zugeschrieben, daß daß geldt gewisße auff Johannj soldte erlegt werden. Vndt weiln ich nuhn auf de rechte bestimbte zeidtt, alß nemlich auff ostern, habe mein diener hin ghenn Rostock geschicket midt deß furstlichen obligation vorgeschrieben summa zu empfhangen vnndtt nu widderumme auff vorgangen Johanni deßglichen midt hochgedachtes m: g: h: ihre f: g: obligation, vndt had mein diene[r] hir zu Rostock in Joachim Loherman sein hausß gelegen itzliche thage vor, auff vndt nach Johannj vnd sich in doctor Lucas Bachmester sein hauß angeben, weiln daß geldt dahr zuerlegen auffkekundigtt wahr, seindt doch keiner nicht da gewesen, der solche summa außzelen woldte. Vnnd gleichwol schreibt der rentemester inn bemeldte sein leste schreiben, daß die gantze summa vor Johannj ist beisamen gewesen vndt begierdt (weiln dahr keiner nicht meinentwegen auff Johannj ist gewesen, derß entfhangen woldte, schreibt er), daß ich doch wil her auß schicken vndt die 2000 thaler entfhangen lasßen, damidt Tyche daß vbrige [kon]te hinaußgeschicket werden, auff welchen sein skreiben ich mich nicht gnugsam vorwundren mach. Vnd habe ihme drauff schrifftlich geandtwortett, daß weiln ich nun zwey vnterschiedtliche mall, alß nemlich auff ostern vnnd Johannj, mein gewisße potschafft mitt hochgedachtes meines gnedigen fursten vnnd hern, j: f: g: obligation hir zu Rostock gehabtt, so wil ich nun zum vberfluß selbst zum dritten mal midt dem ersten kommen.</p><p>So bin ich nuhn, lieber bruder, hir zu Rostock heudt dato angekhommen, vnd auff den abent ist einer (deß namen oder pershon mihr vnbekandtt) bey mihr gewesen vndt auf Meyer sein wegen angegeben, das er habe 5000 thaler vormacht auff Prag, vndt dabey berichtet, daß ich mihr erkleren woldte, ob ich daß vbrige hir entfhangen woldte oder ghen Wißmer darnach zihen, worauff ich ihnen vor andtwordt gegeben habe, daß ich nu hergekommen ware die gantze summa midt sampt allen nachstendigen zinsen, auffgelauffenen skaeden vndt vnkost [zu empfangen], oder woferner solches nicht geschege, worde ich hochvorursachet andre mittel zu gebrauchen, welches Gott weiß ich vor mein pershon so wol alß mein bruede[r] Tycho vngerne thadten. Bitte dich derhalben freundtlich, lieber bruder, du woldtest doch meinem genedigen fursten vnndt hern anmelden, daß ich hir wardte, vndt daß sein f: g: sich gnedigst woldte erkleren, ob ich die summa mochte nuhn bekommen, weiln seiner f: g: schreiben sub dato Dobbran 17. junij anlanget, das ich ein kleine weile erwardten woldte, vorhoffe s: f: g: wirdt mihr ein gnedigen andtwordtt hir auf geben. Jch bitte dihr ghar freundtlich, du woldtest mihr vnuordecht haben, daß ich dihr so offte midt meinen sachen bemuhe; wohr ich dihr mein dage widderumme dienen kan, findest du mihr alletzeidt willich vndt erpottigh, vnd thue vnß beiderseidtz in den schudtz deß Almechtigen emptfhelen.</p><p>Datum Rostock den 8. augustj anno 1601.<br>Eske Bilde</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the noble and honourable Joachim Stralendorff, chamber-junker of His Princely Grace of Mecklenburg, Ulrich, to the hands of my friendly dear brother, amicably written.</b></p><p>My friendly and wholly willing service, with the wish of all good, beforehand, friendly dear Joachim Stralendorff, brother and especial good friend. In friendly intent I cannot conceal from you that my blood-relation and good friend <b>Tycho Brahe</b>'s letter was handed to me 9 or 10 days ago, from which I well perceived that he does not doubt (he writes) that <b>the ten thousand thalers</b> (about which I last wrote to you) were indeed paid out to me on the past <b>St. John's Day</b>. And a few days before, <b>Andreas Meier</b>'s letter was also handed to me, wherein he reminds me that someone, on my behalf, was [said] to have demanded <b>two thousand thalers</b> in the antechamber of my gracious prince and lord, <b>Duke Ulrich</b> of Mecklenburg, at <b>Doberan</b>, and had no proof; whereupon I wrote back to him that I knew of no 2,000 thalers (as indeed I do not), but he should well recall, concerning the <b>10,000 thalers</b> which I had given notice on to my aforesaid gracious lord at the past Michaelmas — on which I also have Meier's written answer, that such sum, together with the interest, should certainly be paid at the past Easter (as was fair); and when that did not happen, he wrote to me again that the money should certainly be paid on St. John's Day. And since I have now, at the right appointed time — namely at Easter — sent my servant to Rostock with the princely bond to receive the aforesaid sum, and now again on the past St. John's Day likewise with my gracious lord's bond — and my servant lay here at Rostock in <b>Joachim Loherman's house</b> several days before, on, and after St. John's Day, and presented himself at <b>Doctor Lucas Backmeister's house</b> (since the money was given notice to be paid there) — yet <b>no one was there who would pay out such sum</b>. And yet the rent-master writes, in his last letter, that the whole sum was together before St. John's Day, and desires (since no one was there on my behalf to receive it) that I would send out here and have the <b>2,000 thalers</b> received, so the rest could be sent on to Tycho — at which letter of his I cannot wonder enough. And I wrote back that, since I have now twice — at Easter and St. John's Day — had my sure messenger with my gracious lord's bond here at Rostock, <b>I will now, for good measure, come myself, a third time</b>, at the first [opportunity].</p><p>So I have now, dear brother, arrived here at Rostock this very day; and in the evening someone (whose name and person are unknown to me) was with me and stated, on Meier's behalf, that <b>he had transferred 5,000 thalers to Prague [by bill]</b>, and asked therewith that I declare whether I would receive the rest here, or travel to <b>Wismar</b> for it; whereupon I answered that I had now come here [to receive] <b>the whole sum, together with all outstanding interest, accrued damages, and costs — or, failing that, I would be highly compelled to use other means</b>, which (God knows) I, for my person, as well as <b>my brother Tycho</b>, would unwillingly do. I therefore ask you amicably, dear brother, that you would announce to my gracious prince and lord that I am waiting here, and that His Princely Grace would graciously declare whether I might now receive the sum — since His Princely Grace's letter, dated <b>Doberan 17 June</b>, asks that I wait a little while; I hope His Princely Grace will give me a gracious answer hereupon. I ask you quite amicably to hold me unblamed, that I trouble you so often with my affairs; wherever I can serve you in return in my days, you find me always willing and ready; and I commend us both to the protection of the Almighty.</p><p>Given at Rostock, the 8th of August, the year 1601.<br>Eske Bille.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Sender &amp; recipient.</b> <b>Eske Bille</b>, Tycho's Danish kinsman and <b>plenipotentiary</b> in the Mecklenburg-debt affair (cf. brahe-t_16010604001, brahe-t_16001116001), writing to <b>Joachim Stralendorff</b>, <b>chamber-junker (<i>Kammerjunker</i>) of Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg</b> — the duke's man through whom Eske seeks an answer. The MS is the <b>original letter</b>, filed at Schwerin under <i>Debita passiva principum</i>.</li><li><b>Significance — the climax (and impasse) of the Mecklenburg 10,000-thaler debt, August 1601.</b> This first-hand account from the creditor's agent, <b>two months before Tycho's death</b>, shows the collection in deadlock:</li><li><b>The muddle.</b> Rent-master <b>Andreas Meier</b> keeps writing of a <b>2,000-thaler</b> claim \"demanded in the antechamber at <b>Doberan</b> without proof\"; Eske replies he knows nothing of any 2,000 — only the <b>10,000-thaler principal</b> he gave notice on at <b>Michaelmas [1600]</b>, due (per Meier's own letters) first at <b>Easter</b>, then <b>St. John's Day [24 June 1601]</b>.</li><li><b>The failed collections.</b> Eske's servant came to Rostock with the <b>ducal bond</b> at both terms, lodging at <b>Joachim Lohmann's</b> and presenting himself at <b>Backmeister's house</b> (the appointed pay-place) — but <b>no one paid</b>. Meier nonetheless claims the sum \"was ready,\" and asks Eske to take <b>2,000</b> so \"the rest could be sent to Tycho at Prague.\"</li><li><b>Eske's third trip &amp; ultimatum.</b> Disbelieving, Eske has now come <b>himself, a third time</b>; an unnamed man (for Meier) reports <b>5,000 thalers transferred to Prague by bill</b> and offers the rest at Rostock or <b>Wismar</b>. Eske demands <b>the whole sum with all interest, damages, and costs — or \"other means\"</b> (legal action), \"which I and my brother Tycho would unwillingly do.\" He asks Stralendorff to get <b>Duke Ulrich's</b> answer (the duke's letter of <b>17 June, Doberan</b>, had asked him to \"wait a little while\").</li><li><b>Persons named.</b> Eske Bille; <b>Joachim Stralendorff</b> (ducal chamber-junker); <b>Tycho Brahe</b> (\"my blood-relation … my brother\"); <b>Andreas Meier</b> (rent-master); <b>Duke Ulrich III</b>; <b>Joachim Lohmann</b> (the Rostock host); <b>Lucas Backmeister</b> (Superintendent of Rostock, the pay-place). Places: <b>Doberan</b>, <b>Wismar</b>, <b>Rostock</b>, <b>Prague</b>.</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>Rostock den 8. Augustj anno 1601</i>; Lutheran Rostock kept the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar; the <i>Doberan 17. Junij</i> ducal letter likewise O.S. Header marked [O.S.].</li><li>Correction (2026-07-12). English corrected the house-owner's surname from 'Lohmann' to 'Loherman', matching both source_text and Dreyer (XIV p.230); the companion letter no.225 (XIV p.229) spells the same man 'Lorman'.</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — dense but clear chancery German, read securely; the substance (the 2,000-vs-10,000 confusion; the two failed collections at Easter and St. John's Day via the servant at Backmeister's house; the disputed \"ready\" sum and the 5,000 transferred to Prague; Eske's third personal trip and his ultimatum of \"the whole sum or other means\"; the appeal to Duke Ulrich via Stralendorff) is explicit and is the creditor's-side climax of the debt-thread (cf. brahe-t_16010604001, brahe-t_16010718001). No textual crux.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [B], was [A].</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.228-231 as page images; the audited letter (Eske Bille to Joachim Stralendorff, 8 Aug 1601) runs pp.229-230, bracketed on p.228-229 by a companion letter (no.225, Bille to Andreas Meyer, 24 July) and on p.231 by the next (no.227, Duke Ulrich to Meyer). Collated the opening/closing, every sum (10,000 / 2,000 / 5,000 thalers), every date (Easter, St John's Day, Doberan 17 June, Rostock 8 August 1601), and every proper name (Stralendorff, Andreas Meyer, Duke Ulrich of Mecklenburg, Doberan, Lucas Backmeister, Wismar, Tycho). All check out against Dreyer except the one name substitution noted above.</p><p><b>1 defect found:</b><br>- <b>MISTRANSLATION</b> (low). The house-owner's surname is printed 'Loherman' in both our source_text and Dreyer (a companion letter on the facing page, no.225, spells the same man 'Lorman'); the translation silently substitutes the distinct, more common modern surname 'Lohmann', misidentifying a named person in the financial narrative. At: “Joachim Lohmann's house”.</p><p><b>Repaired (2026-07-12).</b> Verified against the Dreyer page image (TBOO XIV p.230, letter 226), which prints the house owner's name as 'Loherman'; the facing letter no.225 (p.229) spells the same person 'Lorman', confirming this is a named individual and not the modern surname 'Lohmann' the translation had silently substituted.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Reports receiving Tycho Brahe's letter claiming 10,000 thalers were paid on St. John's Day, and denies knowledge of a 2,000-thaler demand at Duke Ulrich's court.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010808001/"}, {"s": "brahe-t_16010809001", "d": "1601-08-09", "dir": "third", "per": "exile", "c": "Ulrich III, Duke of Mecklenburg", "ck": "Ulrich 3., hertug af Mecklenburg", "ck2": "Andreas Meier", "g": "A", "lb": "German", "pl": "Güstrow", "t": "Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg → Andreas Meier — Güstrow, 9 August 1601", "m": "<p>Language: German (chancery) · Edition of record: Dreyer,<i> Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia </i>(vol. VIII,<i> Epistolae</i>) · MS witness: A = Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B (Originalbrief)</p>", "n": "", "la": "<p>DEM EHRSAMEN VNSERM LIEBEN GETREWEN ANDREAe MEYERN, RENTMEISTERN, ZUR WISSMAR.</p><p>VON Gottes gnadenn Vlrich, hertzogk zu Mekelnburgk etc. Ehrsamer lieber getrewer. Was Eßke Bilde der 10000 tahler halbenn, damit Vnsere vnmündige vettern vnd pflegesohne, die jungen hertzogen zu Mekelnburgk etc., Tycho Brahen verhafft, an Vnseren cammerer Jochim von Stralendorffen abermals gelangen thuet, das hast du aus beyuerwartem seinem originalschreiben nach mehrer lenge zuersehenn.</p><p>Nun seind Wir der meynung, es werden die zehen tausend thaler sampt den zinsen, so gemelter Tycho Brahe haben soll, noch beysamen seinn, das sie auff begeren gegenn wiedereinandwortung der außgegebenen verschreibung alßbald außgezehlett werden konnen, wie Wir dan solches newlicher zeit, jnmaßen du aus inligender copey zuuernehmen, von Vns geschriebenn. Weil aber Eßke Bylde vnter andern in seinem schreiben gedenket, das sich gestern abend einer bey jhm zu Rostogk angegeben, so jhm von deinentwegen angemeldet hette, das du obgedachtenn Tycho Brahenn 5000 thaler hinauff nach Prage vbermachet, vnd Wir nicht wißenn können, was es hirumb vor eine gelegenheit hat, alß begeren Wir demnach an dich hiemit gnediglich, das du Vns vngeseumet bey zeigern hieuon eigentlichen vnd gewißen bericht zuschreibest, dan weill Eßke Bilde selbst vmb des geldes willen aus Dennemarken gekommen ist vnd zu Rostogk liegt, konnen die sachen gar keinen verzugk leiden. Vnnd haltenn Wirs auch dafür, weil du Vns das geringste dauon nicht angezeigett, das du das gelt hinauff nach Praag soltest vermachet haben, welches dan auch Vnsers bedünckens vor wiedererlangung der außgegebenen verschreibung nicht fuglich hat geschehen können, es werde die gantze summa noch bey einander seinn, damit es volnkomlich, sintemal es Eßke Bilde auch volnkomlich haben will, außgezehlet werden könne vnd müge. Vnd wirst du zeigern mit der begertenn andwort desto zeitiger wiederumb an Vns abfertigen, damit Wir dieselbe noch vor vnserm auffzuge von hinnen (so ob Gott wil am zukünfftigen dingestage morgen geschehen wirdt) gewiße mügen vberkommen. An dem erstatest du Vnsere gnedige zuuerleßige meynungk.</p><p>Datum Güstrow den 9. augustj an:o 1601.</p><p><i>[Postscript — restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, pp. 231–232; dropped by the working transcription. See Apparatus.]</i><br>[In schedula:] Post Scripta. Auch ehrsamer lieber getrewer, weil Eßke Bilde itzo zur stedte ist, vnd auf Vnserer vnmundigen vettern vnd pflegesohne vnkosten auf das geldt wartet, vnd Wir Vns erjnnern, das das geldt alhie jn Vnsere renterey verhanden ist, so begheren vnd beuehlen Wir dir demnach hiemit gnediglich, das du dich angelicus briefs erhebest vnd alhie noch vor Vnserm aufzuge bej Vns anlangest, damit Wir nicht allein von dir selbst rechten bericht erfharen, sondern auch die sachen desto ehe jhre richtige maße erlangen, vnd besorgender schade vnd vngelegenheit verhuetet pleiben muge.</p>", "en": "<p><b>To the honourable, our dear and faithful Andreas Meier, rent-master, at Wismar.</b></p><p>By the grace of God, Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg etc. Honourable, dear, faithful one: what Eske Bille, concerning the 10,000 thalers with which our under-age cousins and foster-sons, the young Dukes of Mecklenburg etc., stand bound to Tycho Brahe, once again addresses to our chamberlain Joachim von Stralendorf — that you may see at fuller length from his original letter, enclosed herewith.</p><p>Now, we are of the opinion that the ten thousand thalers, together with the interest which the said Tycho Brahe is to have, are still on hand together, so that, upon demand, against the surrender of the bond that was issued, they may at once be paid out — as indeed we wrote to you about this recently (as you may gather from the enclosed copy). But since Eske Bille, among other things in his letter, mentions that yesterday evening someone presented himself to him at Rostock, who had reported to him on your behalf that you had remitted 5,000 thalers up to Prague to the aforesaid Tycho Brahe — and we cannot know what the circumstance of this may be — we therefore graciously desire of you herewith that you write us, without delay, by the bearer, an actual and certain report of this; for since Eske Bille has himself come from Denmark for the sake of the money, and lies at Rostock, the matter can suffer no delay whatever. And we also hold it [to be so] — since you have indicated to us not the least of it, that you should have remitted the money up to Prague, which moreover, in our judgement, could not properly have been done before the recovery of the issued bond — that the whole sum is still together, so that it may and can be paid out in full (since Eske Bille, too, wants it in full). And you will dispatch the bearer back to us with the desired answer all the more promptly, so that we may certainly receive it before our departure from here (which, God willing, will take place next Tuesday, tomorrow). In this you fulfil our gracious and trusty intent.</p><p>Given at Güstrow, the 9th of August, in the year 1601.</p><p><i>[Postscript:]</i> [On a slip:] Post Scripta. Also, honourable, dear, faithful one: since Eske Bille is now on the spot, and waits for the money at the cost of our under-age cousins and foster-sons, and We recall that the money is here on hand in our treasury, We therefore desire and command you herewith, graciously, that upon [receipt of] the present letter you set out and present yourself to Us here before our departure, so that We may not only learn the true report from you yourself, but also that the matter the sooner attain its right measure, and the apprehended damage and inconvenience remain forestalled.</p>", "ap": "<ul><li><b>Direction &amp; sender.</b> A <b>ducal letter</b>, from <b>Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg(-Güstrow)</b> (1527–1603), to his own treasury official — the third surviving document of the Tycho/Mecklenburg debt thread, written from the ducal residence at <b>Güstrow</b>. (The dataset labels the sender <i>Ulrich 3.</i>) It is included in the Tychonic correspondence because it concerns, and quotes, the recovery of Tycho's loan.</li><li><b>Recipient.</b> <b>Andreas Meier</b>, ducal Mecklenburg <b>rent-master at Wismar</b> — the same officer pressed in Tycho's own letters of 27 May and 18 July 1601 (brahe-t_16010527001, brahe-t_16010718001). The Duke, his master, now demands an accounting.</li><li><b>The transaction.</b> The <b>10,000-thaler loan</b> owed Tycho by the <b>young (under-age) Dukes of Mecklenburg</b>, the Duke's <i>vmündige Vettern und Pflegesöhne</i> (\"under-age cousins and foster-sons\") for whom Ulrich stood guardian. The Duke believes the full sum is in hand, to be paid against the surrender of the <b>bond (<i>Verschreibung</i>)</b> — but Eske Bille has reported a rumour that Meier already <b>remitted 5,000 thalers to Prague</b>, and the Duke demands urgent clarification: a partial remittance before the bond's recovery would be irregular.</li><li><b>Persons named.</b></li><li><i>Eßke Bilde</i> = <b>Eske Bille</b>, the Danish nobleman and Tycho's kinsman-agent, who has \"come from Denmark for the sake of the money\" and waits at <b>Rostock</b> (cf. brahe-t_16010527001).</li><li><i>unser Kämmerer Joachim von Stralendorf</i> = <b>Joachim von Stralendorf</b>, the Duke's <b>chamberlain</b>, to whom Eske Bille's latest letter was addressed.</li><li><i>Tycho Brahe</i>, the creditor; the <i>junge Herzöge zu Mecklenburg</i>, the under-age debtors.</li><li><b>Places.</b> <i>Güstrow</i> (ducal residence, place of issue); <i>Wismar</i> (Meier's seat); <i>Rostock</i> (where Eske Bille waits); <i>Prag</i> = <b>Prague</b> (where Tycho is, and whither the disputed 5,000 thalers were allegedly sent).</li><li><b>Register &amp; formulae (protocol §5.5).</b> The princely <b>first-person plural</b> (<i>Von Gottes Gnaden … Wir … Unsere</i>) and the subject-address <i>unser lieber getreuer</i> (\"our dear and faithful one\") mark the sovereign-to-servant register, distinct from the burgher-courteous tone of Tycho's own letters to Meier. <i>Datum</i> → \"Given.\" The internal deadline <i>am zukünftigen Dienstag, morgen</i> = \"next Tuesday, tomorrow\" (the Duke's imminent departure from Güstrow).</li><li><b>Calendar (protocol §3).</b> Dateline <i>den 9. Augusti a:o 1601</i>; the dataset gives no style marker. As a <b>Lutheran north-German chancery</b> document, Mecklenburg in 1601 was still on the <b>Julian (Old Style)</b> calendar (Protestant Germany kept it until 1700) — to be contrasted with the explicit <i>stylo novo</i> of Tycho's Prague letters in the same thread. The date is reproduced as written; no conversion is performed (protocol §3).</li></ul><ul><li><b>§1 Dreyer collation (2026-06-02).</b> \"[In schedula:] Post Scripta\" enclosed slip (Duke Ulrich commands Meier to come at once, the money being in the ducal treasury, Eske Bille waiting), restored from Dreyer XIV pp.231–232 (dropped by the working transcription).</li></ul>", "cf": "<p><b>Confidence: [A]</b> — a clear ducal chancery letter; the parties (Duke Ulrich, Eske Bille, Joachim von Stralendorf, Andreas Meier, Tycho), the disputed 5,000-thaler remittance, and the bond-against-payment mechanism are all explicit. No textual crux. The only point requiring care — that this Old-Style north-German date sits opposite the New-Style Prague dating of the companion letters — is flagged, not reconciled.</p><p>---</p><p><b>Independent fidelity audit (2026-07-12) — grade [A], confirmed.</b> Collated against <b>Dreyer, <i>Tychonis Brahe Dani Opera Omnia</b></i> (the edition of record), reading the printed page images. Read Dreyer TBOO XIV pp.230-234 as page images (Read pages=1-4; page 5 not needed, letter fully contained by p.232). Collated the whole German letter including the 10,000/5,000-thaler figures, the Eske Bille/Joachim von Stralendorf names, Rostock/Wismar/Prague place-names, the Tuesday-departure clause, and the 9 August 1601 dateline word-for-word. Also specifically verified the editorial claim in source_text/published_english that the postscript was 'restored from Dreyer, Opera Omnia XIV, pp.231-232' -- confirmed: Dreyer genuinely prints the postscript ('Post scripta. Auch ehrsamer lieber getrewer...') on pp.231-232 under the marginal note '[In schedula:]', matching the published translation exactly. No fabrication, omission, mistranslation, hedge-loss, or text-error found.</p>", "ms": null, "w": ["A Schwerin, Landeshauptarchiv, Debita passiva principum, Vol. XXVIII B. Originalbrev"], "th": null, "ka": 0, "bk": 0, "ab": "Encloses Eske Bille's letter on 10,000 thalers owed to Tycho Brahe, confirms the sum is ready, and questions a report of 5,000 thalers remitted to Prague.", "url": "https://tekstnet.dk/books/brahe-t_letters/brahe-t_16010809001/"}];
